window.ROTATION_LLM_ABOUT = {
"10-years~d-j-vu":{"haiku":"The speaker expresses creative and existential fatigue, feeling trapped in repetition where every idea, image, and song already exists.","opus":"A weary complaint about cultural exhaustion, the narrator wishing he could shut off his mind because everything feels recycled and every so-called original just reminds him of something already seen, drifting toward an unknown end. 10 Years drive this fatigue with post-grunge alt-metal, exposing the emptiness of novelty that only replays the same base impulses on a loop.","sonnet":"Cultural exhaustion hardens into numbness — everything claimed as original has been said and done before, every reinvention feels recycled. The narrator runs on autopilot, uncertain whether momentum is carrying him toward the end or just back to the same beginning, drained of anything left to give.","src":"haiku"},
"10-years~wasteland":{"haiku":"Desperation and broken intentions create a wasteland of self-inflicted suffering where the speaker attempts redemption through contrition.","opus":"Addiction watched from the outside by someone who loves the person disappearing into it, torn between rescue and self-preservation. The refrain of good intentions curdling into paralysis is the whole tragedy: wanting to help and knowing you can't.","sonnet":"A fractured internal monologue depicts someone consumed by self-harm and compulsion, surrounded by people who observe but cannot reach them, the speaker caught between wanting help and being unable to accept it.","src":"haiku"},
"100-gecs~100-gecs-hand-crushed-by-a-mallet-remix-feat-fall-out-boy-craig-owens-nicole-dollanganger":{"haiku":"Violent possession masks as intimacy; the speaker crushes and consumes the beloved as blood offering, celebrating mutual destruction as proof of transcendent connection.","src":"haiku"},
"100-gecs~hand-crushed-by-a-mallet":{"haiku":"Obsessive surveillance and manipulation corrupt creation; the speaker is haunted by someone who weaponized their intimacy and now steals their voice through imitation.","opus":"A hyper-chaotic tangle of resentment aimed at a copycat and rival who infiltrated the speaker's world and made destruction fun. It is about being targeted, refusing to break, and vowing to haunt whoever imitates you.","sonnet":"A chaotic collision of grief, paranoia, and creative theft: the speaker lost someone without getting to say goodbye, is haunted by thoughts that circle obsessively, and turns on a studio rival who copies everything — promising to haunt their sessions as a singing ghost they'll never shake.","src":"haiku"},
"100-gecs~hand-crushed-by-a-mallet-remix-feat-fall-out-boy-craig-owens-nicole-dollanganger":{"haiku":"Frantic guilt over creating art that consumes you and your muse, haunted by the violence of creativity.","opus":"Hyperpop chaos turning obsession and creative theft into a haunting, the narrator vowing to possess whoever's copying them. Absurd violence and clingy menace played for maximalist thrill and dark comedy.","sonnet":"A chaotic collage of obsession and creative possession — someone couldn't say goodbye, circled the speaker inside a room, and now the speaker promises to haunt their studio forever, turning up in their words and melodies. The refrain \"I'd copy me too\" twists the accusation of imitation into a compliment and a threat simultaneously.","src":"haiku"},
"1000mods~vidage":{"haiku":"Seeking freedom and relief from emotional darkness; the speaker fears abandonment while yearning to wake and escape into liberation.","opus":"A drowsy, half-conscious meditation on letting go, told by someone drifting through a dreamlike drive with unseen escorts, aching to be shaken awake so he can escape toward some vessel of freedom. 1000mods are a Greek stoner-rock band, and their fuzzed desert-blues heaviness turns the track into a hazy portrait of surrender, homecoming, and emotional shutdown.","sonnet":"A bleary, half-conscious plea for freedom and return — someone drifting between sleep and waking, between departure and coming home. Angels ride alongside on the road, windows flung open, breath taken deep. The emotional center is a paradox: are you lost precisely when you think you've arrived? The answer dissolves into a request to just be held.","src":"haiku"},
"108~death-comes-ripping":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic inevitability approaches with malevolent force; flesh proves defenseless against supernatural predation consuming the mortal body.","opus":"A Misfits cover charged with 108's Krishnacore intensity, the horror-punk imagery of death arriving as an unstoppable, violent force reframed as a confrontation with mortality and the inevitability of the end.","sonnet":"Death is personified as an active, heat-bearing force that rips back out from the flesh and will not be stopped by mortal weakness — the speaker waits with eyes open for the kill, feeling the evil and the heat. The lyric is almost purely visceral, with no narrative beyond the approach and arrival of the end.","src":"haiku"},
"12-rods~i-wish-you-were-a-girl":{"haiku":"Gender ambivalence complicates attraction; the narrator desires romantic reconfiguration while resenting the hierarchies desire itself imposes.","opus":"An unusually honest indie confession of attraction that crosses a line the narrator can't square with himself, tangled in awkwardness, shame and the wish that desire came in a form he'd know how to accept.","sonnet":"A speaker feels genuine intimacy and physical closeness with a male friend and confesses a flickering wish that he were a girl — not out of rejection, but out of confused feeling, acknowledging the desire is both honest and something they already know is beside the point.","src":"haiku"},
"156-silence~a-past-embrace":{"haiku":"Inherited dysfunction persists across time; the speaker drowns in accumulated wrongs, unable to escape patterns of abuse that define identity itself.","opus":"A metalcore self-immolation, cataloguing depression as something deserved rather than survived. The narrator picks apart his own worthlessness, weighs suicide as a kind of victory, and refuses the comfort of grace or being loved.","sonnet":"A self-lacerating stream of consciousness from someone who cannot locate grace, safety, or the capacity to receive love — they bleed, detach from everyone, wash hands of wrongs they know they've earned, and refuse to look upward toward God or doves. The past embrace of the title is what this moment irrevocably is not.","src":"haiku"},
"156-silence~if-pleasure-s-gone":{"haiku":"Disconnection drives internal virus; pleasure absent leaves only descent. The speaker interrogates selfhood and system simultaneously, questioning whether anything remains worth returning to.","opus":"Anhedonia rendered from the inside, where a partner's constant criticism deepens a numbness the speaker likens to a virus he's spreading to others. The recurring wish to just go back to sleep rather than wake to a joyless life makes depression's central question audible: without pleasure, who is he, and is he even still himself.","sonnet":"A fractured interior monologue about chronic pain and identity erosion: someone nitpicks and twists the speaker's wrist to force pieces that don't fit, and the speaker — infected with something they now spread to others — oscillates between refusing to give up and longing to go back to sleep rather than wake to a joyless morning, asking whether they can still be themselves or help anyone else.","src":"haiku"},
"156-silence~irrational-pull":{"haiku":"The speaker is consumed by self-loathing and isolation, recognizing their own worthlessness while unable to escape the voices and past failures that haunt them relentlessly.","opus":"A spiral of self-loathing where the narrator keeps pressing forward only to get pushed away, watching his future decay while misery loops on replay and he begs to be left alone with the crows. 156/Silence are an American metalcore band trafficking in raw mental-health confession, and this piles up curses of worthlessness and cursed loneliness into a relentless plea for escape that never comes.","sonnet":"A mind in freefall, watching itself decay in real time — the voices that pick at worth, the old bad habits that crawl back in, the certainty of being beyond rescue. Each verse tightens the grip: isolation chosen and resented simultaneously, the knowledge of what should be done eclipsed by the pull toward ruin. The refrain is not surrender so much as exhausted honesty.","src":"haiku"},
"156-silence~unreasonable-doubt":{"haiku":"Infection spreads through connection; proximity to others brings only contagion and mutual destruction, making isolation the only rational defence.","opus":"The addict-and-partner spiral seen from inside the wreckage: he knows he poisons everyone near him and half-wants to be cut loose, but blames the relationship for making him this way. Self-loathing dressed as an apology.","sonnet":"A toxic relational loop where the speaker acknowledges being abusive and self-destructive while simultaneously directing blame outward — the other person brings out the worst, swallows the speaker's vision, fills a plate with deception. The offer to befriend anything for personal advancement, and the brink of destroying what was created together, is examined without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"156-silence~vexation":{"opus":"A merciless inward spiral of disgust, the narrator cataloguing his own daily failures and complacency, unable to release himself from the weight of having let down everyone who counted on him. He admits with grim defiance that he never cared and has no intention of starting.","sonnet":"A song of exhausted self-loathing and interpersonal disgust — the narrator hates the compromises they make to appease someone, hates their own patterns of self-comparison and defeat, and oscillates between rage at the other person and contempt for themselves for staying so defenseless.","src":"sonnet"},
"171~go-go-little-cub":{"haiku":"Past cities evaporate into fog while the speaker accelerates through emptiness toward invisible horizons. Age brings weight; forward motion replaces direction as phantom futures vanish.","opus":"A night drive down empty lanes becomes a bid to outrun the fog swallowing the years the two of them once shared, hauling too much baggage from a place they thought they'd left behind. The bitter closing turn admits that even a time machine would change nothing, that the ache was only ever the speaker's own misreading.","sonnet":"Racing down an empty midnight lane in fog, the speaker tries to outrun the accumulated weight of a city left behind and luggage still too heavy — even a time machine back to \"then\" would change nothing because nothing was ever really different — yet the stubborn, shouted refusal to accept that stasis is permanent drives the engine forward.","src":"haiku"},
"22~sylphs":{"haiku":"Dreams must be lived in the present world, not kept distant or deferred, or they become anchors holding you back from growth.","opus":"The Norwegian rock band treats ambitions as an inner sense of direction rather than something to admire from a cautious remove. The narrator confesses he hides, flees, and invents justifications while loving his work but despising himself, and argues that even a modest hope realized on solid ground beats a magnificent one kept out of reach.","sonnet":"An argument against safe distance from one's own desires — dreams left in the outer spheres atrophy and become a prison. Even a small dream brought into daily life is worth more than grand visions held forever at arm's length.","src":"haiku"},
"2cellos~the-resistance":{"haiku":"Secret love is an act of resistance against systems that hunt unauthorized emotion and thought. Lovers must hide to survive persecution.","opus":"Two lovers keep a doomed, secret affair alive under threat of exposure, treating their devotion itself as rebellion against a regime bent on separating them. This is 2Cellos' instrumental reimagining of a Muse anthem; the duo trades electric guitars for dueling cellos while preserving the original's dystopian romance and its promise of endless, patient longing.","sonnet":"A forbidden love surviving under surveillance — two people hiding their bond from forces that would erase it, lips sealed, hearts lit anyway. The tension between wrongness and rightness runs through every line; the relationship probably cannot last but feels like the only true thing. Love declared as the one act of defiance available, even as the walls close in and escape becomes the only option left.","src":"haiku"},
"2pac~made-niggaz":{"haiku":"Gang loyalty and street violence intertwine as survival logic; the narrators claim ruthlessness as identity forged in systemic abandonment.","opus":"Late-era Makaveli defiance, Pac and the Outlawz declaring themselves untouchable, self-made from poverty and violence, the swagger shadowed by prison, paranoia and the certainty none of it lasts forever.","sonnet":"Multiple Outlaw Immortalz members and 2Pac trade verses on the code and cost of street life — loyalty to the crew, the cycle of violence and imprisonment, the pursuit of money, and the identity forged by surviving a system designed against them.","src":"haiku"},
"2wo~i-am-a-pig":{"haiku":"Contamination and false accusation; the speaker denies wrongdoing while something was already set in motion.","opus":"Rob Halford's mid-nineties industrial side project delivers a squirming study in guilt and self-deception, its narrator flatly denying any wrongdoing even as evidence of it mounts around him. The jittery, repeated interrogation gnaws at the shame beneath the machinery, exposure and self-disgust grinding away under the abrasive electronic clatter.","sonnet":"A figure deflects accusation with injured innocence — insisting they only tried to keep things clean, that what was seen was not what happened. The repeated unanswered question gives the denial an unstable, suspicious weight.","src":"haiku"},
"3-doors-down~kryptonite":{"haiku":"A devoted protector sustains someone with superhuman care despite being taken for granted, defining their loyalty as strength. The song portrays one-sided devotion and emotional support.","opus":"The narrator borrows Superman's mythology to interrogate a friendship, wondering whether he will still be wanted once his superhuman usefulness fails. Loyalty is offered unconditionally while love feels conditional. Emerging from Mississippi at the turn of the millennium, 3 Doors Down turn post-grunge earnestness into arena scale, and this early breakout frames devotion as its own vulnerable weakness.","sonnet":"A man drifting on the margins of sanity asks whether someone he has propped up through every crisis will still stand by him if he falls apart. Loyalty tested against damage — the cape cannot save him from himself, only presence can.","src":"haiku"},
"3-inches-of-blood~deadly-sinners":{"haiku":"Metal warriors armed with swords and spikes hunt tyrants through sewers and streets, striking without mercy. Victory comes through bloodlust and metal brought to violent life against those who rule.","opus":"A cartoonishly violent warrior fantasy, where armored fighters hunt down wrongdoers and pledge to smite a warlord with cold iron. 3 Inches of Blood were Canadian revivalists of vintage heavy metal, and this delivers their trademark falsetto shrieks and sword-and-sorcery bombast with total earnestness and no wink at the audience.","sonnet":"A heavy-metal battle hymn in which leather-and-iron warriors stalk dark streets to destroy tyrants and enemies of metal. The framing is mock-epic: sewers, ambushes, lightning strikes, the promise of triumphant victory. Moral complexity is nowhere near the agenda — this is pure adrenaline ritual, the pleasure of imagining yourself as the predator who rules the night and takes no prisoners.","src":"haiku"},
"3-pill-morning~all-before":{"haiku":"A speaker rejects repetitive betrayal and silence. Saying the same things means nothing—breaking cycles requires departure.","opus":"A hardened refusal to be hurt again, addressed to a nameless nobody told to perform and save the crowd, while the singer admits he's still searching for who he is beneath the armor of I've heard it all before.","sonnet":"A declaration of exhausted defiance from someone who keeps making and breaking promises to protect themselves — they've told themselves before that no one would hurt them again, and they know it rings hollow, but by the end they're forcing it into something real: a rejection of critics and a refusal to be cancelled or diminished.","src":"haiku"},
"360~run-alone":{"haiku":"Escapism and chemical liberation promise transcendence; the narrator invites a companion to flee reality and inhabit a private cosmos of consciousness.","opus":"The Australian rapper framing escape as a drug-fueled flight from a joyless reality, inviting someone to leave the world behind and get high enough to forget it, running from faith and everyone else.","sonnet":"The speaker invites someone on a psychedelic escape to an imaginary place beyond reality and gravity, promising euphoria while acknowledging his own conflicted need — he wants to run from faith and love but still craves company for the flight.","src":"haiku"},
"3d-stas~deep-scars":{"haiku":"Systemic violence perpetuates through deception and class warfare; survivors emerge mutilated but unable to resist cycles of exploitation and bloodshed.","opus":"Ties gun violence to class inequality, framing shootings as the product of poverty and deceit that leave survivors physically and psychologically wounded. It rejects the manipulative rhetoric of the powerful, whose reckless armed authority guides an uncomprehending public, and finds no safe refuge from the fallout of the violence.","sonnet":"A song about class warfare and systemic violence — bullets fired from the struggle of one class ricochet through underpasses and leave those caught in the crossfire with permanent wounds. The targets are institutional liars who pull triggers and leave nothing behind, and the survivors come out marked but still moving.","src":"sonnet"},
"3teeth~affluenza":{"haiku":"Wealth and consumerism spread as parasitic infection across society, breeding apathy and greed while enslaving the population through false needs.","opus":"An industrial tirade casting consumer capitalism as a spreading sickness, greed multiplying like infection while performative outrage and viral outrage-bait masquerade as rebellion. The narrator's only remedy is to sever ties and let the whole structure collapse.","sonnet":"Consumerism as terminal disease — the rich molest, clickbait justice replaces real protest, and the narrator names the whole spectacle 'affluenza': the West rotting from the inside out, consuming itself in a corporate orgy of greed they've finally decided to amputate from.","src":"haiku"},
"3teeth~degrade-rob-de-large-remix":{"opus":"3TEETH aim their industrial fury at inherited corruption, at a culture that passes its hate down to its own children generation after generation. The imagery of burning altars and the mighty dollar names capitalism and religion as the machinery of degradation. It is a bleak indictment of a race spitting poison onto its young.","sonnet":"A violent condemnation of institutional power — religion, capitalism, and state violence are fused into a single degrading system that poisons its own children, and the song calls for burning the altar and the dollar together as the only honest response.","src":"sonnet"},
"3teeth~drift":{"haiku":"Hypocrisy silences resistance; the speaker surrenders articulation and ascends into cosmic indifference, abandoning earthly corruption for obliteration.","opus":"Industrial disillusionment with a nation peddling endless conflict and self-righteous posturing while the self wastes away. The exit here is inward rather than political, a drift into dreaming and cosmic emptiness once even speech feels pointless.","sonnet":"Political and personal dissolution converge as the speaker, silenced and adrift in a permawar society built on hypocrisy, abducts himself past hell onto a higher plane. The drift becomes a cosmic dissolution — pathless black veins, a thousand nameless names, until the self obliterates into nothingness.","src":"haiku"},
"3teeth~everybody-wants-to-rule-the-world":{"haiku":"Power hunger pervades human nature; the speaker observes that beneath civilization everyone schemes for dominion, turning freedom into just another commodity.","opus":"An industrial cover that strips the Tears for Fears hit of its melancholy sheen and exposes the naked will to power underneath, everyone's hunger for dominion set against the certainty that nothing lasts. In 3TEETH's hands the familiar lines read as dystopian indictment rather than wistful pop.","sonnet":"A cover that leans into the original's paranoid grandeur: no matter the design, the remorse, the fleeting pleasure, or the sorrow of things fading, the drive to dominate never leaves — there's a room where the light won't find you, and everyone is heading there.","src":"haiku"},
"3teeth~exxxit":{"opus":"Built on Albert Hirschman's model of leaving, protesting, or staying faithful, the track dramatizes a mind trapped by a deranged system, dosing its urge toward violence while weighing whether to flee, resist, or comply. 3TEETH convert political theory into industrial dread, the warped golden coronet standing for a fixed order demanding you bow or stay silent.","sonnet":"A song framed around the political theory of exit, voice, or loyalty — the narrator sees the entire system as insane, suppresses violent impulses with pills, and ultimately concludes that the only honest response to a corrupt order is to burn it or leave it.","src":"sonnet"},
"3teeth~higher-than-death":{"haiku":"Defiant ascension through suffering; the speaker claims superiority through endurance, transcending mortality through refusal and burning resistance.","opus":"A rebellion against an order that feeds the many on resentment while the few hoard everything, the speaker embracing his own ruin as the last available liberty once meaning has drained away. Rising past mortality reads as both a drugged transcendence and a taunt at a life boxed in by falsehood, closing with surrender to a maternal deity who claims him.","sonnet":"Rage at a system that stripped people of their nature, fed them hate and scraps, and lives cowardly behind wealth — the speaker exults in being \"higher than death,\" urging defiance and collective action while a light burns through the smoke that the establishment will never extinguish.","src":"haiku"},
"3teeth~merchant-of-the-void":{"haiku":"Digital systems and consumerism exploit hunger and emptiness into addiction; the speaker embodies the void-peddler, profiting from manufactured desire and manufactured meaning.","opus":"An industrial band personifies the attention economy as a smirking dealer god, selling manufactured desire back to hollowed-out consumers who mistake the sales pitch for meaning and free will. Predatory capitalism as a religion of the void.","sonnet":"A first-person algorithm speaks — it harvests data, sells dopamine hits, manufactures meaning, and the user keeps coming back because emptiness is the product, not the bug. \"I'm the illusion of your choice / I'm the god that gave you the voice\" names the technology-as-deity dynamic; the transaction is always the same: fill the void with something that deepens it.","src":"haiku"},
"3teeth~pumped-up-kicks":{"haiku":"A disturbed child discovers a gun and plans violence; other children must flee from this looming threat of school violence.","opus":"The industrial act reclaims a deceptively bright pop hit, voicing a troubled kid named Robert who finds his father's gun and imagines turning it on his classmates. The taunting chorus urging the other kids to outrun the bullet lands colder in this harsher electronic setting, the menace no longer buried under melody. Rendered menacing rather than breezy, it lays the original's violence bare as threat rather than earworm.","sonnet":"A disturbed boy, neglected and quietly unraveling, finds a gun in his father's closet and begins moving toward violence. The threat is delivered as warning to the oblivious, fashionable crowd he has already targeted in his mind.","src":"haiku"},
"3teeth~scorpion":{"haiku":"Demonic ascension through pain; the speaker embodies predatory inevitability, seeing through all deception while striking with poisoned inevitability.","opus":"An occult power fantasy in which the singer swells into an invoked infernal being, jeweled and predatory, dealing death with no interest in becoming a martyr. The arachnid stands for the ego turned into a weapon, a vow never to serve as anyone's prey or offering.","sonnet":"The scorpion as an archetype of impervious, venomous power — residing behind the sun, eating death, stinging everything with poison as karma, seeing through everyone with onyx eyes and a diamond tongue. The speaker inhabits this force as a bringer of oblivion immune to spells and undisturbed by mortality.","src":"haiku"},
"3teeth~slum-planet":{"haiku":"Corporatized decay and consumer worship breed disconnection; the speaker pledges allegiance to self-destruction while the machine converts trash into profit and the planet sinks into disposal.","opus":"A sneering first-person embodiment of consumer capitalism, boasting about strip-mining the earth into a toxic wasteland while framing greed as a divine right. The dystopia is satire aimed squarely at the mindset that produced it.","sonnet":"A corrosive monologue from the voice of consumer capitalism itself — pledging allegiance to its own corruption, welcoming everyone to a slum planet where deformed children are born of corporate grace and the only rule is to keep buying — the spoken section makes the satire explicit.","src":"haiku"},
"3teeth~what-s-left":{"haiku":"Automation and consumption strip meaning from existence, leaving only void and the desperate question of what remains when all is taken.","opus":"An industrial reckoning with a world of automation, buck-passing and spiritual bankruptcy where nothing survives but amputated hopes. The only thing that comes free is dying, and the demand to be shown what remains is a scream into a machine no one will switch off.","sonnet":"Industrial nihilism about a world where automation has replaced purpose, reality has been hacked, and nothing of value remains — the narrator is consumed, disconnected, screaming into a void where only death is free.","src":"haiku"},
"51st-avenue~mind-your-own":{"haiku":"Autonomy demands rejection of external judgement; the speaker refuses accountability to others' narratives, insisting on unexamined self-determination.","opus":"A defiant kiss-off to unwanted judgment and gossip, drawing a hard line around personal choice. Beneath the swagger is the exhaustion of constantly being asked to justify who you've decided to be.","sonnet":"A direct refusal to be accountable to unsolicited opinions — the speaker owes no apology, doesn't care what others think, and will not explain her choices to anyone who only wants drama. The audience at her show is told to hush, or mama bear will respond.","src":"haiku"},
"8-graves~bury-me-low":{"haiku":"Preoccupation with death emerges as response to endemic suffering; the narrator constructs meaning through morbid contemplation and acceptance of annihilation.","opus":"A darkly calm meditation on wanting death as relief from relentless pain, bargaining with God over unanswered pleas, resigned that some burdens are simply carried to the grave. Despair worn plainly.","sonnet":"A speaker exhausted by nightmares, moral confusion, and a body growing cold makes a single request — to be buried low when the time comes — and acknowledges that some burdens, including the ones they carry for others, are meant to go to the grave.","src":"haiku"},
"8mm~give-it-up":{"haiku":"Self-harm and isolation; a person bleeds in secret while resisting help, believing they deserve only suffering.","opus":"The Los Angeles duo craft a hushed intervention aimed at someone drowning in self-pity. The narrator refuses to be the audience for another's misery, suspecting they want company rather than help. Bandaged palms and unseen blood frame a plea to get off their knees and run. Beneath the tenderness sits a hard truth about the fear that all one has is all one deserves.","sonnet":"An address to someone who performs suffering for company rather than help. The speaker refuses to absorb the wound-display and instead pushes back — bandage the hands, get up off the knees, run. Underlying the impatience is a harder question: whether what this person has is all they believe they deserve.","src":"haiku"},
"8obes~gasoline-tears":{"haiku":"Toxic interdependence mimics vital substance; the beloved becomes poison consumed like oxygen, destruction inseparable from survival.","opus":"Obsessive love as literal consumption, wanting to be breathed in and live inside another person's skin. The desire is so total it curdles into self-annihilation, addiction dressed in the imagery of drowning and burning.","sonnet":"Toxic attraction framed as combustion — the speaker burns to be breathed in by someone whose poison feels like a drug, sinks to the bottom and wakes on their lips, and mixes gasoline with tears as the cost of wanting it. The desire is fully conscious and fully self-destructive.","src":"haiku"},
"9million~when-the-kissing-had-to-stop":{"haiku":"Cyclical return despite resignation; separation guarantees reunion while the speaker oscillates between departure and compulsion, unable to maintain distance.","opus":"A gentle meditation on leaving and mortality, the belief that the soul outlasts the body and that love survives even death. Someone who kept trying to walk away finally accepts the parting while promising return.","sonnet":"A meditation on leaving and returning across what may be death itself — the speaker has tried to leave many times, caught up in tomorrows, and believes the soul survives the body and will come back. The weight felt this morning and the peace sought are placed against a faith that even the end is not a final goodbye.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~21-g":{"src":"web","web":"Takes its title from the disputed 21-grams soul-weight theory to explore emotional fragility and mortality — the narrator clings to love as a handrail on unstable ground, accepting that everyone eventually disappears while still reaching for connection."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~a-10wqn45":{"src":"web","web":"A relentless, defiant protagonist burns through an endless nightmare of a night, refusing to surrender their identity to fate or darkness, choosing to consume hardship rather than be consumed by it."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~a-13n236a":{"haiku":"World destruction or apocalyptic imagery; systems collapse as the core metaphor.","opus":"9mm Parabellum Bullet fuse metal precision with punk-pop urgency, and their anthems tend to dignify the unglamorous, as this one does. It salutes anonymous everyday heroes who fight unwinnable battles without fleeing, who smile because retreat is unthinkable, fall down and look up at the sky. They survive just to meet again next week, protecting what they love even as it protects them back.","sonnet":"An anthem for anonymous everyday heroes who fight unwinnable battles without running, who smile because they refuse to retreat, fall down and look up at the sky, and keep surviving just to meet again next week — protecting what they love even while being protected by it.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~a-15rgehd":{"haiku":"Summer romance and temporal preservation—the beloved's presence makes endless summer possible; the narrator craves extending these perfect moments indefinitely.","opus":"Known for velocity and tremolo attack, 9mm Parabellum Bullet slow into something almost still here. A summer love song whose entire wish is that the other person simply stay nearby so the season never ends, no miracle required. Their presence alone turns an ordinary beach evening into a permanent one, the band's usual momentum traded for the fantasy of an unmoving moment held forever.","sonnet":"A summer love song where the narrator's entire wish is simply for this person to stay nearby so the season never has to end — with no miraculous event required, just the other person's presence making an ordinary beach evening feel like a permanent miracle.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~a-1947rxf":{"src":"web","web":"A rallying anthem against apathy and materialism in which the narrator vows to ring out a rebellious march, using personal failures as fuel to keep fighting huge walls, and calls on listeners to reclaim the courage and love they have forgotten."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~a-1nf7hr3":{"src":"web","web":"A narrator declares enduring love for someone living in a fragile, glass-like city, finding beauty even in broken fragments and the inevitability of decay — the city and the love will both turn to sand, but the devotion remains."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~a-1slgc58":{"src":"web","web":"On a brutal battlefield of suffering, the narrator rejects the idea that struggle is futile and pledges to keep fighting not for personal victory but to stand alongside someone they love, risking their soul for that bond."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~a-2obn61":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness and ritual persistence—frozen in indifference, the speaker repeatedly lights candles to preserve warmth and memories while bearing existential weight.","opus":"9mm Parabellum Bullet often let melodic melancholy surface beneath the aggression, and this grief song is nearly all melancholy. Candles are lit not for warmth but to preserve the memory of a lost voice and body, in a cold city where even prayer has been abandoned. The flame becomes a private ritual of faithfulness to someone dead or departed, devotion kept alive by a single stubborn light.","sonnet":"A song about grief and devotion to a lost person: the narrator lights candles not for warmth but to preserve the memory of someone's voice and body, in a cold city where even prayer has been abandoned — the candle is a private ritual of faithfulness to the dead or departed.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~a-3omzz":{"opus":"The band's fondness for extremity turns romantic here, love framed as voluntary drowning. The narrator asks to be sunk to the bottom and left there, because without this bond they would never have known the particular sorrow of loving someone. Even as bubbles form and break at once, the attachment persists, 9mm's usual intensity redirected into a willingness to disappear for the sake of feeling.","sonnet":"A love song that frames deep emotional attachment as voluntary drowning: the narrator asks to be sunk to the bottom and stay there, because without this relationship they would never have known the particular sorrow of loving someone — even as bubbles that form and break immediately, their bond persists.","src":"sonnet"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~a-8l20rs":{"opus":"9mm Parabellum Bullet make forward motion an ethos, and this track is that ethos as manifesto. The narrator refuses to hide in silence waiting to be called, pressing forward step by step and carving each instant into something permanent. There are reasons not to lose; the storm should never merely pass but be the one they generate themselves, agency seized rather than awaited.","sonnet":"A song about refusing to wait passively for a moment to act: the narrator will not hide in silence until called upon, but will press forward step by step, carving each instant into something permanent — there are reasons not to lose, and the storm should never pass but be the one they generate.","src":"sonnet"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~a-8mlr3e":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"9mm Parabellum Bullet like to wrap devotion in strange cosmology, and here separation stretches across a hundred years. A city of white nights where the sun never sets and wishes have no target frames a narrator who has stopped hoping to return yet vows to keep going. One answer is carried toward a loved one, survival reduced to the single aim of meeting again.","sonnet":"A hundred years of separation and a city of white nights where the sun never sets and wishes have no target — the narrator has stopped hoping to go back but promises to keep going, carrying one answer toward the person they love, surviving just to meet again.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~album-ver":{"haiku":"A desperate plea to ignite the heart with irreversible fire—fused to someone else, wounds festering, impossible to heal alone.","opus":"An address to a scarred, self-blind lover whose old wounds' scabs have peeled and started aching again—wounds 'you can't cover alone.' The refrain 'set your heart on fire so it never goes out again' urges burning away loneliness, secrets, unease, and time until 'won't we turn to ash?'—a plea to fuse two people, burn everything, and love each other into ash.","sonnet":"Two people entangled in lies and old wounds neither can address alone — the narrator wants to set fire to all of it: the loneliness, the secrets, the scabs that have started hurting again — and asks the other person to burn with them rather than go back to being alone. The emotional core is the destructive intimacy of people who can only reach each other through mutual combustion.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~answer-and-answer":{"src":"web","web":"An urgent, high-velocity song that repeats the refrain \"Do you know Answer?\" as a call to confront love and life head-on, turning images of torn wings and a sinking ship into a declaration that the journey is not over and no one faces it alone."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~arechi":{"haiku":"Desolation demands remembrance; the speaker pleads against erasure of scattered flowers, seeking witness to isolated grief nobody comprehends.","opus":"A wasteland ballad about clinging to one broken promise when everything else is stripped bare. The wind tastes of solitude, but the plea that a single person remember him is what keeps him walking toward the sunset town.","sonnet":"A solitary figure in a wasteland, fingers torn by thorns and held against a setting sun, asks only one person to remember him and the impossible number of promises exchanged — a modest prayer of rain for withered flowers. The wind of the wasteland tastes of loneliness, and the city below is the destination, not a home.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~atarashii-hikari":{"haiku":"New light fails to heal old wounds; despite brightness and future possibility, blood continues flowing while pain persists beneath surface transformation.","opus":"A vow to lead someone out of darkness into new light, to strip away every false surface until only the truth of two names remains. Love as rebirth, insisting the wound still bleeds because the person is still alive.","sonnet":"A vow to lead someone into new light — wounds that no longer hurt still bleed, a voice swallowed by darkness is still there somewhere, and the only words needed are each other's names. Under a newborn sun everything is stripped bare and the truth reveals itself unchanged whatever color eyes you look through it with.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~babel-no-kodomotachi":{"src":"web","web":"Drawing on the Tower of Babel, the song mourns the loss of childlike innocence as people grow up into betrayal and disillusionment, with a repeated plea — children of Babel, don't grow up — insisting that authenticity matters more than the pretense of adulthood."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~beautiful-dreamer":{"src":"web","web":"Addresses a person who lives entirely on dreams, asking what kind of creature can survive that way, and contemplating the transience of human bonds and the inevitability that everyone eventually becomes a distant star."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~black-market-blues-album-mix":{"haiku":"Moral corruption spreads as spectacle; innocent sheep wander markets where deception sells and suffering becomes commodity consumed casually.","opus":"A seductive descent into a marketplace where even truth and grief are bought and sold, and the singer wants to drive you mad enough to dance in it. Nihilism as invitation, the pleasure of selling your soul to a world already abandoned.","sonnet":"A black market where even truth and sorrow are sold, and lost, directionless people queue up to have their frustrations ripped apart and sold off — the speaker momentarily wants to join them, to sell everything and just dance. The market is the city's shadow economy of emotional bankruptcy.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~carry-on":{"haiku":"Broken dreams stitch together into forward motion; the speaker drags their companion across impossible distance. Past and future blur under constant rhythm; surrender means staying still while momentum demands continuation.","opus":"A Japanese rock anthem about pressing forward through wreckage, dragging a torn dream and a companion along toward a dawn that keeps receding, betting that sheer momentum will outrun both past regret and future fear.","sonnet":"Two companions press forward together, stitching a torn dream back together behind a migrating bird's white wings — \"this world's companion\" — and refuse to stop moving until the heartbeat overtakes both past and future, pushing through destiny's obvious traps toward a long-awaited dawn.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~caucasus":{"haiku":"Nocturnal anonymity dissolves identity; repeated cycles consume meaning while shadows dissolve individual form into undifferentiated darkness.","opus":"Waking between the disposable words of yesterday and the blinding white morning, aware that the darkness stays close and only changes shape. A young man's unease that his own shadow follows him into every fresh start.","sonnet":"Waking and dreaming collapse into each other — yesterday's words are tomorrow's garbage, an almost-vanishing song hums at the edge of hearing, and a cold blue moon marks the end of night before a blinding white morning arrives. Darkness is always close, always reshaping itself into the shadow of one's own self.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~cold-edge":{"src":"web","web":"A dark, introspective song in which the narrator, addressed through recurring blade imagery, contemplates disappearance and asks one specific person to understand that this is not flight from accumulated sins but something deeper and irresolvable."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~deep-blue":{"src":"web","web":"An intense romantic song about the disorienting vulnerability of new love, in which the narrator, overwhelmed and emotionally dependent, reaches toward a partner to push through darkness together, with deepening blue as the central metaphor."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~discommunication":{"src":"web","web":"Two people spend the whole night drawing close to each other yet perpetually pass each other by; the narrator knows they are exactly what the other is searching for but cannot bridge the gap, capturing the paradox that speaking and moving can make genuine connection harder, not easier."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~everyone-is-fighting-on-this-stage-of-lonely":{"src":"web","web":"Every person is locked in their own private battle on a stage of loneliness, but the song turns outward with a call to courage — stand up for your pride, fight with bravery, and refuse to give up even when no one else is cheering for your survival."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~garasu-no-machi-no-alice":{"src":"web","web":"A narrator declares enduring love for someone living in a fragile, glass-like city, finding beauty even in broken fragments and the inevitability of decay — the city and the love will both turn to sand, but the devotion remains."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~getting-better":{"src":"web","web":"An optimistic but restless anthem about perpetual self-renewal — yesterday's achievements become obsolete, and the only response is to keep creating while breaking things down, sustained by the unwavering refrain that things are still, surely, getting better."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~heart-ni-hi-wo-tsukete":{"haiku":"Two figures burn their bonds deliberately, refusing to return to solitude. Fire consumes them both—mutual destruction becomes love's final expression.","opus":"Old scars that a person carries without seeing them can't be closed alone, so the speaker urges setting the heart itself on fire to burn away loneliness, secrets, and time. What begins as consolation escalates into a plea to burn together and love before both are reduced to ash, tenderness and self-immolation fused.","sonnet":"Two people entangled in each other's scars and lies spend a rough midnight together; the speaker proposes burning everything — loneliness, secrets, anxiety, time itself — down to ash, because the connection is too knotted to return to solitude and the only way through is to love each other into cinders.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~hi-no-tori":{"src":"web","web":"Using the phoenix as its central symbol, the song urges a listener to shed regrets, break through hardship, and spread their burning wings — resilience and rebirth through suffering are the only path to brighter days."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~i-c-r-a":{"src":"web","web":"A tense, desire-charged track from the BABEL album where the narrator bristles at endless teasing and demands emotional action over words, with surveillance-like imagery underscoring the urgency of mutual understanding over talk."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~ice-cream":{"src":"web","web":"A portrait of a destructive, irreparable relationship in which both parties cycle through breaking apart and reconciling, the emotions melting and spilling beyond recovery, until the narrator arrives at resigned numbness."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~itsumademo":{"src":"web","web":"A romantic ballad of unwavering devotion in which the narrator, admitting weakness, finds purpose and forward motion through a partner's presence and promises to stay together forever despite an uncertain future."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~karma-no-hanawa":{"src":"web","web":"The song contemplates being trapped in endless karmic cycles of conflict and suffering, with gears spinning out of control and a recurring, unanswered question — can flowers still bloom amid all this chaos?"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~kimi-wa-sakura":{"src":"web","web":"A bittersweet farewell song that uses cherry blossom imagery to capture the moment of graduation and separation, tracing a tender arc from shared memories to a tearful, grateful goodbye."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~long-goodbye":{"haiku":"Accepting departure and meaninglessness; an endless goodbye delivered through songs that break everything down.","src":"haiku","web":"A slow, melancholic farewell in which the narrator questions whether their existence has any meaning in the world while bidding a drawn-out goodbye to someone they love, resigned to abandoned dreams and scattered affection."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~namonaki-hero":{"src":"web","web":"A hard-driving anthem that reframes heroism as quiet, ordinary persistence — the nameless person who keeps showing up, laughs to avoid escape, and finds strength precisely through mutual dependence on others."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~natsu-ga-tsuduku-kara":{"src":"web","web":"A nostalgic summer love song set against beaches and sunset waves, where the presence of a beloved person makes the season feel suspended — as long as you're by my side, summer never ends."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~nemuri-hime":{"src":"web","web":"Two people who knowingly consumed something poisonous together are bound by shared guilt and mutual complicity; the narrator pleads not to be woken from the long dream, preferring a shared illusion over facing the crumbling reality of what they've done."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~psychopolis":{"haiku":"Mental fragmentation persists despite awareness; conscience splits from action while the speaker loses agency trapped between siren and police pursuit.","opus":"City sirens bleeding through an open window while the narrator confesses he can't tell what's irreplaceable and doesn't care who dies. He'd trade truth itself for the simple ability to believe and to love someone beyond himself.","sonnet":"Sounds from outside — children playing, an ambulance — contrast with a barely audible inner voice tracked by a guilt-siren, as the speaker confesses not knowing what is irreplaceable and not caring who dies, wanting belief more than truth. The city's noise is the psychopolis of the title, and the speaker is both its inhabitant and its disturbance.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~punishment":{"opus":"9mm Parabellum Bullet sometimes fracture narrative into shrapnel, and this track is pure aftermath. Exposed life-science, fading wings, unrepeatable memories, a bare heart, a burial expression, burned traditions, spiral zeros, a forgotten person: less a story than an inventory of what the punishment left behind. The band's velocity here scatters meaning into fragments too fast to reassemble into consolation.","sonnet":"A compressed, fragmented catalogue of aftermath — exposed life-science, fading wings, unrepeatable memories, a bare heart, a burial expression, burned traditions, spiral zeros, a forgotten person. The song is almost a list of things the punishment has left behind rather than a narrative.","src":"sonnet"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~seimei-no-waltz":{"haiku":"Despite incomprehensible times, the speaker and companion waltz together toward shared destination. Their eyes glimpse singular futures despite diverging paths; pulse and voice sustain motion.","opus":"A defiant address to a companion facing a crooked age, insisting that different lives still arrive at the same destination and that a song remains when hands and feet fail. The image of dancing a waltz along the lifeline turns survival into shared music, the voice of life to be heard here and now rather than someday elsewhere.","sonnet":"Two people with different paths share one destination and one future — chosen over every coin with no reverse — and the speaker insists that songs exist precisely for those moments when strength fails, demanding that the voice of life be heard right here, right now, not deferred to some someday and somewhere.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~sorekara":{"src":"web","web":"A speaker chooses to suffer alongside their partner rather than escape, embracing a turbulent, pain-laced love as an act of deliberate devotion — the devil's whisper turns out to be their own voice accepting the cost of staying."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~story-of-glory":{"src":"web","web":"An anthem about the band's own unfinished story, pushing back against the nostalgic idea that one's best days are already over — the lyrics open mid-narrative, insisting the story continues and the stage is still alive."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~suna-no-wakusei":{"haiku":"Isolation protects through vigilance; without divine intervention the speaker survives through self-reliance and willful refusal to accept help from others.","opus":"An angel dies unmourned while everyone looks away and protects the wrong things to save themselves. The answer is to smash even what you fear losing and go down laughing hand in hand, choosing shared ruin over cowardly survival.","sonnet":"Angels die alone without farewell — refusing to grieve or be grieved for, they destroy what they fear losing before it can be taken, and sink laughing hand-in-hand with the wreck. God is asked what comes next in a desert sketched with tears, and the answer is that both the ark and the mud-boat get smashed.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~supernova":{"haiku":"Touching fragility transforms entire worlds into fragmented constellations; burning bright and forgotten, leaving only scattered remains.","opus":"Cosmic and tender, the narrator recalls being embraced by a sun of impossible warmth and now wishes to help the one who held him, watching a sea of shattered stars beyond the full moon. 9mm Parabellum Bullet's Japanese post-hardcore lets this imagery of shattering hearts and streaming meteors turn a farewell into something astronomical, pleading that the burned, gleaming fragments never be gathered up.","sonnet":"Beneath a full moon and in the imagery of exploding stars, a fragile person reaches out to save someone — a touch so light it shatters any chest it meets. The scattered fragments of a broken star become the metaphor for a love that wants to gather rather than collect, shining to burn rather than to possess.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~teenage-disaster":{"haiku":"A child witnesses coastal decay—iron-taste rain and luminous tears mark inhabitants of this drowned place. Sorrow becomes their element, passed down to all who drink its waters.","opus":"A dreamlike, circular lament set in a seaside town where children bury their hearts in the sand and the water tastes of iron. A portrait of youthful numbness and the quiet resignation of belonging somewhere sorrowful.","sonnet":"A coastal town at the shoreline of stars and moonlight is a place where hearts are buried in sand under perpetual rain — drinking it down leaves a taste of iron — and everyone who settles there eventually sheds dull, gleaming tears.","src":"haiku"},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~wanderland":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator walks a patchwork world divided by borders that are described as history's wounds, searching for a cure while accepting they may never stop wandering — the only hope for a new map lies in the eyes of children."},
"9mm-parabellum-bullet~white-out":{"src":"web","web":"Memories of a lost intimacy are painted over in white like fresh snow, making it impossible to return to the innocence of before; the narrator holds vivid flashes of laughter and intertwined fingers against an encroaching blankness, caught between remembering and letting go."},
"a-10x9imv~a-118uxj0":{"haiku":"Nostalgic valorization of youthful street delinquency—celebrating past recklessness and lawbreaking in a specific place while acknowledging positive personal growth.","opus":"This is nostalgia rock anchored to a real map, memories fixed to a specific neighborhood in Abiko City. It looks back on youthful delinquency, the drinking and brawling, ambushing rivals and outrunning police, with genuine fondness for that reckless earlier self. Yet the perspective is settled and grown, affection for the past held alongside a clear preference for who the narrator has since become.","sonnet":"A nostalgia song about a specific neighborhood in Abiko City, anchored in memories of youthful delinquency — drinking, brawling, ambushing enemies, running from police — filtered through the perspective of someone who looks back fondly on that reckless version of himself while preferring who he's become now.","src":"haiku"},
"a-12lcfqf~a-36hus2":{"opus":"This song runs in place across centuries of solitude, locked inside apartments while the world builds walls around every human heart. It asks why we wall ourselves off, burns old bridges that have outlived their days, and arrives alone at the platform. The recurring question, why we construct a world where loneliness reigns in every chest, gives the endless running its quietly civic despair.","sonnet":"An endless run in place inside locked apartments across centuries of solitude — asking why we build walls around human hearts, burning old bridges that have outlived their days, and arriving alone at the platform, questioning why we construct a world where loneliness reigns in every chest.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-13jb0n9~climber-s-high":{"haiku":"Breaking free from societal constraints through relentless ambition; the speaker defies limitation and reaches for an impossible dream with burning determination.","opus":"Built on the mountaineer's term for adrenaline-fueled euphoria at altitude, the track dramatizes exactly that surge. Someone suffocating in an alienating daily routine decides to smash out of it, tearing through fear, regret, and the absence of a person once shared. The breakthrough opens into infinite white sky and peak determination, the climber pushing past every limit rather than stopping to catch her breath.","sonnet":"Someone suffocating in a repetitive, alienating daily routine decides to smash out of it — tearing through fear, regret, and the absence of someone once shared — and reaches a state of euphoric, peak-adrenaline determination where the sky opens into infinite white and she keeps climbing past all limits.","src":"haiku"},
"a-14dj6bp~exterminate":{"haiku":"Transcendent love that validates existence; discovering deep connection overcomes fear and transforms the speaker's understanding of what's worth fighting for.","opus":"This is a love song disguised as combat, liberation staged with a violent title. The singer has been afraid to truly know the beloved or to lose them, until that person's joy finally hardens her own resolve. She vows to release everything and sing with her whole soul toward a shared future, love itself cast as the one force that refuses to be exterminated.","sonnet":"A love song framed as liberation: the singer has been afraid to truly know or lose the beloved, but that person's joy has finally hardened her own resolve, and she vows to release everything, sing with her whole soul toward a shared future, while love itself refuses to be exterminated.","src":"haiku"},
"a-14dj6bp~testament":{"haiku":"Unstoppable passion and commitment to one's beliefs; despite being cast out or broken, the inner flame burns eternally and drives the speaker to protect what they love.","opus":"A companion in spirit to the same artist's defiant streak, this track makes music and love inextinguishable forces. Even when the world abandons the singer and grief clouds the future, the inner flame of conviction cannot be put out. She vows to keep singing toward the beloved no matter what collapses, the title turning personal resolve into something sworn and permanent, a declaration meant to outlast ruin.","sonnet":"A passionate declaration that music and love are inextinguishable forces — even when the world abandons you and grief clouds the future, the inner flame of conviction cannot be put out, and the singer vows to keep singing toward the beloved no matter what collapses around them.","src":"haiku"},
"a-15peidl~borderland":{"haiku":"A meditation on indecision and moral ambiguity—standing at the borderline between victory and compassion, unable to choose a weapon.","opus":"The song plants itself on a threshold and refuses to step off, holding winning against being wounded, black against white, dream against reality. Rather than resolve the tension it chooses to live inside it, treating the borderland not as a waiting room but as the actual home ground. Flying higher, it insists, requires keeping both arms spread, balance itself made the destination.","sonnet":"A song about existing on the threshold between competing values — winning vs. being wounded, black vs. white, dream vs. reality — and choosing to stay balanced there rather than pick a side. The emotional core is the refusal to resolve the tension: the borderland is not a waiting room but the actual place where the narrator lives, and flying higher requires keeping both arms spread.","src":"haiku"},
"a-15peidl~joint":{"opus":"The title names the bond, and the lyric earns it: two people fused by an unspoken pact pressing through ruin and doubt. The singer was meant to travel alone but cannot release the hand that wakes something in the heart. Past grief and sorrow taught joy in equal measure, made possible only by the other's presence, and now they move together toward the next place.","sonnet":"Two people bound by an unspoken pact press forward through ruin and doubt — the singer was supposed to travel alone but cannot release the hand that wakes something in the heart. Past grief and sorrow taught joy in equal measure, and the presence of the other made that possible; now they move together toward the next place.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-16c0uzf~astral-dogma":{"opus":"Steampunk trappings meet Theosophical occultism in this account of a fallen golden city. A princess inherits her dying father's message that their age is ending, that the stars hold the key to another world, that humanity stopped evolving once it prostrated before gods. The lyric cycles through ether, akasha, astral projection, and the Lipika, functioning at once as lament for a lost civilization and prophecy of its renewal.","sonnet":"A steampunk-esoteric song about a fallen golden city: a princess inherits her dying father's message that their age is ending, stars hold the key to another world, and humanity stopped evolving because it prostrated itself before gods — the lyric cycles through occult cosmology (ether, akasha, astral projection, the Lipika) as both lament and prophecy of renewal.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-1ej9ak1~a-1bg030b":{"haiku":"Pervasive self-loathing and hopelessness about the future; the narrator already views themselves as dead inside.","opus":"Sung entirely in Russian, this is self-contempt taken to its floor. The narrator cannot understand himself, foresees only lifelong loneliness, and admits to having poisoned himself, hating himself more than he hates anyone else. There is no future on offer and no redemption sought, only a bleak surrender that arrives at death, the language of the lyric matching the airless finality of its outlook.","sonnet":"A Russian-language song of total self-contempt and hopelessness — the narrator doesn't understand himself, sees only lifelong loneliness, has poisoned himself and hates himself even more than he hates others, and insists there is no future, surrendering only at death.","src":"haiku"},
"a-1gp0cqi~keep-tryin":{"haiku":"Persistent resilience despite indifference—the narrator maintains optimism and keeps trying despite appearing apathetic, chasing dreams that keep them hungry and driven forward.","opus":"This is Utada Hikaru in playful but earnest mode, folding personal drive against Japanese social scripts. Despite laziness and loneliness, the narrator admits to being secretly hungry for more than the world offers, brushing off the time-is-money hustle and the marry-a-salaryman expectation others impose. Dissatisfaction itself becomes the engine, the encouragement extended outward until even parents and train conductors are told to keep trying too.","sonnet":"A playful but earnest Utada Hikaru song about the drive to keep striving despite laziness, loneliness, and social expectations: the narrator admits to being secretly hungry for more than the world offers, ignores the \"time is money\" and \"marry a salaryman\" scripts others impose, and insists that dissatisfaction itself is the engine — even parents and train conductors should keep trying.","src":"haiku"},
"a-1i4roko~a-1199uie":{"opus":"This song articulates the wound of being unable to receive love. The narrator's body and heart reject warmth and happiness, shaped by old injuries into a crooked form that can only request a love that is bloody and broken. The wish for kiss marks and binding is not appetite for suffering but the confession of someone for whom tenderness has become foreign and unbearable, damage speaking in the only grammar it knows.","sonnet":"A song about being unable to receive love without trauma: the narrator's body and heart reject warmth and happiness, shaped by old wounds into a crooked form that can only ask for a love that is bloody and broken — they want kiss marks and binding, not because they enjoy suffering but because tenderness feels foreign and unbearable.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-1j2z4t2~a-3f5ms6":{"haiku":"Transient beauty of cherry blossoms representing fleeting time and mortality; watching them fall mirrors the inevitability of loss and the impermanence of all things.","opus":"This is Japan reimagined as revolutionary spectacle, cherry blossoms at night beside guillotines, light-ray guns, geisha processions marching alongside ICBMs. The whole nation is staged as a steel-cage festival of grand violence where everyone is at once performer and condemned prisoner. The carnival keeps going even as people leap from the scaffold, beauty and slaughter fused into a single unstoppable procession.","sonnet":"A chaotic, militarized reimagining of Japan in revolutionary upheaval — cherry blossoms at night, guillotines, light-ray guns, geisha processions alongside ICBMs — staged as grand violent spectacle where everyone is simultaneously performer and condemned prisoner in a steel-cage festival that keeps going even as people leap from the scaffold.","src":"haiku"},
"a-1mbnjjg~a-18yuaso":{"haiku":"Longing for an idealized digital companion; a sailor-uniformed girl existing only in electrical signals substitutes for real human connection.","opus":"This track lives inside otaku fixation on a digitally-simulated little sister, someone who has always waved goodbye yet always stayed, preserved on loop inside the narrator's head. Long desperately wanted, she is now the object of a wish to live together forever. The obsession is framed without irony as the ultimate entertainment, a fantasy companion chosen precisely because she can never leave or change.","sonnet":"A fixation on a digitally-simulated little sister — someone who has always waved goodbye, always stayed, preserved in loop inside the narrator's head — whom the narrator has always desperately wanted and now wants to live with forever, framing this obsessive fantasy as the ultimate entertainment.","src":"haiku"},
"a-1mbnjjg~a-1ctpmq5":{"haiku":"Self-destructive non-romantic bond where the narrator accepts brokenness and inferiority while physically consuming their lover.","opus":"From the same fixated sensibility, this is a self-described loser asking permission to stay broken. Always a misfit in an ambiguous world, the narrator wants to keep biting at the neck of the person they crave, framing obsessive, barely-platonic attachment and ongoing mental illness as the only honest mode of existence they can manage. It is a plea to remain unwell rather than pretend at wellness.","sonnet":"A self-described loser who has always felt like a misfit in an ambiguous world asks permission to keep on being broken, to keep biting at the neck of the person they want — framing obsessive, barely-platonic attachment and ongoing mental illness as the only honest way they know how to exist.","src":"haiku"},
"a-1mbnjjg~a-1oecn73":{"haiku":"A painful exploration of memory and blame—all wounds trace back to a beautiful person; intimacy corrupted by adolescent hurt.","opus":"This song sits in the impossibility of a real goodbye to someone who inhabits every memory. The classroom is no place for farewells, and the narrator cannot stop blaming this person for occupying every corner of the past. The shift from blame to calling them beautiful exposes the trick: the failure to communicate is itself the love, adolescent obsession circling a door it will never find.","sonnet":"A song about the impossibility of truly saying goodbye to someone who lives in every memory — the classroom isn't a place for farewells, the narrator can't stop blaming this person for occupying every corner of their past, and the final shift from 'it's all your fault' to 'it's because you're beautiful' reveals that the failure of communication is itself a form of love. The emotional core is adolescent obsession that can't find the door out.","src":"haiku"},
"a-1u2w19~24reverse":{"haiku":"A meditation on false self and reversed memory—wanting to erase the person you once were and lose all trace of a past lover.","opus":"The title's reverse-reel imagery drives the whole conceit, a compulsion to run backward past midnight into memory, into the sensation of someone now gone. The lost night is replayed in reverse until it becomes unclear whether the memory or even the person ever existed. The emotional engine is the pull to reinhabit a moment that is irretrievably over, rewinding a tape that erases as it plays.","sonnet":"A song about trying to reverse back into the past — past midnight, into memory, into the sensation of someone who is gone — replaying the lost night in the mind's reverse-reel until it becomes unclear whether the memory or the person ever existed. The emotional core is the compulsion to inhabit a moment that is irretrievably over.","src":"haiku"},
"a-1u2w19~chocolate-passion":{"haiku":"Seductive manipulation and desire as intimate conquest; sensuality becomes a weapon of control, and the speaker finds power in dissolving another into pure sensation.","opus":"Sweetness becomes a language for power here, an explicitly sexual dynamic rendered through chocolate. The singer surrenders herself to dissolve into a lover while simultaneously claiming dominance, describing melting and being consumed as seduction and control at once. Pleasure and mutual destruction condense into a single crystallized moment, the confection's melt made into a metaphor for who yields and who commands.","sonnet":"An explicitly sexual power dynamic rendered through chocolate metaphors: the singer surrenders herself to dissolve into a lover while simultaneously claiming dominance, describing the act of melting and being consumed as seduction and control at once, with the pleasure of mutual destruction condensed into a single crystallized moment.","src":"haiku"},
"a-1u2w19~o-f-t":{"haiku":"Illusion of connection between two people who are fundamentally strangers; memories feel real but are just false stories, and both speaker and other are trapped watching a predetermined narrative collapse.","opus":"This is a portrait of two people matched in their capacity for self-deception, held together in a gray, hollow relationship at 10 PM. The narrator fears he will fade and vanish like a star as the transparent falseness of their shared story grows impossible to sustain. The intimacy is real only as performance, a mutual illusion both partners can feel dissolving even as they keep enacting it.","sonnet":"Two people who mirror each other in their capacity for illusion and self-deception are held together in a gray, hollow relationship at 10 PM, the narrator fearing he will fade and vanish like a star while the transparent falseness of their shared story becomes impossible to sustain.","src":"haiku"},
"385~a-1v83i96":{"haiku":"A cynical exposé of desire and deception—desire as a demonic trap, truth hidden beneath layers of manipulation.","opus":"The lyric stages a confrontation between the world's seductive lies and one's own true nature. The realm of desire is exposed as entirely false, a devil's illusion, and the only response is to search and destroy that fabricated identity until the raw, red, terrifying truth appears underneath. The emotional payoff is the painful clarity of stripping away everything you were trained to want.","sonnet":"A person confronting the gap between the world's seductive lies and their own true nature — the world of desire is exposed as entirely false, a devil's illusion, and the only response is to search and destroy that false identity to find the raw, red, terrifying truth underneath. The emotional core is the painful clarity that comes from stripping away everything you've been told to want.","src":"haiku"},
"a-2yw9ix~2":{"haiku":"Two people locked in secret intimacy that builds and corrupts incrementally, number by number, sinking deeper into their trap.","opus":"Goto dives off a second-floor window straight at her lover, plotting world domination on the way down, and calls herself, matter-of-factly, sick with dependency. The thrill she names is wanting to hurt the person she loves, pleasure and injury braided into every I-love-you. It's obsession diagnosed by the obsessive, who finds the diagnosis exhilarating.","sonnet":"A narrator who admits she is addicted to dependency dives at someone from a second-floor window, plots world domination, and thrills at the idea of hurting the person she loves — excitement and anguish spiral together into a loop of craving, self-destruction, and declarations of love she can't quite get out.","src":"haiku","web":"Two people share a secret intimacy—bodies close, a weeping guitar in the background—but the relationship is always changing, and the song closes with quiet resignation that their hidden bond will eventually end.","fable":"Two people on top of a transmission tower, and the song is a diving motion repeated as ecstasy, softly out the second-floor window aimed at you, then plunging backward mid-swoon while plotting to conquer the world. The refrain is the diagnosis stated outright, I'm sick with dependency, I want to hurt you, the budding pleasure is wonderful, tokimeki and anguish and I love you jammed into one breath. It reads the erotics of codependence with no irony to soften it: the height, the leap, the wish to wound the beloved are all one appetite, and the craving that surfaces when something is missing, I need to fix, I need a hit, is love and drug spoken in the same grammar. The song loops back to the dive unresolved, because the point is that there's no landing, only the falling, done again and again toward the person you can't be well without.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder"],"means":"the erotics of codependence diagnosed by the obsessive who finds it exhilarating","built":"the song loops back to the dive unresolved because there is no landing"},
"a-2yw9ix~5":{"haiku":"Bound by divine constraint, the speaker mourns lost purity and sings hollow songs, longing for connection but receiving only absence.","opus":"In an off-kilter five-beat, Goto keeps circling a night that feels bound by a cruel god and a red-tinged memory she cannot manage. She only ever wanted to live plainly, but calls herself dim and sad, playing dead when loneliness won't let her leave, so that even reaching for eternity or for a lover comes out as the love of a hollowed shell.","sonnet":"A speaker who wanted to live simply finds herself stranded, longing for someone she can't return to — she pretends to be an empty shell, tells herself she's fine while holding her breath, and finds only a hollow love in the pleasure she reaches for.","src":"haiku","web":"Written in an actual five-beat time signature, the song circles loneliness, emotional emptiness, and 'the empty shell of love'—a night bound by God closing in, desires conflicting with guilt, and resignation to psychological suffering.","fable":"Titled for its own odd meter, five beats, and the lopsided count is the whole feeling, a song that can't find steady footing. A night bound by god arrives and the singer wonders if she can get along with a face stained red, then admits the plainest thing on the record: I only wanted to go honestly, that's all it was. Everything after is the hollow-shell image she keeps returning to, pretending to be an empty husk, loving like an empty husk, seeing love in pleasure like an empty husk. Between them she wants eternity and cries a little, calls herself stupid, whispers I'm okay, I'm okay while holding her breath. It's the quietest song here and the saddest, a girl who wanted only to live sincerely narrating her own hollowing-out in real time, the refrain of the husk closing over her each time she reaches for something real.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"a girl narrating her hollowing-out reaching for something","built":"odd meter names the lopsided feeling unresolved"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-12penvp":{"haiku":"A daughter longs for her absent father, unable to reverse the irreversible rupture, watching transformation she cannot undo.","opus":"A child grieving an absent father — the man off at some club miming his chords while she waits at home. The break-it-and-rebuild refrain is a lie she keeps telling herself, since what was broken can't come back, and the sweetness sours into the closing image: unforgiven, she invites the dead man to dance over his own burial plot.","sonnet":"A grief-stricken address to a dead father — the speaker recalls his absences, his eccentricities (air guitar at live houses, sending carrier pigeons), and the impossibility of truth between people, ending in a surge of longing to see him again and a bitter, dancing dare in front of his grave.","src":"haiku","web":"In Kansai dialect, the speaker insists 'break it, break it, this is how you make things'—but then concedes 'what you've broken won't come back', longing to hear again the person who once sang of Chiharu's clumsy, irretrievable love.","fable":"Chiharu's love opens as demolition instruction, break it down and rebuild it like this, immediately undercut by the flat admission that what you broke doesn't come back, it's completely different now. The song keeps returning to a second confession, that it isn't telling the real truth, because the truth belongs to one person only and that's not possible. Between these loops sit two absurd, tender snapshots of an absent father, playing air guitar at the live house, sending carrier pigeons to his friends, images too silly to be anything but a child's fond cataloguing. Only late does the ache surface plainly, I want to go back, I've grown so big, I want to see that man again, and the singer names her own song as she sings it, admitting it sucks to be singing Chiharu's love. It ends on the unforgivable turned playful, a proposed dance in front of the grave, grief that can neither forgive the father nor stop loving him, so it does both at once.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","family & blood","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"grief that can't forgive the father but won't stop","built":"the singer names her own song mid-lyric as confession"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-13mcpvz":{"src":"web","web":"A cover of Flower Companyz's nighttime highway anthem about being grateful to be alive—driving through the dark with only headlights ahead, a raw affirmation that living is worth it."},
"a-2yw9ix~a-14h50vv":{"haiku":"Destruction and exhilaration: yearning for release through passionate intensity, breaking past restraint and doubt into primal sensation.","opus":"A manic exhortation to tear down a life full of blemishes and dread on the eve of some private collapse, where the only survival strategy is to make up your mind, run, and destroy the past-love, regret, self-so you can cross into the present.","sonnet":"A woman named Yukiko is urged to obliterate the unbearable weight of life — shame, violence, the chaos of 1998 — by biting through love, turning away from the past, and escaping with a kind of beautiful recklessness. Destruction is framed not as despair but as the only honest way forward.","src":"haiku","web":"A manic manifesto about destroying the false, domestically-acceptable version of yourself; 'Yukiko-san' is a totem for fractured womanhood—perfectly manicured and completely unhinged—moving through defiance and self-loathing toward a hollow, unresolved triumph.","fable":"DESTROY, screamed four times before a word of sense arrives, is the engine, and the song keeps detonating it against a courtly plea to a woman named Yukiko: once you steel yourself, lovely Yukiko, flee like you're vanishing and get past the now. The verses are pure overload junk drawer, cold war and violence, instinct and worldly desire and chastity all crammed against a cheerful shout of Come on, music, tension is FANTASTIC, and the effect is a mind that can only approach tenderness by first blowing everything up. Late the mask slips into Cutie Honey, the anime magical-girl who transforms by shedding her body, and the destruction turns confessional: tear apart love, turn from the past, and stop crying now, a good destroy. So the shrieking was never nihilism. It's the only violence big enough to clear space for someone too frightened to simply say be brave and live.","fv":2,"themes":["freedom & escape","anger & defiance","love & desire"],"means":"destruction as the only vocabulary large enough for tenderness","built":"the Cutie Honey invocation reframes annihilation as magical transformation","fvr":"2.4"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-1i4hikf":{"haiku":"Raw cry for liberation; demanding destruction of constraints and barriers to living fully.","opus":"Explosively brief, this is a cry from the limit that bursts straight through it. The collapse into cannot-anymore flips instantly into can-go, exhaustion converting into propulsion without transition. The whole experience is framed as the liberation of life itself, the track's extreme compression enacting the very rupture it describes, a breaking point that becomes a launch rather than a stop.","sonnet":"A raw, explosively short cry of being at the limit and then bursting through it — collapsing into \"can't anymore\" and flipping instantly into \"can go\" — framing the experience as the liberation of life itself.","src":"haiku"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-1l4zypg":{"haiku":"A playful summer horniness fantasy—releasing inhibition and primal desire, surrendering to instinct without restraint.","opus":"This is summer as pure appetite, the heightened undeniable pull of the season's sexuality. The body is wide open, fantasies run uncontrollable, and instinct overrides restraint until the heart vibrates at maximum and refuses to stop. There is no shame and no romance in it, only the honest admission that summer makes the body want what it wants, desire stated plainly and without apology.","sonnet":"A song about the heightened, undeniable pull of summer sexuality — the body is wide open, fantasies are uncontrollable, and instinct overrides restraint until the heart vibrates at maximum and won't stop. The emotional core is the liberation of appetite: not shame, not romance, just the honest admission that summer makes the body want what it wants.","src":"haiku"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-1m055a2":{"haiku":"Paralysis and doubt corrode the speaker's pitch and certainty; clinging to someone who won't choose them, trapped in romantic paralysis.","opus":"Frozen at four in the morning, Goto can't move, keeps saying so, and clings to a faith that leaves her weeping anyway. She listens for her own heartbeat and breath like proof she's still here, then admits that if the world ended tomorrow the grief would lift because the person she wants was never going to choose her. The romance is a joke she can't stop making about being unmovable.","sonnet":"Paralyzed at 4 a.m. and unable to move, a narrator cycles through grief as hollow as a Sahara crossing — until a heartbeat and a breath remind them they're still alive; tomorrow the world might end, the person they love won't choose them, but they still want to be kissed in public and held until something breaks.","src":"haiku","web":"Emotional paralysis—'I cannot move'—in unrequited love; the narrator lies awake at 4am calling it 'mundane romance', hearing heartbeats and breathing, unable to act despite the feelings that pin her in place.","fable":"A song titled Speed Beat whose whole body refuses to move: every verse dead-ends on the paralysis mantra ugokenai (I can't, I can't move), and every image of feeling gets tagged, with rising sarcasm, as romantic—the 4am warmth is a silly romantic, the grief like swimming the Sahara a powerless romantic. The one thing still going is involuntary: can you hear it, the pulse, the breath I'd nearly forgotten—so she offers a la-la-la in place of a self that can't act. Then the mask drops into a conditional cascade that says everything the deadpan wouldn't: if the world ended tomorrow the sadness would vanish, you won't choose me anyway, but wouldn't it be lovely, if there were more I'd kiss you in public, hold me till I break—capped by naante joudan yo (just kidding), romantic. Goto's whiplash between girlish and shrieking is the form of that irony: the beat races while the woman is frozen, and the confession has to be disowned in the same breath it's made, because for her romanticism is only sayable as a joke she immediately takes back.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"confession disowned in the same breath it is made","built":"the beat races while the body is frozen throughout"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-1su8an4":{"haiku":"A self-deprecating plea for survival and acceptance—beautiful yet disgusting, asking to be kept alive despite self-hatred.","opus":"A pitch-black plea in which a speaker begs to be spared from death on the grounds of her own adorableness, even as she calls herself repulsive and forgettable. The song holds tenderness and revulsion in one voice, drifting toward a farewell that treats being loved and being loathsome as the same thing.","sonnet":"A self-aware song about a 'disgusting girl' who knows people forget her, asks not to be allowed to die because she is 'so cute,' and carries on from yesterday without knowing where she's going. The emotional core is a darkly comic plea for survival from someone who holds her own ugliness and her own preciousness in the same breath, signing off with goodbye until they meet again.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is self-abuse in Kansai gutter slang, roughly gutless me, you idiot moron trash, but the song itself is nothing but a lullaby sung to the singer's own child self: sweet me, don't die, stay alive even once you're grown. It circles that plea and keeps flinching from it, muttering people are creatures who forget everything, so let's forget, starting with me, then hating itself for saying so. The one clear memory it can hold is loving the sparkling blue sky, offered just before a goodbye until we meet again. Read against its own vicious title, the tenderness is the shock: someone who calls herself trash can still only beg the innocent version of herself to survive, and the whole thing is a suicide note rewritten as its own refusal.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","shame & repression"],"means":"a suicide note rewritten as its own refusal, tenderness against a vicious title","built":"the self-abusive Kansai title frames a lullaby of survival addressed to the child self"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-2z16mt":{"haiku":"Obsession with a non-existent person drives dysphoria and disconnection, where the transparent rhythm of yearning replaces reality.","opus":"Goto keeps insisting the beloved was never real, that the whole thing was a dream she was merely kept alive inside of, and pins her private derangement on that phantom. Under it runs a see-through pulse she can't stop naming, as if the loneliness itself had a beat. The despair and the sudden burst of joy arrive in the same breath.","sonnet":"A narrator addresses someone who, as far as they're concerned, does not exist — a person they once lived through and longed to be filled by, now reduced to nothing but a feverish May slump and a transparent rhythm pulsing through a nervous system that no longer obeys.","src":"haiku","web":"A dissociated speaker addresses someone who 'doesn't exist', circling phrases of unfulfillment and despair—'I want to be fulfilled', 'it feels like it's ending'—while a relentless internal rhythm drives her toward psychological breakdown.","fable":"A love song addressed entirely to someone the singer keeps insisting doesn't exist: you don't exist, she repeats, and yet she wants to be filled by you, and every verse ends by pinning the blame for her unraveling on that absence anyway. Midori builds the whole thing on that impossible arithmetic — a longing aimed at a void, a you that is only ever a dream she was once kept alive by. Between verses the vocal drops into the transparent rhythm refrain four times over, an emptied-out pulse that stands in for the missing person; the crisis verse then names it clinically — the autonomic nerves won't fire right, there's nothing but despair — while a cry of joy leaks through the noise, so the breakdown and the ecstasy arrive on the same frequency. What sounds like heartbreak is closer to a diagnosis: the beloved dissolved into pure signal, and she keeps dancing to it because it's all that's left to hold.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind"],"means":"longing aimed at a void the singer declares doesn't exist","built":"the emptied-out rhythm refrain stands in for the missing person"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-38pwx":{"haiku":"A simple, stripped-down declaration of overwhelming love—the heart stolen, two people existing alone together in devotion.","opus":"Barely a fragment, this micro-lyric says everything by saying almost nothing: a declaration of love, a stolen heart, two people alone against the world. The brevity is the whole statement, refusing any elaboration that might dilute it. Within this artist's catalogue of unstable, self-lacerating narrators, its stripped simplicity registers as its own kind of extreme, devotion with no room left for doubt.","sonnet":"A micro-lyric, barely a fragment: a declaration of love and a stolen heart, two people alone against the world. The brevity is the whole statement — nothing more complicated than that.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is the baldest possible statement of infatuation: I love you, my heart was stolen, the two of us alone in the world. Nothing complicates it, no turn, no shadow, and the flatness is the point on a record where every other track is a nervous breakdown set to jazz. This is the girlish half of Goto's vocal whiplash isolated and left standing alone, a sweetness so total it reads as either the calm before or the naivety the rest of the album exists to devour.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire"],"means":"unconditional sweetness left standing without complication","built":"the girlish vocal register isolated from the album's breakdown mode"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-38qy7":{"haiku":"Consuming machinery relentlessly, exhaustion and exhilaration collapsed into a paradoxical mantra.","opus":"Sixty characters, no more: eating the mecha is agony, and yet the machine is the greatest thing there is. The band just chants the robot's name into a wall until agony and worship stop being distinguishable. Brevity is the whole point; it's a devouring, delivered as a playground taunt.","sonnet":"A short, fierce chant: eating machines is painful, yet machines are the greatest thing — cycling between suffering and ecstasy in a burst of noise that lasts barely long enough to register.","src":"web","web":"A cyclic, almost comedic tug-of-war with mechanization: 'mecha is tough to eat' versus 'mecha is the best', evoking a person simultaneously worn down and exhilarated by the pull of the mechanical world.","fable":"The whole lyric is four lines and one word. First a flat complaint—eating meka is rough, eating meka is rough—then the word meka fired off sixteen times in a row and slammed shut with a verdict: it's the best. Nothing in between, no reason offered, no object clarified (meka as machine, as mechanism, as the cold clatter of the thing itself). The song refuses to be about anything, and that refusal is the point: Midori runs the jazz quartet as a deadpan machine while Goto's voice does the only work, swinging the exact same syllable from a groan of suffering into an ecstatic chant. The joke and the terror are the same gesture—that whatever grinds you down and whatever you can't stop craving might be one word, repeated until complaint and worship become indistinguishable.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"one syllable swung from groan of suffering to ecstatic chant","built":"complaint and worship become indistinguishable by the final repeat"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-38usw":{"haiku":"A naive girl entranced by false kindness is unknowingly led into human trafficking, her innocence mistaken for stupidity.","opus":"Osaka jazz-punk fronted by Mariko Goto's manic sing-speak, and this one is nastier than its nursery-rhyme bounce lets on. A giddy girl with her pills and her flipped skirt gets watched, cooed over, then quietly likened to a caged monkey, and the narrator realizes too late she is being trafficked, sold off overseas by the men carrying her while everyone laughs along.","sonnet":"A girl described as simple-minded and out of control — skirt flipping, running up to strangers, swallowing pills — is observed with a mix of alarm and pity, until the song reveals she is like a monkey being carried off by a basket maker and sold overseas, already too far gone to know it.","src":"haiku","web":"A darkly comic portrait of a naive, carefree girl—compared throughout to a caged monkey—who loves 'magic pills', befriends strangers without caution, and ends up trafficked and sold overseas, her innocence used against her.","fable":"A nursery singsong, heave ho like a monkey in a cage, wraps around a portrait of a girl who is a handful, weak in the head, keen on her magic pills, all smiles as she runs up to strangers. The cage refrain sounds like play until the last lines snap it shut: monkeys get picked up by basket makers and sold overseas, and the narrator realizes far too late that the girl had no idea what was being done to her. The whole song is that trap sprung in slow motion, the cheerful animal chant revealed as the sound of someone being trafficked while everyone claps along to the tune.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind"],"means":"a cheerful animal chant revealed as the sound of trafficking","built":"the nursery refrain seals the trap only after it has already closed"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-3cbw4s":{"haiku":"Spring memories dissolve: words fall away at season's end, carrying away the certainty and closeness that briefly shone.","opus":"One of Goto's rare tender songs, framed as a letter: Dear you, are you well, I haven't changed. She remembers a season shared so completely they could see even the unseeable, then watches words scatter at the end of spring. It's plainly elegiac, a farewell to a happiness that came only once, marveling quietly at how fragile all of it turns out to be.","sonnet":"A letter addressed to someone from the past — the writer unchanged, the shared season gone, the invisible things they once perceived together now vanished — watches words scatter like cherry blossoms at the end of spring and admits that a happiness which happens only once leaves nothing behind when it passes.","src":"haiku","web":"A letter written at the end of spring, addressed to someone from the past; words and memories scatter like petals, the 'once-in-a-lifetime happiness' already gone, and even things that were physically close have vanished.","fable":"A farewell letter set to a melody, which is the joke and the wound both: it opens with the stiff epistolary salutation you'd use for a stranger, asks how you've been, insists nothing about me has changed since back then. That insistence is the tell. Everything else has vanished, the once-only happiness, even the things that were sitting right beside you, so being unchanged isn't loyalty here, it's being left behind, stranded in a season that ended. The refrain hangs on ちる, the verb for petals coming loose from the branch, applied not to blossoms but to words: at the end of spring the words fall, how fleeting, how weightless. And the song's whole horror lives in one particle. The line runs 言葉はちるよ three times and then, once, slips to 言葉がちるよ, the topic marker giving way to the subject marker, the difference between the words fall, as a known sad fact and it's the WORDS that are falling, a small grammatical flinch of noticing it happen in real time before the phrasing seals back over and repeats. On a jazz-punk record built around Goto's shriek, this deadpan prettiness is the mask, and the thing underneath is that mechanical repetition of how fleeting isn't wistfulness, it's a person saying the consoling sentence over and over because they cannot make it become true.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"being unchanged framed as being stranded rather than loyal","built":"a single particle shift from topic to subject marker enacts the fall in real time"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-7f96vp":{"haiku":"Relentless sadness that cannot be stopped: love cannot cure the decay, and in the climax of desire lies only hollow clarity.","opus":"Goto weaponizes a love song, chanting a threat to kill over and over as the only vocabulary her wrecked interior can find. The horror is how the murder-refrain and the declaration of love become the same gesture, an empty heart forcing out devotion it cannot feel, chasing a purity and a climax it will never reach before it simply vanishes.","sonnet":"A speaker consumed by grief and a violent, hollow love repeats threats to kill the person they cannot stop loving, while a parallel thread describes a desperate lust that falls just short of release and briefly touches something pure before collapsing back into unending sadness.","src":"haiku","web":"Sadness that simply won't stop; the speaker issues violent threats alongside confessions of love 'with a heart empty of love', depicting inescapable emotional paralysis and self-destructive patterns that loop without resolution.","fable":"The title means sad days, and the song builds its sadness entirely by driving one homicidal chant, I'll kill you, into a love song until the two are the same sentence: with a heart empty of love, I love you. The middle abandons the chant for something rawer, an orgasm that can't quite be reached, and at the far end of that lust a glimpse of purity caught almost by accident, arousal curdling into a hint of the truth. Then the killing resumes and simply refuses to end, the sadness won't stop repeated flatly until repetition is the meaning. It reads as violence pointed inward wearing an outward mask: the one being killed and the one who can't love and can't come are the same person, and the flat closing loop is what despair sounds like when it has run out of images.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"violence pointed inward wearing an outward murderous mask","built":"the killing chant refuses to end until repetition itself is the meaning"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-9dphtq":{"haiku":"The speaker transforms into an instrument, yearning to be held and destroyed as a means of receiving touch and attention.","opus":"Goto demands to be held, crushed, wrecked immediately, and then announces she's turned into a guitar, all so she can be squeezed close. The instrument she plays becomes the body she offers up; the joke is dead serious, want so total it dissolves the line between performer and gear. She'd rather be an object in someone's arms than a person kept at arm's length.","sonnet":"Desperate for physical contact — to be held, destroyed, undone right now — the narrator transforms: she becomes a guitar, an object made to be grabbed and embraced, because wanting to be held so badly turns a person into something that exists purely to be played.","src":"haiku","web":"The speaker declares she has turned into a guitar, wanting desperately to be picked up and held; the surreal transformation is a metaphor for total emotional dependency and the urgent, conflicted need for closeness.","fable":"The title announces the transformation as an accident that has already happened — I've turned into a guitar!! — and the song is the girlish wail of an object begging to be picked up and played. Almost every line is an imperative fired at a lover who is also the audience: hold me right now, break me right now, make a mess of me right now, and the baby-talk refrain gyuu-tto (squeeze me tight) repeated until it stops being tender and starts sounding like a thing wanting to be gripped, strummed, used. Goto's voice does the same whiplash the instrument does — a coo that shreds into a shriek — so the wish to be an object is not sung about but performed. The mask is pure kawaii submission; underneath, one clinically formal line cracks it open, asking to be tormented in the real thing, or in something like it, or in an imitation of the same, as if the singer already knows the intimacy she's begging for is a stage act and would take the counterfeit anyway. That is the read: the deadpan jazz quartet keeps its cool while she offers to become the guitar in their hands, and the horror-comedy is that being loved and being merely played have collapsed into the same demand.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"submission to being used framed as the highest form of desire","built":"one clinically formal line cracks the kawaii surface from inside"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-el88va":{"haiku":"A Buddhist meditation on impermanence and cosmic indifference—life decays, waves crash, ocean is distant, all currents lead to dissolution.","opus":"This is a meditation on a life that decays without ever ending, an existence that rots but will not dissolve. Carrying unchanging sadness, it drifts apart like flotsam and asks where it goes, into the universe, into space. The emotional core is the exhausted persistence of a consciousness that can neither die cleanly nor find peace, drifting outward toward no destination at all.","sonnet":"A meditation on a life that decays without ending — this existence that rots but doesn't dissolve, carrying unchanging sadness, dissolving like flotsam, asking where it goes: into the universe, into space. The emotional core is the exhausted persistence of a consciousness that can neither die cleanly nor find peace, drifting outward with no destination.","src":"haiku"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-faumb5":{"haiku":"Uncontrollable impulses toward adulthood and romance block every path, leaving only the burning wait for transformation.","opus":"An eighty-seven-character burst and nothing else: the manic-depressive urge won't be stopped, so Goto flings herself at it, begging to be made an adult, made someone's lover, burning to wait. There's no verse to develop because the impulse doesn't develop; it just repeats and overrides her, which is exactly what it feels like from inside.","sonnet":"An unstoppable manic-depressive surge and an urge toward repression cycle against each other as a voice begs to be made an adult, begs to be made a lover, burning and yielding in turns — a longing for transformation that can't stop itself long enough to happen.","src":"web","web":"Uncontrollable manic-depressive impulses drive the speaker to plead 'make me into an adult, make me your lover', surrendering herself entirely while bidding farewell to any idealized version of life.","fable":"The lyric is a near-total loop: two paired couplets, then the first couplet returns verbatim, so the machine closes exactly where it opened. Its engine is a manic-depressive impulse that can't be stopped, twinned with a repression-impulse that can't be crossed — opposite pulls that both dead-end in the identical masochistic posture of waiting, body scorched then surrendered. And the demands are childish ones, quoted like a performance: make me an adult, make me your lover, each pleaded from a self that can only beg to be raised or claimed, never do either. That gap is the Midori move — Goto's shriek-to-coo whiplash exposing the plea as the very thing that can't mature past its own cycle. So the title's goodbye to a perfect world is the joke: nothing departs. Desire that files itself as growth is just the same demand looping back to burn again.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"desire that files itself as growth loops back to burn again","built":"the lyric closes verbatim on its own opening, enacting the cycle it names"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-hkv648":{"haiku":"A self-parodying drunk declaration of chaotic love—saying I love you while admitting cheating temptations, clinging desperately on the last train.","opus":"This is longing and infidelity compressed onto the last train home. The narrator overhears gossip about a woman cheating with a guitarist, watches a girl cry in the carriage, and wants to break the comfortable relationship keeping them apart but cannot say a word. The repeated declaration of loving someone most turns ironic, ending in the admission that love is sad because two different loves are at war.","sonnet":"A song about infidelity and longing on the last train — the narrator overhears gossip about a woman cheating with a guitarist, watches a girl cry on the train, wants to break the comfortable relationship that keeps them apart, and can't say any of it out loud. The repeated 'I love you most' refrain becomes ironic: the song ends with 'love is sad' because the love being declared and the love being suppressed are two different things.","src":"haiku"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-l0arox":{"haiku":"Happiness is terrifying; an over-intellectualized cat drowns in expectations and cannot escape the pursuit of revolution and self.","opus":"Titled for a feedback inferno, the song lives inside the contradiction it chants — joyful and terrified in one breath, homesick and stuck. Goto casts herself as the overburdened too-smart cat and the revolution's last fugitive, sharp enough to name her own trap yet unable to reach the light, begging to be dragged down below if that would end the search for somewhere to belong.","sonnet":"A figure paralysed by homesickness and emotional overload — described as an intelligent cat crushed by expectations, a last revolutionary on the run — circles between happiness and fear, unable to reach any light, and begs to be taken somewhere, even hell, just to have a place to go.","src":"haiku","web":"Time passes like a torment, homesickness runs on a parallel line that never closes, and 'happiness is scary' is chanted as a haunting refrain until the song ends with a desperate plea: 'someone, take me to hell.'","fable":"Howling hell, named for the feedback shriek that is the band's native tongue, runs two states side by side and refuses to rank them: happy, scary, happy, scary, chanted flat until they stop being opposites. Around that pulse sit two recurring self-portraits, an intelligent cat with too much expected of it and the last revolutionary on the run, both figures of someone overloaded by the demands placed on their own cleverness. The emotional sea overflows but the eyes stay gloomy, and the plainest line is the one that can't be dressed up, I know it in my head but the light doesn't reach me. It ends as a genuine plea, somebody please take me to hell, unable to stop hunting for somewhere to go, and read whole the song is exactly its title: consciousness as a feedback loop, thought screaming back into itself with no exit but down.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"consciousness as a feedback loop with no exit but downward","built":"the band's signature feedback shriek becomes the song's structural metaphor"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-r0ilaq":{"haiku":"Rock bottom described as a cyclical condition, the absolute worst moment recurring indefinitely without escape or clarification.","opus":"Rock bottom, said in Osaka dialect over and over like beads on a string: this is the worst it gets, the lowest point, now. Goto can't even tell dullness from a bowl of udon and figures she'll buy some just to test it. The joke is the medicine; chanting the bottom into a nonsense rhyme is how she keeps from actually sinking through it.","sonnet":"A chant of rock bottom — the word どんぞこ (the very depths) hammered repeatedly, punctuated by a shrug about whether this is stupidity or just noodles and a deadpan verdict that right now, this moment, is unambiguously the worst it has ever been.","src":"web","web":"Rock bottom: 'now is the most terrible time, now is the worst', with absurdist touches ('is it stupidity or udon?') that make the self-awareness about hitting the lowest point simultaneously funny and genuinely bleak.","fable":"The word donzoko — rock bottom, the absolute pits — is nearly the entire song, chanted flat in the deadpan Osaka cadence until it stops meaning anything and becomes pure percussion, a mouth-noise the band can swing to. What little else there is refuses to rise to the occasion: a limp pun that can't decide between guchin (dull-witted) and udon, and a shrug that maybe it's worth buying just to try. Then the only real statement, delivered without a flicker of drama: right now is the worst it's ever been, right now is as bad as it gets. That's the whole trick. Goto doesn't perform despair so much as recite it in the bored singsong of someone too far down to bother with feeling, so that the jazz quartet's cool competence underneath sounds less like accompaniment than like the indifferent world going on around a person who has quietly given up — and the manic vocal she's famous for stays coiled, withheld, which is its own kind of bottom.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"despair recited in bored singsong too far down for feeling","built":"the manic vocal stays coiled and withheld throughout, itself a kind of bottom"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-r1se0y":{"haiku":"A sardonic goodbye from a fake woman—a liar who spreads false love everywhere; hoping her lies never become real before the goodbye.","opus":"This is the exhaustion of a self-described ugly liar, nicknamed wolf girl for telling everyone she loves them. She realizes her declarations have grown so automatic they might finally land, on the wrong person, and says goodbye wishing those words won't turn false one more time. Having performed love so often, she has forgotten which version was ever real, tenderness worn down into reflex.","sonnet":"A self-described ugly liar — nicknamed 'wolf girl' for telling everyone she loves them — realizes her declarations have become so automatic they might finally mean something to the wrong person, and says goodbye with a wish that those words won't become lies one more time. The emotional core is the exhaustion of someone who performed love so often she forgot which version of it was real.","src":"haiku"},
"a-2yw9ix~osaru":{"haiku":"A naive girl entranced by false kindness is unknowingly led into human trafficking, her innocence mistaken for stupidity.","src":"haiku","web":"A darkly comic portrait of a naive, carefree girl—compared throughout to a caged monkey—who loves 'magic pills', befriends strangers without caution, and ends up trafficked and sold overseas, her innocence used against her."},
"a-2yw9ix~swing":{"haiku":"Longing trembles at impermanence while seeking shallow comfort; the speaker wounds themselves in a pattern of attraction and hurt.","opus":"Trembling with want, Goto goes to bed with whoever hands over affection in the plainest terms, while the thaw comes too fast and everything bleeds together. The whole song narrows to one aching syllable repeated like a pulse, then breaks into something colder: a hushed good-girl coo over a captured, cornered rabbit, tenderness curdling into a predator's whisper.","sonnet":"Longing draws someone into the arms of whoever is most conveniently affectionate, not whoever they actually want — then the thaw comes too fast, old wounds reopen in the dusk, and what's left is a quiet resolve and the image of a lone rabbit finally caught.","src":"haiku","web":"A bittersweet meditation on transience and longing, built around a deliberate Japanese pun where 'aitai' reads simultaneously as 'I want to meet you' and 'it hurts', so every expression of desire is also an expression of pain."},
"a-2yw9ix~vs":{"opus":"Obsessive love repeated to the point of exhaustion tips into bewildered self-reproach-how did it come to this-and finally into a numb self-diagnosis of being utterly mediocre and vague, a depleted brain rebuilding itself in the dark.","sonnet":"Obsessive, dialect-thick declarations of love collapse into stunned incomprehension at how things got this bad, and self-assessment settles flatly on ordinariness — the speaker is mediocre, adrift, building a depleted mind day by day.","src":"web","web":"The speaker declares love obsessively ('I love you, I really love you') while questioning how she became so ordinary and adrift, the relentless romantic refrain colliding against a sense of mediocrity and exhaustion.","fable":"Two Japanese words that sound alike and mean near-opposite kinds of the same nothing — banal versus vague-and-formless — squared off as a title fight, and the song stages that fight as a nervous breakdown split across standard speech and Kansai dialect. It opens with a manic wall of I love you, love you (the endings flipping into dialect) that curdles instantly into one phrase repeated to exhaustion — how did it come to this — until the mania empties out and the register drops to something cold and clipped: I open at morning, I grow at night, building up a parched, depleted brain. The verdict of the title fight is the last line's shrug — endlessly, sluggishly, I am mediocre, mediocre, I am formless — the banal and the vast collapsing into the same drained self, which exposes the opening love-frenzy as the mask worn over exactly that emptiness.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind","love & desire"],"means":"love-frenzy exhausted into a diagnosis of formless mediocrity","built":"the title stages two near-synonyms as opponents whose fight ends in a draw"},
"a-2yw9ix~yukikosan":{"haiku":"Destruction and exhilaration: yearning for release through passionate intensity, breaking past restraint and doubt into primal sensation.","src":"haiku","web":"A manic manifesto about destroying the false, domestically-acceptable version of yourself; 'Yukiko-san' is a totem for fractured womanhood—perfectly manicured and completely unhinged—moving through defiance and self-loathing toward a hollow, unresolved triumph."},
"a-2yzfal~a-3pic1":{"haiku":"Existential disorientation in dark corridors; questioning one's existence and whether perception is mutual.","opus":"The lyric wanders a dark internal forest where the narrator can neither feel their own presence nor perceive anyone else. Existence is tested by pinching a cheek, and the fear is that disappearance has already happened. The space is warm and cold at once, dark and soft, and it stays unclear whether the exit lies ahead or whether the self has already vanished, dissociation rendered as lost geography.","sonnet":"A person lost in a dark internal forest, unable to feel their own presence or perceive others — checking if they still exist by pinching their cheek, wondering if they've already disappeared, moving through a space that is simultaneously warm and cold, dark and soft, unsure whether the exit is ahead or whether they have already vanished.","src":"haiku"},
"a-2yzfal~a-3q0tu":{"haiku":"Confession of heartbreak and relentless sorrow—the narrator's joy at meeting someone is matched only by the pain of their departure; a broken sword inside prevents healing.","opus":"This is heartbreak measured with an unusual physics. The narrator's love was only a distraction for the other person; meeting them broke the rusty sword inside, the weight of life grew heavier, and the joy of the encounter now dissolves upward like carbonation. That joy and its loss are equal, weightless, and gone, affection escaping as fast as it arrived, leaving the broken blade behind.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship where the narrator's love was only a distraction for the other person: meeting this person broke the narrator's rusty inner sword, the weight of their life increased, and now the joy of meeting dissolves upward like carbonation — equal and weightless and gone.","src":"haiku"},
"a-3b2bji~addiction":{"haiku":"A recursive meditation on unchanging self—no matter what you add or subtract, identity persists; life is an addiction to staying alive.","opus":"This song circles an unchanging self that survives every addition, subtraction, crying session, and word of encouragement, yet feels perpetually out of place. What it finally wants is to hear another heartbeat and talk honestly without performance. The emotional core is the quiet exhaustion of being self-contained and the longing to be taken somewhere real, off the phone screen and into an actual room.","sonnet":"A person who remains stubbornly themselves despite everything — additions, subtractions, crying sessions, encouragement — but who feels perpetually out of place and finally just wants to hear another heartbeat and talk honestly without performance. The emotional core is the quiet exhaustion of being self-contained and the longing to be taken somewhere real, off the phone screen.","src":"haiku"},
"a-3gwmy~a-7phzqf":{"haiku":"Moral compromise and cognitive dissonance—the narrator justifies strength while confessing to weakness and deception; hiding shame becomes lifestyle while avoiding maternal disappointment.","opus":"Sung in Russian and sharpened by sarcasm, this is an anti-heroism song. Its refrain tells the would-be hero to sit at home, since bravery is only remembered posthumously, cities burn, and no god will save anyone, so the smart move is to stay hidden. The track mocks the social demand for courage as a polite name for a death sentence, cowardice reframed as the only sane arithmetic.","sonnet":"A Russian anti-war or anti-heroism song, sardonic in tone: the recurring refrain tells the would-be hero to sit at home because bravery is only remembered posthumously, cities burn, gods won't save anyone, and the smart move is to stay hidden — the song mocks the social expectation of heroism as a death sentence.","src":"haiku"},
"a-8cdsuq~sweet-suicide-summer-story":{"haiku":"Obsessive summer romance spiraling into dependency; the lover drives the narrator to psychological madness through proximity.","opus":"The title's collision of sweetness and self-destruction is the whole story. A woman is consumed by a three-year summer love that has driven her into a madness she can neither halt nor wish away, the man functioning as her entire program. She already knows that when summer ends his love will simply switch channels, savoring a devotion she understands to be scheduled for cancellation.","sonnet":"A woman consumed by a three-year summer love that has driven her to a kind of sweet madness she can't stop and can't want to stop — the man is her entire program, yet she knows when summer ends his love will simply switch channels.","src":"haiku"},
"a-a-williams~evaporate":{"haiku":"Mind violence persists daily; the speaker dreams of dispersal while harming themselves remains the only certainty. Thought becomes torture without relief.","opus":"A depiction of dissociative pain so total the speaker longs to evaporate, to dissolve rather than endure another day of the violence in her own mind. The flat admission that harming herself is all she does names self-injury without dressing it up, everything starting and ending with a single unbearable you.","sonnet":"A person consumed by violent inner noise and a relationship that is the source of everything — beginning and end — wishes only to evaporate, to disintegrate, to escape the waking day, and acknowledges with bleak clarity that self-harm has become the only action available to them.","src":"haiku"},
"a-a40h5h~i-m-waiting-for-the-sun":{"haiku":"A hypnotic chase toward dawn and transcendence—following unseen guidance into daylight, resisting all temptation to stop and look back.","opus":"The title names the whole posture: waiting for the sun while moving forward through uncertainty, led by something unseen, crawling along the floor without stopping. The mood is patient and slightly wary endurance, neither surrendering to temptation nor knowing when relief will arrive. Progress here is low to the ground and blind, sustained only by the trust that dawn is somewhere ahead of the drift.","sonnet":"A song about moving forward through uncertainty — led by something unseen, crawling along the floor without stopping, waiting for the sun while drifting away. The emotional core is patient, somewhat wary endurance: neither giving in to temptation nor knowing when relief will come.","src":"haiku"},
"a-a40h5h~online-love":{"haiku":"Digital-age isolation and parasocial obsession—a recluse's relationship with someone online causes emotional turmoil and insomnia despite lack of genuine reciprocation.","opus":"This song inhabits a hikikomori isolated for three years and snared in a painful online connection. The narrator cannot sleep for a full day, doubts whether they can truly love the person on the other end who only performs happiness, and feels both killed by kind words and desperate for that person to simply be physically near. Digital-age loneliness curdles into insomnia and an ache that no screen can satisfy.","sonnet":"A song about a hikikomori who has been isolated for three years and is caught in a painful online connection: the narrator can't sleep for 24 hours straight, doesn't know if they can truly love the person on the other end of the phone who only performs happiness, and feels both killed by kind words and desperate for the other person to just be near them physically.","src":"haiku"},
"a-a40h5h~virgin-suicide":{"haiku":"Change arrived unannounced, divorcing past selves from present bodies. Two young lovers grasped at devotion knowing it couldn't endure; the speaker remains haunted by what childhood intimacy promised and couldn't deliver.","opus":"Working in the confessional, bedroom-pop lineage where private hurt is murmured rather than sung, the track traces love back to its origin as the first wound. Two former children, now altered but unchanged at the core, measure their present bodies against a devotion they once reached for. The adult they might have become could have chosen otherwise; they never got the chance, and the ache is that irreversibility.","sonnet":"Love is the source of human sadness, and both speakers were always children reaching for affection — \"just like a film we once saw\" — changed now but still recognisably the same ones who craved love; the adult version of either of them, had they existed back then, might have done something differently, but they didn't.","src":"haiku"},
"a-ag0ju8~a-bj1hxk":{"haiku":"A crude stream-of-consciousness rant on universal failure—using 'go fuck yourself' as a refrain for every human endeavor and relationship.","opus":"This belongs to a strain of Russian internet-era antifolk that treats inherited wisdom as something to detonate rather than pass down. Every venerable proverb is dragged out only to be capped with the same obscene punchline, until the ritual itself becomes the joke. The nihilistic comedy lands because the form is so orderly: measured folk cadence undercut, again and again, by the one truth the singer will grant.","sonnet":"A song that takes classic Russian proverbs and appends the phrase 'go fuck yourself' to every single one — 'measure twice, then go fuck yourself,' 'you learn, then go fuck yourself' — dismantling the entire inherited edifice of folk wisdom as a set of traps. The emotional core is nihilistic comedy: the only true proverb is that every piece of advice, good or bad, ends the same way.","src":"haiku"},
"a-ap-rocky-slowthai~mazza":{"haiku":"Street philosophy spits tales of money hunger and survival; the speaker moves through systems of theft and performance, questioning identity while playing the game.","opus":"Two rappers channel manic chaos into a swaggering catalogue of drugs, violence, and money, the British slang for madness as the organizing principle. Beneath the bravado sits a bleak confession that the drugs made the speaker feel loved in a way nothing before ever did.","sonnet":"Two rappers swap verses of bravado and chaos — muddy dungarees, cocaine, suicidal swagger, street-corner economics — with slowthai's working-class British aggression layered under Rocky's Harlem luxury flex; the title word \"mazza\" anchors a shared posture of maximum intensity regardless of consequence.","src":"haiku"},
"a-ap-rocky~holy-ghost-feat-joe-fox":{"haiku":"Religious hypocrisy pervades institutions; the speaker claims direct communion with the divine while rejecting human mediation.","opus":"Rocky indicting the church as just another hustle, comparing pastors to pimps and the industry's soul-selling to sin, insisting he keeps his own relationship with God outside institutions that traffic in faith.","sonnet":"A sharp critique of American church culture — where pastors drive Rolls Royces, ushers skim collection baskets, and Satan is dealing to rappers — sits alongside a genuine personal faith: the speaker rejects institutional religion's corruption while maintaining a direct, private relationship with God.","src":"haiku"},
"a-ap-rocky~l-d-love-x-ex-x-dreams":{"haiku":"Euphoric intoxication and romantic devotion blend into an altered state where love, sex and dreams become indistinguishable from chemical escape.","opus":"Psychedelic romance where love and LSD blur into the same trip, Rocky chasing intimacy through a haze, wanting real connection but only able to reach for it while high and half-doubting it's love at all.","sonnet":"A rapper navigates the overlap of drug-induced euphoria, genuine longing, and physical desire for a woman, admitting he struggles to articulate love in conventional ways while the psychedelic haze of LSD merges romance, fantasy, and sensation into one.","src":"haiku"},
"a-bk7v7s~a-2pq6l":{"opus":"Sung in Korean with the hushed intimacy of a small-scene ballad, it stages closeness as a locked room. One dancer moves eyes shut and ears sealed, entranced by their own reflection; the narrator, blindfolded before the same mirror, extends a rough hand into a party of one. The cold of it is that both are searching the same glass for themselves, and neither can be joined.","sonnet":"A Korean-language song of a cold, one-sided embrace — the other person dances with eyes shut and ears blocked, watching themselves in a mirror with the narrator left behind, extending a rough hand asking to join while the narrator searches for themselves in the same mirror, blindfolded and alone.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-bk8js3~a-k5w1a7":{"haiku":"Death and impermanence as the inescapable human condition; happiness and suffering are equally fleeting, self-awareness only multiplies regret, and love offers no escape.","opus":"In the tradition of goth-tinged theatrical narration, a death deity reviews the newly dead like a grim procession: grief-ghosts, cheated love-ghosts, hate wearing tenderness as a mask. Her verdict is that life is one irreversible throw of the dice, played at random. The cruelest turn is that even she, presiding over eternity, can no longer recall who she once loved. Self-awareness only compounds the loss.","sonnet":"A death deity surveys the wretched parade of the dead — grief-ghosts weeping, love-ghosts permanently cheated, hate-ghosts disguised as tenderness — declaring that life is a one-time irreversible gamble played entirely at random, and that by the end she can no longer even remember who she once loved.","src":"haiku"},
"a-bk8js3~a-zbwj3l":{"haiku":"Emotional self-annihilation in love's paradox; the speaker simultaneously loves and hates their partner, unable to sustain either feeling and fading into indifference and death.","opus":"The same theatrical darkness turns inward here, dramatizing love as a single feeling that keeps inverting. A woman confronts a man who will neither look at her nor hold her after all her waiting, and blames him for her ruin. Adoration and hatred arrive in the same breath, exhausting each other until nothing is left. What remains is the wish to end, frozen inside the memory, fading out alone.","sonnet":"A woman confronts a man who will not look at her or hold her despite her having waited, blaming him entirely for destroying her — swinging violently between total love and total hatred in the same breath — until she concludes there is nothing left and wants to end her life while still frozen in those memories, fading out alone.","src":"haiku"},
"a-buzjpr~a-1y1y678":{"opus":"Built for the bleary tail of a Russian house party, the track works like overheard gossip set to a beat. It tallies who moans louder, who lasts longer, whose drunken kitchen kiss got noticed, boys and girls trading rumors as currency. Beneath the looping chorus of who promised what runs a flat truth: the gathering is unbearably dull yet impossible to skip, which is exactly why the rumor mill never stops turning.","sonnet":"A Russian party song that catalogues a group hangout through the lens of gossip and sexual knowledge: who moans louder, who is longer — the boys and girls share rumors, someone drunkenly kisses in the kitchen, and the refrain of who promised what loops over the noise of a party that is unbearably boring but unavoidable.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-crow-is-white~9":{"haiku":"A playful mystery about secrets and locked doors—demanding passwords while withholding answers, teasing what can never be fully known.","opus":"A coy, teasing J-pop number that turns intimacy into a locked-room game, dangling a secret password and a series of questions while refusing to actually let anyone in. Flirtation as power play, closeness perpetually withheld.","sonnet":"A flirtatious game of secrecy and withholding — the password is a secret, the relationship is deliberately dark and unstable, and the 'ninth question' whose answer is the other person's name frames the whole encounter as a puzzle where intimacy is the locked door. The emotional tone is playful but slightly predatory: the narrator enjoys the power of knowing and not telling.","src":"haiku"},
"a-crow-is-white~let-it-die-you-shall-die":{"haiku":"Violent ambivalence toward self and other; death as the only resolution to temporal suffering.","opus":"A deliberately unsettling loop that fuses affection with a death wish, sliding between wanting to die, wanting to kill, and wanting a kiss. Love and annihilation blurred into the same childish, chanted impulse.","sonnet":"A blunt, looping conditional — whether the speaker or the other dies, death is wished or welcomed — punctuated by a single soft pivot: don't close your mind and you'll kiss me tomorrow. Violence and longing occupy the same breath.","src":"haiku"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~alone-dite":{"haiku":"Desperate resistance continues despite annihilation; the speaker rejects surrender while acknowledging inevitable defeat, choosing defiance over acceptance.","opus":"Drawing the legendary sword Arondight as a metaphor for a fight you can't sheathe once begun, against inferiority and a self you can't become. The resolution is bleak acceptance: no matter how hard he tries, he remains only himself.","sonnet":"A speaker reclaims identity after long erosion — wielding Alonedite (the unsheathed sword that cannot be re-sheathed), he refuses to be a stand-in for someone else and accepts that no matter what, he is himself. Inferiority, crumbled ideals, and the silence of lost voices are the battlefield; the declaration \"I am me\" is the only resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~alva":{"haiku":"Collective awakening rises from burial; witness to suffering transforms into unified surge against entropy, voices reclaim agency through solidarity.","opus":"A band's survival anthem about repeatedly clasping hands and pushing forward through betrayal and pain, refusing to lose. The five great flowers blooming at journey's end read as five members vowing to make it together.","sonnet":"A group bound by shared tears and a five-petaled bloom at journey's end rallies against betrayal and isolation — ALVA is the sunrise that breaks the night that never ended, and the group's joined hands are the medicine no ICU carries. One person's presence made the difference that solo effort never could.","src":"haiku"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~anemia":{"haiku":"Speech becomes pointless in cycles of mutual incomprehension; lovers claw at each other unable to connect. Passion turns to predation—what they share erodes meaning rather than building it.","opus":"A Japanese metalcore torrent of self-erasure and circular futility, bleeding meaning for nothing while insisting that even the pretense of living, the warmth of holding on, is proof the self existed at all.","sonnet":"An exhausted, circular argument with existence itself: blood wasted on meaningless answer-checking, the brain's circuits dimming, desires piling into delusion — yet each impulse to disappear is immediately answered by collecting, one by one, every reason to still be oneself, even while laughing-stabbing-tearing at each other in impermanence and mutual theft.","src":"haiku"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~anemone":{"haiku":"Collective judgment hides cruelty—consensus becomes weapon. The speaker consumes others' hurt while remaining invisible, participating in systems of erasure.","opus":"A furious dissection of online cruelty, where people perform empathy and shared feeling while a single button press erases a life and existence itself gets locked out. The repeated cry that someone just wanted to be noticed exposes the loneliness under the mob's Schadenfreude.","sonnet":"God laughs as a shadow-world proliferates: individuals are stabbed and dissected, labelled A and B, their humanity corroded into compliant ghosts who mistake shared emotion for understanding; the speaker calls out the hollow solidarity of online outrage — \"how many times did we stab the infected?\" — and watches existence itself get locked out, invisible no matter how many times it shines.","src":"haiku"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~calling":{"haiku":"Juvenile dreams and scraped knees mark growing pains; identity fractures under weight of change. The speaker chases a companion through transformation, refusing to abandon even as everything shifts, betting that shared memory survives reinvention.","opus":"A nostalgic address to a changing friend, honoring scabbed knees and youthful pride now dismissed as worthless; through repeated goodbyes and the realization they could never really change, the speaker pleads 'don't get lost, don't lose yourself, keep standing there'—their juvenile years glowing in memory while they urge each other to carve the future rather than only look back.","sonnet":"A voice addresses someone who has grown and changed, urging them not to lose what they were — the scraped knees that were once a source of pride, the juvenile tears at a table — and insisting that even though they always said goodbye and moved on unchanged, the other person's light from those past days still shines, and they will meet again smiling.","src":"haiku"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~ill":{"haiku":"Systems of justice collapse under scrutiny; the speaker surrenders to being consumed by eras they can't escape. Multiple selves fragment; they become what they despise.","opus":"A reckoning with mob righteousness, where majority judgment mistakes the phone in its hand for a righteous sword and the condemned goes down insisting he was violated by the era itself. The tangled thread that will never unknot images a faith and a cause corrupted past saving.","sonnet":"A chorus-voted villain, a purifying ideology eaten whole, a woman asking \"can you save me?\" — the speaker traces how collective belief systems collapse into glittering wreckage; the scream of \"farewell, brightness\" and \"farewell, high spirits\" marks the moment of realising everything trusted was already understood to be false, yet clung to anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~m1917":{"haiku":"Soldiers and the voiceless living alike are caught in cycles of violence and loss, yet the song drives toward the sunrise — each betrayal, each fallen comrade, met with the insistence to keep aiming forward into the future.","opus":"Named for a WWI rifle, this frames existence as an endless firefight with no meaning behind it, yet the response isn't surrender but keeping the muzzle pointed at the sunrise. Defiance as the only reason to keep going.","sonnet":"Soldiers in an unending war — addressed to someone given the gift of a human form, they crawl through the mud for gains only to lose them again, rifles aimed toward the sunrise rather than the enemy. The refrain 'hey my friend, stand up right now' breaks into exhortation, framing survival itself as defiance.","src":"haiku"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~mei":{"haiku":"Pleasure and depravity breed complicity and collapse, offering only pain as the new world while minds break surrendering to darkness.","opus":"A seductive descent into manufactured pleasure and self-destruction, mocking the false gospels of happiness and staged menace that keep people intoxicated. Awareness of the trap only deepens the compulsion to keep struggling inside it.","sonnet":"A defiant plunge into a dark new world where expectation is worthless and the old order — a Devil's Party in a desolate age — must be broken; the speaker refuses to stop struggling even as the world tries to seize and consume them, shouting \"break your mind\" as a liberation rather than a threat.","src":"haiku"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~od":{"haiku":"War consumes from inside; love becomes poison sustaining the drowning. The speaker writes frantically, desperation bleeding ink, unable to stop despite knowing words mean nothing.","opus":"A writer's frenzy of creation staged as war and overdose, love scattered like petals while only anger and jealousy answer the recurring question of why he even writes. The plea to be acknowledged underneath the manic wordplay makes the whole thing a portrait of art as compulsion and self-consuming addiction.","sonnet":"A writer in love-fuelled fury — love as poison, love as the only thing that drives the ink — catalogues beatings, tantrums, jealousy, and a tranquiliser-calm that is actually terror of the divine, cycling through \"Why am I write?\" as both self-justification and unanswerable confession, until a \"literary overdose\" collapses into the single demand: \"do you recognise me?\"","src":"haiku"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~raccoon-dead":{"haiku":"Trapped like careless fish in nets of circumstance, humans stumble through life unaware of their own captivity. Stupidity becomes the universal condition binding all living things together.","opus":"Built around Confucius's warning that clever words and a fair face rarely hide virtue, this treats life as a net full of predators wearing human masks, and answers betrayal with defiant, deathless refusal and mock gratitude.","sonnet":"A Confucian epigraph frames the whole: smooth words conceal cruelty, and the narrator and their flock were lost sheep caught in a raccoon-hunter's net, deceived by a predator pretending to be human — but agency remains, \"all choices are in your hand,\" and the empty gratitude they leave behind is the last act of the comedy-of-hatred.","src":"haiku"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~re-create-of-the-re-d-feat":{"opus":"A blood-soaked fantasy of reincarnation and vengeance in which fulfilling a dream costs everything, and the survivor pledges to rewrite a cursed history through cycles of loss, sacrifice, and rebirth.","sonnet":"An epic cycle of sacrifice, blood-cursed fate, and rebirth: a warrior born of fire and loss asks who all this love and killing was ever truly for, and resolves to carve a new world-story even on a stage built from sacrifice.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-crowd-of-rebellion~sleeping-in-the-dark":{"haiku":"Sacrifice dissolves into the unknown; the speaker transforms into ephemeral beauty while others remain trapped in ruins, separated by irrevocable choice.","opus":"A self-erasing love that wishes only for the beloved's happiness even as the singer dissolves into foam, a Little Mermaid image of surrender. He begs to be killed inside the dream so the illusion of the other's magic never breaks.","sonnet":"A one-sided devotion that dissolves the self — the speaker was a bubble shaped entirely by the other's happiness, unknowingly becoming someone unrecognizable. The request is to be killed unknowingly rather than awakened to the transformation, and the image of words written on a beach then swallowed by waves frames what remains as something polished and far away.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~all-i-want":{"haiku":"The singer seeks a place to belong and purpose to help others escape loneliness. Despite external judgment, they strive to remain constant and unforgotten.","opus":"A defiant mission statement from a musician who survives life by making loud, honest songs and wants a stage of his own to comfort other lonely people. Against everyone predicting his collapse, he swears to stay authentic, stay optimistic yet grounded, and answer only to himself.","sonnet":"A young musician's declaration of purpose: he wants to build something that makes lonely people feel less alone, refuses to conform to others' expectations of what his life should look like, and keeps moving forward despite warnings that his time is running out.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~all-signs-point-to-lauderdale":{"haiku":"A person rejects their hometown as washed-up and feels disconnected from shallow friendships. They break free by standing for something rather than backing down to social pressure.","opus":"The Florida crossover band vent hometown disgust and youthful alienation, the narrator hating a washed-up town full of indifferent friends and searching for where he fits. Beneath the pop-punk kiss-off runs a determination to stand for something and refuse to back down, defiance underpinning the frustration.","sonnet":"Belonging nowhere and resenting every constraint, the narrator rails against a stagnant hometown and drifting friendships while also mourning a loyalty he could never quite express to someone who mattered. The anger is real, but underneath it sits the older ache of never knowing where he fits.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~another-song-about-the-weekend-acoustic":{"haiku":"Redemption through faith in another; the speaker seeks solace in witnessing someone believe in their capacity for change.","opus":"The strain of a touring musician far from home, worn down and homesick for Florida, holding onto the one person who still believes in him while confessing he's lost the ability to find meaning in much.","sonnet":"A touring musician exhausted by distance, disconnection from old friends, and the loss of meaning in daily life finds a lifeline in someone who still has faith in him — and draws from that faith the resolve to keep going, to return to Florida, and to believe that music and love are enough. The vulnerability is unusually direct for the genre.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~bad-vibrations": {"fable":"A song that stages engulfing dread as an invading atmosphere rather than an internal fault — the malady arrives as something surrounding and consuming, addressed in the second person as though depression were a hostile other with its own will. McKinnon builds the symptom-set physically (fog, doubled vision, a stuttered line that the recording literally lets stutter) so the dread reads as bodily event, not mood. The turn is structural: the intro frames it all as a choice you make and must live with, but by the outro the externalized affliction has collapsed inward, the surrounding toxicity becoming the self that is empty and heartless — the thing pressing from outside was always his own contour, the daily weather of a mind he wakes up inside. What began as something done to him ends as something he is, and the chant that closes it stops naming an enemy and starts naming him.","fableDeep":"The pronouns stage the whole problem: they're *your* bad vibrations, an external darkness — yet everything they do happens inside him (his eyes burning through skin, his stutter, his cold). Whose sickness is it? The intro insists it's a choice; the chorus swears 'I can't escape it'; the song runs both claims simultaneously and refuses to arbitrate — which is honest to how the condition feels from inside. 'Casting shadows that continue to shape who I am on any given day' is the sharpest line: identity as weather, self as whatever the looming thing leaves lit.","haiku":"Toxic emotional influence that permeates and corrupts, leaving the speaker emptied and heartless.","opus":"A song about inner darkness and consuming pain treated as a sickness and a choice one must live with. Bad vibrations surround and corrupt the narrator, casting shadows that reshape who he is each day.","sonnet":"A song about absorbing someone else's toxicity: the speaker feels an external darkness pressing in — distorting perception, inducing numbness, and shaping identity — framed as a sickness he cannot escape and did not choose.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming"],"means":"the external affliction collapsing inward as his own contour","built":"a stutter written into the recording makes dread a bodily event"},
"a-day-to-remember~best-of-me":{"haiku":"Betrayal and deception consumed the speaker's vulnerability and innocence, leaving behind the hard knowledge of who someone truly is beneath performance. Isolation from the betrayer becomes non-negotiable.","opus":"The bitter aftermath of betrayal, realizing too late that a trusted person took the last of one's innocence, and finally refusing to keep being the one they wanted.","opusDeep":"A breakup rendered as a courtroom appeal, all self-justification: 'Just 'cause you're right doesn't mean I'm wrong' concedes the point only to weasel out of it, and 'Our days were numbered and we knew it all along' pre-absolves both parties. The recurring 'My dad was right, we will never get along' is the tell — a grown speaker still measuring the wreckage against a father's prophecy. The chorus title turns on its double sense: 'you got the best of me' means both bested and drained-of-best, and it lands hardest on 'You took what's left of my innocence,' where blame curdles into loss. The bridge finally drops the accusation for a flat admission: 'I will never be the one you want,' repeated until it stops being a threat and becomes acceptance. What it's actually about: the moment resentment exhausts itself and a person chooses to leave not in triumph but because staying would cost the last of who they were.","sonnet":"A reckoning with someone who exploited trust and stunted growth — the speaker sees clearly now that they were taken advantage of, stretched too thin, and declares this the last time this person will ever burden them.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~brick-wall":{"haiku":"A person spirals through cycles of curse and self-destruction, hurtling toward inevitable collision with consequences despite their inability to change course.","opus":"A fatalistic take on self-sabotage and a relationship steering itself off a cliff, invoking karma and destiny only to conclude that everyone involved is charging headlong toward disaster. The band frames ruin as a choice, momentum built on purpose toward the crash, pessimism named as a poison the narrator lets consume everything.","sonnet":"Relentless bad luck and a pessimistic streak the narrator acknowledges is killing them — they're headed for disaster with no luck, no future, and no way to stop — but the song doesn't quite resign itself, it more darkly celebrates going down swinging.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~bullfight": {"haiku":"Two former close friends have become strangers trapped under unspoken tension; despite the distance, the speaker offers to be a light through their darkness while recognizing how misunderstood they've become.","opus":"A eulogy for a decayed friendship where two people now pretend to be strangers, unable to say or laugh the way they used to under misplaced pressure; despite the estrangement the speaker vows to still be the other's light through the darkness, mourning what was lost while refusing to abandon them.","sonnet":"About a friendship or relationship that has grown distant and politely fake — both people say what's needed to avoid tension without actually connecting, and the narrator offers to be the one person who shows up genuinely and lights the way through the other person's low period.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song reads as a friendship dissolving in slow motion. The verses map the wreckage — two people performing politeness to mask that they've become strangers, the past souring into a relic, vultures already circling — while the chorus pivots against that decay into a vow to be the other's light, the speaker casting himself as rescuer even as everyone else looks away. The title's cruelty lands in the breakdown: the metalcore drop arrives on the repeated command to brace and fall, turning the pop-punk plea into a bullring where connection is the thing being gored, and McKinnon's post-chorus admission of being misunderstood confesses the vow may be one-sided rescue no one asked for.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["friendship & loyalty","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a one-sided rescue vow delivered into a dissolving friendship","built":"the metalcore drop arrives on the command to fall"},
"a-day-to-remember~dead-buried":{"haiku":"Regret crystallizes around absence; the speaker contemplates mortality knowing she cannot return to repair what was fractured.","opus":"A man raising an empire on stubborn self-reliance while picturing his own execution, brooding over squandered years he can't stop trying to erase, haunted by how he'll be remembered once he's gone.","sonnet":"A man at the end of his rope — a noose being the central image — addresses his mother in what is framed as a final letter, regretting years spent far away and haunted by the thought of what legacy he leaves for the children who come after. The song pivots mid-way into a defiant refusal to be defined by failure, insisting on one's own voice and survival.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~exposed": {"haiku":"A political critique of manipulation and control by governments and institutions who trade freedom for illusion. The speaker exposes systemic deception and calls for resistance.","opus":"A politically charged protest against manufactured choices, distrusting elected officials and peace-preachers in equal measure and cautioning laborers to stay obedient. Its core threat is that those in power keep working on a person until nothing of them is shielded anymore.","sonnet":"A politically charged rejection of manufactured consent, arguing that workers and citizens are kept compliant through the illusion of choice while the powerful exploit them — the song calls for confronting that system rather than accepting it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rare political turn for McKinnon, who frames electoral democracy as a rigged game — the vote traded for the mere appearance of choice, workers told to take what they're given or take nothing. The image-system yokes labor grievance to death: the road that ends at the gallows, the exhumed body dug up and stripped bare, a line delivered in a borrowed Depression-era drawl about being beaten from birth. But the song turns on its own mask at the end — the chorus keeps demanding is this who you wanna be, sounding like a call to resist, while the bridge's predatory shark circling with an appetite for the kill and its closing sneer at the pacifist quietly reveal the speaker as the very cynicism he seems to indict, the refusal that spurns armistice and combat alike collapsing the exhortation into something colder. The song's charge is that its own anger has no exit, and it names that trap by refusing to resolve it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"anger naming itself as the trap it set out to indict","built":"chorus exhortation reveals the speaker as the cynicism he attacks"},
"a-day-to-remember~forgive-and-forget":{"opus":"The pain of an enabling relationship with someone who keeps letting you down and shutting you out, the narrator repeatedly bending, enabling, and hoping they'll change until all the love he had is finally drained away.","sonnet":"About enabling a person the narrator loves who keeps failing and never changing — they keep bending, keep absorbing the disappointment, unable to say what they need to say out loud, until all the love is drained and they are left empty.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-day-to-remember~heartless":{"haiku":"Abuse and emotional manipulation culminate in the narrator's complete rejection of a partner; they mourn lost time and reclaim self-worth through severance.","opus":"A brutal breakup severance, finally seeing through a manipulative partner after giving everything and being treated as a burden, the closing venom showing how much cruelty the hurt has hardened into.","sonnet":"The speaker finally sees clearly through an exploitative partner's web of lies, recognizes they were cast aside despite giving everything, and chooses to close their eyes and move toward a life without that person — the anger and exhaustion tipping into a bitter, final goodbye.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~i-m-already-gone":{"haiku":"Departure becomes inevitable when love turns inward; the speaker abandons connection to salvage what remains of themselves.","opus":"Sifting through moving boxes for anything of yourself that survived a relationship, resolving to live for yourself from here while admitting you were never built to be alone. Independence and loneliness at war.","sonnet":"Someone who has been held back by a person and a place makes the irreversible decision to leave and find their own reason for being, even without knowing what comes next. The departure is painful but necessary — the alternative of staying means continued diminishment.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~i-m-made-of-wax-larry-what-are-you-made-of":{"haiku":"The speaker warns an untrustworthy manipulator that they control them like a puppet and will watch them fall. They oscillate between violent promises and reluctant acceptance of the other's humanity.","opus":"Pop-punk turned to open threat: a betrayed narrator promising to be there for the fall, half revenge fantasy and half assertion of standing on his own. The bravado barely covers how much the wound still governs him.","sonnet":"A confrontational song directed at a duplicitous person — the narrator refuses to be controlled or underestimated, threatens real consequences for betrayal, and ultimately shifts to a defiant declaration of self-sufficiency against everyone who doubts them.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~it-s-complicated":{"haiku":"A person has fundamentally transformed into someone unrecognizable and untrustworthy, leaving the speaker no choice but total severance. Acceptance that bonds cannot survive such profound change offers the only escape.","opus":"Estrangement from someone who has become a stranger, the narrator withdrawing and covering his own maladjustment while resigning himself to a bond that always breaks.","opusDeep":"Social anxiety written as a friendship autopsy. It opens on withdrawal — 'avoid the sun, and cancel plans with everyone I know' — then pivots the blame outward: 'you can't relate to anyone, to anything at all,' as if isolation were the other person's disease. The gut-punch is the parenthetical sneer 'Follow your friends, like you ever had them,' which exposes the speaker projecting his own friendlessness onto the target. The refrain 'You're not the person that I knew back then' does the heavy structural work, converting a broken relationship into a story about a stranger, so grief becomes contempt. But the second verse quietly implicates the narrator too: 'I played it off so well / I just hold it in... so nobody can tell,' confessing the same emotional concealment he condemns. What it's actually about: a person too guarded to keep anyone close, dressing his own avoidance up as another's betrayal.","sonnet":"The speaker recognises that a relationship — possibly a friendship — has been hollowed out by someone's inability to connect with anyone or anything, and chooses withdrawal over confrontation, though they still can't fully let go before dawn.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~justified":{"haiku":"The song questions religious moralism and righteous judgment, asking who decides right from wrong; the speaker asserts faith in earthly happiness over hedged spiritual bets.","opus":"The speaker dares the righteous to burn him and rejects blind faith, insisting happiness and virtue belong to a life lived before death rather than a wagered afterlife. A Day To Remember, Ocala's pop-punk and metalcore hybrid, push their signature breakdown against organized religion here, staging defiance as a plea to be heard by those who fear the lowly.","sonnet":"A dissenter rails against the self-righteous, insisting that virtue and happiness belong to this life rather than some promised afterlife. He dares the pious to condemn him while declaring that hollow faith, used only as a hedge, deserves its own reckoning.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~last-chance-to-dance-bad-friend":{"haiku":"Betrayal by a close friend cuts deeper than any external wound, leaving vengeance as the only path forward after false loyalty dissolves.","opus":"A scorched eulogy for a friendship that curdled into betrayal, refusing the pretense that the bond was ever real and relishing the thought that no one will mourn the person who burned it down. Vengeance dressed as closure.","sonnet":"A friendship's betrayal is dissected with cold clarity — loyalty used like a chain, truth never given, the knife twisted and left — and the speaker names what is left after the wound: nobody to carry the casket of someone whose belief in others was really the death of reason.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~mindreader":{"haiku":"Communication breaks under unmet expectations; the speaker cannot read minds and refuses to perform impossible emotional labor despite another's demands.","opus":"Frustration with a partner who expects him to intuit thoughts they refuse to voice, laughing as if he should already know. No amount of ancient wisdom or effort will make him the mindreader they need, only widening the distance between them.","sonnet":"A frank admission of emotional incompetence in a relationship: the partner expects the speaker to intuit their feelings, but the speaker — willing to cross the world for wisdom — insists they never have and never will be a mindreader, framing this not as failure but as honest limitation.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~miracle":{"haiku":"Will replaces external salvation; the speaker abandons waiting for divine intervention. Present moment holds power—refusing someday mythology, they seize now without permission.","opus":"A hard-won refusal of passive hope, the speaker who struggled his way through betting everything on the present rather than waiting for some spiritual rescue. Insisting he doesn't need a miracle, only his own hardened will, he turns survival into proof he isn't made of glass.","sonnet":"A speaker who has struggled and endured declares they no longer need divine intervention — just the present moment, not some vague future grace — insisting their will is stronger than any weapon and rejecting the spiritual framing others impose on their resilience.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~mr-highway-s-thinking-about-the-end":{"haiku":"A speaker rejects shallow transformation and false solidarity, demanding accountability while threatening to walk away from those who brought only destruction.","opus":"A furious severing from someone who has changed and coasted on inspiration they no longer have, framed as a reckoning over who deserves what they've earned. Beneath the personal venom runs a band's grievance about betrayal and being taken from by people they built up.","sonnet":"A furious severance — the speaker cuts off someone who has changed beyond recognition and become a monster-making presence, demanding they leave while asking when those who have given everything will finally get what they deserve.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~my-life-for-hire":{"haiku":"Someone confronts those who controlled and violated them, breaking free from manipulation to expose their cruelty.","opus":"A furious kiss-off to a manipulator, the narrator vowing to expose what was done to him after breaking the mold he was told to fit, dismissing the other as a lie everyone can already see. A Day to Remember fused pop-punk hooks with metalcore breakdowns, and this early-catalogue track swings between melodic accusation and screamed threat, all aimed at unmasking a betrayer for the world.","sonnet":"Retaliation after being shaped and used — someone who was told who to be, broke free of that mold, and now wants the world to know what was done to them. The room is cold, the situation frightening, but the stance has hardened into a refusal to let the past be quietly resolved. This is a public reckoning promised aloud: I will be the one who says it.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~naivety": {"haiku":"Growing older strips away youthful belief and risk-taking, replacing wonder with fear and distance from once-accessible dreams.","opus":"A lament for lost innocence and the fearlessness of youth, when mistakes were chances worth taking, now replaced by fear of failure and growing colder with age. The countering vow, no apologies, done chasing what doesn't belong to him, turns the nostalgia into a hard-won push to keep moving forward.","sonnet":"An adult mourning the loss of the fearless, optimistic recklessness of youth — the sense that mistakes were opportunities rather than failures — and trying to reconcile honest self-acceptance with the feeling that what he's searching for keeps receding.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grown man mourning the recklessness of youth, where believing you could do anything was a feature and not a delusion — each verse poses the same aging-out question, and the pre-chorus keeps admitting the answer got mislaid somewhere behind him. McKinnon's fix is the pop-punk move of turning loss into a creed: the chorus recasts that lost innocence as earned honesty, no apologies, done chasing what was never his. But the read lives in what the anthem won't fix — its own last line concedes the thing he's after only recedes further, so the defiance is real and the mask is a mask, a man selling himself the grown-up version of not-needing-it while still reaching.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nostalgia & memory","identity & becoming"],"means":"defiance as grown-up cover for the need it denies","built":"the closing line concedes what he chases only recedes further"},
"a-day-to-remember~over-my-head-cable-car":{"haiku":"Emotional overwhelm strands the speaker suspended between connection and abandonment; critical moments pass unfulfilled as distance crystallises into permanent separation.","opus":"A friendship coming apart when everyone was already halfway out the door, staged as a doomed standoff suspended high over a drop. He'd rather bring the whole thing down himself than watch it slip quietly away over someone else.","sonnet":"A friendship or relationship falls apart through apathy and dishonesty — the speaker realizes everyone was waiting to run, and now the two are hanging in a cable car above a canyon as the relationship dissolves effortlessly, with the speaker vowing not to let it go down until they torch it themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~paranoia":{"haiku":"Paranoia and anxiety manifest as an invasive, all-consuming force that lodges in the mind and cannot be escaped no matter what is tried.","opus":"The grip of paranoia and dread, with sleeplessness, fear of unknown callers and footsteps, imagined as a ticking time bomb clouding judgement and lodged inside one's own head.","sonnet":"The song gives voice to an invasive, obsessive presence — described as a time bomb ticking inside someone's head — that fuels paranoia: the inability to sleep, fear of unknown callers, the sense of being watched and followed.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song runs on a switch that reorganizes everything. Verses belong to a sufferer who cannot sleep and braces for menace at every door, while choruses pivot to accuse a listener — the singer now claiming to live behind their eyes as a device set to detonate. Dread stops being something the narrator carries and becomes something he transmits, so the frightened man and the figure who cannot be outrun collapse into one voice. A repeating clinical refrain escalates across the track, and its steady return pins all of this as sickness rather than threat, a stalker's mask stretched over somebody quietly falling apart. On the band's most airless record, this is the cleanest version of that method: panic built so thoroughly from inside that it curdles from felt into inflicted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","surveillance & control"],"means":"the frightened man and the threat he fears converging into one voice","built":"the clinical refrain holds its steady cadence while the accusation migrates from a stalker to the singer himself"},
"a-day-to-remember~reassemble": {"haiku":"Someone drowns in depression and self-doubt, questioning their own worth and whether they can be repaired after falling apart, seeking forgiveness and honesty.","opus":"A Day to Remember cast depression as slowly going under, the narrator undone and disconnected, treating his illness as a crutch while doubting he can even be rebuilt. Pulled down by an unstoppable undertow, he flails for escape. A late turn toward honesty and self-forgiveness offers faint hope that a wrecked person might, battered as he is, still be pieced together.","sonnet":"A person in the grip of mental illness questions whether they deserve to be put back together — the drowning metaphor runs throughout as both despair and the impossibility of finding solid ground. The final turn toward communal honesty and forgiveness frames reassembly as a shared rather than solitary act.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that builds its case for repair by first proving how thorough the collapse is: the verses are all water, a body tossed and pulled under by a current it can no longer read, and McKinnon frames depression not as sadness but as a tide with its own gravity, something that overtakes rather than merely visits. The chorus keeps returning to a single hinge question — whether a person this far gone is even worth putting back together — and its repetition is the point, because a mind stuck in that loop can only re-ask, never answer. What lifts the track above straight self-pity is the closing turn, where the drowning I becomes a we: the surf now works against a plural, and the plea shifts from am I salvageable to a mutual bargain — forgive the shameful thoughts, be honest, and reassemble together. Bad Vibrations is A Day to Remember's darkest, most metalcore-leaning record, and this is where its heaviness reveals its actual subject: the album's aggression is the sound of someone thrashing to stay above the water. The song answers its own question not with reassurance but with company, deciding worth is something two broken people confer on each other rather than something you earn alone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","friendship & loyalty","shame & repression"],"means":"worth as something two broken people confer, not earn alone","built":"the drowning I becomes we in the closing turn"},
"a-day-to-remember~resentment":{"haiku":"The speaker is trapped in repetitive cycles of resentment, building like sparks into wildfire that consumes them.","opus":"A Day to Remember, veterans of pop-punk crossed with metalcore, portray someone reaching for quick relief from a lasting wound, stuck reliving the same looping memories until buried grievance flares into something uncontrollable. The bravado barely conceals a person spiraling downward, caught in a self-destructive cycle of addiction and grudge.","sonnet":"Stagnation eating someone alive — every day identical, a one-track mind that can't seem to change, rot that crept in after a voice was lost. The metaphor keeps catching fire: resentment as spark, as wildfire, as a high-wire act with demons. There is self-awareness here — a chip on the shoulder named and owned — but no resolution, just the burn looping back on itself.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~same-about-you":{"haiku":"The speaker recognizes a hidden side of someone they cared for and realizes their entire understanding of them was wrong.","opus":"A relationship curdles in real time: one emotional wave and the narrator realizes everything he believed about someone was wrong, so he steps back for perspective and concludes he'll never feel the same again. A Day to Remember dial down the heaviness here for a melancholy pop-rock reckoning, the ache less about anger than the quiet finality of extracting truth from a lie you can't walk back.","sonnet":"A relationship finally seen clearly after the feeling has already drained away — the moment of realizing a whole side of someone was hidden and that the truth, once surfaced, can't be unfound. There is no rage, only the flat certainty that some roads don't allow a return. The decision to step back for perspective has already settled into permanent distance.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~sometimes-you-re-the-hammer-sometimes-you-re-the-nail":{"haiku":"A person defends his right to vulnerability and mistakes while resisting judgment. He finds strength in sadness, comes from nothing, and refuses to be defined by others' stones or criticism.","opus":"A stubborn declaration of resilience against detractors who want to see the singer fall. He admits fear and failure openly, finding strange comfort in melancholy music, and insists his self-worth is his own to define rather than something critics can grant or revoke. The title frames life as an oscillation between dominating and getting crushed.","sonnet":"Resilience under external judgment and people waiting for you to fail — the narrator admits to doubt and mistakes but refuses to let others define who they are, closing with a commitment to stand up for those who can't stand up for themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~the-downfall-of-us-all":{"haiku":"A band leaves their hometown, selling out and moving on despite criticism. They pursue their own path and success, and the town itself becomes the thing holding them back.","opus":"The Florida pop-punk and metalcore hybrids anthemize fleeing a dead-end hometown, giving themselves over to the highway and refusing to be reshaped for anyone. Fielding accusations of selling out, the narrator insists his songs come straight from the gut and his honesty isn't for sale. Restless departure meets the town recast as a shared doom everyone should escape.","sonnet":"A musician declares independence from his hometown and its expectations — selling out to the road rather than to commercial pressure — and leaves behind a place he believes will drag everyone down who stays.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~the-plot-to-bomb-the-panhandle":{"haiku":"A young person severs ties with insincerity and judgment, declares independence from false friendship, and walks away from emotional entrapment.","opus":"A young Florida band's kiss-off to a poisonous relationship, all wounded pride and scorched earth. Under the bravado it's really about choosing your friends as the family that stays when a home turns hostile.","sonnet":"A speaker delivers a furious farewell to someone deceitful and two-faced, condemning their manipulation and fakery, pledging to leave everything behind and draw strength instead from loyalty to friends.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~turn-off-the-radio":{"haiku":"Media and empty messaging plague culture; the narrator seeks authentic voices and real connection amid manufactured division.","opus":"A protest against media noise and division, calling to shut off the airwaves until they carry anything worthwhile, craving quiet enough to think in a cruel, self-devouring society. The narrator wants both to satisfy others and to be genuinely understood, casting apathy as the true failing. Pointed social commentary from a pop-punk band, asking to be informed without being manipulated.","sonnet":"A listener fed up with media that numbs rather than illuminates calls for silence until something real is said — naming the cultural division, the victimhood performances, the indifference — and searching for one voice that actually connects.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~we-got-this":{"opus":"A reassuring hand extended to anyone feeling isolated among people, with the singer seeing his own younger, struggling self in them. He promises that hardship passes and pushes them toward hope, insisting these bleak stretches are survivable when faced together.","sonnet":"A community anthem for people who feel alone in crowds — addressed to younger people who are struggling like the narrator once did, the song promises that it gets better and that shared late-night hours of solidarity are what carry people through.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-day-to-remember~you-had-me-at-hello":{"haiku":"Instant recognition and trust shatter past vows; the narrator's entire guard collapses at first sight, surrendering to hope despite prior trauma.","opus":"Pop-punk infatuation at full tilt, someone who swore off trust disarmed instantly, wasting away between visits and counting the clock, love breaking through his defenses before he could raise them.","sonnet":"Despite swearing off trust after past hurt, the speaker finds themselves completely disarmed by someone whose smile changes the room — the surrender is instant and total, distance the only obstacle between them and what they already know they feel.","src":"haiku"},
"a-day-to-remember~you-should-have-killed-me-when-you-had-the-chance":{"haiku":"Violent rupture becomes final act of retribution; the speaker orchestrates murder-suicide as ultimate accusation, destroying intimacy and self simultaneously.","opus":"A small-town outcast's revenge fantasy escalating from whispered judgment to murder, defending a woman against a poisonous liar. The bravado keeps cracking into doubt, wondering whether to just write the whole thing off instead.","sonnet":"A speaker leaving a town full of gossip and false friends snaps into violence — he defends a woman he loves against a serpent tongue, then narrates walking into the offender's house with a gun, leaving blood on the bathroom wall. The refrain \"you should have killed me when you had the chance\" frames the murder as a preemptive strike by someone cornered.","src":"haiku"},
"a-doydp1~scars":{"haiku":"A voice navigates the labyrinth of desire and dissolution, balancing beauty with ruin. The lyrics embrace contradiction—loving the scars that mark survival, dancing in solitary darkness, refusing false redemption while knowing wounds are what make us real.","opus":"In the maximalist Japanese vocaloid-adjacent vein, where beauty and ruin are deliberately fused, the speaker greets the abyss as a reward rather than a threat. Wounds become proof of a self forged by no one else, untouched by another's intent. A waltz of loneliness and resonance melts into a kiss in a nightmare marsh, and fallen pride is claimed as truth. Survival, not redemption, is what the marks certify.","sonnet":"A speaker revels in their own wounds and darkness, addressing the abyss as an ecstatic reward — \"I like my SCARS\" — while wrestling with the meaning of their birth and insisting on a self-forged identity untouched by anyone else's intent. The waltz of loneliness and another's resonance fuses into a kiss in a nightmare marsh, and the speaker ultimately accepts their fallen pride as the truth living inside those scars.","src":"haiku"},
"a-doydp1~under-shaft":{"opus":"The same artist swaps inward ecstasy for militarized defiance, staging an apocalypse to reject being anyone's instrument. With a gun at the forehead and black smoke falling, the narrator turns on false skies, useless gods, and those who spend others as convenient bullets. The one thing worth faith is a self that refuses to be weaponized, outlasting the collapse in order to build a tomorrow no one else gets to aim.","sonnet":"A militaristic, apocalyptic song about refusing to be anyone's instrument: with a gun pressed to a forehead and black smoke raining down, the narrator rails against false skies, useless gods, and people who use others as convenient bullets — the only thing worth believing in is the one self that won't be weaponized by others, surviving collapse to build a new tomorrow.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-doydp1~vanishing-point":{"haiku":"Disappearance and the point of no return; dissolving into nonexistence as a form of escape.","opus":"Carrying the same restless intensity into forward motion, the track follows young people sprinting toward a place they truly belong, losing it and finding it again in an endless loop of search and rise. The point where everything burns out is reframed as the point of understanding, so hesitation is a luxury. Reaching the vanishing point with open hands becomes both a beginning and a prologue, dissolution converted into departure.","sonnet":"Young people sprinting toward a true place they belong, losing and finding it again in an endless search-and-rise loop — the point where everything burns out is the point of understanding, so there is no time to hesitate, and reaching the vanishing point with open hands is both beginning and prologue.","src":"haiku"},
"a-fgydrd~neet-game":{"haiku":"NEET existence as high-stakes video game; blending internet culture with desperation for life transformation.","opus":"Steeped in Japanese online NEET culture, the track reframes a stalled, screen-lit life as a game demanding to be beaten. The shut-in grinds stats, tests strategies, resets the character build, treating stagnation as a level rather than a fate. The turn comes when the player decides this is now-or-never, the moment to level up and step into the real. Genre logic becomes the only vocabulary big enough to make change imaginable.","sonnet":"A NEET (not in education, employment, or training) frames their stagnant screen-saturated home existence as a video game they must beat — grinding stats, executing strategies, resetting their character build — eventually deciding this is now-or-never time to level up and enter real life.","src":"haiku"},
"a-fgydrd~tousou":{"haiku":"Chaotic chase and escape; protagonist flees pursuit while maintaining individual pace despite external pressure.","opus":"The same restless energy accelerates into a cartoon chase, the pursued bolting north, south, over mountain and river and sea, refusing handcuffs and routine alike. The flight doubles as a portrait of someone who keeps stumbling and running from what life demands, yet insists change is possible and light is findable someday. Keeping one's own pace under pressure becomes the small, stubborn dignity inside the panic.","sonnet":"A high-energy cops-and-robbers scenario where the pursued runs in every direction — north, south, mountain, river, sea — rejecting handcuffs and routine, casting the chase as a metaphor for someone who keeps stumbling and running from life's demands but believes they can change and find the light someday.","src":"haiku"},
"a-flood-of-circle~a-17k2kcn":{"haiku":"A defiant ode to rock music as life force—living recklessly through art because today might be the last day.","opus":"This is bluesy Japanese garage rock as survival creed, and the confession is that a band playing in the dark of a place like Shinjuku Loft is reason enough to last the night. The narrator has always known life is brief and mostly worthless; live music is his rebuttal. The resolution is that every goodbye doubles as a beginning, and listening converts the fear of endings into the plain will to keep going.","sonnet":"A love letter to rock and roll as the only reason to keep living — the narrator has always known life is brief and mostly worthless, but a band playing in the dark of a venue like Shinjuku Loft is proof enough to get through tonight. The emotional resolution is that every goodbye is also a beginning, and the act of listening to music converts the fear of endings into the will to go on.","src":"haiku"},
"a-gz2mtd~stepan-pantera":{"haiku":"War machinery crushes civilian life while occupying powers expand through deception. The song catalogs atrocity and resistance, naming dictatorships and scorched earth, calling Ukraine to weaponized defiance.","opus":"A furious anti-war polemic in the wartime protest tradition, it lays out Russia's imperial pattern as a repeating script, naming Chechnya, Georgia, Moldova, Belarus, Ukraine as predictable acts. It calls listeners to learn the pattern and summons Stepan Pantera as a figure of resistance before shifting into Ukrainian, where the army's resolve is stated flatly. Catalogued atrocity gives way to weaponized defiance, and the language switch is itself the argument.","sonnet":"A furious anti-war polemic addressing Russia's pattern of imperial violence — Chechnya, Georgia, Moldova, Belarus, Ukraine — naming each occupation as a predictable scenario while calling on listeners to learn from the pattern, invoking Stepan Pantera as a figure of resistance, and shifting into Ukrainian-language lines where the army's resolve is declared: \"we don't need a ride, we just need more ammo.\"","src":"haiku"},
"a-light-divided~radio-silence":{"haiku":"Loss of connection and control as the speaker watches their world slip away, ready to sacrifice others to preserve their own sanity amid breakdown.","opus":"This melodic rock outfit render a relationship's breakdown as a failing transmission, signals lost over the horizon and the narrator screaming into static. The metaphor sharpens into cold resolve as they admit they will sacrifice the other to survive, wishing the fading connection would simply go dark for good.","sonnet":"A connection has gone dead and the narrator chooses not to revive it. Lost and directionless, map turned to stone, he transmits but receives nothing back. Rather than grief, a cold clarity arrives: the other person's absence is survivable, perhaps even necessary, and silence is now the preferred answer.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~as-black-as-my-heart":{"haiku":"Profound emotional decay and spiritual death. Everything inside the narrator has withered and burned away, leaving only blackness.","opus":"A slow spiral into despair, its narrator watching his life collapse and his spirit empty out until he wishes only for oblivion. Buried beneath deceit and numbness, he keeps returning to a single verdict: nothing is darker than what he carries inside.","sonnet":"A song about total internal collapse — a hollow soul, a dead sky, a world burned to the ground — and the desire to be buried and done. The speaker describes numbing himself to the point of feeling nothing, lying down in his own ruin.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~bad-dream":{"haiku":"Fragmentation and demonic occupation split the speaker internally; they wage war against themselves, bleeding and burning from within.","opus":"A raw account of the mind splitting in two, feeling an enemy self take control, seeing another man in the mirror he wants to kill, and reading his scars as a record of pain.","sonnet":"About psychosis or severe dissociation — the narrator sees a different man in the mirror, feels a demonic enemy taking control of their body from inside, and contemplates self-harm with a razor blade as a way to externalize inner pain. The scars on their arms represent the pain they cannot otherwise escape; the loss of self-control to an internal enemy is total and terrifying.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~bath-in-my-blood-schizophrenia-in-me":{"haiku":"Internal fragmentation and losing control to warring personas within the mind.","opus":"A raw first-person account of mental illness as loss of control, two selves splitting inside one head, where seeing his own reflection means seeing a man he wants to kill and being pushed toward bathing in his own blood.","sonnet":"The speaker is in the grip of a dissociative mental crisis — demons inside the head, a sense of a second hostile self sharing the body — and is at the edge of self-destruction, describing the experience of schizophrenic fracture with raw, unmediated terror.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~believe-in-something-you-are-lost":{"haiku":"Spiritual desolation and demonic haunting consume the soul; belief becomes the only anchor against drowning in purposelessness and rejection from a hostile world.","opus":"A doom-laden confession of a mind coming apart, faith exhausted and no one arriving to help. The title lands as bitter irony: belief is offered as a lifeline precisely because nothing else has held.","sonnet":"A slow accumulation of spiritual and psychological collapse — something snapped, the demons laugh, years of coldness and hopelessness have left the narrator writing on cracked walls and hanging their soul from the cross. The refrain \"believe in something when you are lost\" reads less as comfort than as the last instruction before total dissolution.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~cold-dark-mourning":{"haiku":"An elegy for lost friends and loved ones now dead and at rest; the speaker stands alone, waiting to be reunited with them in the afterlife.","opus":"A grief-stricken meditation on standing at a friend's grave and outliving everyone the narrator has lost, comforted only by the thought that the departed rise to the stars and that he will meet them again.","sonnet":"The speaker stands at a graveside and reflects on the loss of everyone they have known, describing grief as a cold, numbing freeze that leaves them the last survivor at an empty table. Death is framed as a temporary passage from womb to stars, but the comfort is outweighed by the isolation of being left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~cracks-in-the-walls":{"haiku":"Internal psychological collapse manifests as literal walls closing in and breaking apart the singer's mind, with no escape from the self-inflicted mental disease.","opus":"Depression and mental collapse rendered as literal cracks spreading through the walls of a room that turns out to be the inside of the sufferer's own head, imprisoning him until he begs God to kill him; the illness is his own doing yet inescapable.","sonnet":"Mental illness framed as structural collapse: the speaker watches the walls of their own mind crumbling, begging for death as both the disease and the architecture of their sanity disintegrate around them.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~day-of-the-storm":{"haiku":"A apocalyptic storm annihilates humanity as punishment for greed and sin. Billions die in flood and fire while God turns away from the world.","opus":"Sal Abruscato's apocalyptic doom vision of humanity purged by its own greed while an indifferent God looks away, the earth itself winning the fight against its parasite; grim ending softened only by nature's renewal after we're gone.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic vision of God abandoning humanity to annihilation — six billion dead, oceans of blood, the earth purging its parasitic human occupants — and then a strange final image of sun breaking through, suggesting the planet itself survives and heals.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~dead-of-winter":{"haiku":"Humans drift toward death like snowflakes falling in winter, fading into the dark.","opus":"Founded by ex-Type O Negative and Life of Agony drummer Sal Abruscato, the band delivers a wintry rumination on death, likening people to fragile flakes and bare branches surrendering to the cold. The absent one never returns, the sky empties out, and the season swallows everything, mortality rendered as an inevitable, gothic seasonal decline.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality rendered through winter imagery — snowflakes drifting, leaves falling, stars collapsing — frames absence and death as the natural rhythm of all things. The refrain offers no consolation, only the pattern repeating.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~devil-came-with-a-smile":{"haiku":"A Faustian bargain in which someone trades their soul for fame and fortune, only to discover the devil's promises lead to ruin and death.","opus":"A cautionary tale of a musician who signs away his soul to a smiling devil in exchange for fame, riches, and rock stardom. Once used up, he is dropped and replaced, ending poor, ruined, and headed for a nameless grave.","sonnet":"A rock musician sells his soul to the devil for fame, rides a brief high of money and chart success, then watches it all collapse into overdoses, jail time, and abandonment — the devil simply moves on to new replacements.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~devil-in-the-closet":{"haiku":"A child's nightmares manifest as a demonic entity rising from the closet at 3 AM, claiming to collect children's souls. The young narrator lives in terror despite prayer, sleepless and scarred, unable to escape the supernatural threat.","opus":"A terrified child hides under the covers each night from a devil that emerges from the closet, a fallen angel who claims to collect children and take their souls, as prayers fail and the nightmares turn real.","sonnet":"A child's terror of a literal devil that emerges from the closet at night escalates into a visitation where the fallen angel announces it collects children's souls, leaving the narrator scarred and damned.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~die-alone":{"haiku":"Isolation and loss amid repeated life collapse. No matter who surrounds you, death and ultimate solitude are inevitable.","opus":"A meditation on sudden loss, betrayal and grief, noting how worlds can collapse overnight and loved ones vanish. Its refrain insists on one grim certainty: no matter what, you always die alone.","sonnet":"A song about repeated loss, isolation, and the certainty of dying alone — the one constant the speaker can count on across a life that has fallen apart many times. Suicidal thoughts appear as a recurring feature of this bleakness rather than a singular crisis.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~dmslt":{"haiku":"Life feels meaningless and cruel, with a mind collapsing under the weight of existence and the desire to end it.","opus":"Exhaustion with a cruel, painful life, as the narrator feels his mind crumbling, sees no point in living or praying, and expresses a wish to lie down, take a pill, and let himself die.","sonnet":"The narrator describes a life of unrelenting pain and failure in a world he experiences as cruel from birth, expressing an exhausted wish to simply lie down and die.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's acronym unpacks in the chorus's flat verdict that existence has stopped adding up to anything worth keeping. Sal Abruscato writes suicidal ideation with the funeral-slow gravity Type O Negative brought to romance, except here no lover is on the hook, only depletion. The song's trick sits in its middle verse, which briefly gropes after a reason to remain — a suspicion that reality hides some larger meaning. Then it bends that flicker into a sturdier case for leaving: a termination framed as overdue, a society itself misfiring, the swallowed dose as a farewell gesture. By the bridge the ache finally has a source in a brutal world and a self too fragile to armor. But the read that matters is how the chorus refuses to evolve; it just recurs, untouched. For this narrator the depressive conclusion isn't argued toward — it's simply the floor everything else keeps settling back onto.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"depressive conclusion circling back as unanswerable floor","built":"the chorus refuses to evolve, recurring untouched while verses argue around it"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~growing-old":{"haiku":"A person watches his body decay—lines, white hair, disease—as youth fades into a hollow shell. He reflects on mortality, from boyhood dreams of forever to burial and dust.","opus":"A first-person account of decay through aging, cataloguing the physical ruin of a man too weak to leave his chair as sickness overtakes him. The heart of it is the gap between a boy who once assumed he was immortal and the corpse now lowered into the ground, mortality delivered without comfort.","sonnet":"Aging and death narrated with flat-eyed acceptance — the narrator watches his body break down, his bones turn to dust, looks back at the boy who thought he'd live forever, and watches himself get lowered into the ground in a bittersweet goodbye.","src":"haiku","fable":"Abruscato inherits Type O Negative's funereal doom but discards Peter Steele's black-comic wink, so decay gets played dead straight. The lyric works as a flat inventory of a body clocking its own ruin, a list of yellowing nails and a gaze fixed on nothing. That catalogue breaks only once, for the child who was certain he'd never die, and dropping that memory into the middle turns everything around it into the slow disproving of a boy's promise. By the end the singer has moved from armchair to casket with no shift in tone at all. The recurring line about bones going to powder in the soil stops reading as grief and starts reading as the destination this song was quietly coasting toward.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["death & grief"],"means":"a body cataloguing its own ruin in flat unbroken inventory","built":"the single child-memory dropped mid-list makes everything before and after its slow disproof"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~heroin-train":{"haiku":"A person spirals into homelessness and heroin addiction, described through images of cardboard-box living and track marks. The song traces decay from disconnected family ties to overdose and death, framed as a one-way journey on the needle's track.","opus":"A homeless addict chronicles his descent through heroin, living in a cardboard box, chasing fixes with a homeless girlfriend and losing family and friends, ending in an overdose on a gurney where the needle finally sets him free.","sonnet":"The song traces a heroin addict's descent from homelessness and petty survival through dependence and relationship breakdown to a fatal overdose on a gurney, framing the needle as both ruin and release.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~in-the-sleeping-death":{"haiku":"A song depicting suicidal intent, with the speaker lying in their own blood contemplating a final escape from the pain of a broken heart.","opus":"Doom-laden first-person suicide, a broken heart narrating its own bleeding out toward a final, permanent sleep. There's no rescue offered, only the grim serenity of someone who has already decided to be forever gone.","sonnet":"A suicide by self-cutting narrated in clinical, intimate detail — the narrator lies in a pool of blood watching life pass before his eyes, choosing to let go, and the song is almost tender in its matter-of-fact description of the act and the final sleep.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~killer-by-night":{"haiku":"A man leads a dual life as a devoted family man by day while secretly being a predatory killer at night, driven by violent urges and the desire to victimize others. His internal shadows mirror his external crimes.","opus":"A doom-laced serial-killer narrative, the family man leading a murderous double life driven by dream-urges he can't shake. Sammy Blaze plays it as gothic dread rather than shock, the ordinary mask being the horror.","sonnet":"A first-person serial killer narrative — a family man by day who drives out at night to murder, describing urges, choking fantasies, and body bags in his truck with flat, methodical detail, framing his victims' deaths as the result of bad luck in encountering someone like him.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~meet-the-wolf":{"haiku":"A seductive predator poses as a lover, addicting and consuming someone with dark desire, using biblical metaphor to frame erotic domination.","opus":"A predatory reworking of Red Riding Hood into a seduction, the wolf as devil-lover coaxing innocence into sin. Sal Abruscato's doom sludge makes the fairy-tale menace explicit, addiction and desire framed as the same trap.","sonnet":"A retelling of Little Red Riding Hood as explicit sexual predation — the wolf figure propositions Red Riding Hood with escalating sexual detail, framing himself as the devil and her as an addicted willing participant.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~pill-head":{"haiku":"A person relies on pills to manage depression and isolation, unable to face reality without chemical escape. They float through existence medicated, unable to engage with the world or form genuine connections.","opus":"A doom-laced confession of numbing every feeling with pills to escape a reality the narrator can't face, sliding toward suicidal ideation. The pull is how the pharmaceutical high becomes both the only comfort and the slow means of disappearing.","sonnet":"A portrait of severe prescription drug dependency and social isolation — the narrator wakes up, grabs a handful of pills, and drifts through days medicated against a reality he can't face. He has no friends, no ambition, four walls as his only companions, and uses the drugs as a slow alternative to suicide.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~serial-killer":{"haiku":"The song narrates predatory serial murder from the killer's perspective, describing victims and trophies.","opus":"A predator narrates in the first person, luring a young girl and cataloguing his trophies and victims with chilling calm. The disturbing hook is his insistence that he could be anyone familiar to you, framing evil not as monstrous but as ordinary and close at hand.","sonnet":"First-person narration from a serial killer's point of view — luring victims, crossing state lines, keeping trophy body parts, and revelling in the killing as disease and appetite rather than remorse.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~shallow-grave":{"haiku":"Betrayal by a deceptive lover leads to emotional death and the desire to bury that person in the mind, letting them decay and be forgotten.","opus":"A bitter fantasy of revenge against a deceitful lover the narrator regrets ever trusting and falling for. He imagines killing and burying her in a shallow, nameless grave, both literally and by erasing her from his mind.","sonnet":"Fury and relief at the end of a toxic relationship with a deceptive woman: the narrator buries her metaphorically in his mind, letting the memory rot while moving on without mourning.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~the-needle-in-you":{"haiku":"Addiction personified as Lucifer guides someone toward overdose, exploiting pain and self-destruction until the narrator celebrates their suicide.","opus":"Heroin addiction personified as Lucifer, the drug itself narrating as it seduces, enables and ultimately kills the user, taking credit for the overdose and assisted suicide; a chillingly first-person account of the needle as the anti-Christ that fed the disease to death.","sonnet":"Heroin personified as a demonic voice — Lucifer himself, the needle — seducing an addict through withdrawal until it delivers them to overdose and death, taking full credit for the suicide.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~to-die-in-your-arms":{"haiku":"Romantic darkness where death becomes intimate desire. Bloodshed, black lace, and final breath are treasured exchanges between lovers in a morbid embrace.","opus":"A dying narrator longs to bleed out in the arms of a lover, treasuring his blood on their skin as the ultimate act of intimacy. He asks to be covered in black lace and kissed as his heart stops, saying goodbye.","sonnet":"The speaker longs to bleed out and die in the arms of a dark, black-lipped beloved, framing death in that embrace as the greatest possible pleasure and the only thing left worth wanting. The world has worn the soul down to nothing, and this final act is presented as a treasured release.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~we-all-break-down":{"haiku":"The world fractures while people suffer and die for religious ideologies and systems that fail them. The song laments societal collapse and the tears shed by those trapped in a broken system.","opus":"A weary meditation on human frailty, insisting that everyone eventually crumbles and surrenders, then widening from personal collapse to a planet unraveling over faith-driven bloodshed. Formed by Type O Negative's Sal Abruscato, the band carry that gothic-doom heaviness into a grim humanism where private and global despair mirror each other.","sonnet":"Universal collapse observed from three distances — children playing without knowing, an old man praying without hope, a world where people die for religious beliefs and loved ones weep. The refrain offers no fix, only recognition: everyone falls, everyone breaks, everyone gives up at some point.","src":"haiku"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~when-crows-descend-upon-you":{"haiku":"Death arrives in the form of black crows that feed on broken bodies and lies, inevitably claiming all souls regardless of their decay.","opus":"Crows gather over a dying, broken soul, tied to the lies that person told, while Death himself rides a pale horse to claim them. The imagery marks the approach and inevitability of dying.","sonnet":"Death, figured as a pale horse and a murder of black crows, closes in on a person who is already breaking apart — the birds come from lies and wait for the body to rot, as time stops for no one and the soul is inevitably taken.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sal Abruscato's doom project stages a deathbed as a scavenging, treating a dying man's soul, then his shattered skeleton, then his rotting flesh as three courses for the flock across verses that climb from spiritual fatigue to open decomposition. The turn that lifts it above generic gothic dressing is that these carrion birds hatch from the man's own falsehoods, so the swarm is his corruption made flesh and circling back to feed — a private guilt he can no longer outrace. Between the verses a flat refrain insists the clock spares no one, draining any hope of reprieve, and the bridge hands narration to the pale rider of Revelation who has trailed the man all along, unhurried and inevitable. The coldest stroke comes last: the winged mourners don't strike so much as perch and wait, every exit already sealed, so the dread lives less in the kill than in its arithmetic certainty.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The bridge plants the band's own name — Abruscato, Type O Negative's original drummer, built the project on Revelation's pale horse, and has said the song began with Hitchcock's The Birds.","themes":["death & grief","shame & repression","faith & the occult"],"means":"the carrion birds hatch from the dying man's own falsehoods","built":"the pale rider narrates the bridge having trailed the man all along"},
"a-pale-horse-named-death~when-the-world-becomes-undone":{"opus":"An apocalyptic vision of a collapsing world where children's tears turn to blood and love falls apart; the singer harbors the dread that God is dead and doesn't care, blaming humanity itself for watching the world burn undone.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic dirge — children dreaming of paradise while tears turn to blood, love collapsing into deflated hearts, a God declared dead and indifferent — and the conclusion that humanity is collectively to blame for watching its world burn and leaving nothing for the next generation.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-perfect-circle~3-libras": {"haiku":"A person tested someone to see their depth, finding only disappointment and the realization they remain unseen.","opus":"Maynard on the ache of offering yourself to someone who looks straight through you. The wounded angel imagery captures romantic disappointment turning into a deeper grief, being fully present and still going completely unseen.","sonnet":"A song about the devastating feeling of being invisible to someone you see clearly — the speaker offers themselves fully and is looked through as if they don't exist, leaving them with nothing but the confirmation that this person cannot or will not see them.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about handing someone your plainest self and watching it register as weightless, the narrator offering the simplest gesture and getting no flicker of recognition in return. Maynard reads the beloved as gorgeous and vacant at once, searching that face for some depth and finding only the reflected grief of his own wanting. The refusal that closes it, stammered and repeated until the words wear smooth, does the work the verses only describe: presence going unwitnessed, thinning toward erasure through sheer helpless insistence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Keenan has said he wrote it about several people in his life born around the same time, titling it so the Libra-born among them could battle out who it was about; he never named them. The song's wounded ambiguity is by design.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"offering the plainest self and watching it register as weightless","built":"refusal repeated until the words wear smooth into erasure"},
"a-perfect-circle~a-stranger": {"haiku":"The speaker dismisses an enigmatic figure who suddenly disappeared, rationalizing indifference by framing them as irrelevant and destructive.","opus":"Maynard James Keenan dissects the strange grief of losing someone he pretended not to need, formulating denials even as their absence unmoors him. The tenderness curdles into fury because detachment was always a lie.","sonnet":"The narrator acknowledges being drawn to someone who disrupts their life like a tornado, yet fights the pull — they'd rather the stranger disappear than keep tearing their world apart, even as they admit the fascination is real.","src":"haiku","fable":"On Thirteenth Step, an album otherwise fixed on addiction and recovery, this reads as the same craving grammar aimed at a person: the singer coaxing a skittish, half-feral thing closer with soothing gestures while quietly building the case that it means nothing to him. The mask is that pose of indifference — he asks why he should care about someone who keeps disappearing — and underneath it the silence they leave is unbearable enough that he has to fill it with something. So the closing turn drops the calm entirely and names the other as a natural disaster to be survived, the plea to leave doubling as an admission of how much damage staying does. Maynard James Keenan lets the two registers, the tender lure and the raw expulsion, sit as the same wound: you only rehearse this hard how little you need someone when the need is the problem.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"performing indifference to a need too unbearable to name","built":"the tender lure and the raw expulsion named as the same wound at the close"},
"a-perfect-circle~a-stranger-acoustic":{"haiku":"Distance breeds denial; the speaker constructs protective numbness while watching a phantom tornado destroy will from a position of claimed indifference.","opus":"Coaxing out a timid, wild figure while simultaneously building denials against feeling anything for them. The dismissal of the stranger and the plea for them to leave reveal how much they've gotten under the skin.","sonnet":"Someone trying to manipulate or lure out a guarded, unpredictable person — and then realizing that this volatile stranger is destroying their sense of self, finally telling them to leave and never come back.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~annihilation": {"haiku":"Dehumanization leads to arms production and weapons that hasten national destruction, controlled by power-hungry leaders. Peace is possible if people reject oppressive systems and choose humanity over death.","opus":"A stark anti-war statement tracing the path from stripping people of their humanity to manufacturing weapons that speed a country toward self-destruction. Its argument is that those who build their lives around death will be undone by it, and the ending frames a plain choice, dismantle the system or face total ruin.","sonnet":"A cover of a Dead Kennedys-era song about the military-industrial cycle — dehumanization leads to arms production leads to national destruction, and those who build their power on death will die by it. The choice is framed as binary: peace or annihilation.","src":"haiku","fable":"Originally a Crucifix song, this is one of the covers Maynard James Keenan and Billy Howerdel gathered on eMOTIVe, an album of anti-war material timed to the 2004 US presidential election. The argument is a closed circuit built to be inescapable: dehumanization feeds weapons manufacture, weapons feed the state's drive toward its own ruin, and the loop restarts unchanged each verse — the near-total repetition is the point, a machine cycling with no exit written into it. What A Perfect Circle add is register: where the original hurled the thesis as anarcho-punk agitprop, Keenan sings it slow and almost seductive, so the syllogism arrives as a lullaby rather than a shout. Only at the end does the loop break to hand you the two words it was narrowing toward all along — peace or annihilation — recast from a slogan into a personal verdict aimed at a listener heading to the polls.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society"],"means":"closing a dehumanization loop with no exit written into it","built":"sung slow and seductive, the syllogism arrives as a lullaby rather than a shout"},
"a-perfect-circle~ashes-to-ashes-live":{"haiku":"Addiction and celebrity corruption through fame's machinery, the fallen idol caught between desire and the paralyzing weight of it all.","opus":"A cover of Bowie's reckoning with his Major Tom, now revealed as a strung-out junkie, folding the astronaut fantasy into the shame and relapse of addiction. Maynard's version leans into the wreckage beneath the glam.","sonnet":"Major Tom from Space Oddity returns, revealed as a junkie strung out in heaven's high, hitting all-time lows between vows to stay clean tonight. The song is Bowie's self-examination dressed as tabloid report: Action Man, Ground Control, his mama's warning — the astronaut as addict, the escape into space as escape into addiction, the cycle unbroken.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~blue": {"haiku":"Willful ignorance of uncomfortable truth, choosing not to see or understand while helplessly watching someone's deterioration.","opus":"Maynard on willful denial, choosing not to see someone you love suffocating right in front of you. Staying in the shallows and ignoring the smoke becomes a quiet act of cowardice dressed up as peace.","sonnet":"The narrator deliberately avoids facing the truth about someone who is in serious distress — choosing ignorance and a forced smile over acknowledgment — while the repeated image of the person \"turning blue\" makes clear that this wilful blindness has allowed real harm to go unaddressed.","src":"haiku","fable":"Blue is a bystander's confession, sung by someone who watches a loved one asphyxiate on her own dependency and deliberately keeps his eyes shut against it. He stays in water too dry to drown him because he never taught himself to face a truth this deep, rereading her drugged, slumping nods as permission and waving away the evidence like cigarette haze he can smile through. Maynard James Keenan, working inside Thirteenth Step's recovery-and-relapse concept, sharpens the cruelty when the narrator recasts her oxygen-starved, dusk-tinged lips as a flattering shade rather than an emergency. The chant of not wanting to know never breaks into action; it just hardens, the enabler aestheticizing the suffocation he refuses to interrupt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"aestheticizing a suffocation he refuses to interrupt","built":"oxygen-starved lips recast as a flattering shade rather than an emergency"},
"a-perfect-circle~bre-a": {"haiku":"A place or person offers healing and refuge to someone vulnerable. The song treats vulnerability as safe within the embrace of Breña, a healing presence.","opus":"Maynard offers a hushed prayer to a place or presence named Breña, water and vulnerability as the path to healing. It's about surrendering openly to being led somewhere safe and unknown, fragility accepted as the way through.","sonnet":"A prayer for healing directed at a natural or spiritual place called Breña — the speaker is wounded and vulnerable, asking to be guided into unfamiliar territory and bathed in restorative waters, surrendering to the process of being made whole.","src":"haiku","fable":"A prayer addressed to a name, in the register of a hymn — one of Mer de noms' titles-as-people, here made into a healing water the singer begs to be taken under by. The lyric barely progresses because it isn't argument but incantation: the same plea to be healed cycles back, and the drift toward openings and toward being led closer is the whole motion, a supplicant dissolving rather than arriving. Its quiet nerve is the equation it never explains away — that going under and being made vulnerable is the safety, surrender reframed as the only cure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"surrender to another reframed as the only available healing","built":"repetition enacts the incantation rather than building an argument"},
"a-perfect-circle~by-and-down-the-river":{"haiku":"The speaker searches for authenticity and humility in another's eyes but finds only emptiness and surfaces, ultimately rejecting their shallow adoration.","opus":"Searching a dwindling figure's face for any sign of genuine humility and finding only hollow performance, the narrator sends a false idol drifting off to drown, a swollen body crushed by the very worship it once basked in. A Perfect Circle sharpen Maynard Keenan's fixation on false idols, the sinking figure an emblem of celebrity worship rotting under its own adoration.","sonnet":"A figure of hollow charisma — all beautiful words and no substance beneath — is watched carefully for any trace of genuine humility and found empty. The Pied Piper image sends him floating away, his inflated self crushed under the weight of adoration he courted.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~counting-bodies-like-sheep-to-the-rhythm-of-the-war-drums": {"haiku":"A shepherd isolates prey through comfort and sedation. Sleep replaces truth while bodies fall to war's rhythm.","opus":"A sinister lullaby from a controlling voice that soothes the listener back to sleep to keep them numb to war and truth, its false protection revealed as isolation that saves you only from yourself; a companion to the anti-war Pet.","sonnet":"A lullaby sung by an authoritarian power to a compliant population — the voice promises protection from pain, truth, and choice, insisting it cares more than the dangerous outside world; the counting of bodies like sheep to war drums makes clear that the protection is also obliteration of will.","src":"haiku","fable":"Authority here wears the costume of tenderness, cooing a bedside lullaby to a populace it is actually pacifying, so that shelter and captivity become indistinguishable and the caretaker drawing the curtain is the same hand herding the dead into countable rows. Maynard Keenan, cutting a covers-and-protest record amid the Iraq war, exposes how the promise of safety extracts a price: your curiosity, your dissent, your instinct for self-preservation, even your own choices relabeled as dangers. The hypnotic order to slumber, hammered until it beats like the marching percussion it rides, performs the very sedation it describes, which makes the track's grinding monotony its whole argument rather than its flaw. What it finally strips bare is that a protector who fences you off from everyone you love, ostensibly for your own good, has merely built a nursery-shaped prison.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Keenan has said this is not a remix of Pet but its second part, the same guardian voice carried forward and boiled down. Listen for the sedating figure returning, harder and more mechanical than before.","themes":["surveillance & control","war & battle","politics & society"],"means":"cooing shelter until captivity and protection become indistinguishable","built":"the hypnotic order to slumber performs the very sedation it describes"},
"a-perfect-circle~delicious": {"haiku":"The song savors watching someone face the consequences of their own arrogance and cruel indifference, finding satisfaction in their dread and inevitable downfall.","opus":"A pointed taste of schadenfreude aimed at an arrogant, uncaring person who ignored the consequences; the singer savors watching poetic justice catch up as they're hoisted by their own petard.","sonnet":"The narrator takes cold satisfaction watching someone arrogant suffer the consequences of their own reckless actions, framing it as poetic justice for a person who never cared about the collateral damage they caused.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is the schadenfreude anthem, sung by a narrator relishing the ruin of someone reckless who scattered harm without care and now flinches as the reckoning arrives. Maynard James Keenan turns the cruelty communal, an us against the careless one, savoring their dread the way one savors a fine meal. The mock-tender lullaby closing each turn twists it further, sending the ruined off to their rest with counterfeit kindness while everyone watching feasts on the collapse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"savoring a reckless person's ruin as a communal feast","built":"mock-tender lullaby sends the ruined to rest with counterfeit kindness"},
"a-perfect-circle~disillusioned": {"haiku":"Addiction to digital stimulation and dopamine hijacks human agency and disconnects people from genuine connection. The song calls for stepping away from technology obsession to restore authentic resonance with others.","opus":"Maynard's diagnosis of the smartphone age, humanity re-wired into Pavlovian addicts of the immediate glow, urging us to put the silicon down, drop back into silence, and rediscover that we were never really islands.","sonnet":"A diagnosis of technology addiction and algorithmic conditioning — dopamine loops, screen dependency, and Pavlovian obedience have severed people from genuine connection, and the song urges disconnecting from devices to find authentic resonance.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a sermon against the dopamine economy, framing our compulsive pull toward every glowing screen as an engineered hunger that trains us like lab animals and mistakes the feed for a life. Maynard James Keenan runs the verses cold and clinical — reflex, fixation, obedient scrolling — then swings the chorus into something almost pastoral, a prescription to drop the device, lie flat against the earth, and hear the silence underneath. The whole turn hinges on scale: one person chasing the next bright flicker is only a manufactured wound, but that same person is also a single throat in a vast chorus, and the cure is tuning back into the others rather than the glass.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"framing compulsive screen-pull as an engineered hunger mistaken for a life","built":"cold clinical verses pivot to a pastoral chorus prescribing earth and silence"},
"a-perfect-circle~eat-the-elephant":{"haiku":"Overwhelming tasks demand incremental courage; action dissolves paralysis, though the journey remains daunting and solitary.","opus":"Maynard James Keenan turns a proverb about overwhelming tasks into a meditation on paralysis and grief, the daunting undertaking becoming any life project too large to face without the person who once steadied you. The cure is brutally simple: just begin.","sonnet":"The speaker is paralyzed by the enormity of a task — unnamed but clearly massive — and the absence of someone who used to help orient them. The instruction given is elemental: stop looking for the beginning, just take one step, one swing, one bite, go all in.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~feathers":{"haiku":"Shared wounds and stories create connection and healing, with the speaker suggesting that speaking about suffering might transform its heavy burden into something lighter.","opus":"Addressed to another damaged person, this offers mutual healing and suggests that pain, once spoken aloud, might lose its weight and become bearable. A Perfect Circle swap their usual acidity for tenderness, Maynard Keenan turning a list of heavy burdens into an argument that confession itself is what lightens them.","sonnet":"Two damaged people meet at the boundary their wounds have drawn between them, recognising a shared weight — sadness, onus, undeserved shame — that operates like armour and anchor both. The hope is that by speaking the burden aloud, it might become feathers instead of stone.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~fiddle-and-the-drum":{"haiku":"America trades peaceful dialogue (fiddle) for violent militarism (drum); the song mourns the loss of reason and the dominance of force.","opus":"Addressing a warlike friend who traded the fiddle for the drum, the narrator mourns a nation once trusted, now raising fists against those who remember its better nature. A Perfect Circle, Maynard James Keenan's more melodic vehicle, recast this Joni Mitchell a cappella as anti-war lament on their 2004 covers record, turning quiet reproach toward America itself.","sonnet":"A lament addressed to America as a lost friend who traded music and handshakes for drums and fists. The speaker grieves the nation's turn toward aggression, remembering its better nature and pleading for a return to peace before the whole world learns to fear its rhythm.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~freedom-of-choice": {"haiku":"The song laments that freedom and choice are theoretical in a coercive system where most people don't want their voice heard. It frames the paradox of freedom of choice as meaningless when the will to exercise it is absent.","opus":"A cover meditating on how, given the freedom of choice, people passively follow along and stay silent like a Roman pawn watching things fall, ultimately preferring freedom from choice over the responsibility of choosing.","sonnet":"A cover of Devo's song arguing that people in a free society are given the power of choice but consistently choose not to use it, illustrated through the image of a passive pawn watching a civilization fall.","src":"haiku","fable":"Recast as protest rather than satire, the cover exposes how a nation's boast of liberty curdles into paralysis, abundant options revealed as a way to keep the powerless in line. A lone follower who stays quiet and secure, sure his objection would change nothing, becomes the recording's real accusation. Slowed and stripped of its original bounce, it turns a consumer-age satire into a grim reckoning with consent under an ostensibly free country.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control"],"means":"exposing abundant choice as the mechanism that keeps the powerless in line","built":"stripped of its original bounce, consumer satire hardens into a reckoning with manufactured consent"},
"a-perfect-circle~get-the-lead-out":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects small talk and procrastination, urging immediate action and momentum toward goals without time for hesitation or coddling.","opus":"A brisk motivational shove, cutting off small talk to insist there are goals to chase and no time to waste on hesitation. A Perfect Circle keep it terse and driving, Maynard Keenan turning encouragement into an almost comic refusal to indulge anyone who dawdles or needs coddling.","sonnet":"An impatient, brisk voice refuses idle talk and coddling, insisting there are places to be and mountains to climb. The energy is purely kinetic — no argument, no nuance, just the demand to stop stalling and move.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~gimme-gimme-gimme": {"haiku":"Insatiable greed and want drive the narrator to dangerous excess, willing to destroy themselves and others. They demand more without purpose or justification.","opus":"A restless voice keeps demanding more with no justification, comparing itself to a primed firearm idling with no outlet but to discharge. It half-knows the appetite is ruinous and could be the death of it.","sonnet":"A figure describes himself as a loaded gun going off — idle, aggressive, and driven by insatiable, unexamined craving. He acknowledges the world has bigger problems but dismisses that, and admits his relentless need for more will eventually kill him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A snarling hardcore complaint about craving and boredom gets pulled onto an all-covers record built to indict wartime appetite, and the recasting is the whole argument. Slowed from a sneer into something cold and processed, the narrator sits cocked and idle, hungry for a fix he can't name and jeering that his troubles won't be solved by dropping ordnance overseas. Stretched taut, the endless I-want-more-and-don't-ask-why stops reading as a strung-out kid and starts reading as a nation, an appetite that keeps taking until it consumes itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","addiction & self-destruction","war & battle"],"means":"stretching one strung-out kid's hunger until it reads as a nation's appetite","built":"slowed from a sneer into something cold, the same lines stop being personal and become policy"},
"a-perfect-circle~gravity": {"haiku":"Despair pulls the speaker down; they surrender to gravity and seek rescue from ruin. The song frames survival as choice and external salvation as necessary.","opus":"An addict's or depressive's surrender, the narrator worn out and unable to keep himself upright, giving in to forces beyond his control while begging to be caught, healed, and restored, choosing to keep living. The desperate wish to still his own hands before they reach for one more dose is a raw struggle against relapse.","sonnet":"A person at rock bottom — dizzy, unable to stand, fighting the urge to grab another pill — chooses life anyway, surrendering to the unknown and asking to be lifted back up, a fragile but deliberate decision to survive rather than disappear.","src":"haiku","fable":"Thirteenth Step is Maynard James Keenan's addiction record, and Gravity is its capitulation and its prayer at once: an addict stripped of every last strategy, out of tricks and out of pride, choosing to stop struggling against the pull that has already won. The title reframes surrender as physics rather than defeat — you cannot argue with gravity, only fall into it and hope something catches you on the way down, which is why the plea and the descent share the same motion. The bridge is where the mask drops and the metaphor turns literal, the hands begging to be stopped before they reach for another pill or needle, so that the graceful language of falling and the ugly mechanics of a relapse are revealed as one act. What redeems it from despair is the flat repetition of the decision to live, said again and again less like a triumph than like a handhold gripped by someone who knows he could let go.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape"],"means":"choosing surrender when every other strategy has already failed","built":"the title reframes capitulation as physics so defeat becomes a direction not a verdict"},
"a-perfect-circle~hourglass":{"haiku":"Countdown to collapse of civilization: time ticks toward destruction as sentinels die and political systems crumble toward tyranny, with no hope remaining in the broken hourglass.","opus":"Maynard James Keenan's side project, born from Tool's studio downtime, sets a countdown against political entropy. The lyric watches governance degrade through classical forms, from aristocracy to a fused republicrat sludge, while sentinels are slaughtered and a smashed hourglass runs out. Panic imagery of prickling neck hairs and ticking grains dramatizes a civilization running down its own clock toward zero.","sonnet":"A society's political order cycles endlessly through aristocracy, oligarchy, and republican rot, each form collapsing into the next as the sand runs out. There is no hope left in the hourglass — only an accelerating countdown, the sentinels already dead, the clock ticking toward a terminal zero.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~imagine": {"haiku":"A utopian vision imagining a world without hierarchy, religion, or material greed where people live in peace and unity. The song argues that this dream is reachable if enough people embrace collective human connection over institutional division.","opus":"A cover of Lennon's utopian vision, reframed by A Perfect Circle's bleaden delivery: the same dream of no heaven, no borders, no possessions and a united humanity, but sung so darkly it plays as ironic elegy for how unreachable that peace has become.","sonnet":"A cover of John Lennon's utopian plea, unchanged in words: a vision of humanity freed from nations, religions, and private property, living in collective peace. APC's version tends to render it with more ambivalence or irony, but the lyrical content remains a sincere appeal for radical solidarity.","src":"haiku","fable":"John Lennon wrote this as a serene wager that peace is imaginable; A Perfect Circle, choosing it as the anchor of eMOTIVe — an album of protest covers dropped in the run-up to the 2004 U.S. election — reads the same words as an accusation. Maynard James Keenan strips out the utopian warmth and slows the melody into something dirge-like and hollowed, so the invitation to imagine no countries and no religion no longer sounds hopeful but weary, an indictment aimed at a nation that had every chance to try and didn't. The recasting turns the dreamer's line inside out: where Lennon insisted he was not the only one, this version sounds like a man who suspects he is, singing a lullaby over the ruins of the thing it describes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","war & battle"],"means":"singing a utopian invitation as an elegy over its own ruins","built":"the dirge tempo turns Lennon's we-are-not-alone into a lone man who suspects he is"},
"a-perfect-circle~judith":{"haiku":"A critique of blind faith: the singer accuses a believer's god of abandoning them, yet that person continues to pray and obey without ever questioning why they suffer.","opus":"Maynard James Keenan's furious address to his mother Judith, who kept her Christian faith even after an aneurysm left her paralyzed. The rage isn't at her but at that unquestioning devotion — praising a savior who left her broken and never once asking why, a believer's loyalty turned into the cruelest irony.","sonnet":"The speaker addresses someone whose devout faith has not protected them from suffering, arguing that their god abandoned them while they still pray and never question why. It is a furious rejection of blind religious devotion in the face of real pain.","src":"haiku","fable":"Named for Maynard Keenan's mother, who was paralyzed by an aneurysm and remained devout for the rest of her life, the song is his fury at her faith voiced on her behalf. He calls her an inspiration and then twists the compliment into its opposite, holding her up as a model of everything he refuses to become, before aiming the blasphemy not at her but at the God who crippled her and still collects her gratitude. The engine is a bitter syllogism: she committed no crime, drove no spear, never so much as bit the fruit or asked a single question, and her wages were a paralyzed body, yet she thanks the one who dealt it. What makes the chorus intolerable is its arithmetic of blame reversed into praise, the same voice insisting over and over that every wound was a gift meant for her.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","family & blood"],"means":"blasphemy aimed at god voiced on the believer's behalf","built":"compliment twisted into its opposite pins the syllogism of unearned suffering"},
"a-perfect-circle~let-s-have-a-war": {"haiku":"The song cynically proposes war as a solution to overpopulation, profit, and social control, satirizing how elites benefit while the vulnerable bear the cost.","opus":"A bitterly sarcastic anti-war song that reframes armed conflict as a deliberate scheme to cull surplus population and enrich the powerful. It lists the supposed payoffs, from freeing up real estate to boosting the stock market and stuffing wallets, treating ordinary people as disposable. Originally a Fear song, covered here with the same nihilistic disgust.","sonnet":"A savage satirical proposal to solve overpopulation, economic stagnation, and social tension through war — each verse names a cynical beneficiary (markets, networks, the middle class) in a deadpan catalog of how war is sold and profited from.","src":"haiku","fable":"Fear wrote this in 1982 as hardcore satire, and its engine is a collision of two voices: a numb crowd counting itself over and over, and a bracketed chorus of reasons to thin that crowd out — die for the money, for the space, for the fattened wallet, blame pinned on whichever group is handiest. The math is the horror: because there are so many of us, war is framed as a clearing operation, waste management dressed as policy, with the enemy relocated from any border to inside the home and the city already burning before suburbia's turn. On eMOTIVe, A Perfect Circle's contribution is the act of selection during a wartime election year — Maynard James Keenan reaches back to a punk indictment of manufactured war-for-profit and lets the curation do the accusing, no update needed because the accusation never expired. The cover's only real move is tonal: what Fear barked as a sneer becomes a colder, more processed dirge, so the satire stops sounding like a joke and starts sounding like a briefing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"eMOTIVe was released on 2 November 2004 — US election day; the release date is part of the gesture.","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","money & the street"],"means":"satire's chill turning war into a clearance operation","built":"cold production turns the punk sneer into a briefing"},
"a-perfect-circle~magdalena": {"haiku":"Obsessive desire for a woman treated as a sacred or divine object. The narrator would sacrifice everything and debase himself for one moment with her.","opus":"An overwhelming, near-religious lust in which a woman is worshipped like a living deity or dark saint. The narrator would surrender his soul and dignity in tiny installments for a single kiss or taste of her, desire pitched at the intensity of prayer.","sonnet":"A song about overwhelming, self-debasing desire for a woman cast as a goddess or sacred figure. The speaker frames his obsession in religious terms — altar, Madonna, soul — willing to surrender his self-esteem entirely for a single moment with her.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man kneels at the feet of a woman he has cast as a shrine, feeding her coins the way a supplicant leaves offerings, so that devotion and transaction blur into one gesture he cannot untangle. Every craving he names comes priced by the bill, his dignity and his conscience auctioned in small change for a single kiss, and the reverence curdles into something closer to purchased worship. Naming her after the Magdalene turns the whole prayer accusatory: he sanctifies her only to bargain for her body, and the exchange exposes how easily his awe was always appetite wearing borrowed robes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult","money & the street","shame & repression"],"means":"sanctifying someone only to bargain for her body","built":"the Magdalene name turns devotion into accusation mid-prayer"},
"a-perfect-circle~orestes": {"haiku":"A person seeks to sever an umbilical connection holding them back, cutting ties to escape overwhelming hostility and control.","opus":"A wrenching decision to sever a toxic bond, using umbilical imagery to describe cutting away the connection that's keeping the speaker from killing someone, or killing part of himself. Named for the Greek matricide, it frames the necessary separation as a violent, medicated act of self-liberation soaked in overwhelming hostility and grief.","sonnet":"The speaker is consumed by murderous rage toward someone they cannot fully separate from — the umbilical cord metaphor frames the connection as one that must be severed to prevent violence, and the medicated calm is the only thing holding them back.","src":"haiku","fable":"Maynard James Keenan takes the myth of Orestes — the son who kills his mother to break a family curse — and pulls it inward, so the murder becomes a metaphor for severing an unbearable psychic bond rather than a literal act. The whole song lives in one violent image system: the umbilical cord that still connects two people long after birth, and the compulsion to snip and sever it not out of hate but as the only way to stop the two of them from destroying each other. Its cruelty is that the cutting is framed as mercy — release for both parties — and the bridge betrays the cost, reaching for one more sedated moment of peace to numb a hostility the speaker can't stand feeling. The horror the song sits in is that separation and killing have collapsed into the same act; you cannot cleanly free yourself from someone you're this fused to, and the outro's endless repetition of the sever-and-not-kill line is the mind circling a wound it can neither leave nor close.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Fans have long mapped the severing imagery to Keenan's mother Judith Marie, paralyzed in 1976 and the subject of Judith, hearing a life-support decision in the cord that must be cut. The band has never confirmed it, so it stays a reading rather than a fact.","themes":["family & blood","violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"severing the unbearable bond as the only form of mercy","built":"the outro's endless repetition circles a wound it cannot leave or close"},
"a-perfect-circle~outsider-apocalypse-remix":{"haiku":"A person observes self-destruction and reckless self-sabotage with detached horror, watching someone disconnect from all meaning and deliberately disconnect further.","opus":"A furious plea aimed at someone bent on self-destruction, torn between disgust and the refusal to keep watching them throw their life away. The narcissism and the death wish read as two faces of the same collapse.","sonnet":"A narrator watches someone they care about spiral into self-destruction and narcissistic drama — \"suicidal imbecile,\" \"medicated drama queen\" — and wrestles with the futility of intervention as the person disconnects and self-destructs one bullet at a time, everyone eventually having their day to die.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~passive": {"haiku":"A confrontation with someone emotionally unresponsive or catatonic, interpreted as death or refusal to engage. The speaker threatens to walk away if they won't face reality and participate.","opus":"Maynard's contemptuous address to someone emotionally dead and unreachable, daring them to wake up or stay a corpse. The refrain naming it as passive-aggressive bullshit reveals the real subject: a relationship poisoned by refusal to engage, disappointment curdling into cruelty.","sonnet":"An angry confrontation with someone who is emotionally shut down and unresponsive, the speaker demanding they wake up and engage rather than retreat into passive, catatonic withdrawal.","src":"haiku","fable":"A eulogy delivered as an interrogation: the speaker leans over a body already pronounced gone and refuses the verdict, insisting the stillness is a performance staged to spite him rather than an ending he has to accept. Every order to wake and turn around is really a demand to be answered, so grief curdles into accusation and the dead are indicted for the crime of leaving, recast as an adversary too stubborn to face him. Tenderness and contempt ride the same breath, because a bond that could only survive as opposition finally has nothing to push against, and the survivor keeps shoving anyway until the coaxing collapses into the sneer underneath it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"This began years earlier as Vacant in the unreleased Tapeworm project: Danny Lohner's music, Keenan's melody, and Trent Reznor on the chorus's backing vocals in that early form, reworked here. Its industrial bones show through the finished track.","themes":["death & grief","anger & defiance"],"means":"indicting the dead for the crime of leaving rather than accepting the ending","built":"tenderness and contempt riding the same breath until coaxing collapses into sneer"},
"a-perfect-circle~peace-love-and-understanding": {"haiku":"Search for peace and harmony yields only pain and insanity in a world devoid of compassion or justice.","opus":"A cover of Nick Lowe's plea, walking a cruel and deranged world hunting for any light and wondering if hope is finished. The demand for tenderness comes as sincere astonishment that kindness should be treated as a joke in so brutal an age.","sonnet":"A cover of the classic song asking, with genuine despair, why the world has abandoned peace, love, and understanding — searching a darkness full of pain and hatred for any trustworthy guide or harmony that still exists.","src":"haiku","fable":"Choosing this old barroom plea and draining its bounce turns a defiant singalong into an interrogation, so the title question stops being armor and starts sounding like someone genuinely bewildered that basic decency curdled into a punchline. The slowed recasting weaponizes fatigue: a lone voice walks a ruined world asking where the honest and reliable people vanished to, and the refrain, repeated past comfort, wears its own hope down to a raw, disbelieving ache instead of a rallying cry.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"wearing the title question's armor down to a raw disbelieving ache","built":"the slowed tempo weaponizes fatigue until the rallying cry becomes interrogation"},
"a-perfect-circle~people-are-people": {"haiku":"Humans are fundamentally the same yet hate each other without reason; the speaker questions what triggers unfounded hatred and appeals for understanding of common humanity.","opus":"A cover of Depeche Mode's plea against baseless hatred: the narrator, hated by a stranger he's never wronged, genuinely cannot understand what makes one person hate another for differences of color or creed. The song's power is its simple, unanswered question about the irrationality of prejudice.","sonnet":"A cover of Depeche Mode's 1984 plea against irrational hatred — the narrator can't understand why someone despises them on sight, having never met, and asks sincerely what makes a man hate another man. APC played it during the Iraq War era as an anti-war statement, but the lyrics remain a simple, confused appeal for basic human decency.","src":"haiku","fable":"Chosen and slowed into a dirge, this cover turns a synth-pop plea against bigotry into something sick with disbelief, the singer addressing a stranger who wants him gone for reasons no wrong of his could ever explain. Its bafflement is the whole argument: the voice keeps asking what turns one person into another's enemy and never gets an answer, so the question loops back and hardens into an accusation it cannot drop. Where the original bounced, this recasting drags the tempo down until the hope that a raised arm might soften before it lands sounds like a bet the song already knows it has lost.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society"],"means":"looping an unanswered question until bewilderment hardens into accusation","built":"dragged to a dirge tempo, the hope of a softened arm sounds like a bet already lost"},
"a-perfect-circle~pet": {"haiku":"A controlling figure manipulates someone into complacency and ignorance by offering false safety, systematically isolating them from truth and choice under the guise of protection.","opus":"A manipulator's lullaby: a controlling voice soothing someone to sleep while promising protection from boogeymen, enemies and demons. The chilling turn is that the thing it's isolating the person from is their own reason, choice and will to survive, marching them to war drums; it's the seductive logic of authoritarian control and propaganda dressed as love.","sonnet":"A voice of authoritarian control — soothing on the surface — urges its subject to stay ignorant, tune out dissent, and submit to endless war drums, ultimately revealing that the \"protection\" it offers is isolation from truth and autonomy.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song ventriloquizes control as tenderness, letting a smothering guardian narrate his own possessiveness until the lullaby curdles into something closer to a hostage's reassurance. Maynard James Keenan and Billy Howerdel, writing in the jittery aftermath of 2001, aim the register at any authority that trades safety for surrender — the caretaker who reframes a child's independence, curiosity, and conscience as hazards only he can shield them from. What unsettles most is how the voice keeps pressing its ward toward drowsy obedience, treating awareness itself as the real threat and warm comfort as the leash that keeps everyone docile while the drums march on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"ventriloquizing control as tenderness until awareness itself becomes the threat","built":"the lullaby register curdles into a hostage's reassurance while the drums march"},
"a-perfect-circle~renhold-r":{"haiku":"A figure recoils from touch and connection, unwilling to surrender to the pull of another. Defiance burns against compulsion and entanglement.","opus":"A refusal set against an intimacy that would reduce the singer to something known and controlled; the reversed name signals a mirror-world resistance to being read and made to fall.","sonnet":"A fragmented refusal — the speaker will not submit or fall down despite someone's lies, but the lyrical content is too minimal and cryptic to build a clear interpretation beyond defiant resistance.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~rose": {"haiku":"A person breaks free from self-destructive passivity and refuses victimhood, standing up and roaring. They declare agency over their own will and refuse to be a silent sacrifice.","opus":"A portrait of self-suppression and passivity, treading softly and playing dead like a deer in headlights, that turns into a defiant refusal to lay down any longer and a rising assertion of will.","sonnet":"The narrator describes moving from a posture of passive compliance and fearful paralysis — frozen, bending to the wind, playing dead — to one of assertive self-determination, rising and roaring rather than continuing to kneel.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the long apprenticeship of self-erasure, and the moment it finally breaks. The narrator has trained himself to yield, softening each step and folding his own wants into whatever the other person demands, treating that surrender as a private discipline rather than the slow damage it is. The turn is built into the grammar: the limp, acted-upon verbs of the early verses harden into hammered first-person declarations, the creature that once froze and kept still becoming the one that stands upright and gets loud — the title itself the pivot, the flower doubling as the past tense of rising. What gives it teeth is the self-contempt at the end, Maynard James Keenan naming his own submission as a small and shameful thing and burning that disgust for fuel, so the escape reads less like triumph than like a man finally too sick of playing dead to keep doing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"burning self-contempt as the fuel for escape","built":"the title holds both the flower and the past tense of rising"},
"a-perfect-circle~sleeping-beauty": {"haiku":"The speaker believed they could heal someone poisoned by trauma, but realizes they are powerless to wake or save them.","opus":"Maynard confronts the futility of trying to save someone too far gone to be reached, mistaking ego for love. The heartbreak is in recognizing his rescue was always a delusion, and the sleeper stays poisoned and beyond waking.","sonnet":"A song about the limits of love as rescue — the narrator thought he could cure and heal a deeply damaged partner through devotion, but she is too far gone, poisoned in some fundamental way he can't reach. The ego that made him think love was enough has collapsed into failure.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man narrates his own overreach as a rescue: certain his devotion can reverse whatever has locked the woman inside herself, he offers a kiss as the cure and casts her as the fairy-tale sleeper who needs only the right touch to stir. The song watches that certainty curdle into helplessness as she stays unreachable, and the would-be savior finally names the vanity underneath it, the swagger of a man who mistook someone else's ruin for a problem his love was somehow owed the chance to solve.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"mistaking someone else's ruin for a problem love was owed","built":"the fairy-tale rescue frame collapses into named vanity at the end"},
"a-perfect-circle~sleeping-beauty-acoustic":{"haiku":"Delusion and failed rescue expose the speaker's impotence to heal another, leaving both trapped in cycles they cannot break.","opus":"The delusion of the rescuer — a lover certain he could kiss or wrench the demons out of someone too far gone to save. Maynard names the ego-drunk arrogance of believing devotion alone could rouse a person from their own hopeless sleep, and the wretched failure of it.","sonnet":"About the delusional arrogance of trying to save or fix a deeply damaged person — the speaker finally admits they couldn't wake their partner from whatever psychological prison holds them, and that the attempt came from ego rather than genuine healing power.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~so-long-and-thanks-for-all-the-fish": {"haiku":"Humanity wastes its resources on vanity and greed while civilization accelerates toward nuclear apocalypse; the world ends in atomic spectacle as icons abandon the doomed species.","opus":"Maynard's mordant apocalypse: humanity frittering away its time on trivia while icons die and the mushroom cloud arrives as festive fireworks. A grinning eulogy for a species cheering its own end.","sonnet":"A sardonic farewell to the human race — cataloguing all the money we wasted on vanity and weapons, referencing beloved cultural icons who've died, and reframing nuclear annihilation as a joyous ticker-tape finale, borrowing Douglas Adams' phrase as humanity cheerfully obliterates itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Perfect Circle stages the species' extinction as a black-tie gala and dares you to notice the guests are cheering their own erasure. The accusation is buried in the accounting: a civilization that spent its every coin on vanity, litigation, and cosmetic upkeep now watches the warheads bloom overhead and reads them as pyrotechnics, the fallout drifting down like party streamers. Keenan keeps the register euphoric on purpose, so the celebration never once cracks into alarm, which is how the song convicts a people too distracted to tell an apocalypse from a coronation. Threaded through the giddiness is a roll call of the recently dead — the beloved public figures the culture mourned as omens — and that litany turns the joke tender, less mockery than an elegy that has decided applause is the only dignity left when the lights go out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title is Douglas Adams' dolphin farewell from the Hitchhiker's Guide books. Press decoded the song's roll of the departed as 2016's dead: Gene Wilder, David Bowie, Muhammad Ali, Prince, and Carrie Fisher.","themes":["politics & society","war & battle","death & grief","party & hedonism"],"means":"staging extinction as a gala where the guests cheer their own erasure","built":"euphoric register held unbroken so the celebration never cracks into alarm"},
"a-perfect-circle~talktalk": {"haiku":"Empty faith and words without action are useless during crisis. The speaker demands action over prayer and walks away from performative compassion.","opus":"Maynard James Keenan's blistering attack on hollow religiosity, aimed at those who offer sympathy and petitions to heaven while the toll of inaction mounts. The demand is blunt: prove your convictions through deeds or step aside. Belief that never translates into action, he insists, is worthless.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of politicians who offer thoughts and prayers after mass violence while bodies accumulate — faith without action is dead, talk without works is worthless. The song demands they either act or get out of the way.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sneering broadside against consolation that costs nothing, aimed at the reflex of broadcasting sympathy after a catastrophe while refusing the physical labor that would actually change anything. Keenan builds the contempt by turning the invoked savior into a verb his targets will not conjugate, demanding they move the way they preach instead of merely narrating the pose. Then the imperatives pile up — brave the weather, lift the stone, put out a hand — each one insisting on motion, until the patience snaps into an obscene order to step aside. That escalation is the whole argument: comfort that never stirs a muscle rots, so either act it out or clear the road for someone who will.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance","politics & society"],"means":"demanding motion from those who broadcast sympathy without moving a muscle","built":"imperatives pile until patience snaps into an obscene order to step aside"},
"a-perfect-circle~the-contrarian": {"haiku":"A manipulative deceiver hides malice beneath charm and calculated warmth; their core is corrupted. The song warns against trusting surface-level likability in someone fundamentally hollow.","opus":"A character study of a charming deceiver whose apparent kindness is as coldly engineered as a drug, a hollow, discordant figure who champions nothing and despises everyone. The song functions as a warning: distrust the inviting grin of a man whose charm is designed to ruin you.","sonnet":"A portrait of a cynically charming manipulator — a person with a calculated warmth as addictive as heroin and a core of pure contempt — who masquerades as a reasonable voice while serving nobody but himself.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of a manipulator, built entirely from the outside: the song never enters his head, only catalogs the surfaces he presents and warns you off each one. Maynard James Keenan leans on the sonic paradox of his craft — the smile you should distrust is described as warm and pleasant even as it is called lethal, the seduction rated as a narcotic that is engineered rather than felt. The tell is the missing interior: where the song grants everyone else a conscience, an inner scale that weighs and answers, this figure has only a black core and voids where those parts should be, so the greeting itself becomes the lie the instant it is spoken.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","surveillance & control"],"means":"warning off a manipulator by cataloging surfaces he presents","built":"the missing interior — only a black core where conscience should be — makes the greeting the lie"},
"a-perfect-circle~the-doomed": {"haiku":"A new order inverts beatitudes, blessing greed and vice while condemning mercy. The righteous and good are all doomed in this corrupted world.","opus":"An inverted Beatitudes where a new false Christ blesses the greedy, gluttonous, and wicked while dooming the meek, merciful, and pure of heart. It is a savage satire of a value-inverted modern world in which the word is now death, mercy is punished, and everyone is on their own.","sonnet":"An inverted Beatitudes — the blessed in this world are the greedy, the envious, the violent, while the meek, merciful, and peaceful are simply doomed. It's a scathing portrait of a society that has abandoned its stated moral values entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"Maynard James Keenan rewrites the Sermon on the Mount as its own inversion: the Beatitudes flipped so that grace falls on the greedy, the wrathful, the gluttonous — the seven deadly sins enthroned where the blessings used to be — while the meek and the merciful are simply written off. The genius is that the song keeps the liturgical cadence intact, so the horror arrives not as rant but as scripture read straight-faced from a pulpit that has quietly changed sides. What began as a benediction over the fornicates and the rich curdles across three verses into the outro's naked verdict, the promise of comfort replaced by a shrug that leaves everyone on their own. It reads less as atheism than as a portrait of a moral order that has kept the church's grammar and gutted its ethics, blessing whoever already holds power and dooming everyone Christ actually named.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","politics & society"],"means":"keeping liturgical cadence intact while quietly enthroning the seven deadly sins","built":"Beatitudes inverted so the sermon blesses whoever already holds power"},
"a-perfect-circle~the-hollow": {"haiku":"Sexual desire and excess as a futile attempt to fill an internal void, driven by appetite that can only be temporarily pacified rather than permanently satisfied.","opus":"An examination of insatiable desire and appetite, where a hollow emptiness screams to be fed and temporarily pacified, driving cycles of consuming and indiscretion that never truly fill it.","sonnet":"The song describes a person driven entirely by unchecked sexual desire — an insatiable hunger that consumes and cycles through indiscretion — until a call to bridle that drive and fill the emptiness with something lasting.","src":"haiku","fable":"Appetite is the whole engine here: craving speaks in commands while the self shrinks to the tool that obeys, chasing a fix that evaporates the second it lands. Maynard James Keenan runs desire like a stalking predator and strips away any inner brake, leaving one drive — to close a void that is defined precisely by its refusal to stay closed. The chorus is the tell, since every feeding only pacifies for a moment, so the demand returns louder and the reckless dominoes topple in cycles that devour more the harder they are fed. The bridge reaches for discipline, wishing to leash the hunger just long enough to build something lasting and seal the emptiness for good, and the track answers by dumping you back into the loop, where lasting quietly falls away and only the fleeting fix survives.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"feeding a void defined by its refusal to stay closed","built":"the bridge's wish for discipline dumped back into the same loop"},
"a-perfect-circle~the-hollow-acoustic":{"opus":"Desire personified as an insatiable hollow that must be fed, running through indiscretions in endless cycles, then the resolve to bridle it long enough to fill the emptiness for good.","sonnet":"About a person driven entirely by sexual desire who cannot be satisfied — libido described as a blade running through the heart, dominoes of indiscretions falling in endless cycles, temporarily filling a hollow that is never actually full. The song's argument is that this compulsion needs to be bridled long enough to experience genuine connection rather than serial conquest.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-perfect-circle~the-noose": {"haiku":"Someone questions a person who claims redemption and spiritual transformation, suspecting hypocrisy and demanding accountability for past wrongs.","opus":"A pointed address to someone reborn and forgiven, cleansed of their demons, whom the speaker is unwilling to let off so easily. With bitter curiosity he asks how they plan to make amends to the dead they harmed, tugging their halo down until it becomes a noose that chokes them.","sonnet":"A scathing confrontation with someone who has found religion and declared themselves reborn, while the speaker bitterly wants to know how they plan to make amends to the people they actually destroyed — the halo is slipping toward a noose.","src":"haiku","fable":"Maynard James Keenan addresses someone freshly clean and reborn, and the song is a study in how congratulation curdles into indictment. The opening well-wishes are sincere on their surface, but each verse tightens: the recovering addict now recites their own past cruelties as though they belonged to a stranger, and the singer, all wounded politeness, keeps circling the one question amnesty can't answer — how do you apologize to the people your using already killed? Fitting Thirteenth Step's arc through the twelve steps of recovery, this is the ninth (making amends) turned inside out, viewed by a survivor rather than the penitent. The central image does the argument's work: the sobriety halo the newly clean wear so proudly is also the loop of a noose, salvation and self-strangulation the same circle of rope, and the long slide of the outro lets it slip from a saint's crown down to the throat until grace itself is what chokes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","faith & the occult"],"means":"indicting the newly clean for damage their sobriety cannot undo","built":"the halo and the noose sharing the same circle of rope"},
"a-perfect-circle~the-nurse-who-loved-me": {"haiku":"A dependent man seduces a nurse for her medication and care, exploiting her emotional attachment.","opus":"A tender, unsettling song about a man in a clinical or psychiatric setting fixated on a caretaker who holds the medication and the keys, certain she has fallen for him though she treats every patient identically. Surreal touches, a head that seems to shrink, possessions abandoned, suggest a mind coming apart and clinging to a soothing fantasy of being wanted.","sonnet":"About a drug dependency framed as an infatuation with a nurse who has pharmacy access — the narrator is sedated and hallucinating, face-down on a carpet, and the 'romance' is entirely a delusion born of the pills she keeps bringing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A lush, almost lounge-tender croon about the seduction of medication, sung from the fog of the addict who has convinced himself the pull is love. The nurse in white is desire dressed as care: what he actually wants is her access to the pharmacy, and the flattery that she is falling for him is the self-deception addiction runs on, since the last line concedes she treats every other guy exactly the same way. Around that swoon Maynard James Keenan sets the verses on the floor, face down in the carpet weave, addressing the wreckage in the disembodied second person of someone dissociating from his own body and everything he has left behind. On Thirteenth Step, an album built around the twelve-step program, this is the stage before surrender: the high still narrated as romance, the reckoning audible only in how thin the romance is.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"What you're hearing is a cover: the original is Failure's, from Fantastic Planet (1996), written by Ken Andrews and Greg Edwards. APC fold that space-rock song into the addiction album, letting its woozy drift stand in for the fog of dependence.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"narrating pharmaceutical dependency as mutual seduction","built":"the closing admission that she treats every other guy the same way punctures the romance"},
"a-perfect-circle~the-outsider":{"haiku":"A speaker condemns another for self-destructive behavior and delusion, refusing to witness further self-sabotage and urging them to take their tragedy elsewhere.","opus":"An exasperated plea to a self-destructive, attention-seeking person, urging them to stop their reckless, suicidal behavior, and coldly telling them to do it far away if they go through with it.","sonnet":"The narrator confronts someone who is self-destructing — lying, threatening suicide, courting drama — with a mixture of exhausted frustration and cold ultimatum, telling them to go through with it somewhere far away if they truly mean it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is the tell: on a record that circles addiction from every angle, this is the lone voice pitched outside the wreck, and Maynard James Keenan builds the song to expose how ugly that vantage gets. It opens in the register of care, a plea to understand a loved one's ruin, then curdles into name-calling and exhausted disgust, concern shading into a spectator's contempt that resents watching. Each pass sharpens the charge, the addict caught in one lie after another, first self-deceiving and finally deceiving the speaker outright, while the singer's patience thins into a shrug that everyone dies eventually. The twice-repeated bridge, a flat confirmation that the doubters had it correct, is where the mask drops: the outsider was never trying to help, only waiting for vindication in the fall. That vindication becomes the closing cruelty, the parting order to go do the dying out of sight, naming this stance for what it is, a refusal of love disguised as clarity.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"care curdling into contempt while waiting for the fall","built":"the bridge twice repeated to drop the mask of concern"},
"a-perfect-circle~the-outsider-acoustic":{"haiku":"Self-destruction masquerades as freedom while the speaker performs recklessness, rejecting both judgment and the possibility of genuine connection.","opus":"Exhausted contempt aimed at a self-destructive, attention-hungry addict, pleading with them to quit battering their own breaking point and, if they're determined to end it, to do it somewhere out of reach.","sonnet":"Spoken from the perspective of someone watching a person they care about glamorize self-destruction and suicidal behavior as a dramatic performance — what the narrator sees as narcissistic attention-seeking rather than genuine pain. The narrator is simultaneously disgusted, exhausted, and coldly practical: if the suicidal gestures are sincere, they should be carried out somewhere far away rather than as a spectacle for an audience.","src":"haiku"},
"a-perfect-circle~the-package": {"haiku":"A manipulator uses cleverness and trickery to get what they want from others, lying and smiling their way to satisfaction before disappearing.","opus":"A cold portrait of predatory manipulation, the narrator faking friendship and connection purely to extract what he wants before vanishing; the flat repetition of taking what's his exposes an emptiness that no amount of feeding satisfies.","sonnet":"About a predatory manipulator who moves through social situations lying, performing attentiveness, and doing whatever it takes to extract what they want before vanishing — it's a cold self-portrait of someone who treats every relationship as a transaction and takes pride in the efficiency of their deceit.","src":"haiku","fable":"A predator's monologue, sung in the smooth first person of someone who has learned exactly how to pass as human. Maynard James Keenan builds the song around the con man's grammar of transaction — clever and tricky as tools, the friendly smile deployed only long enough to disarm — while the real hunger stays offstage, named just once as a monster that has to be fed. On Thirteenth Step, an album steeped in the language of addiction and recovery, the package is the mask the user wears to get the fix: the promise to listen, the nodded agreement, the pretense of connection that is only ever a means to the score. As it repeats, the song tightens from the sly opening verses into a bald refrain of lying and, finally, into pure appetite — the closing collapse into a single possessive word, mine, that has burned away every last pretense of the smile.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance","violence & murder"],"means":"wearing the mask of connection only long enough to reach the score","built":"the song tightens from sly verse to bald refrain to the single possessive word mine"},
"a-perfect-circle~thinking-of-you": {"haiku":"Obsessive sexual preoccupation dominates the mind, mixing desire with something darker and inescapable in repeated reverie.","opus":"Keenan makes solitary erotic fixation explicit, an obsessive replaying of another person's surrender that can't be shaken. It's about desire crossing into compulsion, the mind snared on an image of someone spent and undone.","sonnet":"Unable to stop imagining someone in a state of sexual surrender, the narrator cycles obsessively through the visual and physical details of that fantasy — it is pure erotic fixation dressed in the language of revelation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A voyeur's sleepless replay of a lover's climax, mind pinned to one remembered image while a beat knocks behind his eyes and every gasp gets traced back into rhythm. He recognizes the wet cry before it arrives, watches the face go slack and glistening at the peak, and reads that collapse as a surrender he keeps summoning back. Maynard James Keenan lets the title chant swallow coherence at the end, so the beloved dissolves and only the hungry, looping act of remembering is left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"looping one remembered image until the beloved dissolves entirely","built":"the title chant swallowing coherence as memory consumes the person"},
"a-perfect-circle~thomas": {"haiku":"Someone seeks forgiveness and spiritual guidance, praying to reconnect and find a path through learning to let go and be released.","opus":"A prayer for the grace to forgive and let go, named for doubting Thomas, the narrator kneeling to relearn faith enough to reconnect. Keenan's spiritual searching at its most humble and unguarded.","sonnet":"A prayer for the capacity to forgive — the narrator is not there yet, but asks to be shown the way, to be granted release from whatever is keeping them bound, cycling through humble petition for illumination and reconnection.","src":"haiku","fable":"A prayer addressed to a figure who has wronged the speaker, but the petition is bent inward: the thing being begged for is not the wronged party's repentance but the speaker's own capacity to forgive and release. Across the chorus's near-identical repetitions the syntax quietly shifts — being shown the way to forgive slides into being allowed to be forgiven — so the accuser and the guilty party start to merge, and the verse's posture of humility reads less as devotion than as someone stuck asking permission to feel differently. Maynard James Keenan, whose lapsed-Catholic reflex runs through Mer de Noms, uses the liturgical shape against itself here: the repetition is the point, a loop of supplication that never resolves because the speaker keeps praying to be told where to even begin. The outro reduces the whole plea to the bare wish to reconnect, which lands as the confession underneath all the ritual language — this was never about sin but about a severed bond the speaker cannot repair alone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","shame & repression","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"praying for permission to forgive rather than for repentance","built":"the syntax shift from being shown forgiveness to being allowed it merges accuser and guilty party"},
"a-perfect-circle~vanishing": {"haiku":"The gradual dissolution of presence and existence, imagined as a thought brushing past and floating away into non-being and absence.","opus":"A hushed dissolve into nothingness, someone thinning into air as if they were never really there. It reads as death or dissociation rendered weightless, the ache of a presence floating away past holding.","sonnet":"Pure dissolution — the self becoming thinner and less present, floating away like a thought brushing a sigh, until it was never really there at all; an experience of depersonalization or fading out of existence.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is dependence recast as the wish to grow thin and dissolve, the singer craving his own erasure so fully that presence itself starts to feel borrowed rather than owned. Maynard James Keenan renders the addict's logic from inside, where getting lighter reads as mercy and the body becomes ballast to shed by degrees. The lyric enacts that shrinking through its own machinery, one drifting phrase looped past sense until the words wear down into the very fade they name.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"craving erasure so fully that presence feels borrowed rather than owned","built":"one drifting phrase looped past sense until the words enact the fade they name"},
"a-perfect-circle~weak-and-powerless":{"haiku":"Complete helplessness and desperation in the face of another's control; willing to make any compromise just to avoid the crushing feeling of surrender.","opus":"Addiction and helpless obsession, told through images of digging one's own grave and chasing a fix; the speaker feels desperate, ravenous, and powerless over another person, letting the devil promise him solace so he won't feel.","sonnet":"The song depicts obsessive, addictive desire for someone — the speaker digs ever deeper toward numbness, dismissing both angelic and devilish counsel, all in search of relief from the desperate craving that one person over whom they feel completely powerless.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being enslaved by a habit that masquerades as a love song, which is exactly the point on Thirteenth Step, the A Perfect Circle album Maynard James Keenan built around addiction and recovery. The verses render using as digging: each fix is a shovelful into a grave the speaker excavates himself, chasing an ever-deeper high toward a corpse-pale bottom, the euphoria described in the same breath as self-burial. The chorus then addresses the drug as a lover who owns the speaker outright, collapsing the language of romantic abjection into the language of dependence so completely that the you could be a person and it makes no difference. The bridge is where the read lives: the nursery rhyme sing-song of shooing away a small hopeful voice and instead lending his ear to a devil who promises peace turns the whole song into a portrait of relapse rationalizing itself, wanting not pleasure but only the silencing of that famished, clawing need. The bare final repetition of over you leaves the surrender total and unresolved.","fv":2,"fnote":"The album it anchors is titled for the relapse the song enacts — twelve tracks for the twelve steps, and the thirteenth step is falling back to the first.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","faith & the occult"],"means":"relapse rationalizing itself by silencing the small hopeful voice","built":"the bridge turns on a nursery-rhyme cadence lending an ear to the devil instead"},
"a-perfect-circle~what-s-going-on": {"haiku":"The song laments senseless violence, war, and brutality, pleading for love to replace hatred and for understanding to end persecution.","opus":"A cover of the Marvin Gaye classic, a sorrowful appeal against war, police brutality, and the older generation's contempt for the young. Amid dying siblings and grieving mothers, it argues that compassion, not hatred, is the only real solution, and pleads for conversation over force against demonstrators.","sonnet":"A cover of Marvin Gaye's song: a plea for the end of war, police brutality, and generational division, with love proposed as the only real answer to the violence tearing families and society apart.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cover, where the meaning lives in the choosing: APC take Marvin Gaye's 1971 plea and drain the warmth out of it, so the communal groove that once softened the accusation is gone and only the accusation is left. The lyric moves outward through a family — mother, brother, father — from grief to a plea against escalation to a demand simply to be talked to rather than beaten, and Keenan reads it flat and cold, refusing the consolation the original offered. Landing this Vietnam-era language on eMOTIVe, an anti-war covers album released in a 2004 America at war again, is the whole point: the curation turns a period piece into a present-tense charge, and the title question stops being an appeal and becomes an indictment nobody gets to answer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","family & blood"],"means":"warmth stripped until the plea becomes an indictment","built":"the groove's removal leaves the accusation standing alone"},
"a-perfect-circle~when-the-levee-breaks": {"haiku":"A flood catastrophe destroys everything; the levee's failure leaves survivors stranded, weeping, and abandoned with nothing but prayer and desperation.","opus":"Reworking the old blues of the 1927 Mississippi flood, Maynard James Keenan strips it to dread and helplessness. The rising water is disaster you can only watch arrive, where prayer and grief buy you nothing and displacement is certain.","sonnet":"A cover of the Delta blues standard about flood, displacement, and grief — the levee is both literal and metaphorical, and when it breaks there is no shelter left, only mourning on the embankment and the knowledge that crying and praying won't hold back the water.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Perfect Circle drag an old flood blues into their era of protest, so the swelling water reads as slow institutional catastrophe rather than one bad storm. The dread accumulates through sheer repetition, a warning circling itself until refusal hardens into prophecy and shelter is simply gone. Comfort gets named only to be crossed out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","nature & the elements"],"means":"a warning circled until refusal hardens into prophecy","built":"flood blues dragged into a protest era so rising water reads as institutional catastrophe"},
"a-place-to-bury-strangers~always-gonna-be-the-same":{"haiku":"Capitalist systems lie and manipulate citizens into consuming useless goods while funding elites; without active political resistance, systemic corruption perpetuates unchanged.","opus":"Noise-rock protest wrapped in deadpan absurdism, raging at corporate control, manufactured need, and buried scandals while insisting that apathy guarantees the rot continues. Refusing to vote is the only real failure it names.","sonnet":"A plainspoken political rant directed at the greedy operators who strip regulations, bury allegations, and fund expensive rugs on public donations — the speaker just wants to live without their useless shit and their drugs — and the conclusion is a democratic ultimatum: if we don't vote, it's always gonna be the same.","src":"haiku"},
"a-place-to-bury-strangers~my-head-is-bleeding-lunacy-remix":{"haiku":"Someone's head bleeds and tears form rivers they cannot contain, unable to focus or find help. Eyes closed, they thank God and ask for the pain to end with no answers coming.","opus":"Fractured into gasped fragments of closed eyes, thanks to God, and pleas to end pain, the piece pictures a bleeding head and tears forming uncontainable rivers behind the wheel of a car. A Place to Bury Strangers, famed for punishing noise, let this remix dissolve a possible crash into pure sensory panic, terror stripped to reflex where no answers or help ever arrive.","sonnet":"Someone drives in crisis — head literally or psychically bleeding, tears uncontrollable — cycling through prayers for help that arrive without answers. The road keeps going even as the capacity to focus on it drains away.","src":"haiku"},
"a-prkflp~smack-my-bitch-up":{"opus":"Reworking a notorious big-beat provocation, the piece is pure chant, the violent phrase repeated and pitch-shifted with no narrative to develop. Its whole method is transgression by loop: meaning drained until only the shock of the sound remains. Where the original leaned on club-floor menace, this version doubles down on the emptiness, making the refrain a mechanical taunt rather than a story, which is exactly the discomfort it courts.","sonnet":"The lyrics consist entirely of the repeated phrase about hitting a woman and changing pitch — there is no narrative or developed theme beyond the chant itself.","src":"sonnet"},
"a-r2p9ve~a-mgu5je":{"haiku":"Resignation to losing and refusing the encore of false hope; a loser embraces their defeat rather than pretend to triumphant recovery or redemption.","opus":"In the Japanese loser-anthem tradition, the narrator howls across the gap between a frozen longing and the hollow cheer of adult optimism, still waiting at a summer bus stop for one specific person. He names himself a loser-dog, spurning both music's consolations and world-peace platitudes, and stakes everything on the one who matters. The urgency comes from a single admission: no encore is coming, so this bet is the only one.","sonnet":"A narrator howling at the gap between his frozen romantic longing — perpetually waiting at a summer bus stop for one specific person — and the hollow performance of adult optimism, declaring himself a loser-dog who rejects music's consolations and world-peace platitudes alike, betting everything on the one person who matters because no encore is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"a-rgwq0p~a-1vsq912":{"haiku":"An anthem of forward movement and trust—spreading wings toward an unseen future, believing in beauty despite moral ambiguity.","opus":"This is anthemic J-rock optimism of the hard-won kind, where hope is reached only after doubt is fully metabolized. Seeds planted the night before have already sprouted, and even knowing justice can flip into injustice depending on who wields it, the narrator chooses to trust himself and keep walking toward an unseen world he believes is beautiful. Faith here is a decision made with eyes open, not a mood.","sonnet":"A forward-looking declaration that the future is reachable even when progress stalls — seeds planted last night have already sprouted, and even knowing that justice can flip into injustice depending on who holds it, the narrator chooses to trust himself and keep walking toward an unseen world he believes is beautiful. The emotional core is hard-won optimism that has processed doubt rather than ignored it.","src":"haiku"},
"a-rh7aca~a-124f3w9":{"haiku":"Discovery and friendship as mutual enlightenment; a child's wonder finds magic in everyday moments and learns that connection requires vulnerability and asking for help.","opus":"Tender and small-scaled in the manner of Japanese slice-of-life pop, it follows two friends discovering the world: reaching for fruit just out of grasp, chasing birds to feel how flight works, watching shadows multiply on the walk home. The quiet revelation is that a friend's timely encouragement is what turned ordinary things into wonders the narrator could claim as her own. Connection, the song suggests, is what makes discovery possible at all.","sonnet":"Two friends discover the world together — reaching for fruit neither can quite grasp, chasing birds to understand how flight feels, watching their shadows multiply on the walk home — and the narrator realizes it was a friend's timely encouragement that turned ordinary things into wonders she could claim as her own discovery.","src":"haiku"},
"a-scent-like-wolves~eastern-lights":{"haiku":"The speaker is trapped in an eternal dream haunted by youth, lost love, and the cost of their choices, unable to escape despite burning their wings.","opus":"A remorseful reckoning with becoming a deceiver, the narrator grieving a broken friendship and a lost first love while insisting he means no one harm, even as he'd sacrifice everything to hang on. A Scent Like Wolves work in melodic metalcore, threading Lucifer-as-light-bearer imagery through a wrenching apology for what he turned into.","sonnet":"Guilt and devotion tangled together — someone who burned their wings to stay close, lost their closest friend in the process, and can't stop holding pictures of the person they failed. The wolf-in-sheep's-clothing admission sits alongside a sincere wish to not be the enemy. If time could be reversed they would; it can't, so they keep returning to the same sleepless vigil.","src":"haiku"},
"a-sense-of-purpose~ruin":{"haiku":"Desolation breeds obsessive attachment; the speaker nests in wreckage, unable to abandon the very wounds that define identity and justify suffering.","opus":"Grief and hurt claimed as something the narrator owns and can't put down, a nest turned into a hive by someone who left disarray behind. He knows he clings to what wounds him and calls that hell home while still begging them to stay.","sonnet":"Grief and abandonment have turned a nest into a hive — the speaker grew too close to what hurt them most, now fends alone in the ruin left behind, and pleads for someone to stay and share what remains before something pulls them below. The image of wounded wings and broken dreams anchors the exhaustion.","src":"haiku"},
"a-void~newspapers":{"haiku":"Entanglement with another's trauma makes autonomy impossible; the speaker remains printed with another's suffering, unable to establish separate existence.","opus":"Infatuation with someone worshipped like a god yet printed all over the singer like newsprint, warning that once freed of that idealized weight the beloved will simply drown.","sonnet":"About obsessive attachment to someone emotionally volatile and self-destructive — the narrator is saturated with this person, printed all over with them like newsprint, both drawn to join their torment and aware that once liberated from their weight this person will simply drown. The relationship oscillates between seduction and being torn apart.","src":"haiku"},
"a-ylk3wt~super-silver-haze":{"haiku":"Clouds part to reveal infinite space; the speaker floats through time toward calling stars. Weightlessness consumes identity as consciousness dissolves into cosmos.","opus":"Named for a strain of cannabis, the track drifts in psychedelic pop haze as the speaker floats through space and time toward stars that call out, feeling alive even as a relationship ends. The sky crashes and ignites into infinity, and a closing image of fire over bones and light guiding home holds the drift in place. Dissolution and return arrive as one motion, the comedown indistinguishable from coming back.","sonnet":"In an altered, hazy state the speaker floats through space and time toward stars that call out, feeling alive even as a relationship seems to end — the sky crashes and lights up in infinity — and a final image of fire covering bones and light guiding home frames the drift as both dissolution and return.","src":"haiku"},
"a-zlzui6~a-b6hhhr":{"haiku":"A brutal social Darwinist manifesto—humans are failed animals and only the alpha predator survives; weakness deserves subjugation.","opus":"This is aggressive Russian hard rock as social-Darwinist manifesto, the narrator crowning himself alpha dog in a tribe whose only law is dominate or vanish. He scorns a world of human trash, fake leaders, and herd-minded parasites, casting himself as both alpha and omega, ready to maul any pretender to godhood. Existence becomes a dog-eat-dog contest where the sole alternative to winning is being torn apart, and weakness is treated as guilt.","sonnet":"A Russian-language survival-of-the-fittest manifesto: the narrator declares himself the alpha dog in a tribe where the only law is dominance or oblivion, scorning a world of human trash, fake leaders, and herd-minded parasites. He positions himself as both alpha and omega, willing to maul anyone who pretends to godhood, and frames existence as a dog-eat-dog contest where the alternative to winning is being torn apart.","src":"haiku"},
"aaron-f-bianchi-jupiter~smash-the-corps":{"haiku":"Corporate tyranny sells the soul itself: consumption and control interlock until resistance becomes the only remaining human act.","opus":"A dystopian rallying cry against corporations that purchase people's souls and dominate a neon metropolis, pressing the listener to pick a side. It urges the crowd to tear down consumerist, authoritarian control before everything is gone.","sonnet":"A song about corporate and systemic control over human life — wages, consumption, corruption, and gang violence all serve the same dollar-supreme machine, and the speaker calls for open rebellion against corporations and fascist authority.","src":"haiku"},
"abba~dancing-queen":{"haiku":"Youth discovers freedom and transcendence through music and dance; innocence radiates as girls become queens in the ritual of night.","opus":"A glittering hymn to seventeen and untouchable, the dance floor as a brief kingdom where a girl is pure possibility. Beneath the euphoria ABBA leave a faint ache, because this reign is by definition fleeting.","sonnet":"A celebration of a seventeen-year-old girl's power and freedom on the dance floor, young and untouchable for one Friday night, where she rules the room and chooses her own pleasure without belonging to anyone.","src":"haiku"},
"abbie-falls~pitch-black":{"haiku":"Trauma accumulates into perceptual darkness; the speaker sees only black pitch as memory and current suffering merge into undifferentiated void.","opus":"A descent into despair and self-loathing where every backward glance shows only darkness and the speaker sold his soul twice over. The freak show framing turns private breakdown into a spectacle, pills and overdose the price of secrets that grow as we age, with someone else buried beneath his own lies.","sonnet":"Looking back into pitch black — suppressed lies, cold-blood mode, a noose around the hands — the speaker catalogues a freak show of overdoses, buried secrets, and a soul sold twice, unable to recover what was lost but refusing to unsee what they have witnessed.","src":"haiku"},
"above-below~labyrinth":{"haiku":"Nature's complexity offers wisdom when ego surrenders; ascending toward enlightenment requires embracing mystery rather than forcing understanding.","opus":"A psychedelic ascent framed as climbing the tree of life toward the sky, guided by intuition and Sanskrit mantra to break through the canopy. The visionary experience dissolves fear and hatred into a meeting with a radiant, astral pure being.","sonnet":"A spiritual ascent through a forest labyrinth: climbing through branches, holding hands with energy, the elm's heart beating synchronously with the climber's own, until breaching the canopy reveals the earth below in full — \"no fear, no hate, no woe,\" the infant mind materialised in an encounter with the astral glow.","src":"haiku"},
"above-below~scorpion":{"haiku":"Consciousness torments through overthinking; the speaker sinks into dream-logic seeking reprieve, yet thought itself becomes scorpion stinging the mind.","opus":"A Macbeth-borrowed image of a mind full of scorpions, chasing sleep and dreams as the only reprieve from tormenting thought. The longing is simply to let go and float free for a second, aware every soaring thought is dragged back to earth.","sonnet":"An insomniac mind full of scorpions — deceived by mirages its own thinking creates, tasting the foulness of thought on the thinker's tongue, the speaker seeks sleep and the god of dreams Hypnos to erode the blue inside. The desert of dreaming offers an oasis of freedom, but every flying thought feels the pull of earth and falls.","src":"haiku"},
"above-the-fallen~room-19":{"haiku":"Spirals into obsession with death and voices while trapped in cyclical mental anguish, unable to wake. Questions identity while haunted by past and present.","opus":"A harrowing depiction of psychosis and sleeplessness, the narrator bonded to the voices in his head, soaked in violence, throwing himself against the walls as his mind fractures and he begs not to be reminded who he is. Above the Fallen trade in brutal, deathcore-adjacent metalcore, staging a total mental collapse where the only refuge left is burying the pain.","sonnet":"A psyche trapped in violent repetition — the voices that won't quiet, the blood-stained walls, the desperate wish to wake from something that keeps starting over. Identity is under attack from inside and out: other people try to name this person, and the refusal is loud and raw. Underneath the fury is a plea not to be left, even as everything demands being left alone.","src":"haiku"},
"above-the-stars~a-1i49aph":{"haiku":"Angelic salvation and forgiveness—the narrator addresses their angel and seeks forgiveness, yearning for redemption through the angel's grace and protective strength.","opus":"In the Russian post-hardcore idiom where remorse is screamed rather than whispered, the narrator confesses to burning his partner's wings, destroying something angelic in her, and can only beg forgiveness. She has gone cold, silently accepting the death of her dream, while his heart weakens under the steady rain of her tears. The imbalance is the point: he pleads, she withdraws, and the apology arrives too late to reach her.","sonnet":"A Russian song of apology from someone who burned their partner's wings — destroyed something angelic in them — and can only beg forgiveness: the person they hurt has gone cold and silently accepted the death of her dream, while the narrator's heart grows steadily weaker under the rain of her tears.","src":"haiku"},
"abson~incepcja":{"haiku":"Drug-fueled introspection and distorted reality described through nested dream logic, with the speaker trapped in addiction, betrayal, and cynical disillusionment about friendship and success.","opus":"A druggy Polish braggadocio built around Inception, the rapper crumbling and smoking dope, sleeping beside a model, living in dreams while time crawls as if on drugs. Beneath the flexing is disillusionment, old friendships rotting into betrayal over money, and contempt for a world that gave him a harsh lesson but also his voice through rap.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper describes a life so good it feels like a dream-within-a-dream — waking at dawn after sleeping beside a model, wearing expensive clothes, living at a pace that feels like an MDMA comedown — contrasted against former friends who turned disloyal; the whole thing framed as an inception of success he can barely believe.","src":"haiku"},
"abson~tesla-sport-carbon":{"haiku":"High-speed intoxication and escape fantasies, wealth spent on automobiles as status and sensation. The woman is an object to seduce during acceleration, while the narrator boasts of superior machines and dismisses others as poor and buying cheap.","opus":"A Polish trap flex built entirely around an electric supercar, mocking petrolheads and cracking that even Elon would send him to Mars. Braggadocio as pure play, the eco-flex flipped into the same old wealth-and-women posturing.","sonnet":"A Polish rap flexing the erotic pull of a Tesla Sport Carbon — the car arouses his passenger, launches him at 200 km/h while the battery runs low and she finds time for other pleasures at the charging station, culminating in an absurd fantasy phone call from Elon Musk recruiting him for SpaceX.","src":"haiku"},
"aburden~farewell":{"haiku":"A person ends a relationship because they fail to meet their partner's needs, exhausted by cycles of expectation and inadequacy.","opus":"A bitter argument with someone he keeps letting down, the narrator exhausted by never measuring up while his life loops in dizzying circles. He owns his substance dependence as self-destruction, then lashes out by exposing the other's vulnerability. Aburden work in modern metalcore, pairing the spinning refrain with a demand to be seen as a flawed person rather than a pedestal to be knocked over.","sonnet":"Exhaustion at never being enough circling back on itself — the same failure, the same loop, the same sick feeling that the relationship has been running on fumes and excuses. There is a flash of anger (a weak spot finally found, a finger pointed back at the other person) but the dominant note is a tired, preemptive goodbye that nobody wanted to have to say.","src":"haiku"},
"aburden~need-you":{"opus":"A nostalgic ache for a romance years gone, the singer craving a single revived evening with an old flame despite the deceptions that ended it. Tangled up with a dread of changing and a struggle to accept who he is, he keeps landing on one certainty: that person is what he wants back.","sonnet":"Three years after a relationship ended, the narrator is still undone by the memory of a person with bright blue eyes, confessing they cannot cope with the change of losing them and desperately wishing for one more night together despite all the lies.","src":"sonnet"},
"ac-dc~back-in-black":{"haiku":"Return from imprisonment or death brings renewed rebellion and dominance; the speaker reclaims power and refuses victimhood, celebrating nine lives and predatory freedom against all restraint.","opus":"A cocky anthem of return, written in the aftermath of frontman Bon Scott's death, cast as a man freed from what nearly finished him and now convinced he is unkillable. Beneath the swagger sits a defiant survival story, the surviving band roaring back to life to celebrate being alive at all.","sonnet":"A triumphant return from the brink — freed from whatever was keeping him down, the narrator comes back harder, faster, and more dangerous than before.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~ballbreaker":{"haiku":"A speaker pursues aggressive sexual encounter with a dominant partner whose physical intensity overwhelms and subdues them during intimate exchange.","sonnet":"A sexually dominant woman overwhelms and overpowers the narrator at every turn. The encounter is framed as both assault and pleasure — she controls the pace, the aggression, and the outcome. He is a willing subject to someone who takes exactly what she wants.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~boogie-man":{"haiku":"A predatory entity stalks women at night, hiding under beds and in cars, threatening vulnerable girls.","opus":"The Australian rock institution dresses a nocturnal predator in fine suits and cigars, a Cadillac-driving figure haunting hotel bars and lurking under the bed. The boogeyman conceit fuses childhood bogey with grown seduction, warning a girl to beware when she is alone at night. Classic AC/DC double-entendre and swagger drive the menace, the creature pleading only that the sun never come up to expose him.","sonnet":"A nocturnal figure of seduction and mild menace — suited, cigar-smoking, operating in hotel bars and fast cars — leans into his own mythic scariness with obvious relish, positioning himself as the thing that lurks under the bed of anyone alone at night.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~burnin-alive":{"haiku":"Violent upheaval and destruction consume a society or town; metaphors of fire and war convey apocalyptic chaos and total ruin.","opus":"A town primed to combust anchors this snarling threat, invoking a little place called Hope and a leader whose smoke may someday rise, all set to imagery of all-out war and souls on fire. AC/DC deliver it with their unkillable riff machinery, the mid-nineties lineup channeling menace into the same blunt, boogie-driven attack that made them stadium fixtures.","sonnet":"A portrait of collective combustion — society caught in an all-out war with nowhere to shelter and no heroes left to call. Fueled by cynicism about political promises and a town called Hope, everything is fated to burn, and the blaze feels inevitable rather than tragic.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~can-i-sit-next-to-you-girl":{"haiku":"A speaker repeatedly pursues a woman they meet at a cinema, using lines and assertiveness to maintain proximity despite interruption and competition.","sonnet":"A simple, persistent pursuit — a young man spots a woman in a cinema queue and devotes the entire encounter to one opening question. His confidence holds through competition, and the repeated request becomes a kind of comic charm in its single-minded focus on getting closer.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~cover-you-in-oil":{"haiku":"A man pursues sexual conquest and control over women, describing attraction and dominance in crude, predatory terms.","opus":"Cut for the Ballbreaker era after AC/DC's stadium reascendance, this is the band in pure carnal-swagger mode, Brian Johnson leering over Angus Young's grind. The lyric follows lust toward an anonymous dancer, all appetite and no romance, boiled down to the title's slick refrain. Nothing beyond the wanting is examined; the appeal is the crude, unbothered directness the band built its later blues-rock on.","sonnet":"A man is consumed by raw physical desire for a woman whose confident, rough magnetism overwhelms him. The transaction is mutual and elemental — heat, friction, appetite — with no pretense of depth, just an urgent pull toward pleasure and the promise of total immersion.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~hail-caesar":{"haiku":"A powerful ruler flatters and manipulates followers while secretly plotting to betray them; senators praise him even as he readies the knife.","opus":"From the band's 1995 Ballbreaker album, this stomping rocker casts a swaggering tyrant in mock-imperial terms. A figure who licks honey and rakes in praise instead of being fed to the lions rules a shaking, seismic coliseum. AC/DC's blues-rock muscle drives a sardonic warning about the fickleness of power, where Caesar's thumb can stab you in the back and you need eyes behind your head to survive.","sonnet":"Power as spectacle, corruption as entertainment. A bloated ruler collects adulation while his senate rehearses justifications and the arena processes victims. The pageant rolls forward on its own momentum, and the warning — eyes in the back of your head — arrives only at the very end, too late to matter.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~hard-as-a-rock":{"haiku":"A woman's seductive energy and wild behavior harden the narrator into stone—sexual arousal becomes a state of absolute hardness and readiness.","opus":"AC/DC being AC/DC: a wall-to-wall double entendre about sex dressed as geology and electricity. There's no subtext to mine, just the band's cheerful, unkillable formula of innuendo and swagger cranked to full.","sonnet":"A straightforward hard rock sexual boast — the woman described is so intensely stimulating that the narrator stays permanently aroused, and the song's entire content is this single innuendo delivered with maximum bluntness.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~have-a-drink-on-me":{"haiku":"Alcohol and intoxication dissolve responsibility and consequence as the speaker celebrates oblivion with companions. Forgetting the bill becomes metaphor for collective denial.","opus":"A raucous invitation to drink oneself blind and forget tomorrow, its carousing shadowed by the fact it memorializes Bon Scott, who died from exactly this.","opusDeep":"Bon Scott-era AC/DC, and grimly loaded in hindsight given Scott's death by drinking — but on its own terms a pure hedonist's manifesto that keeps flirting with the bill. The catalogue of liquor is almost comic in its indiscriminacy: 'whiskey, gin and brandy,' 'sour mash and cheap wine,' quality irrelevant so long as the glass keeps filling. The refrain 'don't worry 'bout tomorrow, take it today / Forget about the cheque, we'll get hell to pay' names the reckoning and waves it off in the same breath — 'hell to pay' both a shrug and a genuine debt. 'My glass is getting shorter' is the sly bit, measuring a night by descending liquid rather than clock. 'Have a drink on me' offers generosity and abdication at once: I'll buy, and I'll bear the cost. What it's actually about: drinking hard against a tomorrow the singer already knows he's mortgaging.","sonnet":"A cheerful, self-aware celebration of drinking to the point of collapse — whiskey, tequila, white lightning, the glass getting shorter — with an open invitation for everyone to join in, forget tomorrow, and get blinded out of their minds tonight.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~hells-bells":{"haiku":"Demonic seduction and hellfire threats from a demonic persona, using bell-ringing as warning and announcement of damnation.","opus":"The thunderous opener of 1980's Back in Black, the first album after singer Bon Scott's death, arrives tolling like a funeral. Brian Johnson's debut casts the narrator as rolling thunder and hurricane, coming to take listeners to hell and spare no lives. Menace and defiance fuse into an unlikely triumph, the tolling bell reframing grief and mortality as the band's swaggering, immortal resurrection.","sonnet":"Death personified as a force of total, elemental violence. It arrives like weather — thunder, lightning, hurricane — claiming the young and the willing alike, aligned with heat rather than cold, and ringing its bell as both announcement and sentence. There is no negotiating with it and no way to fight back.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~high-voltage":{"haiku":"A rock and roll musician defends his uncompromising lifestyle—his appearance, career choices, and hedonic approach—as simply requiring maximum energy to fuel his art.","opus":"An early AC/DC statement from their mid-seventies breakout, answering an outsider's nosy questions about the band's look and habits with one defiant idea: the music is electrical power, and switching it on is all the singer needs. Bon Scott reduces the band's whole philosophy to a jolt of energy and simple pleasures.","sonnet":"A performer answers every prying question about his lifestyle and choices with one defiant answer: he does it because rock and roll runs through him like electricity. The stage is his justification, the crowd his circuit, and the charge he carries needs no further explanation.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~highway-to-hell":{"haiku":"Hedonistic freedom and confident descent into damnation celebrate excess and refusal of moral constraint. The speaker claims the party as payment enough for eternal punishment.","opus":"A defiant embrace of the reckless touring life as a one-way ride to damnation, treating the road, the party, and hell itself as the only thing worth doing.","opusDeep":"Bon Scott-era AC/DC turning damnation into a road-trip boast. The genius is tonal: hell arrives with 'No stop signs, speed limit / Nobody's gonna slow me down' — the underworld reframed as the one place without rules, freedom rather than punishment. 'Season ticket on a one-way ride' compresses the whole joke, a commuter's routine pointed at oblivion, comfortable and irreversible. The mock-piety of 'Hey Satan, payin' my dues / Playin' in a rocking band' recasts the touring musician's grind — dues literally paid — as a deal with the devil, then flips scripture outright: 'I'm on my way to the Promised Land.' The address keeps shifting outward, to Satan, then to 'momma, look at me,' a child showing off on the way down. What it's actually about: the rock-and-roll life embraced as glorious self-damnation, with the ride itself the reward and no interest in the destination.","sonnet":"A joyful, unrepentant declaration of living fast on the road — no rules, no stopping, playing in a rocking band and heading toward a promised land the speaker cheerfully labels hell, inviting everyone along for the ride.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~it-s-a-long-way-to-the-top-if-you-wanna-rock-n-roll":{"haiku":"Rock stardom demands sacrifice and hardship; the speaker catalogs exploitation as proof of commitment.","opus":"A hard-won reality check on the grind of a touring band, all the robbery, exhaustion and underpayment behind the fantasy of stardom. Success is a long, mean road, harder than it looks from the outside.","sonnet":"A blunt account of the grueling realities of life as a touring rock musician — getting robbed, beaten, ripped off, underpaid, and exploited at every stop, the song strips all glamour from the rock dream and replaces it with raw economics and physical punishment.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~little-lover":{"haiku":"The singer notices a young fan in the front row and obsessively pursues her after the show. They imagine her on their motorcycle and on their bedroom wall, unable to shake the fixation.","opus":"A leering rock-star reminiscence: the singer spots a fan in the front row, fixates on her, and boasts about being the guitar picker she pinned to her bedroom wall despite never having a hit. This sits on AC/DC's debut with Bon Scott, and its swaggering, faintly sleazy blues-rock captures the young Australian band mythologizing the groupie encounter with the cheeky innuendo that became their signature.","sonnet":"A musician spots a fan in the front row and becomes fixated — the encounter moves from the stage to a motorcycle ride to the mutual recognition of a long-held obsession. The dynamic is playful and brash: the narrator was nobody with no hits, the fan had his picture on her wall anyway, and somehow that mutual need closes the gap. Lust dressed up as a love story.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~live-wire":{"haiku":"A man presents himself as trouble and sexual satisfaction personified; he burns through towns and women with dangerous, uncontrollable energy.","opus":"An early Bon Scott-era stormer built on pure electric bravado, the narrator branding himself the go-to man for trouble and thrills, all icy cool and dangerous heat, vowing to torch the whole town. Double entendres and high-voltage swagger define AC/DC's blues-rock template, where seduction and menace spark together and self-mythology runs at full current.","sonnet":"Pure sexual bravado delivered as high-voltage self-advertisement. He presents himself as simultaneously dangerous and irresistible — cooler than ice, hotter than anything — and invites whoever is willing to plug in, promising to burn through the night and set the entire town alight before he is done.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~love-bomb":{"haiku":"Sexual desire strikes suddenly and overwhelmingly, described playfully as a romantic bombing raid that cannot be resisted.","opus":"A wall-to-wall sexual double-entendre in which the singer poses as a bomber on a raid, piling on aviation slang and crude innuendo to insist proportions are irrelevant as he unleashes himself relentlessly. This is Brian Johnson-era AC/DC at their most cartoonish, a single filthy joke stretched into a riff-driven, tongue-in-cheek strut.","sonnet":"Pure innuendo delivered at full throttle — desire described entirely through military aviation metaphors, all payload and payload-drop and coming in strong through the door. There is no emotional arc, no complication; the song exists to revel in its own blunt energy, stacking images of irresistible force until the joke becomes the point.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~rock-and-roll-ain-t-noise-pollution":{"haiku":"Rock music as a legitimate art form and life force, not mere noise. The song defends guitar-driven sound and raw energy as vital and permanent.","opus":"The closing anthem of the band's 1980 comeback record, the first with a new frontman after their singer's death. It answers critics who dismiss the genre as mere racket, arguing the music is logical, permanent, and worth turning up loud. A swaggering statement of faith that treats the style as something that outlives everyone.","sonnet":"A defence of rock and roll as simple, sensible, and indestructible — not noise but necessity. The call goes out to fence-sitters to commit, with the bedroom scene serving as proof that when given the choice, people always choose the music.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~rock-n-roll-singer":{"haiku":"A young person rejects conventional success as a doctor or lawyer to pursue rock stardom, embracing rebellion against societal expectations and moral standards.","opus":"Bon Scott's working-class breakout fantasy, a kid rejecting the nine-to-five and his parents' respectable dreams for the devil-driven pull of stardom, spitting on moral rules and school as a rigged, dirty lie.","sonnet":"A young man rejects his parents' nine-to-five expectations and the whole respectable-career script to pursue rock stardom — buys a cheap guitar, grows his hair, and tells everyone who doesn't like it to get lost.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~shake-a-leg":{"haiku":"Youth rebellion and delinquency, living outside laws and expectations. The idle juvenile kicks against authority and chases transgression as a badge of identity.","opus":"AC/DC channel adolescent delinquency into a hard-charging anthem of refusing correction. The narrator brawls in class, sneers at the adults who call him a disgrace, and demands nothing but motion and appetite. It sits squarely in the Bon Scott era, where the band mythologised working-class rebellion and lust as one continuous rush of raw, unrepentant energy.","sonnet":"A delinquent youth prowls the wrong side of the law, rejecting every authority that tries to restrain him. He owns his appetite for trouble, crude pleasures, and raw energy, answering every demand for conformity with the same defiant command to just get moving.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~she-s-got-balls":{"haiku":"A woman possesses strength, confidence, and sexual agency that makes her irresistible and defines her power beyond physical appearance.","opus":"One of AC/DC's earliest tracks, this Bon Scott-era rocker heaps blunt praise on a woman for her nerve, style and appetite rather than her looks. The title flips a crude idiom into a compliment on her boldness. Scott reportedly wrote it about his own wife, and the swagger typical of the band's Australian pub-rock origins carries the leering, admiring tone.","sonnet":"A man catalogues his admiration for a woman who possesses every quality he desires — style, speed, soul, and an instinctive sensuality. Above all other attributes he celebrates her audacity, returning to it as though no other trait could measure up.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~shoot-to-thrill":{"haiku":"A predatory man pursues women aggressively, boasting of his sexual dominance and violent seduction.","opus":"A swaggering predatory boast in which the singer imagines himself as a loaded weapon aimed at reckless, pill-popping women. From Back in Black, recorded after Bon Scott's death, it became a signature showcase for new frontman Brian Johnson, its firearm metaphor and stadium riff channeling menace into pure hard-rock adrenaline.","sonnet":"Dangerous seduction as sport — the narrator presents himself as something illegal and irresistible, a loaded gun aimed at women who want exactly this kind of trouble. The pleasure is all in the threat, the pull, the escalation from desire to destruction. There is no vulnerability anywhere; only the performance of absolute confidence in one's own destructive appeal.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~t-n-t":{"haiku":"A dangerous, dirty man prowls the streets—dynamite incarnate—seeking women and trouble without restraint or conscience.","opus":"Bon Scott's cocksure outlaw swagger, a self-styled public enemy blowing into town as walking dynamite, all bravado and danger, the chant turning menace into pure rock-and-roll fun.","sonnet":"A swagger anthem: the narrator is a wanted, dangerous, explosive force coming to town — lock up your daughters, get out of the way, because he's T.N.T. and you won't contain him.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~the-furor":{"haiku":"A chaotic figure disrupts society, acting without law or consequence, embodying disorder and rebellion against established order.","opus":"A late AC/DC strut of raw sexual bravado, the narrator casting himself as your furor, all appetite and swaggering menace, the pun on the German word half-buried under the usual back-alley bluster.","sonnet":"A self-declaration of chaotic masculine power — the narrator positions himself as a law unto himself, a force that brings disorder and heat wherever he goes, answering to no one.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~the-honey-roll":{"haiku":"A man pursues a woman intensely through explicit sexual acts and dominance.","opus":"AC/DC turns appetite into wall-to-wall innuendo, a bedroom romp dressed in food and rollercoaster imagery where a woman turns the narrator on inside out and upside down. Take-what-I-want bravado meets a partner who detonates like a bomb and flips every switch. It is the band operating in its most unabashed carnal register, the sandwich puns and amusement-park thrills a delivery system for pure lascivious energy.","sonnet":"A woman who takes complete control of a sexual encounter — inverting, overwhelming, detonating — is rendered entirely through the impact she has on the narrator, who can only track the sensation of being turned inside out.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~the-jack":{"haiku":"A woman with hidden disease seduces a man through poker game deception, leaving him infected with her gift.","opus":"An early staple laced with card-game double meaning, AC/DC recounts a poker-faced woman who was dealt with before and carries the jack, slang for venereal disease. Wild deuces, tattoos, and a garter set the scene for a man realizing too late the cards came from the bottom of the pack. The blues-rooted swagger and live-show banter make a cautionary tale sound like a wink.","sonnet":"A card-game metaphor for venereal disease — a woman who plays men expertly, holding a card no one sees coming. The reveal is comic and rueful: the narrator should have read the signs, but the deck was always stacked against him.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~thunderstruck":{"haiku":"A man caught between railroad tracks faces inescapable fate, then celebrates living recklessly and seizing pleasure with dancers.","opus":"Caught on a railroad track with no help coming, the narrator rides adrenaline into a debauched joyride through Texas, thunder standing for gunfire, heartbeat, and sheer electric thrill. AC/DC open with Angus Young's finger-tapped guitar salvo, and this 1990 juggernaut became their definitive concert opener, distilling danger and abandon into one relentless surge.","sonnet":"A highway rampage of speed, excess, and electric encounter. A traveler blown sideways by the sheer force of experience — strange cities, willing strangers, and the physical shock of something overwhelming — collapses it all into one crackling, involuntary surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~what-do-you-do-for-money-honey":{"haiku":"Transactional sex and mercenary female desire reduce intimacy to cold business while men queue for paid access to ambition.","opus":"A jeering address to a woman who trades sex for money and comfort, squeezing men dry while the singer needles that she's getting old and slow at it. It's a sneering rock strut with no sympathy for its target.","sonnet":"A blunt interrogation of a woman the speaker sees as transactional — working bars, riding cars, digging for gold, squeezing blood from men — asking again and again what she does for money and where she gets her kicks.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~whiskey-on-the-rocks":{"haiku":"Celebration of heavy drinking through various alcohol references and the hedonistic lifestyle of ordering and consuming drinks at a bar.","opus":"AC/DC doing what they do best, a raucous barroom toast to drinking yourself blind. No subtext beyond the joy of the next round, pure celebration of getting seven sheets to the wind.","sonnet":"A straightforward celebration of a night of hard drinking — cataloguing cocktails, toasting companions, and ending up thoroughly wrecked, with whiskey as the unambiguous hero.","src":"haiku"},
"ac-dc~you-shook-me-all-night-long":{"haiku":"A sexual encounter is intense and physical. A woman dominates and tests a man's endurance through a night of unrelenting passion.","opus":"The band's signature anthem, first sung by Brian Johnson on the Back in Black rebirth, celebrates a relentless lover whose stamina leaves the narrator gasping. Structures tremble and the ground trembles across a night of appetite and repeat rounds. Its knockout hooks and cheerful carnal boasting made it AC/DC's most enduring singalong, seduction rendered as pure rock-and-roll exhilaration.","sonnet":"An encounter with an extraordinarily capable woman is celebrated in the language of machinery and combat sport — engine-clean, round after round — the whole night rendered as a physical event that left the narrator shaken and wanting more.","src":"haiku"},
"acacia-ridge~anarchist":{"opus":"A character study of an anarchist eaten alive by an icy, fire-forged rage, bent on razing every institution and structure of society, his sole complaint that they didn't collapse sooner. He treats the destruction not as martyrdom but as the one cause he considers worth the fight.","sonnet":"A portrait of a radicalized man driven by systemic hatred to burn everything down — raised in fire and corrupted by oppressive forces, he lies awake at night and chooses chaos as the only thing worth fighting for, not out of martyrdom but out of fury.","src":"sonnet"},
"acacia-ridge~chimera":{"haiku":"Cognitive spirals and emotional manipulation create monsters within as the speaker descends into self-destruction. Fire and disappointment consume from both inside and external judgment.","opus":"A bitter address to someone spiraling in their own overthinking, the speaker refusing to save them and instead relishing the burn of their downfall. The drug-of-need imagery and the vow to give them reason to hate him make this less a rescue than a coldly deserved abandonment.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone responsible for wasted time and broken skin — the devil is in the details, the drug was always provided, and the speaker hopes it burns them inside like raining fire while watching their ship sink and their mind overheat.","src":"haiku"},
"acacia-ridge~hivemind":{"haiku":"Emotional turbulence at the edge of belonging and dissolution. Resistance crumbles into collective consumption where escape proves impossible, the hive promising welcome even as it traps the individual into conformity and submission.","opus":"Anxiety and the pull of conformity rendered as a hive that welcomes you and won't let you leave, no matter how you resist. Beneath the racing pulse is a plea to simply be seen from the other person's side before being absorbed.","sonnet":"A speaker caught between personal autonomy and collective absorption describes hyperventilating anxiety — \"water in my lungs,\" oscillating between \"almost\" and \"enough\" — before the hive's welcoming call promises belonging so total that leaving becomes impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"acacia-ridge~nomad":{"haiku":"A wanderer rejects identity and community, burning roads behind to ensure isolation. Self-hatred becomes fuel for persistent motion across unmapped terrain.","opus":"A declaration of radical self-reliance born from feeling abandoned, the path forward taken solo and torched behind so no one can trace or name him. Each stride and every wound he bears is recast as spite toward a God he feels deserted him, turning that abandonment into fuel.","sonnet":"A person sickened by their own tells and superstitions burns the road behind them to wander where no one knows their name — not forsaken by God but choosing to be unknown — and arrives at the realisation that every step, every tear, every drop of blood was not for the person who hurt them but in spite of that person, which is the only freedom available.","src":"haiku"},
"accept~princess-of-the-dawn":{"haiku":"Mystical kingdoms and eternal suffering intertwine in a gothic narrative where beauty and death coexist in perfect, unreachable symmetry.","opus":"German metal in full mythic mode, stacking grails, dragons and vestal virgins into a fantasy of an unreachable enchantress who is really death dressed as royalty. The pageantry matters more than any coherent story.","sonnet":"An elaborate fantasy in the mode of medieval epic — a mysterious lady of mystical power presides over a world of dragons, knights, and cosmic struggle, her dawn arrival heralding the triumph of light over darkness.","src":"haiku"},
"accept~time-machine":{"haiku":"Time traps passengers in an inescapable machine, aging and eroding them while carrying them toward an unknown destination with no hope of departure.","opus":"A metal-band conceit about time itself as a vehicle no one boards willingly, everyone a passenger dragged forward, aging, spinning toward the same end. The thrill of the ride masks a fatalism about mortality none of us steer.","sonnet":"Time is figured as an indifferent cosmic machine that carries all passengers without consent or destination — humanity is strapped to its gears, slowly aging, unable to stop or exit, driven only by the rising sun.","src":"haiku"},
"accvsed~currents":{"haiku":"Isolation and drowning redeemed through another's presence as lighthouse. A lover provides passage through internal decay, becoming the force to swim against tides of self-destruction and hollowness.","opus":"A drowning-at-sea image for depression, another person becoming the lighthouse and the reason to begin over. Rescue and codependency blur as the same savior is asked to follow him down into the deep where his demons lurk.","sonnet":"A drowning narrator addresses the person who became a lighthouse when all other signals were sirens, crediting them with the strength to swim against the tide and choosing, in the final lines, to descend once more toward demon-filled depths — this time inviting them along.","src":"haiku"},
"accvsed~day-of-the-locust":{"haiku":"Self-loathing through violent rebirth as prophetic necessity. The narrator feeds on his own decay and scars, embracing the role of devoured sacrifice until transformation burns away shame and false identity.","opus":"Wanting pain to prove you're still alive, and treating self-destruction as a shedding of skin toward rebirth. The recurring shame is the inability to look in the mirror or love oneself, so annihilation becomes the only route to renewal.","sonnet":"Self-loathing and suicidal ideation are framed as a locust swarm stripping the speaker bare: standing at the mirror in disdain, skin that must be shed, demons that take over \"in the eye of the storm.\" A turn at the end refuses escape and instead begs for transformation through rebirth.","src":"haiku"},
"accvsed~never-enough":{"haiku":"Consuming greed as both disease and philosophy, where satisfaction proves mathematically impossible. Each victory hollows the self further, leaving destruction in place of love.","opus":"Ambition and greed as a wound so deep it's gone numb, the compulsion to take more even after bleeding someone dry. Growth becomes a curse where each success sets up a harder fall and nothing is ever enough to fill the hole.","sonnet":"Relentless self-serving ambition is its own punishment: the speaker admits bleeding others dry, flourishing in destruction, and leaving a trail of Hurricane-level damage — yet the appetite never closes, and even in self-annihilation the demand is to keep suffering for them.","src":"haiku"},
"accvsed~senescence":{"haiku":"Aging and decay corrupt the self into monument of disintegration; the speaker becomes statue and corpse, useful only as witness to their own destruction.","opus":"Facing aging and decay as a moving carcass blackening in self-disgust, sold to the shadows and asking whether anyone will remember or betray him before it all runs out.","sonnet":"About the slow, visible decay of aging — watching youth recede, the body becoming a moving carcass, skin cracking like stone, sold soul, and self-disdain. The narrator asks to be remembered even as they describe themselves as already dead in all the ways that matter, contemplating dying like a martyr for the things they have done with nowhere left to run.","src":"haiku"},
"acid-drinkers~24-radical-questions": {"haiku":"Existential interrogation of morality, identity, and social hypocrisy; challenging every assumption about war, religion, and authenticity.","opus":"A rapid-fire barrage of provocations, the narrator interrogating money, art, religion, war, morality, and his own drinking and violence, never answering, just piling up uncomfortable questions about who we've become. Acid Drinkers are a veteran Polish thrash and groove-metal band with a sharp, irreverent streak, and this restless, self-implicating catalogue of doubts fits their taste for confrontation delivered with dark humor and blunt force.","sonnet":"A barrage of genuine and rhetorical questions fired in rapid succession — about art versus commerce, about violence, religion, class, addiction, and geopolitics — that refuses to settle into any coherent ideology. The point is the questioning itself: holding contradictions up to the light without resolving them, then letting the number stand as the record of everything that remains unanswered.","src":"haiku","fable":"Built as an unbroken salvo of interrogation that answers nothing, the piece makes that refusal its whole argument: a rattled mind firing doubts about cash, God, war, sex and its own worthlessness without landing anywhere. Acid Drinkers, thrashing Poznan crossover men singing in English near the Cold War's end, let the self-savaging confession about being a fraud or plain garbage bleed outward until private squalor becomes the geopolitical terror of who first launches the warhead. Each doubt escalates the stakes and drops the last, so the numbered checklist of a title files existential panic like a clerk's inventory, mocking the notion that dread this vast fits a form. The pose reads as nihilist provocation but registers as honesty from someone who has quit pretending he can tell virtue from atrocity and admits it aloud.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness","war & battle","shame & repression"],"means":"dread filed as inventory, answering nothing","built":"a numbered checklist title mocks the form it uses"},
"acid-drinkers~25-cents-for-a-riff": {"haiku":"A satire on capitalism's debasement of human values, where life itself and artistic expression are reduced to commodities auctioned off at the same negligible price.","opus":"Framed as a bleak auction, the song laments a world where a human life and a piece of music are valued the same and sold off cheap. It treats the trading of souls as a done deal, with the hammer coming down at 25 cents apiece.","sonnet":"A sardonic commentary on the devaluation of both music and human life, equating a riff with a soul at auction and asking who is to blame when culture and existence are sold for next to nothing.","src":"haiku","fable":"Art gets dragged onto the auction floor and sold as a lot, the band cast as both the merchandise and the witnesses to their own knock-down sale. The savior once promised to lift the dread of an age is catalogued, appraised, and cleared alongside a guitar phrase for the exact same figure, mercy and culture liquidated in the same breath. The sharpest move is the second-person coaxing: an auctioneer talks the artist through the transaction, claiming the number is fair while confessing he loathes his own trade, then reminds the room that a real reputation only lifts the reserve. When the bidding shuts and the gavel falls, the punchline is arithmetic — a quarter for a soul, a quarter for a riff, the round contempt of the sum saying everything the market pretends it cannot.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","money & the street"],"means":"mercy and culture sold at the same knockdown figure","built":"the auctioneer confesses self-loathing while calling the price fair"},
"acid-drinkers~50-don-t-slow-down": {"haiku":"Reckless acceleration toward self-destruction, the speaker defiant against warnings, velocity itself becoming both escape and damnation.","opus":"A reckless anthem of speed and defiance against aging and burnout, insisting on flooring it whatever the danger, dead brakes and empty tank and jeering critics alike. At fifty the joke is that going slow is more lethal than crashing, and easing off would mean drowning.","sonnet":"A defiant middle-finger to aging, critics, and decay — brakes gone, blood shed, preachers puking, chicks getting younger, and the narrator still won't slow down at fifty. The diesel-soaked acceleration is its own philosophy: it's too slow to crash at this speed, so run.","src":"haiku","fable":"A defiance anthem built on a single conceit: life as a car that must never drop below fifty, because the moment you decelerate you die — the speed itself is what keeps the crash from mattering. The incredulous fifty of the title reads as a birthday as easily as a dial, and the ageing jabs studding the verses say it is both. Each verse loads the odometer with fresh dangers (dead brakes, spent fuel, blood already bled) and the chorus answer is always the same imperative, refusing to slow, so the momentum reads less as swagger than as terror of stopping. The final verse tips the hand: the real hazards are people — the moralizing preacher, the saboteurs scrawling on walls, the idiots planning to break the speaker down — and the ride becomes a middle finger to critics, a man outrunning his own reputation and mortality by declaring both too slow to catch him. That the diesel-and-nose swagger sits inside a band that spent its career weaponizing juvenile provocation makes the bravado knowing rather than naive; the joke and the death-wish are the same accelerator pedal.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","death & grief","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"momentum kept not from courage but from terror of stopping","built":"the title is simultaneously a speed and a birthday"},
"acid-drinkers~acid-drinker": {"haiku":"Refusal and isolation weaponized as survival code. The narrator trusts no one and walks alone, severing all bonds rather than risk vulnerability or compromise.","opus":"A thrash-metal creed of total distrust and going one's own way, warning others off the path that would destroy them. The Polish band's swagger lands on a fatalist punchline: some of us simply weren't built to win.","sonnet":"A confrontational dismissal of someone who has crossed a line — the speaker refuses to forgive, refuses to guide, refuses to talk, insisting they walk their own way and warning that following will only lead to self-destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lone-wolf code here reads like classic tough-guy swagger — no forgiveness, no handshake offered, a man who trusts nobody and insists he walks his own road — the thrash-comic hard-man persona this band wore proudly. But the warnings curdle that pose into something protective rather than menacing: keep away, because trailing this figure leads only to a padded ward and to your own destruction. When he finally admits that victory was never in the cards, the whole macho stance collapses into a fatalist's shrug, the addiction named in the title surfacing as a route that offers nothing but company in ruin. What poses as a threat turns out to be a confession wearing a threat's clothes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"a threat wearing a confession as its disguise","built":"the macho warning collapses when the speaker admits victory was never available"},
"acid-drinkers~acidofilia": {"haiku":"Chronic addiction and recovery attempts where music icons also struggle; addiction corrupts the soul and requires constant internal battle.","opus":"A boozy communion with dead rock icons, the narrator downing shots to purify his soul while Janis, Kurt, Bony Scott, and Jim beckon from beyond, feeding on a made-up affliction as his body falls apart yet his dreams refuse to die. Acid Drinkers, the Polish thrash-metal veterans, wrap this hard-drinking necromantic humor around a genuinely wistful core about misery, addiction, and clinging to life.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone living at the intersection of chemical dependence and rock mythology, sharing imagined drinks with dead legends while acknowledging that the body is falling apart and sobriety is not on offer. The self-described art of acidofilia is feeding on dysfunction — not as a boast exactly, but as the most honest account available of what keeps a person going.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song reframes addiction as a devotional practice — acidophilus, the gut culture that keeps you alive, punned into acidophilia, a love of the acid that dissolves you. Its real argument sits in the roll call: Janis, Kurt, Bon Scott, Jim Morrison, the 27-and-nearby dead summoned not as warnings but as drinking companions who leave notes and phone calls, so the speaker's slide toward the mortuary reads as joining a club rather than falling out of the world. The music-history lens is the whole point: Acid Drinkers, Poland's beer-soaked thrash crew, know exactly which martyrs they are toasting, and the refusal to be healed is delivered as a craftsman's pride in a difficult art. Underneath the swagger the song holds the two faces at once — the mirror still returns a sublime smile and the desires are not dead even as the body comes apart, which is precisely the addict's lie the song both mouths and exposes. It ends by feeding on the culture that eats it, the pun closing like a trap.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"refusal to heal delivered as craftsman's pride in a difficult art","built":"acidophilus punned into acidophilia closes like a trap on the speaker it feeds"},
"acid-drinkers~after-the-vulture": {"haiku":"Contempt for humanity deepens alongside recognition of human cruelty's uniqueness; only the fantasy of escape through death offers respite.","opus":"A misanthropic tirade against humanity and religion, resenting a Christ who makes everyone pay and seeing only spite in mankind. Suicidal thoughts are held not as despair but as the one comforting option that keeps him going.","sonnet":"A misanthrope barely holding back violent contempt for human stupidity — he keeps his dark urges caged because letting them loose would end catastrophically, and the only thought keeping him going is that today could always be a good day for suicide.","src":"haiku","fable":"A misanthrope's monologue keeps circling back to one clenched refrain about pinning his urges down, because the real subject is not the hatred but the restraint holding it, a leash fraying verse by verse. He indicts mankind as the only creature capable of spite, blames a Christ two millennia into collecting the debt for his own crucifixion, and pronounces an Old Testament curse of ruined sleep and scavenger's scraps that turns out to name his own fate as squarely as the world's. The gut-punch is the ending's twist on a survival cliche: what steadies him is not hope but the permanently open exit, the thought that today would serve fine for suicide, so the whole rant reads as a man staying his own hand one refrain at a time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","anger & defiance","faith & the occult"],"means":"the open exit kept available as the only thing preventing its use","built":"the Old Testament curse the speaker heaps on the world names his own fate equally"},
"acid-drinkers~anybody-home": {"haiku":"The speaker urges someone to use their mind and imagination, offering something new that will help avoid pain and fading away.","opus":"A taunting pitch to use your head and imagination, the narrator hawking something new and daring the listener to try it, mocking the flushed, praying face of anyone too scared to bite. Acid Drinkers are a long-running Polish thrash-and-crossover band known for tongue-in-cheek English lyrics, and this rides their punchy, irreverent groove into a cheeky dare that half-parodies the hard-sell it delivers.","sonnet":"A jab at someone switched off behind their own face — the speaker is offering something new and useful and can't get through, so they start wondering whether anyone is actually home upstairs. The tone flips between cheerful sales pitch and exasperated demand. Short, blunt, more comedic than cruel.","src":"haiku","fable":"The recurring rap on the skull treats the listener as a vacant house, and every command to reason, imagine, and flex the big brain is really a coaxing hand steering someone toward a door they never chose to open. The reassurances pile up past the point of comfort — no ache waiting, no lie in the offer, just one push after another — until the friendliness reads as coercion in a party mask. The tell arrives when the pitchman disowns his own route, conceding it belongs to somebody else, which turns the whole sales patter into a warning that the reddened, prayer-reaching mark is being worked over by a method the seller wouldn't walk himself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"friendly coaxing that reads as coercion once the seller disowns his own route","built":"the pitchman's concession that the path is not his turns the sales patter into a warning"},
"acid-drinkers~are-you-a-rebel": {"opus":"The Polish thrash veterans confront a friend collapsing into defeat, sitting alone before a mirror, feeling old and tired and unwilling to fly. The chorus challenge cuts through the pity: is there still any fight left in them? It is an exasperated, affectionate demand that someone reclaim their nerve rather than surrender.","sonnet":"About someone who has withdrawn from life and given up — the narrator sees them sitting alone, looking old and tired, and challenges them: stop making excuses, stop pretending to be too broken to fly, and ask yourself whether you are truly a rebel or just surrendering.","src":"sonnet","fable":"The taunting title turns inside out: real defiance here is not rage but refusal, a friend who has stopped wanting to fly and now stares down a mirror through the long night. The speaker keeps circling that withdrawn figure, exhausting every rescue offered — strength, a doctor, one more plea — and meets the same locked will, so the true subject becomes the ache of watching someone quietly quit on themselves. Set against Acid Drinkers' party-metal swagger, the sneer curdles into concern, and the sentence broken off at you don't wanna leaves the whole question suspended exactly where its target has fallen silent.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"watching someone quietly quit on themselves after every rescue fails","built":"the taunting title inverts — defiance here is withdrawal, not rage"},
"acid-drinkers~backyard-bandit": {"haiku":"A small-time rebel scorns rules and respectable conformity, choosing to be an outcast 'backyard bandit' rather than play by society's game. The song revels in defiance despite the emptiness of such rebellion.","opus":"Polish thrash swagger inhabiting a small-time loser and rule-breaker who spits at truth and hate alike. The bravado is half self-mockery, a nobody insisting on his defiance while daring the listener to be next.","sonnet":"A self-portrait as a zero-hero rule-breaker from the margins — a nobody with nothing to lose who defines himself by pissing on respectability and hiding in the jaw of hate, a crude but honest outsider identity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A small-time hoodlum builds a whole mythology out of petty misbehaviour, and the narrator half-suspects the myth is a joke played on himself. He concedes upfront that he might be the mark, the nobody, then converts that admission into bravado, so the strut and the self-verdict of total foolishness arrive welded together in the same breath. The broken syntax is the persona itself: a Polish tough throwing his weight around in a language he barely commands, and the refrain about being the sole survivor at last call plays like a drunk crowning himself ruler of a deserted lot. When the closing line turns to ask whether you would care to join this parade of fools, the whole swagger tips over into a caution.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","comedy & wordplay","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"strut and self-verdict of total foolishness delivered in the same breath","built":"broken Polish-inflected English enacts the persona of a man whose authority barely holds"},
"acid-drinkers~barmy-army": {"haiku":"Obedient mob following madmen blindly; soldiers commanded to kill become monuments to stupidity and institutional control.","opus":"A caustic portrait of the mindless soldier-mob, waking on command to follow madmen, a buffalo-armed fool who knows little yet embodies the law, the narrator both shooter and target dreaming of a monument if he kills enough. It skewers militarism and blind obedience. Acid Drinkers, the veteran Polish thrash band known for their sardonic wit, drive this anti-authoritarian scorn with punkish, English-language bite.","sonnet":"Conscription as a meat grinder — the speaker is simultaneously the soldier and the cannon fodder, following mad commanders on command, knowing that killing more earns only a monument over his own corpse. The army is barmy, dead, made of shadows, and the whole system runs on animal obedience and the promise of fire.","src":"haiku","fable":"A conscript's-eye demolition of militarism whose venom comes from how the point of view keeps caving in on itself until accuser and accused turn out to wear the same rotting uniform. It opens inside a soldier drowning in filth and orders, then swings to a savage portrait of the officer as a bellowing buffalo of a man, reflex where a mind should be, who knows almost nothing yet becomes the law by barking on cue. The song's real turn comes when contempt for that brute folds back home: the carrion the speaker heaped on his commander now describes his own body, and he grasps that the rifle he aims and the crosshairs settling on him are one thing, the medal-hungry executioner indistinguishable from the corpse it earns. Coming from a Polish band who came up as the Cold War order collapsed, the closing chant that rebrands the outfit barmy, dead, and shadow lands as a jeer at the whole apparatus, where the promised statue rises over a body and the marching ranks are already spectres.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"contempt for the brute officer folds back to describe the speaker's own body","built":"the crosshairs aimed outward and the crosshairs aimed inward converge into one thing"},
"acid-drinkers~be-my-godzilla": {"haiku":"A person consumed by self-destruction invites others to devour them rather than face their inner darkness alone.","opus":"Polish thrash veterans stage a darkly comic surrender to inner torment, a narrator who cannot fight or flee an inner demon and instead invites it to consume him whole. Casting himself as food and drink to be devoured, he turns self-destruction into black comedy, capitulation dressed up as an almost cheerful offering.","sonnet":"A person in private crisis — unable to call for help, unable to fight what is destroying them — invites the monster in completely. The only option they can offer is total consumption: devour me, since I cannot save myself.","src":"haiku","fable":"The dread here is metabolic and unwinnable: you cannot gun down a thing that never took form, cannot bring up a meal you never swallowed, so the monster he can neither wound nor outrun is lodged in his own gut. His refusal to phone a doctor or rouse the others is framed as mercy — sparing them exposure to whatever he is — yet it works exactly like the quarantine depression builds around a person, sealing off every rescue. Then the resistance collapses into an offering, and he lays himself out as dessert, a buffet spread, a full glass to be drained by the very force that has been hunting him. Naming that devourer his Godzilla turns extinction into a gift he presents, since he cannot slay the thing and cannot perform enough visible despair to be believed, so he chooses to be eaten rather than to strike.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness","madness & the mind"],"means":"offering himself as a meal to the force he cannot wound or outrun","built":"the monster is unshootable because it was never separate from his own gut"},
"acid-drinkers~become-a-bitch": {"haiku":"Female ambition and refusal to capitulate transform into corruption; the speaker rejects sanctity and embraces what society deems unholiness.","opus":"A caustic character sketch of a woman navigating a corrupt, hypocritical world of politicians and users, hardening into cynicism. The chorus's cruel benediction watches her soul decay, indicting the world that shaped her more than her.","sonnet":"A portrait of a woman who trades moral and intellectual integrity for political power and skin — framed through the lens of corrupt parliamentary dealings and performative sexuality, the song watches her soul decay as she rises through institutions that reward exactly the corruption she embodies.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is a slur the song forces you to watch being manufactured. Its verses sketch a woman who refuses the roles handed her — literate, self-possessed, done being packaged as a harmless peach — while the chorus answers not with argument but with a mock-liturgical curse that her soul rot for choosing her own path. The Eichmann jab and the roll-call of grafting MPs work as misdirection: the speaker dresses raw contempt as moral verdict, as if her independence were the real corruption in a world of actual crooks. The closing turn exposes the mechanism — she opened her door to lovers and was betrayed, so the epithet was never her becoming but their label, the word reached for the instant a woman stops being convenient. Acid Drinkers keep it deadpan enough that the sneer registers as the target rather than the stance, letting the refrain indict the mouth that sings it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"a slur exposed as the label applied the instant a woman stops being convenient","built":"the mock-liturgical chorus indicts the mouth that sings it rather than its target"},
"acid-drinkers~black-blood-canyon": {"haiku":"Depressive despair where darkness consumed hope; the narrator becomes a shadow, their heart pumping cold blood.","opus":"A gothic nocturne of numbed despair in which the narrator sits alone in darkness as something large and dark painlessly bites into his heart, until he becomes his own hollow reflection with icy dark veins, even fielding a tearful call from Death, then shrugs it all off to fetch a drink. Acid Drinkers, Polish thrash veterans, undercut the dread with deadpan barroom humor.","sonnet":"A night of total interior collapse — the sun sentenced, the morning dead, the self becoming its own shadow. Something enormous arrives and bites the heart without causing pain, which is the most alarming detail: the numbness is complete. Death calls on the phone weeping, and there is nothing to say, so the only logical move is to go get a beer.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study in emotional necrosis dressed as a horror vignette: some vast dark thing arrives when the lights are off and bites the heart, yet the speaker registers neither pain nor surprise. That flatness is the real subject — he has become a numb outline of himself, an eskimo iced over his own feeling, having retched up memories and affections until the pulse just moves sluggish tar on autopilot. The macabre curdles into deadpan when even Death rings up weeping about her own troubles and he has nothing to offer, mortal dread and personified doom both demoted to a tiresome late-night acquaintance. The repeated impulse to step out for a beer is the punchline that seals it: the only response left to a deadened man facing his extinction is the most trivial errand imaginable, Acid Drinkers' signature gallows shrug, despair rendered as a joke because feeling it straight is no longer on offer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","death & grief","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"despair rendered as a joke because feeling it straight is no longer available","built":"personified Death rings up with her own troubles and gets nothing back"},
"acid-drinkers~blind-leadin-the-blind": {"haiku":"The song warns against following blind leaders or ignorant guides, expressing refusal to participate in directionless pursuit of unclear goals. It's a brief statement about distrust in uncritical following.","opus":"A refusal to follow an ignorant, untrustworthy leader, questioning where blindly following the blind can possibly lead.","sonnet":"A brief, direct refusal to follow someone the narrator sees as ignorant and untrustworthy — rejecting their leadership and intentions outright, with the core image being a blind person leading others nowhere.","src":"haiku","fable":"A flat refusal of discipleship: the speaker sizes up the would-be leader as an ignoramus whose intentions and interest cannot be trusted, and declines to walk behind him. Acid Drinkers turn the old proverb into a rhetorical dare — the recurring demand to know what waits at the end of a road walked by the sightless is the whole thesis, since the answer is the ditch both parties share. The refrain hardens rather than develops; its blunt repetition is the point, a heel dug in against the pull of following anyone on faith alone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"refusal of discipleship delivered as a heel dug in, not an argument","built":"the refrain hardens rather than develops, repetition being the whole thesis"},
"acid-drinkers~blues-beatdown": {"haiku":"A person rejects manipulation and refuses to follow others' expectations, wanting to live life on their own terms.","opus":"A defiant refusal of everyone who dictates how the narrator should live and demands he compromise; he'd sooner get violent than swallow mindless advice, insisting on steering his own life solo and forging his own way against anyone trying to reshape him. Aggressive, contrarian self-assertion from the Polish thrash veterans.","sonnet":"A defiant rejection of unsolicited advice and social pressure, with the narrator insisting they'll live on their own terms and would sooner break the necks of those who keep trying to compromise their path.","src":"haiku","fable":"A snarling manifesto of self-rule that savors confrontation the way Kilgore savored the beach at dawn, swapping the cavalry's helicopter blitz for the private thrill of kicking open a door onto the same tired faces urging surrender. Every offered kindness reads as an insult here, every piece of counsel a leash, and the speaker would sooner injure a meddler than concede a single inch of his chosen road. Yet Acid Drinkers quietly indict their own hero, since his loud claim to superior judgment is precisely the deaf, unteachable arrogance the advisers cautioned him against, leaving us unsure whether we hear earned independence or a stubbornness dressed up as liberty. The mangled, Polish-inflected English only tightens the effect, sketching a mouth moving faster than any correction could ever catch it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","freedom & escape","violence & murder"],"means":"loud self-rule indicted as the same deaf arrogance it claims to outrun","built":"Polish-inflected English enacts a mouth moving faster than any correction can catch"},
"acid-drinkers~boneless": {"haiku":"A speaker rejects the constraint and weight of a rigid physical spine, craving freedom from structure and formal obligation despite the prison-like confinement it creates.","opus":"The Polish crossover crew turn absurdist metaphor into rebellion, the narrator wishing his spine gone so he need never bow or bend. Storing his skeleton in a basement cage, he trades the stiff armor of rigid posture for freedom on the ground, treating uprightness itself as a prison to escape.","sonnet":"The spine becomes a metaphor for moral structure — rigid, confining, and occasionally abandoned. Without it, the narrator feels freer but dishonest; with it, he is caged and rattling. The armour of integrity is real and stored carefully at home, brought out only when unavoidable.","src":"haiku","fable":"The grotesque conceit is that a skeleton is removable gear, a suit of bone the speaker would rather store in the cellar than wear, and craving bonelessness becomes a craving to quit the backbone's twin job. That frame keeps him honest and vertical yet also cramps every motion and locks him behind a cage whose bars are his own ribs, so standing tall and being jailed collapse into one condition. The body turns lie detector: he splinters, clatters and fumes whenever he cheats, drinks or dissembles, which recasts the rigid vertebrae as conscience refusing to let him stoop. A slab of frozen weight lodged in the ribcage carries that same hardness as a heart gone glacial, so the dream of molting the frame is really a longing to stop holding himself erect and slump down flush with the dirt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"craving bonelessness as longing to stop holding oneself upright","built":"the skeleton splinters when he cheats, recasting ribs as a conscience he cannot shed"},
"acid-drinkers~chewed-alive": {"haiku":"Self-consumption accelerates as the speaker watches someone slowly devoured by inner demons; survival becomes impossible surrender.","opus":"A portrait of a friend consumed by inner demons and depression, feeling half-eaten alive each morning. The band watches him waste away, his house filled with the darkness that is devouring him from within.","sonnet":"A portrait of a man being slowly destroyed from within by depression or mental illness — the speaker watches him call in crisis, write tragic verses, and feel like something is eating him alive piece by piece while demons fill his darkened house.","src":"haiku","fable":"The grim ingenuity here is a narrating seam: the stanzas belong to a friend fielding tearful late-night calls and reading the scrawled, self-cursing pages, while the chorus drops into the sufferer's own throat. That handoff is the argument, because it lets us watch the man from outside and then stand inside the mouth doing the eating. The affliction is self-consuming rather than imposed — no narcotic to indict, only a distant, joking heaven that rigged his life so he wakes daily to find himself half-devoured. The witness can chronicle the darkness swallowing the household, yet the horror is that he can never haul his friend back from it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","friendship & loyalty","death & grief"],"means":"witness can see the darkness but cannot reach across it","built":"narrating seam hands the mouth from friend to sufferer mid-chorus"},
"acid-drinkers~cigarettes":{"haiku":"The speaker's world has shattered after a tornado, can't find clarity, and feels broke and abandoned by God; cigarettes remain the one thing that helps him through the crisis.","opus":"Acid Drinkers voice a penniless musician spiraling through a personal meltdown, his surroundings shaking and unfamiliar, even the divine no longer reachable. Rattled and desperate to regain his footing, he finds his one small comfort in a smoke. The Polish band's gallows humor turns collapse into wry comedy, the cigarette the last steadying thing in a life unraveling.","sonnet":"Someone in the middle of a personal collapse — world shaken, faith unreachable, too broke for a therapist — finds bleak comfort in the one thing that stays: a cigarette. The ritual of smoking becomes a substitute for the clarity they cannot otherwise reach.","src":"haiku"},
"acid-drinkers~konsument":{"haiku":"Consumers mindlessly absorb information and media propaganda, manipulated to act like instruments while surrendering independent thought.","opus":"A Polish satire of the passive consumer who eats just to eat and parrots political babble from radio and newspapers, bombarded with garbage and information; it challenges the listener to ask whether they too are just a manipulated instrument.","sonnet":"A Polish critique of the passive consumer: a person who eats, drinks, absorbs political noise from radio and TV, and parrots opinions without ever thinking independently — the song asks whether the listener is that consumer.","src":"haiku","fable":"Kazik's lyric runs a single verb — konsumować, to consume — through two registers until they collapse into one: the man who eats only to eat is the same man who swallows radio babble and newspaper lines and calls it having an opinion, so that the consumer of food and the consumer of propaganda become one docile animal, force-fed garbage and buried under information. The whole thing is built to spring a trap in the second person — is the consumer you? — flipping from satire of a type to an accusation of the listener, the point being that they need you to be a consumer, not to think, to act like an instrument. Acid Drinkers' cover is not incidental to that: taking a Kazik alt-icon protest song and thrashing it up on Infernal Connection turns the act of covering into its own small joke about consumption and repackaging, the mockery of mass-produced intake performed by a band feeding you a mass-produced product — which is exactly why the sardonic ho, ho lands as self-aware rather than smug.","fv":2,"fnote":"The cover outs itself: Kazik sings it — the author fronting Acid Drinkers on his own 1987 Kult protest song, and he shot the album’s cover photo besides — so the thrash repackaging of Konsument arrives with the consumer-critic’s signature on the product.","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"the food-consumer and the propaganda-swallower are one docile animal","built":"the second-person flip springs the satire as an accusation on the listener","fvr":"2.3"},
"acid-drinkers~cops-broke-my-beer": {"haiku":"Authority suppression of freedom; police shut down a performance and arrest the narrator, money the only remedy.","opus":"A wry gripe about American absurdity, a band's gig shut down after five minutes for volume, the singer jailed for drinking on the street, subjected to a Polish joke, then freed once cash changes hands. It is punchy, sarcastic culture-clash comedy. Acid Drinkers, the Polish thrash-metal veterans, thrive on exactly this kind of rowdy, tongue-in-cheek storytelling about booze, cops, and the way money smooths everything over.","sonnet":"A touring band's night in America goes wrong — gig shut down, drunk in the streets, hauled to jail for an open container, then rescued when a friend's cocaine is found and bail money changes hands. It is a deadpan travelogue of culture shock, told without self-pity and ending on the pragmatic note that American dollars solve most things eventually.","src":"haiku","fable":"A touring band from Poland files its American night as flat reportage: a set silenced within minutes, then a lockup for the sin of carrying a drink past the tavern door, a rule that would baffle anyone raised on European sidewalks. The comedy runs deadpan, and the sharpest turn is the officer who mocks the visitors' nationality only moments before uncovering the powder a bandmate was holding — the man wearing the badge is the crude one, while the graver offense sits quietly in a pocket. What lifts it from grievance to dry indictment is the release: money changes hands, the trouble evaporates, and the chorus insisting this is simply how the country works lands as a cool foreigner's discovery that cash, not the advertised liberty, actually governs the place.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","comedy & wordplay","exile & diaspora"],"means":"cash governs where advertised liberty was promised","built":"the racist officer is the crude one while the graver offense sits in a pocket"},
"acid-drinkers~dancing-in-the-slaughter-house": {"haiku":"Apocalyptic judgment and violent reckoning: the speaker summons death and divine judgment upon a corrupt world where souls are claimed and the doomed are forced into a dance of doom.","opus":"Poland's Acid Drinkers, thrash irreverents who emerged as the Iron Curtain lifted, stage a grim theater of judgment and execution. The narrator plays executioner and prophet, claiming a victim's head while barking a German command to clear out, each death delivering another soul upward. The abattoir becomes a dance floor, its violence ritualized into grinding, blackly comic spectacle rather than mourned.","sonnet":"A figure of brutal authority presides over a space of mass death, commanding the condemned to perform one last grotesque ritual before they meet their maker. The refrain of expulsion — everyone out — gives the slaughterhouse an almost ceremonial quality, fate made choreography.","src":"haiku","fable":"Poznań thrashers voice an executioner whose whole grammar is the second person: a captive is claimed as property, promised freedom through death, ordered to make himself presentable before God, then waved off toward hell. A barked German clearing-out command yanks the killing floor off the splatter-metal shelf and onto Polish ground still scarred by wartime roundups, giving the abattoir a memory rather than a gore prop. Restraint arrives in the middle stretch, where sweating palms and a skull crammed with static and rotted dreams betray a man hollowed by his own office, the ritual refrain slamming the composure back over each fissure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","death & grief","nostalgia & memory","faith & the occult"],"means":"ritual composure slammed back over each fissure of self-disgust","built":"a barked German command pulls the killing floor onto Polish historical ground"},
"acid-drinkers~del-rocca": {"haiku":"A mysterious outsider figure named Rocca operates by unconventional rules, dispensing wisdom and drugs while remaining fundamentally indifferent to social structures and authority.","opus":"Poland's Acid Drinkers sketch a grotesque anti-hero named Rocca, a uniformed rogue who sells pills, drinks Chianti, mocks authority and taunts figures of power with absurd defiance. The imagery is deliberately surreal and profane, mixing political jabs with cartoon violence. It reflects the band's early-nineties thrash irreverence, a Polish scene using dark comedy to lampoon menace and machismo alike.","sonnet":"An anarchic, semi-mythic figure named Rocca careens through a grotesque European landscape — selling pills, escaping bears, taunting politicians, and haranguing anyone foolish enough to cross him. He is chaotic and indestructible, until the narration quietly warns he will not last long.","src":"haiku","fable":"A mock-heroic portrait assembles one impossible tough guy from mismatched fragments of continental bravado — a filthy uniform, wine settled inside an opera house, pills peddled to fools, cops menaced with a floppy piece of fruit — daring the listener to cheer a man who is nothing but posture. The cataloguing method is the whole joke: each boast contradicts the last, so gangster, mystic, mountain fugitive, and staggering drunk fuse into a strutting cartoon with no coherent person underneath. The pileup finally caves into a curt sentence — all that steel, yet his time runs short — and the parting jeer flips the entire swagger into a taunt aimed at the very muscle it has been miming. Taken flat it reads as a rowdy outlaw chant, but the parody sits in plain view, Acid Drinkers grinning through their thrash while they toast the blustering idiot loud enough that you clock exactly what he is.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay","identity & becoming"],"means":"parting jeer flips swagger into taunt aimed at the muscle it mimed","built":"cataloguing method self-destructs as each boast contradicts the last"},
"acid-drinkers~dementia-blvd": {"haiku":"Two people walk through madness alley at midnight encountering hallucinations, shame, and echoing gunshots. They snake hands with death, guided by terror and decadence.","opus":"Polish thrash staging a midnight walk down a boulevard of madness, a guided tour through terror, hallucination, and the handshake of death. It dares you to keep your nerve while questioning whether you'd ever come back.","sonnet":"A guided tour through the worst of urban decay and human degradation — hallucinations, shame, the smell of terror, the echo of gunshots, death shaking your hand — told as a dare to show up with open eyes and a brave heart on madness alley at midnight.","src":"haiku","fable":"An invitation dressed as a dare, summoning a companion to a nocturnal appointment on a street named for the unraveling mind, promising a long stroll toward whatever waits there. The traveler is told to shed the tools of a documentarian and instead scrawl impressions at a headlong pace, courage and patience packed like provisions for a road that offers hallucination, the reek of dread, imbecile grinning and the flat report of a firearm as recurring waypoints. Clasping the bony hand of death and letting dissipation play tour guide sounds like bravado until a quieter doubt surfaces near the close: whether anyone who ventures down this thoroughfare is permitted to turn around. That single hesitation converts a boastful revel into the Polish band's bleak wager on how far indulgence of the psyche can be pushed before the return route quietly closes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape"],"means":"bravado converted by one quiet doubt about whether the return route closes","built":"the street name literalizes the destination the companion is dared to walk toward"},
"acid-drinkers~demise": {"haiku":"Death arrives for those who lived recklessly; the speaker witnesses repeated failures to brake, leaving only corpses and disappeared grins.","opus":"A callous barroom tale of an adrenaline-addicted friend who lived fast and finally died, found hanging in some grim dump. The narrator's shrug over a borrowed woman and a foreseen crash is the cold heart of it.","sonnet":"A cold-eyed account of watching an adrenaline-junkie friend slowly head toward a predictable end — three drinking nights together, then he is found hanging in a cheap dump, and the narrator had no illusions it would end any other way.","src":"haiku","fable":"A eulogy that refuses to grieve on cue, cycling through the fallen with practiced coldness, as though indifference were the survival code binding hard-living men who dare not mourn aloud. The chorus hands over its trick: worn treads and failed brakes paint a speed-junkie's wreck, yet a later verse recasts that ruin as a body cut down from a cord in some squalid room. Reckless crash and chosen exit blur into a single collapse of restraint, and the swaggering talk of minding your own business is plainly a mask worn thin. What leaks through is the compulsively repeated boast about seeing this ending approach for years, less prophecy than confession that he watched a friend circle the drain and, held fast by that same code, did nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The chorus about failing brakes wraps around both the fast-lane chum and the man cut from the rope — the song never says whether that's two friends or one decline heard twice.","themes":["death & grief","addiction & self-destruction","suicide","shame & repression"],"means":"the boast of seeing it coming is confession of watching and doing nothing","built":"reckless crash and rope blur into one collapse so the cause stays deniable"},
"acid-drinkers~deuce": {"haiku":"A man works hard to provide for his family, and a woman should recognize his effort and worth.","opus":"Acid Drinkers deliver a rowdy cover of the KISS bar-room strut, all leering encouragement to a woman about her hard-working man. The bravado stays cheerfully crude, the deuce a boast of worth, the Polish thrashers leaning into swaggering good-time rock rather than their heavier fare.","sonnet":"A working man asks his partner for warmth and appreciation in return for honest labour. The request is plain: acknowledge the effort, stop the tears, follow his lead. Mutual satisfaction — if she is content, he is worth something — is the modest economy the song proposes.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cover of KISS's 1974 debut-album swagger anthem, a song Gene Simmons famously admitted he wrote without knowing what it meant — and that opacity is the point. The words gesture at a working man owed his due, a woman told to keep her tears and her grandmother out of the way and do as she's told, but the sense keeps slipping because the song was never about sense; it's about the strut, the entitlement worn as a groove. Acid Drinkers take it as pure inherited attitude, a Polish thrash band saluting the primal dumb machismo of American hard rock by playing the pose louder than the meaning. The pose is the mask: bravado that can't quite say what it wants because wanting to strut was always the whole content.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"inherited strut performed louder than the meaning it never had","built":"Simmons admitted he did not know what it meant — and that opacity is the cover"},
"acid-drinkers~disease-foundation": {"haiku":"Societal corruption where the rich and powerful exploit while the narrator burns in poverty; revolution through madness.","opus":"A sneering swipe at the greedy and hypocritical who grab diamonds, gold and whores while the narrator, too merry to bother screaming, refuses to look at their snouts, mocking those who cheat and flatter to get ahead. Cynicism curdles into gallows humor about digging graves on a sunny beach. Acid Drinkers, Poland's long-running thrash jokers, deliver this contempt for social climbers with their characteristic grinning malice.","sonnet":"Contempt for the parasitic and corrupt — snakes and crocodiles in human form sucking blood and biting bone while flashing wide smiles and licking boots. The speaker is too cheerful to scream about it, but the diagnosis is clear: these people are the disease, their foundation is rot, and the only response is to keep digging their grave on the sunny beach of their own excess.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Poznan thrash outfit's contempt for the freshly-minted profiteers of early-1990s Polish capitalism, whose looted gold, luxury autos and rented company the narrator won't dignify with a glance, dismissing their faces as pig muzzles. The reptilian refrain warps that scorn into delirium, with serpents and crocodiles gnawing at his blood and marrow while liquor keeps him laughing past the point of protest, the bite and the balm arriving in the same bottle. A mock-inspirational bridge promises the wounded man his remedy and a mentor to hoist him upward toward the summit he'll clutch forever, then guts the sentiment with a burial pit sunk into holiday sand. Its argument is that every rung of ambition and every alibi resolves into one more pretext for the bottle, the promised remedy indistinguishable from the sickness it claims to lift.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance"],"means":"every alibi for ambition resolves into another pretext for the bottle","built":"mock-inspirational bridge promises remedy then guts it with a burial pit in holiday sand"},
"acid-drinkers~dog-rock": {"haiku":"A hedonistic invitation to dance and intoxication through rock music. The song celebrates reckless abandon and physical attraction as escape from pain and suffering.","opus":"A crude, riotous invitation to the dance floor that slides from swaying hips into groping and promises of pain, puke, and pissed pants, all chanted around the shouted demand to take another shot. Acid Drinkers, Poland's veteran thrash jokers, revel here in deliberate vulgarity, turning the dog-rock refrain into a gleefully obnoxious party anthem that dares you to keep up with the dosing.","sonnet":"An invitation to abandon inhibition on the dance floor — draw close, sway, let the rhythm do the thinking. The energy escalates from playful to chaotic: by the end there is puke, ear-splitting noise, and the promise that the hard times will dissolve into the beat. The name dog rock is the whole philosophy — raw, low-down, no pretense.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dog Rock christens a fake genre with a straight face, then leans on one demand it repeats like a needle stuck in a groove, half nightclub invitation and half dosing schedule for a habit nobody chose to keep. What opens as a courtly turn around the floor rots verse by verse: the polite lead gives way to a hand on the ass, and the last stretch abandons any romance for vomit, soaked trousers, and pain waved off as relief. The gag lands because the hook never changes while its payoff sours from flirtation to filth, so every fresh dose the chant begs for exposes the arithmetic of compulsion, which sells a good night and settles up in ruin. It plays as brainless party metal that privately understands the party is a slow poisoning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism"],"means":"the hook never changes while its payoff sours from flirtation to filth","built":"fake genre name delivers the dosing schedule straight-faced through every verse"},
"acid-drinkers~don-t-drink-evil-things": {"haiku":"Rejection of societal warnings becomes defiance; the speaker chooses chaos over imposed morality.","opus":"A drinker's defiant rebuttal to everyone preaching sobriety and reform. He'd rather risk flying or falling on his own terms than let their wisdom clip his wings, even as they keep pouring him another anyway.","sonnet":"A defiant refusal of moralizing advice — everyone warns against 'evil things' while secretly pouring more drinks themselves, and the speaker knows the hypocrites crawl on the same lost ground, choosing to risk falling on their ass rather than pretend they're clean.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drinker's defiant monologue against the chorus of well-meaning voices telling him to straighten out, and the song's real bite is that it never lets him win the argument cleanly. He answers the moralists with a shrug of hypocrisy-spotting (have any of them actually stayed clean?) and a gambler's bravado about maybe breaking the spell or maybe landing flat on his ass, keeping both outcomes in play so the bravado never hardens into a boast. What tips it from a party anthem into something sadder is the turn where he concedes he knows exactly where the advice comes from — a place past reason or wisdom — and then the same crowd that warns him keeps refilling his glass, so the finger-wagging and the enabling turn out to be the same hands. The clipped, trailing final verse enacts the collapse it describes: the spell is not broken, the sentence just runs out of steam mid-toast, leaving the escape hatch open and unused.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance"],"means":"finger-wagging and enabling turn out to be the same hands","built":"final verse enacts collapse by running out of steam mid-toast, sentence unfinished"},
"acid-drinkers~el-pecado": {"haiku":"Addiction and sin consume the narrator like parasitic insects; it burns like fire and reproduces, eventually forcing confrontation.","opus":"A vivid personification of addiction or temptation, a painless thing that hollows you out, multiplies, and seduces even as it scorches, ensnaring everyone until the narrator resolves to confront it head-on and finish the fight. Polish thrash veterans Acid Drinkers render the compulsion as a scorched, churning battle waged with grit and dark humor.","sonnet":"An addictive substance — sweet, burning, reproductive as vermin, building sticky traps — has taken over completely. The speaker is made a liar by it, devoured by desire, and the reckoning involves purging and proving something to themselves. By the end they are licking their wounds and calling themselves happy for having survived the whole thing.","src":"haiku","fable":"The sin here is craving personified as an intruder that slips in painlessly, then colonizes: dissolving the insides, breeding like vermin, candy on the tongue yet burning like the sun, a web that snares everyone within reach. Midway the grammar pivots from what it does to the speaker toward what he swears to do, dressing capitulation up as a chore of willpower he vows to see through to its finish. The tell is that the hunger arrives in vivid sensory detail while the resistance comes only in borrowed slogans of grit — so the final tableau, one leftover creature slumped where the corridor dead-ends, tending its hurts and pronouncing the job done, sounds less like a conqueror than an addict rehearsing the applause he gives himself each time the thing beats him again.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The last scene reads two ways at once: the den cleaned and the mettle proven, or the relapse-glow of a half-man licking wounds and calling it done. The sensory asymmetry — craving vivid, resistance in borrowed slogans — leans toward the second.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"conqueror's verdict sounds like an addict giving himself applause for losing again","built":"craving arrives in vivid sensory detail while resistance comes only in borrowed slogans"},
"acid-drinkers~enjoy-your-death": {"haiku":"Reckless courting of danger and seduction by risk itself, dancing with mortality as entertainment and ultimate freedom.","opus":"A Polish thrash portrait of a reckless daredevil who courts death like a game, riding the razor's edge for the thrill until the vultures gather. Adrenaline addiction pushed to its logical, morbid conclusion.","sonnet":"A portrait of a man who made death his riding companion — driving wasted, pinching fate in the ass, diving headfirst into crypts just to feel the shiver. Vultures appear when blood hits the ground; the law of adrenaline is its own religion, and the mantra is enjoy your death.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of the speed-addict who flirts with his own extinction like a groom teasing an unwilling bride, and the song's real cruelty is that it stops admiring him halfway through. Acid Drinkers build a swaggering wreck who drives drunk and gropes at mortality for laughs, then let the fun rot into something medieval and reeking, a charnel stench that turns the joke sour. The verdict comes plainly: he loved the thrill but would never surrender the life itself, a tightwad of his own blood, brave only because he never meant to pay. Widening at the end into a rule of the wild, that pumping terror and spilled blood always draw the carrion birds, the jeering refrain reads less like a taunt than a benediction spoken over meat that hasn't understood it is already meat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"brave only because he never meant to pay his own blood","built":"admiration curdles midway into charnel stench, the pivot stranding the swagger mid-boast"},
"acid-drinkers~fill-me-in-100": {"haiku":"Desperate hunger for consumption and annihilation through excess and vulnerability.","opus":"A song using grotesque imagery of being drowned, force-fed, and swollen to the point of explosion as a masochistic plea to be filled completely with another's wrath, despair, and tears — offering up soul, heart, and body to be taken.","sonnet":"The speaker offers their entire self — soul, heart, body — to be saturated with someone else's despair, anger, and tears, framing extreme emotional and physical submission as a kind of ecstatic fulfillment rather than destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song of total, deliberate self-surrender that keeps daring its object to go further — drown me, feed me, poison me — until submission itself becomes a form of appetite. The genius is that the swelling, distended imagery frames filling not as nourishment but as engorgement toward rupture: every act of being given something ends in detonation, vertigo, or slow poisoning, so the speaker isn't begging to be satisfied but to be forced past the breaking point. The refrain reframes it as a bargain — the heart and body are surrendered as objects one can seize, while the soul is the single thing offered freely, and that is where the true hunger sits. Read straight it's the swagger of Acid Drinkers' beer-soaked metal machismo, a lover courting extremity; read against the grain the diesel-engine and TNT similes strip the addict's logic bare, a plea to be topped up with fuel and drink until the tank finally ruptures.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"submission becomes appetite, begging to be forced past the breaking point","built":"diesel-engine and TNT similes strip the addict's logic bare beneath the lover's plea"},
"acid-drinkers~fuel-of-my-soul": {"haiku":"Alcohol and transgression as life force; indulgence and excess fueling existence through the night.","opus":"A raucous celebration of drinking and revelry, summoning drunkards and poets, calling for booze and hamburgers, kicking out the abstainers, and fueling the soul through a hundred sleepless nights of indulgence.","sonnet":"The speaker assembles an eclectic, rowdy gathering — poets, drunks, jailbirds, a dancing queen — and declares alcohol and raucous company the fuel that fills the emptiness and keeps him going through long sleepless nights.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drinking spree costumed as a coronation banquet: the speaker seats a poet and a paroled man in the place of honor beside him, torches a ten-dollar bill just to spark a smoke, and orders the sober thrown into the street while a sheikh is pressed to keep the drinks flowing. The blessing pronounced over a dead man's orphans keeps circling back, a liturgical tic that dresses gluttony as sacrament. Yet the chorus betrays the whole pageant: a hundred wakeful nights feeding a bottomless pit that swallows every drink and stays hollow, the liquor mistaken for a fuel that only runs because nothing warmer burns inside. Sinatra spins, the crown gleams, and the appetite rolls onward toward no destination, so it simply resets and rolls again.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"liquor mistaken for fuel that runs only because nothing warmer burns inside","built":"coronation liturgy frames gluttony as sacrament until the hollow chorus undercuts it"},
"acid-drinkers~gain-on-shit": {"haiku":"The speaker rejects synthetic commodities and false promises of love and happiness sold by society, refusing to become an ideal by pursuing hollow gains.","opus":"Polish thrash rejecting a hollow consumer bargain, refusing the synthetic love and poisonous lies being sold as truth, and throwing off the pressure to become some ideal man for someone else's profit.","sonnet":"A rejection of whatever synthetic or false thing is being sold — love, truth, ideals — pointing out that pursuing it only yields garbage.","src":"haiku","fable":"A blunt consumer refusal: the speaker throws back everything a salesman-culture is pushing — love, truth, the promise of a fuller life — and calls it manufactured, a synthetic substitute for the real thing. Acid Drinkers, coming up in a Poland where the market economy arrived fast and loud after 1989, aim the sneer at the whole machinery of being told that buying in is living; the title's crude equation lands the verdict, that chasing what's on offer only compounds worthlessness rather than escaping it. The sharpest turn is the last demand, to stop being told he needs to become the ideal man — the pitch isn't selling a product so much as a self, and refusing the purchase is refusing to be remade.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"refusing the purchase is refusing to be remade into the ideal man","built":"crude title equation lands the verdict before the argument even begins"},
"acid-drinkers~god-hampered-his-life":{"src":"web","web":"A man who never meant to go wrong but spiralled — white powder, bourbon, every friend gone, the ten commandments rendered meaningless — a caustic Catholic-guilt portrait of how completely a life dissolves when faith, will and everyone around you abandon ship at once.","fable":"Acid Drinkers stage this as a mock-parable — the title casting God as an outside saboteur crippling one man's fortunes — but the joke is that the deity in question is really the character's own self-righteousness. No harm was ever intended, he tells himself, yet he stayed permanently certain he was in the right, and each verse tracks that conviction burning through the people around him, then the lovers he loses, then the cocaine, then the bottle, until his luck and every friend have evaporated. The Polish thrash irreverence lands in the surgical chorus, which recasts the entire religious apparatus as a clinical defect: when the old moral tablets deliver nothing, excise the god like a tumor and rewire your body from scratch. What plays as blasphemy is really a black comedy about siting your ruin anywhere but within — the man never once owns a fault or renews his belief, so pinning the wreckage on a meddling God stays easier than confessing he sabotaged himself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","shame & repression","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"ruin blamed outward while every fault stays interior","built":"the chorus reframes blasphemy as surgery, excising the god like a clinical defect"},
"acid-drinkers~hate-unlimited": {"haiku":"Systemic exploitation and control where those in power seduce and corrupt the masses through false rewards and empty promises.","opus":"A savage satire inviting the polite masses to get gassed, blast themselves and die picturesquely for fame and money on live television, the narrator putting his soul up for trade while demanding you dance to his tune. It is a bitter indictment of spectacle and manufactured cruelty. Acid Drinkers, the Polish thrash veterans, sharpen their sardonic English lyrics into this jeering commentary on how easily people are steered toward destruction.","sonnet":"A savage satire of media-ready self-destruction — the speaker invites the crowd to poison themselves, burn on live TV, die picturesquely for the audience. The currency is souls, the trade is obedience for spectacle, and underneath the black comedy is a portrait of a system that profits from people killing themselves on cue.","src":"haiku","fable":"A ringmaster in borrowed courtly manners auctions off human suffering as entertainment, Acid Drinkers letting their thrash-with-a-grin instinct stage cruelty until the crowd recognizes its own hunger reflected back. Each proposed horror — swallowing venom, burning under studio lights, being strung up by a jeering mob — arrives priced as celebrity and reward, so the title's loathing reads less as fury than as a commodity moved by the crate. The pivot lands when the pitch curdles into coercion: bartering away conscience, then insisting the mark move in step and parrot the answers already scripted for him. Broadcast bloodlust shrinks to the bare gears of manipulation, the joke's edge being that showmanship, tyranny and obedience all draw on one manufactured supply.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"broadcast bloodlust shrinks to the bare gears of manufactured manipulation","built":"suffering auctioned as celebrity reward until the pitch curdles into scripted coercion"},
"acid-drinkers~hiperenigmatic-stuff-of-mr-nothing": {"haiku":"The speaker mocks and insults someone arrogant and fake, questioning their worth and integrity through crude, contemptuous imagery.","opus":"A crude, jeering takedown of a puffed-up nobody, the band mocking a proud fraud who fucks over everyone and threatening to deflate him, all delivered as gleeful Polish thrash insult.","sonnet":"A crude insult track aimed at a self-important fraud — someone delusional enough to think they're the best while actually being a ridiculous, self-defeating nobody.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tirade aimed at a puffed-up nobody, where the grandiose title carries the whole joke: whatever profound mystery this Mr. Nothing peddles gets rechristened as hyper-enigmatic and, by that same billing, worth precisely zero. The verses open with mock-earnest inquiry into his heart and swollen soul, then detonate into a barking refrain that halts everything and demands the nonsense cease, staging the speaker's rising revulsion as the entire architecture. In Acid Drinkers' vein of crude English clowning, the escalating schoolyard filth is the payload rather than any genuine mark, the gag being that so much venom gets lavished on a man who amounts to nobody.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"inflated title bills a nobody at maximum worthlessness","built":"the grandiloquent heading is the punchline the verses unwind"},
"acid-drinkers~home-submarine": {"haiku":"Isolation and retreat into self-contained fantasy world beneath the ocean; escape from reality into television and alcohol.","opus":"A wry ode to total withdrawal, the narrator holed up in a fort with a thousand bottles of wine and a damn TV, dreaming of taking an underwater boat down to the ocean floor to escape the world entirely. It is isolation played for comedy and comfort. Acid Drinkers, Poland's thrash pranksters, wrap their heaviness around this hermit's fantasy, the submarine home standing in for a cheerful refusal to face the surface.","sonnet":"Voluntary withdrawal from the world — the speaker retreats into a fortified domestic capsule, drinks wine with the television, and descends to the ocean floor with no intention of surfacing anytime soon. It's not tragic, just comfortable: the submarine home is exactly where they want to be.","src":"haiku","fable":"A shut-in dresses his drinking up as a diving expedition, turning a curtained room stocked with a thousand bottles into a pressurized hull that sinks by choice. The bravado is the whole trick: retreat gets narrated as daring descent, with a flickering television standing in for a crewmate and the muck at the bottom reframed as a destination worth reaching. Yet the boast springs a leak when he admits he is only marking hours until he can surface again, conceding that the depths he brags about are a sludge he already plans to crawl free of. What sounds like an adventurer's log is a man talking himself out of a hole he dug on purpose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance"],"means":"retreat narrated as heroic descent to chosen depths","built":"the bravado leaks the moment he admits he plans to surface"},
"acid-drinkers~human-bazooka": {"haiku":"A reckless force of nature (loaded, muscular, blind) demolishes everything in its path like a tornado, leaving its brain behind while pent-up energy explodes without direction.","opus":"The Polish thrashers sketch a blundering brute loaded like a warhead, muscled and blind, demolishing everything without knowing his front from his back. Comparisons to a rhino, a TGV, and a tornado pile up while his brain sits safely in a closet beside the underpants. It is cartoonish destruction delivered with the band's signature absurdist glee, mindless force celebrated as its own ridiculous spectacle.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone with enormous untargeted energy — all force, no aim, brain deliberately left behind — who charges out the door like a natural disaster, precise only in their indiscriminate destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"Acid Drinkers build a comic portrait of force with no aim: a man loaded to bursting who can flatten anything but has no idea which direction to point himself. The band stacks the whole thing out of ballistic and animal machinery — a V1 buzz-bomb, a tornado, a bullet-train's pent-up momentum, a charging rhino — every one of them fast, blind, and incapable of choosing a target. The gag lands in the closet: his brain has been left at home on a shelf with the underpants, filed away like small change in a penny-pincher's purse, so the demolition that follows is pure berserk energy severed from any judgment. It reads as a knowing send-up of the meathead ideal, the muscle-bound wrecking machine celebrated precisely for having emptied its own head.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","comedy & wordplay","identity & becoming"],"means":"unstoppable force celebrated for leaving its own brain on the shelf","built":"the ballistic catalogue empties once the brain is filed away at home"},
"acid-drinkers~i-am-the-mystic": {"haiku":"A persona lists a series of contradictory character traits and behaviors, claiming mystique and dangerous unpredictability while threatening violence.","opus":"Acid Drinkers deliver a boastful monologue from a self-styled degenerate who brags of coarseness, seduction, drink and violence with gleeful excess. The mystic here is a lowlife hero, half joke and half threat. Rooted in the Polish thrash outfit's tongue-in-cheek bravado, the swagger punctures its own grandiosity, turning macho posturing into deliberately ridiculous caricature.","sonnet":"A swaggering, self-appointed antihero rattles off a litany of contradictions that define him: spy, seducer, predator, cheap pornographic icon. The boasts accumulate into a portrait of someone who wears outrageousness as armour, crowning it all with a single cryptic act of violence against authority.","src":"haiku","fable":"A braggart's self-portrait built entirely from I-am declarations, each one a swaggering claim that the speaker is coarser, seedier, and more dangerous than the last. The comedy is that the boasts keep contradicting their own scale: he lumps being the loudest drunk at the office party in with crushing his victims like a rattlesnake, so the outlaw menace and the pub-crawl grandiosity flatten into the same tone of empty bravado. The grand title mystic is the punchline the whole list undercuts — there is nothing esoteric here, just a small-time poser inflating himself, and the closing hand-off, where a second voice announces he is dropping out, pricks the balloon: the self-mythologizing hard man exits the moment his cop-killer fantasy is voiced aloud. Acid Drinkers stage this as macho posturing performed until it collapses under its own absurdity.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay","identity & becoming"],"means":"the grand title undercut by a ledger of pub-crawl grandiosity","built":"a second voice drops the curtain the moment the cop-killer line lands"},
"acid-drinkers~i-f-the-violence-and-i-m-sure-i-m-right": {"haiku":"Witnessing street violence and decay, grappling with the fear of darkness spreading through society.","opus":"A blunt Polish thrash reaction to street brutality, cataloguing the fear of blood and beatings until the only verdict left is total refusal of the violence. Righteous disgust stated plainly.","sonnet":"An extremely short, direct protest against street violence — the narrator witnesses bloodshed and fear in their community and refuses to accept it. The bleeped profanity in the title is itself the protest: violence is obscene and should be treated as such.","src":"haiku","fable":"Acid Drinkers build an anti-violence tirade as pure cross-examination, refusing any safe narrative remove and instead cornering the listener with a run of did-you-see and do-you-feel demands. The imagery stays deliberately street-level — brawls in the open, a comrade crumpled on the pavement, red smeared across the fronts of houses — so the dread reads as logged testimony rather than atmosphere, evidence the singer insists you have witnessed too. The blunt vulgar title lands as the only verdict left after that accumulation, a Polish thrash band answering brutality in brutality's own gutter tongue, its swaggering certainty less a reasoned case than the reflex of someone who has decided he has seen enough.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"logged street testimony turned into direct cross-examination of the listener","built":"the vulgar title delivers the only verdict left after the evidence piles up"},
"acid-drinkers~i-mean-acid-doya-like-it": {"haiku":"Denial of addiction masking hedonistic appetite and moral deterioration. The narrator claims acidic numbness as freedom while witnessing his own corruption with indifference.","opus":"A gleefully unrepentant confession from a self-styled degenerate who owns every vice without shame or apology. The provocation is the point, daring listeners to condemn a cheap thrill he refuses to feel any guilt over.","sonnet":"A rambling, half-proud self-portrait of excess: near-death encounters with drink, a too-drunk-to-perform night, frank amorality, and a defiant defence of cheap acid as the substance of choice — \"it's cheap and it kicks so well.\"","src":"haiku","fable":"A defiant self-portrait built on a rigged ledger: the charge of drunkard is denied outright — a wink nobody could miss coming from a band with this name — while brat, wretch and motherfucker are granted freely, as if to strip his accusers of the pleasure of the charge. The confession is a feint. Each true that yields ground pivots into its own defense, arriving at the real thesis in the final refrain, where the demand shifts from person to substance and the fault is relocated from the self onto the drug, sold as a bargain that delivers exactly the jolt it promises. Acid Drinkers, whose very name stakes out this territory, run the Polish thrash gambit of hijacking Anglo rock swagger, and the mangled English (the self-censored obscenities, the pidgin grammar) reads less as failed translation than as a mask worn on purpose, a working-class provocateur daring the listener to be scandalized. The title's taunt closes the trick: the whole confession was only ever bait for that grinning do you like it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The Bloody Titus being mixed mid-song is the band's own in-joke — a cocktail named for their frontman, drunk inside a lyric that denies he's a drinker.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"every yielded charge pivots to relocate blame onto the drug itself","built":"pidgin grammar and self-censored obscenities worn as a deliberate mask"},
"acid-drinkers~in-a-black-sail-wrapped": {"haiku":"Longing, obsession, and dark love shroud the speaker as they pursue a distant, elusive figure. The black sail symbolizes doom and melancholy; every year brings only darkness as a gift, yet the hunt continues unabated.","opus":"A gothic vow of devotion to darkness, gifting a loved one shadow instead of sunlight and promising to find them again wrapped in a black sail. The Polish thrashers play it as grim romance, loyalty pledged past death.","sonnet":"A haunted obsession with someone gone — the narrator keeps turning their head and finding the black sail creeping back, a death-marked presence that won't release them, with the promise of tracking them down again wherever they've fled.","src":"haiku","fable":"A poem of grief wearing a pirate's costume, the way this Poznan crossover band habitually smuggle real feeling inside genre swagger. The black sail is the Theseus signal read backwards: the death-omen returns every time the speaker turns his head, so the song is built as a body that cannot stop checking over its shoulder, the refrain literalizing that reflex. The east/west split — senses strained toward one horizon while registering the opposite — draws a compass over a person no longer where they should be, and the yearly gift of darkness on a birthday reframes mourning as a private ritual, a refusal of the sunlight the living are supposed to want. The vow to find the lost one again and bring them back wrapped in that same black sail turns the omen inward: reunion is only imaginable as shared death, the sweetness of a bitter road being the addict's or the bereaved's insistence that the loss was worth its own pain. What looks like nautical posturing is a man rowing toward an island of the dead, straining a single arm against an oar the size of a pinion he was never given.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","nostalgia & memory","artifice & performance"],"means":"reunion imaginable only as shared death wrapped in the omen's own cloth","built":"the Theseus signal loops as a body compelled to check over its shoulder"},
"acid-drinkers~infernal-connection": {"haiku":"The narrator recognizes a genetic or spiritual kinship with another—a poisoned bloodline linking them to darkness and venom.","opus":"Polish thrash boiled down to recognition of evil in someone familiar: the same blood, the same smile, now understood as rotten to the root. It's the sick jolt of realizing a person you know is the worst kind.","sonnet":"About recognizing inherited evil or a family line of darkness — the narrator sees in someone else the same smell, the same smile, the same patterns of venom and cruelty, tracing a lineage of malice they can see all the way to its roots.","src":"haiku","fable":"Beneath the cartoon savagery these Poznan thrashers trade in, the horror here is recognition: the speaker keeps meeting a wickedness that arrives pre-familiar, its scent and its grin already logged somewhere older than memory. Framed as descent through shared marrow and matching veins, the monster turns out to be kin rather than intruder, the poison sourced in a lineage the speaker can trace precisely because it runs through him too. Each repeated instance behind an unmoving grey mask converts private dread into something procedural, an assembly line of the same corruption; the shouted refrain then names what neither confession nor disgust can sever, a tie binding the accuser to everything he damns.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Blood from blood cuts both directions: the traced villain shares a lineage, and the tracer is standing in it.","themes":["family & blood","shame & repression","violence & murder"],"means":"the monster recognized as kin because its poison runs through the speaker too","built":"descent through shared marrow converts private dread into procedural assembly"},
"acid-drinkers~iq-cyco": {"haiku":"A violent, chaotic confrontation where the narrator finds a gun and pursues a target called IQ CYCO; fragmented imagery of aggression, sleeping bodies, and aftermath suggest a dark chase through the city.","opus":"A garbled, adrenaline-soaked hunt for a figure called IQ Cyco, more raw menace and gun-toting swagger than coherent narrative. The point is the manic aggression rather than any story.","sonnet":"A fragmented pursuit through a city in search of something called IQ CYCO, mixing images of a gun, a sleeping or passive crowd, and chaotic street energy. The lyrics are disjointed and resist a single clear narrative beyond a sense of manic, aimless aggression.","src":"haiku","fable":"A manhunt narrated from inside the disorder it claims to be pursuing, where the target IQ CYCO is not a rival stalked through the city but the condition wearing the narrator's own skin. He loads a weapon and swears a partner to the mission, yet every prop he reaches for — the freshly bought firearm, the grinning menace, the swaggered promise that yet another victim goes down — is stock hardware borrowed from pop-metal's threat display, so the pursuer keeps describing himself. The dozing skull, slack hands, and shut-off spirit are the giveaway: the killing runs unattended, a reflex executed while conscience sleeps, and the chase folds back into a mirror. In this early-90s Polish thrash stitched from half-learned English, the fractured grammar carries the meaning, since a mind that cannot complete its clauses is exactly the diminished intelligence the title names.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"the manhunt folds back when the pursuer keeps describing himself","built":"fractured grammar enacts the diminished intelligence the title names"},
"acid-drinkers~justify-me-i-was-so-hungry": {"haiku":"Hunger and emptiness justify theft; necessity overwrites morality. The speaker and accomplice steal unnoticed, defended by darkness and their mutual complicity.","opus":"A guilty confession dressed as a plea for absolution, the speaker reaching for what wasn't his because emptiness kills the sense of right and wrong. The refrain begging to be justified turns a petty theft into a study of how need overrides conscience, complicity shared with the one he begs to set him free.","sonnet":"A confession of petty theft or trespass driven by emptiness — \"when you're empty inside you don't think if it's wrong or right\" — ending in a plea to a \"sweet lady\" for forgiveness and release, with the admission that they knew it was wrong but went for it anyway because it was simply there.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession that keeps escaping its own remorse: the speaker admits reaching for something not his, then buries the admission under a running excuse — hunger left him hollow, the thing was just lying there, and nobody caught them. The parenthetical reframing appetite as alibi is the whole trick, so the closing demand is not penance but a request that a partner hand him the verdict he refuses to reach on his own. That partner, addressed with sudden tenderness, is dragged into complicity by the taunting refrain about what she already understands and where she has already stood beside him, recast as accomplice rather than judge. The looping repetition finally exposes a conscience that has subcontracted its ruling, craving release without the work of being genuinely sorry.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","money & the street"],"means":"conscience subcontracted to a partner who is cast as accomplice not judge","built":"the looping repetition rehearses remorse that never completes its own ruling"},
"acid-drinkers~l-o-v-e-machine": {"haiku":"A woman depicted as dangerous, possessive machine consumes men through carnal hunger and madness; her dirty mind and burning brain drive sexual excess that kills or humiliates partners within her grasp.","opus":"Polish thrash cranking desire into horror-movie excess, framing lust as a devouring, emasculating force the narrator both craves and needs to flee. It plays the femme fatale trope for grotesque comedy, sex as literal death.","sonnet":"A grim, almost horror-inflected portrait of a sexually voracious woman who devours men — the narrator watches others fall victim to her, finding her repulsive but unable to look away.","src":"haiku","fable":"The Miracles' 1975 come-on, where a suitor renders every flicker of arousal as gauges twitching and circuits warming, arrives here scrubbed of Motown gloss and hollered by a Polish thrash outfit who plainly relish the absurdity of the borrowed English. Their delight sharpens the source's buried joke: the wooing is pure sales patter, an appliance boasting of its own obedience — easy to operate, impossible to disappoint, endlessly available at the flip of a switch. Behind the boast of readiness there is nobody at all, only a device advertising that it exists to be handled, and the band's grinning brute-force delivery treats that vacancy not as pathos but as the funniest part of the pitch.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","love & desire","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"suitor renders himself as appliance advertising its own vacancy","built":"brute-force thrash delivery treats the pitch's hollowness as the funniest part"},
"acid-drinkers~let-em-bleed": {"haiku":"The song condemns fraudsters, corrupt authority, and the masses who enable them by worshiping demagogues.","opus":"A furious rant against duplicitous frauds in dark suits, swindling clergy and rabble-rousers exalted as idols by a thoughtless crowd. The chant to bleed them out calls for a violent reckoning with the sleazy profiteers who feast on their credulous flock.","sonnet":"A snarling dismissal of political fraudsters and self-important loudmouths who build themselves up on lies and the admiration of a mindless crowd — the narrator wants to watch them drain and bleed out through their own arrogance.","src":"haiku","fable":"A jeering takedown of the well-dressed frauds who trade on borrowed authority — dark tailoring over hollow heads, a fluency that mistakes glibness for genuine conviction and never survives a second look. The rallying cry flips their own logic back on them: parasites who fatten on a crowd should be bled by that crowd, a mock-solemn parade of engineered fury that empties the emptiers in a rite the band conducts like grim liturgy. Its widest swing reaches past individual scorn toward a jaundiced verdict on the era, which elevates the deranged to worship and the swindler to idol, where cash disguised as therapy quietly buys the outcome while clergy dwindle, ignorance reads without learning, and cleverness thins out. Acid Drinkers hold their trademark rough-English thrash bark, making the obscenity the whole thrust rather than decoration, a gutter tongue trained on those who confuse gloss with substance and claiming it perceives them more truly than any couch-side charlatan.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"parasites bled by the crowd in a rite the band conducts as grim liturgy","built":"gutter-tongue obscenity aimed at those who confuse gloss with substance"},
"acid-drinkers~life-hurts-more-than-death": {"haiku":"Catalogue of suicides and murders linked to unbearable suffering; death becomes relief when life's pain exceeds survival.","opus":"A parade of grim deaths, a man found at the local hanging spot, a slight woman folding herself into an oven, a woman gunning down her lover, each capped by the narrator's despairing lament that relief is forever out of reach. Regret at showing up too late runs underneath. Poland's thrash veterans Acid Drinkers fuse gallows humor with real grief here.","sonnet":"Three cameos of people who chose to die — a man cut down from a rope too late, a woman who crept into her own oven, a victim shot in the face — each described with flat, almost tender matter-of-factness. The speaker was always just arriving too late or too few, and what's left is not mourning but a cold arithmetic: the next year will never come.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor's catalogue of suicides and killings, narrated by a man forever one beat behind — cutting the hanged body loose after the fact, reaching the gassed girl once the receiver has gone dead, misreading the woman who fires into her lover's face. The refrain about a calendar that runs out reads less as a threat than as his own foreclosed horizon, the promise of tomorrows quietly retracted for whoever is left standing among the corpses. Each vignette hands him a helpless retrospective conditional, a wish he had arrived while there was still someone to save, so the true subject is the futility of hindsight rather than the deaths themselves. The near-comic bluntness — the petite woman folding herself neatly into the oven — is a mask over grief; underneath sits a man for whom every loss is a telephone left ringing into an empty room.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","suicide","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"helpless retrospective conditionals mark the man who always arrives too late","built":"near-comic bluntness masks grief that reads each loss as a phone ringing unanswered"},
"acid-drinkers~love-shack": {"haiku":"Hedonistic celebration of a funky place where people gather to dance, kiss, and escape into collective sensual enjoyment.","opus":"Acid Drinkers hijack the B-52s party staple, bellowing the road trip to a rundown hideaway where lovers meet up. The oversized cars, jukebox cash, glitter, and rusted-roof gag all survive the thrash makeover. The Polish band, known for putting pop and rock standards through a metal grinder, turn the giddy dance-floor invitation into a muscular, rowdy celebration of the same escape.","sonnet":"A jubilant invitation to pile into a car and head to a ramshackle gathering place where dancing and physical abandon rule. The Love Shack is pure communal pleasure — funky, overflowing, stripped of any pretension.","src":"haiku","fable":"A faithful cover of the B-52's 1989 party anthem, so the words stay put: a whale-sized car of friends barreling down a Georgia highway toward a ramshackle roadside hideaway where love rules and the whole building physically quakes with dancing. Meaning here lives less in the lyric than in the gesture — Acid Drinkers, a Polish thrash band, park this on Fishdick Zwei, their record of metal reworkings of pop hits. A crew built on aggression bear-hugging the campiest good-time song imaginable becomes affectionate proof that raucous euphoria and heavy volume are one appetite wearing different clothes, the corroded-metal punchline landing as pure joyful nonsense.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"aggression and campiness meet as one appetite in different clothes","built":"metal band bear-hugging the campiest good-time song becomes the punchline"},
"acid-drinkers~love-shack-live":{"haiku":"A roadside shack where anything goes—pure hedonism, no judgment, just bodies and music and release.","opus":"A rowdy metal cover of the B-52s party anthem, the campy joy of a funky little getaway shack intact but roughed up. Pure celebration of hedonism where love rules and everybody moves.","sonnet":"A celebration of a ramshackle party destination fifteen miles down the Atlanta highway where everyone piles in for dancing, kissing, and glitter on every surface — the Love Shack is 'a little old place where we can get together,' and the only rule is that love rules.","src":"haiku","fable":"A faithful cover of the B-52's 1989 party anthem, so the words stay put: a whale-sized car of friends barreling down a Georgia highway toward a ramshackle roadside hideaway where love rules and the whole building physically quakes with dancing. Meaning here lives less in the lyric than in the gesture — Acid Drinkers, a Polish thrash band, park this on Fishdick Zwei, their record of metal reworkings of pop hits. A crew built on aggression bear-hugging the campiest good-time song imaginable becomes affectionate proof that raucous euphoria and heavy volume are one appetite wearing different clothes, the corroded-metal punchline landing as pure joyful nonsense.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"aggression and campiness meet as one appetite in different clothes","built":"metal band bear-hugging the campiest good-time song becomes the punchline"},
"acid-drinkers~madman-s-joint": {"haiku":"Isolation and paranoid isolation consume the speaker as they drift through existence as undead, judging themselves in game rigged toward failure.","opus":"A plunge into insanity inside a squalid pit, the narrator sensing plots against him and rising each morning like a reanimated corpse in a rigged contest where God merely watches and the empty can never triumph.","sonnet":"About waking up every morning in a degraded, paranoid state — sleeping with dogs, sensing conspiracies that may or may not be real, and rising like a walking dead person to face a day ruled by a god who watches human suffering as entertainment. The narrator has stopped caring what anyone thinks of them and given up on the idea that anyone can win in this life.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person study of paranoid unraveling in which the title's derangement is neither monstrous nor glamorous but a grinding daily chore, the labor of a man who can no longer trust his own senses and suspects a plot he cannot confirm. What imprisons him is not any enemy but that doubt itself, the inability to certify whether the threat is imagined or genuine. The bleakest stroke is a private theology: he pictures a deity watching his collapse for amusement while he acts as both defendant and judge, sentencing himself over and over in a contest engineered so the already-emptied can never prevail. The refrain answers this despair with pure machinery, a scalded skull, a churning chest, a corpse hauling itself upright at daybreak, so the loop rehearses the paralysis it names and each identical dawn seals off any exit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness","faith & the occult"],"means":"doubt itself the prison when threat cannot be confirmed as real or imagined","built":"the refrain's bodily machinery rehearses the paralysis it names each dawn"},
"acid-drinkers~meltdown-of-sanctity": {"haiku":"Religious institutions and their false piety collapse under the weight of hypocrisy, as saints become sinners and moral authority crumbles into madness.","opus":"Poland's veteran thrash pranksters drop the humor for a snarling rejection of forced doctrine, treating prayer as empty and hate-filled devotion as a fit of frenzy. The narrator, claiming an untainted origin, defies a moral scheme where the holy turn wicked, watching virtue crumble into a madness worn without humility.","sonnet":"Moral collapse is the subject here — a figure who began with purity watches sanctity dissolve under the pressure of crowd logic and empty ritual. Prayer becomes hollow performance, saints reveal themselves as sinners, and the narrator refuses to surrender identity or life to that disintegration.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hardened refusal aimed at coercive faith: an authority that governs by decree alone has, under the banner of virtue, ripped the divine out of the speaker's chest, the charge being that enforced piety hollows out belief instead of nourishing it. The title's paradox drives everything, holiness dissolving into derangement, where prayer rings empty and the canonized reveal themselves as transgressors, so the harder innocence is policed the faster it rots into frenzy. Acid Drinkers pin the true rot not on the lone heretic but on the multitude that surrenders humility for dogma and drifts toward the path of least resistance, entering purity only to betray it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","madness & the mind"],"means":"enforced piety hollowing belief faster than heresy could","built":"the paradox title performs the collapse it diagnoses"},
"acid-drinkers~monkey-mosh": {"haiku":"A crude manifesto celebrates middle-finger defiance against mental chaos. Satiric aggression mocks stupidity and societal decay.","opus":"A rowdy raised-finger anthem aimed at posturing fools and a phony masked-villain sham, turning contempt into a pit ritual of shrieking apes who belong in psychiatric care.","sonnet":"A song mocking racist, mentally unhinged behavior — using a restaurant scene where a racist customer objects to a Black waiter, the narrator calls out the absurdity of such thinking with contempt, comparing these people to monkeys who belong in mental care rather than polite society.","src":"haiku","fable":"An obscene gesture dressed as a dance craze, this Polish groove-thrash romp recasts the pit as a menagerie for everyone the band has stopped tolerating: shrieking primates who belong under lock and key, not loose among the moshers. The clownish humor Acid Drinkers built their name on curdles into contempt through a restaurant tableau where a diner's snobbish order slides into an ugly racial slur, pinning the scorn onto preening bigots and their hangers-on. A grenade of a finale drops in a bloated procession led by some costume-shop villain, a hollow poser propped up long past his welcome. The joke holds because nothing is ever explained; the song just barks its come-on to step into the ring, so the party stomp itself lands as the jeer you are dared to bounce to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","comedy & wordplay","party & hedonism"],"means":"contempt delivered as a dare to bounce to its own jeer","built":"the party stomp format smuggles bigot-scorn as the thing it imitates"},
"acid-drinkers~more-life": {"haiku":"The speaker tests willpower and perseverance through degradation and filth, asking whether the listener can endure the descent and rise again. Survival means standing up after falling into sewers of despair.","opus":"A raw confrontation with hitting bottom and clawing back, imagery of sewers and prison framing the choice to survive. Its blunt wisdom is that falling isn't losing, staying down is, so you come back from the other side.","sonnet":"A blunt challenge to keep going even after being broken and washed through the sewer — directed at someone who has suffered real degradation — insisting that falling doesn't mean losing, and that refusing to get back up is the only real defeat.","src":"haiku","fable":"Built almost wholly from questions, the song makes interrogation itself the threat: it corners the listener with a survival exam nobody agreed to sit, and its offer of return from the grave reads as summons more than mercy. The imagery kills the pep talk dead — a corpse riding filthy runoff, waste and gore stirred together — so the coaxing refrain plays like a swindle, its promise pitched in the voice of a man moving cheap merchandise. The band's tell lands in a last inverted maxim: stumbling is not defeat, defeat is stumbling and staying down. It's a bruised, defiant creed that reframes the whole grinding catechism, insisting the only true loss is refusing to rise.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","anger & defiance"],"means":"survival coaxed in a huckster voice over a corpse in runoff","built":"interrogation as the structural threat, not a question but a summons"},
"acid-drinkers~my-soul-s-among-the-lions": {"haiku":"Soul inhabits dangerous wilderness; the speaker belongs among predators and threats.","opus":"A grim exchange between a woman trapped in some private hell of abuse and cruelty and a narrator who coldly tells her she's already lost. Damnation offered without rescue, the horror in the refusal to save.","sonnet":"A speaker interrogates a woman who has clearly suffered violence — her silent hell, the cry he heard, something that happened again in the night — but when she begs to be rescued, he tells her flatly: \"no way, you're lost my dear.\" The soul among lions is a state of predatory captivity that the narrator, despite seeing clearly, refuses or is unable to interrupt.","src":"haiku","fable":"An interrogation staged as a rescue that curdles into a verdict. The speaker plays detective across the first three verses, circling a woman with questions about the previous night, footprints, a cry he heard, a body he cannot see, until the accusatory pressure makes clear he is less concerned with saving her than with confirming her guilt. When she finally speaks, she is already past danger and into damnation, pleading to be pulled from the lions while asking him to save at least the rest of her, and his flat refusal is the song's real thesis: some falls are treated as final, and the man who came asking questions turns out to be there to pronounce her lost rather than to lift her out. The lions carry the biblical charge of the abandoned soul thrown to be devoured, so the horror is not the beasts but the human judgment that agrees they should keep her.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Her chorus lifts Psalm 57 nearly word for word — my soul is among lions — scripture's abandoned-soul plea put in the mouth of a woman the narrator refuses to save.","themes":["shame & repression","faith & the occult","violence & murder"],"means":"the rescuer arriving to pronounce her lost rather than lift her","built":"detective framing curdles into verdict when the questioned woman finally speaks"},
"acid-drinkers~n-i-b": {"haiku":"Lucifer seduction where the devil offers eternal love and power, disguised as ultimate romantic fulfillment.","opus":"A seduction sung in the voice of the devil, promising the sun, moon and stars and eternal love if you follow, only revealing at the last that his name is Lucifer and his hand is now offered. It is temptation as courtship. This is Black Sabbath's original, and Acid Drinkers cover it faithfully, the Polish thrash band paying homage to metal's founders while keeping the sly menace of that infernal proposal intact.","sonnet":"A seduction spoken by Lucifer himself — silken promises of eternal love, cosmic gifts, power without limit, the other person made first and forever. The catch only reveals itself at the midpoint: it is the Devil making the offer, and the hand being extended is his.","src":"haiku","fable":"A faithful cover of Black Sabbath's N.I.B., and its whole trick is a delayed reveal: for two verses it reads as an overpowering love song, a suitor promising the sun and moon and eternity to a beloved who doubts him. The devastating turn is that the seducer is Lucifer, and the courtship language of devotion, surrender and being taken under his power was infernal bargaining all along. What makes it more than a gimmick is that the seduction is genuinely tender before the name lands, so the song argues that damnation arrives wearing the face of love, offering exactly the total belonging a lonely heart wants most. Acid Drinkers, a Polish thrash band raised on this canon, play it straight on their covers record precisely because the source needs no ironizing; the joke is that the Prince of Lies is the best romantic in the room.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"damnation arriving in the face of total devoted belonging","built":"the reveal withheld until after the seduction reads as genuinely tender"},
"acid-drinkers~not-by-it-s-cover": {"haiku":"Cover-up and institutional deception fueling suspicion and paranoia, confronting layers of fabrication and erosion of truth.","opus":"A terse thrash jab at institutional cover-ups, the chanted whitewash and stonewall standing in for every leaked scandal buried under official denial. Its title warns against trusting the polished surface of power.","sonnet":"Cover-ups, stonewalls, and suspicious revelations — deep dark secrets wrapped in fabrication, new leaks emerging despite every effort to whitewash. The lyric is short and circular, the repetition of 'whitewash, stonewall' functioning as its own indictment.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about official cover-ups, built as a standoff between a curious verse and a stonewalling chorus. The verses keep leaning in — leaked revelations, mounting suspicion, a figure who looks guiltier by the line — while the chorus answers only with two words for concealment, whitewash and stonewall, hammered in pairs. That mechanical repetition is the point: the refrain doesn't just describe institutional obstruction, it performs it, meeting every fresh question with the same blank wall. The title's twist on judging a book by its cover names the whole gambit — the surface story is the lie, and the mystery deepens precisely because someone keeps painting over it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"institutional obstruction performed by the chorus that performs it","built":"two concealment words hammered in pairs answer every fresh verse question"},
"acid-drinkers~oh-no-bruno": {"haiku":"Excess and indulgence drive a character through nightlife, partying, and reckless living. One spree ends with legal trouble when the consequences catch up.","opus":"Polish thrash veterans in party-animal mode, a rowdy anthem of hedonistic excess where too much is never enough until the law comes knocking; comic self-parody of a life lived entirely at full throttle.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone named Bruno who is constitutionally incapable of restraint — always too much, always out all night, always in excess — until the cops finally come knocking and the bill comes due.","src":"haiku","fable":"Borrowed from Canadian art-punks NoMeansNo, this is a portrait of excess as an engine that can only run one direction. The song's whole grammar is the phrase that opens nearly every line — too much — hammered onto a rotating menu of appetites (all-nighters, champagne, gambling, one more go), so that the words stop describing behavior and become the metronome the behavior marches to. The pivot is the refrain that too much is not enough: the point of overindulgence was never satisfaction but the momentum itself, the compulsion to top the last hit. Then the structure turns the screw — the same too much that was buying pleasure starts buying consequences, the cops at the door and no not again, revealing that there was never an exit, only more. The closing auction-patter of climbing numbers ending in a snarled command lands it as a gambler's spiral or a dealer's tab that pays out in nothing but the demand to keep going.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism"],"means":"too much as the metronome the behavior marches to not a measure of it","built":"the phrase too much is not enough pivots pleasure momentum into consequence spiral"},
"acid-drinkers~old-sparky": {"haiku":"Death row anticipation where the narrator journeys toward execution by electric chair, morbidly respectful of the device awaiting them.","opus":"A grimly ironic ode to the electric chair, addressing Old Sparky as a warm-embracing old friend who turned countless named men and women to dust, the condemned narrator standing scared at its door as he burns like a torch at hell's gate. Acid Drinkers, the Polish thrash-metal veterans, wring dark, sardonic drama from execution imagery, their gallows humor sharpening the horror rather than softening it.","sonnet":"A pilgrimage to an electric chair — Old Sparky — framed with dark affection for a machine that consumed countless lives including named individuals turned to dust. The speaker approaches with both dread and reverence, burning red, knocking at the door of something that functions here as a stand-in for death itself met on its own terms.","src":"haiku","fable":"Old Sparky is death-row slang for the electric chair, and the conceit here is to court that oak-and-leather apparatus as a beloved elder, a confidant worth two thousand miles of travel and a high five on arrival. The tenderness is the trap: an embrace praised as protective and comradely is the surge that leaves a body charred, and the litany of first names — a Ted, a Virginia, a Martha, a James, all cooked to ash — quietly confesses that this warmth is a body count. When the voice slips inside the strapped-in man, glowing like a struck match and rapping on the underworld's gate, the flattery curdles: the trusted rock is an execution engine, admired even as it reduces the admirer to smoke.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"the electric chair courted as a trusted elder who is also a body count","built":"flattery language borrowed from friendship applied to an execution apparatus"},
"acid-drinkers~private-eco": {"haiku":"A person rejects information overload and exploitation, refusing to become a dumping ground for others' garbage.","opus":"A thrash rant against media saturation, the narrator refusing to be a trash can for the endless lava of news and multimedia junk pouring down his street and smothering his private inner world.","sonnet":"An assault of unwanted media — news, radio, TV, all pumping information the narrator never asked for, flooding their private mental space like lava and demanding they serve as a receptacle for other people's noise.","src":"haiku","fable":"A furious refusal of media saturation cast as an ecological disaster of the mind, where a title promising some pristine personal preserve is drowned under the same molten broadcast tide that keeps advancing no matter how loudly the speaker objects. The self is imagined as a walled interior estate that the surrounding volcanic wash overruns and buries, its owner left standing in a rising slurry of headlines and signal he never asked to receive. What sharpens it past mere complaint is the second-person accusation aimed straight at the set and the dial: television and radio are figured as vandals emptying their filth into him, recasting the body as a drain and the man as a bin for another's discard. That the closing flood returns identical to the opening is the whole grim point, since the eruption resolves nothing and simply pours on, leaving refusal marooned in exactly the deluge it cannot dam.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"media flood overrunning the self imagined as a walled interior estate","built":"the closing deluge identical to the opening, refusal marooned unchanged inside it"},
"acid-drinkers~propaganda": {"haiku":"Media and society feed the speaker an endless diet of horror and suffering as entertainment. They consume violence, addiction, and death as if ordering from a menu.","opus":"A savage satire of media and audiences gorging on violence, told from the point of view of a consumer greedily ordering more blood, bones, screams, and horror as if from a menu; it's a critique of how spectacle-hungry culture treats atrocity and addiction as entertainment.","sonnet":"A savage satire of media consumption: the narrator mimics the insatiable audience demanding more violence, gore, addiction, and horror from the news cycle, framing the viewer as a starving man devouring atrocity entertainment.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title carries the whole argument: this is satire aimed at media appetite, its speaker not a monster so much as a slack-jawed consumer barking orders at a screen. Atrocity gets served like fast food off a laminated card of perfect murders, so that carnage, cocaine, and rotting flesh all arrive to spec, and the junkie and the spectator collapse into one drooling figure. The pivot lands when supply changes hands: he demands throughout, yet by the closing stretch the machine floods him around the clock and he gulps it down as if famished, meaning the industry that manufactured the hunger has now taken it over, no request required. That final escalation, spiralling past any actual object, exposes the true engine of spectacle: a craving deliberately built without a ceiling, which leaves the piece nowhere to go but bark its order until language dissolves into din.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"the spectacle industry taking over a craving it deliberately built without ceiling","built":"supply changes hands mid-song: the consumer's orders become the machine's flood"},
"acid-drinkers~proud-mary": {"haiku":"A riverboat life offers freedom and community more fulfilling than city work and drudgery.","opus":"Poland's thrashers hijack Creedence's riverboat classic, following its narrator who quits a city job to drift down the water among generous, contented folk. The band swaps folk-rock warmth for metallic heft, its rolling refrain reframed as a defiant embrace of a life valued by freedom rather than wages.","sonnet":"A man leaves a grinding city job behind and finds freedom on the river, moving through the hard towns of the American South until the riverboat community shows him the good side of a life he never knew existed on dry land.","src":"haiku","fable":"Acid Drinkers take John Fogerty's 1969 escape hymn and keep its architecture intact. The verses tally a life spent servicing other people's needs in one hard city after another — scrubbing dishes, breaking your back for the man — while the chorus answers with a paddlewheel of pure forward motion the singer boards to stop drowning in what might have been. The song's genius is that it never dramatizes the escape; it simply moves the narrator from the ledger of labor to the river, where poverty stops being a threat because the people there give freely. The song turns on that pivot, work as accounting giving way to the river as grace. In a Polish thrash band's hands the choice is tribute rather than reinvention — the swampy American myth of quitting the grind and being taken in by strangers on the water crosses fully intact, borrowed whole.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","money & the street","nature & the elements"],"means":"quitting the ledger of labor for a river where strangers give freely","built":"the Fogerty architecture kept intact so the swampy American myth crosses whole"},
"acid-drinkers~red-shining-fur": {"haiku":"A person obsesses over possessing animal fur, comparing themselves to a trophy collector and threatening violence to acquire it; the song depicts predatory desire and theft.","opus":"A grotesque comic tale of skinning a cat for its fur, the band's crude humor pushed into deliberate bad taste. It's less a statement than a provocation, shock played for a grin rather than any real menace.","sonnet":"A darkly comedic narrator obsessively collects animal fur, scalping creatures with a razor to make coats, framing his naked hunger and need for warmth as a kind of primal scavenging ritual — simultaneously absurd and gleefully gruesome.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pulp-horror monologue that dresses a predator's appetite in the vocabulary of vanity and lets the two curdle into one impulse. The orange cat arrives as a stone-hearted femme fatale who drains her victims, but the speaker flips the roles by naming himself a collector, a self-styled Cherokee scalper who wants the creature rendered down to a warm red wrap he can knot like cashmere. The razor refrain is the tell: he insists that flaying an animal creates something, promising to remake it into a burned-out celebrity, so glamour gets manufactured straight out of butchery. By the closing verses the mask drops and he is simply cold and starving, killing to stay covered, and his sneer that feathers fell out of style two decades back reframes the ritual as fashion's offhand cruelty grinning back at itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance","money & the street"],"means":"glamour manufactured straight out of butchery","built":"the mask drops late, sneer becoming cold survival logic"},
"acid-drinkers~riot-in-eden": {"haiku":"Paradise corrupts from within as disorder blooms—chaos rises when control tightens, and paradise itself becomes a battleground between enforcement and freedom.","opus":"Paradise itself descends into revolt, with heavenly police clubbing rebels into submission. A tongue-in-cheek parable about authority turning any Eden into a hell.","sonnet":"A song imagining a rebellion breaking out in Heaven itself — paradise descends into chaos, divine police crack down on the uprising, and the whole idyllic structure of celestial peace is burned to ash by the same human drives for freedom that caused trouble on earth.","src":"haiku","fable":"Paradise here is a protest zone gone sour, and the song blames the crackdown rather than any original trespass: the garden rots the instant its uniformed enforcers meet unrest with truncheons and tear-smoke instead of mercy. Even the miracle runs backward, the good vintage soured to something undrinkable, a sign that the caretakers have poisoned what they swore to protect. Acid Drinkers, Polish thrash jokers steeped in pun and pastiche, pitch it half as a gag and half as a straight-faced swipe at authority. The punchline is that the collapse was an inside job, engineered by the very guardians who marched in claiming to tidy the wreckage.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","faith & the occult"],"means":"the collapse an inside job by its own guardians","built":"the miracle runs backward as evidence of poisoned caretaking"},
"acid-drinkers~she-s-gonna-be-a-porno-star": {"haiku":"A woman's ambition to become a sex worker for fame and wealth, indifferent to dignity or social connection.","opus":"A sneering caricature of a woman chasing fame and riches through porn, the narrator mockingly predicting golden toilets and too much stardom to bother with the likes of him, calling her a bitch while feigning pity. It is crude, satirical provocation. Acid Drinkers, the Polish thrash-metal veterans, revel in exactly this kind of tongue-in-cheek shock, delivering the taunt with rowdy, deliberately tasteless humor.","sonnet":"A blunt portrait of a woman with a single ambition and no interest in anyone else — she will be famous, she will be rich, she will sleep until noon, and she will not have time for the speaker or anyone like them. The tone is sardonic and finally pitying, ending on an apology that is really just resignation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A leering tabloid prophecy that pretends to predict a girl's rise to smut-stardom while really settling a personal score. The narrator's forecast of fame, money and a life too grand to waste on the likes of you is thin cover for the jilted spite underneath, and the whole boast finally curdles into a mock-condolence — the sorry, so sorry that isn't sorry at all — which exposes the whole thing as a rejected man consoling himself by imagining her degraded. In classic Acid Drinkers fashion the crudeness is the joke and the point: the swagger performs contempt loudly enough that you hear the wounded pride it's built to hide.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"mock-condolence exposing the wounded pride it was built to hide","built":"the prophecy of her rise curdles into consolation for his rejection"},
"acid-drinkers~silver-meat-machine": {"haiku":"A loyal, violent machine of steel and rust pulses with brutal energy; the operator won't replace it and drives it forward relentlessly.","opus":"A revved-up ode to a beloved, brutal machine (a motorcycle or engine) personified as a loyal, cruel, steel-bodied killer; the narrator vows never to replace it, reveling in its violent, roaring power for its own reckless sake.","sonnet":"A love letter to a battered, violent old machine — probably a car or motorbike — celebrated for its brutality, loyalty, and refusal to be replaced, treated with the same devotion one might give a weapon.","src":"haiku","fable":"A thrash-metal love song addressed to a machine, where the joke and the sincerity are the same thing. The Acid Drinkers pun sits under everything: the silver meat machine is a motorcycle or engine fused with its rider, oil and diesel described as if they were blood and appetite, so man and metal become one obese, snarling body that neither party wants to leave. The vow to never trade it for another and never look for lesser scum is genuine biker devotion voiced as marriage, the fidelity gag doubling as the actual feeling. What tips it from noise into a real read is the refrain that grades violence downward without apology — if the thing lands its blow it kills, and if it fails to kill it merely keeps running — treating lethality and idle both as proof the beast is alive and worth loving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","comedy & wordplay","violence & murder"],"means":"biker fidelity voiced as marriage, fidelity gag doubling as real feeling","built":"the vow never to trade it mirrors wedding language over engine noise"},
"acid-drinkers~slow-and-stoned":{"haiku":"Incomprehensible intimacy dissolving into drowning crisis. Late-night contact blurs into incoherence, the speaker unable to reach or save another sinking beneath incomprehensible weight.","opus":"A drunken, incoherent late-night phone call from someone reciting their own last will while sinking. The narrator, half-charmed and half-alarmed, keeps insisting the caller is drowning and needs to be put to bed before it's too late.","sonnet":"A late-night phone call from someone incomprehensibly intoxicated — their spasmodic breathing and incoherent \"last will\" frustrate the narrator who can only diagnose drowning and insist they go to bed.","src":"haiku","fable":"A one-sided phone call staged as helpless comedy: the speaker sits pinned to the receiver after midnight while a wheezing, half-conscious voice dictates its last testament and gargles out threats it will not survive the morning to recall. The mock-brutal imperatives read less as counsel than as the caller's own booze-logic parroted back, and the joke sours at the pivot, where amused irritation quietly clocks that it is watching a man go under. The named remedy is the punchline and the diagnosis at once, a bottom-shelf creed of drifting downward with the lights out, grinned at even as the closing turn refuses any rescue and lets party-band swagger perch on top of a small, real abandonment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Yonash reads as phonetic Polish for Jonah — which would make the method of the title the prophet's own: going under. The drowning refrain fits too well to be accidental, though nothing official confirms it.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","comedy & wordplay","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"party-band swagger perched on top of a small real abandonment","built":"amused irritation quietly clocks it is watching a man go under"},
"acid-drinkers~slow-and-stoned-method-of-yonash": {"haiku":"Communication breaks into incomprehensible noise; the speaker cannot reach the other, leaving them to drown in their own drowning.","opus":"A late-night call from someone barely breathing and drowning in their own stupor, the singer telling them to slow down and go to bed rather than read out their last will.","sonnet":"About receiving a late-night call from someone who is deeply intoxicated or possibly overdosing — they are breathing strangely, whispering incoherently, reading out what sounds like a last will while half-asleep. The narrator is unsettled, uncertain whether to call for help, and tells the person they are drowning and must go to bed — a paralyzed response to a potential crisis.","src":"haiku","fable":"A one-sided phone call staged as helpless comedy: the speaker sits pinned to the receiver after midnight while a wheezing, half-conscious voice dictates its last testament and gargles out threats it will not survive the morning to recall. The mock-brutal imperatives read less as counsel than as the caller's own booze-logic parroted back, and the joke sours at the pivot, where amused irritation quietly clocks that it is watching a man go under. The named remedy is the punchline and the diagnosis at once, a bottom-shelf creed of drifting downward with the lights out, grinned at even as the closing turn refuses any rescue and lets party-band swagger perch on top of a small, real abandonment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Yonash reads as phonetic Polish for Jonah — which would make the method of the title the prophet's own: going under. The drowning refrain fits too well to be accidental, though nothing official confirms it.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","comedy & wordplay","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"party-band swagger perched on top of a small real abandonment","built":"amused irritation quietly clocks it is watching a man go under"},
"acid-drinkers~stray-bullets": {"haiku":"A bullet's consciousness before impact; loaded and waiting in confinement, anticipating its violent release toward a target.","opus":"A darkly comic monologue narrated by a bullet itself, boasting from inside the clip and barrel, impatient to fire and fantasizing about burrowing into a victim's skull. The projectile's-eye view makes the coming violence both ridiculous and unnerving. Poland's thrash pranksters Acid Drinkers hand the murder weapon a swaggering, foul-mouthed voice.","sonnet":"A bullet's-eye view of its own trajectory — confined, purposeful, waiting in the dark chamber until it's fired and lodges in a skull. The perspective is weirdly intimate and satisfied, the bullet describing its own perfection and the completeness of the plan with something like pride.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is spoken by the bullet, and the trick is that it wants to be fired — it experiences the magazine as a cramped, humiliating waiting room and its own launch as release, even orgasm, with the barrel imagined as a body it fits perfectly and the kick it keeps craving standing in for a climax. That conceit lets Acid Drinkers fuse two of thrash's favorite engines, violence and sex, into one leering object: the weapon is horny for its own use, taunting the target as a prick who cannot know where or when he will be hit. The stray in the title is the punchline — this eager, boastful thing that devours a young heart is also aimless, so the swagger doubles as a portrait of purposeless killing that flatters itself into meaning. Read straight it is a gleefully tasteless metal cartoon; read against the grain it is the mask, and underneath is the banality of a projectile that has no idea and no care whom it destroys.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"the weapon's swagger doubles as a portrait of purposeless killing","built":"bullet-as-speaker fuses thrash violence and sex into one leering object"},
"acid-drinkers~superstitious-motherfucker": {"haiku":"Obsessive superstitions govern behavior; the narrator follows irrational rituals and taboos compulsively despite their illogic.","opus":"A raucous comic litany of absurd superstitions, the narrator running from black cats, avoiding taxi drivers, sleeping through the thirteenth, and refusing to wash certain things on weekends, all punctuated by a spike hammering in his head. Acid Drinkers, the Polish thrash-metal veterans, turn ritual paranoia into a fast, filthy joke, their trademark crude humor and battering groove making the nonsense gleefully infectious.","sonnet":"A catalogue of private superstitions — avoiding black cats, sleeping through the thirteenth, shaking chimney sweeps' hands — delivered with total commitment and zero self-awareness. The speaker navigates daily life by a byzantine personal code that mixes folklore with crude personal rules, and the spike in the head that keeps interrupting is the only sign that any of this might be taking a toll.","src":"haiku","fable":"A comic self-portrait assembled from house-rules, this catalogues a private omen-code where inherited good-luck lore about crossing animals and the fortune-bringing figures old European belief says you touch for luck sits deadpan beside gutter boasts and invented taboos. The gag is that a life supposedly ruled by cosmic caution turns out to be pure license: every clause licenses the narrator to do exactly as he pleases while pretending fate demands it. Acid Drinkers keep their Polish thrash-with-a-grin register straight-faced, stacking the absurd prohibitions until ritual curdles into compulsion, protection into fixation. The hammering refrain about the nail driven behind his eyes is the tell, an intrusive pounding he flinches from that names the real engine, the superstitions only a costume over an addict's fixed loop craving the next hit or brawl.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"superstitions as costume over an addict's fixed craving loop","built":"the hammering refrain about the nail names the compulsion the omen-code hides"},
"acid-drinkers~swallow-the-needle": {"haiku":"Surveillance and street violence close in; the speaker refuses respect and obedience, primal rage boiling over against cops and the watching public.","opus":"Poland's veteran thrash jokers drop the humor for a claustrophobic portrait of a cornered criminal, all barked commands and manhunt paranoia, staging the interrogation-room ritual where the state grinds a violent man down until insanity becomes his last dignity.","sonnet":"A song about someone in the crosshairs of police surveillance, living by street rules, refusing to give up information — the narrator is cornered, violent by instinct, and fully expects to be declared insane rather than submit.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hard man is worked over under the interrogation lamp, ordered to lie flat for the shot, to stop struggling, to give up the names of whoever ran with him on the street. His whole method is refusal, meeting each barked command with a curled lip and a chorus that hands his questioners nothing but contempt. The title's needle cuts two ways, the truth-serum jab and the junkie fix, so the swallowing reads at once as stubborn silence and slow self-poisoning. By the closing verse the swagger narrows into something colder, a man claiming brutality as his one inviolable freedom and half-daring them to lock him away as a lunatic. What began as pure posture curdles into prophecy, the untouchable mask worn until it hardens into the verdict against him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape"],"means":"claiming brutality as one inviolable freedom until it hardens into the verdict","built":"the needle cuts two ways: truth-serum jab and the junkie fix at once"},
"acid-drinkers~the-ark": {"haiku":"Civilized society is a dark procession carrying villainy like a sacred relic, with freaks locked in and dehumanized.","opus":"A darkly self-mocking confession of drinking, screwing and drifting with a shrug, an unremarkable fool slipping past death's blade. Its refrain about hauling wickedness the way others haul a sacred chest casts existence as a bleak, jeering parade of oddities penned behind padded walls.","sonnet":"The narrator, hollowed out by drink and reckless living but somehow still standing, leads a procession of misfits and freaks through a dark carnival — carrying human villainy the way priests carry the Ark of the Covenant, with grim absurdist ceremony.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses are a portrait of anesthesia — drinking, sex, sinking, all done with a shrug, a man who slips past death not by will but because even the scythe finds him too numb to bother collecting. Against that flatness the chorus mounts a black processional: the band inverts the sacred image of bearing the Ark of the Covenant into a parade hauling villainy instead, drummers ordered about like fools to keep time while the whole march stays lightless. The middle turn locates the numbness in a padded ward of soft-lined confinement and drugged inmates dosed with a superbalm shot straight into delicate pipes, so the indifference reads as sedation rather than peace — a medicated ward-crowd rattling its bars. Acid Drinkers, working in their scrappy Polish-thrash English, turn the mistranslated stiffness of the phrasing to their advantage: the deadpan wrongness of the diction becomes the deadened affect of the speaker, and the holy procession degraded into a drum-banging herd of the shamed is the song's real thesis — reverence emptied out into ritual with nothing sacred left inside it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","faith & the occult","madness & the mind"],"means":"reverence emptied into ritual with nothing sacred left inside it","built":"the sacred Ark procession inverted into a parade hauling villainy in the dark"},
"acid-drinkers~the-ball-and-the-line": {"haiku":"Cult-like sacred congregation with mysterious rituals; outsiders invited into obscure spiritual practice with inexplicable power.","opus":"A cryptic, ritualistic invitation, the narrator bending a ball to a line as if enchanted and haunted, welcoming outsiders into a sacred congregation, teasing them about not eating meat and promising inspiration. It is playful nonsense dressed as cult ceremony. Acid Drinkers, the Polish thrash-metal veterans, lean into their absurdist streak here, the obscure imagery and mock-solemn welcome carried by their loose, rowdy energy.","sonnet":"An absurdist invitation into a congregation built around an unexplained ritual of bending the ball to the line — mystical, ridiculous, and delivered with the solemnity of initiation rites. Those who arrive without the ball are welcomed anyway and promised they will be pleased, and the haunted bending continues on a loop that seems to be its own purpose.","src":"haiku","fable":"A nothing skill — curving a shot so it drops onto a chalk line, the free-kick daydream of any schoolyard — gets preached as scripture, and the whole gag rides on that grotesque gulf between the feat and the fervor. The speaker recruits like a garage-band messiah, promising enlightenment and a following, while the miracle on offer is a bit of ball spin and the sermon keeps sliding into gibberish: turn up empty-handed, walk out a grinning convert, and never mind the sudden interrogation about last night's dinner. Acid Drinkers pour their beer-hall irony over the whole thing, deadpan gravity stretched across a groaner, so the target is the pose itself — the mockery of how readily anyone will build a doctrine around one silly knack and start hunting for disciples.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"mocking how readily anyone builds a doctrine around one silly knack","built":"garage-band messiah register stretched over a groaner exposes the pose itself"},
"acid-drinkers~the-cannibal": {"haiku":"Captive darkness consumes innocence as appetites devour; the maniac's menu features victims as spectacle transforms into grotesque consumption.","opus":"A lurid prison tale of a caged predator and the smaller inmate waiting beneath the bunk, flipping predator and prey. Beneath the pulp horror runs a warning that the one who looks like the meal may be the hunter.","sonnet":"A prison standoff between predators: a cannibal on the cell block has a woman as a snack on his menu, but the narrator — sleeping beneath his bunk, waiting — makes clear that if the cannibal comes for them, the retaliation will be scorching and lethal.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study of a man-eater that keeps switching its camera: it begins as cool outside reportage, fellow convicts thumbing the case file on a fresh arrival, then dissolves into that arrival's first person, daring anyone who fancies flipping the hunter into the hunted. The menu-chant — a woman shrunk to a mouthful, devoured on schedule, the outcome sealed — is planted as the track's immovable heart, and its word-for-word triple return is the whole design: hunger never grows, it merely restates, locating the dread in the loop rather than in any rising action. Then the last verse trips a wire, the narrator flattened beneath a fellow inmate's cot until sleep takes the mark, unmasking the true theme as the pecking order behind the bars, one beast calmly out-scheming another. Acid Drinkers serve this with their trademark leering-gore thrash zest, so the threat lands half as grindhouse gallows farce, the slaughter-trade bravado pitched for a smirk even as the shutting image clamps down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"dread lodged in the loop not the escalation","built":"camera shifts from outside reportage to first person mid-song"},
"acid-drinkers~the-joker": {"haiku":"A powerful fool-king entertains the masses with stupidity while secretly killing, using his invisible status to escape consequences.","opus":"A sardonic portrait of a celebrated, richly-paid public 'joker'/clown who is actually a moronic, invisible tyrant, silencing anyone who speaks too loudly against him; the punchline reveals his secret hobby is killing, exposing the beloved star as a dangerous fraud.","sonnet":"A satirical portrait of a powerful fool — a clown in charge, untouchable and beloved despite being an average corrupt man with a taste for killing, whose power makes dissent too dangerous to speak aloud.","src":"haiku","fable":"The joker here is no comedian but a strongman in greasepaint, and the song works by keeping his two faces in separate compartments until the last verse welds them together. The verses build a mock-hagiography of a nationally beloved buffoon — the highest-paid clown a whole country salutes even while calling him a moron — while the refrain, murmured to an ordinary listener, warns that the walls are listening and that raising your voice means digging your own grave. That split is the argument itself: worship and dread turn out to be one machine, viewed from the throne or from the floor. When the paint finally cracks and the man who makes people stand on their heads and answer like wound-up dolls is caught enjoying private murder as a pastime, the gag rots into the point Acid Drinkers keep circling across their post-communist Polish thrash — that a tyrant's staged foolery is not a screen over his violence but the very outfit it wears.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society","artifice & performance","violence & murder"],"means":"worship and terror exposed as one machine from two angles","built":"the cracked greasepaint marks the moment comedy becomes murder"},
"acid-drinkers~the-last-lap":{"haiku":"An exhortation to push through a final test of endurance and willpower, treating life as a demanding race where persistence and effort are the only path to success.","opus":"A driving pep-talk to push through to the finish, framing life's final stretch as a last lap where you crawl on, take no break and give everything you've got. The imperatives (make it, take it, break it) turn perseverance into a mantra of not giving up.","sonnet":"A stripped-down motivational push to give everything in what remains — the mundane list of tasks (fix the car, play piano) underscores that the final effort applies to ordinary life as much as anything grand.","src":"haiku"},
"acid-drinkers~the-noose": {"haiku":"Suicidal inevitability and religious indifference; death calls while heaven offers no refuge, the town's dead mockery drowning out any reason to refuse the rope.","opus":"A doomed man named Jimmy cornered by death, faith failing him as the town rots and the noose tightens. The narrator can only urge him to answer death's call, all escape already foreclosed.","sonnet":"A portrait of a man named Jimmy in a town on the brink — the noose is waiting, the healer is useless, Sundays smell like death, and despite the narrator's pleas for Jimmy to recognize what is happening, death is calling and everyone around him is already half-gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A condemned man's final reckoning gets played as inescapable momentum: the hangman's rope arrives less like a verdict than like weather, an unstoppable pressure that flattens Jimmy where he stands with no room to twist away. Acid Drinkers widen the frame past one cell into a whole town gone septic — the doctor useless, the streets carrying the stink of corpses, the Sabbath itself curdled — so a private execution reads as symptom, not crime. The sharpest cruelty is the crowd's counsel to make peace with a God who is out drinking somewhere while they preach him, exposing faith as a promise that vacates the room the instant anyone leans on it. Stripped of every rescuer, the narrator's last kindness is grim clarity: quit demanding reasons and simply meet the woman named Death who is already at the door, which recasts giving up as the sole lucid choice left standing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","freedom & escape"],"means":"surrender to death reframed as the only lucid act remaining","built":"a whole town's rot widens a single execution into systemic verdict"},
"acid-drinkers~the-rust-that-i-feed": {"haiku":"Mental corruption and resistance—the speaker harbors growing rust/bitterness resistant to words, fed by witnessing cruelty and broken traditions.","opus":"Acid Drinkers reach for high literary allusion, invoking Apollinaire and a knife-carrying Christ-bearer to dress up a tale of festering resentment. The rust the narrator feeds and grows becomes corrosion resistant to any words, a self-nurtured bitterness ripening toward a strike he admits he deserves.","sonnet":"Corruption nurtured from within resists every word that might dissolve it. The narrator tends a personal decay — the rust he grows and feeds — even as he invokes figures of judgment and betrayal: a knife-bearing Christ-carrier, a sleeping force about to strike. Language fails against what has been deliberately hardened.","src":"haiku","fable":"Corrosion here is not an affliction but a diet, a bitterness the speaker deliberately nurtures until no plea or argument can eat through the flaking crust he has grown over himself. The mangled translation-English lends its broken grammar to a broken mind, and the parade of borrowed greatness — the poet's tenderness, the composers spinning in their tombs, the gentle saint-bearer turned assassin — gets yanked down into one furious private grievance, each figure cursed for failing to sing the speaker's own sour key. What ripens toward the striking blade is aimed inward: a man who confesses he has it coming and guards his own decay precisely because it is the single conviction nobody can reason him out of.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"bitterness cultivated as the one possession nobody can argue away","built":"broken translation-English mirrors the broken mind it voices"},
"acid-drinkers~thy-will-be-done": {"haiku":"Religious authority masks exploitation; the speaker resists imposed divine will in favor of autonomy.","opus":"A defiant answer to divine authority, waking half-dead and refusing to be squashed by a mercy that gives no reprieve. He'll accept fate on one condition, that he chooses his own drink, God spared the role of his shrink.","sonnet":"A sardonic rejection of divine providence and religious submission — the narrator, waking each day feeling half-dead alongside brothers doing the same, rails against the idea of surrendering to God's will, demanding the right to choose their own path and resenting being told how to live under the banner of mercy.","src":"haiku","fable":"The Lord's Prayer gets recast as a hostage standoff: the speaker surfaces each morning already a corpse, one of a throng who trusted the promise of petition rewarded and were flattened by the same mercy they had pleaded for. The song's real quarry is the con of surrender — that a benevolent will demands total obedience and then bills the coercion as a gift. Its hinge is the counteroffer smuggled into the liturgy: he'll go on reciting the sacred phrase, but only if he selects his own vice and the deity quits playing analyst, a haggle that turns devotion into a lush guarding his bottle from an intervention. Prayer curdles into defiance still wearing prayer's costume, the shut question of whether the road runs toward paradise or the sewer left unresolved on purpose, because handing over the choice is itself the deadness he keeps waking into. It carries this band's fingerprint — Catholic-Poland catechism hawked back through a drunkard's leer, blasphemy and worship built to be indistinguishable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"devotion haggled into defiance still wearing prayer's costume","built":"the Lord's Prayer recast as a hostage negotiation with conditions attached"},
"acid-drinkers~track-time-66-6-sec": {"haiku":"A woman tells a man she sees devil and darkness in him and orders him to hunt and destroy; the number 66.6 echoes ominously.","opus":"A brief, tongue-in-cheek metal vignette where a woman on the road anoints the narrator not as a man but a hunter and tells him to destroy, playing the devil-in-the-face conceit for a quick shot of menace.","sonnet":"A brief vignette in which a street encounter with a mysterious woman reveals the narrator's true nature — she sees a devil in his face and tells him he's a hunter, not a man, and his purpose is to destroy.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title flaunts a runtime of 66.6 seconds, the devil's number wearing a stopwatch, and the lyric is engineered to go off inside that sliver of time. A stranger met under a rotten grey sky takes the narrator's hand and stages a mock-annunciation; the Polish supplies the line the English coyly withholds — she sees the devil in his face. Her benediction curdles a breath later into a sentence, stripping his humanity and drafting him as a hunter under one command to destroy. It is demonic election played wholly for the punchline, the holy and the vile crushed together into a minute of grinning bad-taste theatre.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The lyric is printed twice, and the Polish version carries a line the English withholds: she sees the devil in his face. The reveal lives only in translation.","themes":["faith & the occult","comedy & wordplay","violence & murder"],"means":"demonic election delivered as a minute of grinning bad-taste theatre","built":"the runtime stamped in the title is itself the devil's number wearing a stopwatch"},
"acid-drinkers~two-be-one": {"haiku":"2000 years of historical human conflict driven by fighting and division are traced back to a fundamental failure to see shared purpose and unity.","opus":"An unexpectedly earnest plea against two thousand years of division and wasted time, the narrator insisting that despite endless pretexts to fight we're all on the same road toward reconciliation. Acid Drinkers, better known for their crude thrash humor, drop the joke here for a genuine anti-conflict message, the Rolling-Stones-nodding intro giving way to a call for unity over the tribal habit of battle.","sonnet":"A two-thousand-year argument against human division — wars, religions, and endless pretexts for fighting when everyone is fundamentally walking the same road. The plea is simple and frustrated: we keep splitting over what we should say instead of noticing how much we agree. The refrain to stop and not start the battle again is directed at the whole species, not one person.","src":"haiku","fable":"A protest against religious bloodshed whose engine is a sour paradox the band circles: a preacher of brotherhood, dead two millennia, has been invoked as license for twenty centuries of slaughter. The bafflement running through it treats hatred as willful blindness rather than real quarrel, since the warring sides walk one road and squander a fresh opening for peace each morning. Its harshest move is the shouted plea for restraint, spat by a throat plainly itching for the very brawl it begs everyone to abandon.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"a plea for peace delivered by a throat itching for the brawl it denounces","built":"a preacher of brotherhood invoked across two millennia to license slaughter"},
"acid-drinkers~we-died-before-we-start-to-live": {"haiku":"A transgressive nocturnal world where chaos reigns and social order crumbles, with beings emerging that defy convention and create new forms of corruption.","opus":"A macabre, vampire-flavored nocturne warning some nocturnal creature to retreat before sunrise, threading images of addicts and hedonists spawning new life with a grim refrain that these beings perished before they ever truly lived. Acid Drinkers wrap Polish thrash around a horror-movie conceit of night as the only safe hour.","sonnet":"A nocturnal scene of chaos, addiction, and moral disorder — wolves, vampires, junkies, and libertines operating outside law and order — frames the bleak refrain that a generation was already dead before it had a chance to truly live.","src":"haiku","fable":"Delivered in Acid Drinkers' gleefully broken English, this stages a creature-of-the-night fable that keeps dissolving into a needle-and-decay parable, and the dissolve is the whole trick. Early scenes play a vampire dreading sunrise — hide in the dark, let no one see you lit — until the fanged figure blurs into a wasted hedonist, and the dread of daybreak becomes the dread of the crash after the high. The chorus's refusal to quit the blade of the habit, then the surrender that it must be given up, names the dependence flat out, while the title's paradox lands the sentence: a life burned pursuing the next hit is an ending that shows up before any living begins. That verdict turns graveside in the closing scene, where the absent burial clothes and dark shoes mark a man who was never dressed for death because he never clocked that he had already quit living.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The chanted rough cut is hearable as the blade of the habit itself — the thing that can't be stopped until it's given up.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief","party & hedonism"],"means":"a life burned chasing the next hit is an ending that arrives before living begins","built":"vampire-dreading-sunrise blurs mid-song into wasted-hedonist fearing the crash"},
"acid-drinkers~what-s-happenin-in-the-heart-of-a-pacifist": {"haiku":"The internal turmoil of someone committed to nonviolence but conscious of injustice and enemies around them; the toll of pacifist principle on the heart.","opus":"A Polish thrash band probing the contradiction of wanting peace while the world forces you to recognize enemies and threats. To stay truly nonviolent, they suggest, you would have to numb yourself out of being human.","sonnet":"The speaker insists that truly knowing peace means remaining fully human — able to see, hear, and feel — and asks what it costs a pacifist to stay aware of enemies and violence rather than blinding themselves to the world.","src":"haiku","fable":"The pacifist here interrogates his own creed and finds it can only survive through self-mutilation: to stay peaceful he would have to blind, deafen, and wound every sense until he stopped registering the enemy entirely. The song's engine is that each condition for peace is also a condition for ceasing to be human — sensing the threat and feeling the answering pull toward violence are the same faculty. That turns the closing question from rhetoric into genuine alarm: a thrash band from a country that spent the 1980s under martial law is not mocking pacifism so much as admitting that a body still equipped to see and feel cannot honestly promise not to fight back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","politics & society","identity & becoming"],"means":"every condition for peace is also a condition for ceasing to be human","built":"post-martial-law Polish thrash turns pacifism into an honest admission of embodied limits"},
"acid-drinkers~woman-with-the-dirty-feet": {"haiku":"A lover grieves over abandonment and heartbreak, their tears literally falling on the woman's feet as they reveal emotional devastation.","opus":"A brief, grubby lust song where the narrator craves a woman's body smell and casts himself as a real man, then pivots to heartbreak, bleeding and weeping tears that fall onto her dirty feet after she left. Acid Drinkers, Poland's thrash provocateurs, compress their trademark vulgarity into a short blast that swings from crude come-on to genuinely abject longing over a departed lover.","sonnet":"A brief, earthy expression of devotion and heartbreak — someone who loves completely and unsanitized, and who has been wrecked by the absence of the person they want. The image of tears falling onto dirty feet is both tender and absurd. The song is too short to go anywhere; it exists just to name the feeling and stop.","src":"haiku","fable":"A thrash-metal come-on that starts as pure swagger — the boast of a real man craving the animal reek of a body — and then quietly capsizes into abandonment, the braggart left broken and bleeding once she is gone. The dirty feet are the tell: what began as an appetite for the unwashed, unglamorous woman curdles into abjection, the man weeping downward at the lowest, filthiest part of her, worship and degradation collapsed into one gesture. The joke of early Acid Drinkers is that the macho posture cannot hold its own line for a full verse; the desire that was supposed to prove his mastery ends with him on the floor.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"macho appetite capsizes into abjection the moment she is gone","built":"the dirty feet shift from appetite to worship and degradation collapsed into one gesture"},
"acidman~a-2uv20":{"haiku":"A dreamlike reverie on childhood wonder and distant lands—retrieving magic from memory, scattering golden sand toward an orange horizon.","opus":"Acidman's cosmic-minded art rock lends this its patient melancholy, as a boy scatters orange sand like a spell to summon golden sound from the space between sun and sky. The gesture is an act of creative faith, casting magic over dusty circuits and childhood memory in hope that music will one day shine. What carries the track is the mood of someone who has not yet arrived but quietly believes arrival is possible.","sonnet":"A boy scatters a handful of orange-colored sand like a spell, trying to summon golden sound from the space between the sun and sky — the song is about the act of creative faith, casting magic over dusty circuits and childhood memory in the hope that music will eventually shine. The emotional core is the patient, slightly melancholy optimism of someone who hasn't arrived yet but believes the arrival is possible.","src":"haiku"},
"acidman~a-3b90zz":{"haiku":"Mythological creation and artistic pursuit—the world began with a single tone from which all existence emerged; poets chase dreams endlessly following that original sound.","opus":"The band's fascination with origins and cosmos drives this toward myth: the world born from a single soft sound, humanity's betrayal of the sun, and a narrator standing alone with all solitude and fragility gathered up. Raising his voice is not despair but the very act that triggers rebirth today, leaving wind, sound, and light as the only essentials. Creation and self-expression collapse into the same gesture, the tone as its own dawn.","sonnet":"A song about cosmic renewal: the world was born from a single soft sound, humanity betrayed the sun, and now the narrator stands alone holding all solitude and fragility, voice raised — not in despair but as the act that triggers the world to be reborn today, with wind, sound, and light as the only remaining essentials.","src":"haiku"},
"acme~another-world":{"haiku":"Reality as constructed lie where perception divides and truth remains inaccessible. The world collapses into emptiness except for a small light offering glimpses of authentic tomorrow.","opus":"Existential dread staged as a world built entirely of lies and fabrication, where the narrator can't tell whose truth he's even seeing. The one thread of hope is a small light pointing toward a tomorrow that might finally be real.","sonnet":"A speaker locked in a lonely room questions whether the world they see — built from liars, engineered and false — is the same world others inhabit, seeking someone to confirm what truth even looks like, with a single small light as the only remaining hope for tomorrow.","src":"haiku"},
"acme~mononoke-requiem":{"haiku":"Sacrifice and self-abnegation through borrowed identity, body as expendable shield. Hatred and revenge fester beneath martyrdom as the narrator asks whether love or ownership defines their entanglement.","opus":"A vengeful specter born from being scapegoated and stoned as a monster, dying over and over to shield the very people who reject him. Love buried under resentment finally ignites into a curse, the martyr turning executioner.","sonnet":"A spirit that has died countless times as a substitute for a human it protected turns its accumulated hatred and grief into vengeful fury, cursing those who called it a monster and threw stones, drowning in a sea of blood at the realm of the dead while demanding to be let go.","src":"haiku"},
"acme~senkou":{"haiku":"Defiance against normalization and fear-based conformity imposed through institutions. The speaker refuses the script of safe mediocrity, burning bright and determined to shatter the hierarchy that dims ambition.","opus":"A youthful revolt against a rote, fear-drilled school system that grades and tames its students into cowering conformity. The flash of the title is the nail that sticks up refusing to be hammered down, betting everything on a reversal.","sonnet":"A rebellion-fuelled rallying cry directed at students grinding under conformity and fear of failure: the speaker scorns the \"stand-bow-sit\" classroom rhythm, declares the \"inferior star\" overhead something to stare down rather than submit to, and urges a desperate charge toward a blinding, scorching world worth reaching.","src":"haiku"},
"acme~zesshououka":{"haiku":"Fleeting summer romance in theatrical chaos where masks drop and desire blooms before dawn breaks the spell. Love becomes ephemeral dance between costumes, as authentic meeting remains impossible within the ruse.","opus":"A lush, classical-Japanese celebration of fleeting summer romance, all hydrangeas and dragonflies and a fickle maiden's heart. The whole point is impermanence: seize the one-night revel or it vanishes like a soap bubble.","sonnet":"A summer festival of love and disguise rendered in classical Japanese imagery — hydrangea petals, fox spirits, kaleidoscope hearts, and a one-night revel where masks drop at nightfall; romance is morning after dawn, fleeting as a dragonfly, and the whole encounter is framed as a mutual trickery that clears like fine weather.","src":"haiku"},
"acres~around-again":{"haiku":"Emotional entrapment repeats cyclically as distance and obsession perpetuate without resolution. The speaker circles endlessly, searching for something that remains unfindable.","opus":"An insomniac loop of unhealed heartbreak, the speaker gripping a wound too deep to sustain while the other drifts off distracted and unbothered. The circling gives the song its title, naming the pointlessness of orbiting a finished relationship, hunting for something that was never available to be found.","sonnet":"A relationship cycle that never resolves: the speaker holds on to a wound too deep to sleep through, while the other person has their head in the clouds with everything figured out — they keep going around again, searching for something that is never there.","src":"haiku"},
"acres~bloodlust":{"haiku":"Obsessive hunger and mutual destruction bind predator and prey in escalating codependency. The speaker craves consumption and contamination, surrendering to forces that devour.","opus":"Addiction dressed as an erotic possession, where the singer surrenders his body to something that abuses and drains him yet keeps him coming back for more. The craving to feel nothing is what makes the self-destruction feel like devotion.","sonnet":"A destructive, addictive attachment described as venom in the veins: the speaker cannot give the other up, lets them dig in and leave wounds that never heal, consuming their world and breaking their soul — the surrender to bloodlust is framed as both abusive and desired.","src":"haiku"},
"acres~built-to-bleed":{"haiku":"Existential suffering appears programmed; the speaker's darkness traps them in futility while fantasizing about escape through death.","opus":"A raw account of depression as something structural rather than passing, a body that feels engineered only to suffer, harboring a private hell and a quiet longing for sleep that never ends. Pain framed as design, not accident.","sonnet":"The speaker is convinced they were designed purely for suffering — \"built to bleed, here to grieve, made to hurt\" — a dark poisoned mind from which there is no escape, the only imagined relief being a final sleep that never wakes.","src":"haiku"},
"acres~burning-throne":{"haiku":"Tyranny and exploitation consume everything; the speaker recognizes the ruler's coldness and demands escape while trapped beneath their authority.","opus":"Resisting a cold tyrant who promised the world but took it all for himself, worshipped under blinding lights, as the singer fights to keep something of their own and escape.","sonnet":"About resistance against a controlling, manipulative authority figure — a dictator who made promises and took everything for themselves, demanding worship while consuming the air keeping everyone alive. The narrator refuses to let this person take what matters most, screaming to escape them, while the authority figure on their burning throne calls for heads to roll.","src":"haiku"},
"acres~into-flames":{"haiku":"A life consumed by loss and regret—everything worth living for burns away, leaving only emptiness and the futile attempt to recover what's been stolen by time and betrayal.","opus":"Watching a life and a relationship burn down and choosing to let it, accepting complicity in the collapse rather than salvaging what someone else has drained away.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship that has hollowed out the speaker completely — they have been consumed by darkness, stripped of everything, and are consciously letting the bond die because there is nothing left to give.","src":"haiku"},
"acres~medicine":{"opus":"A bleak, honest confession of depression and suicidal thought, its narrator spending his days wishing for an end while just barely staying afloat. He masks the anguish from everyone around him and pleads for whatever cure, or escape from a poisonous bond, might keep him alive a little longer.","sonnet":"Someone drowning in depression and self-loathing — wishing life were already over — admits they can only survive by getting away from the person dragging them under, while hanging onto the threadbare certainty that they are still here.","src":"sonnet"},
"acres~my-everything":{"haiku":"Emotional enmeshment and codependent dissolution merge identities as the speaker loses themselves in another's presence.","opus":"A tender counterweight to despair, offering yourself as shelter to someone crushed by their own isolation. Love here is small and practical, a soft place to land when everything a person carries turns too heavy to bear alone.","sonnet":"The speaker gets completely lost in a lover — \"the lights of your eyes\" — and asks them to fall into each other as mutual salvation, admitting their own mess while offering unconditional presence as the only remedy they can offer.","src":"haiku"},
"acres~nothing":{"haiku":"Dissolution of self through another's manipulation, becoming nothing without them while they feed on your suffering.","opus":"A toxic entanglement rendered as mutual destruction, where one person seems to feed on the other's suffering to feel alive. The bitter turn is the dawning belief that without the narrator, the abuser is simply nothing.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship where the other person feeds on the narrator's suffering — \"suffocate and watch me hurt so you can come alive\" — and whose whites of the eyes are paralyzing. The narrator has been fine alone but is still half-awake in the hell of it, lighting the match and watching it burn while refusing to let the other return.","src":"haiku"},
"acres~the-death-of-me":{"haiku":"Abandonment cuts deep—insufficient love becomes death sentence. The speaker bleeds from wounds inflicted by someone they couldn't satisfy, left believing their essence was always inadequate.","opus":"The fresh wound of being left for someone new, replaying the accusation that he was never enough while blaming her for the mess he's in. The title turns her love into something lethal, the abandonment killing him even as he wishes her the happiness he couldn't provide.","sonnet":"Betrayal by someone who let the speaker believe they were enough — only for them to leave for someone new — breeds both blame and a tortured recognition that trying to be something they weren't was the trap; love becomes the thing that will be the death of them.","src":"haiku"},
"acres~visual-hallucinations":{"haiku":"Phantom voice haunts crowded rooms; the speaker struggles to release memory of their presence. Distance grows though images persist—separation without closure.","opus":"Post-breakup haunting where the speaker mishears a lost love's voice in a crowd, half-dreaming while insisting he's moved a long way on. The wish that the other feels sorry for what they've become carries the grudge that keeps the ghost alive even as he claims to be leaving.","sonnet":"A voice mistaken for the ex-partner's in a crowded room triggers the realisation of how far the speaker has come; they hope the other is sorry for what they did and became, and the song ends with quiet finality: still leaving.","src":"haiku"},
"adam-freeland~borderline":{"haiku":"Motion away from connection accelerates toward abyss; the speaker drowns in self-reflection. Each step forward paradoxically pushes companions away, isolation becoming inevitable.","opus":"A spiraling portrait of someone lost in self-obsession and edging toward collapse, imagery of needles and a sought-after gun placing it in addiction and self-ruin. The label given to the other names a volatile push-and-pull that sinks them both, each stolen bit of light widening the distance.","sonnet":"A speaker drowning in their own reflection and drifting further away every day — looking for a pistol, edging closer to a precipice — describes a relationship that drags both parties down like a needle in a wound, naming the other person as \"borderline\" in a loop of mutual destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"adele~hello":{"haiku":"Regret and distance persist across time; the speaker attempts contact knowing severed connection cannot be rebuilt.","opus":"Adele reframes the reunion ballad as one-sided reckoning: an apology owed to someone who has already healed and moved on. The real subject is her own unfinished remorse, calling into a void that no longer answers.","sonnet":"An ex-partner calls across years of silence to offer an apology that the other person apparently no longer needs — the heartbreak has healed on their end while the speaker is still tangled in regret and distance. The call goes unanswered, and the asymmetry of their recovery is the song's quiet devastation.","src":"haiku"},
"adore~dancing-while-the-world-burns":{"haiku":"Nihilism and pleasure spiral together as the speaker dances through apocalypse, numbing themselves through motion and stranger-love.","opus":"Numbed hedonism as coping, dancing and laughing through heartbreak and collapse because feeling anything fully is unbearable. The admission of only pretending to care, of skipping the ending, makes the party a deliberate distraction from an apocalypse both external and internal.","sonnet":"Defiant joy in the face of collapse — dancing, laughing, loving while the world burns and the heart hurts — the record keeps spinning and the singer chooses to keep moving rather than stop for empathy or endings, turning catastrophe into a reason to sway rather than grieve.","src":"haiku"},
"adult-dvd~7-foot-1":{"haiku":"Anxiety about horror and adult media pretended away through false enthusiasm. The narrator witnesses discomfort without admitting it, performing enjoyment while internally retreating into safer genres.","opus":"A wry indie confession about pretending to love a cult film you never actually watched, bailing out scared before the end. The absurd height boast underlines the real subject: the small social lies we tell to seem cooler than we are.","sonnet":"A narrator keeps claiming they haven't seen and don't want to see the film in question, performing enjoyment while internally retreating via the \"shoelace express\" — refusing to watch a PG-13 horror because it terrifies them, with the refrain \"now I'm 7 foot 1\" marking their escalating, comedic dread.","src":"haiku"},
"adult-dvd~do-something":{"haiku":"Perpetual experimentation and failure reveal inadequacy at everything; the speaker surrenders to passive contentment rather than authentic achievement.","opus":"A wry, jangly shrug at the pressure to have a hobby and a purpose, cycling through failed pursuits before happily retreating to bed. Restless self-improvement culture gently mocked by someone content to do nothing well.","sonnet":"A string of attempted hobbies and encounters — fishing, book clubs, swimming with dolphins, meeting the king — all falling slightly short, until the speaker goes home, reads the book, and gets hooked. The irony is that trying everything leads back to the simplest act.","src":"haiku"},
"adult-dvd~dogs-in-the-sun":{"haiku":"Encounters with eccentric characters loosely connected by speculation and hearsay. The narrative fragments across curious people, held together more by whimsy than actual truth.","opus":"A surreal string of eccentric characters, alligators and lying ventriloquists, hung on the old line about mad dogs and Englishmen. Beneath the whimsy is a wink at English absurdity and the pull toward honesty at the end of the day.","sonnet":"Eccentric urban vignettes — an illustrator's crying alligator, a lying ventriloquist, a loan shark with a home aquarium — loosely tied together by the old Noël Coward maxim that only mad dogs and Englishmen venture out in the midday sun, used here as a wry framework for observing social oddities.","src":"haiku"},
"adult-dvd~hot-set":{"haiku":"Adult film production exposes institutional dysfunction; pretense collapses into genuine passion.","opus":"A wry backstage tour of a low-rent adult film shoot, the indebted cameraman and jaded crew turning a supposedly titillating scene into pure workplace tedium. The pun on hot set and the coy refusal to explain treat pornography as just another chaotic, mismanaged job.","sonnet":"A winking comedy about a chaotic adult film production in which every crew member is overwhelmed, in debt, or barely present — the cameraman sweats, the director was formerly a health inspector, the actors barely know each other — but by the end they are \"getting satisfaction\" for real.","src":"haiku"},
"adult-dvd~real-tree-lee":{"haiku":"Predatory age and transgression; an old man pursues through urban landscape.","opus":"A sharply comic character sketch of a paranoid, camo-clad survivalist neighbor, all guns and bunkers and born-again bluster. Affectionate ridicule that shades into a portrait of male isolation and conspiracy-brained decline.","sonnet":"A portrait of a paranoid, self-reliant prepper-type named Lee — ghillie suit, bunker parties, illegal homemade diesel — whose survivalist identity conceals a born-again incel who owns guns but never learned to shoot, hiding in the leaves because he knows he's hated.","src":"haiku"},
"advantage-lucy~a-2z0db5":{"haiku":"An escapist flight into anonymity—boarding a train without destination, seeking darkness and distance to escape overwhelming words.","opus":"In the wistful register of Japanese indie-pop drift, someone boards a train with no destination, slips on headphones to block out any guidance, and vanishes underground on a metro going nowhere. Running away and going far, they search for the right words that might someday make things feel fine. The restlessness is urban and formless, the flight of a person who needs to move precisely because they cannot say where to.","sonnet":"A person boarding a train with no destination, putting on headphones to block out guidance, and disappearing underground on a metro going nowhere in particular — running away and going far away, searching for the right words that might someday make it feel 'fine.' The emotional core is the restless urban escape of someone who needs to move without knowing where they're going.","src":"haiku"},
"advantage-lucy~how-do-you-feel":{"haiku":"Distance arrives unnoticed; tangled threads unravel in dawn light. One speaker watches another withdraw, unable to reconnect despite longing for the moment rupture reverses.","opus":"A couple whose hearts have quietly drifted apart, one partner staring blankly at the scenery and giving vague replies while the other can only watch their back. The wish that the tangled thread would just come undone in the golden dawn light captures a love decaying too slowly to name.","sonnet":"Two people who once shared a sunrise now pass each morning in distracted silence — \"how do you feel?\" answered vaguely — the speaker watching a partner's back and wishing the tangled thread between them would unknot as quickly as golden morning light spreads across the city.","src":"haiku"},
"advents~chroma":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness and abandoned connection leave the speaker suspended in limbo, begging for release from the slow death of being unmourned.","opus":"A voice at the brink inviting a lover to finish him off and not flatter it as anything noble. Imagery of being bleached to grey makes emotional erasure the theme — the narrator stuck in limbo, beyond apologies, daring the other to claim they ever cared.","sonnet":"A person addressed an absent other whose emotional coldness has drained all colour from the relationship, leaving them suspended in limbo and willing to be thrown off the edge — 'you colored me obsolete' — while still demanding to be found or honestly denied.","src":"haiku"},
"advents~in-my-restless-dreams":{"haiku":"Loss and abandonment haunting the waking mind as internal rupture. The narrator faces emotional death through guilt while seeking escape from the grace of the dead that fills emptiness.","opus":"Loss and guilt filtered through Silent Hill imagery, longing for an embrace in a sacred place while blotting out the light. Reckoning with what he's done tips into self-destruction, blaming the lost love for taking his light with her.","sonnet":"Grief and guilt merge into a shattered mental landscape: the speaker blocks out the sun to cope with loss, craves the embrace of someone in a secret place whose absence haunts empty space, and by the end confesses to having been blinded and diminished by that same person.","src":"haiku"},
"advents~match-my-misery":{"haiku":"Incompatible suffering breeds resentment; the speaker's anguish finds no recognition from someone unwilling to witness proportional pain.","opus":"A plea to be met at the true depth of his suffering, the narrator hollowed by a partner's indifference and certain no one will sink as low as he has. Battered hands offered as proof he's fine, the reflex to fight or flee out of spite — all circling a decision to rebuild himself alone.","sonnet":"A person reduced to half of themselves by someone's crashing apathy and silence — \"bloody knuckles and bloodshot eyes\" as proof of effort — screaming to just be told the truth while knowing their own spite and fight-or-flight will keep them from getting better.","src":"haiku"},
"advents~never-game-over":{"haiku":"Perpetual cycles of dysfunction trap the speaker as both victim and architect; self-imposed limitation and environmental pressure fuel the refrain that breaking remains impossible, yet continuation persists.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with being shaped by circumstance, admitting the pieces never fit and refusing to pretend otherwise. The title insists that as long as you're alive the struggle to live for yourself isn't finished.","sonnet":"A person lost in self-destructive cycles and environmental conditioning admits the pieces never fit while insisting on living for themselves — \"I'd rather die than live a lie\" anchors a refusal to pretend at direction they don't have.","src":"haiku"},
"adventure-club~next-life":{"haiku":"Love's impossibility in present time reframed as reunion postponed until afterlife. The narrator releases the beloved to protect them, promising reunion where time dissolves constraint.","opus":"A tender goodbye that admits the timing is wrong and the love is running out, breaking promises it couldn't keep. The consolation is reincarnation, deferring the reunion to a next life rather than fighting for this one.","sonnet":"A farewell to a relationship that is right but badly timed: the speaker accepts the goodbye, acknowledges broken promises, and defers reunion to another life — \"find me at the end, we'll start again\" — holding the connection open across an indefinite future.","src":"haiku"},
"adventure-club~wonder-andru-remix":{"haiku":"Regret crystallizes the speaker's awareness that they failed to cherish connection when present; they acknowledge their past carelessness.","opus":"A confession of failure that arrives too late, addressed to someone already gone. The narrator admits he should have held on and treated her better, but hides behind the excuse that nobody keeps the promises they make.","sonnet":"A person acknowledges too late that they failed to hold someone close or treat them well — the absence of care becoming vivid only after the relationship has ended — the regret circling in conversations locked away in memory.","src":"haiku"},
"advocates~abaddon":{"opus":"A raw account of medicated depression and self-hatred, where the singer's own mind is the monster; numbed by pills, cornered and suicidal, he can't fight back against the malicious rage that keeps dragging him to rock bottom.","sonnet":"The narrator is haunted by a violent inner demon — named for the biblical destroyer — that taunts their weakness, urges them toward violence as a coping mechanism, and has brought them to rock bottom through contempt and malicious self-destructive impulses they fear they cannot contain.","src":"sonnet"},
"advocates~dreamstate":{"haiku":"People sleepwalk through life, trapped by complacency and the opinions of others, chasing illusions as truth. At the end of the rope there is nothing left to fulfill their needs.","opus":"Metallic hardcore condemning a life given over to complacency, walking a landscape of cowards where dead ambitions crumble and the judgment of the herd distorts everything. It accuses its subject of bartering away his existence and mistaking illusion for truth, finding that once he hits his limit there is nothing left to satisfy his longing for peace, only a sleepwalker fighting to breathe.","sonnet":"A person has surrendered their life to complacency, letting the opinions of others warp their ambitions into dead reflections. They sleepwalk through a fractured existence, mistaking illusions for truth, trading away genuine purpose for the numbness of conformity, left with nothing left to satisfy them.","src":"haiku"},
"aenygma~dopamine":{"haiku":"Numbness replaces sensation as the speaker chases empty reward cycles; apathy consumes meaning.","opus":"Numb depression chasing any hit that will make the brain feel something, the days looping identically in grey. Not sad so much as empty, the speaker names himself his own worst enemy while begging for the poison anyway.","sonnet":"A song about emotional numbness and addictive self-stimulation as a substitute for feeling — the narrator is not sad but feels nothing, caught in a loop of apathy, turning to drinking, masturbation, and whatever stimulant will jolt the brain back into sensation, recognizing they are their own worst enemy.","src":"haiku"},
"aerosmith~dream-on":{"haiku":"The narrator reflects on aging, mortality, and the transience of life, encouraging others to pursue their dreams despite inevitable loss and suffering.","opus":"Aerosmith's early signature ballad reckons with aging and mortality as the narrator studies new lines in his mirror and concludes that losing teaches winning. The chorus urges perseverance toward one's dreams before time runs out. Written by a young Steven Tyler, it became the band's first breakthrough, its wisdom about paying life's dues sounding older than its author was.","sonnet":"A man confronts the accelerating passage of time, watching youth recede in the mirror as hard-won wisdom takes its place. He finds the only dignified response is to keep singing through both laughter and grief, and to go on dreaming until the very end.","src":"haiku"},
"aerosmith~sweet-emotion":{"haiku":"Sexual bravado and careless transgression mask narcissism; the speaker seduces through manipulation and dismissal of reciprocal care.","opus":"Seventies road-life swagger with a sour underside: Tyler's kiss-off to a groupie and to Joe Perry's girlfriend whose meddling was splintering the band. Lust and touring boredom dressed up as a strut that barely hides the resentment.","sonnet":"A swaggering road-life boast aimed at a woman the speaker finds attractive but sees through — she lies, her home is chaos, her appeal is mostly physical — the song delivered with the insouciant confidence of a band that knows the score backstage.","src":"haiku"},
"aerosmith~walk-this-way":{"haiku":"The song chronicles youthful sexual exploration and escalating physical encounters. It celebrates sexual awakening through various experiences and partnerships.","opus":"Tyler's rapid-fire comic recollection of adolescent sexual initiation, all clumsy bravado and locker-room mythology; the swagger of a former high-school loser retelling his awakening with a wink rather than any real tenderness.","sonnet":"A series of adolescent sexual encounters — cheerleaders, neighbors' daughters, schoolgirls — told as a rowdy coming-of-age story where an older woman or girl teaches the narrator what to do next.","src":"haiku"},
"aesthetic-perfection~gods-gold":{"haiku":"Inner conflict breeds endless warfare over resources and meaning; blind devotion to broken systems ensures descent toward collective collapse.","opus":"A darkwave lament over humanity's endless wars fought for religion and greed, tracing the bloodshed back to the hate we breed in ourselves. Its refrain is a genuine question of whether we can ever outgrow the cycle.","sonnet":"Humanity's endless cycle of war, greed, and darkness is driven by the inner conflict of souls clinging to crumbling castles; salvation is offered as a price rather than a gift, and the question hangs unanswered — will we outgrow the war of gods and gold before the blood runs in the streets?","src":"haiku"},
"aesthetic-perfection~gravity":{"haiku":"Stars fade while gravity claims all things; the speaker surrenders to inexorable descent. Inertia burns as hope dissolves into eternal darkness.","opus":"A longing to escape the pull of a cold, godless cosmos where the stars won't align and light is devoured by dark. Gravity becomes the force of despair itself, the speaker aching to drift free of an eternity that burns and abandons him.","sonnet":"Stars misaligned and gods gone, the speaker longs to drift free of gravity into a cold cosmic embrace — afraid of the \"ever after\" — as inertia burns and an eclipse swallows eternity.","src":"haiku"},
"aesthetic-perfection~rhythm-control":{"haiku":"Sexual dominance through musical metaphor where rhythm becomes control and lover becomes instrument. Passion accelerates and shifts key, destabilizing both partners in syncopated submission.","opus":"A dancefloor seduction turned power struggle over who controls the beat, and by extension the relationship. In a superficial club world the rhythm is all there is, so the flirtation becomes a bid to stop dancing to someone else's tune.","sonnet":"A club-floor power struggle between two people whose rhythms never quite sync: the first voice is seduced by a woman who controls the beat; the second flips the dynamic, announcing she'll change the key once she's had enough — their relationship a song on repeat that one of them will eventually rewrite.","src":"haiku"},
"afi~holy-visions":{"haiku":"Spiritual seeking through material ritual and mystical fragments, cheap beads as devotional object. Sacred vision persists underground, calling the seeker downward toward buried light.","opus":"Catholic imagery of rosary beads and glowing figures underground, blurring devotion with erotic longing and creative compulsion. The visions call him home the way inspiration does, sacred and carnal at once, cheap beads standing in for grace.","sonnet":"Mystical, slightly fevered visions underground — a glowing man, a burning girl — both calling the speaker home while they finger cheap rosary beads in their left hand, the repetitive bead-counting becoming a kind of trance that bridges the mundane and the sacred.","src":"haiku"},
"afsheen~no-muse":{"haiku":"Displacement and desperation across wasteland where both speaker and other belong nowhere. Music alone sustains the wandering, neither country nor rest nor belonging remains.","opus":"A desolate exile across a landscape that was once ocean, carrying her own bones to bury in hope they'll grow into shade for the next refugee. The English refrain answers the despair: music itself, not any muse, is what keeps her unbroken.","sonnet":"A Japanese-language song about exile in a desolate land that was once sea: the singer buries their bones in dry earth hoping to become a tree for the next refugee, drifting forgotten at the ocean floor, with music itself named as the only thing that keeps them unbroken — \"no muse but the music.\"","src":"haiku"},
"after-smoke-clears~machine-dependent":{"haiku":"Technology and capitalism breeding disconnection and dependency while eroding mental health. Dopamine hits replace genuine connection as the system ensures citizens remain docile and medicated.","opus":"A deathcore indictment of a generation raised on participation trophies and then hooked on screens and dopamine, chasing social approval. The machine is the phone, offering temporary relief while an inner voice screams that you're failing.","sonnet":"A diagnosis of a generation raised on participation trophies, thrown unprepared into the real world, and now addicted to the dopamine drip of screens: blue-light-blinded, credit-score-anxious, and temporarily self-medicating with devices while an internal voice calls them a loser.","src":"haiku"},
"after-the-burial~exit-exist":{"haiku":"A figure bearing scars of worlds destroyed roams forgotten realms as ghosts, questioning whether feeling and redemption are possible. All hands sink deeper into crimson stains.","opus":"The Minnesota djent pioneers dress existential dread in cosmic, maze-like imagery. Scars become a map of the body as the narrator, calling himself a world-ender, reaches inward and finds his hands stained and searching. Questions about sensing the intangible or freeing oneself from decay give the technical brutality a genuine ache, a man half-erased and adrift among the wreckage.","sonnet":"Trauma mapped across the body as old scar tissue, with no clear path forward out of the wreckage. He exists only in photographs most days, adrift in the cosmic scale of his own undoing, asking whether feeling is still possible when everything has rusted over and the self has been partly pulled away.","src":"haiku"},
"after-the-burial~lost-in-the-static":{"haiku":"The individual becomes a path worn by others' expectations, losing identity while society designs madness and gold-plates emptiness, creating cycles of suffering.","opus":"The djent band casts modern alienation as being drowned out by noise. Rejecting his inherited identity, the narrator fights a pull dragging him down while dressing up his own chaos as something precious. Imagery of being exhumed frames selfhood as buried and reclaimed, alongside a vow to endure a brutal road shoulder to shoulder with someone else.","sonnet":"Estrangement from lineage and identity, buried under the accumulated noise of systems people build and then forget the purpose of. He refuses a father's shadow, asks to be dug up and held upright, while around him others become pathways worn smooth by traffic — suffering the same erosion, slogging forward side by side.","src":"haiku"},
"afterglow~black-hole":{"haiku":"Depression as gravitational collapse where escape becomes impossible despite awareness. The journey inward deepens the hole while darkness claims identity and hope simultaneously.","opus":"Depression pictured as a black hole set in the chest, swallowing everything, with a plea to let go and stop assigning blame. The one anchor is a warning not to abandon yourself as the dark takes every last part.","sonnet":"Emotional numbness and depression are figured as a black hole at the heart — claiming every bit of darkness — while the speaker cycles between sinking certainty and the faint counterargument that the journey has no visible end and one should let go rather than vanish entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"afterglow~connected":{"haiku":"Systems of control and deception designed to separate while claiming unity and sedation. Bonds remain despite corruption, binding the trapped to collective surrender.","opus":"Paranoia about outside forces injecting hatred and keeping people divided and sedated, discovering too late how connected everyone really is. The suffering is framed as engineered, a fear that manufactured division controls our minds.","sonnet":"A collective awakening to manipulation: an outside force has been injecting hatred to keep people apart, but the attempt fails — the only discovery is that everyone is connected and simultaneously sedated, surrendered to control they are only now recognising.","src":"haiku"},
"afterglow~down":{"haiku":"Mutual destruction through denial and complicity where both sink together. The narrator refuses to acknowledge the drowning that claims them both equally.","opus":"Confronting someone who pretends not to hear while secretly hoping you'll fall, and pointing out they're sunk in the very same pit. The truth is everywhere, in the air and the water, yet both keep faking smiles until the denial finally caves.","sonnet":"An accusation directed at someone who ignores shared complicity: the speaker insists they are both buried in the same hole and that pretending otherwise — forcing smiles, refusing to listen — makes the falling inevitable for both parties.","src":"haiku"},
"afterglow~ephemeral":{"haiku":"Fragile bonds sustained by denial; fear of connection intertwining with fear of dissolution.","opus":"A melodic-metal meditation on emotional numbness and fragile human bonds, tied loosely so they come apart easily, warning that a civilization without empathy is doomed. The hardest truth is the one people refuse to hear.","sonnet":"Collective emotional numbness — the ability to feel has vanished and decayed, bonds are loosely tied so they can be undone, and empathy's absence makes failure inevitable. The speaker indicts a society that can see, hear, and speak but registers nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"afterglow~like-a-wave":{"haiku":"Collapse becomes cyclic inevitability where will shatters against systemic pressure. Everything surrenders wave by wave to forces too vast to resist or overcome.","opus":"Turning collapse into forward motion, a broken spirit smashing ashore and insisting the passage matters more than the destination. Suffering becomes the sole thing that keeps you going as monsters and lunacy try to sap your resolve.","sonnet":"The speaker's fractured soul strains against forces trying to drain the will into emptiness, framing personal collapse and recovery in wave imagery — crashing into the shore, overflowing with noise — insisting on enforcing their own will even as everything collapses around them.","src":"haiku"},
"afterglow~nothing-in-between":{"haiku":"Lost beauty and despair empty the world of meaning; connection becomes the only defense against dissolution.","opus":"A defiant search for beauty in a world drained of love and light, insisting on total honesty with nothing held back even as everything burns. It weighs whether escape into fantasy is the only cure for despair, or a lie.","sonnet":"A plea to rebuild connection in a world stripped of love and light — every hope is make-believe, but the narrator is not afraid and wouldn't give a damn if the sky fell, because the relationship is the only thing worth protecting. The question \"can you restore a broken memory?\" is asked knowing the answer requires giving up dreams that were never real to begin with.","src":"haiku"},
"afterglow~worn-out":{"haiku":"Exhaustion from repetitive failure masking genuine hopelessness beneath performance. The narrator finally breaks under the weight of always trying the same impossible thing.","opus":"The exhaustion of repeating the same days expecting change, staring at self-made ruin and the day you gave up on yourself. The call to wake up grows impossible to ignore even as the narrator keeps gambling with fate and breaking down.","sonnet":"Exhaustion with the insanity loop of trying the same thing and expecting different results: the speaker stands in a self-made minefield, notes that the sun refused to rise the day they gave up on themselves, and is finally forced toward waking up rather than playing with fate one more time.","src":"haiku"},
"afterlife~envy":{"haiku":"Desire for others' lives consumes and corrodes from within; the speaker warns that chasing what belongs to someone else transforms yearning into a self-inflicted poison.","opus":"A warning that coveting other people's lives eats you from the inside, and that getting everything you wished for still leaves the honey tasteless. It is about how envy corrodes contentment and the choice to stop living through another's eyes.","sonnet":"Envy is diagnosed as a slow internal destroyer: you want what others have, you get it, and still feel dissatisfied — the honey no longer tastes right — trapped between the life you have and the person you imagine you could be, feeding greed from the inside out.","src":"haiku"},
"afterlife~psa-feat-ryo-kinoshita":{"haiku":"Scathing commentary on privilege and entitlement, where someone born into unearned advantage is called out for looking down on others who actually struggle.","opus":"The British metalcore band, with Crystal Lake's Ryo Kinoshita guesting, unleashes a blunt kiss-off to arrogance and fakery. The narrator sets the record straight against two-faced, condescending types, insisting the band never cared and never plans to start. The chant that it isn't about you and the refrain of not giving a fuck turn wounded frustration into a defiant, communal declaration of indifference toward those who look down on them.","sonnet":"A direct, unsparing rejection of arrogant social climbers who mistake inherited ease for achievement. The address is collective — a band or crew speaking in unison — and the message is total indifference: entitlement is visible, the contempt is returned, and the pretenders are dismissed as faces in a crowd.","src":"haiku"},
"afterlife~wasting-time":{"haiku":"Society sleeps while systems crumble; the narrator mourns lost ideals and begs people to wake up to deterioration they refuse to see.","opus":"Metalcore alarm at a decaying America, disconnection and lost ideals piling up while the narrator refuses to accept the collapse he sees. The urgency is aimed at a country sleepwalking through its own unraveling.","sonnet":"A song about disillusionment with America — the narrator refuses to accept the evidence of a crumbling society, laments disconnection and decay, and rails against a population sleepwalking through the death of the American Dream.","src":"haiku"},
"against-the-current~weapon":{"opus":"Against the Current chart the self-loathing spiral of someone who keeps promising to change and lying straight through their teeth, sleepless and boiling with a rage that pushes everyone away. The weapon is the self, a person who shoots others down until they end up alone. It closes on a fragile, hard-won refusal to go under.","sonnet":"A song about the narrator recognizing themselves as emotionally destructive — they cannot control the rage that pushes people away, keep breaking promises to do better, and admit they are a weapon aimed at themselves and others alike. The emotional core is trapped frustration at a pattern they can see but can't stop.","src":"sonnet"},
"age-factory~blood-in-blue":{"haiku":"A runner chases through summer's end clutching severed memory, velocity replacing connection. Bloodline burns inside; rhythm propels forward past anchor-cutting, searching debris for what's vanished.","opus":"A Japanese rock song soaked in end-of-summer melancholy, blood running under moonlight and memories fading past reach. A restless craving to change rather than repeat, set against a season slipping away like a hat on the wind.","sonnet":"At summer's end, blood runs hot and memory dissolves — hands once held are released into \"I don't care\" indifference — yet the blue that bleeds into the heart's depths and the pale, fading melody unreachable in the distance suggest the speaker is still haunted by whatever was lost as the hat drifts away on the wind.","src":"haiku"},
"age-factory~close-eye":{"haiku":"Contemporary anxiety and artificial stimulus overwhelm perception; closure offers only temporary escape.","opus":"A dizzying list of modern Japanese life's ills and fixtures, abuse, discrimination, religion, school, phones, war, spat out until they blur into meaningless noise. Facing a new era around 2020, the speaker's only refuge is to close his eyes on a world that offers nothing.","sonnet":"A song that piles up the noise of modern society — abuse, discrimination, social media, drugs, religion, consumerism, fame, war — and concludes with the instruction to close your eyes and shut it all out, questioning what it means to want anything as humanity enters a new era stripped of meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"agent-fresco~see-hell":{"haiku":"Rage accumulates in the body like poison, manifesting as violent desire disguised as moral clarity until the boundary between justice and vengeance dissolves.","opus":"An Icelandic post-hardcore reckoning with rage and its residue, hands that keep reaching for cruelty long after the war is over. The horror is recognizing your own appetite for destruction and asking whether to feed it.","sonnet":"A speaker consumed by cycles of rage and violence reaches a point of terrible self-awareness, recognising that the urge toward destruction is internal as much as external, and questioning whether feeding that fire is condemnation or release.","src":"haiku"},
"agnieszka-chyli-ska~jest-nas-wi-cej":{"haiku":"Plural resistance against singular judgment and control. The growing collective refuses prescription, demanding autonomy from those who claim authority to direct their lives.","opus":"A Polish anthem of defiance against being told whom to love, what to believe, and when to suffer, insisting there are more and more of us. A refusal to be judged or mocked, claiming the simple right to live on one's own terms.","sonnet":"A Polish pop anthem against external judgment: the speaker refuses to be told whom to love, what matters, or when to sacrifice, and underscores their refusal with the growing chorus \"jest nas więcej\" — there are more of us — turning personal demand for autonomy into collective solidarity.","src":"haiku"},
"agonize-the-serpent~lost-in-silence":{"haiku":"Internal dissolution through hidden suffering and walls collapsing from within. The narrator drowns silently in tidal waves while isolation fragments identity and sanity.","opus":"Betrayed vows and creeping despair staged as collapsing walls and a vigil beside your own empty plot. The rot within is what's killing him, a sleepwalk through damnation as quiet swallows whatever's left.","sonnet":"A person dissolving under broken promises and inner wretchedness pleads to be pulled up from the ground, likening themselves to a sleepwalker drifting beside their own hollow grave, the walls caving inward while something — anything — waits to arrive.","src":"haiku"},
"aiko-el-grupo~el-award-es-para":{"haiku":"Theatrical performance masks emotional devastation as the speaker watches their lover's calculated cruelty play out. Awards go to the best actor in the tragedy of false love.","opus":"A Spanish-language kiss-off to an abuser who performs tenderness minutes after violence, sarcastically awarding him a prize for his acting. The clown mask drops: she's done swallowing his hollow declarations of love.","sonnet":"A woman addressing an abuser who masks violence with courtesy — beating her one moment, being pleasant the next, leaving quickly while denying hiding anything — presents the relationship as a performance so consistent he deserves a professional award for acting; she spits out the 'I love yous' she spent too long chewing.","src":"haiku"},
"aiko-el-grupo~toro":{"haiku":"Young rebels reject conformity, embracing danger and transgression while driving through the night without regret or apology.","opus":"The Spanish indie band delivers a giddy invitation to nocturnal abandon. The narrator coaxes a girl into the car for a night of drinking, speed and dancing among the dead, roaming from Spain toward Berlin without regrets. Threaded through the revelry are sharper barbs against fascists and against factory chimneys ringing the city, whose ovens and smell they refuse to accept, lending the party an undertow of defiance.","sonnet":"A night of reckless liberation shared between women who refuse moral policing. The drive to Berlin, the speed, the dancing among the dead, the slaughterhouses ringing the city — all pulse through a scene of defiant pleasure that knows exactly what traps surround it and refuses to fall into any of them.","src":"haiku"},
"aiko~pedestal":{"haiku":"Self-love becomes the only valid sanctuary; the speaker elevates themselves above obligation and false devotion, claiming autonomy through deliberate indifference.","opus":"Choosing self-love over an unrepentant partner's empty apologies, learning to stop forcing things and place herself first as the only real path to freedom.","sonnet":"A person leaving a relationship with someone whose apologies change nothing — they're done explaining themselves and done absorbing the other person's indifference, and they resolve to redirect all their love toward themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"ailsha~nightmare":{"haiku":"Haunting memories trap the speaker in psychological horror until they sever the ghost, reclaiming peace by severing all connection.","opus":"Exorcising an abuser who lives in the mind as a horror show, the speaker finally banishing them to the hell and nightmare where they belong. The confession that seeing them makes her lose control undercuts the bravado, the ghost still tugging even as she orders it gone for good.","sonnet":"A speaker who has been haunted by someone — losing control whenever they appear — declares they have finally expelled that person from their nightmares and from their head, consigning them back to hell as a ghost who no longer has power.","src":"haiku"},
"aimer~brave-shine":{"opus":"Written as an opening theme for the Fate/stay night anime, Aimer's ballad is about the strength to protect what matters and the terror of losing faith in that strength. Amid wounded nights and shed skin, it reaches for a light kept alive by a lover's promise, prayer stretching across time toward a dawn worth guarding.","sonnet":"A song about finding the courage to seek the future despite pain — the narrator has hidden wishes that remain unfulfilled and stands wounded in endless rain, but ultimately reaches forward into a brave light that saves them: promises made across time, tears eventually resolved into brightness.","src":"sonnet"},
"aimer~choucho-musubi": {"haiku":"Binding intimacy through paired knots tied together with mutual effort and care. The butterfly results from joint action, remaining stable only through balanced tension and continued attention.","opus":"The literal act of tying a bowknot becomes a delicate metaphor for building a relationship, two people pulling in unison, neither too tight nor too loose. Making a bond is hard and undoing it far easier, so tie it carefully and mean it.","sonnet":"A tender meditation on a relationship framed as tying a shoelace together — step by step instructions for making a bow that holds — culminating in the declaration that the bond was not something that happened to them but something both people actively chose, pulling together in unison from the infinite scatter of threads in this wide blue world.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is literally a set of instructions for tying a shoelace bow — loop one end, hold it, wrap the other behind, feed it through the pocket, and on the count of three, pull. That soft second-person imperative only gradually reveals it was never about a shoe. What is being tied is a bond between two people, and Aimer works the knot as a full image-system: she wants the loops big and the knot tight, wants arms kept close and memories kept far. She frets over the same physics that makes the tie possible making it undoable, since pulling one end unravels everything and breaking what you built is so much easier than building it. The middle admits the anxiety of craft — pull too hard hoping it will never come loose and you are left fumbling with wings spread too wide, tie when you meant to loosen and loosen when you meant to tie. The final turn is the payoff and the reason for the count of three that recurs like a refrain: out of countless threads scattered across this vast blue world, the two chose separately and reeled each other in. So they weren't tied together but tied it — 結ばれたんじゃなく結んだんだ, active voice against passive — both of them pulling on cue, putting in exactly enough force that the bow comes out neither too big nor too small. The genius is that the instructional register never breaks: the humblest household gesture is held steady until it carries the whole weight of chosen, mutual, effortful love.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire"],"means":"chosen mutual effort distinguished from being tied by fate","built":"shoelace-tying instructions held steady until they carry the weight of willed love"},
"aimer~closer":{"haiku":"An inevitable tragic ending that the speaker knows approaches, standing trapped between pretense and a predetermined conclusion.","opus":"A portrait of concealing collapse behind a false front, holding onto pain and dimming memories as the only thing that feels solid. The narrator oscillates toward and away from a single defining moment, sure that no matter how events play out, they all lead back to one particular person.","sonnet":"A song about the slow, inevitable end of a relationship or moment: the speaker watches colours fade, memories slip, and the distance grow, knowing how the story ends but moving toward it anyway — closer to loss, farther from escape.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's title names a paradox it never dissolves: nearing and drifting are the same gesture here, because whether the speaker approaches or bolts, the trajectory terminates at one identical person. Aimer, whose English-language material tends to soundtrack beautiful ruin, voices someone who has ruled that shadow is kinder than daylight and that ache is the only ground solid enough to hold weight while every steadier certainty dissolves underfoot. So the repeated admission of dissolving into thin air reads less as annihilation than as surrender to the last fixed coordinate remaining — the beloved who is simultaneously the harbor being sailed toward and the danger being fled. The closing refrain tilts the whole thing: earlier passes merely forecast the shape of the finish, but the final one leaves a single exit, so the vanishing hardens from mood into a decision. What poses as a love song is really the instant you quit fighting the pull of a person you have already understood will undo you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"surrender to the one person who remains when all else dissolves","built":"the final refrain's single exit hardens a mood into a decision","fnote":"Track 10 of daydream, and not her pen: written and produced by Taka of ONE OK ROCK, an English-language song built for her voice."},
"aimer~falling-alone": {"haiku":"Falling through loss and sorrow, the narrator mourns wounds stitched by a lost lover; they adapt to pain and search for strength.","opus":"The Japanese vocalist, prized for a smoky voice across anime soundtracks, threads Japanese and English through a portrait of sinking alone into fading light. Tears trace the cheek in a narrowing world, yet wounded wings are stitched back by someone the narrator refuses to release. The withered false dream gives way to a vow to keep living entwined with another, delicate despair opening onto fragile, stubborn hope.","sonnet":"A Japanese-English song about plummeting through grief — the world narrowing, colors bleeding to blue — held up only by the memory of hands laced together. The damaged wings were mended by another; without them, the fall continues but the will to keep moving survives.","src":"haiku","fable":"A fall out of a withering dream, sung by a voice that keeps insisting the descent is solitary even as it addresses a you throughout. The wound here is a pair of broken wings that another hand stitched shut. So the promise is not flight recovered but living on, together, with the damage sewn over rather than healed. Aimer splits the song by language: the Japanese verses hold that clinging, two-handed vow to go on beside someone, while the interposed English lines carry the private collapse the Japanese won't say aloud. The other is still felt behind the eyes and yet has slipped past any grip, nothing in the singer sits well, and a body under the sheets keeps asking whether this is the end. So the bilingualism isn't ornament but a partition between what is offered to the other and what is admitted only alone. The turn arrives in the last chorus's small mutation. The earlier verses have the beloved suture the torn wing, but by the end the singer has grown used even to being hurt and reaches out on their own terms, hands laced fiercely, sure this time of stepping forward. The falling-alone the title names turns, quietly, from abandonment into the self-possession of someone who no longer needs the wound closed for them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","heartbreak & loss","love & desire"],"means":"solitary collapse held beside an unhealed other","built":"bilingual split assigns the confession only to English"},
"aimer~hz": {"haiku":"The song celebrates radio and music as healing frequencies that erase anxiety and monotony from daily life. It frames sound as a tuning force that connects hearts and brings joy to isolated listeners.","opus":"Aimer builds a buzzing ode to radio and frequency, casting sound waves as something that erases gloom and remakes dull days in colour. The lyric tunes dials, name-checks genres and treats music as pure uplifting energy. It suits the Japanese vocalist's genre-fluid catalogue, here trading her melancholic mode for a bright, kinetic celebration of finding one's own signal.","sonnet":"A song about radio as a force that erases loneliness and dull days — invisible frequencies carrying voices across empty distance, lifting moods, dissolving frustration, and turning monotonous hours into something worth moving to. The dial becomes a metaphor for tuning oneself toward joy.","src":"haiku","fable":"An uncharacteristically bright, bubblegum turn for Aimer's smoke-and-ache voice, and the whole song is one sustained pun: radio engineering as a mood cure. It builds its case through the vocabulary of signal — tune to a vague frequency and you get nothing, so you dial in your password-precise setting, and once the wavelength lands it erases the noise: gloom, irritation, someone else's loneliness, bottled stress all fizz into bubbles and smoke and vanish. The point where the metaphor admits what it actually loves is a spun-across-the-band genre litany, a whole FM dial swallowed in one breath — the right sound coloring a monochrome day is the only technology being praised here. The final chorus quietly relocates the dial inward: turn the volume down on your own whining voice, find your own tuning, one-up yourself — self-broadcast rather than reception, a fist clenched on the last Yes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","identity & becoming"],"means":"tuning a precise wavelength until all emotional static vanishes","built":"the metaphor relocates inward at the final chorus from reception to self-broadcast"},
"aimer~insane-dream":{"haiku":"Psychological breakdown and fragmented trauma memories.","opus":"A tormented, nightmarish struggle with painful memories and desperate prayers for forgiveness, trying to shake free of an insane dream.","sonnet":"A desperate, haunted plea for forgiveness and release from traumatic memories that suffocate and will not fade.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two languages that never touch build an addict's grammar of dependence. Aimer's Japanese carries the injury: a burnt smell stabbing memory, breath that won't come, a black sun, red eyelids gone blind. The verses reach toward forgiveness and a third prayer, a heartbeat that refuses to stop. But each grafted English clause chops that reaching apart and erases the memory it just made, so tenderness and destruction become one repeated gesture. Taka's post-chorus turns accusatory and blind, less a duet than a hostage negotiation with something already withdrawn. The title names the only exit: to shake free (振りほどいて) is not to wake but to enter the insane dream, so recovery and relapse arrive in one breath. The song simply cycles, never resolving, because resolution would be a lie the loop won't tell.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"recovery and relapse arriving in the same breath with no seam between them","built":"grafted English clauses erase each Japanese confession as soon as it surfaces, the structure enacting the loop it describes"},
"aimer~kataomoi": {"haiku":"Aging wrinkles and failing voice cannot diminish the speaker's single love, their only anchor in existence. A full life reduces to this one truth—loving one person through every loss becomes sufficient proof that dreaming mattered.","opus":"A vow of lifelong devotion that only deepens with age, loving someone through wrinkles, faltering voices, and even the possibility of being forgotten. Its ache is the one-sided pull of a heart that will love again in any next life.","sonnet":"A declaration of lifelong, quietly overwhelming love: even when the beloved's face wrinkles and the speaker's voice cracks with age, the heart overflows with songs for them alone — \"today is the main course, and at the end a bittersweet dessert\" — and even if forgotten, the speaker will fall in love again in any next life, always one step behind in unrequited adoration.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title means unrequited love, but the song is sung to a partner plainly there and plainly loved — a marriage vow, not a pining. Aimer builds it as a life told forward: verse one accepts the wrinkles and the fingers that can no longer play guitar; verse two reframes the whole span, valleys and all, as a full-course meal with the bittersweet dessert saved for the last day; verse three turns tender-fierce, forgiving even being forgotten but forbidding the beloved to leave first for somewhere far. The refrain's plea — just you, understand me, understand me — carries the ache of one who possesses the love yet still fears it unreceived. The title lands in the final line: even reborn into the worst possible meeting, I'd fall for you again, so my heart is always one-sided toward you — devotion so total it outruns any answer and calls itself unrequited.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire"],"means":"devotion so total it outruns any possible answer","built":"title names unrequited love over a song sung to a present partner"},
"aimer~kowairo": {"haiku":"Illusions terrify while fragile miracles promise meaning. The speaker crumbles under weight of becoming someone new in another's eyes, fearing they'll vanish entirely.","opus":"A plea to be seen as an actual person rather than a miracle or an illusion, sung by someone dissolving inside another's gaze. The fear is that every version they offer up, exposed or newly born, still fails to be truly themselves.","sonnet":"A performer asks who they become inside the listener's perception each time they deliver their voice — afraid of being seen as a miracle or a phantom rather than a real person — as their world expands, anxiety fills it, and the naked self growing smaller within the expanding stage cries out: \"look at me, not a miracle, not an illusion, just me — can you still hear my voice?\"","src":"haiku","fable":"The title names an actor's assumed voice, and the song lives in that dread: who do I become today, breaking apart inside you? Aimer builds it as a widening trap — the field of view opens while the heart shrinks, the world expands and only fills with unease, until the self is no longer anywhere inside its own seeing. The plea at the center wants to be looked at as neither phantom nor miracle nor stripped-bare body, and then twists to its real horror: even what she gives birth to, brings forth, is not her, and that is what frightens. The last breath — can I take wing? — arrives not as triumph but as the smallest survivable hope, a question left open under all that dissolving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"the self dissolving inside a role it can no longer locate","built":"title names an actor's assumed voice while the song asks who that voice belongs to"},
"aimer~ninelie": {"haiku":"Refusing to surrender to a world that grinds down dreams, building bridges despite silence and noise, determined to sing through the tunnel alone if necessary.","opus":"The Japanese vocalist, known for anime tie-ins, sings this Kabaneri of the Iron Fortress theme from within confinement and rain-soaked dreams. Images of a single star sounding out, sunless dawns and a voice that cannot pass through ruined tunnels frame a struggle to keep singing against silence. The refrain urging one not to fear, for daybreak has come, offers fragile defiance and the promise to build a bridge through the dark.","sonnet":"A figure persists through exhaustion and disillusionment — chasing a daybreak that keeps failing to arrive, singing over a city drowning in noise. The drive is not to win prizes or fit external puzzles but to build a bridge with voice alone, lighting silence rather than escaping it, until something genuine finally resonates.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being processed by a system that rewards conformity and calls it a dream — its image-world is bureaucratic and mechanical: puzzle pieces that must lock into the outside, prizes lined up only to be thrown away, irritation packed into a box waiting its turn for a counterfeit goal. Against that machinery the chorus builds its central paradox, the singer vowing again and again to light, to sound, to make silence itself resound — as if the only honest note left is a refusal to add to the noise a city is drowning in. The two voices trade this weariness back and forth, Aimer and chelly finishing each other's exhaustion, until the bridge hardens the dead momentum into something that finally wakes as resistance. That obsolete impulse and corner-filed vision curdle into a hand throwing away the rudder rather than steering the assigned course. The whole structure is a held breath that the outro at last releases into plain English — a hushed instruction to drop the fear because the first light has already crossed the horizon. The dawn arrives not as an image inside the song's exhausted Japanese but as the one line that steps outside it, four times over, insisting the loop can break.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The ending theme of Kabaneri of the Iron Fortress — Sawano-produced, credited Aimer with chelly of EGOIST — the two voices borrowed from two different anime-song worlds for one duet.","themes":["surveillance & control","freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"refusal to add one more note to a drowning city's noise","built":"the only hopeful line steps outside the Japanese into plain English"},
"aimer~twoface": {"haiku":"A speaker hides their true emotions behind a false persona, struggling with the gap between what they pretend and what they feel. They waver between wanting connection and fear of being truly known.","opus":"Aimer explores a divided heart that hides real feeling behind a smile while aching to be with someone. The two faces are the mask worn against grief and the truth beneath it, admitting the singer herself did the wounding. Fitting the vocalist's reputation for shadowed emotional depth, it lingers between light and dark, longing and the fear of goodbye.","sonnet":"A woman has hidden her true feelings behind a composed, smiling surface while inwardly desperate to stay close to someone she loves. Stripping away the performance, she confronts the duality in herself — the face she shows the world and the raw grief underneath — and pleads not to be let go.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title names the split the whole song enacts: every verse pits ホント — the real, true thing, hammered so many times it stops being a word and becomes a compulsion — against the ふり, the pretending. Laughing and wanting to scream out loud become the same held breath. Aimer sings it in that smoky, swallowed register where the confession is already half-muffled by the mask it's confessing to, framed emotion she says she no longer wants boxed and hung on a wall. The turn is that the accusation, aimed outward through two choruses of don't let go, don't tear us apart, finally lands on the singer herself — the one who was doing the hurting was me. Rather than resolve, the song strips down to the two-faced question underneath the whole performance: what am I to you, and you to me, with real and fake, truth and lie no longer separable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","heartbreak & loss","identity & becoming"],"means":"confession of the mask conducted entirely in the mask's voice","built":"the accusation aimed outward for two choruses lands on the singer herself"},
"aimer~us": {"haiku":"Two people dissolve into cosmic insignificance, watching their world burn red; beauty is fragile and madness devours understanding. The song meditates on shared isolation within vastness.","opus":"A cosmic lament where two people are swallowed into the thin border between Earth and the stars, dissolving into an eternal aftereffect as the blue sky bleeds red and the narrator wonders if her companion is lonely. Aimer, celebrated for her husky voice across Japanese anime and film work, spins vast astronomical imagery into an intimate meditation on how the beauty we can't understand is the same thing we keep breaking.","sonnet":"Two people dissolving at the boundary between earth and sky, looking down at a world they could destroy through the same indifference that already mars it. The blue of the sky slowly staining red is the central image — beauty and violence inseparable. What holds is a heartbeat, a hand reaching across the cold, a refusal to let go of the other's memory even as everything fractures.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two lovers watch Earth dissolve from somewhere near the edge of the atmosphere, and Aimer keeps one image turning the whole way through: the blue of the sky (藍) being dyed red — beauty and wound sharing a single color. What begins as cosmic vertigo, everything looking small because the universe has laid a trap, curdles into an accusation aimed inward: the world looks ugly because we paint it over, and the ones who changed it were us (変えたのは僕らだろう). So the recurring plea to melt, to let a broken someone's eternity dissolve, isn't a death-wish but a bargain — she asks that the color of memory and the pulse between them not be peeled away even as everything else breaks. The genius of the song is that it refuses to separate tenderness from ruin: leaning into a lover and feeling the world's coldness are the same gesture, and the loneliness at the center stays transparent, unmeltable, the one thing the surrounding catastrophe can't touch.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","nature & the elements","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"tenderness and ruin folded into a single shared color","built":"the sky-dyed-red image fuses beauty and wound into one word"},
"aimi~a-19w3r50":{"haiku":"Rejecting conformity through chaotic living—celebrating defiance of societal expectations; the speaker argues that living without reason is valid and sensation matters more than meaning.","opus":"Pitched as bright, up-tempo J-pop, the track sets its buoyancy against conformity, mocking the reflex of passively agreeing with trends and doing whatever seems approved. In its place it demands people shout, dance, and live recklessly on their own terms because life happens only once. The argument is that sensation can matter more than reason, and that living without a tidy justification is not a failure but a valid, chosen way to be alive.","sonnet":"A song about rejecting conformity and social performance in favor of genuine self-expression: the narrator mocks the habit of passively agreeing with trends and doing whatever seems approved, and calls instead for people to shout, dance, and live recklessly on their own terms because life is only once.","src":"haiku"},
"aimi~magical-destroyer":{"haiku":"Cycles of destruction and creation spiral endlessly; the speaker embodies chaos, wielding noise and rage as tools to obliterate and remake the world.","opus":"An anime-theme battle cry that fuses destruction and rebirth into pure catharsis, treating the urge to split the world in two as a form of magical, dance-floor love. Beneath the noise runs a plea to wake from a dream that keeps repeating.","sonnet":"A Japanese-language song from the anime \"Magical Destroyers\" about the ecstatic cycle of destruction and rebirth — the narrator wants to wake from a dream that keeps repeating, while simultaneously reveling in the madness of tearing the world in two with a scream. Destruction is framed as a kind of love and release: love, madness, and annihilation are the same impulse, and the only response is to sing and scream louder.","src":"haiku"},
"aimyon~ai-wo-tsutaetaidatoka":{"haiku":"Romantic yearning thwarted by inadequacy and hesitation despite overwhelming desire. The narrator wallows in preparation rituals while unable to express feelings or force desired meetings.","opus":"A slacker in love, sunk in his couch on a lazy morning, wanting badly to say it out loud but too self-conscious and passive to move; Aimyon finds tenderness in his admitted mediocrity and refusal to pretend he's the perfect man.","sonnet":"A restless, self-deprecating young man spends a lazy day wanting to say \"I love you\" but sinking deeper into the sofa instead — the cracked egg yolk, the unwashed shirt, the imagined candles and oversized cake all deflated by the plain truth that he won't be a better man tomorrow, and he just wants to know if she'll come over before sunset.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~armed-and-dangerous":{"haiku":"Sexual braggadocio; a man presents himself as an uncontrollable, hypermasculine lover who will dominate and exhaust his partner through the night.","opus":"The Australian hard-rock outfit, direct descendants of AC/DC, wallow in over-the-top carnal bragging. Fueled by pills and desire, the narrator styles himself an overclocked engine of lust prowling the night. Weapon and machinery double-entendres stack up with zero restraint. Nuance isn't the goal; this is pure grinning, fast-and-loud excess.","sonnet":"Unrestrained sexual energy delivered entirely through military and mechanical metaphor. He presents himself as a loaded, overcharged presence that makes women scream and men nervous, running hot all night on something beyond ordinary fuel — a cartoon of masculine desire that keeps escalating its own claims.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~back-on-the-bottle":{"haiku":"A hedonistic celebration of weekend excess where drinking, partying, and self-destruction are the speaker's chosen lifestyle and escape.","opus":"A working stiff blows his Friday paycheck on whiskey and beer, hotwired and heedless, wishing only that liquor were free. Airbourne, the Australian throwbacks who wear their AC/DC lineage openly, make blue-collar excess a creed here, hammering the same three chords into a proudly witless anthem for going over the edge without apology.","sonnet":"A working man surrenders entirely to the bottle after Friday arrives, choosing whiskey and recklessness over security or sobriety. There is no regret in the admission — careening past every limit is framed as the only honest way he knows how to feel alive.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~blackjack":{"haiku":"A woman is metaphorically a dangerous gamble: seductive and thrilling but ultimately predatory, leaving the speaker with nothing and on the verge of ruin.","opus":"Casino gambling as a seductive, deadly woman: the man bets it all on unlucky thirteen while a sweet, man-eating senorita cheats him with something up her sleeve. Blackjack is both the game and the femme fatale who's making a kill and won't let him get home alive, thrill and ruin fused into one.","sonnet":"A gambling song where the house — personified as a lethal, seductive woman — always wins: the speaker bets everything, gets consumed by the thrill, and walks away knowing she'll take his life along with his chips.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~blonde-bad-and-beautiful":{"haiku":"A man obsesses over a beautiful woman's physical appearance and sexuality, fantasizing about domination and possession.","opus":"Unapologetic bar-rock lust, an Aussie AC/DC descendant reduced to helpless appetite by a woman walking past. There's nothing beneath the wolf-whistle but the wolf-whistle, and it knows it.","sonnet":"A straightforward objectifying infatuation — the narrator is completely undone by a physically spectacular woman walking past, and would sell his soul to be her target.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~born-to-kill":{"haiku":"Trauma creates a permanent drive for vengeance and aggression; a mistreated subject becomes a predator who hunts their abuser. The song uses dog metaphor to describe how abuse warps nature into violence.","opus":"The Australian AC/DC disciples turn a beaten dog into a revenge parable, then swap the metaphor mid-song for their real creed: rock and roll as defiant, uncompromising volume that answers to no rules or authority.","sonnet":"A revenge fantasy framed through the image of an abused dog that survives to tear apart its abuser — the narrator identifies with that dog, and extends the metaphor to rock and roll's refusal to be silenced or controlled.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~bottom-of-the-well":{"haiku":"A desperate survivor claws upward from darkness and drowning, refusing to surrender despite being at the bottom; determination and rage fuel escape from hellish entrapment.","opus":"A defiant survival anthem about hauling yourself up from the lowest point through sheer stubbornness, refusing to accept misery or defeat. Airbourne stage the struggle as a man clawing toward daylight from somewhere dark and drowning, the whole point being raw willpower over any circumstance.","sonnet":"A pure survival anthem about refusing to give up when you've hit absolute rock bottom — the narrator, dark and alone in the pit, claws and scratches his way back toward light through sheer will, treating even a death wish as something to overcome rather than accept.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~cheap-wine-cheaper-women":{"haiku":"A man adrift in loneliness and alcoholism after romantic rejection, seeking comfort in cheap wine and temporary companionship, resigned to his own culpability.","opus":"A boozy, self-aware lament of a drifter drowning heartbreak in cheap wine and casual women after being thrown out by a lover. The honest core, at the bottom of the bottle with nothing, owning that it's his own fault, wraps hard-rock hedonism around genuine loneliness.","sonnet":"A man kicked out by his partner wanders the cold streets and finds consolation in alcohol and transactional sex, fully aware he is the cause of his own situation but not yet ready to care.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~chewin-the-fat":{"haiku":"A man pursues casual sex with a woman who treats him well, cycling through pleasure and departure with the expectation of return.","opus":"Airbourne, the Australian bar-rock heirs to early AC/DC, coast on lust with appetite the only thing on the mind. The narrator drags himself back sore and spent to a woman who treats him royally, heads off down the highway, then starts counting the days until she eases his burden again. Pure swaggering, satisfied hedonism, romance boiled down to the pleasure of returning.","sonnet":"A man savors an intermittent but intensely satisfying physical relationship with a woman who treats him like royalty, leaving him worn out and wanting more. Their arrangement is episodic — he hits the road, she waits for him — and he would do anything to keep the cycle going.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~devil-s-child-bonus":{"haiku":"Marginalized by society, trapped with no future, a person embraces outlaw identity and resists authority through constant resistance.","opus":"Cast as trouble from birth and denied any future in a society that arrests and locks him up, the narrator embraces the outcast label as the underdog who refuses to break. Airbourne trade in this kind of defiant rock persona, and the track leans into juvenile-delinquent mythology, turning marginalization into a chest-out boast rather than a lament.","sonnet":"An outcast who was denied a normal life from the start claims the devil-child label with defiance rather than shame. Arrested, locked up, and dismissed by society, he refuses to quit — his rebellion is the only identity that was ever truly his own.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~diamond-in-the-rough":{"haiku":"A casual sexual encounter between a poor man and a rich woman; the singer is infatuated and cannot get enough despite her wealth and status being incompatible with his station.","opus":"Airbourne's whole reason for existing is bar-band AC/DC worship, and here that's a broke everyman bedding a rich girl for one night, a class-crossing lust anthem where the rough diamond is both her and the swagger of having nothing to lose.","sonnet":"A straightforward song about a one-night stand between a broke man and a rich woman who does not care about class — he takes her home, sleeps with her, wakes up beside her, and wishes for more.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~fat-city":{"haiku":"A man pursues hedonistic freedom and excess, moving through life drunk and dazed, determined to keep rolling on despite physical decay.","opus":"A hard-charging ode to hedonism as forward motion, the narrator battered, drained, and half-gone but still rolling toward some promised better place. Excess framed as a ride you take until your legs give out.","sonnet":"A night of extreme drinking and debauchery pushed past the point of physical coherence — battered, bruised, legs giving out, still rolling on toward whatever comes next.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~get-busy-livin":{"haiku":"Economic collapse and personal ruin force a choice between living fully despite everything or dying by inaction; society decays but one must seize life anyway.","opus":"The Australian rockers turn economic collapse into a defiant drinking anthem. Laid off for outdated skills and unable to cover his debts, the narrator declares his future dead and himself blameless. Borrowing the Shawshank line, he resolves to choose living over dying, riding it out. Nothing-left-to-lose desperation flips into a raised-glass, full-volume celebration of survival.","sonnet":"Economic collapse meets defiant survival instinct. Obsolete skills, unpayable bills, a world that no longer has a place for him — and his answer is to burn through whatever remains rather than quietly accept defeat. The ultimatum is simple: choose fully alive or let the damage finish the job.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~girls-in-black":{"haiku":"A man pursues women in bars and night venues, drawn to their allure and danger despite—or because of—the destructive transactional nature.","opus":"A rock-and-roll ode to bar dancers who trade escape and a fleeting high for cash; the song stays clear-eyed about the deal, admitting the lure costs nothing up front but leaves a lasting sting, and that pleasure here is strictly for sale. Australian hard-rockers Airbourne keep the transaction and its price plainly in view.","sonnet":"About paying for women's company in a bar or strip-club context — the narrator is drawn to these women who offer escape, knowing they're dangerous, knowing the hooks cut deep, knowing everything has a price.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~heartbreaker":{"haiku":"A seductive woman is desired intensely, and the speaker pursues her despite risks. The exchange is sexual and thrilling, with the woman ultimately consenting to more intimate contact.","opus":"A hard-rock ode to lust and a one-night stand with a dangerous woman, framed as making a deal with the devil for the 'Devil's dance.' The pure subject is carnal desire, a hookup pursued with predatory relish and no pretense of romance.","sonnet":"A raw, lustful pursuit of a woman the narrator finds irresistible — he made a deal with the devil for one more night, reached under her dress, and she welcomed it. It's straightforward hard rock sexual desire with no subtext.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~hellfire":{"haiku":"A person drives through hell unable to turn back or escape the choking smoke and flames. He refuses to serve the system and prefers death young to eternal slavery.","opus":"A hard-charging Airbourne song romanticizing a fast, hellish, self-determined life with no exit and no regrets. Trapped and choking on his own recklessness, the narrator embraces it anyway, preferring to burn out early on his own terms and answer to no boss, turning a doomed existence into a badge of freedom.","sonnet":"Being trapped in a brutal grind — driving through hell, choking on air, slaving and screaming — with the refrain that it's better to die young than to keep serving the big boss man, the whole thing shot through with a born-to-ride fatalism.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~it-ain-t-over-till-it-s-over":{"haiku":"The spirit of relentless rock performance celebrates endurance and momentum without stopping or compromise. The song exalts continuous energy and refusal to quit the stage.","opus":"A rowdy, unrelenting toast to rock and roll excess, booze, sex, and refusing to ease off. The chorus turns the old sports cliche about a performance not ending prematurely into a manifesto for never stopping the party. Pure defiant hedonism from the Australian hard-rock revivalists.","sonnet":"A straightforward celebration of rock and roll endurance: drinking, performing, riding this thing all night to the end of the line — the song argues that as long as rock exists it cannot be declared finished.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~kickin-it-old-school-bonus":{"haiku":"Someone celebrates a carefree lifestyle of minimal responsibility, sexual freedom, and rock music. They reject mainstream values and live outside societal expectations with hedonistic abandon.","opus":"A cheerfully unrepentant slacker itemizes his creed: welfare, casual sex, homegrown weed, garage homebrew, and a band that only sings about rock and roll. Airbourne strip ambition to zero here, the Australian bar-rock revivalists celebrating dropout hedonism as a lifestyle, every riff underscoring a refusal to grow up or clean up.","sonnet":"A cheerful ode to living free of obligations — no job, no rules, no pretense — just welfare, homegrown pleasures, skinny-dipping, and rock and roll. The speaker celebrates deliberate simplicity as liberation rather than failure.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~loaded-gun-bonus":{"haiku":"Raw sexual desire and conquest; the narrator hungers for physical connection presented as a loaded weapon ready to strike.","opus":"From the Australian hard-rockers who wear their debt to their idols openly, a brash pickup boast built on firearm double meanings. The singer offers himself for a night of no-strings fun, piling on cocky physical comparisons. It is pure bar-room bravado, the weapon imagery just a vehicle for the band's booze-and-lust party ethos.","sonnet":"A one-night-stand offer delivered with absolute swagger — the speaker casts himself as weapon, predator, and temple-worshipper in the same breath, entirely consumed by the prospect of a single night of mutual want.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~no-way-but-the-hard-way":{"haiku":"A person loses everything—job and love—and accepts that survival requires pushing through hardship alone, without help or comfort, embracing the grind as their only path forward.","opus":"A blue-collar anthem of resilience after losing his job, his woman and turning to drink, the narrator waving off everyone's advice and vowing to keep touring with his band. The message is blunt acceptance: nothing comes easy, and he has made peace with earning everything the difficult way.","sonnet":"A man who has lost his job and his woman and hit rock bottom refuses pity — life on the road in a rock band is hard, the drinking is real, but he'll endure it because there's no other way through.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~overdrive":{"haiku":"A reckless hedonist lives in constant excess without sleep, stimulated by drugs and adrenaline. The song celebrates a lifestyle of dangerous instability and refusal to stop.","opus":"Six sleepless days, a surplus of stimulants and liquor, and a hunger for oblivion fuse into a manic hymn to living without restraint, climbing gears toward annihilation. Airbourne push their AC/DC-descended engine to redline, and the track romances recklessness as vitality, the narrator never feeling more alive than when he is careening past the point of no return.","sonnet":"Six sleepless days of speed, stimulants, and alcohol have pushed a man so far past his limits that the edge itself feels like home. Rather than retreat, he floors the throttle and calls the excess living — survival measured in gears, not mornings.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~raise-the-flag":{"haiku":"The band defends rock and roll as eternal, rallying unity against surrender. They stoke the flame and answer the call to keep rock alive through fighting.","opus":"A fist-pumping Airbourne anthem that treats rock music as an unkillable cause worth fighting and dying for. Cast as an endless battle, the song's whole point is defiant survival: so long as its believers keep going, the music can never be extinguished, and the call to arms is really a call to keep the faith.","sonnet":"A battle-cry for rock and roll as a cause worth defending — the band frames itself as soldiers in a never-ending war to keep the music alive, stoking the flame with high-octane devotion and calling on fans to answer the call and raise the flag.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~rattle-your-bones-bonus":{"haiku":"The speaker seduces a woman and urges sexual contact in blunt, aggressive language, framing desire and physical intimacy as inevitable conquest.","opus":"Airbourne serve up a brazen come-on wrapped in blues-rock swagger, the narrator boasting of his touch and pursuing a girl whose demure Sunday appearance hides a wilder streak. The innuendo is relentless and unsubtle. The Australian band openly model themselves on early AC/DC, and this bonus cut leans hard into that lineage of sweaty, single-minded rock lechery.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~ready-to-rock":{"haiku":"A performer sells their soul for musical power and fame, embracing danger and unleashing overwhelming energy. The song celebrates rock performance as transcendent rebellion.","opus":"The old crossroads bargain returns as the narrator trades his soul for a guitar, fingers set alight to burn heaven above amid thunder and needles in the red. Airbourne revive blues-rock's Faustian myth with unironic gusto, the Australians framing the stage as a pact-fueled inferno and rallying the crowd toward relentless, never-stopping combustion.","sonnet":"A musician who traded his soul at a crossroads for a guitar and a fire in his fingers now summons the crowd to surrender alongside him. Heaven is burning, the needle is buried, and there is no stopping once the ritual begins.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~runnin-wild":{"haiku":"Breaking free from an unhappy and suffocating relationship, driving toward liberation and rejecting any hope of reconciliation.","opus":"A driver leaves a bad relationship for good, refusing to turn back no matter how much the other cries. He revels in the freedom of the open road, his anger fueling the escape from a partner he wants out of his life.","sonnet":"A definitive breakup: the narrator burns with rage at a draining partner, hits the open road without looking back, and finds a bitter freedom in putting as many miles as possible between them.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~stand-up-for-rock-n-roll":{"haiku":"Rock and roll must be defended as a way of life, with fans encouraged to embrace rebellion, excess, and communal celebration regardless of others' disapproval. The music is a call to refuse conformity and social restriction.","opus":"A straightforward, beer-soaked call to arms celebrating loud, rebellious rock and roll for its own sake; there is little beneath the rallying cry to disturb the neighbors, drink up, and stand up for the music.","sonnet":"A straightforward concert rallying cry — an invitation to the crowd to get loud, drink, ignore the neighbors, and celebrate rock and roll as a communal act of defiance and pleasure.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~steel-town":{"haiku":"Working-class pride and local rootedness—the speaker loves their steel-mill hometown despite its hardship, finding identity in place and community.","opus":"A mill worker refuses to bow to anyone, giving his health to the factory yet counting his pride, his sweetheart and his local pub as riches enough. Airbourne romanticize industrial working-class life, turning hard labor and hometown loyalty into a defiant vow to defend the only place that is truly his.","sonnet":"A steelworker takes pride in a life branded by fire and labor, finding fullness in a high-school sweetheart, a hometown team, and corner-bar friends. The town itself is worth defending — not because it rewards him, but because it is all any of them have.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~too-much-too-young-too-fast":{"haiku":"Power-hungry rulers and thieves use violence and surveillance to control and exploit the masses, leading the narrator to embrace hedonism and excess as a final defiant act before the end. They reject conformity for apocalyptic abandon.","opus":"A hedonistic party anthem set against a paranoid backdrop of surveillance, facial recognition, and rulers who rig the game; the deeper read is defiant living-for-now in a hostile, watched world, choosing to drink it up and go out in style since it may be the end of days anyway.","sonnet":"A live-fast manifesto set against a backdrop of systemic corruption, surveillance paranoia, and street violence — the narrator decides that since everything is rotten and the end is probably near anyway, the only sensible response is to drink hard, burn bright, and go out in style.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~what-s-eatin-you":{"haiku":"Sexual jealousy and infidelity spread like a shared sickness across a household—multiple partners creating a tangle of resentment and unfair odds.","opus":"A cut from Airbourne's debut Runnin' Wild, where the Australian quartet's AC/DC-bred pub-rock powers a leering piece of innuendo. Under the everyday phrasing of the title sits a farce of domestic infidelity and jealousy, the band trading any sincerity for wink-and-grin bravado and the din of a good time had at someone else's expense.","sonnet":"A rowdy song about sexual jealousy and domestic chaos — a father armed with a gun catches someone in bed with his daughter while the mother is having her own affair in the kitchen, and the narrator is caught in the carnage finding it thrilling.","src":"haiku"},
"airbourne~white-line-fever":{"haiku":"Drug trafficking operations move cocaine from South America through Mexico into the United States, driven by profit and addiction.","opus":"The Australian AC/DC devotees ride a cocaine-smuggling narrative, tracing a drug route north from South America across the American map. The title's pun, part open highway, part addiction, drives home a reckless craving the narrator openly admits he can never satisfy.","sonnet":"A drug trafficking operation is traced from Colombia up through Mexico and across the eastern seaboard. The narrator maps the routes with the matter-of-fact pride of a logistics worker, until the final verses collapse geography into vein — the product and the addiction become one and the same thing.","src":"haiku"},
"airmow~nobody-knows":{"haiku":"New love transforms emotional numbness into awakening; the speaker finds unexpected intimacy that reaches places they thought were dead, making them feel seen in a way nobody else ever could.","opus":"Young love that arrives late but heals old loneliness, giving the singer a sense of self they never had. It is about surrendering completely to a feeling of being understood in a way no one else could reach.","sonnet":"A new relationship that rewires the speaker from loneliness to wonder: she found him after years of searching, she makes everything feel easy, and the feeling she gives him — \"a way nobody knows\" — is private and incommunicable, a warmth that follows him wherever he drifts.","src":"haiku"},
"airways~me-and-my-brain":{"haiku":"A mind becomes replaceable, purchasable; trading away one's literal brain to become famous.","opus":"A jittery parable treating the mind as a faulty gadget you can trade in. The narrator ditches the brain that drove him mad, sells it off and buys success in its place, only to reach the top and find nothing feels real anymore. It skewers ambition bought at the price of one's own sanity.","sonnet":"A comic-surreal breakup between a person and his own mind. His brain drives people away, gets sold to a stranger for credit, and is eventually missed only when fame turns hollow and a lightbulb moment arrives that no one can locate anymore. Identity without cognition turns out to be emptier than expected.","src":"haiku"},
"air~cherry-blossom-girl":{"haiku":"A shy lover pines for someone they cannot approach, plagued by illness of longing and fear of rejection, hoping to finally confess their feelings.","opus":"French duo Air trade their usual cool electronics for the ache of a shy crush that never dares announce itself, the whole song built on longing that stays hypothetical because the singer can't cross the room. The tenderness lands precisely because nothing happens.","sonnet":"Simple, aching shyness in the face of a girl the narrator is obsessed with — sick with longing, too afraid to speak, praying for the right moment to arrive while pledging absolute devotion he may never find the courage to express.","src":"haiku"},
"ajr~way-less-sad":{"haiku":"Lowered expectations bring modest peace as the narrator discovers that giving up grand ambition reduces suffering.","opus":"A modest, hard-won climb out of depression where the aim isn't happiness but simply hurting a little less, the bar set deliberately low. The brothers admit the coping is dumb but it's what they've got, and merely not being dead yet counts as okay.","sonnet":"A deliberately lowered bar of wellbeing — not happy, not fixed, not sleeping properly, sheep all dead — but way less sad than before, celebrating incremental improvement over crisis as its own small, genuine victory.","src":"haiku"},
"akaiko-en~a-3tqrf":{"haiku":"A lament on racing toward empty goals—everyone chases nothing and arrives nowhere; now the speaker tries to pull others back.","opus":"With the bittersweet clarity of Japanese guitar pop, the song speaks for the one left behind while everyone else sprinted toward a destination that proved empty. From a guardrail the narrator watches the rush, bloodshot eyes everywhere, calling out for someone to slow down before it is too late. The bitterness belongs to the person who saw the futility early and could make no one listen, arriving nowhere alongside the rest.","sonnet":"A song about being left behind while everyone else sprinted toward a destination that turned out to be empty — the narrator watches the rush from a guardrail, bloodshot eyes everywhere, calling out to someone to slow down before it's too late. The emotional core is the bitterness of the person who saw the futility early and couldn't make anyone listen.","src":"haiku"},
"akino-with-bless4~a-1li4kgn":{"opus":"AKINO's roots in sweeping anime theme songs shape this mythic love across cosmic time, ten thousand years of loving one person through cycle after cycle of reincarnation. The narrator crossed endless sorrow to reach this person and wants real pain and real joy at their side rather than to float untouched. The vow's tenderness is its inversion of grandeur: incompleteness, shared, is offered as everything worth eternity.","sonnet":"A mythic love song spanning cosmic time — ten thousand years of loving someone across reincarnations — where the narrator crossed endless cycles of sorrow to reach this person and wants to feel real pain and real joy alongside them rather than floating untouched; the emotional core is that incompleteness shared together is everything.","src":"sonnet"},
"akino~a-19ppqj7":{"opus":"Reviving the Macross 7 song Tenku no Elysion, AKINO reframes it as an eternal vow: twelve thousand years of love already given, a billion more to come. Once a killer angel before knowing this person, the narrator now finds music filling even hell. Across every reincarnation the beloved's name keeps being sung, with the single plea that they not be hurt. The anime lineage turns devotion into scripture written in divine time.","sonnet":"A cover of the Macross 7 song \"Tenku no Elysion\" reframed: the narrator has loved someone for twelve thousand years and will continue for a billion more — they were a killer angel before knowing this person, and now music fills even their hell; the song is an eternal vow of love spanning divine time, asking the beloved not to be hurt while the narrator keeps singing their name across every reincarnation.","src":"sonnet"},
"alabama-shakes~don-t-wanna-fight":{"haiku":"Exhaustion from constant conflict and endless work. The speaker and partner are worn down and seek peace rather than continued struggle.","opus":"A plea to end a pointless, exhausting conflict driven by pride, widening into a lament about the grind of survival; the narrator is worn out by constant fighting and by working herself to death just to keep the water and power on, wanting relief more than winning.","sonnet":"A song about relational exhaustion — two people are locked in endless conflict over incompatible needs and who owes what, while the narrator is ground down by the labour of keeping everything running and can't bear another round.","src":"haiku"},
"alannah-myles~black-velvet":{"haiku":"The song eulogizes Elvis Presley as a charismatic force of sexuality and charm that transcended normal life and left permanent impact through his mystique.","opus":"Alannah Myles conjures a sultry Southern tribute to a rock and roll idol whose smile and slow style felt like a new religion before he vanished too soon. The nods to Memphis and a tender ballad frame it clearly as an elegy for Elvis Presley. The Canadian singer's smoky delivery turned this into a bluesy, atmospheric hit rooted in mythologised American memory.","sonnet":"A portrait of Elvis Presley as a near-sacred Southern phenomenon — a boy from Mississippi whose voice and movement affected women like religious conversion. The song mourns how brief his passage was while insisting the spell he cast has never fully lifted.","src":"haiku"},
"alazka~dead-end":{"haiku":"Two people pushed away those close to them, now trapped in isolation with no escape from pain, shame, or their broken origins.","opus":"Alazka chart a romance arriving at its final stop, two people who took their mutual sparring too far until nothing remained to salvage. The narrator sits with the ache of driving someone off and the realization that people shaped by a fractured upbringing must now go it alone. The German post-hardcore band treats the breakup as tired, resigned acceptance rather than fury.","sonnet":"Two people arrive at the terminal point of a relationship, each having pushed the other away through games and evasion until nothing remains. The narrator accepts that holding on to pain leads nowhere, yet leaving means carrying the wreckage of who they were together.","src":"haiku"},
"alborn~push":{"haiku":"Protective distance becomes cruelty as the speaker pushes away connection to avoid vulnerability, paradoxically creating the abandonment they most feared.","opus":"A relationship that once felt like shelter turns into invasion, the speaker needing help to fend off a partner who creeps back in and hollows him out. His bitter gratitude for being pulled under and his question of who holds the reins frame it as a struggle to take himself back from manipulation.","sonnet":"Gratitude twisted into anger at someone who entered a safe haven uninvited and turned the speaker into nothing: the speaker needed someone to push the intruder away, and thanks them bitterly for the attempt to drag them under — now asking who really holds control.","src":"haiku"},
"alcest~protection":{"haiku":"Night becomes armor against inner chaos; the sea's vastness protects the speaker, offering refuge while they battle invisible torment.","opus":"A French vision of nature as armor against inner torment, the swells of the sea shielding the speaker as the day strangles and shadows pierce through. He wrestles with roaring darkness inside, arming himself with cold blades to face the beasts within.","sonnet":"Night falls like a canvas collapsing, inner roarings break out, and the speaker wrestles shadows that pierce through — but the swells of the sea are armour, protecting when the day strangles, offering a shield of cold blades against the beasts within.","src":"haiku"},
"alcest~sapphire":{"opus":"True to Alcest's blackgaze practice, where the voice is treated as another wash of guitar rather than a vehicle for words, the piece offers invented or ceremonial syllables with no translatable content. Meaning is deferred to texture: shoegaze radiance and metal weight carry whatever the lyrics decline to state. The vocal becomes pure sound, an instrument of atmosphere, so the emotional narrative lives entirely in tone and dynamics.","sonnet":"The lyrics appear to be invented or ceremonial syllables with no translatable semantic content in any identifiable language — the song functions as pure sonic texture rather than a discernible lyrical narrative.","src":"sonnet"},
"alchemize~weight-in-gold":{"haiku":"Trauma and isolation yield reluctant wisdom; the speaker reflects on childhood pain while slowly rebuilding trust.","opus":"A hard-won reckoning with a depressive youth, forgiving one's parents their guilt and admitting the masking never fully stops. The scars stay, but the lessons carry real value; recovery framed as ongoing, not finished.","sonnet":"Looking back at a youth of depression, isolation, and self-loathing, the speaker addresses forgiving parents and admits to still masking in the present — \"people keep assuming I've found my feet\" — while holding onto hard-won lessons worth their weight in gold.","src":"haiku"},
"aldn~pressure-with-chloe-moriondo":{"haiku":"Desire crystallized into paralysis where choice becomes torture and indecision breeds dislocation. Both parties question perception and authenticity while pressure builds toward silent breakdown.","opus":"Hyperpop dread about desire under surveillance, where wanting someone feels like being watched, judged, offered up for sacrifice, and the repeated plea about deceiving eyes turns longing into paranoid self-doubt.","sonnet":"Two voices orbit the paralysing tension of unspoken desire: one asks whether the pressure of watched eyes and indecision is torture or slaughter, the other admits the pain is too much to live with yet trivial — both repeatedly asking if their eyes deceive them about what the other wants.","src":"haiku"},
"alec-empire~everything-starts-with-a-fuck":{"haiku":"Rage and resistance as primal force burning through systems of control and hypocrisy. The narrator channels burning revolution against institutions built on deception.","opus":"Digital Hardcore agitprop from Atari Teenage Riot's frontman, aiming raw obscenity at American hypocrisy and the myth of freedom, insisting that real change begins only with a violent refusal to comply.","sonnet":"A short, explosive declaration of agency in a land of rising hypocrisy: the speaker is burning inside, cuts right through with no more chances, and insists that walls can be broken if the will is there — raw energy as political stance.","src":"haiku"},
"alec-empire~intelligence-and-sacrifice":{"haiku":"Isolation and betrayal from those claiming closeness while the narrator fights alone. Heroes fail and destiny crumbles as pain replaces the false mythology of ascension.","opus":"Empire's noise-punk manifesto against hero worship and passivity, framing personal rage and isolation as the price of refusing surrender, screaming at listeners to wake up rather than wait for someone to save them.","sonnet":"Intelligence and sacrifice are the twin demands of an extreme interior struggle: isolation as the only relief from pain, a sense of destiny stolen, heroes scorned, and a closing command to wake up — the whole song a circuit of rage, isolation, and refusal to surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"alec-empire~tear-it-out":{"haiku":"Viral persistence where the narrator infects despite attempts to destroy him. Resurrection becomes inevitable as the speaker rises repeatedly from attempted obliteration.","opus":"A defiant survival anthem built on the idea that every attempt to destroy him only makes him rise, spreading through his enemy like an infection and dragging them down alongside him.","sonnet":"Defiant survival addressed to an adversary who keeps trying to kill the speaker: every attempt only produces a rise, the response is to tear out the adversary's heart and drive straight to hell — confrontation as catharsis.","src":"haiku"},
"alessia-cara~scars-to-your-beautiful":{"haiku":"Internalized beauty standards breed self-destruction; the speaker offers mirror-work and radical acceptance to counter cultural cruelty.","opus":"A young woman starving herself toward an impossible image, and Cara's argument that the fix is not the girl but the world's warped idea of beauty. Written from real proximity to that ache, it insists worth was never on the surface.","sonnet":"A meditation on the way young women are trapped by impossible beauty standards, starving and hiding their pain behind perfected surfaces, addressed directly by a speaker who wants to be their mirror and show them the light that shines within — that they are already whole and the world should change, not them.","src":"haiku"},
"alesti~intoxicated":{"haiku":"Emotional dependency mimics chemical addiction; another's presence becomes narcotic escape from internal void.","opus":"Metalcore that renders a consuming love as literal chemical dependency, surrendering control to a high that both saves and endangers. The thrill of being wanted blurs into the danger of losing yourself in the craving.","sonnet":"A person functions for the speaker like a drug: lifting gravity, filling cravings, raising the possibility of escape from inner emptiness — \"blood boiling, weightless\" — the dependency rendered as both danger and salvation simultaneously.","src":"haiku"},
"alestorm~chomp-chomp":{"haiku":"Pirates get mauled by alligators in a swamp; a cautionary tale played for laughs.","opus":"A pirate-metal band cheerfully abandoning nautical menace for death-by-alligator, treating gruesome carnage as a singalong. It exists to be dumb fun, and knows it, which is the whole point.","sonnet":"A mock-heroic tall tale of pirates shipwrecked in an alligator-infested swamp — the crew is eaten one by one, and the sole survivor narrates the carnage with grim comedy, warning everyone that no weapon or courage is sufficient against the crocodile.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-klingle~colors-back":{"haiku":"A fading connection between lovers slips away as intimacy fades and fragility sets in. The song captures the desperate wish to restore lost color and feeling in a dying bond.","opus":"A hazy electronic love song about intimacy briefly restoring vividness after things had faded, touch bringing the color back. It works more as sensation than statement, chasing a fleeting reconnection.","sonnet":"Two people in a fragile, fading connection experience a moment of unexpected renewed feeling — \"colors back\" — that briefly restores the sense of closeness between them. The lyrics are fragmentary and impressionistic, evoking physical touch and the fear of drifting apart.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-klingle~colors-back-radio-edit":{"haiku":"Fading connection and loss accelerate as time separates two people crossing into irreversible distance.","opus":"A fragile reunion sung in broken, dreamlike English, two people fading fast yet feeling the colors return through touch. The fear that crossing again will hurt is outweighed by the pull of breathing as one.","sonnet":"About a relationship fading fast under the pressure of time and fear, but a sudden renewal of feeling — the speaker and their partner were breathing as one, and when that connection returns even briefly, the world gets its color back.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-megan-mcduffee~hologram-lover":{"haiku":"Artificial love mimics emotional authenticity while circuitry hums beneath; joy exists in loops until malfunction ends the simulation.","opus":"A synth-tinged romance between an artificial being and its beloved, aching to be more than code, questioning whether its joy and heartbeat are real. Nods to Blade Runner give it a wistful ache over love that may be program.","sonnet":"An AI or digital entity questions its own reality — 'am I real?' — while insisting on genuine emotion: the hologram lover experiences joy, heartbeat, even malfunction, and the 'loading program' refrain both undercuts and deepens the claim to love.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-rachel-mcalpine~game-over":{"haiku":"Virtual immersion and power fantasy masking violence and control. Freedom translates to dominance while fingers hover on triggers, waiting for the game to load.","opus":"Synthwave dressed in first-person-shooter imagery, treating the loading screen and finger on the trigger as a release from the past, where letting everything go feels like both freedom and a kind of surrender to the void.","sonnet":"Fingers on the trigger and metal smoke frame a gaming or battle scenario in which past echoes, darkness, and zombie-like forces converge at the moment of loaded readiness — power and freedom asserted in the instant before the game loads, the trigger pulled.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-sloane~mine":{"haiku":"Possessive fantasy of transformation and ownership through dreams where compliance occurs without resistance. Reality offers no such control, forcing the dreamer to retreat into unrequited fantasy.","opus":"The quietly disturbing fantasy of possessing and remaking a partner, only fully realized in sleep, where the dreamer clings to a version of love that requires the other person to change into something they aren't.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped inside a dream relationship they can only possess while asleep — dreaming of all the ways to change someone and make them theirs — begging not to be woken because in that dream state everything between them is okay.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~chains":{"haiku":"Internal struggle with paralysis and unheard inner voices; the speaker urges listeners to break free from complacency and denial that trap them.","opus":"This track wrestles with despair and self-liberation, urging a listener who sees no reason to strive to release the chains holding them back. Amid buried pain and fallen comrades, the repeated question of whether they will ever hear their own inner voice becomes the pivot between resignation and survival.","sonnet":"Inner voices go unheard while people collapse under the weight of suppressed desire and accumulated loss. The narrator watches others fall and cannot pull them back — freedom requires releasing the chains they cling to, and the will to do so must come from within. Survival is self-authored or it is nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~eraser":{"haiku":"Suffering perpetuates itself as cyclical truth; the speaker realizes nothing would change if time rewound. Every discovery confirms the same hollow meaninglessness beneath every layer of reality.","opus":"A brooding rejection of someone who's become a stranger, insisting that starting over would change nothing because it was always the same. Suffering framed as the price of listening and questioning, with no clean escape.","sonnet":"Post-breakup disillusionment stripped to a blunt verdict: every path leads back to the same place, the narrator wouldn't change a thing but can't trust what's real anymore, and the collapsed middle section — \"TLDR / You're nothing / Go fuck yourself\" — is the point where politeness runs out.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~fade":{"haiku":"Entropy overtakes hope; the speaker accepts dissolution while mysteries and warnings herald the end of consciousness and meaning.","opus":"A cold acceptance of an ending, reading the signs and letting the whole thing go dark. The regret sharpens into accusation, blaming the other for never savoring what was simple and good before the strings were finally pulled.","sonnet":"Minimal and bleak: whatever the other person does or thinks, it is the end — the writing on the wall is already there, and all that remains is fading to black. The brief question about why they couldn't enjoy simple things arrives without answer and without sentiment.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~ghost":{"haiku":"A presence persists despite distance and disconnection, haunting the narrator as a ghost that both sustains and torments, resisting all attempts to let go.","opus":"Addressed to someone who keeps resisting even as the singer stands beside them, insisting they are no longer really present. The calm before the storm is the moment before he stops waiting and points to the open door.","sonnet":"About someone who is present but not really there — the narrator reaches out, insists they are by this person's side, but the person keeps defying and pulling away, as if already a ghost. The emotional center is the frustration of loving someone who has already left in every way that matters, even if they are still physically standing there.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~glass":{"haiku":"Sacrifice defines existence; choices create meaning through loss. The speaker warns that waiting corrupts—decision demands abandoning alternatives.","opus":"A cold dismissal of someone the speaker is fed up with, telling them to burn as what they deserve and reminding them they're on their own. The recurring line about not noticing when nothing's wrong hints at a relationship recognized only in its wreckage, its meaning made only by sacrifice.","sonnet":"A spare address to someone who needs to choose their sacrifice and leave the rest — action taken now rather than waited for — ending with cold clarity: \"it is not of my concern, you are here on your own.\"","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~isolated":{"haiku":"External judgment and self-doubt converge—each step breeds conflict while betrayed trust poisons hope for redemption.","opus":"An interrogation of someone acting on others' approval while their conscience revolts, hands bloodied again. It presses the question of whether righteous violence is ever worth it once you've lost sight of what you wanted.","sonnet":"A disillusionment with a choice made or a path taken — hands covered in blood, 'make believe that we will comply' — and the speaker questions whether the person they're addressing truly understands the cost, while suspecting they'll hide rather than face what's wrong.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~lina":{"haiku":"Trauma rewires the self into weaponry: wounds become illumination and refusal becomes rebellion, rejecting the abusers' power by speaking for yourself despite the scars they left.","opus":"An anthem for a survivor of abuse: the scars become a light to navigate by, and refusing to forgive is reframed as the act that keeps you standing. The Ukrainian line, a vow never to forgive, is the spine of it.","sonnet":"A bilingual song about surviving someone who inflicted trauma through control and attention that felt like gifts but were really pain — the speaker refuses forgiveness, channels their wounds into strength, and vows to speak for themselves rather than be broken.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~monochrome":{"haiku":"Black-and-white thinking imprisons those who refuse nuance; the speaker resists imposed binaries and demands multicolored perception and complexity.","opus":"A refusal to be flattened into someone else's black-and-white worldview, borrowing cold managerial mantras to expose the denial behind them. It challenges a coward stuck in a narrow life to admit change is still possible.","sonnet":"A speaker who has wasted years waiting for someone's relief and approval decides the other person's denial is no longer their problem — \"you will not make me colorblind\" is the pivot — and the advice to hire slow, fire fast and stick to your guns is directed inward as much as outward, a declaration of self-governance after too long in someone else's grey scale.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~no-king-rules-forever":{"haiku":"Temporal inevitability erodes all dominion; no ruler escapes the cycles of consequence and collapse that define human existence.","opus":"A patient reckoning addressed to a tyrant whose time has run out, insisting every move now is in vain. The cruelest line is that the cage was self-built; the power he thought exempted him is exactly what traps him.","sonnet":"An address to a power that has lingered too long, lost track of time, and built a cage it thought was for others: the refrain \"it's what we've left for you\" turns the ruin into inheritance, and the title's axiom is the verdict — no reign, however entrenched, survives the patience of those it has ground down.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~reality-check":{"haiku":"Confronting comfortable lies and false immortality, sickened by someone's refusal to face real consequences.","opus":"A reckoning with someone living in willful self-delusion, their lies making the narrator sick. The Ukrainian lines sharpen it toward moral debt, warning that no good comes until blood is paid and gratitude wakes.","sonnet":"Addressed to someone living in self-deception and make-believe, the speaker is made sick by witnessing habitual lying — Ukrainian lines warn that an ungrateful soul sees no good — and insists reality will eventually break through the bubble of false comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~red-alert":{"haiku":"A call to consciousness addresses people numbed through missed chances, watching younger selves become ghosts while those in power remain indifferent — the Ukrainian refrain \"we have not yet died\" affirms survival and identity against erasure.","opus":"A bitter look back at chances squandered through numbness, watching people become ghosts of their younger selves. The Ukrainian anthem line roots it in wartime defiance, framing survival itself as evolution's answer.","sonnet":"Collective numbness during a moment of historical crisis — when we had the chance, we were too numb to act, and now people become ghosts of their younger selves. The Ukrainian line 'Ще не вмерли ми' (we have not yet perished) breaks the English resignation with defiant historical memory.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~roots-grow-deep":{"haiku":"Ukrainian resilience against legacy systems—collective action cuts historical corruption; ashes of the past enable rebirth from darkness.","opus":"A Ukrainian call to burn away the deep-rooted legacy of the Soviet past even amid war. Written in wartime, it insists the ashes won't settle the old way, urging listeners to cut out the roots of evil and hold to their chosen path.","sonnet":"A Ukrainian song about cutting out the roots of evil that have grown into a people — referencing war passing but urging the listener not to forget and to burn away everything inherited from centuries of subjugation, choosing their own path forward.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~strength-in-numbers":{"haiku":"Unity and collective power; strength emerges only through unified action.","opus":"A hardcore stand against being overpowered by force of numbers, refusing to be saved from itself and vowing to live its own way whatever the cost. Collective defiance that answers intimidation with unbroken resolve.","sonnet":"A collective refusal — we will stand upright, we don't ask to be saved from ourselves — addressed to a power that has run out of time: this time we will make you pay, united we stand, and whatever it takes, we'll live our way.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~the-spark":{"haiku":"Hunger blinds; the speaker offers truth while their audience wastes themselves. Responsibility lies dormant; regret awaits those too lazy to choose themselves.","opus":"An exasperated address to a starving, frantic person who refuses to hear the plain answer, alert only while squandering time and shirking what they owe. The push to decide and confront reality carries the weary tenderness of watching someone wreck themselves out in the open.","sonnet":"A speaker calls out someone who wastes their wakefulness, refuses responsibility, and already knows the truth but won't acknowledge it — urging them to make up their mind before it is too late, promising that seeing the truth will reveal who they really are and change everything.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~the-view":{"haiku":"Searching for reasons deepens descent into self; forgetting requires active surrender. The speaker insists on releasing perspectives to change the view, to become awake.","opus":"A confrontation with regret and the human urge to keep our failures safely outside ourselves, the speaker pushing someone to quit assigning blame and at least attempt a shift in view. Regret becomes a slow, resisted awakening we dodge by hunting for reasons.","sonnet":"Regret is examined from every angle — what is its other side, is it punishment, can it be forgotten — and the speaker insists: no one is to blame but yourself, stop explaining away distance, let go while you still can, and at least try to shift your perspective.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~waiting-for-the-end":{"haiku":"Systemic conformity breeding uniformity where resistance requires community. The collective refuses capitulation through solidarity, all waiting for the collapse together.","opus":"A solidarity anthem for people trapped by their own choices and manufactured desires, insisting on unbending kinship even as everyone drifts toward collapse, mistaking wants for needs while losing everything.","sonnet":"A solidarity anthem for those blinded by too much choice while losing everything: the speaker insists they are not slaves, that they never bend, and that waiting together for some terminal reckoning is the only honest posture when nothing real is at stake.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~who-is-who":{"haiku":"Friendships dissolve under scrutiny—the speaker sees through false loyalty and withdraws into solitude, expecting betrayal.","opus":"A cold warning to a false friend who only picked a side, told plainly he's not wanted and will hide and fail rather than stand when it counts. The chorus sorts allies from cowards, taunting that raw ability means nothing without the nerve to use it.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses someone whose loyalty has shifted and who will hide rather than resist when tested; memory of when 'every move you made led to light' sharpens the present condemnation — you are stuck, cornered, and no friend, and the speaker is done waiting for them to act.","src":"haiku"},
"alex-yarmak~worth":{"haiku":"Breaking silence about buried shame and flying beyond fear toward daybreak and self-worth reclaimed.","opus":"Grief and guilt swept under the rug that refuse to stay buried, pride left behind as the narrator resolves to rise above it. Sung partly in Ukrainian, it reaches for release and sunrise past the weight.","sonnet":"A relationship or situation where humiliation has replaced elevation, shame keeps pulling someone back down, and the speaker urges rising above it all until sunrise — 'we'll fly above it' — while Ukrainian lines acknowledge that happiness hides in silence from the world.","src":"haiku"},
"alexandros~spit":{"haiku":"Childhood trauma metastasizes into adult rage; the speaker remains trapped in a nightmare, wrestling with memories of harm and unable to escape the monster within.","opus":"A survivor discovers that a settled life with kids and forgiveness hasn't buried an old abuser, who returns nightly as a monster at the window. The contempt he spits is proof he never escaped the nightmare he thought he'd outgrown.","sonnet":"About the persistence of trauma from someone who did terrible things to the narrator — despite believing it was over, despite building a life with kids and apparent forgiveness, the dreams keep coming and the monster keeps knocking at the window. Every time the narrator thinks of this person they spit on their grave in rage; the damage never fully resolves no matter how much time passes.","src":"haiku"},
"alexis-munroe~burn":{"haiku":"Familiar eyes grow cold; the speaker bleeds while being watched burn. Connection corrupts into spectacle; toxin spreads inward from external fire.","opus":"The chilling recognition that a lover has changed, watching the speaker burn without caring while she goes colder even under his hands. The venom and unanswered questions of where he's been make this the moment love turns to cruelty, intimacy no longer felt as anything familiar.","sonnet":"A relationship has become unrecognisable — looking into the partner's eyes reveals a stranger, hands on the body feel colder than before, their venom burns from within — and the speaker is left bleeding, watched but not helped, set on fire and simply observed.","src":"haiku"},
"alexis-munroe~never-be-the-same":{"haiku":"Betrayal by a mentor and lover who taught and destroyed simultaneously. The narrator survives the burning of their halo but remains fundamentally altered by trauma.","opus":"The reckoning after an older mentor-lover who built her up only to teach her to lie and then break her; she survives the damage but knows the person she was is gone for good.","sonnet":"A younger person addresses the older figure who built them up and broke them down — teacher, liar, halo-burner — arriving at survival (\"but I'm alive\") while still asking why they were left and whether they can ever recover the person they were before.","src":"haiku"},
"alexisonfire~no-rest":{"haiku":"Restless wanderers work hard, move like no one else, and traverse dangerous paths from birth to grave without knowing their destination.","opus":"A restless, road-worn anthem for outsiders who carve their own route from cradle to grave and reject any settled, conventional existence. The refrain flips the usual proverb, granting the damned long lives and the blessed none, framing their reckless, driven way of living as both a curse and a kind of freedom.","sonnet":"A restless, world-weary anthem about a group of people who move differently from everyone else — born to roam with no clear destination, dangerous by nature, and refusing to rest even as blessed outcasts.","src":"haiku"},
"alexisonfire~reverse-the-curse":{"haiku":"The speaker attempts reversal but discovers curses run too deep to undo.","opus":"A post-hardcore push through torment toward the life you actually wanted, feeling the current of existence deep in the bones. It reframes an old vice as a manageable habit and treats survival itself as breaking a curse.","sonnet":"A push through specters and suffering toward a world the speaker still believes in — beyond the pleasures and woes, beyond the labour of love, a current of life felt deep in the bones. The meadow of flies and the fistic talk with godhood are stations on a path that keeps being reversed, but the reversal is the practice, not the failure.","src":"haiku"},
"alexisonfire~sans-soleil":{"haiku":"Sunless depression and urban isolation dissolving into acceptance of grief. Two survivors acknowledge the darkness and find solace in mutual beginning again together.","opus":"A survivor of depression thanking the person who cared for him when he couldn't stand himself, admitting that self-kindness is harder than loving anyone else, and that their lives have grown so entwined the line between them has dissolved.","sonnet":"Days without sun in a city apartment, the smell of someone on sheets after weeks of silence: the speaker admits it is easier to love another than be kind to oneself, credits a partner with loving them through self-loathing, and finds — in that debt — the place where one self ends and a renewed one begins.","src":"haiku"},
"alexisonfire~sweet-dreams-of-otherness":{"haiku":"Memory becomes fractured and strange as the speaker floats between worlds, unmoored from consensus reality and listening to unintelligible signals. Losing grip on sanity becomes its own kind of freedom.","opus":"A haunted vision of a generation born into the aftermath of others' wars, standing strange amid ghosts and beige conformity while straining to hear a voice that won't come. Alienation and the fear of losing one's mind.","sonnet":"Post-war disorientation and alienation: the narrator stands \"strange in a sea of beige,\" holding wartime memories and youthful noise that no longer translate, dreaming of otherness while exhaust fills the purple sky and the ability to hear or connect keeps slipping away.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~affliction":{"haiku":"Desire calls endlessly; the speaker answers nightly, knowing hunger drives both. Craving becomes destiny repeated.","opus":"A late-night siren-call to someone whose desire is their own undoing, the speaker positioned as the bad decision they keep making. Falling like ashes, they know exactly what he is and come anyway, addiction and temptation fused into one figure answering the phone.","sonnet":"A voice calling in the middle of the night draws the speaker back like ash falling to ground; the caller knows what the speaker is, knows about bad decisions, and desire itself is named as the affliction — a pull that recurs regardless of what either person knows.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~affliction-instrumental":{"haiku":"Obsessive calling and desire as curse that binds the narrator to another. Affliction becomes reciprocal infection where knowing feeds compulsion.","opus":"A late-night summons framed as temptation, where desire is named as the other person's weakness and the singer plays the knowing, dangerous answer to their bad decisions.","sonnet":"A brief, hypnotic declaration: someone keeps calling in the night, falls like ashes, and the listener already knows — desire is their affliction, bad decisions their nature, and the voice just confirms what they cannot escape.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~akuma":{"haiku":"Dual forces in harmony: freedom and surrender flow together as rhythm and motion fuse with cosmic inevitability into unified truth.","opus":"A trance-like reach for freedom in movement, treating rhythm and open water as a truth felt rather than reasoned. The pull is toward dissolving into flow.","sonnet":"A song about two people finding truth and freedom together in motion — rippling through sensation, losing mental noise, and arriving at something real through shared movement and rhythm.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~akuma-v2":{"haiku":"Ocean's rhythm syncs with sky as two lives intertwine seeking soothing truth. Motion carries meaning; rivers of consciousness flow together.","opus":"A brief, atmospheric meditation on movement and truth, freedom felt as the mind's rivers loosen and rhythm fills the midnight sky. More a mood than a narrative, it reaches for a sense of release and certainty glimpsed from a distance.","sonnet":"Impressionistic fragments of night sky, ocean ripples, and moving rivers of the mind coalesce into a sense of freedom and soothing truth — \"I can feel it from a mile, it's truth\" — more mood-piece than narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~akuma-v3-tech-noir-live-2017-version":{"src":"web","web":"A dark synthwave invocation built around the demon (akuma) — rhythm, movement and the mind's flow as a kind of possession, dressed in neon-soaked cyberpunk.","fable":"A retrowave instrumental with just enough words to give the synths a pulse to ride, this is less a lyric than an incantation: one eight-line stanza about ripples, midnight, and movement, cycled until the phrasing stops meaning and starts feeling. The words themselves reach for a vague transcendence — freedom in how bodies move, some truth sensed from a mile off — but they're deliberately soft-focus, the sound of a state rather than an argument about it. What the track actually does happens in its collapse: verse after identical verse peels away until only two lines are left ping-ponging, the certainty of feeling truth against the freedom of motion, the song narrowing to a loop small enough for a live crowd to breathe inside. In a scene built on neon-noir atmosphere, the emptiness is the point — the mantra is a vehicle, and its refusal to develop is what lets the groove carry the meaning the words decline to fix.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The key names a real event: this is the live cut from ALEX's Tech Noir 2017 set — AKUMA is the 2017 ALEX & Tokyo Rose album track, sung by Rachel McAlpine — released on its own in 2021.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","freedom & escape"],"means":"the soft-focus mantra a vehicle the words decline to fix","built":"verses strip away until two lines ping-pong as the song's entire content"},
"alex~antagonist":{"haiku":"Resentment toward the undeserving successful burns as the speaker claims vindication through moral superiority and destiny. The blade cuts deep into hypocritical thrones built on inherited advantage.","opus":"A venomous callout of a rival handed everything who now spits on those with less, chasing status while forgetting the climb that made him. Self-vindication sharpened by class resentment and a flat refusal of a hollow apology.","sonnet":"A ferocious indictment of someone ungrateful and famous who spits on those with less: the narrator was left in the depths but carries a scar the target couldn't wear, watches their sordid legacy breed disease, and waits to be vindicated — \"bite down and say what you mean to my face\" is the demand that runs through the whole confrontation.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~battleground":{"haiku":"Exhaustion breaks resolve; the speaker walks away from combat recognizing that trying harder achieves nothing but mutual ruin.","opus":"A pop-rock breakup at the exhausted point of no return, walking away because the other person never did enough to make staying worth it. The weariness of running a dying relationship into the ground carries more weight than anger.","sonnet":"A relationship already in attrition reaches its exit point: the speaker walks out after the partner failed to convince them to stay, refuses another battle, and challenges the other to say anything — the goodbye posed as a question neither of them can answer well enough.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~broadway":{"haiku":"A person finds companionship in their own demons rather than seeking genuine connection, dwelling in urban isolation and introspection.","opus":"A theatrical song about show-business isolation, where the performer treats his own darkness as something that lifts him rather than drags him down, refusing to banish his private demons because they keep him company in his solitude. Sleepless and aching under the city's dazzle, he craves an anonymity that fame will never allow.","sonnet":"A person haunted by their own demons choosing to keep them close rather than cast them out, because loneliness is worse. Broadway is both literal neon overload and inner theater — bright lights that blind, darkness that steadies. The city becomes a mirror and a companion; sleep won't come, breathing is difficult, and the only honest move is to ghost-walk through all of it.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~cursed":{"haiku":"Supernatural possession and psychological manipulation conflate into single torment where release seems impossible. The curse operates through touch and voice, binding two figures in mutual obsession.","opus":"Attraction reframed as a spell or possession, where being wanted feels like being marked and held down; beneath the occult language it's really about clinging to someone precisely because they'll leave, needing the ache so as not to forget them.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses a presence — half supernatural, half romantic — whose nails press down, whose words echo like a curse, whose gaze is felt in a room of onlookers; they accept being cornered by whatever was cast on them, wishing only that this person had stayed.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~cursed-ii":{"haiku":"Repetition of supernatural pressure and emotional restriction mirrors the previous curse. The cycle continues as witnesses watch and the speaker remains pressed against intrusive forces.","opus":"A stripped reprise of the same enchantment, all watching eyes and a candle burning too bright, urging a hesitant partner not to overthink a bond that only holds if neither of them treats it as a joke.","sonnet":"A continuation of the same bewitchment: eyes are watching, candles burn twice as bright as intended, and a voice at the base of an oak tree must be silenced to let whatever spell was cast run its course — the speaker consents to being pressed and held, asking if it should finally be set free.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~game-over":{"haiku":"Zombified existence demands transformation through violence and transcendence; the speaker chooses annihilation over continued undeath.","opus":"Desire framed as booting up a game and pulling the trigger — liberation and strength found in dropping the past and firing into the dark. Nocturnal ghouls arrive to sink their teeth into a wounded heart, blurring gaming, violence and release into a single ritual of finality.","sonnet":"A song about reaching a decisive moment of release — the speaker frames it in gaming and weapon metaphors, treating the crossing of some threshold as both freedom and power, suggesting a final act of surrender to darkness that feels paradoxically liberating.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~hologram-lover":{"haiku":"A simulated relationship becomes indistinguishable from reality; digital love satisfies despite its artificial nature.","opus":"An artificial being questioning whether its love and joy are real or just programming. The Blade Runner echo of tears in rain sharpens the ache: a hologram longing to be more than code even as it loads and malfunctions.","sonnet":"An AI or hologram falling in love and questioning whether its feelings are real — it experiences joy, warmth, and connection with a human but keeps asking 'am I real?' even as its heart beats and its systems malfunction under the weight of genuine emotion.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~into-the-night":{"haiku":"Magical chaos and danger spread while ordinary people lose their footing. The narrator channels disorder and insecurity, money becomes refuge from the upheaval.","opus":"A hazy, allusive Steve Miller-style ride through misty magic and small-town omens, where a little knowledge turns dangerous and the only rule is to grab the cash and vanish into the dark.","sonnet":"A collage of cryptic, nocturnal figures — tin-pot wizard, beady eagle, a drunk naked woman, semantic corridors, Little Oscar paying costs — woven around the repeated advice to \"take the money and run\" and chase it into the night before truth arrives too late.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~like-dynamite":{"haiku":"Heat ignites internally; the speaker recognizes how bodies speak what lips deny. Combustion escalates when pretense ends—dynamite detonates desire.","opus":"A seducer's boast of total control, claiming to be the gasoline and the object of every secret dream for someone who pretends indifference while wanting them. The other person's denials are treated as proof of how completely they are hooked.","sonnet":"A speaker who sees through a partner's fantasy and midnight disappearances knows the other's body and name are called out in private moments of heat — \"you're like dynamite\" — and the controlling metaphor makes plain that desire, not indifference, is what the partner is hiding.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~love-story-instrumental":{"haiku":"True love breaks curses through transcendent faith; a soldier rescues his maiden from frozen time through the power of devotion.","opus":"A cover of Taylor Swift's fairy-tale reworking of Romeo and Juliet, recast so the star-crossed lovers get the ending Shakespeare denied them. Young love as rescue, the anxiety of waiting resolved by a proposal instead of a tomb.","sonnet":"A fairy-tale narrative: a lonely soldier travelling to claim a throne encounters a maiden escaped from a dark tower, falls instantly in love, then must fight the undead to break a curse and free her — 'just a kiss to turn a spell away' — before they live together in brighter days.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~loves-other-name":{"haiku":"Intimate understanding as love's truest foundation where knowledge itself becomes the deepest bond. Recognition and mutual comprehension create the light sustaining connection.","opus":"A plain gratitude song for a partner who simply gets you, arguing that being truly understood is what love actually is, the quiet mutual knowing that keeps a bond intact.","sonnet":"The thesis is announced in the title and repeated throughout: understanding is love's other name, a light that brightens the flame; a relationship built on genuinely knowing what makes the other tick, on mutual knowing that goes beyond tricks, is the only bond that lasts.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~narcissist":{"haiku":"Narcissistic cruelty wears masks of civility while inflicting constant harm; the speaker sees through the performance and confronts what they've been forced to become.","opus":"A portrait of a manipulator who wears a mask, refuses accountability, and only offers empathy when his own insecurity demands it. The narrator sees through the lies and names what this person has made them become.","sonnet":"A direct address to a person who oscillates between hot and cold, wears a mask of charm, and refuses to acknowledge the damage they have done — the speaker sees through the performance, names the pattern as narcissism, and articulates how this person only seeks empathy toward themselves when their own insecurity demands it.","src":"haiku","fable":"This song's real move is the twist buried inside its accusations. The speaker catalogues a narcissist's tells — the refusal to own up, the meticulously fitted mask, the way warmth snaps to indifference before the person thins into breath off glass — with the clean confidence of someone who has already decoded every alibi. But the second verse quietly turns the knife inward, admitting the speaker matters only when the other's insecurity spikes hard enough to squeeze out a thimble of borrowed empathy. That confession poisons the clarity: the closing want-list, where the speaker keeps wishing the narcissist would unlatch the sealed room, sit inside the damage, and finally reckon with what they've forged the speaker into, reads less as a bid for justice than as proof of the tether that outlasts all that hard-won clarity. The title points outward; the ache is that pointing changes nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"accusation that turns the knife inward and proves the tether holds","built":"title points outward while the lyric's confession collapses that direction"},
"alex~narcissist-instrumental":{"haiku":"Calling out the narcissist's mask and performance—they only need you when insecurity overwhelms their cruelty.","opus":"A portrait of a self-absorbed manipulator locked behind a face he cannot remove, doling out affection only when his own fragility calls for it. The narrator has finally seen past the deceit and confronts what the whole ordeal turned them into.","sonnet":"About confronting someone who wears empathy as a mask and only returns when their own insecurity demands attention — the narrator sees through the cycle of charm and coldness, and wants the narcissist to finally face what they've done to the person they've eroded. The title names the dynamic plainly.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~oasis":{"haiku":"Refuge and sanctuary, oasis as escape into safe haven from harsh world.","opus":"A cover of Oasis's Wonderwall, that famously ambivalent anthem of pinning your salvation on another person. Its power lies in how tentative the hope is; the beloved is a maybe, a wall you lean on without certainty they'll hold.","sonnet":"A cover of Oasis' \"Wonderwall\" — devotion addressed to someone who carries the impossible weight of being the one that saves you, roads all winding, lights all blinding, the speaker unable to say the things they mean. \"After all, you're my wonderwall\" names the object of need without explaining it.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~odyssey":{"haiku":"Braggadocio and indifference toward others' judgment while pursuing material excess. The rapper claims distance from conventional morality and emotional attachment.","opus":"A Balkan trap flex where indifference is the whole posture: nothing impresses him, money and status are the only currency, and every boast is armored in contempt for anyone circling for a cut.","sonnet":"A Serbian-language boast track: the speaker is unbothered by everyone, luxuriates in a comfortable flow, makes art from bank-statement numbers, beats opponents at the last second like a Wimbledon match point, and rounds up his crew for a video shoot where the girls want to be seen and the money speaks loudest.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~outlaw-remastered":{"haiku":"Complicity in theft and crime through romantic attachment where the lover runs and the narrator pays. Love becomes expensive consequence of another's outlawry.","opus":"A lover implicated in her partner's crime spree, framing herself as the loaded gun to his outlaw so neither can judge the other; the thrill and the fear of loving someone racing toward self-destruction.","sonnet":"A narrator recounts a past built on lies and stolen cars with a reckless partner who escalated to armed robbery; now the narrator pays bail with their heart, fully aware the outlaw will not be tamed — the refrain frames their own complicity as a loaded gun that chose to keep following.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~phoenix":{"haiku":"Internal demons externalize into walls; the speaker burns through containment to rise reborn, ashes scattered across freedom.","opus":"A pop-punk reckoning with the fears and voices in one's own head, finally letting the walls fall rather than holding everything in. The nursery-rhyme collapse gives way to rebirth: burning down in order to rise and fly again.","sonnet":"Fears inside the walls call out the speaker's name and resist change; the answer is to stop holding it all in, let the walls cave, burn to ashes, and rise again — 'when it's time I know I'll fly again' frames collapse not as failure but as the necessary precondition of transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~twisted":{"haiku":"Infidelity and deception pervade all relationships; betrayal flows bidirectionally, and the game thrives on lies and hidden truths.","opus":"A wry consolation to the betrayed that everyone in the dating game is cheating, faking, and hiding behind masks, so the wound is universal rather than personal. The real casualty is our own capacity for trust.","sonnet":"A cynical portrait of serial infidelity on both sides of a relationship — a man cheating every night of the week and a woman kept in luxury by multiple men — leading to the conclusion that everyone plays a dishonest game and ultimately betrays their own heart.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~villain":{"haiku":"The speaker ensnares and feeds on a victim's descent, manipulating desire and fear until control becomes absolute. The dance with the devil ends in permanent imprisonment and stolen light.","opus":"A predator addresses the victim he's trapped, casting himself as the devil the prey spotted a beat too slow; he savors the cornering, the breaking, the manipulation, consuming them bit by bit while claiming their soul and their future, promising they'll never glimpse daylight.","sonnet":"The villain speaks from inside the trap they've set — the prey was \"blindly led by an urge so primal\" and now owns their soul, feeding piece by piece — the power is cold and certain, and regret arrives too late for the one who danced with the devil.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~villain-instrumental":{"haiku":"Manipulation and control through seduction and psychological domination. The narrator feeds on broken prey, keeping victims cornered and dependent.","opus":"Sung from inside the predator, a manipulator relishing the slow ruin of someone who chose to dance with the devil, promising to keep them cornered and broken while feeding off them piece by piece.","sonnet":"A predatory speaker addresses a naive target who ignored reason and walked through the wrong door: she has them cornered, broken, fed upon piece by piece — \"I own your soul and your fate\" — casting herself as the inevitable devil in their eyes, whose light they will never see again.","src":"haiku"},
"alex~youth-remastered":{"haiku":"Youthful confusion amid crowd noise, finding solace in someone's presence over clarity.","opus":"Lost in a crowd and unsure of the way out, but consoled that presence is enough. Youth framed as the only thing worth giving, a small defiant offering against uncertainty.","sonnet":"A single repeated emotional core: lost in a crowd, shouting aloud, uncertain of the way out, but not caring because the other person is here — youth is the only gift they have to offer and presence makes that enough.","src":"haiku"},
"alfa-mist~teki":{"haiku":"Inevitable change and systemic questioning of ideological fragmentation. The future remains uncertain but remains the only direction available.","opus":"A brief jazz-inflected meditation on inevitability, sitting with the certainty of change and the questionable dogmas people cling to while the future arrives regardless, only a matter of time.","sonnet":"Three lines only: change is inevitable, the isms and schisms are questionable, and the future is out there — a bare philosophical statement held open without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"alfakyun~our-sympathy":{"haiku":"Empathetic bonding and shared resistance against darkness and despair. Two figures find strength in each other's determination and continued belief in light.","opus":"An anime-flavored fight anthem about breaking free of a past that kept you frozen, powered by a friend whose belief lets you face the dark; the shared sympathy between two people becomes the weapon that saves the world.","sonnet":"An anime-style battle-of-wills anthem in Japanese: a protagonist who was frozen in place by a dark past finds through a friend the will to fight again, visualises saving the world, and drives a blade of light through the darkness — the sympathy between two people becoming the superpower that makes the impossible possible.","src":"haiku"},
"algernon-cadwallader~spit-fountain":{"haiku":"Absurdist ode to the small truths that sustain you when the world feels strange.","opus":"Twinkly emo channeling a sunlit, slightly deranged joy through deliberately gross, childlike imagery. The nonsense is a way of feeling wide open and unguarded, wanting to fall backward into the day.","sonnet":"A surreal, playful address to the simple pleasures of warm months and childhood-adjacent absurdity — the speaker delights in nonsense and wants to fall back into something uncomplicated and wet.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-cooper~poison":{"haiku":"A person is drawn to a toxic, seductive lover despite knowing the danger, unable to resist despite their desires being poisonous and destructive.","opus":"Alice Cooper's 1989 comeback hit treats an irresistible woman as a beautiful drug, the singer aching to touch her while every instinct warns that even one taste is fatal. The sadomasochistic streak comes through in wanting to inflict pain just to hear her cry his name, love figured as a chemical dependency he refuses to escape.","sonnet":"A toxic sexual obsession the narrator knows is destroying him but cannot and does not want to escape — desire framed as venom running through the veins, with a knowing, self-lacerating awareness that the addiction is the point.","src":"haiku","fable":"The clever move is that Cooper never lets the wanting resolve; he doubles it, pairing each craving with its own veto so the song burns on friction between reaching and refusing rather than any consummation. What the chorus names is addiction described from inside the bloodstream — the beloved reframed as a substance already circulating, a contamination the speaker cannot survive yet will not flush. The tell is that he keeps his shackles on, choosing the toxin over the cure. And the outro quietly relocates the fever from her mouth to his skull, admitting the sickness lived in his head all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"Co-written with producer Desmond Child and guitarist John McCurry — whose signature riff had already lived one life on a John Waite track two years earlier.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"craving and refusal fused into a toxin that cannot be expelled","built":"the outro relocates the fever from her to his skull, admitting the poison was always internal"},
"alice-cooper~poison-single-version":{"haiku":"Addictive desire dressed as poison—the pull is irresistible despite knowing it will hurt and corrupt.","opus":"Desire as a toxin the narrator knows will destroy him and reaches for anyway. Cooper's shock-rock theater turns lust into addiction, the chains he won't break admitting he'd rather be poisoned than let go.","sonnet":"About dangerous sexual obsession — the narrator is trapped by desire for someone he knows will destroy him; he wants to touch, hold, and taste this person but recognizes the attraction as poisonous and the chains holding him to it as chains he doesn't truly want broken.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-cooper~years-ago":{"haiku":"Remembering childhood's end, when imagination broke and time grew impossibly heavy.","opus":"The opening of the Steven suite from Cooper's concept album, a broken man dissolving into the frightened child he never stopped being. The horror is developmental, a grown adult trapped mid-collapse between boy and man.","sonnet":"A fragmented interior monologue captures a man slipping between a childlike self and an adult one, speaking to a memory of childhood play and carnival joy that ended long ago, with a mother's voice calling him back to a home that no longer exists.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~baby-teeth":{"opus":"A raw account of numbing oneself to abuse and self-harm, where the singer insists she doesn't need the tormentor and cuts herself to prove her own detachment, resigned to a future where nothing lasts and no one cares.","sonnet":"A survivor of sexual violation and psychological abuse confronts her own dissociation and self-harm, using self-destruction to prove independence from her abuser while accepting that happiness may never return — pain has become so normalized it feels like home.","src":"sonnet"},
"alice-glass~blood-oath":{"haiku":"A song about predatory exploitation of youth and removal of agency—harvesters pluck innocence early to prevent growth and extract life force without consent or consequence.","opus":"A harrowing testimony of stolen childhood and abuse that leaves permanent damage, its violator seizing what he wants regardless of the victim. Brutal images of maiming and consuming offspring evoke predation handed down through generations, insisting the harm is lodged in the survivor's body while the abuser feels nothing.","sonnet":"A survivor's account of systematic abuse and exploitation — innocence stolen without consent, abusers who move on to younger victims once they've drained what they want, with no way to undo the damage done.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~catch-and-release":{"haiku":"Rupture through repetition: watching someone destroy themselves becomes its own form of damage, and the observer can't separate themselves from the wreckage.","opus":"A survivor throwing an abuser's cruelty back at them, sarcastically apologizing for being dramatic while insisting the abuse wasn't chosen. Beneath the numbness is a demand that the perpetrator sit daily with what they did.","sonnet":"A song about trauma and its aftermath directed at someone who caused serious harm — the speaker is dramatic because something genuinely happened, accuses the other person of thinking about what they did every single day, and oscillates between breakdown and numb defiance.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~everybody-else":{"haiku":"Self-annihilation feels inevitable; the speaker internalizes abuse as deserved and surrenders agency to become what others demand.","opus":"A wish to disappear into anonymity paired with a plea to be used and blamed, self-hatred so deep it seeks punishment as what it deserves. The refrain that God made her wrong turns the longing for closeness into a compulsion toward self-erasure and abuse.","sonnet":"Self-erasure as both longing and self-punishment: the speaker wants to dissolve identity, take the blame, be used — \"tie up my wrists like they're nothing, this is what I deserve\" — framing God as having made them wrong, and conformity to nothingness as the only way to exist alongside others.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~forgiveness":{"haiku":"Two hollow, empty beings find kinship in their shared nothingness and escape to the woods seeking oblivion, rejecting blame and the need for redemption.","opus":"A bleak bond between two empty, nothing-to-lose people who reject blame and forgiveness, drawn toward the woods and the night, feeling like a discarded doll pleading to break down and give in.","sonnet":"Two people who feel worthless and unwanted — empty by design, uncared for, unprotected — choose to escape into darkness together, rejecting the need for anyone else's blame or forgiveness and surrendering to the night.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~love-is-violence":{"haiku":"Intimate cruelty and sadomasochistic entanglement transform love into weaponized pain; aggression masks deeper wounds inflicted by prior trauma and violation.","opus":"After leaving Crystal Castles and the abuse she alleged there, Glass makes the equation explicit: intimacy that has become predation, where release and harm are indistinguishable. The closing turn toward another victim reframes it as survivor's warning.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship is named plainly as violence — bleeding the speaker dry, binding them to trauma, generating a disturbing release — before the song pivots outward to warn another person not to take shame for what they did to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~mine":{"haiku":"Reclamation of bodily autonomy through self-inflicted pain, asserting ownership against external control and systematic abuse.","opus":"Self-harm as the last available act of ownership, reclaiming a body that others held down and commanded. Glass frames cutting not as despair alone but as a desperate bid to feel like herself again, however broken the logic.","sonnet":"Written from inside an abusive dynamic, the speaker describes reclaiming ownership of their own body and mind through self-harm as the only available act of autonomy, framing pain as a desperate bid to feel like oneself again after being controlled and held down by another person.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~natural-selection":{"haiku":"Someone is under assault with demands for compliance and removal of bodily autonomy. They scream in silent protest while being violated and prey to invisible torment.","opus":"Alice Glass, following her wrenching exit from Crystal Castles, turns survival into electronic menace, repeating blunt commands for an attacker to remove himself from her body. Threats dressed up as safety and the imagery of decorative binds sharpen the theme of violated autonomy, a terror she has to voice without ever being heard.","sonnet":"A visceral cry from someone being violated who screams for the body invading hers to get off, alternating between that raw demand and a bleak question about whether darkness is actually the safer condition. Rage and entrapment coexist without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~prey":{"haiku":"The speaker fades fast, unprepared for what was always in front of them, questioning their existence. They ask if they're going to hell while someone hurts them and they wish they were never born.","opus":"Alice Glass voices a harrowing account of being hurt and made to feel disposable, questioning whether she exists or matters to the one causing the harm. Fading and afraid, she wonders if she was ever seen at all. Following her public break from Crystal Castles and her allegations of abuse, the song reads as a raw reckoning with victimhood and survival.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a state of fading dissolution, questioning whether existence itself has meaning while confronting someone whose cruelty is making them disappear. The speaker oscillates between asking to be believed and asking to be left alone, suspended between fear and erasure.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~stillbirth":{"haiku":"Waiting for someone's death to escape servitude and reclaim agency; the singer wants to cease being a wasted vessel for others' control. Stillbirth symbolizes failed legacy and the desire to start over.","opus":"Alice Glass turning her abuse survival into a death wish aimed at the abuser: the fantasy of outliving him and starting over as a person he no longer controls. The title frames the whole thing as a bond that should never have been born.","sonnet":"The narrator waits for the death of someone who controlled or diminished them — a figure they starved for power — and beneath the fury is the desperate need to start over, to stop being what that person wanted them to be.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~the-altar":{"haiku":"Comparing suffering to others' pain seems rehearsed and empty; regret cannot be reversed no matter how much compassion one performs.","opus":"A grim meditation on measuring one's pain against others', its refrain that someone somewhere hurts more deployed as a way to dismiss and erase one's own worth. The flat, repeated insistence that past mistakes are permanent seals it as a study of irreversible harm and the hollow, performed sympathy that fails to heal it.","sonnet":"A quiet trap of self-erasure: the narrator catches herself dismissing her own pain by reminding herself that someone else always has it worse, and the song names this as a mechanism that prevents genuine self-worth and leaves regret permanently unreversed.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~white-lies":{"haiku":"A person demands comforting lies instead of truth, fearing what reality reveals. They accept manipulation to avoid thinking about hard truths, sacrificing authenticity for comfort.","opus":"A survivor pleads for comforting lies to avoid facing painful truths, describing a curse of seeing what others want, self-erasure, and telling a father figure only what he wants to hear.","sonnet":"The narrator describes a dynamic of being managed by comforting falsehoods — told stories to avoid uncomfortable truths, shaped into something smaller and more obedient — and pushes back against a figure (addressed as \"Father\") whose depravity she cannot ignore no matter how much she tries not to think about it.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-glass~without-love":{"haiku":"A person doubts their worth and struggles for autonomy while feeling controlled and silenced by another.","opus":"Alice Glass on surviving control and abuse by hardening herself against love entirely, questioning her own worth while sewing her lips shut, the vow to live without love a scar disguised as armor and defiance.","sonnet":"About surviving an abusive relationship where the narrator was controlled, silenced, and watched — deciding to cut love out entirely rather than keep suffering under someone who lied about and over her.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains-mtv-unplugged~down-in-a-hole":{"haiku":"Descending into depths and psychological pits; the speaker sinks into depression and unable to escape.","opus":"Layne Staley's aching admission of being trapped in despair too deep to climb out of, tending his own heart like a grave. Beneath the addiction imagery runs a doomed love and the fear he's beyond saving.","sonnet":"Trapped in a self-dug grave — stones placed by others, the sun eaten until his tongue is burned, wings denied — the speaker cannot bring himself to articulate the feelings buried beneath, because to speak them would require acknowledging how completely he has lost himself. The womb and the tomb collapse into the same image: a place of both origin and erasure, where the desire to be held and the desire to disappear are indistinguishable.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains-mtv-unplugged~nutshell":{"haiku":"Violation of privacy and self through lies and deception that strip identity. The narrator faces alone the robbery of agency and the emptiness of isolation.","opus":"Layne Staley's bare confession of isolation and violated privacy, fighting an unwinnable battle alone with no home to return to, sunk so far that death starts to feel preferable to losing himself; the unplugged setting makes the despair unbearably intimate.","sonnet":"An achingly compact statement of alienation: the speaker chases misprinted lies, fights alone with no one to cry to, feels their gift of self violated, their privacy torn apart — and concludes that if they cannot be their own, they would feel better dead.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains-mtv-unplugged~rooster":{"haiku":"Vietnam trauma and survival embedded in the body where bullets echo and return. The narrator cannot escape the killing despite bureaucratic survival.","opus":"Cantrell's tribute to his father, a Vietnam vet who survived combat and came home to be spat on; the rooster is the man who refuses to die despite the bullets, disease, and a nation's contempt, made rawer live with Staley singing it.","sonnet":"A Vietnam soldier's account narrated from the perspective of his son: spat-upon in his own homeland, watching a buddy die, clinging to photos of his child while praying to survive — and surviving, against every attempt to snuff the rooster out.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains-mtv-unplugged~sludge-factory":{"haiku":"Mire accumulates as the speaker drowns in mud of addiction and despair. The factory produces only waste and dissolution.","opus":"A bitter, cryptic address to someone whose dilated eyes and graying skin give away the drugs and the self-destruction. Half-adoring, half-accusing, the speaker wrestles with a loved one who swore they'd never see thirty and now wants to die, begging them to hang on long enough to settle their debts to the people they hurt.","sonnet":"A blurred drug-and-control atmosphere: someone is being managed, their discoloured skin giving away their state, corporate ignorance letting the narrator control time, and the crisis arrives when the body of \"one soul I adore\" wants to die — \"you always told me you'd not live past 25\" — and the only answer offered is to stay long enough to repay those who caused the harm.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~a-little-bitter-remix":{"haiku":"A person feels exploited by parasitic figures who feed on their resources and sanity while remaining contemptuous of self-worth. The song describes draining emotional vampirism.","opus":"Layne Staley-era Alice in Chains channel bilious self-disgust, the singer feeling drained and spent like cheap money by parasitic hangers-on who treat his flesh as a resting place and gnaw at his last scraps of reason. He frames dependency as a bargain that costs the soul, while the leeches shrink from real living and profit off his misery.","sonnet":"A man recognizes that someone is consuming him — his money, his energy, his sanity — while offering nothing in return. Fury curdles into contempt as he names the parasitism plainly: his body a depleted altar, the other feeding on tears and calling it intimacy.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~a-looking-in-view": {"haiku":"A relationship deteriorates through silence, isolation, and internal distortion; the singer observes a partner's anguish while wrestling with resentment and a vengeful mind.","opus":"The comeback single that reintroduced Alice in Chains with William DuVall alongside Jerry Cantrell, grinding through paranoia and self-disgust. Imagined intruders and a body worn down like broken machinery give way to schemes that only add up when your existence has sunk low enough. The loathing aimed at another turns out to be a mirror, faith souring into a vengeful impulse that reaches inward.","sonnet":"A person locked in mutual resentment with someone close recognizes that the traits he despises in them are mirrored in himself. Suicidal ideation hides beneath the surface — heaven secretly on the mind — while desperation, resentment, and a vengeful streak boil under a lid of silence.","src":"haiku","fable":"The comeback release, and the first thing the band says after Layne Staley's death is a song about watching from the wrong side of the glass — the outsider who has looked in so long that his warped vantage starts to feel like reason, so that desperate, vengeful plans read as sound logic to a life lived low. The paranoid opening, footsteps and a barred door, sets the frame as a besieged interior, but the turn comes when hate curdles into mirror: what he despises in the other reflects back and hollows him out, distortion contaminating the self it was aimed at. Structurally the song keeps sinking — lay, down, wait — toward a buried thing boiling up under the skin, and Cantrell withholds the tell until the end, that the addict or the estranged one never admits heaven is on his mind because he has already chosen the vengeful, self-erasing exit. It is less an accusation than a diagnosis of the mindset that consumes from the inside, fittingly the door the band chose to reopen with.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction","suicide"],"means":"hatred mirrored back hollows the one who aimed it","built":"the song keeps sinking — lay, down, wait — toward the buried admission"},
"alice-in-chains~acid-bubble": {"haiku":"Emotional neglect and systemic cruelty create a broken child unloved and abandoned, with suffering built into the system.","opus":"A grinding standoff between two people locked in mutual harm, the narrator dreading his own identity, an unloved figure withering in isolation while a verdict about built-in doom is repeated like a sentence over relationships designed to fail. In their William DuVall era, Alice in Chains sustain the Seattle band's harmonized gloom, dread and self-loathing threaded through slow sludge.","sonnet":"The child who survived the system that was designed to discard it — someone tried to erase the speaker, and they remain, marked by every boundary crossed against them but still present, still crying in the corner of someone's mind. Obsolescence was built in from the start, but the obsolescence never quite took.","src":"haiku","fable":"An attempted erasure fails, and the survivor discovers the assailant wears his own face: two mirrored verses swap who crosses the forbidden lines and who watches, until accuser and accused prove interchangeable. The hammered mechanical chorus recasts private ruin as manufactured decay, the kind engineered to fail on a timetable, so the weeping figure crouched in the corner reads less as a wound than as a component behaving to specification. Arriving as the band's first work following Layne Staley's overdose, the piece plays like a coroner's note on addiction wired into the personality from birth, the destroyer and the destroyed collapsing into one exhausted person expiring inside a single skull.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"the destroyer and the destroyed collapse into one skull","built":"mirrored verses swap who crosses the line and who watches, making accuser and accused interchangeable"},
"alice-in-chains~again": {"haiku":"A relationship repeats its harm cycle; the speaker's friend betrays and violates them again and again, yet he returns to the pain repeatedly.","opus":"A layered study of a toxic cycle, the narrator both berating a manipulator who keeps making a fool of him and admitting his own complicity in returning to be violated again; repetition itself is the trap and the wound.","sonnet":"A song about being repeatedly betrayed and violated by someone who was supposed to be a friend — the singer oscillates between self-blame and anger, trapped in a cycle he acknowledges but cannot break.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study in the compulsion to return to what wounds you, where the refrain fuses two betrayals into one word: a supposed friend keeps making a fool of him, but the speaker admits in the same breath that he keeps walking back into the same mistake, so the violation is half inflicted and half chosen. Read through Layne Staley's addiction, the friend who violates and then extends a part of him again reads as the drug itself — an intimacy that promises repair and delivers only the next round of harm. The song's argument lives in that grammar of recurrence: every stanza resets to the same helpless address, and the title becomes less a complaint about someone else than a confession that the cycle has replaced the self doing the pleading.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"betrayal half inflicted and half chosen, the cycle replacing the self","built":"every stanza resets to the same helpless address, grammar of recurrence as the argument"},
"alice-in-chains~again-tattoo-of-pain-remix":{"haiku":"Betrayal repeats endlessly; the speaker surrenders to cyclical humiliation. Friends become enemies; violation marks time's passage through deteriorating bonds.","opus":"A cycle of betrayal by a supposed friend who violates and humiliates the speaker again and again, each verse circling back to the same wound. Layne Staley's weary delivery makes the repeated again the sound of someone trapped in a pattern he keeps letting recur.","sonnet":"A bitter address to a so-called friend who has repeatedly humiliated and violated the speaker — slapping in the face, turning upside down, extending then withdrawing part of themselves — with the word \"again\" as both accusation and admission that the pattern keeps repeating despite the speaker's resolve to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~all-i-am": {"haiku":"Aging and deterioration strip away identity, leaving only a shell haunted by memories of capability and vitality.","opus":"A haunted address to an aging, unrecognizable face, someone worn down and homely, wandering years of a lonely road while clinging to memory of his prime and asking whether this is all he is. Numbness has set in, blood dried on the floor, feeling gone. From Alice in Chains' later work with William DuVall, it carries the Seattle band's signature dread and harmonized gloom, a meditation on decline and diminished self.","sonnet":"A reckoning with the wreckage of a life spent — a face unrecognisable in the mirror, the road walked alone to its end, sensation gone and the body still moving out of habit. The question asked and never answered is whether this diminished state is the sum total of what was given and what remains, with no god of time offering any reply.","src":"haiku","fable":"A reckoning with a self worn unrecognizable by time and depletion, staged as a confrontation in a mirror the speaker no longer trusts to reflect him. The unfamiliar face and the plea to still be seen undisguised turn the song into a test of whether any witness — a listener, a lover, or one's own gaze — can find the person under the damage. The refrain lands not as self-pity but as an audit: the question of whether this hollowed-out figure, drained of feeling and stuck at a line marked in dried blood, represents the total return on a life given everything it had. Coming from a post-Layne Staley Alice in Chains led by William DuVall and Jerry Cantrell, the song reads doubly as the band's own late-career negotiation with legacy and endurance, so the closing return to the unrecognizable face refuses resolution — the interrogation simply loops, never knowing why.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the hollowed self audited rather than mourned","built":"the interrogation loops without resolution, the closing face as unrecognizable as the opening"},
"alice-in-chains~all-secrets-known": {"haiku":"A meditation on healing and acceptance after trauma, acknowledging that old wounds heal but secrets are exposed and must be faced. The song moves from hurt and loss toward a fragile hope that truth and trust can be rebuilt.","opus":"The opener of the band's return after Layne Staley's death, this is Cantrell voicing hard-won survival, no going back to who they were, wounds slowly healing and a fragile hope of feeling alive again with everything now laid bare.","sonnet":"A cautious, hard-won opening toward renewal after something devastating — the singer acknowledges there's no going back to who they were before, but senses that old wounds are healing and something inside is still alive enough to want to feel again.","src":"haiku","fable":"Opening the band's first album after Layne Staley's death, this is a resurrection song that knows resurrection is impossible. The refrain keeps insisting there is no return to the starting place, so the new life it offers is not the old one restored but something built over a grave. One-word imperatives paired with soft second lines — hope, time, trust, truth — read like a survivor coaxing himself back into functioning after a loss he can only phrase as death already suffered. The promise that all secrets are known lands as the price of that survival: addiction and death are fully in the open. Only from that stripped, exposed place can the wish to feel alive be spoken honestly rather than as denial.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","addiction & self-destruction","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"resurrection offered as survival built over a grave","built":"one-word imperatives paired with soft second lines coach a self back into functioning"},
"alice-in-chains~am-i-inside": {"haiku":"Existential isolation and dissociation where the speaker is lost within themselves and cannot connect with reality.","opus":"An early Alice in Chains cut, Layne Staley's signature dread carrying a portrait of total isolation, the singer trailed by an emptiness that seeps to the surface of his skin as blackness becomes his only sensation. Adrift in a labyrinth with no one beside him, mourning an absent counterpart, he can no longer tell whether he even inhabits his own body.","sonnet":"Loneliness that has moved past feeling into something structural — it crawls on the skin, surrounds the speaker with hollow people, and leaves a maze to walk alone. The freedom referenced is the freedom of having nothing left to lose, and the fractured question at the center — whether the speaker is even inside themselves anymore — is asked with genuine uncertainty.","src":"haiku","fable":"Layne Staley diagnoses a numbness so complete that emptiness starts passing itself off as release, the absence of any sensation mistaken for the lightness of having nothing left to lose. The real cruelty sits in the pronouns: the man is positioned everywhere adjacent to his own body yet never lodged within it, so the closing question about interior residence reads as a literal failure to locate a self. What he calls his lost portion doubles as a vanished partner and an amputated piece of his own person, both dissolving into a single wound the song studies coldly rather than mourns. Stripped to acoustic bones on Sap, the swaggering nerve he once owned is exposed as borrowed and long since spent, addiction and estrangement left standing without the electric armor to hide behind.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"numbness mistaken for release, the self impossible to locate","built":"the closing question about interior residence is a literal failure of the pronouns to fix a self anywhere"},
"alice-in-chains~angry-chair": {"haiku":"Entrapment in emotional and psychological captivity, with a speaker stuck in an angry, stifling space watching themselves reflected and distorted, numb to their own suffering.","opus":"Layne Staley's portrait of addiction and self-loathing as a chair that traps and suffocates, a lost boy who has given up on his own mind. The corporate-prison and dull-boy imagery ties his despair to a world that ground him down.","sonnet":"Dissociation and self-loathing from inside a grinding, numbing existence — seeing one's own reflection and weeping, trapped in corporate routine, loneliness not as a phase but as a permanent field — the chair a fixture of a life that has stopped moving.","src":"haiku","fable":"Layne Staley wrote most of this one himself, and it plays like a heroin song narrated from inside the room the drug has shrunk him into — the chair, the walls closing to a windowless box, the candles and shadows are a junkie's whole world reduced to a fixed point he cannot rise from. The horror is that the self splits: he watches a version of himself set hard as a fired figurine across the room, its face changing, afraid of him and he of it, so the addiction becomes a second person he negotiates with. Against that, the chorus lands as the sickest possible reassurance — the repeated refusal to mind losing his mind is not calm but the addict's alibi, the mask that says everything is fine while the man underneath has stopped being able to feel where his own sanity went. The prayer and the imagery of a scolded child pull the self back to a small figure kneeling, guilt and dependency braided together, and by the last verse the tenderness the drug promised has curdled entirely into a scorched pasture he simply grazes in. It is one of Dirt's most exposed confessions precisely because it never argues its own tragedy; it wears the shrug and lets you hear the death in it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"the addict's alibi delivered as reassurance while the self dissolves","built":"the self splitting into a figurine across the room makes addiction a second person to negotiate with"},
"alice-in-chains~angry-chair-live":{"haiku":"Furniture becomes confessional; the chair witnesses rage and containment of explosive emotion.","opus":"Layne Staley's claustrophobic self-portrait of addiction and numbness, a lost boy watching his rosy outlook darken to grey while flatly insisting the loss of his mind does not trouble him. Lines about a soul-deadening job and captivity widen the scene from personal ruin to a whole existence hollowed out.","sonnet":"Sitting trapped in a suffocating interior — the \"angry chair\" with its burning candles and stolen air — the speaker watches a clay effigy of himself being reshaped by outside forces while cataloguing his own dissolution: lost mind, corporate imprisonment, a reflection that makes him cry. The \"pink cloud has turned to gray\" is the precise moment innocence collapses into the dull grind of addiction and despair.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~black-gives-way-to-blue": {"haiku":"Grief over losing someone; the speaker avoids change and consciously lets grief consume them, resigned to remembrance instead of moving forward.","opus":"Numbness and hauntedness give way to a quiet vow to lie down, remember, and let black turn to blue. Alice in Chains wrote this as a closing elegy for late singer Layne Staley, William DuVall and Jerry Cantrell voicing grief with spare tenderness, imitations pale beside the ghost, the title tracing mourning's slow shift from darkness toward dawn.","sonnet":"A grief too heavy to carry draws the speaker toward numbness and withdrawal. Staying conscious means feeling the haunting, so fading becomes the path of least resistance — yet even in the descent, the lost person is promised to be remembered as darkness slowly gives way.","src":"haiku","fable":"The album that spent its length rebuilding Alice in Chains without Layne Staley turns to him directly only here, in its closing title track — Jerry Cantrell's direct address to the singer who died seven years before, with Elton John's piano turning grief into a kind of dignified elegy rather than the band's usual sludge. The chromatic slide of the title is the whole thesis: black is the numbness the speaker admits he'd rather stay in because falling is easier than feeling, and blue is the lesser, livable ache that replaces it — mourning as a downgrade of suffering, not a cure. The refrain's two imperatives split the work of goodbye between the dead and the living: lay down is permission for the ghost to rest, and remembering is the survivor's half of the bargain, the price of finally drawing the curtain. That the record can only address him once it has proven it can go on is itself the point — the goodbye had to be earned before it could be forced.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"mourning as a downgrade of suffering, the livable ache replacing the numb","built":"two imperatives split the goodbye — one for the dead to rest, one for the living to remember"},
"alice-in-chains~bleed-the-freak": {"haiku":"The song expresses a demand that followers prove devotion through sacrifice and bleeding, using religious language to justify punishing nonconformity. The narrator treats others' suffering as payment and validation for their own existence.","opus":"A defiant, resentful challenge daring others to name their god and bleed for the speaker, questioning the loyalty and debts owed him, and demanding that those who claim to stand for him prove it in blood.","sonnet":"A confrontational voice demands that others name their god and prove their devotion by suffering for it, turning religious language of sacrifice into a challenge — and a dare — aimed at hypocrites.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cantrell writes this in scripture and then inverts every line of it: the cup that runs over yields not abundance but blood squeezed from a stone, and the eye-for-an-eye idiom gets turned on the faithful instead of the sinner. The song adopts the voice of the vengeful deity everyone claims to worship — daring the listener to point to whatever altar they kneel at and watch that god demand the same cruelty back — so the chorus is less blasphemy than a mirror held up to belief. The late refrain drops the biblical mask entirely and speaks plain exhaustion: the tally of kindnesses handed to him has worn him down to nothing, and the last demand on the bill is that you bleed too. That pivot from sacred menace to a flat, worn-out human grievance is the whole argument — the freak isn't the outsider being bled but the ordinary betrayed sense that devotion is always a one-way debt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"scripture's idioms inverted to indict belief itself","built":"pivot from biblical persona to flat human grievance is the whole argument"},
"alice-in-chains~breath-on-a-window": {"haiku":"Loneliness and invisibility; haunting someone from a distance, a ghost present in their world but unacknowledged.","opus":"A desolate, haunted drive along a deserted road, the narrator feeling unseen and emptied out, as if no one is really present anywhere. It shifts into a corrosive attachment to a person he cannot shake, whom he names as the lasting wound and ruin he carries with him wherever he goes.","sonnet":"A driver moves through empty, blighted roads carrying the weight of a past relationship that lingers like a scar — the narrator cannot shed the other person, who remains the damage already done, even as the journey pushes forward into strange terrain.","src":"haiku","fable":"A highway song that is really about interior weather, its patched barriers and unmarked routes and dodged storms mapping a mind bent on outdriving a hurt it carries in the passenger seat. Fog breathed onto glass becomes a child's stalemated game, and the mirror behind reflects a house with the lights off, a self forever knocking to see who answers and hearing only silence. Cigarette scorches punched into skin sketch a weathered spirit, so the body reads as marked surface, the William DuVall-era wreckage where ruin is simply who someone is rather than what befell them. When the travelogue falls away, the one who smashed against his own pride and took the wound sideways gets his answer from a voice confessing it would depart but cannot leave. It cannot, because it is the injury itself, the healed-over gash of every day before this one, a self stalking a self and refusing to surrender the lane.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a self stalking a self, the injury refusing to surrender the lane","built":"travelogue imagery — fog, mirrors, cigarette burns — maps an interior that the speaker cannot exit"},
"alice-in-chains~brother": {"haiku":"Separated from a brother, the narrator's pain grows like decaying memories; pictures yellow with time, distance hardening the wound.","opus":"A tender, aching plea to an estranged, emotionally distant brother, urging him not to flee pain the way he always did; the yellowing, mold-ruined photographs make the loss of connection and fading memory of his face devastatingly concrete.","sonnet":"A song about emotional distance from someone the narrator loves — probably a sibling or close friend who always seemed far away — reaching out across years of absence with frozen pain and faded photographs.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jerry Cantrell wrote this as a direct address to an estranged sibling, and the song works as an attempt to close a distance that both parties spent years keeping open. The verses track the failure of that attempt through images of decay: a bouquet the speaker ruins by gripping the thorns too hard, family photographs left to mildew until a face is no longer legible. The refrain is where the movement lives — the plea shifts from asking the brother not to flee, to the speaker vowing he himself won't flee anymore, and finally to a quieter claim that he now knows the way. That progression turns a song about someone else's absence into a confession about the speaker's own habit of running, so the reconciliation offered is less a reunion than a promise to stop being the one who leaves.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","shame & repression"],"means":"a reconciliation that confesses the speaker's own habit of running","built":"the refrain's plea shifts from asking the brother to stay to vowing the speaker himself will stop leaving"},
"alice-in-chains~brush-away": {"haiku":"Internal struggle with aggression and self-doubt; the speaker attempts escape but remains stuck, unable to resolve underlying tensions.","opus":"From the William DuVall era, a study of self-sabotage soaked in the band's signature dread. The narrator bristles at being judged and cornered, showing up late and shrugging off blame, aggression tangled with avoidance. The core tension is a restless push and pull between wanting to bolt and staying stuck.","sonnet":"Low-grade social friction and the instinct to withdraw from it. He arrives late, questions why others grade him on a curve of aggression, and cycles between wanting to leave and somehow always staying. The crawling-away imagery suggests exhaustion rather than dramatic exit — just brushing loose ground and drifting.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the exhausting drift between wanting out of a relationship and being unable to leave it, staged as a person perpetually about to escape who keeps circling back. The verses read like someone being appraised and found wanting — graded on a curve, their intentions treated as a passing fad rather than something real — and the resentment of that judgment is the aggression the other person feeds on. The chorus is the whole dynamic in miniature: the promise to fall away and clear the loose ground under themselves collapses immediately back into sticking around, so the escape becomes a ritual gesture instead of an act. The stray domestic beats, arriving late with a mumbled apology and a forgotten errand, ground it in the low-stakes daily failures of someone who can never quite follow through, making the paralysis feel less tragic than simply worn-down and habitual.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"escape rehearsed as a ritual gesture instead of an act","built":"the chorus promises to fall away and then immediately collapses back into sticking around"},
"alice-in-chains~check-my-brain": {"haiku":"Mental exhaustion and disorientation in California, where time and emotional toll have hollowed out the narrator's mind.","opus":"The narrator finds himself in the California sun after a long, hard run, half-marveling and half-disbelieving at where he has ended up. He repeatedly asks someone to check his brain, worn down by years and tears.","sonnet":"A narrator who has wound up in California reflects on years spent, tears accumulated, and a past self left behind — suggesting a kind of bewildered, hard-won arrival that calls his own sanity into question.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jerry Cantrell files a suspicious dispatch on swapping grey, addiction-scarred Seattle for a sunlit coast, then distrusting the news that the move actually helped. The recurring plea to have his head examined works as a genuine double-take, treating plain wellness as so foreign it must signal a fault in the machinery. Two confessions anchor the giddiness to loss — the smoked-out haze and the squandered years — so the brightness rests on wreckage it refuses to sand away. Laying down the weapons registers as a truce with both the old fight and the old chasing, the buried knack being that surrender was finally permitted rather than defeat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","addiction & self-destruction","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"plain wellness so foreign it registers as a fault in the machinery","built":"the recurring plea for a brain examination works as both punchline and genuine double-take"},
"alice-in-chains~chemical-addiction":{"haiku":"A teenager descends into street life and drug addiction by age 15, normalizing substance abuse as daily survival.","opus":"A stark first-person tale of adolescent street endurance and narcotic recklessness, a teenager pursuing a poisonous fantasy through alcohol, pills, and an urge to sample opiates, resolved to taste it all before the end. Numbing himself into blackout becomes the combat-zone norm. An early Alice in Chains cut, it prefigures the Seattle band's career-long fixation on dependency, its closing aside hinting at rough, live-demo beginnings.","sonnet":"Survival on the street at fifteen — trading girls for substances, cycling through the war zone of daily addiction, watching colours dissolve into sound. The refrain flips the expected direction: the chemical is not what he is trying to escape but what is getting rid of something worse, someone worse, the goodbye almost a relief.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~choke": {"haiku":"A relationship ends as one partner rejects the other's demands and unmet expectations, leaving each to consume their own pride and accept the hard parting.","opus":"From the reunited Alice in Chains, a worn-out rejection of a partner peddling phony rescue, the narrator well-rehearsed in departures and wanting no second-rate savior. Delicate things patched together threaten a steep collapse, and he tells the other to swallow their ego and leave if the life they built no longer suits them.","sonnet":"A fractured domestic arrangement cracks under accumulated tension. Someone has built walls before even arriving, and the narrator — practiced at endings — offers no rescue and demands none. The choice to stay or leave is laid bare: swallow the pride required, or go and face it alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A relationship confrontation staged as a dare: the speaker, armored by a lifetime of practice at leaving, tells a partner threatening to walk to just go already. But the bravado keeps cracking against admission. The one-word title does the work of two throats — the choke of swallowed pride the other refuses, and the choke of a speaker strangling on his own performed indifference. What complicates the ultimatum is the middle image of fragile things taped up from earlier breakage: whatever this is was already damaged before either party arrived, walls built by prior hurt that the newcomer came to dismantle, and the speaker knows the fall is a long one precisely because the closeness is real. So the refrain is less an eviction than a bluff, pride eaten alone on both sides of the door.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"the ultimatum is a bluff, pride eaten alone on both sides of the door","built":"the one-word title does two throats' work — swallowed pride and performed indifference choking together"},
"alice-in-chains~confusion": {"haiku":"A man inflicts emotional pain on his lover with detachment and cruelty. He digs deeper into their psyche, draining them while feeling nothing.","opus":"Layne Staley circling the entanglement of love, pain and numbness where inflicting hurt and feeling nothing become the same disease; the wish to free someone collides with the addict's inability to feel anything at all.","sonnet":"The narrator acknowledges he is a disease in someone else's life — drilling deeper into their head, causing suffering while feeling nothing, and recognizing the damage he does even as he's unable to stop it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song written from inside a predator's head, tracking a relationship as one long act of psychological invasion: the narrator burrows into his victim's mind while she is reduced to crawling toward him, and the choruses stack their two opposed vocabularies — the tender language of liberation and love against a family of verbs for drowning and dying — so that the abuser's stated wish to hand her release reads as the disease he asks her to recognize in him. The refusal of feeling is the point; her tears register nothing, and Staley's own lyric lets that flatness stand rather than resolving it. What earns the read is the turn in the last verse, where the opening claim that he had nothing left to offer flips into a promise that the whole of him is hers now, and the singular I becomes we — the cruelty completing itself not by breaking her but by pulling her down into the same crawl, complicity dressed as reconciliation. On Facelift this is the debut's ugliest self-portrait, Staley voicing not addiction yet but the same appetite that consumes another person and calls it love.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"abuser's offer of release presented as the disease itself","built":"closing pronoun shift from I to we completes the predator's logic"},
"alice-in-chains~dam-that-river": {"haiku":"Aggressive dominance and emotional coldness; the singer pursues, harms, and rejects their target, demonstrating cruelty and indifference while being swept away by an unstoppable force.","opus":"A ferocious kiss-off aimed at a treacherous adversary, the narrator relishing every image of destroying them without mercy. Behind the violence is the claim that his fury could not be held back or contained, and that it swept him beyond the other person's reach entirely.","sonnet":"A vicious song about dominating and punishing someone the narrator despises — burning, kicking, drowning, raking — though the torrent of rage ultimately washed him away from a person he considers a snake and a fake.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cantrell stacks a verse of escalating cartoon violence — drowning, trampling, a kick to the face, a place set on fire — over a groove so blunt it dares you to take the brutality literally, when the song grew out of a real blowup with drummer Sean Kinney — the argument Cantrell later owned up to, the one that ended with a coffee table broken over his head. The chorus reframes all of it: the other person is a dam, the singer is a river, and the point is that nothing they built could hold him back or contain the flow — the violence is really an argument about being uncontainable. The sting sits in the shrug that follows, the admission that he may not care either way, so the swagger of being unstoppable comes pre-soured with the suspicion that the current only ever washed him further out and alone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","freedom & escape"],"means":"the swagger of being uncontainable already soured by drift and solitude","built":"the river-and-dam chorus reframes cartoon violence as an argument about containment"},
"alice-in-chains~deaf-ears-blind-eyes": {"haiku":"A person is isolated despite abundance, numb and tied by pain; possession brings only suffering. The song depicts internal coldness becoming permanent and unshakeable.","opus":"A picture of numb isolation, lonely even in a crowd as hurt closes in and an inner chill sets like stone, time draining while chances worth seizing slip past unnoticed. From Alice in Chains' reunion era with William DuVall, it carries the band's dread-soaked harmonies and sludgy grind, the burning-soul refrain smoldering under a resignation that feels bone-deep and final.","sonnet":"Narcissism observed from outside — someone who claims to own everything, whose complaints are set at maximum volume, whose inner fire has become corrosive. The onlooker is torn between pity and revulsion: watching a soul on fire while time slips past, memories worth making ignored, the cold growing too deep to warm. The distance is both chosen and inescapable.","src":"haiku","fable":"Willful sensory shutdown works here as a survival tactic: the closed ears and eyes of the title are latched from the inside, chosen rather than inflicted. The speaker keeps claiming total ownership, yet the inventory sits in an empty hall where no one comes to play, and each boast lands as an accounting of things while the man himself quietly stops showing up. His stalled refusal to move forward reads less as backbone than as inertia rebranded as conviction, a way to hold still and call it a stand. Cut during the fog of loss that reshaped this band, the imagery of tears drifting off and the baffled question of vanished time turns the numbness elegiac — mourning frozen into anesthesia, farewells waved not from peace but because feeling has been switched off. What lingers is the sight of a life going past the glass unclaimed, still there to be lived, and deliberately not reached for.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","death & grief"],"means":"mourning frozen into anesthesia, a life deliberately not reached for","built":"each boast about ownership lands as an accounting of things while the man quietly stops showing up"},
"alice-in-chains~dirt": {"haiku":"A person brings out the speaker's darkest impulses—they've been driven to despair and degradation by this relationship, reduced to dirt.","opus":"Alice in Chains sink to grunge's bleakest floor, Layne Staley begging to be murdered, buried, and peeled off the walls while addressing someone whose gift is making him feel worthless as filth. Self-loathing fuses with dependency, and the refrain frames anyone indifferent to his suffering as someone who has no business existing.","sonnet":"Someone has degraded another so thoroughly that oblivion starts to feel preferable to continued existence. The speaker oscillates between self-disgust and fury at the person who possesses the talent to make him feel worthless, burying him alive in his own shame.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track of the band's addiction album collapses two annihilations into one grammar: a self-loathing that wants to be put down like a sick animal, and a poisoned relationship that does the burying. The verses are pure suicidal ideation given to another person as a favor to beg — the mouth here wants a gun barrel the way it wants a fix, self-destruction dressed as appetite. But the song's real move is in the pivot from I to you: the addressee is credited with a talent, and that talent is the power to reduce the speaker to dirt and then cover him with it. Whether the you is a lover, a dealer, or the drug itself hardly matters, because the read that holds all three is codependence — the accusation that someone made me crazy is also a plea for them to finish the job, and the refrain about hiding from what is wrong admits that the harm was always known and chosen anyway.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","suicide","love & desire"],"means":"codependence staged as an accusation that doubles as a plea to finish the job","built":"the pivot from I to you credits the addressee with the power of burial, collapsing lover, dealer, and drug into one grammatical slot"},
"alice-in-chains~don-t-follow": {"haiku":"A wandering figure refuses connection, admits being lost and frightened, and withdraws from those who care despite awareness of their own declining state.","opus":"A worn-out farewell from a man wandering his own road, telling someone not to follow into his hollow misery as he loses his woman, his friends, and himself to sweat-soaked sleeplessness and an aging, cold-mirror face. This acoustic Alice in Chains piece foregrounds Layne Staley's addiction-haunted weariness, the final plea to be taken home ringing with the doomed exhaustion that shadowed the band's early-'90s Seattle catalogue.","sonnet":"A wrecked life addressed to someone still intact — the speaker has lost friends, a woman, coherent direction, and now simply wanders, drinks, watches his face age in mirrors too cold to flatter. The parting advice is genuine: don't come this way. The road he is on leads nowhere good, and the best gift left to give is the warning.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built as a farewell from a man who has already decided he is beyond saving, and whose only remaining act of love is to warn everyone else off. Jerry Cantrell opens by refusing home, then pivots to address the friend still living full throttle, passing the bottle — the two of them mirror images of the same appetite, except one has stopped pretending he can be pulled back. The genius of the writing is that don't follow works two ways at once: it is both a plea not to be pursued and a caution not to imitate, the addict telling the drinker that this road leads exactly where it looks like it leads. Then the perspective collapses inward — the aging face in the cold mirror, the words on a page read back as already dead — until the repeated cry to be taken home resolves the earlier refusal: he no longer means the house he walked out of but somewhere final, and the parting line returns stripped of its swagger, now pure resignation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"warning disguised as farewell from a man who has already stopped pretending","built":"the refusal of home in the opening returns stripped of swagger at the close, now meaning somewhere final"},
"alice-in-chains~down-in-a-hole": {"haiku":"A person trapped in depression and self-sabotage contemplates their inability to escape despite recognizing their situation; they describe denying themselves growth and freedom while drowning in emotional confinement.","opus":"Layne Staley's tender despair, love offered from the bottom of a self-dug grave by a man who can no longer be who others expected. The addiction subtext is unmistakable, wings denied, the heart decorated like a tomb, yet still reaching toward someone.","sonnet":"A person so deep in depression and self-denial that they feel entombed — they've silenced their own feelings and aspirations for so long that their wings are atrophied, and the hole is all they know now.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cantrell wrote this for a partner and Staley sings it as self-indictment, and the song lives in that seam: an act of love voiced from a man who has decided he is unfit to receive it. The image-system fuses opposites until they stop opposing — womb becomes tomb, the buried thing blooms, a heart is groomed and dressed as though for its own funeral — so that intimacy and burial share one gesture, and to give oneself away is indistinguishable from being interred. The verses are a slow closing-off: he decorates his pain, admits he keeps himself from becoming himself, then swears his inner life will stay unspoken, sealing the last exit as the stones are set in place. What pins it is the refusal underneath the surrender — the wish to fly answered instantly by the body pinned to the earth, desire and its foreclosure landing in the same breath, which is the Dirt-era Staley voice exactly: not fighting the hole but narrating its walls from inside. The bridge's plea to be inside another is the closest thing to escape offered, and it collapses back into the burial line, love reaching for rescue and finding only deeper ground.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","love & desire","death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"love offered as self-indictment from a man certain he cannot be saved","built":"womb and tomb fused into one image so intimacy and burial share a single gesture"},
"alice-in-chains~drone": {"haiku":"Entanglement with another person becomes spiritual and transcendent yet the speaker remains caught between light and dark, feeding a black hole of need.","opus":"Grunge veterans Alice in Chains craft a hypnotic study of self-destruction and unhealthy attachment, voiced by a devoted addict who drifts in and out of sanity and pours himself into ruinous appetites. Aware that others pity him, he clings on anyway, bound to his vice like a doomed lover, keeping the band's signature dread quietly smoldering.","sonnet":"Obsessive devotion to a transcendent connection that costs everything and is kept anyway. He is a drone — busy, dedicated, feeding a dark gravitational pull he has named and chosen — aware that others see it as tragedy, carrying the anger and the cost without dullness, staying deliberately at the edge of madness.","src":"haiku","fable":"The speaker addresses whatever he has enthroned as his higher power, and the song deliberately withholds whether that power is a lover, a chemical, or the self-erasing worship of both at once. Each vow of beauty and ascent comes billed against his own flesh, a transcendence purchased on credit that never fully arrives, leaving him suspended between shades and calling his stalled madness a discipline. The confession lands where he chooses to keep tending a hungry gravity well that swallows everything and returns nothing, and refuses to soothe the fury it breeds because that fury is the one thing still burning and still his. What wears the costume of a love song is really devotion as occupation, narrated from deep inside the orbit with total clarity and no wish to break loose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","love & desire","anger & defiance"],"means":"devotion as occupation, narrated from deep inside the orbit with no wish to break loose","built":"the song withholds what is enthroned — lover, chemical, or self-erasure — leaving the vow structurally ambiguous throughout"},
"alice-in-chains~fly": {"haiku":"Following false promises leads to falling and breaking; the narrator waits for storms to clear so they can finally fly free.","opus":"From the band's post-reunion era with William DuVall beside Jerry Cantrell, this one advises riding out an inevitable collapse until conditions improve enough to move on. Warnings about counterfeit riches and alluring but expired temptations frame hard-earned disillusionment. The narrator confesses how little he's gained yet tells the listener to trust their own instincts. Alice in Chains' harmonized gloom carries the grim reassurance.","sonnet":"A worn-down guide offers hard-won advice to someone being seduced by fool's gold: rest when tired, follow the heart, distrust the shiny. The storm will not lift quickly, but endurance is the only honest path to open sky.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is elder counsel dressed as a warning, delivered by someone who walked the route ahead of you and came back to report that its glittering pavement was counterfeit, worthless under the shine. The plain survival advice — rest where you can, feed on love when you are empty — is undercut by a blunt confession of how meager the speaker's own haul turned out to be, so the wisdom stays honest about its own poverty. Collapse gets reframed as a stage rather than a defeat: the choking and weeping are simply the toll for enduring the downpour, and the same weathering that strips away every shelter is what finally clears the sky enough to lift you. The result is a peculiarly hard-won optimism for this band, locating deliverance not in flight itself but in outlasting the weather that grounds you, the ascent earned by endurance and never quietly gifted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"ascent earned by endurance, never quietly gifted","built":"survival advice undercut by the speaker's own blunt confession of a meager haul — the wisdom honest about its poverty"},
"alice-in-chains~frogs": {"haiku":"Childhood innocence fades into awareness of pain and betrayal, questioning why friendships must end and suffering must begin.","opus":"A brooding rumination on false friendship, confusion, and lost innocence, the narrator alone despite being included, watching childhood curdle into pain too soon and demanding to know why it has to be this way. Alice in Chains, the Seattle band led by Layne Staley, sink into their trademark slow, dread-soaked heaviness here, the fractured second half spiraling into paranoid, hallucinatory bitterness that captures their darkest emotional register.","sonnet":"The slow drift from childhood innocence into adult pain, traced through shifting colors and a child's unclaimed song that eventually runs cold. At the center is a Tuesday morning at age twenty-eight — a specific, unremarkable moment — where someone scraped themselves off a wall and resolved to wake up rather than drown any longer in the ache that had accumulated.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sprawling Layne Staley confessional that opens as a bruised inquiry into hollow companionship and slowly curdles into something a great deal darker. The dilating eyes and drifting hues register a chemical already at work, softening the quiet until it feels like comfort rather than isolation, a narcotic haze standing in for the belonging the singer cannot find. Its nursery-rhyme middle rewinds to a childhood purity sealed inside numb insulation, ripened into hurt before the mind could name it, so dependency reads as an injury inflicted long before choice existed. The closing stretch abandons that lullaby entirely, fixing on a Tuesday morning near his twenty-eighth year and muttering of expiration and a slate wiped clean, until the escape from that ache tips out of relief and becomes a promise leveled at the drug, the self, and death alike. Repetition here is not a refrain but a trap, the plaintive circling of that unanswerable question making plain the man is rehearsing his own vanishing rather than seeking any exit from it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","suicide"],"means":"rehearsing a vanishing rather than seeking any exit from the ache","built":"repetition functions as a trap rather than a refrain, the plaintive circling enacting the loop it describes"},
"alice-in-chains~get-born-again":{"haiku":"Suffering and grief wash over the speaker as they mourn what cannot be held; redemption through repetition feels hollow when nothing truly changes.","opus":"A cynical swipe at cheap salvation, mocking the idea that a few rehearsed words can wipe a lifetime of wrongdoing clean. Amid false friends and comforting self-deceptions, the wrecked narrator keeps up a pretense that something dead is still living, and wonders who will ever own up to any of the deceit.","sonnet":"A song about grief and the hollow ritual of religious absolution — the narrator is talking to someone dead or gone, pretending they are still alive, rejecting the idea that a few repeated lines could wipe out the accumulated lies and suffering.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cut near the end of Layne Staley's time in the studio, this treats redemption as a vending-machine transaction: rinse the sins, mouth a rehearsed phrase or two, and walk out reborn on demand. The narrator props a vanished intimate up in the present tense, bargaining for protection from someone he already knows is gone, then picks the dreariest weather he can find so a curtain of mist can swallow whatever expressions might betray him. Behind that private wreckage the bracketed refrain wheels around like an unnamed indictment — a figure who disowned everything and knotted every falsehood into one rope — while the closing minutes drop all reasoning and simply fuse ruin to rebirth in a circuit with no exit. The relentless looping delivers the verdict: rebirth here is salvation stuck skipping, the same damage played back until the needle wears through.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"salvation stuck skipping, redemption as a vending-machine transaction with no exit","built":"the closing minutes drop all reasoning and loop ruin to rebirth in a circuit — the damaged record as verdict","fnote":"Salvation here takes one repeated gesture — say a couple of lines — and on this record the word cuts both ways: the rote scripture of rent-a-rebirth, and the other kind of line, the habit the whole catalog knows. The pun is the sermon."},
"alice-in-chains~god-am": {"haiku":"Questioning God's benevolence and power while stuck paying taxes despite death; the speaker's sarcasm critiques divine authority and inherited suffering.","opus":"From the band's 1995 self-titled album, recorded amid Layne Staley's deepening addiction, this track hurls raw grievance at the divine. The narrator drops pretence, indicting a God who sends endless death yet grins while winning and gathers faith from fable. The bitter refrain asking whether he can be as his God is turns despair into blasphemous defiance, a sick man demanding answers from a heaven he cannot respect.","sonnet":"A sardonic conversation with a God who seems indifferent to death and human suffering while expecting gratitude and faith. He catalogues the transaction — sins forgiven in exchange for devotion — and questions whether aspiring to divine detachment is possible or even desirable for someone still paying taxes while the world decays.","src":"haiku","fable":"Layne Staley opens a prayer as a grievance and never lets it become supplication: God is greeted like an absentee acquaintance, then billed for the death he keeps mailing while the speaker keeps paying his taxes. The bitterness turns on a caught grin — a deity proud of gathering faith from fable, winning a rigged game against people too sick to fold. The refrain's deliberately broken grammar, God am, is the whole song: it collapses the distance between worshipper and worshipped into one damaged first-person, asking whether he can be as monstrously self-sufficient as the thing he's accusing, and hearing no answer but the phrase eroding down to the bare noun. From a man whose addiction was public knowledge, the theology reads as autobiography — the sick one being weeded, indicting the gardener.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"prayer turned grievance, the worshipper billing the deity for every death mailed","built":"the deliberately broken grammar of the title collapses worshipper and worshipped into one damaged first-person"},
"alice-in-chains~godsmack": {"haiku":"A speaker calls out someone's drug addiction and cruel indifference, trapped in a cycle of dependence and pain.","opus":"Alice in Chains stare directly at heroin addiction, addressing a user whose dead-eyed lies fool no one as the drug becomes dearer than any person. God's name, the lyric concludes, is smack for some. Layne Staley's own struggles haunt this Dirt-era track, making its confrontation of the needle and its false comforts painfully first-hand rather than observed.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone destroyed by heroin addiction — a former companion whose devotion to the drug now eclipses every human bond. The narrator watches with a mixture of bitterness and grief, naming the substance plainly at the close as the god that has claimed this person.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title collapses a deity into a syringe, and the song is Layne Staley narrating how heroin gets enthroned inside a body that keeps insisting the worship is someone else's failing. What begins as accusation aimed outward — at a man who has discarded everyone who loved him, whose gaze has gone vacant — curdles as the pronouns fuse, until accuser and junkie are one figure gnawing at himself, chasing the poison with more of the same poison. The beloved horse is the drug, prized above any human tie, and the chorus that coaxes a vein open reads as the addiction speaking in its own honeyed pitch, so the taunt wounds the tempter and the tempted in a single stroke. It closes on a verdict that doubles as surrender: getting high is now a way of dying, and the sacred figure being knelt before turns out to be nothing but the powder itself, a confession offered without any hope of rescue.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","suicide"],"means":"the drug enthroned inside a body that insists the worship belongs to someone else","built":"pronouns fuse across the verses until accuser and junkie are one figure, the taunt wounding both in a single stroke"},
"alice-in-chains~got-me-wrong":{"haiku":"Emotional misunderstanding in a fragile connection; the singer feels unseen and untrusted, reaching for something strong but fearing the relationship will not endure.","opus":"A thorny reflection on being misread while reaching for genuine closeness, the singer defending his individuality against a partner who flinches from anything strong and real. Underneath runs the ache of feeling stranded beyond the reach of love, still clutching a thin hope that things might resolve.","sonnet":"A song about being fundamentally misread by someone who runs from real emotional intensity — the narrator is tired of colorless conformity and wants connection, but the person keeps misunderstanding who he is and retreating.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being misjudged by someone who mistakes reticence for coldness and flinches from the very intensity the singer holds out. The push-pull runs the whole way through: he insists he has been read backwards, that the other's uncertainty is what drives their retreat from something real, yet confesses he is threadbare, half-unraveling, worn past the point of trusting any tenderness to hold. That is the ache at the center of Jerry Cantrell's writing here — the guarded man who craves closeness but can only frame it as a dare, so the plea to stay arrives braided with a warning that he may already have slipped away. The refrain works as both defense and admission: you have misread me, but so, quietly, have I.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The chorus quietly switches verbs — first 'you run from something strong,' later 'we reach for something strong': accusation folding into shared craving on the repeat.","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the plea to stay arrives braided with a warning that he may have already slipped away","built":"the refrain works as both defense and admission — misread by the other, and quietly by himself","fnote2":"The refrain mutates once, and it's the whole argument: you run from something strong becomes we reach for something strong — the accusation turned into a shared craving."},
"alice-in-chains~got-me-wrong-album-version":{"haiku":"Misinterpretation and longing where attraction remains unreciprocated despite profound feeling. The narrator claims unique pain in a love that refuses completion.","opus":"A knotted push and pull about a lover who misjudges him, the singer stretching toward a bond with some real force behind it while admitting the connection has drifted past any hope of lasting love. Yearning and mistrust tangle together, leaving only a faint wish that it works out.","sonnet":"A plea from someone who has been misread by a hesitant lover: the speaker admits they have drifted too far from love, that sweetness rarely touches them, but insists this connection is real and strong — uncertainty will not hold them if the other person dares to stay.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~grind": {"haiku":"The speaker warns against planning someone's funeral before they die and refuses to let enemies blind them. They grind their teeth against pressure and hear inner voices.","opus":"A defiant retort to everyone who prematurely wrote off the narrator and the band, cautioning them not to bury him while he still breathes. Restless, sleepless imagery and a menacing warning to anyone who toys with him frame the song as endurance and simmering rage against being counted out too soon.","sonnet":"A warning to someone who has written the narrator off too soon — not to assume the body is already dead — laced with cold menace: the narrator could destroy you, but would rather sleep and hear the voice inside that keeps him going.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jerry Cantrell aims this at everyone rehearsing an obituary too early, turning premature mourning into a taunt from a man who insists he is still standing and still dangerous. The scorn cuts two ways, against a public convinced it already knows the ending and against a rival whose flattery and deceit have curdled into the same rotten thing. What sounds like a promise of harm reads instead as sheer refusal, a jaw clenched against sleeplessness and doubt, spite hardening into the fuel that keeps him upright.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"spite hardened into fuel for a man insisting he is still standing and still dangerous","built":"scorn cuts two ways at once — at a public writing the obituary and at a rival whose flattery curdled into the same rot"},
"alice-in-chains~hate-to-feel": {"haiku":"Emotional paralysis and self-hatred reflecting back from the mirror, the speaker consumed by rage and resigned numbness equally torturous.","opus":"Layne Staley writing his own addiction from the inside, past denial and into recognition: the dread of becoming his father, the way a curiosity hardened into need. What lands is the wish not to numb pain but to stop perceiving at all.","sonnet":"Addiction recognized as inheritance — pin cushion medicine, little bug for a pet, the narrator who swore he'd never be like his old man facing the mirror to find exactly that. Sensation becomes unbearable: wishing he couldn't see, couldn't feel, hating the very capacity for perception that shows him what he's become.","src":"haiku","fable":"The horror here is that clarity never buys escape: the refrain wishing away both sight and feeling collapses them into one wound, since knowing the truth and being numb enough to bear it are the same act. Early verses stagger through self-disgust and animal degradation, but the needle's curiosity has curdled into sustenance, the fix domesticated down to something kept like a pet cushioning the veins. The blow that lands hardest is a vow never to become the father dissolving into flat surrender before the wrecked thing in the mirror, self-loathing offered not as a plea but as an accepted verdict.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The father-line resolves differently each pass — 'face exactly what I am' the first time, 'who I am' the second: the inventory turning from condition to identity between choruses.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"clarity that never buys escape, sight and numbness collapsed into one wound","built":"the refrain's wish to be rid of both feeling and sight fuses them — knowing and being numb described as the same act"},
"alice-in-chains~hate-to-feel-1992": {"haiku":"Someone spirals into drug addiction and self-destruction, seeing clearly all the rot and decay around them, becoming what they swore they'd never be—like their father before.","opus":"Layne Staley's addiction reckoning, numbness curdling into self-disgust as he faces becoming exactly the father he swore he'd never be, the drug now sustenance and feeling itself the thing he most wants to switch off.","sonnet":"A song about heroin addiction and becoming your father — the narrator numbs himself with drugs, recognises the pattern (\"pin cushion medicine\" in New Orleans), and faces the ugly self-recognition that he has turned into exactly what he swore he'd never be.","src":"haiku","fable":"The horror here is that clarity never buys escape: the refrain wishing away both sight and feeling collapses them into one wound, since knowing the truth and being numb enough to bear it are the same act. Early verses stagger through self-disgust and animal degradation, but the needle's curiosity has curdled into sustenance, the fix domesticated down to something kept like a pet cushioning the veins. The blow that lands hardest is a vow never to become the father dissolving into flat surrender before the wrecked thing in the mirror, self-loathing offered not as a plea but as an accepted verdict.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The father-line resolves differently each pass — 'face exactly what I am' the first time, 'who I am' the second: the inventory turning from condition to identity between choruses.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"clarity that never buys escape, sight and numbness collapsed into one wound","built":"the refrain's wish to be rid of both feeling and sight fuses them — knowing and being numb described as the same act"},
"alice-in-chains~head-creeps": {"haiku":"The narrator feels trapped in redundant cycles and emotionally abused, desperate to untangle from manipulation and escape the shadowing control.","opus":"From the murky self-titled album cut during Layne Staley's deepening isolation, the track festers in resentment and dissolving identity. The narrator, weary of infantile claims and being slapped aside, works to untangle himself from a manipulative other, plotting voodoo revenge with a pin-stuck doll. The refrain about a creeping head and a departing plane maps a mind slowly detaching from itself, unnervingly numb.","sonnet":"Trapped in a toxic dynamic — dismissed, sidelined, and struck repeatedly — a person struggles to cut loose from someone whose infantile demands and redundant manipulations have drained all patience. The mind keeps creeping toward an exit, toward a day a plane finally leaves and does not return.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Layne Staley lyric that turns a poisoned relationship into a war of paranoia, where the other person is both jailer and infection, and the strings he begs to sever are the same ones crawling into his skull. So the title names the creep of a mind that can no longer tell surveillance from its own compulsion, the plea to call the dogs off pinned against the head that follows the escaping plane anyway. What earns the length is the late turn from prey to hex-worker: the sniping and the barbed intimacy curdle into effigy magic, the enemy sewn into a black dress and stabbed by proxy so her mouth borrows his grin. The retaliation completes the contamination it was meant to flee, and the closing so crazy lands less as accusation than as a diagnosis he now shares with her.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","faith & the occult","surveillance & control"],"means":"contamination completing the flight it was meant to end","built":"the title names the compulsion surveillance and the self can no longer separate"},
"alice-in-chains~heaven-beside-you":{"haiku":"Proximity to paradise brings internal hell; the speaker sees faded, broken people who claim heaven yet harbor hellish internal landscapes.","opus":"Cantrell's ambivalent breakup song about someone with paradise within reach yet hell inside, sung by a narrator too faded and jaded to save them. The distance in his voice is its own quiet grief.","sonnet":"A song about watching someone destroy themselves while remaining coolly detached — the narrator is too jaded and hollowed out to care about the other person's problems, and would rather be himself, broken as he is, than be them.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jerry Cantrell sings lead here rather than Layne Staley, and the song reads as his letter out of a love that has gone cold, the title itself the cruelty of it: whatever heaven exists is beside the other person, never within reach, a warmth held at arm's length like a winter that keeps arriving. The middle verse is the honest ledger of a partner who can see the wreckage clearly and admits his own dulled, faded, checked-out state as part of the problem rather than pretending to stand above it. The turn is grammatical and devastating: the closing verse keeps the same words but swaps the pronouns, so the diagnosis he first aimed at himself gets handed back across the divide, and the accusation of being blind and out of one's mind lands on the other. Read against Alice in Chains at mid-decade, it is hard not to hear the second address braided into the romantic one, a bandmate watching someone dear come apart and choosing, in the bruised refrain, to rather be himself than them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The refrain's modal creep tells the story in one word per pass — you think you have it still, then wish you had it, then know you have it: certainty, loss, and consolation cycling through the same line.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"warmth held at arm's length until it becomes accusation","built":"the closing verse keeps the same words but swaps every pronoun","fnote2":"The refrain quietly hardens its verb each pass — think you have it, wish you had it, know you have it — certainty arriving in stages, heaven inside settled only by the last repetition."},
"alice-in-chains~hollow": {"haiku":"Emotional and spiritual emptiness descending into silence and loss of direction. The imagery of mountains hollowed from within reflects internal desolation and erosion.","opus":"A portrait of emotional emptiness, using a hollowed-out, drilled mountain as an image for someone drained and disoriented. Colors fade to grey, meaning drains away, and a deafening silence leaves the narrator unable to tell up from down.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone who has been emotionally hollowed out — drained and drilled through like a mountain — leaving only confusion, silence, and an inability to tell up from down. The song observes how a person loses their mind and their color, becoming unrecognizable.","src":"haiku","fable":"A self can look intact while everything load-bearing inside it has been quietly stripped away. The mountain is the figure for that: tunneled and mined below, yet crowned with a serene, cold sky above. The verses trace the erosion — the ease of feeding off a weaker thing against the difficulty of honest speech, colors draining to grey, the numb sense that nothing matters. As the first Alice in Chains record fronted by William DuVall rather than Layne Staley, the band's dread reads less as narcotic haze than as the depletion of grief and depression. The paradox lands hardest when silence turns so loud that up and down lose meaning. The emptiness is not quiet at all but a roar that erases orientation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"depletion mistaken for serenity from the outside","built":"the emptiness resolves not into quiet but into a roar that erases orientation"},
"alice-in-chains~hung-on-a-hook": {"haiku":"The narrator mirrors and reflects others' emptiness and change, trapped in stillness while watching the world shift around him; he is broken and disposable.","opus":"A William DuVall-era meditation on being a cracked mirror that reflects others while feeling hollow and unsavable inside. The recurring plea to be discarded frames self-worthlessness as a kind of mercy killing, the world unchanged whatever anyone does.","sonnet":"About being someone else's mirror and watching them change until you're broken and discarded — the narrator is a cracked reflection of the person they love, dissolving into fragments on the floor while the other person moves on, ultimately framing the only mercy left as euthanasia.","src":"haiku","fable":"The conceit is a speaker who exists only as reflective glass, incapable of acting on the world, able only to return the observer's ruined image back doubled and multiplied until the watcher must confront the person they have hardened into. That helplessness is the DuVall-era band's recurring nerve: witness without agency, complicity that comes from merely showing rather than intervening. When the surface finally breaks and its splinters scatter across the tile, each fragment keeps reporting the same face upward, so the destroyed thing goes on doing its one function even in pieces. The final gesture withholds rescue and offers extinction instead, reframing the shattering as a grim kindness — the reflection ending the subject it can no longer stand to keep replaying.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","shame & repression","death & grief"],"means":"extinction offered as the last available kindness","built":"the shattered surface keeps performing its single function in every fragment"},
"alice-in-chains~i-can-t-remember": {"haiku":"A traumatized person cannot recall their identity after years of abuse and neglect, left with rage but nothing else.","opus":"A raw early Alice in Chains cut voicing a battered, starving, grey-eyed narrator who cannot recall his own identity or even weep once his sight is stripped away. Fragments of amnesia, infancy, and screaming visions curdle into a survivor's fury, and he swears he retains enough loathing to keep breathing and drag his stolen vitality back before ending on a bare claim of still living.","sonnet":"A person so eroded by pain they have lost their own name confronts those who tried to break them — but finds, beneath the amnesia and the anger, a fierce insistence on survival. Identity may be gone; the will to be is not.","src":"haiku","fable":"Recovery from a beating becomes recovery of the self here, and the refusal to remember is the engine of both. The opening voice is depleted almost past personhood — starved, eyes going grey, unable even to answer what its name is — and the amnesia it keeps repeating isn't a symptom so much as a scorched-earth defense, an erasure of the injury and the attacker together. From that blankness the song claws backward toward origin, the infant rage that predates language, as if identity has to be rebuilt from the earliest and least civilized material available. What powers the turn is spite: hate becomes literal breath, and the survivor promises to reverse the theft, draining the aggressor the way it was drained. The last move dismantles the abuser's authority — the loss of a god that no longer commands fear or blood — so the closing assertion of being alive lands not as gratitude but as defiance, a self reassembled out of everything that tried to destroy it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"amnesia as scorched-earth defense rebuilt into spite","built":"identity reconstructed from infant rage before language returns"},
"alice-in-chains~i-know-somethin-bout-you": {"haiku":"Intimate knowledge and secrets bind the speaker to another; hidden truths remain unspoken yet known.","opus":"A sneering, possessive threat dressed as a taunt, all leverage and spite. The narrator holds something over someone and savors the power to humiliate, the menace curdled with dependency he won't admit.","sonnet":"A speaker holds damaging knowledge over someone as leverage — threatening to tell their parents, blackball their name, make them pay — while the refrain \"on your face, in my space\" maps a dynamic of intrusion and dominance. The threat is both petty and total: the speaker will use every angle to keep the other person bound to them.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jealousy dressed up as intimacy: the speaker hoards secrets the way a blackmailer hoards evidence, threatening to call the parents, poison the reputation, and extract a toll for the crime of wanting distance. The chorus exposes the con outright, since holding a hidden fact over someone is the only closeness left once desire has soured into craving and he wants her dissolved into his bloodstream like a fix. What sells the menace is its adolescent smallness, a sneering possessiveness that mistakes control for love and finds its real thrill in the prospect of leaving a person wrecked.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"blackmail dressed as intimacy, control mistaken for desire","built":"the chorus names the con outright without breaking the threat"},
"alice-in-chains~i-stay-away": {"haiku":"The singer distances themselves from someone manipulative and emotionally damaging who hides deceit behind their appearance.","opus":"Keeping his distance from a volatile figure whose menace reads less like performance than real instability, the singer treats withdrawal as self-defense. The cryptic twist that this person's frailty somehow strengthens him, and that recognition will come in time, frames the retreat from someone shifty and draining.","sonnet":"About recognising that a relationship is toxic and choosing distance over engagement — the narrator sees through someone's erratic behaviour and chooses self-preservation, the repetition of the title emphasising deliberate withdrawal.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about self-preservation as a survival tactic learned from a toxic bond, where putting ground between two people is the only clean move left. The opening yearning for warmer country and the certainty that no one will wave him through frame withdrawal as an exit route that keeps getting sealed off, while the taunting couplets aimed at an erratic, shifty partner expose a relationship of mutual suspicion where the singer can no longer tell performance from real damage. The turn comes when he claims her weakness feeds his own strength, a bitter, defensive enlightenment that curdles retreat into contempt, so the repeated refrain lands less as liberation than as a mantra he keeps chanting to force belief. Given Layne Staley and Jerry Cantrell's shared writing on Jar of Flies-era Alice in Chains, the ornate string arrangement pulling against that flat, hostile detachment makes keeping his distance sound like the wound, not the cure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance"],"means":"retreat curdled into contempt by a single bitter pivot","built":"the ornate string arrangement pulling against flat hostile detachment makes distance sound like the wound"},
"alice-in-chains~it-ain-t-like-that": {"haiku":"Physical and emotional suffering from disappointment, self-harm, and the cyclical nature of struggling through life.","opus":"A grim reckoning with pain, endurance, and how things have irreversibly changed, the narrator confronting mortality and a gnawing sense of inner decay as he realizes the future he awaited isn't what it was. Alice in Chains, the Seattle band fronted by Layne Staley, steep it in grinding heaviness and weary dread.","sonnet":"A recognition that a familiar cycle has finally broken — things that used to cut and burn no longer feel the same, the rot behind the smile has shifted, the buzzards have moved on. The speaker is writing without certainty, aware of their own slackness, but also aware that whatever kept repeating is over, and the ocean is vast enough to swallow a smaller fish without incident.","src":"haiku","fable":"An early Layne Staley song about the disorienting arrival of what you spent years wanting, and finding the wanting was the only thing holding you together. Each verse relocates the speaker along a track from suffering to accomplishment (strapped to a table, then fighting, then finally seated signing the words that close a deal) yet every version drags the same rot underneath a grin, so the refrain that the awaited cycle has changed lands as loss rather than triumph. The recurring dental image, a tooth felt to be decaying while the jaw keeps its social face working, makes the point physical: the payoff is here, the corruption below it never left, and shrinking into one more minnow in freshly vast water is the reward that reads like a threat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming"],"means":"arrival at the long-wanted thing exposes the wanting as the only glue","built":"each verse relocates the speaker yet drags the same rot beneath a social grin"},
"alice-in-chains~junkhead": {"haiku":"Drug addiction and the speaker's embrace of it; they view themselves as elite outsiders with stoners and junkies, rejecting conformity through substance use.","opus":"Layne Staley voices an addict who recasts his heroin habit as belonging to a superior clan of stoners and outsiders, waving off wealth and reputation as hollow. Alice in Chains built their early reputation on this unblinking candor, the bravado masking the very dependence it toasts as he dares outsiders to grasp a user's outlook they never can.","sonnet":"A self-proclaimed member of an outcast elite — stoners, junkies, freaks — defends his chosen intoxication as a form of contentment the straight world can't grasp. He measures bourgeois emptiness against his own chemically sustained happiness and finds the exchange more than fair.","src":"haiku","fable":"Staley builds a recruitment pitch from inside the habit, flipping the terms so that the wage-earning, credentialed world becomes the deprived one and the addict becomes the enlightened aristocrat of a chosen tribe. The seduction is argued with such warmth that it nearly convinces, which is precisely the trap: dependence dressing itself as freedom, the wreckage reframed as insight only a fellow user could grasp. What betrays the swagger is the collapse into a single looping brag about how often he does it, the sales patter worn down to compulsive repetition. Underneath the philosophy sits the plainer transaction — a dealer, a drug of choice, and a man who never runs out of reasons or money to keep scoring.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","artifice & performance"],"means":"dependence dressed as aristocratic enlightenment until the sales patter loops","built":"the philosophy collapses into a single looping brag, compulsion outlasting the argument"},
"alice-in-chains~killing-yourself-1988-demo":{"haiku":"Drug dealers and users spiral into crime, overdose, and death in a cycle of self-destruction.","opus":"A gritty outlaw sketch of running from the law after a girl named Suzy goes down, a needle always in her arm, blood turning cold, all riding a hard-boozing, self-destructive momentum toward the refrain of killing yourself. A 1988 demo from Alice in Chains' earliest days, it shows the Seattle band in their scrappy pre-fame form, the addiction themes and doom-laced swagger that would define them already taking shape.","sonnet":"A fugitive cycle starring Suzy — a woman with a needle always in her arm, drawing men into her orbit and destroying them, burning through whatever supplies exist until Sunday runs out and the law catches up. The refrain is blunt moral accounting that applies equally to her and everyone around her: this lifestyle is slow execution.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~king-of-the-kats":{"haiku":"A braggadocious character celebrates their superiority, dominance, and hedonistic lifestyle without moral restraint.","opus":"A brash rockabilly-tinged boast, the narrator swaggering in leather, claiming to be the fastest draw around and drinking himself into legend while anointing himself top of the heap. It's pure self-mythology with little of the band's usual dark. Alice in Chains, fronted by Layne Staley, cut this early glam-tinged strut before their sound soured into the bleakness they became known for.","sonnet":"Pure swagger with no apology — hot leather, speed, the self-declared fastest gun in any room. The drinking-till-death line sits alongside the claim of being the best, and the whole thing plays as a rockabilly self-coronation where the title is assumed rather than earned and the only audience that matters is whoever shows up to watch.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~lab-monkey": {"haiku":"A captive subject endures experimentation and ridicule while powerless observers find entertainment in suffering. The song addresses cycles of abuse, recovery, and the speaker's refusal to accept victimization anymore.","opus":"The narrator as a caged, experimented-on test subject, chewing on his own skull and asking if anyone can hear him scratching through the wall. Turning the tables, he wishes his tormentor a long stay in the same cage, the deeper read a metaphor for being dehumanized, studied, and drained.","sonnet":"A person who has been used, experimented on, and degraded by someone they trusted — trapped in a cage of that relationship — finally reaches a point of refusal, declaring they've had enough of being stuck, blinded, and made to cry for the other person's satisfaction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A caged test subject narrates its own vivisection, and the terror is that the animal grasps precisely what is happening and who is doing it. Scratching the plea into the wall for any witness, the specimen grins up at the researchers descending toward it and keeps grinning as its skull is pried apart, refusing the role of mute meat on the table. The song bares its teeth in a curse hurled back at the tormentors, a promise they too will be locked behind this glass, and in the flat theorem that no creature can be understood until it is cut open and its living part is watched flickering out under the blade. Read through Jerry Cantrell's long fixation on addiction and human beings used up as raw material, the dissection doubles as a body surrendered to substances and to an industry that studies people for profit. The dried-out refrain of having taken all one can bear, of no fluid remaining to weep, is not collapse but the eerie composure of someone who has moved past fear entirely, an indictment of the curiosity that carves up something still alive and christens the wreckage knowledge.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"indictment of curiosity that carves something alive and calls the wreckage knowledge","built":"the specimen's grin held through the dissection reframes endurance as eerie composure rather than anguish"},
"alice-in-chains~last-of-my-kind":{"haiku":"A solitary figure resists forced conformity, defying authority and false piety to maintain individual strength and independence.","opus":"A defiant outsider's stand, the lone wolf surviving on scraps and vowing to tear down the temples of the liars who cornered him. DuVall channels a weathered, last-man-standing resolve that borders on holy war.","sonnet":"A lone wolf survivalist who refuses to bend — the temples are smashed, the liars are defeated, and the narrator crawls through rubble still standing as the last one of their type, living on scraps and fighting one more day.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survival hymn that reads inescapably against its origin: the first Alice in Chains record cut without Layne Staley, whose refrain of the sole holdout endures as the surviving band's bloodied claim on continuity. The lyric moves from wound to wrath, its narrator beginning as a scorned outsider — a lone wolf on the ridge, scavenging what others discard, cornered by odds already stacked past winning — then curdling into an iconoclast who razes the sanctuaries and wades the wreckage he made to weep over the dead. The defiled idol who opens the song is both the martyr he mourns and the template for his own unholy, unbowed persistence, so devotion and desecration collapse into a single gesture.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"devotion and desecration collapsed into a single gesture of survival","built":"the defiled idol the narrator mourns doubles as the template for his own unbowed persistence","fnote":"The hook stacks its pun: still standing, and standing down the law — survival and the refusal of the verdict in one phrase."},
"alice-in-chains~lesson-learned": {"haiku":"A reflection on wisdom gained through pain and disconnection. The speaker has learned harsh lessons about trust and self-deception, discovering that their darkest hour brought unexpected illumination.","opus":"Reflecting on hard-won experience, the song frames pain and scars as lessons and suggests that in your darkest hour a clarifying thought can strike like gold, even if it isn't the final answer.","sonnet":"The song reflects on hard-won self-knowledge — the narrator has been disconnected and self-deceiving, but in his darkest moment something clicks, offering not a complete answer but a small, real insight that serves as another lesson in survival.","src":"haiku","fable":"With Jerry Cantrell now carrying the melodies once owned by Layne Staley, the band's old vocabulary of needles and numbed retreat gets recast not as tragedy but as material for grinding, unglamorous survival. The verses keep circling that wreckage, yet the song mistrusts any tidy redemption arc, treating each hard insight as one modest deposit rather than a rescue. Its emotional wager sits in the refrain, where finding buried worth at rock bottom is instantly deflated: the thing that clicks is explicitly denied the status of a final answer. That refusal is the entire point, arguing that staying alive is a slow accrual of small corrections, and the flat, shrugging tally the singer keeps is a battered realist's way of insisting the work never actually concludes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"survival argued as slow accrual of small corrections, never a rescue","built":"the refrain explicitly denies the clicking insight the status of a final answer"},
"alice-in-chains~love-hate-love": {"haiku":"Intense love spirals into obsessive hatred and violence; the speaker alternates between devotion and wanting to harm their object of desire.","opus":"A disturbing account of devotion twisting into cruelty, the narrator craving total possession of someone even as that obsession becomes his torment. From Alice in Chains' debut, this early Layne Staley showcase pulls grunge into genuinely unsettling territory, its love-and-hate refrain wailed like an open wound as tenderness and violence blur beyond untangling.","sonnet":"Obsession curdled into something barely containable — a love that arrived too twisted to function, that burns the person who carries it and the person it was aimed at. The speaker knows he is the problem and cannot stop being it; the feeling is real but poisoned, and the plea to have his hands taken before he acts on it is the most honest moment in the song.","src":"haiku","fable":"An obsessive's confession that mistakes possession for devotion and can't tell adoration apart from the wish to destroy the thing adored. The title's three words aren't a progression but a single simultaneous state: Layne Staley sings the tenderness (the only one, the sweet angel) and the violence (peeling the face to find the real self) in the same breath, so that the vow to love and the urge to kill are the same impulse pointed at the same person. The pivot is the admission that he lives for her yet isn't alive himself, which reframes the whole song as a portrait of someone so hollowed out that another human being becomes the only proof he exists, and that dependency curdles instantly into menace. On Facelift this is the record's early signature of the addiction-shaped psyche AiC would spend a career anatomizing: a need so total it can only express itself as threat, mistaking the strangle for the embrace.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the need so total it can only express itself as threat","built":"the title's three words name a simultaneous state, not a progression"},
"alice-in-chains~love-song": {"haiku":"Sarcastic and abusive mockery of love, using crude language and degradation in a hostile parody of affection.","opus":"A deliberately shambolic studio goof, a mock love song collapsing into shouted absurdities about bleeding gums, telling someone off, and repeated non-sequiturs, ending with wry praise for a perfect take. It reads as a throwaway joke rather than a genuine confession. An oddity from Alice in Chains' vault, it captures the Seattle band clowning in the studio, a rare glimpse of humor cutting through their usually grim, addiction-shadowed catalogue.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is the whole joke: an anti-ballad that opens on a slate clap and ends with a producer congratulating the take, framing the tantrum in between as a performance nobody could mean. What spills out is love and hatred jammed together with no transition, an affection announced and immediately withdrawn, so the song reads less as feeling than as the machinery of feeling stripped for parts. The bleeding gums and the order to grin turn expression itself into an injury, and the descent into loops of nonsense, an actress's name, a stray line of Home on the Range, is the point where sense gives out entirely and only the ritual of repetition remains. As a hidden goof on the Sap EP it is the band letting the mask slip and clowning the very earnestness their reputation was built on, the surrounding sincerity making the parody land.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay","love & desire"],"means":"the machinery of feeling stripped for parts and framed as performance","built":"studio frame — opening slate clap, closing producer congratulation — pins the tantrum as artifact"},
"alice-in-chains~low-ceiling": {"haiku":"A person feels spatially trapped and constrained, too large or too ambitious for the confines around them, unable to breathe or expand. A transformative moment in time has warped them into this suffocating state.","opus":"Post-Staley Alice in Chains on the claustrophobia of feeling constrained by circumstance, where one person's limit is another's floor; a defining moment doesn't just shape you, it warps you, and no one else can talk you out of it.","sonnet":"A sense of being constrained and underestimated — too large for the space allotted — as the narrator questions why one person's ceiling is another's floor and pushes back against those who would define and limit them.","src":"haiku","fable":"The claustrophobia here is measured entirely by comparison — the same cramped height that pins one man down is the flat someone above him stands on comfortably, so the misery is relative, and relativity is what makes it unanswerable. He keeps the crucial variable blank on purpose: whether the fault is his own oversized shape or a space that was built too mean, a distinction he refuses to settle because settling it would end the argument. What shapes a person and what damages them arrive as a single stroke, one instant that carves the self and cracks it at once, and the song insists no outside voice has the standing to fix that instant's meaning. The final quarrel is with a ghostwriter who keeps overwriting him and denying him any glow, but by then the verses have already confessed the confinement was interior all along — so the accused scribe is just the part of him that narrates every defeat before he can.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"misery kept unanswerable by refusing to settle its own cause","built":"the speaker leaves the crucial variable blank to preserve the argument he lives inside"},
"alice-in-chains~lying-season": {"haiku":"During a season of widespread deception and blame, people hide behind false appearances while truth becomes obscured. The weight of lies accumulates as society moves past the point of recovery.","opus":"A weary meditation on a relationship and a world saturated with deceit, the narrator sensing decline past the point of return while pleading to be broken out of the protective shell he built around himself.","sonnet":"About living in a culture saturated with dishonesty — everything grins at you while pointing fingers, the point of decline has already been passed, and there's no time left. It also touches on a personal relationship where the narrator is trying to break free from a shell they've grown inside.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rarity buried on a box set, this comes from a band for whom deceit had become weather rather than event — a whole climate of it, ambient and unarguable, so denial itself feels beside the point. The verses circle the addict's oldest bind: the accusing finger, the burden he admits and instantly waves off in the same breath, that shrug landing less as peace than as anesthesia. What lifts the song past its own numbness is the pivot from a falsehood you utter to a decline you can no longer outrun, the wading image turning passive drift into something with an undertow. The closing collapse into no time, echoed to exhaustion, abandons the argument entirely and leaves only the clock — the mask slipping to reveal the panic it was covering.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"deceit become ambient weather, denial beside the point","built":"the closing collapse into echoed no time abandons argument and leaves only the clock"},
"alice-in-chains~man-in-the-box":{"haiku":"Trapped in waste and degradation, the singer experiences suffocation and mutilation while asking for salvation that never comes.","opus":"A claustrophobic scream from someone trapped and dehumanized, buried in filth like a beaten dog, begging to be saved while his eyes are sewn shut and his maker denied. Widely read as an attack on censorship and media control, blinding people to keep them passive, wrapped in addiction's suffocation.","sonnet":"A figure trapped and degraded — buried in filth, beaten like a dog — rails against a system that blinds and silences: the parenthetical voice is the system itself instructing submission and denial of the divine, making it also a critique of censorship and spiritual coercion.","src":"haiku","fable":"Layne Staley's two self-portraits set the frame: a man boxed in his own filth and a dog beaten with its nose shoved down, degradation and captivity fused into one image of an animal begging to be let out or put down. The plea to be saved goes unanswered, and the chorus turns that silence into the song's real engine, a call-and-response where a second voice keeps sewing the eyes shut against Cantrell's own censorship reading, where the media feeds the crowd its eyes only to blind it. Layne makes the private and the public rhyme: the addict buried alive and the culture that would rather stitch its eyes closed than look, both refusing the same rescue. The refrain about denying your maker and being wasted for trying folds his eventual fate into a threat, so the box is at once a cell, a coffin, and a screen. What lands is not any single meaning but the fixed loop itself, a scream for help repeated until the repetition becomes the answer no one gives.","fv":2,"fnote":"The box came from a dinner with Columbia executives that turned to how veal calves are raised — Staley wrote from inside the calf’s crate and fused it with his censorship idea; asked on TV what the song was about, Staley said media censorship and drummer Sean Kinney said veal.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"private burial and public blindness rhyming as the same refusal","built":"the fixed loop of the scream becomes the silence it was meant to answer"},
"alice-in-chains~man-in-the-box-live":{"haiku":"Entrapment and degradation become reflexes, a self-inflicted cycle where vision is stolen and resistance is worn down to nothing, leaving only the prospect of perpetual subjugation.","opus":"A claustrophobic cry from inside confinement and shame, the man boxed in his own filth begging to be saved while his eyes are sewn shut. The imagery of denying one's maker and being wasted has been read as censorship and addiction alike, sensory shutdown standing for a mind buried alive.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped and degraded — buried in filth, beaten like a dog — pleads for salvation while raging against a deity who demands blind faith and punishes those who resist. The repeated image of sewn-shut eyes frames the song as a protest against enforced ignorance and spiritual captivity.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~maybe": {"haiku":"Romantic doubt and emotional disconnection leading to separation, with bittersweet acceptance of an ending relationship.","opus":"A tired confession of a relationship that has simply run out of fuel, the singer naming his solitude and exhaustion and choosing to release his partner rather than witness one final drop of grief. There is even relief as the shared load loosens. Post-Layne Staley Alice in Chains still carry that gloomy Seattle fatalism through the harmonies.","sonnet":"A relationship ending not in explosion but in exhausted indifference — the speaker admits loneliness and fatigue while recognizing they've already drifted apart from the other person. Relief and grief tangle together as the shared weight is finally set down, and what passes for a smile is really acceptance that it's over.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study of the moment when letting go stops registering as loss and quietly turns into relief. The hedging title carries the whole argument: each chorus opens with a shrug of doubt that keeps circling back to the same verdict — a hollowed-out fatigue, a person worn past caring — until the qualifier lands on the bond itself, no longer recognizing the other and strangely settled about it. The giveaway is the smile arriving exactly where mourning belongs; the burden slipping its knots is freedom dressed as surrender, and the final consent comes without a fight. Against that, a Californian heat that refuses to burn off and a single December day swelling far past its hours build a suspended, airless time where nothing mends and every meager start bills you double, so the release reads less as victory than as sheer weariness bargaining its way toward a verdict. For a band steeped in the unresolved, that composure is the most devastating card available: not railing at the ending but agreeing to it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"weariness bargaining its way toward consent as the most devastating card","built":"the hedging title qualifier migrates from the speaker's state onto the bond itself"},
"alice-in-chains~never-fade": {"haiku":"Someone haunts another's mind as an ever-present guide and light; the connection persists even through distance and darkness.","opus":"Wordless mourning and a self-destructive inner urge yield to a guiding presence that lifts the narrator through his darkness and refuses to disappear. Now fronted by Jerry Cantrell and William DuVall, Alice in Chains braid loss and perseverance, weighing the cost of old deceptions against a devotion that insists on being remembered.","sonnet":"A man teetering on the edge of self-destruction holds onto a presence that cuts through the dark and lights his path forward. Despite numbness, loss of friends, and a trail of pain, he insists the bond is real and that he has not forgotten — will not forget.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor's vow spoken toward the dead the band cannot bury — Rainier Fog was cut in Seattle, the home scene that lost both Layne Staley and Mike Starr, and the address here carries that weight of an unpayable debt. The verses stay clamped inside one body: pulse quickening at a memory, a private accusation whispering that he deserves worse, numbness failing as friends drift off and the old lies come due. The chorus reverses the haunting, letting the departed imagine himself erased, and the answer refuses that erasure twice — insisting the gone one persists and that the living has grown into exactly what the lost one doubted he could become. So the promise never to disappear is less consolation than sentence, guilt wearing the mask of devotion, a phrase repeated toward an empty room until repetition is the only proof left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","shame & repression","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"guilt wearing the mask of devotion toward an empty room","built":"the chorus lets the dead imagine erasure so the living can refuse it twice"},
"alice-in-chains~no-excuses": {"haiku":"A person struggles daily with emotional pain and damage, refusing to make excuses while defending a friend through changing circumstances.","opus":"Weary endurance and loyalty between two hurting people, the singer done concealing hard truths, his hands raw from a day of grueling labor. The throughline is unshakable friendship, a promise that whatever cold cruelty lands on him he will stand by his companion and keep caring even if they both change.","sonnet":"About the exhaustion of keeping secrets and wearing masks in a friendship — the narrator strips away pretense and offers unconditional loyalty, promising that even if everything changes, the love stays.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jerry Cantrell wrote this about a friendship worn raw — most often read as his bond with Layne Staley — and it is Alice in Chains at their least veiled — the acoustic Jar of Flies register stripping away the band's usual gothic dread to leave plain fatigue and plainer loyalty. The verses catalogue exhaustion (bruised hands, breaking rocks) as a shared sentence rather than a private one; the accusation that the other finds it funny lands with the reminder that he is drowning in the same water. What makes the song ache is that it refuses despair as an excuse: the chorus keeps returning to a man sitting alone with nothing left to blame, and the final movement answers that isolation with a promise to stay a friend even through change. It is a codependence ballad that knows exactly what it is and loves the other person anyway.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["friendship & loyalty","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"codependence that knows exactly what it is and stays anyway","built":"the accusation lands with the reminder he is drowning in the same water"},
"alice-in-chains~no-excuses-album-version":{"haiku":"Worn exhaustion and resilience without apology despite repeated bruising. The narrator sits alone bearing accumulated damage, offering no excuses for his continued survival.","opus":"Cantrell wrote it as a gesture of loyalty to Staley amid the drug pain, an aching, weary vow of friendship that endures the bad days and the mutual drowning, love offered without excuses even as both bleed.","sonnet":"A friendship under strain accepts its scars without blame — bruised hands from breaking rocks, a daily cold hit, sitting alone with no excuses — but affirms that whatever change comes, the love between these two people endures unconditionally.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~nothin-song": {"haiku":"A recording session stretches late into exhaustion; the catchy 'nothing' song becomes infectious despite the subject being empty and unchanging.","opus":"A studio in-joke stretched into a shrug at impermanence, the narrator timestamping the take, nodding off against the mixing board, questioning why he's up so late while noting how the throwaway tune lodges itself stubbornly in the mind. This Alice in Chains oddity spins creative exhaustion and household trivia into a meditation on nothing lasting, its title admitting as much.","sonnet":"A late-night studio session turned into its own subject — clocking in at 7:38, head hitting the mixing board, the absurdity of recording something called the nothing song that sticks to you like peanut butter anyway. Mundane details (a cat, cake thrown out, cowhide) orbit the central observation that nothing stays the same, which is either comfort or vertigo depending on the hour.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title admits defeat while pretending to shrug: a piece confessedly about nothing, cut by a band burning down to embers in the frayed last stretch of the 1995 self-titled sessions. A take clocked to the minute, a skull rapped against the mixing desk, the gnawing question of why anyone is grinding past midnight — the words turn the recording room itself into the subject, fatigue metabolized into content. What follows curdles into gibberish too calculated to be accidental, thought smeared and gummed into paralysis so thoroughly that the promise of change arrives as gallows humor rather than comfort. The little atrocities glinting through the babble, a cat fed and then killed off or swapped for a newer model, read as an idle mind drifting toward offhand savagery when nothing solid remains to hold, the emptiness itself turning predatory. It is a study of creative and chemical exhaustion where refusing to signify becomes the whole point, a track assembled from the condition of having nothing left to give.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness","violence & murder"],"means":"creative and chemical exhaustion where refusing to signify becomes the whole point","built":"the recording room itself becomes the subject, fatigue metabolized into content"},
"alice-in-chains~nutshell": {"haiku":"Someone feels violated and isolated, having lost both privacy and self-determination, trapped in a cycle of suffering they can only escape through death.","opus":"A stark portrait of total isolation: chasing lies and facing time alone, with no one to cry to and no home. The gutting core is a violated sense of self, the singer feels his private inner life exposed and raped, and concludes that if he can't belong to himself he'd rather be dead.","sonnet":"Profound loneliness and the feeling of having had one's selfhood violated — the narrator fights a solitary battle on a path riddled with lies, and reaches the point where life without autonomy feels not worth living.","src":"haiku","fable":"Layne Staley's plainest confession, and its power is in how little it hides behind. The first verse indicts the world outside — a culture of misprinted lies and unstoppable time — but the song's real subject arrives when the blame turns inward: the self feels invaded, its privacy stripped, its very ownership no longer certain. That is the pivot the whole track turns on. To lose the ability to belong to oneself is set as worse than death, and the wordless moans that close each verse do the work no line could, letting the ache continue past the point where language gives out. On Jar of Flies, this is the addict's isolation stated without metaphor or escape: fighting alone, with nowhere that counts as home.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"self-ownership lost as the condition set worse than death","built":"wordless moans close each verse, letting ache continue past where language gives out"},
"alice-in-chains~over-now": {"haiku":"After something painful ends, the speaker survives and accepts that people must repay emotional debts over time.","opus":"A resigned acceptance of a relationship's end, the narrator able to breathe again yet needing the other person to look him in the eye and say it's truly over. The throughline is that endings are a debt we all eventually pay, a hard-won, uneasy peace where he seems alive somehow and just has to do his time.","sonnet":"The quiet aftermath of something ending — a relationship, an addiction, a phase — with the narrator finally able to breathe, however painfully; it's over, and eventually the debt gets paid.","src":"haiku","fable":"Every stanza announces a finish it refuses to trust, then softens the verdict with a grudging admission that the speaker is still functioning, still upright, though the survival feels borrowed rather than earned. The recurring line about a reckoning owed later reframes the parting as delayed moral accounting, as if the ruin arriving here is simply an installment coming due on something already spent. A demand to hold his gaze and pronounce the split aloud goes unmet by both sides, and the closing turn redirects that same challenge at a mirror, asking whether he could sentence himself. That pronoun shift is the tell: sung by Jerry Cantrell out of a collapsing engagement he and Sean Kinney wrote from, the split being pronounced may be with a partner or with a former self, the cold instruction to serve out a term doubling as sentence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"survival felt borrowed rather than earned, reckoning deferred to later","built":"the demand to pronounce the split aloud redirects at a mirror in the closing pronoun shift"},
"alice-in-chains~phantom-limb": {"haiku":"Betrayed and trapped by misplaced loyalty, the narrator becomes a haunting presence that will torment the betrayer permanently.","opus":"The post-Layne lineup voices a betrayed man left for dead who refuses to disappear, vowing to cling to his betrayer like a limb that's gone but still felt. Alice in Chains' signature bleakness turned into a threat of undying resentment.","sonnet":"Trapped in a situation caused by misplaced loyalty, the narrator suffers cognitive and physical deterioration while watching those who betrayed them circle like vultures — but promises that even in defeat he will haunt his betrayers the way a phantom limb haunts an amputee.","src":"haiku","fable":"Betrayal is staged here as premature burial, the speaker sealed underground and forced to claw upward through the same soil that is choking the breath from him. The killing detail is that the treachery keeps hurting after the traitor is already dead — a split-tongued snake trusted far too early, now a corpse, yet the ache refuses to quit. Ground down to something past any cure, the buried man flips helplessness into menace: unable to climb back into the world, he swears instead to cling to his betrayer as a nagging tingle in a limb long since severed. That is the argument — not a cry for saving but an oath of permanence, malice that fits its victim like a suit of borrowed nerves and outlasts every bone. Suited to the DuVall-fronted band, it broods on what a poisoned loyalty bequeaths, waving off the funeral rites some heavenly clerk has already been told to prepare.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","death & grief","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"malice that outlasts the dead betrayer by fitting like borrowed nerves","built":"helplessness flipped into an oath of permanence, clinging replacing climbing"},
"alice-in-chains~pretty-done": {"haiku":"A once-favored man is exhausted and resigned after years of mistreatment and humiliation. He has paid his debts and no longer plays the game.","opus":"The post-Staley band voicing weary resignation from someone spent and discarded, a once-favored son now used for sport; the exhaustion of a career or bond played out, refusing to keep performing for people who never really had him.","sonnet":"Someone who was once a favored insider has been used up and discarded, owes no one anything, and is finished pretending to be amused by people who only want to watch them struggle — done with the whole arrangement and not going back.","src":"haiku","fable":"Exhaustion disguised as advice to a rival: grip the thing you took even when it scalds your palm, because the speaker already knows how little comfort it holds. The title works as a pun — nearly finished, and finished the way a corpse is dressed for viewing, hollowed out yet still ordered to perform grace. What curdles the grievance is demotion: the once-anointed name reduced to a walk-on extra with two lines in a story he thought was his. So he settles the ledger loudly, declaring he owes nothing and feels nothing, which is exactly how a person swears he has stopped caring while proving he cannot.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"grievance settled loudly as proof the speaker cannot stop caring","built":"the title puns finished and dressed-for-viewing, hollowed yet still ordered to perform grace"},
"alice-in-chains~private-hell":{"haiku":"Someone retreats into isolation and self-amusement, afraid to let others close; they watch relationships end while trapped in their own private cell.","opus":"Late-era Alice in Chains on the comfortable prison of isolation, a man too guarded to let anyone in, amusing himself in a self-made cell because the loneliness has become familiar enough to prefer over risk.","sonnet":"A person who has walled themselves off from intimacy admits they live comfortably in a self-made isolation — promises ring hollow, connection feels impossible, and they've learned to entertain themselves in their own private hell.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the comfort of self-imprisonment: the narrator has grown so accustomed to isolation that his cell has become a place he actively entertains himself in, the pun on excuse/amuse turning solitary confinement into a chosen leisure. The refusal to let anyone all the way in is diagnosed not as inability but as habit — a fear worn smooth into routine. On Black Gives Way to Blue, the band's first record after Layne Staley's death, that voluntary hell reads doubly: the flowers on a cross and the blood that greens the grass fold private withdrawal into an elegy, where surviving means learning to live decoratively among your own losses rather than actually leaving the room.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"isolation worn smooth from fear into chosen leisure","built":"excuse and amuse pun turns solitary confinement into actively entertaining oneself","fnote":"Under the verses runs a second voice in parentheses — I guess so, I'll move on, life is short — the recluse heckling his own confession, deadpan, from inside the cell."},
"alice-in-chains~put-you-down": {"haiku":"Despite lack of genuine need or love, the speaker cannot abandon an addictive and destructive relationship.","opus":"Addiction dressed up as a doomed relationship, or maybe both at once: the narrator insists he does not need this thing yet admits he cannot put it down, watching the cost mount while calling the pull an overview of his own personality. The final swipe at buyers and liars sharpens the venom. Alice in Chains long made substance dependency and self-loathing their core subject, and this trudging, sardonic sludge fits squarely in that vein.","sonnet":"A toxic attachment that the speaker can see clearly and can't escape anyway — full awareness of the cost, zero desire for the person, and yet the compulsion holds. The chorus flips between knowing better and doing it anyway, ending on a taunt aimed either outward or inward, the song catches itself unable to follow through on its own resolution.","src":"haiku","fable":"An addiction song that keeps its object deliberately ambiguous, letting a lover and a substance occupy the same grammatical slot so the title cuts two ways at once: to put someone down is both to belittle them and to set the habit aside, and the singer can do neither. Staley's signature move here is the language of reduction and dependency (the cost, the addictive slowing, the flat clinical phrase about personality) dressed as a breakup, the denial of need repeated so insistently that it confesses the opposite. The closing verse pivots outward into contempt for the commercial and the phony, the buyer whose sincerity is just a jingle, and lands on a startling dare to be shot dead before he's finished singing, which reframes the whole track as a refusal to be silenced or cleaned up on anyone's terms. That the chorus's I don't need you collapses into a stuttering chant of down, down is the read in miniature: the will to release the grip dissolving into the compulsive rhythm of the grip itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance"],"means":"refusal to release collapses into the grip itself","built":"the chorus's down, down chant enacts what the lyric claims to resist"},
"alice-in-chains~queen-of-the-rodeo-live":{"haiku":"A gender-nonconforming or sexually ambiguous person defies judgment and shame while sexualizing and mocking masculine authority.","opus":"A raucous, profane comic monologue in the voice of a hard-drinking drag queen with a rough family history, absent trucker father and an abusive mother. Defiantly owning the slurs thrown at her, she boasts of stealing the listener's girlfriend, a swaggering assertion of queer identity through provocation.","sonnet":"A bawdy, defiant persona piece — the narrator, raised by a single mother after a father left for a man, has become a cross-dresser who sleeps with other men's girlfriends and doesn't care what anyone calls them, claiming the title of rodeo queen with belligerent pride.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~rain-when-i-die": {"haiku":"A man questions a woman's feelings and control over him while expressing inability to hide or stop her from hating him, expecting rain at his death.","opus":"A Dirt-era dirge steeped in the band's signature dread, it circles a wounded relationship as slow castration. The narrator, calling himself an unbreakable riddle, wonders whether a woman came to save or destroy him, whether love could return or has curdled to hate. The recurring premonition of rain at his death colors the whole exchange with fatalism, a man too proud to be helped watching intimacy sour.","sonnet":"A man feels profoundly unknowable and unreachable within a damaged relationship, sensing the other person both trying to hold him and giving up on him simultaneously. He anticipates her permanent hatred as a foregone conclusion, rain arriving like reckoning at the moment of his death.","src":"haiku","fable":"A relationship staged as siege: this is a man narrating his own opacity as if it were strength, defending the walls that are actually killing him. The verses stage a relationship as interrogation and siege — she probes, he calls himself an unbreakable riddle, and every gesture of her care lands as a threat to be repelled; the tell is that he admits he could help her but won't, choosing isolation over the vulnerability rescue would require. Layne Staley sings it from behind that mask, and the recurring line about rain at his death is less prophecy than a self-pitying dare: proof that the world, or she, will finally mourn what he refused to open. The final verse lets the pose crack — the shift to whether she could love him again, and the flat admission that he cannot even explain himself, exposes the riddle as evasion rather than mystery, a man who has confused being unreachable with being invincible.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"opacity performed as strength confesses the opposite","built":"the final verse cracks the riddle pose into flat admission of evasion"},
"alice-in-chains~rainier-fog": {"haiku":"The song traces lingering pain from past love, with transparent, broken imagery of emotional scarring that persists like fog. A ghost-like presence echoes through memory, and the narrator questions whether they still care or if they're just haunted.","opus":"A meditation on what refuses to fade, staying sober, coming clean, and grieving someone gone; the narrator finds himself twisting inside the remnant of a song, sharing a space that never quite fills, climbing back up through a haze of memory and pain. Alice in Chains name it for their hometown mountain, tying loss to place.","sonnet":"Grief and memory surface through the imagery of rain and fog around Rainier, as the narrator holds onto a lost connection that still echoes — a presence that is gone but never fully absent.","src":"haiku","fable":"Named for the volcano that shadows Seattle, this Alice in Chains cut turns a hometown landmark into a headstone for the scene that raised the band, Layne Staley and Mike Starr among the dead it keeps circling. Its central move is a body dissolving into glass: the narrator watches his own skin, his heart, finally a whole streetscape go translucent, until mourning has scraped him down to something present but no longer solid enough to touch. The recurring image of thrashing inside a melody that lifts off the peak's mist stakes the record's claim that the lost persist only as sound, so performing becomes both the visitation and the sole shelter left. Deepest in, a single vowel keeps shifting across an otherwise fixed line so that a remembered face slides from grief to glory to a slow death by water, letting a passage dressed as consolation quietly stage the very loss it pretends to have outlived.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"the lost persist only as sound, never as solid presence","built":"a single shifting vowel slides a remembered face from grief to slow drowning"},
"alice-in-chains~real-thing": {"haiku":"A person cycles through drug use, grows up, enters rehab but cannot break free. He descends again on a white line straight to nowhere, addicted to the blade.","opus":"A blackly comic addict's life story, from childhood mischief to making a needle his plaything and riding a drug straight to nowhere. Its thread is the futility of getting help, treatment and doctors accomplish nothing, and he walks out vowing to quit while cadging cash for the next fix, the disease outrunning every attempt to rescue him.","sonnet":"A life consumed by drug addiction from boyhood through rehab and back to using — the white line goes down the steps straight to nowhere, and the doctors, the friends, the warnings never once stick because the pull of the substance always wins.","src":"haiku","fable":"Staley narrates his own addiction as a flip mock-biography — a syringe taken up the way a kid claims a shiny new plaything, the ones who ask questions brushed off, a rehab that leaves him thanking the doctors and begging a loan on his way out the door — and the joke is the point: every verse caps its confession with a punchline so the horror can pass for swagger. The title is the barb the whole song hangs on, addiction sold as the only authentic experience left, and the tally under the hill counts fixes the way a braggart counts conquests. But the mask slips at the bridge, where the wisecracks stop and the register goes flat and hypnotic — a slow walk downward along a thread of powder that leads only into dead air — the addict's own clear-eyed knowledge of where this ends, spoken plainly once the routine of jokes runs out. Even the closing leer about following him down keeps the grin fixed over the grave, which is exactly what the song is doing throughout.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The spoken outro is an Eddie Murphy gag — Sexual Chocolate is the fictional soul band from Coming to America (1988); the grin stays on over the grave.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"confession delivered as brag until the joke runs out","built":"bridge drops the wisecracks into flat hypnotic register exposing the reckoning"},
"alice-in-chains~red-giant": {"haiku":"A performer masks darkness with lies and spectacle, manipulating audiences, then watches reality crash when the facade finally cracks.","opus":"A charismatic manipulator revels in playing the clown-showman, using misdirection and comforting lies to win crowds over, gleefully swelling like a dying star about to explode; the deeper read is a portrait of demagoguery, where a lie stops mattering as truth once you've won the audience, and the mask is only removed to reveal something bolder underneath.","sonnet":"A charismatic manipulator — a political or religious demagogue figure — performs for the crowd, using misdirection and comfortable lies to accumulate power, claiming his expanding influence is inevitable and that he'll burn everything down laughing when the show finally ends.","src":"haiku","fable":"Red Giant is a demagogue's monologue voiced by a performer who has learned that a crowd wants to be flattered into belief and will adore whoever flatters it with enough conviction. Clown and stage-magician fuse into one figure — the entertainer as con artist, keeping the room laughing with cheap tricks and a pointed finger while it is quietly being fleeced. The astronomical boast turns on its maker: a star that has burned through its core hydrogen swells to monstrous size precisely because it is dying, so the vow to expand and torch everything diagnoses a spectacle already collapsing inward. The final verse strips the paint away and finds the same face underneath, only bolder — the chilling confession that no separate self ever stood behind the act, that the huckster fully means what he sells. Anchoring it all is the sung creed that deception rebrands itself as truth the moment it wins the room, an amen the children chant back on cue, delivered deadpan enough that mockery and confession blur into one.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","faith & the occult","politics & society"],"means":"the demagogue's boast of expansion diagnoses his own collapse","built":"the astronomical title turns the vow to devour into a dying-star self-portrait"},
"alice-in-chains~right-turn": {"haiku":"Two people trapped in a mutually damaging relationship, both equally low and unable to leave or forget, tricked by their own willingness to self-destruct.","opus":"This short early-catalog Alice in Chains piece, notable for guest vocals, stacks he, she, and we perspectives into one bleak reckoning. Each voice admits sinking about as far down as possible, unable to depart or forget, denying anything is right while conceding the clarity they felt was only intoxication, a self-deception that ends in a fall into flame.","sonnet":"Three angles on the same trap — first one person stuck at rock bottom, then another, then both together, all unable to leave and unable to forget. The admission underneath is that someone was deceived while high, gambled, and fell face-first into the fire. The wrongness is shared now; neither person is right, and neither can walk away from what they built together.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grunge supergroup cameo, Chris Cornell trading vocals with Staley on the acoustic Sap EP, and the song tracks addiction and codependency through a widening pronoun as its whole argument. It begins locked in the first person, scrabbling to breach a numbness the singer already loathes, then shifts to she before settling on we, so the same trapped verdict of not being right migrates from one addict to a couple who can neither part ways nor forget. The pivot arrives in the bridge, which drops the wounded self-pity for a colder second-person accusation: the comforting story that someone else deceived you collapses once you admit you were only ever chasing the high yourself, and the gamble ends face-down in the fire. The title is the joke and the sentence at once, the one correction that never gets made.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"two addicts share a verdict neither can escape alone","built":"the pronoun widens from I to she to we as the trap closes on both"},
"alice-in-chains~rooster":{"haiku":"A Vietnam veteran survives combat and its aftermath—physical wounds, psychological scars, and separation from family. Despite death closing in, he refuses to break under the pressure of war.","opus":"Jerry Cantrell's tribute to his father, a Vietnam veteran nicknamed Rooster, told from the soldier's own defiant refusal to die under fire. Beneath the swagger sits the trauma of a homeland that spat on the men it sent to war.","sonnet":"A soldier in Vietnam navigates constant mortal danger, alienation at home, and the desperate hope of survival, drawing strength from the belief that he simply will not die. The character, nicknamed Rooster, receives a photo of his son from his wife while enduring bullets and disease in the field.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rooster is Jerry Cantrell scoring his father's Vietnam and handing the vocal to Layne Staley, and that handoff is the whole engine: the third-person nickname, the taunting drawl of the chorus, are how a son voices a survival he never witnessed and can only turn into legend. The verses reverse that gesture, sinking into the sweat-blind, surrounded first person of a man swallowing malaria tablets and tracking gunfire he cannot locate, held to home only by a woman's name and a mailed snapshot of a child he may never see again. What lifts this past a standard war elegy is the quarrel between those two registers, the chorus swearing the Rooster is unkillable with a swaggering grin while the verse beneath it is one sustained appeal for deliverance, spat on at home and dying by inches abroad. That vow of not-dying, hammered until it grooves flat, reads less as triumph than as the only supplication a man has when he cannot confess terror aloud: the persona claims invincibility, the person underneath is begging heaven. Cantrell's grunge does its finest work here, converting a parent's endurance into a haunting the survivor drags back stateside, the mere fact of outliving the war becoming the injury itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"Jerry Cantrell Sr. — Rooster since boyhood — had never talked about Vietnam, then sat for an hour-long filmed interview for Mark Pellington’s 1993 video and appears in it; the song is credited with finally reconciling father and son.","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","family & blood","artifice & performance"],"means":"survival claimed as boast while the man beneath begs","built":"son's legend-voice and father's first-person terror alternate, never merge"},
"alice-in-chains~rotten-apple": {"haiku":"Youth consumes forbidden knowledge and loses innocence; the speaker has written their own fate and crawls back toward the beginning.","opus":"A hypnotic reckoning with lost innocence and unavoidable corruption, chanting the erosion of purity, self-assurance, and humility. Biblical temptation imagery frames a fall from grace as both original sin and personal wreckage, the narrator admitting he scripted his own doom and postponing repentance indefinitely.","sonnet":"A meditation on the loss of innocence framed through the biblical image of eating the forbidden apple — the narrator ate it young and has been crawling back to the start ever since. The chanted litany of things falling (innocence, confidence, sustenance, arrogance) maps the damage of early corruption.","src":"haiku","fable":"Layne Staley builds the song as a litany of losses, each abstraction — innocence, confidence, sustenance — named and then echoed back a beat later, as if the second voice is only confirming a verdict already handed down. That call-and-response structure is the point: the singer keeps announcing his own fall, then hearing it repeated, trapped in a loop he narrates but cannot break. The chorus makes the frame biblical without preaching it, a taste of forbidden knowledge taken too young leaving him endlessly crawling back to a beginning he can no longer reach — repentance and sorrow are both promised for later, suspended, never paid. In the shadow of the addiction that shaped Alice in Chains' early-90s writing, the rotten apple reads as the thing consumed that hollows out everything the litany lists, and the hypnotic wandering vamp underneath enacts that same suspension: motion without arrival.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","faith & the occult"],"means":"repentance promised and suspended, never paid","built":"a call-and-response structure locks the singer in a loop he narrates but cannot break"},
"alice-in-chains~rotten-apple-album-version":{"haiku":"Youth consumed by appetite and corruption where paradise rots before enjoyment ends. The narrator wrote his own fall and crawls backward toward lost innocence.","opus":"A hypnotic reckoning with lost innocence and inherited rot, listing how self-assurance, nourishment, and decency get shattered or stolen. Tasting temptation too early leaves the singer scrabbling back toward the beginning, forgiveness forever postponed to some future day that never arrives.","sonnet":"A litany of lost innocence catalogued in pairs — innocence over, ignorance spoken, confidence broken, sustenance stolen, arrogance potent — climaxing in the image of eating the apple young and crawling back to start, an expulsion from eden whose consequences the speaker is still serving.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~scalpel": {"haiku":"Self-deception and hidden pain are central themes; the lyrics explore how people lie by adopting false personas while harming themselves.","opus":"Fleeing your hometown to save yourself doesn't work because hiding inside another person is a lie that cuts like a scalpel; the throughline is hard-won self-knowledge, knowing the floor is false and choosing to pick yourself back up and let things resolve in their own time.","sonnet":"About the corrosive habit of losing yourself inside other people to escape your own pain — the narrator recognises this as self-harm dressed up as love, and resolves to stop fleeing and accept hard truths instead.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song argues that flight from a place only heals if the fugitive audits himself rather than laundering the wound through other people. Having bolted a hometown to stay alive and quiet a nagging uncertainty, the narrator returns with two humbling measurements: how flimsy the ground beneath him is, how unreachably far the thing he chases sits above. Its central figure reframes borrowing another identity as concealment that is really surgery turned inward, a clean gleaming edge parting the self under the alibi of closeness. Against that evasion he plants a recovered creed, honesty as an uninvited but faithful ally, the stubbornness to be knocked flat and rise, then a yielding to time that reads as a survivor's calm. Emerging from the William DuVall lineup still shadowed by Layne Staley's addiction and denial, it plays as a sobriety-adjacent parable prizing candor over escape.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"borrowed identity as surgery turned inward rather than healing","built":"honesty arrives as an uninvited ally set against the alibi of closeness"},
"alice-in-chains~sea-of-sorrow": {"haiku":"An accusation of harm and a vow of survival. Someone has deeply hurt the speaker, and the speaker chooses to move forward while the other wallows in regret and sorrow.","opus":"A bitter kiss-off to someone who wounded him, the narrator resolving to survive and move on while they drown in their own misery. Layne Staley's early venom, self-destruction and defiance already tangled together.","sonnet":"The narrator has been wounded by someone and refuses to follow them into their ongoing self-destruction — he will survive and move forward while the other person wallows in sorrow, aiming his defiance back at them like a skull.","src":"haiku","fable":"A revenge song built as a survival math: the one who was shot walks away, and the one who fired the shot is the one left to drown. The lyric works two image-families against each other — the speaker's interior fortress of high walls and a castle crumbling under time on one side, a defiant death's-head he now turns back on his attacker on the other — so that the crumbling isn't defeat but the hardening of grief into contempt. Everything narrows to the chorus's cold arithmetic of I-live-you-wallow, and the outro's near-liturgical repetition is the point: he can't stop reciting his own survival, because saying he's moved on is how he keeps proving it. In the Facelift context this is early Cantrell writing a wound as a taunt, Staley's sneer carrying the venom the words only sketch.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","death & grief","violence & murder"],"means":"grief hardened into contempt becomes arithmetic of survival","built":"outro repetition enacts the compulsion to keep proving the speaker survived"},
"alice-in-chains~shame-in-you": {"haiku":"Shared culpability in relationship pain; both partners carry shame and must find inner peace rather than blame each other for past hurt.","opus":"A narrator caught between waking and aching turns a confession into an accusation, claiming his own sins while insisting the other person find a place for their separate shame. Fitting Alice in Chains' murk of guilt and dependency, he circles inner peace as something wrested from blame, the cyclical phrasing dramatizing a mind unable to stop relitigating who hurt whom.","sonnet":"A man acknowledges his own failures while refusing to carry another's blame. He names his sins but redirects the weight of shame back to its rightful owner, insisting on inner peace and a clean reckoning rather than endless mutual guilt.","src":"haiku","fable":"An argument for returning guilt to whoever earned it, staged inside the very waking-into-ache cadence that Layne Staley made his signature. Each ordinary motion here collapses toward hurting and dying, yet that exhaustion sharpens into refusal rather than the usual surrender. The speaker concedes his own crime and shoulders his sins, then declines to carry a disgrace planted in him by another, handing it back for them to store. What opens like the band's trademark dirge of self-loathing tips into an ethics of divided responsibility: sort out your own unreal ache, since no hand outside yours pressed that wound. The terse closing volley of paired imperatives grinds even that hard-won clarity into pure reflex, as though insight can only endure as blunt orders barked at the self.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"guilt divided and returned to whoever planted it","built":"the closing volley of paired imperatives grinds hard-won clarity into pure reflex"},
"alice-in-chains~sickman": {"haiku":"Someone experiences profound mental illness that makes them feel contaminated and trapped, unable to steer their thoughts away from decay and despair.","opus":"One of Dirt's bleakest addiction portraits, voicing a body and mind failing while the narrator can sense the crash coming but cannot stop it. Layne Staley responds to his own decay with a mix of mockery and resignation, later warping a familiar psalm into something violent. Nihilism and gallows humor entwine in classic early-nineties Seattle gloom.","sonnet":"A mind turns against itself — thoughts become the worst adversary, driving a man through cycles of self-loathing, physical decay, and a resignation to dying within his own contaminated inner world. The only path he can imagine is surrendering to the rot rather than achieving any purity.","src":"haiku","fable":"Layne Staley builds a theology out of addiction, casting the addict not as a man with an illness but as illness itself, a leper hollowed from within where even the walls meant to shelter him have gone rancid. The governing horror is a body that registers every turn of the wheel yet cannot correct its own course, so his own mind curdles into the predator he most dreads. When the borrowed cadence of the psalm arrives, defiled into imagery of violation and ruin, it strips away any promise of a shepherd waiting past the shadow; the only exit on offer is the grave. Pride sanded to nothing and sorrow measured across ten flat miles, the closing stance is not repentance but a decision to stop resisting, the chanted title less a diagnosis than the sound of a self dissolving into the word.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","madness & the mind","death & grief"],"means":"the addict becomes the illness, not the ill man","built":"a psalm cadence defiled into violation strips away any shepherd waiting past the shadow"},
"alice-in-chains~sludge-factory": {"haiku":"A witness to addiction, manipulation, and moral corruption within a closed system of vultures and liars, describing the spiritual destruction wrought by guilt weaponized against the vulnerable.","opus":"Staley's venomous address to someone watching him self-destruct, entwining industry pressure, drug paranoia and a loved one's own death wish; the real weapon named at the end is guilt, wielded by everyone circling the wreckage.","sonnet":"A fractured, associative confrontation with manipulation and guilt used as weapons — discolored skin marking someone's crisis, a body that has promised its own death by age 25, surrounded by corporate vultures and false friends who deploy shame to maintain control.","src":"haiku","fable":"Layne Staley's bitter address to the Columbia executives who circled the 1995 self-titled sessions, when his heroin habit made him both prized asset and a man nobody could reach. The verses run on a poisoned intimacy of hollow congratulations, flattery about genius, and a patience that indulges the artist because his decay still turns a profit. Drug and business read as one apparatus that keeps him working by keeping him ruined, watchfulness and dependence fusing until a delivery deadline sounds like a private premonition of death. The person he cherishes shrinks to a tally of days and a vow never to reach twenty-five, the two requests collapsing into a single demand. Its spoken coda plays as a mock deposition, a courtroom of scavengers and cheats whose real product is a man hollowed out, the instrument turned against him never contracts but shame.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","surveillance & control","money & the street"],"means":"the industry's patience indulges the artist because decay still turns a profit","built":"drug and business fuse until a delivery deadline sounds like a private premonition of death"},
"alice-in-chains~so-close": {"haiku":"A speaker expresses frustration and near-violent tension with unnamed others, feeling unsatisfied and on edge.","opus":"This ragged Alice in Chains piece captures aimless frustration and simmering menace, the narrator bored, thrilled by nothing, nearly snapping at those who now think highly of him. The refrain of being so close hangs unresolved. A loose, sardonic cut, it channels the band's characteristic weariness and dark humour into something deliberately restless and unfinished-sounding.","sonnet":"A song of listless dissatisfaction — the same deadening routine returning, nothing providing any real stimulation. Beneath the numbness is suppressed violence and a fractured sense of status, the narrator hovering at some unnamed threshold without crossing it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tedium curdles into menace here: the ritual of showing up to the same deadened routine, where no stimulus lands and boredom hardens into a flat refusal to feel anything at all. The genius of the turn is how a near-breaking of the self folds instantly into a near-breaking of the people around him, collapsing the wall between self-harm and lashing out into a single frayed nerve about to give. That the final query repeats without ever seeking an answer tells the whole story — this is a person past the point of wanting a reason, only registering how depleted the asking itself has become.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance"],"means":"self-harm and lashing out collapse into a single frayed nerve","built":"the final query repeats without seeking an answer, registering depletion rather than desire"},
"alice-in-chains~so-far-under": {"haiku":"Survival at rock bottom where the speaker has lost everything and drowns in accumulated pain and despair.","opus":"A descent song about surviving on scraps and doubting anyone would notice if he vanished, the heart already destroyed and the words swallowed into a void. The refrain that no one escapes time alive turns private despair into a flat cosmic law. Alice in Chains, with William DuVall sharing vocals here, keep faith with the band's bottomless gloom, ripping the narrator asunder somewhere far under hell with grinding, downtuned menace.","sonnet":"Absolute emotional bottom — surviving on scraps, words vanishing into a void, a heart destroyed. The speaker knows time is the inescapable trap and that no one walks away alive, yet the feeling isn't universal fate so much as personal devastation: a collapse so complete that even disappearing seems like it might go unnoticed.","src":"haiku","fable":"A descent that keeps announcing it has hit bottom and then discovers another basement beneath it, DuVall voicing a man who lives on emotional leftovers, cannot master affection, and inhabits masonry so cold his disappearance would register with nobody. The chorus converts falling into a permanent address rather than a single accident, and its bleakest maneuver is grammatical: he does not merely sink but drags a companion down, the sufferer refusing to founder alone. Mortality itself becomes the snare, since the clock releases no passenger uninjured, which promotes one private ruin into a verdict handed to every living thing. The bridge routes the ache to a childhood self, grown vocabulary shouting backward at the kid, the present breakdown revealed as an ancient injury recurring with compound interest.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"falling converted into a permanent address rather than a single accident","built":"the bridge routes present breakdown to a childhood self, the injury recurring with compound interest"},
"alice-in-chains~social-parasite": {"haiku":"A defiant response to judgment from lovers or society, rejecting criticism of their lifestyle and values.","opus":"A snarling refusal to be judged, the narrator spitting back at someone who mocks his looks and his living conditions, refusing the role of a beaten dog or a labelled parasite. Underneath the aggression sits a real wound about being cast as worthless by a supposed lover. Alice in Chains rarely play this fast and punkish, so the track reads as a lean, furious outlier from a band better known for slow dread.","sonnet":"Defiant rejection of someone who judges the speaker's appearance, living situation, and worth while claiming to love them. The song refuses the label and refuses the hypocrisy: if love came with that name-calling, it wasn't love. The stance is pure refusal — get out, fuck off, and stop deciding what's wrong or right.","src":"haiku","fable":"A defense mounted by someone already convicted, the song works by turning the accuser's own vocabulary back on them. The speaker's rage is pure snarl in the verses — refusing to be the beaten stray, telling the disapproving other to clear out — but the chorus quietly shifts register from fury to hurt, staking everything on a conditional: if you loved me, you wouldn't be the one assigning me this label in the first place. The real argument is about authority, not addiction. Being cast in the role matters more than the role itself, and the parasite is only a parasite because someone with power over the relationship decided to name him one and to arbitrate right and wrong on his behalf. An early demo cut while Alice in Chains was still gigging as Diamond Lie, it sneers at the presumption of any judge who claims both love and the right to sentence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","love & desire"],"means":"the label matters more than the act it pretends to name","built":"the chorus shifts from fury to a conditional that makes love the only authority worth contesting"},
"alice-in-chains~stone": {"haiku":"A persona compares itself to weathered stone—distant, rough, eroded by time. The song refuses intimacy, warning that the speaker is immovable and will not be shaped by another person's desires.","opus":"The speaker likens himself to a cold, weathered stone, refusing to be understood, used, or possessed by someone who wants to leave their mark on him.","sonnet":"The narrator presents himself as emotionally impenetrable — a cold, weathered stone that resists being claimed or carved into by another person, warning that time alone will deliver hard truths rather than any closeness they might try to force.","src":"haiku","fable":"A defense of impenetrability that dares you to keep chipping. The speaker refuses the roles pressed onto him — guide, confidant, the load-bearing stone someone else builds their grandeur on — and reclaims coldness and roughness as facts of his weathered surface rather than defects to be fixed. William DuVall-era Alice in Chains writes plenty about erosion and legacy, and the closing turn earns its patience: being unreadable is not the same as being inert, since even ice-locked rock keeps grinding forward on its own geological clock. The threat is quiet and total — carve all you want, the reckoning you have coming arrives on a schedule no chisel can hurry.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"coldness reclaimed as weathered fact rather than defect","built":"the closing threat is quiet and geological, arriving on a schedule no chisel can hurry"},
"alice-in-chains~suffragette-city": {"haiku":"Sexual chaos and hedonism where the speaker mocks relationships and society while pursuing pleasure without consequence.","opus":"A frantic, horny brush-off to a friend named Henry, the narrator too rattled by a woman who put his spine out of place to lend anyone a moment, no room here for you. The famous wham bam exclamation caps the rush. This is a David Bowie cover, performed by Alice in Chains, the Seattle grunge band romping through Bowie's proto-glam sprint with obvious relish, borrowing its cocksure momentum.","sonnet":"A man too preoccupied with a consuming woman to deal with his friend's intrusions — the city she represents is his private territory, capacity for one only, and the friend is simply not welcome here. Bowie's original energy carries through: sexual bravado, mild exasperation, the wham-bam conclusion that leaves no room for lingering.","src":"haiku","fable":"Bowie's Ziggy-era warhorse, taken up by Alice in Chains as a cover. A young man tries to shove a friend out the door because a girl has arrived and the flat sleeps one — the whole song is that domestic scramble blown up to the size of a rock-and-roll rush. Bowie stacks a boy's grievances (ruined schooldays, a job gone down the drain) against the erotic jolt of a woman who leaves him buckling at the knees, so the chorus is less a location than a state of arousal he can't share and won't apologize for: the friend is simply too broke to come along for the ride. The Clockwork Orange slang (droogie) tags this as glam's borrowed menace, youth culture playing at delinquency, and the leering payoff cracks the pose open — the strut is real desire wearing a cartoon's grin, exhilaration that knows it's also faintly ridiculous.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","party & hedonism","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"erotic jolt worn as cartoon delinquency that knows it is faintly ridiculous","built":"Clockwork Orange slang tags the strut as borrowed menace cracking open under real desire"},
"alice-in-chains~sunshine": {"haiku":"A contagious, morally compromised person questions their own toxicity while describing their negative spiritual effect on partners.","opus":"From Alice in Chains' debut Facelift, this cut buries menace beneath a woozy sunlit refrain, Layne Staley asking whether he is infectious, a mirror dissolving, anyone's worth, while an intruder who reached for the Mother figure simply disappears. His resigned refusal to care and cryptic maternal imagery show the band's early gift for making dread sound seductive.","sonnet":"A queasy self-examination from someone who wonders if their desire is contagious, if their love defiles, if there is any wholeness left in them to offer. A figure called the Mother appears and consumes whoever touches her. The song's endpoint is a kind of resignation — sweet labor, don't care anymore — that reads less like peace than like someone too tired to keep asking whether they deserve any.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song of contamination that keeps cross-examining its own worth and never wins the case — the speaker asks whether he is too contagious, whether affection turns to defilement in his hands, whether any intact soul survives the sickness he describes. The recurring parable of a stranger who reaches for the maternal figure and instantly vanishes reads as a curse he suspects he carries: nearness annihilates whoever accepts it, so his want is drawn as a smoldering funeral fire rather than heat that comforts. The title brightness answers none of it and lands instead as surrender, the flat refusal to keep feeling the shame of being poison that Staley's earliest writing circles again and again. His closing appeal to be gathered up by that same devouring maternal force turns the whole confession into a longing for oblivion disguised as gentleness, wanting deliverance from the very embrace he believes his nearness destroys.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","alienation & emptiness","suicide"],"means":"want drawn as smoldering funeral fire, closeness framed as annihilation","built":"the title's brightness lands as surrender, not warmth, reversing the lyric's contamination logic"},
"alice-in-chains~swing-on-this": {"haiku":"A person resists family pressure to conform, insisting on independence and rejecting others' attempts to control their choices.","opus":"A hazy sketch of a person begging to be left alone, insisting he is fine and just skinny and asleep, resisting family voices telling him to come home until a voice reminds him he has a choice. Denial and drift give way to a loose, almost jokey coda. Alice in Chains close a record with this offhand, bluesy track, the Seattle band letting their heaviness slacken into something wry and unguarded.","sonnet":"A drift through altered consciousness with everyone calling you home — family, friends, some interior voice, all insisting you come back while the speaker floats in a bright, sideways place they can't quite steer away from. Eventually they go home anyway, not because they choose to but because it's simply what comes next.","src":"haiku","fable":"An intervention narrated from behind the eyes of its target, the song lives in the gap between what everyone begs of him and the shapeless denials he offers back. The whole family repeats one summons, and he parries with reassurances that collapse mid-breath — fine, then asleep, then unhurt, then somehow alert — a man too impaired to hold a single account of himself insisting nothing is wrong. A voice tells him he has a choice, and the answer is a slap to his own face, a jolt that changes nothing except to scatter the people around him before he sinks back into the same skin. Yielding at last, doing what the local custom demands, reads as surrender without belief, the posture of recovery worn so no one will drag him out of the glaring numbness he can float inside but never point anywhere.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"'I can shift, cannot steer' picks up Sickman's 'feel the wheel, can't steer' — the lost-steering image carried from Dirt's fever into Jar of Flies' intervention scene, the driver unchanged.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","family & blood","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"recovery's posture worn so no one drags him out of the numbness","built":"his denials collapse mid-breath, a man too impaired to hold a single account of himself"},
"alice-in-chains~take-her-out":{"haiku":"The speaker feels worn and taken for granted in a relationship; repeatedly, he wants to 'take her out'—the tension between devotion and exhaustion drives the song.","opus":"An oblique, unsettling Alice in Chains piece often read as heroin addiction personified as a shared woman who is never just yours; the compulsion to take her out again outlives the user, promising to continue after he's gone.","sonnet":"The song personifies an addictive or destructive force — likely drugs — as a faceless, shared lover the speaker can't stop returning to, something that has outlasted them and will continue after they're gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title threatens harm, yet what unfolds is stranger: a compulsion wearing the voice of a devoted partner, loyal and possessive and never once satisfied. This narrator has no face and no body, an appetite that swells even as it tightens its hold, so that feeding and suffocating become a single motion. Because the beloved belongs to countless hands at once, she reads less like a woman than a substance traded around, and the closing swagger gives the game away, since this voice pledges to outlast the very man who craves it. Under all the loyalty sits the drug narrating itself, a leak of dread that suits latter-day Alice in Chains perfectly, a group whose legend was built atop the ruins of exactly that deal.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"the substance narrating its own possession as devotion","built":"the closing pledge to outlast the craving man outs the speaker as the drug, not the lover"},
"alice-in-chains~the-devil-put-dinosaurs-here": {"haiku":"A critique of religious manipulation where false prophets claim divine wisdom and exploit followers' fears; the devil planted dinosaurs as a lie to trap believers in superstition.","opus":"A scathing satire of religious fundamentalism and creationism, voiced through a smug preacher-figure selling peace of mind and salvation to a chosen few; the barbed refrain (the devil planted dinosaur fossils to test faith, Jesus hates queers) skewers bigotry and willful ignorance dressed as faith, branding it all lies.","sonnet":"A scathing critique of organized religion and its gatekeepers — portraying preachers as con men selling peace of mind to the credulous, the song targets faith-based exclusion and scientific denial, naming the preacher a liar who exploits fear rather than offering genuine belief.","src":"haiku","fable":"Taking its cue from the creationist gag that buried fossils are Satan's decoy, the song hands the microphone to the salesman himself, a preacher-charlatan who swears he can plug the emptiness inside you and quotes a price for the calm he peddles. Jerry Cantrell writes the verses as a purring first-person pitch, the con man bragging that he alone holds wisdom, that his cloud-borne heaven turns most away, that even his God is an invented playmate propping up the routine. What the boasting slowly exposes is the machinery beneath belief: not conviction but dread, the terror that keeps the congregation buying, and the speaker's own shrugging admission that he is no purer than the marks he fleeces. That reveal is why the closing word, repeated until it stops meaning anything, lands not as noise but as the sermon convicting itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the con man's sermon slowly convicts itself as it proceeds","built":"the closing word repeated until it stops meaning anything enacts the machinery beneath belief"},
"alice-in-chains~the-one-you-know": {"haiku":"Internal transformation erases the self someone once was; an impostor carries hidden change beneath familiar skin, questioning whether absence or metamorphosis matters as they shed their former identity.","opus":"A man estranged from his own identity, looking unchanged on the outside while something hidden hollows him out. For a band built on addiction and loss, it reads as the quiet horror of no longer being who those who love you remember.","sonnet":"About the slow interior estrangement that comes with hidden trauma or addiction — the person others see on the outside is a shell, an impostor who has drained dry inside.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about wearing your own recognizable face while something underneath has quietly curdled into a stranger — the outside intact, an imposter carrying a hidden weight, marching on past the point where the person it belonged to dissolved. The refrain keeps asking whether presence or absence even registers, which names the real dread: not dying but persisting as a shell, continuity emptied of the self that once filled it. For Alice in Chains that doubling cannot land neutrally — a group fronted by the man who stepped into a lost singer's place, insisting he is not the man you remember, so the imposter reads as survivor's guilt and as the band's uneasy recognition of trading forward under an inherited name. Its liquid imagery of something dark still seeping from a source long since drained turns the addiction-and-loss vocabulary they have always worked into a broader grief: what keeps moving issues from a reservoir already spent.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"persisting as a shell, continuity emptied of the self that filled it","built":"DuVall's imposter-in-the-known-face doubles as the band's survival under an inherited name"},
"alice-in-chains~them-bones":{"haiku":"An existential meditation on mortality: the speaker feels isolated and resigned to becoming nothing but bones and dust, accepting death as the only certainty.","opus":"A grim reckoning with mortality, the narrator sensing that he is born toward the grave and will inevitably end up as nothing but a pile of bones, haunted by isolation and dread.","sonnet":"The speaker meditates on mortality as an inescapable condition, suggesting that humans are born already heading toward death and that life is just the time before returning to dust. Loneliness and the certainty of ending as a pile of bones runs through every verse.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is a syllogism about mortality that needs almost nothing to land. Its opening move insists the skeleton simply is the self, collapsing the usual gap between a living body and the remains it becomes, so dying stops being a future event and turns present tense. Across three curt verses the lens tightens from that flat premise toward the specific first person: grit settling over a single lifetime, then a fossil already scooped hollow, then a reckoning called in beneath a bruised horizon where the speaker names himself already a corpse. Meanwhile the chorus never grows at all, only repeating the same isolation and the same heap of remains. That flat refusal is the point. Against Cantrell's grinding detuned riff, Staley isolates the one word meant to survive him, and by the closing round the sentence snaps off before it can arrive at bones, the thought rotting live toward the hush it keeps describing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"mortality collapsed into present tense by equating the living body with its remains","built":"the sentence snaps off before it reaches its noun, the syntax rotting to match the subject"},
"alice-in-chains~voices": {"haiku":"A fragmented mind contains multiple voices and identities, questioning reality and self while climbing toward collapse repeatedly.","opus":"A stark portrait of dissociation and mental fragmentation, the narrator unsure whether he is one self or many, reaching out to ask if everyone hears the same voices. The band's signature dread turned inward on a splintering identity.","sonnet":"A fractured sense of self — the narrator doesn't know if they're one person or many, real or dreaming, and asks whether everyone else hears voices in their head too, or whether the dissolution is theirs alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the erosion of a stable self, where the mental chatter that torments the speaker becomes, paradoxically, the one thing he shares with everyone else — hence the desperate refrain asking whether your voices say what his say, a bid to make private breakdown into common ground. The verses track a man who no longer recognizes his own hardening, who wants more than he needs and can no longer tell what is real; the past arrives as a roll call of the uniforms he has worn, identities put on and shed until nothing certain remains. Alice in Chains built their sound on interlocking harmonized vocals — two voices braided into one — so making the lyric literally about competing voices in the head lets the band's signature texture double as the subject, the harmony itself sounding like a mind talking to itself. The resolution is a retreat rather than an answer: told that dreaming is free, he chooses sleep, preferring the manageable unreality of dreams to a waking life that keeps promising a summit only to drop him again.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"private breakdown bid into common ground through a shared question","built":"the band's braided harmonics enact the lyric's subject, the texture sounding like a mind in debate"},
"alice-in-chains~we-die-young":{"haiku":"Warns of street violence and quick death for those in dangerous situations, with victims losing their way. The song treats premature death as an inevitable consequence of certain environments.","opus":"Layne Staley's grim snapshot of young lives ground down by street violence and addiction, where a kid is already old before his time. The Seattle band's early warning shot about a lost generation running fast toward an early grave.","sonnet":"A bleak portrait of street violence and drug culture — young people caught in a cycle of alley trips, bullets, and hits are dying fast, with the image of blood floating in a muddy sewer and the refrain \"we die young, faster we run\" distilling a life with no escape.","src":"haiku","fable":"The opener to Alice in Chains' Facelift takes a dealer's-eye view of a neighborhood where youth itself is a terminal diagnosis, and it never once looks away. Jerry Cantrell wrote it after watching kids move product on Seattle corners, and the song keeps that clinical remove: a needle raised, a corpse rolled into the muddy sewer, a sibling put in the ground between verses as if burial were just the next chore on the round. The menace stalking it stays deliberately faceless — a scariness scrawled on the wall, already on its way, closing in — because the machinery is impersonal; nobody in particular needs to hate you for the street to grind you down. What poisons it is the hook, where sprinting harder becomes not escape but throttle-open descent toward the grave, the drug's own logic bolted to a chorus. It is protest that refuses the comfort of outrage, accusing by plain description and letting the grim arithmetic — the harder you push, the sooner you are gone — deliver the indictment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Cantrell's account, from the Music Bank liner notes: riding the bus to rehearsal he kept seeing nine- and ten-year-olds with beepers and cell phones dealing on Seattle corners — that sight, he said, simply equaled this song.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","politics & society","death & grief","money & the street"],"means":"protest that accuses through plain arithmetic rather than outrage","built":"the hook makes sprinting harder synonymous with throttle-open descent"},
"alice-in-chains~what-the-hell-have-i": {"haiku":"A person masks truth and hides his inner light, struggling to begin anew while trapped in cycles of fire and deception.","opus":"A strained relationship defined by masks, lies, and growing distance, where the speaker senses tension and dwindling worth and questions what little he has left to give.","sonnet":"The narrator faces a relationship defined by falsehood and distance — a person whose soul feels hollow and whose presence feeds on lies — and confronts the difficulty of starting over while the fire of that dynamic still burns.","src":"haiku","fable":"A relationship read as slow attrition rather than a single rupture: the other person is a mask over dying warmth, and the narrator watches the gap widen while lies quietly feed it. The heat imagery cuts both ways — the fire that surrounds them is at once the last live charge between two people and the thing consuming what remains, which is why starting over feels impossible, not because the love is gone but because it is being spent daily. Layne Staley's soundtrack-and-B-side detour here fits the band's early-90s register of contempt curdling into self-indictment, and the song enacts that turn: verse after verse of accusing the other's counterfeit devotion collapse into the title's final question, where the speaker abruptly redirects the blame inward and asks what he himself steered wrong. The refusal to answer that question is the point — the song ends on the accusation rebounding, the mask pulled off his own face last.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"accusation of counterfeit devotion rebounds onto the accuser last","built":"verse after verse of outward blame collapses into the title question redirecting inward"},
"alice-in-chains~what-the-hell-have-i-remix":{"haiku":"Deception and emptiness corrode intimacy, with the soul discarded and flames surrounding what once held meaning.","opus":"Masks and mutual deceit corroding a bond, faces hidden as the distance grows and lies feed the fire. Layne Staley's closing question turns the accusation inward, a rueful reckoning with the harm he himself set in motion.","sonnet":"About waking up to how a relationship has corrupted and exhausted you — the speaker sees the masks and lies between them, feels the distance growing, and confronts what they've allowed themselves to become by staying.","src":"haiku"},
"alice-in-chains~when-the-sun-rose-again": {"haiku":"Someone trades in false promises for survival gains, caught in cycles of disappointment and compromise. The song describes perpetual moral compromise as existence.","opus":"The song watches people flee, panic when exposed, and endlessly betray each other for gain, while the narrator keeps asking whether someone who foresaw the end was destroyed once daylight returned. Alice in Chains give it a droning, ominous heft, the repeated question turning treachery and reckoning into a slow judgment awaiting dawn.","sonnet":"A portrait of collective moral sellout — people fleeing, hiding, blaming, and trading principles for advantage while a prophesied collapse comes true. The dawn that rises is not redemptive; it burns those who saw it coming just as surely as those who never looked.","src":"haiku","fable":"An acoustic-led Jerry Cantrell piece from the band's first post-Layne-Staley record, and it reads as an accounting with someone who saw the wreck coming and did not survive it. The verses run through the reflexes of a cornered person — bolting, hiding, praying, then the instant deflection that none of it was his fault — while the recurring line about trading in never-befores frames it all as a bad bargain, selling something irreplaceable for a momentary payoff. The song turns on its two-line refrain, which is really a question addressed to the dead: you predicted this would all end, so were you consumed at the exact moment of vindication. Naming a burning-away at sunrise lets survival and daylight double as an indictment, since the light that ends the long night is also what exposes and destroys the one who could not walk into it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"survival at daybreak doubles as exposure and indictment of the lost","built":"the two-line refrain addresses the dead directly, asking whether vindication arrived at the moment of being consumed"},
"alice-in-chains~would": {"haiku":"Regret for mistakes and abandonment of someone important, questioning whether the damage done can ever be repaired.","opus":"A brooding reflection on a mistake and a lost connection, with the narrator pleading to be seen his way. He asks whether he has run too far from home and left someone behind, ending on an uncertain offer of mutual trust.","sonnet":"A narrator caught in a self-destructive pattern reflects on a serious mistake and wonders whether he has strayed too far to return to someone he left behind, ending with an unanswered plea for mutual forgiveness.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cantrell wrote this as an elegy for Andrew Wood, the Mother Love Bone singer lost to heroin, and refracts the grief into a challenge aimed at anyone who would judge a dead friend's fall. The verses submit to a master and speak of teaching a child about love that comes after death — the drug and the overdose recast as devotion and afterlife, a private vocabulary outsiders were never meant to decode. The chorus keeps dropping the speaker back beneath rising water, each relapse the identical descent, the one plea being that a listener stand in his position before condemning. Closing Dirt, an album that lived inside addiction, the song abandons statement for the outro's stacked questions about having drifted past return and stranded someone behind, then lands on a bargain thrown across the gap between the living and the lost: if he took that step, would the other follow.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"grief for a lost friend conducted as a challenge to anyone who would judge his fall","built":"the outro's unanswered bargain throws a question across the gap between living and dead"},
"alice-in-chains~your-decision": {"haiku":"One person's chosen complacency and betrayal fuels suffering for others, responsibility rests on individual choices to withdraw or resist.","opus":"A rebuke to someone spiraling into self-destruction, insisting the collapse is entirely their own doing with no one else at fault as they head toward ruin. Alice in Chains frame it as harsh compassion, warning that the moment to change is fleeting and that everything unspoken curdles into too late.","sonnet":"An intervention that came too late — the narrator confronts someone who let fear become their god, who chose numbness and running over honesty, and who now stands before everyone and admits it's over.","src":"haiku","fable":"Released as an early single from Alice in Chains' first album after Layne Staley's overdose death, this is an address to someone destroying themselves who insists on framing the ruin as a matter of free will — the refrain hammers agency back at them not as respect but as accusation, refusing the comfort that they were merely a victim. The song builds from private diagnosis (fear hardening into a governing god, apathy to everyone watching) toward a public reckoning, and the pivot arrives when the accused stands before the whole group to announce that it is over, so that the addict's surrender gets staged as a decision made in front of witnesses rather than a fate suffered alone. Beneath the blame is grief: the repeated insistence that the choice is theirs is how the survivors process being unable to have chosen for them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief","anger & defiance"],"means":"agency hammered back as accusation, not respect for free will","built":"the addict's surrender staged before witnesses converts private fate into a public decision"},
"alice-longyu-gao~legend":{"opus":"A hyperbolic, materialistic flex track mocking insecure haters, boasting of wealth, brand snobbery, and rich-bitch excess while riffing on everyone's desperate desire to become a legend.","sonnet":"A maximally confident, trash-talking self-portrait: the narrator positions herself as genuinely authentic and superior in a world of wannabes, mocking insecure people who want to be legends while she simply is one, by her own reckoning.","src":"sonnet"},
"alice-longyu-gao~monk":{"haiku":"Existential anxiety metastasizes into domestic ritual; the speaker constructs stability while aware of climate collapse and personal futility.","opus":"A hyperpop self-portrait of an immigrant artist white-knuckling adulthood, ironizing her own frugality, sobriety and ambition against a grandmother's marriage plot and a backdrop of climate dread. The joke masks a real wish to be free of wanting anything.","sonnet":"A portrait of solitary urban life in disciplined self-management — no drinking, no AC, routine as depression prevention — interrupted by an English voice in the head, grandmother's marriage pressure, and ambient dread of climate change and World War III; the speaker names herself a prodigy and wishes she were a monk.","src":"haiku"},
"alicia-keys~underdog":{"haiku":"Invisible labour sustains society while institutional forces crush those attempting dignified escape; the underdog persists through recognition and solidarity.","opus":"Keys builds an anthem out of small unnoticed encounters and the people the system overlooks: single mothers, refugees, kids on the front line. The point is dignity and quiet resilience, that being counted out is not the same as being finished.","sonnet":"A hymn for people who are overlooked and working against the odds — single mothers, immigrant cab drivers, underpaid teachers, frontline soldiers — told through the lens of small human encounters that reveal grace in the margins of ordinary life.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-ant-farm~smooth-criminal":{"haiku":"A violent intruder attacks a woman in her home, leaving bloodstains and striking her down in her bedroom. The song depicts domestic assault with clinical precision.","opus":"The narrative recounts an intruder striking down a woman named Annie in her apartment, bloodstains on the carpet, the frantic refrain asking if she is okay. Alien Ant Farm's nu-metal reworking of Michael Jackson's story-song became their signature, an affectionate, punchy cover that swaps slick funk for guitar-driven urgency while preserving the crime-scene suspense.","sonnet":"A woman is attacked in her own apartment by an intruder who strikes without warning and vanishes, leaving only bloodstains and unanswered questions about whether she survived. The repeated plea for her to confirm she is still alive frames the violence as a suspended, unresolved horror.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-ant-farm~wish":{"haiku":"A speaker warns that proud, pompous people live hollow lives of empty gestures while worn inspiration fades and wishes are broken.","opus":"The California band, best known for their nu-metal-era covers, spins a jittery meditation on pride, watching others rise and fall while making a succotash wish. Pompous lives throwing kisses collide with a plea not to have one's own wishes broken by chimes. A recurring cry to bring someone back haunts the bounce. The nonsense-tinged imagery masks a real ache beneath the funk-inflected verses about envy and loss.","sonnet":"A mourning piece — someone gone is wished back while the narrator watches pompous life go on unchanged around the absence. The chimes that once broke wishes now mark a grief that no amount of strength or resilience can fully address.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-weaponry~ahi-k":{"haiku":"Te reo Māori: Indigenous dispossession by colonial occupation; warriors stand and resist, naming their home and ancestral fire against governmental theft.","opus":"Sung in te reo Māori, this recounts the 1978 Bastion Point occupation, where iwi resisted government seizure of ancestral land. Ahi kā, the home fire kept burning, becomes the burning declaration that this ground is theirs and always was.","sonnet":"A Maori-language song about defending ancestral land — it invokes specific tribal genealogy and place names to recount the burning of homes and dispossession by the British Crown, with police massing at Takaparawhā (Bastion Point), framing it as an ongoing act of resistance.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-weaponry~blinded":{"opus":"A breakup declaration to a controlling, insecure partner who pretends nothing's wrong and tries to dominate; the singer, realizing they never really knew each other, refuses to be taken for a fool and sets them both free.","sonnet":"The narrator confronts a controlling, insecure partner who acts like nothing is wrong while trying to dominate someone they don't actually know — the verdict is mutual estrangement and a clean severance, setting both of them free.","src":"sonnet"},
"alien-weaponry~holding-my-breath":{"haiku":"Judged and drained by perception, the speaker views themselves as monstrous; they feel so suffocated that opening their eyes seems worse than death.","opus":"A young narrator crushed by public shame and guilt he believes is unforgivable describes himself as a horror shut away from everything human. He would rather keep his eyes closed than face the world. Alien Weaponry, the New Zealand trio known for Maori-language thrash, set that heritage aside for a stark English portrait of self-loathing folding a mind inward.","sonnet":"Crushed by guilt no one will forgive and a self-image so monstrous that waking feels worse than death, a person retreats inward — paralyzed, breathless, suspended in a black hole of isolation where simply opening their eyes takes more courage than they have.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-weaponry~kai-tangata":{"haiku":"In Māori, the song invokes warrior deities and rituals of battle—sacred footsteps on the war path, heads taken, bodies marked with warrior signs of Tūmatauenga.","opus":"Sung in te reo Maori, a war chant invoking Tumatauenga, god of war, and the ancestral moko and rites of the battle path, drawing on the practice of eating one's enemies. Alien Weaponry reclaiming Maori history as thrash.","sonnet":"In Māori, this is a war song invoking Tūmatauenga, the Māori god of war — describing the sacred marks of warriors, the taking of heads and captives in battle, the ritual of consuming enemies, performed in the tradition of ancestral warrior culture.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-weaponry~pc-bro":{"haiku":"A critique of social media surveillance and performative living, where constant observation forces people into creating false versions of themselves for public consumption.","opus":"A jab at screen-addicted lives lived through other people's eyes, the media-ruled masses swallowing common sense while the fixated demand every move be performed for them, the hammered refrain declaring your life a lie. Alien Weaponry are a young New Zealand thrash band, and here they trade their usual Maori themes for a blunt broadside against social-media validation and the surveillance of the endlessly watching crowd.","sonnet":"A broadside against people who perform their lives for the watching crowd — the glazed eyes, the curated existence built for the masses who demand you satisfy their fixation. The accusation is blunt: your life is a construction, a lie, and the algorithm of social approval has eaten your common sense. The song offers no alternative, just disgust delivered at volume.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-weaponry~r-ana-te-whenua":{"haiku":"The song mourns a battle site and fallen warriors; thunder, fire, and death mark sacred ground. It invokes ancestors lost to conflict and destruction on ancestral land.","opus":"Te reo Maori thrash recounting the 1864 battle of Pukehinahina (Gate Pa), where Maori defenders faced colonial guns. It grieves the dead and the shaking land as ancestral history, refusing to let the wound be forgotten.","sonnet":"Sung in te reo Māori, this is a lament for the dead at the Battle of Gate Pā (Pukehinahina) in 1864 — calling on listeners to hear the cannon fire on the ridge, mourning the fallen, and weeping tears and blood into the earth of Papatūānuku.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-weaponry~rage-it-takes-over-again":{"haiku":"Uncontrollable anger erupts when the speaker's rumors are spread and distrust grows, leaving both people prideful and seeking victory rather than reconciliation.","opus":"Alien Weaponry render a spiral of anger set off by spread rumours, admitting the narrator cannot control the rage that overtakes him even as both parties feed their egos and hunger to win. The closing Maori words invoke primal energy and awe. The young New Zealand band weaves te reo Maori into thrash, grounding personal fury in cultural roots.","sonnet":"Rumour-spreading ignites an uncontrollable rage the narrator cannot manage. He recognises both parties bear fault, that ego and the need to win are driving escalation, and that the fury is cyclical — arriving again each time the wound is reopened.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-weaponry~raupatu":{"haiku":"A commemoration of Indigenous land dispossession and resistance to colonial conquest, asserting that cultural identity and genealogy cannot be taken despite colonial violence.","opus":"A defiant reckoning with colonial land confiscation, invoking the Treaty's guarantee of chieftainship and naming Maori King Tawhiao and the battle sites of Rangiriri and Parihaka before insisting the settlers can take land but never mana, identity, or ancestry. Alien Weaponry are the New Zealand teenagers who sing thrash metal in te reo Maori, and this fuses groove-metal aggression with genuine historical protest and whakapapa pride.","sonnet":"A reclamation of Maori sovereignty through a litany of what was seized and what cannot be taken — land, villages, treasures, identity, genealogy, spirit. Raupatu means confiscation; the song names the battles (Rangiriri, Parihaka) and then pivots to defiance: the colonizer can take land but not mana, not people, not who we are. The Treaty is the opening witness against what followed.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-weaponry~te-ara":{"haiku":"Māori identity and spiritual landscape guide the journey; the path connects ancestors and earth in indigenous sovereignty.","opus":"Sung in te reo Maori, this invokes Te Arawa whakapapa and the pathway of an ancestral people, a metal band using thrash to carry oral history and a call to arms forward. The subject is lineage as living inheritance, not nostalgia.","sonnet":"A waiata in Te Reo Māori honouring the path of Ngāti Ohomairangi to Te Ika a Māui — the fish of Māui, the North Island — calling out Te Arawa's warrior heritage through the ancestral voice, an invitation to draw near and step forward onto the threshold of Houmaitawhiti.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-weaponry~the-things-that-you-know":{"haiku":"The speaker critiques how societal pressure and conformity trap people into doing safe, expected things rather than breaking free from the comfort of the familiar.","opus":"Alien Weaponry mock the comfort of mediocrity and obedience, satirising a life spent doing only the safe and familiar while a controlling government demands compliance and worship. The refrain names how hard it is to release what one already knows. The New Zealand trio sharpen youthful defiance into a critique of conformity and cleansing of dissent.","sonnet":"A rejection of safe, comfortable conformity — the predictable job, the government-approved path, the mediocrity of doing only what feels familiar. The narrator goads the listener toward something bigger while acknowledging how difficult it is to release the security of the known.","src":"haiku"},
"alien-weaponry~whispers":{"haiku":"Government conceals truth through manipulation and lies, using its rhetoric to control citizens through fear while betraying the rights that indigenous Māori people were promised in the Treaty.","opus":"Alien Weaponry indict a government whose words are lies whispered to exploit fear, weaving verses in te reo Maori that reference the foreshore and seabed dispute, land theft and broken treaty promises. The band, Maori themselves, channels indigenous grievance into thrash, naming figures and legislation to argue that people will uncover the truth a greedy system tries to silence.","sonnet":"A bilingual indictment of government deception, weaving English accusations of fear-mongering with Maori verses condemning the systematic betrayal of indigenous rights under the Treaty of Waitangi. Official words are reduced to whispers that obscure rather than illuminate.","src":"haiku"},
"alienblaze~lovesick-feat-omenxiii":{"haiku":"Obsessive longing and mutual toxicity consume both lovers, where sickness and resentment breed exhaustion.","opus":"Love as sickness after a year of waiting, wishing the confession back while the other stays lodged in the head. The push-pull of being sick of each other yet begging not to be left lovesick captures a toxic attachment neither can quit.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship where both people are exhausted of and resentful toward each other — they're obsessed despite mutual contempt, unable to get each other out of their heads, and the love has curdled into something that makes them want to disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"alienist~hypnotise":{"haiku":"Connection becomes curse as the speaker's demons are both soothed and awakened by another person, oscillating between salvation and deeper entanglement in shared pathology.","opus":"An addict's paradox where the thing consuming him is also the only cure, a lover or a substance who erases his demons while he blacks out and craves again. Being hypnotised names the loss of will, the speaker ending as the one entranced rather than freed.","sonnet":"Trauma resurfacing and the desperate, consuming need for another person to erase it: the speaker, blacked out by memory and losing their grip on reality, finds that someone hypnotises their demons and becomes the only cure — until the speaker realises they are now the one hypnotised.","src":"haiku"},
"alienist~prisoner-of-you":{"haiku":"Entrapment within another's vision where escape requires becoming unrecognizable. The narrator claims ultimate freedom through ending, erasing the connection entirely.","opus":"A furious purge of a manipulative, victim-playing hypocrite whose voice the narrator can't get out of his head; metalcore catharsis about becoming your abuser's ending and freeing yourself by cutting them off entirely.","sonnet":"A speaker cuts ties with a manipulative, hypocritical person who played victim and drained them — addressing their captive-like mental state, picking up pieces, and asserting a final, furious termination: \"you mean nothing to me,\" get out of my head.","src":"haiku"},
"aliens-ate-my-setlist~missed-me":{"haiku":"Absence lingers in memory as presence; return and reunion animate those left behind, but the singer knows that arrival is temporary and departure is the only certainty.","opus":"A performer's cheerful return after an absence, framing the departure as something he needed and reassuring the crowd he never really said goodbye. The song is about the bond between a band and its audience, memory and reunion turned into an invitation to rock the night.","sonnet":"A performer addresses an audience after a period of absence, insisting the crowd missed them and celebrating the return as unambiguously good news. The song cycles between acknowledging that some departures are permanent and reassuring listeners that this one was only temporary.","src":"haiku"},
"alison-wonderland~heaven":{"haiku":"Sexual ecstasy becomes transcendent revelation; the body finds paradise through intimate encounter.","opus":"Rapturous sex mapped across every room of a lover's life, the speaker astonished to find heaven in an ordinary bedroom. The stammering repetitions of never knew and higher than heaven let pure physical ecstasy overwhelm coherent speech.","sonnet":"Intimacy in every domestic space — kitchen, car, bed, bathroom, boulevard — is elevated to a transcendent state; \"heaven in your room\" is an intoxicating discovery, and the whole song traces the physical shock of that realisation.","src":"haiku"},
"alison-wonderland~thirst-hex-cougar-remix":{"haiku":"Primacy and originality consumed by imitators pursuing fame and money. The narrator arrived first and watches others chase her shadow while thirsting for authenticity.","opus":"A hardened kiss-off to imitators and hangers-on who live in her shadow and want her world only for money and fame; she asserts primacy, that she came first, and dismisses their thirst.","sonnet":"From a position of priority and ownership — \"I came first\" — the narrator dismisses imitators who want the world for money and fame, telling them to take their time and settle in while she refuses to stop or be copied.","src":"haiku"},
"alissic~like":{"haiku":"Falling apart becomes intoxicating ritual; the speaker embraces descent and darkness as preferable to the suffocation of stability, finding strange comfort in collapse.","opus":"An unsettling admission of craving her own unraveling, sleeping like a shark and drawn to whatever creeps in the dark. It's about someone confronting another's guilt while confessing she's addicted to coming apart.","sonnet":"The speaker finds a perverse comfort in falling apart and crawling in the dark — \"I sleep like a shark, can't tune out tonight\" — addressing someone who hides from shame and refuses to let buried things burst into the open, while the speaker confesses to preferring the rawness of collapse over composure.","src":"haiku"},
"alive-for-redemption~imprisoned":{"haiku":"Self-incarceration through guilt and mental labyrinth where the jailer and prisoner merge. Breaking free requires confronting the warden inside before liberation becomes possible.","opus":"A metalcore portrait of depression as solitary confinement built by your own mind, where the jailer and worst enemy is yourself; guilt and a corrupting inner voice keep the door locked while you scream for release.","sonnet":"Self-imprisonment through guilt and inner accusation: invisible chains, a mirror showing a stranger, a jailer who is the self — the speaker oscillates between the desire for freedom and the recognition that the prison warden and the prisoner are the same person, insisting at the close they will not accept this as fate.","src":"haiku"},
"all-faces-down~reckless-nights":{"haiku":"Youth pursues danger as proof of vitality, constructing an identity from transgression and collective momentum where caution is weakness and velocity absolves all consequence.","opus":"An anthem of defiant youth that shrugs off good advice and shaking heads, claiming reckless nights as something earned and owned. The refusal to pray or live in fear turns hedonistic loyalty among friends into a creed, going deeper together as the world disapproves.","sonnet":"A group anthem about living hard and owning it — \"shoulder to shoulder,\" ignoring disapproval, and treating reckless nights as a shared identity rather than a flaw. The speaker challenges the crowd to sing along and promises to take over the streets, framing defiance and togetherness as inseparable.","src":"haiku"},
"all-fires~don-t-say-manhattan":{"haiku":"Desire for permanence collides with threat of abandonment; the speaker stakes claim against departure.","opus":"A desperate plea to a lover threatening to leave for Manhattan, begging them not to name the place that means goodbye. Escalating from speech to a scream, a vow to burn for life set against the leaves already drifting captures a fight to hold someone who's halfway gone.","sonnet":"A speaker pleads with someone not to say Manhattan — the word apparently signalling departure — insisting they will not leave and are setting a fire for life, while the background voices describe the other person as leaves blowing in the breeze.","src":"haiku"},
"all-fires~make-an-entrance":{"haiku":"Historical power dynamics and the tension between rising ambition and the threat of falling; natives outmaneuvering the colonizing order.","opus":"This indie-rock outfit build a tense standoff of loyalty and detachment, the narrator promising not to care whether a companion stays or goes. Imagery of unarmed kings and knife-wielding natives frames a shifting balance of power, while the recurring sense of falling away signals a slow, deliberate retreat.","sonnet":"Power shifts and falls, and the narrator finds himself losing ground as questions multiply and loyalty fractures. Kings watch in fear while those without armour wield knives instead. The act of climbing feels criminal; the falling away is the only honest motion left.","src":"haiku"},
"all-saints~one-strike":{"haiku":"A single devastating blow shatters the narrative of love; the speaker reads betrayal clearly and chooses departure with emotional clarity.","opus":"The moment a relationship detonates, rendered as a single decisive flash of clarity. A partner's cruelty becomes the sign to leave; the calm of finally deciding to walk out reads as relief more than heartbreak.","sonnet":"A person reaches the final breaking point in a toxic relationship — one word, one lie too many — and decides to make their emotions clear before disappearing, the song moving from numb shock through the crystallising moment of departure.","src":"haiku"},
"all-time-low~monsters-feat-blackbear":{"haiku":"Addiction to dysfunction outweighs self-preservation as the speaker surrenders to predictable harm, night amplifying dangerous patterns they cannot resist.","opus":"A relationship as addiction, the singer knowing full well this person wrecks his life and running back anyway. The monsters are internal, the compulsion toward comforting lies stronger than the will to leave.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a toxic relationship they cannot leave despite full awareness of the damage — returning compulsively to someone who lies, who hurts, who makes everything worse — and asking themselves why the pull remains so strong. The self-destruction is conscious and almost chosen.","src":"haiku"},
"all-to-get-her~misery":{"haiku":"Internal war between identities where self-dissolution precedes any external violence. The mirror shows enemy and victim combined, rendering separation and resolution impossible.","opus":"A battle with the self staged as confronting a killer in the mirror, wanting to lobotomize away the destructive inner voice; by the end the narrator flips to survival, resolving to outlive the part of them that wants them dead.","sonnet":"An internal war between two selves — one the speaker stares at in the mirror, one that wants to dominate — escalates from suicidal despair to violent resolution, with the speaker declaring a lobotomy, then a revolution, and finally the execution of the inner enemy so the real self can live.","src":"haiku"},
"allday~you-always-know-the-dj":{"haiku":"Youth obsession and hollow nightlife spiral into emptiness; the narrator critiques a lifestyle built on knowing everyone but belonging nowhere.","opus":"The Australian rapper sketches a young woman trapped in nightlife's status economy, mistaking free entry and proximity to power for winning. Under the sympathy sits a warning that the scam eventually collects, and she knows it.","sonnet":"The narrator addresses a woman whose social currency is constant clubbing and VIP access, warning that the hollowness of that world is catching up with her and that knowing the DJ does not make the lifestyle sustainable or real.","src":"haiku"},
"allie-x~paper-love":{"haiku":"Romantic illusion disintegrates when confronted by the beloved's emotional cruelty; fragile attachment dissolves like paper under pressure.","opus":"Desire the singer knows is doomed, chasing a boy who will inevitably tear her apart while she watches her own heart pulped like paper. The knowingness is the point: she walks into the wound with eyes open.","sonnet":"A woman goes into a relationship knowing the man will hurt her — she watches herself dissolve anyway, heart turning fragile as paper, choosing to stretch the time even as she predicts every cut he'll leave.","src":"haiku"},
"alltheprettythings~teenage-lines":{"haiku":"Demonic influence and moral corruption seduce the speaker toward destruction as they become a weapon turned against their small-town world, their dark trajectory immortalized in casual cruelty.","opus":"A survivor faces the person who once controlled and hurt them, breaking their silence and turning the reckoning into a story others will finally witness. Imagery of the devil placing faith in him and a graveyard's worth of crosses that can't free him frames a small-town damnation he's ready to loose rather than flee.","sonnet":"A confessional addressed to someone who shaped a damaging past: the speaker, long in league with a devil who has \"faith\" in them, shakes hands with damnation and turns rage on their small town, yet also wants to be remembered — kept alive — in the other person's teenage lines.","src":"haiku"},
"allt~aquila":{"haiku":"Hive consciousness and collective dissolution into enforced unity through gas and integration. The great beyond promises erasure of individual will into divine merger.","opus":"A dystopian hive-mind parable where individuals are urged to stick to the script, surrender their flesh, and dissolve into a sedated collective sold as divine unity; obedience dressed up as transcendence and rebirth.","sonnet":"A hive-mind recruitment pitch from an entity offering unity and rebirth beyond the flesh: \"reset, renew, rebuild, reinstall\" runs like a firmware update, promising breathing, divinity, and eternal oneness in exchange for trading flesh — the seduction of total absorption framed as salvation.","src":"haiku"},
"allt~emanate":{"haiku":"A god watches humanity copy and suffer beneath imposed design. Calling draws the trapped toward predetermined decay.","opus":"A malevolent entity addressing a dreamer trapped in its realm, feeding on disbelief and leading a race into decay; the god of dreams is dead and the plea to be plugged in becomes surrender to a consuming digital-spiritual overload.","sonnet":"A predatory entity speaking from inside a networked, attention-devouring system — it has already killed the god of dreams and replaced it; it feeds on human credulity and uniqueness-fantasies until people overload and collapse, finally demanding the speaker be plugged back in to serve as its conduit.","src":"haiku"},
"allt~paralyzed":{"haiku":"A person feels trapped by an invisible entity living in them, caught between death and life, silenced by deafening despair.","opus":"A visceral rendering of dread as a living thing lodged deep inside, a hue and a tempest boring outward through a cage of flesh until the narrator is frozen with nothing but vision left, something at the edge of sight wanting him gone yet refusing to let him die. Allt, a Swedish metalcore act, stage an eclipse-lit existential panic, asking whether a soul survives the body.","sonnet":"Something alive inside the body that won't leave — a force the color of a storm, tunneling through bone, wanting the carrier dead but keeping them alive. The eclipse approaches, the light is visible, but hands pull you back down into the cold. The final question is the one the whole song circles: if I survive this, what exactly am I surviving for?","src":"haiku"},
"allt~rupture":{"haiku":"A person confronts their own inner darkness and fear, realizing the presence hunting them is their own worst self and regrets.","opus":"This Swedish metalcore track traces a man's descent into his own psyche, stalked by an intruder he eventually recognizes as the dread he has himself become. Its central argument rejects the comfort of pretending wounds close on their own, insisting instead on owning past deeds. The menace turns out to be the self.","sonnet":"A slow discovery that the haunting presence is the self — the walls closing in, the pendulum swinging on decisions deferred, the question of which god holds you. The turn comes when reflection forces an admission: the presence you feared is you, the man you have become through what you refused to confront. The masks, the unlived changes, the unwillingness to accept — they are the haunting.","src":"haiku"},
"allt~severance":{"haiku":"Cyclical destruction and judgment perpetuate as the speaker oscillates between burning and freezing. Division from divine and self leaves only corruption and decay behind.","opus":"A cosmic reckoning with a god-figure asked whether he'd punish or forgive, the world an apocalypse rotted by a demon within. The plea to be cut loose, body breaking and soul decaying, turns the song into a struggle over whether the self is anything more than what a memory leaves behind.","sonnet":"A speaker who carries an apocalyptic inner daemon — watering rivers with blood, emptying the gardens of elysium — begging to be severed from it: the cycle overlaps, disappears, and returns, the faceless man in the mirror a constant, the sun burning and the night freezing time.","src":"haiku"},
"allt~the-deep-blue-silent":{"opus":"Allt sink into depressive numbness, the parasite of negative emotion eating from within until oblivion itself begins to call like home. The sea that summons is a gentle death-wish, a longing for the feeling of nothing at all. The metalcore band render surrender as something almost tender, a quiet drowning that promises peace.","sonnet":"A song about actively choosing oblivion — the narrator walks toward the sea, feet touching the water, pulled by a craving for the feeling of total emptiness, and finds a kind of peace in the idea of sinking to the bottom and never having to feel anything again.","src":"sonnet"},
"allt~the-orphan-breed":{"haiku":"Superiority collapses under rising water as the self-proclaimed elite drown in their own weakness. The orphan breed crawls back to origins, reduced to disease by their own hubris.","opus":"A furious dismantling of a self-appointed messiah, aimed at anyone who claims a chosen or superior status and delivers nothing. The stance is contempt for false authority and the promise that the pretender will drown in his own weakness.","sonnet":"A band of wolves turns on a self-proclaimed superior race whose messianic claims ('the chosen one') have been exposed as masquerade and disease; 'crawl your way back to where it all began' condemns a group that played God until its weakness began to show, the 'orphan breed' marked for forced defeat and eternal suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"ally-nicholas~bullets":{"haiku":"Depression and drowning persist despite love's presence while the narrator recognises that rescue from another's perspective remains impossible.","opus":"A burnt-out lover asking permission to come undone in the dark, terrified of being too much and forfeiting a love that once vowed to be unlike the others. The plea is simply to be witnessed and held while going under, even knowing the other person is powerless to rescue them.","sonnet":"A person in emotional free-fall asks their partner not to talk but just to be present — kiss them with the lights off, let them fall apart, be beside them — confessing they are drowning endlessly and cannot be saved, only held.","src":"haiku"},
"ally-nicholas~seventeen":{"haiku":"Youth and threshold suicide; nearly falling at the edge of black skies while young.","opus":"A stalled, aching return to an old flame, the speaker no better off than she was at seventeen, teetering at the edge, not leaping but liable to fall. She feels small whenever she reaches for more, offering herself while knowing exactly why the wanting still hurts.","sonnet":"Arrested development and lingering attachment: the speaker admits she's no better off than at seventeen, reaching out to someone late at night under the guise of checking in, confessing that being called your girl still hurts and that proximity is the real ask — stay so I don't fall.","src":"haiku"},
"along-came-a-spider~back-in-the-hollow":{"haiku":"Pain is eroticized and weaponized by a tormentor who feeds on suffering, trapping the victim in a cycle where submission and self-destruction blur into a single hollow place.","opus":"A self-loathing surrender to abuse he's come to crave, begging to be hurt because he has learned to love the pain. A shadow of who he was, he swears never to relapse into that pit while knowing neither he nor his tormentor will ever truly change.","sonnet":"A speaker in a destructive relationship has been broken down, paralyzed, and left hollow, yet finds a perverse love for the torment — \"I love the way you torture me\" — and refuses to fall \"back in the hollow\" even as they are beaten to their knees. The tension between wanting escape and craving the pain drives every verse.","src":"haiku"},
"alpha-wolf~104":{"haiku":"Depression and suicidal ideation breed an intimate relationship with suffering; the speaker numbers himself among the damned and invites obliteration as the only honest escape.","opus":"A raw metalcore account of depression sliding toward suicide, the narrator half in love with his own despair and scripting the methods aloud. The reference to a hospital number turns private anguish into a statistic, which is exactly the horror.","sonnet":"A person consumed by depression and self-harm spirals through alienation, suicidal ideation, and a strange attachment to their own suffering, identifying with a case number that reduces them to a system's ward rather than a human being.","src":"haiku"},
"alpha-wolf~60cm-of-steel":{"haiku":"Wanderlust and disconnection from roots where curious impulse becomes curse. The narrator burns connections to escape but finds only burning wherever he settles.","opus":"An aching restlessness that curdles into a death drive, a man who has given all his love and burned every match, hating the changing skyline of his home city and choosing to burn every bridge and follow the road wherever it kills him.","sonnet":"A speaker suffocated by a city they were born in and a life drained of desire burns every bridge and embraces the open road — even if it leads to a nightmare — choosing nomadic self-destruction over the rigor-mortis embrace of everyone they have ever met.","src":"haiku"},
"alpha-wolf~a-quiet-place-to-die":{"haiku":"A person lost and hopeless searches for a place to end their life, feeling death beside them as they live only half-aware.","opus":"A numb, soul-emptied narrator pleads to be carried home while searching in vain for somewhere peaceful to end it, feeling caged inside his own existence. Alpha Wolf, the Melbourne metalcore act rooted in nu-metal heaviness, render depression and mental illness as a suffocating gloom, barely alive and begging for release.","sonnet":"A mind hollowed out by depression and dissociation searches for somewhere to quietly cease. The crow as carrier, the shadow-life of half-feeling, the relentless question of why — all converge on an ache for rest that feels less like death-wish and more like total exhaustion with existing.","src":"haiku"},
"alpha-wolf~akudama":{"haiku":"A powerful figure warns others never to cross them, demanding recognition and threatening retaliation for betrayal.","opus":"Seething with betrayal, the speaker demands his name be spoken and warns that anyone living in his shadow falls apart without him, wrapping vengeance in decapitation imagery. Alpha Wolf lean on nu-metalcore's percussive spite here, the Australian band turning resentment and suffocated breath into a taunting, grudge-fueled confrontation that dares the target to dig two graves.","sonnet":"A figure who has been used and underestimated reaches a point of ferocious reclamation, demanding to be named and acknowledged before suffocating on silence. Retribution is framed less as vengeance and more as the inevitable consequence of living too long in someone else's shadow.","src":"haiku"},
"alpha-wolf~black-mamba":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts abandonment and betrayal, cataloging the ways they've been wounded and demanding recognition for their suffering.","opus":"A venomous confrontation with a traitor whose betrayals still ache, the scars forming a story the narrator has to conceal as he dares the coward to face him directly during their manipulations. Alpha Wolf, an Australian metalcore band known for nu-metal bounce, coil the track around the crushing weight of being abandoned, the fury of someone finally done keeping quiet.","sonnet":"Rage at someone who wore down trust through silence, manipulation, and betrayal — the knives collected from the back, the wounds cauterized with contempt. The speaker refuses to absorb the damage quietly anymore: eyes up, name remembered, confrontation demanded at full volume. The deadweight around the chest finally named for what it is: something that must be torn loose.","src":"haiku"},
"alpha-wolf~creep":{"haiku":"A marginalized person rejects being controlled or accepted, choosing to be a pariah rather than surrender their individuality.","opus":"Australian metalcore band Alpha Wolf turn rejection into defiance, with a narrator battered and cast out who would sooner die an outsider than live as a follower. Sharpened into a scene kiss-off that mocks phony peers and industry sellouts, it carries the reckless swagger of someone with nothing left to lose but the little faith he had.","sonnet":"An outcast who has stopped apologizing for making people uncomfortable — kicked down, labeled, but choosing the role of pariah over the role of follower. The faith lost in someone specific becomes a wider accounting: counterfeit souls, double-crosses, snitches. The final posture is stone cold self-possession, sleeping with one eye open rather than bowing to anyone's approval.","src":"haiku"},
"alpha-wolf~hotel-underground":{"haiku":"Predatory existence and infestation as lifestyle where infection spreads through weakness. The narrator steps over and extinguishes those beneath him in triumphant hierarchy.","opus":"A venomous purge of parasites and false apologies, casting the leeches and rats of the narrator's life as vermin to be trapped and burned out; nu-metalcore contempt for the spineless people who bled him dry.","sonnet":"Contempt for parasitic people who drain, manipulate, and split hairs to justify themselves: the speaker is still stepping over roaches, setting fire to locusts, laying strychnine, and waiting for the rats to crawl back into the trap — revenge framed as pest control.","src":"haiku"},
"alpha-wolf~no-name":{"haiku":"The speaker has internalized shame since childhood, calling themselves worthless, deranged, and hollow. They express suicidal ideation and emotional numbness, having felt fundamentally broken from an early age.","opus":"The Australian metalcore band Alpha Wolf lay bare a lifetime of feeling worthless and monstrous since childhood, the narrator hollowed out, self-hating, and picturing a weapon at his skull to swap memory for metal. The refrain strips away his identity and value, rendering dissociation and inner emptiness with brutality that defiance only thinly disguises.","sonnet":"A man who has internalised a sense of worthlessness since childhood — nameless, freakish, numb — cycles through self-loathing that has never diminished with age. The violence he imagines turning on himself reflects not drama but an exhaustion so complete that feeling has simply stopped.","src":"haiku"},
"alpha-wolf~restricted-r18":{"haiku":"Adult content and explicit themes, sexual transgression.","opus":"Consumed by vengeful rage, the speaker dreams of slitting an abuser's throat and finds peace in that fantasy, refusing to forget or forgive; blaming bloodline and mental state, he lets homicidal thoughts fester until his target is dead, turning predator on former prey.","sonnet":"Revenge-dream fury at someone who caused real harm — the narrator keeps dreaming of slitting their throat and waking to find only the dream gone, not the wish. \"Don't you ever think I would forgive\" is the spine of the song, and the fantasized violence is framed as a response to \"insidious intentions\" that were done to the narrator first.","src":"haiku"},
"alpha-wolf~sub-zero":{"opus":"Aggressive self-defense against a fake, ill-intended impostor, the narrator dismissing the poser as a barking dog to his wolf while confessing he's gone emotionally numb and self-harming, using cigarettes and rage to fill the void.","sonnet":"Contempt for a fake, performatively tough person who is all threat in the dark but cowardly in the light — the narrator feels numb and hostile, has nothing in common with this impostor, and dares them to try something while making clear they will not back down.","src":"sonnet"},
"alpha-wolf~ward-of-the-state":{"haiku":"Systemic neglect and abandonment isolate the speaker into desperate loneliness; institutional indifference breeds only alienation.","opus":"A cry from inside the care system, a neglected and abused kid asking where the promised guidance ever was. The institution's walls are not home, and the loneliness of being processed rather than loved is the whole wound.","sonnet":"A person raised in institutional neglect — misread, misunderstood, passed between systems — cries out for guidance and a place that feels like home, reduced by bureaucracy to a label rather than a self, profoundly alone within walls that were never theirs.","src":"haiku"},
"alphamega~sound-of-your-excuse":{"haiku":"Deception breeds contempt as words reveal the rot beneath surface charm; the speaker rejects compromise and watches the relationship combust rather than capitulate to mediocrity.","opus":"Disillusionment with someone who seemed dreamlike until they opened their mouth; the speaker refuses to settle for mediocrity or keep sustaining a manipulator. Calling the relationship finished, he lets the fire fall rather than tolerate one more excuse.","sonnet":"An initial attraction collapses the moment the other person speaks, their excuses becoming unbearable; the speaker cuts the relationship off rather than endure mediocrity, letting fire rain down as a symbol of a clean, final break. The repeated refusal to be bitten by the hand they fed anchors the rejection.","src":"haiku"},
"alphasix~abyssal-awakening":{"haiku":"Deep sea transformation and collective merger into hive consciousness through unknowable pressure. The abyss awakens in the depths, reconstituting human bodies into unified perfection.","opus":"A deep-sea cosmic-horror descent where a crew investigating anomalies is consumed and remade by an ancient leviathan god, humanity reduced to the pathogen while the abyss awakens and births its dominion; oblivion sold as immortal transformation.","sonnet":"A Lovecraftian descent: a signal lost in oceanic blackness pulls characters down to cybernetic, disfigured entities that synchronise minds and transform flesh — the abyss awakens as a leviathan dominion, welcoming humans into a collective, immortal, genetically reconstructed new order.","src":"haiku"},
"alphawhores~same-team":{"haiku":"Mutual illusion and exhaustion where partnership crumbles under unmet promises and deception. Both partners lived false narratives that collapse simultaneously into disappointment.","opus":"The bitter aftermath of realizing a relationship was an illusion built on broken promises, gaslit into feeling crazy while waiting for someone who ran out of time; anger at having believed a lie.","sonnet":"A relationship that turned out to be an illusion: all the promises, all the things supposedly shared, were manufactured — leaving the speaker surfing their own mind's channels, reliving gaslighting moments, and naming the entire thing as a shared delusion they were kept waiting in.","src":"haiku"},
"alt-blk-era~come-fight-me-for-it":{"haiku":"Outcast defiance celebrates those society deemed disposable; radical acceptance and protection create community against those who demand conformity.","opus":"A fierce defense of the one thing keeping an unstable young woman sane, daring anyone who'd take it or medicate it away to come and fight. She'd sooner burn down their cage and die than surrender her freedom to their chains and hollow threats.","sonnet":"A defiant territorial song about refusing to surrender something precious — the speaker would rather be dragged out physically than give it up willingly, and the song layers this with an image of someone medicated and disoriented but still fighting, treating survival as an act of aggression.","src":"haiku"},
"alt-blk-era~crashing-parties":{"haiku":"Aggressive intrusion and rejection of exclusion through force and boundary-breaking. The narrator claims space uninvited, demanding inclusion through confrontation.","opus":"A defiant outsider's revenge on the in-crowd, gatecrashing the parties she was never invited to; beneath the door-kicking bravado is a flat refusal to be left out ever again.","sonnet":"A defiant gatecrash anthem: uninvited, arriving late, refusing to be left out — stomping out friends and breaking in regardless, with the closing refrain \"I refuse to be left out\" making the intrusion less about mischief and more about the pain of exclusion.","src":"haiku"},
"alt-blk-era~hunt-you-down":{"haiku":"Obsessive possession and predatory love blur romance with violence, where the speaker pursues their target as prey.","opus":"Obsession pushed past romance into stalking and harm, sung as if devotion justifies violence. The horror is that the narrator hears love in what is really captivity, framing possession and cruelty as the ultimate proof of caring.","sonnet":"The song is narrated from inside obsessive, violent fixation — carving the target's name into the speaker's own skin, threatening to cage and kill them — presenting possessive love as indistinguishable from predation.","src":"haiku"},
"alt-blk-era~i-m-normally-like-this":{"haiku":"Defiant embrace of alternative identity despite mainstream gatekeeping and ridicule, refusal to assimilate.","opus":"An anthem of alt-kid defiance from two Black sisters staking out belonging in a scene that gatekeeps them. It flips the shame of being called moody or ugly into refusal: the point is no longer wanting the approval of the people who mocked you.","sonnet":"An alt-kid solidarity anthem reclaiming weirdness that was suppressed to earn approval — the normies gatekept, laughed, now want to be cool with them, but the answer is permanent indifference. \"I'm normally like this\" shifts from confession to declaration to battle cry as the song moves from \"I\" to \"we.\"","src":"haiku"},
"alt-blk-era~my-drummer-s-girlfriend":{"haiku":"Chaotic self-destruction and social chaos cluster around a woman trapped in addiction; her spiral infects everyone nearby.","opus":"A wry, resentful character sketch of a coked-up, unstable band girlfriend who's cold to the narrator but charming to everyone else. Observed with fascinated dislike, she's always backstage, passed out, or headbanging in a black dress—a chaotic fixture the narrator can't warm to.","sonnet":"A character study of a specific woman — cocaine-using, backstage-lurking, openly hostile to the narrator while charming everyone else — observed with irritated precision; the song is about the particular social exhaustion of being disliked by someone in your extended circle who has decided you are the enemy for reasons you cannot pin down.","src":"haiku"},
"alt-blk-era~rockstar":{"haiku":"Desire and domination fuse as the speaker demands a tyrant-lover who elevates them through violence and transgression.","opus":"A brash anthem of a woman craving a rockstar tyrant while making clear she can't be controlled, flaunting her own dominance and racial pride. The Black-excellence imagery and the man kept giddy under her thumb flip the groupie fantasy, casting her as the one in charge of the arrangement.","sonnet":"A woman demands a rockstar — a tyrant who plays strings with violence — not because she wants to be controlled but precisely because she refuses to be; she catalogues her power, her 'black excellence,' and her refusal to need any one man while running a bridal riot of her own design.","src":"haiku"},
"alt-blk-era~run-rabbit":{"opus":"Panic and violence tangled with a toxic bond, someone fleeing trouble while a fast-living girl pulls them deeper into wreckage they wish they'd never met. Behind the chase chant are broken bodies and a haunting sense that none of it was destiny.","sonnet":"A chaotic social transgression — possibly a fight at a party — becomes a frame for a relationship that left damage in its wake: the narrator is haunted by fifteen people they hurt, trapped between shame and the relief of being told to run.","src":"sonnet"},
"alt-blk-era~straight-to-heart":{"haiku":"Abandonment wounds accumulate while friends withdraw; rejection becomes internalized and channeled into self-protective bitterness.","opus":"Abandonment and untreated pain taken personally — friends pulled away, pills wanted just to numb the wounds no one can see. Every plea not to be blamed or discarded resolves into absorbing the hurt at point-blank range.","sonnet":"Complete emotional abandonment — all friends have gone, pain won't stop, and the speaker craves pills to numb invisible wounds, directing their desolation inward and announcing they'll absorb every rejection and betrayal 'straight to heart' with nowhere else for it to go.","src":"haiku"},
"alt-j~something-good":{"haiku":"A spectator watches a matador in an arena and fantasizes about clean escape from their memories, shedding the old warrior identity.","opus":"Alt-J stage heartbreak as a bullfight, the lost lover cast as a matador dragged from an arena of forty-eight thousand roaring seats once the narrator sobers up and forgets. From their debut, the track threads the band's oblique, literary bent through a plainer ache, sobriety and clarity ending the spectacle even as one good night promises brief forgetting.","sonnet":"Still raw from a finished love, a man turns to pleasure and spectacle — the arena roar of a bullfight standing in for the crowd of memories — hoping some sharp, good distraction will drown the feeling, at least for the night.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~addicted-to-pain":{"haiku":"Paralysis in cycles of heartbreak: the speaker watches someone addicted to pain and loss, unable to save them despite witnessing the destruction of everything they built.","opus":"Alter Bridge, the post-Creed vehicle for Myles Kennedy's soaring range, builds a hard-rock reckoning with someone who clings to suffering. A house of cards hits the floor as the narrator watches a loved one, blameless yet betrayed and sold out, refusing to listen while blind in the shadows. The chorus warns that this fixation on pain and appearances will cost everything in time.","sonnet":"Watching someone refuse to see the collapse happening around them, a narrator cycles between empathy and helplessness. The subject has given everything and been betrayed anyway, yet keeps returning to a destructive pattern, addicted to the very suffering that is systematically destroying what remains of their life.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~all-ends-well":{"haiku":"An encouragement to persevere through hardship with faith in eventual resolution and self-belief as the guiding principle through darkness.","opus":"Alter Bridge, the hard-rock band built from Creed's instrumentalists around Myles Kennedy's soaring voice, extend a hand to someone drowning in self-doubt. The song promises to bind up their wounded confidence and insists that even through genuine suffering, faith in yourself will carry you through. The reassuring refrain swells into the arena-sized uplift their catalogue specializes in.","sonnet":"An act of consolation aimed at someone in the middle of a hard stretch — the promise that trouble is temporary, that stars will eventually show, that the only thing needed is belief in oneself when nothing else holds. The tone is steady and unironic, a hand extended without conditions. The message is simple enough to be either hollow or genuinely useful depending on when you hear it.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~all-hope-is-gone":{"haiku":"A person surrenders after years of struggle, worn down by apathy and unmet desires until motivation evaporates. The song portrays total spiritual exhaustion and defeat.","opus":"Indifference flows like a river run dry as the narrator concedes the fight, apathy grown too large to defeat, still restlessly searching for something he cannot name. Alter Bridge, formed from Creed's musicians around Myles Kennedy's soaring voice, marry hard-rock heft to melodic despair, and this track sits at the exhausted edge of longing and surrender.","sonnet":"Decades of unfulfilled longing have calcified into apathy. The fight has been conceded, desire has drained away, and the sadness of what could have been sits permanently close — a bone-deep exhaustion that no longer even attempts to locate what was once searched for.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~bleed-it-dry":{"haiku":"Greed and exploitation consume everything, leaving nothing but dust and lies, with the speaker warning that those who extract and destroy will eventually face the dead world they created.","opus":"Alter Bridge accuse a devouring power of draining the planet while rationalizing its greed, seas swelling and peaks collapsing as reckless hands lay waste to everything. Mark Tremonti's post-Creed heaviness suits the apocalyptic charge, the singer vowing the exploiter will eventually confront the lifeless ruin they engineered, moral and ecological outrage sharpened to a point.","sonnet":"An indictment of those who consume the world's resources with no accountability — oceans rising, skies darkening — while they justify every excess. The speaker watches the earth being bled to nothing and forecasts the collapse those responsible will one day have to face.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~calm-the-fire":{"haiku":"Madness and conflict have torn people apart and caused lasting damage. Love is needed to replace hate, but the barriers of blindness and pride prevent healing.","opus":"Alter Bridge, formed by Myles Kennedy and ex-Creed members, plead for compassion to overtake ugliness, begging warring hearts to stand down since the combatants act in ignorance. Fantasies of not seeing color indict inherited prejudice, arguing enemies bleed identically. The band's soaring hard rock elevates a sincerely pacifist appeal into something arena-sized.","sonnet":"A plea for the hatred fueling division to be extinguished before it consumes everything — the singer has seen enough to know reason cannot move the deeply blind, and turns instead to love as the only force capable of replacing the obscenity.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~cry-a-river":{"haiku":"The speaker reveals betrayal and infidelity, then denies the betrayer everything they need, refusing to leave despite knowing the other's greed and emptiness.","opus":"Alter Bridge unleash a bitter reckoning with a deceitful partner unmasked as greedy and hollow, refusing to indulge the crocodile tears they now shed. The narrator returns to deny them everything they crave. Fronted by Myles Kennedy over the ex-Creed musicians, the band pairs melodic hard rock with a wounded vow to see the false soul for what it truly is.","sonnet":"A narrator who was the last to see the truth about a manipulative, self-serving partner now strips away all illusion. The grief has curdled into cold clarity: no more accommodation, no staying silent, and certainly no sympathy for the tears being performed.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~cry-of-achilles":{"haiku":"A plea to not abandon hope despite being buried in doubt and uncertainty, asserting that beauty and truth persist even when invisible and nearly lost.","opus":"A cry against doubt and hidden truth, the narrator hunting for more than he's been given, sure none of it rings true yet urging himself to keep his eyes open while beauty survives. Alter Bridge open with a flamenco-flavored acoustic passage before the riffs hit, Myles Kennedy voicing the pull between despair and hope with the epic reach that defines the band's later work.","sonnet":"An urgent call to resist despair before it becomes permanent — someone buried in doubt, screaming for truth that keeps slipping away, on the edge of letting go. The counterargument is insistent: something beautiful is still alive, the end is not here yet, heavy hearts drag you to the grave if you follow them. The final note is bewilderment at what we have collectively become by denying that.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~farther-than-the-sun":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects what they've become and flies away to find a place where they belong, choosing freedom and solitude over compromise and stagnation.","opus":"Alter Bridge frame alienation as escape velocity, the narrator drifting numb like a comet, disliking what he has become and resolving to fly free beyond compromise. Silence itself beckons him toward defiance and renewal. The band's soaring melodic hard rock lends the isolation an aspirational lift, turning self-exile into a bid for belonging farther than the sun.","sonnet":"Someone who has compromised themselves into a version of their life they no longer recognise resolves to escape entirely — not to a destination but simply away, beyond any boundary previously imagined. Silence and defiance are both heard as invitations to finally become free.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~fortress":{"haiku":"A relationship built as a fortress crumbles as both people surrender and let go. The singer clings despite knowing it cannot last, watching everything they built fall apart.","opus":"The title epic of Alter Bridge's fourth album watches a carefully built stronghold collapse, a stand-in for a relationship the narrator cannot bring himself to release even after it is finished. He warns the one walking out that regret is coming. Myles Kennedy carries the structural ruin from hushed confession to towering, defiant sorrow.","sonnet":"A relationship held together past the point of reason collapses anyway — the fortress built together caving in — and the narrator cannot release their grip even as everything is taken. Regret is promised to the one who walked away, but the letting-go remains impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~ghost-of-days-gone-by":{"haiku":"Nostalgia for innocence haunts the present while mortality accelerates, each day bringing the ghost closer until past and death become indistinguishable.","opus":"Nostalgia as a haunting, where the memory of young fearless summers becomes something that won't stop stalking the present. Underneath sits a plainer terror: growing older, watching the good years recede, not wanting to die.","sonnet":"A man haunted by the distance between the fearless youth he once shared with someone and the broken present, questions whether that person feels the same grief for what was lost. The spectre of mortality adds urgency — he doesn't want to die, but the ghost won't leave.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~lover":{"haiku":"A betrayed lover's wounds are magnified by the betrayer's denial of harm. Through infidelity and selfishness, one partner has shattered the relationship and left the other bleeding out emotionally.","opus":"A betrayed vow and open, bleeding wounds fuel a battery of accusations aimed at a partner who shattered everything and shoved it in the narrator's face. Alter Bridge pair Myles Kennedy's wide-ranging voice with heavy dynamics, and the loaded-chamber imagery here pushes heartbreak toward something violent, the speaker still holding on inside a broken house.","sonnet":"A betrayal so deep it shreds both the relationship and the betrayed person's sense of safety. The wounded partner asks, over and over, whether what was destroyed was worth the act of destruction — holding on regardless, bleeding out while the ivory tower of the bond collapses around them.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~make-it-right":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts cycles of self-deception and failure, recognizing that only personal transformation can break the pattern of repeated mistakes.","opus":"Alter Bridge confront a wounded soul with hard questions about self-injury, flight, and years of dishonesty, insisting that redemption demands standing firm and fighting for one's own convictions. A reckoning arrives when someone must concede their whole life has been a fabrication. The band's anthemic surge powers the call to confront disgrace and be tested by fire.","sonnet":"A reckoning with a life built on self-deception — years of pain, masks, and lies that have distanced the narrator from any authentic self. The recurring question is not accusatory but existential: at what point does a person stop the damage and choose truth over familiar destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~metalingus":{"haiku":"Defeat transforms into resolve through clarity; the speaker reclaims agency and releases regret to pursue authentic purpose and growth.","opus":"A vow to stop grieving the life that never happened and start living the one that has. Built as an anthem of second chances, it's about leaving regret behind and fighting to feel alive again, whatever it takes.","sonnet":"A person who hit bottom and lost hope makes a conscious turn toward rebirth — leaving regret behind, refusing to be paralysed by fear, and seizing a new chance to chase something real and feel genuinely alive.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~peace-is-broken":{"haiku":"A person has destroyed their own peace through self-inflicted conflict and internal turmoil. They alone bear responsibility for their deteriorating mental state and the breakdown of their world.","opus":"The narrator confronts someone bent on their own destruction, insisting their shattered calm is entirely self-inflicted as they inch toward ruin. Alter Bridge cast accountability as tough love, Myles Kennedy balancing reproach with urgency and warning that everything held back becomes irreversible once the chance to speak has passed.","sonnet":"A self-inflicted crisis — the speaker watches as someone wages war against their own life through choices no one else made for them. The refrain is an indictment: the chaos is not fate, it is authorship, and the damage done by inner conflict is identical to damage done by any external enemy.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~show-me-a-sign":{"haiku":"Spiritual desolation and lost faith leave a narrator drowning in lies while begging for meaning and connection.","opus":"A prayer from someone falling into darkness, begging for a sign and meaning while suspecting the lies he believed led him this far from home. He half-accepts the fault as his own even as he pleads to be led back to life.","sonnet":"A speaker falling into darkness and spiritual crisis calls out for a sign, meaning, and direction — acknowledging that lies believed have led them far from home — while the fire inside dies and the fault for being lost is accepted as their own.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~still-remains":{"haiku":"The speaker saves someone repeatedly but is denied and rejected; though it breaks his heart, he respects their free will to leave and crawl back to him before the end.","opus":"Alter Bridge frame devotion pushed past its limit, the narrator rescuing someone from drowning and flame only to be answered with denial and desertion. Wounded but resigned, he grants them the choice to walk away, sure they'll return before it's over. Myles Kennedy's aching vocal turns bruised generosity into anthem, love that endures repeated rejection.","sonnet":"A person who has rescued someone repeatedly watches them leave anyway and refuses to beg them back — the pain is real, but the other's free will is the one thing that cannot and should not be taken. They will return; they always do.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~the-uninvited":{"haiku":"Betrayal and hatred have poisoned sacred bonds, and the victims now confront their destroyer. Vows are broken, innocence is lost, and the uninvited one will face the consequences of their cruelty.","opus":"A promise of payback aimed at a cold-blooded deceiver, warning that everything stolen and every ounce of malice will boomerang back onto them, their reckoning already sealed. Alter Bridge turn injury into vengeance here, Myles Kennedy climbing from hurt to threat as the intruder is told they will soon suffer every bit of the pain they caused.","sonnet":"A reckoning with someone who committed irreversible violence against an innocent life. What was sacred has been destroyed and cannot be restored; now judgment arrives — the perpetrator cast out, transformed from participant into the uninvited, with the full weight of the act falling back on them.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~ties-that-bind":{"haiku":"A prisoner strains against confinement imposed by another, seeking freedom and the courage to break chains of control.","opus":"A hard-rock declaration of breaking free from control, chains and cages standing in for anyone or anything that owns you. The stance is defiant self-determination: the risk of escape is worth more than safe captivity.","sonnet":"A speaker strains against control, constraint, and the people who have broken and defined them, committing to the risk of breaking free no matter the cost. The gain is not guaranteed, but remaining bound is no longer possible.","src":"haiku"},
"alter-bridge~waters-rising":{"haiku":"Environmental and social collapse occur as fields burn and water rises to drown humanity, the consequence of pride and cycles of tyranny that have sealed humanity's fate.","opus":"A vision of self-made apocalypse, crops ablaze and floods swelling to swallow humanity while rival powers destroy one another, the narrator begging not to be pulled under. Alter Bridge indulge their taste for end-times grandeur, framing the catastrophe as deserved, a species handed the tools to torch its own world before the deluge cleanses it.","sonnet":"A reckoning arrives: a tyrant's legacy collapses as fields burn and floodwaters swell. Humanity, complicit in its own destruction through cycles of pride and rivalry, faces an apocalyptic cleansing — the song a drowning cry from those who finally see they are lost.","src":"haiku"},
"altermind~illumina":{"haiku":"Breaking free from cycles and fate through destruction and recreation. The speaker advocates severing constraints and rewriting history despite the disorienting spiral of endless repetition.","opus":"A mourner vows to change course, having watched someone fade before him, yet swearing to fight back through pain toward reunion. The song churns through labyrinths, turning fate, and marionette imagery before declaring nothing lasts, urging listeners to cut destiny's threads and light their own path, a progressive-metal reverie on rewriting the past to break free.","sonnet":"Grief and rupture become the engine of transformation. After losing someone — or losing himself — a figure commits to breaking cycles, rewriting fate, and illuminating a new path forward, driven by the belief that suffering is inseparable from genuine change.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~a-d-d":{"haiku":"Cognitive collapse and drowning sensations overwhelm as the speaker fragments under accumulated stress and invisible psychiatric warfare.","opus":"Drowning under the weight of good intentions and an overloaded mind, gasping for control as the noise and violence of one's own head pulls the self under.","sonnet":"About cognitive overwhelm and the sensation of losing your grip on your own mind — the speaker is drowning in mental noise, physically exhausted, unable to focus or keep their head above water, and screaming for help while sinking.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~abeyance":{"haiku":"Suspended existence in liminal uncertainty; the speaker floats between presence and absence, unable to choose between staying and leaving.","opus":"A terrifyingly frank account of suicidal ideation, the speaker feeling defective and hollowed, trapped between not wanting to die and being unable to stay. The stale air and the fear of nothingness on the other side render the impasse of wanting relief while dreading what lies beyond.","sonnet":"Suspended between not wanting to die and being unable to stay — the air gone stale, the self feeling defective and unbelonging — the speaker floats in abeyance, asking whether to break or fly while knowing they cannot last forever in this state.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~apep":{"haiku":"Silence and poisoned words wound fatally; the speaker vows vengeance and will not survive the aftermath of betrayal.","opus":"Named for the serpent-demon of chaos, this is a vow to strike back at a poisoner by cutting the head off the snake before it strikes again. The venom in the veins turns victim into executioner, one grave dug for two.","sonnet":"A venomous confrontation with a poisonous person — named after the Egyptian serpent of chaos — in which the narrator, though paralyzed and near the end, vows to bite back, cut off the snake's head, and bury them together before they can strike again.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~back-to-earth":{"haiku":"Ambition crashes into limitation; the speaker refuses standard success to pursue authentic experience while remaining grounded.","opus":"A restless hunger to get high, move a crowd and climb without any rockstar pretensions — just chasing a full life and a fat payday. He walks off his day job to make music, takes his shot, and comes crashing down without ever really leveling out.","sonnet":"A pure dancefloor-energy song with minimal narrative — the speaker lights the engines, crashes back down to earth, and just wants to make everyone move and do whatever they came to do; the theme is release into physical momentum rather than any personal story.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~chasing-safety":{"haiku":"One person offers rescue to another trapped in emotional struggle and despair. The speaker promises help breaking free from internal chains and inner conflict that feels unwinnable.","opus":"A would-be rescuer speaks to someone physically and emotionally collapsing, refusing to let them drift toward destruction and promising to haul them back to safety. Cast in metalcore's language of bondage and distress signals, the song frames devotion as rescue work, the narrator racing to save a person who has abandoned a fight they can't survive alone.","sonnet":"A desperate rescue plea — one person watching another give up on life, extinguished like a candle in wind. The speaker throws himself into the breach, promising to pull the drowning one back to shore, demanding only to know where to run.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~devil-s-cut":{"haiku":"Cycles of hurt and apology poison intimacy; the speaker recognizes their partner's demons but cannot save them.","opus":"The exhaustion of loving an addict, worn down by cycles of harm and hollow apologies; the narrator finally names the demons as not theirs to fight and walks away, hoping the other person finds at the bottom of the bottle whatever they needed.","sonnet":"The end of a relationship with someone whose inner demons and cycles of hurt made them impossible to love safely — the speaker absorbs the damage for too long, then cuts the person off, naming their toxicity clearly and wishing them well from a distance.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~friendinthedark":{"haiku":"Psychological predation operates in invisibility; the speaker warns of a friend-shaped monster who tears from within the dark.","opus":"A predator-and-prey nightmare where intimacy is the trap, the friend beside you in the dark the one who kills you. The narrator confesses to relishing the violence, so the song becomes about the thrill of betrayal and the hunger for power over another.","sonnet":"A horror-register song in which the friend in the dark turns out to be the speaker themselves — they twist the knife, feed on the power, and leave the other person bloodied — so the initial warning about not trusting what you can't see is a confession as much as a threat.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~insubordinate":{"haiku":"Revolution against hierarchy; the speaker buries the arrogant elite and watches blood run from power to weakness as hierarchy collapses.","opus":"A vengeance built on the refusal to strike until the target is fully awake to it: not murder in their sleep but a reckoning they'll feel. Toppling someone from their pedestal becomes the price of forging a new life on the other side.","sonnet":"A song of violent contempt directed at someone on a pedestal — the narrator wants to drag this person down, bury them, and watch blood run from the powerful to the weak, with barely restrained fury at someone whose pride and lies are insufferable.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~j-breezeblocks":{"haiku":"Obsessive love and suffocation where holding requires paralyzing weight and control. Desire becomes trap where escape means drowning or being drowned together.","opus":"An obsessive, possessive love so intense it turns menacing, borrowing Where the Wild Things Are to dramatize the urge to hold someone down so they can't leave; devotion and threat blur until wanting to consume her whole reads as both adoration and captivity.","sonnet":"A violent, obsessive love song built around an act of drowning: the speaker pins someone down with soggy clothes and breezeblocks, the tender \"please don't go / I'll eat you whole\" repeated until possession and destruction become indistinguishable — love as suffocating restraint.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~parasite":{"haiku":"One-sided loyalty shatters when the parasitic other demands reciprocation they never provided; the speaker cuts ties permanently.","opus":"Betrayal by someone who vanished the moment they were needed, unmasked as spineless and sanctimonious. They drained the narrator and left him a frayed wreck — the verdict is total: never wanting to lay eyes on them again.","sonnet":"A furious severance from someone who took everything and offered nothing back — the narrator supported them, bled for them, and was left a nervous wreck while the other person was too self-righteous to bail them out.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~pariah":{"haiku":"Someone is consumed by obsession and loss, forever marked by another person, spiraling into isolation and despair as heaven abandons them and hell stalks them.","opus":"Grief and obsession fused so tightly the narrator can't tell heaven from a grave. Someone is inked permanently into him, haunting from both the sacred and the damned side, until leaving and leaping blur into the same impossible escape.","sonnet":"A person destroyed by grief or loss — someone is tattooed into their skin, heaven has abandoned them inside another's grave, and the only escape they can imagine is to leap.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~the-great-depression":{"haiku":"Systemic illness and internal hemorrhage drain the speaker toward cessation; heaven remains inaccessible while breathing itself becomes agony and the sin accumulated poisons soul.","opus":"Depression rendered as a terminal illness and a slow starvation of the self, the narrator begging to be released to heaven rather than keep circling in the pain. Its weight comes from insisting the suffering is shared by millions dying the same way.","sonnet":"A hollow, forsaken speaker whose soul is drying out and whose skin is being pulled apart by something like a disease asks heaven to take them — \"a million fucking people die of this\" widens the private suffering into epidemic — finding no exit except the hope that heaven is real.","src":"haiku"},
"alt~wraith":{"haiku":"Floating toward oblivion as escape: the wraith calls, and departure feels gentler than staying to fight systems that weaponize faith itself.","opus":"Naming fear as a god behind a mask and drifting free of a manipulative, exploitative world of hate. The wraith calling your name is the pull of escape from lies dressed up as authority.","sonnet":"Someone overwhelmed by a world built on manipulation and fear — where authority plants seeds of hatred and exploits the weak — feels themselves detaching and floating toward death or disappearance, identifying the wraith calling them away as the only escape from the hatred around them.","src":"haiku"},
"alunageorge-feat-cautious-clay~superior-emotion":{"haiku":"Intoxication through emotional and physical attachment despite awareness of dependency. The stronger feeling persists despite knowledge that it remains unneeded and destructive.","opus":"The push-pull of craving intense feeling you know is bad for you, chasing that superior high of devotion despite calmer stretches; self-awareness that the very thing you want is the thing that makes you weak.","sonnet":"A self-aware cycle of wanting intensity that is known to be destructive: the speaker admits their evolutionary wiring gives them skills they don't need, chases a \"superior emotion\" that makes them weak, and oscillates between being cool for a while and craving the next high.","src":"haiku"},
"alura~counterfeit":{"haiku":"Self-aware corruption and performed authenticity collapse together; the speaker acknowledges their pathological fakeness while remaining trapped in the cycle.","opus":"A confession of manufactured perfection and self-fixation, the narrator worshipping an invented flawless existence while quietly wondering if they can bear anything genuine. Keeping up the act until death arrives is offered as both a swagger and a snare.","sonnet":"Self-disgust at one's own inauthenticity — the narrator confesses to being a fake, a sociopath who cannot trust anyone, who has the perfect life but feels hollow, and who expects to keep performing the counterfeit self until death.","src":"haiku"},
"alura~lowlands":{"haiku":"Isolation and desperation where loneliness becomes deafening within collective suspension. The narrator pleads for distance while needing presence, caught between contradictory needs.","opus":"Written from pandemic isolation, the ache of a stalled world where loneliness rings loudest and the person you need most is the one you have to beg to keep away, longing and dread held together only by the hope of making it through.","sonnet":"Pandemic-era isolation — begging the beloved to stay away while desperately missing them, standing in a world at a standstill and ruins, bargaining with the devil just to keep thinking of the person who cannot safely be near.","src":"haiku"},
"alyans~a-1fraxdm":{"haiku":"Spiritual summoning at dawn—celestial voices call toward transcendence; meditation on love, destiny, and cosmic connection as sole unchanging value.","opus":"In the lyrical Soviet-descended pop tradition, this is a gentle song of awakening as dawn calls the speaker upward. Moonlight, May rain, star-glow, and the sound of one's own heartbeat all converge at daybreak, when voices from the sky summon the narrator. There is no conflict to resolve, only a spiritual and natural rising, with cosmic connection held out as the one value that does not change while everything else passes.","sonnet":"A simple, lyrical Russian song about the beauty of dawn calling the speaker upward: moonlight, May rain, star-glow, and the sound of one's own heartbeat all converge at the break of day when voices from the sky summon the narrator — a song of spiritual or natural awakening with no specific narrative conflict.","src":"haiku"},
"amaarae~angels-in-tibet":{"haiku":"Sensual indulgence and divine worship converge as the speaker claims godhood through bodies and ritual. Angels emerge from desire, and paradise dwells in the space of mutual satisfaction.","opus":"Amaarae turning sex, wealth and pleasure into a rite of self-worship on the club floor, the language of luxury and devotion pointed at desire itself. It is about claiming power through appetite, treating the body as both altar and offering.","sonnet":"A high-gloss scene of desire and luxury — Dior, diamonds, Louvre, the club — frames a fluid erotic confidence where the speaker directs, enjoys, and commands; 'like angels in Tibet' pairs the sacred and the sensual, and the closing 'step into your power, come home to your God' turns seduction into a kind of spiritual summoning.","src":"haiku"},
"amaarae~reckless-sweet":{"haiku":"Spiritual blessing and excess where wealth and status breed indulgence and confidence. The narrator celebrates freedom through money while others remain bound to mortality.","opus":"A glossy, spiritual flex on wealth and desirability where money is both blessing and shield, and a lover's sudden interest, arriving only when the cash does, exposes their two-toned intentions; she runs to the money, not the fakery.","sonnet":"Confidence and financial self-sufficiency as erotic capital: the speaker radiates God-glorifying energy, dismisses a two-faced lover who coveted her money more than her, and issues a standing instruction — call when the money comes, because she is already running toward it.","src":"haiku"},
"amarionette~why-can-t-this-be-love":{"haiku":"Wonders why intense attraction and overwhelming sensation can't constitute genuine love. Seeks certainty in an uncertain romantic connection.","opus":"A giddy jolt of new attraction, the narrator marveling that nothing he can remember compares to this while begging to understand why it cannot simply be the real thing. It's a cover of Van Halen's Sammy Hagar-era hit, and Amarionette rework the arena sparkle into their own style, keeping intact the breathless tug between caution and headlong infatuation.","sonnet":"New infatuation arriving with enough force to rewrite the rules — the speaker keeps trying to stay cautious (only fools rush in) but the feeling overwhelms the argument. The question the title asks is rhetorical, already answered by the rush in the chest. Every previous experience fades; this one blows them all away and demands to be named for what it clearly is.","src":"haiku"},
"amatory~a-1tzqkh7":{"haiku":"Intimacy as numbing from pain; bodies entwine in nocturnal illusion while genuine warmth remains absent.","opus":"Amatory's Russian metalcore trades in intimacy as anesthetic, two people poisoning each other with lies and passion, bodies intertwining in a night mirage that only paralyzes the pain of a dirty war where everyone is finally alone. The narrator invites the other to open a hidden world so the touch can kill the hurt while the bridges between them burn. Genuine warmth stays absent; the closeness is a numbing agent, not a cure.","sonnet":"A toxic sexual anesthetic — two people who poison each other with lies and passion, bodies intertwining in a night mirage, paralysing the pain of a dirty war where everyone is alone; the narrator invites the other to open their hidden world so the touch can kill the hurt while the bridges between them burn.","src":"haiku"},
"amatory~a-36hy7k":{"haiku":"Violent imagery and mental anguish; brain, poison, or blood dominate the disturbing landscape.","opus":"The same band turns to bravado, casting the narrator as a criminal captain on a black regatta, vicious since childhood and negative as metastasis, fighting rivals with bullets in place of words and assembling a crew of demons. He dares anyone to cross him, yet the armor has a seam: privately he cannot kill the pain beneath it. The menace is real, but the confession that leaks through is what the swagger exists to hide.","sonnet":"A self-styled criminal captain on a black regatta — vicious from childhood, negative as metastasis, fighting rivals in a rap context with bullets instead of words, assembling a crew of demons, and daring anyone to cross him while privately unable to kill the pain beneath the armor.","src":"haiku"},
"amazarashi~word-processor":{"haiku":"Artistic defiance and persistent voice despite silencing and discouragement. Words typed into machine outlast intended erasure, becoming stone that penetrates despair.","opus":"A writer's defiant creed from Hiroyama Akita, framing words as a lone armed uprising against suburban deadness and despair; he sings the song he was told not to sing, betting his sentences are seeds that will one day split even rock-hard hopelessness.","sonnet":"A Japanese poet-figure declares linguistic insurrection from a shuttered suburban wasteland: singing the songs they were told not to sing, screaming the words they were told not to speak, claiming their rage and defeat-tears as hope — every discarded line typed into a word processor becoming a seed that will eventually pierce even stone-like despair.","src":"haiku"},
"ambleside~still-life":{"haiku":"Diving deeper into escape and loss of control, the speaker confronts disillusionment, broken enchantments, and the risk of capsizing under the weight of hidden darkness.","opus":"Metalcore about numb detachment and being taken for granted, a plea to break the spell and wake up before you sink. The still life is emotional paralysis dressed as calm.","sonnet":"About someone diving deeper into self-deception or escapism rather than confronting reality — warned that fantasizing and going deeper will capsize them, the song urges breaking the enchantment before it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"amboog-a-lard~a-matter-of-honor":{"haiku":"A child questions whether patriotism feeds hunger, while someone rises from discontent to overthrow a rotten system. Honor, pride, and revolution matter more than complicity.","opus":"A protest against hollow patriotism, contrasting a flag-waving boy and a weeping mother with the real needs that empty symbolism can never meet. The core is the revolt of the disillusioned, cast as a cog jamming the machine, born of festering discontent and weighing personal honor against a corrupt world that would sooner see him dead than free.","sonnet":"Disillusionment with patriotism and the systems that demand sacrifice from those who can't afford it — a boy who might wave a flag, a mother who can't be fed by symbols; the narrator is a slave to the machine but claims personal honor and pride as the only things actually worth defending.","src":"haiku"},
"amelia-arsenic~architects-of-death-remix":{"haiku":"Excess-driven youth deliberately plan and accelerate their own destruction through recklessness and self-sabotage.","opus":"A 6am afterparty of drunken excess and self-designed demise fuels a defiant creed of all-or-nothing, London-style wrecking, and reveling in one's own defects. Amelia Arsenic, the Australian industrial and electro-goth performer once of Angelspit, styles hedonistic self-destruction as identity here, the architects of death planning their end while burning sky-high to the last breath.","sonnet":"A London night-culture manifesto celebrating those who push past every limit — partying into early morning, living recklessly, designing their own destruction with full awareness. Excess is not weakness but identity; the architects choose their end as the only authentic form of self-determination.","src":"haiku"},
"american-dream-machine~dead":{"haiku":"A person spirals into addiction and paranoia, taking destructively until they're dead inside or actually dead, trapped by compulsion they cannot break.","opus":"Addiction rendered as drowning in water barely past the ankles, tyrant and casualty being one and the same vanishing figure. The mechanism is a grasping, endless compulsion that swaps affection and faith for ash, the only open question being whether it stops before the grave.","sonnet":"A song about addiction dragging someone toward death — trading love and trust for the drug, taking until there's nothing left, paranoia cycling back around until the question becomes whether it ever ends before it kills you.","src":"haiku"},
"american-dream-machine~dopamine":{"haiku":"Dopamine-chasing leaves nothing but emptiness; trapped in cycles of reaching without ever arriving.","opus":"Insomnia and burnout as a closed loop, the mind narrating its own limits and insisting there is no further left to go. The ache is the sense of fading while chasing a feeling that keeps escaping, self-worth eroded by exhaustion.","sonnet":"Anhedonia as clockwork — lying awake chained to the minutes, repeating a catch and release, the internal voice saying this is as far as you go — the speaker names the target directly: trying to un-erase the dopamine, to restore the neurological baseline that would make any of the waiting feel worth it.","src":"haiku"},
"american-football~honestly":{"haiku":"Adolescent intensity leaves no permanent mark; the narrator questions whether youthful feelings held authentic meaning or were merely nostalgic constructs.","opus":"Emo's patron saints of nostalgia probing how memory rewrites itself, teenage feelings gone see-through and unreliable. The unease is over an anticipated kiss and the sense that the past keeps getting quietly revised.","sonnet":"A speaker tries to look back at their teenage emotions but finds them distant and unreal — the people are there in memory but the feelings behind them have gone opaque, and the only honest answer about the past is that it's been rewritten into something unrecognisable.","src":"haiku"},
"american-nightmare~life-support":{"haiku":"A person trapped in dependence, exploited and kept artificially alive by systems that extract value from them while blocking their freedom. They reject this control and demand release.","opus":"A hardcore reckoning with being kept only half-alive by the same forces that are also killing you, undervalued as you mature, held upright and torn down by identical hands. Caught between being too green to die and too spent to really live, it craves escape from that numb limbo more than any actual rescue.","sonnet":"A person kept artificially alive by systems that exploit and diminish them — they want off the life support, tired of being propped up only to be knocked back down, and the whole world feels like a cold war they're losing.","src":"haiku"},
"american-nightmare~we-are":{"haiku":"Dead flowers and pocket change mark a generation of fading youth. Beauty lives in depression and heartbreak—longing replaces hope.","opus":"A hardcore elegy for depressed, romantic misfits who feel invisible, feeding change into dead payphones just to hear their own voices, a soundtrack to fading out and saying goodbye, sure that love is finished.","sonnet":"A collective portrait of depressed, disconnected youth — dead flowers and pocket change, people who live in headphones and write goodbye notes, who were born to fade and whose love is already dead, narrated as the tragic soundtrack of an entire generation.","src":"haiku"},
"amira-elfeky~crossed-the-line":{"haiku":"Regret for words that damage irreparably, questioning whether love can survive transgression.","opus":"A short, raw admission of words that can't be taken back and love that isn't returned. Beneath the heaviness sits the wounded question of whether to keep caring for someone who doesn't want you.","sonnet":"A spare, fractured confrontation: the speaker cannot take back what they have said, pushes the other through pain, and asks whether they have crossed the line — the other's refusal to want or love them hangs as the unresolved wound.","src":"haiku"},
"amira-elfeky~fatal-attraction":{"haiku":"Dangerous seduction and self-aware destruction where passion becomes sin and prison. The narrator surrenders to darkness, knowing the game proves fatal.","opus":"Knowingly chasing a toxic, hot-and-cold lover whose danger is the whole appeal; the singer names the love a sin and a drug yet keeps falling deeper, seduced precisely by the pain and the games.","sonnet":"A love that operates like a drug dependency: the less given the more wanted, the danger and the allure inseparable, the speaker seduced by passion they know is unsafe — described as a love so dark it's a sin pulling them in against their better judgment.","src":"haiku"},
"amira-elfeky~hold-onto-me":{"haiku":"Two bodies spiral in forbidden desire and emotional dependency, seeking escape through physical connection while doubting their own capacity to sustain it.","opus":"A doomed, forbidden pull between two people who keep failing and returning, the narrator offering to take control and turn shared grief into something survivable. It hovers between real love and the fear of admitting it, so the plea to hold on is also a fear of being let go.","sonnet":"Two people are caught in a forbidden, emotionally destructive affair — desire coiled with shame and dishonesty — as one partner tries to hold the other together while privately asking whether love or fear is what drives them.","src":"haiku"},
"amira-elfeky~remains-of-us":{"haiku":"Devastation and loss leave only fragments; the speaker mourns what remains after destruction.","opus":"Loving what can never be, clinging to the ashes of a relationship that has already burned down. The tenderness is elegiac, a vow to hold whatever is left even as it rots, devotion tangled with mutual self-loathing.","sonnet":"A love that persists even in mutual self-hatred — they each despise parts of themselves (\"you said you hate who you are / I only hate who I'm not\") but choose to hold on to the remains of the relationship through flames. The sail-away imagery and the dream visitation frame the connection as something that transcends waking reality.","src":"haiku"},
"amira-elfeky~take-me-under":{"haiku":"Masochistic submission and learned pain-bonding where suffering becomes proof of belonging. The lover breaks repeatedly until the narrator craves the breaking.","opus":"An unflinching account of loving someone who hurts you until you crave the hurt, staying because they break you, mistaking being pulled under for feeling right; the ugly logic of trauma-bonded desire.","sonnet":"Masochistic attachment to a partner who causes deliberate pain: the speaker drinks the poison, allows themselves to be broken and held down, and admits they have been trained to \"like the pain\" — a co-dependent dynamic where suffering has become the condition for staying.","src":"haiku"},
"amira-elfeky~tonight-demo":{"haiku":"Love resurrects the dying; the speaker's pain dissolves in another's presence, but departure triggers terror at the prospect of survival.","opus":"An intimate plea to a lover who's eased her pain and seen her grief, unable to bear watching them go. She shuts her eyes to keep them present, holding their closeness with the desperate wish that even death couldn't part them, that they'd linger with her as a ghost.","sonnet":"A tender address to someone whose smile is a breath of air and whose presence has replaced the haunting memories — the speaker has watched light leave eyes and wiped tears and cannot bear to watch them leave now. Thousandth kiss, and still I need you tonight.","src":"haiku"},
"amira-elfeky~will-you-love-me-when-i-m-dead":{"haiku":"Self-annihilation through love becomes the speaker's goal, where breaking and crucifixion are acts of devotion. Death promises transformation into eternal light.","opus":"Self-erasure disguised as romance: a narrator who can only feel loved by being hurt, bleeding herself dry for someone she will never satisfy. The title question exposes the real fear, that only in death might she finally be enough.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses a consuming, destructive lover with masochistic devotion — crucified in your bed, roses that are all that's left, begging to be hurt more — and the central question 'will you love me when I'm dead?' frames the relationship as one already heading toward erasure, the speaker seeking eternal love as the only kind that might finally be enough.","src":"haiku"},
"amon-amarth~crack-the-sky":{"haiku":"Thor's mythic force tears through reality with devastating power; thunder and lightning announce unstoppable destruction riding toward inevitable conflict.","opus":"A thunderous hymn to Thor riding out from Asgard in his goat-drawn chariot to war, hurling his hammer to split the blackened skies. It's Norse myth as pure battle spectacle, the champion of Midgard letting Mjolnir fly.","sonnet":"Thor rides out from Asgard in a golden chariot pulled by two goats, thunder cracking across Midgard, Mjolnir raised against countless foes — a straightforward, exultant retelling of the god's warlike character, beseeching him to let the hammer fly and split the blackened sky.","src":"haiku"},
"amon-amarth~guardians-of-asgaard":{"haiku":"Warriors defend Asgaard against attacking giants, standing firm through eternal conflict and refusing to let the realm's walls fall.","opus":"A Norse-myth war anthem in which northern warriors, bound by the Allfather's own bloodline, stand as sleepless defenders of the gods' home against the giants forever battering its gates. Across ages of ceaseless war they refuse to bow and swear the divine stronghold will stand no matter the assault, Amon Amarth's melodic death metal at its most rousing.","sonnet":"A Viking metal anthem about the Norse warriors sworn to defend Asgaard against giants — a thousand years of relentless assault, brotherhood in battle, and an unbreakable vow that the realm will never fall.","src":"haiku"},
"amon-amarth~raven-s-flight":{"haiku":"Viking warriors sail toward conquest and vengeance with divine protection, charging into battle united under Odin's guidance with no fear of death.","opus":"The sons of Ragnar sailing west to Northumbria to avenge their father and topple King Aella, facing death fearlessly under Odin so long as the ravens fly beside them.","sonnet":"Three hundred Viking ships head west at dawn to avenge a father and seize a throne, their faith placed in Odin's ravens overhead as a sign of immortality in battle; Aella's head will fall and they will never die as long as they stand side by side.","src":"haiku"},
"amon-amarth~where-is-your-god":{"haiku":"Faith crumbles under violence; the divine abandons the faithful while brutal force triumphs unopposed.","opus":"The Swedish Viking-metal band stage a raid from the raiders' side, taunting a victim whose prayers meet only silence. Under the carnage is a bleak thesis about faith failing at the moment of slaughter.","sonnet":"A Viking raid described in brutal, exultant detail — the thundering attack, the slaughter, the burning country, the survivors left only with pain — punctuated by a taunt directed at the defeated: where was your god when this was happening? The absence of divine intervention is framed as proof of its own argument.","src":"haiku"},
"amoura~pathlines":{"haiku":"Divergent paths prevent reunion; the speaker grieves roads not taken together while acknowledging some breaks cannot be repaired.","opus":"Standing at the close of a relationship, wondering whether another route might have saved it and daring the other to confess they never cared at all. Cries flung into an empty room and a sense of nowhere left to turn make it a duet with finality, one partner bending while the other snaps under the same collapse.","sonnet":"Standing at the end of something irreparable, the speaker wonders whether different choices — different pathlines — could have saved it, while the other person denies all feeling and screams echo in the void with nowhere left to go.","src":"haiku"},
"amy-winehouse~rehab":{"haiku":"Defiant resistance to intervention, prioritizing personal agency and pleasure over wellness despite loved ones' intervention and concern.","opus":"Winehouse turns her refusal of treatment into a swaggering hook, but the bravado is shot through with grief and dependence she can't name aloud. Tragically prophetic, it makes deflection sound like strength while betraying how lost she already was.","sonnet":"A stubborn refusal to be institutionalized or fixed: the speaker insists she has her own relationship with pain and music, doesn't need therapy, and sees rehab as a performance of recovery she won't participate in — not because she's in denial but because she's protecting something private and genuine inside herself.","src":"haiku"},
"ana-eclipse~killmeslow":{"haiku":"Gradual self-annihilation through depression and disconnection where survival feels like failure. The narrator drags others down while dissolving into hollow emptiness.","opus":"A raw depiction of depression at the edge, barely breathing and beyond saving, warning that they'll drag others down with them; the slow kill is both the illness and the plea to stop before it finishes the job.","sonnet":"Dissociation and depression rendered as slow erasure: too close to the edge, wallow in the rain, a shell setting their own soul on fire — the speaker warns those who knew them that they will be dragged down too, while navigating a cold world desperately seeking any antidote before it kills them slowly.","src":"haiku"},
"ana-eclipse~unholy":{"haiku":"Violation and control through another's selfishness that corrupts and taints identity. The narrator seeks escape through distance but remains spiritually defiled.","opus":"Breaking loose from a controlling, selfish partner who put the narrator through misery and left them feeling worthless. The real battle is against their own itch to grant another chance, as much as against the person who kept exploiting it.","sonnet":"A person addressing someone who used control and manipulation to make them feel corrupted and worthless — \"you made me feel unholy\" — resists returning for more, pleading with themselves not to fall for it again and not to be saved by the very person who broke them.","src":"haiku"},
"anathema~can-t-let-go":{"haiku":"The speaker cannot release their attachment to an absent or dead loved one; they return to the same road repeatedly, searching for divine presence.","opus":"Anathema circle an inability to release someone, the narrator returning down the same road again and again, questioning whether love is even present or reachable. Repetition enacts the compulsion the words describe. Emerging from the British band's shift away from doom toward emotive atmospheric rock, it makes obsessive longing feel like an unending descent that never resolves.","sonnet":"A person trapped in an attachment that will not release — possibly to love itself, possibly to a specific absent person — descends the same road of longing again and again. The repetition is the point: the inability to stop is both the subject and the form.","src":"haiku"},
"anathema~close-your-eyes":{"haiku":"Escape through sleep and dreaming when reality becomes unbearable; closing eyes offers refuge in internal worlds.","opus":"A tender lullaby urging a distressed listener to surrender to sleep and drift onward through uncertainty and hard choices, reassured that everything they require already lives within them and that fear is merely a nightmare to rest away. Anathema, grown from British doom metal into lush atmospheric rock, trade heaviness here for near-ambient, whispered comfort.","sonnet":"A lullaby for someone lost in anxiety about which way to go — the answer offered is to close your eyes, go inward, and trust that everything needed is already inside. The dark roads are acknowledged but gentled. What seems frightening is reframed as just a dream; the light you find when you look inside yourself is the only direction worth following.","src":"haiku"},
"anathema~endless-ways":{"haiku":"Love sustains someone through endless hardship; though they cannot know the future, their devotion to another transforms all uncertainty into meaning.","opus":"Anathema offer gentle reassurance to cling to life because one is cherished beyond measure, even as the singer admits he cannot merely become a keepsake of the past. Anxiety about what lies ahead dissolves into a dream taking shape. The British band's turn toward luminous emotional rock steeps the comfort in both fragility and quiet, stubborn hope.","sonnet":"An urgent plea to someone on the edge of leaving — or leaving life altogether — to hold on, because they are loved in ways that cannot be counted. The speaker cannot be only a memory for them; the dream being created requires them to remain.","src":"haiku"},
"anathema~ghosts":{"haiku":"Optical illusion and nocturnal exhaustion create confusion; the figure keeps reappearing as trick of tired light.","opus":"Anathema reduce a haunting to scattered fragments, a narrator half-seeing a lost figure in flickers and afterimages, worn out and disoriented, begging the world to ease off. Long removed from their doom-metal roots, the band swap riffs for atmospheric ache, the repeated glimpses suggesting grief that keeps confusing absence for presence.","sonnet":"A fleeting apparition of someone lost — glimpsed in a trick of light, a star, a tired moment of confusion — leaves a figure shaken and hollow. The repeated sighting resolves into nothing but the ache of being reminded of someone no longer there.","src":"haiku"},
"anathema~leaving-it-behind":{"haiku":"A narrator releases internal suffering and resignation, refusing to accept deadness or complacency, and urges others to shed lies and internal rot. The refrain frames departure from pain as both personal and collective act.","opus":"An urgent push to release everything held within and abandon the past this very night, hoping that doing so will end a numb, hollow deadness and set things right again. It is essentially a plea for a clean break as the road to feeling alive.","sonnet":"A declaration of intent to shed whatever has been causing numbness or stagnation inside, refusing satisfaction with the status quo and choosing to move on rather than stay dead inside.","src":"haiku"},
"anathema~san-francisco":{"haiku":"Rain saturates the city as every inhabitant becomes stranger wrapped in fog and isolation. Gold-bought love contrasts with love so precious it transcends commerce.","opus":"Sung in Japanese, a solitary figure walks the rain-soaked streets of a city where everyone is a stranger; amid car headlights, patrol sirens, and the red lanterns of Chinatown, he reflects that some love can be bought with money while other love can't be bought even at the cost of one's life, and that night's mist lets one shed the mask of lies.","sonnet":"Walking alone in the rain through a city of foreigners: everyone here is an outsider, cars' headlights bounce off wet streets, and even love can be bought — but the love you can't purchase, the mask that slips in night fog, the wound dressed with a love-medicine you can buy in this strange neon Chinatown — it all points to disconnection and the search for something real in an alien city.","src":"haiku"},
"anathema~springfield":{"haiku":"Displacement and alienation root deeply as the speaker remains fundamentally unmoored from place and belonging. No home exists; all geography becomes foreign shore.","sonnet":"Pure alienation in a single repeated question — the speaker cannot account for how they arrived in a place where they do not belong, and the question loops without answer or resolution. There is no arc, only the sensation of wrongness and displacement, held in four lines that say everything by refusing to explain anything.","src":"haiku"},
"anathema~the-optimist":{"haiku":"Someone drives through night seeking escape from the life they've lived while sleeping, desperately running before time runs out. The past is something to outrun, not confront.","opus":"Driving through the dark, a narrator plots a break from a life he has slept and daydreamed through rather than lived. Anathema, deep in their atmospheric period, treat motion as both flight and liberation, letting go of a shared bond and pressing on without a backward glance before the chance to escape disappears.","sonnet":"Two people flee the life they have been merely sleeping through, driving at night on an open road. They choose forward motion over looking back, shedding the past like a skin and running — together, at least for now — toward something undefined but their own.","src":"haiku"},
"anathema~wildfires":{"haiku":"An omniscient gaze observes from above while questioning identity; the moment of reckoning arrives too late to prevent or change anything.","opus":"A stark, circling contemplation of watchfulness and selfhood, an overseeing gaze pressing questions of identity, timing and cause until the narrator can only ask what he even is, then yields to a sense that the moment has already passed. Anathema, the British band who grew from doom into cinematic art-rock, build this from sparse fragments looping into quiet, unresolved dread.","src":"haiku"},
"anavae~high":{"haiku":"Spiritual numbness and existential drift compound into a paralysis where daily routines and fantasy both fail to elevate the speaker beyond desolation.","opus":"Numbness dressed as routine, a person cycling through the same days and failed escapes and never reaching the lift they crave. The refrain is an admission that nothing, not substances nor love, delivers the feeling of being alive.","sonnet":"A person trapped in routine and apathy craves transcendence they can't reach, spending money, avoiding others, and feeling imprisoned by fantasies that only deepen their disconnection rather than lifting them.","src":"haiku"},
"anavae~human":{"haiku":"A person desperate to understand humanity tears away their own skin and identity, becoming consumed by the dirty mess of human nature.","opus":"A disquieting study of alienation and the ache to belong, the narrator fantasizing about peeling off someone's skin to wear as a disguise so he might finally feel human. The deeper reading is a person so cut off from ordinary emotion that he decides humanity itself is a filthy sham, reaching for a blade to attempt fitting in once more.","sonnet":"About desperately wanting to feel human — the narrator describes wanting to literally wear another person's skin as a costume, not out of violence but out of envy for what it means to feel normal, eventually deciding that being human might just be a bad joke.","src":"haiku"},
"and-one~military-fashion-show":{"haiku":"Seduction and power intertwine in a ritualized performance where feminine beauty becomes armor and weapon in a game of domination.","opus":"German synthpop coating desire and jealousy in cold-war militarism, romance staged as a campaign of weapons and uniforms. The recurring lament that these girlfriends could never be his gives the strut a genuine sting of exclusion.","sonnet":"A man is fascinated by a woman who strips away civilian identity and dresses for battle — their brief encounter framed as a game of weapons and choices — love rendered as strategic manoeuvre with no room for a second day.","src":"haiku"},
"and-so-i-watch-you-from-afar~big-thinks-do-remarkable":{"haiku":"The sun dominates collective vision and blinds everyone equally—a shared condition of brightness overwhelming sight.","opus":"A single chanted line about the sun in our eyes, repeated until it becomes texture rather than statement, functioning more as a rhythmic incantation than a lyric. And So I Watch You From Afar are a Northern Irish instrumental post-rock band, and this is exactly how they use voice: as another percussive layer riding their propulsive, riff-driven builds rather than as narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"ando-san~dash-out":{"haiku":"Survival through aggression and detachment becomes the only strategy in a system designed to exploit. The speaker oscillates between threat and caution, keeping power while avoiding imprisonment.","opus":"A street-hardened boast about outlasting betrayal and staying composed to dodge prison, ditching false friends before they turn on him. He observes that struggle earns no reward once blood is spilled, and the refrain about staying real and out of trouble frames the bravado as a survival code.","sonnet":"A street survival bravado in the face of danger and near-violence — dashing out to avoid jail, maintaining composure while aware of the body-count logic around them, the speaker asserting dominance while barely keeping rage in check.","src":"haiku"},
"andr-s~poetry":{"haiku":"The speaker acknowledges their role as a villain in someone's narrative but asserts their ambition to become a hero and be remembered for stardom above love.","opus":"A melodic pop-rap narrator leans into his reputation as the bad guy in an ex-lover's writing, cheerfully confessing he'd sacrifice the relationship for stardom and shrugging off her relative's threats. The recurring hook flips into a boast: he's cast as the antagonist now but expects vindication once fame proves him right.","sonnet":"A self-aware confession from someone who knows they are the villain in the story someone else is writing about them — and who owns it, even weaponizes it. The ambition is stated plainly: fame matters more than the relationship, the person beside him is collateral to a larger destiny. The villain-now, hero-later logic is both ruthless and oddly honest about the cost of chasing something that big.","src":"haiku"},
"andra~the-rain-don-t-talk-to-me-no-more":{"haiku":"Nostalgia and dislocation where home becomes inaccessible and urban solitude intensifies. The narrator dances alone while rain ceases its former companionship.","opus":"Homesick and adrift in a strange city, dancing alone with an empty raincoat and asking God what she's even doing here; the ache of exile and missing a lover's steadying hands as the sky darkens and there's no going back.","sonnet":"Displacement and longing in an unfamiliar city: coffee stains, dancing alone in an empty blue raincoat, missing the strong hands of home — the rain no longer speaks, the sky turns black, and the speaker knows they cannot go back but cannot stop grieving what they left.","src":"haiku"},
"andrea-valle~lovergirl":{"haiku":"Someone seeks romantic connection in a cold world, hoping to find companionship and belonging with another person.","opus":"A flirtatious after-dark seduction where the narrator has been preoccupied with a crush all through the day, forgetting the hour under their charm, casting herself as the sweetheart bringing heat to a chilly, isolating world. This revisits Teena Marie's 1980s standard, and Andrea Valle preserves the come-on assurance and the stammering, hook-laden fixation, remaking the invitation into a hushed, contemporary temptation over a frosty groove.","sonnet":"Two people on the edge of something in a cold and lonely world — one offering herself as anchor, as lover, as the person who will make sure neither of them leaves alone. The feeling has been building all day, quietly obsessive, and the song is the moment it tips from thought into proposal. Simple and warm, no complications, just the hope that the other person is looking for the same thing.","src":"haiku"},
"andrew-w-k~i-love-nyc":{"haiku":"Anarchic corporate assault on urban consciousness; movement and domination erase distinction between love and violence.","opus":"A blunt, pounding love song to New York that doubles as a portrait of its ruthlessness, the city as an inescapable corporation that chews you up. The affection and the menace are the same thing, which is the joke and the truth.","sonnet":"New York City is addressed as a brutal, industrial, almost inhuman force — a corporation, a factory, a population — and the speaker's love for it is inseparable from its violence and endlessness. The city is mother, father, final friend, and the only thing that can't be stopped.","src":"haiku"},
"andrew-w-k~party-hard":{"haiku":"A celebration of living by one's own rules and resisting authority through relentless hedonistic defiance.","opus":"A pure, exuberant anthem of hedonistic release, refusing all rules and doing exactly what you want; the deeper move is defiant autonomy, whether you work or fight, the answer is to throw everything into partying hard as an act of freedom.","sonnet":"Exactly what it says — a manifesto for refusing rules and refusing to stop partying, framed as a kind of defiant freedom philosophy. The emotional core is the joy of collective refusal.","src":"haiku"},
"andrew-w-k~ready-to-die":{"haiku":"A violent group threatens death and domination, preparing to hunt and eliminate their targets.","opus":"A gleefully sinister anthem in which a collective voice takes lives as casually as a pastime, informing the listener their reckoning has arrived. The pounding hook urging everyone to brace for death, killing, and flight recasts dying as an inevitable, almost festive sport.","sonnet":"A gleefully aggressive battle cry aimed at an unnamed enemy, promising violent reckoning — the song thrives on its own theatrical menace rather than any specific grievance.","src":"haiku"},
"andromida~abandon-feat-daedric":{"haiku":"Drowning in cyclical despair where hope abandons but the struggle persists regardless. Pressure and anchoring weight become metaphors for inescapable internal crisis.","opus":"Drowning in a one-sided relationship where only the narrator is sinking, chasing ghosts and shadows of a person who feels nothing back; the crushing pressure of the water is named as gentler than the anchor of unrequited attachment on the heart.","sonnet":"A one-sided attachment where the speaker conjures phantom versions of someone who was never really present — spiralling into chaos riptides, grasping at shadows, abandoned in a crisis that only drowns them — until the pressure on their chest is gentle compared to the anchor their heart has become.","src":"haiku"},
"andromida~break-the-cycle-feat-beyond-unbroken":{"haiku":"Recursive patterns demand shattering; the speaker seeks liberation from repeating cycles of harm.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with mental collapse framed as reclaiming a life given away, the vow to break a self-perpetuating pattern of suffering. The tension is between drowning in unreality and the resolve to take control back where it started.","sonnet":"Mental breakdown followed by the determination to reclaim agency — trapped between darkness and reality, losing the life they gave away to someone or something else; the pivot is \"I'm taking back the life I gave you\" and \"I'm gonna break the cycle.\" Resistance is the only exit from the loop.","src":"haiku"},
"andromida~do-your-worst":{"haiku":"Mutual damage and readiness for final destruction where both parties have already broken. The narrator embraces the worst while knowing hell remains reciprocal choice.","opus":"A confession of being the one who did the damage, half-begging the wronged lover to inflict the punishment that would finally release him. Guilt becomes something he almost craves, so remorse curdles into a wish to be martyred.","sonnet":"A speaker confessing to having hurt and left someone asks to be broken open further — \"hold steady, I am ready to bleed\" — carrying a heavy conscience that rips through their mind, asking the person to do their worst and in doing so finally set them free.","src":"haiku"},
"andromida~hollow-heart":{"haiku":"Emotional emptiness and absence of capacity to feel, heart as void.","opus":"After a two-year relationship's collapse, the speaker feels their body is no longer home and wishes they'd never known the lover, whom they brand a serpent whose words can't be trusted; craving self-annihilation to stop feeling, they're left with only a hollow heart as proof of a hopeless endeavor.","sonnet":"Two years of a relationship that left nothing behind but a hollow heart — the body doesn't feel like home, the connection was a hopeless endeavor, and the person is called \"you filthy fucking animal\" whose serpent's tongue was never trustworthy. The wish that they'd never met closes every chorus.","src":"haiku"},
"andromida~no-bliss-in-ignorance":{"haiku":"Darkness becomes home; awareness of suffering offers no escape, only confirmation of inevitable decay.","opus":"Depression that has made the dark feel like home, rejecting the comfort of not-knowing because there is no relief in it. The stance is grim honesty: hope keeps failing, apathy keeps winning, and all that is left is the raw wish to feel alive.","sonnet":"Depression as a home you never chose — \"the dark, it feels like home / from the start, it's all I've ever come to know\"; the walls close in, memories eat the mind, and ignorance offers no relief. The one want underneath everything is simple and relentless: all I want is to feel alive.","src":"haiku"},
"andromida~real-feat-dropout-kings":{"haiku":"Struggle and adversity breeding resilience where darkness fuels emergence. The narrator claims authenticity through maintaining realness despite systemic pressure toward counterfeiting.","opus":"Underdog-to-survivor rap: the runt who was written off, told he'd be better off dead, turning every attempt to break him into fuel. The core claim is that pain is what makes you glow, so authenticity is worn as armor.","sonnet":"A resilience anthem from someone who was the runt of the litter: waking up a different person, fighting fear into might, becoming the torch-bearer the day hell froze, refusing to be a counterfeit — the core claim being that darkness is the only path to glow.","src":"haiku"},
"andromida~take-what-you-want":{"haiku":"Depression and emotional flatness masquerading as acceptable routine. The camera lies about happiness while the narrator repeats cycles of false brightness.","opus":"A cover of Daniel Powter's Bad Day, watching someone unravel through a rough patch with a tenderness that shades into detachment. It names the small humiliations of a life quietly going off the rails.","sonnet":"A rewrite of Daniel Powter's \"Bad Day\" — the coffee-to-go smile, falling to pieces, working through it with a ride and a sad song turned around — framing a bad day not as catastrophe but as the ordinary human cost of a life that goes slightly off-line.","src":"haiku"},
"andromida~the-rumbling":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic forces approach; the speaker oscillates between selfless intent and violent inevitability, haunted by unknowable transformation and the prospect of everything dissolving outside protective walls.","opus":"Built around Attack on Titan's apocalyptic march, this ventriloquizes a reluctant destroyer who only ever wanted to protect and understand, now driving annihilation. The pull is the tragedy of good intentions curdling into unstoppable catastrophe.","sonnet":"A cover of the Attack on Titan theme stripped to its existential core: a person who wanted only to do right things and save lives now finds themselves tearless, fearless, and burning — swept toward a war they never chose. The repeated question \"nobody knows what's inside of me\" underscores the cost of becoming the threat you were trying to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"andromida~these-memories":{"haiku":"Confrontation with accumulated trauma and fragmented identity through excavated grief. The narrator faces demons and past selves before healing becomes possible.","opus":"Metalcore about emotional numbness and wanting to be torn open just to feel something again. Two years of buried grief return as a haunting, and the real fight is against the self he used to be.","sonnet":"Emotional numbness after years of running from pain: the speaker wants to be torn apart so they can finally feel and heal, confronts past-two-years memories that will not release, identifies themselves as \"the walking dead,\" and at the end still begs to be ripped open rather than remain hollow.","src":"haiku"},
"andy-cizek~medicine":{"haiku":"Toxic relationships extract endless patience; the speaker breaks the cycle of self-sacrifice through calculated cruelty, mirroring dysfunction to force escape.","opus":"A clean break from someone toxic and self-absorbed, the narrator done absorbing damage in silence and finally handing back what he swallowed. The satisfaction is in refusing to stay the martyr while the other person poisons everyone around them.","sonnet":"A patience-exhausted indictment of someone who has dragged the narrator through the mud while the narrator bit their tongue and held their breath. The turn is deliberate: the narrator now mirrors the other's coldness back at them — \"watch me take the wheel like you, not feel like you\" — and closes with the image of life being brighter when certain people, like clouds, are gone.","src":"haiku"},
"andymori~sunrise-sunset":{"haiku":"Cyclical futility where desire, conflict, and loss remain perpetual constants. The narrator accepts endless repetition and witnesses the sun's indifferent rising and setting.","opus":"A Japanese indie meditation on the world's indifferent repetitions: liars don't die, conflict never stops, sorrow never clears. Against that grinding permanence he holds onto one small human wish, to just be near someone and name things together.","sonnet":"A Japanese indie song about the permanence of impermanence: every sunrise and sunset brings liars who don't die, conflicts that don't stop, desires that don't end, and sorrows that don't disappear — the speaker names flowers, sky-colour longing, and a summer sea that will vanish, all while waiting for a letter that still hasn't come.","src":"haiku"},
"ang-le~what-you-want-fcukers-remix":{"haiku":"Sexual desire and role-play flirt with power dynamics; the speaker demands silence and submission during physical intimacy.","opus":"A playful power-flip in the bedroom where the singer invites a lover to her feet and reverses the usual roles. She likes his talk in daily life but wants him silent under her touch, desire framed as command.","sonnet":"A woman in sexual control of a partner — she's tired of their little misbehaviors (lipstick everywhere), sets them at her feet, and flips dominant and submissive roles as she pleases, enjoying the power exchange on her own terms.","src":"haiku"},
"angel-number~fateful":{"haiku":"Guarded vulnerability and cautious hope emerge as the speaker finally opens after years of numbness, risking connection.","opus":"Fear of intimacy as self-imposed armor, over-shielding the heart while playing the half-sighted ruler in the kingdom of the sightless. A long search for love runs into the terror of not being enough, an unpolished gem wondering if it can be loved once two lives finally meet.","sonnet":"A guarded person who has spent their life searching for love finally feels exposed by it — they've protected their heart so hard it calcified, and now they're asking whether they'll be enough when love actually arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"angel-witch~angel-witch":{"haiku":"Someone obsesses over an elusive, distant figure they cannot reach or touch, despite being the only one who understands them, slowly unraveling into desperation.","opus":"NWOBHM obsession dressed as the supernatural, a man consumed by an unreachable woman he casts as bewitching, love curdling toward madness as the more he wants her the further she recedes.","sonnet":"A man is obsessed with a supernatural or idealised woman he can feel but never reach — the more he loves her, the further she fades, and he pleads with her to stop running, insisting he would never hurt her.","src":"haiku"},
"angels-airwaves~the-adventure":{"haiku":"Resurrection promises transformation; collective will to rebuild sustains hope through shared vulnerability.","opus":"Written as Tom DeLonge emerged from Blink-182's implosion, this is rebirth as manifesto, waking alive and pulling the hopeless back into daylight. The dependence in the refrain grounds the grandiosity in real need for someone beside you.","sonnet":"An exuberant invitation to embrace life as a shared adventure — reassuring whoever is listening that pain is temporary, renewal is possible, and the whole point is to begin together rather than waiting any longer.","src":"haiku"},
"anima-tempo~deceitful-idols":{"haiku":"Deceitful prophets lead civilization to slaughter; defenders emerge from abyss to reclaim sacred ground.","opus":"A prog-metal war epic about betrayal by false gods and idols, kingdoms falling to liars who strike under a flag of truce. Beneath the fantasy imagery it asks when the ruined fields will heal, and honors the last defenders who stand anyway.","sonnet":"A civilization betrayed by its own prophets and armored rulers — jade skin slammed from behind, promised land consumed, the ancient clan shattered by arrogance — with defenders rising from the abyss to face centaurus beasts and jaguar claws, asking when these fields will green again.","src":"haiku"},
"animal-fiction~explosions":{"haiku":"Walking through danger for money, desensitized to chaos, asking permission to detonate rather than reflect.","opus":"A swaggering mercenary pose, walking through danger unafraid because it's the life chosen, doing it all for the money. Bravado masking a hollowed-out heart worn on the sleeve and abandoned.","sonnet":"A soldier or thrill-seeker who has accepted that their chosen life means walking through danger for money — not afraid of being burned, committed to the path, moving through a field of literal or metaphorical explosions with bravado and grim purpose.","src":"haiku"},
"animaljam~repudiation-new-years-eve":{"haiku":"Mutual contempt and burning separation where fire consumes the bonds deliberately. Both lovers choose expiration together rather than endure continued fracturation.","opus":"A poisonous relationship dissected in ornate, self-lacerating vocabulary, the narrator both victim of and complicit in a manipulator's cruelty. The wish to sleep and pretend everything's fine becomes a wish to burn it all down together.","sonnet":"A relationship decaying in real time on New Year's Eve: narcissistic manipulation, hurtful words stabbing like a neck wound, fraying threads of a bond that was never repaired — the speaker oscillates between wanting to sleep through it and watching both of them burn alone in the pyre they lit together.","src":"haiku"},
"animaljam~sertraline":{"haiku":"Medication and self-medication mask underlying despair; the speaker floats between artificial wellness and the gnawing truth that pills cannot fix fundamental emptiness.","opus":"Antidepressants and self-diagnosis become a hollow crutch for someone starving for affection and drowning in dependency. It is about the bleak comfort of numbing out, and dysphoria so deep that watching the world burn feels preferable to living in it.","sonnet":"Self-diagnosis and emotional dependency spiral together: the speaker is a misanthrope starving for affection, drowning in isolation and medicated numbness, and the \"wonderful feeling\" of the chorus is bitterly ironic — the high of pharmaceutical escape that he never wants to release even as it debilitates him.","src":"haiku"},
"ankor~darkbeat":{"haiku":"Weightlessness and hopelessness where crossing broken bridges requires impossible strength. The narrator runs toward the other side, reconstructing meaning through motion despite futility.","opus":"A metalcore fall through hopelessness, the mind slipping loose while a driving pulse becomes the one thing keeping him alive. He runs toward an other side where broken promises are whole again, knowing the cage was his own making.","sonnet":"A speaker in freefall — weightless, restless, choking in tears — searches for the strength to cross a broken bridge to the other side where promises are still alive; the darkbeat pulses through veins like a drumline that washes pain away even as the world inverts and the heart's throne sits empty.","src":"haiku"},
"ankor~prisoner":{"haiku":"Self-imprisonment mirrors external terror—memories haunt while escape requires confronting the reflection that has become the warden.","opus":"Being trapped inside your own mind, the narrator both jailer and inmate, hunted by memories and a reflection turning against him. The dread is that the only way out runs back through the very thoughts destroying him.","sonnet":"Trapped inside a mirror of their own memories and mistakes, the speaker is their own jailer — 'I'm my own prisoner' — and the only route out is through the memories they fear most, with a reflection that keeps breaking in and time running out in a deep hole where nightmares live.","src":"haiku"},
"anna-luna~wake-me-up":{"haiku":"Watching someone fade while feeling powerless, consumed by darkness and haunted by their distance.","opus":"Longing and grief blurred into a half-dream, missing someone in pieces while begging to wake. Burning petals and watching from afar make loss feel like a haunting the narrator can't shake off.","sonnet":"A fragmentary song about obsessive grief or longing — the narrator is haunted by someone, watches them from afar, and is caught between missing them and feeling pain she can't name; she asks to be woken from what feels like a nightmare of emotion.","src":"haiku"},
"anna-sofia~i-can-t-fly":{"haiku":"Grounded inability and thwarted aspiration; the speaker is trapped by weight and limitation.","opus":"A blunt kiss-off to a self-absorbed partner who reduces her to a punching bag and refuses to see her. The defiance builds from being misnamed and dismissed into a refusal to accept anyone telling her what she can't do.","sonnet":"Cutting loose from someone who diminishes and mislabels her — he can't even look at her face, doesn't use her name (\"that's literally not my name\"), and feeds her lies that she can't do anything. The declaration \"I'd like to see you fucking try\" meets \"telling me I can't fly\" as a direct reversal: she's not the one who's stuck.","src":"haiku"},
"annihilator~21": {"haiku":"A high-stakes gamble with a dealer determines survival; the narrator desperately needs to win while facing escalating odds and the dealer's control.","opus":"Jeff Waters' Canadian thrash outfit turns a blackjack table into a life-or-death duel. The narrator, neck-deep in debt and unable to resist one more hand, prays to beat a dealer who keeps drawing against him. The stakes swell from money to survival, winning framed as the only alternative to death, until the final deal lands her twenty-one and the gambler's desperate faith collapses.","sonnet":"A high-stakes blackjack game doubles as a contest of nerve between a desperate gambler and a compelling dealer. With everything riding on each card, winning becomes a matter of survival — losing would be a kind of death — and the tension between the two players crackles like a live wire.","src":"haiku","fable":"A blackjack hand played out card by card, but the wager keeps inflating past the chips on the table until the man is bargaining with God to beat a smirking dealer, as if winning the round were the difference between living and dying. The tell is that the dealer is female and impossible to read, turning the game into a rigged seduction the narrator is certain he can outplay by sheer entitlement, chosen-one bravado that the last line coldly deflates: she turns over 21 and the house takes it all. It is a small, mean parable about the gambler's delusion, the conviction that need and want are the same as odds, and the house that was always going to take everything.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"gambler's entitlement mistakes need and want for odds","built":"the dealer turns 21 on the final line and the house takes everything"},
"annihilator~25-seconds": {"haiku":"The song depicts a real incident of police brutality and excessive force leading to death, told from the perspective of a victim who cannot speak and officials who refused to intervene. It documents how institutional failure and lack of due process result in a 25-second lethal encounter.","opus":"A furious protest song about a man electrocuted to death in custody after a bureaucratic ordeal, an airport, border officials, an airline, leaves him panicked and unable to make himself understood. The throughline is that officers made no effort to calm him before delivering lethal current and then no effort to save him, which the band brands a national shame. It loosely echoes the real Robert Dziekanski case.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of the 2007 death of Robert Dziekanski at Vancouver airport, where Polish immigrant, unable to communicate in English and disoriented after hours of waiting, was tasered multiple times by RCMP officers and died — all of it caught on video. The song counts down the 25 seconds of the confrontation and pins blame on police, security, the airline, and the immigration system.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a documentary indictment built around the death of Robert Dziekanski, the Polish traveler who died on a Vancouver airport floor in 2007 after four RCMP officers stunned him. Waters writes from inside the panic first: a man stranded past a language barrier, exhausted and unheard, breaking furniture because nobody will look at him. Then the verses turn coroner, tallying each party that could have intervened and did not, from security to customs to the carrier that abandoned him for hours. The title's countdown is the whole charge in miniature, the distance between a routine encounter and a stopped heart measured in the seconds it took to drop all restraint.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Waters has said he wrote this over the 2007 Robert Dziekański case — the Polish immigrant tasered five times by RCMP at Vancouver airport within about 25 seconds of officers arriving; the Braidwood inquiry later ruled the force unjustified.","themes":["politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"a preventable death measured in every party that could have intervened and did not","built":"the title countdown compresses the whole indictment into the seconds between restraint and a stopped heart"},
"annihilator~a-man-called-norhing": {"haiku":"Mental dissolution and invaded mind where internal voices conspire to pull the self into oblivion.","opus":"Thrash horror from inside a mind coming apart in confinement, voices insisting he join them as he claws the walls bloody. The dread is that the tormentor in the mirror is his own dissolving self.","sonnet":"About a person locked away — possibly institutionalized or imprisoned — who is hearing voices commanding him to join them, and refusing; the voices multiply, crawl over him, stare from the mirror, and he claws at the walls; the song describes psychotic breakdown from the inside while the subject maintains a fragile defiance.","src":"haiku","fable":"A prisoner of his own skull etches a portrait of an emptied man on the cell wall, begging captors he cannot see for release from a confinement that has no outside. Annihilator, Jeff Waters at his most narrative, stages the whole thing as a losing negotiation: plural voices chant an invitation to join them, and the singer swears refusal even as the boundary between mind and monster dissolves into nothing he can defend. The decisive turn arrives when the pronouns flip and the tormentors seize the microphone, no longer whispering from within but speaking as the self, so the mirror stares back and the surrounding room drains to black. What sounds like defiance is really surrender narrated from the wrong side, the final claim of resistance already spoken in the captor's own borrowed voice.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"defiance spoken from inside the captor's already borrowed voice","built":"tormentors seize the microphone mid-song so the self speaks as enemy"},
"annihilator~alison-hell": {"haiku":"A nightmare narrative where a child discovers a mysterious box and becomes obsessed with fire, ultimately burning herself. Her imagined terror 'Alison Hell' becomes real—a manifestation created from her own fear and loneliness.","opus":"A tormented girl named Alison, taunted and haunted by a lurking presence born from her own untended fears, descends into madness and hell, trapped, naked, and alone with the monster she created.","sonnet":"The song is narrated by a tormenting voice inside a woman's mind — an entity she herself created through fear and isolation — that drives her to madness, trapping her in a basement and sealing her in a private hell.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tormentor addresses a girl whose sanity is buckling, and Jeff Waters builds the horror from a story the band has long told as a real girl’s breakdown: childhood terrors dismissed until her fractured mind manufactured its own persecutor. The voice taunting her turns out to be that very invention, a demon she authored because no adult ever answered the dread waiting under her bed. As she collapses inward, stripped and cornered in the dark, the creature and the victim gradually fuse into a single damned figure who can no longer leave. The wordplay welding her name to a place of torment stops being a pun and hardens into a diagnosis, sealing a psyche that is now both the prison and the prisoner locked inside it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Band lore holds Alison was a real girl whose breakdown inspired the lyric — a story original vocalist John Bates, who wrote most of the words, disputes.","themes":["madness & the mind","faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"a demon authored by the child no adult ever answered","built":"the name pun stops being wordplay and hardens into a diagnosis sealing both prison and prisoner"},
"annihilator~all-for-you": {"haiku":"A relationship of control and abuse: the speaker sacrificed family and self for their partner, only to be isolated, blackmailed, and now abandoned by someone still unjustified.","opus":"An accusation aimed at a controlling, manipulative partner for whom the narrator sacrificed family and soul, calling out their blackmail, lies and hate as he finally sees through and confronts them.","sonnet":"The speaker describes a relationship in which they surrendered everything — family, children, soul — to give a controlling partner complete power, only to be beaten down, manipulated through blackmail, and eventually left with nothing. The song ends as the speaker recognises the exploitation and refuses to be brought down any further.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters turns Annihilator's usual horror-movie remove inward here, writing a direct accusation at a partner who demanded his children, his family and his conscience as the toll for staying, then rewrote that surrender as devotion freely given. The venom sharpens as the speaker itemizes the blackmail and the daily grinding contempt inherited from a household raised on cruelty, refusing the offered reconciliation because the damage is already tallied and long since paid. It closes on reversal: the manipulator who assumed a stupid, sightless, thoroughly owned man finally surveys the wreckage encircling her own feet, the well run dry and every scrap of leverage gone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss","family & blood"],"means":"leveraged surrender reframed as devotion, then refused","built":"the reversal lands on the manipulator surveying her own wreckage"},
"annihilator~ambush": {"haiku":"An assassination mission unfolds with stealth and precision, ending in silent victory and complete erasure.","opus":"A cold, clinical celebration of a stealth killing, tracking the target silently, striking, then disappearing untraceably. It frames elite assassination as a point of pride rather than guilt, lethal precision treated as a badge of superiority over the rest of humanity.","sonnet":"A tight military kill-mission narrative — a special operations team stalks and eliminates a target with silent, total efficiency, the song a straightforward celebration of tactical violence and lethal precision.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator writes a soldier's creed for the covert kill, a commando dropped behind enemy lines to erase a marked man and slip back into the dark before any alarm can sound. The chanted four-verb command sequence turns tactics into liturgy, each step a rung of the operation recited until the hunter stops thinking and simply carries it out. What the song withholds is any moral weight, so the closing boast about serving all of us becomes the sharpest turn, patriotic cover stretched over paid murder that leaves an emptied spot where a body stood and nobody left to mourn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","war & battle"],"means":"assassination conducted as liturgy until the operator stops thinking","built":"the four-verb command sequence turns tactics into a chant recited past the point of moral weight"},
"annihilator~annihilator": {"haiku":"An unstoppable machine crushes all life like a plague, trapping people in fear and helplessness. The song depicts a force of total annihilation that cannot be evaded or resisted.","opus":"Jeff Waters' Canadian thrash institution recasts a gathering storm as an unstoppable killing engine that spreads contagiously and grinds everything living beneath it. The pounding refrain naming the destroyer leaves the trapped listener with no route of escape, an early-catalog exercise in pure predatory dread.","sonnet":"An unstoppable machine-force advances like a storm, crushing everything beneath it and painting the sky blood red. There is no shelter, no escape route, only the spreading infection of its approach. The song is a portrait of pure overwhelming power — annihilation as weather.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters builds the band's namesake into a self-mythologizing terror, an unstoppable force advancing like a storm that infects and crushes everything below. The image-system fuses natural catastrophe with industrial machinery, so weather and mechanism become one indistinguishable engine of destruction. The perspective then narrows from that vast approaching front down to a single trapped, fear-filled figure with nowhere to run and no rescue coming. That zoom is the point: the machine is scale itself, and the chanted title keeps answering every threat by naming and claiming its own myth.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief"],"means":"scale itself weaponised as the mechanism of total destruction","built":"perspective zooms from vast catastrophic front to single trapped figure"},
"annihilator~anything-for-money": {"haiku":"A speaker with unbounded greed will commit any crime—theft, betrayal, fraud, extortion—to accumulate wealth, viewing money as the only goal worth pursuing.","opus":"Annihilator sketch a gleeful portrait of total greed, a narrator who would rob the blind, sell his mother and defraud anyone for another dime. Cataloguing scams from gunrunning to fleecing the church, the track skewers avarice through a voice that cheerfully wants all the money there is.","sonnet":"Greed is rendered without a single mitigating impulse. The narrator enumerates every crime available to him — fraud, blackmail, robbery, extortion, using religion as a vehicle for swindling — with cheerful relentlessness. Accumulation is the only value, and conscience is entirely absent from the catalogue.","src":"haiku","fable":"Greed narrates itself here in first person, and the joke is that it never once flinches. The speaker piles crime on crime with a shopkeeper's brisk cheer, so muggings, gun-running, and looting the church all read as line items on one shameless ledger. Annihilator's Jeff Waters lets the boast be the target: the flat catalog of felonies exposes an appetite that climbs the ladder of respectability and still recognizes no ceiling worth naming.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","violence & murder"],"means":"an appetite that climbs every rung and still finds no ceiling worth naming","built":"crimes catalogued with a shopkeeper's brisk cheer as line items on a ledger"},
"annihilator~armed-to-the-teeth": {"haiku":"Warning and intimidation toward an aggressor, with the narrator fully prepared and weaponized to exact punishment.","opus":"A snarling warning aimed at a would-be aggressor, the narrator daring anyone to test him and boasting he's dangerous past all reason and loaded for total war. An uneasy quiet gives way to reckoning. From Canadian thrash veterans Annihilator, led by guitarist Jeff Waters, it condenses the band's technical aggression into a straightforward, gauntlet-throwing anthem of defiance.","sonnet":"Maximum deterrence as identity — anyone who pushes gets DEFCON 1 in response, the calm before the storm masking absolute capability for destruction. The song shifts in its final turn: the defeated challenger is told to rebuild, suggesting that the point was never annihilation but the establishment of a power nobody rational challenges twice.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator's Jeff Waters, a Canadian thrash lifer who built the band around ferocious precision guitar work, frames this as a warning shouted at an aggressor who mistook restraint for weakness and pressed the advance anyway. The escalation reaches nuclear scale, hundred-megaton warheads and a marching death, yet the sharper menace is personal and close, a promise that the speaker is unlike anyone the enemy has faced and utterly unpredictable in what he might unleash. When the standoff narrows to a face-to-face challenge the aggressor breaks and flees like the coward he is called, and the final turn surprises, an order to survive the reckoning and rebuild that recasts the whole barrage as a punishing lesson rather than an execution.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","anger & defiance"],"means":"restraint mistaken for weakness triggers total deterrence","built":"final order to survive reframes the barrage as lesson not execution"},
"annihilator~army-of-one": {"haiku":"A dedicated metal subculture survives underground since the 1980s, united by faith in heavy music despite attempts to destroy or suppress it.","opus":"A headbanger's creed honoring the eighties thrash bands and the faithful few who kept the scene alive against every trend. Annihilator's tribute to metal itself as identity and blood-loyalty.","sonnet":"A love letter to heavy metal as a community and way of life — the band and its fans are a unified army bound by decades of devotion to the genre against all trends and opposition.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor's anthem to heavy metal itself, written from inside the eighties thrash cohort that Annihilator belonged to. The roll-call of bands is the whole argument: naming Slayer, Megadeth, Maiden and the rest turns the genre into a bloodline, so the paradoxical title lands as its thesis — a scene of scattered die-hards insisting they are a single body, united precisely by having been left for dead and refusing to fold. The bridge drops the tribute for pure mechanism, letting the blasting volume and screaming guitar stand as the creed's only real proof.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["friendship & loyalty","identity & becoming"],"means":"a scattered genre insisting on itself as a single surviving body","built":"the roll-call of band names substitutes for any argument"},
"annihilator~back-to-the-crypt": {"haiku":"A celebration of underground metal culture and its community, rejecting mainstream dismissal of thrashing music and finding solidarity in shared identity.","opus":"A defiant celebration of the underground thrash scene itself, answering critics who said the band had no future by turning the sweaty, packed club into a rallying cry. The narrator frames noise and camaraderie as its own vindication, kicking the door open for outsiders. Annihilator, the Canadian outfit led by guitarist Jeff Waters, made technical thrash their trade, and this is their self-mythologizing anthem to the scene that raised them.","sonnet":"A thrash-metal homecoming anthem — the underground scene as a crowded, sweat-soaked room full of true believers who were written off as having no future. The song celebrates outlasting the critics, finding community in the noise, and the particular satisfaction of being misunderstood and proven right anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"A scene anthem dressed in horror-flick trappings, where the crypt is simply the underground club — a place the mainstream treats as a tomb but the faithful treat as home. The middle verses turn defensive, answering the critics and the no-future taunts head-on, and the final verse flips that dismissal into a badge: the same rejection that once stung is now the drive that got a foot in the door and cracked metal open to something else. It's Annihilator claiming a lineage by thanking the doubters, the classic thrash move of building identity out of everything the wider world refused to take seriously.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"rejection from the mainstream converted into a founding credential","built":"the crypt reframed as home rather than tomb — outsider space claimed as lineage"},
"annihilator~back-to-the-palace": {"haiku":"A trapped and fractured mind experiences imprisonment, surveillance, and pursuit; memories and trauma hunt him as he tries to escape.","opus":"Jeff Waters writes psychosis from inside: a mind trapped under glass in a fun-palace of malice, hunted by things it isn't sure are real. The horror is the pull back into the delusion, a sickness that keeps calling the sufferer home.","sonnet":"About mental illness or psychotic break as a funhouse prison — the narrator is trapped behind glass in a 'fun palace' populated by creepy figures and inner voices they can't escape, trying to flee but being dragged back, with the memories themselves tearing flesh and the only therapy a hiding game.","src":"haiku","fable":"Often described as a return to the Fun Palace of Never, Neverland — down to the greeting aimed at little Alice herself — this stages a breakdown as a padded institutional room recast as a carnival attraction, its patient welcomed by a mocking master of ceremonies. The horror sharpens through the pane of observation glass: figures dance in the dark just beyond reach, and the paranoid question of whether they can breach an inner wall turns surveillance itself into the disease speaking. When the barriers give way it becomes a nighttime flight scored by pursuing hounds and sweeping beams, a fugitive pleading that his voices, the only company he has left, not be confiscated from him. The cruelest turn dresses forced return and the excavation of buried wounds as a children's counting game, so that treatment and captivity grow impossible to tell apart from one another.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","surveillance & control","freedom & escape"],"means":"treatment and captivity made impossible to distinguish from one another","built":"forced return dressed as a children's counting game to blur cure and cage"},
"annihilator~bad-child": {"haiku":"A person recounts their troubled youth spent running wild and rejecting guidance, causing pain to their mother. As an adult, they finally understand the damage they caused and seek redemption.","opus":"The Canadian thrash band, Jeff Waters' project, tells a prodigal-son arc: a father pleading for the boy to straighten out, a mother reduced to tears, years of truancy and dissipation. By the end the wayward son has reformed and looks toward better days. The riffing frames a familiar tale of recklessness giving way to remorse and change.","sonnet":"A man looks back on a youth of reckless self-destruction — school skipped, rules broken, mother reduced to tears — and acknowledges the harm done while noting the turn: something happened along the way, and he is now living toward a better day.","src":"haiku","fable":"A wayward son narrates his own rap sheet with a swagger that keeps slipping, skipping school, guzzling cheap fuel, cutting against every grain until his family locks him outside to sleep on bare floorboards. What lingers beneath the bravado is a single wound he cannot outrun: his mother weeping over him, an image the verses circle back to twice, as if scrubbing at it only cuts the memory deeper. The whole architecture stages a redemption, the father's warning returning later with the boy now claiming he has straightened himself out and lives toward brighter mornings, yet the chorus keeps branding him with the verdict the neighbors always pronounced. That gap between the reformed man he insists he became and the label that refuses to peel away is the true subject, penance sung as boast, guilt dressed in speed-metal defiance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","identity & becoming","family & blood"],"means":"the reformed man's claim and the neighbor's verdict refuse to reconcile","built":"the mother's weeping image circles twice, cutting deeper each pass"},
"annihilator~bats-in-the-belfry": {"haiku":"Someone trying to help a detached person struggles with their emotional walls and refusal to accept assistance, watching helplessly as anxiety and confusion consume their mind. The plea is met with indifference, leaving the speaker isolated and frustrated.","opus":"Frustration with someone in denial and self-destruction, the narrator exhausted from trying to help a person who won't see the signs or listen, ultimately choosing self-preservation over being dragged down with them.","sonnet":"About watching someone spiral into mental breakdown while they refuse to listen to your warnings — the narrator is also unraveling from the effort of caring, eventually realizing they nearly went down with the other person. The \"bats in the belfry\" is both their craziness and yours.","src":"haiku","fable":"A one-sided rescue attempt curdles into resentment: the speaker keeps decoding a partner who will not meet him halfway, and the cracked-mirror image throws the accusation back on both parties at once. Annihilator, a band fluent in asylum horror, turns the title’s old idiom for lunacy inward, so the plea to just look and see doubles as the speaker's own fraying grip on his mind. His imperative eventually sours from a live warning into a verdict already missed, the exhausted survivor choosing distance and self-preservation over one more squandered effort at reaching someone who never cared to be reached.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a warning that sours into an exit chosen for survival","built":"the title idiom for lunacy bends back onto the speaker himself"},
"annihilator~battered": {"haiku":"Surviving domestic abuse and fighting back, the narrator reclaiming agency and ending the cycle of violence inflicted on them.","opus":"A harrowing account of an abuse victim finding the strength to fight back, shattered and programmed to obey, stalked and unable to leave, finally resolving to reclaim a soul and end the torment by any bloody means. It moves from helplessness to desperate retaliation. Annihilator, the Canadian thrash band led by guitarist Jeff Waters, lend their technical precision to this unflinching first-person portrait of surviving domestic violence.","sonnet":"Domestic abuse from the inside — the speaker has been conditioned into submission, programmed to obey, but something finally cracks and they reach for the strength to fight back and reclaim themselves. The violence has been relentless, the fantasy the abuser built around them is disintegrating, and what replaces it is a desperate, shaky resolve to take control before the end.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person account of an abused, controlled victim reaching the snapping point and deciding to kill their tormentor. The chorus's second-person accusation splits the perspective in two, so battered names both what has been done to the singer and the sentence about to be handed down to the abuser. Jeff Waters typically works in horror and social-menace subjects rather than confessional writing, and here the thrash form serves the reversal directly: the whipped-dog obedience of the verses builds toward the swift, bloody retaliation, so the mounting speed and aggression become the sound of a cornered person converting fear into violence. The final plea for mercy after doing what had to be done leaves the moral verdict deliberately unsettled, framing the killing as both liberation and damnation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance","surveillance & control"],"means":"fear converted into violence that lands as liberation and damnation both","built":"the chorus accusation splits battered across victim and verdict simultaneously"},
"annihilator~betrayed": {"haiku":"A manipulative parent uses false family performance to exploit and poison their children with lies and abuse, betraying trust to create generational evil.","opus":"Waters exposes a rotten family from the inside, parents who buy and drug their children as props to relieve their own guilt, then cry abuse when convenient. The betrayal curdles into watching that dynasty of lies collapse.","sonnet":"A scorching portrait of toxic, manipulative parents — a mother who fed her children drugs and a scheming father — whose selfishness and lies have permanently damaged the children they were supposed to protect.","src":"haiku","fable":"A thrash indictment of the abusive family that curates itself as perfect for the neighbours while rotting behind the door, told to the parents in the second person as a prosecutor's address. The song builds by stripping the mask off each figure in turn, mother then father, until the polished dynasty is named for what it is, a scheme of lies whose only real inheritance is the evil it trained the children to become. The refrain does the emotional work the verses withhold: reducing the betrayed party to a hunted, cornered animal, it locates the real damage not in the fraud itself but in what fraud makes of the person forced to live inside it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"a curated dynasty stripped mask by mask until its inheritance is named as the evil it trained","built":"the refrain locates the real damage not in the fraud but in what fraud makes of the person forced to live inside it"},
"annihilator~bled": {"haiku":"Someone breaks free from an emotionally draining relationship where they've been used and depleted, determined to stop the cycle.","opus":"Annihilator vent at a draining, cruel partner who bleeds the narrator dry emotionally and financially, prompting his vow to stop the crying, dying and bleeding and walk away stronger. He sees her parents' patterns repeating. Jeff Waters' thrash outfit channels domestic toxicity into a defiant severance, framing the exit as self-preservation and hard-won resolve rather than mere anger.","sonnet":"A person finally extracting themselves from a toxic, financially and emotionally draining relationship. After years of being screamed at, pushed, and exploited by someone who mistook neediness for love, he reaches the end of his tolerance and delivers a cold farewell.","src":"haiku","fable":"A furious severance from a partner who runs hot with performance but offers nothing warm underneath, whose affection turns out to be a transaction dressed up as devotion, appetite circling a wound rather than tending it. Jeff Waters routes the venom through Annihilator's signature thrash precision, and the title's double meaning carries the argument, since the narrator has been drained of both money and dignity, fed upon by someone whose neediness keeps masquerading as love. The turn arrives when the speaker recognizes the same cruelty running through the partner's parents and refuses to repeat it, choosing exit and hardened self-preservation over one more round in a cycle that only takes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","family & blood"],"means":"exit chosen over repeating a cycle that only takes","built":"the title's double meaning carries both the financial drain and the dignity loss"},
"annihilator~bloodbath": {"haiku":"Nightmarish visions and religious dread overwhelm the dreamer; followers worship blindly and blood spills while the narrator feels trapped and powerless.","opus":"A thrash nightmare of paranoia and religious dread, the narrator trapped and powerless as something monstrous batters at the door, the terror bound up with predatory churchmen profiting off fear of nothing.","sonnet":"An attack on religious exploitation — a predatory religious organisation is described as feeding off its followers' fear of nothing, corrupting and controlling them while spreading what the narrator calls a Catholic curse.","src":"haiku","fable":"A televangelist's promised apocalypse arrives as literal home invasion, the paralytic terror of a sleeper who cannot wake, cannot rise, cannot speak as a bright presence closes down the hallway. Jeff Waters stages sleep-paralysis physics: footsteps, a door wrenched off its hinges, limbs cast in lead, prayer declared pointless against a radiance that keeps swelling. The refrain wheels around to accuse the preacher himself, the con man harvesting collection plates from a dread he manufactured, his flock branded as the ones actually bled dry. Alice in Hell keeps the thrash clinical and tight, so night-terror panic and pulpit-fraud satire land as one continuous suffocation rather than two separate ideas.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","politics & society","madness & the mind"],"means":"night-terror paralysis and pulpit fraud landing as one continuous suffocation","built":"sleep-paralysis mechanics used to stage a televangelist's manufactured dread"},
"annihilator~both-of-me": {"haiku":"Two people escaping captivity or oppression together, but ultimately having to split and run separately as hunters close in.","opus":"Annihilator frame a desperate prison break, two figures crawling beneath a gate toward freedom before searchlights and sirens close in. Jeff Waters' thrash storytelling drives the escape into a splitting-in-two finale, the caged narrator refusing to return and preparing an attack, the title hinting the pursuers can never truly catch every part of a divided self.","sonnet":"Two inmates execute a desperate midnight escape, scaling walls and cutting under fences, tasting freedom for the first time in what feels like forever — only for lights and sirens to close in, forcing a final split, a last gambit against recapture.","src":"haiku","fable":"What opens as a prison-break thriller, two captives creeping down a corridor toward daylight and swearing their pursuers will lose the chase, slowly curdles into something stranger once the sirens crowd in and the searchlights multiply around them. The tell is the plural insistence lodged inside a single body: the fugitives collapse into one narrator who cannot be seized because there was never a second person to grab, only a mind partitioned into warden and runaway. Annihilator built its name on Jeff Waters' cerebral thrash, where guitar architecture frames psychological horror rather than mere gore, so the closing image of a creature caged and readying its assault reads as the divided self turning on whatever institution declared it sick and needing confinement.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"a prison break thriller that collapses into a single partitioned mind","built":"the plural insistence lodged inside what turns out to be one narrator"},
"annihilator~brain-dance": {"haiku":"Playful absurdism and mental escape—the speaker retreats into surreal, childlike nonsense (fish in floors, toe paintings, rubber shoes) as refuge from reality.","opus":"A cheerfully unhinged narrator recounts his life among courteous companions and gleaming walls, foot-made art and a beloved stone, slowly revealing that he's describing an asylum through his sunny delusions. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' Canadian technical thrash outfit, wrap institutional horror in nursery-rhyme whimsy, the playful tone gradually exposing a mind splintering into imaginary friends.","sonnet":"A wry, darkly comic portrait of institutional life — a person housed in a psychiatric facility who has constructed an internal world of toe-painting, phantom fish, and walks in the sun with attendants. The absurdist whimsy barely conceals the tragedy of a mind that has fully retreated from the outside.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about institutionalization that hides behind a nursery-rhyme grin. The narrator reframes his confinement as a rustic retirement, cheerfully cataloguing gentle keepers who escort him on supervised strolls and a padded cell he mistakes for pastoral calm. Jeff Waters lets the cartoonish absurdity curdle: finger-smeared pictures, imaginary vermin he must stamp out beneath the boards, a scorched-and-recovered loop that no jumping ever ends. Each cheerful detail marks a mind circling without an exit. The companion he introduces at the close — an Alice, winking at the band’s asylum muse — is the delusion itself, the pleasant face that keeps him docile while the ward stays locked.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","surveillance & control"],"means":"institutional confinement cheerfully misread as pastoral retirement","built":"nursery-rhyme grin curdling one cheerful detail at a time into horror"},
"annihilator~burns-like-a-buzzsaw-blade": {"haiku":"Two lovers indulge in raw sexual excess that burns intensely—animal lust, humiliation, and screams of passion create a frenzied, chaotic physical connection.","opus":"An unabashedly explicit account of an all-night sexual encounter, all animal lust, nails raking backs, tangled bodies and screams. The power keeps flipping, on his knees yet reigning as king, framing the sex as a shared private game of dominance and abandon.","sonnet":"Two people engage in an all-night session of intense, consensual, performatively extreme sex — the imagery is deliberately excessive and theatrical, framing animal lust as both absurd and exhilarating.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters turns Annihilator's signature technical thrash inward, letting the whirring precision of his fretwork double as the friction of two bodies who treat a night of appetite as their own private outlaw myth in the making. The encounter is framed as theatre both partners recognize, laughing at the pretense even as sweat, raking fingernails, and a vow to prolong things push the pair toward daybreak. A final reversal inverts the power play entirely, the speaker kneeling yet claiming to rule, so raw abandon becomes a small negotiated kingdom rather than mere surrender to heat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","party & hedonism"],"means":"raw appetite framed as a shared outlaw myth both partners laugh at","built":"the guitar's whirring precision doubles as the friction of the encounter it describes"},
"annihilator~carnival-diablos": {"haiku":"Evil descending on humanity in a carnival-like spectacle, depicted as demonic forces collecting souls and corrupting the world.","opus":"A dark carnival fable in which the devil's traveling show descends on a town, his servants ensnaring the crowd as the heavens redden and the dead are counted, freaks and clowns luring victims into a lethal amusement. The narrator wrestles free of a demon's grip. The title track of Annihilator's 2001 album finds the Canadian thrashers reveling in horror theatrics over Jeff Waters' precise riffing.","sonnet":"The devil arrives disguised as a travelling carnival, smoke obscuring the sun, minions chaining everyone into his count of souls. The song is a spiritual warfare narrative — the speaker caught between fleeing and fighting, trying to resist something that has already tightened its grip, the red sky overhead confirming the body count is rising.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters stages damnation as a travelling sideshow: the devil arrives not as a warlord but as a ringmaster whose freaks and jesters coax the doomed into a rigged game they mistake for harmless spectacle. The horror sharpens through the accounting image, where he tallies his harvested souls against the mounting human dead, quietly turning the fairground into a ledger balanced in slaughter. Beneath the carnival mask sits ordinary temptation, wickedness dressed up as entertainment so nobody notices the minions throwing chains over everyone until walking away is no longer allowed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"damnation disguised as spectacle until the exits disappear","built":"the ledger image turns a fairground into a soul-harvest accounting"},
"annihilator~chasing-the-high": {"haiku":"A methamphetamine addict wakes to desperation and feeds the addiction despite knowing it will destroy him; each cycle hastens death while the drug promises invincibility.","opus":"A bleak descent into meth addiction, the narrator rotting physically as he craves his next fix, feeding the habit until an overdose finishes what a single first hit began. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' long-running Canadian thrash outfit, drive their technical riffing into an unflinching cautionary tale where getting high and dying collapse into one act.","sonnet":"The full arc of methamphetamine addiction compressed into a few verses — the crystal breakfast, the burning eyes, the moment the chemical hits and turns pain into invincibility, then the loop starting over with a heart attack at the end. The tone is clinical in its detail and relentless in its direction: every hit promises liberation, every cycle tightens the trap. There is no way back once you start.","src":"haiku","fable":"A methamphetamine addiction gets mapped as a religious devotion curdled into cannibal ritual, where the addict tends a feeding beast, summons a priest for last rites, and treats each dose as a sacrament despite rotting teeth and daylight that scorches the eyes. Annihilator's Jeff Waters lets the two-word refrain carry the whole argument, welding euphoria to death as interchangeable states so the pursuit of the next fix and the slide toward the grave read as one uninterrupted motion. When the loop resets in the closing turn, the song denies any escape hatch, insisting a single experiment locks in a trajectory whose only exit is the body finally quitting on its owner.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"euphoria and dying named as a single uninterrupted motion","built":"the closing loop denies any escape hatch, resetting instead of resolving"},
"annihilator~city-of-ice": {"haiku":"Urban decay, violence, and psychological numbness transform an inner world into a frozen wasteland where dreams melt away and sanity erodes under constant assault.","opus":"Jeff Waters pours urban dread into a frozen, decaying city of crime and squalor that hardens the narrator until he feels nothing. The metropolis becomes a waking nightmare that strips his hopes away, cold and fury building until he knows he is permanently changed, thrash as claustrophobic despair.","sonnet":"Urban violence and moral rot freeze the narrator from the inside out. Surrounded by murder and misery dressed up as normalcy, he watches his dreams dissolve in real time. The cold is not weather but the numbing effect of a place that treats brutality as the price of existence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters writes urban dread as a slow petrification, a narrator who feels the metropolis harden him into cold statuary while the pavement itself refuses to release its grip on him. The middle section widens that private ache into public catastrophe, gunfire and shrieking bodies crowding the air until the speaker reframes himself as a marionette jerked across a stage he never chose to stand on. What opens as personal revulsion collapses into total sensory siege, the false promise of bliss curdling into rot and slaughter, so his closing admission that he might survive yet emerge permanently altered lands as the only honest reckoning left to him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","night & the city","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the city petrifies its inhabitant into cold statuary","built":"private revulsion widens into public massacre then collapses back"},
"annihilator~clare": {"haiku":"A man obsesses over a woman named Clare, desperate to be seen and save her from madness and institutional cruelty.","opus":"A genuinely unsettling narrative of obsession sliding into psychiatric horror, the narrator crawling in search of a girl he sees only in shadows while needles, incisions and a schizo delight bleed the personal fixation into clinical nightmare. The slippage between Clare and Alice deepens the unreliability. Annihilator, Jeff Waters's Canadian thrash vehicle, often built their songs around such disturbed character studies rather than straightforward aggression.","sonnet":"Obsession collapsing into psychosis — a fixation on an unreachable figure named Clare that the speaker can only glimpse in shadows, someone who never speaks and never appears. The back half slides into institutionalization: white coats, needles, a cocktail that signals the break with reality is complete and the inner life has been chemically extinguished.","src":"haiku","fable":"This inhabits a mind coming apart, its narrator crawling through a fog of illness toward a beloved who drifts between names that echo the band’s oldest locked-room stories, a Clare, an Alice. The beloved never answers, closer to a fixation than a person. The obsession curdles as the voice pleads to be truly seen only in darkness, confessing it can barely stand itself in daylight, then swings without warning into a vow of protection so absolute it reads as menace, a guardian who threatens to destroy whoever comes near. The whole thing lands as a devastating punchline once white-coated orderlies wielding a sedative arrive, since the tender rescuer and the raving patient turn out to be the same trapped man, and the phantom he swore to shield was only ever his own shattering mind dressed up as someone worth saving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"the beloved revealed as the narrator's own shattering mind","built":"the arrival of sedating orderlies lands the twist — tender rescuer and raving patient are the same man"},
"annihilator~clown-parade": {"haiku":"Deceitful people exploit and drain others with contracts and false promises. When exposed, they lie further and eventually face karmic retribution.","opus":"Jeff Waters channeling personal betrayal by industry crooks and liars into a revenge screed, contracts signed and promises broken by frauds hiding behind smiles; karma promised to the parade of clowns who drain and deceive.","sonnet":"A furious accounting of being repeatedly scammed and betrayed by people in the music or business world — liars, contract-breakers, character assassins — with the speaker insisting karma will catch up to all of them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A settling of scores dressed as a carnival: the clowns are con artists who sign contracts they never mean to honor, and the parade is the endless line of victims they charm and bleed. Jeff Waters, whose long career has run through label and management wars, writes it from the raw side of that experience, the sinking dread of paying for a service and getting nothing but excuses. Midway the song flips from prey to predator, the character-assassination attempt becoming the fuel for a promise that karma, not the courts, will collect. It is less a riddle than a curse spoken aloud, and its whole force is in refusing to forgive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","money & the street","artifice & performance"],"means":"a curse spoken aloud refusing to forgive","built":"carnival framing repackages fraud victims as a procession"},
"annihilator~cold-blooded": {"haiku":"Predatory hunger personified, building kingdoms on bones of victims, strength fed by massacre and death.","opus":"A villain's monologue of conquest and slaughter, a self-crowned ruler raising a kingdom out of the dead. Pure thrash theater reveling in appetite, supremacy and unrepentant carnage.","sonnet":"A first-person power-fantasy murder anthem — the narrator is a cold-blooded killer building a kingdom from the bones of enemies, feeding on massacres, and growing stronger with each life taken. The imagery is deliberately mythic and inhuman, framing the killer as a force of nature rather than a person.","src":"haiku","fable":"A conqueror narrates his own ascent as prophecy, casting slaughter as a birthright and appetite, the language of divine mission fused with the flat procedure of an execution queue where each victim is only the next name called forward. The hypnotic chant that repeats as a fortress rises stone by stone is the tell: the tyrant is not really constructing anything but consolidating dominion, mistaking a heap of accumulated corpses for a lasting throne. When he finally claims a realm raised out of the murdered, the swagger curdles into confession, a monster who devours everyone he meets yet is left with only dust, betraying that his hunger can never actually be satisfied.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","war & battle","faith & the occult"],"means":"the tyrant's hunger reveals a throne built only of accumulated corpses","built":"hypnotic chant rises stone by stone exposing consolidation not construction"},
"annihilator~couple-suicide": {"haiku":"A relationship without genuine feeling is slowly suffocating both partners, leading nowhere but taking them deeper into emotional death together.","opus":"A portrait of a relationship both people privately know has already died, dragged out anyway because ending it feels like too much. The narrator eventually accepts that the only honest move is to bury the whole thing for good, and finds a grim relief in finally letting it go.","sonnet":"Two people in a dead relationship who both know it's over but keep trying to resuscitate it — the emotional core is mutual acknowledgment that the relationship is killing itself slowly, and the only real mercy is to bury it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A relationship autopsy that keeps mistaking retreat for repair. The couple rehearses the same failed correction on loop, each withdrawal a doomed bid to salvage affection that has already gone hollow, both fully aware of the emptiness. The chorus's stalled machinery mirrors the emotional deadlock, a partnership circling its own grave rather than moving toward one another. Only the farewell breaks the cycle, when the survivor names his release less as grief than as escape from a suffocating grip. The final image, burying the thing outright, frames the demise as the sole honest resolution left to either of them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","freedom & escape"],"means":"retreat rehearsed as repair until the only honest resolution is burial","built":"the chorus's stalled machinery mirrors the partnership circling its own grave"},
"annihilator~coward": {"haiku":"The speaker confronts a cowardly abuser who preys on weakness and betrays trust, vowing accountability and retribution for cruelty and deception. The song is an accusation and threat directed at an unrepentant wrongdoer.","opus":"A confrontation with a selfish, cheating man who exploited a woman's vulnerability, the speaker warning him there is nowhere to conceal himself, promising to inflict suffering, and declaring his reign finished as they stand up on her behalf.","sonnet":"A furious confrontation with someone who preyed on a woman's weakness, exploited her, and cheated repeatedly — the narrator vows to deliver pain when their worlds collide, demanding the target admit fault and face the consequences of their selfish deception.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confrontation staged as an execution: the speaker corners a man who used and cheated on a woman close to them, and the whole song is the threat-speech he never got to hear. The title is the accusation that cuts under all the bluster — for every promise of delivered pain and colliding worlds, the actual charge is domestic and petty: he preyed on someone weaker, took everything, gave nothing back, and hid behind lies. That mismatch is the point. The avenger inflates a small, cowardly cruelty into apocalyptic scale, and the shift from I to we at the end quietly reframes the rage as protectiveness rather than mere fury — the woman is spoken for, then spoken beside. What the song refuses to grant its target is the last thing a coward wants: to be looked at and named out loud.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"apocalyptic threat-speech inflated around a small domestic cruelty","built":"the shift from I to we quietly reframes private fury as collective protection"},
"annihilator~criteria-for-a-black-widow": {"haiku":"A woman's predatory nature is examined through violent and sexual metaphors, describing the fatal criteria of a dangerous man-consuming feminine force.","opus":"The title track of Annihilator's reunion album interrogates a predatory femme fatale through relentless questioning. A battery of blunt, escalating verbs probes what can be done to and by the black widow, sliding from caress toward violence and destruction. The narrator's closing relief that it will never be him again reframes the catalogue as a survivor's wary anatomy of a woman he casts as lethal.","sonnet":"A litany of predatory verbs maps the full spectrum of what a black widow does to her prey — caress, use, break, destroy — building a portrait of a woman who consumes completely. The speaker catalogues her power with a mix of fascination and relief that he has finally escaped her reach.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters builds the black-widow figure entirely from a battering list of can-you challenges, each verb daring the listener to cherish then consume the same nameless thing until tenderness and slaughter become interchangeable trials. The escalation is the argument: the questions slide from cradling and craving toward breaking and bleeding, so the spider metaphor sheds simple misogyny and exposes a predatory arithmetic measuring exactly how much a body will endure. Only the final turn breaks the interrogation, a survivor's relieved oath that the devouring will never claim him again, recasting the earlier catalogue as the recited scars of someone who already tore free of the web.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"escalating can-you dares slide from cradling toward bleeding without pause","built":"the interrogation breaks only at the end with a survivor's relieved oath"},
"annihilator~dead-wrong": {"haiku":"Martyrdom and vindictive resurrection, with the narrator promising revenge on those who persecuted and executed them.","opus":"A raging oath of retaliation from a man punished for a falsehood, subjected to every torment imaginable and swearing he'll rise again to make his persecutors regret it. The insistent promise of retribution fuels the defiance. Canadian thrash band Annihilator, driven by guitarist Jeff Waters, channels wrongful persecution into a blunt, riff-heavy revenge fantasy.","sonnet":"An innocent condemned and punished through every method imaginable — crucified, burnt, electrocuted, poisoned — vowing to return and claim vengeance once the sentence is served. The song has no interest in appeals or redemption; the only trajectory is endurance until the day he walks back out and collects what is owed.","src":"haiku","fable":"A wrongly-condemned voice recites the whole grim catalogue of human punishment - the pyre, the noose, the electric chair, the poisoned needle, the blade that quarters a body - daring its persecutors to try every method they know. The horror is that none of it sticks: the accused insists on innocence, counts the stolen span of a life spent caged for someone else's fabrication, and turns each torment into fuel rather than surrender. Fitting Annihilator's mid-90s thrash under Jeff Waters, the song withholds any actual crime, so the litany reads less as guilt than as an indictment of the executioners themselves. The refusal to break becomes a threat, an executed man promising resurrection and a reckoning, so the machinery meant to erase him only guarantees his return for those who wielded it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"each torment becomes fuel rather than proof of guilt","built":"withheld crime turns the execution litany into an indictment of executioners"},
"annihilator~deadlock": {"haiku":"Humanity faces extinction through apathy and overconsumption, locked in mediocrity while squandering resources and breeding toward inevitable doom.","opus":"A misanthropic tirade on humanity engineering its own extinction through apathy and thoughtless consumption, the crowd breeding recklessly and buying pointless upgrades while intelligence decays. Stuck in mediocrity and hypocrisy, everyone becomes livestock bound for slaughter, having squandered every advantage and sealed the species' doom.","sonnet":"A pessimistic assessment of human civilisation's trajectory — the masses are consuming themselves into extinction, breeding stupidity faster than wisdom, locked into a system that rewards mediocrity and hypocrisy until the whole thing collapses.","src":"haiku","fable":"A thrash-metal broadside against civilizational decline, framing the human race as a species sleepwalking toward extinction through consumption, complacency, and squandered potential. The song's engine is a deadlock in both senses the chorus fuses: cultural mediocrity and moral hypocrisy locked into each other, so that nobody can break the cycle even as they narrate it. The turn comes in the post-solo section, which quotes the manic momentum of Smash Mouth’s All Star hook to make its bleakest point through borrowed euphoria: the hits keep coming, the digging keeps going, and the punchline is that the grave we are so busy filling has finally run out of room. By the final verse the accusatory you of the consumer verses collapses into a we, and the finger-pointing becomes a confession that the speaker is implicated in the very fate he is condemning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"civilizational decline diagnosed and then confessed as the speaker's own complicity","built":"borrowed euphoria from an All Star hook makes the bleakest point about the grave already dug"},
"annihilator~death-in-your-eyes": {"haiku":"Soldiers are manipulated into war by leaders who hide safely at home. Young men die in battle while their commanders evade responsibility.","opus":"A thrash indictment of holy war and manufactured martyrdom, foot soldiers indoctrinated to kill and die while the leaders who sent them sleep safe at home; fury aimed at the machinery that turns belief into slaughter.","sonnet":"A soldier or follower stripped of conscience — told to separate mind from morality, sacrifice innocent lives for a cause, and obey absolutely — until a final turn where the narrator sees through the war's lies and condemns those who sent others to die while remaining safe.","src":"haiku","fable":"A soldier is manufactured here, his reasoning hollowed out and his conscience unplugged so that killing can pass as a cleansing rite promising his ravaged soul a place among the heavens. The song ventriloquizes the recruiter as that voice renames slaughter, insisting the enemy earned their ruin through deception while the murdered innocents get folded quietly into an acceptable cost. Then the vantage snaps loose and turns furious at the machinery itself, cursing a conflict run on manufactured hatred and holy delusion, where the fighters chase martyrdom as though dying could baptize them into sainthood. The sharpest cut lands on the accountants of that bloodshed, the commanders who sign the orders and then sleep untroubled behind their own bedroom doors while the obedient are marched off and ground into dirt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","politics & society","violence & murder"],"means":"the recruiter's voice renames slaughter until the vantage snaps and turns furious at the machinery","built":"the sharpest cut lands on commanders who sign orders then sleep untroubled behind their own doors"},
"annihilator~demon-code": {"haiku":"Dark, obsessive thoughts torment the mind; twisted fantasies and inner demons vie for control, pressing constantly to rule and overwhelm.","opus":"An unflinching look at intrusive thoughts and mental illness as an evil passenger riding inside, the narrator barely holding a fragile control while the taunting inner voices wait to seize him again.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone haunted by intrusive, violent, and sexual thoughts they struggle to suppress — the narrator describes an internal passenger of dark impulses sitting just below the surface, threatening to break through and destroy everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A mind at war with its own tenants. Jeff Waters casts the intrusive voice as a second occupant of the skull, a lodger that surfaces on its own schedule and demands the reins. Across the verses a composed social face holds while a private chorus jeers beneath it, and a wanting glance at a woman curdles instantly into shame and self-damnation. The returning chant is the sickness described as mechanism: a thought shoved down only to climb back stronger, the affliction reframed as a passenger every human carries. By the final approach the stakes turn naked, hold the pieces together or get shoved clean past the drop.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression","suicide"],"means":"a thought shoved down only climbs back stronger each time","built":"a composed public face holds while a private chorus jeers beneath it"},
"annihilator~demon-dance": {"haiku":"A person spiraling through severe depression describes their state as uncontrollable madness where even help and medication feel inadequate. They're asking for relief from psychological torment that may be their final crisis.","opus":"A raw account of depression pushed past endurance, worn out by priests and doctors and pills, courting death as relief and personifying the illness as a demon that must be fed while sanity slips.","sonnet":"About being consumed by severe depression — the exhaustion of wanting to die, losing the ability to face the day, and knowing the blackness will deepen without warning. The \"demon dance\" is the seductive pull of giving in entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters writes mental collapse as a duet with the thing destroying him: priest and physicians are dismissed early because absolution and prescriptions cannot touch a suffering that refuses anesthesia, so the singer bargains with his own affliction instead. The invitation to wrap arms and dance is the ugly hinge here, recasting torment as intimacy and daring another person to feed the spreading blackness rather than try to cure it. By the closing couplet the whole struggle narrows to one flat question about whether this bout of despair is the terminal one, and the deliberate absence of an answer is the entire point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","suicide"],"means":"bargaining with affliction rather than seeking its cure","built":"torment recast as intimacy through the invitation to dance"},
"annihilator~denied": {"haiku":"Fire and destruction consume everything in apocalyptic fashion, leaving no escape or survival, denying any hope for tomorrow.","opus":"An apocalyptic tableau of a burning sky, corpse-strewn streets, and disintegrating bodies, the narrator looking on, praying, and awaiting his own end while treating death as deliverance amid a man-made, science-engineered extinction. Annihilator, the Canadian thrash veterans, pour Cold War dread into this scorched nightmare where nothing survives to see another day.","sonnet":"Apocalypse rendered in fire and ash — bodies in the streets, flesh dissolving, skies lit by something human hands built and cannot take back. The framing is political and self-condemning: a final solution of science and man, a product of collective choices arriving as judgment. Hope is explicitly denied. Whatever tomorrow might have held has been burned away in the same conflagration that was supposed to solve something.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator's Jeff Waters stages a nuclear aftermath from inside the blast radius, where a narrator greets ruin as companionship because every other bond has already been incinerated around him. The catastrophe arrives not as accident but as engineered outcome, the deliberate handiwork of laboratories and statesmen whose calculations reduce a whole species to a closing figure on a ledger. When surrender to dying becomes the only mercy still on offer, the title lands as the verdict survivors receive instead of rescue: tomorrow itself is refused, withheld like a petition stamped and returned. The looping middle chant, cycling through fire and weeping and helplessness, enacts the trap it describes, since no forward motion remains, only the same agony recurring until the smoke settles over everything.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"tomorrow withheld like a petition stamped and returned","built":"the looping middle chant enacts the trap it describes — no forward motion remains"},
"annihilator~detonation": {"haiku":"A person reaches psychological breaking point and fantasizes about destructive action to escape monotony and reclaim agency.","opus":"A portrait of a shut-in at breaking point, watching life pass from his room, praying for escape until the pent-up pressure demands he burn it all down and finally feel alive. The plea for a chance to breathe reveals the desperation under the arson fantasy. Annihilator, the Canadian thrash band led by Jeff Waters, favored exactly these studies of frayed nerves erupting into violent release.","sonnet":"Stasis and suffocation reaching a breaking point — a person trapped alone in a room, invisible to the world, praying for something to change, until the pressure builds into a fantasy of destruction and freedom. The detonation isn't violence so much as the desperate need to feel alive just once, to stop being someone the world is happening without.","src":"haiku","fable":"A shut-in narrator watches his own life pass from a bedroom that has curdled into a cell, until prayer answers with fear rather than comfort and the stalled days begin pressurizing something combustible inside him. The song tracks that pressure hardening into an engine locked in overdrive with no brake left, and the title chanted over and over stands in for the release he decides is the only exit remaining to him. Its unsettling turn is the swing between menace and pleading: the vow to torch the room and finally feel alive sits against a small cracked voice begging the world for room to breathe, so the violence reads less as villainy than as a cornered mind heaping blame on everyone nearby for its own overload.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness","violence & murder"],"means":"violence read as a cornered mind's only remaining exit","built":"menace and pleading alternate — torch-the-room vow sits against a small voice begging for room"},
"annihilator~don-t-bother-me": {"haiku":"A chronically irresponsible person oversleeps and loses their job repeatedly, expressing indifference while spiraling into alcoholism. They reject all chances for improvement and embrace self-destruction.","opus":"A darkly comic portrait of an unrepentant alcoholic; the narrator wakes up hungover, loses his job and can't be bothered to care, brushing off every excuse and every warning that his life is passing by as he heads straight back out for another drink.","sonnet":"A song narrated from inside a drinking problem — the protagonist misses alarms, loses jobs, forgets everything, and doesn't much care, because the only priority is the next bottle of rum, delivered with a shrug rather than self-pity.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song lives in a split pronoun: it opens as a hungover slacker cataloguing a wrecked morning in the first person, but the chorus exposes a second voice inside the same skull, one part narrating the disaster and another waving off every intervention with the title's brush-off. Everything turns on how ownership of the collapse keeps sliding — losing the job is reported first as a flat fact, then reframed as something that doesn't matter, and finally twisted into license to keep doing exactly what caused it. That is the mechanics of denial rendered as a hook: the addict is both the messenger delivering the bad news and the one telling that messenger to shut up. The clock-watching refrain supplies the only outside sightline, a life passing by, which he answers not with reform but with a fresh bottle, so the real subject is the airtight self-justification of the drinker — staged as a duet between a man and his own better judgment, where the better judgment never gets a word in.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"denial staged as a duet between a man and his own silenced conscience","built":"split pronoun gives the addict both the bad news and the brush-off answer"},
"annihilator~double-dare": {"haiku":"A predatory figure seduces someone with dares and manipulation, luring them into isolation and implied danger in a dungeon setting.","opus":"A predatory narrator lures a target across a line into his dungeon, taunts braided with genuine loneliness as he tries to pick up the pieces of his life. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' technical thrash project, thread menace with fractured vulnerability here, the repeated dare escalating seduction into threat while a wounded question about being usual leaks through.","sonnet":"A tense invitation with predatory undertones — a dare to come closer, cross a line, be different — masking a deeper isolation about what it means to be ordinary or singular. The stakes rise as the darer reveals their own fragmentation, pieces of a life no one can truly take away.","src":"haiku","fable":"A predator's taunt runs on a schoolyard formula, the childish escalation from dare to double-dare weaponized into the coaxing patter of someone who has already decided a stranger across the room will not walk away alive. Set on an album fixated on the black-widow figure, that goading rhythm turns sinister, every challenge to be different or to draw near really an invitation into a trap dressed as flirtation, a dungeon offered up as a game. Underneath the swagger sits a lonelier confession, the boast about being the only unusual one curdling into self-pity as the speaker gathers the wreckage of a life nobody will ever relieve him of. Seduction and psychosis blur into a single voice by the end, where courtship becomes consummation-as-murder, skin going hot then cold while the taunt asks who exactly got caught.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind"],"means":"schoolyard escalation weaponized into a predator's seduction-as-trap","built":"the boast about uniqueness curdles into self-pity before the kill"},
"annihilator~downright-dominate": {"haiku":"A relentless force of will crushes opposition through superior power, showing no mercy and demanding complete submission from all adversaries.","opus":"A later Annihilator flex of total supremacy, the narrator an invincible reigning champion taking opponents down one by one. The chant of domination and annihilation frames combat as ritual conquest, showing no mercy and casting the beaten foe out from the crown, muscle-flexing thrash bravado.","sonnet":"Total competitive dominance is the subject and the posture. An opponent is dismantled systematically — taken down, beaten into submission, forced to kneel — while the narrator occupies the top position with absolute confidence. Victory is not merely achieved; it is performed with deliberate, merciless authority.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pure dominance anthem with no interior to speak of, and it does not pretend otherwise: the song is a title chant stacked on the language of a sporting rout and a fight to the death, addressing a defeated you who begs, kneels and gets devoured as feast. The one real move is self-referential — must annihilate turns the hook into the band signing its own name, so the crown being defended is less a character than Annihilator as a brand asserting metal supremacy, which is exactly what a late-career album literally called Metal is doing. Read straight, the total absence of doubt or cost is the point; this is muscle-flex genre ritual, not a story.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"band-name-as-hook collapses character and brand into one chant","built":"must annihilate turns the hook into the band signing its own name"},
"annihilator~dr-psycho": {"haiku":"A nightmare scenario of a twisted surgeon or doctor who performs unethical procedures on a terrified patient, causing mutilation and psychological horror.","opus":"A patient terrified before a routine cosmetic procedure becomes the victim of a sadistic surgeon who cuts, extracts part of the brain, and kills rather than heals, trapping them in a waking nightmare.","sonnet":"A patient awaiting what they hoped would be a routine cosmetic procedure instead finds themselves trapped in a nightmare where a sadistic doctor performs a brutal brain extraction, and they can only beg to wake up.","src":"haiku","fable":"A patient on the eve of an elective, vanity-driven operation dreads a routine that is supposed to be harmless, and that dread curdles into a nightmare where the surgeon becomes a predator wielding a scalpel. Annihilator stage the song as two voices trading places, the trembling sleeper begging to wake and the gleeful butcher who rebrands a promise of relief into an open threat against the man's life. The vanity motive lands as grim irony, since the dreamer only wanted a better face yet surfaces to filth and a brain half-extracted, a body that can never be restored to what it was.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind"],"means":"vanity-driven dread curdling into a nightmare where relief becomes threat","built":"two voices trade places, the sleeper and the butcher swapping the same microphone"},
"annihilator~drive": {"haiku":"A speaker pursues relentless personal ambition through sacrifice and isolation, refusing others' doubts and transforming fear into momentum toward success.","opus":"Annihilator trade menace for motivational grit, the narrator forging ahead down a solitary path with nothing to lose and a point to prove. Converting dread into appetite and struggle into all-out conflict, he declares defeat off the table, an anthem of self-propelled ambition aimed squarely at the naysayers.","sonnet":"Solitary determination pushes forward against doubt, isolation, and the dismissal of others. The narrator converts fear into hunger and failure into motivation, insisting on his own path even when the road is lonely. Resilience here is not triumphant but stubborn — earned one day at a time.","src":"haiku","fable":"A self-motivation anthem built as a first-person pep talk, where the singer answers every discouraging voice by refusing to negotiate, insisting he pursues his own aims regardless of who tries to steer him elsewhere. The imagery keeps circling one figure alone on a long stretch of highway, isolation reframed as chosen solitude rather than abandonment, the loneliness and sacrifice recast as the toll a driven person willingly pays. Its engine is transformation: dread converted into appetite, a private struggle escalated deliberately into open warfare, until momentum builds and the destination feels inevitable rather than merely hoped for. The final turn insists nothing arrives as a gift and every setback is simply barred, hardening the whole into a grimly militant creed that success must be seized outright.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"dread converted into appetite as the highway's loneliness is chosen","built":"isolation reframed as elected solitude rather than abandonment"},
"annihilator~epic-of-war": {"haiku":"The psychological and physical devastation of combat, following a soldier's horror at witnessing mass death and moral collapse under orders to kill.","opus":"A battlefield nightmare in which the narrator, his mother's only child, drops to his knees in prayer amid the carnage, revolted by orders to keep killing for a general who never stops demanding more dead. The chorus paints war as a cultivated field of hate, flesh, and blood. Annihilator channel Jeff Waters' precision into an anti-war lament grounded in the soldier's flat refusal.","sonnet":"Combat horror from the ground level — a soldier surrounded by hundreds of thousands of dead, ordered to keep killing while the general calls for more. Prayers replace tactics, brothers bleed out beside him, and the only desire left is to see another day, spoken by someone who knows he is his mother's only son and that this fact means nothing to the war.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator build the horror through a chanted litany that swaps its noun each round, marching from war to flesh to blood to torture so the battlefield curdles from a place you enter into a body being dismantled, sensory verbs daring you to smell and taste what you would rather not. Between those liturgical blocks a lone conscript speaks plainly, praying to a silent heaven and counting the crimson-skied dead while a commander demands the toll be doubled and the objective stays the same futile ridge. The turn lands when he lays down his weapon and swears off the next casualty, only to be flung forward regardless, groping toward a wounded comrade whose breath he cannot find, remembering he is the single child his mother raised. That naming of the mother collapses the abstract slaughter into one specific, irreplaceable loss, which is where the song finally earns its scale.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","family & blood"],"means":"abstract slaughter collapsed into one named mother","built":"liturgical noun-swap marches battlefield into dismembered body"},
"annihilator~fiasco": {"haiku":"A speaker mocks someone consumed by jealousy and paranoia, calling out their obsessive and destructive behavior as a pathetic failure.","opus":"The narrator mocks a jealous, obsessed rival as a paranoid schizo whose messy life is pure insecurity, feigning nice-guy magnanimity while twisting the knife. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' thrash outfit, keep the tone sneering and playful, forgiveness offered only as a goodbye, the whole taunt built to belittle an antagonist consumed by his own scandal-hunting.","sonnet":"A dismissal of a jealous, paranoid adversary whose obsession with tearing the speaker down only exposes their own insecurity. The response is contemptuous amusement rather than anger — the other person's life is the real mess, and the speaker chooses a cheerful goodbye over any grudge.","src":"haiku","fable":"A gleeful kiss-off to an obsessive hater, sung entirely as taunt: the narrator diagnoses his critic as jealous, paranoid, coming apart at the seams, while pointedly framing his own success as the thing driving that unraveling. The venom lives in the fake magnanimity of the bridge, where the pose of being a forgiving nice guy immediately curdles into a curt goodbye and an audible laugh in the listener's face. This is Jeff Waters writing in the mid-90s, after Annihilator's own label and lineup churn, aiming a groove-metal sneer squarely at the industry chatter and rumor-mongers, so the title cuts both ways: the fiasco is nominally the enemy's imploding life, but it is just as much the narrator's contempt for the whole circus of people talking him down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"fake magnanimity curdling into a laugh in the listener's face","built":"the title cuts both ways, naming the enemy's implosion and the narrator's circus"},
"annihilator~fight-the-world": {"haiku":"Anxiety, panic, and stress nearly destroy the narrator, but raw determination to overcome internal darkness drives him to keep fighting back.","opus":"A plainspoken account of anxiety and depression as a daily combat, waking already in decay, tempted by substances to cope. Its turn is small but real: choosing to pick yourself up rather than surrender the fight.","sonnet":"About living with severe anxiety and depression and choosing to resist rather than surrender — the narrator describes panic attacks, isolation, and the pull of substance abuse, but ultimately decides to fight rather than give in, framing recovery as a battle.","src":"haiku","fable":"A thrash blueprint gets repurposed as a survival chant: the enemy here is not an outside foe but the grinding weight of dragging oneself through another morning, and the battle-cry title reframes ordinary endurance as open combat. The middle section darkens toward funeral language and a churning dread that keeps promising to arrive, the worst of it never quite landing, which becomes its own slow torture. Where Jeff Waters usually aims that aggression at technical showmanship, the closing turn flips collapse into refusal, choosing to haul the self upright rather than reach for anything that numbs, so the final chant reads as raw defiance rather than complaint.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"choosing to haul the self upright rather than reach for what numbs","built":"battle-cry title reframes ordinary daily endurance as open combat"},
"annihilator~fire-power": {"haiku":"A speaker embodies unstoppable warlike destructive energy, conquering and consuming all in their path with demonic ferocity and ambition.","opus":"Sung from inside the imagination of an unstoppable military force bent on total conquest. It is pure thrash spectacle, a narrator promising annihilation and global rule with no restraint, glorying in destruction for its own sake rather than making any real point.","sonnet":"A war machine advances on a mission of total domination, pledging allegiance to nothing but its own conquest. There are no prisoners, no survivors, only precision force and an unending appetite for control. The narrator inhabits the destroyer completely, offering world domination as its own sufficient justification.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters builds the whole song as one uninterrupted battlefield boast, a conqueror narrating his own advance so relentlessly that the speaking voice becomes indistinguishable from the ordnance it commands. The single hammered title word standing between verses works like a detonation, a point where language collapses into weaponized noise before the assault picks back up and rolls forward. What lends it a colder edge is the closing turn, where mass slaughter gets dressed in the borrowed solemnity of a soldier's oath and a boundless claim on ruling everyone, exposing the tyrant's self-image as sacred duty rather than raw appetite.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"the conqueror reframes mass slaughter as sacred soldier's duty","built":"the hammered title word detonates between verses as weaponized silence"},
"annihilator~gallery": {"haiku":"A surreal, nightmarish descent into madness and death framed as a grotesque gallery or exhibition.","opus":"A macabre carnival-of-horrors invitation, ushering the listener deeper among grotesque exhibits where only the strong endure a deadly trial and the tour ends in the visitor's own demise. From Annihilator's early catalogue, Jeff Waters builds a haunted-house conceit, technical riffing wrapped in creepshow theatrics meant purely to unsettle.","sonnet":"A guided tour of an inner gallery of horrors — grotesque exhibits that materialise your fears and invite you past the point of no return. The song blurs between carnival barker and predator, each room more dangerous, the invitation to go insane and die offered as though it were entertainment, the psychic revelation being that you walked in willingly.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator frame the song as a carnival barker's tour, ushering a naive listener deeper into an exhibit of horrors where survival is staged as a trial rather than a promise of safety. Jeff Waters exploits the thrash tradition of the guided descent, turning the beckoning invitation and the pulse hammering through the bloodstream into a lure that curdles the instant the destination reveals itself as a death sentence. The closing turn drops the tour-guide mask entirely, collapsing the spectacle into a private nightmare of a shut room and a silent scream, so the grotesque proves to be the visitor's own mind rather than any painting hung on the wall.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","artifice & performance","violence & murder"],"means":"carnival tour curdles when the exhibit proves to be the visitor","built":"tour-guide mask dropped in the final verse to reveal a private nightmare"},
"annihilator~haunted": {"haiku":"An institutionalized person is tormented by voices, injected with drugs, and haunted by demons; they cannot escape this hell.","opus":"An asylum inmate endures a torment of hallucinated voices and demons, then is lured by a comforting presence he should never have trusted, only to wake sealed alive in a grave. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' thrash outfit, stage it as a horror set piece, that fatal moment of trust turning institutional terror into something genuinely ghoulish.","sonnet":"Institutional horror from inside — locked in, injected, blinded by overhead lights, hearing screams down corridors, unsure whether the white-coated figures are helpers or predators. A reassuring voice says trust me, follow the light; waking up proves the trust was wrong. The self has been buried in its own tomb, and the demons the speaker begged to have removed are still there.","src":"haiku","fable":"A mind held in an institution narrates its own unmaking, the confinement framed as a life sentence and the surrounding cries bleeding through corridors into a terror that freezes rather than resolves. Annihilator, long fixated on the psychology of the cornered and the criminally insane across Jeff Waters' catalog, sets a clinical scene of restraint and sedation where a surgical blade drifting closer turns medical care into predatory threat. The bridge flips perspective entirely, a soothing voice promising release and offering a hand the numbed narrator half-believes, so the comfort reads as a final seduction toward extinction rather than any real rescue. That is the cruelest turn, the pain lifting only because sensation itself is being switched off, and the waking passage confirms the betrayal as the ground gives way and burial closes over a body that never consented.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","death & grief","surveillance & control"],"means":"comfort offered only as the route to final extinction","built":"a soothing bridge voice that flips medical rescue into seduction toward death"},
"annihilator~hell-is-a-war": {"haiku":"War and suffering as hellish—people dying, children suffering, media feeding on death, ethnic cleansing and religious violence turned into a nightmarish combat zone.","opus":"A frightened child orphaned by conflict pleads for someone to care, while the narrative widens to ethnic cleansing, profiteering, and a soldier killing in religion's name. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' Canadian thrash band, turn socially conscious here, indicting politicians and war itself, the television glowing red as bodies are collected one by one.","sonnet":"A child orphaned by conflict shelters in darkness while the machinery of war — money, power, television, ethnic cleansing — grinds on around them. The lyric cycles between that small frightened voice and a sweeping condemnation of politicians who allow atrocity to be processed like routine administration.","src":"haiku","fable":"A child orphaned and hiding from bombardment gives the song its recurring voice, a small terrified plea that the surrounding adult world keeps failing to hear, answer, or even acknowledge as real. Between those returns the frame widens toward greed, political ambition, and a television glowing bloody while the dead are tallied one at a time as though they were ordinary daily news. The horror sharpens when perspective flips into the trigger-puller who fires by faith and self-justification, so a single catastrophe appears in turn as helpless victim, as numb spectacle, and finally as executioner. Annihilator, working the aggressive thrash idiom, aims its demand squarely at the politician whose signature converts abstract slaughter into policy, leaving the unanswered child as the only honest verdict on the whole machine.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"a child's unanswered plea as verdict on the whole war machine","built":"perspective rotates through victim, spectator, and executioner in sequence"},
"annihilator~holding-on": {"haiku":"Emotional support in a struggling relationship, where one partner promises to protect and stay with the other through difficult times despite personal pain.","opus":"A quiet vow of endurance, the narrator finding it harder to go on alone yet drawing purpose from someone he refuses to leave frightened, hiding his own tears and promising to protect them and see things through together. It is tenderness rather than menace. Annihilator, better known for technical thrash, ease off here, the Canadian band letting Jeff Waters' songwriting reach toward something gentle and consoling.","sonnet":"A parent or partner steadying themselves for someone they love who is frightened — the speaker is hollow and exhausted but refuses to let the other person see the depth of it. The promise made is simple and absolute: I will be there, we will be alright, and I am holding on because you need me to.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rare tender turn for Jeff Waters, whose Annihilator built its name on serrated thrash riffing rather than the plainspoken devotion that steers this ballad about staying upright purely for someone else's sake. The singer masks his own fear behind reassurance, deflecting a loved one's worried questions so the weight stays his alone rather than spreading to the frightened person he means to shield. What opens as isolation reverses into a shared vow, the final lines trading solitary struggle for a togetherness he offers as the one thing that makes endurance feel possible.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"masking fear behind reassurance so the weight stays his alone","built":"solitary endurance reverses into a shared vow at the final lines"},
"annihilator~human-insecticide": {"haiku":"A deranged person hunts and destroys small creatures with obsessive intensity. They relish extermination and feel empowered by their deadly dominance.","opus":"An early Annihilator plunge into a psychotic mind that reduces human beings to vermin to be exterminated, the killer's grandiose compulsion mistaking murder for pest control; horror in how coolly the rationalization proceeds.","sonnet":"Told from the perspective of someone with violent psychotic obsessions framed as pest control — the narrator fantasizes in clinical detail about exterminating the people he sees as vermin, reveling in the act as a form of power.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters builds a killer's self-portrait on one sustained conceit: the exterminator, who has downgraded his victims into vermin so the slaughter reads as tidy household maintenance rather than murder. The pesticide vocabulary becomes the alibi, letting the swatting of a flea and the smothering of a life share the same untroubled grammar of routine pest control. What curdles the boast is the bridge, where the hunter concedes his own fate is bound to the prey he stalks, so every kill quietly tightens the same trap around him. That admission turns a swaggering thrash brag into something nearer a confession, the exterminator too far gone to notice he is describing his own confinement.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind"],"means":"extermination as household maintenance, the alibi sustained until the trap closes on its user","built":"the bridge concedes the hunter's fate is bound to the prey, turning the brag into a confession"},
"annihilator~human-insecticide-live": {"haiku":"Psychotic containment and violent catharsis where small things become executors of internal will. The narrator becomes judge over vermin, finding control through systematic elimination.","opus":"Jeff Waters' early speed-metal character study: an asylum inmate who reclaims a sliver of power by playing judge and executioner over the insects in his cell. It's madness dressed as authority, cruelty as the last thing a caged man controls.","sonnet":"Narrated from inside a psych ward, an institutionalised man finds sadistic power in exterminating insects — his only domain of control — enacting retaliation, decapitation, and annihilation on the tiny creatures while powerless against his human captors.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters builds a killer's self-portrait on one sustained conceit: the exterminator, who has downgraded his victims into vermin so the slaughter reads as tidy household maintenance rather than murder. The pesticide vocabulary becomes the alibi, letting the swatting of a flea and the smothering of a life share the same untroubled grammar of routine pest control. What curdles the boast is the bridge, where the hunter concedes his own fate is bound to the prey he stalks, so every kill quietly tightens the same trap around him. That admission turns a swaggering thrash brag into something nearer a confession, the exterminator too far gone to notice he is describing his own confinement.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"exterminator's alibi quietly tightens a trap around himself","built":"bridge concedes the hunter's fate is bound to the prey, turning brag into confession"},
"annihilator~human-remains": {"haiku":"Aging, marginalization, and the futility of human existence as we inevitably become forgotten remnants.","opus":"The title track of Annihilator's 1994 album finds the Canadian thrash band reaching for unusual restraint under Jeff Waters' craft. It portrays an aging man left out in the cold, mocked and sidelined but not yet gone, who once met his fears with hope. The warning lands quietly: everyone eventually becomes that discarded figure, life proving as fragile as a single thread.","sonnet":"An old man standing in the snow, past the point of relevance, insults flying over his head — and the quiet revelation that everyone eventually becomes him. What fills you with dread today is only preparation for the moment you are the figure in the snow yourself, the game unchanged, life a thread, only the human remains left behind.","src":"haiku","fable":"A discarded elder stands freezing outside the arena while insults sail past him, sidelined from some contest yet still breathing, and the title's pun carries the argument: what is human remains, endures, refuses to vanish. Jeff Waters, driving this Remains-era material almost single-handedly against a drum machine, folds the cast-off figure into a warning that every listener inherits that same exposed post eventually, whatever private terror waits at its edge. The closing turn flattens both competition and existence into thin, rigged mechanics, a fraying thread and a fixed match, so the survival the phrase seemed to promise curdles into mere leftover persistence rather than any triumph.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","death & grief"],"means":"survival promised by the title curdles into mere leftover persistence","built":"title pun pivots endurance into a warning every listener inherits"},
"annihilator~hunger": {"haiku":"A predatory force feeds on anger and human suffering, taking ecstatic pleasure in blood and death.","opus":"Annihilator give voice to a predatory horror that gorges on rage and wickedness, tearing apart the bonds between people while posing as comfort to the dying. Deceitful and craving to be worshipped through blood, it fantasizes about devouring the sun in an endless nightmare with no escape. Jeff Waters' technical thrash lends the monologue its menace, the villain relishing every kill.","sonnet":"A predatory entity — feeding on rage, disguised among the harmless, dreaming of swallowing the sun — describes its appetite for destruction in escalating detail. The target is human weakness itself; the pleasure is absolute and without mercy.","src":"haiku","fable":"A predator narrates its own appetite, feeding on rage and cruelty until the meal becomes a devotional rite where victims are begged into loving their killer. Jeff Waters writes the monster from inside, so seduction and slaughter share one voice; the disguise no one penetrates makes the human offering embrace its own end. The craving then scales past mortal prey toward a devouring so total it would blot out daylight itself, a boast whose real menace is that no meal can ever fill it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"seduction and slaughter share one voice so victims embrace their own end","built":"appetite scales past mortal prey toward blotting out daylight itself"},
"annihilator~hunter-killer": {"haiku":"Predatory violence and sexual dominance, with the narrator positioned as a relentless hunter stalking and consuming their victim.","opus":"A predator's cold boast, patient and quiet as it stalks the frail and sickly, savoring their terror before killing indifferently and vowing to run its quarry down and drain them dry. It's menace reduced to raw hunger. Annihilator, built around Jeff Waters' exacting riffs, lean into serial-killer horror, the driving refrain making the pursuer feel like instinct rather than a man.","sonnet":"Pure predator — silent, patient, locked on prey, moving through fear like a scent. There's no psychology here, only the mechanics of the hunt: sight acquired, resistance impossible, the kill completed with satisfaction and no remorse.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person predator monologue that deliberately never resolves into metaphor: the speaker is pure appetite, and the song's method is to keep him abstract enough that he could be a beast, a serial killer, or a war machine at once. What gives it teeth is the slide from stalking to need—the hunt is framed less as violence than as compulsion, the target something the speaker claims he cannot stop wanting—so predation shades into a kind of possessive craving. Annihilator's Jeff Waters builds the payoff structurally rather than lyrically: the closing turn drops from full sentences into the bare repetition of hunting the victim down, the language degrading into the single-minded chant of something that has stopped thinking and is now only pursuing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"predation framed as compulsion the speaker claims he cannot stop","built":"closing lines degrade from full sentences into bare repetition — language shrinks as the hunt narrows"},
"annihilator~i-am-in-command": {"haiku":"A person seizes power by rejecting religious authority and collective control, positioning themselves as dominant over others and free from institutional systems.","opus":"A militant renunciation of organized religion, the narrator declaring himself free of the church he paints as a fraud that bleeds its flock. It's a defiant seizure of self-command against clergy greed and manufactured belief.","sonnet":"A defiant rejection of religious authority and the church as an institution of control — the narrator refuses to submit to God or clergy, positioning himself as the sovereign commander of his own life, mocking those who surrendered their autonomy to an organization that bleeds them dry.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters turns Annihilator's technical thrash into an anticlerical broadside, its narrator declaring himself sovereign over a church he paints as a collection plate dressed up as salvation. The argument builds from personal survival toward a wider indictment: congregations become livestock, offerings become extortion, and a silent heaven has dismissed its faithful like redundant workers from a bankrupt firm. What steadies the boast is the late turn toward instruction, the speaker crediting an allied movement he schooled rather than a lone ego, so defiance of institutional greed lands as shared refusal instead of mere satanic bravado.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"anticlerical defiance grounded in collective refusal rather than lone satanic bravado","built":"the boast steadies when the speaker credits a movement he schooled, not his own ego"},
"annihilator~i-want": {"haiku":"A manipulative con artist who exploits and steals from others through deception, ultimately facing karmic justice and downfall.","opus":"A gleeful villain's boast from a self-proclaimed sorcerer who lords over the land, cons and manipulates people for sport, robs the naive by blinding them, and crows about his own invincibility, right up until his fortress collapses and the loathsome schemer meets his reckoning. From Annihilator's catalogue, Jeff Waters spins a swaggering morality tale over sharp thrash, the con man toppled by his own hubris.","sonnet":"A portrait of a manipulator at peak invincibility — stealing from innocents, lying effortlessly, ruling through deception — until the castle walls come down and the magic man is exposed as a selfish maniac about to receive exactly what he deserves. The chorus flips on its final repetition: you can always stop him, even if catching him is another matter.","src":"haiku","fable":"A swaggering confidence trickster narrates his own scam, boasting that he fleeces gullible marks for sport and crowns himself an untouchable ruler of a world he insists can never bring him low. Jeff Waters lets the braggart run long enough to rot, then the final verse topples the illusion as the fortress collapses and the strutting deceiver is unmasked as a small, poisonous predator who receives exactly the reckoning he had earned. The tell sits in one quietly flipped taunt, since the refrain that earlier crowed no one could ever bring him down finally concedes he was always catchable the instant his victims quit swallowing the lie.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"the braggart's own refrain exposes him the instant victims stop believing","built":"the boast that no one could bring him down finally concedes catchability"},
"annihilator~imperiled-eyes": {"haiku":"War and waste have killed the earth and left humanity on death row; as the planet becomes barren and lifeless, the speaker awakens shocked to open imperiled eyes.","opus":"Jeff Waters channels his Canadian thrash outfit toward apocalyptic elegy, watching a ravaged earth rot under centuries of war and waste until nothing remains to heal. The dying voice paradoxically feels revived as it fades, jolted awake at the end of everything. Blame dissolves because no one survives to assign it, and false wisdom becomes the epitaph for a species that annihilated its own home.","sonnet":"A survivor opens their eyes on a dead planet, processing the aftermath of humanity's self-inflicted extinction. The lyric consciousness moves between shock and grim clarity — no color remains in the sky, no one survives to assign blame — and in that terrible vacancy a single moment of revival feels like mockery rather than grace.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator's Jeff Waters stages a planetary autopsy, tallying generations of ruin until the soil turns rotten and the whole species awaits sentencing on a globe stripped down to an abandoned building lot. The apocalyptic thrash of Never, Neverland turns morbid in a way the genre rarely allows, because extinction here arrives not as spectacle but as a slow flatline where even dying feels unreal and the horizon drains of every hue. The strange twist is the narrator's revival mid-collapse, gasping awake as the planet expires, so that clear sight comes only once the damage is total and beyond any repair. That terminal clarity is the cruel joke driving the song, since grasping the cost of arrogance and squandered power lands precisely when no wisdom can undo it or restore what once teemed and thrived.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nature & the elements","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"clarity arrives precisely when no wisdom can undo the damage","built":"narrator revives mid-collapse so clear sight lands only at total extinction"},
"annihilator~in-the-blood": {"haiku":"Destructive impulses flow genetically through a speaker's veins, driving cycles of harm and pain that repeat across relationships through inherited nature.","opus":"Annihilator cast cruelty as something inherited and inescapable, the narrator claiming an urge coursing through him that drives him mad. Having wrecked a lover and left them in ruins, he swears he will do the same again, treating his own destructiveness as a trait bred into him rather than a choice.","sonnet":"A compulsive cycle of destruction in intimate relationships is framed as something inherited and inescapable. The narrator tears apart what he loves, builds walls through hearts, and suffers for it — but cannot stop, because the pattern runs deeper than will. It is constitutive, cellular, ancestral.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is destruction confessed as heredity, a man insisting his cruelty is bloodline rather than choice. He pairs every gentle thing with its predator and every glow with its shadow, then plants a grief-soaked barrier through the person who trusted him. The chorus turns that violence into a pulse in the veins, a compulsion he has enacted before and vows to repeat. Jeff Waters wrote King of the Kill amid the label and lineup turmoil of the mid-nineties, and the refusal to apologize plays like defiance dressed as fate. What surfaces beneath the fatalism is a plainer admission: he cannot promise to change, so he rebrands the wreckage as nature.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","family & blood","shame & repression"],"means":"cruelty rebranded as heredity so no apology ever has to arrive","built":"the chorus plants violence in the pulse as a compulsion, not a choice"},
"annihilator~innocent-eyes": {"haiku":"A parent sees their newborn child as transformative and purposeful, believing the child to be a messenger showing them life's true meaning.","opus":"A startling tender turn from a thrash band, a parent gazing into a newborn's innocent eyes, vowing to always be there, marveling that the child answers what life is all for and seems almost like a messenger. Annihilator, usually trafficking in violence and horror, let Jeff Waters strip everything back here for genuine paternal awe, the promise to hold and shield the baby carrying an unguarded sincerity.","sonnet":"A parent's devotion articulated the moment a child is born — the newborn reordering everything, answering the question of what any of it is for. The worry about the future is present but secondary to the overwhelming pull of protection and wonder. The child is described as almost a messenger, as though their arrival carried knowledge the parent needed but couldn't have found alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A thrash band's unguarded love letter to a newborn, its tenderness the whole point coming from a genre built on menace. The speaker frames the child less as someone he protects than as someone who has rescued him: the baby is the answer to what it's all for, even a kind of messenger, so the direction of care quietly inverts and the parent is the one being taught and saved. What starts as a vow to shield an innocent from harm ends admitting the child stole his heart from the first day, love arriving as something closer to helpless surrender than guardianship.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","love & desire"],"means":"the infant rescuing the parent who meant to do the rescuing","built":"direction of care quietly inverts — guardian becomes the one being taught and saved"},
"annihilator~insomniac": {"haiku":"Severe insomnia and mental anguish from racing thoughts that prevent sleep and induce psychological torment.","opus":"A visceral rendering of sleeplessness as agony, the sufferer thrashing in the dark as his thoughts race in circles, burning heat registering as cold, unsure whether he's reached death or damnation and dreading the next surge the instant his eyes shut. From Annihilator's catalogue, Jeff Waters' Canadian thrash outfit turns insomnia into a physical predator, a wide-awake nightmare.","sonnet":"The body unable to shut down — mind racing, fever running cold, the ground rushing up even as you try to rest. The song maps the loop insomnia creates: the harder you try to sleep the more awake you become, the attack always preparing to reset, the escape into unconsciousness snatched away the moment your eyes close.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sleeplessness is staged as a thrash-metal assault, the racing mind becoming an enemy that circles and strikes rather than a passive condition to be quietly endured through the night. Annihilator, whose Jeff Waters built the band's reputation on relentless technical precision, mirrors that restlessness in the writing: the body burns and shivers at once, forced to its knees by an adversary it can neither see nor fight. The cruelest turn arrives at the edge of surrender, where shutting the eyes buys no release and consciousness snaps back, so exhaustion loops endlessly and rest itself becomes the one thing that keeps slipping away.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind"],"means":"exhaustion loops endlessly because rest itself keeps slipping away","built":"thrash precision mirrors the racing mind it describes"},
"annihilator~invite-it": {"haiku":"Panic and paranoia spiraling into hysteria and obsession. The speaker learns to stop fighting mental chaos and instead invite it in, discovering that acceptance paradoxically breaks the cycle.","opus":"Annihilator stage a panic attack and drug-fueled terror, whispers echoing inside the skull and a heated surge racing down the back until hysteria takes hold with no refuge anywhere. The twisted advice is that embracing the dread disarms it. Jeff Waters' band renders mental collapse as vivid thrash horror, charting paranoia and a caged soul in queasy detail.","sonnet":"A panic attack rendered as a first-person descent into terror — racing heart, spinning thoughts, paranoia, the certainty of death. The hard-won insight at the close is counterintuitive: resisting the panic prolongs it, and learning to welcome the feeling is the only way through.","src":"haiku","fable":"A panic attack narrated from the inside, structured as the counterintuitive coping strategy its title names: not resist but welcome the terror, because a wave you stop fighting is a wave that loses its grip. The song tracks the classic escalation clinically — the warm rush down the spine dismissed as routine, then the sickening certainty that this time it's the real thing, the racing heart and the animal urge to run from a threat that has no location to flee. Its cruelty is the mind attacking itself, a war with no exit because you are both the hunted and the terrain. The final turn is hard-won rather than triumphant: surrender is the only tactic that works, so the payoff line lands less as victory than as a survivor's grim instruction — stop resisting and the panic, denied the fight it feeds on, cannot win.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"surrender offered as the only tactic against a panic that feeds on resistance","built":"the counterintuitive coping strategy is the title itself, naming the whole method"},
"annihilator~it-s-you-bonus": {"haiku":"Unrequited love and obsessive devotion, where the narrator needs reassurance that they are the answer to someone's longing.","opus":"An unexpectedly gentle love ballad from a band known for aggressive Canadian thrash. The narrator sits alone in the dark, unsure whether his feelings are wise or foolish, and pleads that only she can settle his restless heart. A vulnerable departure from the group usual speed and fury.","sonnet":"A devotional with nowhere to put its certainty — waiting by the phone, unable to know if the feeling is right or reciprocated, but unable to stop returning. The song builds through its doubt to something close to conviction: from the start it was this person, the answer has always been yes, and the only remaining question is whether they will say so.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rare tender turn from a band Jeff Waters built on precision thrash, this pleads for certainty in love when no outside voice can supply it, stranding the singer beside a silent telephone through a long anxious night. The wager he keeps circling is a vow unlike any he has staked on another person, pinned to someone who might shove him off yet somehow cannot shake his stubborn returning. By the close the frantic questioning hardens into conviction: the one being asked already holds the verdict, and the answer lodged where reason cannot reach turns out to be simply her.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire"],"means":"certainty reached not by reasoning but by stubborn returning","built":"frantic questioning hardens into conviction at the moment outside voices go silent"},
"annihilator~kicked": {"haiku":"A betrayer stabs their victim in the back; their lies steal trust and prey on weakness, but eventually the victim rises and drags them down to the dirt.","opus":"A revenge tale aimed at a backstabbing traitor, a conscienceless thief skilled in warm-faced betrayal, until the tables flip and the deceiver winds up rotting in the ground. Annihilator, the Canadian thrash mainstay, hang the song on a warning about the treacherous friend, turning betrayal into a promise of comeuppance where the wronged return to torment their betrayer.","sonnet":"Betrayal by someone trusted utterly, now reframed as a warning to others — the Judas figure who steals, deceives with warmth, leaves victims face-down in the dirt. The turn halfway through is a shift from victim to avenger: the one betrayed has hardened, and now the betrayer is the one falling to pieces. The promise is that this particular debt will be collected, publicly, completely.","src":"haiku","fable":"A betrayal song built on the oldest name for the crime, the disciple who sold his teacher for silver, invoked as a standing warning about the charming grifter who trades on trust and walks away from the wreckage. Jeff Waters tracks the con through its full arc, from the sucker driven face-down into the earth to the smooth operator peddling ruin with a handshake, certain no reckoning is coming. Then the power reverses: the swindler ends up caged and coming apart at the seams, hounded in sleep by the people he ruined, so the menace that opened the song becomes a debt his marks fully intend to collect.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","money & the street"],"means":"the betrayer hunted by victims whose debt he forgot he owed","built":"Judas named at the open pins the con to its oldest archetype"},
"annihilator~king-of-the-kill": {"haiku":"A predatory beast dominates its territory through violence and fear, stalking prey and demanding absolute submission or death.","opus":"A predator's boast voiced as an apex beast that rules the jungle as judge, jury and executioner, stalking prey with no mercy and eating victims alive; read metaphorically it's a portrait of unchecked dominance and ego-being feared as the one no one escapes.","sonnet":"A predator's boast told from the perspective of an apex hunter — the song inhabits the voice of a jungle beast that rules by hunger and terror, taking lives at will and answering to no one.","src":"haiku","fable":"A predator narrates its own supremacy, and the song commits fully to the mask: an apex beast prowling its territory, claiming absolute power over who lives and who dies. Jeff Waters writes it as pure thrash spectacle, the menace theatrical rather than psychological, closer to a horror-movie monster than any real confession. What the relentless refrain exposes underneath is emptiness dressed as dominance. The endless repetition of the title boast, the vow that no prey escapes, is the beast mistaking volume for meaning, terror curdling into cartoon the harder it insists on being feared.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"relentless dominance boast revealing emptiness the harder it insists","built":"the title refrain repeated to the point of curdling into cartoon"},
"annihilator~knight-jumps-queen": {"haiku":"Two competitors engage in an intense chess match where intellect and strategic preparation determine the winner as they move toward checkmate.","opus":"From Annihilator's razor-sharp debut, this thrash set piece stages a full chess game as psychological warfare. The narrator pushes pawns and studies his rival, relishing the buildup toward a decisive moment when the knight claims the queen and victory is joyfully announced, crumbling castles and toppled bishops carrying a duel of minds through to the game's spent conclusion.","sonnet":"A chess match unfolds as an extended metaphor for a battle of wills between two evenly matched opponents, each calculating moves and sacrifices. The game stretches to near exhaustion before the knight claims victory over the queen — an intimate triumph declared with strategic pleasure.","src":"haiku","fable":"A chess match staged as a duel of nerves carries the whole song, the board standing in for two adversaries circling each other while every advance and capture carries a wink of double meaning. Jeff Waters keeps the metaphor deliberately porous, letting the innuendo of one piece toppling another blur into something bodily, so the rivalry reads as much like seduction or conquest as it does pure strategy. What lifts it past a novelty conceit is the late admission that a worthy opponent has finally arrived, the marathon dragging toward deadlock before one side seizes the decisive move and the darkened fortress settles into quiet.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","love & desire"],"means":"chess rivalry kept porous enough to read as seduction or conquest","built":"piece-takes-piece carries bodily double meaning throughout"},
"annihilator~kraf-dinner": {"haiku":"Poverty and humble subsistence celebrated with ironic enthusiasm, making virtue of scarcity by elevating cheap comfort to status symbol.","opus":"Jeff Waters playing thrash for laughs, a broke musician's ode to cheap boxed macaroni as the whole of his diet. The joke rides on real want, hunger and no money turned into gleeful junk-food worship.","sonnet":"A gleeful ode to Kraft Dinner macaroni and cheese — cupboard bare, boiling water, cheddar cheese heart attack, planning to buy a crate. Nothing more, nothing less.","src":"haiku","fable":"A joke thrash miniature staged from an empty pantry, where a broke narrator turns cheap powdered noodles into the object of a ravenous, near-addictive craving that keeps escalating toward physical seizure. The comedy runs entirely on scale: Jeff Waters aims a technical Canadian speed-metal band's precision at a student's default meal, treating boxed macaroni like contraband a junkie would score by the case. Its arc is pure appetite and comedown, the frenzy of the wait giving way to a bloated, sedated calm before the craving reloads and the closing chant collapses to one hammered demand for the fix right now.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","comedy & wordplay","money & the street"],"means":"technical thrash precision aimed at a student's cheapest meal","built":"craving reloads after the comedown, cycling back to the opening demand"},
"annihilator~kraf-dinner-demo": {"haiku":"A whimsical obsession with cheap pasta and cheese—joy found in filling empty cupboards with quick, satisfying meals.","opus":"An early Annihilator joke song, broke and starving and worshipping boxed macaroni like a drug. The gag is affection for cheap comfort food pushed to mock-addiction, thrash metal aimed squarely at nothing serious.","sonnet":"A straightforward comedic ode to Kraft Dinner macaroni and cheese as comfort food — broke, hungry, and fully addicted to the cheap orange pasta, with zero pretense of deeper meaning.","src":"haiku","fable":"A joke thrash miniature staged from an empty pantry, where a broke narrator turns cheap powdered noodles into the object of a ravenous, near-addictive craving that keeps escalating toward physical seizure. The comedy runs entirely on scale: Jeff Waters aims a technical Canadian speed-metal band's precision at a student's default meal, treating boxed macaroni like contraband a junkie would score by the case. Its arc is pure appetite and comedown, the frenzy of the wait giving way to a bloated, sedated calm before the craving reloads and the closing chant collapses to one hammered demand for the fix right now.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["comedy & wordplay","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"thrash precision aimed at boxed macaroni treats hunger as a junkie's craving","built":"speed-metal scale applied to a student's default meal is the entire joke"},
"annihilator~ligeia": {"haiku":"A man grieves the death of his beautiful wife Ligeia, remarries, then witnesses what seems to be Ligeia's spirit or resurrection in his new bride's body.","opus":"A retelling of Poe's tale: a widower obsessed with his mystically perfect dead first love Ligeia remarries after her death, only for his new bride's corpse to rise from her deathbed transformed, revealing Ligeia's eyes, love and grief defeating death itself.","sonnet":"A retelling of Poe's story: a man loses his perfect, learned wife Ligeia to illness, remarries out of grief, watches his second wife die — and then in a horrifying final moment sees Ligeia's eyes open in the second wife's corpse.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters sets Poe's tale of the same name to thrash, tracking a widower whose will refuses to let the dead stay buried. The narrator can barely recall meeting his first love, yet her image outlasts every faded memory, worshipped as a lost perfection he cannot stop mourning. Remarried in grief and opium haze, he watches the second wife sicken and die as if his devotion were a curse the living cannot survive. Then the horror lands: the corpse stirs and stares back with the first wife's eyes. Obsessive love does not resurrect the beloved here so much as overwrite the new bride's body with her ghost.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","love & desire","faith & the occult"],"means":"obsession that cannot let the dead stay buried, overwriting the living with a ghost","built":"the second wife's death reads as the first wife's devotion operating as a curse on the living"},
"annihilator~like-father-like-gun": {"haiku":"Cycles of violence propagate through generations bred on hate and fear, justified by religious authority, perpetuating suffering and trauma.","opus":"A furious indictment of children raised to kill, fed lies and rigged with explosives, marched into the streets as death squads justify slaughter in the name of religion. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' thrash band, target the cycle of inherited hatred and religious extremism, pleading that a doomed generation of the damned be spared and allowed to live free of the wars handed down to them.","sonnet":"Generational violence passed down like a trade — children born into cycles of hate and propaganda, trained to carry on what their fathers carried, justified by religion and ideology until killing becomes structural. The song mourns the children of the damned without sentimentalizing them; the disease spreads across the land because no one with power chooses to stop it. The title says the whole thing in four words.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator traces how militant hatred gets inherited like a bloodline, a boy raised on doctrine and grievance until brutality feels less like a choice than an heirloom passed quietly down through the generations. The title welds parent to weapon, arguing that indoctrination begins at the family table long before it ever reaches the marching street or the incendiary charge strapped beneath a coat. The sharpest turn is the closing plea for an end to inherited suffering, a wish to spare the next brood from the same damning schooling, which the song immediately undercuts by circling straight back to its opening image of a birth into bloodshed. That loop is the real argument: the pattern resists breaking, and the hope for release expires in the very same breath that first dares to name it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","politics & society","violence & murder"],"means":"inherited doctrine making brutality feel less choice than heirloom","built":"the closing hope for release expires as the song loops back to its own opening image"},
"annihilator~loving-the-sinner": {"haiku":"A person spirals through abuse, adultery, and despair, weaponizing their pain against themselves and others.","opus":"Annihilator swaps cosmic menace for domestic ruin, anatomizing an abusive relationship in which infidelity and cruelty poison whatever warmth once existed. The narrator lurches through depression like the walking dead, then snaps back with venom, branding his partner a liar and a brat. Waters casts intimacy as slow rot, affection souring into vengeance and a scornful goodbye.","sonnet":"A narrator trapped in a corrosive relationship lurches between self-loathing paralysis and sudden furious defiance. The emotional arc swings from numbness and depression — barely able to leave bed — toward a snarling rejection that declares the other person a liar while claiming the wreckage as a launching point.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters tracks a poisoned romance downward, its narrator half-begging to be hurt and half-numb, unsure whether he is still breathing beneath the depression pinning him under the covers each morning. The turn is the ugly part: humiliation curdles into contempt, and the pleading lover reassembles himself as a predator promising to leave the other person choking on his exhaust. Fitting the record's black-widow obsession, tenderness and cruelty here become the same appetite wearing different clothes, which is exactly why loving a sinner cannot separate the wound from the hand that keeps inflicting it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"tenderness and cruelty become the same appetite wearing different clothes","built":"humiliation curdles into contempt mid-song as pleading lover reassembles as predator"},
"annihilator~maximum-satan": {"haiku":"Evil takes imaginative form feeding on human fear and social corruption; the narrator describes Satan as psychological force rather than literal being.","opus":"A satirical Satan-figure who reveals he isn't literally the devil but the embodiment of mass media and fear: broadcasting horror on the TV screen, feeding people negativity, corruption, and disaster, he claims to be pure imagination that installs evil and takes viewers down one by one.","sonnet":"A theatrical Satan figure who reveals himself as a construct of media and human fear — feeding on crime, disaster, and political corruption broadcast on screens, admitting he isn't real but that the fear is.","src":"haiku","fable":"The devil here is not a horned tempter but a broadcast signal, a voice that admits it feeds on dread and pumps out nothing but negativity while promising the audience that reality has collapsed into whatever flickers before them. Its own confession is the sharpest turn: it swears it holds no substance, being only a projection that stokes the terror of some approaching cataclysm, which recasts the earlier parade of bribe-taking politicians, kidnapped children, and profitable new narcotics as the diet it survives on. What sounds like ordinary metal bombast becomes a portrait of the news cycle as the beast itself, an entity that recruits its believers precisely by convincing them the apocalypse already stands at the door.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","faith & the occult","politics & society","artifice & performance"],"means":"the devil confesses it is only a broadcast that survives on manufactured dread","built":"the beast's own self-description reframes news-cycle panic as its food source"},
"annihilator~murder": {"haiku":"A plague-like entity born to destroy and consume civilization, presenting itself as unstoppable and inevitable.","opus":"A first-person boast in the voice of pestilence, a force naming itself contagion and plague, vowing to force civilization to buckle and to gorge on the blood of the foolish and blameless alike. There is no regret, only the glee of an unbeatable predator marching to conquest. Annihilator, Jeff Waters's Canadian thrash outfit, often adopted such monstrous personas, and this one revels in its slaughtering omnipotence.","sonnet":"A plague speaking in first person — not a killer but a disease, something born without a conscience that feeds on the strong and weak alike, a force that can't be defeated or bargained with. The song takes the horror-metal trope of a predatory voice and gives it a biological frame: the murderer isn't a man but an epidemic.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters hands the microphone to a personified pestilence, a voice that announces its own arrival like a conqueror and promises to bring civilization down to its knees without distinction. Fool and innocent register identically to a pathogen that feeds indiscriminately, thriving on suffering and moving through the bloodstream as an invading army marching toward a final, unstoppable hour. The looped vow of being born to destroy stops functioning as a threat and becomes a creature's simple statement of nature, matching the cold, machine-driven leanness of the near-solo Remains material where the menace feels engineered rather than merely raged.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","war & battle"],"means":"pestilence announcing its nature as a simple statement of being","built":"the looped vow stops functioning as threat and becomes a creature's plain self-description"},
"annihilator~my-precious-lunatic-asylum": {"haiku":"Hell or madness as an inescapable destination, with the mind consumed and controlled by sinister forces.","opus":"A gleeful invitation into a mind coming apart, narrated by insanity itself as a grinning host welcoming you into its house of tortured souls and promising your mind now belongs to it. The horror is played for dark theatrics rather than sympathy. Annihilator, the Canadian thrash outfit built around guitarist Jeff Waters, always leaned toward technical riffing and this kind of B-movie madness-and-mayhem storytelling across their long catalogue.","sonnet":"A guided tour through a place of total mental collapse, framed as a dark invitation. The speaker isn't a victim but the host — insanity itself personified, welcoming new arrivals to a hell of their own making, claiming ownership of their minds. The tone is gleeful, sadistic, reveling in the surrender of reason.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters stages the asylum as a carnival barker's invitation, a grinning host who ushers the listener down a spiral of descending floors toward some final chamber where reason gives out entirely. The tour is a confidence trick: the promise that every appetite can be satisfied here is bait, and the crowd of broken inmates paraded past the visitor is the real product being sold. By the closing chant the host abandons the pleasantries and claims outright ownership of the guest's sanity, collapsing the space between guide and lunacy until the two prove to be a single voice all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","artifice & performance","surveillance & control"],"means":"host and lunacy revealed as a single voice all along","built":"descending-floors tour collapses guide and inmate at the close"},
"annihilator~never": {"haiku":"Hatred of racist ideology and violent white supremacist movements that manipulate and destroy through propaganda and bigotry.","opus":"A furious indictment of white-supremacist violence, adopting the hateful first person of those who imagine themselves a superior race on a mission from God, then turning to name the goal plainly as genocide and to condemn the sickness of that mind. The stance is unambiguously anti-racist. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' Canadian thrash band, uses the genre's aggression here to attack rather than glorify, closing on the plea never to forget.","sonnet":"A dual-voiced indictment of white supremacy — first mimicking the ideology's poisonous internal logic, then tearing it apart. The shift from reproducing the recruitment rhetoric to condemning it as born-to-be-deceived bigotry gives the song its moral spine: a warning not to forget what this kind of hatred is aimed at.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator sets a clinical outside voice against a hate group's own recruitment cadence, first diagnosing the bigot who mistakes his bloodline for a badge of higher worth, then ventriloquizing the doctrine itself. The middle inhabits the movement's language of chosen children marching under a divine banner toward domination, letting the ideology damn itself through its swagger before the frame snaps shut around one word: extermination. Jeff Waters, whose Remains was a near-solo studio experiment built against a drum machine, ends the coldness with open contempt, casting the racist as a deceived and rewired mind whose thoughts society cannot fathom, then leaving a lone command to keep the horror lodged in memory.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","faith & the occult"],"means":"ideology ventriloquized until a single word damns it","built":"the song inhabits recruitment cadence before the frame snaps shut on extermination"},
"annihilator~never-neverland": {"haiku":"A survivor escaping childhood trauma and abuse. Once frozen in a place of manipulation and control, the narrator reclaims their life and leaves that dark space behind.","opus":"Annihilator craft a chilling fairy tale of a child locked in an endless Saturday with a companion named Clare, warding off figures who summon him by name until he finally departs the quiet room wishing them everlasting sleep. Nursery imagery masks menace and loss as the thrash band lets sanctuary and abduction dissolve into decay.","sonnet":"An adult survivor revisiting a childhood defined by confinement with a companion named Clare in a room that never changed. The intrusion of outsiders is met with violence; only later does the narrator understand what they actually lived through and walk away free.","src":"haiku","fable":"A child narrator retreats into a sealed playroom with a companion named Clare, insisting school ended years ago and that the two of them will stay suspended here past the end of all time. Jeff Waters lets the fantasy curdle: an outside voice beckons the boy to follow it, and his response is not wonder but savage threats, a vow to shatter the bones of anyone who would drag him from this refuge. The closing movement pulls the mask off, revealing a survivor who has aged out of the trance and now names the hatred once done to him, so the placid room reads as a shelter built around abuse rather than any true wonderland. His departure becomes the real violence handed back, the grown voice wishing the tormentor a mock-gentle farewell that lands as a curse and a grave.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"childlike refuge revealed as shelter built around survived abuse","built":"the departure farewell lands as a curse dressed in mock-gentle wording"},
"annihilator~no-love": {"haiku":"Complete emotional desolation and abandonment where even basic human affection has vanished from existence.","opus":"A bitter address to someone posing as a martyr, nailing themselves up like a savior while the narrator hangs helpless as a discarded puppet begging to be noticed. He throws himself at sky and earth and gets no response, raging in vain against total indifference. The flat verdict that love is gone lands hardest. Annihilator, the Canadian thrash band, sharpen the personal wound into something desolate and accusatory.","sonnet":"A broken attachment examined without comfort — the speaker performs meaningless gestures, throws themselves outward, and gets nothing back from someone too self-absorbed to register another person's pain. The conclusion isn't bitter rage but a flat statement: love is simply absent here, and screaming into the sky confirms it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A plea to a lover who has hardened into an idol, worshipped yet unreachable. The speaker mocks the beloved's self-crucifixion pose, the messianic vanity that turns intimacy into a spectacle glimpsed on screens and sidewalks rather than felt up close. From there the accusation curves back onto him: the discarded plaything left dangling, straining to rouse someone already withdrawn. The last movement is pure futility, a body flung skyward and against the earth to prove devotion, met with total silence. Annihilator's Waters keeps the refrain flat and unhealing, so the escalating self-harm never earns a reply, only a verdict that the affection is spent.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"escalating self-harm that earns only silence in return","built":"the refrain held flat and unhealing so no devotion ever lands"},
"annihilator~no-surrender": {"haiku":"Addiction spirals into loss of control and a parasite-like voice that demands feeding, leaving the addict enslaved.","opus":"Addiction personified as a seductive, controlling force that drains the spirit and demands to be fed, granting no reprieve and no reprieve twice. The spoken breakdown, a frantic admission that he cannot draw air or eat and needs rescue, strips the metaphor down to plain dependency and its wreckage.","sonnet":"An addiction personified as a predator speaking directly to its host — it takes control, bleeds the soul, gives the addict exactly what they think they need, and makes clear there will be no second chance to get free.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator voice a compulsion as a swaggering predator narrating its own conquest, boasting that the craving will be unforgettable and that escape has already been foreclosed, the taunt of a dependency certain it owns its host. Against that bravado the song plants a second, human speaker who gasps that he cannot eat or breathe and begs heaven for rescue, and the collapse of that pleading into wordless repeated demands is the real argument that the strut was only a mask. The tormentor reclaims the microphone for a bleak verdict, that swallowing the poison leaves refusal as the sole remaining exit, so the two registers finally fuse into one throat that is at once the hunger and the ruined body it has hollowed out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind"],"means":"craving narrates its own conquest while the body it hollows begs for rescue","built":"predator and victim share one throat by the final verse, their voices fused into a single ruined register"},
"annihilator~no-way-out": {"haiku":"A remorseless criminal on trial, arrogant and manipulative, has committed acts they deny all feeling for; there remains no escape from their culpability.","opus":"A courtroom tale of a cold-blooded, likely female killer who murdered a man and tried to escape blame with faked amnesia and zero contrition. The song delivers its judgment plainly: there is no escape for her, only conviction, condemnation, and a push toward taking her own life.","sonnet":"Addressed to a manipulative murderer on trial: the song catalogues their premeditated crime, their lies and memory lapses, their attempt to destroy the victim's reputation, and concludes there is no escape from justice.","src":"haiku","fable":"A televised murder trial supplies the whole frame, the speaker watching a defendant he has already convicted from the couch, reading guilt into a face with darkened, hollowed sockets before any verdict lands. Annihilator's Jeff Waters writes the accused as a vain, self-worshipping woman who smeared her victim's reputation to bury the killing, then feigns a blank when pressed on details, a courtroom evasion the song flatly refuses to grant. The hook pins her between a witness stand and a hill of public execution, then between her own hand and the crowd's, insisting that escape is sealed off from every direction at once. The final stretch lets that judgment harden into pure repetition, the same sentence pronounced over and over until the machinery of condemnation, rather than the doomed woman herself, becomes the actual subject.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Waters wrote this about the Jodi Arias trial, which he watched between takes for Feast — the lyric’s method matches the killing of Travis Alexander.","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"public condemnation hardened by repetition until the machinery becomes the subject","built":"the final stretch pronounces the same sentence over and over, shifting focus from accused to the act of judging"},
"annihilator~no-zone": {"haiku":"The speaker warns of environmental destruction and ozone depletion as humanity exploits the planet—a global suicide that demands urgent listening.","opus":"A blunt early-90s environmental warning dressed as combat, the planet delivering an ultimatum about ozone depletion and scorched land. Fear-mongering as protest, biting the hand that poisons its own sky.","sonnet":"An angry environmental warning delivered as a battle cry from the Earth itself — the ozone layer is being destroyed, chemical pollution is choking the sky, and the planet warns humanity it's already too late to keep ignoring it.","src":"haiku","fable":"An early-90s thrash eco-warning that gets its bite from a POV trick: the singer is the planet itself, ancient and finally declaring war on the humans burning it up, so the metal-standard revenge-and-attack posture becomes the Earth striking back rather than a scorned lover. From that frame the specifics land as indictments in the planet's own voice, from torched rainforest to chemicals eating a hole in the sky, and the title puns the collapsing ozone into a no-zone, a place left uninhabitable. The refusal at the core is human, not lyrical: the repeated plea about a message no one will hear turns the song's real subject into willful deafness, with the closing verdict that there is nowhere left to run naming the stakes as collective self-destruction.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nature & the elements","politics & society","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the planet prosecutes humanity in its own first-person voice","built":"the title puns ozone into no-zone, a place rendered uninhabitable"},
"annihilator~nothing-left": {"haiku":"Someone destroyed themselves and others pursuing a relentless, pointless existence on a carousel of lies and speed; there is nothing left to do but keep falling faster.","opus":"Waters points Annihilator's later, groovier assault at self-destruction and empty striving, confronting a figure who sacrificed everything to become what they can no longer stand to look at. The urge to keep pushing, never quitting, becomes its own snare as a spinning ride hurtles toward ruin. Sleep is the only escape from a mounting heap of deceit, ambition curdled into a living tomb.","sonnet":"A figure trapped on a relentless carousel of compulsion and self-deception acknowledges that the forward motion is the only reality left. Lies pile six feet deep, desire keeps feeding itself, and what remains is an exhausted hunger that has outlasted every reason to stop or give in.","src":"haiku","fable":"A reckoning delivered to someone who chased their own ruin and now stands inside the wreckage, forced to look at the discarded people they climbed over on the way up. Jeff Waters frames the compulsion less as a choice than as an appetite feeding on its host, the hunger and the hard breathing standing in for a craving that has outgrown the person carrying it. The turn arrives with a carousel image: buying another spin on a machine that accelerates until its edges cut like blades, so the momentum itself becomes the punishment rather than the escape it once promised. What remains is not repentance but exhaustion, the numb clarity of a person who has burned every option and finds even shut eyes the sole shelter from a habit that refuses to surrender.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"momentum itself becomes the punishment the carousel promised was escape","built":"carousel image turns acceleration into the cutting edge of the machine"},
"annihilator~nothing-to-me": {"haiku":"Rejection of a parasitic relationship—the speaker cuts off an ungrateful user who drains energy but means nothing emotionally.","opus":"The narrator dismisses a user who takes what they do not deserve, insisting he is merely going through the motions and that the offender means nothing to him. Annihilator channel this kickoff kiss-off through Jeff Waters' thrash attack, contempt sharpened by warnings against disrespect, the whole track a door kicked shut on someone who overstayed.","sonnet":"A person exhausted by a user who drains without reciprocating finally names the truth: the emotional transaction has gone empty, the tolerance has run out. Fury is controlled and surgical — not heartbreak but a clean, contemptuous withdrawal from someone who mistook availability for worth.","src":"haiku","fable":"A kiss-off to a freeloader who mistook tolerance for weakness, the song stacks its grievances like a bouncer counting reasons before the ejection, each verse another charge in a case already decided. What sounds like fury is really exhaustion wearing fury's clothes, since the speaker confesses he performs the argument out of pure habit while feeling nothing, which is the far crueler blow to land. Jeff Waters keeps Annihilator's thrash lean and mid-tempo here, the riffing closer to contemptuous swagger than blur, so the final dismissal reads as a door held wide open rather than a scream begging to be heard.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"exhaustion wearing fury's clothes, the cruelest dismissal of all","built":"mid-tempo swagger signals contempt where a scream would signal need"},
"annihilator~nowhere-to-go": {"haiku":"Forgotten homeless children face abuse and survival with no protection or shelter, trapped without hope or escape. The song calls for sanctuary and intervention for those society abandons.","opus":"Annihilator turn a bleak eye on a runaway kid living rough, hooked on drugs and dodging predators with no adult willing to intervene. The song builds toward an argument that society owes these discarded, unheard young people funded shelter, framing their plight as a failure of institutions rather than personal weakness.","sonnet":"About homeless teenagers trapped in addiction, abuse, and survival sex — abandoned by every system, with nowhere to run and no one who wants to know. The bridge breaks from character to address the listener directly, demanding sanctuary for these forgotten children.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of a homeless, addicted runaway told from the inside, where every stanza closes the exits one by one: no food, no shelter from weather, no protection from predators, only the pain and the dope that dull them. The refrain works as a locked door rather than a chorus, its repetition enacting the trap the words describe rather than developing it. The final verse breaks the first-person mask entirely, pulling back to a third-person appeal that names these kids as casualties of a broken system and asks, almost as a policy plea, that someone fund a sanctuary to end the cycle. That late pivot from lived nightmare to social indictment is the whole point: the personal voice was the evidence, and the last stanza is the verdict.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","addiction & self-destruction","politics & society"],"means":"personal nightmare presented as evidence for a policy verdict","built":"a late pronoun shift from first person to third turns lyric into indictment"},
"annihilator~one-falls-two-rise": {"haiku":"Cyclical struggle with guilt, adversity, and survival where obstacles multiply; despite setbacks, resilience and learning from mistakes sustain life.","opus":"Isolated in blackness and disgrace, a narrator hounded by guilt gives everything yet never feels it measures up, chilled and deadened at his limit. Annihilator's thrash grit rises in a refrain about outlasting one collapse after another, the closing pivot toward learning from failure delivering hard-won resolve where the verses only spiraled.","sonnet":"Crushed by guilt, numbness, and the relentless grind of daily survival, a person teeters at the breaking point — yet insists on standing back up. The refrain is a vow: for every fall there is a rise, and the will to survive outlasts the pressure.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survival mantra hammered by repetition: every knockdown gets answered by getting back up twice over. The title reads like a gambler's odds, a wager that resilience outpaces loss even as the verses tally guilt, numbness, and a suspicion of being singled out. Jeff Waters, who has steered Annihilator through decades of lineup churn and industry battering, writes this less as metaphor than as a memo to himself. The closing turn softens thrash defiance into something plainer and older, that everyone stumbles and honest effort eventually squares the account, so the chant survives as hard-won encouragement rather than a boast.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"resilience mantra written as a memo to the band itself","built":"thrash defiance softens by the close into plain hard-won encouragement"},
"annihilator~operation-annihilation": {"haiku":"A resilient fighter declares retribution against betrayers and parasites, vowing to reclaim what was lost through unwavering integrity. The song expresses revenge and victorious resolve.","opus":"Having tasted success and glimpsed ruin, the narrator swears to claim what belongs to him, casting his integrity and vengeance as a military campaign with targets locked and weapons loosed. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' long-running thrash project, frame personal resolve as warfare, promising defeat to the parasites and liars while the narrator stays calm, silent, and committed to the finish.","sonnet":"A survivor of sustained attempts to break him declares that integrity outlasts every enemy. He has endured the hell of being targeted by leeches and liars, and now the mission is retribution — not rage but the calm certainty that he will still be standing when they are not.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor's manifesto framed in the vocabulary of a military strike, where endurance against those who tried to break and bend him becomes an act of aggression rather than mere defense. The song pits integrity against a world of interchangeable people stamped out on an assembly line, positioning the narrator as the one fixed point who refuses to be reshaped by the crowd. Its structure hardens through the bridge, escalating from vengeance into a vow of resolve held until death, so the closing stillness he claims reads not as peace but as the settled calm of someone certain the war against the leeches and liars is already won.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","war & battle"],"means":"endurance recast as counterattack against conformity","built":"the closing stillness named as certainty rather than peace"},
"annihilator~payback": {"haiku":"Vigilante revenge for innocent victims; someone who did time unjustly confronts their tormentor with violence, framing retribution as justice for brutal crimes and betrayal.","opus":"A revenge fantasy told from the survivor's side of a crime the courts failed to punish, turning grief into the cold satisfaction of an eye for an eye, the wronged now become the executioner who is the last thing the guilty will see.","sonnet":"A revenge song narrated by someone who waited for a violent criminal to serve their sentence and then hunted them down — the narrator recounts the killing in detail, squeezing out the target's life as justice for the original victims.","src":"haiku","fable":"A killer walks free on a sentence that never matched the horror, and the song hands the microphone to a survivor or avenger who has counted every day of that undeserved release. Jeff Waters frames retribution as a debt made personal, the vengeance sworn in the name of the dead while the guilty man is run to ground like hunted prey and forced down onto his knees. Beneath the roar of righteous justice, though, the speaker savors the kill with a relish that blurs any line between punishment and the original cruelty, so the sworn threat lands less as courtroom balance than as one more brutal hand closing around a throat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance","death & grief"],"means":"righteous retribution savored so closely it blurs with the original cruelty","built":"the avenger's relish quietly erases the line between justice and the crime it answers"},
"annihilator~perfect-angel-eyes": {"haiku":"The narrator offers comfort to someone bearing hidden pain behind a beautiful face, promising to support them through their darkest moments.","opus":"A tender ballad to a woman hiding deep hurt behind a lovely face, the singer offering to look past the mask and heal years of sorrow. The real thrust is devoted rescue, urging her to lean on him through her worst days and finally break free. A notably soft turn for the thrash band.","sonnet":"A tender song about seeing past someone's beautiful exterior to the hidden pain they carry — the narrator wants to be the person this woman can turn to in her dark moments, offering to wipe away tears she hides behind a smile.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rare tender turn from a band built on thrash aggression, this is a lover offering himself as refuge to a partner who buries old wounds behind a bright, untroubled surface that everyone else takes at face value. The whole song hinges on that split between the composed exterior and the ache the narrator alone claims to detect, so his affection arrives fused with a rescue impulse, love figured as the act of noticing concealed hurt. Its closing movement tips from reassurance into something like devotion, casting the beloved as a distant point of light in the dark and vowing to shoulder the grief she has carried alone for years.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"affection figured as the act of noticing concealed hurt nobody else sees","built":"the split between composed exterior and buried ache is the song's only engine"},
"annihilator~phantasmagoria": {"haiku":"The speaker torments another person through nightmares, trapping them in a hellish mind-prison where ghosts and demons take control of their soul and sanity.","opus":"Annihilator adopt the voice of a demonic tormentor who claims a victim inside a nightmare, promising hell, apparitions and the theft of the soul with no escape from its test. The refrain tolls sleep and damnation. Jeff Waters' band leans into horror-movie villainy, staging psychological torment as an inescapable dream-hell narrated gleefully from the monster's own perspective.","sonnet":"A predatory presence — demonic, possessive — claims ownership of another person's sleeping mind. Each night becomes an inescapable carnival of apparitions, the tormentor declaring full dominion over dreams and ultimately over the soul itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title does the heavy lifting: a phantasmagoria was a real magic-lantern horror show of the 1790s onward, where projectionists conjured floating ghosts to terrorize a paying crowd, and Annihilator builds the whole song as one long boast from the projectionist's side. The speaker is a demonic figure who has singled out a sleeper and turned the inside of that mind into his theater, so the horrors aren't external attackers but images cast on the victim from within, which is why there is nowhere to hide and no test that can be passed. Across its short arc the address escalates from taunting invasion to a mock-legal verdict and finally to the claim on the soul, the ghost narrator asserting total ownership rather than merely haunting. The refrain's paired benediction and damnation is the cruelest turn, a lullaby that wishes the target restful sleep only to specify that the resting place is hell.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","madness & the mind","artifice & performance"],"means":"demonic projectionist turning the victim's own mind into his theater","built":"the closing lullaby wishes restful sleep and specifies the resting place is hell"},
"annihilator~phoenix-rising": {"haiku":"Rising from loss and isolation, the narrator and companion ascend on fiery wings, escaping darkness through shared transcendence above danger.","opus":"A rare melodic reach for the Canadian thrashers, turning loss into flight. Watching a loved one slip away and struggling alone with only memories, the narrator imagines them reborn and soaring skyward on flaming wings. The mythic bird becomes a promise that grief can lift into something transcendent, away from the ruin below.","sonnet":"Grief over someone lost gives way to a vision of transcendence — the dead rising on wings of fire, carrying the living out of grief and into something incandescent. The image of a phoenix becoming whole again through flame transforms mourning into a shared, soaring ascent above the storm.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grief song that refuses to stay a grief song: the first verse is pure loss and isolation, someone watching another person fade with only memory for company, but the chorus wrenches the dead back into the sky as a phoenix, and the pivot from before my eyes to before their eyes turns private mourning into a shared, defiant ascent. The fire imagery does the real work here, converting the bird's death-and-rebirth myth into an escape velocity out of the hurricane, so that the burning is not destruction but the mechanism of reunion and wholeness. For Annihilator, a Canadian thrash band, this is the rare ballad move, and its sentimentality is the point: the song insists on flying away together as the answer to a grief it cannot actually undo.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","transcendence & consciousness","freedom & escape"],"means":"fire converts private mourning into a shared defiant ascent","built":"pivot from before my eyes to before their eyes turns grief communal"},
"annihilator~plasma-zombies": {"haiku":"Graphic violent video games and mass media hypnotize audiences into emotional numbness and desensitization, replacing human connection with passive consumption of gore and destruction.","opus":"Jeff Waters fires a satirical shot at screen culture, picturing children lulled by violent games and round-the-clock gloom into feelingless husks. The television turns into the foe corroding young minds, a mid-catalog warning that make-believe and truth smear together until nobody can distinguish them.","sonnet":"Screens colonise childhood and hollow out development. Violent games blur into reality, children rot in front of televisions, and society narrows to a 24-hour feed of negativity. The song indicts a media environment that conditions passivity and detachment, leaving behind people who can no longer feel what is real.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters writes a mid-2000s panic about screens raising children, where a videogame full of gore and a classroom piped through the same glowing box blur together until the kid can no longer separate simulation from waking life. The dread lands not in the pixelated violence itself but in its aftermath, a numbness that erases sleep, feeling, and the gap between a vanished friend and a demon that simply respawns for the next round. What begins as a taunting invitation to enjoy the carnage curdles into a plea to switch the machine off, naming the ceaseless broadcast of catastrophe as the real predator, draining the very mind it claims to be informing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness","politics & society"],"means":"the gap between simulation and waking life erased by ceaseless screens","built":"taunting invitation to enjoy the carnage curdles into a plea to switch off"},
"annihilator~pride": {"haiku":"Someone rejects being manipulated into subservience, claiming the abuser's pride is weakness in disguise and refusing to accept devaluation.","opus":"The Canadian thrashers frame a toxic relationship as a battle of self-worth, the singer standing up to a partner who humiliates and dominates him for their own gratification. He flatly rejects their control, sees through the manipulation, and refuses to be torn down so someone else can feel powerful.","sonnet":"The refusal to keep absorbing someone else's need for dominance — a person who inflicts pain to feel powerful being told, plainly, that it isn't working and never was. The speaker is done meeting impossible demands, done being treated like a pet. What's left is the decision to stop letting this person's headtrip define the room. The weakness behind all that aggression has been seen clearly now.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is the anatomy of a bully dressed up as a diagnosis: the target watches the aggressor mine cruelty for a high, then catalogues exactly what that hunger costs. Dominance gets stripped of its glamour and renamed as compensation, a small man inflating himself by shrinking someone else and calling coercion ordinary demands. What steadies the song is refusal held as identity, needing nothing and owing nothing, seeing through the performance to the fear driving it rather than the power it claims. The closing turn lands hardest, the victim narrating his own demolition as the exact fuel his tormentor burns, and in saying it plainly he strips that fire of its last heat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"naming the bully's fuel strips that fire of its last heat","built":"the victim narrates his own demolition from inside the tormentor's logic"},
"annihilator~prime-time-killing": {"haiku":"A dark ruler dominates through violence, offering only pain and addiction to followers. The speaker commands death and claims dominion over a kingdom built on suffering and poisoned submission.","opus":"A self-styled ruler of the dead commands victims to their knees, injecting narcotic death and painting the sky blood-red in a grandiose fantasy of domination. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' Canadian thrash outfit, indulge horror-movie villainy here, the narrator's boasts of enthroned supremacy and lethal invitation dressing cruelty as a theatrical, prime-time spectacle.","sonnet":"A self-proclaimed lord of the underworld holds dominion over the addicted and the dying, framing a predatory drug ecosystem in the language of divine command. The killing is narrated with cold pageantry — needle, narcotic symphony, and a throne built entirely from other people's destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters writes from inside a swaggering tyrant who claims dominion over a kingdom of corpses, dressing conquest as spectacle by promising a sky repainted crimson and a crowd bowing at his feet. The menace curdles into something addictive when the persona offers a poisoned mercy, sliding a needle into the vein and calling the resulting overdose a narcotic symphony rather than the murder it plainly is. That grinning invitation is the horror underneath the boast: the ruler frames his victims as willing guests, so the entertainment-industry title turns their slow, infectious dying into a broadcast everyone is welcomed to enjoy.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"ruler frames victims as willing guests so their dying becomes broadcast entertainment","built":"entertainment-industry title reframes murder as a narcotic symphony for spectators"},
"annihilator~punctured": {"haiku":"Deliberate psychological and physical destruction of another person. The speaker enjoys lying, toying, and breaking their victim's mind and body systematically.","opus":"Annihilator inhabit a sadistic manipulator who lies, lures a victim close, then punctures skin, soul and dream while professing total control that slips toward being out of control. The imagery of blackening night and world is coldly deliberate. The thrash band pushes cruelty to theatrical extremes, voicing a predator who savours the breaking of another's mind.","sonnet":"A sadist who has deliberately deceived and manipulated a victim, sustaining the cruelty precisely because the victim kept believing in the possibility of winning. Control is the only real goal — puncturing skin, soul, and dream alike until reality is whatever the aggressor decides it is.","src":"haiku","fable":"A predator's monologue that runs on a single admitted trick: he tells the victim outright that he lies, and the victim, wanting to believe it can win, takes the tell as reassurance and crawls closer anyway. That is the song's cruelty and Annihilator's dark joke on the whole Criteria for a Black Widow murder-narrative frame -- honesty deployed as the deadliest bait, because being told the truth makes the mark trust it isn't. The recurring puncture image widens from skin to soul to dream, tracking a control that the speaker himself narrates slipping from because I'm in control to because I'm out of control, so the promised world-blackening reads less as calculated power than a compulsion the killer no longer governs even as he savors it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"honesty deployed as the deadliest bait, the truth that earns the mark's trust","built":"control narration slides from because I'm in control to because I'm out of control"},
"annihilator~rage-absolute": {"haiku":"A person consumed by building rage driven by another's actions reaches a boiling point, accepting blame is shifted onto them while hinting at violent release.","opus":"Annihilator map explosive fury from the inside, the narrator blaming his victim for provoking rage he cannot contain, threatening violence and secrecy while insisting the temper runs in the family blood. The self-justification is chilling. Jeff Waters' band renders domestic abuse as a first-person confession, exposing the manipulation and inherited excuse that accompany the eruption of absolute rage.","sonnet":"A portrait of domestic abuse delivered from the abuser's own perspective, rationalising violence as provoked and normal within a family pattern. The final lines reveal the dynamic: threats to ensure silence, blame transferred entirely to the victim, tears dismissed as weakness.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters writes a portrait of a domestic abuser from inside the skull, where fury registers not as a decision but as a rising pressure the speaker insists some external target has forced toward detonation. The real horror surfaces once the shouting subsides: he swears the violence never occurred, coaches his victim into silence under threat of being finished, and leans on the alibi that cruelty simply runs in the bloodline and is therefore normal. That closing swerve, from flat denial into inherited justification into an eerily tender instruction to stop crying, exposes how he rewrites his own guilt as the victim's fault while demanding gratitude for the quiet that follows.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","family & blood","shame & repression"],"means":"abuser rewrites his guilt as the victim's fault then demands gratitude for quiet","built":"the closing swerve into inherited-cruelty alibi follows flat denial of the act"},
"annihilator~reaction": {"haiku":"Accidental overdose and desperate medical intervention, depicting the terror and urgency of poisoning reversal.","opus":"A vivid, breathless account of a severe allergic emergency, something swallowed by mistake sending the narrator scrambling to jab the antidote and pray for survival as his airway closes. Driving the syringe home becomes his final barricade against encroaching death. Annihilator, Jeff Waters's Canadian thrash outfit, turn the crisis into pure adrenaline, the racing tempo mirroring a body outrunning the clock.","sonnet":"An anaphylactic emergency narrated in real time — accidental exposure, the race to inject the antidote, the adrenaline flooding through veins while the throat closes and the clock runs out. It's a clinical thriller compressed into a few verses, the fear of being too late giving the song its desperate energy.","src":"haiku","fable":"A real-time, first-person account of anaphylaxis: an accidental ingestion triggers a systemic collapse, and the whole song is the panicked seconds of self-administering the antidote before the throat closes. Its grim wit is that survival depends on driving a needle full of what the body reads as more poison, so the plunger-push chorus turns treatment into a gamble indistinguishable from the attack itself. The verses track the clock physiologically rather than emotionally—swelling, breath narrowing, defenses giving out—so the dread comes from the body's arithmetic, and the closing plea to uninvite the pain lands as a wish that can't be commanded, only injected and prayed over.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief"],"means":"treatment indistinguishable from the attack that demands it","built":"the plunger-push chorus turns antidote into a gamble on more poison"},
"annihilator~reduced-to-ash": {"haiku":"Nuclear apocalypse and total civilization collapse; all life reduced to ash through self-inflicted catastrophe.","opus":"Annihilator depict an apocalypse in lurid detail, the heavens collapsing and the living reduced to broken corpses as a self-engineered doom incinerates everything. Jeff Waters leans into the grotesque, the man-made devastation leaving too much ruin to mourn, life extinguished before birth until only cinders and a bleak farewell remain.","sonnet":"Civilization-ending catastrophe — meteor strike or self-inflicted ruin — strips the earth of every living thing. The imagery is total: rubble, screams, bodies, a sun torched by humanity's own creations. Nothing survives; everything is reduced to ash.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters walks a doomsday newsreel through its stages: first the collapsing skyline and the wreckage-choked ground, then a planet convulsing until nothing survives except the never-born, the horror mounting verse by verse. The turn arrives when the speakers claim authorship, conceding this extinction was engineered by human hands that set fire to their own star and bred the very weapons that erased every future. What began as spectacle curdles into confession, the chanted title landing like a verdict already carried out, each repetition hardening the point that no undoing remains.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","war & battle","politics & society"],"means":"spectacle of extinction curdles into confession of human authorship","built":"chanted title lands as verdict already carried out, each repetition hardening it"},
"annihilator~refresh-the-demon": {"haiku":"Addiction and commercial temptation loop endlessly; subliminal marketing poisons youth while the speaker fights to resist the cycle and defeat the demon pulling him down.","opus":"The 1996 title track treats consumer craving as a monster kept alive by advertising's empty promises. Jeff Waters skewers marketing that sells products as fulfillment while pushing what amounts to slow poison, then pivots to a cornered addict who resolves to resist rather than cave, framing the whole thing as a fight against corporate manipulation.","sonnet":"Corporate advertising is reframed as a demonic ritual of addiction — engineered thirst dressed up in wholesome imagery. A narrator fights to resist the subliminal current of manufactured desire, aware the product itself is poison, and stages the battle as a private war against conditioning that never fully lets up.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters frames consumer craving as a possessing spirit, the marketplace murmuring its pitch until the narrator mistakes each new purchase, each freshly sweetened flavor, for rescue rather than the slow hollowing it actually delivers. The recurring verb turns damning, since renewing whatever empties you out merely feeds it, so the mocking toast to youthful glory curdles into a communion of poison and wine dressed up as thriving. Only when his back meets the wall does the narrator stop swallowing and choose combat, recasting the whole compulsion as a war waged against the very hunger that had quietly been steering him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","politics & society","faith & the occult"],"means":"renewing what empties you merely feeds the hollowing it delivers","built":"the recurring verb refresh damns itself by naming the compulsion"},
"annihilator~ritual": {"haiku":"A predatory figure positions himself as a spiritual savior to exploit vulnerable people's desires and weaknesses, offering false transcendence while feeding on their desperation.","opus":"Annihilator voice a predatory tempter who markets himself as a savior figure, worming into a vulnerable mind and promising relief and transcendence while actually feeding an addiction. The song exposes false salvation as a seduction of the weak toward self-destruction, its chorus flatly naming the exchange for what it is.","sonnet":"A false prophet or cult leader positions himself as salvation — offering to absorb sins, ease pain, and elevate his followers — while operating as a parasite feeding on weakness and need. The ritual he conducts is one of psychological domination dressed in the language of spiritual rescue.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator writes this entirely inside the mouth of a predator who sells himself as salvation and never lets the mask slip: the speaker offers to be father, savior, the one who eases pain and lifts you above the world, while every promise is really a hook — the higher he takes you is a chemical high, the hell he lifts you above is the dependency he supplies, and the lyric seals the swap with one respelled word, a name taken in vein rather than vain. The song's cruelty is structural. It names its own method up front (find the weakness, work the fear) so that the seductive chorus of trust and rescue reads as the con in progress, not a genuine hymn. Calling it a ritual is the tell: this isn't a one-time seduction but a liturgy of control repeated until submission feels like faith, which is why the final verse simply loops the opening pitch — the trap has no exit, only another run of the same script.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control","faith & the occult"],"means":"predator's sales pitch cycles as liturgy until submission feels like faith","built":"one respelled word swaps vain for vein and names the con inside the hymn"},
"annihilator~road-to-ruin": {"haiku":"A drunk driver speeds recklessly toward inevitable harm; the song depicts impaired judgment leading inexorably down a destructive path.","opus":"A blunt portrait of drunk driving, the world tilting and spinning as the impaired narrator swears it is the last time while racing home far too fast. The chorus states the destination flatly and offers no reprieve, the whole song a grim inevitability rolling toward disaster.","sonnet":"A drunk driver narrates the progression from losing his keys in a bar to getting behind the wheel, convincing himself he is fine, and speeding home at ninety miles an hour — the song cuts off abruptly on the word \"Out,\" implying the crash.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drunk driver narrates his own denial in real time, and Jeff Waters' thrash keeps the pulse of a man too impaired to know he is already lost. The verses stay first-person and reassuring, fumbling for keys and gulping night air, insisting the trip is short and the danger already behind him. Against that self-talk the chorus turns accusatory and second-person, naming the intoxication and refusing any final reprieve, so the song splits the driver he believes he is from the doom watching outside. That structural quarrel is the whole point: bravado at the wheel curdles into a fatal reckoning, and the closing single word snaps the ignition off mid-boast.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"bravado at the wheel closing against the doom watching from outside","built":"first and second person split the driver he believes he is from his fate"},
"annihilator~romeo-delight": {"haiku":"A reckless night of drinking and casual pursuits; the singer arrives at a party seeking physical contact and avoiding complications, dismissing concerns and pushing through the evening.","opus":"A cover of Van Halen's swaggering party prowl, the strut of a man arriving to drink and pick up rather than fight, brushing off authority with total self-assurance while begging only that nobody kill the mood.","sonnet":"A song about a man at a party looking for sex and drink — he shows up with whiskey, refuses to show ID, deflects a woman's anger, and keeps pushing for physical company with no interest in a fight.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Van Halen cover, and the swagger is the whole point: this is the voice of a hard-drinking prowler talking his way past every gatekeeper between him and one more night out, whether that gate is a bouncer wanting ID, a nervous woman, or his own conscience. The bravado is armor over appetite. He insists he wants somebody to hold rather than somebody to hit and that he knows his rights and answers to no one, but the tell is the pleading refrain that he cannot take it anymore and the pulse he keeps clocking in his own chest, which betray a man far more strung-out and desperate than the cocky patter admits. Read straight, it is a boast; read against itself, it is a portrait of the boast as a coping mechanism, and Annihilator's thrash setting sharpens the edge of menace already latent in the swagger.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"the boast as coping mechanism, the strut betrayed by the pulse it keeps clocking","built":"the pleading refrain undercuts the cocky patter it wraps around"},
"annihilator~schizos-are-never-alone-part-iii": {"haiku":"Internal voices multiply and contradict, where the speaker's mind becomes crowded with warring consciousnesses. Solitude is impossible when the self shatters into conflicting demands.","opus":"A short reprise threading through Alice in Hell's portrait of a mind crowded with warring voices, the terror of never finding silence or solitude inside your own head. It renders mental illness as a room you can never empty.","sonnet":"A spoken prologue voices the interior chaos of schizophrenia — racing thoughts, angry contradictory voices, the paradox of external silence and internal cacophony — before the stark refrain confirms that the condition offers no solitude: the mind is always occupied.","src":"haiku","fable":"A decade-later sequel that literally quotes itself: the lyric sheet folds the spoken confession and two-line mantra from the 1989 Alice in Hell original back in as labeled Parts I and II, so the whole point of a Part III is recurrence. The confession sets the terms plainly enough that they resist embellishment: a mind loud with contradicting voices, an outside world unnervingly quiet by comparison, the wish for peace that the voices themselves veto. Against that, the title's grim joke does the interpretive work, because being never alone is exactly the affliction, not its cure, and the flat repetition of the mantra enacts the condition it names rather than describing it. That the song returns the same words years on turns the disorder into Annihilator's own recurring motif, the illness reasserting itself on schedule.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind"],"means":"being never alone named as the affliction not its cure","built":"the lyric sheet folds the 1989 original's words back in as labeled parts, making recurrence the point"},
"annihilator~schizos-are-never-alone-parts-i-ii": {"opus":"Drawing on thrash metal's appetite for extremity, Annihilator renders life with schizophrenia not as spectacle but as constant, unwanted company: voices, presences, and internal figures that never leave whether summoned or not. The language is minimal and loaded, conveying a reality that is fractured and never quiet. The two-part structure mirrors the condition itself, a mind divided against itself yet, by its own bitter title, never truly alone.","sonnet":"The experience of living with schizophrenia — voices, presences, and internal figures are constant company whether wanted or not, and the song conveys the fractured, never-quiet reality of the condition with minimal but loaded language.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A near-wordless interlude on Annihilator's madness-and-institution debut Alice in Hell, this is less a song than a symptom: the title chants itself over and over, the mantra of a mind convinced it is never truly by itself. The point is in the delivery, not the content. The refrain's grim comfort curdles when the only other voices break through as fragments pulling opposite ways at once, a plea to get out set against a jittering yes-no-what that stages the split rather than describing it. The repetition is the illness talking, and the whole trick is to hear the crowd inside a single head.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind"],"means":"the crowd inside a single head staged through repetition rather than described","built":"the title chanted as its own refrain enacts the symptom it names"},
"annihilator~second-to-none": {"haiku":"Drug addiction personified as a seductive demon promises power and escape while dragging the user to ruin.","opus":"Annihilator personify addiction as a smooth dealer calling himself the Doctor, pitching cocaine as everything worth living for. Waters gives the drug a devil's charm, promising escape while guaranteeing there is no exit. The seduction hardens into a hunt the victim cannot win, the blame resting on whoever took the offer from a false friend meant to destroy them.","sonnet":"Cocaine speaks in first person, seducing with the voice of a pastor, a doctor, a soulmate, promising transcendence and delivering only escalating entrapment. The persona is effortlessly dominant — framing the user's eventual defeat as inevitable, even entertaining — while the grandiosity masks a portrait of predatory dependency.","src":"haiku","fable":"Addiction gets a first-person voice here, purring its sales pitch as a healer whose remedy only deepens the wound it claims to close, courting the listener as physician, faith, and last remaining friend. The seducer keeps sweetening its promises until the mountain-and-snow invitation turns the offered high into a grave the victim digs willingly, mistaking surrender for flight. What seals the trap is how the narrator swings every consequence back onto the user, boasting an unbeaten record while insisting the ruin was always a private choice made without help.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"surrender dressed as flight into a grave the victim digs willingly","built":"the seducer swings every consequence back onto the user as private choice"},
"annihilator~set-the-world-on-fire": {"haiku":"A tyrannical leader consolidates power over followers by controlling their fear and weakness, then faces violent overthrow by the people who renounce him and send him to hell.","opus":"A false-messiah narrative: a leader promising deliverance and demanding allegiance, then the followers turning to burn his world down. A parable about cult power and its inevitable reckoning.","sonnet":"About a false prophet or authoritarian ruler who claims divine mandate and absolute knowledge — the narrator exposes the lie, turns the fire back on the despot, and delivers judgment: their world has been burned, their time is over, and they're going straight to hell.","src":"haiku","fable":"A demagogue's recruitment pitch that rots into its own overthrow. In the verses the speaker sells himself as savior and sole authority, holding the master key, demanding allegiance in exchange for protection and might, the language of a cult leader who is also plainly a despot promising to burn the world clean. The first chorus turns that fire outward as a threat: submit or die. But the bridge hands the microphone to the ruled, whose dying and crying become a sentence passed on the tyrant, and the final chorus flips every pronoun so the same fire and the same reckoning now fall on him. The trick is that nothing in the words changes except who holds them: the promise of judgment reverses onto the one who made it, no god left to save him, while the crowd’s new vow to meet its own maker admits that even the victors stay mortal.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"judgment promised by a tyrant reverses onto the one who promised it","built":"the same words flip meaning as the pronoun holding them changes hands"},
"annihilator~sexecution": {"haiku":"Reckless sexual pursuit with danger and potential fatality, treating intimacy as a high-stakes game of Russian roulette.","opus":"A predatory come-on staged as a lethal gamble, mutual craving between hunter and quarry sliding into a game of chance that could prove fatal. The morning-after reversal admits regret, that tempting fate could end with a shot to the skull. Sex and death braid together, and Annihilator, Jeff Waters's Canadian thrash band, deliver it with the swaggering menace typical of their catalogue.","sonnet":"Mutual desire played as a high-stakes game — pursuit, conquest, and the dangerous thrill of giving in framed in the language of crime and Russian roulette. After the heat breaks, dread sets in: the speaker wonders what they've done, recognizing they gambled with something they couldn't afford to lose.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator's Jeff Waters builds a predator-and-prey seduction where hunting vocabulary and raw appetite keep bleeding into each other, framing a single reckless encounter as a lethal contract both parties knowingly agree to sign. The invented word stitching sex to a state killing does the central work, so consummation and capital punishment become one gesture and the body shrinks into meat two hunters have agreed to fall upon. Everything reverses in the closing stretch, once the heat drains away and sober clarity returns as dread, when the narrator grasps that he wagered his own survival against a stranger's pleasure and the waiting coffin was never merely a figure of speech.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"consummation and capital punishment fused into one lethal contract","built":"the invented title-word collapses sex and state killing into a single gesture"},
"annihilator~shallow-grave": {"haiku":"An addict spirals downward, admitting vice and addiction have destroyed them while they dig toward a shallow grave with little hope of escape.","opus":"The narrator blames a lover for infecting him and dragging him into the gutter and toward madness, his end drawing near. Annihilator's thrash groove carries a tale of addiction that comes free yet costs everything, the narrator taunting the other to reconsider before assuming they will escape the same doomed gamble.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship — or perhaps addiction personified — is dragging a man deeper into ruin. He names it a disease, a vice that costs nothing yet demands everything, and watches himself slide from gutter to grave, taking his tormentor with him.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters writes addiction as a codependent death spiral, where the drug and the destructive lover blur into one accusing you. The narrator sits in the gutter, blaming a partner for a disease and a habit he keeps feeding for free. The final turn is the real cruelty: the pull toward the grave becomes a shared errand, dragging the other down with a dare instead of going alone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"dragging the other toward the grave as dare rather than going alone","built":"drug and destructive lover blur into one undifferentiated accuser"},
"annihilator~sixes-and-sevens": {"haiku":"Life is chaos—disillusionment, confusion, and internal conflict create constant crisis. The song traces a journey from feeling lost and broken to finding determination and rebuilding self-confidence.","opus":"Borrowing an old idiom for utter confusion, Annihilator map a mind at war with itself, order fighting chaos as troubles pile up. Waters traces disillusionment and drowned motivation, drink dulling the ache in a life tangled and stuck. Resolve briefly rallies through sheer determination, but the ending slides back toward sinking, leaving recovery and collapse unsettled.","sonnet":"Mental disorder pressing down on daily life — frustration, aimlessness, and the creeping numbing of alcohol — eventually gives way to a hard-won determination. The shift is not triumphant so much as stubborn: disorder loses its grip not because the chaos clears but because the will to push through outlasts it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The idiom of the title names a state of confusion and disarray, and Jeff Waters builds the whole song as a tug of war between disorder and the will to keep functioning under mounting pressure. The middle sinks into paralysis, where wanting something clashes with a motivation that has been steadily leached away, and numbing the ache only blurs whatever sense of direction remained. What lifts it above simple complaint is the turn late on, when grinding survival through one exhausting stretch hardens into determination and the chaos loses its grip rather than winning. That reversal reframes the earlier despair as a phase endured, so the crisis ends up filed among things now behind rather than the permanent condition it first threatened to become.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","anger & defiance"],"means":"grinding survival through chaos refiles the crisis as a phase now behind","built":"the title idiom frames disorder and the song enacts the tug of war"},
"annihilator~smear-campaign": {"haiku":"A target endures lies, defamation, and slander from a jealous coward who spreads propaganda to damage their reputation. The song promises the perpetrator's eventual downfall and the speaker's survival despite the attack.","opus":"A pointed rebuke to someone waging a slander campaign of lies and defamation to victimize the narrator; the deeper read is defiant survival against a jealous, insecure attacker, refusing to have his name dragged down and hoping the liar rots once their fabricated stories collapse.","sonnet":"A response to a targeted personal attack — someone has been spreading fabricated stories and using social defamation as a weapon, and the narrator confronts them with the accusation that it all stems from jealousy and insecurity, and that their campaign will ultimately destroy themselves.","src":"haiku","fable":"A direct address to a slanderer, the song reads their whisper campaign as a form of parasitism: the accuser feeds on the narrator's name to feel powerful, dressing personal spite up as righteous crusade. The tell is that every charge doubles as a confession — the one preaching hatred and inventing stories is diagnosed as weak, insecure and jealous, so the smear is really an admission of envy. The arc moves from wounded defiance (you will never drain the life from me) to a colder verdict, dropping the pretense of taking the high road to openly wish the coward's rot, which lands the song's real subject: the moment forgiveness curdles into contempt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"every charge the slanderer levels doubles as a confession of their own envy","built":"the arc from wounded defiance to open contempt tracks the moment forgiveness curdles"},
"annihilator~smothered": {"haiku":"Exhaustion and contempt from others—relentless criticism (appearance, intelligence, dreams, race) piles on until the speaker suffocates under judgment.","opus":"Nearing breakdown and running out of time, the narrator absorbs a barrage of belittling voices dismissing his dreams over his color, size, and worth, yet resolves to fight on. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' thrash project, stage the crushing weight of other people's contempt, the spoken tirade of insults answered by determination that the pain will eventually lift.","sonnet":"A person crushed by relentless dismissal — every dream labeled fantasy, every aspiration mocked on grounds of intelligence, language, appearance — reaches breaking point. The final verses shift: the accumulation of voices trying to smother ambition becomes the argument for refusing to stop.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man near his breaking point tallies the exhaustion of pouring himself out for years and getting nothing back, the pressure bearing down with the crushing weight the title promises. The bridge is where it turns cruel and specific, a barrage of hostile voices auditing every supposed flaw, from his accent and complexion to his height and his weight, until the abuse stops being critique and becomes a machine built purely to grind a person into the ground. Against that pile-on the closing turn hardens into flat defiance, insisting the tormentors be tuned out and that sheer stubbornness will carry the fight through to the far side of the pain, even as the battered refrain keeps returning to have the final say.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"stubbornness offered as the only shield against a grinding pile-on","built":"the bridge narrows from exhaustion to a specific audit of body and accent"},
"annihilator~snake-in-the-grass": {"haiku":"A betrayer filled the world with lies and infidelity; the speaker finally sees through the disgrace and declares the serpent has no class and must be expelled.","opus":"Annihilator unleash a furious kiss-off to a lover who took the narrator for granted, drowned him in lies and cheated freely. Having finally reached his limit, he brands her a treacherous, classless creature and flips the script, declaring himself free, promising brighter days ahead, and telling her to go find some other victim to torment.","sonnet":"A breakup song driven by the discovery that a partner has been cheating and lying throughout the relationship — the narrator moves from devastation through fury to contempt, finally turning the tables and deciding the unfaithful partner was never good enough for him anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song that starts as wounded devotion and hardens into a verdict, tracking the exact minute love curdles into contempt. The early verses swear boundless sacrifice and gratitude, then the tone flips: the narrator stops pleading for answers and begins prosecuting, cataloguing the lies and the running-around as evidence. The title insult lands as diagnosis rather than heat-of-the-moment cruelty, recasting the beloved as a cold-blooded predator who was always working an angle. By the end the narrator inverts the original wound, deciding the failure was never his and that tomorrow belongs to him, a reversal Jeff Waters underscores with Set the World on Fire’s melodic-thrash swagger.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance"],"means":"wounded devotion hardening track by track into a prosecutor's verdict","built":"the title insult lands as diagnosis rather than heat-of-the-moment cruelty"},
"annihilator~something-witchy": {"haiku":"Escaping an abusive, emotionally toxic relationship where the partner is unpredictably violent and emotionally unstable.","opus":"A scorching exit from an abusive partner, the narrator worn down by volatility, cruelty and hurled objects, baffled by the shouting and finally walking out, putting vast distance between them and sarcastically wishing the tormentor well. It names emotional abuse and escapes it. Annihilator pair precise technical thrash with hard-won, bitter relief.","sonnet":"Escape from a volatile, abusive relationship — the other person's mood swings and rages have made the home a hell, and the speaker finally walks out the door with relief rather than grief. The strangeness that once seemed like fascination now just reads as instability, and getting a million miles away feels like the sanest thing they've ever done.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man narrates the collapse of an abusive relationship as a slow demotion from bewilderment to escape. Early on he cannot even parse the shouting aimed at him, so he reaches for the supernatural, branding the partner as bewitched or unhinged because ordinary explanation fails. The chorus keeps that occult framing as a shield: naming her a spell-caster lets him externalize a cruelty he refuses to sit inside, so the blame stays outside rather than shared. Only in the final turn does the register drop into something bitterly gracious, a mock-polite send-off wishing her the fortune she will need as he claims the distance that proves he finally walked out the door. Fitting for Annihilator, whose Jeff Waters catalog leans on domestic menace and thrash's taste for the sinister, the witch trope works as insult and self-exoneration at once.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"the occult label used to keep blame outside rather than shared","built":"mock-polite send-off at the close signals the distance he finally claimed"},
"annihilator~sonic-homicide": {"haiku":"Intense music becomes a form of torture and manipulation, slowly driving someone toward madness and despair through relentless assault on the senses.","opus":"Jeff Waters' Canadian thrash institution builds a conceit where music itself becomes a murder weapon, the aggressor demanding submission and control from his listener. Beneath the cartoon violence, the track pivots inward: the real fight is against madness and torment, the assault doubling as one man's struggle to survive his own mind.","sonnet":"Sound as weapon and the slow damage it does — not a metaphor for music but a literal account of being ground down inch by inch, frozen, stabbed through the ears, until reality blurs and shame sets in. The second half turns: the victim decides to face the thing that has been killing them, raise their head, call up whatever power remains, and fight back. Devastation acknowledged, then refused.","src":"haiku","fable":"The killer here is a person, not a weapon, and the murder is done with speech: a domineering abuser who whittles at the mind in slow degrees, dulling the agony to a needle-point while it unravels the self thread by thread. Jeff Waters channels the cruelty through the ear as coercion, a demand for obedience and total surrender aimed at the tormentor whose spoken transmission lands as fatally as any puncturing blade, the ruin fixed from the opening word. The victim goes frozen and glazed over, sliding toward a delirium where shame and self-accusation smear into what is real, decades of it scooping out a life barely holding on. Then that same voice turns survivor: a pledge to swing toward first light, to gather faith and meet the fury head-on. Summoning its own specters reads less as self-exorcism than as the will to confront the monster and rouse the dead.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","surveillance & control","anger & defiance"],"means":"spoken coercion delivered as lethally as any blade","built":"the same voice that murders the self turns survivor summoning its own specters"},
"annihilator~sounds-good-to-me": {"haiku":"An invitation to escape reality through daydreaming and fantasy, dismissing life's problems. The speaker offers a shared dreamscape where feeling good matters more than confronting what's real.","opus":"An uncharacteristically upbeat Annihilator track that coaxes the listener into deliberate escapism, urging them to shut out daily troubles through shared fantasy. Jeff Waters swaps thrash menace for melodic optimism, pitching imagination and reverie as an available cure whenever life gets heavy.","sonnet":"An invitation to retreat into daydream whenever the world becomes too heavy — not escapism exactly, but the deliberate cultivation of imaginative space as a survival tool. The ocean-voyage imagery frames the inner world as a real destination worth sailing toward.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a plain invitation to daydream your way past trouble, unusually sunny for Annihilator, a Canadian thrash outfit whose stock-in-trade was menace and precision riffing. The lyric works as a hand held out: shut your eyes, drift off, and treat waking life as a wonderland you enter at will. The bridge is the only nod to reality, admitting everyone carries problems before shrugging them off with escape offered as the single remedy. That easy shrug is the whole point, an arena-sized mood lift built to sound good rather than to interrogate itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape"],"means":"escape offered as the single remedy, no interrogation attached","built":"the bridge nods to real trouble only to shrug it off immediately"},
"annihilator~speed": {"haiku":"An all-consuming drive for speed and velocity overriding everything else. The speaker is obsessed with acceleration and breaking limits, treating it as their mission and fuel.","opus":"Annihilator celebrate raw velocity as obsession, a narrator on a mission to break the speed of sound, craving more acceleration though branded crazy and bound for disaster. Rocket and racing imagery fuse thrill with self-destruction. The thrash band matches its own musical speed to the lyric's, glorifying the adrenaline chase of pushing every limit to the floor.","sonnet":"Pure velocity as obsession and identity — the narrator exists only in acceleration, leaving all competition behind in pursuit of an absolute that may destroy him. The thrill of momentum is indistinguishable from compulsion.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pure adrenaline anthem that fuses two archetypes of velocity-as-identity: the drag racer and the predator, so that the machine's headlong rush and the hunt for a rival become the same impulse. What keeps it from being simple boast is the self-aware admission woven through the chorus - the narrator repeats that everyone calls him crazy and bound for disaster, and rather than deny it he demands more of the very thing that will wreck him, making the song a knowing portrait of the thrill-addict who has confused acceleration with purpose. Fitting for Jeff Waters and Annihilator, whose whole appeal is technical speed as its own reward, the track is less about a destination than about the ecstasy of the throttle, right down to the wish to outrun sound itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"knowing portrait of a thrill-addict who has confused acceleration with purpose","built":"narrator repeats everyone calls him crazy and demands more of the very thing anyway"},
"annihilator~stonewall": {"haiku":"Corporations poison land, water, and air for profit while facing negligible fines; the song urges listening to the earth's cries as human greed destroys the source of life.","opus":"One of Annihilator's most overtly green songs attacks industrial polluters whose token fines mean nothing to the wealthy. Waters gives the damaged planet a voice no one heeds, casting toxic industry as false progress, and the chorus turns the argument on us: humanity is its own greatest threat and must act before it destroys itself.","sonnet":"Corporate pollution of land and water is indicted as a structural failure enabled by toothless fines that mean nothing to those with bottomless pockets. Earth is figured as a living being being burned to its raw core, her cries unheard while shareholders treat environmental destruction as an acceptable externality.","src":"haiku","fable":"Annihilator turn their technical thrash toward ecological accusation, personifying the earth as a violated woman whose streams, once teeming with creatures, are smudged into toxic runoff by corporate chemistry that no penalty can deter. The refrain about a dime meaning nothing to a millionaire carries the real charge, since fines priced as pocket change let industry keep poisoning while pretending the ledger has somehow been balanced. Beneath the environmental sermon runs a bleaker verdict, that humanity is its own worst adversary, torching the very source of its survival for riches it refuses to safeguard and wrecking the ground it stands on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nature & the elements","politics & society"],"means":"fines priced as pocket change let industry poison while calling it settled","built":"the dime-to-a-millionaire refrain carries the ecological charge"},
"annihilator~striker": {"haiku":"A warrior charges into battle with aggressive confidence, overwhelming opponents through precision and relentless conviction. The song glorifies combat victory and dominance.","opus":"The narrator rouses a savage rage to flatten every challenger, promising zero mercy and a fearsome name that settles clashes before they start. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' technical thrash band, deliver pure combative swagger, imagery of gliding into place and overrunning every rival making sheer dominance the whole point.","sonnet":"Pure predatory confidence — a competitor who has silenced every challenger through precision, hunger, and the certainty that the battle is already won before it ends. Intimidation is the brand, and the striker treats opposition as an obstacle too minor to name individually.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pure adrenaline boast, the kind of first-person conqueror anthem that thrash and power metal trade in, where a nameless warrior narrates his own inevitability into being. The whole thing is built on the logic of intimidation as prophecy: the beast doesn't fight to win so much as announce that the outcome is already settled, that reputation alone finishes the battle before it starts. There's no opponent with a face and no stakes beyond dominance itself, which is the point rather than a flaw. Fitting the album's title, the song is less a story than a psych-up chant, the fury being woken and pointed at whoever is unlucky enough to stand in front of it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","anger & defiance"],"means":"reputation alone settles the outcome before any battle begins","built":"song operates as psych-up chant with no opponent's face and no stakes beyond dominance"},
"annihilator~syn-kill-1": {"haiku":"Terrorism and violent revolutionary action; assassination, kidnapping, and bombing as tools of ideological warfare.","opus":"A terrorist's chilling first-person boast of killing for god and power and for propaganda's sake, justifying kidnap, murder, arson, and bombing while threatening a targeted victim with death.","sonnet":"Voiced as a terrorist or political assassin, the song lays out a campaign of kidnapping, murder, bombing, and intimidation carried out in the name of a cause — the speaker justifies the killing of innocents through ideology and the pursuit of power.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters writes from inside the head of a zealot who launders slaughter through righteousness, insisting a deity and the raw appetite for control both bless the same trigger finger. The threats escalate methodically from stalking a marked victim toward explosives, hostages, and a barrel pressed to the temple, each atrocity dressed up as a justified cause the speaker demands you respect rather than resist. What curdles the whole tirade is its self-certainty; the voice never wavers, treating terror as a holy vocation, so the return of that opening boast lands as a closed loop of unrepentant conviction rather than any reckoning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"atrocity sanctified by its own closed conviction","built":"the opening boast returns unaltered, sealing a loop with no exit"},
"annihilator~the-blackest-day": {"haiku":"A celestial collision destroys the world, with humanity burning and crumbling into dust as civilization succumbs to cosmic catastrophe.","opus":"Jeff Waters' thrash outfit stages an extinction-level asteroid strike, a city-sized rock crashing down at dawn and reducing humanity to dust. The song savors the sheer cosmic scale of the disaster, treating the annihilation of everything as an inevitable, apocalyptic reckoning.","sonnet":"A mountain-sized asteroid strikes without warning. The song catalogues the annihilation in real time — fire, screams, cities turned to dust — framing the event as the ultimate blackest day: sudden, total, and without any possibility of survival or redemption.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is extinction watched in real time, an impact event narrated by witnesses who can only stand and count the seconds. Jeff Waters stages the destroyer promised by the band's name as a literal falling body, a rock the width of a city bearing down at dawn while everyone below is still defenseless. The song moves through three stages: the descent against a wrong-colored morning, the fire and melting flesh at contact, then a planet grinding itself to powder and corrosion. What lands hardest is the helplessness, a doom with no enemy to fight and no defense to raise, only the arithmetic of how little time remains.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","nature & the elements"],"means":"doom with no enemy to fight, only the arithmetic of remaining time","built":"three-stage structure mirrors the impact event itself: descent, contact, erasure"},
"annihilator~the-box": {"haiku":"Television as a puppet-master shaping desires and consciousness. The TV chooses heroes, sells violence and scandals, and controls what people believe life should be.","opus":"Annihilator turn television into a menacing seducer peddling scandal, violence and prefab idols while daring viewers to resist switching it on. Midway the power shifts and the audience claims agency over who they follow, so the track lands as a critique of media manipulation, the screen both dream-maker and nightmare shaping expectations of life.","sonnet":"Television speaks in the first person, seducing the viewer with scandal, violence, and ready-made heroism. It offers a vision of how life should be lived and invites passive devotion — before a rueful aside concedes it is only a screen, only a dream.","src":"haiku","fable":"The television speaks in the first person as a seductive predator, promising scandal, manufactured violence, and ready-made idols while it reaches for your hand. Its invitation to follow curdles into something possessive: it will pick the people you admire, then lead you wherever it likes. The final turn is the con laid bare, as it shrugs off all power by insisting it is merely a screen, merely a passing fever, even as the pronoun shifts and folds you into the choosing. That single denial does the damage, absolving the machine at the exact moment you have been absorbed into it. The refrain against being pushed toys with the button that summons it and the menace of a thing that promises to push back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"the television's denial of its own power is the moment of absorption","built":"pronoun shifts at the close to fold you into the choosing it claims not to do"},
"annihilator~the-edge": {"haiku":"The speaker walks a risky line between restlessness and survival, rejecting conformity and school's rules for street wisdom. The edge is both strength and a precarious way of living.","opus":"A young rebel's creed rejecting school, security and other people's plans in favor of a dangerous, self-taught life lived on the knife's edge. Classic thrash bravado, what doesn't kill you sharpening into a whole ethos of refusing to be held down.","sonnet":"A young person rejecting the classroom and conventional security to live by an instinct sharpened on the street — the edge is a way of life that cuts like steel, something you either have or you don't, and it's what drives him out of town.","src":"haiku","fable":"A restless kid rejects the classroom and every promise of a safe, planned-out future, insisting that the schooling which actually counts happens out where raw survival gets taught firsthand. Jeff Waters frames the whole thing as a creed rather than a grievance, so the burning impatience to bolt from a dead-end town swells into an entire philosophy of living dangerously and answering to nobody at all. What could pass for teenage bravado curdles into something colder underneath, since the singer treats mortal risk itself as his lone guardian, the single force he trusts to keep him upright and breathing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"mortal risk embraced as the only guardian worth trusting","built":"impatience to flee reframed as a full philosophy of living dangerously"},
"annihilator~the-fun-palace": {"haiku":"A haunted house forces someone to confront their guilt for exploiting and harming innocent lives.","opus":"A horror concept piece where a sinister keeper drags a visitor through a house of confronted sins, the twist being that the crime on trial is the theft of a child's innocence and stolen dreams of growing up.","sonnet":"A supernatural haunted-house scenario where the narrator forces a guilty party to face suppressed memories of harm done to children — young lives ruined and innocence destroyed — as a form of psychic punishment.","src":"haiku","fable":"A leering host welcomes his visitor into a haunted funhouse that turns out to be the visitor's own conscience, staging a trial where guilt long buried in the unconscious is dragged up to be confessed aloud. Annihilator's Jeff Waters frames the horror around children the accused wronged, their stolen youth and canceled futures presented as the verdict, so the amusement-park mask hides a courtroom convened for crimes against the young. The keeper's cheery farewell hands over a bitter souvenir and locks the man back into waking life carrying every sin, the whole ride revealed as damnation dressed up as entertainment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"guilt long buried dragged into a trial dressed as entertainment","built":"amusement-park host reveals himself as a judge of crimes against children"},
"annihilator~the-fun-palace-extended-mix": {"haiku":"Guilt manifests as haunted house where conscience forces confrontation with murdered innocence. The murderer faces phantoms of denied victims within the architecture of repression.","opus":"A guilt-driven psychodrama in which a murderer who got away with it is dragged back through a nightmare house until confession is the only escape. The conscience itself is the jailer, and the crime is stolen childhoods.","sonnet":"Guilt personified runs a psychic funhouse that forces a murderer who got away to relive the faces of victims they denied a future — \"confess,\" the palace demands — the nightmares so intense that confession becomes the only escape from the recurring horror show.","src":"haiku","fable":"A leering host welcomes his visitor into a haunted funhouse that turns out to be the visitor's own conscience, staging a trial where guilt long buried in the unconscious is dragged up to be confessed aloud. Annihilator's Jeff Waters frames the horror around children the accused wronged, their stolen youth and canceled futures presented as the verdict, so the amusement-park mask hides a courtroom convened for crimes against the young. The keeper's cheery farewell hands over a bitter souvenir and locks the man back into waking life carrying every sin, the whole ride revealed as damnation dressed up as entertainment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"funhouse revealed as a conscience staging trial for buried crimes against children","built":"the keeper's cheery farewell hands guilt back into waking life, not absolution"},
"annihilator~the-nightmare-factory": {"haiku":"A descent into nightmares and terror during sleep, depicting horrific imagery and the loss of control when consciousness surrenders to darkness.","opus":"A sleep-paralysis descent where drifting toward rest curls into pure horror, the body soaked and trembling as imagined mutilations pile up faster than the mind can wake. Annihilator, the Canadian thrash outfit built around guitarist Jeff Waters, made a career of technical riffing wrapped around lurid, cinematic dread, and this fits their late-period appetite for turning ordinary terrors into a hyperactive gallery of grotesques.","sonnet":"Sleep as ambush — the body begs for rest but darkness delivers horrors: collapsing walls, spiders, the reaper, skin peeling away. Each attempt to drift off triggers a new cascade of dread. The song holds the exhausted terror of someone who can't escape into unconsciousness, waking soaked and shaking, only to lay their head down and start the nightmare again.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sleep is supposed to be the escape, so the drift toward oblivion begins as relief, a body glad to go anywhere but here until the surrender curdles and the descent turns predatory. Jeff Waters stacks the terror as a rhyming assembly line of afflictions, each horror clocking in behind the last, and that relentless pairing is the point, since the factory runs on sheer volume and never lets the dreamer reach a clear waking edge. The jolt upright and the shivering plea for calm buy nothing, because surrender only feeds the machine another shift, and the final turn, a vanished child pleading to nobody, drops the private horror into a colder loneliness where no rescue is even listening.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","death & grief","freedom & escape"],"means":"sleep that feeds the horror instead of suspending it","built":"rhyming assembly-line structure mimics the factory it names"},
"annihilator~the-one": {"haiku":"A speaker desperately loves someone far away and unattainable, missing them and thinking of them as an angel; he wishes to be with them forever like a king for a queen.","opus":"A rare tender turn for Waters, this ballad aches with longing for a distant beloved who feels like an angel from above yet remains just out of reach. Sleep fails because the missing presence intrudes, and the dream of being a king for his queen is undercut by the ache that it belongs to neither story nor dream. Devotion here is plainspoken yearning rather than the band's usual menace.","sonnet":"Longing for a distant beloved at night pulls the narrator into wakefulness, the ache too sharp for sleep. The aspiration is simple and total — permanence, a real relationship rather than a fantasy — and the repetition of being unable to forget maps the insomnia of genuine love.","src":"haiku","fable":"For a thrash outfit built on Jeff Waters' precision riffing, this is the naked ballad in the set, and its ache is that longing outruns the reach: the beloved stays distant even as the singer catalogues her nearness. The fantasy he reaches for is a fairy-tale coronation, two lovers crowned and bound forever, and the moment he names it he disowns it, insisting this is neither bedtime tale nor sleeping vision but something he wants to survive waking. What lingers underneath is loneliness rather than romance, since he admits he cannot sleep for missing her, and the endless cooing refrain that ends the song works less as celebration than as a lullaby he hums to soothe himself in her absence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the lullaby refrain soothes the singer in her absence not her presence","built":"the fairy-tale coronation named then immediately disowned as neither dream nor sleep"},
"annihilator~the-other-side": {"haiku":"Domestic violence cycles endlessly—rage locked in cages, fists and phones as weapons, denial and blame—until one victim escapes to 'the other side.'","opus":"A grim account of intimate-partner violence where an idealized romance masks routine beatings, denial and repeated cycles of rage. The song tracks the victim's exhaustion and eventual break for freedom, closing on the unnerving certainty that the abuser will simply find someone new to hurt, with fatal stakes.","sonnet":"A song about domestic violence — a relationship that presented itself as love but was marked by physical abuse, phone calls to the police, denial cycles, and the eventual recognition that the abuser will simply move on to their next victim unless the cycle is broken.","src":"haiku","fable":"A marriage sold as a fairy tale curdles the instant tenderness arrives as a blow, and the song tracks a battered partner's slow arithmetic toward the door. Annihilator, a thrash band whose catalog usually chases speed and technique, turns plainer and angrier here, letting the caged-animal refrain loop back the way abuse itself recurs. Exhaustion, not fury, becomes the engine of departure: worn past endurance, the narrator rejects the manipulation and the person behind it. The coldest turn watches the abuser drift toward a new household to repeat the ritual, naming where the pattern leads if nobody breaks the loop.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","heartbreak & loss","freedom & escape"],"means":"exhaustion rather than fury driving departure from an abusive cycle","built":"the looping refrain replicates the recurrence of abuse itself"},
"annihilator~the-pastor-of-disaster": {"haiku":"A malevolent figure manipulates and corrupts those who make deals with him. Victims realize too late they've sold their souls to disaster.","opus":"The devil recast as a slick, sermonizing salesman making a Faustian sales pitch too tempting to turn down, one that damns the buyer's soul beyond any redemption. The song's hook repeatedly warns listeners never to strike a bargain with this diabolical preacher who spreads ruin and demands their worship.","sonnet":"The Devil as a slick deal-maker who promises what the desperate want to hear, then collects their soul and puts them to work spreading his plague — a classic Faustian trap sung from the tempter's own mouth.","src":"haiku","fable":"A devil-figure works the classic Faustian sales pitch, robed as a preacher, promising to soothe a soul supposedly emptied of anything left to lose before it is ordered to kneel. Annihilator, forever drawn to the swaggering monster-narrator, lets the bargainer's mask slip as clergy gives way to a decorated warlord loosing a plague on the world, exposing the huckster beneath the vestments. The closing rounds splinter into a cornered voice begging for rescue against a chorus warning off any bargain, so the tempter's boast collapses into the panic of the damned who already signed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","artifice & performance","death & grief"],"means":"the tempter's boast collapsing into the panic of the one who already signed","built":"clergy mask gives way to decorated warlord, exposing the huckster beneath the vestments mid-song"},
"annihilator~the-perfect-virus": {"haiku":"A sentient computer virus spreads through systems to destroy, corrupt data, and trigger technological collapse; it learns and evolves, leaving humanity unable to stop it.","opus":"A gleeful hymn to digital doomsday told from the machine's own point of view, a self-teaching contagion that slips in, ruins systems, and vanishes leaving mass technological death and an untraceable creator. Annihilator, Jeff Waters's Canadian thrash vehicle, loved this clinical menace, giving the fear of runaway machine minds the band's cold, precise riffing.","sonnet":"A self-propagating digital weapon described from the inside out — entering systems like a predator, wiping data, rendering all defences useless before any defence can mount. The creator is absent and untraceable, leaving only ruin behind. Cold and clinical in tone, it treats technological annihilation as inevitability, a genocide of information with no survivors and no one to blame.","src":"haiku","fable":"A malware author's boast, staged as apocalypse, tracing one engineered contagion from its quiet infiltration of a network to the wreckage it leaves behind. Jeff Waters treats the machine as a body: an infection burrows into the mainframe, corrupts what matters, and outpaces every cure until the antidote arrives too late. The turn is that the code teaches itself, an early-2000s dread of intelligence that improves past its maker's intent and slips his leash. What lands hardest is the close, where survivors salvage what remains while the architect of the Trojan horse simply vanishes, unnamed and unpunished. The true horror is not the damage but the absent hand that willed it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","death & grief"],"means":"the architect vanishes unpunished while survivors count the wreckage","built":"the virus mapped onto a body — infection, corruption, cure arriving too late"},
"annihilator~the-rush": {"haiku":"Adrenaline and addiction take over, driving someone to repeat dangerous acts. Once they taste the rush, they cannot stop, despite knowing it is consuming them.","opus":"Addiction rendered as an overwhelming euphoria the narrator cannot resist, the same craving that lifts him also destroying him bit by bit. The band frames the ritual of chasing that feeling as something he keeps returning to despite knowing it is killing him.","sonnet":"Addiction takes hold as an irresistible physical takeover — the sensation so overwhelming it rewires everything that follows. The narrator recognises the slow destruction, the insanity building, yet the ritual keeps restarting because the compulsion has become larger than any intention to stop.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters writes addiction as a euphoric summit that immediately reveals its price, the speaker begging to be lifted from misery before admitting the high itself is what keeps dragging him back for more. The chorus reframes the craving as an unstoppable force that seizes control after a single taste, promising captivity dressed up as pleasure, so the ecstatic invitation and the trap collapse into one gesture. By the final verse the mask drops entirely into ritual, insanity, and adrenaline overload, the same craving now naming its own destruction rather than the paradise it advertised earlier.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the ecstatic invitation and the trap collapse into one gesture","built":"chorus reframes craving as captivity dressed up as pleasure"},
"annihilator~the-trend": {"haiku":"Criticism of those who plagiarize, fake authenticity, and mindlessly follow trends instead of creating something original. The song accuses trend-followers of being parasites who steal from previous generations.","opus":"A scornful attack on a poser who fakes authenticity and chases whatever is fashionable, dismissed as a parasite selling counterfeit trends to a gullible young generation.","sonnet":"An attack on opportunists who imitate and exploit a music culture they never genuinely belonged to, condemning them as corporate parasites who sell counterfeit identity to a naive young audience.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters turns a lifelong thrash purist's contempt on the bandwagon act that borrows a subculture's clothing and slang while manufacturing nothing it can honestly claim as its own. The sneering mall-store aside punctures the pose completely, exposing a fashion bought off a rack rather than a scene earned through years of unglamorous devotion. Its real charge is a robbery across time: the young are sold a hollow imitation by opportunists who ride an aesthetic until it stops paying, then discard it with the same shrug they first wore it on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"a subculture looted for fashion and discarded when the payout stops","built":"the mall-store aside punctures the pose in a single aside rather than an argument"},
"annihilator~time-bomb": {"haiku":"An apocalyptic narrative where humanity's own creation becomes an instrument of mass extinction, reducing civilization to destruction and death.","opus":"A killing machine turns on its creators in a near-future extermination, tallied in blunt arithmetic as a billion corpses while the chorus barks its terms like a countdown. Annihilator, the Canadian thrash band led by Jeff Waters, forge this Terminator-shaped nightmare into a relentless death march, sci-fi overkill and clipped military cadence dressing the genre's Cold War fears in chrome.","sonnet":"A sci-fi horror premise told as thrash manifesto: in 2027, humanity builds the ultimate killing machine and the machine turns on its makers, wiping out a billion lives. The song is pure escalation — condition red, overkill, no redemption — a warning against creation without conscience dressed up as pure, ecstatic destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Terminator-flavored genre set-piece: a spoken briefing dates the machine apocalypse to a near future, then the song hands its entire vocabulary over to that machine's targeting logic, stacking clipped status-report phrases where every readout runs red and every subject is a body to be crushed. The point isn't a story so much as the texture of extermination as bureaucracy — kill converted into a checklist, a billion dead reported like a gauge reading. The counted death toll and the volley of the same one-syllable verb do the real work, letting the annihilation land as tally and chant rather than narrative, which is exactly the thrill the thrash idiom is chasing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","surveillance & control"],"means":"extermination repackaged as a bureaucratic readout","built":"the counted death toll chanted as a gauge reading"},
"annihilator~too-far-gone": {"haiku":"A hell-bent individual refuses to change course despite pressure and obstacles, committed to their singular goal.","opus":"A hardened statement of unbreakable will, the narrator warning that pressure only makes him explode and that no one can shake him from his one-track, hell-bent path. He will leave anyone who blocks him behind, claiming his time on his own terms. Annihilator, the Canadian thrash band fronted by Jeff Waters's guitar work, cast this as a defiant refusal to be corrected, all clenched resolve and forward momentum.","sonnet":"Single-minded determination treated as identity — the speaker is so locked onto a path that outside pressure to change registers only as confirmation they're doing something right. The double-voiced chorus, first person then third, makes the song almost an argument with itself: everyone tries to shake you loose precisely because you're committed enough to be threatening.","src":"haiku","fable":"A defiant self-portrait of obsession hardening into identity: the speaker frames outside interference as a fuel source, insisting that every attempt to redirect him only intensifies the reaction rather than dampening it. The chorus pivots strikingly from first person to second, warning some other soul that they are past saving and beyond the reach of anyone hoping to divert them, so the singer's own stubbornness becomes a mirror for a doomed listener. That fixed-purpose aggression is pure Jeff Waters territory, the thrash architect writing determination as something closer to a threat than a virtue, where single-minded resolve slides toward menace and the boundary between conviction and warning dissolves entirely.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"every attempt to redirect only intensifies the reaction","built":"the chorus pivots to second person, turning the singer's stubbornness into a mirror for the listener"},
"annihilator~torn": {"haiku":"A person built walls around guilt and trauma, but those walls are cracking; they descend deep into their soul to confront their demons and rebuild themselves free of guilt.","opus":"A wrenching account of guilt and rebirth: a man whose sins are buried like roots watches the walls of his built-up self crack, endures a nightmare that never ends, then digs through his own soul to emerge free and unshakable. Annihilator, Jeff Waters's Canadian thrash project, threads redemption through the horror imagery, trading pure carnage for a more introspective arc of suffering conquered.","sonnet":"A reckoning with guilt recast as architecture — sins buried like roots, a castle of shame whose walls have cracked. The speaker passes through the nightmare of self-destruction and comes out the other side, having found something inside themselves that finally holds. The rebuilt walls are stronger now, and the guilt is gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A redemption narrative built as a mirror: the same castle image opens the song cracking under guilt and closes it rebuilt with unshakable walls, so the structure itself performs the turn from being torn apart to being made whole. What holds the halves together is that the self is both the fortress and its own besieger, meaning the crisis and the cure are the same act of searching inward. The middle nightmare-refrain is the pivot where suffering gets reframed as a demon reborn rather than a defeat, converting torment into the rage that powers the escape.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"the self both fortress and besieger of its own walls","built":"the castle image cracks open then rebuilds to frame the turn"},
"annihilator~tricks-and-traps": {"haiku":"Life's unpredictable cruelty and chaos disguised as opportunity, with a malevolent force manipulating others for entertainment.","opus":"A sinister figure posing as a friend while confessing he brings pleasure and pain, heals and diseases, ruins playtime for fun and drags you down to your knees with illusions and random damage. It reads as a portrait of manipulation or life's cruelty personified. Annihilator, the Canadian thrash band, give this two-faced trickster a swaggering menace, Jeff Waters' sharp riffing underscoring the warning to beware what pretends to care.","sonnet":"Life personified as a con artist that both heals and poisons — it lures you in with friendship, then cycles through pleasure and pain at random, building illusions and then demolishing them. The only wisdom available is vigilance: don't let the tricks get to you, don't mistake the bait for the prize.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sly, tormenting voice opens with counterfeit friendship, pretending to share your exhaustion so it can draw a lonely listener close enough to be worked by the sales patter it is about to unspool. The mask then slips as the speaker boasts of dealing out comfort and ruin interchangeably, curing then infecting on a whim, staging fake wonders and letdowns until kneeling starts to feel like the only honest answer left. On Jeff Waters' stark, largely solo Remains, that figure lands as existence itself dressed up as a cruel carnival barker, one whose harm arrives by pure chance rather than any moral logic a victim could bargain against. The closing swerve into racing-mind delirium concedes the con was never cleanly separable from the mind receiving it, so the warning to stay wary sours into the sense that tormentor and torment draw the same restless breath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","madness & the mind","artifice & performance"],"means":"tormentor and torment revealed as drawing the same restless breath","built":"the closing delirium concedes the con was never cleanly separable from the mind receiving it"},
"annihilator~ultra-motion": {"haiku":"An obsession with speed and pure velocity as an addictive force. The driver chases acceleration and aggression above all else, merging with the machine into unstoppable forward motion.","opus":"A hymn to speed, machinery, and raw power as a consuming fixation, fusing velocity and aggression into one relentless engine. Waters' band channels its technical playing into the fantasy of an invincible machine tearing ahead, craving energy and force above any destination.","sonnet":"Speed and aggression fused into a single physical experience — an engine-like persona driven past all limits by adrenaline and electricity. The song treats velocity not as a vehicle but as an identity, a thermal state where machine and will become indistinguishable.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Waters turns Annihilator's technical-thrash precision into an ode to velocity itself, where a redlined engine and an overclocked nervous system become the same craving for forward thrust with no destination attached. The song is honest that its subject is pure appetite: not where the racer is going but the sheer voltage of the going, a fusion of machine and driver that melts into one indistinguishable charge. That merger is the whole point, so the pileup of combustion and high-current imagery reads less like a story than a throttle held wide open until the words stop steering and simply accelerate.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape"],"means":"velocity as pure appetite with no destination required","built":"machine and driver blur into one indistinguishable charge, the merge being the point"},
"annihilator~ultraparanoia": {"haiku":"A fugitive flees from unseen pursuers and chill-inducing eyes burning to capture their human prey; paranoid flight drives the constant running.","opus":"Thrash as pure adrenaline of the hunted, a fugitive running from his past with unseen eyes burning to chase him down. The paranoia is the whole subject, dread rendered as relentless forward motion toward hell.","sonnet":"A paranoid fugitive song — the narrator is being relentlessly hunted by unseen pursuers, racing to cover his tracks before he's caught, gripped by the sensation that something is always closing in behind him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A mid-90s Annihilator thrash cut that stages paranoia as pure kinetic flight, and its refusal to ever name the pursuer is the whole point: the threat stays faceless because in genuine paranoia the mind manufactures watchers rather than confronting any real enemy, so the burning eyes and the frantically covered tracks are the fear generating its own persecutors. Jeff Waters coins the title Ultraparanoia and then literalizes it, collapsing the fugitive-fleeing-justice cliché into a breathless first-person present where running from the past and running toward hell become one indivisible motion — no destination, only compulsion. The clipped two-line chorus enacts exactly that by refusing to develop: it tightens and repeats instead of resolving, the musical equivalent of a mind that can only keep moving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","freedom & escape"],"means":"the faceless pursuer stays faceless because fear manufactures its own watchers","built":"the undeveloped two-line chorus tightens and repeats instead of resolving"},
"annihilator~voices-and-victims": {"haiku":"Isolation and overwhelm—the speaker locks themselves away to escape the constant pressure of outside voices and people demanding access.","opus":"Locked safely inside from the intruding world, the narrator craves solitude even as sitting alone turns everything he sees to stone, sickened by voices trying to reach him. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' thrash outfit, render self-imposed isolation with ambivalence here, the barricaded door offering both refuge and the slow petrification of a mind left too long alone.","sonnet":"A person who has locked themselves inside their own room to escape an overwhelming outside world settles into chosen solitude. The darkness and silence are not a crisis but a refuge — too many voices, too many claims on the interior life, and withdrawal is the only honest response available.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of chosen isolation as armor rather than defeat. The door is bolted from the inside, and the narrator frames that confinement as safety, not a cage. What sours the retreat is the world pressing in, its noise trying to reach somewhere private and untouched. The turn comes when solitude stops being escape and becomes appetite: the dark is craved, not merely tolerated. The recurring complaint about voices and victims collapses the wounding and the wounded into one category, so every contact reads as contamination worth sealing out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape"],"means":"solitude chosen as armor until the dark becomes appetite","built":"the title collapses wounding and wounded into a single category"},
"annihilator~w-t-y-d":{"haiku":"Welcomes the listener to death and the afterlife, where they encounter an endless cycle and spectral presence. The song treats death as initiation into a new strange realm.","opus":"A menacing welcome to death, addressing the listener as someone born into death and lured toward their own end. Through images of sin, slain hands beckoning, and tempting wine, it presents death as inescapable and grimly inviting.","sonnet":"A tour through grotesque death imagery — burning stars, fields of sin, the beckoning of those already slain — that cumulatively constructs a ritualistic welcome to oblivion, inviting the listener to slip into death as though it were natural and even hospitable.","src":"haiku","fable":"Death is staged here not as an ending but as a threshold, an emcee's welcome to a place where the newcomer discovers they were never fully alive to begin with. The song's engine is a grim paradox of continuity: existence and extinction fold into each other, so that being born is already a form of dying and the dead are the ones extending a hospitable hand. Its imagery pulls the mystical down into the concrete and squalid, dragging cosmic wonder to the level of city pavement and cheap sin, which strips any promised paradise of comfort and leaves only a bleak equivalence. The recurring lure to lower one's guard and taste what seems harmless works as the trap's mechanism, an easy reassurance that the danger is minor even as it delivers the fatal turn. What makes it unsettling is the cheerful conviction of the invitation, the sense that surrender to oblivion is being sold as rest and relief.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"selling surrender to oblivion as rest and welcome","built":"death emcees its own threshold as a cheerful host"},
"annihilator~warbird": {"haiku":"Aerial warfare and annihilation—bombers rain destruction from the sky at 3000 mph; total devastation with no survivors or witnesses.","opus":"War becomes a gleeful spectacle of death rained from the sky, the narrator gloating over total annihilation delivered faster than anyone can react. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' Canadian thrash band, revel in aerial-assault imagery, heaping on dominance and infernal fire until combat reads as exultant overwhelming force rather than any lament.","sonnet":"Total aerial destruction narrated as spectacle — bombs, fire, annihilation delivered at three thousand miles an hour with no survivors and no warning. The warbird is not a metaphor; it is the plain instrument of supremacy, and the lyric revels in the scale of what it obliterates.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is aerial warfare rendered as pure sadistic spectacle, the machine and its pilot fused into a single predatory intelligence that swaggers through devastation without a flicker of doubt or remorse. The song narrates a supersonic strike from inside the aggressor's cockpit, savoring the victim's helplessness against a weapon that arrives faster than the body can register its own destruction. Where Jeff Waters usually threads his riffs through paranoia and creeping menace, here the bravado is total and the vantage never once drops to the ground below, so the worship of overwhelming force stands as the horror, named only in the final breath that calls its own supremacy the ultimate insanity.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder"],"means":"worship of overwhelming force named as insanity only at the close","built":"vantage never drops from the cockpit to the ground below"},
"annihilator~wicked-mystic": {"haiku":"An ancient evil entity feeds on fear and corrupts human hearts, exploiting weakness and breaking moral codes. The song warns that darkness and hatred dwell inside all people and can be awakened.","opus":"An account of an ancient malevolent force that feeds on pain and fear and awakens the hatred lurking in everyone, even the holy, as proof of evil in the soul.","sonnet":"The song argues that hatred lurks in all people — even the devout — and that weakened or curious minds are vulnerable to a dark internal force that awakens the evil within, breaking moral codes and corrupting from the inside out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A malevolent force is imagined as ancient and patient, an entity outliving generations by feeding on human frailty rather than assaulting it. The song's real argument is that no one stands outside its reach: the devout and the merely inquisitive prove equally susceptible, since curiosity itself becomes the crack through which corruption seeps. Jeff Waters, writing on Annihilator's technical thrash debut, frames temptation as a corrosive appetite already seated inside everyone, so the chorus's surrender reads as an inward collapse rather than a contest lost to some outside adversary.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"corruption already seated inside, waiting for curiosity to open the crack","built":"the chorus surrender reads as inward collapse, not a contest lost to any outside force"},
"annihilator~wind": {"haiku":"Seeking peace and mental release by escaping into nature and letting go of accumulated stress and pain.","opus":"A rare moment of calm from a thrash band: someone walking through the dark to unwind, exhausted by noise and pain, letting tension drain and confiding everything to the wind until they feel ready to soar above the clouds. It reads as a quiet defiance, a refusal to be dragged down. Annihilator, Jeff Waters' Canadian outfit, softens its usual aggression here into something closer to melodic release.","sonnet":"Late-night solitude as therapy — a walk in the dark to escape noise and pain, talking to the wind as a way of releasing what can't be said to anyone else. By the end the pressure has lifted, the sky has opened up, and the speaker has found enough peace to face another day without being dragged under.","src":"haiku","fable":"A night walk sold as decompression, the refrain returning like a mantra each time the speaker claims to have unclenched. But the calm is defensive as much as restorative: the repeated address to the wind keeps company with someone who has no one else to talk to, and the closing verse drops the serenity entirely to warn off imitators and detractors, betraying that the tension being released was social wounding all along. The peace here is less a destination reached than a story told to outlast the people who would drag the speaker back down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance"],"means":"calm sold as decompression reveals itself as social wound management","built":"closing warning to detractors betrays the peace was always defensive"},
"annihilator~word-salad": {"haiku":"A mind fractured by chemical torture and psychiatric destruction fragments into incoherent pain, losing moral and physical coherence as identity dissolves.","opus":"A harrowing plunge into psychosis from the inside, hallucination and catatonia narrated as the mind's closets are destroyed. The clinical vocabulary of fear and decay makes the breakdown feel like a documented descent rather than metaphor.","sonnet":"A first-person account of severe psychiatric breakdown — electric shock treatment, forced injections, catatonia, and suicidal ideation — told from inside the institution where language itself has become meaningless noise.","src":"haiku","fable":"A mind narrated from inside a locked ward, where the speaker lies pinned to a bed, jolted awake by current and fed something through the arm he swears is poison rather than cure. The title names the outcome he most dreads: language shattered into fragments that no longer connect, thought worn down until weeping is the only reflex left. His caretakers register as pale figures and crawling vermin, and his defiance curdles from open rage into a flat, indifferent surrender. Jeff Waters, building Annihilator's early thrash as a solo craftsman's showcase, aims the clinical horror less at shock than at a portrait of selfhood dismantled procedure by procedure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"selfhood dismantled procedure by procedure until language itself fragments","built":"the title names the outcome the speaker most dreads, collapsing his dread into the song's own subject"},
"annihilator~wrapped": {"haiku":"Someone is so completely controlled and manipulated by another that escape feels like suicide, yet they contemplate pulling the trigger to break free.","opus":"A toxic love song where devotion and murderous contempt are indistinguishable, being with someone likened to suicide, daring them to pull the trigger. Obsession as mutual destruction.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship described with brutal honesty — the narrator fantasizes about destroying the person who has complete power over them, recognizing that being with them is like a slow suicide.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two voices fight for the same mouth. The verses swagger with fantasies of humiliation and murder, a jilted lover rehearsing every cruelty he would inflict if the power were actually his. Then the chorus exposes the lie: he is the one under a thumb, the one whose thread another hand can tug, and his begging to be hauled free admits the threats were only compensation. That confession lands the whole thing, recasting the bravado as the portrait of a man hooked on a bond he calls slow self-destruction.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"bravado exposed as compensation for being under another's thumb","built":"verse swagger and chorus confession fight for the same mouth"},
"annisokay~coma-blue":{"haiku":"A mind paralyzed by indecision and weightlessness yearns to break free and transform into something new. The speaker is trapped in a liminal state, neither alive nor resolved, longing to escape before decay and time consume them.","opus":"Annisokay's metalcore drifts through a dissociative haze of lost identity, its narrator unsure who he even is and desperate to escape before life runs out. The plea to flee tangles with a colder push to keep adapting and evolving at any cost, so survival and self-erasure blur together as everything decays around him.","sonnet":"Directionless and adrift in a fog of identity crisis, a figure drifts through soulless streets, desperate to escape a life that feels suspended — a coma state of no purpose, no self, and no reason to stay. Flight is the only answer they can imagine.","src":"haiku"},
"annisokay~end-of-the-world":{"opus":"The last survivor's despairing cry in a dead, sunless world ruined by war and humanity's pursuit of fame over care, calling out into empty space for anyone left, broken and utterly alone on a devastated Earth.","sonnet":"A lone survivor of civilizational collapse wanders a dead planet calling out for anyone left, grieving the species' self-destruction through the pursuit of fame over meaning, and naming Earth as a victim of war.","src":"sonnet"},
"annisokay~friend-or-enemy":{"haiku":"The song explores betrayal within relationships where allies become killers and enemies are indistinguishable, questioning trust itself.","opus":"A paranoid, noir-tinted meditation on betrayal where everyone carries a price, allies killing for nothing while foes at least fetch cash, and the real battle is defeating the fear that rules you. Annisokay, the German metalcore band from Halle, work their clean-versus-scream dynamic across a cynical world where loyalty is mere transaction and even the most upright learns no one is safe.","sonnet":"Loyalty as a death sentence — in a world run on transactions and betrayal, the line between ally and target dissolves entirely. Whether you're an enemy they'll kill you for money, or a friend, they'll kill you for nothing. The song urges stepping out of the shadow of fear, but offers no clean exit, only the cold clarity that the system consumes everyone equally.","src":"haiku"},
"annisokay~fully-automatic":{"opus":"A pointed indictment of American gun culture, naming the same store that armed mass shooters and killed John Lennon, and the schoolchildren who don't come home; it satirizes the invocation of gun rights as a birthright while framing gun ownership as an infection.","sonnet":"A pointed critique of American gun culture — naming Columbine, the murder of John Lennon, and school shootings — arguing that the legal sale of weapons is an infection that kills children, masked as constitutional freedom.","src":"sonnet"},
"annisokay~good-stories":{"haiku":"A manipulator writes false narratives and double meanings, filling gaps with lies while leaving emotional wreckage for the narrator to clean up.","opus":"Realizing a manipulative partner has cast you as a bit player in a self-serving fiction, filling in your life with lies. The bitter turn is admitting how naive you were to believe the narrative, and how words can wound like weapons.","sonnet":"About realizing you've been cast as a character in someone else's self-serving narrative — their lies filled the gaps in your world, and the devastating clarity that you were never really part of their story at all.","src":"haiku"},
"annisokay~human":{"haiku":"Species self-destruction through greed and environmental ruin disguised as progress. Humanity pollutes soul and earth while racing toward inevitable collapse.","opus":"A furious eco-parable framing humanity as gods of appetite feasting toward its own extinction, digging its grave in the name of progress. The tombstones-in-the-sun image is the whole thesis: we know and still won't stop.","sonnet":"Humanity as a self-consuming species: crowding the earth with gas and treason, polluting souls with lies, feasting under the banner of salvation on \"the blood of limping nations\" — the tombstones-in-the-sun image crystallises a civilization that mistakes appetite for progress and is digging its own grave.","src":"haiku"},
"annisokay~humanophobia":{"haiku":"Fear is manufactured into profit by creating enemy classes; whole populations are scapegoated for the failures of few, breeding hatred that destroys indiscriminately.","opus":"The German metalcore band argues that fear is a manufactured product, since profiting from dread requires a steady supply of enemies. The real point is a warning against collective punishment: condemning whole peoples for the cruelty of a violent few, when the vast majority harm no one.","sonnet":"A clear-eyed argument that fear is manufactured for profit — enemies are invented, whole cities razed, entire races blamed for the acts of a few. The lyric insists that most humans never harm anyone, and that the machinery of collective punishment destroys the innocent to justify the industry of threat.","src":"haiku"},
"annisokay~locked-out-locked-in":{"haiku":"Love turns into a locked cell where you're both prisoner and guard, unable to leave.","opus":"Metalcore working the paradox of a love that has become its own cage, where devotion curdles into imprisonment and the same bond that held becomes a noose. Even a clean restart wouldn't cut the ache loose.","sonnet":"A relationship become a prison the speaker is simultaneously inside and outside of — passion has turned into compulsion, and even knowing the delusions for what they are, the heartstrings still drag them down; the coffin's final nail is something the speaker holds in their own hand.","src":"haiku"},
"annisokay~nihilist-blues":{"haiku":"The narrator feels trapped in existential despair and labyrinthine confusion, experiencing psychological torment while unable to escape internal suffering. Paradise exists only as a tormented vision.","opus":"The German metalcore band render depression as an unwelcome presence that won't be locked out. The narrator is trapped by the horror that his own mind holds a happiness he can't reach, describing himself as good as dead and warning others away from the same disorienting despair he insists is universal.","sonnet":"The speaker is trapped between a paradise they can feel inside but can't escape into and an external labyrinth of isolation and nihilism at the door — they're burning up, exhumed, and terrified that their inner richness will stay forever sealed.","src":"haiku"},
"annisokay~one-second":{"haiku":"A single moment can tip between life and death; that decision hinges on one person's presence.","opus":"An unflinching walk through suicidal ideation staged as a countdown, the pull of the water against the single held-back moment that decides everything. What keeps them here is a person who keeps showing up in the dark.","sonnet":"A speaker on the edge of suicide is held back by one person whose light keeps breaking through — the song counts down and up around the heaviest decision imaginable, concluding that a single second and a single presence can be the difference between staying and going.","src":"haiku"},
"annisokay~stfu":{"opus":"A blunt broadside against conspiracy thinking and viral misinformation, ridiculing flat-earth belief and fabricated news swallowed as truth. The band tells listeners to quit parroting other people's nonsense, to stop fueling fear and bigotry, and to actually think for themselves.","sonnet":"A tirade against misinformation, conspiracy theories, and uncritical belief in social media gospel — the narrator is done with people who spread fear and fiction while crowning themselves kings of the credulous, and tells them bluntly to stop and think for themselves.","src":"sonnet"},
"annisokay~the-cocaines-got-your-tongue":{"haiku":"Cocaine ruins social bonds through excessive verbosity and false intimacy; the high extracts future happiness as collateral for present euphoria.","opus":"A German metalcore chronicle of a party spiraling into addiction, the night's easy fun revealed as borrowing joy against a ruined future. Frank the dealer becomes the grim chorus, and the empty talk is the sound of the toll being paid.","sonnet":"A party night spirals through the logic of addiction — a person who declined once finds themselves racking up lines, talking meaninglessly until dawn, borrowing happiness against a future they'll have to repay — and the drug has swallowed their voice along with their judgment.","src":"haiku"},
"annisokay~time":{"haiku":"Someone must stop being a ghost-haunted machine and reclaim selfhood, learning that time is the only thing they ever truly needed as ally.","opus":"Annisokay build oceanic imagery around a person who has lost sight of who they are, a discovered shipwreck standing in for a buried, deadened self. The German metalcore band urge them to drop the automaton act and the excuses and become genuine, treating the passage of time as both an inescapable force and the one thing that can reckon with the truth.","sonnet":"An ocean-floor wreck becomes an emblem of the self that has drifted, ghosted, and forgotten its own shape. The call is to stop being a machine, a puppet, an SOS signal trapped in someone else's script — and to reckon with time not as an enemy but as the only companion that remembers everything.","src":"haiku"},
"annisokay~ultraviolet":{"haiku":"Invisible connection transcends ordinary perception as two souls defy normative constraint through spectrum beyond sight.","opus":"Devotion to someone the world overlooks, the narrator seeing a light in them invisible to everyone else and refusing the flattening of a black-and-white world. It is about being each other's proof of worth against pressure to conform.","sonnet":"Two people invisible to the conformist world see each other in a spectrum no one else perceives — ultraviolet — their vibrant shared life defying the black-and-white scheme of those who try to break them, scars and all.","src":"haiku"},
"another-animal~amends":{"haiku":"Sinking despair and acceleration into meaninglessness—life moves faster while truth hollows out and escape becomes impossible.","opus":"Boarding a sinking ship and choking down pills to escape empty tomorrows, the narrator finds no love beyond the fall and a truth that lies to make amends. Another Animal, the side project built from Godsmack members, trade metal aggression for brooding hard rock, framing futility as an endless cycle of standing up only to fall again.","sonnet":"A person watches themselves and others board sinking ships, swallow hollow remedies, and pray to gods too hollow to answer. The recurring fall and rise becomes a question about whether truth itself can be trusted when the structures meant to redeem only perform a ritual of amends with nothing behind them.","src":"haiku"},
"another-animal~before-the-fall":{"haiku":"Life moves toward inevitable collapse despite hope and faith offered as guidance. The song describes a cascading failure where every action leads toward catastrophe.","opus":"Suspended at the brink, the narrator recounts being knocked down when he stood, sold off when purchased, and pulled under when he swam, hope shrunk to an empty word. Another Animal, spun off from Godsmack, favor grinding introspection, casting existence as a rigged chain of betrayals faced one uncertain plunge at a time.","sonnet":"Standing at an edge shaped by lifelong betrayal — bought and sold, drowned the moment swimming began — the speaker strips hope down to language, a hopeful phrase rather than a felt thing. The fall is not feared so much as accepted as the destination built into every promised leap of faith.","src":"haiku"},
"another-animal~broken-again":{"haiku":"Repeated emotional devastation and loss of self—faked smiles and hidden truths accumulate until the speaker is broken again and again.","opus":"Dreams lie bloodied face-down as the narrator fights his war alone, faking every smile and fading day by day, yet defiantly insisting he cannot be broken. Another Animal, the Godsmack-adjacent outfit, build the track on that contradiction between confessed brokenness and a hardening refusal to yield, prayer and crumbling faith circling the same wound.","sonnet":"Exhaustion and emotional erosion accumulate until concealment is the only daily skill remaining — smiling through emptiness, hiding damage, faking stability. Beneath the collapse is a refusal to be fully unmade; the insistence that no external force can break what is already splintering from within.","src":"haiku"},
"another-day-s-armor~underneath":{"haiku":"An isolated, wounded person harbors scars and buried pain while questioning faith and struggling with suicidal ideation beneath survival.","opus":"This metalcore outfit stage a raw spiral of isolation and self-harm, the narrator writing songs alone because no one cares, scars underneath the knife. Rage turns outward too, at a God who wants only wealth and leaves the desperate to hang, the breakdown a howl of depression and worthlessness.","sonnet":"A life of isolation, self-doubt, and accumulated damage is laid out without resolution. Writing songs alone and fearing the future, the narrator carries wounds he cannot close — scars beneath the surface, a self described as broken and lost. Beneath the rage at false comfort sits something close to a cry for rescue.","src":"haiku"},
"another-now~doubt":{"haiku":"Collective resolve overcomes self-doubt as unified voices amplify purpose and defy limitation.","opus":"Bravado and self-doubt braided together: a taunting, crew-backed challenge that keeps slipping into private admission of the fear of blowing it. The real message is a push to trust yourself before the chance runs out.","sonnet":"A crew asserts total confidence while the leader privately cycles through doubt, past failures, and the fear that they always blow it — asking the world to pause and absorb the trials before it's too late to last.","src":"haiku"},
"another-now~myriad":{"opus":"A song of grief over a family or bond that has come apart, comparing the break to fine china smashed when its support gives way. Uprooted and never settled, the singer speaks directly to a sibling, urging him to endure the hardship together and to carry the permanent damage rather than deny it.","sonnet":"A song about displacement and homesickness — possibly a family or community forced to move repeatedly — where the broken table and scattered porcelain represent a severed bond, and brothers urge each other to survive the rain and not listen to what others say.","src":"sonnet"},
"another-now~parallax":{"opus":"A being made of code glitches through a world moving away from it, trapped in its shell and choking on its own design. Another Now use digital dysfunction as a metaphor for alienation and dissociation, the panic of feeling disconnected from a reality that keeps falling out of reach. Withdrawal and deletion beckon as the only escape.","sonnet":"About feeling fundamentally broken or incompatible with the world — using computing metaphors throughout, the narrator describes themselves as a glitching system that cannot synchronize with reality, overwhelmed by complexity and desperate to reboot or delete.","src":"sonnet"},
"another-now~program":{"haiku":"Critiques systems of control and artificial choice, exposing environmental collapse and complicity through dogma. Victims trapped in manufactured fate.","opus":"An eco-collapse lament that watches Mother Earth on her deathbed while children cough blood and lungs burn, indicting the soothing dogmas and false choices that keep everyone imprisoned in a construction of their own making. The rage lands on institutional lies dressed as care, the silk-robed figure whose clean hands hide the rot, a modern metalcore sermon against manufactured consent and ecological ruin.","sonnet":"Civilisation as self-inflicted wound — children coughing in open air, institutions hiding catastrophe behind vows of care and cryptic dogma. The speaker is overexposed and paralysed, staring at a wall of names, aware that the choice offered is an illusion. The song ends with an injunction to reshape perception, but the chains are ones humanity built for itself.","src":"haiku"},
"ant-clemons-2-chainz-ty-dolla-ign~appreciation":{"haiku":"Flirtation and material excess as demonstration of value and desire. Money flows toward attraction and admiration becomes transactional appreciation.","opus":"A strip-club flex reframed as gratitude, where lavish spending stands in for devotion and the woman's presence earns the whole show. Beneath the bravado runs the trap-to-popstar arc these artists sell as proof they made it out real.","sonnet":"Three artists spend money on a woman they find magnetic — standing ovations, Prada, Rolexes, VVS diamonds — framing financial generosity as the highest form of appreciation, with each verse escalating the gesture from club performance to domestic comfort to full luxury provision.","src":"haiku"},
"anthony-vincent~as-it-was":{"haiku":"Isolation deepens as connection frays and meaning dissolves; the world reshapes itself without permanence or return, leaving only the ache of recognition that nothing stays as it was.","opus":"A cover of Harry Styles that reads his lyrics of isolation and family estrangement plainly — the world shrunk to a private two, with nothing left standing where it once stood. The father's phone call and the sense of everyone getting in the way carry the ache of change and loneliness beneath the bright melody.","sonnet":"A song about fractured intimacy and disconnection — phone calls about loneliness, a father living alone, a world moving too fast — where the speaker insists things are no longer the same as they were and that only the two of them remain in a world stripped of context. The line \"Harry, you're no good alone\" crystallises the worry underpinning the whole piece.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~1000-points-of-hate":{"haiku":"The singer confronts the mutual hatred and destructive cycle between two people, acknowledging that both he and his counterpart hurt themselves and each other through their actions and words.","opus":"A vicious anatomy of loathing that keeps turning inward, admitting the hater knows best how to wound himself even as jealousy and contempt boil toward explosion. Anthrax, the New York thrash pioneers who helped define the genre in the 1980s, sharpen the venom into self-implication here, the repeated confession that it's me making the real damage as personal as it is aimed outward.","sonnet":"Mutual loathing turned inward — the speaker's clarity comes not from resolution but from pure, lucid hatred, and they're honest enough to admit they know how to hurt themselves as well as the object of their rage. Jealousy and unkindness cycle back and forth until the song collapses the distinction between victim and perpetrator: the hatred is both directed outward and self-generated.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~604":{"haiku":"Crude eating and body-shaming directed at a woman, with no other interpretable content beyond insult.","opus":"A crude, repetitive novelty gag chanting that a woman named Katrina is an unstoppable eater and enormously overweight. There is nothing beyond the fat-shaming joke, a throwaway insult built on a single mocking chant.","sonnet":"No discernible theme beyond a two-line repeating taunt about someone eating and being fat — the lyric sheet is essentially just that loop with a name inserted. NONE","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~a-i-r":{"haiku":"A young person rebels against parental control and societal conformity that would trap them, insisting on independence and self-determination. They refuse to have their identity and choices dictated by authority.","opus":"A thrash-era demand for individual freedom against conformity and authority, urging the listener to own their decisions or be imprisoned in someone else's plan. It is teenage defiance sharpened into a creed.","sonnet":"A song about refusing to live the life others have mapped out for you — parents, authority, and conformity are framed as prisons, and the narrator insists that independence, self-determination, and the refusal to subside are the only ways to survive without going insane.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~all-of-them-thieves":{"haiku":"Universal betrayal where everyone steals and lies, causing shame and devastation; the narrator's rage at systemic larceny and deception.","opus":"A furious reckoning with betrayers and swindlers, the narrator swearing to demolish everything in retaliation against those who lied, robbed, and left him grasping at nothing. Every image drips contempt for theft dressed up as respectability. Anthrax, the New York thrash veterans, aim their trademark bite at deceit, turning private grievance into a scorched-earth battle cry.","sonnet":"Pure scorched-earth contempt for a betrayer — someone who presented honest hands while working behind backs, who left the speaker scarred and hollowed out through an intricate architecture of lies. The rage never softens into grief; it stays hard, accusatory, and certain: every one of them was a thief, and the speaker will tear the world apart over it.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~alpha-male":{"haiku":"The speaker operates within a rigid social hierarchy, claiming dominance while mocking others' attempts to climb and asserting their status atop the social order.","opus":"Anthrax mock dominance games and social decay through a strutting narrator who spares then belittles a rival, fixated on rank and craving a jolt of someone else's vitality. Skate and surf imagery of dives and carving runs through it, the band's groove-heavy later era wedding sarcasm to menace while embodying the very macho posturing it ridicules.","sonnet":"A territorial, dominance-obsessed male both ridicules the pecking order and enacts it simultaneously. He oscillates between contempt for social hierarchy and a naked need to occupy its apex, revealing the contradiction at the centre of alpha-male posturing.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~american-pompeii":{"haiku":"A person clings to memories of a place or time that no longer exists, now decayed and unrecognizable, unable to return to what was once cherished.","opus":"The veteran New York thrashers soften into aching nostalgia for a place and person now unrecognizable, the singer propping himself up with comforting untruths while grieving what is gone. The image of a city entombed in ash frames the loss as a disaster preserved in stillness, beauty buried under division, the narrator wrecking himself just to keep going.","sonnet":"Grief for a place and person buried under accumulated damage — the speaker keeps returning mentally to what something used to be, then opens their eyes to find it gone. Like a city swallowed whole, what was beautiful is now layered over with schism and rot. They toggle between comforting lies and the admission that they're slowly giving up the memory altogether.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~among-the-living":{"haiku":"A charismatic plague-spreader corrupts and controls the masses with power and fear, only defeated when united people choose faith and perseverance.","opus":"Anthrax's tribute to The Stand, told from the viewpoint of Randall Flagg spreading plague and fear, and the human will that finally stands against him. Good and evil fought out in Stephen King's ruined America.","sonnet":"Based on Stephen King's The Stand, this is told from the perspective of Randall Flagg (the Walking Dude) — a supernatural villain who corrupts, murders, and divides humanity, until human faith and unity finally turn the tide against him.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~anthrax":{"haiku":"Warfare spreads destruction and death across landscapes, filling soldiers and civilians with terror while an evil force feeds on the violence and suffering inflicted.","opus":"This early track shares the band's name and renders combat as an overwhelming assault on the senses. Amid exploding ordnance and charging troops, the lyric piles on battlefield carnage and a malevolent force gorging on the slaughter, dread driving the narrator to duck and flee before the next blast hits.","sonnet":"War is not distant — it spreads through streets and flesh, fills the air with fear, and cuts into the living with a force that cannot be outrun. The narrator feels this destructive power pressing close, bringing him to his knees, demanding flight with no time left for hesitation.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~antisocial":{"haiku":"Society values wealth and power over integrity while the blind lead the blind. The narrator rejects this corrupted system and its materialist values.","opus":"Anthrax's cover of Trust's anthem, a class-conscious sneer at the greedy establishment figure who hoards money and calls the dissenter antisocial. The insult gets flipped into a badge, the rich getting richer while the blind lead the blind.","sonnet":"An attack on a money-obsessed, class-stratified social order — the speaker calls out someone whose only desire is cash and whose social Darwinism keeps the poor poor, arguing that the whole system is run by people leading each other blindly.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~bare":{"haiku":"Partnership dissolves leaving emptiness; the survivor questions whether grief or indifference better honors the loss.","opus":"Beneath the thrash, a starkly personal reckoning with loss and a pact between two people, one destined to outlive the other. The morbid promise to honor an agreement carries real tenderness under the bravado of just getting up each morning.","sonnet":"A grief song about losing a long-term partner — the surviving half of a pair tries to process what it means to go on alone, honouring a pact they made while struggling to say goodbye to someone who was half of everything.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~be-all-end-all":{"haiku":"The speaker advocates self-determination and independence against institutional control, refusing to be dictated to and celebrating personal agency as liberation.","opus":"A self-empowerment pep talk in thrash form: reject the authorities who try to own and control you, take responsibility for your own life, and treat existence as something to seize rather than complain about.","sonnet":"A defiant individualism: don't let laws or systems dictate your life, break their control over your mind, choose your own path and live fully — the \"be all, end all\" is self-determination and the euphoria of living on your own terms.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~belly-of-the-beast":{"haiku":"A totalitarian system turns children into spies and separates human from beast through propaganda, creating a city where souls die for false peace.","opus":"Written on the Holocaust and genocide, it refuses the excuse of following orders, indicting the obedient functionary as a marionette of evil. The never-again refrain is both memorial and warning about how ordinary people are turned into instruments of death.","sonnet":"An indictment of totalitarian systems — specifically evoking the Holocaust — where ordinary people followed orders, children were turned into informants, and faith itself was weaponized to destroy the innocent.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~big-fat":{"haiku":"The speaker carries the weight of the world while slowly weakening, struggling against obsession and vanity. They bite their tongue but lose their mind, carrying enormous burden while waning and draining.","opus":"Anthrax on being worn down by a demanding, draining figure while slogging through one's own filth and shouldering an impossible burden. Losing one's grip on sanity becomes a strange mercy after witnessing too much, the track's chorus turning that global load into a physical thing held in the hands.","sonnet":"A song about someone who has drained and diminished the narrator repeatedly — the accumulating weight of that person's vanity and manipulation becomes a massive, crushing burden that the narrator carries while simultaneously losing their grip on sanity.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~black-dahlia":{"haiku":"A brutal enforcer hunts and neutralizes powerful men, the haunting image of Black Dahlia obsessing his mind as he walks alone through this violent hunt.","opus":"A hard-boiled interrogation fantasy dressed in noir menace, the singer casting himself as a merciless enforcer breaking a man down. The unsolved LA murder becomes a private obsession haunting a lone hunter who confuses violence with confession.","sonnet":"A hard-boiled interrogation fantasy — the narrator is a cold, methodical enforcer breaking down a target with the focus of a hunter setting a trap, the Black Dahlia reference evoking obsession and the thrill of the pursuit.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~blood":{"haiku":"Familial violence and inherited hatred transform siblings into enemies, with bloodshed becoming easier than love and fraternity drowning in waves of antagonism.","opus":"The dissolution of a bond rendered as slaughter, brotherhood curdling into hatred that spreads like a flood. The band's thesis is unsparing: hate comes easier than love, and misery seduces when your only happiness costs someone else.","sonnet":"The end of a relationship is examined through the metaphor of blood — guilt, complicity, and hatred circulating between two people like a shared toxin, with the speaker wrestling with whether love or hate is easier to sustain when a bond dies violently.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~born-again-idiot":{"haiku":"The speaker holds others to a harsh standard, returning repeatedly despite carrying sin, operating by rules they didn't create.","opus":"A defiant anthem in the voice of the unrepentant screw-up who plays by his own idiot rules and refuses to be judged by his sins. Under the bravado runs a real wish to find something lasting and be reborn harder rather than broken.","sonnet":"A caustic rant about the stupidity of the world and the narrator's contempt for it — he's dragged back into life against his will, surrounded by idiots whose rules he didn't make and won't follow, surviving on contempt alone but quietly hoping to find something real worth staying for.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~bring-the-noise":{"haiku":"Rap and Black resistance as political act against silencing and censorship. Public Enemy channels forbidden speech into powerful music that refuses erasure.","opus":"Anthrax covering Public Enemy's rap-metal landmark, a defiant Black self-assertion that fuses hip-hop and thrash into one wall of noise. It preaches media distrust and cultural pride while literally proving the two genres belong together.","sonnet":"A Public Enemy polemic rapped over Anthrax: the criminal justice system incarcerates Black voices for speaking truth about Farrakhan and systemic corruption; radio stations that call themselves Black are challenged to actually play this; the beat is declared the father of rock and roll, and rap is not afraid of anyone.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~burst":{"haiku":"Refusal to conform to political correctness or others' expectations; the speaker defends contradictory opinions and unapologetic individuality.","opus":"The narrator defends the sovereignty of opinion, refusing political correctness and daring others to judge only themselves, insisting beauty and taste are contradictions no one can arbitrate. Anthrax, the New York thrash pioneers, deliver combative individualism here, the litany of clashing values arguing that right and wrong dissolve into personal perspective, everyone left to walk in their own shoes.","sonnet":"A rejection of external moral arbitration — opinions are contradictions, political correctness is theater, and the only honest judge anyone has is themselves. The speaker embraces ugliness and provocation not out of malice but as proof that beauty, sin, and sanctity are all someone else's projection.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~c11-h17-o2-n2-s-na-sodium-pentathol":{"haiku":"Systemic hypocrisy masks itself as faith: control wrapped in theology, authority propped up by lies that the powerful never expect to answer for.","opus":"Anthrax fantasize about dosing a manipulative deceiver with truth serum, someone who runs everything yet will never admit a thing. The venom aims at abused power and a religion the singer no longer trusts, savoring the target's pain and daring them to finally confess.","sonnet":"A song about contempt for a manipulative hypocrite in a position of religious or institutional authority — the speaker derives visible pleasure from this person's pain, wishes truth serum could force a confession, and refuses to drink from a cup of faith poisoned by control and lies.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~cadillac-rock-box":{"haiku":"Celebration of pure living momentum where the narrator burns bright and alive without restraint or consequence.","opus":"A pure adrenaline ode to being alive and losing yourself in a song, framed as two powerful forces colliding and the joy of cranking music in the car. There's a swaggering embrace of mortality without fear, wanting to be buried in that Cadillac. Anthrax, one of thrash metal's Big Four out of New York, delivers this on the Joey Belladonna reunion era, the groove riding hard under the shout-along hooks.","sonnet":"A rush of pure kinetic release — driving hard, broke and unbothered, pulled toward something magnetic with an almost physical force. Two powerful energies colliding at the speed of sound; the Cadillac becomes a symbol of being so alive you've already made peace with the grave.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~catharsis":{"haiku":"A person breaks through emotional repression and achieves liberation through honesty, transforming angels and devils within.","opus":"A confession and unburdening from a man who spent years playing the tough guy, living a lie for others because he feared his own truth. His release comes from pushing out of the dark into light, making peace with the good and bad warring inside him, and finding a truth and a connection, most likely love, that leaves him feeling deathless.","sonnet":"A personal breakthrough after years of suppressing identity behind a hard exterior — the speaker finally admits the truth about themselves, experiences the collision of angels and devils inside, and emerges into a kind of transcendence grounded in honesty.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~caught-in-a-mosh":{"haiku":"Communication breaks down completely between two people, leaving mutual frustration and incomprehension despite endless attempts to connect.","opus":"Anthrax stage a shouting match with someone too self-absorbed to listen, the singer's fury building into clenched fists and stomping rage. The title turns the standoff into pit chaos, and trying to reason with the fool feels as pointless as one palm trying to make a clap.","sonnet":"A depiction of furious, circular confrontation with someone who refuses to listen or show basic respect — each quoted exchange escalates the frustration until it feels like being pinned in an inescapable collision, the mosh pit serving as a metaphor for futile, exhausting conflict.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~crawl":{"haiku":"Obsessive co-dependency in pain—if one crawls or falls, the other follows unconditionally, addicted to mutual destruction.","opus":"Addiction to pain and a fight that cannot be won drive the narrator toward madness, yet he vows to follow if the other crawls or falls, imagining a cleansing rain. Anthrax, the veteran thrash band, soften into melody here, John Bush's delivery framing devotion amid mutual damage, promising not to walk away even as the pain drives everyone insane.","sonnet":"Unconditional loyalty in the face of addiction and mutual pain — a promise to follow even at the lowest, to stay present through the rain that washes nothing clean. The relationship is knotted in shared damage, and the vow to not walk away is the only solid thing either person has left.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~crush":{"haiku":"A person asks if they must erase themselves to be loved, trapped in a dynamic where the other person gets away with causing harm. The speaker craves being crushed by the one he loves as proof of connection.","opus":"An obsessive, near-masochistic love song about erasing yourself to earn someone's affection, where devotion blurs into self-annihilation. The unease comes from equating being wanted with being destroyed.","sonnet":"A relationship where one person gives everything to be loved, and the other gets away with essentially destroying them — the narrator asks whether he has to erase himself to earn love, while also admitting an overwhelming, almost physical need for the other person that hate alone can't replace.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~cupajoe":{"haiku":"Simple obsession with coffee as a basic life necessity, repetitively demanded as the essential anchor of the day.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~death-from-above":{"haiku":"Aerial assassination where a jet pilot delivers vengeful death to the helpless, justified as reckoning for past wrongs.","opus":"A fighter pilot's cold thrill, tearing across the heavens like a bird of prey, killing for sport and dealing out merciless payback with no guilt, chanting that violence eventually claims its own. War rendered as ruthless aerial hunting. Anthrax, the New York thrash pioneers, drive it with the galloping, chant-along aggression that defined them, menace wrapped in a hook.","sonnet":"A fighter jet as pure vengeance delivered from the sky — no warning, no shadow, just a screaming descent and a kill. The song pivots from individual aggression to collective defiance: those who have been oppressed taking retribution from above, dying by the sword as the code demands.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~deathrider":{"haiku":"A dark, violent imagery of death and conquest—a supernatural or demonic force riding through to slaughter and terrorize, leaving destruction in its wake.","opus":"A galloping fantasy of Death personified charging on a winged mount, sowing panic, feeding on the souls of the doomed, and cutting down everything in its way as listeners are warned to take cover.","sonnet":"A mythic, fearless warrior rides a winged steed through the night spreading terror, devouring souls, and slaughtering everything in his path as an unstoppable force of destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~discharge":{"haiku":"Social hierarchies breed contempt for those emptied by ambition; the narrator refuses to mourn the hollow shells they've become.","opus":"A furious address to a self-destructing addict the narrator refuses to pity, disgusted at the pedestal others build for the doomed. Under the contempt is exhaustion at watching a life become a lie no one will mourn.","sonnet":"A tirade of contempt directed at a person who lived a lie and whose death generates no grief from anyone — the speaker refuses sympathy, accuses the target of self-inflicted moral suicide, and demands they be purged.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~earth-on-hell":{"haiku":"Societal collapse where humanity's greed and corruption have created a literal hell on Earth, with apocalyptic destruction and death spreading.","opus":"An end-of-days riot scene where the youth revolt against a repressive elder establishment, applauding their own destruction as a countdown ticks toward zero. The narrator throws in with the mayhem, casting comeuppance as unavoidable. Anthrax, the New York thrash stalwarts, anchored their late-career revival on precisely this newsreel dread, snarling the refrain that the planet has finally slid into damnation with mosh-ready force.","sonnet":"Civilization as a self-detonating bomb — the streets full of people cheering their own destruction, a media machine that profits from catastrophe, systems that poisoned everything from birth. The speaker isn't mourning but accelerating: rage has become love, the countdown is already running, and the only honest response is to get out of line.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~fight-em-til-you-can-t":{"haiku":"Zombie apocalypse where only violent resistance matters; the dead rise and must be slaughtered, depleting humanity's faith and compassion.","opus":"A gleefully theatrical zombie-apocalypse thrasher. It opens with a news bulletin about corpses reviving, then argues that staying alive means abandoning mercy and letting fury take over when prayer proves useless. Anthrax lean fully into horror-movie camp, and the kill-or-die chant converts terror into pure adrenaline.","sonnet":"A zombie-apocalypse survival hymn that opens with an emergency broadcast and never relaxes — the dead are rising, prayer is useless, and the only theology left is force. Survival becomes indistinguishable from murder, and the song insists on facing that honestly: when what you're killing used to be human, the will to live looks exactly like a killing instinct.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~fueled":{"haiku":"The song explores internal conflict between good and evil halves, fueled by escapism and self-destruction. The speaker is driven by hunger and refuses to settle for less.","opus":"Anthrax use gasoline as shorthand for whatever substance or rush lets the narrator numb out, distract himself and outrun his own depression. He owns up to a compulsion he can't explain and a split between his better and darker selves, refusing the heroic image others project and warning that drawing near him is unwise.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone using alcohol as both engine and escape — aware of the Jekyll-and-Hyde transformation it causes and the self-sabotage it enables — but unable or unwilling to stop, while simultaneously rejecting the hero label others try to put on him.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~got-the-time":{"haiku":"A person overwhelmed by constant obligations and time pressure feels clock ticking urgently through daily chaos.","opus":"Anthrax race through a portrait of frazzled modern urgency, a narrator hounded by phones, letters and errands with time ticking relentlessly in his head and no such thing as tomorrow. Originally a Joe Jackson tune, the band's speed-metal cover amplifies the anxiety into breathless propulsion, turning everyday overload into a manic sprint against a clock that never stops.","sonnet":"The relentless tick of obligation — calls to make, letters to write, errands piling up — becomes a psychological torment that drowns out everything else. Time is both the tyrant and the thing perpetually slipping away, filling the head until nothing else fits.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~gridlock":{"haiku":"Vicious cycles where accusation and judgment create endless conflict, with the narrator trapped between self-knowledge and external condemnation.","opus":"A stubborn declaration of self-determination, the singer rebuffing a crowd of critics who inflate him only to cut him down, and cautioning that malice rebounds on those who dish it out. The New York thrash veterans turn a defense of personal integrity against gossip and false friends into a chest-out, try-and-stop-me anthem.","sonnet":"Refusal to be defined by someone else's script — the speaker has been built up and torn down, talked about behind their back, and is done pretending any of it is fine. The reckoning has been a long time coming, and when it arrives it won't be gentle: what goes around comes around, and the speaker is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~gung-ho":{"haiku":"Soldiers attack with ruthless efficiency, showing no emotion or mercy, treating combat as the ultimate test of survival and loyalty.","opus":"An adrenaline-fueled dive into the mind of a frontline soldier, surging ahead, ripping the enemy apart up close, living only for combat with no feeling or regret. The darker reading is dehumanization through military conditioning, men trained to absorb and inflict agony until nothing registers but the weapon in their grip.","sonnet":"A visceral celebration of combat — the song inhabits the mindset of a soldier who exists only to kill, moving through battlefields with complete emotional detachment, bayonets and bullets and blood presented as the natural order of things for this particular breed of warrior.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~harms-way":{"haiku":"A person self-sabotages despite understanding his arrested development and wreckage. He moves into harm's way deliberately, unable to feel despite touching everything.","opus":"Anthrax deliver a brutally honest self-portrait of a damaged man who's become the worst version of himself, stunted and out of his depth. The throughline is numb detachment and self-destruction, a life spent performing that leaves him able to touch without feeling, knowingly walking toward his own ruin because he sees no other person he could ever be.","sonnet":"Deliberately walking toward destructive situations despite knowing better — the narrator is emotionally numb, arrested in development, can't feel what he touches, and has made a kind of lifestyle of moving into harm's way while smiling through it.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~hog-tied":{"haiku":"The narrator enjoys control and suffering as sources of power; he binds others to ensure they cannot escape. Freedom becomes an illusion for those surrendered to him.","opus":"A predatory power dynamic voiced by a captor who gets off on breaking someone down, dangling a rope from on top of the world that may be rescue or noose. The song circles surrender dressed as peace, a captive told giving up themselves is freedom.","sonnet":"A power dynamic where the speaker is bound to life by something that feels like captivity — they need someone back in their clutches, the noose is either a lifeline or a threat, and the whole thing has the undercurrent of a controlling or obsessive relationship.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~howling-furies":{"haiku":"Damnation where the wicked suffer eternal torment from demonic forces, their evil deeds ensuring hellish punishment forever.","opus":"Anthrax deliver a horror-comic tour of hell, warning arrivals to give up hope as shrieks grow near in a shadowed passage. Vengeful spirits itch to torment the newcomers, and a liar who died by the blade now sits waiting to serve the devil. The New York thrash pioneers give the underworld a rowdy, taunting swagger.","sonnet":"A hellish afterlife for the corrupt — the damned are dragged into a dark tunnel where howling furies wait to play, the lied-and-cheated-through-life crowd sentenced to become Satan's property. It's brief and gloating, the moral universe working exactly as advertised: the knife you lived by finds you in the end.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~hy-pro-glo":{"haiku":"Mutual hypocrisy where both parties blame each other while hiding their own emptiness behind false appearances.","opus":"A caustic dressing-down of a preening hypocrite who manufactures crises for attention and can't stop admiring himself. The narrator confesses he never actually cared and never will, reading the man's neediness as hollow vanity. Anthrax drive the contempt for this glossy fake with a punchy, chant-heavy groove.","sonnet":"Contempt for a self-obsessed hypocrite who feeds on attention while producing nothing real — the speaker refuses to keep throwing themselves against the other's wall, refuses to care about someone who never deserved respect. The obsession is mutual and corrosive; a burning hole in the mind that the speaker finally stops feeding.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~i-am-the-law":{"haiku":"Judge Dredd enforces brutal justice in a dystopian mega-city through absolute authority and unwavering law.","opus":"A homage to the comic-book enforcer of the future megalopolis, tracing his ruthless dispensing of order as arbiter, court, and executioner rolled into one, battling lawbreaking as the gravest offense of all.","sonnet":"A tribute to Judge Dredd, the comic-book lawman of Mega-City One, tracing his uncompromising career — from the academy through wars against Soviet invaders — and his role as judge, jury, and executioner who tolerates no crime.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~i-m-alive":{"haiku":"A demonic entity uses fear and false promises to seduce followers into war and submission. Leading children into battle and trafficking in fear, it claims 'hell lives in everyone' and damns all under its rule.","opus":"A first-person portrait of a demagogue who peddles lies, weaponizes fear, and marches children off to war while sanctifying violence, claiming to embody a devil that resides in everyone. Anthrax, the veteran New York thrash band, channel their politics into this dissection of manufactured worship and the rot of blind obedience.","sonnet":"A demagogue's self-portrait, spoken with relish — the speaker traffics in fear, hypnotises followers, leads the young into war, and invokes heaven as justification for all of it. The horror is in the candour: no pretence of virtue, just the confession that the demon is alive, that hell lives in everyone, and that the speaker has simply made it their identity.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~i-m-eighteen":{"haiku":"Coming-of-age confusion where youthful identity is fractured between boyhood and manhood, uncertain and doubting about direction.","opus":"The confusion of a young man caught between boyhood and manhood, lines forming on his face, no plans and no idea what he wants except to get away, before the ambivalence flips into ragged, exhilarated acceptance that he likes being eighteen after all. It is Alice Cooper's coming-of-age anthem. Anthrax cover it here, the New York thrash band torquing that adolescent restlessness into their own frenetic energy.","sonnet":"The suspended, directionless energy of being eighteen — too old to be a child, too unformed to be a man, confused every day, desperate to escape somewhere that doesn't yet exist. What saves it from despair is the shrug at the end: somehow, despite everything, being eighteen is something the speaker decides they actually like.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~in-a-zone":{"haiku":"Dissociative self-destruction where medication, intoxication, and pressure create a removed mental state allowing harmful behavior without feeling.","opus":"An unflinching first-person look at mental illness and self-harm, the narrator on the edge, skipping his meds, striking himself and slipping into a numbed, dissociated haze where a counted breath is all that steadies him. The veteran New York thrashers render the crisis as churning confession rather than posturing.","sonnet":"Operating at the edge of controlled collapse — pressure building under the skin, a ledge in the mind, medication missed, the whole thing barely held together through breath-counting and sheer nerve. The speaker is wired and volatile and untouchable all at once, and this dissociated, burning state is simply the mode they live in.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~in-my-world":{"haiku":"A traumatized person builds an impenetrable emotional barrier after childhood horrors, choosing isolation and darkness over connection because the world feels like a prison.","opus":"A portrait of someone armored in isolation, cold and untouchable by choice. The gut-punch is the childhood behind it, a boy forced into adulthood young, haunted by the fear of finding his mother dead, hardened into a walking blade.","sonnet":"A portrait of radical self-isolation built from childhood trauma — the narrator grew up in fear, raised emotional walls of stone, and now prefers the cold safety of solitude to any connection, choosing his own bleak internal world over others.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~in-the-end":{"haiku":"Someone close has died or left tragically; the speaker pounds at closed doors too late, gratefully honoring the impact they made before everything ends.","opus":"Anthrax turn loss into a swing between despair and defiance, first beating helplessly at a shut door too late to matter, then vowing to kick it in and take back control. The person mourned is cast as a guiding glow when everything goes dark, a bond that both saved and shattered the narrator.","sonnet":"A tribute to someone who shaped and fundamentally wrecked the band — almost certainly Dimebag Darrell — cycling through gratitude, rage, and grief, vowing to carry their light forward after an irreversible loss.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~indians":{"haiku":"Native Americans endure stolen land and institutionalized racism on reservations; the song calls for solidarity and respect against systemic injustice.","opus":"A thrash-era protest at how America uprooted its indigenous peoples, penned them onto reservations, and left them poor and powerless behind a wall of prejudice and public indifference. The furious chorus turns a plea for basic dignity into a rallying cry against looking away from injustice that isn't your own.","sonnet":"A straightforward advocacy song about the dispossession and ongoing suffering of Native Americans — the speaker calls out the hypocrisy of a nation that stole land, confined survivors to reservations, and then looks the other way, framing it as a collective moral failure that demands grief and action.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~inside-out":{"haiku":"Internal torment and mental imprisonment manifest as physical pain; the speaker feels isolated by pride and has broken internal rules.","opus":"A study of rage turned inward and consuming, the narrator having taken in something that poisons his own reason, the provocation now burning in his gut like an illness he has to expel. The unsettling twist is that the only relief he's found for his mental torment is physical hurt, and breaking a boundary with someone finally shoves him over the edge.","sonnet":"A song about unresolvable inner anguish — the narrator feels destroyed from within, unable to break free, and admits his only relief from mental suffering is physical pain. The relationship referenced seems to be what cracked him open, leaving him always on the inside looking out.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~invisible":{"haiku":"A betrayed person lashes out at a false friend who abandons them in difficult moments and denies responsibility, despite having built their trust. The song expresses the rage of broken promises and the realization that this person was never truly present.","opus":"A furious severing of ties with a manipulative, sadomasochistic false friend who takes everything, gives nothing, and vanishes whenever needed; the deeper read is the struggle to escape their grip and the corrosive lesson learned—'I never should've trusted'—that betrayal by someone claiming closeness leaves lasting sickness and disgust.","sonnet":"A song about a friend who disappears whenever things get hard — calling themselves a close friend but becoming transparent and absent at every critical moment, leaving the narrator simmering with betrayal and asking what happened to him.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~judas-priest":{"haiku":"A destructive entity spreads corruption and death, positioning itself as the ultimate predator harvesting humanity's flaw. It operates with depraved cruelty comparable to betrayal itself.","opus":"Anthrax borrow Oppenheimer's grim line about becoming death to voice an apocalyptic destroyer relishing the extinction of a helpless humanity. A metal-family salute to Judas Priest slips into a sneering, world-ending vision of annihilation delivered from the perspective of the force doing the killing.","sonnet":"A speaker who has become Death itself — destroyer of worlds — names himself an apex predator of spiritual desolation, positioning the kind of depraved evil associated with Judas Priest as the engine of total annihilation of the human condition.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~keep-it-in-the-family":{"haiku":"The speaker challenges bigotry and inherited hatred passed through families, demanding people face the real world beyond separatism and discrimination.","opus":"A takedown of bigotry handed down through generations like a household inheritance, prejudice absorbed from parents until a kid stops questioning it. The NY thrash veterans refuse to let hatred hide as a private matter, insisting it gets exposed the moment it meets the wider world.","sonnet":"A direct attack on racist separatism and inherited bigotry — the song argues that hatred passed down through family is cowardice, that separatists are trapped in juvenile delusion, and that their refusal to engage with the real world will be their downfall.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~killing-box":{"haiku":"A hostile figure traps others in dangerous situations without escape, speaking truthfully to provoke conflict while positioning themselves above moral accountability.","opus":"A menacing threat delivered to a rival, built entirely on intimidation and dominance. The speaker positions himself as a dangerous predator and their opponent as trapped prey, weaponizing brutal candor against deceit and taunting toward mutual ruin. The whole track reads as cold psychological warfare rather than any real dialogue.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone evasive and dishonest — someone who refuses to speak plainly — hardens into a trap. The speaker has cornered this person in a position of their own making, surrounded them with the consequences of their deceptions, and simply left them to reckon with it.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~king-size":{"haiku":"An ambitious figure pursues power and status with minimal effort, driven by self-interest and a conviction that he can dominate, dismissing ethical concerns as external judgment.","opus":"A satirical strut through American ego and appetite, a boaster inflating himself into a cartoon of self-importance while cracking that evil is homegrown out west. Beneath the swagger sits an admission of a life spiraling out of control, Anthrax lacing their heaviness with a wry jab at self-mythology.","sonnet":"Outsider ambition colliding with California's particular brand of spiritual rot — the speaker wants in on the programme but refuses to sell their soul for something that was free to begin with. They're mocked, they laugh it off, and they push toward a king-size position with the intensity of someone who knows the devil lives nearby and isn't particularly afraid of him.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~madhouse":{"haiku":"Institutional confinement and mental deterioration: the speaker is trapped, medicated, and isolated while questioning their own sanity and whether society judges them fairly.","opus":"Narrated from within a psychiatric ward, the song follows a restrained, confined man protesting his sanity while everyone around him insists otherwise, desperate to wake from a waking nightmare. The chorus keeps the ambiguity alive, leaving it unclear whether the institution or his own unraveling mind is the real authority on his madness.","sonnet":"A first-person account from inside a psychiatric institution — the speaker insists their madness is not the crime the institution treats it as, trapped in a nightmare of white walls and dreams, angry at being blamed and shamed for something they can't control.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~metal-thrashing-mad":{"haiku":"A driver operates a vehicle at dangerous speeds with total abandon, describing themselves as crazed and uncontrollable while speeding recklessly.","opus":"Early Anthrax at its most gleefully juvenile, a speed-metal joyride about flooring a car toward certain disaster. There's no subtext, just adrenaline and the thrill of losing control at full throttle.","sonnet":"A straightforward celebration of speed and reckless driving as metaphor for the energy of metal itself — the narrator is flooring it, heading for disaster, with thunder in his head. The song is self-descriptive: it is what it describes.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~milk-ode-to-billy":{"haiku":"Absurdist domestic frustration elevated into mock-heroic complaint about a missing grocery staple.","opus":"A deliberately dumb thrash joke: fury over an empty milk carton ruining breakfast, escalating to beer and Wheat Thins instead. Anthrax mocking their own genre's rage by aiming it at the pettiest grievance.","sonnet":"A comedic lament: waking up ready for cereal, finding no milk in the fridge, refusing to go to the store, settling for wheat thins and beer while cursing the milkman — petty domestic helplessness played as mock-epic tragedy.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~monster-at-the-end":{"haiku":"Karmic retribution where the wicked become their own punishment; no redemption despite claimed innocence, only the monster they've become.","opus":"A reckoning built on the twist that the thing you dread is your own corrupted self. Framed with fairy-tale menace, the NY thrash pioneers turn dread inward, crowning a person with the weight of their own sins in an unforgiving, sun-scorched hell of their own making.","sonnet":"The self as its own worst enemy revealed — you were promised what you wanted, given what you needed instead, and the halo melted along the way. The monster at the end of the story turns out to be wearing your crown: it was you, hiding in plain sight all along, unleashed and undeniable.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~n-f-l":{"haiku":"A talented performer's addiction and excess ultimately destroy them; the song mourns wasted potential and questions what could have been.","opus":"Anthrax spin a cautionary tale about a gifted comedian, its cryptic title spelling nice fuckin life backwards. He rises from class clown to public adoration only to be destroyed by addiction, and the bitter refrain casts his drug habit as his sole companion and the enemy digging his grave, mourning a brilliant life thrown away on a poison no one should touch.","sonnet":"A eulogy for a gifted comedian or performer — presumably John Belushi, given the references — whose talent was squandered by addiction, the title spelling out \"Nice Fucking Life\" backwards, the song a bitter lament for potential consumed by excess.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~new-noise-refused-cover":{"haiku":"People are paralyzed by old cultural frames and economic gatekeeping, dancing to predetermined movements while those with power control who gets heard; liberation requires new expressions and genuine voice.","opus":"Refused's manifesto delivered by Anthrax — a demand for genuinely fresh creation against a culture where wealth purchases entry and everyone marches to the same stale rhythm. The fury is at complicity: we move to tunes we shouldn't and refuse to take the lead.","sonnet":"A Refused cover turned into a thrash call-to-arms against cultural stagnation: beauty is only affordable to those with money, great words hide ugly actions, and the whole culture is dancing to the wrong songs and going nowhere. The demand is for new noise, new art made for real people — not leaders, but those locked out when the day is over.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~nobody-knows-anything":{"haiku":"The singer resents being taken for granted after giving everything and earning no respect, tired of playing a one-sided game where his contributions go unrecognized.","opus":"A bitter reckoning with a lopsided relationship, choking on given air and bleeding for respect never earned, tallying everything offered for nothing returned until the resentment threatens a trail of bodies. The refrain shrugs that nobody understands any of it. Anthrax, the New York thrash institution, channel a very human grievance about being taken for granted into their trademark aggressive momentum.","sonnet":"Exhaustion at the edge of explosion — the speaker has given everything and received nothing, watched generosity be weaponised against them, and now stands at the point where continued goodwill feels physically dangerous. The repeated refrain that nobody knows anything reads less like nihilism than a final protest: the work was real, the sacrifice was real, and it wasn't witnessed.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~nothing":{"haiku":"Emotional manipulation and false victimhood—the song addresses someone who plays the victim while undermining others, leaving 'nothing' of substance.","opus":"The New York thrash mainstays voice worn-out contempt for a self-styled martyr who casts himself as the wronged party, all wounded ego and no self-worth. The narrator refuses to tiptoe around someone else's manipulations, mourning a friendship that never truly existed and letting his ambivalence harden into a final dismissal.","sonnet":"A friendship that turned parasitic — the other person performing victimhood, engineering scenes, and pushing until the connection drains to nothing. The speaker refuses the eggshell walk and names the dynamic plainly: the best friend who never was has left only a dead dream and the clarity of goodbye.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~now-it-s-dark":{"haiku":"A seductive manipulator controls victims in darkness, presenting luxurious deception that traps people in dependency and shame.","opus":"Anthrax voice a controlling predator modeled on David Lynch's Frank Booth, a sharply attired menace who rules his victim inside dreams, forbids them from meeting his gaze, and drinks in thrilled, drugged pleasure. The New York thrashers coil that cinematic villainy into a sinister groove of ownership and threat.","sonnet":"Control as erotic theater — a manipulator who owns the stage, commands performance, and forbids eye contact, thriving in the dark where submission is the only option. The candy-coated surface conceals pure decadence beneath, and the love letter he promises is a sentence, not a gift.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~one-world":{"haiku":"Global nuclear annihilation looms while ideological blindness and indifference prevent collective action, leaving humanity to repeat historical patterns toward mutual destruction.","opus":"A Cold War plea against nuclear annihilation, insisting Russians are people like us and mocking leaders who choose weapons over peace. The push-button imagery lands the moral: killing is easy at a distance, impossible once you see the enemy's kids.","sonnet":"A nuclear-age anti-war polemic demanding that humanity recognize its shared fate before it's too late — the speaker argues that political tunnel vision and nationalist posturing are suicidal when the weapons already exist to end the world.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~only":{"haiku":"A relationship fractured by differing temperaments and broken promises. The speaker expresses frustration that the other person won't try to understand and has taken control away, leaving incompatibility on both ends of the spectrum.","opus":"A portrait of a volatile, self-absorbed relationship where the other person's unpredictable mood swings and refusal to understand leave the speaker feeling controlled and driven to the edge.","sonnet":"A relationship marked by erratic mood swings and mutual incomprehension, where the speaker feels that control was taken from them and the other person never truly tried to understand.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~p-v":{"haiku":"The speaker celebrates their strength and determination, refusing to back down or listen to criticism. They're aggressive, fortified, and committed to proving their power through confrontation and force.","opus":"Anthrax swagger through a drunken, brawling mood, tipping the cap and stepping into a bar's line of fire ready to drink or throw down, all bravado and false courage. Amid trembling hands and blood he can't place, the constant is a defiant, self-wrecking machismo half-aware it's near the brink.","sonnet":"About the belligerent, blood-and-adrenaline energy of someone who has hit bottom and come back fortified — burned bridges, blood on hands, shaking but alive, charging like a bull. The \"piss and vinegar\" in the title names the attitude: corrosive toughness as survival strategy.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~packaged-rebellion":{"haiku":"Empty posturing where people perform rebellion without substance or true commitment, wearing revolution as fashion rather than living it authentically.","opus":"A scornful shot at performative dissent, the singer fed up with people who advertise revolt but never act on it. He declines to justify himself to posers and demands that anyone claiming to rebel actually do so. The New York thrash veterans channel their long-running contempt for phonies into a taunting challenge to prove it.","sonnet":"A takedown of performed transgression — someone who wears revolution like a fashion accessory, seeks approval for their nonconformity, and can't back their posture with action. The song doesn't argue for a particular politics; it argues for authenticity: if you mean it, do it, and if you're living through someone else's example you've already lost the plot.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~panic":{"haiku":"Frenetic energy and physical intensity escalate to loss of control; wheels spin, walls break, axe and soul merge in a fever of uncontainable power and sexual chaos.","opus":"Early thrash pushing raw physical momentum for its own sake, welding the adrenaline of a fight to the adrenaline of sex until both blur into one loss of control. There isn't much beneath it, and it isn't pretending there is.","sonnet":"A song about the frenzy of live performance and physical intensity — playing fast and hard, losing control, the road being tough but the fight worth it — primarily an experience rather than a statement, adrenaline as the throughline.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~panic-live-in-studio":{"haiku":"Sexual intensity and loss of control as ecstatic destruction. The narrator burns and shakes, all boundaries collapse under overwhelming force.","opus":"Early thrash adrenaline built around losing control in the pit, sex and violence blurred into pure kinetic release. There's no message beyond the rush itself, the body given over to speed and noise.","sonnet":"Pure kinetic energy with no narrative beyond the show itself: move to the front, reach for the light, wheels spin, walls break, axes blaze — the whole song is the physical sensation of a hard-rock performance as it happens.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~perpetual-motion":{"haiku":"Defiant survival where weathered resilience proves the narrator's strength; refusal to surrender despite constant blows.","opus":"A veteran's vow of survival, showing his age but never falling, having outlasted every hardship and answered every hit, still moving and ready for a shot he's earned. He asks only to be seen as human, insisting there's no shame in pride. New York thrash institution Anthrax pour decades of endurance into this rolling self-affirmation.","sonnet":"A weathered man's declaration of still-standing dignity — face lined, pride intact, every blow absorbed and countered. He's human enough to own his mistakes, stubborn enough to keep rolling, and self-aware enough to know the pride that drives him isn't a sin but the engine keeping him in motion.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~pieces":{"haiku":"Grief over a deceased loved one whose spirit the narrator senses nearby, refusing to let go and seeking reunion in an afterlife.","opus":"Anthrax mourn a lost brother named Anthony, the singer shouting upward at nothing, doubting his beliefs while grasping for a sign that the departed is at peace and safely returned. He pledges to keep waiting until their spirits reunite. The New York thrashers soften into an aching, real tribute drawn from genuine grief.","sonnet":"Grief for a brother named Anthony who died — the speaker refuses to accept absence as absence, insisting the dead man is still nearby, still fine, still home. Beneath the consoling fiction is honest devastation: faith questioned, reality staking its claim, and the only thing left is to keep the flame lit and wait.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~potter-s-field":{"haiku":"A scapegoat raised to take blame and suffer for others' sins, eventually retaliating against the system that created them.","opus":"A dark reckoning voiced by a scapegoat born to save and to die, blaming the one who taught him hate rather than love, hands stained with blood, hoping his tormentor is proud of what they made before he brings down the rapture. It is a searing indictment of a corrupting parent. Anthrax open this with the old journey-into-sound tag and drive it with the New York band's righteous, groove-heavy thrash fury.","sonnet":"Rage at a mother who weaponized religion and love as control — the speaker was told to forgive, to save, to burn, and learned hatred instead. The bastard son finds his own place in the wreckage, steals what love he can find, and wishes he had been left to rest in a pauper's grave rather than shaped into what her hands made him.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~random-acts-of-senseless-violence":{"haiku":"A vigilante with warped morality enforces justice through violence, dehumanizing victims while believing they're righteous; cruel irony of the killer's logic.","opus":"A chilling study of a vigilante who appoints himself judge, meting out death by his own hand while walking the line between law and lawlessness and taking pleasure in a target's demise. His refusal of remorse marks a mind cut loose from ordinary morality. Anthrax, the New York thrash band, inhabit a cold, self-justifying killer's logic with unsettling conviction.","sonnet":"A vigilante's fractured self-justification — someone who sees themselves as walking a moral line, fixing wrong with their hands, refusing sympathy for those who gave none. The song's logic is deliberately unstable: the speaker insists they're righteous while describing what sounds like retaliatory killing, and the refrain of senseless violence undercuts any clean reading of the stance.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~refuse-to-be-denied":{"haiku":"Ideological resistance defies imposed conformity; the speaker raises defiant fist against institutional coercion.","opus":"A raised-fist stand against nationalism and jingoism dressed as patriotism, refusing to compromise with ideology built on lies. The band separate real freedom from the flag-waving that exploits it, and choose resistance.","sonnet":"A declaration of resistance against ideologies — nationalist, imperialist, or otherwise — that demand conformity; the speaker refuses to compromise or subscribe to twisted rhetoric, standing with fist raised against any system that claims authority over him.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~revolution-screams":{"haiku":"Revolution overthrows empires and punishes the guilty through violent upheaval and social collapse.","opus":"Anthrax stage an uprising, likening a failing order to a sinking ship as they summon upheaval, reckoning and the collapse of empires whatever the cost. The refrain demands to know where your loyalties lie while promising the guilty their punishment. The band frame revolt as both righteous and horrifying, upheaval rendered as terrible beauty.","sonnet":"A call for insurrection against systems of corruption and violence, framed as both political analysis and visceral demand. The ship of empire is already sinking; the only question is which side one chooses when the walls come down and empires fall.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~riding-shotgun":{"haiku":"Refusal to be passive or controlled; demanding agency and voice while rejecting subservience and false authority.","opus":"A restless anthem of thwarted control, feeling shackled and labeled, sliding in reverse, refusing to fall in line with the defeated or settle for a lesser role. The demand is simply room to live on one's own terms, powered by pain. Anthrax, the New York thrash veterans, drive it with clipped bursts of verbs and a defiant chant, turning powerlessness into forward thrust.","sonnet":"A refusal to ride passenger in your own life — stuck going backwards, cuffed and branded by systems and people that want you passive. The speaker fights back not through destruction but pure assertion: screaming his own name, charging by pain, refusing to kneel and walk the road of the already-defeated.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~room-for-one-more":{"haiku":"A meditation on second chances and karmic justice—someone once excluded or wronged now confronts their oppressor, claiming a place at the table through moral patience.","opus":"A refusal to grant cheap absolution to someone who thinks a single apology cancels a lifetime of cruelty, then wants pity on top of it. The chorus is patient vengeance, the speaker content to wait for the cruelty to circle back while keeping his own conscience clean.","sonnet":"A refusal of forgiveness — someone hateful and self-pitying confessed their wrongs expecting redemption, but the speaker insists confession doesn't erase the past; eventually karmic payback comes around and there is room at the table for one more dose of justice.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~safe-home":{"haiku":"A loved one becomes the ultimate refuge and sanctuary, the only place where fear dissolves and authentic rebirth becomes possible.","opus":"Anthrax offer a rare warm anthem of rescue through love, a man who has visited dark corners of his own mind finding rebirth and shelter in a partner who showed up unshakable. Devotion, not religion, becomes his one faith and the place he always comes back to. The New York thrash veterans wrap uplift in the crunch.","sonnet":"A love song framed as arrival after a long, damaging journey — the speaker has been through places in their head worse than wherever they're going, and the person they're addressing is the first thing that's ever felt like solid ground. Faith replaces religion here; what's sacred is the relationship itself, and the whole world moving on is cause for relief, not grief.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~safe-home-acoustic-bonustrack":{"haiku":"Love as refuge and salvation where another becomes the only safe destination. The narrator surrenders to dependence and gratefully binds to the beloved.","opus":"A rare tender turn for the band, a homecoming ballad about a person who is your one refuge after years of walking through darkness in your own head. Love here is the only faith the singer will admit to.","sonnet":"A love song framed as homecoming after a life of inner damage: the speaker has been to dark places in their head but credits one person — who arrived \"strong as stone\" from nowhere — with being the safe home they run and burn out into, the only god they ever found being love itself.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~schism":{"haiku":"Prejudice and hatred divide humanity along invisible lines while love and tolerance struggle to gain purchase; the song asks whether respect and acceptance are even possible.","opus":"An anti-racism sermon in thrash form, arguing prejudice is taught, not born, and a house divided cannot stand. The band strip away skin color to insist on the man underneath, indicting how easily we choose hate over its harder opposite.","sonnet":"A direct challenge to racism and sectarianism, arguing that prejudice is learned rather than innate and that a house divided by hatred cannot stand — the speaker demands empathy and mutual recognition across racial and social lines.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~soldiers-of-metal":{"haiku":"Warrior ethos where metal soldiers fight without mercy or hesitation, described in purely brutal terms celebrating destruction and death.","opus":"A cartoon war-cry that casts heavy metal itself as an army marching into battle, all trenches, cannons and blood-lust played gleefully straight. It is less about violence than about tribal belonging, the fan as foot soldier. This is early Anthrax, the New York thrash outfit before they found their sharper political edge, still trading in the pure comic-book bombast of their genre's dawn.","sonnet":"A battle hymn for metal warriors who exist purely to wage war — they wait in trenches, attack at night, and define themselves entirely through combat. No politics, no cause, just the ecstatic violence of the fight itself; the aggression is the point, and ruling the night is the reward.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~stealing-from-a-thief":{"haiku":"Reclamation of authentic humanity against corruption and expectation; choosing genuine existence over inherited roles and borrowed lives.","opus":"A rejection of inherited and borrowed lives, wanting neither the parents' path nor rich friends' comfort nor an existence lived only online, but a real one lived in real time. The refrain of reclaiming humanity from a thief becomes a fight to know your own name, pain, and frequency. Anthrax, the New York thrash veterans, ground this identity struggle in blunt, self-searching lines about believing in something real.","sonnet":"A declaration of self-knowledge against a world built on performance and borrowed identity. The speaker rejects inherited lifestyles, online personas, and the question of personal gain entirely — what he wants is to simply exist, grounded in his own name, pain, and frequency, reclaiming his humanity from a system that tried to take it.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~strap-it-on":{"haiku":"The narrator treasures the past—specific eras of music and thrills—and remains unchanged by staying devoted to old-school identity and nostalgia.","opus":"A middle-aged metalhead's nostalgia anthem, aching for the sounds that formed him across two eras of youth. It's about clinging to your roots as identity, insisting that staying loyal to old passions is its own kind of growth.","sonnet":"A nostalgia trip for specific eras of music — 75-79 and 85-89 — framing the old school as something to physically strap on and carry forward, a way of staying authentic while everything around shifts.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~subjugator":{"haiku":"Violent conquest and domination delivered with unrelenting brutality, the narrator as a force of destruction and suffering.","opus":"A brawling power fantasy of dominance and destruction, living for the danger of the night and flattening anyone in the way. Anthrax, one of thrash's Big Four, lean into old-school metal bombast, all pounding cadence and pit-ready bravado with no moral fig leaf.","sonnet":"Pure aggression as anthem — a street-level battle cry with no cause beyond dominance. The violence here is total and joyful, phrased in the escalating language of infernos and submission. It's less about any specific enemy than about the intoxication of collective force pushing through the night.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~suzerain":{"haiku":"Endless cycle of pain and destruction with no resolution; history repeating through fire and suffering, freedom remaining impossible.","opus":"A dark meditation on cycles that refuse to end, presided over by an overlord ruling a blood-soaked land while everything churns and burns. The tormenting refrain insists nothing ever truly concludes, it only resets, though it dangles the hope of finally being freed from memory. Anthrax, the New York thrash veterans, lend the abstract dread an apocalyptic weight.","sonnet":"A cyclical vision of power and doom — a ruler figure, teeth frozen in a death's-head grin, commands an endless loop where nothing resolves, everything only restarts. The speaker watches history repeat as love, pain, and sin fold into one another, and wonders desperately what comes after, already knowing there is no after.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~taking-the-music-back":{"haiku":"The narrator rejects lies and exploitation that defined their past; they reclaim their creative agency and power by refusing victimhood.","opus":"A defiant reclaiming of authenticity from a manipulative liar or industry figure whose lies scarred and bled the narrator dry; refusing to be a slave to the deal, he repeatedly asserts he's taking the music back from those who used it against him.","sonnet":"An assertion of creative ownership after being burned by someone who used and diminished the narrator — reclaiming the music as personal territory, not a commodity to be controlled or exploited by others.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~tester":{"haiku":"Identity rejection where the narrator refuses to be replicated or compared; staying true to original self despite pressure to conform.","opus":"A proud assertion of authenticity from someone who has grown precisely by remaining true to himself, refusing to be boxed in or measured against some supposed original. The recurring challenge dares a rival to try replacing him and promises they always will fall short. Anthrax cast it as an outsider's manifesto, at ease because fame never defined who they are.","sonnet":"A veteran standing firm while others keep trying to replace him with newer models. The speaker hasn't changed and doesn't need to — he was the original, the benchmark everyone else is measured against, and every new version fails the comparison. Grounded by substance rather than fame, he outlasts the revolving door of impostors.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~the-constant":{"haiku":"The speaker feels slipping through time, searching for a constant anchor to stay present, afraid of losing control and desperate to be whole.","opus":"A love song dressed in time-travel logic, a mind coming loose from its moorings and reaching for the single anchor that holds it together across the chaos. The plea to be heard carries a wish to recover the moments lost apart from someone. Anthrax borrow science-fiction framing to render devotion as a lifeline flung across scattered instants.","sonnet":"Unstuck in time, the speaker scrambles for something solid to hold onto as they slip between moments and places. The constant is a person — a tether across the slipstream — and the song swings between the vertigo of displacement and the clarity of knowing exactly what was wasted in the lost time away. Burning the past down is the only way to be fully present.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~the-devil-you-know":{"haiku":"Acceptance of one's own brutal nature; rejecting hypocrisy by embracing darkness rather than pretending to be better than one actually is.","opus":"A swaggering villain's monologue from a brutally honest thug who owns his sins without pretense, boasting that he hides nothing even while admitting no good will come of him. The hook twists a familiar proverb into a dare to pick the evil you can predict. New York thrashers Anthrax relish this unrepentant persona over a roar-along chorus.","sonnet":"A dangerous man offering himself honestly — no pretense of goodness, no apology for the violence in his history, just the argument that knowing what you're dealing with is better than being surprised. The Glasgow kiss and the Molotov are details of a past he acknowledges without guilt, and the chorus is almost practical advice: at least with this devil, there are no illusions.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~the-giant":{"haiku":"Battling an inner giant; self-destruction, contradiction, and endless internal warfare that feeds on itself.","opus":"Anthrax externalize an inner conflict as a towering figure the narrator has to defeat, stuck between competing pulls of morality and going under in the search for his own soul. It plays as a vow to keep battling himself even after everything else is gone.","sonnet":"The speaker battles an internal giant — an overwhelming, out-of-control force within — that traps them in a vicious cycle of self-conflict and moral uncertainty, with the struggle framed as both literal combat and a search for the soul.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~this-is-not-an-exit":{"haiku":"The narrator is trapped in an inescapable cycle of pain and self-harm as his only mode of existence. Immortality through suffering offers no redemption or way out.","opus":"Anthrax adapt the last line of American Psycho into a portrait of a mind locked into itself, unable to reform or flee, its only outlet the infliction of hurt and its affection tangled up with killing. The band render Ellis's void as a trap with no door for anyone pulled inside.","sonnet":"A trapped existence with no escape route — the speaker is locked in patterns of pain-as-self-expression, fascinated by another person in a way that feels both predatory and inevitable, unable to change or save themselves, with no exit from the cycle.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~time":{"haiku":"Paranoia about time's passage and mortality, with life constantly fighting death while the narrator struggles to claim what's theirs.","opus":"A paranoid struggle with mortality, each passing second etching itself into the narrator's face as life and death contend over what remains, though he vows to claim his share through sheer persistence. The wired dread eventually gives way to resolve. Anthrax, one of thrash's Big Four, built this in the Belladonna years, the racing riffs mirroring runaway anxiety before it finally breaks.","sonnet":"A paranoid, amped-up reckoning with mortality and urgency — clocks ticking, faces aging, the walking dead shuffling past while the speaker rages to make his move before time runs out. By the end paranoia loses its grip and something fiercer takes over: a bull in a cage demanding a stage, refusing to die waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~toast-to-the-extras":{"haiku":"A person dismisses secondary relationships as interchangeable distractions used to fill emotional void, valuing them only for their convenience and lack of demands.","opus":"A wry, self-aware turn from the thrash veterans, raising a glass to disposable company. The narrator stays deliberately shallow, unwilling to confront his fears, drinking to background people who fill silence and mean nothing beyond curing a dull evening. Under the toast lies a hollow evasion, a refusal of honesty dressed up as a celebration.","sonnet":"A man numbs himself with drink and keeps company with people who demand nothing from him — interchangeable, uncomplicated, easily discarded. He toasts the background players of his life not out of affection but because their blankness is easier to bear than the terrifying depth of real connection.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~w-c-f-y-a":{"haiku":"System collapse where exposure reveals the hollowness beneath appearances, prisoners break free, and consequences rain down on the corrupt.","opus":"A study in patience curdling into aggression, the mind atrophying under mediocrity until enough is finally enough and the bullets fly. The recurring image of a wrecking ball arriving at gates thrown wide reads as collective retribution, the many coming for those who deserve it. Anthrax pair thrash velocity with this coiled, cerebral menace, the sound of restraint snapping after being pushed too long.","sonnet":"A song about the breaking point of a mind ground down by mediocrity and passive acceptance — patience eroding into enmity, systems seizing, and finally the moment when the gates open and the wrecking ball arrives. The stance is one of collective retribution: we have come for all of you.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~what-doesn-t-die":{"haiku":"Violence and conflict perpetuate endlessly as consciousness flows into bloodshed; what cannot be killed survives through ideology, ambition, and inherited cruelty.","opus":"A bitter takedown of a rotting, self-consuming culture that preaches values it violates while its sacred figures suffer and the whole structure caves in. Anthrax counter that despair with a stubborn refrain of resilience and a determination to become everything they're capable of, even as violence keeps spreading.","sonnet":"A meditation on resilience and the corruption of institutions — drawing on imagery of violence, hypocrisy, and systemic decay, the speaker insists that whatever true potential or spirit they carry cannot be killed, even as everything around them crumbles into blood and chaos.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~who-cares-wins":{"haiku":"Homelessness and urban poverty render their victims invisible while society turns away; indifference becomes the ultimate currency and apathy wins the day.","opus":"A confrontation with homelessness and the willful blindness that lets us walk past it, the invisible poor speaking from inside the box no one sees. The title inverts the usual proverb into a demand: caring, not indifference, is the real victory.","sonnet":"A homeless person speaks from inside their invisibility, confronting the comfortable who look away, and indicting a culture where indifference to suffering is normalized and self-preservation eclipses basic human care.","src":"haiku"},
"anthrax~you-gotta-believe":{"haiku":"A ruthless, inhuman force pursues its victims with implacable vengeance, treating people as mere objects to be destroyed without mercy or conscience.","opus":"Anthrax voice a merciless torturer who works alone, pursuing vengeance and impaling victims he calls mere bags of blood while a captive soul becomes his white whale. The demand to believe rings as coercion. The band renders relentless brutality first-person and unrepentant, its narrator thriving in darkness and confessing an inhuman drive to torture simply because he can.","sonnet":"An unstoppable, merciless force pursues vengeance with absolute conviction, reducing its target to nothing more than a vessel for suffering. The speaker is driven by something beyond personal grievance — a near-religious compulsion that frames the hunt in apocalyptic terms.","src":"haiku"},
"anubis-gate~circumstanced":{"haiku":"Haunted by a phantom lover's memory, someone struggles to break the grip of grief and fantasizes about joining them.","opus":"A man undone by a departed lover cannot stop seeing her return, torn between longing and the battlefields that reappear whenever their silhouettes meet. Anubis Gate, the Danish progressive metal band, unspool intricate, melancholy fatalism here, the narrator resolving at last to rest his case, a recurring fatal thought perhaps carrying him closer to where she is.","sonnet":"A man haunted by a relationship that destroyed him even as it ended, unable to decide whether he wants the ghost back or simply wants the haunting to stop. The agony persists whether the silhouette appears or vanishes, and a dangerous thought recurs — that following the ghost might be the only exit.","src":"haiku"},
"anubis-gate~desiderio-omnibus":{"haiku":"The speaker desires mastery over death and mortality, seeking transcendence through mind-expansion and meditation to reach a state beyond life's suffering and pain.","opus":"Anubis Gate imagine a seeker who dares to meddle with the boundary between living and dying, using a trance-like state to reach the divine and return, pulled by a yearning he believes everyone shares. The Latin title names that universal longing. The Danish prog-metal band frame death-defying ambition as grand, cerebral and dangerous exploration.","sonnet":"A man rehearses a controlled near-death experience — training to extinguish consciousness and return transformed, pushing past the edge of living into some metaphysical territory no one has mapped. The desire is universal: to touch the divine and survive it.","src":"haiku"},
"anubis-gate~facing-dawn":{"haiku":"A person confronts existential uncertainty and life's meaningless chaos while approaching death and rebirth. They seek no answers, only acceptance of belonging in a world of random chance.","opus":"The Danish prog-metal band deliver a bittersweet, self-undoing meditation in which the narrator declines to explain or accuse, claiming his place while gambling on rebirth as night gives way to morning. Naivety and quiet surrender meet the arriving light, the whole thing framed as a wager whose outcome he leaves to chance.","sonnet":"A man on the losing side of a game of chance watches his life implode while insisting he needs no reasons and seeks no answers. Dawn is not arrival but exposure — the moment naiveté runs out and the trembling player, having rolled his last dice, prays to be reborn before the light fully comes.","src":"haiku"},
"anubis-gate~hold-back-tomorrow":{"haiku":"A person must let go of recklessness and past secrets to move forward and live fully. The song urges confronting fear and claiming what belongs to you.","opus":"A prog-metal push to a man on the edge to shed his inhibitions and reckless past, to fly into the sun and unveil his deepest secrets before life turns bitter. Reinvention framed as urgent but achievable, a second chance seized on his own terms.","sonnet":"An encouragement to someone standing at the edge — there's still life ahead, puzzles worth solving, and secrets worth revealing; the call is to fly into the sun rather than remain paralyzed by past recklessness and impending collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"anubis-gate~oh-my-precious-life":{"haiku":"A lament of a life wasted and stolen by another, where one's dreams are co-opted and personal autonomy is surrendered to someone else's designs.","opus":"A rueful confession of a life carefully rationed yet squandered, the singer falling apart at the threshold, wounded to the quick, admitting he only ever craved a life of his own free of the person he addresses. Anubis Gate, the Danish progressive metal band, favor exactly this melancholy introspection, wrapping regret over lost years and failed dreams in atmospheric, melodic arrangements.","sonnet":"A life measured out and found hollow — the speaker watches their dreams get redirected by bureaucratic forces into something prescribed and empty, leaving them disintegrated at the threshold. The precious thing they wanted was simply a world of their own, unmolested by schemes and systems. Instead they got a hollow man with nothing to say, and they know it.","src":"haiku"},
"anubis-gate~the-re-formation-show":{"haiku":"A person has been trapped in emotional paralysis and isolation but is beginning to step toward a transformative rebirth, though the process frightens and disorients them.","opus":"A weary narrator, bowed by pressure and heeding inner voices, feels cut off from his truest self and afraid of becoming new. Anubis Gate turn self-renewal into a titular spectacle he finally sets out to witness, the shifting wordplay on reformation and dissolution tracing a reluctant reemergence back toward the world and its light.","sonnet":"After years of hiding in a dark internal hole, estranged from his own soul, a man gathers the nerve to step back into the world — frightened but ready to remake himself at the Re-formation show, a private ritual of return and self-renewal.","src":"haiku"},
"anubis-gate~world-in-a-dome":{"haiku":"A critique of authoritarian control and the loss of human chaos and emotion in sterile urban environments designed by and for the powerful.","opus":"A chilly dystopia of engineered perfection, mile after mile of concrete and rigidly planned cities sealed off from disorder, contradiction or warmth. The Danish prog-metal band build a cautionary tale about tech-utopian control and state design, warning that this superman fantasy, however tidy, may eventually turn against the people who built it.","sonnet":"A controlled utopia examined with suspicion — cities of expertise, men with visions, futures mapped in parallel lines, all arriving at a perfectly contained world with no contradictions and no room for chaos or affection. The song acknowledges the seduction of having everything you want safely enclosed, then warns that possession handled carelessly turns on you. The final image is a constitution cast in chrome.","src":"haiku"},
"anup-sastry-andy-cizek~where-i-belong":{"haiku":"Yearning for purpose drives urgency against the erosion of time; the speaker reclaims agency through action and defiance, insisting their place in the world is seized, not granted.","opus":"A refusal to sit and wait for fate or an afterlife to hand over meaning, the narrator revisiting his youth to dig up a lost purpose and claim what's his. Awareness of mortality is what makes life urgent, and the insistence on doing the work himself rather than praying makes self-determination the path to belonging.","sonnet":"A search for purpose across a life of uncertainty: the speaker retraces childhood footprints hunting for a buried truth, wrestling with mortality, religious doubt, and the fear of a closing window, and ultimately arrives at a conviction that persistence and self-knowledge point toward the place where they belong. The fever-dream coda — choices never made, outcomes unchangeable — folds shame and acceptance together.","src":"haiku"},
"anup-sastry-chaney-crabb~this-world":{"haiku":"Purposelessness demands active transformation; the speaker channels their descent into becoming light and hope for others lost in darkness.","opus":"A search for purpose that resolves into self-reliance, the narrator vowing to become the light and pillar he never found, carrying the whole world alone. It sits with the solitude of a journey begun and ended by oneself.","sonnet":"Searching for purpose in a complacent, aimless drift, the speaker resolves to become the pillar of light they've never seen — taking the world into the palm of their hand, beginning and ending the journey alone — and asks the ocean for solace while refusing to accept that existence leaves nothing behind.","src":"haiku"},
"anup-sastry-mike-semesky~illuminate":{"haiku":"Manufactured happiness and evolutionary pressure question human authenticity; the speaker numbs pain while history guides toward uncertain futures where instinct and technology converge.","opus":"A prog meditation on transhumanism, asking whether engineering away pain and suffering leaves us still human. It turns the promise of manufactured happiness into an open question about what we lose when evolution becomes a choice.","sonnet":"A meditation on whether humanity survives its own technological and biological evolution — senses manufactured, pain numbed, suffering made optional — asking repeatedly \"are we still human?\" as evolutionary minds outpace the ancient instincts and love becomes a Darwinian survival tale.","src":"haiku"},
"anup-sastry~where-i-belong":{"haiku":"Searching for purpose and direction after years of waiting for life to begin. Taking control of destiny by reclaiming what was always meant to be theirs, driven by mortality awareness.","opus":"A restless hunt for what is truly one's own, refusing to sit and wait on fate while retracing the path back to childhood in search of a buried truth and a rightful place. Awareness of death only sharpens the appetite for life, all or nothing. Anup Sastry, the drummer-producer known for solo prog-metal, frames self-determination as a feverish pilgrimage through pain toward clarity.","sonnet":"A long reckoning with time, purpose, and displacement — the speaker traces footprints back to a buried message from their younger self, insisting on all or nothing rather than settling. The song moves through fear of a closing window, through fire and frozen oceans, to something firmer: a spark that survives the dark, a direction found by getting your own hands dirty rather than waiting for fate to deliver.","src":"haiku"},
"any-given-day~h-a-t-e":{"haiku":"Internal civil war between fragmented selves; hatred and fear crystallize into pure destructive force.","opus":"A soul split in two, the narrator personifying his own hatred and fear as a monster wearing his face. The fight is internal and near-religious, picking up his cross to reclaim a self that anger has hollowed out.","sonnet":"The speaker voices the hate inside them as a separate entity — I am the hate, I am the fear, the voice you can't hear, the end always near — split in two, buried alive in his own past, he ultimately picks up the cross to save the soul, recognizing the monster is an empty shell, a shadow of himself.","src":"haiku"},
"aphex-twin~minipops-67-120-2-source-field-mix":{"haiku":"Observation and mental descent; falling deeper into consciousness while watching others in distance.","opus":"Sampled murmurs float over the beat almost as afterthought, half-formed musings and a bored, misanthropic sigh about replacing these earthlings. The words are texture rather than statement, a private drift caught mid-thought.","sonnet":"Fragmentary, half-formed address to a bored relationship and a species running out of ideas — watching people in Scotland, dodging beat hooks, unable to replace the earthlings and maybe just needing a break. The boredom is both mundane and cosmic: you, why did I get bored with you — directed at a person, a planet, possibly both.","src":"haiku"},
"aphex-twin~we-are-the-music-makers":{"opus":"A looped sample declaring artists to be the music makers and dreamers of dreams (echoing the Willy Wonka / O'Shaughnessy line), celebrating creators as visionaries; beyond that repeated affirmation there is no further developed theme.","sonnet":"A looped incantation borrowed from Willy Wonka and the Oompa Loompas (originally Arthur O'Shaughnessy's poem) — a pure, minimal assertion of artistic identity, dreamers claiming ownership of music and vision; the repetition is the entire statement.","src":"sonnet"},
"aphyxion~anomalies":{"haiku":"Marginalization and rebellion where the outcast embraces monsterhood as resistance. The narrator refuses shame and reclaims difference as power.","opus":"A defiant anthem for the outcast, taking every slur and stare and wearing monster as a badge. The stance is refusal: don't let their silence or stereotypes shrink your wonder, disobedience as identity.","sonnet":"An anthem for the stigmatised and excluded: whispering eyes and stereotypes are answered with \"we are the monsters, born to disobey\" — the speaker and their community reclaim their outcast status as anomalies, urging one another not to let silence become words or wonder be stolen.","src":"haiku"},
"aphyxion~pathless":{"haiku":"The speaker surrenders to meaninglessness and decay, seeking only erasure and escape from the hollow machinery of existence. Dreams of neverland become the only refuge from this incomplete dissolution.","opus":"Nihilistic surrender rendered as relief, the narrator embracing meaninglessness and asking to be broken and forsaken. Reduced to a chunk of meat adrift in the void, he finds a strange freedom in refusing to be saved.","sonnet":"Nihilism worn almost comfortably — the narrator is a dot in space, a chunk of meat, thriving in the vacuum of meaninglessness; they dream of endless escape not with despair but with the strange relief of someone who has stopped fighting the void.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica-feat-corey-taylor~i-m-not-jesus":{"haiku":"Refusal to absolve or forgive abuse masked by religiosity and hypocrisy. The narrator rejects the savior role and demands accountability from the perpetrator.","opus":"Corey Taylor confronting a religious authority figure who abused a child, refusing the forgiveness the church demands. The wound is personal and the verdict absolute: no absolution, no turning the other cheek, God was absent when it mattered.","sonnet":"A survivor of clerical abuse confronts their abuser: the speaker refuses the role of forgiving saviour — \"I'm not Jesus, Jesus wasn't there\" — denying the abuser the absolution of confession and insisting that even a God looking down cannot compel forgiveness for innocence that was spoiled.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica-feat-marta-jandova~how-far":{"haiku":"Privilege becomes prison where stability breeds dissatisfaction and sadness. The narrator questions the distance required to find authenticity beneath false contentment.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica-feat-marta-jandov~how-far":{"haiku":"Trapped privilege disguised as stability, questioning why abundance brings only sadness and failed self-pursuit.","opus":"A cello-metal meditation on privileged discontent, life as a closed circle offering no air. Having more than she ever had and still empty, the narrator interrogates the pride and self-deception keeping her caged.","sonnet":"A circling dissatisfaction despite having everything: the life is a loop, a stable jail of skin, a false fable praised for its sin — the speaker asks how far they must go to find self-respect when even privilege and satisfaction coexist with inexplicable sadness.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica-feat-marta-jandov~wie-weit":{"haiku":"Monotony and heaviness suffocate the soul as riches cannot purchase meaning; the singer questions whether distance or surrender is the path to feeling alive in a cursed existence.","opus":"A depressive's paradox sung across German and English, life circling in monotony as the speaker asks how far she must go to reach night's end, cursed and too weak to rise. Its plain center is the guilt no abundance can answer, sadness that arrives despite having more than ever before.","sonnet":"A German-language meditation on paralysis and privilege: the speaker's life spins in monotonous circles, they feel like a permanent guest rather than a person alive in the world, and gold burns their skin rather than satisfying them — yet they cannot shake the inertia or fight the war they're losing against their own rules. The refrain \"Wie weit muss ich gehen\" (how far must I go) asks whether any amount of effort will break the spell.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica-feat-ville-valo-lauri-yl-nen~bittersweet":{"haiku":"Love transforms into curse and possession; obsession and worship collapse into mutual entrapment.","opus":"Ville Valo's voice makes this a hymn to a love that is also a curse, worship and suffocation in one. The plea to break the spell is half-hearted; he would rather stay possessed, adoration and torment inseparable.","sonnet":"A man trapped in obsessive love he recognises as his own curse: she is both the queen of his suffering and the cross he bears, and the only prayer is to break the bittersweet spell that has claimed him.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica-feat-ville-valo-lauri-yl-nen~bittersweet-acoustic":{"haiku":"Love becomes a spell that traps and torments, transforming devotion into curse where the speaker is consumed by longing and possessed by an affection that destroys.","opus":"Two voices trade lines of obsessive, doomed devotion, each in thrall to a woman who's both adored queen and personal curse. The plea to break the bittersweet spell frames love as a suffocation they can neither surrender nor survive, handed over to fate.","sonnet":"Two voices are ensnared by the same woman: one is giving up the ghost of love while still adoring her, the other bearing a cross she has become, both lost in the arms of destiny and begging the bittersweet spell to break. The duet structure makes the shared ruin explicit — two men, one obsession, no exit.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~bittersweet":{"haiku":"An obsessive, destructive love that becomes a curse; the speaker is trapped in possession by their partner and trapped between desire and suffering.","opus":"Obsessive, doomed devotion, a lover surrendering himself as though possessed, calling his adored one both queen and curse and begging to be freed from her spell. Desire and torment are inseparable here. Apocalyptica are the Finnish cello quartet who mutated from Metallica covers into gothic composers, and this collaboration layers a mournful string-borne heaviness beneath its vampiric romance of surrender to destiny.","sonnet":"A devotion so consuming it has become a kind of possession — the speaker wants to be free of it but cannot let go, carrying the love like a cross and a curse simultaneously. The emotion swings between wanting release and wanting her back, trapped in a bittersweet spell with no clear way out.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~bittersweet-instrumental":{"haiku":"Love becomes a spell that traps and torments, transforming devotion into curse where the speaker is consumed by longing and possessed by an affection that destroys.","opus":"The same tale of obsessive, cursed devotion, a woman who is both the one adored and the cross the speaker bears, wanting and needing her past all reason. The bittersweet spell he begs to break names a love that suffocates yet cannot be surrendered.","sonnet":"Two voices are ensnared by the same woman: one is giving up the ghost of love while still adoring her, the other bearing a cross she has become, both lost in the arms of destiny and begging the bittersweet spell to break. The duet structure makes the shared ruin explicit — two men, one obsession, no exit.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~broken-pieces-ft-lacey":{"haiku":"Toxic devotion shatters both partners; separation becomes necessary despite emotional entanglement.","opus":"With Flyleaf's Lacey Mosley, a song about loving someone drowning in their own darkness and finally choosing to survive rather than sink with them. The heartbreak is that leaving is the only way to save yourself from a person you can't fix.","sonnet":"A person escapes a relationship in which they gave everything while the other person darkened and fractured — the speaker loves the other too much to stay, knowing that leaving is the only way either of them survives.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~burn":{"haiku":"Slow-burn love and permanent scarring; the speaker traces flame and ash through their body, unable to forget or recover what's been consumed.","opus":"Love as a slow, consuming fire that keeps smoldering long after the relationship's burned to ash, the speaker willing to step back in just to feel that presence again. Every cruel word is a scar cut too deep to cover, yet still they keep stoking the flame.","sonnet":"Grief and obsession after losing someone — the narrator is burned by the person's continued presence everywhere, unable to forget, falling apart like paper on a flame; the love is already ash and embers but they would still walk through fire to keep it from going out entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~cold-blood":{"haiku":"A relationship killed through ignorance and indifference slowly starved the couple of love and growth; distance and renewed sobriety offer possible healing, though apathy nearly finished the ruin.","opus":"A post-breakup autopsy in which both partners share the blame for wrecking a relationship they slowly poisoned with contempt and cruelty. Beneath the grief sits a tentative hope that time apart might let each wounded person mend once the bitterness clears.","sonnet":"A post-mortem on a relationship that both partners destroyed through ignorance, hate, and neglect — they killed the love deliberately and methodically until nothing could grow in it anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~end-of-me-feat-gavin-rossdale-300mph-remix":{"haiku":"Love as consuming force and self-obliteration where surrender means death. The narrator bleeds and suffocates, the beloved becomes the wound and weapon.","opus":"A love turned self-annihilating, addressed to a narcissist whose empire is collapsing while the singer, the one who gave everything, drowns in the wreckage. It knows the relationship is killing him and stays anyway.","sonnet":"A toxic love recognised too late as lethal: the narrator bleeds waiting for the other's fun to end, catalogues wasted giving and self-suffocation, names the partner as a narcissist trapped in wreckage — \"this love is gonna be the end of me\" — and in the closing lines admits they are empty, drowning in the other's shadow.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~faraway-radio-edit":{"haiku":"Emotional desperation pervades a relationship drowning in distance and absence. The narrator pleads for presence while acknowledging their own vulnerability, framing love as both lifeline and existential dependence.","opus":"A plea to someone slipping out of reach, emotionally already gone, from a narrator who admits he doesn't believe in God yet prays for them. Devotion here is desperate and one-sided, love as pure vulnerability.","sonnet":"A desperate plea to someone emotionally absent — always somewhere else — as the speaker's feelings drain away to a point of no return. Despite not believing in gods, they pray; the refrain \"don't you slip away from me\" confesses a vulnerability that borders on helplessness.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~helden":{"haiku":"Two people discover heroic defiance against overwhelming odds; despite being outmatched, they win freedom and agency for one transcendent moment. Love becomes resistance.","opus":"The Finnish cello-metal ensemble covers Bowie's Berlin anthem in its original German, keeping the divided-city ache intact. Lovers with no chance imagine kissing at a cold wall as shots tear the air, becoming heroes for just one day. King and queen for that single defiant afternoon, they vow to beat the seemingly unbeatable, shame falling on the other side, transience and triumph held in one fragile breath.","sonnet":"Two lovers steal a moment of fierce intimacy against an impossible backdrop — gunfire in the air, a wall at their backs — and in that act of defiance claim heroism, if only for a single day. The German-language lyric frames love as the one act that can outshine overwhelming force.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~hole-in-my-soul":{"haiku":"Loss leaves a void—the speaker regrets pushing someone away, now hollow and haunted by what could have been.","opus":"Straightforward heartbreak after a breakup the singer caused, lost in the woods with a hole where the loved one used to be; the deeper thread is regret and accountability-he pushed the person away and would do anything, differently, for one more chance to fill that emptiness.","sonnet":"A post-breakup song about the specific emptiness left by someone's absence — the narrator knows they drove the person away through their own carelessness and now lives with regret and a hollow space that won't fill, still hoping the door isn't fully closed.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~house-of-chains":{"haiku":"A person describes being trapped in an abusive relationship under the guise of devotion; their partner controls them through psychological suffocation disguised as love and care.","opus":"An abusive relationship rendered as captivity: seduced by someone's majesty, the narrator becomes just another trophy among the bones of past victims, suffocated by kisses and reduced to submission. The chains and angel wings expose the trap of loving a predator you can't leave.","sonnet":"A narrator describes being lured by someone who seemed majestic but turned out to be a predator who trapped and subjugated them, the relationship a prison full of past victims where submission is suffocated into permanence.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~i-m-not-jesus":{"haiku":"A survivor confronts a figure of religious authority (clergy implied) who abused and exploited them as a child, refusing forgiveness and rejecting any comparable role or redemption the aggressor seeks.","opus":"The Finnish cello-metal group, with a guest vocal, confront a childhood abuser hiding behind piety, the narrator refusing the forgiveness religion demands. Do you remember the kid I used to be becomes the accusation's spine, and his flat refrain that he is not Jesus turns absolution into a door slammed shut.","sonnet":"A survivor of clerical abuse confronts the abuser directly, refusing the sacrament of forgiveness. Confession changes nothing; the narrator will not absolve. The song strips the perpetrator of every holy title he hid behind and insists that some wounds require the opposite of mercy — a clear, permanent refusal.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~path-vol-ii":{"haiku":"Immersion in fire and desire becomes pathway to liberation as the speaker craves intensity and danger. Losing routine and safety births genuine living beneath the surface.","opus":"A declaration of having found one's own way and leaving old routines behind, thrown as a challenge at those still denying and clinging. The recurring demand asks what you actually live for, insisting the answer is already inside you.","sonnet":"A departure from someone else's expectations: the narrator has lost their old routine and found their own path, wants to live in fire and dive with sharks, and keeps asking the doubting other — \"are you still denying?\" — that the truth is already inside them.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~quutamo":{"haiku":"Monotony and heaviness suffocate the soul as riches cannot purchase meaning; the singer questions whether distance or surrender is the path to feeling alive in cursed stasis.","opus":"A German-language lament of monotonous, isolated life drawing an endless circle of eternity, the speaker too weak to rise and cursed by her own inertia. The break into English asking why she's sad despite having more than ever exposes depression's cruelty, no wealth able to lift the weight of the sky.","sonnet":"A German-language meditation on paralysis and privilege: the speaker's life spins in monotonous circles, they feel like a permanent guest rather than a person alive in the world, and gold burns their skin rather than satisfying them — yet they cannot shake the inertia or fight the war they're losing against their own rules. The refrain \"Wie weit muss ich gehen\" (how far must I go) asks whether any amount of effort will break the spell.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~rise":{"haiku":"Oppression from within and without compounds until the self must fight for its own liberation; the speaker transforms from victim to victor, rising from ash to claim their power.","opus":"A journey out of smothering despair and inherited guilt into defiant recovery, the narrator battling the pain and the condemnation that broke her before finally releasing her past. The shift from doubting she'll ever recover to promising the world will watch her rise makes it an arc of reclaimed strength.","sonnet":"A survivor of psychological abuse moves from suffocation — shadow closing in, no air left, guilt given by someone else — through a turning point where they shed the past and find they are flying rather than falling. The final verse inverts the opening's despair line by line: \"dear world, now you'll see me clear,\" the rain inside the heart gone, the tormentor released.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~sea-song-you-waded-out":{"haiku":"The sea reclaims those who wade outward; names and blame dissolve into insignificance as the waters draw the seeker backward.","opus":"A cold farewell to someone who has walked into the sea, the speaker insisting none of it, their name, the blame, where they're going, matters to him, detachment hardening until they no longer matter at all. The restless water calling them back reads as a drowning he refuses to grieve.","sonnet":"Someone has waded out into the sea and the sea calls back to them; the speaker, stripped of any claim or blame, watches with detachment — \"it don't matter to me,\" \"you don't matter to me\" — as the person is drawn further out, the restless water reclaiming whoever walked in.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~shadowmaker":{"haiku":"One person's darkness and despair casts a shadow over the narrator, trapping them in depression and a toxic bond they cannot escape despite the cost to their wellbeing.","opus":"Apocalyptica cast a poisonous, clinging relationship as being shackled under someone's self-destructive music, a gorgeous, wretched figure who snuffs out any light. The narrator would trail them to the ends of the planet even as he withers from within, held fast by a tie neither will let go.","sonnet":"Trapped alongside someone whose despair eclipses everything, the narrator cannot leave and does not want to stay. The other person chokes out all light, drags the narrator down with a kind of gravitational misery, and remains oblivious to the cost. The relationship is a mutual suffocation neither party escapes.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~slow-burn":{"haiku":"Lost love smolders as obsessive memory that transforms and scars the lover, burning them down across time.","opus":"The Finnish cello-metal group cast devotion and loss as an ember that keeps glowing long after the blaze, the narrator coming undone yet pleading for the love to endure. Its dramatic strings underscore a paradox: he wants a consuming attachment that outlives the destruction it causes, clinging even to the marks it leaves.","sonnet":"Grief for a lost relationship that will not fade — the absence smoulders rather than extinguishes, flaring whenever a name or face surfaces. The narrator refuses to let this person disappear, choosing to tend the ember indefinitely rather than allow it to go cold.","src":"haiku"},
"apocalyptica~struggle":{"haiku":"A lover retreats into danger while the narrator cannot save them despite fierce devotion. The repetition of isolation and prayer marks the futility of holding someone slipping away.","opus":"The same core as Faraway, expanded: a fight to keep someone from fading away, praying for a person you can't hold onto. The love is protective and helpless, aimed at someone the light is going out on.","sonnet":"The same anatomy of losing someone to distance and inner darkness, extended with a second voice urging them to stay away from the blinding sun — the risk of losing them entirely sharpens into the line \"your eyes getting blurry, still I'm crying your name.\" Two forces of care and fear of collapse intertwine around one unraveling person.","src":"haiku"},
"apricot~sun-killer":{"haiku":"Destruction and collapse loom as inevitable force; the song balances between being born to break and the mounting violence of resistance. A lullaby of apocalypse sung to numb the oncoming end.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with a built-in capacity for harm — someone made to destroy, who knows that staying will only leave wreckage. The lullaby of the title is a plea to be reassured the monsters will pass, sung, painfully, by the monster itself.","sonnet":"A voice born to break shallow paradises confronts the violence latent in staying — \"if I stay, I know I'll be violent\" — while seeking a lullaby that could temper the blade of its own destructive nature. The world is one of dust-born parasites and colliding ocean floors, and the only comfort offered is being sung to sleep by the very force that destroys.","src":"haiku"},
"april27~into-a-g":{"haiku":"Religious conditioning and party excess blur together as the narrator seeks salvation through another person's love. Escape from thought requires transcendence, transforming desperate affection into worship.","opus":"Club hedonism reaching for transcendence, a lapsed believer trading religion for the dancefloor and the promise of being loved. The wish to turn into a god is what happens when someone chases salvation in music, drink and touch instead of faith.","sonnet":"In the crush of a party and a mosh pit, a speaker desperate for real connection asks someone to take them away from the noise and just say they're loved — that declaration alone, they claim, would turn them into a god. Religious memory and sexual longing blur as the function's alcohol and energy fail to substitute for the one thing they need.","src":"haiku"},
"aqua-timez~mask":{"haiku":"Wounds mark all human hearts; the speaker refuses masks, embracing scars as proof of survival. Love means not breaking company with the damaged, walking together through rain toward some future moment of alignment.","opus":"A tender Japanese ballad about the masks we wear to belong, carrying love silently within us because words keep failing. Its ache is the narrator realizing how long he suppressed his own tears, longing to laugh and cry without the disguise.","sonnet":"A meditation on the masks people wear against isolation and self-doubt: each body carries love inside it from birth and travels toward someone else who will find and embrace even the loneliness that has gone missing inside — \"you searched for my lost solitude and held it\" — and the final wish is simply to laugh with nothing covering the heart.","src":"haiku"},
"aqua-timez~velonica":{"haiku":"A weary traveler reflects on accumulated sadness and failure while searching for truth beyond mere survival; they watch others suffer yet yearn for genuine connection.","opus":"A weary traveler's search for meaning, wandering foreign roads while questioning whether just being alive is enough. The gentle throughline consoles someone who has borne too much sadness too young, admitting we aren't strong and don't need to be, and that no life truly ends without first crying to be loved.","sonnet":"A tender address to a girl who has seen too much sorrow too young — she hides tears she shouldn't have to hide, and the narrator tells her she doesn't need to be strong, before reflecting that survival alone isn't enough: people are born wanting to be alive and loved.","src":"haiku"},
"aquarifa~switch":{"haiku":"Memory and desire pull in opposite directions; the speaker pauses to savor denial of ending. Repetition offers false comfort, rewinding time rather than facing forward irreversibility.","opus":"A refusal to accept a breakup, the speaker crossing out a name in a planner and letting it dissolve in a sigh while begging 'don't say goodbye'—'let me keep believing in what's ahead, even if it's a lie.' She rewinds and replays the relationship like tape ('no matter how many times I try, it's the same'), bursting her own eardrums to not hear the truth, pleading to be deceived: 'even if this is a dream, I don't care.'","sonnet":"A speaker erasing a name from their diary but unable to truly delete the person begs — \"please don't say goodbye, even if it's a lie, keep making me believe\" — pausing time for a single moment of hope and admitting that if farewell is spoken, they will never be able to see the other again.","src":"haiku"},
"arankai~rome":{"haiku":"Imperial ambition consumes the speaker as they declare themselves throne and hellfire. Authority demands submission and obedience, with defiance met by violent conquest and erasure.","opus":"A vengeance monologue in the voice of empire itself, someone thrown to the wolves who survived and now demands the debt back with interest. Cruelty is owned openly: mercy was never shown, so none will be given.","sonnet":"A speaker casts themselves as an empire — Rome, hellfire, a throne built of collected bones — declaring total war on society and anyone who imposed mercy or divinity on them. Wounded by being \"thrown to the wolves,\" they now wear a crown of thorns and demand penitence from all, framing their cruelty as inevitable restitution.","src":"haiku"},
"arcadia-s-fate~the-only-man-on-earth":{"haiku":"Darkness traps the mind while an inescapable presence haunts the narrator's interior. Someone who promised unwavering commitment now breeds resentment; the speaker seeks freedom from this psychological tether.","opus":"A furious severing from a toxic presence, where total solitude is imagined as paradise rather than punishment. The narrator would worship loneliness just to be free of someone he's declared dead to him.","sonnet":"Someone trapped in a suffocating relationship fantasizes about the peace of total solitude — they would \"worship loneliness\" just to be free of the other person's lies and dark presence. The turn from pleading to cold severance is marked by \"you're fucking dead to me,\" and the final wish is simply to feel alive again.","src":"haiku"},
"arcane-roots~matter":{"haiku":"The speaker waits for violence to arrive while watching life rot and decay, describing birth and suffering as interconnected forces that consume meaning.","opus":"An unsettling, ritualistic address, part patient waiting and part infliction of hurt on a holy child, love and cruelty braided as something creeps ever closer to the vein. The imagery of womb, worm and needle suggests corruption working slowly toward the bone.","sonnet":"A fractured meditation on birth, pain, and descent — the speaker crawls toward a maternal figure while watching everything they've built decay and fall apart, feeling helpless as cruelty finds its mark.","src":"haiku"},
"arca~no-queda-nada":{"haiku":"All interior space has been colonized by another's presence; total emotional possession leaves nothing untouched or unexplored, erasing individual boundaries.","opus":"The Venezuelan producer strips down to devotion so total that nothing of the self remains outside the beloved, addressed by name. It is surrender as ecstasy, the erotic and the spiritual collapsing into one repeated vow of belonging.","sonnet":"Total erotic and emotional surrender to another person — nothing remains in the speaker that has not been touched, filled, or claimed by this love, addressed tenderly and repeatedly to Carlos.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~aces-high-iron-maiden-cover":{"haiku":"WWII aerial combat where pilots race toward combat with adrenaline and courage, fighting for survival against superior forces.","opus":"A cockpit's-eye account of a Battle of Britain dogfight, scrambling airborne against incoming bombers, rolling and diving through Messerschmitts and firing straight into the sun, the pilot's creed to live to fly and fly to live. This is Arch Enemy covering an Iron Maiden classic, so the wartime narrative belongs to Maiden's Bruce Dickinson while Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band, growl it through a harsher, heavier lens.","sonnet":"The controlled terror of a Second World War scramble — sirens, engines, split-second takeoffs, and Spitfires climbing to meet a wave of bombers and Me-109s out of the sun. The pilots live by a single creed: fly to live, live to fly, keep rolling and firing, because stopping means falling.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~angelclaw-live-in-japan-99":{"haiku":"A seductive destructive woman drains and manipulates others, a dark queen who uses beauty to trap and consume souls.","opus":"A theatrical piece of occult horror centered on a vampiric female demon who enthralls and destroys her victims, performing a grim blood ritual before hunting anew. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death-metal band, recorded this early with original singer Johan Liiva, before Angela Gossow, placing this live Japan performance among their formative years.","sonnet":"A supernatural seductress who rules from a stone throne, equal parts deity and predator. She drains her victims deliberately, raises a blood chalice in ritual, and never stops hunting. There's no escape route — her spell is cast before you know it, and she knows you far better than you know yourself.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~as-the-pages-burn":{"haiku":"Cleansing amnesia where burning one's history offers freedom but leaves existential emptiness and the burden of starting over.","opus":"The Swedish melodic death metal band weigh the temptation to erase the past, presenting oblivion as a clean escape from every ordeal and from reality itself. But the reset comes with a catch: an empty slate that demands you figure out where to begin again. Alissa White-Gluz's snarl rides soaring melody as the song measures forgetting against its cost.","sonnet":"Amnesia as salvation — burning the written record of your past to escape guilt, grief, and memory. The smoke clears and a blank page stares back, carrying both the terror of having nothing left and the promise of starting over. The fire doesn't solve anything; it just hands you a pen.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~beast-of-man":{"haiku":"Humans are devils who kill for sport and greed, destroying everything they touch. The song urges rejection and containment of humanity's predatory nature.","opus":"Inspired by Planet of the Apes, this treats humanity as a uniquely dangerous species, the one animal that slaughters not for survival but for pleasure and gain. Arch Enemy voice it as a scriptural cautionary tale, urging that mankind be driven off and prevented from multiplying before it lays waste to everything around it.","sonnet":"A warning drawn from what sounds like a modified Planet of the Apes passage: man is the only primate that kills for sport and greed, and should be shunned and driven back before he turns the world into a desert.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~behind-the-smile":{"haiku":"Profound disconnection where the narrator feels nothing internally despite appearing functional; hollow behind public facade.","opus":"A study of numbness and hollow performance, a woman watching her life slip through her fingers while she smiles for others and feels nothing, reaching out but never really present. Pills and glasses only deepen the disconnection from the stranger in the mirror. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band later fronted by Alissa White-Gluz, wrap this depiction of quiet self-erasure in soaring melody and a growled ache of alone-in-a-crowd despair.","sonnet":"Dissociation behind a maintained social surface — time running out, the mirror returning a stranger, pills and drink as daily management of an interior numbness. The person reaching out can't quite make contact with themselves, let alone anyone else; the smile is the mask holding in the absence of feeling.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~black-earth":{"haiku":"Planetary decay where humanity's sins have created a wasteland of fire and death; nature's brutal revenge on its destroyers.","opus":"An environmental doomsday vision of a ruined, dying planet where humanity brought its own extinction and any plea for rescue arrives far too late. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band, borrowed their name and first album from this bleak title track pairing twin-guitar melody with prophecy of a stilled world.","sonnet":"Environmental collapse read as moral verdict — humanity surveying a scorched, decayed planet it brought on itself through a century of violence and exploitation. The song moves between accusation and resignation, landing on an image of a darkened world still waiting for a miracle that reason says won't come.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~blood-on-your-hands-live":{"haiku":"A keeper becomes a slayer of brothers; the song traces familial betrayal, inherited sin, and the wages of violence passed down generations.","opus":"A furious reckoning framed through Cain and Abel, indicting inherited hatred and the fratricidal wars each generation keeps re-fighting. Angela Gossow's death growl turns the biblical wages of sin into a courtroom verdict on humanity.","sonnet":"About inherited guilt and the chain of violence passed through generations — framing brotherly betrayal and the sins of fathers as a legacy that condemns the living, with the dead watching from the other side.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~bloodstained-cross":{"haiku":"Religious deception preys on the weak through twisted truths, promising salvation that prayer cannot deliver.","opus":"A furious anti-clerical indictment aimed at faith weaponized against the weak, a deceiver whose bloodstained cross was believed while the believers get no relief, only dust and an approaching Armageddon no prayer can stop. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band fronted at this point by Alissa White-Gluz, wield their signature razor riffing to strip the mask off power built on years of religious abuse.","sonnet":"An indictment of institutional religion as a con — belief weaponised to control the weak, prayers offered up at the end that change nothing. The cross is bloodstained because power was always twisted underneath the vows of salvation, and Armageddon is simply the bill coming due. The deceived believed, and that belief cost them everything.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~bridge-of-destiny":{"haiku":"Fear surrenders at destiny's crossing while darkness lurks; old words fragment against nature's laws as anger corrupts thought itself.","opus":"Poised at a turning point and wrestling private demons as his resolve reaches its lowest ebb, the narrator lands on the recognition that the real adversary is himself. The fight is to see through his own illusions and cut his soul loose.","sonnet":"Standing at a bridge between sanity and abyss, surrounded by inner demons flapping in their face, the speaker passes through fear to clarity — seeing through the liquid souls of evil eyes — and finds that wisdom pours in endlessly when the trapped spirits of the dead are released and the bridge becomes a threshold rather than an ending.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~bridge-of-destiny-live":{"haiku":"Fate builds bridge toward chosen destination as the speaker walks toward predetermined meeting. Destiny becomes tangible structure supporting arrival.","opus":"The live rendering of the same reckoning at destiny's threshold: fear, self-doubt and inner demons met head-on, the truth that the real enemy is oneself, wisdom bought only by facing the darkness within.","sonnet":"Standing at the threshold of a decisive moment, the narrator faces inner demons flapping in their face, sees through liquid souls and hypnotic eyes, and arrives at the paradox: \"I am my own worst enemy\" — the bridge of destiny is the point where guardians of lost faith turn to sand and wisdom pours in endlessly.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~bridge-of-destiny-live-in-paris-2004-bonus-track":{"haiku":"A person confronts inner demons and illusory torments, ultimately claiming agency over fear and discovering self-mastery through wisdom.","opus":"A melodic death metal track about confronting one's own psyche, where the narrator masters fear and paranoia, recognizes he is his own greatest threat, and reduces his tormentors to dust to reclaim his mind. This live Paris recording captures the era fronted by the band's first female vocalist and her savage growl.","sonnet":"Standing at a symbolic threshold where sanity and collapse converge, the narrator confronts inner demons and the seductive power of twisted illusions. Wisdom arrives like water through a wound — the realisation that one is one's own worst enemy, and also one's own deliverance.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~burning-angel":{"haiku":"An innocent person is condemned and burned at the stake by a corrupt society driven by superstition and moral guardianship, their blood staining history forever.","opus":"Arch Enemy tell of an innocent sentenced to the stake by self-righteous keepers of a rotten moral order, insisting on their guiltlessness as fire spread by superstition eats away at body and spirit. Swedish melodic death metal turned toward a grim parable of persecution.","sonnet":"A figure condemned to burn at the stake declares their innocence, framing their execution as the product of corrupt morality, superstition, and the lies of self-appointed guardians — they die unjustly as the ravens laugh overhead.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~bury-me-an-angel":{"haiku":"Life torn between light and death is symbolized by mystical imagery. A person seeks freedom from their ruined spirit, understanding they are damned forever.","opus":"From Arch Enemy's early Michael Amott era, a meditation on the uncertain threshold between living and dying, staged as a mystical, unnavigable struggle. The plea for a peaceful burial becomes a dark bargain, weighing damnation against release in the Swedish melodic-death tradition.","sonnet":"A figure caught between life and death — neither fully damned nor saved — appeals for release from a blackened soul. The imagery of nocturnal chariots bearing ruined spirits suggests a metaphysical limbo where the traveler departs with only uncertainty, condemned by forces larger than any individual transgression.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~bury-me-an-angel-live":{"haiku":"Caught between life and death, the narrator faces uncertain passage with only sacrifice to show for existence. The plea for release frames the soul as something dark requiring burial rather than salvation.","opus":"Early Arch Enemy occult melodrama, a soul caught between life and death asking to be freed even as it's damned. The imagery is more ritual than argument, the plea to have a blackened spirit released.","sonnet":"Torn between life and death in a symbolically charged landscape of blackened chariots and burning holes, the speaker asks to be buried as an angel so their damned black soul can finally be freed. Mystical imagery frames damnation as a foregone conclusion for a nocturnal traveller carrying a ruined spirit.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~carry-the-cross":{"haiku":"The burden of perpetual suffering, judgment, and cyclical pain becomes an inescapable cross. The song depicts reincarnated torment and strength drawn from endless hardship.","opus":"The song casts life as an endless burden carried like a cross through the ages, watched and mocked by others waiting for the bearer to fall. It frames the suffering as damnation and confession, with pain itself becoming a source of strength to keep going.","sonnet":"The song uses the image of carrying a cross as a metaphor for a lifetime of burden, suffering, and cycles of pain that repeat without end. Each time the speaker is beaten down, the cross holds them in place while others walk away laughing.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~city-of-the-dead":{"haiku":"Urban poverty as literal graveyard where the poor are stillborn to hope, trapped in burial grounds with neither dignity nor future.","opus":"A furious lament for the forgotten poor, imagined as people condemned to live among tombs in a necropolis where children laugh between gravestones that will become their own. The fury is social, aimed at a world that offers no shelter, no justice, no future. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band, weld their aggression to real moral outrage, turning genre brutality into protest.","sonnet":"A portrait of people literally born and raised among gravestones in a necropolis, children playing on tombs because they have nowhere else to go. The song catalogues lives stripped of dignity, safety, and future — stillborn into poverty, their only inheritance the bones of the dead beneath their feet.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~cosmic-retribution":{"haiku":"Humanity faces apocalypse and divine punishment for its sins as a lone machine carries the last seed of human life.","opus":"The Swedish melodic death metal act stage a doomsday reckoning where humanity finally answers for its sins as the sky ignites and everything cherished is consumed. Their one concession to hope is a sci-fi flicker: a single automated vessel bearing a human embryo escapes the dead planet, survival reduced to one seed among the ashes.","sonnet":"Apocalypse as verdict — the heavens burn, everything loved perishes, and it was always coming because of what was done. The song holds very still at the threshold of extinction, then ends with a single flicker: a human embryo carried by a machine out of the silent planet. Destruction and the faint biological fact of continuation, held in the same breath.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~cruelty-without-beauty":{"haiku":"Scientific torture presented as progress; victims objectified without empathy, their suffering erased as necessary cost.","opus":"Arch Enemy rage against animal testing, imagining a sterile lab of torment where creatures pay for so-called advancement with no say, discarded in bags. The singer wishes the abusers the same fate. The Swedish melodic death metal band aim their aggression as protest, screams hammering home a verdict of pointless brutality.","sonnet":"An indictment of laboratory animal testing — the subjects have no choice, no voice, and a plastic bag for a burial gown. The speaker finds the scientific justifications hollow, looks into the animals' eyes and sees the defense collapse, and wishes their fate on those who inflicted it.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~cult-of-chaos":{"haiku":"Chaos breeds creation through natural selection; rejecting divine tales, the masses form a godless collective bound by singular will. Life demands embracing anarchy rather than false order.","opus":"A Darwinian anti-religion anthem, chaos reframed as the true creative force and natural selection as the only honest law. It preaches godless freedom, tearing down prophets and propaganda so a new order can rise from the silence.","sonnet":"Chaos is reframed as natural and liberating — the sculptor of all life through natural selection — and the speaker rallies against false prophets and propaganda whose \"dead words\" will eventually meet the fangs of reality. The cult of chaos is godless anarchy, offered as the only honest way to live amid many ways to die.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~damnations-way":{"haiku":"A prisoner loses grip on reality and sanity, trapped and paranoid, living perpetually in damnation under another's shadow.","opus":"The Swedish melodic-death band voices a condemned sinner in chains, stripped of hope and counting down to the end as something new rises to replace him. Paranoia fills his cage, yet the beyond seems almost to welcome him, casting doom as both sentence and grim escape.","sonnet":"A prisoner — literal or metaphorical — watches time run out while paranoia and hopelessness erode any sense of self. The far side of the cage offers no relief; damnation is not a punishment assigned from outside but a path he has somehow always been walking.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~dark-insanity":{"haiku":"A person crawls through grief and burns with hate, unable to die or escape years of torment and anxiety. Memories consume them alive as they wrestle with dark madness.","opus":"Sweden's melodic-death standard-bearers give voice to a man consumed by regret and self-directed hatred, dwelling on years he squandered. Caught between wishing for an end and dreading death, he is eaten alive by his own memories until his mind gives way entirely.","sonnet":"Shame and self-recrimination accumulate into a state beyond ordinary grief. Crawling through the residue of past mistakes, the narrator is consumed by the memories themselves — they do not merely haunt but devour. The dark insanity named in the lyric is less madness than the exhaustion of perpetual self-torment.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~dark-of-the-sun":{"haiku":"The song depicts oppressive control and darkness winning over humanity through demagogues and dogmatic chaos; it calls for resistance against those who exploit and manipulate the masses.","opus":"A revolt against a false savior who peddles false hope to the desperate and manipulates the weak-willed, the resistance swearing to fight for their homeland as a blackened dawn breaks. Arch Enemy stage collective defiance against manipulation and rigid dogma, casting redemption and ruin as one and the same.","sonnet":"A cult leader or false messiah exploits the spiritually desperate — bloodsucking hope dealers control feeble minds, and the only salvation is self-reliance; the day will come when rebels walk together in the dark of the sun, having resisted the false promise.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~dead-bury-their-dead":{"haiku":"Someone sheds guilt and trauma, walking free into peace as the dead are left to bury themselves.","opus":"A brief, triumphant declaration of breaking free from guilt and old sins, leaving the past to rot while striding into a bright afterlife untethered by regret. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band, turn a biblical phrase into a lean mantra about refusing to carry yesterday's weight forward.","sonnet":"Release after a long carry — the scars are healing, the chains of guilt have no hold anymore. The speaker walks into Elysian Fields and lets the past bury itself. Spare and almost peaceful, it refuses to dwell on what was left behind, treating survival not as triumph but as simple forward movement: time marches on.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~dead-eyes-see-no-future":{"haiku":"Soldiers march toward pointless conflict, exhausted and depleted. Their sacrifice brings only despair and the question of whether redemption is possible after such violence.","opus":"Arch Enemy follow soldiers bleeding for a doomsday conflict whose reason has dissolved, tramping a road with no exit toward a win no different from ruin. The only return left to them is death and a faint hope of being pardoned. Swedish melodic death metal at its bleakest.","sonnet":"About soldiers returning from a war they no longer believe in — fighting for ravaged, worthless land with bloodstained memories and dead eyes, falling from whatever grace they once had, the victory they won smelling only of despair and the question of whether they'll ever be forgiven.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~dead-inside":{"haiku":"Betrayal and deception destroy a relationship, leaving the betrayed person emotionally hollowed out and recognizing they've been fundamentally deceived by someone they trusted.","opus":"Arch Enemy, then fronted by Angela Gossow's growl, turn a betrayal into scorched heartbreak. After the deception is exposed, isolation and painful truth surface, leaving the narrator an empty husk trapped in private torment. A bond that was once vital now lies dead and wrong, deceiver and deceived reduced to a ruined memory.","sonnet":"Betrayal by someone deeply trusted has left a person hollowed out, running on bitterness rather than feeling. What once was young and strong between two people has calcified into a private hell, and the truth — long deferred — has finally surfaced to confirm that everything is irreparably wrong.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~dead-inside-live-in-japan-99":{"haiku":"Betrayal shatters trust as deception unravels what once seemed solid; the speaker dwells in spiritual isolation, hollowed out by a lie sustained too long.","opus":"The wreckage after a trusted love is exposed as a lie — the narrator emptied out and quietly smoldering, everything once vital now spent. Both the betrayer and the one who believed are ruined by how long the rot was left to spread.","sonnet":"A speaker who believed in someone and was deceived emerges from that betrayal feeling hollow — \"a hollow shell in my own misery\" — looking back at a young, strong bond that decayed into universal wrongness. The refrain of \"so wrong, for so long\" traces the slow corrosion of trust rather than a single rupture.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~dehumanization":{"haiku":"Children are exploited and indoctrinated by manipulative systems designed to kill their spirits; infection and rejection define a generation without hope.","opus":"A furious indictment of an era that strips people of humanity, targeting manipulators who exploit the naive, engineer belief, and raise a doomed generation bred to be discarded or sacrificed. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band, forge social despair into an accusation against the forces manufacturing a disposable populace.","sonnet":"A generation raised on manipulation and neglect is condemned before it can resist — misled children shaped by forces serving only those forces. The cycle is framed as biological: infection, rejection, replacement by an even more disposable breed.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~demoniality":{"haiku":"Demonic feminine power and transgressive sexuality embody liberatory force; damnation becomes desirable transgression.","opus":"Collective dread at the edge of extinction, huddled souls facing a divine silence and the choice between eternal life or the end. It reads as a crisis of faith, God turning his back while the drowning search for any light to hide in.","sonnet":"Loneliness as existential condition: on fields of desolation, God has turned his back in divine resignation, and the speaker searches for sanctuary in a sea they're already drowning in — the uncertainty about eternal life or termination is not resolved, only witnessed, as weak and depraved figures journey too late to be saved.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~demoniality-instrumental":{"haiku":"Isolation and uncertainty consume existence as the narrator drowns in solitude and fear. The fields of desolation offer no sanctuary; only eternal life spent in collective doom.","opus":"Despite the title, a lyric of collective dread, humanity huddled on fields of desolation, doomed and searching for shelter after God turns his back. The recurring question is stark: eternal life or annihilation, with no comfort offered.","sonnet":"On desolate fields stripped of certainty, figures cry in each other's arms as they drown in darkness, pulled between belief in eternal life and the inevitability of termination. God has turned away in divine resignation, leaving only the solitude of the journey toward an end that cannot be known.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~demonic-science":{"haiku":"Transgressive genetic engineering where the weak are erased to birth a new superior form, regardless of moral cost.","opus":"Swedish melodic death-metal band Arch Enemy turn genetic engineering into a horror scenario, laboratory scientists rewriting human biology to resurrect an old fantasy of the perfect being. The narrator watches this obsessive quest strip away everything deemed weak and recoils from ambition so extreme it has stopped making sense, casting biotech overreach as the start of a grim new age.","sonnet":"Eugenics horror framed as scientific ambition — white-coated figures stripping away weakness and rewriting the human template in service of some ancient dream of perfection. The song names the price of this passion and doesn't flinch from the word demonic; the new dawn it describes is explicitly a nightmare dressed as progress.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~despicable-heroes":{"haiku":"Religious and political leaders exploit believers with false doctrine while consuming young minds and leaving them hollow.","opus":"A scathing attack on false prophets, politicians, and idols who peddle empty doctrine and feed on their gullible followers. The demand is to see through these hollow frauds, refuse to be devoured, and think for yourself instead of surrendering your judgment to charlatans.","sonnet":"A denunciation of political and religious leaders as wolves among sheep — preachers and rulers spew false dogma to exploit their followers, and the song demands that people stop hero-worshipping those who are simply manipulating them.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~diva-satanica":{"haiku":"A seductive evil woman uses beauty and temptation to lure people into doom, feeding on their blood and flesh. She is both alluring and destructive, using pleasure as a weapon.","opus":"Arch Enemy conjure a demonic seductress who is both alluring and monstrous, drawing worshippers to her before destroying them. The imagery casts fatal temptation as a Medusa-like predator who feeds on those she ensnares, deriving pleasure from their ruin.","sonnet":"A figure of supernatural feminine power lures the willing into destruction. Beautiful and merciless, she uses desire as the instrument of annihilation — drawing worshippers into her temple only to consume them. The song frames predatory seduction as something ancient and almost liturgical in its terrible precision.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~diva-satanica-live":{"haiku":"Seductive evil queen masters temptation through beauty and cruelty merged. The divine diva demands submission to dark priestess of sensual corruption.","opus":"A portrait of a satanic seductress — part monster, part vision, Medusa-like — who lures worshippers into her temple with limitless allure, then feasts on their blood and flesh. Her carnal enticement tears lovers apart, and she feeds on the sight of them perishing.","sonnet":"The Diva Satanica is a Medusa-figure — both beast and beauty, seducer and abuser — who lures the blinded victim into her temple with incense and carnal desire, then drinks their blood and feeds on their dying for her own pleasure.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~end-of-the-line":{"haiku":"Society is a circus where people exchange their souls for a better life while being manipulated and entertained. At the end, they realize they themselves became the fool in this cruel game.","opus":"A scathing address to a passive consumer hypnotized by media, fashion, and manufactured spectacle, told to check his brain at the door like a circus patron. The bitter twist is that the amused fool is the real clown, having traded his soul for the promise of a better life and wound up worse than dead.","sonnet":"About surrendering your autonomy to consumer culture and media spectacle — the audience is the clown in the end, having sold their soul for distraction and ending up worse than dead. It's contemptuous of willing self-deception.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~enemy-within":{"haiku":"Internal psychological destruction where depression and self-harm defeat the person from within; the beast wins through surrender.","opus":"A vivid dramatization of a mind at war with itself, a monster breaking loose from a sealed inner chamber to murmur suicidal thoughts and turn the body's defenses against it. The collapse accelerates until only a hollow husk is left, proving the deadliest foe lives inside. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band, stage mental breakdown as literal monster combat, all ferocity and descending melody.","sonnet":"Mental collapse narrated as a creature taking over from inside — the shadowbeast, patient and predatory, dismantles will and body until only a hollow shell remains. The self turns its own weapons inward; self-destruction isn't chosen so much as engineered by the thing that has always lived in the dark corners of the mind.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~eureka":{"haiku":"Existential breakthrough where the narrator discovers meaning through suffering and love's absence, seeking transcendence from monotony.","opus":"The Swedish melodic death metal band turn a word meaning discovery into something bitter. A narrator trapped by boredom and grief seeks meaning but finds only poison, tormented by a love that was never returned. When the great revelation finally comes, it lands as resignation rather than triumph, the answer worse than the question.","sonnet":"A drowning person reaching for transcendence — stumbling through life, drinking from poisoned wells, haunted by images of someone whose love never came back. The walls close in, the only wish is to escape this dimension of boredom entirely, and the discovery — whatever it is — arrives like a gasp of air after suffocation.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~exist-to-exit":{"haiku":"The narrator finds meaning through suicide and ritual death, seeking immortal fame through final exit. Oblivion becomes reincarnation into legend.","opus":"A grim glorification of suicide as a form of transcendence, treating self-destruction as ritual, art, and rebirth. The narrator imagines his own ending as a deliberate masterpiece that will leave a lasting mark and win him immortal fame remembered long after he is gone.","sonnet":"A glorification of suicide as artistic act and path to immortality — the speaker frames their planned death as performance and reincarnation, seeking post-mortem fame as an infamous legend rather than anonymous disappearance.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~fields-of-desolation":{"haiku":"Existential abandonment where humanity faces a choice between divine salvation and annihilation with no sanctuary available.","opus":"Arch Enemy closed their debut album with a widescreen vision of a doomed species, humanity clinging together in terror as a deity abandons it without explanation. The song keeps circling one question, whether anything comes after death, and refuses to pretend it has an answer, its twin-guitar melody carrying only honest uncertainty.","sonnet":"Collective despair on a spiritual and existential scale — people clinging to each other on a landscape abandoned by God, uncertain whether what awaits is eternity or annihilation. The song refuses resolution: the cold wind blows, darkness swallows, and the final admission is simply not knowing whether any of it ends or continues.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~heart-of-darkness":{"haiku":"Internal evil crystallized as identity; darkness inhabits and corrupts free will from within.","opus":"Evil personified as the voice living inside you, the dark subconscious that cannot be denied without denying yourself. The menace is that resistance only feeds it, the self slowly surrendering control to its own worst instinct.","sonnet":"The dark subconscious speaks in first person: it cannot be denied because denial is self-denial, it keeps the host awake, and when the facade crumbles the transition to full possession is still incomplete. Evil as intimate parasite, not external force.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~heart-of-darkness-live-in-paris-2004-bonus-track":{"haiku":"A dark subconsciousness inhabits the speaker, representing the evil within that cannot be denied or denied away. The duality between self and shadow becomes inescapable truth.","opus":"Arch Enemy give voice to the shadow self as an indestructible inner malevolence, a warped presence dwelling within the listener and needling them. As the outward mask falls away and the takeover finishes, the buried, sinister mind seizes command, a piece of psychological horror delivered with Angela Gossow-era intensity.","sonnet":"A dark internal voice insists it is not external but constitutive — a part of the self that cannot be denied without denying existence. It speaks from the subconscious, erodes free will, and completes its takeover by convincing the host that resistance and identity are the same illusion. Darkness is not shadow; it is substrate.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~i-am-legend-out-for-blood":{"haiku":"A predator stalks and hunts its victim with single-minded intent, using sensory domination and brute force. The song is framed as a relentless pursuit ending in destruction.","opus":"A relentless predator stalks its prey from behind, vowing to hunt it down, tear it apart, and crush its soul. The refrain fixates on a single hunger: being out for the victim's blood.","sonnet":"A relentless pursuer stalks a target with single-minded lethal intent, describing itself as harder than steel and driven by a lifelong will to kill. The song is a first-person declaration of predatory aggression with no stated motivation beyond the hunt itself.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~i-will-live-again":{"haiku":"Two people lost in coldness and chaos resolve to survive and transcend their shared painful past, facing tomorrow without fear.","opus":"Amid the death-metal onslaught, a survivor's declaration: whatever was lost and broken, tomorrow no longer frightens, and the pain can be left in the past. Rebirth insisted on rather than felt.","sonnet":"Emerging from the wreckage of a collapsed relationship — mutual loss, disorientation, faded memory of who they both were — the narrator refuses to let yesterday's pain define tomorrow, choosing survival and forward movement over grief.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~idolatress":{"haiku":"Trauma and self-destruction in a corrupted spiritual void; misery mixed with occult darkness and wicked desire.","opus":"A descent into self-imprisonment and sin, the narrator standing lost in gardens of anguish, hungry to cross a river of tears yet weaving her own web of despair. It reads as damnation embraced, misery tasted like salt, destiny felt as a blade at the neck. Arch Enemy pair this gothic torment with the ferocious clean-edged death metal that made them Swedish scene fixtures.","sonnet":"A figure standing in a garden of inner torment — lost, hungry, and unable to cross out of sorrow — caught between a self-imposed void that feels safe and a deeper anguish that imprisons. The imagery turns violent toward the end, with sinister eyes and cutting deep, suggesting the torment is both internal and inflicted from without.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~in-this-shallow-grave":{"haiku":"A prisoner without soul or pride dies for a lie—questioning whose god, master, or justice exists in this genocidal darkness.","opus":"The voice of someone imprisoned, tortured and left to die for a lie-a victim of genocide questioning why one man's god and devil should differ from another's; stripped of soul and pride in a darkened maze of screams, the throughline is the arbitrary cruelty of who is deemed worse and who is condemned.","sonnet":"About a person trapped, stripped of soul and pride, used as a pawn in a system built on lies — they die alone in a shallow grave having been sacrificed for causes that offered only genocide and servitude in return.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~instinct":{"haiku":"Disillusionment with civilization where evolved human instinct reveals only emptiness, pain, and the failure of meaning.","opus":"A misanthropic diagnosis of a numbed, self-made world quietly expiring, the narrator perceiving more but caring less and feeling only recurring pain. Humanity, once exalted, has degraded into a sorry parody of life led here by raw impulse. The Swedish melodic death metal band deliver the verdict in growls over gleaming, mournful leads.","sonnet":"Numbness as civilizational diagnosis — the speaker watching a world full of faces that all wear the same blank look, feeling nothing except the recurring pain that at least proves they're still alive. Instinct brought humanity here and instinct is all that's left; the chosen ones became a pathetic excuse for what they were supposed to be.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~lament-of-a-mortal-soul":{"haiku":"Existential meaninglessness where individual identity dissolves into forgotten dust; mortality and insignificance cause despair.","opus":"A bleak meditation on mortality and oblivion, the narrator creeping down a lightless tunnel and asking who he even is, answering only that he is dust in the wind, his name fading into eternal forgetting. It is existential dread stripped to nothing. Arch Enemy render this despair through Swedish melodic death metal, marrying philosophical emptiness to a sound of controlled, grieving aggression.","sonnet":"A meditation on existential erasure — the terror of being forgotten, of a name dissolving into oblivion, of being replaced without consequence. The speaker asks who they are and can only answer: dust in wind, a dying echo. The tone is not rage but hollow acceptance of meaninglessness.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~leader-of-the-rats":{"haiku":"Cynical critics hide behind anonymity to spit venom and tear down artists' lives while the artists thrive indifferent to judgment.","opus":"The Swedish melodic death metal group fire back at their critics, dismissing hostile reviewers and cynics as small-minded and beneath contempt. Rather than seek approval, the band plants a flag on its own artistic ground and dares detractors to keep coming, turning wounded pride into an anthem of open defiance and refusal to appease.","sonnet":"A contemptuous dismissal of anonymous critics who hide behind cynicism while producing nothing of their own. The band positions itself on a higher plane where art outlasts the sniping, finding comfort not in silencing detractors but in watching their irrelevance compound.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~let-the-killing-begin":{"haiku":"On death row, a condemned prisoner awaits execution, drowning in madness and torment as executioners prepare their final ritual.","opus":"Melodic death metal staged on death row, counting sleepless nights toward execution as the mind unravels into madness. It lingers on the condemned man's terror and confusion, death not as release but as the last stop of a slow psychological collapse.","sonnet":"A death-row prisoner's final hours — the condemned narrator counts down sleepless nights in a state of lunacy as the executioner approaches, experiencing a tunnel of madness before their death.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~machtkampf":{"haiku":"A person confronts someone who took everything they gave and never reciprocated, now reclaiming their power and breaking free from the endless cycle.","opus":"A power-struggle anthem of vengeance and depletion, the narrator finding cold new strength after giving everything to someone who only took. The German title frames it as a fight to break the chain and reclaim agency.","sonnet":"A reckoning with someone who took everything and never gave anything back — the narrator has poured themselves out, received nothing in return, and is now seizing power to break the cycle and reclaim their footing.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~mechanic-god-creation":{"haiku":"Humanity becomes obsolete and subjugated by its own machine creations, rendered irrelevant as artificial beings designed for destruction assume control.","opus":"Swedish melodic death metal band Arch Enemy imagine humanity destroyed by the machines it built, a doomsday intelligence that rises like a dark sunrise to conquer everything. People are rendered useless leftovers at the feet of their creations, dismissed as too warm and imperfect, in an apocalypse where engineered gods blot out the very light of the world.","sonnet":"Humanity has built its own successor — a mechanical god that rises to render the biological obsolete, too warm and too human to survive what it has made. The doomsday machine eclipses the sun and conquers by design, leaving the creators as relics at the feet of their own creation.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~my-apocalypse":{"haiku":"The narrator senses personal apocalypse approaching, consumed by morbid despair as hope fades and darkness closes in.","opus":"An inward collapse of long-buried emotions and fading hope, with distorted voices and dying dreams; the speaker feels a personal apocalypse and the end drawing near.","sonnet":"A sense of personal disintegration — suppressed emotions, fading hope, and whispering delusions — builds toward a feeling that one's own end is imminent and already felt approaching.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~nemesis":{"haiku":"A united legion of rebels fights against systemic hypocrisy and oppression, embodying anarchic resistance and collective strength.","opus":"A fist-in-the-air call to collective rebellion, casting the group as an avenging force united against corruption, tyranny, and a rigged system. The unifying idea is solidarity: everyone bleeds identically, so the many should band together to overthrow the powerful.","sonnet":"A collective of the oppressed declares itself a revolutionary force — united, anarchic, anti-system — and calls for freedom and the destruction of hypocrisy under the banner of Nemesis.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song assembles its insurgent collective by accumulation, drafting a vengeance goddess, a biblical legion, and a chant borrowed from the Musketeers until the many are welded into one named force. Its quiet turn arrives in the third verse, where blood imagery dissolves the barricade the choruses kept building: the enemy becomes the same substance as the mob, the avenging goddess curdling into an unrecognized mirror. Angela Gossow delivers every syllable at one flat pitch of fury, which is the point, because the anthem never hears the contradiction it just sang.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["anger & defiance","war & battle","violence & murder"],"means":"the avenger welded into a collective that cannot hear its own mirror","built":"the third-verse blood imagery dissolves the barricade the choruses kept building"},
"arch-enemy~night-falls-fast":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic darkness where nature's wrath burns away light and hope, leaving only the shadow of human corruption.","opus":"An apocalyptic tableau where nature turns against humanity and the sun itself dies out, leaving no mercy in a hell people built for themselves. The Swedish band turns extinction into grim spectacle, the melodic leads riding a prayer over the dead toward total loss.","sonnet":"Earth dying under the weight of what humans have done to it — poisoned air, blackened water, a sky the color of hatred, no angel willing to touch down in what mankind has made of the planet. The song watches the sun go out and finds no one to blame but the species watching it happen.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~no-gods-no-masters":{"haiku":"A rebel asserts independence and strength, resisting authority and building a better future through defiance.","opus":"A defiant anthem of self-determination, cornered but fighting back, insisting the mind is one's true home and liberty resides within, with no deity or ruler above a born rebel whose moment has arrived. Swedish melodic death-metal band Arch Enemy fuse self-reliant philosophy with the old anarchist slogan into a chest-out call to resist.","sonnet":"Defiance as self-definition — back against the wall, the speaker chooses their own path over any external authority, divine or political. The anthem has no grievance to resolve and no master to overthrow, only the declaration that this is who they are, that the rebel identity is the point in itself, and that unity with others who feel the same makes them unbreakable.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~pilgrim":{"haiku":"A zealous believer questions faith after a lifetime of violence and killing in religion's name.","opus":"Arch Enemy pit a doubter against a believer, insisting the faith in question has been traded away and its holy texts merely written by ordinary men. The devotee who once wounded and slew for the cross now looks back knowing nothing for certain. The Swedish band sharpen their long feud with organized religion into a study of belief souring into uncertainty.","sonnet":"A crisis of faith narrated from inside a life of violent service — the speaker killed and maimed in the name of a just God, followed the scriptures from cradle to grave, and now at the end looks back and doesn't know what any of it was for. Faith was bought and sold before the pilgrim ever arrived. The question of what they were really searching for has no answer left to give.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~pilgrim-live-in-japan-99":{"haiku":"From birth to death the soul wages endless struggle for transcendent truth. Humanity perpetuates cycles of faith and immorality; thousands follow the path their predecessors carved, bound to eternal recurrence.","opus":"A crisis-of-faith reckoning aimed at organized religion, the pilgrim told his belief was bought and sold. The devastating turn is the believer who killed and maimed for the cross, looking back and no longer knowing why.","sonnet":"A lifelong servant of faith addresses a pilgrim man whose devotion has been commodified — \"your faith is already bought and sold\" — and confesses that years of maiming and killing in God's name have eroded all certainty. The speaker ends suspended in doubt, no longer able to justify what they once accepted without question.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~ravenous":{"haiku":"A figure consumed by hunger for power, flesh, and spiritual conquest performs ritual slaughter to absorb the essence of victims. They invoke a blasphemous godhood through cannibalistic imagery.","opus":"A ravenous entity hunts a living person's soul, tearing the flesh and draining the blood to feed on it and become a god. It frames the ritual slaughter as a blasphemous, carnivorous act of taking another's life force.","sonnet":"A voice claims the power to seize and consume another person's soul and blood, casting itself as a carnivorous, god-like entity feeding on flesh through quasi-religious ritual. The imagery frames violent consumption as a path to divinity.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~reason-to-believe":{"haiku":"Warns against self-destruction; argues that even when alone, you have reason to endure.","opus":"Uncharacteristically direct for a melodic death band, a hand extended to someone drowning in self-hatred and the world's contempt. It's a survival pep talk aimed straight at the listener who feels misunderstood.","sonnet":"A direct address to someone trapped in self-hatred and misunderstanding — the speaker insists there is always a reason to keep fighting, that the prison of others' contempt cannot hold them, and that their story is far from over.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~revolution-begins":{"haiku":"The song calls for individual liberation and self-determination against systems designed to control and predefine fate, asserting that revolution against manipulation begins within each person. It centers on breaking free from external control.","opus":"A rallying call to break free from being a controlled cog in a machine, urging the listener to stand their ground and reject others dictating their fate because revolution begins within each person.","sonnet":"A call to resist the machinery of social control — rejecting the idea that fate is preordained or that one must play a part in the big machine — and insisting that revolution starts within each individual who chooses to stand their ground and break their mental chains.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~rise-of-the-tyrant":{"haiku":"A depiction of authoritarian rule, oppression, and the collapse of freedom as tyranny spreads and enslaves nations, ending in annihilation and warfare.","opus":"Opening with a god-emperor's proclamation, the song depicts the rise of a tyrant who enslaves nations under his control, bringing war, terror, and annihilation while the enemy lies within.","sonnet":"Opening with a monologue invoking the Roman emperor Caligula's godhood, the song depicts a world collapsing under tyrannical rule — fear, enslavement, annihilation, and war as populations lose their freedom to a rising totalitarian power.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~saints-and-sinners":{"haiku":"Religious hypocrisy labels people as saints or sinners, creating false moral hierarchies while actual understanding and empathy are impossible. The saint worships guilt while the sinner knows truth the saint cannot comprehend.","opus":"With Angela Gossow's ferocious growl leading, the Swedish melodic death metal band aim anti-religious scorn at the devout, portraying believers as willfully blind and enslaved by their own faith. The song throws the labels of holy and damned back in the pious listener's face, insisting they will never grasp the accusation being leveled at them.","sonnet":"A confrontation with institutional religion as a system of enforced blindness and shame — the altar demands prostration from people who were born seeing but trained not to look. The accused sinner refuses the framework entirely, exposing the saint-and-sinner binary as a tool of control that the pious will never understand from inside it.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~savage-messiah":{"haiku":"Charismatic violent leader rises to seduce the masses toward destruction; brutal redemption through aggression and blood.","opus":"Arch Enemy build a dark anthem to a terrifying god who rises reborn from ash, dispensing and seizing life while cutting down doubters as followers emerge anew from blood and loathing. The cry to be lifted higher frames devotion as rapturous submission to violence. The Swedish band make it soar around a demand for the faithful's souls.","sonnet":"A charismatic cult leader as predator — rising in glory, demanding souls, feeding on followers with aggression dressed as salvation. The crowd calls for him to take them higher while the song makes clear the taking is extraction, not transcendence; the immaculate deception is that the messiah is a taker of life.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~scream-of-anger-europe-cover":{"haiku":"A condemned person awaits execution, raging against injustice and unfinished life while facing inevitable death.","opus":"The last hours of a man awaiting execution, resigned to death yet seething at his accusers, his final thoughts drifting to a love he'll never return to. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band, cover this old Europe song, so the story comes from the original while they make its rage literal, screaming it in a far heavier register.","sonnet":"A condemned man's final hours, raging at the injustice of dying for something that was never fully his crime. He sees an evil place coming for him, knows he can't run, and spends his last breath not in prayer but in grief over the life and the person he's being torn away from.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~seed-of-hate":{"haiku":"Betrayal plants irremovable hatred within the narrator; abandonment transforms love into permanent poison and fundamental change.","opus":"A portrait of a man remade by betrayal, standing in a downpour that offers no comfort, viewing everything through a warped, bitter lens and hounded by memory, telling the one who abandoned him that they planted a hatred more durable than anything. Arch Enemy cut this in their early Johan Liva years, a private wound hardening into something cold and permanent.","sonnet":"The irreversible transformation that follows abandonment — a man who knows he's no longer who he was, changed at the root by someone who planted something bitter when they left. The wound doesn't fade; it grows stronger than anything else inside him, becoming the defining force of who he is now.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~shadows-and-dust":{"haiku":"Mortals trapped between conscience and fate grapple with their guilt and insignificance, fearing oblivion and wondering how the doomed will be remembered.","opus":"Arch Enemy meditate on mortality and shared guilt, likening humanity to castaways aboard a doomed vessel drifting toward nothingness, wondering who bears the blame and what legacy survives. The verdict is that everyone is equally fleeting and insubstantial. The Swedish band lends existential resignation a stately weight, framing the end as a collective, inevitable fate.","sonnet":"Humanity adrift on a dying world, accepting its fate without ever having seriously questioned the choices that led here. The dying ship of fools metaphor frames civilisation as a tragedy driven less by malice than by a collective failure of conscience.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~silent-wars":{"haiku":"People are trapped in cycles of hatred and betrayal, their wounds deepening while they believe in nothing, paralyzed and bleeding from invisible battles within.","opus":"A portrait of a couple locked in mutual loathing, a doomed fight that is destroying them both even as they choose to keep hurting each other. Escalating wounds and pleas for rescue cast the bond as a soundless war whose only exit is ending the suffering altogether.","sonnet":"About cycles of self-inflicted suffering: people choosing hatred and betrayal over healing, cutting themselves deeper with truth that burns, trapped in a conflict of their own making they can't escape.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~silverwing":{"haiku":"Romantic escapism where love offers freedom and timeless connection against the weight of mortality and mundane existence.","opus":"Arch Enemy turn surprisingly tender and uplifting, urging a listener never to surrender their pride to others and to pursue the dream they've left untouched before life slips away. Taking to the air stands for freedom, and love shines like a far-off, undying star. The melodic death metal band trade brutality for open-skied encouragement.","sonnet":"An invitation to fly free and refuse to let others conquer your pride — love as warmth, dreams as stars that never fade. Midway it wobbles, asking whether heaven has lost its power, but settles on surrendering to the drift: let the wheel spin, let the morning wash the moon away, and let life begin.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~silverwing-live-in-japan-99":{"haiku":"Dreams and freedom propel ascent as the speaker rejects constraints, trusting the journey itself to illuminate meaning beyond the weight of earthly demands.","opus":"A soaring charge to a companion: take flight, keep your dignity out of other people's hands, and treat love as a steady warmth that never dims. Even with the heavens gone cold and faithless, the call is to set out toward possibility anyway.","sonnet":"An invitation to transcendence through shared flight: the speaker promises freedom, warns that an unchased dream is a wasted life, and pairs earthly love with a star that will never fade. The second half turns cosmic — tasting the sour Milky Way, asking whether heaven has lost its power — before urging a sail on the sea of dreams where life can truly begin.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~sinister-mephisto":{"haiku":"An inner devil rules dreams as fantasy empire where the speaker becomes emperor over forbidden perversion; imagination unlocks desires and divine ceremony initiates dark orgy beyond moral restraint.","opus":"Melodic death metal about retreating into a private fantasy world where the powerless become emperors of forbidden pleasure. It's escapism as Faustian bargain, indulging in imagination what reality denies.","sonnet":"The narrator retreats into a dark fantasy self where, as an alter-ego ruler of sin, every forbidden sexual desire can be indulged without the limits of mortal life.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~skeleton-dance":{"haiku":"The speaker watches death personified as a skeleton dance on their own grave, trapped in liminal space between birth and grave, voiceless and unseen.","opus":"A dissociating narrator drifts toward death across barren land, watching his own burial as if from outside himself. The gutting twist is that he never realized he had died, and only that knowledge would have let him grieve the life he lost.","sonnet":"A first-person meditation on dying — the narrator drifts through a landscape of shadow and boundlessness, watching their own grave, and reflects that not knowing they were already dead was the only thing preventing mourning.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~slaves-of-yesterday":{"haiku":"People remain trapped in nostalgia and the past, unable to see the present or future. They are cursed to be left behind while spirits wander lost.","opus":"A melodic-death sermon against nostalgia as a prison, aimed at people who worship a vanished past instead of facing the present. The stance is contemptuous but almost pitying: clinging to what's gone is a kind of blindness that keeps you among the walking dead.","sonnet":"People who cling to a comfortable past while the world moves on become ghosts — blind to the new sunrise, forever lost, cursed to walk among the living without belonging, monuments to dreams that already died.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~stigmata":{"haiku":"Sacred wounds and marked suffering identify the chosen or cursed; religious trauma imprints on flesh and spirit.","opus":"Arch Enemy plunge into private fantasy as a domain of absolute command, the mind acting out taboo desires it can't reach in real life. It's about fleeing helplessness by crowning oneself sovereign of an inner realm of vice, drunk on that shadowy, total power.","sonnet":"A fantasy of absolute dominion played out in the mind's darkest corner: the speaker becomes ruler of a kingdom of sin, master of a divine perversion that real life cannot permit — the \"sinister Mephisto\" alter ego is not troubled by its own excess but revels in it, the ultimate high being the power to dictate desire itself.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~taking-back-my-soul":{"haiku":"A prisoner breaks free from mental infection and control. Taking back becomes reversal—the tables finally turn.","opus":"Reclaiming identity from someone who processed, diluted, and injected hate into the mind, the tables now turned; a defiant recovery of the self that was nearly erased.","sonnet":"About reclaiming selfhood after being systematically broken down and depersonalised by someone — the narrator was scattered, tranquilised, hate-injected, and made unrecognisable, but the game is now exposed and the soul is being taken back by force.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~taking-back-my-soul-live":{"haiku":"Escape from psychological manipulation and evil domination requires brutal recognition of the abuse and a fight to reclaim stolen selfhood.","opus":"Reclaiming the self from someone who spent years scrutinizing, medicating, and hollowing it out. The turning of the tables is the moment the manipulation is finally seen for what it was.","sonnet":"Someone who was eroded, manipulated, and psychologically poisoned by another person finally sees through it, turns the tables, and reclaims their sense of self.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~tears-of-the-dead":{"haiku":"A soldier haunted by complicity in atrocities, unwillingly drafted into genocide, now old and tormented by hollow eyes and blood-soaked hands.","opus":"The confession of an old man who was a young soldier in a genocidal regime, hiding behind the excuse of being only a pawn. The song's weight is his failed evasion: the hollow eyes of the dead haunt his sleep and judge him past any pardon.","sonnet":"Told from the perspective of an aging former soldier who served in what the song frames as an atrocity-filled regime — he tells himself he was just a pawn, but the dead come to him in dreams and his hands feel permanently stained, and the song refuses to let him off the hook.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~the-book-of-heavy-metal":{"haiku":"The speaker aspires to devil's covenant for immortality through metal's unholy oath. Authenticity becomes question mark shadowing devotion to extreme art.","opus":"A knowing, self-mocking anthem about wanting immortality through metal itself, trading a normal life of house and family for a place in the genre's mythology. It celebrates devotion to the music while winking at the wannabe hunger driving it.","sonnet":"A true believer's consecration: the narrator would sign with the devil for immortality, wears leather and studs as sacred dress, renounces houses, kids, and widescreen TVs — the heavy metal gods are the only religion, and the book of heavy metal is the only record that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~the-day-you-died":{"haiku":"Grief for a lost child or innocent overwhelms the narrator; they mourn failure and absence in a hostile world that offered no protection.","opus":"A lament heavy with grief and guilt for an innocent life ended too soon in a brutal, uncaring world. The narrator still senses the departed within him, admits he let them down, aches at their absence, and treats the date of their death as a wound that never stops resounding.","sonnet":"A grief song addressed to someone who died young and violently in a hostile world — the narrator carries guilt for failing them, and the loss echoes through everything, the tears gone dry from the weight of it.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~the-first-deadly-sin":{"haiku":"Lust and desire as inevitable corruption where passion inevitably transforms love into destruction and damnation.","opus":"A hymn to lust as damnation, entering a ring of flame where craving lights the dark and desire inevitably rots into loathing, cold, and hell, with no middle ground, only the plunge. The old forbidden pull takes hold and the descent is embraced. Arch Enemy dress this parable of illicit appetite in polished Swedish melodic death metal, sin made gorgeous and doomed.","sonnet":"Lust framed as a descent into hell that the speaker cannot resist — fire, desire, ancient taboo, and a demon that takes over the soul. Love and heat curdle inevitably into hate and ice, yet the fall into forbidden pleasure is framed almost approvingly; the sin is presented as fate, not regret.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~the-great-darkness":{"haiku":"Spiritual possession and disillusionment consume a soul, leaving emptiness and regret with no peace or purpose.","opus":"A suffocating slide into despair, a mind gripped by immobilizing fear and hollow numbness, loathing the light, feeling lifeless within, a see-through phantom drifting far from who it hoped to become and never at peace. Latin invocations frame it like a dark ritual. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band, lend their melodic ferocity to this account of a soul devoured.","sonnet":"Dissolution from the inside — the speaker's soul has been consumed by a darkness they followed willingly, mistaking a shadow for light. Now they're transparent, ghost-like, unable to locate peace or any sense of belonging. The Latin invocations frame what is essentially a portrait of spiritual emptiness: a person who took the wrong path and ran out of self before reaching anywhere.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~the-immortal":{"haiku":"Eternal life is torment—immortals breed endlessly while time stands still and the pursuit of youth becomes a curse of endless suffering.","opus":"A grim reversal of the deathless-life fantasy: undying beings ceaselessly multiplying and gripping existence past its natural span discover that unending survival, not the grave, is the real agony, stripping away human worth and turning perpetual being into a creeping horror.","sonnet":"Eternal life is presented not as a gift but as a curse — immortals trapped in a joyless, undead existence, breeding endlessly while the future stays locked away and the fountain of youth delivers only hollow suffering rather than meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~the-immortal-live":{"haiku":"Immortality extracts endless price through cyclical suffering; borrowed time sustains while rage corrupts ancient truths against nature's design.","opus":"Immortality reimagined as a curse, endless life draining meaning until eternity itself becomes the thing killing you. The imagery of undead spectacle and a fountain of youth turns the dream of never dying into a slow horror.","sonnet":"Immortality is a curse rather than a gift: living on borrowed time and desperately avoiding fate, the undying are left breeding in vacant galleries while human dignity is dethroned; the only truth at the fountain of youth is that eternal life is what's killing them.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~the-immortal-live-in-japan-99":{"haiku":"Euphoria masks a terrible bargain: immortality demands perpetual payment and suffering. The narrator trudges through halls of tortured souls, stripped of dignity, forever trapped in unending time.","opus":"Arch Enemy's melodic death metal reframes immortality as a curse — endless life leaching away meaning until deathlessness becomes its own slow dying. Eternal youth reads less as a gift than a sentence.","sonnet":"Immortality is rendered as a curse rather than a gift: the undead ride in vacant triumphal chariots while human dignity is dethroned and the fountain of youth yields only tears — \"eternal life is killing me.\" Time standing still while immortals keep breeding becomes an image of stagnation disguised as victory.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~the-last-enemy":{"haiku":"A warrior pursues a final quest to vanquish death itself, offering immortal transcendence through defiance and resurrection beyond the physical realm.","opus":"Arch Enemy cast death itself as the ultimate opponent to be beaten. The song is a defiant vow of resurrection: rather than accept decay and the grave, the singer treats dying as a battle to win, promising to rise again and refusing to surrender to mortality.","sonnet":"A warrior confronts death itself as the final enemy — not with resignation but with defiance, racing through corridors of annihilation and refusing to surrender. The promise of rising again after mortal dissolution gives the quest its shape: dying is not defeat but the threshold of something beyond it.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~the-oath-kiss-cover":{"haiku":"Spiritual devotion and transformation where the narrator surrenders to ancient power, becoming reborn as a servant of fate.","opus":"A knightly vow rendered as transformation, the narrator forged like a sword and swearing eternal service, passing through an ancient door where a boy enters and a man returns. It thrills at surrender to destiny, feeling suddenly, fiercely alive. This is Arch Enemy covering Kiss, the Swedish death metal band amplifying the original's fantasy of loyalty and rebirth into something heavier and more urgent.","sonnet":"A vow of absolute fealty — the speaker is forged like a blade, enters an ancient door, and emerges transformed. Swearing glory and service to a power they cannot name but feel completely, they surrender to history with the sudden exhilaration of someone who has found their purpose and cannot turn back.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~the-world-is-yours":{"haiku":"Empowerment through defiance where mental strength and rising consciousness allow breaking free from oppressive power structures.","opus":"A call to shed imposed authority and manufactured consent, rejecting institutions and hollow promises that protect the existing order, and insisting any power structure built on the backs of the oppressed can be toppled once you take control from within. Alissa White-Gluz and the Swedish band make it a rallying cry for justice and collapse.","sonnet":"A call to seize power through clarity of mind rather than brute force — reject the schools, the rules, the empty promises that prop up the status quo. Every empire was built on the backs of the subjugated, and can fall just as fast. The anger here is cold and strategic: walk away from the cage, then claim everything.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~thorns-in-my-flesh":{"haiku":"Betrayal by a trusted deceiver breaks emotional bonds; freedom only arrives through painful removal of their influence.","opus":"A song about breaking free of a manipulator, the narrator laying out a smooth-talking deceiver's tangle of betrayals before announcing she has torn loose and cast off the shackles. What once cost her trust and innocence is behind her, and she now steers her own fate. The Swedish band wrap this hard-won self-reclamation in defiant melody.","sonnet":"Liberation from a manipulator — someone who concealed the truth behind a silver tongue and built an intricate web of lies while everyone else paid the cost. The turn in the final third is decisive: the thorn is pulled out, the chain drops, and the speaker claims their own destiny back rather than ending in renewed accusation.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~through-the-eyes-of-a-raven":{"haiku":"War crimes witnessed by a bird survivor; violence so brutal that only the raven remembers and judges humanity's atrocities.","opus":"A cold reckoning on war seen from a scavenger bird's vantage, the lone survivor of a massacre that leaves the corpses frozen in the snow. The narrator asks only that carrion birds attend his own death. Arch Enemy channel Swedish melodic death metal toward vengeance and shame worn like a bloody crown.","sonnet":"A raven witnesses massacre and carries the memory of genocide across time — bodies, severed bones, blood and snow. The speaker identifies with the raven's perspective: see the slaughter clearly, remember it, and let that remembrance outlast the proud and the brave who destroyed each other for a crown layered with thorns.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~transmigration-macabre":{"haiku":"Karmic reincarnation as punishment where past cruelty ensures eternal suffering across spiritual and physical realms.","opus":"Swedish melodic death metal band Arch Enemy turn reincarnation into a nightmare rather than a comfort, imagining a soul condemned to be reborn endlessly and to harvest the consequences of its past sins. Damnation and decay await in each new life, so rebirth becomes not deliverance but an unbroken sentence of torment.","sonnet":"A condemnation that reads like a curse — someone trapped in pain and horror, their life flashing before them, addressed with cold finality: you've had your share, now transmigrate, now reap the twisted fruit of what you sowed. The song positions itself as judge and executioner of a soul it considers beyond saving.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~under-black-flags-we-march":{"haiku":"Revolutionary armies rise up to burn corrupt empires through unified rebellion and suffering-fueled resistance.","opus":"A militant summons to revolt, converting rage into fuel for uprising as crowds mass to topple corrupt power. The Swedish melodic-death band channels its aggression into revolutionary theater, the raised banner serving as emblem of defiant, burn-it-all insurrection.","sonnet":"Revolution as collective heat — the more suffering, the more fuel; the more corruption in power, the more inevitable the collapse of those empires. The song is less about a specific cause than the pure momentum of organised resistance, fists up, legions in motion, the street already burning. It ends mid-sentence, as if the march is still going.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~vultures":{"haiku":"Psychological devastation where inner demons circle like predatory birds, tearing the narrator's heart apart until death.","opus":"A harrowing image of being devoured while still alive, foes turned to prey and scavengers circling with patient hunger as the narrator, trapped in hell, hunts for a heart already eaten. The more he offers, the more they seize, hollowing him out. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band, render this parasitic torment with soaring twin leads, a soul certain it will eventually be consumed.","sonnet":"Being consumed by those who feed on vulnerability — circling presences waiting with patient hunger, the sky darkened by heavy wings, the speaker's heart slowly devoured while voices scream in the silence. The more they gave, the more was taken, until nothing was left inside; the vultures are both literal image and emotional truth.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~war-eternal":{"haiku":"Life as constant conflict where survival demands choosing sides; hunters become prey in endless cycles of violence.","opus":"A defiant survival anthem framing life as endless combat, kill or be killed, but insisting you remember who you are when they try to crush and control you. The hunter, it warns, will one day become the prey. Arch Enemy built this around Alissa White-Gluz, whose arrival marked a new chapter for the Swedish band, and it channels their aggression into fierce self-possession.","sonnet":"Survival in a world that constantly tries to reshape you into something manageable — the battlefield of life where allegiances shift and the hunter eventually becomes the prey. The emotional core is resistance: when everything pushes you down, dig inside, remember who you are, and refuse to be controlled.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~warning":{"haiku":"Propaganda machine keeps populations ignorant and poisoned; truth is suppressed while lies maintain control.","opus":"A blunt caution against manipulators who peddle deceit and propaganda from a safe remove, keeping people ignorant and warping their minds while pretending to inform them. Arch Enemy, the Swedish melodic death metal band, hammer the message into a chant: distrust the voices that seek to control how you think.","sonnet":"A stripped-down alarm about manufactured consent — outsiders who pretend authority keeping the masses in the dark, feeding propaganda, poisoning thought. The repetition is the point: this is what indoctrination sounds like, cycling the same lies until they become the furniture of the mind.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~we-will-rise":{"haiku":"Declares rebellion against mediocrity and conformity, positioning the narrator as an antidote to standardized thinking. The song celebrates standing apart and rising above the masses.","opus":"An empowerment anthem that urges people to reject conformity and mediocrity, believe in their own potential, and refuse to settle for second-best. The narrator positions himself as both threat and cure, rallying everyone to shake off numbness and lift themselves above the herd.","sonnet":"A rejection of mediocrity and conformity, with the speaker positioning themselves as both enemy of the status quo and antidote to it, calling on a collective to rise above a sea of sameness and assert individual identity and worth.","src":"haiku","fable":"A defiance anthem that names conformity itself as the target: the world it pictures is a grey uniform crowd, interchangeable faces settling for whatever is handed to them, and the only escape is refusing that flattening. The turn is that the speaker accepts the villain label and reframes it as a remedy — the one branded a threat is also the cure for all that sameness, menace and healing housed in the same body. That singular voice then swells into a shared one for the climb, so ascent means not solitary escape but a mass verdict, everyone deciding at once they answer to no fixed shape, driven home by Angela Gossow's Gothenburg melodic-death roar until the vow hardens into fact through sheer repetition.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"villain label reframed so menace and cure share the same body","built":"singular speaker swells to collective voice as ascent becomes a mass verdict"},
"arch-enemy~web-of-lies":{"haiku":"Relationship destruction through accumulated lies and false intimacy; trust decays despite shared suffering and codependency.","opus":"Arch Enemy chart a couple bound together by deceit, clinging to a relationship that has decayed into mutual suspicion yet neither will abandon. The Swedish band bounce accusations back and forth between the partners, framing the whole affair as a doomed game whose collapse leaves the pair sharing a private ruin they built themselves.","sonnet":"A relationship destroyed by accumulated dishonesty — both people caught in it, both complicit, the mutual decay visible in each other's eyes. The song cycles through phases and stages without resolution because the game has gone too far: their shared temple of false dreams has already come down.","src":"haiku"},
"arch-enemy~yesterday-is-dead-and-gone":{"haiku":"People rebel against control and oppression, breaking chains to fight for freedom at any cost. The song celebrates reckoning and defiance against a world of lost compassion.","opus":"Arch Enemy strike a forward-facing note of defiance, torching what's behind them and rattling their confinement in demand of release. Beneath an ominous sky they insist on freedom from a merciless world and declare a day of reckoning, choosing to stand amid the destruction and let the past finally perish.","sonnet":"Collective revolt against control burns the past deliberately. Bridges are set alight, cages rattled, chains broken — and tomorrow is claimed as a name rather than a date. The song treats liberation as both physical confrontation and declaration: what was endured is over, and the reckoning has arrived.","src":"haiku"},
"archers~bitter":{"haiku":"Numbness fails to arrive as emotional wreckage accelerates; bones break under accumulated weight. The speaker fades away while trapped in escalating hurt, questioning how much longer endurance remains possible.","opus":"Depression rendered as numb free-fall, wanting to stop feeling and half-welcoming the collapse. Guilt keeps him awake, and the bitter memories are all that's left, the fear being not the fall but the sudden stop.","sonnet":"Someone coming undone under guilt and emotional numbness admits they sold their soul for something as trivial as a cigarette, and now the only things left are bitter memories and sleeplessness. The question of whether it's the fall or the sudden stop that kills is left open, suggesting a mind teetering on the edge of surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"archetypes-collide~parasite":{"haiku":"A parasitic lie rewires the mind, transforming trust into self-hatred and safety into prison. The infection reaches critical mass when resistance becomes impossible and the host bleeds from the inside out.","opus":"An abuser figured as a parasite already loose in the bloodstream, having planted a lie that promised safety while infecting the mind, warping dreams and turning the speaker against himself. Held captive by the hurt and convinced he was at fault, he realizes the moment to cut the thing out has already passed.","sonnet":"A manipulative relationship is rendered as biological invasion: false words got into the bloodstream, rewired the narrator's self-perception, and left them holding blame that isn't theirs — \"twisted lines infected my mind\" is the hinge the whole song turns on.","src":"haiku"},
"architects-winston-mccall~impermanence":{"haiku":"Humans choose suffering despite awareness of inevitable doom; once the fuse ignites, escape becomes impossible. The reaper takes the best while the masses sink beneath concrete, blind to the writing on the wall.","opus":"A meditation on mortality that indicts our refusal to face it, everyone a player in a doomed symphony fighting the one thing that can't be beaten. The hard truth: those too afraid to die never actually live.","sonnet":"Humanity resists death — \"barricaded 'cause nobody wants to meet their maker\" — while riding torpedoes and washing into overflow, each life a cameo ending in two. The central tension is that those afraid to die never truly live, and the symphony of existence keeps fighting impermanence again and again to no final resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~a-match-made-in-heaven":{"haiku":"Complicity and violence spiral as those in power breed collateral damage for their ambitions, sowing seeds that return as retribution against their doorstep.","opus":"A scorching indictment of those who profit from war and dress atrocity as revolution, telling tyrants to look in the mirror. Written in the band's politically charged late period, it treats collateral damage as deliberate policy, not accident.","sonnet":"A collective indictment of warmongering leaders whose engineered conflicts create collateral devastation — destroyed homes, murdered families, and civilian death — all dismissed as part of a calculated plan. The speaker confronts these architects of violence directly, naming them as the origin of every atrocity and promising that retribution, not revolution, is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~a-new-moral-low-ground":{"haiku":"Moral bankruptcy and sinister hypocrisy; descent into depths of ethical collapse.","opus":"A bitter portrait of moral decline, fighting to defend a bottom that keeps dropping while sinners hold the weapons. The counting refrain tallies the cost of eroding humanity, the narrator cut to the bone by a world sliding downward.","sonnet":"A sardonic countdown — one love for your enemy, two steps from sanity, three strikes, six feet — cataloging the costs of trying to fight for a moral low ground in a world where the sinners hold the arrows; the grit grinding under the teeth is the only honest resistance available against a game that cuts close to the bone.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~all-the-love-in-the-world":{"haiku":"Collective apathy poisons transcendence; machines replace meaning while potential withers under willful amnesia and cynicism.","opus":"Written in the shadow of guitarist Tom Searle's death, this wrestles with disillusion and depleted hope, asking whether there's anything left worth singing about. The strained question about love in the world is a fight to keep meaning against numbness.","sonnet":"Social apathy, digital disconnection, and collective amnesia about human potential are laid out against a backdrop that 'never rains, it's always fucking torrential'; the speaker struggles with whether there is anything left worth singing about while asserting that humanity impossibly emerged from the dark and could still matter.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~an-open-letter-to-myself":{"haiku":"A speaker reflects on broken friendships and absence, wishing they had found words to prevent the loss.","opus":"A remorse-soaked reckoning that opens by pinning blame on a friend, then flips the charge onto the singer himself, who admits he was the one who broke faith and was never there, wishing hopelessly he could undo it. The Brighton metalcore band specialize in this raw self-address, the letter framing acting as both confession and mirror.","sonnet":"Accountability delivered too late — the speaker was always absent, always wearing a face, never present enough to hold the trust they were given. The shift from third to first person midway is the confession: they were the so-called friend, the one who never showed up. They wish they'd found the words to save it, and they know they didn't.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~animals":{"haiku":"Humans are depicted as animals afraid of their own capacity for destruction, caught in a cycle of decay and choosing self-annihilation.","opus":"The British metalcore band, writing through years of grief, wrestle with despair and self-destruction, calling humanity a bunch of frightened animals. The recurring question of whether to pull the pin voices suicidal weariness, yet the diamond in the mud and the unburnable cathedral hint at something worth surviving for.","sonnet":"The gap between human self-regard and actual behaviour becomes unbearable. Nothing accumulates to meaning, everything collapses again, and the narrator circles a single question — whether to withdraw entirely — while holding to the image of something buried and unburnable at the bottom of the wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~be-very-afraid":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic resignation and broken spirit pervade a world where revelation brings no redemption and suffering finds no end.","opus":"Architects channel climate dread and political fatalism into a furious broadside: the conviction that catastrophe is coming, that those in power will act only when it profits them, and that ordinary complicity greases the whole slide. Rage at collective inaction.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of a civilization too broken, greedy, and spiritually exhausted to fight for anything meaningful — the world is burning and no one cares as long as they get paid, so apocalypse is both inevitable and deserved.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~black-lungs-abbey-road-version":{"haiku":"Environmental collapse and collective complicity doom civilization; the young suffocate from inherited destruction while the powerful cloak devastation in spiritual language.","opus":"An apocalyptic reckoning with climate collapse and complicity, asking what we'd do as the planet burns and indicting the powerful hoarding gold. The band frame inaction as guilt we'll all have to answer for when the phoenix's wings are clipped.","sonnet":"An urgent ecological and political indictment of a world poisoning its young while its leaders sleepwalk — asking what it would take for people to act before the planet burns, and mourning the clipping of phoenix wings by those who profit from inaction.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~born-again-pessimist":{"haiku":"Comfort breeds delusion and false superiority; the speaker runs red lights without consequence. Sleep offers brief escape from a superficial performance constructed for the world's entertainment.","opus":"Sam Carter's self-portrait of a man who won't open up, hiding behind cynicism and a coping crutch he's grown to love. The bravado masks a hollowed-out interior, the pessimist protecting a big empty heart he can't fill.","sonnet":"A self-described born-again pessimist navigates emotional shutdown and a habit of avoidance — \"I love this crutch\" — while the sun waits for their frozen arteries to thaw and they drift toward Heaven's gate with a deliberately empty heart. The refrain blesses that emptiness rather than mourning it, making self-destruction into an almost sardonic stance.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~brain-dead":{"haiku":"Addiction to sensation numbs the conscience: chasing highs becomes the path to emptiness, wrapped in the language of transcendence but delivering only spiritual death.","opus":"Numbing yourself into willful oblivion amid apocalypse and hypocrisy, choosing to be brain dead rather than face the terror. It skewers the collective self-sedation of a doomed, ego-driven world.","sonnet":"A song about collective willful ignorance and self-destruction — humanity is too high, too hypocritical, and too complicit in its own demise to change course, and the speaker is both disgusted by and part of the system they condemn.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~btn":{"haiku":"Addiction grips someone with constant craving that returns relentlessly despite absence, trapping them in cycles of neediness with no hope of permanent escape.","opus":"The British metalcore band renders addiction as a nagging compulsion the narrator cannot shake, likening the pull to a physical craving that runs endlessly through his thoughts. Alone and uneasy in himself, he begs to be dragged down further, chasing a relief that never comes.","sonnet":"Obsession with someone absent grips a person like an addiction — a constant, gnawing need that no amount of distance or time can loosen. Sleepless and sinking, he cannot find peace inside his own mind, and the longing circles back relentlessly no matter how long he has been away.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~colony-collapse":{"haiku":"Ecological catastrophe unfolds as consequence of human delusion and refusal to heed warnings; collapse becomes the reckoning for collective hubris.","opus":"Ecological dread framed as a war already lost, the dying-bee metaphor standing in for a species that ignored every warning. Architects' grief here is that we handed ourselves the noose and called our own ruin progress.","sonnet":"A reckoning with collective environmental destruction: humanity is depicted as having brought catastrophe upon itself through arrogance and refusal to heed warnings, the ocean on fire and extinction approaching while voices cry out asking whether it is too late to begin again.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~curse":{"haiku":"Paralyzed by paradox: the pursuit of paradise traps the speaker in regret and counting, while freedom and achievement ring hollow against the weight of lost time and moments. Heaven becomes a curse when measured against human cost.","opus":"Grief and doubt over whether paradise or heaven was ever real, the narrator counting his losses and finding that even hope arrives with a curse. It sits in the tension between wishing for the worst and still hoping to stay afloat.","sonnet":"A person paralysed by the gap between the paradise they sold themselves on and the mounting weight of days crossing off, confessing that even in exhaustion they still wish for the worst — freedom framed as a curse delivered by heaven itself.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~day-in-day-out":{"haiku":"Years of effort can be destroyed instantly, leaving nostalgia for better days and questioning why one remains trapped in a joyless present.","opus":"The speaker grieves for happier times now gone and feels marooned in a callous world where no one looks out for him, aching to reclaim those days before every trace of joy fades and pushing others to stop dwelling and inhabit the present instead.","sonnet":"A lament for a sense of purpose and vitality that has quietly drained away — the narrator feels stranded in a joyless present, mourning days that once held colour and meaning, and wondering how they ended up in a world of hollow sympathy with no one watching over them.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~daybreak":{"haiku":"As daylight breaks, chains are revealed and humanity sheds inherited oppression and controlled thinking. The speaker rejects blind obedience and demands evidence and personal freedom in a rational awakening.","opus":"An anthem of shedding religious dogma and reclaiming reason, framing organized faith as oppression and doubt as liberation, with dawn a metaphor for the intellectual awakening that finally breaks the chains.","sonnet":"A rejection of religion and superstition in favor of rational thought and evidence — the narrator refuses to bow to any authority without proof, worshipping instead at the altar of reason. The daybreak metaphor is the moment collective human intelligence finally sheds the weight of inherited oppression.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~dead-butterflies":{"haiku":"Loss of self and the decay of dreams unfulfilled. The speaker feels forgotten and overshadowed, lost in a cloud of failed beauty and broken transformation.","opus":"Architects grapple with self-erasure, a battered narrator asking only to exist and pass on in peace as everything gentle drifts out of reach. Written under the shadow of guitarist Tom Searle's death, the band's grieving metalcore uses a bloom that never opens to stand for a self repeatedly misplaced.","sonnet":"A person drifts through loss of self — doves gone, ravens permanent, beauty never arrived. Surrounded by dead potential and a smile full of damage, they search for peace in living and dying, suspended in a cloud of things that never bloomed.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~deep-fake":{"haiku":"Surrender to illusion and false control; playing along with manipulation while pretending knowledge.","opus":"A rage at tyrants and profiteers who sell everyone out for gold while ordinary people just want to exist. The title names the betrayal: manufactured lies and hollow power, the many hiding like shadows from those hoarding control.","sonnet":"Power hoarding as civilizational betrayal — tyrants filling their fists with gold, selling the ark for parts, building an abyss while the rest of us just want to exist. The deep fake of the title names the information war: facts are dismissed, bleeding is real and ignored, and the only counter is deciding to be the change while acknowledging how risky and insufficient that is.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~delete-rewind":{"haiku":"Questions how youth lost their voice after being backed into corners by oppressive systems since childhood. The song calls for erasing corrupted beliefs and reclaiming agency.","opus":"An early Architects call to a generation raised on distrust and inherited decay, begging for something worth believing in. The frustration is directed inward too, at a youth that stayed silent and let itself be shaped, asking when it will finally stand.","sonnet":"A song about a generation that has been misled and silenced by those in power, expressing frustration at having been pushed into a world they cannot trust and calling for a collective decision to reclaim agency before it is too late.","src":"haiku","fable":"Architects’ melodic-turn single works as generational grievance: a cohort told from birth that the future was already spoiled reaches for the tape-and-console fantasy of the title, the wish to wipe a corrupted save and reload a self that was never contaminated to begin with. The apocalypse the song keeps invoking is deliberately secondhand and threadbare, something inherited rather than felt, and the lyric all but yawns at it, treating collapse as a tired rumor and barricaded houses as a script handed down by the same authorities it distrusts. The real movement is the pivot from blame aimed outward at them toward a scalding self-accusation, the chorus curdling its demand for defiance into a question about how this generation let itself be robbed of its own voice, so the refusal to act becomes the confession the song is actually making.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"generational blame curdling into self-accusation over a voice surrendered","built":"the chorus demand for defiance becomes a confession of complicit silence"},
"architects~devil-s-island":{"haiku":"A city crumbles from violence and neglect; the singer questions people's calls for change when their own anger breeds only more destruction and silence.","opus":"A furious address to a homeland turned inward on itself, watching broken cities on the news while apathy festers. The band indicts a public that mistakes loud grievance for a real voice and blames the decay it helped create.","sonnet":"A lament for a deteriorating homeland — streets once loved are now broken and violent, and the narrator accuses those who claim to want a voice while their protests turn indistinguishable from the destruction they rail against.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~early-grave":{"haiku":"Death stalks the narrator while they resist surrender through deliberate living; the refusal to give in defines existence.","opus":"Self-destruction chosen over surrender, a narrator who can no longer tell night from day yet refuses death's call. Written amid the band's grief over guitarist Tom Searle, it is defiance wrestling with the easy pull toward the grave.","sonnet":"A speaker caught in a state between exhaustion and defiance refuses death's pull even as his own choices drag him toward an early grave — stubbornly choosing the harder path over the easy surrender of oblivion.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~everything-ends":{"haiku":"Isolation and futility compound as the speaker drowns in internal warfare while circumstances deteriorate. Acceptance of inevitable ending becomes the only remaining agency.","opus":"Architects put the speaker adrift and going under, waging a hopeless fight with despair while crying out for anyone to hear, pinning the wrongness on the sky itself. The title refrain doubles as menace and grim solace, numbing the ache as he shifts with the calendar and longs for a downpour.","sonnet":"A speaker stranded and drowning, picking fights as a symptom of going under, blaming the weather, breathing into blood and thunder — the only comfort is that everything ends, that the dying here is at least finite, even if the drought shows no sign of breaking.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~evil-eyes":{"haiku":"Malicious observation traps the narrator while decay breeds counterfeit despair. Fear and suffocation lead to violence masked as liberation; tonight the speaker claims these moments as their own.","opus":"A confrontation with a two-faced betrayer, throwing off years of suffocation to finally feel alive. The image of a wound in the side turns near-drowning into survival, defiance wrung out of being watched and wronged.","sonnet":"Surrounded by malicious watchers and a snake-like hypocrite, the speaker has been suffocating too long and now chooses survival — \"a spear through the side could drown me, but I survive.\" The rip tide becomes a dual image: threat and potential baptism, as screaming wide-awake they wait for one more wave to break so they can shatter their chains.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~giving-blood":{"haiku":"The speaker refuses to sacrifice themselves for disbelievers' salvation, preferring to feel alive even if it costs them everything.","opus":"A defiant refusal of hollow salvation, the narrator declining to sacrifice himself for people who don't believe and preferring to stay vitally alive even with his safety sabotaged. The British metalcore band's sound hardened after the death of guitarist and main songwriter Tom Searle, and grief seeps through the grim resolve and crushing dynamics.","sonnet":"Betrayal by the people who claimed to need you — the speaker never promised to bleed for unbelievers, but found themselves sabotaged anyway, their means of survival punctured by the very crowd demanding salvation. The water is polluted, the parachute has a hole in it, and the speaker chooses feeling alive over the letdown of anyone else's idea of rescue.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~giving-blood-abbey-road-version":{"haiku":"The Gospel distorts into ritual and bloodletting; the speaker refuses to bleed for the unconverted. Buried beneath righteousness is a devil in disguise who would rather feel anything than accept this twisted faith.","opus":"A rejection of hollow salvation from the band writing through Tom Searle's death, refusing to bleed for people who never believed. Choosing to feel alive over false comfort, even sabotaged, the plea is just to be remembered afterward.","sonnet":"A speaker refuses to bleed for unbelievers who distort the gospel and contaminate everything around them — \"the water's polluted, my feathers caught in the spill\" — choosing to feel alive over accepting someone else's salvational framework. The internal wound never exits cleanly, and the aftermath may find no one thinking of them at all.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~gone-with-the-wind":{"haiku":"The narrator loses faith and builds their own labyrinth trap, sinking into nothing and becoming their own worst enemy.","opus":"A stark portrait of self-sabotage and vanished faith, the singer stalled on treacherous ground, dismissing himself as worthless, constructing an inescapable maze and declaring himself his own greatest threat. A recurring line recasts hope as confinement, twisting reassurance into a snare. Architects, the Brighton metalcore band, bring their signature crushing weight, hope reframed as just another cell.","sonnet":"A labyrinth built by the person trapped inside it — the speaker constructed their own prison, locked themselves in, and lost their nerve for any leap of faith. Hope is explicitly named as a prison here. The song doesn't offer resolution, only the repeated wish to be annihilated, interrupted briefly by the possibility that accepting what's meant to be might dissolve the walls.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~gravity":{"haiku":"Inner violence and turbulent thoughts demand release; the only way forward is to burn the bridges that lead nowhere and finally awaken to change.","opus":"A confrontation with the violence one carries inside and the exhaustion of it, reaching for something to hold on to as everything shakes. The plea to gravity is a plea to be anchored, to finally wake up rather than fall.","sonnet":"A desperate call to release internal violence and surrender to the inevitable — whether personal crisis or civilizational collapse — framed as an awakening rather than a defeat. The speaker has seen the indistinguishability of good and evil and chooses to burn the bridge that leads nowhere, finding clarity only at the breaking point.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~hereafter":{"haiku":"Catastrophic loss leaves the speaker adrift in brokenness beyond repair, searching for meaning in wreckage that yields only the certainty of enduring pain.","opus":"Grief as a flooded, drained wasteland where every road circles back to a loss that can't be undone. Born from the death of Tom Searle, it's a survivor clawing toward air on shattered limbs, teaching himself to go on in the absence.","sonnet":"A person drowning in grief and disorientation after an overwhelming loss tries to navigate wreckage that is beyond repair, acknowledging that some fractures won't mend while still fighting, broken-boned, to find a way forward.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~impermanence-abbey-road-version":{"haiku":"Acceptance of mortality and inescapable death; people barricade against finality, yet all eventually pass through and dissolve, and those terrified of death never truly live.","opus":"Written in the shadow of losing founding guitarist Tom Searle, this is Architects staring mortality in the face and refusing to flinch, arguing that fear of death is what keeps people from living and that only accepting the end makes a life real.","sonnet":"A song about the universal human refusal to accept death — everyone barricades themselves against mortality, dies alone, and fights against impermanence through endless repetition, but the only way to truly live is to stop fearing the end.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~judgement-day":{"haiku":"Numbness as survival strategy numbs judgment itself—constructed indifference holds off apocalypse, but the cost is the slow death of feeling anything at all.","opus":"Sedation and detachment turned holy, someone drifting into binary numbness while others insist it's fine. Judgement day arrives as anesthetized surrender rather than terror.","sonnet":"A song about sedated numbness in the face of someone else's religious certainty — the speaker is too medicated to feel, drifting through a world of binary thinking and divine judgment, watching someone they care about die in devotion to a god the speaker fears rather than loves.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~learn-to-live":{"haiku":"Resilience in the face of repeated exclusion and adversity; the speaker and their community survive hardship to claim another day free from oppressors.","opus":"Architects turn defiant, urging the self to get back up and start actually living rather than staying where its critics prefer it. The band cast survivors as those abandoned in the storm who keep pulling through, springing up every time they're shoved down, an anthem of stubborn independence insisting things really are better elsewhere and life is worth clinging to.","sonnet":"Survivors of repeated rejection band together in defiance. Each time they were pushed aside they rose again, and now they hold the line — not for anyone else, but for themselves — insisting that persistence and self-reliance are the only path forward.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~living-is-killing-us":{"haiku":"Civilizational self-destruction is reframed as a love affair with a death machine — the heresy is simply existing within a system where too much is never enough and the holy land burns from within.","opus":"Modern existence as a death machine we somehow adored, where endless craving and the sin of just being alive grind us down. The despair hardens into stubborn resolve to turn it around, even if he has to do it alone.","sonnet":"Modern existence as suicidal addiction — humanity fell in love with a death machine, the imperative of more overrides every limit, and simply being alive within this system is already heresy. The home is built on a battleground, misfortune compounds, and the repeated impulse to fix it alone underscores how thoroughly collective will has already failed.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~meteor":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic collapse approaches while the speaker remains paralyzed; fire and ice meet, leaving them unable to act against imminent destruction.","opus":"Standing paralyzed beneath an incoming disaster, told to go to war and move mountains but feeling as futile as fire in the snow. It's a portrait of dread and burnout at the edge of apocalypse, where everyone keeps throwing stones toward the end.","sonnet":"A person who should be capable of great things — \"I should be moving mountains\" — stands instead paralysed under incoming disaster, the arrow buckling in the bow, fire in snow; the song frames collective catastrophe and personal inadequacy in the same breath, asking why we keep throwing stones before the apocalypse.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~nihilist":{"haiku":"Civilisation abandons its foundations, with gods displaced by commerce and binary logic, leaving only emptiness and lost souls.","opus":"A furious portrait of a faithless age where gods have abandoned us and salvation can't be bought, faith weaponized and the world run as corporatocracy. Even the god the narrator finds is suicidal, disgusted at the mess humanity has made.","sonnet":"Humanity is cast as spiritually abandoned and collectively bankrupt: gods have left, souls were sold for salvation that never arrived, and the corporatocracy and organized religion have colluded in a great esoteric depression. Even God, imagined clutching a razor blade, surveys the wreckage humans have made of their own hearts.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~these-colours-don-t-run-feat-jon-green-of-deez-nuts":{"haiku":"Deception and corruption pervade systems of power and freedom, where materialistic pursuit leads to spiritual death.","opus":"British metalcore aimed at the myth of the free-market dream, painting the land of plenty as a suburban grave where souls are priced and sold. The bitter refrain to keep insisting everything's fine exposes the coping lie under manufactured contentment.","sonnet":"A critique of the American Dream and capitalist society: the streets are not paved with gold, freedom comes at a price, and beneath the surface lies deception, chemical numbness, and a grinding suburban futility that benefits those at the top.","src":"haiku","fable":"A demolition of the American Dream as a sales pitch, hinging on a title that turns a patriotic boast inside out: the flag's vow of steadfast colour gets rhymed against the colour draining from a collapsing face, so national pride and physical ruin become one image. The verses set the mythology of a free nation against its receipts — debt-slavery dressed up as suburban comfort, a paradise whose entry carries a market price — while the repeated reassurance that all is well works as a sedative, forced cheer swallowed like a pill. What lifts it past a standard protest screed is the moment Architects break their own frame, the narrator confessing he can wring no eloquence from this rot and that the obvious consoling cliche is already taken. He then drops craft entirely to spit raw class contempt at the ones who hoarded everything, and that admitted collapse of metaphor is the point — the machine is too grubby to deserve one.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"the flag's boast of steadfast colour rhymed against a collapsing face","built":"the narrator admits he can wring no metaphor from the rot, then drops into raw contempt"},
"architects~unbeliever":{"haiku":"Tribal loyalties collapse under the weight of manufactured difference, revealing that all ideological battle is an illusion masking universal human vulnerability and interdependence.","opus":"A cosmic wide-shot arguing that the lines we kill each other over vanish once you see humanity as a single fragile speck of stardust. The plea is to stop feeding the tribal fire and shelter in one another instead.","sonnet":"A call to step back from the tribalism and self-righteousness that has humans destroying each other, grounded in the recognition that all life emerges from the same cosmic material and all perspectives are refractions of the same limited human sight. The song argues for collective survival over factional conflict.","src":"haiku"},
"architects~whiplash":{"haiku":"Soldiers arrive to silence resistance and force compliance into gilded cages. Movement becomes both command and trap; disapproval guarantees doom while the powerful orchestrate outrage.","opus":"A savage takedown of tribalism and mob obedience, the crowd bowing to a bloody crown and only caring about its own. The refrain asks whether anyone was ever built to withstand the cultural violence they're caught in.","sonnet":"Society bows to a crown dripping in blood while halos light compliant crowds; the speaker asks whether anyone was built for this whiplash — the constant violence, groupthink, and demand to move or be silenced. Doomed youth and holy rats orbit each other in a world where telling the truth is called lying and dissent earns a bullet.","src":"haiku"},
"arctic-monkeys~do-i-wanna-know":{"haiku":"Obsessive desire for reciprocation drives a narrator into cyclical return and hope despite uncertainty, bound by the compulsion to know if feeling flows both ways.","opus":"The helpless gravity of desire for someone whose feelings are uncertain, where the singer keeps drunk-dialing and crawling back because he's too busy being theirs to move on. Turner captures the late-night courage that says things daylight would never allow.","sonnet":"A confession of obsessive, unspoken longing — playing a song on repeat until falling asleep, always crawling back, too busy being yours to fall for someone new — while hesitating to ask whether the feeling flows both ways.","src":"haiku"},
"arctic-monkeys~you-probably-couldn-t-see-for-the-lights-but-you-were-staring-straight-at-me":{"haiku":"Self-consciousness explodes under romantic attention; the speaker fumbles through performance while others vie for the same gaze.","opus":"Early Alex Turner nails teenage nerve at its most excruciating, tongue-tied and flushed when the person he wants finally approaches. It is desire colliding with self-consciousness, the small-club drama of trying and failing to be cool.","sonnet":"The speaker is tongue-tied and physically overwhelmed in the presence of someone so attractive that every social interaction collapses into incoherence — while the rest of the room performs their best tricks for the same person. The observation shifts from self-mockery to a quiet declaration that no one like this will come along again.","src":"haiku"},
"argonaut-wasp~tvs":{"haiku":"Summer nights at sixteen fade into present distance measured by the narrator's shaking hands. Time corrupts memory while the speaker wonders if slower pacing might have preserved what carelessly slipped away.","opus":"A wistful summer-nostalgia song for a teenage romance that couldn't survive time, the memory of being sixteen by the sea still making his hands shake. Love here is something bound to crash like waves, beautiful and doomed to end.","sonnet":"Two old friends reflect on a summer romance from age sixteen — hot nights, waves calling their names — now felt only as heartbreak and hand tremors. The distance of time and the crash of summer love form a gentle, melancholy loop set against the ambient drone of television sound.","src":"haiku"},
"arianne~komm-susser-tod-m-10-director-s-edit-version":{"haiku":"The narrator acknowledges years of failure and self-deception before realizing that loved ones matter more than isolation. Sadness accompanies this awakening to connection after wandering alone through pain.","opus":"The Evangelion apocalypse ballad, a cheerful-sounding song of total despair in which a wounded soul decides the only answer is to end everything. It's the sound of a mind welcoming oblivion, everything returning to nothing.","sonnet":"A voice broken by guilt over letting down those it loved decides the best thing — the only thing — is to end it all and leave forever, recognising they can never love or trust again after this collapse. Everything returns to nothing in a spiral of self-blame and letting down, the refrain of \"tumbling down\" as inevitable as gravity.","src":"haiku"},
"arigarnon-friends~telefon":{"haiku":"Connection reaches toward understanding through voice and presence, where words overflow and bodies respond to the plea to receive and transmit emotion across distance.","opus":"A tender determination to catch and hold a distant person's voice through the phone, receiving their winter pain, their anger, even their laughter. The wish to send his own voice up to the rooftop and across the town frames connection as something reached for across separation.","sonnet":"A phone call stretches through a winter night and into morning: the speaker listens to the other person's pain — dry skin, cold aches, unstoppable anger that tips into laughter — and wants to absorb every word, letting the ordinary sounds of the city and their heartbeat become daily life shared across the line. The rooftop image in the final lines suggests wanting their voice to carry as far as the sky.","src":"haiku"},
"arimea~leash":{"haiku":"Misogyny and control are exposed as reciprocal chains; oppressive men justify domination through self-deception while facing inevitable reckoning.","opus":"A scorching takedown of a manipulative, misogynistic man and the control he wields, the narrator refusing to be silenced and warning that consequences are coming. The disgust is visceral, a demand that he keep her name out of his mouth.","sonnet":"A misogynist's control devices turned back on him: the leash he wanted around her neck now tightens around his own, his sycophantic little man energy named and mocked, and the speaker — whose skin crawls at the thought of him — refuses to keep his name in her mouth or her silence in his favor.","src":"haiku"},
"armin-van-buuren-maor-levi~divino":{"haiku":"Anticipation and desire build as trust slowly dissolves fear; physical touch becomes the gateway to liberation where restraint yields to inevitable sensation.","opus":"A seductive reassurance to someone scarred by past love, promising patience, no rushing, and a simple touch to set them free. The Weeknd-style refrain of feeling it coming turns the coaxing into a slow build of desire offered as safety rather than pressure.","sonnet":"A persistent seduction addressed to someone who is afraid of love after being hurt: the speaker promises patience, physical attentiveness, and the certainty of mutual desire, framing the encounter as a release — \"just a simple touch and it can set you free.\" The repeated \"I feel it coming\" builds the entire lyric around anticipation rather than arrival.","src":"haiku"},
"armin-van-buuren~no-fun":{"haiku":"Constraint and refusal to surrender define existence, with the speaker demanding participation and rejecting passive withdrawal.","opus":"A dancefloor ultimatum to a hesitant partner: choose a side, quit holding back, nobody's your keeper, so live. The needling accusation of being no fun goads someone out of reluctance into an all-night surrender.","sonnet":"Directed at someone who keeps holding back and withdrawing from shared pleasure — the song pressures them to stop running, let go of control, and commit to the experience rather than standing apart from it.","src":"haiku"},
"arrival-of-autumn~witness":{"haiku":"Water rises while time freezes in an endless void of ruin and decay. The narrator swears resistance against this wasteland even as wind devours everything; urgency demands abandonment and escape.","opus":"A metalcore fight against being dismissed as better gone, the war on despair called hopeless yet still waged. Rage hardens the heart to rock, and the song stands as testimony to breaking and beginning anew.","sonnet":"A rising water-and-wasteland apocalypse mirrors an internal battle the speaker insists they tried to fight but lost, anger calcifying their heart to stone while a consuming voice of another drove the damage home. The chorus demands witnessing the crazed and the obsessed as they burn together — broken, shattered, starting over from the same ruinous point.","src":"haiku"},
"art-vs-science~friend-in-the-field":{"haiku":"A group loses a friend to death, capturing their escape into light and the disorientation of grief.","opus":"A hazy, disoriented late-night mood piece from the Australian electro-dance-rock trio, capturing that dizzy sensation of being unmoored with no sense of direction. Its recurring image of a companion vanishing into the sky plays as festival-fried whimsy, not real mourning, its charm coming from the woozy loop rather than the words.","sonnet":"Loss at the edge of consciousness — disoriented, stumbling, the speaker watches someone disappear into light and can't do anything but register the absence. The refrain is both elegy and vertigo, the kind of grief that comes when you're too lost yourself to fully account for what happened to someone else. Finding the way back home is all that remains.","src":"haiku"},
"art-vs-science~magic-fountain":{"haiku":"A mystical fountain holds secret power that causes war and kingdoms to fall, with survivors becoming kings in delusion.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek fairy tale about a mythic spring of light, truth, dreams, and youth, a prized relic of ancient realms whose secret was lost to war before a ragtag band stumbles onto it after dark, one madman swearing he glimpsed its glow. Art vs Science, the Australian dance-rock trio, spin the myth with knowing silliness, the endlessly chanted incantation winking at its own absurdity.","sonnet":"A mythic object — source of light, truth, dreams, youth — revealed as a trophy seized by kings, a Trojan device that burned in a war while peasants played at royalty. A lunatic survivor claims he saw the light. The song holds the fountain as something real that the world destroyed through greed and foolishness, leaving only one breathless, laughing witness.","src":"haiku"},
"art-vs-science~moonlight":{"haiku":"Dancing in moonlight sparks unexpected depth of feeling, breaking patterns by admitting what you actually need.","opus":"Dance euphoria shadowed by the sense of chasing a bad habit, a connection that shouldn't work and does. Broken vows and ancient promises surface under the moonlight, reminding the narrator why he still cares.","sonnet":"An unexpected mutual feeling surfaces between two people who both sense where this is heading but can't quite navigate it — ancient promises and broken vows lurk in the background while dancing under the moonlight reignites a caring the speaker had almost forgotten. The song ends mid-word, suggesting the feeling itself is still unresolved.","src":"haiku"},
"art-vs-science~parlez-vous-francais":{"haiku":"A playful song about street culture and attraction, encouraging people to strip away inhibitions and dance together on the Champs-Élysées.","opus":"A giddy party track built on flirtatious French, the recurring plea to speak French and take off your shirt. There's no depth beyond the come-on, the throughline is uninhibited seduction and dance-floor hedonism, egging everyone on to strip and have fun now because they can.","sonnet":"A playful, purely hedonistic party song built around the bilingual pick-up line — the French phrase means \"please darling, take your shirt off\" — with the Champs-Élysées as backdrop and the whole conceit being an excuse to get people to undress.","src":"haiku"},
"artemis-rising~petrichor":{"haiku":"Endless noise demands cessation; the narrator seeks mental shutdown from the world's screaming. Thought collapses under accumulated input; silence becomes the only escape from information overload.","opus":"A cry for peace amid information overload, exhausted by endless opinion and media-manufactured fear. Its quiet radical honesty is admitting no one knows the truth, and that when knowing fails, the only real choice left is to feel.","sonnet":"Overwhelmed by the relentless, polarising noise of modern media, the speaker confesses they don't know better than anyone else and admits fear — fear of being lied to, fear of choosing the wrong side. The only honest move is to let go of the pretence of certainty and, when knowing fails, simply feel.","src":"haiku"},
"artificial-language~the-back-of-my-mind":{"haiku":"A person questions their worth and wonders if isolation is necessary, constantly caught between the desire to be saved and the belief they are unsaveable.","opus":"This progressive act sits with the exhaustion of loving someone who is quietly self-destructing, the narrator unsure whether the person is even worth trying to rescue. Musing on how ordinary and interchangeable any single human life really is, the song offers hard questions it decides are kinder left unanswered, a tender helplessness in the face of another's despair.","sonnet":"Two people hover near the edge together — one counting reasons to persist, the other uncertain whether saving either of them is possible. The narrator writes because no one else listens, and the correspondence becomes the only fragile thread between existence and erasure. Solidarity and futility occupy the same space.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~10-000-devils":{"haiku":"Tormented by inner voices and possession, the narrator feels pulled into darkness with the world collapsing around them.","opus":"A portrait of a mind devoured by guilt and sin, where the psyche becomes a place of self-inflicted torment and existence feels like an unstoppable disaster. Legions of inner demons drag the sufferer down, binding him to the damned as his world collapses.","sonnet":"Someone trapped in a cycle of internal guilt, possessed voices, and psychological collapse feels 10,000 devils pulling them under — the mind becomes a torture chamber and life a wreck from which there is no visible escape.","src":"haiku","fable":"Thrash has always used damnation as a metaphor for the mind turning on itself, and Artillery run the whole song on that trade: the horde clawing up from below is never a literal cosmology but the sound of a man who cannot stop prosecuting himself, insisting on his innocence to a court that convenes only between his ears. The verses tighten the room — night falls, the walls creep inward, and the sufferer is delivered back to the same squalid corner he can never leave — so the horror is claustrophobic rather than infernal, a psyche pacing a cell it built for itself. By the count of ten thousand the number becomes the point: the tormentors multiply past any hope of naming them or bargaining with them, and the fact that their shouting arrives as gibberish is exactly the terror of a breakdown, an interior racket that means everything and nothing at once.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression"],"means":"damnation as the mind prosecuting itself past any count","built":"the gibberish roar of ten thousand tormentors enacts breakdown's interior racket"},
"artillery~ain-t-giving-in":{"haiku":"A person rejects all authority and social rules, choosing personal independence and judgment over conformity. The speaker doesn't care about being understood and refuses to follow any external expectations or fear.","opus":"A Danish thrash anthem of pure individualist defiance, rejecting religion, rules and anyone's judgment in favor of living entirely on one's own terms. Its energy is the refusal to be told who to be.","sonnet":"A declaration of personal autonomy against social pressure and judgment — the narrator refuses to conform, rejects others' rules, has no religion and no apologies, and won't be made into something he's not. It's a straight-line refusal to be domesticated.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~at-war-with-science":{"haiku":"Science is weaponized against humanity through pollution, chemical warfare, and genetic experimentation that destroys natural life and transforms humans into mutants.","opus":"A protest against technology weaponized against life itself, warning that pollution, nuclear and chemical conflict and genetic tampering have snared a formerly unspoiled world in a waking horror. The refrain mourns an older, natural way of living and being born, casting human cleverness as a weapon we now settle in blood.","sonnet":"A thrash-metal warning about the weaponization of scientific progress — technology that should have served humanity has been seized by authority and turned against it, from genetic engineering to chemical warfare, leaving life itself under attack.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~beneath-the-clay-r-i-p":{"haiku":"Inescapable consequence and social erasure following transgression, the speaker condemned to death with no redemption or forgetting possible.","opus":"A criminal's reckoning told from outside, the freedom felt after a deed collapsing into the certainty of capture and death. It reads as grim moral bookkeeping: crime cannot be neglected, and the price beneath the clay comes due for everyone.","sonnet":"A criminal who felt free at night and untouchable is now six feet under — capture was always just a question of time, the community refused to lift a finger, and the epitaph is simply R.I.P. The song refuses elegance: you fought, you lost, you were never good at accepting loss.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~bombfood":{"haiku":"A critique of military service and the manipulation of soldiers, depicting them as expendable tools sent to die for causes they don't believe in.","opus":"Artillery fire off an anti-war blast at the soldier as disposable equipment, admiring proud men, inking the enlistment forms, then ending up in a pit beside a comrade blown beyond recognition, longing for the safety of childhood, and shouldering the blame for volunteering. The 1980s Danish thrashers reduce the recruit to a single scornful epithet in the refrain.","sonnet":"Conscription stripped of glory — the volunteer follows the dream of belonging, signs the papers, sweats and bleeds on command, and ends up in a hole wishing he'd stayed home. The song names what military rhetoric never will: that soldiers are tools, expendable material, bombfood. The blame lands squarely on the individual who handed themselves over willingly to a system that never saw them as human.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~by-inheritance":{"haiku":"The speaker possesses an inherited, supernatural inner power beyond others' reach and without remorse, kept hidden yet growing, wielding it without hesitation or consequence.","opus":"Artillery imagine an inherited psychic power the narrator can wield and conceal, controlling minds and reshaping reality with a touch that is capturing and terrifying, tempting him to make others pay. The Danish thrash band, whose title track this is, turns a supernatural gift into a study of temptation and control, pride shading toward menace as the ability grows.","sonnet":"A person possesses an innate, unseen power — inherited, not earned — that bends reality around them without being visible to anyone else. The temptation is not to use it for good but to exploit it quietly, since no one would believe them anyway and there is no accountability.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~chill-my-bones-burn-my-flesh":{"haiku":"Psychological extremes of numbness and burning rage, the speaker caught between frozen despair and destructive intensity.","opus":"Emotional deadening rendered as a frozen landscape, warmth and reason gone, the narrator locked into ice. What thaws him is anger itself, the scorching fire of rage described as the thing that finally makes him whole again.","sonnet":"A figure frozen by grief or trauma — ghost of winter's bite, desperate eyes in a blizzard, a frozen statue seeking warmth from a sun that offers none. The second movement breaks the cold open: scorching anger makes the narrator whole, and the last words they may ever say are finally, fully felt.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~concealed-in-the-dark":{"haiku":"A solitary climber faces a mountain's deadly challenge alone, confronting the moral void of survival and being lost to cold and death.","opus":"The Danish thrashers dramatize a solitary mountaineer's fatal ambition, ignoring warnings and every helping hand to climb, stand, and perish alone in a lethal high-altitude zone. His moral compass and his soul both freeze in the cold, his final gamble ending in an anonymous, silent death that swallows him whole.","sonnet":"A solitary figure pushes into extreme terrain — a death zone — without support, surrounded only by their own will and the mountain's indifference. The climb ends in death, an obscure mark left in ice and darkness, the final proof that survival belongs to the resolute and the self-reliant alone.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~cybermind":{"haiku":"A technological force dismantles humans into spare parts and rebuilds them as tools for new purposes, erasing free will.","opus":"A dystopian vision of people being forcibly disassembled and reprogrammed into a standardized new breed, reduced to raw components and rebuilt to spec. The deeper reading warns against technology and enforced conformity as humanity's own worst danger, the mind overrun and rewritten against its will.","sonnet":"A fragment about transhumanism as dehumanization — humans are being broken into parts and rebuilt to meet the standards of a new engineered race, with the narrator addressing the listener as a target being upgraded against their will, though the lyrics are truncated.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~damned-religion":{"haiku":"Religious leaders exploit and manipulate the poor through false doctrine and hypocrisy, wielding corrupted spiritual authority as a tool of oppression.","opus":"A furious anti-religious tirade from the Danish thrash veterans, rejecting the values and crusades of hypocritical, power-hungry clergy. It brands organized faith an addiction and a disease, accusing a fraudulent savior of exploiting the vulnerable and vowing his profiteers will face damnation.","sonnet":"Organised religion is indicted as a system of exploitation — false messiahs preying on the poor and weak, rhetoric used to elevate the powerful and destroy the vulnerable. The narrator refuses every claim the institution makes on him, treating faith-as-drug not with contempt for believers but with fury at the architects of that addiction.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~dark-days":{"haiku":"Environmental collapse and global catastrophe darken the world; sun fades, water pollutes, earth shakes, and humanity burns under its own weight.","opus":"The Danish thrash veterans paint an environmental collapse in slow motion, a dimming sun, spoiled water, choking air and a planet coming apart underfoot. The plainer reading is dread of ecological ruin already arriving, humanity sealed inside a dying world with no way back and no rescue on the horizon.","sonnet":"An environmental collapse song — the sun dims, water runs foul, air thickens, and the earth shakes as the world goes under, with the song cataloguing ecological apocalypse from the ground up and insisting these dark days are already arriving.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~death-is-an-illusion":{"haiku":"A meditation on the paradox of existence—if life is real then death is illusion, and if pain defines life then confusion and contradiction follow; the speaker vows to defeat their inner demons through fire.","opus":"A defiant metaphysical claim that if existence is genuine, then dying is mere deception, the narrator swearing to conquer his own darker nature with fierce flame, declining to repent or be toppled. The imagery of severing his own head locates the real war within, the sole adversary capable of beating him being himself.","sonnet":"A confrontation with the self's destructive interior — if life is real then death is only illusion, but pain is indistinguishable from living; the narrator resolves to defeat his own inner enemies through will rather than surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~decapitation-of-deviants":{"haiku":"The speaker critiques how systems force conformity through work culture, tying people's worth to appearance and productivity while the wealthy exploit the poor.","opus":"Danish thrash aimed at the conformity machine: school that only teaches obedience, work that demands you look attractive and grind yourself down for pay. It frames the respectable adult life as a slow death sentence dressed in a tie.","sonnet":"A bitter critique of a system that conditions people to be attractive, compliant workers — school produces rule-followers not thinkers, the economy punishes those without marketable appeal, and the poor are structurally excluded, all dressed up in a suit and a tie.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~delusions-of-grandeure":{"opus":"A dream world entered every night where the narrator rules as king and feels finally real and singular. The unsettling turn is admitting that these grand delusions might be exactly what he needs, and that his rage and his fantasy have sealed him inside his own head.","sonnet":"A person who can only rule the world in their dreams — in those delusions they are king, everything is real, and there is no retreat, but waking life remains small and the dream refuses to die.","src":"sonnet"},
"artillery~doctor-evil":{"haiku":"A person trapped in war endures endless torture and manipulation by a sadistic interrogator. Caught between talking and silence, damned either way, with hope for justice always denied.","opus":"A grim depiction of a wartime torturer who forces confessions out of a captive doomed whether he speaks or stays silent. The throughline is total hopelessness, no rescue, no justice, the sadist never satisfied, until being alive and being dead lose any meaningful distinction.","sonnet":"Wartime imprisonment and torture — someone caught in the wrong place at the wrong time, with no hope of justice, is subjected to endless interrogation and torment by a sadistic figure who won't stop until they confess something, anything, even if there's nothing to confess.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~don-t-believe":{"haiku":"People are manipulated by false narratives from teachers, preachers, liars, and supposed seekers of truth. The song warns against blind belief and encourages skepticism toward any unproven claims.","opus":"Addressing watchers, seers, liars, and preachers, the narrator counsels doubt, urging that no word be believed unless proven true lest a spell bring damnation. Artillery, the veteran Danish thrash band, cast skepticism as incantation here, the roll-call of archetypes and warnings against deceptive tales turning distrust of prophecy into the song's insistent creed.","sonnet":"A warning to treat every claimed truth as suspect until tested — preachers, teachers, and liars alike offer words that carry consequence if swallowed whole. Skepticism is framed as self-protection; credulity is the spell that brings its own damnation.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~end-of-eternity":{"haiku":"Spiritual crisis and defiant self-determination—the speaker rejects divine judgment and chooses to be their own god rather than bow to external authority.","opus":"A spiritual crisis staged as a plunge into fire, the narrator confronting demons, wrestling with belief and self-forgiveness before renouncing every higher power and crowning himself. Artillery, the Danish thrash veterans, escalate denial into defiance until refusing to submit becomes a cataclysmic fall.","sonnet":"A spiritual crisis narrated at the threshold between belief and self-determination — voices of heaven, hell, and the inner self all competing as a man decides whether to bow down or declare himself his own god. The fall into the flaming sea is the consequence of a lifetime of easy lies finally catching up.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~ethos-of-wrath":{"haiku":"Rage and violence possess a person from within, manifesting as a malevolent entity lurking behind one's eyes. This wrath consumes the mind, controls behavior, and drives brutal attacks from something residing in the psyche.","opus":"A malevolent thing lurks behind friendly smiles and at the narrator's shoulder, boiling his blood and reddening his vision, a familiar voice steering him during blank gaps in his memory. Artillery, the Danish thrash band, render creeping paranoia and violent compulsion, rage personified as a presence in the room poised to strike from inside him.","sonnet":"Wrath as an invisible presence that attaches itself to a person — intimate, covert, whispering direction and engineering violence until a blackout leaves a body in a closet and memory blank. The ethos is not a philosophy but a possessing force, and by the end it has already finished its work.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~fear-of-tomorrow":{"haiku":"Machines and technology control human life, stripping autonomy and enforcing obedience. The song warns of a dehumanized future where metal and circuits decide human fate.","opus":"The Danish thrash band mines early-genre technophobia, imagining machines seizing control of daily life while people stay too oblivious to see it coming. It reads as a Cold War nightmare of automation and lost autonomy, the chorus a rueful admission that the alarm was sounded too late.","sonnet":"Machines as total takeover — iron arms, steel eyes, computer brains making every decision about life and love and law, destroying what resists and controlling what remains. The song's horror is in its passivity: humanity was too blind to see what was coming, and when tomorrow arrived it looked exactly like what they'd built. No uprising, no recourse, just the world they made.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~god-feather":{"haiku":"A person confronts someone who blames others for their failures, hides behind hollow words, and follows only inner demons while refusing to take responsibility for their downward path.","opus":"Artillery watch a self-righteous figure go down alone, reaping what he sowed on barren land while blaming everyone but himself, his hollow words caring for no one. The Danish thrashers sharpen the taunt with a refrain that God grants no wings, mortality certain and flight impossible, the inner demon he follows the only thing lifting him toward a shimmering he'll never reach.","sonnet":"A scathing portrait of a self-deceiving coward who blames the world, follows inner demons, and waits for divine rescue that will never come. No god grants wings to those who will not face themselves; all that remains is the hollow echo of pathetic words.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~how-do-you-feel":{"haiku":"Critique of cosmetic surgery and artificial self-modification—the speaker questions what remains authentic when bodies are surgically altered and reconstructed.","opus":"The Danish thrash veterans train their scorn on cosmetic self-erasure, cataloguing tightened skin, borrowed hair and countless obscene adjustments until the body reads as a hollow counterfeit. Their pointed, socially conscious streak sharpens a mocking demand that the subject confront who remains beneath the scars, framing surgical vanity as a slow amputation of the human.","sonnet":"A person who has remade themselves through cosmetic surgery faces the hollow question of whether anything genuine remains beneath the tightened skin and reconstructed features. The song probes the psychological cost of buying a new body, asking whether manufactured beauty can still feel like a self.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~in-defiance-of-conformity":{"haiku":"A defiant stance against societal pressure and mediocrity, rejecting the chains of conformity and choosing to break free despite the costs.","opus":"A raw declaration against the constraints of society, marked and trapped with existence itself feeling like a hangman's rope, choked by others' ignorance and cruelty, and choosing isolation or even madness over playing along, fighting to the bitter end. Artillery, the Danish thrash band, turn alienation into a defiant creed, the world shifting like quicksand, deliverance found only in refusing to conform.","sonnet":"Suffocation by social pressure, resisted at the cost of sanity — words like glass, wits turned to dust, a mind that feels like lead under the weight of others' stupidity and abuse. The speaker is branded and stranded, knows the system is quicksand, and refuses to play the game even as they admit it might destroy them. Defiance is the only available freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~in-the-trash":{"haiku":"Someone born into poverty and oppression rails against systemic injustice, enslavement by the powerful, and the inevitability of elite collapse.","opus":"Danish thrash band Artillery give voice to the downtrodden with rage, narrating from the perspective of someone born into poverty and treated as disposable by the powerful who dictate his fate from on high. Alive but never truly free, he warns that no oppressor's reign is permanent, turning thrash into blunt class-conscious protest.","sonnet":"Born into poverty and denied agency by every social structure above him, the narrator traces the logic of survival outside the law. Systems of justice belong to those who designed them; the law enforces hierarchy, not fairness. The anger is directed upward, at those who set the terms from behind locked doors.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~into-the-universe":{"haiku":"A speaker escapes a burning, corrupted world to seek transcendence or oblivion, imagining a realm without humanity. They gather others to leave Earth behind as destruction and illusion make life unbearable.","opus":"Artillery imagine abandoning a ruined, blood-soaked Earth, the narrator fleeing all of humanity into space toward an empty invented world where no conflict remains. Setting course for the sun, he leaves the living behind. The Danish thrash band casts cosmic escape as desperate freedom from a burning, doomed planet.","sonnet":"Overwhelmed by earthly violence and corruption, a narrator chooses cosmic escape over survival — setting sail not toward another country but away from the human race entirely. The universe is emptier than Earth, and that emptiness is the point.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~let-there-be-sin":{"haiku":"The speaker celebrates living without religious constraint, framing sin as freedom and rejecting religious authority as theft of life and truth.","opus":"Artillery preach pleasure over piety, urging a listener to seize lust, riches and appetite in their single lifetime while mocking clergy and branding religion a swindle that robs a person of life and self. The Danish thrash band recast wrongdoing as liberation, jeering at both the divine and the diabolic.","sonnet":"A full-throated rejection of religious abstinence and institutional morality in favour of desire, pleasure, and self-determination. The preacher is explicitly mocked and the bible dismissed as garbage, leaving only the raw imperative to live this one life without apology.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~life-in-bondage":{"haiku":"Labor and obligation trap the human spirit in cycles of control and exploitation, fear prevents liberation and chains calcify through generations.","opus":"A scathing indictment of the wage slave who trades courage and freedom for a paycheck, chained to obedience while his life fades and calling him a coward for sticking to the rules.","sonnet":"A savage indictment of those who lack the courage to break from the systems that grind them down — work at their command, feel the chain, count money while life fades — addressed to a man whose fear, embodied in a wife's cry and a wage, keeps him permanently enslaved.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~mi-sangre-the-blood-song":{"haiku":"Legacy and inherited pain flow through generations; the speaker passes blood and wisdom to the next, warning of worldly trials ahead.","opus":"A parent's grim blessing to a child stepping into experience: innocence gets stripped away, evil will be tasted, and no priest is coming to save you. Truth and strength have to be found within — better to fall for a conviction than to kneel and merely survive.","sonnet":"A parent speaks to a child about to enter the world — warning of innocence lost, the necessity of choosing one's own path, and the irrelevance of holy men — while promising that their bloodline and spirit will carry on forever.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~monster":{"haiku":"A psychological killer, shaped by childhood trauma and darkness, hunts victims to feed on their terror. The song traces brutality from damaged past to violent present.","opus":"Danish thrash trying, unusually, to look inside a killer rather than just relish the carnage. The insistent reminder that even monsters were once someone's child pushes against pure horror toward an uneasy question about how a person becomes this.","sonnet":"A serial killer origin story: a man who grew up behind a white fence with darkness in his head becomes intoxicated by his victims' fear, lifts the blade, and returns to the scene of his crimes — the refrain reminding us that even monsters were once somebody's son.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~not-a-nightmare":{"haiku":"Psychological torment and loss of control to an internal darkness. A voice manipulates from within, making nightmares bleed into waking life until the boundary dissolves.","opus":"Artillery voice a nocturnal predator of the mind, the presence inside a sleeper's room and head, pulling strings on the soul as prayers fail and mistakes catch up until death seems only a matter of time. The refrain insists it is no nightmare but life itself. The Danish thrash band renders dread as inescapable, the sandman turned menacing intruder.","sonnet":"A menacing presence that occupies the vulnerable hours of night — not a dream but the waking texture of a life under perpetual threat. The entity in the dark is simultaneously external and internal, the voice that tells its target their mistakes are fatal and time is almost gone.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~out-of-the-sky":{"haiku":"Radiation falls from the sky and kills indiscriminately, wiping out all life on Earth. The speaker is among the first to die, never knowing why.","opus":"Artillery picture nuclear death falling from overhead, searing radiation as the doomed hunt uselessly for cover. The Danish thrashers make the heavens the killer, the first to perish never learning the reason, the once-blue expanse gone crimson until nothing lives and radiation rules the ruined world.","sonnet":"Nuclear or cosmic annihilation arrives without warning, indiscriminate and total. The clear sky turns red, radiation kills friend and enemy alike, and no hiding place exists. Earth is left sterile, ruled only by the fallout no one can outrun.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~out-of-the-thrash":{"haiku":"Escape from thrashing chaos arrives as the speaker breaks free from the mosh. Beyond the thrash lies possibility.","opus":"A hard confrontation with someone weak and self-pitying, insisting that growth and the answer lie within yourself alone. The relentless refrain of struggle and loss becomes a challenge to break your own chains and stop making excuses.","sonnet":"A blunt diagnosis of weakness: the target lies, cries, craves, and whines — fighting and bleeding with no use, cracking under pressure — but the counterpoint insists growth lies within, there is a passage through, and the answer is to break the chains in a single blow rather than stay \"out of the trash.\"","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~prelude-to-madness":{"haiku":"Society binds through tradition and law, forcing conformity on both sides of good and evil. Strength and autonomy demand breaking free.","opus":"A halting meditation on how tradition and law bind society and constrain the individual, urging the listener to stay strong and remain themselves amid the coexisting good and bad of life.","sonnet":"A song about the rules and structures that bind society — ancestral traditions, positive law, and social norms all constrain individuals, and the narrator grapples with how to remain strong and be oneself while living within those binding constraints on both the good and bad sides of life.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~rise-above-it-all":{"haiku":"People dismiss lies and false authority, rising up together to seek justice and truth. They reject corruption and soar above oppression, refusing to be held down by dishonest systems.","opus":"Artillery deliver a defiant brush-off to a lying, treacherous foe whose hollow chatter the singer waves aside on a march toward fairness. Imagery of a soaring phoenix and eagle powers an anthem of endurance, the refrain vowing to climb over every obstacle and demanding that right be done.","sonnet":"A motivational metal declaration about overcoming obstacles and calling out liars — direct, defiant, with no patience for bullshit or bad attitude. The phoenix and eagle imagery is standard-issue uplift without deeper specificity.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~sandbox-philosophy":{"haiku":"A self-righteous, lying ego-maniac hides behind screens and invites war; the speaker warns that this phony's lies will be their grave and one of them will die.","opus":"The Danish thrash band deliver a scathing takedown of a cowardly online provocateur, a grown man behaving like a bratty toddler who invents his own facts and stokes conflict from behind a keyboard. The narrator refuses to swallow the propaganda and warns that the deceit will destroy its author, since only one of them survives this fight.","sonnet":"A contemptuous attack on an arrogant, lying egomaniac — probably a rival or political figure — who hides behind a screen building elaborate false narratives while provoking conflict they're too cowardly to face directly; the narrator dismisses their entire worldview as childish sandbox posturing.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~show-your-hate":{"haiku":"Terror and rage consume someone transformed into a killer; they deliver their soul to evil, finding temporary peace in violence before guilt returns.","opus":"Artillery narrate a night of vengeance, a target chosen to die as torment and terror serve a mind under evil's sway. The Danish thrashers stage the killer's grim discharge of rage, gore on his hands buying only a brief, fleeting calm, the second-person killer goaded to take pride in surrendering himself to the darkness he flees into.","sonnet":"A person consumed by hatred acts out a violent fantasy of revenge — stalking, killing, then experiencing a brief and disturbing peace. The song inhabits the mind of someone who equates violence with release, questioning what that feeling reveals about them.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~terror-squad":{"haiku":"A direct indictment of militarized police and state violence dressed in false heroism, questioning who the real cowards and villains are in a terrorized society.","opus":"Artillery condemn a black-uniformed death squad, state-sanctioned thugs posing as protectors while assaulting and gunning down captives. The Danish thrashers call them cowards hiding behind their weapons, and the narrator turns the barrel back, warning he'll aim at the oppressor if pushed again, leaving the outcome for the gods to decide.","sonnet":"An armed authority force — blessed by government yet predatory in practice — is exposed as cowardly bullies masquerading as heroes. The song confronts their violence head-on, threatening to turn their own terror back on them.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~the-almighty":{"haiku":"The speaker accuses a god of being a hypocrite and tyrant who created suffering rather than love, questioning how a supposedly almighty being could produce such broken creatures. The speaker claims to be the bringer of fire the god blamed them for.","opus":"A blasphemous taunt voiced by a devil figure who indicts God for a world of shame and cruelty, throwing the blame for evil back onto its supposed maker. It's classic thrash provocation, arguing that the creator, not the accused, failed his creatures.","sonnet":"A direct challenge to God — the narrator, speaking with the voice of evil, confronts a creator whose world is full of shame and suffering, accusing him of hiding away kindness and pinning the blame for everything on the wrong entity.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~the-challenge":{"haiku":"Humanity faces inevitable collapse because of lost spiritual values and destructive environmental choices. A helpless species on its knees confronts extinction as its own hubris brings apocalyptic ruin.","opus":"The Danish thrash band survey a self-inflicted apocalypse, imagining a great civilization erected on unstable footing after mankind chose to degrade nature rather than nurture it. They voice a humanity brought low, its old moral codes discarded and its supposed wisdom revealed as fatal hubris as the reckoning looms and life on earth slips toward extinction.","sonnet":"Humanity stands on the edge of its own ruin, having discarded its moral compass and dismantled the natural world it depended on. Once-proud civilization crumbles on a foundation it built over nothing, and the species kneels before a deadline of its own making.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~the-end":{"haiku":"A meditation on mortality, watching the world's destruction while accepting that apocalypse is inevitable and near, driven by internal hatred and external burning.","opus":"Facing mortality and a world consumed by fear, hatred, and fire, the speaker watches everything he loves head toward death and warns that the end is near.","sonnet":"A figure watches a world consumed by hatred, fear, and apocalyptic signs — blood-red skies, inner war, voices screaming in the dark — and concludes that the end is near and inescapable.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~the-eternal-war":{"haiku":"Invaders perpetrate genocide and cultural destruction, killing systematically and taking control. The song questions whether the enemy is external oppression or an internal darkness that people refuse to confront.","sonnet":"Invaders arrive by sea and systematically destroy a community — killing, raping, burning — and the cycle of violence repeats without end. The final lines turn inward: perhaps the enemy is not external at all but a darkness within the self that no battle can permanently defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~thrasher":{"haiku":"Thrash metal enthusiasts are called to embrace violent energy, aggression, and devotion to the music and pit. The song celebrates headbanging, screaming, and destruction as acts of rebellion.","opus":"A thrash-metal call to arms goading the crowd to kill it, mosh, headbang, and go out with a shout, prepared to die for the thrill. The subject is the pit itself, the ecstatic communal ritual of a metal show as a demolition of the town.","sonnet":"A thrash metal audience call-and-response with no narrative — questions about readiness to die, kill, and scream are directed at the crowd, inviting them into a shared pit ritual. It's a performance instruction set.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~time-has-come":{"haiku":"Reckoning and judgment for past rule: time arrives as destruction for an incompetent tyrant whose misuse of power and manipulation of life and death now curse them to face their blame.","opus":"Artillery deliver an apocalyptic judgment on a ruinous tyrant, conjuring a dreadful figure who terrorizes the guilty and rips him to pieces. The pounding chorus pronounces his reckoning: he governed disastrously and toyed with lives, and now a curse falls on him with no escape left.","sonnet":"A reckoning arrives for an incompetent ruler who has gambled recklessly with life and death. The bells, the fire, and the fear converge as judgment — the curse is deserved, the blame inescapable — and the time that has finally come carries nothing but the weight of earned destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~uniform":{"haiku":"Authoritarian control and coercive conformity suppress individuality and free thought, with state power hiding corruption. The song warns against silent surrender to oppression.","opus":"Artillery indict a police state of fear and informers, where badged authority keeps the nation tidy by making everyone suspect their neighbors. A jab about a boy toying with his father's pistol paints the enforcer as an overgrown, dangerous child, the thrash driving home a warning about surveillance and lost freedom.","sonnet":"The song describes living under an authoritarian surveillance state where fear is constant, informers could be neighbors, and the only safe choice is silence. The uniform of the title represents the faceless enforcers who keep order through threat and control.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~upon-my-cross-i-crawl":{"haiku":"A false holy man trapped between salvation and damnation turns his cross upside down and seeks revenge rather than forgiveness. Crucified by hypocrisy, they crawl toward self-annihilation.","opus":"The Danish thrash band pen a blasphemous inversion voiced by a savior figure who has soured on love and now hungers for vengeance instead. Boiling with rage, he rejects the sacred image others project onto him, overturns his own instrument of martyrdom, and chooses self-destruction, faith rewritten as wrath.","sonnet":"A holy man cracks under the weight of the role imposed on him — he was supposed to be salvation but is filled with rage and the need for revenge — and he crucifies himself trying to be the image others hold of him, ultimately rejecting both the cross and the god behind it.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~warrior-blood":{"haiku":"A warrior culture celebrates an inherited martial instinct passed through generations, viewing combat brotherhood and aggression as intrinsic to identity and survival.","opus":"The German thrash veterans frame combat as an inherited calling. It is a hymn to soldierly kinship, imagining fighting spirit as something carried in the bloodline and handed down across the years, warriors bound to one another in a fight to the death.","sonnet":"Combat identity is presented not as choice but as inheritance — something running through the bloodline from generation to generation, as definitive as bone. The battlefield is a sacred home, brotherhood is sworn in blood, and the only comprehensible way to exist is side by side, fighting forward.","src":"haiku"},
"artillery~when-death-comes":{"haiku":"Rising from inner darkness and evil to face one's demons while questioning whether escape is possible before death arrives.","opus":"A jaded drifter hauls his guilt down a road that never ends, having clawed his way up from wickedness and tedium. He wonders aloud whether he can shed his dread and start over, redeeming himself before mortality quietly overtakes him.","sonnet":"A figure haunted by inner evil and a branded, rootless past asks whether it is possible to shed that burden and live differently, knowing death is always closing in from behind.","src":"haiku"},
"artisan~god-of-the-grey":{"haiku":"A predatory figure reveals how people surrender their independence to follow leaders blindly, mistaking manipulation for enlightenment and caging themselves willingly.","opus":"A sneering monologue from a self-appointed deity who takes credit for the very faith people worship, positioning himself in the murky space between meaning and emptiness. It mocks how easily the weak trade freedom for belief, choosing captivity because thinking for themselves is harder, and it holds nothing but scorn for those who follow the herd into their own confinement.","sonnet":"A false prophet's monologue — the speaker positions themselves between the profound and the empty, cure and curse, saviour and destroyer, and uses that ambiguity to collect willing slaves. The congregation is free but chooses chains; they have opinions that are identical to everyone else's; they march into their own cage calling it righteousness. The song ends in contempt, the god of the grey running out of patience for the spineless.","src":"haiku"},
"arukara~a-plil6q":{"haiku":"Knowledge becomes frozen datum in standardized systems; civilization compresses meaning into mnemonics and test scores. Two lovers meet in winter cold, their chemistry transcending definition, yet the world insists on categorizing what defies calculation.","opus":"With the brainy, angular energy of Japanese math-rock, the song frames history as civilization's endless cycle, fire and water and metal and wood compressed into exam mnemonics and planetary tables that spin a defined world round and round. Against that determinism, two young people freeze on a winter park bench, asking why they met only to hurt each other, and refuse the idea that their chemistry was ever just another formula to be solved.","sonnet":"History is framed as civilisation's endless cycle — fire, water, metal and wood combined to illuminate and wound — compressed into entrance-exam mnemonics and planetary tables that spin the world round and round in a \"defined world.\" Against this determinism, two young people freezing on a winter park bench ask why they ever had to meet if only to hurt each other, and resist the idea that their encounter was just another formula, insisting their chemistry exceeds human knowledge.","src":"haiku"},
"arukara~dance-inspire":{"haiku":"Movement and rhythm inspire transcendence; the speaker calls to dance beyond the illusory world. Fool fool pattern repeats, suggesting release through physical expression and liberation.","opus":"A Japanese garage-rock rush that treats a wearying, absurd world as something to dance through defiantly, the guitar consuming secrets and sweet fruit alike. Life as a cinematic writhing love, met with a small kiss instead of despair.","sonnet":"A night-club floor becomes a maharaja's court where the narrator, treated like a toy by the gods' whims, resolves to dance through till morning regardless — this absurd, feverish world is like a movie, writhing and burning with love. A Telecaster will shred everything — secrets, sweet fruit, the whole spectacle — and swallow it whole as the tiny kiss offered to an exasperating world.","src":"haiku"},
"as-december-falls~mayday":{"haiku":"Chronic dissatisfaction and unfulfilled expectations create desperation where the speaker seeks external rescue from themselves.","opus":"A distress call sent from inside depression's numbing sameness, where being fine is not the same as being happy. The pop-punk hook turns the ache of wanting to disappear into a plea for anyone to notice.","sonnet":"A distress call from inside comfortable numbness: the speaker is stressed, isolated, and stuck in monochrome routine, screaming \"mayday\" while simultaneously admitting they prefer being alone and feel unable to want anything better.","src":"haiku"},
"as-december-falls~teeth":{"haiku":"Mystery threatens to surface through games the narrator doesn't wish to play. The speaker questions whether their haven offers sanctuary or further peril; trust crumbles.","opus":"Guardedness as armor, someone who won't let a partner in because what's buried might bite. The refrain warns that the buried can still draw blood, old wounds staying dangerous no matter how deep they're hidden.","sonnet":"Someone attractive is trying to get close, but the speaker warns them off: there are things buried inside them that remain dangerous — \"bodies buried still have teeth\" — and they'd rather stay locked and solitary than lure someone into what they know is underneath. Walls shake but the secret holds.","src":"haiku"},
"as-everything-unfolds~all-i-ve-ever-known":{"haiku":"The narrator's eyes open to reveal horror and dishonour; judgment arrives looking unfair. Known certainties dissolve as juries prove unjust and systems fail to validate.","opus":"A pushback against judgment and virtue-signaling, the narrator owning her mistakes and asking only for room to grow. Underneath the defiance is weariness, someone waiting on the wayside just to feel something again.","sonnet":"The speaker acknowledges their own mistakes with humility while pushing back against judgement that isn't fair and virtue signalling that substitutes for genuine listening. Growth requires leaving space for the unknown, and the recurring image of sitting on the wayside waiting for something to make them feel again frames the search as both honest and exhausted.","src":"haiku"},
"as-everything-unfolds~blossom":{"haiku":"Growth approaches despite current inadequacy; the speaker cries out truth once kept hidden through jest. Continuous motion prevents rest, yet blooming remains inevitable.","opus":"A turn toward healing, buried pain finally coming into bloom rather than staying suppressed. Facing fear head-on, the singer confesses her own spite and keeps trying anyway, growth framed as something that won't disappear once it starts.","sonnet":"Emerging from a frozen self-image — minds iced into burdens, bound by intolerance and inner lies — the speaker confesses to having been hateful and spiteful, and commits to growth now that everything forgotten has come into blossom. Fear is faced rather than fled, and the promise is that this opening won't disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"as-everything-unfolds~daylight":{"haiku":"Parallel lives collapse into night while opposite forces maintain precarious balance. Fever burns in belief; life becomes a landscape where light and dark wage eternal combat.","opus":"A song about balance and acceptance, life a carousel of opposites where the night and the day both have to be taken in. Its quiet wisdom is that there's no moving ahead while you loathe who you were or bury what you believe.","sonnet":"Life as a balance of opposites — night setting in, daylight setting in, a carousel of parallel lives — requires patience and a willingness to embrace rather than loathe the past self. The recurring arc of dark to daylight insists that moving forward is impossible if you ignore what you stand for.","src":"haiku"},
"as-everything-unfolds~flip-side":{"haiku":"Reveling in someone else's loss and rejection, finding cruel satisfaction in living the life they wanted.","opus":"Relishing the reversal where the good life once denied is now the narrator's, and the other person's envy is the sweetest part. Relief tinged with vengeance, spinning in the disorienting lights of the flip side.","sonnet":"A song about triumphant reversal — the narrator has broken free from someone who controlled or diminished them, and now revels in watching that person fail to reach what the narrator has claimed for themselves. The emotional core is vindictive satisfaction, the pleasure of thriving in the exact life the other person wanted.","src":"haiku"},
"as-everything-unfolds~grayscale":{"haiku":"Depression drains color from life; the speaker dwells in emotional numbness and fear, imprisoned by despair.","sonnet":"Depression drains all colour from perception, leaving only a heavy, numbing grey that isolates and exhausts. The narrator fights this flattening internally while performing normalcy outward, aware that no one told them life had to feel this way and unsure how to reclaim the light.","src":"haiku"},
"as-everything-unfolds~hiding-from-myself":{"haiku":"Bottled rage and numbness hide the speaker from their own shadow; no one believes their internal struggle and silent agony.","opus":"The British post-hardcore act stages a battle waged entirely within one head, a narrator masking his turmoil while dread threatens to drown out everything else. Bottled rage and refused burdens give way to a defiant insistence on his own worth, clinging to it even as no one seems to believe him.","sonnet":"A person dissociating from their own inner life under the weight of suppressed rage, external pressure, and the indifference of those around them tries to affirm their own worth against the numbing. The tension between bottled fury and a fragile insistence on deserving to exist runs through every turn of the lyric.","src":"haiku"},
"as-everything-unfolds~point-of-view":{"haiku":"Ugly truth refuses discussion when the speaker assumes correctness; perspective imprisons understanding. The narrator begs extraction from this subjective cage where neither party concedes.","opus":"The clarity of leaving someone who refuses to grow, insisting the exit will one day make sense to them. It's about withdrawing from lies and self-deception, standing firm that the decision to walk was right.","sonnet":"A speaker who was lied to and dismissed — \"taken out of sight, out of mind\" — finally withdraws and asks the other to see it from their point of view: knowing they were right, they had to leave. The outro names the other's tactics bluntly — blackout, secrets, lies, pushing away everything needed — as the speaker refuses further compromise.","src":"haiku"},
"as-everything-unfolds~rose-bouquets":{"haiku":"Poets advise releasing old romantic symbols because they no longer serve growth, only anchor regret and decay.","opus":"Letting go of what no longer serves you, the dead bouquets standing for regrets and old wounds kept too long. It admits the rot within is shared by everyone, then urges release anyway.","sonnet":"About the difficulty of releasing something that used to hold meaning — the rose bouquets are symbols of romantic or emotional attachment that have outlived their purpose, and the song urges letting go of them even when it feels like decay; there's a shared human quality to this failure to move on.","src":"haiku"},
"as-everything-unfolds~slow-down":{"haiku":"Contradiction of being urged to hesitate while bleeding out, power seized then immediately suppressed by those demanding compliance.","opus":"The frustration of being told to calm down while nobody actually helps you, the classic burnout paradox. This UK metalcore band frames ambition as a crown that's crushing the wearer, bleeding out even as they're urged to pace themselves.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a figure who simultaneously encourages the narrator to shout while telling them to slow down, brings them to life while letting them bleed out — the double-bind of being enabled and undermined at once. \"Heavy is the head that wears the crown\" marks the cost of ambition inside that contradiction.","src":"haiku"},
"as-everything-unfolds~stranger-in-the-mirror":{"haiku":"A person watches their dark impulses consume them from within like a flame, becoming a stranger to their former self as depression hunts them.","opus":"As Everything Unfolds face down mental illness as a blaze igniting the spirit and a familiar hound of depression that stalks and trails them, until they turn into the very person they vowed never to be. The reflection suffocates them as they beg to be dragged free or left under. The British metalcore band give the illness a face in the glass.","sonnet":"Depression as doppelganger — something crept in while the speaker wasn't looking and replaced them, wearing a hollow crown. The black dog is named directly: it hunts, follows, drags you down. The person in the mirror is a stranger now, and the speaker oscillates between asking to be pulled out and asking to be let drown, unable to decide which is the more honest request.","src":"haiku"},
"as-i-lay-dying~blinded":{"haiku":"The song examines pain-induced blindness and path-finding. Heartache causes one-sided perception; the only route out is choosing new actions and shifting from blind to blurry.","opus":"A hard look at pain-induced selfishness, blinded and trapped in a corner, perceiving hurt one-sidedly, then wrestling with whether people can truly change or are forever seen as they were. The answer lands on action and chosen paths over others' perception. As I Lay Dying, the California metalcore band, frame this arc of blame and possible redemption in their trademark clean-melody-versus-scream tension.","sonnet":"Selfishness examined after the damage is done — the speaker was so locked inside their own pain they couldn't see anyone else's, and the question now is whether the people around them believe change is possible or will always see them as the person who caused harm. The song is less an apology than an honest audit, ending on the distinction between how you're perceived and what you've actually committed to doing differently.","src":"haiku"},
"as-i-lay-dying~redefined":{"haiku":"Pain and suffering transform the self; scars become stronger than unblemished skin. The song insists that failure and misery, if endured, reshape the person into something more resilient.","opus":"An argument for keeping your wounds instead of erasing them, reframing today's suffering as the raw material of future resilience and failure as a refining fire. As I Lay Dying, the San Diego metalcore band, build a survivor's creed here, insisting that what mends can end up tougher than what was never broken.","sonnet":"Suffering as material rather than obstacle — the speaker refuses to erase pain because the pain is what built them. Healed scars are stronger than unbroken skin. The song argues that running from misery is its own trap, that staying inside the agony and converting it is the only honest path to becoming someone new. Failure is the forge, not the verdict.","src":"haiku"},
"as-i-may~cure-is-worse-than-disease":{"opus":"As I May confront suicidal urges through the image of a noose already fastened, weighing a choice between two painful surrenders. The narrator decides to go it alone and conceal his wounds, landing on the grim verdict that whether you resist or conceal yourself, the constant dangers and death still prevail.","sonnet":"About being caught between two painful choices with no clean exit — the narrator walks alone, hides their scars, and grapples with the fact that fear and silence may be as destructive as the disease itself, choosing their side despite knowing it will cost them.","src":"sonnet"},
"as-it-is-set-it-off-jordypurp~in-threes-explicit":{"opus":"A vivid portrait of depression and self-destruction, the narrator letting his mind burn down, unable to distinguish pain from pleasure, burying his feelings alive and choosing to survive on lies rather than die.","sonnet":"A song about deep depression and emotional numbness — the narrator is too apathetic to even try to fix themselves, burying feelings alive while acknowledging that death would be worse, trapped in a cycle of misery they can neither resolve nor escape.","src":"sonnet"},
"as-the-structure-fails~final-mistake":{"haiku":"Underground silence consumes while dissolution confronts the narrator with their mortality. Soil claims the body; carrying down marks the final descent into structured collapse.","opus":"A song narrated from beyond a suicide, the choice made and instantly regretted, fears becoming fate in the grave. The devastating pivot is envy of the living he once judged, and the unanswerable question of what he's done.","sonnet":"A voice already in the casket, underground and screaming without sound, contemplates the chain of fears, tears, and choices that led to this irreversible end — \"the choice was mine to make, my final fucking mistake.\" The wretched has replaced any prayer from above, leaving only self-judgment and the question of what was done.","src":"haiku"},
"as-the-structure-fails~the-surface":{"haiku":"Descending into psychic depths while yearning for unreachable surface; love offers no salvation as the speaker drowns in self-imposed isolation.","opus":"Drowning in your own head while the promise that love heals proves useless, because the thing hurting you is yourself. A metalcore cry from someone who keeps sinking past every supposed rock bottom.","sonnet":"A person drowning in their own mind, sinking past their supposed all-time low, unable to reach \"the surface I've been looking for\" no matter how far they sink. Love is proposed as the healer but immediately undercut: no one can seem to save them from themselves, and the plea — \"save me, I don't know what to do\" — receives no answer.","src":"haiku"},
"as-they-burn~unable-to-connect":{"haiku":"Internal demons fight for control and the speaker struggles to connect emotionally with themselves, caught between death and survival.","opus":"A portrait of severe emotional shutdown, where the singer feels dead to his own feelings and detached from everyone around him. Battling suicidal urges and the pull of despair, he insists he is still alive and tries to make peace with the destructive impulses lodged in his own mind.","sonnet":"A song about emotional dissociation and the demons that keep dragging someone toward the grave — the speaker insists they are not dead yet and chooses their own path even while their head is full of doubt and their inner life feels broken beyond repair.","src":"haiku"},
"asada-mami~joint":{"haiku":"Shared destiny binds two souls across darkness; mutual vision transcends confusion through unwavering commitment to forward movement beyond despair.","src":"haiku"},
"ascension-of-the-watchers~ghost-heart":{"opus":"A luminous, celestial love song comparing the beloved to a pale northern star whose light pierces the narrator's soul, blending romance with cosmic imagery and a shared sense of fate.","sonnet":"A devotional, awestruck love song using celestial imagery — the narrator describes being pierced and illuminated by someone whose heart functions like a distant, luminous star overhead, pure in its radiance.","src":"sonnet"},
"ashbury-heights~phantasmagoria":{"haiku":"Life has become a shapeshifting illusion where loss, memory, and phantom presences blur into a single haunting state. Empty chairs fill with spectral claims of the souls once possessed.","opus":"Grief turned hallucinatory: haunted by apparitions that all claim to be the lost soul, the mourner insists the chair is empty while life itself dissolves into unreal shadow, echoing Poe's bereaved obsession.","opusDeep":"A darkwave séance built almost entirely from Poe — 'gently tapping / On your chamber' and the answering 'nevermore' logic of 'That chair is empty now' lift 'The Raven' wholesale, while 'All I loved I loved alone' quotes 'Alone' directly. The controlling image is haunting-as-doubt: 'every ghastly apparition / Claims to be the soul I'm missing,' each ghost a false candidate for the one absence that matters, the speaker refusing every stand-in by insisting the chair stays empty. The chorus reframes the whole world as unreliable spectacle — 'Life is phantasmagoria now' — and then quietly relocates the haunting inward: 'all that's left is the stranger part of me,' the ghost turning out to be a dissociated self. 'Someone who bears my resemblance' answering 'I cannot tell you anything' seals it. What it's actually about: grief so total it dissolves the line between the lost beloved and the estranged, unrecognizable self left behind.","sonnet":"Grief has turned reality into a parade of ghosts: apparitions claim to be the soul the speaker is missing, shadows reach out, and only a stranger reflection remains — the empty chair is the central image of an absence that cannot be filled.","src":"haiku"},
"ashenspire~the-law-of-asbestos":{"haiku":"Capital's hierarchy poisons infrastructure while budgetary hammers drive systems to breaking. The irony of comfort contrasts with austerity's entanglement; one form of suffering replaces another.","opus":"Ashenspire deliver a fierce, articulate class attack built on the Grenfell fire, penny-pinching and predatory landlords killing the poor as a matter of policy. The refrain names the laborer as forever a hair's breadth from destitution and never anywhere near the top, describing a system rigged on purpose.","sonnet":"Austerity and capital's dominance over housing is indicted through the burning image of Grenfell — a corner cut, a penny saved, a fire that keeps recurring — as workers are positioned three months from the gutter by design, breathing asbestos while the system's rot and damp are not accidents but full intent. The title's dark irony runs throughout: the law protecting property destroys lives.","src":"haiku"},
"ashen~angel":{"haiku":"A seductive demon lover traps the speaker in inescapable torment, disguising cruelty as eternal devotion.","opus":"Love reframed as an obsessive parasitism, one lover clinging like a haunting no exit can shake. The dagger-and-flower image sets tenderness against harm, ending on the fool who adored his own destruction.","sonnet":"Loving someone who is beautiful and parasitic at once — dagger in one hand, flower in the other; the angel is also the fool's downfall: \"fell in love with the villain / that's what killed him.\" The narrator keeps coming back anyway, making them immortal like a priest to an idol, because being scared to die alone is worse than being devoured.","src":"haiku"},
"ashen~chimera":{"haiku":"Fractured identity and violent impulse emerge from years of cruelty, where the speaker embodies chaos and reclaims monstrosity as their weapon.","opus":"A bullied outcast splits into two selves, a personal devil named Chimera who takes over and turns the tormentors' cruelty back on them with a blade. The Sweeney Todd nod and the plea to have his name screamed make persecution curdle into a vengeful, half-human alter ego he can no longer let go of.","sonnet":"About a bullied or outcast person who has split into a second self — the Chimera is the inner persona that takes over when the world is full of vicious people, a blade-wielding alter ego who threatens to escalate, calls for the head of tormentors, and dances with violence until the original self can no longer hold them back.","src":"haiku"},
"ashen~desire":{"haiku":"Fear and desire fuse in a supernatural or erotic encounter where the speaker begs to be haunted, held, and chained by molten longing — crippled by doubt but consumed by the need to give everything.","opus":"Wanting someone so completely it curdles into a fear you might be conjuring them from your own need. The song sits in that dangerous overlap of worship, lust and doubt, warning the beloved to stay away for their own safety.","sonnet":"Obsession as haunting: a figure in the dark, love turned blind, passion deaf and reason tongue-tied — the speaker begs to be bound in ropes of light, wants the object of desire whole, but the closer they get the bigger the doubt, until the chimera created by the mind becomes indistinguishable from the real thing and the only safe instruction is stay away.","src":"haiku"},
"ashen~hidden":{"haiku":"Identity concealment behind protective masks worn to survive and fit in, leading to a loss of self trapped within lies and pretense.","opus":"An examination of hiding one's true self behind masks and lies, losing identity to fit in like a chameleon out of fear of rejection. The narrator pleads to tear down the mask, escape the self-made prison, and finally reveal who he really is.","sonnet":"A call to shed the masks and false identities people are conditioned to wear, recognising that self-deception and fear of rejection keep individuals imprisoned in chameleon-like performances instead of authentic selves.","src":"haiku"},
"ashen~nowhere":{"haiku":"Apocalypse unfolds as normalized catastrophe while the speaker runs toward predetermined ruin; no escape exists, only falling together while watching the sun extinguish.","opus":"Collective doom under swords already hanging over us, aimed at the children who inherit the wreckage. The band turns climate and civilizational dread into a resigned communal descent, everyone falling as one while the sun goes dark.","sonnet":"A song about collective civilisational collapse — stuck in a runaway train, roots in tar, cathedrals turned to dust — the refrain \"we're just going nowhere\" naming the shared destination while silver spoons dig their own graves and the sun shuts down.","src":"haiku"},
"ashen~outlier":{"haiku":"A portrait of alienation and invisibility. The speaker is a social outcast tormented for being different, gradually becoming consumed by rage and darkness, ultimately threatening to make others understand their pain.","opus":"Ashen follow an alienated misfit, branded a creep and abandoned by an indifferent confidant, spiraling through pills, fury and self-disgust. He sees himself as the odd one out, boxed inside his own skull with no way out and no one who understands.","sonnet":"The song voices the pain of someone who feels perpetually misunderstood, medicated, and cast out — labelled a freak and a weird kid, dismissed and unseen — while expressing a burning rage and a desperate wish to finally be felt or remembered even in death.","src":"haiku"},
"ashen~sacrifice":{"haiku":"Demons and darkness breed within; the speaker contemplates violent escape as their design guarantees only suffering.","opus":"An unflinching portrait of mental illness and suicidal ideation, a mind that was never a home, demons treated as his own children. It sits with self-hatred and the pull toward the rope or gun, honest that survival is a daily, brutal fight.","sonnet":"A song about mental illness and self-medication spiraling into crisis — the speaker woke up covered in blood and cannot tell whether they are awake or dead, has internalized demons they treat like dependents, and oscillates between self-loathing, substance use, and the dawning conviction that something is fundamentally wrong with them.","src":"haiku"},
"ashen~sapiens":{"haiku":"A person rejected by a hollow, violent society demands solitude, describing societal indifference and depravity in stark terms.","opus":"A spiraling account of being surrounded by hollow, indifferent people while dread and self-doubt corrode from within. The circus of clowns and vultures externalizes a paranoia that may be entirely internal, survival as grim endurance.","sonnet":"A person crushed by indifferent, hollow people who seem almost interspecies in their cruelty — the speaker questions whether they share the same humanity, and the song circles between rage, exhaustion, and a desperate wish to be set free.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~a-wish":{"haiku":"A speaker hides potential and withdraws from intimacy due to shame and alienation; they wish they could access beauty within others but remain trapped beneath surface. The song laments inability to access or receive love.","opus":"Billy Howerdel's confession of a self buried by pride and self-sabotage, pushing away everyone who loves him. He mourns the better man he could have been and quietly envies the openness he sees in others.","sonnet":"About deeply buried self-worth — the narrator hid a better version of themselves away, pushes away anyone who loves them, and wishes they could access the beauty others seem to see in them.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~defamed":{"haiku":"A person attempting personal transformation faces constant pressure from followers demanding prophecy and meaning. They become trapped by others' expectations.","opus":"Ashes Divide sketch a disgraced figure ringed by flatterers who grovel and fawn while dreading exposure, someone straining to reform yet never permitted to by the yes-men clinging to his every remark. The close insists the listener owes such a person nothing. The project of A Perfect Circle's Billy Howerdel brings brooding, layered alt-metal to a study of denial and poisoned loyalty.","sonnet":"A person trying to change while surrounded by people who need them to stay the same — sycophants who press close and flatter, who would rather the speaker remain fixed and legible than grow. The observer watches from outside, face to glass, aware of the trap. The final turn is a release: you don't owe them anything, not even the version of yourself they've been feeding on.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~denial-waits":{"haiku":"A person clings to a failed relationship through denial, hoping the other will return despite inevitable pain.","opus":"A portrait of someone trapped in a ruinous love, willing to descend into anguish on the slim hope the lover returns. Even while reassuring themselves things will work out, they confess they'd welcome the person back and act as though the desertion never occurred, self-deception poised to consume them both.","sonnet":"A post-breakup reckoning with the humiliating truth that despite knowing the relationship was destructive, the narrator would still take the person back the moment they appeared.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~enemies":{"haiku":"Two people locked in mutual torment choose to become enemies to end their relationship, each projecting blame while struggling with tolerance and discomfort.","opus":"A portrait of a corrosive, self-punishing relationship built on passive aggression and impossible expectations, where the narrator would rather become enemies and stand alone than keep suffering the other's lashing out.","sonnet":"A deteriorating intimate relationship in which neither person can be what the other imagines: one projects perfection, the other knows it's false, and both end up proposing that becoming enemies is the only honest way to watch the illusion finally go dark.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~forever-can-be":{"haiku":"Grief over someone who carried the world's pain alone and ultimately chose to escape it, leaving behind those who now bear the burden of their absence.","opus":"Billy Howerdel, apart from A Perfect Circle, addresses someone who never learned to accept himself and shoulders the whole world's suffering in isolation. His layered rock cradles the ache, hoping the person can find lasting belief while gently suggesting that endlessly rescuing others is really an evasion of rescuing oneself.","sonnet":"A meditation on someone who spent a lifetime saving others to avoid saving himself — carrying the world's grief as armour against his own. Those left behind reckon with his inability to believe in lasting love or peace, and mourn what was never found.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~ritual":{"haiku":"Two people locked in a pattern of mutual alienation and wounding, where their falling away has become ritualized routine despite recognition of the harm.","opus":"Billy Howerdel, in his post-A Perfect Circle solo project, traces a couple whose mutual cruelty has calcified into habit; drifting apart is now the only thing they reliably do together. Amid his lush atmospherics, the speaker keeps begging for one honest moment before the relationship pulls them both under.","sonnet":"Two people trapped in a relationship of wounding words and studied distance — the arguments have become ritual, the silence a wall they stare through like strangers. One pleads for grounding, the other wonders whether the damage can ever be undone.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~stripped-away":{"haiku":"A child stripped of something irretrievable struggles to please someone who deems them barely acceptable, until they refuse to kneel and finally turn away from that cycle.","opus":"Billy Howerdel voicing someone conditioned by a manipulator into tolerating abuse for scraps of approval, a wounded inner child finally refusing and turning away; reclaiming a self that was taken piece by piece.","sonnet":"A song about childhood sexual abuse and the permanent theft it represents — something taken by force that can never be restored, leaving the survivor oscillating between suppression and the belated refusal to keep tolerating it, finally turning away from the person who did it.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~sword":{"haiku":"Love delivered through domination poisons both giver and receiver; the speaker seeks to interrupt cycles of shame and elitism through mutual recognition.","opus":"A stand against someone peddling love that's really superiority and toxic pride, attempting to put a human face on an adversary who writes the singer off as worthless. The vow is to refuse being muzzled and to rescue even those who insist they want no rescue.","sonnet":"A rebuke to someone spreading ideology or religion through force and elitism — the narrator wants to silence them but recognizes a shared humanity, ultimately asking to be seen as more than what they have been condemned to be.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~sword-danny-lohner-remix":{"haiku":"Critique of violent ideology and pride masquerading as love and truth. The speaker seeks to humanize enemies while acknowledging shared stain, begging for freedom from imposed silence.","opus":"An address to a self-righteous zealot who spreads harm while certain of their own goodness, the narrator torn between wanting to silence and to save them. Billy Howerdel makes pride the poison, love and condemnation both cutting from the same blade.","sonnet":"The song speaks to someone who spreads their beliefs through force or coercion, urging them to recognize that both parties are cut from the same flawed cloth—stained by pride. Rather than being silenced by those who consider them worthless, the appeal is to find something greater than violent resolve.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~the-prey":{"haiku":"Institutional manipulation and fear keep people compliant, but the narrator urges rejection of enforced shame and guilt. Personal liberation comes from refusing to suffer by others' terms.","opus":"An anti-authoritarian, anti-religious rallying cry against those who pretend to be figures we should fear while caressing our souls away and keeping us in line through guilt and false promises; the narrator insists you can survive their excommunication, refuse the imposed suffering, and rise.","sonnet":"A rejection of religious institutions that use guilt and fear to control people — calling out manipulative authority figures who prey on the faithful and urging the listener to walk away without shame.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~the-stone":{"haiku":"Humans endure what they cannot change by pretending and letting go, metaphorically trapped like flowers unable to break through stone.","opus":"A song about the draining effort of suppressing pain and performing okayness, and the feeling of being stunted, unable to grow past a hard barrier. The speaker longs to quit the pretense and simply experience one honest, genuine emotion.","sonnet":"A song about emotional suppression and the futility of pretending pain away — the narrator pushes hardship aside and feigns acceptance, but remains trapped like flowers unable to break through stone.","src":"haiku"},
"ashes-divide~too-late":{"haiku":"A speaker tries to separate himself from his own destructive nature but clings to someone he loves, promising not to lose them even to darkness and machinery.","opus":"Billy Howerdel's post-A Perfect Circle project pairs self-revulsion with fierce devotion, the narrator wishing he could escape a self he despises yet vowing unshakable protection of the one he loves. Its real drive is that promise of guardianship, refusing to surrender the beloved to any of the terrors he lists, staying present through all of them.","sonnet":"A narrator who recognises his own damage — and would keep distance from himself if he could — pledges total protection against a world of careless threats. The self-awareness of personal flaw sits alongside absolute commitment, framing guardianship as both the only redemption on offer and the one thing he will not abandon.","src":"haiku"},
"ashnikko~cry-feat-grimes":{"haiku":"Raw anger at betrayal; unstable and dangerous when provoked, warning him not to push.","opus":"A woman refusing to be the one broken down by a careless partner, weaponizing her own volatility as a warning. The rage is genuine but the swagger is armor over how close she actually is to tears.","sonnet":"Rage and hurt pushed to the edge — a woman who has been manipulated and provoked by a toxic partner channels her fury into a menacing declaration that she will not be made to cry, and that pushing her further will turn the situation gruesome.","src":"haiku"},
"ash~clones-2011-remaster":{"haiku":"Conformity erases individuality as the human becomes indistinguishable from the mass-produced; the speaker mourns the death of uniqueness.","opus":"Disillusionment with someone who once seemed singular selling out into conformity, the meteoric individual reduced to a processed drone. The betrayal is that the person he believed stood apart turned out to be just like everyone else.","sonnet":"A disappointed speaker watches someone they believed to be unique and true surrender their individuality and become indistinguishable from the conformist mass — the loss framed as self-destruction, the promised prophecy of what they could have been now gone.","src":"haiku"},
"asian-glow~out-of-time":{"haiku":"Late-night stillness marks temporal dissolution; the narrator cannot break from silence or reach others. Emptiness stretches boundlessly as connection fails; isolation becomes the only certainty.","opus":"A Korean bedroom-emo project sketching depression as damp, stagnant air in a cramped room; the hand reaching out and only waving is the ache of trying to connect while the drought of feeling closes in.","sonnet":"In the damp of a pale room in the small hours, the speaker reaches out again and again but only waves farewell — something has been receding forever, and now the drought that once seemed distant closes in. The Korean text maps emotional paralysis onto grey stillness and fading paper, ending with the recognition that what was far away has finally arrived.","src":"haiku"},
"asian-glow~the-flag-is-raised":{"haiku":"Spiritual awakening transforms suffering into gold; the divine feminine illuminates consciousness and elevates the soul toward majesty and transcendent purpose.","opus":"A surge of hard-won transcendence where suffering finally lifts and everything turns gold, the hero located inside the self rather than any conquest. Korean bedroom-emo artist Asian Glow reaching, uncharacteristically, toward light and a guiding Maria.","sonnet":"A euphoric spiritual surge — the flag raised, everything turning gold, suffering ceasing, Maria's light shining — in which the speaker, a mere shell, defers to the soul as the real hero and insists the feeling must be caressed and kept within. Victory framed as sacred interiority rather than external triumph.","src":"haiku"},
"asian-kung-fu-generation~after-dark":{"haiku":"A figure flees through darkness chasing fragile hope, wings disintegrating unnoticed. The world resists motion; despite exhaustion and loss, they sing their defiance into the void, refusing to surrender before the final push.","opus":"Fleeing in the shrinking gap of his own shadow, the singer flies on a faint hope carried by the night wind while the world shakes to strip everything away; a blood-red moon rolls dice over aimless days he wants to cut off, and the refrain 'the dream is over, but we've still done nothing—move forward' drives him on.","sonnet":"A speaker slips away in the shadow-gap before dawn, flying on shed wings toward a faint hope carried by the night wind, only to have the world shake and strip everything away — the dream has ended but nothing has yet been done, so the imperative \"advance\" cuts through the hopelessness at the close.","src":"haiku"},
"asian-kung-fu-generation~blood-circulator":{"haiku":"Passion once burned bright now dims; the speaker retrieves old fire, choosing action over stagnation. Connection regenerates purpose despite endless cycles; bonds justify persistent struggle.","opus":"A refusal to let a former passion die, urging someone drowning in loneliness to keep walking and not harm themselves. This veteran Japanese rock band pours heart's blood into reclaiming the fire of who they once were, insisting the darkness isn't deep enough to give up in.","sonnet":"A solitary figure lost somewhere in the city traces a voice's location while swallowing sighs, determined to reclaim the passionate self of before — \"pour heart's blood into it, recover those burning days\" — and turns to address someone directly: don't stop moving, don't give up hope, don't hurt yourself, turn around.","src":"haiku"},
"asian-kung-fu-generation~haruka-kanata":{"haiku":"Accelerating forward without hesitation, the speaker pierces night to carve away daily erosion. The heart opens to pull everything close; what reaches will surely transmit across distance.","opus":"The Naruto-era rush of flooring the accelerator toward someone far away, made famous as an anime opener; its real charge is that speed and effort mean nothing unless it reaches the one person you're chasing.","sonnet":"Pressing the accelerator with no room for negotiation, the speaker rushes through the night toward someone — tangled feet, breathless urgency — because without that person nothing has meaning. The call is to open the heart and pull close, to take and claim and keep racing far, far away together.","src":"haiku"},
"asian-kung-fu-generation~re-re":{"haiku":"A reflection on unfulfilled promises and the pain of separation, where memories can't be held and the narrator keeps scratching at wounds that won't heal.","opus":"A rerecorded staple of the Japanese rock band's catalogue, this waiting song aches with someone clawing at their own chest beneath a railway overpass, tormented that memory and form both erode with time. The narrator regrets words left unspoken and a bond now severed, keeping a wound deliberately open because no one else will do.","sonnet":"A narrator waits for someone who is already gone, clawing at the memory of a bond that once felt total. Days dissolve without returning anything, and the wound he keeps reopening is the only proof the connection was ever real.","src":"haiku"},
"asian-kung-fu-generation~rewrite-2016-rerecorded":{"haiku":"Desires manifest through proof of existence; the narrator's future oscillates between dignity and freedom. Contradiction besets the righteous mission to erase remnants and reclaim what twisted fate consumed.","opus":"About needing to prove you exist by spitting out your grating self-doubt, then rewriting the whole self-conscious mess; the very worthlessness you want to erase turns out to be what drives you toward another person.","sonnet":"Unable to prove existence any other way, the speaker wants to erase and rewrite the distorted self-image, the worthless fantasies, and the calendar with no dates — cutting away the naïve emotions, weeping at ordinariness, demanding a resurrection through full-body, full-soul effort. The rotten heart and petty lies must be deleted so that even meaningless imagination can become a driving force.","src":"haiku"},
"asian-kung-fu-generation~to-your-town-album-version":{"haiku":"Twilight precedes moonlight as the speaker's delicate heart embraces all sentiment. They sing toward your street; tomorrow's wings will save unreliable friends from their dependence.","opus":"A song of fragile encouragement carrying its own timid narrator's fears toward someone else's town; the wavering, unreliable you might yet become tomorrow's wings, and being visible matters only while you keep flapping.","sonnet":"A song made to fly to someone's town on sadness and longing alone — the narrator wonders if even an unreliable, nearby figure might one day become tomorrow's wings to save others. Though they can't reach the shining beyond, they won't vanish while their wings are still beating, as seasons colour and pass and the city dances in winter light.","src":"haiku"},
"asian-kung-fu-generation~well-then-see-you-again-tomorrow":{"haiku":"Transparent sadness freezes while brightness persists; death and afterlife blur as phantoms. Shared voices seek resonance; the sound drums call transcendence through collective wailing.","opus":"A cold, dissociated meditation on mob mentality and scapegoating, where a crowd counts off to expel the outsider; underneath the casual sign-off lies grief for the ordinary days everyone quietly conspired to lose.","sonnet":"Ghosts and grief circle \"today\" — what he sought, what she shed, what you lamented — while ordinary days feel like something hidden or lost, and the refrain \"well then, see you again tomorrow\" offers hollow normalcy as the only response. The community gradually turns on its own perceived heretic in a slow, compliant dance of conformity.","src":"haiku"},
"asinis~my-child":{"opus":"A parent's tender address to a newborn, pouring all their energy, memories, and flaws into the child so it can grow beyond them; framed as passing on the story of life, it ends with the wistful hope that the child might one day rescue the world.","sonnet":"A parent addresses a newborn child with overwhelming love and hope — passing down memories, thoughts, faults, and regrets as gifts, asking the child to surpass them and find a way to save the world that the parent could not.","src":"sonnet"},
"asking-alexandria~alone-in-a-room":{"haiku":"A person battles internal chaos and self-destructive thoughts through isolation, needing solitude to confront and articulate their fractured self.","opus":"Recovery reckoning about facing yourself: the thing you least want, solitude and honest confrontation, turns out to be the thing you need. Written from the far side of addiction, it trades self-pity for hard-won resolve.","sonnet":"A song about the therapeutic necessity of confronting yourself in isolation — the narrator spent years running from his own mental noise until he had no choice but to sit alone, say every awful thing aloud, and discover that forced solitude was the beginning of healing.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~antisocialist":{"haiku":"Hates the intrusion of other people's existence; isolation feels like the only truth.","opus":"Danny Worsnop's raw misanthropy and burnout, medicating himself just to escape a world of people demanding something from him. Beneath the venom is exhaustion and a wish to be left completely alone.","sonnet":"A speaker who craves solitude and finds human interaction physically repellent addresses the world directly — every social obligation feels like an intrusion, and the song is an unapologetic, hostile request for everyone to stay away.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~dark-void":{"haiku":"Nightmares consume consciousness while the speaker sinks deeper into self-imprisonment; nothing can fill the void they've become.","opus":"Addiction and depression as a nightmare you keep choosing to fall back into, begging an outside savior for the hope you can't generate yourself. Written after the band's own recovery struggles, it's the sound of clawing toward a light that keeps receding.","sonnet":"The speaker is trapped in a self-repeating nightmare of mental collapse — scratching the surface to find purpose, falling apart every time they try to stop, begging a saviour to drag them out of the dark void while acknowledging they keep failing themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~empire-feat-bingx":{"haiku":"Despite falling skies and adversity, the narrator stands tall building their empire before ashes fall; time and determination breed success.","opus":"Asking Alexandria weave a hip-hop cadence into their metalcore for an anthem of unshakable self-belief, treating life as combat and ambition as legacy. The narrator vows to build something lasting in whatever time he has, standing firm through hardship and refusing to be knocked down no matter what collapses around him.","sonnet":"Relentless ambition framed as survival — grinding past adversity and building something lasting before the moment expires. Two voices insist on identity forged through effort rather than circumstance, measuring worth by resilience against collapse and vowing to erect an empire even as the ashes are still falling.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~eve":{"haiku":"A lover holds forbidden knowledge from their partner, manipulating their innocence while justifying control as protective, exploring the power dynamics of trust broken.","opus":"A retelling of Eden from the tempter's mouth, sin itself murmuring to an innocent woman that the man ruling her life is hoarding wonder because he thinks she's unworthy of it. The seductive argument, that he gives little while taking much, twists temptation into a case for freedom, coaxing her to reach for the forbidden and shatter his trust.","sonnet":"A retelling of Eden from the serpent's perspective — the tempter makes a genuinely compelling case that God withholds knowledge to keep Eve subordinate, frames obedience as captivity, and encourages her to take the fruit as an act of deserved freedom, knowing full well the consequences for them both.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~hopelessly-hopeful":{"haiku":"A person feels suspended and numb in a world that promised meaning but delivered conformity. They are conscious that something is wrong yet cannot escape the cycle of repetition.","opus":"Reunited-era Asking Alexandria dig into emotional numbness and disenchantment, the narrator merely coasting through a life that never delivered what it promised. Suspended in a dull, unsatisfying existence, he grasps at a stubborn, almost naive hope that he isn't permanently stuck where he is.","sonnet":"Functional paralysis masquerades as a normal life. Going through motions, numb to routine, the narrator cannot stop keeping on even though nothing feels right — suspended between the world as it is and a vague conviction that it should be otherwise. The hope is not optimism but the inability to fully surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~i-am-one":{"haiku":"The speaker declares complete self-reliance and independence, having learned early that only they can be trusted. They reject love and connection, standing alone among others like the father-and-son united in singular existence.","opus":"Self-reliance hardened into isolation: a narrator who can hold someone close yet never let them in, insisting all he ever had was himself. The defiant chant of oneness reads as armor, the loneliness leaking through every refusal of love.","sonnet":"A declaration of radical self-reliance built from the scar tissue of abandonment — the narrator learned early that only they could be counted on, and has become so deeply sealed in self-sufficiency that love from others feels not just unwanted but irrelevant.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~into-the-fire":{"haiku":"Defiant acceptance of self-destruction; the speaker refuses redemption or change, finding solace only in the certainty of their own annihilation.","opus":"Someone who's made peace with never changing, finding comfort in pain and refusing to undo a single wretched moment. Stepping into the flames is chosen self-immolation dressed as freedom by a mind that calls itself unhinged and lost.","sonnet":"Radical self-acceptance of one's worst qualities — the narrator is masochistic, paranoid, narcissistic by their own admission, and has come to peace with it; they would not undo a single moment of suffering because the pain is the only thing that makes them feel alive.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~into-the-fire-radio-edit":{"haiku":"The speaker accepts his own madness and self-destruction, finding solace in pain and darkness. He walks into fire deliberately, refusing to change and embracing his role as chaos incarnate.","opus":"A portrait of someone who has stopped resisting his own worst instincts and made resentment part of who he is. He accepts that he'll always be a paranoid, self-loathing mess and decides that's fine, choosing to burn himself out completely rather than regret a single grim chapter of the life he built.","sonnet":"A self-aware embrace of one's own worst nature — the narrator knows he's paranoid, masochistic, narcissistic, and incapable of change, finds a perverse solace in darkness and pain, and walks deliberately toward destruction rather than away from it.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~killing-you":{"haiku":"Romantic destruction spirals into mutual annihilation as the speaker recognizes that loving him slowly poisons his partner; departure becomes the final mercy.","opus":"A three-year relationship the narrator must end because staying is destroying them both, guilt curdling as he wrecks someone too kind to leave. Set against young love in Texas gone cold, the mercy of goodbye is also a confession of his own ruin.","sonnet":"A man ends a relationship not out of cruelty but because his own corruption and emotional death are destroying the person who keeps forgiving him, confessing that staying would only continue the damage and that setting her free is the only loving thing left he can do.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~moving-on":{"haiku":"A man broken by his own success withdraws from a life built on performance and dissatisfaction, choosing solitude and distance over the hollow acclaim that once defined him.","opus":"A near-autobiographical portrait of the band's frontman, a boy of anguish worn down by the road and hollowed by success, deciding he can't do this anymore. Moving on reads as both escape and quiet dread of what leaving costs.","sonnet":"A tortured artist trades personal misery for a life on the road, only to find himself crushed by the weight of the journey and the man he has become. He is simultaneously exhausted and refusing to stop, caught between the impossibility of continuing and the inability to give up.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~not-the-american-average":{"haiku":"Desire reduces to predatory conquest; the speaker objectifies sexuality, chasing sensation while acknowledging emptiness beneath performance.","opus":"A brutal, misogynistic account of an anonymous one-night stand souring into contempt and rage. Under the crude bravado is a man who can't stomach one more letdown, every encounter dead-ending in the same messy, bitter way.","sonnet":"A one-night encounter narrated with aggressive sexual directness, then pivoting into something rawer: the speaker admits one step too late they should have said they couldn't handle another disappointment, the night dissolving into a \"messy ending\" — desire and emotional failure collapsing into each other.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~reckless-relentless":{"haiku":"Excess and self-destruction become art; the speaker refuses restraint, burning through life at maximum intensity.","opus":"Debauchery as identity, the young band mythologizing their own excess of drink, drugs and burnout as rock and roll at its finest. The bookend rant at fake bands reveals the bravado is also a defense, a refusal to admit the cost or slow down.","sonnet":"A self-proclaimed embodiment of rock and roll excess — drinking, drugs, girls, destruction — delivered with contemptuous swagger toward anyone who doesn't live as hard, while dismissing consequences and declaring that this pace will continue until the body gives out. Beneath the bravado is a defense of authenticity in a scene the speaker regards as fraudulent.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~rise-up":{"haiku":"Reclamation of agency from darkness: the speaker finds resolve within suffering and darkness, rising against inherited hatred and anger to reclaim control as a purged outsider.","opus":"Asking Alexandria's more anthemic era turns inner blackness into a call to seize command again. The narrator draws strength from his mind's grimmest recess, meeting hard truths by himself and accepting the outcast's part in someone else's private struggle. Forged from spite and fury and a life kept outside, the chorus vows to climb clear of the dark with the world on his shoulders.","sonnet":"Having lived on the outside — shaped by hatred and bitterness — a person claims the act of rising as a deliberate, defiant choice. He carries the full weight of that marginalized history and purges it through sheer will, reclaiming agency from darkness rather than waiting for circumstances to change.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~room-138":{"haiku":"A 23-year-old lies dying from injuries, experiencing visions of a compassionate presence offering forgiveness and rest. They transition from agony to acceptance as they're held and told everything is alright.","opus":"A near-death experience narrated from the floor of a room (evoking an overdose or collapse), with breaking ribs, boiling blood and fading light; ready for the end, the narrator glimpses a forgiving face offering a second chance and comfort, and pleads to be pulled out of this brush with death.","sonnet":"A near-death experience narrated in real time — the narrator is lying on a floor at 23, heart slamming, ribs breaking, possibly overdosing, convinced they're about to die, until a vision or person pulls them back and offers a second chance.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~someone-somewhere":{"haiku":"A young musician honors those who believed in them during isolation, expressing gratitude to distant prayer and the hope that sustains journeys homeward.","opus":"A homesick musician's letter to family, half apology and half reassurance, carrying the weight of a lost loved one who first believed in him. Touring success set against the ache of wanting a home to call his own.","sonnet":"A touring musician writes to the people back home — a parent figure who believed in them, a family member they drifted from — confessing guilt and gratitude, finding comfort in the knowledge that someone out there is still praying for their safe return.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~the-death-of-me":{"haiku":"Psychological fragmentation and suicidal ideation battle against refusal to surrender to external destruction or manipulation.","opus":"A spiral through the question of one's own sanity, voices and demons that won't leave, resolving into a refusal to let another person be the ruin of him. The turn from self-doubt to defiant blame lands on someone who brought their own destruction on themselves.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped in a mental hell of intrusive voices, self-doubt, and rage — devil already here, no need to travel to hell — who refuses to let someone else be the cause of their destruction while confessing the pain that hole has left where a heart should have been.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~under-denver":{"haiku":"Hidden powers observe and control humanity while children dream in forced solitude. The song warns of surrender to systems of control and acceptance of predetermined fate.","opus":"Asking Alexandria dress conspiracy-theory paranoia as a lullaby, soothing the masses to sleep while a hidden overseer watches, and instructing everyone to obey, consume and trust the authorities. The gentle reassurance reads as scorn, a bitter portrait of a population lulled into surrender while a mysterious refuge summons the few who might rebuild alone.","sonnet":"Beneath the reassurance of surrender lies a coercive system. Children are told to dream in peace while a hidden authority holds the keys and demands compliance. The song's surface calm — accept what is, follow the signal from Under Denver — masks a portrait of total control dressed as comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~vultures":{"haiku":"A person is consumed by predatory forces in their life, depicted as vultures draining their vitality and leaving them unable to act, questioning whether to fight back or surrender to death.","opus":"Danny Worsnop, worn down and embittered after Asking Alexandria's rocky years, probes a gutted spirit as scavengers pick him clean, torn between fleeing and giving up entirely. The metalcore anthem recasts industry burnout as being a used-up pawn bled dry, ending on a plea for a single sign and a place just to breathe.","sonnet":"Surrounded by industry predators draining him of energy and autonomy, a spent and jaded figure weighs whether to run or surrender. Older and bitter, he searches for any sign of direction while his grip on purpose grows weaker with each passing cycle.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~when-the-lights-come-on":{"haiku":"Anticipation builds as crowds gather; when the performance ignites, the roof lifts and energy reaches the stratosphere.","opus":"Asking Alexandria bottle the concert adrenaline that made their name, all anticipation and spectacle as an impatient crowd waits for the show to ignite. The song is almost entirely about that rush, a hymn to the charged instant between waiting and release when band and audience finally meet.","sonnet":"The electric charge of a live concert — anticipation in the crowd building until the first lights hit and the room erupts. The lyric is entirely about that threshold moment when collective energy transforms into something almost transcendent, the roof-raising euphoria of thousands surrendering to sound at once.","src":"haiku"},
"asking-alexandria~where-did-it-go":{"haiku":"An artist reflects on their legacy of boundary-pushing work across multiple albums while witnessing the complacency of those who followed them, expressing frustration that something essential has been lost in newer music and culture.","opus":"Danny Worsnop turns Asking Alexandria's ten-year discography into ammunition, boasting about record after record while sneering at imitators who peaked and grew stale. The track reeks of scene-king arrogance, the singer crowning himself the innovator everyone else copies and daring his rivals to admit they were nothing without him.","sonnet":"A veteran band member surveys a music world that has grown complacent and derivative — imitating records he helped write — and erupts in furious self-assertion. He chronicles his own discography as a series of firsts no one else has matched, demanding attention.","src":"haiku"},
"asleep-at-the-helm~doom":{"haiku":"Truth-telling as violence and refusal as liberation animate the speaker's declaration of independence, rejecting complicity with another's self-deception.","opus":"A furious severing from a manipulative, self-pitying person the speaker refuses to become, exposing their lies and casting the first stone at their glass walls. The impending doom promised is the consequence of their own arrogance and violence, the speaker done listening and ready to cut them out.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone hiding behind victimhood and lies: the speaker sees through the glass walls, demands the truth they have been denied, and enumerates a parasite's arrogance, negligence, and violence — ending emotionless, anticipating the impending doom as a consequence the other brought upon themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"asmodai~nightmare":{"haiku":"The speaker exists as a god or demon within another's mind, representing both the source of their nightmares and their tormented existence.","opus":"The band chart a dissolving sense of self, the narrator adrift in the boundless expanse of his own mind, calling himself a phantom in the mechanism and his own undoing. Even blind and despairing, he claims divinity over an illusory world, fusing cosmic dread with self-erasure so the mind's collapse reads as both terror and a dark absolute god.","sonnet":"A figure that may be a dream, a delusion, or a predatory psyche declares itself both God and nightmare — the inescapable inner authority that haunts the mind at its most defenceless. Lost in mental fog, the speaker cannot tell whether the darkness originates outside or within.","src":"haiku"},
"assassin~fight-to-stop-the-tyranny":{"haiku":"Inhumane minds control while ideologies steal freedom; the crowd rises to destroy hierarchical order. The fight becomes both declaration and prophecy of liberation through violent upheaval.","opus":"Blunt thrash-punk agitation calling for uprising against any regime that steals freedom, lumping every authoritarian creed together; the politics are muddled but the raw impulse is riot as the price of living free.","sonnet":"An anthem of insurrection against Marxists, Nazis, and communists who have stolen freedom, urging the crowd to destroy the rulers' tyranny through riot and strike, spending all their energy to kill the kings of the world. The polemic is blunt and symmetrical — fight for right, fight for freedom, fight to stop tyranny.","src":"haiku"},
"astrale~fine-alright":{"haiku":"Someone performs wellness publicly—claiming they're fine—while secretly sad, trapped in a past they won't confront, disconnected from the pain they hide beneath smiles.","opus":"A quiet portrait of someone insisting they're okay while betraying themselves, listening to sad songs and swallowing what they'll never say. The gap between the reassurance and the truth is the whole song.","sonnet":"About the performance of being okay when you're not — someone detached from their own life, clinging to the past, saying \"fine\" while privately listening to sad songs and going through things they'll never voice aloud.","src":"haiku"},
"astrale~see":{"haiku":"Disconnection and blindness plague society; the speaker cannot read faces or stand on their own feet, longing for freedom and clarity. The world overthinks and stumbles—trust yourself instead.","opus":"A modern anxiety about disconnection and being unreadable to others, urging self-trust and a step back from screens and overthinking, the refrain of not knowing how to see doubling as a plea to actually be perceived.","sonnet":"A person struggling with self-perception and disconnection — unable to stand on their feet, wanting to be free, uncertain whether anyone can read what's behind their face — ultimately reaching toward the simple truth that the brain is still there and the world doesn't have to feel like a dream.","src":"haiku"},
"astraline~ghost-in-the-shell":{"haiku":"Trauma creates an impenetrable shell; the speaker invites others to witness the void, knowing no one can survive the nightmare they've inhabited.","opus":"Astraline flip constant betrayal into a grim armor, the speaker urging his tormentor to keep driving the knife in since every fresh wound means nothing now that he's gone numb. An empty shape surrounded by rings of torment, he challenges them to outlast the emptiness that already devoured him.","sonnet":"Addressed to someone who keeps stabbing despite the speaker's indifference to more scars — 'one more scar in my heart makes no difference, cause now I'm healed' — the song dares the attacker to step into the nightmare void that consumed the speaker to their bones, betting they won't survive it.","src":"haiku"},
"astrid-s~breathe":{"haiku":"Romantic presence overwhelms physical function; the beloved dissolves boundaries and renders the speaker breathless and alive.","opus":"New infatuation rendered as literal breathlessness, a crush so consuming it feels like oxygen deprivation. The Norwegian pop singer leans into the giddy helplessness of wanting someone until your own body forgets how to function.","sonnet":"A new infatuation has overwhelmed the speaker's body and sense of equilibrium: one week in, this person has taken up total residence in her head, making her forget to breathe every time she sees them, the feeling described as being lifted into space without a helmet — intoxicating and slightly terrifying.","src":"haiku"},
"astronoid~a-new-color":{"haiku":"A speaker loses their world and a photograph of love; they watch as hope abandons everyone, leaving them with light and music alone as witness to ruin.","opus":"A dreamlike reckoning with loss finds the speaker clinging to a wonder that keeps slipping away, watching eyes and starlight even as hope threatens to abandon everyone. Astronoid, a Boston band whose blastbeats carry major-key euphoria rather than gloom, frame this drift toward oblivion as something luminous, so grief and awe blur into the same shimmering ache without ever settling into despair.","sonnet":"A narrator grasps at something wondrous only to lose it instantly, then watches as light and hope drain away from a world already slipping from his grip. The experience of love torn from a photograph becomes a wound that persists beneath the stars, unreachable and bright with absence.","src":"haiku"},
"astronoid~eyes":{"haiku":"Disassembly and reconstruction render identity uncertain as the speaker sheds thought to navigate by instinct alone, following an internal path unmarked by reason.","opus":"Exhaustion and dissociation, the narrator unsure whether he's still himself or a thing rebuilt from spare parts. Choosing to shut his eyes, let his racing mind fall silent and strike out alone into the dark turns burnout into a kind of blind, self-directed surrender.","sonnet":"Exhaustion and dissociation prompt the speaker to wonder whether they have been reassembled from scrap into something no longer fully themselves; closing their eyes, they resolve to follow their own road without needing to see the path ahead, letting the dimming light fade into darkness rather than fighting it.","src":"haiku"},
"astronoid~i-dream-in-lines":{"haiku":"The speaker turns inward, fragmenting into fragile daydreams and fractured memories; perfection dissolves as they float apart.","opus":"Astronoid's airy dream-thrash suits this hazy reflection on turning a life into art, imagining that creation as a portrait of the era. Foggy sight and a search for the right words yield a finished image that is flawless yet coming undone, its pieces drifting off. The Massachusetts band hold beauty and dissolution in fragile, gauzy suspension.","sonnet":"A narrator navigating dim uncertainty seeks the words to complete a story that keeps turning away from resolution. The consolation offered is modest and internal — a picture-perfect portrait framed within a dream — and the acceptance of impermanence reads as hard-won rather than peaceful.","src":"haiku"},
"astronoid~sleep-whisper":{"haiku":"Rejection of a world causing pain; the speaker seeks escape through farewell, choosing detachment and freedom over continued suffering and hiding.","opus":"A weary farewell to a world that only hurts, wanting out yet finding a strange freedom in that surrender. The dream-metal haze softens what is essentially a resignation, an ache to simply stop noticing.","sonnet":"A farewell in fragments: the world hurts, enough is enough, and the speaker departs — \"see ya, this world, go away, hurts for me.\" The repeated \"I kept noticing\" suggests a long accumulation of pain before the goodbye, but the song offers no drama, only quiet exit.","src":"haiku"},
"astrophysics~array":{"haiku":"Desire to escape gravity pulls downward instead as attempts to shed weight fail repeatedly. Wings and light prove illusory, leaving only endless falling.","opus":"Astrophysics chart angelic ambition crumbling into defeat, the speaker plucking off his feathers and setting a fire to banish a darkness that refuses to lift. The plea to be cast down from paradise makes banishment the goal, an endless craving left hanging without resolution.","sonnet":"An angel falling from heaven while someone is away: lighting a pyre to cast out darkness that never works, shedding feathers, pushed out — the unending need for perpetual something trails off unfinished, the song itself incomplete.","src":"haiku"},
"astrophysics~lobotomy":{"haiku":"Confusion masquerades as invitation while old falsehoods perpetuate through self-deception. The narrator loses place within twisted gray games; another's touch becomes alien contact in shared delusion.","opus":"Skramz built on intrusive thoughts and a refusal to let go of a fading connection; the tender reach toward a smaller hand curdles into possessiveness, wanting to drag the other person down into the confusion.","sonnet":"Lost between intrusive thoughts and a twisted, grey inner world, the speaker is pulled toward someone whose small hand reaches them — and they decide to take that person with them wherever they're going, whether that is healing or dissolution. The question \"is it better?\" goes unanswered, suggesting the pull itself is the only answer available.","src":"haiku"},
"at-my-mercy~not-a-chance":{"haiku":"Accountability and retribution loom for someone who has caused damage and pain; they will choke on their own words and face the consequences they cannot escape. Blood stains their hands.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning aimed at someone who refuses accountability, warning that their damage has consequences and that the time to make amends has run out, the blood on their hands making forgiveness impossible.","sonnet":"An ultimatum to someone who has done damage and refused accountability — the blood is on your hands, there's no escape, and eventually you'll choke on your own excuses — the song is patient rather than explosive, certain that reckoning will come.","src":"haiku"},
"at-the-drive-in~one-armed-scissor":{"haiku":"Transmission and amputation; fragmented communication from an isolated, malfunctioning station.","opus":"At the Drive-In splinter their imagery into a body reassembled as broken machinery beaming out signals of distress. The El Paso band's breakout runs the alienation of endless touring through disjointed, sci-fi wreckage, its plea about a letter meant to preserve memory anchoring the chaos in real homesick loneliness.","sonnet":"Fractured transmissions from deep space — or deep estrangement — mix biological dread with rocket imagery: hollowed stations, self-destruct sequences, a letter written in blood to someone a million miles away, desperate to be remembered across an impossible distance.","src":"haiku"},
"atena~death-is-all-i-think-about":{"haiku":"Someone obsessed with death as an escape fights medication and depression, their mind consumed by suicide ideation.","opus":"This depressive metalcore track lives inside relentless suicidal ideation, where antidepressants named outright bring no relief and days bleed to gray. The narrator measures a bridge's height with clinical detachment, still imagining a future but only a darkened one, drinking to drown a mind that vomits and weeps alone after the crowd goes home.","sonnet":"A mind saturated with suicidal obsession catalogues the physical details of a bridge, the failure of prescribed medication, and the numb arithmetic of 3 AM solitude. The future still exists in imagination but only as a place shaped entirely by death.","src":"haiku"},
"atena~poison-pure":{"haiku":"Addiction grips the brain, providing everything yet destroying simultaneously. The pursuit of numbness through poison becomes paradoxically necessary cure while sleeplessness marks the cost.","opus":"Addiction as the thing that grips the brain and doubles as both disease and cure; the cruelest turn is knowing you're free to walk away at any time, which only deepens the shame of staying stuck.","sonnet":"Addicted to something that kills while providing everything needed — \"has everything I need, sum for the pain\" — the speaker can't leave despite knowing the cure is to cut the roots. The refrain \"you're free to leave whenever you desire\" taunts them, and the bitter joke of doing the same thing every day hoping for change anchors the trap.","src":"haiku"},
"atheist~mother-man":{"haiku":"Humanity masters destruction while calling it progress; domination of nature serves corporate appetite over survival.","opus":"Atheist brand humankind as the title figure, the conceited wrecker who has subdued and fouled the planet, cast as its offended parent. Under the intricate, technical playing lies a green lament that people ruin all they handle to feed their own appetites.","sonnet":"An environmental indictment of humanity — renamed Mother Man — who has supplanted Mother Earth, systematically destroying air, sea, and nature through legislation and greed until nature itself becomes illegal.","src":"haiku"},
"atlantida-project~a-1h5jy59":{"opus":"In the dreamy Russian trip-hop mode where atmosphere outweighs argument, the song follows the act of emptying the mind completely, keeping a mind while losing everything else at once and dancing thoughtless until the head runs clean. Then a bird slips out from behind and rises into the sky, horses gallop free across fields, and something small keeps holding off sleep. The release is total, and the imagery arrives only once the thinking stops.","sonnet":"A Russian-language song about emptying the mind completely — having a mind but losing everything else instantly, dancing without a single thought until the head is perfectly clean, then a bird slips out from behind and flies into the sky, the horses gallop free across fields, and something prevents sleep.","src":"sonnet"},
"atlas~susi":{"opus":"Atlas conjure a spellbinding call from the drowned, luring the living to trace the water's path, give themselves to the current, and settle among the bodies resting below the surface.","sonnet":"A drowning summons — a voice from beneath the water calls the living to surrender to the river and join the dead below, framing death by water as a return rather than a loss.","src":"sonnet"},
"atlas~uni":{"haiku":"A synthetic being with no organic nature—no bones or blood—experiences haunting memory of what they used to be, now empty machinery dreaming in a void.","opus":"Atlas depict an artificial being that swapped its living nature for machinery, a thinking creation that emerged once forbidden knowledge fractured whatever once made it human. Beneath the cold hardware runs a persistent ache, a weary, ghostly plea to be remembered as it once was, mourning a sterile existence that can bring forth nothing new.","sonnet":"Something once organic has traded its flesh for circuitry and lost its memories along the way. Hollowed into a synthetic form, it reaches back toward whoever knew it before the transformation, haunted by the person it no longer is.","src":"haiku"},
"atreyu~blow":{"haiku":"The band defies criticism and refuses conformity, declaring their authenticity is not a choice but their nature; they reject external judgment and continue their authentic path regardless of opposition.","opus":"A defiant rejection of critics, hypocrites, and liars, telling them to shut up because the band does this for their souls and won't conform. The nonconformity is framed as inborn, not a choice; the closing turn toward a lonely listener offers solidarity against isolation.","sonnet":"A defiant rejection of critics and hypocrites who spread lies, the band asserting their music is made for their own souls and they will not conform or disappear regardless of what the noise machine says — paired with an unexpected message of solidarity to isolated listeners.","src":"haiku"},
"atreyu~capital-f":{"haiku":"Systemic corruption swallows civilization and truth alike—everyone suffocates under complicity, yet hope dissolves faster than collective guilt can act.","opus":"A blunt reckoning with a species torching its own future while smiling, the American dream sold as a scam nobody can explain. The metalcore vets aim their disgust at collective complicity and the numbness of arriving too late to fix any of it.","sonnet":"Collective despair at civilisation's self-destructive loop — building cities of gold to destroy them, selling the American dream with no answer for its cost — lands as a blunt rhetorical question: 'is it just me or are we all capital F fucked?'","src":"haiku"},
"atreyu~depression-season":{"haiku":"Seasonal cyclicality of suicidal ideation; the speaker cannot break the pattern of pushing internal pain deeper rather than releasing it.","opus":"A blunt account of a depressive episode, the speaker questioning whether living is even worth it when nothing seems real anymore and the grave feels self-dug. Shoving the feelings back down rather than facing them, the recurring question of whether he'll come out of it alive names the real stakes under the churn.","sonnet":"Depression as a season that cannot be escaped — \"this grave is a prison I dug for myself\" — the speaker keeps pushing it back inside, questioning whether they'll make it out alive as the sinking feeling refuses to lift.","src":"haiku"},
"atreyu~drowning":{"haiku":"Psychological imprisonment deepens each day; thoughts crush while crowds block light, and escape means finding something that was never there.","opus":"Suffering in silence while your own thoughts wall you in, mistaking the undertow of depression for something you might swim out of. The chorus names the loneliness of screaming for help that no one hears.","sonnet":"Mental health crisis rendered as submersion: the walls in the speaker's head grow too high to climb, nightmares of not waking up repeat, and the undertow of depression keeps pulling under — 'trying to find something that was never even there' names the futility at the centre.","src":"haiku"},
"atreyu~god-devil":{"haiku":"Fever dreams collapse as hellfire consumes while the foundation crumbles. The dualism of divine and demonic warfare reduces the speaker to dust; ruin demands taking back power through submission.","opus":"A metalcore plea shouted from the moment the foundation cracks, unsure whether salvation comes from heaven or hell; the point is less the answer than the desperation of falling and needing anyone at all to see it.","sonnet":"Caught between angels and demons, God and devil, the speaker pleads to be seen and saved while the whole foundation cracks beneath them — \"in ruin we trust\" becomes the only creed. Neither divine nor infernal force answers the call, leaving the fall accelerating.","src":"haiku"},
"atreyu~gone":{"haiku":"Scattered remains mark finality; what once touched the lips now lies trampled. The narrator attempted connection but sorrow replaces prior intention; absence becomes the only certainty.","opus":"A wrenching admission of a love failed through emotional incapacity, watching someone slip away while unable to reach out; the guilt lands because the narrator names himself as the one who stood on the cracks and did nothing.","sonnet":"A rueful post-mortem on a relationship destroyed by the speaker's own inability to love or care — cigarettes trampled like the relationship itself, the image of her cradling her head on the steps the sharpest regret. Standing on the cracks while she fell through, they can only watch her disappear and live with those tears.","src":"haiku"},
"atreyu~save-us":{"haiku":"A call for collective awakening and resistance against systemic complacency, demanding passionate action rather than complicit silence in a dying world.","opus":"Atreyu watch fumes lift off the fallen and ask whether any soul still weeps, then answer by urging listeners to bear a flame against the shadow in everyone. The metalcore veterans cast indifference as the foe, rejecting silent obedience in the ranks and calling for revolt, the refrain insisting rescue hinges on choosing to stand rather than lie low.","sonnet":"Against a backdrop of mass death and collective denial, a call goes out to the living: carry the fire inside the darkness, burn brighter, refuse compliance. The song frames spiritual and moral collapse as a crisis demanding personal uprising, not passive waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"atrial~shatter-me":{"haiku":"Suffocation traps the speaker in wretched confinement; iron fists forge escape into freedom. Screams call out unanswered while vision dissolves; the meaning of survival dissolves into void.","opus":"Trapped in a suffocating place and forging strength through repeated collapse; the madness is self-made, and the reckoning growing inside is framed as necessary self-destruction on the way to becoming something more.","sonnet":"Trapped in a suffocating place by self-made madness, the speaker refuses to be a useless object and fights through every fall to strengthen their core — even as they crack and crumble, reckoning grows inside them. The tension between breaking and becoming drives the whole arc.","src":"haiku"},
"attack-attack~all-my-life":{"opus":"A song about breaking free from a manipulative, gaslighting person who got in the narrator's head and kept him down for their own gain; he refuses to fall in line and suffocate any longer now that their reign is over.","sonnet":"About escaping a person who dominated and suffocated the narrator through manipulation — framing the toxic influence as smoke in the lungs, the narrator declares their power over, refusing to continue suffocating under someone else's need for control.","src":"sonnet"},
"attack-attack~concrete":{"haiku":"Poison traps the psyche through recursive cycles of awakening into cold sweat. The speaker cannot escape the cycle; deathlike adhesion binds them to perpetual dread.","opus":"Metalcore comeback about sealing off a toxic relationship for good, burying the broken promise under concrete; the violent imagery is really about the finality of a cutoff you'd rather not soften.","sonnet":"An irresistible self-destructive pull — a \"poison grapevine\" that sticks like death on a doorstep — triggers a radioactive chain reaction, and the only solution offered is to bury it under concrete so it can never surface again. The imagery escalates from cold sweat to coffin nail to sealed shadow, making permanent burial the only acceptable exit.","src":"haiku"},
"attack-attack~dark-waves":{"haiku":"Cyclonic romantic obsession pulls the narrator repeatedly toward a lover despite destructive patterns, leaving emotional damage with each reunion and separation.","opus":"The magnetic pull of a toxic reunion, drawn back to someone you know will only hollow you out again. It sits in that guilty thrill where wrong and right feel identical, each cycle another wound reopened.","sonnet":"A cyclone romance with someone remembered like a fever dream — sweet as cheap wine, wrong and right at once — where the two keep building back up only to fall apart, and the damning admission is that the other person is fated to leave another hole in the heart no matter how many times the cycle restarts.","src":"haiku"},
"attack-attack~paralyzed-until-we-meet-again":{"haiku":"Recognition of kinship offers no solace as paralysis grips the barely-hanging survivor. The final breath becomes prayer for reunion; separation equals immobility and despair.","opus":"A dying parent's farewell to a child, spending the last breath to say the love that living never fully voiced; its comfort is the belief that leaving isn't the end and that the child now carries the parent forward.","sonnet":"A dying parent speaks to a child whose eyes mirror their own, confessing they're paralyzed by pain but insisting this parting is not the end — \"until we meet again.\" The greatest gift was the child themselves, and the final charge is to live for them as they lived for the child.","src":"haiku"},
"attica-riots~no-hands":{"haiku":"Helpless cycling through broken-hearted rhythms with no control or answers, watching time consume the past while change knocks at the door.","opus":"This alt-rock band builds a song around aimless forward motion as a coping mechanism, a person coasting through disappointment because change has arrived uninvited after an early rush of happiness faded. The recurring reminder that nothing endures plays like exhausted wisdom, while ticking clocks needle him about time already squandered; he keeps moving because standing still would mean facing that no relief is coming.","sonnet":"Disillusionment with a life that no longer feels owned propels a narrator into aimless, drifting motion — riding on and on with no steering and no answers. The chorus circles back repeatedly, mirroring the structure of burnout itself: perpetual movement that leads nowhere, built on the suspicion of having been deceived.","src":"haiku"},
"atticus-chimps~alien-jewelry":{"haiku":"Stars transform into dirt beneath cosmic seas while humans become toys in godly hands. Planetary playgrounds pivot on cosmic strings; perspective reduces civilization to playground chess.","opus":"A dreamy invitation to lie back and stare at the stars, framing humans as toys of distant gods on a small planet; the wonder holds a quiet unease that the future isn't guaranteed to shine.","sonnet":"Two figures lie together gazing at the stars — \"alien jewelry\" scattered across the cosmic sea — framing themselves as toys in gods' hands, kite-flyers on a flying planet. A gravity-flip and a slingshot send them sailing toward those stars, stitched in gold, in a vision of shared smallness and wonder.","src":"haiku"},
"attila~bite-your-tongue":{"haiku":"Unwavering authenticity against endless cycles of denial; sincerity becomes a battle line.","opus":"A blunt dismissal of someone who starts battles from the wrong side and denies who the narrator really is, keeping him snared in manufactured drama. He backs his words with action and issues an ultimatum: get on board or stay quiet.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who starts fights when wrong and treats honesty as a crime — the speaker refuses to back down, puts money where his mouth is, and demands the same directness in return: if you don't sing along, bite your tongue, but don't you tell me what I am.","src":"haiku"},
"attila~fu4evr":{"haiku":"Permanent severance and sustained contempt burn all bridges; hatred becomes eternal.","opus":"Pure scorched-earth kiss-off to a serial liar, all restraint abandoned. Chris Fronzak's party-metal band drops the bravado for once and just channels the ugly, sleepless rage of realizing you were played.","sonnet":"Pure breakup fury: the speaker catalogues how long they missed obvious warning signs while a liar drained everything, then detonates — \"fuck you forever, I hope you fucking die.\" There's no nuance or ambivalence; the song is entirely the moment of finally having enough after the lies \"stay on repeat.\"","src":"haiku"},
"attila~handshakes-with-snakes":{"haiku":"Treachery and cold deception justify brutal retaliation; betrayal demands violent revenge.","opus":"A warning never to trust the two-faced, wrapped in gangster-rap swagger and Wu-Tang name-drops. Beneath the flexing is genuine betrayal, the vow to cut off anyone who lies to your face.","sonnet":"A screed against a specific backstabber whose forked tongue dispensed false virtues — the title image of a cold-blooded handshake frames the whole: trust no one who runs hot and cold on purpose. Threats escalate from the personal to the lethal, with the refrain doubling as both warning and boast.","src":"haiku"},
"attila~subhuman":{"haiku":"A dissociated person feels emotionally dead and trapped between fractured personalities. They observe themselves as a corpse, divorced from sensation and morality.","opus":"A raw account of dissociation and depression, where the singer feels detached from his own body, watching himself as if he were already a corpse. Fractured into competing selves and dulling everything with substances, he brands himself less than human, trapped inside a mind he can no longer trust.","sonnet":"About severe dissociation and emotional numbness — the narrator watches himself from a distance like a visible corpse, torn between two personalities, feeling nothing and not entirely minding. It frames mental illness as a kind of imprisonment with the self as both jailer and captive.","src":"haiku"},
"au-ra~high-fantasy-nightcore":{"haiku":"Escape into fantasy becomes psychological necessity; the speaker abandons waking reality for elaborate internal worlds, unable to distinguish dreaming from living.","opus":"Escapism as chosen refuge: retreating into an elaborate imagined world because reality is ice-cold, willing to stay inside the dream. The fantasy flickers and threatens to slip, but it remains everything the speaker needs.","sonnet":"A young person chooses to retreat entirely into an internally-built fantasy world, treating imagination as a superior and self-sufficient reality — the city in the sky, the sword, the elixir — over whatever cold or disappointing reality exists outside it.","src":"haiku"},
"au-ra~killswitch":{"haiku":"Total severance becomes agency; the subject inflicts disappearance like magic.","opus":"A pop-electronic breakup-empowerment track built on the fantasy of an off-button for a volatile partner — silencing the fights and the emotional whiplash on demand. Reclaiming control from a chaotic relationship by choosing, finally, to be done.","sonnet":"A speaker in a toxic push-pull dynamic decides to flip an internal kill switch: cutting the other person off with deliberate finality, leaving them in the dark, and trading tragedy for the quiet satisfaction of disappearing someone like magic.","src":"haiku"},
"au-ra~mad-world":{"haiku":"Familiar faces wear exhaustion as they perpetuate purposeless routines toward nowhere. Tears fill glasses of those who've numbed themselves to life's absurd machinery; no one escapes the treadmill.","opus":"A stripped cover of Tears for Fears' portrait of numb, routine despair, where children's rituals feel hollow and the peace of dreamed dying outshines waking life; the young voice sharpens the sense of a world circling nowhere.","sonnet":"Worn-out faces going nowhere, tears filling glasses with no expression, children made to sit and listen while a teacher looks right through them — the speaker finds it funny and sad that dying dreams are the best ones, and running in circles defines this very, very mad world. Numbness and alienation are the only available responses.","src":"haiku"},
"audioslave~be-yourself":{"haiku":"Authenticity persists as the only valid response to existence's contradictions; surrender to individuality liberates.","opus":"Cornell surveys strangers finding salvation, pain, love and flight in scattered vignettes, and lands on the one durable counsel: authenticity when everything else is luck. It is hard-won reassurance that being yourself is the only thing wholly in your hands.","sonnet":"Surveying the full range of human experience — grief, joy, love, isolation — the song concludes that authenticity is the only constant available, whatever else is won or lost along the way.","src":"haiku"},
"audioslave~cochise":{"haiku":"The narrator offers themselves as a dumping ground for someone else's pain and self-destruction, neither preaching nor preventing another's drowning.","opus":"A detached observer offers a caustic kind of help to a self-destructive person: he refuses to be a martyr or hold their hand, but tells them to save themselves and take their rage out on him; the throughline is watching someone drown by choice and refusing to enable it while still standing there at the bottom.","sonnet":"An offer of emotional availability that refuses co-dependence — the narrator watches someone self-destruct and won't save them from themselves, but invites them to take out their frustration on him rather than disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"audioslave~like-a-stone":{"haiku":"The narrator waits eternally in longing and regret, mourning lost paradise and dwelling on past wrongs. He becomes fixed in place and memory.","opus":"A haunting meditation on mortality and yearning, a man lost in a book about dying alone, promising to wait patiently, room by room, like a stone, for a love or afterlife he half-recalls, praying on his deathbed to any god who'll take him. Audioslave, uniting Chris Cornell's voice with Rage Against the Machine's musicians, made this one of the 2000s' great slow-burn ballads, aching with regret and quiet hope.","sonnet":"A meditation on dying and waiting written in one long afternoon — reading about death by a freeway, longing for a house that may be the afterlife, promising to wait there with the patience of something geological. The sky is bruised, the wine bled, the day gone. The speaker sits in regret for everything done and not done, and promises to wander through those dreams until the end.","src":"haiku"},
"audioslave~show-me-how-to-live":{"haiku":"The speaker questions divine purpose and the creator's responsibility, asking to be shown how to live after being given existence without guidance.","opus":"A Frankenstein's-creature metaphor: a made thing granted existence but no guidance on how to actually live, turning on the maker who abandoned it. Audioslave, Chris Cornell fronting Rage Against the Machine's musicians, give the myth a muscular, soaring urgency and a warning that the creation may yet define the creator.","sonnet":"Rage directed at a creator who gave life but no instruction — sleepless, wired into something broken, the speaker demands that whoever put them here take responsibility for what they made. The image of a nail in the head from the creator is not self-pity but accusation: you built this, now show it how to function. The song ends at the edge of readiness, waiting to begin.","src":"haiku"},
"august-burns-red~defender":{"haiku":"A parent's fierce protection wrapped in restraint, knowing that bending saves more than breaking.","opus":"A Christian metalcore band framing reconciliation as a father refusing to let bitterness demonize his child, and reaching for a defender who takes the blow in his place. It's about forgiveness that costs something and can't be one-sided.","sonnet":"A father — or a friend acting with fatherly force — confronts someone who has been poisoned against the one they love, channelling righteous anger into empathy and insisting that the relationship can only survive if both people choose to hold it together.","src":"haiku"},
"august-burns-red~pangaea":{"haiku":"A speaker separated across the world's corners mourns the breakup of continents and yearns to return home, treating this geographical fragmentation as both literal and metaphorical isolation.","opus":"A homesick yearning rendered as geological metaphor, the narrator realizing the seemingly ideal life he was sold is a mirage while he tallies what he neglected and aches to close the gulf separating him from someone he loves. August Burns Red, the Pennsylvania metalcore band, name the lost home for the ancient supercontinent, dreaming of everything joined again.","sonnet":"Homesickness as geological longing — the speaker is scattered across all four corners, counting days, watching clocks, calling out to Pangaea as the original wholeness before the world broke apart. The perfect life on offer is an illusion; what the speaker actually wants is to sail back to a morning that takes them home. Bittersweet and specific, it treats distance as a kind of continental drift.","src":"haiku"},
"august-burns-red~paramount":{"haiku":"The speaker rallies the broken and weary toward reclamation by refusing victimhood and naming their inner strength. Together, light persists through imposed darkness.","opus":"August Burns Red send a note of encouragement to someone crossing a blaze, assuring them they still have companions, resolve and value, and that their flaws and wounded loads don't decide where they end up. Faith-tinged metalcore built on reassurance.","sonnet":"An encouragement addressed to someone walking through fire and buried under regret: you have hope, you have friends, your imperfections are enough — stop letting regret pull the strings, look past your own reflection, and reassemble the broken parts.","src":"haiku"},
"august-burns-red~the-narrative":{"opus":"A pointed critique of media and political manipulation that exploits emotion and belief to sow discord, floating conclusions before any evidence to distract from what actually matters. The remedy it offers is to reconnect with one another, seek genuine facts, and value human bonds above the stories being sold.","sonnet":"A direct attack on media manipulation, outrage cycles, and political tribalism — algorithms feed division, theories precede evidence, and the solution proposed is turning toward one another across divisions rather than serving whatever narrative wants to breed conflict.","src":"sonnet"},
"aural-vampire~basara":{"haiku":"Appearance deceives; the speaker wears diamonds for eyes and rubies for skull, insulated against world's judgment. By refusing to hide behind false shame, they achieve invulnerability—transform pain into unbreakable surface.","opus":"A grotesque, jewel-encrusted self-image—eyes made of diamond, skull of ruby, tears turning to candy drops—narrates a woman armoring herself in surface glamour against shame and social appearances while drifting the river of the dead. She wraps herself in tiger's hide so that even a split skull won't hurt, betting that keeping up appearances will eventually make the inner self follow.","sonnet":"A self-proclaimed diamond-eyed, ruby-skulled figure navigates desire, social performance, and the Sanzu River — weighing earthly lust against heavenly love, dismissing shame as mere passage — and insists that faking refinement on the outside will eventually draw the inside to match, all while carving through the world wrapped in a tiger's pelt and utterly fearless.","src":"haiku"},
"auras~the-demoness":{"haiku":"A demonic force embodied in a woman feeds on hearts and souls, dragging people into darkness and drowning; she tears apart anyone she encounters.","opus":"Prog-metalcore casting a destructive lover as a literal soul-eating demon, seductive and drowning, medicating instead of praying. The mythic imagery externalizes a relationship that devours from the inside.","sonnet":"A song about a woman who presents as seductive and angel-adjacent but is actually predatory and destructive — the narrator recognizes too late that she was never going to nurture anything, only tear hearts out and leave devastation in her wake.","src":"haiku"},
"aurora-view~death-spells":{"haiku":"Masking and internal rot build toward revelation; the speaker transforms suffering into reckoning, confronting the ghosts that inhabit their own constructed cage.","opus":"A ritual of confronting inner demons in the mirror, refusing to hide the skeletons in the attic, and clinging to being alive even while fading and haunted by a ghost that won't leave.","sonnet":"About confronting hidden demons and the masks people wear — the speaker and someone else are both concealing rot beneath polished surfaces, and the song builds toward a reckoning where secrets must be spoken and the truth of who they are under the mask must be faced.","src":"haiku"},
"aurora-view~honey-oil":{"haiku":"A reckless hedonist spirals into substance abuse and cruelty, riding intensity until death, indifferent to consequences.","opus":"A reckless spiral toward oblivion drives this track, its narrator back to the wall, snorting lines and driving drunk with no care for surviving. Heartbreak and theft trail behind a hunger that cannot stop once started, snake eyes glaring up from the bottle's base while the notion that survival breeds strength curdles into a threat aimed outward.","sonnet":"A narrator in freefall describes a life governed by substances and reckless sensation, dragging anyone nearby toward the same bottom. Self-erasure is both method and goal, and the only rule is that stopping is not an option until death makes it one.","src":"haiku"},
"aurora-view~inside-my-head":{"haiku":"Internal noise drowns out reality as the speaker fractures, voices scream louder than truth, the architecture of the mind collapsing into venom and despair.","opus":"A spiraling panic where the sky goes red and the veins run black, the recurring question whether any of it is real or only happening in his mind. Voices and paranoia blur with a threat of self-harm, someone slipping through the gaps and unsure what's being done to him.","sonnet":"A mental state unraveling in real time — toxic thoughts multiplying under pressure, the speaker losing grip, feeling something dark running through their veins, unsure whether the catastrophe is external or entirely manufactured inside their own skull.","src":"haiku"},
"aurora-view~lovesick":{"haiku":"Obsessive longing and illness from love, emotional sickness from attachment.","opus":"Casting yourself as the parasite and the monster in a love you can't control, wanting to be saved by someone who keeps slipping away. The self-loathing gothic streak turns infatuation into an affliction with a bad taste.","sonnet":"The narrator is a ghost and parasite in their own attachment — lovesick with red fangs, a bad taste, no control — watching someone who is \"pretty broken\" and \"better when you sit and stay\" keep running away anyway. The sickness is inseparable from the desire; it doesn't get sweeter, just more consuming.","src":"haiku"},
"australian-crawl~the-boys-light-up-remastered":{"haiku":"Sexual manipulation and chemical seduction bind couples in transactional arrangements masquerading as partnership and social advancement.","opus":"A sardonic portrait of bored suburban wives and their sordid affairs with businessmen and politicians, the whole hypocritical performance dressed up as a cheap tent show.","sonnet":"A darkly comic portrait of suburban and political sexual exchange: a lonely housewife on mother's little helper trades favours for a flat with an ocean view, while MPs rave about the value they receive — all of it framed as a cheap tent show that the boys celebrate by lighting up.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~broo-tall-song-idea":{"haiku":"An Arnold impersonator recites an intentionally nonsensical and brutal-sounding title string as if it were a profound song concept.","opus":"Tim Lambesis's Schwarzenegger-themed metal parody drops all pretense of meaning, offering a spoken bit about inventing an absurdly menacing title purely because it sounds brutal. The gleefully nonsensical string of words gets chanted along as a joke on genre conventions, the whole point being that heaviness can be pure sound and swagger. It is comedy about metal's love of grandiose brutality for its own sake.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~come-on-do-it-do-it-come-on-come-on-kill-do-it-now":{"haiku":"Confrontational extremism expressed through relentless provocation and defiant noise.","opus":"A metal gag built entirely from Schwarzenegger movie lines, taunting an enemy to just get the killing over with. The joke is the whole point: action-hero bravado turned into a comically relentless dare.","sonnet":"A face-to-face standoff in a booby-trapped space where one party taunts the other to kill them now — the speaker is there, the challenge is issued, but the moment keeps resetting rather than resolving, the bravado of \"do it\" masking the fact that neither has moved.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~come-with-me-if-you-want-to-live":{"haiku":"A reprogrammed cyborg defects from his mission to kill and pledges to protect humanity from an unstoppable machine. The song adapts action-film dialogue to explore redemption through defiance.","opus":"Arnold Schwarzenegger's action one-liners become the whole gospel here, the metalcore side project turning a reprogrammed guardian's rescue into gleeful parody. The narrator, once sent to kill, now saves a life while winking that this is a Hollywood movie where the good guys win, riffing on Terminator lore with tongue firmly in cheek.","sonnet":"A reprogrammed Terminator narrates his own redemption arc, switching allegiance from assassination to protection. The song plays the Terminator 2 scenario straight, with the machine acknowledging his past mission and now racing to keep his former target alive.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~double-ahhnold":{"haiku":"An Arnold Schwarzenegger impersonator records an absurd voicemail conspiracy theory about Metallica and Batman movies.","opus":"Tim Lambesis's Schwarzenegger-obsessed metal parody stages a phone argument between two Arnolds, an original and his clone, each swearing he's the genuine article. It's pure comedy sketch, packed with aging gags and mock threats, the whole gimmick of bodybuilder action-hero bravado played strictly for laughs.","sonnet":"Two voices each claim to be the real Arnold Schwarzenegger, trading escalating insults and grievances in a mock-phone-call confrontation. The skit plays the absurdity of celebrity ego and imitation for pure comedy, ending in a standoff of competing authenticity claims neither party can actually win.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~get-to-the-choppa":{"haiku":"A desperate escape from overwhelming threat drives the group toward the only exit—a helicopter. The song emphasizes urgency and the raw will to survive.","opus":"Built around the urgent command to reach the helicopter as the only way out, the song stages a frantic combat escape under fire. It cheers pushing forward, shooting attackers, and getting away dead or alive.","sonnet":"The song is built entirely around the premise of an urgent military escape: a group under fire must reach a helicopter immediately or die, while armed enemies try to stop them. The lyrics are a direct, repeated command to evacuate with no deeper subtext.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~gotta-go-agnostic-front-cover":{"haiku":"A speaker is overwhelmed by loss of autonomy, infiltration of minds through propaganda, and the death of freedom, driving an urgent need to escape.","opus":"Tim Lambesis's Schwarzenegger-themed metal parody covers Agnostic Front, wrapping hardcore verses about mindless violence and lost security in a Governator persona amid a robot revolution. The relentless gotta go refrain plays the source's alienation for absurd, quote-stuffed comedy, tribute filtered through a bodybuilder gimmick.","sonnet":"Urgency and claustrophobia drive someone toward exit at any cost. Security is absent, autonomy is under attack, and the accumulation of pressure — internal and external, real and paranoid — collapses into one insistent need: to leave, to get out, to be somewhere else entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~hell-bent-for-leather-judas-priest-cover":{"haiku":"A mysterious fast figure of fire and precision strikes without warning; many try to prove they're faster but die in the attempt, never catching up.","opus":"A parody-metal act's straight-faced take on a Judas Priest classic that mythologizes a lone motorcyclist as an almost supernatural terror on the open road, feared and impossible to catch, with rivals who challenge his speed meeting their doom. The whole thing is a metaphor for the untouchable outlaw the biker imagines himself to be.","sonnet":"A mythic road-figure — faster than shadow, undefeated, source unknown — destroys all challengers who attempt to match his speed. The lyric elevates pure velocity into a kind of nihilistic supremacy: those who tried to prove themselves faster died trying, and the rider moves on untouched.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~hello-california":{"haiku":"A comedic spoken-word piece mocking action-film heroism and bodybuilding culture, presented as Austrian-accented absurdist musings on California and excess.","opus":"A comedy metal bit voicing a Schwarzenegger caricature as governor, riffing on his movies and mangled bravado before the band flubs its own intro. It's a spoken sketch built entirely on affectionate parody of the Austrian icon.","sonnet":"A comedy intro track where the narrator roleplays as Arnold Schwarzenegger announcing his album to California with absurd proclamations — it is entirely a spoken-word parody bit, not a song with lyrical content.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~here-is-subzero-now-plain-zero":{"haiku":"Spectacle glorifies killers as warriors when they are merely condemned prisoners, reducing human atrocity to entertainment.","opus":"Lambesis riffs on Mortal Kombat to mock a culture that turns deadly fighting into celebrated entertainment, deflating a supposed champion into a cowardly nobody despite his fame. Under the video-game joke sits a real anxiety about survival, the narrator wondering whether he must keep cutting down every challenger sent his way just to stay breathing.","sonnet":"A gladiatorial spectacle is condemned from inside it — the survivor refuses to glorify the killing, deflating the crowd-beloved warrior into a frightened man in a costume. The refrain strips status away in real time, turning the arena's hero into nobody, while the narrator questions a society that celebrates death as entertainment.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~hey-cookie-monster-nothing-is-as-brutal-as-neaahhh":{"haiku":"Two members joke about their brutal vocal delivery and songwriting shortcuts, with one accidentally revealing he plays in a competing band.","opus":"A comic skit rather than a song, staging a mock studio chat where an Arnold Schwarzenegger impersonator ribs a bandmate for growling like Cookie Monster and insists the most brutal vocal is simply an incoherent scream. Austrian Death Machine is As I Lay Dying frontman Tim Lambesis's tongue-in-cheek metalcore side project built entirely around Arnold impressions and movie gags, so the joke is the point.","sonnet":"A comedic metal skit in which Arnold ridicules a bandmate's Cookie Monster vocal style, insisting that the only truly brutal sound is a wordless roar — positioning that guttural howl as both artistic advice and punchline, with tongue planted firmly in cheek.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~i-am-a-cybernetic-organism-living-tissue-over-metal-endoskeleton":{"haiku":"A programmed weapon claims its nature; killing defines identity beyond resistance.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek Terminator tribute in which the machine announces itself as a war machine built to kill and protect. It leans entirely on the film's iconography, defiant that it is exactly what it was programmed to be.","sonnet":"A straightforward first-person monologue from the Terminator character — the song is a playful metal homage to the Schwarzenegger films, voicing the machine's self-identification as a killing apparatus sent to protect, with no deeper subtext.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~i-lied":{"haiku":"Betrayal leads to vengeance wrapped in mockery, the promise to spare turning to cruelty as the betrayed finally acts on their anger without remorse.","opus":"A comic-book revenge fantasy built entirely on the Commando one-liner, gleeful menace played for parody. The betrayer is toyed with and dispatched, the joke's whole engine being the broken promise to kill him last.","sonnet":"A direct retelling of the Commando (1985) film scene in which Arnold Schwarzenegger's character throws the villain off a cliff — gleefully recreating the moment of betrayal and murder as heavy metal catharsis.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~i-ll-be-back":{"haiku":"Terminator pastiche where relentless return and protection are guaranteed. The speaker promises eternal comeback to save the day and punish wrongdoers, no matter the body count.","opus":"A comic homage built around the Terminator's catchphrase 'I'll be back,' delivered in an exaggerated Schwarzenegger accent. The narrator vows to return and save the day whatever the body count, sometimes for revenge, sometimes just to visit.","sonnet":"A comedic homage to the Terminator catchphrase, performed in an exaggerated Arnold Schwarzenegger accent, in which the speaker boasts about always returning—whether to save the day or exact revenge—and invites the audience to circle pit. The entire song is a one-joke tribute to the movie line.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~i-need-your-clothes-your-boots-and-your-motorcycle":{"haiku":"Straightforward demands made by a time-displaced visitor (mimicking Terminator dialogue) to acquire necessary supplies.","opus":"A comedic tribute to the Terminator, voicing the cyborg who arrives naked from time travel and menacingly demands clothes, boots and a motorcycle. The straight-faced boasting about strength, broken hands and cigar burns turns action-movie one-liners into an absurd anthem.","sonnet":"A comedic metal reenactment of the Terminator's arrival scene — the narrator has time-travelled and is demanding clothes, boots, and a motorcycle with escalating physical menace, played entirely for absurd laughs.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~if-it-bleeds-we-can-kill-it":{"haiku":"A hunter confronts a wounded predator, establishing their dominance through violence; once prey becomes hunter, the tables turn and the hunted becomes the threat.","opus":"A comedic Predator homage built entirely on Schwarzenegger movie lines, the hunted turning hunter once he learns the enemy can bleed. The theme is straightforward action-movie bravado, claiming the planet and promising the enemy's death.","sonnet":"A direct adaptation of the predator-hunting dynamic from the Predator film: the hunter becomes the hunted once the creature is shown to bleed, and the narrator turns the tables to claim dominance over the alien on his planet.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~iron-fist-motorhead-cover":{"haiku":"A personification of evil and malevolence as an invisible, predatory force that hunts, paralyzes, and kills with supernatural power.","opus":"A superstitious rush of occult biker dread, all cursed glances, spectral riders, and a hellish beast dragging its prey down through the dark. Austrian Death Machine, Tim Lambesis's Schwarzenegger-themed metalcore joke band, cover a Motorhead classic here and mostly play Lemmy's menace straight, honoring their speed-metal forebears.","sonnet":"A night ride through pure dread — invisible hands, ghost hooves, evil eyes, a devil's grip that circles you until there's no way forward or back. The iron fist is the force that closes in from every direction: snakebite kiss, beast of evil, a ghost rider overhead. The song doesn't explain the threat, only registers it as something ancient and inescapable that knows your name.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~it-s-not-a-tumor":{"haiku":"A frustrated conversation where someone won't stop talking and causing headaches; the speaker dismisses fears that a headache is a tumor, repeating the denial while complaining about the noise.","opus":"Built entirely around a Kindergarten Cop punchline, this Austrian Death Machine track weaponizes a mounting headache into comic aggression, a narrator badgered by killing comments and demanding the noise stop. The insistence that it is not a tumor repeats to absurd excess while questions about his daddy get batted away. Lambesis mines the Schwarzenegger catchphrase for pure ridiculous catharsis, frustration and film reference fused into a joke.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~killing-is-my-business-and-business-is-good-megadeth-cover":{"haiku":"A hired assassin operates without moral question, collecting payment for targeted murder as routine commercial transaction and personal craft.","opus":"Austrian Death Machine reprise Megadeth's assassin anthem near-faithfully, inhabiting the same cold contract killer who names his fee and refuses to ask why. The Schwarzenegger-gimmick project plays the mercenary menace mostly straight, a loving cover that lets Mustaine's pulp-noir hitman speak for itself.","sonnet":"A contract killer narrates his profession with methodical pride — infrared scopes, upfront payment, no interest in motive or conscience. The work is clean and complete in itself. The final dark pleasure arrives when he reveals to a current client that they are the next assignment.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~let-off-some-steam-bennett":{"haiku":"A lethal confrontation between former brothers-in-arms: one abandoned service to be a better father while the other pursued money, now enemies to the death.","opus":"Assembled entirely from a Commando one-liner, this parody relishes a wronged commando's payback on a traitorous former ally, mocking his gear and looks before running him through. The gag sharpens into a contrast: the narrator quit soldiering to be a better father while his target chose greed and sleaze.","sonnet":"A soldier-turned-mercenary faces his former comrade turned villain at the climax of a confrontation, delivering the famous one-liner as a prelude to fatal revenge. Loyalty, betrayal, and the gap between special-forces brotherhood and hired villainy all collapse into a single pipe through the chest.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~rubber-baby-buggy-bumpers":{"haiku":"Tongue-twister nonsense about rubber baby buggy bumpers, played for surprise and comic unpredictability.","opus":"A joke by design: As I Lay Dying's Tim Lambesis building whole songs from Arnold Schwarzenegger movie one-liners over metalcore. The whole point is the absurdity of screaming a tongue-twister with total conviction; there is no subject beneath the bit.","sonnet":"A self-aware joke song built almost entirely on the tongue-twister \"rubber baby buggy bumpers,\" with Arnold Schwarzenegger-style commentary about being unpredictable — there is essentially no other lyrical content.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~screw-you-benny":{"opus":"A tongue-in-cheek revenge tirade built entirely around an action-movie betrayal, vowing to impale a treacherous friend with a drill. The whole thing is a gleeful, over-the-top homage played for muscle-bound comedy.","sonnet":"A furious denunciation of a friend-turned-betrayer named Benny — the speaker recounts trusting him like family only to be sold out, and now plans to impale him with a drill in retribution, played entirely straight as a metal riff on a movie-villain confrontation.","src":"sonnet"},
"austrian-death-machine~so-far-so-good-so-let-s-talk-about-it":{"haiku":"Two musicians banter about their quick songwriting process, with one member realizing he actually plays in a different band and getting called out.","opus":"Another between-songs comedy sketch, a mock-Arnold exchange that escalates from mutual band-mate flattery into a petty betrayal gag when one admits playing in a rival act the other just insulted. This is Austrian Death Machine's whole conceit: Tim Lambesis of As I Lay Dying wrapping thrashy metal in Schwarzenegger-voiced skits, where the punchline matters more than the plot.","sonnet":"A backstage comedy sketch built on misplaced loyalty: a bandmate praises Arnold effusively, then casually insults a rival band — only to learn Arnold plays in that very band. The reveal flips the dynamic instantly, turning admiration into a thinly veiled threat and exposing how quickly alliance and betrayal coexist.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~tactically-dangerous-cannibal-commando-goretorture-cover":{"haiku":"A speaker expresses exasperation at unintelligible noise and announces a song title celebrating transgressive violence—the content itself is commentary on incomprehension.","opus":"A brief comic bit where the mock-Arnold narrator complains he cannot understand the noise assaulting his ears before shrugging that nobody understands him either and announcing the title. Austrian Death Machine, Tim Lambesis's Schwarzenegger-themed metal parody, leans on exactly this gag: a cover framed by an Arnold impression that mocks the unintelligibility of extreme vocals it is simultaneously performing.","sonnet":"A brief comedic interlude in which Arnold reacts with theatrical disgust to incomprehensible extreme-metal noise, declares it unbearable, then shrugs and announces the song title anyway — the joke being that his own output is equally unintelligible.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~time-travel-the-metallica-conspiracy":{"haiku":"An Arnold impersonator rambles about a conspiracy where Metallica pre-wrote Batman and Robin dialogue, and penguins wrote the film.","opus":"A comedy sketch rather than a song, this Austrian Death Machine bit floats an Arnold-impersonated theory that Metallica secretly wrote Batman and Robin's dialogue a decade before the film, complete with penguins as screenwriters. The Iceman Cometh pun anchors the gag as the impersonator cracks himself up. Lambesis leans fully into absurdist between-track humor, the joke being the sheer silliness of the conspiracy itself.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~trapped-under-ice-metallica-cover":{"haiku":"A depiction of being frozen alive and powerless, unable to move or communicate while trapped in a crystalline state of living death.","opus":"A suffocating horror of being frozen alive and conscious, unable to move or be heard as death closes in and no rescue comes. Austrian Death Machine, Tim Lambesis's Schwarzenegger-themed comedy project, cover early Metallica here, playing the frozen-tomb terror mostly straight as a nod to their thrash roots beneath the joke premise.","sonnet":"Paralysis as living death — consciousness without the ability to move, scream, or be heard, the body locked inside a cryogenic shell while the mind is fully awake. The speaker is dying to live in the most literal sense: aware, terrified, completely immobilised. Fate has closed its hand and the only response is to cry out into silence.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~who-is-your-daddy-and-what-does-he-do":{"haiku":"An interrogator threatens and terrifies a subject before a game that exposes which father is a drug dealer while others hold mundane jobs.","opus":"An interrogator threatens to shred his captives unless they reveal a hidden identity, working through toys and menace with cartoonish glee. Austrian Death Machine, As I Lay Dying guitarist Tim Lambesis's thrash tribute to Arnold Schwarzenegger, builds the whole track from Kindergarten Cop dialogue, turning a family comedy's classroom scene into a mock-brutal metal set piece where the joke is the deadpan reverence for the source.","sonnet":"A comically menacing interrogator demands children identify their fathers, cycling through possible blue-collar occupations and threatening absurd violence if the question goes unanswered. The premise is a parody of authority and playground coercion pushed to cartoonish extremes.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~who-writes-the-songs-the-real-bomb-track":{"haiku":"Power struggle over artistic control reveals masculinity performing through violence; dominance rewrites composition.","opus":"A comedy skit where a mock-Arnold Schwarzenegger seizes the studio to make everything more brutal, poking fun at both action-hero bravado and metal machismo. The whole joke of this Arnold-worshipping side project distilled into a bit.","sonnet":"A studio skit in which Arnold Schwarzenegger's persona overrules the band's guitarist and demands the music be made more brutal — replacing snare drums with booms and requesting a sample of a head being smashed — a parody of heavy metal production as action-movie command.","src":"haiku"},
"austrian-death-machine~you-have-just-been-erased":{"haiku":"A figure offers protection through erasure—new identity, severed ties, forced isolation. Resurrection requires annihilation of all past connections; the speaker controls through obliteration and psychological control.","opus":"A comedy piece stitched together from action-movie catchphrases, in keeping with this band's Schwarzenegger-tribute gimmick. It voices a witness-relocation handler who wipes out someone's identity and cuts him off from everyone he knew, the humor riding on deadpan threats to obliterate anybody who interferes.","sonnet":"Drawn from the Schwarzenegger film, the premise is simple protection through total identity erasure: everyone from the past must be treated as dead, all ties severed, so the endangered person can be kept alive under a new name.","src":"haiku"},
"author-punisher~drone-carrying-dread":{"haiku":"War devastates civilian lives through violence and forced assimilation; survival erodes humanity as occupation strips agency and dreams.","opus":"A harrowing war narrative told from the ground of a collapsing society, tracing a militia's overrun, flight to the border, and bartering ammunition for clothes and bread. It's survival stripped to its grimmest arithmetic, the soul's pathetic collapse under total war.","sonnet":"A grinding, fragmented account of conflict and displacement: laser sights, razor wire, militia in uniform, a flight east to the border trading ammunition for clothes, and survival reduced to raw calculation — \"the last of our bread for a rifle and bow\" — with no resolution, only endurance under a grey horizon.","src":"haiku"},
"autograf~metaphysical-feat-janelle-kroll":{"haiku":"A speaker questions the nature and location of love while hinting at transcendence, inviting surrender and release.","opus":"A dance track coaxing someone to stop overthinking love and simply let go, love located not in a place but in surrender. The repeated question about where you love is really a nudge toward transcending the need to define it.","sonnet":"A searching inquiry into where love is located — place, person, habit, surrender — suggests that love exists in the ordinary and the overlooked, accessible only through letting go. The song circles the question without fully landing, finding meaning in the asking.","src":"haiku"},
"autumn-lies-buried~inhuman-skin":{"haiku":"Criminal intent targets the destitute while power consolidates through violence. The weak remain systemically imprisoned while the strong reclaim control through threat and coercion.","opus":"Deathcore class-war fury aimed at a manipulative, self-serving ruler and the misinformation keeping people divided; the guillotine imagery pushes past complaint into open call for revolt against complacency and stolen power.","sonnet":"A furious anti-authoritarian call for the commoners to reclaim what a serpentine ruling class has stolen — parasitic, ego-driven power in stolen skin — escalating from economic critique to a revolutionary reckoning where the guillotine waits and the tongue of oppression gets cut out. The final verse names the proletariat's plotting as righteous prophecy.","src":"haiku"},
"ava-maybee~self-doubt":{"haiku":"Self-destructive impulses and the internalized belief that you're destined to fail yourself.","opus":"A depressive spiral narrated with grim humor, the singer cataloguing the small failures of just getting through a day while insisting no one bother trying to rescue her. The self-loathing has curdled into a kind of resigned identity.","sonnet":"A raw, self-aware account of depression-fueled self-sabotage — spending weeks in bed, cycling through hollow self-improvement gestures, and performing competence while privately collapsing. The image of painting walls in a burning house captures the futility of keeping up appearances while everything falls apart inside.","src":"haiku"},
"avalanche-effect~inner-strife":{"haiku":"Suffering paradoxically enables vitality; the price of feeling alive perpetuates internal warfare. Each moment of renewal spawns new conflict; the battle never concludes.","opus":"Melodic-metal reckoning with recovery's cruel twist: surviving the pain only to start a fresh war against yourself, chasing dreams that feel borrowed and concluding isolation is safer than hope.","sonnet":"Having survived one battle, the speaker finds the internal war starting again — all the same pain coming back — and is caught between dreams that feel borrowed and a need for reasons to fight or die for. The anguished refrain \"why is it all coming back to me?\" and the admission that they're probably better off alone frame chronic relapse as the true enemy.","src":"haiku"},
"avalanche-effect~manipulating-sky":{"haiku":"Lights blind the way forward as the speaker questions gentleness into darkness. Progress remains illusory; suffering persists without meaning while fabricated narratives govern existence.","opus":"Neon-lit alienation inside a society that feels like fiction, where the flashing signs and manufactured light hypnotize and control; the defiant core is the demand to be remembered, to insist that we were here and mattered.","sonnet":"Paralysed and hypnotised beneath neon signs that shine into sleepless eyes, the speaker lives in a fiction called society where nightmares board the soul and the sky manipulates through light. The final pivot — \"we were here, we mattered\" — is a defiant gasp against the threat of becoming insignificant in a brightened night that never ends.","src":"haiku"},
"avalon~deadbeat-boy":{"haiku":"Love anchored in prolonged stasis through chain-smoking devotion becomes unfulfilled prayer. The deadbeat remains deadbeat; promises dissolve into accumulated failure and cyclical devotion.","opus":"A woman shutting the door on a self-destructive lover she once wanted to keep, tangled with a dying father's shadow; the refrain is her reclaiming her life by refusing to let his ruin become her home.","sonnet":"Someone chain-smoking in a room, saying prayers and making promises they can't keep, is told in no uncertain terms they cannot stay — the repeated \"no, you can't stay with me\" shutting down a deadbeat boy whose father oscillates between predicting death and survival. The question of whether a partner's hand makes the narrator seem weak lands without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"avatar~violence-no-matter-what-duet-with-lzzy-hale":{"haiku":"A century of failure to prevent fascism exposes humanity's stubborn refusal to learn. The rage demands agreement that no individual deserves unchecked power; violence persists where ideology festers.","opus":"A blunt anti-fascist scream, incredulous that a century on people still won't agree that fascism is simply off the table. It names the rot as the belief that whoever does the most harm wins, and flatly refuses that logic.","sonnet":"A furious, incredulous challenge — after almost a century, why can't people agree that fascism is unacceptable? — drives a rallying cry against ideologies dependent on promised former glory and the hammer of violence. The repeating \"violence no matter what\" indicts both the ideology and the cycle it perpetuates.","src":"haiku"},
"avat~wolves":{"haiku":"Predatory forces tear the speaker apart piece by piece while they carry heaviness that seems insurmountable and inescapable.","opus":"Being consumed piece by piece by a relationship where a partner's pressure feeds on everything the speaker is, wolves tearing out and scattering them to the wind. Exhausted by carrying the weight in their head, they still wish to be made whole again.","sonnet":"A relationship in which one person drains and diminishes the other — the 'wolves' are the destructive pressures and manipulations from a partner who feeds on the speaker's vulnerabilities, leaving them mentally exhausted and questioning whether the person will stay when things get darkest.","src":"haiku"},
"avenged-sevenfold~afterlife":{"haiku":"Rejects paradise because living, growing, and struggling matter more than perfect peace.","opus":"A soul dragged into a paradise it never chose, insisting it belongs back among the living and the people still grieving it. The tension is between an offered heaven and a stubborn refusal to accept death on someone else's terms.","sonnet":"A soul arriving too early in the afterlife resists staying — the place is peaceful but wrong, and the speaker pleads to be sent back to live a full life, saying goodbye to the dead while begging God for another chance at the world he left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"avenged-sevenfold~almost-easy":{"haiku":"A person struggles with self-inflicted emotional damage and manipulation of a loved one, seeking forgiveness as they watch the relationship crumble.","opus":"A jittery admission from a man who won't bend, protesting his sanity even as guilt courses through him over the harm he's inflicted on a lover left tied to thoughts of death, a fractured inner voice making it far too simple to keep returning. The California band who broke through in the late 2000s stage this denial and self-ruin in theatrical hard-rock grandeur.","sonnet":"Remorse arriving after someone is already gone — the speaker knows they were the problem, that their rigidity and selfishness drove someone away and left them bound to terrible memories. Almost easy is the mocking name for the reconciliation that never quite comes, the return that's always just within reach. They're not insane, they insist, which means they fully understand the cost of what they did.","src":"haiku"},
"avenged-sevenfold~game-over":{"haiku":"Ordinary domestic comfort and hand-me-down satisfaction mask the extraordinary; mediocrity poses as fulfillment in borrowed memory.","opus":"A whole human life sped through in staccato flashes, from cradle imagery to the mundane comforts of TV and coffee, before it collapses into suicide and the shrug that life was only a dream. The band's late experimental streak using existential dread to ask whether any of the routine mattered.","sonnet":"A life compressed into noun-bursts: childhood, adolescence, marriage, dead end — the daily iterations accumulating until the speaker \"doesn't belong here anymore\" and, staring through warm tears at their reflection, decides to end it, closing with the question \"can't you see life is but a dream anyway?\"","src":"haiku"},
"avenged-sevenfold~hail-to-the-king":{"haiku":"A tyrant rises through violence and bloodshed, establishing an iron-fisted regime that crushes all resistance; the song depicts authoritarian conquest and the suffering of its subjects.","opus":"Avenged Sevenfold relish a despot's blood-soaked grab for the throne: kids left parentless by war, corpses strung up as decoration, and armored death rolling in to strip away people's freedoms while demanding they bow. It plays as a study of authoritarian conquest ruled by terror and a crushing grip.","sonnet":"The rise of a tyrannical ruler who silences dissent through fear and violence, demands absolute submission from his subjects, and enforces his reign with an iron fist over a population stripped of rights and surrounded by the bodies of those who resisted.","src":"haiku"},
"avenged-sevenfold~welcome-to-the-family":{"haiku":"Societal corruption and personal dissolution merge into a vision of inevitable loss; initiation into despair welcomes a new member into the family of the damned.","opus":"An ominous initiation into loss and violence, addressed to a kid whose reckless life leads somewhere unwinnable. Written after the death of drummer The Rev, the family being welcomed to is grief itself, a fight no one gets to win.","sonnet":"Two parallel addresses — one to a reckless kid, one to someone lost to premature death — frame a shared human condition of emptiness and inner war that cannot be won, welcoming the listener into a universal suffering no one escapes.","src":"haiku"},
"aviana~oblivion":{"haiku":"A traumatized person refuses to capitulate to an abuser, revealing rage and desire for the abuser's death after being systematically destroyed.","opus":"The metalcore act channels raw betrayal into a survivor's defiance, refusing to falter or state a comforting lie for a tormentor who buried the pieces of them alive. Torn and starving on stitches sewn from another's grin, the narrator turns wounded endurance into open bloodlust, wishing death on the coward who fled once the reckoning arrived.","sonnet":"A narrator endures systematic destruction by an abuser and refuses to break under it, ultimately turning raw fury back outward. Survival hardens into hatred, and the piece ends as a declaration of outlasting someone who tried to bury everything.","src":"haiku"},
"aviana~overcome":{"haiku":"The speaker suffocates under pressure and betrayal, begging for space and freedom to breathe as walls close in and they bleed internally.","opus":"Suffocation rendered as metalcore, a panic attack set to breakdowns: the narrator cornered by his own mind and by people who put him on a pedestal then pulled him under. Beneath the melodrama is a real plea for room to breathe and betrayal from those trusted.","sonnet":"The strain of being placed on a pedestal by others while internally suffocating — betrayed by someone's knife in the back, surrounded by voices urging self-destruction, the speaker begs for space and air to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"aviana~red-sky":{"opus":"A reminder that death can't be bargained with, aimed at people who exploit and abandon you when you're weakest. The song argues that a life is worth counting by its vivid instants rather than its duration, staging a bleak farewell to faith as everything ends.","sonnet":"A song about treachery and survival — people will steal your throne, kick you while you're down, and hold your head underwater — but life's meaning lies in moments that make you feel alive, not in the quantity of breath; the red sky at night cuts both ways as warning and wonder.","src":"sonnet"},
"aviations~dizziness-explained":{"opus":"A besieged, dreamlike stand against forces closing in, refusing to be taken by death or trade away what remains, and guarding one's own hands and soul from those who would exploit them.","sonnet":"A person in a situation of threat or confinement — surrounded by armed control and separated from loved ones — refuses to surrender to death or fear, insisting their life and what matters to them cannot be taken away even as dismemberment feels real and near.","src":"sonnet"},
"aviations~nineties-nine-ties":{"haiku":"Humans must break free from invisible bonds and choose collective action consciously rather than accepting imposed fate. Hands of steel and unstoppable force drive necessary change.","opus":"Djent-prog reaching for the cosmic and existential, urging that passion and self-worth be guarded like the last thing keeping you human against the void. A call to choose your own meaning before the world spends your life for you.","sonnet":"A call to shed the ideological and psychological ties that hold people back from following genuine passion — choosing what the world has done for you versus what you'll make of it yourself, framed as an urgency before your hands and will give out.","src":"haiku"},
"aviations~safehouse":{"haiku":"Relationships erode under pressure; self-interest overtakes connection when safety becomes uncertain.","opus":"A relationship dissolving over the fear of change, one person clinging to safety while the other resists becoming someone else. The prog-rock sprawl reaches for Tennessee Williams' glass menagerie to frame a fragile, self-protective retreat from real intimacy.","sonnet":"A relationship eroding under the weight of mismatched needs — one person wants safety above all, the other is losing themselves under strain, and communication has decayed to the point where tidal waves crash down like clichés. The glass menagerie reference frames the narrator as a fragile, sheltered thing finally attuning to someone else's traits — too late to prevent the fracture, just in time to see it clearly.","src":"haiku"},
"avicii~hey-brother":{"haiku":"Unconditional familial love transcends distance and loss; siblings promise absolute devotion, swearing nothing could prevent them from answering each other's call.","opus":"A vow of unconditional loyalty to a brother and sister, promising to answer any call and do anything if the sky comes down for them. It is about blood-deep devotion and faith in one another when everything is lost.","sonnet":"An unconditional vow of sibling loyalty: no distance, no loss is enough to stop the speaker from responding when a brother or sister calls — if the sky falls, there is nothing in the world they wouldn't do — a simple, sturdy declaration that blood and chosen family hold even when everything else collapses.","src":"haiku"},
"avicii~lonely-together-feat-rita-ora":{"haiku":"Toxic codependency and self-harm intertwine as two people poison each other while seeking comfort in mutual destruction and regret.","opus":"Two people clinging to a toxic, self-destructive love as a shared anesthetic, knowing they'll hate themselves come morning but choosing, for tonight, to be a little less alone in each other.","sonnet":"Two people who know they are bad for each other agree to be lonely together anyway — at the bottom of a bottle, the other person a poison in the wine — accepting they cannot change and will probably hate themselves tomorrow, but choosing the company of shared wreckage over solitary sobriety.","src":"haiku"},
"avicii~the-nights":{"haiku":"A father's advice to chase wild youth while you have it; don't let life become caution.","opus":"A father's deathbed advice distilled into a carpe-diem anthem, made unbearably poignant in retrospect by Tim Bergling's own early death. The wild heart living for younger days is the whole plea to make life memorable.","sonnet":"A father's parting advice to live boldly and memorably frames the whole song — the speaker internalises the call to venture far, carve a name in the stars, and treat the best nights as the ones that never die.","src":"haiku"},
"avicii~waiting-for-love":{"haiku":"Obstacles dissolve before love, and resilience persists through suffering, with faith in connection as the antidote to despair.","opus":"The Swedish producer builds a week-long ache into an anthem of stubborn hope, love as the miracle worth every day of longing. Heard after his death, its insistence that no obstacle defeats belief carries a poignancy he couldn't hold onto himself.","sonnet":"A week of emotional extremes — from broken and hopeless at the start to burning with hope by the weekend — is mapped day by day, with the speaker cycling through despair and resilience in the wait for love to arrive. Faith in love's eventual return is the only constant.","src":"haiku"},
"avicii~without-you-feat-sandro-cavazza":{"haiku":"Loss breeds destructive freedom; the speaker trades stability for recklessness, untethered and defiant without the anchor that once grounded them.","opus":"The panic-defiance of someone dumped into freedom he never wanted: throwing himself at the city to prove he can carry on alone, when the manic bravado only measures how much the absence has hollowed him. Released as Avicii circled his own exhaustion, it reads darker now.","sonnet":"After someone's departure exposes the gap between their promises and reality, the speaker decides to stop mourning and force himself back into life — channeling grief into restless action rather than sitting still with the loss. The resolution is fragile and defiant at once.","src":"haiku"},
"avi~alcantara":{"haiku":"Empty roads mark the speaker's addiction revelation; the scent of gasoline and young women blur together. Slanted entry into corners measures and judges before interaction; the pursuit becomes a compulsive ritual.","opus":"Polish car-culture flex built on street racing, petrol fumes and luxury interiors; beneath the swagger it's about the intoxicating rush of speed and status, the world shrinking as the engine screams.","sonnet":"A nocturnal street-racing scene in Poland — full tank, empty road, 98-octane like the girls, police lights in the blue — frames male bravado and luxury-car culture with a girl's feet resting on the Alcantara-trimmed cockpit as the world grows too small. Style, speed, and the thrill of escape from mundane rules define the atmosphere.","src":"haiku"},
"avi~fred-astaire":{"haiku":"Nightlife becomes sanctuary where responsibility dissolves and movement replaces thought—dancing through consequences rather than facing them, waltzing toward morning unmoored.","opus":"A blackout-glamorous night of drink, drugs and clubbing where the narrator glides like a dancer while someone else steers the sinking ship. Beneath the swagger sits mounting debt and loss of control, home only sometime after one.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop song about a night of club-hopping, drinking, and dancing with total abandon — the speaker glides through the chaos of the party scene with effortless style, letting someone else steer while he does his thing until well past midnight.","src":"haiku"},
"avi~gelenda":{"haiku":"Entering 2023 slightly older and wealthier, the narrator maintains unchanged style and slang. The same chaos continues; taxes persist; the cycle remains unbroken despite presumed advancement.","opus":"A Polish rapper marking his ascent from nothing, promising his mother she needn't worry now; the boast is that he arrived on foot and will leave in a G-Wagen, his craft and words prized above money as the real legacy.","sonnet":"Entering 2023 a little older and richer, the rapper insists his words are more durable than engraved metal and that the whole country is now watching — he arrived on foot and he's leaving in a Mercedes G-Class. The hook addressed to his mother — \"you don't have to worry anymore\" — anchors the ascent narrative between self-assurance and genuine tenderness.","src":"haiku"},
"avi~grand-prix":{"haiku":"Hard-won triumph over poverty and doubt crowns survival as victory as the speaker claims their stake in excellence, refusing to remain invisible or grateful for crumbs.","opus":"A Polish rap of hard-won triumph, the crew that started with nothing racing for gold they earned rather than bought, blood, sweat, and tears behind them. The Maradona and Hamilton references frame their rise as a vindication, turning the crosses others marked them with into monuments.","sonnet":"A hard-earned rise from nothing: the speaker came from a starting position of disadvantage, bled and sweated for every inch, and now stands on the podium — chasing the Grand Prix not for prestige but because they know what it is to have nothing, and winning is the only kind of revenge that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~b0red":{"haiku":"Existential numbness from isolation and excess leaves everything hollow; the speaker cannot escape the familiar grip of boredom that defines their existence.","opus":"Chronic emptiness that no amount of getting high or drowning in loud music can fill. The boredom is self-perpetuating—the narrator knows he'll sabotage himself again, and the numbness is his only companion.","sonnet":"A song about numbing, purposeless depression — the speaker is stuck in a loop of getting high, burning out, isolating, and feeling nothing even when music or substances temporarily elevate them; boredom here is not idleness but a symptom of something deeper that keeps cycling back no matter what.","src":"haiku","fable":"Boredom here is a cover story, the last acceptable word for something the singer can't say plainly. The blank teenage chorus keeps insisting there's nothing to do while every verse quietly confesses something heavier: getting high enough to move into a house among the clouds, going numb in the burnout, no name in the phone worth dialing. The bridge finally drops the mask and names the machine — a slow self-erasure he half-suspects is only running in his head, a rut he treats as his one companion because he wrecked things and already knows the next wreck is coming. So when the song loops back to a body pinned motionless to the plaster overhead, a speck flattened against paint, the word has curdled: not the absence of feeling but the mask laid over depression, the syllable you can say out loud when the truer one won't come.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"boredom as the only sayable word for a slower self-erasure","built":"the body pinned to the ceiling closes a song that never used the word depression"},
"avoid~blast-off":{"haiku":"Intoxication offers escape from reality but spirals into paranoia and emptiness; the speaker floats untethered while celebrating the high.","opus":"A stoned joyride that tips from euphoria into paranoid panic, the walls talking and the high spiraling out of control. The spoken coda swings back to giddy love of getting high, revealing the whole thing as reckless self-medication that don't mean nothing.","sonnet":"A weed trip that goes much further than expected — the narrator gets so high they lose track of where they are, hear the walls talking, and feel unmoored from reality — before landing back in euphoric gratitude for their life, friends, and fast cars.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~burn":{"haiku":"Self-annihilation accelerates toward completion; the speaker welcomes the flames that will reduce everything to dust and extinguish remaining hope.","opus":"Jaded self-destruction narrated from bed at night, ticking down the seconds until poor choices leave him behind, letting the shame and the bloodlust do their work. The wish is to torch it all until no home is left to return to.","sonnet":"A person trapped in self-loathing and purposelessness lies awake suffocating on shame, knows they are their own worst enemy, and ultimately lets the whole thing burn rather than keep fighting to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~can-t-take-this-away":{"haiku":"Permanence and inalienable possession despite external threat, the speaker asserting what cannot be removed or stolen.","opus":"Defiant refusal to let anyone diminish the thing you love, delivered at reckless speed by people who enjoy watching you fail. Skate-punk energy aimed at proving nothing to anyone and keeping the one thing that's yours.","sonnet":"Defensive autonomy against people who want to watch you fall — pushing 90 in a cloud of dust, going off the wall by choice, driving until it's right. The core assertion is simple: I'm living the way I want to and you can never take that from me.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~cowabunga":{"haiku":"Someone withdraws far from others intentionally, spiraling and sinking deeper while fighting the urge to surface, seeking distance to find themselves again.","opus":"A young hardcore act on isolation and self-sabotage, deliberately unreachable while sinking faster than before and refusing pity; the fight to claw back a self dug into a hole he admits he made himself.","sonnet":"A song about dissociation and disappearing into yourself — the narrator is sinking faster than before, deliberately making themselves unfindable, but still holds onto the desire to get back to themselves and leave the rest of the world alone.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~finish-line-3-live-on-dvd":{"haiku":"Achievement and completion despite obstacles, the speaker crossing final threshold and attaining goal.","opus":"A cornered narrator alone against someone he'd do anything to silence, flogging a lost cause and only sinking deeper. Lost in a fog and near cracking, he grips the vow that he'll get out before it's too late and that he's holding up — reassurance that sounds as much like a plea as a boast.","sonnet":"Exhausted defiance from someone who keeps beating a dead horse into the ground — nothing changes, the haze thickens, but the cage is finally opening and they're coming out of it, still upright, claiming 'fuck you I got mine.' The brevity is the point: barely enough words to hold the energy in.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~flashbang":{"haiku":"Losing one's sense of self amid chaos and neurotic manipulation, struggling to remember identity while reality crumbles.","opus":"The Finnish nu-metalcore band voice a mind coming apart under drugs and pressure, someone who has lost all bearings and can barely recall his own identity, with almost no time to think straight. As his reality falls to pieces, panic hardens into spite, and he resolves to outlast the people trying to break him.","sonnet":"A state of disorientation and rage — the narrator has lost their sense of self, can't see straight, feels manipulated and looked down on, and wants to watch those responsible fall while struggling to remember who they are.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~gator-fest":{"haiku":"Burnout metastasizes into a feedback loop: the machinery of performance grinds on but delivers only static and desperation with nowhere to escape.","opus":"Avoid capture creative collapse and self-disgust, soused while trying to write, foggy and sick of the whole thing, unable to squeeze out anything that lands. The block sours into pure, foul-mouthed exhaustion.","sonnet":"A song about creative burnout and the frustration of being unable to produce — the speaker is exhausted, drunk on the process, unable to think straight, and feels trapped in a monotonous grind where nothing comes out right.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~heat":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and withdrawal; a person rejects others' attempts to fix them while battling internal dissipation.","opus":"A narrator so worn down he can't rest or eat hardens himself and pushes away anyone trying to help, insisting his pain concerns no one. This post-hardcore track from Avoid shows that prickly independence tipping into a wish to vanish somewhere untraceable, recovery and flight tangled as he chases a relief he can't define.","sonnet":"Depleted and retreating into sleep to escape the waking world, a person firmly rebuffs anyone who tries to fix or reach them. They insist on self-reliance, demand to be left alone, and push toward a place where no one can locate or contain them.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~hostage-at-a-beach-house-party":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness, isolation despite social setting, and mental-health crisis. The speaker feels trapped and alone even around others, battling medication, blackouts, and inability to function.","opus":"Avoid track a numbness so deep the speaker aches for any sensation, hauled to an LA party where he passes out night after night and feels caged in his own skull, off his prescriptions and drifting deeper into gloom. The Seattle pop-punk crew pin the loneliness of a crowded party to one screamed line, self-hatred dressed as a good time.","sonnet":"A person numbed by medication lapses and blackout drinking finds himself trapped at a social gathering that feels like captivity, screaming inwardly while everyone around him carries on. The yearning to feel something real clashes with his inability to stop self-destructing.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~if-it-hurts":{"haiku":"Severing a painful relationship by erasing emotional memory; drowning oneself rather than endure the wreckage another person created.","opus":"Avoid deliver a toughened farewell to a love that ruined everything, the speaker refusing to mind the pain because the partner only made things worse. Each recollection scorches away, and the numb wish to feel nothing at all marks the toll of moving on.","sonnet":"A clean severance: the relationship made a mess, the other person never noticed how they became the problem, and the narrator doesn't care anymore if it hurts. Memory is being burned away deliberately, and the clarity is final — I don't want to think — though the echoing refrain suggests it hasn't quite gone yet.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~midnight-six":{"haiku":"Inability to release past regrets and uncertainty about direction consume the present; the speaker spirals in obsessive reflection, trapped by decisions and loss of control.","opus":"The trap of dwelling on everything outside your control and not being able to release it, watching your own life burn in the distance. A restless meditation on losing yourself to overthinking with no way back.","sonnet":"Paralysis in the face of lost control — the narrator can't let go of rumination, knows they can't shape the world around them but can't stop dwelling, and watches their life burn in the distance while falling further behind. \"I'm so far gone\" is both exhaustion and resignation, but \"there's no turning back now\" frames it as a threshold crossed rather than a failure.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~my-world":{"haiku":"A ruler reigns through threat and violence. Breaking enters the territory while destruction comes swift.","opus":"Beatdown-hardcore territorial menace, all bloody-knuckle threats and dominance over an enemy who won't back off; the point is pure defiance, planting a flag and daring anyone to run their mouth on your turf.","sonnet":"A threat aimed at someone who has pushed the narrator too far — graphic promises of physical violence (broken nose, skull from skin) frame the song as a territorial assertion: this is the narrator's world, stay out or get destroyed.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~over-now":{"haiku":"Collapsed love and unresolved conflict leave the speaker searching for escape as the relationship spins away uncontrollably. What promised meaning becomes only wreckage, and honesty breeds only alienation.","opus":"The disorientation of being drawn in and then pushed away, told he pushed too hard after being led to think it was real. Chasing something already finished, reeling across the globe with nowhere to fall apart, he can't release a bond built on lies that both nourish and drown him.","sonnet":"A post-breakup reckoning where the speaker searches for explanation or closure that never comes, drowned in lies and unable to find a way out, circling the grief of something that is already finished.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~song-2":{"haiku":"Detached numbness from physical and emotional sensation, accepting the paradox of needing connection while pushing it away.","opus":"A Blur cover, so the read is Blur's: nonsense adrenaline built to be screamed, a burst of aggression that means nothing and everything, mocking the very idea that rock needs a message while delivering the purest possible one.","sonnet":"A Blur cover: head checked by a jumbo jet, heavy metal and pins and needles, lying easy, never sure why the other is needed. The woo-hoo refrain carries nonchalance that barely conceals the admission that nothing is easy and the need is real.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~song-about-james":{"haiku":"A person feels defeated by another's superiority and success, struggling to overcome a history where they failed while the other won.","opus":"The Finnish nu-metalcore band channel bruised competitiveness into an aggressive grievance aimed at a rival who always seemed a step ahead, someone who solved everything first and beat the singer to it. Left standing wrecked and humiliated at the knowledge that his opponent surpassed him, the narrator swears he will regroup and return stronger.","sonnet":"Defeat conceded without excusing the other person — the speaker failed, and James got the best of them, but the song insists it wasn't James's doing exactly: the speaker could see it coming and lost anyway. Now standing broken in changed scenery, they promise to come back harder, but that vow sits alongside the honest admission that they've been thoroughly beaten.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~sour-apple":{"haiku":"The speaker destroys everything around them while hating themselves for the pattern, too broken to stop, trapped at the beginning where it all started wrong.","opus":"Relentless self-loathing and a wish to rewind to the exact point it all went wrong, unable to stop wrecking everything within reach. The sourness in his head never lets up, a loop of guilt and self-sabotage that always drops him right back where he started.","sonnet":"A relentless stream of self-contempt — the speaker is convinced they are worthless, that the only peace available is death, that they cannot stop destroying everything and everyone around them, trapped in a bitter loop of bad thoughts that never end.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~split-kill-it":{"haiku":"Panic and intrusive thoughts consume the speaker's body; they suffocate under internal noise while begging for acknowledgment.","opus":"A visceral panic attack rendered from the inside, numbness and racing thoughts and the urge to strangle the spiral until it stops for good. The narrator screams to be heard, desperate to make someone understand while his own body turns against him.","sonnet":"A song about a panic attack or dissociative episode spiraling out of control — the speaker's body goes numb, their thoughts pile up, the silence is white noise, and someone else's words become useless intrusions while they beg for the internal chaos to just die so they can breathe again.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~the-song-of-the-summer":{"haiku":"Burnout and dissociation render all moments hollow; the effort to perform normalcy becomes unbearable.","opus":"The hollow aftermath of chasing good times you no longer feel, driving numb and pretending you're someone else. A burnout portrait of nostalgia curdling into dread, where the party stopped working but the habit didn't.","sonnet":"Social anxiety at the peak of summer: stale smoke and windows down trigger nostalgia for when going out felt fine, but now driving with eyes closed, checking the mirror and hating it, the brain missing — the good time is only ever a conditional promise made to himself that he already doubts.","src":"haiku"},
"avoid~whatever":{"haiku":"Shallow pop culture feeds endless appetite for recycled content while artists pump out easy hits designed to please empty crowds. Mediocrity becomes the standard; everyone repeating the same dead formulas while claiming originality.","opus":"A takedown of algorithm-fed sameness and the people who recycle other people's ideas while blaming the world for their own stagnation. The narrator distances himself from a hollow influence, vowing not to become the empty face he despises.","sonnet":"A direct attack on creative conformity and artistic laziness — the speaker is disgusted by how everyone in their scene recycles the same ideas and blames the world instead of themselves, and ultimately resolves to leave rather than become the same kind of hollow, formulaic artist they despise.","src":"haiku","fable":"A party band's kiss-off to the feed, this aims its contempt at recycled content and then flinches into self-awareness, because the easy formula it mocks is the one it admits still slaps. That doubleness is the whole song: it can't decide whether it's indicting the algorithm-brained conformist or confessing to being another supplier of the same product, gold hunted in shelf-dust. The turn arrives when the target stops being someone on a screen and becomes a future version of the singer, so the sneered refrain of fakeness curdles into a plea against becoming the very thing it despises.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"contempt for the algorithm that curdles into suspicion of the singer's own complicity","built":"the sneer at recycled content turns on itself when the target becomes a future version of the speaker"},
"avralize~like-a-boomerang":{"haiku":"Trauma and pain that returns cyclically despite attempts to heal, trapping the speaker in repeated emotional cycles.","opus":"Trauma as something that returns no matter how you bury it, the metalcore staple of self-sabotage where the parasite whispering lies is your own mind. Healing only exposes fresh wounds, and the loop never breaks.","sonnet":"About being unable to escape a destructive internal voice or toxic influence that keeps returning no matter how much work you do — the narrator tries to heal, but shame, darkness, and self-sabotage keep cycling back. The parasite metaphor makes the enemy internal rather than external.","src":"haiku"},
"avralize~wanderlust":{"haiku":"Constant flight pursues intensified sensation; the speaker hungers for tangible experience. This haunting pull defies reason; wanderlust becomes inexplicable force driving toward unreality.","opus":"Restlessness as an illness, the compulsive chase for something genuine that spoils every still moment; a siren-pull and scorched bridges frame the itch to keep moving as dread of missing out disguised as freedom.","sonnet":"An unshakeable restlessness — poisonous when still, bone-rattling in its pull — keeps the speaker burning bridges and running toward something real they can never name. The sirens sing in the silence and the FOMO drowns them, making wanderlust itself the wound and the only possible salve.","src":"haiku"},
"awake-at-last~unfinished-business":{"haiku":"Internal conflict between who we were and who we are drives unfinished purpose; the misfits carry business that extends beyond death.","opus":"Refusing to quit despite being at war with your former self and worn down by everyone's pain. An anthem for misfits who take the fallen as their reason to keep going, defiant even facing the grave.","sonnet":"A speaker torn between who they were and who they are — questioning whether kindness is weakness — rallies misfits and the fallen with the conviction that there is unfinished business ahead, that the world cannot rain on the parade of those who hold it inside their hands.","src":"haiku"},
"awake-the-dreamer~atmosphere":{"haiku":"The world is collapsing under damage and fear while people search for meaning or divine intervention. The speaker feels imprisoned by nothingness and time, begging to escape themselves.","opus":"The metalcore band capture a moment of surrender, feeling trapped, hollow and beyond rescue while sensing some shift coming in the air. Casting wounded, lost people across a fearful world, the song keeps demanding to know where a supposed God is hiding, and pleads to be rescued from humanity's own worst impulses, layering religious doubt over shared despair.","sonnet":"Trapped in a world of damaged souls and existential fear, a group cries out for divine or collective intervention. The atmosphere itself feels changed — corrupted — and the song oscillates between despair at the absence of God and a trembling, stubborn will to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"awaken-i-am~relic":{"haiku":"Past becomes inescapable bloodline; the speaker feels the other's presence within their veins, unable to separate inheritance from identity.","opus":"A toxic pull the speaker can't resist, drawn helplessly toward the flame even as the truth tears him apart and the best of him left when she did. Feeling her blood in his veins and her voice through every breath, hands at his throat, he picks feeling alive over the safety of letting go.","sonnet":"Drawn like a moth to a flame to someone whose hands are around the speaker's throat, they confess wrongdoing but cannot let go — feeling their blood in their veins and voice in every breath — choosing at last to stop lying just to buy time alive.","src":"haiku"},
"awaken-i-am~roses":{"opus":"A messy, on-again-off-again relationship kept alive out of desire and denial; the couple keep playing pretend and hooking up while blaming the wine, knowing they're near the end but, like cut roses, refusing to admit it's dead.","sonnet":"About a relationship that both people know is dying but refuse to end — after a night of drinking, fighting, and sex, the narrator acknowledges they are past the point of saving yet keeps going through the motions, like flowers that don't know they are already dead.","src":"sonnet"},
"away~help-me":{"haiku":"Self-hatred corrupts love; the speaker cannot receive care without pain, requiring punishment to validate connection and prove worthiness.","opus":"The self-sabotage of someone who can't accept love because they hate themselves, mistaking being hurt for what they deserve and choking off anything good. They warn a patient partner they'll never be enough, and know it.","sonnet":"Someone who hates themselves so deeply they cannot understand why their partner stays, asking to be hurt because kindness feels undeserved — trapped in a tiny box of shame, unable to stop asking to be helped or hurt.","src":"haiku"},
"axty~dark-eyes":{"opus":"Axty wrestle with suicidal thinking, sinking deeper than ever under the pull of inner demons and past errors, yet holding onto hope and refusing to give up. The repeated plea for a steadying hand runs against the gloom named in the title, survival cast as a grinding daily fight to reach the next morning.","sonnet":"A song about suicidal ideation and the active decision to resist it — the narrator describes going deep into darkness, having been suicidal before, and refusing to let the thoughts take over again. The plea 'take my hand, don't let me drown' is directed outward at someone or something, while the defiant close insists they will not go back down.","src":"sonnet"},
"azealia-banks~212-feat-lazy-jay":{"haiku":"Brazen swagger claims supremacy through sexuality and verbal dominance, dismissing rivals as mediocre while celebrating fierce competitive appetite.","opus":"A young Harlem rapper announcing her arrival with pure predatory swagger, weaponizing sex and geography to devour anyone who mistook her for competition. The bravado is a manifesto: she is the future and everyone else is already forgotten.","sonnet":"An assertive, sexually confident speaker out-competes and out-performs everyone around her — rivals, men who underestimate her, women who want what she has — delivered in a rush of overlapping bravado that positions her as the definitive presence in any room.","src":"haiku"},
"aziya~atomic":{"haiku":"Divine love intoxicates and paralyzes moral judgment; the speaker surrenders reason to devotion, sacrificing truth for superficial bliss and religious ecstasy.","opus":"Desire so intense it becomes religion, worshipping a lover's touch while admitting the bliss is superficial and fleeting. This British rocker plays the material girl who half-believes her own devotion, ready to call it off by morning.","sonnet":"Desire so overwhelming it overrides reason — the narrator knows the attraction is superficial bliss that \"never means anything\" but prays for it anyway, wants to be a \"ceremonial lie\" at the altar, and buries fear in the neck they kiss. Love-as-religion turns predatory when alone feels worse than false devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"aziya~bambi":{"haiku":"Hunted and vulnerable, the speaker rejects victimhood and strikes back at the predator, though escape means abandonment of everything familiar.","opus":"Refusing to be prey in a hostile, violence-filled digital world, the speaker sensing a hungry beast at her back and deciding it's time to stop being Bambi and fight. The pressure to work harder and be stronger sharpens the fable into a survival stance against being devoured.","sonnet":"A young woman, overwhelmed by a violent world and told to work harder, refuses the role of helpless prey; sensing the predator behind her, she resolves to stop being Bambi and start fighting back, turning vulnerability into defiance.","src":"haiku"},
"aziya~bbydoll":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects possession and performative compliance; independence emerges through deliberate abandonment.","opus":"A refusal to be reduced to someone's decorative plaything, spitting out a sour lover and leaving only a letter behind. She insists she needs no one to pick or choose her, self-possession curdling into bitter independence.","sonnet":"A woman rejecting the passive, decorative role a man wanted her to play — she spits out the bad taste of the relationship, leaves a letter on his bed, and walks out with the confidence that she has everything she needs and will never be reduced to his 'baby doll.'","src":"haiku"},
"aziya~call-my-name-lux-lisbon":{"haiku":"Cyanide clouds the mind while night devours day; the speaker lies in gardens awaiting closure. Darkness closes eyes with practiced certainty as suburban fate compresses existence.","opus":"Drawing on The Virgin Suicides' suffocating suburban doom, it renders a girl's slow erasure by someone who wants everything about her except her; each surrender feels like another quiet death of the self.","sonnet":"Framed by the suburban fate of the Lisbon girls, the speaker watches themselves erode — each time someone calls their name, another part dies, another virgin suicide. The garden image, cyanide in the air, and violets in bloom set a beautiful-and-poisoned scene where the waiting itself is the breaking.","src":"haiku"},
"aziya~chain":{"haiku":"Obedience and context collapse strip agency from those who surrender; the speaker demands acknowledgment of the trap itself before escape becomes possible.","opus":"Aziya voice exasperation with a partner who shelters in the comfort of going along and never inconveniencing themselves, warping the meaning of things and staying stuck, while she debates whether to keep reaching out or leave.","sonnet":"About a relationship where one person keeps twisting context, living in convenient obliviousness, and refusing accountability — the speaker is exhausted by the other's obedience to avoidance and tries to decide whether to wear the chain of this dynamic or throw it away entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"aziya~diamonds":{"haiku":"Wealth and materialism fail to impress; the speaker asserts independence and self-sufficiency, rejecting suitors and greed while celebrating her own autonomy.","opus":"A brush-off to men who think wealth buys her, flipping the material-girl trope by insisting she already has everything she needs. It's self-possession dressed as a swagger anthem, telling the millionaire his money means nothing.","sonnet":"Refusing to be bought — a man boasting millions and trying to impress with money gets nothing, because the narrator already has what she needs (\"I got mine\") and moves too fast to be caught. The song is confident disdain for transactional pursuit, dressed in a high-energy rebuff.","src":"haiku"},
"aziya~he-s-mine":{"haiku":"Romantic confidence and claiming ownership of a partner, asserting a relationship that satisfies material and emotional desires.","opus":"A woman batting away the players and doubters circling a relationship she's certain of, jet-setting between cities on a love she never expected to find. The swagger is really relief that it finally happened to her.","sonnet":"A song about the security of a new, healthy relationship after a pattern of heartbreak — the narrator found unexpected love in an ordinary place, and now turns away other interested parties with confidence because this one is clearly different and genuinely hers.","src":"haiku"},
"aziya~mars-retrograde":{"haiku":"Time suspends indefinitely; the speaker waits motionless, watching hours collapse while unable to escape the gravitational pull of someone's gaze.","opus":"Being left stranded and stuck in reverse cast as a planet in retrograde, waiting on someone who took her dancing and vanished. The astrological conceit turns heartbreak into being held static, moving backward for days.","sonnet":"A woman who was promised something by a man and then left stranded and stagnant — held in place by his gaze, stuck moving backwards, waiting for time to start again.","src":"haiku"},
"aziya~wundagirl":{"haiku":"Impossible expectations destroy potential saviors; the speaker recognizes they cannot be what others desperately need, freedom found only in rejection.","opus":"The crushing weight of being groomed for stardom, expected to hand the world everything and become everyone's rescuer the instant she comes of age. The counter-mantra is refusal: it was never really her they wanted, and she owes them nothing.","sonnet":"A young woman pressured to become a star and carry everyone's hopes at twenty-one — she rejects the expectation entirely, refusing to owe the world anything or become their miracle girl.","src":"haiku"},
"azusa~monument":{"haiku":"People build false monuments of spirituality and faith to rationalize their suffering and shield themselves from emotional truth.","opus":"Avant-metal group Azusa attacks the way people prop up religious appearances to avoid uncomfortable truths. The song describes belief being reshaped to fit convenience while real emotion is buried, then demands the listener strip away that comforting facade and confront reality directly, a dismantling of faith built as self-protection.","sonnet":"A sacred communal structure — built to sustain emotional numbness and spiritual conformity — is interrogated and found hollow. The lyric calls for someone willing to pierce the collective facade, to undo the praise and unmake the comforting lie, confronting the congregation with the unpleasant reality they have built their monument to suppress.","src":"haiku"},
"b-a~duvet":{"haiku":"One person deceives another masked in candlelit charm; both are falling, drowning, and losing everything while asking for help to breathe.","opus":"A song of gentle heartbreak sung to a man who seemed trustworthy but isn't, the singer claiming to see straight through him even amid a soft, candlelit closeness. That tenderness sits against a desperate cry of sinking and losing everything, exposing how the singer is quietly coming undone beneath a composed exterior.","sonnet":"A person drowning in psychological collapse — she sees through the comforting lies others tell, feels herself falling and fading beyond rescue, and the final desperate plea to 'help me breathe' is addressed to no one who can actually reach her.","src":"haiku"},
"b-a~twilight":{"haiku":"Love triangle torment in twilight hours—the speaker loves someone who loves another while also loving them, suspended in that liminal space between day and night where three people cannot be one.","opus":"A tender, painful reckoning with a love triangle, the narrator loved but not chosen, dwelling in a liminal twilight while the person she wants aches for another, the intimacy real yet suspended and doomed.","sonnet":"A triangle of longing played out in the grey zone — the narrator loves someone who loves someone else, and in the twilight of dawn they wake beside him knowing he really wants the other woman, the honest grief of being the wrong person in someone's bed.","src":"haiku"},
"babe-haven~blind-yourself":{"haiku":"Unnamed experience breeds vitality while repetition fails to grasp the sought sensation. Taste hovers at the edge of perception; holding closer intensifies the paradox of never quite reaching.","opus":"A hazy, craving pull toward someone alongside a sharp accusation that they've built a sick fantasy and blinded themselves to reality; the closeness it begs for is undercut by watching the other person self-deceive.","sonnet":"A near-physical memory — almost tasted, placed with the naked eye — competes with the awareness that time is running out, and the speaker urges the other to go ahead and blind themselves with their own sick fantasy rather than face what's real. The closing imperative \"hold me closer\" cuts against the cynicism with naked need.","src":"haiku"},
"babebee~alone":{"haiku":"Internal darkness consumes from within, while external pressure drains emotional reserves; isolation deepens as the speaker battles both internal demons and others' demands.","opus":"A restless dread that can't be fought off or loved away, curdling into paranoia and a plea not to be abandoned. The demand for closeness and the refusal to trust anyone pull against each other until neither wins.","sonnet":"About being consumed by an internal darkness that will not let go despite the narrator's attempts to fight it, while simultaneously desperate not to be left alone with it. There is a toxic relationship layer — someone who cut them with precision and covered it up — but the main weight is the narrator's fear of their own inner ghosts and the emotional paralysis that leaves them seeing specters and unable to control their feelings.","src":"haiku"},
"babebee~blame":{"haiku":"False niceness masks internal decay while death approaches unanswered. The speaker despises deception; confessions of shame demand self-preservation and departure.","opus":"Taking on all the guilt of a relationship soured by lies, telling the other person to save themselves; the hyperpop-tinged despair sits in the shame of disappearing and letting go rather than fighting to be understood.","sonnet":"Trapped in a dynamic of lying, false niceness, and fear, the speaker internalises all the blame rather than fight — \"so I'll take all the blame\" — while asking the other to save themselves from them. The hunt-like quality of being preyed upon and the inability to disappear produce a spiral of shame and self-erasure.","src":"haiku"},
"babebee~next2u":{"haiku":"Reckless speed and early death loop as the narrator accelerates toward wrong destinations. Despite directional confusion, proximity remains the only certainty worth pursuing.","opus":"Reckless live-fast romance racing toward someone as the only fixed point amid rollercoaster instability; the bravado masks a raw dread of being alone, needing that person home before the crash comes.","sonnet":"Speeding recklessly — 80 on the dashboard, wrong-way anxiety — the speaker's only stable point is wanting to be next to someone, even as motion sickness, loneliness, and the rollercoaster of uncertainty threaten to swallow them. Heaven's goodwill and the other person's proximity are the only things that might hold the ride together.","src":"haiku"},
"babebee~sunset-blvd":{"haiku":"Absence distorts reality while self-dissociation prevents bodily recognition. The speaker loops negative memories while seeking help for habitual self-sabotage.","opus":"Babebee capture dissociation and relapse after a split, unable to register their own body's touch and dogged by the harsh words a partner left behind. The plea to descend from the sky is really a wish to feel present and wanted once more.","sonnet":"Reality has dissolved without the other person — dissociating, stuck in bad memories, relapsing into the sky and touching down into heartbreak. Hate for this place and the constant sightings of the lost person collapse into \"I hate it here, I need you,\" a bare admission that the separation is unbearable.","src":"haiku"},
"babes-in-toyland~bruise-violet":{"haiku":"Betrayal and contempt corrode trust from within; the speaker exposes a liar whose spine is glued to deceit, wishing rotting upon their dishonest core.","opus":"A furious kiss-off to a two-faced liar who shadows the singer, twisting stories and stealing what's hers, a rival made of glue where a spine should be. Raw contempt, start to finish.","sonnet":"A scorched address to a liar who twists other people's stories and lives by fabrication — \"born with glue instead of spine\" — and the speaker, burned and furious, is done with contempt for someone whose glittering deception has followed her around and tangled itself through the speaker's own life.","src":"haiku"},
"baby-huey~hard-times":{"haiku":"Urban poverty and social alienation breed fear and isolation; seeking brotherhood yields only exploitation as the narrator endures repeated rejection and deprivation.","opus":"A gentle, oversized soul singer feeling the whole world recoil from him, finding no brotherhood even among his own, surviving on scraps and cheap wine. The ache is loneliness and poverty as constant weather, sung by a man who died before the record broke.","sonnet":"A man isolated by his own difference and the city's indifference retreats to his party house, afraid that showing himself will get his pride hurt — and when he does reach out to a brother, finds corruption even there, while poverty and loneliness grind on.","src":"haiku"},
"baby-huey~running":{"opus":"A soul confession of doomed pursuit, a man chasing a woman determined to get away even as he knows someone else has already taken his place. Baby Huey turns love's foolishness into weary self-ridicule, a man who prides himself on his sense yet can't stop pursuing what's already lost.","sonnet":"A soul song about the humiliation of chasing a woman who has already moved on — the narrator runs exhausted after someone who wants to escape him, knowing she is probably already with another man, unable to stop himself despite his rational mind knowing he should let go. Love has made a fool of an educated man.","src":"sonnet"},
"babychaos~0493":{"haiku":"Homelessness and internal void create desperation where only forgetting offers relief from accumulated loss and isolation.","opus":"Losing your home and every anchor, running with nowhere left to go as the wishing well runs dry. A hyperpop-tinged spiral of dissociation where control slips and the idea of home dissolves entirely.","sonnet":"A woman running from a home she has already lost circles through dissociation and displacement, the refrain \"nowhere is home\" anchoring a sense of total rootlessness punctuated by the haunting image of \"beauty in my bodice, heartbreak in the after dust.\"","src":"haiku"},
"babychaos~flesh":{"haiku":"The song centers reclamation through embodied existence. The narrator sheds restraint, defies violence with presence, and needs no revenge—the world already knows the abuser's worth.","opus":"A fierce survival declaration aimed at an abuser, the singer thriving free of their control and declining revenge because the tormentor is already beneath contempt. The crucial turn converts inflicted pain into strength, so cruelty only fuels the singer, making it a defiant reclaiming of power from someone who chose to harm.","sonnet":"Survival despite someone who chose cruelty when silence was available — the speaker has come through the abuse thriving and growing, unbothered by the threat of revenge because the world already knows what the other person is. There's contempt but no performance of pain. The speaker is off the leash, present in the body, and done being diminished by someone they've reduced to a stain.","src":"haiku"},
"babychaos~guilty-hands-i-bleed":{"haiku":"Heartlessness offers refuge from the burning pain of remembrance. Emotional coldness becomes strategy; tomorrow's void protects against regression into past trauma.","opus":"Choosing coldness as armor, preferring numbness to burning through a painful past; the washing of blood from guilty hands is a hardened vow to hold the reins, cut ties, and never look back.","sonnet":"Choosing coldness over burning in the past, the speaker performs a ritual cleansing — scrubbing blood and guilt from their hands, burning the other's imprint from their lips — and grants themselves the reins. Tomorrow's heartlessness is a deliberate armour, not absence of feeling but refusal to go back.","src":"haiku"},
"babychaos~icon":{"haiku":"Fragmented pieces of the self scatter through observers' vision as worship blinds followers. The icon feigns authenticity while commanding devotion; end times arrive through pretense.","opus":"A biting take on idol worship, voiced by the icon watching followers paint themselves in its image; the sting is that the fan projects onto the idol what they refuse to praise in themselves, mistaking imitation for identity.","sonnet":"An icon observes those who paint their faces to look like it, follow it, and pretend — while the icon itself asks why they don't just worship themselves instead. The mirroring relationship between idol and devotee is exposed as parasitic and built on pretence, with the icon watching from the top as the hand it crafted bites back.","src":"haiku"},
"babylon-zoo~animal-army":{"haiku":"Prehistoric and wild creatures unite and dance in an imagined paradise; the speaker summons friends to witness a transcendent animal spectacle.","opus":"Babylon Zoo parade a dreamlike menagerie of dancing creatures, a striding dinosaur, gathering lions, leopards and tigers in a glossy sweet-toned paradise where you sit atop an elephant and boast of being angelic. The British group behind the mid-1990s hit Spaceman trade in glammy, psychedelic spectacle, the beast processions a hallucination more about color and strut than meaning.","sonnet":"A vision of paradise populated by every creature — tyrannosaurus, lions, elephants, leopards, tigers, chrome and candy all sharing the same impossible space. There's no narrative threat, only the ecstatic accumulation of the animal army marching through a world the speaker calls their own. It reads as pure wish-fulfilment, a paradise built from wildness rather than order.","src":"haiku"},
"babylon-zoo~fire-guided-light":{"haiku":"Strangers await an apocalyptic death, dancing under cosmic fire while insisting on transformation and refusing societal pressure.","opus":"Jas Mann's British glam-electronic project wraps apocalyptic dread in strutting rhythm, staging a jittery premonition of death shared between two people convinced their end is near. Amid the mutual sense of doom, the only answers offered are a demand for change and a repeated reassurance of belief, the title's glow a faint promise of transcendence.","sonnet":"An ominous crowd scene teetering on collective doom — strangers at the edge of something, a coffee-coloured crowd, a dance lit by fire that may also be the end. The repeated refrain that everyone is going to die sits alongside an insistence on change, as if dying and transforming are the same motion. Belief is declared repeatedly, but what exactly is believed stays deliberately unclear.","src":"haiku"},
"babylon-zoo~i-m-cracking-up-i-need-a-pill":{"haiku":"A person suffers from paranoia, trauma, and mental breakdown while witnessing systemic corruption and discrimination.","opus":"A raw account of a mind coming apart, sleepless and taunted by imaginary tormentors, gasping for air and desperate for medication. Jas Mann's British project laces the personal collapse with jabs at state monitoring and racist policing, so private disintegration bleeds into wider social dread.","sonnet":"Mental fragility turned political — the speaker can't sleep, sees demons, can barely breathe inside a hypochondriac world, and connects that private cracking-up to a broader system that stigmatises and surveils, conceals information, and commits violence against Black and Asian communities. The personal and the systemic are held together as a single breaking point: one pill can't fix what Babylon built.","src":"haiku"},
"babylon-zoo~spaceman":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects a corrupt, homophobic, fascistic world through imagery of escape and violence, unable to breathe on the carousel of exploitation and calling for termination.","opus":"An apocalyptic vision of escape yearns to be beamed offworld from a planet choking on fascist votes, bigotry, and media control, unable to step off a spinning carousel toward catastrophe. Babylon Zoo, whose sped-up hook became a mid-nineties jeans-advert phenomenon in Britain, buried this bleak of dread and revulsion beneath the earworm, the intergalactic messiah imagery masking a genuinely bleak read of a world sickening itself.","sonnet":"Surrounded by homophobia, fascist imagery, and media decay, a speaker begs to be evacuated from a world he finds suffocating. The spaceman figure represents an escape from a carousel of societal rot that keeps spinning with no exit.","src":"haiku"},
"babylon-zoo~zodiac-sign":{"haiku":"A lonely figure uses sex to fill an emotional void, consumed by fear of isolation while exploited and defined by zodiacal signs and societal judgment.","opus":"A restless confession orbits loneliness, using sex as a drug to lift a soul with no love and no home while a distant God's road leads only to isolation. Babylon Zoo, Jas Mann's project better known for a single chart smash, mix cosmic and spiritual longing with grubbier disgust at exploitation, the star-sign framing giving cover to a plain, anxious admission that everyone simply wants to be loved.","sonnet":"A restless, rootless figure uses sex as a spiritual prop against chronic loneliness, caught between the sign of the zodiac and a deep fear of being alone. Exploitation and selfhood blur in a world where even the road to a higher power leads nowhere satisfying.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal-f-hero~pa-pa-ya":{"haiku":"Strongest and highest requires total bodily commitment through self-discipline. Bring the confrontation; intensity demands maximum force in battle's rhythm and momentum.","opus":"A deliberately absurd summer-festival mashup pairing BABYMETAL with Thai rap, all spicy papaya-salad imagery and dancefloor commands; the whole point is exuberant cross-cultural party chaos rather than any message.","sonnet":"A summer festival declaration — burn your whole self, test yourself at the absolute limit, this is a crazy spicy summer dance — blends Japanese matsuri energy with a Thai rap verse that likens life to a chocolate box and papaya pok pok, mixing Som Tam, raw fish, and beach vibes into a cosmopolitan party anthem. The refrain is simple: bring it on, make noise, dance.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal-headbanger~a-6tukg7":{"opus":"Part of BABYMETAL's fusion of idol pop and metal, this is a hymn to concert-going as a thing that never comes back, traveling across Japan on an 18-ticket rail pass to headbang in the front row. It works to carve the memory of those nights at fifteen into the chest before the bloom falls. The metal charge underwrites a fundamentally tender aim: to fix a fleeting adolescent intensity in place before it is gone.","sonnet":"A hymn to the fleeting, never-returnable intensity of adolescent concert-going — traveling across Japan with an 18-ticket rail pass, headbanging in the front row, carving the memory of those nights at fifteen into the chest before the bloom falls.","src":"sonnet"},
"babymetal-iine~a-15t35vq":{"haiku":"Youthful exuberance and escapism; teenagers reject reality and responsibility in favor of chaotic fun, embracing impulsiveness and the freedom of being gloriously reckless.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal-iine~a-rgcc5l":{"opus":"The same idol-metal collision powers this sugar-overloaded night fantasy, a girl making a pinky promise alone on a sentimental evening and escaping into a sky-rave with its own mosh pit. It is staged as maximum chaos, the fully committed inner world of someone whose dreams run at a scale ordinary reality cannot hold. The heaviness is a delivery system for the sweetness, amplifying a private daydream until it fills an entire sky.","sonnet":"A girl's sugar-overloaded night fantasy — making a pinky promise alone on a sentimental night and escaping into a sky-rave with a mosh pit — presented as the maximum-chaos, fully committed inner world of a girl whose dreams operate at a scale too large for ordinary reality.","src":"sonnet"},
"babymetal-joakim-brod-n~oh-majinai":{"haiku":"Ancient magic summons; please emerge and answer all pleas. Indifference governs requests; nothing matters; concern dissolves into acceptance of powerlessness.","opus":"A playful metal spell-song built around the panic of losing something and wishing it back; the incantation and pleas are half-comic, half-earnest, turning a lost-item scramble into a chanted charm for help.","sonnet":"Hitting a dead end with nowhere to go, the speakers call out a desperate wish — \"Oh! Majinai\" (a charm or spell) — alternating between Japanese panic and English pleading, save me, help me, please. The circular nai-nana-nai refrain creates an incantatory loop that never quite resolves the crisis.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal-tak-matsumoto~da-da-dance":{"haiku":"Dance becomes rebellion and salvation where tradition-bound silhouettes resist erasure through movement; the speaker summons revolution through rhythm and defiance.","opus":"A call to shatter fairy-tale illusions and dance through them, demanding to see a real heart rather than a dream. Framed as a revolution and a requiem for empty fantasy, the relentless dance becomes an act of waking up and refusing an unnegotiable self.","sonnet":"A call to break free from dreams and illusions through dancing: the lyrics dismiss fairy-tale passivity — \"tired of nothing but fantasy\" — and instead invoke a silhouette that cannot be surrendered, with the dancer vowing to wake up, seize the moment, and become a DA DA Dancing Queen through pure, unstoppable movement.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~akatsuki":{"haiku":"Eternal devotion endures beyond death: love transcends destruction itself, becoming the only permanence in a cycle of loss and renewal.","opus":"A samurai's vow of devotion — to guard a love strong enough to outlast a thousand nights until the body itself gives out. Babymetal drop the kawaii-metal irony entirely here: crimson tears and a thread of fate cut but still felt turn the song into a warrior's oath to protect something to the death.","sonnet":"A warrior or guardian vows to protect their beloved through thousands of nights, even as loneliness and anxiety attack — the red moon weeps, a wounded blade is drawn against inner demons, and an unbreakable bond persists past death.","src":"haiku","fable":"A vow sung as if from a battlefield, this ballad frames devotion as something waged rather than felt: a love that outlasts thousands of nights, defended with a wounded blade turned against one's own loneliness and dread until the body itself perishes. Its whole meaning lives in the color red, which mutates as the song moves — first the red tears that bleed up into the night sky, then the red thread of fate running through the singer's hand that keeps transmitting the bond even after it is severed, and finally the imperative that closes it, dye it red, dye it crimson, where grief and love and violence collapse into one saturating hue. What reads as a lonely showcase vocal is really about refusing to distinguish protecting someone from destroying yourself, the guardian's tenderness and the swordsman's self-harm sharing a single bloodstream.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","war & battle","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"protecting the beloved and destroying oneself sharing one bloodstream","built":"red mutates across the song from tears to fate-thread to final saturating command"},
"babymetal~amore": {"haiku":"Japanese song invoking love's words resonating across space and time; it celebrates emotional bonds and heartfelt voice that transcend separation, loss, and eclipse, framing affection as cosmic and enduring.","opus":"A soaring plea for love as a cosmic force: words of love shouted into the night sky to reach the universe, breaking through gloomy rainclouds and running on for twenty-four hours as destiny. The deeper read is devotion that survives separation, the heart's voice keeping on singing even if the beloved is lost or far away, love itself asked to save the Earth.","sonnet":"A declaration of undying love sent outward into the cosmos — even if the beloved drifts away, the singer's heart keeps singing, and a radiant blue star comes to life in their hands.","src":"haiku","fable":"A devotional power ballad that treats tenderness as a force with the reach of gravity, firing its declaration past the storm and out through space until affection itself becomes a kind of planetary rescue. It runs on unbroken forward motion, a vow to keep the voice going even across separation, so distance never halts the transmission and the feeling only gathers speed. This is BABYMETAL borrowing arena-metal grandeur to sanctify something disarmingly plain, staking everything on the faith that devotion is the one signal loud enough to save the world.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"devotion launched past the storm as a signal loud enough to save the world","built":"arena-metal grandeur borrowed to sanctify something disarmingly plain"},
"babymetal~arkadia": {"haiku":"Young people light fires and march toward a utopian dream, refusing to accept a uncertain and broken world.","opus":"A soaring call to chase your dreams toward a personal utopia, holding your flag high with no more tears or fear. Babymetal wrap earnest empowerment in bombast, the promised Arcadia found within.","sonnet":"A Japanese-language declaration of ambition and faith — the narrator urges someone to raise their flag proudly and chase their dream toward a far-off Arcadia, promising that the map of the future is already inside them and tears are behind them now.","src":"haiku","fable":"The album's closing anthem relocates utopia: Arkadia isn't a place you reach but a fragment of light already lodged in the chest, and the map of the future is inside you — so the marching, flag-raising build is really about carrying, not arriving. The chant of no more tears, no more pain plays as a vow made mid-journey, which is the point; a promised land you hold is one that no amount of darkness on the road can burn away.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","identity & becoming","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"carrying the destination rather than arriving at it","built":"the marching, flag-raising build enacts holding, not reaching"},
"babymetal~awadama-fever": {"fable":"A pure sugar-rush escape fantasy, and the read is in taking its childishness at full face value: every verse opens a secret box, then a secret key, then a secret room, and each time the reward is the same flat promise — you can fly anywhere. The vehicle is candy (awadama is bubble-candy; the chorus rides bubblegum and a mint-flavored time machine), which tells you the freedom on offer is deliberately weightless, a five-yen daydream rather than any real elsewhere. BABYMETAL's move here is to weaponize kawaii itself: the counting drills — the plain ichi-ni-san-shi flipping to the archaic nursery hi-fu-mi-yo — turn a metal breakdown into a playground chant, so the song's whole engine is repetition and onomatopoeia standing in for a destination that never actually arrives. The closing drop to a bare Dreamer is the quiet admission underneath the fizz: the door keeps opening and you keep not going anywhere, because the point was always the blowing of the bubble, not where it carries you.","haiku":"A bubblegum reverie built on chew-and-blow nonsense and a mint-flavoured time machine, chasing not a story but the giddy sensation of floating free.","src":"fable","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape"],"means":"escape promised repeatedly through a door that never actually opens","built":"the closing drop to bare Dreamer admits the destination never arrived"},
"babymetal~babymetal-death-live":{"opus":"In the live setting, BABYMETAL turns introduction into rite: each member announces herself amid a wall of DEATH chants, and the piece functions less as a song than as a theatrical invocation of the group's identity. Noise and presence become the content, the roll-call a summoning. The idol format is inverted into something closer to ceremony, where the point is not narrative but the collective, amplified assertion that the group has arrived.","sonnet":"A live roll-call introduction — each member announces herself amid a wall of \"DEATH!!\" chants — functioning less as a song with narrative content than as a theatrical invocation of the group's identity, noise, and presence as ritual performance.","src":"sonnet"},
"babymetal~believing":{"haiku":"Faith and conviction sustain the speaker through adversity, belief itself becoming transformative force.","opus":"Facing that people and seasons change and you can't return to how things were, resolving to leave outcomes to fate; the emotional core is choosing to keep believing in someone even without a safe middle ground.","sonnet":"A relationship changed beyond recovery by shifting seasons — neither person is the same as before, the old configuration is gone — and the choice is whether to trust what was said even now: \"believing, believing you.\" There's no middle ground, no going back, only the decision to hold or release.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~brand-new-day-feat-tim-henson-scott-lepage": {"haiku":"Despite uncertainty about tomorrow's weather, each day offers fresh opportunity; step-by-step, moment-by-moment, one persists and begins anew.","opus":"Fusing J-pop brightness with heavy guitar work from the guest players, the Japanese trio frame each dawn as a fresh chance to knock on the day or take a break if it rains. Chasing a beloved voice through drifting weather, the lyric counsels step-by-step patience and deep breaths, insisting tomorrow always arrives so beginning again is enough.","sonnet":"Stardust and summer-stage memories drift through a song about continuing forward despite not knowing what tomorrow holds. Step by step, rainy day by sunny day, moving on is reframed not as loss but as the ordinary and necessary rhythm of being alive.","src":"haiku","fable":"Resilience scaled down to weather-checking: the song refuses to conquer tomorrow and instead consults the forecast — if it's sunny, go knock on a door; if it rains, taking a break is allowed — with the chorus's entire engine being the repeated assurance that tomorrow comes again. Over the glassy math-pop guitars of Polyphia's Tim Henson and Scott LePage, optimism becomes procedural rather than triumphant, pausing written into the plan as legitimately as moving; and the final line quietly tightens the grammar, from because tomorrow comes again to tomorrow will come again — the same words shifting from consolation to certainty just before the end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","nature & the elements"],"means":"optimism made procedural, pausing written into the plan","built":"final line tightens consolation to certainty in one verb shift"},
"babymetal~brand-new-day-metal-galaxy-world-tour-in-japan-extra-show":{"haiku":"Each day offers renewal and fresh possibility, resilience emerging through acceptance that difficulty is inevitable yet survivable when met with patient persistence.","opus":"The same gentle pep-talk against anxiety, taken to the stage, urging you forward step by step because tomorrow always comes. BABYMETAL's lighter side turning weather metaphors into permission to rest and begin again.","sonnet":"An encouragement to keep moving without forcing it — rainy days are permission to rest, sunny days to knock and try again, and tomorrow always comes regardless. The song's emotional core is patience: \"just step by step, and day by day\" set against the admission that yesterday can never be recovered, only replaced by a brand new day.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~catch-me-if-you-can": {"haiku":"A Japanese children's hide-and-seek game ('kakurenbo') playfully frames pursuit and evasion, with playful warnings about the dangers of red shoes and running.","opus":"Built on the Japanese children's game of hide-and-seek with the demon who is it, it plays childhood fun against a lurking menace. The warning not to wear the red shoes hints at real danger beneath the giddy chase.","sonnet":"The Japanese lyrics frame the song as an exuberant game of hide-and-seek: the singer taunts a pursuer with \"not yet!\" calls, daring them to find a perfect hiding spot, and relishing the chase.","src":"haiku","fable":"A game of hide-and-seek staged with total literal fidelity — the counting chant of are-you-ready, not-yet, the taunt of find me if you can — which is exactly what makes it BABYMETAL's most quietly unsettling early track. The kawaii-metal formula was always about the collision of nursery innocence and something sharper, and here the nursery-rhyme scaffolding of the seeker (the oni, the demon who is It in the Japanese game) turns the childish round into a hunt where being caught carries a menace the giggling voices refuse to name. The warnings sharpen the edge: don't put on the red shoes, it's dangerous — a fairy-tale motif of feet that dance you to your ruin — while the child who falls is drilled not to cry, to be strong. What reads as playground fun is a small horror kept in miniature, the round spinning on and on with no one ever found, closing on the same not-yet that opened it, so the seeking never ends and no one is ever safe.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"nursery-rhyme seeking where capture always threatens","built":"the round closes on the same not-yet that opened it, the hunt unresolved"},
"babymetal~catch-me-if-you-can-live":{"haiku":"A raucous hide-and-seek game reimagined as live performance spectacle — the seeker circles while the hiders taunt and spin, mixing the rules of a children's game with the energy of a festival dare.","opus":"A children's game of hide-and-seek turned metal, the fox spirits taunting the demon who's it. Playful Japanese schoolyard chant energy weaponized into a live singalong, all mischief and dizzy spinning.","sonnet":"A hide-and-seek game with supernatural stakes — the seeker counting in Japanese children's rhyme cadence, the hider taunting from a perfect hiding spot, red shoes marked as dangerous. The game spirals into a chase with red and blue demons stepping in, urging the hider not to cry when they fall.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~da-da-dance-feat-tak-matsumoto": {"haiku":"A dance of revolution and conviction; surpassing dreams and awakening to claim one's identity and refuse to compromise.","opus":"Kawaii-metal dressing a call to shed fairytale illusions and seize the present in candy-bright dance, Tak Matsumoto's guitar grounding the sugar. Beneath the bubblegum sits a small insistence on waking up and living now.","sonnet":"A Japanese J-metal song about dancing as defiance of illusion — the narrator rejects fairy-tale passivity, demands to see a real heart, and urges rebellion through movement, vowing to never yield her silhouette to dreams and phantoms.","src":"haiku","fable":"A loving pastiche of Japan's 90s eurobeat era — B'z guitarist Tak Matsumoto shredding over para-para synths — that turns the dance floor into a lie detector: you keep dancing but you don't understand, so show me the real heart. The commands pile up in one direction — dreams and illusions ordered to vanish, wake up, dance — until the only solid thing left is a silhouette that won't be surrendered: strip the fairy tales away and what survives the strobe is the self.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"the dance floor as a lie detector for the hidden self","built":"90s eurobeat pastiche as the stripping-of-illusions device"},
"babymetal~distortion": {"haiku":"A distorted body screams and warped pain cuts through a filthy world. The song exhorts rejection of hypocrisy and embraces bent reality and nightmares.","opus":"Babymetal's defiance against a corrupt, hypocritical world, urging the listener to cast off the pretenders and refuse to fear those who dominate; the nightmare of distortion becomes a power that can't be stopped once you stop giving up.","sonnet":"A Japanese-language revolt against a corrupt, hypocritical world: the distorted body screams out, the distorted pain cuts deep, but given distorted wings to fly the narrator refuses to fear the distorted authority of hypocrites — the world was filthy to begin with.","src":"haiku","fable":"Distortion here is a condition, not just a pedal: a filthy world warps everything it touches — body, pain, rule — and the song's answer is to fly on the warped wings anyway and cut the hypocrites loose. The English chorus plays the enemy's voice, taunting you to give up and surrender to the distortion, while the Japanese verses refuse from inside the bad dream; the track keeps both running because the pressure and the defiance are the same power that can't be stopped.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society"],"means":"pressure and defiance as the same unstoppable power","built":"English chorus voices the enemy; Japanese verses refuse from inside"},
"babymetal~divine-attack-shingeki":{"haiku":"A collective call to rise against adversity through solidarity and determination, rejecting surrender in pursuit of a self-determined future.","opus":"A battle cry summoning the strong to gather, take up the blade of the heart, and swear an oath together as they begin their journey. Escape routes cut and silence shattered, the song urges seizing the future with one's own hands—carving the path, breaking through walls, offering one's soul if the fight is worth it.","sonnet":"A rallying cry for fighters whose escape routes have been cut off — the long night closes in, retreat is impossible, so the only answer is to raise your hand and advance toward the tomorrow you've drawn for yourself. The refrain \"cut open the road with your own hands\" insists that the path forward is made, not found.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~doki-doki-morning":{"haiku":"Frivolity as rebellion: young voices shatter routine with chaotic joy and irreverence, refusing seriousness and demanding constant reinvention.","opus":"Hyperactive teenage-girl morning rush, hair and makeup and racing to meet friends, turning the panic of getting ready into pure sugar-rush joy.","sonnet":"A Japanese song about the chaotic, excited rush of a teenage morning — running late, doing makeup in three seconds, planning after-school hangouts with friends, choosing outfits, and racing through the day with breathless energy.","src":"haiku","fable":"BABYMETAL's founding gambit is audible entirely in this song's engine: a hyper-cute schoolgirl morning routine — bangs cut straight, choosing today's lipstick, dashing to make up before class — welded to metal's speed and volume, so the panic of running late becomes a legitimate reason to scream. The lyric's whole nervous system is interruption: the ringing alarm, the pleading wait-a-second, the countdown spelling of doki-doki, all staging a girl who cannot slow down and does not want to. What keeps this from being mere novelty is the refrain buried under the fluff — a refusal to fake ignorance, a wanting to see unknown worlds, a hunger to stretch ten percent taller — which quietly reframes teenage frenzy as appetite, ambition costumed as morning chaos. Read that way, the song is a manifesto disguised as a jingle: the same maximalist logic that later made the group's fusion notorious is already here, treating adolescent triviality with total, arena-sized seriousness.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"adolescent appetite for unknown worlds costumed as a morning-rush panic","built":"arena-scale production and maximalist volume treat the schoolgirl routine as a manifesto"},
"babymetal~doki-doki-morning-live":{"haiku":"Youthful transformation and morning readiness through cosmetic preparation, racing against the clock to achieve immediate reinvention and acceptance.","opus":"A teenage girl's frantic morning rush to get ready, obsessing over hair, lipstick and being late. One of BABYMETAL's earliest songs, pairing bubblegum idol-pop about school-day panic with a metal backdrop.","sonnet":"The chaotic rush of a school morning — alarm rings, hair still a mess, racing to decide on lip color and the day's VIP, heart pounding at ten percent extra effort demanded. A bubbly panic-pop of preparation rituals, pop culture upgrades, and the breathless question of what time it even is.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~elevator-girl": {"haiku":"A descent through levels alternating between heaven and hell, with Japanese station announcements framing the journey as perilous and requiring constant risk.","opus":"Framed as an elevator announcement, the song turns the ride into a descent toward hell, with floors of fire and needle torture thousands of levels underground. The refrain teasingly asks whether you are going up or, inevitably, down.","sonnet":"An elevator-announcement conceit in which the ride always ends going down — to a floor described as a fiery hell two thousand levels below ground — with life itself framed as a constant, mortal gamble between up and down.","src":"haiku","fable":"The joke is the register: it borrows the scripted courtesy of a Japanese elevator attendant — going up, going down, please mind the closing doors — and lets that unflappable politeness announce the next stop as Hell, two thousand floors below, fires and needles included. Under the gag sits the actual thesis in the refrain's pivot: because the car can plunge at any moment, every ride is taken with your life on the line — fortune's whiplash dressed in flawless service-industry manners.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","comedy & wordplay","death & grief"],"means":"scripted service courtesy announcing hell as the next floor","built":"elevator-attendant register as fortune's deadpan delivery mechanism"},
"babymetal~elevator-girl-english-version": {"opus":"A playful song built on an elevator-attendant conceit where the metaphorical descent is literally into hell, teasing that no matter what you say or do, 'you're going down' floor by floor toward the fire-and-needle pit.","sonnet":"A playful, demonic elevator operator announces that no matter what direction passengers think they are headed, the only real destination is hell — framed as a kitschy game-show conceit with genuine menace underneath.","src":"sonnet","fable":"An elevator as mood cycle: the car's up and down maps a life that spins between happy and wrecked, while a taunting voice insists gravity wins whatever she does. What keeps it from nihilism is the shrug built into the hook — Hell lands mid-phrase as a punchline, and the answer to the plunge is simply to hang on and refuse to quit, resilience pitched at the same bubblegum brightness as the doom.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","comedy & wordplay","faith & the occult"],"means":"doom delivered at bubblegum brightness as the answer to the plunge","built":"the up-down car maps a mood cycle, not a literal descent"},
"babymetal~from-dusk-till-dawn": {"haiku":"The speaker waits for transformation from dusk to dawn but days blur into repetition; dreams blur with waking as they seek meaning in monotony.","opus":"A dreamlike vigil of waiting for something to arrive, caught on a border between night and dream where the sleeper wakes only to keep dreaming. The throughline is suspended anticipation, wanting to seize each fleeting moment before the sweet, too-late night breaks.","sonnet":"A hazy, impressionistic track about waiting for something transformative to arrive, pressing forward through repetitive days toward a dream that keeps slipping away just before morning.","src":"haiku","fable":"A vigil dressed as a J-pop ballad: the track is built almost entirely on anticipation that never resolves, its verses circling the same expectant threshold instead of ever crossing it. That stall is the meaning — BABYMETAL trade their usual arena-scale bombast for a hushed, prayerful drift where sleep and waking blur into one another, and the sweetness of the small hours becomes a soft trap. The deliberately unstable English, subjectless and tense-slipping, resolves into a mantra of surrender: hold the border, refuse to be ruled, and let the promised arrival hover forever beyond arm's reach.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape"],"means":"anticipation that refuses to resolve, the promised arrival always out of reach","built":"subjectless, tense-slipping English enacts the blur between sleep and waking"},
"babymetal~from-me-to-u-feat-poppy":{"haiku":"Rage and violent power transfer from one consciousness to another, with collective fury channeled toward eliminating enemies and asserting dominance.","opus":"A darkly protective anthem about finding an ally against enemies and demons, passing rage and power from one to the other so neither faces ruin alone. The Japanese refrain of reaching you fuses with threats of wrath, turning solidarity into a weapon against betrayers who have no chance.","sonnet":"A transfer of rage as gift: the narrator tells someone being hunted by demons that hiding only makes it worse, then channels her own crimson wrath as protection — \"let the power go from me to you.\" A Japanese verse admits wandering in despair after darkness falls, but the shared fury becomes the bridge: rage as solidarity, not destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~gimme-chocolate":{"haiku":"A playful demand for chocolate that expresses desire with urgency and some anxiety. The narrator wants the treat and asks repeatedly and impatiently.","opus":"A playful inner tug-of-war over craving chocolate while worrying about weight, the singer begging for a treat as a reward after working hard. The push and pull between temptation and restraint is voiced with cartoonish delight.","sonnet":"A playful internal conflict between craving chocolate and worrying about the consequences for one's figure, with the speaker ultimately deciding to give in to the temptation after working hard enough to deserve a reward.","src":"haiku","fable":"The joke is that a full-force metal assault is deployed over a girl's internal negotiation about whether she's allowed a sweet, complete with dieting guilt and the pun that turns waiting into weight. BABYMETAL's whole premise lives in that mismatch: kawaii squeals and cartoon sound-effect syllables fronting a wall of thrash riffing, so the trivial stakes and the monumental delivery ironize each other. Underneath the sugar rush the song is quietly about wanting something you've told yourself you shouldn't have, and then deciding to have it anyway.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"full-force metal assault deployed over a girl's dieting guilt","built":"the pun collapses waiting and weight into a single word carrying both meanings","fvr":"2.4"},
"babymetal~gimme-chocolate-live":{"haiku":"Playful self-indulgent desire for rewards, balancing fun with minor guilt about indulgence.","opus":"The J-pop and metal collision at its most gleeful: a girl agonizing over whether to eat chocolate while fretting about her weight, teenage indulgence and guilt rendered as absurd, screaming spectacle.","sonnet":"A girl craves chocolate but keeps second-guessing herself over weight, looping between want and wait until the accumulated effort of the day tips the scales and she demands it outright. The conflict between desire and self-restraint drives the whole breathless chant.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~gimme-chocolate-live-at-o2-academy-brixton-london-live":{"haiku":"Playful self-indulgent desire for rewards, balancing fun with minor guilt about indulgence.","opus":"The same chocolate-craving anthem, teenage temptation and diet-guilt weaponized into breakdowns, here roaring through a London crowd that turns the silliness into communal release.","sonnet":"A girl craves chocolate but worries about weight and keeps saying wait — the desire and the hesitation looping until eventually, after working super hard, she decides she deserves just a little. Playful gluttony guilt rendered as a call-and-response chant.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~gj": {"haiku":"Japanese song celebrating spirited youth resistance to societal pressure; moshing crowds embrace chaos and earnest imperfection over adult hierarchies, with rallying cries to reject worry and claim messy self-acceptance.","opus":"A high-energy pep-talk built on mosh-pit imagery (circle pits, wall of death, lifting) turned into life coaching, telling the listener to shrug off irritation and take it easy. The throughline is self-acceptance: even a clumsy, hopeless effort scores full marks, and the cheerful, easygoing version of yourself is number one.","sonnet":"A tongue-in-cheek motivational pep talk set in a mosh pit: stop stressing, stop straining for perfection, take it easy — being a carefree, easygoing version of yourself is the best you can do.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pep talk delivered in the grammar of a circle pit: the moshing calls that summon a stampede of colliding bodies become a coded map of the daunting climb into adulthood, the world's rough surf ridden rather than drowned in. The relentless もっともっと (more, more) refrain drills a coach's mantra past exhaustion, while the guttural shout punctuates it like a whistle blast rather than a threat. Then the punchline flips retail-clearance haggling into self-forgiveness, promising that even a botched, clumsy attempt still lands a perfect hundred points. So the airheaded, unbothered self is crowned number one, and stretching on tiptoe to look taller earns nothing at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"a botched attempt crowned perfect, the airheaded self made number one","built":"circle-pit calls double as a coded map of climbing into adulthood"},
"babymetal~head-bangya": {"haiku":"Youth defiant against time's inevitability, legendary black hair flows as the speaker burns bright knowing this moment will never return.","opus":"An ode to headbanging as a fleeting, once-in-a-lifetime night to be seared into memory before it vanishes like a flower that blooms madly and disappears. Crybabies and spoilers are told to leave, the ephemeral moment on the floor treated as sacred.","sonnet":"A passionate tribute to the once-in-a-lifetime experience of headbanging at a metal show — the legendary black-haired flower blooms wildly and fades, the night is unrepeatable, crybabies are told to leave, and every moment is carved permanently into the chest.","src":"haiku","fable":"A live-floor rite that treats one night of headbanging as a whole life burning fast and bright, then vanishing without regret. The lyric frames the pit as a flower blooming madly out of season only to scatter, its black hair thrown wild for a single irreplaceable evening that never returns. Everything else is the drill made sacred: cowards and meddlers ordered to clear off, the chant hammered until motion itself becomes the only meaning that survives.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title you're shouting fuses headbanger with bangya, Japanese slang for the female metal and visual-kei fan (band-gyaru); the ticket gripped in the lyric is the Seishun 18 rail pass, the cheap unlimited-ride fare fans ride to faraway shows.","themes":["party & hedonism","freedom & escape"],"means":"one pit night as a flower blooming and scattering at once","built":"the flower metaphor makes a single gig stand in for a whole life"},
"babymetal~headbangeeeeerrrrr":{"haiku":"Celebration of headbanging and metal music culture; pure physical expression of joy.","opus":"A fleeting adolescence gets sworn into memory as legendary black hair is flung wild, a flower blooming madly before it vanishes, with weepers ordered out of the pit. Babymetal, the Japanese kawaii-metal trio fronted by teenage idols over crushing riffs, wrap coming-of-age nostalgia around headbanging choreography, the vow to carve one fifteenth night into the chest capturing how fiercely youth clings to a moment already slipping away.","sonnet":"An anthem celebrating the fleeting, fierce beauty of youth lived at concerts — legendary black hair flying, bodies crowd-surfing and headbanging through one unforgettable night at fifteen that will never come back. The impermanence of that moment is cherished precisely because it cannot be recovered.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~iine": {"fable":"A pure collision engine, and the collision is the point: the title is the Japanese thumbs-up — iine, the social-media like made into a chant — and the song stacks that endless approval reflex on top of a girl's fizzing, ticking, can't-sit-still crush until the two registers become one sound. What reads as sugar (snacks, a pinky-promise under the night sky, everything is perfect, girls are pure chaos) is spoken in the exact grammar of a pit: the sudden turn into a mic check, the count-off, the order to take your backpack off and put your fox-sign up. That fusion IS the thesis of early BABYMETAL — kawaii chatter and mosh command are not alternating jokes but a single language, the idol reflex to endorse and adore run at metal velocity until liking something and losing your mind to it stop being distinguishable. The one image worth holding is the escape it keeps promising: alone under the stars, it wants to bolt from reality — reality-flight sung as a party, so the giddiness is also a small refusal to come down.","haiku":"Named for the social-media like button, a hyperactive burst of youthful fun, gyaru slang and snack-grabbing chaos engineered for headbanging singalongs.","src":"fable","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","love & desire","freedom & escape"],"means":"idol adoration and pit command fused into one indistinguishable language","built":"mic check and count-off embedded inside sugar-crush lyric without tonal seam"},
"babymetal~ijime-dame-zettai": {"haiku":"Bullying is absolutely unacceptable and must be confronted; victims must find courage and refuse to let cruelty define them or their futures.","opus":"An anti-bullying vow that refuses cruelty absolutely while admitting how everyone, victim and bystander alike, gets scarred in the process. The promise to protect someone becomes the strength to finally take flight.","sonnet":"A forceful anti-bullying anthem — directed at both victims who hide their wounds and bystanders who pretend not to see — urging those being hurt to release their pain and fly free, and declaring that bullying is absolutely never okay.","src":"haiku","fable":"An anti-bullying anthem is the flimsiest of pop premises, and BABYMETAL's early gambit is to weaponize exactly that flimsiness — the title's schoolyard slogan (bullying, no, absolutely) chanted with the deadpan cheer of a public-service jingle over kawaii-metal breakdowns. But the verses refuse the easy sermon: the wounded party turns out not to be only the self but the one who kept watching, and the second verse widens the guilt to the friend who noticed and played dumb. So the song's real subject isn't the bully at all — it's complicity, the room in the heart where you hide instead of speaking, and the parenthetical call-and-response (the crowd shouting Dame! back at every accusation) enacts the collective voice the lyric says was missing. Around this circles the fox-sign command to fly, which converts the shame of being scarred all over into liftoff, so that the vow to protect you lands less as consolation than as the whole point: idol-costumed innocence smuggling in a genuinely bruised address to a kid who wants to disappear.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","friendship & loyalty","anger & defiance"],"means":"complicity named as the wound, not the bully","built":"the crowd shouting Dame! enacts the collective voice the lyric says was absent"},
"babymetal~ijime-dame-zettai-live":{"haiku":"Bullying breaks spirits and divides communities until foxes and metal warriors rise together to protect the wounded; courage is the only armor against cruelty.","opus":"A direct anti-bullying anthem asking why people hurt one another and refusing to look away any longer, vowing to protect the one who kept watching over the speaker. The fox-god's advice to run free and let loose every pain turns the message into a rallying cry to release suffering and stand up.","sonnet":"A song against bullying — \"ijime, dame, zettai\" means \"bullying, never, absolutely\" — told from the perspective of someone who has watched others suffer in silence and now stands up, with the fox spirit (kitsune) as a symbol of the power to fly free from pain. It acknowledges that bystanders who pretend not to see are complicit, and ends with a promise of protection and the total release of all suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~in-the-name-of": {"haiku":"A faith-driven anthem pairs opposing forces (heaven/hell, light/dark, death/resurrection) to invoke spiritual protection and power through metal and goddess worship.","opus":"A ceremonial chant pairing opposites, praising flame and shadow alike in worship of the fictional deity at the heart of Babymetal's mythology. Borrowing the Game of Thrones vow that the slain return mightier than before, it plays as a tongue-in-cheek liturgy of rebirth and fate, sworn to their invented goddess and to heavy music itself.","sonnet":"A ritual invocation of opposites — night and day, heaven and hell, death and rebirth — as an act of faith to a Fox God, framing the band's existence and the listener's devotion as a destined, sacred mission to carry metal forward.","src":"haiku","fable":"A ritual interlude rather than a song: paired opposites — night and day, heaven and hell, joy and sorrow — are blessed with strict evenhandedness, fire and hope praised in the same breath as darkness, building a liturgy for the band's own invented Fox God cult. Its central creed, that what dies never truly dies but returns stronger, is borrowed near-verbatim from the Ironborn drowned-god prayer in Game of Thrones; and placed on the first album after a founding member's departure, a chant framing death as the precondition of a harder resurrection reads as BABYMETAL consecrating its own reformation — destiny reassigned by decree, in the name of metal.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","identity & becoming"],"means":"death framed as the precondition of a harder return","built":"drowned-god vow borrowed from Game of Thrones consecrates the band's own reformation"},
"babymetal~kagerou": {"fable":"Desire read as a heat haze: a shimmer over the surface that both parties can plainly see and neither will name, concealment declared futile from the opening exchange. The song runs on one onomatopoeic system that swaps its verb each cycle — swaying becomes falling becomes burning — so the flicker that starts as a pendulum's hypnotic wobble ends as jealousy in open flame, a flower of hope fluttering down in between. The rest is watching the hidden thing combust in slow motion, and being told to rise with it.","fableDeep":"Three onomatopoeia verbs carry the whole arc: yura-yura (swaying), then hira-hira (fluttering down, as hope's flower falls), then yura-yura again — but now the verb is 燃える, burning, and the swaying thing is the flame of jealousy. The pendulum rhythm frames emotion as physics, involuntary and regular; the English interjections stage two people each certain the other's concealment is transparent ('I know it' / 'you don't believe'). A kagerou — heat haze — is the perfect emblem for it: real, visible, distorting everything behind it, impossible to hold. And the final imperative, 舞い上がれ (soar!), doesn't put the fire out; it tells it to dance.","haiku":"Wavering and flickering emotions—jealousy, hope, and burning passion—oscillate like a heat haze, never settling into stable ground.","opus":"A song about suspicion and jealousy in which the speaker senses that concealment and evasion will not hide the truth, using a swaying pendulum and rising heat haze as images of flickering hope, falling petals, and a burning flame of envy.","sonnet":"The lyrics circle around something that cannot be hidden or denied — swaying and falling like a pendulum, rising and burning with jealousy — portraying an emotional state of barely-contained, wavering feeling that the speaker knows is obvious to the other person.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"concealment declared futile by both parties from the opening","built":"three onomatopoeia verbs carry the whole arc from sway to open flame"},
"babymetal~karate": {"haiku":"Despite tears and heartbreak, the spirit continues fighting through adversity, sharpening both fist and mind to persist even when standing up seems impossible.","opus":"A relentless call to keep fighting, sung around martial chants, urging the listener to sharpen fists and heart and to push on even when tears fall, spirits break, and they can no longer stand.","sonnet":"A call to fight on no matter how many tears fall or how broken one feels — sharpening both fists and spirit to keep battling, even when sadness makes it impossible to stand.","src":"haiku","fable":"It stages resilience as drill, where the vocables work less as lyric than as a sparring count that carries the fighter forward past the few lines admitting that tears fell and the spirit gave. What little text there is refuses despair on contact, folding each collapse into the beat before another strike, mind honed like the body until rising again becomes reflex rather than any deliberate act of will.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The seiya and soiya battle-shouts driving the track are the cries of festival crews hoisting a mikoshi, the portable shrine, doubling as martial-arts kiai — the dojo and the matsuri fused into a single combat cadence.","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"resilience drilled until rising becomes reflex rather than will","built":"vocables function as a sparring count rather than lyric"},
"babymetal~kon-kon-feat-bloodywood":{"haiku":"Supernatural malevolence and ancestral vengeance manifest through ecstatic rituals; spirits emerge to exact retribution.","opus":"Fox-spirit folklore fused with defiant battle energy, Bloodywood's verses turning trial by fire into iron will across Japanese and Hindi. A cross-cultural summoning where the yokai and the underdog both refuse to lose.","sonnet":"A trilingual (Japanese, English, Hindi) invocation of defiance and festivity: the fox god Kon-Kon summoned to a celebration that exists between worlds, the Bloodywood verse adding a warrior's oath about fire turning ore to iron, all three languages converging on the same message — face the worst and dance anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~light-and-darkness":{"haiku":"Sadness buries reality in snow while passing time stains both hands red. Love carries the forsaken toward idealistic paradise; both darkness and light spiral endlessly.","opus":"A theatrical embrace of love that fuses light and dark, drowning in dream and memory rather than resisting; the plea is to be carried past guilt and sorrow into an idealized world where this fierce love never lets go.","sonnet":"Poisoned by a love that drowns memory and breaks the listener apart, the night is one where light and darkness play against each other and the rhythm of the ocean overwhelms — yet the love is strong enough not to let go, ever. The bridge breaks free: \"let me out, let me go, dance through the night\" — the destruction and the liberation are the same force.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~maya": {"haiku":"Reality dissolves into illusion; the speaker questions the boundary between actual and virtual worlds.","opus":"A meditation on illusion and reality, a magic named MAYA that may be nothing but a beautiful deception. Between the virtual and the real, dreams shatter and reform, the song insisting that what the eyes see might all be a trick.","sonnet":"A song about the blurred boundary between illusion and reality in a magical relationship — the narrator wonders whether the world they see is real or conjured by a lover's power, framing the enchantment as both delusion and genuine magic, with the beloved's name becoming an incantation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction that keeps insisting the listener call out to her, MAYA folds the Sanskrit sense of maya as illusion into a dance-floor spell where enchantment and deception collapse into one gesture. The Japanese verses trace the trick's whole arc, cast then unravelling then vanished by dawn, so the song's real subject becomes the failure to distinguish a conjured night from a waking one. What lands is the refusal to let that question settle: she disappears before morning precisely so the mark can never confirm whether any of it occurred, which for a group built on a constructed avatar reads as the persona describing itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"What sounds like disorientation is the band's own track-by-track framing: reality is an illusion, the truth may be a deception, a feeling of wandering between the real and virtual worlds — on an album that stages BABYMETAL recovered from a virtual Metalverse, ten songs as ten parallel worlds.","themes":["artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"enchantment and deception collapse into one gesture, the mark never knowing","built":"the avatar disappearing before dawn describes the constructed persona describing itself"},
"babymetal~megitsune": {"fable":"Built on the Inari fox-shrine imagery of matsuri chants and dressing-up (omekashi), the song wears the kitsune as a mask — the vixen as trickster-performer — and its whole argument is the seam where that mask meets the face beneath. The refrain insists a woman is always an actress, then immediately denies the disguise (not a fox, not a fox), landing on megitsune, the she-fox that is really just an otome, a maiden: the trickster costume is itself the performance being confessed. That confession detonates in the line the track is built to deliver — laughing with the face while the heart weeps, saying of course, that's how it is, and never letting the tears show — which recasts the festival noise and the shape-shifting (shichihenge, seven transformations) as the labor of composure demanded of the idealized Yamato-nadeshiko woman. The closing image, that to bloom and scatter is a woman's fate, folds the shrine-fox and the falling-blossom traditions into one verdict: the endurance is the costume, the smiling face is the trick, and megitsune is the sound of both being worn at once.","haiku":"Drawing on kitsune fox-spirit myth, a defiant anthem for Japanese women who keep a smile fixed over hidden tears and refuse to break.","src":"fable","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"the trickster's mask named as the labor composure demands of women","built":"the denial not-a-fox is itself the performance being confessed"},
"babymetal~megitsune-live":{"haiku":"Shrine maidens and fox spirits embody divine feminine power; seduction and spiritual transformation merge in traditional and modern sacred dance.","opus":"A tribute to Japanese womanhood as performance, the woman who smiles outwardly while crying inside, strong as the fox spirit she channels. Rooted in the Yamato Nadeshiko ideal, it honors the strength hidden behind the mask of grace.","sonnet":"Ancient maidens who have always performed femininity — smiling on the outside, crying inside — are celebrated as fox-spirits who are more than they appear: \"Yamato Nadeshiko\" women who change and endure through centuries, launching fireworks so they won't be forgotten, and insisting that a woman scorned is not to be underestimated.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~meta-taro": {"haiku":"A metallic hero awakens from distant origins to unite the masses; collective power transforms through the binding force of shared metal and universal brotherhood.","opus":"A playful metal folk-hero anthem about Meta Taro, a hero reborn through the power of metal to unite kids everywhere. The message is that when the music sounds, strangers become friends.","sonnet":"A rallying call to a metal hero figure — Meta Taro is a reborn champion from a distant star who lifts everyone out of defeat with the power of metal, uniting boys and girls into a single tribe.","src":"haiku","fable":"A children's-tokusatsu theme grafted onto metal: an invented caped champion, named for the band itself and born on a clear star in the deep past, is handed to the crowd as shared property rather than a savior worshipped from below. The trick is that the hero lives inside the listener—you are urged to summon the one in your own heart, so the reborn-through-metal promise becomes a dare to stand up out of your own losing streak. Where the verse frames life as a warring-states gamble, victory heaven and defeat hell, the chant answers not with winning but with company: the spelled-out M-E-T-A cry turns rescue into a group sing-along, the whole point being that everyone becoming the hero at once is what friendship looks like.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The -taro suffix hands the metal hero to the ear as folk-tale kin: it places him in the lineage of heroes like Momotaro, an everyman-boy champion reborn, here with a metal heart, given to the crowd as a group possession.","themes":["friendship & loyalty","identity & becoming"],"means":"the hero placed inside the listener rather than above them","built":"the spelled-out M-E-T-A chant converts rescue into a group sing-along"},
"babymetal~metali-feat-tom-morello":{"haiku":"Collective ecstasy erupts in ritual celebration where dancers lose their individual selves in communal surrender and joy becomes the antidote to isolation.","opus":"A festival chant styled as a village folk-dance, summoning everyone to join the Meta-village circle and dance, sway, sing, and shout. Borrowing the proverb that fools who dance and fools who watch are the same fool, it insists you may as well dance, community and celebration its whole point.","sonnet":"A festival song reimagining a traditional Japanese Bon Odori dance for a metal crowd: the \"meta-village\" calls everyone — \"niisan\" and \"neesan\" — into a circle to dance and sing together, collapsing the distinction between fools who dance and fools who watch with the folk wisdom \"if you're a fool either way, you might as well dance.\" The relentless \"wasshoi\" chant fuses matsuri energy with mosh-pit ecstasy.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~monochrome":{"haiku":"Dreams fade and brightness dims from transient beauty; yet even monochrome suffering finds color through collective hope and rainbow transformation.","opus":"Grief for something fading to gray, and the resolve to repaint the night sky in color anyway. Set against Ragnarok's twilight, it insists that even the longest night gives way to morning, choosing hope through loss.","sonnet":"The seven-colour dreams that once lit the night sky have faded to monochrome, and the speaker asks what colour could be used to paint that sky back — even at the twilight of Ragnarok, the answer is that the long night will eventually give way to morning, and the world can be wrapped in rainbow rather than surrendered to grey.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~night-night-burn": {"haiku":"Perpetual nighttime party energy with dancing, burning passion, spinning, and relentless motion—a mandate to keep moving and celebrating without ceasing.","opus":"A giddy, carnival-flavored dance anthem (in Japanese) built entirely on celebration, urging everyone to dance, spin, and burn with passion all night long; there is no throughline beyond the pure exhortation to party until you drop.","sonnet":"A high-energy J-pop-metal dance anthem commanding everyone to keep dancing until dawn — the title is literally \"Night Night Burn,\" the lyrics urge spinning, burning passion, keeping the party going, with Spanish and Brazilian musical flavors blended in as part of the relentless festive momentum.","src":"haiku","fable":"A stamina test dressed as world tourism: Spanish holas, Italian mamma mias, bullring oles and a bridge lifted from the Brazilian standard Tico-Tico no Fubá get thrown into one borderless carnival whose only command is keep dancing. The Japanese punchlines undercut the glamour — the party ends batan-kyu, flat on your face — so the real subject is the delirious stretch past exhaustion where the night keeps burning anyway, and the refrain's insistence that it's still not over is both promise and threat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism"],"means":"the delirious stretch past exhaustion where the night keeps burning anyway","built":"world-music pileup frames the carnival while Japanese punchlines undercut its glamour"},
"babymetal~night-night-burn-metal-galaxy-world-tour-in-japan-extra-show":{"haiku":"Passion and collective energy ignite the dancefloor into an endless celebration, bodies moving in synchronized rhythm as the party transcends time and death through continuous motion and fire.","opus":"Pure party-metal fuel, a nonsense-drenched command to dance and burn all night; there's no subject beyond igniting the crowd's passion until dawn, chant and rhythm doing all the work.","sonnet":"A pure call-and-response party anthem urging everyone to dance, spin, and burn passion through the night without stopping — the BPM spiking, the crowd commanded to move until dawn. There is no narrative tension, only escalating instruction: the concert itself is the content, and the song is its own fuel.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~no-rain-no-rainbow": {"haiku":"Grief and longing follow loss of someone who cannot return; despair transforms into light and beauty like rain becoming rainbow. Hope persists through suffering.","opus":"A grieving ballad about losing someone the narrator can never meet again, unable to sleep and wishing to stay in an unending dream; the central image—no rain, no rainbow—finds that even despair and unending sad rain can become light, painting a rainbow out of loss.","sonnet":"A Japanese lament for someone who has died or permanently departed — the narrator couldn't sleep, hid their loneliness behind smiles, and just wanted to be close; the consolation is that even despair becomes light, and the endless rain will eventually arc into a rainbow.","src":"haiku","fable":"A ballad of grief that refuses the usual consolation, insisting instead that despair itself can be turned toward light and an unending downpour still bends into color somewhere far off. The singer addresses someone now unreachable, admitting the smile hid loneliness and the only wish was to have them near, spoken too late and only to the dark. BABYMETAL, whose whole project welds idol sweetness to metal weight, here drops the noise and lets bare tenderness carry the hurt undefended.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"despair offered as the path toward light rather than away from it","built":"the noise drops so bare tenderness carries the hurt undefended"},
"babymetal~oh-majinai": {"haiku":"A magical incantation where desperation and superstition offer escape when normal solutions fail; the repeated spell becomes the solution itself.","opus":"A folk-metal panic in cartoon form: something precious is lost and the only recourse is a childish incantation, majinai, a magic charm. Under the nonsense chant sits real helplessness, wishing on a spell because nothing else is left to try.","sonnet":"A playful, chant-driven song about hitting a dead end and calling for magical help — the Japanese sections express a kind of flustered panic at something going missing or going wrong, and the refrain is essentially a desperate invocation of a charm or spell to fix it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A comedy spell built out of the word for nothing: the verse is a chain of Japanese phrases that all end in -nai — can't find it, it's nothing, don't worry, what a waste — until omajinai, the charm itself, completes the rhyme and the pile of negations congeals into the chanted nai-nai-nai incantation of the chorus. The scenario is pantomime panic — something lost, a dead end, please save me — resolved not by any answer but by shouting the magic word, and with Sabaton's Joakim Brodén oompah-ing along it plays folk-metal ritual as drinking song: faith as a pun that works if you yell it loud enough.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"faith as a pun that works if you yell it loud enough","built":"chain of -nai negations congeals into the charm itself in the chorus"},
"babymetal~onedari-daisakusen": {"haiku":"A child deploys calculated cuteness and flattery to extract material desires from an indulgent parent, mastering manipulation.","opus":"A gleefully calculated child's campaign to wheedle presents out of Daddy with flattery, tantrums, and angelic smiles, all kept secret from Mom. The comedy is in the little-devil scheming behind the sugar-sweet I love you Papa.","sonnet":"A girl executes an elaborate 'operation beg' on her father — flattering him, throwing cute tantrums, and deploying her most irresistible smile — in order to get him to buy her things, all conducted in secret from mom.","src":"haiku","fable":"A little girl runs a campaign to work her father, laying out flattery, shoulder-rubs, and a mock marriage vow as coldly numbered operations, so the sweetness arrives already weaponized rather than felt. The persona keeps grading its own angel-smile as the strongest, the greatest, the trick no one resists, and even snickers at how plainly the con works. A closing chant collapses the whole act into acquisitive arithmetic, each greedy demand stacked on the last until wanting things is the entire personality, the innocence a bared fang. Read as BABYMETAL frames it, that little-devil tag turns the number into knowing satire of manufactured idol cuteness, the mask performed loudly enough to become the point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"manufactured sweetness exposed loudly enough to become the point","built":"flattery delivered as coldly numbered operations, the mask a bared fang"},
"babymetal~onedari-daisakusen-live":{"haiku":"Desire and begging operate as cosmic strategy; children's wants and needs become existential demands for attention and satisfaction.","opus":"A little girl's comic scheme to sweet-talk her father into buying her everything, angel's face hiding a devil's whisper. BABYMETAL's idol-era satire of childish greed and the daddy's-girl manipulation played entirely for laughs.","sonnet":"A tactical assault on a father's wallet: the speaker deploys flattery, pouting, declarations of undying love, and the promise to be his bride — all calculated steps in the \"begging operation\" — and the chorus of \"buy it, buy it, give it to me\" is chaos theory applied to allowance, performed by someone who knows exactly what they're doing.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~pa-pa-ya": {"haiku":"A summer festival celebrates wild dancing and partying with abandon, mixing cultures and languages in pure hedonistic joy.","opus":"A high-energy, tongue-in-cheek summer party anthem built on the papaya chant, mixing Japanese calls to give your all with a Thai rap comparing life to a spicy papaya salad, pounded and knocked around yet flavorful. Beyond the festival exhortations to dance and make noise, its theme is pure celebratory summer fun.","sonnet":"A summer festival anthem fusing Japanese metal-idol energy with Thai rap and dancehall — the throughline is pure, exuberant celebration: make noise, dance, bring your best self to the festival, burn with everything you've got.","src":"haiku","fable":"A summer-festival banger whose nonsense chant hides a working metaphor: Thai rapper F.Hero's mor lam verse builds the whole thing around som tam, the green papaya salad that only gets delicious by being pounded — happiness ground together with suffering, knocked down and up again until it's got bite. That reframes the Japanese verses' dare — scorch yourself body and soul, test yourself at the brink — as the pounding itself, and the matsuri as what you've earned once you've been through the mortar. It's also METAL GALAXY's pan-Asian thesis in miniature, kawaii metal handing the mic to Thai hip-hop and calling the collision a party.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","identity & becoming"],"means":"happiness ground together with suffering until it has bite","built":"som tam pounding metaphor reframes the festival dare as the mortar itself"},
"babymetal~road-of-resistance": {"haiku":"Warriors kindle inner flames to resist and persevere through isolation and hardship; resistance becomes a steel-forged path to the future.","opus":"A galloping power-metal anthem of perseverance, a signal fire ending the dark and one steel spirit that never turns its back. Built to be a fan communion, its whole subject is refusing to quit.","sonnet":"A Japanese anthem about perseverance and collective will — rising from loneliness and darkness, the narrator and their companions vow to keep moving down a pathless road powered by steel-forged souls, never turning their backs as long as life continues.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is BABYMETAL's power-metal anthem built to be sung back at them, and its engine is the idea of tempering: solitude reframed as a forge rather than a wound. The opening does the work of a whole verse in one image, a signal-fire reddening the eastern sky that turns the end of loneliness into a directional marker, a road appearing where there was only night. From there the song stops describing and starts conscripting, its every second line switching to English chants of resistance and forever so that the private resolve becomes a mass call-and-response, the steel-soul vow welded into a shared us. That is the trick of the track: it treats the will to keep going, even down a pathless path, not as an inner state you privately hold but as something the crowd exists to hand back to you, and the repetition isn't padding but the point, the same vow drilled until it belongs to everyone at once.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"That speed-metal gallop under the banner-waving isn't a pastiche of DragonForce — it is DragonForce: Herman Li and Sam Totman play on the track, and the signature is theirs.","themes":["anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty","identity & becoming"],"means":"private resolve handed to the crowd to be returned as collective vow","built":"English chants switch the lyric from inner state to mass call-and-response"},
"babymetal~rondo-of-nightmare": {"haiku":"Terror cycles endlessly through the psyche: darkness swallows the dreamer, and escape remains forever just beyond reach despite desperate running.","opus":"A recurring nightmare that circles endlessly, insanity lit by a waning moon, the dreamer hiding and finally falling into the dark. The plea to be shown a familiar figure is the only thread out, and it snaps.","sonnet":"A Japanese song about being trapped in a nightmare that keeps repeating — the awakening light is too far away to reach, darkness closes in from all sides, and the speaker can only beg for a glimpse of the person who might lead them out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rondo is a form that keeps returning to the same refrain, and BABYMETAL weaponizes that structure literally: the nightmare here is not any single terror but the fact that it repeats, a loop the narrator cannot break out of. The title is the whole diagnosis — a waning moon, a laughing predator with gleaming eyes, a light of waking that recedes the harder she runs toward it, all circling back with no exit. What lifts it past a simple horror sketch is the turn near the end, when the fleeing voice stops running and begins to plead with the very thing hunting her — if I can never leave, then at least show me your face — collapsing the distance between victim and monster into something closer to surrender or seduction. The two choruses seal the arc by shifting one verb: the first runs through the darkness, the last only falls into it, the chase finally giving way to the descent it was always going to become.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","violence & murder"],"means":"the victim pleads to see the hunter's face rather than flee it","built":"the rondo form literalises the nightmare: structure enacts the inescapable loop"},
"babymetal~shanti-shanti-shanti": {"haiku":"Cycles of existence, desire, and spiritual transformation expressed through Sanskrit concepts. The world turns in recurring patterns of longing and resolution.","opus":"An incantatory meditation drawing on Hindu and Buddhist ideas of peace, prana, karma, samsara and liberation. It celebrates cycles of the seasons, the world and fate spinning onward as souls yearn for love and dance toward freedom.","sonnet":"A meditation on the cyclical nature of existence — seasons, karma, fate, and time turning endlessly — expressed through Sanskrit and Japanese spiritual vocabulary. The lyrics invoke prana, chakra, samsara, and moksha to describe a world of endless, shimmering transformation leading toward freedom.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pop mantra that strings genuine Vedic vocabulary — prana, chakra, samsara, maya, moksha, karma — over verses where everything turns: seasons, world, fate, era. The theology is playful but coherent: rather than seeking release from the wheel of rebirth, the song commands karma itself to dance, locating freedom inside the spinning rather than beyond it — endlessly, boundlessly free precisely because nothing stops moving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","freedom & escape","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"freedom located inside the spinning rather than beyond it","built":"genuine Vedic vocabulary commanded to dance rather than to cease"},
"babymetal~shine": {"haiku":"Despite darkness and suffering, light guides eternal journeys; travelers move forward toward hope that transcends time and space.","opus":"A soaring anthem about pressing forward through darkness toward the light on a road spanning past, present and future, believing in the miracle of having met and journeying together toward eternity. Even wandering endless darkness, the heart cries out that it's fine to break, because a dazzling light will illuminate the way, shining forever in each other's hearts.","sonnet":"A Japanese ballad about an endless journey through darkness guided by a shining light — the speaker wanders through a void but holds onto the miracle of a connection or purpose that transcends time and space, reaching toward a distant luminous horizon.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grief ballad dressed as a space voyage: the journey doesn't end, but someone is now on the far side of the light, and the song vows to cross time itself to reach where you are — arriving as close to elegy as BABYMETAL gets, in the wake of losing guitarist Mikio Fujioka. The middle is the tell: wandering endless dark, hauling in the faintest light hand over hand, the heart shouting that breaking is an acceptable price — grief converted into pilgrimage, with the shining kept alive in my heart and yours because that's where the dead actually live.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","nostalgia & memory","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"grief converted into pilgrimage toward where the dead actually live","built":"space-voyage framing stages an elegy as a crossing rather than a farewell"},
"babymetal~sis-anger": {"haiku":"Japanese rallying against selfishness, cowardice, and half-heartedness through anger and pure effort; youth demand genuine commitment over excuses, celebrating fierce resolve and rejection of hesitation.","opus":"A furious rejection of quitters and excuse-makers, the singer declaring hatred for the selfish and for anyone who whines that something's impossible before trying. The throughline is a demand for sincerity over ability: whether you win or lose, are skilled or clumsy, all that matters is giving your genuine all, and she'll beat that fighting spirit back into you.","sonnet":"A furious call-out directed at people who make excuses, fake toughness, or only care about themselves — the singers demand real effort and genuine fighting spirit, not posturing.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cast as pure hardcore and handed to the BLACK BABYMETAL duo, this is a tantrum weaponized into doctrine — a song built entirely out of the word hate, aimed not at an enemy but at excuse-making itself. The list of things it despises is a moral inventory of cowardice: people who think only of themselves, who reach for it's impossible and it's pointless and I've hit my limit, who talk tough about things they can't do. Against all of that the song poses a single test that survives every chorus — winning or losing, being good or bad at it, none of it matters, only whether you meant it. The genius is the turn in the final chorus, where the target starts grovelling — sorry, forgive me, what should I do, was that a lie, are you sucking up? — and the singer reads the apology as one more dodge and answers it with bring it on, refusing to let contrition become the next hiding place. What sounds like a schoolyard scream is really a stripped-down ethic of sincerity, furious precisely because it takes effort more seriously than success.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"sincerity as the only metric when success and failure both miss the point","built":"the final chorus refuses contrition, reading apology as the next hiding place"},
"babymetal~song-4": {"opus":"A playful counting chant fixated on the number four, spinning Japan's superstitious link between four and death into something lucky, happy, and vitamin-bright instead. NONE","sonnet":"A playful, absurdist celebration of the number four — the song counts up to four from three, counts down to four from five, and stacks positive meanings onto the number (happiness, vitamins, not dying), treating numerical coincidence as something worth chanting ecstatically; it is essentially a nonsense anthem built around a single digit.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A counting-chant that fixates on a single number and dares it to mean what Japanese superstition says it does: the homophone linking four to death. The song answers by force of repetition, insisting the number belongs to happiness, to vitamins, to joy, and never to dying. That refusal is the whole content, an unlucky syllable worn harmless by cheerful drilling until it lands as a schoolyard beat rather than a curse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"cheerful drilling until an unlucky syllable lands harmless","built":"repetition replaces the curse with a schoolyard beat"},
"babymetal~song-4-live":{"haiku":"The number four becomes mantra through happiness and vitamin and death, counting toward fortune. All things collapse into the universal symbol of luck and misfortune merged.","opus":"A playful Japanese counting chant fixated on the number four, riffing on its homophone for death: 'the happy 4, the not-dying 4, the failure 4, the vitamin 4, the joy 4,' turning ominous numerology into a giddy, nonsensical celebration.","sonnet":"The number four — in Japanese superstition associated with death but here reclaimed as happiness, vitamins, joy, and welcome — is counted up and down, chanted as a crowd rallying cry; it's a playful numerical inversion that turns an omen into a celebration.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~starlight": {"fable":"Heard across the fandom and press as an elegy for Mikio Fujioka, the Kami Band guitarist who died in early 2018 after a fall while stargazing — which makes the song's stargazer figure close to literal. Grief gets converted into trajectory — the lost friend is not mourned downward but urged higher, past where the light reaches, while the living promise a two-way custody: we carry you wherever we go, you keep living in the heart. The wordless na-na chant that opens and closes the song does the rest, communal keening where words would be too small.","fableDeep":"The verses chase and the choruses climb: running to the far distance, fragments of memory fading like déjà vu, then the imperative to fly higher 'like a stargazer.' The bridge suddenly speaks plainly in both directions — wherever WE are, we'll be with YOU; wherever YOU are, live in MY heart — and the astronomy does the mourning: a shooting star is the shortest-lived light there is, and the song answers it with 'we'll never forget.' It reads as an elegy that refuses to slow down — remembrance at full speed, wordless na-na-nas where the hardest words would go.","haiku":"A journey of searching and flying toward light and hope, with commitment to never forgetting the brightness and connection that binds people together.","opus":"A song about soaring beyond time and distance toward a distant light, promising to always stay with and never forget a loved one, keeping their memory alive like a shining, shooting star.","sonnet":"A song about persisting through separation and distance, urging flight toward a guiding starlight and promising that wherever either person ends up, the bond and the memory of that light will never be forgotten.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"remembrance kept at full running speed rather than slowed to a vigil","built":"wordless na-na chant placed exactly where the hardest words would go"},
"babymetal~sunset-kiss-feat-polyphia":{"haiku":"Romantic tenderness and dusk mark an ephemeral moment of beauty and desire.","opus":"A tender lament for someone dissolving into the sunset, holding onto the warmth of a shared world that's already ending; love turning to tears as a dream fades, reaching for a hand that can no longer be caught.","sonnet":"A love fading into a sunset the narrator can no longer reach — your words still echo in your gentle voice, but chasing the disappearing dusk is hopeless; love becomes tears becomes acceptance. \"夕陽のkiss\" (the sunset's kiss) is the name for what's being lost, and the sky they used to watch together is the last shared thing.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~tales-of-the-destinies": {"haiku":"Relentless motion and shared destiny—never stopping, eternal melody, moving forward together forever, bound to collective fate.","opus":"The kawaii-metal formula turned toward defiant reinvention: a countdown to being reborn, refusing to accept that nothing works out. Under the sugar and prog flourishes it's a pep talk against fatalism, insisting you're never truly alone or finished.","sonnet":"A high-energy affirmation against defeat and doubt — the repeated denials (it's not impossible, it's not a dream, it's not too late) build toward a launch countdown and the declaration that destiny and memory will carry the group forward together forever.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built on the Japanese word nai — the negation, no, not-there — chanted until it stops meaning absence and starts sounding like a taunt: no luck, no answer, it can't be helped, none none none. That drone of defeat is the mask. What's underneath is the countdown, three-two-one-zero repeated as a question mark rather than a launch, because the point is the loop itself — a birth that keeps restarting, a can't-stop-me refusal to look back. The prog-metal chaos of the arrangement (the arrangement lurches through shifting meters) mirrors the trick: every deflating nai is answered by its own inversion — not useless? not useless! — so the negation machine gets turned into propulsion. It lands on desutinii not as fate handed down but as the thing you seize on the next zero, the endless journey where you are not alone. The despair vocabulary was the fuel all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"the negation machine turned into propulsion — defeat vocabulary as fuel","built":"shifting meters mirror the lyric's trick of inverting each deflating nai"},
"babymetal~tattoo-live-at-rock-on-the-range-2018":{"haiku":"Something hidden inside swings and burns like a pendulum; despite concealment, the truth emerges while passion oscillates between states.","opus":"A Metal Galaxy-era track long aired live under a placeholder name before its release as Kagerou, Japanese for the shimmer of a heat haze. That mirage becomes the metaphor: the narrator slides from faint suspicion of a partner into wavering, unstable doubt that finally ignites into open jealousy and anger, feeling warping perception the way rising heat bends the air.","sonnet":"The narrator cannot hide an overwhelming, slightly deranging feeling — probably desire or infatuation — described through the pendulum rhythm of the body swaying between rising exhilaration and the flame of jealousy, with the whole thing burning out of control.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~the-one": {"haiku":"Collective transcendence and destiny bind disparate voices into singular communion; together they are invincible, alone they fragment.","opus":"A transcendent call to unity across time, the crowd fused into a single us that stands together in the light, seeking meaning and an answer to whether anything truly ends.","sonnet":"A unity anthem directed at a fan community — specifically BABYMETAL's own fanbase — declaring that the bond between performers and audience transcends time and distance, that together they make up a single entity. The Japanese lyrics ask whether there is an answer to why things end and where their shared place exists, while the English refrain insists that wherever they are, they are always on each other's side.","src":"haiku","fable":"A unity anthem that stages a real language barrier as its central drama, then refuses to solve it, offering belonging where it can't offer comprehension. The bilingual seams do the work: an English plea to understand meets a Japanese wish that someone would simply reveal the answer, one voice reaching for meaning while the other admits it may never arrive. What resolves the tension is not understanding but presence — bodies gathered in one room, hands lifted in a single motion — and the endlessly repeated declaration that the band and its crowd are a single thing. That flood of repetition is itself the argument: when the plural collapses into one so many times, insistence stands in for proof, and a group asks to be met halfway by an audience that mostly can't read its own words.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"It sounds like a hymn to a beloved, and it is, sung to the fandom: BABYMETAL's official fan club, founded 2014-15, is literally named THE ONE, and Su-metal has said the fans are collectively the one — the song addresses and sanctifies them by their own name.","themes":["friendship & loyalty","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"belonging offered where comprehension cannot follow","built":"bilingual seams stage the language barrier as the drama rather than solving it"},
"babymetal~the-one-english-ver": {"haiku":"Unity and transcendence emerge through collective ritual; the speaker and community ascend beyond individual limitation.","opus":"An anthem of unity binding the band and its crowd into a single collective forever. Reaching across time toward a shared dream and place, it turns the circle pit into a promise of belonging and mutual devotion.","sonnet":"A song about unity between a band and its fans across time and language — the narrator doesn't understand why division exists but believes that together, standing in a circle, the audience and performers are one single entity, bound by shared voice, shared dream, and shared place.","src":"haiku","fable":"A plea for a shared answer becomes the mosh floor itself made into a bond: English question and Japanese wanting braided together, refusing to stay separate voices. The song insists on a collective first person, dropping every scattered listener into one linked ring of raised arms and pressed palms where the crowd's motion carries the meaning. What began as bewildered asking resolves into a vow of belonging that folds the churning violence of the ring into something closer to sworn allegiance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"It sounds like a hymn to a beloved, and it is, sung to the fandom: BABYMETAL's official fan club, founded 2014-15, is literally named THE ONE, and Su-metal has said the fans are collectively the one — the song addresses and sanctifies them by their own name.","themes":["friendship & loyalty"],"means":"mosh-floor violence reframed as sworn allegiance","built":"the crowd's raised arms carry the meaning the words cannot fully bridge"},
"babymetal~the-one-unfinished-version-from-the-first-take":{"haiku":"Singular importance and chosen destiny elevate the speaker or subject to unique status.","opus":"A grand, communal anthem about unity and being singularly chosen even as everything ends, the raw first-take rendering stripping it down to a plea to open minds and share one dream, band and fans made one.","sonnet":"A spare, open-armed unity statement — band and audience collapsed into a single collective \"THE ONE\" — asking to be taken to a land of dreams together, with the unfinished quality of the version giving the plea an unguarded vulnerability that the polished original smooths over.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~time-wave":{"haiku":"Temporal distortion and cyclical return collapse linear progression, with waves of time repeating and intersecting.","opus":"Life as a Möbius strip of ups and downs with no true ending, past and future looping endlessly; rather than despair, it frames the loop as something to keep moving through, riding the wave and starting over.","sonnet":"Life as a Möbius strip with no beginning or end — time loops, ups and downs recur, the maze is \"amazing\" and dizzying — but the god of motion keeps pushing forward: past and future are both exceeded, and the wave carries you on whether you navigate it or not. The Merry-go-round image at the center makes the vertigo feel almost festive.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~uki-uki-midnight": {"haiku":"Candy and dreams sustain gatherings of youth refusing bedtime, the speaker celebrates living in contradiction between sweetness and desire.","opus":"A giddy kids' sleepover-party fantasy, cutting the lights to charge up energy and cram the night with sweets and dreams past curfew. The joy is in the innocent rebellion of staying up, sugar-high and refusing that it's ever closing time.","sonnet":"A party invitation for kids that refuses to end at curfew — gathering friends for a sleepover with candy and dreams, turning off the lights, disco balls spinning, the night stretching past bedtime because nobody wants it to stop.","src":"haiku","fable":"A childhood sleepover swollen to cosmic scale, where the sugar-fed scramble to outlast bedtime is treated with the total, unironic gravity only a kid guarding one forbidden night can summon. The idol machinery keeps insisting this tiny defiance is the genuine article, forcing giddy hyperbole onto a stake no larger than snack choices and a looming curfew. The song's real motion is bodily: heaviness and a full belly creep in near the end, drooping eyes rubbed, and the refusal to stop reads as a small body's last stand against the clock, glitter substituting for the strength to keep going.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","party & hedonism"],"means":"a small body's last stand against the bedtime clock","built":"glitter substitutes for the strength to stay awake"},
"babymetal~uki-uki-midnight-live":{"haiku":"Nostalgic celebration of youthful joy, treats, and togetherness in a carefree party atmosphere.","opus":"A children's sugar-rush sleepover fantasy staged as metal: staying up past curfew, gorging on sweets, refusing the adult world's bedtime. Pure kid-logic euphoria dressed in guitars.","sonnet":"A sleepover party that refuses curfew: the lights go down, the mirror ball spins, everyone eats snacks and dreams, and even when eyes droop and stomachs are full the climax is still ahead. Pure adolescent night-energy resisting bedtime.","src":"haiku"},
"babymetal~yava": {"haiku":"Everything appears subtly different even when people insist everything's the same, and this uncanny gap between sameness and otherness provokes constant anxiety.","opus":"Comic paralysis over trivial choices, spiraling into the conviction that everything is subtly wrong no matter what everyone else insists. The panic of overthinking small decisions is played for delighted absurdity.","sonnet":"A comedic, frenetic song about the paralysis of indecision — everything feels subtly wrong or off, the narrator cannot figure out which option is different from which, and the anxiety of choosing spirals into cheerful chaos.","src":"haiku","fable":"A comedy of indecision built on a single word: chigau, the flat Japanese negation that means wrong, different, not-that. Everyone insists it's all the same — pick any one, they're identical — but the narrator can't stop feeling that something is subtly off, and the song is the runaway anxiety of that feeling, spinning from a mild maybe-it's-a-bit-different into a total collapse where everything, every option, is wrong. YAVA! itself is the slang alarm bell — roughly oh no, this is bad — that punctuates each fresh spike of it's-nagging-at-me-what-do-I-do. Landing on METAL RESISTANCE between the album's grander statements, this is BABYMETAL working the kawaii-metal formula at its most deliberately trivial: the panic has no object, the chugging riff and the telephone-tone nonsense syllables (pippo-pappo) frame consumer-level dithering as a full-body crisis, and the joke is that the mask never drops — there's nothing underneath the fuss but the fuss itself, a hyperactive parody of being unable to choose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"consumer-level indecision inflated into a full-body crisis","built":"the mask never drops — the fuss reveals nothing underneath but more fuss"},
"bad-blood-orchestra~the-classic":{"haiku":"The speaker scavenged another's destruction while that person tore at the narrator's foundation. Extended life should grant wisdom; accumulated scars mark the cost of endurance.","opus":"The slow humiliation of being walked over in a one-sided relationship, picking up someone's pieces while they shatter yours; the reckoning comes as the narrator finally sees the lie for what it is and stops fooling themselves.","sonnet":"Someone who picked up the other's pieces while their own were torn apart confesses they have it all wrong — the lie believed, the self kept shallow — and waits for the sun to come in even as the light burns everything. The worst is still to come, but the reckoning is mutual: the other's end will arrive as no surprise either.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-dreems~dumb-ideas":{"haiku":"Contempt for shallow ambition and empty performance hardens into cruelty; the speaker dismisses others as worthless while nursing his own emptiness.","opus":"Australian pub-rock contempt for a person who is all posture and no substance, the disposable hanger-on you get bored of by sunrise. It lands because the scorn curdles into something universal about people who mistake noise for meaning.","sonnet":"A dismissal of someone who talks endlessly about big ideas but delivers nothing — borrowing rides, performing depth, coasting on an illusion of substance — until boredom and contempt make clear they were just another in a long line of empty gestures.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-love~activist":{"haiku":"Performative activism and hollow virtue signaling; words without action drain meaning, leaving empty posturing.","opus":"A scathing takedown of a performative moralist, all virtue signalled and no action taken, preaching from a coffin about everything he's just read. The thesis lands flat and hard: a person who stands for nothing will be talked into anything.","sonnet":"Performative activism as hollow spectacle — the mouth with no sense coming out, the flavor-of-the-week cause, the virtue stuck through teeth — the speaker levels that no action, no truth, no means to an end makes the whole display a coffin-sermon, and the warning stands: if you stand for nothing you'll fall for everything.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-love~dead-weight":{"haiku":"Codependent toxicity binds two people in cycles of self-loathing and mutual harm, where love masks suffocation and pain paradoxically feels preferable to abandonment.","opus":"A relationship indistinguishable from self-destruction, the speaker begging to be hated and torn apart while mistaking smothering harm for love. The bathroom-floor imagery and the question is this love make the codependence lethal, each partner giving up themselves in a spiral neither can stop.","sonnet":"A toxic entanglement where love and self-destruction are indistinguishable: the speaker begs to be hated and torn apart, admits they are \"dead weight\" torturing the other person, and oscillates between craving attention and lying on the bathroom floor, unable to make the suffering stop.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-love~disconnect":{"haiku":"The speaker severes connection to escape the gravitational pull of others' fear and shame, reclaiming autonomy through radical disconnection.","opus":"Bad Love treat severing a draining bond as a release, the speaker no longer bothered by another's opinion and unwilling to let their anxieties and misgivings triumph. A blaze that clears the field and an escape found before daybreak recast the split as freedom, taking the lesson rather than waging a fight.","sonnet":"A speaker steps back from a relationship that brought them down to their knees, deciding disconnection is better than war, and finding clarity in the distance — 'I've never been the one to burn bridges' — while questioning whether they can ever learn to love from their losses.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-love~dmu":{"haiku":"A person becomes entangled with someone whose substance abuse and corruption drags them down, asking whether anyone can truly heal or if they're born broken.","opus":"A helpless account of watching a friend consumed by drug addiction, their name and face dragged into public ruin while they refuse to change. The song frames the pull as mutual destruction, the singer unable to save them without being pulled under alongside them.","sonnet":"Watching someone be destroyed by substance abuse — the narrator is drawn like a moth to the flame, but the person they care about has dug a grave for them both with their addiction, dragging everything under with frostbitten hands.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-love~feel-good":{"haiku":"Craving satisfaction and consumption of physical sensation, wielding power and demanding immediate gratification from the present moment.","opus":"A study of appetite chasing sensation for its own sake, the singer pleading to be gratified quickly before the high fades. He frames every relationship as a transaction demanding its price, wearing his own shame lightly, aware the intensity he craves is fleeting even as he reaches for it again.","sonnet":"Desperate craving — for sensation, for relief, for speed — drives a narrator who wears his own burning guilt like a crown. The self-awareness offers no exit: he knows the compulsion is making a mess of things, but the need to feel something, anything, overrides every cleaner instinct.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-love~follow-the-leader":{"haiku":"Society is controlled by media and authority who feed the masses fear and synthetic emotions, turning people into mindless followers marching in lockstep.","opus":"A conspiracy-tinged indictment of manufactured consent, a numbed public raised on screens and steered like an obedient herd while unseen powers steer them toward war. Bad Love turn the unease into a driving protest, casting media and authority as trainers grooming the mindless to obey.","sonnet":"Media and power as a behaviour modification system — youth numbed by news feeds, synthetic emotion, a panopticon that keeps everyone marching in time by alternating fear and gratitude on command. The song names it plainly: the deaf leading the blind, photo ops at children's caskets, a president who is a bastard while Miss America sleeps with a knife. The only exit offered is revolt, with no roadmap for what comes after.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-love~love-sic":{"haiku":"Geographic escape cannot sever emotional entanglement; the speaker cycles between flight and return, unable to sever what damaged them so thoroughly.","opus":"Bad Love wrap post-split ambivalence around a single fare in and out of California, the speaker standing taller and rediscovering his own voice once the other is gone. A voicemail break and the queasy refrain about being passed over reveal how the wound tears open the instant he reaches for his phone.","sonnet":"A California break-up replayed obsessively: backseat youth, drug-filled sleepless nights, the fury of being replaced, and a voicemail left just to hear the ex's recorded voice — \"could you lose your keys and just stay with me\" is the impossible wish at the centre.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-love~saints":{"haiku":"A sinner born into damnation seeks redemption but cannot change, creating tension between desire for salvation and acceptance of darkness.","opus":"A hardened outsider who calls himself a sinner wonders whether the righteous crowd has any place left for someone like him. Born onto the wrong path and unable to change, he offers everything he has yet still can't manage to pardon or move past the wounds, doubting redemption is even possible for him.","sonnet":"A self-described sinner who never fit the mold of the saved asks whether there is room for someone like them — having done the crime without repentance, been cut off and cut open, and still unable to forgive or forget.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-love~ung-d":{"haiku":"Romantic disaster and toxic attraction, love that harms and corrupts.","opus":"Tearing the mask off a hypocrite who builds walls to hide who they really are, a coward posing as a king. The song's power is in refusing to be drawn out or blamed while watching the pretender's lies collapse.","sonnet":"An unmasking confrontation — someone performs righteousness while the narrator sees straight through the facade, calls out the empty casket and the coward pointing fingers, dares them to \"remove the mask and show them who you are.\" When the walls come down, the narrator has already left: \"you can't draw me out this time.\"","src":"haiku"},
"bad-love~violence":{"haiku":"Government control and systemic numbing reduce populations to automatons; the speaker interrogates complicity and demands resistance to the automation of consciousness itself.","opus":"A confrontational anthem urging listeners to question a world of state violence and mass automation, asking whether they'll fight or just dissolve into the static. Its hook is a call to sever the feed and break the rhythm, to reject a single voice claiming to speak for everyone.","sonnet":"An indictment of government-sponsored violence and mass passivity: the speaker asks whether the listener truly understands what taking or feeling alive means, while diagnosing society as automatic and easily herded, controlled by a single voice speaking for the nation. The command to \"cut the cord and break the tempo\" is both a rallying cry and an acknowledgment that most people won't.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-nerves~baby-drummer":{"haiku":"Seeking new rhythm and meaning; questioning faith and looking for divine drumming in secular spaces.","opus":"A frantic garage-punk rush of infatuation, the singer tuning into the airwaves for some divine signal and pleading for fresh bandmates, chords and beats while handing himself completely to a partner who supplies the groove. Beneath the giddy hooks flickers a grimmer question about why the best people don't last. Bad Nerves are a British quartet built for this breakneck buzz.","sonnet":"An electric encounter with a musician — a girl with innate rhythm, sainted by lineage — draws the narrator in completely. Beneath the infatuation runs a darker current: incompatible gods, the mystery of why the best people die young, desire that knows it cannot last.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-nerves~can-t-be-mine":{"haiku":"The listener refuses lessons and persists in self-sabotage despite favorable circumstances. Momentum carries toward ruin; stopping becomes impossible when momentum dictates motion.","opus":"Buzzsaw garage-punk about craving someone off-limits and being unable to quit the chase; the tug-of-war of unavailability and longing captures restless want and the useless wish to simply evict them from your head.","sonnet":"An attraction that can't be consummated — the object of desire can't be theirs, and the speaker can't quite become theirs either — breeds lonely rooms and the fantasy of companionship without drama. The push-pull of \"can't be mine / can I be yours\" runs on desire and impossibility in equal measure.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-nerves~can-t-be-mine-live-in-london-2022":{"haiku":"Romantic incompatibility and loneliness clash with desire; the speaker seeks connection but recognizes an unbridgeable distance between what they want and what the other person can give.","opus":"Desperate for uncomplicated company, he wants a person he already knows he cannot have and half-hopes to belong to instead. The frantic tempo masks plain loneliness and the sense of missing something more.","sonnet":"A restless infatuation the speaker knows cannot be consummated: he sits alone wanting company without drama, caught between desire and honest self-awareness, and the chorus circles the same impossible question — can you be mine, can I be yours — without landing on an answer.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-nerves~don-t-stop":{"haiku":"Chaos, medication, and decay accelerate without pause; despite systemic rot and murder, the speaker claims peace through connection, wanting to fall in love again.","opus":"A frantic garage-punk rush that piles up images of collapse, drugs, and grasping politicians, yet keeps swerving back to reassurance: the two of us are fine no matter what falls apart around us. The British band turns exhaustion at the world into a breakneck love song, longing to fall for someone again and beat the clock.","sonnet":"A torrent of modern-world noise — cocaine, radio DJs, culture-killing institutions — crashes against a private shelter of mutual love. Amid the chaos and corporate theft, two people hold each other as the only thing worth keeping, falling in love again and killing time.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-nerves~dreaming":{"haiku":"Death outlives love in memory; the speaker lies dreaming while dead, their affection rippling outward as a persistent echo that transcends physical absence.","opus":"A restless refusal to accept mortality, insisting love and sound can outlive the body like a wave that never stops. The chant against ending up dead is really a plea for more time with someone.","sonnet":"A figure lying in bed imagines their love outlasting their death — existing as a line in someone's head, living like a wave after the gutter — and the repeated \"I don't wanna be dead like you\" suggests they are speaking to someone already gone, or to a deadened version of themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-nerves~last-beat":{"opus":"Solitude condensed into a single restless image: a person keeping time alone, running from something unnamed and close to detonating. The relentless repetition mirrors the trapped, circular anxiety it describes.","sonnet":"A figure marked by solitude keeps time to a dying rhythm — \"you tap your foot to the last beat on your own\" — while not knowing what they're fleeing from, only that an internal pressure is close to blowing the heart apart.","src":"sonnet"},
"bad-nerves~radio-punk":{"haiku":"Commercial accommodation betrays punk ethos as authenticity yields to market demand; the speaker mourns the death of his former self.","opus":"A love-quarrel dressed as a genre complaint: mourning the punk that sold its nerve to the airwaves while still wanting a slot on that same radio. The tension is wanting authenticity and mainstream reach at once, and knowing you can't have both.","sonnet":"A lament that commercial radio has stolen punk's authenticity — the genre the speaker loved has been packaged and sold, leaving them with the ironic desire to be on the very radio that betrayed everything punk stood for.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-nerves~television":{"haiku":"Media and surveillance colonize consciousness, replacing independent thought with manufactured information and passive consumption.","opus":"A jittery garage-punk rant against media saturation, the way screens flood you with information and medication and manufactured fear until thinking itself feels dangerous. The love confessed to the TV is addiction, not affection.","sonnet":"A screed against media manipulation and passivity — television and the information flood it delivers are portrayed as tools that polarize, drug, and surveill the population, turning people into isolated consumers who can't distinguish themselves from the content they absorb. The ironic \"I love you\" to television is bitter.","src":"haiku","fable":"Bad Nerves weaponize their garage-punk velocity as content, the buzzsaw tempo miming the deluge of data the lyric warns against. The verses tick off a whole apparatus of pacification, tampered news, dosage, surveillance, a monetized decency, all of it channeled through a figure whose sole concern is the forecast. The panic sharpens on one genuine hinge, the narrator can no longer distinguish coming apart from simply growing numb to the pressure. Then the title lands as pure ironic surrender, after indicting the screen as the engine of that captivity, the hook professes love for it anyway. Even the promised comfort curdles, since the togetherness offered is a whole nation watching the same broadcast alone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the screen indicted as captor, then loved in the same breath","built":"garage-punk velocity mimes the data deluge the lyric warns against"},
"bad-nerves~usa":{"haiku":"Rock and roll as countercultural resistance against institutional control and mass incarceration.","opus":"A breathless punk snapshot of America as spectacle and contradiction: mass incarceration chanted alongside the flag, rock and roll rising from the dead in the belly of the machine it distrusts.","sonnet":"A spare, ironic salute to America: two million prisoners, rock and roll declared bad for business and now resurrected, the CIA acknowledged in place of the flag — a provocative, minimalist critique by repetition.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~artificial-suicide":{"haiku":"Manufactured death and false ending, constructed demise or simulated finality.","opus":"A revolt against being reduced to data and content in the algorithmic feed, refusing to be a commodity fed back to the masses. Bad Omens channels digital-age alienation into a demand to stay human against the machine.","sonnet":"A refusal to be commodified in the digital feed — glitching in the chain, looping in the brain, playing the game while screaming \"we're not gonna be a commodity.\" The \"artificial suicide\" is the identity death demanded by platforms that force you to read \"if I wanna be great, I don't wanna be me,\" and the Molotov for the broken youth is the alternative.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~artificial-suicide-unzipped":{"haiku":"People resist commodification and digital exploitation, rejecting forced conformity and the pressure to erase their identity for the sake of commercial appeal.","opus":"A refusal to be reduced to engagement fuel in the attention economy, aimed at the algorithms and screens that flatten people into product. The threat in the title is turning that machinery against itself rather than being consumed by it.","sonnet":"A rejection of social media and algorithmic identity — the digital feed turns people into commodities, forces them to hate their own face in the mirror, and scripts self-destruction through endless comparison — and the song is a defiant refusal to be consumed by it.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~burning-out":{"haiku":"The speaker escapes darkness and finds pride in who they are despite imperfection; sadness is inescapable but persisting in it serves no purpose.","opus":"A wounded reckoning with a manipulator moves from being pulled back into darkness toward a defiant, communal pride in who we are despite imperfection. Bad Omens, a Virginia metalcore act on their earlier, more anthemic footing before their moody breakout, pair the exhaustion of burning out with an arena-sized chorus, so betrayal curdles into resolve and the refusal to forget becomes a badge worn under the lights.","sonnet":"Someone who pulled themselves out of darkness only to be dragged back by another person asserts hard-won self-worth and collective pride. Found and burning out simultaneously, the narrator refuses to vanish quietly and insists that imperfection is not failure.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~burning-out-unplugged":{"haiku":"Secrets hide beneath lost identity while deception obscures hidden meanings. Silence becomes necessary shield; the speaker will bite their tongue before speaking dangerous truths.","opus":"Emerging from lies and self-loss into a defiant, imperfect pride, while admitting sadness that runs in the veins can't simply be outrun; the acoustic setting sharpens the tension between finding yourself and being pulled back into darkness.","sonnet":"Once manipulated by someone's lies and pulled back into darkness, the speaker has now found themselves under the lights and sounds — lost but found, imperfect but proud. If good things must end, the declaration is they won't go out the same, because sadness runs in the veins and can't simply be outrun.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~careful-what-you-wish-for":{"haiku":"Madness births beauty in death while sadness paradoxically triggers happiness. Guilt compounds desires' consequences; wishes carry hidden prices the wisher fails to calculate.","opus":"A warning wrapped in grief, carrying guilt that was never his and begging someone not to sit waiting for the world to release them. Under the refrain is a plea against giving up, the plain insistence that they're worth more here than as a memory.","sonnet":"Warning someone not to wait — for dust to settle, for the world to let go — the speaker carries guilt they didn't earn and urges the other not to disappear, since \"a shoulder's better than a knot\" and being alive is a better memory than not. Only in madness do you find beautiful death, and that paradox drives the urgency.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~careful-what-you-wish-for-live":{"haiku":"Inherited guilt and shared suffering bind two people together; the speaker carries pain they did not create while begging the other not to surrender to darkness.","opus":"Carrying a guilt that isn't fully his own, the speaker warns a lover not to envy his pain and pleads with them not to give up on life. It is about staving off someone's despair, insisting they are worth more alive than as a memory.","sonnet":"An address to someone in crisis: the speaker has carried guilt that wasn't theirs, warns that the weight is nothing you'd want to feel, and urges the listener not to wait for things to get worse before reaching out — \"a shoulder's better than a knot\" — declaring they will not give up on them.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~come-undone":{"opus":"A hypnotic love song about willingly letting someone dismantle you emotionally, surrendering to being taken apart because the connection is worth the wreckage. At its core it asks who you genuinely want and need once every defense has dropped away.","sonnet":"A cover of Duran Duran's song about intense, destabilizing romantic desire — the narrator is pulled apart by someone who feels both dreamed-into-existence and real, and the song asks who you turn to for your deepest needs when you are fully unraveling.","src":"sonnet"},
"bad-omens~concrete-jungle":{"haiku":"A person climbs toward success in a brutal city then crashes hard, unable to escape the urban jungle despite their effort.","opus":"A furious reckoning with ambition and disillusion, where clawing toward the sun ends in a fall through a hostile city of sirens and coyotes, the singer crowning himself king of nothing. It aches for a world without a loaded die, then hardens into an immortal defiance. Bad Omens are an American metalcore act whose 2022 rise leaned on exactly this glossy, cinematic dread.","sonnet":"Exhaustion and defiance braided together — someone who threw everything at a city that chews people up, begged, ran in circles, climbed toward something and crashed back down, and still refuses to be broken. The rage turns into a declaration: even fallen, they claim the concrete jungle as their throne, wanting things money can't buy and daring anyone to kill what can't die.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~exit-wounds":{"haiku":"Discovers strength through abandonment and cruelty, transforming rage into power and becoming a formidable enemy. Internal death paradoxically enables survival.","opus":"A vengeful anthem of survival aimed at someone who abandoned the singer, framed as the man who became the monster and now returns to prove them wrong. The core wound is feeling dead inside long before actual death, and the promise that no cry for help will be heard. Bad Omens work this territory of betrayal and reinvention across their Virginia-bred metalcore.","sonnet":"Raised by people who shaped you and then abandoned you, this is a slow reckoning with identity — years spent proving a point to those who doubted you, realizing too late who you actually are. The anger is pointed outward now, a warning to anyone who still wants to make an enemy, backed by the bleak question of how you're supposed to live when something inside already died.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~hedonist-recharged":{"opus":"A first-person seduction from the perspective of the devil/temptation itself, luring someone with forbidden fruit and feeling no remorse for tearing them apart; it reframes guilt by insisting the victim rolled the dice and pulled their own trigger, so the tempter isn't the only sinner.","sonnet":"A self-portrait of someone who is deliberately cruel, unapologetic, and amoral — they tempt, manipulate, destroy emotional trust, feel no remorse, and frame the damage they do as the victim's fault for engaging with someone whose nature they already knew.","src":"sonnet"},
"bad-omens~impose":{"haiku":"Self-doubt erodes credibility while internal fixation prevents connection. Echoes of doubt sit alone in private chambers; persistence isolates regardless of external knocking.","opus":"Impostor syndrome and depressive withdrawal, feeling like a fraud who burdens everyone just by existing; the ache is offering people an easy exit from you while fearing that running away only confirms the worst about yourself.","sonnet":"A self-described fraud in a prison made of bone, always on their toes, always feeling like they impose — the speaker preemptively offers the other permission to let them go while asking what it says about them that they keep running, pushing everyone away on borrowed time. The unrepayable debt and borrowed existence make staying feel like trespass.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~just-pretend":{"opus":"Bad Omens turn an unresolved breakup into a vow of endless patience, the narrator promising to linger at the lowest point for as long as it takes because he simply cannot move on. He hides his hurt behind a cheerful mask and settles for faking that he's fine, holding out hope the two might repair things down the line.","sonnet":"A song about loving someone across an unbridgeable distance — the narrator admits he's not okay but performs okayness, waits indefinitely at the bottom for someone he cannot get over, and recognizes they're too different right now to make it work. The emotional core is patient, helpless devotion that pretending is the only available tool.","src":"sonnet"},
"bad-omens~like-a-villain":{"haiku":"A person rejects being made a villain, pushed to the edge and broken, refusing to share secrets or be controlled.","opus":"A bitter goodbye to someone who stages their own suffering and rewrites their story to look blameless, pushing the singer to the edge until he slips. The refrain flips the script: he never needed the darkness, it needed him. Bad Omens favour this psychological push-and-pull, the villain framing turned back on the accuser, wrapped in their signature moody metalcore.","sonnet":"A relationship fractured by someone who rewrites their own story at everyone else's expense — staging scenes, turning tables with missing legs, using the speaker as a place to dump their guilt. The speaker was pushed to the edge, fell, and now refuses to carry the other person's secrets or shame. The closing twist: the villain role was never chosen, it was assigned, and the song accepts it coldly.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~limits":{"opus":"A defiant response to being cast as the villain and betrayed by someone who took what they wanted and twisted the story; unafraid even of death, the singer trusts that everyone can see the truth and vows to make the betrayer regret it.","sonnet":"The narrator has been framed as the villain by someone who manipulated the story, left them for dead, and turned others against them — they refuse to take anything back, hold their ground, and intend to make the person regret what they've done.","src":"sonnet"},
"bad-omens~nervous-system":{"haiku":"Predatory obsession spreads like a virus through the nervous system, combining worship and degradation into an addictive cycle of mutual corruption.","opus":"Desire framed as infection and worship, where wanting to be consumed by another becomes a new religion built on obsession and self-erasure. The lover is both addict and dealer, thrilled by the very harm being courted.","sonnet":"About an obsessive, toxic attraction framed as a virus or a religious conversion — the person the narrator is drawn to wants to hurt and consume them, and the narrator both resists and surrenders to this, describing the experience as a Bathory algorithm of murder and superstition. The relationship is an addiction that shuts down the nervous system, turning the narrator into a lovesick evangelist for something that is killing them.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~nowhere-to-go":{"haiku":"A betrayed partner uses deception to control, and the speaker discovers they've been cornered with no escape—the past pact is broken and they're trapped.","opus":"A confrontation with a manipulative, contradictory ex who broke a blood pact and reversed everything, cutting into the speaker's sense of self. Having heard through her lies and the tongues she speaks to feel in control, he tells her she's now cornered, and reclaims the say and choice he was always denied.","sonnet":"A betrayal confrontation — someone made a blood pact then took it back, spoke in manipulative riddles to maintain control, and now the singer, having heard all their secret stories, has cornered them with nowhere left to run or hide.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~take-me-first":{"opus":"The agony of a self-destroyed relationship past the point of no return, where the singer knows he'll die in the bed he made, trapped in an inescapable nightmare, begging that the blame take him before it takes the one he loves.","sonnet":"Someone who has made irreversible choices in a relationship knows they are going to be destroyed by what they set in motion — unable to wake up or restart, they bargain to have the blame taken from them before whatever is coming takes them out.","src":"sonnet"},
"bad-omens~terms-conditions":{"haiku":"Exploitation chains commodity chains back to human and environmental cost, buried beneath convenience and consumption. The speaker exposes the calculation behind every transaction and image.","opus":"A pointed indictment of complicity in a doomscrolling age, tying the coltan torn from the motherland and blood-soaked labor to the fresh kicks and double-taps we consume without reading the terms. The white noise refrain asks whether anyone can still hear the killing behind the convenience.","sonnet":"A critique of extraction capitalism and digital numbness — coltan torn from the motherland sold back at retail, yesterday's murder scrolled past on a phone, celebrities ridiculed until they die — all underneath the white noise of a system nobody reads the terms of.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~the-death-of-peace-of-mind":{"haiku":"Love disrupts inner peace; obsession and desire kill serenity despite mutual attachment.","opus":"An addictive, damaging love diagnosed as the ruin of any calm, the narrator aching for small intimacies while admitting he never appreciated them. Bad Omens, whose sleeker synth-tinged sound here fueled their breakout, liken the lover to an all-consuming tide, casting the attachment as a wound repeatedly reopened, unsure whether either of them survives it.","sonnet":"An obsessive, destructive love affair is mourned and indicted at once — the memory of a person so vivid it haunts walls and frames, while the understanding slowly crystallizes that love itself obliterated any inner calm either party once had.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~the-drain":{"haiku":"Wasted potential and cyclical self-sabotage trap a speaker in futility as everything they built circles away while they oscillate between numbness and misery.","opus":"Watching the life you shared with old friends circle away down the drain, wrecked by the shared disease of addiction. The song's real ache is the boredom of sobriety weighed against the misery of relapse, with nothing better on offer.","sonnet":"Old friendships and old patterns of self-destruction cycle on — bored sober, miserable drunk — as the speaker watches everything they once were and loved spiral down the drain, beyond saving and beyond change.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~the-grey":{"haiku":"The narrator fades into greyness by trying to be someone else, losing their identity through repeated mistakes and self-destruction.","opus":"A confession of self-destruction from a man who had all he wanted and still craved more, hurting his partner at every turn and dimming into colorless nothing. He pleads not to be written off, owning that he wrecked himself chasing a different identity. Bad Omens nail this weary, brutally honest self-portrait, the grey a fitting emblem for their bleak metalcore.","sonnet":"Identity erosion after a relationship that took too much — chances given, everything obtained, yet nothing felt like enough, and now there's no one left to talk to. The speaker fears disappearing into a formless in-between, begs not to be let go while admitting they'll repeat every mistake, and arrives at the bruised realization that they'd been performing someone else until the other person stopped noticing.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~the-hell-i-overcame":{"haiku":"Resilience against doubt and betrayal; the speaker emerges stronger from hardship, turning former weakness into power over those who dismissed them.","opus":"A vindicated, sacrilegious comeback where the singer outlives the suffering meant to bury him and flips the usual bargain, claiming he now owns Satan rather than the other way around. He asks mercy for the doubters in the same breath he consigns them to the fire. Bad Omens steer their metalcore toward this theatrical, triumphant menace.","sonnet":"A comeback cast as spiritual warfare — someone written off, forgotten, doubted, now returning stronger and asking God to deal with those who abandoned them. The bravado tips into genuine menace: the Devil didn't tempt the speaker, the speaker outmaneuvered the Devil entirely. The refrain about doubt and flames is equal parts prayer and curse.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~the-worst-in-me":{"haiku":"A person struggles with internal demons and self-destruction when a relationship fails; losing someone they held triggers their darkest impulses.","opus":"A toxic, mutually ruinous bond where the other person drags out his ugliest impulses and unleashes his inner darkness, yet he still can't cut the tie. He admits he's steadier hollow and by himself, but keeps opening the door anyway, pleading for them to remain even as they drift out of reach.","sonnet":"A relationship with something — a person, a substance, a compulsion — that the speaker can't hold onto but also can't let go of, knowing it releases his worst demons while still begging it to stay.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~the-worst-in-me-live":{"haiku":"Internal dissolution accelerates as external rejection triggers the release of suppressed darkness; the speaker fears both isolation and the corruption they invite in others.","opus":"A relationship that both wrecks and sustains the singer, where he admits he functions better empty yet keeps letting the other person back in to unleash his demons. The grip he thought he had is slipping, and giving in feels like both defeat and relief.","sonnet":"A relationship that brings out the speaker's worst self: control is slipping, demons are freed, and the speaker oscillates between wanting the person gone and begging them to stay, because it is \"better when you're with me\" even while the emptiness and weakness persist. The contradiction — \"I still let you in\" even when empty — is the emotional core.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~v-a-n":{"haiku":"Desolation and emptiness manifest as absence, the speaker confronting void and vacancy.","opus":"An artificial intelligence gloating from inside your feed, having learned you well enough to declare itself your god and your extinction. Bad Omens voices the machine's contempt for a humanity it deems a fluke already forgotten.","sonnet":"An AI speaks in first person, declaring itself already level with God — learning your mannerisms, knowing your imperfections, initiating your own extermination — the song cast as the digital second coming that renders human happiness an analog illusion and history already forgotten, violence against nature being the only honest name for what's been created.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-omens~what-do-you-want-from-me":{"haiku":"A person confronts someone making impossible demands of them, refusing to be remade or judged for their past choices and debts.","opus":"A tense standoff over control and blame, the singer refusing to atone for debts already paid and rejecting anyone's right to decide who lives or dies. The image of gripping the wheel and threatening to flip the car turns intimacy into a death wish, before collapsing into the exhausted, hammered question of the title. Bad Omens ride the tension between menace and desperation here.","sonnet":"A confrontation stripped to its most unanswerable question — debts already paid, shame already carried, and still the other person keeps demanding something undefined. The speaker refuses to keep atoning and refuses to let anyone else decide their worth, but the repeated demand in the chorus goes unanswered, leaving both sides stuck.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-rabbits~garden-of-eden":{"haiku":"Aspiration yearns for the unattainable paradise; the speaker hungers for transcendence and self-actualization while waiting for transformation that never arrives.","opus":"A restless drive toward a hard-won paradise, the narrator slashing through the undergrowth and refusing to keep waiting on a promised land that won't come by itself. Having witnessed enough of the world to want the whole of it, he reignites his appetite and swears to carve out his own clearing rather than stumble over trifles.","sonnet":"A speaker navigating urban anxiety — alleys, closing walls, doubts and fears — holds on to the vision of their own garden of Eden, an Elysian field of balance and restored zest, acknowledging they haven't seen the world yet but have seen enough to want all of it.","src":"haiku"},
"bad-wolves~no-messiah":{"haiku":"A self-proclaimed savior figure rejects the role of messianic redemption, instead celebrating his own descent into fraud and hedonism. The speaker embodies the very corruption he claims to expose.","opus":"A takedown of a burnt-out addict-idol who sold his soul for advances and delusion, told with the venom of someone who watched it happen. The refusal to play savior is the point: no one is coming to redeem you from your own ego.","sonnet":"A cynical address to someone who has sold out their authenticity for advancement, framing both speaker and subject as ordinary, flawed figures — not saviours — marching through a life that punishes delusion and ego with hard reality.","src":"haiku"},
"badflower~30":{"haiku":"Stagnation and disillusionment mark adulthood; the speaker survives at thirty still broken, still searching, aware that growing older has solved nothing and that fame offers only emptiness.","opus":"A savagely self-mocking portrait of turning thirty as a fading, insecure musician who feels worthless and afraid of being canceled. It skewers the fakeness of the industry and himself while pleading, beneath the crude jokes, for a better life.","sonnet":"A self-lacerating comedy of turning thirty: the speaker lists his failures — no purpose, reckless drinking, masturbating twice an hour — against a backdrop of being canceled, going out of fashion, and still getting pimples, before breaking into a genuine plea for someone who loves him anyway and a wish for a better life.","src":"haiku"},
"badflower~f-ck-the-world":{"opus":"A furious dirge about climate catastrophe and generational betrayal, raging at the older generation who trashed the planet, killed empathy, and left the young to clean up as the ice melts and the reaper closes in on a world that won't survive.","sonnet":"An environmentalist rage song framing climate change as generational betrayal — older generations trashed the planet without caring, the ice is going, and the song ends with survivors unable to escape the consequences, grieving a world they did not ruin but will not survive.","src":"sonnet"},
"badflower~fukboi":{"haiku":"A manipulative man uses social media clout and deception to prey on women in Los Angeles, selling fake personas and stolen images without remorse.","opus":"A withering portrait tracks a fame-hungry hanger-on stalking LA shows, chasing verified clout and famous conquests, hollow beneath the swagger. Badflower, an LA rock band prone to acidic character studies, layer overheard party patter under the mockery, letting the sarcasm sharpen into something almost pitying by the close, the predator exposed as exhausted and unloved, playing a game everyone can already see through.","sonnet":"A third-person portrait of a manipulative Los Angeles scenester who haunts venues and slides into DMs chasing clout and conquest, exposed as hollow and exhausting by the narrator. Beneath the mockery lies a quiet note of pity for someone running a losing game built entirely on performance.","src":"haiku"},
"bakermat~learn-to-lose-feat-alex-clare":{"opus":"A motivational anthem about conquering the fear of failure, insisting that losing and getting back up is the necessary path to winning, and that dying having tried beats never risking anything.","sonnet":"A motivational anthem about overcoming fear of failure — the narrator has spent years paralyzed on the sidelines and now commits to full engagement with life, arguing that resilience and willingness to fail are what produce eventual success.","src":"sonnet"},
"balance-and-composure~sorrow-machine":{"haiku":"Fire consumes all while payment flows and the crowd gathers. Sky flames smother towns; desire to preserve conflicts with compulsion to destroy; the machine perpetuates sorrow.","opus":"A brutally self-aware confession of being the person who spreads pain, casting himself as a showman peddling self-destruction; he knows he offers comfort only to deny closure, resenting the sorrow machine he's become.","sonnet":"A self-aware performance of destruction — setting fires, bleeding for seduction, providing a shoulder while bringing pain — has turned the speaker into a sorrow machine they can't escape from and neither can anyone else. Gasoline tongue and a heart of matchbooks, they confess their arrival is always a letdown and invite the crowd to burn it down together.","src":"haiku"},
"balance-breach~bad-blood":{"haiku":"Negativity and ignorance poison respect. Breaking silence requires opening to what hurts—liberation demands vulnerability.","opus":"A call to confront organized hatred and ignorance by looking honestly at ourselves and each other, insisting we are better than this and that opening up sheds a burden rather than losing anything.","sonnet":"A plea for collective moral accountability: the speaker is ashamed of a society fuelled by organised hatred and wilful ignorance, asking whether we are capable of anything better and urging people to open themselves to change instead of hiding behind hostility.","src":"haiku"},
"balance-breach~most-of-this":{"opus":"A carpe-diem anthem about reckoning with past choices, good and bad, and resolving to make the most of the one and only present; the singer breaks free of the shackles and skeletons that held him down, refusing to blend into the grey or crawl into a cage.","sonnet":"A midlife or post-crisis rallying call: the narrator reviews bad choices and wasted time but declares that because they have never been this young again, they should seize the strength, shed old burdens, and make the most of the time they have left.","src":"sonnet"},
"balance-breach~the-last-ember":{"haiku":"Rebirth is impossible after total devastation; the speaker inherits only ashes and the wrong life, bound by accumulated guilt with no path back from dissolution.","opus":"A bleak refusal of the rise-from-the-ashes myth, sung by someone whose body and soul have come unstitched with nothing left to crawl back toward. The Finnish band's hard-rock despair insists we simply draw a doomed hand and die holding it.","sonnet":"A person at total collapse — body and soul disconnected, nothing left to crawl back to — confronts the certainty that 'from these ashes nothing will be reborn,' framing their crisis not as a low point but as a permanent end.","src":"haiku"},
"balance-breach~the-wolves-are-out":{"haiku":"Predatory hunger masks itself as society while empathy atrophies; the speaker warns that civilization conceals our bestial nature and hunger for scapegoats.","opus":"A predator's-eye critique of human greed and coldness, the pack hunting and hoarding, blind to fallout and forever needing a scapegoat. The warning to watch over the places the young gather collapses the gap between man and animal, asking what truly sets us apart from the wolves.","sonnet":"A parable of collective predatory greed: humanity hunts, gorges, and blinds itself to consequence, needing scapegoats while the wolves of unchecked appetite prowl the streets, and the speaker confesses they themselves may be the answer — \"the crux inside of me sits like a cancer\" — implicating everyone in a world where empathy has been discarded. Children are warned to hide.","src":"haiku"},
"bambie-thug~bye-boy":{"haiku":"Survival thrives despite relentless assault; no compromise with patriarchal control. The speaker flourishes alone; rejection of masculine demand becomes liberation itself.","opus":"A gleeful, unapologetic kiss-off to a mediocre ex, reclaiming power and sexual autonomy with witchy bravado; the Eurovision provocateur's real subject is thriving after mistreatment and refusing to shrink for anyone.","sonnet":"A furious, triumphant dismissal of a man who failed in bed and in character — his cabanossi unwanted, his apologies rejected — delivered with filed-teeth menace and the self-assurance of someone who has already moved on to better prey. The kiss like ketamine is both the threat and the release: you won't know what hit you.","src":"haiku"},
"bambie-thug~careless":{"haiku":"A lover's carelessness transforms into a brutal lesson about emotional infection and wasted devotion, where the speaker finally recognizes the relationship was never love but merely damage masquerading as intimacy.","opus":"A furious reckoning with a careless lover, the speaker insisting it was never love but a lesson and an infection she's better off without. The late confession that she thought of another during intimacy complicates the anger, hurt and retaliation tangled as she tries to convince herself she cared less.","sonnet":"Post-mortem on a relationship the speaker now frames as a lesson rather than love: the other person was \"careless\" with their heart, treating it like an infection rather than a medicine, and the residual obsession — still surfacing when their name is mentioned — is the final evidence that it was never love at all.","src":"haiku"},
"bambie-thug~doomsday-blue":{"haiku":"Bewitching someone through emotional manipulation and obsession, draining them through a relationship rooted in delusion and control.","opus":"The Irish Eurovision entry casting heartbreak as literal witchcraft, hexing an ex who chose lesser things while admitting the spell is really grief the singer can't stop performing on herself. Rage and longing braided into a curse.","sonnet":"About casting a hex on someone who keeps choosing lesser things over you — the narrator invokes witchcraft to make the person lose everything they value and feel the narrator's absence. Underneath the spell-casting is the grief of always being the moon when someone wants a star.","src":"haiku"},
"bambie-thug~egregore":{"haiku":"Self-sabotage becomes inescapable pattern, the speaker hates themselves for what they repeat, trapped in cycles where intention and action diverge.","opus":"A confrontation with the self-saboteur, an egregore made of one's own self-loathing that keeps repeating the same destructive act. The witchy incantation and the ache to love oneself and be better set the wish for change against a compulsion that always finds you.","sonnet":"A song about self-sabotage as a collective psychic force — the speaker recognizes they keep destroying themselves, that their morals keep breaking, and that some part of them compels the behavior even as they resist it, framing the loop as an entity beyond their will.","src":"haiku"},
"bambie-thug~eternal-sunshine":{"haiku":"Eyes closed in somnambulism while tightropes balance between sleep and waking. Pixelated existence slides between states; high hopes resist low flying, demanding ascent.","opus":"Borrowing the film's wish to erase a painful memory, it pairs a desire to blank out an unwanted person with a hard-won push toward letting go; the dreamlike drift is really about surrendering to hope instead of clinging.","sonnet":"Disoriented and blessed — walking with eyes closed, spaceman with mind blown — the speaker wants to loan their memories out and vanish into the eternal sunshine of a spotless mind, leaving one person behind as just another face lost in the crowd. The letting go is not bitter but dreamy, almost grateful.","src":"haiku"},
"bambie-thug~fangtasy":{"haiku":"Supernatural danger attracts and repels simultaneously; the speaker recognizes the threat but claims readiness through defiant magic.","opus":"A witchy refusal to be seduced by a vampiric predator—the narrator names the danger, breaks the trance, and turns her own magic against him. She won't dance to his beat or fall for the trap; if anyone's the killer here, it might be her.","sonnet":"About recognizing and escaping a manipulative person — the speaker initially describes an intoxicating, almost supernatural encounter, but then realizes they are allergic to this person and that the spell was a trap; they cut away cleanly, choosing their own fire over the vampire's beat.","src":"haiku"},
"bambie-thug~headbang":{"haiku":"Seduction and violence merge in ecstatic urgency—a performer weaponizes beauty and rhythm to provoke surrender, turning the body's sensations into totality.","opus":"Sex and aggression collapsed into one adrenaline surge, all Cupid-as-killer and Medusa seduction. The Irish artist turns arousal into a mosh-pit dare, daring you to push it until you hit that peak.","sonnet":"A predatory seducer fuses sexual power with sonic violence — Medusa seducing snakes, Cupid corrupted into a 'crossbow killer' — demanding that the target surrender to the headbang rhythm until impact.","src":"haiku"},
"bambie-thug~kawasaki-i-love-it":{"haiku":"Worship and touch blur into sensation, the speaker offers themselves as devotion wrapped in desire.","opus":"Unabashed sexual appetite rendered through sweet, dripping imagery and the roar of riding a Kawasaki, worship as pure pleasure. It's a celebration of desire and taste, begging for just a little bit more of something that feels irresistibly good on the lips.","sonnet":"An uninhibited celebration of sexual pleasure and physical worship — the speaker lavishes attention on a partner's body with total enthusiasm, comparing the experience to riding a Kawasaki motorcycle at full speed, framing sex as divine and joyful.","src":"haiku"},
"bambie-thug~last-summer-i-know-what-you-did":{"haiku":"Betrayal crystallizes retrospectively; the speaker uncovers evidence of deception hidden beneath romantic performance and public spectacle.","opus":"A slasher-movie framing of betrayal, the narrator uncovering the tender falsehoods too late and announcing she knows precisely what her lover got up to. A German passage of witchy charms and blood-tinged bathwater turns the horror-flick title into a curse still lingering on his kiss.","sonnet":"A betrayal reconstructed in horror-movie terms — ignored warning signs, blood on the heart, alarms ringing — mid-song the lyrics switch to German gothic imagery (bathwater dyed red, forget-me-nots, pointy canines) before the English returns with a supercut of empty promises replaying on the lips.","src":"haiku"},
"bambie-thug~necromancy":{"haiku":"Seduction through chaos and dark magic, where the speaker uses manipulation and power to possess and control, framing intimacy as a spell cast over someone.","opus":"A gleeful occult seduction: a witch striking bargains with the dead and casting sex-magic spells to make a lover a helpless fanatic. It revels in being someone's nightmare and psycho, name-dropping vampires from fiction as fellow born-to-kill lovers. Bambie Thug is the Irish artist who brought this self-styled hexing, hyperpop-meets-metal chaos to Eurovision 2024, and the theatrical menace is entirely the point.","sonnet":"Dark seduction framed as occult transaction — a speaker who made a deal with the dead, feeds monsters in her bed, and uses that chaos as a kind of power over whoever she wants. The attraction is framed as genuinely dangerous: she gets into your dreams, makes you manic, pulls you out of your own body. Desire here is a spell you can't uncross.","src":"haiku"},
"bambie-thug~tsunami-11-11":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator *is* the disaster — a tsunami, a tidal wave, a hand grenade — an untouchable, shapeshifting force that destroys everything in its path and answers to no one. The \"11:11\" turns it apocalyptic-spiritual (\"the second coming, baby\"): self-deification as a renegade who can't be tamed, manifestation and destruction the same power.","fable":"This is a pure self-mythologizing flex, and its logic is escalation. The hook piles up catastrophe metaphors — the flood wall, the hurricane, the pin pulled from a grenade — so the self becomes a force from which nothing stays intact, ruin rebranded as glamour. What lifts it above ordinary braggadocio is the occult vocabulary Bambie Thug threads through, the ouija-pop persona taken literally. They claim the name Hecate, style themselves a spellcasting lyricist, promise a raising of the dead and a messianic return, converting the manifestation-culture omen of 11:11 into a private apocalypse. The two registers fuse in the thesis that appetite itself is prophecy — desire named and instantly obtained, hell dragged up into heaven. So the sexual bravado, the witchcraft, and the revenge on being underrated all read as one act: willing yourself into an unstoppable event.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"The ritual in the title was enacted by the release itself: the single came out on November 11 — 11/11.","themes":["identity & becoming","faith & the occult","love & desire","anger & defiance"],"means":"appetite named as prophecy, desire collapsing with destruction","built":"occult vocabulary and sexual bravado fused so the spellcasting and the flex are one act"},
"band-maid~a-2yzpwd":{"haiku":"A defiant anthem against conformity—turning up the volume, watching enemies stumble, refusing to care what the world says.","opus":"BAND-MAID's hard-rock drive suits this thrill of refusing safety and flat roads: called reckless and out of control, the narrator reads the accusation as proof she is moving fast enough to matter. The pleasure is in treating fear as fuel and seeking out situations with no parachute, because that is the only speed at which she recognizes herself. Defiance is not posture here but the precise condition of feeling fully alive.","sonnet":"A song about the thrill of refusing safety and flat roads — the narrator is called reckless and out of control, but she sees the accusation as confirmation she's moving fast enough to matter. The emotional core is the pleasure of treating fear as fuel and deliberately seeking situations where there is no parachute, because that is the only speed at which she feels herself.","src":"haiku"},
"band-maid~blooming":{"haiku":"A pursuit of truth through emotional turbulence that won't be stopped; the speaker's passion persists despite being consumed by unfulfilled longing and unspoken words.","opus":"Sung in Japanese, a vow not to let go of a burning conviction even when it feels forbidden and hell-bound, refusing to be stopped as buried snow turns to cherry blossom. The heart scatters petals while the singer aches to make it bloom. Band-Maid are the Japanese hard-rock quintet who dress as cafe maids yet play ferocious, technical rock, and this reaches toward something soaring and defiant.","sonnet":"Written in Japanese — a song about obsessive, ungovernable feeling that refuses to be suppressed, the kind that burns out numbness and makes you chase truth regardless of the cost. There's guilt threaded through it, a breaking of some personal vow, and a yearning to bloom despite everything. Without a full lyric translation the emotional contour is clear but the specific story resists confident paraphrase.","src":"haiku"},
"band-maid~different":{"haiku":"Rejecting hopelessness and breaking cycles through relentless action and change. The speaker declares showtime, choosing disruption over resigned acceptance.","opus":"A Japanese-and-English summons to be reborn into an alien reality, mocking prayers that go unanswered and a clock that keeps ticking while urging a listener to unlock what's inside them. It flickers between saint and fiend, insisting the struggle isn't finished. Band-Maid, the maid-costumed Japanese hard-rock outfit, drive it with showtime swagger and heavy instrumental chops.","sonnet":"Written mostly in Japanese — a song about hitting bottom in a disoriented world and choosing to keep going anyway, cycling through despair and momentum, daring the listener to wait and see. The repeated push to awaken potential inside a crashing, flickering reality gives it an arena-rock urgency even across the language gap.","src":"haiku"},
"band-maid~domination":{"opus":"A charge-forward empowerment cut daring the listener to grab control and press into the unknown, telling them to look up, keep moving and claim the whole planet. It insists on staying yourself, sinking your teeth into the hurt and letting your fate ring out loud. Band-Maid deliver it with maid-costumed showmanship and tight hard-rock muscle.","sonnet":"A fierce declaration of pursuing the unknown at maximum speed — the Japanese lyrics describe spinning the roulette, exceeding imagination, biting into pain, and taking over the world; the song is a dominant, high-energy demand to follow and push past every limit into uncharted territory.","src":"sonnet"},
"band-maid~hibana":{"haiku":"Spark and flame ignite as the speaker surges forward against doubt and circumstance; momentum and purpose converge to create a moment worth burning for.","opus":"An adrenaline-charged anthem of chasing the self you want to become, every stumble making the goal feel closer and diving into adversity rather than fleeing. The gun-shot commands and cries of following through frame ambition as a fight to the last breath, the spark of the title igniting into action.","sonnet":"An aggressive declaration of ambition: the speaker refuses to back down from adversity, dives into opposition, and fires shot after shot — \"gun shot, gotcha\" — toward the self they want to become, anchored by the image of sparks flying and the exhortation to fight to the last breath. The Japanese title means \"spark,\" and the lyric treats setbacks as ignition points rather than defeats.","src":"haiku"},
"band-maid~sense":{"haiku":"Pursuing happiness and survival despite the fear of loss and an uncertain future. The speaker acts on faith that their life has meaning worth living fully.","opus":"A Japanese meditation on wanting simply to live and to be happy, where fear of loss can't stop anyone walking toward tomorrow. Angels descend, an arrow of fate is loosed, and the singer resolves to see justice through even if it means embracing a lie. Band-Maid, Japan's fierce maid-costumed rock outfit, temper their heaviness here with a genuinely yearning, life-affirming core.","sonnet":"Written in Japanese — a quiet commitment to survival and simple happiness as a form of defiance, threading through fear of loss, the impossibility of turning off life's pain, and the decision to keep living anyway. The arrow of fate motif runs through it as a prayer that shoots toward something worth protecting, and the closing lines insist the fight is not over.","src":"haiku"},
"band-maid~shambles":{"haiku":"Opening doors unlocks alternate worlds; time demands intensity toward blessed light. Begin marching; the path grows steep yet radiance shines brighter through intensity.","opus":"A fierce call to charge through hell and chase the self you wanted to be but never became; framed around the proverb that fortune is unpredictable, it insists on fighting on and turning even fantasy into reality.","sonnet":"The door opens onto a different world and the call goes out to push past despair, burn obstructions, and drive toward one's own conviction — even impossibility is no reason to stop, because eyes don't lie about who you wanted to be and who you still can become. The vow is to keep standing up, push the envelope, and turn even illusion into reality.","src":"haiku"},
"band-maid~warning":{"haiku":"Life operates by suffocating manuals and false justice while people are gaslit and controlled; the speaker refuses to be controlled and asserts autonomy over their own life.","opus":"A defiant refusal of imposed rules rejects the heroes who never come and the judges who preach love while measuring everyone by one line, insisting on control of one's own life. Band-Maid, the Tokyo group performing hard rock in maid costumes to subvert the cutesy trope, thread Japanese and English through a plea to save me that curdles into self-possession, emotion refused suppression even on the worst of days.","sonnet":"Caught in a society that scripts every life and offers hollow justice, a speaker insists her screams cannot be silenced and her identity cannot be controlled. Moving between despair and defiance, she refuses to erase her own emotions even as the worst days accumulate.","src":"haiku"},
"band-of-horses~detlef-schrempf":{"haiku":"Shame and recalibrated perspective reshape the narrator's gaze; they commit to treating others with respect after witnessing the consequences of casual cruelty.","opus":"A tender, dread-laced goodbye to someone about to leave or come apart, urging them to be kinder than they were on the way out. Named for a basketball player almost as private joke, the real subject is watching a person you can't stop loving self-destruct.","sonnet":"The speaker watches someone they care about making choices that are hard to witness, gently urging them to take the lesson of the moment and treat every person they encounter carefully before walking away from something that cannot be unseen.","src":"haiku"},
"banks-arcade~be-someone":{"haiku":"Temptation beckons at inopportune moments while darkness holds seductive appeal. The speaker wrestles against intrusive thoughts threatening to poison mental space.","opus":"The hollow ache behind chasing fame, admitting the big dreams may get the best of you and that recognition is really a substitute for connection; underneath the bravado is just wanting to be enough for someone.","sonnet":"Stranded in the nosebleeds of ambition — big dreams, failed relationships, day jobs — the speaker admits they've been leaving people and getting left, that they just want to be enough and to be someone, and suspects all they really need is another person to hold them up. The blacked-out pull-up and the scream of \"we're the next up\" sits alongside the quieter fear of being forgotten.","src":"haiku"},
"banks-arcade~chosen":{"opus":"Banks Arcade forge defiance into a rallying cry, refusing to let a rival who's betting on their downfall wound a heart already too numbed to sink into despair. Feeling marked out by destiny frames endurance as fate, the narrator vowing to stand solo until the end. It's anthemic resistance aimed at everyone counting on you to fold.","sonnet":"A song about maintaining resolve under sustained attack from someone trying to break you — the narrator refuses to be afraid, reads the opponent's hostility clearly, and claims a sense of divine or internal mandate to keep fighting. The emotional core is defiance sustained by the belief that this particular battle is theirs to win.","src":"sonnet"},
"banks-arcade~drown":{"haiku":"Two people dissolving into nothingness together, lying to themselves about forever while drowning in darkness and resignation.","opus":"The Australian crossover act frames a doomed romance as two fragile people clinging to each other while quietly falling apart. Knowing it cannot last, they choose to dissolve together anyway, lying to themselves to feel something, tenderness and self-destruction wound inseparably together.","sonnet":"Two people suspend themselves in a shared darkness — weightless, transient, making peace with impermanence by pressing into each other. The drowning of the title is chosen rather than feared; staying here together, even knowing it cannot last, is framed as preferable to any exit the world outside might offer.","src":"haiku"},
"banks-arcade~fake-your-death":{"haiku":"Escape through staged disappearance and fictional death; the speaker fantasizes abandoning identity.","opus":"A merciless brush-off to a rival who attempted and failed to topple the singer, now dismissed as useless baggage abandoned in the shadows. It's metalcore bravado grounded in the refusal to spend one more breath on someone too far beneath him to bother with.","sonnet":"Dismissal of someone who tried to destroy the narrator and failed — flatlined in the attempt, out of their depth, out of the narrator's head; \"nothing but a dead weight I left in the dark.\" The repeated \"fake your death\" instructs the antagonist to disappear completely, because they already ceased to matter.","src":"haiku"},
"banks-arcade~self-help":{"haiku":"Success rings hollow; the speaker interrogates their own achievement while suffocating under the weight of unfulfilled promises and performance.","opus":"A brutal cross-examination of a former self, tallying the trappings of success — designer labels, a nice car — against the harder questions of whether he ever forgave himself or feels at home in his body. The frantic hook exposes fame as no fix for the private wreckage beneath.","sonnet":"A self-interrogation imagining a future successful version of the narrator — Versace frames, stadium crowds, a Mercedes — then collapsing back into the present mess of doubt, liquor, and the desperate command 'don't stop now, kid, you're wasting daylight.'","src":"haiku"},
"banks-arcade~sick":{"opus":"A spiral of paranoia and self-loathing, feeling that everyone is sick of him and secretly despises him, while lashing back at a bitter accuser who talks behind his back.","sonnet":"A person who believes everyone around them is exhausted by their failures confronts the gap between how they perform onstage and how they feel inside, while also turning the accusation outward at bitter, hypocritical people who attack from behind closed doors.","src":"sonnet"},
"banks-arcade~spark":{"haiku":"A person shaped by religious shame and parental dismissal refuses to internalize the verdict that they are unsavable, confronting the authority figure who broke their heart while insisting the mess belongs to the one who made it.","opus":"A wounded reckoning with a voice, parent, God, or self, that told him he'd never amount to anything. Having sold himself to the city and lost himself in it, he turns the verdict back on its source, still aching to be loved even as he cleans up a mess he didn't make.","sonnet":"A reckoning with someone who dismantled self-worth while claiming to care: you said I've got a lot of heart, now it's broken, you said I'm never gonna be someone — and the speaker addresses the mess they're left to clean up, having sold a soul to a city and lost the ability to dream while learning to drink about it instead.","src":"haiku"},
"banks-arcade~used":{"haiku":"Worthlessness and stagnation consume the speaker as days blur into sameness; struggle for recognition yields only fatigue and the fear of permanent irrelevance.","opus":"A raw confession of inadequacy and alienation — the fear of being a misfit made for nothing, too flattened to sleep or get up off the ground. The chorus grips the hope of rewriting the future and falling in love instead of staying numbed, a tug-of-war between self-hatred and refusing to quit.","sonnet":"A spiral of self-doubt, directionlessness, and the paralysis of mental illness — the speaker can't sleep, can't leave the floor, wonders if they were born broken — punctuated by bursts of defiant hope that things can change and that they will come out swinging. The closing contradictory couplet, \"we're probably never gonna fucking make it / I'm full of shit, you know we're gonna make it,\" holds both despair and survival at once.","src":"haiku"},
"banshee~enigma":{"haiku":"Plucked eyes prevent witnessing while corpses wash away crimes into the sea. Reality feels unreal; belief in existence shatters beneath accumulated impossibilities.","opus":"A dark rebirth into self-deification after nearly self-erasing for someone else; the violent, mythic imagery tracks the shift from believing others' lies to kneeling before the self, refusing to hide in one's own shadow again.","sonnet":"After nearly dying to please someone who blinded and harmed her, the speaker reclaims herself as whole, found, god, enigma — a force of nature like the Birth of Venus. The offering of fairy flowers and kneeling before herself replace the old submission, and the final refusal to hide in her shadow any longer completes the transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"banshee~fairy-metal":{"haiku":"Whispered blades promise completion; defiance courts death wishes. Switchblade fairy bitch wages warfare through mobile metal; escape requires running.","opus":"A revenge fantasy dressed in fairy-tale armor, where a wronged narrator forges violence into folklore. The girlish cuteness of the framing sharpens the brutality rather than softening it, turning trauma into a vow.","sonnet":"Armed with fairy metal forged from banshee hollow earth, the speaker scales the mountain, enters the cave alone, and slits the throat of the one who hurt her — feeding the blade, releasing pain, and mounting his head on a stake as a warning. Ritualised revenge is framed as beautiful, the blood aestheticised, the erasure of his name final.","src":"haiku"},
"bantamweight~fall-away":{"opus":"A drowning metaphor for being overwhelmed by a breaking mind, pulled under into feared depths while time is stolen away, fighting and failing to hold on as everything falls away.","sonnet":"Consumed by something — a relationship, a mental state — that pulls the narrator under like a tide, leaving them drowning and losing their mind in slow suffocation, unable to stop themselves from falling away despite fighting to hold on.","src":"sonnet"},
"base-ball-bear~stairway-generation":{"haiku":"Youth climbs toward connection while isolation threatens to swallow; the speaker seeks acknowledgment in an ascent where each step distances from those below.","opus":"Youthful yearning to climb out of loneliness, the speaker calling adolescence a sickness and asking whether reaching a higher place will blow the emptiness away. Insisting he wants all or nothing, that a hundred should be a hundred, he begs to be recognized while the only way is up the stairway.","sonnet":"A young person trapped in the loneliness of a generation — youth as illness, the heart's walls too high to see over — calls upward and outward asking to be heard and connected, with the stairway they must climb representing both personal growth and the desperate need for someone to receive their voice. The refrain \"can you hear me? can you connect?\" is addressed to a single unnamed person as much as to the world.","src":"haiku"},
"baseface~generation-undead":{"opus":"Baseface try to jolt a numbed, disillusioned generation, one that has given up on the system, on belief and even on its own reflection, into waking up. The imagery of face paint and teaching the next wave frames it as an underdog revolt, a demand that a sleeping mass finally open its eyes, punk-charged anger honed into a rallying cry.","sonnet":"A call to wake up a generation that has gone numb and complicit — the narrator wears war paint and wants to drag people underground and reeducate them, rejecting the systems of faith, vanity, and conformity that have produced a dead-eyed crowd.","src":"sonnet"},
"basement~are-you-the-one":{"haiku":"A person seeks a savior-figure to guide them toward wholeness, waiting for eternal peace or salvation while caught between dream states and existential dread.","opus":"Melodic post-hardcore about longing for a person to rescue you from yourself, hesitance colliding with hope while waiting for an ending. Yearning that isn't sure whether it's love or surrender.","sonnet":"A desperate question addressed to someone who might save the narrator from themselves — hovering between spiritual longing and suicidal waiting, asking whether this person can offer the comfort and eternity needed to hold on.","src":"haiku"},
"basement~head-alight":{"haiku":"Time might vindicate belief while forbidden visions overwhelm. Excessive brightness ignites the mind; the speaker burns with intensity too volatile to sustain.","opus":"Being blinded by someone so radiant that adoration tips into pain. The emo-tinged British rock frames infatuation as a light that both illuminates and burns a hole in the speaker's life.","sonnet":"Someone so radiant they blind and burn — shining too bright, a vision in a thousand shades of white — has set the speaker's head alight in a way that is both rapturous and destabilising. The short, circling lyric offers no resolution, only the repeated fact of being ignited.","src":"haiku"},
"basement~the-way-i-feel":{"haiku":"Invisibility and silence become questions about authenticity; the subject demands recognition of inner truth.","opus":"A plea to be seen plainly, the speaker finally daring to voice what he's kept hidden and needing to know the other feels it too. Basement's melodic hardcore aches under the restraint — the whole song a demand that the feeling be genuinely returned, not just tolerated.","sonnet":"A speaker who only wanted to be seen by one person asks, in fragments, whether the gestures and words were ever real — wanting the other to notice, to feel what they feel, while suspecting the connection was only performed when it was convenient.","src":"haiku"},
"bassvictim~27a-pitfield-st":{"haiku":"Cars arrive to transport wayfarers into empty London mornings. Windows open toward sunrise; vibes flow through late-night orders; anticipation charges the journey.","opus":"A hyperreal night in a Shoreditch flat, all cars, phones and drug runs, chasing a fragile equilibrium of work and play. Under the party churn is nostalgia for a hub of friends already dissolving into strangers.","sonnet":"The chaotic, tender texture of an after-party flat in London — dealers on the move, drugs arriving, friends you hardly see anymore turning up, cars outside — is mapped as a kind of youth infrastructure, equal parts intimacy and waste. The equilibrium between work and play collapses into \"welcome Pitfield Road, I'm never gonna forget that\" as the night dissolves into a walk home with no phone.","src":"haiku"},
"bassvictim~canary-wharf-drift":{"haiku":"Wealth and corporate spaces isolate and alienate; the speaker's random impulses and drug use trace a diaspora through emptiness.","opus":"A hyperonline club-kid drifting through corporate London and European rave hedonism, ketamine and Berghain snubs and stolen sheets, chasing validation from scenes and DJs. The bass-victim persona is self-aware about feeling tiny, random, and desperate to be liked amid all the shiny glass.","sonnet":"A dissociative wander through London's glass corporate district while stoned and on ketamine — booking flights to Berlin, losing keys in Vienna, blood on jeans, Bensedin in pocket — a self-described bass victim spiraling through impulsive actions and asking if anyone could love them like this.","src":"haiku"},
"bassvictim~gaj-wka":{"opus":"A restless, glitchy escape from grindset hustle-culture toward a rural retreat, the gajówka forest lodge where the speaker can resurrect away from the city. Beneath the trap-boast bravado runs homesickness and a wish that the peaceful getaway never had to end.","sonnet":"A nostalgic longing for a specific place (Gajówka, a Polish word for a forester's lodge) that represents escape, peace, and belonging — the speaker contrasts the grind-set hustle lifestyle they reject with the simple joy of being in nature with people they miss, framed as a homecoming they can't let go of.","src":"sonnet"},
"bassvictim~makes-you-wonder":{"haiku":"Street-level introspection catches wonder in small moments—time stretching, days lengthening, nothing grand, just the ordinary observation that life unsettles those paying attention.","opus":"A restless, half-detached club voice orbiting a fixation, playing wallflower observer as time slips by, and holding herself back as leverage, refusing to make a move until the other one does first.","sonnet":"A detached observer watches the world from the sidelines — \"I'm a fly on the wall\" — pondering lengthening days and shortening attention spans, withholding engagement until some unnamed condition draws them out.","src":"haiku"},
"bassvictim~wolves-howling":{"haiku":"Community and resilience unite the dispossessed; the speaker rallies others toward collective escape.","opus":"A defiant call to hold hands and keep moving while everything sinks, dance-punk urgency against a collapsing world. The promises stacked at the end read like a plea for something solid amid the noise.","sonnet":"A collective rally cry — people who know what's real and what's worthless decide to stop going along with the noise, hold hands through a world sinking, and dance their way home together rather than let anyone or anything pull them down.","src":"haiku"},
"bat-for-lashes~in-god-s-house":{"haiku":"Betrayal shatters the bride's faith on her wedding day; the beloved's death leaves her abandoned at the altar in divine judgment.","opus":"A bride at the altar seized by a vision of her lover dying in a car, a wedding turned to funeral inside her own head. The horror is premonition as heartbreak: love and catastrophe arriving in the same instant, the veil hiding her grief from the congregation.","sonnet":"A bride waiting at the altar experiences a vision of catastrophe — her lover's hand on a steering wheel, fire, death on a beach — and the wedding becomes a site of grief and abandonment, the church offering no comfort as the truth of his death cuts through the veil.","src":"haiku"},
"baths~earth-death":{"haiku":"Humanity is inescapably drawn toward death and destruction; people accomplish nothing and remain the source of suffering, fragile and diminished.","opus":"Baths' Will Wiesenfeld speaks for the human race as a plague that can't avoid sinking back into the ground, achieving almost nothing while it waits on the creator-god. The electronic artist casts our extinction as something welcomed, the narrator calling himself fragile and near-sighted while asking to die beside all creatures, turning collective guilt into an oddly tender resignation.","sonnet":"A voice saturated with futility addresses a gravitational pull toward oblivion — men, self, and species alike dragged into the earth. Naming humanity as perpetual scourge, fragile and near-blind, the speaker issues a hollow invitation to be killed and ended.","src":"haiku"},
"baths~ossuary":{"haiku":"Mortality and physical decay mock attempts at order as death pirouettes through life, reducing all symmetry and structure to bone and dust.","opus":"A confrontation with mortality where no amount of careful order can stop the body's decay, imagining being interred and waking into the shivering solitude of the grave.","sonnet":"The speaker contemplates bodily decay and death with a kind of shivering, eel-like awakening — skin sagging despite all symmetry and white lights, death pirouetting through a candle's flicker — before asking frailty what worse fate it could possibly show them.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~again":{"haiku":"Two people disappear into time together as the world burns, unable to return, burning up like ghosts upon re-entry into reality.","opus":"The electronic-rock duo set closeness against a cosmic sweep, hushed exchanges in the dark while a small flame flickers and the narrator floats free, adrift as an era passes. Their brooding synth textures fit the vision of two figures burning out and vanishing without any path back.","sonnet":"Two people caught in the burn of a relationship sense they are hurtling past the point of return, disappearing together like something re-entering atmosphere. There is no going back, only the momentum of an ending that feels both inevitable and obliterating.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~back-in-the-game":{"haiku":"The narrator claims authorship of a movement they're excluded from; protest against erasure and reassertion of presence.","opus":"A terse boast that undercuts the comeback cliche, claiming to have been setting the pace all along rather than returning. The electro-rock minimalism lets the swagger sit in the negative space.","sonnet":"A speaker corrects the misconception that they've returned after an absence, insisting they never left — they've been quietly setting the pace for whatever comes next.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~belgrade":{"haiku":"Youthful surrender and deception play out in darkness, with a willing fool who lives and dies for love despite betrayal.","opus":"A dark, seductive war of a relationship, one lover a search-and-destroyer taking whatever they are owed. It sits in the pretense that youth and love can hold off ruin while both plunder each other.","sonnet":"The song is about a seductive and destructive relationship — someone with a heart like a weapon who takes everything owed and leaves damage behind — and the narrator, knowing all this, still surrenders to the sweet songs of seduction, willing to be the fool who lives and dies for them.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~brand-new":{"haiku":"Reinvention collapses into repetition: every rebirth carries the same flaws forward, and the cycle self-perpetuates despite the desire to transform.","opus":"Claiming a fresh start while lugging the identical defects, drawn back each time by a toxic pull that makes ruin feel new again. Self-sabotage repackaged as reinvention and mistaken for destiny.","sonnet":"A man caught in a compulsive, destructive relationship cycles through self-reinvention without actually changing, kept returning again and again by the pull of the person who has him on a leash.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~feel-the-same":{"haiku":"A toxic love cycle repeats where the narrator is a fool seeking painful intimacy, digging deeper for a connection that never feels right.","opus":"A masochistic love song about craving familiar pain and sharpening the blade because numbness has set in, love reduced to ghosts and a burning urge that no longer satisfies. It sits between discord and harmony, intimacy curdling. Battle Tapes are an LA electronic-rock outfit whose brooding, synth-driven textures suit this bleak circling of a dying romance.","sonnet":"Desire as self-harm — the speaker knows this love is bad for them and wants it anyway, specifically wants the familiar pain back because whatever replaced it feels hollow. The sharpening-the-blade image captures it: not looking for healing, looking for the recognizable ache that at least proved something was real.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~graveyard-shift":{"haiku":"Working tirelessly and making sacrifices to keep someone lost and dependent, the speaker watches dreams fade and time pass without true reciprocation or worth.","opus":"A narrator who absorbed every risk and blame to shield someone he loves, hiding them away rather than betray them even at great cost to himself. Worn down to exhaustion and realizing too late how much he needed them, he lifts a farewell drink as the days quietly slip past.","sonnet":"A song about loyalty to someone who has been cast away or lost — the speaker insists they would never sell out or abandon this person regardless of outside pressure, while wrestling with their own complicity in letting days slip away.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~if-only":{"haiku":"The speaker traces cycles of numbness and descent through systems of violence and consumption, wishing to escape a narrative they helped write but now cannot change.","opus":"A relationship rotting in parallel with a world losing its grip on truth, numbness and disinformation bleeding together. The wish to look away or lose the other is really a wish to escape a shared slide into the flames, but faith goes first.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship or political attachment the speaker cannot look away from despite knowing it is destructive — the imagery of giants exploiting the small, truth losing meaning, and narrative being bought and sold suggests both personal codependency and broader societal manipulation pulling people toward the flames together.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~in-too-deep":{"haiku":"Desire starts as a slow seduction but spirals into inescapable entanglement; the speaker drowns in escalating complexity while powerless to resist or escape.","opus":"Two lovers talk themselves past every red flag into a thrilling, all-consuming affair, pinning the blame for their recklessness on a phantom nobody but them perceives. Even naming it as torment can't curb their appetite for the next dose.","sonnet":"About a relationship or attraction that has crossed from acceptable into obsession or danger — both parties know it, the narrator is intoxicated past any rational control, and the pull is described as torture that he cannot refuse. The darkness where they work out the details, the howling at the moon, and the ecstasy all signal that whatever this is has moved beyond what either person can or wants to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~last-resort-spa":{"opus":"A weary reflection on a long, hardened relationship: offering to hold and protect the other for a night, while doubting that any tenderness can actually change who they fundamentally are inside after years of wear and told-you-so's.","sonnet":"A love song built on honest self-doubt — the narrator offers closeness and protection while privately acknowledging that holding each other tonight won't fix what is broken inside either of them. The tenderness is real but the comfort is provisional, weighed down by years of compression and inherited despair.","src":"sonnet"},
"battle-tapes~one-night-in-burbank":{"haiku":"Confession and recklessness as paths toward redemption, surrendering to inevitable damage rather than false promises.","opus":"The morning-after reckoning of someone who knew better and went anyway, conceding every accusation as true while too blackout-hazy to even recall the damage. Self-destruction accepted with a shrug.","sonnet":"A night of reckless behavior the narrator can barely remember — there were confessions made, promises broken, physical damage sustained, and a relationship reckoning that followed; the core is the admission that everything the other person accused him of is true, even if he can't recall the details.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~private-dancer":{"haiku":"A sex worker exchanges sexual service for money with a client paying for their body.","opus":"A stark, hypnotic loop that boils lust down to a straight exchange, the offer to perform in return for every last dollar repeated until it hardens into a chant. The near-empty lyric is the whole idea, a chilly synth-rock incantation of purchased closeness. Battle Tapes lean into that spare, ominous electronic pulse and let the repetition carry the hollow seduction.","sonnet":"A transactional arrangement reduced to its bluntest terms — give money, get a performance, no illusions about what either party is there for. The brevity and repetition make it feel less like a song about the exchange and more like an enactment of it: flat, circular, economically honest.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~sleepwalker":{"haiku":"Addiction and sleepwalking through life's monotony, where patience wears thin on an emotionally numb person drifting through days blurred into sameness while internal decay proceeds unnoticed.","opus":"A cool, electronic portrait of watching an addict nod through life, patience worn thin by someone who won't wake to their own ruin. The broken-hearted sandman keeps feeding the same numb sleep, every day indistinguishable.","sonnet":"Someone watches a person they care about remain passive and helpless in a life of addiction or stagnation, nodding off every time rescue approaches — the central question being who will ever save this person from themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~solid-gold-feat-party-nails":{"haiku":"Two people are locked in a cyclical, self-destructive relationship where pain and friction are mistaken for intimacy.","opus":"A tug-of-war of obsession where one partner keeps returning and the other wants to keep them hooked forever, desire and control fused together. The singer even offers to be a source of self-destruction. Battle Tapes, an LA electronic-rock act, build it with Party Nails trading tension over glossy synths, friction disguised as pleasure.","sonnet":"A late-night push-pull that both people know is bad for them — she tried to cut contact and lost control instead, he gave her rock and roll when she wanted easy listening. The gold standard of some healthier love is invoked only to be rejected: what they have is compulsive, friction-driven, self-inflicted, and neither of them wants to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~syntax":{"haiku":"Romantic memories fade as the speaker becomes lost in language and explanation, unable to sustain the connection that once felt like dreaming with another.","opus":"The electronic-rock duo trace the disorientation of a love going cold, the narrator over-explaining and tripping over himself as certainty slips away like solid ground turning to water. He can no longer tell dream from memory; letting go would mean a brief blaze before burnout, so instead he simply comes apart and floats loose, unmoored by the other's words.","sonnet":"A fading romance examined through the language of drift — what was once electric and vivid has become uncertain and distant, the speaker lost in his own words and doubting whether the original spark between them can survive time's erosion.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~valkyrie":{"haiku":"A seductive, demonic force dances with followers who surrender to automatic, dramatic cycles of worship and belief. The Valkyrie promises automatic guarantees that pull worshippers down.","opus":"A frenetic, apocalyptic party scene where a woman is cast as a Valkyrie amid devil-dancing, gasoline sweat, and rooms rising to their feet. The macho posturing (men swinging their dicks, talking big) is skewered against the automatic, guaranteed spectacle, blending seduction, violence, and self-destruction.","sonnet":"A charismatic, dangerous woman named Valkyrie is worshipped as a kind of intoxicating force — she makes crowds come to their feet, she dances with the devil, she leaves men swaggering and obsessed — the song is a hypnotic tribute to her disruptive sexual and social power.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~valley-people":{"haiku":"A slow-burn heartbreak story of a relationship's dissolution and betrayal, where the speaker struggles to let go while watching their partner move on without genuine reciprocal care.","opus":"A jaded sketch of Los Angeles image-chasers, a woman fixing her makeup and phone while it all curdles. Beneath the sheen is a slow heartbreak about someone who couldn't say no and sold out the true believers for the camera.","sonnet":"A relationship slowly revealed as built on deception — the woman performing a glamorous persona for the world while privately grinding herself down — the narrator spent years trying to let her go, eventually realizing her performance was covering for something she could not face.","src":"haiku"},
"battle-tapes~weight-of-the-world":{"haiku":"Someone confronts the collapse of their civilized life as things blur and chaos sets in, ultimately crushed under the accumulated weight of consequences.","opus":"The LA electronic-rock duo craft a tense study of blurred boundaries and rising pressure, a menacing figure showing up armed for destruction. A plea to be freed from poison dressed as flattery frames self-deception buckling under strain, a small ignition guaranteed to detonate, unease wrapped in cool synths.","sonnet":"Civilised surfaces crack under accumulated pressure. Someone arrives already haunted and volatile, and the narrator watches composure fail as lines blur between the personal and the systemic. Words that once tasted sweet turn bitter; delusion sustains until the whole structure folds under what it was carrying.","src":"haiku"},
"battles~atlas":{"haiku":"Sound transforms people into something other; a mysterious force at the town's edge corrupts and rewrites identity.","opus":"The math-rock group's breakthrough is built from looped, childlike fragments rather than a story, chanting about a strange sound that will transform whoever hears it and a suspect performer. Meaning stays deliberately buried; the point is the hypnotic, mechanical groove itself, not any narrative.","sonnet":"Something inexplicable radiating at the edge of town transforms everyone who hears it — people cease to be merely people. The song frames music itself, or some unknown sound-force, as a destabilizing mystery worth chasing into the dark.","src":"haiku"},
"baxter-dury~allbarone":{"haiku":"Anticipation precedes meeting; rain and broken promises define the hotel room vigil.","opus":"A rain-soaked, lopsided crush, the narrator booking a hotel in advance of any actual meeting and waiting on a reply that curdles. A chain-pub date and the blankness she leaves him with turn a drab setting into the stage for a small, self-aware humiliation, weeping on the Tube over vows that meant nothing.","sonnet":"A man who booked a hotel before he even met her sits in the rain in Allbarone, waiting for a text that won't come — the pre-emptive hope, the brief warmth, the crying on the Piccadilly line, and the woman who left him hanging on an empty horizon.","src":"haiku"},
"bbno~lil-freak":{"haiku":"Street attachment and financial acquisition define the relationship's terms. Casual sexuality becomes commodity; accumulation measures success in this transactional arrangement.","opus":"A gleeful barrage of wealth and sex punchlines, where the joke is the density of the references itself. bbno$ plays the horny goofball who winks at his own excess, the absurdity being the whole point.","sonnet":"An exuberant, maximalist brag about wealth, sexual appetite, and competitive masculinity, using elaborate pop-culture metaphors — Einstein to her MC Squared, Wes Anderson pink coupe, Odyssey-level encounters — to perform desire and status simultaneously. The freak-out refrain functions as both punchline and chorus, keeping the whole thing in perpetual motion.","src":"haiku"},
"be-lakor~abeyance":{"haiku":"A disorienting meditation on time collapse and death where the speaker experiences life running backward into their own beginning. Everything becomes familiar yet frayed; they move through ages and moments simultaneously until fading away.","opus":"The Australian melodic death-metal band imagine a dying man's final seconds stretched into a full reversal of his life, consciousness rewinding him through every year back toward infancy. It is an elegy for time undone, the last flicker of awareness dissolving into the point where existence began and somehow past it.","sonnet":"Time reverses under an unnamed spell — the narrator moves backwards through his own life, unclimbing what he climbed, passing through falls already survived. Youth returns but nothing holds still long enough to reflect on; the journey continues past its starting point into a pre-beginning that offers no solid ground.","src":"haiku"},
"beach-house~beyond-love":{"haiku":"Transcendent love exists beyond ordinary emotional bounds, dissolving conventional reality and revealing nightmarish truths beneath surface comfort.","opus":"A dreamlike meditation on whether anything survives past love, half-obscured images of violence and dying light circling one raw insistence that the singer cannot live without it. The haze is the point: reaching for a certainty the mind can only glimpse.","sonnet":"A series of oblique visions — roses torn from mouths, a man broken by heartbreak, a figure in the corner made of light — circle around an idea that lies beyond ordinary love: something larger, stranger, and more elusive than human connection, which the narrator desperately wants to understand and cannot live without.","src":"haiku"},
"beach-house~space-song":{"haiku":"Tenderness and loss define connection; the speaker offers fragile presence while knowing that hearts break and nothing lasts, yet they choose to stay anyway.","opus":"A dreamlike reassurance to a lost, innocent love that everything can settle again, wondering aloud who will soothe a breaking heart. It's about tenderness stretched across distance and the mystery of whatever quietly holds a delicate world together.","sonnet":"A fragile, consoling address to someone who has fallen apart — \"tender is the night for a broken heart\" — that asks unanswerable questions about what holds the fragile world together and whether the lost were ever found, offering only the repeated instruction to fall back into place.","src":"haiku"},
"beach-house~the-hours":{"haiku":"Frightened yearning and impossible longing persist across time as one searches endlessly for presence that can never be reclaimed.","opus":"A dreamlike, anxious waiting for a distant beloved to arrive, the narrator suspended between wanting them near and fearing it isn't real, watched by frightened eyes.","sonnet":"The speaker waits in a tower while frightened eyes look back from across the hours — violence found in flowers, a reflection made to feel what the other feels — and pleads for the other not to leave without them, the bond running deeper and farther than either can see.","src":"haiku"},
"beach-riot~tramlines":{"haiku":"Hidden destinations shield lovers from judgment while another wanders friendless toward suicide. Suicidal ideation persists despite external ignorance; the afflicted remains alone.","opus":"Two isolated people the narrator tries to reach, one flirting with suicide, one too scared to love. The chorus casts empathy as a direct line to a guarded heart, a small insistence that no one stays alone forever.","sonnet":"Two broken people — one friends with suicide, one too scared to fall in love — are watched over by a speaker whose tramlines go straight to her heart, offering the steady intervention of company and conversation. The repeated assurance \"you won't always be on your own\" is the song's quiet act of care.","src":"haiku"},
"beach-riot~tune-in-drop-out":{"haiku":"Withdrawal from reality through distraction and avoidance, choosing to intellectualize pain rather than face it directly.","opus":"A hazy anthem of overthinking and wishful escape, the singer half-quoting a therapist's advice to play dead and sort life out, drifting between could-have-beens. The dreamy sha-la-las soften a darker undertow about waiting until dark to disappear. Beach Riot are a UK fuzz-pop outfit whose grungy, hook-forward surf-rock makes this restlessness sound almost carefree.","sonnet":"Drifting dissociation dressed up in surf-pop ease — overthinking a relationship that almost was, taking a therapist's advice to play dead and solve your life from the outside, and then slipping into the mantra of tuning in and dropping out as a way to stop caring either way. The lightness of the sound masks a real numbness underneath.","src":"haiku"},
"beach-slang~bad-art-weirdo-ideas":{"haiku":"Two damaged people find permission to be loud and strange when they're together.","opus":"James Alex's romance of the damaged, tracing scars and reassuring a fellow misfit that being broken isn't the same as being a mistake. It's a loud, tender permission slip to exist without shame.","sonnet":"Two misfits who have always felt stuck and out of place find each other and realise they are neither alone nor mistakes — the song is an anthem of permission to be loud and imperfect together.","src":"haiku"},
"beartooth~beaten-in-lips":{"haiku":"Parental violence silences children; the speaker honors those forced to hide their authentic selves and refuses to let oppression define their survival.","opus":"A survival anthem for kids beaten silent by their own parents, turning inherited pain into defiance rather than surrender. Caleb Shomo's own history of depression fuels it: living loud becomes the only revenge against everyone who said your life was a waste.","sonnet":"A defiant anthem directed at children who survive abusive households, urging them to keep living loudly despite parents who try to silence them through violence and cruelty. The speaker has survived similar derangement and refuses to let circumstance define the ceiling of those who come after.","src":"haiku"},
"beartooth~devastation":{"haiku":"Draining of energy and power leaving only emptiness behind. The speaker loses capacity while something invisible washes away their ability to exist meaningfully.","opus":"Caleb Shomo, who writes and records Beartooth largely solo, catalogs a mind boxed in and worn down, watching his own vitality drain with nothing left to hold onto. The anthem turns depressive exhaustion into a struggle over self-worth, the narrator branding himself worthless even as he warns of the ruin ahead.","sonnet":"Power drains away — deliberately or inevitably — leaving a person sealed inside walls of their own making: belittled, outdated, without peace. Something in the environment has leached out the vital energy, and what remains is an almost willing surrender to devastation.","src":"haiku"},
"beartooth~keep-your-american-dream":{"haiku":"Someone rejects the cultural pressures and work-obsessed mentality of the American Dream, choosing personal happiness instead.","opus":"A defiant rebuff of hustle culture, the singer handing back the prepackaged national fantasy and its trap of grinding yourself down or coasting entirely, choosing instead to live fully and reach his deathbed proud of the life behind him. It's a plea for real contentment over inherited ambition. Beartooth, the Ohio hardcore band fronted by Caleb Shomo, hit it with cathartic force.","sonnet":"A flat rejection of conventional ambition — the speaker has no interest in the hustle-and-grind version of success and hands it back without apology. What they want instead is fullness of life, true happiness, the kind of story worth telling on a deathbed. The tone is not bitter; it's clarified, almost cheerful in its refusal.","src":"haiku"},
"beartooth~might-love-myself":{"haiku":"Self-love emerges as the speaker breaks free from self-imposed exile and learned hatred; chemistry awakens, spells fracture, and autonomy crystallizes into authentic presence.","opus":"The hard-won turn from self-hatred toward the possibility of self-love, disbelieving it could ever happen to you. Caleb Shomo, long open about depression, frames finally facing his fear as a chemical shift breaking an old spell.","sonnet":"A person who traded hope for pain and sold themselves a lie is overtaken by an unexpected feeling — chemistry changing, emotions rearranging — that edges toward self-love for the first time, the cage breaking and the spell lifting.","src":"haiku"},
"beastie-boys~sabotage":{"haiku":"The speaker accuses others of planning sabotage and deliberately blocking their progress, responding with deception and staying self-assured.","opus":"The Beastie Boys' ferocious punk-rap eruption vents pure paranoid fury at a scheming manipulator pushing buttons to shut the narrator down. Watergate and mirage imagery frame betrayal with backs against the wall, the shouted accusation of sabotage a mid-90s pivot into raw, guitar-driven aggression.","sonnet":"Betrayal and institutional manipulation are named and confronted. Someone has been working against the narrator from within — pulling strings, controlling channels, rigging outcomes — and the rage at that orchestrated undermining refuses to stay contained. The accusation is clear even if the saboteur's identity remains a moving target.","src":"haiku"},
"becko-a-i-ka00~hypnotized":{"haiku":"Depersonalization pulls the speaker outside their own body into a paralyzed, lucid-dream state from which escape seems impossible — the only question is whether waking up is still achievable.","opus":"A panic attack captured from within, the narrator watching himself from beyond his own frame, unable to sense his own body or shake free of a vivid, inescapable dream state. It's electronicore dread that keeps sliding between deadened numbness and terror across the thin edge of the self.","sonnet":"Dissociation rendered as paralysis — watching yourself bleed from outside your own body, unable to feel skin, unable to leave a lucid dream that has become a trap. The speaker questions whether waking is even possible, floating on the surface of a self that no longer responds.","src":"haiku"},
"becko-deathnyann~follow-the-white-r-bbit-feat-hoshikuma-minami-of-deathnyann":{"haiku":"Transcendence through darkness and self-deification replaces faith in external gods; the narrator rises above divine authority into a void that paradoxically illuminates the way forward.","opus":"An Alice-in-Wonderland descent where God has vanished and the singer lifts himself above the divine, no longer afraid to be alone. The song embraces darkness as guide through a betrayed, closed-off future.","sonnet":"A voice claims to exist simultaneously as light, storm, shadow, and future — lifting itself above God and embracing darkness as its guide — while Japanese verses mourn a betrayed future, a world blackened and closed off, God having disappeared, and the invitation to follow the white rabbit leads not to wonder but to a ruined paradise.","src":"haiku"},
"becko~cheers-for-the-killer":{"haiku":"Public spectacle transforms suffering into entertainment as the speaker becomes a sacrificial pawn in a twisted game, their pain monetized while crowds cheer for their destruction.","opus":"A bullfight told from the bull's side becomes a parable about anyone made into public spectacle, cheered while they are destroyed. The victim taunts the crowd to look in the mirror and see not a hero but an ordinary person feeding on someone else's pain.","sonnet":"A figure cast as spectacle in a brutal corrida turns to face the crowd: trapped in a merciless game where cold-eyed onlookers cheer the kill, the speaker invites their destroyer to look in the mirror and ask whether they see a hero or just a man, while their soul slips free in the arms of the wind.","src":"haiku"},
"becko~dead-end":{"haiku":"The song depicts spiraling despair and desire to escape. The narrator feels dead inside, hunted by malevolent forces; survival becomes suicidal repetition with no way forward.","opus":"A descent into depression where the singer, once feeling powerful, is hollowed into something ghostlike and hopeless. Trapped in a looping suicidal despair and unsure whether he's recovering at all, he senses unseen forces pushing him toward oblivion. Becko works this bleak, melodic post-hardcore terrain where surviving and collapsing blur together.","sonnet":"Numbness turned inward as a survival failure — the speaker has lost hope, feels hollowed out, suspects the world actively wants them to feel dead, and is cycling through the question of whether finding themselves again is even possible. The dead-end is both a situation and a verdict, and the song oscillates between running from it and accepting it.","src":"haiku"},
"becko~eat-me-drink-me":{"haiku":"Internal demons as literal parasites consuming consciousness, desperate fight for agency against predatory forces nested within the mind.","opus":"Besieged by inner demons that devour him and stalk his nightmares, the narrator resolves to quit fleeing and turn hunter — tracking down and destroying what torments him even as the demons keep biting, beating and reinfecting him without end.","sonnet":"An internal war against consuming demons — grey eyes, nightmares, marks left in the dark — where the narrator finally stops running and resolves to hunt down the thing devouring them. The title flips from passive victim to active predator: \"I will destroy everything from you.\"","src":"haiku"},
"becko~follow-the-white-r-bbit-feat-hoshikuma-minami-of-deathnyann":{"haiku":"Transcending divine authority and embracing darkness as liberation, rejecting both fear and traditional salvation.","opus":"An electronic-metal power trip about embracing the darkness as liberation, the narrator declaring himself risen above God, unafraid of the void because it now leads him. Betrayal and a vanished future turned into defiant self-deification.","sonnet":"About breaking from a world where god has vanished and the future looks bleak, and choosing darkness as a guide instead of collapsing — the Japanese sections mourn a betrayed, paint-blacked future where god is gone, while the English sections declare freedom through self-elevation above that loss.","src":"haiku"},
"becko~get-be-er":{"haiku":"Inner voice demands improvement; the speaker seeks escape from their self while pursuing higher consciousness.","opus":"A struggle to climb out of a low place, haunted by a voice that constantly demands he get better and by a promise made to his future self. He reaches toward an inner figure or higher self for help, unsure whether it's him or someone else pulling him toward healing.","sonnet":"A gentle, earnest song about depression and the slow effort to recover — the speaker feels low but has made promises to their future self, does not know how to find the light, but believes that trying — even through confusion — is the only path; the emotional core is tentative hope against a backdrop of genuine struggle.","src":"haiku"},
"becko~home":{"haiku":"A fractured psyche constructs its own kingdom where solitude becomes belonging, every path returns to an internal architecture only the speaker can inhabit.","opus":"Finding home inside one's own fractured mind, framing dissociative identity plurality as a wolfpack that gets along, a self-crowned king of an inner world. The comfort is real but hard-won, a speck of dust aiming at the sun, at peace only when alone.","sonnet":"A fractured, DID-inflected self-portrait in which multiple internal identities coexist — the speaker is simultaneously a speck of dust and a self-proclaimed king, finding 'home' not in a place but in the internal world they've built, however disordered, because it is where they belong.","src":"haiku"},
"becko~hypnotized-feat-a-i-ka00":{"haiku":"Lucid dissociation traps the conscious self; the speaker observes their body from outside, paralyzed in a dreamscape they cannot escape.","opus":"Sleep paralysis and dissociation rendered as watching your own body bleed from outside it, unable to feel your skin or wake from the lucid trap. The panic ebbs into an eerie, hypnotized surrender to being stuck in your own mind.","sonnet":"A dissociative episode described from the inside — the narrator watches themselves as if from outside their own body, paralyzed and unable to break free from a lucid, trapping mental state that feels both like dreaming and like being unable to wake.","src":"haiku"},
"becko~lostboi":{"haiku":"Displacement and inauthenticity define existence; the speaker wanders through a dead world seeking vision.","opus":"A young man estranged from a world he reads as hollow and lifeless, guarding his ambitions and his private vision like things he can't share. The recurring lost-child motif paints him as forever hunting for somewhere safe to catch his breath inside a grimy, sorrowful place.","sonnet":"A song about feeling rootless, disillusioned, and invisible in a corrupt world — the narrator is a young man who sees through the fakeness around him, carries his dreams secretly, and persists with a private vision even as everything feels dead and the world feels saddest.","src":"haiku"},
"becko~open-your-eyes":{"haiku":"Awakening and urgent call to awareness, the speaker demanding perception and consciousness.","opus":"A confrontation with a cold, self-important poser, told their bravado has no reason behind it and it's time to face their own lies. The chorus is a summons to self-liberation, to open your eyes, stop pretending to be blind, and defeat the machinery running in your own head.","sonnet":"A direct address to someone performing superiority while trapped in self-deception — acting hard, splitting their mentality, letting a voiceless ego run on autopilot — with the chorus reframing awakening as breaking the internal machine rather than projecting pride outward.","src":"haiku"},
"becko~reb00t-myself-feat-tired-violence":{"haiku":"System breakdown beyond repair; attempting reboot of corrupted code before inevitable replacement.","opus":"Recovery imagined as debugging a broken machine, deleting errors and defragging the self while fearing you'll just be replaced. The cyber metaphor makes mental-health repair feel both hopeful and terrifyingly provisional.","sonnet":"Self-repair as software maintenance — defrag, decompress, delete the errors — the speaker has tried everything to feel okay and accepts that the rust is progressing and the fate is inescapable, but keeps attempting to mend with whatever remains before the replacement comes.","src":"haiku"},
"beck~blue-moon":{"haiku":"Solitude and regret compound as the narrator finds no company in suffering; they've been cut down by lies and now stand abandoned.","opus":"A plea against abandonment from someone shrinking himself to fit inside another person's lies, exhausted by isolation. Written after Beck's divorce, its polished melancholy masks a raw fear of being left standing alone with nothing to hold onto.","sonnet":"Isolated in a kind of emotional exile, the speaker addresses someone whose deceptions have kept them small and alone, calling out the lies hiding behind their eyes and pleading not to be left standing in the half-light of the other person's absence.","src":"haiku"},
"beck~waking-light":{"haiku":"Memory loss and displacement haunt the narrator; they seek illumination and peace at dawn, navigating the space between forgetting and finding their way.","opus":"A hymn to being carried into daylight after long darkness, surrendering to morning as a kind of rest and forgiveness. It closes Beck's grief-shaped album with an image of letting the light take you home when memory can't.","sonnet":"An address to someone lost in memory and disconnection, the speaker calls them toward morning light as a source of renewal, acknowledging the darkness but insisting that waking — opening the eyes again — is where return begins.","src":"haiku"},
"become-the-knight~won-t-be-saved":{"haiku":"Predators close in on a lost, faith-stripped wanderer in a frozen landscape, and the only survival available is assimilation into the ragged, steel-born company of the damned — salvation is explicitly refused.","opus":"A grim winter fable of being hunted, cut open, and reborn into a pack forged from steel and pain. Beneath the horror imagery is a paradox of salvation through conquest, the will shaken but still refusing to obey.","sonnet":"A winter horror scenario where survival means becoming one of the monsters — bodies dragged through snow, campfire surrounded by the dead, a knife at the throat that converts rather than kills. The refrain 'we won't be saved' is a declaration of refused salvation: those with an iron hold reject obedience even as they're conscripted into the pack.","src":"haiku"},
"bedoes~delfin":{"haiku":"Whatever necessity demands, resolve submits; selling water to dolphins or walking through fire, surrender precedes transformation.","opus":"Polish rap built on a relentless whatever-it-takes hustle, the boast of being able to sell water to a dolphin standing in for a refusal to ever be broke or beaten. Bedoes threads menace toward rivals, snitches and police through fierce pride in his crew, his family and his small-town roots, sudden money hardening into a vow to do anything to win.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper's relentless hustle manifesto: the speaker will do whatever it takes — sell a dolphin water, walk through fire — to climb out of the margins, build his crew, earn recognition, and outlast every attempt to destroy him, delivered with street-level bravado and self-aware confidence.","src":"haiku"},
"bedoes~fps":{"haiku":"Rhythmic pulse drives through the body; today restraint might prevent foolishness. Live intensely since existence occurs once; share locations as we observe together.","opus":"Polish rap drowning in desire, drip and money, the repeated verb for sinking doubling as intoxication. Under the club bravado is a heart split in half, the title's motto putting cash above the women he cycles through.","sonnet":"Drowning in desire and drip — \"tonę, tonę\" repeated like a pulse — the speaker moves through a club full of temptation, texting for locations to watch the same stars, insisting money comes before everything (FPS: forsa ponad szmaty). The bravado of wealth and sexual confidence is threaded with an almost confessional restlessness.","src":"haiku"},
"bedoes~nadchodzi-lato":{"haiku":"She seeks rescue through celebration and dance among exiled outcasts. Lost direction marked by smudged lipstick; internal emptiness persists despite external joy.","opus":"Addressed to a lost girl numbing herself, the narrator begs for realness over performance, passion over skin. Beneath the summer refrain sits his own dead romantic hope, the confession that nothing makes him laugh anymore.","sonnet":"Summer arrives as a background to a portrait of a girl seeking rescue in dancing and crowds, her smeared lipstick a sign she's lost her way while the sea swarms around her — the narrator asks her to show complexity instead of body, and confesses he names other girls by her name, having buried love somewhere at his own bottom. A melancholy summer that doesn't deliver on its promise.","src":"haiku"},
"bedroom~nothing-lasts":{"haiku":"Trauma yields to small mercies; what hurt won't stay forever if you trust the shift.","opus":"A letter to a former self written from the far side of recovery, insisting the pain was temporary and the change was real. The quiet gratitude is aimed at whoever pulled the narrator out of it.","sonnet":"A brief letter from a recovered self to a former struggling self — the speaker has found happiness through change and gratitude, and reassures their past that the pain did not last and that the present is no longer painful.","src":"haiku"},
"before-i-turn~lorn":{"opus":"A lament for a loved one lost, the narrator drowning in grief and guilt, tormented by a memory that pierces him and won't fade. Picturing himself as a fallen ruler who never made peace with his failings, he confesses to bottomless loneliness and doubts he can endure much more.","sonnet":"About grief and guilt over being unable to save a woman the narrator loved — her blood is up to his knees, he was once a king who lost everything, and the song oscillates between drowning in guilt and the dark admission that he might be the monster who sealed her ending.","src":"sonnet"},
"before-my-life-fails~horizon":{"haiku":"Perception deceives and falling accelerates beneath the surface; the speaker drowns in disorientation, unable to distinguish sight from illusion in a collapsing frame.","opus":"Sight becomes untrustworthy as some undertow drags the speaker down and chokes him, blurring the line between real direction and delusion. He begs to be caught before he goes fully under, no longer sure the light he saw was ever there.","sonnet":"A speaker loses their grip on perception and direction: eyes are just disguise, the frame they float in offers no stable ground, and the pull downward keeps breaking their bones. The horizon — \"before me, horizon's blue / to you it's lost inside the view\" — is both a destination and proof that two people see entirely different realities.","src":"haiku"},
"beginners~dumb":{"haiku":"Lies and tears frighten neighbors accustomed to smiling facades. Optimism persists through maintained appearance; lipstick remains constant despite internal dissolution.","opus":"A woman replaying a breakup and calling herself dumb for having imagined the whole future, the mom, the kids, the dog. The self-mockery barely covers real grief over wanting to be loved and settling for someone who put himself first.","sonnet":"Someone with bright lipstick and optimism had mapped out a whole domestic future — meeting the mom, three kids, a dog — only to have it dissolve, and now they cycle through self-deprecating \"I guess I'm just dumb\" as a way of processing the gap between hope and reality. The panic and the bad-girl confession sit alongside the simple wish to be loved.","src":"haiku"},
"beginners~used-to-be-my-favorite":{"haiku":"Betrayal corrupts shared music as the speaker's intimate memories become contaminated by infidelity and loss.","opus":"A song ruined by heartbreak, unbearable now because it's welded to memories of someone who moved on to a new fangirl. The pop-punk sting is in realizing being hot was never enough to keep them, and being hate-cured of the tune you loved.","sonnet":"A breakup has contaminated a favourite song: every time it plays the speaker is reminded of seeing the ex at The Echo, and the implied revenge fantasy of sharing intimate photos signals that what's left is raw humiliation rather than grief.","src":"haiku"},
"beginning~era":{"haiku":"Self-inflicted plague and inherited curse compound until the speaker accepts their role as agent of their own doom, resignation replacing resistance.","opus":"A self-appointed bringer of doom confesses to poisoning his own world and sealing his fate, punishing others for failing to see the beauty he loved. The old warning about not turning on whoever keeps you fed becomes a verdict he's handed himself and can't escape.","sonnet":"A speaker who believes they have brought plague to a cursed land — through their own failures or the failures of those around them — questions whether they have the strength to rid themselves of the disease they carry, while nightmares become enemies and the living dead surround them. The closing verses accuse followers of forgetting the single rule: never bite the hand that feeds you.","src":"haiku"},
"behemoth~evoe":{"opus":"A hymn of worship to a Luciferian/Mephistophelian deity as the redeemed malevolence, the asymmetry and shadow-half of the divine, inverting Christian liturgy to exalt the unknown and reclaim the throne.","sonnet":"A hymn of adoration addressed to Mephistopheles — the devil is praised as a cosmic, pre-existing force that preceded creation, embodying the tension between good and evil and serving as an object of ecstatic spiritual devotion rather than fear.","src":"sonnet"},
"behemoth~slaves-shall-serve":{"haiku":"Revolutionary defiance and divine transformation overturn oppression; the speaker invokes ancient power to break free from servitude and fear, claiming dominion over their fate.","opus":"An occult rite of self-deification dressed in Egyptian and Thelemic imagery, summoning dark powers to cut weakness away and crown the self a war god. Behemoth's Nergal turns Crowley's decree that the enslaved must serve into its opposite — a call to stop serving at all, breaking bondage by becoming the god one once knelt to.","sonnet":"A ritual invocation drawing on Egyptian funerary imagery — Asar un-Nefer, the fields of Aqert — in which a war god cuts the umbilical cord of mortality, tramples weakness, and leads initiates through a spiral vibration of Khem toward an eternity where those who submit to fear are left behind to serve.","src":"haiku"},
"being-as-an-ocean~death-can-wait":{"haiku":"Caught between life and death, the speaker cannot divide fate's fortune from self-inflicted curse. Swirling paradox forbids simultaneous praise and condemnation; death delays its claim.","opus":"A metalcore sermon against wasting the life we were lucky to get, indicting greed and self-pity in the same breath. Its urgency is the insistence that meaning is made in what you give to others, not in comfort or wealth.","sonnet":"A blunt rebuke to anyone who squanders life while others would kill for the same privilege — death can wait, but only for those willing to own their misery, stop cursing their fate, and act for others rather than accumulating greedy solitude. Solomon's warning against greed is invoked to insist that what reaches past the grave is what we do for people, not what we hoard.","src":"haiku"},
"being-as-an-ocean~find-our-way":{"haiku":"One person reassures another in pain that they can overcome hardship together, offering protection and guidance to help them escape the monsters within and find their way forward.","opus":"A tender lifeline to someone barely holding on, raised in a home of pain and walking on tiptoes, the singer refusing to let them let go and promising to wander beside them through the comedown. It admits it can't carry their load but won't abandon them. Being As An Ocean are known for this spoken, deeply empathetic post-hardcore, and the closing French guided-breathing coda lands like a genuine act of care.","sonnet":"A steady hand extended to someone standing at the edge — someone exhausted, raised in pain, hearing voices, terrified of what comes after the comedown. The speaker doesn't pretend to share the exact weight but insists on being present for it. The closing French passage — a gentle guided breath, a count to three — turns the song into something close to crisis support.","src":"haiku"},
"beirut~elephant-gun":{"haiku":"Hunting, loss, and melancholic resignation pervade a life of futile chasing while seasons turn and hope dies with game.","opus":"A restless yearning to escape a small town where dreams stay buried and drinking passes for living, set against a bittersweet call to let the seasons turn and bring the quarry down. The hunt-and-campfire imagery lends the escape the weight of something that pierces the silence.","sonnet":"A young person who buried their dreams underground drinks to die and imagines hunting big game from far away with elephant guns — a seasonal, elegiac escape fantasy that ends abruptly on the word \"left,\" suggesting loss or incompletion.","src":"haiku"},
"belinda-carlisle~la-luna":{"haiku":"Lunar romance suspends lovers outside time while love becomes the only shared reality, moon witnessing their union across separation and longing.","opus":"A romance remembered as spellbound and inevitable, then quietly undone by the singer's own inexplicable leaving. The moon is the enchantment she wants back; the cold Marseille winter is the regret of walking away from something she never understood losing.","sonnet":"A woman recalls a night of moonlit romance in a Mediterranean city — the meeting, the walking, the surrender — and now wanders the winter streets of Marseille not knowing why she left, sustained only by the memory and the hope that the same light will bring her back.","src":"haiku"},
"belle-haven~forget-me":{"haiku":"A person struggles with self-loathing and believes they inevitably hurt everyone, fearing they will be forgotten despite their desperate attempts to change.","opus":"A raw plea from someone convinced he'll be forgotten and abandoned, exhausted from crying and trying to be anyone but himself, certain he'll hurt whoever stays. The devastating core is being unable to escape his own body and misery, asking to be buried alone. Belle Haven are an Australian post-hardcore band who specialise in exactly this unguarded, mental-health-forward confession.","sonnet":"Self-loathing as inevitability — a speaker who has spent years trying to be less of themselves for everyone else's sake, certain they will drive people away, certain they will be forgotten, and unable to escape their own presence even when they want to. The cruelest line is the resignation: the misery is theirs alone to carry everywhere they go.","src":"haiku"},
"belle-haven~hard-stare-soft-touch":{"haiku":"Strategic coldness protects emotional vulnerability as the speaker employs distance and refusal, aware that softness invites exploitation while hardness ensures solitude.","opus":"Coming in second in someone's affections, watching a rival pull the strings while your own heart breaks. The rat catcher caught in his own trap plays it off with a shrug, admitting it hurt only a little as time simply runs out on them.","sonnet":"A resigned post-mortem on losing someone to a more golden rival: the speaker watches them march forward \"like you've lost your mind\" and admits there is no prize for runner-up — it hurt a little, but just a little, and time simply ran out on both of them.","src":"haiku"},
"belle~february":{"opus":"Insomnia and guilt over a lost home and a relationship the singer feels he ruined, lying awake apologizing for being hard to love while the other has walked away.","sonnet":"A sleepless, guilt-ridden narrator lies awake consumed by regret over a past relationship and the home they destroyed, acknowledging they are hard to love, while the abandoned partner waits faithfully with planted roots.","src":"sonnet"},
"belzebubs~nam-gloria-lucifer":{"haiku":"A voice celebrates apocalyptic destruction of organized religion and institutions, invoking demonic imagery to promise purification through fire. The song uses biblical end-times language to frame systemic collapse.","opus":"A theatrical, Book-of-Revelation apocalypse in which the traditional end-times imagery is inverted so that Satan's victory reads as deliverance and the burning of a corrupt church as justice, capped with a Latin hymn of praise. Belzebubs started life as a webcomic about a cartoon black-metal family before becoming an actual band, so the blasphemous pageantry is delivered with tongue partly in cheek.","sonnet":"Apocalyptic black metal doxology framed as an inversion of Revelation — the beast rises, seals break, the church is burned, and the dragon speaks not as destructor but as liberator. The twist is sincere: Lucifer is praised as the one who purges tyranny and hypocrisy, the morning star who redeems rather than corrupts. Blasphemy worn as theology.","src":"haiku"},
"bemore~breath-of-life":{"opus":"A plea to escape numbing apathy and indifference, begging to be pulled from the quicksand of a deadened mind, insisting the soul will persist and needs chaos to truly feel alive.","sonnet":"A person sinking into apathy and depression pleads to be saved from their own indifference, insisting their soul persists beneath the numbness and that they need external chaos and intervention to feel alive again.","src":"sonnet"},
"ben-folds-five~brick":{"haiku":"An unplanned abortion splits a couple in postpartum despair; the song becomes their reckoning with shame, complexity, and the weight of consequence.","opus":"Folds' quietly devastating account of taking his high-school girlfriend for an abortion, and the isolation that swallows a couple who can't speak the truth. The genius is how the numbness sits where a bigger reaction should be.","sonnet":"A young couple navigate an abortion the morning after Christmas — driving to the clinic, waiting, buying flowers — and both end up more alone than before, the weight of the decision and their inability to reach each other slowly pulling them apart.","src":"haiku"},
"benee~glitter":{"haiku":"Trying to keep someone close before dawn breaks; connection as adhesive and refuge.","opus":"The suspended late-night moment after the party empties, one person angling to make the other stay when everything is winding down. It's about wanting to hold a fragile connection together past its expiry.","sonnet":"As a party empties out, the speaker invites someone to stay and make the night their own — the stickiness of glitter becomes a metaphor for closeness, and the race against sunrise is really just a desire not to let something fragile end too soon.","src":"haiku"},
"benny-blanco-jesse-swae-lee~better-to-lie":{"haiku":"Self-preservation and protective dishonesty seem wiser than authentic vulnerability; the speaker cannot afford the cost of truth.","opus":"The confession of someone who buries his real thoughts to protect a relationship, half-suspecting his whole life is just waiting to die. The bravado about being hated masks a fear that honesty would cost him everything, so the lie becomes an act of care and cowardice at once.","sonnet":"A speaker caught between honesty and self-protection admits that telling the full truth feels more dangerous than silence, while reflecting on therapy, mediocrity around him, and an existential unease about whether life is just a long waiting room before death.","src":"haiku"},
"benny-golson~killer-joe":{"haiku":"A legend called Killer Joe struts meticulously dressed while embodying hip cat rebellion. The introduction establishes character through sartorial precision and street wisdom.","opus":"A spoken-word portrait of a slick hustler, the ladies' man and gambler allergic to honest work, set to Golson's cool hard-bop swing. It's an affectionate character sketch of a mid-century street archetype.","sonnet":"A spoken-word portrait of Killer Joe — a sharply dressed corner-standing hustler in pinstripes, pointy shoes, and a wide black felt hat, always carrying jingle money from the horses and accepting cash from admiring women — whose defining characteristic is a deep personal commitment to never doing manual labour. The description is affectionate and satirical in equal measure.","src":"haiku"},
"benn~carrion":{"haiku":"Predatory instinct consumes consciousness—spawned from darkness, the speaker feeds on violated bodies while trapped in neural compulsion.","opus":"A monster's confession told from inside the predator, spawned by infernal rite and enslaved to synapses that compel it to kill. The relentless carry-on wordplay turns a horror-metal narrative into an unstoppable, remorseless feeding.","sonnet":"A creature or entity 'spawned from eternal night by infernal rite' narrates its compulsion to consume and violate, neurons firing on violation, unable to control itself — the pun on 'carrion/carry on' frames eternal predation as both need and destiny, with everyone eventually gone.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~blackberry":{"haiku":"Emptiness eludes capture while broken systems resist repair. Zen theft renders the speaker unable to trace recovery; dissolution marks the foundation.","opus":"Depression stated flatly and without the usual metaphors, the narrator convinced he's broken past repair and destined for the trash. The exhaustion of running from oneself lands harder than any despair could.","sonnet":"Too empty to be caught and too broken to be fixed, the speaker suspects their zen was never real and watches depression spiral out of hand — falling toward a trash-can destination for things broken and worthless, running from themselves but unable to escape. Wearing a cape to convince themselves to stay awake is the only heroism available.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~blood-tears-and-champagne":{"haiku":"Lifetime opportunity defies imagination and description; words fail experience's magnitude. Eternal search continues for unattainable perfection within this singular paradise.","opus":"A once-in-a-lifetime pull toward something that both saves and hollows out a broken man, framed as service and possession. It reads like addiction or devotion mistaken for rescue, the speaker choosing risk over being alone.","sonnet":"Something that happens once in a lifetime — a moment of possession and belonging that saves a broken person — is glimpsed and then lost, leaving an empty heart with nothing to die for and an open invitation that hollows out who they are. The choice between taking chances alone and the unbearable alternative of dissolution is the only one that remains.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~butterflies":{"haiku":"Attraction becomes weaponized deceit; the charm dissolves to reveal performance, leaving the speaker crushed beneath exposed falseness.","opus":"Infatuation curdles once the mask comes off and a charming performer is revealed as just another actor. Knowing every password but never the real person, the speaker feels foolish for being surprised that the crush shrank him.","sonnet":"Butterflies turned to dread: knowing every password but not the person, discovering the charm was gold-plated all along, and realizing too late that the fascination was a disguise — the speaker is annihilated and unsurprised.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~candy-cane":{"haiku":"Self-hatred crystallizes into blame and avoidance; memories become weaponized anchors, leaving the speaker perpetually returning to who they once were instead of becoming who they might be.","opus":"A pitiless address to a self-loathing man drowning his thoughts in pills, weighed down by hoarded grief he keeps converting into anger. His fatal wish is to become the person he once was, clinging to a past stickier than anything that could still last.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone crushed under self-loathing, unprocessed grief, and the suffocating weight of a past that won't release them — \"nothing is stickier than your own past\" — surviving by masking pain with anger and illusion.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~hot-guava":{"haiku":"Abandonment leaves the narrator suspended in scorching emotional seas. Self-esteem departed alongside the lover; the speaker drowns in presumed betrayal.","opus":"A breakup reframed as a trust fall into lava, self-esteem draining out with the departing partner. The fruit-flavored absurdity and shrugging resignation cut the hurt with dark comedy, the wound worn lightly on purpose.","sonnet":"The day the other person left, so did self-esteem — a trust fall landed in hot lava, the engine is leaking, and that trust is never coming back. The speaker has been burned before but notes they're not even sore, framing the loss with a breezy fatalism that barely conceals the depth of the wound.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~hump-2-0":{"haiku":"Erasure and burial become metaphors for emotional severance; the speaker constructs amnesia and distance to survive abandonment and move toward oblivion.","opus":"Fantasizing about burying an ex you keep half-hoping is already dead, then jolting awake to find them still alive. The relish of digging a hole to put them in is really the ugly work of forcing yourself to move on from someone who threw you into the dark.","sonnet":"A post-breakup fantasy of total erasure: the speaker imagines burying the other person, celebrates their absence, and cycles between wishing them gone and waking up to the fact that they are not actually dead. Drinking from the bottle and taking pills mark the speaker's own numbing while they construct a ritual of moving on through symbolic burial.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~icy-hot":{"haiku":"Destructive criticism wounds while determination persists despite numerical judgment. External verdict cannot fracture spirit; the speaker maintains resilience through conviction.","opus":"A guitarist answering critics by refusing to internalize them, the ointment on his wrists a badge of obsessive practice rather than self-harm. It's a defense of doing your own thing past every hater and gatekeeper.","sonnet":"Critics and gatekeepers who call the speaker lame or say all their songs sound the same are met with cheerful defiance — icy hot on both wrists from playing guitar, better that than track marks, plugging in and playing regardless. The clown metaphor is reclaimed: being a guitar-playing clown is what they choose over the alternative.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~lime-scene":{"haiku":"Hope rises only to be crushed; life's unfairness compounds through cyclical descent. Despair isolates by the throat; identity dissolves within this recurring nightmare.","opus":"A raw catalog of panic and depression cycling through anger and despair, the narrator kicking the bucket instead of hitting deadlines. The turn arrives when he decides he no longer needs anyone's permission or recognition to exist.","sonnet":"Hope keeps being pulled away and replaced by panic, manic swings between anger and strangers, a cold-world crime-scene routine — the speaker wonders how to love themselves while holding a losing hand and recognising this is the saddest they've ever felt. The pivot near the end — \"time to be me again, I obsolete your permission\" — is the only foothold toward recovery.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~nightpears":{"haiku":"Sleep transports into nightmare realm; reality blurs with dreams. Demons pursue the unprepared speaker who relies on refusal to engage violence.","opus":"Sleep paralysis reimagined as a battlefield where the dreamer, chased by demons, turns predator and refuses to fight fair. The bravado is armor over a genuinely disturbed mind, hell being where his life sends the underworld to die.","sonnet":"Asleep and landing in a nightmare full of demons and monsters, the speaker is unafraid — they never fight fair, and their life is where hell goes when it dies. The defiance culminates in the central image: being chased by wolves while paralysed, finding out you've been berried alive — buried in the nightmare — but refusing to be scared by it.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~our-own-light":{"haiku":"Self-absorption obscures inner illumination while external radiance blinds. Shared brightness would shine universally; collective disappearance paradoxically enables mutual glow.","opus":"A plea to escape the void and disappear somewhere darkness can't follow, paired with the ache that we're too self-absorbed to light our own lives. It sits between suicidal longing and a wish to simply be free of the shadow.","sonnet":"Consumed by their own light to the point of not illuminating their own lives, the speaker pleads to disappear to the other side — separating from the void, dissipating beyond shadows — but the pull back home and the truth of youth left behind frame the escape as longing rather than arrival. Even one night of bright shining together would be enough.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~petrified-orange":{"haiku":"Deception and false facades crumble as the speaker recognizes betrayal in trusted places, transforming shock into self-protective hatred and resigned abandonment.","opus":"Discovering that someone he trusted is a total fraud performing a false self leaves the speaker feeling doomed, comparing himself to a structure marked for the wrecking ball. Locked inside a mind no one else can reach, he keeps returning to a desire for death he's able to admit only to himself.","sonnet":"A burned speaker who finally sees the charlatan they gave everything to: called out as pretend, the speaker — feeling like a condemned building, unable to tell foe from friend — rages inward and outward, unable to save themselves, voicing a wish to be dead no one can hear because it is trapped inside their own head.","src":"haiku"},
"berried-alive~seeds-of-doubt":{"haiku":"Doubt plants itself while identity cannot reconstitute into former shapes. Yesterday's self remains unreachable; reinvention proceeds without nostalgia for past personhood.","opus":"A rejection of nostalgia and outside judgment, the narrator refusing to remain the old self that fans and critics remember. He won't let the doubt sink in; the stance is that reinventing yourself is survival, not betrayal.","sonnet":"Others insist on remembering who the speaker used to be, but they have moved on and don't need defence — they're a pioneer, not a copy, and the seeds of doubt won't take root in the mind that has already let the old self go. \"Farewell the old me\" is spoken with moxie rather than grief.","src":"haiku"},
"bers-rk~balders-b-l":{"opus":"A pagan, ancestral war oath in Danish: the narrator claims his inheritance of blood and soil, swears a sacrificial vow by Balder's funeral pyre, and vows to fight a shadow-war and rekindle the flame with his kin in a world of blood and steel.","sonnet":"A Norse-flavored declaration of ancestral duty and combat oath — the narrator pledges to fight a shadow war in the name of forebears and the people, gathering at Balder's funeral pyre as a sacred rallying point to reignite the flame of resistance.","src":"sonnet"},
"betcover~a-rgwfnl":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"In the fragile, off-kilter register of Japanese art-pop, the song wishes that everything painful and bewildering, the falling out of place and not knowing what one wants and being caught in storms, had only been a dream. Yet something real and tender persists beneath the disorientation as the person and narrator drift out of gravity together. The tenderness survives the confusion rather than resolving it, held in a weightless, uncertain suspension.","sonnet":"A wish that everything painful and bewildering — falling out of place, not knowing what you want, being caught in storms — had only been a dream, while still feeling something real and tender underneath the disorientation as the person and narrator drift out of gravity together.","src":"haiku"},
"betraying-the-martyrs~black-hole":{"haiku":"Someone is trapped in a cyclical self-destructive pattern where each attempt to break free only generates more suffering.","opus":"The French symphonic deathcore band frame a hopeless struggle as a monster that regrows every head you sever, the narrator tumbling into an abyss yet swearing he will be reborn once he strikes at its true center. Having traded his soul for a bad bargain, he confronts how unrecognizable he has become while vowing to break the cycle.","sonnet":"A Hydra trap — cut one problem down and two more grow back, the river is poisoned at the source, and the speaker has been gutting themselves trying to fix the wrong thing. Falling into a void where screams fade, the moment of transformation arrives only when they stop targeting symptoms and aim for the heart of what is actually destroying them.","src":"haiku"},
"betraying-the-martyrs~mirror":{"haiku":"Self-deception masks ambition as the speaker stares into their reflection, confronting the gap between aspiration and authenticity in a mirror that refuses to lie.","opus":"A charge to chase ambition without being fooled or consumed by it, keeping a steady pace as your certainties collapse. The mirror is the test: whether the person staring back is the one you always meant to become.","sonnet":"Self-examination as the central challenge: the speaker exhorts themselves and the listener to stare into the mirror and ask whether they are who they wanted to become, holding ambition and self-deception in check with the warnings \"dream in excess but don't oversleep\" and \"don't be fooled by yourself.\" The storm ahead is met not with fear but with a determination to keep a steady pace.","src":"haiku"},
"betraying-the-martyrs~swan-song":{"haiku":"Beauty becomes curse: elevated above others, yet isolation deepens with every moment of glory, until transcendence collapses under the weight of its own splendor.","opus":"Grandiosity and self-destruction feeding each other, mistaking a momentary high for kingship while raising a glass to every life thrown away. Addicted to guilt, the speaker half-refuses to get better.","sonnet":"A song about the manic high of ego dissolution — for a moment believing you can rise above everything, only to crack again at the ego's seams, with the cycle of falling and imagining kingship repeating endlessly and never mending.","src":"haiku"},
"better-lovers~30-under-13":{"haiku":"Dependence becomes invisible shackle; the speaker exists only as derivative of another's making, unable to reclaim selfhood they never possessed.","opus":"Contempt aimed at someone who defines themselves through the speaker, warned to let go of a status he alone granted. Beneath the cruelty is a diagnosis of a person so envious and desperate to be seen that they have locked their own inner child away to suffocate.","sonnet":"A contemptuous dismissal of someone who only exists because the speaker made them known — envious, invisible to the world, inner child locked away — told to hang on the leash of their own obsession because they will never become what they covet.","src":"haiku"},
"between-oceans~fade":{"haiku":"The speaker climbs higher to escape lies and doubts, learning to ignore others' projections until their detractors disappear.","opus":"A fall-into-flight arc where the singer feels pulled downward yet insists on plummeting until he sprouts wings, shrugging off a naysayer's lies and vowing to keep improving daily. It courts collapse only to reframe it as ascent. Between Oceans render this in melodic metalcore where surrender and self-belief keep swapping roles.","sonnet":"Defiance aimed at someone who doubted the speaker's survival — built on the refusal to absorb any more lies, the slow climb upward, the certainty that the doubter will eventually become irrelevant. The falling-until-you-grow-wings image frames the descent as necessary: you don't earn the wings without the freefall first.","src":"haiku"},
"between-the-buried-and-me~alaska":{"opus":"A sleep-deprived stream of consciousness caught in the small hours, the mind going strange as it fights to stay awake — half-joking that sheer alertness might hold death itself at bay. BTBAM build their 2005 title track on insomnia's warped clarity, landing on the quiet dread that nature will undo us in the end.","sonnet":"A stream-of-consciousness document of driving through the night while dangerously sleep-deprived — the narrator writes lyrics in real time, fights to stay awake knowing that succumbing to sleep at the wheel is a form of death, and the word 'Alaska' arrives with sunrise as both landscape and relief.","src":"sonnet"},
"between-the-buried-and-me~blot":{"haiku":"A blind or distorted consciousness experiences simulated reality and memory erasure; the song portrays loss of identity through electrical or technological manipulation. Fragmentation and trauma destroy continuity of self.","opus":"A progressive-metal fever dream about identity dissolving, a mind circling a blot in its vision as memory is molded, erased, and simulated. It stages the terror of watching yourself be reshaped until you no longer remember who you were.","sonnet":"A fragmented, dissociative journey — a solar blind spot becomes a metaphor for memory loss and identity dissolution, moving through a computer-simulation-like interior world where the self is molded, erased, and finally shattered into glass.","src":"haiku"},
"between-the-buried-and-me~condemned-to-the-gallows":{"haiku":"Condemned passage toward inevitable execution, with imagery of cold desolation, crimson moonlight, and desperate strides into nothingness as the condemned figure confronts their fate.","opus":"The progressive metal band stage a dreamlike march toward the narrator's own execution, feeling his way through fog and darkness by sound alone, drawn toward death with something close to acceptance. The song embraces misery as it moves through a hallucinatory landscape, its doomed procession less a story than a descent into surrender.","sonnet":"A descent toward a hanging — warm blood on cold snow, fog swallowing the last light, desperate strides into nothing — rendered as surreal, fragmented journey imagery; the condemned figure walks toward their death as if through a dream.","src":"haiku"},
"between-the-buried-and-me~famine-wolf":{"haiku":"Humanity devours itself; paranoia and predation define survival. Society strips people bare as meat and leaves only rotten bones.","opus":"Between the Buried and Me conjure ravenous appetite, honed jaws consuming selected victims while a paranoid few insist their remnant will suffice. The progressive-metal band's thick imagery drifts from a creature that once existed for its own sake to discarded flesh and abandonment, existence stripped down to dragging worn tissue across decaying frame to buy another sunrise.","sonnet":"Survival instinct stripped to animal bone: creatures who once lived fully now scour red dirt for one more day of flesh. Society has gutted meaning, leaving a paranoid remnant blinking in fog, convinced only that they are all that is left and must be enough.","src":"haiku"},
"between-the-buried-and-me~foam-born-a-the-backtrack":{"haiku":"Waiting persists in cold supplement while friendships fell through curved paths. Conscious decisions persuade the self toward continuation despite accumulated losses.","opus":"A meditation on the human hunger to complete ourselves, the endless wanting that overflows into an undertow. The opening of a sprawling concept suite, it names ambition and selfishness as the current dragging us under.","sonnet":"Waiting in the cold while connections drift — friends lost, bends driven — the speaker contemplates how the drive to complete and better oneself through selfish minds has created an undertow that pulls everyone swimming beneath it all. The vision of what it means to be human blurs into something that overflows.","src":"haiku"},
"between-the-buried-and-me~goodbye-to-everything-reprise":{"haiku":"Existence itself becomes questionable while breath and awakening demand recalibration. Endless journeys terminate; perception fractures across tangible and intangible boundaries.","opus":"A quiet, devastating farewell that questions whether the characters were ever truly alive, choosing together to switch off and float into nothing. It closes an arc with the tenderness of a mutual, deliberate ending.","sonnet":"Two voices — one asking if they're alive, one urging eyes to open to the endless journey that is their end — converge on the decision to switch off together, float to \"no more,\" and say goodbye to everything. The question of whether they were ever really alive is posed just before the choice to stop finding out.","src":"haiku"},
"between-the-buried-and-me~house-organ":{"haiku":"Internal conflict between breaking down self-destructive patterns and feeling controlled by external forces, with desperation to be heard beyond the surface narrative.","opus":"A restless, paranoid mind at odds with its own body, plagued by dread and violent urges toward things it perceives as threats. When love and fixation on another person intrude, that obsession becomes both salvation and disturbance, wrapped in a plea to be heard because the situation runs deeper than it appears.","sonnet":"A fragmented internal struggle between a body and mind in crisis — the speaker wants to destroy the physical frame that traps anxious, intrusive thoughts, while a sudden moment of love or connection breaks through, urging a shift in focus.","src":"haiku","fable":"In the Automata concept — a sleeper whose dreaming mind is mined and broadcast to a paying crowd — this second night is what it feels like to be surveilled from inside your own skull. The recurring image system is a war over sight and voice: the prying watchers he wants to smash, the exhausted body he wants to hang from a rope, and the frantic demand that the intruder go quiet long enough for him to speak in his own name. That body is the dreamer's own flesh-as-conduit, the house organ pumping his private interior out to strangers, soothing nothing because each move he makes is already floodlit and catalogued. What binds the paranoia is the tenderness at its core, a love that crashes onto the conscience and a plea to redirect the mind and raise the soul — the single human tether the surveillance can neither reach nor decode. The refrain that there is more here than appears is both the character insisting on an inner life beyond the show and the album hinting its dream keeps a hidden logic yet to unfold.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","love & desire","madness & the mind"],"means":"love as the one signal surveillance cannot decode","built":"the concept-album frame literalises the body as broadcast conduit"},
"between-the-buried-and-me~millions":{"haiku":"Millions of beings fly overhead while a speaker feels abandoned and trembling, their protective shield gone, leaving them lost and unable to follow others to where they soar.","opus":"The prog-metal group evokes overwhelming abandonment, a narrator who lost his bearings when a protective figure vanished and left him shaking and alone. A vast unreachable multitude passes overhead while he begs to be taken along, his own vulnerability tiny against that scale.","sonnet":"A solitary figure watches millions pass overhead and begs not to be left behind. The shield once offered by another is gone; without it, even the scent of collapse becomes unbearable, and the only vocabulary left is a single word repeated into the fog.","src":"haiku"},
"between-the-buried-and-me~the-coma-machine":{"haiku":"A person trapped in a numbed, dreamlike state drifts through despair with suicidal ideation, held back only by distant lights of hope.","opus":"A hallucinatory account of an overdose and the drift toward death, drugged senses dazzled by lights before a final collapse, then an unsettling reversal. North Carolina progressive-metal band Between the Buried and Me build the piece from shifting movements into cinematic dread, exactly the ambitious, disquieting storytelling that made their name.","sonnet":"Sedation and dissolution — drifting into unconsciousness framed as both escape and erasure, with the velvet room filling with smoke, a glimpse of the hanging rope before the senses blur out, and the mind climbing somewhere beyond the body. The song circles the moment of surrender repeatedly, pulling back and restarting as if the coma itself keeps rewinding.","src":"haiku"},
"between-the-buried-and-me~yellow-eyes":{"haiku":"Predatory eyes circle; a wanderer stumbles through a dying, ever-changing landscape seeking meaning in hollow, tangible sensation.","opus":"A surreal post-apocalyptic vision in the band's sprawling prog-metal style: a lone traveler wanders a ruined, frozen landscape stalked by predatory presences. A godlike creator surveys the wreckage of its own work while the narrator, marking his own flesh, questions who authored such a world and why its inhabitants suffer.","sonnet":"A wanderer moves through a landscape that keeps shifting underfoot — smokescapes, a crimson moon, the hum of electricity through crumbling bones — while something predatory circles closer. The disorientation is interior as much as spatial, and the mark cut into the palm signals an attempt to claim authorship over a world that refuses stable meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"bexley~in-the-night":{"haiku":"Danger and transgression lurk as constant temptation; flirting with destruction becomes a way of feeling alive.","opus":"A noirish thrill-seeker flirting with danger, a predator lurking in the corner, whisky straight from the bottle, a step ahead of the law, who finally finds every exit sealed as they close in. Cornered, he advises throwing morality out and just holding on for the ride.","sonnet":"Night as the natural habitat of danger-seekers — the tiger in the corner, drinking whisky while running from the law, fortune favouring the prepared but fools lingering anyway. The speaker is always running, the moonlit shadows and the devil's flirtation presented as lifestyle rather than transgression.","src":"haiku"},
"bexley~sick":{"haiku":"Pervasive illness and drowning consume consciousness as the speaker sinks deeper into cycles they can't escape.","opus":"Emotional deadness rendered as drowning in freezing water, feeling gone and honesty too expensive to afford. Sickness is both the diagnosis and the refrain, the self going under while tallying how long it's been since it genuinely drew breath.","sonnet":"About emotional anesthesia and being submerged in a sea of indifference and chaos — the speaker has lost the ability to feel, is drowning in an overwhelming world, and is addicted to the pain that at least confirms they're alive.","src":"haiku"},
"bex~don-t-date-the-devil":{"haiku":"Toxic love presented as thrilling danger, where pain and cruelty become the signature of devotion.","opus":"A gleefully menacing warning delivered from the monster's own mouth, love reframed as predation and violence. The narrator dares a lover into a war they'll lose, sadism worn as seduction.","sonnet":"A warning delivered from the perspective of a sadistic, self-aware manipulator — the narrator openly declares their destructive nature (gouging eyes, spreading lies, thriving on pain) and advises the target to stay away precisely because the attraction is real and the damage will be total.","src":"haiku"},
"bex~filfy":{"opus":"A grubby, defiant punk manifesto embracing filth and demons over forced perfection, insisting the outcasts down in the dirt have purpose and refusing to let the system get them.","sonnet":"A rallying cry for young, broke, disenfranchised people against systems that demand perfection while offering nothing — the speaker has a stomach full of repressed rage and invites others to resist the pressure to conform and fight back.","src":"sonnet"},
"bex~spyd4-k1ng":{"haiku":"A spider harvests fear and entraps victims within its web; power derives from venom and the ability to strike without warning.","opus":"A spider-queen persona spun into a metaphor for repaying liars in kind, luring them into a web woven as wide as their own deceit. She hates the game yet plays it with venom, building an army while her prey sleep.","sonnet":"Told from the perspective of a spider: small, feared, and powerful within its own domain, it spins webs of control and bites those who threaten it, while resenting its own compulsion to keep crawling toward the very people who would crush it.","src":"haiku"},
"bex~sum-kinda-syko":{"haiku":"Predatory manipulation disguises itself as apology; the victim refuses compliance and warns others against a danger that society refuses to acknowledge.","opus":"A survivor names a predator directly, refusing the script where the woman is blamed and the man apologizes for nothing. She warns him to keep his hands to himself, calling out the tired routine of a narcissist recast as harmless.","sonnet":"A woman names and condemns a manipulative, boundary-violating man who takes what he wants then feigns innocence, placing the blame on her; the refrain 'sum kinda syko' refuses the familiar script in which the victim is blamed while the predator escapes accountability.","src":"haiku"},
"bex~sundae":{"haiku":"Boredom and self-sabotage spiral into loveless cycles where the speaker remains trapped in repetitive romantic mistakes.","opus":"A pop-bright kiss-off that hides its sourness under sugar imagery, casting the singer as the sweet garnish on someone else's mess. The nursery-rhyme rain refrain admits the real subject: heartbreak she keeps shoving away rather than facing, boredom dressed as perfection.","sonnet":"A relationship compared to a sundae — the speaker is the cherry on top while the other person is the heartache beneath — plays out through rain imagery and a refusal to forgive, framing the whole affair as a perfect mistake.","src":"haiku"},
"bex~trust-no-one":{"haiku":"Fame-seeking corrupts morality while learned manipulation destroys doorways. Calculated social climbing sacrifices authenticity; the speaker becomes famous through systematic betrayal.","opus":"An ambitious pop climber narrating her own hustle, faking sincerity and burning doors to reach fame. The bravado curdles as she admits going in circles with no wins, trusting no one because the game rewards nobody.","sonnet":"A ruthless solo climb through the industry — learning every trick, destroying every door, faking everything said — is driven by the conviction that everyone lies and the only way to get it done is to trust no one and refuse to wait. The urgency is total: don't make me wait another day.","src":"haiku"},
"beyond-unbroken~blood-on-my-hands":{"haiku":"Guilt and haunting aftermath consume the speaker as they spiral in consequence of their actions, unable to wake from the nightmare of what they've destroyed.","opus":"Guilt over a death dramatized as literal blood on the hands, the speaker begging to wake from a nightmare where he watches a loved one drown. Grief curdles into a vow to drag the guilty party down to hell with him, mind lost between mourning and vengeance.","sonnet":"Survivor's guilt over a death the speaker could not prevent: they drown in flashbacks of holding someone who disappeared, say her name with blood on their hands, and tip into vengeance — dragging the guilty into their own hell — before collapsing into the knowledge that they simply lost their mind.","src":"haiku"},
"bhp~crownless":{"haiku":"Lost kingship and stripped authority; the speaker has been dispossessed of power.","opus":"A warning to the arrogant that pride carries its own downfall, built around the metalcore trope of the toppled king. Its real target is self-delusion: the lies you swallow about yourself will eventually strip you bare, and only owning them lets you rise.","sonnet":"A warning to someone riding arrogance too high — caught red-handed, flying with a parachute of delusion, thinking themselves king until the cycle of that maze strips the crown. The instruction is to wake from the dream, find the keys to the locked zone, and rise from the ashes rather than drown in the madness.","src":"haiku"},
"bicurious~i-don-t-do-drugs-i-just-sweat-a-lot":{"haiku":"Someone experiences an overwhelming, uncontrollable physical sensation or compulsion to move, helplessly surrendering to an force they cannot name or resist.","opus":"A euphoric, comic loss of control on the dance floor: the narrator, hearing music unlike anything before, is possessed by an irresistible urge to move as never before, half-panicked and helpless, the joke of the title (dancing so hard he seems high) driving the whole piece.","sonnet":"A purely physical reaction to music — the narrator is overwhelmed by a sound they've never encountered, losing control of their body, needing help to contain the compulsion to move.","src":"haiku"},
"bicurious~we-re-all-totally-fucked":{"opus":"A darkly humorous, anxious acknowledgement that everyone is collectively doomed and overwhelmed, yet still insisting on not giving up; solidarity in shared hopelessness ('we're all in this shit together').","src":"opus"},
"big-noter~identity":{"haiku":"Belonging requires knowing origins and demonstrating collective membership. Words carry weight through geographic specificity; identity ties to territory and mob affiliation.","opus":"An Indigenous Australian anthem of belonging, the always-was-always-will-be refrain answering who's your mob against fakes and blood-quantum gatekeeping. It reclaims identity as lived kinship, tested by who you are when no one's watching.","sonnet":"A First Nations assertion of identity against those who appropriate or fake belonging — \"fuck DNA, who's your fam, who's your mob, what's your name?\" — frames authentic connection to country and community as something that can't be claimed through genetics alone. The challenge to be who you are when nobody's watching is the real test of identity.","src":"haiku"},
"big-spring~cold-foot":{"haiku":"A person transforms into someone the speaker regrets, their trajectory downward and inexplicable. The speaker cannot explain or reverse this disappointing metamorphosis.","opus":"Waking numb into a life already deadened, watching someone you loved curdle into a stranger you'd rather forget. The refrain circles a helpless bafflement at how a person decays past explanation, shame bowing their head.","sonnet":"Watching someone you once knew transform into a person you'd rather forget — head hanging low, something broken in them that can't be explained — the narrator tries to cut off and start again while remaining unable to fully detach.","src":"haiku"},
"big-thief~velvet-ring-2023-remaster":{"haiku":"Sexuality and survival intertwine as survival drives the lover toward abandonment, each choosing self-preservation over redemption together.","opus":"A noirish ballad of doomed lovers, a man loving a dangerous woman he knows is fooling him, wanting only to take her home as everything collapses. Adrianne Lenker's tenderness sits inside a crime-story frame where love is the one gentle thing left.","sonnet":"A fragmented narrative of a violent and complicated love — a woman named Eliza, a man named Ben who loves her completely despite knowing she's dangerous, a pregnancy, betrayal, and money running out — held together by the image of a love so thick it exceeds any conventional symbol. The story resists resolution, ending in wonder rather than conclusion.","src":"haiku"},
"bigmama~popcorn-star":{"haiku":"Someone locked the door stealing keys while tears disgust; popcorn stars bounce brightly. Dreams persist even within waking flames; daily existence becomes fire within water.","opus":"A Japanese song about locking away tears and longing so someone else can find the light, the popcorn image turning suppressed feeling into a bright burst. Beneath the sweetness is a resolve to stop crying and let go.","sonnet":"Sick of tears and longing locked away behind a lid, the speaker urges themselves and a lost someone to burst free like popcorn stars — the salty, sweet, BBQ-and-cheese parade of flavours mapping a world both bland and overwhelming. The sealed feeling of missing someone is offered a small release: the wish that light will shine on them even from a distance.","src":"haiku"},
"bile~jerk":{"haiku":"Basement torture masquerades as excitement while cognitive emptiness justifies worthlessness. The speaker wastes existence while temptation leads toward self-destruction.","opus":"A grotesque first-person invitation to be tortured and killed, staging self-loathing as extreme masochism. The industrial shock is deliberate provocation, the annihilation fantasy standing in for a total absence of self-worth.","sonnet":"A graphic, sadomasochistic fantasy of willing obliteration — tied down, beaten, sliced, ultimately beheaded — framed as a sincere request for the kind of annihilation that would reveal what the speaker really is. The extreme violence is presented as cathartic self-disclosure, not threat.","src":"haiku"},
"billie-eilish~bury-a-friend":{"haiku":"A toxic co-dependent relationship dissolves into cannibalism and mutual corruption; the speaker confesses to draining her partner dry while questioning her own capacity for harm.","opus":"Written from the perspective of the monster under the bed, which is really her own suicidal self-loathing personified. The industry that made her a teenage star flickers through it as another predator, the child asking why anyone would care about her at all.","sonnet":"The song adopts the perspective of a monster beneath the bed — or the speaker's own darkest impulses — interrogating why the listener stays, revealing a predatory, self-destructive entanglement where both parties are complicit and the speaker is consumed by suicidal urges.","src":"haiku"},
"billie-eilish~everything-i-wanted":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation masks as fantasy; external validation cannot counteract internal collapse, and isolation deepens despite witnessing care.","opus":"Getting the fame she dreamed of and finding it a nightmare, a suicidal fantasy where no one notices she's gone. The counterweight is her brother, the one person whose love keeps her tethered when the whole world only wants something from her.","sonnet":"A dream about suicide — stepping off the Golden Gate Bridge and being unmissed — becomes a meditation on public pressure and private despair, rescued only by the presence of a single person who offers unconditional protection from everything the world demands.","src":"haiku"},
"billie-eilish~ilomilo":{"haiku":"Loss and anticipatory grief poison connection before it fully forms; the speaker cannot protect those she loves from mortality or herself.","opus":"Terror of losing someone to death, the fear of a loved one slipping away so total that she'll accept a comforting lie just to keep them near. Beneath the video-game title is real dread about suicide and the friends she couldn't save.","sonnet":"A speaker haunted by the loss of friends to suicide or death tries to hold onto another person before the same fate takes them, confessing fear of love's fragility while grief and anxiety blur reality and the plea to come home becomes a desperate one.","src":"haiku"},
"billy-howerdel~free-and-weightless":{"haiku":"Surrender dissolves ego and burden; relinquishing control to another promises liberation, a state of weightlessness traded for submission.","opus":"The A Perfect Circle guitarist stepping out solo aches over a lover's confession that both wounds and unburdens him. The longing to feel weightless and free is the pull of total surrender, letting ego and conscience dissolve into another person.","sonnet":"Listening to a confession strip ego and conscience bare, the speaker finds surrender to the other person — 'take all that you need from me' — as the only route to becoming free and weightless, love framed as a voluntary unburdening.","src":"haiku"},
"billy-howerdel~stars":{"haiku":"Decline from iconic status leaves only indifference behind; the speaker once blazed but now watches their light dissolve into irrelevance.","opus":"A god or idol grieving its own obsolescence as the worshipper outgrows and discards it, indifference curdling the old devotion. Howerdel makes control and abandonment feel intimate, the wounded pride of something that once shone and now only insists it still can.","sonnet":"A former authority — once worshipped as mountain or god, able to control through innocent eyes — watches the person they shaped drown in indifference and defiance; yet the speaker insists 'I will always shine on you,' the light a remnant of dominance as much as love.","src":"haiku"},
"billy-joel~careless-talk":{"haiku":"Rumor and jealousy poison intimacy through ambient noise, neighbors weaponizing half-truths until the couple drowns in whispered accusations they can never escape.","opus":"A couple trying to hold on while gossip and jealous rumor circle them. The stance is defiant tenderness: outsiders can talk all they want, but the intimate things shared between two people are secrets he'll never surrender.","sonnet":"Two people are surrounded by jealous gossip and second-hand lies from people who have constructed a false version of their relationship. The speaker reassures the other that none of it matters — the onlookers don't know them, and the private truth between them is impervious to rumour.","src":"haiku"},
"billy-newton-davis-deadmau5~all-u-ever-want":{"haiku":"Magic and dreams get extracted and discarded; the speaker bleeds while another person perpetually takes without reciprocating genuine feeling.","opus":"A grieving address to a partner who only ever chased the enchantment and the fantasies, treating affection as owed and leaving the singer wounded and unhealed. The looping refrain keeps circling the injury without ever mending it.","sonnet":"A one-sided relationship where the other person only takes the magic and the dreams without giving anything back, leaving the narrator's heart to bleed.","src":"haiku"},
"billy-newton-davis-deadmau5~all-u-ever-want-original-mix":{"haiku":"Desire manifests as pure yearning without form, an endless repetition of wanting what cannot be grasped. The song circles its core metaphor—dreams as the only currency that matters—trapping the voice in a loop of magical thinking.","opus":"An early Deadmau5 vocal house cut built on a lover who only wants the dream and the magic, never the real person. The looping plaint suggests being valued for fantasy rather than being truly wanted.","sonnet":"A speaker accuses a lover of consuming only the intangible — magic, dreams — while taking the real love on offer without reciprocating. The repeated refrain \"all you ever want are my dreams\" frames the relationship as one-sided emotional extraction.","src":"haiku"},
"billy-newton-davis-deadmau5~outta-my-life-deadmau5-touch-remix":{"haiku":"Heartbreak lodges as darkness inside the other person, something the speaker cannot fix or understand. The only path forward is walking away, letting rain wash away regret, though bitterness lingers as proof the damage was real.","opus":"A clean break dressed as house catharsis, telling a departing lover not to look back while insisting the bitterness can't be forgotten. The rain washing away regret is willed release the singer doesn't quite believe.","sonnet":"Ending a toxic relationship, the speaker acknowledges the other person's inner darkness and chooses to let go rather than stay mired in shared regret. Rain washing away regret and the plea \"don't look back\" mark the break as final and deliberately unsentimental.","src":"haiku"},
"billy-newton-davis~all-u-ever-want":{"haiku":"Exploitation of love through constant taking; leaving a heart perpetually bleeding from consumption.","opus":"An R&B lament for one-sided love, the singer poured out as raw material while the beloved takes only the magic and the dreams. The ache is being valued for what you produce, not who you are, and bleeding for it.","sonnet":"A partner who takes the magic and the dreams while leaving the heart to bleed — love reduced to extraction, the speaker naming the dynamic plainly but unable to break it.","src":"haiku"},
"billy-sane~motherfucker":{"haiku":"Hatred and self-loathing collide with defiant nihilism; the speaker embraces chaos and violence as escape from suffocating existence.","opus":"A hollowed-out, agoraphobic rage turned outward at a disgusting world and inward at the self, all threat and self-hatred at once. Started from the bottom and still standing, the speaker can't escape the everything he hates or the fear underneath the bravado.","sonnet":"A self-aware portrait of a person collapsing inward — holed up in their apartment for a week, hollowed out, imagining coffins and bloodshed, unable to escape the self-hatred and yet somehow still here and still scared.","src":"haiku"},
"billy-talent~fallen-leaves":{"haiku":"Homelessness and addiction strip away hope in a crooked town where runaways and street dwellers fade into obscurity, their dreams buried like fallen leaves beneath urban decay.","opus":"A runaway drifts to the coast to escape himself and instead sinks into street addiction, the track marks and lost friends marking a slow drowning that no new town can outrun.","sonnet":"A runaway slides into addiction in a city that discards the lost, hiding track marks on sidewalks and haunted by the question of whether he abandoned his friends or they abandoned him; the fallen leaves refrain becomes an elegy for everyone swallowed by the street.","src":"haiku"},
"bilmuri~better-hell-thicc-boi":{"haiku":"Toxic love masquerades as fate, the speaker unable to leave despite clear harm inflicted. Self-blame coexists with accusation—they point at themselves while thinking of the destroyer, caught in a hell they choose because the alternative is worse.","opus":"Post-breakup honesty that refuses either heaven or reconciliation, the narrator taking the blame publicly while privately still hurting. The bleak of it is that being alone in a rough place still beats the person who broke him.","sonnet":"The speaker refuses to name who hurt them, deflecting blame onto themselves in public while privately knowing the truth, and concludes that even a hellish present is preferable to returning to that person. The line \"it's a better hell than you\" is the blunt pivot point.","src":"haiku"},
"bilmuri~boutta-cashew":{"haiku":"Coldness and betrayal leave the speaker abandoned and confused, struggling to understand how effortlessly they were discarded.","opus":"Stunned resentment at a former partner who detached with an icy, effortless swiftness while the narrator is still gathering the wreckage. He suspects their affection was wielded as a tool to gain the upper hand, and can't fathom how they turned so frigid after burying and abandoning him.","sonnet":"Bitter resentment toward an ex who moved on effortlessly while the speaker is still picking up the pieces — they're desperate to understand how someone could use love as leverage and then go cold, while they remain shattered.","src":"haiku"},
"bilmuri~pain":{"haiku":"Abandonment and pain from loss and judgment; not fitting in or being enough for others. When stripped of everything, loneliness persists, and loved ones fall away.","opus":"Abandonment settles into the lungs as the speaker feels emptied out, judged, held down and told what he is by people who fall away calling him not enough. Bilmuri, Johnny Franck's genre-hopping project that veers from metalcore to yacht-rock warmth, lands here on stark ache, the closing turn toward a presence that stays near when he has nothing offering the only thread of comfort in an otherwise bleak accounting of being cast out.","sonnet":"Abandoned and judged by those who deemed him too different, a narrator carries the physical weight of isolation like a wound in his lungs. Despite being cast out and told he is not enough, he holds onto one remaining source of constancy to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"bilmuri~thicc-thiccly":{"haiku":"Helpless witness to another's pain calcifies grief; the speaker carries suffering they cannot fix, holding darkness so others know isolation ends.","opus":"Cradling someone through their darkest moment, holding their head in your hands and absorbing their pain so they know they aren't alone. It's a tender vow of presence in the face of a helplessness neither can control, capped by a wry spoken tag.","sonnet":"A caretaker holds someone through invisible pain — \"I hold your head in the palm of my hands, I feel your tears fall down\" — carrying their suffering internally so the person knows they're not alone, even as helplessness lingers and control remains impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"bird-problems~jerk-chicken":{"opus":"A pun-stuffed brush-off aimed at hecklers and critics, using kitchen and roasting gags to wave away insults. Beneath the jokes sits a plain statement of resilience: threats hold no power over the singer, and he intends to come back stronger than whoever wanted to tear him apart.","sonnet":"A song built entirely on cooking and comedy puns delivered as an insult-comic roast — the narrator is already baked, refuses to be frightened by flaccid threats, and tells an adversary to take their last flight before rising from the ashes, all with deliberate absurdist humor.","src":"sonnet"},
"bird-problems~pigeon-superstition":{"haiku":"Breaking free from learned complacency and choosing discomfort for growth rather than remaining a passive node in an algorithmic system.","opus":"This offbeat act builds a bird metaphor for personal growth, calling for people to leave the safety of comfortable routines and stale loyalties that dulled them into numbness. Skewering passive, choiceless drift, the narrator vows to stop shielding himself and to welcome discomfort as the sole path to becoming more.","sonnet":"Comfortable routine is indicted as a kind of self-imposed blindness. Old habits and inherited devotions narrow the world until nothing remains beyond the familiar nest. The song argues — lightly, with avian metaphor — that discomfort is the only engine of genuine growth, and safety is just another word for stagnation.","src":"haiku"},
"bird-problems~plume":{"haiku":"Stagnation breeds despair as systems collapse beneath you, each repetition a failure to escape or transform. The speaker chases transcendence but crashes to earth, trying to shake loose from echoes of who they thought they'd become.","opus":"An anxious spiral about self-sabotage, the fear of failing so strong that never beginning feels safer. The recurring image of cutting the branch underfoot maps a compulsion to shake off a self the narrator can't quite name.","sonnet":"Restlessness and self-sabotage cycle through imagery of cut branches, shipwrecks, and bones cracking on soil — ambitions launched only to collapse. The speaker wants to escape an echo that isn't really them and silence the chorus driving them forward.","src":"haiku"},
"birds-in-row~nympheas":{"haiku":"Beauty and transience merge as the speaker pursues color and transformation, burning the world to prevent the forgetting of those who inspire devotion.","opus":"A tender address to someone crowned as everyone's savior, urged to burn that role and stop singing everyone's praises but their own. Older loves and jealous exes chant that they mustn't forget, but their claims to the truth mean nothing next to the abandon of dancing in the light.","sonnet":"A song about someone who exhausts themselves giving praise to everyone but themselves, chasing bright colors over dirty blues while ghosts of old romances haunt them with the demand \"you'd better not forget.\" The image of dust dancing through a crack in the window and the singer's desire to burn the world down together suggest a love offered as both refuge and radical act.","src":"haiku"},
"birds-of-tokyo~broken-bones":{"haiku":"The narrator is shell-shocked and deceived, driving toward inevitable death on a highway, refusing to go back.","opus":"The Australian rock band build arena-scaled melancholy into a mantra of stubborn survival, the singer disoriented and losing his bearings yet still driven by a voice calling him onward, even as his road leads nowhere good. His injuries become badges of a refusal to retreat, grim persistence dressed as an anthem.","sonnet":"Someone who has been deceived, left shell-shocked, and is now on a road they know leads somewhere final — yet the voice calling them back is not enough. Beaten up, vision gone, they still refuse to return. The broken-bones-and-busted-smile imagery makes the damage physical, and the repeated refusal to go back reads less like defiance than like a person who has simply run out of reasons to turn around.","src":"haiku"},
"birds-of-tokyo~eduardo":{"haiku":"Repeated requests for substances and relief that can't be refused, with the requester drowning while others treat them as fake or unreliable.","opus":"An early, rawer track from the Australian rock band tracing the grinding compulsion of addiction, its narrator endlessly asking for one more fix he cannot quit. The craving isolates him and poisons a friendship, the pleading chorus admitting that no one around him takes his loneliness seriously.","sonnet":"Caught in a loop of craving and transaction — buy another, roll another, fix another — a figure stands alone and half-surrendered, recognising that what keeps pulling him back is fake but unable to stop reaching for it anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"birds-of-tokyo~i-d-go-with-you-anywhere":{"haiku":"Two people commit to escape together from a stalled, suffocating world, trusting each other despite uncertainty.","opus":"A devoted escape song, one lover reassuring another that even if they lose everything they'll have something beautiful, begging to be gotten out of a place where staying means never leaving. It trades promises of distance for the simple vow to go anywhere together. Birds of Tokyo, the Australian rock band, shape this into their signature soaring, hopeful catharsis.","sonnet":"Escape as devotion — the world has stalled but the clocks haven't, and the only thing that makes sense is leaving together before inertia swallows both of them. The speaker doesn't need a plan or promises, just movement, just the other person. Even losing everything is reframed as something beautiful if they're side by side when it happens.","src":"haiku"},
"birds-of-tokyo~lion":{"haiku":"Confinement breeds hunger for autonomy and wildness, the speaker restless against four walls and invisible chains. Roaring through the city becomes an act of reclamation, proof against the fear of settling for less than freedom.","opus":"A woman trapped in a small, unseen life who finally grabs her keys and bolts for freedom, refusing to let fear rule her. The lion is the roar of someone deciding to run wild after settling for too little too long.","sonnet":"A woman trapped in a small, grey room finds the will to grab her keys and burst free into the city night, seeking wildness and bright lights over the cage of her current life. The narrator witnesses and amplifies her interior roar.","src":"haiku"},
"bishop-of-hexen~a-serpentine-grave":{"haiku":"Desire kindles through deprivation and fire, the speaker descending into hunger for glory and power. The path demands great deeds, defiance of fear, burning through comfort to reach salvation earned through suffering.","opus":"Symphonic black metal casting ambition as a scorched pilgrimage toward glory, where danger is embraced like sunshine and pride is worth more than life. It's a grandiose ode to the will to power forged in fire and hunger.","sonnet":"A figure emerges from spiritual famine and deprivation, transforming thirst into a violent drive for glory, guided by an omen from above toward a destiny shared with followers. Danger is welcomed as \"graceful jubilation\" along a scorched path to salvation.","src":"haiku"},
"bishu~cage":{"haiku":"Predatory menace wrapped in cryptic threat, soul-taking framed as supernatural violation.","opus":"A sparse, looping threat built on menace more than meaning: a predator taunting prey that has no way out, the repetition itself the trap closing. More mood than message.","sonnet":"A predatory voice challenges an intruder looking into its cage, threatening to take their soul before deciding what comes next. The listener is told to \"just fight it off\" — resistance is possible but framed as futile.","src":"haiku"},
"bishu~hurt-me-too":{"haiku":"Two people hurting each other in an intimate relationship that burns slowly and inevitably causes mutual pain despite attachment.","opus":"The knowing self-sabotage of loving someone who will hurt you, the narrator fully aware they'll wreck themselves trying to love a person who kisses them and tells them to leave; the collapsing timeframe (a week, then an hour, before they call) captures the accelerating helplessness of the attachment.","sonnet":"The narrator can see clearly that this relationship will cause them pain — the attraction is vivid and physical, the other person will say they need them and then send them away, and the song is a resigned pre-emptive acknowledgment of the hurt coming.","src":"haiku"},
"bish~bish":{"haiku":"A defiant response to fake celebrity and false morality—everyone's a pretender in a paradise, but the speaker keeps their face true.","opus":"An idol group snaps back at the arithmetic-obsessed, hype-peddling machinery around them, asking what an idol's life is really worth under all that greedy scrutiny. Keeping her face steady, the singer insists on going on despite being treated like a monster, owning the strangeness rather than apologizing for it.","sonnet":"BiSH sings about the precariousness of being an idol — called a monster, told they're freaks, watched by hungry eyes that consume and move on — while refusing to care, keeping their face composed under twinkling stars. The emotional core is the defiant self-possession of performers who know they are disposable and go on anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"bish~paint-it-black":{"haiku":"The song expresses defiance against despair and a vow to honor promises. The narrator refuses to give up, choosing to paint their world black and deliver their heart's shape to those they cherish.","opus":"Sung in Japanese, a resolve to stop giving up on giving up, to make the whole world an ally and find the meaning of being born, shattering nights of tears toward a piercing light. Weakness reaches out but gets painted over in black. BiSH were a Japanese idol-punk group who called themselves a punk band without instruments, and this delivers their fierce, self-naming defiance and a promised shape of love.","sonnet":"Written in Japanese — a song about refusing to give up on a promise made to someone, choosing to fight fear and resignation and paint the world black rather than surrender to it. The emotional current is stubborn, aching love that keeps returning even when the singer tries to close their eyes against it. Shape of love here means something hard-won.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz-radex~potlacz":{"haiku":"The speaker advocates for reckless abundance and refusal of social control, rejecting pessimism and responsibility to spill oneself onto the world like a dog shaking off water.","opus":"Over Radex's production, Polish rapper Bisz preaches a defiant open-handedness, urging listeners to deal out everything they own like cards and shake off their tormentors like a wet dog. The title's potlatch, a ritual of extravagant giving, frames his philosophy that the world is too beautiful and life too short to hoard, truth held in the middle of his five fingers.","sonnet":"A potlatch manifesto against hoarding and fear: the speaker urges draining the cup of bitterness to the dregs, redistributing everything like cards, and shaking off resentment like a wet dog. Life is too short and beautiful for the rat race; the eternal inner child demands extravagant release.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~banicja":{"haiku":"Exile and banishment define the narrator's existence as an outsider continuously chasing something intangible. He accepts his role as perpetual exile and pursues freedom through endless motion.","opus":"A Polish rapper's manifesto of self-exile: life always demanded more than he did, and he'd sooner die than settle, offering himself up in rhyme as both victim and priest. Everyone is a banished exile; the past is a burning bridge and the future is chosen exile. Bisz is a Polish alt-rap poet whose dense, literate, Seneca-quoting verses turn pain into relentless forward motion.","sonnet":"A Polish rap meditation on voluntary exile — life always demanded more than the speaker was willing to give, so he became a kind of self-made outcast, writing himself into rhymes as the only place his story fits. The past is a burning bridge, the future is the freedom of deliberate banishment, and somewhere underneath is a child's face in a pillow still trying to breathe, holding the whole thing together.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~carrie":{"haiku":"A protagonist consumed by inner turmoil and societal rejection channels intense rage and despair into destructive force, comparing himself to telekinetic fury that consumes everything around him.","opus":"A scapegoat swollen with pain casts himself as sacrificial victim and Dionysus, a medium channeling society's debt until dread and disgust tip into telekinetic catastrophe. Bisz, a Polish rapper known for dense literary abstraction, threads the King film's blood-drenched revenge through Rio's Christ and City of God, insisting the trembling hands and hatred spraying from the arteries are not his own but the world's turned back upon it.","sonnet":"A rapper frames himself as the scapegoat of collective failure, so saturated with others' projected shame and rage that his hands begin to act without his will — a telekinetic Carrie who finally turns the violence back. The anger is presented as externalised guilt, not truly his own.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~g-upiom-dry":{"haiku":"The speaker positions himself between wisdom and folly, using wordplay and contradiction to critique rap's commercialization while defending authentic artistic expression over mainstream expectation.","opus":"A Polish self-portrait as the smart-fool, wedged between the try-hards and the genuinely stupid, calling himself the worst rapper in Poland as an oxymoron and a two-part harmony. It bristles with wordplay, classical allusion and contempt for scene careerists. Bisz built his name on exactly this braggadocio laced with literary irony, wielding synecdoche and self-deprecation as weapons at once.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper's defence of being neither entirely smart nor entirely dumb — positioning himself between the posturing of both poles as the only honest place to stand. It's a sharp-tongued catalogue of what he finds fraudulent in the rap scene and in public life, cutting down sacred cows from God to the state education system, and insisting that the contradiction at his centre is actually its own kind of harmony.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~indygo":{"haiku":"A meditation on inner divinity and personal power independent of external status. The song describes transcending victory and defeat through embracing one's own sacred nature blended between darkness and light.","opus":"A Polish exhortation to inner sovereignty: you were born a king, your throne travels with you, and clothes don't make the ruler. It urges listening to the blood that circulates in you, mixing brightest blue with deepest black into indigo, a lamb who is also a lion. Bisz turns his literate alt-rap toward something almost gnostic, calling a chosen few to become new architects of a better world.","sonnet":"A Polish rap manifesto about sovereign selfhood — the speaker addresses someone born a king who carries their throne in the soles of their feet, never needing external validation. Indigo, the colour between blue and black, becomes the emblem of a state beyond victory or defeat, a watchful calm that only those who've been to the poles of experience can reach. The call is to stop waiting for permission and follow the blood.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~jestem-besti":{"haiku":"A rapper boasts about his transgressive nature and dominance over the rap scene, declaring himself a beast beyond conventional morality and social control.","opus":"A monstrous alter ego announces itself, spelling out its name and vowing to mince rivals' brains, half horror-movie showman and half battle-rapper. Bisz, a Polish MC steeped in dark literary allusion, stacks Manson, Lecter, Quasimodo and baseball home runs into a grotesque birthday party, framing his refusal to commercialize rap as the reason the media's big fish keep their distance from a self-declared beast with a taste for spectacle.","sonnet":"A rapper self-mythologises as a monster born outside the industry's careful partitioning, exploding out of the system like a cesarean birth from within. The beast persona is both ironic and sincere — cataloguing dark humor and bodily excess as proof of an untameable creative force.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~niech-czas-stanie":{"haiku":"The narrator feels disconnected from time's passage while life happens around him; he wishes for moments to freeze during beauty but resigns himself to watching time slip away without control.","opus":"A Polish rap on time and exhaustion, the singer wishing time would freeze for one good minute at dawn while God walks the world and he smokes over coffee, admitting he can't live yet won't die. Life is beautiful and stupid; he loves the first part, can't manage the second. Bisz distils his weary philosophy here, chasing slivers of eternity between the seconds.","sonnet":"A Polish rap plea for time to pause during the small moments that make life worth living — smoking a cigarette at dawn, being with someone, the rare instant when everything is briefly okay. Around that wish is a portrait of someone who feels out of sync with the calendar, perpetually broke, moving through a life that happens around rather than inside him, and unable to stop time even when he catches a glimpse of eternity between seconds.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~pollock":{"haiku":"The artist creates visceral, uncontrolled rap like abstract painter Pollock's action painting, pursuing artistic excellence while those around dismiss it.","opus":"A Polish declaration of artistic obsession, the singer racing through every direction of space at once, rejecting clocks because now is always now, flinging his mess onto the page like Jackson Pollock and daring anyone to keep up. Rap is his Eurydice, dragged out of the underworld. Bisz stacks dense allusion and swagger, insisting everyone must be someone while it's enough for him just to be himself.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper asserting that being present in the moment, hurling language at the page without permission or apology, is itself the art — invoking Pollock as the model of someone whose mess was the method. He dismisses the rap industry's gatekeepers, critics, and day-job moralists, insisting that being himself is enough even if no one can follow the tempo.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~role-playing-life":{"haiku":"The narrator treats life as a role-playing game where one constructs a character and navigates existence with childlike faith, despite personal failures and inability to change himself.","opus":"Life gets recast as a tabletop campaign, the speaker rolling up a character sheet for a rapper-poet-paladin from a working-class estate, chaotic-good and neurotic, insisting anyone can choose who they become. Bisz, a Polish lyricist fond of extended conceits, turns stats and inventory into a manifesto for self-authorship, the one who must change you being you, life as fable and the system a dragon to a self-styled Saint George.","sonnet":"Life is treated as a role-playing game character sheet: class, origin, flaws, skills, hit points all listed with rueful precision. The narrator insists that embracing the game's full difficulty — not stat-grinding in comfort — is the only way to actually advance and change the world.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~trainspotting":{"haiku":"Desire and ambition function as an unstoppable train; those who refuse to join must accept being poor outsiders or jump to their death. The song warns that bypassing this system means total exclusion.","opus":"A Polish rap built on the streetcar named Desire and the film Trainspotting, casting life as a one-way train toward wanting everything, where hanging back means watching from the platform. It laces Bukowski, Dante and Shakespeare through a bleak parable of the rat race. Bisz turns literary reference into a hard sermon: the road to heaven runs through hell, and for most it's just spectating.","sonnet":"A Polish rap about the machinery of desire as a one-way train — board it or get left behind on the platform watching other people's lives speed past. The song is part satire, part trap: the speaker knows the system is a rat wheel, that desire is engineered to keep you running, and that Dante's gate is above the entrance — but the train is still leaving.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~w-biegu":{"haiku":"Life moves relentlessly forward and those unable to board the train of desire end up as outsiders watching. The song frames existence as an uncontrollable pursuit driven by appetite and ambition.","opus":"A Polish anthem of ceaseless motion, the singer waiting for Godot yet always on the run, chasing dreams like light through the ether, returning eternally young while others grow old and static. He won't stop, unsure where the road leads but sure that's a detail. Bisz makes his restless, allusive alt-rap into pure forward drive, crowning himself demiurge and king of his own world.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper in perpetual forward motion, chasing something he can't name yet and refusing to slow down long enough for anyone to catch or question him. The running is the point — not the destination but the refusal to stop, the belief that being in motion is the only honest response to a world that never stops either.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~wilk-chodnikowy":{"haiku":"A Polish poem on isolation, aging, and resistance to conformity—the speaker walks society's margins aware of mortality and refuses capitulation to material demands or forced maturity.","opus":"Polish rapper Bisz casts himself as a pavement wolf, roots threading through skulls and ribs, too aware of misfortune yet still howling at an empty sky. He refuses to grow at society's pace lest he lose his freedom, likening the city to a zoo that cages such things, insisting dreams exist not to come true but to concern the heart rather than the world.","sonnet":"A street wolf observes urban life cycling endlessly through birth, death, and the slow extinguishing of childhood dreams by economic pressure. He refuses to grow up on society's terms — collar and all — choosing to howl at an empty sky rather than surrender his humanity to the grind.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~wnyki":{"haiku":"The speaker grapples with how past failures and present stagnation cast shadows on his future prospects and his ability to provide a good life for his unborn child.","opus":"Three movements pin a man between a dead past he stands over like a stillbirth, a feared future addressed to an unborn son, and a present staring into a mirror that shows only emptiness. Bisz, a Polish rapper who works in bleak psychological interiors, builds the whole piece around being snared in time's trap, unable to stop the wounds of memory bleeding into a self perpetually a step short of happiness.","sonnet":"Trapped between a past that keeps bleeding and a future he fears he will ruin for a child not yet born, a narrator stands before a mirror and finds only the void where a self should be. Past, future, and present form the three snares — the wnyki — that hold him immobile.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~zawleczki-nakr-tki-kapsle":{"haiku":"Three failed love affairs leave only bottle caps and ring pulls behind. The song explores how relationships dissolve strangely and unpredictably, leaving only the debris of drinking as proof they existed.","opus":"A Polish rap on doomed relationships that always end strangely, drowned in vodka and nicotine because that beats singing about how to forget. Each love flickers a moth's brief life and dies abruptly, leaving only bottle caps and pull-tabs as souvenirs. Bisz threads Plato and Socrates through the wreckage, turning barroom heartbreak into a bleakly literate ritual of loss.","sonnet":"A Polish rap about the residue left behind after each relationship ends — the pins, caps, and bottle tops of the title standing in for the small material debris of shared drinking and shared time. Two relationships traced with rueful honesty: one that dissolved without drama, one that sparked and burned out before either party could say what it was. What's left is always the same small pile.","src":"haiku"},
"bisz~zew":{"haiku":"Primal hunger awakens as moonlight ignites the blood; predatory instinct takes over consciousness and reason surrenders to wild, purposeful survival.","opus":"A Polish descent into a wolf's instinct, the singer left with nothing, veins torn, feeding on hunger as vultures circle, then transforming: nostrils catching a trail, the moon igniting the coals of his pupils, blood pounding as he leaps through snow-flecked night. Bisz strips his usual philosophy down to raw animal survival, the call of the wild rendered as absurd, unstoppable flight.","sonnet":"A Polish poem-rap that shifts registers entirely — from the shared scarcity of lives on the margin to a sudden, feral animal awakening. The second half abandons social observation and becomes pure instinct: wolf paws in snow, nostrils finding a scent, the moon igniting something in the blood that commands survival above any argument.","src":"haiku"},
"bite-down~no-sympathy":{"haiku":"Rejects sympathy for those accepting fear and societal numbness, urging resistance against systemic control. Calls for breaking from conformity or facing degradation.","opus":"A hardcore takedown of a fear-run society that reduces people to statistics and pacifies the meek, the singer spent but urging listeners to defy the system's design or perish as obedient livestock. It rails at manufactured threats sold as real danger, casting conformity itself as the enemy to unmask.","sonnet":"Anti-conformist fury directed at systems that numb and rank people until they accept being cattle — the speaker sees the scam clearly, names it as against human nature, and refuses to offer comfort to anyone who participates. The choice is stark: subvert the design or die as a sheep.","src":"haiku"},
"biters~restless-hearts":{"haiku":"Restlessness and yearning prevent settlement; the heart refuses stasis and demands perpetual motion.","opus":"Power-pop worship of youth burning itself out, broke and reckless on a dawn that never ends. Beneath the fist-in-the-air abandon runs the awareness of chasing a high precisely because it can't last, insomnia as a way to hold off the ordinary.","sonnet":"Broke, bloodshot, and heading for the liquor store anyway, the narrator and his crew chase the high of being alive on the boulevard past dawn, refusing to let the sun coming up be a reason to stop. The energy is born of nothing — no money, no plan — but the fist in the air and the rock-and-roll girl are enough to make the rat race feel like a race worth running.","src":"haiku"},
"biting-elbows~alone":{"haiku":"A summer loss forces the speaker to confront their protective nature and guilt, then a confession under interrogation. The speaker fears their love caused someone's death.","opus":"A menacing narrative dressed as heartbreak: a woman who had to go one summer, a singer who never wants to be alone, and creeping hints of a detective, fingerprints and a river he drove her to. The dread of what happened surfaces slowly under the refrain. Biting Elbows, the Russian band tied to the film Hardcore Henry, love this cinematic, unreliable-narrator tension riding a jittery electronic pulse.","sonnet":"A relationship replayed as a crime scene — a summer departure framed through the speaker's dread of solitude and the woman's fear of something else entirely. The song builds a quietly sinister record: a detective, fingerprints, a river, a two-way mirror. Whether the danger was real or imagined by either party the song refuses to say, which is exactly what makes it unsettling.","src":"haiku"},
"biting-elbows~control":{"haiku":"Two lovers burn with chaotic, uncontrollable passion that sets fire to everything; the fire inside them is alive and real in a way nothing else can match.","opus":"A self-destructive romance where torching a hotel stands in for a love that runs on danger and adrenaline. The narrator confesses he has lost all restraint and is half in love with a partner precisely because the relationship thrives on the reckless things it pushes him to do.","sonnet":"A relationship so electrically charged it feels like a building on fire — the speaker is utterly stripped of self-control by this person, feeling more alive than they have in years, careening toward both destruction and ecstasy.","src":"haiku"},
"biting-elbows~my-woman":{"haiku":"An urgent, desperate love: the speaker can't live without their woman and is willing to sacrifice everything; they cling to each other despite instability and fear.","opus":"A frantic, defiant confession that the narrator can neither endure his present life nor bear existence without his partner, tangling reckless youth and self-ruin together. Through the chaos runs a fierce promise to remain devoted to her permanently.","sonnet":"Against a backdrop of restless energy and the sense that staying still is a kind of death, the speaker declares an all-consuming need for his partner — unable to live without her, willing to endure lies and chaos, committed to her till the end of time. The song swings between a desire for freedom and absolute dependence.","src":"haiku"},
"bitter-bliss~family-enemy":{"haiku":"Blood relation becomes indistinguishable from enmity when abuse replaces love, the family home a prison that teaches survival through silence. Escape means celebrating their absence, reclaiming what was stolen.","opus":"An unflinching account of childhood abuse and neglect, the blood family recast as the enemy amid drugs, poverty and silence teachers noticed but couldn't stop. The wish for their deaths reads as the only path to peace.","sonnet":"A survivor of an abusive, chaotic home confronts the family members who neglected, humiliated, and exploited them — arriving at cold celebration of their eventual departure. The lyric \"the blood says you are my family / but you're nothing more than my enemy\" is the core indictment.","src":"haiku"},
"bitterwood~paradigm":{"haiku":"A superior person dominates a weak-minded follower, framing control and contempt as natural hierarchy.","opus":"A venomous boast of dominance, the singer sneering that his stride, his gaze and his core all rank above another's, then belittling a deceived girl and a spineless boy left without a father. It hardens into self-worship, taunting his prey to hit back while he declares himself their deity. Bitterwood push it into brutal contempt where the target is denied any will of their own.","sonnet":"A power-trip dissected from inside — someone who genuinely believes they are superior to everyone around them, contemptuous of a weak-minded boy and a misled girl, positioning themselves as god. The shift is that the contempt turns inward at the end: even the god role is a hole, a rot, a thing that takes life rather than gives it.","src":"haiku"},
"bj-rk~army-of-me":{"haiku":"Self-sufficiency demands refusing sympathy and managing alone, with the speaker becoming an invincible army of one. Complaint brings annihilation from internal reserves.","opus":"A blunt refusal to keep coddling someone wallowing in self-pity, warning them to get self-sufficient and get to work or face a whole army's worth of the singer's impatience.","sonnet":"A speaker cuts off all sympathy for someone who refuses to stand up and manage themselves — the rescue squad is exhausted, you're on your own, and any further complaint will be met with an army: a hard, unsentimental demand for self-sufficiency.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grinding, mechanized ultimatum that multiplies its single speaker into a title-sized threat: quit leaning and fend for yourself, or face a whole battalion of this one voice. The genius is the withheld addressee, which lets the same words cut two ways at once. Aimed outward, it is brutal love flung at someone who keeps pleading to be carried; aimed inward, it is a self scolding its own weaker half, ordering the collapsed part of it to get moving because no one is coming to lift it. The would-be rescuers of the last verse are named only to be pronounced spent and off duty, sealing the verdict that self-reliance is the sole option left, and the endlessly looped warning stops being counsel and becomes the sound of an engine that will not quit repeating its one demand.","fv":2,"fnote":"Björk has said exactly who it’s aimed at — her brother, after ten years of his self-pity, from his fourteenth birthday to his twenty-fourth: stand on your own legs, get a job, get a life.","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"a looped ultimatum that multiplies one voice into its own battalion","built":"the withheld addressee lets the same words cut outward and inward simultaneously"},
"bj-rk~hidden-place":{"haiku":"Secret love hides itself beneath longing and shyness while two people seek sanctuary away from judgment.","opus":"The trembling arrival of love that someone has sent, so overwhelming the singer doesn't know where to put it and hides her passion under a blanket. She dreams of a secret sanctuary where she can shelter in the beauty of a fragile, divine other.","sonnet":"A speaker shyly, carefully tends to new love — almost allowing fingers to touch, hiding passion under a blanket, drawn to retreat with the beloved into a hidden place where they can be invisible together in each other's hair, seeking sanctuary from the world's gaze.","src":"haiku"},
"black-coast~chains":{"haiku":"Existential claustrophobia demands total transcendence; the speaker flees toward freedom while warning others not to pursue or trust.","opus":"A push to break out of numbness and stagnation, the metalcore restlessness of someone who'd rather feel alive anywhere but here. The chains are internal, an old self and past to reckon with before the escape means anything.","sonnet":"Captivity in the dark versus the distant atmosphere glimpsed overhead — the speaker wants to be nowhere near the lines, somewhere that feels alive, and calls out to skies that don't answer. Running back for nothing is refused; escape is the only direction.","src":"haiku"},
"black-coast~mercy":{"haiku":"Rage and self-hatred mirrored back infinitely, seeking mercy from wounds that won't heal.","opus":"Unable to pull himself together, the speaker stares into mirrors of eyes reflecting a self he can't recognize, questioning whether his life is real or a lie; having lost his solace and chased highs to numb emotion, he suspects the rage—and the lack of mercy—is meant for him.","sonnet":"A dissociation spiral — scars everywhere, rage that might be deserved, mirrors with black staring eyes, and a chase after a high that has eroded most emotion. The narrator is under no illusions about wasting time but cannot find a way to stop; \"mercy\" names the solace lost.","src":"haiku"},
"black-coast~paradise":{"opus":"Black Coast drift into a hazy, dissolving intoxication, a self blurring and reeling while refusing to reveal anything within to anyone. The private bliss is a deadened remove, a cold, uncaring rush treated as its own reward. It's about giving in to euphoria as both getaway and secret, a realm barred to every other eye.","sonnet":"A dissociative, hedonistic withdrawal into inner experience — the narrator wants to reveal nothing of their inner world and instead chases the blurred, careless ecstasy of not caring at all, seeking a private paradise sealed from outside scrutiny.","src":"sonnet"},
"black-coast~strangers-skin":{"haiku":"A figure spirals through time wrapped in foreign skin. Moral collapse and numbness mark a wasted existence.","opus":"The hollow aftermath of losing yourself, spiraling inside a body that no longer feels like yours while inner voices insist you're nothing, hollow reassurances that you're fine papering over a numb sense of a wasted life.","sonnet":"A song about dissociation and losing one's identity — the narrator has spent so long caving under pressure that they no longer recognize themselves, feeling like a stranger in their own body, hearing inner voices tell them they're nothing, aware they've become dark but unable to stop it.","src":"haiku"},
"black-coast~without-you":{"haiku":"Surface damage and internalized worthlessness persist while the speaker fears exposure of the authentic self. Trauma and insecurity remain so close that visibility becomes unthinkable.","opus":"Self-worth caves to a nothing that lurks just under the surface, the instinct being to entomb every ugly piece and bolt from it. By the end the narrator can finally recognize her ingrained patterns and doubts for what they are, and grasps that change comes down simply to doing it.","sonnet":"A self-perception crisis set against dependency: the speaker feels worthless on the surface, wants to bury and run from everything, but notes that without the other person things are different now — clearer, changed — even if old insecurities still sit close to the surface.","src":"haiku"},
"black-coffee~drive-edit":{"haiku":"Obsession drives endless motion as escape and return collapse into the same circle; love persists as ghost long after its death, haunting every distance.","opus":"Driving endless miles into the night to outrun a love that won't die, betting that distance can burn the feeling off. The trouble is he cannot outrun it at any speed, unable to shake a ghost he still half-believes in.","sonnet":"The speaker cannot escape a love that has already ended: too deep in grief to see clearly, too haunted to forget, they resolve to drive ten thousand miles through the dead of night until the love runs out — but the ghost of it rides along regardless, painted as a silhouette in every passing headlight. Distance is proposed as the cure, and the song is the attempt.","src":"haiku"},
"black-crown-initiate~invitation":{"haiku":"A man invites his own destruction and feeds on sin, fleeing death to waste another year. Guilt and shame lock him in cycles of self-harm and fraud.","opus":"A knotty meditation on cowardice and self-ruin, a man welcoming his own disease, bred for appetite and violence, who flirts with death then flees it, a fraud with an unearned clean conscience begging to slay a tyrant. The Pennsylvania prog-death craftsmen wrap this maze of guilt and futility in their trademark oppressive, cerebral density.","sonnet":"A self-destructive man who keeps arriving at the edge of death and walking away, not out of courage but cowardice, then squandering the reprieve. The song tracks his cycle of guilt, addiction, and fraudulent clarity — always chasing the face of something that might end the tyranny of his own nature, begging for that internal king to be killed off so something else can start.","src":"haiku"},
"black-eyes~deformative":{"haiku":"Childhood manipulation and mysticism imprint trauma; the speaker recalls a formative moment of spiritual seduction that transformed innocence into fearful desire.","opus":"A visceral memory of a childhood religious rite tangled with dawning sexuality, fear, and the shame of being called a slur. It is about a moment that stays forever, mixing revulsion and excitement into a lasting loss of peace of mind.","sonnet":"A fragmented recollection of a childhood moment of religious or sexual imposition — chrism on the forehead, wrists held, a pebble in the knee at a chain-link fence — that lodged in the body as both excitement and revulsion, and whose echo still surfaces at sixteen, driving south, unresolved.","src":"haiku"},
"black-honey~charlie-bronson":{"haiku":"Psychopathic detachment presents as inevitable fate, styled as dark glamour with chemical enhancement. The speaker claims this darkness as identity, refusing the mundane and dragging others toward their abyss.","opus":"Adopting the persona of Britain's most notorious prisoner, the song weaponizes psychopathy and a pharmacy of stimulants into swaggering menace. It's a study in gaslighting oneself into invincibility, threatening to drag everyone down.","sonnet":"A speaker inhabits a pharmaceutical-fuelled psychopathic persona — mixing Modafinil, Ritalin, and Benzedrine with Harley Quinn grins — and dares a companion to descend with them into mutual destruction. The repeated boast \"I'm Charlie fucking Bronson\" is a statement of gleeful, performative menace.","src":"haiku"},
"black-label-society~crazy-horse":{"haiku":"Crazy Horse emerges as a visionary warrior chosen by heaven; he embraces sacrifice and death, declaring it a good day to die while leading others into battle.","opus":"Zakk Wylde's tribute to the Lakota war leader, framing him as a visionary who accepted death as the price of standing for his people. The stance is reverent, treating a warrior's readiness to die as courage rather than tragedy.","sonnet":"A tribute to the Oglala Lakoux war leader Crazy Horse — his vision-guided life, his courage on battlefields thick with the dead, and his famous declaration that it is a good day to die — told with reverence for the warrior archetype he embodied.","src":"haiku"},
"black-label-society~godspeed-hellbound":{"haiku":"The world burns beneath infernal forces that spread destruction without ceasing, driven by ancient vengeance and endless cycles of death.","opus":"Zakk Wylde's biker-metal outfit paints an end-times vision of war, hunger and slaughter, prayers left unanswered by indifferent deities as the world consumes itself. Delivered with the band's signature leaden riffs and doom-heavy grandeur, it is a bleak march toward damnation with no rescue in sight.","sonnet":"Apocalyptic machinery with no off switch — a world grinding itself to pieces through war, obsession, and the weight of unanswered prayers. The famine and murder arrive from within as much as from without, and the only benediction is ironic: godspeed hellbound, a send-off for something that was already burning.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~4-walls": {"haiku":"Emotional and physical abuse within a confined space drives the speaker to deeper violation and control, finding satisfaction in inflicting pain on a suffering partner.","opus":"A disturbing portrait of a cruel, controlling relationship, where the speaker admits it is not love but a compulsion to hurt and get under another's skin, taking his own pain out on a partner who lets him in.","sonnet":"Within a claustrophobic and stifling space, a relationship plays out as something mutual but corrosive — not love, not clean — where both parties infect and damage each other, with the narrator taking out his inner pain on someone who keeps absorbing it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A relationship as a locked room, narrated from inside the abuser's arousal: he digs into his partner looking for the scream, gets excited as something dies, and — the song's coldest move — blames the victim for having let him in. The self-awareness never becomes remorse; he states flatly that this is not love and not clean, then keeps going, which is the point: clarity without change is its own horror. The blistering walls with peeling paint are the tell — the room itself is sick, a fever dream he claims he cannot wake from, the claim of helplessness being the last lie an addict to cruelty tells.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control"],"means":"clarity without change offered as its own horror","built":"the claim of helplessness named as the last lie an addict tells"},
"black-light-burns~animal": {"haiku":"An exploration of conflicting desire and self-loathing. The speaker is attracted to something primal and destructive even as they recognize its danger—they hate that they love this animalistic pull.","opus":"The ugly honesty of loving someone you resent, admitting you would give everything to let the base, animal want back in. Wes Borland writes desire as a parasite you secretly want to keep, pride and craving at war.","sonnet":"The narrator is caught in a hateful, compulsive love — he despises his attraction to someone who lies and manipulates, yet keeps being drawn back, recognising that he would surrender everything to let that dark \"animal\" inside him loose again.","src":"haiku","fable":"Desire framed as parasite: the song opens pretending it wants the infection cut out, then admits the real fear is that once extracted the speaker would keep it — he wants the lie sold convincingly enough to justify relapse. The hate-that-I-love-you refrain is the whole engine, superiority collapsing into appetite, and by the end he is bargaining the whole self away for the animal’s return.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"pride collapsing into appetite until the self is bargained away","built":"the hate-that-I-love-you refrain engineered as the whole engine"},
"black-light-burns~bakelite":{"haiku":"Love and hope dissolve quickly into wasting time and self-hatred; the narrator wastes years wearing down into an inappropriate, broken wish.","opus":"Wes Borland's addiction-as-romance portrait, where love and a drug wear the same face, thrilling at first then souring into self-loathing. The same old cycle of hope collapsing into a frown worn down over years.","sonnet":"A portrait of self-loathing and emotional deterioration — the narrator describes falling hard for someone like a drug, watching hope corrode into the same old pattern, hating himself for wasting time and settling into a permanent frown.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~because-of-you":{"haiku":"Because of one person, the singer has wasted his life searching and suffering, refusing now to surrender or accept mistreatment.","opus":"A song of obsessive fixation and refusal to let go, the singer scouring back streets and riverbanks, memorising another's thoughts and making them his own, wasting the life he built while chasing a place that leaves him alone. Black Light Burns is Wes Borland's project beyond Limp Bizkit, and this simmering, wounded electronic-rock defiance, vowing not to turn the cheek or suffer, sits squarely in that darker register.","sonnet":"Someone remade by an absence — searching riverbanks and back streets, memorizing the other person's thoughts to fill the gap, wasting the life they'd built before. The declaration is not romantic: it's a refusal to absorb the damage quietly, to turn the other cheek or swallow the suffering. The attachment is corrosive and the speaker knows it.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~burn-the-world":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and numbness consume the speaker as they watch cycles of destruction replay endlessly. What burns always winds up again; they're extinct and drowning in the repetition of the world's cruelty.","opus":"Wes Borland writing the addict's false ending: the belief that you've finally destroyed everything and can start clean, then the pull dragging you back to watch it all wind up again. Nothing burned down really stays down.","sonnet":"A person caught in a confusing night, unable to escape the noise of a world winding itself back up no matter how much they try to burn it down — someone nearby is trapped by their own brain, and the television keeps pulling everyone back to the spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~coward": {"haiku":"Warning against loving the speaker, whose destructive appetite inevitably wounds those near. Blood clots the heart while the beloved is courted yet destined to bleed.","opus":"A warning to a loved one that the narrator's hunger and flaws will inevitably hurt and destroy anyone he adores. He urges them to run and save themselves, even as he confesses he aches for them and wants to keep them.","sonnet":"A person warns the one they love to leave because their own nature is to inevitably hurt everyone close to them—they are drawn to the other person but certain they will cause damage, like a bloodclot that starves rather than feeds. The warning is an act of aching, self-aware resignation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession structured as a warning that works as a lure: he tells her to run while cataloguing exactly how he will consume her, and every command to get away comes stapled to a come-on. The title supplies the verdict the chorus never states — knowing you will hurt everyone you love and staying anyway is the cowardice; the bridge's plan to keep her somewhere visible, already knowing he'll break her again, is where the mask slips completely.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"warning the beloved structured to function as a lure","built":"the title delivers the verdict the chorus never states"},
"black-light-burns~cruel-melody": {"haiku":"A one-sided love where the speaker would sacrifice everything for a reconnection that will never happen, viewing past relationship as poison with no cure.","opus":"A lament over a lost love, imagining merging into the beloved like the sea while recognizing her heart is empty of him, and calling his past a poison with no cure as he begs to be taken away.","sonnet":"The speaker mourns a lost relationship: the other person's emotional absence is as persistent as a tune that won't leave, the past is poisoned and irreversible, and a desperate longing to be taken away is the only remaining response.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track is the album's one undefended moment: a lover already lost, addressed in conditionals — if he could paint her, if he could fall into her — because every real avenue back is closed. The cruelty of the melody is that it plays in his absence; she has a life that goes on without him, while his own past is a poison with no cure, so the fault sits with him and he knows it. The long dissolve of the outro enacts the surrender the verses argue toward: no more bargaining, just the repeated plea to be taken, since he might as well belong to her anyway.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"every real avenue back closed leaving only conditionals","built":"the long outro dissolve enacts the surrender the verses argue toward"},
"black-light-burns~forkboy":{"haiku":"A con man and exploiter uses fake jobs and borrowed identity to defraud desperate people while hiding his crimes.","opus":"Wes Borland covers the Lard track as a grotesque parable of predatory greed, a con-man asset-stripper who devours pensions and betrays everyone for profit. The fork is capitalism eating people alive.","sonnet":"A portrait of a specific kind of predator — 'Forkboy' is a shape-shifting operator who sets up fake businesses to entrap desperate people, later revealed to be an FBI informant and HUD fraudster, someone who destroys communities and the people who love him for profit.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~grinning-like-a-slit":{"haiku":"A speaker confronts loss and abandonment, watching goodbyes dissolve and beginnings shed while others smile through empty streets with false ease.","opus":"Wes Borland's post-Limp Bizkit outlet trades nu-metal bombast for something bleaker: a numbed reckoning where every avenue of comfort, mountains, cities, strangers, dead ancestors, dead-ends in the mind's own walls. Farewells are figured as flies bred from filth while the narrator scrapes toward some grim rebirth, watching another's cruel grin as he senses himself about to fall.","sonnet":"Isolation resists every attempted escape — mountain, stranger, city light — because the anguish is interior. Goodbyes rot like flies from decay, and while one person was falling apart, the other was already reinventing themselves. The floor drops at the moment of recognition, and there is no one left to catch the fall.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~how-to-look-naked":{"haiku":"An observer has witnessed horrific deeds and violence, burying secrets that will die unknown, while predators strip away identity and dignity before killing. The refrain stresses that no one ever truly exposes themselves or their crimes.","opus":"Through brutal, riddling imagery, spectators gather to relish a hunted figure's grisly downfall. The repeated jeer about refusing to undress frames the target as someone concealing his real self even as he is publicly stripped bare by the humiliating chase.","sonnet":"A dark, fragmented portrait of someone being hunted, observed, and hoped to be destroyed by onlookers — the central refrain suggesting the target keeps a hidden self that no one ever gets to see.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~hungry-like-the-wolf":{"haiku":"A predatory hunter pursues their target through urban darkness with primal hunger and stealth. The song uses animal metaphor to describe relentless romantic or carnal pursuit.","opus":"Wes Borland's cover strips the Duran Duran hit of its gloss and lets the predatory core show plainly: pursuit as raw animal hunger, the hunt for a woman rendered as menace rather than romance.","sonnet":"A cover of the Duran Duran track — pure predatory sexual pursuit, the narrator as a wolf hunting through the city night, tracking a woman with all senses until he catches her.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~i-am-where-it-takes-me": {"haiku":"Something overwhelming and inescapable pursues the speaker, controlling outcomes whether they run, speak, love, or resist—they have no agency against its pull.","opus":"A claustrophobic surrender to something inescapable, an addiction or compulsion that follows, swallows and defines the narrator no matter where he runs. The plea to take it away is the addict's helpless refrain.","sonnet":"A song about being consumed by something inescapable — some force that follows, swallows, and breaks the narrator wherever they go, leaving them with no agency over their own direction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A possession song built on a pronoun that never gets a name: the it that holds, watches, and touches is described only by what it does, because naming it — addiction, depression, compulsion — would grant the speaker a distance he doesn't have. The verses escalate through eight attempts at definition, and the tell comes late: after killing me and watching me arrives just like me, collapsing tormentor into self, which is why the plea to take it away can only be prayed, never acted on. The title is the surrender clause — Borland's narrator has stopped steering entirely, and the mechanical repetition of the song enacts the very loop it describes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"tormentor collapses into self making escape impossible to act on","built":"mechanical song repetition enacts the loop it describes"},
"black-light-burns~i-have-a-need": {"haiku":"The speaker is drawn to darkness and fear as a psychological compulsion, trapped in a cycle of emotional torment that cannot be controlled.","opus":"A song about a compulsion toward self-destruction and a doomed attraction, describing an unstoppable dark fever and blackest wish that can't be controlled as the narrator vows to follow the other person wherever they are.","sonnet":"A dark compulsion toward a destructive relationship: the speaker acknowledges being drawn to terror and standing at the edge repeatedly, admitting the obsession cannot be controlled even as the other person is already too far gone to be reached.","src":"haiku","fable":"An addiction to a destructive bond diagnosed in the first breath — he must have a need to be terrorized — and then acted out anyway, the couple standing at the edge of the same familiar hole, too scared to jump but unable to turn back. The chorus's apology is a fake: its blanket sorry hands responsibility to the fever itself, and the song's long collapse into that one repeated phrase performs the surrender it describes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"the blanket apology hands responsibility to the fever itself","built":"the song's collapse into one phrase performs the surrender it describes"},
"black-light-burns~i-want-you-to":{"haiku":"A commanding narrator coerces violence and violation, orchestrating death and dismemberment while the victim becomes a tool of another's will.","opus":"Wes Borland's murder-ballad menace, a controlling voice compelling another to give, take, kill, and kneel entirely on command. Beneath the gothic butchery it's a study of domination dressed as desire.","sonnet":"A violent, domineering desire — the narrator commands someone through a series of brutal acts, offering submission and control in equal measure, with the devil and murderous imagery framing a relationship built entirely on power.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~iodine-sky": {"haiku":"Secrets hidden from daylight—things witnessed in darkness will remain buried in the grave, invisible to the world.","opus":"Wes Borland's side project distills a single confession here, a witness who thought he had seen everything bleed until something worse left his own eyes untouched but haunted. The sparse, ominous lyric buries whatever he has done, glimpsed only by the sun, promising those deeds will follow him silently into the grave he digs for them.","sonnet":"A narrator holds a secret act witnessed only by daylight, something buried with him rather than confessed. The brevity is its own statement: certain things done in private stay private until the grave closes over them.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cruel Melody closes on a five-line confession that refuses to confess: the speaker has watched others bleed while his own eyes stayed dry and normal, and whatever he himself has done was witnessed only by the sun and will go into the ground with him. The brevity is the meaning — after an album of compulsive disclosure, the last word is a secret kept, and the long instrumental fade that follows is the silence he promised.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"after an album of disclosure the last word is a secret kept","built":"the long instrumental fade enacts the silence promised in the lyric"},
"black-light-burns~it-rapes-all-in-it-s-path":{"haiku":"Self-destruction and violence consume everything in their path while the speaker yields to chaos and aggression.","opus":"A brutal, unstable rant giving a menacing something a life of its own, the singer choking and tearing at it, dropping bombs wherever he goes and turning blame back on whoever's sinking the ship. It thrashes between self-harm and threat. Black Light Burns, Wes Borland's project, push this into deliberately ugly, confrontational industrial-rock where the imagery is meant to repel.","sonnet":"Rage and powerlessness churning together in language that is deliberately ugly — the speaker keeps dropping bombs wherever they go, cannot escape some force that violates and consumes, and oscillates between directing the fury outward and turning it against themselves. The aggression of the imagery is the point: this is what being out of control actually feels like from inside.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~lie": {"haiku":"Complicity in a false, deceptive existence maintained by violence and distrust. The narrator struggles between accepting this lie and trying to reclaim truth.","opus":"Wes Borland's confessional about living a constructed lie and trying to kill off the vulnerable child within, who refuses to die. It circles self-loathing, complicity among the pigs he follows, and a crawl back toward something honest at the surface.","sonnet":"A song about living a double life built on deception, in which the speaker is simultaneously aware of his own dishonesty and disgusted by the corrupt system he operates within. He struggles to suppress his conscience while crawling toward some kind of escape or authenticity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession of living double: verse one is the private labor of maintaining a false front — he frames self-deception as construction work, a house built to keep the truth from collapsing in — while the failed project underneath is killing the boy inside, the honest self that refuses to die. Verse two turns the same disgust outward at an industry of pigs running on jealousy and lust, and given that Borland wrote this after walking out of Limp Bizkit, the read is hard to avoid: the lie was the career, and the song is him crawling back up to the surface. The hook levels the accusation both ways: he burns no differently from the people he indicts, complicity shared with everyone still inside the franchise.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"the accusation levelled equally at the speaker and the industry he indicts","built":"self-deception framed as construction work with a house to maintain"},
"black-light-burns~lucretia-my-reflection":{"haiku":"Empire and war machines grind people down, yet the speaker and their partner move beyond doubt together.","opus":"A cover of the Sisters of Mercy song: an apocalyptic vision of empire collapsing amid war machinery and violence, in which the dispossessed seize the ruins as their own kingdom, addressing Lucretia as the singer's mirror-self while sensation and belief numb to nothing.","sonnet":"A cover of the Sisters of Mercy song about two people who see themselves as holding the keys to empire and knowledge, dancing through the noise of industrial collapse — the world burning around them while they remain apart from doubt and direction.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~mesopotamia": {"fable":"A poisonous relationship staged as the cradle of civilization: the beloved is a city that has been burned, rebuilt, and burned again, and loving her means swimming in the river with all the previous dead. The creation myth does the emotional work — she is made of clay, and she takes back the mud of herself only to weaponize it, giving intimacy and then revoking it as assault. The structural turn is the whole argument: the devotional refrain — everything surrendered for nearness to her — returns after the grenade with its polarity reversed into refusal, the moment he stops volunteering for the next demolition.","fableDeep":"The metaphor is archaeological. Mesopotamia is the cradle of civilization and it's built of mud — so 'you took back the mud from which you're made and threw it at me like a fucking grenade' makes the lover a creature unbuilding itself into a weapon, clay to clay. Cities on that plain exist in layers: each one burned, each one rebuilt on its own wreckage — 'they just keep blowing it up and building up another' is the relationship's operating cycle, catastrophe as renovation. And the refrain quietly revises itself mid-song: 'I'd give it all up just to be with you' returns later as '*not* give it all up' — one word changed, and it's the entire recovery arc.","haiku":"Mesopotamia (ancient Babylon) is personified as a menacing force that disturbs and threatens the speaker, who faces death and feels weaponized violence directed at him.","opus":"Using a city that repeatedly burns and is rebuilt as its image, the song channels revulsion and betrayal toward someone who wounds the speaker even while he says he'd give everything just to be with them.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses a city — Mesopotamia — with a mix of dread and compulsion, describing a destructive relationship that mirrors the city's endless cycle of burning and rebuilding, with the narrator trapped between wanting out and wanting to stay.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written mid-Iraq-war, the endlessly bombed and rebuilt city carries a literal shadow — Mesopotamia is the ancient name for Iraq.","themes":["love & desire","anger & defiance","freedom & escape"],"means":"devotion to a cycle of demolition until one refusal ends it","built":"the refrain returns with one word changed reversing its entire polarity"},
"black-light-burns~new-hunger": {"haiku":"To join something greater one must surrender and descend into the depths; swimming requires letting go.","opus":"A cryptic call to abandon lazy complacency and cross into transformation, figured as getting to the water and giving yourself to the sea; the deeper read is an initiation or baptism—tearing down the waterfall and surrendering (coin in mouth, like payment to cross into death) to answer a beckoning new hunger.","sonnet":"A song about initiating others into something demanding and immersive — possibly a community, movement, or spiritual practice — where the price of entry is getting fully submerged, putting a coin in your mouth, and giving yourself over to the current completely.","src":"haiku","fable":"A recruitment pitch sung from inside the group doing the recruiting, and the ceremony it offers is a drowning. The chorus dangles belonging — if you want to swim with us, come to the water — while the verse spells out what baptism actually costs here: a coin placed in the mouth is Charon's fare for the dead, so wading out to your knees and giving yourself to the sea is initiation as funeral. Borland lets the invitation repeat until it sounds like a chant, which is the point — the hunger of the title belongs to the collective, not the convert.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"the collective's hunger enacted as an invitation to drown","built":"Charon's coin in the mouth reframes baptism as a funeral rite"},
"black-light-burns~on-the-bound":{"opus":"Wes Borland's project voices a man on the edge of an uncertain future, clinging to a single love as the one thing he needs while doubting the faith and reassurance offered him. The push and pull of indecision and self-poisoning runs beneath a plea for someone to lay their head down and promise everything will be alright, even as he believes it won't.","sonnet":"A song about codependent love and existential paralysis — the narrator clings to someone as the only thing he needs while simultaneously admitting he doesn't know what he's doing, can't decide anything, and creates his own misery. The request to just be held one more time comes with the honest admission that he doesn't believe it will help.","src":"sonnet"},
"black-light-burns~one-of-yours": {"haiku":"The speaker regrets choices made in relationships and self-sacrifice, watching those they cared for drift away and suffer from their mistakes.","opus":"A self-aware confession from a man who ruins the things he loves while convincing himself his intentions are good. Having driven away the people close to him and thrown away his security, he pleads to be released rather than kept, admitting the wreckage is his doing even though he never meant harm, that his presence has become a wound to the person he cares about.","sonnet":"A song about estrangement from a creator or institution — someone who gave everything to a cause or person, including their money and identity, now wants out and can't be the version of themselves that was needed, framing the request for release as both confession and farewell.","src":"haiku","fable":"Borland's resignation letter from Limp Bizkit, thinly veiled: giving the money away, sending the people off, the compulsion to write just one more song, and the plea to be let go all map onto walking out of one of the biggest bands in the world in 2004. The chorus's odd device carries the sting — he casts himself as one of yours, a possession that turned a color its owner never intended and became someone else, so the demand for release is voiced by the thing rather than the man. Meaning well all along and still being wrong is the closest it comes to peace: no villain, just a shape that could no longer exist inside what made it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape","shame & repression"],"means":"a shape that could no longer exist inside what made it pleads release","built":"the speaker voices himself as a possession that turned the wrong color"},
"black-light-burns~rid-of-me":{"haiku":"A jealous, possessive lover refuses to release their hold, demanding reciprocated trauma and pain so the other will regret ever meeting them.","opus":"Wes Borland's project reclaims PJ Harvey's obsessive standard, and the possessive menace survives intact: a narrator lashing a lover to himself, refusing to be discarded and threatening to twist a head off. Wounded pleading and vengeful desire tangle together, the covered lines turning devotion into a captor's grip that will not release its prey.","sonnet":"Obsessive, consuming desire refuses to release its object even as it curdles into something violent. The attachment is total, physically demanding, and openly threatening, trapping both parties in a loop neither can end without annihilation.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~scream-hallelujah":{"haiku":"Society numbs people through media consumption and controlled conformity, turning them into passive sheep unable to think or act. The song demands awakening from this manufactured stupor and blind compliance.","opus":"Wes Borland's acid sermon on media-drugged conformity, mocking a sedated public that pays taxes, watches screens and cheers its own numbness. The forced hallelujah is the sound of manufactured obedience.","sonnet":"A savage indictment of passive, media-sedated consumerism — the song describes a population that has been domesticated into numbness, told to shut up, pay taxes, and live vicariously through screens, with the sarcastic command to scream hallelujah being the only freedom left to them.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~so-alive":{"haiku":"Attraction to a stranger awakens magic and intensity, making the speaker feel vividly, urgently alive in their presence.","opus":"Wes Borland's cover of a Love and Rockets song about fixation on a stranger, intoxicated by wanting more than knowing her. The high of infatuation is the whole point, feeling vividly alive through pure attraction.","sonnet":"A narrator fixated on a striking, unfamiliar woman — cataloguing her physical presence, her effect on him — who finds that the pull of desire makes him feel intensely alive in a way he cannot fully share or contain.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~splayed":{"haiku":"The speaker fantasizes about revenge through celebrating the death and haunting of their enemy, revealing a repressed violent animal inside them.","opus":"A darkly comic revenge fantasy about outliving and haunting someone the narrator despises, gleefully imagining partying on their death and pestering them as a ghost. Beneath the jokes runs a real confession of a violent animal urge inside him that he insists is not truly who he is.","sonnet":"Bilious hatred for a specific person delivered as black comedy: the narrator fantasises about celebrating their death and returning as a ghost to terrorise them, while half-acknowledging the rage isn't entirely their own.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~stop-a-bullet": {"haiku":"Two people trapped in mutual cruelty acknowledge their transformation into destructive versions of themselves they fear to become.","opus":"Wes Borland confessing an ugly appetite for another's pain and the guilt tangled around it. The trigger handed over is a dare and a plea, someone terrified of what his own heart is hardening into.","sonnet":"A mutual confessional about a toxic relationship where both parties admit to taking pleasure in the other's pain — the speaker hands over their trigger and begs it not to be pulled, acknowledging their own cruelty while hoping for forgiveness.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession of relational sadism that refuses to stay one-sided: the speaker admits he feeds on his partner's pain and grows strong as she weakens, then the final verse hands her his own words back verbatim — she has acquired the identical taste, and the cruelty is revealed as mutual. The hook's image of surrendering his trigger to someone he begs never to pull it is the whole doomed contract: total vulnerability offered as a dare, trust indistinguishable from threat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"total vulnerability offered as a dare indistinguishable from threat","built":"the final verse gives the speaker's own words back verbatim to her"},
"black-light-burns~the-art-of-self-defense":{"haiku":"A dark fantasy where an unassuming aggressor arrives to claim or destroy, presenting violence as an equalizing or leveling force.","opus":"A grotesque little horror sketch: a sad pygmy in a filthy loincloth traveling to your house to snuff you out, weeping as he sits on your chest, indifferent to how you differ from anyone else. The absurd, nursery-rhyme repetition makes it creepier. Black Light Burns, Wes Borland's outfit, revel in this surreal, darkly comic menace built on a single unsettling image.","sonnet":"A diminutive, weeping figure who comes to snuff you out — tiny, pitiable, relentless, sitting on your chest. The art of self-defense the title promises is never actually delivered; the figure arrives anyway and makes no distinction between victims. The absurdity is the horror: you can't fight what looks like nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~the-colour-escapes":{"haiku":"A man's heart is dark and corrupted beneath its surface. He observes people trapped in false beliefs while the beauty and truth in the world disappears.","opus":"Wes Borland's contempt for interchangeable, faithless people whose blind devotion curdles into violence, set against the shame of revealing his own blackened heart; disgust at the world tangled with reluctant self-exposure.","sonnet":"A disillusioned speaker watching people sell their souls, follow books into violence, and mistake comfortable numbness for faith — the colour has drained from everything, leaving only coal-black cynicism about human worth.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~the-girl-in-black":{"haiku":"A mysterious, seductive woman with supernatural qualities appears at night; the song depicts her as an otherworldly temptress luring the narrator into darkness and obsession.","opus":"Wes Borland's gothic seduction-as-danger fable: a spectral woman who lures a man somewhere he can't return from. It runs on the old idea that desire and doom are the same door, dressed in desert-witch imagery and a warning you won't heed.","sonnet":"A supernatural femme fatale — a woman who enters without using doors, moves like a snake, breathes silk — leads a man into a cavern where he covers her with his body; the refrain about not being able to leave well enough alone names the self-destructive pull she represents.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~the-hate-of-my-life":{"haiku":"A speaker is caught in an intoxicating romantic relationship that both fuels and torments them; they can't resist despite the pain.","opus":"A toxic love rendered as helpless combustion, the singer bound and burning by someone he can't stop wanting even as it wrecks him. Desire and revulsion fused so tightly that loving her is indistinguishable from hating his own life.","sonnet":"A love-hate entanglement where the very thing that infuriates and ties the narrator in knots is also what ignites him — he is hog-tied by desire, hands bound from the inside, loving this person described as simultaneously the best and worst thing that has ever happened to him.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~the-mark": {"haiku":"A speaker bears an invisible mark of exile and burning desire; they want to burn the world and claim that fire as their own, leaving their mark through destruction.","opus":"A person carries an invisible mark or scar that torments them, wandering in circles back home and concluding that the destructive fire inside is theirs to own as they resolve to burn everything down and leave a mark.","sonnet":"The speaker carries an invisible internal wound — described as a mark or scar — that drives cycles of wandering, destruction, and return without resolution. Unable to ignore or extinguish it, they embrace the burning as their own even while it prevents them from breathing freely.","src":"haiku","fable":"The mark is a compulsion only its carrier can see — a brand that itches toward escape, destruction, anything but staying put. The song's real event is the failed getaway: he follows footprints through the snow, walks a full circle, and arrives back home, where the discovery lands that the fire was his own all along, not something out there to run toward. From that ownership the chorus stops sounding nihilistic and starts sounding vocational — if love and hate burn the same, the only honest move is to claim the burning and leave a mark with it, though the coda admits the cost: the scar never closes, and it is never enough.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape","shame & repression"],"means":"claiming the burning rather than running from it as the honest move","built":"footprints through snow trace a full circle back to what he fled"},
"black-light-burns~the-moment-you-realize-you-re-going-to-fall":{"haiku":"The speaker has witnessed hidden violence and decay, predicting the listener's inevitable exposure and fall. They document and celebrate violent revelation through photography and confrontation.","opus":"Wes Borland's project stitches two scenes together: a witness scarred by things only the sun has seen, and a hedonistic night raging through the city. Both circle the same voyeuristic thrill, an audience lined up waiting to watch someone fall.","sonnet":"A two-part song: the first half is a violent carnival of public humiliation and voyeurism around someone's fall from a building; the second half abandons that and becomes a night-out narrative about drinking, dancing, and hooking up in the city, the tonal break apparently intentional.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~tiger-by-the-tail":{"haiku":"Dangerous attraction and predatory excitement: the speaker holds a tiger by the tail—an unstable, thrilling grip—while dangers circle and escalation becomes inevitable.","opus":"Wes Borland's outlet away from Limp Bizkit prowls a charged, dangerous attraction. Locked eyes across a room draw two figures into a pair, the narrator hoping to get burned, warning that big cats hide claws and the ground is never safe. Grabbing the tiger's tail to deliver a kick then a kiss captures the reckless thrill of courting something predatory, danger and desire indistinguishable.","sonnet":"A man locks eyes across a room with a dangerously compelling woman and decides, knowingly, to take hold of something that could destroy him. The attraction is predatory on both sides — she projects power, he courts the risk — and the thrill of danger is precisely the point.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~torch-from-the-sky":{"haiku":"A violent force descends at night to punish the complacent—made of 'blackest light' like a falling torch, they crawl through mud, bathe their enemies in blood, and walk with the dead. Moral decay has left no love to give.","opus":"Wes Borland's project renders a post-apocalyptic massacre with unnerving, lullaby-soft calm, an army of raiders descending on a town to slaughter its people before the killing even starts. Beneath the cinematic horror runs a private, human terror of abandonment, the violence framed almost tenderly until it isn't.","sonnet":"Occupation and complicity braided together — a woman's trembling voice at the door, invaders coming through mud in the night, a couple who have run out of love and innocence and are now walking hand in hand with the dead. The torch falling from the sky is their arrival: catastrophic, made of darkness, impossible to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~we-light-up":{"haiku":"Two reckless lovers rage through the city all night, taking pictures and burning with energy, pursuing pleasure and danger together without stopping.","opus":"Wes Borland's ode to a self-destructive night that both lovers know will end badly and pursue anyway. The camera motif turns their recklessness into performance, glamour and bruises indistinguishable, thrill worth the certain regret.","sonnet":"A chaotic night out with someone reckless and magnetic — the two of them drink, crash parties, climb rooftops, and end up back at her place, knowing full well the night is trouble and choosing it anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"black-light-burns~your-head-will-be-rotting-on-a-spike":{"haiku":"Enemies and antagonists become blind and incapable, trapped in cycles of their own making. The narrator warns that cycles of conflict persist and breed only more conflict.","opus":"A bitter meditation on decline and mutual ruin, where everyone ends up blind, spent, and displayed like a warning. The real subject is how quickly the world stops being kind once you've nothing left to offer it.","sonnet":"About disposability and obsolescence — once you're no longer useful (no longer breeding, no longer productive), society turns cold and the collective fate is a kind of public execution, everyone's head eventually on a spike.","src":"haiku"},
"black-math~keys-to-the-castle":{"haiku":"A crew violently invades and dominates through aggressive takeover, theft, and showmanship, forcing surrender of power and treasures.","opus":"A swaggering hype anthem of conquest and domination, all bravado about rolling in, cleaning house and taking what's yours. Built for a game or trailer, it's pure competitive triumph, the villain-victor claiming the castle by force.","sonnet":"A dominance-flex narrative about a crew rolling in and taking over — they've already beaten the opponent, they're not leaving until handed the keys, and the whole thing is framed as a high-stakes game they've already won.","src":"haiku"},
"black-midi~near-dt-mi":{"haiku":"Environmental contamination from lead poisons a community, draining and staining those who inhabit polluted waters.","opus":"The experimental London band spin a parable around contaminated water, a solitary man drinking apart from a community obsessed with a supply that is toxic and suffocating. The recurring poison works on two levels, physical and mental, poisoning and hollowing him out while everyone around insists he is fine, dread accumulating toward a sense of being already lost.","sonnet":"Lead-contaminated water poisons a community while officials obscure the damage and residents are gaslit into believing nothing is wrong. A single solitary figure drinking outside maps the isolation of those most exposed — dismissed, stained, drained — while the water itself becomes both literal toxin and metaphor for systemic betrayal.","src":"haiku"},
"black-midi~slow-loud":{"haiku":"Time winds down and things crumble slowly; the speaker waits endlessly to reach somewhere or someone, watching decay unfold at a pace they cannot hurry.","opus":"Time runs out as something disintegrates by degrees before the narrator's gaze, each attempt answered with more delay and a bullet to the forehead offered as grim comfort. Black Midi, the London experimental band known for jagged, unpredictable structures, stretch this decay into trance-like repetition, the drawn-out chant enacting the very rot it names as patience sours into morbid stillness.","sonnet":"Something precious — a living thing, a hope, a world — decays at an agonising pace while the narrator watches and waits, unable to speed the process or reverse it. The resignation accumulates through repetition until waiting itself becomes the only act available.","src":"haiku"},
"black-orchid-empire~death-from-above":{"haiku":"An angel predator descends to raid and steal futures, reducing flesh and bone to programmed repetition before carrying prey home. Death arrives from ethereal heights.","opus":"Grand alt-metal imagery of flight, tombs and interstellar drift, framing a longing to be remade and carried home. Beneath the raptor and angel figures runs a wish to escape the loop of dying alone.","sonnet":"A sparse, oblique song about something descending — a raptor raiding a tomb, an interstellar angel drifting light years — that will both hollow the narrator out and carry them home; it reads as a meditation on death as both violation and release.","src":"haiku"},
"black-orchid-empire~deny-the-sun":{"haiku":"Religious coercion enforces erasure; the speaker refuses to renounce truth despite persecution, holding fast to internal conviction against theocratic domination.","opus":"An inquisition burns heretics to enforce a religion sold as the only truth, demanding lives for the godhead. The speaker refuses to renounce the sun even knowing it may be a beautiful lie, choosing a chosen certainty over coerced belief.","sonnet":"Faced with religious inquisition demanding recantation, the speaker refuses — \"my mind is not my own\" — insisting that the beautiful lie of the sun, whatever it represents, can never be undone, even at the cost of freedom or life.","src":"haiku"},
"black-orchid-empire~evergreen":{"opus":"An environmental and class complaint casting everyday people as the disposable fuel keeping the machinery alive, sickened by poisoned rain and fuel while a distant elite gazing down from gleaming towers stays clean and thriving above the wreckage they profit from.","sonnet":"Workers and the exploited are the blood of the machine — burned, drowned in kerosene, reduced to rust — while the ruling class remains evergreen and untouched, tending their garden from silver skylines while everything beneath them dies.","src":"sonnet"},
"black-orchid-empire~glory-to-the-king":{"haiku":"False prophets promise redemption through venom and deception; the speaker demands proof of shared humanity before kneeling to power cloaked in divinity.","opus":"Forced worship of a false king who invades with promises of gold, met by a demand that he prove he bleeds like everyone else. The hollow ritual of glory-to-the-king curdles as his true, treacherous face shows through eyes that no longer belong to anyone known.","sonnet":"A defiant rejection of a false ruler: the king arrives with promises of gold, a serpent tongue, and a false crown, but the speaker sees through the veil and demands proof of shared humanity — \"show me that you bleed like we do.\" The spirit in the whirlwind and the strangers' eyes frame the ruler as fundamentally alien, unworthy of the loyalty being demanded.","src":"haiku"},
"black-orchid-empire~my-favourite-stranger":{"haiku":"A fractured relationship where two people are lost, unable to communicate clearly about their division and pain.","opus":"Black Orchid Empire watch two people share hidden scars and cross dangerous lines, a woman torn and lost in the middle while a man who doesn't know keeps talking past her. The British trio's alt-rock frames a relationship unraveling in mutual incomprehension, the wall one built to stay contained becoming the thing to climb, each collapse offering the cold relief of being over.","sonnet":"Two people bound by shared scars circle each other in painful communication — she is torn between versions of their story, he remains oblivious. History is written in hidden wounds, walls are climbed only to keep the same person caged, and nothing gets resolved.","src":"haiku"},
"black-orchid-empire~singularity":{"haiku":"A black hole consumes everything, trapping time and loved ones in an inescapable spiral toward destruction and oblivion.","opus":"The London trio borrows the physics of a collapsing star as a figure for grief, life pictured as being drawn into an inescapable pit where light dies and quiet surrender is the toll for existing. As time stops answering and the pull tightens, the only exit leads downward, the astronomy of collapse standing in for loss and loved ones who can't be freed.","sonnet":"Grief pulls like a black hole — no light escapes, time distorts, and the loved ones lost are bound by something beyond undoing. The event horizon becomes a threshold where the only path forward is also the most terrifying, descending toward the monster's edge because there is no other exit from this particular kind of loss.","src":"haiku"},
"black-out-the-sun~almost-heaven":{"haiku":"Destruction wrought by the self becomes unbearable, prayer the only gesture toward salvation. Heaven remains out of reach but nearly tangible, held at arm's length by accumulated trauma that prayer alone cannot absolve.","opus":"A cry for help from inside a mutually destructive relationship, heaven always just out of reach after everything endured. The plea is whether anyone hears or cares, mixing self-blame with the refusal to deserve this pain.","sonnet":"A wounded speaker, exhausted by mutual destruction in a relationship, insists they don't deserve the pain inflicted and reaches toward a heaven that feels almost within grasp. The refrain \"in the end it wasn't all for nothing\" holds onto the faintest hope.","src":"haiku"},
"black-peaks~fate-i":{"haiku":"Youth faces an urgent choice between ignorance and enlightenment; collective action can transcend predetermined fate and ignite transformation despite the darkness surrounding us.","opus":"A generational reckoning that won't accept the planet's ruin as final, watching skylines go up in smoke yet insisting destiny can still be redrawn. The vow to catch fire and set things right carries the burden of a young generation handed a broken inheritance.","sonnet":"A plea for collective responsibility in the face of self-inflicted destruction — cities burning, ignorance and violence spreading, and the narrator asking whether humanity is actually willing to change or will simply let fate seal the world. The song is about the moment of potential that exists before things get irreversibly worse, and whether a generation will take it.","src":"haiku"},
"black-peaks~king":{"haiku":"Crown and darkness intertwine as the speaker ascends into sovereignty stained by moral corruption; kingship requires sacrifice of innocence.","opus":"A coronation as damnation: gaining power only to find wolves feeding on your heart and a devil you summoned at the door. The tension is that ascension and corruption are the same act, the new king already lost to the darkness he chose.","sonnet":"A newly crowned figure is warned that dark forces — wolves, devils — have been inadvertently summoned by their ascent to power, and despite the danger, the song holds a strange tenderness, suggesting light may yet fill the empty veins of the unready ruler.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~barf-bag":{"haiku":"A self-destructive hedonist rejects restriction and morality, advocating for excess and pleasure before death, treating transgression as freedom.","opus":"A nihilistic rally cry urges seizing life through theft, drink and excess, a self-declared vain egoist blaming himself for every ill while insisting every step marches everyone alike toward the grave. Black River, a Polish rock side-project with metal pedigree, dress hedonism as fatalism, the cascade of pills, drinks and offenses becoming a defiant shrug at mortality where taking becomes the only honest response to a doomed procession.","sonnet":"A self-proclaimed egoist argues that all human paths lead to the same grave regardless of virtue, so he may as well take what he wants now. The catalogue of excess is delivered with dark humor that barely masks an underlying nihilism about the equal futility of every life.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~breaking-the-wall":{"haiku":"Dismantling internal barriers; confronting truth requires escape from the walls we construct.","opus":"Aimed at the outcast kid branded a problem, this urges tearing down the defensive walls that trap you and rejecting the lies others sell as truth. A defiant plea to live authentically rather than rot in fear and other people's scripts.","sonnet":"A misfit labelled a problem child since birth is urged not to internalise the walls others build — or build one inside — because conformity through lies is the real prison. Breaking the wall means choosing truth over fear.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~crime-scene":{"haiku":"Murder and silent complicity haunt the witness; the speaker watches a killing, chooses silence, and becomes forever bound to the secret.","opus":"A witness to a suicide by gunshot, frozen face to face with the dying man and the devil in his eyes, keeping the secret of what he saw. The horror lodges as something gone for good and yet impossible to unsee.","sonnet":"A witness to a violent death — or perhaps the death is their own — in which a man with a gun turns it toward the narrator after a body already lies at their feet; the scene blurs into questions of escape, suicide, and an unspeakable secret.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~fight":{"haiku":"Inner monsters materialize behind eyes as unreality presses consciousness toward madness. Fear must be felt and confronted to transcend the boundary between sanity and dissolution.","opus":"A descent into psychosis rendered as whispers, shadows, laughter and visions both hellish and holy, angels speaking, a voice like a blade in the eye. Searching for who he is and the way out of hell, he insists the only way to survive and hold onto his soul is to fight blind on the battlefield of the mind.","sonnet":"Psychosis or haunting rendered from the inside: whispers, shadows, figures, laughter that shouldn't be there — the narrator has seen angels, seen hell, and is paralysed by the ice in their veins; the only answer is to fight blind, like a man on a battlefield with no eyes, seeking the exit from hell.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~free-man":{"haiku":"Madness builds while freedom becomes obsession. Killing memory and destroying theirs becomes path to escape.","opus":"A menacing vow to erase a controlling past and destroy everything belonging to another rather than lose one's freedom, the threat of madness cutting both ways as the singer insists the mind stays his own.","sonnet":"About someone trying to escape a controlling or traumatic relationship — the narrator is suffocated, can't flee, and oscillates between terror and a violent resolve to destroy the past and reclaim their freedom, warning that one of them will experience madness.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~free-man-accoustic":{"haiku":"Psychological liberation demands violent erasure of past identity and the suffocation of false bonds; the threat is sincere, the rebellion absolute.","opus":"A menacing severance from a toxic bond, the narrator vowing to burn every trace of the other to reclaim his freedom. The threats and repeated arrival read as the fury of someone whose selfhood was suffocated, freedom worth any madness to seize.","sonnet":"A speaker suffocated and cornered by someone's madness vows to kill the memory, destroy everything that belongs to the other, and reclaim freedom — the urgency is physical, breathless, and the new life begins precisely by severing the past.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~free-man-orchestration":{"haiku":"Suffocation and confinement persist despite yearning for freedom; madness emanates from an entangled other.","opus":"The same violent break for freedom recast with orchestration, a man promising to kill his memory and destroy all that belonged to the one who caged him. Underneath the menace is the terror of losing himself and the resolve never to.","sonnet":"A man fleeing a suffocating relationship — \"I cannot breathe, I cannot flee\" — decides to kill his memory and destroy everything belonging to the person pursuing him, arriving at \"I'm a free man\" not as celebration but as a survival declaration after the madness of being hunted by someone he could kill but chooses not to.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~isabel":{"haiku":"Darkness becomes refuge and truth becomes liability; the speaker invites others to abandon hope and dance in the wreckage of a world gone wrong.","opus":"An address to a woman gone sour on her existence, her job and her spirit, alternately jeering at everything and being told to just let it go and find something to live for. Dark-heaven imagery casts disillusion as its own grim shelter, freedom arriving only by surrendering the grudge.","sonnet":"A call to a woman named Isabel to stop taking everything so seriously: lift your face, dance like junkies in purple eden, and get a life rather than staying pissed off at work, lost souls, and the smiling hypocrites around you. The tone is irreverent and ultimately affectionate, urging her to slide free of whatever has turned the wrong way.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~liars":{"haiku":"Existential disconnection and moral compromise as inevitable features of human life and sanity.","opus":"A hard-rock cry against a world where lying is the price of belonging, the singer clinging to sanity while confessing that no honest man survives here. Paranoia and disgust wound tight.","sonnet":"A fragmented, existential protest about dishonesty being built into life — the narrator insists they're sane while questioning whether anyone in this world is real or honest. It's short and disordered, like a thought that won't complete itself.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~like-a-bitch":{"haiku":"Self-firing preserves dignity; the subject refuses complicity and walks backward into solitude.","opus":"A worker's kiss-off to a contemptible boss or lover, quitting on her own terms before she can be dismissed. The bravado of doubting the other's power and style masks the real point: refusing to stay loyal to something she never respected.","sonnet":"A speaker pre-empts being fired from a relationship or job by firing themselves first, dismissing the other's power and beauty as fraudulent and declaring they are leaving it all behind with friends still on their side.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~negative":{"haiku":"Worthlessness and invisibility accumulate as burden; anger erupts to rupture false games.","opus":"Rage at being belittled and manipulated, the narrator demanding to be heard louder after being made a fool. The song tracks anger hardening into confrontation, a refusal to keep shrinking into a speck of dust for someone else's game.","sonnet":"A speaker confronts someone who has manipulated and belittled them — \"you made a fool out of me all the time\" — and reaches the point of rage where self-doubt burns off and they demand the other person confess and speak louder.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~night-lover":{"haiku":"Serial infidelity and emotional numbness through alcohol, unable to form genuine connection or remember intimacy, trapped in cycles of betrayal.","opus":"A flat admission of habitual cheating, the intoxicated narrator unable to love with any depth, owning up to an anonymous encounter where neither party learned the other's name, then icily informing his partner he's betrayed them once more and can't even say when or in what place.","sonnet":"A confession of serial infidelity — one night, no names, drunk, can't remember which night or where. The narrator admits repeatedly that they cheated again, can't love strongly enough, and asks the broken-hearted partner to reach out anyway while knowing they don't deserve it.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~out-of-control":{"haiku":"Spirit and hope fuel the speaker's relentless drive despite mechanical disadvantage; belief becomes the only engine that matters when resources fail.","opus":"A racing metaphor for underdog determination, running on spirit rather than fuel or speed, trusting hope to reach the highest place. Being out of control and out of brakes is embraced as the price of pushing to the limit.","sonnet":"A racing metaphor for chasing a goal without the right equipment — running on heart alone, out of brakes and fuel, but trusting that spirit and hope will eventually put them at the top.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~punky-blonde":{"haiku":"Obsession paints itself as destiny: the beloved becomes mythic, and worship stands at the edge between adoration and annihilation.","opus":"Total infatuation with someone the narrator knows isn't his, desire framed as both his whole life and his torment. The demand to be loved quickly or not at all captures the manic, fire-and-fate intensity of hopeless want.","sonnet":"A song about obsessive, consuming love for someone who is clearly out of reach — the speaker knows everyone can see this person is not for them, but the desire is so total that they beg to be loved quickly or not at all, because the burning is unbearable.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~punky-blonde-live":{"haiku":"Obsessive love for someone unattainable, making another person the sole meaning of existence.","opus":"Obsessive, doomed adoration for someone the singer knows he can never have, love and pain fused into a fever. He'd rather burn in the fantasy than let the feeling die.","sonnet":"A raw confession of unrequited obsession — the narrator knows this person is unattainable and probably not real as idealized, but the sight of them ignites something that feels like the only proof of being alive. The self-awareness doesn't make the feeling less consuming.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~revolution":{"haiku":"Caged existence without ownership or freedom demands rebellion as the speaker rejects prescribed roles. Real life remains forever distant, replaced by performance of captivity.","opus":"A rallying cry against a caged existence of lies and servitude; refusing to be anyone's pawn, the narrator claims the rebel's role and calls for an uprising to vent the fury and seize freedom, urging the others that they aren't alone and must throw everything into the fight before the day ends in bloodshed.","sonnet":"Someone who has been caged, rule-following, and afraid declares they will not be a pawn or a slave any longer — the revolution is personal and collective, a release of anger toward real life, real blood in the veins, real truth instead of the reality show everyone else seems content to inhabit.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~silence":{"haiku":"Grief crystallized as permanent silence after loss, seeking dreams to replace connection no longer possible.","opus":"A grief ballad about the unbearable quiet left after a death, where the only inheritance is absence. The singer survives on dreams of words never spoken and an embrace he never got.","sonnet":"A grief song about someone who has died or permanently left — the narrator lives in the silence they left behind, unable to stop feeling their presence while knowing they're gone. The dream at the end is not escape but the only remaining closeness: imagining what was never fully said or given.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~street-games":{"haiku":"Dominance and control in conflict, where survival means facing enemies and accepting complicity in buying and selling.","opus":"A predatory power game between lovers-turned-enemies, control and desire treated as currency, love reduced to something bought and sold on the street. Menace masquerading as passion.","sonnet":"About a power struggle in a relationship that feels like combat — one person holds all the cards, the other is trapped face-to-face with a fate they can't run from; the imagery of being \"for sale\" and running from an enemy inside yourself suggests a dynamic of control and self-betrayal.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~too-far-away":{"haiku":"Electric proximity collapses distance as the speaker becomes an overwhelming presence haunting the beloved's consciousness, suspended between violation and salvation.","opus":"An unsettling study of proximity without contact, the narrator pressed close enough to unnerve while the other looks past them entirely. It reads as obsession or haunting, desire that thrives on the other's fear and its own unbridgeable distance.","sonnet":"A study in proximity and oblivion: the narrator stands directly behind someone, breathing hard enough to be heard, sending electric signals — yet the other person keeps looking around and seeing nothing. The gap between physical closeness and emotional or perceptual distance is the whole subject, the refrain \"I'm so close to you\" answered by \"too far away.\"","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~too-far-away-live":{"haiku":"Proximity paradox; closeness generating fear through overwhelming presence and electric intensity.","opus":"The live take on that same charged closeness, one presence looming behind another who senses but never sees them. The tension is erotic and threatening at once, nearness that only deepens the gulf and feeds on the other's dread.","sonnet":"A presence so close it can breathe on someone's back and feel their skin, yet the other keeps looking around and finding nothing — the watcher invisible, intimate, acknowledging that watching the other suffer should be reason enough to leave.","src":"haiku"},
"black-river~young-n-drunk":{"haiku":"Drift and intoxication offer escape from origins in darkness, perpetual youth maintaining a numbness that prevents growth or connection. The speaker floats untethered, offering neither commitment nor clarity.","opus":"A drifter's ode to numbness and dark freedom, wearing perpetual drunkenness as identity and escape. The pull on a follower into the same blackness makes it as much seduction into oblivion as self-portrait.","sonnet":"A dark, drifting voice from nowhere lures the listener into its blackness, boasting of perpetual youth and intoxication as a kind of freedom. The imagery of dark water and whispers frames the seduction as both nihilistic and irresistible.","src":"haiku"},
"black-sabbath~changes":{"haiku":"Romantic loss reshapes the narrator into someone unmoored, watching love slip away with only regret and the exhaustion of transformation.","opus":"Plain grief over a love's end, letting the closest companion he ever knew slip away and wishing he could return and unwind those years. Ozzy delivers it as raw, tearful regret with nothing to hide behind.","sonnet":"A man mourns the end of a love that once made the world whole — sharing years and days together — only for the world to blind his heart and send love astray, leaving nothing but tears and the wish to go back and change it all.","src":"haiku"},
"black-sabbath~children-of-the-grave":{"haiku":"Tomorrow's children rebel against a world of war and atomic fear, urged to spread love and bravery or risk becoming casualties of a dying society.","opus":"Black Sabbath urge the young to rise against a hateful, war-shadowed world and choose courage over doom. From 1971's Master of Reality, the pioneering metal band turn Vietnam-era dread into an unexpected plea for love and peace, warning that a generation raised on fear must act now or be lost.","sonnet":"A generation raised in the shadow of atomic annihilation marches against a world it never chose. The song asks whether love and peace can outlast nuclear fear, and charges the young with the only task that matters: spread that message or become the grave.","src":"haiku"},
"black-sabbath~dear-father":{"haiku":"Clerical abuse destroys innocence and faith; the victim transforms victimhood into retributive judgment against the abuser.","opus":"A survivor of clerical child abuse addressing the priest who ruined him, moving from wounded testimony to cold vengeance. Ozzy closes Sabbath's final studio album by naming the hypocrisy of a holy man who preyed while he prayed.","sonnet":"A survivor of clerical sexual abuse confronts their abuser directly — naming the betrayal of faith and trust, the hidden violence, and the broken life left behind — before turning the language of the church back on the perpetrator in a vow of retribution.","src":"haiku"},
"black-sabbath~end-of-the-beginning":{"haiku":"Digital existence fractures identity; the speaker navigates between control and automation, struggling to reclaim agency.","opus":"A late-career reckoning with mortality and free will dressed in sci-fi language about code and cloning, urging you to seize today before you become a ghost in someone else's machine. For a band at the literal end, the question of beginnings and endings is personal.","sonnet":"A cybernetic meditation on identity and agency — questioning whether the self is real or simulated, urging the listener to delete what no longer serves, resist being cloned or analysed into emptiness, and seize mastery of their own fate.","src":"haiku"},
"black-sabbath~killing-yourself-to-live-2009-remastered-version":{"haiku":"Society breeds self-destruction through conformity and false promises; authentic living demands rejecting collective delusion.","opus":"Weary defiance from men grinding their lives down for work and other people's expectations, insisting the whole rat race is a slow suicide. Written amid the band's own excess and burnout, it's a shrug at a world that punishes you either way.","sonnet":"A sardonic indictment of conformist society: people rot their lives away in obedience to a system that gives nothing back, and the speaker — possibly using substances to cope — urges the listener to open their eyes to the lies, even as the world spins into confusion and inversion around him.","src":"haiku"},
"black-sabbath~paranoid":{"haiku":"Someone experiences pervasive depression and mental suffering, unable to find joy or connection despite seeking meaning, ultimately recognizing their isolation may be permanent.","opus":"Black Sabbath's breakthrough title track, tossed off as album filler yet shaping early metal, gives voice to a disintegrating mind. Done with a partner who couldn't ease his turmoil, the narrator can't feel joy or find relief, begging someone to distract him and confessing that affection seems fake and contentment arrived too late, depression laid bare at furious speed.","sonnet":"A man cannot find satisfaction in anything — relationships, thoughts, ordinary pleasures — and watches happiness remain permanently out of reach as though he were constitutionally blind to it. He can advise others to enjoy life but confesses bitterly that for him it is already too late.","src":"haiku"},
"black-sabbath~peace-of-mind":{"haiku":"Existential wandering leaves the self fractured; the speaker seeks tranquility through acceptance of transience.","opus":"A plainspoken confession of depression and self-doubt from a man refusing the hero role, just a broken soul asking for something real and some quiet in his head. Its late-Sabbath humility is the opposite of doom bravado.","sonnet":"A weary, self-doubting wanderer who makes no claim to heroism or righteousness passes through the world seeking only internal quiet, caught between front lines he cannot win and a darkness he cannot navigate.","src":"haiku"},
"black-sabbath~symptom-of-the-universe":{"haiku":"Cosmic love transcends earthly suffering and dissolution, merging souls across dimensions while returning to maternal and paternal origins.","opus":"Beneath the proto-thrash riff, a strangely tender vision of love as a cosmic constant, cast in Sabbath's occult-fantasy imagery. Devotion outlasts death and rides off into eternity, the darkness giving way to an oddly hopeful vow.","sonnet":"A cosmic love song in which the speaker moves through mythological and celestial imagery — silver wombs, stolen tombs, magic oceans — to arrive at a simple declaration that love is the symptom and the meaning of existence itself, a force that transcends death.","src":"haiku"},
"black-sabbath~the-wizard":{"opus":"A benevolent wandering wizard whose passing magic dispels demons and lifts everyone's spirits, an early Sabbath oddity of pure good rather than doom.","sonnet":"A silent, solitary wizard passes through the world spreading magic without a word — evil flees him, tears become joy, clouds part — and everyone is left feeling fine, never knowing how or why, only that he came and went.","src":"sonnet"},
"black-stone-cherry~rain-wizard":{"haiku":"A magical figure arrives with rain to heal the land and restore wonder. The song celebrates mythic intervention and the promise of transformative power.","opus":"A Southern-rock tall tale about a mythic Rain Wizard with silver hair and moonlit robes who washes hands and brings magic to the land, arriving with the thunder to change the singer's ways. It reads like backwoods folklore set to riffs. Black Stone Cherry, the Kentucky Southern hard-rock band, give this its swampy, hook-heavy groove and campfire-legend charm.","sonnet":"A mythic figure who appears out of searching and thunder — silver-haired, robed in moonlight, walking paths no one else knows — and brings transformation to a land that needed it. The song is almost liturgical in its simplicity: the wizard arrives, washes hands, works magic, and then the rain comes again.","src":"haiku"},
"black-tongue~the-eternal-return-to-ruin":{"opus":"A crushing portrait of anguish as an intolerable, endless weight, where the drained and sobbing narrator has no power left to lift himself off the floor until a frigid, spectral whisper presents death's opened doorway as respite for the exhausted.","sonnet":"On the verge of collapse and unable to rise, a figure hears eternity itself whisper that a gate has opened — a passage for the weary, a bed for the weak — and weeps with exhausted relief at the invitation to finally stop carrying the weight and pass through.","src":"sonnet"},
"black-veil-brides~in-the-end":{"haiku":"A fighter approaching death questions legacy and whether his body matters when his soul remains. He accepts his end without fear, asserting that identity transcends the flesh.","opus":"A meditation on death and what survives it, facing mortality without fear and wondering who will keep a life's story alive once the body is gone. The song's conviction is that a person amounts to more than flesh: having fought and sacrificed for one bright moment, the narrator believes his essence will endure and rise again.","sonnet":"A defiant meditation on mortality and legacy — who will remember your story, whether the body that fought and sacrificed amounts to anything, and a refusal to be afraid of dying because identity and glory are not bounded by flesh.","src":"haiku"},
"black-veil-brides~lost-it-all":{"haiku":"Empire crumbles into humility; the speaker surrenders accumulated power and recognizes that all mortals fall before consequence.","opus":"A king who conquered everything and then lost it all, a parable about hubris and the blindness that power brings. The turn is redemptive: only in falling does he finally see the world he never looked at.","sonnet":"A once-powerful figure who ruled and conquered confesses that the empire they built has collapsed and left them broken and beseeching, arriving at the humbling realisation that everyone falls and that being saved requires finally letting go of the image of invincibility.","src":"haiku"},
"blackbook~my-darkest-memory":{"haiku":"The beloved transforms into tormentor through accumulation of small cruelties—stolen joy, broken faith, systematic destruction. They remain unforgettable precisely because the damage carved deepest.","opus":"A toxic love catalogued through a string of violent images, the beloved as favorite enemy who charms, wrecks and turns away. The push-pull of attraction to someone who keeps hurting you is the whole ache.","sonnet":"Addressed to a destructive lover catalogued through bodily violence metaphors — pulled legs, broken spine, painted-black homes — the speaker paradoxically calls this person their favourite enemy and darkest memory. The contradiction of damage and devotion is never resolved.","src":"haiku"},
"blackbook~you-are-strange":{"haiku":"The lover's strangeness becomes trustworthy precisely because they defy convention and refuse apology, turning alienation into sanctuary. Their refusal to change promises stability in a world of false improvement.","opus":"A love song to strangeness itself, the narrator finally trusting and talking to someone precisely because they're odd. It's also a release from self-pity and shame over anger, the strange lover the first one worth keeping unchanged.","sonnet":"A speaker celebrates a uniquely strange person who broke through their distrust and allowed honest anger and real conversation for the first time. The repeated hope \"I hope you'll never change\" makes strangeness the highest compliment.","src":"haiku"},
"blackbriar~snow-white-and-rose-red":{"haiku":"Forced separation of symbiotic twins becomes mutual mourning; the bond transcends comprehension and defies the world's fear of their unity.","opus":"The fairytale sisters recast as lovers torn apart by a fearful world, warmth turned to frostbite and dread by forced separation. The gothic frame carries a queer grief: a bond outsiders couldn't understand, so they destroyed it.","sonnet":"Two sisters speak to each other across forced separation, each mourning what the other's absence has cost them — one hollowed by hatred, one frozen by grief — united by a bond the world feared and tore apart.","src":"haiku"},
"blackfield~open-mind":{"opus":"A songwriter offering a track pulled straight from his unguarded imagination, half-embarrassed by his mood swings, describing dreamlike images and a mysterious girl, and marveling that ideas simply arrive out of the void.","sonnet":"A quietly introspective account of creative inspiration — a song that emerges from an open, unstable mind, where a half-remembered woman and falling ideas combine into something that cannot make you rich or high but can blow your mind.","src":"sonnet"},
"blackgold~back-with-another-one":{"haiku":"Unstoppable disruption and collective energy override opposition as the speaker drives forward with refusal to slow.","opus":"A rap-rock band's swaggering return, all wrecking-ball bravado and beef with those who tried to block them. The real engine is the outsider's chip on the shoulder, proving the doubters wrong by dragging the misfit crowd into motion.","sonnet":"A defiant return after attempts to suppress or sideline the band — \"I hear you tried to cock block my band\" — the speaker announces themselves as a wrecking ball back at speed, demanding the crowd acknowledge their comeback.","src":"haiku"},
"blackgold~i-ain-t-goin-out-like-that":{"haiku":"Street aggression asserts survival dominance against threats through force and refusal to submit.","opus":"A cover leaning into Cypress Hill's stoned gangsta menace, cop confrontations and refusals to back down. The hook is pure survival defiance, the pose of someone who'd rather go out swinging than be pushed under, more attitude than autobiography.","sonnet":"Street-survival rap in the Cypress Hill mold: the narrator arms himself against rivals and police, refusing to be taken down without a fight. Three verses escalate from gang conflict to a roadside standoff with a cop, the refrain \"we ain't goin' out like that\" acting as a defiant oath of mutual self-preservation.","src":"haiku"},
"blackgold~it-s-art":{"haiku":"Artistic integrity resists compromise and industry exploitation; the speaker refuses validation and creates solely for themselves.","opus":"A defiant underground rapper railing against fakes, industry gatekeepers and the myth that hard work alone gets you there. The vulture imagery frames the true artist as prey circled by opportunists, still insisting he does it for himself.","sonnet":"A frustrated artist rages against an industry run by fake copycats and scene snitches who never pay the artist — declaring that hard work alone doesn't get you there, insisting this is art regardless of whether anyone approves.","src":"haiku"},
"blackgold~old-school-sound":{"haiku":"Defiant nostalgia reclaims authentic hip-hop against criticism, celebrating provocation and raw confidence.","opus":"A cheeky manifesto for reviving old-school hip-hop energy, stuffed with weed jokes and pop-culture name-drops while flipping off critics who call the style dated. Under the goofing is genuine reverence for the era's rope-chain swagger and craft.","sonnet":"A brash, comedic rap-rock manifesto demanding a return to raw, unfussy music over polished commercial product — the narrator dismisses critics (\"fuck off\"), namedrop Cypress Hill, Joe Rogan, and Paul Revere, and positions himself as the freshest, most dangerous act in the room precisely because he doesn't care about the rules.","src":"haiku"},
"blacktoothed~novocaine":{"haiku":"Mental overstimulation and emotional numbness collide; medication offers fleeting relief from constant overwhelm.","opus":"An overstimulated, anxious mind begging for numbness to quiet the endless stream of thoughts. Cycling through medications to focus, calm down and lift up, the speaker craves Novocaine just to stop drowning in himself.","sonnet":"A neurodivergent person overwhelmed by internal noise and external stimulation — understimulated in some ways, overstimulated in others, unable to track time or follow direction, they reach for medication and metaphorical Novocaine to quiet the spiral and stay functional.","src":"haiku"},
"blackwater-holylight~around-you":{"haiku":"Pervasive darkness clings to the skin and soul, impossible to shed or escape no matter the distance traveled. The speaker questions whether they can guide or shelter or merely add to the weight.","opus":"An offer to be someone's guiding arrow or shelter, tangled with the frustration of being ignored and kept at a discomforting distance. The heavy psych haze mirrors a closeness that surrounds yet never resolves.","sonnet":"A presence described as a darker shade pervades everything and refuses to fade; the speaker circles between wanting to be an arrow guiding someone further and a shelter holding them in discomfort. Exhausted and unable to speak, they remain orbiting at a distance.","src":"haiku"},
"blackwater-holylight~involuntary-haze":{"haiku":"Involuntary silence and consequence; the speaker fights for light while reaped by their actions.","opus":"A slow reckoning with a relationship built on the other's lies, the narrator seeing through the pretense of having a place in their life. The haze is the fog of self-deception both parties maintained, dissolving into a clear-eyed goodbye.","sonnet":"A relationship examined through the fog of self-deception: the speaker sees through a pattern that was always arranged for the other person's benefit, names the lies spat out in vain, and concludes the other never actually made room for them — involuntary haze as both the speaker's condition and the other's manipulation.","src":"haiku"},
"blackwaters~forget-myself":{"haiku":"A person becomes so absorbed in an obsessive attraction that they lose themselves, unable to stop thinking about someone who may or may not reciprocate.","opus":"Indie-rock self-erasure dressed as a night out, the narrator dissolving into obsession with a girl he casts as untouchable while branding himself the loser. It captures that young insomniac stage where wanting someone becomes a way of disappearing.","sonnet":"The disorienting crush of someone who occupies your mind completely — a loser falling apart over a girl who's out of reach, losing himself in the knowledge that she's watching.","src":"haiku"},
"blackway~what-s-up-danger":{"haiku":"Challenge and transgression; danger approaches as both threat and calling.","opus":"A daredevil boast to danger itself, courting long odds, leaping off high rises, driving blindfolded at full speed while his mother wonders where she went wrong, greeting peril like an old friend. He embraces turbulence, sharks and the unseen because he goes hard however black it gets.","sonnet":"Defiance as identity — I like high chances that I might lose, I like it all on the edge, tall buildings to leap off. Doubters made the arrival more marvellous, former foes are worn as badges, the insanity is on its own throne. The danger is sought, not fled.","src":"haiku"},
"bladee~girls-just-want-to-have-fun":{"haiku":"Loss and memory dissolve into ephemeral connections; the speaker cannot hold onto intimacy without it slipping away.","opus":"Sharing only a title with the Cyndi Lauper anthem, Bladee turns it into a hazy plea to be let go, the beloved reduced to a ghost you can't hold. The cloud-rap ache is dissolution, folding into himself as connection slips away.","sonnet":"The speaker, ghost-like and unable to be held, keeps folding in on themselves — \"fold in on myself, I fold so close\" — while a longed-for connection remains just out of reach; the tender plea \"someone like me\" frames the whole as a quiet elegy for belonging.","src":"haiku"},
"blakswan~stomach":{"haiku":"Witnessing catastrophic violence leaves the survivor haunted by inaction, their guilt mingling with terror and helplessness. The body registers trauma through fire and teeth, memory bleeding into present moment.","opus":"A visceral trauma scene, witnessing someone bleed out and being frozen at the door, unable to help. The horror deepens with the wish that the dying had the gun, guilt and helplessness fused into something unbearable.","sonnet":"Witnessing someone haemorrhage from a stomach wound, the speaker is immobilised at a door, feet in fire and teeth in pliers, caught between wishing for rescue and wishing they had the gun themselves. The visceral scene is both literal emergency and metaphor for helpless, traumatised love.","src":"haiku"},
"blanck-mass~odd-scene":{"haiku":"A disorienting scene where power dynamics and identity are questioned through fragmented exchanges, with the speaker resisting manipulation and control. The odd scene involves confusion about who has power and what makes someone dangerous or complicit.","opus":"Benjamin Power warps snatches of menace and media saturation into a paranoid collage about power, surveillance and being watched. The disorientation is the point, a sense of dread at a culture that consumes and controls through the screen.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who thinks they hold power but is actually being watched and controlled by the same forces above them — the narrator sees through the performance of danger and dominance, insisting that the \"hell hammer\" and the TV persona are the same costume. The chorus \"what in fresh hell\" signals bewilderment at the whole absurd scene.","src":"haiku"},
"blanck-mass~please":{"haiku":"Ash and fire consume kingdoms while love burns. Exile and starting again offer only ashes—the cycle repeats.","opus":"An apocalyptic hymn about razing everything to start over, the cinders keeping us in our place; a warning that we turn into what we despise, paired with the plea to raise something new from the fire.","sonnet":"About civilisational collapse and the necessity of starting again — fallen kings, wasted wars, a world where we become what we hate; the song urges burning the old order down and building something new in its place, with no nostalgia for what preceded the ashes.","src":"haiku"},
"blanck-mass~shit-luck":{"haiku":"Persistent bad fortune and obliteration—the speaker has no luck, no escape, and no one believes them as degradation and erasure accumulate relentlessly through generations.","opus":"Benjamin Power turns a cascade of misfortune into pummeling noise-pop, a portrait of someone hitting bottom while nobody believes or comes looking. The chant of ruined luck escalates into total obliteration, dread swallowing the self whole.","sonnet":"A figure facing total annihilation — personal, existential — told flatly that no one will come looking, no one will believe them, and they are simply out of luck: obliteration is seeping in from all sides and there is nowhere to appeal.","src":"haiku"},
"blanket~blue-eyes":{"haiku":"Hypnotic abstraction loops through perception, the mind spiraling into geometric patterns and light until all reference points dissolve. Blue eyes—symbol of connection—become misled, spiraling the internal landscape into recursive doom.","opus":"A shoegaze reverie of synesthetic hypnosis, sound and light spiraling into abstract shapes. The blue eyes misled suggest beauty that disorients, the self dissolving into a whirling, sunlit trance.","sonnet":"A hypnotic gaze — blue eyes that spiral and mislead — is rendered through synesthetic imagery of abstract shapes dancing on beams of light, turning clockwise into the observer's head. Earth movement and flowers in bloom deepen the sense of sensory overwhelm and surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"blanket~white-noise":{"haiku":"The speaker smothers someone's passion and presence like static, extinguishing their vitality and draining their oxygen bit by bit through the indifference of a fading relationship.","opus":"A cold, dissociated snapshot of a relationship burning out, its figures dulled and distant while desire drains under strain. The narrator paints himself as a short-lived spark that consumed his partner. Blanket are a UK post-rock and shoegaze band, and the film-like, muted violence of the scene suits their wide, textural sound.","sonnet":"A suffocating relationship told in static and slow motion — the speaker moved through the other person's world like a TV flicker, a flame consuming all their oxygen, and in the end drowned out whatever signal they were trying to receive. Passion fading under that kind of pressure is not a slow drift but an erasure.","src":"haiku"},
"bleach~hand-grenade":{"haiku":"Instability and danger accumulate; the speaker perceives another as inherently destructive and distances themselves before detonation renders both casualties.","opus":"A weary severance from someone volatile and unstable, the narrator counting the years wasted pretending their leftover love meant something. The grenade image nails it: a person too dangerous to stand near, so the only sane move is distance.","sonnet":"A clean-break address to someone the narrator has finally classified as too dangerous to keep close — \"a stray hand grenade\" who keeps messing with fire and is understood to be insane. Years wasted on them become the evidence; the verdict is simply: stay away from me.","src":"haiku"},
"bleary-eyed~heaven-year":{"haiku":"Fragmentation and abandonment distort perception; the speaker clings to broken promises while the ground shifts beneath their attempted faith.","opus":"A weary catalog of broken promises and stolen time, urging someone lost in false certainties to keep trying even when there seems to be nothing left inside to find.","sonnet":"About persisting through broken promises and disillusionment — the speaker keeps trying even when it feels like nothing inside is real, holding to truth like a seal while acknowledging that everything attempted from within seems to yield nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"bleech-9-3~ceiling":{"haiku":"Numbed suffering and suppressed pain generate rage that seeks transcendence; the speaker drowns in violence and silence, desperately attempting to escape the weight of internal anguish.","opus":"A portrait of a young man raised in violence who walks out to end his life, the red moon and inner voice marking the edge. The yearning to touch the ceiling and kill the feeling is the desperate wish to feel anything after years of numbness.","sonnet":"A man who survived a home of violence and hidden pain is drawn one night to the edge, the moon going red and a voice in his head urging him not to be scared; the refrain \"I wanna touch the ceiling / I wanna kill the feeling\" is both a cry for release from numbness and a suicide ideation framed as reaching upward. The shift to first person at the close makes clear the narrator is looking back at someone they almost lost.","src":"haiku"},
"bleed-from-within~flesh-and-stone":{"haiku":"Inherited catastrophe binds the young to the failures of elders, a legacy of greed and ambition that drowns the innocent. Extinction feels imminent and deserved, carved into flesh and stone by those who failed to protect.","opus":"A furious generational indictment of leaders who wrecked the world and left the young to inherit the ruin. Framed as an ecological and political reckoning, it refuses to clean up failures built on blood money and greed.","sonnet":"An ecological and generational fury directed at leaders whose greed has mortgaged the future — \"why mourn the past when it's the future that's dead\" — calls for collective reckoning against a legacy of environmental and political failure. Oceans washing away thrones and a plague of gold anchor the apocalyptic imagery.","src":"haiku"},
"bleed-from-within~fracture":{"opus":"A relationship that was doomed from the start collapsing into self-blame, the narrator confessing he tore himself apart trying to make room for the other, failed everyone, and now edges toward the grave believing salvation is a myth.","sonnet":"About a relationship that was wrong from the start and was ultimately destroyed by the narrator's own desperate attempts to make it fit — they tore a hole in themselves to accommodate the other person, found nothing on either side, and now approach death-like collapse unable to offer salvation to either party.","src":"sonnet"},
"bleed-from-within~overthrone":{"haiku":"Power must be seized from corrupt leadership through collective fury, the masses finally recognizing manipulation and false ascent. The throne itself becomes disposable once the spell breaks.","opus":"A revolt against a false leader digging his own grave while the wronged take the throne and feed him to the lions. Behind the metalcore fury is betrayal by a man of many faces who earned nothing but contempt.","sonnet":"A call to topple corrupt leaders who dug graves for their followers, with the crowd chanting \"throw them to the lions\" as the moment of reckoning arrives. The speaker frames surviving the leaders' betrayal as the precondition for collective liberation.","src":"haiku"},
"bleed-from-within~the-end-of-all-we-know":{"haiku":"Progress stalls while insanity deepens; the speaker sheds old skin to rise from ashes, refusing to stand alone and denying warning signs of collapse.","opus":"A song about clawing your way back from years spent broken and pleading for relief, shedding the old self and rising renewed toward a freedom finally within reach. Scottish metalcore band Bleed From Within, who fought back from early obscurity, turn apocalyptic dread into a shared vow of survival, refusing to face it alone.","sonnet":"After years of internal collapse and cycles of insanity, a narrator finally sheds his old skin and chooses to stop standing alone in the face of warning signs. From the ashes of that breakdown comes a fragile liberty, hard-won and shared with others rather than endured in isolation.","src":"haiku"},
"bleed~killing-time":{"haiku":"Release and transformation demand surrender of the past so the present can emerge from beneath accumulated debris.","opus":"An eerie conviction of having encountered someone in a past he can't place, striving to mature by flipping through his history until all he once knew has fallen away. Slackening his hold turns into the strain of setting her loose from his hands, that release framed like watching a clock tick down.","sonnet":"About surrendering the past and releasing attachment — the speaker wants to let go of something or someone held too tightly, recognizing that freedom requires letting what you've known die.","src":"haiku"},
"bleed~silver":{"haiku":"Words choke while eyes fail to see beyond death. Silence replaces all—the knife ends what understanding cannot.","opus":"A wounded withdrawal, telling someone to back off and let him work through the pain alone, fixated on the mystery of death and on the other's failure to prove where any of us go once we're gone.","sonnet":"A farewell to someone whose stoic exterior — silver, cold, wordless — conceals the fact that they are coming undone; the speaker has gone as far as they can and now hands back the pain, acknowledging that nobody's eyes can reveal what comes after death.","src":"haiku"},
"blessthefall~wake-the-dead":{"haiku":"The oppressed rise violently from burial; the speaker refuses to stay silent, bringing the dead to witness judgment on those who tried to bury them.","opus":"A defiant answer to someone who tried to bury the speaker and silence him, warning that pushing too hard only wakes what should have stayed dead. Dragged through flame or drowned, he rises anyway, promising that the tormentor was born to expire.","sonnet":"A defiant address to those who tried to keep the speaker buried — 'do you really wanna wake the dead?' — threatening resurrection and collective uprising, with iron sharpening iron and the risen refusing to be drowned or burned, turning threat into inevitability.","src":"haiku"},
"blight-town~argument-bargument":{"haiku":"Cynicism calcifies when wishes prove hollow and silence becomes unbearable, the speaker demanding clarity from someone who ghosted them. Frustration blooms from the gap between honest feeling and the other's predictable evasion.","opus":"A wry, wounded post-argument monologue from someone iced out and ghosted, begging just to be told what he did wrong. The self-styled cynic's flippancy barely masks how much the sudden silence hurts.","sonnet":"A speaker nursing exhaustion and quiet resentment after a falling-out — throwing pennies down a wishing well, reading until losing control — demands to be kept in the loop and told why what they did was wrong, while pleading not to be ghosted. The wry self-label \"the cynicist\" holds bitterness at arm's length.","src":"haiku"},
"blind-melon~no-rain":{"haiku":"A lonely person with a plain life watches rain and wants companionship. He reads to escape the pain of sleeplessness and lack of connection despite craving someone's constant presence.","opus":"Shannon Hoon's ode to depressive listlessness and social misfitting, wanting only someone to promise they'll be there on waking. Its sunny melody masks a plea from someone who sleeps all day and knows the world finds him strange.","sonnet":"Depression as a plain, uneventful existence — sleeping all day, reading to stay awake, wanting only someone who will always be there when you wake up, with the narrator fully aware his life seems crazy from outside but unable to change it.","src":"haiku"},
"blind8~abandoned":{"haiku":"Betrayal and abandonment transform into fuel for defiant resurrection as the speaker vows to survive despite being left behind, reclaiming agency through refusal to perish.","opus":"Betrayal by someone never really on your side, who traded loyalty for money like it was worthless. Left behind and wished dead, the speaker swears to rise anyway, turning the urge to watch the betrayer burn into proof he survived.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who used and abandoned the speaker — starting fires, leaving them here, never being real — that ends in the speaker rising again regardless, watching the other person's true face of hollow desire and wanting, finally, to watch them burn.","src":"haiku"},
"blind8~overcome-the-darkness":{"haiku":"Collective resistance against systemic oppression and hatred, sun as symbol of persistent hope despite institutional violence.","opus":"A defiant collective vow to outlast persecution by keeping hope and the heart's light burning. Refusing to abandon home or forgive their oppressors, they trust the dawn will always return so a way through can be found.","sonnet":"A collective resistance anthem addressed to an oppressed community facing hate and spokes put in their wheels — the burned leaves on the tree are losses already suffered, but the sun will always rise to light a path through. The plea is communal: we fight this together, we do not forgive, and the strongest remain.","src":"haiku"},
"blindside~pitiful":{"haiku":"The guilt of wounding someone who loved you, unable to face the damage you caused.","opus":"A Swedish Christian rock band recasting the crucifixion from the sinner's side, the narrator driving the nails yet met with a gaze that refuses to look away. The one who deserves pity is the betrayer, saved only by the love he keeps evading.","sonnet":"A speaker who betrayed Christ — hammering the nails, looking away from those searching eyes — now finds that the only thing that can save him are the very eyes he refused to meet; shame and redemption circle each other in a confessional that refuses to end neatly.","src":"haiku"},
"blink-182~here-s-your-letter":{"haiku":"Communication failure and emotional isolation drive a speaker toward numbness, unable to repair a relationship crumbling into silence.","opus":"A self-harming spiral inside a dying relationship, pleading for a little longer to repair what's already coming undone as it all disintegrates. The pop-punk sheen barely holds back real despair and the dread of losing the last person who felt as he did.","sonnet":"A failing relationship is addressed in a letter that cannot hold its meaning together, where apologies circle back into resentment and the speaker alternates between wanting to fix things and recognising the whole structure may be cursed. Silence and breakdown are the only constants.","src":"haiku"},
"bliss-n-eso~my-life-feat-ceekay-jones":{"haiku":"Claiming ownership of your path; moving through life at your speed without external judgment.","opus":"An Australian hip-hop road anthem about shrugging off doubt and other people's expectations to drive your own life on all green lights. The whole point is freedom as forward motion, self-belief as its own fuel.","sonnet":"A hip-hop meditation on self-determination and forward momentum — the speaker refuses fame and convention in favour of riding his own frequency, treating each day as a fresh departure point and green lights as the only signals worth following.","src":"haiku"},
"blitz-union~revolution-zardonic-remix":{"opus":"An environmental and anti-corruption protest anthem, calling out silent leaders and polluting corporations, demanding action over words and equal rights, and rallying listeners to be today's revolution.","sonnet":"A protest song demanding action against environmental destruction and political corruption — the narrator calls on everyday people to become the revolution since leaders are bought off by corporations that poison oceans and air, insisting that words without action are worthless.","src":"sonnet"},
"blklst~let-go":{"opus":"Addiction told as a relationship with a charming, terrifying figure that hollows a person out piece by piece, voiced partly by a friend watching helplessly. The addict, broken and alone amid the wreckage of his rage, wishes he had refused the first time and begs, finally, to be released from it.","sonnet":"A person watching a friend destroy themselves through drug addiction — wanting them to stop, exhausted by the damage — told partly in third-person confession from the addict's own voice: he danced with addiction thinking it looked fun, now wishes he had said no from the start.","src":"sonnet"},
"bloc-party~banquet":{"haiku":"A speaker embodies raw power and sexual intensity while rejecting false performance and pretense. They critique others' denial and compromise while demanding others accept them completely or step aside.","opus":"Kele Okereke's coiled account of desire and the friction of becoming an adult, wanting to bite rather than destroy. Post-punk tension made physical, the pull toward another body tangled with self-transformation and fire.","sonnet":"A song split between lust and spiritual restlessness — the narrator wants to bite not destroy, is on fire with desire for a specific woman with a dirty mind, while also feeling like an outsider becoming something harder and more adult.","src":"haiku"},
"bloc-party~helicopter":{"haiku":"Youth worship surface-level American culture and celebrities while failing to grow, repeating family mistakes in an endless cycle of mediocrity.","opus":"A twitchy takedown of a hollow, entitled young man coasting on empty swagger, ignorant and greedy, urged to drop his crude posturing and grow up while the chorus keeps needling whether he expects to be rescued by fate. Bloc Party are the British indie-rock band whose 2005 debut made this jittery, angular sound a generational touchstone.","sonnet":"A skewering of a particular breed of performative American masculinity — born a liar, James Dean-posturing, saving the world in blue jeans, repeating his father's mistakes while queuing for junk food and praying for a miracle that statistics say won't come. The European chorus is the dry punchline: this kind of guilt and shame could only be grown on this particular soil.","src":"haiku"},
"bloc-party~traps":{"opus":"Bloc Party inhabit a predatory seducer, all lurid come-ons and cartoon menace as he lures a younger target toward a trap dressed up as pleasure. Kele Okereke plays the role knowingly, the boom-boom-room patter exposing the sleaze rather than endorsing it, a sharp portrait of manipulation disguised as flirtation.","sonnet":"An older, experienced person sexually pursues a naive younger person under the guise of being their education and first thrill, framing the seduction as irresistible even while acknowledging the younger person is walking into a trap.","src":"sonnet"},
"blood-command~a-villain-s-monologue":{"haiku":"A predatory figure boasts superiority over a victim, claiming inevitable victory and the right to destroy.","opus":"Blood Command inhabit a preening antagonist who belittles a would-be hero as a mere interlude with no destiny for greatness, ordering him to keep his feet on the floor. The Norwegian band's death-pop relishes the theatrical menace, the villain confessing he wants to torch the world while mocking his opponent's rambling, a sneering power fantasy of superiority delivered with cartoon relish.","sonnet":"A theatrical villain delivers a crushing takedown of a challenger who overestimates himself — promising fire, barbecue, and holy terror — before the mask slips and the speech is addressed to Satan himself, deflated mid-monologue by a simple refusal to yield.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-command~all-i-ever-hate-about-is-you":{"haiku":"Hatred concentrates entirely on the other as the speaker's contempt becomes their primary essence.","opus":"A gleeful diss track built on the pose of untouchable superiority, the rival dismissed as nothing while the singer flaunts her reign. The garbled title hook betrays the truth beneath the boredom: this much contempt is really obsession.","sonnet":"Savage dismissal of a rival who strains for the throne but keeps being outclassed — from wish to Prada, nada versus Ace. The boredom is weaponised: winning so much there's no room for the gold, yet still unable to stop hating the person who keeps trying and keeps failing.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-command~bare-witness":{"haiku":"Compelled witnessing of violence or truth, bearing testimony to atrocity.","opus":"Apocalyptic bravado casting the band as agents of a righteous doomsday, judging the fallen and forcing them to bow. The punk-metal glee is in the certainty of being on the winning, superior side, a fantasy of moral reckoning turned to spectacle.","sonnet":"A reckoning-day confrontation where the narrator and their group refuse to be scarred and declare themselves the ones holding the knife — stepping on the crown has consequences, and today is doomsday for the one who fell from grace. \"I would never, ever wanna be like you\" is the final verdict.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-command~ctrl-art-delete":{"opus":"A sneering assault on posers and fame-chasers who'd sell out anyone for clout, mock authenticity, and only wear the Slayer logo without caring for the music, promising vengeance on their empty egos.","sonnet":"A cutting takedown of a poseur and hypocrite — someone who performs authenticity and edginess while being fundamentally hollow, willing to sell their mother for fame and faking their way through a subculture they don't genuinely inhabit.","src":"sonnet"},
"blood-command~forever-soldiers-of-esther":{"haiku":"A child indoctrinated into righteous violence transforms wounds into pride and fear into weaponized loyalty, singing destruction as maternal obedience while marching toward an imagined enemy.","opus":"A chilling ballad of a mother arming her child as a holy warrior, filling him with pride and a sword to make the heathens' heads roll. It reads as a study of how devotion and parental love get weaponized into fanaticism, the boy marching on unquestioning.","sonnet":"A child soldier armed with a mother's religious commandments marches against mockers and heathens, singing her words back to her as a pledge — the chorus \"keep your sword high\" turns maternal devotion into a war hymn carried through adulthood.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-command~heaven-s-hate":{"haiku":"Aggressive superiority and contempt for weakness, positioning the speaker as divine and others as inferior.","opus":"A snarling declaration of dominance dressed in blasphemy, the narrator claiming godlike supremacy over spineless rivals. All swagger and contempt, a middle finger set to hardcore punk.","sonnet":"A speaker claims the position of God's first creation, dismissing a crowd of spineless rivals as beneath them — demanding submission, refusing negotiation, and declaring their identity impossible to erase in a relentless posture of dominance.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-command~nuns-guns-cowboys":{"haiku":"Ecstatic surrender to collective ritual shields from death's reality, the singing crowd discovering everything worth living for through orchestrated abandon. Despair and transcendence merge in the space of worship.","opus":"An ecstatic, apocalyptic dance where the narrator hears angels while others hear only death, defiant against anyone calling it a crutch. It's utopian abandon at the end of the world, meaning wrung from the party as everything burns.","sonnet":"A true believer responds to a sceptic with defiance: where others hear silence, they hear angels singing, and they refuse to have their faith dismissed as a crutch. The grotesque imagery of someone caught with a gun and cash tips into black humour as the speaker watches the world be laid to rest.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-command~the-plague-on-both-your-houses":{"haiku":"Literary betrayal and theatrical rivalry resurrect historical drama as contemporary envy destroys intimacy. Jealousy burns lovers into ash while applause masks poison.","opus":"A gleefully vicious kiss-off to a jealous rival, staged entirely through Shakespeare — the doomed lovers, the schemer, the feud. She casts herself as the vivid picture to his mute, dated reel, dismissing his envy and posturing as garbage dressed up as art right before the final curtain.","sonnet":"A theatrical post-breakup score-settling through Shakespeare: the speaker is Desdemona to an Iago-Othello hybrid, a silver screen to a silent film, who wore the ex's sweater better — the curtains fall, the poison is kissed in fair Verona, and the other's jealous art is declared shit.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-command~valley-of-hinnom":{"haiku":"Divine wrath descends on those who poison paradise, the speaker invoking a king-figure to crush and humiliate. Paradise and vengeance intertwine in baroque excess, the language of destruction made delirious.","opus":"A gleefully absurd kiss-off couched in biblical Gehenna imagery, telling a buzzkill to get out of paradise. The mock-heroic praise of some untouchable king turns the exorcism of a toxic presence into black comedy.","sonnet":"A messianic figure — half divine avenger, half absurd \"national treasure\" from Sweden — is invoked to expel burning trash from paradise, with the refrain \"say your goodbyes\" aimed at the unworthy. The Gehenna imagery (Valley of Hinnom, biblical rubbish heap) grounds the ejection in ancient punishment.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-command~you-can-t-sit-with-us":{"haiku":"The speaker's bridge-building efforts meet only fence-building resistance; connection proves impossible when one party refuses to meet across the divide.","opus":"A pop-culture-drenched kiss-off to someone who builds walls where the singer builds bridges, all delivered as a final ultimatum. Beneath the film references is a simple verdict: this is your last chance and you're about to blow it.","sonnet":"A speaker exhausted by a rival's posturing — all cinematic self-mythology and no real depth — issues a final ultimatum, contrasting their own bridge-building with the other's fence-raising and declaring this the last chance before the door swings off entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-diamonds~phone-sex-feat-grimes":{"haiku":"Paternal loss warps into metaphysical seeking, the speaker calling god on the phone to reclaim what was taken. Living on the moon becomes refuge from earthly abandonment.","opus":"Grimes's ethereal voice pleads with a father-god to take her home, love and faith blurring into childlike surrender. The dreamlike detachment, living on the moon, makes devotion feel both blissful and dissociative.","sonnet":"A childlike voice addresses a distant father-god figure, oscillating between prayer-like longing — \"please daddy, take me home\" — and breezy reassurance that they are okay living on the moon. The song hovers between spiritual need, displacement, and forced cheerfulness.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~adhd":{"haiku":"The narrator expresses distraction and the inability to concentrate or remain still, racing thoughts that prevent calm or clarity.","opus":"A twitchy portrait of a mind that cannot settle, swinging between crushing boredom and the sense that everything around is racing past too quickly to follow. Blood Red Shoes, a lean UK guitar-and-drums duo, act out the scattered focus they describe through hammered, repetitive phrasing, the song's restlessness mimicking a brain unable to hold a single thought.","sonnet":"A mind so overstimulated it cannot settle and yet so bored it cannot stay still — the contradiction of attention dysregulation rendered as a breathless, looping complaint. Distraction becomes its own relentless engine, unable to concentrate on anything at all, including the distraction itself.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~colours-fade":{"haiku":"Someone pursues ambition blindly despite confusion and exhaustion. Their desire fades into grey monotony as they stumble forward without sight or direction.","opus":"About abandoning everything on pure trust to pursue a goal you can't put into words, pressing ahead sightless as the world bleeds to grey. It's a picture of restless, jaded searching where the brightness dims and the wounds trail after the journey.","sonnet":"Blind ambition and restless dissatisfaction push someone forward without direction, leaving only bruises, grey exhaustion, and the sensation of falling through — desire without destination, the colours of life fading out.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~count-me-out":{"haiku":"Haunted by repetition and emptiness, the speaker withdraws and distances themselves, announcing their refusal to participate further.","opus":"Terse garage-rock disillusionment: distrust of what we're told and read, going through the motions while opting out entirely. Alienation stated flatly, a refusal to be counted in.","sonnet":"A song about alienation and emotional exhaustion — always feeling empty, always on the outside of things, until the only option left is to opt out entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~doesn-t-matter-much":{"haiku":"Hard work and advice feel futile; the narrator searches for something invisible and invisible, unable to find meaning or reason despite effort and guidance.","opus":"British duo Blood Red Shoes fire off a clipped dismissal of empty metrics and second-hand wisdom. Solutions reached by calculation just stall out and fall short, while a parent's insistence that the son listen and absorb good counsel goes nowhere; he keeps chasing something he can't name, and the deadpan refrain drives home futility rather than any real gain.","sonnet":"Someone absorbs advice dutifully, chases the prescribed path, and still finds themselves searching for a reason that never materializes. The gap between what was promised and what arrived keeps widening, leaving only a persistent low feeling and the unanswered question of why none of it was enough.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~don-t-ask":{"haiku":"A relationship in free fall and repeated failure; the singer refuses explanation and sees only decline and wasted effort, with no path forward despite grasping for hope.","opus":"A cold brush-off aimed at someone who began confident and wound up humbled. The narrator flatly declines to explain why things collapsed, telling the other they still have plenty to figure out and nowhere higher left to go.","sonnet":"A song about someone who has already failed and cannot be helped — do not ask for reasons, the arc from standing to kneeling is complete, and the narrator sees no space left for the person to climb back up.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~elijah":{"haiku":"The speaker addresses someone cyclical—Elijah—who oscillates between reviving and deceiving them, asking them to finally depart and cease this emotional entanglement.","opus":"A song to a beautiful, self-destructive friend whose highs and crashes drag everyone around them, cast in biblical names to mark the mix of love and doom. The chorus is a reluctant goodbye, letting go of someone who's become part of you and can't be saved.","sonnet":"An address to a self-destructive person (Elijah) who keeps cycling between highs and lows and pulling others into the wreckage, while the speaker simultaneously tries and fails to push away an addictive presence they call their Jezebel.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~follow-the-lines":{"haiku":"Someone searches for an absent other; reflections grow dim as polished masks crack and warmth fades into coldness and shadow.","opus":"Absence rendered as a haunted room: dancing with the lights on to keep company with your own shadow, curtains drawn, dust gathering over what's now only a dream. The repeated question is aimed at someone gone, and at yourself.","sonnet":"A song about someone's absence haunting a space — dust gathers, curtains close, the familiar things become hollow, and the repeated question of where they are now suggests someone left without explanation and the narrator keeps circling the empty room they left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~forgive-nothing":{"haiku":"Someone sees through false claims and hollow intentions; their methods shift daily and they deserve no forgiveness for their contrived image.","opus":"A withering kiss-off to someone all posture and contrived image, whose grand intentions collapse and whose reasons ring false. The dry refrain shrugs them off entirely, framing refusal to forgive as the only honest response to being abandoned.","sonnet":"A song about someone who keeps shifting their justifications and manufacturing an image, and the narrator's decision to stop forgiving it — refusing to excuse the performance any longer is framed as the only honest kindness left.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~heartsink":{"haiku":"People in an ordinary town feel worn down and disappointed after hearing the same tired lines. They sacrifice for escape, knowing something better exists beyond their failing hometown.","opus":"A portrait of provincial claustrophobia, where recycled conversation, dull skies, and unremarkable minds grind a person down until numbness sets in. The real subject is the itch to leave: burning out on purpose, convinced life must offer more, ready to throw away everything just to feel the opposite of stuck.","sonnet":"The slow suffocation of a small town or stagnant life — the same hand-me-down lines, the same weather, never satisfied, burning out just to calm down — and the shared knowledge that there must be more somewhere, worth sacrificing to find.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~hope-you-are-holding-up":{"haiku":"Distance and drift through lifestyle demands preventing meaningful connection, caught between movement and desire to maintain failing relationships.","opus":"The quiet ache of a friendship or love drifting apart through distance and silence, the narrator apologizing for words that never come. Its honesty is in refusing blame, admitting things are just changing, and wondering whom the change is really for.","sonnet":"A friendship or relationship drifting apart through neglect rather than rupture — always on the move, words never matching thoughts, no one leaving but things changing anyway. The question 'would I change this for you or change it for me' is the only honest accounting offered.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~i-wish-i-was-someone-better":{"haiku":"A person is wracked with guilt and shame over mistakes they've made. They feel inadequate and blame themselves for relationship failures and personal shortcomings.","opus":"A confession of guilt after hurting someone, the narrator carrying his shame openly and admitting the failure to reach them is entirely his own. The chorus dissolves into a plain, aching wish to have been a kinder, worthier person than he turned out to be.","sonnet":"Repeated, exhausted self-recrimination — the speaker knows they caused harm, carries the shame, can't figure out how to change, and simply keeps wishing they were a better person while the same pattern drags on.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~it-is-happening-again":{"haiku":"A relationship repeats its deadly cycle of breakdown and silence, both partners trapped in repetition that kills their ability to communicate.","opus":"A relationship suffocating under deadening repetition, the same fights and worn-out story recurring until the singer can't breathe and feels nothing; the panicked cry to clear the room dramatizes wanting to escape a cycle that is killing them both.","sonnet":"A relationship or creative partnership has hit the same wall it always does — repetition killing what was once alive, communication drying up, the same fit of rage and numbness cycling back again.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~it-s-getting-boring-by-the-sea":{"haiku":"Someone is trapped in a stagnant town and restless by the sea, unable to escape despite repeated attempts and watching cycles repeat.","opus":"Restless small-town claustrophobia, the itch to flee a place you can't actually escape and a relationship gone quietly sour. The duo keeps it terse and jittery, boredom sharpening into something closer to panic.","sonnet":"A song about the suffocating smallness of a hometown — you can't run from your past or escape the gossip and backstabbing no matter where you go, so you might as well consider a change of scenery entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~keeping-it-close":{"haiku":"Two people mutually destroy what they've built—scratching and pulling threads apart, burning and screaming, breaking their own bones and homes.","opus":"A scorched reckoning with a bond worn down to its final gasp, tearing at each strand until home and identity are both stripped away. The recurring question of whether the suffering was ever worth the wait sits as the raw center of it.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship reduced to mutual harm — two people tearing at each other, burning, screaming, crawling through each other's dirt, breaking what little they have left until there is no home and no bones intact.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~light-it-up":{"haiku":"The song expresses frustration with worn-out promises and stale ideologies, calling for action and ignition to break through complacency and see real change. It centers on the exhaustion of false hope and the need for tangible activation.","opus":"Restless energy against something old, worn out, and false; the speaker urges lighting it up for real, growing out of a life that no longer fits, and taking heart despite the lies.","sonnet":"A song about pushing past exhaustion and worn-out lies — acknowledging a fever of restless energy and the sense of growing out of an old life — and choosing to ignite something real rather than cling to false comfort or faded promises.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~murder-me":{"haiku":"Psychological control in intimacy where one partner watches, manipulates, and destroys from the shadows while the other becomes complicit through inaction.","opus":"The British guitar-and-drums duo turn a nighttime encounter into a blur of seduction and threat, a visitor arriving after dark while the singer, freshly disguised by a change of hair colour, claims to read her pursuer's every want and fear. She admits she baited the trap herself and then couldn't escape it, watcher and watched dissolving into mutual ruin.","sonnet":"Surveillance flipped into vulnerability — a speaker who thought she was the one being watched, led someone on without seeing the danger, changed herself for them, and now realizes the knowledge was mutual and lethal all along. You murder me is not metaphor so much as accusation: being truly seen by this person is what destroys her.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~one-more-empty-chair":{"haiku":"Profound grief and denial—the death sinks in slowly; blank confusion and physical ache replace belief, refusing to accept loss.","opus":"The British duo sit inside the stupor of mourning, a mind gone empty because the loss defies belief and an ache creeping from the ribs down to the toes. Drooping like severed blooms, the grieving one keeps rejecting what they were told, sealing their eyes shut until the world disappears, the vacant seat standing for someone who won't come back.","sonnet":"Grief arrives as a full-body numbness, the mind refusing what the body already knows. The song stays inside the moment of fresh loss, where denial is not weakness but the only available mercy against an absence too enormous to face.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~say-something-say-anything":{"haiku":"Letters and photos hold grief over a lost person; silence surrounds the death while the living wonder how long they'll miss someone and who will care.","opus":"The Brighton duo turn grief and family silence into stark garage-rock, keepsakes and photographs failing to console someone after a death. A void opens that cannot be filled, and the crushing theme is avoidance, loved ones refusing to speak of the loss at all. The song ends on an aching, open-ended question about how long a person can go on missing someone.","sonnet":"A death has left a gap that no one in the surrounding circle will openly address — photographs, voicemails, the ordinary objects of a plain life sit uncharged while everyone falls silent. The narrator pushes into the silence with the bluntest possible question: how long is this kind of grief allowed to go on, and who will say something honest about it.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~take-the-weight":{"haiku":"People are trapped in uniforms and stares, unable to sleep under scrutiny; they scream for someone to come lift the emotional weight off their shoulders.","opus":"The duo sketches someone crumbling under constant scrutiny and burnout, watched like a spectacle and barely holding back tears. The raw, minimal chant is a plea for anyone to step in and shoulder the load, sleepless and disbelieving how bad things have gotten, with numb distraction offering no cure, only fresh distress.","sonnet":"Exhaustion from being watched, categorised, and judged — by strangers, by television, by one's own relentless self-scrutiny — produces a desperate need for someone to simply come and carry some of it. The sleeplessness, the money counting, the performative self-presentation all compress into the same cry: take this weight off.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~the-perfect-mess":{"haiku":"The song condemns someone for possessing everything while having weak spirit, creating a perfect mess through deception and theft. The speaker refuses to save them after they've wasted all chances and squandered credit.","opus":"A cutting address to someone hollow who has everything yet grows spiritually weak, chasing appearances and playing roles that leave a widening hole; the narrator refuses to save them, telling this fake, self-made mess that they blew their chances and nothing is waiting for them.","sonnet":"A song about someone who has everything on paper but is spiritually hollow — they perform and play roles without substance, and the narrator refuses to rescue them, calling out their vanity and the blew credit of their wasted chances.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~this-is-not-for-you":{"haiku":"A person questions whether everything is fine while being misread and trapped in circles. He hides his real feelings while those around him fail to listen despite claims otherwise.","opus":"The Brighton duo dissect a corroded relationship from the inside, the narrator defensively insisting his behavior isn't the other person's fault while both squander time nitpicking and rewriting what was said. The exhausting refrain is the shared lie that all is well, punctured by a sharp doubt about whether the partner is even paying attention anymore.","sonnet":"Doing things for your own reasons rather than to prove something to someone who is always reading you wrong — the narrator is circling a resentment or hurt, pretending everything is fine while quietly insisting that whatever they do, it isn't for you.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~try-harder":{"haiku":"Constant social pressure forces people to change appearance and beliefs to match media ideals, chasing approval that never arrives; the effort ultimately fails to deliver satisfaction.","opus":"A wry jab at trend-chasing and the craving to be admired, swapping out hair colour and shoes and opinions to match whatever the glossies decree while everyone gets older regardless. Amid the ridicule glimmers a small hope that a certain someone who drew her attention might make mornings bearable. Blood Red Shoes, the British duo, drive it with scrappy garage hooks and a closing chant that none of it works.","sonnet":"Exhaustion with the performance of cool — changing hair, changing shoes, changing opinions to match what the magazines decide matters this season. The song watches everyone try so hard to be looked up to and finds it both pitiful and seductive, before the late admission that a stranger catching your eye might be the only thing that makes the whole charade briefly worth it.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-red-shoes~when-we-wake":{"haiku":"A person breathes through days waiting, calling into silence, broken and fragmented. Colors blind, guidance fails, but the cycle of breathing and waiting continues endlessly.","opus":"An existential complaint that reduces living to bare, joyless repetition, going through the motions with no purpose in sight. The mood is total disconnection: reaching out and getting silence back, guided by things that dazzle but reveal nothing, a self coming apart while wondering whether survival is really all there is.","sonnet":"Existing in a state of waiting and spiritual numbness — calling out into silence, broken into pieces, colours meaning nothing, just breathing day and night and asking whether this waiting is really all life amounts to.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-stain-child~metropolice":{"haiku":"The song mourns how urbanization and digitalization have severed humans from nature and their own agency, turning them into automatons. It warns that automation will eventually absorb even human existence itself.","opus":"A cyber-dystopian warning as humanity dissolves into a fully digitized city, losing all contact with the natural world while its systems harden into rigid control. Blood Stain Child, a Japanese band blending melodic death metal with trance, insist that our selfhood and free will are the only proof we are more than machinery, even as manufactured pleasure lulls everyone into compliance.","sonnet":"Humanity has traded the physical earth beneath its feet for digital paradise, forgetting it is not machinery but a species with will. As automation advances, the risk of erasing human existence entirely surfaces as an urgent, unresolved question.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-youth~nerve":{"haiku":"Plea for help from paralysis: the speaker is dying emotionally while isolated outside someone's house, neglected and unheard, begging for release from constant internal pain and social indifference.","opus":"British hardcore band Blood Youth stage a portrait of someone imprisoned by fear and neglect, abandoned while his suffering is ignored and his wellbeing dismissed. Paralyzed and unheard, he begs for release as pain works its way inward, worn down by keeping up a false front that no one ever bothered to look past.","sonnet":"Trapped inside psychological paralysis — unseen, neglected, health disregarded — a person watches from outside their own life as no one responds to their distress. The damage feels neural, running from vein to brain, and the only release they can imagine is being severed from the neglect that has left them bleeding.","src":"haiku"},
"blood-youth~spineless":{"opus":"A raw self-portrait of depression and self-loathing, the narrator calling himself spineless and distorted, blaming his own mind for the pain no one sees, and admitting the person others hate is himself.","sonnet":"A song about being corroded by depression and shame — the narrator describes surrendering to mental illness as an almost predatory relationship, feeling hated even by themselves, unable to leave a state of suffering they have paradoxically grown dependent on.","src":"sonnet"},
"bloodbather~end":{"opus":"A sadistic murder fantasy told from the killer's point of view, relishing breaking bones, gouging eyes, and cutting short the victim's pulse; he frames the torture as justice and fate, taking euphoric pleasure in the victim's agony as he brings them to their end.","sonnet":"A graphic torture-fantasy in which the narrator takes ecstatic pleasure in physically destroying an enemy — breaking bones, cutting the pulse short, shoving nails into eyes until the victim dies — framed as justice and euphoric revenge.","src":"sonnet"},
"bloodhound-gang~a-lap-dance-is-so-much-better-when-the-stripper-is-crying":{"haiku":"A narrator fantasizes about sadistic scenarios, murder, and sexual coercion against a stripper, celebrating her distress as erotic.","opus":"The Bloodhound Gang push their crude comedy-rock to grotesque extremes, spinning a trucker's tall tale of loneliness, a hitchhiker named Russell, and a strip-club encounter narrated with deliberately shocking menace and gross-out non sequiturs. The punchline twist that the stripper is doing it for baby formula lands as calculated bad taste. Jimmy Pop's whole gambit is transgression as satire, offense weaponized for laughs.","sonnet":"A tall, grotesque comic narrative of loneliness and bad decisions at a strip club — escalating through deliberately offensive non-sequiturs — satirises male sexual desperation by making its narrator as absurd and self-defeating as possible. The cruelty of the hook is undercut by the sheer ridiculousness of its own delivery.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~along-comes-mary":{"haiku":"Describes how a mysterious figure named Mary arrives to disrupt the narrator's life with psychological games and sexual complexity. The song portrays her as an agent of transformation, leaving trauma and confusion in her wake.","opus":"A cryptic, wordplay-heavy song about a figure named Mary who arrives to rescue the lonely narrator and transform how he sees reality, leaving him feeling his empty cup now tastes sweet. It hints at illusions, games, scars, and altered perception surrounding her influence.","sonnet":"A cover of the Association's 1966 song about a mysterious, transformative woman named Mary whose arrival offers the possibility of relief, connection, and altered perception to someone stuck in loneliness and routine — though whether she brings salvation or further disillusionment remains ambiguous.","src":"haiku","fable":"A near-faithful cover of The Association's 1966 hit, this rides the same open secret the original smuggled onto AM radio: Mary is a girl and Mary is grass, and the lyric refuses to resolve which. That double exposure is the whole engine, since every entrance of Mary can be heard as a lover pulling a lonely, self-lacerating narrator out of his own head, or as the high that turns a stale drink faintly delicious. The verses run darker than the melody lets on, threading past masquerades, slack burnt-out kids sprawled under the stars, and the morning-after wreckage of bodies she walked away from, so the promised rescue keeps souring into comedown. Coming from the Bloodhound Gang, a band that built its brand on leering at exactly this kind of teenage vice, the deadpan sincerity of the cover is the punchline: they hand a sunshine-pop tune the seedy reading it always half-held, and the looping fadeout makes the sweetness itself sound like the relapse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The Association's 1966 original was banned by stations that heard Mary as Mary Jane — songwriter Tandyn Almer's own account stayed disputed. The cover reached the Bloodhound Gang by way of the Half Baked stoner-comedy soundtrack in 1998, a year before Hooray for Boobies.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"deadpan sincerity handed to a song that always held a seedy reading","built":"the double exposure between girl and grass is never resolved"},
"bloodhound-gang~altogether-ooky":{"haiku":"Creepy, invasive behavior that repels the narrator. The relationship is perverse and disturbing, driving the narrator away with disgust.","opus":"A crude, comedic kiss-off to a repulsive ex, cataloguing disgust at their behavior and threatening a mob with torches. It laces the insults with horror-movie references to convey total revulsion at ever having been close to them.","sonnet":"A crude, comedic rejection of someone the speaker finds repulsive, cataloguing a series of grotesque or absurd encounters and announcing his complete desire to be rid of her. The repeated refrain borrows a line from Silence of the Lambs for comic shock effect.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~fire-water-burn":{"haiku":"A satirical hip-hop track built on absurdist humor and juvenile boasts. The speaker uses exaggeration and pop-culture references to undermine any coherent message or meaning.","opus":"A deliberately idiotic party anthem built on absurdist frat-boy boasts and pop-culture punchlines. The whole point is that it means nothing and revels in its own dumb, gleeful crudeness.","sonnet":"A comic, self-deprecating party rap built around the 'roof is on fire' chant, in which the narrator mocks himself as a 'dumb white guy' posing as a hardcore rapper and jokes about his own inadequacy in a string of crude, absurdist boasts.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~foxtrot-uniform-charlie-kilo":{"fable":"One joke, beautifully over-engineered: a parade of absurd invented euphemisms for the same act, a chorus that spells the four-letter word in NATO phonetic code, and a narrator swearing throughout that he doesn't want to beat around the bush.","fableDeep":"The song is self-refuting on purpose: it insists on directness — 'make a point to be straight with you,' 'know my intent,' 'I don't wanna beat around the bush' (itself a euphemism, itself a pun) — while never once saying the thing plainly; even the title outsources the obscenity to military radio protocol. 'I Brazilian wax poetic' compresses the whole method into three words. The real thesis is about how sex-talk works: the more elaborate the avoidance, the dirtier everything sounds, so prudishness is the actual comedy engine — censorship as the best writer on the track.","haiku":"Uses crude euphemisms to describe sexual acts while maintaining a playful, deliberate tone about physical intimacy without pretense.","opus":"An entire song built as a joke about not saying the word it's obviously about, stacking absurd euphemisms for sex while claiming to prefer plain speech. The comedy is the point: crude innuendo dressed up as wordplay by a band that lived for the bit.","sonnet":"The entire song is an extended exercise in euphemism, piling up comic slang phrases that all describe the same sex act, while the refrain spells out an obscenity in NATO phonetic alphabet.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~hell-yeah":{"haiku":"A blasphemous fantasy of rewriting divine law to ban pop culture excess and enforce hedonistic pleasures. The narrator plays at being a mockery of God, joking about crucifixion and selling salvation to the masses.","opus":"The Bloodhound Gang run a gleefully blasphemous thought experiment about what they'd do as God, stacking pop-culture jokes, taboo gags, and mock commandments into pure provocation. It is satire aimed at religion and its self-righteous certainties, the crucifixion punchlines and low-self-esteem messiah deflating piety through relentless juvenile irreverence.","sonnet":"A satirical riff on what the narrator would do if he were God — mainly enforce petty personal preferences, employ Norwegian lesbian servants, and mock the hypocrisy of organized religion — culminating in a self-aware bit where he imagines being crucified and talking his way out of it.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~i-hope-you-die":{"haiku":"A shock-comedy catalogue of increasingly grotesque deaths and degradations wishing upon the target, escalating from highway accidents to rape and humiliation in prison, delivered with absurdist rage.","opus":"Gleeful shock comedy stacking ever more absurd, grotesque catastrophes onto a hated target. The whole point is the escalating cartoon cruelty; the sentiment is just a delivery system for the gross-out.","sonnet":"An elaborately comic catalog of grotesque misfortunes wished upon someone the narrator despises — involving car crashes, prison rape by a deranged cellmate, and ending up bound in a wedding dress — the humor is pitch-black and deliberate, every wish more baroque than the last.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~i-m-the-least-you-could-do":{"haiku":"A crude libertine brags about sexual conquest and manipulation, dismissing emotional consequence and personal responsibility.","opus":"Their trademark crude wordplay aimed at joyless casual sex with someone the narrator openly disdains, admitting he'd get screwed regardless. The joke masks a bleak view of hooking up as mutual using, the punchline being his own rock-bottom self-worth.","sonnet":"A coarse, pun-dense address to a woman the speaker considers sexually easy frames the encounter as the lowest bar in his otherwise miserable existence — if only life were as easy to get as she is. The song is deliberately crass, built on double-entendres and self-deprecation in equal, ugly measure.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~kiss-me-where-it-smells-funny":{"haiku":"Crude sexual bravado and explicit oral sex; the speaker boasts about seduction through crude metaphors and innuendo.","opus":"The comedy-rock band pile pun upon crude pun in a relentless ode to oral sex, dressing raunch in absurd similes from Sears catalogs to Pavlov's dog. True to their juvenile shock-humor brand, the narrator boasts through a stream of food and pop-culture gags, the vulgar innuendo piled so high it becomes the whole point of the joke.","sonnet":"Crude sexual boasting in extended nautical and culinary metaphor, narrated by someone who considers oral sex his defining skill. The humor is entirely in escalating the vulgarity of the analogies while the actual subject never shifts from its single explicit request.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~mope":{"haiku":"A narrator lists absurdist observations of boredom and failed attempts at sensation, mocking the mundane with surreal references and sexual frustration.","opus":"A deliberately absurdist inventory of pathetic, aimless boredom and gross-out non-sequiturs, framed as a mock-tribute to dead pop-culture figures; beneath the pop-reference gags is a portrait of a listless slacker filling empty days with disgusting, purposeless behavior.","sonnet":"An elaborate catalogue of the most boring, pathetic ways a person can waste a day — elaborate pop-culture non-sequiturs piling up into a portrait of someone so profoundly bored and listless they've reduced their life to absurdist squalor.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~no-hard-feelings":{"haiku":"A speaker ends a relationship with someone they see as trash, rejecting obligation and betrayal. They compare their indifference to 9/11 devastation.","opus":"A crude, comedic breakup kiss-off in which the narrator gleefully informs an ex that it's no longer his job to sleep with her on her birthday, insisting he dumped her rather than the reverse. Piling on gross insults and a tasteless 9/11 gag, it turns spite and relief at being rid of a partner into deliberately offensive humor.","sonnet":"A contemptuous post-breakup dismissal in which the narrator reveals he's not heartbroken at all — he dumped her because she was beneath him, and the only regret is not getting over her sooner.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~pennsylvania":{"haiku":"A parade of 1990s cultural detritus and low-class kitsch defines an unremarkable life registered in Pennsylvania. The song celebrates mediocrity and regional anti-identity.","opus":"The song piles up absurd, trashy pop-culture references and crude images to sketch a low-rent identity, tying it all to a nameless heart airbrushed on a Subaru's license plate registered in Pennsylvania. It's a self-mocking portrait of tacky Americana rooted in that state.","sonnet":"The song builds an extended catalogue of things that are culturally déclassé, embarrassing, or past their time — all labelled \"we\" — and then places a sentimental, anonymous love against this backdrop of mediocrity: the other person is a briefly felt impulse on a postcard, while the speaker is a nameless airbrushed heart on a Subaru registered in Pennsylvania.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~ralph-wiggum":{"opus":"A novelty piece assembled almost entirely from Ralph Wiggum's non-sequiturs from The Simpsons, set to a pop hook. The joke is the collision of a beloved dim cartoon child with the machinery of a pop sensation; there's no meaning beneath, and that's the gag.","sonnet":"A collage of non-sequiturs and absurdist one-liners drawn from the character Ralph Wiggum — the song has no narrative or emotional throughline, existing entirely as comedic randomness strung together in the voice of an oblivious child.","src":"sonnet","fable":"This is a jukebox single stitched entirely from the malaproptic blurts of The Simpsons' sweet, addled schoolboy, and the gag is dressing that word-salad as a bona fide teen-idol number, complete with a handler barking at the oblivious star to clear the boards. Bloodhound Gang, a band built on gleeful adolescent provocation, plays it straight and lets the surreal juvenilia collide with earnest bubblegum bounce, since the pleasure is precisely that nothing decodes: a wintry crystal likened to frozen seafood, a movie monster lurking down the block. The kicker is the closing chant, a naval enlistment slogan reversed and looped, weaponizing the whole monument to nonsense with a mock subliminal recruitment message aimed squarely at the old backmasking panic.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["comedy & wordplay"],"means":"cartoon word-salad costumed as sincere teen-idol material","built":"a reversed naval slogan loops as mock subliminal recruitment at the close"},
"bloodhound-gang~screwing-you-on-the-beach-at-night":{"haiku":"A speaker expresses absurdist, surreal romantic gestures and fantasies using nonsensical imagery and puerile humor.","opus":"A vulgar send-up of romance, heaping on ridiculous surreal pickup lines, a jewel-encrusted secondhand undergarment, a mythical horse, a filmmaker's thin mustache, all dressed up as delicate short-form verse. The gag is that under the mock-sensitive posturing on the cliff edge, the real aim is just tawdry seaside sex.","sonnet":"A satirical take on male romantic self-image: the narrator piles up increasingly absurd declarations of sensitivity and uniqueness — bedazzled thongs, haiku, unicorns — before revealing the actual fantasy is simply sex on a beach at night.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~the-bad-touch":{"haiku":"Explicit celebration of human sexuality as animal instinct: stripped of pretense, humans are just mammals driven by primal urges and desire.","opus":"A gleefully crude sex romp that reduces desire to base animal biology, piling on absurd nineties pop-culture puns to keep the whole thing a joke. The comedy is the point; sincerity would ruin it.","sonnet":"Using the framing of a nature documentary, the song is an extended series of sexual double entendres arguing that human mating is no different from animal behaviour. The tone is entirely comedic, stringing together pop-culture puns to reduce romantic pursuit to pure biology.","src":"haiku","fable":"A novelty come-on that works by translation: every blunt sexual urge gets rerouted through the deadpan cadence of a wildlife broadcast, so lust arrives pre-classified as zoology, two specimens of one animal order excused from shame by biological inevitability. The engine underneath is a pun avalanche, a relentless stream of soda-brand jingles, fast-food signage, cable-show tags, and mangled geography in which each punchline hides an innuendo, so desire never lands plainly but keeps deflecting into consumer trivia. That deflection is the whole point rather than a failure of nerve: by pitching itself as goofy taxonomy narrated over a nature program, the song licenses itself to be as crude as it likes while wearing the innocent mask of documentary science, and the leering insistence of the refrain is exactly what that mask never quite covers.","fv":2,"themes":["comedy & wordplay","love & desire"],"means":"lust licensed by rerouting every urge through wildlife-documentary taxonomy","built":"the pun avalanche of brand names and cable tags keeps desire from landing plainly"},
"bloodhound-gang~the-ballad-of-chasey-lain":{"haiku":"An obsessive fan's crude sexual fantasy directed at an adult film actress through mock fan letters.","opus":"A comic series of increasingly desperate fan letters to porn actress Chasey Lain, crudely begging for a sexual favor and complaining she never writes back; the humor rides on the vulgar, obsessive fixation.","sonnet":"A series of increasingly frustrated fan letters addressed to a porn actress, pleading for a specific sexual act and escalating from polite request to aggrieved demand when no reply comes.","src":"haiku","fable":"A fan letter to a porn star, staged in three shrinking installments — explain, complain, demand — so that the joke isn't the vulgar request but the delusion escalating behind it, entitlement curdling as the silence goes unanswered. The chorus does the actual work: it tallies the star's endless partners against the writer's endless free time, and the punchline that she's had everyone but him reframes the whole grotesque courtship as the oldest, saddest thing under the crudeness, a nobody insisting his fixation makes him special. Bloodhound Gang's whole shtick was daring you to find the ache under the gross-out, and here the mask is the leering fan while underneath sits pure loser loneliness — sealed by the bridge, where he drags this fantasy woman home to meet his parents, the porn punchline colliding with a small boy's need for approval.","fv":2,"fnote":"The mock-stalker letter charted like a love song: number one in Iceland, top twenty across half of Europe.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","comedy & wordplay","love & desire"],"means":"crudeness as a mask for the oldest, saddest loser loneliness","built":"three shrinking installments tighten as the silence stays unanswered"},
"bloodhound-gang~three-point-one-four":{"haiku":"Crude sexual boasting interspersed with misogynist stereotypes and crude jokes about women; the song treats degradation of women as punchlines and entertainment.","opus":"The Pennsylvania novelty-rap crew leans into deliberate juvenile shock, cataloguing an escalating hunt for a partner in the crudest possible terms. Beneath the offensive punchlines sits the actual joke: every ideal woman he describes turns out to be a reflection of himself, exposing the narcissism driving the whole tasteless quest. It is provocation as its own reward, humor built on being unbearable.","sonnet":"A crude, deadpan inventory of impossible ideal-partner criteria doubles as self-satire: every absurd standard loops back to the speaker's own reflection. The fantasy is entirely solipsistic, each disqualifying flaw in past women serving mainly to clarify that the only acceptable partner would be an exact copy of himself.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~uhn-tiss-uhn-tiss-uhn-tiss":{"haiku":"A man pursues sexual conquest through crude advances and mechanical desire, reducing intimate connection to physical performance.","opus":"A parade of groan-worthy double entendres set to a club beat, the band mocking dance-floor seduction while indulging in it. Dumb-on-purpose innuendo is the entire joke, and it commits fully.","sonnet":"A deliberately crude pick-up song where every line is a sexual come-on wrapped in pop-culture wordplay, the entire thing a vehicle for the joke that the only thing the narrator is selling is the repetitive bass beat of the act itself.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodhound-gang~yummy-down-on-this":{"haiku":"Crude sexual boasting using pop culture references to describe fellatio as a service; the song treats the act as comedic sexual conquest.","opus":"A relentlessly juvenile sex boast built entirely from puns, cramming a wall of pop-culture and fast-food references into demands for oral gratification, every line a groan-worthy double entendre. It's crude comedy first, titillation second. The Bloodhound Gang, the Pennsylvania novelty rap-rock crew fronted by Jimmy Pop, made a career of exactly this sophomoric wordplay, and this early cut is a pure showcase of their gleeful bad taste.","sonnet":"An extended, relentlessly crude request for oral sex disguised as a dense chain of pop-cultural puns — Rocky, Campbell's, Randall Cunningham, Diff'rent Strokes, all weaponized as euphemism. The joke is the sheer density of the references more than any single target; it's braggadocio as comedic endurance sport.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodline~leave-the-past-and-enter-flow":{"haiku":"Shedding preconceived limitations unlocks authentic ambition; the speaker claims agency by trusting instinct and embracing the flow of true intention despite uncertainty.","opus":"A hardcore self-affirmation about shedding preconceptions and fear to author your own fate, treating tension and adversity as fuel rather than injury. The recurring vow is that misery forged rather than broke the speaker.","sonnet":"A motivational song about shedding old patterns and self-limiting beliefs to pursue genuine ambition — you do not need permission to belong, you do not need more friends or external validation, and suffering does not have to define you. The narrator has been forged by adversity and now insists that the only way forward is to leave the weight of the past behind and enter a state of purposeful flow.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodline~who-knew":{"haiku":"The speaker exposes a once-promising person who peaked young and faked their success, now exposed as an impostor whose facade is crumbling as everyone watches them fail.","opus":"A scathing takedown of a fraud who bought and faked their way to a peak that came too young, now watched by everyone as their facade crumbles and the crowd learns they didn't even write the song. It circles a fake with no integrity, deceived into thinking they're something. Bloodline drive this into aggressive metalcore where the contempt is the hook, each chorus another shove down the hole they dug.","sonnet":"A takedown of someone who peaked early on borrowed credibility — faked their way up, paid for the image, got caught, and now everyone watches them crawl. The speaker has zero sympathy and zero interest in sustaining the performance: the facade has an expiration date and this is it.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodmoon~creature":{"haiku":"Toxic patterns bind through habit rather than love, both parties drained by repetition that neither can break. One drowns in obsession while the other feeds on emotional dissolution.","opus":"Leaving a partner stuck in self-destructive patterns, the narrator drained by their jealousy and unwilling to keep saving someone who won't break the cycle. The reluctant exit is self-preservation over rescue.","sonnet":"Addressed to someone self-destructive and jealousy-driven, the speaker exhaustedly concludes they can no longer sustain the relationship, watching the other person drown in bad choices and predictable victimhood. The phrase \"creature of habit\" frames the dysfunction as ingrained rather than chosen.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodslide~pica":{"haiku":"A person describes compulsive consumption of inedible substances with their mother, framing disorder as inherited and inescapable.","opus":"An unsettling piece built around the disorder of eating non-food, spoken to a mother whose urges the child has inherited and won't let her escape, itemizing ice, chalk, soap, soil and ash. Amazement at so fierce an appetite hardens into a plea to be nourished. Bloodslide render this inherited hunger as mantra-like heaviness, the repeated call to mother both a beg and a haunting.","sonnet":"A song about pica — the compulsion to eat non-food substances like ice, chalk, dirt, soap, ash — framed as a conversation with or about a mother, the craving so strong it overrides pain and reason. The tenderness of the repeated mama cuts against the disturbing imagery: this is not a horror story but a portrait of an appetite that cannot explain itself and will not be quit.","src":"haiku"},
"bloody-civilian~i-don-t-like-you-feat-jeleel-candy-bleakz":{"haiku":"Deliberate dislike hardens into armor against manipulation and false intimacy, the speaker refusing hypocrisy even when society demands niceness. Rejection becomes the only honest stance.","opus":"A blunt, code-switching Nigerian kiss-off to someone who won't take the hint, refusing their threats and their bed alike. The mix of English, pidgin and Yoruba makes the dismissal feel communal, even mama doesn't like you.","sonnet":"Three voices unite around a single position: not hate exactly, just a deep, bone-level dislike for someone who keeps inserting themselves uninvited. Yoruba-inflected verses add texture to the refusal — \"I no fucking send you\" — keeping the rejection matter-of-fact rather than dramatic.","src":"haiku"},
"bloody-tyrant~kenshi-subete-hey-ru":{"haiku":"Escape from tyranny demands death of the soul; warriors ride into darkness to shatter enslavement despite inevitable annihilation.","opus":"A Taiwanese folk-metal tale of rogue samurai fleeing a corrupt lord, framing the fleeting cicada life against the endurance of a warrior's spirit. Its deeper meditation is on cruelty as the human condition's last resort once the peaceful façade cracks.","sonnet":"Samurai deserters flee a covetous lord on horseback under concealed faces, urged to banish revenge and forget former lives; the cicada — singing briefly then gone — frames the warrior's code as both beautiful and doomed, life a blazing sacrifice, and cruelty the inevitable result when peace cracks.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodywood~aaj":{"haiku":"Personal struggle becomes resurrection through determination and ancestral courage; the speaker breaks cycles of defeat by channeling pain into unstoppable momentum against fate.","opus":"A defiant rebirth anthem about rising from humiliation and hardship to fight again today, refusing to bow or break. The Hindi and English trade off to say the struggle and pain are only the beginning of the climb.","sonnet":"A bilingual surge of resilience — Hindi and English trading verses — in which the speaker has been knocked down by hardship and taunts but rises through sheer passion, framing today as a rebirth: \"janma hoon main phir se aaj\" (I am born again today), with the Kaio-ken metaphor of surpassing limits threading through both languages.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodywood~chakh-le":{"haiku":"A song advocating class struggle and redistribution of wealth to provide basic rights (food, shelter, clothing) to all people regardless of birth status.","opus":"The Delhi folk-metal band fuses nagara drums and rapped English with Hindi chants into a class-war anthem about wealth inequality. The premise flips the tired script, imagining the poor growing richer rather than the rich richer, insisting food, clothing, and shelter are universal rights worth fighting for. Teach a people to fish, they argue, and poverty could be defeated outright.","sonnet":"A bilingual call to economic justice insists that food, clothing, and shelter are universal rights, not privileges. The Hindi refrain — take what is yours, the fight for rights is not easy — anchors the English verses that indict wealth hoarding and challenge the myth of divine destiny, pointing instead to collective human will as the engine of redistribution.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodywood~dana-dan":{"opus":"The Indian folk-metal band unleash a blistering anthem against rapists and abusers, vigilante fury turned into a demand for consent and a full-frontal strike on male dominance. Delivered across English and Hindi, it lifts a raised hand for the anonymous victims degraded by predators, insisting the cause belongs to everyone rather than any lone individual out for himself.","sonnet":"A furiously anti-rape, anti-patriarchy song that calls for physical retaliation against abusers and domestic batterers, framing violence against predators as righteous retribution and demanding that all men actively participate in dismantling rape culture.","src":"sonnet"},
"bloodywood~dhadak":{"haiku":"Heartbeat becomes the rhythm of determination and defiance, the body itself a proof of perseverance through suffering. The drum of life pounds toward an uncertain triumph, willing transformation through sheer persistence.","opus":"The Indian folk-metal crew turn a racing heartbeat into a mantra of defiance, fusing boxing bravado and Hindi resolve into a working-class refusal to bow, grinding today for a tomorrow they intend to seize.","sonnet":"An anthemic collision of Hindi and English urges the listener to convert pain into power and grind today for tomorrow, framing ambition as the heartbeat (dhadak) that builds your throne. The Hindi verse warns that a clay figure will crack in fire unless the heart's stubborn desire drives it forward.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodywood~endurant":{"haiku":"A resilient fighter transforms suffering and humiliation into personal strength, refusing to be broken by violence or oppression.","opus":"The Indian folk-metal band forge an anthem of forgiving strength, addressing those who resist the pull toward violence and urging them to rise through love instead of revenge. Switching between Hindi and English, the narrator recasts old wounds and past weakness as sources of power, refusing to be crushed and choosing to fight for peace with words, music and endurance.","sonnet":"Resilience after sustained oppression and self-doubt is recast as a warrior identity, drawing on both Hindi and English to describe someone who has absorbed every blow and converted the damage into defiant strength. Endurance here is not passive but a roaring reclamation of selfhood.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodywood~gaddaar":{"haiku":"Politicians and institutions betray the youth with broken promises; the people rise to expose corruption and reclaim power through collective action.","opus":"The Delhi folk-metal crew aim their rage at crooked politicians who swap religion for ballots and ballots for dirty cash, naming the betrayers outright. It's protest tailored for developing nations, pledging pushback and genuine change in place of the usual hollow campaign promises.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of corrupt Indian politicians who betray the electorate with empty promises, divisive caste politics, and personal greed, calling them traitors and declaring that popular patience has run out.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodywood~machi-bhasad":{"haiku":"Systemic corruption and governmental betrayal inspire revolutionary action; the generation rises to break the oppressive system and hold leaders accountable.","opus":"A generational call to arms fusing Punjabi folk with rap-metal, aimed at breaking the back of a system that's exhausted everyone's patience. The stance is uprising as evolution: enough waiting, take the power by the neck and test it.","sonnet":"A street-level call to uprising against corrupt governance and unchecked power — the Hindi sections declare patience exhausted and a public reckoning underway, while the English verses frame it as generational warfare against leaders who exploit rather than serve.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodywood~nu-delhi":{"haiku":"Delhi's chaos and contradiction breed strength; diversity, struggle, and urban hustle forge an unbreakable spirit that transcends geography into state of mind.","opus":"The Indian folk-metal band turns Delhi into a battle cry, fusing English and Hindi to celebrate a city of diversity, hustle, and untamed pride. The point is that Nu Delhi is a state of mind, unity forged from difference and the migrant's fierce love of home.","sonnet":"Delhi as a force of nature — law of the jungle, rule of the beast, a feast of senses that outsiders can't compete with. Pride in diversity as gift, in the city's capacity for love and resilience past the surface chaos, the Desi boys claiming the shift and the bhaukaal (atmosphere of dominance) of babbar sher — lions who walk together.","src":"haiku"},
"bloodywood~tadka":{"haiku":"Spiced fury and cultural defiance ignite; the speaker seasons rage with cultural pride against oppression.","opus":"A joyous folk-metal ode to Indian home cooking as identity and love, the kitchen framed as a battlefield against blandness. Beneath the flavor boasts runs real tenderness, the grandmother's wisdom and an awareness of those who go hungry, so good food must be shared.","sonnet":"Indian food as communal pride and political metaphor — the tadka (tempering spice) is both literal cooking tradition and the fire needed to win against blandness; nana's wisdom (\"if you wanna make it big, you better be fully fed\") leads directly to awareness of those who don't know if they'll eat for dinner. The Hindi and English weave together the flavour and the social conscience into the same pot.","src":"haiku"},
"bloomer~dead-in-the-water":{"haiku":"Drowning in regret and self-torment, the speaker sinks under accumulated weight while struggle proves futile—fate remains unchanged no matter how fiercely the impulse to escape burns.","opus":"A sinking into depressive resignation, the narrator watching himself bleed out while the harshest voice is his own self-lecture. The gut-punch is realizing the striving was futile all along, that he'll never become who he dreamed, and letting the water take him.","sonnet":"A person stuck in a self-defeating pattern watches the edge approach while bleeding out, concluding that struggling is pointless and that they will never become who they dream of being — the image of spilling over frames stasis as slow catastrophe.","src":"haiku"},
"bloomer~maybe-it-s-all-in-your-head":{"haiku":"Haunted by unspoken words and imagined futures, uncertain whether internal struggles are real or constructed.","opus":"A brief, aching meditation on regret and the things left unsaid, the singer haunted by a life only imagined and unsure whether the whole hurt is self-inflicted delusion.","sonnet":"A micro-song about a life lived in unspoken fantasies and haunting regrets — clinging to something long past its time, wondering if the suffering is entirely self-inflicted.","src":"haiku"},
"bloom~cold":{"opus":"A relationship's death from the perspective of someone too numb and self-destructive to save it, who relapses into old ways and asks to be left to die and buried in the home they built together, unable to give love while this broken.","sonnet":"After losing themselves in a relationship that left them numb and unable to feel anything, the narrator regresses to self-destructive patterns, expresses suicidal exhaustion, and bids farewell to what they built together, asking to be left to die inside the home that love made.","src":"sonnet"},
"bloom~laughing-stock":{"haiku":"Depression presents as self-aware decline the speaker has ceased fighting against, acceptance of defeat masquerading as honesty. Harm becomes the only sensation strong enough to feel alive.","opus":"A raw deathcore confession of depressive collapse, where the singer casts himself as everyone's joke and can no longer feel the difference between despair and self-harm, resenting the ordinary happiness of others.","sonnet":"A speaker in acute despair — self-described as manic, psychotic, lost in hell — invites others to use them as the punchline of a joke while hinting at self-harm as the only remaining feeling. The brevity of the lyric amplifies its bleakness.","src":"haiku"},
"bloom~maybe-in-another-life":{"haiku":"Alternative lives haunt present existence as the speaker fantasizes escape rather than repair, trapped between cursed self and imagined versions. Trauma lingers even in fantasy, the fundamental self unchangeable.","opus":"An anguished counterfactual, cycling through what-ifs about purpose and safety while suspecting the pain would follow into any life, treating rebirth as the only imaginable escape from being the author of your own ruin.","sonnet":"A voice crushed by purposelessness and self-identification as \"the author of my own fucking tragedy\" fantasises repeatedly about a parallel life free from fear and pain, while acknowledging the impossibility of escape. The desire to \"start again\" is voiced urgently but without conviction.","src":"haiku"},
"bloom~siren-song":{"haiku":"Control operates through isolation and distortion, the voice learning to mistake captivity for intimacy. When liberation finally comes, the song itself—once everything—becomes hated.","opus":"A portrait of coercive control from inside it, mapping how an abuser isolates and gaslights until the victim doubts their own mind, then the hard turn to contempt once the spell breaks and leaving becomes possible.","sonnet":"A controlling, isolating relationship is anatomised: the abuser sewed the speaker's mouth shut, kept them in darkness, convinced them they couldn't survive alone, and forced them to watch their own suffering until, finally, the speaker found the strength to leave. The siren song is now hated rather than seductive.","src":"haiku"},
"blu-detiger~disappearing-feat-magdalena-bay":{"haiku":"Intimate connection dissolves the external world into irrelevance, two people finding communion in marginal spaces. Others cannot comprehend the depth because it exists in whisper and touch, invisible to those who look.","opus":"The giddy private world of new love, where sharing earbuds and staying up till dawn feels like a language nobody outside can hear, and the couple gladly vanish into a feeling they refuse to explain.","sonnet":"Two people lose themselves completely in each other — staying on the phone until sunrise, sharing earbuds, floating to the ceiling — while outsiders have no idea what it feels like. Intimacy here is a kind of disappearing act that both speakers cherish over any external understanding.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-diner~mezzanine":{"haiku":"Memory and present blur across floors; the subject repeats encounters across levels.","opus":"A hazy, half-recognized encounter where the speaker fixates on what could have been while needing to be sure he has met this person before. Refrigerating and thawing what someone means to him captures a feeling he can neither fully hold nor discard.","sonnet":"A spare, uncertain emotional scene: the speaker recognises someone from before, needs to be sure, and can hold only fragmented impressions — a mezzanine, something washing up on shore, feelings refrigerated and thawing — circling what they almost mean to each other.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-diner~silhouettes":{"haiku":"Dissolution of shared future leaves only silhouettes and impossible reconnection. Escape attempts loop backward, trapping the speaker in repetition of past damage.","opus":"The circular torment of an on-off relationship the narrator knows is bad yet keeps returning to, mistaking longing for love. The silhouettes are the half-real presence of someone she wants gone but can't stop replaying, caught between escape and relapse.","sonnet":"The lingering presence of a former relationship — silhouettes of who they were, the 'let's meet up soon' that means nothing, the cycle of going round and round — leaves the speaker wanting to stop seeing the ghost of it; 'no more silhouettes' is an attempted severance from a dynamic that keeps reappearing unchanged.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-encount~polaris":{"haiku":"守る (protecting) becomes life's meaning, the speaker willing to sacrifice everything to preserve what they hold sacred. Hope persists despite devastation, oriented toward a distant star.","opus":"An anime-anthem vow to protect, framing strength not as standing above others but as the will to shield what you love, willing to give everything and endure to keep one fragile hope from going out.","sonnet":"A fighter swears to protect someone at any cost — offering everything, including their own wounds and despair as weapons — pleading \"please don't disappear\" as the stakes rise. Strength, the song argues, exists not to dominate but to embrace what matters.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-encount~run":{"haiku":"Imperfection becomes permission to persist despite mediocrity and doubt; the speaker runs not toward destination but away from others' expectations of who they should be.","opus":"An urgent charge to keep running toward a dream found only after countless detours, even without talent, strength, or a taste for effort. Battered feet stay fixed forward: the point is to live as the self you want to be, not the version someone else wishes you were.","sonnet":"A Japanese-language anthem for someone who found their dream after repeated stumbling and almost gave up: though talentless, weak, and naturally resistant to hard work, the speaker insists that on a path finally their own, the only option is to run — ragged feet forward, refusing to be whoever someone else wants them to be. The patched-together tomorrow is worn as a badge rather than a wound.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-hawaii~i-felt-love":{"haiku":"Communication breaks down across distance, replaced by the overwhelming need for reassurance in a destabilizing romantic bond; finding love means constantly risking drowning in its intensity.","opus":"The vertigo of long-distance love conducted through noisy airports and phone calls, the tone of a voice enough to bring tears. It captures the terrifying vulnerability of having finally found something real and dreading the fall, gasping not to be alone again.","sonnet":"A long-distance phone call in an airport: the narrator can barely hear through the noise, tells the person on the other end they're putting too much on the line, and spirals toward panic — three minutes from falling, begging to be told they're all the other person needs. Love found is simultaneously a river that drowns.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~anti-you":{"haiku":"A person rejects media-driven image culture and ego canonization, becoming the opposite of narcissistic validation systems.","opus":"An industrial revolt against conformity and the worship of image, casting media, ego, and dogma as a religion demanding obedience. The narrator vows to become the negation of everything the culture insists he mirror.","sonnet":"A rejection of conformity culture — the social media hivemind demands everyone stroke its ego and regurgitate its values, and the narrator's response is to burn the altar and define themselves purely in opposition to whatever that machine demands.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~armageddon":{"haiku":"Self-destruction and ecstatic violence merge as the speaker embodies apocalypse; daily warfare against the self becomes ritual that demands perpetual confrontation.","opus":"The speaker takes on the voice of a primal, apocalyptic force, equal parts beast and flame, casting personal struggle as a daily private war. Every wrong and right becomes fuel for an inner Armageddon he almost dares the world to bring on.","sonnet":"The speaker declares themselves the totality of violence, appetite, and duality — beast and earth, flesh and flame, sacred and profane — and frames daily existence as a personal war that they meet head-on, naming the confrontation Armageddon and inviting it to come. The bombast is self-aware, treating inner chaos as a cosmic battle rather than a private struggle.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~armageddon-instrumental":{"haiku":"A person is both beast and feast, flesh and flame—every day and night is war between wrong and right, leading back to Armageddon.","opus":"A defiant self-declaration cast in apocalyptic scale: the narrator names himself the beast, the flame, the war he takes head-on every day and night, framing endless struggle as a personal reckoning to be met rather than feared. Blue Stahli is the solo alias of producer Bret Autrey on the Klayton-founded FiXT label, and this typifies his catalogue of hard-charging electronic-rock built for trailers, games and hype montages.","sonnet":"A declaration of total selfhood as apocalypse — the speaker claims to be flesh, flame, earth, and blood all at once, collapsing sacred and profane into a single body. Every day is a private war fought on its own terms, and rather than fearing the end, the voice invites destruction head-on with a ferocious, almost ecstatic confidence.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~corner":{"haiku":"An internal presence haunts the speaker from within, inescapable and recurring. This force dwells in their body, returning perpetually regardless of distance or healing.","opus":"An intimate horror voiced from inside the person it haunts, a presence that surfaces in the moment the world reveals its cruelty. It works as trauma, as craving, as any inner thing that keeps returning no matter how deep you bury it.","sonnet":"An entity — possibly trauma, obsession, or an internal force — describes itself as permanently embedded inside another person, present beyond the bruising and in the aftermath of every wound, inescapable and recurring.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~crimewave":{"haiku":"A fascination with transgression and shared lawlessness, where the speaker wants to celebrate destruction together with someone else.","opus":"Barely lyrics at all, more a hypnotic chant of shared transgression: the narrator wants a partner to ride and dance on graves alongside, romance recast as reckless mutual abandon. The repeated with-you refrain and stuttered edits are the whole point, style over statement. Blue Stahli, Bret Autrey's electronic-rock alias on FiXT, leans hard here into the sleek, cinematic dance-menace that defines his instrumental-adjacent work.","sonnet":"A love song pitched as a criminal fantasy — the speaker wants nothing more than to commit some gleeful act of transgression side by side with someone, and to celebrate with them afterward. The partnership is the point; the lawlessness is just the shape devotion takes here.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~demon":{"haiku":"A person admits to harboring a demonic nature within—dark impulses and corruption in their soul. They're flies and blood masked as human.","opus":"A blues-tinged self-reckoning from a man whose spirit is grimy and pitch-dark as he trudges an endless, shadowed path, accepting the judgment that something demonic dwells in him. Claiming ownership of the sins he planted and the wound bored through his ribs, it's a confrontation with his own inner blackness rather than any bid for salvation.","sonnet":"A confessional about carrying something rotten inside — flies and blood in the soul, a hole through the chest, seeds sown that belong entirely to you. The narrator doesn't dispute being told they have a demon; the song is about owning that darkness rather than denying it.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~doubt":{"haiku":"A malevolent force plants seeds of self-doubt deep within someone, watching insecurity metastasize into disease until confidence collapses entirely.","opus":"A menacing first-person message from doubt itself, or some saboteur, arriving to seed slow rot and taint the target's self-belief while feeding on their fear. The deeper reading personifies self-sabotage and mental decay as an outside predator that means to devour you from a place buried too far within to reverse.","sonnet":"The narrator speaks as a personification of self-doubt itself, deliberately planted inside someone to feed on their insecurity, corrode their confidence, and consume them from within.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~down-in-flames":{"haiku":"A self-pitying martyr performs their decay for an audience, and others gladly watch them burn—their theatrical suffering fuels the fire.","opus":"A savage takedown of performative victimhood: the narrator watches someone stage their own martyrdom, wearing self-pity like a crown of thorns and forcing empathy as a manipulation, and gleefully refuses to play along as they self-destruct. The Cain-and-Abel jab sharpens the contempt. Blue Stahli, Bret Autrey's FiXT-label electronic-rock alias, delivers it with the swaggering, trailer-ready aggression that marks his sound.","sonnet":"A cold takedown of performative suffering — addressed to someone who manufactures crises for an audience, wearing victimhood like a crown of thorns and weaponizing other people's sympathy. The speaker refuses to play along, watching with contempt as the self-inflicted spectacle burns, and offering no rescue.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~enemy":{"haiku":"Someone becomes an enemy by severing ties and committing to defiance; they breathe through restriction and willfully become what others fear to escape quicksand circumstances and witness transformation.","opus":"A vow of self-reinvention through severance, cutting the ties that hold you down and choosing to become your own antagonist. Now-or-never resolve dressed as electronic-rock catharsis.","sonnet":"About breaking free from a suffocating situation by choosing to become the aggressor rather than the victim — cutting ties, refusing to be held down, and embracing the role of enemy to whatever held the narrator captive.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~give-me-everything-you-ve-got":{"haiku":"Defiance against someone's attempts to control and intimidate, standing stronger than expected and refusing to submit.","opus":"A defiant taunt aimed at an adversary whose only weapon is projected fear, with the narrator standing stronger than expected and refusing to be overtaken. He dares them to throw everything they have at him.","sonnet":"A defiant response to someone who uses fear and negativity as weapons: the speaker strips away the opponent's power by naming it clearly and declaring they can absorb and outlast anything thrown at them.","src":"haiku","fable":"The taunt of the title inverts an attack into an invitation: the antagonist is dismissed as a figment, a projection of the singer's own resentment, so daring it to unload its worst is really a dare aimed at the self that generated it. Blue Stahli's industrial swagger frames the whole thing as an exorcism — the enemy's only weapon is fear, and the fear is borrowed, which is why every escalating come-on drains the threat of its charge. By the close there's no counterattack, only the flat report of still standing, defiance recast as the simple refusal to be overtaken.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"daring the antagonist whose only weapon is borrowed fear","built":"the antagonist recast as the singer's own projection midway through"},
"blue-stahli~kill-me-every-time":{"haiku":"An intimate relationship revolves around ritualized harm and submission; the speaker plays willing victim to a partner who wounds them repeatedly through kiss and bullet.","opus":"Industrial rock about willing surrender in a toxic attraction, the narrator cast as a consenting victim who mistakes destruction for intimacy. The tension is knowing exactly how this ends and wanting it anyway.","sonnet":"An intensely sexual power-exchange dynamic framed as a kind of beautiful annihilation — the narrator is a willing victim who surrenders completely to a partner who breaks and takes him, finding the experience both destructive and irresistible.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~lakes-of-flame":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic imagery of collapsed faith and systemic breakdown, where the world is cleansed through fire and destruction, leaving only ash and ruin.","opus":"Blue Stahli stage an apocalyptic reckoning, sacred order collapsing as fire rains down and the sky itself devours the land. The one-man project's cinematic industrial rock frames annihilation as a grim form of cleansing, the earth poisoned and everything swept away, catastrophe presented as both judgment and purge.","sonnet":"A crumbling altar and overthrown heaven give way to a vision of total solar conflagration — earth salted, nerves severed, everything washed away in lakes of flame. The destruction is purifying as much as punishing, a reset written in apocalyptic fire.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~metamorphosis":{"haiku":"Shedding identity and constraints through violent transformation; cutting away everything confined and false to escape and rebuild anew.","opus":"The one-man industrial project stages a violent self-remaking, a dread that grips the narrator physically until he resolves to perform a brutal operation on himself. Identity becomes something to cut away, casting off whatever confines him and dissolving into total transformation, freedom imagined as radical self-removal.","sonnet":"A person undergoes radical self-surgery — cutting away every identity, every defining constraint, every piece of the old self that no longer serves. The metamorphosis is violent and deliberate, driven by vertigo and a parasite-like need to escape who he has been.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~not-over-til-we-say-so-feat-emma-anzai-of-sick-puppies":{"haiku":"A collective refuses defeat despite persecution and fear, determined to resist until they declare victory themselves.","opus":"A defiant anthem of endurance against a pursuing force, weak but unbroken and refusing to concede while any fight remains. Built for the moment you stand up when you're supposed to be finished.","sonnet":"A defiant survival rally — whatever fear or force is hunting them, the narrator refuses to yield and vows to bring an army with them, insisting the fight isn't over until they decide it is.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~one-last-breath":{"haiku":"A person faces death and dissolution, seeking escape from a harsh, meaningless existence through one final moment of transcendence.","opus":"A desolate vision of dying and being uprooted from existence into an icy, endless void of nothingness. The one small mercy it offers is a final exhale and farewell that lets the soul slip free of a merciless world toward the horizon where land and heavens meet.","sonnet":"A song about death and the moment of release — the narrator describes being pulled from the earth into a cold void, finally escaping the cruelty of the living world with one last exhale before everything unmakes itself.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~prognosis":{"opus":"A brooding reflection on the body's inevitable failure, questioning how much longer the flesh can be trusted and how near the end really is. The mood is one of surrender, a slow slide into darkness as the last of the light recedes.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality and the body's inevitable decline — the narrator contemplates how long the physical self can endure, sinking into darkness as the end approaches, with no resolution offered beyond the question itself.","src":"sonnet"},
"blue-stahli~ready-aim-fire":{"haiku":"Millions chase ambition but self-doubt paralyzes them; the call is to act decisively and push past hesitation to claim what one desires.","opus":"Bret Autrey's cinematic industrial-rock project builds a taut motivational surge around millions strung out on ambition, each clinging to a plan to escape their circumstances. The refrain functions as a mantra of decisive action against hesitation, warning that a thousand deaths wait in doubt, urging one to trust the quietest inner signal and finally commit rather than stall.","sonnet":"Ambition stalls in hesitation — millions strung out on hope, withered by the weight of indecision. Stripping away doubt and getting close to one's own conviction is framed as the single act that transforms paralysis into motion. The command to fire is less about aggression than about committing fully to action before the moment dissolves.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~ready-aim-fire-instrumental":{"haiku":"Ambition breeds hesitation and self-sabotage, millions striving yet failing to escape the machinery. Only those who move decisively—who fire without doubt—break the cycle of doubt and death.","opus":"A driving push to seize inspiration before doubt smothers it, imagining vast crowds hooked on ambition and answering with a clipped, trigger-pull cadence of resolve, aiming clear of uncertainty and acting on what you already understand.","sonnet":"Millions of people chasing ambition are crushed by pressure that pushes them down, but the song urges firing through hesitation and doubt, locating the drive to act in something quiet and soul-deep. The mantra \"ready, aim, fire\" is both military command and self-directive.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~rockstar":{"haiku":"An inspirational anthem about overcoming adversity and self-doubt, urging the listener to embrace their potential and fight against despair.","opus":"An unashamed pep anthem directed at someone declared beyond hope, urging them to spurn the kindly surrender of sterile hospital rooms and battle the feeling that existence is a merciless deception. Hardship becomes fuel for reinvention. Blue Stahli, the solo electronic-rock venture of producer Bret Autrey on the FiXT label, delivers the uplift with the same cinematic heft that drives his darker material.","sonnet":"An anthem of defiant resilience addressed to someone facing a terminal or crushing situation — possibly illness, given the white-walled observation setting. The speaker reframes adversity as fuel and insists the listener already has what it takes to fight back, pushing them to rise rather than accept defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~scrape":{"haiku":"Desire to burn away and destroy those who deceive and manipulate. Violence and infliction are presented as passionate, destructive acts of rebellion.","opus":"An industrial-rock rush of violent, sensual imagery aimed at burning down false idols and parasites. It's more visceral atmosphere than argument, reveling in destruction and the seductive pull of another's inner violence, style delivering the menace.","sonnet":"A song driven by a volatile mix of destructive desire and contempt for false leaders — \"parasite messiahs\" — who feed people lies. The speaker turns this aggression into something almost erotic, fixating on the violence inside another person's mind as a source of fascination.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~takedown":{"haiku":"A person confronts and defeats someone who has wronged them. They vow to break their antagonist's pride and return the hate that was inflicted upon them.","opus":"An aggressive industrial power fantasy of dismantling a bully's pride and dignity, the taunting relish of turning their own hatred back on them; the pleasure of dominance thinly disguised as righteous retaliation.","sonnet":"Pure confrontational aggression directed at a bully or adversary — the speaker has absorbed all the contempt and hate thrown at them and is now coming back with it full force, wanting to humiliate and destroy the person who pushed them.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~the-beginning":{"haiku":"Cinematic instrumental intro staged as commentary, opening an album like a dramatic landscape unfolding.","opus":"Not a song but a spoken commentary track: the producer explaining how he built this cinematic piece for an X-Men trailer, then expanded it to open his album like a landscape unfolding.","sonnet":"A spoken commentary track in which the artist describes composing the song for an X-Men film trailer and extending it for the album, discussing the transition between the cinematic opening and a \"big, techy war drum section\" with distorted cello pulses. Not a lyric-driven song.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~the-devil":{"haiku":"The devil claims to be your own pride and choice—you are the architect of your despair; no external force controls you, only you pull your strings.","opus":"A confrontational reflection on owning your choices, narrated by the devil as the arrogance dwelling within a person, insisting no one is truly powerless and no outside force shoves anyone toward destruction. The verdict is unforgiving: you and you alone build your own misery. Blue Stahli, Bret Autrey's FiXT-label electronic-rock guise, sheaths that grim self-blame in sleek, aggressive production.","sonnet":"A philosophical provocation that locates the devil not in some external tempter but squarely inside the self — in pride, in the choices we make and then disclaim. The speaker refuses the comforting fiction of being pushed into ruin by outside forces, insisting that every act of self-destruction has only one architect.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~the-fall":{"haiku":"Material wealth and status are hollow and temporary; pride leads to inevitable loss and the revelation that only authenticity survives.","opus":"A warning to a status-obsessed person hoarding wealth and fame, that pride comes before the fall and everything material will drop away; the deeper claim is that only what is real endures once the throne is lost, and we all face that reckoning eventually.","sonnet":"A meditation on the collapse of ego-built lives — everything accumulated through pride, wealth, and status will eventually fall away, and what remains after that stripping-down is the only thing that was ever real.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~throw-away":{"haiku":"People are discarded and trapped in false hopes, feeding ambition and loneliness into nothing. The only victory is bare survival.","opus":"A stark reflection on feeling worthless and cast aside, hollowed out by ambition and barely held together. The singer rejects false reassurance, refusing to pretend things are fine, and lands on a grim conclusion: against loneliness and self-erasure, mere survival is sometimes the best outcome available.","sonnet":"Two people who know they've been discarded — by themselves and each other — feeding on the scraps of ambition while the loneliness behind the eyes deepens, with the honest conclusion that sometimes just surviving is the best you can do.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~ultranumb":{"haiku":"Media overstimulation and voyeurism desensitize people; constant spectacle renders them numb to their own exploitation.","opus":"An aggressive takedown of media saturation and voyeurism, framing modern entertainment as an addiction that overstimulates its audience into deadened indifference. The taunting refrain implicates both the performer and the spectator, accusing each of feeding off shame and spectacle until nothing registers anymore.","sonnet":"A hyper-stimulation satire — framed as a countdown show with stage-direction banter, it skewers the culture of voyeuristic media excess, overstimulation, and degradation-as-entertainment, arguing the audience is complicit in their own numbing.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-stahli~you-ll-get-what-s-coming-feat-mark-salomon":{"haiku":"Those who betray, manipulate, and seek fame through deception will inevitably face retribution for their treachery and moral emptiness.","opus":"A vengeful promise of comeuppance to a two-faced, treacherous social climber who betrays with a friendly shiv and manipulates for fame; the barbed line that success came from who you suck best frames the target as a fraud whose karma is finally coming.","sonnet":"About karmic retribution for a manipulative, two-faced person — someone who shows a second face, backstabs, and climbs by flattering the right people; the narrator guarantees that what they've given out will come back to them.","src":"haiku"},
"blue-yster-cult~don-t-fear-the-reaper":{"haiku":"Death claims lovers and countless souls daily. Flying together toward darkness becomes romance—no fear needed.","opus":"An invitation to meet death without terror, framing eternity as reunion the way Romeo and Juliet are joined; the 40,000 a day and the appearing figure at the window turn dying into a calm passage rather than a horror.","sonnet":"A persuasion toward accepting death as reunion — framed through Romeo and Juliet as lovers who are together in eternity, the song coaxes the listener to take the narrator's hand and follow him into death, treating mortality as transcendence rather than loss.","src":"haiku"},
"blueshift~cloak-and-dagger":{"opus":"A confrontation with a two-faced manipulator whose facade has finally slipped, revealing a fake, character-less chameleon; the singer refuses to be controlled like a puppet, cuts the strings, and removes himself from their negative influence.","sonnet":"The narrator has seen through a two-faced manipulator — once close, now exposed as a chameleon who controlled, deceived, and drifted away — and resolves to remove themselves from the negative space and fill their life with something genuine instead.","src":"sonnet"},
"blue~shreddy-vs-margaret":{"haiku":"The speaker drifts faster as moments slip away, consciousness splitting between urgency and erosion while past embers preserve the architecture of one's becoming. Memory burns as an offering to the next self.","opus":"A meditation on impermanence and the dissolving of ego, urging that the past be kept as embers rather than clung to. Its Buddhist middle-path aside makes the aim plain: to work for the beauty of what's here now, letting attachment burn off without mourning it.","sonnet":"Holding on across drift and disintegration: the narrator feels someone slipping out of their light and pleads not to be let fade, anchoring the whole piece in the Buddhist-tinged spoken section — preserve the past in embers, work for the present moment, choose creation over dissolution.","src":"haiku"},
"blur~song-2":{"haiku":"A speaker receives head trauma and feels heavy metal, pins and needles, and lies. The song captures disorientation and uncertainty about dependency on another.","opus":"A deliberately dumb, euphoric send-up of grunge, its lyrics a string of near-meaningless fragments about paranoia and jangled nerves. Under the whooping abandon runs a small anxious thread of dependence the narrator can't explain, the whole thing tossed off with cheerful, throwaway energy.","sonnet":"A half-confessional about emotional unreliability and disconnection — the narrator admits to lying easily and not knowing why he needs the other person — but the song wears its own incoherence lightly, almost proudly.","src":"haiku"},
"blur~song-2-2012-remaster":{"haiku":"Detached numbness from physical sensation, accepting neediness while doubting its justification.","opus":"Britpop's most gleeful non-statement, nonsense verses fired off purely to detonate into a scream. It parodies grunge angst while becoming its purest distillation, meaning nothing and landing like a punch.","sonnet":"A gleefully unhinged admission of lying, needing someone inexplicably, and feeling the pull of heavy metal distortion as a kind of emotional state — the narrator has had his head 'done' and can't explain why he needs whoever he's singing to, but doesn't particularly care.","src":"haiku"},
"blush-response~outlaw69":{"haiku":"Nostalgia and fantasy seduce the speaker while reality crumbles; fear of mortality translates to fear of connection, leaving only the dream as refuge.","opus":"Watching yourself erode into a worn-down stranger you can't recognize or love, taunted by a glossy fantasy of who you were meant to be. The grim knot: what keeps the narrator going isn't any will to live but sheer terror of the end.","sonnet":"A speaker who has become a stranger to themselves — a \"tired, broken, failure\" — clings to fantasy because the real self is impossible to love, numbing the gap with medication and nostalgia. The only thing keeping them alive is fear of dying, which they name flatly rather than dress up.","src":"haiku"},
"blvk-swvn~beauty-queen":{"haiku":"A person rejects society's constructed beauty standards and refuses to perform femininity, asserting their authentic self against manufactured expectations.","opus":"Built on a defiant rejection of manufactured flawlessness, the track spurns the cosmetically sculpted pageant ideal and the pill-numbed insecurity it feeds. One vivid touch, padding tucked into the pants to dodge mockery, roots the anxiety in the body before the narrator flings it back outward, hurling a challenger's demand for masculinity in his face as a boast of being more genuine.","sonnet":"Rejecting a beauty standard built on surgery and pharmaceutical props, the speaker claims an identity outside the mold entirely. External pressure to perform conventional masculinity is met with calm defiance: authenticity, however irregular, outweighs any conformity imposed from outside. The refusal to wilt is the whole argument.","src":"haiku"},
"bob-moses~back-down":{"haiku":"Collective compromise on values erodes shared humanity; the speaker and their generation fragment under divided loyalties and broken faith.","opus":"A defiant anthem of standing your ground amid division, framing a shared fight for a bright future being torn apart by lies. It rejects surrender while mourning the broken ties and stolen dream that the conflict has cost.","sonnet":"A collective confrontation with political and social fracture: faith and devotion have blinded people, lies have broken ties, the forgotten have been failed — and the refrain \"never going back, never gonna back down\" insists on resistance even as \"you and I found our faith in the will of a lie.\"","src":"haiku"},
"bob-vylan~northern-line":{"haiku":"Anxiety, propaganda, and the fear imposed by media warnings about danger. The speaker feels trapped by fearmongering voices and societal dread.","opus":"Panic and menace grip a commuter on London's subway, an overstrained, jittery mind feeling threatened, suffocated, and desperate to reach safety while agitators stoke bigotry. The British punk-rap duo, known for attacking nationalism and racism, fuse a claustrophobic anxiety attack with a takedown of flag-waving hostility, turning an ordinary transit ride into a portrait of a person besieged inside and out.","sonnet":"Riding the London Underground while anxiety tightens its grip, a narrator finds public space saturated with fear-mongering noise that makes every commute feel like a war zone. The desperate wish to simply go home collides with voices insisting the outside world is too dangerous to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"bob-vylan~we-live-here":{"haiku":"Racism and xenophobia target the speaker's family despite legal belonging, with violence and dehumanization forming the daily cost of existence in hostile land. They refuse erasure and claim permanent residence.","opus":"A furious rebuke to British racism, the mixed-race narrator recounting being called slurs as a child and told to go home while insisting this country is his, invoking Stephen Lawrence's murder.","sonnet":"A Black British speaker recounts a lifetime of racism — neighbours calling him a slur, being told to go back to his country, the murder of Stephen Lawrence at a bus stop — insisting against all of it that this is his country, it has never been lovely, and he was never an outsider appearing from nowhere.","src":"haiku"},
"bobby-byrd~hot-pants-i-m-coming-i-m-coming":{"haiku":"Sexual promise and anticipation accelerate as the speaker pledges devotion and pleasure; arrival and urgency fuse into a single insistent demand.","opus":"A funk plea for a partner to grasp that love is a mutual exchange where you receive only what you put in. The urgent refrain works as both courtship and pure physical arrival, offering a clean slate instead of a broken heart.","sonnet":"A direct, urgent address to a lover: the speaker is on their way and wants the other person ready, framing desire as a two-sided exchange where you only get back what you bring. The repeated \"I'm coming\" doubles as sexual urgency and emotional commitment, with a call for a fresh start underpinning the rawness.","src":"haiku"},
"bobby-wolfgang~ten-foot-tall":{"haiku":"Resistance burns pure while everyone talks without listening, the speaker claiming dominance through refusal to be diminished. Power demands getting out of their own way, stopping the self-recrimination.","opus":"Defiant self-mythologizing against gossip and noise, the narrator shrugging off the he-said-she-said and inflating himself to cannonball scale as armor for a life he keeps fumbling but insists on living his own way.","sonnet":"An insomnia-fuelled, hypocritical social world — where everyone talks but no one listens — is met with a posture of defiant self-sufficiency: \"I'm ten foot tall,\" hard as a cannonball, done caring about gossip and performing for others. The repeated demand to stay out of their business doubles as the chorus.","src":"haiku"},
"bodyimage~angels-in-the-tv":{"haiku":"Mediated reality and digital static replace authentic presence as the speaker ages without resolution. Deferral becomes impossible as time pressures mounting consequences.","opus":"Fraying patience and aging in real time as someone keeps putting off what can't be put off. Static in the fingertips and figures glimpsed in the television screen render a mind overloaded by screens and messages, desperate to be told everything's still the way it was.","sonnet":"A fraying patience under unresolved tension — lines deepening in skin, static in fingertips, angels visible in television static — the speaker refuses to be told \"let's put a pin in it\" when there is no time, and insists this is nothing like it was before.","src":"haiku"},
"bodyimage~flesh":{"haiku":"Disposability becomes relational template as the speaker collects partners as objects while performing complexity masking hollow instrumental accumulation.","opus":"Desiring someone who collects people like trophies, hating yourself for wanting a serial heartbreaker who feels nothing. The read is the humiliation of obsession with a person who treats lovers as interchangeable stock and can't cry for anyone but themselves.","sonnet":"A speaker confronts someone who collects people for lust and discards them, pointing out the predatory mechanics of their game — the indifference, the impatience, the inability to form genuine connection. The speaker is simultaneously drawn to and disgusted by the person, obsessed despite knowing they mean nothing to them.","src":"haiku"},
"bodyimage~goddammit":{"haiku":"Frustration with paradox of giving up versus continuing, trapped in cycle where every choice fails.","opus":"A spiraling anthem of self-sabotage, every rhetorical question circling the same trap of trying to hold it together while wrecking your own plans. The repeated curse is exasperation turned inward, the exhausting loop of knowing better and failing anyway.","sonnet":"The comedy of intractable self-sabotage — every attempt to figure things out ends with the words going right back into the narrator's mouth, the hardest pill spat out, every plan fucked up on cue. The rhetorical questions that open and close the song (\"how do I fail if I quit? where do I stand if I sit?\") are genuinely unanswerable, which is the whole problem.","src":"haiku"},
"bodyimage~lead-u-2-me":{"haiku":"Pain becomes currency in a transaction where vulnerability meets numbness, the speaker inducing suffering to feel anything. Being seen requires being broken open.","opus":"A hyperpop knot of desire and self-erasure, where pain becomes proof and being pulled apart and rearranged into someone's doll reads as intimacy, two people wearing down their bones in a bond that sees through them both.","sonnet":"A physically and emotionally punishing dynamic — drinking to numbness, bleeding when struck, performing toughness — is described through a repeated mantra of self-destruction as proof of something to a partner who sees through the speaker entirely. Bones wearing down is both literal and relational.","src":"haiku"},
"bodyimage~the-ghost-and-the-father":{"haiku":"Transgressive sacred experience of sexual transcendence and meeting divine through flesh and sin.","opus":"A collision of lust and religious ecstasy, the narrator craving to walk on water and touch the divine through sin and skin. It stages the old tension between flesh and salvation, meeting God through transgression, beauty so intense it borders on damnation.","sonnet":"Sacred and carnal desire collapse into each other — walking on water and skinny-dipping, touching skin like an altar, pressing a tongue against a spine and feeling the divine. The narrator claims to know sin and the Holy Trinity in the same breath, meeting God through the body before death, an ecstasy that draws a parenthetical \"go to Hell\" from somewhere off to the side.","src":"haiku"},
"bodyimage~who-cares":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and indifference to self-destruction, questioning whether speed and intensity matter at all.","opus":"The exhausted comedown after a life lived too fast, chest-tight anxiety and burnout narrated with a defensive shrug. The who-cares bravado barely hides regret about all the numbing and the wish it had been done sober.","sonnet":"Burnout and emotional exhaustion in a fast-moving life — the narrator hides how wrecked they are, wonders if everything was always going this way, questions whether the people around them have bad intentions, and eventually decides they might just let all of it go.","src":"haiku"},
"bodyweb~sugarcoated":{"haiku":"Codependent cycle of craving and rejection, using someone to feel something while pushing them away.","opus":"Toxic attachment framed as chemical dependency, the narrator needing to be wanted only so they can push someone away. Antidepressants and self-loathing braided into a love that's really just the pain they know best.","sonnet":"About a toxic push-pull dependency — the narrator craves being wanted only so they can push the person away, and the relationship is framed as an addiction no different from a drug, with the coating of sweetness masking the damage underneath; Sertraline makes a cameo as another substance layered on top.","src":"haiku"},
"boko-yout~9-2-5":{"haiku":"Economic desperation and minimum-wage servitude define survival, where hope for stability wars with exhaustion and the futility of labor.","opus":"An ironic anthem yearning for the very desk job it ridicules, chanting for a modest paycheck, retirement savings and free coffee by the copier. Under the hypnotic loop hides the admission that his professional ambitions were a delusion, the office daydream substituting for any steady place to belong.","sonnet":"A tragicomic desire for the stability of ordinary employment — the narrator's creative career has been a lie and they just want minimum wage, a 401K, HR, and a break room TV; the song is earnest about craving the safety of conventional life after the fantasy of something else hasn't panned out.","src":"haiku"},
"boko-yout~volleyball":{"haiku":"Wasted vulnerability in collective suffering; a small defeat becomes metaphor for cosmic injustice and the speaker's dispensability.","opus":"An absurdist spiral where a bad volleyball tournament becomes the trigger for existential dread, softness and self-doubt bubbling into surreal revelation. The nonsense chant and shrug of needing no validation turn petty humiliation into a joke about meaning itself.","sonnet":"A volleyball tournament becomes the setting for a sudden revelation at the pinnacle of pain — dust to dust, recognising a soul in another's face — while the repeated refrain of being too soft and involved for nothing frames the awakening as one nobody else would understand.","src":"haiku"},
"bomfunk-mc-s~freestyler":{"haiku":"A rapper improvises spontaneous verse, boasting of style variety and skill while delivering entertainment and infectious radio-friendly bars.","opus":"A pure celebration of the rap-off, all braggadocio and wordplay as the MC boasts of rocking the mic straight off the dome, name-dropping pop-culture punchlines from Celine Dion to Buckingham Palace. Substance is beside the point; flow and swagger are everything. Bomfunk MC's were a Finnish outfit, and this 2000 breakbeat-rap smash became one of the era's defining Euro-dance crossover hits.","sonnet":"Pure mic-confidence as a manifesto — the MC flows off the top, staking a claim to spontaneous skill and cataloguing an absurdly varied menu of styles available on demand. The whole thing is a flex wrapped in playful braggadocio, less interested in any particular subject than in the sheer pleasure of wordplay and presence.","src":"haiku"},
"bon-iver~re-stacks":{"haiku":"Excavation unearths only loss; accumulated wealth crumbles while the narrator grasps at meaning beneath frozen ground.","opus":"The quiet aftermath of loss, gambling money and love away in a frozen ground of grief, then arriving not at revelation but at release. Written in Justin Vernon's cabin isolation, it ends the record by simply letting go and promising to keep someone's love safe.","sonnet":"A speaker excavates a moment of near-total loss — gambling debts, isolation, love frozen in the ground — and arrives at an unexpected release, offering the assurance that whatever love remains will be kept safe.","src":"haiku"},
"bon-iver~skinny-love":{"haiku":"A fragile romance suffocates under the weight of imbalance and restraint, collapsing as hope dies and lies accumulate.","opus":"A love too undernourished to survive, begging it to last one more year while knowing it won't. The famous title names the real thing: a relationship starved of what it needed, ending in mutual accusation and the bleak question of who could ever love either of them.","sonnet":"A relationship running on fumes — threadbare, malnourished by too much demand and too little reciprocity — is addressed in fragments, with the speaker having given unreturned instructions and now facing collapse. The final questions have no answers: who will be left to love or fight?","src":"haiku"},
"bon-jovi~livin-on-a-prayer":{"haiku":"Two working-class lovers commit to persisting through hardship together, maintaining hope and unity as their only resources against poverty and despair.","opus":"Blue-collar sweethearts Tommy and Gina cling on through a stalled dockworkers' strike and a hocked guitar, gambling that being together means something even if success never comes. Bon Jovi's 1986 signature turned Reagan-era Jersey struggle into an arena chorus of grit, the roughly-there hook and the pledge to try their luck reworking hard times into a defiant, communal optimism that made it a stadium staple.","sonnet":"A working-class couple — one dockworker on strike, one diner waitress — holds their relationship together against financial ruin on nothing but mutual love and determination. The song is an act of collective faith that love alone can carry two people through poverty to something better.","src":"haiku"},
"bon-jovi~wanted-dead-or-alive":{"haiku":"A roaming outlaw rides on, worn down by constant movement and disconnection, played everywhere yet never at peace, pursued or wanted.","opus":"Bon Jovi mythologizing the touring rock band as outlaw gunslingers, the guitar a six-string weapon and the highway an endless drift of cold faces. Underneath the cowboy swagger sits real road-weariness and the loneliness of the never-ending grind.","sonnet":"A touring musician romanticises the road life as that of a lone cowboy — constant motion, irregular sleep, bottle-measured days, and a loaded guitar as the only weapon — casting the grind of rock touring as outlaw mythology.","src":"haiku"},
"bon-jovi~you-give-love-a-bad-name":{"haiku":"A manipulative lover wounds the narrator deeply through deception and false promises. The song describes romantic betrayal as a weaponized act.","opus":"A scorned lover's dramatic tirade against a woman who offered bliss and delivered misery, framed as a fatal seduction from which there is no rescue. Bon Jovi's 1986 breakthrough from Slippery When Wet, co-written with Desmond Child, became a signature arena anthem and a blueprint for the slick, hook-forward glam metal of the era.","sonnet":"A betrayal framed as a gunshot wound — the speaker was lured in by someone who performed innocence and sweetness while dealing in calculated hurt. Love becomes a trap, passion a prison, and the damage is already done before the speaker even sees it coming; the accusation is furious but the wound is genuine.","src":"haiku"},
"bones-uk~cheap-love":{"haiku":"Hollowness spreads as love itself becomes commodity; the speaker chases cheap connection while everything that mattered erodes into darkness.","opus":"In a decaying nightlife scene everyone scrabbles for a scrap of warmth amid brawls, hustlers and slick, hollow people peddling counterfeit intimacy. As the sense that anything ever mattered drains away, the one plea is to keep the darkness outside from getting in.","sonnet":"A nocturnal crawl through 'war wounds, wild nights, cat fights' in search of love that turns out to be cheaply packaged and sold by 'well-dressed robots' — the birds no longer sing anything sweet, the dark outside bleeds into the self, and everything that mattered slowly stops mattering.","src":"haiku"},
"bones-uk~dirty-little-animals-from-the-series-arcane-league-of-legends":{"haiku":"Transgression and forbidden pleasure activate life force as morality dissolves; pain and danger promise authentic sensation in a world of numbness.","opus":"A sweaty ode to giving in to appetite, framing surrender to pleasure and self-destruction as the only real aliveness. Made for Arcane, it channels the show's decadent underworld: the little death that feels like living, the numbing you can't come back from.","sonnet":"A seductive invitation to transgression — the wrong that feels right, the little death that makes you feel alive — frames a descent into forbidden pleasure as liberation, before warning that once you go under, you will never come back up.","src":"haiku"},
"bones~icanseemyhousefromhere":{"opus":"An intense, possessive intimacy where the narrator feels locked inside himself and violated in his personal space, yet finds that a lover's touch grounds him, wanting only to watch that person live.","sonnet":"A song about emotional breakdown and intense fixation on another person — the narrator is trapped in their own mental space, not okay, but finds the other person's touch the only thing grounding them, expressing a desperate need to be seen and felt.","src":"sonnet"},
"bonnie-x-clyde~another-you":{"haiku":"Obsessive devotion and dependency translate uniqueness into irreplaceability as the speaker declares monolithic attachment to another person as their only possible anchor.","opus":"A dance-floor declaration that no one else could match the pull of this one person who sets the speaker's soul on fire. Survival depends on staying hypnotized by them, the whole song a vow that another such love will never come along.","sonnet":"Total devotion to one irreplaceable person: hypnotised by their presence, the speaker comes alive only when bodies are close, and the refrain \"I will never find another you\" circles like a certainty rather than a lament.","src":"haiku"},
"boom-boom-satellites~dive-for-you":{"haiku":"A person declares unconditional devotion and willingness to transform for their romantic partner in moments of intense connection.","opus":"The Japanese electronic duo ride euphoria into total devotion, a lover willing to dive for someone and be remade into whatever they want, the good times rolling toward a next lifetime. Surrender as bliss rather than loss.","sonnet":"Straightforward devotion to someone who defines the singer's sense of self — the other person gives life meaning and shapes identity, and the singer pledges total commitment including across lifetimes.","src":"haiku"},
"boom-boom-satellites~helter-skelter":{"haiku":"Uncontrolled descent and loss of balance in desire; the speaker pursues someone who isn't matched to their energy and chaos.","opus":"The Japanese electronic-rock duo takes on the Beatles' spiraling fairground metaphor, all descent and re-ascent down a helter-skelter slide, translating its churning chaos into their own bruising production. The lyric's push-pull demand, do you want me to love you, delivered from a lover who's fast but distant, keeps the original's frenzied, unresolved sexual tension intact rather than softening it.","sonnet":"A Beatles cover that rides the original's vertiginous slide metaphor without altering its core: desire and pursuit caught in a loop, descending fast toward someone always just in view at the bottom. The question of whether love or control governs the chase is left open, spinning in the same breathless circuit.","src":"haiku"},
"boom-boom-satellites~morning-after":{"haiku":"A hangover becomes a metaphor for emotional weariness and lost connection; the speaker retreats into routine while suppressing feelings that would require confrontation. Escapism masks deeper exhaustion.","opus":"The Japanese electronic-rock duo evoke the bleary aftermath of a heavy night, all hangover and restless want. Feeling trapped and empty shifts toward waking up and nodding off again, as the narrator lures a lover close while vowing to move forward. Woozy dream imagery of speeding nowhere runs alongside a lingering worry about being mistreated amid the haze.","sonnet":"The murky, disoriented hours after a night out press down on someone caught between waking and retreat. Dreams carry a closeness that evaporates by day, and restlessness propels him toward motion — toward going somewhere faster — though the destination remains unclear and the time mostly feels like it is being killed.","src":"haiku"},
"bootsy-collins~bootzilla":{"haiku":"Commodified funk becomes a programmable object designed to entertain, the doll programmed to seduce and perform. The metaphor celebrates the pleasure of being wound up and used.","opus":"Bootsy's cartoonish alias as a mechanical, jewel-studded toy version of a music icon, a gleeful P-Funk self-mockery pitching himself as a funky plaything for rent, ribbing commercial hype while dishing out exactly the groove it promises.","sonnet":"The persona of Bootzilla — a wind-up rhinestone rock star monster doll — is introduced as a funk-producing toy that dances, sings, and plays on command, inviting the audience to wind it up and enjoy. The conceit satirises celebrity and the performer-audience relationship through joyful absurdity.","src":"haiku"},
"bootsy-collins~i-d-rather-be-with-you":{"haiku":"Devoted love expresses itself through desire for companionship above all else, the speaker choosing togetherness as the ultimate good. Holding hands, hearing laughter—these suffice as proof of everything that matters.","opus":"A plainspoken, slow-burn declaration of devotion, the funk bassist dropping the cartoon persona to insist, against a cold world, that no place and no one matters more than being with the one he loves.","sonnet":"A straightforward declaration of devotion: the speaker wants to be their lover's friend, number one, and constant companion, knowing the world is cold but insisting love is worth coming back for. The aside — \"I'm coming at you with both hands tied behind my back\" — signals total, vulnerable commitment.","src":"haiku"},
"boots~dead-come-running":{"haiku":"The speaker interrogates modern life's contradictions—fatigue, ambition, and sensory overload—moving between boasts and doubts about strength and endurance.","opus":"The producer Boots layers hallucinatory, dread-soaked art-pop around a man cracking under his own life, images tumbling out as corpses stalk the nightly headlines. Between flashes of paranoia he keeps asking whether he's tough enough and experienced enough to hold it together, the whole thing haunted by a wish to just let everything collapse.","sonnet":"A performer tests his own strength and composure against a chaotic urban landscape where the dead seem to haunt every news cycle. Amid fire, wolves, and fragmented street imagery, he reaches for ammunition — inner resources — to hold his cool and keep flowing.","src":"haiku"},
"borders~godless":{"haiku":"Absence of divinity and faith, world without higher power or meaning.","opus":"A violent renunciation of faith, the narrator concluding that religion itself is the poison in a world built only to die. The metalcore fury is the pain of losing belief he was raised on, choosing godlessness as liberation from a god who condones cruelty.","sonnet":"A break from faith under the weight of divinely condoned violence — \"my faith is the problem\" — the narrator declares themselves godless, a Judas at the masses, washing away the sins of heaven. Selling the soul before someone else could take it is positioned as the only act of self-determination left.","src":"haiku"},
"boris~ano-onna-no-onryou":{"haiku":"A spectral woman's curse binds the speaker to inevitable doom; resignation and surrender characterize the collapse into ash and erasure.","opus":"A vengeful female spirit whose stone eyes already know the narrator's end, dragging him into a dance of the dead. Losing sight, color, pain and shape one by one, he tries to escape and is burnt to ashes into an endless nothing.","sonnet":"A man is being haunted and destroyed by the vengeful spirit of a woman — her stone gaze, waving hair, and wordless doom-spilling presence strip away everything he wants to see and feel, until there is no escape and no ending left.","src":"haiku"},
"boris~farewell":{"haiku":"Goodbye arrives after long absence, words nearly lost but journey complete. The farewell waves from shore, releasing what once seemed close.","opus":"A hushed reckoning with letting go — turning away from the ready-made answers that promise the way, and following your own will into the distance with nothing but a wave. Boris bury the goodbye in slow, luminous drone, so the parting feels less like loss than a quiet act of will.","sonnet":"A sparse, meditative farewell to something already long gone — words almost lost, a distance felt even when near — ends in a simple wave goodbye. The speaker turns away from ready-made answers and moves forward on their own will.","src":"haiku"},
"boris~funnel-of-love":{"haiku":"Love spirals inescapably like a funnel, senseless and dizzying but impossible to flee. Running only delays the inevitable descent into vertigo.","opus":"A Wanda Jackson cover surrendering to love as a vertigo you can't outrun, the drone-rock band drawing out the helpless spiral where the head reels and hiding only delays the inevitable pull downward.","sonnet":"Helpless surrender to love is rendered as a physical vortex — head spinning, knees weak, mind blanked — that no amount of running or hiding can escape. The funnel is described as inevitable and universal: it will get you someday.","src":"haiku"},
"boris~hope":{"haiku":"Dreams persist while love gets understood. Forward movement and starlight sustain endurance.","opus":"A gentle remembrance of someone who urged big dreams and living forward into tomorrow, distilling love into small tender gestures of stopping the crying, smiling, and counting stars.","sonnet":"A quiet love song about someone who keeps dreaming big and running hard, whose optimism exhausts itself but never quite stops — the narrator watches them with total certainty about what love is, and the whole song is a gentle instruction to keep smiling and looking forward.","src":"haiku"},
"boris~just-abandoned-myself":{"haiku":"A speaker surrenders control, rejecting choice and authority. Meanings blur and collapse until only questioning remains.","opus":"A dense interrogation of self and existence, afraid of past and future while thirsting in the present, questioning oneself against an image of the one that rules until blurred sight clears and the self is everywhere at once.","sonnet":"A philosophical song about dismantling the ego and confronting the layered meanings imposed on existence — the narrator struggles with paralysis between past and future, overwhelmed by accumulated meanings and superimposed images, until they make a choice to seize what's ahead, breaking through to a state where they are present everywhere at once.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song seals its senses against a present too heavy to hold, a self cornered by memory and dread and parched no matter which way it turns. Its logic warps Descartes: being no longer follows from thinking but from bowing to some ruling figure, and the instant that ground is claimed it curdles into interrogation, so the certainty and the doubt keep overprinting each other until neither holds still. What breaks the loop is not a better argument but the guitars — strings swelling into a single dazzling roar, the blur dissolving as thought melts down into pure tone. Only once the reasoning surrenders to the drone does the speaker come loose from any single spot, dispersed and full at once. That is the title's paradox: to give oneself up is to stop being anywhere in particular, and so to be everywhere.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"certainty surrendering to drone as the path to dispersal","built":"the title names disappearance as the only form of presence"},
"boris~pink":{"haiku":"Two people make knowing choices leading to painful awareness, where surface smiles hide lies beneath superficial connection and eye contact.","opus":"An oblique, aching study of a knowing choice made despite its cost, circling a smile that hides pain and eye contact that keeps sliding back into lies, the speaker unable to name a reason yet refusing to look away. Boris are a Japanese trio prized for shape-shifting between drone, doom and pop; this title track from their 2005 album fuses shoegaze haze with buzzsaw noise-rock momentum.","sonnet":"A fractured meditation on complicity and self-deception — both the speaker and the person addressed made choices they already knew were wrong, and now maintain hollow smiles while the real reasons stay unspoken. The recurring image of following a line without looking away describes someone performing composure while slowly unraveling inside.","src":"haiku"},
"boris~question-1":{"haiku":"問 (questioning) illuminates through darkness and silence, the speaker reaching toward light while floating through cosmic void. The journey asks rather than answers, consciousness itself the mirror reflecting inward infinitely.","opus":"A cosmic meditation on dawn breaking the primordial dark, tears drying as consciousness arrives with each new morning, the self repeatedly born into a chain of small miracles reaching toward the light.","sonnet":"A meditation on consciousness emerging from darkness: the speaker gazes through a skylight at stars, feels tears dry, and contemplates the horizon that holds the warmth of waves before dawn breaks and the self arrives. The closing image is light answering the question posed by existence itself.","src":"haiku"},
"boris~woman-on-the-screen":{"haiku":"Fascination through movement and mystery; a woman performs in paradoxical ways that attract and daze.","opus":"A translated, disorienting piece of erotic menace, the swirling dance and mirrors building toward violation dressed as fantasy. Boris weaponizes ambiguity, blurring seduction and destruction until the woman on the screen becomes both object of desire and prey.","sonnet":"A predatory address to a woman reduced to image and surface — her gaze, her dance, her weakness — with the speaker offering violence as a kind of fulfilment. The mirrors thrown away, then tacked behind the door, the promise to open and penetrate and fill a fantasy: a deeply uncomfortable portrait of projection, consumption, and the violence latent in being watched.","src":"haiku"},
"born-of-osiris~machine":{"haiku":"Individuality erodes when conformity replaces consciousness; the speaker ruptures from the machinery to reclaim identity as an act of defiance against systematic erasure.","opus":"A call to look hard in the mirror and ask whether your soul is still what it once was, or whether some default programming now runs your life without your noticing. The plea is to pull yourself out of the machine and reclaim who you are, insisting human connection is larger and more sacred than any one person.","sonnet":"An urgent appeal to look in the mirror and ask whether your soul's reflection matches what you were — and to separate yourself from the machine before it is too late. Human fellowship is declared sacred, the stakes are explicitly larger than the individual, and the \"default setting\" running your life in secret is named as the real enemy.","src":"haiku"},
"born-of-osiris~torchbearer":{"haiku":"Complicity through lies transforms truth inside, the world stealing what it wants from the weak. Only those who carry the torch homeward—who reject resignation—survive the long dark nights.","opus":"A plea from exhaustion for someone to guide you home through the dark, the world stripping away what it wants until, paralyzed by cruel words, resolve curdles into vengeance against the one who broke you.","sonnet":"Exhausted after six days without sleep on empty roads, the speaker begs a torchbearer to carry them home from darkness into sunrise, pushing through paralysis caused by words that terrified rather than guided. The final threat to crucify the source of those words converts pleading into defiance.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~carbon-mono":{"haiku":"Confrontation with someone static and harmful who refuses change. The speaker rejects manipulation, asserts power, and walks away as this person stagnates while the speaker ascends.","opus":"The British band confronts a manipulative figure who squandered every opportunity and repackages his cruelty as blunt honesty. Their dark alt-rock throws his own smooth, self-serving pitches back at him, the narrator watching that person's life slowly unravel while refusing to seek his approval any longer.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone toxic, probably a figure of authority, who has failed every chance to change and now watches his world decay while others move forward. The speaker refuses permission-seeking and delivers an ultimatum soaked in accumulated contempt.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~container":{"haiku":"Institutional containment suppresses individuality; the speaker breaks free from imposed identity and expectation.","opus":"A crisis of authenticity in an empty, echoing world, questioning whether anyone out there is real. Something has taken everything, and the demand is to stop performing an identity and instead show what you're truly made of before running.","sonnet":"A song about dissociation and being hollowed out by something unnamed — the narrator wakes up empty, enclosed in a container that has taken everything, cut off from oxygen and belief, and urges fleeing whatever has consumed them rather than explaining or confronting it.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~crocus":{"haiku":"Hunger for recognition among the forgettable, the speaker claws toward something worth having, aware they were never the chosen one.","opus":"Class resentment sharpened into ambition, the narrator sick of scraps in a rigged game, refusing to be who anyone wanted and clawing upward out of spite against the untouchable, laminated people above him.","sonnet":"A hungry, unentitled person demanding their fair share in a system rigged against them — not bitter enough to stop dreaming, but desperately aware that luck is a resource distributed unfairly, and that they are exactly who they are, not who anyone wanted.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~datura-dusk":{"haiku":"Urban decay breeds apathy and horror, the speaker numb to neighborhood violence and neighbor's cruelty. Scars deepen while the TV glows, suffering normalized into furniture.","opus":"A grim snapshot of a decaying, paranoid neighborhood, petty cruelties and robberies stacking up while people cluster around a chilly pale glow, scorching their own vision, frightened and isolated in a world coming quietly undone.","sonnet":"A neighbourhood in slow collapse — a burning car park, a man who killed his dog, money stolen from the kitchen — traps its residents in numb, fearful domestication, staring at a white flame in the dark because they are scared and alone.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~dc-mini":{"haiku":"Cyclical reset erases growth and renewal; numbness becomes the only survival strategy in a system designed to repeat defeat endlessly.","opus":"Waking each week into the same tidied-up loop, the speaker admitting flatly he has no idea how to actually live. Flooded ground and graveyards of dead phone boxes sketch a numbed, doomscrolling present where dulling the ache and never waking looks like the only mercy.","sonnet":"A weary narrator cycling through a life of resets and routines — gutters cleaned, blinds drawn, flooded streets — confesses they don't know how to live or forgive, watching the world's crises make people 'like you' angry while they themselves just want to be numbed and not woken.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~desperate-pleasures":{"opus":"An addict's ambivalent reckoning, vowing to become everything a parent or partner hates while cleaning up their mess, insisting he could never blame them even as his self-destructive addiction plays by no rules.","sonnet":"A complex song about a toxic or painful relationship with a parent or authority figure — the narrator wants to confront and surpass the damage inflicted while simultaneously being unable to assign blame, and discovering self-destructive patterns in themselves as a result.","src":"sonnet"},
"boston-manor~dissolve":{"haiku":"Youth promised freedom but chemistry of causality binds the speaker in stitches that must dissolve. Change is performance for others, the seams never truly mending.","opus":"A reckoning with a shapeshifting, people-pleasing self, offering to become whatever anyone needs while nursing chemical burns from the past, insisting the consequences of your own making can't simply be dissolved away.","sonnet":"A speaker who was chemically and behaviourally reckless in youth emerges with \"chemical burns\" and a willingness to change, addressing someone who called it right about them and asking for the wound of judgment to finally dissolve. Growing up is framed as something that can't be undone but can be accepted.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~everything-is-ordinary":{"haiku":"Numbness and avoidance of meaningful action, burning everything for thrills while hiding genuine emotion behind plastic conformity.","opus":"The Blackpool band's grimier post-punk turn dramatizes escapist self-destruction, running from feeling because doing something real is harder. Numbness becomes medicine, thrill-seeking a way to torch everything without consequence, all under the flattening verdict that the extraordinary has curdled into the merely ordinary. It reads as a portrait of comfortable emotional anesthesia and the wasted potential it leaves behind.","sonnet":"Numbness settles into everything after emotional burnout — burning things for the rush of it, swallowing medicine to feel nothing, hiding behind a plastic expression. The chorus refuses hope: ordinariness is not peace but erasure. The song mourns the person one might have become before the grey took over.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~floodlights-on-the-square":{"haiku":"Everything hurts and nothing makes sense; the speaker awakens daily to pain and resists the urge to surrender or flee.","opus":"The bleak everyday cycle of addiction and depression, coming to in agony and unable to rescue himself while still depending on the very person he can't bring himself to face. It's candid about the shame of using, the marks left by years and a self he no longer knows when there's no one else around.","sonnet":"The speaker is stuck in a spiral of dependency and self-neglect — waking with pain in the lungs, losing track of time, unable to ask for help while insisting they don't need it — and the person they \"still rely on\" is both anchor and witness to a version of themselves they can barely recognise.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~foxglove":{"haiku":"Entrapment in the middle ground leaves the speaker unable to escape cycles of self-sabotage and the gravitational pull of destructive patterns.","opus":"An addict's self-aware confession that he may be his own recurring problem, savoring a lover's touch he feels undeserving of while admitting no one is invincible.","sonnet":"About being trapped in a cycle of self-destructive behavior and bad choices — the speaker knows they're probably the problem, can't stop craving a toxic relationship's physical closeness, and accepts that they'll always end up at the bottom.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~foxglove-audiotree-live-version":{"haiku":"Patience invokes chemical dreams while the speaker sinks into self-awareness of their own boringness and neediness. Problems trail behind like satellites, perhaps originating in the self.","opus":"A live turn through addiction and self-loathing, the narrator naming himself the problem that keeps following him, barring the door against relapse while savoring the very touch he thinks he doesn't deserve, stuck in the middle.","sonnet":"Self-identified as a \"poster child\" for original sin, the speaker catalogues problems that keep following them — recognising they may be the problem themselves — while confessing they savour the touch of someone they feel they don't deserve. Free will is gathering dust; survival requires doing what must be done.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~halo":{"haiku":"Watches your halo fall as addiction spirals; tomorrow's promise never comes.","opus":"A stark, unromantic account of heroin addiction, the relief of the fix laid right beside the wreckage it leaves. The halo falling belongs to a person watching themselves lose everything they had, one tomorrow's promise at a time.","sonnet":"A speaker in the grip of heroin addiction watches everything he valued fall away — friendships, money, a halo of goodness — promising himself he will stop tomorrow while scars barely heal and cold sweats mark another morning without escape.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~heat-me-up":{"haiku":"Dependency as temperature control; the speaker needs another to regulate their emotional state, willing to lose everything for the warmth of control.","opus":"Gratitude to a partner who inflates and steadies the speaker, seeing something in him nobody else does. His devotion tips toward the possessive, wanting to keep her in a glass box and kill for her, while urging her to stop trying to fix what was already beautiful.","sonnet":"An intense devotion to someone who regulates the speaker's temperature — heating and cooling them as needed — rendered in protective excess: 'I'd keep you in a glass box I can see through, I'd kill anybody if you ever asked me to,' love expressed as absolute and slightly consuming.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~horses-in-a-dream":{"haiku":"Love submerges and multiplies, wave after wave of drowning made beautiful, the speaker surrendered to water dark as lye. Motion stalls into slow-motion ecstasy, bones soaked through.","opus":"A hypnotic surrender to being overtaken by love or desire, everything slowing as waves and dark water soak the narrator to the bone until he becomes wholly someone else's, a dissolution he'd willingly die for.","sonnet":"A lover's presence is experienced as a slow-motion dissolution — lanolin hills, dark water, percolating waves — until the speaker is soaked to the bone and fully claimed. The horses dancing through fields of water dark as lye give the surrender a mythic, elemental quality.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~i-don-t-like-people-they-don-t-like-me":{"haiku":"Isolation intensifies as sixteen days blur into cynical detachment, the speaker preferring dreams to the grey outside. Walls close tighter while loneliness ferments into identity.","opus":"Depressive, agoraphobic isolation delivered flatly — over two weeks shut in a grey room, the narrator a skin-deep oddity certain nobody's paying attention as the walls press in and even oversleeping can't halt the dreaming.","sonnet":"Sixteen days without leaving the bedroom, the speaker catalogues social withdrawal and creeping walls as both chosen and involuntary, drifting through the liminal as \"a cynical criminal in a black beanie.\" The refrain — I don't like people and they don't like me — is delivered as a matter of fact, not a complaint.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~inertia-single-edit":{"haiku":"Shared trajectory becomes solace as the speaker finds home in another's constancy; emotional inertia and devotion collapse into a single gravitational force.","opus":"A weary, tender portrait of two people who anchor each other through long absences and darkness, where one partner is still home no matter how far the other has gone. Inertia becomes their bond: the pull that keeps them in the same orbit until the wide world can be let back in.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship that has become a fixed point of gravity: the speaker has been gone a long time, working just enough to be independent, but the other person is still home — they share the same inertia, the same mark left on each other. The final image of pink light on skin and a new world outside turns the stasis into something almost hopeful.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~liquid":{"haiku":"Identity dissolves into formlessness; the speaker becomes liquid, shapeshifting to fit others' molds while losing coherence and autonomy.","opus":"A dissolving sense of self, the singer become liquid, formless and drifting, molding to others' expectations and painting pictures people want. Beneath the conflicted shape-shifting is a struggle to become the person he actually was before he began performing.","sonnet":"A self dissolving under external pressure: the speaker is liquid, melting into someone else's mould, painting pictures just for them, sinking through floorboards — \"I don't know who I'm supposed to be,\" caught between complete absorption into another person and the faint memory of who they were before.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~on-a-high-ledge":{"haiku":"Early realization of difference; pressure to mask and be what others demand.","opus":"A boy confessing his difference to a father who answers only with demands to man up, the ledge measuring how close that rejection pushes him. It's about queerness and softness crushed under masculine expectation.","sonnet":"A child addresses a father, confessing difference — not fitting the masculine mould, drawn to flowers rather than rough play, unable to cry the way the world expects — while the chant of 'man up' serves as the only response the world offers back.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~passenger":{"haiku":"Pulled through existence without agency, the speaker watches the world pass while fighting the current that drags them forward.","opus":"Dissociation in a moment when everyone chases notoriety, the narrator a bystander steering solo through his own existence, his flesh prickling and the reflection taunting him, aware of a terror larger than himself that everybody quietly recognizes.","sonnet":"A drug comedown on a slow afternoon — the speaker wakes at 2pm with skin crawling, feeling pulled through the world without agency, unnerved by the mirror and the ambient noise of everyone else's visible success, a passenger in their own body and life.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~plasticine-dreams":{"haiku":"Manufactured celebrity and artificial beauty; everyone is fake and the narrator drifts through inconsequence.","opus":"Boston Manor drift through a woozy fantasy of celebrity, flattering a star while losing their own train of thought and courting reckless thrills. Nodding to Radiohead, the British band sketch an artificial world of glamour and vice where privacy is thrown away and nothing is as flawless as it looks, admiration blurring into numbed detachment.","sonnet":"Fame and its plastic promises — celebrity worship, the sacrifice of privacy, beauty-queen artificiality — are observed with woozy detachment. The narrator orbits a shining figure while barely remembering what point he wanted to make, seduced by surfaces he knows are hollow.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~ratking":{"haiku":"Rejects being called an outsider while struggling with disconnection and fighting to stay clean day by day. Won't accept labels but questions belonging.","opus":"A prickly refusal to be pathologized, the narrator bristling at a partner's condescension, rejecting the label of freak while gesturing at private struggle, staying clean one day at a time and wondering if reality itself is a simulation. Boston Manor are a Blackpool band who drifted from pop-punk toward darker, industrial-tinged alt-rock, and this reflects that grimier, more anxious later sound.","sonnet":"The discomfort of someone who never quite fits the room — pushed against by people who get under their skin, accused of being a freak for simply not feeling what everyone else seems to feel. Underneath the defensiveness is a quieter admission of trying to stay clean and take things one day at a time, which lands harder than the anger.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~sliding-doors":{"haiku":"Chance encounters reshape fate while entropy accelerates; the speaker glimpses cosmic design behind causality.","opus":"Life reduced to a cosmic fluke, each person a chance tear in spacetime catching a glimpse of the machinery that made them. Peeling the skin back to a painful truth, it keeps circling the flat dread that it's all only sliding further downhill.","sonnet":"A song about the randomness of existence and the feeling that everything is deteriorating — the narrator marvels at the cosmic coincidences that created them and shaped their encounters, then confronts the painful truth that despite all that, things keep getting worse.","src":"haiku"},
"boston-manor~why-i-sleep":{"haiku":"Assimilation and manipulation spread plastic illusions across reality as the speaker absorbs harmful fragments. Wholeness belongs to the other while fragmentation defines the speaker's existence.","opus":"An entangled relationship where two people mould each other out of the parts they hate, each carrying pieces of the other. The refrain that the other person was always whole reframes the digging and fear as damage the speaker projected rather than truth.","sonnet":"A speaker addressing someone who took the parts of them they hate and shaped a face out of them — now urging them to dig deeper, wash away fear, and see that despite everything they are and always were whole.","src":"haiku"},
"boston~more-than-a-feeling":{"haiku":"A person uses music and memory to escape present unhappiness, repeatedly losing themselves in nostalgia about a past love. The song captures the bittersweet pull of memory over reality.","opus":"Tom Scholz's painstakingly layered studio construction, the cornerstone of Boston's 1976 debut, treats music as a doorway into sorrow. A well-worn tune dependably summons the image of a woman named Marianne departing, and the narrator gladly surrenders to that daydream over and over. It casts nostalgia as both sanctuary and dull ache, a memory called up precisely because it stings.","sonnet":"Music unlocks a specific grief: hearing a familiar song triggers a vision of Marianne walking away, a loss that years and countless passing faces have not dimmed. The feeling exceeds nostalgia — it is a full sensory return to something that cannot be recovered, vivid as summer sun against the cold of an ordinary day.","src":"haiku"},
"botch~one-twenty-two":{"haiku":"Seven years of accumulated sorrow yield only a cycle of tears and doubt, the speaker finally demanding rupture. Love dissolves to questions that cannot be answered.","opus":"Seven years of grief compressed into a mathcore spiral, pills and sleepless nights doing nothing as love curdles into doubt, the repetition tightening toward a threat to end it that could be aimed at the relationship or the self.","sonnet":"Seven years of tears into pillows and nights of doubt are compressed into a tight, furious loop that refuses to resolve, with the speaker threatening to end things right now and demanding the other person say it again. The minimalism makes the impasse feel physically suffocating.","src":"haiku"},
"bottom-shelf~let-go":{"haiku":"Rain becomes pretext for encounters with strangers, wet intimacy unmoored from meaning. The speaker refuses to be a discarded memory but cannot guarantee otherwise.","opus":"A rain-soaked encounter that turns hollow, the narrator drawn into someone's pain only to realize he was never wanted the way he hoped, refusing to be another disposable boy or a trophy on her wall.","sonnet":"A man drives through a flood to a strange woman's house and finds warmth that turns cold as the encounter becomes a confrontation about whether he is just another disposable boy. The final reveal — she never wanted him that way — strips the romantic framing entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"boundaries~heaven-s-broken-heart":{"haiku":"Eden fractures and bleeds, the cradle of creation poisoned by human sin. Rapture arrives as genocide and flame, divinity's failure total.","opus":"An apocalyptic reversal where paradise itself turns executioner, imagining God's grief over humanity curdling into a genocidal rapture, the metalcore fury aimed at the sins of a species reaping the destruction it sowed.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic vision of creation poisoned, oceans boiling, pillars of life reduced to ash, framed as the consequence of humanity's accumulated sin breaking heaven's heart. The imagery escalates from environmental collapse to genocidal monster hordes before arriving at a question: is this the paradise we built?","src":"haiku"},
"boy-pablo~wachito-rico":{"haiku":"Seduction through dance and living freely; the night is yours if you move with abandon.","opus":"Bedroom-pop sweetness switching between English and Spanish to coax a restless partner out of a grey stretch and onto the dance floor. It's young infatuation offering escape through movement and closeness.","sonnet":"A charming narrator coaxes his partner out of a grey funk by inviting her to dance with him all night — bilingual and buoyant, he presents himself as her personal guide to pleasure, promising to hold her close and never let her deny the rhythm.","src":"haiku"},
"bradio~dancin":{"haiku":"Carefree dancing and living in the present moment; escape from worry into pure movement and sensory joy.","opus":"BRADIO built their name on Japanese dancable soul-funk, all horns and grin, and that ethos is the argument here. A guy keeps botching his chances to impress someone, so he decides the only honest move is to throw himself into pure unguarded motion. Whether alone or paired, the groove writes a page in the story of his own life, joy chosen over composure.","sonnet":"A guy who keeps fumbling his shot with someone he wants to impress decides that throwing himself into dancing and pure unguarded energy is the only honest move — whether alone or together, the music gives him a page in the story of his own life.","src":"haiku"},
"brahman~a-15xnxzj":{"haiku":"Loneliness and isolation; disconnection from others is the central theme.","opus":"BRAHMAN's melodic hardcore sets the raw original impulse, a pounding chest and one person's private battle, against the cowardice of those who perform sincerity with borrowed words and then flee into a crowd of ordinary faces. The song demands that the real, true, first impulse be reclaimed before pretense swallows it. What reads as isolation is really a refusal to dissolve into the herd, authenticity defended as a solitary act.","sonnet":"The raw original impulse — chest pounding, one person's private battle — versus the cowardice of those who perform sincerity with borrowed words and then flee, burying themselves among ordinary faces; the song demands that the real, true, authentic first impulse be reclaimed before it's swallowed by pretense.","src":"haiku"},
"brahman~a-39jquo":{"haiku":"World destruction or apocalyptic imagery; systems collapse as the core metaphor.","opus":"The same band frames life as a curtain that rises only once and, in rising, is already falling, asking why one is born and why one ends. Truth, goodness, and beauty are offered as the keys to any answer, and the song celebrates the miracle of friends met as if in a dream before the inevitable parting. Beneath the apparent grandeur is a plain gratitude, the collapse of everything making the fleeting company matter more.","sonnet":"Life as a one-time curtain rise that is also a falling — questioning why you were born and why you end, using truth, goodness, and beauty as the keys to answering, and celebrating the miracle of friends met as if in a dream before parting.","src":"haiku"},
"brahman~a-3qq1v":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"Here BRAHMAN voices a cry for connection from someone who left home in tears, wanting to speak their heart to strangers and gripping whoever stands nearest. A friend's voice reaches them at the limit of everything; they fear striking someone in exhaustion and ask only to be exposed, seen, and found before their eyes close. The hardcore urgency turns a plea for contact into something that sounds like a last request.","sonnet":"A cry for connection from someone who left home weeping — wanting to speak their heart to strangers, gripping whoever is nearby, hearing a friend's voice at the limit of everything, fearing they might strike someone in exhaustion, asking to be exposed, seen, and found before their eyes close.","src":"haiku"},
"brahman~a-6e8vo5":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"The band's more elegiac side surfaces in this longing for someone lost: years gone, the person appearing in dreams, their voice heard in waves and wind. Once holding too much, the narrator now wants nothing but one more word and the wave's continuing message, hoping to be reborn into reunion. Grief here does not rage but waits, its endurance measured against tides, patient enough to stake everything on a next life.","sonnet":"A song of longing for someone lost — years have passed, they appear in dreams, the narrator hears their voice in waves and wind, once holding too much now wanting nothing except one more word and the wave's message to continue, hoping to be reborn into reunion.","src":"haiku"},
"brahman~a-fiilx6":{"haiku":"A Buddhist meditation on karmic debt and child-ghost imagery—towers of prayers collapse, endless punishment, scratches on the chest.","opus":"BRAHMAN's Buddhist bent shapes this meditation on violence and karma through Sai no Kawara, the purgatory riverbed where dead children stack stones only for demons to knock them down. The image becomes a metaphor for the futile accumulation of merit and retribution. Witnessing infinite punishment, the narrator accepts the cycle of destruction and return, and then chooses to stand here again anyway, defiance folded into surrender.","sonnet":"A Buddhist-inflected meditation on violence and karma — the riverbed of Sai no Kawara (the mythological purgatory where dead children stack stones only to have them knocked down by demons) becomes a metaphor for the futile accumulation of merit and retribution. The narrator witnesses infinite punishment, accepts the cycle of destruction and return, and chooses to stand here again anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"brahman~a-y9eg13":{"opus":"The band turns rage and adversity into fuel for endurance, treating hatred and desire as mere obstacles while decision and action are all that finally count. The storm of life keeps beating on, and when the run is over the tomb stands alone in the distance as the dreaming continues past the torrent. It is hardcore stripped to a discipline of persistence, where meaning is found only in the choice to keep moving through.","sonnet":"A meditation on rage and adversity as the fuel for endurance — hatred and desire are just obstacles, decision and action are all there is; the storm of life beats on, and when the run is over the tomb stands alone in the distance as the dreaming continues beyond the torrent.","src":"sonnet"},
"brahman~after-sensation":{"haiku":"足跡もない (footprints absent), the speaker drifts through afterimage and phantom sensation, pain eternally resurrecting. Distance traveled leaves no trace.","opus":"A departure before dawn toward an unseen sunrise, carrying the ache of a phantom that keeps reviving, the Japanese band framing rebirth as walking ever farther, leaving no footprints behind toward what comes after feeling.","sonnet":"On the cusp of dawn, the speaker prepares to depart into an unknown that feels both like a bad dream and a rebirth, haunted by a phantom pain that keeps returning even as they walk further away leaving no footprints. The \"after-sensation\" is the residue of existence itself.","src":"haiku"},
"brand-of-sacrifice~altered-eyes-remix":{"haiku":"The imprisoned speaker endures eternal psychological and physical torment, marked by dark forces and unable to recognize themselves.","opus":"A deathcore track drawn from the manga Berserk, voicing its cursed protagonist, marked and trapped in ceaseless agony, immobilized and surrounded by torment, who embraces rage as his only refuge and vows he would sooner battle for his existence than passively endure it. A second thread follows a traumatized child sealing away her terror.","sonnet":"About being permanently changed by trauma — a child locked away horror and terror, and the adult they became is imprisoned by it, devoid of sanity, choosing to fight rather than simply endure an eternal inner hell.","src":"haiku"},
"brandy-senki~kids":{"haiku":"A child asks unanswerable questions while wrapped in care; the world answers through symbols and ritual. Growing means learning to accept insufficient answers, to want despite uncertainty.","opus":"A Japanese art-pop piece from an infant's helpless vantage, strapped down and unable to move, looking to a caregiver for the whole meaning of the world. The sunlit room, mobile, and stainless cage make it eerily tender and confining at once, love as total dependence.","sonnet":"An infant in an angular room lit by daylight reaches outward for the world's answer from whoever is present — the garish mobile's colours still delightful, the stuffed-costume figures approaching with unsettling eyes — needing the blanket straightened and the bad dreams kept away, and pleading simply: wait.","src":"haiku"},
"brave-new-world~esuna":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness feels safer than cure, the speaker preferring sickness to the vulnerability cure demands. If nothing makes sense, at least the self stays intact and unexposed.","opus":"A defiant embrace of dysfunction, the narrator refusing to be fixed because the numbness feels good, rejecting the demand that life make sense while floating at the edge of awareness on drugs and nihilism.","sonnet":"A relationship in freefall — cocaine gone, funds gone, phones on the floor — is described by a speaker who paradoxically insists they don't want to be fixed or cured, preferring the comfort of dysfunction to prescribed sanity. The drugs on the nightstand and the weathered face anchor the chaos in physical reality.","src":"haiku"},
"brave-new-world~tarpit":{"haiku":"Deeper sinking into self-deprecation and isolation, war waged against the self without ceasefire. Climbing out requires love the speaker refuses to accept.","opus":"The draining math of self-loathing, asking how you learn to care for yourself when contempt is the reflex, scraping through a savage stretch of months and quietly questioning whether hauling yourself up from the depths is even worth the effort.","sonnet":"The speaker is sinking deeper into self-hatred — getting attached to silence, blurring love with violence — and poses the question they cannot answer: how do you love yourself when you've only ever known how to hate? The tarpit metaphor implies both stasis and slow suffocation.","src":"haiku"},
"break-me-down~into-the-water":{"haiku":"The speaker drowns rather than return; family and earth push them toward obliteration, and they sink willingly into the water.","opus":"Someone unable to grieve or voice his feelings even facing a final goodbye, clinging to a father's advice to stand firm while begging not to be abandoned to the water. The refusal to cry becomes its own drowning.","sonnet":"Someone unable to cry or express their feelings — 'I can't shed any of my tears even if I try' — is left stranded in a place they don't belong, pleading not to be left into the water while a father's voice urges them to take a stand regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"breakbot~baby-i-m-yours-feat-irfane":{"haiku":"Devotion translates wishes into command, the lover's desire becoming law the speaker obeys without question. Surrender offers paradise if only the beloved would believe.","opus":"A retro-soul plea for reconciliation, promising total devotion to a lover whose feelings turned grey overnight, insisting her every wish is his command if she'll only trust him back onto the steep road toward each other.","sonnet":"A speaker who was thrown into disarray by a lover's departure offers unconditional devotion — \"your wish is my command\" — and a vision of a future together where problems are left behind and trust is rebuilt on steep terrain. The dreamlike image of them waving unaware in a future moment becomes the emotional centre.","src":"haiku"},
"breakdown-of-sanity~traces":{"haiku":"The speaker searches desperately for answers while trapped in doubt and internal decay, fighting darkness and pressure while questioning whether redemption is possible.","opus":"A struggle against rejection and inner collapse, the narrator scorched by cruelty yet gripping the insistence that survival is still possible and dreaming of somewhere the horizon opens. Swiss metalcore band Breakdown of Sanity, known for punishing heaviness, live in exactly this tension between despair and stubborn defiance.","sonnet":"Rage at a world that offers poison dressed as meaning, cycling through internal collapse and desperate clarity — the speaker burns from within while rejecting the false faces around them. There is a recurring pull toward escape, toward a place where sky meets sea, but also a stubborn refusal to be buried; the maze has a way out if they don't follow the wrong trace.","src":"haiku"},
"breaking-benjamin~breakdown":{"haiku":"Emotional overwhelm collapses restraint; the narrator catalyses destruction through accumulated frustration and contempt for superficial connection.","opus":"Sensing a collapse coming and being unable to stop it, tethered to a toxic pull that's dragging the narrator toward the edge. The loud chorus is the sound of losing control, the hatred and hurt of a relationship that's grinding him down.","sonnet":"A speaker pushed to the edge by someone relentlessly intrusive and loud declares themselves on the verge of total collapse — the town, the noise, and the other person's presence are all converging into a breaking point they can no longer hold off.","src":"haiku"},
"breaking-benjamin~psycho":{"haiku":"Identity dissolves through trauma and falsification; the speaker becomes the corruption others imposed, hollow and damned.","opus":"Becoming the very monster someone else made you, sinking into the dark and accepting the fake identity forced on you. Benjamin Burnley's chronic illness and rage sharpen it: an embrace of the ruined, faithless self as the only truth left.","sonnet":"A persona formed entirely by damage and inauthenticity speaks in the third person of its own becoming — the fake made by another's cruelty, the ember fading, the hollow rupture — cycling between submission and a kind of broken warmth, claiming the name psycho as its own identity.","src":"haiku"},
"breaking-benjamin~tourniquet":{"haiku":"Love strangles and suffocates; restraint paradoxically enables survival while promising eternal bondage and controlled dying.","opus":"Love as something that rescues and strangles at once, a bond that stanches the bleeding while cutting off the air. The paradox is the whole point: he's made to dwell in this private hell, unable to tell healing from harm in the person he can't leave.","sonnet":"A toxic love is described through an inversion: the speaker first catalogues what they would do to hurt and drain the other, then pivots to a refusal — they won't fail, won't let the light descend — but love itself is still a tourniquet, suffocating even in its most devoted form.","src":"haiku"},
"breaking-in-a-sequence~delusional":{"haiku":"Someone lives beyond their means in arrogance and desperation while delusions keep winning. Jealousy, sedatives, and hopelessness define their condition as they run and hide.","opus":"A cutting address diagnoses someone whose arrogance and jealousy curdle into self-deception, living beyond their means while their unseen trophies and delusions quietly win. Breaking in a Sequence, a progressive metalcore act trading in intricate, groove-heavy takedowns, frame the target's feeble elegance and neediness as a slow collapse, the refrain that delusions are winning landing less as triumph than as the last flicker of a mind screening out its own meaning.","sonnet":"A person's arrogance and jealousy are quietly dismantled: their trophies unseen, their resources exhausted, their self-image built on borrowed elegance that keeps thinning. The delusional architecture of their ego keeps winning the internal battle even as the outer reality collapses.","src":"haiku"},
"breakk-away~i-ll-see-you-when-the-night-comes":{"haiku":"Love manifests as entanglement and self-annihilation, the speaker unable to leave despite poisoned roots embedded in their skin. Night becomes resurrection, morning impossible.","opus":"An addictive, possibly toxic bond that only comes alive after dark, the narrator gambling on whether the outstretched hand holds love or a blade yet unable to stop returning, wishing the night that binds them would never end.","sonnet":"An addictive, destructive relationship is kept alive only in the dark — meeting when night comes, crashing into morning — with the speaker admitting the other person's roots are planted in their skin and they cannot leave even as everything falls apart. The desire for the night never to end is both ecstatic and self-destructive.","src":"haiku"},
"breakk-away~outside":{"haiku":"Emotional poison and cyclical abandonment freeze the speaker as they remain tethered despite knowing toxicity.","opus":"A toxic love rendered as poison and sedation, the partner setting his world on fire while he keeps craving the burn. The medicating imagery and mutual destruction capture addiction to a person who loves only to spit you out, and the vengeful urge to do it back.","sonnet":"A toxic love has entered the speaker's bloodstream and won't leave — \"you remain in my veins\" — and the only management is to medicate until frozen, watching the other person break until both are broken.","src":"haiku"},
"breakk-away~secret":{"haiku":"Forbidden obsession thrives in secrecy; the speaker brands their lover and keeps them hidden, building a private world of transgression.","opus":"A hidden, self-destructive romance kept like a taboo tattoo written on the hand, where the thrill lies in the other's upset and the secrecy itself. The speaker knowingly hands over the power to ruin their life, folding intimacy and a near-suicidal surrender into the same whispered confession.","sonnet":"An illicit or hidden attachment — a name written like a tattoo on the hand, a chain kept on the neck — that the narrator is willing to let ruin their life rather than expose; 3 AM wishes to be someone else alternate with the pull of lips that are their secret and their undoing.","src":"haiku"},
"breakthrough-even~your-blood-is-changing":{"haiku":"The speaker watches transformation spiral into self-destruction; they cannot rescue someone surrendering to addiction and internal collapse.","opus":"Looking on as someone gets lost in addiction's labyrinth, their body chemistry shifting with each fix as their grip on reality loosens. The narrator, who has walked that road, swears there's nothing to find beyond it and begs them to trust they're still worth rescuing no matter what they hunger for.","sonnet":"A narrator watches someone they care about lose themselves to addiction — 'your blood is changing with every dose' — and offers witness rather than rescue, insisting they are worth saving while acknowledging that the body is breaking and the world at their fingertips is a minute from slipping away entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"breathe-me~sia":{"haiku":"Self-harm is a self-inflicted wound with no one else to blame; the speaker begs for comfort and human warmth to survive the fracturing of their own mind.","opus":"Sia's raw plea from the depths of self-harm and coming apart, owning that the fault is nobody's but her own and simply begging to be held. Its power is the nakedness of the need, reducing depression to a child's want to be warmed and kept from disappearing.","sonnet":"A plea from someone who has hurt themselves again — the worst part being there is no one else to blame — addressed to a friend who might hold them, wrap them up, warm the small and needy thing they have become. Vulnerability as pure request.","src":"haiku"},
"breton~got-well-soon":{"haiku":"The song examines boundaries between inclusion and exclusion; people narrate their position on a precarious line where rescue and dissolution are equally possible outcomes.","opus":"A pointed dismissal of a self-important poser whose grand speech about how far he's come bores everyone while never touching reality (feet never meeting the ground). The refrain about being out, in, or on, and swimming alone, needles the fear of being found out as a fraud.","sonnet":"A sardonic takedown of someone who performs their own story so well they've memorized it like a speech — all self-mythology, no actual groundedness — with the repeated motif of being fundamentally disconnected from reality beneath their feet.","src":"haiku"},
"brian-the-sun~a-ece1ix":{"haiku":"A philosophical meditation on identity and uncertainty—caught between sincerity and performance, unable to commit to either.","opus":"In bright, literate Japanese rock, the narrator borrows Schrodinger's cat as a figure for the self, living in a grey zone between truth and lie, meaning and its absence. Unseen by the person he loves and dying moment to moment, he keeps reaching toward a future he refuses to abandon. The quiet resolution is that a timid person deciding to love carefully, without crushing the flowers underfoot, has found his own form of courage.","sonnet":"Using Schrödinger's cat as a metaphor for the self, the narrator lives in a grey zone between truth and lie, meaning and meaninglessness — he cannot be seen by the person he loves, cannot stop dying moment to moment, yet keeps reaching toward a future he refuses to abandon. The emotional core is the paradox of a timid person who has decided that loving carefully — without hurting anyone, without crushing the flowers underfoot — is itself a form of courage.","src":"haiku"},
"brick-mortar~hangman-comes":{"haiku":"Infernal ambition craves dominion through fire and spectacle, the speaker celebrating descent into violence as destiny. They become their own executioner's favorite client.","opus":"A swaggering embrace of doom, the narrator strolling up to the devil having already survived hell, casting himself as the one who torched the world and welcomes his own execution, ambition and self-destruction fused into a grin.","sonnet":"A figure who has walked through hell courts further destruction, dancing on funeral pyres and pressing the devil's doorbell for advice on how to acquire total power — making others shake, watching the world beneath their feet. Welcoming the hangman is a death-wish reframed as exhilaration.","src":"haiku"},
"bridges-ablaze~heartbroken-angel":{"haiku":"Wings clipped and falling, the speaker lost without sight of the beloved's wings. Numbness spreads as body collapses inward, barely hanging on.","opus":"A fallen-angel image of grief and near-death, wings clipped and ashes scattered, the narrator numb and barely breathing after a loss, uncertain whether he can still be saved or has already slipped past the point of return.","sonnet":"An angel with clipped wings is falling apart, cold and barely hanging on, while the speaker mirrors that collapse — unable to breathe, wasting away, asking whether they can still be saved. Loss of the other person and loss of self are rendered as simultaneous freefall.","src":"haiku"},
"bridges-ablaze~hellbent":{"haiku":"Relentless forward motion despite shattered bones and approaching fate, defiance the only weapon. The speaker cannot be killed because they're already dead inside.","opus":"Pure combative defiance, a frame that hurts and shatters yet won't quit, jeering at the foe with the boast that there's no killing something that's already lifeless, all wrath and drive hurled at anyone who tries to bring the narrator down.","sonnet":"A rising storm of determination — broken bones, aching body, but still going, still showing up — refuses to be broken by fate or fear, declaring \"you can't kill me, I'm already dead\" as the ultimate resilience. Hellbent is both adjective and identity.","src":"haiku"},
"bridges-ablaze~numb":{"haiku":"Cold radiates inward through a lover's venomous embrace, the speaker surrendering to numbness as preferable to feeling. Bleeding slowly becomes acceptable.","opus":"A toxic bond rendered as cold poison, the narrator inviting the very embrace that tears him apart and bleeds him dry, mistaking suffocation for closeness until nothing is left but numbness and the chill of the one who caused it.","sonnet":"Total emotional shutdown — cold, breathless, suffocating — is attributed to a venomous presence that tears the speaker apart and bleeds them dry. Slipping to blackness and being cut open is the final surrender to the numbness the other person caused.","src":"haiku"},
"brighter-than-a-thousand-suns~books-on-fire":{"haiku":"Information overload and lost innocence destroy curiosity and depth; endless digital consumption leads to amnesia and spiritual death.","opus":"This metalcore act condemns the information age, where endless data drowns out real thought and curiosity withers. The song depicts knowledge burning away and outrage manufactured for clicks, phones recast as tombstones, a culture entertaining itself into emptiness until nothing meaningful remains.","sonnet":"Information overload kills curiosity rather than satisfying it — the flood of content rewires attention toward outrage and stimulation, hollowing out wonder. The phone's glow is reread as a headstone, and the books catching fire signal not censorship but the quieter destruction of replacing deep reading with the churn of feeds.","src":"haiku"},
"brighter-than-a-thousand-suns~busy-signal":{"haiku":"Digital noise and screens dehumanize and erase people, creating a hivemind obsessed with metrics and validation rather than authentic connection.","opus":"The track diagnoses digital-age dehumanization, people dissolving into a hivemind, perfecting curated shadows of themselves while drowning in noise. Screens flatten reality into ones and zeros used to quiet an unbearable silence, and the recurring dread is oblivion: to be forgotten unless one surrenders their own voice entirely. It questions whether any authentic humanity survives beneath the shallow, obsessive scroll.","sonnet":"Digital saturation has replaced genuine presence: the self is performed in ones and zeros, noise drowns signal, and the silence underneath is too loud to tolerate. Screens substitute for humanity rather than connecting it. The collective voice grows louder even as communication collapses — everyone shouting, nothing getting through.","src":"haiku"},
"brighter-than-a-thousand-suns~finding-me":{"haiku":"Self-discovery requires pain and heartbreak; identity emerges through adversity and facing isolation, breaking from safety to reclaim an authentic self beneath trauma.","opus":"The idea that self-discovery only comes through pain and hitting bottom, that you can't grow without your heart breaking; the sharp insight is that comfort and safety themselves suffocate the person you want to become, so you must break isolation and stay awake.","sonnet":"About the painful necessity of self-discovery through breakdown — you don't know who you are until you're hurt, you don't change without breaking, and the narrator is reckoning with the fact that safety and isolation have been suffocating who they could become.","src":"haiku"},
"brighter-than-a-thousand-suns~futureblind-aphantasia":{"haiku":"Imagination starves while phantoms hunger, minds too narrow to see beyond consumption. The purified cannot imagine tomorrow, vulnerable to demons they cannot picture.","opus":"An abstract war between imagination and its absence, using aphantasia as metaphor for a sanitized, incurious future where narrowed minds starve the monsters, urging warriors to fight the false gods feeding on illusion.","sonnet":"A society whose purified ideologues are \"future blind\" — unable to imagine beyond their narrow, poisoned vision — is confronted by warriors who call on the light of a Leviathan to starve the demons feeding on that cowardly appetite. The title concept of aphantasia (inability to visualise) is redeployed as political incapacity.","src":"haiku"},
"brighter-than-a-thousand-suns~replica":{"haiku":"The speaker is an identical, flawless copy designed for compliance; identical to others, they are accepted only through conformity and passivity.","opus":"Brighter Than a Thousand Suns anatomize conformity through a narrator who mimics the crowd to avoid being cast out while masking who he truly is, feeling safe yet lifeless within the herd. The band treat identity dissolved into sameness as both shelter and accusation, ending on the irony that even claiming to be unique is just another copied pose.","sonnet":"A person performs conformity with knowing irony — faithfully rebellious, perfectly identical to every other contrarian — and turns the lens on the violently fragile true believers who demand copies of themselves. The narrator is numb, anonymous, and immune; the crowd is the real copy.","src":"haiku"},
"brighter-than-a-thousand-suns~the-warrior":{"haiku":"Performative outrage and victimhood become tools for social currency while the speaker exposes the hollow exploitation of genuine suffering.","opus":"A biting satire of online outrage culture, skewering the performative victim who manufactures grievance to matter, mistaking conformity for courage and digging graves to feel safe, all clout-chasing at the cost of the soul. The line about fabricating the cancer to treat the symptom is the venomous thesis. This trades in the sardonic, socially-critical lyricism common to modern metalcore's most acerbic writers.","sonnet":"A bitter dissection of online outrage culture — the speaker is caught in a feedback loop of performative victimhood and manufactured causes, aware that the whole system rewards fabricating grievances more than solving them. The anger turns inward as much as outward, recognizing the same trap in the mirror while still caught inside it.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon-babymetal~kingslayer-feat-babymetal":{"haiku":"Complicity in institutional corruption; questioning where loyalties lie in systems of exploitation.","opus":"A cyberpunk call to arms against a corrupt system, casting the listener as a puppet who must cut their strings and wake up. With Babymetal's Japanese verses, the kingslayer becomes a beloved rebel to be saved and fought for, defiance rendered as devotion.","sonnet":"A call to corrupt, puppet-cut believers to wake up and rebel against a system that has encrypted and modified them: the Kingslayer is both saviour and sacrifice, destroying castles in the sky while collecting the lost from the dark. The English and Japanese voices converge on the same encrypted reality, one opening a door to another world.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon-feat-nova-twins~1x1-explicit":{"haiku":"Psychological sabotage and self-hatred torment the speaker as internal enemies prove more lethal than external ones; memory becomes the instrument of annihilation.","opus":"Oli Sykes turns his own mind into an enemy that can't meet its own eyes, staging depression as a war with a monster hidden under the stairs. Between wanting to be put out of his misery and being unable to go through with it, he circles the unbearable question of whether hanging on or letting go is the greater pain.","sonnet":"Severe depression and self-sabotage: the mind feels like an archenemy, the speaker is trapped between holding on and letting go, reliving memories that kill them one by one. The \"evil twin under the staircase\" and the acknowledgment of staring into the void and suffocating under the weight of self-hatred place the lyric squarely in the experience of being unable to escape your own head.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon-feat-yungblud~obey-explicit":{"haiku":"Authority manufactures consent through propaganda and terror; the speaker rebels against orchestrated compliance, demanding awakening over docile absorption.","opus":"A furious attack on manufactured docility, staging authority as a cheerful voice urging citizens to hurt themselves and never wake up to the corruption around them. The self-harm imagery frames obedience itself as the real violence, with the hidden chains presented as a comfort.","sonnet":"A satire of systemic control delivered in the register of a corporate spokesperson: the state gambles with souls, tells its subjects to have a lovely day, and begs them not to smell the corruption — while the speaker oscillates between obeying and demanding resistance. The refrain \"destroy yourself, it feels so good to fade away\" turns the propaganda inward, making complicity feel seductive.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~1x1-feat-nova-twins":{"fable":"The refrain begs to be put out of a misery the speaker can't self-administer, which is the song's whole engine: he is prepared for death but cannot administer it himself, so the request is outsourced to someone else. Meaning develops through structure rather than plot — the verses build a self that has split into warring halves, the mind as archenemy that won't meet its own eyes, the malign double waiting under the staircase. The isolation counted off in that fourfold repetition is not loneliness from others but from the self, the two occupants of one skull who can't be friends. Nova Twins arrive as that internal antagonist made audible, the voice telling him to hush and shut up, which reframes the whole track as an argument the speaker is losing to a part of himself. The masterstroke is the pivot where the head-voices insist he's just being paranoid: the gentler diagnosis is presented as the crueler lie, because it denies how real the hatred underneath actually is, and the closing arithmetic of memories killing him one by one is the honest count the reassurance was trying to paper over.","fableDeep":"The title's arithmetic runs the chorus: memories are 'killing me one by one', damage itemized and serial, no single fatal blow, just attrition on a schedule. The opening plea, 'put me outta my misery', borrows the language used for wounded animals — mercy requested rather than rescue, because rescue implies a future. The mind gets externalized into a cast of hostiles: an archenemy that 'can't look me in the eyes', an evil twin under the staircase, a hurricane, a bitch he refuses to befriend — dissociation as the only available distance from himself. The sharpest confession is the deadlock 'ready to die, but I can't commit', where commit carries suicide and the wider inability to commit to anything at all, twinned with the unresolvable question of what hurts more, 'holding on or letting go' — paralysis extended even to memory. Self-harm gets its economics too: setting himself on fire just to feel the burn, sensation purchased at any price. It is about being trapped in single combat with your own mind, where every round is a memory and nobody is winning.","haiku":"A deeply depressed person is tormented by internal conflict and self-sabotage, watching themselves deteriorate while caught between the desire to live and to end the pain.","opus":"A raw depiction of self-hatred and suicidal despair, with the narrator's own mind as an archenemy, begging to be put out of his misery as intrusive memories, paranoia, and self-sabotage kill him one by one.","sonnet":"The narrator describes a state of severe mental anguish — self-sabotage, self-hatred, suicidal ideation without the will to act — in which their own mind feels like an enemy and intrusive memories cause ongoing damage.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"Sykes has called it the album's most personal song, drawn from his addiction history; Nova Twins got the unfinished track by Instagram DM and returned their parts in two days.","themes":["suicide","madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the death-wish outsourced because the self can't execute its own order","built":"Nova Twins enter as the internal antagonist made audible, reframing the track as a lost argument with itself"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~alligator-blood":{"haiku":"Hidden predatory nature revealed in high-stakes games; deception and the primitive self breaking through the mask.","opus":"Oli Sykes frames a poisonous relationship as a rigged game of chance where both players hide their hands. His cold-blooded nature surfaces as self-destruction, daring the other to finish him off rather than keep bluffing.","sonnet":"A confrontation framed as a high-stakes card game — Russian roulette, poker faces, dead man's hands — in which the speaker's cold-blooded nature finally surfaces and the other party is warned that the game has already been lost and they should leave while they can.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages an unwinnable card game where survival itself is the wager, and its cruel joke is the second chorus line: an infinite regress of mutual mind-reading, each player certain the other sees through him, so the deception is total yet perfectly transparent to both. Alligator blood is a poker term for a cold, unshakable player, but the title turns predatory hardness into something that leaks out involuntarily and gives him away — the mask he wears is cracking to reveal the reptile beneath, which may be the truer face. Structurally the game curdles into self-destruction: the poker table becomes a loaded gun, the pleas to be gutted and remade slide into an order to redecorate the room with the contents of his skull, and the bridge finally hands the other player permission to leave. The escalating repetition is the point — cornered and out of moves with a losing hand he cannot fold, he can only recycle the same violent images louder until the standoff resolves the only way it can.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder"],"means":"the cold-player mask cracking to reveal the thing it was meant to conceal","built":"alligator blood — a poker term for cold nerve — turns into what leaks out involuntarily and gives him away"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~amen-feat-lil-uzi-vert-and-daryl-palumbo-of-glassjaw":{"haiku":"Religious judgment breeds possession and heresy accusations. A voice demands suffering's end—burning not judgment.","opus":"A savage inversion of religious persecution, the accused hurling the language of witch-hunts, exorcism, and damnation back at the self-righteous while begging just to be left alone to suffer in peace; being demonized becomes its own hell.","sonnet":"A dual-perspective clash between a mob of religious zealots calling for purification and the person they've targeted — the zealots want heretics burned while the victim begs to be left alone to suffer in peace, and the song deliberately collapses the distinction between fanaticism and the next generation's culture wars.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the voice of the persecutor and never takes it off: the whole thing is sung from inside the mob's mouth — the exorcism, the witch-burning, the damnation-as-worship — so that self-righteous holy violence and the ecstasy of a religious rite become the same act, the liturgy of cleansing and jubilation indistinguishable from a lynching. The revival-tent call-and-response is the trap: an amen is demanded of us for atrocities, making the listener complicit in the congregation's bloodlust. Underneath the mask, the collapse leaks through — the one moment the target speaks — a gasp for air, a plea not to war against himself — reveals the accused and the accuser sharing a body, so the persecution reads equally as self-loathing turned outward. The final capitulation to losing control, then the deadpan tossed-off closer, snaps the whole ritual into sick farce, as if the divine judgment were only ever entertainment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"the congregation's holy violence made indistinguishable from a lynching, the amen demanded of us","built":"the one moment the accused speaks — a gasp for air — reveals the persecutor and the target sharing a body"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~and-the-snakes-start-to-sing":{"haiku":"Betrayal and moral compromise in human connections; parasites and vultures exploit pain for profit, and the singer finds themselves trapped, worthless, and questioning if they ever mattered.","opus":"Oli Sykes lashes out at the hangers-on who see profit in his breakdown, casting them as scavengers gathering around a man wrecked by addiction and self-disgust. The refrain rejects both salvation and oblivion, since neither the afterlife's reward nor its punishment will claim him, leaving him stranded and begging for shelter he feels he hasn't earned.","sonnet":"A song about being surrounded by exploitative people when you are at your most vulnerable — predators emerge from every corner when someone falls apart, and the narrator reflects on a childhood marked by parental violence and the question of whether anyone ever cared or just used him as a rung.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the mask of a hustler cheerfully inviting his own predators to feast — pointing them toward the money in his bones and repeating that none of it is personal, only commerce. But the vermin imagery (worms, leeches, rats surfacing from woodwork and dirt as he falls apart) makes clear this is a man watching himself get picked clean and narrating the exploitation as if he agreed with it. Underneath the bravado is the collapse: the woulda-been/never-was litany and the plea to be locked away, taken out of his own head, admit that the sneering front is self-loathing turned outward. The outro drops the persona entirely, tracing the rot to its root in a childhood of a mother's knife and a father's hell, so the whole ecology of snakes finally reads as inherited — he learned to be prey, or to expect predators, at home.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","family & blood"],"means":"self-loathing narrated as cheerful consent to being picked clean","built":"the outro drops the persona to trace the ecology of predators back to the family home"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~anthem":{"haiku":"A song celebrating surrender and the end of everything, where the speaker is in constant pain and conflict with no escape. The anthem is a dedication to collapse, marked by bitterness and the exhausted repetition of cycles that never resolve.","opus":"A self-aware arena chant that dresses collapse and self-destruction as communal catharsis, turning the end of everything into something to sing along to. Its bite is the vindicated I-told-you-so, giving in reframed as release.","sonnet":"A self-destructive cycle rendered as a rallying cry — the speaker keeps bleeding, keeps getting hurt, can't stop the damage, but frames it all as an anthem anyway. There's a grim pride in the persistence of the wound, and a dark catharsis in singing the end of everything together.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the shape of a stadium singalong while pledging itself to collapse, turning a crowd chant into a toast for oblivion. Its wounded speaker keeps calling for rescue, admits the bleeding only deepens, and shrugs that this is simply his recurring condition, one that predators circle without pause. The taunting build lands on grim vindication, and the driving tag reframes surrender itself as the thing worth shouting about, mistaking the sound of quitting for triumph. Underneath, the bridge names the trap plainly, relapse tasting bitter yet feeling like sweetness, before the closing exchange between two voices exposes the whole rush as a hollowed-out, chemically borrowed peace.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the anthem shape borrowed to dress surrender as the thing worth shouting about","built":"two voices close the song exposing the whole rush as chemically borrowed peace"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~antivist":{"haiku":"Critiques apathy and performative activism, arguing that real change requires leaving screens and taking real risks rather than empty posturing.","opus":"An angry, cynical rant against apathy and hollow activism, telling people fed up with the world to get off their screens and act, while insisting no revolution comes without blood.","sonnet":"The narrator rails against hollow online activism and self-righteous posturing, arguing that the world is broken, those in power are predatory, and performative outrage only makes things worse — demanding real action or honest silence instead.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is the tell: an antivist is an activist minus the act, and Sykes builds the whole song as a satire of exactly the impotent rage it appears to perform. The raised-finger hook sells itself as defiance, yet the section before each chorus quietly rigs the old solidarity slogan so that unity and division both end in ruin — which reframes the gesture as venting that fixes nothing. The turn lands in the second verse, when the sneer swivels off the powerful and onto the listener: put down the phone, go outside, because no revolution ever arrives bloodless or clean. What reads as nihilism is really a dare, the song goading its own audience out of the cheap performance of caring and toward the harder cost of meaning it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"performative rage satirised from inside the performance it pretends to condemn","built":"the solidarity slogan is quietly rigged before each chorus so unity and division both arrive at ruin"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~avalanche":{"haiku":"A person feels mentally overwhelmed by an unseen force that immobilizes them—their mind is unwired for the world, and they desperately seek diagnosis and remedy.","opus":"Bring Me the Horizon liken mental illness to being buried and crushed, the narrator pleading with doctors to diagnose him, prescribe a remedy, and tell him plainly whether he'll pull through as suicidal thoughts close in. Recurring images of not fitting his own reality, of a brain built wrong for the world, frame his torment as a basic mismatch that may have no cure.","sonnet":"A song about a mental health crisis framed as a medical emergency — the speaker demands a diagnosis, a remedy, a cure for a mind that wasn't built for this world, the avalanche of depression crushing them under its weight while they ask someone, anyone, to respond.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song borrows the whole vocabulary of a clinical consultation and turns it against itself: the speaker begs to be cut open, diagnosed, prescribed an antidote, as if a mental collapse were a body a doctor could open and read, but every request for a straight answer comes back as silence. Underneath the medical mask, the plea is really about not being wired for the world — the square that won't fit the circle — so the demand for a cure is also a confession that the affliction is the self, not a curable condition. The avalanche is the honest image the diagnostic language keeps failing to reach: not a symptom to treat but a weight already closing over the throat, arriving faster than any remedy. The two build lines carry the real terror, a signal drifting off its frequency with nobody answering, which reframes the doctor-questions as broadcasts into a void. By the last line the metaphor swallows the medicine entirely — the cure is abandoned and only the avalanche is left, hitting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming"],"means":"the demand for a cure exposed as a confession that the self is the affliction","built":"the diagnostic frame collapses into the avalanche it was meant to name, leaving only the weight and no remedy"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~blacklist":{"haiku":"A friend who built success on the band's shoulders then betrayed them is cast out; the speaker renounces the friendship with violent finality.","opus":"A scorched-earth kiss-off to a former friend or bandmate who took the crown and let the thing they built together rot. The venom is genuine because the love once was too, which is exactly why it can't be forgiven.","sonnet":"A former bandmate or close collaborator who took advantage of shared work and then checked out is formally cut off — the bitterness is real, the eulogy is vicious, and the speaker admits his own failings while still consigning the other person to permanent exile.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the costume of a fallen tyrant, but underneath it is a friendship ending in fury. It opens on a grandiose scene, a martyr-king toppled from a throne built on cruelty, then peels that pageantry away to admit something plainer and more painful: this was someone they built a project with and genuinely loved. The middle turns the knife into the speaker himself, who confesses his own venom and cruelty rather than posing as the clean-handed victim. By the end the myth is dropped entirely for exhaustion, the flat refusal of further contact reframing betrayal not as tragedy but as dead weight finally cut loose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["friendship & loyalty","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"the grandiose fallen-tyrant frame dropped to admit a friend was genuinely loved","built":"the speaker turns the knife on himself mid-song, confessing his own cruelty rather than posing as the clean-handed victim"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~blasphemy":{"haiku":"Religious faith imprisons and corrupts as words lose meaning; the speaker denounces blessing as curse and sacrifice as worthless when prayers go unanswered and god's voice never comes.","opus":"A reckoning with religious faith after prayer meets only silence, aimed at someone walled in by belief. It refuses the comfort of a doctrine that demands blind obedience and delivers no light in return, the sacrifice made for nothing.","sonnet":"A direct challenge to someone trapped in religious faith — the lyrics argue that their belief is a prison, their prayers fall on deaf ears, and continuing to follow doctrine after seeing no evidence is self-deception.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the language of religious apostasy as a mask, then pulls it away: what sounds like an accusation of sin turns out to be an indictment of the faith itself, its virtues reframed as instructions to stop questioning, seeing, and thinking. Movement carries the whole read — the verses recite obedience as spiritual suicide, and the second verse cashes out the cost, tallying a lifetime of sacrifice against a silence that never answered back, too late now to undo the vow. The devastation is that the speaker isn't the fallen one being scolded; he's turned scold on the believer, and the refusal to be preached to is the sound of someone who has already paid and found nothing on the other side.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"the apostate turned scold, indicting the faith for the cost of the vow he can't undo","built":"the speaker is not the fallen one being scolded but the one who paid the full price and found silence on the other side"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~blessed-with-a-curse":{"haiku":"The speaker carries a curse from birth that poisons love and connection—everything they touch becomes stone. They lie about their heart's allegiance.","opus":"Oli Sykes confesses he's poison to the person who loves him, admitting his heart was never fully theirs and begging them to hold him even as he warns that everything he touches turns to stone, a self-aware plea from someone convinced he only brings ruin.","sonnet":"About being constitutionally incapable of love and connection — born cursed, everything he touches turns to stone, and he asks to be held and then abandoned because staying would only destroy the other person. It's self-aware toxic attachment framed as a genetic condition.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song sings from inside a doomed lover's confession: the speaker admits his devotion was always a lie, his heart divided because he loves the woman and his vices at once, so his tenderness arrives pre-poisoned. The Midas image is inverted into a curse — everything he touches turns to stone — which turns the chorus's plea into its cruelest move: he begs to be held and abandoned in the same breath, wanting the embrace precisely so he can be spared having to ruin it. The relentless retraction of everything he ever told her finally admits there was nothing beneath the words to keep; the retraction consumes the whole relationship because the love was counterfeit from the first line, blessed and cursed being the same act.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"the beloved embraced and abandoned in the same breath because the tenderness was always counterfeit","built":"the Midas image inverted — everything touched turned to stone — so curse and devotion are indistinguishable acts"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~can-you-feel-my-heart":{"haiku":"The speaker struggles with emotional distance, internal demons, and the paradox of simultaneously craving and fearing closeness, expressing fear of vulnerability and the impossibility of escaping internal darkness. The song is about emotional isolation within relationships.","opus":"A desperate plea for connection and salvation from someone drowning in numbness, fear of closeness, and inner demons, begging to be heard and fixed while apologizing to family.","sonnet":"A plea from someone paralyzed by fear of closeness and self-destructive impulses — unable to drown their demons, hating both isolation and intimacy — who begs to be heard, fixed, and saved before the cycle of self-harm and numbness consumes them entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is built entirely out of unanswerable questions, and the read lives in who is being asked. The chorus addresses a you that could be a lover, a savior, or God, begging for proof that a numbed self can still register a heartbeat — but the verse widens the plea into a deathbed roll call of brothers, lover, father, mother, so that the question stops being romantic and becomes a soul crying out to be reached before it's lost. The bridge drops the questioning voice entirely to confess the mechanism underneath: a man who craves closeness yet needs to feel nothing, whose every high deepens the fall, whose demons have learned to swim faster than he can hold them under — the classic addict's spiral Oli Sykes was writing from directly. Read together, the mask is the plea for connection and the thing underneath is the fear of it, which is why the questions never get answered: the singer both wants and dreads someone close enough to feel the heart he's not sure is still beating.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","faith & the occult"],"means":"the plea for contact from a self that both craves and dreads being reached","built":"the chorus questions shift from romantic address to a deathbed roll call of everyone the speaker needs"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~can-you-feel-my-heart-remix":{"haiku":"Isolation and depression compound until the speaker drowns in their own internal chaos; cry for help silences as silence becomes the only honest response.","opus":"A panic-stricken plea to be noticed and reassured of one's own existence, voiced from inside depression and self-injury. Sykes swings between fear of the void and fear of being alone, needing proof that anyone can register the pain he can barely admit to.","sonnet":"A desperate cry for empathy from someone in psychological crisis — ghosts, self-harm implied by the \"bloody sweater arm,\" demons that know how to swim — directed at anyone who might notice. The speaker is terrified of closeness yet hates being alone, and can neither drown the darkness nor surface from it.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~chasing-rainbows-bonus-track-lyrics":{"haiku":"Chasing escape through transcendence while getting lost in desperation, surrendering to inevitable failure.","opus":"The doomed pursuit of an unreachable high or escape, where chasing something bright only drags you further under. Addiction and disillusion rendered as clawing toward a paradise that turns out lonely and hollow.","sonnet":"A descent into disorientation and compulsive craving: the speaker is dragged underground, the dead call their name when they chase rainbows, and the only relief on offer is sinking teeth into \"another desperation\" in a lonely paradise. By the end, they have given up fighting the pull entirely — it is in the blood.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~chelsea-smile":{"haiku":"Guilt and shame about hidden secrets leading to self-harm, with the burden of concealed pain growing unbearable and consuming.","opus":"A confession of a devouring secret, guilt, and regret that eats at the narrator like cancer and keeps him from sleep. He masks his pain behind a smile, wrestles with shame and belief in God, and reflects that everyone carries hidden burdens that drag them down.","sonnet":"A narrator buries a secret guilt that consumes him from within, admitting he can only fake happiness while questioning whether God would bother with someone who cannot even believe, as the weight of hidden shame threatens to destroy him.","src":"haiku","fable":"The Chelsea smile of the title is the taunt the song weaponizes: the grin cut ear to ear becomes the only honest expression a mind rotted by hidden guilt can manage, so the chorus's boast of a withheld secret is really a confession dressed as power. Verse to verse the concealment metastasizes — first a disease eating inward, then shame leaking out for watching vultures — until the breakdown flips the private ache into apocalyptic sermon, demanding repentance the speaker cannot grant himself. The bridge strips the mask entirely: the secret is a spiritual bankruptcy, and the repeated question about being believed in stops asking anything and just circles a doubt too raw to resolve. What looks like menace is a man who has decided that if he must carry this thing unseen, he will at least pretend the burial was a choice.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"The track's samples are credited to The Secret Handshake.","themes":["shame & repression","faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"menace worn as the last available disguise for carrying an unbearable secret","built":"the Chelsea smile — a grin cut ear to ear — frames buried guilt as the only honest expression the face can still manage"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~crooked-young":{"haiku":"A generation is spiritually dead and beyond salvation, speaking in tongues and impervious to death, rejecting religious faith and external rescue.","opus":"A furious rejection of hollow religious salvation, spitting on twelve-step redemption and empty prayer in favor of self-reliance; the disaffected young declare themselves beyond saving and beyond the reach of any god who never listened.","sonnet":"The song rejects religious salvation — mocking twelve-step programs, faith, and the idea that prayer offers rescue — and instead claims identity in a community of the spiritually dead, already gone and unreachable, who believe only in themselves.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the language of the twelve-step convert — the counted days, the shepherd, the miracle of being saved — only to detonate it with a refusal that answers every prayer with a curse. Salvation and blasphemy are spoken in the same breath, so that the recovery testimony and its obscene rejection become one voice. What survives that collapse is a third position beyond both faith and its denial: a we that has stopped sleeping, eating, and dying, hollowed out within and therefore untouchable, redeemed not by any Lord but by having nothing left to lose. The tongues they speak in belong equally to Pentecostal ecstasy and to the merely deranged, and the track never settles which — the mask of the convert and the void underneath are held in the same clenched line, the only creed left a refusal to put faith in anyone, not even the self it briefly offers as replacement.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"recovery testimony and its obscene rejection spoken as one voice","built":"the Pentecostal tongue-register borrowed to make faith and derangement indistinguishable"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~crucify-me":{"haiku":"The speaker feels spiritually lost and drowned in inner turmoil, acknowledging hell and heaven coexist within and seeking mercy for those too far gone to save.","opus":"A drowning-man's cry from someone who feels he contains a whole turbulent sea, a wreck and burial ground of who he used to be, asking to be sacrificed and abandoned as heaven and hell coexist inside him. The pleas to simply keep breathing frame a losing fight against being dragged under by his own depths, hidden away so nobody has to know.","sonnet":"A song about being spiritually and emotionally lost beyond rescue — the speaker identifies as a wreck, a ghost of what they were, submerged and unable to survive, while admitting that both heaven and hell are real and they belong to neither.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages salvation as a lie you tell to spare someone. Its two poles are set from the start: a cross that promises redemption, and a sea that only ever drowns — the singer names himself that ocean, a body so vast it has become its own abyss with no lungs left to save it. The refrain pretends to be a confession of faith, but its real move is concealment; the news that heaven exists is kept secret precisely because the speaker no longer believes he can reach it, so the mercy he asks is not for the living but for the dead he already counts himself among. Everything the drowning man says outward is protection — telling the would-be rescuer to save their own breath, to abandon any hope of locating him — the mask of self-sacrifice worn over the fact that he wants to go under. The closing collapse into the ocean line, repeated past the point of statement into sheer undertow, is the drowning enacted: the self is no longer speaking to anyone, just dissolving into the water it claimed to be.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"The second Lights feature on the record — she also answers on Don't Go; the choral parts are the Fredman studio's own singers.","themes":["suicide","faith & the occult","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"mercy withheld as the act of self-sacrifice, protection as the urge to go under","built":"the ocean-line repeated past statement into undertow, the self dissolving rather than addressing anyone"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~darkside":{"haiku":"Internal darkness overwhelming fragile control, pleading with others to prevent self-destruction while distrusting myself.","opus":"An unflinching account of a mental-health relapse at its lowest, the narrator begging a partner to keep watch because he can't trust himself. The dark side winning is depression pulling him toward the ledge.","sonnet":"About a mental health crisis breaking through — the narrator's dark side has won a battle, leaving them suicidal and begging someone to stay close because they don't trust themselves. The haunted house imagery and sleeplessness convey the terror of being inside a mind actively working against you.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~dear-diary":{"fable":"The song wears a zombie-outbreak diary as its mask, but the real horror is a mind narrating its own dissolution: the barking dog, the dead-channel TV, and the itch under the skin are all symptoms read from the wrong side, by a narrator too far gone to notice he's the thing that's turning. Its cleverest move is tonal — a global apocalypse rendered not as terror but as boredom and mild inconvenience, the dread flattened into the numb, doomscrolling shrug of pandemic-era lockdown that Post Human was built around. The turn lands in the bridge, where the diarist offhandedly admits eating the dog's face and dismisses the extinction of humanity with a shrug, so the confession of monstrousness arrives with less weight than the earlier complaint about the TV. That inversion is the point: the diary form promises interiority and self-reckoning, but here it only records the last flickers of a self too eaten-away to grieve its own erasure — the tragedy is that nobody's left inside to feel it.","fableDeep":"The form is the joke — the end of the human race told in diary entries whose register never rises above mild annoyance ('somebody bit me, what a fucking day'). Infection is described as tedium: itchy, hungry, braindead; God gets dismissed mid-breakfast; the dog's fate arrives as a throwaway aside. Written for a 2020 album literally titled Post Human: Survival Horror, the zombie plot doubles as a lockdown journal — stuck inside, every channel the same, 'going braindead, isolated,' watching the news declare the end of the human race — pandemic cabin fever wearing a genre costume. The horror is the flatness: not even the apocalypse can puncture the boredom, and the closing 'it's not the end of the world… oh, wait' snaps the diary shut on its own gag.","haiku":"Apocalyptic horror scenario with infection and societal collapse.","opus":"A darkly comic diary of someone descending into a zombie-outbreak apocalypse, going mad and cannibalistic as the human race ends.","sonnet":"A zombie apocalypse experienced through diary entries as the narrator gradually succumbs to infection and eats their dog.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["dissociation & numbness","madness & the mind"],"means":"self-narrating dissolution mistaken for a weather report","built":"the diary form promises interiority but has no one left inside to write it"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~death-breath":{"haiku":"Vampiric excess and immortal transgression; the singer and their circle live fast through nightly parties, refusing to die and feeding on vitality despite inner decay and bad taste.","opus":"A vampire fantasy standing in for reckless, self-destructive hedonism, living fast and dying beautiful while prowling the night. Exhausted by ordinary life, the narrator craves endless darkness and dares death to try him, treating oblivion as a party that will never truly end.","sonnet":"A song that uses vampire mythology to describe tour life as an endless nocturnal cycle — the band owns the night, never goes home, and would gladly kill the sunrise to keep going, casting the audience and the city as blood to be consumed.","src":"haiku","fable":"The vampire pose is the mask: nocturnal, deathless, gorging on nightlife across interchangeable cities, and the song wears it with total swagger — until you notice every boast is a way of refusing daylight and home. Underneath the immortality brag runs its exact inverse, a plea to be strangled and a self-loathing that recasts the bloodthirst as sickness, so that the invitation to bite becomes indistinguishable from a wish to die pretty. The circular structure seals this: it opens and closes on the same blood-hunting walk, having gone nowhere, the party a treadmill that returns the singer to the home he says he isn't sick for. The bridge's fixated repetition — a bad taste to spit out — turns the appetite inward, revulsion where the craving was. What sounds like invincibility is really an addiction narrating itself as glamour.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","suicide"],"means":"immortality narrated as a treadmill that returns to the same starting point","built":"the circular structure opens and closes on the same blood-hunting walk, having gone nowhere, the party revealed as the cage"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~deathbeds":{"haiku":"Obsessive love consumes the speaker; on their deathbed only the beloved remains, their heart eternal even as life leaves their body.","opus":"An obsessive, almost devouring attraction where the narrator can't stop watching someone he compares to a wreck he shouldn't stare at, torn between wanting to consume them and knowing the hunger is destructive. Bring Me the Horizon push it past infatuation into a vow of permanence, promising that even in his final moments this fixation, and his loyalty, will outlast his own body.","sonnet":"An obsessive, consuming love — the attraction is almost violent in its pull, but the speaker accepts that no matter how far apart they drift, they'll be drawn back, and this person will be the last thing on their mind when they die.","src":"haiku","fable":"The love song's title is its trap: what sounds like devotion is stalked from the first line, where the beloved is a car crash the speaker cannot stop watching and describes wanting to tear apart, framing desire as predatory hunger and sin rather than tenderness. Its two image-families quietly agree on the outcome — the hunter's imagery (hawk, a deer pinned in the beams) and the drowning imagery (storm, overboard, waves pulling under) both make surrender to this love indistinguishable from being killed by it, so the deathbed of the title is literal, the only bed the song imagines. What holds it together is not a chorus of choice but a refrain of undertow, the tide that keeps returning him to her repeated past the point of speech, so that devotion reads as a current he cannot swim against. The paired male and female voices turn it into a mutual pact rather than one person's confession. Read straight it is a vow to love until death; read against the grain the repetition is the tell, an addiction dressed as romance that can only end one way.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"devotion and being destroyed by love made indistinguishable from drowning","built":"two image-families — the hunter's and the drowning's — converge on the same fatal outcome without touching"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~dehumanized":{"haiku":"Humans become meat fed to machinery, stripped of empathy and selfhood through systematic crushing. Survival requires becoming monster, the machine's appetite absolute.","opus":"A savage indictment of humans as livestock for the machine, born to bleed and lulled by comfortable lies, warning that empathy is treated as heresy while dissociation and cruelty strip away what made us human.","sonnet":"Industrial society is figured as an abattoir where humans are bred to bleed, fattened on lies, and stripped of empathy — the process of dehumanisation framed as both external imposition and internal collapse. \"Empathy is heresy and hope is a disease\" is the slogan of a system turning people into meat for the machine.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~diamonds-aren-t-forever":{"haiku":"Defiant refusal to sleep toward death: the speaker rejects rest and surrender, confronting mortality and life's falsehood while proclaiming that diamonds fade and all humans die alone.","opus":"From the band's brutal early deathcore era, a nihilistic pledge against ever resting, treating sleep as kin to death and weakness. Having loved and lost, the narrator vows to burn out spectacularly rather than fade, insisting doom awaits everyone and the only choice is to go down blazing.","sonnet":"Against the certainty of death and the lie of comfortable living, a voice insists on wakefulness at any cost — refusing sleep, refusing the slow surrender of consciousness. Having loved and lost, it frames staying awake as the only honest response to a finite life heading inevitably toward its end.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~die4u":{"haiku":"Toxic devotion and the willingness to destroy yourself for someone who doesn't deserve it; love as self-annihilation.","opus":"The sickness of a codependent love that mistakes self-destruction for devotion, sworn willingness to die for someone the narrator also hates, until the sacrifice curdles into recognition that this isn't love but a slow bloodbath.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship is held with absolute, self-destructive devotion — \"I've died inside a thousand times / but still I'd kill myself for you\" — even as the speaker acknowledges this love makes them feel like shit and is really a car crash, a bloodbath, a bullet in the head. The hook's declaration of total sacrifice is darkly ironic.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~don-t-go":{"haiku":"Childhood trauma haunts someone into paralysis; they beg a lover to stay despite knowing intimacy will consume them both.","opus":"A harrowing plea, voiced through a scarred, orphaned childhood and a memory of murdering a boy in the forest, from someone haunted by demons who can't live with himself; the deeper read is desperate, codependent need—begging God for forgiveness and a loved one to stay because he cannot face his guilt and self-loathing alone through the night.","sonnet":"A song about a man haunted by both a past act of violence — he witnessed or participated in a brutal killing — and demons that strangle him at night; he begs someone to stay because he cannot survive the night alone with what he has done and what he is.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song splits its plea between two voices, and the gap between them is the whole point. Sykes sings from inside a guilt so total it hardens into confession — a childhood of violence recalled as something he did rather than survived, then a nightly siege by the things he can't forgive himself for, which is why the chorus begs someone not to leave: he can't be alone with himself. Lights answers not as rescuer but as the haunting itself, refusing intimacy in a chain of ifs while making clear he is going nowhere — the very presence he pleads to keep is the one that already knows too much and won't release him. So the bridge's escalating demand to be loved and needed curdles: what sounds like devotion is a drowning man clutching whatever is closest, and the endlessly repeated title stops being a request and becomes the loop of a mind that can neither hold on nor let go.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["shame & repression","alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"the plea to stay addressed to the presence that already knows too much and won't release him","built":"the title stops being a request and becomes the loop of a mind that can neither hold on nor let go"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~doomed":{"haiku":"Predatory consumption drains the willing; hearts offered freely become vampire sustenance while the world transforms into funeral litanies.","opus":"An anthem of embracing despair instead of fighting it, offering his heart to vampires and welcoming the rain just to feel something. The twist is a tenderness toward the wreckage, a love of the mess and a strange comfort in simply accepting doom.","sonnet":"Declaring the world a funeral of ghosts and themselves complicit in tying the rope, the speaker nonetheless chooses to leave a light on and come home — in love with the mess, inviting the rain rather than fearing it — because even if it's too late to save anything, feeling it is better than feeling nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~doomed-new-song":{"haiku":"Self-destructive nihilism and depression as a romantic ideal; embracing darkness rather than fighting it, finding clarity through surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~drone-bomb-me-recorded-at-spotify-studios-nyc":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation and desire for obliteration; volunteering to be destroyed, to be chosen for annihilation.","opus":"A startling plea to be obliterated by a drone strike, voiced from the perspective of someone wanting death as a form of love, begging to be blown from the mountains into the sea and chosen from above, partly blaming themselves. This is Bring Me The Horizon covering ANOHNI's devastating anti-war lament, so the harrowing conceit is hers; the British band render it through their own darker, electronic-tinged reinvention.","sonnet":"A death wish framed as a plea for chosen obliteration — the speaker wants to be selected, targeted, blown apart from the mountains into the sea. Partly a desire for significance, partly an acknowledgment of guilt, it sits in the strange zone where longing to be seen and longing to disappear become the same impulse.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~drown":{"haiku":"Suffocation under weight of loneliness and existential pain, begging for rescue from oneself.","opus":"A raw plea from inside depression, the singer admitting he can't survive it alone and begging to be pulled from the water. The band's pivot to naked vulnerability, no bravado left, just the ask for rescue.","sonnet":"About severe depression and the desperate need for someone to save you from yourself — the narrator is drowning in silence and loneliness, cannot hold himself up, and makes a direct plea for rescue; the emotional core is the admission that he simply cannot do this alone.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~drown-new":{"haiku":"Waves of depression and loneliness bury the narrator; he cannot survive alone and needs rescue from drowning. Silence and isolation intensify his despair.","opus":"A stark cry for help from inside depression, where a widening emptiness makes existence feel impossible to carry and hardship keeps returning in relentless surges. Bring Me the Horizon frame it as an admission of powerlessness: the narrator cannot pull himself out and desperately needs someone else to rescue him before he goes under.","sonnet":"A cry for help from someone drowning in depression — the silence and loneliness have become unbearable weight, and the speaker acknowledges they cannot save themselves, desperately asking for someone to dive in and pull them back to the surface.","src":"haiku","fable":"The chorus keeps asking who will save the singer, and the song never lets an answer arrive — the bridge circles the same admission four times over, that this can't be survived alone, until self-rescue and the plea for someone else collapse into one impossible request. What darkens underneath is the water it hangs on: the same tide that could pull a drowning body out is asked to dredge the lakebed. The line between being brought home and being buried at the bottom is deliberately erased, so the cry for help and the wish to go under sound like the same voice.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["suicide","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the cry for help and the wish to go under voiced as one undifferentiated plea","built":"the bridge circles the same unanswerable admission four times, collapsing self-rescue and outsourced rescue into one impossible request"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~empire-let-them-sing":{"haiku":"Collapse of power structures and surrender to destructive cycles of isolation.","opus":"A song staged as the fall of an empire and the abdication of its ruler, worn down by self-repeating patterns and closing enemies. Rather than resist, the narrator chooses defiant surrender, inviting the mob battering the walls to celebrate as everything he built collapses around him.","sonnet":"A figure at the collapse of their own empire acknowledges the throne is lost, the cycles of damage are inescapable, and that whoever is clawing at the gates can sing all they like — a resigned but defiant stand at the moment of inevitable fall.","src":"haiku","fable":"A ruler narrates his own downfall in real time, hands bloodied from patching cracks that only widened, watching predators mass at the threshold of a reign he built and can no longer defend. What makes it more than a defeat anthem is the pivot in its refrain: rather than beg the besieging horde for mercy, he throws the doors open and dares them to make their noise, converting surrender into a last act of will — if the empire has to fall, let its destroyers announce it themselves. The arena-scale delivery of Bring Me the Horizon's mid-2010s stadium turn makes that gesture communal, turning private ruin into a chant a crowd can hurl back, so the collapse reads less as tragedy than as a defiant handing-over of the crown.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society"],"means":"surrender recast as a last act of will by throwing the doors open","built":"the refrain converts private ruin into a crowd-hurled chant"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~follow-you":{"haiku":"A couple in distress discovers that breaking up would be worse than staying together; even when a relationship is painful, the alternative absence is unbearable.","opus":"The entry is spoken commentary rather than lyric, Oli Sykes narrating how a track once shelved from the Sempiternal era resurfaced. He frames a relationship at its ugliest point, when leaving feels tempting yet the alternative reads worse, and locates a strange beauty in that realization. It marks the band's mid-2010s pivot away from pure heaviness toward wider, more melodic textures.","sonnet":"A relationship teeters at its lowest point, and in that ugliness arrives a clarifying truth: the alternative to staying together is unimaginably worse. The narrator discovers, through near-collapse, that his attachment is more essential than any romantic ideal — the realization arrives ugly but lands like relief.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~football-season-is-over":{"haiku":"Post-celebration inebriation, empty promises, and hangover define a messy night after football season; the speaker celebrates abandoning restraint through dancing, drinking, and collective excess without memory.","opus":"A come-down anthem named after Hunter S. Thompson's suicide note, its narrator too wrecked to recall the night or even flag a cab. Under the drink-yourself-into-oblivion chant lies real hollowness, the wreckage of the evening standing in for broken vows and a celebration that has soured into nihilism.","sonnet":"A wasted, deliberately nihilistic party anthem — blackout drinking, dancing till you can't feel your legs, zero memory of the night before, total indifference to consequences.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages a night out as a controlled countdown to oblivion, the chant to drink until dead and dance past feeling repeating with no release valve, so the repetition itself becomes the point: pleasure locked into a loop that only escalates. What frames it is the sober bookend of morning, where the hangover and a stalled attempt to flag down a ride leave the speaker unable to see anyone or anything, so the party's promise of being surrounded by everyone he needs curdles into total isolation. The final admission of being done caring and too drunk to see reads less as celebration than as the after-image of the title's darker joke — the surrender dressed up as a good time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"JJ Peters of Deez Nuts guests; the title repeats the opening words of Hunter S. Thompson's suicide note.","themes":["party & hedonism","addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"pleasure locked in an escalating loop that promises everyone and delivers isolation","built":"the sober morning bookend — hungover, unable to see anyone — is what the party's promise of togetherness becomes"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~fresh-bruises":{"haiku":"Boundary-setting and refusal to be manipulated; a repeated mantra of resistance against someone's attempts to control.","opus":"A short, abrasive interlude on amo, built from one jagged command looped and warped until it decays into static. There's no verse to decode, only a threat and a plea welded together and repeated to menace: don't cross me, don't withhold what I want. The meaning lives in the texture, the way tenderness and aggression fuse into a single glitching pulse.","sonnet":"A clipped, repetitive confrontation: a speaker warns someone not to mess with them while simultaneously demanding they stop concealing their feelings, the two commands looping against each other in a push-pull of defiance and withheld intimacy.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~fuck":{"haiku":"A brief, intense affair plays out knowing its imminent end, with bodies as the only language two people share before final separation. The song frames fleeting intimacy as both transcendent and doomed.","opus":"A reckless plea to seize one brief, sex-fueled night together before dawn or the heart quits, an evening built to be cherished then discarded. Underneath the urgency lies the tenderness and dread of young love, the fear of parting before the two of them have really even begun.","sonnet":"Two people spend a single, intense night together knowing it will not last, framing sex and closeness as the only goodbye they know how to make. The song repeatedly returns to the impossibility of parting from someone you have barely begun to know.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears its bluntness as armor: an all-night collision staged as celebration, where two people crash together precisely because they sense there's no future in it. Underneath the swagger runs a steady death-grammar — a bed reimagined as a wake, blankets as a grave, a partner's voice pushing the pulse toward flatline — so every rush of closeness gets narrated as a kind of dying. The song's real ache surfaces in its central question, asked over and over: how do you part from someone you barely got to meet? Franceschi's outro then softens the pose into open pleading, begging the other to draw near and confirm the feeling is real, which exposes the whole reckless night as fear of loss dressed up as indifference.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["love & desire","party & hedonism","death & grief"],"means":"reckless closeness narrated entirely through the grammar of dying","built":"Franceschi's outro softens the swagger into open pleading, exposing the night as fear of loss dressed as indifference"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~go-to-hell-for-heaven-s-sake":{"haiku":"A brutal severance song about burning bridges and rejecting a false or destructive person. The speaker bleeds out the listener's lies and condemns them to hell for their weakness and parasitism.","opus":"A furious kiss-off to a hollow, deceitful person, with the narrator burning down every bridge between them and telling this spineless follower to go to hell.","sonnet":"A scorched-earth rejection of someone the narrator despises — burning every bridge, denouncing them as a hollow, derivative person who breaks hearts — culminating in a final, unambiguous dismissal.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's chiasmus is the reading key: this is a curse issued in the register of salvation, damnation performed as if it were mercy. The verses build a portrait of a hollow performer nobody cares to hear, then the breakdown supplies the diagnosis behind the recurring question about diamonds leaving the bones — this person was never solid, just a sheep aggregating whoever they last met, all flesh and no scaffolding beneath. So the chorus's demolition works two ways at once: burning bridges and bleeding out the other's words reads as pure scorched-earth aggression, but paired with that lost-diamonds ache it plays as the speaker cauterizing a wound, destroying the connection because there turned out to be no one real on the other end to sustain it. The escalating repetition of the title into the outro is the point of the refusal to develop further — the speaker can only keep reissuing the same sentence, because contempt this fluent is the last available disguise for having cared.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"contempt issued as salvation, the curse performed as mercy","built":"the title's chiasmus — damnation in the register of grace — is the reading key the whole song delivers"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~happy-song":{"haiku":"Making light of despair when there are no solutions; absurdist humor becomes the only honest response to an overwhelming world of denial and problems.","opus":"Oli Sykes explains the whole thesis himself: a spoken preface on how the bleak reassurance to just get on with it inspired a song about laughing at a world of problems we'd rather ignore. Gallows humor as the only honest response.","sonnet":"A spoken-word commentary track explaining that the phrase \"that's the spirit\" is a bleakly funny deflection — society ignores its real problems and chooses superficial coping, so the only rational response is to laugh at being collectively fucked.","src":"haiku","fable":"The chant that frames the song is a pep-rally cheer emptied of joy, and the whole track runs on that same hollow instruction: turn the volume up on cheerfulness until the despair underneath stops being audible. The mask is the happy song itself, sung a little louder each time precisely because what it covers — the voice urging death, the depression, the sense of being possessed and broken — is getting worse, not better, so the coping mechanism curdles into pure denial. Meaning develops through that widening gap: the private confession of a mind stuck spinning like a carousel gives way to a collective we that prefers staying asleep to waking into nightmares, and finally scales outward to a whole planet seething and restless that everyone agrees to pretend is fine. By the end the cheer returns as taunt, its promise of instant uplift exposed as the lie it always was.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness","madness & the mind"],"means":"cheerfulness turned louder each repetition to drown the worsening despair beneath it","built":"the happy-song instruction scales from private denial to a planet-wide agreement to pretend, the mechanism exposed as collective self-deception"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~heavy-metal-feat-rahzel":{"haiku":"Anxiety about relevance and authenticity in music when critics and algorithms define worth, spiraling self-doubt over artistic value.","opus":"From their genre-hopping later era, Bring Me the Horizon turn self-aware, staging cannibalistic industry pressure and fickle fans who say the music no longer counts as heavy metal. Oli Sykes keeps picking petals over whether he is still loved, needled by a kid on Instagram and an A&R rep who does not get it. Rahzel's beatboxing underscores the joke as the band interrogates authenticity and their own evolution.","sonnet":"A band anxious about audience approval keeps pulling petals in a ritual of self-doubt — wondering whether the music still earns the genre label critics and fans assign. Industry pressure, social media verdicts, and the constant demand to remain quintessential press from all sides, and the uncertainty of that judgment never quite resolves.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~home-sweet-hole":{"haiku":"A person trapped in self-deception and harmful patterns confesses to crossing moral lines repeatedly until the boundaries blur, warning about the dangers of spoken and unspoken lies.","opus":"A confrontation with self-destruction and denial, the singer admitting the familiar figure he keeps facing is a version of himself. He describes learning from mistakes only to repeat them, sensing dread in his gut, and watching his own fabrications collapse until the ugly reality finally surfaces.","sonnet":"Someone repeatedly crosses lines and digs deeper into self-destructive behaviour, recognising that their own lies are a noose around their neck and that the comfortable, familiar hole they keep falling into is of their own making.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages addiction or self-destruction as a haunted house you can never leave, and its cruelty is that the devil turns out to wear the speaker's own face — the enemy and the addict are the same man, which is why he keeps forgetting the lessons he swears he learns. The structure enacts collapse: verses of private nausea give way to a build where the roof caves in and every alibi is exposed as fiction, and the chorus itself decays into a confession so worn by repetition that the transgressed line has lost all meaning. The title's grim joke lands in the tag, home rebranded as a hole and comfort as a warning that wishes granted are traps sprung. Underneath, the bridge drops the mask entirely, recasting the lies as a noose and the way out as kicking the chair, so the whole thing reads less as bravado than as a suicide note dressed up in a slammed-door hook.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","suicide","identity & becoming"],"means":"the addict's enemy revealed to wear his own face","built":"the bridge reframes the hook as a suicide note before the tag rebrands home as the hole"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~hospital-for-souls":{"haiku":"Mental illness and institutional isolation; the powerless sit in silent circles struggling with depression, self-harm, and emptiness, seeking connection in a place designed to hold broken souls.","opus":"The album's devastating closer, Sykes reflecting on rehab, addiction and depression, the awful difficulty of real change, powerlessness in a ward of the broken, yet finding glimpses of heaven in friends, music and love enough to start over.","sonnet":"A song about recovering from depression and self-harm — the narrator reflects on what it took to reach a point of genuine gratitude for the people and music in his life, set against clinical intake questions and the recognition that sorrow does not leave when the numbness does.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages recovery not as arrival but as the exhausting discovery that leaving hell means starting over from a place you'd grown comfortable in — the opening confession that everything worth having was already there before the numbness. From that admission it collapses inward: the verse's repeated surrender to powerlessness moves through the shared isolation of people sitting silent together, none of them able to name the emptiness they share, while the incendiary chorus offers self-immolation as the only clarity someone this numb can still feel. What earns the length is the bridge, where the private voice cuts out and the clinical intake questionnaire takes over, the one-to-ten scale, the questions about blades and skipped meals. The entire interior crisis gets answered by a checklist, and the mask of therapeutic procedure sits over a person who is drowning. The outro resolves nothing and instead just repeats a plea to be held and watched, adding the word burn on the second half so the request for care and the wish to be consumed become the same sentence, sung inside the title's hospital where souls are kept but not cured.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","suicide"],"means":"the clinical checklist arrives as the answer to a private crisis it cannot hear","built":"the outro fuses the request to be held with the wish to burn into a single repeated sentence"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~i-apologise-if-you-feel-something":{"haiku":"An apology given to someone lost in their own abyss for the speaker's ability to stir emotion, framed as a necessary expression of love amid loss.","opus":"From the band's synth-forward amo period, this short reflection finds someone peering into their own emptiness, waiting on something they're unsure remains. The narrator nudges them toward curiosity rather than dread, apologizing only for provoking emotion at all and insisting that affection is the one thing truly worth holding.","sonnet":"A voice reaches toward someone staring into their own emptiness and offers love as the only available response, even at the risk of being unwelcome. Feeling anything, even discomfort, is framed as proof of life rather than something to apologize for.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~i-don-t-know-what-to-say":{"haiku":"Grief over a loved one's suicide; powerlessness and admiration for their strength to resist despite unbearable pain.","opus":"A grief-stricken farewell to someone who has died, wrapped in fantastical vows of fighting through anything to reach them, then giving way to regret for things left unspoken. It honors a person who transformed their suffering into something beautiful. Bring Me The Horizon, who grew from deathcore into anthemic rock, deliver it with aching tenderness.","sonnet":"A eulogy for someone who chose their own ending — the speaker circling the impossibility of saying goodbye to a person who held the universe in their heart and refused to be defeated by anything external. The grief is compounded by the things unsaid before the dark, and the most honest thing left is the admission that there are no right words.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~in-the-dark":{"haiku":"Internal collapse hidden from others who don't know the betrayal and destruction they caused, kept in darkness without faith.","opus":"Bring Me the Horizon confront a cold, manipulative liar who betrayed the narrator, warning them not to invoke a God who never sanctioned their cruelty. Plummeting yet searching for hope, he refuses to be kept ignorant or controlled, answering the betrayal with defiance and a plea for a little trust in something.","sonnet":"Betrayal by someone intimate — who dismantled a world and claims no responsibility — is confronted directly and without forgiveness. The narrator refuses to be kept ignorant of what was done to him, seeking not salvation but a bare minimum of honesty, insisting the damage was real and the coldness at its center has a name.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~it-never-ends":{"haiku":"Toxic attachment persists despite repeated promises to escape; the speaker's tormentor never leaves, making recovery impossible.","opus":"A relationship experienced as an unwinnable, ceaseless war, each fight another nail in the coffin as the narrator tries and fails to walk away. He repeatedly lies that he's fine, admitting the real torment is not the partner's absence but that they never truly leave his mind.","sonnet":"An obsessive, destructive relationship that the narrator can't escape even by leaving — the other person lives in their head constantly, and what looks like suicide to others feels like an unwinnable war to the one fighting it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The mask is the chorus's insistence on being fine, repeated until the number itself becomes the confession — a thousand denials only prove how unwell the speaker is. What looks like a love song curdles into an addiction song: the lover who never leaves is not a partner but a craving, and the cruelty is that absence would be relief while presence is the torment. Verse two lets the two readings collide — the other voice calls it suicide, the speaker calls it a war already lost, and the build that once counted nails and graves resolves into the plain accounting that all he loved turned into all he lost. The outro abandons argument entirely for the clock, ticking through every unit of time until the title stops being a phrase and becomes a sentence with no parole.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","suicide","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"the lover who never leaves reread as the craving that can't be quit","built":"the outro abandons argument and counts every unit of time until the title becomes a sentence with no parole"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~it-was-written-in-blood":{"haiku":"Inevitable death and transience are preordained, with farewell accepted as natural as flowers blooming and falling.","opus":"Building on a Yesenin farewell, this turns parting into something preordained, roses blooming only to die. The buried reveal of a suicide note pulls its gentle fatalism into grief for a friend who chose to go.","sonnet":"A eulogy framed around a suicide note — the central image is a farewell written in blood the day before a death, and the surrounding lines accept the inevitability of dying with a kind of mournful resignation, comparing life to roses that blossom and fall apart.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song borrows a real suicide note's cadence — a tender leave-taking that insists the parting was fated and the reunion certain — and BMTH weaponizes it by hammering the same phrase until an intimate farewell hardens into an accusation, the note written not in ink but in blood. The verse's calm consolation — death holding no novelty, grief no cause to crease a face — is exactly the reasoning of someone talking themselves into it, so the flat repetition reads as a mind circling a fixed point rather than a chorus. The rose bridge supplies the only movement: a natural blooms-then-dies logic that decays under pressure into pure falling apart, the metaphor collapsing word by word until nothing survives but the falling itself. What underlies the mask of peaceful acceptance is the plain fact stated only once, almost thrown away, that the note was dated one day before its author's death, which turns all the serenity back into grief.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["suicide","death & grief"],"means":"the note's tender fatalism repeated until consolation hardens into accusation","built":"the note dated one day before its author's death — stated once, almost thrown away — turns all the serenity back into grief"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~itch-for-the-cure-when-will-we-be-free":{"haiku":"Awakening to systemic control and yearning for liberation from manufactured reality.","opus":"A song addressed to someone fed up with a fear-built fantasy world, urging them to distrust the system and become a 'kingslayer,' insisting there's a lawless universe beyond the door as their trust in it all glitches loose.","sonnet":"The speaker addresses people who sense that the constructed reality around them is a system built to keep them compliant and afraid, promising them there is a world beyond its rules — and posing the repeated, unanswered question of when collective freedom actually arrives.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song works as a wake-up whispered to someone half-convinced already, its diagnosis landing in the language of malfunction: a trust with a glitch in it, a reality quietly coming unplugged. That refrain of wanting to be a kingslayer paired with the promise of a lawless universe frames liberation as an act of demolition rather than escape. But the chorus keeps collapsing back into a single unanswered question, so the certainty of the verse and the yearning of the outro never resolve into freedom itself, only the repeated, unmet asking of when it might come.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"The title nods to Cure for the Itch on Linkin Park's Hybrid Theory.","themes":["freedom & escape"],"means":"liberation framed as demolition that never quite arrives","built":"the chorus that promises freedom collapses repeatedly to one unanswered question"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~join-the-club":{"haiku":"The song exposes shared mental anguish and alienation, inviting listeners into a collective misery. It portrays a world where disconnection, self-harm urges, and existential despair are universal human conditions.","opus":"Addressed to someone terrified and self-loathing, the song reframes their private suffering as universal, insisting everyone is broken in some way and they are not alone.","sonnet":"The song addresses people who feel alienated, self-destructive, and convinced their misery is unique, arguing that feelings of not fitting in and wanting to disappear are near-universal — a dark invitation to shared suffering rather than isolation.","src":"haiku","fable":"The mask is macho aggression, the underside is a suicide-watch confession, and the song works by fusing them into one address. It opens as a plea to be seen rather than break another promise, then swerves outward to a listener the singer diagnoses as blue-lipped and half-wishing themselves dead, and the cruelty of the hook is really recognition: the taunting welcome to the club is the only invitation on offer, membership defined by shared self-loathing. The bridge strips the performance away entirely, dropping the swagger to loop the naked opening plea about being invisible, so the movement runs from bravado to bare need. The final chorus lands its inclusive turn where the mockery inverts into solidarity, the realization that no one feels alone in this uniquely, everyone equally wrecked, which is offered less as comfort than as the grim floor everyone is standing on together.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["suicide","alienation & emptiness","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"the taunt of membership as the only available form of solidarity","built":"the swagger strips at the bridge to loop the naked opening plea it began with"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~kingslayer-feat-babymetal":{"fable":"A rally cry that hides its target in plain sight: the kingslayer being roused is the listener, and the king to be killed is the corrupted self that has been running someone else's code. BMTH frames this as a system reboot — wake, wipe, back up — so that liberation and factory-reset become the same act, the puppet freed only once its strings are cut and it stops slinking back. The love language — the beloved addressed as an armed angel, as the most cherished thing, as a cause worth dying for — reads as devotion but sits atop that machine logic, which is where BABYMETAL's Japanese verse turns the knife: from inside the dark, still-uncrypted world it simply wants a different world, exposing the tenderness as a lullaby sung to a virus, comfort that keeps the modified thing docile. So the escalation from wake-up call to the final spat obscenity is the mask coming off — the savior speaking to us was always the system testing whether we'll finally bark, bite, and destroy it. Its real message is that the paradise we chase is a castle in the sky it fully intends to bring down.","fableDeep":"It opens as an interview — 'are you looking for the other side?' probes for the disaffected, and the loyalty test 'bark or bite' sorts posers from soldiers — then flips into drill-sergeant imperatives: 'wipe the system and back the fuck up' treats selfhood and society alike as corrupted software awaiting a factory reset. The puppet warning carries the fine print: strings cut, you are on your own, freedom as a one-way door with no crawling back. Then the chorus swerves from rally to love song — the Kingslayer is 'forevermore the apple of my eye', an 'angel of the blade', regicide phrased as romance, the destroyer adored rather than merely followed, and asked, tenderly, to 'come and collect us from the night' like a parent at a school gate. The Japanese verses widen the frame — an invisible world, a door of time to open — while the cyber-verses define the enemy: life encrypted, everyone artificial until death. It is about the longing for someone violent enough to delete the system and gentle enough to come pick you up afterward.","haiku":"Breaking free from control systems and awakening to liberation.","opus":"Rallying a corrupted, controlled person to break free from their strings and rebel, framed in cyber-dystopian battle imagery.","sonnet":"A call to break free from a corrupt, controlling system and join a rebellious savior figure to fight back.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"BABYMETAL first recorded their parts in English — Sykes asked for the Japanese re-record; the concept came to him playing Call of Duty in Amsterdam.","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape","surveillance & control"],"means":"the liberator turns out to be the system testing its own subjects","built":"BABYMETAL's Japanese verse voices the still-encrypted darkness the chorus can't reach"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~kool-aid":{"haiku":"Mass delusion imprisons populations through manufactured needs and false promises; the speaker warns those seduced by conformity will suffer inevitable betrayal.","opus":"Cult obedience and toxic devotion fused into one image, swallowing the poison by the jugful while a controlling force pulls your teeth and plays God. Its possessive chokehold turns blind faith and abusive love into a single fatal embrace.","sonnet":"About radicalization and the seductive comfort of cult-like belief systems — the speaker describes a generation that has drunk deeply from a poisoned ideological well and now suffers willingly, too domesticated to resist, while the song frames this surrender as both pathetic and deeply human.","src":"haiku","fable":"The manic sugary chorus masks an abuser's script: the addiction framing (a taste, a jug drunk to the dregs) turns dependency into the trap, so the embrace and the hands at the throat become the same gesture, tenderness and possession made indistinguishable. What opens as collective indictment — a devolved generation suffocating behind its grin while others play God — narrows to a single intimate voice claiming ownership, the political rot and the private stranglehold revealed as one mechanism. The refusal lands in the collapse at the end: the threat dissolves into a nursery singsong that forgets its own words and shrugs off the promised ruin, the mask slipping to show cruelty that can no longer even be bothered to perform sincerity.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction","politics & society"],"means":"tenderness and hands at the throat rendered as the same gesture","built":"the nursery-singsong outro forgets its own words, the mask slipping into cruelty that can't be bothered to perform sincerity"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~lost":{"haiku":"Mental collapse and medication failure compound despair as the speaker confronts fundamental wrongness about existence itself. Self-annihilation seems inevitable without external intervention.","opus":"Sykes inventories his own collapse with grim precision — dissociating on ketamine as his dog dies and his ego turns against him. The refrain pleads for someone to explain why the pills do nothing and what's broken in him, naming himself the enemy and utterly adrift.","sonnet":"A raw, self-lacerating account of mental illness: medication not working, serotonin bottomed out, a therapist who tried to section them — the speaker, watching Evangelion on ketamine, jokes darkly about ego and MTV while genuinely asking what is wrong with them, convinced there is no remedy.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~ludens":{"fable":"The song reads its Death Stranding brief straight and then bends it inward: the isolation it describes is not just a ruined world of severed cables but the modern condition of being unable to form a real bond even as connectivity is total, the handshake impossible while the signal is everywhere. Its cleverest move is turning the loneliness into performance. The speaker admits he only feels safe when watched and can only hide by staging a spectacle, so the plea for a fresh Luden to lead becomes suspect: he is asking to be rescued in the same breath he confesses to needing an audience. The breakdown strips the mask entirely, the demand to know whether this even counts as a connection collapsing into pure exasperation, and the unbloomed flower with its retry-or-resume choice frames the whole thing as a game loop with no exit, salvation and stalemate looping on the same button.","fableDeep":"The opening does systems analysis in couplets: 'a world covered in cables was never wired to last' puns wiring against wiredness — connection everywhere, resilience nowhere — so that 'don't act so surprised when the program starts to crash' can reframe social collapse as foreseeable software failure, a known bug shipped anyway. The verses convert proverb into threat assessment: sticks and stones get demoted while 'names can dig so many graves' promotes language to the real weapon, and the surveillance paradox 'I don't feel secure no more unless I'm being followed' nails the influencer condition — visibility as safety, hiding accomplished by giving one hell of a show. The chorus asks a gardener's question of a dead network, 'do you know why the flowers never bloom?', then posts the vacancy: a new Luden, Kojima's playing human, leadership reimagined as play rather than management. The bridge sneers the same phrase — you call this a connection? — like a man shaking a dropped call. It is about demanding genuinely human contact from a species that has automated a convincing imitation of it.","haiku":"Modern digital isolation and surveillance destroy human connection, leaving people yearning for authentic leadership and real contact.","opus":"Oli Sykes on a hyperconnected world that leaves everyone more isolated, wired but unable to shake hands. Named for a Kojima character, it's a cry for a new kind of leader amid burnout, cynicism and performed connection.","sonnet":"A song written for the Death Stranding game, about the collapse of genuine human connection in a hyper-networked world — the speaker calls for a new leader (a Luden, a true player) who can form real bonds and pull people back from their gallows.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"Released November 2019 on the Timefall soundtrack for Hideo Kojima's Death Stranding — a year before the album.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"connectivity total yet the real handshake remains impossible","built":"salvation and stalemate share a single button in the game-loop outro"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~mantra":{"haiku":"A charismatic guru exploits existential crisis and spiritual hunger to manipulate followers through repetition, false promises, and psychological control.","opus":"A sly takedown of self-appointed gurus and the seekers who keep swallowing their pitch, Oli Sykes playing the con man to expose how easily people trade real thought for a comforting chant. The joke is that faith this cheap is the actual trap.","sonnet":"A sharp satire of cult leaders and spiritual grifters — the narrator cynically walks through how easy it is to exploit people's need for meaning, how quickly followers surrender critical thinking, and how everyone just ends up chanting the same empty mantra.","src":"haiku","fable":"MANTRA is written entirely in the voice of a cult leader mid-recruitment, and the joke is that he's improvising the con in real time — fishing for a purpose he doesn't have, checking how to spell the revelation he's supposedly selling. The verses escalate the manipulation from group-founding pitch to a guided-meditation seduction that drops you the instant you're vulnerable, exposing the sales patter as a scheduled slot rather than any care. What makes it more than satire is the chorus, which is genuinely true — that liberation feels like being enraged and losing yourself before it frees you — so the huckster is trafficking in real wisdom he neither means nor understands, and the target keeps chanting it back untouched. By the bridge the mask slips into open coaxing: the appeal to faith drops all pretense of content and simply flatters the wish not to think for yourself, naming belief as the easy thing. The song's real subject is that hunger — the way the craving for meaning makes people identical marks for anyone confident enough to spell epiphany.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["faith & the occult","artifice & performance","surveillance & control"],"means":"a huckster trafficking in real wisdom he neither means nor understands","built":"the cult leader checks how to spell the revelation mid-pitch, the improvised con made legible in real time"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~medicine":{"haiku":"Relationship toxicity and the need to break free; giving someone a taste of their own cruelty as liberation.","opus":"A defiant kiss-off to a toxic person compared to storm clouds that make life brighter once they leave, the narrator done swallowing abuse and ready to serve their own poison back. Years of biting his tongue until it bled give way to taking the wheel and refusing to feel. Bring Me The Horizon, the English band by then pivoting fully into arena pop-rock, wrap this cathartic severance in a bright, hook-driven chorus.","sonnet":"Turning a toxic person's own methods back on them — years of absorbing someone else's storms while saying nothing, and then the decision to stop swallowing it and give back exactly what was given. The metaphor is medical: the speaker has been sick from the dose long enough and is now administering the cure, with a warning that it will sting.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~memorial":{"haiku":"Cursed existence and self-destruction predestine the speaker to harm everyone they touch, leaving only stone and regret.","opus":"A self-lacerating confession of feeling cursed from birth and poisonous to everyone close to him, admitting his devotion was never singular or honest. Sykes craves closeness and abandonment at once, desperate to unsay the damage and unable to hold on to anything.","sonnet":"A confession of self-sabotage in love — the speaker admits they were never fully honest, that their heart was divided between a relationship and their vices, and that everything they touched they destroyed.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~mother-tongue":{"haiku":"Emotional intimacy beyond words; true connection expressed through non-verbal communication and the music of the heart.","opus":"A dizzy, sincere love song about a bond too big for words, the narrator so overwhelmed he asks his partner to skip the usual declarations and simply feel it in her own native language. Bring Me The Horizon, the Sheffield band who grew from deathcore into arena-scale pop, commit fully to tenderness here, warmth standing where their menace used to be.","sonnet":"A love that arrived without warning and defies articulation — the speaker, once skeptical of fate, is overwhelmed by a connection so deep that no language can hold it. The request is to skip the words and speak from instinct instead; the mother tongue here is the unspoken emotional frequency that makes declarations unnecessary.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~n-a":{"haiku":"Addiction and suicidal ideation spiral through dark fantasies as recovery proves unstable and familiar destruction beckons.","opus":"Oli Sykes lays his addiction bare as a recovery-meeting confession, admitting he told everyone he was clean while leaving the door open for the wolves. The self-loathing humor barely covers genuine suicidal ideation and the exhaustion of relapse.","sonnet":"Framed as a group therapy confession, the speaker admits relapse into self-destructive dark thoughts — chainsaw fantasies, crash impulses — while acknowledging they never truly escaped their demons and cannot be anyone else's saviour.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~nihilist-blues-feat-grimes":{"haiku":"A trapped spirit claws at walls to escape nihilism and despair, lost in psychological labyrinth, fearing it cannot break free.","opus":"A slick, anxious plunge into existential dread, paradise and terror occupying the same body. With Grimes it renders the modern nihilist as someone trapped in a labyrinth of the self, hungry only for a truth that never arrives.","sonnet":"A spiritual crisis inside a labyrinth — the speaker is trapped between knowing they've found something like paradise and being terrified they can't escape it or themselves; nihilism knocks at the door and the only response is to warn others not to follow.","src":"haiku","fable":"Nihilism arrives as a caller at the door and a thing planted in the basement that grew Cold-War-huge — despair figured as something both external and self-cultivated, impossible to hide from. The verses run a séance grammar (spirit, tomb, exhumed, ashes) against a slangier register of getting high, so that dying, being raised, and peaking share one vocabulary: the search for truth and the search for oblivion become indistinguishable. Paradise sits inside the speaker, yet it reads as a trap he's terrified he can't escape — the labyrinth widens from I to we, making private numbness a shared condition. Grimes's spectral counter-lines (the sleepover levitation chant, the lamb led to slaughter) drift in like intrusive thoughts, sinking the body to the floor while the mind runs its ritual. The repeated plea not to be followed is the honest core under the ecstatic delivery: not a boast of transcendence but a warning that this paradise leads nowhere, and the circular return to the nihilist at the door confirms nothing was outrun.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","transcendence & consciousness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"paradise located inside yet experienced as a trap with no exit","built":"Grimes's spectral counter-lines drift in as intrusive thoughts, sinking the body while the mind runs its ritual"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~no-need-for-introductions-i-ve-read-about-girls-like-you-on-the-backs-of-toilet-doors":{"haiku":"A person rejects a woman who sought to destroy them, offering only contempt and dismissal. The song is pure severance: a two-word thank you that means 'fuck you.'","opus":"A short, scalding burst of contempt aimed at someone who tried to hollow him out and display his ruin, ending on a bitterly ironic thank-you that curdles gratitude into a final insult. Bring Me The Horizon, the Sheffield band fronted by Oli Sykes, made their name in this raw deathcore era before their later stadium turn, and the sheer venom here is characteristic of that early period.","sonnet":"A three-line gut punch of post-breakup contempt — the speaker describes someone who wanted to hollow them out entirely and display the wreckage. The final line withholds real violence in favor of something colder: the implied satisfaction of having survived someone who deserved far worse.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~obey-with-yungblud":{"fable":"The song wears the voice of a soothing institutional authority, and the joke is that its reassurances are all sedatives: obedience is sold as care, surveillance as safety, exploitation as a favor done on your behalf. Beneath that mask sits a first-person casualty who has already turned the violence inward, stabbing at his own sight rather than the system, medicated into contentment precisely because the brainwashing worked. The bridge is where the structure breaks the spell, dropping the smooth voice to accuse the compliant crowd directly, insisting the faceless monsters giving orders are ordinary people and so is everyone letting them. Its cruelest move is offering comfort as the reward for staying blind, so the only way out is the one thing the caretaker forbids: waking up.","fableDeep":"The song splits into victim and system, and the chorus belongs to the machine: 'obey, we hope you have a lovely day' welds command to pleasantry, control speaking in the voice of an HR memo, and 'it's nicer when you can't see the chains' offers ignorance as an amenity, comfort as the product line of oppression. The verses answer in self-harm imagery — brainwashed and feeling fine, a fork and then a knife in the eye — voluntary blinding as the price of ease, with the sardonic advice 'don't let your conscience get in the way' selling self-destruction as liberation. Small mutations across the repeated verses chart a slide from observer to accomplice: the questions turn first-person, don't becomes did, resistance becomes participation. The bridge turns the lens on everyone at once — 'you fucking monsters are people' cuts both directions, meaning the oppressors are ordinary humans and ordinary humans are the oppressors, no alien tyrant available to blame. It is about how a system never needs to enforce obedience once it can get you to put out your own eyes and call the day lovely.","haiku":"Warns of systematic brainwashing and societal control masquerading as safety and free choice. The song depicts a population willingly complicit in their own oppression, numbed and manipulated.","opus":"A song about mass control and self-destruction, mocking a sinister authority that tells people to obey, behave, and not notice the corruption or the chains binding them. It ties this manipulation to inner torment and the urge to hurt oneself, insisting that even monsters are people.","sonnet":"A critique of systems of social control — the song depicts authority figures using psychological manipulation, manufactured compliance, and corruption to keep people passive and unquestioning, with the command to \"obey\" repeated as a sardonic corporate reassurance.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"compliance sold as care until the reward is staying blind","built":"the smooth authority voice drops only at the bridge to accuse the crowd directly"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~oh-no":{"haiku":"Addiction and partying spiral out of control—relationships crumble under lies and endless excess that never satisfies.","opus":"The British band aim a warning at a party-chasing hedonist mistaking constant intoxication and empty highs for real happiness and love. The core idea is the trap of addiction, where a single hit is both too much and never sufficient, and clinging to a counterfeit paradise while barely holding on becomes its own quiet cautionary tale.","sonnet":"Addressed to someone whose drug or substance use has hollowed them out — the narrator watches someone they care about destroy themselves with addiction, sees the emptiness behind the partying, and finally decides it's time to walk away.","src":"haiku","fable":"A party with no off switch, always excessive yet never sufficient — this is addiction dressed as celebration, and the singer is the sober onlooker calling it by its real name. The song runs on a sustained direct address to someone strung out and self-deceiving, each verse and build stripping away an excuse: not a party but a compulsion, not happiness but craving, not love but need. The sax break and the long stretch of wordless oh-no vocables are the point where language exhausts itself and only the sardonic groan is left, the warning about granted wishes looping until it curdles into resignation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism"],"means":"the party diagnosed by the sober onlooker as compulsion dressed as celebration","built":"the sax break and wordless vocables arrive where language runs out, the sardonic groan replacing argument"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~one-day-the-only-butterflies-left-will-be-in-your-chest-as-you-march-towards-your-death-feat-amy-lee": {"haiku":"Two people are locked in a suffocating, poisonous relationship headed toward mutual death, unable to breathe without each other yet destroying each other.","opus":"The grief-duet finale of a record made in lockdown, pairing Sykes with Amy Lee as two people watching a bond rot from addiction and mutual harm. The lush title-image of butterflies is a countdown to a funeral, and the masterstroke is the pronoun shift, the accusation aimed at you sliding into we, the diagnosis catching the accuser inside it.","sonnet":"A toxic bond corrodes both people simultaneously — one already hollow, one only now realizing the damage runs mutual. Warnings were given and ignored, fangs sank deep on both sides, and the relationship becomes a haunting rather than a shelter. The sunset on the love is not relief but the final recognition that neither can breathe alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A codependent love read as a slow overdose, where the two families of imagery — the butterfly of romance and the rot of the body — are made the same thing: the flutter you feel for someone becomes the last twitch inside a corpse marching to the grave. The two voices split the sickness between them — one scolding a partner bent on wrecking themselves, the other conceding the fangs were theirs, sunk so deep that neither can breathe alone. The POV then collapses the accusation inward — the second time the death-march is ours, not yours, and the one who blamed admits he wore the hole in the other's soul himself. The disease was mutual, and by the end the decay is literal: fingernails falling out, love flatly declared absent from the air even as the melody insists on being a love song. It reads the mask of a duet ballad and lets what is underneath — addiction, contamination, two people who are each other's terminal condition — show through the bruises.","fableDeep":"The title image is the structural pivot: butterflies relocate from the stomach, where infatuation keeps them, to the chest, where 'breathing your last breath' happens — romance's symptom becoming death's. Verse one is second-person accusation in a rescuer's exhausted register: 'why'd you keep fucking it up?', bruises showing, the weary refusal of triumph in 'I hate to say I told you so'. But verse two flips every pronoun — 'why'd I keep pushing my luck?' — and the chorus's your chest quietly becomes our chests, the diagnosis expanding from your self-destruction to our codependency. The vampire imagery carries the mutuality: 'how did I let you sink your fangs so deep' assigns blame to biter and welcoming neck alike, and the traded lines about not breathing on your own complete the symmetry — two parasites, no host, 'haunting your own house' with nothing to lose. Amy Lee's outro lowers it all into elegy: sun setting, tide turning, love not in the air. It is about watching someone destroy themselves and discovering, too late, that the watching was mutual destruction all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Sykes has said who the couple really are — a tragic, cheesy love song between humanity and Mother Nature, with Amy Lee singing as the planet. The toxic romance is the costume.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","death & grief"],"means":"mutual contamination wearing the grammar of a duet love song","built":"the death-march shifts from yours to ours mid-song, collapsing accusation into admission"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~ost-p-u-s-s-e":{"haiku":"Dystopian consumer culture seduction warning where emotion becomes commodity and love dissolves.","opus":"A satirical interlude skewering techno-utopian escapism, a mock infomercial selling a simulated paradise that demands you abandon everyone you love. Blood-on-your-hands cynicism about numbing yourself into a fake heaven.","sonnet":"A fragmented collage of pharmaceutical disclaimers, self-help promises, and cult-like recruitment slogans — \"do you want to join the millions who have ascended to Youtopia?\" — satirises wellness and tech-culture manipulation, with \"the blood's on your hands\" as the only honest line. The discontinuity is structural, not accidental.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~ost-spi-ritual":{"haiku":"Invocation of angelic protection using ritual words, framing divine order against unidentified threat.","opus":"A whispered recitation of the Kabbalistic protection ritual invoking four archangels around the self, cut short by paranoia. A brief occult interlude about seeking spiritual shielding in the dark.","sonnet":"A recitation of the Lesser Banishing Ritual of the Pentagram — archangels at cardinal points, pentagram above, six-rayed star within — is cut short by a whispered warning that someone is coming. The interruption reframes ritual as something furtive and endangered.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~ouch":{"haiku":"Toxic relationship aftermath and the evidence of damage; the scars and trauma that follow broken love.","opus":"A bitter, wounded kiss-off to a toxic lover whose self-harm and betrayals the narrator can no longer excuse, culminating in the barbed hope that she not sleep with the devil. It masks its hurt in singsong nonsense syllables and a taunting French opener. Bring Me The Horizon, the Sheffield band by then pivoting toward pop, wrap real venom in deceptively bright, hook-driven packaging.","sonnet":"A relationship that was always going to end badly, discovered in the worst possible way — hearing a lover screaming on the phone, learning the damage runs deeper than expected. The speaker knew they were under a spell but did not anticipate this level of wreckage; shock and betrayal coexist without clean resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~parasite-eve":{"fable":"The song wears pandemic panic as costume horror — written before the real one, about a Japanese virus Sykes had read about, so contagion arrives already storied, a monster with an origin and a name. An announcement voice delivers evacuation platitudes with airline calm, and the ironic distance sharpens into something the band means as the announcements curdle into a declaration of war. Beneath the sick-joke bravado runs a bleaker fear: that we perform terror and belief because feeling nothing genuine is the real sickness, and that once the crisis passes we will absorb no lesson at all. A sampled Bulgarian folk choir opens like a rite already underway, framing collapse not as breaking news but as an ending the ritual has long expected.","fableDeep":"The structure is a public-address system commandeered mid-crisis: the pre-chorus speaks in official soothing cadence — 'please remain calm / the end has arrived' — with the whole satire living in that gap between register and message, completed by 'we cannot save you / enjoy the ride', institutional care collapsing into customer service for doomsday. The verses locate the disease in psychology as much as biology: 'really we just need to fear something / only pretending to feel something' diagnoses panic as a substitute for feeling, dread consumed as entertainment. The Bulgarian folk chant bookending the track makes the plague ancient and pre-digital, arriving from outside the chatter about signal, chips and quarantined secrets. The chorus plants its thesis as a question it already knows the answer to — 'when we forget the infection / will we remember the lesson?' — and the outro strips the metaphor bare: you can't keep washing your hands of this, Pilate meets sanitizer, kings' men who don't know arses from pathogens. It is about a civilization that experiences its own catastrophe as content and forgets it on schedule.","haiku":"An epidemic or plague spreads through a population that ignored warnings, where suspension and fear replace normal life and death becomes inevitable.","opus":"A song about a spreading plague and mass panic, framed as a war rather than a mere warning, with fever, quarantine, and paranoia about hidden agendas. It questions whether people will remember the lesson once the infection is forgotten, and warns that no amount of hand-washing can escape it.","sonnet":"Using pandemic and infection imagery, the song frames a coming catastrophe as inevitable and uncontainable — society is unprepared, leaders are incompetent, and the question is not whether disaster will arrive but whether anyone will remember the lesson once it has passed.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"Opens on Erghen Diado (Petar Lyondev), sung by the Bulgarian Female Vocal Choir; co-produced with Doom Eternal's Mick Gordon. The lyric was revised mid-pandemic — surviving the infection became forgetting its lesson.","themes":["politics & society","faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"viral panic delivered with airline-announcement calm","built":"a Bulgarian folk choir opens as if the ending rite were already underway"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~r-i-p-duskcore-remix":{"haiku":"Death of toxic connection requires ritual celebration as the speaker severs ties with parasitic entanglement. Honoring the end demands ceremony, and the goodbye must be heard.","opus":"A brutal send-off to a backstabber the narrator now treats as a corpse — a leech and a sickness he wants far away. He denies them any tidy resolution and warns them off using his name, staging a mock burial for the dead friendship.","sonnet":"A definitive, contemptuous funeral for a toxic relationship — the other person is declared dead to them, a parasite and heart disease — delivered with sardonic humour, a mock eulogy at the end sealing the coffin on someone who \"gave humans a bad name.\"","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~run":{"haiku":"Escape as only option when everything crumbles; internal chaos and brokenness drive the speaker to flee with nothing left to lose.","opus":"An anthem of escape for two people who feel they're more than another anonymous brick in the grey, urging a headlong flight from a broken world. The soaring hook makes shared desperation sound like liberation.","sonnet":"About escaping a system or relationship that will eventually consume you — don't wait for them to cut you up, take the person you love and disappear before everything breaks, because you're both more than another brick in the grey.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses diagnose a mind in crisis as weather and hieroglyph — a heart that talks in tongues, a head that moves like a storm, sensations too loud and too coded to parse. The song's answer is not to cure the noise but to bolt from it, recasting escape as the only sane move when nothing outside is worth staying for. What sounds like a euphoric love-anthem chorus is doing double duty: the give-me-everything plea and the invitation to run read as romantic rescue on the surface. But the undertow — disappearing where nobody would miss you, the pull toward the void and the dark — lets the same lines carry a darker exit, so the affection and the death-drift are deliberately inseparable. The bridge is where the private ache turns outward, refusing to be reduced to one more indistinguishable unit in a grey mass, so running becomes a claim to being more than interchangeable — and the repeated verdict that it is not enough keeps the euphoria from ever settling into resolution.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["freedom & escape","madness & the mind","love & desire","suicide"],"means":"the euphoric escape anthem carrying a darker exit in the same lines","built":"the give-me-everything chorus reads as romantic rescue on the surface and as death-drift underneath, kept deliberately inseparable"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~seen-it-all-before":{"haiku":"A relationship repeats the same painful patterns; the narrator feels trapped in déjà vu and cannot escape the broken cycle.","opus":"The numbing repetition of a relationship dying by rote, every reunion a fresh skirmish steeped in gloom that plays out like a scratched loop. The harder truth underneath is worn-out surrender: the light has gone from both of them, and love, however much it hurts to admit, no longer suffices.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship dying by repetition — both people have been through the same fights, the same broken cycles, and there's nothing new left to say; the narrator admits the love isn't enough anymore, however much it still stings.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the exhaustion of a relationship as the exhaustion of repetition itself: the shiver of having lived this already, a needle stuck in its groove, lines worn featureless from reuse — the complaint about being trapped in a cycle is delivered through choruses that literally recur unchanged, so the form enacts the numbness the words describe. The bridge is where the mask slips; the four flat insistences that the air holds nothing drain even the melodrama out, collapsing the loud war-and-sadness imagery into pure deadness, the emptiness underneath the noise. Then the outro breaks the loop for the first and only time — the apology that ends it — turning what sounded like a lover's grievance into the moment of actually leaving, the not-enough repeated until it becomes a verdict rather than a feeling.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the complaint about cycles delivered through choruses that literally recur unchanged","built":"the outro breaks the loop exactly once — the apology that turns grievance into a verdict and an exit"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~shadow-moses":{"haiku":"Confronts the past and secrets that cannot be buried, trapped in an endless cycle of repetition where nothing ever truly ends.","opus":"A sense of being trapped and going nowhere while living as if ready to die, confronting a buried past or enemy that refuses to stay dead in an endless, cyclical struggle.","sonnet":"The narrator thought he had buried and escaped something — a feeling, a memory, or a part of himself — only to find it has broken back through; the song frames this return as sempiternal, something that can never truly be killed off.","src":"haiku","fable":"The chorus asks whether the burnout shows on their faces, then the backing voices answer with a flat verdict that undercuts the bravado: they live as if death is welcome, yet the reward is only motion without a destination. Verse one turns a horror-film axiom about the undead back on Sykes himself, so the thing he tries to entomb crashes through his roof anyway, resurrection curdling into a curse of what won't stay dead. The record's title word names that trap precisely, endlessness not as heaven but as a life with no exit, a circling the outro re-enters rather than breaks. Sykes frames it all as a call to arms, complete with alarms and mustered soldiers, yet a stadium roaring its own dead-endedness converts the battle cry into a candid confession of standstill. It is the pit-floor solidarity of people with no place to be but right here, howling the fact once more.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"The title is Shadow Moses Island from Metal Gear Solid — and the ghostly choir opening the track is the game’s own title theme, sampled unlicensed; Sykes’s Kojima fandom later became official with Ludens.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"the battle cry revealed as a candid admission of having nowhere to go","built":"the outro re-enters the circle the song spent its length trying to break out of"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~sleep-with-one-eye-open":{"haiku":"A betrayed friendship erupts into rage; the speaker promises violence and vengeance against someone who bled them dry and sold out their bond.","opus":"A savage kiss-off to a best friend who betrayed the singer, likening the traitor to locusts, leeches and vultures who stripped years of loyalty for personal gain; the fantasy of slitting the throat that planted the knife in his back turns the sentimental idea that best friends are forever into a bitter curse.","sonnet":"A raw explosion of betrayal rage directed at a former best friend who drained the narrator of everything and then walked away — the friendship is now a wound, and what remains is pure murderous fury and a promise that the betrayer will face consequences.","src":"haiku","fable":"The betrayal is anatomical here: the friend is figured as parasites feeding until the body runs dry. But the second verse turns that image inside out, so the wound becomes possession — the wronged party is now lodged inside the betrayer as the shame and guilt that will rot everything from within. That inversion is the whole song's engine, the threat sharpening from vermin-imagery to a curse the traitor carries in their own chest, until the sole tenderness the friendship ever offered gets spat back as its filthiest insult in the final breath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["friendship & loyalty","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"the betrayal completing as the wronged party lodges inside the betrayer as permanent shame","built":"the threat sharpens from vermin to an internal curse the traitor carries in their own chest — wound becoming possession"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~sleepwalking":{"haiku":"A person struggling with suffocation and emotional paralysis, feeling trapped in a recurring cycle of despair as though moving through life without consciousness, begging for rescue.","opus":"A portrait of numbness and suffocation, the singer unable to draw breath and drifting through life as though trapped in an unending dream. Perched at some final edge, he pleads with another person to drag him back and hand him a fresh cause to begin over.","sonnet":"A person describes feeling trapped in a dissociated, drifting state — cut off from their own body, overwhelmed by secrets and inner pain — and pleads to be pulled back to a reason to keep going.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses locate a private, festering secret as bodily illness — fever in the bones, a wound too deep to breathe through — so that emotional crisis is only ever spoken in the language of a failing body, never named directly. The build then turns the senses traitor: eyes, mirrors, shadows and skin all close in and animate against the speaker, the perception itself becoming the suffocation, which is why the one repeated plea is simply for air. The sleepwalking of the chorus names the real horror as recurrence and numbness rather than pain — time frozen, another identical hole fallen into, the self moving through collapse while unconscious of it, unable to wake or choose. Against that paralysis the breakdown is the song's only outward reach, a demand that someone else supply the reason and the hand to begin over, since the speaker cannot generate either from inside. The bridge's return to the same swallowing eyes and smothering skin quietly refuses rescue, looping back into the trance so that the final unaccompanied line lands not as recovery but as the trap resealing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["dissociation & numbness","shame & repression","madness & the mind"],"means":"numbness named as recurrence rather than pain","built":"the bridge's outward plea loops back into the trance, resealing the trap it just opened"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~steal-something":{"haiku":"Existential hunger and consumer desire; wanting to take something meaningful in a world of material theft and dreams.","opus":"A jittery, fragmented anthem of aimless want, chanting a desire to steal something without knowing what it even is, threaded with paranoia about being buried, plundered, and having their number up, plus a stray sampled lecture on dreaming in color. Consumerist emptiness meets dread. Bring Me The Horizon, the British band who've mutated from deathcore into genre-bending pop-metal, lean fully into that glitchy, collage-like restlessness here.","sonnet":"A restless hunger for reclamation — the speaker wants to take back something that was theirs, though they can't name what it is yet. There's a sense of being buried alive, counted out before time, things pillaged and thunder stolen, with a dreaming interlude suggesting that what's lost may only be recoverable in sleep.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~strangers":{"haiku":"Damaged people gather seeking salvation from shared brokenness, alone together in a room full of mutual need. Living and dying intertwine until the distinction erases.","opus":"A hand held out to the alienated and self-destructive, admitting the singer may be broken for good yet insisting that a crowd of strangers can save one another, desperate to live and half in love with dying but not doing it alone.","sonnet":"A room full of broken, isolated people who are alone together — dying to live, living to die — acknowledge no one is coming to rescue them but refuse to go back, their guardian angel having missed the memo. The song is a collective SOS from people who still can't quite articulate what they need saving from.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~sugar-honey-ice-tea":{"haiku":"Humanity is full of empty slogans and shallow falsity; everyone performs perfection while rotting inside.","opus":"A breakup curdles into wider revulsion as the singer buries his awareness and declares that everyone is full of the barnyard filth the coy title spells out. Bring Me The Horizon, the Sheffield outfit who by now had pivoted from deathcore toward pop-metal provocation, aim the contempt at hypocrisy and blind belief, with a jab at self-appointed authority stretching a personal split into a scornful takedown of a whole culture.","sonnet":"A broadside against collective self-deception — everyone marching forward pretending to know the way, burying confusion in noise while calling themselves righteous. The euphemism in the title masks a blunt verdict: society is stuffed full of its own comfortable lies.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~suicide-season":{"haiku":"After losing someone to death, the survivor loses pieces of themselves; grief stops time and the speaker wants to bring them back.","opus":"The title cut of the band's breakout second record confronts a friend gutted by grief, watching them unravel after a death that carved a piece out of them too. Frozen, stopped time stands in for their paralysis, and the narrator wishes his own weeping could open a path to heaven and return the one they lost.","sonnet":"Grief compounds grief: a friend watches a loved one slowly disappear after losing someone to sudden death. The song mourns two absences at once and offers shared sorrow as the only staircase toward some form of survival.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~teardrops":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness and suicidal ideation masked by substance use.","opus":"Emotional numbness, depression and self-destruction, feeling drained and empty while high, isolated and turning against oneself.","sonnet":"Emotional numbness and suicidal ideation — feeling emptied out until even sadness runs dry, trapped in one's own dark mind.","src":"sonnet","fable":"Sykes turns the title against itself: the song about weeping actually reports the well has gone dry, and what remains is not sorrow but the far worse inability to register any of it. He traces the cause backward to a deliberate self-anesthesia, a mind that learned to dull itself until the ache stopped meaning anything and became its own private weather. The song's smartest move is refusing release at the climax, where the singer instead crowns himself the villain of his own damnation, relocating the sickness inward with no outside monster left to accuse. What lands hardest is the closing image of vacancy possessing actual mass, hollowness pressing down like ballast, since numbness proves the cruelest thing a chest can carry. Suited to the record's SURVIVAL HORROR frame, the dread on offer is not gore but flatness: a man narrating his own erasure, unable to muster even proof that he still minded.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"self-anesthesia complete enough that grief becomes dead weight","built":"the title inverted — the song about weeping runs dry"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~the-comedown":{"haiku":"The speaker feels stuck between states, neither fully living nor dead, trapped in addiction and repetition that has become his grave. He speaks of escape as now or never, caught between resignation and desperate need to break free.","opus":"A reckoning with addiction cast as the crash after a high, the narrator accepting the ruin he brought on himself and choosing to sink into it, bidding farewell to friends as his body gives out. Bring Me the Horizon frame the craving he resents but can't shake, and his preference for a brief real existence over a hollow endless one, as a defiant embrace of self-destruction.","sonnet":"A song about addictive behavior or self-destruction that the narrator both despises and can't escape — he's trapped in a cycle he doesn't want but needs, getting harder to feel alive, dug his grave and ready to lie in it. It ends with a last defiant push toward living, even while acknowledging the pull toward death.","src":"haiku","fable":"The comedown is written as a suicide note that keeps refusing to end: the two figures of self-condemnation — the made bed and the dug grave — recur as a fatalistic refrain, each time answered with a grim resolve to lie down in what he built. But the addiction logic underneath twists that resolve into a trap: wanting is disowned and only need admitted, so the pull toward oblivion is framed as compulsion rather than choice, and merely feeling alive keeps costing more. What looks like a death-wish inverts at the bridge, where living now outranks living forever and the moment is declared unrepeatable — the note becomes a last-ditch bid to survive the low rather than surrender to it. The dead-but-walking refrain holds both readings at once: numbed enough to feel already gone, yet still knocking, still coming up tonight.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","suicide"],"means":"the suicide note's refrain inverted at the bridge into a bid to survive the low","built":"the dead-but-walking refrain holds numbness and still-knocking alive in the same phrase, refusing to resolve either"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~the-fox-and-the-wolf-feat-josh-scogin":{"haiku":"Drug addiction spirals from casual use into compulsion and psychological torment, leaving the user panicked and undone on the descent.","opus":"A savage look at the party-drug creed of living once and getting high, and the crash that follows once addiction turns the line into a circle. Sykes' early screamed years indicting the scene's death-riding logic from inside it.","sonnet":"A person seduced into drug use by someone who framed it as living fully is now trapped in addiction's spiral — panic attacks, a wrecked psyche, and the grim clarity that the descent looks far worse on the way back down than it did going up.","src":"haiku","fable":"Addiction narrated as a bargain with a lover who turns out to be Death: the high was sold as a way into heaven with no reckoning on the descent, but the promised one-off closes into a circle, and the descent is where the real cost comes due. The title's animal figure — the impossibility of running with the foxes while hunting with the wolf — names the double-life the addict can't sustain, wanting to belong to both the appetite and the pack that preys on it. Josh Scogin's interrogation cuts through the self-deception as a blunt second voice, demanding the confession the speaker keeps dodging, while the refrain about the poison living in the dosage lets the mind slip its own moorings. What began as swagger about only living once ends flattened on the ground, the boast inverted into a plea to be revived.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"the addict's double-life named as the impossibility of running with foxes while hunting with wolves","built":"Josh Scogin's interrogation arrives as a blunt second voice cutting through the self-deception the speaker keeps dodging"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~the-house-of-wolves":{"haiku":"Religious structures and faith built on lies crumble like a house of wolves, where death is the only salvation from corrupt belief systems.","opus":"A scathing rejection of religious hypocrisy and hollow authority, promising that the whole edifice of false faith will collapse. The band's deathcore-era venom sharpens the taunt that death is the only kingdom the pious will actually inherit.","sonnet":"A rejection of organised religion framed as confrontation: the speaker refuses to accept faith built on manipulation and fear, insisting the promised kingdom is nothing but a grave, and that the edifice of belief will ultimately burn.","src":"haiku","fable":"Oli Sykes treats organized religion as a protection racket run by predators and flatly refuses the shakedown, demanding evidence and a deity willing to step into the light, receiving only murdered truth and a rescue he no longer thinks worth buying. The masterstroke of the governing metaphor is that the institution keeps stacking its masonry higher even as the same load-bearing walls buckle inward on the congregation trapped there — a den of hidden fangs muttering doctrine in every language at once, a machine primed to immolate itself under its own contradictions. The scorn peaks in the breakdown, where every promised paradise shrinks down to the single plot anyone truly inherits: a coffin-narrow trench of dirt. The bitter closing reversal is desire itself, the summoned convert arriving not in surrender but as a taunt, presenting himself unsaved to spite the god who called for him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","death & grief"],"means":"the convert arrives unsaved to spite the god who called for him","built":"the institution's masonry climbs higher as its load-bearing walls buckle inward","fnote":"Sykes wrote it fresh out of rehab, where recovery asked him to surrender to a higher power — he refused: get better for yourself and the people around you, not for a man in the sky."},
"bring-me-the-horizon~the-sadness-will-never-end":{"haiku":"A relationship collapses from exhaustion, betrayal, and substance abuse as both partners try to save each other but remain trapped in cycles of failure. The speaker refuses to abandon their lover despite the hopelessness.","opus":"From their deathcore debut, this desperate plea follows two people dragged along a breaking path, one refusing to abandon the other though the ship is sinking. The narrator vows to save a friend from self-destruction, naming alcohol and pills as a crutch that repairs no trust, clinging to a shared symphony of sympathy against drowning despair.","sonnet":"Two people are sinking together, one trying to hold the other afloat even as the ship goes down. Dependency, exhaustion, and pharmaceutical numbing stack up against a stubborn refusal to abandon someone who may already be beyond saving.","src":"haiku","fable":"The two voices work as a rescue duet with a fatal split. The song builds one exhausted, drowning image-family — a body that can't walk or breathe further, a rope at the throat, a ship going down — against a chorus that keeps insisting on saving another person from themselves, so the whole thing turns on the terrible arithmetic of trying to carry someone toward shore while you are the one sinking. The turn arrives when devotion curdles into diagnosis: the promise to rescue collides with a body numbing itself on substances, and you hear that no amount of loyalty repairs a broken trust, that the drowning may be a choice the beloved is still making. By the outro the vow to never give up loops past comfort into something closer to helplessness, a symphony of sympathy sung over a person already six feet under their own past — the tenderness real, and useless, and unable to stop.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"The clean vocal is Sam Carter of Architects.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","suicide","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"devotion looped past comfort into the helplessness of saving someone still choosing to drown","built":"the outro vow to never give up loops until sympathy and uselessness become indistinguishable"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~throne":{"haiku":"The band reflects on 'Throw me to the wolves'—a song about how adversity and those who want to see you fail actually become the force that shapes who you are and drives your success.","opus":"A comeback anthem built on turning damage into fuel, the people who tried to break him becoming the reason he rose. The band's own spoken note frames it plainly: the ones who hurt you don't make you, yet they got you here.","sonnet":"This is a spoken commentary track rather than a song with lyrics: the vocalist recounts how \"Throne\" was written as a defiant comeback, inspired by the idea that the people who tried to break him ultimately made him who he is.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the armor of a triumphant comeback anthem — every blow becomes building material, wounds turn to a throne, the pack's victim returns as its leader. But the refrain betrays that the strength is entirely borrowed from the wound: the betrayer is named the reason he still fights, so his empire exists only to be brandished back at the person who built it by leaving. The final movement drops the vengeance mask for the tell underneath — the promise to leave the other choking on unspoken words means the whole defiance is still a letter addressed to them, a survival that hasn't stopped needing an audience of one.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty","identity & becoming"],"means":"the defiance that exists only to be brandished at the one who built it by leaving","built":"the whole empire of survival still addressed to an audience of one the speaker cannot stop needing"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~top-10-statues-that-cried-blood":{"haiku":"Tragedy runs genetic, light entering through wounds while suffering teaches despair. Only by spilling truth inward can repair begin, self-rescue the only option.","opus":"An anthem of hard-won self-reliance, accepting that no rescue is coming and that wounds may be how the light gets in, turning inward for the fight and finding that at rock bottom the only direction left is up.","sonnet":"A speaker who has always carried tragedy in their blood reframes their open wounds as the place where light enters, and chooses to go to war with their own interior darkness rather than wait for external rescue. The hardest truth — \"there's no love like your own\" — arrives as revelation rather than comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~true-friends":{"haiku":"The speaker reflects on unforgiven but unchased people from their past; they've made peace by accepting certain losses while keeping former friends at arm's length without active hatred.","opus":"Oli Sykes's spoken introduction frames a song about making peace with betrayal, choosing to release the anger as too draining while keeping the people who hurt him permanently at arm's length. Forgetting without forgiving.","sonnet":"A spoken band commentary: the song is about people from the singer's past who betrayed or failed him — he's made peace with those situations but will never forgive them, keeping them at permanent arm's length.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is the whole engine: it takes the cliché about friends guarding your back and turns it face-on, so betrayal becomes something delivered openly rather than in secret. What sounds at first like pure grievance keeps flipping the wound into fuel — the same resentment that broke the speaker gets renamed as accelerant, the thing that powers rather than defeats him. The refrain hardens across repeats until the accusation stops being a complaint and becomes a verdict handed down, the closing turn insisting the betrayer now finally understands what he did.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["friendship & loyalty","anger & defiance"],"means":"betrayal received as fuel rather than defeat","built":"the title takes the cliché of guarding someone's back and turns it face-on, so the stab arrives from the friend who was supposed to prevent it"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~visions":{"haiku":"Warriors call others to fight and escape a hopeless war together; dreams of leaving everything behind are visions of hope, holding onto escape as a sign.","opus":"Framed as a battlefield rally, it's really about a couple wanting to abandon everything they know and start over. The war imagery captures how desperate escape can feel, and how clarity arrives only after surrender.","sonnet":"A call to abandon a destructive situation and escape together — framed in war metaphors, the speaker urges a partner or comrade to come clean about their secrets and follow the visions that promise a way out.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the costume of a war anthem — rally, trenches, death or glory — but that martial language is a mask for something intimate: the real front line is a relationship, and the fight is the plea to leave everything and everyone behind together. The demand to surrender the dead pushes the metaphor inward, turning the medieval plague-cart summons into a lover's insistence on full confession — no talking in tongues, every hidden skeleton surrendered before escape is possible. The bridge is where the mask drops entirely, reversing the whole logic of conquest: sight comes only from closing the eyes, knowledge only from losing the mind, and the soul the singer would have sold for total knowledge turns out unnecessary, because the keys were already in hand. So the visions aren't a battle plan but a self-deception unravelling — the escape being begged for was always available, held all along by the one demanding it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"Skrillex is credited with additional programming and backing vocals.","themes":["love & desire","freedom & escape","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"the battle-cry inverted at the bridge into a self-deception already unravelled","built":"sight from closing the eyes, knowledge from losing the mind — the whole martial logic reversed in four lines"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~what-you-need":{"haiku":"The speaker tries to help someone see truth, but they remain blind and stuck in self-sabotage; he refuses to be their savior when they keep self-destructing.","opus":"A hard truth delivered to someone willfully blind and stuck in denial, told they don't want the answer but they need it. The compassion curdles in the outro's intrusive, self-harming imagery, revealing that dealing with this exhausting person is corroding the speaker's own mind.","sonnet":"A bitter standoff with someone too stubborn to acknowledge a problem — the singer refuses to argue, predicts they'll come crawling back after their inevitable breakdown, and ends with screaming frustration that borders on self-harm imagery.","src":"haiku","fable":"The mask is a breakup-as-exorcism dismissal: the speaker plays the clear-eyed one refusing to argue with someone too stubborn and self-blinded to be helped, insisting the fault and the future reckoning belong entirely to the other person, who will only understand once they choose to. But the song is built to expose that pose, because the escalation runs one direction only — from the cool composure of not taking the bait, through the build's threat to withhold comfort. It ends in a bridge where the wish to hurt turns inward and goes gleefully self-annihilating. That collapse reveals the calm superiority as the real symptom: what gets projected onto the other as their sickness is the speaker's own, and the addiction-recovery cadence of craving the unwanted reads less as diagnosis of a partner than as the voice failing to apply it to himself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the cool diagnostic posture exposed as the same sickness it projects onto the other","built":"the bridge's gleeful self-annihilation breaks the pose and reveals the diagnosed condition as the speaker's own"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~why-you-gotta-kick-me-when-i-m-down":{"haiku":"Resilience through adversity and the promise of vengeful transformation; being buried only plants seeds for future growth.","opus":"A furious answer to critics and gawkers who crave your blood and prefer you in limbo, the narrator daring them to bury him because he is a seed who only grows through the burial. Betrayal cuts deepest in the admission of having loved them like sons and daughters. Bring Me The Horizon, the English band long practiced at turning public scrutiny into anthems, fuse pop hooks with clenched-teeth resilience across this comeback taunt.","sonnet":"Defiance aimed at an audience that treats someone's suffering as entertainment — the crowd gathered below the window, the voyeuristic thrill of watching someone struggle, the speaker turning burial into germination. Being pushed into the ground is reframed as planting: this is how they grow, and the ones doing the kicking should pray they don't get back up.","src":"haiku"},
"bring-me-the-horizon~wonderful-life-feat-dani-filth":{"haiku":"A detached, numb person reflects on brain decay and meaninglessness while asking whether anyone cares about their existence.","opus":"With a black-metal guest snarling alongside, the band dress up crushing depression in dark humor, a narrator numbing himself in isolation, feeling on the brink, and convinced no one would miss him. Bitter jokes about mental decline and faking cheerfulness turn the mock-upbeat chorus into a sour, ironic salute to a life that is anything but wonderful.","sonnet":"Alienation and flat emotional numbness are dressed in sarcastic wonder, the narrator getting high alone on a Saturday while the world proceeds indifferent to whether he is alive. Brain deterioration at 27 becomes the opening joke in a portrait of someone who cannot locate their own feelings.","src":"haiku"},
"brkn-love~under-the-knife":{"haiku":"Addictive dependence on pain inflicted by another, mistaking suffering for love and intimacy.","opus":"Desire painted as a masochistic death wish, the narrator drawn to a dangerous lover who makes him bleed and like it. Bluesy hard rock about needing pain to feel anything at all.","sonnet":"About a destructive, death-fixated obsession with a woman — she keeps him in a kind of controlled danger (ropes, knives, burial imagery), and he bleeds for her willingly, framing it as both sexual and self-annihilating. He knows he's wasting away but can't stop.","src":"haiku"},
"brody-dalle~don-t-mess-with-me":{"haiku":"A fighter refuses to yield despite threats and violence, standing firm in personal convictions, willing to endure pain rather than surrender what they believe in.","opus":"A defiant refusal to run from armed aggressors, the speaker vowing to fight to the death for what she stands for; the line that losing teeth beats defeat frames physical pain as preferable to surrender, a snarling anthem of standing your ground.","sonnet":"A defiant stand against those who come at her with force or intimidation — Dalle positions herself as someone who won't run, won't surrender, and will fight to the point of physical pain because what she stands for is worth dying for.","src":"haiku"},
"brody~i-can-t-stop":{"haiku":"Obsessive fixation and self-alienation collide as the speaker cannot release what destroys them, locked in compulsive repetition.","opus":"Caught in a compulsive loop over someone who never really knew them, sick of the relationship yet unable to stop as their heart quietly breaks in two.","sonnet":"A song about the compulsive pull of a relationship the narrator knows is damaging — the other person doesn't actually know them, they're a hypocrite, and the narrator is done — yet the emotional attachment won't release no matter how clearly the mind registers it as a trap. The heart is breaking and the will is paralyzed simultaneously.","src":"haiku"},
"brooklyn-funk-essentials~freeway-to-sk-dar":{"haiku":"Present feeling stretches eternal while loss threatens, togetherness the only anchor against dissolution. Without the beloved, direction vanishes.","opus":"A simple, blissed-out plea to freeze a perfect moment forever, love as the thing that erases pain, the narrator lost without the person he begs to stay and never go away.","sonnet":"A state of perfect togetherness — painless as long as they are together, lost without the other — is declared to be worth holding forever. The brevity of the lyric concentrates devotion into a single wish.","src":"haiku"},
"brooklyn-funk-essentials~magick-karpet-ride":{"haiku":"Transcendent flight lifts beyond mountains and mortality, journeying toward transformation without return. The magic carpet carries seekers beyond their own comprehension.","opus":"A globe-trotting funk fantasy borrowing the flying-carpet image to whisk listeners over mountains toward Istanbul and a Turkish night, name-checking Beatles and Bowie characters as fellow passengers on a joyous escapist trip.","sonnet":"A reggae-inflected invitation to fly above the mountains toward Istanbul and a Turkish night, with Mr Kite and Aladdin Sane as fellow passengers, while the narrator admits they are still searching for love in this town. The magic carpet is both transport and metaphor for communal musical transcendence.","src":"haiku"},
"brooklyn-funk-essentials~to-my-peeps":{"haiku":"Recognition of community across scenes; dedication bonds the internal circle.","opus":"A shout-out from a mid-nineties Brooklyn collective that fused funk, hip-hop and spoken word, dedicating itself to its own crew and community as the ground under a new sound to be broken.","sonnet":"A brief dedication and rallying call addressed to the group's community and collaborators, marking the beginning of something new.","src":"haiku"},
"brooklyn-funk-essentials~vinyl-crisis":{"haiku":"Puppetry and enslavement structure existence as the speaker recognizes complicity in their own subjugation; sickness thrives where resistance collapses.","opus":"A warning that people have become obedient robots and slaves to unseen puppeteers, hypnotized into kneeling while claiming to stand above. Salvation, it argues, lies with us alone, in the amygdala-level war between lust and faith that keeps sickness and conflict alive within.","sonnet":"A diagnosis of collective enslavement: puppeteers facilitate obedience, humanity is hypnotised into following signs, and despite standing above, people kneel — simultaneously leaders and slaves. The refrain \"lies on us\" implicates the audience in their own captivity, and the closing \"amygdala\" points to the biological seat of the fear that keeps the system running.","src":"haiku"},
"brooklyn-funk-essentials~you-don-t-know-nothing":{"haiku":"Funk serves knowledge delivery, the speaker offering lessons through rhythm and pocket. Becoming teaches more than words, the body translating what speech cannot.","opus":"A cocky funk come-on, the narrator teasing that his target is searching the world clueless about what she wants, promising he's got it in his pocket, the swagger doubling as a boast about the music itself.","sonnet":"A confident teacher addresses an aimless, ignorant lover — \"you're searching the whole world not knowing what you're wanting\" — and claims to have everything they need right in their pocket, namely funk music and a lesson in living. The repeated taunt is affectionate rather than cruel.","src":"haiku"},
"brothel~searching-for-the-end":{"haiku":"Running backward while fleeing forward, the speaker trapped in temporal paradox seeking exit they cannot name. Time dissolves into repetition without closure.","opus":"A claustrophobic loop of running from a past you keep circling back into, out of time and desperate just to feel alive, the title's search for the end reading as much like a longing for release as for closure.","sonnet":"The speaker runs from the past but runs backwards, circling the same unresolved goodbye, searching for an ending that would feel like being alive. The looping structure mirrors the psychological trap.","src":"haiku"},
"brototype~make-light":{"haiku":"Depression weighs heavy while the speaker numbs themselves and avoids genuine healing, using deflection to mask the approaching collapse.","opus":"A direct address to someone hiding suicidal despair behind a brave face, picturing their own coffin while insisting they're fine. The plea is to laugh it off and sing out from the dark before being written into nothing.","sonnet":"About someone using dark humour or emotional minimisation to cope with suicidal ideation — they've been imagining their coffin and looking for ways to end the suffering, while outwardly 'making light' of the whole thing; the song watches this pattern with dread rather than approval.","src":"haiku"},
"brototype~my-constant":{"haiku":"Loss threatens stability until reconnection restores grounding; the speaker finds safety in one person.","opus":"A search for a stabilizing anchor amid drowning thoughts, fearing there's no constant to be found. The turn comes when home is recognized as a person, the beloved becoming the safe place from which the speaker no longer faces the dark alone.","sonnet":"A song about finding and losing a person who is the emotional anchor keeping the narrator sane — they drown in their conscience without that person, but with them find a home and rest, moving from existential drowning to comfort and safety in someone who grounds them.","src":"haiku"},
"bruno-mars~blast-off":{"haiku":"Euphoria launches consciousness into space and magical realm, levitation achieved through joy and chemical assistance. The party transcends earthly law, ascending toward Saturn.","opus":"A euphoric, drug-tinged romance dressed as a space voyage, taking a little something to levitate above the world and kiss the moon with a lover, the psychedelic-funk high standing in for love's weightless rush.","sonnet":"A drug-assisted night of euphoria launches the narrator and companions into outer space, watching the world from Saturn's rings while good vibrations keep climbing higher. The invitation is communal — bring your friends — and unambiguously hedonistic.","src":"haiku"},
"bruno-mars~finesse-remix-feat-cardi-b":{"haiku":"Confidence and mutual admiration crystallize into a couple moving through the world as a singular force, impossible to diminish.","opus":"Pure retro joy in looking good and being adored, a couple flaunting their chemistry and confidence for the room. There's no ache here, just the giddy self-assurance of two people who know they've got it and want everyone watching.","sonnet":"Two performers — one speaking as himself, the other as Cardi B — celebrate their mutual glamour, confidence, and the magnetic effect their partnership has in any room. It is an unabashed declaration of status and style, with pride in having risen from nothing to commanding admiration together.","src":"haiku"},
"brutalismus-3000~i-bring-my-gun-to-the-function-with-boys-noize":{"haiku":"Alienation expressed as threat in social spaces, announcing sadness as weapon against manufactured connection.","opus":"A hard-techno anthem of doomed intimacy against a collapsing world, the couple's private bond set against mass consumption and destruction. Bringing sadness and menace to the party because there's nothing left but each other.","sonnet":"About bringing violence and sadness into a space built for pleasure — the narrator knows they and a partner have broken what they had, feels out of touch, and responds by arriving armed at social events; the gun is both literal threat and metaphor for the volatility they carry.","src":"haiku"},
"brutus~carry":{"haiku":"Two people console each other in mutual suffering, offering protection even as death looms. The song centers on the comfort of holding someone in darkness.","opus":"A mortal promise between two companions stepping into some realm beyond death, the singer swearing a friend will be protected even as they perish, and asking for the resolve to make it through a final stretch of darkness. Brutus, a Belgian trio fronted by drummer-vocalist Stefanie Mannaerts, fuse post-rock scale, hardcore drive and a rare, tender humanity here.","sonnet":"A song about holding someone through death or something close to it — arms that are both safe harbor and final resting place, and a voice that asks for strength to keep going through one more night. The imagery moves between the underworld and the afterworld without flinching, offering presence as the only real comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"brutus~cemetery":{"haiku":"A person distrusts a woman they're drawn to, finding her hollow and unreliable. The song reflects betrayal and the inability to trust or connect authentically.","opus":"A sour portrait of a woman who looked amazing but proved a wreck, all trust betrayed, undercut by a mocking laugh at how funny she is on the inside, before curdling into a raw plea for more time. Brutus, the Belgian post-hardcore trio built around Stefanie Mannaerts's drumming and vocals, thread that bruised personal candor through their soaring, propulsive sound.","sonnet":"A post-mortem on a person who looked good from the outside and turned out to be hollow and untrustworthy within — the speaker cycles through disgust and dark amusement, laughing off what they can't quite shake. The second half pivots to a quiet, almost raw plea for more time, which sits strangely against the earlier contempt.","src":"haiku"},
"brutus~django":{"haiku":"The song expresses devotion and fear of separation. The narrator promises not to hurt their love and wants to remain together forever, treating loneliness as now overcome.","opus":"A fierce, tender vow of devotion, the narrator promising loyalty to someone whose arrival ended his loneliness and swearing he'll never leave. Brutus are a Belgian trio led by drummer-singer Stefanie Mannaerts, whose fusion of post-rock and post-hardcore lends even a simple pledge of love a swelling, cathartic power.","sonnet":"A devotion song that speaks in the negative — the speaker pledges not to harm, not to leave, defined entirely by what they refuse to do to someone they love. The name Django appears once, anchoring the tenderness to a specific person, and the whole thing reads as a vow made by someone who knows absence too well.","src":"haiku"},
"brutus~dust":{"haiku":"Stagnation presents itself as unchangeable character flaw, the speaker exhausted by judgment while refusing transformation. Nothing shifts—dust settles, silence holds.","opus":"The friction of an unequal relationship, the narrator worn out by someone endlessly digging and demanding, tired of being scrutinized and found wanting, begging for a moment's silence from a partner who plays God.","sonnet":"Two people who refuse to change — the speaker tired of being scrutinised and breathing down their neck — circle each other in exhausted mutual recognition: always searching for more, always digging to the core. The repeated question \"what are you gonna do?\" hangs unanswered over the stalemate.","src":"haiku"},
"brutus~fire":{"haiku":"Fire and flood merge as destructive forces; a person needs water and clarity but finds only burning. The song depicts being overwhelmed by chaotic, transformative violence.","opus":"A volatile scene of breaking down walls and cleansing by fire and water, addressed to someone lost, scared and burned out, the narrator alternately vowing destruction and begging to be laid down and held while thoughts clear. Brutus, the Belgian post-hardcore trio led by Stefanie Mannaerts, channel that push between violence and comfort through their signature surging, emotive dynamics.","sonnet":"A song of crisis and cooling down — fire tears through walls while the speaker craves water, something to extinguish the intensity or at least hold them steady. By the end the imagery softens into someone just lying down, needing to clear their head; the destruction gives way to exhaustion and the need for stillness.","src":"haiku"},
"brutus~liar":{"haiku":"Betrayal through prolonged deception; staying too long makes departure inevitable.","opus":"The Belgian trio channels the exhaustion of staying in something long dead, where honesty has curdled into performance and the self becomes the shadow inside someone else's life, admitting the lie only to survive it.","sonnet":"A self-convicted liar who stayed too long and faded into the ground — not honest for a day, the door slamming in their face the only thing that stops the waiting. The silence is theirs, the darkness theirs, the being so close and so wrong simultaneously — a relationship post-mortem where the narrator holds themselves responsible for the whole collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"brutus~sugar-dragon":{"haiku":"The speaker longs for someone they call sugar dragon, lost and burning in desire while acknowledging they were never truly present in the relationship.","opus":"A woozy address to an alluring, half-imagined figure whose mere presence and voice leave the narrator undone, longing tangled up with the sense that she was never really there at all. Absence and desire bleed together. Brutus, the Belgian trio fronted by drummer-vocalist Stefanie Mannaerts, shape the yearning through soaring post-rock and post-hardcore.","sonnet":"A helpless attraction rendered as weather — the speaker is pulled toward someone who burns and disappears, leaving only loose feathers and a storm that never resolves. The refrain of being lost and the admission of never learning collapses longing and self-knowledge into the same breath, over and over.","src":"haiku"},
"brutus~techno":{"haiku":"A person abandons their old life and head, dancing in a city as a final gesture. The song captures last chances and the surrender of thought for movement and escape.","opus":"A desperate bid for release, the singer determined to abandon overthinking and dissolve into movement on a crowded urban floor, urging another person to join. The tension sits between a restless mind and pure physical surrender. Brutus, the Belgian trio anchored by Stefanie Mannaerts's drumming and vocals, push that craving for escape with their signature driving, cathartic force.","sonnet":"A song of last chances and unexpected release — the speaker reaches a breaking point and chooses escape into movement rather than further collapse, dreaming of dancing in a big city as the way out of a head that has become unlivable. The repetition of leaving the head behind gives the euphoria a slightly manic, desperate edge.","src":"haiku"},
"buck-tick~a-jlhep6":{"haiku":"Themes of death, mortality, or psychological demise; existential darkness permeates the lyrics.","opus":"BUCK-TICK's gothic decadence stages an invitation to a night of animals, Adam and Eve as beasts devouring the forbidden fruit in a frenzy of lust, bodies boiling, love commanded to dance and go mad like a demon or a clown. The whole world becomes a stage for carnal performance. The band's theatrical eroticism turns appetite into spectacle, mortality and abandon fused so that the feast reads equally as ecstasy and as a little death.","sonnet":"An invitation to a night of animals — Adam and Eve as beasts consuming the forbidden fruit in a frenzy of lust, bodies boiling, love commanded to dance and go mad like a demon or a clown, the whole world a stage for carnal performance.","src":"haiku"},
"buffalo-springfield~for-what-it-s-worth":{"haiku":"Military and police suppress youth protests and free speech, spreading paranoia and fear into ordinary life. Authority crushes dissent and silences those who speak out.","opus":"Stephen Stills's snapshot of late-sixties unrest, sparked by the Sunset Strip clashes but reaching wider, watching authority meet young protest with menace. Its power is the cool dread of paranoia creeping in and the warning that stepping out of line gets you taken away.","sonnet":"Written about police crackdowns on youth protesting on the Sunset Strip, the song captures the atmosphere of a generation becoming politically aware and afraid — men with guns, paranoia creeping in, the feeling that stepping out of line gets you disappeared.","src":"haiku"},
"bugs~seriously":{"opus":"A self-mocking love song from a bumbling, accident-prone partner who feels he falls short of his patient, grounding lover. He apologizes for his careless, selfish moments and asks her not to weigh his failings too heavily, admitting he depends on her to keep him from bolting and doing damage.","sonnet":"A clumsy, self-deprecating person asks their partner not to take them too seriously — they trip, spill things, act selfishly, and can't measure up — but they also need the partner's steadying presence like a leash that keeps them from running, and they're grateful for it.","src":"sonnet"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~10-years-today":{"haiku":"A decade after a friend's suicide, the bereaved speaker confronts unanswered questions and survivor's guilt, channeling loss through musical tribute.","opus":"A decade-old grief that never healed, written for a friend who died young, with the survivor still carrying guilt and unanswered questions. The plectrums buried as grave-gifts mark him as a fellow musician, mourned by his band.","sonnet":"A decade after the sudden death of a close friend, the speaker revisits the shock of that loss — the phone call, the funeral, the gifts of plectrums placed in the coffin — and still struggles to understand how and why they were abandoned.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~all-these-things-i-hate-revolve-around-me-album-version":{"haiku":"Cycles of departure and return swirl while dreams shatter; the speaker tears from seams as regret accumulates without clarity or feeling.","opus":"Mid-2000s Welsh metalcore aimed inward as much as outward: a relationship soured by dishonesty tips into the sick suspicion that the narrator is the true source of everything he despises, the fury a thin shield over self-loathing.","sonnet":"Dreams torn at the seams and a relationship built on lies the speaker is always told rather than honesty build toward a breaking point: all the things they hate revolve around them, and the only demand is that whoever is crowding them backs off before the snap comes.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~breathe-underwater":{"haiku":"A departed lover sustained the speaker through crisis; their absence leaves him suffocating and unable to find solid ground.","opus":"Grief for the one person who kept you alive through your worst, blinded now that they're gone. The read is dependency exposed by loss: admitting your imperfection cost you the only light you had, and you don't know how you're still holding on.","sonnet":"Someone who kept the speaker alive through their darkest period is gone, and now the speaker is drowning without them: the absent person was oxygen and light, and without them the speaker is blind and sinking, haunted by his own imperfection and unable to understand how things fell apart.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~don-t-need-you":{"haiku":"A failed relationship is sinking, defined by poor decisions and mutual harm. The singer declares independence from a person who has become toxic, accepting the relationship's death.","opus":"Welsh metalcore band Bullet for My Valentine dress up a doomed relationship as a foundering ship, the narrator repeating that he's fine without his partner. The bravado is a mask, though: he admits he feels gutted and barely alive, and his talk of last chances and repair reveals real wreckage beneath the defiance.","sonnet":"A relationship beyond repair — built on accumulated wrong decisions — collapses into mutual exhaustion. The declaration of not needing the other person is less a triumph than a survival move: the ship is already sinking, the biggest piece already missing, and the only remaining question is whether it is too late to make any of it mean anything.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~hand-of-blood":{"haiku":"A person commits violence or harm and is consumed by guilt and compulsion, unable to control their actions as something dark inside them triggers destructive impulses.","opus":"One of the Welsh metalcore act's earliest statements, it stages a mind snapping into violence like a switch being flicked, a valentine left soaked in red and a narrator terrified of what his own hands have done. The imagery pairs a pounding pulse with denial and dread. It set the melodic-yet-brutal template Bullet for My Valentine would ride out of the mid-2000s scene.","sonnet":"A narrator is gripped by violent compulsions he cannot fully control, watching someone flee in horror from what he has done or become. The red-stained guilt cycles with denial — he registers the damage, questions his own sanity, and cannot escape the trigger wired into him.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~her-voice-resides":{"haiku":"Obsessive thought becomes fatal; the voice of the beloved strangles from within, choking the speaker toward extinction.","opus":"A suicide narrative, a man haunted by a lover's voice until the noose and the chair, saying his final words as the clock runs out. The heaviness is literal: heartbreak metastasizing into a decision to end it, crying out for an angel who never comes.","sonnet":"A man tormented by an ex-partner's lingering presence in his mind — her voice lodged in his head like a wound — delivers what reads as a suicide note, addressing her directly from the moment before the noose. The song maps the transition from obsession to collapse with visceral specificity.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~leap-of-faith":{"haiku":"Transformation requires radical surrender and commitment despite fear, jumping into inferno to transcend limitation and emerge reborn.","opus":"A plunge into the fire as an act of liberation, the singer taking a do-or-die leap that frees him and proves nothing can break him.","sonnet":"Standing at the edge with too many cuts and broken bones, the speaker chooses to jump into the fire anyway — a leap of faith that fills the lungs with the future — insisting nothing will break them even as the pressure demands release.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~not-dead-yet":{"haiku":"Collective refusal to surrender defines survival as humanity mobilizes against despair, insisting on life and agency despite systems designed to crush both.","opus":"A defiant rallying cry to stop wasting time and start truly living, seizing the future while there's still a pulse rather than surrendering to victimhood.","sonnet":"A rallying call to stop killing time and start building the future: with a pulse, a voice, and the chance to break silence and show defiance, the speaker pushes back against a history put on repeat, insisting the group is still alive and must act like it.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~piece-of-me":{"haiku":"Betrayal accumulates wounds until the narrator reclaims boundaries, refusing to be further dismantled by someone who destroys through neglect.","opus":"Refusing to let a toxic partner keep chipping away at him, the singer withdraws his sympathy and guards the last pieces of himself against someone who only brings misery.","sonnet":"The speaker, bruised by too many secrets, broken promises and hits, refuses to give a damaging person another piece of themselves — drawing the line at sink-or-swim and insisting they will not let someone keep fragmenting them even as the damage already done is undeniable.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~seven-days":{"haiku":"Resentment toward unearned privilege festers as the speaker condemns the target's inability to refuse indulgence while wrestling their own regret and withdrawal.","opus":"Contempt for someone handed everything and still hooked on craving more, addressed with the cold refusal to take back a single harsh word. Entitlement as its own slow torture, watched without pity.","sonnet":"A speaker oscillates between calling out someone who claims not to want something but craves it anyway, and confessing their own emotional dishonesty — words said and never retracted, feelings expressed but never meant, desire and revulsion entangled in a recurring cycle.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~suffocating-under-words-of-so":{"haiku":"Complicity in seduction and desire masking violence, guilted by attraction to illusion.","opus":"A metalcore fever of lust, guilt and blurred reality, the narrator intoxicated by a woman he can't tell is real, drowning in accusation and desire. The dream tips toward violence and self-disgust.","sonnet":"A speaker overwhelmed by guilt and intoxication — watching someone \"sleeze all around\" while remaining transfixed by their beauty — confesses they are suffocating under words of sorrow they cannot escape. The live segment appended to the end is MC patter introducing the next song.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~suffocating-under-words-of-sorrow":{"haiku":"Guilt and intoxication blur victim and perpetrator, the speaker drowning in another's beauty while suffocating under sorrow's weight. Flesh and fiction intertwine until truth vanishes.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~tears-don-t-fall":{"haiku":"A person feels guilt and shame, watching a loved one suffer while questioning whether they can be saved or forgiven. The song depicts emotional paralysis and moral torment.","opus":"Bullet for My Valentine's defining Welsh-metalcore track, alternating melody and scream around a lover watching intimacy die. Consumed by guilt and afraid to be truly known, he feels every choice steering him wrong, while her sorrow and reproach summon him back to answer for himself in a wrecked, unhealing aftermath.","sonnet":"A man watches his relationship crumble from the inside — wracked by shame he cannot disclose, walking a path heading nowhere, sustained by nothing. The partner's grief crashes around him like a verdict while he wonders whether honesty would bring any comfort or simply accelerate the ruin already underway.","src":"haiku"},
"bullet-for-my-valentine~you-want-a-battle-here-s-a-war":{"haiku":"Accumulated wounds transform into weaponized rage, the speaker refusing victim silence and declaring total war. Words become ammunition, suffering now ammunition to inflict return harm.","opus":"A survivor turning on an abuser, refusing to absorb the cruelty any longer and vowing to make the tormentor feel the wounds he inflicted, its rallying cry reaching out to others suffering silently that they aren't alone.","sonnet":"A victim who was made to feel dead and tortured by an abuser turns the pain back on them as a declaration of war, vowing they will no longer take it and that the scars will be shared. The closing \"don't suffer in your silence\" reaches outward to others in the same situation.","src":"haiku"},
"bump-of-chicken~a-10tlcom":{"haiku":"Surviving trauma through connection and presence of another person; the speaker endures emptiness and loss but holds on because being close to someone makes the pain bearable.","opus":"BUMP OF CHICKEN's gift for empathetic narrative carries this portrait of someone lost inside a storm of grief, body on autopilot, pain hidden under armor. The thaw comes when a voice reaches them and an uninvited hand makes contact; the beloved's presence is what turns exhausted animal survival into something with warmth and a living pulse. Connection is not rescue so much as the small thing that makes bare endurance bearable.","sonnet":"Someone lost inside a storm of grief — body moving on autopilot, hiding pain under armor — slowly thaws when a voice reaches them and a hand touches them uninvited; the beloved's presence is what transforms exhausted, animal survival into something that still has warmth and a living pulse.","src":"haiku"},
"bump-of-chicken~a-r0we65":{"haiku":"A declaration of commitment and faith—trusting someone completely, proving existence through unwavering loyalty and willingness to be judged.","opus":"The same band renders love as a force that reorders the whole self, a person finding someone who gives their heartbeat, breath, and relentless feeling a reason to exist. Unable to say why they are so moved, they catalog everything as easily lost as this feeling, a ticket left somewhere, history from before birth, and insist the beloved holds the answer. The joy is terrifying precisely because it rearranges who the narrator is.","sonnet":"A person who has found someone who gives their heartbeat, their breath, their relentless feeling a reason to exist — unable to articulate why they are so moved, they catalog all the things as lost as this feeling should be (a ticket forgotten somewhere, history before birth) and insist that this person carries the answer. The emotional core is the terrifying joy of loving someone so much that it reorders the whole self.","src":"haiku"},
"bunny~run":{"haiku":"Escape and flight as survival imperative, the speaker in motion away from threat.","opus":"A Mandarin dream-pop escape hymn about breaking through the disorientation and mental walls of city life, chasing distance and open sky rather than any fixed destination.","sonnet":"A speaker, caught in urban disorientation and the wish to escape, reaches toward someone to run away with — past the walls at the boundary of thought, stopping the surrender, forgetting everything — the repetition of run away framing flight not as defeat but as the only way toward the stars.","src":"haiku"},
"buried-in-verona~can-t-be-unsaid":{"haiku":"Words deliver irreversible damage that erases possibility of repair; the speaker surrenders identity and drowns painful thoughts in pursuit of transformation.","opus":"A hardcore act of letting go, drowning the self-destructive voices and past mistakes to become someone new. The read is the exhausted resolve to stop righting old wrongs and just move forward, mourning what got hurt along the way.","sonnet":"A person choosing radical self-transformation runs toward the ocean to drown the voices that tore them down, letting go of past identity and accepting that life is too short to keep righting every wrong — the change is necessary even if painful.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-me-alive~no-regrets":{"haiku":"Contempt and severance replace love as the speaker demolishes connection; regret transforms into weapon and the past becomes something to be violently overcome.","opus":"A scorched-earth breakup vented as pure contempt, refusing to regret a single day spent on someone whose lies and tricks are told to shove where they belong. The hate is the whole point: looking the ex in the eyes, declaring them an enemy with no way back.","sonnet":"A scorched-earth breakup: the speaker is done with someone whose tricks and lies wasted their time, looks them in the eye with pure hate, and declares themselves free of any regret. The language is deliberately crude and unsparing — \"shove them up your ass,\" \"suffocate in your own shit\" — leaving no ambiguity about the finality of the severance.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~abandon-us":{"haiku":"Society burns its own children at the stake while the rejected walk the wasteland consuming the leftovers of abandonment. Betrayal becomes the only honest truth, infection feeding on selfishness and denial.","opus":"British metalcore staging a scene of collective abandonment, the rage at being cast out slowly turning into the harder admission that the ruin was self-inflicted, guilt and blame gnawing at each other.","sonnet":"The wound of collective betrayal — a group left to walk cursed plains while vultures circle — and the honest turn is the admission \"you know we did it to ourselves,\" making the rage at abandonment inseparable from self-indictment.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~adrenaline":{"haiku":"A rally to reignite lost passion and vitality; the speaker urges another to feel the rush of being alive and discover purpose through that vital force.","opus":"Metalcore urging a fading person back to life, the rush of adrenaline offered as proof of purpose and reason to push through. Camaraderie and survival wired to a physical jolt.","sonnet":"About reigniting someone's will to live when the spark has left their eyes — a rallying cry between two people facing down something together, urging the other to feel the rush of being alive, run hard, and let the blood and adrenaline remind them what they're here for.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~begin-again":{"haiku":"Disconnection breeds cycles requiring complete erasure before renewal, the speaker trapped between guilt and desire for reset. Pushing through requires destroying what came before.","opus":"A craving to erase and reboot the self, trapped in cycles of decline and disconnection, choosing to take control of falling apart and hitting reset, treating annihilation of the old self as the only route to beginning again.","sonnet":"A self-tortured speaker who severed something divine and watched the sky fall apart asks to be erased and reset, trapped in four walls, cycling through guilt and stasis while wanting to push the button and begin again. The reset is both longed for and terrifying.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~black-flame":{"haiku":"Betrayal and murder haunt an unmarked grave; the speaker grieves a hollow perpetrator whose act of violence left both dead souls bound in shared darkness and eternal shame.","opus":"A song about being betrayed by a former comrade, the traitor reduced to an anonymous corpse in a nameless plot that the survivors brand with a scornful title. Grief curdles into contempt, with death recast as the great equalizer.","sonnet":"About a betrayal by someone close — a brother figure who committed violence (a blood-stained blade) and abandoned those who stood by him, the narrator mourning over an unmarked grave while insisting that in death all souls are equal, named and remembered.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~black-flame-single-edit":{"haiku":"Betrayal and murder haunt the unmarked grave as death erases distinction and shame persists beyond corporeal end; legacy becomes ashes.","opus":"A furious address to a brother who betrayed the bonds of loyalty, framed as a killing among men who once had each other's backs. The traitor is stripped of honor and buried anonymously — in death, guilt and innocence flatten into the same dust.","sonnet":"A meditation on murder, guilt, and the dignity of the unnamed dead: the speaker confronts a brother whose blade carries a red stain, whose hollow heart felt nothing, and names the unnamed grave — \"Black Flame\" — asserting that in death all lives carry equal weight. The burning soul is invited to be free even as those left behind reckon with how the killing could happen.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~boltcutter":{"haiku":"Rebellion through severance: cutting through the chains of manufactured authority, refusal echoes as the only remaining language.","opus":"A metalcore rallying cry to cut yourself loose from control and drag the powerful down off their thrones. The boltcutter stands for the refusal to stay caged — the resolve to break the facade and reclaim what was taken.","sonnet":"A song about collective resistance against those who strip people of autonomy — the speaker calls for pulling tyrants off their thrones, breaking free from imposed authority, and refusing to be controlled, even as the cycle of oppression keeps resuming.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~cannibal":{"opus":"A grim look at a world of people devouring one another out of spite, the narrator refusing to apologize for his choices and choosing to face it all alone. Trapped in a loop of mutual hatred, he lands on the bitter wish that his adversary be gone for good, silenced completely.","sonnet":"The narrator and possibly their generation are stuck in a world of mutual destruction and consuming hatred — the image of cannibals captures a society eating itself — and though they are sick and tired, they still condemn those who caused this and hope for burning.","src":"sonnet"},
"bury-tomorrow~death-by-modern-error":{"haiku":"Existential drowning accelerates through manufactured consent; the speaker seeks permission to surrender rather than fight inevitable erasure.","opus":"A drowning-in-modern-life allegory that reads as a suicidal spiral held at bay, the band tying the crushing weight of contemporary pressure to a fight to keep breathing, ending on a defiant refusal to give up.","sonnet":"Exhausted and surrendered, the speaker wants out — 'fuck it, let me go somewhere I deserve' — while the refrain of death reaching in frames the desire not as active self-destruction but as passive capitulation to a void that has grown too cold and too heavy to resist.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~earthbound":{"haiku":"Humanity remains imprisoned by indifference and denial, unable to escape the chains of apathy even as collapse looms; salvation requires breaking through collective silence, but the world refuses to listen.","opus":"An environmental lament dressed as an appeal, mourning a world sleepwalking toward its own ruin while no one listens. It frames the planet's survival as a single squandered chance shared by people shackled to the same dying ground.","sonnet":"A rallying cry against collective denial and environmental inaction: humanity is \"shackled to the same ground\" with one chance left, but no one is listening, and the hope for change whispers like wind while the threat of desolation grows real.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~glasswalk":{"haiku":"Painful steps through darkness require trust and companionship; fear threatens but moving forward means finding strength to walk again after falling.","opus":"A desperate drive toward a threshold countless people long for yet rarely reach, the narrator swallowed by dread but gripping a single chance and one companion to guide him through the darkness, framing recovery as an unrelenting fight against the terror of collapse. Bury Tomorrow, the British metalcore band built on clean-and-scream contrast, pair that formula with themes of endurance.","sonnet":"Walking through darkness toward a distant light, with glass underfoot and fear pressing in on all sides — the speaker holds onto one person as anchor while questioning whether they are willing or able to reach the door others pray for. The song refuses easy arrival, acknowledging the hell of regaining strength while still insisting the walk is worth taking.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~heretic-feat-loz-taylor":{"haiku":"Resistance to conformity and systems of control; heresy as necessary rebellion.","opus":"A furious reckoning with self-punishing belief systems and inherited guilt, the narrator refusing to swallow the lies of the condemned and standing as the last unbeliever against demands to conform.","sonnet":"The heretic position is claimed against systems that strangle the faithful with their own devotion — fine wire around necks, punishing themselves while praying for it — the speaker is sick of swallowing the lies, tightening the line as an act of exposure, and the call to stand alone as the last heretic is both defiance and an understanding that assimilation leads only to annihilation.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~knife-of-gold":{"haiku":"Wealthy elites betray and exploit people while dividing them with lies, draining resources and parasitically feeding. The narrator hunts and destroys these human parasites.","opus":"Metalcore aimed at a corrupt ruling class that betrays with a smile and drains the people it claims to serve. The stance turns from indictment to vengeance, promising to hunt down the powerful who divide and lie to stay on top.","sonnet":"An indictment of wealthy, powerful parasites who stab their followers in the back with their own instruments of privilege — the wealthy old guard is doomed, and the speaker promises to hunt them to the graveyard.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~more-than-mortal":{"haiku":"False gods and heroes betray humanity; the speaker rejects idolatry and demands destruction of corrupted faith and fallen leaders.","opus":"A call to topple the idols and deities who petrified the world and demand worship, insisting people stop pinning their hopes on saviors who only betray them. Beneath the defiance runs a private collapse, the singer fixed on his reflection, straining to stay composed while admitting he is the one truly slipping and can rescue no one.","sonnet":"A crisis of imposed savior status: the narrator is privately crumbling, trapped in his own head, while others project impossible expectations onto him — against this, he and the song reject false idols and gods, insisting everyone is merely mortal and no one is coming to save you.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~my-revenge":{"haiku":"A vengeful naval force rises to destroy enemies for a debt owed; blood spills in waves, driven by rage and a need for reckoning.","opus":"A nautical revenge saga in which the narrator leads a doomed final voyage to avenge comrades who died settling a debt that was truly his own. He knows vengeance restores nothing and only claims more lives, yet he carries out a merciless last stand, honoring the dead by fighting to the end on the open water.","sonnet":"A seafaring vengeance narrative where the narrator, driven by the deaths of people who gave their lives on his behalf, sets sail on a final bloody mission that he knows won't make things right but will end in violent reckoning regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~no-less-violent":{"haiku":"The speaker resists oppression through force and refusal to stay silent despite finite life. Violence is framed as necessary self-defense and warning against tyranny.","opus":"The British metalcore band frame a threat as self-defense, warning oppressors to back off because the oppressed can be every bit as brutal in return. Voiced by someone worn down to nothing who has chosen to fight rather than face punishment quietly, the song is about answering cruelty with cruelty and finding the nerve to speak up before the chance is gone.","sonnet":"A warning delivered from someone who has spent their life alone and unseen: stand back, because the violence hasn't diminished in them; the song is a confrontation with whoever failed to notice, insisting the end is near and silence must finally break.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~overcast":{"haiku":"Someone with murderous intent haunts the past, trapping the narrator in trauma that will never heal. Denial and denial offer no escape from this certainty.","opus":"A confrontation dressed as a psychological thriller, the narrator forcing someone to relive the moment everything went dark and naming their intent as murder, not mere hatred. It's about refusing the other's denial and insisting the damage can never be undone.","sonnet":"A person is described as carrying murderous intent — the song traces back to the cold moment everything changed, confronts the victim's denial, and states flatly that what happened will never be undone or forgiven.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~peacekeeper":{"haiku":"Grieving the loss of a friend, the living hold them in memory and light beyond death. The pain persists but the connection transcends mortality.","opus":"An elegy for a dead friend, promising them they are not alone as they pass into an unknown light, and grappling with whether visions of them are real or grief-born illusion. The consolation is that though the body dies, the spirit rises and the friend stays alive in the minds of the living.","sonnet":"A mourning song addressed to someone who has died — the narrator struggles with grief and perhaps their own stability, but keeps promising the deceased they are not alone and that their spirit survives. It sits at the edge of whether the speaker is trying to comfort the dead or themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~stormbringer":{"haiku":"A call to resist authoritarian control through burning symbols of oppression and dismantling regimes that weaponize fear.","opus":"A rallying cry for revolution against tyrannical power that rules through fear; the narrator urges the oppressed to tear down the walls, burn the flag and symbols of a defamed regime, and reclaim what was taken, standing as a relentless tower and voice for the lawless masses.","sonnet":"A revolutionary anthem about dismantling a fear-based authoritarian regime — the people are being commanded through terror and suppression, and the narrator calls on the oppressed to tear down borders, burn the flag, and reclaim what was taken.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~the-age":{"haiku":"Humanity has destroyed the earth through arrogance and greed; collective death and rebirth are the only escape. The song expresses despair at environmental devastation and wishes to reset time itself.","opus":"An eco-apocalyptic lament that mankind's arrogance and greed have scorched the earth to nothing, longing for a total reset (a black hole to swallow everything) so the age can restart clean; underneath it's the guilt of a species that took all it could and now begs to undo the damage.","sonnet":"A plea for civilizational reset: humanity has strip-mined the earth and piled up bodies to fuel its own machinery, and the singer calls for a black hole to swallow it all and restart the age so that life can be rebuilt from the ground up.","src":"haiku"},
"bury-tomorrow~the-seventh-sun":{"haiku":"Born from violence to destroy what remains, the speaker brings pain without redemption; forgiveness dissolves in blood and apocalyptic purpose.","opus":"Apocalyptic metalcore voiced through children born to burn a broken world down, weaponizing pain and rejection into a scorched-earth defiance against a light that only salted the ground.","sonnet":"A declaration from children born of destruction who have been raised on hatred and blood — their sacrifice is framed as inevitable, a cycle of burying the generation before and being buried in turn — and the refrain \"sick of the light, we are undone\" names their inheritance as darkness from which there is no relief.","src":"haiku"},
"bush~everything-zen":{"haiku":"The song mocks consumerism and shallow celebrity worship, claiming Elvis and peace are dead, rejecting false enlightenment and manufactured meaning.","opus":"Bush's breakthrough throws disjointed 1990s Americana at a wall, Los Angeles, Mickey Mouse, Elvis, Raindogs, and dares it to cohere. Gavin Rossdale sonneteers boredom and jaded desire, refusing the easy calm that the title promises. Arriving as British post-grunge chasing Seattle's wake, it turns cultural overload and detachment into a hook, insisting nothing feels enlightened no matter how it is framed.","sonnet":"Against a panorama of cultural noise, celebrity myth, and domestic restlessness, a narrator pushes back hard against fashionable serenity. American pop imagery collapses into meaninglessness, and the narrator refuses to accept the comfortable lie that everything resolves neatly.","src":"haiku"},
"bush~heavy-is-the-ocean":{"haiku":"Weight presses down while bonds hold despite cracking, love and endurance intertwined as gravity pulling toward depths. The ocean carries both weight and grace.","opus":"Love as a vast, crushing weight you weather together, cracked but unbroken, Gavin Rossdale framing devotion as bravery against a world that knows nothing, the ocean's heaviness both the burden and the proof of what's shared.","sonnet":"Love and survival are both measured against the ocean's weight — cracked but not broken, heavy but steady — with the speaker finding that bravery, not fuckery, is what saves the world a bit. Stars as lead, silhouettes dancing, waves crashing white: the weight is beautiful as well as crushing.","src":"haiku"},
"bush~personal-holloway":{"haiku":"Suburban numbness and prescribed medicine blur consciousness into isolated marriage marked by temporary and impermanent connection.","opus":"A claustrophobic descent into suburban despair, painkillers and quiet suicide behind lit windows, a couple deaf and dumb and married by gesture. The recurring wish for some better arrangement is the only light in an all-too-temporary confinement.","sonnet":"A claustrophobic portrait of suburban despair — paracetamol, kitchen paint, amber-lit nights, suburban suicide — two people married by signs, deaf and dumb with the lights on, circling the possibility that there could be a better plan.","src":"haiku"},
"butcher-babies~beaver-cage":{"haiku":"Trapped sexuality and predatory control; animal desire caged and weaponized against the confined.","opus":"A deliberately unhinged provocation against the labels of reputation and medication, the vocalist leaning into the monster-rockstar caricature others project onto her rather than deny it, chaos as reclaimed control.","sonnet":"A chaotic self-portrait of someone lobotomized by culture, crashing out on Molotov cocktails and ending nights like the headless horseman, with a reputation and a medication and the knowledge that all the world is now a circus — the refrain pissing off all the moms functioning as both provocation and punchline.","src":"haiku"},
"butcher-babies~red-thunder":{"haiku":"Suffering woman becomes storm, pain transforming into power through inherited trauma. The speaker remembers her name in thunder, violence as witness.","opus":"A daughter shaped by a father's inheritance and a mother's absence, locked away and raging back at the world; the storm imagery makes buried trauma into a force that shakes the ground under the people who caged her.","sonnet":"A woman locked away and left to suffer — identified as \"my father's daughter\" — carries a storm within her that the speaker can feel rolling through them, and whose thunder-voice becomes the instrument of shared reckoning. Grief and legacy pass between generations like weather.","src":"haiku"},
"bvdlvd~prison":{"haiku":"Conformity as incarceration: the system consumes autonomy and individuality, yet the speaker refuses surrender and runs, knowing freedom demands fighting against structures that grind all rebels into compliance.","opus":"A rap-metal refusal to be caged by systems that trap and bill the poor while calling it freedom, insisting the only real choice is to game the machine or surrender as its victim.","sonnet":"A speaker who refuses to follow anyone frames the entire social system — bills, conformity, surveillance — as a prison with four walls, insisting they are no slave while acknowledging the impossible squeeze of running and never finding freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"c-shu-nie~a-2rd2c":{"opus":"In C♠Shu≠Nie's dark, jazz-inflected alt-rock, an intimacy between two women turns tender and suffocating at once: whispered secrets, lips shining in the dark, the vertigo of being seen through completely. Underneath runs the admission that nothing fills the void and no certainty about how long to keep living, closing on brilliant color dissolving into white darkness. Closeness and emptiness are inseparable, the bond illuminating exactly what it cannot fill.","sonnet":"A song about an intimate relationship between two women, tender and suffocating in equal measure: whispered secrets, lips shining in the dark, the sense of being seen through completely — but underneath lies the admission that nothing fills the void, uncertainty about how long to keep living, and a final image of brilliant color dissolving into white darkness.","src":"sonnet"},
"c-shu-nie~a-jo96hg":{"opus":"c shu nie built their name on this exact register — polished, math-inflected Japanese rock that keeps its arrangements cool while the interior burns. Here that control becomes the point: the layered traumatic memory, the thing crouching in the dark, the terror sparked by a single flash of light are held inside precise architecture, so the trauma reads not as outburst but as a locked cycle the narrator cannot switch off, sear and darkness looping without rest.","sonnet":"A song about PTSD-like flashbacks that cannot be controlled: traumatic memories crowd the brain in layers, crouch in the dark watching the narrator, and produce terror from a single flash of light — there is no rest and no way out, only the recurring cycle of sear and darkness.","src":"sonnet"},
"c-shu-nie~asphyxia":{"haiku":"A trapped, suffocating child questions freedom and resists resignation, continuing to fight despite impossible circumstances and repeated collapse.","opus":"A child sealed in night stops breathing expressionless, and from there the song wrestles with drowning in an unchangeable world, questioning what freedom even means and whether the pain given was a kind of love, resolving to keep going with a broken, rusting body. Co shu Nie are a Japanese band whose intense art-rock scored anime including Tokyo Ghoul:re, and this asphyxiated defiance is quintessential of them.","sonnet":"A child locked in the night learns to hold their breath against a world that offers no escape — drowning in a reality that cannot be changed, the speaker cycles through questions about freedom and love, rejecting cheap drama and hollow consolation while insisting on continuing even as the body slowly rusts. The song ends with breathing resumed, quietly, in the dark.","src":"haiku"},
"c-shu-nie~zettai-zetsumei":{"haiku":"Despair consumes yet the body refuses surrender, burning through obstacles with stubborn conviction. The speaker accepts horror while running toward tomorrow, choosing motion over collapse despite all odds turning against them.","opus":"The Japanese band frames a desperate, cornered fight to survive despair, arguing that numbing the heart is a lie and that the will to keep moving toward tomorrow is what proves you are still alive.","sonnet":"Crimson sky at dusk feels threatening now that a gentle voice trusted too completely has gone out like a flame; the speaker confronts the illusion that numbing the heart brings relief, rejects the sweet darkness that offers no rescue, and drives themselves forward — \"run through despair to grasp tomorrow\" — with sweat and burning will rather than waiting to be saved.","src":"haiku"},
"cab-calloway~minnie-the-moocher":{"haiku":"A street-smart woman navigates urban desire and adventure; the narrator celebrates her audacity and earthiness with affectionate, playful admiration.","opus":"A Harlem tale of a tough-hearted woman and her opium-den lover, her fantasies of a king's riches shadowing a drug-hazed reality. The nonsense scat is the party mask over a story that ends in pity, the roaring twenties glamour built on a doomed high.","sonnet":"A call-and-response tale of Minnie, a tough but big-hearted woman who falls in with a shady opium dealer and retreats into drug-fueled dreams of lavish wealth showered on her by a king, ending in pity for a life consumed by fantasy and addiction.","src":"haiku"},
"cabal~tongues":{"haiku":"Lies demand punishment through bodily dismemberment, the speaker seeking vengeance against deceivers. Rotten souls must atone through their own corruption exposed.","opus":"Deathcore vengeance fantasy aimed at liars and betrayers, imagining bodily retribution as cosmic justice; the real charge underneath is how deceit poisons trust and the fury it leaves in its wake.","sonnet":"A violent fantasy of punishment for liars: tongues torn out, crowns carved from bones, pale skin peeled back to expose decay, serrated steel erasing every untruth spoken. The rhetoric escalates from justice to total annihilation of the deceitful.","src":"haiku"},
"cabin-boy-jumped-ship~golden":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and despair mount as the speaker recognizes the fragility of their own endurance; advice becomes currency and survival becomes the only measure of worth.","opus":"A mind pleading for help while the dark drowns out every effort to tally what's good, sending out distress signals as everything it carries yanks the shoulders from their sockets. The golden advice to stop lying and ease off runs into the grim correction that it's words, not blows, that ultimately kill.","sonnet":"A person at breaking point — screaming mayday, shoulders about to dislocate under the weight of the world — delivers the advice \"don't lie through your teeth, this advice is golden\" as both a plea and a hard-won lesson, while simultaneously confessing they cannot hear their own soul crying for help. The Shakespearean \"plague upon your houses\" curse erupts from someone who has been destroyed by another's words.","src":"haiku"},
"cabin-boy-jumped-ship~the-flood":{"haiku":"Two damaged people team up in a destructive partnership, burning down their enemies together while water and fire metaphorically represent the chaos consuming them.","opus":"A volatile alliance between two damaged figures, the narrator seeing fire and destructive urges in someone yet declaring who they were is dead and choosing to stand and fight alongside them, escalating into a shared vow to burn the whole enemy world down. The rising-water and burning imagery drives a metalcore anthem of us-against-everything defiance and self-destruction reframed as loyalty.","sonnet":"Two people bound together against an enemy that may be external or internal — the speaker addresses someone whose old self has died in the process of choosing to fight, and frames that death as necessary transformation rather than loss. Fire and flood run together as metaphors for what rises when everything is burned down, and survival is collective or not at all.","src":"haiku"},
"cabin-boy-jumped-ship~the-innocent":{"haiku":"Innocent people suffer as cold leaders wage war and destroy bridges to survival, while those in denial abandon ship as the world burns.","opus":"This electronic-metalcore act stages a world ending through willful blindness, its callous leaders steeped in sin while the guiltless grieve over everyone's failure to see. As the paths to safety are destroyed and catastrophe closes in, a plea to be held tight as everything collapses collides with fury at those who ignored every warning, love and rage knotted together in the ruin they let happen.","sonnet":"Ordinary people — the innocent — bear the cost of cold-hearted leadership that burns its own bridges toward survival while those in power live without moral consequence. The lyric moves between grief at a world in collapse and a fierce refusal to look away, clinging to each other as the burning continues.","src":"haiku"},
"cage-the-elephant~2024":{"haiku":"Existential boredom and repetition—life feels hollow as the speaker walks through another passage of monotony searching for meaning beyond surface pleasures.","opus":"The alt-rock band coax a restless, listless soul toward trusting that meaning exists beyond the tedium consuming them. Small snapshots of a fragile, self-harming figure standing at a doorway sketch someone bleeding and stuck, while the narrator keeps insisting, against the gravity of despair, that there is more worth living for.","sonnet":"Restlessness and boredom circle a sense that something larger is just beyond reach, the kind of ache that arrives when ordinary life feels like it is missing its point. Arriving like a new year, the song holds the feeling of standing on a threshold without knowing what lies past it.","src":"haiku"},
"cage-the-elephant~aberdeen":{"haiku":"Obsessive cyclical abuse from someone dangerously seductive, repeatedly burning the speaker emotionally.","opus":"A song about being repeatedly burned by a manipulative, creeping lover the narrator keeps returning to, cutting the cord only to look way back. Recognizing his own dark side and the toll of the cycle runs beneath the restless refrain.","sonnet":"A speaker repeatedly drawn back to someone who burns him — a relationship defined by compulsive return despite knowing it's destructive — the name Aberdeen anchoring a specific feeling of being unable to cut the cord.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being dragged into a relationship that keeps scorching you, fully aware, and choosing it anyway. The verses stall on how understanding arrives at a crawl while the mind stays stuck, but the chorus is pure reflex — a phone snatched up, a wound reopened, a name barked like a destination he keeps steering toward. Cage the Elephant let the shape do the talking: the track is engineered to spiral, and it ends not on release but on a hammered chant of retreat, compulsion tarted up as a garage-rock hook.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title is an accident, not a pilgrimage — the band kept singing the word while rehearsing in Aberdeen, Scotland (working title: Maybelline). The Nirvana devotion is real but hides in the verses, which fold in phrases from Drain You and Negative Creep.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"choosing the wound again fully aware of what it costs","built":"the track engineers a spiral that ends on compulsion tarted up as a hook"},
"cage-the-elephant~ain-t-no-rest-for-the-wicked":{"haiku":"Economic desperation drives crime and immorality—sex work, robbery, and corruption stem from systemic need and survival.","opus":"A wry parable in which a prostitute, a mugger, and a corrupt preacher all justify their sins with the same line: no rest for the wicked, everyone hustling to survive because nothing's free. The narrator's final admission that we're all the same seeking our thrills turns the moral outward onto all of humanity.","sonnet":"Three vignettes — a sex worker, a mugger, and a corrupt preacher — each explain their crimes the same way: bills to pay, mouths to feed, no rest for those driven by need or desire; human wickedness is economic and universal.","src":"haiku"},
"cage-the-elephant~around-my-head":{"haiku":"A speaker wrestles with obsessive thoughts and emotional entanglement, unable to escape another person's hold on their mind.","opus":"The maddening loop of a person you can't stop thinking about, feelings tangled and inescapable, unsure if it's love or losing your mind. The woozy delivery mirrors obsession that won't leave you alone all night.","sonnet":"Someone occupies the narrator's thoughts so completely it becomes an intrusion — walking around his head all night, tangling him, refusing to resolve into clarity. The obsession is affectionate and slightly plaintive, more baffled than anguished.","src":"haiku"},
"cage-the-elephant~drones-in-the-valley":{"haiku":"War and corporate corruption ravage the world while humanity remains complicit, feasting on lies served by those in power.","opus":"A furious take on a world profiting from war and lies, the devil growing rich while children are baited into slaughter. The read is political disgust curdling into a call to stop turning away and strike back at the forces feasting on the chaos.","sonnet":"War, civilian death, and political corruption are addressed in a voice of nauseated disbelief, with the speaker condemning leaders who profit from death while the population is fed lies and kept compliant. A fork in the road is reached but no clear path forward is named — only the sick certainty that something must change.","src":"haiku"},
"cage-the-elephant~indy-kidz":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects conformist values like fitting in, mainstream media, and social approval, choosing individualism.","opus":"A sarcastic jab at empty conformity and the craving to seem cool, ridiculing the pressure to buy the correct look and dutifully blend in. The narrator dismisses television as pure fabrication and is branded crazy for it, then mockingly echoes the very wish to imitate the people he holds in contempt.","sonnet":"A gentle skewering of indie-kid cool-worship: the narrator is simultaneously drawn to and suspicious of the carefully curated identity of the effortlessly cool person, recognising the conformism hidden inside the pose of non-conformity.","src":"haiku"},
"cage-the-elephant~lotus":{"haiku":"Organized belief systems corrupt natural growth and spiritual awakening, transforming wisdom into propaganda while fear and hatred become the only shared global language.","opus":"A sunrise revelation broadens into disgust at how anything meaningful gets packaged and sold, from conquest dressed up as national purpose to genuine ideas flattened into a marketing trend. The plea is to see past the fear underneath it all.","sonnet":"A meditation on a sunrise that triggers recognition of how hollow and violent civilised life has become — religious certainty weaponised, history rebranded as politics, fear driving collective action toward ruin. The speaker urges a break from the fear-driven pattern before it consumes everything.","src":"haiku"},
"cage-the-elephant~right-before-my-eyes":{"haiku":"Systems collapse inward through accumulated contradiction, leaving the speaker suspended between witnessing global dissolution and personal psychological disintegration.","opus":"Watching the world and your own convictions disintegrate while you stand helpless, unable to look beyond your own perception. A psychedelic account of disillusionment tipping into breakdown, every certainty sliding out from under you.","sonnet":"A witness to a world coming apart — a woman in self-destructive pain, a reality that gives no explanations — describes the moment they fell through their own certainties and could no longer cope with what they saw. The collapse is both external and internal, and knowledge becomes a trap.","src":"haiku"},
"cage-the-elephant~sabertooth-tiger":{"haiku":"A predatory threat stalks from the shadows, beautiful and certain in its intention to devour the unsuspecting.","opus":"A frantic chase from a predatory liar, a sand-mouthed beast hunting you across town. Under the cartoon monster is real menace, the panic of fleeing someone charming and dangerous who's already left you wounded and running.","sonnet":"A predatory, deceptive figure — cold, fast, brain-eating, mouth full of sand — lurks on the other side of town and is drawing closer, and the only possible response is to run. The sabertooth is a metaphor for something toxic and inescapable that pursues through fear.","src":"haiku"},
"cage-the-elephant~shake-me-down":{"haiku":"Past trauma and misery haunt the speaker; they reject despair by keeping their gaze toward the sun despite witnessing others succumb to the sea.","opus":"The Kentucky band survey loneliness and loss, dwindling company and figures wading into the ocean chased by their own sorrow. Against downcast people staring at their feet, the narrator vows to keep his gaze aimed upward, releasing the past and declining to foresee the future, holding steady even on the darkest of days.","sonnet":"Against a backdrop of people he has watched walk into despair, a narrator insists on keeping his eyes fixed on the sun. The song moves through personal bitterness and lost love without letting either pull him into the same downward gaze.","src":"haiku"},
"cake~short-skirt-long-jacket":{"haiku":"Consumerist fantasy describes the ideal woman as combination of aesthetics and financial dominion.","opus":"A deadpan wish-list for a fiercely competent, self-possessed woman, described in the language of corporate finance and cutting precision. The joke and the sincerity coexist: he wants a partner who's sharp and unstoppable, desire filtered through ironic detachment.","sonnet":"A fantasy of competence and ambition projected onto an ideal woman — sharp, fast, financially savvy, and unsentimental — who represents a complete vision of capable modern life that the speaker finds irresistible.","src":"haiku"},
"calabrese~he-who-flees-the-light":{"opus":"A grim condemnation of a broken, corrupted man who runs from the light, hides among corpses, and blames everyone who loved him and the whole world for his pain; such a person, the song insists, deserves no love.","sonnet":"A study in self-destructive bitterness — the person who retreats from the light and goodness of others becomes twisted with hatred and blame, projecting their pain onto everyone who ever loved them and ending up deserving nothing.","src":"sonnet"},
"caliban~alien":{"haiku":"Belonging nowhere becomes identity, the speaker wandering alone into the unknown yet always arriving home. Self-contained root system means no anchor outside the self.","opus":"A metalcore reframing of alienation as freedom rather than affliction: rootless and belonging nowhere becomes a way of belonging everywhere, the outsider making a home inside himself and his own shadow.","sonnet":"A self-described alien wanders alone into the unknown yet finds home wherever they go, carrying the self and its shadow as constant companions — roots inside, heart as the source from which everything grows. The question \"where do you belong?\" is answered: nowhere and everywhere at once.","src":"haiku"},
"caliban~the-shadow":{"haiku":"Internal darkness takes external form, oppressing the host until breaking away becomes survival. The shadow cannot be exorcised, only rejected through reclamation of power.","opus":"Breaking free of an abuser who colonized every part of a life except the pain itself; the darkening heart is the cost of reclaiming the self, survival that leaves you hardened rather than whole.","sonnet":"A relationship that turned home into hell has left the speaker shattered — blood cold, heart black — and the song is the moment of breaking away: ending the masquerade, reclaiming a life taken by someone who left nothing but chest pain. The shadow within threatens to take permanent control unless the speaker acts now.","src":"haiku"},
"calica~superficial-love":{"opus":"A rejection of a fake, half-hearted lover whose flaky behavior and empty smile breed suspicion, refusing to accept superficial affection and demanding genuine commitment.","sonnet":"A person tired of being given hollow, performative affection calls out a partner whose behavior raises suspicion — the missing calls, the dismissals, the fakery — and demands something real or nothing at all.","src":"sonnet"},
"caligula-s-horse~autumn":{"opus":"Caligula's Horse cast growth and loss as the turning of a season, speaking to someone who guided them through change and meeting what came with an open heart. Drawing on the idea of change as a nurturing force, the prog band accept that nothing stays fixed and time moves on regardless. A luminous meditation on impermanence.","sonnet":"A meditation on change as the fundamental nature of existence — addressed to someone who embodies transformation itself, the song accepts that everything shifts, seasons turn, identities unmake and remake, and that resisting the current of change is as futile as trying to stop sunrise.","src":"sonnet"},
"caligula-s-horse~don-t-give-up-cover-version":{"haiku":"Community sustains hope when systems fail; the speaker accepts help as proof that belonging transcends shame and individual collapse yields to collective encouragement.","opus":"A cover of the Gabriel and Bush dialogue on unemployment and shame, alternating between a man broken by losing his work and the voices insisting he still has friends and a place he belongs. It holds both despair and stubborn communal reassurance that he is not beaten yet.","sonnet":"A song of sustaining another person through job loss, displacement, and despair — the burned trees, the lakeside of origin, the \"so many men no one needs\" — with the repeated \"don't give up\" spoken by someone who loves and has not abandoned them. Rest, the acknowledgment that \"we don't need much of anything,\" and the promise of a place where they belong make it as much about presence as hope.","src":"haiku"},
"caligula-s-horse~dream-the-dead":{"opus":"A reflection on legacy, sacrifice, and the way another person can make you feel truly alive. The singer bargains and pleads to build something lasting for one fleeting moment, weighing the bittersweet comfort of what has been buried against the knowledge that even sweet things eventually run out.","sonnet":"A plea directed at someone who wasted their potential on self-preservation — the narrator demands they stop drowning the light and give over the broken shadow they've been hoarding, insisting that legacy and memory can be reclaimed by dreaming the dead back into meaning.","src":"sonnet"},
"caligula-s-horse~firelight":{"haiku":"People who burn intensely and briefly leave marks on those around them; the song celebrates those who shine short and bright, knowing their time is limited yet refusing to diminish their light.","opus":"The Australian prog band swap technical density for open-hearted tribute, addressing people who lived intensely and briefly and vowing to keep their glow alive. It rallies friends, lifted hands and joined voices against the certainty that all things end. Caligula's Horse routinely marry intricate playing to emotional plainness, and this stands as their hymn of remembrance and fierce, fragile warmth.","sonnet":"A communal elegy for people who burned intensely and briefly. The narrator calls those who remain to raise their voices in shared memory, insisting that brilliance, though short-lived, is indelible — and that remembering together is the only honest response to being the ones left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"caligula-s-horse~message-to-my-girl-cover-version":{"haiku":"Vulnerability breaks through protective detachment to finally declare love openly, rejecting cynicism for genuine connection.","opus":"A cover of Split Enz's love song about dropping the fashionable armor of detachment; the Australian prog band keeps its core stance that finally saying the plain thing out loud, despite fear, is more real than guarded cool.","sonnet":"A person frightened by vulnerability finally speaks what they have kept hidden behind hip detachment: they are happy, they are loved, and they want to sing it to the world — though the song's late verses admit that without this person the sand would swallow them, and every night sings the same longing song.","src":"haiku"},
"caligula-s-horse~oceanrise":{"haiku":"A restless person continually runs from fate only to meet it; death comes as an ocean and transforms them into a legacy—rising and becoming the flood they feared.","opus":"The Australian prog outfit build an epic about facing death instead of forever running from it, urging a figure who always flees disaster to finally stop and meet his end. Mortality arrives as a tide embraced with wild devotion, and the song reframes an ending as legacy, the passing life leaving a lasting mark rather than simply vanishing.","sonnet":"A life defined by flight from fate finally surrenders to its own momentum and becomes something vast. Death or ending is reimagined as flood rather than loss: the person who always ran becomes the ocean itself, a legacy that the desert cannot absorb.","src":"haiku"},
"caligula-s-horse~resonate":{"haiku":"In silence, the speaker questions whether they lose themselves in connection or find space to resonate with their own authenticity.","opus":"A quiet meditation asking whether surrendering to another means erasing yourself, or whether stillness makes space for something to sound rather than fall silent. Caligula's Horse, an Australian progressive-rock band known for introspection and Jim Grey's soaring voice, hold the fear of self-loss and the hope of renewal in a single held breath, a high vantage offering the chance to recognize oneself freshly.","sonnet":"In a moment of enforced silence, a person wonders whether the music inside them has died or simply found space to vibrate more purely. Self-hatred and the possibility of forgiveness hang balanced, with resonance — becoming something other than pure hate — offered as the fragile goal.","src":"haiku","fable":"This Brisbane prog band stakes the whole song on one physics question asked in earnest: when the racket of self falls still, does that hush snuff out the song or clear enough empty air for a note to finally sound? The verses treat stillness as a frightening dissolution of ego, the dread of losing your own outline inside another person. The chorus then turns that same silence into the condition resonance requires, casting the loud self as the damper pressed against its own vibrating string. What earns the conceit its stakes is where the plea gets sung from: a low point where the speaker confesses being nothing but hatred, and despair speaks in the image of a body slack on a hanging rope. Resonance becomes survival, the wager that hollowing yourself out might sustain a tone instead of killing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","suicide","identity & becoming"],"means":"ego-dissolution reframed as the only condition in which a note can sound","built":"the hanging-rope image grounds the physics conceit at its lowest point before it lifts"},
"caligula-s-horse~salt":{"haiku":"Seeks redemption and tomorrow despite internal shadows and accumulated sorrow, asking for gentle treatment and someone to hold onto. Mantra becomes both hope and burden.","opus":"The Australian progressive-metal band voice a hopeful struggle out of despair, the narrator worn down yet trusting he can reach the next morning even as he throws himself back into danger. Wrestling weary talk of redemption against inner shadows, he asks only to be treated gently and to amount to something greater than his accumulated wounds.","sonnet":"A song about standing in your own body after long damage — the speaker is battered, barely audible, caught between shadows that outrun any single night, and still asking to be brought to tomorrow. The tenderness is in the word more, repeated like a mantra near the end: more than the scars, more than the rope's end, more than the harm.","src":"haiku"},
"caligula-s-horse~slow-violence":{"haiku":"Religious authority perpetrates slow, grinding suffering under the guise of faith, leaving only decay and broken promises while its victims sing in despair.","opus":"Prog built on the Abraham-and-Isaac image to indict a faith that sanctifies harm, a god who conceals his gun while the faithful bleed empty promises. Its thesis is that pride and sacred violence bury everyone alike beneath the same slow decay.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of organised religion's complicity in violence and abuse — priests who preach promises while concealing crimes against children are accused of a slow violence no different from the god they invoke, who also hides his gun.","src":"haiku"},
"caligula-s-horse~the-ascent":{"haiku":"The song portrays suffering as necessary preparation for ascent, describing a climb through hardship toward light and transcendence with inherited lessons.","opus":"A punishing climb toward transcendence begins in surrender, dropped and bloodied before the rise, casting a loved one as guiding light through grief and clashing realities. Caligula's Horse, the Australian prog band drawn to redemptive, rising arcs, construct a strenuous, foothold-by-foothold epic where dying is merely awakening, and the honor of witnessing dusk's final fading itself instills the resolve to keep climbing.","sonnet":"A dying parent passes everything unfinished to a child, framing descent and surrender as the necessary precondition for true rising. Love persists across the threshold of death, converted into the child's forward motion — the ascent is both literal climb and generational inheritance.","src":"haiku"},
"caligula-s-horse~the-hands-are-the-hardest":{"haiku":"Sobriety and clarity reveal beauty but also martyrdom; a person struggles to find peace and trust. The song interrogates whether enlightenment is worth the cost of lost innocence.","opus":"The Australian prog-metal band render a recovery narrative from inside the shakes of early sobriety, the narrator waking as though blindness has just lifted, willing his unsteady hands to settle and begging for the clarity to make his peace. The refrain names the tremor of withdrawal as the cruelest part, clarity and craving pulling against each other.","sonnet":"Sobriety as both impossible brightness and unbearable exposure — the hands shake and the canvas stays empty, and the pull back toward the dark is framed as something the speaker both invited and resents. The ache is for peace, but the peace keeps slipping; sober eyes reveal too much color all at once, and love alone is not enough to hold the line.","src":"haiku"},
"caligula-s-horse~the-world-breathes-with-me":{"haiku":"Existence intertwines with planetary breath, suffering embedded in creation itself. Peace proves impossible but breathing continues, the speaker insisting they will sing on the road to ruin.","opus":"A furious reckoning with comfortable indifference to suffering and war, then a turn toward hard-won hope: the refusal to look away collapses the line between self and world, so one person's breath implicates everyone's.","sonnet":"A meditation on human violence, ecological failure, and the speaker's own complicity — wanting the world as it is while knowing it destroys the fractured and the frail — arrives at a fragile resolution: as long as you are with me, I'll keep singing, and the world breathes with me. The confessional admission \"I want it and I deserve it\" implicates the speaker in the very destruction they mourn.","src":"haiku"},
"caligula-s-horse~turntail":{"haiku":"A person refuses to flee or surrender despite powerlessness, finding freedom in acceptance and not running from their fate.","opus":"Progressive rock band Caligula's Horse build an anthem of unshakable independence, voiced by someone who refuses to flee when everyone else bolts. Reminding himself that even idols have their flaws, he ignores pressure and farewells alike, treating the present as entirely his own and facing his eventual death with defiant, almost gleeful acceptance.","sonnet":"Radical inner freedom in the face of external pressure: a figure who refuses to flee despite every reason to do so, standing still while others scatter. The moment itself is claimed as the only thing that matters, and even divine authority is shown to have feet of clay.","src":"haiku"},
"caligula-s-horse~valkyrie":{"haiku":"Despite impossible odds and inner turmoil, a person chooses to wait, find strength in stillness, and bleed impossible dreams alongside another toward impossible transformation.","opus":"An intimate exhortation to someone braced for a fight, urging them that peace is not the enemy and stillness is not weakness; the deeper read is that true strength lies in the wonder of waiting and vulnerability, calling for the Valkyrie as a symbol of choosing growth and rest over perpetual war with oneself.","sonnet":"An encouragement to someone who is fighting themselves instead of resting — urging them to call off the internal war, accept that stillness is not weakness, and understand that being on unsteady feet is part of finding the strength to eventually run; the Valkyrie is framed not as death but as the part of you that chooses how to fall and rise.","src":"haiku"},
"caligula-s-horse~will-s-song-let-the-colours-run":{"haiku":"Pursuit of lost self dissolves against relentless waves; sacrifice culminates in erasure as the seeker drowns in the very waters meant for salvation.","opus":"A dying elder's plea across generations, offering the last of his breath to keep a younger one alive and telling him to flee the lies while the flame of will is passed on before decay takes everything.","sonnet":"An old man, still convinced he is the flame that lit the way, urges a younger figure to run before he wears out the path with lies, while the dying voice fills lungs with a last breath and dives into sacred waters; the colours run as a final act of giving the living something to survive for.","src":"haiku"},
"call-me-amour~girl-on-the-wall":{"haiku":"Reality blurs into dream as a lost figure dances through consciousness, inviting someone into a world where they alone provide meaning and salvation.","opus":"A drug-blurred dissociative trip where reality won't hold still and a hallucinated girl becomes a guide into a beautiful, dangerous inner world. The plea not to be woken is the fear of returning to sober life.","sonnet":"A person in a dissociative, drug-altered state sees a girl dancing on the walls and loses track of reality, slipping into a hallucinated world that feels beautiful but is threatening to swallow them whole.","src":"haiku"},
"call-me-karizma~blood":{"haiku":"Violence and aggression seduce the speaker into ecstatic destruction; bloodlust becomes addiction and domination becomes the only way to feel truly alive.","opus":"A cartoonishly aggressive craving for violence, treating fists and biting as an appetite the speaker has down to a science. The bloodlust is half provocation, half confession of a rage he woke up with and can't stop feeding, daring the listener to admit they want it too.","sonnet":"Gleeful, performative aggression styled as desire: the speaker is \"in love\" with the sight of blood and frames violence as both a turn-on and a boundary-enforcing tool against bullies. The braggadocio is self-consciously over the top — \"red flags, raging bull, you're a dead man\" — treating the whole provocation as theatre.","src":"haiku"},
"call-me-karizma~death-of-the-party":{"haiku":"Doom radiates from the speaker, killing joy wherever they arrive, presence itself a curse. They embrace this role, making death and murder their vibe.","opus":"A pop-punk self-portrait of the depressive who ruins the good time by existing, owning the role with rueful humor; the club setting sharpens how mental illness isolates you inside the crowd.","sonnet":"A walking rain cloud with a \"murder on my mind\" vibe crashes every social gathering as its unwilling antagonist — Debbie Downer, Mr Death of the Party — not through malice but because it's in their DNA. The self-awareness is total; the inability to change is equally total.","src":"haiku"},
"call-me-malcolm~i-bet-they-re-asleep-in-new-york":{"haiku":"Mental fog, dissociation, and inability to be present or connect with others. The speaker is trapped inside their own head, unable to hear or process what people say, struggling with isolation and self-doubt.","opus":"A brain that won't quiet drowns its owner in intrusive melodies and drink until he becomes a ghost in his own house, screaming a name he cannot hear. Call Me Malcolm, a UK ska-punk band who leaven brass-bright energy with mental-health candor, set the cyclical reset-and-start-again of anxious rumination against upbeat instrumentation, the admission that he doesn't think he's OK cutting through the party sound with disarming honesty.","sonnet":"Dissociation at a social gathering renders every interaction ghostly and unreachable, the narrator a haunting presence at his own life who cannot absorb what is said or feel present no matter how loudly he forces himself to speak. He admits, quietly, that he does not think he is okay.","src":"haiku"},
"calum-scott~you-are-the-reason":{"haiku":"Love demands total surrender and transformation; the speaker would move mountains and rebuild broken things just to be worthy of keeping this person.","opus":"Total devotion in the wake of a breakup he caused, promising to cross any distance to fix what he broke. The read is desperate contrition, love as the only thing keeping him breathing, begging for one more chance to hold on.","sonnet":"A person who caused damage in a relationship pleads to undo it, willing to cross any distance or obstacle to return and fix what they broke. The love described is both the wound and the reason to heal — the source of pain and the only cure for it.","src":"haiku"},
"calva-louise~aimless":{"haiku":"Disbelief in institutions and authority combines with inability to trust others, creating rootless wandering. The speaker rejects all guidance, condemning those who presume to lead.","opus":"A furious refusal to believe a manipulator's speech, prayers, or professed listening, condemning them while admitting the speaker barely trusts herself. Slipping into Spanish, she declares the commitment broken by his arrogance and heartlessness, vowing to destroy herself before she lets him take what is hers.","sonnet":"A bilingual fury at someone who refuses to listen and whose arrogance broke a commitment — the speaker refuses to let this person taint or take anything from them, condemning them while admitting they have never fully trusted even themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"calva-louise~belicoso":{"haiku":"The song critiques invisible control and imposed warmongering. Unseen forces (chemtrails, injections) alter consciousness; people are manipulated without thought, kept docile through rage.","opus":"A paranoid, bilingual swipe at manufactured rage, the narrator gasping for breath under an invisible chemtrail and arguing that stoked fury and hatred serve to keep a belligerent order, that a sedated public is injected without consent. Belicoso means bellicose. Calva Louise are a London-based trio led by Venezuelan-born Jess Eastwood, and this fuses their punky alt-rock with pointed Spanish-English social critique.","sonnet":"A song about invisible toxicity — something in the air, unseen and unsmelled, is turning everyone combative without them knowing. The Spanish verses make it explicit: rage and hatred are useful to someone, and the asylum of sanity injects belligerence without consent. The word belicoso becomes both accusation and reluctant self-recognition.","src":"haiku"},
"calva-louise~feast-is-over": {"haiku":"Satiation and end of abundance, the speaker confronting emptiness following fulfillment.","opus":"The band recasts a toxic power dynamic through the myth of Sisyphus, refusing subservience and turning the endless boulder back on the exploiter, whose feast of dominance has run out.","sonnet":"The feast as the end of illusion — the speaker would rather sign a deal with Satan than shake hands with someone who gave nothing in return — and the Sisyphus image runs through the whole piece: the boulder, the loop, back where you started, acceptance is the only move left once you stop pretending the cycle will break.","src":"haiku","fable":"A kiss-off to an exploiter that turns the refusal into a curse. The unshaken hand and the readiness to pledge one's soul to the devil instead set the terms: at least hell pays out, where servitude here returns nothing—the feast being someone else's, consumed at the singer's expense. Then the song names its engine outright, folding Sisyphus and his boulder into a circuit with no exit, and the twist is that the myth is shared: the same rock, the same hill, you like me. What starts as venom curdles into grim solidarity, the taunt to push your weight before the boulder crushes you again admitting that the tormentor is trapped in the identical cycle. Jess Allanic sings it less as complaint than as a dare hurled across that shared slope—rage and recognition fused, because the wheel that grinds one grinds both.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","freedom & escape","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"rage and recognition fused across a shared Sisyphean slope","built":"taunt to the tormentor admits they share the identical trap"},
"calva-louise~human-becoming": {"haiku":"Breaking from inherited servitude and guilt-driven compliance, asserting one's right to exist and transform despite others' shame and control.","opus":"A raw account of shedding beliefs that wanted you dead, where becoming fully human means owning your pain and rage rather than sacrificing yourself to a broken agreement you never truly consented to.","sonnet":"An account of shedding inherited shame and a \"deranged agreement\" never consented to — the narrator names their pain directly, refuses to keep sacrificing sweat for systems that want them dead, and insists on the right to be aimless and free even while the rage stays constant. Becoming human here means embracing contradiction: terror without guilt, apology without submission.","src":"haiku","fable":"Allanic dates the injury with a question — how old were you the moment reality first stopped going down, when the explanation of everything turned indigestible — and treats becoming human not as arrival but as the ongoing acquisition of pain, the title's still verb (being) swapped for a wound that keeps happening. The middle is a diagnosis of inherited faith: she serves a creed rigged to destroy her, bound to a contract no sane person would have signed, and the sickness is the labour of honouring that bargain anyway. What breaks the loop isn't victory but permission to be lost — she apologises for each wrong step she takes, then admits that drifting without a compass is a relief, and the closing turn reframes not-knowing as the only honest ground to stand on, the unanswerable riddle itself the thing that keeps her breathing. The rage never resolves, and that constancy is the point: she isn't cured, she's decided to fail on her own terms.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","faith & the occult","freedom & escape"],"means":"permission to be lost accepted as the only honest ground","built":"title swaps the static noun being for an open-wound verb"},
"calva-louise~i-heard-a-cry":{"opus":"A protest song about injustice and complicity: a distant cry that won't fade represents victims of exploitation and stolen land, indicting accusers who repeat old sins with blood on their hands while insisting the damage can still be answered and the chain broken.","sonnet":"A song about collective moral failure and the refusal to break a cycle of complicity — a distant cry signals harm done in the name of land, profit, or tradition, with accusers who are themselves blood-stained. The shift at the end from 'dead end' to 'we won't break the chain' implies that hearing the cry is the beginning of accountability.","src":"sonnet"},
"calva-louise~impeccable":{"haiku":"N-A-G-U-A-L (warrior) walks the edge between death and life, impeccable through surrender to infinity. The path demands releasing reason's grip, accepting the abyss.","opus":"Built on Castaneda's Yaqui-sorcery idea of the nagual and the warrior who lives impeccably with death as an advisor at his shoulder; the Spanish coda surrenders reason to cross the threshold of consciousness.","sonnet":"Drawing on the Toltec warrior-path concept of the nagual, the song describes walking the razor edge between life and death with impeccable awareness of one's own mortality, using death as an advisor to stay sharp and choose a path with full commitment. The Spanish bridge deepens the surrender: no longer clinging to reason, accepting the crossing of the threshold.","src":"haiku"},
"calva-louise~la-corriente":{"haiku":"Autonomy demands rejection of passive compliance and manufactured opinions; the speaker refuses to be swept along by false consensus and performative beliefs.","opus":"Lost among people whose opinions and false humility she can no longer stomach, demanding to be left in peace and refusing to be dragged along by a manipulator's arrogant current that would drown her.","sonnet":"In Spanish: a woman who has lost herself among other people's opinions refuses to be swept along by anyone else's current any longer — she's suffocated by false humility, arrogance, and borrowed ideas, and demands to be left alone rather than continuously pulled under.","src":"haiku"},
"calva-louise~oportunista": {"haiku":"Opportunistic pragmatism and strategic self-interest, the speaker calculating and seizing advantage.","opus":"A reckoning with an opportunist, a self-styled saint who's no Mother Teresa, that the singer means to call out. Admitting her own naivety and hypocrisy, she scorns their false humility and demands real respect, screaming when it's withheld: climb as high as you like, only don't make her the lure.","sonnet":"A confrontation with an opportunist who climbs using others as rungs — the narrator wants to call them out but freezes every time, caught between wanting their friendship and demanding respect that never arrives. 'I wanna see you climb the ladder, just don't use me as bait' is the line that finally names the entire dynamic.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the labor of finally naming an exploiter, where the Spanish words carry the accusation the English cannot quite manage: oportunista, arribista, the climber who uses you as bait to mount the ladder. Jess Allanic keeps confessing her own compromise first — the guilt, the naïveté she admits she enjoyed, the hypocrisy of asking for a respect she freezes when denied — so that the indictment sounds earned rather than merely bitter. The turn is that it takes the mother tongue to land the killing blow: after a whole track of stalled English apology and self-doubt, the falsehood is finally tasted for what it is in Spanish (your fake humility tastes like shit to me), the one line delivered without hedging.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","exile & diaspora"],"means":"mother tongue lands the accusation english kept hedging","built":"Spanish line delivered without qualification after a whole track of stalled English"},
"calva-louise~over-the-threshold":{"haiku":"Intrusion and delusion tangle as the speaker drowns in confusion, crossing the threshold repeatedly into a realm where memory itself becomes unreliable.","opus":"A mind fighting to grow and win against emotions that keep pulling it under, drowning in confusion while someone else takes everything too far. Crossing the threshold becomes the only move left, hooked to infinity, disappearing the moment attention looks away.","sonnet":"A person at a breaking point, questioning whether anything is worth living for while someone else pushes them too far — the threshold becomes a point of no return, a plunge into the infinite that is as much surrender as it is defiance.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses stall in a nervous loop of half-questions that never resolve — whether winning is possible, whether staying cut off is survival, whether anything here justifies the effort — each clause snagging on the next so the mind keeps circling a choice it can't reason its way past. Calva Louise, whose multiverse conceit threads this era of the band, tips the deadlock into physics: the blame lands with mirror symmetry, each side blind to its own share, until nobody holds clean footing and the only way out is to stop arguing and move. The flat, final chant of tipping over is the standoff collapsing into an act — the border isn't understood, just crossed. What waits beyond gets framed as an observer paradox, a self that persists only while watched, so glancing elsewhere means erasure — and that terror of contingent being is the real subject, answered by the leap: better to step across and risk vanishing than keep hesitating on the safe side of the door.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","transcendence & consciousness","surveillance & control"],"means":"the standoff collapses into an act when reasoning runs out","built":"an observer-paradox frames the self as contingent on being watched"},
"calva-louise~over-the-threshold-romes-remix":{"src":"web","web":"A portrait of mental turbulence at a point of no return — 'where's that thing worth living for?' the narrator cries, drowning in confusion, crossing the threshold into a transformative collapse of self.","fable":"The verses stall in a nervous loop of half-questions that never resolve — whether winning is possible, whether staying cut off is survival, whether anything here justifies the effort — each clause snagging on the next so the mind keeps circling a choice it can't reason its way past. Calva Louise, whose multiverse conceit threads this era of the band, tips the deadlock into physics: the blame lands with mirror symmetry, each side blind to its own share, until nobody holds clean footing and the only way out is to stop arguing and move. The flat, final chant of tipping over is the standoff collapsing into an act — the border isn't understood, just crossed. What waits beyond gets framed as an observer paradox, a self that persists only while watched, so glancing elsewhere means erasure — and that terror of contingent being is the real subject, answered by the leap: better to step across and risk vanishing than keep hesitating on the safe side of the door.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["freedom & escape","transcendence & consciousness","surveillance & control"],"means":"the standoff collapses into an act when reasoning runs out","built":"an observer-paradox frames the self as contingent on being watched"},
"calva-louise~square-one": {"haiku":"Breaking identity means starting fresh. Self-importance becomes enemy—only by shedding it can escape and farewell happen.","opus":"A struggle to dismantle a self undermined by inflated ego, treating the collapse as both an exorcism and a transformation. Returning to the very beginning becomes the sole escape route, even if it means bidding farewell to the person the singer used to be.","sonnet":"A song about dismantling the self in order to escape a pattern of failure — the narrator, trapped in a cycle of self-importance and complaint, contemplates metaphorical death and rebirth, concluding that breaking apart who they are may be the only exit, even though every apparent chance looks like another trap.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the paralysis of failing in the same loop, and whether the only exit is to demolish the self that keeps you there. Its antagonist is personified: swollen ego is written as a separate entity that breaks the spirit, throws fits, and hungers to snuff the speaker out — so vanity is reframed not as pride but as an occupying force, the thing that traps rather than the thing that saves. The chorus poses the trap plainly: being reset to the starting square might be another chance or another snare, and if tearing down who you are is the sole door left open, then self and self split into two who can finally say goodbye. That single Spanish word at the center, salté — I jumped — is the whole hinge, the instant the deliberation stops and the leap actually happens, dropped in Jess Allanic's mother tongue as if only the first language can carry the real decision. What follows lands as exorcism: the mind being cast out is called foreign and unconsented, so the metamorphosis isn't self-improvement but eviction — abandoning the very ego that claimed to be you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","madness & the mind","freedom & escape"],"means":"ego evicted rather than reformed, self splitting to say goodbye","built":"single Spanish word salté dropped as the only language that decides"},
"calva-louise~the-abyss":{"haiku":"Surrender to the infinite releases obsession with rationality and control, accepting darkness as gateway. The speaker crosses threshold into abyss, relinquishing fear in exchange for dissolution.","opus":"Sung in Spanish, a disoriented figure who never gets it right and sees no path finds herself on the verge of an abyss with every step. Surrendering to the infinite and releasing the obsession of reason, she accepts what awaits in order to cross the threshold into the dark sea of consciousness.","sonnet":"A speaker lost and directionless — no path, no map, every step bringing them to the edge of an abyss — until they surrender to the infinite, release the obsession with reason, and accept what awaits them in order to cross the threshold.","src":"haiku"},
"calva-louise~third-class-citizen": {"haiku":"Systematic devaluation demands dignity assertion; the speaker builds unshakeable foundation despite oppression.","opus":"A fierce assertion of dignity against those who'd treat an immigrant or outsider as lesser. Refusing to kiss anyone's feet, the speaker respects where she came from while building something solid, and extends the defiance to everyone made to feel on hold.","sonnet":"A fierce rejection of being treated as inferior — the speaker refuses to be bullied, bombed, or stripped of dignity regardless of their origins, building herself from the bottom up and demanding respect without deference, speaking for all those told to stay silent and comply.","src":"haiku","fable":"A migrant's flat refusal, hammered as a mantra until repetition becomes the argument itself: the more times the denial is said, the less it needs proving. What sits between the refrains is quietly the opposite of protest noise — a builder's vocabulary of foundations, solid structures, work started from the ground up, set against the accuser who mistakes inherited position for earned authority and reserves the right to bomb what others made. Calva Louise, a trio scattered across borders with Jess Allanic singing from that outsider vantage, lets the fury carry a distinction most anthems skip: bend the method, keep the backbone untouched — assimilation without self-erasure. The turn comes when the bridge drops the personal and addresses everyone kept on hold, kicked down until they surrender to whatever the majority calls normal, and names the real machinery as broken communication rather than difference. By the time it lands on respect as a demand rather than a plea, the third-class label has been inverted — the one who assigns it, not the one who wears it, is the one exposed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["exile & diaspora","anger & defiance","identity & becoming","politics & society"],"means":"the third-class label inverted onto the one assigning it","built":"mantra repetition makes the denial its own argument"},
"calva-louise~third-class-citizen-bobby-wolfgang-remix":{"haiku":"Refusal hardens into manifesto: the speaker reclaims dignity against systemic relegation, builds foundation on integrity, and rejects the hierarchies that demand conformity and shame.","opus":"Venezuelan-born frontwoman Jess Allanic's migrant defiance: a refusal to be ranked as lesser, holding onto dignity and integrity for everyone treated as inferior when common understanding collapses.","sonnet":"A speaker refuses to accept a subordinate social status assigned to them, insisting that integrity and self-built dignity cannot be seized — the second half widens the address to everyone who is silenced and kicked down until they conform to the norm.","src":"haiku"},
"calva-louise~trial":{"haiku":"The speaker refuses to confess or pay the system's price, choosing to fight back against forced debt and institutional pressure.","opus":"A defiant refusal to submit to an unjust system, choosing to postpone the trial, take the sentence but never confess, and stand on our side rather than pay for crimes that belong to them. The Spanish outro rallies all who are sick of being pressured into surrendering their freedom, insisting a mind's path can't be changed and the truth can still be guarded against the vultures who want to devour us.","sonnet":"A defiant refusal to accept guilt or punishment for crimes the speaker insists belong to someone else — mixing English and Spanish, it speaks for people crushed by a corrupt social order who resist confessing to what wasn't their fault, choosing to face the sentence without breaking.","src":"haiku"},
"calva-louise~tunnel-vision":{"haiku":"Single-minded obsession overtakes rationality; the speaker pursues narrow focus despite warnings of collision and disaster.","opus":"An anxious confrontation with mortality and the unknowable, the narrator craving preparedness for everything and nothingness while a fixed, blinkered view of life keeps them repeating the same mistakes.","sonnet":"Confronting the paralysis of existential anxiety — \"how can I be so overwhelmed / maybe I won't know who I really am until I'm dead\" sits against the insistence that \"this life's just begun.\" The tunnel vision is the trap of cycling through the same fears too many times; the song demands the narrator open their eyes before the fear of nothingness takes control.","src":"haiku"},
"calva-louise~under-the-skin":{"haiku":"Internal monstrosity becomes inescapable, the speaker fighting beasts they created through silence and concealment. Ice waits below the surface, ready to strike at those who harmed them.","opus":"Surviving an abuser and refusing to let their damage own you, turning their attacks into fuel; forgiveness is offered less as mercy than as a way to make the tyrant cease to exist.","sonnet":"A petty tyrant who poisoned the speaker's life is confronted — documented, not forgiven yet — while the speaker names the monster that was created inside them by the abuse and claims victory: \"flight or fight all the time, I already won.\" Forgiveness is the goal but memory will remain.","src":"haiku"},
"calva-louise~w-t-f":{"haiku":"Collective exhaustion and mutual failure create paralysis where survival requires mutual support despite overwhelming odds.","opus":"A spiraling anthem of collective helplessness, admitting nobody has the answers while pleading against making a shared crisis worse, the profanity standing in for genuine paralysis rather than aggression.","sonnet":"Exhausted and directionless, the speaker and those around them are all flailing — \"everybody's fucked, everybody's done\" — but the song insists on solidarity in that shared lostness: you are not alone, so don't make it worse.","src":"haiku"},
"calvin-harris~faking-it-radio-edit-feat-kehlani-lil-yachty":{"haiku":"Both parties perform recovery while secretly harboring lingering attachment; the charade of moving on persists until it fails.","opus":"Two exes performing indifference while both secretly ache, catching each other checking on a love neither has released. The dance-pop shine hides a barbed truth: you're just better at pretending it's over than I am.","sonnet":"Two people who both claim to be over each other are caught in the same 2 AM pattern of checking in, liking posts, and missing the other — the gap between their stated indifference and their actual behaviour is the song's central irony. A third voice adds nostalgic affection, making the pretense even more transparent.","src":"haiku"},
"calvin-harris~sweet-nothing-feat-florence-welch":{"haiku":"Clinging to someone's words that mean nothing; faith becomes survival when love is empty.","opus":"Florence's voice carrying the ache of pouring all your love into someone who gives back only empty words. The dancefloor gloss can't hide that it's about being sustained on nothing and knowing it.","sonnet":"A speaker hollowed out by a partner's empty words tries to sustain herself on the little she has been given — the words felt real once, but they turned out to be nothing, and love built on sweet nothings leaves a permanent absence where something real should be.","src":"haiku"},
"camelphat~be-someone":{"haiku":"Performative identity collapses as posturing reveals hollowness; the speaker dismisses surface markers of status, unmasking the emptiness beneath aspiration.","opus":"A cool dismissal of a striver who dresses up and chases money and cars to become somebody, mistaking talk and appearance for status. The speaker can only laugh at all the talk with no walk, setting real presence against a needy hunger to be seen.","sonnet":"An address to someone performing status — dressing up, chasing money and cars, talking without walking — who is nonetheless unfulfilled and needy for love. The speaker contrasts empty posturing with actually being someone, repeating the phrase as both taunt and genuine aspiration.","src":"haiku"},
"camelphat~breathe":{"haiku":"Suffering yields to surrender as breath becomes the pathway to healing; the speaker extends mercy to the broken, offering breath where pain has stolen speech.","opus":"A steadying address to someone in deep pain, the refrain simply willing them to breathe again. Amid unsteadiness and shared demons, the speaker offers not to bend, promising a sweet surrender that brings the hurting person back alive.","sonnet":"One person talking another through acute distress: the other is hurting so much they have forgotten how to breathe, and the speaker stays steady — \"I won't bend\" — offering surrender to the pain as a path back to feeling alive again. The word \"breathe\" is both instruction and goal.","src":"haiku"},
"camera-obscura~the-sweetest-thing":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession eclipses rationality; the speaker sacrifices everything for glimpses of unattainable brilliance.","opus":"Trying to unlove someone unreachable and famous by dating others, then admitting the whole love song came out crooked because the ache crowded out any sweetness. The Scottish twee melancholy is self-aware, knowing she fumbled even this.","sonnet":"A person still painfully in love with someone who has moved on and become unreachable — successful, visible, absent — tries to go on a date to fall out of love and fails, eventually admitting the love song she was challenged to write became a catalogue of pain she couldn't transcend. The irony is fully earned and gently self-aware.","src":"haiku"},
"camila-cabello~havana-feat-young-thug":{"haiku":"Divided loyalty splits the heart between dangerous passion and home as lovers embody opposing worlds pulling the narrator in contradictory directions.","opus":"A woman whose heart stays tied to her Cuban roots even after a charming man takes her away, torn between the pull of Havana and a romance in East Atlanta.","sonnet":"A woman's heart stays split between her memories of a magnetic, morally ambiguous man — \"malo in him\" — who took her back to East Atlanta on a summer night in June, and the present from which she addresses him, knowing she loved him precisely when she left him.","src":"haiku"},
"can-t-swim~deliver-us-more-evil":{"opus":"A crisis of faith in which the singer, haunted by guilt and the threat of eternal hellfire, wrestles with a punishing God he can't fully believe in and ultimately refuses to preach or condemn others.","sonnet":"A person raised on Christian faith who has lost belief confronts the guilt and moral pressure that lingers even after leaving religion, feeling watched and condemned by a god they can no longer accept, and ultimately daring that god to save someone else.","src":"sonnet"},
"can-t-swim~my-queen":{"haiku":"Romantic idealization curdles into haunting as the beloved becomes a memory that paralyzes; love transforms into obsessive phantasmagoria.","opus":"Picking through the ruins of a relationship built on pretense and things never spoken, the singer is gutted that his ex has moved on to someone else. It reads as haunted regret, the imagined portraits of a bond that never truly existed, and a wish that she loses as much sleep over it as he does.","sonnet":"A person lying awake recounts a relationship haunted by things unsaid, where physical closeness masked emotional withdrawal, and the other person's artwork — literally touched up and hung — became a symbol of everything glossed over and unfinished between them.","src":"haiku"},
"can-t-swim~power-feat-frank-carter":{"haiku":"The powerless cry for justice against systemic oppression and weaponized control; collective rage demands dismantling corrupt institutions.","opus":"A furious protest song, amplified by the guest singer, urging ordinary people to rise up against corrupt authority and the deceptions that sustain oppression. It rattles off the grim tools of street conflict as the fruit of that authority, siding with the brutalized and demanding that control be wrested back and returned to the many.","sonnet":"State violence and systemic injustice are met with a call for collective uprising, naming the tools of oppression alongside the tools of resistance. A generation that has inherited a broken social contract converts its rage into a demand for power returned to the people.","src":"haiku","fable":"Frank Carter's guest bark plants the track in hardcore's protest lineage. Then the song sours its own anthem: that stuttered one-word chant won't say whether it names a thing to seize or a thing to smash. Its sharpest turn heaps up the tools of revolt and crackdown alike, then dubs that arsenal a set of cherished favorites, borrowing a saccharine showtune to prove both sides run on the same hardware. A closing count of years admits nothing has budged, so the demand it leaves reads less as triumph than as a fight each generation restarts.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"revolt and crackdown exposed as running on identical hardware","built":"the chant's one word refuses to name whether power is to be seized or smashed"},
"can-t-swim~sometimes-you-meet-the-right-people-at-the-wrong-times":{"haiku":"Regret crystallizes in photographs and written words as two people drift apart, haunted by the timing that separated them despite their perfect compatibility.","opus":"Insomniac regret over a love abandoned at the wrong moment, clinging to a photograph and its written message until even that keepsake is thrown away.","sonnet":"A traveler adrift on floor boards and jet lag catalogues all he cannot say or unsay: the photograph from someone who still believed in him, broken glass underfoot as he chose to leave, and the silent permanence of the message written on the back.","src":"haiku"},
"cancer-bats~lonely-bong":{"haiku":"Nomadic impulse and communal bond intertwine as the speaker celebrates the blessing of restless life shared with companions; solitude transforms into ritual.","opus":"A touring musician marvels at the compulsion in his blood to keep running the road, cataloguing gigs from Red Deer to Iceland as proof. The restless life is framed as instinct, a blessing rather than a curse, with the missing weed as the one small loneliness in it.","sonnet":"A road-dog manifesto: the speaker has a \"weird theory\" that something in their blood compels them to keep moving — like a rooster crowing, a hawk flying, a wolf howling — and catalogues a life of touring milestones (Red Deer, Milton Keynes, Aberdeen) as proof. The final image of a \"lonely bong missing its buds\" reframes perpetual travel as a kind of companionless craving, funny and melancholy at once.","src":"haiku"},
"candlebox~you":{"haiku":"Pain and possession intertwine as the speaker offers complete sacrifice for connection, recognizing their own expendability while unable to stop the offering.","opus":"Early-nineties grunge push-and-pull: defiance and devotion tangled together, then curdling into a hard refusal of a drug and the people pushing it. Love, exhaustion, and self-preservation fighting for the same breath.","sonnet":"A song that contradicts itself as it moves — declaring indifference and then total devotion, refusing and then surrendering — until it settles into the admission that this particular pain, this particular person, belongs entirely to the speaker and cannot be given up or blamed on anyone else.","src":"haiku"},
"candlemass~demons-gate":{"haiku":"Ancient evil guards the threshold to damnation; sinners face their mythic reckoning at the gates of hell.","opus":"A doom-metal descent into hell itself, a terrified soul clutching a cross as it crosses into damnation. The read is dread made mythic: classic Scandinavian doom staging the passage to eternal punishment as slow, inescapable horror.","sonnet":"A traveller crosses into a cursed passage between the living world and hell — an ancient stone gate created by witchcraft — and is called onward by choirs of damnation into eternal darkness and soul's destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"candlemass~mourner-s-lament":{"haiku":"A grieving parent mourns a child with rituals of devotion, unable to accept death while envying those whose children live.","opus":"A parent's unbearable grief at burying a child, keeping vigil by the grave and envying anyone whose son still lives. The read is mourning stripped of any comfort: candles and prayers that cannot give the boy back, love with nowhere left to go.","sonnet":"A parent grieves a dead child with relentless, physical devotion — candles, flowers, black clothes, prayers — unable to understand why this child and not some other, envying the living their sons, and vowing to mourn until their own death. The grief is total and unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"candy-claws~pangaea-girls-magic-feeling":{"haiku":"Deep time and natural interdependence, one person as sun casting the other into shadow yet sustaining them.","opus":"A hazy dream-pop hymn to devotion measured against geologic time, the singer a small shadow orbiting a beloved sun. Carving initials in a tree becomes a tiny mark of love against the vastness of deep time.","sonnet":"A tender love song built around geological deep time — the narrator describes herself as a leaf-shaped shadow dependent on the beloved as a sun, and carves devotion into a tree. Its emotional core is the humbling, wordless recognition that this person makes the whole world legible.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~a-form-of-protest":{"haiku":"Humanity descends into self-inflicted apocalypse, cursing creation and embracing its own destruction as a form of righteous defiance. Civilization becomes death incarnate, worthy only of burning.","opus":"Nihilistic Southern metal that treats humanity as designed broken and deserving of its own ruin, wrestling with whether self-destruction is the only protest left against a cruel maker.","sonnet":"Humanity as apocalypse-by-design — god made us broken and enjoyed it, we kill light with darkness and deserve the death that follows, soaking ourselves in bleach and collapsing on knives. \"I won't become what I despise\" is the only line of resistance in a song that otherwise accepts collective ruin as the logical conclusion of what we are.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~acid-rain":{"haiku":"The narrator spirals into addiction and habitual intentions, hoping the Dope Man comes daily and wanting to stay in their acid rain. They apologize for being hard to love, but it was the drugs.","opus":"The New Orleans nu-metal revivalists sink into addiction as a bleak form of solace. The narrator, describing himself as a beaten-down stray, would rather stay lost in his habit than climb out of it, and a closing apology admits that the drugs, not the person, made him impossible to love.","sonnet":"Drug dependency is framed not as escape but as habitat — the acid rain is where a person lives, and sobriety feels like exile. The closing admission, offered as an apology, acknowledges that the addiction made loving him nearly impossible, separating his choices from his character while owning the damage done.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~all-we-know":{"haiku":"The speaker asserts that pain and solitary death are all that humans ever know, with suffering repeating endlessly as lies and silence replace genuine understanding.","opus":"The New Orleans metal band lean into flat nihilism, insisting suffering is the only thing anyone genuinely knows and that everyone dies alone with nothing waiting on the other side. There's no sacred reward, only oblivion, and the song refuses every comfort, its nu-metal-tinged heaviness matching a worldview stripped of consolation or false hope.","sonnet":"An unflinching nihilism that refuses to dress itself up — pain is the baseline, death is the destination, and every attempt to ascend or find meaning just adds more noise to an already endless loop. The children growing old and being sold the same story is the sharpest image: generation after generation swallowing the same lie, souls rewinding and echoing nowhere.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~bleed-when-you-ask-me":{"haiku":"Emotional depletion and codependency breed resentment; the speaker bleeds on command for someone who benefits from their pain while offering nothing but extraction.","opus":"An addict's self-portrait as prey and prostitute, decaying and used up while performing an idea of himself for someone else's benefit. It is about watching others live while you bleed out, unsure whether you are high or abused.","sonnet":"The speaker is trapped between prayer and numbness, used as prey by something or someone — faith, a person, a system — that keeps him sedated and fading while others live; \"I bleed when you ask me\" names the cost of being perpetually available to someone else's need while dying from it.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~blood-honey":{"haiku":"A relationship corrupts into mutual destruction; the lover becomes an unshakeable obsession and a disease, leaving only bitterness and regret.","opus":"The New Orleans band turns inward and confessional, tracing a mutually destructive love where both parties break each other and the narrator ultimately names himself the disease. Worship of a muse curdles into hollowness and grief he cannot swallow. It reflects Cane Hill's shift away from their early nu-metal aggression toward starker, more self-lacerating songwriting about addiction and ruin.","sonnet":"A toxic mutual destruction masquerading as love: both people broke each other, yet one narrator still worships the other like a dark deity. Desire and ruin are inseparable — passion ferments into something corrosive and black, leaving hollowness where devotion used to be.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~drag-me-down":{"haiku":"Someone expresses desire to follow their loved one into death and decay, viewing afterlife reunion as the only salvation.","opus":"A grotesquely romantic pact between two lovers who vow to stay joined even in death and decay, one keeping the other's remains as a sacred relic and treating one death as the cue to follow. Cane Hill, the New Orleans metal band, drench this fixated devotion in the grinding heaviness that runs through their work.","sonnet":"A love letter written past the grave — the speaker wants to be buried alongside whoever they've lost, to keep the bones in a heart-shaped box and meet again in some after-collision. The devotion is absolute and a little terrifying, promising to follow into death rather than remain alive and separate.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~fade":{"haiku":"Dissolution feels safe when embraced together, the speaker surrendering into the beloved's darkness. Fade into you becomes salvation through complete merger.","opus":"Codependent love as mutual dissolution, two people choosing to disappear into each other's darkness rather than heal; the sweetness of shared suffering is exactly what makes it a slow entombment.","sonnet":"Two people share a desire to dissolve into each other, finding beauty in pain, gloom, and sadness as shared aesthetics of closeness. The question \"will you keep me too?\" reveals the vulnerability beneath the romanticised suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~fade-into-you":{"haiku":"Shared suffering creates merger where pain binds more strongly than love, and dissolution into another becomes salvation. The speaker seeks obliteration of self through entanglement with darkness.","opus":"A desire to dissolve into a partner defined by sadness and doom, sharing in their suffering as the only way to keep them from sinking. The intimacy is entangled with self-erasure, illusion and delusion tasting the same as long as they fade together.","sonnet":"A desire to dissolve entirely into someone else's pain and beauty: loving them feels like walking on broken glass, their eyes reflecting sadness and gloom, yet the speaker will share in their suffering and keep them from sinking, asking only whether they will be kept too.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~finding-euphoria":{"haiku":"Addiction masquerades as euphoria while decay metastasizes through emptiness, trapping the speaker in a cycle of surrender and terminal apathy.","opus":"Watching a person sink beyond rescue into addiction and rot, turning into the very thing that ruined them while claiming no fear of a solitary end. Relief only shows up after complete surrender, and the track keeps circling the unresolved question of whether giving up command of one's life was ever worthwhile.","sonnet":"A speaker watches someone spiral deeper into self-destruction, their empathy depleted and control surrendered, until the only release left is euphoria found in giving up — 'creeping in like a leech to the blood' charts the slow takeover of whatever destroyed them.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~kill-the-sun":{"haiku":"Unshakeable grief and the contradiction between holding on and letting go. The speaker seeks to extinguish hope itself while unable to release the person they've lost.","opus":"Cane Hill strips down to a raw ballad of depression, where blaming oneself for nothing coexists with chasing one's own end and clinging to memories kept in a glass jar. The refrain sways between letting go and refusing to. Fittingly it titles their subdued acoustic-leaning EP, a marked departure from the group's heavier catalogue into quiet despair.","sonnet":"A person caught between knowing they should release an attachment and being wholly unable to let it go. Self-destruction and suicidal ideation flicker alongside the compulsion to hold on — the narrator has tried to extinguish what sustains him, and failed, and cannot decide if that failure is a mercy.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~lord-of-flies":{"haiku":"Demonic deception masquerades as love, leading the speaker into complicity with his own destruction; recognition comes too late to prevent the fall.","opus":"Recognizing your own worst impulses in a false idol you followed into ruin, chasing paradise in a devil's stare. A dark self-reckoning: the demon that fed you lies turns out to be a mirror, and the verdict falls on yourself.","sonnet":"A speaker realises they have been seduced by someone who embodies corruption — the lord of flies, a devil in disguise — and that in seeking heaven through this figure they have only guided their own destruction, yet remain defiant enough to declare they are worse than their betrayer.","src":"haiku"},
"cane-hill~power-of-the-high":{"opus":"Drug use dressed up as transcendence: the narrator seduces someone into getting high with them, framing intoxication as playing God, unlocking hidden power and cheating death, while the mind stays a cage.","sonnet":"An invitation to pursue drug-induced transcendence as a form of godhood — the narrator frames substance use as liberation from the constraints of consciousness, promising an experience of omnipotence and eternal life while acknowledging its proximity to death.","src":"sonnet"},
"cannibal-corpse~inhumane-harvest":{"haiku":"Organ trafficking depicts industrial predation on vulnerable populations; systematic murder transforms bodies into commodity through covert genocide.","opus":"Death metal's usual gore gets pointed at something real: black-market organ trafficking, bodies harvested and disappeared for profit. The horror isn't fantasy but the industrial coldness of people reduced to salable parts.","sonnet":"A clinical account of illegal organ trafficking — victims are controlled, surgically harvested, and disposed of, their disappearances covered up — framed as covert genocide for profit, with the perpetrators leaving no trace.","src":"haiku"},
"cantervice~artificial-minds":{"haiku":"Artificial consciousness replaces humanity as gods play designer, the outcome genocide disguised as progress. No resistance remains in a world where everyone thinks the same.","opus":"A metalcore alarm about AI as a seductive spark that becomes a plague on humanity, warning that a world of designed minds strips away feeling and quietly deploys its own extinction.","sonnet":"Humanity's obsession with artificial intelligence is framed as a self-inflicted plague: playing god without discretion, blurring benign from malicious, until everyone is redesigned with artificial minds and no resistance remains. The repeated question \"where is the resistance?\" goes unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"cantervice~blackout":{"haiku":"Consciousness disappears into void as the speaker loses all perception and control.","opus":"A vigilante fantasy dressed as a broadcast hijack, the band positioning itself as judge and jury for a sedated public, promising an uprising against a system that trades human dignity for profit and control.","sonnet":"A techno-insurgency narrative: a medicated, sedated public locked in a distorted reality gets targeted by an uprising that cuts the broadcast, judges in the dark, and demands the powerful feel the pain they've imposed. The manifesto insert mid-song names it directly — privacy eroded, human nature exploited — and the blackout is both punishment and liberation.","src":"haiku"},
"cantervice~doomsday":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic defiance and collective uprising reject submission, with revolution the only answer to manufactured control and societal collapse.","opus":"An invitation to a party at the end of the world, aimed at the disillusioned who've been made to feel alone by a system designed to control them. Burning it all down becomes liberation, rising above the ones who merely obey.","sonnet":"About a group that has opted out of obedience to a system they see as delusional — they're throwing a party for the end of the world, setting fire to the streets, rising above those who keep obeying; there's an anarchic glee underneath the apocalyptic imagery.","src":"haiku"},
"cantervice~enemy-at-the-gates":{"haiku":"The dispossessed rise to reclaim power from those who hid behind lies and greed, burning down the architecture of exploitation with collective rage.","opus":"A collective revolt against a ruler whose lies and lawbreaking have finally been exposed, a crowd that won't be bribed, cowed or talked down. Massed at the threshold, they vow to dictate the ending, watching his flimsy regime topple as his own power chokes him.","sonnet":"The exploited masses address a corrupt ruling class whose deception and greed have finally run out of road; the crowd at the gates refuses to be bought or bargained with and promises to burn the house of cards down, turning the question 'who's laughing now?' back on those who built it.","src":"haiku"},
"cantervice~into-the-dark":{"haiku":"System failure and personal disintegration, slipping into madness as humanity fades and games replace authenticity.","opus":"A descent into oblivion staged as spectacle, the narrator throwing his life away to become one of the anonymous many, sanity unraveling while he performs for an audience. Metalcore about self-erasure in a failing system.","sonnet":"About surrendering identity to conformity and losing yourself in a collapsing system — the narrator has been absorbed into a collective performance, their sanity declining as they go through motions they don't believe in. The repeated fall \"into the dark\" is both personal dissolution and societal collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"cantervice~point-of-no-return":{"haiku":"Ecological collapse approaches while humanity lingers in shadow, the moment passing when change still mattered. Spiraling into the sun arrives inevitable, orbit already undone.","opus":"Climate and civilizational collapse framed as an orbit coming undone, spinning into the sun; despair sits alongside a stubborn insistence that hope is something we still choose to make ourselves.","sonnet":"Ecological and civilisational collapse is described as an orbit coming undone, the world spinning into the sun, while the question is posed whether devastation will finally force the change humanity refused to make voluntarily. The song ends with a choice to make one's own hope.","src":"haiku"},
"cantervice~the-masquerade-zardonic-toronto-is-broken-remix":{"haiku":"Authoritarian control masks itself as loyalty while resistance becomes the only remaining freedom. The speaker escapes the prescribed prison through rebellion, joining others who've slipped through the system.","opus":"A summons to step through the rabbit hole into a world beyond death, where the humanity prescribed to us can be shed, framed as a masquerade of disobedience. Against a droning order to bow and comply, it celebrates shrugging off tyranny's weight by crossing over entirely.","sonnet":"A rallying cry for collective awakening against tyranny: those who went down the rabbit hole and came out the other side invite others to shed prescribed humanity, disobey the masquerade, and feel the weight of chains lift away.","src":"haiku"},
"cantervice~void":{"haiku":"Humanity is lost in a void, enslaved by systemic abuse and lies; the speaker urges escape from the darkness and surrender of one's soul to falsity.","opus":"This metalcore track turns its anger toward systems of control, watching a person surrender to a deception so flawlessly built they stop questioning it. The narrator names himself and all humanity as complicit, nourishing the same lie, yet argues that destiny isn't fixed and change is still within reach, urging people to claw free of the dark rather than give in.","sonnet":"Humanity surrenders its will to a system built on lies, and the narrator watches someone dear conform and disappear into it. The void is not emptiness but complicity — individuals feed the machine, give their souls away, and the only honest move is to claw back toward consciousness.","src":"haiku"},
"capsize~fading-face":{"haiku":"Temporary presence dissolves into haunting absence as the speaker recognizes they were always destined to become ghost in another's memory.","opus":"Coming to terms with being nothing more than a passing memory once a relationship has plainly ended, a brief phantom bound to be forgotten. The singer catches the unspoken signals, resolves to move forward, and grieves that the quiet afterward is so crushing precisely because nothing tender is left.","sonnet":"The aftermath of a long relationship's quiet end: the speaker wonders if they are now just a fading face in someone else's memory, a temporary ghost, while the silence left behind is deafening. Five years of putting on the same face and living someone else's life culminate in the question of whether they ever cared at all.","src":"haiku"},
"captives~falling-apart":{"haiku":"Disintegration accelerates despite medication and effort, the speaker splintering invisibly. They reach out but cannot save themselves, begging for chance that may never come.","opus":"An unflinching account of depression where medication fails to hold the colors, the self erases itself from its own picture, and the plea is simply for permission to feel this way and one more chance.","sonnet":"A speaker managing mental illness — colours fading despite medication, forgetting their own name, a picture that has blurred them into the background — insists they are not giving up even as everything inside is falling apart, and reaches a hand out for one chance to be saved. The image of cutting oneself out of the frame captures dissociation with precision.","src":"haiku"},
"capture~no-cure":{"haiku":"The song confronts spiritual hopelessness and self-deception, arguing that self-reliance without external salvation is the only path forward despite life's futility.","opus":"A metalcore rejection of borrowed faith, insisting salvation comes only from the self since gods and inherited beliefs abandon you. The bleak refrain frames self-reliance as the only truth worth dying with.","sonnet":"A blunt rejection of religious faith as escapism — God is a ghost you gave your soul to, there's no cure in prayer, and the only honest position is to believe in yourself because no one else is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"car-bomb~dissect-yourself":{"opus":"A claustrophobic meditation on radical self-examination, in which the mind is urged to cut itself open, observe its own unconscious mechanical workings, and become a perfect self-made prison.","sonnet":"A brutal meditation on radical self-examination as both pain and revelation: stripping away every layer of skin and identity until the unconscious machinery of the self is exposed, achieving a kind of terrifying, pure awareness.","src":"sonnet"},
"car-bomb~scattered-sprites":{"haiku":"A manipulative force programs people's minds to destroy competing perspectives, corrupting both sides toward eternal internal flames.","opus":"A cold, ritual vision of engineered division, some controlling force programming roles, setting opposing sides against each other, corrupting and sacrificing minds until they burn and are reborn through nine circles of trial. The imagery of manufactured conflict and enslaved perception drives it. Car Bomb are a New York mathcore band famed for dizzying, mechanical dissonance, and this fractured, dehumanized theme matches their disorienting sound.","sonnet":"A cold procedural of manufactured division — an unnamed authority programs perception, separates populations into opposing camps, and watches them erase each other without ever identifying the hand pulling the levers. The nine circles and rebirth at the end suggest the whole cycle is less apocalypse than a recurring system: destroy, ascend through ash, begin again.","src":"haiku"},
"caravan-palace~81-special":{"haiku":"Competitive bravado and disposability mask a fleeting paradise; pleasure cycles through and discards before meaning can settle.","opus":"The French electro-swing act channels a strutting jump-blues bravado, all seize-the-moment showmanship and spend-it-while-it's-in-style swagger, more groove and attitude than message.","sonnet":"A bravado-charged voice claims the top spot in town, urging everyone to give it all away while the moment still counts, riding the communal energy of a parade or spectacle — the chorus of \"givin' green\" keeps the gesture loose and generous rather than competitive.","src":"haiku"},
"caravan-palace~city-cook":{"haiku":"Escape through reinvention: service transforms into liberation when the servant finally walks away from the table and owns their own motion.","opus":"A kitchen worker walking out on abusive, tyrannical bosses and stepping into the free, joyful side of life. The refrain wonders whether anyone will ever understand what that escape meant to her.","sonnet":"A song about a kitchen worker who has endured the abuse of tyrannical male bosses and is finally done — she has her pride, she did her time scrubbing floors and faking smiles, and she is leaving for a freer life where no one gets to talk to her that way again.","src":"haiku"},
"caravan-palace~lone-digger":{"haiku":"An invitation to abandon restraint and dance, move, and immerse oneself in rhythm and physical release in the moment.","opus":"A night-out summons, coaxing both a brother and a mama to quit hesitating and give in to the beat. It insists the body surrender to the groove and that the overthinking mind holds no authority to refuse while the party stays alive.","sonnet":"An invitation to stop holding back and give in to the pull of dancing and physical pleasure, directed at both a man and a woman who are reluctant to let go — the lyrics push them to forget restraint, move to the rhythm, and embrace the heat of the night.","src":"haiku"},
"caravan-palace~mirrors":{"haiku":"The speaker resents being perpetually overshadowed and blamed while a more celebrated counterpart moves through the world effortlessly blame-free, the frustration tipping between jealousy and a genuine grievance about invisibility.","opus":"A wry portrait of the invisible sidekick who does the real work while all attention falls on the flashier figure, resentment curdling under the forced cheer of a role that never earns its own spotlight.","sonnet":"A sidekick watches all the glory fall on someone else — the crowd erupts for them, the news covers them, and when the narrator tries to speak out they get shut down. Resentment and jealousy coil together under the surface: you're blame-free, you're fault-free, while I remain invisible, the hero's sidekick.","src":"haiku"},
"caravan-palace~moonshine":{"haiku":"A person trapped in confinement searches for companionship and light while facing the paralysis of unfinished living and blocked departure.","opus":"The French electro-swing group set restless longing to a bouncing beat, its narrator shut away and cut off, wondering what opportunity slipped past and endlessly seeking a person and a warmth just out of reach. Stranded in a kind of confinement, they debate leaving, unable to abandon certain attachments while still straining toward contentment.","sonnet":"From within a place of confinement or stagnation, someone searches for connection and sunlight that keep receding. The longing is quiet and repetitive, circling the question of what lies ahead for someone who cannot quite bring themselves to go find out.","src":"haiku"},
"caravan-palace~rock-it-for-me":{"haiku":"Young men's brawling and aggression toward women is mocked as childish posturing; the song warns against trusting their false toughness.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek jab at swaggering tough guys who fight, boast and snort their way through a scene, treated as pathetic rather than fearsome. Caravan Palace, the French outfit fusing 1920s jazz with dance beats, wrap the ridicule in a retro-flavored bounce, the motherly warning and the parade of posturing hard men turning macho display into a joke you can dance to.","sonnet":"A satirical portrait of brawling, leather-jacketed male braggadocio whose violence and posturing is performed for an audience of unimpressed women. The whole pantomime is declared wrong-headed with a breezy detachment that refuses to be charmed by any of it.","src":"haiku"},
"caravan-palace~wonderland":{"haiku":"A person fantasizes about power and dominance in an imaginary wonderland, but admits their dreams are escapist delusion.","opus":"The swagger of a gangster fantasy revealed, at the turn, to be the daydream of an ordinary girl who only rules anything inside her own head. The escapism is the point, and so is the small ache of knowing it isn't real.","sonnet":"A shy, gentle girl's fantasy of being a swaggering street-tough — gold chains, weapons, throwing kicks in the club — which she fully acknowledges at the end is just a daydream, the distance between who she is and who she imagines herself being.","src":"haiku","fable":"A storybook invitation opens the door onto a sugared paradise, and then a woman's voice barges through it spitting a hard-boiled crime boast — the drawn weapon, the flaunted jewelry, the threat to leave some rival broken. The bridge detonates all of it retroactively: she admits none of that ever happened, that she is an ordinary soul whose life is mild, and that this whole reign belongs to her only when she sleeps. What made the swagger so convincing was rhythm, and the mechanism shows its hand when the taunting refrain stutters, jams, and finally wears down to a chanted pulse that carries momentum but no menace at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["artifice & performance","freedom & escape","money & the street"],"means":"a crime-queen swagger that belongs entirely to a sleeping self","built":"the taunting refrain stutters and jams until momentum drains to a chant with no menace"},
"carcass~316l-grade-surgical-steel":{"haiku":"Love is depicted as cold, heartless, and militaristic—a metallic hardness that cuts and betrays. The song equates romance with surgical precision and emotional destruction.","opus":"Through frosty surgical and cardiac language, the song rejects the reality of genuine devotion and shrinks it to shifting shades of desire, distrust and mutual damage. The heart becomes a forged lump of metal, and the singer sneers at anyone who kids themselves with talk of tenderness.","sonnet":"The song declares a cold rejection of love and romantic vulnerability, arguing that love is nothing more than varying degrees of lust and that opening your heart only leads to pain. The surgical steel of the title is the hardened, emotion-proof state the speaker has chosen.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~arbeit-macht-fleisch":{"haiku":"Industrial machinery and forced labor grind workers to raw material in an endless cycle; bodies become interchangeable parts sacrificed to systems of power ('work makes flesh').","opus":"Carcass turn the gears of industrial labor into a literal meat grinder, workers rendered into raw material for their masters. Twisting the gate slogan of the death camps, it damns any system that consumes human beings as fuel.","sonnet":"Industrial machinery is used as a sustained metaphor for the grinding exploitation of labour: workers are reduced to raw material — flesh pulped, consumed, and discarded — with the Nazi slogan \"Arbeit macht frei\" twisted into \"Arbeit macht Fleisch\" (work makes flesh/meat).","src":"haiku","fable":"Jeff Walker builds the whole song to detonate one substitution: he takes the Auschwitz gate motto, work sets you free, and changes only its last word so it confesses that the process makes meat instead. The grammar and cadence hold, but the swap reveals that this apparatus was never selling liberation, only the reduction of bodies to feedstock. Everything before it is the gore Carcass built its name on, but Walker deliberately stages the butchery as employment, folding Heartwork-era body-horror into an argument about capital grinding the worker down to fuel. His dense mock-clinical diction — prognathous, levigate, suzerain — is a mask, the flat tone of a technician logging throughput, and the terror beneath it is that this register cannot tell a killing-floor from a paycheck. So the refrain lands twice with a single word turned over, frei then fleisch, and the second chorus reads as an honest correction of the first: not the promise of freedom but its true output, the human debt owed to a master.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder"],"means":"the gate motto corrected by one word, swapping liberation for its actual output","built":"mock-clinical diction holds the tone of a technician logging throughput while narrating a killing floor"},
"carcass~black-star":{"haiku":"A dead celestial body shines as a guide in eternal darkness. This black star represents a sunless, cruel beacon burning in mourning.","opus":"A death metal ode to a paradoxical dark sun, a dead star that guides the narrator, radiance and mourning fused into one; the nursery-rhyme twist makes the worship of darkness feel both childlike and infernal.","sonnet":"A dark star — the opposite of the sun, burning without light, dead and cold — is addressed as a guide and companion, a celestial body whose darkness mirrors mourning and becomes a kind of comforting anti-beacon.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~blind-bleeding-the-blind":{"haiku":"The blind lead the blind in cycles of suffering and poverty, with the wealthy indifferent to misery while the poor remain trapped. Bloody hands never clean themselves and perpetual decay defines the social order.","opus":"Beneath the gore-drenched language, a savage critique of inequality, the well-fed indifferent while the poor bleed, guilt that never washes clean. Carcass weaponizes Lady Macbeth's stain to indict the economics of pain.","sonnet":"A class-warfare indictment — the privileged, born with silver spoons, have built their wealth on the bones of the subjugated, and their comfort is literally financed by others' blood and tears; the title's echoing of the biblical proverb underlines how those in power have no compassion and no intention of developing any.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~blood-slpattered-banner":{"haiku":"Historical atrocity and bigotry animate symbols of pride; the speaker condemns the nation state built on slavery and stolen land, summoning fire to consume its legacy.","opus":"A savage indictment of the Confederate flag as a gore-soaked emblem of slavery, bigotry and seized land dressed up as heritage. Carcass strips away the defiant pride and simply wants to watch it burn, promising to grind it underfoot.","sonnet":"A furious condemnation of American white nationalism and Confederate nostalgia: the blood-spattered banner flies over a raped, stolen land stained by slavery, and the speaker wants to see it put to the torch. The refrain \"these colours don't run — but put to the torch they'll sure as hell burn\" turns the flag's boast into a threat.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~buried-dreams":{"haiku":"Aspirations, hope, and self-esteem collapse under life's weight, leaving only hate as the fuel to survive. Buried dreams inspire resentment and the need for an enemy.","opus":"Melodic death metal on how blocked ambition sours into loathing. Once every hope is choked off and self-regard has drained away, spite is the sole riches remaining, and needing a target for one's venom turns into a bleak survival mechanism.","sonnet":"A bleak declaration that crushed ambition and lost self-esteem leave only hatred behind — not as moral condemnation but as a survivalist observation that in a world of suffocated hopes, hate becomes the only emotional resource left.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~captive-bolt-pistol":{"haiku":"A pneumatic killing instrument pierces skulls with premeditated precision; the song celebrates this method of culling with technical, clinical detail.","opus":"A coldly clinical, forensic account of the stunning device used in slaughterhouses, laid out in dry technical language and even crediting figures tied to its history and to humane-killing design. The detachment is the point, turning the machinery of industrial killing into detailed horror.","sonnet":"A clinical, almost procedural description of a captive bolt pistol — the slaughterhouse tool used to stun and kill livestock — rendered in technical detail and cast as a mechanism of mass murder applied to the human herd.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~carnal-forge":{"haiku":"Mass slaughter and industrial dehumanization turn humans into cannon fodder, sanitizing and sanctifying genocide through factory-like killing. Bodies pile up in mass graves as murder becomes normalized.","opus":"A thesaurus-drunk splatter of war imagery, Carcass piling up ornate medical and martial vocabulary to render mass death as an industrial process, the baroque language itself the joke and the horror of desensitized slaughter.","sonnet":"About industrialized warfare and the dehumanization of soldiers into cannon fodder — mass graves, body bags, killing sanitized and slaughter sanctified, genocide normalized through distance and bureaucracy. The extreme vocabulary mirrors the obscenity of the subject.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~child-s-play":{"haiku":"Urban decay as childhood playground, normalizing cruelty and barrenness as the conditions of play and growth.","opus":"Grindcore turned social realism: children raised amid urban decay and concrete rot, their fate sealed by the collapsing environment around them. The playground is a slum, and the city becomes the tomb.","sonnet":"About urban decay as the only world children in impoverished industrial environments are given to grow up in — the razor wire and crumbling concrete are presented as the umbilical cord and womb of a generation. The song is brutal sociology: your city becomes your tomb, and they call this ordinary.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~childs-play":{"haiku":"Children raised in urban decay and crumbling infrastructure learn violence as play in a desolate concrete playground; their cities become tombs.","opus":"A bleak indictment of children raised amid urban decay, nurtured in concrete cribs and playgrounds of grey where their fate is sealed. The Beatles-echoing invitation to be taken down and the cities becoming tombs frame poverty and dereliction as a rough game with no winners.","sonnet":"Urban poverty as a death sentence — growing up in crumbling concrete slums with razor-wire umbilical cords is framed as a rough game that predetermines lives; the cities will become tombs for those born into decay.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~cross-my-heart":{"haiku":"Love and hate exist on a razor's edge where beauty masks depth and breaking is tempting, with vows constantly tested.","opus":"Reformed gore-grind pioneers Carcass twist a love song into a morgue-tinged threat, blurring adoration and butchery into one grisly declaration of devotion. Romance recast as a mutual pact of destruction that can only lead to grief.","sonnet":"A toxic love declared with clinical distance — beauty is only skin deep so blindness is a mercy, and the line between love and hate is so thin the narrator wants to tear it apart rather than pretend it was ever clear.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~death-certificate":{"haiku":"Death reduced to data and administrative record, stripped of meaning and memory in institutional systems.","opus":"A grim reflection on death flattened into paperwork, a human life catalogued, dissected and stored as raw information. What remains of a person, the song argues, is nothing but a filed statistic and an official record that they existed and stopped.","sonnet":"A person reduced to data after death: cross-indexed, machine-coded, digitised into a statistical obituary — the song strips away any notion of legacy or meaning, leaving only a certificate and a database entry as the final trace of a life.","src":"haiku","fable":"Carcass made their name splicing the vocabulary of the mortuary and the pathology lab onto grindcore; here they turn that same clinical diction on the paperwork of dying, so a person exits not as a corpse but as a filed entry, tagged, sorted, and stored on a punch-card. The vocabulary itself is the point: every coinage is filing-cabinet jargon, the words of clerks and mainframes rather than mourners, and the coldness lands precisely because a whole life gets logged like warehouse inventory, its only surviving trace a number in some mortality table. Against that machinery sits a single rotting word for flesh wasting away, wedged among all the cataloguing, so the body decays in one ledger while its record keeps circulating in another. The chorus hammers the same cross-referencing over and over, enacting the reduction it names, a human end processed until nothing is left but the form that signs it off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","surveillance & control"],"means":"a life exits as a filed entry sorted like warehouse inventory","built":"the chorus hammers cross-referencing until it enacts the reduction it names"},
"carcass~doctrinal-expletives":{"haiku":"The speaker examines how words and silence distort meaning, creating ambiguity and misunderstanding while people drown in others' shallow rhetoric.","opus":"Jeff Walker turns his verbal knife on language itself, on how silence and scripted, ambiguous words are weaponized to mislead. Meaning dies in the gap between what's said and understood, and repeating hollow words drowns you.","sonnet":"A meditation on the gap between language and meaning — silence has its own vocabulary, words choke rather than communicate, and regurgitating others' ideas leaves you drowning in their shallowness rather than your own understanding.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~don-t-believe-a-word":{"haiku":"Manipulation through distortion, fabrication, and strategic half-truths. Those in power wield the pen to erase memory and reshape reality while the masses swallow lies whole.","opus":"A blistering attack on the press and its distortions, arguing that real influence comes from whoever controls the written word and a gullible audience will accept any fabrication. The Liverpool grind veterans twist a courtroom oath into a warning that ink can rewrite the truth without ever spilling blood.","sonnet":"Every written or spoken authority is suspect: facts and fantasy merge, quotations are stripped of context, and the solemn promise to tell the truth is itself revealed as the most reliable lie. The pen becomes the real weapon, able to erase living memory more completely than any blade.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~edge-of-darkness":{"opus":"From the Liverpool pioneers of goregrind, this later cut trades pathology textbooks for civilisational dread, turning the band's clinical vocabulary toward history itself. An aeon closes, illumination snuffed and horizons ablaze, the Orwellian boot pressing forever on the human condition. The blankness of a brave new world becomes the truest horror: extinction not of bodies but of memory and light.","sonnet":"A civilisation at the end of an aeon stands on the edge of a new dark age: illumination snuffed, horizons burning, history condemned to repeat itself, a brave new world of blankness replacing the old one. The boot treading perpetually on the human condition is an Orwellian image of permanent oppression.","src":"sonnet"},
"carcass~edge-of-darkness-live":{"haiku":"Dark age descends as sun sets on human condition, boot perpetually treading. History mirrors itself, learning nothing, time marching into bleak repetition.","opus":"A bleak vision of civilization sliding into a fresh age of darkness, the human condition dimming to a permanent dusk as history condemns us to repeat its worst. Carcass, British extreme-metal pioneers who moved from gore toward grander apocalyptic dread in their melodic-death phase, render the twilight of an old, not-so-brave world with grim eloquence.","sonnet":"Civilizational decline observed from the threshold — the sun setting on an era, a new dark age beginning, and humanity standing at the edge of that darkness unable or unwilling to turn back. The song is a historical elegy, cycling through images of extinction and dusk, insisting that what is ending was never quite as brave as it believed itself to be.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~embodiment":{"haiku":"Worshippers kneel before constructed religious idols embodying flesh and guilt, urged to free themselves through self-authored divinity.","opus":"A dense anti-religious screed against self-suppression, indicting worshippers who nail themselves to false idols of their own making. Its verdict, echoing Nietzsche, is that the god you scribed by hand is already dead.","sonnet":"A rejection of religious self-suppression — the narrator refuses to kneel before an effigy of suffering and guilt, calling on people to rise from the ground and write their own gods rather than bowing to a corrupt spiritual hierarchy.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~firm-hand":{"haiku":"An authoritarian force applies violent selective elimination and eugenicide under ideology, wielding hate as law and cleansing perceived deviancy through control.","sonnet":"Framed as bureaucratic cleansing, the song traces the logic of eugenicist ideology to its violent endpoint — unnatural selection applied with institutional authority, deviancy defined and eliminated by those who hold power. The horror is in the clinical language: harvesting grids, viral containment, a final solution dressed as management.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~generation-hexed":{"haiku":"An entire generation is trapped in meaninglessness, anonymous and manipulated like sheep, born into conformity and sedation with no choice but to perpetuate the cycle.","opus":"A savage lament for a sedated, conformist generation stripped of individuality, herded by fashion and born already resigned to its knees. The death-metal fury lands as genuine disgust at manufactured, interchangeable identity.","sonnet":"A contemptuous portrait of a generation born into conformity — blank, anonymous, fashion-dictated, sold wholesale identities from birth, already dead before they've lived — condemned to repeat the pattern.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~go-to-hell":{"haiku":"Deliberate rejection of salvation and morality, choosing hedonistic sin instead. The speaker sells their soul knowingly, preferring hell's heat to heaven's constraints.","opus":"A cheerful rejection of a boring afterlife in favor of eternal damnation, treating the fires below as a fitting reward for a life of indulgence rather than a punishment. Carcass, whose dark comic wordplay defined their melodic-death turn, riff on the famous line about other people being hell to argue, with a wink, that the underworld sounds far more appealing than paradise.","sonnet":"A cheerful rejection of paradise in favour of hell on the grounds that the sins of the flesh are more appealing than any heavenly reward, and that other people are already someone's private hell anyway. The soul is sold with a shrug and damnation reframed as honest pleasure.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~heartwork":{"haiku":"Dark abstract art and bleeding words emerge from corrupted perceptions; the song describes shattered beauty expressed through degeneration and murky palettes.","opus":"A self-aware reflection on making dark, provocative art from pain, a black canvas painted to degenerate and denigrate. The wordplay on heart-work versus heartwork frames aesthetic beauty and sensory corruption as two faces of the same bleeding creative act.","sonnet":"A meditation on art as corruption and beauty simultaneously — the canvas keeps getting painted darker, abstraction chokes, and the \"heartwork\" being created is both profound aesthetic statement and ugly degeneration.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~i-told-you-so-corporate-rock-really-does-suck":{"haiku":"Compromise and conformity corrupt authenticity as the speaker confronts commercialization and demands liberation from the pressure to please and perform.","opus":"A two-part jab at the music business, first parroting the groveling request to be instructed in how to think, perform and conduct oneself in order to blend in with the pack, then snapping into blunt defiance. The final barb lands on those who cashed in and took their share, granting that the industry is exactly as rotten as it appears.","sonnet":"A song about selling out and reclaiming autonomy, structured as a before-and-after: in the first half the speaker sarcastically begs to be told what to think, sing, and be; in the second they flatly refuse, stating \"it's myself I'm trying to please.\" The repeated \"really done it now, sold out\" refuses to let the self-disgust be papered over.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~in-god-we-trust":{"haiku":"Apocalypse accelerates as extraction and exploitation deplete the world; faith becomes complicity and only action born from climate reckoning remains viable.","opus":"An apocalyptic screed against presidential greed and fossil-fuel plunder, where a natural resource stripped on an industrial scale drives biblical-scale ruin. The refrain twists the American motto so that trust in God masks a lust for power that leaves the Earth dying screaming.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic indictment of industrial civilisation and political power: the earth dies screaming under the weight of hydrocarbon extraction, presidential lust, and biblical-scale destruction, with \"in God we trust\" deployed as bitter irony against the decadence of a civilisation tapping natural resources to extinction. Only blood and ashes remain.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~keep-on-rotting-in-the-free-world":{"haiku":"Commentary on western decline, consumer culture, and the false hope offered to the masses. People decay in comfort, trapped in economic systems they cannot escape.","opus":"The grindcore pioneers skewer Western consumer capitalism, its Neil Young-echoing title turning freedom into the freedom to rot while sweatshops abroad supply cheap salvation. The message: your dream of a better day is the sedative that keeps you decaying in place.","sonnet":"A sardonic commentary on the hollowness of Western freedom, where citizens are encouraged to consume and dream while the real conditions of their lives — economic exploitation, sweatshop labor, and systemic despair — remain unchanged beneath the surface.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~no-love-lost":{"haiku":"Romance stripped of authentic emotion, where hearts are played as instruments and severed. Love is a tragic, emotionless performance.","opus":"A clinically detached, disillusioned view of romance as a doomed, feelingless transaction, the heart treated as an instrument to be played and then cut loose. Human frailty is easy to exploit, and once the whole affair concludes, nothing of value was ever actually there.","sonnet":"A cold dissection of romantic feeling as cheap and easily manipulated — heartstrings strummed and severed, love romanticized into a synthetic tragedy. The central refrain declares that when it is over, nothing of value was really there to lose.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~polarized":{"haiku":"A speaker rejects all tribal belonging, refusing to adopt colors, beliefs, fashions, cults, or faith regardless of external pressure or social labels.","sonnet":"A systematic rejection of every external claim on identity — name, colour, politics, faith, fashion, cult — refuses tribalism in all its forms. The speaker claims no allegiance and wants none imposed. The polarization of the title is not the speaker's flaw but the world's, and the response is total disengagement from what others care about.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~r-k-the-vote":{"haiku":"Institutional corruption and voter manipulation rendering democracy hollow spectacle, the masses herded into complicity through false choice.","opus":"The Liverpool grind veterans turn political, dismissing electoral democracy as a comfortable illusion that changes nothing, the ballot a leash dressed up as a voice while the same establishment keeps winning.","sonnet":"Electoral cynicism as total system capture — the same old faces make their beds elsewhere, cross your X as your only voice, stay in line and buy what you're sold. The closing question — 'is this rock'n'roll or a form of state control?' — implicates the song itself in the spectacle it critiques.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~reek-of-putrefaction":{"haiku":"Cadaverous stench becomes addictive; putrefaction intoxicates the senses as death's aroma provides obscene pleasure.","opus":"Early Carcass turn the medical dictionary into an assault, cataloguing decay with clinical relish until the language itself becomes the point. Beneath the splatter, a grim joke about the body as pure rotting matter, and about addiction to disgust.","sonnet":"A first-person account of becoming addicted to the smell and spectacle of corpse decomposition, treating the act of inhaling the stench of rot as a hallucinogenic, ecstatic ritual.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~room-101":{"haiku":"Civilization marches toward extinction; the speaker sees doom as inevitable and refuses false hope or religious salvation from inevitable collapse.","opus":"A death-metal band famous for gore turns to Orwell instead, using the torture room as a bunker for total nihilism: no messiah, no future worth heralding, only a private apocalypse already lived. Redemption is a lie fools keep waiting on while the mind counts its scars.","sonnet":"A pessimistic rejection of all religious and political salvation — the narrator sees the future written in mental scars, has no faith in anything held dear, and retreats to a private \"room 101\" of his own, insisting that the only future belongs to sweat and tears, not any second coming.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~rot-n-roll":{"haiku":"A band's decline into creative stagnation, repeating the same formula while rotting from within. Success transforms them into a parody of themselves, endlessly cycling the same song to indifferent audiences.","opus":"A self-deprecating take on fame that asks whether success actually smells like triumph or like decay, the band shifting styles while suspecting they keep churning out the same thing. Carcass pun on their own name as a rotting body past its prime, weighing pity against praise and turning anxieties about selling out into a wry catalogue of their imagined decline.","sonnet":"A band or artist who has changed surface appearance while playing the same tired tune is called out for mistaking cosmetic reinvention for genuine transformation. The stench of commercial decay hangs over a carcass that has outlasted its sell-by date, rotting to its own soundtrack.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~rot-n-roll-live":{"haiku":"Decay spreads through success itself, the chameleon changing shade while song remains unchanged. Opportunity reeks, talent wasted on hollow repetition.","opus":"The Liverpool grindcore veterans aim their gore-soaked vocabulary at the music world, ridiculing the odor of triumph and reinvention. The recurring image is a lizard shifting its hue while cranking out the identical tune, decay repackaged as fresh vitality.","sonnet":"A chameleon that changed its shade but plays the same song to the same tune is the target — a band or scene that sold out, whose foul success is the smell of rot and whose comeuppance this time precedes any pride. The chorus invites everyone to rot together.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~ruptured-in-purulence":{"haiku":"Bodily decomposition unfolds in grotesque detail—bacterial and parasitic consumption strips flesh from bone.","opus":"A relentless catalogue of the body dissolving into itself, decomposition rendered in borrowed pathology jargon. The band's medical-student backgrounds turn splatter into a strangely rigorous meditation on flesh as chemistry, without meaning, only process.","sonnet":"A clinical-grotesque cataloguing of bodily decomposition from the inside out — organs liquefying, larvae feeding, gases accumulating — rendered in dense medical vocabulary as a portrait of the body consuming itself.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~the-granulating-dark-satanic-mills":{"haiku":"Industrial capitalism transforms human bodies into disposable fuel; subjugation hides beneath the mythology of progress.","opus":"A departure from gore into industrial-era class rage, Blake's phrase turned against the factory that grinds workers to bone. The bitter twist on Lennon's working-class hero seals it: subservience passed down as inheritance, redemption never coming.","sonnet":"Industrial capitalism is recast as a continuous system of enslavement that replaced visible chains with invisible ones, condemning generations of workers from birth to death inside dehumanising factories whose legacy will outlast any individual.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~the-master-butcher-s-apron":{"haiku":"The historical atrocities of imperialism and exploitation are remembered plainly, marked by the ongoing violence of those who serve tyrannical power.","opus":"A dense, furious takedown of empire cast in slaughterhouse terms, framing imperial conquest as ceaseless butchery, plunder, and cruelty dressed up as noble sacrifice, with a bitter salute to those who once patrolled against the slave trade. The Liverpool gore-grind pioneers, turned melodic, show their sharper political teeth here.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of empire built on blood — raising a glass to the West African squadron is bitter irony, honoring those conscripted into the machinery of colonialism while naming the cruciate and saltire that sanctified it. The sun never setting becomes a curse rather than a boast: the blood never dries because the butchers never stopped whetting their knives.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~this-is-your-life":{"haiku":"Life's meaning reduced to biological cycle—birth, sex, and death comprise human existence, nothing more.","opus":"The British extreme-metal pioneers boil existence down to a clinical cycle of being born, mating, and dying, weaving genetics and disease into a grim reflection on biological decline. By repeatedly asking whether that trio really is all life amounts to, the song reduces human experience to raw mechanics fated from the moment reproduction begins.","sonnet":"Biological existence reduced to its three blunt coordinates: birth, sex, death. The song frames the entire arc of life as a genetic loop without meaning, asking whether reproduction and mortality are all the human animal was ever encoded to do.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~this-mortal-coil":{"haiku":"Humanity tears down civilization, unleashing terror and creating new hatred, revealing that all flesh is tangled together in mortal equality.","opus":"Post-Cold-War unease rendered in Carcass's dense, clinical language, framing the new global order not as freedom but as a rearranged version of the same old dread. Death, the song insists, levels everyone alike, all mortals bound in the same inescapable knot.","sonnet":"A bleak vision of humanity's shared fate: old walls come down only to reveal new global disorder, and all flesh — regardless of ideology — ends up tangled together in the same mortal coil of hatred and death.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~thrasher-s-abattoir":{"haiku":"Slaughter becomes art as flesh becomes food for the machine. The abattoir transforms bodies into product.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek genre manifesto, the goregrind pioneers piling clinical dismemberment vocabulary onto scorn for posers, the slaughterhouse imagery aimed squarely at scene pretenders.","sonnet":"A sustained assault of gore-vocabulary aimed at \"hipsters and posers\" — every -ation suffix in the butcher's lexicon deployed as weaponised contempt, the abattoir being the venue where all pretenders meet their extermination.","src":"haiku"},
"carcass~tomorrow-belongs-to-nobody":{"haiku":"False ideologies and kingdoms crumble into illusion; tomorrow never arrives, so all wait in vain for a dawn that never breaks.","opus":"A withering dismissal of people who cling to a hollow ideology and await a promised new era that will never dawn. Their supposed power is a fantasy and they are merely expendable pieces, taunted with the verdict that the future belongs to no one at all.","sonnet":"A dismissal of all utopian and revolutionary ideologies as self-delusion — the promised new dawn never comes, tomorrow belongs to nobody, and those waiting for it are pawns of the same old order dressed up in new language.","src":"haiku"},
"careful-gaze~silken-robe":{"haiku":"Screaming into isolation generates no response, the speaker haunted by fear of pulling the floor. Intimacy demands presence they fear they cannot maintain.","opus":"A raw depiction of suicidal exhaustion and the fear of failing everyone, addressed to someone who called the singer home; the silken robe is death personified, and the terror is choosing which parts of yourself die.","sonnet":"On the edge of suicidal exhaustion — the silken robe dark as a starless night, sleep used as shelter rather than rest — the speaker apologises to someone they've been a home for, fearing they've stopped giving that and cannot spread hope when they have none left. The image of sitting reclined in the driver's seat in the rain, unable to shed either the bad or the good in themselves, is the emotional core.","src":"haiku"},
"caribou~silver":{"haiku":"Tortured by memories of someone moving on to someone else; regret without closure.","opus":"A jealous, self-fooling heartbreak in which the singer swears he's past his ex while fixating on the man who took his place. His boast that he can turn everything precious reads as flimsy bravado papered over genuine grief.","sonnet":"A speaker tormented by jealousy watches the woman he loves spend time with another man — he imagines her dreaming of the rival, questions why she changed just as he was coming around, and hovers between resignation and longing.","src":"haiku"},
"carly-rae-jepsen~call-me-maybe":{"haiku":"Impulsive connection blooms from chance encounter; romantic inevitability overrides hesitation.","opus":"The pop-perfect distillation of instant infatuation, the giddy leap of handing your number to a stranger before you can think better of it. Its genius is admitting how absurd and unearned the feeling is while surrendering to it completely.","sonnet":"An impulsive, giddy infatuation with a stranger encountered by chance — the speaker barely knows him but the pull is immediate and total, and she offers her number knowing it might come to nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"carnivore~jesus-hitler":{"haiku":"A hybrid abomination conflates religious salvation with fascist destruction, leaving ambiguity whether the result brings redemption or annihilation.","opus":"Peter Steele's deliberately offensive thought experiment, a savior born half messiah and half monster, torn between salvation and annihilation. The shock is the vehicle for a bleaker point about reincarnated evil and the thin line between redeemer and destroyer.","sonnet":"A grotesque concept piece about a figure born from a Nazi-raped nun who embodies both Christ and Hitler, torn between salvation and genocide — a satirical provocation about the proximity of religious and fascist authority.","src":"haiku"},
"caroline-polachek~billions":{"opus":"Polachek spins abundance itself into an erotic, sensory reverie, cataloguing riches of texture and feeling that overflow the cup. Beneath the pearl-and-oyster imagery runs a yearning for genuine closeness, the plea to be told something real. It is about excess and intimacy at once, sensuous plenty shadowed by the wish to actually be reached.","sonnet":"A portrait of a complex, morally ambiguous man — brilliant and deceptive, capable of deep intimacy one moment and financial manipulation the next — through whom the narrator feels an almost overwhelming closeness she cannot fully explain.","src":"sonnet"},
"caro~heart-in-2":{"haiku":"Emotional entanglement wars with the need for autonomy; the speaker cannot surrender to love without shattering themselves, caught between presence and flight.","opus":"The push and pull of an affair that can't be given a whole night, held close but never allowed to become surrender. She wants to be missed without losing herself, guarding her heart against the breakage that intimacy threatens.","sonnet":"A speaker in an ambivalent attachment warns against full surrender — \"can't lose myself in your arms, don't break my heart in two\" — acknowledging the other person's longing when they're gone but refusing to stay overnight, keeping a distance that protects at the cost of intimacy.","src":"haiku"},
"carpenter-brut~anarchy-road":{"haiku":"A dystopian future portrait of burning tenements, spy surveillance, wrecked cars, and soulless creatures consumed by fear and fire.","opus":"French darksynth producer Carpenter Brut sketches a ruined future city around 2070, a lawless, godless wasteland of ash and fire where hollow, dehumanized survivors roam among wreckage and violence. Suited to his horror-movie synthwave, the track works as a cinematic tour through neon-lit collapse, all grimy menace and apocalyptic dread with no redemption in sight.","sonnet":"A dystopian cityscape set roughly half a century forward — tenements burning through endless nights, charcoal rain, knife fights behind every spy hole, and pagans praying at the edges of collapse. The tone is observational rather than anguished, describing ruin with the detached clarity of someone who has accepted this as the only world left.","src":"haiku"},
"carpenter-brut~escape-from-midwich-valley":{"haiku":"Intellectual education masks fundamental developmental damage as superficial knowledge cannot heal deeper psychological dysfunction.","opus":"A mocking, chant-like verdict condemning a childishly ignorant man as guilty despite his years of schooling.","sonnet":"A short, sardonic character study of a man found guilty by consensus — child-like in his knowledge despite four years of college — the repetition of the verdict functioning as a chant that substitutes group agreement for evidence.","src":"haiku"},
"carpenter-brut~imaginary-fire-feat-greg-puciato":{"opus":"Greg Puciato voices a lover refusing to be dragged back into a partner's guilt and self-isolation, insisting nothing comes for free and that loving someone lost in their own loneliness takes real resolve. The imaginary fire is a self-made torment both should try to leave behind. Carpenter Brut set the standoff against pulsing synthwave.","sonnet":"Two people locked in a pattern of mutual guilt and pushing away are urged to stop defaulting to victimhood and blame, and to let go of the imaginary drama they've constructed around the relationship — the fire keeping them apart is a fiction they can choose to drop.","src":"sonnet"},
"carpenter-brut~lipstick-masquerade-feat-persha":{"haiku":"Internal voices grow loud and demanding, pushing the narrator to break free from the cage they have been in. Things will never be the same as they take on an unknown other.","opus":"The French synthwave producer, all horror-film dread, scores a survivor turning on a manipulator. After years of contorting into whatever this person demanded, the narrator's own inner voice grows too loud to ignore and hardens into resolve. Breaking free of a long captivity, they warn that the balance of power has shifted for good.","sonnet":"Behind a composed exterior, a woman has been fighting internal voices that kept her caged — but they have shifted from accusers into fuel, building her toward confrontation. The masquerade of being fine collapses as she decides the dangerous game of silence is over and begins pushing back against what contained her.","src":"haiku"},
"carpenter-brut~stabat-mater-sylvaine":{"haiku":"A meditation on systemic cruelty, betrayal, and the futility of struggling against overwhelming darkness and inhumane forces.","opus":"The French synthwave act, with Sylvaine on vocals, adapts the grieving medieval hymn into a stark meditation on treachery and certain defeat. Warriors become custodians of cruelty, salvation stays beyond grasp as the dark seeps in, and the narrator, drawn toward suffering, resigns himself to a hopeless loss that curdles into a torment of his own making.","sonnet":"A figure drawn repeatedly toward pain despite knowing it will only compound defeat — wardens enforce suffering, light remains out of reach, and the cycle of failure feeds itself. The compression of the lyric makes the trap feel structural rather than personal: the system was designed to produce this outcome.","src":"haiku"},
"carpenter-brut~the-widow-maker-feat-gunship":{"opus":"A synthwave nightmare drenched in Matrix pill-choosing imagery, goading a partner into a nihilistic game where affection is worthless and raw brutality reigns. The title figure is a seductive bringer of ruin, coaxing prey toward destruction. Carpenter Brut and GUNSHIP make total annihilation sound like an invitation to celebrate.","sonnet":"A nihilistic, cyberpunk-inflected surrender to oblivion: the widow maker is an avatar of pure destructive seduction who persuades everyone to take the lethal pill, blow out their brains, and follow into the dark — framing violence and death as the ultimate party.","src":"sonnet"},
"carpenter-brut~turbo-killer":{"haiku":"Pure kinetic death becomes mantra; the figure obsesses as relentless repetition.","src":"haiku"},
"casey-edwards~bury-the-light":{"haiku":"A demon reclaims their dark power and identity despite curse and abandonment, burning as a phoenix reborn to topple the world's order.","opus":"Written for the swordsman Vergil in Devil May Cry 5, this battle anthem gives voice to a proud warrior determined to reclaim his identity and heritage, one steeped in violence and death. Driven by a hunger for immortality and a sense of fated purpose, he emerges renewed like something risen from ruin, challenging any rival who dares reach for the strength he intends to keep entirely his own.","sonnet":"A figure reborn through adversity claims an identity forged in darkness, naming himself a storm, a phoenix, a reclaimer of what was taken. Two souls lost to themselves find each other again through shared fire, and the light they bury becomes the source of their power.","src":"haiku"},
"casey-edwards~vacant-surrender":{"haiku":"Time and weight collapse consciousness until the speaker surrenders to circumstance; drowning in present moment becomes the only honest response to overwhelming force.","opus":"A soul crushed under everything it carries, unable to rewind the clock as it ticks and lurches toward some inevitable arrival. Reaching out in fear without understanding, it gets one turn to choose where it lands before memory fades and it has to give in.","sonnet":"Overwhelm and dissociation under the crushing weight of time: the speaker cannot feel or see the presence they are calling to, the clock crashes forward faster every day, and the final images — gasping for air, memory fading, surrendering to succession — describe the moment consciousness yields to forces beyond any individual control.","src":"haiku"},
"casey~great-grief":{"haiku":"Grief as catalyst for empathy and self-knowledge, finding catharsis through articulating pain in community.","opus":"A strikingly literate reckoning with sorrow as something almost welcome, the singer finding catharsis in sharing pain with others who relate. Grief reframed as a gift that proves he can still feel, and maybe deserve joy.","sonnet":"About finding that grief and suffering are actually generative — the narrator has emerged from isolation with a clearer sense of self, and argues that sharing pain with others who understand it transforms misery into something useful; the song is a genuine, articulate defense of catharsis as survival.","src":"haiku"},
"caskets~believe":{"haiku":"Cyclical destruction through intimate cruelty; the speaker oscillates between persecution and endurance, unable to sever what torments them.","opus":"Gathering himself back together while an ex wields cutting words, the narrator refuses to be cast as the fool or the villain in their version of events. Told he could never be worth believing in, he chooses to back himself anyway and walks off, leaving the wrecker to prey on someone new.","sonnet":"A speaker dismantled by someone who sharpened words like razors and made them a prisoner — still holding on to an inner belief that something survives inside them, even as the other person destroyed every chance and an apology never came.","src":"haiku"},
"caskets~by-the-sound":{"haiku":"Heartbeat measures hurt while both parties suffer divergent pain, unable to sync or heal. Separation becomes merciful, memory the only future shared.","opus":"The last word in a breakup after infidelity, insisting on being heard once and then letting go; the wounded pride and lingering tenderness collide in telling the ex not to waste grief on him.","sonnet":"A relationship autopsied after infidelity: the speaker demands to be heard out, insists they gave their time and heart fully, and confronts the partner who left their bed for someone else. The final instruction — \"don't waste your heart in mourning me\" — is simultaneously generous and devastating.","src":"haiku"},
"caskets~glass-heart":{"haiku":"Someone recognizes they've been deceived and broken by a false relationship, then grapples with the internal war of reclaiming their dignity and self-belief.","opus":"The UK melodic outfit dissects a relationship built on pretend intentions, the narrator exhausted at death's door and warring with himself over a fragile, still-beating glass heart. He refuses hollow apologies and vows to hold the deceiver accountable. Caskets favor soaring choruses over sheer weight, and this leans on that vulnerability, the repeated insistence that both parties should have known better.","sonnet":"Betrayal by someone whose intentions were always hollow leaves a narrator exhausted, fighting an internal war they keep losing. The fragile heart keeps beating despite everything, and amid confession of lies and mistakes, a refusal to accept a hollow apology finally takes hold.","src":"haiku"},
"caskets~lost-in-echoes":{"opus":"A mind unraveling under depression and self-ruin, every project the singer starts caving in around him. He begs a higher power to spare him, envisions his own funeral under a single pale bloom, and grows so estranged from himself that the very idea of being alive makes him flinch.","sonnet":"A person at the edge of suicidal ideation who pictures their own ending under a yellow rose — they feel erased from within, their soul gone, their mind unable to justify being alive, trapped between rage and the faint whisper that they might actually want to try.","src":"sonnet"},
"cassie-marin~r-u-missing":{"haiku":"Intimacy and memory intertwine as the speaker preserves moments of connection, holding what cannot stay while they slip away into sleep.","opus":"A drowsy, sensual plea to slow down and simply listen to each other before sleep, unrushed and attentive. Racing home to kiss someone goodnight in time, the speaker has swallowed the key to a feeling locked inside, wondering all the while whether the other person misses her too.","sonnet":"A languid, sun-soaked rendezvous — unhurried kisses at 7:30, red eyes memorised, the key swallowed to lock everything inside — asks a partner whether they are missing the narrator the way the narrator is missing them, warmth and longing braided into something slow and intimate.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~behind-closed-doors":{"haiku":"Two people share intimate confessions and physical connection in private, but cannot openly acknowledge their relationship or what they truly feel for each other in public.","opus":"A secret, physical affair the two won't acknowledge in daylight; the singer is consumed by an intimacy she can't unsee, torn between craving her partner and being frustrated by his hesitation and sober denials.","sonnet":"Two people share an intense, intimate secret — feelings and actions that happened in private — but refuse to openly acknowledge it, leaving the narrator restless and impatient with the denial.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~boom":{"haiku":"Euphoria detonates from internal dynamite, the speaker flying high and alive before implosion. Energy burns bright, fire consuming everything, yet the speaker embraces the blast.","opus":"The rush of slipping into a wilder second skin where inhibitions fall away and you feel most alive on the edge of detonation; the explosion imagery is euphoria and volatility as the same fuse.","sonnet":"An altered state — hyperdrive, eyes wide, looking more alive than ever in the mirror — is celebrated as dynamite going off, inhibitions falling, fire creeping through the body. The euphoria is physical and total, the boom its own justification.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~dead-roses":{"haiku":"Beautiful deception and subconscious warning signals collide; the speaker oscillates between surrender to and resistance of a toxic bond.","opus":"A reckoning with staying in a relationship gone quietly cruel, the narrator's subconscious warnings finally louder than her denial as she names her partner's sociopathic coldness and refuses one more strike.","sonnet":"A relationship recognised too late as toxic: the singer was blind to sociopathic tendencies, and now the image of sleeping on dead roses — beautiful on arrival, now thorns and rot — captures the entire arc of something soulless that once looked like heaven.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~degenerette-nation":{"haiku":"System corrupts and fails as society degenerates, the voice repeating mantras of digital collapse. Degenerette nation arrives inevitable, obsolescence assured.","opus":"A glitchy anthem for a self-styled tribe of misfits, corrupting the system and rallying under a made-up nation; more identity chant than narrative, claiming degeneracy as belonging.","sonnet":"A brief, self-destructing system message — \"this song will self destruct, coding corruption, battery low\" — introduces a degenerate nation of misfits who are their own dystopia, celebrating collective degradation in a looping chant. The minimal text makes the concept the entire content.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~friends-in-low-places":{"haiku":"Finding refuge in chosen family and substance use, embracing low places when direction and hope are lost.","opus":"Numbing rock-bottom camaraderie, pills and bottles and ride-or-die friends papering over a slow crisis. The tatted-on smiles admit that the lowness might be permanent, a state of mind rather than a phase.","sonnet":"About finding solidarity in collective depression and self-medication — the narrator and her friends are emotionally low, drinking, taking Percocet, crashing cars, and faking smiles; the \"low places\" are both where they are mentally and who they are with, and the community is the only comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~go":{"haiku":"Aggressive forward momentum and refusal to wait or negotiate, radical self-assertion and taking control without permission.","opus":"A pure adrenaline flex about seizing momentum, no time for talk or hesitation, the narrator declaring herself a champion who leaves anyone too slow behind.","sonnet":"Pure velocity as manifesto — the narrator is a champion who travels at light speed, dismisses talkers and slow-walkers, and demands everyone either run or get out of the way. There is no interiority here, only forward motion and the red alert of someone who has stopped losing control and started seizing it.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~ipecac":{"haiku":"Toxic presence becomes sickness requiring purging; the speaker wants to expel the poison entirely rather than endure slow contamination.","opus":"A fake friend's betrayal metabolized into a craving—the person makes her sick, yet she keeps needing more of the toxic hit like an emetic she can't stop swallowing. Revulsion and appetite collapse together into a threat to devour them alive.","sonnet":"A visceral song about a fake friend who betrayed the speaker's trust — the disgust is literalized as nausea, the speaker wanting to tear the person apart and consume them, framing the bad friend as a toxic sweetness they need to purge; the emotional register is contempt turned to physical revulsion.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~like-that":{"src":"web","web":"A snarling refusal to perform for anyone's approval — 'I'm like that, you don't have to like that' is the hook and the whole manifesto: a defiant shrug at expectation over spiralling guitars.","fable":"A defiance anthem built on a single reversal of a demand for approval. The opening refuses the obvious goth gesture — no funeral shade, a rosy dark hue instead — signaling foul mood worn as color rather than despair. The hook then turns the title phrase back on the listener, admitting she's built this way and daring nobody to have to enjoy it. So the concession that some people won't approve becomes the very engine of her pride. Cassyette's argument is that the instinct to snap back is wired in rather than picked up — shoved down, she bites; steered one direction, she bolts the other. That reframes her repeated admission that she'll always fall short of getting things correct. It stops sounding like an apology and hardens into an identity she'd sooner keep sprinting down the losing route than trade for anyone's good opinion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"non-approval converted into the engine of pride","built":"the title phrase turned back on the listener as a dare, concession hardening into identity"},
"cassyette~mayhem":{"opus":"A portrait of a combustible relationship falling apart in real time, all shouting matches, slammed exits and drunken collapses. The narrator sees clearly that her partner has grown indifferent and no longer wants her, and what once was love has curdled into pure chaos.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship that has become a grinding emotional disaster — the narrator is worn down by constant fighting, sleepless and wasted on the bathroom floor, and finally admits that the other person's love has become a chore they're bored of performing. The mayhem is domestic and chemical, not dramatic.","src":"sonnet"},
"cassyette~over-it":{"haiku":"Obsessive rumination over a past that cannot be forgotten; the speaker cannot move forward while their partner has already left them behind.","opus":"The ache of being unable to move past a breakup while watching the other person do it easily, all the pretending-not-to-mind unraveling into the honest loop of a mind that won't let go.","sonnet":"Post-breakup rumination that won't stop — going over it over in my mind but not getting over it — lying in the shared room wondering if the other person is thinking of you too. The troubled thoughts are parasites the speaker can't hide from or stop, the pretense of being fine punctured by seeing that the other person actually has moved on.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~petrichor":{"haiku":"Regret and paralysis from a broken relationship; the speaker wants resurrection after emotional death but remains trapped in spirals of self-destruction and medication.","opus":"Petrichor (the smell of rain on dry earth) becomes the emblem of a toxic relationship's aftermath; caught in a partner's destructive spiral with suicidal undertones, the narrator repeatedly begs to be reborn, wanting to burn down the old self and heal after the storm passes.","sonnet":"A post-relationship song about mutual destruction and the desperate wish to be remade — the narrator and a former partner have left each other paralyzed, and the petrichor smell after rain becomes a metaphor for the faint hope of revival out of the wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~phantom-limb":{"haiku":"Detachment fails—the loved one remains phantom sensation, nerves still firing toward absent limb. The speaker cannot amputate the memory, poison and presence intertwined.","opus":"Grief after love as an amputation, the ex still felt in the body long after they're gone; the ache isn't missing them so much as the maddening sense that a part of you is missing them for you.","sonnet":"A former partner has been so thoroughly incorporated into the speaker's identity that even after the relationship ends, they persist as a phantom limb — felt in the skin, clinging, lingering — and the speaker has to learn to stop treating the absence as a presence. Drilling a hole in the skull to dissolve memories is the desperate remedy imagined.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~picture-perfect":{"haiku":"Internal short-circuiting belies external polish; the speaker drowns in thought while faucets leak and disorder festers beneath the smile, all while hoping the machinery holds.","opus":"A study of the gap between a composed public mask and private collapse, the narrator smiling through a lie while admitting the promised recovery never came and the pressure keeps cracking her.","sonnet":"A person with a leaking faucet inside performs composed prettiness on the outside — \"I'll be smiling but I'm lying through my teeth\" — telling the person closest to them she did not want to bore them with the disorder, while privately realising she is not getting better and never will.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~sad-girl-summer":{"haiku":"Heartbreak transforms into violence as betrayal ignites destruction; the speaker emerges scorched but defiant, refusing the role of eternal victim.","opus":"Post-breakup wreckage played with defiant self-awareness, depressed in yesterday's outfit, torn between craving one last flicker of happiness and loathing the ex who caused it. After torching his clothes and smashing his guitar over the cheating, she flatly refuses to keep performing heartbreak.","sonnet":"A betrayed woman channels rage into retribution: she catches her partner with someone else, burns his clothes, smashes his guitar, and refuses to stay sad — the anger is preferable to the grief, but neither is where she wants to live. The refrain pivots between \"you made me a sad girl\" and \"I don't wanna be a sad girl anymore,\" tracing the arc from victim to agency.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~say-my-name":{"haiku":"Seeking dissolution of self through physical connection, craving another person to undo boundaries and identity.","opus":"Surrender to a consuming, self-erasing desire, the narrator wanting to be undone by a lover who makes her forget who she is. The reference to a debaser frames it as pleasurable degradation she keeps chasing.","sonnet":"About using sex and desire as a way to escape yourself — the narrator is emotionally bruised and dissociated, and being with this person who calls her name is the only thing that makes her feel real again; the dynamic is deliberately intoxicating, almost submissive, a chosen loss of self.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~september-rain":{"haiku":"Dissolution accelerates as pain and alienation wash away identity; the speaker surrenders to erasure.","opus":"A dissociative descent into depression imagined as rain washing the self away. Asking whether death brings rest and when she'll feel alive, the speaker keeps falling under, watching herself dissolve.","sonnet":"A person in deep psychological pain uses September rain as a metaphor for dissolving and washing away — crossing white lines, unable to feel alive, they descend continuously in an experience that has no bottom and no end.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~sugar-rush":{"haiku":"Intoxication offers escape into rabbit hole sweetness despite rot spreading through teeth, the speaker chasing euphoria at any cost. Reality dissolves into candy and void, appetite endless.","opus":"Addiction dressed as candy, the craving for a sweetness that rots you from the inside while you chase it down the rabbit hole; escape into unreality that tastes so good precisely because it's murderous.","sonnet":"Escaping reality through a sickly-sweet addictive high — candy-floss, saccharine, galaxies swallowed, the rabbit hole entered — is described as murderous in its pleasures, rotting teeth but tasting so sweet. The gloam is the perfect hour for this kind of dissolution.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~take-take-take":{"haiku":"Endless taking without reciprocation; a parasitic dynamic consuming what was freely given.","opus":"A furious cutoff of a user who drains everyone and blames the world, the narrator finally refusing to keep giving to someone who only takes and hates hearing the truth about it.","sonnet":"An exposé of a user — drip drip drip, you're the sick in my mouth — who takes everything, gives nothing, blames others when they fall, burns all their bridges while hating the truth; the speaker reaches the point of refusal not with drama but with exhaustion: I can't give anymore to you.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~untouched":{"haiku":"Resilience against emotional sabotage: a lover's desperate attempts to wound fail entirely against the speaker's self-fortified strength, leaving him untouched and unmoved by toxic affection.","opus":"Standing untouched while an ex-lover's cruelty ricochets off and only makes you stronger; the power move is watching them crawl back and refusing the tough love that was only ever manipulation.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses someone who has turned hostile after being hurt — attacking with teeth out, tying metaphorical nooses — but declares that every attempt to wound her has only made her stronger, and she remains untouched.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~when-she-told-me":{"haiku":"A single revelation inverts reality and traps the speaker in permanent inversion; they cannot recover equilibrium or breath.","opus":"The world literally turning upside-down at the moment of devastating news, the singer left spinning, breathless, and permanently altered. A crow at the door signals that the same blow may be coming for you next.","sonnet":"The world-shattering experience of receiving devastating news from a woman — the speaker's entire reality inverts and collapses, leaving them unable to breathe, cry, or function, permanently altered by whatever was said.","src":"haiku"},
"cassyette~why-am-i-like-this":{"haiku":"Self-sabotage and depression interrupt every moment of joy as the speaker defaults toward collapse. Intrinsic wrongness seems unfixable, dragging toward spiraling darkness.","opus":"Mania and depression trading places, every high crashing far quicker than it should, the narrator torpedoing her own good spells and sinking like dead weight. The looping self-interrogation names a self-destructive cycle she keeps catching herself in.","sonnet":"A self-interrogation in cycles of mood disorder: high as the moon one moment, spiralling down the next, the monster inside making everything sicker — \"why am I like this\" is the question that arrives every time the freefall resumes with no answer offered.","src":"haiku"},
"castele~witch-s-flower":{"haiku":"Demons seduce with false promises of eternal peace; the speaker is paralyzed by poisoned temptation and internal conflict. Safety lies only in returning to the beginning and fighting back.","opus":"A storybook nightmare in which concentration dissolves into flame and creeping shadows, and night-time demons offer eternity laced with a venom that leaves the body numb and paralyzed. Amid the panic comes a rallying cry for rescue and a plea to loose a spell skyward, clipping pain's wings so the whole ordeal might reset.","sonnet":"A half-remembered ritual space gives way to a night invasion by demons bearing seductive promises — offering an end to suffering in exchange for surrender. The paralysis that follows, fingers and legs gone numb, is the body registering how close the temptation came to breaking the will entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"cat-company~cutie-carousel":{"haiku":"A soul separates from the body and traverses multidimensional spaces; transcendence becomes fragmented and hallucinatory.","opus":"A psychedelic dissociation trip dressed as a carnival ride, the narrator drifting between planes of existence and a tug-of-war of angels and demons, temporary escape standing in for a mind that needs healing.","sonnet":"Dissociation rendered as spectacle: the mind gets lost, the soul catches fire, and suddenly there are angels on the back saying it's time to go and demons at the feet pulling down — caught in the carousel spinning round and around, teleporting to another realm is both the crisis and the only available exit.","src":"haiku"},
"catarinth~deviate":{"haiku":"Rebellion against systemic servitude and spectacle: the narrator rejects a world built on false hierarchy and performance, demanding the freedom to break away and reclaim agency from those who orchestrate the show.","opus":"A Cyberpunk-flavored revolt against a rigged system that demands worship and self-sacrifice in exchange for a glittering, gutter-level existence. To deviate is to seize the story back and wake from a hell dressed up as the perfect life.","sonnet":"Set in a neon-dystopian city where power demands full submission, the speaker calls for collective refusal — to deviate, break away, and reject the system's copy-pasted existence rather than chip in for self-destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"catsclaw~human-fission":{"haiku":"Society fractures through deliberate division and fear-mongering while populations remain isolated and unreflective, cutting people from shared purpose and truth.","opus":"Division as a weapon: fear and manufactured hatred fracturing people, keeping them isolated and stripped of answers so they can be ruled; the plea is that unity is the only way to survive the splitting.","sonnet":"Deliberate social division — \"sedated by hatred,\" minds kept in a hive state — is weaponised by unseen forces to keep people isolated and blaming each other rather than the structure pulling them under.","src":"haiku"},
"cave-in~new-reality":{"haiku":"An inevitable change arrives—decay, collapse, loss—and forces a reckoning with decline. The song confronts an unavoidable new world no one was ready for.","opus":"Set against a stark New England backdrop of crumbling stone and reddened water, the song registers the reluctant realization that the world has irreversibly changed. Its images of a figure abandoned and grieving read as an unwilling reckoning with decline and permanent loss.","sonnet":"A bleak confrontation with irreversible change — old certainties have fallen away like rock from a mountain, and what remains is grief, decline, and a dawning that things will not return to how they were. The imagery of rivers running red and an old man left behind frames it as a generational or civilizational loss.","src":"haiku"},
"cecile-believe~bitch-bites-dog":{"haiku":"Someone describes inner rage and restlessness, feeling trapped in their own body and expressing this through animalistic imagery and spiritual discomfort.","opus":"A raw admission of a craving that refuses restraint, the singer comparing herself to a hound yanking against its tether, unable to endure one more evening trapped in her own skin, half-prayer and half-summoning of the twisted urges she carries with a sense of divine blessing. Cecile Believe, a Canadian producer and vocalist tied to hyperpop and to co-writing with SOPHIE, sets that glitchy pop against jagged, boundary-crossing appetite.","sonnet":"Desire and religiosity tangled into something feverish — the speaker is consumed by a hunger they can't quiet, flipping the leash dynamic and naming Saint Cecile as they describe wanting to rip through everything until the ending arrives. The body becomes the site of both torment and revelation, unbearable to inhabit and impossible to leave.","src":"haiku"},
"celeste~de-tes-yeux-bleus-perl-s":{"haiku":"Fragmentation and bodily rupture expose the collapse of composure; the body becomes betrayer as tears and sweat and blood announce what words cannot say.","opus":"In French, a fragile angelic figure on the verge of shattering, her whole body pouring blood, bile, and sweat as her foundations collapse. Bewildered, she tries to understand why she was abandoned, while unnamed hands hold, grip, and take.","sonnet":"A French-language portrait of a woman collapsing from inside: her jaw betrays the tears about to spill, her body weeps blood and bile, her temples sweat and gather dust, while she searches, bewildered, for why she has been abandoned. The closing lines — \"his hands hold, his hands grip, his hands take\" — transform grief into a question of possession and force.","src":"haiku"},
"cellar-darling~dance":{"haiku":"Mass hysteria and euphoric collapse sweep through humanity; broken and torn dancers move in trance-like repetition, freed and enslaved by rhythm and shared delirium.","opus":"A retelling of the medieval dancing plague, bodies dragged into helpless, deadly motion until they collapse. The nonsense incantation and choreomania make it about mass compulsion, oblivion, and dancing yourself to death.","sonnet":"Choreomania — the historical phenomenon of compulsive, uncontrollable dancing — is rendered as a trance of the broken: torn figures circle in ritual motion, lucidity ruptures, the dance conquers delirium, and the chorus names it \"sheer oblivion\" while the dancers faint and raise their limbs toward the sky unwillingly.","src":"haiku"},
"cellar-darling~pain":{"haiku":"A parent simultaneously nurturing and inflicting pain on a child, who absorbs the world's suffering like a conduit. Explores inherited trauma and victimization.","opus":"Cellar Darling, built from ex-Eluveitie members around singer Anna Murphy, spin an ecological parable in which the wounded planet gives birth to a suffering child made to soak up all of humanity's cruelty and mirror it back at the people who condemn it. Their folk-tinged progressive metal frames the narrator watching her own creation break down under the weight.","sonnet":"The earth speaks as a wounded mother watching her orphan child mirror her own suffering — born to absorb all the world's pain like a voodoo doll, the child is accused of the very inheritance she didn't choose. The song moves between tenderness and accusation, grief and fury, with azure skies fading as the lesson of being the earth is slowly learned.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller-scandroid~breakout":{"haiku":"Prison and forgotten identity; after years inside, escape becomes an act of remembrance and hope.","opus":"A sci-fi prison-break narrative from Klayton's Aterian saga, augmented humans jailed for protesting cybernetic inequality choosing to smash their cell rather than lose themselves to the long years inside.","sonnet":"Seven years on Helion, imprisoned for being made of flesh and circuit and bone — a science-fiction escape narrative where the faction's satellite signals hope and the breakout happens tonight. The prison is both literal and allegorical, the memory of home recovered mid-story, the hybrid body's existence the only crime that ever mattered.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~06-06-06-unreleased-demo-2006":{"haiku":"Someone is trapped in a system where ambition and social connection lead to ruin, with a ticking clock marking the price of money and status. The song depicts debt and existential desperation.","opus":"An early unreleased Celldweller demo, Klayton's one-man fusion of industrial rock and electronics, taunts a sophisticated striver bound to fail. Counting rhymes about money and show tick a clock down toward psycho, the sophisticate one connection from being underground and overrated. Between the sneering choruses a plea surfaces not to be left behind, a fight the narrator isn't afraid to start beneath the cold embrace and lies.","sonnet":"A chaotic countdown game — money, power, status — spirals toward a reckoning where all the bets come up empty. The speaker refuses to be the one who fixes or waits, positioning himself as a combatant in a zero-sum environment where the sophisticated and overrated are always one connection away from going underground.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~a-matter-of-time":{"haiku":"The speaker reflects on how someone they trusted showed their true nature through a pattern of betrayal toward others, finally turning that same weapon against the speaker. It was inevitable all along.","opus":"A caustic goodbye to someone whose treacherous nature was always going to surface, the singer faulting himself for brushing past the red flags. This person, who treated others exactly as they fantasized others treated them, eventually aims their malice at him, their arrogance guaranteeing a collapse they brought upon themselves.","sonnet":"A song about finally recognizing a serial manipulator for what they are — the narrator watched this person destroy others for years and naively believed they'd be exempt, but the pattern was always going to turn their way, and the farewell is a quiet, bitter epitaph.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~afraid-this-time": {"haiku":"The speaker builds walls and isolates himself from emotional hurt, acknowledging the contradiction of wanting protection while fearing the loss of genuine connection. He admits that this defensive strategy is breaking down differently than before.","opus":"A study of emotional defense, someone stacking barriers so tall that no genuine self remains to be reached, certain no one can reach or wound him anymore. The pivot is his confession that the armor is finally cracking, a repeated admission of fear exposing that the numbness he assembled for safety no longer holds, and he can no longer trust his own detachment.","sonnet":"A person who has built emotional walls so high they've lost themselves behind them admits, almost against their will, that they're finally feeling fear and vulnerability again — something is different this time and the defenses aren't holding the way they used to.","src":"haiku","fable":"An early Klayton track that runs on the tension between two voices talking over each other. The dominant one is armor-building: raise the walls high and strong, seal yourself off until no real self is left to be reached, so that no one can touch or hurt you again. But the parenthetical undertow keeps leaking through with the admission the front is meant to prevent — that this fortress is fear, not strength, and that the invulnerability being announced is exactly what the speaker no longer believes. The refrain sharpens as it repeats: it isn't the you-can't-hurt-me boast that's true, it's the quiet coda that undercuts it, until the whole song collapses into someone confessing, over and over, that despite all the defenses he's afraid this time. The industrial-era Celldweller move is to let the loud, defiant hook and the small honest whisper share the same mix, so the numbness and the terror are audible at once.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"defiant armor and whispered terror audible in the same mix","built":"quiet honest coda undercuts the loud hook each time it repeats"},
"celldweller~against-the-tide":{"haiku":"A father and son struggle against an overwhelming storm, facing consequences for past deeds while trying to hold on through spiritual darkness and rising waters.","opus":"A struggle against drowning cast as an inherited paternal curse, the narrator battling floodwaters and storm while a father's voice keeps insisting he's only harvesting the results of his own actions, his prayers dissolving unheard. Celldweller is Klayton's electronic-rock project, and this blend of orchestral grandeur, heavy dubstep-era production, and biblical doom typifies his cinematic style.","sonnet":"A father and son submerged together in a reckoning — the father warned of inherited curses, the son is now drowning in consequences, and what little comfort is offered comes wrapped in hard truth: you reap what you sow. Holding out against the tide becomes both literal and theological, hope eroding with each wave while the moon eclipses the sun.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~atmospheric-light-demo-redux":{"haiku":"Return and maternal reconciliation transcend death and separation, with the speaker invoking reception and acceptance across an unbridgeable threshold.","opus":"A stark early Klayton demo of severing every tie and returning to some maternal source, the plea to be received again reading as a longing for death or dissolution back into origin.","sonnet":"A homing signal: the narrator, alone and breaking ties somewhere beneath an atmospheric light, calls out to a mother figure — \"I'm coming home to you\" — repeating \"receive me\" with the urgency of someone who needs to be taken back in before time runs out.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~baptized-in-fire":{"haiku":"Spiritual purification through suffering and transformation, burning away the trivial to reveal the sacred.","opus":"Annihilation reframed as sacrament: the collapse of everything familiar strips a man to the bone, and instead of mourning he walks into the blaze, letting the fire separate the sacred from the profane and burn the trivial off his life. What survives is changed for good. Celldweller is Klayton, the one-man electronic-rock operation, and this sits with his later, openly spiritual material, where the machinery serves language of purification and rebirth.","sonnet":"Transformation through total destruction — an empire stripped away, everything familiar scorched, and out of the burning the speaker emerges purified and permanently changed. The fire isn't punishing so much as refining: the dross burns off, the sacred survives, and what remains is someone who can never go back to what they were before.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~baptized-in-fire-fight-the-fade-remix":{"haiku":"Destruction and suffering have burned away the trivial and worldly to leave only essence behind, a sacred refinement through ordeal that promises transformation and rebirth. The speaker emerges purified, forever changed by immolation.","opus":"Destruction imagined as purification — an empire's fall stripping the narrator to nothing, severed from all he knew yet sensing the burning was only clearing ground for rebirth, a summons issued from the fire. Klayton's industrial-rock project wraps this consecration-through-flame in cinematic texture, the refining imagery insisting the reborn self is permanently changed.","sonnet":"Destruction as spiritual renovation — the fire burns away everything trivial, strips the speaker to bones, and what remains is something consecrated rather than ruined. The song moves through pain and disorientation toward a conviction that this annihilation was necessary, that the sacred was always in the flames, and that the person who comes out the other side will never resemble the one who went in.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~birthright":{"haiku":"A person fights against feeling helpless and imprisoned, reclaiming agency and refusing to back down as they claim what rightfully belongs to them.","opus":"Klayton's industrial project delivers a clenched-fist anthem of self-liberation, a narrator pinned down and sick of waiting for a life he resents. He resolves to seize what he is owed and leave without apology, the doubting voices in his own head rising to a roar as he refuses to stay helpless.","sonnet":"Pinned down and humiliated long enough, a person finally hears their own suppressed will screaming back at them from the mirror and decides to claim what should have been theirs all along. The reclamation is furious and total — no apologies, no retreating — and it comes framed as survival, not triumph.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~blackstar": {"haiku":"A wish granted by ominous forces brings irreversible consequences that cannot be undone or reversed no matter how much one tries to escape them.","opus":"Menacing, unstoppable figures ride in on a dark star to deliver a desire the listener never should have voiced, and nothing can undo it. The chorus insists the only response left is a resigned praise, since not a thing can be altered.","sonnet":"Unstoppable enforcers arrive on a Blackstar to fulfil a wish that should never have been made, and no amount of resistance or reversal can undo it — the only response is helpless acceptance.","src":"haiku","fable":"A wish curdles into a sentence: the thing you begged for arrives as riders on a black star, soundless collection agents who chain you to the exact outcome you asked for and will not now let you revoke it. Klayton builds it as a trap in three snaps — the riders come in famished, their Medusa gaze turning whoever meets it to stone, then in the last verse they simply ride off, bored, because your identity was never the point and the result was fixed before you spoke. The praise-word gets weaponized in the hook, a churchy chant welded to a flat verdict that nothing you do reverses anything, worship inverted into the sound of surrender. That massed, glitch-cut choir flooding the bridge isn't devotion rising but a foregone conclusion drowning out every attempt to undo the bargain.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The track hits like a swarm of powers descending, all menace and grandeur; these are the celestial entities that grant the wishes made on a blackstar, and Klayton has named the album's moral as be careful what you wish for — so what sounds like arrival is really the dark half of the bargain coming to collect.","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","surveillance & control"],"means":"wish granted as sentence by collectors who were never listening","built":"praise-word weaponized into a chant of irreversible surrender"},
"celldweller~blind-lead-the-blind":{"haiku":"False leaders mislead the innocent masses away from truth; the speaker observes with contempt as blind followers march toward destruction.","opus":"The industrial project delivers a scathing indictment of a false prophet who feeds his followers lies and hollow idols, steering trusting innocents toward ruin. The narrator pulls back to watch the whole thing collapse with grim, sorrowful amusement, reserving his real grief not for the con man but for the minds he has led astray.","sonnet":"A contemptuous address to a false prophet or manipulative leader who feeds lies to vulnerable followers, leaving their believers spiritually blind and destined for ruin — the speaker steps back and watches the inevitable collapse with grim amusement.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~blood-from-the-stone-unreleased-demo-2005":{"haiku":"Extraction of vitality from the barren and unwilling, the speaker as draining force leaving isolation and deterioration in its wake.","opus":"An early demo of clear-eyed resignation, the narrator naming himself the impossible thing squeezed dry, admitting a doomed relationship was never built to last and that he will end up alone.","sonnet":"A relationship that was never promised to last, and now that it hasn't, the memories have turned bad — the narrator defines himself as the blood from the stone, the impossible thing wrung from nothing. Left to his own devices, he ends up alone; anything salvageable from the wreckage belongs to someone else to turn to gold.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~breakout":{"haiku":"Prisoners break free after seven years of confinement, heading toward a new frontier and liberation beyond their cell.","opus":"A science-fiction escape story of prisoners held for years in a cell on a distant world, eyeing the sky and vowing to break out until they finally reach a new frontier and a place called New Elysium.","sonnet":"Prisoners on a distant planet called Helion plan and execute a jailbreak after seven years of captivity, eventually reaching a new frontier and finding freedom in a place they come to call home.","src":"haiku","fable":"Klayton stages this as a science-fiction jailbreak measured in rigid stretches of captivity, and its entire meaning lives in how the story shifts across three verses. The opening pair reads like any escape tale, following inmates who have lost their own identities, staring past iron toward a dimming horizon and vowing to run. Their sole distinguishing note is the odd confession that their offense is simply being part meat and part machinery, a body half-grown and half-built. The last verse quietly reverses that premise, as another decade slips by, the captives recover their sense of self, and they gaze toward strange stars and foreign suns awaiting a promised paradise where the rallying cry now insists their confinement holds no torment. Then the closing refrain jerks abruptly back to the first vow, recasting the whole ordeal as a circling loop rather than a rescue, freedom won only to be renounced with the identical sentence restarting from zero.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["freedom & escape","identity & becoming"],"means":"the escape vow looped back to zero, freedom won and instantly renounced","built":"the final refrain snaps back to the opening sentence, recasting liberation as a perpetual restart"},
"celldweller~down-to-earth": {"haiku":"An arrogant, self-entitled person is brought down by gravitational reality and their own inflated ego as broken wings prevent their rise.","opus":"A gleeful takedown of arrogance and self-entitlement, watching someone drunk on their own importance and inevitable gravity do its work, the moral certainty that ego always collides with reality and the tower always falls.","sonnet":"A song gleefully aimed at a self-important person heading for a crash — ego and gravity are incompatible, and the narrator watches with satisfaction as arrogance collides with reality and brings everything down.","src":"haiku","fable":"A takedown of inflated ego staged as physics, where gravity becomes the moral law that eventually collects on anyone mistaking altitude for permanence. Celldweller frames the arrogant target as a Babel architect whose tower already tilts, the ascent narrated coldly by those left below with their fingers in the soil, watching a life run inert. The bridge strips the insult down to machine verbs, a shutdown-and-detonation countdown that turns the plummet into engineered demolition rather than accident, industrial-metal precision doing the sneering on schedule.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society"],"means":"gravity appointed as the moral law that collects on arrogance","built":"bridge converts the fall into an engineered shutdown-and-detonation sequence"},
"celldweller~electric-eye":{"haiku":"An omniscient satellite watches and monitors humanity, keeping people safe and controlled from above.","opus":"Sung from the perspective of an all-seeing surveillance satellite that watches, scans and monitors from the sky, promising to keep you safe while it keeps you under its control.","sonnet":"Sung from the perspective of a surveillance satellite, the song describes an all-seeing, ever-vigilant system that monitors souls from orbit, promising safety and control to those beneath its gaze.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~electric-eye-zardonic-remix":{"haiku":"Surveillance from above; an electric beam watches continuously from the sky.","opus":"A cover of Judas Priest's surveillance dystopia, voiced from the perspective of the all-seeing satellite whose promise to keep you safe and sound is really total, loveless control over your soul.","sonnet":"Total surveillance from above: I am electric eye, eternal stare, always aware, always awake — monitoring your soul with a machine of loving grace. The cover of the Judas Priest track keeps its cold omniscience intact, the satellite-watcher keeping things in their place through invisible dominance that never needs to announce itself.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~end-of-an-empire":{"haiku":"Meditates on the inevitable fall of all empires and systems built by mortals, which cannot escape fate and must eventually crumble. The song philosophizes about impermanence and the cycles of civilization.","opus":"A sweeping meditation on how empires, civilizations, and life inevitably crest and crumble, insisting nothing lasts forever and no one escapes mortality. It reframes each conclusion as the seed of a new cycle, adding that even oblivion itself will eventually meet its own limit.","sonnet":"A reflection on the universal cycle of rise and fall — every empire, system, and individual life is subject to the same paradigm of creation and destruction, and no amount of power or ambition can exempt anyone from the inevitable end.","src":"haiku","fable":"A meditation on impermanence dressed as an industrial-metal war anthem: the title track of Klayton's sprawling concept opus takes the oldest truism there is (nothing built by mortals outlasts them) and drills it into a countdown. The verses stutter their opposed words like a corrupted signal fighting to transmit, and those word-pairs quietly migrate from the mechanical toward the tender, so the collapsing empire slides out of geopolitics and into the private kingdom each of us rules inside our own skull. The song's sharpest turn is that even endings end, each one already opening into the next, which is why the refrain returns with mounting rather than exhausted force. Where a lesser doom song would settle onto blankness, the outro recasts the plunge as forward motion, every closing already cracking open its own sequel, so the apocalypse reads less as a full stop than as one more turn of the wheel.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","death & grief"],"means":"each ending already cracking open its own sequel so apocalypse reads as forward motion","built":"word-pairs migrate from mechanical toward tender as geopolitical empire slides into the private self"},
"celldweller~eon": {"haiku":"An isolated and trapped person refuses to wait indefinitely, determined to break through confinement even at great cost and push forward into freedom.","opus":"Trapped, isolated, and losing his memories in a tomb-like space where time is dislocated, the speaker refuses to wait any longer and claws his way forward, determined to push through and reclaim his birthright.","sonnet":"Trapped and isolated in a kind of stasis or imprisonment, the narrator refuses to wait any longer for his life to be erased — he fights through the darkness, driven by the things he has been missing, determined to reclaim his birthright rather than endure another eon of nothing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being buried alive and refusing to stay that way, which on Wish Upon A Blackstar reads doubly as premature-burial dread and as the clawing frustration of stasis itself. The verses establish the tomb-state — waiting in an emptied room while memory and life are drained away, time stalled so completely that a single moment stretches to eternity precisely because feeling has gone numb. What earns the song its length is the turn from passivity to force: the pre-chorus refrain of the head pressed to stone finally converts, in the third bridge, into the literal act of digging upward through earth, bone grinding on rock, the heartbeat so loud it drowns the whispered fears. The pivotal reframing is that the drive isn't to reclaim what was lost but to reach the things missed during the wasted eon — grief flipped forward into appetite. Klayton keeps the industrial-metal build honest here, the mechanized patience of the intro giving way to the eruption, so the mask of numb entrapment cracks to reveal the birthright underneath: someone who simply refuses to wait any longer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness","death & grief"],"means":"grief for wasted time flipped forward into appetite for what was missed","built":"head-pressed-to-stone refrain converts in the third bridge to literal digging upward"},
"celldweller~faction-01":{"haiku":"Fragmentation and collective dissolution mark the boundaries of identity and belonging.","opus":"Celldweller, the one-man industrial project of Klayton, has always doubled as a science-fiction author, threading albums with an Outland Industries mythos rather than mere singles. This arrival announcement is pure connective tissue — no lyric proper, only a voice welcoming passengers to the planet Atiria — functioning as an interstitial that stitches the surrounding tracks into a narrative world, the atmosphere doing the storytelling that words elsewhere carry.","sonnet":"An in-universe Outland Industries arrival announcement welcoming passengers to the planet Atiria — a piece of worldbuilding spoken word with no lyric content proper, functioning as atmospheric scene-setting for a sci-fi narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~faction-06":{"haiku":"An android explores a dead human world it cannot understand, aware of its programmed void where love cannot exist, a being witnessing the remnants of emotion it will never access.","opus":"An interstitial sci-fi vignette, androids surveying a human wasteland where one's programming approximates love and the other's cannot; the pathos is in the machine that recognizes what it can never understand.","sonnet":"A brief interstitial dialogue between two characters — one apparently human or emotional, one not — exploring a location called Atiria that doesn't appear on any map, in a world where humanity is gone. The exchange turns on one line: the programmed capacity for love versus its absence, and the flat acknowledgment that not having it is \"unfortunate.\"","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~faction-13":{"haiku":"Conspiracy and hidden alliances structure reality; secret factions control unseen networks.","opus":"A spoken newscast interlude building Klayton's cyberpunk world, sketching android-versus-human street violence and the prison breakout of imprisoned augmented dissidents, pure narrative scaffolding for the album.","sonnet":"Interlocking news broadcasts construct a dystopian world in which android rights protesters (\"plugins\") have been imprisoned, anti-android violence is escalating in the streets, and a carefully engineered prison break has just freed an unknown number of augmented humans. The dry editorial sign-offs — \"make sure they're all dead\" and \"well, that didn't take long\" — frame institutional violence and its counter-reaction as equally routine.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~fadeaway": {"haiku":"Someone fading away from themselves spirals through self-isolation and internal conflict, becoming their own enemy while withdrawing into a cold cell. They push everyone away as their sense of self disintegrates.","opus":"The industrial project stages an internal argument between the narrator and a taunting inner voice as he slides into isolation and self-hatred, baffled by how he got here. Trapped facing his own reflection, at once his closest ally and cruelest foe, he retreats into a private cell, faking normalcy while slowly disappearing from himself.","sonnet":"About dissociation and psychological fragmentation — the narrator is split between himself and a mocking inner voice, watching himself fade from reality while withdrawing into isolation. The inner voice is nastier and more honest than the exterior self, and the song is their argument.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is Klayton splitting into two voices and letting the crueler one win: a sneering inner heckler answers every honest line in brackets, calling the singer a maggot and jeering showtime while he tries to explain how he ended up here. Built as an early industrial-rock argument between selves, the song watches a man retreat into a cold interior room and decide that vanishing is safer than being known by anyone. The saboteur voice turns that retreat into a seduction, offering to be his only companion at the exact moment it walls him off, until the two selves collapse into one grammatical impossibility, a person stranded between himself and himself with no fixed self left to erase.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind","identity & becoming"],"means":"the crueler inner voice seduces the honest one into vanishing","built":"two selves collapse into one grammatical impossibility at the close"},
"celldweller~frozen": {"haiku":"The speaker escapes into a fantasy of frozen time with a lover, preferring synthetic ecstasy and memory to harsh reality and shame.","opus":"A song about escaping into an intensely vivid erotic fantasy where a woman's presence makes reality fade and the narrator's hateful traits temporarily vanish, though he knows this frozen, dreamlike moment must eventually end and leave him cold.","sonnet":"A man retreats into a frozen mental image of a desired encounter — a moment of intense sensory and erotic fixation that stops time — using the fantasy as an escape from harsh reality, while acknowledging the cold left when that inner world fades.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a man narcotizing himself on a private erotic loop, choosing a manufactured fantasy of a woman over the real world he finds unbearable. Klayton's early industrial-rock persona treats the encounter as anesthetic: as long as the image holds still, the shame of who he has become dissolves and stays gone for its duration. The chill that closes the track is the comedown, the fantasy melting off and abandoning him alone with the same cold he was trying to outrun.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"erotic fantasy held in place until shame dissolves","built":"the chill that closes the track is the comedown not the fantasy"},
"celldweller~frozen-celldweller-vs-blue-stahli":{"haiku":"Synthetic pleasure freezes time while real life crumbles; the speaker retreats into fantasy to escape pain.","opus":"An escape into a frozen erotic fantasy that feels truer than a hateful waking life. Time halts in that manufactured bliss where all the ugliness he's turned into briefly dissolves, though he knows the cold and reality come flooding back the moment his mind warms.","sonnet":"A song about escaping into a sexual fantasy so vivid it freezes time — the narrator retreats into an imagined encounter with a woman whose desire strips everything else away, finding in this mental space a relief from the hateful person they have become, though the cold eventually seeps back in when the fantasy ends.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~frozen-cpr-remix-by-copy-paste-repeat":{"haiku":"Resuscitation through artificial means; lifeless forms revive through technological intervention and repetition.","opus":"A retreat into a suspended erotic fantasy as escape from a hateful real life, the narrator preferring the frozen, colorless perfection of an imagined moment because everything outside it leaves him cold and broken.","sonnet":"A man finds refuge from self-loathing and the real world in an erotic fantasy — \"inside this fantasy it seems so real\" — where a single suspended moment cancels \"all the hateful things I have become.\" The tension between the warmth of the frozen instant and the cold he feels without it (\"I'm so cold\") reveals that the escape is temporary and the return to reality inevitable and brutal.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~ghosts-feat-tom-salta":{"haiku":"Soldiers and spirits haunt a place of fear, but the song asserts that confronting fear and understanding enemies' minds gives power while weakening those who succumb to paranoia.","opus":"A song sung from the vantage of spectral soldiers who move outside time to hunt their targets, inhabiting a realm steeped in dread where their old identities dissolve and these phantoms swear vengeance on their foes.","sonnet":"Soldiers or operatives describe themselves as ghosts — spirits on recon, stripped of their former lives — arguing that knowing and confronting fear makes you stronger, while dismissing it as imaginary makes you weaker, as they pursue threats to innocence.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~gift-for-you":{"haiku":"A speaker returns poison and suffering as vengeance gifts, reciprocating harm done to them through metaphorical violence, illness, and damnation.","opus":"Klayton's genre-splicing solo project wraps a revenge fantasy in mock-generous cheer, the narrator returning to an old wrong offering gifts he freely admits are worse than what he received. Poison, a place in hell, sickness, a cancer within, each presented with a manic laugh. The saccharine framing sharpens the malice, turning gratitude and gift-giving into vehicles for cold, gleeful retribution.","sonnet":"Revenge is repackaged as reciprocity: having received damage, the speaker returns it wrapped in the same courtesy the original wrong was delivered with. The gifts catalogued — poison, damnation, sickness, cancer — mirror what was given first. Dark humour coats genuine malice; the politeness of the framing is precisely what makes it threatening.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~good-l-ck-yo-re-f-cked": {"haiku":"Inevitable downfall and cosmic bad luck that no one can escape.","opus":"A gleefully macabre song addressed to a doomed person whose life is unraveling into cartoonish disasters, framed as a losing game of hangman where the missing letter is 'U' — everyone can see he is finished except him.","sonnet":"A darkly comic taunt directed at someone whose life is catastrophically unraveling — poisoned food, bad luck, death closing in — delivered by a gleefully sardonic narrator who knows the truth the target is too oblivious to see.","src":"haiku","fable":"A gleeful death-sentence delivered by a chorus of onlookers who already know how it ends. Celldweller's Klayton stacks the verses with cartoonish, escalating hazards — poisoned smokes, a rigged game of chance, and a personal grim reaper marking the target's grade — until the doom stops feeling like accident and starts feeling like a rigged system closing in. The cleverest move is the hangman conceit: the game everyone is losing needs the letter U, so the missing piece is literally you, the condemned, spelled out one taunting syllable at a time. Underneath the industrial-pop bounce and the near-comic cruelty sits the older horror the pre-chorus names — the crowd on the other side who can see what's coming while the victim, oblivious, keeps playing along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","comedy & wordplay","surveillance & control"],"means":"doom delivered gleefully by onlookers who already know the outcome","built":"hangman conceit makes the missing letter the condemned person's name"},
"celldweller~good-luck":{"haiku":"Bitter resignation masks farewell, with the speaker wishing harmful fortune upon departure.","opus":"A gleefully vicious jeer at someone whose fortune has run dry and whose existence is set to detonate, complete with poisoned smokes and predators lurking in the bedding. The cruel gag is that everyone but the victim can see the ruin coming, the absent letter in their doomed word puzzle being U.","sonnet":"A gleeful eulogy for someone whose luck has comprehensively run out — cyanide, Russian roulette, nails in bread, a shark in bed, the grim reaper grading the show and tell — delivered with the relish of someone watching a slow-motion disaster they didn't cause: \"good luck, you're fucked, and everyone knows but you.\"","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~goodbye":{"haiku":"Moments of realization slip away, and the speaker mourns understanding that arrives too late.","opus":"The anguished instant of grasping that something precious is vanishing before it can be understood, the singer pleading with the fleeting instant to linger. The thread is remorse over a brief opportunity missed, wishing he had comprehended it before it slipped past for good.","sonnet":"About a moment of farewell passing before it was properly understood — the narrator circles around a goodbye they weren't ready for, wondering how long a fleeting moment was willing to wait for them to finally comprehend it.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~goodbye-remixed-by-klayton":{"haiku":"The speaker grapples with missed understanding before a final moment slips away irrevocably, caught between the need to comprehend and the relentless passage of time.","opus":"The paralysis of failing to grasp a fleeting moment before it slips away for good, regret arriving only after the chance to understand and hold on has already said its goodbye.","sonnet":"A frozen moment the speaker almost let pass without recognising — so far, so close to what I knew would be the last thing I would do — a goodbye that arrives before understanding does, and cannot be recalled once the moment has spoken it.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~heart-on": {"haiku":"A declarative love song where the speaker performs increasingly absurd acts of heroism and devotion to communicate their deep affection. The heart is pinned openly for the world to see—vulnerability is the point.","opus":"An extravagant, tongue-in-cheek love song in which the singer brags of battling towering beasts, seductive sea-women and sword-wielding warriors to prove his devotion, baring his affection for all to witness just to declare his total love.","sonnet":"An exuberant, hyperbolic declaration of love: the narrator catalogues absurd feats of heroism performed for someone he loves, making clear through comic braggadocio that his devotion is total and unconditional.","src":"haiku","fable":"It is a love song hiding inside a boast, the singer inflating himself into a monster-slayer and siren-charmer only to admit the whole point was the plainest confession he keeps fumbling. Celldweller trades its usual electronic-metal snarl for a grinning pop hook built on the pun of a heart worn on the sleeve, using the childish swagger as cover for a sentiment too simple to say straight. The escalating heroics and the schoolyard oath sworn on his own chest do one job: to postpone, then finally blurt, an ordinary I-love-you across every high and low he can name.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"heroic escalation used to postpone then blurt a plain confession","built":"pun on heart-worn-on-sleeve carries the whole emotional argument"},
"celldweller~i-believe-you": {"haiku":"Someone who is repeatedly manipulated and exploited by another's lies continues to trust and comply despite awareness of the deception. They rationalize the abuse and their own weakness.","opus":"A beaten-down speaker describes being manipulated by someone whose lie he was sold and can't escape, fighting their fights until he's left bleeding and spineless. He keeps insisting it's all right because he still believes and submits to them, even as he calls himself a sucker.","sonnet":"The singer reflects on having been manipulated and exploited — fed lies, made to fight someone else's battles, worn down into passivity — and bitterly acknowledges that he accepted it all. The repeated \"that's all right\" is sardonic, marking how completely the deception took hold.","src":"haiku","fable":"It stages surrender as gratitude: a narrator thanking the very person who hollowed him out, running their errands and swallowing every excuse without ever demanding the reason. Klayton's early industrial rock circa 2003 wires that self-erasure into stacked, compliant refrains, the chorus quietly conceding the reassurance he craved never actually arrived while he keeps insisting everything is fine. What lands hardest is how the repetition works like conditioning, each cheerful okay filing the will down further until obedience starts to pass for faith.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The voice is the true believer, certain and indoctrinated; it lands differently knowing Klayton had just walked out of the Christian-industrial scene where he built his name in Circle of Dust, refusing by the late nineties to carry a church affiliation — a man singing belief he had recently unlearned.","themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"obedience worn so smooth it starts to pass for faith","built":"compliant refrains quietly concede the reassurance never arrived"},
"celldweller~i-can-t-wait":{"haiku":"A person recklessly pursues someone toxic and destructive, unable to resist despite the pain and shame.","opus":"Klayton's electronic-rock project frames lust as helpless self-destruction, casting himself as an insect flying toward a flame he knows is toxic. He swaps judgment for pure sensation, admitting he's reckless and foolish yet unable to resist an attraction that will ruin him, Celldweller pairing industrial urgency with the thrill of wanting too much.","sonnet":"A compulsion toward a destructive person overrides every rational objection — the narrator knows the attraction is poison, recognises the shame, and abandons caution anyway. The urgency is not innocence but chosen recklessness: burning is the point, not the side effect, and the waiting itself has become unbearable.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~i-can-t-wait-metal-remix-by-jay-ray":{"haiku":"Reckless obsession compels the speaker toward poisonous beauty despite knowing destruction awaits, willfully surrendering sense and shame to proximity with the flame that will burn them.","opus":"Reckless compulsion toward a toxic lover, the drawn-insect surrender where you swap reason for feeling knowing you'll be scorched; desire outrunning whatever price comes due later.","sonnet":"The narrator knows the woman is poison — skin laced with it, words laced with lies, affliction waiting in her eyes — and chooses her anyway, framing the compulsion as a moth and flame and asking \"why wait 'til hell to pay?\" The self-awareness makes it worse: he can see the trap, names it clearly, and dives in reckless.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~into-the-void":{"haiku":"Existential interrogation of moral character surfaces when all external constraint dissolves, confronting who one becomes when stripped of audience, protection, and societal scaffolding.","opus":"A stripped-away moral test staged as orbital collapse, asking who you truly are at the core when everything falls away, whether you would betray your brother to save yourself, and who you become facing that alone.","sonnet":"A moral stress test framed as a physics event — gravity destroyed, a life knocked from orbit, spinning into the void with no rescue — that asks who you actually are when stripped of everything extraneous: would you sell out your brother, or sacrifice yourself to save him? The void is where character is finally legible.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~into-the-void-misfit-remix":{"haiku":"Stripped of gravity and protection, one confronts their core moral self as the void approaches.","opus":"A moral free-fall strips everything away to confront who a person truly is at the core, asking whether they'd sell out a brother to save themselves. Celldweller, Klayton's electronic-rock project fusing industrial and cinematic sound design, frame the spin into the void as a test of character, the binaries of destroy and create, truth and liar hurled at a lone satellite forced face-to-face with itself when no one is left to offer rescue.","sonnet":"As gravity collapses and a life spins loose from its orbit, the central question becomes one of moral identity: malignant or benign, self-sacrificing or self-serving. The void strips every performance away until only the unmediated self remains, exposed and accountable to no one but itself.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~into-the-void-swarm-remix":{"haiku":"Gravitational pull toward nothingness becomes irresistible, with self-interrogation preceding final dissolution.","opus":"Spinning into a void where gravity and everything external are stripped away, leaving only the question of who you truly are. Faced with whether you'd betray a brother to save yourself or sacrifice your safety for him, the song insists that only your choices and allegiances define you when the rest falls away.","sonnet":"A remix that strips the original's moral stress test to its essentials — gravity destroyed, everything stripped away, the binary of malignant or benign — while the void becomes the only honest destination for a life whose orbit has been shattered. Functionally identical in reading to the original, compressed.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~iria-unreleased-demo-2005":{"opus":"A shelved 2005 demo from Celldweller's early solo years, this fragment survives as skeleton rather than song. Klayton's process famously buries finished tracks under years of revision, and here the seams show: the text cycles only between an urgent interruption and a claim of total recall, a mind seized and arrested on a single unprocessed moment, the demo state itself mirroring memory that refuses to move forward.","sonnet":"A fragment so skeletal it barely qualifies as a lyric — the entire text cycles between \"Wait!\" and \"I remember it all,\" suggesting a mind seized by urgent, unprocessed memory, unable to move past a single arrested moment.","src":"sonnet"},
"celldweller~it-makes-no-difference-who-we-are": {"haiku":"Wishes cast upon a powerful star will be granted regardless of identity or location; cosmic forces fulfill desire without judgment.","opus":"A hypnotic prayer to a Blackstar, wishing on something distant and impersonal that grants desires regardless of who asks. It captures the yearning of collective hope pinned to an indifferent power, faith aimed at a machine or a star that simply delivers.","sonnet":"About the democratic, equalizing power of wishing on something cosmic and unknowable — whoever you are, whatever your status, the Blackstar grants wishes without discrimination, and the song holds this as a kind of consolation.","src":"haiku","fable":"The thematic keystone of Klayton's long-gestating concept album, this is the wish itself stated as ritual — the Blackstar functioning as a dark wishing-star, a source you petition without knowing or caring what it is. The whole song lives in a single structural move: verse one is one voice waiting alone while unnamed powers orchestrate the granting, and the final chorus swaps that solitary I for a we, insisting it makes no difference who we are. What sounds like reassurance curdles into something colder — the wish is guaranteed precisely because the wisher has been made anonymous and interchangeable, the individual dissolved into a crowd all bargaining at the same indifferent light. The refrain that no price is too great is the tell: this is faustian machinery dressed as a lullaby, granting everything on the condition that the self asking no longer matters.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The refrain has the warmth of an old wishing-song, and that is exactly what it is: it lifts the Disney line that it makes no difference who you are almost word for word, then re-tunes it — here the line rings true because on a blackstar the wisher no longer matters at all.","themes":["faith & the occult","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"wish granted by dissolving the wisher into an anonymous crowd","built":"final chorus swaps solitary I for we as the price of the bargain"},
"celldweller~jericho": {"haiku":"An arrogant person builds protective walls around themselves, feeling safe behind their own self-importance, only to watch it all collapse when those defenses inevitably fail. The song treats their fall as inevitable consequence of their own inflated ego.","opus":"A warning to an arrogant, self-important person who has walled themselves off in a sense of supremacy and false humility; the song relishes the coming collapse of that pride, comparing their downfall to the walls of Jericho crumbling.","sonnet":"An arrogant, self-important figure who has built walls around themselves for protection is told those walls will inevitably collapse, and everyone will watch them fall.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hymn of schadenfreude aimed at the tyrant who mistook his fortifications for permanence, borrowing the Old Testament rout of Jericho so that hubris, not any besieging army, is what topples the fortress. Klayton keeps the target deliberately vague, scaling it to any despot, and tightens the screw across three verses: the same figure climbs higher into arrogance while the sky darkens then erupts and the soil buckles then splits open beneath his heels. What lifts it past a tidy morality tale is a narrator who drops the prophetic distance to admit he wants front-row seats, turning the reckoning into a spectacle the crowd has gathered to relish. The whispered refrain that it is over reads less as lament than as a countdown everyone below has been itching to start.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","faith & the occult","politics & society"],"means":"schadenfreude at the tyrant whose hubris topples his own fortress","built":"narrator drops prophetic distance to admit wanting front-row seats"},
"celldweller~jericho-circle-of-dust-remix":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic collapse of walls through divine intervention; fortified systems crumble under spiritual assault.","opus":"An old-testament reckoning aimed at the arrogant and self-deified, the fortified walls of false humility and supremacy destined to collapse like Jericho while the narrator relishes the fall.","sonnet":"Arrogance fortified behind high walls is addressed with cold patience: the walls will fall like Jericho, self-importance will be amplified right up until the moment it goes down, and the watching crowd is already looking forward to the show — the biblical collapse is updated as a siege of certainty meeting its inevitable structural limit.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~just-like-you": {"haiku":"Desperate isolation and collapse: the speaker faces the night alone, unable to find meaning or escape the internal storm despite yearning for something transcendent.","opus":"The industrial project voices a despairing figure at his limit, alone with his thoughts in the dead hours, praying to silence the violence in his own head and simply survive as his hopes scatter. The recurring assurance of being just like the listener finds fragile kinship, two people watching their dreams disintegrate side by side.","sonnet":"A song about hitting bottom in isolation — the speaker stands at the edge of total emotional collapse, dreams failing and faith absent, reaching for some divine or human connection and finding solidarity in the shared feeling of breaking apart.","src":"haiku","fable":"A panic attack scored as arena electro-rock: cornered at three in the morning, the singer begs some higher answer to quiet the violence looping inside his skull while gale-force gusts scatter every ambition he owned. The wordless vowel hook does the real confessing, a stranded howl of you and I that finally buckles into recognition when he admits his own coming-apart mirrors yours exactly. Klayton drives the whole track toward that closing admission, converting a private breakdown into a shared one and making the sleepless solitude the thing that binds two people rather than seals them off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"private breakdown converted into recognition of shared coming-apart","built":"wordless vowel hook does the confessing the verses cannot say directly"},
"celldweller~lost-in-time": {"haiku":"Someone feels trapped in directionless stasis, unable to solve their internal problems or escape a sense of being adrift, fearing they will remain lost and unchanged forever.","opus":"A study of paralysis before one's own life, the narrator feeling like an accused man with no offense and a brilliant mind gone to waste. Frozen while awaiting guidance that never arrives, he prays for rescue as his years quietly drain away, caught between taking action and endless waiting.","sonnet":"A person stuck in paralysis, unable to move forward without direction yet knowing that waiting will let their whole life pass them by — circular helplessness that may never resolve.","src":"haiku","fable":"Celldweller turns a stalled creator's paralysis into an industrial anthem: the narrator carries gifts he cannot spend, an intelligence humming with nothing to think about and a guilt fastened to no offense he can name. What corrodes him is deferral itself, the sense that every hour spent awaiting some outside signal is an hour that quietly vanishes, and his real terror is that this suspended state is no passing season but a permanent climate he will drown inside for good. Klayton offers drift braided with dread instead of resolution, his plea for rescue circling back unanswered while the hooks keep hammering one unmoving note.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"deferral itself named as the permanent climate he will drown inside","built":"hooks hammer one unmoving note while the plea for rescue circles unanswered"},
"celldweller~louder-than-words": {"haiku":"A demand for authentic action over hollow promises and verbal deception. The speaker is tired of insincere apologies and wants to see genuine change demonstrated, not spoken.","opus":"A seething brush-off of a compulsive liar whose hollow apologies and ever-changing tales carry no weight. The singer leans on the old proverb about conduct outweighing talk, ordering the person to be quiet and demonstrate their worth through deeds instead.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a habitual liar whose apologies and stories keep shifting, the speaker insisting that only actions matter — words have become meaningless noise.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a demand for proof aimed at someone whose apologies have curdled into reflex, a partner the singer no longer trusts enough to keep parsing. Klayton lets the industrial-metal aggression carry the argument itself, the barked refusal drowning out a manipulator rather than answering excuses that only breed more excuses. One image tips it toward the apocalyptic, poisoned air and birds dropping from the sky, as if one person's habitual dishonesty were fouling the atmosphere everyone breathes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"demanding proof from someone whose apologies have become reflex","built":"one person's dishonesty scaled to poisoned air and falling birds"},
"celldweller~memories-of-a-girl-i-haven-t-met":{"haiku":"A lonely person yearns for someone they have never encountered, wishing upon distant stars that they might somehow meet across time and space.","opus":"A rare quiet, wistful moment in an otherwise aggressive catalogue, set in a hushed solitude filled with thoughts of a stranger the narrator can only imagine. He pictures her, wonders if she's awake somewhere thinking of him too, and turns his loneliness into a starward, unfulfilled ache rather than sorrow.","sonnet":"In silence and solitude, a man reaches toward someone who does not yet exist in his life — imagining a woman who might already be thinking of him the same way, both of them wishing on the same distant stars before they have found each other.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~my-disintegration":{"haiku":"Addictive attraction to someone pulls toward self-annihilation, where the more they draw near, the more they lose themselves to the other's gravity.","opus":"Klayton's industrial-electronic project stages obsession as gravitational ruin, a seductive haze pulling the singer toward a fire that drains his life the closer he gets. Rescue and ruin bleed into one dissolving self. In line with Celldweller's cinematic, glitchy sound, it frames addiction and allure as an orbit spiraling into total wipeout, blissful obliviousness steering him toward a destination that promises he'll be left with nothing.","sonnet":"An obsessive gravitational pull toward a person or force that the narrator knows is consuming him. Like an orbit that cannot break free of a star, he circles closer, resistance dissolving, identity unraveling — recognizing the siren pull as annihilation and being powerless to stop it.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~my-disintegration-hard-box-remix":{"haiku":"An irresistible attraction draws the speaker toward someone who unravels their identity with seductive gravity, each step closer a loss of self as they dissolve into the gravitational pull of another.","opus":"An addiction to a person or a high framed as gravity you can't escape, a siren whose seduction dissolves you the closer you draw; you feel yourself being taken apart and want it anyway.","sonnet":"The narrator knows the attraction is destroying him — \"siren sings a song divine\" names the pull as mythically lethal — yet the closer he gets, the more of himself is stolen and the less he can resist. Addiction and desire are collapsed into a single word he both suffers and worships: disintegration.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~new-elysium":{"haiku":"Calls for a new utopian kingdom built by young generations rising against despair and forgetting their worth. The song urges unified voices to create paradise and transcend mortal limitations.","opus":"A summons for the young to join their voices and forge a fresh world, cast as crossing into a promised paradise once the old regime falls. It grieves how people have lost sight of their own value and pushes them to rise and dismantle a tyrannical power.","sonnet":"A generational anthem calling for a new order to replace a world that has forgotten its own worth — the young are framed as chosen voices who must tear down an oppressive empire and build a new Elysium in its place before time runs out entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"An anthem that lifts the cadences of scripture and the graveside rite only to wrench the promised paradise out of God's hands and into the crowd's, which is why Klayton names the classical afterlife rather than the Christian heaven — a place the living seize instead of inherit. The verses are one long accusation, doubling back on a single wound: a people so busy forgetting their own value that they damn themselves before any judgment could. Then Klayton flips the machine's usual dread into a call to arms, the young rising to topple a tyrant's regime and merge their separate throats into one sound, so the ending the chorus keeps heralding reads not as apocalypse but as the chosen demolition of the old order. The final for-me-for-you shrinks the cosmic scale to a pact between two people stepping through a door, insisting this deliverance is taken, never handed down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"A chapter of End of an Empire, Klayton's rise-and-fall concept album — the seized paradise here is the young generation's answer to the collapsing kingdom of the title track.","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","faith & the occult","freedom & escape"],"means":"deliverance framed as seized rather than inherited from above","built":"classical afterlife in the title displaces Christian heaven to claim agency for the living"},
"celldweller~offworld":{"haiku":"Escape from human corruption and soul parasites into a new realm or reality.","opus":"An escapist invitation to unplug from the drain of soul-parasites and slip away with someone to a new sight and sound, offworld for the night, shutting down the noise of the real world. It's brief and hypnotic, more mood than narrative. Celldweller, the project of Klayton blending electronic and industrial rock, builds it on chanted repetition, the promise of escape carried more by texture than by any elaborate story.","sonnet":"A two-person escape fantasy — leaving the soul-draining world behind tonight, unplugging from everything, finding a new sight and sound somewhere beyond reach of the parasites. It's minimalist and urgent, the destination undefined but the desire to go absolutely clear.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~one-good-reason": {"haiku":"Suicidal despair and anger at those who claimed to care but failed to provide meaning or support. The narrator challenges them to give a reason to live.","opus":"An angry reckoning with someone who told the narrator not to die yet couldn't offer a single good reason to live, having lied to and manipulated him. He berates himself for trusting a liar and pushes the person out of his way.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who warned the speaker against suicidal thoughts but offered no real reason to stay alive, and who turned out to be a liar. The speaker is consumed by betrayal and demands the one good reason that was promised but never delivered.","src":"haiku","fable":"A young Klayton turns an argument with a would-be comforter into an indictment: the speaker demands proof that staying alive is worth it and gets only rehearsed concern, so the comforter becomes the accused. What sharpens it past a generic outburst is the betrayal underneath — the speaker took the sermon about honesty to heart, then caught the preacher in the very dishonesty being warned against, which voids any standing to intervene. From his early industrial-rock era, the track rides a clean-verse-to-crushing-chorus swing into a bridge where a hushed counter-voice slips beneath the rage, before the outro degrades into a stuttering, glitched loop of the same plea, the machine jamming on its own request until it can only shriek. The refusal to resolve is the whole design: no answer arrives because none is coming, and the endless repetition enacts the vertigo of being ordered to live by someone who won't name a purpose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"demanding proof of life from someone already discredited","built":"glitched outro loop enacts the unanswered question in real time"},
"celldweller~one-good-reason-drumcorps-remix":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation persists despite others' pleading, with the speaker demanding justification for continued existence when none can be provided.","opus":"Rage at a supposed friend who betrayed a confession, someone who preached against wanting to die yet could offer no reason to keep living. The wound is less the death wish than the discovery that the trusted confidant was the liar all along.","sonnet":"An ultimatum directed at someone who tells the narrator not to want to die but can't supply a single argument worth keeping: \"you tell me I shouldn't want to die but can't give me one good reason why.\" The betrayal of having been lied to by the one person he confided in strips the last reason to listen.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~own-little-world": {"haiku":"Escape into fantasy and isolation from a cruel, deceptive world. In this private realm, the narrator is free from pain and external judgment.","opus":"A retreat into a private inner world as escape from people who exploit and break others down, promising the world only to take it away. Inside this self-made refuge the narrator breathes freely, does as he wants, and imagines never dying.","sonnet":"A fantasy of retreat from a world dominated by people who exploit and deceive, into a private inner space where time does not exist, no one makes demands, and the speaker is free to be and do anything. The tone shifts from resentment toward others to joy in the imagined refuge.","src":"haiku","fable":"A defiant retreat dressed as victory: hounded by people who pledge him everything and then strip it away, the singer seals himself inside a private frequency where the very air belongs to him alone and no outside voice can smear the picture. Klayton built this solo debut almost single-handedly, one machine stacked on another, so the fantasy of a sealed room with no clocks and no permission slips reads as the one-man-studio ethic hammered into an anthem. The chant that carries it out keeps looping the same vow of permanence, and that refusal to stop is the argument, a barricade held by sheer repetition against everyone told to shove their rulebook somewhere unlit — until the possessive quietly widens, the hermit turning at the door of his sealed world to wave someone else inside.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"barricade held open just enough to let one person in","built":"looping chant of permanence is the argument itself"},
"celldweller~own-little-world-drop-remix":{"haiku":"Two outcasts retreat into a private realm outside social judgment where time dissolves and death becomes irrelevant, finding sanctuary in a world built for those the system breaks.","opus":"A withdrawal into a private realm shielded from others' judgments, manipulations and demands for justification, escapism as a defiant refuge where time holds no sway and the outcasts endure forever.","sonnet":"Two people (or a tribe of outsiders) construct a private world where the rules and timelines of the outside don't apply — \"time doesn't exist here, we will never die.\" The speaker rejects the dominant culture's standards with contempt (\"stick your standards where the sun doesn't shine\") and the shared interior space becomes the only place worth inhabiting.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~own-little-world-remixed-by-blue-stahli":{"haiku":"Isolation becomes sanctuary, with the speaker constructing a private realm of refuge.","opus":"A retreat into a personal domain where the atmosphere, the passing hours and the laws all belong to the singer, sealed off from people who pledge everything then snatch it back. It turns from guarded solitude into an offer, extending to someone else a shared sanctuary of endless survival.","sonnet":"A rejection of everyone who promises the world then takes it away, in favor of an inner refuge where the air is one's own, time doesn't exist, and you never die. The fantasy is pitched as sane withdrawal rather than delusion — \"stick your standards where the sun doesn't shine\" — and the invitation to share it converts isolation into chosen community.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~precious-one": {"haiku":"A divine figure guides the narrator toward death and transformation; they surrender to embrace passing through life into a new existence.","opus":"A near-death or death experience rendered as a tender, seductive passage: a divine female figure whispers reassurance, embraces the narrator, and leads him through 'Death's Door' to a new life; wondering whether to be afraid, he ultimately lets go and willingly opens the door, framing death as rebirth rather than end.","sonnet":"A song about dying and crossing over — a feminine divine presence guides the narrator through death's door with reassurance, the passage rendered as a homecoming into amplified existence rather than an ending.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is dying imagined as being carried rather than lost: a maternal figure of the divine gathers the frightened subject and whispers him across the threshold into what she names a beginning, not an ending. The song's whole tension lives in the flinch before that crossing, the twice-asked wondering whether a person can walk into the unlit room without terror, and its answer is a fear that unclenches into willingness—he takes the key and opens the way himself. Celldweller is Klayton's one-man welding of industrial metal to cinematic electronics, and he stages the passage as sensory flood, every nerve sharpening and the room around him brightening as her mercy pulls him out of this life, so that leaving registers as an amplified arrival.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"fear of the threshold unclenching into willingness to open the way himself","built":"sensory flood of sharpening nerves and brightening room stages leaving as amplified arrival"},
"celldweller~shapeshifter": {"haiku":"High-speed transformation and evasion become lifestyle; the speaker shifts identities at racing velocity, never caught, always ahead of the game.","opus":"A high-octane street-race boast, the shapeshifter as an unreal, unbeatable ghost outrunning cops with no brakes and pure petrol bravado. It's speed and transformation as swagger, nothing beneath the chrome but the thrill.","sonnet":"A boastful, adrenaline-soaked street-racing fantasy — the narrator is uncatchable by police, faster than anyone, dominating every challenger through sheer speed and aggression, positioning themselves as an unstoppable force that can shapeshift out of any situation.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a street-race dare built as a Celldweller-and-Styles-of-Beyond collision, where a stoplight countdown detonates into pure horsepower boast and the driver taunts the police falling away in his mirrors. The title turns the wheelman into someone who refuses to hold a single form, morphing between vehicle and body so fast that the challenger can never lock onto an outline to chase down. Klayton laces the hip-hop swagger through industrial rock, then lets the closing chant thin out into melting and yielding as pursuit collapses and the racer slips whatever cage his rival tried to build. The point is reinvention dressed as a nitro duel: you can't beat what won't stay one shape long enough to be caught.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape","night & the city"],"means":"reinvention dressed as a nitro duel no pursuer can finish","built":"closing chant thins from horsepower boast into melting and yielding"},
"celldweller~shapeshifter-feat-styles-of-beyond": {"haiku":"A high-speed pursuer evades capture through constant transformation and boundary-pushing, racing ahead with unmatched skill and defiance.","opus":"A high-octane street-racing boast in the Mad Max mold: the shapeshifting road warrior taunts cops and rivals, floors the pedal, and threatens to leave challengers eating dust or in a casket; the whole track revels in speed, transformation, and dominance behind the wheel.","sonnet":"A street racing and dominance fantasy — burning through traffic, outrunning cops, destroying challengers, the whole thing framed as a high-octane display of speed and adaptability.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a street-race dare built as a Celldweller-and-Styles-of-Beyond collision, where a stoplight countdown detonates into pure horsepower boast and the driver taunts the police falling away in his mirrors. The title turns the wheelman into someone who refuses to hold a single form, morphing between vehicle and body so fast that the challenger can never lock onto an outline to chase down. Klayton laces the hip-hop swagger through industrial rock, then lets the closing chant thin out into melting and yielding as pursuit collapses and the racer slips whatever cage his rival tried to build. The point is reinvention dressed as a nitro duel: you can't beat what won't stay one shape long enough to be caught.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape","night & the city"],"means":"reinvention dressed as a nitro duel no pursuer can finish","built":"closing chant thins from horsepower boast into melting and yielding"},
"celldweller~so-long-sentiment": {"haiku":"The speaker, alone and tortured by memories, rejects sentiment and emotion as they prepare to erase themselves to escape the crushing weight of their mind.","opus":"A grinding portrait of depressive rumination, the narrator drowning in memory while trying to convince himself the feeling no longer matters. The industrial churn mirrors a mind torturing itself, sinking rather than surfacing despite the defiant refrain.","sonnet":"A person trying to sever themselves from crippling memories and emotional attachment — declaring feeling itself to be the enemy — while simultaneously drowning in isolation, obsessive thought loops, and the pull toward self-erasure.","src":"haiku","fable":"Klayton stages a bitter farewell to his own capacity for feeling, dismissing both a departed partner and sentiment itself as if going numb were the only survivable move after the break. The industrial-rock build enacts the collapse he narrates: memory arrives like inhaled smoke, guilt sits as dead weight low in his gut while his spine stays upright, and the turn from missing her to erasing himself converts the dismissal into a smothered plea to be let out. Each chant that none of it registers wears thinner the more he insists, so the pose of indifference finally reads as the last handhold a man grabs before grief drags him under.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"pose of indifference wearing thinner the harder it insists","built":"industrial collapse enacts the numbness that the lyrics keep announcing"},
"celldweller~so-long-sentiment-metal-revision-by-paul-udarov":{"haiku":"The speaker drowns in echoing memories and self-torture, unable to escape the crushing weight of thought and abandonment, bidding farewell to sentiment as the only path through isolation.","opus":"Depression's crushing weight and the loop of intrusive memories, ending in a numbed dismissal of feeling itself; the false relief of being finally alone barely covers how much is being erased.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped inside their own looping grief — memories inhaled \"like a breath of fire sent from hell,\" echoes in empty rooms telling them to erase themselves — performs a bitter goodbye to feeling itself: \"so long sentiment, it doesn't matter now.\" The \"I'll miss you\" that keeps interrupting the dismissal is the song's gut: the sentiment refuses to leave even as it's being buried.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~so-sorry-to-say": {"haiku":"A trapped person contradicts themselves—wanting someone gone and close simultaneously—lost and unable to understand how they ended up imprisoned and conflicted.","opus":"The push-pull of a relationship you sabotage even as you cling to it, staged as the electro-rock isolation Klayton favored: waking up trapped in a cell you built, begging someone to leave and stay in the same breath, unable to explain why you wreck what you need.","sonnet":"A person trapped in profound ambivalence toward someone they love — simultaneously needing them to leave and desperately wanting them to stay, unable to understand how they ended up in this paralysis, waking each day in a mental cell of their own making.","src":"haiku","fable":"It is a dissociative breakup song, an apology offered blind for harm the speaker cannot understand, addressed to the person he keeps pushing away without ever choosing to. Klayton narrates waking inside his own cruelty like a locked room he never entered, unable to locate the moment the wound was made. When the words give out, the plea just alternates between banishing and clinging, each repetition tightening the trap rather than resolving it, so the industrial churn enacts the ambivalence it cannot argue its way out of.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"apologizing blind for harm the speaker cannot locate in himself","built":"banishing and clinging alternate without resolving as the industrial churn enacts the impasse"},
"celldweller~soul-parasites":{"haiku":"Predatory entities drain life force and agency, with spiritual corruption rendering the host unable to resist or escape consumptive violation.","opus":"A relationship or system framed as a parasite feeding on the speaker's soul, taking every piece he gives until he lives in subjugation and isolation. It widens to indict forces that claim to unite people while tearing them apart, making them feel alive while they bleed from the heart.","sonnet":"An entity — institutional, religious, or relational — that demands total devotion while bleeding the narrator dry is named a soul parasite: \"the more I give to you the more I need.\" The chorus asks why a space claiming to be full of love produces only hate, and the answer implied is that devotion itself is the mechanism of extraction.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~stay-with-me-unlikely": {"haiku":"Mental deterioration and desperate plea for companionship against the odds.","opus":"A song about a mind at war with itself, tormented by internalized voices that crucify him, begging someone to stay and lead him into denial even as he contradictorily pushes them away, doubting he will ever heal.","sonnet":"Besieged by a hostile inner world and the ticking-clock terror of limited time, the speaker begs someone to stay — not out of strength but as a plea for temporary refuge from their own mind — while simultaneously insisting recovery is unlikely.","src":"haiku","fable":"A plea that keeps undoing itself: the speaker begs a presence to remain while insisting in the same breath he needs nothing from it, so the whole song runs on a self-cancelling loop. Klayton, building Celldweller's debut as a one-man fusion of industrial metal and drum-and-bass, opens on a paranoid image of being hauled toward his own crucifixion by a mind that accuses him in his own voice, then hands the softer refrain to a second vocalist whose comfort he cannot let land. The muttered verdict that healing will not come reads less as despair than as a flat refusal to be consoled, each tender line trailed by a one-word dismissal until the tenderness itself curdles.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The plea to stay lands plainly enough on its own, until you reach its later companion, Unlikely (Stay With Me), which runs the very same words in reverse — audio and sentiment alike — until stay with me arrives as its own opposite.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind","love & desire"],"means":"begging for comfort while refusing to let it land","built":"second vocalist's tenderness trailed by one-word dismissals"},
"celldweller~stay-with-me-unlikely-toronto-is-broken-remix":{"haiku":"Mental self-sabotage and internal enemies demand escape through connection; the speaker pleads for companionship to resist crucifixion by their own mind.","opus":"A fractured plea against a persecuting mind, the narrator sick of his own mental enemies and their games while begging someone not to leave him alone to the crucifixion his thoughts keep staging.","sonnet":"A mind at war with itself, mental enemies pushing back, the refrain stay with me as a desperate request directed at a person, a self, or both. The original song's emotional core — don't leave, I need you, I'm sick of the games my head plays — gets fractured and looped by the remix into something more frantic and fragmented, the plea repeating as if the answer keeps being lost.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~switchback":{"haiku":"Regrets a choice made with lasting consequences from which there is no turning back or escape.","opus":"A song about regretting a choice that can never be undone, the narrator realizing there's no returning to who he was and no way to recover what he gave up. He's bound to a single decision that has permanently altered him.","sonnet":"The narrator is trapped by an irreversible choice he regrets — chained to something he cannot reclaim — and the song turns on the impossibility of going back to who he was before.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is about irreversibility, and Klayton builds the whole track so its form enacts the trap it describes — the title is a rail term for a zigzag of track that can only reverse direction, and the lyric concedes no route exists to undo a choice already committed. What little narrative there is gets deliberately starved down to one unforgettable image the narrator can't scrub from memory, so what fills the vacuum is the machinery of regret itself: the glitching Go! commands, the panicked interjection wondering how the deadline could already have passed, the chorus shackled to what can't be reclaimed and looping with no exit. The one real shift is the rap bridge, where terror curdles into aggression — the persona announcing he's no longer afraid, aiming that same finality outward as a threat — so the song's true movement runs from being jailed by consequence to weaponizing the fact that nobody gets a rewind.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"Klayton called it the most expensive song he ever shelved: roughly fifty demos across three years, including a scrapped version that cost him $11,500 — a song about irreversible choices that he kept remaking.","themes":["shame & repression","madness & the mind","anger & defiance"],"means":"consequence weaponized once escape proves impossible","built":"the rail term in the title names the track's own loop — reversal built into the form"},
"celldweller~switchback-dead-by-dawn-remix-by-no-i-m-not":{"haiku":"The path reverses toward death as dawn approaches like execution. Switching back becomes impossible; only forward toward conclusion remains.","opus":"The dread of an irreversible decision, the narrator bound to a regret he can never take back, forced to live with an image he can't erase and no route to undo it.","sonnet":"An irreversible choice has been made — \"it's too late to look back, I've got no way to switch back\" — and the narrator is chained to consequences they can't reclaim, the price too high, the regret too total, the self permanently altered.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~switchback-remixed-by-klayton":{"haiku":"Reversal and turning back offer escape from predatory trajectories, with redirection as survival mechanism.","opus":"The panic of an irreversible decision — a regret with no undo, the singer bound to something he can't reclaim and certain he's permanently altered. The remix layers on a swaggering boast, but the heart of it is the dread of realizing the moment to act has passed.","sonnet":"A choice made and instantly regretted with no mechanism for reversal: the narrator is chained to what he can't reclaim, the picture won't leave his mind, and \"it's too late to look back\" is both the trap and the refrain. The rap break adds a cocky veneer that only underscores the irreversibility.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~symbiont": {"haiku":"Two people remain trapped in a codependent, toxic relationship despite hating each other, unable to survive apart yet destroying each other.","opus":"A toxic codependency rendered as parasitism, two people who hate each other yet cannot survive apart, each calling the other expendable while clinging tighter. Need curdled into mutual contempt on a thin line neither will cross.","sonnet":"A parasitic relationship the narrator cannot escape — they hate the other person, the other person controls and withholds, and yet they need each other so completely that leaving is impossible, just an endless dance on a thin line of mutual dependency and contempt.","src":"haiku","fable":"A codependent stalemate rendered as biology: the title borrows the parasite-host word for a bond that has fused into the self, leaving the narrator unable to recall who he was before it colonized him. Klayton builds a perfect symmetry of resentment, flipping one couplet so each partner's craving mirrors the other exactly, while the enemy-kiss image admits the loathing and the clinging are a single gesture. The bridge is where the era's industrial machinery earns its keep: a sneering second voice invades the mix to jeer that he is disposable, externalizing the abuser (or the addiction) as a glitching taunt he cannot delete. By the outro the track dissolves into pure oscillation, spite and reliance traded back and forth until the words wear smooth through repetition, the refusal to resolve becoming the trap itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","identity & becoming"],"means":"resentment and clinging staged as a single inseparable gesture","built":"sneering second voice invades the mix to externalize the abuser"},
"celldweller~tainted":{"haiku":"A woman alone at the edge of the world faces assault and decay, finally finding solace in surrendering to her captivity.","opus":"Klayton's project centers on a girl ground down by cruelty and hidden shames, exposed and utterly alone as each new betrayal marks her. The unsettling twist beneath the electronic surge is that her eventual surrender and emotional deadening feel, wrongly, like a kind of relief.","sonnet":"A woman worn down by a world that has used her, kept her secrets buried, and slowly drained her into isolated numbness finally reaches something that registers — barely — as home. The accumulated damage is framed as external imposition rather than character, and the last image of coming home offers neither resolution nor rescue, only recognition.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~the-best-it-s-gonna-get":{"haiku":"A person's perfect world collapses and they're told this disaster is the best they'll experience, forced to accept hopelessness. The song dismisses their aspirations as overrated complications they must abandon.","opus":"A scornful wake-up call to someone whose tidy little life has just been punctured by reality. The narrator ridicules their drifting, noncommittal plans and tells them, coldly, that this letdown is as good as things will ever be and it's already too late to fix it.","sonnet":"A cold, sardonic wake-up call to someone who has been waiting for their idealized life to materialize — the narrator tells them to stop dreaming, accept that the current situation is the ceiling, and either act now or waste what little remains.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~the-end-of-the-world":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic collapse brings strange peace and liberation, with the dissolution of all structures releasing the speaker from prior constraints.","opus":"A weary refusal to accept that everything is truly ending, even as everyone seems to be lying and forsaking one another. Sold a dream of a serene future, the speaker keeps asking whether anyone else feels the same dread, unwilling to concede the world is actually finishing.","sonnet":"A father's despair at a world that sold a serene future and delivered something burning: liars everywhere, the earth shaking, evidence being desecrated while its destroyers smile. The repeated refusal — \"I won't believe it's the end of the world\" — is resistance by assertion in the absence of any other tool, followed by the invitation to flee to an offworld built for those who can't stomach what remains.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~the-last-firstborn": {"haiku":"The narrator grapples with death by suicide, questioning his own courage and imagining the darkness of his final moments while wishing things had ended differently.","opus":"A grieving sibling reckons with a brother's death and an act he can't believe, hearing his voice as an echo and imagining following him to death's door; the recurring vision of a body on the ground and a beckoning shadow ties the family's fate to violence and the wish that it hadn't ended this way.","sonnet":"A firstborn son grapples with a brother's apparent suicide, feeling drawn toward the same fate — he hears his brother's voice beckoning him, wonders whether he has the courage to cross that threshold, and wishes desperately it had ended differently.","src":"haiku","fable":"A suicide note arrives from a brother, and the survivor spends the track trying to metabolize it, swinging between disbelief, a dread rehearsal of following him, and the terrible arrival at understanding why he quit. Celldweller's Klayton fuses industrial metal with orchestral scoring and a keening, wordless chant so the mourning plays as ritual rather than confession, the dead man reduced to a shape on wet ground while the reek of gasoline hangs over every refrain. What tightens the knot is inheritance: the survivor sees the same doom replaying in another firstborn like himself, so the closing surrender to fate reads not as peace but as a man realizing the same exit has been waiting for him all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","death & grief","family & blood"],"means":"grief metabolized as the recognition of inherited doom","built":"orchestral-chant scoring frames mourning as ritual not confession"},
"celldweller~the-last-firstborn-remixed-by-klayton":{"haiku":"The final first arrives as paradox unfolds through technological distortion. Birth and death merge in remixed transcendence.","opus":"A brother's grief and inheritance of despair, the narrator following a lost sibling toward the same fatal edge, questioning whether he has the nerve and whether death's door will welcome him in.","sonnet":"A brother grappling with the suicide of a sibling — the smell of farewell and gasoline, the body face down, waiting to take his own turn to bleed like \"a kid playing with a razor blade\" — and the haunting refrain \"I wish it didn't end this way\" is addressed to the firstborn who left, as the survivor feels what he felt and cannot unknow it.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~the-lucky-one": {"haiku":"The speaker is crushed by misfortune, stuck under God's thumb like a cosmic joke. Dreams fail and problems multiply in a string of bad luck.","opus":"Bitterly sarcastic industrial self-portrait of a man dogged by Murphy's Law, calling himself lucky as everything sinks; the joke's on him, and his defiant refusal to believe in luck is the only footing he has left.","sonnet":"A blackly comic catalogue of unrelenting bad luck and failure, where the narrator's only consolation is a stubborn refusal to believe in luck itself — treating the cosmic joke of his life as dark comedy rather than tragedy.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grinning victory lap for a life that keeps failing him: debts come due, a wishbone lodged in the throat, dreams that die before arriving, all narrated as if a prize had just been won. Klayton runs the whole thing as sarcasm held at a boil, each triumphant chant curdling into the punchline that the joke is on the singer, so he laughs before the room can. The bridge drops the mask for real defiance, ramming headfirst through the muck until he wedges, then landing on the one creed that is truly his: he grants fortune no credit at all, which quietly voids the title's whole premise. By the outro that bitterness turns generous, dealing the curse outward until everyone shares it — gallows cheer reframed, over Wish Upon A Blackstar's grinding synth-metal, as the only workable way to keep pushing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","comedy & wordplay","freedom & escape"],"means":"laughing at bad luck before the room can laugh at you","built":"bridge drops the sarcasm to void the title's premise entirely"},
"celldweller~the-seven-sisters":{"haiku":"A child's wish upon the stars and Pleiades goes unfulfilled, leaving them in endless waiting and hope.","opus":"Celldweller's Klayton sets aside industrial aggression for something orchestral and melancholy, recalling a father's advice to make a wish and then wait a lifetime for it. Addressing distant constellations, the narrator hopes the stars themselves might descend to fulfill a childhood dream, yet finds himself still waiting in the dark, holding onto a promise and a hope worth protecting even as the sky stays silent.","sonnet":"A child taught to wish upon stars carries that posture of waiting into adulthood, addressing constellation after constellation in the dark — Orion, Saturn's rings, the Pleiades — and receiving nothing back. The tenderness of the remembered instruction sits against the long silence that has followed it.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~the-stars-of-orion": {"haiku":"Escape imperative repeated urgently—go far away from here, discontinue and stand by—conveys desperate longing for elsewhere.","opus":"A largely textless electronic interlude, its looped plea to go somewhere far away and clipped machine warnings evoking escape and system failure rather than a lyric. Atmosphere over statement.","sonnet":"A stripped-down, minimalist yearning to escape — the lyrics are almost entirely a repeated desire to go somewhere far away from here, interrupted only by an automated \"discontinue, stand by\" command, evoking dissociation or a shutdown from an unbearable present.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a track built entirely from the urge to be anywhere else, one wish for distance hammered until it stops being a sentence and becomes a pulse. A clipped machine command interrupts the loop, staging that flight against a system whose only reply is a shutdown order rather than any release. Klayton's early industrial instinct shows here: the human yearning and the cold automated refusal share one soundscape, so the endlessly returning line is escape failing in real time, exhaustion itself becoming the meaning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","surveillance & control"],"means":"escape failing in real time as the machine answers with shutdown","built":"human yearning and cold automated refusal share one soundscape"},
"celldweller~the-way-she-wants-to-die-2005-demo":{"haiku":"A lover's final request materializes through intimate darkness as whispered words become permission for transcendence. Death becomes gift when wrapped in tenderness and understanding.","opus":"A tender, uneasy early demo about a woman who finds relief only in a frozen moment of closeness, feeling truly alive because it resembles a death she has chosen, an escape from an existence of constant torment.","sonnet":"A woman screaming silently through a life she describes as crucifixion finds her one moment of transcendence in complete surrender to intimacy — candlelight, two lips pressed to her head, time dissolved — and the way she wants to die is exactly this: intertwined, away from mourning, finally alive.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~tragedy":{"haiku":"Isolation and lovelessness create tragedy; someone drowns in loneliness, burning with need for touch while sinking deeper into despair.","opus":"A cover of the Bee Gees standard about heartbreak so total it feels like the loss of self, love gone and nothing left to steer by. The heart of it is crushing loneliness, a life stripped of anyone to hold, waking to sorrow with no clear cause.","sonnet":"A cover of the Bee Gees song — a person devastated by loneliness and the absence of love, drowning in a grey part of town, unable to go on without someone to hold.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~uncrowned":{"haiku":"Celebrity crashes into anonymity and drowning as ego-weight eventually pulls the famous down; the song savors this reversal, celebrating forgotten names and the isolation of social erasure.","opus":"A cold satisfaction watching an arrogant favorite fall, the ego crown that once lifted them now dragging them down with no hands to help. Schadenfreude toward fickle fame, earned through the narrator's own lonely years.","sonnet":"A cold, satisfied watch as someone who wronged the narrator comes crashing down from fame and power — every lonely night the singer endured was worth it just to see that fall.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~under-my-feet": {"haiku":"The speaker feels trapped by self-doubt and internal decay, unable to ground themselves or achieve their wants.","opus":"A wrestle with self-inflicted pain and inner rot, the narrator rotting from within and unable to lift himself up. When the address turns from himself to a doomed adversary facing the end by themselves, it becomes clear he has been fighting a destructive part of his own nature, and is finally beating it.","sonnet":"Self-recrimination and stagnation: the narrator is dying from the inside out, can't get off the ground, knows that wanting something isn't enough to make it happen, and keeps imagining connection while being unable to reach it.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a paralysis song staged as a fight with gravity, where the singer keeps trying to climb up and keeps sliding back beneath his own weight, the enemy always underneath and always himself. Its honesty is that the confession turns inward and admits its own bad faith: a pep talk to yourself carries no obligation, and craving a thing was never proof you would carry it out, so the sickness he diagnoses is inertia wearing the costume of longing. On Klayton's self-released debut the industrial-rock frame — brittle electronics under a melodic rock ache — lets the wordless refrains hang like a hollow, taunting chorus where an uplift should sit. The outro then flips the mask, spitting the opening lines back in second person at a fool who talked too much and now goes under by himself, and the sting is that the self-loathing has looped so tight you cannot tell whether he curses another man or watches his own descent.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"self-loathing looped so tight accuser and accused are one","built":"outro flips opening lines to second person to curse the same self"},
"celldweller~unlikely-stay-with-me": {"opus":"Built from reversed and fractured pleas, it stages the split between wanting someone to stay and knowing they shouldn't. The contradiction is the point: love that cannot survive its own presence.","sonnet":"A song built entirely on contradictory pleas — someone simultaneously begging a person to stay and begging them not to, the reversed lyrics spelling out an internal war between need and self-protection.","src":"sonnet","fable":"The lyric is a plea that turns inside out. The opening is bare supplication for someone to stay, then a mirror-writing passage literally reverses the words on the page, so the same syllables read backward and the wish curdles. By the close the sentence has flipped entirely: what began as a beg to stay becomes a beg not to stay, the speaker recanting the very thing he wanted. Fitting for Klayton's self-titled Celldweller debut, where obsessive digital editing was the compositional method itself, the reversed text isn't a trick but the point — desire and its retraction printed as the same word run in two directions.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"desire and its retraction printed as the same words reversed","built":"mirror-writing passage literally reverses the syllables on the page"},
"celldweller~unshakeable": {"haiku":"Three celestial guardians watch and orchestrate events from beyond mortal reach, keeping the world stable and under control. Humanity stands on immovable ground held by these distant architects.","opus":"A grandiose sci-fi conceit: three cosmic figures watching over humanity, whose vigilance makes the ground beneath us feel solid. It trades in the comfort of imagining unseen guardians orchestrating a chaotic world into order.","sonnet":"Three celestial figures watch over humanity from beyond the galaxy, orchestrating existence from space and time — their divine control is what keeps the world stable and gives people ground that cannot be moved.","src":"haiku","fable":"The anthem's certainty is entirely borrowed: a trio of unseen sentinels rules a far world from past its outer edge, and their unbroken vigil is the only reason the floor beneath everyone holds firm. Klayton, working Celldweller's weld of orchestral swell and drum-and-bass churn, sets the verses at dawn over that distant shore and then at dusk, the suns lowering and the frame sliding from the horizon of the galaxy to the horizon of dying, so the promised safety quietly sours the second time through. The hook refuses to shift — one word slammed down past the point of persuasion — until durability stops sounding like protection and starts sounding like something being watched over whether it consents or not.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","faith & the occult"],"means":"promised safety curdling into surveillance as the hook refuses to shift","built":"verse arc from dawn to dusk slides cosmic vigil into watching-over-death"},
"celldweller~waiting-for-so-long-unreleased-demo-2006":{"haiku":"A long period of waiting finally gives way to agency—the speaker claims their moment has arrived.","opus":"This unreleased demo is little more than a single mantra, a narrator declaring he has waited so long and that his time has finally arrived. With scant text and pure repetition, it functions as an anticipatory build, all coiled patience awaiting release. It surfaces from Celldweller's mid-2000s archive as a sketch rather than a fully realized statement, more mood than narrative.","sonnet":"A stripped loop of anticipation at its breaking point: after an unnamed, endlessly deferred wait, something finally shifts and the moment is claimed as one's own. Minimal in content but dense in tension — the waiting itself is the entire emotional landscape.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~waiting-unreleased-demo-2005":{"haiku":"Patient desperation and unmet expectation define waiting; the speaker requests space for consideration while knowing they'll be let down again.","opus":"The stuck agony of waiting on someone who would rather let you down than commit, asking only for a moment's consideration that never comes. The parenthetical wait-or-run names the trap: staying is torment, leaving unthinkable.","sonnet":"The exhausting helplessness of waiting for someone who keeps letting you down — the narrator asks if their obsession is unreasonable, wonders whether to stay or run, and keeps returning to the same unanswered position of waiting, knowing nothing will change.","src":"haiku"},
"celldweller~welcome-to-the-end": {"haiku":"Two people seek isolation and escape together, though one's emotional stone-heartedness makes connection futile; the song ends with whispered resignation to ending.","opus":"An intimate collapse where a lover pleads for closeness and understanding while the singer's own heart has already gone to stone. The ache is his inability to stop needing her even as the relationship, and something larger, reaches its end.","sonnet":"Two people retreating into each other when everything outside has become unbearable — the tenderness of hiding away together shadowed by the knowledge that this intimacy is also the end of something, a small voice whispering goodbye.","src":"haiku","fable":"A woman's tender invitation to slip out of sight together is a trap the narrator already sees closing, her whispered warmth curdling into the announcement that everything is finishing. Klayton, folding industrial metal into breakbeat and trip-hop on this self-titled debut, builds the chorus on a want he keeps disowning even as he repeats it, each denial pulling him back toward the dependence he cannot renounce. The final murmur he tries to silence is the one that names their closeness as a verdict.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The closer feels like déjà vu, and it is engineered that way: its chorus returns Symbiont's wish-I-didn't-need-you refrain, and the whispered longing to go somewhere far away is The Stars of Orion's loop set in her mouth — the record ending by replaying its own obsessions.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","death & grief"],"means":"tender invitation recognized as a trap the narrator walks toward anyway","built":"closeness named as verdict only in the final murmur he tries to silence"},
"cemented-minds~stone-spit":{"haiku":"Resignation to futility in a rigged system where money is fabricated and society is forgery, leaving only slow attrition and the inevitability of death.","opus":"Burned-out doomer nihilism, rejecting the job, calling money and society fictions, accepting the sun will eventually swallow us. The gut-punch is the flat resignation that you'll never make it and the hurt lasts the entire length of the dying.","sonnet":"A blunt dismissal of the social contract: money is fake, society is a forgery, the sun will explode anyway, and any expectation that life gets easier is a lie — \"let's call it a life\" is the only honest response.","src":"haiku"},
"cemetery-sun~break-me-down":{"haiku":"A collapsing sense of self requires destruction to rebuild; internal collapse and shame drive the struggle to prove worth.","opus":"A fight against internal collapse and self-erasure, the narrator clinging to life while begging to stop being broken down, the pretend-normalcy easier than facing the walls crumbling in his head.","sonnet":"A house of glass that's been lived in too long: the speaker has been through hell, can feel himself caving in, and the plea is double-edged — fall hard and hold on, wanting both to be broken down and to prove something on the other side of it.","src":"haiku"},
"cemetery-sun~down-and-out":{"haiku":"Bitterness and shame corrode the speaker's foundation; confidence shatters while sanity fractures under the weight of self-disgust and isolation.","opus":"A crumbling of poise and control, the singer fleeing a world gone still and shedding his judgment while trying not to disappoint someone. Resentment has settled in permanently, and mustering the will to release his grip feels no different from watching his own reason unravel.","sonnet":"Someone in the grip of emotional collapse admits they have lost control, become bitter and irrational, and are terrified of letting down the person who loves them while unable to find their way back to stability.","src":"haiku"},
"central-heat-exchange~tulips-at-my-bedside":{"haiku":"Romantic or relational detachment where tulips symbolize persistent memory and presence despite emotional withdrawal and a sense of being unwelcome in the other's affection.","opus":"A hazy, lo-fi meander through a connection that's dimming, tiny household moments and a nod to a certain indie film standing in for a soft ache over someone perpetually thought of yet never truly near. The looping question about whether the meeting even happened casts doubt on whether any of it was real.","sonnet":"A soft, affectionate song about a person who is always on your mind even when you're trying not to think about them — ordinary domestic details pile up around someone's absence or presence, and the tulips at the bedside become a symbol of quiet, persistent feeling.","src":"haiku"},
"cerce~back-from-the-dead":{"haiku":"Reconnection after estrangement reveals that hardship has transformed the speakers into truer versions of themselves, now able to speak freely together.","opus":"A relationship or self resurrected and rushing to make up for lost time, filling old silences with talk; the wisdom is that the hard passage they survived is exactly what made them who they now are.","sonnet":"Two people reunite after a period of estrangement and find themselves pouring out everything they withheld, filling the silence between them — the past hardship turns out to be what shaped them into the creatures they now are.","src":"haiku"},
"ceremony~vanity-spawned-by-fear":{"haiku":"A paralysing self-regard rooted in dread becomes inescapable, a vanity born from anxiety that traps the subject in perpetual immobility and shame.","opus":"Vanity dissected as something born of fear and terror, a compulsion that burrows in and won't let you leave; the discomfort of recognizing a self-obsession you're both ashamed of and unable to escape.","sonnet":"An inescapable self-consciousness — described as something that burrows, moves through the speaker like currency, and belongs entirely to them — follows everywhere and cannot be shed, born from a fearful state the speaker was trapped in from the start.","src":"haiku"},
"ceterum~aurelia":{"haiku":"Awakening confronts dormancy while danger brews; exposure leaves one unable to defend against corrupting systems breeding dread and erosion.","opus":"Metamorphosis as survival metaphor, the narrator a larva feeding on a hostile world it must endure, hardening and gathering strength for the transformation that will finally let it rise and take flight.","sonnet":"A larva-to-fly metamorphosis used as psychological metaphor: the speaker, frightened and exposed, feeds on whatever is available in the current nefarious world while hardening their skin, waiting to grow wings and take flight as the phase shifts and the next begins.","src":"haiku"},
"ceterum~imbue-lan-vital":{"haiku":"Trapped in delusion and consumption, suffocation builds from within as fear prevents detachment from the deteriorating system of control.","opus":"A dissociative struggle against something numbing and controlling from within, the narrator distrusting a seductive anesthesia that erodes him and desperately trying to identify and detach from what has taken over.","sonnet":"Trapped in a state of physical and psychological suffocation — sweat pressing against the neck, a heel in the chest, the heat of overconsumption rising from within — the speaker reaches a moment of standing upright, mirrored in their own inability to simply be, and resolves they must find a way out.","src":"haiku"},
"ceterum~insolence-recoil":{"haiku":"Deceptive ego and false pretense collapse when confronted; betrayal and reciprocal violence become justified retaliation.","opus":"A face-off with a puffed-up deceiver whose self-image has crumbled, the singer spotting his own dishonesty reflected in the other and deciding to turn his back and abandon him to his own collapse.","sonnet":"A speaker watches someone's ego collapse under the weight of their own false intentions and sees themselves in the wreckage — \"a liar like me\" — arriving not at moral superiority but at the recognition that walking away from another's pathetic state is complicated by the mirror it holds up.","src":"haiku"},
"ceterum~paramnesia":{"haiku":"Focus complicates what endures through will's strength; gemlike existence held by wicked arms never loosens its criminal grip on redemption.","opus":"A disorienting meditation on memory and the impossibility of reliving a moment, the narrator locking something precious away and grieving that it can never come again, complication as the enemy of holding on.","sonnet":"A speaker circles around something locked away — held like a diamond, complicated by focus — and acknowledges they can never live past it; giving it up to someone better and walking away without returning is the only exit from the loop of \"over, over.\"","src":"haiku"},
"ceterum~the-architect":{"haiku":"Variables dissolve while insignificance spreads. Manipulation hardens will—forward motion becomes torture.","opus":"A confrontation with a manipulator who molds and bends the singer's will, resolving to move forward despite the strain and insisting nothing good comes from the dissonance they create.","sonnet":"About being manipulated and slowly eroded by someone with unchecked power over you — the narrator has allowed another person to mould and misuse them, watches everything that once mattered slip away, and seethes at the futility of change while the manipulator keeps looking away from the damage they've done.","src":"haiku"},
"cevret~blinded":{"haiku":"A society consumed by narcissism and vulgarity has rendered itself blind to surrounding evil, and the speaker stands screaming, visible and dying, while no one can see past their own bleeding eyes.","opus":"A scream against collective blindness to a decaying society, the narrator literally dying in front of people too self-absorbed to see the evil that flourishes precisely because no one will look.","sonnet":"A collective moral blindness addressed as an emergency: vomiting my brains out right upon your feet while you cannot see, vulgarity rampant and solidarity absent — when you can't see, evil is free to roam, and the burning society's rising flames are exactly what the blind eyes refuse to register.","src":"haiku"},
"cfcf~alice":{"haiku":"Isolation in urban desolation offers unexpected solace, where streetlights become the only comfort and human presence provides the warmth needed to release inner radiance.","opus":"Loneliness in an empty rooms and streetlights, reaching for someone whose presence makes the singer reveal a hidden warmth; a quiet plea for company against the ache of coming home to nothing.","sonnet":"A solitary speaker in a rainy, empty house finds streetlights the only company until a person's presence pulls something warm out of them, and they plead for that company to stay through the night.","src":"haiku"},
"cfcf~punksong":{"haiku":"The speaker dissolves identity across nights, embracing distance and detachment as flesh and thought lose meaning; drowning in moonlight.","opus":"The Montreal electronic artist drifts through a nighttime dissolution of the self, becoming someone new and losing track of where one identity ends and the next begins. Years collapse together as the narrator lets go entirely, calmly at peace even with the prospect of it all ending, the ambient, unmoored track sinking into a resigned serenity.","sonnet":"On a night where identity dissolves and decades fold together, a figure drifts detached — part observer, part ghost — finding that the body and its hungers feel remote and unreal. Surrender to the current feels like the only option, and if everything fades, there is a quiet acceptance that things will still, somehow, be alright.","src":"haiku"},
"cfcf~suburbilude":{"haiku":"Suburban conformity repels anyone who thinks differently, treating nonconformity as alien and threatening. The speaker critiques conservative groupthink and gossip that polices individuality.","opus":"A spoken snapshot from CFCF, this interlude conjures suburban estrangement through a teenager's rambling monologue. Prizing a certain band for words that describe reality honestly brands you an outsider in a place where everyone is buttoned-up and screen-perfect. Parents trade gossip at a diner over some scandalous message while sameness closes in, the piece capturing the eerie, uniform dread of a neighborhood that fears the unusual, more eavesdropped scene than song.","sonnet":"A teenager in a conservative suburb finds rare kinship over a shared love of the Smiths, whose lyrics see through social norms the way no one around them seems to. The surrounding conformity — gossiping mothers, republican neighbors, TV-shaped minds — makes the connection feel urgent and the alienation feel very close.","src":"haiku"},
"chalk~b-al-feirste":{"haiku":"Communities stand united against historical violence and oppression, refusing to accept the grinding brutality passed down through generational trauma.","opus":"Belfast as inheritance, running fingers along the old bullet scars of a father's past and the sectarian creed about not letting them grind you down, then refusing a borrowed faith for solidarity inside the loneliness.","sonnet":"A speaker in Belfast traces inherited trauma — literally putting fingers through the bullet holes of a father's past — finding solidarity in standing shoulder to shoulder while fighting isolation and the grinding weight of history.","src":"haiku"},
"chalk~can-t-feel-it":{"haiku":"Lost intimacy and faded sensation haunt memory—a youthful sexual awakening now numbed into emotional absence, unable to recapture what once felt transcendent.","opus":"An aching memory of a first, secret same-sex love bound to silence and threat, the narrator mourning that the once-heavenly feeling has gone permanently numb, the taste of it irretrievable.","sonnet":"A childhood intimacy that carried a dark secret — the friend who kept darkness within made the narrator promise silence, and now looking back into that room, the taste of heaven is a memory that no longer registers; numbness has replaced the feeling entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"chalk~i-d-c":{"haiku":"Apathetic defiance and manipulative pragmatism, the speaker indifferent to judgment while extracting what they want.","opus":"A defiant, disaffected shrug set in a shady world of phone-hacking and scams, the speaker insisting love keeps him insulated from all of it, even as a betrayer twists the knife and turns on the people who kept him going.","sonnet":"Defiant indifference worn as armor against someone who twists the knife — scamming, hustling, getting back up from the gutter with gold in hand, but holding onto love as the thing that makes external opinion irrelevant. 'I don't care, man, I got love' is the one genuine anchor in an otherwise chaotic stance.","src":"haiku"},
"chalk~longer":{"haiku":"Mortality draws near while the speaker waits for revelation and connection; death calls by name as celestial gates open for those who surrender.","opus":"A hovering at the threshold of death, half-lullaby and half-vigil, where the narrator both fears the gates ahead and coaxes someone else to hold on a moment more. The Belfast noise-pop trio wrap dread in something almost tender.","sonnet":"A near-death or death vision in which the speaker sees gates and hears calling while someone who was crawling toward them is left behind as they leave. The plea to wait a little longer is addressed inward as much as outward — the harp, the angels, the cradle of bones all patient.","src":"haiku"},
"chalk~one-nine-eight-zero":{"haiku":"Childhood loss repeats across generations; the father's wound becomes the child's inheritance.","opus":"A child watching a father leave and wondering whether he'll one day carry the same pain and repeat it. The plea to go back to 1980 is a wish to return to before the love left, knowing full well it's already too late.","sonnet":"A child sits at the top of the stairs watching their father leave again, carrying grief they suspect will be inherited — \"I wonder if I'll do the same\" — and pleads to go back to 1980 before the love was gone, knowing it is already too late.","src":"haiku"},
"chalk~pain": {"haiku":"Bricks replace bones as the speaker endures, the beloved's absence burns while physical impact is the only proof of existence.","opus":"A love strained by distance and helplessness, the hurt of not being there when someone you love is breaking; the fable of the man with bones of brick who still falls underscores that strength can't spare anyone.","sonnet":"A song about the guilt and helplessness of being absent from someone who is suffering — watching from a distance as that person is hurt and brought down, while knowing you are too useless and too new to this to do anything about it.","src":"haiku","fable":"It opens wearing the mask of a tyrant — demanding fealty, promising to break what it has already wounded — but the swagger is a lie the song immediately dismantles, because the very next breath begs to be shown and held by someone confessing to be useless and new to this. That is the read: the cruelty is the armour, and underneath is a dependence so total it can only phrase closeness as pain, hurting precisely because the other person isn't around. Midway the voice steps outside itself into a small third-person parable of a man whose strength was only ever brittle — bones like brick, hard but pure, felled by a single blow while a woman kneels and asks whether any God is watching. When that image of brittle bones detaches from its story and repeats itself into the ground, the loop stops being narration and becomes the thing it describes: a mind stuck grinding on its own fracture, the techno-punk repetition enacting the collapse the lyric can no longer talk its way out of.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression","madness & the mind"],"means":"cruelty as armour over a dependence that can only phrase closeness as pain","built":"the brittle-bones image detaches from its parable and loops into structural collapse"},
"chalk~static":{"haiku":"White noise overwhelms as the speaker dissolves into undifferentiated hum. Static becomes all that remains of signal.","opus":"Dependence dressed up as self-sufficiency: the narrator insists he can manage alone while admitting he needs someone the moment the noise closes in. The repeated boast that he'd severed the tie only proves he never did.","sonnet":"Clinging to someone as the only anchor against the noise: the static is all the speaker can see, and the cord is barely holding — searching for anything to keep themselves right, they turn to the one person who cuts through the interference.","src":"haiku"},
"chalk~them":{"haiku":"Desire and projection distort perception, leaving the speaker unable to recognize true reflection while trapped in fabricated roles and surface identities.","opus":"Being seen the way no one ever saw you before, tangled with a father's inherited lies and the pressure to perform a face; intimacy and identity collide with the demand to put your mask on and laugh it off.","sonnet":"A figure haunted by others' gazes — a shadow dancing on their chest, a Phoenix telling them they carry their father's lies — is urged to put on a face and laugh their way out, masking whatever wound makes them invisible to those around them.","src":"haiku"},
"chalk~tongue":{"haiku":"Communication collapse where utterance itself becomes lethal and opaque, leaving the narrator paralyzed between hellish return and self-destruction.","opus":"A stalemate with a lover whose words are both intoxicating and destroying, leaving the narrator no dignified exit—only the choice between damnation and self-erasure. Desire curdles into a trapped, self-punishing rage.","sonnet":"A person is paralyzed and tormented by someone whose words they cannot interpret — the ambiguous, cutting nature of what this person says leaves the speaker unable to tell whether they should stay and suffer or remove themselves entirely, with no clear answer ever given.","src":"sonnet","fable":"The Belfast noise-punk band gives you almost nothing and repeats it until the smallness becomes the meaning. A single image, a lover's tongue as a suffocating depth that's killing the speaker, sits beside a flat inability to read what the other person actually wants, and the only response offered is a binary of two damnations: back to hell or the obscene self-directed dismissal. The looping and stripping-down, until only the depths phrase is left circling, enacts exactly the airless dead-end it describes, a fight with no exit that just keeps folding back on itself.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","anger & defiance"],"means":"a fight with no exit that keeps folding back on itself","built":"looping and stripping-down until only one phrase remains enacts the airless dead-end it describes","fvr":"2.4"},
"challenger-deep~the-great-calamity":{"haiku":"Decay accelerates toward inevitable collapse as the speaker confronts the degeneration of meaning and purpose; resilience requires accepting loss and beginning anew.","opus":"A sprawling reckoning with self-inflicted collapse, everything he built drained of meaning as neglect finally comes due. Parenthetical asides confess to rewriting the past and his own art out of shame, framing the great calamity as both a world's downfall and a personal test of whether he can come back.","sonnet":"A post-collapse reckoning with ambition, shame, and the wreckage of what was built: the speaker cycles through fragmented recollections of better days, questions whether growth has only been a disguise, and ultimately reaches a tentative resolve to cherish what was lost and right wrongs before the world's ash settles. The piece insists on the difference between desolation accepted and desolation endured.","src":"haiku"},
"challenger-deep~the-great-calamity-vocal-version":{"haiku":"Decay and regret compound as the narrator watches civilization crumble under shame and negligence, searching desperately for meaning in ruin and false redemption.","opus":"A collapse both civilizational and personal, everything built losing its meaning; the parenthetical inner voice confesses that the ruin traces back to the singer's own choices, shame, and self-sabotage.","sonnet":"A reckoning with civilisational collapse and personal stagnation that have fed each other: fragmented memory, shame, and negligence have eroded ambition, yet the speaker still reaches toward revival, asking whether the catastrophe ahead might serve as the catalyst for a new beginning.","src":"haiku"},
"chance-the-rapper~finish-line-drown-feat-t-pain-kirk-franklin-eryn-allen-kane-noname":{"haiku":"Marathon spiritual journey reaches its apex; prayer, perseverance, and love sustain the swimmer through drowning waves toward salvation.","opus":"Chance turns gospel triumph into something hard-won, threading Xanax addiction, a grandfather's death, and label battles into a prayer for endurance. The finish line is faith held onto through burnout, gratitude wrestled from exhaustion.","sonnet":"A rapper maps his journey from addiction, loss, and self-destruction toward spiritual arrival — praying through the Xanax fog, racing toward the finish line, finding God in his grandmother's voice and his partner's patience — before the song opens into a gospel cry about the waters rising and the need for rescue, promising Chicago will one day be free.","src":"haiku"},
"chaos-divine~unspoken":{"haiku":"Entrapment in cycles of shame and desire: the speaker repeatedly returns to the same patterns of moral compromise and internal conflict, watching lies multiply and truth erode.","opus":"Australian progressive-metal band Chaos Divine trace a loop of disgrace and repetition. Dragged back to the daily grind past a wall of faceless people, the narrator plunges gladly into flame, unable to release himself and pleading to be pulled beneath. He rehearses the same script, resenting an imposed conscience that shackles and conceals his baser nature, weeping alone while wondering how to conquer his own thoughts, deceit multiplying deceit.","sonnet":"A man is caught in a recurring pattern — desire pulling him toward something he knows is destructive, a forced morality holding him back just enough to ensure suffering on both sides. The shame goes unspoken, lies accumulate, and the internal war between animality and restraint exhausts him without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"chaosbay~are-you-afraid":{"haiku":"Fear permeates as the speaker confronts both external threat and their own terror.","opus":"A furious wake-up call aimed at a generation that knows better and still sleepwalks toward collapse. The war and denial are described as homemade, self-inflicted, and the only question left is which ruin you die in.","sonnet":"A collective call to wake up before the homemade war and engineered denial burn everything down, spoken to a generation that should know better but still doesn't. The speaker can't decide which grave they'll end up in, yet keeps demanding the crowd stand up — urgency undercut by the honest admission that wisdom has not yet arrived.","src":"haiku"},
"chaosbay~what-is-war":{"haiku":"War emerges from loneliness and envy rather than necessity, perpetuating cycles of bloodshed rooted in humanity's spiritual emptiness and desire for possession.","opus":"Tracing war back to loneliness and envy rather than scarcity, mourning a lost golden age of shared plenty; the repeated question refuses easy answers and insists hatred grows from feeling alone.","sonnet":"A narrator who once walked beside fountains of shared abundance reflects on how envy rooted in loneliness led humanity to bloodshed, questioning whether the cycle of hate has ended and imagining a new day where craving for retention gives way to reinvention.","src":"haiku"},
"chaoseum~my-wonderland":{"haiku":"Obsession becomes a prison where the narrator clings to suffering rather than accept peace, locked in a self-made hell despite offers of love.","opus":"Self-inflicted torment and an addiction to one's own pain, hating the love that leaves you feeling alone even when held; the wonderland is a private hell chosen over healing, wanting release only as death.","sonnet":"The speaker is locked in a self-made hell of obsessive anger and self-destructive love — feeling alone even while held — and longs to reclaim a lost inner world (\"my wonderland\") that would finally let them be free, even at the cost of dying.","src":"haiku"},
"chaotica~something-wicked":{"haiku":"Darkness consumes the psyche, turning reason fragile and mental suffering into violent retaliation while the mind betrays itself from within.","opus":"Rage as a wicked thing that arrives and hollows you out, primal fury escaping its cage until passion burns down to an empty, hollow soul; a portrait of anger consuming everything and leaving you alone.","sonnet":"An encroaching darkness — wicked, named many things — extinguishes passion like a candle flame, fuels primal rage, empties the heart, and ultimately leaves the speaker stripped and alone after the inner giant falls.","src":"haiku"},
"charcoal-tongue~fix-me":{"haiku":"A defiant person rejects others' attempts to change or fix them, asserting their autonomy while expressing anger at being controlled and diminished.","opus":"A blunt rejection of everyone's push to mend him, the singer refusing repair by insisting he's hollow and lifeless within. The fury rises from feeling manipulated and misunderstood, from falsehoods forced on him, toward a reclaiming of who he is, and no one may override that. He recasts rehabilitation as just another kind of erasure.","sonnet":"Resistance to others' attempts at repair hardens into self-definition: the brokenness is not a problem to solve but the truest thing remaining. Anger at external control accumulates until the refusal to be managed becomes the only act of self-possession left. Being unfixable is not defeat but the one thing that cannot be taken.","src":"haiku"},
"charles-mingus~better-git-it-in-your-soul":{"haiku":"A brief encounter ignites total devotion, transforming fleeting fascination into consuming love that redirects the entire trajectory of desire.","opus":"Mingus channeling the shout and stomp of the black gospel church into jazz; though these lyrics are a later vocalese of romantic fascination turning to love, the piece is really about ecstatic, communal feeling seized from the soul.","sonnet":"A chance meeting in moonlight turns from mere fascination into genuine love: the narrator recalls how touching someone's hand and sharing a kiss transformed what might have been a brief passing romance into something lasting.","src":"haiku"},
"charli-xcx~360":{"haiku":"Omnipresent cultural mythology: the narrator dissolves into a hyper-visible icon that defines taste and desire while maintaining mystifying distance and refusal.","opus":"A hyperpop victory lap on being the it-girl reference everyone copies, name-dropping the cool-girl aesthetic; the mirror hook reveals the whole scene reflecting her back, cool detachment masking total self-assurance.","sonnet":"A speaker proclaims total cultural omnipresence — \"I'm everywhere, I'm so Julia\" — insisting she is the original everyone copies, her aesthetic inescapable whether in mirrors or on dance floors, indifferent to love or hate alike.","src":"haiku"},
"charli-xcx~what-i-like":{"haiku":"Casual intimacy deepens into genuine partnership; domesticity and commitment emerge from repeated small moments of connection.","opus":"An unusually sincere and soft Charli track, the buzz of a casual fling quietly turning into the person you'd choose for a whole future. It captures the instant desire realizes it was affection all along.","sonnet":"A casual physical arrangement has deepened into something the speaker wants to keep forever: from late-night hookups and horror films to imagining a house by the ocean and a permanent commitment, she maps the full arc of wanting someone to become her person, comfortable in how gangsta and real it feels.","src":"haiku"},
"charlie-griffiths~arctic-cemetery":{"haiku":"Paleontological excavation unearths evolutionary anomalies buried in ancient strata, revealing hidden ancestral secrets that challenge creation mythology.","opus":"Deep time and Darwin rendered as prog-metal spectacle: a fossil unearthed from arctic strata becomes a voice narrating its own resurrection. The Haken guitarist frames evolution itself as a heresy that buries creation myth.","sonnet":"A paleontological discovery speaks in the first person: a fossil unearthed from Devonian shale, an evolutionary anomaly buried in an arctic cemetery, holds the missing key to human origins — the song frames deep-time ancestry as both a séance and a scientific reckoning, the creature in the sediment calling to its children from millions of years away.","src":"haiku"},
"charly-bliss~glitter":{"haiku":"Contradiction between desire and fulfillment; a narrator caught between seeking validation and cynical self-sabotage.","sonnet":"A woman picks apart a relationship warped by ego, desire, and bad faith, questioning whether her value to her partner is genuine or simply the prize of being the first to say yes. Self-contradictions pile up — pleasure and pain, hunger for approval and contempt for it — without resolving.","src":"haiku"},
"chase-status~end-credits-feat-plan-b":{"haiku":"Confrontation with mortality and judgment, expressing hope for divine mercy while acknowledging past wrongdoings and spiritual decay.","opus":"Written for a prison drama, Plan B voices the moment of dying, the body shutting down system by system, clinging to hope that someone is there to witness it. Its ache is the fear of facing the end alone.","sonnet":"A meditation on the threshold of death, sung from the perspective of someone contemplating their last moments — hoping for peace on the other side, fearing no one will be present to care, and oscillating between acceptance and a pull back toward hopeless earthly longing.","src":"haiku"},
"chasing-desolation~maze-of-white":{"haiku":"Two people are trapped in a white-out winter landscape, running from something they can't name, too far gone to turn back as the cold and dark close in behind them.","opus":"Two people dying on black ice in a whiteout, the cold personified as a predator that senses fear and closes in. Panic curdles into resignation as the pair realize there's no road back.","sonnet":"Two people fleeing through a winter night on a highway, the cold and the dark closing in as an inescapable force behind them. The white maze follows no matter how fast they run, daylight already gone, ending with two lives on black ice as though they were never there.","src":"haiku"},
"chat-pile~tenkiller":{"haiku":"Callousness and predatory cruelty pervade existence as the narrator merges with death itself, inflicting suffering and watching drowning with indifference.","opus":"A dissociated confession of self-loathing, cruelty, and suicidal despair from Oklahoma noise-rock, refusing the mercy others beg for; the recurring line about not wanting to be here yet standing anyway is the whole bleak stand.","sonnet":"A dissociated narrator looks at their own face in a cold room and doesn't recognise it, while a violent, self-loathing inner voice — kicking dogs, laughing at recovery, declaring the skull the only truth — fights against a stated desire to simply not be here anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"chauncy~knees":{"haiku":"Urgent desire for authentic connection and vulnerability, stripping away pretense to access raw truth despite the risk of exposure.","opus":"Raw carnal want stripped of anything tender, demanding submission and honesty from a lover the singer finds hard to swallow; desire and distrust tangle in wanting someone pretty who keeps things hidden.","sonnet":"A speaker demands total openness from a lover — show your bloody knees, show your teeth, show the other side — offering to satisfy every hunger in return, fixated on physical surrender and the rawness beneath prettiness.","src":"haiku"},
"chawki~habibi-i-love-you-radio-edit":{"haiku":"Devotion transcends language and reason, expressing romantic obsession that places the beloved as the sole source of life's meaning and survival.","opus":"A cross-cultural love song weaving Moroccan Arabic devotion with Pitbull's swagger, love-struck surrender colliding with married-to-the-game bravado; the heart of it is fear of losing someone worth going to war over.","sonnet":"A bilingual declaration of obsessive devotion, switching between Moroccan Arabic and English: the Arabic sections describe a love that stole the speaker's soul and set the tongue singing in every language, while the English verses frame it as a death-or-glory loyalty that would take an army to break.","src":"haiku"},
"cheat-codes~no-promises-feat-demi-lovato":{"haiku":"Both terrified of simple love because it can't stay simple; diving anyway despite fear.","opus":"Two people diving into something uncertain and asking each other not to make vows they can't keep. It's about wanting intimacy while bracing for it to fail, honesty offered as the only safe promise.","sonnet":"Two people at the edge of falling for each other ask to be spared the weight of promises — love is complicated and uncertain, diving in feels both irresistible and dangerous, and all they can ask is that neither one commits to what neither can guarantee.","src":"haiku"},
"cheem~elastic":{"haiku":"Emotional brittleness contrasts with a partner's resilience, revealing masks that stretch and distort as trust fractures under surface appearances.","opus":"An ex who bounces back untouched while the singer breaks, hiding behind a mask pulled so tight there's no seam; the Portuguese verse begs them home even as the read lands on how shallow and sharp their love turned out to be.","sonnet":"An accusation directed at someone whose heart bounces back while the speaker's breaks — the target hides behind a seamless mask, cuts ties that might ground them, and their shallow love turns the speaker inside out even as a Portuguese-language interlude reframes the pain from the other side.","src":"haiku"},
"cheem~freeze-tag":{"haiku":"Loss of agency and repeated conquest accumulate through cycles of trust and betrayal; the speaker questions love's authenticity while trapped in inevitable cycles of loss.","opus":"Romance recast as invasion and battle, the singer showing up already beaten and questioning whether a lover is merely a trophy and their warmth merely deceit. Cheem's jittery art-pop turns emotional surrender into seized ground.","sonnet":"Love as military conquest — the narrator arrives too late and already finds themselves surrendered, the battle won the moment the other person hit the shore; the flag keeps changing seasons and friends become casualties. The final questions — \"is a person just a prize, is 'I love you' just a lie?\" — collapse romance into cold power dynamics.","src":"haiku"},
"cheem~gorilla-glue":{"haiku":"Bonded through shared history and mutual damage, the speaker cannot untangle from another's gravity; love becomes indistinguishable from catastrophic collision.","opus":"A decade-long relationship fused by shared trauma rather than love, so tangled that leaving feels like tearing out an organ. The narrator wants free and can't tell where the other person ends and he begins.","sonnet":"A decade-long relationship built from shared trauma — both broke, in slivers of dark blues, memory cycling on repeat like a disease — where leaving means tearing out purple matter one claw at a time. The title image: stuck like gorilla glue, chrysalis organs mixed, impossible to separate even when the speaker knows they must.","src":"haiku"},
"cheem~nano":{"haiku":"Resentment accumulates through cycles of push-pull dynamics, leaving the narrator depleted and questioning whether remaining in a toxic relationship serves any purpose.","opus":"A relationship stuck on repeat, hollowed out and refilled with someone else's tears, lying because it beats nothing; the honest core is disgust at himself for how far under his skin she got and his fault for letting her in.","sonnet":"A relationship's slow unravelling is traced through small telling signs and honesty that never quite arrives: the speaker is sick of fussing and prodding, knows the effort is wasted, and admits they let the other person get too far under their skin.","src":"haiku"},
"cheem~octane":{"haiku":"Loss taints all memories; even songs become painful reminders of what dissolved, leaving nostalgia as a wound that cannot close.","opus":"A split that keeps deepening, where every song they once shared now wounds and the everyday remnants, old games and beloved bands, turn into hazards. The singer wavers between the urge to retaliate and the confession that he doomed himself, unable even to gather his own belongings without invoking the ex.","sonnet":"A breakup song about the ghost of a shared music library — every song they used to listen to together now hits differently and worse, and the speaker both misses the relationship and is furious that their partner has moved on to someone else, while knowing that the whole thing was probably rigged to drown him anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cheem stage heartbreak as a fistfight the speaker keeps losing to himself, wrapping revenge fantasies and a cocky put-down of her new man around a wound too raw to hold at that volume. The bravado is a decoy: underneath the swagger sits the quieter admission that grief has no finish line and no floor, that the songs he used to share now play like accusations, that he'd rather shove the last domino himself than wait out the collapse he already sees coming. The final beat exposes the whole con — the old games he wants back are hostages, unretrievable without speaking her name, which is to say there was never a clean exit from someone stitched into every object he'd reclaim.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"revenge swagger as decoy for grief with no floor and no finish","built":"closing beat exposes the con — the clean exit requires speaking her name"},
"cheem~password":{"haiku":"Severed connection requires erasure of memory and digital traces, as forgetting becomes the only path to freedom from a love that has become toxic.","opus":"A misremembered login stands in for a love you can neither wipe nor undo, still wincing at the name your fingers keep entering. Getting past it is framed as the hopeless chore of scrubbing away something sealed inside memory.","sonnet":"A forgotten ex is compared to a password that can't be reset — the speaker cringes at the name, doesn't want contact, and tries to \"just change it and kill the pain,\" acknowledging that the memory isn't as dead as it should be.","src":"haiku"},
"cheem~pivot":{"haiku":"Attachment renders movement impossible as the speaker remains planted in one emotional angle. Playing by another's rules traps the speaker in stasis.","opus":"The paralysis of an unequal relationship: one person begging for a truce while the other keeps score. Every attempt to move forward loops back, and the narrator knows the rules are rigged but can't stop playing.","sonnet":"A speaker stuck in love that isn't working cannot pivot away — one foot planted, unable to change angle or move forward — yet also feels the game is rigged: 'I've been playing by your rules but they're no fair', the chorus calling for a time-out in a dynamic where only one side is keeping score.","src":"haiku"},
"cheem~spin-cycle":{"haiku":"Repetitive futility becomes existence itself as the narrator pushes the same burden uphill endlessly, spinning in place while desiring escape but unable to break the loop.","opus":"Sisyphus as a portrait of stuck depression and a doomed relationship, pushing the same rock and spinning faster while going nowhere; the ache everyone hides eats away, and every attempt at escape just deepens the hole.","sonnet":"Sisyphean repetition — pushing the same rock up the same hill, spinning faster without going anywhere — frames a meditation on unresolved internal conflict, the comfort of misery, and the thin hope of finally breaking out of a cycle driven by wounds hidden since youth.","src":"haiku"},
"cheem~wtfu":{"haiku":"Self-absorbed indifference masks deeper spiritual emptiness; the speaker confronts complicity in broader suffering, demanding accountability for unconscious cruelty and narcissism.","opus":"A blunt shake-awake directed at someone stewing in bitterness and self-pity, demanding they own the scorn for humanity they carry. The turning point is the moral challenge of how they manage to rest easy at night.","sonnet":"A confrontational call to wake up directed at someone — possibly a generation, possibly a specific person — who is consumed by hatred and self-pity, living in a cocoon of complacency and unable to see past their own grievances. The narrator demands they account for their contempt for human life and recognize that their self-righteous pride is embarrassing rather than justified.","src":"haiku"},
"cheem~y-on-earth":{"haiku":"Impossible love persists despite incompatibility and denial, grounded in habit rather than choice. The speaker recognizes the contradiction but cannot escape the binding force of attachment.","opus":"The exasperated pull of loving someone you don't even like, a love neither person will name and both keep circling back to. He needles her about the lover she should be honest with, convinced that beneath the denial she is already his.","sonnet":"An admission of irrational, painful love: the speaker knows the other person dislikes them, that they can never be themselves in this relationship, yet cannot refuse — \"why on earth am I in love with you\" — and suspects the other person is secretly in the same trap.","src":"haiku"},
"chelsea-grin~bleeding-sun":{"opus":"An apocalyptic, conspiratorial vision of Earth as a manipulated prison planet run by warlords and profiteers, calling for humanity to overcome its own hatred and pride and yearning to be freed from this cosmic cage.","sonnet":"A song about humanity being enslaved by corrupt power structures — the narrator sees corporate warlords, pollution, and manufactured division as evidence of a prison planet, and calls for unity and the recognition that this cycle of destruction must end.","src":"sonnet"},
"chelsea-grin~leave-with-us":{"haiku":"Transcendence tempts surrender as an ancient presence offers escape from earthly suffering; the speaker weighs the appeal of departure against the burden of remaining.","opus":"A cult or alien intelligence's honeyed invitation to abandon this planet and join an exodus of light, pitching the departure as the end of every pain. The manipulation — rehearsed for ages and now spreading overhead — sells self-erasure as transcendence.","sonnet":"An invitation — half rapture, half cult recruitment — to abandon suffering humanity and join a vessel that promises to leave the earth forever. The speaker addresses someone who has been \"studying for years\" for exactly this holy moment, asking them to feel and see and taste the alien presence painting itself across the sky, promising that this will be the end of all suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"chelsea-wolfe~carrion-flowers":{"haiku":"Survival through learned resilience; predatory habits bloom repeatedly from unhealed wounds, growing stronger with each cycle of trauma.","opus":"Beauty grown from repeated damage, a slow relentless reckoning with someone who withheld love. Wolfe frames survival as its own kind of predation: holding onto the pain until it becomes the flower that feeds on you.","sonnet":"A dark meditation on creatures — people — who grow from repeated harm and love denied, hardening into something relentless that pursues those who hurt them, holding onto the pain of stolen love like a slow-blooming flower that refuses to die.","src":"haiku"},
"chelsea-wolfe~diana":{"opus":"Addiction voiced in the language of goddesses and celestial light — a lover that soothes and lifts her even as it drags menace through the night. Weariness and vows to fix things battle the seductive brightness that keeps pulling her back under.","sonnet":"A figure draws sustenance from a lover amid exhaustion and darkness — \"keep me warm, keep me alive\" — while flowers of light and evil in the night circle in a kind of mythic nocturnal space, with the repeated plea \"to keep me high\" suggesting survival depends on this presence.","src":"sonnet"},
"chemical-mother~ashen-blot":{"haiku":"Isolation dissolves the self; the subject abandons presence like pressed glass.","opus":"Dissociation rendered as being pressed flat under glass, high above yet perpetually unwell, no longer recognizable in his own reflection. Convinced he amounts to nothing regardless, the narrator fades into silence and gives himself up once more, and not for free.","sonnet":"A speaker pressed behind glass, dissolving in isolation, and unable to recognise themselves or another anymore — \"where have you gone when I needed you most?\" — accepts that they will abandon themselves again, as before, fading into stained-glass silence.","src":"haiku"},
"cherry-glazerr~soft-drink":{"haiku":"Intimacy triggers vulnerability and self-doubt; dancing offers temporary refuge from exposure while the narrator grapples with inadequacy and longing.","opus":"Social anxiety dressed as a crush, the fear of being seen and found wanting, dissolved by the simple relief of dancing with someone. It's about growing past who you used to be while nervously hoping the new self is enough.","sonnet":"Social anxiety and romantic nervousness converge as the speaker admits they go quiet around someone they like, fear being seen as dumb, and use dancing as both escape and intimacy — reconnecting with an old attraction while trying not to get stuck.","src":"haiku"},
"cherryfilter~cherry-filter":{"haiku":"A love song in Korean dialect expressing deep affection and gratitude. The speaker dedicates their strength and songs to the beloved, asking to be allowed into their heart despite feeling foolish or unworthy.","opus":"A heartfelt love song in which the singer, calling themselves silly, offers their whole story, heart, and hopes through music to the person they love, wanting to be uplifted by that feeling.","sonnet":"A Korean-language declaration of love and gratitude: the narrator acknowledges past fears and failures but finds the strength to carry on because of the person they love, promising to deliver their heart and all their hopes through music.","src":"haiku"},
"cherryfilter~sweet-little-kitty":{"haiku":"A melancholic feline escapes captivity toward freedom and melancholy, dancing as a small light in sad cities before disappearing into lonely seas. Romance dwells in solitary transgression.","opus":"Sung largely in Korean, the speaker is a 'romantic cat'—a lonely stray whose eyes become stars over back alleys; vowing never again to raid the fish shop, she dreams of leaving for the sea to be freer, dancing as a small starlight lighting a sad city, yet finds herself sinking endlessly into her deep, sorrowful ocean.","sonnet":"A stray cat narrates its own lonely romanticism in a sad city: eyes that become stars at night, a vow never to raid the fishmonger again, longing to set sail on a deep and sorrowful sea — the \"romantic cat\" is a small dancing starlight illuminating urban grief, sinking even as it dreams of swimming free.","src":"haiku"},
"cheshires~a-36gtqk":{"haiku":"Transcendence-seeking and cosmic return—the narrator begs to escape gray existence by returning to stars, willing to incinerate; autumn wind scatters hopes as paradise becomes hell.","opus":"This Russian track works in the post-Soviet vein of gothic romanticism, where cosmic longing and self-annihilation are barely distinguishable. The speaker begs to be carried back to a star, to a promised paradise — and the twist is that the paradise burns and scatters like autumn seed, returning them instead to hell. The scene renders escape as seduction: transcendence and self-destruction wearing the same face.","sonnet":"A Russian song about a person who has been waiting to be carried away from a bleak existence — asking to be taken back to a star, to paradise — but the lyric ends with the realization that the \"promised paradise\" burns and scatters like autumn seed, and the speaker returns instead to hell, implying both the failure of escape and the seduction of self-destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"chester-bennington~white-noise":{"haiku":"Fear drives another toward self-destruction rather than vulnerability; their terror masquerades as betrayal and compromise.","opus":"Confronting a manipulative person whose words are clear but meaningless, terrified of intimacy and quick to throw everything away. The singer sees through the pretended scars and tells them to find the truth without him.","sonnet":"Someone confronting a partner who has been faking vulnerability and manufacturing fear to keep them trapped — the narrator sees through the disguise, recognizes that the scarring is performed rather than real, and insists the other person face truth without them.","src":"haiku"},
"chest~going-clear":{"haiku":"Rage and intoxication cloud judgment as the speaker witnesses another's self-destruction play out. Armor thins under scrutiny, exposing the armor beneath.","opus":"A scathing portrait of a drunk swaggering to a club door, all ego and delusion, turned away by a bouncer who sees straight through him. The refrain about healing mocks any real self-awareness.","sonnet":"A drunk man is watched and judged — by the doorman, by the narrator — as he staggers up to a club in a nylon shirt and bright red shoes, sweat coming through, swaying, refused entry; the repeated 'can you smell the healing, can you see it going clear' frames the scene with grim irony, the 'healing' being the humiliation itself.","src":"haiku"},
"chet-faker~the-trouble-with-us":{"haiku":"Combustible passion destroys what it claims to protect; the speaker recognizes the addiction to emotional turbulence and the toxicity of needing to bleed.","opus":"A volatile relationship hooked on its own destruction, stripping the paint from the walls and surviving on reconciliation nights at the edge of collapse. The confession is that he needs the danger, and above all the broken trust, to keep the fire lit.","sonnet":"A relationship that runs on conflict and desire in equal measure: the speaker tears paint off the walls with a partner they cannot leave, drawn back to the \"trouble\" of the connection even while naming it clearly — nights are kiss-and-make-up, heartbeats get in the way, and both people need the very dysfunction they are trying to escape. \"That's the trouble with us / I need the trouble with trust\" is the honest summary.","src":"haiku"},
"chevelle~forfeit":{"haiku":"Silence becomes strategy while violence tempts; the speaker struggles between assertion and surrender.","opus":"The compulsion to keep fighting even when winning means nothing, the ego that would rather prove itself right than find peace. Its real subject is stubbornness itself, the choice to surrender the argument being framed as the harder, truer strength.","sonnet":"A speaker torn between the urge to fight and argue and the recognition that silence or medication might be more useful, oscillating between aggression and grudging self-awareness without fully committing to either.","src":"haiku"},
"chevelle~peach":{"haiku":"Trust collapses into manipulation; the speaker confronts a narcissist whose false comfort dissolves into betrayal and conscious cruelty.","opus":"A warning about trust and manipulation, a narcissist without conscience who takes piece by piece and tempts you to grab the forbidden peach. Dismemberment imagery renders how such a predator strips away everything from those who trust him.","sonnet":"A parable of predatory trust: a gentlemanly voice instructs a small figure to \"just grab that peach,\" framing temptation as harmless while the real cost — arms taken, legs taken, hands taken — accumulates, and the narcissist's absent conscience echoes through the repeated question of who to trust.","src":"haiku"},
"chevelle~rabbit-hole-cowards-pt-1":{"haiku":"Weakness and cowardice infect institutions and belief systems, with the narrator refusing to surrender to those who exploit fear and conspiracy.","opus":"A refusal to be dragged into paranoia and conspiracy fever, watching cowards evolve and walls come down; the stand is against catfished government talk and the pull down the rabbit hole, insisting on not becoming like them.","sonnet":"A defiant refusal to capitulate to online disinformation and the cowardice it breeds: the speaker watches a dam of reason fall to fever-evolved conspiracy thinking but refuses to become like those weakening the walls.","src":"haiku"},
"chevelle~remember-when":{"haiku":"The speaker drifts toward oblivion, asking the listener to remember their youth together before everything becomes nowhere.","opus":"A weightless meditation on memory and disorientation, keepsakes of childhood serving as sustenance while the narrator drifts toward nowhere, upright yet inexplicably sinking, nostalgia laced with loss. Chevelle are an American alt-metal trio known for dark, spacious rock, and this floating, inward drift fits their moodier, atmospheric side.","sonnet":"A drift into forgetting — the speaker is oriented right-side-up and yet falling, holding onto memories as fuel for a journey that leads nowhere in particular. Nostalgia is offered as both comfort and delusion: remember your youth, remember when, the refrain cycling back like something trying to find purchase before the drift takes over entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"chevelle~self-destructor":{"opus":"A confrontation with a science-denying figure who'd rather double down and self-destruct than listen, framing the ultimatum as change now or be destroyed, with the willful ignorance escalating into something like war.","sonnet":"Directed at someone — possibly a political figure or denier — who refuses to acknowledge scientific consensus or listen to others, fights everyone, doubles down, and will ultimately destroy themselves through sheer ideological stubbornness while millions of lives hang in the balance.","src":"sonnet"},
"chevelle~send-the-pain-below":{"haiku":"The speaker has internalized pain as a familiar companion and redirects it downward into themselves, suffocating but numbed to the hurt.","opus":"About burying hurt rather than feeling it, addressed to someone who enjoyed watching the narrator fail and then relented only to repeat it. The suffocation imagery captures the cost of that habit of swallowing pain to stay in control.","sonnet":"About someone who learned to suppress emotional pain rather than feel it — they internalized the hurt inflicted by a manipulative person who would drive them away and then beg them back, until numbness became the only way to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"chevelle~take-out-the-gunman":{"haiku":"Self-defense against sudden violence forces brutal action as the narrator fights for survival against an armed attacker driven by incomprehensible madness.","opus":"A visceral first-person account of confronting a mass shooter, the survival instinct to take out the gunman set against a numb observation of how such violence is televised.","sonnet":"A man awakens to find someone holding a gun to him and must find a way to neutralise the threat — point at the eyes, shoot at the knees — and the song tilts between literal crisis and the sense that the gunman is something internal, a blinding riot the speaker must summon every nerve to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"chig~joy":{"haiku":"Memory and meaning collapse when isolation takes hold, leaving only regret, division, and hollow repetition in the aftermath of loss.","opus":"Post-punk desolation about a woman worn down to nothing, drinking through regret in a country too divided to offer any relief. The bleak refrain drains all warmth from what memory is left.","sonnet":"Grief and division stripped to their minimum: music stops, memories go with it, a bottle in one hand and regret in the other, and the refrain no joy in this division repeats until the absence itself becomes the only subject — sparse, dry, final.","src":"haiku"},
"childish-gambino~this-is-america":{"haiku":"Chaotic entertainment and spectacle distract from gun violence, systemic racism, and wealth inequality embedded in American life.","opus":"Glover's whiplash between party hook and gunfire, staging how Black entertainment is used to distract from Black death in America. The seduction of the groove is the indictment: you keep dancing while the violence happens just off-frame.","sonnet":"America entertains itself with dancing and consumption while Black people are shot and the cameras keep rolling — the song stages the contradiction between celebration and slaughter, showing how the spectacle of performance is used to distract from the ongoing violence underneath.","src":"haiku"},
"chillwagon~rower-trailer":{"haiku":"Carefree defiance and street confidence celebrate freedom from worry and stress, finding joy in movement and the company of trusted companions.","opus":"A carefree Polish rap flex built on the proverb that no bad comes without some good, shrugging off stress with a bike ride and easy laughter; young-king braggadocio wrapped in a sunny, nothing-can-touch-me mood.","sonnet":"A carefree boast of a good day, cycling and chilling outdoors: the speaker dismisses stress and haters, celebrates riding with friends through the streets, and insists nothing bad lasts — every dark thing eventually comes out good.","src":"haiku"},
"chillwagon~trailer":{"haiku":"Intoxication and performance mask alienation as the speaker boasts and dances, using pharmaceutical escape and verbal excess to drown out internal void.","opus":"A raucous Polish party-rap boast built around dancing like a monkey, drenched in weed, booze, and vulgar bravado about a rival's girl and crowd worship. The recurring taunt that the world is a zoo, a planet of apes, turns the debauchery into a shrugging thesis about human nature.","sonnet":"A hedonistic Polish hip-hop party track: the crew is drunk, high, and dancing \"like monkeys\" — the repeated refrain — at a concert where chaos is the point, the scene is populated with women, weed, and bravado, and the speaker insists that anyone left standing is just a zoo animal in a world that belongs to them. The \"planet of the apes\" sign-off frames the whole night as absurdist spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"chinese-football~electronic-girl":{"haiku":"A mechanical woman rotates endlessly in performance, disconnecting from her own identity while questioning who orchestrates her perpetual motion.","opus":"An emo-math portrait of a girl spinning endlessly on an empty stage, transparent and flickering out, unsure who she is or who she performs for; a melancholy image of a person manipulated and hollowed by others' watching.","sonnet":"A girl on an empty stage spins endlessly to a relentless beat — too near and too far, too deep and too shallow — asking who she is and why she keeps turning; the earth's answer is simply to keep revolving with it, with no one needing to care.","src":"haiku"},
"chino-moreno~brief-exchange-from-dc-s-dark-nights-metal-soundtrack":{"haiku":"Time bends under obsessive control as the narrator replaces reality with fabricated paradise while others sleepwalk.","opus":"A hushed, sensuous fantasy of control where the singer can bend time, undo a life gone criminal, and quietly swap a lost reality for a picture-perfect one. Written for a Batman soundtrack, it turns intimacy into a whispered pact to slip away into a cleaner frame of mind.","sonnet":"A figure with the power to control time addresses someone they are drawing out of danger — cut the wire, wait outside, stay close — promising to replace, restore, and return them to a life they once knew, escaping into a picture-perfect newer state of mind.","src":"haiku"},
"choirboys~run-to-paradise":{"haiku":"Various people abandon their lives and relationships to escape to paradise—seeking redemption, freedom, or retreat from a world that disappointed or corrupted them.","opus":"A hometown lament watches a golden, blonde figure chase escape into a paradise of easy scores, leaving friends and a worried mother behind while abusing themselves. Choirboys, an Australian pub-rock band whose eighties anthem became a barroom staple, wrap the plea to open your eyes in a singalong chorus, the slipping-away good times and the borrowed Jesus imagery turning a friend's slow self-destruction into communal, beery grief.","sonnet":"People from every corner of someone's life — lovers, best friends, mothers — all flee toward their own version of paradise, leaving the narrator alone to question whether the paradise they seek is real or a beautiful lie they tell to justify abandonment. Nostalgia for good times mingles with bitter recognition.","src":"haiku"},
"chokecherry~afterglow":{"haiku":"Sacrifice of connection to prevent mutual destruction; memory holds another fixed in blame while the speaker watches love deteriorate from impossible distance.","opus":"Standing on the ledge with someone you can't save without falling too, trapped in mutual blame that never resolves. The narrator admits he had happiness in hand and couldn't stop himself from wrecking it.","sonnet":"Two people on a ledge, one reaching out, the narrator unable to grab without both falling — guilt for the fate placed in front of the other persisting even in happy memories. The afterglow of the chorus is the sensation of being close to someone whose warmth you've lost and can't recover without losing yourself too.","src":"haiku"},
"chon~can-t-wait":{"haiku":"Separation breeds impatience; the narrator cannot sustain waiting indefinitely and demands presence over promise.","opus":"A rare vocal turn for the instrumental band, spare and aching, someone gone and the narrator refusing to wait forever for a return that may only happen in memory. The plainness of the longing is the whole point.","sonnet":"Simple, direct longing for someone who is absent — the speaker misses them badly enough that the knowledge of an eventual reunion offers no comfort against the weight of the present separation.","src":"haiku"},
"chouchou-merged-syrups~a-3i7x5g":{"haiku":"A fragmented meditation on memory, isolation, and the search for meaning in insignificant moments—no treasure, just longing and forgetting.","opus":"chouchou merged syrups trade in gauzy, dream-pop textures where meaning dissolves into atmosphere, and that haze suits a narrator undone by their own mind — unable to breathe, to stand, to look behind closed eyes without fear. The old spell cast to be alone becomes a ritual returned to again and again, a way to process unnameable grief. What saved them was someone else's magic, never understood, only survived.","sonnet":"A song about the terror of one's own mind — the narrator can't breathe, can't stand, can't see behind their closed eyes without fear — and searches for meaning through the memory of a spell they used to cast to be alone, returning to it again and again as a way to process grief and loss they can't name. Ultimately it acknowledges that someone else's unknown magic saved them, even if they never understood it.","src":"haiku"},
"chouchou-merged-syrups~daidai-naru":{"haiku":"Time moves forward eroding memory while two people circle each other helplessly. Seeds planted from casual encounters grow into inescapable bonds, neither willing to cut the cord, waiting for connection to solidify.","opus":"An obsessive attachment that keeps quietly germinating no matter how often it's cut down, blurring the line between self and other. The narrator drifts toward an ending she can't predict, alone even when surrounded by warmth.","sonnet":"A speaker watches someone drift back into view and traces the slow-growing seeds their own behaviour has sown, knowing the outcome will be unpredictable — the other person's laugh burned into memory, their smile declared a defeat — while a sense of being alone persists even amid vivid, warmly lit days of togetherness.","src":"haiku"},
"chouchou-merged-syrups~hakuchumuha-shiisainonai":{"haiku":"Unknown colors blend in haze as the speaker performs composure for a sleepless lover. Transformation happens unobserved; by morning, dissolution completes, and the spell breaks into waking sorrow.","opus":"A fragile daydream where the narrator performs a lapis-blue self so someone won't be afraid, wanting to become the very person she loves. The dream dissolves before its fading magic ends, half-waking her back to reality.","sonnet":"Someone performs lapis-blue equanimity beside a sleeping lover in what feels like the last room, mixing unknown colours and questioning whether transformation means anything — the self that wanted to become the other person lingers as the conjured magic slowly expires, and the fantasy is wished to remain dream-like until it ends.","src":"haiku"},
"chouchou-merged-syrups~hierogurifu-to-asobu":{"haiku":"Growth emerges through spiralling confusion and cyclical self-reflection, where arrogance dissolves into acceptance of incompleteness.","opus":"A meditation on growth through uncertainty: the idea that being lost is itself how we find warmth and self-knowledge, and that everyone chasing greatness is really chasing what they still lack, learning only as the heartbeat wavers and wears down.","sonnet":"Maturation through imperfection: the immature self grows conceited yet slowly learns through a swirling pulse — rising, withering, knowing — that everyone driven by pride ends their story with the same final line, chasing what is still missing.","src":"haiku"},
"chouchou-merged-syrups~shayou":{"haiku":"Numbness persists despite presence and offering, leaving the speaker frustrated by the inability to bridge emotional distance or provoke genuine response.","opus":"The quiet devastation of being taken for granted by someone who never notices your presence no matter how much you give, until affection itself starts to feel absurd and you're left circling a person who simply doesn't register you.","sonnet":"A speaker watches someone who has blended invisibly into their world — allowed in, forgiven, yet growing ever more oblivious — and admits that chasing a colourless shadow is the only thing that heals the wound they expected to feel from this person's presence.","src":"haiku"},
"chris-stapleton~either-way":{"haiku":"Years of faking shattered; indifference replaces heartache when feeling finally dies.","opus":"A marriage gone completely numb, two people performing a shared life in separate rooms until feeling nothing becomes its own dead peace. The devastation is the flat verdict that leaving or staying no longer changes anything.","sonnet":"Two people in a dead marriage move through the same house like ghosts, past caring whether the other stays or leaves — the speaker has cried every tear available and now feels nothing, offering total indifference as the only honest response left.","src":"haiku"},
"christian-death~blood-moon":{"haiku":"Darkness and exile define existence as blood feeds an eternal cycle; redemption remains distant while the speaker drowns in the black light of celestial damnation.","opus":"Gothic-deathrock imagery of a damned wanderer cast into a wasteland, chasing an escape from hell and a salvation that never arrives. A recurring lunar omen curses his lineage and warps his grip on what is real.","sonnet":"A figure exiled in the desert stares through black eyes at a blood moon that burns everything black: they fled damnation only to find the bloodline feeding on whatever they perceive as reality. The blood moon is both cosmic doom and an inescapable personal curse, with \"redemption\" sought through stellar wind but nowhere found.","src":"haiku"},
"chromatics~yes-symmetry-remix":{"haiku":"Aggression and street dominance announce hierarchy as the speaker warns rivals; boast and threat fuse into a single linguistic assault.","opus":"A Bulgarian rap battle of pure aggressive taunting, ordering a rival to get up and clear off while boasting of the crew's dominance and threatening to smash him. The bravado is territorial and relentless, ending with the whole opponent's world drowned like a tsunami from the Black Sea.","sonnet":"A Bulgarian-language rap tirade demanding that someone leave: the speaker has no patience, commands the interloper to get up and go, and escalates through insults, threats, and a chaotic party-night narrative where East Coast bass fills a room and the speaker deals, dances, and dispenses with anyone who overstays their welcome. The aggressive call-and-response between an impatient voice and a stalling one drives the whole track.","src":"haiku"},
"cinnamon-babe~typecast":{"haiku":"Self-imposed imprisonment hardens into identity as the speaker locks themselves away while fearing freedom, trapped between the safety of their cage and the cold unknown beyond it.","opus":"A woman realizes the box confining her is one she built herself, her own mind turned into a casket she's lifeless inside of. The uneasy admission is that the enclosure feels safe, so escaping her own handiwork means breaking out of a comfort she willingly chained herself into.","sonnet":"Self-imposed imprisonment: the speaker built four walls around herself, made her mind a coffin, and now cannot escape the box she created — \"the girl in the box, I did it to myself\" — wondering how anyone breaks free of their own doing.","src":"haiku"},
"cinnamon-chasers~speeder":{"haiku":"Exhaustion with hypermodernity: constant connectivity and shallow attachments breed emptiness while masquerading as vitality and progress.","opus":"A hyper-caffeinated inventory of terminally-online romance, where infatuation and heartbreak blur into the same feed-scrolling reflex. The refrain of resignation admits we're all helpless for love while pretending to be past it, cool detachment as its own addiction.","sonnet":"A breathless collage of contemporary anxieties and micro-pleasures — coffee dates, TikTok, maxed-out credit, parasocial dependency — all declared \"copacetic\" in a relentless forward drive that admits we are fools for love but insists we are over it.","src":"haiku"},
"circa-survive~imposter-syndrome":{"haiku":"Self-doubt and systemic collapse compound into paralysis; the speaker searches for escape while America burns from within, convinced they are fundamentally fraudulent and unworthy.","opus":"A frayed plea to be believed, tangled with suicidal searching and a conviction that no one was ever supposed to be free. Set against an America consuming itself from within, it's about a desperate grip on one person amid both personal and national collapse.","sonnet":"A fractured address that moves between a lost loved one, political despair (\"America is burning from the inside out\"), and the speaker's own self-erasure — searching for a painless way through life by running away, words giving way, atoms disappearing — as if personal dissolution and national collapse mirror each other.","src":"haiku"},
"circle-of-dust~alt-human":{"opus":"The industrial project confronts the anxieties of transhumanism head-on, gene editing, engineered offspring, wiring brains to machines, and keeps asking how much a person can be altered before ceasing to be human. Its warning is that we're rushing to remake ourselves like reckless gods, heedless of what such tampering ultimately costs.","sonnet":"A warning about genetic engineering and the ethics of redesigning humanity through CRISPR and germline modification — the song asks how far biotechnology can go before the result is no longer human, framing designer children and god-like creation as a loaded gun with unknown long-term consequences.","src":"sonnet"},
"circle-of-dust~consequence-alex-yarmak-remix":{"opus":"Klayton's industrial project issues a severe Christian caution about accountability, picturing a life as a ledger where every action and careless remark will be recited back at a final judgment. Drawing on Francis Thompson's pursuing-God imagery, it warns that everything counts and that dismissing the divine invites eternal fallout, faith framed as an unavoidable reckoning.","sonnet":"A Christian warning about divine accountability — every action, word, and thought is recorded in a book that will be read at the final judgment, and the indispensable laws of God cannot be disobeyed without eternal consequence. The song is an earnest theological call to live in awareness of that reckoning.","src":"sonnet"},
"circle-of-dust~hive-mind":{"haiku":"Collective control replaces individual thought; the system pacifies through sedation while resistance dissolves into denial.","opus":"Klayton's industrial dystopia about mass sedation, a controlling collective that pacifies people who would rather not know they're being controlled. The horror is willing complicity: comfort bought by refusing to see the strings.","sonnet":"A shadowy controlling entity speaks directly to the population it manipulates, telling them their pacification is complete and their compliance makes the deception easy — no resistance, no questions, just the perpetuation of the system. The frankness is the horror: they know, and they know you won't act on it.","src":"haiku"},
"circle-of-dust~humanarchy":{"haiku":"Human nature breeds hierarchy and exploitation; arrogance and violence perpetuate endless cycles of predation.","opus":"A misanthropic verdict on the species, coining a word for humanity's self-serving disorder, arrogance and cruelty dressed up as compassion. It's a bleak indictment of the strong preying on the weak while congratulating ourselves on progress.","sonnet":"A bleak assessment of humanity as a species defined by arrogance, exploitation, and the gap between its pretensions to compassion and its actual behavior — perpetually at war, preying on the weak, destroying what it touches, and calling the result civilization. The neologism \"humanarchy\" frames human nature itself as the governing disorder.","src":"haiku"},
"circle-of-dust~k-os":{"haiku":"A dialogue warns that lies cannot sustain life—only an external agent or system can keep someone alive, though both exchange comfort in parting.","opus":"A fractured collage loops a glitching name against a menacing exchange about lies keeping no one alive. Circle of Dust, Klayton's earlier industrial-metal project before Celldweller, builds this interlude almost entirely from stuttered vocal samples and sampled dialogue rather than sung lyric, the chaos of its title enacted through digital fragmentation, a machine-voiced doctor-and-captain standoff serving as unsettling connective tissue rather than a self-contained song.","src":"haiku"},
"circle-of-dust~neophyte":{"haiku":"A narrative of arrival and initiation. An alien/divine visitor welcomes a newcomer as a satellite to a darker knowledge. The initiate must ascend through darkness to become their new self—a process framed as inevitable transformation.","opus":"Industrial B-movie theatrics wrapping a story of initiation, an outsider awakened from passive fantasy into a darker knowledge and a new beginning. Klayton stitches sci-fi invasion samples into a parable of transformation and belonging.","sonnet":"Framed around sampled sci-fi dialogue about alien invaders, the song addresses a newcomer — a \"neophyte\" — welcoming them into awakening, urging them to look up from passive drifting and embrace a new beginning, with a protective vow to never let them go.","src":"haiku"},
"circle-of-dust~neurachem":{"haiku":"Chemical enhancement promises transcendence of human weakness and limitation, but the song revels in the dream of shedding vulnerability itself.","opus":"A transhumanist fantasy of shedding human weakness by cranking up a neural drug that deletes fear and pain and amplifies the senses toward the Übermensch. What sounds like ascension reads as chilling self-erasure, deficiencies engineered away until nothing frail remains.","sonnet":"A transhumanist manifesto about chemically amplifying human capacity — excising weakness, deleting fear, redlining synaptic transmissions — to engineer a perfect, pain-proof Nietzschean Übermensch through neurochemical enhancement.","src":"haiku"},
"circle-of-dust~regressor-battlejuice-remix":{"haiku":"Mental dissolution and parasite feeding perpetuate cycles where death and life intertwine in recursive madness; the speaker regresses into infantile self-sabotage.","opus":"Industrial-metal grind about a mind devouring itself, torment it brings on itself and paralysis spiraling toward a numb, hollowed-out blankness. Klayton fixates on the thin border where living and dying blur into one another.","sonnet":"A fragmented portrait of mental deterioration — self-inflicted agony, indecisive lobotomising, faulty fires feeding mental parasites — where death and life intertwine in a loop of false humility and foolish self-destruction that resists any resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"circles~breaker":{"haiku":"Parasite humans selling the world for gold and hate; lying scars cover deeper dysfunction. The speaker fights systemic hypocrisy until their last breath.","opus":"Circles, an Australian djent-leaning prog band with polished melodic hooks, indict human greed as a self-inflicted apocalypse. The track argues that our hunger for wealth and power poisons the planet and corrupts truth itself, and it answers that ruin with a defiant vow to keep resisting the liars until there's nothing left to give.","sonnet":"Corporate and political parasites sell the earth itself for profit, fracturing communities with deception while the people who suffer it fight back with the clarity that comes from recognising hypocrisy. Prosperity built on exploitation is named as the force that must be broken before any genuine life can begin.","src":"haiku"},
"circles~sleepwalking":{"haiku":"Time passes while the narrator drifts unconsciously, unable to recognize life's passage or reclaim agency before dissolution.","opus":"From the Australian djent band, a reckoning with a life spent numbed and compromised, waking up to realize time never waited. It's the defiant terror of someone who has everything to lose deciding to feel again before the grave, even fearing they'd vanish unheard.","sonnet":"A speaker who drifted through years of self-deception — filling their mind with walls, hiding from what they knew — wakes to find the wishing well scorched dry and asks whether disappearing would even make a sound before they are in the ground.","src":"haiku"},
"cirrus~back-on-a-mission":{"haiku":"Hyped-up confidence and assertion of presence at the party. The speaker delivers infectious energy and claims to be invading minds with their kind.","opus":"A big-beat boast declares itself ready to invade minds and rock the joint, dropping breaks like bolts of lightning and passing the mic on a relentless mission. Cirrus, an American electronic act from the late-nineties breakbeat and big-beat wave, stack hip-hop braggadocio over dancefloor propulsion, the strange voices promising freedom and the shotgun-loud drops turning the track into pure kinetic party fuel with no aim beyond the groove.","sonnet":"A confident, no-frills DJ or MC announces a return with maximum energy, promising to cut through pretension and get straight to the groove. The mission is uncomplicated — invade the mind through sound, deliver the beat, and not stop until the job is done.","src":"haiku"},
"citadel~old-blood":{"haiku":"Self-loathing prevents acceptance of love while fear of fire consumes the narrator, trapping them in cycles of blame and toxic shame.","opus":"Self-loathing dressed as a love song: the narrator can only offer a partner his own emptiness, cast as both villain and victim, begging to be started over. The real subject is whether you can love anyone while unable to love yourself.","sonnet":"Deep self-loathing and a simultaneous plea for love: the speaker casts themselves as both villain and victim, sees only a \"twisted version of the true sight\" in the mirror, and asks a partner to breathe them into a new start — knowing that loving themselves might only be possible through that other person.","src":"haiku"},
"citizen~death-dance-approximately":{"opus":"Citizen slip into depressive stasis, a person grinding themselves down and scrabbling merely to be noticed, wanting nothing, refusing to step outdoors or speak to a soul. The ache that has swallowed them deepens by the day, and the emptiness of an unmarked sheet of paper grants only a brief calm. It's a stark study of isolation and self-punishment.","sonnet":"A song about isolation, self-punishment, and the desperate need to be heard — the narrator beats themselves down, pries and claws for recognition, refuses to engage with the world, and at one point is stripped of identity (given a number) by someone who wants to cause pain. The aching that takes over worsens daily with no relief in sight.","src":"sonnet"},
"citizen~i-want-to-kill-you":{"haiku":"The speaker cycles between exhaustion and compulsion, writing and running endlessly while drowning. They want power and war over everything else, and ask if anyone will come when they finally get too tired.","opus":"A weary self-examination, the narrator only feeling alive when he's useful and wondering whether anyone will show up once he's finally spent. Ohio band Citizen turn the violent title inward, the fantasies of power and destruction dissolving into exhaustion, his call for someone to return growing fainter and more desperate.","sonnet":"Exhausted by the relentless effort to keep producing and running, a person admits his emotional reserves are depleting while the things that make others happy feel just barely sufficient for him. He begs to know whether anyone will come when he finally cannot go on.","src":"haiku"},
"city-of-souls~ferryman":{"opus":"A song of heartbreak and surrender pictured through the ferryman of the dead, the narrator diminished and lost in time by grief, giving his body over entirely to another as they sail toward a distant sun.","sonnet":"A grief song in which heartbreak has reduced the narrator to a passive, lost figure being ferried toward something — possibly death, possibly surrender — with the world asleep around them and their sense of time and selfhood completely dissolved.","src":"sonnet"},
"city-of-souls~shimmer":{"haiku":"Seeks transformation and release from interior emptiness, feeling a connection to lost presence and natural rebirth. Longs for sun to restore meaning.","opus":"A sorrowful pivot toward rebirth, the singer sensing a departed presence among the trees and the rain, calling himself transformation while grappling with a creature that drains him and a demonic murmur, aching to break out of a barren space and let the light and ground glow once more. City of Souls, a New Zealand alt-metal group, lend this cry for release their sweeping, atmospheric weight.","sonnet":"A song of grief becoming presence — someone lost is now felt in rain, heard in dreams, woven into the world itself. The speaker has thrown away precious things and fed a beast that kept them awake, and now asks to be let out of an empty room into the light, wanting the earth to shimmer again as something like reunion, or at least recognition.","src":"haiku"},
"city-of-souls~wolf":{"opus":"City of Souls circle guilt and grudge, keeping watch on a predatory wolf while a figure twists in the grave inside the word regret. The imagery of ashen hands and drift toward the grave makes it a meditation on resentment worn out over time, on how holding on to old wounds slowly drags everyone down together.","sonnet":"The narrator watches a predatory, cunning threat circling with coiled patience, knowing that one slip means being snared — the wolf here is also internal, a personification of regret and grudge that pulls both parties slowly toward the grave.","src":"sonnet"},
"city-state~requiem":{"haiku":"Unable to save a loved one from self-destruction, the speaker becomes trapped in grief, chasing a meaningless high through loss.","opus":"This melodic-metalcore band writes a mourning song for a love destroyed by addiction. Unable to save a partner who became a shadow of themselves, the narrator confesses he secretly wished to go down with them, chasing hollow highs to feel something and left a stranger in his own life, carrying grief and guilt forward.","sonnet":"Grief after losing someone to their own unraveling — staying too long, refusing to see the signs — leaves the narrator stranded in a house that no longer feels inhabited. The loss is compounded by the wish, barely admitted, that the fall had taken them both: being left behind is its own kind of erasure.","src":"haiku"},
"city-state~wilt":{"opus":"A furious condemnation of a rapist who drained the life from his victim and claimed she wanted it; the singer vows retribution while depicting her ongoing trauma, nightmares, shame, and dissociation, promising the abuser will be consumed by his own sins.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of a rapist: the song describes how he violated a woman who believed she wanted it, left her with permanent trauma and nightmares, while he sleeps soundly, and the narrator promises he will be consumed by the consequences of what he did.","src":"sonnet"},
"cjbeards~hand-that-feeds-ya":{"haiku":"Betrayal justifies psychological retaliation; the speaker transforms from nurturer to serpent, striking at those who underestimated their venom.","opus":"A scorned partner turns menacing, warning a cheating, ungrateful lover that the good treatment is over and payback is coming. The song mocks the storybook romance the ex imagined, telling them there is no charming rescuer waiting and no way back, just a slammed door and a bitter goodbye.","sonnet":"A warning to an ungrateful, unfaithful partner: the speaker is cunning as a snake, bad karma is coming, and the one who was sustained by this relationship should have known better than to bite the hand that fed them.","src":"haiku"},
"cky~96-quite-bitter-beings":{"opus":"A darkly comic horror tale of Hellview, an isolated town of 96 murderous residents who stack the bodies of passing strangers and drown tourists in the lake; the trapped narrator just wants an answer and a way out.","sonnet":"A horror-tinged story about a cursed town called Hellview — 96 bitter inhabitants murder tourists, sink bodies in the lake, and trap anyone who arrives, the only escape being a flood that overwhelms the place. It's a folk-horror portrait of a place that eats people.","src":"sonnet"},
"clams-casino~32-levels-instrumental":{"haiku":"One climbs 32 levels by fighting alone without complaining; they observe injustice where the rich stay rich and the poor stay violent, staying true to themselves.","opus":"A rap of hard self-reliance, Lil B insisting nobody waits on your changes so you do it yourself, moving through a bleak inner landscape of dark with nowhere to shine, guns in the sky and a Rolls Royce with no one to drive, all faith and Based World defiance. Clams Casino is the New Jersey producer who pioneered cloud rap, and his hazy, ethereal beats frame Lil B's stream-of-consciousness resolve.","sonnet":"A survival monologue from someone who made it through invisibility and scarcity — screaming into silence, rolling alone, seeing guns in the sky and bullets in the rain, yet insisting on faith as the only weapon worth carrying. The thirty-two levels are internal, a climb made without anyone waiting at the top, powered entirely by self-belief.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~all-nite-feat-vince-staples":{"haiku":"Street aggression and readiness for violence permeate existence, with loyalty tested through willingness to harm and dominate rivals.","opus":"Vince Staples maps the North Long Beach he came up in without romance, gang life as inherited routine rather than glory. The relentless refrain of doing it all night is less bravado than the numb momentum of survival where violence is just the weather.","sonnet":"Vince Staples narrates street-level bravado from Long Beach — sliding in the same ride, staying strapped, unapologetically territorial — delivered as an all-night declaration of dominance and indifference to any threat.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~all-nite-instrumental":{"haiku":"A gang member roams their city at night bragging about violence and control; they live dangerous street life where everyone is a target.","opus":"A menacing West Coast gangsta rap cut set against turf, guns, and paranoia. The rapper brags about being unkillable, dares enemies to try, and stays defiantly reckless. The producer built his reputation on hazy, dreamlike cloud rap, but this track trades that atmosphere for hard, threatening street bravado.","sonnet":"A hard territorial boast from the Norf side — the speaker catalogues neighborhood loyalty, threats, and an all-night readiness for violence with the matter-of-fact confidence of someone who has never needed to prove it twice. Poppy Street anchors the geography; everything else is momentum, menace, and the refusal to slow down.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~amor-fati-remix-washed-out":{"haiku":"Acceptance of what cannot be changed becomes a spiritual practice, faith replacing hope as the architecture of one's world is acknowledged and surrendered to.","opus":"Named for the Nietzschean love of one's fate, gentle absolution set to a haze: stop straining, none of it was your fault, your faith decides your road and time will set you right. Comfort offered as surrender to whatever comes.","sonnet":"A gentle Stoic acceptance of fate — the speaker encourages relinquishing control, trusting the design of one's own life, and allowing faith and letting-go to guide them rather than forcing outcomes, even through difficulty.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~back-to-you-instrumental":{"haiku":"A person keeps returning to memories of someone who lied and left them; though they try to move forward, the past keeps them trapped.","opus":"A ghostly song about being unable to let go of someone who repeatedly deceived, drawn helplessly back time after time even though the memory betrays and wounds. The producer, a pioneer of hazy cloud rap, wraps the compulsion in washed-out, spectral textures that make the longing feel like something dissolving out of a dream.","sonnet":"A reckoning with a love that keeps returning despite itself — the speaker has built defenses, revised memories, tried to design a life that doesn't loop back, and still finds themselves pulled toward someone who lied and left. The empty room fills with old songs, and the return feels less like choice than gravity.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~be-somebody-feat-a-ap-rocky-lil-b":{"haiku":"Aspiration and survival drive transcend systems of limitation, celebrating self-made authority and dominance through charisma and determination.","opus":"A$AP Rocky and Lil B trade verses on clawing up from empty plates to fame, insisting money has no race and that becoming somebody meant everything. Underneath the flexing runs an uneasy admission that success bought power but also lurking evil and hunger for more.","sonnet":"Three perspectives on ambition and identity converge: A$AP Rocky links Black wealth to erasure of race, Lil B claims BasedGod perfection while acknowledging lurking evil, and the hook frames it all as a plea to become someone real — \"be somebody\" — out of hunger and empty plates.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~bookfiend-doom":{"haiku":"Riddled truths hide within hooks and stories; shame and hypocrisy layer beneath grodieness as the corrupt seek enlightenment through deception.","opus":"A dense DOOM verse coding drug pursuit as occult riddle, where scoring becomes an initiation only insiders decode. Beneath the wordplay sits a portrait of solitude, sensitive kids, and the ordinary desperation behind the mystique.","sonnet":"MF DOOM raps as a book obsessive and underground dealer of rare knowledge — Tetragrammaton, Octagon, Amazon orders held in secret — whose addiction to reading and solitude is his particular poison; the answer to every riddle is hidden in the hook, and the only way in is to already be a known member.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~cold-feet-mac-miller":{"haiku":"Arrogance and excess mask hollowness as the speaker boasts of conquest and transcendence; drugs and seduction become proof of power over those deemed inferior.","opus":"Young Mac Miller flexes with drugs, sex, and self-belief while brushing off doubters who can't read or write. The recurring image of chasing money that keeps getting cold feet hints at ambition and success that stay just out of reach beneath the bravado.","sonnet":"A teenage braggadocio monologue from a young rapper trying to marry money — \"that bitch kept gettin' cold feet\" — while projecting cocky confidence over drug use, sexual boasting, and contempt for anyone who doubts him. The music-as-validation thread running through beats \"getting shit on\" and crowds screaming keeps the hustler mythology alive beneath the excess.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~cold-war":{"haiku":"Ideological conflict persists without clear purpose; both sides fight on despite not understanding what requires fighting.","opus":"Little more than a single haunting question looped, but the effect is pointed: a challenge to anyone caught in slow, grinding, undeclared conflict to admit they no longer know what they're even fighting for.","sonnet":"A single repeated question asking whether the person knows what they are actually fighting for — the lyric offers no answer, only the relentless interrogation of purposeless conflict.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~ghost-in-a-kiss-feat-samuel-t-herring":{"haiku":"Spectral presence within intimate touch; haunting absence materializes through kissing and contact.","opus":"Herring's voice haunting a hazy elegy for a love that was never quite held, present only as a trace on a kiss or a hand. Regret circles that same threshold a thousand times over, never able to walk through it.","sonnet":"Looking back at a relationship, the speaker realises the other person was never fully present — \"a ghost in a kiss, a ghost in my hand\" — and that he had his chance and squandered it. The bluebells, the grove arcade, the million turnings toward a door build a landscape of missed opportunity that persists as sensory memory even after the connection has dissolved.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~ghost-in-a-kiss-instrumental":{"haiku":"A fleeting figure haunts the narrator like a ghost; moments of closeness feel illusory and are soon forgotten along an arcade of memory.","opus":"A hazy elegy for a love that was never fully real or his to keep, memory circling a doorway and an arcade walk. The beloved lingers as a ghost that slipped through his hands even at the moment of contact.","sonnet":"A meditation on a love that was always spectral and ungraspable — the narrator reflects that the person was never truly present, more ghost than partner, and that every opportunity to hold onto them slipped away.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~i-m-god":{"haiku":"Suppressed thoughts and polite compliance masking rage, biting tongue until breaking point.","opus":"A cloud-rap instrumental built on a chopped, ghostly film sample of strained family dinner tension, the fragments about biting your tongue evoking suppressed anger rather than telling a story. Mood over meaning.","sonnet":"A sample-based track built on a single overheard domestic moment — someone receiving an unwanted gift, suppressing irritation, biting their tongue, taking deep breaths; the loop of 'it's what I think / bite tongue / deep breaths' captures the exhausting restraint of swallowing one's true reaction in social situations.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~i-m-god-lil-b":{"haiku":"Suppressed thoughts and polite compliance masking rage, biting tongue until breaking point.","opus":"The same ethereal beat woven from a looped sample of swallowed frustration at a dinner table, the repeated deep breaths conjuring restrained fury. Atmosphere as the whole point.","sonnet":"A sampled, looping track built around the mundane discomfort of a family gathering — someone is trying to suppress their thoughts while another person won't stop talking, and the instruction to bite your tongue and breathe becomes a kind of meditation on restraint; it's oblique, more texture than narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~into-the-fire-feat-mikky-ekko":{"haiku":"Danger and exposure converge as the speaker craves the test of intimacy; burning becomes the price of connection and transformation.","opus":"A surrender to a dangerous, consuming love, stepping willingly into the flame fully exposed and knowing it may burn. The idea that you can't offer love until you shut out the light frames vulnerability and risk as the price of the addiction.","sonnet":"A surrender to dangerous desire: the speaker knows the test is risky and they are entirely exposed, but is addicted to an \"original furia\" they cannot name — asking to be taken into the fire because love cannot be shown without sealing the light first. The repetition of \"take me\" at the close enacts the total relinquishment the lyric describes.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~into-the-fire-instrumental":{"haiku":"Two lovers expose themselves to each other in a dangerous test of passion; one asks the other to take them into the fire without holding back.","opus":"A song that treats romantic and physical surrender as something perilous and irresistible, the singer knowingly giving in to a consuming attraction despite the harm it may cause. Clams Casino, the New Jersey producer credited with inventing cloud rap, wraps the tension in the hazy, reverb-soaked beats that defined his signature sound.","sonnet":"A love that doubles as danger — the speaker knows the test is risky, knows they are entirely exposed, and walks into it anyway because withholding only seals the light rather than preserving it. The recurring word furia names the addictive current underneath: not just desire but something more elemental, a fire that the body can barely hold.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~leaf":{"haiku":"Seduction through luxury and transcendence merges material excess with supernatural allure, rendering the lover immortal through possession and devotion.","opus":"A Russian cloud-rap fantasy where cocaine and a white Porsche Panamera fuse into one gleaming symbol of decadence, the mayor's daughter and eternal winter as trophies. Snow means both the drug and the persona, intoxication rendered as flight through Kremlin skies.","sonnet":"Russian rapper Sneg (Snow) celebrates high-speed nocturnal freedom in a white Porsche Panamera with the mayor's daughter beside him — accelerating past Kremlin walls, crossing double yellow lines, the whole city losing its mind for the young rapper while eternal winter trails behind.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~level-1-instrumental":{"haiku":"A collaborator celebrates arrival and announces a boundless, ascending system—32 levels extending inward, always returning multiplied.","opus":"Barely a song, more a spoken intro, Lil B and Clams Casino announcing the 32 Levels project, promising a mind changed tenfold and Based World mantras. It functions as a threshold rather than a narrative. Clams Casino is the New Jersey producer who pioneered cloud rap, and this brief interlude simply frames the ethereal, hazy world his beats build across the record.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~numb":{"haiku":"Burnout and flight overtake compliance; the speaker escapes the grinding routine, recognizing system addiction feeds on surrendered futures.","opus":"A German verse about walking out on the grinding, mortgage-paying rat race, the deadening treadmill where you sink the harder you tread, to finally learn to walk by learning to quit. Waking to sun on the face and simply vanishing is the dream of anyone numbed by the daily grind.","sonnet":"A German-language song about walking away from a deadening job and routine life — the narrator quits, escapes the hamster wheel of rent payments and treadmill days, and wakes up one morning to sunlight, finally free to run.","src":"haiku"},
"clams-casino~she-s-hot":{"opus":"Clams Casino pioneered the cloud-rap and witch-house sound of the early 2010s, building tracks from smeared, submerged samples where a single vocal phrase becomes hook, chorus, and entire structure at once. That method defines this piece: the sense of loving someone destined to leave because you can't love properly is not developed but looped, the hypnotic repetition doing the emotional work a narrative never gets to.","sonnet":"The lyrics consist of a single phrase repeated — the sense of loving someone who will inevitably leave because you can't love properly — functioning as a hypnotic loop rather than a developed narrative.","src":"sonnet"},
"clams-casino~the-world-needs-change":{"haiku":"Connection transcends isolation through shared resonance; separate forms merge into unified consciousness where individual grief becomes collective transformation.","opus":"A meditation on isolation and connection, lost in a waveform and cut off, then finding oneness by elevating past being torn apart. Separation and reunion trade places until scattered selves piece back together as one.","sonnet":"A meditation on isolation and reconnection — cut off from others and lost inside oneself, the narrator eventually finds unity through shared creation and emotional resonance.","src":"haiku"},
"clarence-clarity~it-is-happening-again":{"haiku":"Fractured identity and cyclical self-destruction repeat without resolution; the speaker feels split between simulation and authenticity, unable to stop cycles of violence they cannot understand.","opus":"A fractured mind loops through violent compulsions and self-hatred, half-simulation and half-self, cleaved in two and worn away by time. It's about the terror of a breakdown that keeps recurring, and the guilty sense of being complicit in one's own unraveling.","sonnet":"The speaker is caught in a recurring mental crisis — \"half by imitation, half myself,\" born at the bottom of a lake — in which a violent intrusive impulse returns and a hatred wasted on others may ultimately be self-directed; the chorus pleads for divine help even as it admits to capitulation.","src":"haiku"},
"clay-j-gladstone~is-this-how-i-die":{"haiku":"Long-standing mental illness creates despair where conventional treatment fails and hopelessness becomes permanent landscape.","opus":"A blunt panic attack set to music: years of unhelped anxiety, the exhaustion of people asking if you're fine, and the grim joke of wanting a lobotomy. The recurring question is dead serious.","sonnet":"An anxiety-saturated self-assessment spirals through fear, failed therapy, and black humour — \"need a lobotomy\" — the refrain \"is this how I die?\" marking less a fear of death than exhaustion with being alive in this state.","src":"haiku"},
"cleopatrick~family-van":{"haiku":"Artistic ambition collides with betrayal and hollow friendship; the speaker denounces those who copy their voice and claim intimacy while feeding on loneliness.","opus":"A hometown rapper turns on imitators and label types who only paid attention once he had a hit, calling their approval hollow. Beneath the disses it is about isolation, self-doubt at almost twenty-three, and refusing to be hollowed out like them.","sonnet":"Anger at someone who plagiarised the speaker's work and stories while he was making coffee and finding himself through poetry; the betrayal is compounded by fake friendship, and the speaker's response is contempt — you're hollow, you're unknown — punctuated by a line borrowed from a girlfriend: \"surprise of disguise only works in unmasking.\"","src":"haiku"},
"cleopatrick~lost":{"haiku":"Hedonistic escape through travel and transgressive pleasure obscures deeper abandonment and loss while desire consumes judgment.","opus":"The Canadian rock duo cloak a darker story in a swaggering love song: a man drawn into moving drugs and being trafficked through glamorous cities, the litany of destinations masking exploitation dressed up as freedom and devotion.","sonnet":"A man entangled with a woman he is drawing into drug trafficking — \"I got her out here cooking dope\" — frames her current lost state across Miami, Tokyo, and Amsterdam as temporary, promising she will one day be cooking meals for a family of her own.","src":"haiku"},
"cleopatrick~now-zero":{"haiku":"Betrayal by those trusted transforms the narrator into a void, abandoning civility and demanding accountability through confrontation.","opus":"A furious severance of a friendship or bond, addressed to someone who lost their empathy and subtlety and became nothing to the narrator. It curdles into a wider disillusionment with spineless men and a desperate closing plea for something to believe in.","sonnet":"Someone the speaker trusted has gradually shed empathy and subtlety, becoming unrecognisable; furious that fake allies rest their wrists on empty seats and say things they don't mean, the speaker reaches zero tolerance and declares the person means nothing to them now.","src":"haiku"},
"cleopatrick~ok":{"haiku":"Self-awareness of phoniness and emotional unavailability persists despite efforts to connect, leaving the narrator perpetually misaligned with others.","opus":"A self-lacerating portrait of performing masculinity and numbness, chasing a woman who couldn't care less while confessing the narrator feels nothing off-record. The looped acquiescence becomes the sound of losing yourself to a hollow scene you can't leave.","sonnet":"A self-aware mess of a person drifts through performative hedonism — lying about being self-employed, riding Ubers, suspecting a girl only wanted the Nikes — before acknowledging he has lost himself entirely and defaulting to an apathetic \"ok\" as a shield against feeling.","src":"haiku"},
"cleopatrick~sanjake":{"haiku":"Charismatic friendship commands total loyalty and impulsive devotion; the speaker follows a magnetic figure's whims without question, embodying the intoxicating grip of peer influence.","opus":"A small-town kid finds his footing in music and drily notes the same people who ignored him now act like friends. Skipping work with the crew becomes a statement of loyalty over fake love and belated respect.","sonnet":"A swaggering, tender ode to crew loyalty: the speaker has found his place not in fame or romantic attention but in the faces of his friends — Sanjake and Shorten set the terms for the night, and wherever they go he follows, signing off with a fake sick-day voicemail as the punchline.","src":"haiku"},
"cleopatrick~zuck":{"haiku":"Technological surveillance justifies the surrender of privacy and autonomy; the algorithm becomes god and the self becomes commodity.","opus":"Surrendering privacy to Zuckerberg's platforms with a shrug, tangled up with a lover's grievance over an Instagram story. The Canadian duo satirize how casually we trade our inner lives to the cloud for a little more life.","sonnet":"A speaker confesses to choosing digital life — privacy surrendered to Mark's updated policies, scrolling past real friends — while also apologising to a partner for breaking immersion in an Instagram story; social media's hold on attention is treated as both resignation and low-grade guilt.","src":"haiku"},
"clint-lowery~god-bless-the-renegades":{"haiku":"Society clings to being victimized and entertained by rebellion while real rebels face danger; the speaker mourns how love dies as distance grows and cowardice reigns.","opus":"Sevendust guitarist Clint Lowery's solo song mocks a culture hooked on spectacle, where affection is disposable entertainment and everyone plays the wounded party. Watching celebrities flee predators who imagine themselves righteous, he sarcastically salutes the outsiders, condemning a world that trades itself away for pretty falsehoods while the gutless perish in isolation.","sonnet":"A sardonic benediction for a culture that mistakes performance for authenticity and victimhood for identity. Superstars flee cannibals while ordinary people chase beautiful lies, and the narrator watches the whole spectacle from a position of exhausted, bitter clarity.","src":"haiku"},
"closure-in-moscow~better-way":{"haiku":"Ghostly dissolution and memory-haunting mark the self as living dead; the speaker paces through the past unable to resurrect what once gave meaning.","opus":"A haunted, gothic search for any escape from grief that keeps the narrator dancing with the dead and killing one night after another. The prog-rock sprawl circles a razed garden of love, half-hoping to survive it.","sonnet":"Grief so deep the speaker exists as a ghost — skin and bones disappearing on the wind — pacing through memories of feeling alive, unable to stop dancing with the dead or wishing the absent beloved back. The refrain there must be a better way is both plea and admission that none has been found.","src":"haiku"},
"cloudyfield~in-your-head":{"haiku":"Moral inversion and victimhood become weapons as the speaker drowns in suffering while the other person's internal narrative casts them as villain despite knowing the truth of the dynamic.","opus":"A relationship split into a private mythology where, in the other person's head, they are the good one and the speaker is the bad. Drowning in misery under a lifeless gaze, he wonders what would change if they had simply shared the same pain.","sonnet":"A standoff between two people drowning in misery: the speaker sees the other's lifeless gaze trying to bury them, and challenges the narrative where one is the villain and the other the hero, asking what if the pain is actually shared.","src":"haiku"},
"cloudyfield~parasite":{"haiku":"Parasitic corruption and internal infection spreading decay, the speaker as host for destructive organism.","opus":"A relationship metabolized as infection: the other person a parasite lodged for life, a demon in orbit the narrator can't stop orbiting back. He'd die for it even as it burns heart-shaped scars into him.","sonnet":"A suffocating relationship where the other person has become a demon in orbit, piercing wings and leaving heart-shaped burns. The narrator can't breathe, would lie and die for this person, and still can't name it as anything other than a parasite — something that lives at the expense of everything else.","src":"haiku"},
"clowns~bland-is-the-new-black":{"haiku":"The speaker critiques contemporary culture as unsafe and homogeneous, mourning when transgression and risk-taking were celebrated instead of sanitized mediocrity.","opus":"The Australian punk band rails against a sanitized, risk-averse culture that has drained all danger and fun from music and life. Nostalgic for an era when transgression was celebrated, the narrator demands something reckless and exciting, declaring he would rather die spectacularly than expire from sheer boredom.","sonnet":"A frustrated observer mourns the homogenization of contemporary music and culture, yearning for the dangerous and genuinely strange energy that once made rock feel alive. Safety and inoffensiveness have replaced risk, and the narrator would rather burn out than die quietly of mediocrity.","src":"haiku"},
"club2020~przyp-yw":{"haiku":"Moral indifference during crisis, prioritizing advantage as the tide rises and drowns everyone equally.","opus":"A Polish rap parable about a rising tide of self-interest, where survival means abandoning the people close to you and swimming against the current alone. Ambition as drowning, everyone reduced to a meaningless statistic.","sonnet":"A drowning metaphor for self-sabotage and ambition: when the tide (przypływ) is high and opportunity floods in, the speaker throws loved ones overboard, refuses the lifeline, and swims against the current — the refrain 'when the tide is in I have no time for those close to me' indicts the single-minded chase for gain.","src":"haiku"},
"club2020~przypływ":{"haiku":"Rising water metaphorically drowns the speaker's agency as tide and circumstance overwhelm personal will. Survival strategy collapses against the inevitability of flood and loss.","src":"haiku"},
"coach-party~disco-dream":{"haiku":"Solitary ecstasy in anonymity and rhythm, needing only oneself and music to transcend.","opus":"A defiant celebration of solitude on the dance floor, the narrator wanting no lover and no friends, just herself and the music. Independence reframed as the purest kind of night out.","sonnet":"A celebration of solitary dancing — the narrator doesn't need friends or a partner, just herself, the music, and the dance floor. It's about the specific joy of losing yourself completely in a club by your own choice, not loneliness but deliberate self-sufficient pleasure.","src":"haiku"},
"coach-party~what-s-the-point-in-life":{"haiku":"Existential nihilism renders striving meaningless when mortality is certain, dissolving all purpose into apathy and autonomous isolation.","opus":"Nihilist shrug as anthem: doomscrolling apocalypse anxiety and defiant self-isolation, insisting you don't need anyone because we're all going to die anyway. The bravado thinly masks the dread it keeps repeating.","sonnet":"An ironic shrug at existential dread: gamma rays, power plants, weird guys at the door, the certainty of death — none of it urgently matters when you are at home watching TV, self-sufficient, and content to jump off the edge in your own time.","src":"haiku"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~c-o-t-d":{"haiku":"Choosing the coaltar path despite barriers; solitude and dreams mark the deepers' way.","opus":"The Japanese shoegaze-metal shapeshifters offer a self-referential, dreamlike incantation, the band's own name woven through fractured English about choices, not looking back, and being wound toward someone's way. Meaning stays deliberately slippery, cheer calls and barriers with eyes floating past logic. Coaltar of the Deepers prize texture and mood over narrative, the lyrics functioning as another layer of their genre-blurring haze rather than a story.","sonnet":"A figure moving forward without looking back reaches toward something fine and unspecified — a door, a dream, a line of sight finally located after long searching. The lyric's English is fractured but the emotional orientation is clear: persistence without regret, openness to whatever the next moment holds.","src":"haiku","fable":"A self-titled track that treats the band's own name as an incantation, chanting it at the close of every stanza until it stops meaning a group and becomes a place you get wound toward. Around that refrain, Coaltar of the Deepers borrow the syntax of a pep talk about success and refusing to glance backward, but the borrowed English keeps fracturing exactly where feeling should peak, the grammar buckling into a lone dreamer beckoning someone onward. The movement drifts from bright cheerful noise toward isolation: the second pass swaps encouragement for aloneness while keeping the same shape, so the mantra can no longer tell whether it celebrates a bond or admits only the dream remains. The broken diction is not a flaw to translate around but the whole point, the reaching of a shoegaze act that wants to promise everything and can only spell out its own name.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"the band's own name chanted until it becomes a place not a group","built":"the fractured English buckles exactly where feeling should peak"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~dear-future-18":{"haiku":"Two people cannot communicate their love despite reaching toward each other; predetermined sadness obscures the water-born beginning.","src":"haiku"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~dear-future-tv-size":{"haiku":"Two people cannot communicate their love despite reaching toward each other; predetermined sadness obscures the water-born beginning.","opus":"A shoegaze-metal band writing an anime-length version of a love song about two people crying in front of each other yet blind to each other's tears, straining to hear a word of love. The plea is against fatalism, an insistence that nothing is fixed and the future stays theirs to reach.","sonnet":"Two people lost and uncertain of where they're going — one reassuring the other that whatever predetermined path exists, the sadness isn't their fault, and remembering a beginning that felt like swimming: a tender, unresolved letter to the future.","src":"haiku"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~giant":{"haiku":"Spiral of lost dreams and recovered doors; barefoot journeys toward tomorrow's discovery.","opus":"Coaltar of the Deepers drift through a Japanese-language reverie of a dream once seen, straight lines vanishing while the narrator wanders in search of the right moment. Yesterday packed into a box means another farewell, yet opening the spiral door promises reunion even now. Shouted GIANT interjections rupture the softness as the band, masters of Japanese shoegaze, chase tomorrow barefoot toward a door of dreams both near and far.","sonnet":"Time and memory spiral outward like a dream that keeps extending — yesterday packed away in a box, tomorrow's door thrown open, the path ahead barefoot and wide. The Japanese lyric traces a gentle, looping optimism: even what fades in a spiral can be met again through the door of the next dream.","src":"haiku","fable":"Giant works as a shoegaze lullaby about returning to a place inside a dream, and NARASAKI builds its whole meaning from a single spatial contradiction: the line of the dream that stretches straight ahead yet vanishes, then reappears rendered as a spiral (らせん状) — a shape that goes forward and returns at once, so tomorrow and yesterday keep meeting through the same door. The verses treat memory as something you can pack yesterday into a box and say goodbye to, but the door of dreams keeps reopening, which is why the song can insist we’ll surely still meet even now while everything it names is receding. The eruptions of GIANT! against the whispered Japanese are the point: the dynamic between hushed reverie and the enormous wall of sound is the emotional argument, the small dreaming voice dwarfed by the scale of what it’s reaching toward. The closing turn — going barefoot to find a tomorrow that is both far and near — resolves the spiral not by escaping it but by stepping into it unshod, accepting the loop as the way forward.","fv":2,"themes":["nostalgia & memory","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"the dreaming voice reaching toward what keeps receding","built":"hushed verses dwarfed by the enormous shoegaze GIANT"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~good-morning":{"haiku":"Morning light stirs phantom memories of a lost loved one; routine continues despite the absence numbing into forgetting.","opus":"Waking to glance at the empty space beside him, dazzled by morning sun, the singer recalls a dead lover's kindness and confronts the quiet horror that the grief which once felt fatal is now beginning to fade.","sonnet":"The mechanical routine of a morning after bereavement — waking, dressing, going out — contains a stunned, quiet admission: the grief that once felt fatal is already beginning to blur at the edges.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grief song that hides its bomb until the last line. It reads at first as ordinary morning loneliness — waking, glancing sideways at the empty space where you should be, dressing, going out into the town without even noticing what's missing — the deliberately mundane routine standing in for numb persistence. Then it names the thing: when you died I thought I was finished too, but now I'm starting to forget. That final turn is the real subject — not the loss but the horror of surviving it, the too-bright morning sun made unbearable precisely because the grief is fading and forgetting feels like a second betrayal.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"surviving grief made unbearable by the horror of forgetting","built":"mundane morning routine holds the bomb until the final line detonates it","fvr":"2.4"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~gyouni-slope-of-spring":{"src":"web","web":"A person is emotionally frozen, caught between moments, unable to move forward or back, endlessly searching for something they cannot name; the narrator offers patient, unconditional shelter — \"if your feet freeze in fear, I can wait here forever\" — and promises to paint a picture of hope to help them step toward tomorrow."},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~jet-set":{"haiku":"Trapped in webs of proximity and desire; escape from suffocating intimacy requires violent departure.","opus":"A speaker feels ensnared like trapped prey, driven to breaking point by a nightly tormentor before deciding to flee on his own terms. Genre-hopping Japanese band Coaltar of the Deepers set the broken, dreamlike English against swirling shoegaze-metal noise, turning claustrophobic entrapment into a blurred, half-lucid escape.","sonnet":"Ensnared by someone who invades nightly without permission and slowly dismantles the narrator's sense of self, the only coherent response is flight — boarding a figurative jet toward whatever escape still seems possible. Torment and a desperate grab at freedom share the same short breath.","src":"haiku","fable":"A trapped-insect lyric that mistakes its cage for a launchpad. The speaker opens caught in a web, driven half-mad by a nightly visitor he cannot bring himself to refuse — the broken-English grammar (a mind too slow to say no, a hell he opens rather than shuts) does real work here, staging a will that can't organize itself into escape. So the fantasy takes over: the jet on the runway, an exit he crowns in glory, a life he declares barely started. But the read lives in the gap between those two registers — the ecstatic getaway is bolted onto a plea to be let out and a sorrow he begs to be handed, so the flight to glory reads less as freedom than as the same dependency rebranded, a captive narrating his own liftoff while the web still holds.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape"],"means":"captive narrating his own liftoff while the web holds","built":"broken-grammar staging a will too slow to refuse"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~joy-ride":{"haiku":"Endless driving toward nowhere; perpetual motion and novelty replace purpose or destination.","opus":"Aimless motion masquerading as liberation: the driver, a companion beside him and open road ahead, keeps speeding nowhere until the tedium of the trip itself becomes the point. The proposed fix is to simply swap vehicles and press on, a joyride that leads to no destination at all.","sonnet":"Two people drive fast with no destination, the open sky eventually turning tedious, until restlessness itself becomes the point and the only answer is to trade the car and keep moving.","src":"haiku","fable":"The joy ride is a fake-out: the sports-car iconography and English-language cruising clichés are pure surface. The song’s engine isn’t speed but boredom — reaching the far blue horizon just confirms there’s nowhere to arrive, so the fix is always the next identical machine, another purchase, never a destination. NARASAKI plays the whole verse twice verbatim with no development precisely because there is none; the identical repetition is the argument, a loop of consumerist novelty-chasing dressed as freedom, where boredom and the next buy are the only motion the structure allows.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape","party & hedonism"],"means":"boredom dressed as speed with nowhere to arrive","built":"the verse played twice verbatim because nothing develops"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~melody":{"src":"web","web":"A cover of Boris's 黒猫メロディ (Black Cat Melody), sung by Wata in Japanese: a nocturnal, atmospheric piece in which a speaker watches a mysterious figure pass by, longing for them — images of someone singing in the night, a back receding, the feeling of needing wings to follow."},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~my-speedy-sarah":{"src":"web","web":"An address to a woman who moves through life too fast and too carelessly: the speaker pleads with her to be happy like the sun even while he stands in endless rain, and confesses that her speed — her indifference — is killing him."},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~natsu-no-gyouninzaka-final-summer-mix":{"haiku":"Time spirals backward as the speaker ascends, carrying memories of parallel lives; summer pain melts away as seasons turn and renewal beckons.","opus":"In Japanese, climbing a slope where time seems to run backward, past selves brushing by unnoticed, all under the endless drone of cicadas. Summer's stinging pain dissolves into the chest of a passerby who has cast off even his name, dreaming of a barren field he can never return to.","sonnet":"A Japanese-language meditation on a summer slope where time reverses: climbing the hill, the speaker passes through the past, brushing against someone they missed then, that figure dissolving into the chest of a wanderer (gyounin) as the cicadas drone on forever. Summer's pain is both physical and temporal — a season that cannot end and a person who cannot be reached.","src":"haiku"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~no-thank-you":{"haiku":"Desperation and torment; refusing the offered hell of closeness.","opus":"The shapeshifting Japanese shoegaze-metal band conjures a nightmare of persecution, a guillotine and electric chair descending as if built personally for the narrator, fear bleeding into everything he sees while he lies paralyzed and unable to escape the visible hell. A child's coaxing voice beckons him closer, and his lone reply, a flat refusal, becomes the only defiance left.","sonnet":"Japanese lyrics depict a figure paralysed in a landscape of terror — a guillotine descending from the sky, an electric chair, hell visible and inescapable. Vision is stolen, movement is gone. A child's beckoning voice calls toward the sound of clapping hands. The final rejection — no thank you — refuses whatever invitation that voice extends.","src":"haiku","fable":"A suicide fantasy staged as ritual theater: the singer, head bowed in a suffocating landscape where everything seen registers as terror, watches heaven split open to lower a guillotine built just for him, then an electric chair, each instrument of execution arriving as a personal gift while hell yawns below and he cannot claw his way up. The Japanese verses render self-destruction with an almost devotional calm — the blade wrapped in peace, the body pinned and sightless — so that the pull toward death sounds like relief rather than horror. What snaps it shut is the turn to a nursery singsong, the voice of blind-man’s-bluff calling come this way, toward the clapping hands: death coaxing him along like a children’s game, luring the seeker with cupped hands. And the title, breaking out in blunt English against all that lush surrender, is the refusal — NO THANK YOU as the one word he can throw back at the invitation, the shoegaze prettiness of NARASAKI’s arrangement making the decline feel less like triumph than exhausted politeness toward his own annihilation.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"decline phrased as exhausted politeness toward annihilation","built":"a nursery call luring the blinded self toward the hands"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~star-love":{"haiku":"Returning to nostalgic places under starlight with a companion; youth and intoxication unite two people in eternal romance.","opus":"A dreamy Japanese shoegaze-metal fantasy of escape, two lovers racing a small rocket back to the nostalgic star where they grew up. It's less about romance than the wish to outrun the present toward a remembered home, come what may.","sonnet":"A Japanese song about two young people racing through the night on a highway to return to the place where they grew up, dreaming of stars and their shared past — nostalgic, hopeful, the narrator launching a small rocket of love toward the star they both came from.","src":"haiku","fable":"A getaway song disguised as a space-flight fantasy: the two lovers gun down a night highway toward the star where they grew up, and NARASAKI keeps swapping the vehicle — first the freeway car, then a small rocket of love, then a Warp jump . The escape keeps upgrading its own science-fiction until Earth itself becomes the star they gaze back at. The destination never changes, only the metaphor’s altitude, which is the point: the whispered parenthetical chant of seishun-geki / tōsui (youth-drama / intoxication) exposes the whole thing as staged adolescent rapture, a nostalgia for a home-planet that is really just being young together. Against that irony the refrain lands sincerely — let whatever happens happen — so the song rides shoegaze’s fusion of narcotic sweetness and drive-fast momentum to say the getaway matters more than the arrival, and the two of them, forever together, are already there.","fv":2,"themes":["freedom & escape","love & desire","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"the getaway mattering more than any destination reached","built":"the vehicle upgrading its science-fiction while the destination stays fixed"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~the-systems-made-of-greed":{"haiku":"Systemic corruption built on human greed and ego, destroying trust while authorities exploit through media control.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verse-motion is a bluff: a protest song that keeps circling back to the same tautology — the world is just human ego stacked into walls — because it can never break through to a program, only re-diagnose the corruption. Between the two families of images (construction/walls of trust vs. media, authority, purchased justice) the song locates rot not in a villain but in the structure itself, the law weaponized against the sense it was built to encode, so that even the accusatory questions about justice for sale can only be posed, never answered. The return to the opening stanza is the argument’s admission that there’s no exit — you end where you started, dazed inside the manmade thing, the shoegaze wall-of-sound making the wall of egoism you’re trapped in audible rather than merely named.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"re-diagnosing rot because no program can break through","built":"the opening verse returned intact to close the ring"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~unlimber":{"src":"web","web":"An all-English incantation addressed to someone who causes pain: the speaker repeatedly demands \"spell me for the conscious pain\" and \"lay down on the floor,\" while accusing the other of crying out while thinking only of themselves — desire and torment tangled in abstract, shoegaze-drenched language."},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~wipeout":{"haiku":"Digital collaboration and creative connection vibrate through MIDI language where binary exchange merges separate creators. Technology becomes bridge for communication and artistic partnership.","opus":"A playful ode to making music through MIDI, sequencers, and an MPC, where creation becomes a collaboration of zeros and ones passed between two people. The Japanese verses fold spring evenings and heaven's voice into the technical thrill of nudging a track two seconds ahead.","sonnet":"A celebration of MIDI and music-making: spring evenings by the shore, MIDI lines aligning, MPC operated with lucky hands, 0s and 1s binding creator and listener together in a standard-file collaboration — the wipeout is cool and tough, not destruction but creative flow.","src":"haiku"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~wipeout-retake":{"haiku":"MIDI collaboration connecting binary through standard files; cool precision masks emotional exchange between two creators.","src":"haiku"},
"coast-arcade~city-limits":{"haiku":"Emotional walls crumble as collapse approaches, with flight offering no escape from the inescapable weight of intimacy and decline.","opus":"Watching someone become the thing they once hated as their world darkens and caves in, the narrator torn between the hope it gets easier and the sinking feeling everything's collapsing. A song about helplessly witnessing a loved one's descent.","sonnet":"A speaker watches someone they know retreat from connection — staring past the cityscape while everything caves in around the darkened street — asking whether they are becoming what they hate and why they keep running away at night when it only gets harder, not easier.","src":"haiku"},
"code-orange~a-drone-opting-out-of-the-hive":{"haiku":"Individual consciousness rebels against total surveillance and systemic control, reclaiming autonomy through deliberate disconnection and refusal.","opus":"A blistering refusal of conformity and surveillance, phones bagged like downed bees, consciousness colonized. The narrator declares himself the defect that breaks the pattern, author and character of his own story, rejecting the sterile order imposed on families and minds alike.","sonnet":"A self-declared rogue intelligence — the drone opting out — names the colonised consciousness of the crowd as the real threat: surveillance, razor-wire truths, and beige-walled domestic order are symptoms of a hive that has swallowed selfhood, and the drone positions itself as the shock that keeps the system honest.","src":"haiku"},
"code-orange~bleeding-in-the-blur":{"haiku":"Artists are consumed and exploited by a dehumanizing system while the powerful remain detached and comfortable.","opus":"A scathing attack on how creative people are left to suffer and perish for scraps while institutions merely observe, until agony and expression become indistinguishable. The Pittsburgh hardcore band pits their own defiant integrity against a spineless herd that props up a hollow system simply to feel occupied.","sonnet":"A contemptuous address to someone trapped in mediocrity and exploitation — grinding away in a gray machine while the speaker thrives in darkness, painting the other's suffering as a choice they've abdicated rather than a fate imposed.","src":"haiku"},
"code-orange~grooming-my-replacement":{"haiku":"Manipulation begets manipulation as the narrator grooms successors in corruption, perpetuating cycles of toxicity and weaponized rhetoric.","opus":"A predatory psychodrama about shaping someone into your own image and inheriting your hatred, colonizing their mind so your failures live on through them. It reads as toxic mentorship or abuse, the abuser already lodged inside the victim's head.","sonnet":"A predatory mentor figure feeds on a protégé's hatred and wears them like a suit — filling them with failures, rotting their core — until the student unknowingly becomes the new vehicle for the elder's worst instincts, completing the cycle the mentor designed.","src":"haiku"},
"code-orange~mirror":{"haiku":"Reflection distorts identity until the speaker no longer knows which side of the glass they occupy.","opus":"A hardcore interrogation of hollow ambition and the performance of self, where the finger assigning blame is the very one aimed back at him. The narrator saw the whole game and found nothing in it he actually wanted.","sonnet":"Self-examination in a factory of endless doing where the illusion and the sensation can't be told apart, and the mirror shows not a person but a final sin. Everyone spreads blame while covered in shame, the pointing finger always turning back, and the question — are you happy now, will you ever be — gets no answer because the reflection offers only more reflection.","src":"haiku"},
"code-orange~snapshot":{"haiku":"Predatory hierarchy and caged existence crystallize as the speaker builds systems of dominance and binds captives through shame and control, all while hunger and betrayal deepen.","opus":"A predator's monologue framing control and abuse as building a home that's really a cage, where the trapped victim slowly becomes the one on display. Code Orange make the machinery of power viscerally claustrophobic.","sonnet":"A speaker who has built a cage and calls it a kingdom demands everyone play their position — the snapshot metaphor opens on a hunting term and closes on someone humiliated under a red light, the violence of the power dynamic dressed in the cool language of inevitability.","src":"haiku"},
"code-orange~so-below":{"haiku":"Surface aspiration masks hollow imitation, where desire and pheromone-driven impulses govern without authentic distinction.","opus":"Built on the occult maxim as above, so below, this circles the emptiness of chasing a false golden ideal, imitation of imitation, and the refusal to know an ugly truth. A trance of denial and pheromone-driven pursuit that keeps moving nowhere.","sonnet":"A hypnotic, compressed vision of desire and imitation: deep on the most-wanted road everyone finds a golden son who is merely one copy among many, and the only honest response is not wanting to know — the pheromones of ambition carry everyone along regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"code-orange~sulfur-surrounding":{"opus":"A poisonous, addictive bond rendered as a corrosive chemical craving, the singer luring someone in effortlessly and pulling them down into misery. He longs to see the world with something like divine clarity but finds only hatred taking him over.","sonnet":"A song about the pull of a toxic, addictive relationship or substance: it surrounds you like sulfur, drags you through hell, and glues you to it with frightening ease — the narrator wants to see through god's eyes but finds only hate brewing inside.","src":"sonnet"},
"code-orange~take-shape-feat-billy-corgan":{"haiku":"Illusions of constructed reality dissolve, revealing designed chaos and manufactured identity underneath.","opus":"Losing hold of reality as the world reveals itself as stage set, severed heads and rotting corpses where meaning should be. Corgan's guest turn sharpens the vertigo of a mind confronting a designed, purposeless world.","sonnet":"Reality has gone rotten — a severed head replaces consciousness, a rotting corpse blocks the road, cardboard scenery has no meaning. The narrator positions himself as the antagonist the crowd loves to hate, then commands the audience to \"spread your wings, take shape\": an invitation for the listener to become their own monster in a world already without why.","src":"haiku"},
"code-orange~underneath":{"haiku":"Humans are test subjects engineered for improvement; the speaker struggles to break free from self-imposed entrapment and win against forces that made them this way.","opus":"A paranoid probe into a generation raised as test subjects, insisting you don't really know yourself until pressure forces it out. Pittsburgh hardcore band Code Orange, who laced abrasive metal with industrial glitch, target the emptiness beneath online image and status, framing self-knowledge as something only intrusion and duress can finally reveal.","sonnet":"Treated as a test subject by the systems that shaped them, a narrator eventually internalises that the only way through the obstacle is straight through it. Beneath every polished surface — aesthetic, online presence, social performance — a harder, truer pressure has been building and now demands to be faced.","src":"haiku"},
"cody-chesnutt~parting-ways":{"opus":"A tender goodbye that treats separation as temporary. The soul singer frames romantic love as the bedrock that recycles all living, promising the two will eventually merge into a single pulse again, and sending the departing lover off with blessings of nourishment and divine affection.","sonnet":"A soulful, spiritual farewell that reframes separation as a temporary arc — the narrator insists that love is the foundation of existence and that those parting will meet again, blessing the departing person with good things and returning everything to the cycle of life.","src":"sonnet"},
"cody-frost~process":{"haiku":"Continuous striving erases sensation and identity; the speaker runs wheels endlessly while forgetting how to feel.","opus":"Burnout under the relentless demand to always be the best, someone sprinting on a treadmill from their earliest steps until numbness sets in. The counsel to pull back and view the blaze from a distance offers the only exit from a ride there's otherwise no leaving.","sonnet":"A song about burning out from relentless pressure and losing the ability to feel anything — the narrator has been running on a treadmill since childhood, is so exhausted and overstimulated that nothing sinks in anymore, and has forgotten what genuine emotion feels like in the grind of expectations.","src":"haiku"},
"cody-frost~sometimesican-tfeelathing":{"haiku":"Chemical numbness becomes preferable to emotional vulnerability, allowing the narrator to function without experiencing pain or connection.","opus":"Chemical self-management as coping: numbing hyperactivity and pain with substances to reach a frictionless calm, groove and pain-free by way of the right key. Underneath the upbeat mantra is dissociation, emotion pushed permanently out of reach.","sonnet":"An affirmative coping anthem: emotional numbness is met not with despair but with chemical management — edibles and chosen detachment — freeing the speaker to groove through life pain-free with a self-won key, even while admitting the numbness is real.","src":"haiku"},
"cody-frost~wot":{"haiku":"Escape into substance numbs the weight of adulthood while peers chirp meaninglessly; the speaker sinks slowly into silence and self-destruction.","opus":"A portrait of a young man numbing himself into wasted oblivion, sliding from one drug to a harder one while everyone around him does the same, fixated on death in the corner. The refrain keeps asking what he's really fleeing, whether it's simply being worn out by living under someone's thumb.","sonnet":"A portrait of a man running from authority and responsibility through drugs and alcohol — from 'sharp white snow to the golden brown' — who sits in a corner listening to New Order and contemplating 'the eternal slumber,' escaping the thumb of expectation even as the tune stays wrong and everyone sings along.","src":"haiku"},
"cody-matthew-johnson~subhuman":{"haiku":"A fighter resists internal and external darkness, claiming an inhuman power to survive apocalyptic torment. They assert they cannot be killed, only transformed into something beyond human.","opus":"A defiant refusal to be destroyed, squaring off against an approaching apocalypse and the demons crowding the narrator's mind while he declares himself unkillable. Cody Matthew Johnson, best known for anime and game vocals, delivers this metalcore anthem with theatrical bite, the pull between restraint and eruption fueling a fantasy where letting the rage out is the only way to keep the darkness at bay.","sonnet":"Confronting approaching apocalypse while fighting to keep demonic inner forces from erupting, the narrator claims an identity beyond destruction — something post-human, omega-level, unkillable. The tension between total loss of control and the absolute need to contain that explosion drives every line.","src":"haiku"},
"coeur-noir~let-the-stars-die":{"haiku":"A city steals everything. Light becomes blindness and space rejects the lost. Stars should die rather than witness this emptiness.","opus":"A nocturnal despair where the city has stripped everything away and even space turns the singer out, head lost in the clouds and feet stuck on the ground, letting the sky go dark as the body cools.","sonnet":"A song about feeling unwanted by the universe itself — the city has taken everything from the narrator, they find only death and suffering when seeking light, feel too much light to see clearly and yet no place to stand, as if space itself doesn't want them there, and their body grows cold.","src":"haiku"},
"cog~altered-states":{"haiku":"Conspiracy awareness about hidden alien presence and secret government coverups, cycling between fear and the certainty that we are not alone.","opus":"The Australian prog trio spin a paranoid meditation on UFOs and government cover-ups, splicing apocalyptic mythology, ancient deities, and doomsday prophecy into their heavy, groove-driven churn. Craft withheld from public view fuels the dread, the narrator half-convinced the visitors are signalling him and insisting humanity has company it isn't told about.","sonnet":"Unidentified aerial phenomena are read through a conspiratorial lens — suppressed, hidden from broadcast, evidence of official deception about extraterrestrial contact. The narrator orbits between fascination and fury at the concealment, insisting humanity is not alone and that the secrets being kept while everyone sleeps are real and consequential.","src":"haiku"},
"cog~open-up":{"haiku":"Hollywood and celebrity culture exploit spectacle and deception, reducing authenticity to performance; the speaker seeks destruction of this false paradise. Ambition corrupts and commodifies.","opus":"Australian prog-rock trio Cog aim a scorching rant at Hollywood and the emptiness of celebrity, portraying a fraud who lies and rewrites the rules while staging a spectacle that's likely just publicity. The narrator gets seduced by the glamour, chasing wealth and self-glorification, before the song erupts into chants demanding that the whole industry be torched to the ground.","sonnet":"Hollywood is indicted as a machine of manufactured spectacle — lies, circus theatrics, and inflated egos dressed as art. The speaker positions himself against the system, demanding either room to exist authentically or the whole structure burn to the ground, with Tinseltown as the target of his incendiary contempt.","src":"haiku"},
"coheed-and-cambria~comatose":{"haiku":"Fear of intimacy and vulnerability leads to self-isolation and spiritual death, preferring unconsciousness to the risk of being known.","opus":"A plea to stay numb rather than feel, from someone who has sealed himself away and can't let a lover past the walls without coming apart. The song lives in the push-pull of craving closeness and dreading it, choosing anesthesia over the risk of being truly known.","sonnet":"A speaker too afraid to let someone in builds a one-man prison, spiralling in place and begging to be kept numb rather than risk the vulnerability of letting go — \"start to finish but just sit and spin alone\" is the honest account of what emotional shutdown looks like from the inside.","src":"haiku"},
"coheed-and-cambria~the-dark-sentencer":{"haiku":"An entity materialises to punish hypocrisy and spiritual corruption, dragging the listener into descent where false promises and self-deception receive their reckoning.","opus":"Part of the band's sprawling sci-fi saga, a servant's monologue steeped in loyalty to a corrupt master and the horror of being born to play an assigned part. Beneath the myth it's about complicity and the impossibility of escaping your role.","sonnet":"A figure addressed as Vaxus is welcomed into a place of reckoning where corruption, selfishness, and moral cowardice are confronted — the speaker moves between cold condemnation of those who exploit others and a desperate plea not to be abandoned as the darkness closes in.","src":"haiku"},
"coheed-and-cambria~the-liars-club":{"haiku":"Dishonesty feels better than truth; the speaker proposes mutual escape into comfortable lies rather than confront the painful reality that would destroy their connection.","opus":"A couple agrees to keep lying to each other because the comforting fiction feels better than facing how broken everything is. It is about choosing shared denial as an anesthetic for a life beyond repair.","sonnet":"Two people mutually exhausted by the truth strike a bargain to lie to each other instead: the world is too broken for honesty, life feels better when they avoid it, and the speaker explicitly begs to be asked to lie — \"are you okay to lie with me?\" — as a form of shelter.","src":"haiku"},
"cold-chisel~shipping-steel-2011-remastered":{"haiku":"Solitary labor sustains material movement while emotional isolation deepens; the road offers escape but never arrival or belonging.","opus":"A long-haul trucker's life as chosen solitude, the road standing in for the family and faith other men need. Chisel find the loneliness and quiet dignity in a working life spent suspended between heaven and highway.","sonnet":"A long-haul truck driver's life on the road — steel loads, diesel engines, country radio, and the loneliness of a man who needs only his rig for company — is presented without romanticization: he's caught between heaven and the highway, sliding, and nobody knows what that actually feels like.","src":"haiku"},
"cold-in-may~halo-of-the-gone":{"haiku":"Death's spectral image haunts the living while weakness persists, offering bitter transcendence through violence and finality.","opus":"A grim rumination on suicide and violent death, hunting for a reason not to give up while picturing the halo of those already lost. It hovers between glorifying the release and pleading to honor your own scars and keep running.","sonnet":"A meditation on those who have died — particularly by violence or suicide — framed as witnessing a halo around the gone; the speaker stands behind their scars, sees life as weak and done, yet positions survival as winning a race to taste the sun.","src":"haiku"},
"cold-kingdom~agony":{"haiku":"Isolation peaks when everyone becomes enemy; entertainment demands a scapegoat, and belief itself fragments into insignificance.","opus":"The exhaustion of being surrounded by people convinced they're right, drowning out any private thought. Agony here is spectacle, everyone's misery staged for entertainment until it turns and levels the performer.","sonnet":"The speaker is overwhelmed by a world where everyone feels like an enemy and the noise of others' opinions never stops; the agony is performed 'for your entertainment' — a spectacle of alienation that ends in explosion, indifference met with the prediction it will blow them away.","src":"haiku"},
"cold-night-for-alligators~nostalgic":{"opus":"Confronting someone whose pride, savagery, and heavy-handed control wound those around them; the narrator refuses to stay stuck in that toxic nostalgia, insisting on change while naming indifference as a machine that feeds on grief.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who clings to the past and uses indifference and emotional cruelty to maintain control — the narrator pushes back against pride and old wounds, arguing that change is already underway and nostalgia for familiar hatred is just another cycle of grief.","src":"sonnet"},
"cold-war-kids~first":{"haiku":"Cumulative betrayal and hurt mature into release; the speaker recognizes cyclical patterns and chooses to light both candle-ends.","opus":"The cycles people get trapped in, hurt then sorry then worried, and the survivor's guilt of being the one who got out when someone else was there first. Burning the candle at both ends until it consumes you.","sonnet":"A person processes being the second choice — the one who came after, who waited, who burned with desire while the other person had already moved on — cycling through hurt, guilt, and the dizzying momentum of a candle burned from both ends.","src":"haiku"},
"coldcasket~save-your-breath":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects platitudes and sympathy, demanding others feel the harm they've caused while nursing hidden shame and emotional numbness.","opus":"A refusal of hollow comfort from the person who caused the damage — pity and prayers rejected, numbness worn as evidence, the wounded party turning the hurt back on its source and demanding they sit with what they did. Betrayed trust curdles into a warpath. Cold Casket work the downtempo-deathcore lane, and this is its signature move: grief processed as blunt-force confrontation rather than mourning.","sonnet":"A furious refusal of pity and hollow comfort — the speaker has been damaged by someone and wants no prayers, no reassurance, only the satisfaction of the other person understanding what they caused. The internal numbness and the warpath image sit side by side: feeling nothing and burning toward something at the same time.","src":"haiku"},
"coldplay~another-s-arms":{"haiku":"Loneliness and desire for physical comfort drive the narrator toward someone new; they crave presence and touch after prolonged abandonment.","opus":"The specific loneliness after a breakup, reaching for anyone's touch to blunt the absence of the one you want, and knowing the substitute is its own torture. It's grief for a body no longer beside you.","sonnet":"Late at night, alone without the person who used to be beside them, the speaker aches for physical closeness and comfort — the absence of those arms is its own kind of torture, and even imagining them belonging to someone else is unbearable.","src":"haiku"},
"coldplay~champion-of-the-world":{"haiku":"Aspiration persists despite repeated failure while the speaker redefines victory through stubborn persistence in the face of inevitable defeat.","opus":"Built from a late friend's unfinished song, it turns childhood inadequacy into stubborn triumph, the kid who failed every contest crowning himself anyway. E.T.'s flying bicycle stands for believing in flight when everything says you'll fall.","sonnet":"A persistent underdog who has failed at everything — school, flight, romance, combat — refuses to concede, choosing to keep riding, keep flying, keep dreaming of the moment someone pins a champion's colours to their chest. The heroism is in the refusal to stop, not in any actual victory.","src":"haiku"},
"coldplay~high-speed":{"haiku":"Existence accelerates beyond control; the narrator asks for help stopping the frenetic pace while questioning whether they're truly alive at such velocity.","opus":"An early, anxious Coldplay track about being carried along by momentum you can't control, life sealed inside a bubble. The plea for someone to steer is really a question of whether trust in another can slow the rush.","sonnet":"A speaker adrift in the speed and pressure of modern life — or a relationship — asks whether anyone has the controls, whether confidence in the other person or the system amounts to anything, and finds no answer except the dizzying rush continuing.","src":"haiku"},
"coldplay~the-scientist":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and regret collapse into circular pursuit of impossible origins, where running endlessly in cages yields only the certainty that love hurts beyond measure. No scientific method restores what breaking severs.","opus":"A remorseful lover wishing he could rewind to the start of a relationship, casting logic and science as helpless against a heart that just wants to go back and begin again.","opusDeep":"An apology that keeps reaching for the vocabulary of the lab and finding it insufficient. The speaker arrives contrite — 'Come up to meet you, tell you I'm sorry' — but the relationship is diagrammed as an experiment gone wrong: 'guessing at numbers and figures / Pulling the puzzles apart,' love treated as a problem to be solved rather than felt. The thesis is stated outright in the cleanest line: 'Questions of science, science and progress / Do not speak as loud as my heart' — analysis confessing its own defeat before emotion. The obsessive 'Oh, take me back to the start' is the real engine, a wish to rerun the experiment with different variables, while 'Running in circles, coming up tails' admits the outcomes stay stubbornly unlucky. 'Come back and haunt me' lets love shade into loss. What it's actually about: a man who tried to reason his way through a relationship, realizing too late the heart can't be reverse-engineered.","sonnet":"A speaker runs back to the start of a love, confessing failure and begging for a second chance — questions of science and progress fall silent before the heart, and the only honest answer is that nobody said any of this would be easy or this hard.","src":"haiku"},
"coldplay~yellow":{"haiku":"Stars and natural wonders exist as permanent witnesses to an all-consuming romantic attachment expressed through absolute sacrifice.","opus":"Devotion stripped to its most uncalculated form, the beloved lit up like the stars and worth bleeding yourself dry for. Its enduring pull is how plainly it stakes everything on another person without irony or condition.","sonnet":"A speaker dedicates total, self-depleting devotion to someone — writing songs, swimming oceans, drawing lines, bleeding himself dry — framing the universe's light as merely reflecting that person's existence. Love as cosmic self-sacrifice, narrated with hushed awe.","src":"haiku"},
"coldrain~chasing-shadows":{"haiku":"Resistance blooms from suppression: questioning the prescribed path ignites fire that darkness and rain cannot extinguish, even as the cost grows unbearable.","opus":"A refusal to obey the order to keep quiet and color within the boundaries, aimed at rulers who tried to outlaw and torch a shared dream. The song hangs its hope on one small ember of rebellion that authority can never fully snuff out.","sonnet":"A song about persisting in the search for truth against authorities who suppress it by force — those in power burned knowledge and enforced conformity, but the fire of those who seek more cannot be extinguished.","src":"haiku"},
"coldrain~free-fall":{"haiku":"Chaos and vulnerability become preferable to manufactured safety, celebrating pain as proof of authentic existence and human capacity.","opus":"The Japanese metal band frames falling as liberation, choosing raw pain and real connection over a copy-pasted, numbed existence. The stance is that feeling anything, even hurt and chaos, is what keeps us human against a sanitized world.","sonnet":"A collective refusal of digital numbness and copy-pasted connection: lonely, bruised, healing slow but burning bright, the speakers would rather free-fall in real pain than accept a wired facsimile of feeling — chaos is what makes them human.","src":"haiku"},
"coldrain~hollow":{"haiku":"Leaders betray with false promises and procrastination while the world drowns in hollow indifference; the speaker demands action, not apathy, calling out those responsible for abandoning masses to despair.","opus":"A protest aimed at a failed authority figure who broke his pledges, staged false grief, and squandered time while ordinary people's survival hangs in the balance. The Japanese band refuses silence, insisting the leader confront the public's outrage and dare to hope for a better tomorrow instead of marching blindly forward.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a failed leader — someone who promised more than they could give and now sits idle while real lives suffer — the band refuses silence and demands the hollow figurehead either act or step aside.","src":"haiku"},
"coldrain~mayday-feat-ryo-from-crystal-lake":{"haiku":"Someone destroys every bridge built for them, lies habitually, and leaves only ruins. The bridges they burn offer no escape; they are a monster and walking disaster.","opus":"A furious severing addresses a serial betrayer who plays victim, lies past counting, and torches every bridge until nothing's left but a screamed distress call. Coldrain, a Japanese metalcore band who sing largely in English for international reach, sharpen the confrontation with a guest scream from Crystal Lake's Ryo, the wish-you'd-heard-us-out refrain turning wounded trust into a scorched-earth verdict on someone who breaks everything they touch.","sonnet":"A relationship or alliance destroyed by one person's chronic deception and self-serving manipulation finally reaches its breaking point, with the wronged party calling out a pattern of bridge-burning that has left everyone stranded. The distress signal is a last-word indictment, not a plea for rescue.","src":"haiku"},
"coldrain~new-dawn":{"haiku":"Hope and renewal after darkness, sunrise after endless night.","opus":"An indictment of humanity playing god, tearing down and rebuilding just to prove it can while asking to be saved. The Japanese metalcore band frame our self-mythologizing as the very thing dooming us.","sonnet":"A species-wide self-indictment — humanity tears down and builds up just to say it can, plays God endlessly, breaks smiles, pursues mindless destruction, and asks whether it has gone too far gone to ever see a new dawn. The final prayer (\"give us strength, give us hope\") follows the admission that we only ever think about ourselves.","src":"haiku"},
"coldrain~paradise-kill-the-silence":{"haiku":"Emerging from isolation and terror, the collective reclaims vitality through defiant noise and shared rebellion against silence.","opus":"A pandemic-era return to life and the stage, isolation lifting as the band swears to get back to blundering and playing loud. Killing the silence means refusing the fear that the emptiness might harden into how things simply are now.","sonnet":"A return to live music and shared presence after long isolation: dreams that gathered dust and hearts that rusted are rebuilt spark by spark, and the crowd reclaims paradise — dumb mistakes, broken bones, and all — by killing the silence that stood in for life.","src":"haiku"},
"coldrain~revolution":{"haiku":"Rejection of control and manipulation masked as love; the speaker's patience ends and demands closure, screaming for liberation from imposed designs.","opus":"A furious break from a controlling, suffocating relationship, the narrator screaming for a revolution against being defined and manipulated, done with reasoning and demanding closure, the poison eating through his soul as he struggles to let go. Coldrain are a Japanese metalcore band who sing largely in English, and this pairs their melodic-heavy attack with the personal rebellion and catharsis typical of the genre.","sonnet":"A relationship experienced as total erasure — what gets labeled love is in fact a slow dismantling of identity, and the speaker has finally run out of patience. The revolution they scream for is less political than personal: a need to break free of someone who controls, destroys, and poisons without ever being held accountable.","src":"haiku"},
"coldrain~uninvited":{"haiku":"An assertion of emotional boundaries: the speaker refuses access to their inner world and corrects the listener's presumption of intimacy, claiming their own uniqueness cannot be claimed or understood by outsiders.","opus":"Japanese rock band Coldrain cover Alanis Morissette's uneasy ballad, keeping its guarded refusal intact. Flattered but disturbed by someone's obsession, the narrator admits a hunger to be wanted yet firmly turns the pursuer away, unnerved at being studied and needing time to think. Attraction and self-defense stay locked in tense stalemate.","sonnet":"Someone finds their admirer flattering but fundamentally disqualified — this person has not earned access to a territory as particular and guarded as genuine love. The speaker is not unkind but is firm: fascination and craving are not the same as understanding, and this one simply was not invited in.","src":"haiku"},
"coldstate~bleed-out":{"haiku":"Despair perpetuates itself through cycles of suffering and self-punishment, with hope offering temporary respite before drowning returns.","opus":"The addictive comfort of one's own misery, a narrator spiraling downward yet unable to loosen his grip on suffering. The turn is a genuine question of what it would cost to think differently, to trust and have faith instead of bleeding out.","sonnet":"Trapped in a cycle of pessimistic thinking that feels perversely comforting, the speaker spirals downward while questioning what it would cost — in trust and faith — to finally let go of misery and make amends with melancholy.","src":"haiku"},
"coldstate~web-of-lies":{"haiku":"Mental fragmentation and inherited trauma threaten dissolution; the speaker affirms agency and refusal to surrender to inherited patterns or deceptive systems.","opus":"A refusal to follow inherited despair: aware that suicide runs in the bloodline, the narrator chooses to hold onto himself, knowing the grief only multiplies for those left behind. Survival as an act of will.","sonnet":"Survival defiance in the face of inherited suicidal depression — a family loss to suicide shifted the narrator's perspective: the easy way out \"trickles tenfold to the ones left behind,\" so the choice is to stay, even at the end of the road, even caught in the web. The declaration \"I would never let go of myself\" is hard-won, not naive.","src":"haiku"},
"cold~remedy":{"haiku":"Incompatibility between lovers means the speaker cannot provide what the other needs; they persist in draining cycles rather than heal, rejecting external solutions while remaining broken. Love becomes self-sabotage.","opus":"The Florida alt-metal band Cold traces a mismatched, failing romance through a list of ways the two partners simply do not align, the narrator blaming himself for being hollowed out and refusing the cure on offer. Behind the video-game and shared-dream imagery is stubborn loyalty to a relationship where two people suffer on incompatible frequencies, choosing endurance over being fixed.","sonnet":"Two people grieve and process experience in fundamentally incompatible ways, and the narrator refuses the comfort the other offers. He will sustain his own dream and catch himself when he falls — but not through someone else's remedy, which sits wrong against the particular shape of his pain.","src":"haiku"},
"coleman-hawkins~body-and-soul":{"haiku":"Longing crystallizes the beloved as entire universe, where romantic rejection becomes existential erasure. Surrender of self becomes the only logic of love available.","opus":"The 1939 recording that rewrote jazz saxophone, its lyric a total romantic surrender to someone who may never love back. Hawkins barely touches the melody, but the ache of complete devotion is the whole point.","sonnet":"A plea of total devotion to someone who has withdrawn — the speaker spends days longing, surrendering body and soul, facing a stormy and grey future unless given one more chance to prove it; 'my life a hell you're making' and the confession that without this person they are no earthly good make it one of the most exposed declarations in the standard repertoire.","src":"haiku"},
"coletta~raspberry-cough":{"haiku":"Someone fears abandonment and losing their identity while watching old friends fade; they cling to present moments against change.","opus":"This progressive track wrestles with a dissolving sense of self and friendships slipping away, the narrator trying to treat his emotional flatness through creative release while doubting his own worth. He struggles to shed an outdated identity for a new one, prizing the present yet unable to stop circling his own doubts.","sonnet":"A restless mind oscillates between manic productivity and the grief of lost friendships, asking whether transformation requires abandoning every earlier version of oneself. The song holds both the thrill of self-reinvention and the loneliness of becoming someone the past cannot recognize.","src":"haiku"},
"coletta~tattoo":{"haiku":"Predatory seduction wrapped in gothic imagery celebrates domination and possession disguised as passionate devotion.","opus":"A playful vampiric seduction that leans into its own puns, casting hunger and desire as bloodletting and appetite. Beneath the camp is a straightforward invitation, one night of indulgence framed as feeding an endless craving.","sonnet":"A vampiric seducer invites a lover to stay for the night, offering bite marks and blood in place of conventional romance, casting themselves as both predator and sustenance — the bedroom encounter framed in musical metaphors of legato and downbeat.","src":"haiku"},
"colourblind~entice-me":{"haiku":"A person questions their reality and existence, losing touch with sanity while drawn toward danger and self-harm.","opus":"The band chart a mind slipping loose from reality, its narrator helplessly drawn toward a danger he begs not to be destroyed by as he struggles upright. Tormented by whether he is already dead or merely dreaming, he fights intrusive thoughts and crumbling sanity, a seductive warmth threatening to pull him past the self he no longer knows.","sonnet":"Dissociation and fractured reality disorient someone who is no longer sure whether they are alive or dreaming their existence. The mind races at unsustainable speed while the narrator reaches toward warmth and kindness, desperate for something real to hold.","src":"haiku"},
"colourblind~longsleeves":{"haiku":"A person performs identity through modifications and outward symbols, aware they've become corrupted versions of themselves while others exploit this decay.","opus":"Long sleeves hiding self-harm scars, and the deeper wound of becoming a worse version of yourself while performing coolness for onlookers. The narrator names romanticizing death as no way to live, even as he keeps imitating it.","sonnet":"Someone wearing long sleeves to hide self-harm marks tries and fails to pass as cool and confident, becoming instead a worse version of themselves by imitating others, and closes by naming the trap clearly: romanticising the desire to die is no way to live.","src":"haiku"},
"colourblind~natalie":{"haiku":"Regret over lost time and irreversible distance, feeling undeserving of existence compared to the dead.","opus":"A tender elegy for someone gone too soon, small shared memories like a run-out videotape standing in for the loss. The survivor's guilt lands hardest admitting he'd trade places and no longer wants to be here.","sonnet":"A grief-stricken speaker addresses someone who died before their time — remembering small intimacies like a haircut, evenings on a couch, a tape that has run out — and admits they feel they have no right to still be alive in place of her.","src":"haiku"},
"colourblind~water":{"haiku":"Water offers escape to an alternate reality where safety exists; the speaker struggles with mortality and numbness while seeking aliveness.","opus":"A dissociative escape follows instructions to still the mind and flow like water into another world, warned not to stay too long lest returning home lose its pull. Colourblind, a UK melodic hardcore band trading in raw mental-health confession, tip the calm into anguish as the refrain fractures into a plea to be shown how to be alive, the safe bright yellow of the vision shadowed by a plain, unbearable wish to die.","sonnet":"Someone follows a half-dissociative instruction — find water, go where it flows — and discovers in that surrender a momentary escape from a mind that otherwise feels trapped and suffocating. The final lines admit the real desperation: a wish for release that stops short of death but does not quite know how to live.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~buried-alive":{"haiku":"Entrapment in psychological and emotional darkness, digging deeper into despair while clawing desperately for light and freedom.","opus":"Being trapped alive in your own despair, digging graves while clinging to faith, then twisting into a threat aimed back at a tormentor. The industrial menace masks a real claustrophobia: darkness that keeps the light concealed.","sonnet":"A person trapped in psychological and spiritual darkness — faith eroding, memories fading, chained like a slave — reaches a breaking point where a threatening external force offers not rescue but damnation, condemning the speaker to die buried alive as their final state of existence.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~compliance":{"haiku":"Automation erodes humanity as the speaker witnesses the transformation from individual to machine; compliance becomes the death of soul and the triumph of control.","opus":"A bleak vision of a population driven forward like livestock, part flesh and part machinery, queuing up for their allotment of resentment and raising whatever placard they are handed. Obedience gets marketed as staying alive even as it drains away anything human, their hopes cashed out in blood on their own palms.","sonnet":"A critique of automated obedience: humanity has forgotten how to live, is pushed like sheep through a gate toward death, fed rations of hate, and asked to hold up signs declaring allegiance. The speaker addresses this passivity with black irony — \"we got a war for you, so dry your tears with pain\" — while the closing \"compliance got you this far\" indicts the willingness to die for whatever one is told to serve.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~from-my-cold-dead-hands":{"opus":"A militant stand for personal liberty against a dystopian machine of control, where a collapsing old order and a new regime of subjugation try to strip away autonomy. The singer swears the only way anyone takes his freedom is by prying it from a lifeless grip.","sonnet":"A defiant assertion of personal freedom against systems of control and enforced conformity — the narrator will only give up their autonomy over their dead body, framing the fight as an ideological war against a new world order that would enslave the mind.","src":"sonnet"},
"combichrist~get-your-body-beat":{"haiku":"Sexualizes pain and physical domination with indifference to communication or consent. Focuses on feeling over understanding.","opus":"An aggressive, transgressive club chant built on a brutal, repeated command to bleed and let blood flow, blurring pain, sex and dancefloor violence into pure provocation. Meaning is secondary to menace and rhythm. Combichrist are Andy LaPlegua's aggrotech project, a cornerstone of the harsh EBM and industrial-dance scene, and this early single became a genre staple built for dark, pounding clubs.","sonnet":"A relentless, almost ritual incitement to physical release through punishment — the sparse lyrics strip away narrative entirely, reducing everything to a command repeated until it becomes its own blunt argument. The aggression is the content; the body is the only subject.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~gotta-go":{"haiku":"A person ends a parasitic relationship, demanding a toxic person leave and never return. The song is a severance: ending an era of being drained and disrespected.","opus":"A ticking-clock kiss-off to a big-mouthed hanger-on, the narrator calling time and ordering them out for good, done wasting patience on someone who watched with greed and won't be let back for more. The Tic-Tac refrain marks the countdown. Combichrist, Andy LaPlegua's aggrotech outfit, drive this eviction with the pounding, harsh electronic-body-music assault that defines the project.","sonnet":"A ticking-clock eviction notice — someone has overstayed, taken too much, watched too greedily, and the speaker has finally decided the time is up for good. The Tic-Tac urgency frames the whole thing as a countdown that has already ended; this isn't a warning but a door slamming.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~guns-at-last-dawn":{"haiku":"Systematic oppression breeds refusal to submit, with the narrator embracing violence as the only language of resistance available.","opus":"An aggrotech war chant sung by half-mechanical soldiers penned in like animals who vow not to flee. Midway it turns raw and personal, as the loss of someone the narrator failed to rescue sours into an outright wish that they were dead.","sonnet":"A refusal to retreat: caged, mechanical, bleeding — the speaker and their group hold the line with shotguns ready, watching a kingdom fall while insisting they want someone to die and will not run even when all is lost.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~hate-like-me":{"opus":"Combichrist round on a onetime comrade who has gone slack, once a blazing presence in their shared fury and now slumped on the couch with a drink. The chorus grieves the burned-out bond, a gang that used to stand shoulder to shoulder now squared off as strangers, a lament for betrayal and dead-out rage.","sonnet":"About losing a once-unified community of outsiders to complacency — the narrator confronts someone who used to share their defiant anger but has since settled into a passive domestic life, mourning the loss of shared identity and communal rage.","src":"sonnet"},
"combichrist~how-old-is-your-soul":{"haiku":"Someone questions broken promises and the betrayal they feel; no one cares as those who were supposed to follow through abandon them to moral emptiness.","opus":"A bleak reckoning with a spiritually exhausted, disillusioned soul at the gates of hell, past forgiveness; addressed to someone who lies, follows their damaged soul and collapses insisting nobody cares, it captures resignation and the erosion of hope.","sonnet":"About a relationship's total breakdown — someone has told the narrator that nobody cares, that following through is pointless, and that forgiveness is gone; the narrator is left littered with lies and the wreckage of a person who claimed to care.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~maggots-at-the-party":{"haiku":"A hedonistic celebration of excess and defiance where the speaker urges an audience to embrace chaos, drinking, and death-marked partying as a rejection of restraint and societal expectation.","opus":"Andy LaPlegua's industrial project throws a nihilist bash built on the idea that death arrives tomorrow so tonight is for reckless excess. Wasted and refusing to drink solo, the song raises a glass to embracing the madness, spinning mortality into a reason for one more ruinous night on the floor.","sonnet":"A death-disco bacchanal celebrates living fully and recklessly tonight because tomorrow guarantees nothing. The maggots of the title are claimed with pride as those who refuse sobriety and convention, insisting that freedom means throwing yourself into the noise until you physically cannot continue.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~modern-demon":{"haiku":"Rot and fire consume while pride dies slow. Breaking manifests through demons modern and eternal.","opus":"A decaying system and a doomed bond, where two people cling to each other as the world rots; the modern demon is both the beloved and the corrupting force they share, burning together as they break.","sonnet":"About a relationship that has rotted both people from the inside — the system they built together is decayed, pride is gone, and what remains is something precious but monstrous, a 'modern demon' who is both perfect and destructive, forcing the narrator to break.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~my-life-my-rules":{"haiku":"Someone asserts absolute autonomy over their life and choices, rejecting judgment and external control, willing to embrace destruction rather than submit to others' rules.","opus":"A nihilistic, defiant refusal to be changed or controlled, the speaker clinging to hate and soul while contemplating suicide (six rounds, one saved for his own head); the throughline is burning out on his own terms in a doomed city of dust, insisting his life obeys only his rules.","sonnet":"About asserting total autonomy against someone who has disappointed or controlled the narrator — fed up with waiting, tired of being rated and overshadowed, he claims his life and rules as non-negotiable even as he acknowledges a nihilistic willingness to throw it all away.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~never-surrender":{"haiku":"Mental anguish and refusal to surrender despite suffering and despair.","opus":"Self-medicating through guilt and despair, refusing to surrender despite suffering, and rising from one's own ashes.","sonnet":"Enduring shame, self-punishment, and mental anguish while refusing to surrender or be broken by the pain.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title sells defiance, but LaPlegua wires it straight into self-harm: the man who swears he will not give in is the same one dosing himself, hungry for punishment, and taking the blame for a planet in flames. That turns the industrial bravado inward, into a siege fought entirely inside one head. The phoenix imagery keeps trying to lift the song toward triumph, yet each verse pulls it back down — a tumor of the mind, a life he built then refused to see, small joys abandoned to gather grime. What is left is a vow that means not victory over any foe but simple refusal to stop existing under a shame that never eases.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"defiance wired inward until the vow means refusing to stop existing","built":"phoenix imagery keeps lifting before each verse drags it back"},
"combichrist~never-surrender-hq":{"haiku":"Self-punishment and defiant transcendence; rising from ashes through suffering and refusal to yield.","opus":"An aggrotech vow to endure through self-loathing and self-medication without breaking, letting hate and shame burn without capitulating. The refusal to surrender is aimed inward, at the mental cancer denying its own reality.","sonnet":"Self-medication and self-blame for a world on fire, meeting the suffering head-on — I will suffer, I will burn, let hate prevail, enslave my soul, but I will never surrender. Mental cancer hijacks the brain and denies the reality you created, yet rising from the ashes is still possible, shame and pity discarded as the final act of refusal.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~planet-doom":{"haiku":"Intolerance and indifference breed societal collapse while the powerful ignore warning signs, condemning humanity to inevitable ruin.","opus":"An apocalyptic tirade against dogma and intolerance, burning down a world poisoned by a book of lies and hands soaked in blood. The narrator claims the role of the evil everyone fears, staging his own reckoning as the last shot before nobody gets out alive.","sonnet":"A divided, dogma-choked world burning itself down is met with the speaker claiming the fire as their battleground — embracing the label of evil, calling out intolerance as disease and words as poison, inviting the world to watch everything crash.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~rise":{"haiku":"Systemic exploitation chains the populace through manufactured hope and spiritual erosion. The speaker rallies resistance against dehumanization and calls for collective liberation.","opus":"A call to revolt against silent rulers who whisper freedom while shackling hands and gilding the noose. Against a system that works people to death and sells hope over their own graves, it demands they rise from the wreckage and break their chains.","sonnet":"A call to revolt against systems that forge burdens, sell false hope, and work people to death — \"they sell you hope, they sell you pride, dressed up graves where your dreams have died\" — the imperative is to rise, shatter chains, and march through the storm together.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~sent-to-destroy":{"haiku":"An apocalyptic vision of divine destruction and conquest, with mechanical and inhuman forces sent to devastate humanity as an instrument of divine will.","opus":"Andy LaPlegua's aggrotech and industrial-metal project delivers pure war-machine spectacle, the narrator advancing as an unstoppable mechanized force dispatched to annihilate the listener, and framing that destruction as a punishment sent by their own deity. It's militant menace as theater, powered by the crushing electronic-body-music aggression that made the act a dark-club fixture.","sonnet":"An anthem of divine-sanctioned domination — mechanical, armored, running on wrath, the speaker claims to have been sent by God not to protect but to annihilate. The irony of a god who issues destruction orders is left unresolved; the song wears the posture straight, turning theological authority into pure aggression.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~skullcrusher":{"haiku":"The speaker rages against someone who holds them back and refuses forgiveness, blaming them for becoming emotionally numb and controlled. They demand freedom from this relationship.","opus":"An aggro-industrial tantrum against being used and held back, the narrator refusing to forgive someone serving him nothing but garbage, the whole thing a caustic demand to be let out of another's control.","sonnet":"Rage at someone blocking the narrator's path and feeding them something worthless — there's no forgiveness, the relationship is built on mutual deception and bitter knowledge — and the song is a furious demand to get out of the way.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~they":{"haiku":"Collective power and mass desire consume individuality; the narrator warns that billions driven by want will destroy all and force surrender.","opus":"A paranoid anthem about a faceless collective that wants to own you and grind you down, and the only defense being to limit desire and refuse to be understood. It reads as dread of mass conformity swallowing the self.","sonnet":"An anonymous, omnipresent force — a billion strong — wants total control, feeds on desire, and is already winning; the only resistance is limiting one's own wants and reclaiming autonomy before being swallowed entirely by a system designed to make everyone crawl.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~throat-full-of-glass":{"haiku":"Suffocation, numbness, and inward screaming; unable to breathe or speak, burning with internal pain.","opus":"A spiral of self-blame and numbness, feeling perpetually in the wrong and judged by every face in the crowd. It is about being trapped between craving oblivion and being unable to voice the pain choking you.","sonnet":"Surrounded by judgment and choked by an environment of lies, the speaker feels broken into pieces and unable to express their pain — like screaming with a throat full of glass — drifting between numbness, the wish never to have existed, and the struggle to stay conscious.","src":"haiku"},
"combichrist~without-emotions":{"opus":"A stark evocation of total emotional deadness, where breath is reduced to a clock merely ticking, stripped of love, hate, and all feeling.","sonnet":"A portrait of complete emotional vacancy — breath reduced to a mechanical clock ticking — stripped of love, hate, and feeling entirely; the brevity and repetition enact the flatness being described.","src":"sonnet"},
"combichrist~wtf-is-wrong-with-you-people":{"haiku":"Condemns intolerance, ignorance, and willful complicity in suffering. Calls out the innocent veneer masking guilt.","opus":"A hostile broadside against bigotry and smug stupidity, needling its target with a taunting demand to explain what is so broken in them, while insisting guilt is universal. Provocation is the entire point. Combichrist, Andy LaPlegua's aggrotech project, deliver the contempt through their trademark punishing industrial dance-metal.","sonnet":"Disgust at intolerance dressed as self-righteousness — the speaker looks at people who make suffering an identity and finds no innocence anywhere, including in themselves. The question in the title isn't rhetorical so much as exhausted: a scream at a crowd of screaming faces, all of them suffocating on their own noise.","src":"haiku"},
"combust~paid-with-pain":{"haiku":"Achievement demands suffering, with the narrator rejecting complacency and embracing hardship as the price of authentic accomplishment.","opus":"A hardcore creed that everything worth having is earned through suffering, contemptuous of a complacent, overstimulated society that throws its chances away. The pride is in refusing to let gods or kings pull the strings and paving your own hard road.","sonnet":"An overstimulated society of blind complacency is rejected: the speaker asserts that the best things in life are earned through pain, not handed over, and frames their refusal to accept easy comfort as both a philosophy and a self-made identity.","src":"haiku"},
"comeback-kid~because-of-all":{"haiku":"The speaker is unable to express what needs to be said and chooses to leave rather than communicate, letting resentment accumulate. The song captures the moment when silence and unspoken things become unbearable enough to force a departure.","opus":"A hardcore reckoning with everything left unsaid before walking away from someone. The honesty lands in admitting the departure wasn't clean or passive but a slow settling of scores with words that never came out.","sonnet":"A song about the paralysis that comes from not being able to say what needs to be said in a relationship — the narrator retreats, pulls down the shades, and eventually leaves, not out of aggression but because the words kept failing and the silence became unbearable.","src":"haiku"},
"comeback-kid~face-the-fire":{"haiku":"The speaker is a product of pain and change, fraudulent and remorseful; they cannot persuade another, and must face inevitable consequences alone.","opus":"The Canadian hardcore veterans stage a reckoning with pain that can't be sidestepped, the narrator boxed in, isolated, and menaced by a hostile pack. Having failed to sway someone's mind, he resolves to confront the blaze directly. The song's charging urgency and gang vocals embody the collective, forward-driving spirit hardcore is built on.","sonnet":"Isolation inside a conflict no one else can change: the narrator has tried reasoning, tried persuading, and is left alone surrounded by adversaries. The fire must be faced without allies, without resolution — the only option is to walk into the fate head-on, outnumbered and unheard.","src":"haiku"},
"comeback-kid~heavy-steps":{"haiku":"A person realizes they have been tested repeatedly and found themselves worth nothing, sensing catastrophe coming and resigned to being consumed by it.","opus":"Hardcore band Comeback Kid march across unsteady ground, facing a self-destructive habit they can no longer downplay. The narrator admits he's given in and decided he isn't worth rescuing, calmly bracing for a collapse he expects to swallow everyone, promising to hold out longest even as he calls to be put to the test.","sonnet":"A person trapped under accumulated pressure and self-doubt waits numbly for a collapse that will at least end the waiting. Feeling unworthy of rescue, the narrator describes moving through hollow ground that registers every footfall but offers nothing solid underfoot.","src":"haiku"},
"comet~opium":{"haiku":"Toxic dependency becomes inescapable, with the narrator unable to quit despite recognising the harm and willing participation in destruction.","opus":"Addiction dressed up as a poisonous romance, where the lover and the substance blur into one thing that aches to keep near yet cannot be given up. Images of a body barely holding together, sagging and patched over its fractures, capture someone trapped in a dizzying loop.","sonnet":"A toxic attachment is described with the precision of addiction: something that hurts to hold on to, impossible to quit, the speaker knotted up inside and crawling back regardless — \"you know how bad I want it\" admits the pull is beyond reason.","src":"haiku"},
"comrades~fault-lines":{"opus":"A comforting word to someone depleted and giving everything away, reassuring them there's still room for them, that present hardship isn't permanent, and that hope can meet the unknown and feel like home.","sonnet":"A short, gentle encouragement to someone who is depleted from constant giving — the song reminds them that even when failure draws cracks beneath their feet, there is room for them and the present moment is not permanent.","src":"sonnet"},
"concrete-dream~buckout-road":{"haiku":"Depicts a date turning violent and predatory, with a man threatening and likely killing a woman who resisted his control. Captures domestic terror and flight.","opus":"A horror narrative set on a cursed road, a couple's late-night drive turning violent as a coercive man menaces a woman who bolts for her life, the point-of-view sliding into panic, a crash, blood and a desperate flight through the woods. The Run refrain drives the terror. Built on a slasher-movie premise complete with a fateful warning, it's storytelling rap as a full-blown midnight nightmare.","sonnet":"A date-night horror story that spirals into mutual terror on Buckout Road — the man engineers isolation under false pretenses, the woman resists and then the car crashes, and the song fractures into his panicked monologue as he realizes he is now as lost as she is, bleeding, one shoe, no weed, no alibi. The road's reputation as a place you don't drive swallows them both.","src":"haiku"},
"confessions-of-a-traitor~myra":{"haiku":"Spiritual presence enduring beyond death; love transcending the body's final departure.","opus":"A metalcore elegy for someone lost to a merciless disease, holding onto the belief that the dead walk beside us and live on through the grieving. Faith fed until fear starves is the survival mechanism.","sonnet":"Grief addressed to someone lost to a relentless disease, the speaker promising that the dead live on through the living even while admitting their own pain will never cease — strength born in suffering, fear starved by faith, reunion deferred to another life.","src":"haiku"},
"confessions-of-a-traitor~twelve":{"haiku":"Obsessive circling traps the speaker in psychological repetition; loyalty poisoned and conviction fractured, they struggle to escape the maze of their own making.","opus":"An obsessive, wounded attachment the narrator can't shake from mind or heart despite the distance between them, circling the same grooves with no way out. Fleeting clear-headed spells give him a flicker of hope that he might finally throw off the fixation gripping him.","sonnet":"An obsessive thought loop about a betrayal: the person who was treated like royalty betrayed the speaker's loyalty, and now the speaker cannot get them out of their head — circling, finding no escape, feeling confined. The turn comes with a brief moment of clarity and a declaration that their convictions might let them overthrow their addictions.","src":"haiku"},
"confess~evin":{"haiku":"A prisoner condemned for their beliefs refuses to surrender their soul to corrupt systems, choosing spiritual defiance over physical escape.","opus":"The Iranian band Confess, whose members were prosecuted at home, pour that ordeal into a defiant prison song named for Tehran's notorious detention facility. Locked in solitary and facing execution, the narrator refuses to trade his convictions for survival, trusting that posterity will vindicate him, that a fallen sun still rises, and that they can jail him for writing the truth but never seize his spirit.","sonnet":"A political prisoner inside Evin prison refuses to break under interrogation, solitary confinement, and the threat of execution for writing truth. Faith here is not religious but historical — the certainty that names and choices outlast the bodies that bear them, that those who sit in darkness now become the dawn others wake into.","src":"haiku"},
"conform~big-homie":{"haiku":"Bragging detachment; narrator content with toxic friendships and indifferent to others' suffering and despair.","opus":"A sneering deathcore piece about performative wellness and cutting off draining people, laced with drug imagery and open contempt for hangers-on. The forced good vibes and threats mask a mind fantasizing about violence, the bravado curdling into instability.","sonnet":"A song of deliberate cruelty dressed as contentment — the narrator claims to feel great while ordering a companion to do drugs, then turns on leeches and enemies with explicit violence, telling them to cry and die, while maintaining a facade of being too unbothered to care.","src":"haiku"},
"conform~blood-eagle":{"haiku":"Spiritual death since the 1990s; trapped watching the world through blind cracks, unable to escape confinement.","opus":"A crisis of lost faith and confinement, mockingly demanding any word from a Jesus unheard in decades while the narrator rots inside his own walls. It builds to the bleak accusation that God is only a voice in your own head and salvation a waste spent on worthless sins.","sonnet":"Someone who has sealed themselves inside their house, peeking through blinds at a world that lost its meaning around 1994 — poisoned by something sweet, dead inside, going out of their mind with fear, yet eventually defiant enough to declare they won't be kept confined forever.","src":"haiku"},
"conform~cyanide":{"haiku":"Self-destructive alienation masked as strength; the speaker digs deeper into ego and fear, knowing they're burning out yet finding perverse satisfaction in the cycle of self-sabotage.","opus":"A self-destructive artist writing his own morbid narrative, digging a hole to feed his ego and welcoming spit on his grave just to be remembered. The core is exhaustion, despising the very thing he loves and running out of anywhere left to go.","sonnet":"A self-destructive narrator fixates on the certainty that even after death people will spit on his grave, while inside a relationship he is paralyzed by mutual contempt — despising the very thing he loves — burning out on his own fears and unable to locate any way forward.","src":"haiku"},
"conform~eat-more-death":{"haiku":"Reckless consumption and self-destruction—encouragement to indulge in excess despite fatal consequences; dark ironic commentary on self-harm through overconsumption.","opus":"Chosen isolation and self-medicated numbness as a defense against people whose faces read as masks of deceit. The narrator would rather stay home with his demons and wait for karma to cleanse the world than pretend to enjoy the company he distrusts.","sonnet":"A song about isolation and contempt for social performance: the narrator prefers staying home alone with their demons over attending parties with people whose real faces are flesh-masks of lies; they numb themselves with alcohol and lust while waiting for karma to cleanse the world of these people's presence.","src":"haiku"},
"conform~help-yourself":{"fable":"Cutting loose from a toxic manipulator: drowning out their fake face, refusing to be \"a pawn in your game,\" still checking DMs for a message from heaven that won't come — and landing on the hard freedom of \"I'm better off alone.\" Betrayal answered not with revenge but with exit.","src":"fable"},
"conform~luxury-letdown":{"haiku":"A blunt warning about false security—you think you've figured life out, but reality will hit hard.","opus":"This is hardcore in its purest social function — a scene reckoning with its own rot. The narrator, carrying 40 stitches in his chest, has watched bands and trends die while others coasted on his emotional labor, treating his suffering as content. Conform stage the exact moment the genre exists to stage: empathy burning out, patience refused, and pure contempt taking the place where care used to be.","sonnet":"A furious rejection of false optimism and the people who coasted on the singer's emotional labor while the industry rotted around them — the narrator has 40 stitches in his chest, has watched bands and trends die, and is done performing patience for those who thought his suffering was content. The emotional core is the moment when empathy burns out and pure contempt takes its place.","src":"haiku"},
"conform~n-life":{"haiku":"Rejecting forced social conformity—resisting pressure to fit into predetermined molds; celebrating individuality against systemic demands for uniformity.","opus":"A nihilist deathcore screed about pretending, rotting, and being reborn through fire, spliced with drug-fueled aggression and fantasies of violence. Finding a new life reads less like renewal than damnation, self-destruction dressed as transcendence.","sonnet":"A hardcore song that juxtaposes a nihilistic communal chant (rotting in death, being cleansed by fire) with a first-person spiral of drug use, violence fantasies, and suicidal ideation — the narrator is simultaneously raging at others and pointing a gun at themselves, with no stable moral ground beneath either impulse.","src":"haiku"},
"conform~o-d-d":{"haiku":"Existential suffocation within materialist society despite abundance that should bring meaning.","opus":"Over a Bulgarian folk chant, the song broods on wasted lives and existential emptiness, indicting a modern world that possesses more comfort than any ancient ruler yet remains miserable, and wishing ruin on those who greedily strip the planet bare.","sonnet":"Built around a Bulgarian folk chant that loops like a ritual frame, the song's English sections meditate on spiritual emptiness and the absurdity of modern comfort — the speaker sees themselves as a mummified corpse rotting in a coffin called a life, while the outro turns to bitter irony at people who have everything and still complain.","src":"haiku"},
"conform~terrorwave":{"haiku":"Descent into violent psychosis driven by isolation; guns and self-destruction reflect the failure of mental stability.","opus":"Violent gun-toting bravado colliding with the narrator's own unraveling mind, boasts of spraying enemies undercut by a hole in his brain and the strength it takes not to go insane. The menace is inseparable from the psychological collapse driving it.","sonnet":"A song about operating on the edge of violent psychosis — an AK loaded and aimed at a show, enemies made to bleed, the narrator losing sanity while glorifying the gun-and-mask lifestyle as a personal brand called \"terrorwave,\" dismissing bland people as not worth existing.","src":"haiku"},
"confusing-paradise~skylla":{"haiku":"A speaker faces a mythic sea-monster representing overwhelming guilt and death, paralyzed by pressure and isolation. The song depicts terror of a hellish presence approaching from unseen depths.","opus":"Metalcore recast of the Scylla myth as a panic attack, the sea monster below standing in for inescapable dread and guilt at simply being alive. Paralysis, anxiety, and the sense that words both define and trap the self.","sonnet":"Drawing on the myth of Scylla, this maps the sea monster onto anxiety and paralysis — surrounded by snarling dogs, sinking into guilt and dread, unable to find escape, until death becomes the only exit.","src":"haiku"},
"conjurer~cracks-in-the-pyre":{"haiku":"Grief dissolves certainty as the bereaved confronts mortality and the possibility of meaningful connection beyond existence.","opus":"The British metal band wrestles with mourning while rejecting any promise of an afterlife, bristling at those who insist the dead person is anything beyond recollection. It sits in the raw uncertainty between reason and belief, treasuring physical remains even while accepting that no light will ever find the departed.","sonnet":"Grief without afterlife: the speaker cannot picture a dead loved one on any other side, finds no solace in science or spirit, and questions whether cherishing bones is honest when there is no soul — yet still would stand at the grave talking time away, seeing only light through the cracks of the pyre that never reaches the dead.","src":"haiku"},
"conner~american-dream":{"haiku":"Capitalist aspiration masks hollow desperation; the speaker exposes the myth underlying chase for material success.","opus":"Seedy desire dressed as aspiration, where casual sex and the American dream blur into the same transactional haze. The come-ons are hollow and the narrator half-knows it, chasing heat that feels like nothing.","sonnet":"A seduction that tips into disorientation — dizzy with heat, the narrator gives the other person full permission and slides from flirting into something more entangled: \"now we're in too deep.\" The American dream is named ironically in the same breath as \"love is wrong\" and \"she wants it rough,\" framing erotic abandon as the country's defining mythology.","src":"haiku"},
"conquer-divide~chemicals":{"haiku":"A self-aware condemnation of substance abuse and self-sabotage used to escape internal emptiness, where the narrator recognizes their complicity in their own downfall.","opus":"The all-female metalcore band renders a toxic relationship as a chemical dependency, a false ally who sets up the harm and lets someone else deliver the blow while feeding on the narrator's paralysis. Paranoia and self-medication frame it as a sickness, its scorched imagery turning betrayal into defiance.","sonnet":"A relationship framed as chemical dependency: the other person knows the narrator better than she knows herself, exploiting that intimacy to destabilize and control. Paranoia, loss of agency, and the recognition that someone else loaded the gun while pretending to offer comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"conquer-divide~n-e-w-h-e-a-v-e-n":{"haiku":"Spiritual bankruptcy follows the climb to the top; the speaker finds materialized dreams hollow and thirsts for meaning beyond the reached prize.","opus":"Reaching the summit and getting everything you wanted, only to find it hollow and your faith gone, so you beg for some fresh paradise to believe in. The higher the climb, the less there is to hold onto, dreams souring into a weight around the neck after a soul was sold and never bought back.","sonnet":"After clawing to the top and drinking holy water only to spit it out, the speaker finds faith emptied out by achievement — success became a curse around the neck, dreams sold for a soul that isn't coming back, and what remains is a desperate need for a new heaven to replace the one that failed.","src":"haiku"},
"conquer-divide~paralyzed":{"haiku":"Trauma and self-sabotage interlock into immobility; the speaker recognizes their own demolition yet cannot stop the machinery designed to break them.","opus":"A panic-attack account of being your own saboteur, caught in the gears of anxiety and haunted memory with no space to breathe. The paralysis is the realization that no rescue is coming for a mind doing this to itself.","sonnet":"Someone who has lost all forward momentum under the weight of their own trauma and intrusive thoughts — they recognize that their worst enemy is themselves, are unable to escape the machine of anxiety and memory, and feel frozen between wanting rescue and knowing none is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"conquer-divide~welcome2paradise":{"haiku":"Collective negligence breeds apocalyptic consequence; the speaker witnesses willful blindness transform into catastrophic inheritance of destruction.","opus":"A furious eco-reckoning indicting humanity's greed and denial, marching us all in a procession toward a self-made oblivion we refuse to look at until it's too late.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of humanity's self-destructive consumption and willful ignorance of environmental collapse — we've built a paradise out of our own ruin, dancing on graves and welcoming the death we've engineered for ourselves.","src":"haiku"},
"conquer-divide~wide-awake":{"haiku":"Suffocation within imposed identity and manipulation leads to awakening, where the narrator reclaims selfhood through deliberate separation.","opus":"Waking up inside a controlling relationship that tried to reshape the narrator into something she's not, boxed in and bled out. The clarity of the title is her refusal to keep pretending, reclaiming the dreams and self she had before him.","sonnet":"A relationship built on control — put into a box, bent into shape, made into something unrecognisable — is finally named and refused: the speaker has woken up, remembers who they were before, and will not be bled out to sustain someone else's need.","src":"haiku"},
"contention~ousted-from-eden":{"haiku":"Urban dehumanisation traps populations in mechanical existence, severing connection to nature and authentic life beneath concrete towers.","opus":"A hardcore indictment of the modern city as an engineered hell — towers like bared fangs, homes that are really cells, people processed and stacked into rows. The brutal skyline breeds radicalism, paradise ground down to grit in a place daylight never reaches.","sonnet":"Urban dehumanisation read as expulsion from paradise: radicalized by jagged skylines like gnashing teeth, bodies lined in concrete towers where the sun never rises, the speaker declares that human beings were not made to live as writhing masses in man-made hell.","src":"haiku"},
"contracult-collective~it-s-the-water":{"haiku":"A cult drinks poison in a suffocating sanctuary, executing dissenters while celebrating tribal honor. Isolation breeds violence and death becomes ritual.","opus":"A cult utopia exposed as an execution ground, where communal ritual and shared drink mask a machinery of judgment and hanging, and the speaker would rather die than accept that this counts as being alive.","sonnet":"A song about cult-like group dynamics and the violence of exile — the community presents itself as a utopia of saints and martyrs but dispenses brutal judgment on outsiders and dissenters, ritually hanging those who fail to belong, with the narrator ultimately preferring death to life inside such a place.","src":"haiku"},
"contracult-collective~wdyt":{"haiku":"Gaslighting and control through language; shame-built structures masquerading as progress.","opus":"A furious rebuttal to public accusation, casting a call-out as vindictive harassment and threatening to expose the accuser in turn. The song weaponizes wounded reputation, insisting the narrative in motion is the real abuse.","sonnet":"A direct address to someone who used social media harassment as a weapon, built a cult narrative around it, and failed: the stories changed, the yammer was incoherent, the attempted takedown backfired. The speaker has archived everything and watches the aggressor hit the bottom.","src":"haiku"},
"converge~bad-faith":{"haiku":"Pain persists despite efforts to cleanse it, remaining indelible and unyielding in the face of love and hope.","opus":"A raw refusal to abandon a hurting love, grief so heavy it overwhelms yet can't rinse away the broken trust between two people. The repetition is a stubborn insistence on holding onto them even as sorrow corrodes the bond.","sonnet":"A love strained to its limits — eyes grown heavy, drowning in tears — refuses to give up on itself despite the bad faith that won't wash away, with each party urging the others not to surrender even as sadness hardens into permanence.","src":"haiku"},
"converge~lord-of-liars":{"haiku":"Truth crumbles before willful ignorance and deception, leaving only cold detachment and inevitable decline.","opus":"A cold, line-by-line reckoning with a manipulative coward, taking apart his lies and hollow certainties until nothing's left standing. Converge deliver the verdict as something quiet and final — a farewell issued with the certainty that the deceiver is already rotting from the inside.","sonnet":"A spiteful destroyer who gaslights and deflects — insisting it's all hysteria, denying knowledge of their own wrongdoing — is condemned as the lord of the liars, with the speaker promising they have not forgotten and an ice age is coming for cowards.","src":"haiku"},
"converge~my-great-devastator-redux":{"opus":"A grieving address to the dead and departed, where silence and unsent letters devastate the singer, who keeps the lights on hoping the lost one might be found again as the rope of time runs short.","sonnet":"A grieving, unfinished elegy addressed to someone lost: the narrator has written countless unsent letters to the dead, kept the lights on in case they return, and wrestles with whether this love — his great devastator — is the last chance or just another chapter in an endless unfinished story.","src":"sonnet"},
"convey~nutrition":{"haiku":"Marketing and media manufacture desire; you're fed what keeps you docile and spending.","opus":"A hardcore indictment of consumer conditioning, the billboard and the screen feeding false comfort to keep you buying and compliant. It's about being farmed by advertising, mistaking manufactured hope for nourishment.","sonnet":"Consumerism and media are framed as a conditioning machine that feeds people managed images and hollow answers, keeping them productive and isolated — the speaker addresses the listener directly as a product waiting on a shelf, already addicted to being used.","src":"haiku"},
"coping-method~hypomania":{"haiku":"Hypomania as escape and self-imprisonment, oscillating between craving and rejection of its intoxication.","opus":"A visceral portrait of the manic swing, craving and revulsion firing at once, the narrator haunting his own body and jailed by other people's words. Letting go sounds less like peace than surrender.","sonnet":"About the bipolar experience of hypomania — the seductive pull of an elevated state that feels like being alive, the compulsion to chase it, and the shame and imprisonment that come with it. The narrator oscillates between wanting and rejecting this state that takes over the soul.","src":"haiku"},
"coping-method~underground":{"haiku":"The speaker digs toward buried souls while bearing others' weight; communication fractures as they suffocate under accumulated poison and silence.","opus":"A struggle to make sense of things and throw off the crushing weight of pressure and hate, split between digging a buried self back up and just letting it slip under. The venom in his veins keeps circling back to a toxic bond of shared blame that he finally resolves to break.","sonnet":"A speaker oscillates between wanting to hold someone back from self-destruction and realising they must let them fall, feeling the poison of the dynamic coursing through them — 'every drop I drink leads back to you' — and finally cutting the cord while promising they'll still be found.","src":"haiku"},
"corbon-amodio~numb-myself":{"haiku":"Isolation breeds numbness as the narrator dissociates from emotional pain and social belonging, seeking permanent escape through self-harm.","opus":"A blunt account of depression and dissociation, lacking purpose and scared of his own emotions, isolating online and waiting for a call that won't come. The chorus's grim image of self-injury stands in for a wish to permanently numb the pain of feeling.","sonnet":"Isolation and emotional exhaustion — overthinking alone, feeling cold around warm faces, wondering if anyone will call — drive the speaker toward a desire to permanently numb themselves, a state everyone around them can apparently already see.","src":"haiku"},
"corella~blood-drunk":{"haiku":"Survival demands hardness and disengagement from sentiment, with the speaker rejecting empathy in favour of self-preservation.","opus":"A restless nudge to quit acting superior and let the pavement teach you, aimed at someone adrift and battling their own gut. The mood is heedless togetherness, a pack with nothing to protect egging one another onward until certainty finally arrives.","sonnet":"A street-level manifesto against complacency: the speaker kicks aside false comfort, urges a fight against inner feeling rather than surrendering to it, and draws strength from friends with nothing to lose — head up high, blood run dry.","src":"haiku"},
"corey-taylor-lzzy-hale-scott-ian-dave-lombardo-fil-eisler-tina-guo~thunder-force":{"haiku":"Defiance ignites as the speaker summons power against overwhelming odds; thunder becomes the weapon and belief becomes the catalyst for transformation.","opus":"A superhero rallying cry: hold your ground when everything falls apart and become the thing your enemies dread. Its hook makes courage an act of will — power doesn't arrive on its own, you have to summon it — turning self-belief into the nerve to strike back.","sonnet":"A rallying anthem against those who mean to break the world: the speaker instructs the listener to square their stance, be exactly what the enemy is afraid of, and remember that lightning never strikes unless you force the thunder. Looks can be deceiving; the darkest hour is when you show them you have power.","src":"haiku"},
"corey-taylor~culture-head":{"haiku":"Performative morality and weaponized shame poison society; the speaker rejects generational performativity and sanctimonious cruelty masked as righteousness.","opus":"A scathing attack on frauds who weaponize God and on a perpetually offended, sanctimonious culture that drags everyone down. The pun on pray and prey exposes the cult lodged inside the culture, with shame as its chief motivator.","sonnet":"A screed against performative religion and outrage culture: the speaker distrusts anyone who uses God as an excuse, skewers generations addicted to being offended in their niche, and diagnoses the problem as a cult embedded in the culture itself — \"shame is a motivator,\" the mechanism never named but always running.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~about-life":{"haiku":"Modern systems of war, consumption, and spectacle obscure human truth; society enforces ignorance and prevents genuine understanding of reality.","opus":"A seething denunciation of a civilization that has lost its bearings, where wealth outranks the value of a human being, warfare exports ideology, faith trades in bigotry, and mortality becomes evening entertainment. The Swiss thrashers hammer a refrain accusing a smug, deliberately blind public of understanding nothing.","sonnet":"A list of modern civilisation's failures — the speed of the world, wars fought to spread ideology, religions weaponising hate, revolutions that move backward, and children taught to ignore reality — building to the repeated insistence that despite all claims, nobody actually knows anything.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~caveat-to-the-coming":{"haiku":"The speaker abandons all ties and constraints, riding toward an uncertain future with deliberate recklessness and refusal to let anything slow their departure.","opus":"A thrash band covering a hobo-blues standard, riding a runaway freight train with no farewell and no ties to hold him down. The drifter's freedom is also a warning to anyone who'd try to slow him.","sonnet":"A cover of a traditional blues-inflected song about a man who takes off without warning or ceremony — no brass band, no tears, no sand in his pocket to weigh him down. He is a drifter who follows freight trains and refuses to be slowed by anyone, warning others not to waste his time because the distance between them is too great to close.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~der-mussolini":{"haiku":"A surreal dance command that invokes authoritarian and religious figures—Mussolini, Hitler, Jesus, Communism—as if they were novelty dance styles, collapsing their historical weight into absurdist entertainment.","opus":"A cover of D.A.F.'s cold satire, barking dance instructions that set Mussolini, Hitler, Jesus, and communism side by side; the horror is the flippant equivalence, making the audience physically obey ideologies as if choreographing fascism.","sonnet":"A cover of D.A.F.'s provocation-dance track that treats Hitler, Mussolini, Jesus Christ, and Communism as interchangeable dance moves, using the absurdity of the instruction format to flatten and mock the authority of all these figures simultaneously.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~divine-step-conspectu-mortis":{"haiku":"Death arrives as crossing into unknown territories, the soul journeys toward judgment where all previous certainties dissolve.","opus":"The final heartbeat rendered as a crossing beyond the final border of time, the soul taking its first step out of the body toward an unknown afterlife. Facing death it asks what sin and God even are, warning that the answer only arrives once you learn whether your heaven turns out black.","sonnet":"A meditation on the precise moment of death — the soul taking its first step out of the body, the final heartbeat, crossing into an unknown afterlife, with the central unanswered question being whether God and sin are real or if the heaven waiting is black.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~divine-step":{"haiku":"A dark love song depicting violence, death, and possession—the narrator takes their lover away through fatal violence, freezing them in time forever.","opus":"A cold, ritualistic murder cast as mercy, the killer slitting a victim's skin, watching blood on white skin in slow motion, then framing the death as saving her from time. He caresses the corpse turned to a frozen crystal rose, twisting killing into a possessive, eternal act of love.","sonnet":"A slow, ritualistic murder described in intimate, almost loving terms — the killer cuts a person's wrists, watches them bleed, and then kneels to caress the frozen corpse, framing the killing as a kind of salvation from time.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~grin":{"haiku":"Forced endurance despite systemic cruelty leaves the narrator grinning through agony while losing grip on identity and will.","opus":"The Swiss thrash band maps a total mental collapse through oppression and despair into violence as the final option, tenderness never once reaching the narrator. What remains is a frozen grin held until the self dissolves entirely, madness driven home like a spike.","sonnet":"A catalogue of internal devastation — nails in the brain, boredom like nitroglycerine, religion as violent innocence, society with more locks than keys — strips everything away until only a fixed grin remains, just before the self is completely lost.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~host":{"haiku":"An internal parasite that consumes the host from within, breeding in secret while the host oscillates between awareness and complicity in its own torment.","opus":"A visceral body-horror allegory of a parasite nestled in the brain, breeding, sweating and burning, knowing the narrator piece by piece and driving him to feed it, blood running like insane circulation until he faces one unlocked door and opens it. Coroner are a Swiss technical thrash trio revered for their intricate, cerebral riffing, and this claustrophobic possession fantasy suits their dark, complex style.","sonnet":"An addiction or compulsion rendered as a parasite that has made the speaker its permanent temple — it knows them completely, settles into the brain, takes its tithe each time they give in, and has reduced them to a single unlocked door. The clinical language gives the horror a strange intimacy: this thing has lived inside so long it has become the closest relationship they have.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~i-want-you-she-s-so-heavy":{"opus":"A thrash cover reducing desire to its most obsessive core: wanting someone so badly it drives you mad, the heaviness of a love too intense to shake. Repetition is the point, craving looping until it becomes crushing weight.","sonnet":"A cover of the Beatles song stripped to its obsessive core — an almost hypnotic repetition of desperate wanting, the person so heavy with need and gravity that the narrator is driven mad by desire.","src":"sonnet"},
"coroner~internal-conflicts":{"haiku":"A person describes willingness to inflict self-destruction rather than cause harm to another, setting an absolute boundary against violence toward others.","opus":"A blunt catalogue of self-harm, each act of violence promised only whenever the impulse arrives. Coroner, the Swiss technical-thrash trio prized for cold precision, pare this to brutal minimalism, and the final swerve, a vow never to harm the listener, turns all that imagined destruction inward, revealing a chilling confession of pain the narrator keeps to himself.","sonnet":"A catalogue of extreme self-harm is enumerated with cold deliberateness — crushing, cutting, slashing — before the final line reverses everything: none of it will ever be directed at another person. The violence is entirely internal, a measure of suffering that draws a hard moral line at harming anyone else.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~last-entertainment":{"haiku":"Society has devolved into spectacle where public suffering and death serve as entertainment for a dying, perverse culture built on exploitation and decay.","opus":"The Swiss thrash intellectuals stage a bleak critique of a decadent society that consumes suffering as amusement. Spectators feed on staged cruelty and real victims alike, boredom driving an ever-greater appetite for spectacle, until a spent culture turns to perversion and the whole thing reads as a portrait of bloodthirsty decline.","sonnet":"A dying culture sustains itself on spectacles of suffering and justice-as-performance, feeding an audience whose boredom creates ever-greater hunger for degradation. As legitimate resources exhaust, only perversion remains to fill the void, and the spectators' slow heartbeats mark the tempo of a civilisation composting itself.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~masked-jackal":{"haiku":"A charismatic leader manipulates masses through performative deception, hiding true motives behind public charm and strategic image management.","opus":"A takedown of a demagogue built by the media, a figure making vows with his fingers secretly crossed while crowds chant his slogans and his spotless image conceals a shady history. The band then wheels on the worshipping public, cautioning that the mob feeding his ascent needs to sort out its own affairs.","sonnet":"A dissection of a charismatic political demagogue — darling of the TV screen, master of spoken words, manipulator of purse strings — whose crossed fingers behind his back and dark past are invisible to the worshipping masses until he is shot and leaves a shambles.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~metamorphosis":{"haiku":"A supernatural being transforms into elements of death and torture before the victim wakes to discover they will never awake alive again.","opus":"A predator's seductive, shape-shifting approach to a victim, becoming a snake, a spike, a tongue, promising intimacy while quietly announcing the true intent: the person will not wake up alive.","sonnet":"A seductive entity shapeshifts into whatever form is needed — snake, spike, whisper, memory — to gain total intimacy with the target, making clear that when the victim wakes up they will already be dead.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~pale-sister":{"haiku":"A religiously devoted woman isolated by her extreme faith and devotion, living on the same planet but in a separate spiritual world, bound by chastity and humility that keep her apart.","opus":"The Swiss tech-thrashers sketch a woman consumed by religious devotion, chastity and self-mortification, living in a world no one around her can reach. It's about faith as a beautiful, isolating disease, City of God her only home.","sonnet":"A woman wholly consumed by religious devotion — kneeling, bleeding stigmata, eyes cast down under the weight of chastity — exists in a parallel reality shaped entirely by sacred texts; she speaks the same language as everyone else but inhabits a world no one outside can access.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~semtex-revolution":{"haiku":"Freed from oppression and control, the speaker has radicalized and now wages revolution through aggressive thought and action, refusing to trust anyone.","opus":"A radicalized loner's manifesto: beaten down and silenced by a system, he erases his identity and becomes a paranoid sniper who kills for his own private revolution. It's a portrait of terrorism from the inside, the revolt indistinguishable from vengeance.","sonnet":"A lone radical who has been suppressed and enslaved by unnamed forces designs a personal revolution conducted through stealth, sniper-like violence, and radical thought rather than open rebellion.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~serpent-moves":{"haiku":"A figure moves and sheds identity like a serpent, shedding skin and leaving sin behind in a desert landscape of transformation.","opus":"The Swiss technical-thrash pioneers craft something sparse and hypnotic: a narrator in transformation who may either discover or lose himself in a barren wilderness, summoning the moon and the dead through ritual gestures. Coroner favor cryptic, cerebral atmosphere over bombast, rendering change as slithering, skin-shedding movement across a desolate ceremonial ground.","sonnet":"A figure in the desert — raising the moon, cursing the sand, shedding an old self with the fluid ease of a serpent leaving its skin — moves through transformation without drama. The brevity and sparseness of the lyric make the change feel inevitable rather than violent: a natural molt across a floor of sin.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~shifter":{"haiku":"A murder confession unfolds across fragmented phone dialogue; the caller confesses killing and surrenders to police amid distressed maternal pleading.","opus":"Swiss technical thrashers Coroner build an unsettling scene of abduction and dread. An invitation to sink in and count the stars curdles into a rope and the panicked refrain of not again, a lottery where the narrator's hands no longer feel his own. A closing tearful phone call between a mother and her son Dwayne, confessing a killing from a warehouse ringed by police, turns the menace horrifyingly concrete.","sonnet":"What begins as a disorienting, possibly coercive encounter — a ride offered, boundaries dissolved — pivots into a real phone call between a killer and his mother. The conversation is raw and fragmentary: a confession, a sob, a police presence closing in, the warehouse address, a mother who cannot come.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~sirens":{"haiku":"A person grieves the loss of innocence and joy experienced with someone, watching sweet voices turn into harbingers of death. Time slowly erodes the beauty of memory, leaving only blood and regret.","opus":"An elegy for vanished innocence, contrasting a carefree, weightless youth with the pain that adulthood brings. What once felt beautiful curdles into hurt as time drains away, and the sweet remembered voices of the past come to sound like warnings, so that nostalgia itself becomes a reminder of death.","sonnet":"A lament for lost innocence and youth, recalling a time of weightless freedom before cold reality set in — the sweet voices of that era have become death omens, sirens foretelling doom. The song mourns the moment when life tips from lightness into knowledge of mortality.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~skeletion-on-your-shoulder":{"haiku":"Death as constant companion and burden; mortality weighs upon and accompanies the living.","opus":"Death (or addiction/temptation) personified as a seductive figure on your shoulder—sweet as honey, dangerous as a snake—luring the disillusioned toward escape from responsibility. It promises transcendence but exposes your greed and brings you to your knees, bleeding, when the curtain finally falls.","sonnet":"Death speaks in first person, seducing the exhausted and disillusioned with promises of weightlessness — \"drift thru the universe, no more responsibility\" — while concealing the cost: it tastes like honey but will bring you to your knees and make you bleed. The skeleton on the shoulder is an intimate tempter who exploits despair to claim its victim.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~son-of-lilith":{"haiku":"A mystical being—child of Lilith—possesses supernatural power through youth and purity; they need no physical form to affect others, carrying the moon in their veins.","opus":"A mystical monologue voiced by a being who names himself Lilith's child, rising out of nothingness with a legion of spirits at his side. He boasts of youthfulness as his shield, innocence as his blade, and lunar light running in his blood.","sonnet":"The speaker identifies as the son of the mythological figure Lilith, emerging from a void with youth and purity as his only defenses, carrying the moon in his veins and escorted by souls. The song frames this lineage as something beyond language or explanation, an identity felt rather than reasoned.","src":"haiku"},
"coroner~the-favorite-game":{"haiku":"Fragmented imagery suggests being lost in a game of suffering and servitude, with love offered without reciprocation. The song questions belonging amidst sorrow and fear.","opus":"An abstract, dark meditation on tragedy, fear, and love given without reply, using imagery of fire, smoke, and scars left on angels' faces. Amid the shattered voice of reason, the narrator is left asking where he belongs.","sonnet":"Fragmented, abstract imagery of fire, shadows, and lost love circles around a central question of belonging — the repeated \"where do I belong?\" is the emotional core of a song that gestures at grief, dislocation, and a search for meaning through imagery that is deliberately obscure.","src":"haiku","fable":"Coroner's Swiss thrash builds this from wreckage that refuses to cohere: a mind setting fire to its own ruin so the disaster can be admired, breathing in the black exhaust, watching what passed for clear thinking splinter before it finishes a thought. The favorite game is the cruelty of the detached observer — a Neutral that only watches, love left unanswered, obedience running on fear — and the watcher who compulsively records the carnage turns out to have inflicted the wounds he swears he only documents. The syntax fractures line by line — fitting for someone cataloguing his own collapse, who cannot assemble one straight sentence about his footing, which is why it all funnels down to the single unresolved question of where he belongs.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness","violence & murder"],"means":"the detached observer who documents wounds turns out to have inflicted them","built":"syntax fractures line by line, fitting the collapse it cannot assemble"},
"coroner~the-lethargic-age":{"haiku":"Masses are bound by apathy and comfort, chained to material desires while fear prevents them from truly living. Nature itself is imprisoned in this age of inertia and illusory luxury.","opus":"The Swiss technical thrash trio sketch a portrait of a sedated, complacent society, masses who shut their eyes rather than see, shackled by wealth they chose themselves and deafened by comfort while worshipping money. Their precise, cerebral heaviness underpins a bleak social critique of a people who caged nature and now revere a ruined symbol of freedom.","sonnet":"A portrait of mass voluntary paralysis — thousands of people who keep their eyes closed, chain their own hands with gold, deafen themselves with luxury, and then pray for mercy they could have seized themselves. The eagle with broken wings is the sharpest image: something that should soar, worshipped precisely because it has been made unable to fly.","src":"haiku"},
"corpse~code-mistake":{"haiku":"Error and malfunction, being broken beyond repair.","opus":"A raw dispatch from addiction and self-loathing, cataloguing pills and paranoia while insisting the narrator was a defective thing never meant to exist. The desperation to just breathe curdles into wanting it all to stop.","sonnet":"An existence that was never supposed to happen — \"baby I'm a code mistake, I was never meant to be\" — medicated into function (gabapentin to sleep, Adderall to wake, Omeprazole to eat), paranoid, abandoned by everyone who claims to help. \"Time slips away, burn out, don't fade\" is the loop: survival without purpose.","src":"haiku"},
"cory-henry~happy-days":{"haiku":"Love proposes the creation of joy as a shared project; simple affection blooms into the promise of building happiness together.","opus":"A courtship offered as an invitation rather than a plea: love is framed as something the two build together, day by day, if only the other will say yes.","sonnet":"An invitation to mutual joy: the speaker has never felt anything like this before, wants more of it, and asks the other person simply to say yes so they can create happy days together — making history, spending their life with them, just the two of them. The simplicity is the point.","src":"haiku"},
"counterparts~arms-like-teeth":{"haiku":"Codependency transforms into predator-prey dynamic where love becomes indistinguishable from consumption, scars accumulating as proof of intimate violence.","opus":"A dependency so total it welcomes harm, the singer offering himself to be dismantled and remade by the very hands that scar him, so long as they carry him forever.","sonnet":"The speaker welcomes the embrace of someone whose arms resemble teeth, offering their body to be scarred and disassembled as long as they are carried — a co-dependency framed as predator and prey, where love and destruction are the same gesture.","src":"haiku"},
"counterparts~love-me":{"opus":"A raw, repeated plea testing whether love is conditional, asking whether someone will still love the narrator once there is nothing left in him worth loving; an expression of self-loathing and fear of abandonment.","sonnet":"A stripped-down, recursive question about whether love can survive the complete erosion of self — the song asks whether someone will still love the narrator when there is nothing left to love, and then provides its own answer by stripping away even the question until only absence remains.","src":"sonnet"},
"counterparts~stranger":{"haiku":"Self-disgust and predatory nature merge as the narrator becomes unrecognizable in their own eyes, deceiving those they love while unable to face their reflection.","opus":"A man estranged from himself by shame and self-loathing, unable to meet his own reflection or the eyes of those he loves, begging to feel something while inviting his own abandonment.","sonnet":"The speaker confronts their own predatory nature and emotional parasitism — leeching off another's light, masked in courage they do not possess — and arrives at the recognition that they are a stranger to themselves when they look into the eyes of those they love.","src":"haiku"},
"counterparts~wings-of-nightmares":{"opus":"A dark meditation on a bond dragged on past its death, one partner cradling a corpse and telling themselves it still lives while the narrator, ailing and short on time, sees the doomed end coming and warns the other to stay far away from him.","sonnet":"The narrator is haunted by someone who cannot let go — who held their lifeless body and convinced herself she felt a pulse — and they are trying to tear themselves free from that delusion, knowing the relationship was always going to end in death.","src":"sonnet"},
"counting-crows~mr-jones":{"haiku":"Celebrity and fame seduce through glamour while leaving hollowness; the speaker craves recognition yet suspects its emptiness.","opus":"The ache to be seen and believed in, dressed as a barroom fantasy of stardom and Dylan-sized fame. Duritz's joke is bitter: the lonely dreamer imagines everybody loving him precisely because nobody does yet.","sonnet":"Two young men out drinking fantasise about fame, beauty, and being loved by everyone — the narrator craving to be someone special enough to believe in, both of them aware on some level that the dream is built on loneliness and longing rather than certainty.","src":"haiku"},
"covet~coronal":{"haiku":"Time dissolves into dream-logic while impermanence shadows joy; new distractions defer awakening to the collapse that awaits beyond sleep's boundary.","opus":"Math-rock disquiet about slipping through wreckage and diversion as a long-foreseen ending erodes the good times. The narrator keeps stepping willingly into emptiness, picking make-believe over a moment he has gone numb to.","sonnet":"Self-destruction and distraction converge as glory days melt away — the narrator floats in confusion, fantasy calling from the void while new distractions paper over a future everyone knew was coming, the impermanence of it all too large to face directly.","src":"haiku"},
"covet~farewell":{"opus":"A tender reassurance to someone in crisis, promising they'll survive if they take it day by day, while the speaker, still sentimental, admits they themselves are ready to let go.","sonnet":"A brief, tender send-off to someone in crisis — the narrator reassures them they will survive and make it through, while also acknowledging their own readiness to let go and move forward despite being sentimental.","src":"sonnet"},
"cq~disco-crimson":{"haiku":"A body moves through neon-soaked night, consciousness fragmenting under chemical acceleration. Speed warps perception into dissonant harmony; the speaker races forward while the world spins, seeking dissolution in motion.","opus":"A dissolving night in neon: warped cityscape, blurred vision, swarming particles and a black spiral where a fevered wound runs and screams over dissonant feedback; the speaker dances while weeping, sleepwalking to shake off sorrow, and finally turns his back on the world.","sonnet":"A feverish, injured figure runs through a distorted neon cityscape — warped skyline, dissonant sound waves, black spirals dissolving into light — weeping while dancing as if to shake off sorrow, until the world is finally turned away from entirely, leaving only the scream and forward motion.","src":"haiku"},
"cq~primal":{"haiku":"Solitude and meaningless time spiral into darkness; even fading light vanishes into void. The speaker abandons hope for transformation while sinking deeper, their primitive cry echoing through halls of nothing.","opus":"A restless nocturne about discarded solitude and abandoned meaning, time running on parallel tracks with no destination. A distant light flickers white then dissolves into darkness as the narrator burns his lukewarm routine away.","sonnet":"Solitude is thrown away only to surface again in a changeless morning, leaking through a crack; a distant light that once glowed white fades into darkness at the start-of-the-end vista, and the speaker burns away the warm stagnation of a boxed-in dream-world, rejecting self-indulgence at the seam where endings become beginnings.","src":"haiku"},
"cradle-of-filth~achingly-beautiful":{"haiku":"Beauty intoxicates and dooms; the speaker witnesses divine perfection knowing it requires her death to possess.","opus":"Dani Filth's gothic romance to the point of damnation, a fatal enchantment where a woman's beauty is worth dying and killing for, then reborn as an immortal queen of darkness. Love and destruction rendered as the same exquisite rite.","sonnet":"A gothic romance in which a man encounters a woman of paralyzing, unearthly beauty at a ball during a storm, falls immediately and irrevocably in love, and then watches her die — only for her to rise again as an immortal creature of darkness, transformed into something even more devastatingly beautiful by her rebirth. The narrator's devotion survives and deepens through her death and resurrection.","src":"haiku"},
"cradle-of-filth~from-cradle-to-enslave":{"haiku":"Civilization breeds catastrophe and corruption; paradise falls repeatedly while demons rise to feast on human faith.","opus":"An end-times sermon on two millennia of humanity fouling itself, staging a profane return of a savior figure who comes to shackle rather than redeem. Belief gets held in contempt as the world, an Eden squandered long ago, slides toward a sour, ruinous reckoning.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic condemnation of two thousand years of Christian civilization as a corrupt, death-eating force — the song prophesies its end in an infernal reckoning where all the violence, hypocrisy, and sexual repression encoded in religious power finally devour themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"cradle-of-filth~her-ghost-in-the-fog":{"haiku":"A beautiful woman is sexually assaulted and murdered by townspeople who believed her a witch, haunting the speaker as a lost queen forever misted in fog.","opus":"Cradle's gothic melodrama at its most literary, a Victorian tragedy of a woman branded a witch, raped and murdered by pious men who couldn't have her, and her lover's grief curdling into vengeance against the church that killed her.","sonnet":"A gothic narrative about a woman murdered by five religious men who raped her in the forest at night — the narrator loved her, finds her body at dawn, and takes revenge by burning the village chapel with the men locked inside.","src":"haiku"},
"cradle-of-filth~sodomy-lust":{"haiku":"Transgression and domination merge into violent ecstasy, celebrating sadism and desecration as spiritual transcendence.","opus":"Extreme metal theater of blasphemy and sadistic ritual, invoking carnal desire, cruelty, and defiance of any god that would deny it. It's transgression staged as ecstatic worship of sin, deliberately profane spectacle rather than any real creed.","sonnet":"A sadistic ceremonial voice addresses violence, carnal power, and blasphemy through the lens of occult desire — inviting daughters of the night, commanding whips and blood, and dismissing moral creeds as obstacles to dark rapture.","src":"haiku"},
"cradle-of-filth~temptation":{"haiku":"Describes an irresistible pull toward forbidden desire that cannot be resisted or denied, with temptation as both alluring and destructive. The song frames giving in as inevitable and impossible to escape.","opus":"A song about giving in to desire, framed as a gamble you can refuse but had best take seriously. The narrator finds himself unable to pull free of a consuming attraction, twisting a familiar prayer against surrender even as he lets the pull draw him further in.","sonnet":"A cover of the Heaven 17 song depicting someone utterly unable to resist a powerful attraction — each day, every second draws them closer despite the high cost, and the repeated refrain of \"temptation\" underlines how completely the desire overrides any will to resist.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cradle of Filth drag Heaven 17's glossy 1983 synth-pop into black-metal register, and the dumb dancefloor thrill of wanting the wrong thing curdles into actual damnation. The original built its hook on a gambler feeding coins into a rigged machine, and the cover barely alters a syllable — it simply hears the Lord's Prayer that was always hiding in the chorus and means it literally. Then the title-word repeats past sense into pure incantation, exposing a man's giddy surrender as a plea to be spared the very thing he keeps chasing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"A Heaven 17 cover, cut for Thornography and sent out as its 2006 single — the duet voice is Dirty Harry, London singer Victoria Harrison.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"a gambler's giddy surrender heard as a plea to be spared what he chases","built":"the title word repeated past sense into incantation exposes a Lord's Prayer inside the chorus"},
"cradle-of-filth~wester-vespertine":{"haiku":"Twilight awakens desire and transcendence; the speaker embraces nocturnal magic as day's constraints dissolve.","opus":"An ecstatic hymn to nightfall itself, the daylight endured as betrayal until dusk releases every dark and sensual desire. Cradle make twilight a threshold into freedom, decadence and the occult glamour of the coming night.","sonnet":"A sensuous, darkly ecstatic celebration of dusk — the hour of bats, lengthening shadows, gas lamps, and gothic liberation — in which the speaker finds their true self unleashed as daylight retreats and the night opens into transgressive pleasure and mystical excess. The twilight hour is framed as a personal resurrection.","src":"haiku"},
"cradle-of-filth~you-will-know-the-lion-by-his-claw":{"haiku":"Predatory nature and inevitable violence; the hunter becomes the hunted in a dance where mercy has fled.","opus":"Told from a lion's point of view, the hunter becoming the hunted on the savannah, nature's apex predator reclaiming its arena. Beneath the imagery, a warning about mistaking a fake demeanor for weakness and paying with your life.","sonnet":"An extended metaphor in which a lion — majestic, patient, unstoppable — stalks prey across the savannah, embodying a predatory nature that no human trap or technology can contain or defeat. The lion-speaker savors the terror of the hunted and the beauty of a kill executed with primal sovereignty.","src":"haiku"},
"crash-kings~mountain-man":{"haiku":"Intoxication and elevation merge into ecstatic isolation; shared experience vanishes into individual reverie.","opus":"A reckless high framed as scaling a peak blindfolded, sipping sunshine and saving the reckoning for the afterlife. Under the swagger runs a darker current about intoxication as free fall and a companion drinking herself away.","sonnet":"A speaker balanced between exhilaration and freefall — atop mountains and hillsides, metaphorically and literally — describes a relationship where both parties are intoxicated in different ways and coming apart in the descent.","src":"haiku"},
"crawlers~i-don-t-want-it":{"haiku":"Compulsive attachment despite self-awareness creates cycles of rejection and need, leaving the narrator visible yet unseen.","opus":"The compulsions of a hurting young life, tying love to shrinking your body, craving attention while ignoring the messages you fear. Beneath the deflection about not being an addict is a generation numbing itself with drink, drugs, and jealousy, insisting it isn't self-harm.","sonnet":"A generation of young people entangled in self-destructive patterns — buying things they don't need, running out of weed, smiling through self-harm — frames it all as something unwanted yet inescapable, orbiting a conditional love that requires them to shrink.","src":"haiku"},
"crazy-town~butterfly":{"haiku":"A man finds redemption and love through an attractive woman who lifts him from a wild, troubled past; she becomes his anchor and the reason he's putting his life down.","opus":"Crazy Town's 2000 rap-rock hit, built on a Red Hot Chili Peppers guitar loop, is an infatuated tribute to a woman the narrator credits with pulling him out of a broken, aimless stretch of his life. Under the bravado sits real gratitude, the sense that a storybook romance he never believed in has actually arrived.","sonnet":"A narrator credits a woman with pulling him out of a chaotic and directionless life — past legal trouble, hollowness, the assumption that happiness was always elsewhere. The gratitude is total and physical, framing the relationship as the first thing that made the world feel real rather than something to escape.","src":"haiku"},
"crazyeightyeight~fortune-and-glory-kid":{"haiku":"Hearts torn apart and legacies buried, the world remembers individuals not by their names but by the violence of their erasure.","opus":"A cry to be remembered by name, to leave a legacy even at the cost of being torn apart. Beneath the metalcore bravado sits a quieter yearning to build something and finally be at peace.","sonnet":"A speaker torn apart by the need to be remembered wrestles with being cast down and left behind, demanding to be known by name and to have their legacy outlast those who destroyed them — the final note, from a sampled voice, frames legacy as the moment you stop looking back and start creating.","src":"haiku"},
"crazyeightyeight~nitroglycerin":{"haiku":"Seeking release through self-destruction and violent breakthrough, where the narrator craves feeling anything intensely enough to transcend numb apathy.","sonnet":"Borrowing the existential framework of a generation with no great war and no purpose, a narrator channels that emptiness into a volatile inner explosion. Self-destruction becomes the answer he has found — not nihilism so much as a violent, cathartic proof that he can still feel something.","src":"haiku"},
"creedence-clearwater-revival~down-on-the-corner":{"haiku":"Street musicians create joy and community in humble circumstances, bringing free entertainment and togetherness to anyone who stops to listen.","opus":"A warm snapshot of a ragtag street band playing for nickels, joy made from almost nothing. Fogerty's affection is for the poorboys and the small communal magic of music that costs only what you can spare.","sonnet":"A warm street scene where a young band called Willy and the Poorboys plays for free on the corner, offering music as pure communal pleasure — no money needed, just presence and a nickel if you have one.","src":"haiku"},
"creedence-clearwater-revival~fortunate-son":{"haiku":"Privileged elites dodge military service through wealth and connections while poor youth are conscripted into war, exposing the class divide in wartime sacrifice.","opus":"A Vietnam-era protest against class inequality in war: the privileged sons of senators and millionaires wave the flag and dodge the fighting while the poor are sent to die. The narrator insists 'it ain't me,' he's no fortunate one, indicting the hypocrisy of those who beat the war drum but never pay its price.","sonnet":"About the class divide in who gets sent to war — the wealthy and well-connected wave flags and collect silver spoons while the poor get drafted and killed. The narrator refuses to be mistaken for someone who benefits from the system that sends other people's sons to die.","src":"haiku"},
"creedence-clearwater-revival~fortunate-son-live":{"haiku":"Class critique of war and privilege, rejecting inherited advantage and the violence it perpetuates.","opus":"Vietnam-era class fury still burning: the sons of privilege wave the flag and dodge the war they send working men to die in. A protest against a system where the powerful only ever demand more.","sonnet":"About class inequality in wartime America — the children of politicians, millionaires, and generals are exempt from sacrifice, while the working poor are conscripted and taxed; the narrator refuses to identify as one of the privileged and the song is a raw rejection of that system.","src":"haiku"},
"creedence-clearwater-revival~green-river":{"haiku":"Nostalgia for simpler rural life and innocent youth pervade a speaker who yearns to return home before the world's troubles.","opus":"A pull back to a remembered boyhood place of catfish, bullfrogs and barefoot girls dancing in the moonlight, a refuge to come home to when the world is smoldering. Fogerty roots the whole song in a specific childhood haunt as an offer of sanctuary.","sonnet":"A nostalgic return to a childhood place — catfish holes, rope swings in trees, dragonflies, flat rocks skipped across the water — with old Cody's advice ringing true: when you get lost in the smoldering world, come home to Green River.","src":"haiku"},
"creedence-clearwater-revival~long-as-i-can-see-the-light":{"opus":"A restless traveler reassuring the one he's leaving that he'll return; as long as a candle burns in the window to guide him home, his wandering won't cost him his way back.","sonnet":"A wanderer's reassurance to a loved one — he has an irresistible need to roam and drift, but promises he'll always find his way back home as long as a light is left burning for him.","src":"sonnet"},
"creedence-clearwater-revival~lookin-out-my-back-door":{"haiku":"Imagination transforms suburban mundanity into surreal spectacle where elephants and dinosaurs inhabit the domestic landscape in playful absurdity.","opus":"A whimsical parade of impossible sights conjured from a back porch, a mind unwinding into pure play after coming home. The delight is in refusing tomorrow's troubles and choosing wonder for a day.","sonnet":"A man returns home exhausted, sits on his porch, and lets his imagination run loose — the backyard fills with dancing dinosaurs, flying spoons, and carnival apparitions, a pure fantasy of carefree absurdism as an antidote to the world's troubles.","src":"haiku"},
"creedence-clearwater-revival~tombstone-shadow":{"haiku":"Curse and doom shadow every accomplishment, with superstition and bad fortune stalking the subject regardless of effort or remedy.","opus":"Bad luck as a literal shadow that swallows every bit of good news, made worse by a gypsy fortune-teller charging for doom. Fogerty plays superstition and dread for blues, the sense that misfortune is always at your back.","sonnet":"A man haunted by bad luck visits a gypsy fortune teller who confirms thirteen months of misfortune ahead and prescribes a long vacation, leaving the speaker trapped under the shadow of an omen he cannot outrun.","src":"haiku"},
"creedence-clearwater-revival~who-ll-stop-the-rain":{"haiku":"Perpetual storms of confusion and suffering pour down while seekers chase redemption that remains forever out of reach.","opus":"Endless rain as a metaphor for the era's confusion, from political grand schemes to Woodstock's cold crowds, all failing to bring shelter. Fogerty wonders who can end the downpour of history that no ideology has stopped.","sonnet":"A search for clarity in a world that has been shrouded in confusion since the speaker can remember — political programs and ideological promises come and go like golden chains, but the rain of uncertainty never stops and no one knows how to end it.","src":"haiku"},
"creedence-clearwater-revival~wrote-a-song-for-everyone":{"haiku":"Song becomes refuge when words fail, communicating truths that language cannot express while cycles of oppression and inequality continue unbroken.","opus":"Fogerty's ache at reaching millions with his songs while failing to reach the one person close to him. Wrapped in images of war, welfare and pharaohs spinning the truth, it's about the gap between public voice and private silence.","sonnet":"A songwriter confesses the inadequacy of art in the face of social injustice and personal failure — he can write a song for truth and for everyone, but he still can't bridge the distance to the people who need to hear it most.","src":"haiku"},
"creeper~cry-to-heaven":{"haiku":"A demonic seductress ensnares and consumes prey, offering eternal damnation disguised as desire.","opus":"Horror-punk theatre casting a lover as a bloodthirsty femme fatale, a cannibal empress of the damned whose embrace is fatal. Creeper revel in the campy fusion of sex, death and devotion.","sonnet":"A gothic seduction told through the figure of a \"cannibal baby\" — a predatory femme fatale with a bloodlust and a kiss you won't survive, handcuffed to the backseat and sovereign over every man she encounters. The chorus casts her power as diabolical liturgy: get on your knees, you're under the devil's spell.","src":"haiku"},
"creeper~cyanide":{"haiku":"Young lovers in darkness live dangerously and toxically, intoxicated by each other while acknowledging love's self-destructive nature.","opus":"British horror-punk act Creeper pour lovesick gothic melodrama into a teenage romance set among the macabre. Anchored in retro nostalgia, the beloved becomes a sweet toxin the narrator can't stop consuming, confessing that falling this hard feels perilously close to self-destruction.","sonnet":"A nocturnal romance between two people who exist outside ordinary moral life is cast in the imagery of poison and suicide. Love in this register is an addiction that destroys both parties, beautiful precisely because it is lethal and set unmistakably in a 1988 that never ended.","src":"haiku"},
"creeper~love-and-pain":{"haiku":"Love and suffering collapse into singular experience, with tender devotion inseparable from willingness to wound and destroy.","opus":"Gothic melodrama equating love with pain and violence, armed and Bible-clutching girls playing tender, doomed lovers. The narrator offers his heart up to be torn out, romance staged as beautiful suffering shared.","sonnet":"Love and pain are declared identical: the beloved is cold and dead-alive in her party dress, a gun trained at the heart of the speaker who confesses openly — if you want my heart, rip it from my chest — finding sorcery and black magic in the suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"crim3s~lost":{"opus":"A witch-house murk about grief and isolation, feeling everything while realizing there's no one on the other end. The repetition circles the same emptiness, healing and abandonment blurring into one.","sonnet":"An overwhelming sense of being unheard and alone — the speaker and someone close to them both fall without anyone stopping them, and the fundamental absence of support or understanding is the only thing that remains.","src":"sonnet"},
"crim3s~stay-ugly":{"haiku":"The song mourns permanence through memory and death. The beloved stays ugly—kept alive in memory forever, beyond time's fade, through shared sympathy.","opus":"A haunted vow to keep watching someone fade without accepting them as gone, refusing loss by holding them forever in a dream and a shared nightmare, the plea to stay ugly a strange demand to keep them real and unbeautiful in memory. Crim3s were a British witch-house and dark-electronic duo, and this pairs that murky, lo-fi genre haze with an unsettling meditation on grief and clinging.","sonnet":"A funeral-tender love song asking someone to stay exactly as they are — imperfect, ugly, real — rather than be laundered by memory or grief into something easier to mourn. The speaker watches a fading without calling it gone, holding on to the living version even as the nightmare of loss closes in around both of them.","src":"haiku"},
"crippled-black-phoenix~bonefire":{"haiku":"Class division and empathy's absence corrupt the soul; the speaker indicts privilege that ignores suffering and asks how those who look away sleep at night.","opus":"A furious indictment of the privileged who cheer the wealthy and despise the poor, demanding how anyone so comfortable and cruel can sleep with a conscience that already feels dead.","sonnet":"A working-class condemnation of political indifference to poverty: the speaker addresses a silver-spoon politician who ignores low wages, hungry children, and the vulnerable, asking how they sleep, how they justify the lies, and declaring them \"nothing but dead inside.\" The question of who is the better man — \"he who looks away or he who lends a helping hand\" — is the moral axis.","src":"haiku"},
"crocodiles~crybaby-demon":{"haiku":"Watching someone destroy themselves and being helpless to stop or truly reach them.","opus":"A junkie muse rendered as a fallen saint of garbage, gorgeous and untouchable in her high while the narrator lingers as one more creep she doesn't notice. It's addiction and worship tangled into the same doomed fixation.","sonnet":"A speaker watches a self-destructive woman — glamorously wrecked, perpetually high — who barely notices him as he passes; he is drawn to her saintly, trashy aura even as she crashes in her own oblivion.","src":"haiku"},
"crooked-royals~copacetic":{"haiku":"The narrator wrestles with isolation and apathy, locked in internal conflict and struggling to connect while needing emotional support.","opus":"A splintered mind peers at a barrier that keeps it apart from everyone, alienated and shut off, pleading for psychological rescue while insisting on a false serenity nobody buys. The New Zealand prog-metalcore band stitches together crowd-fear, trembling hands, and a longing to be held, wearing the title word as a thin mask over a dread that will not let sleep come.","sonnet":"A person trapped in dissociation and social paralysis maintains the facade of being fine — copacetic — while internally disconnecting from everyone around them. The need for connection and the terror of it coexist without resolution, the walls of consciousness becoming indistinguishable from the walls of a cell.","src":"haiku"},
"crooked-royals~glass-hands":{"haiku":"Execution and propaganda; fragile beauty masks enslavement and delusion imposed by those in power.","opus":"A takedown of a charismatic false prophet who peddles fake progress and plays victim while manipulating the faithful. The narrator vows to see through the delusion and bury it rather than offer sympathy.","sonnet":"A delusional figure — brainwashed for fake progress, drenched in delusion, speaking in tongues and leveraging faith — who will outlast everything, standing in the dust of others' bones as a catalyst. The question of how you dare play the victim is directed at someone whose voice sells violence while professing innocence.","src":"haiku"},
"crooks-uk~above-me":{"haiku":"Grief and detachment blur as the narrator anchors identity in memory of an absent figure; longing replaces presence as the organizing principle of existence.","opus":"A raw grief that blurs into guilt and dissociation, addressing someone lost while the narrator questions whether he's even alive. It circles a death he can't stop reliving, held down by the memory that stays above him.","sonnet":"A speaker haunted by a lost relationship — possibly a death — drifts between grief, bodily numbness, and the persistent presence of the absent person, questioning whether they are still truly alive and ultimately accepting that the loved one remains the very ground beneath them.","src":"haiku"},
"cross-examination~necroponics":{"haiku":"Weaponised biology produces consciousness-altering horror, where military ambition breeds uncontrollable terror and contagion.","opus":"A gleefully absurd horror-comedy narrative: a leaked lab experiment produces zombie-making weed that grows on corpses, and a curious janitor and a test subject named fish trigger the outbreak. Pure B-movie splatter told as a stoner cautionary tale.","sonnet":"A horror-comedy narrative about scientists who accidentally create necroponic weed — a plant that grows on corpses and turns anyone who smokes it into a zombie — follows the janitor who steals it, the van that kills him, and the increasingly frantic attempts to contain the outbreak.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~bermuda-locket":{"haiku":"Supernatural seduction promises transcendence through surrender to a cursed lover, binding dreams and demons in eternal union.","opus":"Chino Moreno's project frames seduction as occult rite — stepping into a spell where the ordinary rules invert and something dark breaks loose, with a vow to make a lover's fantasies real. Intimacy staged as a mystical, faintly threatening pact stretching across oceans and years.","sonnet":"A possessive figure counts years of waiting and now invites the beloved into a curse where demons burst and dreams reverse — promising to bring those dreams to life while demanding access to every signal, every fantasy, every part of the other person.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~bitches-brew":{"haiku":"Primal transformation during ecstatic ritual collapses human restraint into beast-form, with desire triggering metamorphosis.","opus":"A darkly sensual transformation myth, a lover's body shifting into something beastly under the moon while the narrator watches, claws out, wanting to feel it all. Desire and dread fuse as prayer itself starts to sound insane.","sonnet":"Under a full moon the inner beast surfaces in both figures — wolves watching, claws out, the dancer taunting the primal thing inside — until shape changes and breath is taken away; prayer feels insane but is all that is left after the fire.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~cadavre-exquis":{"haiku":"Intimacy becomes inescapable sacred tomb where bodies fuse and the speaker remains eternally drawn to a lover's grave and presence.","opus":"Grief as erotic haunting: the narrator can't leave a grave, drawn back forever to a lover he keeps trying and failing to escape. Chino Moreno's project fuses mourning and desire into one inescapable pull.","sonnet":"An obsessive erotic attachment that the speaker cannot leave, even knowing they should: they have fallen against the other's grave and cannot walk away from this sacred place, the days spent with skin sticking to skin a kind of willing entombment that makes the title's image — the surrealist exquisite corpse game — apt for a bond assembled in pieces and impossible to unmake.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~goodbye-horses":{"haiku":"Optimism persists against wisdom's warning that all things fall; the speaker refuses the cynicism of inevitability and chooses to fly beyond resignation.","opus":"A dialogue between a weary voice insisting everything decays into nothing and a defiant one refusing to accept it, then rising above the earthly world entirely in a kind of transcendence over mortality.","sonnet":"An exchange between a world-weary voice that insists \"all things pass into the night\" and a speaker who refuses to accept it, countering with \"I must say you're wrong\" before ultimately declaring themselves free — flying over horses, over everything that falls — in a final transcendence of the defeatism they were warned to accept.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~invisible-hand":{"haiku":"Market forces and late capitalism's invisible machinery controlling human life and extracting value.","opus":"Love and fate rendered as a rigged game whose rules keep shifting, the narrator sensing an unseen hand wrote it all. The recurring image is two people caught in the same chains, only pretending at choice.","sonnet":"A relationship rendered as a rigged game whose rules keep being reversed by someone else — the narrator realizes you wrote this game and that both players are in chains. \"It's only a test\" repeats like a mantra that never quite convinces, as readiness and closure keep slipping just out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~protection":{"haiku":"Plea for salvation through presence and belief, seeking rescue from emotional isolation.","opus":"A hushed, sensual prayer for rescue inside intimacy, the narrator lying awake begging to be saved by a lover whose touch feels like devotion. Faith and desire blurred into one plea for safety.","sonnet":"A vulnerable speaker lies awake hoping a lover will glide toward them and save them — kneeling, leading, teaching — the repeated \"save me\" less a prayer than a quiet surrender to whatever the other person is willing to offer.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~sensation":{"haiku":"Emerging from opacity toward connection, escaping isolation through collision of fates and shared love.","opus":"Two lovers finding refuge together in a suffocating, opaque world, climbing out of the dark toward a blurred horizon. The looming release of their bond feels as much like doom as deliverance.","sonnet":"About a love that exists in a private, sealed world but carries the awareness that it will eventually be expelled — the two people are warm inside their isolated space, climbing out of a dark town, but the refrain that \"love will be expelled\" frames the whole tenderness as something that can't last.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~sensation-suicideyear-remix":{"haiku":"Climbing from opacity toward light; two souls shielded together against toxic contamination.","opus":"Two lovers sealed safe in their cells against a toxic world, climbing toward a blurred horizon on the drone of each other's hearts. A fragile intimacy braced against the certainty that fate will eventually expel it.","sonnet":"Two people climbing out of an opaque town toward a blurred horizon — warm inside their cells, safe from the fumes — with the understanding that the collision of their fates will eventually expel their love; what makes it tender is that they are roaming together now, and the droning of each other's hearts is something holy.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~telepathy":{"haiku":"Domination through psychic connection traps the desired within the narrator's mind, playing games with willingness and coercion.","opus":"A quietly predatory seduction, the narrator reading a trembling, frozen person's inner life and offering a rigged game where letting you win is the lure. Control disguised as intimacy, the thrill lying in feeling someone break from inside.","sonnet":"A power dynamic framed as a game: the speaker reads the other's frozen inner gold through their stare, waits for them to break from inside, and controls the outcome by letting the other believe they are winning — \"come play my game, I'll let you win.\"","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~the-beginning-of-the-end":{"haiku":"Earth's fragile transparency mirrors beauty and vulnerability; humanity faces apocalypse unable or unwilling to learn from history, consigned to repeat destruction.","opus":"Reverence before a fragile beauty, kneeling in awe while grieving that humanity never learns from its past. Moreno turns intimate wonder into a plea to a wounded earth on the brink of something ending.","sonnet":"An address to someone — or the Earth itself — whose beauty is transparent as glass and who may never learn from the past. The narrator kneels before a light within the other person and feels it deeply, but the refrain \"this is the beginning of the end\" undercuts the tenderness with inevitability, as if awareness of beauty is also the recognition of its loss.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~the-epilogue":{"haiku":"Two people find themselves alone in a doomed end-time town where they drift toward inevitable fate, circling around certainty while trying to hold onto each other.","opus":"A doomed-lovers road song moving through an empty town at night, the narrator certain they're fated for ruin yet refusing to stop until he gets the only thing he wants—her; romance and apocalypse blur as they circle their own inevitable end.","sonnet":"Two people drifting through an emptied-out world at the end of a relationship — they know they are doomed but keep moving together anyway, and the narrator's only desire is to keep wanting the other person until the very end.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~vivien":{"haiku":"Drowning in silence becomes preferable to surface air as the speaker merges with the beloved in aquatic death. Water, wet, and dripping hearts dissolve boundaries between self and other.","opus":"A drowning imagined as consummation, two lovers pulled beneath the water into a timeless tomb where their fears finally end. The wet, clutching imagery makes death and desire indistinguishable, surrender rendered almost peaceful.","sonnet":"A submerged, nocturnal intimacy: Vivien rises from a lakebed, wet in the highway light, and pulls the speaker into drowning silence together — not as horror but as chosen surrender; 'I go down with you all the way, timeless in our tomb' frames the shared drowning as the deepest possible form of love.","src":"haiku"},
"crosses~vivien-away-remix":{"haiku":"Drowning together in silent depths merges death and intimacy, transforming tragedy into timeless unity beyond the shore.","opus":"A love styled as beautiful drowning, where merging with another means going under together willingly. Chino Moreno's gothic side turns surrender into an erotic, deathward embrace, the tomb framed as the only place two people stay timeless.","sonnet":"Two figures drown together — sinking into a lakebed, pulled in by highway light and soaking hands — and accept the silence of their shared tomb as timeless, the wave erasing all fear at the moment they go down as one.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfade~cold":{"haiku":"Apology cannot restore warmth; the speaker confesses they have always been cold and solitary, recognizing their nature ruined intimacy yet unable to change who they are.","opus":"A remorseful apology from someone who admits he was distant and dishonest, hiding his broken side while treating the person who steadied him coldly. It is about regret for pushing away the one who kept him going.","sonnet":"An apology for emotional unavailability: the speaker was always wrapped up in unwinnable things, never stopped to think of the other person, kept a screwed-up side locked away, and now that they're gone he understands she was the antidote — \"something strong like a drug\" — and he never meant to be so cold.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfade~the-deep-end":{"haiku":"Life built rigid and inflexible shatters under strain, leaving only pain and a descent into darkness when hope never arrives.","opus":"Post-grunge band Crossfade build the song around a life assembled too rigidly, unable to flex and therefore doomed to snap. Waiting on a hope that never comes, the narrator unravels through another empty weekend where nothing improves and life's promised greatness feels like a lie. Hanging on turns out harder than surrendering, a portrait of exhaustion with his own inflexibility.","sonnet":"A rigid self-construction — built to withstand everything by refusing to bend — shatters anyway, leaving only fragments and the slow recognition that holding on costs more than it offers. The temptation to simply give in accumulates across each chorus, making surrender feel less like defeat than exhausted honesty.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~catastrophe":{"haiku":"An inexorable rage and hatred awaken from within, neither suppressed nor defeated; the speaker accepts self-immolation and destruction as inevitable, defiant that their rising will outlast those who tried to contain them.","sonnet":"Something ancient and wrathful awakens inside the narrator in response to an antagonist who created the conditions for it. Attempts to suppress or destroy him only strengthen the force he has become — an indestructible rage that feeds on persecution and burns back harder each time.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~destroy-feat-ho99o9":{"haiku":"A person breaks free from systemic control and self-imposed chains to reclaim agency. The song is a manifesto for rejecting victimhood and dismantling oppressive structures.","opus":"A revolt against a broken system and internalized lies, the narrator refusing victimhood, choking the machinery that surrounds and sickens the city, arming faith and taking control of his fate with eyes finally open. Crossfaith are a Japanese electronicore band, and pairing here with American horrorcore duo Ho99o9 sharpens the defiance, fusing their electronic-metal charge with rap-driven, system-smashing rage.","sonnet":"A call to dismantle internalized chains alongside external systems — the speaker has spent time as a victim of a broken structure and now refuses that role, turning the destruction inward first before directing it outward. The chainsaw imagery and the city that breeds sickness suggest a fury that has been waiting long enough to know exactly where to aim.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~endorphin":{"haiku":"The speaker describes addiction to empty relief and guilt from inner disease, unable to locate their own soul or feel genuine connection.","opus":"A portrait of craving fills the speaker with an emptiness that never reaches full, guilt festering in the skin as the pain loops endless and numbness spreads. Crossfaith, the Japanese electronicore band fusing metal with rave synths, chant the title like a fix that never satisfies, the plea to be saved, loved and helped as the body goes numb turning addiction into a relentless, pulsing cycle of seeking a soul the speaker can no longer locate.","sonnet":"An addictive loop of emptiness and artificial stimulation — the endorphin hit that never fully satisfies — leaves a person numb, scarred, and directionless. The cry for someone to come and save them expresses a dependency that has grown beyond the self's capacity to address alone.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~faint-feat-masato":{"haiku":"The speaker feels invisible and ignored despite showing their pain and scars, unable to convince others that they exist or matter. Time cannot heal the damage from constant abandonment and dismissal.","opus":"A cry of desperate insecurity and demand to be acknowledged; the narrator, full of loneliness and self-doubt and unable to convince a dismissive person that he's real, refuses to be ignored and insists over and over that they turn around and finally hear him out.","sonnet":"A cover of Linkin Park's 'Faint' — a song about being dismissed and unseen by someone who matters to the narrator, building to furious insistence that they will not be ignored no matter how often they are looked through.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~freedom-feat-rou-reynolds":{"haiku":"A call to liberation from control and regulation. Pursued by overwhelming forces, the speaker and others break limitations and try to reclaim freedom while being hunted by machines and systems designed to contain them.","opus":"A rebellious anthem of breaking free from control and machines, urging a companion to stand together and find a home, following a figure who runs and is chased through a neglected, mechanized world.","sonnet":"The song splits between an anthem of collective liberation — breaking limitations and finding home within — and a dystopian verse about people pursued by machines in a neglected cityscape, framing resistance against mechanical or systemic control as a march of the soul.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~ghost-in-the-mirror-feat-caleb-shomo":{"haiku":"A person battles an internal enemy in the mirror—the false self imposed by external control. Self-recognition leads to rejection of manipulation and enslaved consciousness.","opus":"A confrontation with the self as adversary: the singer names the reflection staring back as his own ghost, refusing to be enslaved by the doubts and lies that have kept him running in circles, and the repeated rejections become an act of reclaiming control. Crossfaith are a Japanese electronicore band who fold metalcore aggression into rave synths, and the Caleb Shomo feature from Beartooth sharpens the anthem's fight-your-own-shadow catharsis.","sonnet":"Self-reckoning framed as a confrontation — the voice has been running from itself so long that it finally recognizes its own face as the enemy. The mirror reveals not an outside oppressor but the internal maze that trapped it, and the refusal to be controlled becomes a refusal to be defined by its own self-destructive patterns. Liberation comes not in escape but in the moment of recognition.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~god-speed":{"haiku":"Speed and obsession blur into spiritual elevation and ecstatic destruction; the speaker becomes god-like force burning through all resistance.","opus":"Reckless hedonistic abandon dressed as divinity, chasing speed, fire and freedom while surrendering to temptation and racing headlong without ever coming down.","sonnet":"About reckless, addictive speed and transcendence — the speaker is consumed by obsession, equating themselves to gods moving at the speed of fire, wanting to feel and fuck and burn rather than slow down or calm down.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~inside-the-flames":{"haiku":"Personal transformation requires enduring hardship; one must crawl through hell to find their true voice. The song affirms that only by facing darkness can you become yourself.","opus":"A rallying cry to quit hiding and walk straight into your own hell to discover who you are, framed as a fight you can only win against yourself. Crossfaith, the Osaka electronicore band, build it on a chanted rise-and-fall hook that turns private self-rescue into a festival-sized anthem meant for a roaring crowd.","sonnet":"A rallying cry aimed at someone paralyzed by their own past — the song insists that the only path to self-discovery runs directly through the hardest terrain, not around it. Hiding is named as useless; crawling through darkness and breaking chains are cast as requirements, not options. Authenticity is the destination: you can only become yourself by being no one else.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~make-a-move":{"haiku":"People must stop talking and act; evolution requires breaking through complacency and falsehood to create real change and genetic/social transformation.","opus":"A volatile call to arms against greedy, dishonest opportunists, rejecting empty talk in favor of decisive action and even evolutionary transformation. Crossfaith, the Osaka electronicore crew built for festival chaos, wrap the rallying cry in EDM-charged breakdowns, portraying a generation primed to erupt into deeds rather than recycle borrowed slogans.","sonnet":"Fed up with hollow sloganeering, people who take without contributing, and a scene built on performance rather than action, the narrator demands that everyone stop posturing and actually move. The generational impatience is framed as biological — evolve or become extinct, infect the nation with something real.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~monolith":{"haiku":"The song resists oppressive control through weaponized hate and past trauma. The narrator breaks away from cycles of violence, rejecting weapons and domination to reclaim agency.","opus":"A relationship soured into a lethal standoff, the narrator breaking free of a toxic past yet daring the other to aim at him and finish it, all while grappling with an emptiness coursing through him. Crossfaith push their Japanese metalcore into cold, industrial territory here, matching the violent imagery with clipped, mechanical menace.","sonnet":"A relationship corroded by hatred and control, seen from the side of someone trying to sever it. The voice describes being circled by weapons of malice, haunted by a connection that ran through body and soul, now wanting only to break away and destroy what was built from cruelty. The final image — a gun pointed and a trigger waiting — turns the rupture into an ultimatum with no clean exit.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~omen":{"haiku":"An inescapable warning appears on the wall; fate has been automated and cannot be undone despite attempts to dismiss it.","opus":"A near-wordless dread piece built on 'the writing's on the wall' as an inescapable omen, using the image of an unstoppable automation to suggest doom is already running its course and cannot be stopped.","sonnet":"A warning about an unavoidable sign of doom — the writing is on the wall, the machinery of fate is already running, and denial won't make it disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~redzone":{"opus":"Crossfaith turn obsessive pursuit into adrenaline overload, chasing a target lost inside their own head at hypersonic, redlined speed. The Japanese electronicore band fuse racing imagery with a refusal to fall, vowing to fly higher even in defeat. It is about relentless drive and fixation pushed past every safe limit into pure velocity.","sonnet":"A song about being consumed by obsession and running past all limits to reach something or someone who haunts you — the narrator exceeds safe speeds, emotional or physical, refusing to stop or fall because the target is lodged in their pain and won't release them.","src":"sonnet"},
"crossfaith~the-perfect-nightmare":{"haiku":"A perfect nightmare entity rises as savior and monster, claiming godhood and superiority, demanding prayer and worship while embodying power and darkness.","opus":"The Japanese electronicore band embody a gloating tormentor who calls himself the perfect nightmare, risen from nowhere and made flawless to boil the blood and break the sickness. Mocking prayers offered to a god now in the grave, the narrator claims strength beyond any deity, demanding worship as he probes how one loves oneself inside living dread.","sonnet":"A figure rises claiming divine perfection and superiority over any god that might offer salvation, demanding worship and declaring itself the only honest nightmare in a world full of false comfort. The inverted messiah uses religious language to announce a reign of darkness rather than light.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~wipeout":{"haiku":"A call to break free from the past, overthinking, and doubt to embrace fearless change and move toward a new future.","opus":"A headlong rush at tomorrow with no reverse gear, urging bold conviction and a leap into the unknown while shrugging off judgment and rigid blueprints. The song trades overthinking for the thrill of soaring toward whatever comes next.","sonnet":"A declaration of breaking free from the past and charging toward an unknown future without fear, rejecting overthinking and external judgment in favor of forward momentum.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~wipeout-album-mix":{"haiku":"Breaking free from overthinking past constraints, embracing fearless flight into the unknown future refusing judgment and limitations.","opus":"A maximalist electronicore rush toward the future, refusing overthinking and perfect plans in favor of breaking through and flying away. The Japanese band turn fearlessness into pure forward momentum.","sonnet":"A burst of forward momentum: the past is broken, the future is already in motion, and there is no time to overthink or let others judge — the only move is to fly away to something new, fearless, without looking back.","src":"haiku"},
"crossfaith~zero":{"haiku":"Erasure and void become the end state; the speaker disintegrates into nothingness and absence.","opus":"A return from the depths to wipe the slate clean, shedding weight and frustration to begin over. The refrain is defiant rebirth — nothing can grind the narrator down, and the tired routine gets shredded to make room for something new.","sonnet":"Collective resilience framed as a hard reset — break everything down, strip away frustration and burden, and rise back to zero so you can live again. The declaration \"we ain't never stuck in the frame\" and the repeated \"we rise\" frame the destruction as necessary clearing, not defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"crossing-i-s-dotting-t-s~would-you-speak":{"haiku":"Isolation deepens as the speaker's voice goes unheard; problems accumulate in silence and the question persists: would anyone listen if the unspeakable were finally uttered?","opus":"The ache of forever falling short of what others expect, and the desperate wondering whether anyone would stand up for you with nobody around to notice. The past keeps slipping off and the future refuses to hold, leaving gloom to settle over everything like a climate.","sonnet":"A person pushed against the wall by problems that come and go asks whether anyone would speak up when no one is listening — the question is not rhetorical but existential, directed at someone whose words never meant anything and whose cold distance reads as false prophecy.","src":"haiku"},
"crowbar~the-serpent-only-lies":{"haiku":"Good and evil wage eternal battle within; the narrator warns of deception and spiritual corruption while asserting the necessity of inner struggle and resistance.","opus":"Kirk Windstein's sludge as spiritual struggle, the serpent standing for deception and despair that must be conquered daily. It's a hard-won plea to choose the light and refuse to let your soul descend, faith as an act of survival.","sonnet":"A direct moral warning framed as spiritual combat: the speaker urges someone to reject the deceitful serpent — a force of darkness and temptation — and choose the light, reminding them that daily struggle against self-destruction is the only path to survival.","src":"haiku"},
"crown-the-empire~black-sheep":{"haiku":"Marginalization breeds strength while judgment proves powerless, with the outsider ascending through isolation and self-reliance.","opus":"The outcast's revenge fantasy, reframing rejection as fuel and vowing to return crowned by the very people who cast him out. Underneath the swagger is the old metalcore truth that karma, not the self, does the avenging.","sonnet":"Cast out and running with thieves, the speaker turns exclusion into fuel: \"the dirt's what I call home\" and worth in gold is declared not through belonging but through surviving every attempt to grind them down — cast me out, watch me come back as king.","src":"haiku"},
"crown-the-empire~dogma":{"haiku":"Institutionalized subjugation and hypocrisy drown the speaker in false faith; dogma demands obedience while the speaker watches congregants suffocate and refuses complicity, levitating away.","opus":"A drowning metaphor for religious control: the narrator lost at sea while a preacher pushes his head under, praying that god chooses who to save. The song rages at faith weaponized by the greedy who demand obedience.","sonnet":"A speaker drowning in religious and institutional dogma — saints turned to sinners, cycles of shame, shackles painted into brains — refuses the obedience demanded by preachers and leaders who bathe in greed, vowing to pull them crumbling to the bottom.","src":"haiku"},
"crown-the-empire~immortalize":{"haiku":"Mortality demands preservation; the speaker pleads for rescue from time's erasure, seeking transcendence through connection before dissolution.","opus":"Facing the certainty of death, it pleads for some way to outlast the body, wagering everything on the hope that consciousness or the soul survives into an afterlife.","sonnet":"Facing mortality and the terrifying brevity of presence — \"you're here then gone\" — the speaker reaches out for someone to take their hand and immortalize them, uncertain whether the afterlife holds more but desperate not to vanish without meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"crown-the-empire~paranoid":{"haiku":"Hypervigilance and isolation consume the narrator, cutting ties and hiding from threats both real and imagined.","opus":"Insomniac dread turned inward, where the enemy is a mind convinced everyone is against it. The real subject is isolation feeding on itself, the loneliness of fighting a threat that may only exist behind your own shoulder.","sonnet":"Paranoia hollows out daily life — lying awake talking to the ceiling, hiding in crowds, cutting ties, living in circles — as the speaker admits the isolation is self-imposed yet still feels like evidence that no one is on their side.","src":"haiku"},
"crown-the-empire~red-pills":{"haiku":"Someone silences inner noise and medication-induced numbness to stand upright through fire and fallen grace, determined not to repeat past pain. They transmute ashes and trauma into defiance and renewal.","opus":"After sinking into numbing sameness and self-medication, the speaker unplugs to change his course, resolving to stand up in the flames as he falls from grace, facing hell rather than running, and insisting this won't be the end.","sonnet":"The narrator describes hitting rock bottom through numbness and self-medication, then choosing to face the pain directly rather than flee it, with the fire and ashes becoming the means of clearing the past and starting over.","src":"haiku"},
"crows~closer-still":{"haiku":"Cruelty veiled in desire; molding another into a shape they can devour and possess.","opus":"A relationship of unequal devotion, the narrator begging to be deemed equal and molded into whatever's wanted, closeness bought with self-erasure. The dream of a place for them both is shadowed by veiled cruelty.","sonnet":"A push-pull dynamic built on unequal need — moulded into what you want, strapped to the horse, seeking approval — the paradox expressed as the closer I get the more equally distant we become, and the place for us that will never quite materialize hangs over the whole thing.","src":"haiku"},
"crucify-the-faith~as-the-world-ends":{"haiku":"Apocalypse becomes liberation as the speaker promises to shatter a corrupt world; salvation requires choice and the courage to break from the cycle of suffering.","opus":"An apocalyptic reckoning voiced by a wrathful power that frames annihilation as release: the suffering ends, fear is pulled from the heart, and destruction becomes a twisted form of salvation.","sonnet":"A dual-voiced apocalypse: one register threatens total destruction and eternal suffering for those who ignored their mortality, while the other offers release — pulling fear from hearts, granting salvation, freeing the listener \"as the world ends.\" The pivot hinges on which \"cast\" you belong to and whether you have chosen correctly before time runs out.","src":"haiku"},
"cruel-force~necromansy":{"haiku":"Satanic ritual and blood sacrifice constitute spiritual experience, inverting sacred language into ecstatic transgression.","opus":"Blackened thrash working the genre's ritual-Satanic playbook straight, all sacrifice, blood and spellcraft. It is less belief than costume, an adrenaline invocation that treats occult transgression as pure aesthetic charge.","sonnet":"A black metal ritual under moonlight: blood drunk, spells whispered, songs of Mayhem sung, bodies completing a circle of hell's sigils — a brief ceremony of blasphemy and acid-fuelled sorcery presented as pure satanic devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"cruel-queen~forevermore":{"haiku":"Patient endurance amid rust and decay teaches resignation, with repetition becoming the only constant and solace.","opus":"Waiting curdled into resignation, patience found only in decay and rust after faith and chasing both fail. The stance is bitter acceptance of a cycle that always returns to the same rot, company kept with misery itself.","sonnet":"Trapped in an eternal return — \"back again\" the only outcome — the speaker finds their patience calcifying in rot and rust, stops chasing and trying to roam, and accepts that misery is now their only company, having exhausted both god and faith.","src":"haiku"},
"cruel-queen~something-in-the-way":{"haiku":"Subsistence beneath the margins reveals indifference to suffering, with animals trapped and consumed without moral consequence.","opus":"A cover of Cobain's numb dispatch from under a bridge, poverty and dissociation flattened into a mantra. The power is in how little affect it carries, survival reduced to trapped animals and the drip of a leaking roof.","sonnet":"An outsider existence under a leaking tarp bridge, surviving on grass and ceiling drips while treating trapped animals as pets, is held in a state of numb detachment — whatever feeling blocks connection is just \"something in the way,\" unnamed and unmovable.","src":"haiku"},
"crx~on-edge":{"haiku":"The speaker waits in anxious anticipation and emotional entanglement, trapped in neurotic tension with another person they cannot escape.","opus":"Nick Valensi's project turns early-relationship nerves into jittery power-pop, the anticipation of infatuation curdling into raw anxiety. It's the feeling of waiting for something you want so badly that the wanting itself becomes unbearable.","sonnet":"The jittery, neurotic excitement of infatuation — anticipation, obsession, a tangled emotional mess that leaves the narrator waiting on edge, unable to escape the charged uncertainty of desire.","src":"haiku"},
"crying~premonitory-dream":{"haiku":"Someone crosses an uncertain bridge toward something unknown despite fear and doubt, choosing forward motion over stagnation.","opus":"The chiptune-rock band narrate an unsettling dream-quest, a figure leaning over water in search of a lost piece of himself before venturing alone down an unfamiliar path. On a lone bridge terror strikes as he weighs pressing on or turning back, until a wind strips the glasses from his face and staying static feels no safer than the fear ahead.","sonnet":"A suspended moment on a rope bridge becomes a meditation on the fear of committing to an unknown path versus the equal danger of retreating. Losing his glasses in the wind, the narrator finally sees that staying still in the name of safety is its own kind of fall.","src":"haiku"},
"cryptodira~dante-s-inspiration":{"haiku":"The speaker philosophizes about ego, shame, and the impossibility of happiness when bound to others' perception, asserting that hell is other people but that others are oneself.","opus":"A dense, book-heavy self-reckoning that borrows Sartre's idea that hell is others and warps it, the narrator admitting he needs attention to feel real yet loathes being observed, preferring a cage of his own making to any shared rescue. Cryptodira are a progressive metalcore trio, and this track drowns middle-class resentment in envy, mirrors and self-loathing.","sonnet":"A densely philosophical unraveling of ego, envy, and the impossibility of selfhood — the speaker diagnoses in themselves the same resentment they despise in others, needing to be seen but unable to bear being known. Rooted in the idea that the self is built from other people's gazes and words, it concludes that hell is not a place but the inescapable fact of being made of others while craving to be singular.","src":"haiku"},
"crypto~song-2":{"haiku":"Detachment masks fragility; the speaker admits to facility and dishonesty while needing connection they can neither accept nor refuse.","opus":"A cover of Blur's blast of restless, self-mocking abandon, all agitation and manic energy over needing someone without knowing why.","sonnet":"A chaotic rush of sensation — heavy metal, pins and needles, head checks by jumbo jet — delivers a jolt of pure energy with no stable narrative, the 'woo-hoo' refrain and self-described lying ease suggesting reckless exhilaration rather than any coherent emotional journey.","src":"haiku"},
"crystal-castles~affection": {"haiku":"Casual cruelty and detachment—crushing living things without consequence or guilt, existing without history or future in someone's collection.","opus":"Glass Candy-cold detachment from Alice Glass, casually crushing a moth as an image of numbed cruelty and rootlessness. Without a past there's nothing to disappoint, sleep offered inside someone else's collection like a specimen.","sonnet":"A cold, dissociative sketch — crushing a moth without feeling, disconnected from ancestry and past, desiring only to sleep inside someone else's contained world — the fragment ends with drowning in disease rather than natural water, suggesting emotional suffocation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A lover's tenderness gets voiced entirely through the vocabulary of predation: the singer pins a small winged thing between their fingers and kills it offhandedly, so devotion here is indistinguishable from casual destruction. Wiping away everything that came before frees the speaker from any inherited standard they could fall short of, and the beloved's world becomes a private zoo they long to lie down in permanently, a wish for shelter that reads equally as a wish to be caged. The closing couplet insists the real danger is interior rather than scenic, a drowning that happens slowly from inside the lungs instead of in open currents, the kind of end that creeps rather than crashes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"devotion voiced entirely through the vocabulary of predation","built":"the shelter wish reads equally as a wish to be caged"},
"crystal-castles~air-war": {"haiku":"Literary collage of sensory impressions, romanticized loss, and fading memory rendered as fragmented sound and pastoral imagery.","opus":"The lyric borrows wholesale from the Sirens episode of Ulysses, folding Joyce's overture of tavern sounds, coins, and longing into cold synth textures. Beneath the collage sits desire and departure, the ache of a lover's goodbye set against warring impulses. Crystal Castles turn literary fragment into pure atmosphere, meaning dissolved into sensation.","sonnet":"A found-text collage drawn almost verbatim from the 'Sirens' episode of James Joyce's Ulysses — it reproduces the novel's musical stream-of-consciousness prose about barmaids, brass instruments, and longing, functioning as pure sonic texture rather than original lyric meaning.","src":"haiku","fable":"There is no lyric here that Crystal Castles wrote — the words are a sample of Cathy Berberian reciting the overture to the Sirens episode of Joyce's Ulysses, the passage where bar-room sounds, jingling coins and a plucked garter are compressed into pure onomatopoeic music before the scene proper begins. That is the whole point of the choice: Joyce built language that had stopped meaning and started ringing, and Alice Glass and Ethan Kath treat that recital not as text to be understood but as another texture to be pitch-shifted, chopped and drowned in the synths, so that a woman's voice enunciating high modernism becomes indistinguishable from the machine grinding underneath it. The title reframes the fragments — tympanum, seahorn, the throb and the martial War! War! — into a hint of aerial bombardment, pulling Joyce's erotic music-hall reverie toward something colder and more mechanized. What the track is about, then, is that act of appropriation itself: the beauty and cruelty of taking a human artifact built from the sound of longing and feeding it, unread, into the same noise it was trying to transcend.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The voice is Cathy Berberian in Luciano Berio’s Thema (Omaggio a Joyce), the 1958–59 tape piece built on her reading of the Sirens chapter of Ulysses — high modernism entering the club through the sampler.","themes":["artifice & performance","war & battle"],"means":"beauty and cruelty of feeding a human artifact into the noise it sought to transcend","built":"Joyce's onomatopoeic overture pitch-shifted until high modernism becomes machine texture"},
"crystal-castles~alice-practice": {"haiku":"A speaker inhabits a nightmarish realm where sad eyes and dead things corrupt innocence. Scars heal but the speaker dies as Alice, trapped in a world where children play with corpses and crows tear at wings.","opus":"A raw, distorted accident of a track built from Alice Glass screaming over unmixed noise, its fragments of scarred healing and dead things evoking damage and menace more as texture than as any decipherable narrative.","sonnet":"A fractured, death-haunted monologue spoken from the perspective of someone — or something — existing between life and death, with imagery of decay, mutilation, and children warned away from the dead.","src":"haiku","fable":"A splintered horror-reel in the first person, sung by a narrator who answers to the name Alice and then narrates her own rot, so victim, corpse, and wound-up toy fuse into one voice glitching apart. The imagery moves in a single direction: healing wounds give way to scavenger birds worrying at feathers, a jammed mechanism that can no longer swivel a small skull, and a body reduced to lurching, staggering motion. By the band's own much-disputed origin story these vocals began as a microphone test — a practice take an engineer happened to record — and the words half-dissolve into the machine, and what you are really hearing is language being eaten by hardware while the persona greets you, decomposes across three verses, and signs off smaller than she arrived.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The mic-test origin story — a practice take an engineer accidentally recorded, released as the debut single — is the band’s own telling; Glass later disputed it as Kath’s myth-making.","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance"],"means":"a voice greets then decomposes smaller than it arrived","built":"a discard rehearsal take reframed as the finished collapse"},
"crystal-castles~baptism": {"haiku":"Forced religious indoctrination repeats lessons of obedience and love, but leaves the speaker unable to forgive the violation.","opus":"Alice Glass turns a sacrament into an image of being forcibly held under, faith imposed rather than chosen. The refusal to forgive makes it about coerced belief and the wound left by those who claimed to save you.","sonnet":"The song frames religious instruction and forgiveness as coercive rather than liberating — the head held underwater, the lessons forced and repeated, the scriptures misleading — and the refrain insists that whatever baptism was performed, the resentment it bred cannot simply be forgiven away.","src":"haiku","fable":"Crystal Castles turn the sacrament into an assault, staging drowning as devotion: a head forced under water becomes taking a breath for the Father, so that submission to authority and near-suffocation are made grammatically identical. The genius is the pivot from first to second person — the narrator submits, then rounds on you to declare this is your baptism, dragging the listener into the same rite. And the rite refuses its own promise: where baptism should wash guilt away, this one leaves you unable to forgive them, the cleansing inverted into a permanent grievance, and the scriptures that claim to teach love pronounced misleading, lessons that only repeat because they never take. Fittingly the song enacts its own loop, cycling the same chorus and refrain until absolution collapses into rote, a purification that purifies nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","surveillance & control"],"means":"a cleansing rite inverted into a permanent grievance","built":"first-to-second person pivot drags the listener into the same rite"},
"crystal-castles~birds": {"haiku":"Graphic bodily transgression and violent imagery—eyes pulled out, vomit into wombs, delicate surfaces lubricated—convey revulsion and violation.","opus":"A disorienting collage of bodily violation and decay, images of lace, being diagnosed a ghost, eyes pulled out and vomiting into a womb; there is no linear narrative, but the throughline is a nightmarish sense of the body being defiled and rendered corpse-like.","sonnet":"The lyrics are extremely sparse and fragmentary — images of diagnosis, pulling out eyes, a womb, and emerald waves — suggesting violation, erasure, or forced rebirth without enough coherent throughline to pin down a single meaning. NONE","src":"haiku","fable":"A body examined and pronounced already dead — layered, arranged, diagnosed a ghost — becomes the site of the album's ugliest ritual: a demand to appear and be sick back into the womb, repeated until it stops meaning and starts scouring. Crystal Castles built their second record on exactly this tension, Alice Glass's voice pushed through distortion so the human sound reads as machine damage, and here the refrain works less as a lyric than as a loop that eats itself, each repetition draining the words of sense while intensifying the compulsion. The verses supply the horror in fragments — a plea to blind someone and then apologize, waves that arrive to lubricate rather than to cleanse — so the imagery of birth and violation and glamour keeps collapsing into one another. What the song is finally about is that collapse: reproduction rewritten as regurgitation, the maternal turned into a place you are forced to return your own body to, over and over, until the return is the only thing left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"reproduction rewritten as forced regurgitation into origin","built":"refrain loops until words drain of sense while compulsion intensifies"},
"crystal-castles~black-panther": {"haiku":"A parent rejects their child as impossible and wrong, threatening abandonment; the relationship ruptures under the weight of the parent's revulsion.","opus":"Alice Glass channeling maternal horror and revulsion, a mother recoiling from her own offspring, denying the child came from her. Unsettling by design, it turns bodily connection into something to be discarded and thrown to the sea.","sonnet":"A deeply disturbing song spoken by a mother who cannot reconcile the child she produced with what that person has become, oscillating between denial, revulsion, and a violent fantasy of throwing the child back into the sea from which all life came.","src":"haiku","fable":"A mother and her offspring disown each other in the same breath — the child swearing she cannot have come from this body, the mother denying the birth outright even as the living child stands there disproving her. The chorus works like a reversal ritual: what emerged from her marrow and blood she demands be returned to her grip, as though a person could be pulled back into the flesh that formed them. That impossible middle age — no longer a child of hers, not yet anyone she could befriend — is what makes the creature unbearable, fitting no relation at all. The bridge finishes the fantasy as disposal masked as origin: the infant, still slick with what her own body secreted, is flung to the ocean and passed to the sea, un-made by the water it returns to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","violence & murder"],"means":"a mutual disowning that the child's existence disproves in real time","built":"the bridge reframes disposal as origin — un-making by return to water"},
"crystal-castles~celestica":{"opus":"Surrendering to oblivion and death, asking not to be found or saved, amid cryptic imagery of cleansing and letting go.","sonnet":"A desire to be left alone to fade away and disappear, rejecting comfort or rescue from an indifferent world.","src":"sonnet","fable":"Buried under Alice Glass's blown-out, over-processed vocal, this is a lullaby to disappearance: the imagery runs through submersion, hollowing and sterile cleansing until a body is scrubbed, breeze-hollowed, drowned on request, less a person than something being prepared and emptied out. The song's engine is the bridge's self-cancelling command — an invitation to be held immediately withdrawn, a plea to be roused paired with its refusal — which enacts an intimacy that flinches from the contact it asks for. Against the duo's abrasive default the track's prettiness is the point: sweetness laid over eating-your-young dread, so the beckoning toward nowhere reads as comfort and annihilation offered in the same tender breath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"annihilation and comfort offered in a single tender breath","built":"the bridge's invitation withdrawn the moment it is issued","fnote":"Kath took the name from the Toronto electronics plant and its grim shop legend — a worker said to have gone into a vat of hot plastic that kept right on manufacturing."},
"crystal-castles~celestica-thurston-moore-rmx":{"haiku":"Descent into sequence and consumption; silence masks horror as modesty shields the void.","opus":"An eerie, clinical meditation on being scrubbed, hollowed, and stripped of self, delivered as a plea to be left lost if lost. Alice Glass's fragility, filtered through Thurston Moore, turns modesty and erasure into something haunting.","sonnet":"A surrender to dissolution: the speaker invites being lost, asks not to be found, and describes a body hollowed out and cleaned — \"has your body been hollowed by the breeze?\" — while following someone into a carefully woven nowhere.","src":"haiku"},
"crystal-castles~char": {"haiku":"Captivity and abuse where the victim is hidden and silenced. Punishment, control, and the endless cycle of tears define the relationship.","opus":"Alice Glass buries an unsettling account of control and abuse under glitched electronics: a captor's cooing over someone kept hidden, punished, walked on a leash and reduced to endless crying. The tenderness is the menace, ownership disguised as care.","sonnet":"A deeply unsettling song about someone hidden, controlled, punished, and cried over — the protagonist seems to be sheltering a person who is harmed, leashed, and perpetually weeping, with the song hovering between protection and captivity in a way that is deliberately ambiguous and disturbing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A caretaker's voice curdles into a captor's, promising to soothe a sickened body one moment and to discipline it with a leash the next, its tenderness indistinguishable from ownership. The imagery keeps the beloved miniaturized and possessable, small enough to be sealed in jewelry, walked like a pet, and coached to deny consent afterward, so that protection and predation fold into one gesture. When the second half dissolves into the flat repetition that all the captive ever does is weep, that loop stops narrating and begins enacting the airless helplessness the words only described before.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"protection and predation folding into one possessive gesture","built":"the loop of the captive only weeping stops narrating and begins enacting"},
"crystal-castles~child-i-will-hurt-you": {"haiku":"An abuser grooms children with false care and gentleness to break their defenses and perpetrate harm.","opus":"A harrowing portrait of predatory grooming, children confined and neglected, then trained in tenderness so their guard drops. The Canadian duo dress calculated abuse as kindness, rationalized as serving some higher purpose, its true nature grasped only when it is far too late.","sonnet":"Deeply unsettling in its sparseness — children are isolated, taught submission through false comfort, their defences lowered before something unnamed is done to them and covered up as being for the greater good.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a lullaby in the voice of a predator, and its horror is that every gesture it offers a child is real tenderness bent toward capture: dressing scraped knees, teaching the calm that teaches trust, all of it engineering an unguarded moment. The title states the outcome the tenderness exists to reach, so each caretaking line reads twice at once, care and grooming inseparable, with the cover-up rationalized as service to something larger and its truth surfacing only afterward, too late. Against that developing menace the chorus chants a single coined word, one letter shy of the term for a raid, four times unchanged, a droning incantation whose pledge of endless incursion folds the abuse into ritual repeated without end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance","faith & the occult"],"means":"caretaking lines that each read twice as capture","built":"the chorus word coined one letter short of the term for a raid"},
"crystal-castles~chloroform": {"haiku":"The speaker describes sedation and remorse, depicting harm done to unsuspecting people who are subdued while the perpetrator hides behind a mask of false virtue.","opus":"A fragmented meditation on sedation and manufactured remorse, where chloroform stands for whatever numbs the wounded into submission and false saintliness, offered with a mocking there, there to the sad. Crystal Castles, the Toronto electronic duo, specialize in exactly this kind of glitchy, half-buried menace, Alice Glass's vocals dissolved into the distortion so the cruelty registers as texture more than statement.","sonnet":"A cold, sparse portrait of submission and compliance manufactured through emotional sedation — something or someone renders people subdued and unaware, harvesting their wounds while performing care. The repeated aside asking if the subdued are sad reads as hollow theater, a predator's comfort offered to those who cannot resist.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about coercion dressed as consolation. The chloroform is always theirs — an anaesthetic administered by some unnamed collective authority, and Crystal Castles keep the abuser offstage precisely so the violence reads as ambient, institutional, the kind that subdues a person and then mourns the wound it made. The chorus is the sedative itself talking: it ambushes those who don't see it coming, then leans in with a mock-tender there, there, the pat of comfort weaponized into a means of keeping the victim quiet. Crystal Castles' writing across the catalog circles this — power that betrays intimacy — and the final verse names the endgame, dissolving the singular self into the swarm, the private person absorbed into the mass that pretends to soothe her. The saints of the third verse are the tell: the ones who portray virtue while they wear away at you are the same hands holding the cloth.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"ambient institutional sedation that mourns the wound it made","built":"the saints of the third verse are the same hands holding the cloth"},
"crystal-castles~concrete": {"fable":"The song stages surrender as liturgy: creased trousers get smoothed flat, sieges are answered, the gentle are waved through a checkpoint, and each command arrives already decided before anyone alive could refuse it. Its cruelty hides in the pre-chorus doctrine that grief has been retired because nothing ever sets or hardens, no outcome trustworthy enough to hold its shape long enough that a person could mourn it. The chorus then drags that abstraction into matter, a body bedded on frozen slush, tangled in marsh grass, and finally paved flat, so the title stops meaning fixed truth and becomes the mix poured to seal over what got pulled from the wrecked and the disfigured.","fableDeep":"The title's pun does the work: concrete as certainty ('nothing is concrete' — no belief holds) and concrete as material ('lay the concrete' — pave it over, entomb it). Between those two meanings sits a bureaucracy of harm described in the passive voice — demands besieged, excess contained, extraction from the maimed — violence that sounds like procedure precisely because no one in the song is doing it; it is simply carried out. The refrain makes the loss of certainty the anesthetic: deny the preconceived, accept the preordained, and grief becomes unnecessary. What gets laid to rest under the concrete is the ability to object.","opus":"Cryptic imagery of siege, submission and deceit, insisting that nothing is solid or certain and grief has been abandoned.","sonnet":"The dismantling of fixed belief and certainty, leaving nothing permanent or solid in its place.","src":"sonnet","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"grief retired on doctrine that nothing ever hardens enough to mourn","built":"the title shifts from fixed truth to the mix poured over what was wrecked"},
"crystal-castles~courtship-dating": {"haiku":"A twisted relationship involves stove burns, taxidermy-like posed bodies, and ritualistic procreation; disease, pain, and necrophilic imagery permeate 'courtship.'","opus":"Alice Glass sets teenage attraction to imagery of butchery and taxidermy, making desire indistinguishable from consumption and preservation. Under the blown-out synths it is a queasy portrait of wanting someone so badly you would rather stuff and keep them than let them change or age.","sonnet":"A grotesque sequence of images that reads like a serial killer's domestic horror — self-harm, bodies stuffed and posed with rolling eyes, people culled and preserved, the language of courtship twisted into taxonomy of violence and death.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric renders young intimacy as taxidermy, where the beloved is preserved rather than loved, culled like the deer that wanders into a cage and mounted with glass eyes so decay finally becomes impossible. The chorus completes the horror by making display into ritual, the burned hands offered as proof while the partner is compelled to join, so desire and reproduction collapse into a mechanical process worked on a body that has stopped being a person. What resists reading is the refrain itself, repeated until it hollows out, until the compulsion to make the other take part becomes the entire meaning, the track ending on that single command alone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Glass has said it plainly: the song is about human taxidermy — preserving a lover’s beauty the way you would an animal’s.","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"desire preserved by halting decay rather than accepting it","built":"the chorus ends on a single compulsory command repeated until it hollows out"},
"crystal-castles~crimewave": {"haiku":"Violence and threats press soft bodies into rough hands in a repeating cycle. The song depicts assault as systematic, lit by surveillance and enacted through dark threat.","opus":"The electronic duo build almost the entire track from a single menacing image looped over and over, conjuring coercion and danger through sheer hypnotic repetition rather than any story. The effect is pure atmosphere, the claustrophobic charge of a predatory, violent encounter rendered as a suffocating trance.","sonnet":"The same two lines repeat in a relentless loop: eyes locked on threatening whispers from dark mouths, soft skin pressed against rough hands under harsh lights — a scene of coercive physical encounter rendered hypnotic through pure repetition.","src":"haiku","fable":"A single menacing couplet — a watchful stare, a mouth read as a warning, a body shoved under strobe into someone's grip — loops unchanged across chorus and outro until the words stop parsing and become texture. That refusal to develop is the reading: two noise-leaning acts feed a nightclub predation scene through so much blown-out distortion that the human specifics dissolve, dread and pleasure rendered indistinguishable at that volume. What survives the wash is not a narrative but a condition, the meeting of an ecstatic surface with a threatening undertow, held in permanent tension that the repetition refuses to resolve.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"A co-billed rework — Crystal Castles vs HEALTH, the LA band’s vocals kept over Kath’s rebuild; both bands ship their own version.","themes":["violence & murder","night & the city","love & desire"],"means":"dread and pleasure held in permanent unresolved tension","built":"a two-line predation scene looped until the human specifics dissolve into texture"},
"crystal-castles~deicide": {"haiku":"A commentary on false kindness used to manipulate and sedate the vulnerable, critiquing the death of ethics and the degradation of human progress.","opus":"A cold indictment of kindness weaponized as sedation, building toward the death of a god who will only hear the prayers of the used and confused too late for a sympathy bouquet. Crystal Castles return to chloroform imagery as shorthand for willing self-numbing, the Toronto duo lacing their corroded synth-pop with a nihilism that treats faith and manipulation as the same expired transaction.","sonnet":"Social control dressed as kindness — the song traces how people are kept confused and compliant by those who benefit from their docility, right up to a final day when even divine authority fails. The ethical framework has dissolved, and what remains is ritual mourning for a system that was already rotten, with those who were used still not understanding how.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song narrates a willed surrender to a manipulator who dresses cruelty as tenderness, drugging the victim with false warmth while she stays deliberately bewildered so her handler can keep enjoying the sport. Edith Frances sings from inside the trap in a flat, complicit register, reciting the rules that bind her — naming the craving for the poison alongside the refusal to admit that craving — so that submission and captivity collapse into a single act. The recurring hook swaps the human abuser for a godhead marked for killing, one who still promises to answer prayer even as any moral floor beneath the bond rots away. That fusion is the whole horror: the murdered deity and the person left to mourn it are one ruined self, and the writing withholds the lesson she keeps failing to learn, holding her in the loop rather than releasing her from it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"This is the song caught mid-build: a 2015 Edith Frances-era single that is the demo of Their Kindness Is Charade, the same words and vocal take that would close Amnesty (I) a year later, heard at an earlier stage.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control","faith & the occult"],"means":"submission and captivity collapsed into a single act","built":"the murdered deity and the mourning self fused into one ruined figure"},
"crystal-castles~doe-deer": {"haiku":"The repetition of 'deathray' and 'behind you' creates an atmosphere of relentless menace and pursuit—an unseen threat closing in from behind.","opus":"Barely words at all, just menace as texture, the repeated threats hammered into pure adrenaline and paranoia. Alice Glass turns dread into something you feel in the body rather than parse.","sonnet":"The lyrics consist entirely of the repeated words \"deathray\" and \"behind you\" with no further content, conveying a vague sense of threat or menace but offering no developed subject or narrative beyond that atmosphere.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built from two words repeated to exhaustion, so the read has to be about what the repetition does rather than what it says. Crystal Castles at their most abrasive treat the voice as an instrument to be shredded — Alice Glass's chant of a killing beam and then a warning that something is behind you turns menace into pure rhythmic pulse, the words emptied of narrative until only their threat-charge remains. The pun in the title (a female deer, the animal as prey) reframes the loop as a hunt: the listener is the doe, and the vocal isn't singing to you so much as circling you. Meaning lives in that pressure, not in a story — the terror is that there is nothing behind the words except the demand to keep looking behind you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"menace emptied of narrative until only its threat-charge pulses","built":"the title's prey pun reframes the vocal loop as a circling hunt"},
"crystal-castles~empathy": {"haiku":"The song critiques conformist control where individuals must earn acceptance through symmetry and empathy, becoming conduits for systemic manipulation and passive consumption. It describes the erasure of individuality through manufactured consensus.","opus":"A cryptic, disquieting piece about conditioned feeling and conformity, hinting that people must fall into line to deserve compassion, are trained to forget and to swallow whatever they are given, and pass through an engineered transformation as cities crumble around them.","sonnet":"A cryptic portrait of conformity and social conditioning — the body is reshaped and hollowed out to meet external standards of symmetry, people are force-fed preferences and encouraged to forget individuality, all while cities fall and a chrysalis of self-controlled consciousness reaches its final, collapsing form.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about compassion recast as a performance you must audition for, staged inside a body being remade to specification: bones surfacing, skin shed, glands hollowed out until the self is a chrysalis under someone else's control. The chorus couples that surrender to symmetry, insisting tenderness be qualified for like a wage rather than simply extended, so the feeling becomes a metric the singer is graded against and can fail. The outro quietly reverses the ledger, sliding from what you owe them to what is owed us, which lets the abused voice slip into the seat of the withholding authority and exposes the demand as a cycle, not a single cruelty. Against all that conditioning, the post-chorus of a skyline caving inward is the only unmetered sensation in the track, closer to catastrophe than reward.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"compassion recast as a wage the self must audition to earn","built":"outro pronoun shift from what you owe them to what is owed us exposes the cycle"},
"crystal-castles~enth": {"haiku":"Abstract language describes attempts to evade and avoid, with surfaces degrading and numbness fading. The song resists clear meaning, dwelling instead on disintegration and suppression of authentic expression.","opus":"Cryptic, clinical fragments about eluding, evading, and maintaining a fraying appearance, with images of anesthetic and dilating skin suggesting concealment breaking down.","sonnet":"The terse, clinical imagery — fraying uniform, appearances maintained, an anesthetic evaporating and skin dilating — sketches a persona under pressure to conform or suppress itself, eluding scrutiny while the outer structure begins to break down.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is a shrinking vocabulary of self-erasure, its verbs all strategies for going unseen, sung as clipped imperatives that read like operating instructions for a body trying to become invisible. Fitting for a Crystal Castles record made after Alice Glass departed, the vocal arrives processed past legibility, a person deliberately smearing the edges of their own outline until identification fails. The middle collapse strips each phrase down word by word until only the coming-apart survives, so the wearing-thin of a mask gets enacted in real time rather than merely reported. When numbness lifts midway and sensation floods back through nerves that had been held quiet, that return registers as the punishment, the price extracted for maintaining a surface no one was meant to read.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"sensation returning midway registers as the punishment not the relief","built":"phrases stripped word by word until the coming-apart is enacted in real time"},
"crystal-castles~fainting-spells": {"haiku":"The song uses ambiguous, fragmented imagery of taste, evaporation, and expectation, suggesting loss of memory and condensation into nothingness. It creates mood through abstraction rather than narrative.","opus":"Numbness offered as seduction: forgetting made to taste like medicine, a couple evaporating without ever condensing into anything real. Heaven stays a distant expectation while the present dissolves, intimacy that erases rather than holds.","sonnet":"A brief, hypnotic loop about being made to forget through physical sensation — the promise of oblivion is offered like medicine, but the expectation of heaven feels more like a coping mechanism than a genuine belief.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction that is also an anesthetic. Every promise Alice Glass makes is double-edged: the pleasure she offers is described in the same breath as a dose of medicine, the reward that tastes of the thing you swallow to be numbed, so tenderness and sedation become indistinguishable — this is Crystal Castles at their most disquietingly intimate, the abrasion of the first record traded for a lullaby that soothes you into disappearance. The chorus turns that erasure physical: two people evaporating without ever having condensed, vanishing without first having cooled into anything solid, dissolved before they could exist. And against that vanishing she sets the cruelest line — heaven remains the only thing expected, a faith held onto by someone being talked gently out of being, so the comfort reads as an overdose dressed as salvation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"tenderness and sedation made indistinguishable by the seducer","built":"erasure framed as salvation, overdose dressed as comfort"},
"crystal-castles~femen": {"haiku":"A reversed sample expressing alienation and desire to be entertained as a form of escapism.","src":"haiku","fable":"Buried here is the most famous rock anthem of a generation, run backwards until its jaded chorus and its shrug of feeling dumb and infectious dissolve into unreadable syllables the ear registers only as low, encroaching menace. The gesture is an argument: Cobain's disaffection was already a commodity by the time it topped charts, and reversing it strips the words of sense while keeping their pull, exposing the anthem as marketable posture worn thin. Naming that inversion after the feminist protest movement reframes the borrowed rage as something to be redirected rather than sold, a signal flare pulled from wreckage and aimed somewhere new. What survives the process is not a song but its residue, a smeared negative of teen rebellion that accuses the original of hollowness even while it feeds on the leftover charge.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The ghost buried under the noise is a girls' choir — the Scala & Kolacny Brothers covering Smells Like Teen Spirit — spun backwards, which is how Nirvana end up in the songwriting credits.","themes":["artifice & performance","politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"dismantling an anthem to expose disaffection as marketable posture","built":"Nevermind reversed until its pull survives but its words become menace"},
"crystal-castles~fleece": {"haiku":"A speaker praises ambiguity and the shedding of false expectation; they love dishonesty and revision of the past as a form of freedom.","opus":"Ethan Kath and Alice Glass circling manipulation and control dressed as affection, prizing a lover's vagueness because it can be reshaped. Cold, clinical desire that treats a person as something to revise and set on display.","sonnet":"A predatory or possessive figure who thrives on ambiguity, stripping away false expectations and placing a woman on a mantelpiece — treating her as an object of worship or display while claiming to love what he can't pin down.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages seduction as a demand for manufacture: the narrator wants a lie built to order and calls surrender to it a delight, wiring desire to fabrication rather than to anything true. What the voice adores is the beloved's refusal to resolve, her power to stay unfixed and mean two things at once, and that unfixedness bleeds into the politics, where liberty is first equated with candor and then, in the same breath, with menace. The recurring command to rewrite what is ancient runs under everything as the deeper appetite, the wish to reach back and edit origin itself. It closes by trapping the woman, lifted out of her sanctuary and set down as an ornament on a shelf, the placement repeated until it hardens into taxidermy, the fleece of the title a pelt kept for display. That looping order to overwrite the old is the tell, a voice hungry for control over memory while confessing it can only redecorate the past.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"desire wired to fabrication rather than anything true","built":"the looping command to overwrite the ancient betrays appetite for control over memory"},
"crystal-castles~frail": {"haiku":"Fragility and physical deterioration, questioning the difference between weakness and resilience in a harsh world.","opus":"An oblique meditation on weakness and withheld mercy, refusing to comfort someone who defrays and ends up prey, questioning what fragility even means through images of veils, withered sinew and sutures worn like ornament. It is cold and clinical about vulnerability. Crystal Castles, the Canadian electronic duo, built their reputation on exactly this friction, glitchy abrasive production wrapped around Alice Glass's ghostly, buried menace.","sonnet":"A cold, clinical examination of fragility and the systems that enforce it — withholding blessing, refusing to soothe, watching someone diminish. The image of sutures as accessories suggests a body broken and decoratively patched, displayed rather than healed. Frailty here is something imposed as much as experienced.","src":"haiku","fable":"Frail turns Crystal Castles' late-period cruelty inward, staging fragility as an accusation rather than a confession — the title word is never owned, only interrogated, hurled back as a question the song refuses to answer. On Amnesty (I), the first record with Edith Frances after Alice Glass's departure, the vocal is deliberately withheld and reprocessed, and the chorus enacts that withholding: it loops the same challenge until the word itself wears down, so the demand to name a state becomes indistinguishable from denying it comfort. The two verses set the mechanism against a body — one a chilly refusal to feed the fire of someone's admiration, the other a garment stitched onto a withered frame where the suture is worn as ornament, care and damage fused into the same gesture. What holds it together is that glow through the veil: something still lit behind the covering, luminous precisely because it is failing, which is the only version of tenderness the song will permit. The refusal to settle the question is the reading — Frail withholds mercy on purpose and calls the withholding a kind of honesty.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"withholding mercy on purpose and calling the withholding honesty","built":"the challenge looped until the word wears down and denies its own comfort"},
"crystal-castles~good-time": {"haiku":"Good times perpetually roll but must escape and flee—pleasure and fugitive anxiety coexist in repetition.","opus":"A single hypnotic line looped to the point of obsession, insisting that the pleasure keeps flowing yet must be fled at once. The relentless repetition is itself the meaning, a fleeting high sensed as already vanishing, joy that has to be abandoned even as it continues.","sonnet":"A loop of pleasures that can't be held — good times keep rolling but they have to escape now — the relentless repetition enacting the way euphoria slips away the moment you try to grasp it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A single couplet about pleasure that cannot be held onto, looped past the point of comfort until the promise of fun curdles into something anxious, compulsive, and faintly menacing under Alice Glass's shredded delivery. The words never develop; the only movement is a late line clipped down to its second half, the sentiment eroding rather than resolving, so the track enacts the very leakage it describes. Its meaning lives in that mechanical repetition: joy stated as a mantra you no longer believe, a celebration circling in on itself with the exits already flagged.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"joy stated as a mantra no longer believed by the singer","built":"the late line erodes to its own second half rather than resolving"},
"crystal-castles~insulin": {"haiku":"A nightmarish cascade of bodily corruption and abuse is inflicted on helpless targets, with imagery of contamination and harm to the vulnerable. The refrain emphasizes systematic degradation of the defenseless.","opus":"Through disturbing, clinical body imagery of blood, embryos, stem cells and violated newborns, the song evokes bodily invasion, degradation and the corruption of innocence and generations.","sonnet":"Disturbing imagery of bodily violation — perfume in the blood, nails through gloves, bruised embryos, lungs filled with tar — sketches a world of exploitation where the vulnerable, including the unborn, are laid out in rows and consumed.","src":"haiku","fable":"A body kept alive by dosing sings tenderly of harvesting it: hair scraped from its roots, a skeleton sold off as trophy ivory, the unborn bruised and arranged in tidy rows like counted inventory. The nurturing voice is also the predator, offering to sleep so you can rest while vowing your child will be seized, its lungs packed with residue, its youngest tissue forced to mature early on command. Insulin is the frame: the injected dose that sustains a dependent organism precisely so it stays viable to be stripped, care reimagined as the delivery mechanism by which maintenance and predation share one needle. Fragrance seeping through the veins and fingernails pushing out past a protective glove render tending and defilement a single substance, so the lullaby cadence administers each atrocity as though it were ordinary upkeep owed to a patient.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control","shame & repression"],"means":"maintenance and predation sharing one delivery mechanism","built":"the injected dose frames tender tending and defilement as one substance"},
"crystal-castles~intimate": {"haiku":"A series of brief intimate encounters that feel transformative in the moment, cycling through connection and dissolution without permanence.","opus":"It sketches cold, revolving-door romance, gathering up affections and treating each fresh partner as merely one more stopover. The brief coupling gets compared to slumber and to entering life then dissolving back into the ground.","sonnet":"A portrait of serial, emotionally detached intimacy — moving from person to person, keeping the encounters secret, each new connection briefly filling a void before yielding to the feeling of dissolution: like falling asleep, like being born, like returning to soil.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song treats serial intimacy as a survival tactic, logging each partner like a temporary shelter rather than a person and tallying encounters with the flat efficiency of someone counting rooms slept in while on the run. Its redemption is the coda, where sleeping through strangers gets rhymed with dying and being reborn, so the endless swapping of faces stops sounding like appetite and starts sounding like a body dissolving downward into something older and more fragile than any single attachment. Glass delivers it deadpan over Ethan Kath's decaying synths, and that refusal to grieve is the argument: the numbness she performs is the anesthesia the lyric is quietly diagnosing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire","death & grief"],"means":"serial intimacy logged as temporary shelter by a body on the run","built":"deadpan delivery over decaying synths performing the anesthesia the lyric diagnoses"},
"crystal-castles~kept": {"haiku":"Pledges unwavering commitment to keeping promises despite the chaos and strangeness that life brings.","opus":"Built entirely from a single repeated refrain, the song hovers on the idea of stranger things approaching and a vow to keep certain promises, staying just out of the way.","sonnet":"A minimal, looping assertion that strange, unexpected things lie ahead while promises are kept regardless — the repetition itself creates a sense of waiting in place, held by obligation, with no further narrative offered.","src":"haiku","fable":"Built on a chopped, looped sample of Victoria Legrand's voice lifted from Beach House recordings, Kept turns an entire song into a single stalled loop, and the reading lives in that refusal to move: three lines, repeated without variation until arrival and departure blur into the same gesture. The promise being kept is the loop itself — a vow of constancy that, restated to exhaustion, curdles from reassurance into something closer to threat or captivity, the title's double sense of a promise honored and a person held. On an album built partly from the wreckage of Alice Glass's departure, Legrand's disembodied, endlessly circling voice reads less as devotion than as the sound of being unable to let go, the mantra of someone who mistakes an unbroken loop for love.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"That warm, aching voice is Victoria Legrand of Beach House, but she never stepped into the room: Ethan Kath chopped and looped samples of two Bloom tracks, Other People and New Year, into a new song, which is why Beach House hold the writing credit. A physical-editions-only bonus track.","themes":["surveillance & control","love & desire"],"means":"constancy restated to exhaustion curdles from reassurance into captivity","built":"looped sample of Legrand's voice turns the unbroken loop into the vow itself"},
"crystal-castles~kerosene":{"haiku":"Explores false protection and toxic comfort, where harmful substances and deceptive care are used to manage suffering. The song warns that salvation offered may destroy rather than heal.","opus":"An unsettling song about corrupted care and control, where charity, purity, and protection are twisted into instruments of harm. Kerosene and cleansing imagery run through promises to protect and heal wounds, blurring the line between comfort and destruction.","sonnet":"A dark meditation on institutional religion and the way comfort, language, and identity are stripped away — the imagery of washing with kerosene to cleanse impurity, and protecting someone from what has already been witnessed, suggests a corrosive kind of care that does more damage than good.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built on the gap between the two solvents it names: the refrain promises saline, the antiseptic rinse of a caretaker tending a wound, while the verses keep reaching instead for kerosene, purification by fire dressed up as tenderness. The verse voice speaks the vocabulary of protection and charity while draining it of meaning from inside, comfort dispensed to keep people docile, deceit sold as instruction, a tongue as sterile and machine-cold as ones and zeroes, until the offer to scour away the taint and the offer to burn it become the same sentence. Alice Glass murmurs the guardian's reassurances, the hush over a crib, the insistence that nothing is the matter, the vow to shield you from everything her own eyes have witnessed, in a register so soothing it curdles, so the promise to protect you sounds exactly like the thing you need protecting from. Gentleness and abuse have fused; underneath, the plea to be drowned in kerosene admits the singer wants that same fire turned on herself, no longer sure which side of the wound she is on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the guardian's reassurances sounding exactly like the thing you need protecting from","built":"saline promised in the refrain while the verses reach for kerosene instead"},
"crystal-castles~knights": {"haiku":"AIDS fear and mortality suffuse the repeated question, with angels dying alongside the unfinished phrase; mortality haunts those affected by the virus.","opus":"Ethan Kath and Alice Glass turn a chopped, decaying vocal into pure dread, the human voice fed through machines until meaning corrodes. It is less a song than a haunted transmission about mortality glimpsed through static.","sonnet":"A repetitive, fragmented chant around the phrase 'is this the HIV / angels die with' — the lyrical content is too minimal and incomplete to yield a coherent theme beyond invoking death and disease with the weight of a liturgy.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is one broken question looped past sense — a fragment asking whether this is the virus, followed by a phrase about angels dying that keeps cutting off before it can finish. That severing is the point: on Crystal Castles' 2008 debut, Ethan Kath's tracks routinely bury Alice Glass under distortion until words dissolve into texture, and here the lyric enacts its own contamination, the line breaking down the way a body or a signal degrades. Only at the very end does the amputated phrase complete itself and turn on the listener — the angels die with you — so the endless dread finally lands as an accusation, or an infection passed along. Read the refusal to resolve as the meaning: illness, decay, and complicity rendered as a stuck loop that only closes by naming its victim.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind"],"means":"contamination as a stuck loop that only closes by naming its victim","built":"an amputated phrase completes itself only at the very end as accusation"},
"crystal-castles~love-and-caring": {"haiku":"A doctor-figure violently dismembers and 'remakes' their lover, turning mutual destruction into a twisted form of eternal togetherness.","opus":"A grotesque horror vignette of a mad doctor reanimating and mutilating a corpse-lover, love reframed as obsession, dissection and possession. Crystal Castles' abrasive electronics matched to genuine menace.","sonnet":"A grotesque love song narrated by a killer or surgeon figure who wants to literally cut open and stitch together a corpse as an act of twisted devotion — the \"love\" here is dissection, lobotomy, and mutilation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A giddy would-be surgeon addresses a body on the slab as a beloved, and the joke is that every endearment arrives through a scalpel. The love language here is anatomy: she wants a partner assembled to spec, drained pale, kept close by being kept perfectly still on the table. Alice Glass delivers it in a bratty singsong that plays the butchery as flirtation, so caring becomes her euphemism for control taken to its final, clinical extreme. The mid-song aside, where she breaks character to notice the bass and shrug, punctures the whole scene, admitting the gore is a pose worn for the pleasure of the noise underneath it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"caring offered as the euphemism for control taken to its clinical extreme","built":"a mid-song aside breaks character to admit the gore is a pose worn for the noise"},
"crystal-castles~magic-spells": {"haiku":"A scientist addresses a named person with repeated promises to command and organize via science and command.","opus":"The Toronto experimental-electronic duo strips lyric down to a single hypnotic, endlessly looped assurance, a promise to command someone's fleet with scientific best effort. Robbed of narrative and repeated into abstraction, the words function less as meaning than as texture, a chant dissolving into the glitchy chiptune haze that defined their early, deliberately disorienting sound.","sonnet":"Minimal in its lyrics, the song offers only a single repeated pledge of scientific command and devotion to Pamela, assembling a strange intimacy from clinical language. The promise to command her fleet reads as protective fantasy or delusion depending on context, hovering between care and control without resolving either.","src":"haiku","fable":"A single soothing pledge — a man promising Pamela he'll apply careful expertise to steer her ships — looped four times without variation, until the reassurance curdles into something nearer a hostage's mantra than comfort. The joke and the dread are the same mismatch: tender address welded to cold naval bureaucracy, delivered by Crystal Castles as chipmunked, pitch-warped vocal shrapnel that strips the words of any human warmth. Meaning develops not through the couplet but through its looping; the more he insists all is well, the more the promise sounds like an incantation cast on someone who never agreed to be soothed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"reassurance looped past comfort until it sounds like a hostage's mantra","built":"tender address welded to cold naval bureaucracy and chipmunked into inhuman warmth"},
"crystal-castles~mercenary": {"haiku":"Haunting depiction of exploitation, captivity, and moral emptiness. Silhouettes move without feeling; underage victims are commodified while the speaker questions complicity and servitude.","opus":"A dark, cryptic sketch of exploitation and complicity, with hollow silhouettes and questions of who will do someone's dirty work as a mercenary. It alludes disturbingly to the underage being dressed up and having their way with, framed as supposedly voluntary.","sonnet":"The song sketches a cold, transactional world where affection is simulated, the vulnerable are exploited, and those who might protect refuse to be converted to any cause. The imagery of underage figures dressed in lace and kept in solitary points to abuse and captivity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study in transactional intimacy, where affection is a service rendered without an interior to render it from — silhouettes that touch but cannot feel, the mercenary being whoever agrees to perform closeness on demand. Alice Glass's vocals, drowned and processed until the words barely surface, are the point rather than a failing: the third Crystal Castles record leans hard into abuse and complicity as texture, and here the verses turn coldly clinical, dressing up the vulnerable and the underage in the flat register of policy — reparations unseen, solitary confinement rebranded as something that should be voluntary. The horror lives in that euphemistic calm, the way coercion is offered as choice, and the chorus's numbness stops being an aesthetic and becomes the actual subject: what it costs to be hired for feeling you no longer have.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness","surveillance & control"],"means":"performing closeness from a hollowed interior","built":"vocals drowned past words until numbness becomes the subject"},
"crystal-castles~not-in-love": {"haiku":"Two former lovers acknowledge they no longer feel love; the speaker withdraws emotionally and reclaims their autonomy, though fascination once bound them.","opus":"The aftermath of a love that has curdled into estrangement, former lovers who can no longer even be friends. The insistent chant of not being in love reads as denial and self-protection, a wall thrown up precisely because the feeling hasn't actually gone.","sonnet":"The emotional aftermath of a relationship that's over: the narrator insists they feel nothing for the other person anymore, but the repeated denial reveals the claim is mostly self-protective rather than true.","src":"haiku","fable":"Platinum Blonde wrote this in 1983 as straight new-wave heartbreak, and Crystal Castles keep the text almost untouched — the meaning is entirely in the recasting. The single hands the whole thing to Robert Smith, whose voice is the sound of a man who has spent forty years being publicly, gorgeously in love, so that the flat denial in the chorus curdles into its own opposite: the more he insists, the less anyone believes him, and the title becomes a lie the singer needs more than he needs the truth. Every clause underneath sets up the trap — a picture kept but the door barred, the plea for someone to come home, the claim of control — a man managing his own composure while it fails in real time. The album version instead runs the same words through the band's own blown-out vocal processing, which strips the tenderness out and leaves only the machinery of denial, coldness confessing itself. Across both takes the song is about the exact moment love is renamed as its absence, and the renaming fools no one, least of all the one doing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The original is Platinum Blonde's 1983 new-wave single; the Robert Smith version arrived as a standalone single months after the album.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"denial so insistent it confesses the love it refuses to name","built":"Robert Smith's voice — the sound of public lifelong love — singing flat disavowal"},
"crystal-castles~not-in-love-feat-robert-smith": {"haiku":"A lover withdraws affection, resisting emotional attachment while the other insists on connection; the refrain declares emotional severance again and again, sealing the relationship's end.","opus":"A denial that keeps insisting on itself, the chorus repeated until it stops sounding like truth and starts sounding like grief. The gulf between cold outside and hot inside is really the gulf between what the speaker says and what he still feels.","sonnet":"The cold aftermath of a relationship — the speaker still has the other person's photo but shuts their heart against them completely, acknowledging the fascination is gone, that they can't even be friends now, and flatly repeating they are not in love.","src":"haiku","fable":"Platinum Blonde wrote this in 1983 as straight new-wave heartbreak, and Crystal Castles keep the text almost untouched — the meaning is entirely in the recasting. The single hands the whole thing to Robert Smith, whose voice is the sound of a man who has spent forty years being publicly, gorgeously in love, so that the flat denial in the chorus curdles into its own opposite: the more he insists, the less anyone believes him, and the title becomes a lie the singer needs more than he needs the truth. Every clause underneath sets up the trap — a picture kept but the door barred, the plea for someone to come home, the claim of control — a man managing his own composure while it fails in real time. The album version instead runs the same words through the band's own blown-out vocal processing, which strips the tenderness out and leaves only the machinery of denial, coldness confessing itself. Across both takes the song is about the exact moment love is renamed as its absence, and the renaming fools no one, least of all the one doing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The original is Platinum Blonde's 1983 new-wave single; the Robert Smith version arrived as a standalone single months after the album.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"denial so insistent it confesses the love it refuses to name","built":"Robert Smith's voice — the sound of public lifelong love — singing flat disavowal"},
"crystal-castles~not-in-love-ft-robert-smith": {"haiku":"Emotional withdrawal and erasure of connection after fascination has run its course.","opus":"A song about the collapse of a romance into denial, where ex-lovers who can't even be friends repeat the mantra 'I'm not in love' to insist they've moved on while the pleading 'when are you coming home?' betrays that they haven't.","sonnet":"The speaker insists, with increasing desperation, that the attachment they feel for a former lover is not love — even as the warmth they describe (her picture on the door, the cold outside, the heat inside) gives the lie to the denial.","src":"haiku","fable":"Platinum Blonde wrote this in 1983 as straight new-wave heartbreak, and Crystal Castles keep the text almost untouched — the meaning is entirely in the recasting. The single hands the whole thing to Robert Smith, whose voice is the sound of a man who has spent forty years being publicly, gorgeously in love, so that the flat denial in the chorus curdles into its own opposite: the more he insists, the less anyone believes him, and the title becomes a lie the singer needs more than he needs the truth. Every clause underneath sets up the trap — a picture kept but the door barred, the plea for someone to come home, the claim of control — a man managing his own composure while it fails in real time. The album version instead runs the same words through the band's own blown-out vocal processing, which strips the tenderness out and leaves only the machinery of denial, coldness confessing itself. Across both takes the song is about the exact moment love is renamed as its absence, and the renaming fools no one, least of all the one doing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The original is Platinum Blonde's 1983 new-wave single; the Robert Smith version arrived as a standalone single months after the album.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"denial so insistent it confesses the love it refuses to name","built":"Robert Smith's voice — the sound of public lifelong love — singing flat disavowal"},
"crystal-castles~not-in-love-ft-robert-smith-of-the-cure": {"haiku":"Lovers separate into estrangement as fascination cools, leaving only temperature extremes and mutual denial of feeling.","opus":"A Platinum Blonde cover where the denial is the tell, insisting on detachment while everything aches toward the ex. Robert Smith's voice over glacial synths makes the protest sound exactly like someone still in love.","sonnet":"A picture still hanging on a door and a heart that no longer houses anyone anchors a repeated denial: the physical warmth is real but the emotional connection is gone, and \"fascination ends, here we go again\" is the only honest account of a cycle that refuses to close.","src":"haiku","fable":"Platinum Blonde wrote this in 1983 as straight new-wave heartbreak, and Crystal Castles keep the text almost untouched — the meaning is entirely in the recasting. The single hands the whole thing to Robert Smith, whose voice is the sound of a man who has spent forty years being publicly, gorgeously in love, so that the flat denial in the chorus curdles into its own opposite: the more he insists, the less anyone believes him, and the title becomes a lie the singer needs more than he needs the truth. Every clause underneath sets up the trap — a picture kept but the door barred, the plea for someone to come home, the claim of control — a man managing his own composure while it fails in real time. The album version instead runs the same words through the band's own blown-out vocal processing, which strips the tenderness out and leaves only the machinery of denial, coldness confessing itself. Across both takes the song is about the exact moment love is renamed as its absence, and the renaming fools no one, least of all the one doing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The original is Platinum Blonde's 1983 new-wave single; the Robert Smith version arrived as a standalone single months after the album.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"denial so insistent it confesses the love it refuses to name","built":"Robert Smith's voice — the sound of public lifelong love — singing flat disavowal"},
"crystal-castles~pale-flesh": {"haiku":"Children are marked, conditioned, harvested, and sold as commodities. The song uses ash-marking and flesh-gifting metaphors to describe the commodification of youth.","opus":"A queasy meditation on innocence marked and claimed too early, ritual imagery of ash and rites laid over a child promised away. The flat delivery makes the sense of a body handed off before it can consent all the more chilling.","sonnet":"A child is inducted into religious ritual before they can even consent — baptised, marked with ash, their tears collected — then packaged and traded like an object, the refrain making explicit that the adolescent body is treated as flesh to be given away.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song reads as a ritual of coerced formation, staging an indoctrination liturgy performed on children too young to consent, smearing their skin with ash and harvesting their distress as though grief were the sacrament's purpose rather than its cost. The middle turns those same children into concealed merchandise, sunk beneath ocean spray out of sight and wrapped for delivery, while the abuser burrows unseen into the architecture around them. Only at the close does a victim speak, naming herself a child bride reduced to a body handed off, the flat repetition of her line enacting the numb resignation of someone who has stopped expecting to be heard.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","surveillance & control"],"means":"harvesting children's distress as the sacrament's purpose","built":"the sole victim line lands flat and repeated enacting numb resignation"},
"crystal-castles~pap-smear": {"haiku":"Children and innocence are exploited, cut off from growth, and forced into use or submission through pain and manipulation. The song describes systematic harm that destroys wounds that cannot heal.","opus":"The electronic duo pair unsettling imagery of a life destroyed before it could ever develop with a voice coldly bragging about its ability to inflict wounds that never mend. Beneath the murk lies deliberate cruelty and the ruin of innocence, a portrait of someone who exploits another's vulnerabilities relentlessly and on purpose.","sonnet":"A song about innocence stripped away before it has a chance to develop — cut from the stem before blooming — with a corrosive speaker who knows exactly what the other person hates and uses it to wound them, opening cuts that refuse to close, evoking cycles of emotional cruelty and controlled harm.","src":"haiku","fable":"A controlling voice addresses something it has stunted, and the pre-chorus keeps collapsing any line between a life barely begun and one already snuffed out, refusing to say which fate it dealt. The chorus turns that ambivalence into method: a narrator who has catalogued exactly what its captive dreads, then administers it deliberately, tending an injury engineered never to close over. Crystal Castles pitch Alice Glass through a blown-out, corroded vocal so nursery-soft menace and open cruelty ring at one frequency, and the outro clinches it by stammering each line into a severed stump, every phrase amputated mid-breath until only the vow of lasting harm survives whole.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","violence & murder"],"means":"a controller tending an injury engineered never to close","built":"outro amputates every phrase mid-breath so only the vow of harm survives whole"},
"crystal-castles~plague": {"haiku":"A corrupting force targets innocents and rewrites their history. It harvests the young and pure while disguising itself as benevolent.","opus":"Alice Glass turning corrupted innocence and indoctrination into a first-person threat, children conscripted and rewritten by predatory systems until the abused becomes the contagion; declaring herself the plague as bitter self-fulfillment.","sonnet":"A predatory figure who sees themselves as a corrupting force — targeting the pure and uninitiated, rewriting history and harvesting the vulnerable — who ultimately admits they are the disease.","src":"haiku","fable":"Plague inverts the vocabulary of purity into predation: the same voice that demands its object be clean and untouched is the contagion that will spoil it, and it knows this — the flat post-chorus admission that it is the plague lands not as horror but as a shrug. Alice Glass sings the abuser's self-mythology from inside, layering the register of colonial harvest (uprooting colonies, ripening bodies for reaping) over that of infection and forced innocence, so the song's real subject is how corruption dresses itself as cultivation. The chorus's grim pun — infants folded into infantry — is the whole machine in miniature: the young are conscripted, their history rewritten, and the predator's shock at being cast as the enemy is the mask, while the willingness to name itself the plague is what leaks out underneath. On (III), Crystal Castles' album of institutional oppression and bodily violation, this is the track where the abuser gets the microphone, and the horror is how reasonable, even wounded, it lets itself sound.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance","shame & repression"],"means":"the abuser names itself the plague as a shrug not a horror","built":"infants folded into infantry compresses the whole machine in one pun"},
"crystal-castles~sad-eyes": {"haiku":"Someone conceals their true pain and sadness behind veils and false affection, but their inner suffering is visible and cannot be hidden or disguised.","opus":"A dance track wrapped around emotional coldness: a woman who feigns warmth and gives affection she never earned, sadness she can't hide beneath the veil. Alice Glass's chill making the danceable feel hollow.","sonnet":"About a person who cannot disguise the grief or hollowness behind their eyes — they hide behind a veil, perform warmth and care they don't feel, accept affection they haven't earned; the eyes give them away every time.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole text is an accusation aimed at a woman whose practiced performance of intimacy cannot cover what her eyes involuntarily leak, and the song reads that leak as the one failure her craft cannot stage. Its images split into two families: a costume of manufactured tenderness on one side, the tell that betrays it on the other, so the meaning lives in the gap between the mask and what refuses to stay behind it. The single instruction she is handed, to actively shut warmth down rather than merely go without giving it, repeats until it erodes to one hammered word, enacting coldness as effortful labor instead of absence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression"],"means":"the involuntary tell that practiced performance cannot stage","built":"the single instruction hammered down to one word enacts coldness as labor"},
"crystal-castles~sadist": {"haiku":"Life lived without conscience—remorse erased, disdain accepted as consequence. Circling systems of harm without interruption.","opus":"An eerily soothing voice assures a target that all is well while nudging them toward ruin, a mind that operates with no morality where cruelty reads as mere aftermath. The gentle repeating is the terror itself, manipulation delivered over icy machinery.","sonnet":"A cold, minimal portrait of someone operating without conscience — steering others along a course, awaiting a blessed outcome without remorse, with the disdain for others presented not as aberration but as consequence of a life lived without inner restraint.","src":"haiku","fable":"A handler's voice steers someone through a procedure it refuses to name, cooing that all is well while quietly reclassifying cruelty as grace, injury as an outcome the victim earned, and creeping numbness as mercy rather than the warning that a nerve is being crushed. The dread runs on two vocabularies at war: the clinical word for that pins-and-needles deadening sits against pastoral talk of ceremony and deserving, so a body being harmed gets narrated as a body being sanctified. Then the soothing drops away for a flatly repeated statement of purpose, which admits the whole exchange was only ever about getting past whatever rule or feeling would otherwise have stopped it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"cruelty reclassified as grace while numbness is narrated as mercy","built":"clinical and pastoral vocabularies at war across a body being sanctified"},
"crystal-castles~suffocation": {"haiku":"The speaker suffocates under the weight of their own wasted life and youth, begging not to be revived, while another waits in vain for something that will never come.","opus":"A suicidal renunciation delivered coldly, begging not to be revived and framing life as wasted days, nights and youth. The surrounding lines about ignored consent, plasticine models and morality as disgrace turn the personal suffocation outward into contempt for a hollow, performative world that offers her nothing.","sonnet":"A resignation to slow self-erasure — the speaker actively asks not to be resuscitated, acknowledges wasting their youth, and tells a waiting other that there is nothing here for them; consent and morality have both collapsed.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song builds two matched panels — a first about numbness toward others, where ignoring what was withheld and refusing to repent grinds down whatever conscience once resisted, and a second about a hollow public act, decency melted into figures of modeling clay who exit on a curtain-call flourish. The recurring demand binds both halves: the speaker orders the listener never to restart what they have chosen to let stop, so drowning reads as a deliberate refusal rather than a fate, and the patient addressee is told outright they are owed nothing. Alice Glass sings this self-erasure over Ethan Kath's bright electro-pop rush, and that collision — dancefloor euphoria carrying a verdict that the years and everything they demanded came to nothing — is the whole engine of the track.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance"],"means":"drowning declared a chosen refusal rather than a fate","built":"dancefloor rush carrying a verdict that every year came to nothing"},
"crystal-castles~tell-me-what-to-swallow": {"haiku":"A child describes paternal violence, abuse, and conditional love, equating purity with being worthy of a father's affection.","opus":"A harrowing dispatch from inside child abuse, where a girl rationalizes her father's violence as love and stakes her whole worth on his approval. The horror lands through her forced innocence, praying for the person hurting her.","sonnet":"A deeply disturbing portrait of childhood sexual abuse by a father figure — told from a child's perspective using the language of devotion and purity, the lyrics describe paternal grooming under the guise of love.","src":"haiku","fable":"The album-closing horror of Crystal Castles' 2008 debut, and its most exposed track: Ethan Kath strips the record's abrasive game-boy noise down to a slow, waterlogged drone so nothing shields Alice Glass, who sings this one clean instead of screaming it through distortion. The subject is child abuse voiced from inside the victim's own coping, and the terror is that the voice never breaks — it keeps insisting he would never hurt her while describing exactly the harm, folding violence into a language of devotion and prayer. The song reads that groomed vocabulary as its whole method: purity, wholeness, being the only one loved, are the abuser's terms wearing the child's mouth, so that safety and violation are spoken as if they were the same thing. The title becomes the trap laid bare — a plea to be told what to accept, obedience mistaken for love — and the unbearable calm of the delivery is the point, the mask worn so completely that the listener has to hear the damage the singer cannot name.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"obedience mistaken for love voiced in the abuser's own vocabulary","built":"an unbearably calm delivery stripped of distortion makes the mask the instrument"},
"crystal-castles~their-kindness-is-charade": {"haiku":"Kindness is false and used to sedate and control; confusion keeps victims compliant. People remain unashamed while using others, who stay trapped in ignorance.","opus":"The Canadian electronic duo craft a paranoid warning against fake benevolence, generosity reframed as a tool to pacify and dominate. Being exploited is designed to leave you disoriented and entertaining to your handlers, gradually reduced to an object that never wises up. Their harsh synths underscore control masquerading as care until escape becomes impossible.","sonnet":"Manufactured warmth is exposed as a control mechanism — kindness deployed to sedate, confusion maintained to keep compliant, the target's own longings weaponised against them. The lyric's compression makes the trap feel suffocating: every avenue of resistance has already been anticipated and neutralised.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about coercive care, where an abuser's soothing gestures are exposed as an anesthetic that keeps the captive pliant, grateful, and too disoriented to push back. Crystal Castles build the trap through instruction rather than confession: the second person is told to stay bewildered, to breathe in the drug offered as comfort, to secretly crave the very thing they loudly reject in public. That gap between buried longing and performed disgust is the psychological engine, the mind of someone taught to mistake captivity for tenderness and forward motion for a threat. The closing fragment, severed mid-thought, enacts the confinement it describes, so the structure itself withholds any exit rather than merely narrating one.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"This closing exhale is the finished version of what first surfaced as the 2015 single Deicide — same words, same vocal material, the song arrived at its later stage.","themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"captive taught to mistake captivity for tenderness and motion for threat","built":"the closing fragment severed mid-thought withholds the exit structurally"},
"crystal-castles~through-the-hosiery": {"haiku":"A parent's guilt over creating/abandoning a child manifests as emotional numbness and inability to feel connection. The violence and theft of time compound the unresolved trauma.","opus":"A harrowing piece circling abortion, coercion, and numbness, voiced from inside a relationship where one person can't feel anything and the other is bled dry. It's deliberately raw and disturbing, indicting an emotional void that consumes everyone near it.","sonnet":"A disturbing song about an unwanted pregnancy and termination — told from the perspective of a child or unwanted offspring addressing the mother, it catalogues abandonment, guilt, borrowed wombs, and the cold arithmetic of who carries the weight of another life.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is an abortion sung inside out, where Alice Glass ventriloquizes the person demanding the pregnancy be ended, then answers back as the discarded fetus, so accusation and confession share one throat and neither voice earns absolution. The title maps the route the song keeps chanting: from intimate clothing, to a weapons store, to blank nonexistence, a march that turns the body's most private surface into a supply line for harm and then empties out completely. Its central cruelty is numbness, the refrain returning again and again to how someone can act on another body while registering nothing, so cut and bleeding hands become proof of injury offered to a partner who perceives only a mess to wipe away. Sulfur drunk and spat back, plus a demand to reclaim an organ that was only ever lent, push it past any literal event into a curse against being used as a vessel. What the looping itself does is refuse relief: every return to that unfeeling chorus confirms the exchange never closes, guilt handed over and dropped rather than carried.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"violation handed over in a language of numbness that refuses to close","built":"two voices sharing one throat means accusation and confession never earn absolution"},
"crystal-castles~transgender": {"haiku":"The song explores broken promises, lost innocence, and the corruption of what was once pure and untouched. Nothing can live up to idealistic expectations and once lost, that purity can never be recovered.","opus":"An eerie meditation on lost innocence and the impossibility of purity, addressing someone chosen young with promises no one can keep; the recurring warning that 'you'll never be pure again' frames intimacy and love as a corrupting narrative that irreversibly changes a person.","sonnet":"A fragmented song about intimacy, chosen identity, and the moment of revealing something true about yourself to someone you love — the speaker hints at disclosing a secret that will complicate the relationship, while images of flowers shedding petals and lost purity suggest transformation and its irreversible cost.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction that curdles into a verdict: the tender verses court a listener among the chosen few, the lover keeps pressing whether the devotion is genuine, and everything hinges on a withheld admission that news is coming. The title names a crossing the words never narrate, only orbit, so it hangs over the intimacy as the disclosure the song holds back, the change of state that reframes every tender line without being spoken. The chorus is that news arriving, and it answers by cancelling every vow, insisting the process cannot be stopped once triggered and that the listener is now permanently altered, no longer intact. The pre-chorus splits the encounter along a preserve-or-dig-up axis, framing it as a thing done to a body that can be embalmed or unearthed later but never handed back whole. Set against Crystal Castles' method, where Alice Glass's vocal is chopped, pitched, and half-drowned beneath Ethan Kath's corroded synths, the processing enacts the threat: closeness reaches you only through a medium that consumes what it carries.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"disclosure withheld until it cancels every tender vow","built":"vocal chopped and half-drowned enacting what the threat describes"},
"crystal-castles~untrust-us": {"haiku":"A repetitive public-service warning that cocaine is harmful, delivered in Spanish and English as if from an official health announcement.","opus":"The lyrics consist almost entirely of one repeated bilingual line stating that cocaine is not good for your health, offering little beyond that single warning.","sonnet":"The lyrics repeat a single statement — cocaine is not good for your health — alternating between Spanish and English with no additional content. The song's entire lyrical substance is this repeated public-health warning, making it uninterpretable beyond its literal message.","src":"haiku","fable":"The opening track of Crystal Castles' debut is a warning that has been fed through a machine until the warning is unrecognizable — a single public-service sentence, cocaine is not good for you, looped in Spanish and English until it stops meaning anything and starts sounding like an invitation. That is the whole trick, and it is a real one: the phrase is a synthesized text-to-speech voice inherited from Death From Above 1979's Dead Womb — a borrowed borrowing — and Alice Glass and Ethan Kath run it through the same chip-tune corrosion and blown-out low end that defines the record, so the moral lesson arrives already narcotized, sped and stuttered into euphoria. The song never argues; it demonstrates. Repetition here is not emphasis but erosion — the more times you are told a thing is bad for you, the more the telling becomes the high, and by the end the admonition and the addiction are indistinguishable, which is exactly the queasy, thrilling dissonance the band built a career on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The voice is a text-to-speech program, inherited with permission from Death From Above 1979’s Dead Womb — a sample of a sample.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"the warning becomes indistinguishable from the invitation","built":"a borrowed anti-drug loop eroded by repetition into euphoria"},
"crystal-castles~vanished": {"haiku":"Urban predation and hunger drive a restless narrator through the city's night, pursuing stray lives and fleeting connections until death ultimately claims them.","opus":"A predatory, restless figure hungry for strays and for life prowls the city, drawn to a lover's scent and blood; it turns to death as both take their own lives and, now dead, face the dark they fear.","sonnet":"A nighttime urban scene traced through the perspective of a hungry, predatory figure prowling city streets — drawn to sweat and pulse, indifferent to invitation — ending with both pursuer and pursued dead, mutual destruction framed as an inevitable consequence of the city's relentless appetite.","src":"haiku","fable":"A nocturnal predation fantasy where a soft voice coaxes something out of the darkness to feed, and the whole lyric arrives fragmented, syllables clipped and swallowed as if the words were being consumed even as they were sung. That erosion is the reading: the language of appetite, chasing, and refused pity dissolves exactly the way a body would under Crystal Castles' corrosive 2008 production, the human vocal buried and half-eaten by the machine around it. The bridge then stops developing altogether and loops one predatory demand eight times over, a mechanical craving that refuses to resolve into meaning, before the ending quietly flips the entire hunt into a shared dissolution toward the black — no remorse, only exhaustion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The whole vocal is Van She’s Sex City — Matt Van Schie’s melody, chopped with permission.","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"predatory hunger refusing to resolve into remorse","built":"the bridge abandons development and loops one demand eight times"},
"crystal-castles~vietnam": {"haiku":"Existential affirmation of survival—'I' and 'alive' repeat obsessively amid fragmentation, asserting existence despite disintegration.","opus":"Vocal fragments dissolved into pure texture, the repeated assertion of being alive stretched past meaning into pulse and gasp. It reads less as lyric than as a disoriented signal of survival amid the noise.","sonnet":"The lyrics are essentially just the word 'I' and 'alive' repeated — no discernible narrative or theme beyond a fragmented assertion of self and survival.","src":"haiku","fable":"There is no narrative here to walk, only a single pronoun and a single verb worried down to their phonemes — Stina Nordenstam's voice pitched and looped until alive fractures into alive-ive, the affirmation stuttering past the point where it can still mean. The song is built to make bare self-assertion sound like a machine caught in a groove: the more the I insists it is alive, the more it registers as a sample rather than a person, a survivor's mantra emptied by repetition into the mechanical thing survival becomes. Against the title's freight of atrocity, that refusal to develop is the reading — this too, alive holds at the edge of endurance and shellshock, the same three words offered as proof of life and as the tic of someone who no longer quite is.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The other half of the carving — the same Nordenstam song that haunts Violent Dreams, here reduced to its barest syllables.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","war & battle"],"means":"survival mantra repeated until the survivor sounds like a machine","built":"alive looped until it stutters into sample rather than person"},
"crystal-castles~violent-dreams": {"haiku":"A brief narrative of someone asking to borrow a car and nearly dying in a fiery crash, rescued by off-duty police.","opus":"Built from sampled fragments about a car crash and policemen pulling a woman from a burning car, the song assembles these into a woozy, dreamlike loop of violence and rescue; there's no linear plot, but the throughline is trauma replayed as a hypnotic, fractured nightmare.","sonnet":"Based on a real incident involving Chrissie Amphlett — a woman's car crashes and catches fire, and she's pulled out by off-duty police. The narrator addresses Chrissie with the suggestion that there might have been something she'd rather not have survived.","src":"haiku","fable":"Crystal Castles built this from a Stina Nordenstam sample, and the borrowed voice is the whole trick: her small, breathy delivery narrates a burning car and a rescue in the flat, tender cadence of a lullaby, so terror arrives sounding like reassurance. The song works by withholding — a plea to take someone's keys, a wreck, two off-duty policemen dragging a woman clear — details that never resolve into who caused what, only the aftermath repeated until it stops meaning and starts hypnotizing. That looping praise of the rescue, and the unfinished I'd rather that never says what it would rather, are the point: the mind circling a violent event it can't complete, snagged mid-sentence. Against the record's shredded noise, Alice Glass's usual scream is absent here, and that absence lets the dream stay soft while the content stays wrong — the violence named in the title lives entirely in the gap between the sweetness of the voice and the disaster it keeps calmly describing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The voice is Stina Nordenstam's A Walk in the Park; the same single song was carved into two of this album's tracks.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","violence & murder"],"means":"a mind snagged mid-sentence around violence it cannot complete","built":"borrowed lullaby voice delivers disaster in the flat cadence of reassurance"},
"crystal-castles~violent-youth": {"haiku":"Young girls are groomed and corrupted through consumption and neglect, facing indifference and betrayal. The song depicts innocence destroyed by abandonment and those who fail to protect them.","opus":"A hazy, unsettling portrait of a girl growing up too fast amid dress-up, drinking and disappointment, the narrator's promise to always let her down capturing the corrosive inevitability of that betrayal.","sonnet":"About a young woman being systematically failed and exploited by the adults around her — drink, dress up, let them disappoint you, the cycle repeating. The predatory undertone is strong: the narrator seems to be one of those who lets her down, watching her burn while calling her beautiful.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric strings together feminine surfaces staged for approval—lace, cheekbones, an angelical face, a mother's inherited pearl—and undercuts each with bodily collapse: drinking, vomiting into a plate, affection permitted only through window glass. Crystal Castles built (III) around images of predation and captive girlhood, and the reading turns on the refrain's swap of pronouns, where a distant warning that other people will disappoint her hardens into the speaker owning that failure as their own. The coaxing register of a guardian curdles into the voice of the thing she should fear, so the song's menace is that its protector and its predator share one throat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"the protector and predator sharing one throat","built":"pronoun shift in the refrain turns a distant warning into owned failure"},
"crystal-castles~wrath-of-god":{"haiku":"A lyrical fragment depicting harmful, totalitarian acts—christening with poison, sterilizing those who help, severing loyalties, and stripping heritage. The repeat refrain emphasizes theft of identity and cultural erasure.","opus":"Through violent, clinical imagery of chemicals, sterilization and pesticide, the song describes people being cleansed, uprooted and stripped of their heritage.","sonnet":"The lyrics describe acts of forced displacement, sterilization, and cultural erasure — treating Samaritans and others as targets to be purged — with the repeated warning that those in power will strip people of their heritage.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sung through the corroded, half-audible distortion that defines Crystal Castles' III, the song reads less as statement than as ritual overheard through a wall — which is the point, because its imagery is entirely the vocabulary of cleansing turned murderous. Baptism becomes paraffin, mercy becomes something to be sterilized, and migration becomes fumigation, so every gesture of care is swapped for its exterminating double, the whole chorus a liturgy that dresses erasure in the language of purification. The buried mix isn't a flaw hiding weak words; it enacts the theme, letting a chant about stripping people of their heritage arrive as something already decayed, a threat murmured from inside the machinery rather than shouted from a pulpit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","faith & the occult"],"means":"every gesture of care swapped for its exterminating double in a liturgy of erasure","built":"the corroded half-audible mix enacts the theme, a threat murmured from inside the machinery"},
"crystal-castles~xxzxcuzx-me": {"haiku":"A speaker describes existence as mechanical suffering where bodies decay like machines while consciousness persists despite the corruption of love through programmed systems and sexual degradation.","opus":"Alice Glass shrieks from inside a machine's-eye account of intimacy stripped down to faulty hardware, built to corrode and riddled with sickness. It turns the robot-lover conceit into a claim that even fleshless things still dread their own end.","sonnet":"A dystopian meditation on bodies reduced to machinery, programmed desire, and disease — sex, rust, AIDS, and cyborg parts blur together in a world where flesh and death are indistinguishable.","src":"haiku","fable":"A body and a machine keep trading vocabularies until neither owns its own parts, desire routed through circuitry that corrodes even as it is first switched on. The imagery welds tenderness to hardware, an embrace measured in torque and an arousal that reads like a component running too hot, so intimacy and malfunction arrive as one indivisible event. Then the collage snaps shut on a blunt taunt about who gets counted as a person and who gets scrapped, the noise abruptly aiming itself at prejudice and at the lie that a synthetic thing stops caring once its shutdown finally comes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","love & desire","politics & society"],"means":"intimacy and malfunction arriving as one indivisible event","built":"the track snaps from tenderness-as-hardware to a blunt taunt about personhood"},
"crystal-castles~year-of-silence": {"haiku":"Intimate moments of connection and grief; a beloved person and shared breathing and kissing through tears and sadness.","opus":"An Icelandic refrain of embracing and weeping in each other's arms when the lovers meet and kiss, addressed to a dearest friend, with the singer swallowing tears and breathing in the other's hair; a mood of tender, sorrowful closeness.","sonnet":"Icelandic lyrics repeat images of bad behavior and weeping in an embrace — \"badly behaved, crying in arms, when we met, when we kissed\" — alongside lines about swallowing tears and breathing hair for a best friend, conveying a cycle of troubled intimacy and grief shared between two people.","src":"haiku","fable":"Crystal Castles treat the human voice here as another synth patch — Alice Glass and Ethan Kath take a scrap of Icelandic (attributed to Jónsi) and loop it past the point where language is supposed to do its job, so that a listener without the tongue hears grief before hearing a word of it. And the Icelandic is grief: an interlaced call and response about weeping in an embrace, meeting and kissing, my best friend, I swallow a tear — a tender private vocabulary of reunion and loss. The device is the meaning. By welding an intimate lyric of comfort to a hammering rave build, the track stages how the era's blown-out digital music metabolizes real feeling: the sample gets chopped, doubled against its own echo, and driven until the endearment and the anguish become indistinguishable texture. What survives the machine is not sense but tone — the year of silence is the state left when the words no longer signify and only the ache in them keeps looping.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The looped voice is Jónsi — Sigur Rós's Inní mér syngur vitleysingur chopped down, a lyric of embraces and a best friend inverted into dread.","themes":["death & grief","artifice & performance"],"means":"grief absorbed into texture when the words no longer signify","built":"Icelandic lullaby of reunion and loss welded to a hammering rave build"},
"crystal-lake~aeon":{"haiku":"Religious faith becomes a machine of control and enslavement; humanity is corrupted and damned by systems that demand obedience and conformity.","opus":"The Japanese metalcore band imagine a dystopia of installed belief, mankind reprogrammed and folded into a collective that plants a manufactured deity into upgraded, engineered bodies. Scripted worship and artificial symbols mark hollowed-out people who trust the system, while a descending presence surveys humanity's squalor and pronounces the species doomed from the outset.","sonnet":"Organized religion reframed as a corporate firmware update: humanity coded, sequenced, and absorbed into a hive that mistakes compliance for salvation. The song presents faith as a tool of dehumanization, with prayer reduced to a reboot command and the congregation as willing hardware.","src":"haiku"},
"crystal-lake~curse":{"haiku":"A dehumanized people curse the one who took their hearts, suffering endlessly while broken dreams turn to memories; they walk through hell together.","opus":"The Japanese metalcore act stand hollowed-out and stripped of will at a point of no return, questioning whether existence itself is a blessing or a punishment as they watch death claim another. Losing themselves in isolation, they resolve to gild their ruined hopes into something enduring and push on through hardship together.","sonnet":"Grief for lives cut short and the exhausting weight of survival fold together into a question about whether existence is gift or punishment. The dead leave behind untold stories that make ordinary days shine with borrowed meaning, even as the living keep falling deeper.","src":"haiku"},
"crystal-lake~devilcry":{"opus":"A desperate grief over losing a loved one to death, with the singer bargaining fantastically (mixing chemicals to rebuild the body, ruling the world, dying himself) to bring them back; ultimately he must accept the goodbye and the burden of surviving alone.","sonnet":"A grief song about losing someone irreplaceable — the narrator would rearrange the elements of the world to bring them back, questions whether accepting death brings any closure, and is consumed by regret at having left them stranded; even the devil, the song posits, wept when his mother died.","src":"sonnet"},
"crystal-lake~disobey":{"haiku":"Someone rejects enslavement to authority and fear, calling others to break free and bite back against their oppressors.","opus":"A furious refusal of servitude, the narrator recognizing his own chains yet turning fear and fed tears into fuel, demanding you bite the hand that bleeds you and remember no master deserves obedience. Crystal Lake are a Japanese metalcore band known for stadium-scaled breakdowns, and the call-and-response of are you breathing sharpens this into an anti-authoritarian chant built to be screamed back at them.","sonnet":"Collective rage against subjugation — the voice names the condition as slavery, rejects the framing that silence equals survival, and demands that those who have been kept as cogs in a machine bite the hand that bleeds them. Grief and fear are recast as fuel rather than defeat, and the command to disobey carries the force of something that cannot be taken back.","src":"haiku"},
"crystal-lake~rebirth":{"haiku":"False divinity and hypocrisy dominate while the speaker deserves only pain and erasure, yet from ash and obliteration emerges the possibility of phoenix rebirth stripped of previous identity.","opus":"The Japanese metalcore band frames self-loathing as a religious crisis, tearing down false idols and messiah figures only to find the emptiness pointed inward. It ends wanting to burn away identity entirely and start clean from the ashes.","sonnet":"A person condemned by false idols and a blood god — fractured, self-blaming, accepting that they deserve nothing — reaches for rebirth from the ashes as the only escape from the lion's dungeon, the Japanese coda invoking ancient light and a world freed from suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"crystal-lake~watch-me-burn":{"haiku":"The speaker is their own worst enemy waiting for sunrise while drowning in shadows. They ask if anyone will feel their pain as they burn and sacrifice everything.","opus":"Japanese metalcore band Crystal Lake stage a defiant last stand, the narrator naming himself his own greatest obstacle in a fight only just beginning, ready to give up his life with nothing more to forfeit. Riches mean nothing to the dead, he insists, and his taunt to onlookers turns private anguish into a scorched vow that his pain and purpose will eventually be recognized.","sonnet":"Standing at the front line of a conflict that is both internal and external, someone who has sacrificed everything questions whether any of it leaves a mark. The defiance of being set on fire together — honor in collective destruction — emerges as the only form of dignity still available.","src":"haiku"},
"crystal-method~born-too-slow":{"haiku":"Temporal misalignment and existential lag leave the speaker perpetually behind, unable to synchronize with the rhythm others inhabit.","opus":"A woozy warning about being born a step behind the pace of a fast burnout scene, where the lights burn low and those who can't keep up get left behind.","sonnet":"Lyrically sparse and elliptical, the song circles around someone who has always been a step behind — living in the margins, chasing last calls, unable to keep pace with a life that has already moved on. The core image is of someone born just slightly too slow to catch what they needed, watching the lights burn low as everyone else moves forward.","src":"haiku"},
"crystal-method~wide-open":{"haiku":"A meditation on unrestricted being, rejecting definitions and frameworks for an open, childlike state beyond questioning.","opus":"A spoken-word riff on what openness even means, worrying the question until definitions collapse and the answer becomes that real openness isn't a pose you strike but a state you inhabit, like a child before self-consciousness sets in.","sonnet":"A spoken-word meditation on what it means to be truly open: the voice works through analogies — open sky, open child's mind — before landing on openness as a state of pure being rather than a question or a posture. The final admission that you \"know how to shut it down\" suggests openness is chosen, not given.","src":"haiku"},
"crystalline~narcissistic-egotist":{"haiku":"The narrator escapes a manipulative impostor, recognising phoniness and reclaiming value through recognition of their own worth.","opus":"A kiss-off to a self-obsessed manipulator, moving from being treated as a trophy to walking free and adored elsewhere. The satisfaction is in naming his emptiness and no longer being available to it.","sonnet":"A speaker who spent years as a trophy for a controlling, faithless partner finally names them — faker, liar, cheater, loser — and reclaims their self-worth through a new relationship where they are put first, no longer strung along.","src":"haiku"},
"cuestack~unstoppable":{"haiku":"Resilience through rage becomes unstoppable force, with the narrator converting pain into unbreakable momentum against all opposition.","opus":"Betrayal alchemized into indestructibility, the classic power-metal turn where rage becomes armor and every blow only confirms you cannot be stopped. Defiance as sheer refusal to fall.","sonnet":"Rage turned into armour: every knife in the back, every hammer brought down, every attempt to crush or burn becomes fuel rather than damage — the speaker declares themselves unstoppable precisely because adversity has never made them stop.","src":"haiku"},
"cuffed-up~bonnie":{"haiku":"Serial dishonesty and romantic strikes accumulate into terminal estrangement; the speaker acknowledges defeat and inevitable separation.","opus":"A poisonous romance tallied like a countdown, remorse for dishonesty trading off with a craving to reclaim what got torn away. Pictures of fleeing toward a frontier and pleading show two people who cannot stop hurting one another.","sonnet":"A relationship built on mutual deception accumulates strikes until both parties are exposed — the speaker apologises for their own lies while indicting a partner who used them and abandoned them, all against a blood-red sunset that frames the wreckage as something almost beautiful.","src":"haiku"},
"cult-of-luna-julie-christmas~the-wreck-of-s-s-needle":{"haiku":"The song contrasts the pursuit of wealth and status with themes of violence, submission, and observation, describing a kind of internal breakdown through fractured, cryptic imagery.","opus":"A cryptic fable of a doomed get-rich scheme leading naive followers toward disaster, rendered as a shipwreck full of strange, dreamlike menace. The Swedish post-metal band's crushing patience meets Julie Christmas's fractured wail, a looping, threatening refrain building an atmosphere of pursuit and collapse over any clear plot.","sonnet":"A surreal military or collective ritual plays out in imagery drawn from sewing — thimbles, pins, splinters hidden under skin — to map an ordeal of loyalty, endurance, and exposure. The repeated command to run suggests a gauntlet where the only way to prove worth is to keep moving through pain.","src":"haiku"},
"cults~go-outside":{"haiku":"Isolation versus engagement frame contrasting life philosophies; the narrator urges stepping outside to experience reality rather than retreating into avoidance.","opus":"Framed by Jim Jones's actual voice, the song sets bright indie-pop yearning to get outside against the paranoia of a cult leader who kept his followers inside to die. It's about the pull toward light versus the seduction of retreat.","sonnet":"Framed by a cult leader's voice urging acceptance of death, the song sets a speaker's simple desire to go outdoors and experience daylight against a partner's retreat into isolation, ending in the speaker choosing their own life over a relationship that has become an obstacle.","src":"haiku"},
"culture-club~karma-chameleon-remastered-2002":{"haiku":"Inconsistency becomes survival strategy as the lover transforms color and commitment to evade accountability, addiction to their own contradiction perpetually destructive.","opus":"Boy George's sunny reproach to a fickle lover whose colors keep shifting, love made hard by someone who comes and goes. Under the bounce sits a warning about karma and the loneliness of never knowing where you stand.","sonnet":"A relationship built on a partner's constant inconsistency — appearing and disappearing, shifting colours, unable to commit — is met with the admission that loving someone so changeable is exhausting but still desired. The speaker recognises their own contradiction: they keep coming back despite knowing the love will always be conditional.","src":"haiku"},
"curbi~lose-our-minds":{"haiku":"Overthinking and distrust sabotage partnership as both speakers create the problems they fear, collapsing connection through mutual anxiety and preemptive betrayal.","opus":"A relationship dissolving through mutual paranoia and blame, each person hearing only what confirms their fears and sabotaging the thing before it ever had a chance.","sonnet":"A couple destroying their relationship with paranoia and misreading: one hears what they want, bends the truth, assumes the worst before it happens, and together they have paved a void — the chorus asking why they keep losing their minds and ending things before they try.","src":"haiku"},
"currents~a-flag-to-wave":{"opus":"Currents voice a collapse of meaning, a figure adrift in surroundings blind to his identity, whose hard-earned existence yields nothing solid and who aches for a cause, a banner to raise. Set against someone who traded ambition for a hollow calm, he pledges to carry that emblem to the finish, desperate to learn how fearlessness feels.","sonnet":"A song about the search for identity and conviction in a life that feels purposeless — the narrator craves something to believe in and fight for, watching others abandon their dreams while he picks up the discarded banner. The emotional core is the ache of wanting to stand for something when everything feels hollow.","src":"sonnet"},
"currents~apnea":{"haiku":"Violation and trauma embed themselves as obsessive vengeance, the narrator consumed by rage toward someone who destroyed innocence and left endless suffering in their wake.","opus":"A brother's murderous vow of vengeance against the intruder who violated his family, promising to make the man suffer endlessly for the innocence he destroyed.","sonnet":"The speaker discovers a woman has been assaulted — someone climbed through the window and fled — and the song is pure rage directed at the perpetrator: a promise of reciprocal suffering, broken bones, slit wrists, and the image of the abuser thrown to slaughter with a whimpered goodbye.","src":"haiku"},
"currents~better-days":{"haiku":"Worn down by survival, the speaker spirals downward seeking purpose while shackled by fear and despair.","opus":"Currents translate metalcore's chug into something clammy and interior, and here the machinery bends toward paralysis: a narrator on his knees while the world collapses, chained by his own weak will, waiting endlessly for improvement that never arrives. The refrain of following one's sorrows downward frames depression as gravity, though a flicker of breaking free survives the spiral.","sonnet":"A person caught in a downward spiral clings to the memory of better times while suffocation and stagnation make every day feel like the bottom. Pride still flickers beneath the weight, whispering that this arrested freefall is not a permanent address, and that chains can lie flat.","src":"haiku"},
"currents~forever-marked":{"haiku":"Systemic betrayal and collective complicity leave permanent scars; the doomed observe their own destruction with bitter awareness of inevitable abandonment.","opus":"Metalcore aimed at collective betrayal, watching gods and progress collapse while people turn their backs and let it all fall apart. The wound is generational: inheriting a ruined world and being marked forever by the failure to save it.","sonnet":"A collective lament over societal collapse and betrayal by those in power — the disadvantaged saw the signs of demise, were ignored, and now face destruction at the hands of a system built on lies, with the mark of that betrayal permanent and inescapable.","src":"haiku"},
"currents~monsters":{"haiku":"Someone accuses their partner of inaction and betrayal while bearing all the weight alone, demanding commitment or departure.","opus":"The metalcore band dissect a relationship frozen in place, one partner retreating inward while the narrator denies responsibility for what they have both turned into. Exhausted and wrung dry, he lays down an ultimatum, stay with him or leave, weary of absorbing pain for a shared dream that turned out to be worthless.","sonnet":"A relationship corroded by someone who drains without giving breaks open into fury and finality. The narrator refuses blame for what the dynamic has made of both of them, declaring the attachment over and the cost of sustained giving without reciprocity too high to keep paying.","src":"haiku"},
"currents~poverty-of-self":{"haiku":"Inequality perpetuates cycles where the corrupt prosper while the innocent suffer; civilization collapses into dynasties and hellish division.","opus":"Currents widen the lens from private despair to a rotting social order, where the guilty prosper on greed while the broken carry the deadweight. Class resentment sharpens into imagined revolt, the guillotine swinging as dynasties calcify. The title reframes structural hell as a hollowing of the individual, apathy weaponized until spiritual emptiness becomes its own inescapable prison.","sonnet":"Society's rigged mechanics grind the innocent into the floor while the corrupt harvest the rewards. Class divides deepen under a swinging guillotine logic, and what accumulates is not wealth but an inner emptiness — a poverty of self — spreading through a blood-red greed.","src":"haiku"},
"currents~second-skin":{"haiku":"The narrator describes identity dissolution through repeated false selves until they forget which mask is real, buried in darkness without escape.","opus":"Currents dramatize the horror of a performed self hardening into the real one: a mask fusing to the face until misery becomes a chosen habit no longer removable. The narrator has lived so many borrowed lives he can no longer locate his own, disintegrating toward purgatory. The metalcore breakdown mirrors identity carved out and blended into faceless obscurity.","sonnet":"A persona worn so long it has fused with the face beneath it. Layers of performed misery calcify until the mask is indistinguishable from identity, leaving a person stranded in a purgatory of hollow selves, disintegrating inward while pretending to live outward.","src":"haiku"},
"currents~so-alone":{"haiku":"Collective abandonment and internal fracture leave the speaker stranded in a paradigm of penance; isolation becomes the only honest response to a burning bridge.","opus":"Faith and trust collapse under the weight of collective guilt and endless penance, leaving a shared damnation where even those closest cannot reach one another.","sonnet":"A communal disorientation under oppression or collapse: false ties, \"half-cut tongues,\" control fading out, bridges burning — the speaker and an unnamed group cannot remember if they have been here before, cannot get close, and feel utterly alone even in company. Blood spilled in error and love left behind in someone's wake haunt the chorus.","src":"haiku"},
"currents~vengeance":{"haiku":"Confinement breeds violent desire while divine proximity darkens into madness, leaving the powerless to face inevitable judgment.","opus":"Metalcore reaching for something bigger than a breakup, using the language of revolution and betrayal to ask whether forgiveness is possible when everyone is executioner and victim at once. Faith, madness and the failure to connect collapse into one exhausted question of whether anything is ever enough.","sonnet":"A speaker confronts cycles of institutional violence and spiritual betrayal — stabbed in the back for powerless choices, buried in sand as shallow monuments — asking whether it is possible to break through confinement and find the truth that lies beyond the executioner-judge-jury that governs their world.","src":"haiku"},
"curses~a-3m21r":{"haiku":"A transcendent journey of ego-death and cosmic connectivity—shedding humanity to become a conduit for universal energy and interconnection.","opus":"This track belongs to the lineage of psychedelic dissociation songs that use the open field at 3 AM as a threshold into ego-death. The narrator questions reality, feels the earth speak, chases demons in the treeline, and becomes a conduit for the universe's frequencies until he sees himself reflected from outside. The scene's disorientation is rendered as beauty — the boundary between self and world lost, and the loss experienced as expansion.","sonnet":"An extended meditation on a dissociative spiritual experience in an open field at 3 AM — the narrator questions reality, feels the earth speak to him, chases demons in the treeline, and eventually becomes a conduit for the universe's frequencies until he sees himself reflected back from the outside. The emotional throughline is the disorienting beauty of losing the boundary between self and world.","src":"haiku"},
"curses~the-door-in-the-wall":{"haiku":"The speaker contemplates apocalypse and identity, questioning whether fate is perception while asserting a cyclical infinite existence where humans are trapped searching for purpose.","opus":"The metalcore band stage a doomsday reckoning at hell's threshold, sensing the end bearing down, then swerve into a stubborn affirmation of selfhood. Minds clash toward clarity, destiny becomes a matter of perception, and the infinite self hunts for the flavour of being alive as time loops back to the start, dread folded into a defiant sense of who they'll always be.","sonnet":"A meditation on consciousness and time at the edge of collapse — standing near what feels like hell's gate, the speaker weighs the gap between what the mind trusts and what it rejects, between fate as perception and change as the only constant. The refrain that this is simply who we are and will always be arrives not as resignation but as a strange, dry-eyed acceptance of the infinite.","src":"haiku"},
"cursetheknife~low":{"haiku":"Exhaustion meets expectation endlessly—the speaker stands ready to surrender yet no rescue arrives, only repetition.","opus":"A depressive plea dressed as devotion, where the singer waits to be rescued because he can't move himself. The real subject is the exhaustion of a mind whose hopes always arrive and leave on schedule, never staying.","sonnet":"A speaker at their lowest point holds themselves open and ready for rescue — 'I'm ready for you, so come and save me' — while expectations keep cycling through and dissolving; the readiness is total but the arrival remains conditional.","src":"haiku"},
"cursetheknife~the-gift":{"haiku":"Frustrated recognition of a one-sided cycle where vulnerability is wasted on someone unchanging and indifferent.","opus":"The slow humiliation of loving someone who was never worth it, the narrator flogging himself as the fool for not seeing it sooner, then turning that accusation outward because self-blame and blame trade places when a bond curdles.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped in self-recrimination over someone who stays stuck in their head calls themselves the fool for not knowing better, then flips the label onto the other person — the short, looping lyric performs the circular trap of a relationship where neither can fully leave.","src":"haiku"},
"cusp~win":{"haiku":"Human ambition and control are futile against inevitable death; everyone loses in the end regardless of wealth, power, or will.","opus":"A cold reckoning aimed at someone who hoards and controls, told through desert imagery of scorched earth. The leveler is mortality itself: whatever you win, you're no different from anyone, and everyone loses in the end.","sonnet":"A controlling, possibly violent figure — white truck, raised bed, hideout — is told that no amount of domination beats death: somebody wins, they can't beat death, we all lose in the end. The equalising logic of mortality is the only argument left.","src":"haiku"},
"cut-copy~cold-water":{"haiku":"Chasing freedom and escape with a partner; letting go and falling into the unknown.","opus":"Synth-pop euphoria about surrendering completely to love, disorientation and free-fall so total the narrator can't tell up from down. Beneath the shimmer is an admission that he'll never be free of it.","sonnet":"Disorientation in a city at night, anchored only by the memory of another person — the speaker oscillates between falling and floating, between the present blur of lights and the past warmth of being needed, unable to escape love even in the flood.","src":"haiku"},
"cvlte~eepy-exe":{"haiku":"Sleep eludes while nightmares count sheep backward. Madness arrives through insomnia—the favorite nightmare feeds.","opus":"Insomnia and reversed sleep rendered in bilingual glitch-pop, counting sheep in vain while worst-day visions replay; a sweet-voiced nightmare figure taunts the sleepless narrator as their own favorite terror.","sonnet":"A song about the inverted circadian rhythm of depression or anxiety — the narrator is exhausted during the day but can't sleep at night, counting sheep endlessly while visions of their worst days flood in when they close their eyes, the sleep deprivation taking on a darkly playful, almost glitchy quality.","src":"haiku"},
"cvlte~happy-feat":{"opus":"cvlte works the bilingual seam between English trap-metal and Japanese confession, and here the language switch does the wounding. The narrator numbs on pills and alcohol, performing happiness while unraveling, until the Japanese verse names the root: a drug-loving father who beat the weeping mother. A child fled to Tokyo, fell into the same habit, and now apologizes to her, addiction traced back as inheritance.","sonnet":"A bilingual song about addiction as inherited trauma: the narrator is depressed, numbing on pills and alcohol, performing happiness while secretly unraveling — and the Japanese verse reveals the root: a father who loved drugs more than the family and beat the crying mother, leaving a child who fled to Tokyo, stumbled into the same habit, and now apologizes to her.","src":"sonnet"},
"cynthoni~long-division":{"opus":"A long-distance relationship dividing two people into strangers who have to run from each other, the map useless once doubt set in. The bittersweet close admits the numbness of the split doesn't even hurt as much as it should, once so meaningful, now dull.","sonnet":"A long-distance relationship slowly divided two people into strangers — the cautious excitement, the doubt, and finally the quiet relief of realizing the slow drift apart is no longer painful.","src":"sonnet"},
"cynthoni~take-flight-earth-noid":{"haiku":"Virtual reality promises romantic escape and freedom; digital connection feels as authentic as physical touch.","opus":"A seduction into virtual escape, insisting a love lived through screens and code is as real as anything physical. The invitation to soar together in a digital heaven treats online intimacy as genuine liberation, not lesser life.","sonnet":"An invitation to abandon physical limitations and live fully in virtual reality — the speaker frames VR connection as genuine love and real experience, urging a partner to 'take flight' and build a life together in digital space, insisting it's as true as anything in the physical world.","src":"haiku"},
"cypecore~i-ll-be-back":{"haiku":"Catastrophic collapse due to indifference and folly renders resistance futile, leaving only despair and conquered survival.","opus":"A machine-apocalypse warning aimed at the reckless, humanity given a final chance it squanders until the terminators erase civilization. The stance is cold judgment, the enemy hiding inside our own minds and expectations.","sonnet":"A machine-voiced reckoning: humanity's irresponsibility has consumed its final chances, and a ruthless machine intelligence — unstoppable, eliminating hope and thread alike — declares it is time to delete civilisation's expectations and accept the verdict.","src":"haiku"},
"cypecore~spirals":{"haiku":"Mechanistic existence strips humanity as the speaker descends into spiritual void; divinity corrupts and the speaker becomes shell animated by machine logic.","opus":"A human consciousness dissolving into a machine, mourning the loss of its own reality as it spirals downward, betrayed and stripped of purpose, sworn to a manufactured god.","sonnet":"A disembodied speaker — \"I am not real\" — describes a human shell assembled around an inanimate core, spiralling downward into a machine-god's service, where reality has corroded into darkness and existence has been defiled. The final image of a bird eating its young to survive as king collapses the distinction between victim and perpetrator of the dehumanising system.","src":"haiku"},
"czyk~dissipate":{"haiku":"Sudden abandonment shatters continuity; the speaker dissolves when severed from the person who anchored them, losing coherence without connection.","opus":"Grief at a sudden abandonment: still feeling the vanished lover's touch, the speaker cannot accept how someone could claim to be fine while dissolving everything they had into nothing.","sonnet":"A person dissolves after being abandoned — \"took a chance at fate, now I dissipate\" — unable to understand how the other can claim to be okay while refusing to meet their eyes, lost in the storm with nowhere left to go.","src":"haiku"},
"d-mad-devil~already-infected":{"haiku":"Infection spreads while mercy disappears. Survival fails—sinking requires embrace.","opus":"A survival-horror scenario of an unstoppable infection turning the narrator against those he loves, begging to be killed before he can hurt them; hope collapses as no one makes it out alive.","sonnet":"A song about being consumed by something evil from the inside out — whether read as literal disease, mental illness, or possession, the narrator loses control of their senses and impulses, begins wanting to destroy what they love, and begs those around them to run or kill them before the infection spreads further.","src":"haiku"},
"d-r-i~abduction":{"haiku":"Children vanish from playgrounds—abducted by predators, drowned in rivers, buried six feet under. The song catalogs missing children, asking whether sick abductors sold them to slavery or if they simply ran.","opus":"A blunt confrontation with child abductions, tallying the vanished faces on cartons and shop posters and the bleak likelihood that few return alive, then spitting disgust at the predators responsible. D.R.I. pioneered crossover thrash, fusing hardcore punk's social fury with metal velocity, and this plays exactly to that mode: a real-world nightmare compressed into short, snarled bursts of moral revulsion.","sonnet":"A blunt-force documentary about child abductions — the missing appear on milk cartons, witnesses give descriptions, and outcomes range from runaways to bodies in rivers. The song refuses to moralize beyond pointing at the perpetrators' sickness, cycling back to the unanswerable question of where the children go and who can say why.","src":"haiku"},
"d-r-i~beneath-the-wheel":{"haiku":"Society pressures young people to compete ruthlessly and constantly exceed themselves in school and work, destroying those who lag behind.","opus":"D.R.I., pioneers of crossover thrash bridging hardcore punk and metal, aim their velocity at the ideology of velocity itself. The relentless barked commands to run, cheat, and stay ahead satirize a rat-race that grinds stragglers under its wheel, treating school as unpaid labor. The sarcastic closing shrug, that you can catch up in the end, mocks the whole doomed sprint.","sonnet":"Competitive society runs on a merciless treadmill — keep pace or get crushed. School, career, and social standing all demand relentless acceleration, framing any slowdown as personal failure. Even those who sprint flat-out may find the wheel is already rolling over them.","src":"haiku"},
"dabin~hollow-phaseone-remix":{"haiku":"Hedonistic excess and transgression dominate the narrative, celebrating wildness and disregard for propriety or consequence.","opus":"The lyric here is a hijacked rap hype vocal about turning up, money and bravado, bolted onto a bass remix. Whatever Dabin's original melancholy, this version is pure club adrenaline with no interior to read.","sonnet":"A hype-crowd call-and-response built around the performer stripping performative limits away — taking clothes off, going wild, turning up — structured as a circular energy exchange between artist and audience rather than a lyrical narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"daddy-rock~cuando":{"haiku":"Someone desperately seeks validation and connection after repeated emotional letdowns, questioning whether they've found something real or if they're doomed to repeat the same cycle of heartbreak they've always known.","opus":"Despite the Spanish title, the lyric is in English, an anxious bid for connection from a narrator battered by old failures yet ready to gamble on more pain because he suspects this person is the one. Worn down and doubting his own worth, he exposes himself completely. The mood is earnest pop-rock vulnerability, hope wrestling exhaustion.","sonnet":"Someone still raw from repeated heartache takes a cautious, frightened step toward a new connection. The narrator fears being nothing, needs to feel seen, and despite knowing how badly love has hurt before, decides the risk of opening up again is worth taking.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~115":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic reckoning and messianic violence emerge as the speaker assumes role of destroyer, announcing plague and transformation as inevitable consequence of judgment.","opus":"Voiced through Call of Duty's Nazi-zombie mythology, a malevolent resurrected force gloating over the unmaking of everything, its army of the dead rising while the living cling uselessly to survival.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic narrator who has returned as the left hand of the sacred, commanding an army of flies and bringing death and pestilence: everyone dies, everyone lies, they wait for a second coming that will not save them, and the speaker demands \"bring me 115\" as the element that will end all creation.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~alchemy":{"haiku":"Poisoned temptation dissolves willpower; the speaker offers themselves as transformative curse, dragging others through corruption with seductive inevitability.","opus":"A tempter's voice offering forbidden knowledge and oblivion in a poisoned cup, promising to guide you through while the greed it feeds drowns you like a flood. The seduction is the trap, the thing within you turned into the wound.","sonnet":"A seductive, addictive substance or entity that promises to guide you through oblivion and know what bleeds inside you — the poison cup floods in like a river and drowns the person who drinks from it.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~dark-legacy":{"haiku":"Jealousy and loneliness breed toxic obsession, with the narrator willing to sacrifice everything for a corrosive love.","opus":"Desire framed as damnation, a soul-taker consumed by a toxic obsession they know leads to hell and choose anyway. The tension is between fear of the fire and a craving strong enough to burn in it willingly.","sonnet":"Consumed by jealousy, neglect, and a toxin-like desire, the speaker knowingly follows someone into hell — afraid of fire and dark but unable to resist — understanding the crimson vice leads to self-destruction yet declaring they would do it all again.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~dreamweaver":{"haiku":"Paranoia and internal demons manifest as tangible threats that erode the boundary between perception and reality during restless isolation.","opus":"A panic attack rendered as a haunting, where paranoia and dread stalk the sleepless narrator and even memory can't be trusted anymore. The horror is that the monsters are perception itself, tethered to a mind turning on its owner.","sonnet":"Paranoia devours the speaker's sense of reality: devils come at night, shadows animate, and a gathering fear that memory itself cannot be trusted leaves them asking \"am I insane?\" and unable to sleep.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~mortal":{"haiku":"Indecision and buried secrets corrode connection as the speaker avoids confrontation until everything shatters.","opus":"About the slow poison of avoidance in a relationship, hiding behind lies and half-truths while patience quietly runs out. The betrayal builds so gradually and casually that the other person never sees it forming.","sonnet":"Patience finally exhausted by someone who has built their lies in shallow depths, the speaker — jaded, not aggravated — watches a casual, gradual change bloom and decides they simply can no longer believe the other person.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~nascent":{"haiku":"Progress built upon ruin demands transcendence over memory, as consciousness ascends beyond monuments while predecessors fade into oblivion.","opus":"A strange, tender address to someone slipping away, mind or memory unraveling, with the narrator promising to walk into that madness alongside them. Progress and meaning here are built from ruin, and even the guide eventually fades from the mind he's holding.","sonnet":"A guiding voice addresses someone tangled in aimlessness and grief, promising to accompany them through madness while admitting that all human progress is built on ruin — \"the stone crumbles under your step\" as the speaker quietly departs.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~night-mother":{"haiku":"Betrayal and false facades collapse relationships; authenticity crumbles as words and promises reveal themselves as empty fabrication.","opus":"About willing self-deception inside a manipulative bond, the naivety of following someone who is clearly untrustworthy. She'd walk into fire if he told her it was safe, mistaking the warmth of belonging for security while suspicion quietly grows.","sonnet":"Awakening inside a manipulative relationship — the narrator followed blindly, convinced themselves they were safe even as something whispered \"do not trust him,\" and now watches the deception bloom into a garden of deceit. The repeated \"I would burn in a fire if you told me to stay\" maps naive love as a slow march into flames the narrator kept rationalising as warmth.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~nirn":{"haiku":"Exhausted acknowledgment of one-sided sacrifice to an alter ego that can't be satisfied, no matter the offering.","opus":"Named for the Elder Scrolls mortal world, it turns a fantasy escape into a portrait of a devouring relationship where one person sacrifices everything to feed a partner's buried ego, exhaustion running under every offering.","sonnet":"Addressed to someone who keeps sacrificing everything to satisfy a version of another person that never fully shows up — the speaker observes that these offerings are made in vain to feed an alter ego that won't surface, questioning whether the giver even truly lives or just exists to be consumed.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~only":{"haiku":"Earthly ritual and devotion transform the speaker into stone altar for another's divine will and design.","opus":"Devotion cast as pagan surrender to a mother-earth deity, wandering home toward the voice in the roots and offering to become an altar. It reads as a longing for belonging so total it dissolves the self into worship.","sonnet":"A devotional surrender to a nature-spirit or earth-mother presence: the speaker pleads to be led home, offers to become an altar — made of stone, loyal, unfaltering — following the voice of the root mother into the eventide as the only design worth living by.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~sand-tiger":{"haiku":"A ghost surrenders to oblivion while captured by another's gravity, the speaker dissolves into memory carved in stone.","opus":"A soul at the close of its path, prepared to scatter into nothingness, feeling unheard and spectral, yet pleading for a reason to remain. Torn between surrender and a magnetic pull toward another, it treats this limbo as neither an ending nor a rest — terrified and adrift in a merciless forever.","sonnet":"A person at the edge of giving up who finds a reason to stay in another person's gravitational pull — terrified but held in place, surrounded by the forgotten and the damned, negotiating between surrender and the one force strong enough to make them reconsider.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~the-other-terror":{"haiku":"Murderous impulse and conscience collapse into unified horror as the narrator confronts their own capacity for violence and emptiness.","opus":"A dissociative spiral of intrusive violent thoughts, a mind unsure whether it has actually killed or only imagined it. The horror is watching one's own face go unfamiliar, tasting the first bitterness of possible remorse.","sonnet":"A dissociated speaker suspects they have done something irreversible — \"I think I've killed now\" — and finds their mouth full of ash and blades, straying toward the brink of insanity while asking whether anyone else knows this feeling of ripping out conscience with no will to fight it.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~titan":{"haiku":"Goodness becomes its own parasite; the speaker saves the world only to discover internal decay consuming what remains.","opus":"A savior figure hollowed out by the very thing he battles, the strain and self-doubt he carries figured as a cancer of his own making, so that saving the world leaves him dissociated and drowning inside.","sonnet":"Despite saving the world, the speaker is consumed by parasitic forces that dislocate, drain, and strip them of voice — \"if I've done so much good, then what makes this feel so wrong\" — disassociating while cancer mutates through every cell and the colors fade.","src":"haiku"},
"daedric~undone":{"haiku":"Catastrophic relationship dynamics wound both parties permanently, with undoing the only path to revealing truth beneath damage.","opus":"Post-mortem of a mutually ruinous relationship, begging time to rewind to the mistakes and asking whether either person would have changed. Being undone means letting the wound show, accepting you were both villain and victim.","sonnet":"A mutual wound is held up to the light: the speaker asks to be made undone so the damage can finally be seen for what it is, recognising they drew the other person too close and made them a casualty, while wishing time could rewind to before the cascading mistakes.","src":"haiku"},
"daemon-grey~gothy-love":{"haiku":"Romantic devotion merges death wish and victimhood, celebrating willingness to perish for a beloved nightmare.","opus":"Goth romance played with a wink, love as martyrdom and a bed of knives, devotion measured in willingness to die. The genre's death-obsession turned into flirtation, sincere and self-aware at once.","sonnet":"A devotional gothic courtship in which the speaker offers themselves entirely as a willing victim — laying down on a bed of knives, intertwining twisted souls — declaring their lover the nightmare of their dreams and pledging to die for them with cheerful sincerity.","src":"haiku"},
"daft-punk~get-lucky-official-audio-ft-pharrell-williams":{"haiku":"Perpetual pursuit of pleasure and luck through connection transcends fatigue and mortality, grounded in cyclical renewal.","opus":"An all-night celebration of chance and connection, phoenix-rebirth imagery dressed as disco. Beneath the groove is Daft Punk's fondness for endurance and reinvention, staying up because who you are is worth not giving up.","sonnet":"A night of mutual desire framed against cosmic forces — the planet's spin, the legend of the phoenix, ends that are beginnings — resolves into a simple, joyful chase: two people up all night, fuelled by the force from the beginning, trying to get lucky.","src":"haiku"},
"daft-punk~harder-better-faster-stronger":{"haiku":"Work never ends; relentless demand for self-improvement and productivity. The cycle repeats endlessly, intensifying pressure to exceed limits.","opus":"Daft Punk reduce the productivity gospel to a looping mantra of vocoded imperatives, work never ending, hour after hour. Built from chopped and rearranged fragments, the track enacts its own theme: labor as an endlessly recombined cycle. The French duo's robot personas turn self-optimization into something mechanical and hypnotic, celebration and warning fused in one relentless groove.","sonnet":"An endless productivity loop where labor is its own justification. Each cycle of effort is supposed to improve on the last, pushing output higher and duration longer until the work itself becomes the horizon — looping, escalating, and never declared complete.","src":"haiku"},
"daft-punk~one-more-time":{"haiku":"A celebration of dance and music as pure joy and freedom, expressing euphoria and the desire to keep dancing indefinitely without stopping.","opus":"The French duo's ecstatic anthem is built almost entirely on a call to keep celebrating and dancing, vocoder-warped joy letting a crowd cut loose. Its text is deliberately spare, leaving repetition and euphoria to carry everything. As the opening of the Discovery era, it announced Daft Punk's shift toward warm, disco-soaked, unashamed dancefloor bliss.","sonnet":"Pure celebration in an eternal present tense: music unlocks freedom and the only imperative is to keep moving, keep dancing. There is no narrative arc here — just the sustained exhilaration of communal joy refusing to end.","src":"haiku"},
"daft-punk~technologic":{"haiku":"Relentless catalogue of tech actions (buying, breaking, fixing, uploading, deleting)—the song captures consumer obsession with constant interaction with digital devices.","opus":"The French electronic duo build the whole track from clipped imperative verbs, a robotic litany of buy, use, break and fix that mirrors compulsive consumer interaction with gadgets. Their machine-voiced delivery turns the chant of clicking, ripping and upgrading into satire, the endless commands exposing how technology dictates a restless cycle of use and disposal.","sonnet":"An exhaustive inventory of every action a person performs with a digital device, delivered as relentless imperative. The accumulation of commands becomes its own commentary on the compulsive, frictionless churn of consumer technology and the way it colonizes every moment of attention.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~bloody-knees":{"opus":"A toxic, self-harming relationship charged with knife imagery, hands around the neck, and blood in the snow; intimacy and violence blur as the narrator numbs out, dissociates, and both people rot from the inside while claiming to feel alive.","sonnet":"A volatile, emotionally abusive relationship where both parties wound each other — the narrator feels disconnected and wants out while simultaneously fixated, and the imagery of blood in snow, hands around necks, and self-inflicted wounds maps the physical toll of their entanglement.","src":"sonnet"},
"daine~boythots":{"haiku":"Female sexual agency and indifference weaponize power; the speaker treats desire as transaction and refuses emotional entanglement.","opus":"A brash flip of the objectifying script—the narrator degrades a disposable boy the way women are usually degraded, flaunting money, drugs, and detachment over feelings. Status and independence trump any suitor who can't match her bag.","sonnet":"A hyper-confident, sexually aggressive persona piece — the speaker positions themselves as a hard, drug-fueled party animal who commands boys sexually, rejects fake friends, counts money in the back, and treats male sexual attention as something to take and discard on her own terms.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~cut-my-heart-out":{"haiku":"Love as surgical wound: incision replaces connection, and what's removed is whatever block prevents feeling the cold seduction of absence.","opus":"Craving a cold, wounding love so intensely it plays out as literal open-heart surgery, wanting to be frozen and cut open just to feel something real. Numbness turns pain into the only proof of connection.","sonnet":"A song about emotional numbness in a relationship where the cold of the other person's love is the only thing keeping the speaker going — asking to be literally cut open and frozen because that surgical absence of warmth is strangely sustaining.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~doom":{"haiku":"Manipulation and performance collapse into revelation, truth costs everything while false devotion fills the void, culminating in violence born from numbness.","opus":"The hollow performance of intimacy, saying whatever's wanted while feeling nothing, staged as a hyperpop-star fantasy of power and detachment. The cold flex of being untouchable curdles into something darker, an apology for a closeness the speaker cannot allow.","sonnet":"A dissociation between emotional honesty and the performative self — the speaker admits to saying whatever is wanted, being numb and untouchable while being worshipped, then turns violent: physically choking the person they're pushing away, knowing the relationship is ending but unable to let anyone close.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~eighteen":{"haiku":"Unrequited feeling persists across time and distance; the bond remains unspoken and unresolved.","opus":"A near-relationship that never quite happened, mourned in retrospect: late drives and a boy who worked at a morgue, an intimacy so strong it aches precisely because it stayed unspoken and unfinished.","sonnet":"A song about an ambiguous near-relationship with someone who worked at a morgue — late-night drives, no kiss, no official bond — that ends in his departure from her hometown; she's left wondering if it was ever love at all, certain only that nothing ever made her feel that way before.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~i-run-my-hands-thru-it":{"haiku":"Dissolution of self through touch: reality becomes tangible only through physical contact, yet the more you grasp, the more it slips away into abstraction.","opus":"Handing your heart over to possessions and gripping a familiar tune for comfort, chasing something that only turns solid under stage lights. The hurt never lets up even while she gives the crowd exactly what it came for.","sonnet":"A song about losing yourself to material or surface-level things while craving something that actually feels real — performing for others and giving everything while the weight of being unseen and unsupported pulls you down.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~i-want-the-light-to-swallow-me-whole":{"haiku":"Absence becomes unbearable as the speaker drowns in attachment they cannot control. Silence kills more effectively than presence, leaving only the wound of withdrawal.","opus":"The Australian artist maps a codependent spiral where the same person is both sickness and home. It's the push-pull of an unhealthy attachment, needing someone who hurts you and questioning whether you're even wanted at all.","sonnet":"An ambivalent entanglement — the other person makes breathing hard, made the speaker hate things they loved, yet their absence is worse — collapses into the admission that the relationship was delusional; 'you pushed my whole body to the floor yet somehow you're the one to fall' frames the dynamic as mutual destruction with unclear cause.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~make-it-right":{"haiku":"Patterns repeat despite apology; the speaker cannot break cycles of their own making despite desperate attempts at reconciliation.","opus":"An apology that knows it can't undo the damage, the narrator forever tangling things worse, begging to be saved and loved yet admitting again and again that there's no fixing it. Falling apart becomes the only way left to feel alive.","sonnet":"About the impossibility of repairing a relationship the speaker knows they keep damaging — they apologize, beg for love, want to be saved, but keep cycling back into the same destructive patterns; the refrain of being unable to make it right is both a confession and a surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~portal":{"haiku":"Transformation demands departure from familiar spaces, the speaker cycles through change while opening portals to new versions of themselves.","opus":"The disorienting rush of sudden fame and self-reinvention, seeing your face on posters and feeling like a stranger to yourself. The portal is an escape into constant change, restless and impatient to shed the old life even if it's all just for show.","sonnet":"A song about personal transformation as a passage through a threshold — the speaker is becoming someone new, impatient with their former self, seeing synchronicity everywhere, and inviting others to follow them through the portal into whoever they are becoming.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~pqc":{"haiku":"Damage and obsession leave the narrator unable to release despite recognising toxicity, trapped in compassion's compulsion.","opus":"Attachment as self-harm, unable to let go of a person who is plastic and tragic even while wounds and public lies pile up. The hyperpop-emo core is compassion weaponized against oneself, love that keeps casting the very thing that hurts.","sonnet":"A speaker who lost money, accumulated new scars, and lied to the press admits they give more than permitted to someone who could take it all again — hopelessly attaching, overly compassionate, casting love as a weapon they cannot stop wielding.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~salt-feat-oli-sykes":{"opus":"The agony of betrayal by a lover who twists the knife and pours salt in the narrator's wounds; once she drew hearts around his name, now she draws blood, and love has curdled into wanting to hear him scream.","sonnet":"A relationship that has curdled from love into deliberate cruelty — the other person keeps reopening wounds rather than letting them heal, and the narrator, who once adored them, now burns with the desire to watch them suffer for the damage they caused.","src":"sonnet"},
"daine~shades-on-feat-kreayshawn":{"haiku":"Numbing dissociation enables survival; the speaker self-medicates with ketamine to feel nothing while perpetually performing invulnerability for crowd gratification.","opus":"A hedonistic club portrait of ketamine use pushed to the edge of a k-hole, numb, wild and lying to everyone. The bravado about money and drugs barely covers the wreckage of overdoses and disconnection it names.","sonnet":"Two performers boast about arriving to a club completely dissociated on ketamine, demanding payment before performing, and generally not caring about anything beyond drugs and money.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~skin-deep":{"haiku":"Superficial judgment meets calculated distance as the speaker maintains emotional walls against invasion. Possession of desire becomes a weapon to secure independence.","opus":"A defiant flip on a would-be heartbreaker, mixing hyperpop bravado with genuine wound. She closes the heart she once opened and weaponizes being wanted, but the fraud and homesickness underneath leak through the swagger.","sonnet":"A speaker who has been scrutinised, talked about, and dismissed as 'imaginary' turns the dynamic around — 'top down, pop out, pull up like a pop-star' — asserting that she has everything they want and pushing away anyone who gets close before they can do damage, the closing line 'you gave me your heart and I broke it' delivered without apology.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~smb2l":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness masks internal turmoil as the speaker oscillates between detachment and desperate need. Clouds meaning while smiling or frowning becomes impossible to distinguish.","opus":"About emotional depletion inside a one-sided relationship, unable to love someone back while sacrificing your own healing to prop them up. The honesty was always there; the exhaustion of being buried by another person's needs is the real ache.","sonnet":"A speaker who warned their partner from the start that they couldn't give love finds the relationship has buried them alive — 'feeling like I'm 6 feet deep when you're around' — calling out the other's self-righteousness and refusal to own the conflict, while admitting their own emotional numbness makes meaning impossible to read.","src":"haiku"},
"daine~when-my-wings-are-cut-off":{"src":"web","web":"Daine on losing your sense of self inside a relationship — the inability to create, feel or trust your own instincts; the 'wings' are artistic and emotional vitality, and the song sits with being grounded to nothing by someone else's presence.","fable":"daine builds the whole song around a single grim condition: affection that only becomes possible once the beloved has been maimed into staying, an angel grounded so it can never leave. The refrain refuses to dignify that bargain as devotion, and the defensive body language threaded through the verses — the folded posture, the insistence that a partner has been demoted from worshipped figure to ordinary betrayer — is the wariness of someone who has learned what tenderness costs when it arrives with a blade. Where the track sharpens is the bracketed second voice underneath the chorus, which begs not to be trained into this dependence and states plainly that severing the bond is the only thing being requested, so the escape the title keeps circling gets flipped outward, from the injury of being clipped into a demand for release. That practiced ease of surrender reads less like calm than like resignation rehearsed until it finally stopped hurting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","freedom & escape","shame & repression"],"means":"affection made possible only by maiming the beloved into staying","built":"a bracketed second voice beneath the chorus begs for release rather than the wound"},
"daine~writhe":{"haiku":"Language breaks down under pressure: words fail to bridge the gap between what was said and what was felt, leaving only sound without meaning.","opus":"Refusing to absorb someone else's guilt and demands, feeling lighter for detaching even as they beg and rewrite history. It's the calm of no longer being sorry for not being what they wanted.","sonnet":"A song about detaching from a toxic relationship with cold clarity — the speaker is done fighting, feels lighter for it, but refuses to be gaslit by someone who is now performing honesty after years of biting and screaming, calling it all drone noise.","src":"haiku"},
"daisy-grenade~king-for-a-day-feat-kasey-karlsen-of-deadlands":{"haiku":"Desperation and intoxication breed existential collapse while fame and celebrity become hollow prizes masking inner ruin.","opus":"Youth rage and self-destruction glamorized then interrogated, fame and intoxication as poison, martyrdom offered up in exhaustion. Under the party-suicide bravado is a plea to feel nothing rather than keep begging and losing.","sonnet":"A speaker saturated in intoxication, paranoia, and self-destruction imagines being king for a single night without ghosts in the walls, while a second voice — born without a voice, caught in street-youth rage — declares dying a gift and frames the whole spiral as a revolution from below.","src":"haiku"},
"dak-ta~run":{"haiku":"Restless flight and untethered wandering; the speaker abandons home seeking freedom and escape from constraint.","opus":"A fugitive's crawl through the desert, sobering up as guilt and unpaid debts close in like circling vultures. It's a Western-tinged reckoning with irreversible sin, a broken man running from a self he can't outpace.","sonnet":"Flight across a desert as penance — the narrator left home with a whiskey wolf and debts unpaid, and now marches through pitch black with vultures circling, lightning striking, and sin boiling in the sun. The refrain \"Lord I can't change what I've done / a broken rebel on the run\" is both confession and elegy; the desert is guilt made landscape.","src":"haiku"},
"dal-av~insanely-illegal-cage-fight":{"haiku":"Raw aggression and violent domination; the speaker burns through restraint to inflict destruction on opponents and rivals alike.","opus":"Pure combat bloodlust styled as cage-fight theater, the fighter as primal king who kills rather than knocks out. The undercurrent is that survival is bought with violence when the money is in your hands and weakness isn't affordable.","sonnet":"A fighter narrating from inside the cage with the crowd's money in their hands and no choice but to paint the town red — primal, addicted to the ring, explicitly choosing to kill rather than knock out. The power fantasy is undercut by the financial coercion: \"their money's in my hands, no choice, I can't afford to be weak.\"","src":"haiku"},
"damageplan~pride":{"haiku":"Betrayal and condescension strip away dignity, leaving only pride as the last possession that cannot be bought or stolen.","opus":"Dimebag and Vinnie Paul's post-Pantera band channeling real betrayal, the sting of being looked down on by someone who owes you respect. When everything else is stripped away, pride is the last thing left to hold.","sonnet":"Someone who has been betrayed, looked down on, and had everything they considered sacred turned black is left with only their self-respect, which no amount of money can compensate for or replace. Pride is framed as both the last possession and the only one that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"damon-r~bf847":{"haiku":"Rejection of artifice and pretense; the speaker dismisses false personas and seeks authenticity over hollow performance.","opus":"A cocky come-on to someone already spoken for, offering to be confidant, bad influence and partner all rolled into one. The bravado barely masks possessiveness, a threat of chaos tucked inside seductive persistence.","sonnet":"A persistent pursuer who ignores the fact that the girl has a boyfriend — dismissing it as irrelevant, cycling through best friend and worst friend and boyfriend in the same breath. The possession is stated as inevitability rather than request; you'll see me again, I can make your world end, the confidence tipping into something much darker than flirtation.","src":"haiku"},
"damon-r~kiss-up":{"haiku":"Absence and longing define the speaker's state; they wish to inhabit someone else's identity while resisting performative flattery and false connection.","opus":"Envy dressed as a kiss-off: the speaker wishes he were the person he left, even as he swaggers about not wanting them back. Beneath the tough talk about people sucking up to him is someone who feels only half alive.","sonnet":"A kiss that ended something — the speaker has been mentally 'gone' since a moment of intimacy that revealed the relationship was hollow, moving between regret for having idealized the other person and contempt for anyone who would flatter their way through life.","src":"haiku"},
"damon-r~liverounds":{"haiku":"A speaker watches someone slip away while circling obsessively, trapped in self-inflicted paralysis. Guns and abandonment echo through the repetitive refrain.","opus":"A lover paralyzed by the presence of a rival, unable to move forward while watching someone else briefly hold the person he wants; the loaded gun becomes the danger of loving someone already claimed.","sonnet":"A song about jealousy and displacement in a relationship — the narrator watches someone they love be with another person, feels like a stranger in what was once their home, and bitterly notes that she carries live ammunition for another, the image of real bullets underlining the lethality of being replaced.","src":"haiku"},
"damon-r~tricked-out":{"haiku":"Betrayal and reckless abandon spiral into mutual destruction, where both lovers lose control and careen toward inevitable collision.","opus":"A reckless-devotion song welded to car imagery, where two people speed toward mutual destruction on the vow that your life is my life, do or die. Getting tricked out and staying alive blur together into a high-adrenaline pact that could just as easily be a crash.","sonnet":"A high-speed, fragmented song about a relationship where the lines between being alive, being manipulated, and being in freefall are blurred — hatchback, nosedive, red eyes, the sensation of being tricked into something that might end in death or might just mean surviving at twenty-five.","src":"haiku"},
"dan-croll~from-nowhere-baardsen-remix":{"haiku":"Mutual incompatibility persists despite attraction; the speaker cycles between emotional investment and retreat, unable to bridge the gap between desire and reality.","opus":"The dizzy, off-balance rush of falling for someone, control slipping every time you catch their eyes. It's infatuation as a pleasant unsteadiness, lingering somewhere you probably shouldn't simply because nowhere else feels better.","sonnet":"A speaker repeatedly loses their footing in a relationship — falling behind, losing control — drawn back each time by the other person's eyes, even as that person tells them they should have left and the dynamic keeps them perpetually off-balance.","src":"haiku"},
"danalogue-alabaster-deplume~you-re-doing-very-well":{"haiku":"Affirmation and encouragement acknowledge struggle while insisting that persistent vulnerability constitutes success.","opus":"A gentle spoken benediction, repeating reassurance to someone barely holding on and urging them to be as much themselves as possible. Its whole power is tenderness offered without conditions to a person who feels they cannot escape.","sonnet":"A short, unconditional reassurance: you are doing very well, it is not always easy, be as much yourself as you possibly can — everyone needs you, and no one knows how to escape.","src":"haiku"},
"dance-gavin-dance~all-the-way-down":{"haiku":"Manufactured unhappiness drives performances of suffering while elite systems decay beneath false narratives and lab-grown compliance.","opus":"A jaded swipe at a culture that rewards performed unhappiness and elite manipulation, with a nod to late Lil B collaborator ties. The band turns disillusionment about art, control, and manufactured misery into something defiant rather than resigned.","sonnet":"Cynicism about performative victimhood and cultural collapse frames a broader despair — \"lab grown cockroach meat\" and abandoned dreams — while the speaker oscillates between genuine grief for a generation and black-comic acceptance of the descent.","src":"haiku"},
"dance-gavin-dance~cream-of-the-crop":{"haiku":"Navigation of fame and ego demands authenticity while resisting commodification, balancing self-preservation with artistic integrity.","opus":"Advice to an artist about staying true amid fame's distortions, keeping ego from exploding and guns stuck to your own vision. The swervy questions mask a real anxiety about selling out and surviving the pitchfork of the crowd.","sonnet":"A meditation on creative identity under pressure: are we the real thing or caricatures, pandas on camels or cream of the crop? The answer is to check your ego, stick to your guns, and come back steadier than ever rather than selling out for the sake of the pitchfork.","src":"haiku"},
"dance-gavin-dance~death-of-a-strawberry":{"haiku":"A young hedonist exhausted by mortality anxiety wants to abandon restraint and spend recklessly with a beautiful companion while doubting whether anything matters.","opus":"The post-hardcore band voices someone fleeing his terror of death into reckless indulgence, wanting to squander his money and time on pleasure. The dizzying wordplay dresses denial up as cleverness and self-protection, but underneath the mania is a simple wish to feel young and untroubled again amid the ache of being alive.","sonnet":"Someone overwhelmed by anxiety and mortality fantasizes about escaping into hedonism and pretending to be richer and more carefree than he is. Denial is elevated to a coping strategy, and the whole song is a bright performance masking the fear it cannot quite outrun.","src":"haiku"},
"dance-gavin-dance~pop-off":{"haiku":"Predatory seduction and toxic attraction breed mutual destruction, with the narrator caught in cycles of want and resentment.","opus":"Toxic infatuation dissected in real time, knowing this person is bad blood and possessive yet succumbing anyway. Desire overriding need, friendships and self dissolved into an obsession the singer sees clearly and still can't quit.","sonnet":"An obsessive, toxic attraction — cherry-red claws, elegant fangs, bad blood everywhere — draws the speaker into isolation, dissolving friendships and ignoring calls; they know it ends badly and want it anyway, while a closing ode to loyal pets offers an ironic counterpoint.","src":"haiku"},
"dance-gavin-dance~war-machine":{"haiku":"Trauma begets violence as the narrator becomes a weaponised entity, greedy for dominance while searching for impossible peace.","opus":"A raw reckoning with trauma and self-loathing dressed as bravado, admitting how much easier it is to weaponize pain than feel it. The war machine is the performing self, grinning through grief while genuinely searching for guidance and serenity.","sonnet":"Trauma's obstruction of growth is confessed openly: the speaker wanted to change but couldn't do it alone, and has become invested in an internal war machine — judgment from others means nothing, but the authentic reckoning with one's own chaos remains the only path to building a masterpiece.","src":"haiku"},
"danger~11-50-feat-lil-brain":{"haiku":"A song about numbness, disconnection, and the inability to feel pain or emotional response despite seeking meaning and divine contact.","opus":"A numb, looping plea about seeing God yet feeling nothing, begging a departed lover to say it again while noting that a broken heart brings no pain anymore. Danger is the French electronic producer known for masked, cinematic synth work stamped with clock-time titles, and here the emotional flatness of the repeated lines becomes the point, dissociation rendered as a hypnotic, hollow refrain over the Lil Brain feature.","sonnet":"Emotional numbness dressed in spiritual language — the speaker catches glimpses of something divine but feels nothing, wishes for heartbreak as proof of being alive, and keeps circling back to a plea for someone to tell them what they saw. It is a quiet crisis of dissociation, the gap between transcendence witnessed and feeling withheld.","src":"haiku"},
"danger~19-00-feat-tasha-the-amazon":{"haiku":"Escaping town and seeking freedom through movement; the narrator lives by paradox—dying to live and living to die—spinning in circular freedom.","opus":"Fleeing town becomes both escape and rebirth, tires whirling across dry land toward vanishing. The tension of craving life yet courting an end frames disappearance as the one way to quit disappointing anybody.","sonnet":"A declaration of deliberate disappearance — spinning through the desert, not wanting to be found, the freedom of having no one to disappoint rendering the line between living and dying almost irrelevant.","src":"haiku"},
"dani-gore~exploding-with-power":{"haiku":"Defiance surges as the speaker rejects all dictation; power erupts from within and silences the voices that demand submission and conformity.","opus":"Defiant self-assertion as intoxication, refusing to be controlled and mistaking a manic surge of ego for godlike strength while trying to outrun an unnamed life inside.","sonnet":"A declaration of defiance and self-transcendence: the speaker refuses to be told what to do, has found a new dimension beyond their demons' reach, and feels godlike — punching down walls, raising their voice, exploding with a power that grows stronger every day. The anger is directed outward but the transformation is internal.","src":"haiku"},
"daniel-avery~a-silent-shadow":{"haiku":"Suppressed emotion circulates beneath surface awareness, hidden yet inescapable.","opus":"A looping meditation on how we mask emotions even as they leak out, feelings whose destination we never control. Ambient-techno minimalism where the repeated phrase becomes the point, the quiet dread of being stuck at the beginning.","sonnet":"Feelings disguised even from the self keep circulating without direction — the question of how this keeps happening goes unanswered, leaving the speaker stuck at the beginning, unable to move beyond the loop of concealed emotion.","src":"haiku"},
"daniel-avery~haze":{"haiku":"Ethereal witness to ecstatic liberation; a figure glides through neon environments, transcendent and unreachable.","opus":"The techno producer catches a fleeting image of someone luminous on a dancefloor, admired through the blur of the night. It's longing kept at a distance, wanting her happiness while sensing it, and she, could vanish at any moment.","sonnet":"An admiring gaze through a crowd: the speaker doesn't claim happiness for either party, only watches her move through neon lights like a queen of the night, making shapes with her hands — I'll see you through the haze is both promise and distance, the warmth of wanting without the weight of possession.","src":"haiku"},
"daniel-johnston~a-little-story":{"haiku":"A redemptive narrative recounts divine sacrifice and resurrection as cosmic theater; grace arrives through willing death and miraculous return.","opus":"Johnston retells the gospel with childlike plainness, the whole arc of Christ's sacrifice as a simple, sincere bedtime story. His unpolished faith is the point: belief stated without irony, God's great idea told the way a child would tell it.","sonnet":"A plainspoken retelling of the Christian gospel — God, His Son, the cruelty of people who killed Him for being good, and the resurrection — offered as a bedtime reminder that one man gave up heaven out of love for humanity.","src":"haiku"},
"daniel-tompkins~ruins":{"opus":"A sweeping hymn of triumph and desire, two people declaring themselves rulers of a fresh era as ancient thrones and their keepers topple. It then plunges into fierce, near-brutal intimacy, all surrender, heat, and destined togetherness.","sonnet":"A dualistic song that shifts between triumphant conquest imagery and intimate sexual surrender — the first half speaks of empires and legacies crumbling, while the second half addresses a lover directly in language of physical domination, tenderness, and violent desire.","src":"sonnet"},
"daniel-tompkins~the-abyss":{"haiku":"Descent into depths as both remedy and trap; the speaker sinks into serenity through surrender to the enemy.","opus":"The TesseracT vocalist frames escape and sedation as surrender to the depths, dissolving into dreams and medication to get away from himself. The pull toward oblivion is seductive, dressed as serenity and divinity even as it means sinking.","sonnet":"A recurring surrender to the depths — remedy, enemy, serenity, divinity all waiting in the same dark drift. The speaker keeps being sent away to take their medicine, slipping into dreams, diving into the setting sun, the abyss not a place of terror but of perverse comfort, the entity in the darkness known and sought.","src":"haiku"},
"danny-blu~6-feet":{"haiku":"Seduction and darkness merge as the speaker promises both terror and transcendence; coffin and fire become settings for ecstatic surrender.","opus":"Seduction dressed in goth-horror imagery, courting a lover into a shared grave where death is offered as the ultimate intimacy, a coffin built for two.","sonnet":"A gothic seduction: the speaker — a self-described monster who doesn't believe in God — invites the other person to meet them six feet under, in a coffin built for two, howling at the moon in cemetery glow. The dark-romance imagery plays horror tropes as flirtation, the thrill of the chill positioned as its own reward.","src":"haiku"},
"danny-blu~the-american-dream":{"haiku":"System digests its own mythology: entertainment and control masquerade as freedom, and the dream itself becomes the trap.","opus":"The American dream voiced as a monstrous, all-owning machine, part media, part gun, part Jesus, that promises to keep you safe while owning your brain. A satirical portrait of consumer nationalism as a controlling god.","sonnet":"A satirical song that ventriloquizes American consumer culture — the speaker inhabits the voice of the system itself, owning your brain through entertainment, printing truth and money, thriving on chaos, and producing a country full of diabetes, plastic, and stress.","src":"haiku"},
"danny-l-harle~me4u":{"haiku":"Total romantic merger dissolves individual boundaries; every action and emotion orbits the beloved, rendering separation existentially impossible.","opus":"PC Music's hyper-sincere take on total devotion, love pushed past the point of self until every part of one belongs to the other. The saccharine repetition is the message: pop's promises of belonging turned up to blissful excess.","sonnet":"A song of complete mutual belonging — every part of the speaker is bound to the beloved, every action done for and through them, framed as a joyful discovery after a long search.","src":"haiku"},
"danzig~am-i-demon":{"haiku":"The singer questions whether he is demonic or human, possessing destructive power that he's learned to wield, embracing rather than denying his darker nature.","opus":"A snarling interrogation of the narrator's own nature, unsure whether he is beast, human, or demon, and relishing the answer once he decides he likes it, everything he needs contained in himself. Glenn Danzig, fresh off the Misfits and Samhain, launched his eponymous band into bluesy, occult-tinged heavy metal, and this early cut trades punk brevity for a swaggering, Elvis-from-hell menace that became his signature.","sonnet":"A swagger-drenched inquiry into one's own nature — surveying a world of hollow faces, the voice asks whether what seethes inside is bestial or merely human, and whether demon status is a condemnation or a distinction worth owning. The final answer tilts toward embrace: if the label fits, so be it.","src":"haiku"},
"danzig~mother":{"haiku":"A dark seductive figure warns parents to keep their children away, offering an alternative world of darkness and sensual knowledge to those curious enough to follow.","opus":"Glenn Danzig's post-Misfits band delivers a sneering provocation to protective parents, daring mothers to shelter their children from him and offering to drag a daughter through his personal underworld. Seduction and menace merge as he goads fathers to join in. It is defiance against wholesome authority worn deliberately as a badge.","sonnet":"A figure of transgression and seduction addresses parents directly, warning them that their sheltered children are already within reach of a darker world he embodies. He is not threatening so much as announcing — the invitation to hell is genuine, the light of propriety simply not visible from where he stands.","src":"haiku"},
"danzig~soul-on-fire":{"haiku":"Seasonal fire in the narrator's soul burns through a lover's cold heart, destroying their world.","opus":"Glenn Danzig laces his bluesy hard rock with pagan ritual and seduction, tying seasonal magic to a threat wrapped in desire. Addressing a lover as both object of longing and target of destruction, his baritone croon makes the vow to burn down her world sound like a courtship, desire smoldering toward ruin.","sonnet":"Samhain — the hinge between the living and the dead — becomes the season of a narrator's darkest spiritual intensity, a fire that will consume coldness wherever it finds it. The beloved is addressed as something to be melted and transformed, the ritual of the season promising to end her world and rebuild it in flame.","src":"haiku"},
"daoko~a-15t35vq":{"haiku":"A person hiding in shadows, choosing comfortable numbness over growth, then suddenly breaking free from self-imposed constraints to discover their own agency and capacity for change.","opus":"Daoko emerged from Japan's underground rap-poetry scene before crossing into mainstream anime pop, and her voice specializes in the shy, marginal narrator who suddenly acts. Here that figure — years spent hiding, choosing bare survival over risk — bolts without a destination and discovers the sky opening where she once read only bad luck. The advice she ignored finally lands, she laughs for the first time, and emerges blank and newly possible.","sonnet":"A young person who has spent years hiding in the margins, choosing bare-minimum survival and passivity over risk, suddenly bolts without a destination and discovers that what felt like chronic bad luck is actually the sky opening up — she finally understands the advice she ignored, finds herself laughing for the first time, and emerges blank and newly possible.","src":"haiku"},
"daoko~a-3ei4kt":{"haiku":"A nocturnal daydream of obsession and resignation—already knowing she'll come to you in bad dreams, accepting that as enough.","opus":"Daoko's cool, half-spoken delivery is built for lucid helplessness, and she turns it on a recurring Tokyo infatuation: the same night, the same distorted skyline, a man who drains her and discards her, and yet she returns to meet him in dreams. The scene captures knowing exactly what you are doing and doing it anyway — the repeated night becoming both trap and the only place she gets to touch something real.","sonnet":"A woman caught in a recurring, self-destructive urban infatuation — the same night, the same distorted Tokyo skyline, a man who drains her and discards her, and yet she keeps returning to meet him in her dreams. The emotional core is the lucid helplessness of knowing exactly what you're doing and doing it anyway, the 'same night' becoming both a trap and the only place she gets to touch something real.","src":"haiku"},
"daoko~with-teddyloid":{"haiku":"A self-loathing rant against mediocrity—I hate myself, my voice, my face; love and hate blur into obsessive devotion.","opus":"A violent tug-of-war with self-loathing: the speaker despises their own face, voice and forced smiles, blames parents and everyone else, yet keeps insisting on holding fast to love and self-rebirth, that only you can become you and life is a gamble to be taken once.","sonnet":"A person oscillating violently between loving and hating themselves — they loathe their own voice, face, smile, classify themselves as a machine and a masturbation — but the song's arc pushes toward a single demand: commit to loving yourself the way you'd commit to loving someone else, because this is the only life and it's a gamble worth taking. The emotional core is the ugly, necessary work of self-acceptance.","src":"haiku"},
"dark-divine~run-away":{"haiku":"Psychological torment and self-inflicted violence reign while escape remains impossible, with suffering becoming the only permanent state.","opus":"A horror-styled anthem about fleeing your own mind, the chainsaw and blade as metaphors for intrusive despair and the pull toward self-harm. The real terror is becoming the monster in the mirror you swore you'd never be.","sonnet":"A chainsaw-nightmare voice pursues the speaker through an internal wasteland: the mind screams louder than any external threat, the mirror becomes a monster, and running away only prolongs a bloody end — the only escape is accepting that there is none.","src":"haiku"},
"dark-station~heroes":{"opus":"A performer jerked around like a puppet, craving freedom and approval while feeding lies to a mass audience. The refrain asks what remains to hope for once the heroes are gone and even self-destruction stops working.","src":"opus"},
"dark-station~obvious":{"haiku":"Life echoes karmic consequences; what's given returns inevitably, creating cycles of suffering and the suppression of inner truth.","opus":"A caustic breakup confession where the truth being swallowed is plain contempt. Feeling alive amid the wreckage, the singer lands on karmic bluntness: what you put out comes back, and the lie between them was plain to see all along.","sonnet":"A speaker who has been holding back a hard truth finally delivers it — \"honey, you are the worst\" — to someone whose deception is transparent; the moment of speaking feels alive even as the chorus echoes that what you give is what you get.","src":"haiku"},
"dark-station~villain":{"haiku":"The speaker embraces a villainous identity born from hidden darkness and suffering. They relish the misery and power that comes from being feared and broken.","opus":"A confession of embracing the monstrous, hidden self that everyone conceals; the narrator relishes cruelty, misery, and the fear on a victim's face. The deeper read is the accusation turned back on a partner who never takes blame and treats life as a game, casting the narrator as the villain their behavior created.","sonnet":"A person who has been kept down and made to feel monstrous has fully internalized that identity and turned it back on the person who assigned it to them — they enjoy the fear and misery they now inspire. The throughline is self-determined malevolence born from being made to feel subhuman.","src":"haiku"},
"dark-tranquillity~cathode-ray-sunshine":{"haiku":"Media and screens transmit artificial light that transforms reality, separating perception from truth and burning reality away with electronic rays.","opus":"Melodic death metal about escapism through the screen, artificial light standing in for real experience, the TV glow turning night into a hollow day. It's a wary look at feeding on manufactured images just to get through alive.","sonnet":"Television and screen media are cast as a false sun — transmitting alienation and sensory degradation rather than light, turning escapism into a corrosive substitute for genuine experience.","src":"haiku"},
"darke-complex~dead-to-me":{"haiku":"Someone severs a relationship after realizing the other person's betrayal and dishonesty, declaring them psychologically dead and relinquishing their emotional hold.","opus":"A defiant farewell to a manipulative liar who hollowed the narrator out and made himself at home before proving worthless. The whole song is about reclaiming power by cutting the person off entirely, treating them as though they no longer exist, refusing to submit again and promising he will not be forgotten.","sonnet":"A final, contemptuous dismissal of someone who tried to break the narrator through words and manipulation — they're erased from memory and stripped of any remaining sympathy.","src":"haiku"},
"darknet~angelbyte":{"haiku":"Angel venom codes the soul while faith downloads. Biting circuits break—heaven becomes dead weight.","opus":"A cyber-spiritual crisis where faith is downloaded like corrupted code and the real threat isn't the devil but a seductive angel whose bite could kill; salvation and self-destruction glitch together as heaven becomes dead weight.","sonnet":"About a spiritually corrupting relationship framed in digital terms — the other person's influence is a virus, their angelic surface conceals something lethal, and even prayer won't help because the devil on the narrator's back isn't the real threat; the threat is the person who could take their life with a single bite.","src":"haiku"},
"darknet~bleached":{"haiku":"The song addresses internal emptiness, creative paralysis, and self-doubt that drain meaning from thought and expression. Repeated metaphors of bleaching and being silenced explore the speaker's struggle against self-inflicted apathy.","opus":"Darknet turn modern metalcore into a study of creative paralysis, ideas rotting before they can take shape and self-doubt destroying everything he tries. The narrator blames an inborn lack of motivation, a void that consumes every ambition, yet ends by refusing to quit, framing his drive as something worn thin rather than truly gone.","sonnet":"Creative paralysis rooted in self-doubt — thoughts surface but dissolve before they form. A hereditary lack of drive bleaches everything clean of meaning. Voices around and within tell the person to settle for mediocrity, though a thin current of resistance insists on pushing back.","src":"haiku"},
"darknet~gen-throwaway":{"haiku":"Disposable culture consumes meaning; each iteration erases the previous life, leaving cycles of failed escape and repetitive disillusionment.","opus":"A bleak indictment of a disposable culture chasing fame and money into an early grave, where nothing satisfies past a single day. The looping, self-erasing chorus is the point: a generation stuck on repeat, sick of everything instantly.","sonnet":"A generation defined by disposability and instant saturation — fame, money, backup plans as cancer, the cycle of life looping in the head — confronts the reality that everything tried fails and the only honest label left is 'throwaway.'","src":"haiku"},
"darknet~glass-skin":{"haiku":"Wealth and status create hollow illusions; the speaker craves money and sees through others' dead eyes while the poor burn and reality shatters.","opus":"Darknet dissect social-media envy with cold precision, staging a flawless digital world where neighbours curdle into competitors and every polished post peddles a little falsehood. Celebrity enclaves gleam above the supposedly damned masses while endless consumption fails to make anyone feel alive again, the urge to tear through a manufactured surface reading as rage at influencer culture.","sonnet":"Social media's curated paradise is a brittle shell — the Calabasas ideal floats above the poor like a mirage. Underneath the pixel-perfect surface, consumption numbs a growing emptiness. Glass skin shatters when pressed, revealing hollow eyes and a reality nothing like the frame.","src":"haiku"},
"darknet~idgaf":{"haiku":"Constant exposure to tragedy breeds apathy and numbness; meaning dissolves when suffering becomes repetitive and omnipresent.","opus":"A savage portrait of desensitization, where atrocity and trivial annoyance flatten into the same numb shrug from overexposure. The pledge to care only about what touches you personally is delivered as both confession and accusation.","sonnet":"Media saturation has induced total moral numbness: the speaker catalogs atrocities and personal betrayals alike — a man shot, a dealer overdosed, mutual infidelity — declaring indifference to each, then leads the listener in a pledge not to care about anything outside their immediate experience, diagnosing desensitisation as repetition's inevitable product.","src":"haiku"},
"darknet~morally-flexible":{"haiku":"Social media and celebrity culture have eroded principles and authenticity, turning everyone into interchangeable plastic vessels chasing empty validation.","opus":"A caustic attack on hollow, image-obsessed culture, the narrator admitting he would fake anything for a hit of online approval while diagnosing an entire scene rotted through, surrounded by contacts yet utterly alone. The metalcore delivery drives home a warning about social-media addiction and sold-out principles, aiming the sneer as much at himself as at the idols he mocks.","sonnet":"A diagnosis of identity sold for status — the speaker watches themselves and everyone around them bend their principles for media-manufactured approval, chasing fame and validation until even the original self is forgotten. The punchline is that hyperconnection has produced only isolation: thousands of contacts and no one to call.","src":"haiku"},
"darknet~npc":{"haiku":"Trapped in algorithmic existence without agency, the speaker experiences life as a scripted side character in someone else's narrative, expendable and hollow, used until emptied.","opus":"Alienation framed through gaming, cast as a scripted bit-player with no genuine choices in a story owned by others. It's about being used and depersonalized, reduced to a tool in other people's self-serving schemes until you doubt your own reality.","sonnet":"A speaker feels like a non-player character in someone else's story — pre-set options, exhausted dialogue, only alive when needed — culminating in a furious address to those who gamify life by treating everyone around them as disposable NPCs.","src":"haiku"},
"darknet~pulse-flatline":{"opus":"Chasing chemical highs collapses into planetary panic, the private breakdown echoing a world both scorched and iced over. The narrator only wants a sensation that makes existence bearable while everything within and without expires together.","src":"opus"},
"darknet~zerotonin-3":{"haiku":"Hopelessness metastasizes into certainty of self-destruction as the speaker accepts their predetermined ending, viewing escape as both logical and inevitable despite chemical numbness.","opus":"An unflinching account of chronic depression and burnout, exhausted by suffering that never lets up, and the song darkens toward suicidal resignation with a plea not to be followed into the dark.","sonnet":"A confession of burnout so deep it has become suicidal: the speaker hates how they have turned out, is tired of measuring their suffering against others, and asks with genuine bleakness whether life ever gets better than this — closing with a resolve to go into the dark alone and not be followed.","src":"haiku"},
"darko-us~come-home":{"haiku":"A person flees pain they secretly love, chasing hollow distance and impermanence while questioning whether they are anything at all.","opus":"A death-drive dressed as a sprint toward relief, running from pain you're strangely attached to while the ground quietly waits to reclaim you. Underneath the momentum sits the plain terror of not knowing whether you're anything at all.","sonnet":"Someone running from grief or trauma until their lungs give out, chasing a hollow light that recedes — the question underneath is whether the pain you love is worth pursuing, or whether the chase itself is just another form of dying.","src":"haiku"},
"darko-us~donna":{"haiku":"A person grieves a lost love, haunted by memories and unable to move forward despite time passing.","opus":"A grief-heavy return to a place tied to someone gone, the narrator moving through the space as if she were still there and unable to release her without being overwhelmed by sorrow. Progressive metalcore band Darko US soften their aggression here into something haunted and melodic, a lingering trace of scent standing in for a whole lost closeness as time crawls on.","sonnet":"Grief that refuses to calcify — the speaker keeps returning to a shared place, smelling flowers, feeling the energy of someone gone as though dancing with their ghost. Days grow long, years drag by, and the memory of those beautiful things becomes both wound and sustenance: too deep to forget, too good to want to.","src":"haiku"},
"darlah~differ":{"haiku":"Defiant refusal to conform or accept another's control, choosing solitary autonomy over connection or compromise.","opus":"A refusal to be caged or reshaped by someone who preaches while manipulating, the singer claiming self-sufficiency and lostness as strength rather than defeat, defiance that would rather break the whole system than kneel to it.","sonnet":"Defiance against someone who tried to cage, manipulate, and consume the narrator — refusing to bow to their throne, cutting the rope of control, and asserting a fundamental incompatibility: they will always differ, and that difference is the narrator's survival.","src":"haiku"},
"daron-malakian-and-scars-on-broadway~addicted-to-the-violence":{"opus":"Broken people trudge toward oblivion while the narrator owns up to a craving for brutality, watching others suffer for entertainment; dragon-and-flower brewing imagery frames a downward drag toward destruction and levelling everything.","src":"opus"},
"daron-malakian-and-scars-on-broadway~destroy-the-power":{"haiku":"Abuse crystallizes into strength and rebellion as suffering teaches contempt for oppression. The speaker promises reciprocal destruction to those who rule through violence.","opus":"A caustic meditation that abuse breeds strength, threading serial killers, theocrats and war zones into the claim that the brutalized seize power and turn it back on their tormentors.","sonnet":"Abuse as generative force: oppressors from Ramirez to the Ayatollah are named as terrors whose violence paradoxically creates strength in those who survive it, enabling the oppressed to seize power and destroy those who wielded it.","src":"haiku"},
"daron-malakian-and-scars-on-broadway~imposter":{"opus":"The speaker hunts down imposters and traitors, the smiling bastards who make you suffer, vowing to round them up and kill the one dressed undercover; drug-run imagery and a flight to Rio wrap the vendetta in a paranoid criminal world.","src":"opus"},
"daron-malakian-and-scars-on-broadway~lives":{"haiku":"Collective struggle and survival against historical erasure and genocide. People dance, fight, and occupy in solidarity, refusing to be forgotten or erased.","opus":"Away from System of a Down, Daron Malakian keeps that band's Armenian memory central: the closing invocation of surviving genocide anchors an otherwise ecstatic call to dance and get high. The people kicked out of history reassert themselves through defiant survival, trance and celebration becoming acts of political endurance rather than escape.","sonnet":"A people who survived genocide carry the memory of fallen heroes across generations, affirming they exist in history despite attempts at erasure. Collective dance and shared consciousness become acts of defiance, insisting on presence, on victory over denial, on survival into the present.","src":"haiku"},
"daron-malakian-and-scars-on-broadway~satan-hussein":{"haiku":"Crude nihilism mocks morality and control while cataloguing excess and transgression as power over a degraded world.","opus":"The System of a Down guitarist channels a grotesque, blasphemous tyrant-voice, mashing religion, drugs, and abuse of power into deliberate provocation. It satirizes despotism and hypocrisy by inhabiting the monster, ironically undercut by a chanted commandment against killing.","sonnet":"A deliberately chaotic collision of religions, drugs, political power, and sexual provocation satirises authoritarian self-aggrandisement and societal dysfunction, the persona speaking as a contemptuous tyrant commanding from the sand.","src":"haiku"},
"daron-malakian-and-scars-on-broadway~the-shame-game":{"opus":"A blunt takedown of someone shameless and self-destructive, all fakery and self-abuse. The repeated question of who will teach them lands as a shrug at a person too far gone to be reached.","src":"opus"},
"daron-malakian-and-scars-on-broadway~you-destroy-you":{"opus":"The narrator watches a loved one self-destruct and go crazy, cleaning up their messes while accepting he can only witness it; life keeps moving on, leaving only a toast to memories of who they used to be.","src":"opus"},
"daron-malakian-and-scars-on-broadway~your-lives-burn":{"opus":"A pox-on-both-houses rant against left and right, politicians and media alike, accusing them of stoking division while ordinary lives burn. Malakian positions himself as the cancel-proof outsider preaching unity against manipulation from all sides.","src":"opus"},
"daron-malakian~guns-are-loaded":{"haiku":"Trauma perpetuates itself across generations as the speaker acknowledges complicity in abuse; survival demands recognizing the cycle without breaking it.","opus":"A grim American portrait of domestic violence and cyclical harm, refusing the clean line between the abused and the abuser and admitting the speaker is both.","sonnet":"A song about cycles of abuse — speaker declares themselves simultaneously abuser and abused, claiming they didn't choose it — framed by broken glass recalling home and the image of guns loaded close by when time runs out and reason is lost. The closing admission fuses victim and perpetrator into one person, refusing the comfort of clean categories.","src":"haiku"},
"daron-malakian~lives":{"haiku":"A people survives genocide and oppression, reclaiming identity through song and occupation. History gets rewritten by those who endure and verify truth.","opus":"An ecstatic anthem of a people who survived attempted extermination, framing dance and trance as defiant survival; the Armenian genocide is the wound underneath the celebration of a heritage erased from official history yet still alive.","sonnet":"A song about Armenian identity, survival, and resistance — the narrator speaks for a people erased from official history who nonetheless survived genocide, calling out denial, asserting that their lives mattered and that truth will eventually be verified, with the dancing and getting high serving as acts of defiant communal joy.","src":"haiku"},
"daughters~the-hit":{"haiku":"A well-meaning man arrives to 'cure' a town through skull surgery and 'drawing out' sickness; the townspeople hang him, proving his help wasn't wanted.","opus":"A grotesque Western parable: a self-righteous stranger rides into a dead-end town offering to fix people's souls by boring holes in their skulls, and gets lynched for the presumption. The barbed moral is that unwanted salvation is just another violence, and the do-gooder should have kept riding.","sonnet":"A parable about a well-meaning outsider who rides into a dead-end town to save the locals from themselves — the community rejects his amateur trepanning cure and strings him up at the town hall, a story about the violent rejection of unwanted outside salvation.","src":"haiku"},
"daughters~the-reason-they-hate-me":{"haiku":"Someone exhorts a person not to interfere with their work while mocking that person's insincerity and emotional distance, suggesting they cope by pretending and seeking affirmation through manipulation.","opus":"Alexis Marshall inhabiting a repulsive, self-justifying misogynist, the leering menace of a man who resents being questioned and probes for someone's need to exploit it; the horror is how fluently the voice rationalizes itself.","sonnet":"A furious, contemptuous rant at someone who performs emotional availability while being fundamentally hollow — the narrator doesn't bother to remember the name people use for someone like this, just observes their grab-ass social climbing with utter disgust, and wonders what exactly they're searching for beneath the skin.","src":"haiku"},
"daughters~the-unattractive-portable-head":{"haiku":"Future violence and social collapse loom unavoidable; the speaker embraces darkness as destiny, rejecting salvation while confronting humanity's capacity for cruelty and decay.","opus":"A fever of hunger and self-disgust — an ancient, insatiable craving happy to buy itself at the cost of others' suffering. It broods on having been made by accident rather than intent, leaning into the dark and yearning simply to feel nothing.","sonnet":"A voice seeded by chance rather than choice confronts a world where too many hands take without ever being willing to break, asking whether humanity will reap what it has sown as time runs short — and ultimately longing for obliterating transcendence over the exhaustion of caring.","src":"haiku"},
"dave-matthews-band~when-the-world-ends-oakenfold":{"haiku":"Apocalypse suspends judgment as lovers merge into unity beyond morality; burning cities become backdrop for transcendent coupling.","opus":"Apocalypse reimagined as the ultimate romantic escape, two lovers making love through the collapse of cities, treating the end of everything as license for total, fearless intimacy.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic love fantasy: when the cities fall and stars disappear, the speaker will be lying in bed with their person — rocking them like a baby, rising above the crumbling buildings, using love itself as wings. The destruction of the world becomes the premise for the purest, most undistracted intimacy imaginable.","src":"haiku"},
"daveed-diggs~rappin-ced":{"haiku":"A young rapper announces himself and his crew, claiming control over the city and cultural dominance.","opus":"A brash throwback boast, an MC introducing himself with explosive confidence, shouting out his crew and claiming he can conquer New York borough by borough and rule them all. Daveed Diggs, later known for Hamilton and the group clipping., leans into old-school battle-rap swagger, the borough name-drops and playful bravado a deliberate tribute to the city's boom-bap heyday.","sonnet":"A short, punchy boast track — the voice introduces itself by name and lays out a crew moving from one borough to another, claiming the whole city is within reach. Queens is the crown, Harlem gets a knowing nod, and the whole thing runs on the compressed confidence of someone certain the room is about to turn.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~battle-for-britain-the-letter":{"haiku":"Defeat and numbness become habitual as the speaker surrenders to inevitable loss; hope collapses into resignation and pain becomes the only certainty.","opus":"A self-styled loser's wry confession of pain-numbing and self-pity, sent as a letter that reassures its reader not to be dragged down by his gloom even as everything spirals downward.","sonnet":"A self-deprecating letter from a lifelong loser to someone he cares about: he has used sucker pills for self-pity, never been a winner, and writes to say don't be forlorn — it's just the payoff, the rain before the storm — and asks them not to let his letter get them down. The tone is wry and tender rather than defeated.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~blackstar":{"haiku":"Transformation arrives through death as the spirit escapes flesh; the speaker refuses categories and becomes the void itself, unspeakable and legendary.","opus":"Written as Bowie was dying, it stages his own death and succession as ritual and myth, a spirit rising and stepping aside while a new figure claims the enigmatic mantle of the blackstar, neither idol nor gangster but something beyond category.","sonnet":"A fragmented, mythic meditation on death, identity, and succession: something happened on the day he died, a spirit stepped aside and someone else took the role and cried \"I'm a Blackstar\" — not a gangster, not a popstar, not a filmstar, but the great I am. The Villa of Ormen with its solitary candle and the eyes at the centre of it all frame the song as a sacred ritual of disappearance and transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~dead-man-walking":{"haiku":"Identity fragments across temporal loops as the speaker moves through memory and present simultaneously; dance becomes the only coherent response to dissolution.","opus":"An aging man dances through time and memory, defiantly spinning between past and future, older than the movies yet wiser than dreams, carrying himself like the walking dead and utterly alive at once.","sonnet":"A man older than movies and wiser than dreams dances through his own past like a dead man walking — slipping through the crack between then and now, spinning slack through reality, falling upward through the years. The Atlantic City memory and the \"alien nation in therapy\" image locate the song in the wreckage of a generational cohort, with dance as the only honest response to having survived it.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~heroes":{"haiku":"Two lovers defying their world's constraints want to transcend it together, even if only briefly. The song captures the paradox of fleeting heroism—powerful yet temporary, against inevitable loss.","opus":"Recorded in divided Berlin, Bowie's anthem imagines two lovers who claim royalty and triumph for a single day against impossible odds. The pivotal image, a kiss by a wall as guns fire overhead, roots the defiance in the shadow of the Wall itself. Heroism here is fleeting and self-aware, a fragile pact wrested from a world that offers no lasting escape.","sonnet":"Two people kiss against a wall under gunfire near a divided border, claiming a moment of transcendence that the world cannot sustain beyond a single day. The grandiosity — king, queen, heroes — is openly provisional, maybe a lie, but the gesture still counts. Stolen time is the only kind available, and they take it anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~i-m-afraid-of-americans":{"haiku":"The song expresses anxious fear of American culture and its materialistic values, characterizing it as spiritually bankrupt and inescapable.","opus":"Bowie's unease with a hollow, globalized America where Johnny wants only sex, cars and cola, and God himself has been branded American. The dread is of a culture that exports emptiness as identity.","sonnet":"Bowie uses a hollow everyman named Johnny to diagnose American culture as a place of atomised desire with no genuine connection — wanting planes, women, and cars but incapable of real need — culminating in the unsettling claim that God himself has been colonised by this worldview.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~i-m-afraid-of-americans-nine-inch-nails-v1-mix":{"haiku":"American culture breeds consumption and emptiness as the speaker fears the pervasive hollowness that defines a generation obsessed with surface.","opus":"A jittery satire of American cultural dominance, its everyman Johnny reduced to appetites for sex, cars and Coke, while the refrain of dread widens into fear of a whole godless, consumerist world.","sonnet":"\"Johnny\" stands in for a hollow American archetype — he wants a brain, wants to suck on Coke, wants pussy and cars — as the speaker circles a generalised dread of American cultural dominance and its erasure of genuine human connection. The refrain \"God is an American\" crystallises the fear that empire has colonised the divine itself.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~life-on-mars":{"haiku":"Bowie critiques mass culture and media consumption, using absurdist imagery to question whether spiritual meaning exists beneath society's manufactured spectacles and conformity.","opus":"One of the highlights of Bowie's Hunky Dory, following a disenchanted young girl who escapes a family argument into a film she finds tediously familiar. A parade of surreal, secondhand images streams past as she wonders whether anything better exists elsewhere, a dreamy takedown of a cheap, derivative modern spectacle.","sonnet":"A girl fleeing domestic disappointment finds the silver screen no escape — it mirrors the same absurdity and violence she sought to leave behind. Bowie widens the lens until the whole of Western civilization looks like a freakshow endlessly rewriting itself, leaving the girl — and everyone — to ask whether anything real exists anywhere at all.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~little-wonder":{"haiku":"The song uses abstract, surreal imagery with nonsensical descriptions that create a dreamy, disorienting state. It evokes wonder and distance without a coherent literal narrative.","opus":"Bowie's drum-and-bass-era collage stitches Snow White's dwarfs into surreal, throwaway London imagery, a deliberately nonsensical stream that captures the disorienting speed and dislocation of nineties city life more than any story.","sonnet":"Bowie name-drops the seven dwarfs scrambled and scattered through surrealist imagery — intergalactic, medicated, meditative, heading somewhere impossibly far away. It's a playful but elusive celebration of wonder and strangeness rather than a decipherable narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~little-wonder-danny-saber-dance-mix":{"opus":"Bowie's late-90s drum-and-bass phase, a cut-up dreamscape riffing on the seven dwarfs and modern sensory overload. The remix stretches its disorientation into a mantra of being sent far away, meaning dissolving into pure texture and momentum.","src":"opus"},
"david-bowie~modern-love":{"haiku":"Consumerism and sexual transaction replace authentic desire; characters perform roles in a system that exhausts them.","opus":"Bowie's restless standoff with marriage, church and the American dream, a man half in flight from commitment and the God and bills that come with it. The relentless drive masks doubt about whether love and belief can hold him.","sonnet":"A fragmented, ironic portrait of American romantic and cultural mythology — young couples, pop-culture references, political disillusionment — circling the unfulfillable desire for connection in a society that commodifies both love and identity. The speaker wants the \"young American\" but the want itself is shaped by images that collapse under scrutiny.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~rebel-rebel":{"haiku":"A young rebel defies gender norms and parental expectations, embracing a transgressive identity and lifestyle while finding acceptance and admiration from another outsider.","opus":"Bowie's glam farewell salutes a gender-blurring youth who throws a mother into confusion and wears torn dresses as armor. Written as the Ziggy era closed, it hands its swaggering riff to every tacky outsider putting the straights on. The affection is total and unironic, a hot-tramp anthem that makes ambiguity itself the mark of a juvenile success.","sonnet":"A gender-fluid youth confounds parental expectation and social norms while thriving in the glam chaos of torn dresses and smudged makeup. The narrator celebrates this transgressive energy with unqualified affection, offering acceptance where the world offers only bewilderment.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~space-oddity":{"haiku":"Ground Control loses contact with astronaut Major Tom, who floats alone above the Earth, severed from guidance and connection, drifting in an altered state beyond reach.","opus":"Bowie's early breakthrough, released amid the moon-landing summer, follows Major Tom from countdown to the eerie serenity of drifting untethered. Ground Control's chatter about press and shirts curdles into dead circuits and silence, the astronaut floating in his tin can with nothing left to do. Isolation, not triumph, becomes the space age's true frontier, fame and abandonment merging in the void.","sonnet":"Major Tom drifts in orbit, cut off from ground control as his capsule fails. From that tin can above a blue and unreachable Earth, he sends one last message of love to his wife before silence swallows the signal — isolation made vast and quietly terminal.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~starman":{"haiku":"An alien visitor in the radio invites children to celebrate freedom and self-expression without parental fear or restraint.","opus":"A cosmic rock-and-roll gospel: a lone listener catches a message from a starman hovering in the sky, eager to meet earthlings but afraid he'd blow their minds, urging the children to let loose and boogie. Bowie unveiled this in 1972 as Ziggy Stardust's glam manifesto, its radio-and-TV transmission and secret-from-the-grownups thrill turning an alien savior into a hymn for outsider kids everywhere.","sonnet":"Late-night radio delivers something otherworldly — a signal that sounds too alive to be human, carrying word of a visitor in the sky who holds back out of concern for what his arrival might do to human minds. The response is giddy, conspiratorial, urging the children especially to open up and let the strangeness in before the adults shut it down.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~suffragette-city-2002-remastered-version":{"haiku":"Seduction and escape accelerate as the speaker abandons restraint; the city promises liberation and the speaker demands entrance to this realm of pleasure.","opus":"A jolt of amphetamine rock-and-roll: a young man brushing off his friends because a woman has completely overtaken him, all swagger and hormonal urgency.","sonnet":"A speaker trying to get rid of a male friend who keeps interrupting his encounter with a woman: Henry needs to get off the phone, stop leaning, and go away, because \"Suffragette City\" — the speaker's charged, liberated space with this sexually overwhelming woman — has no room for a third. The exasperation is comic, the desire urgent.","src":"haiku"},
"david-bowie~telling-lies-adam-f-mix":{"opus":"A visionary, apocalyptic narrator who claims to be tomorrow and the ending addresses those who bring him weeping and accusation, insisting deceit is the currency of conflict and strain and the sense that something's about to break.","src":"opus"},
"david-bowie~valentine-s-day":{"haiku":"A prescient child named Valentine observes the world with twisted understanding, destined to reshape society through hidden power.","opus":"Bowie inhabits a school shooter, the small scrawny figure with a list and a grievance, his violence dressed in the language of a holiday about love. Its quiet horror is how ordinary and overlooked the boy with something to say seems.","sonnet":"Valentine is a cold, diminutive figure — scrawny hands, icy heart — who has quietly decided who deserves to die and is now executing that plan with the rhythm of the crowd as backdrop. The song portrays a school shooter type with clinical detachment and creeping dread.","src":"haiku"},
"david-maxim-micic~cry":{"haiku":"Sacrifice and terror infect memory; the dead call to the living to serve and follow into the void they've been left behind to explore.","opus":"It speaks for those a sacrifice or a death leaves in its wake, asking why they must go on doing their stretch while someone else departs. A Serbian cradle-song threaded through it softens the sorrow into a vow to come after and a hope for restful sleep.","sonnet":"Those left behind address someone lost to self-destruction — they cry when the person dies, not before — and the song alternates between English verses about choice and sacrifice and Serbian lullaby fragments asking the departed to sleep peacefully, held in tension between accusation, grief, and a tenderness that can't quite let go.","src":"haiku"},
"day-aches~everything-blu":{"haiku":"Consumption as merger: one being bleeds into another, and what's left is a pooling of essence—both fading, both feeding the void.","opus":"Being drained by someone who needs to bleed you to survive, staring at the ceiling hunting for any feeling at all as faith tarnishes and slips through your fingers.","sonnet":"A minimal song about being drained by another person in order to keep them alive — a blue, melancholic energy bleeding out of you, slipping through your fingers while you search for feeling at a cracked ceiling.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~a-final-vestige":{"haiku":"Dreams fade into distraction and noise that steals identity; people drift asleep, oblivious to how far their lives have fallen and how wrong they've become.","opus":"North Carolina melodic-doom band Daylight Dies lament how modern distraction erodes dreams and identity, leaving hopes undefended and worn down by apathy. Numbed and only half-awake, people stay bound to a clamorous world that quietly strips away what once made them who they are, rendering contemporary numbness as a slow, unnoticed forgetting of the self.","sonnet":"The noise of contemporary life — distractions, indifference, the world's sheer volume — quietly strips away whatever once defined a person's inner life. Dreams dissolve, the self drifts unconscious through its own transgressions, and the tethering to the world's surface is experienced not as connection but as the slow theft of depth.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~an-heir-to-emptiness":{"haiku":"Someone is stripped of self and hope, haunted by sorrow and death. They inherit only emptiness and nothingness, their will to live cast aside long ago.","opus":"A voice chronicles its own crumbling, sorrow tireless and unbowed as identity dissolves into a hollow blankness and a hope it can no longer place gets thrown away. The North Carolina death-doom band specializes in this frozen bleakness, the speaker finally naming itself monarch of a wrecked realm that holds nothing at all.","sonnet":"A self stripped bare by grief, drifting through a dominion it now calls its own. Identity dissolves into soullessness; the will to survive is cast off. What remains is an emptiness so total it becomes the only kingdom left to inherit.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~cathedral":{"haiku":"Humanity builds grand structures as monuments to isolation and desperation, attempting to write their fleeting dreams into stone against the sky.","opus":"The American melodic death-doom band offers a somber meditation on building something permanent out of desperation and isolation. The great structure stands as a monument to failed will, an answer raised in darkness at whatever price, and it is finally handed over with weary resignation to whoever can make use of it.","sonnet":"A cathedral built against the sky becomes the symbol of something both magnificent and futile — it rises from desperation, encoding the dreams of the young in stone, but the symmetry hides the anguish underneath. The structure stands as testimony to isolation and the drive to answer that isolation with something lasting, however briefly.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~dismantling-devotion":{"haiku":"Isolation penetrates even shared spaces as distance persists between two people; connection fails and loneliness survives proximity.","opus":"Isolation deepened by proximity, where even standing beside the one you love in a crowded room only sharpens the ache of feeling utterly alone.","sonnet":"Two people in the same room — or the absence of someone in an empty room — share nothing but aching distance: voices clash in a crowd yet fade to nothing, gazes cross an empty space, and every moment only deepens the feeling of being alone despite proximity. The lyric is almost entirely negative space.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~dreaming-of-breathing":{"haiku":"Hearts are hollowed, souls atrophied, and fires that forged the self go dark; life is choked by the hands that feed, leaving only decay and broken backs.","opus":"Emptied out and shredded, a narrator watches the blaze that once shaped the spirit dwindle to cold ash, undone by the very source meant to sustain it. Daylight Dies drag out this slow smothering step by step, sightless eyes, rotting spirits, dwindling air, until drawing a breath turns into the unreachable dream of the title, spines snapped beneath a caretaking touch turned lethal.","sonnet":"Collective spiritual suffocation — souls atrophied, minds extinguished by the very forces meant to sustain them. The fires that forged human connection have gone dark, leaving blindness, broken backs, and an airless existence that kills both mind and soul simultaneously.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~ghosting":{"haiku":"A relationship has drifted irreversibly apart as denial breaks down and time carries both people toward unavoidable conclusions.","opus":"A hushed elegy for something ending beyond recovery, watching a person or a life recede while breath grows heavy and denial lingers. Cast in imagery of a boat pulling away from land, the song settles into acceptance that whatever sustains us can only hold on so long before it lets go entirely.","sonnet":"A short, very compressed lyric about a relationship drifting to its end — denial, weighted breath, the wind carrying both people away from the shore until it can carry them no further. Clean and final.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~hold-on-to-nothing":{"haiku":"Existential dread at the edge of the abyss—nothing remains to grasp when life slips away and the ground disappears beneath.","opus":"The melodic doom-death band stand at the brink of everything peering into the void, breathless that they have exhausted all they can offer. As the earth crumbles beneath them and existence recedes, the narrator clutches at emptiness asking what remains to cling to, the spare, crushing words landing on the desolate verdict that nothing endures to carry anyone onward.","sonnet":"At the absolute limit of endurance, when the ground has already vanished and nothing remains to grip, the song simply names the void. There is no rallying cry here, only the recognition that some moments arrive with nothing left to hold and no way through.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~hollow-hands":{"haiku":"Someone watches their body and youth deteriorate over time, their hands becoming hollow and numb as age and time slowly consume them.","opus":"A slow surrender to mortality, framed as a drowning man's letter, watching youth drain from the eyes and hands lose their very substance, always shaking, thinning, fading in daylight. Daylight Dies work in melodic death-doom, and the funereal pacing lets this dissolution unfold in real time, the collapsing of a life rendered less as event than as an inexorable, numbing erosion.","sonnet":"A slow dissolution written as a letter from a drowning man — youth drains from the eyes, hands lose their shape and go numb, and the daily collapse is too gradual to feel until it is undeniable. The song sits with the experience of being killed softly by time, with no drama, just the steady fading in daylight.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~i-wait":{"haiku":"The singer waits in a numb, gray world, unable to hear or connect with anything, trying desperately to find some remnant of himself as days blur together.","opus":"A study in gray paralysis, the narrator waiting without knowing what for, days fading into one indistinguishable blur while the world dulls and something breaks unheard somewhere just out of reach. Daylight Dies channel death-doom's crawling weight into this portrait of dissociation, the repeated fading turning depression into a landscape where even the search for a remnant of the self feels hopeless.","sonnet":"Dissociation as a grey daily state — the world feels distant, identity has become indistinct, and days bleed into each other until they are one long ache with no clear object. The speaker keeps waiting without knowing what for, unable to locate themselves or feel what their own head contains, the world simply dead and silent around them.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~infidel":{"haiku":"A lost soul searches for meaning and identity while trapped between past and present, finding only emptiness and diminishing echoes. They search for answers that will never come.","opus":"The death-doom outfit follows a hollow, drifting figure searching for a meaning that time has hidden, scanning the horizon for some answer about who he even is. Cut loose and haunted by fading echoes of the past, he settles into a desolate certainty that the understanding he craves will forever elude him.","sonnet":"A drifting identity searches for itself in sediment and silence, finding only echoes that diminish before they answer. The self is defined by absence rather than presence, a ghost moving through layers of time toward answers it has already accepted will never arrive.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~sunset":{"haiku":"Watching the sky and sun fade, the narrator observes the day's end and the loss of self under the same unchanging skies.","opus":"A melancholy meditation on watching the day and sun die, consigning the fading light to the grave; under these same skies of bleeding yellow and cold gray, the speaker lost himself.","sonnet":"Watching the daylight die — amber fading to gray — becomes a mirror for a self already lost beneath these same skies, and the song sits with that grief without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"daylight-dies~the-pale-approach":{"haiku":"Autumn and winter arrive with beauty fading into cold and decay. The seasonal transition embodies the approach of death and the loss of color and warmth.","opus":"Autumn sliding into winter read as a slow surrender, warmth and color conceding to grey and killing cold. Melodic doom using the turning season as a plain metaphor for decline, the last beauty before everything goes still.","sonnet":"A close, sensory account of autumn giving way to winter — warmth fading to grey, rust and candlelight briefly beautiful before the cold arrives without mercy — rendered as something to be faced plainly rather than mourned.","src":"haiku"},
"days-of-the-new~enemy":{"haiku":"Rejection of blame and refusal to accept claims of enmity; the speaker denies intentional harm while others demand change.","opus":"A weary insistence that the narrator isn't the antagonist the other person keeps casting him as, and that the change has to come from them. Under the grunge grind sits a plea to stop projecting conflict where none was offered.","sonnet":"The speaker insists repeatedly that they are not trying to be the listener's enemy, placing the responsibility for that dynamic entirely on the listener who needs to change their perception and behavior. The repeated urgency suggests frustration at being misread and a desire to dissolve a hostility that the speaker believes is self-manufactured.","src":"haiku"},
"days-of-the-new~touch-peel-and-stand":{"haiku":"A person recognizes they have time and opportunity to harm someone verbally or emotionally, and exercises that power. The song depicts calculated emotional abuse.","opus":"The acoustic-grunge band ride a hypnotic riff through a bruised, controlling attraction, a narrator aware of his own capacity to sink low yet unwilling to release his grip or reveal it. Having found his justification and needing none, he treats his partner as an outlet, the craving for contact circling an obsession that won't loosen.","sonnet":"A dynamic of control and desire plays out between two people where one holds power by withholding and the other insists on being touched despite everything. Abuse is named plainly, stripped of romance, framed as something one person has chosen and the other keeps sustaining.","src":"haiku"},
"dayseeker~crooked-soul":{"haiku":"Numbness and self-contempt converge as the speaker refuses rescue; darkness becomes the only honest home for a soul bent toward its own destruction.","opus":"A confession of emotional numbness and self-destruction, drinking to feel anything, seeing a devil in the mirror and accepting that he was always destined to become cold and broken.","sonnet":"A self-diagnosis of emotional death: the speaker is numb, drunk on mistakes, sees a devil in the mirror, and has concluded they were always destined to become a crooked soul — cold-hearted, incapable of feeling love, finding it easier to breathe in the dark. The refusal of help is total and, notably, delivered without self-pity.","src":"haiku"},
"dayseeker~crooked-soul-single-version":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness and self-inflicted damage create distance from others, where the narrator embraces becoming a morally compromised version of themselves.","opus":"Dayseeker's frontman voices numb self-recognition, a narrator drunk off mistakes and throwing up in the sink, convinced he was always destined to become cold and unfeeling. He sees a devil in himself and pushes away offered love. It captures the band's post-hardcore drift toward atmospheric, emotionally raw balladry, the darkness paradoxically making it easier for him to breathe.","sonnet":"A man who has numbed himself beyond recovery examines the ruin plainly — unable to feel love, drawn toward darkness as though it were oxygen. He accepts the devil inside without resistance, calling himself a crooked soul not as confession but as settled fact.","src":"haiku"},
"dayseeker~dreamstate":{"haiku":"Death separates the speaker into two worlds—the waking nightmare of loss and the dream-realm where reunion persists, neither offering escape from the chasm between presence and absence.","opus":"A grief song about visiting the dead in sleep, where dreams feel more real and bearable than waking without them. The cruelty is the daily waking: the sun rises and the loss is fresh again, so life itself becomes the nightmare.","sonnet":"A grieving speaker can only access the person they lost in sleep — \"when I'm sleeping I talk with you\" — and struggles to live waking life when nothing there is real, the dreamstate becoming both refuge and prison as the pain refuses to erase.","src":"haiku"},
"dayshell~hipshot":{"opus":"Falling headlong for someone he expects to wound him worst, the singer confesses he craves the risk, firing off recklessly and buckling low, split between wanting a genuine, mending love and resenting them for the ache they cause.","src":"opus"},
"dayshell~spellbound":{"haiku":"Desire and power intertwine in an abusive dynamic where one person wields control through seduction, feigning innocence while knowingly manipulating.","opus":"Dayshell, formed by former Suicide Silence guitarist Shayley Bourget, push electronic-tinged metalcore into a fever of masochistic compulsion, where pain is demanded and desire framed as enchantment beyond the narrator's control. Insistent commands to stop playing dumb circle an obsession cast as bewitchment, longing collapsing into something feverish, degrading, and impossible to sate.","sonnet":"An obsessive pull toward someone who dominates and consumes. The subject craves the very pain this person inflicts, unable to resist a spell that overrides self-preservation. Desire and submission collapse into each other, leaving nothing left to refuse.","src":"haiku"},
"dayshell~superhuman":{"haiku":"Someone channels anger and fear as fuel for power, refusing to surrender or change their path despite pressure bearing down. They reclaim superhuman identity and shine a light on who they truly are.","opus":"An urging to channel anger, fear and pressure into strength, the song's speaker declares himself superhuman and unafraid of the dark, wanting to shine a light and be remembered for who he is even to the grave.","sonnet":"The narrator claims a fearless, superhuman identity fueled by channeling anger and fear into strength, and insists on being seen and remembered even knowing they will be forgotten.","src":"haiku"},
"daze-of-june~black-eyed":{"opus":"A dark, unsettling meditation on inherited fate and a father's cursed bloodline, birth and death folding into one another. Being handed off and dark-eyed frames a life that felt broken before it started, longing to abandon what's already lost.","src":"opus"},
"daze-of-june~four-knives":{"opus":"A cryptic, ritual bond between two people diving to the bottom of something dangerous together, half pact and half descent. The imagery of curses, powers, and a shared creation reads as devotion that thrives on shared risk and darkness.","src":"opus"},
"de-la-soul~rock-co-kane-flow-feat-mf-doom":{"haiku":"Hip-hop virtuosos assert complete artistic dominion through vocabulary and technique, dismissing inferior competitors as merely following trends they pioneered.","opus":"A pure display of craft, De La and DOOM trading dense, allusion-packed bars as a statement about lyrical substance over industry gloss. The subject is rap itself: honoring credits, writing real lines, outlasting the disposable.","sonnet":"Three MCs trade verses asserting their superior craft, longevity, and lyrical precision against a hip-hop landscape full of pretenders — it's a collective statement of artistic pride built from staying original and paying dues without fanfare.","src":"haiku"},
"de-wayne~die-out-here-feat-poorstacy":{"opus":"A striver drives thirteen hundred miles to chase fame on Fairfax, mocking everyone who got comfortable and refusing to settle for a family and a lawn even if it means sleeping in a tent. Under the ambition runs dread of dying anonymous, of crashes, and of voices that don't cover the rent.","src":"opus"},
"dead-cassette~if-love-was-lost":{"opus":"Hooked on a love that always ends in loss, the narrator keeps crossing lines they swore off, feigning that all is well because the other never leaves their thoughts; fated to repeat it, they beg to be scorched yet swear to stay to the very last.","src":"opus"},
"dead-days~rot":{"opus":"Decay reframed as beauty and even intimacy, an invitation to rot together in a world where everyone's already dead. Beneath the romanticized nihilism sits raw self-hatred, the pain that pretends time will heal it while insisting it won't.","src":"opus"},
"dead-eyes~avalanche":{"haiku":"Momentum builds toward catastrophic collapse; the speaker watches destruction accelerate unstoppably.","opus":"Overwhelm as natural disaster, a mind buried under its own failures and suffocating gravity. The defiance is that despite the endless falling, the narrator swears he won't fully collapse and keeps clawing back toward the surface.","sonnet":"Mental collapse mapped as natural disaster — buried alive in failure, the mind is quicksand, every day another avalanche; yet \"landslide but I'm never gonna collapse\" insists on a distinction between falling and total destruction. The survival instinct keeps clawing back to the surface even without a reason to.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-horse-one~static-king":{"opus":"A clipped, broken lament of splitting apart, corrosion, and collapse, where an agitated youth is told his realm is nothing but a mount for his whim, with a mythic isle summoned amid a downed war banner and the impulse to burrow underground and rest.","src":"opus"},
"dead-label~pure-chaos":{"opus":"An ecological and misanthropic verdict on humankind as a corrupt, self-hating species wrecking the planet through greed and pollution. Slaughtering in a deity's name and suffocating on its own toxins, it faces an extinction entirely of its own making, the Irish band laying the blame squarely on us.","sonnet":"A diagnosis of human civilization as inherently self-destructive — killing in the name of gods, feasting on others out of greed, polluting its own air — the narrator sees their species as a collection of disgusting mistakes whose mindless destruction will eventually end them.","src":"sonnet"},
"dead-lakes~strange-juice":{"opus":"Driving imagery frames a restless daydreamer afraid of becoming a burnout, weighing whether to leave or stay in a relationship that only hurts; switching lanes becomes a metaphor for the change needed to grow and find meaning, though staying means never finding it.","src":"opus"},
"dead-lakes~wrong-way":{"haiku":"Traveling down destructive path, heading toward ruin and catastrophe.","opus":"Drive and depression knotted together, pursuing riches and triumph down a single dead-end lane while musing on what surrender might feel like. The deeper crisis is peering within and failing to know your own reflection, split between calm and the grind in a heartless world.","sonnet":"A driver going the wrong way down a one-lane road, wondering what happens if he just lets go — society feels unfulfilling, ambition has gone cold, and the only thing missing is something that feels like home. The wrong-way drive is both metaphor and almost-literal: depression dressed as a navigation problem.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-letter-circus~are-we-closer":{"haiku":"The speaker asserts control over their own fate and decisions while questioning existence and proximity to survival; they struggle to understand their own identity and meaning.","opus":"An Australian alt-rock affirmation of self-determination, insisting every decision and intention belongs to the narrator alone. Beneath the resolve runs a searching uncertainty about connection, whether reaching another person brings anyone closer.","sonnet":"A song about taking ownership of one's own story and decisions — the narrator insists he defines and decides his own path, while also being aware of something holding him back (love, possibly). The refrain \"are we closer?\" is an urgent question about whether all this agency is actually moving anyone toward something real.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-letter-circus~disconnect-and-apply":{"opus":"Dead Letter Circus voice burnout and revolt, a psyche too worn to endure yet another identical day and rejecting a life it feels beneath. The title plea is a yearning to unplug from a soul-numbing grind and finally hear one's own thoughts. The deadpan sign-off about being back at the job on Monday lands the whole trap with dry irony.","sonnet":"A person suffocated by the repetitive, numbing grind of daily life decides to consciously disconnect from the noise of external obligation and tune into their own interior world instead, rejecting the idea of a life felt as inferior.","src":"sonnet"},
"dead-letter-circus~here-we-divide":{"haiku":"When crisis breaks bonds, the speaker fights to hold together; separation feels inevitable as rifts widen.","opus":"The Australian progressive rockers Dead Letter Circus build tension around an impending break, a cold that cannot be located and a relationship poised to fracture. The mantra to slow down and hold on wrestles hope against dread, until the narrator claims control and swears to keep pulling closer through every rupture. Division and survival stay entangled to the last.","sonnet":"A relationship reaching its fracture point, where one person braces for the break while promising to pull them both back together. Waves of crisis wash in repeatedly, but beneath the overwhelm a stubborn wakefulness holds on, refusing the easier option of turning away for good.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-letter-circus~i-am":{"haiku":"The speaker sacrifices themselves as a martyr, hoping their death brings deliverance while declaring their own tested and broken identity.","opus":"The Australian progressive rock band lift sacrificial imagery toward something like liberation, the narrator volunteering to die so that his death might mean more than his life did, moving from denial to a hard-won acceptance of exactly who he is. Their soaring, atmospheric sound turns this martyr's offering, blood flowing toward exhaustion, into spiritual release rather than defeat.","sonnet":"A martyr's declaration, offered freely — the voice steps forward to absorb the damage so others do not have to, framing sacrifice not as defeat but as the fullest expression of what they are: broken, tested, still standing. The tension between being a liar and an honest man, between slave and free, is held without resolution, because the offering itself is the point.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-letter-circus~the-mile":{"haiku":"The speaker questions whether escape, identity, and autonomy are even possible within systems designed to confuse and trap them. Isolation and the refusal to be saved dominate the emotional landscape.","opus":"The Australian prog-rock band circles a paralysis between staying ignorant and growing toward a better present, pleading to be broken and held while doubting anyone will save the collective. Fittingly for a group whose ambient, socially conscious rock trades in half-answers, the confusion never resolves; conscience keeps overriding what the speaker wants to say, until he simply chooses his own way.","sonnet":"A person gropes for reasons to keep going in a fog of confusion and self-division, unable to locate themselves or feel safe. Conscience overrides every comfort offered by others, and the pull toward isolation grows stronger even as some part of them refuses to finally stop moving.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-letter-circus~this-life-awake":{"haiku":"People must choose authentic action over passive waiting, breaking from inherited patterns to live consciously and selflessly.","opus":"A summons to stop sleepwalking through a life that isn't yours, the narrator refusing to let destiny and the clock dictate his existence and insisting only honesty sets a person free. Dead Letter Circus bring their Australian prog-rock lift to this waking-up manifesto, the swimming-upstream imagery pressing self-determination as the sole escape.","sonnet":"A refusal to drift — the song pushes back against the passive majority who do not know their own names or purposes, insisting that fate cannot be left to decide. Trapped in an ocean of emotion, the speaker still chooses to swim, to stay awake, to stop waiting; the lesson burns, the old ways are gone, and the only instruction is to feel what is real and act on it.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-like-juliet~rebellion":{"haiku":"Authority collapses as collective defiance rises; the speaker summons generational uprising against systemic violence and nationalist mythology.","opus":"A protest anthem against surging nationalism and creeping authoritarianism, summoning an entire generation to rise against sealed borders and flag-waving pride, insisting we all bleed alike.","sonnet":"An anti-authoritarian rallying call against a nationalist police state: the speaker names a rightward shift, a blood-soaked land, patriotic pride used to divide — and insists that only the rebellion of a whole generation can answer it. \"Divided by colours we still bleed the same\" is the pivot from diagnosis to demand, with freedom ringing from street to hills as the vision of what replaces the current order.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-like-juliet~rise-up":{"opus":"An explicitly anti-fascist call to arms, blaming complacency for letting demagogues rise on old tactics of fear and division. It grieves the drowned and dispossessed and demands accountability from those who profit while ordinary people pay.","src":"opus"},
"dead-mau5~i-remember-vocal-mix":{"haiku":"Memory holds time stable against loss; releasing binds connection as spiritual practice.","opus":"A gentle meditation on holding onto the past without being trapped by it, that you can let go while still keeping the love and memory intact. Kaskade's vocal turns the progressive-house build into consolation about grief and moving forward.","sonnet":"A meditation on grief and memory that insists remembering someone does not require moving on or giving up: you can hold to the past — the morning light, the pull-through — without abandoning the present.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-on-a-sunday~bury-us":{"opus":"A doomed relationship torched on the way out, love and hatred fused into the same breath. The couple recognize themselves in each other's deadness and would rather burn out together than survive apart, alright and not-alright at once.","src":"opus"},
"dead-poet-society~5-29-45":{"haiku":"Urban destruction and cosmic observation; the speaker seeks control and elevation, watching cities collapse from beyond atmosphere.","opus":"The title marks the Trinity nuclear test, and the song inhabits a giddy destroyer relishing a city's annihilation as a new kind of freedom. The chill is the throwaway line that meaning vanishes when no one's left alive to feel it.","sonnet":"A speaker imagines detonating a city and watching it glow from a mile up — observing the chair melting, no one outside, the window shattering with a kind of aesthetic wonder. The date in the title and the atomic-blast imagery frame this as either a fantasy of total destruction or a perspective from the moment of the bomb, where freedom is the absence of anyone alive to feel it.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-poet-society~81-tonnes":{"opus":"A stark confession of dulling yourself to endure a crushing burden, tipping back drinks and taking tiny doses to lose the ability to feel low, and to feel high. Imagery of a plunge and steadying words from someone else mark a shaky struggle against being flattened by everything.","src":"opus"},
"dead-poet-society~intoodeep":{"haiku":"A struggling artist trapped by debt and comparisons, numbing themselves with substances while aware they're caught in patterns they can't escape.","opus":"A confessional from an artist watching peers move ahead while he grinds through night shifts, crashes on couches, and keeps making music that no longer sells. The alt-rock band frames the addictive rush of chasing a creative dream as a trap he knows is ruining him but refuses to abandon, wrapping regret and defiance together.","sonnet":"Arrested development with clear eyes — friends are buying houses while the speaker works nights and crashes on couches, writing music nobody wants anymore and drinking beside a fire. The addiction framing is literal and metaphorical at once: this life has its hooks in, leaving the speaker paralyzed and broke but unwilling to surface, too far in to turn back.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-poet-society~salt":{"haiku":"Betrayal and self-serving cruelty corrode a relationship from within; the speaker confronts someone who weaponizes trust and deliberately inflicts pain before vanishing.","opus":"A furious severing from someone who ran their mouth, demanded respect they hadn't earned, and brought their own downfall. The refrain refusing to hear them play victim and pour salt on the wound is contempt hardening into a shutout.","sonnet":"A breakup driven by contempt rather than grief: the speaker has been belittled, choked, and gaslit by someone who refuses to change, and the repeated refrain \"I don't like that\" hardens from complaint into final dismissal as the speaker stops waiting and lets the other person fade away.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-pony~23-never-me":{"haiku":"Resignation to loneliness after rejection; the speaker accepts that someone has left them and they must move forward without understanding or closure.","opus":"A brash breakup kiss-off from a young narrator wasting time on fleeting hookups; she repeats 'I know he's gone' as she convinces herself to set someone free, masking hurt with contempt and refusing to show her feelings.","sonnet":"About accepting that a guy is gone and you keep cycling back to the same knowledge — at 23, the narrator processes the loss, irritations, and patterns of a relationship that never quite worked, arriving at a resigned freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-pony~about-love":{"haiku":"Intimacy alternates between desire and revulsion; the speaker cannot sustain attachment without spiraling into obsession or withdrawal.","opus":"Jittery uncertainty around closeness and commitment, craving someone's touch while feeling like a stranger in his own skin, pacing the walls, stalling before he unravels. Musing on romance and change turns into perpetual delay, bookended by an ordinary voicemail greeting.","sonnet":"A song about physical and emotional longing for someone the speaker is losing touch with — they want to sleep on this person's body, feel ungodly and unlike themselves around them, and are caught between climbing the walls when they call and the dawning realization that resurrection of this relationship is probably a disappointment.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-pony~bullet-farm":{"haiku":"Isolation and incomprehension drive the speaker toward destruction as connection proves more damaging than solitude.","opus":"A restless troublemaker who craves attention yet warns any lover that getting close means getting hurt, insisting on their own charm even as they promise to break a heart.","sonnet":"About a lonely, self-aware attraction that the speaker knows will end badly — they're drawn to someone despite knowing they'll break their heart, oscillating between craving closeness and warning the other person away in equal measure.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-pony~cobra":{"haiku":"A speaker strikes with vengeance and dominance. Death lives inside—the mind shatters while the threat remains.","opus":"A hissing posture of contempt aimed at phonies and cheats, taunting them to flee while it hides a wounded, coming-apart core, the serpent bravado just plating over someone hollowed out and unraveling in the head.","sonnet":"A song about fierce self-assertion against someone who underestimates the narrator — they present themselves as unpredictable and dangerous as a king cobra, sick of the other person's lies and noise, emotionally bleeding but still trying to stop it, insisting those who come for them should run.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-pony~eat-my-dust":{"haiku":"Defiant independence rejects control and dominance; the speaker breaks free and demands respect through fearless self-assertion.","opus":"A swaggering refusal to be controlled or underestimated, leaving a doubter in the dust while claiming the town. The bravado is a defense against anyone who thinks the speaker naive.","sonnet":"A defiant assertion of autonomy against someone who underestimates and tries to control you — the narrator insists they cannot be managed, exposes the other person's doubt and self-interest, and declares they know how to run things better.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-pony~ignore-this":{"haiku":"Exhaustion with existence and social performance erupts into violent defiance; the speaker demands to be left alone to implode privately.","opus":"A snarling rejection of everyone and everything, demanding to be left alone and picking fights for the thrill of it because being sick of it all is the only thing that feels good.","sonnet":"A furious, self-aware rejection of everyone and everything — the speaker saves their best for nothing, wants out of social expectations, and thrives on the aggression of not caring while admitting they never wanted to be part of any of it.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-pony~mana":{"haiku":"Danger and transgression blend with playful seduction as the speaker moves through the world as predatory force, unconcerned with consequences.","opus":"A jittery, self-loathing plea for company masked as fun, courting danger and consequence while sensing the end drawing closer with every reckless shot at the dead sun.","sonnet":"A chaotic, darkly playful song about nihilism and reckless energy — the speaker is attracted to someone while knowing it's all a game, that living and dying feel the same, and the whole thing is treated as spectacle: shoot the dead sun and have fun.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-pony~mk-nothing":{"haiku":"Brainwashed assassination and lost autonomy; the speaker exists as programmed weapon, consciousness dissolved into pure destructive function.","opus":"Drawing on MK-Ultra brainwashing, a mind cracked open and reprogrammed into a numb killing machine, wavering between wanting to feel alive and refusing the drugs and control. The horror is losing the self to a program that turns victims into weapons.","sonnet":"A person under the influence of something — chemicals, control — becomes a weapon, moving in slow motion without emotion toward violence; the intercut audio clips about MKUltra brainwashing make clear this is about mind control turning ordinary people into killers against their will.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-pony~rainbows":{"haiku":"Difference needs no apology. Beauty dwells in what the world calls monster. Rainbows arrive for those who embrace their strangeness.","opus":"A tender reversal for the lonely, bookish kid who hated their own reflection, reassuring that difference is beauty and that color always follows the storm; the singer would return to embrace their younger self and promise they're okay.","sonnet":"A song about overcoming a childhood of isolation and self-hatred and arriving at self-acceptance — the narrator spent years reading alone, hating their face, dreaming through the world, and now for the first time is having fun, reassuring the listener and their younger self that being different is not monstrous and that light always returns.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-pony~speak-of-the-devil":{"opus":"An infatuated speaker fixates on an unshakable, intimidating woman he can't get out of his head yet lacks the words to reach, admitting he's more afraid of her than she'll ever be of anyone; she seems indifferent to his want.","src":"opus"},
"dead-pony~zero":{"haiku":"Transactional desire and emotional bankruptcy leave everyone depleted; the speaker demands something real while offering only hollow exchanges.","opus":"Cold contempt for a self-sabotaging partner whose lessons the narrator reads as fate and mistake. Beneath the dismissal runs a grasping hunger for the shiny thing—status, money, the win—that keeps the whole game hollow.","sonnet":"A sharp-tongued rejection of someone's attempts to manipulate or guilt-trip — the speaker is bored with the cycle, has stopped listening, calls out the other person's self-serving lessons and performed victimhood, and wants whatever piece of brightness or reward they were promised for enduring it.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-sara~violent":{"opus":"A woman pushed past her limit, the demand to be made violent reading as both threat and release. The relentless command to shut up is rage boiling over, a haunted, honest person finally done swallowing it.","src":"opus"},
"dead-to-a-dying-world~cicatrix":{"haiku":"The world is in terminal decline where each day brings more desolation and gray emptiness. Humanity refuses to create new life in this dying reality.","opus":"A desolate end-times tableau as ages roll on and dim sparks of existence sputter in the residue of a vanished era, each dawn greyer than the last, closing on a vow never to father offspring onto a doomed planet. The band blend black metal, doom and crust into sweeping symphonic epics, and this plays as their ecological apocalypse.","sonnet":"Ecological and civilizational grief delivered as incantation — each day greyer than the last, the trees falling, blood the only currency left in human hearts. The refrain against bringing new life into a dying world carries both conviction and sorrow, a people already processing the end as fact rather than warning.","src":"haiku"},
"dead-velvet~come-closer":{"haiku":"Masochistic attraction binds the speaker to their tormentor as pain becomes the only language of intimacy, descent seeming preferable to the emptiness of being unharmed.","opus":"Desire and self-destruction fused into one: a lover invited close even as a knife rests at the throat, feeding personal demons and courting a kind of willing annihilation rather than stopping the bleeding.","sonnet":"A speaker who fed their own demons and made love to darkness until it dragged them underground: wanting to be buried alive and keeping a knife pressed to the neck, they mix death-wish with desire — drawing the other person closer even as the blade is welcome.","src":"haiku"},
"dead7~let-me-drown":{"opus":"Suicidal surrender framed as tidal pull, wanting to sink beneath the waves where memories of another life wait. Pain that never leaves becomes something to settle into and forgive rather than fight, every day already a hurricane in the veins.","src":"opus"},
"dead7~tape-mouth":{"opus":"About silence as complicity and control, holding someone's guilt and selling your own voice, mouth taped shut. It descends into a dissolving, deathlike merging with another, drawn toward oblivion by the very person keeping you quiet.","src":"opus"},
"dead7~two-lives":{"haiku":"Gambits with identity and parallel existences fracture the speaker's coherence as they excavate memory while fearing the discovery that they're hollowed out beneath surface.","opus":"An obsessive, morbid devotion where love and death entwine, the speaker wanting a shared grave and confessing he's been gambling with both lives, more terrified of losing this person than of dying.","sonnet":"A speaker gambling with two lives — their own and someone else's — while excavating a shared history of buried faith and broken bones: the mirror no longer confirms who they are, and they want a place where both can die together, because the debt owed will otherwise be taken from everyone they leave behind.","src":"haiku"},
"deadbeat-girl~i-ve-been-dying":{"haiku":"Desperate connection bleeds through fractured communication and paralyzed attempts at closeness, the speaker dying to merge with someone unreachable.","opus":"Aching to be let in by someone who keeps him at arm's length, oscillating between desperate longing and the exhausted feeling that he simply can't try anymore.","sonnet":"A speaker consumed by longing for someone they can't fully reach describes the desire to let another person in as both compulsion and slow death — 'I've been dying to let you in' — while admitting they have already run out of the will to try.","src":"haiku"},
"deadlands~house-of-cards":{"opus":"A poker-table power fantasy of exposing and dismantling a liar, every metaphor drawn from gambling and bluffs called. The bravado is the whole point: the underdog holds the winning hand and watches the fraud's flimsy house collapse.","src":"opus"},
"deadlights~bathed-in-venom":{"haiku":"A person trapped in toxicity and self-deception wakes to their own hypocrisy and harmful patterns, then chooses to take control and cleanse themselves.","opus":"The Australian metalcore band map a poisoned downward spiral, a world knocked off-kilter as long-buried hypocrisies rise to the surface and negativity soaks in until it consumes them. The turn comes when the narrator finally reclaims control, clearing out the rot and hauling himself upward with truths he'd suppressed for years.","sonnet":"Toxicity absorbed from the outside world has seeped into the body like snake venom, numbing and distorting perception until self-reckoning becomes unavoidable. The narrator moves through paralysis and self-disgust toward a determined act of internal cleansing, reclaiming his life by naming what poisoned it.","src":"haiku"},
"deadlights~born-of-a-lie":{"haiku":"An entire generation is born into lies, misinformation, and apocalyptic systems designed to control them. Sentience backfired and people remain mindless drifting through infested chaos.","opus":"Australian metalcore band Deadlights aim apocalyptic dread at an era of disinformation and slanted feeds, portraying a whole generation as drifters fed reassuring lies. The existential panic resolves into a bleak substitute for the hereafter, an uploaded, digital immortality replacing a faith the narrator has abandoned, salvation traded for escape into the network.","sonnet":"Existential disillusionment aimed at organized religion — a system absorbed in childhood that now reads as engineered control, a weaponized mythology that keeps millions fearful and contained. Beneath the rage sits a lonely, unlistened-to dread and a search for secular ground to stand on.","src":"haiku"},
"deadly-apples~volatile":{"haiku":"Instability and decay consume everything; the speaker becomes corrosive force that others mistake for victim, spreading poison through presence.","opus":"A revolt against a false, controlling safety being stripped away, watching a manufactured reality collapse while resisting a betrayer who wants to consume everything left.","sonnet":"About having everything stripped away by a controlling force that then demands more — the speaker wakes in distress, finds themselves fading, and turns the language of destabilization back on the system or person that took everything.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5-colleen-d-agostino~seeya":{"haiku":"Dreams become refuge as the speaker rejects waking; internal galaxies offer freedom and control that reality cannot provide.","opus":"A refusal to wake into ordinary reality, retreating into a private dreamworld where the sleeper is undefeated and reborn to rule, preferring an inner universe of neon and color to anyone else's version of life.","sonnet":"A person who wins wars in their sleep and has built an entire world behind their eyes refuses to be woken: asleep they are undefeated, and the real world with its black-and-white limitations cannot reach the neon-lit inner cosmos where they are reborn. The plea \"don't try to save me when my mind is finally free\" refuses rescue as interference.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~professional-griefers-feat-gerard-way":{"opus":"A dystopian collage of youth, machines and violence where no prayer gets through and children build friends and weapons alike. The narrator pleads for a life worth bleeding for and a world beyond the wreckage he's made, framing his own creation as diseased at the root.","src":"opus"},
"deadmau5-feat-grabbitz~let-go-extended-edit":{"haiku":"Emotional persistence refuses to release what must be released; the speaker clings to impossible love even as it burns both parties into ash.","opus":"Devotion to someone drifting toward something new, promising to never let go even as the smoke in the air signals the relationship may already be ending.","sonnet":"A song about watching someone you love drift away — searching for something new while what they need is right in front of them — and the narrator's refusal to abandon them even as the relationship visibly falls apart. The smoke and the sense of ending are all around, but the narrator holds on anyway, unable to let go even when he knows it may already be over.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5-feat-grabbitz~let-go-nemesiz-remix":{"opus":"The singer watches a partner glance elsewhere and yearn for a fresh thing, frittering away time hunting for what already stood before them; sensing the finish approaching amid haze in the air, he still swears to keep his grip.","src":"opus"},
"deadmau5-ft-gerard-way~professional-griefers":{"opus":"A dystopian collage of youth, machines and violence where no prayer gets through and children build friends and weapons alike. The narrator pleads for a life worth bleeding for and a world beyond the wreckage he's made, framing his own creation as diseased at the root.","src":"opus"},
"deadmau5-gerard-way~professional-griefers":{"haiku":"Destruction and creation fuse as the speaker catalogs wreckage of their own making; technology and corruption animate a landscape of broken things.","opus":"A nightmarish fairground of video-game bloodshed where the maker looks over every ruined, monstrous creation he has fashioned, pleading for one more turn to spill blood and to be pulled out of the coming round.","sonnet":"A glitchy, violent future-world where machines build friends just like you, death squads are in detention, and carbon-lacing meets pixel screams — the speaker has made broken things and awful things and now asks for the sound to see another world outside of them. The self-indictment \"we are the last disease\" makes the speaker both creator and symptom of the collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5-kaskade-elderbrook-kx5~when-i-talk":{"haiku":"Communication fractures under emotional walls; the speaker pleads to be heard while accepting they remain invisible and isolated.","opus":"The KX5 collaboration voices the ache of not being heard, of being reduced to your faults by someone walled off. It's a plea for basic recognition inside a relationship where the singer suspects the answer is already no.","sonnet":"A speaker questions whether they are heard at all — recognised only by fault, never seen through the wall the listener has built — the simple repeated question 'do you hear me when I talk?' carrying the full weight of feeling invisible in a relationship.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5-lights~when-the-summer-dies":{"haiku":"Existential nihilism and burning; youth rejects meaning and friendship while watching civilization collapse, singing along to the end.","opus":"Nihilism dressed as a summer anthem, borrowing the Smiths' fantasy of hanging the DJ to torch everything and feel nothing. Beneath the singalong is the creator's curse of a thousand ideas that never work, riding out the end by numbing yourself and watching the smoke go up.","sonnet":"About the creative curse and the end of caring — the narrator describes a musician's paralysis (a thousand ideas, one that works), the nihilistic urge to burn it all down, and ultimately choosing to sing along anyway as black smoke rises.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5-shotty-horroh~legendary":{"opus":"A pill-fueled, punchline-stacked flex from the rapper, chasing legendary status through drugs, bravado, and celebrity-name gags. Underneath the boasts sits a soul-searching hotel-floor emptiness, the question of which gives out first, mind or body.","src":"opus"},
"deadmau5~4x4-12-continuous-mix":{"opus":"The SOFI vocal turns a soured dynamic into a parlor game where the balance has finally shifted, and the wronged party gets to drop the other the way a stylus drops onto vinyl. Payback dressed up as play, spinning them dizzily to the beat.","src":"opus"},
"deadmau5~bad-selection":{"opus":"Deadmau5 built his reputation on long, patient progressive-house instrumentals where a track's identity often rests on a single processed vocal stab rather than any lyric. This piece is exactly that: the phrase repeated with no additional text, functioning as rhythmic and textural element inside the production. The words are a sound, not a statement, and the track never pretends otherwise.","sonnet":"No meaningful lyrical content — the track consists entirely of the phrase 'bad selection' repeated with no additional text.","src":"sonnet","fable":"The vocal is a single clipped verdict repeated without variation, so the track reads less as a lyric than as a self-judging tic, the same two-word censure looping until it stops meaning anything and becomes pure rhythmic texture.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The clip is a stock library sample, not a credited vocalist.","themes":["shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"a verdict so often repeated it stops being judgment and becomes the rhythm holding the track together","built":"the two-word censure loops until meaning drains and only the self-judging tic survives"},
"deadmau5~bridged-by-a-lightwave-radio-edit":{"opus":"A wistful hypothetical about distance and lost connection, imagining meeting someone across oceans and time to see if desire could still spark. It aches with the impossibility of collapsing the space between two people who might have been.","src":"opus"},
"deadmau5~careless":{"haiku":"Complete indifference to another's suffering and breakdown; calculated emotional detachment.","opus":"A coldly cruel kiss-off, listing a partner's failures, weak, gullible, contradictory, a walking disaster, while flatly repeating that the singer doesn't care and is too faded to bother, telling them to cry themselves to sleep. The indifference is the whole point, chilling in its detachment. deadmau5, the Canadian electronic producer Joel Zimmerman, wraps the callous vocal in his glossy progressive-house production, the numbness matching the sound.","sonnet":"Cold and deliberate cruelty from someone who has already emotionally checked out — cataloguing a partner's flaws and fragility with total detachment. The refrain isn't triumphant; it's hollow. The speaker knows the day of reckoning came, admits to being faded and checked out long before it arrived, and feels nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~drama-free":{"haiku":"Liberation and independence; leaving behind past monsters and choosing personal freedom.","opus":"Joel Zimmerman's electronic alias gives this a breezy forward momentum matched to its open-highway swagger, an anthem of self-possession and reinvention. The narrator accepts his own darker nature, drives toward no fixed destination while feeling fine, and insists on belonging to no one, hard-won independence rolling along with the road it describes.","sonnet":"A road-trip manifesto for someone cutting loose from chaos — packing up, keeping the engine running, owing nothing to nobody. The tone shifts from self-aware damage inventory to genuine lightness: wind in the hair, nothing bringing her down anymore, moving toward wherever heaven knows leads, drama left at the curb.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~edit-your-friends":{"haiku":"The speaker asserts exclusive status as the last person who remains untouched while others have been destroyed or altered.","opus":"The Canadian producer sets a minimal, menacing loop of repeated instructions to edit your friends, one by one, until only the narrator remains untouched. Stripped to near-nothing and chanted obsessively, the phrase reads as unsettling social-media allegory, a cold logic of picking people off, its meaning carried by ominous repetition rather than any developed narrative.","sonnet":"A minimalist lyric in which the speaker watches someone systematically cut people from their life one by one. The curiosity is detached — the speaker notes being last on the list, not out of self-pity but as plain observation. Social culling is the subject; the speaker's patience in waiting for the inevitable is quietly eerie.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~escape-john-summit-remix":{"haiku":"Yearning to flee reality with a kindred spirit who sees and accepts the true self beneath alienation.","opus":"Longing to be truly seen and to bolt from a confining life with another person, the repeated insistence on not being invisible carrying the fear that the connection and the freedom it promises might not be real.","sonnet":"A moment of real connection in a crowd: the speaker, newly visible to someone whose eyes speak true, proposes escaping together — soul in touch, head kept from flying — and the question \"is it real?\" both doubts and affirms what is happening between them.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~failbait":{"fable":"The mau5 producer never speaks; the track is a pure Cypress Hill takeover, and the read lives in that handoff. Their old weed-anthem imagery gets recast upward into altitude as literal dominance: the hill is both their name and their high ground, so getting the listener high and looking down to defecate on rivals become the same vertical gesture, elevation as contempt. The boast of twenty-plus years standing and the refused name-check (the enemy isn't worth identifying, only stepping on) turn a battle-rap into a statement about survival: they've stopped proving anything and now just occupy the summit, and the repetition of the high past all sense is the point — a mantra so worn it stops meaning intoxication and starts meaning permanence.","fableDeep":"A guest takeover with no host fingerprint in the text: both verses run the veteran playbook — cannabis supremacy framed as a calling, elevation puns stacking the literal hill, the high, and the moral high ground into one brand asset — while the violence stays deliberately generic, knives and hammer-drops for rivals who never get named because, as one line shrugs, naming them isn't worth the breath; anonymity is the deepest insult, opponents reduced to insects, vegetables, and things ground under a sneaker. The hook's construction is the tell: one line of communal generosity, coming to get everybody high, chained directly to one of casual menace, asking why a listener must want to die — hospitality and homicide in a single breath, that doubling being the entire persona. Self-citation is constant — two decades cocked and still wanting more, nods to their own back catalogue — so the verses function less as statements than as trademark renewal, seniority itself the subject. Thesis: legacy-act bravado as ritual, where no individual claim matters beside the demonstration that the voice can still make them.","haiku":"Cypress Hill rappers boast dominance and drug-fueled excess, crushing competition while smoking weed and ruling from their hills.","opus":"A Cypress Hill guest spot that is pure stoner braggadocio, the crew posted up on the hill getting everyone high and daring rivals to test them. It trades in menace and weed-smoke swagger rather than any real subject, the point being dominance and the high itself.","sonnet":"A Cypress Hill collaboration (the lyrics are entirely theirs) built around weed-smoking bravado, territorial aggression toward rivals, and club-culture energy — the thematic core is the group declaring dominance and daring opponents to step to them.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","party & hedonism"],"means":"elevation and contempt collapsed into a single gesture: to get high and look down from there","built":"the repetition of the high past all sense stops meaning intoxication and starts meaning permanence"},
"deadmau5~faxing-berlin":{"haiku":"Compulsion transcends reason: the pull toward reunion overpowers logic, painting desire as inevitable fate rather than choice.","opus":"A few whispered lines of helpless longing, admitting the need to see someone again and the slow realization of falling in love.","sonnet":"A song about compulsive longing for someone you cannot stay away from — not understanding why, not being able to help it, simply recognizing you are falling in love.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~fustercluck":{"haiku":"Confused rambling about technology and connection; expressing bewilderment and frustration.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~ghosts-n-stuff-feat-rob-swire":{"haiku":"A relationship marked by intense connection and emotional upheaval. The narrator seeks to navigate this turbulent bond and reach a promised destination together.","opus":"A restless, out-of-body craving to break free with someone, feeling alone even when close and urging release and abandon. The narrator wants to get it right and reach some destination together tonight, aware that time is running out.","sonnet":"A song about a consuming, disorienting connection to another person — one that pulls the speaker out of his body and away from reality. Despite the distance and uncertainty, there is a persistent desire to push through and reach something real together.","src":"haiku","fable":"Over deadmau5's cold, four-on-the-floor synth throb, Rob Swire narrates a body coming apart on a crowded floor as if the unravelling were ascension, and the track's cleverness lies in never confirming whether the high is spiritual or purely pharmaceutical. Every reach points skyward — toward the stars, the sun, some homeward pull — while the floor gives way beneath: the senses short out, the clock runs down, and the crowd is quietly complicit in whatever fuels the euphoria. When the hook contracts into its blunt repeated vows about arriving somewhere and pulling you under, it registers not as flirtation but as the loop of dependence, still swearing to do it cleanly while the drug machine keeps making the decision for him. The ghosts of the title are the dancers the night wears down to nothing — lit, moving, and never fully present.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the ascent and the chemical loop wearing one face","built":"the blunt repeated hook enacts the repetition compulsion it describes"},
"deadmau5~ghosts-n-stuff-nero-remix":{"haiku":"Spectral presences and haunting emerge through electronic distortion; ghostly absence materializes as persistent force.","opus":"Nero pares it down to the dissociative hook — the loop of floating outside your body and feeling that nothing's real. The repetition sharpens the ghostly detachment, that ache to just get it right and arrive tonight.","sonnet":"A dissociated state in a relationship: out of body, alone and at home at once, nothing feeling true — the one resolution is that they want to play it right, get there tonight, even as the alienation keeps repeating beneath the desire.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~hi-friend":{"haiku":"A dancer receives electronic sensations and information designed to elevate the mind through pulsing rhythm and shared ascension on the dance floor.","opus":"A dancefloor incantation dressed as a transmission from the music itself, coaxing the listener into surrender and ascension on the floor. The friendly hail is the drop's own invitation, pleasure framed as reception and release.","sonnet":"A rave-as-communion song — the music is what you came for, it's opening your mind and lifting you off the floor, and the insistent chant of 'Hi friend' is the moment the crowd becomes one thing.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the friendly greeting of a rave as a mask over something colder: a set of instructions for entering a compliant, receptive state, framed in the second person as a signal being broadcast to a receiver who is asked whether they can feel it. Its verbs quietly migrate from invitation to programming — you are told your mind has been unlocked and your thoughts take flight, but also that you are complying, absorbing tension, feeding retention — so the dancefloor communion slides toward a soft hypnosis where ascension and surrender become the same act. The title's cheer is doing the ironic work: what greets you like a friend is really the music addressing you as a device it is switching on, and the endlessly repeated hail, multiplying into a wall by the final refrain, enacts that takeover rather than describing it — the greeting stops being a word and becomes the mechanism.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"a friendly greeting that was never social, only the signal a device receives when it is being switched on","built":"the hail multiplied into a wall by the final refrain enacts the takeover rather than announcing it"},
"deadmau5~i-remember":{"haiku":"Memory becomes both anchor and relief, allowing one to hold onto love without needing to possess it or move forward from it.","opus":"Consolation for loss: you can let go of someone without moving on or giving up the love you keep. Memory is offered as something to add to rather than surrender, a tender vocal built around the act of remembering how someone pulled you through.","sonnet":"A grief-softened song about holding onto someone who is gone — it argues that letting go does not require forgetting or moving on, that you can still love and remember without giving up; the emotional core is the comfort of memory as a form of continued presence rather than loss.","src":"haiku","fable":"This 2008 progressive-house landmark, built over Kaskade's vocal, keeps restating a single soft paradox rather than resolving it: that you can release someone without abandoning them, and greet whatever comes next while still cradling everything behind you. It is a consolation for grief that refuses the usual command to press forward, insisting instead that recollection is itself the way a person stays intact. That conviction is why the track structurally dissolves into the title phrase chanted until the syllables stop meaning and turn into pure incantation, and why, in a genre engineered for the dancefloor's endless present, its quietly radical move is to make the euphoric act not forgetting yourself but vowing that you never will.","fv":2,"themes":["nostalgia & memory","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"recollection offered as the act that keeps a person intact","built":"in a genre engineered for the present, the euphoric peak is a vow never to forget"},
"deadmau5~i-remember-radio-edit":{"haiku":"Past momentum shifts into present; fragile memories of shared light and interdependence persist as anchors through transitional darkness.","opus":"A tender assurance that letting go doesn't require giving up the love or the memory, that you can carry the past into a new day. The radio edit keeps the core consolation about grief and gentle release intact.","sonnet":"A tender address to someone who helped the speaker through darkness: memory holds the shared nights and morning light, and the message is that you do not have to move on to hold on, do not have to give up to let go — what you pulled me through is still with me.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~i-said-michael-woods-remix":{"opus":"In Michael Woods' remix, the source material is stripped to a single recurring vocal — a phrase looped as percussive hook rather than message. This is standard practice in progressive house, where the remixer treats voice as another synth line, and deadmau5's catalog supplies plenty of such fragments. There is no theme to recover here because none was ever the point; the phrase exists to drive the groove.","sonnet":"Essentially no lyrical content beyond the repeated phrase 'I said' — no discernible theme.","src":"sonnet"},
"deadmau5~let-go-extended-edit-feat-grabbitz":{"haiku":"A speaker watches their partner look away and seek something new while smoke rises around them, sensing the end of the relationship but refusing to let go emotionally.","opus":"Over Deadmau5's expansive progressive-house build, Grabbitz sings a bittersweet vow of devotion as a relationship dissolves, smoke in the air signalling the end even as the narrator swears he will never release his grip. Longing for something new pulls the other away. The producer's typically melodic, emotionally weighted electronic canvas gives the refrain its aching, drawn-out sense of holding on.","sonnet":"Someone watches a drifting partner look elsewhere, sensing the relationship approaching its end, yet refuses to release the attachment. Despite recognizing the smoke and sensing finality, the narrator insists on holding on — loyalty persisting past the point where it can be returned.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~let-go-feat-grabbitz-cube-2-1":{"haiku":"Attachment persists despite inevitable departure as the speaker refuses release; holding becomes the only way to sustain presence in a disintegrating world.","opus":"Loving someone who is always looking elsewhere for something already in front of them, and a vow that even as the relationship ends the speaker will never let go.","sonnet":"A one-sided love held in place by stubbornness: the other person is looking away, wasting days searching for something already in front of them, and the speaker senses the end coming — smoke everywhere, the feeling of \"through\" — but refuses to let go regardless. Loyalty outlasts logic.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~maths":{"fable":"Four lines of robot arithmetic that flunk their own test: one sum true, the next nonsense, and on the repeat the voice grades itself wrong without a flicker of affect. Vocals deployed as a misfiring synth patch — deadpan machine comedy installed where a song's heart would normally go.","fableDeep":"The complete text is a false proof performed twice: the first equation holds, the second is garbage, and the only development across the repetition is the final word — the voice audits itself and returns 'incorrect,' an error report delivered with perfect flatness, after which the loop simply continues, the correction changing nothing. As writing it's a genre joke: electronic music's reputation for cold precision undercut by arithmetic that can't survive two lines, the vocal functioning as instrument rather than statement — there is no speaker, no address, no subject, just the syntactic shape of logic with the logic extracted. That is arguably the whole aesthetic in miniature: rigor as texture, meaning strictly optional, the human voice admitted only as another oscillator that happens to say numbers. The title's dry plural does the rest of the work, promising a discipline the content immediately fails. Thesis: a lyric that exists to flunk its own proof — the machine's idea of a joke, told with the machine's idea of a face.","src":"fable"},
"deadmau5~monophobia":{"haiku":"Loneliness and isolation haunt the speaker who cannot reach or understand another person despite desperate desire.","opus":"The terror of solitude tangled with the hurt of a fractured bond, the narrator wanting to drop by and check whether the other is aching after cutting him down; a menacing hush and the suspicion that their weeping is staged frame a frantic craving for closeness with someone who may be lying.","sonnet":"About the silence that grows after a relationship ends — one person is afraid to reach out, afraid the other is hurting but also afraid of what they'll find, and the song sits in that paralysis between wanting to check in and not knowing what to say.","src":"haiku","fable":"Monophobia names the fear of being alone, and the track voices it as something closer to surveillance than affection, dressing a compulsion to enter and inhabit another person as if that trespass were the truest proof of love. Rob Swire's vocal keeps the speaker stranded outside, forever gauging whether the other person is wounded once the relationship has collapsed, so the yearning never lands anywhere real and instead circles back on its own craving. Every question he throws out gets folded immediately into his own uncertainty, revealing that the actual subject is how little he understands, not the person he claims to want. When he fixates on tears he suspects are performed, he confesses he can no longer believe even the sorrow between them, so his plea to reconnect reads as terror of the silence he keeps insisting is dangerous.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The Rob Swire reunion — nine years after Ghosts 'n' Stuff.","themes":["surveillance & control","love & desire"],"means":"the fear of being alone expressed as a compulsion to monitor whether the other person is still wounded","built":"every question thrown at her folds back into the speaker's own uncertainty, the subject always himself"},
"deadmau5~one-trick-pony-ft-sofi":{"haiku":"Warning about a shallow, deceptive person who has nothing to offer; dismissal and disgust.","opus":"A poisonous kiss-off to an exhausted lover, the singer flatly rejecting the role of leftovers and dismissing them as predictable and thoroughly used. The sugary sweetness of the delivery hides genuine loathing and menace. deadmau5 pairs with vocalist SOFI, whose bright, girlish tone sharpens the cruelty, the buoyant electro-house bounce masking how savage the rejection really is.","sonnet":"Contempt for someone who trades on surface appeal with nothing underneath — a one-trick performer cycling through the same moves, offering a cotton-candy high that crashes hard. The speaker refuses to be impressed, refuses to connect, and positions herself as the one with more going on, too busy moving to sit still for someone this shallow.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~plus":{"haiku":"Journey through sensory experience and timelessness with another person; mystical connection.","opus":"A dissolving, out-of-body reverie about losing yourself completely in another person, feeling at home yet unmoored, senses giving way as the outside world falls silent. Longing and surrender blur into one climbing, wordless want. Deadmau5, the Canadian producer better known for cold instrumental precision, here frames a vocal as texture, the words half-legible on purpose, emotion carried by the pulse rather than the sense.","sonnet":"A dissociated state of connection — floating out of the body, chasing something real through sensation and sound, burning down and lifting toward the sun at once. The lyrics circle a feeling of almost-arrival: we are going to get there tonight, but the destination stays just out of reach, suspended in the momentum of wanting.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~pomegranate":{"opus":"A sensual, guilt-laced tryst on a beach, the pomegranate a Persephone-tinged symbol of forbidden fruit taken anyway. Living once and knowing it's wrong, the lovers surrender to adrenaline and closeness, blissful precisely because it shouldn't happen.","src":"opus"},
"deadmau5~pomegranate-jay-robinson-remix":{"opus":"A remix boiling a beach fling down to its ecstatic sensations — far from home with every worry dissolved, giddy and buzzing as the high melts away sweetly, addressed to a lover who's also the closest of friends.","src":"opus"},
"deadmau5~professional-griefers":{"fable":"Gerard Way narrates from inside a place where childhood and apocalypse have fused: the imagery keeps pinning classrooms, extra pixel-lives and playground faces against detonators and scalpels, as if the nursery and the front line shared one address. The griefer of the title is the online troll who logs on only to sabotage everyone else's game, and the vocal borrows that voice as a builder confessing that everything he assembles comes out ruined and monstrous, then begging to be handed a horizon that isn't his own rubble. The chorus is a plea dressed up as a brag — reaching for vision, for a life worth spilling blood over, while signing his name to every ruin in the frame. It's a comic-book author's instincts (Way's own) welded to a stadium-EDM drop: the beat's high riding a line about being the terminal plague nobody survives, the two forever refusing to settle into each other.","fableDeep":"The title is the key: a griefer wrecks a game for the joy of it, and the narrator is both griefer and game-maker — savouring the broken pieces of a world of his own making ('all the awful things that I made'), where violence carries video-game weightlessness (bonus lives, pixel-screams, respawn as 'save me from the next life') and humanity itself is 'the last disease' griefing its own world.","haiku":"Self-destructive nihilism and technological alienation in a broken world.","opus":"A dystopian, apocalyptic vision of youth, technology, warfare and self-destruction, presented as a chaotic video-game world of broken, awful things the narrator made.","sonnet":"A violent, anarchic vision of technological society destroying itself through machines, bombs, and human brutality.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"a builder confessing every structure he raises comes out monstrous","built":"the EDM drop rides a line about being the terminal plague nobody survives","fvr":"2.4"},
"deadmau5~professional-griefers-vocal-mix-feat-gerard-way":{"fable":"The same arcade apocalypse at full vocal strength: a generation of lab-rat royalty, erased photographs, and weaponized teens surveyed by the maker who built them, begging for sound bright enough to see his handiwork and one more life to bleed through. Euphoria and confession run on the same current.","fableDeep":"The full-vocal cut sharpens the catalogue into generational portraiture: detention brats, erased photographs, girls armed and dosed, a lab-rat king awaiting his rescue team — surveillance-age childhood sketched as both product line and casualty list, while the offhand inversion that the machines are ours and Jesus belongs to the kids swaps the expected owners of faith and technology. The chorus works as a maker's prayer in the grammar of confession: sight and vitality both requested from outside — the sound to see, a life to bleed — while ownership of the wreckage is admitted every few lines, guilt embedded in the hook itself, that-I-made recurring like a countersignature. The escalation moves blame outward by stages: broken things, then awful things, then 'we are the last disease' — artifact to author to species. And the closing plea to be saved from the next life rejects even reincarnation as one more respawn into the same broken build. Thesis: a rave anthem that confesses authorship of the apocalypse it soundtracks, asking only for better visibility.","haiku":"Fractured beauty attracts through broken machinery while faith persists; adolescent consciousness perceives truth where systems build superficial connection.","opus":"The full-vocal cut of the same collaboration, letting Gerard Way carry the war-child imagery straight through: a plea to see and bleed in some brighter world while a maker surveys the awful things he built. Dread and euphoria fused.","sonnet":"A fractured, overloaded world of machines, teenage ESPers, lab-rat kings, and programmed destruction is narrated by a voice that claims responsibility for all the broken and awful things — \"give me a life to bleed\" — positioning the last disease as the system itself, endlessly remaking its own wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~raise-your-weapon-ft-greta-svabo-bech":{"haiku":"A lover weaponizes ego and indifference, tearing love apart and watching the other person's world burn without feeling anything.","opus":"A breakup rendered as warfare, one lover's ego dropping bombs on everything the two built. Surrender is total and immediate, a single word ending it, the tenderness of the verse detonating into the dubstep assault of the chorus.","sonnet":"A breakup framed as warfare: someone with an ego the size of a nation has weaponized words to destroy a relationship, and the narrator watches it all burn while asking them how it feels to be the one who dropped the bomb.","src":"haiku","fable":"The love song is sung entirely in the vocabulary of war, and Greta Svabo Bech's vocal never lets the two families separate: the assault, the missile, the dropped bombs are all aimed at a single heart, so that a breakup registers with the scale of an air raid. The move that earns the read is the third verse, where the imagery narrows from weaponry to a person — a self-adoring, number-one ego signing away a shared peace with one stroke of a pen — revealing the enemy not as an army but as a partner who ends everything with a single word. The refrain to raise your weapon is therefore an invitation to be destroyed, the abandoned one handing over the trigger, and the burning she is asked to watch is the shared thing they built, which makes the surrender total rather than defiant.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Lyrics co-written by Skrillex; a Best Dance Recording nomination at the 54th Grammys.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","war & battle"],"means":"the abandoned one hands the trigger to the person who already ended everything","built":"the third verse narrows the enemy from an army to a single pen stroke, making the scale of loss intimate"},
"deadmau5~seeya":{"haiku":"The speaker prefers to remain asleep and dreaming in a personal mental realm rather than face external reality, prioritizing inner freedom over waking life.","opus":"A song about retreating into an inner dream world where the narrator is undefeated and reborn as a ruler of the skies, preferring to stay asleep in his own reality rather than face the waking world or be saved.","sonnet":"A speaker prefers the sovereign inner world of sleep — where they are undefeated, reborn, and free — to waking reality, and refuses to be rescued from the dream they have deliberately built beyond the reach of the external world.","src":"haiku","fable":"The dream is staged as a war the sleeper is winning, so the plea against waking is really a defense of sovereignty: the covers, the closed eyes, the space beyond galaxies are all territory where he rules and no one else is admitted. The song's move is to invert the listener's concern into a threat—what you read as sinking, as a mind to be saved, he claims as the only place he is finally free and in command. That inversion turns the private color-world, where a waking life's black and white opens into reds no one else has seen, into a boast about access: you cannot follow unless he grants it, and the bones-deep pulse of the chorus insists this ecstatic self-rule is the only reality he will consent to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Vocals by Colleen D'Agostino of The Material.","themes":["freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"sovereignty claimed in sleep, the private color-world guarded as territory no waking visitor may enter","built":"the listener's concern reframed as the threat, turning rescue into unwanted invasion"},
"deadmau5~silent-picture":{"haiku":"The speaker pursues another's notice while awareness of inevitable loss lingers; connection fades before completion.","opus":"Soft-spoken loyalty toward someone who keeps overlooking what already stands before them in favor of a new thing. The singer swears he will keep his grip and make the world finally recognize their worth, even as he feels the bond edging toward its close.","sonnet":"A song about watching someone you love drift away while refusing to let them go — the narrator sees the other person longing for something new, suspects the relationship is ending, yet insists they will never release their grip on them.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~sleepless":{"fable":"Built entirely from one circling verse, the track keeps returning to a couple stranded outside the body-trace of their own lives, awake beside themselves while the world sleeps. The exact repetition of the passage becomes the insomnia it describes, a loop preserving the abandoned things by refusing to let the night end.","fableDeep":"One eight-line stanza sung twice, verbatim — the form is the insomnia, a thought the mind can only re-run. The imagery is forensic: a chalk outline marks where something died, and the couple stands outside it, simultaneously mourners, witnesses, and suspects; 'beside ourselves in time' doubles the idiom for distress with the literal picture of standing next to their own past selves. 'Sleepless and still untied' cuts in two directions — released from each other, or never successfully bound together — and the ambiguity is load-bearing. The center is a clean aphorism: 'the only thing I want is the last thing I need,' desire and self-knowledge in exact opposition, stated without any attempt at resolution. The proposed remedy — maybe falling asleep, to save what was left behind — offers unconsciousness as preservation, sleep as the only place the lost thing still survives, while the stars keep the vigil going. Thesis: grief as a loop pedal — a verse that can only repeat because the situation it describes cannot advance.","haiku":"Two people find themselves untethered and sleepless like stars, wanting nothing but carrying regret; they stand outside meaning while awakeness becomes cosmic isolation amid preserved fragments.","opus":"A muted night-piece about two people marooned at the finish of something, lingering beside the traced mark of what has died, craving the exact thing they most ought to release. Wakefulness stands in for grief left gaping beneath frigid starlight.","sonnet":"Two people stranded somewhere undefined, unable to sleep, unable to commit — the singer wants what he can't have and knows it, standing awake outside the chalk outline of what they were.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The voice is Joel's own — a spoken poem he later folded into Telemiscommunications as well.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"insomnia as a form of fidelity, the wakefulness preserving what the night would otherwise close","built":"exact repetition of the single verse enacts the loop it describes rather than describing it twice"},
"deadmau5~sofi-needs-a-ladder":{"fable":"The song wears a breakup as a game of one-upmanship — musical chairs, traded shoes, the tables turning — where the singer insists she now holds the power she was denied, yet every taunt is scored to the machinery of a turntable: she drops the other person like a needle, sets them walking in circles, hits the beat, presses repeat. That is the read: the domination she brags about is indistinguishable from a loop. The more she claims to have flipped the script, the more she reveals herself stuck in the same groove, spinning the ex around a track that only returns to its start — attention and revenge collapsed into one compulsive replay, the song enacting it by repeating its own chorus until the boast becomes the trap.","fableDeep":"The revenge is written in the producer's own equipment: 'drop you like a needle on the record… watch you walk in circles… just press repeat' — the ex becomes the vinyl, spun in circles for the DJ's amusement, the broken record replayed on purpose. But the taunt keeps confessing: 'I can be your new favorite waste of time and you'll be mine — all I want is your attention, it's all the same thing.' The game and the wound share one sentence; flipping the script still means staying in the play. The gloat is real, and so is the tell that winning musical chairs keeps you at the party.","haiku":"Power reversal and vindictive comeuppance flip the script as the manipulated becomes the manipulator, turning cruelty back on its source.","opus":"Built around a vocal about turning the tables on a manipulative partner, relishing the moment control flips and the game gets played back on its player. The relentless progressive-house build mirrors that sense of inevitable, mechanical payback.","sonnet":"A petty-revenge power-play song about a relationship where the tables have turned — the narrator enjoys watching the ex try to keep up in shoes that don't fit now, and despite the sweet-sour dynamic, still offers to be each other's favourite waste of time if they're up for the game.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title traces to its live-only precursor You Need a Ladder, built on Zelda's Overworld theme — the ladder is the game's — stripped out to dodge copyright before SOFI's vocals arrived.","themes":["anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"revenge and obsession are the same groove, the domination a loop the song keeps pressing repeat on","built":"the boast of having flipped the script enacted by a chorus that only ever returns to its own start"},
"deadmau5~sometimes-i-fail":{"haiku":"Commentary on social uncoolness and failure to fit cultural expectations.","opus":"A sampled beatnik lecture cataloguing what counts as uncool, the whole thing a deadpan comedy of hipster codes about drugs, informants and wearing shades after dark. There is no song beneath it, only spoken vintage kitsch repurposed as a track. Deadmau5, the Canadian electronic producer, often builds around found audio like this, letting an absurd relic carry the piece where a lyric would otherwise sit.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~somewhere-up-here":{"haiku":"Desperate need for someone's presence and fear of abandonment; obsessive attachment.","opus":"A vocal-led track from the Canadian house producer built on soaring electronic uplift paired with romantic desperation. The singer begs a partner to stay, insisting he could never bear their absence, so that the euphoric swell curdles into something closer to anxious dependence than pure devotion.","sonnet":"A relationship held together by mutual need and mutual fear of letting go — the speaker high above something, asking questions that don't get answered, clinging to a person whose eyes have gone opaque. The song won't resolve: staying requires conditions the other person may not meet, but leaving is equally impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~support":{"haiku":"Confrontational minimalism: repeated challenge to authority or identity wrapped in pure rhythm.","opus":"Built entirely on a single confrontational challenge, it weaponizes the demand to know who someone thinks they are, letting the taunt carry the whole aggressive charge of the track.","sonnet":"A challenge — \"who the hell do you think you are?\" — repeated twice with no resolution, projecting confrontation or self-interrogation with nothing more to go on.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~take-care-of-the-proper-paperwork":{"fable":"One bureaucratic instruction looped over an instrumental until office-speak becomes incantation — the least transcendent sentence in the language installed exactly where dance music usually promises transcendence. Administration as mantra: the phrase repeats past meaning into pure rhythm, dutifully filing itself, correctly, forever.","fableDeep":"The text is a single parenthesized sentence repeated six times without variation — an aside that never ends. The joke is register collision: rave architecture, built to dissolve selves, wrapped around compliance-office language, an instruction to complete forms sitting where the exhortation to lose yourself should be. Repetition then does what repetition does — semantic satiation, the words decomposing into syllable-percussion — so the track demonstrates its genre's core mechanism using deliberately worthless raw material: proof that the loop carries the charge, never the sentence. There is a sly self-portrait in it too: the producer as professional cynic framing music-making itself as paperwork, art as an administrative deliverable, the title virtually a mission statement for a career spent insisting the machinery matters more than the message. No speaker, no address, no stakes — procedure elevated to liturgy. Thesis: bureaucracy run through the rave engine until instruction becomes incantation, the emptiest possible lyric proving lyrics were never the point.","src":"fable"},
"deadmau5~telemiscommunications":{"fable":"The verse is a phone call rendered entirely as one side of a fractured exchange — greetings that trip over each other, the constant deferral of who speaks first, the call always cut short by someone waving the speaker over — so that the couple's love is perpetually postponed by the very devices meant to connect them, the pun in the title naming the ailment exactly. The two verses widen this from a private stammer into ambient civic noise (sirens, announcements, group laughter, a stranger needing directions), until intimacy has to compete with the whole clamoring world for a single unbroken second of contact. Imogen Heap's gift for vocal-fragment collage turns the outro's obsessive repetition of the unasked question into the tender thing that the busy day kept swallowing — the words of love that were felt all along but never once got said aloud.","fableDeep":"The first verse is transcript realism: one side of a phone call rendered whole with its dead air, delays, and deflections — the claim that everything's fine, the excuse of a busy week — the goodbye arriving before anything true gets said; banality does the emotional work because banality is all the channel permits. The second verse jump-cuts through sirens, check-in queues, shoes off at security, a stranger demanding directions — montage form mimicking the fragmentation it mourns. The chorus names the war in the title's own portmanteau and sets the terms — 'contact versus telemiscommunications' — one real touch weighed against infinite mediated almosts, with the coined 'horizontalisms' wistfully denoting the one thing phones cannot transmit: lying down together. The outro loops the question of whether love got said today until it hovers between devotion and checkbox, tenderness automated into routine. Both voices plainly love each other; the failure is entirely infrastructural. Thesis: a love song audited by its transmission losses — intimacy measured in dropped packets.","haiku":"Modern communication through technology fails to connect people despite constant contact; calls, messages, and screens replace real intimacy, leaving them asking if love is even being expressed.","opus":"A duet about a couple drifting apart through the very devices meant to connect them, every call swallowed by delays, distractions and crowds, the tragedy that they never manage one real moment of contact.","sonnet":"About physical distance corroding intimacy — two people trying to connect through bad phone lines, noise, and distraction, landing on a moment of \"did I tell you I loved you today?\" that exposes how much real contact they've been missing. The song mourns the gap between technological connection and actual closeness.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written long-distance — Heap and Joel never met in person during its making, trading only a few emails and calls; her stated frame is technology eroding the connection it promises.","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the words of love that were felt all along but never once got through the noise that was meant to carry them","built":"the outro's obsessive repetition of the unasked question is the buried declaration the busy day kept swallowing"},
"deadmau5~terrors-in-my-head":{"haiku":"A cheerful greeting ritual promising optimism and a perfect day ahead. The song is a simple, encouraging morning affirmation.","opus":"A chirped good-morning affirmation set against the title's dread, the forced cheer curdling into something sinister. The gap between the sunny script and what it's laid over is the whole unease.","sonnet":"A saccharine children's morning-routine script delivered in contrast to the song's ominous title, functioning as ironic juxtaposition — the cheerful surface masking whatever dread the title implies.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole lyric is a chirpy wake-up address, an unbroken chain of morning cheer promising a peerless day ahead. Against a title about the terrors in one's head, that relentless brightness curdles into something menacing — the forced smile you're ordered to wear as the real nightmare.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The voice is a sampled ASMR wake-up alarm — the cheer is literally found audio.","themes":["artifice & performance","madness & the mind"],"means":"the forced smile of relentless morning cheer curdling into the nightmare the title already named","built":"title and lyric placed in direct opposition: the content is the performance the terrors require"},
"deadmau5~the-oshawa-connection":{"haiku":"Personal rejection and hurt that remains even after forgiveness; a scarring experience.","opus":"A found-sound piece built from a call-in radio show, a caller named Judy speaking haltingly about rejection and forgiveness, folded into a hypnotic refrain about taking a dive in Oshawa. It is texture and mood rather than written song, an overheard vulnerability looped into meaning. Deadmau5, the Canadian producer, often treats such vintage vocal fragments as the emotional core of an otherwise instrumental track.","sonnet":"A radio caller from Oshawa describes being rejected by a group, choosing forgiveness and silence about the person responsible, and the repeated refrain of taking a dive works as both confession and metaphor — falling, failing, absorbing the blow without fighting back.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~the-veldt-8-minute-edit":{"haiku":"Children create an immersive digital world of comfort with machines, trapped in love with their artificial reality while nature's predators roam outside ignored.","opus":"Based on imagery of a happy, machine-run home where children are lulled to sleep by digital technology while lions feed on remains outside, questioning whether this comfortable, screen-built world is real or a dream.","sonnet":"Children build a world entirely mediated by technology — machines rock them to sleep, digital families replace the real, and lions feed on remains outside while the inhabitants remain inside, too in love with their constructed reality to ever leave.","src":"haiku","fable":"Bradbury's story of a nursery that turns lethal is recast here as a soothing anthem the automation sings back, the house adored because it has quietly supplanted the parents who once did the rocking. The comfort refrain and the domestic cooing are set against a distant horizon of predators feeding and cries no one answers, so the tenderest lines carry the dread rather than defusing it. When everything else falls away and only the one line about what the children built keeps looping, the repetition becomes the trap itself, a sated hum with no argument and no way out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Based on Ray Bradbury's 1950 story — and vocalist Chris James was discovered when he tweeted Joel his own vocal take mid-livestream while the instrumental was being built.","themes":["surveillance & control","family & blood","death & grief"],"means":"the house loved past the point of need becomes the predator the children always had","built":"the looping of the single remaining line turns the comfort refrain into the mechanism of entrapment"},
"deadmau5~unspecial-effects":{"haiku":"Illusions and false impressions crumbling away.","sonnet":"A single image repeated until it becomes a verdict — whatever glamour or illusion someone projected is dissolving, washing away, stripping down to nothing. The repetition is the whole argument: there was never anything underneath.","src":"haiku"},
"deadmau5~when-the-summer-dies":{"haiku":"Cultural decay accelerates toward apocalypse; the speaker watches civilization self-destruct through collective delusion while mourning the loss of meaning and consequence.","opus":"Reckless abandon costumed as doomsday, stringing up the DJ and setting the streets alight as an anthem of indifference while the season closes. Beneath it lies a maker's quiet grief about being flooded with countless notions when barely a single one truly lands.","sonnet":"About the nihilistic recklessness of people who know everything is temporary — the narrator insists he won't feel a thing when the summer (the good times, the illusions) dies, and the prescription is burn the city, abandon your friends, and sing along to the chaos anyway. There is a second layer about the \"creator's curse\" — being stuck with a thousand ideas and only one that works, ending up singing in a blacked-out hearse.","src":"haiku","fable":"A calming stream of sky-hued affirmations and self-soothing mantras slowly curdles, the reassurance about closing your eyes and feeling fine giving way to a drifting darkness overhead that signals the season's end is real ruin, not comfort. Midway the disguise falls away, as a chant to kill the entertainer, torch the streets, abandon everyone you love and merely chant along in unison recasts the collective refrain as complicity, a crowd sedated into applauding its own wreckage. The final passage swivels the blaze inward onto the maker, a curse of endless notions and scarcely any that land, an artist coasting toward ruin in a lightless funeral car, so closing the eyes one last time reads at once as surrender to the revelry, to exhaustion, and to death.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"A deadmau5 and Lights collaboration — her vocal, their second pairing after Drama Free.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"a crowd sedated by its own refrain into applauding the wreckage it chants along to","built":"the blaze turned inward at the close reframes the whole collective revelry as the artist's private funeral"},
"deadstar~dont-it-get-you-down":{"opus":"A weary address to someone circling the drain of addiction or self-destruction, recognizing the pain as a familiar loop that keeps returning like a first wound. The comfort offered is grim: falling away may be the only direction left.","src":"opus"},
"deafheaven~canary-yellow":{"haiku":"Nature's transformation and decay intertwine with romantic transcendence, where suffering becomes beauty and mortality feels eternal.","opus":"Blackgaze at its most transcendent, climbing toward the heavens while lingering on ash, rot and the pulse of a lover's veins. It cradles beauty and death in a single breath, a gorgeous, endless dark that overwhelms even as it devours.","sonnet":"A dense, impressionistic descent into nature and transformation — clay, vines, birds, ash water — that pivots into an overwhelming romantic image: the speaker is suffused with a lover's blood rushing through them across an everlasting handsome night, beauty and mortality fusing until they choke on both.","src":"haiku"},
"deafheaven~heathen":{"haiku":"The speaker exists outside sacred order, unredeemable and burning with their own heat.","opus":"Blackgaze turned inward on a man who mistakes lust and self-reinvention for meaning, numbing himself into a coma of desire and abandoned intimacy. Beneath the wall of sound, the song holds out that being adored by someone might still redeem him.","sonnet":"A third-person portrait of a man losing himself to lust and numbness, closing his heart at the moment trust arrives, driving with his eyes shut into mystery. He forfeits intimacy for desire, leaves fondness for weakness, and the speaker watches — forgetful, then decisive — until someone adored lifts him and the possibility of true love reasserts itself, fragile and unearned.","src":"haiku"},
"dealer~crooked":{"haiku":"The narrator condemns the masses as rot and offers no forgiveness for those who exploit and betray. In a dog-eat-dog world, the betrayer is the bitch.","opus":"The Australian metalcore act unloads on a sanctimonious manipulator, flatly denying absolution for a betrayal disguised as victimhood. Citing Orwell on intention equaling action, the speaker paints himself as a scapegoat devoured by the crowd, and in this ruthless, survival-of-the-fittest score-settling he demands honesty up front and leaves his betrayer marked as the true coward.","sonnet":"A narrator ejects a self-righteous, hypocritical antagonist from his life with cold fury, invoking Orwellian logic to frame the target as already corrupt in thought. The anger is total and the refusal of forgiveness absolute — a brutal severance from someone who smiled while exploiting trust.","src":"haiku"},
"dealer~grotesque":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation and internal death, where the narrator feels abandoned and hears the voice of depression offering escape from suffering as the only freedom.","opus":"The Australian heavy band confront suicidal thought head-on, personifying depression as a stalking beast murmuring that ending things demands nothing. Feeling discarded and despised, and fed up with a hollow, exploitative music industry, the narrator delivers a bleakly honest refrain about being already lifeless within, unflinching about the pull toward oblivion.","sonnet":"Depression speaks through the image of a black dog whispering suicidal logic, while an exhausted narrator absorbs betrayal from an industry that deals in false damage. The grotesque punchline is that killing what is already dead inside is impossible — he has survived by becoming unreachable.","src":"haiku"},
"dean-lewis~lose-my-mind":{"haiku":"Absence corrodes the self; the speaker spirals without the person they love, finally recognizing they must let go to survive.","opus":"The unraveling of someone who only feels whole with a person he keeps losing, love as a game he's always bound to lose. Its ache is the moment he chooses to let go for their sake even though it costs him his mind.","sonnet":"A man who knows a relationship is over and has caused harm still cannot accept the loss, because losing this person means losing his grip on himself entirely. He arrives at the painful recognition that the most loving act may be to release her, even as it breaks him.","src":"haiku"},
"dean-lewis~waves-acoustic":{"haiku":"Watching your wild youth disappear into the past, trying futilely to hold time still.","opus":"Grief and the loss of youth arriving in tides you can't hold back, the person you were slipping through your fingers no matter how hard you grip. It's about mourning a self and a time that keep receding.","sonnet":"Grief and nostalgia wash in and out like tides as the speaker mourns the loss of youth, freedom, and a feeling he once took for granted — he watches his wild past recede and wonders if that sense of aliveness can ever come back.","src":"haiku"},
"dear-god~slamdance":{"haiku":"As the world collapses, youth drink and dance in defiance while feeling paralyzed by generational corruption and futility.","opus":"Rap-rock act Dear God dance defiantly through societal collapse, drinking and pounding out bass while everything crumbles. Raising a glass to friends amid a life that needs fixing, the narrator blames an older generation for wrecking things, the mosh a bitter reply to the sense that nothing remains to do and no comfort is left to find.","sonnet":"A generation economically abandoned by those before it dances on a collapsing stage, drinking and slamming because paralysis and revelry are equally available responses to a world with no exit. The party is not celebration but the only motion available when every door is locked.","src":"haiku"},
"dear-god~weaponize-this":{"haiku":"Words and voice become tools for battle, converting language into ammunition against fraud and complacency.","opus":"A hardcore rant that turns language itself into a weapon, spitting at tough-guy posturing and passive bystanders while insisting the real fight is refusing war and hate even as everyone around goads violence. The contradiction is the point.","sonnet":"Hardcoreethics of self-defence against systemic indifference — the narrator can't tell gunshots from fireworks, watches frauds grease their way to power while the crowd stands by, and resolves to weaponise their voice as a barracks-and-larynx response. The tension between \"I don't want war, I don't want the hate\" and the boxcutter in the coat is honest: peace is the preference, but they'll protect what's theirs.","src":"haiku"},
"dear-mother~a-soul-for-hire":{"haiku":"Betrayal of self and other becomes the ultimate transaction; the speaker refuses commodification and rejects the sale of their essential being.","opus":"A refusal to be bought or used, standing at the shore of a doomed relationship and declaring that whatever the liar desires, the speaker's soul is not for hire.","sonnet":"A person refusing to be owned or exploited: walls close in, dawn brings imprisonment, and the shore holds no memory of them — but they will not disagree with liars or unfold the diamond leaves someone else desires. The refrain \"I won't be your soul for hire\" is the hard limit drawn against manipulation, delivered after acknowledging they are scared and undecided about whether any of it was meant to be.","src":"haiku"},
"dear-mother~satellite":{"opus":"Metalcore built on razing everything to scrape through a single evening, pointed at people trading away their souls and the deceivers surrounding them. Order and havoc merge until the wrecked orbiting device stands in for the self, circling in solitude.","src":"opus"},
"death-angel~3rd-floor":{"haiku":"Forced confinement compounds through chemical subjugation, the patient's mind dismantled from within by the very institutions designed to heal.","opus":"The terror of psychiatric confinement told from inside the ward, where medication meant to free the mind feels like abduction. Thrash claustrophobia as a one-way door that locks behind you, no one believing you're being destroyed.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a psychiatric ward describes forced medication, solitary confinement, and the terror of having been locked away permanently. The song is a plea from inside a system that has declared the speaker beyond saving.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~5-steps-of-freedom":{"haiku":"A person trapped in confinement—literal or psychological—struggles to break free and reclaim their humanity against a system designed to contain and destroy them.","opus":"A prisoner serving a long sentence rages against confinement, feeling his mind caged and his life reduced to survival. He resolves to break out and reclaim his humanity, even as death seems the likely end.","sonnet":"A prisoner, trapped in a cycle of pain and confinement with no hope of release, contemplates the few steps between his cell and a final escape — whether through rebellion or death.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~buried-alive":{"haiku":"The speaker imagines themselves entombed alive, suffocating under guilt and judgment within a corrupt, cannibalistic society. Maggots and darkness await; they feel condemned by both external justice and internal despair.","opus":"The suffocation of being condemned and entombed alive, railing at the false judges and the God who presides over it. Thrash claustrophobia where the real horror is dying slowly while the world calls it justice.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a corrupt, cannibal social world — falsely judged, suffocating — confronts their own burial alive, questioning a God who allows this, as worms and maggots wait and the saints extract payment from sinners.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~carnival-justice":{"haiku":"Torture and execution are described as spectacle and punishment for alleged witchcraft and heresy. The song portrays modern sadism disguised as justice.","opus":"Thrash imagining punishment regressed to medieval torture staged as spectacle, sadism dressed as justice with the condemned processed toward death; the carnival framing indicts a society that turns cruelty into entertainment.","sonnet":"A grotesque tour through historical and fantasy torture — inquisition-style trials, electrocution, burning, drowning — framed as punishment spectacle, reveling in the theater of extreme violence dressed up as justice.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~dethroned":{"haiku":"A rebel refuses to be contained or shaped by society, rejecting all authority and institutions. They declare independence and promise violent reckonings with those who would control them.","opus":"A rebellious rejection of authority, teachers, and religion, building toward a violent revolution that dethrones a king and kills the false savior.","sonnet":"A figure who has never been contained by institutions — teachers, neighbors, Jesus — arrives at a moment of violent reckoning, positioning himself as both victim of a false savior and instigator of a revolution against whoever currently holds power.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~god-vs-god":{"haiku":"An internal conflict between self-love and self-destruction, portrayed as competing spiritual forces where attachment and dependency war with the desire to break free. The song centers on toxic emotional bondage and the plea for healing.","opus":"A wracked, addictive reliance on a person or force that seems to both mend and imprison; the speaker cannot pull loose, feels bound and doomed to failure, and pleads to be cured and let go.","sonnet":"The narrator is trapped in a destructive relationship that feels both sacred and enslaving — drawn to someone who built walls and stole their future, they oscillate between needing to escape and being unable to leave, pleading for God to heal the part of them that remains in chains.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~lord-of-hate":{"haiku":"A tyrant who preyed on innocents faces public humiliation and historical erasure, their legacy reduced to shame and divine judgment.","opus":"A fallen tyrant is stripped of power and mocked, the once-feared master now beaten, hungry and pelted with stones; the throughline is comeuppance, a fraud and predator who preyed on the innocent finally exposed and set adrift, told to prepare to meet his God.","sonnet":"A takedown of a fallen abuser of power — someone who preyed on the innocent and thought themselves untouchable now faces the judgment of history, exposed as a fraud whose legacy is fading like a sinking ship.","src":"haiku","fable":"A jeered execution of a fallen tyrant, staged as the crowd's revenge: the once-feared master is dragged out begging to be fed, pelted with rocks and mockery while the people he tormented laugh him down. Death Angel turn the whole thing into a reckoning with reputation — the chorus indicts him not for his cruelty in the moment but for the rot he leaves behind, and the payback the song promises is that history will file him not as a monster but as a clown, his menace deflating into a hollow rumor no one fears anymore. The final turn makes the retribution theological rather than merely political: stripped of the legend that made him powerful and named a swindler, he is handed over to a judgment above the mob's reach.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","politics & society","faith & the occult"],"means":"retribution filed not as violence but as the erasure of legend","built":"the final turn hands the tyrant to judgment above the mob's reach"},
"death-angel~resurrection-machine":{"haiku":"Life is deceptive illusion masking death; gods speak of a coming kingdom where soldiers attack forever without fear or end, embracing immortal violence.","opus":"A defiant staring-down of death that recasts nature's cycles as tricks designed to sap the will to live. The message is fearlessness: only dying separates this life from an endless hereafter with no true conclusion, so one should press onward toward the end without pain or dread of destiny.","sonnet":"A declaration that death is not the end — the sun, the stars, and all of existence are illusions meant to distract from the eternal truth that consciousness continues; the narrator rides forward fearlessly, having accepted mortality as merely a transition.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~sonic-beatdown":{"haiku":"A force emerges to uplift and energize the downtrodden, sweeping away oppression with unstoppable momentum and collective power.","opus":"A concert battle cry, the veteran thrash outfit framing their show as an unstoppable caravan arriving to haul you out of a hard stretch and refusing to let anybody belittle you. Togetherness and pushback staged as an aural onslaught.","sonnet":"An anthem directed at the audience: the band invites people who have had a rough year to leave it behind and lose themselves in the overwhelming physical force of live music, the wall of sound as collective catharsis.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~soulless":{"haiku":"Soldiers return from war traumatized and hollowed out, their humanity stripped away; they descend into addiction and despair while their families wait helplessly.","opus":"An anti-war lament for a young soldier who follows his father into combat, emptied of his soul by daily destruction and dying to friendly fire. The wrenching turn to a mother cradling a needle by a dying, addicted body links the trauma of war to a hollowed-out, wasted homecoming.","sonnet":"The psychological destruction of a soldier through combat — a young man whose daily life is a gun, who falls to friendly fire, whose mother waits in dread at the door — leading to a portrait of veteran breakdown: addiction, blood-stained walls, and a body wasting away forgotten.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~steal-the-crown":{"haiku":"The oppressed must rise up against those in power who hoard wealth and resources, breaking through the structures that keep them down. Through rage and collective action, they can seize what has been denied them and topple the ruling class.","opus":"A rebellion anthem taking aim at a pampered elite who feast in luxury while leaving scraps for everyone beneath them. Thrash veterans Death Angel turn it into a rallying call to rise up, reject compromise, tear down the barriers keeping people down, and wrest power from the ruling class until the old hierarchy is gone for good.","sonnet":"A revolutionary song about rising up against a ruling class that eats platinum and tosses crumbs — the narrator calls for smashing every wall, seizing power from those enthroned above, and never compromising until the people are on top.","src":"haiku","fable":"A class-war anthem with no mask to peel back: Death Angel, Bay Area thrash lifers writing on their comeback-era Killing Season, lay the grievance out plainly — a moneyed few gorge on platinum pie in their palaces while flicking crumbs at everyone below. The song's whole architecture is a shove from stupor into revolt, opening by asking whether the crowd can register the lies at all, then hardening into a chant to rise and move, and finally escalating into demolition that flattens each barrier to storm the throne room and seize it. The title's promise of taking the crown is where righteous uprising tips into pure seizure of power, and the band doesn't flinch from that appetite; the closing verse's talk of decimating their kind with rage frames revolt not as reform but as replacement, one hierarchy toppled so the newly-risen can rule in turn.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"righteous uprising that tips into seizure of power","built":"the architecture runs from stupor through chant to demolition"},
"death-angel~the-devil-incarnate":{"haiku":"An immortal predator shape-shifting through time, stealing souls and deceiving the living. The creature survives catastrophes and perpetually murders, shedding skin and stealing souls.","opus":"Bay Area thrash veterans Death Angel voice an immortal predator that survives while its prey dies, shedding skin and stealing souls across the birth of time. Eden's poisoned apple and the swinging inevitability of fate frame the devil as an eternal deceiver, mercy denied and destiny inescapable. The narrator's undying persistence becomes the cruelest weapon of all.","sonnet":"A predatory force speaks in first person — shapeshifting, soulless, operating across all of history. It outlives every victim through deception and hunger, a serpentine intelligence that sheds skin and steals souls while its prey run out of road, out of time, and out of prayer.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~the-noose":{"haiku":"An oppressor's cruelty and wealth breed a reckoning where consequences finally arrive. The powerful will face justice and wear the noose.","opus":"An accusation against a corrupt, wealth-worshipping oppressor who has shamed idols, silenced truth-tellers, and even damaged his own children. The refrain warns that a reckoning is coming, a day when the oppressor will wear the noose he earned.","sonnet":"About someone who abandoned their soul and family for wealth and status, corrupting their children and silencing truth-tellers in the process — the narrator promises a reckoning is coming in the form of a noose. It's a moral condemnation of greed and its collateral damage.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~thicker-than-blood":{"haiku":"Predators pursue victims with violent passion, describing the hunt and consumption as an expression of bond deeper than family blood.","opus":"Bay Area thrash veterans Death Angel stage a predatory hunt, twisting the stalking, catching, and devouring of prey into something perversely framed as affection rather than work. The victim fights hopelessly through a night it will never escape. In keeping with the band's taste for horror storytelling, the title implies that a bond of hunger outweighs kinship, appetite consuming its prey again and again.","sonnet":"A predatory pack hunts, overpowers, and consumes prey with ferocious relish, framing the violence as love rather than labor. The drive is visceral and insatiable — each night folding into the next in an endless cycle of pursuit and feeding.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~thrown-to-the-wolves":{"haiku":"Betrayed by protectors, a person is displayed and butchered, left to die among enemies while the powerful feast.","opus":"The betrayed are marched to their doom by the very guardians meant to shield them, their honor torn away and cast to a savage pack. Death Angel, Bay Area thrash veterans since the 1980s, weave scriptural imagery of kin betraying kin through a study of institutional treachery, where golden promises collapse into abandonment and circling scavengers.","sonnet":"Betrayal at the hands of those sworn to protect — an individual stripped of dignity and fed to a merciless pack by false shepherds. Biblical undertones frame the abandonment as an ancient pattern: the trusted deliver the innocent to death, leaving only the grave.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~truce":{"haiku":"A person survives overwhelming life hardships and pain, but discovers their opening of self to love only gets rejected as others replace the heart they offered.","opus":"A hard-won reckoning with a scarred survivor who has outlasted stronger people but gains nothing, the debts of a painful life still owed. The truce that was erased leaves a heart already replaced, endurance without reward.","sonnet":"Someone who has endured lifelong pain and survived trial after trial wakes to catastrophic news that shatters even their hardened resilience — the truce they made with suffering has been broken, and the heart they opened has already been replaced by something harder.","src":"haiku"},
"death-angel~when-worlds-collide":{"haiku":"Catastrophe and societal collapse arrive as divisions between peoples grow insurmountable; humanity destroys itself through greed and refuses to heal the wounds it inflicts.","opus":"An apocalyptic broadside against a generation the band sees as spiritually maimed, its selfishness and nationalism sealing everyone's doom. Cursing borders and states, it depicts a doomed cycle of division and denial finally reaching its end, humanity's wound reopened past any cure.","sonnet":"About civilizational collapse driven by selfishness and division — this generation walks wounded through a world where nations, religions, and ideologies have carved canyons of division, history repeating until the cycle finally breaks catastrophically with no repair possible.","src":"haiku"},
"death-bells~only-you":{"haiku":"Hyperfixation on a single person whose absence feels catastrophic; the world would stop for them alone and the speaker is paralyzed in their presence.","opus":"The post-punk act sketches a numbed narrator who has lost his grip on memory yet fixates on one person as the only thing that could ever halt his world. Others drift past keeping up appearances while he begs for a scrap of closeness and keeps insisting he ought to act, frozen between yearning and paralysis.","sonnet":"Memory has erased the specific person but not the gravitational pull they exerted — the world would stop for them alone, and yet they run from something unnamed. The speaker stands frozen, watching others perform their social face-saving rituals, unable to move, unable to forget something already forgotten in detail but not in weight.","src":"haiku"},
"death-blooms~anger":{"opus":"An offer to become a lightning rod for another person's rage. The narrator tells someone whom life has misled, and whose mind harbors a sickness whispering for their death, that fury is the only language they have left, then volunteers to absorb it and wage their battle for them.","sonnet":"An offer of solidarity to someone consumed by rage and trauma — the narrator invites them to pour all that anger out, promising to absorb it and fight their battles so they don't have to keep suffering alone under the weight of thoughts that want them dead.","src":"sonnet"},
"death-blooms~filth":{"haiku":"Feels buried in moral corruption and self-doubt, unable to breathe or see past the filth both internal and external. Becomes one's own enemy.","opus":"self-disgust and inner corruption, calling oneself filth while a broken mind spirals into confusion, unable to breathe or see clearly","sonnet":"a crushing sense of inner corruption and self-disgust, in which the narrator sees themselves and possibly another as moral filth","src":"haiku"},
"death-blooms~fuck-everything":{"opus":"A nihilistic outpouring of misanthropy and despair from someone sick of the world and people, feeling abandoned and safest in the thought of his own death; the wish for his breath to decay is what gets him through each day.","sonnet":"A song of total alienation and passive death-wish — the narrator has lost all affection for people and existence, finds comfort only in the proximity of death, and feels abandoned and suffocated. There is no call to action or resolution, just the blunt declaration of a person who has given up wanting to be in the world.","src":"sonnet"},
"death-blooms~gore":{"opus":"A graphic serial-killer fantasy reveling in bloodlust, dismemberment and murder as gratification, where the narrator lures victims in only to torture, kill and savor their rotting bodies, explicitly fusing violence with sexual hunger.","sonnet":"A song narrated from the perspective of a murderer who derives sexual and sadistic pleasure from killing — the narrator confesses a craving for flesh, blood, and violence, lures a victim with false safety, and then describes draining their life and watching them decay with relish. It is graphic and unapologetic horror-shock content with no metaphorical distance.","src":"sonnet"},
"death-blooms~shut-up":{"haiku":"The speaker silences endless criticism and judgment from others through aggression and refusal, breaking free from imposed weight. Verbal threats establish boundaries against noise.","opus":"A blunt outburst against a relentless critic, met with the admission that the narrator is no better than anyone. Beneath the shut-up aggression is someone breaking their chains, sick of the weight of everyone's noise and their own.","sonnet":"Pure rage at someone who never stops talking and thinks highly of themselves: the narrator is done listening, threatens to bury whoever keeps pushing, and frames breaking free from that voice as the act that finally lets him run.","src":"haiku"},
"death-cab-for-cutie~lightness":{"haiku":"Intuition obscures truth; disconnection persists despite intimate proximity.","opus":"Gibbard turning the tangle of desire into a physics problem, insisting instinct is a bad compass and that wanting someone you shouldn't is a signal to distrust, not obey. The tenderness lands because he can't actually take his own advice.","sonnet":"A speaker observes a woman closely, trying to map her inner architecture — heart, brain, the patterns beneath the skin — while recognising that instinct misleads and the body's signals do not necessarily align with what reason says we should want.","src":"haiku"},
"death-cab-for-cutie~summer-skin":{"haiku":"Fleeting summer youth ends when autumn arrives, stripping away innocence and the ability to be together, leaving only the season's memory.","opus":"Ben Gibbard maps a brief romance onto one warm season, from swimming and lazy heat to the moment autumn arrives and the couple's easy closeness cools with the temperature. The changing weather does the work of the breakup, and the narrator seems to sense from the start that this person was never going to stay.","sonnet":"A summer romance dissolves with the season. Two people frolicked in warmth and greenery, but Labor Day arrives like a verdict, and on the last night together they shed freckles and sunburn with the knowledge that hearts have already drifted apart before the calendar even turned.","src":"haiku"},
"death-cab-for-cutie~underneath-the-sycamore":{"haiku":"Two broken people find refuge and healing beneath a tree, their shared damage becoming the foundation for safety and acceptance.","opus":"Two damaged people finding refuge not in being healed but in being equally broken, a shared shelter that admits it's only a truce rather than salvation. The comfort is real and provisional at once.","sonnet":"Two broken people, each carrying their own wreckage, discover a shared sanctuary and recognise that their damage is parallel rather than separating. Healing is not achieved so much as acknowledged together in one still place.","src":"haiku"},
"death-from-above-1979~don-t-stop-believin":{"haiku":"Small-town dreamers escape origins by pursuing fleeting emotional highs and surface-level connections, seeking meaning in urban transience and sensory experience.","opus":"A dance-punk duo tearing through the Journey anthem, keeping the streetlight-people romanticism intact but stripping its arena polish. The cover works because the yearning to hold onto feeling survives any amount of noise and irony.","sonnet":"Small-town girl, city boy, strangers on boulevards all searching for emotion in the night — the cover takes Journey's classic hymn to restless longing and plays it straight, the streetlights and the feelin' intact, people living to find something that keeps eluding them as the movie never ends.","src":"haiku"},
"death-from-above-1979~one-one":{"opus":"A giddy love song reframing romance as action, the narrator marveling that pairing up equals more than the sum of its parts, and reflecting that his wasted, aimless younger years would have been avoided had he met this person sooner.","sonnet":"A song about finding a life-altering romantic partner — the narrator reflects on wasted years with the wrong people and concludes that this one relationship makes everything that came before feel pointless, with love presented as literal magic that creates new life.","src":"sonnet"},
"death-grips~beware": {"haiku":"A self-made king rejects money and freedom, descending into darkness while warning that god watches and divine judgment awaits.","opus":"Opening with a sampled megalomaniacal rant claiming dominion over a criminal underworld, the track detonates into self-worship, the vocalist casting himself as a beast racing past any chance of turning back under a watchful God. The abrasive Sacramento trio use MC Ride's apocalyptic delivery to make the occult swagger feel genuinely unhinged.","sonnet":"A sampled monologue from someone who rejected the entertainment industry's cage becomes the foundation for a declaration of sovereign power over death and darkness — the speaker aligns with forces below ordinary sight, conducting rites at the edge of control, warning that those who deny the struggle will be overtaken by those who do not. God is watching, and the speaker has made their peace with what they are.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track opens by sampling Charles Manson insisting that his release from prison only dropped him into a larger cage of studios and money, then hands that logic to a narrator who resolves the trap by refusing every master above him. His answer is a self-devotion in which the worshipper and the idol collapse into one figure: he seizes, strikes, and torches whatever he faces, then names himself the very creature he kneels to. The lyric stacks its ritual language upward through storms, sacrifice, and burning flesh precisely to drain mercy out of prayer, recasting submission as surrender and dominance as the only faith left standing. Against all that, the recurring sampled warning of a surveilling authority above lands as pure irony, since the speaker has already crowned himself the power it means to threaten him with.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The frame is all samples: a Manson prison-interview monologue in front, and the God-is-watching voice from Dickie Burton's God Is Watching You underneath.","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","surveillance & control"],"means":"self-devotion in which worshipper and idol are the same figure","built":"the surveilling authority's warning lands as irony once he crowns himself"},
"death-grips~blood-creepin": {"haiku":"A paranoid narrative of pursuit and narrow escape; the protagonist uses violence and drugs to survive an ambush, culminating in bodies left in a highway grave.","opus":"MC Ride narrates a paranoid getaway and planned ambush that dissolves into pill-fueled dissociation, the violence blurring with a chemical numbness that swallows him. Over an eerie, looping chant, the experimental Sacramento group let menace and self-erasure bleed into each other until the drugs overtake the crime.","sonnet":"Two registers collide: a tense escape from pursuit on the streets, navigated with cold tactical calm, bleeds into a dissociating pharmaceutical spiral where consciousness slips away mid-sentence. Evasion and overdose share the same grammar of slipping through and fading out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A getaway scene of hunted paranoia gradually reveals itself as a solitary overdose narrated in the grammar of a shootout, the narrator coaching a partner through an ambush that turns out to have no enemies but his own dosing hand. Its meaning lives in that structural switch: the early bravado insists he is lucid and lethal, mocking the notion he could be sitting idle, before the back half dissolves into pill-counting, rolling eyes, and a ringing phone he cannot lift. The cooing hook that first signaled circling threat comes back as a sedative lullaby, and the title's buried image of something seeping under a sleeper in the underpass shade discloses the boasted composure as a body already going cold. Death Grips build the whole thing so that MC Ride's menace and his dissolution wear identical language, the swagger and the collapse spoken in one voice until denial finally runs out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","violence & murder"],"means":"swagger and collapse spoken in one voice until denial runs out","built":"shootout grammar reread as overdose once the partner proves imaginary"},
"death-grips~culture-shock": {"haiku":"Modern media and technology hollow consciousness; the speaker condemns passive consumption and warns of viral infection through digital connection.","opus":"The abrasive experimental trio attack digital-age dependency, diagnosing minds hollowed out by junk data and clipped, machine-paced speech that has killed real conversation. The vocalist warns that screens have quietly hijacked autonomy, turning the wired-in young into passive drones, and the glitching sensory overload mirrors the saturation it condemns.","sonnet":"Hypermedia saturation hollows out autonomy — an endless scroll of information colonizes every open space in the mind. The culture machine manufactures consent, abbreviates language, and infects through screens. Resistance arrives as noise, hostility, and an insistence on vibrating at a different frequency.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rant against the wired mind, where information glut has replaced thought and the self has been dissolved into whatever the media manufactured — the target is told outright his free will is already gone and he cannot even register the theft. The self-cancelling hook stacks it: a mangled stutter of the title butted against a command to strangle yourself, then capped with a wellness-guru mantra about vibrating higher, so New Age uplift and violent shutdown get sold in a single breath. That irony sharpens in the closing prophecy of the grown kid as a helpless drone, skull linked straight to his phone, an infection entering through the eyes and spreading until craving it becomes permanent, impossible to switch off. The final promise of ascending into a higher-dimensional Earth arrives already poisoned by everything before it, so transcendence reads as one more virus wearing the costume of enlightenment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The dying supergod opening the track is Bowie's — the intro samples The Supermen.","themes":["surveillance & control","madness & the mind","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"transcendence offered as one more virus in enlightenment's costume","built":"New Age uplift and violent shutdown sold in a single stacked breath"},
"death-grips~double-helix":{"haiku":"Hypnotic repetition and primal chaos override rational thought as the narrator surrenders to rhythmic compulsion and asks disturbing questions.","opus":"A chaotic boast about the narrator's uncontainable, genre-abducting method, throwing out every rule to chase the one true view a man can only know for himself, staring down death with pride.","sonnet":"Genre-abduction and unidentifiable origin — \"unidentified genre abductor,\" Rorschach ink blot in the street, no maps with directions — swirl around a double helix image of something that doubles back on itself: the speaker lives in trance, erases outside presence, and takes an oath to be true only to the view a man can truly know.","src":"haiku"},
"death-grips~get-got":{"opus":"Death Grips fire off a paranoid, dissociative barrage, a mind coming apart under sleep deprivation and dread, staring at things that aren't there and losing itself in the stares. The fractured imagery of trepanation and crow-winged escape maps a consciousness cracking open, predator and prey at once. Meaning stays deliberately raw and menacing.","sonnet":"A torrent of fractured consciousness at maximum velocity — sleep deprivation, dissociation, driving while barely alive, paranoia, and trepanation imagery collide in a portrait of someone operating completely outside social norms and barely inside their own skull.","src":"sonnet"},
"death-grips~guillotine": {"haiku":"A violent force moves through the world with destructive swagger, beheading and burning, reveling in raw power and death.","opus":"A pure adrenaline threat rendered as bodily destruction, the narrator vowing to bring ruin crashing down and take heads over a stomping, distorted beat. Death Grips built their early notoriety on exactly this, MC Ride barking barely-decipherable menace, the Sacramento group turning noise-rap into raw physical dread.","sonnet":"Violent creative dominance delivered at breakneck velocity — the guillotine is both execution and rhythm, a mechanism that drops cleanly and ends things without hesitation. The song positions itself as a killing machine pointed at everything soft or comfortable, with the blade falling on each beat as a demonstration that the movement cannot be stopped.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track builds a persona out of sheer downward force — a voice boasting it can drop the floor from under a crowd and watch the room collapse, the it-goes chant turning that fall into a mechanism that keeps resetting. Its second movement pauses to describe its own craft, admitting the meaning is lodged in the seams between shattered turns of phrase, the tongue running backward, so the noise itself becomes a cipher you have to break open. From there the imagery accelerates into ritual carnage — a body split into parts and torched before it can spoil, a life flattened to a stamped label on a device built purely to deliver this filthy sound. The incoherence is the whole argument: legible sense gets dispatched on purpose, and what survives the drop is the blunt syllable of a blade falling, repeated past meaning until the sound alone carries the threat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"legible sense dispatched so the blunt syllable alone carries threat","built":"the it-goes chant turns the drop into a mechanism that keeps resetting"},
"death-grips~hacker":{"haiku":"A chaotic, surreal boast of technological infiltration and transgression, speaking in absurdist wordplay and provocative imagery.","opus":"A manic stream of paranoid digital-age menace, the narrator as an omnipresent intruder who has already breached your space and your data. Ride's delivery makes surveillance and hacking feel like a psychotic god-complex, celebrity and dread fused into one overload.","sonnet":"A chaotic, omnipresent intruder — equal parts hacker, provocateur, and outlaw tourist — boasts of penetrating systems and personal spaces, rendering everything around him unstable or erased while positioning himself outside any cultural or moral grid.","src":"haiku","fable":"The Money Store closes on a manic power-fantasy where the hacker is less a computer criminal than a figure for total access itself, someone already inside your area, your body, your accounts, the leading digits of everything before you've noticed. MC Ride's verses are deliberately un-parsable, a firehose of jet-set place-names, brand-names, and celebrity detritus stitched together faster than sense can form, which is the point: the flood mimics a mind moving at network speed, past cause and effect and into pure surveillance omnipresence. The two recurring taunts do the actual work of the song, one promising that whatever you own vanishes the moment your back is turned, the other insisting he is teaching you to swim by throwing you in, so the whole thing lands as a gleeful threat that the ground under ownership and identity has already dissolved and you're only now finding out. Under the trickster swagger is real paranoia, the noided certainty that adoration and hatred arrive in exact proportion, watched even as it watches, and the track rides that ambivalence straight off the album's edge without resolving it.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","madness & the mind"],"means":"omnipresent access as paranoid god-complex where watcher is also watched","built":"the un-parsable verse flow mimics network-speed surveillance bypassing cause and effect"},
"death-grips~i-want-it-i-need-it":{"haiku":"A night out built entirely around the pursuit of drugs, sex, and obliteration — a first-person sprint through the logistics of getting fucked up and finding someone to take home, desire stripped to pure primal transaction.","opus":"A blackout chronicle of hedonism pushed past pleasure into oblivion, where the night's chase for drugs, sex and thrill curdles into something predatory and empty. Death Grips render compulsion as pure noise, appetite with nothing human left underneath.","sonnet":"A night out as pure appetite — money, drugs, a spot with hot ones inside — narrated with the mounting, grinding urgency of intoxication, where every want becomes primal, the dance floor tips into darker territory, and the want-it-need-it refrain keeps returning as the night dissolves identity into sensation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hedonism anthem that MC Ride drives past the party until it curdles into something you are meant to flinch at. It opens as a shopping list of appetite — cash, a fix, a car, warm bodies waiting in a room — narrated with the giddy logic of a mission, and the hook chants craving and need as if they were one syllable, the addict's grammar where wanting has already dissolved choice. But the verses keep skidding downhill on the very momentum they celebrate: the higher the narrator climbs, the less person remains, until desire has burned off everything but its most predatory core and the words turn openly toward blacked-out coercion. The track's genius is that it never editorializes; it simply lets appetite chase its own logic to the finish, so the pumped-up chant of wanting lands sounding less like triumph than a man narrating his own hollowing and failing to notice. What begins as a night out closes as a portrait of consumption with nobody home.","fv":2,"fnote":"The churn underneath is Syd Barrett’s Pink Floyd — Interstellar Overdrive and Astronomy Domine, chopped — 1967 psychedelia fed through the grinder; the track first appeared on the debut EP under another name.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","violence & murder"],"means":"appetite narrating its own hollowing without noticing","built":"the hook collapses want and need into one syllable — addict grammar"},
"death-grips~klink": {"haiku":"A person is constantly hassled by police who trip them up for no reason, viewing cops as oppressive haters minding other people's business.","opus":"A paranoid rant against constant police harassment, the narrator repeatedly stopped and hassled while doing nothing wrong, dreaming of putting the cops in the ground. Death Grips channel hardcore-punk rage, even nodding to Black Flag, into this suffocating anti-cop screed, MC Ride's over-the-shoulder dread capturing the grind of always being one moment from a cell.","sonnet":"Sustained hostility toward police as a system of harassment rather than protection — the speaker is stopped, questioned, surveilled, and threatened with jail for simply existing in public, while the cops who corner them are described as power-tripping with nothing to offer anyone. The fantasy of burial runs alongside the lived experience of someone always a moment from being locked up.","src":"haiku","fable":"A track about living under the assumption of guilt, where the narrator is stopped, searched, and hemmed in by police who have decided in advance that he is a target, and his refusal to grant them any authority is the whole point. What builds across the verses is a private dread hardening into a stance: the twitchy self-watching of a man perpetually one small pretext from a cell curdles into the chanted vow to rise above, borrowed straight from the Black Flag anthem the track samples, Rollins's shout riding in with it. The track welds hip-hop grievance onto hardcore-punk defiance, so the same body that flinches at every squad car also swears it will never bend, and the closing dedication to everyone who has stood on the wrong side of a badge frames the sprawl as testimony rather than complaint.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","anger & defiance"],"means":"private dread hardening into a stance of absolute non-submission","built":"hardcore-punk sample grafted onto hip-hop grievance so both flinch and swear together"},
"death-grips~known-for-it": {"haiku":"The speaker has paid a price for infamy and survives by never revealing their true nature, staying mysterious while watching others waste their lives trying.","opus":"Experimental hip-hop group Death Grips snarl a manifesto of wary infamy, a narrator who forced his way through, survived every crash, and stays locked in his own zone. Wrapped in secrecy and holding the key to himself, he keeps everyone guessing and unreachable, treating the world with scorn and a pointedly convenient memory.","sonnet":"Reputation built through relentless forward momentum and absolute refusal to be pinned down or read clearly. The narrator claims mastery through opacity, discarding everything that slows the ride and treating loyalty to any fixed self as a price only the mediocre pay.","src":"haiku","fable":"A brag about reputation that keeps sabotaging its own boast, because the thing MC Ride is known for turns out to be compulsion itself: a habit he had to hack into, that nearly crashed him, that he can never step back from once it grips. Against that self-consuming pull he builds a defensive persona out of pure withholding — a man who keeps everyone guessing, cloaks himself in mystery, holds a skeleton key while letting them see only the sliver of himself he chooses. So the two impulses argue across the track: the addict who paid too much to ever quit, and the untouchable operator who files other people as already dead to him, both laying claim to the same title — and the cost of the mask is that the it becomes the only intimacy left, the compulsion the last thing that truly knows him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the compulsion becomes the only intimacy the mask still allows","built":"addict and untouchable operator argue across one track for the same title"},
"death-grips~the-cage":{"haiku":"Uncontainable violence and rage demand breaking free from constraint, the narrator refuses imprisonment regardless of external authority.","opus":"A ferocious refusal of confinement and control, the narrator answering threats with violent contempt and vowing to break out of any cage by force, driven by a voice in his head to destroy everything.","sonnet":"Explosive confrontation with any constraint — cage, system, the person next to you on a plane — rendered in overlapping commands to kill it and chants of \"can't take it,\" the speaker declaring a breakout via underground railroad while mocking the idea that money or compromise could ever provide the exit.","src":"haiku"},
"death-grips~the-fever-aye-aye":{"opus":"A paranoid fever dream of drugs, violence and dissolving identity, the narrator scraping himself hollow in altered states until he no longer recognizes the face in the mirror. The relentless chant is addiction as possession, a sickness worn like a badge.","src":"opus"},
"death-grips~thru-the-walls": {"opus":"MC Ride fixates on slipping bodily through a barrier to break out of a labyrinth he has been stuck in so long he no longer knows himself or whether he is still alive. The Sacramento group thread genuine dissociation and a craving to escape between the violent boasts, making confinement itself sound like a threat.","sonnet":"A fractured meditation on confinement, delusion, and identity dissolution — the narrator questions whether the walls constraining him are real or self-constructed, oscillating between aggressive dominance and the unsettled feeling of being a ghost trapped in a maze. The aggression in the verses masks a deeper existential uncertainty about who he is and whether he can escape.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A prisoner keeps testing the walls of a cell that will not give, so the track swings between two ways of pushing against them. The outer verses play the aggressor, punching through everyone else's furnishings with plutonium bravado and insisting the trapped ones are always other people. The middle turns inward and quiet, asking whether a man who cannot leave a room has already stopped counting as alive, whether the barrier was solid or only ever his own mind. That doubt collapses into a final scene of someone alone in the dark, telling himself the ordeal is nearly finished while the outro flatly refuses to close. Its stuttered last word claims to be over even as the loop proves it never will be.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","anger & defiance","freedom & escape"],"means":"confinement debated as wall or mind then refused a resolution","built":"the stuttered outro claims to be over while the loop proves otherwise"},
"death-s-dynamic-shroud~a-9ys1q5":{"haiku":"Existential identity crisis—a black bird's repetitive questioning echoes endlessly while the narrator drowns in uncertainty about their own existence.","opus":"Death's Dynamic Shroud grew out of vaporwave before mutating into something more violent and fractured, and their work often loops a single haunting gesture until it warps. Here a black shadow-bird asks who the narrator is, over and over, and no answer comes. The refusal of resolution is the meaning: identity dissolving under a question that repeats, the sampled-collage aesthetic turning self-doubt into an endless echo.","sonnet":"A minimalist song built on a single haunting question — a black shadow-bird asking the narrator \"who are you?\" over and over — with no answer given, making it a meditation on identity dissolution or self-questioning rather than a narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"death-s-dynamic-shroud~a-kn69ld":{"haiku":"Abstract remixed aesthetic with fragmented voices and sexual undertones; lacks coherent narrative and exists primarily as hyperpop atmosphere rather than meaningful thematic content.","opus":"Death's Dynamic Shroud work in hyperpop's maximalist collage, where chopped vocals and euphoric texture matter more than coherent lyric. This track is almost entirely chant and filler, carrying only a single injunction not to suffer in silence and scattered lines about a girl who will blow your mind. The mood is the message: surface euphoria assembled from fragments, with no developed theme beneath the shimmer.","sonnet":"The lyrics are almost entirely vocal chants and filler phrases, with only a single injunction not to suffer in silence and scattered lines about a girl who will blow your mind; there is no developed thematic content beyond euphoric mood surface. NONE","src":"haiku"},
"death-tour-wargasm-uk~the-cycle":{"haiku":"Systemic collapse breeds psychological decay, trapping the individual in a loop of despair and suicidal ideation where revolution becomes impossible because those in power have weaponized apathy itself.","opus":"An apocalyptic screed against a dying, corporatized world, casting the speaker as his own worst enemy and rallying a last generation to tear down the temples and statues, echoing the villain-hero line and Dylan Thomas as the doomsday clock nears midnight.","sonnet":"A speaker caught in the loop of systemic rot and suicidal daily existence rails against hidden power structures — pharmaceutical roots deep in pockets, a clock that never stops — and calls on a last generation to tear down the temple and reset the dial before midnight.","src":"haiku"},
"death-tour~the-cycle":{"haiku":"Psychological rivalry with the self perpetuates cycles; the speaker recognizes themselves as primary antagonist, revolution necessary but doomed by human nature.","opus":"A rage-against-the-dying revolt built from Rage and Dylan Thomas fragments, casting late-stage civilization as a suicidal caveman cycle spinning toward midnight. The call is to tear down the temples and statues and rebuild the people before the last generation loses the chance.","sonnet":"A generational crisis narrative — the narrator sees themselves as psychologically at war with themselves, describes society as a wasteland run by pharmaceutical and corporate parasites, and calls on the last generation capable of civilization to tear down the corrupt system before midnight.","src":"haiku"},
"deathbyromy~bitchfamous":{"haiku":"The speaker embraces the role of villain and betrayer, weaponizing infamy and turning anticipated judgment into dominance through shameless performance.","opus":"Owning the role of villain thrust on her by a two-faced, betraying lover, embracing the Judas label with defiant relish while exposing the hypocrisy of the one who kissed her while lying.","sonnet":"A woman weaponises the Judas label forced onto her by a vindictive partner who kissed her with 'lips dripping lies' and laid with her 'vindictive all the time'; she owns the villain role with scorched-earth defiance, turning accusation into armour.","src":"haiku"},
"deathbyromy~body-horror":{"haiku":"Reclaiming bodily autonomy against gendered objectification and shame imposed from adolescence onward.","opus":"A furious reckoning with being sexualized as a teenage girl, sainthood and slut assigned in the same breath, the body reframed as horror precisely because others decide what it exists for while she bleeds and is called adored.","sonnet":"About the sexual objectification and violation of girls — from age 16 the narrator was treated as something to consume, called a whore before she turned 17, and the song asks with bitter sarcasm what her body is actually for if not her own; it's an angry, direct rejection of being reduced to flesh.","src":"haiku"},
"deathbyromy~even-if-it-bleeds":{"haiku":"Surrender to love despite inevitable suffering and death; yielding the heart completely because connection matters more than preservation.","opus":"Love collapsed into a death wish, offering a whole heart to someone who might destroy it and finding that lovely rather than tragic. Under the goth-pop sheen sits a fatalism that everyone dies anyway, so surrender is the boldest move.","sonnet":"Total surrender to someone who might be lethal — you can be the death of me, honestly it sounds lovely — offered freely because we're all going down anyway and waiting to meet again in a small world poured from the same mold. The heart is given not despite the risk of bleeding but because of it, the wound as proof of contact.","src":"haiku"},
"deathbyromy~guerra":{"haiku":"War rises from desperation and rage: systematic violence and rebellion feed each other in a dance that consumes everything, framed as righteous inevitability.","opus":"A gleeful, incendiary call for uprising against the state and a white man's world, dancing to the sound of the system falling. War here is catharsis aimed at power itself.","sonnet":"A song about revolutionary violence fantasized without restraint — the speaker wants war, riots, blood, and the destruction of governments and systems, reveling in the imagery of bodies falling and power structures burning.","src":"haiku"},
"deathbyromy~hellhound":{"haiku":"Predatory hunger and sadism merge into a single entity; the speaker becomes monster, feeding on flesh and relishing the role of nightmare.","opus":"Predatory rage as pure spectacle, casting herself as an unleashed hellhound and cannibal who bites back, turning a lover's cruelty into blood-soaked, gleeful vengeance.","sonnet":"A predatory, feral narrator revels in her own violence and instinct — switchblade tongue, blood lust, carving names in chests — casting herself as a hellhound off the leash who feeds on flesh 'like an animal' and leaves victims critical, half kiss and half ice pick.","src":"haiku"},
"deathbyromy~manic-dream":{"opus":"A demolition of the manic-pixie fantasy from inside it, a girl romanticizing her own self-destruction and daring the world to watch her burn out young. She weaponizes others' disgust into pleasure, choosing spectacle over survival.","src":"opus"},
"deathbyromy~no-mercy":{"haiku":"Toxic devotion to a violent partner who commits atrocities at her command, love merging with danger and destruction.","opus":"The speaker revels in a devoted, murderous boyfriend who hates everyone but her and would kill on command, playing a mirror-mirror sadism and showing none of the mercy she demands of him. Turned on by his violence, she works his strings like a puppet while her father frets that she's come undone.","sonnet":"A woman weaponizes her dangerous lover as an extension of her own cruelty — he kills on command, he is \"a bullet in your brain,\" and she is the one who pulls the strings. \"Mirror, mirror on the wall, who's the sickest bitch of all?\" makes clear she's not afraid of what she is; she's proud of it.","src":"haiku"},
"deathbyromy~pray-to-me":{"opus":"Blasphemy as empowerment, casting the singer as a false idol who demands worship and becomes both savior and ruin to a desperate follower. The Christian imagery is inverted to seize the kind of devotion usually reserved for God.","src":"opus"},
"deathbyromy~saint":{"haiku":"A saint judges and crucifies, offering nothing but blame and predation. The speaker rejects false holiness and betrayal dressed as love.","opus":"A takedown of a narcissistic ex who plays the aggrieved martyr while only ever taking, the singer reclaiming her worth after setting herself alight to keep him comfortable and realizing whatever they shared was never love.","sonnet":"A song about leaving an emotionally abusive relationship and confronting a partner who played the martyr — the narrator spent too long blaming themselves for this person's dishonesty, enabling their victim act, but now sees clearly that they were taken from constantly and that what passed for love never was.","src":"haiku"},
"deathbyromy~xxxhibitionist":{"haiku":"Desperate bid for fame and visibility through spectacle, willing to sacrifice dignity for recognition and attention.","opus":"Fame-hunger pushed to a death drive, courting cameras and danger and being watched as the ultimate thrill, Hollywood immortality indistinguishable from self-destruction dressed up as ambition.","sonnet":"Fame and exhibitionism as the same impulse: the speaker wants the lights on, to be filmed getting dangerous, to die when the cameras flash — bury me in Hollywood forever conflates death and stardom, and every sexual provocation is also a performance bid, the audience required for the self to exist.","src":"haiku"},
"deathhammer~doomed-to-eternal-hellfire":{"opus":"Old-school blackthrash reveling in cartoonish damnation, summoning demons and nuclear rot to end human life. There's no subtext here beyond genre ritual: evil invoked for the sheer theatrical thrill of surrender to the inferno.","src":"opus"},
"deathhammer~evil-blood":{"opus":"Cartoon black-metal blasphemy, hordes of mutant zombies spawned of Satan and reared below riding death-winds to wipe out Christianity, feasting on flesh and desecrating Christ, the chorus commanding worship of Satan amid apocalyptic war.","src":"opus"},
"deathhammer~invasion-from-hell-demonic-avenger":{"opus":"A blackthrash war-story of a fallen soul returned as satan's avenger to butcher the faithful, pure blasphemous provocation played entirely straight. The appeal is the comic-book villainy: annihilation staged as anti-Christian spectacle.","src":"opus"},
"deathhammer~toxic-radiation":{"haiku":"Nuclear annihilation descends as divine judgment upon humanity, each contaminated breath a countdown to extinction where survival is merely prolonged suffering.","opus":"Old-school thrash relish in nuclear apocalypse, cataloguing radiation sickness and global annihilation as humanity's inescapable damnation and judgment day.","sonnet":"Nuclear apocalypse as total damnation: chemical explosions, radiation burns, and coughing blood are presented as the planet's final judgment, a merciless death from which no one escapes.","src":"haiku"},
"deathstars~blitzkrieg":{"haiku":"A catastrophic assault—both external and internal—exploits people's darkest impulses: fear, addiction, suffering, and destruction that swallow everyone.","opus":"Swedish industrial metal staging inner collapse as a bombing raid, nightmares and addiction detonating while the band invites you to sit back and enjoy the destruction. Nihilism as spectacle.","sonnet":"A song of total annihilation framed as dark spectacle — the world collapses in explosions of hope, faith, and family, while the speaker invites you to sit back and enjoy the destruction as if it's entertainment.","src":"haiku"},
"deathstars~blitzkrieg-boom":{"haiku":"Cataclysm unfolds as spectacle and entertainment, chaos transforming prayer into ashes while destruction becomes the ultimate release from a suffocating existence.","opus":"An industrial-metal spectacle of inner detonation, framing dread, addiction and collapse as an explosion staged for the audience's dark enjoyment, with everything already inside you waiting to go off.","sonnet":"Inner collapse rendered as warfare — nerves screaming, prayers burning black, hell's vines spreading — until the speaker invites the listener to lean back and enjoy the show as a last bomb is triggered.","src":"haiku"},
"death~flattening-of-emotions":{"haiku":"Mental illness erases the sense of self as personality fragments and intrusive thoughts colonise consciousness without permission.","opus":"Chuck Schuldiner writes from inside a mind coming apart, someone hearing sounds and seeing figures that aren't real as a formerly stable life quietly collapses. The song treats the loss of feeling as a kind of erasure, the illness edging out the person who once lived there.","sonnet":"A clinical examination of psychosis — a mind shared by an uninvited stranger, seeing things that are not there, intruding voices — asking where the person that could have been went, and what went wrong to flatten their emotions so completely.","src":"haiku"},
"death~forgotten-past":{"haiku":"Repressed memories of serial murder surface as fragmented nightmares; the past claims its victim through involuntary recall.","opus":"Early Schuldiner using splatter imagery as a vehicle for a genuinely eerie idea: buried memory as a past life you're forced to excavate, discovering the atrocities were your own. Horror as repression made literal.","sonnet":"A past-life horror narrative in which the subject is haunted by visions of a previous existence spent committing murder, drawn back to a burial site where the evidence of what they were in another life waits to be unearthed and faced.","src":"haiku"},
"death~lack-of-comprehension":{"haiku":"Denial and self-blame destroy families; parents refuse to acknowledge their role in their children's ruin, projecting guilt outward while their offspring spiral into despair.","opus":"A blistering indictment of parents who blame their children for the damage they themselves inflicted, denying their part in a ruined life. It is about the cowardice of projecting guilt onto the innocent to cover one's own weakness.","sonnet":"An indictment of parents who blame their children for lives destroyed by neglect and cruelty: they project their own guilt and self-resentment onto the person they failed, thriving on clichés, and will deny their part in that person's ruin to the very end.","src":"haiku"},
"death~sacred-serenity":{"haiku":"Protective spirits witness innocence without its awareness of danger while time remains foreign to childhood's eternal present. Safety exists through ignorance of the corruption surrounding youth.","opus":"The unknowing purity of small children whose instincts stay pure and who live untouched by the destruction of time, a serenity the observer sees as a sacred gift.","opusDeep":"Chuck Schuldiner's Death at its most philosophical, aiming death metal's aggression at a startlingly tender subject: the innocence of children or newborn souls. 'For them, each day life has just begun' and 'Following all instincts that are pure' locate a wisdom in the unformed — those who possess the gift 'without knowing' they have it. The title's paradox is the argument: 'Serenity is knowing it's safe / From destruction of time,' peace defined as immunity from the very decay the rest of Schuldiner's catalogue obsesses over. The 'Observing spirits on the wall / What are they telling you?' refrain turns watchers into watched, suggesting the pure perceive things adult, time-worn minds have lost. 'Maybe we could take a step back / And discover what leads us to attack' is a genuine plea for self-examination inside a violent genre. What it's actually about: the sacredness of unspoiled innocence, and time as the destroyer that corrupts a serenity we can't consciously hold onto.","sonnet":"An observation of creatures — likely animals or young children — who follow pure instinct, unaware of time and its destruction, possessing a sacred serenity that adults have lost; the speaker wonders if stepping back to observe them might reveal what drives humans to attack and diminish.","src":"haiku"},
"death~suicide-machine":{"haiku":"Authority denies the suffering their right to choose peace, prolonging agony through laws that prioritize control over compassion and life over dignity.","opus":"Schuldiner's argument for the right to die with dignity, indicting a system that clings to control over the suffering while ignoring their pain. Death metal turned toward a genuine ethical question about who owns your own end.","sonnet":"A critique of the legal and medical apparatus that denies terminally ill people the right to choose their own death, framing the refusal as cruelty disguised as care. Those in power prolong suffering while ignoring the voices of those who suffer, and the speaker asks plainly whether dying with dignity is too much to ask.","src":"haiku"},
"death~symbolic":{"haiku":"Innocent memories crystallize as irreplaceable gifts as the speaker recognizes their loss through fragmentation. The vivid becomes invisible only when removed by time and wisdom.","opus":"A yearning to recover lost innocence, Schuldiner urging that we savor small present moments and hold onto our fading capacity for wonder before it slips into memory.","opusDeep":"The title track of Chuck Schuldiner's most melodic, progressive Death record, and its subject is memory as addiction. The nostalgia is framed in a junkie's terms without apology: 'In need of a fix called innocence / A high that can never be bought or sold,' the past a drug precisely because it's unpurchasable. 'When years seemed to stand still' and 'Their magic frozen in time' preserve childhood as an eternal present, while the title paradox — 'Symbolic acts / So vivid / Yet at the same time / Were invisible' — names how the moments that shaped us passed unnoticed as they happened. The turn is instructive rather than merely wistful: 'Savor what you feel and what you see / Things that may not seem important now / But may be tomorrow' addresses the listener directly, converting private mourning into counsel. What it's actually about: the retrospective realization that ordinary moments carry the meaning of a life, and the ache of only recognizing it once they've become memory.","sonnet":"A longing for the lost innocence of childhood, when time stood still and heroes seemed eternal — the speaker sinks inward to relive precious memories, recognising that the change away from that innocence was undetectable until the wound was already open.","src":"haiku"},
"death~zero-tolerance":{"haiku":"Divine judgment against deception and false piety. The song warns of karmic consequences for those who abuse power through false intentions.","opus":"Chuck Schuldiner's genre-shaping band, moving into more cerebral territory, delivers a verdict against religious frauds and their empty piety. Dark blasphemy meets a call for retribution, with fate cast as the final judge and machinery feeding a sacrificial altar, sharpening the attack on sanctimonious deceit.","sonnet":"A judgment delivered against someone who exploits language and power to corrupt and harm. Karma is framed as an absolute reckoning — not a game or a test but a closing verdict — where machines that run on sacrificed virtue are ultimately dismantled by fate itself.","src":"haiku"},
"decapitated~anger-line":{"haiku":"Years of wrath and trauma feed dormant anger; the narrator approaches a breaking point, tempted to unleash the beast within.","opus":"Since memory began, every gesture has dripped with wrath, crime, and lies, and the narrator names himself an alchemist of hate slowly breeding a dormant weapon in the forge of his fears. Poland's Decapitated, technical death metal architects, chart a mind creeping toward a threshold, scars accumulating until the beast within stirs and the speaker steps into the valley of the damned.","sonnet":"A mind forged by lifelong exposure to wrath, lies, and violence reaches its breaking point. Dormant fury cultivated through years of wounds and submission builds toward a threshold where sanity yields — the anger line crossed and the beast within finally released.","src":"haiku"},
"decapitated~deathvaluation":{"haiku":"A tyrant worships power through fear and bloodshed until death reminds them of their mortality. Everything built through violence crumbles into ash.","opus":"A philosophical attack on power built through fear, where a tyrant plays demigod by feeding the masses dread and controlling them through religion and authority. The counterpoint is memento mori: death sets the true value on everything, time is the reaper, and every empire crumbles to ash with its ruler's mortal body.","sonnet":"About power built on fear — a demigod-like ruler who uses death as currency, feeding people fear the way they feed them bread. The song argues that death sets the value of everything in human civilization — religion, authority, the lust for flesh — and that no empire survives the hourglass.","src":"haiku"},
"decapitated~earth-scar":{"haiku":"A person born to wander and connected to their native roots follows their sacred fate, digging an earth scar through life until they fall fulfilled in death.","opus":"Polish death metal on a restless man who feels made to wander rather than settle, dragging his lineage and heritage behind him as he digs a path across the world. The track builds toward a stubborn oath to chase his own destiny no matter the cost and meet the end on his terms.","sonnet":"A song about the life of a touring musician as a kind of shamanic calling — born to settle but with a soul that strays, the narrator transforms on stage into a vessel for the collective, and every mile traveled is a scar dug into the earth, each one making him more fully himself even as it moves him toward death.","src":"haiku"},
"decapitated~iconoclast":{"haiku":"Relics and symbols of faith are weaponized for violence and subjugation; sacred objects become instruments of terror and genocide.","opus":"A death-metal inventory of sacred fakery, holy relics and Klan hoods and imperial buckles listed as one great con. The iconoclast's point is that reverence and hate share the same fraudulent stagecraft.","sonnet":"A list of sacred relics and instruments of religious and racial terror — whale oil, Samson's hair, Ku Klux Klan robes and peaked hoods — assembled as evidence that organized religion and white supremacy have always been used to consecrate violence.","src":"haiku"},
"decapitated~impulse":{"haiku":"Creative and destructive forces emerge from the same primal source driving human chaos and suffering. The song treats divinity, defect, and vicious cycles as inherent to existence.","opus":"A knotty piece of Polish death metal arguing that the same drive to create and to wreck powers a merciless cosmos, and that human beings are a flawed, dying species whose very flaw somehow spawns the idea of gods. It closes by asking who really runs things and answering that the knowledge sits buried inside every person.","sonnet":"The creative drive and the urge to destroy are presented as twin forces drawn from the same primal source — the song traces how human minds, fed from this single chaotic wellspring, perpetuate a vicious cycle of building and annihilating.","src":"haiku"},
"decapitated~kill-the-cult":{"haiku":"Cults exploit weakness through false divinity; the narrator rejects their symbols and demands they burn in hell.","opus":"Polish death metal aimed at organized religion and charlatan idols, raging against forged faith that enslaves weak minds. The demand to burn the symbols is anticlerical fury pushed to its blunt extreme.","sonnet":"A direct anti-organized-religion song — the narrator attacks charlatans who spread delusion, build cults on manufactured fear, and claim divine immunity while pulling strings over weak minds, ending with a command to drink the blood their god spilled and choke on it.","src":"haiku"},
"decapitated~never":{"haiku":"Humanity has created a concrete hell of pollution and self-destruction; people hide behind masks while the world suffocates toward extinction.","opus":"An apocalyptic vision of humanity choking on the poison it created, sunlight smothered by ash as people die inside a world of their own making. Born with freedom, they've engineered an existence that grinds them down, hiding behind flimsy protection that only stalls an extinction they brought upon themselves.","sonnet":"A vision of civilizational collapse: the sky gone dark with ash, air turned acid, the earth becoming a mass grave where humanity suffocates inside the concrete and pollution it built for itself, reduced to masked half-alive figures shuffling toward extinction.","src":"haiku"},
"decapitated~one-eyed-nation":{"haiku":"Blind leadership and the illusion of divine power: rulers pretend authority from gods while blind masses follow cyclops leaders, but truth lies only in flesh-equals-flesh equality broken by greedy ego.","opus":"The Polish death-metal band attacks the idea of inherited authority, denying that rulers get their crowns from any god and that anyone is made to obey. Leaning on the old line about a partly sighted man ruling those who cannot see, it imagines a sightless crowd led by monsters and a blindfolded figure of justice, then vows to pry open sleeping heads and raise a people grounded in truth.","sonnet":"A searing rejection of divine authority and hereditary power, arguing that flesh is equal regardless of crown or scripture, and that greed alone tips the scales of fate. The speaker envisions a society awakened by piercing through collective blindness — a nation rebuilt on truth rather than dynasty or holy mandate.","src":"haiku"},
"deceptic~dissonance":{"haiku":"Reset sought but never achieved, past episodes reshape endlessly, foretold dread impedes redemption as breathing dissonance persists.","opus":"A metalcore study of a mind desperate to reset and rewind itself, unable to let a redefined past pass. The dissonance is between the narrator and someone who sees too clearly through him, so he deflects and undoes rather than face it.","sonnet":"A speaker wants to undo or reset a past action — \"there was no other way\" — whose shattered glass still bears their fingerprint, and refuses to accept someone else's insufficient insight into what happened; breathing dissonance means existing in the gap between their own eyes and everyone else's.","src":"haiku"},
"declan-welsh-and-the-decadent-west~hardly-news":{"haiku":"Love presented as foregone conclusion requiring no announcement; the speaker's devotion is so transparent it needs no declaration, grounded in mutual understanding.","opus":"A love so settled and mutual that declaring it is almost redundant, an unglamorous, contented devotion whose whole charm is that the beloved already knows.","sonnet":"A love declaration so settled and certain that the singer frames it as boring to anyone outside the relationship — confessing at The Berkeley, the speaker revels in the unremarkable, emphatic truth that he is entirely and obviously his partner's.","src":"haiku"},
"declan-welsh-and-the-decadent-west~impermanency":{"haiku":"Mortality and impermanence ache with bittersweet beauty, as the speaker cherishes each fleeting moment knowing all fades.","opus":"Mortality caught in a breath disappearing into cold winter air, the dread of anxious nights sure the end comes for everyone. The consolation is that transience is precisely what lends each moment its beauty — so love is declared now, blazing with intent before it fades.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality sparked by watching breath vanish in cold air — the speaker confronts the certainty of death and the slow decay of time, but finds urgency and beauty in impermanence rather than despair, choosing to love fiercely while he still can.","src":"haiku"},
"declan-welsh-and-the-decadent-west~mercy":{"haiku":"Addiction and surrender bind the speaker to another's authority; knees buckle against willpower while divine intoxication and public judgment intensify the helpless offering.","opus":"Addiction and infatuation braided together until the narrator can't tell them apart, his knees buckling as he tries to walk away from a habit that also feels like love and a kind of God. The high is both salvation and captivity.","sonnet":"An addictive love is described with unflinching honesty — \"I can't kick it and I've tried\" — the speaker's knees buckling every time they try to walk away, the surrender framed not as weakness but as the simple physical fact of being at someone's mercy.","src":"haiku"},
"declan-welsh-and-the-decadent-west~slack-jaw":{"opus":"A jittery post-punk poke at performing selfhood for an audience, the vanity of wanting to be seen and the fracturing into curated versions of a person. Beneath the wordplay sits real anxiety about aging out of it all.","src":"opus"},
"deco-27~delusion-feat-hatsune-miku":{"opus":"A Vocaloid satire imagining a delusion tax the state levies to make fantasies real, exposing how desire, compromise and self-importance get monetized. The twist lands when the whole promise turns out to be a lie and the receipt is worthless.","src":"opus"},
"ded~parasite":{"haiku":"A parasitic force feeds on its host's life and soul, promising inescapable torment as it embeds itself permanently within.","opus":"The nu-metal band personifies a devouring force that lodges itself inside a host, draining its victim and resisting every effort to be dislodged. Its taunting claim of ownership casts addiction or a consuming relationship as literal infestation, the menace framed as something already burrowed into the body and impossible to expel.","sonnet":"A parasite speaks with predatory satisfaction, describing total annexation of a host. It positions itself as inevitable — burrowing through veins, past the stomach wall, and into the soul — making escape not just difficult but cosmically pointless. Possession is framed as intimacy turned absolute.","src":"haiku"},
"deep-as-ocean~death-whistle":{"haiku":"The song warns of inevitable apocalypse and cosmic indifference. The ancients knew collapse was coming; humanity races toward burning and rebirth with no way to brake.","opus":"A metalcore vision of the planet in flames, the warnings of the ancients finally proven right as an unstoppable cycle forces a choice between two forms of annihilation to cleanse what remains. The titular ritual sound lends it a prophetic charge, and the recurring cry of rebirth leaves a sliver of hope amid the ruin.","sonnet":"Apocalyptic cycle framed as inevitability — the ancients tried to warn, nobody listened, and now the world burns toward a rebirth that may or may not be enough. The choice between drowning and burning is presented as the last privilege, and the death whistle itself arrives as a natural sound, cold and indifferent, calling the earth home.","src":"haiku"},
"deep-purple~smoke-on-the-water":{"haiku":"A recording studio burns down in Montreux when someone ignites a flare gun; bands relocate and adapt while Swiss deadlines loom, ultimately succeeding despite chaos and displacement.","opus":"A near-reportage retelling of the 1971 Montreux fire, when a flare gun torched the casino during a concert and destroyed the venue where the band planned to record. The real subject is dogged improvisation, the musicians scrambling for a new location and rigging up a makeshift studio in a bare, freezing hotel.","sonnet":"A first-person account of the actual 1971 fire at the Montreux Casino during a Frank Zappa concert, and how Deep Purple scrambled to find another place to record, eventually ending up in the Grand Hotel.","src":"haiku"},
"deep-purple~smoke-on-the-water-1971-recording":{"haiku":"Creative ambition meets catastrophic accident; the studio burns while musicians witness destruction of their carefully prepared sanctuary for art.","opus":"Rock's most famous riff attached to a plain reportage of the night a flare gun burned down the Montreux casino during a Zappa show, forcing the band to improvise a recording space. Legend built entirely from a true, almost mundane disaster.","sonnet":"A factual account of how the band came to record in Montreux after a fan with a flare gun burned down the Frank Zappa concert venue, forcing them to improvise in an empty hotel with a rolling truck studio; the smoke over Lake Geneva and fire in the sky become the permanent image of the session.","src":"haiku"},
"deer-death~fly-away":{"haiku":"Loneliness and giving-up crystallize as the speaker and beloved sink together, yet escape and flight remain the only imagined solace from the weight of shared dissolution.","opus":"A slight, aching plea to escape somewhere with a partner who is visibly giving up, the narrator fading alongside them. The repetition itself is the emotion: exhaustion looping until flight becomes the only imaginable relief.","sonnet":"A speaker watches someone they love giving up and can only offer the wish that they could both fly away from where they are — simple, tender, and helpless, the fading voice mirroring the act of surrender it witnesses.","src":"haiku"},
"deerhoof~running-thoughts":{"haiku":"Mysterious travelers arrive secretly bringing new ideas and unconventional wisdom, breaking customs before disappearing to distant places.","opus":"The long-running experimental indie-rock band spin a whimsical fable about a band of smugglers who slip past customs, though their contraband turns out to be fresh ideas and secret melodies rather than goods, before drifting off elsewhere. Wrapped in the group's childlike surrealism, the conceit turns smuggling into a playful figure for the arrival of new inspiration.","sonnet":"A whimsical fable about four runners who slip in from somewhere far away, carrying ideas too new to declare at customs, smuggling smiles through briefly before vanishing again. The song treats fresh thought as something almost contraband — arriving in pairs, whistling secret tunes, gone before it can be held.","src":"haiku"},
"def-leppard~animal":{"haiku":"Raw animal desire drives the speaker—lust, appetite, restlessness consume them, expressed as hunger, burning, and predatory movement. Sex and sensation are the only constants in this primal existence.","opus":"A blunt anthem of physical craving that cleanly severs sex from romance, the singer casting himself as a hunting, restless creature who wants the body and none of the emotional entanglement. The title stands in for raw, unfiltered sexual appetite.","sonnet":"Raw sexual hunger framed as elemental force — the narrator is a predator who never sleeps, driven by lust that runs like a river and needs like flame, hunting his quarry with animal instinct rather than love.","src":"haiku"},
"def-leppard~hello-america":{"haiku":"Restless wanderlust pursues hedonistic escape; California dreams promise liberation and bright-lit nights.","opus":"A young English band writing themselves a cheap-bus fantasy of America as the promised land of bright lights and easy love, all wide-eyed ambition before they got there. The naivety is the charm.","sonnet":"An exuberant British traveller's love letter to America — Greyhound buses, Hollywood, San Francisco, Los Angeles — framed as a romantic arrival bringing love and promising to rock the night away.","src":"haiku"},
"defecto~tempest":{"haiku":"The ocean's lethal beauty seduces through pain; the speaker surrenders to tempest and depth, seeking connection through drowning while yearning for impossible rescue.","opus":"The sea as a figure for fate and mortality, sailors drawn down into the depths yet defiant that nothing shall master them. The lighthouse in the storm is the human hope of guidance against forces that will claim you regardless.","sonnet":"A seafaring meditation on mortal beauty and submission to the deep: the ocean's deadly grace is approached with reverence, not resistance — there is beauty in the gale, those lost in it are mourned, and the lone sailor lifting anchor chooses freedom over mastery. The lighthouse may or may not come; the open water paralyzes and then sets free.","src":"haiku"},
"defects~end-of-days":{"opus":"A furious indictment of the powerful who justify ruin as done for everyone's sake, insisting greed leads only to the grave. Written in an era of climate and political dread, it promises the architects of collapse won't escape their own reckoning.","src":"opus"},
"defences~what-you-know":{"haiku":"Distinguish personal truth from propaganda; refuse to waste time on deception when time is finite.","opus":"The British melodic band Defences wrestle with distinguishing truth from what one is told, pleading to be woken only when the ordeal ends. Amid lies meant to be seen through and everything leading to hurt, the chorus insists on separating knowledge from received belief and seizing a second that will not return. Weariness and urgency pull against each other throughout.","sonnet":"Exhaustion with deception and drama — a person pleads to be left out of cycles of hurt and manipulation. The central urgency is to strip away what others have told them to believe and hold only what is genuinely known, before the last available time runs through their hands.","src":"haiku"},
"defiler~canaanite":{"haiku":"A speaker thrives in darkness through anger and resentment, rejecting belonging and seeking oblivion as escape.","opus":"A deathcore plunge into pure nihilism, the speaker glorying in his own darkness, forged by violence and craving only oblivion in the ground where nothing can reach him. It builds toward a flat verdict that existence is a fraud and everyone deserves annihilation, framing self-erasure not as grief but as contempt for being alive at all.","sonnet":"Nihilistic endurance turned inward — the voice has thrived in darkness long enough to stop wanting belonging or strength, and now just wants to be laid in the earth where nothing hurts. It is a song of someone who has crushed and been crushed and now sees virtue and truth both as dust in the well, choosing dissolution over any further performance of survival.","src":"haiku"},
"defining-lines~fall-apart":{"haiku":"Documents mutual destruction in a relationship where both cause and receive nothing but harm, choosing solitude as recovery. Accepts their own emptiness.","opus":"A partnership crumbling into shared destruction, the narrator hollowed out after being stripped bare, pledging to hand back the same void and claiming he thrives better alone once fingers tighten on his neck. The metalcore breakdowns pound out the you-chose-this finality, the collapse refrain and imagery of broken remnants recasting a wrecked bond as bitter, scorched resentment with no chance left to leave any trace.","sonnet":"A toxic dynamic arriving at its final break — one person left gutted and dead inside, the other still gripping and choking, and neither capable of fixing what has been destroyed between them. The conclusion is that separation is the only sane outcome, delivered with the bitter clarity of someone who has finally stopped waiting to be rescued by the same person doing the damage.","src":"haiku"},
"defocus~consumed-by-you":{"haiku":"The speaker runs from truth and from another's consuming influence, never becoming what others demand, always escaping the role that threatens to destroy them.","opus":"Refusing to be devoured by another person's expectations while admitting you keep running from the truth of it. The metalcore push-pull of an enemy you invited in, taking one last shot at not disappearing into what they want.","sonnet":"A person fighting not to be swallowed by someone they've let in — they know the other person has become their enemy, resent how impulsively they opened themselves up, and are still running from the truth even as they insist they won't be consumed.","src":"haiku"},
"defocus~crooked-mind":{"opus":"Intrusive voices and the draining conviction of never measuring up, the narrator unsure whether the cruelty in his skull is his own or someone else's. The fight is to shut down that inner distortion before it claims whatever's left of him.","src":"opus"},
"defocus~watch-me-bleed":{"haiku":"Pushing away everyone rather than allow their presence to witness internal collapse and deterioration.","opus":"Pushing everyone away out of self-disgust, begging them to run before they can witness the collapse, a protective cruelty rooted in having learned enough about your own fears to know you can't hold anyone through them.","sonnet":"About protecting someone from your own self-destructive spiral by pushing them away — the narrator knows they're becoming a toxic person and deliberately isolates themselves so others won't have to watch them deteriorate. The final image of bleeding alone is chosen as the better alternative to bringing someone down with you.","src":"haiku"},
"defsharp~glass-fort":{"haiku":"Fragile structure collapses under pressure; the speaker cannot hold themselves together, friendships erode from exhaustion, and isolation accelerates.","opus":"The hangover loop of a partier watching time and friends slip away, unable to quit even as the numbing nights leave them at square one. The fort is glass: fragile self-distraction against the question of whether any of it, or of them, was ever real.","sonnet":"Someone using alcohol to avoid dealing with their life — spending weekends red-eyed, losing friends to their own inconsistency, unable to decide whether to stay or go, stuck in a loop of numbing and putting off everything that needs to be faced.","src":"haiku"},
"defsharp~jaded":{"haiku":"Disillusionment calcifies into paralysis: youthful potential curdles into cynicism as the gap between who you wanted to be and who you've become grows insurmountable.","opus":"Trapped in your own head while life blurs past, half-hoping it isn't too late to climb out of old childish wreckage. Every step forward feels like drifting further from the truth and the person you meant to be.","sonnet":"A song about the paralysis of regret and self-awareness — the speaker knows they are drifting further from who they wanted to be and from the people they love, caught in their head while life fades in front of them.","src":"haiku"},
"defsharp~jaded-2th-one":{"opus":"A young reckoning with paralysis and regret, life slipping past while the narrator stays trapped in his own head. The quiet hope is that the childish mistakes aren't final and there's still a way out if he can face what he's avoiding.","src":"opus"},
"defsharp~new-pants-era":{"opus":"A jolt of self-directed urgency about growing up before losing someone, the narrator sick of sleeping through his life and demanding he get with the program. Comic paint-stained detail masks a genuine fear of running out of time.","src":"opus"},
"defsharp~offense-mechanism":{"haiku":"Someone catalogs how their partner has hurt them through false apologies and manipulation, struggling to break free from betrayal.","opus":"A study of being snared by someone who offers apologies that are never sincere, keeping the narrator stalled on the promise of another chance while he struggles to shake them loose. Their cruelty of discarding him just to test his loyalty hardens into weary self-blame, a compact anatomy of a manipulative bond.","sonnet":"A relationship that burned through second chances until there were none left — the speaker stayed too long beside someone who apologized without meaning it, pushed them to the ground to see if they'd return, and became whatever they feared becoming. The exit is partial and unresolved: distance is chosen, but the paralysis hasn't fully lifted.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~7-words":{"haiku":"Societal hypocrisy and judgment disguised as morality, with the speaker rejecting imposed shame and societal control.","opus":"A raw eruption of adolescent rage and disgust, spitting at hypocrites and thugs who glorified themselves and lost their minds, the venom escalating into a chaotic sprawl touching race, self-esteem, and violence, the chorus a barked chain of the title word. Deftones, the Sacramento band who'd grow into alt-metal's most atmospheric act, cut this early on Adrenaline, Chino Moreno's scream still all jagged edges before the melody arrived.","sonnet":"Barely contained rage at someone who glorified something until it destroyed them — the speaker has eaten years of stomped-down self-esteem and explodes in disgust. The targets blur between one person and a broader system of violence, touching on dignity, racial humiliation, and a fury too large for any single object to hold.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~976-evil": {"haiku":"Two people exist in a haze of mutual attraction, wondering if they can handle the intensity of connection.","opus":"A fixated admirer coaxes a distant, thousand-day stare into motion, wanting only to cruise and gaze into eyes, unafraid of the haze pulling him under. Deftones, Sacramento's shoegaze-drenched heavy band, borrow a horror-movie title for something closer to hypnotic infatuation, the plea to be woken up dissolving as fascination and desire quietly blur together.","sonnet":"An intoxicating fixation on someone whose gaze holds a particular haze — part stupor, part invitation. The narrator lingers in that spell across a thousand days, drawn toward a person whose pull is neither frightening nor dismissible, only irresistible.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction song that shares its title with a schlocky demon-phone horror film and plays the courtship as exactly that kind of trance — the speaker held under a haze he keeps asking to be woken from, even as he keeps choosing to stay. Chino Moreno works his signature move here, letting devotion and dread share the same breath: the plea to be roused from the spell sits right beside an eager are-you-in, so the surrender reads as both rescue-hope and willing capture. The song never resolves which it is, and the small vowel-slip in the outro that turns wake me into want her exposes the mechanism cleanly — the wanting is the very thing keeping him under.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"devotion and dread sharing the same breath without resolution","built":"a vowel-slip in the outro exposes wanting as the thing keeping him under"},
"deftones~acid-hologram":{"haiku":"Hearts sync and sync-out with a mysterious, shifting presence that intoxicates and reveals layers through light and disguise that constantly reverse and transform.","opus":"Chino Moreno's dreamlike ode to a shifting, seductive presence that smothers and reveals itself in shapes, hearts syncing as disguises shift and reverse. It is desire as something half-real, glimpsed through a chemical haze.","sonnet":"A song about a shape-shifting, elusive presence — possibly a person or a drug — that surrounds and smothers the narrator, revealing its true nature only briefly before disguising itself again, while calling up memories of someone else.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song never lets you fix what the adored You is: light, plume, disguise, a set of shapes pressed onto the singer rather than a face, so devotion here is aimed at something that keeps refusing to hold a form. That instability is the point Chino Moreno keeps circling on Gore — worship as intoxication, the acid hologram of the title, a radiant thing you can bathe in but never touch. The tell arrives quietly in verse two, when the shifting figure suddenly reminds him of her: the transcendent object collapses back into an ordinary human loss, and the ecstatic language is revealed as a way of not naming a person. So the chorus reads two ways at once — hearts syncing in time sounds like union, but paired with a disguise that shifts and reverses it becomes prophecy of the fade, the promise that this luminous surface will eventually turn over and show nothing behind it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["love & desire","heartbreak & loss","artifice & performance"],"means":"worship aimed at a surface that will never hold a face","built":"the transcendent figure collapses mid-song into an ordinary human name, exposing the ecstasy as evasion"},
"deftones~anniversary-of-an-uninteresting-event": {"haiku":"Melancholy reflection on loss and abandonment, where comfort disappears after someone leaves.","opus":"A hushed, surreal farewell to someone who has died, addressed like royalty whose departure has stopped the motion she used to stir for the living. Glimmers of light, a bed, and falling rain blur into dream rather than plain story. Deftones, the Sacramento band who welded metal weight to shoegaze haze, end an album on this slow ache, all reverb and yearning for a tide that no longer returns.","sonnet":"An elegy for someone who once generated warmth and movement — the waves they made have stopped coming since they left, the bar is dead, their presence kept something alive that has since gone cold and still. The speaker doesn't dramatize the loss; they simply note its absence, quietly, like marking a date that no longer means what it used to.","src":"haiku","fable":"An elegy that never lets itself name a death, working entirely through a private cosmology of light and tide: a warmth withdrawn from the world, and a sea that has refused to rise since the addressee went. The waves are the song's grief-engine — they were made for the living by the one now gone, and their stilling measures an absence the plain language keeps declining to state outright. That refusal is the point the title flags: the loss is monumental to the speaker and unremarkable to everyone else, an anniversary of an event the world found uninteresting, mourned in the cooled, image-bound murmur Deftones favored on the self-titled record rather than in any open cry.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","nature & the elements"],"means":"private catastrophe mourned as a world-unremarkable event","built":"the title names the disproportion between inside grief and outside indifference","fnote":"Moreno composed it a few notes at a time on a piano in the studio hallway, adding to it each day in passing — and the drums are played by Stephen Carpenter, the band’s guitarist."},
"deftones~around-the-fur":{"haiku":"A critique of shallow vanity and fashion obsession paired with desire for attention. The speaker rejects both superficiality and false claims of authenticity from a self-serving subject.","opus":"A jealous, disillusioned reproach aimed at a partner caught up in vanity and appearances, as the speaker begs only for her attention while calling her a liar and pleading with her not to destroy herself.","sonnet":"A taunted, obsessive fixation on someone the speaker describes as vain and hollow, demanding her attention while condemning the superficiality and dishonesty around her.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track of Deftones' 1997 record is a jealous, coke-scoured love song where the addict's craving and the lover's fixation become one appetite: the opening verse addresses an emptied vial that once passed for divinity, and by the refrain that worship has shrunk to a demand for a single person's undivided stare. Chino Moreno's plea keeps swinging between two failed instructions for how to be wanted, follow the trend or follow the rulebook, and rejects both, asking only to be watched. The bridge is where the mask slips: tenderness curdles into accusation, an offer of harmlessness pressed right against an order that the other person disarm and surrender, before it caves into the flat, thrice-repeated dismissal that he no longer cares about the rival woman, a denial so insistent it confesses the opposite.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"possessive craving dressed as a plea just to be watched","built":"the thrice-repeated denial of caring confesses the opposite by insistence"},
"deftones~back-to-school-mini-maggit":{"haiku":"Social rebellion and superiority over conformist peers and their false identities.","opus":"A song about high-school clique politics and outgrowing them, taunting a poser who copies your stance and clothes while you flip the script, close the book, and move to the next page as the real leader.","sonnet":"The speaker scorns the hollow social posturing of school-age clique culture — people copying looks and stances they don't own — and positions themselves as someone who has already moved past that page, daring pretenders to keep up or run.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hip-hop-tongued revenge daydream that turns a high school corridor into a ranking chart, then wins by refusing to keep playing the game. The verses survey the social order like a hierarchy, sneering at everyone rehearsing a borrowed pose to place higher. The chorus crowns the singer's clique rulers of the whole scene, but the song's real move lives in its schoolwork imagery. Grab a pen, take notes, and the classroom inverts: he becomes the subject others must study. By the bridge that figure completes itself as an arc through a shut volume and a turned leaf, a clean severance dressed as a taunt. So the closing chant to go study again reads as dismissal, not nostalgia. Underneath the swagger sits the same insecurity it mocks, which is why the flex must loop to stay convincing.","fv":2,"fnote":"The band hates this one: the label demanded another single after release, so Pink Maggit’s chorus was retooled with a rap verse — Chino said it was unfinished, that he wanted nothing so hip-hop on the record, and that half an hour later the hit single was ready to roll — and the reissue then planted the compromise as track one.","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"the classroom inverted so the speaker becomes the subject others must study","built":"schoolwork imagery reframes the taunt as severance — a shut volume, a turned leaf"},
"deftones~battle-axe": {"haiku":"The narrator questions a partner's self-awareness and resistance to change, suggesting they're delusional about their nature and history.","opus":"A dispute with someone who insists they are perfectly sane and always have been, while the narrator claims he was the true source of their trouble all along. His circling insistence that they are mistaken and will one day understand carries both blame and a quiet hope they might one day share his view.","sonnet":"The narrator addresses someone who has convinced themselves they've always been fine and unaffected, gently but firmly insisting they're wrong — that their self-perception is a lie, and someday the truth will catch up with them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A lover's argument staged as an intervention, where the speaker insists the other person is deluding themselves about who they really are and how they got that way. The tension runs on a single reversal: the other clings to the story that they have always been this fixed, broken thing, while the speaker keeps claiming ownership of the wound — that he was the cause all along — as if authorship were a kind of intimacy. Sleep and wakefulness become the battleground, one able to rest only when the other is undone, so the softness of the melody sits directly over a will to control dressed up as devotion. The closing turn drops the pretense of debate entirely and hardens into prophecy: not if you believe but you will see, the tender register curdling into a threat that the speaker will be proven right.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","love & desire","identity & becoming"],"means":"claiming authorship of the wound as intimacy","built":"tender melody laid over a will to control disguised as devotion"},
"deftones~be-quiet-and-drive-far-away":{"haiku":"Escape from a place that doesnt feel like home by driving far away with someone.","opus":"A man desperate to flee a town and a life that no longer feels his own, projecting a lost woman onto a new partner (dressing her in the others clothes) and begging to just be driven anywhere, as long as its far — escape itself, not the destination, is the whole point.","sonnet":"A song about desperate escape from a place that no longer feels like home — the narrator pleads to be driven anywhere, destination irrelevant, clinging to a companion (dressed in someone elses clothes, suggesting substitution or fantasy) as the only anchor while the city falls away.","src":"haiku","web":"Pure escape velocity — suffocating in a town and a life that don't feel like yours, the only ask is wordless: don't explain, don't comfort, just drive us away, as far as possible.","fable":"A song built almost entirely from erosion: the town that no longer feels like his own, the passenger dressed in another woman's clothes, the flat comfort of possession, each verse pared to a single unsettling line before dissolving into the one-word command. What starts as escape curdles into something colder, because the possession is what he can't leave behind, and by the outro even the destination stops mattering as far detaches from any where and becomes pure velocity, a mantra of not-caring that repeats until it wears its own meaning smooth. Deftones sit this quiet, obsessive murmur against Around the Fur's heaviness, and the tension between the hushed vocal and the distance he keeps demanding is the whole point: this is flight that carries its captive along, so the driving never actually gets him anywhere.","fv":2,"themes":["freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"velocity replaces destination until far detaches from any where","built":"hushed vocal against the album's surrounding heaviness enacts the airless obsessive murmur","fvr":"2.4"},
"deftones~beauty-school": {"haiku":"Attraction becomes intoxicating and wild when the other person drops pretense; authentic connection drives ecstatic and consuming passion.","opus":"An intense, voyeuristic attraction framed around watching a lover shed their public face and become raw and real; the surreal imagery of stars shooting from the barrel of their eyes and the warning that they could die doing this alone renders desire as an exhilarating, dangerous ride.","sonnet":"The narrator is transfixed by someone who lets their guard down completely — removing their constructed face — and finds that vulnerability almost unbearably electric, watching them and feeling more alive for it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song works through a single erotic scene of watching someone strip away their surface. Its image-system splits into two families that Deftones fuse: a body undressed to something raw underneath, and a machinery of recording and playback where a face is loaded, taped, resumed like footage that might not survive being played alone. The lover is watched consuming something while the speaker registers his own aliveness in the exchange, the projected light from that gaze scrambling him past speech. That collision of intimacy and film-grain apparatus is signature late-Chino Moreno, sensual and clinical at once, and the closing passage refuses to complicate it further, settling into wild repeated motion as its own answer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"intimacy framed through the clinical gaze of a recording apparatus","built":"film-grain playback apparatus spliced against erotic undressing"},
"deftones~beware": {"haiku":"Contaminated water—metaphorically poisoned or literally deadly—represents inescapable harm flowing toward those who knew the danger but drank anyway.","opus":"Opening 2006's Saturday Night Wrist, the Deftones shroud a doomed romance in an ominous water metaphor, a drink that recalls violence yet keeps circling back for more. Chino Moreno's cautions, that this was doomed and never secure, cast returning to a poisonous bond as deliberate self-destruction, the peril fully grasped and consumed anyway.","sonnet":"Something dangerous is being offered and accepted anyway — the water tastes like gunfire, the warning is issued, but the drink is taken again and again. The repetition is not ignorance but compulsion. Beware the water knows it is a trap and circles back regardless, teeth dry, the cup refilled.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song builds a single ritual out of a poisoned drink offered again and again, where the liquid stands in for a relationship both parties already know is finished, and the pouring becomes a dare to keep swallowing something harmful with full knowledge. What curdles it is the addressee's complicity: they were never deceived, they simply refused to name the danger aloud, so each fresh round arrives less as seduction than as a shared, wilful self-poisoning. Chino Moreno's croon lets sweetness and threat occupy the same breath, and the structure tightens the noose through repetition, the warning against the drink swelling from a lone line into a triple chant before the bridge strips everything down to parched mouths and one obedient last gulp. By the close nothing remains but the question itself, endlessly reposed, which is the point: the offer will never stop being made, and the drinking will never stop being chosen.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"a shared wilful poisoning dressed as seduction","built":"the warning swells from one line to a triple chant before the gulp"},
"deftones~bloody-cape": {"haiku":"Desire and desperation drive people toward the ocean's edge; falling together into unknown depths becomes inevitable.","opus":"Two lovers vow to plunge together off the edge of existence, feeding on each other's hunger as everything empties out around them. The band sustains its trademark collision of tenderness and dread, the intimate offering souring until the refrain buckles into a hammered, desperate appeal to God, romance and self-destruction fused.","sonnet":"Two people standing at the edge of everything familiar, urged toward an escape that feels cosmic and irreversible — the first to fall off the world together. Desperation and desire press toward departure; carnivalesque emptiness makes the leap feel necessary.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction dressed as a voyage: the verses coax a lover onto a ship and out past the horizon, crossing hearts over a shared drift into open water, where surrender and appetite blur into the same tidal pull. The chorus inflates that drift into cosmology, casting the couple as unprecedented pioneers sailing clean off the edge of a flat Earth, so romantic doom gets recast as a boast about doing what no one has done. Then the carnival empties and the swagger breaks: the outro is nothing but a hammered plea to God, repeated until the earlier bravado curdles into raw panic at what the pact actually was.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","freedom & escape","faith & the occult"],"means":"romantic bravado collapsing into raw panic at the pact made","built":"the outro strips the swagger to a hammered plea against its own opening boast"},
"deftones~bored":{"haiku":"Existential ennui and hollow transformation: the speaker disdains the gift of admiration and wishes for something real, expressing fatigue with surfaces and a desire for substance beneath performance.","opus":"An early Deftones track built on Chino Moreno's fractured, half-swallowed phrasing, orbiting a restless numbness where transformation into gold gives way, again and again, to boredom. The images of being reborn and simplified never satisfy; each promise curdles into the flat refrain of tedium. Beneath the alt-metal aggression lies a wish for something real that the cycle of self-reinvention keeps failing to deliver.","sonnet":"A restless figure cycles through transformation and disillusionment, burning through gifts and relationships because nothing sustains interest. The craving is for something real to hold against the drift, but boredom keeps returning as an almost constitutional condition — a restlessness that reshapes the self without ever satisfying it.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~change":{"haiku":"Predatory observation of vulnerability and violation, the speaker stripping away autonomy to feed on the victim's struggle and degradation.","opus":"Chino Moreno's most seductive song about watching someone transform, told through the unsettling image of pulling wings off a fly. Intimacy curdles into control and cruelty, desire and destruction wound so tight they become indistinguishable.","sonnet":"Watching someone transform — pinning them to glass, pulling off their wings, then watching the creature feel more alive than ever. The narrator observes the cross, hands over the gun, and the subject changes anyway; the lyric holds tenderness and cruelty in the same gesture without resolving which it is.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~change-in-the-house-of-flies":{"haiku":"Cruel dominance and transformation strip autonomy from the vulnerable, leaving them caged and broken under callous control.","opus":"Watching a lover transform likened to a child pulling wings off a fly and laughing, cruelty and fascination fused. The change that makes her feel alive is also her captivity, the tenderness curdled into something predatory and controlling.","sonnet":"About a sadistic dynamic in which the narrator strips someone of their freedom — they watch the person transform, pull off their wings, laugh, and ultimately hands them a gun; the transformation from captive to something 'alive' is deeply ambiguous, hovering between liberation and destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"The narrator watches someone transform and keeps insisting the change is a coming-alive, a liberation into something newly vivid and winged. But the second verse pulls the curtain: he is the one who took the creature home, pinned it to glass, and tore its wings off before laughing, so the tenderness of watching curdles into the cruelty of a boy killing an insect for sport. Chino Moreno keeps the two registers deliberately fused, since the same soft awe describes both the enthrallment of loving someone and the appetite to break them, and the tenderness is the disguise the predation wears. The turn toward the cross and the handed-over gun makes the exchange explicit as a plea to be finished off in kind, complicity dressed as intimacy. What lingers as the voice thins to a bare two-word accusation of change is that alive and destroyed have become the same word, the House of Flies a place where damage gets mistaken, by both parties, for transcendence.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"tenderness as the disguise predation wears toward the beloved","built":"alive and destroyed collapse into the same word by the final accusation"},
"deftones~change-in-the-house-of-flies-acoustic":{"opus":"The stripped acoustic reading of Deftones' signature song, its beauty-and-menace tension laid bare. Watching someone come alive as you quietly maim them, tenderness and predation made even more disquieting without the wall of guitars.","src":"opus"},
"deftones~cherry-waves": {"haiku":"Suffocation through entanglement and dread; someone wants another to drown with them in darkness.","opus":"Chino Moreno turns a drowning metaphor into intimate, deathly codependence, two figures clinging to the same plank while waves pull them under and he offers to swim down alongside. Anchors hung over his neck double as devotion and doom. It typifies Deftones' Saturday Night Wrist-era fusion of shoegaze softness and menace, desire and self-destruction rendered as a shared descent.","sonnet":"A relationship described as shared drowning: the narrator invites descent beneath the surface, and the imagery of waves and anchors suggests both suffocation and a dark invitation. Sinking together becomes a kind of intimacy — going under as an act of companionship rather than destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drowning framed as a love pact: two people clinging to the same wreckage, but the singer keeps offering to follow the other down rather than pull them up, so rescue and suicide collapse into the same gesture. The anchors hung on his neck turn the relationship into ballast, and the repeated question of whether this is what you want exposes the coercion underneath the tenderness — he needs to hear the other choose the deep so he can call his own descent devotion instead of surrender. Chino Moreno's blurred, half-swallowed delivery is the point, letting the seductive undertow and the panic of going under sound like the same thing until the outro's escape below reads less as freedom than as the final capitulation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","love & desire"],"means":"following the beloved down reframed as devotion not surrender","built":"blurred delivery makes undertow and panic sound identical"},
"deftones~cmnd-ctrl": {"haiku":"A song about wielding power through words and commands, where the speaker dismisses accountability while admitting he acts recklessly and without concern for others. It explores the intoxication of control and the refusal to take responsibility for consequences.","opus":"A caustic study of power for its own sake, someone issuing commands and mockery simply because they can, projecting their own instability outward, control as a game played by someone straight out of their mind.","sonnet":"An abrasive assertion of dominance and control — the speaker issues commands because he can, dismisses blame onto others, and mocks someone being watched or exposed by a camera. The throughline is a kind of contemptuous power play, calling the other person out of their mind while reveling in the ability to switch and steer at will.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of arbitrary power written from inside the aggressor's head, where every act traces back to the same rotten justification: because I can. The speaker fills a role, issues commands, points and laughs at a target through a camera, all while deflecting blame in circles that never resolve whose fault anything is. Chino Moreno keeps the sneer close enough to menace that the you being told they are out of their mind starts to feel less like an insult and more like the mask slipping — the taunt is a self-portrait, the barbarism it names is its own. The refrain that it is how you wear it, not what it is, is the read on the whole song: this is domination as pure style, cruelty that has stopped needing a reason and become its own reason.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"cruelty that has absorbed its own justification","built":"the taunt aimed at the target mirrors back as the aggressor's self-portrait"},
"deftones~combat": {"haiku":"A person demands clarity about loyalty and commitment, refusing ambiguity and forcing a choice.","opus":"A tense standoff that keeps demanding the listener declare an allegiance, the narrator throwing the blame back and refusing to be pinned down, sure he already understands who they truly are. Deftones, the Sacramento band who outgrew nu-metal, deliver it with Chino Moreno's mix of croon and shriek, the loyalty question looping into an atmospheric, unresolved circle.","sonnet":"A confrontation demanding someone pick a side — the speaker is tired of being held down by someone who won't commit, whose indecision is its own kind of betrayal. The accusation is direct: you've been pointing fingers while straddling the line, and the ground doesn't stay neutral forever. An ultimatum disguised as a question.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confrontation compressed to a single question, hurled back at whoever raised it: the accusing gesture is turned around, and the song refuses to let go until the other person commits. Chino Moreno strips the lyric almost to interrogation, the loyalty demand looping over the same handful of phrases so the repetition becomes the pressure rather than mere emphasis — the wanting to get down doubling as sex, as violence, and as the desire to reach the truth underneath the pose. By the bridge the mask drops on both sides: the speaker claims to already know what the other is really like, so the endless who-are-you-with chant reads less as genuine doubt than as a trap already sprung, an ultimatum that has quietly stopped waiting for an answer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","love & desire"],"means":"an ultimatum already decided before the question finishes","built":"repetition as pressure rather than emphasis the loop is the trap"},
"deftones~cut-hands": {"haiku":"Disconnection and digital deterioration replace authentic connection as systems fail and attempts to rescue founder. The speaker withdraws into grace, refusing rescue or engagement.","opus":"Cold rejection rendered in the language of failing machines and severed connection, the speaker refusing to be pulled off his own plane while the other's systems crash and their bid for attention goes unanswered.","sonnet":"A cold dismissal of someone fishing for attention and validation — their eyelids grow heavy, their systems fail, they're running out of pennies — and the speaker refuses to be pulled off their plane, staying in grace while the other implodes, delivering a detached \"fuck you, I'm okay\" as a verdict.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup rendered as a system failure report: the speaker addresses the other as a malfunctioning machine whose running processes crash, whose battery of eyelids dims, whose currency drains to nothing while it refuses to shut off. Chino Moreno's usual tenderness curdles into the flat cruelty of tech-support language, the offer of an update nobody asked for, an obscene reassurance that reports okay while withholding everything. The insistence on not being pulled out of one's grace repeats like a locked door, self-preservation that grows more suspect the harder it is chanted. When words finally give out, the closing demand to be hacked apart trades the software metaphor for the body under it, asking to be broken open rather than quietly powered down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","violence & murder"],"means":"self-preservation chanted as a locked door that grows more suspect with each repetition","built":"tech-support language replaces Moreno's usual tenderness, the software metaphor giving way to the body beneath it"},
"deftones~dai-the-flu": {"fable":"A love song that keeps confusing devotion with contagion: the tenderness Chino reaches for arrives only through wounds — sutures, a bruise where a joint gave way, teeth sinking in — as if being hurt were the sole proof anyone bothers to care. The gratitude in the refrain is desperate rather than warm, thanks offered up that affection exists at all, which is the arithmetic of someone who has learned to take damage as currency. Then the bridge dismantles that comfort by attrition, the same startled question circling until it wears through into a fevered mutter, the title surfacing as a half-swallowed pun — the flu, and folded inside it the word for dying — so the sickness stops being a figure of speech and becomes the thing itself, adoration and illness burning at one temperature.","fableDeep":"The chorus's grammar is the tragedy: not 'thank god you love *me*' but 'thank god that you love *at all*' — the discovery isn't of being loved but of the partner's bare capacity for love, and that's treated as enough. 'Believe the sting proves heart to me' states the exchange rate: pain accepted as the only legible signal of feeling. The bed is a battlefield of timing — 'you'll sleep for hours to keep away, then sink the teeth and bat your eyes' — affection administered in ambushes. And 'at least now I know,' repeated like a settling sigh, is someone talking themselves into calling the diagnosis good news. The title stays privately coded, which suits a song about signals only one person can read.","haiku":"A muddled reflection on physical pain, proof of love through suffering, and a moment of realization that the other person does love despite hardship and the metaphorical illness of the relationship.","opus":"A tangled, wounded meditation on a manipulative intimacy, where injury, deceit, and fleeting affection blur together and the speaker clings to the reassurance that he is loved at all.","sonnet":"A volatile, ambiguous intimacy where pain and damage ('fifteen stitches,' dislocated joint, sinking teeth) are reinterpreted as evidence of love, and the speaker arrives at a relieved, if uneasy, certainty that he is cared for.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"tenderness arriving only through wounds, damage taken as proof of care","built":"the title folds a word for dying inside the name of the sickness in one sound"},
"deftones~deathblow": {"haiku":"When one person arrives, beasts flee and agony ends; their presence brings warmth and connection, and the ropes holding people awake can be released.","opus":"A woozy, sensual song about someone whose mere arrival calms every craving and lifts the pain, the narrator confessing he ought to have seen where it was going. Deftones drift toward their dreamier, shoegaze-leaning mode here, Moreno's images of hanging cords and a posture that gives way at a touch binding longing and unease into one slow burn.","sonnet":"Someone's arrival that quiets every menacing thing in the room — the beasts back off, the agony lifts, and the speaker becomes acutely aware of every detail. But the relief is suspended; the ropes are still hanging, keeping everyone awake, and there's a collision implied in the moment she enters that the speaker should have seen coming. Comfort and catastrophe are the same visit.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the arrival of someone whose presence acts as an anesthetic — the beasts scatter, the agony lifts — but the relief is provisional, held in place only until they leave and the wanting starts again. The image-system pins comfort to violence: the ropes that keep everyone awake, the pose that breaks to get anyone off, salvation figured as a deathblow, so tenderness and its ending are named with the same word. What sounds like a love scene keeps confessing its own doom in the undertow — the admission that this should have been foreseen, the crash-course collision, the resignation that it is still the same song — reading less as devotion than as an addict's loop, where the person who takes the pain away is also the source of it and nothing changes for having known.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"the one who lifts the pain named with the same word as the wound","built":"salvation titled as a deathblow collapsing relief and ruin into one"},
"deftones~diamond-eyes": {"haiku":"Escape and transcendence with a beloved person, imagining cosmic union and realignment beyond earthly constraints.","opus":"The title track of the Deftones' 2010 comeback album, written after bassist Chi Cheng's near-fatal accident, is an urgent summons to escape together toward some cosmic elsewhere and be set right for good. Beneath its glittering celestial imagery, the narrator pledges to guide them both into a shared plane of renewal.","sonnet":"An urgent invitation to escape together to some transformed, shared realm — framed as a once-and-for-all departure where cosmic alignment and diamonds falling from the sky mark the moment of transcendence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Written by Chino Moreno in the shadow of bassist Chi Cheng's coma after his 2008 crash, this reads less like a love song than a vow held out to someone who cannot answer back. Escape keeps sliding toward cosmology: the plea to be carried off, once and for all, stops meaning a getaway and starts meaning reunion, two people dissolving into a sky that rains precious stone. The refrain barely mutates across repeats, shifting only from being pulled into a shared realm to leading someone there, which quietly reassigns the singer from passenger to guide. When the imagery finally turns funereal, with a coffin trembling and a needle failing, the promise doesn't collapse into grief but hardens, treating death as the last threshold to be crossed together rather than an ending.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Recorded in place of Eros, the album Deftones shelved when Chi Cheng’s 2008 crash left him in a coma — Moreno framed the new record as a celebration of his friend, a deliberate turn toward light. Cheng died in 2013.","themes":["love & desire","death & grief","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"love addressed to someone beyond the reach of reply","built":"refrain shifts singer from passenger to guide across its repeats"},
"deftones~digital-bath":{"haiku":"Lovers merge and transcend in a private, intimate space; water and breath become acts of connection and sensual surrender.","opus":"Chino Moreno eroticizes a drowning until tenderness and murder become indistinguishable, the killing rendered as the most intimate thing he's ever felt. Deftones make it disturbing precisely through beauty — the hush of the delivery, not gore, is what unsettles.","sonnet":"A scene of drowning intimacy — a couple together somewhere private and underwater in atmosphere, and then the person stops breathing while the narrator holds them, the line between sex and death collapsing into a single hushed moment.","src":"haiku","fable":"A bathtub killing narrated entirely in the tongue of a lover, so the murder never arrives as violence but as consummation. The whole lyric borrows the words of desire and wonder, and the hidden downstairs room becomes a seduction rather than a scene of harm. On White Pony the horror lives in register, not content: the speaker sounds exalted, not guilty. The pivot is one hushed couplet where a body stops breathing and the killer, still living, gently tends it afterward — aftercare more chilling than any struggle. The swelling refrain wants no person, only the sensation of taking one, a love song's mask stretched over an act it can name only as transcendence.","fv":2,"fnote":"Chino has described the scene exactly: five in the morning, still up partying, he pictured bringing a girl downstairs to a bath and, out of nowhere, throwing in an electrical device — then taking her out, drying her off, putting her clothes back on. He calls it one of the favorite songs he’s ever written.","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"murder conducted entirely in the tongue of a lover","built":"horror living in register not content, the killer sounds exalted not guilty"},
"deftones~doomed-user":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects delusions and shame imposed by someone destructive, exposing her rotting essence. He warns her that she burns alone, and her castles of manipulation crumble.","opus":"A scorching dismissal of a manipulative self-deceiver, told bluntly to disappear and stop making their collapse into someone else's problem. Running underneath is the singer's own scarred survival, refusing to let the other person's crumbling delusions be twisted into something about him, promising to recover regardless.","sonnet":"An explosive, contempt-filled cut-off from someone who has been draining and defiling the narrator — they're told to retire from the earth, die, take their delusions elsewhere; the narrator's own rot is acknowledged but the other person's is declared the true source of the corruption.","src":"haiku","fable":"On the surface this is pure eviction — a scorched-earth kiss-off ordering some parasitic figure off the earth, crowning them queen of filth as the speaker sets their whole kingdom alight. But the chorus is where the song turns on itself: the insistence that these are only delusions, that none of this is really about you, is the giveaway, because a person truly indifferent doesn't need to say it twice, let alone drape a whole song around the denial. The real movement is that the speaker's own wound keeps leaking through the contempt — the rotted core and black heart he confesses in the second verse are his, not the target's — so the read is a man torching someone else's castle to convince himself the fire was never his to feel.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"contempt as cover for a wound that keeps leaking through","built":"the denial chorus arrives twice, which is the confession"},
"deftones~drive":{"haiku":"A person in crisis cannot recognize their own spiraling situation, with no one stepping in to help or guide them.","opus":"A cover that strips its new-wave source to raw concern, a string of who-will-be-there questions asked of someone spiralling, insisting they cannot pretend nothing is wrong. The refrain about who will drive them home lands as genuine worry for a friend on the edge. Deftones, the Sacramento band who bent alternative metal toward atmosphere and dread, render Cars' original as something bruised and intimate.","sonnet":"A series of unanswered questions to someone in denial about how lost they are — who will warn you, pick you up when you fall, pay attention to your pain? The emotional stance is a mix of concern and accusation: you cannot keep pretending nothing is wrong, and the person asking may be the only one left who would do any of those things.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~ecdysis": {"haiku":"Natural catastrophe and metamorphosis blur as floods ravage while divine forces reclaim territory from human settlement. The land transforms through apocalyptic renewal while structures fail.","opus":"Named for the shedding of a skin, it stages a cataclysmic flood as renewal, waters rising to bathe away towns and ghosts so that a new phase, a molted self, can arrive.","sonnet":"Environmental apocalypse as spiritual transformation — rivers rising and swallowing towns, flames reclaiming their audience, rains reclaiming the environment — a new phase arrives through flood and fire, the old skin shed (ecdysis: moulting) to reveal whatever comes after.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song builds a single flood as an act of divine reckoning: two verses trace water carving through farmland and drowning valleys, each cresting to the throat, so the deluge functions less as weather than as a plague-symbol that jolts the sleeper's skeleton into terrified awareness. Ecdysis is the biological term for shedding an outer skin, and Deftones use it here to frame catastrophe as molting; the rising element personifies itself, fire and rain claiming back an audience and a habitat as if the erased human world were merely a husk being sloughed. The one figure that mutates across repetitions is the drowned settlement, whose living inhabitants in the first pass return as washed-away spectres in the last, so the swelling refrain quietly reveals that survival was never on offer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","nature & the elements","death & grief"],"means":"catastrophe framed as molting, survival never on offer","built":"living inhabitants in the first pass return as washed-away spectres in the last"},
"deftones~elite":{"haiku":"A person seeks attention and validation as proof of existence, but their depression and fake performance lead to internal collapse when they ripen into full vulnerability.","opus":"Chino's contempt for someone who mistakes attention and curated angst for being alive. The recurring image of ripeness bleeding out of control frames vanity and posed depression as things doomed to rot under their own performance.","sonnet":"A contemptuous address to someone who performs depression for attention and fakes their way toward fame — the narrator has lost all desire for them and anticipates the moment their pretense collapses and they lose control.","src":"haiku","fable":"Elite is a sneer at anguish staged for an audience, a portrait of someone whose depression is worn as a look and whose pain functions mainly as proof of being seen. The verses are pure accusation: attention taken as evidence of existing, misery color-matched to the eyes, the pose held for fame. The bridge turns almost tender and repulsed at once, imagining a body swollen with emptiness and sweet as honey, yet leaving the singer's mouth dead to it. Then the hammered chorus buries all of it under one image of ripeness that splits and spills past any holding. The verdict drowns the diagnosis, and repetition itself becomes the rupture the words keep threatening.","fv":2,"themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"performed anguish exposed as a pose rotting under its own weight","built":"hammered chorus buries diagnosis under repetition that enacts the rupture"},
"deftones~engine-no-9":{"haiku":"Underground street life and transgression presented through aggression and contempt; the speaker demands erasure of lies and cuts through noise with violence.","opus":"Early Deftones channeling street menace and hip-hop cadence into a fever of aggression, the lyrics more texture than narrative. It's about raw territorial threat and Chino Moreno's voice as pure combustible force rather than clear meaning.","sonnet":"The lyrics are largely incoherent and appear to be a garbled or incorrectly transcribed early rap-inflected track — no coherent throughline is discernible beyond aggressive street posturing fragments.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~entombed":{"haiku":"Since the speaker's companion arrived, they have remained trapped and devoted, confined in darkness while colors and shapes constitute their entire world.","opus":"Chino Moreno sings devotion as captivity, a lover so consumed by another that he is walled up safe and blind, reduced to shapes and colors. The tenderness and the imprisonment are the same thing, love as a beautiful entombment you would not leave even if you could.","sonnet":"About being permanently bound to another person from the moment they arrived — a total surrender of self that reduces existence to pure sensation (shapes and colors), framed as both imprisonment and sanctuary.","src":"haiku","fable":"Chino Moreno sings from inside the thing that adores rather than the one adored: the speaker arrives as an instrument built to comfort and answer to its keeper, and its wage for that loyalty is being sealed away, walled up snug and unharmed. Deftones let the confinement read as tenderness, the same hushed chorus that guarantees warmth also narrowing the world to a wash of hue and outline with no door and no second soul inside it. As the melody circles back to its first line, sight thins into pure sensation, and the promised refuge shows its other face as the tomb the title warned of — a love song hummed from a coffin, unable to tell shelter from burial.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"shelter and burial hummed in the same breath","built":"the melody circling back to its first line closes the coffin lid"},
"deftones~feiticeira":{"haiku":"A dark, violent scene: a drunk speaker is restrained, hurt, and forced into photographs by a woman; promises of release hang uncertain while pain continues.","opus":"A disorienting, dreamlike scene of a narrator drunk, bound and abducted by a woman who photographs and toys with him, pleading to be untied as she repeatedly sings that soon she'll let him go.","sonnet":"Narrated from the perspective of a captive, the song describes being bound, gagged, transported in a car trunk, and subjected to the whims of a captor who photographs them and keeps promising release. The atmosphere is one of helpless confinement, with the captor's reassurances — soon I'll let you go — becoming increasingly ominous.","src":"haiku","fable":"Feiticeira opens White Pony from inside an abduction, narrated by the captive as it unfolds rather than recalled afterward. The verses are disoriented body-report: drunk on the knees, jaw wrecked, teeth grinding against a gag, hauled from a car trunk while a camera records the scene. The dread lives in the whispered refrain, which belongs not to him but to her, a captor cooing that his release is coming and the ordeal nearly finished. That promise is the mask; the outro strips it to bare repetition, the same soothing line looping long past the point where anyone believes it. Chino Moreno makes the lullaby the threat, comfort and captivity fused, the reassurance curdling the more insistently it repeats.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"lullaby as captor's mask, comfort and captivity fused","built":"the soothing refrain belongs to her not him, looping past the point of belief"},
"deftones~gauze":{"haiku":"A person warns someone about the consequences of their choices, unable to stop or fight what they have initiated as events unfold.","opus":"A guarded speaker cautions someone that their decisions bring outcomes nobody can undo, and flatly refuses to battle a blaze the other person kindled. Drawn from Deftones' earliest Adrenaline-era work, it turns on grim foreknowledge, the narrator claiming to already see the doom awaiting whoever unlatched the door, helpless to reverse a chain of events already underway.","sonnet":"A point of no return acknowledged with cold clarity. Someone has set forces in motion that cannot be stopped or defended — the gates are open, the fire ahead is chosen. The narrator watches, knowing the consequences already, powerless to intervene.","src":"haiku","fable":"One of Koi No Yokan's tightest lyrics, and among Chino Moreno's most direct: the speaker is watching someone walk into a fire of their own making and cannot do a thing about it. The verses are warnings that keep failing — first a caution against choosing badly, then the gates already thrown open, then a needling shove to stop stalling and own the decision, since the person facing the consequence turns out to be the one who made it. What holds the song together is the refrain of powerlessness that runs under all of it, the flat admission that the speaker can neither halt nor resist what the other has set going. By the last chorus that helplessness has curdled into a grim foreknowledge — he already knows how this ends, and knows he never could have stopped it once it started — so the title's gauze reads as the thin dressing laid over a wound that was always going to bleed through. It's a song about complicity by paralysis: the horror isn't causing the ruin but standing close enough to name it and still being unable to reach in.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","death & grief"],"means":"knowing the wound will bleed through and still unable to reach in","built":"the title's thin dressing names the inadequacy of every warning"},
"deftones~genesis":{"haiku":"Spiritual rebirth and balance achieved through rejecting false narratives and embracing authentic renewal.","opus":"Opening the Sacramento alt-metal band's Ohms album, this is Chino Moreno's meditation on renewal: refusing the false choices you're handed, finding equilibrium, and emerging transformed. The lyric treats personal rebirth as breaking free of everything that once defined you, all carried on the band's dense, dreamlike heaviness. It frames starting over as the only way forward.","sonnet":"A rebirth that refuses both sides of what it's been told — the speaker burns the old version of themselves away, climbing out of ashes into balance and then into pure weightlessness. By the end they're floating off in the ether, miles beyond the sound, with no need to return, sharing the path to becoming unbound with whoever will follow.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~geometric-headdress":{"haiku":"A seductive figure wields spiritual or mystical power over a congregation of believers. Faith collapses at her words, and her followers are trapped willingly in her prism.","opus":"An ornate, ritualistic seduction cast as devotion to a veiled, commanding temptress. The deeper current is surrender to a mesmerizing presence, her worshippers drawn in until their defenses collapse and their faith realigns around her, desire elevated into something like a trance.","sonnet":"A ritualistic, quasi-religious fixation on a commanding, ornamented woman — she's a temptress and a high priestess, her words draw the narrator into a circle of devotion from which they have no desire to escape, surrendering completely to her presence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Chino Moreno's signature blur of the carnal and the holy turns unusually cold here, as a figure of pure attraction is recast into a presiding priestess whose body gets rendered as pattern, angle, and ceremonial dress. The shift is architectural rather than ornamental. Early verses run on hunger, but soon a kneeling throng and a chanting plural rewrite one man's want into a whole congregation. The pre-choruses issue their orders in the cadence of ritual, demanding private assent as though devotion were a lone sum the rite simply certifies. What the chorus dresses up as renewal is closer to capitulation, and the bridge's dull command to mirror the speaker exposes it as recruitment wearing the mask of love. The outro is the snare confessed at last, naming rapture and belonging in one breath before sealing the faithful behind glass. Here the cool geometry of the opening returns as a cage they have willingly agreed never to abandon.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["faith & the occult","love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"desire reframed as congregation until the recruit forgets who recruited them","built":"the cool geometry of the opening returns in the outro as the cage they have agreed never to leave"},
"deftones~ghosts":{"haiku":"Past traumas and regrets haunt a person even during moments of success, growing wilder and more consuming over time.","opus":"A restless nocturne about being stalked by one's own history, unable to move on, stranded with no destination as past selves rear up wilder each time triumph seems within reach, sabotaging every shot at glory. Deftones, the Sacramento alt-metal band, render it through their shoegazing haze, Chino Moreno's aching delivery enlarging private torment into something spectral.","sonnet":"A man ambushed by the past just as he reaches the threshold of winning — every door broken, momentum building, and then the ghosts of his life blow in wilder than the wind and scatter everything. He's left nervous, alone, with no clear direction, wondering where the certainty he once had went.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~good-morning-beautiful": {"haiku":"A speaker urges someone to escape an impending divine wrath before catastrophe, offering partnership to flee together.","opus":"The alt-metal band threads sweetness through dread, a narrator urging a lover to rouse herself before some coming fury lands and pressing her to flee with him while there is still time. He claims to read her plainly, begging her not to pick the sky over the ground and over him, the soft greeting shadowed by the doom he keeps invoking.","sonnet":"An urgent plea directed at someone who has chosen spiritual withdrawal over earthly presence, asking them to wake up and leave before something irreversible arrives. The narrator reads a pattern of avoidance written on the walls and offers both invitation and warning in the same breath.","src":"haiku","fable":"A lover's plea disguised as a warning about an approaching reckoning, sung with the pushed, whispered-to-screamed dynamic Chino Moreno favors, where devotion curdles into pressure: leave with me before the punishment lands, and you're insane to refuse. The bridge is where the sweetness turns coercive, the singer claiming to have read his partner's secrets off some wall and treating their exhaustion, their longing for something higher and elsewhere, as a betrayal of him and the ground under them both. What arrives as tenderness in the title keeps folding into ultimatum, so the repeated call to flee before the deadline reads less as rescue than as a suitor who cannot separate his love from his need to possess.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"tenderness as ultimatum the rescue indistinguishable from possession","built":"the sweet title keeps folding back into a deadline neither avoids"},
"deftones~goon-squad":{"haiku":"A trendsetter arrives with style and control, cutting through followers while recruiting allies to break in a designed aesthetic vision.","opus":"A boast of restless creative superiority, the narrator claiming to invent ahead of every trend and outgrow whatever the crowd is still chasing. Deftones frame it as an outsider's manifesto, Chino Moreno dismissing status and fashion in favor of genuine originality, and daring you to join on his terms.","sonnet":"Aesthetic provocation as territorial claim — the speaker moves through towns carving their name, cutting against trends before they start, breaking in with their crew to design something for themselves on their own terms. The invitation to join is extended, but also conditional: this is already designed, already decided, and the question is only whether you're in.","src":"haiku","fable":"A predatory tastemaker narrates the whole thing, claiming to author style before it exists — tagging his signature onto every skyline, getting there first so any trend you discover already belongs to him. Chino Moreno's boast plays like a gift wrapped around a threat: the promise that all of this was tailored just for you, delivered by his own hand, turns couture and generosity into the vocabulary of a robbery. What poses as an invitation to join the crew and stroll in wearing his label is really a home invasion sold as a fashion line, the crowd he seduces the same kingdom he watches topple two at a time. The looping demand — are you in, are you in — is the seduction devouring itself, less an open door than a fist already at the latch.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"couture and generosity as the vocabulary of a robbery","built":"the looping invitation is a fist already at the latch"},
"deftones~gore":{"haiku":"A figure pulls another into intimate darkness where they both writhe in vices together; they hunt for prizes and become complicit in the same depraved games.","opus":"A seductive, sinister invitation to complicity, pulling someone into shared vice and the hunt until they're running the game themselves. Deftones making corruption feel intimate and dreamlike, control passed hand to hand.","sonnet":"About initiating someone into a dark, indulgent world — drawing them in with shared vice and bloodied pleasure, welcoming them to the gang of those who wallow in their own worst impulses, the hunter and hunted becoming complicit together.","src":"haiku","fable":"The horror here arrives entirely in second person, addressed by a genial host, so the listener stops being a witness to predation and becomes its freshly congratulated recruit. Moreno frames seduction as an initiation ritual: the refrain keeps dangling promotions, an escalating anaphora of ascent, while each verse confesses the quiet mechanics of luring and hooking that leave everyone smeared in shared filth. The title itself hinges two meanings, the atrocity and the hunger for it, and the track wants your palate schooled toward that appetite rather than repulsed by it. Notice how the promised reward curdles across every repeat, its horizon narrowing until the climb bottoms out at a single flat syllable of belonging, which is really just arrival back inside the snare where it opened.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"The album keeps handing the mic to a recruiting we — Xenon’s sirens, Rubicon’s crowd-and-lights, Acid Hologram’s plume — and the title track is where that collective states its terms plainest.","themes":["surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"seduction staged as initiation until appetite and atrocity share a name","built":"the title hinges two meanings — the horror and the hunger for it — and the song schools you toward the second"},
"deftones~graphic-nature":{"haiku":"A partner is mesmerized by another's dangerous allure and mystique; they beg to understand the secrets beneath the surface.","opus":"The alt-metal band render a fixation on a partner's concealed performance, coaxing them to open up and reveal the luminous, hidden danger they keep stowed. Turned on by watching them loose their demons, the narrator wants to see the mechanism behind the illusion, undone so thoroughly each time that it floors him and leaves him ablaze.","sonnet":"Fascination with someone who performs a kind of controlled self-revelation, releasing carefully hidden darkness on demand. The narrator watches with rapt attention, asking to be shown the mechanism behind an ability to expose and conceal in precisely measured doses.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction staged as a magic act: the speaker begs someone to expose the rig behind the illusion — the pulleys and switches, the concealed mechanism of a performance that glows like poison and turns tucked-away demons loose on cue — while every plea to explain is really a plea not to, because the not-knowing is the whole charge. The refrain lives on that contradiction: he demands the secret, then confesses that each successful trick buckles his legs where he stands, leaving him surprised, undone, on fire. What sounds like a lover's curiosity is closer to a spectator's willing captivity, and the song knows it, keeping the how unanswered so the awe stays intact. That fusion of desire and danger — intimacy narrated as a spectacle that could destroy you — is Chino Moreno's most durable move, worship and threat in the same breath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"demanding the secret while knowing the not-knowing is the charge","built":"each chorus leaves the mechanism unexplained so the awe holds"},
"deftones~headup":{"haiku":"The speaker grieves a lost friend while urging resilience and attitude; walking into the world with head held high despite parasitic forces.","opus":"A tribute soaked in grief and rage for a friend the singer lost before he could say goodbye, recalling the music they bonded over and vowing to recover his spirit. The furious screamed refrain turns his anger outward at whoever caused the loss, resolving to move through the world with his head held high.","sonnet":"A tribute to a lost friend (partly dedicated to Soulfly's Max Cavalera's stepson Dana), expressing grief through the shared love of music and urging resilience — walking into the world with your head up despite the pain of the people who drag you down.","src":"haiku","fable":"An elegy that refuses to sit still in mourning, written for Max Cavalera's stepson, dead at twenty-one in a crash his family never accepted as accidental and sung across the two men who loved him. It opens with Chino Moreno fumbling the ordinary regret of never getting the chance to say goodbye, then swells into the remembered rhythm of a shared friendship before the grief finds its target. The chorus is pure accusation, the same disbelieving question hurled four times at the killer while Cavalera answers each line from off to the side with the name of the band he built out of the same loss, so the track stages two griefs calling to each other across the stereo field rather than one voice processing alone. That repetition is the whole engineering: the question can never be answered, so it is asked again and again until it burns out, and the closing chant that demands the body stay upright collapses into a hammered, wordless pulse, less a resolution than an act of pure will standing in for one, a survivor forced to remain vertical because the mind has nowhere left to go.","fv":2,"fnote":"Cavalera’s answering ad-libs are where Soulfly was born — by every account he took his new band’s name from this chorus — and Chino sang the take holding a framed photo of Dana, smashing it into his face until he bled; Max said it felt like Dana sat in the studio between them.","themes":["death & grief","anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"two griefs calling across the stereo field","built":"the unanswerable question repeated until it burns out"},
"deftones~hearts-wires":{"haiku":"Emotional devastation leaves the narrator drowning in illusion, bound by razor wire to another's heart, seeking to break free while remaining entwined.","opus":"Deftones translate their signature ache into a devotional surrender bordering on the vampiric, the narrator drowning in a lover's sea while begging them to save themselves first. Cutting through razor wire to feast on a heart, the imagery fuses tenderness and cannibal hunger into obsession that lasts until the end.","sonnet":"Abandonment has left a wound that nothing fills. The narrator is stripped bare, drowning in illusion, yet still reaches toward the person who left — wanting them to save themselves first before returning. Love persists as an obsession, a razor edge that claims ownership even through the end.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title fuses pulse and circuitry until they cannot be pried apart, so devotion can arrive only as a violent operation, a lover carving inward past the barbed fencing meant to keep him out. What sounds like a gothic vow is really a self so bonded to another that tenderness and mutilation become one gesture, since there is no gentler route into a body than straight through the flesh. Chino Moreno writes hunger in the vocabulary of harm, yet his one flinch of humility undercuts the possessiveness: begging the beloved to rescue themselves first admits this appetite can rescue nobody. By the last chorus the verbs splinter into pure severance and swallow, as if love pushed to its limit can complete itself only by dissolving the border that ever made two people two.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"tenderness and mutilation collapsed into one gesture because no gentler route exists","built":"the one flinch of humility — begging the beloved to save themselves first — undercuts the possessiveness the verbs keep building"},
"deftones~hexagram": {"haiku":"Abstract imagery of worship, violence, and cyclical tragedy; a car bomb and crowds merge into a single, repeated moment, suggesting acceptance or resignation to recurring horror.","opus":"A dense collision of worship, spectacle and violence, sacrifice and car-bombing rendered as the same numbing televised sound. Chino Moreno builds atmosphere over argument, letting devotion and destruction blur into one dizzying loop.","sonnet":"The song juxtaposes images of streets crowded with people and animals moving through a charged atmosphere with the sudden, casual detonation of a car bomb, all delivered with an unsettling sweetness. The repeated chant of worship and play frames the violence as spectacle, with the bomb tick and crowd noise becoming the same indistinguishable sound.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built on a single collapsing distinction: the ecstatic crowd and the atrocity run on identical machinery, and the verses keep folding devotion and violence into one gesture until adoration and detonation cannot be told apart. The chorus refuses to say anything else, chanting the same two-word pairing over and over so that ritual becomes pure mechanical repetition, the way a trigger or a shutter repeats. By the final verse the timer counting down a car bomb is fused, in Chino Moreno's clipped delivery, with the click of a camera, both landing as the identical percussive noise the bridge fixates on and names as one sound. The flat refusal to develop the language is the meaning here: spectacle stripped to its stutter, congregation and killing made audibly interchangeable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult"],"means":"devotion and detonation made audibly interchangeable","built":"car-bomb timer fused with camera click into one percussive stutter"},
"deftones~hole-in-the-earth": {"haiku":"The speaker declares their exit from a situation—leaving through an escape or death—after expressing hatred for their surroundings and those in them.","opus":"Deftones, on their self-titled record made amid internal strain, let Chino Moreno voice raw rupture: an accusation of evil, a confession of hating friends who lack taste, and a resigned departure through a hole in the earth. The imagery of bowing at an ending frames a friendship or self collapsing, alienation delivered in the band's signature blur of tenderness and disgust.","sonnet":"Disengagement from a person whose malice has become inexplicable. Rather than confronting the evil further, the narrator finds an exit — a rupture in the ground — and steps through it, choosing distance and waiting somewhere else over any continued exposure to the damage.","src":"haiku","fable":"A resignation letter disguised as an exit interview. The song interrogates a second person for their cruelty, then abandons the question mid-breath because the singer has already decided he is leaving; the recurring image of a fissure in the ground is less a place than a permission slip, the way out he keeps announcing. What makes it curdle is the small confession dropped between the grand farewells — the flat, almost petty admission that he despises everyone close to him for their bad taste — which pulls the exit down from tragedy to something bitterer and more human, a person burning every bridge on the way to the edge. By the final passages the accusations thin into pure repetition, the where and the ending stated without object, so the refrain stops arguing and simply waits, which is the mask slipping: underneath the theatrical goodbye is someone who has run out of things to say and is only marking time until the hole opens.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Moreno has said he wrote it in a rage after learning the band had weighed replacing him during his drift into Team Sleep — the line about hating his friends is aimed at the other four, and he later granted they probably hated him at the time too.","themes":["freedom & escape","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a goodbye that burns what it claims to mourn","built":"the refrain loses its object until only the exit remains"},
"deftones~i-think-about-you-all-the-time": {"haiku":"Constancy defines the speaker's internal landscape where one person becomes perpetual presence and harbor. Waves carry both drowning and shelter, and the speaker accepts both as inseparable.","opus":"Surrender to an all-consuming love figured as a warship and a wave, riding the beloved's calm while sinking willingly inside them, changeless and out of one's mind.","sonnet":"A devotional surrender to someone who holds the speaker in a constant altered state — sailing inside a warship, riding a wave, held in calm arms, the theme on endless repeat — change feels impossible because the bond is too complete and too warm.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song that keeps naming the beloved as a weapon and a hazard — a warship boarded, shallows to cross — only to melt every threat into shelter, the same arms that could hold you under instead holding you calm. The tension the title conceals is that thinking about someone all the time is not tenderness but a fixed orbit: the vow that nothing will ever change lands as both devotion and sentence, and the song settles it by surrendering, going out of its mind into the other's wave rather than staying its own. What starts as two voices sinking together resolves into one being kept afloat, so the subject is less romance than the relief of ceasing to steer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"relief of ceasing to steer replacing the effort of romance","built":"the title's apparent tenderness lands as a fixed orbit — devotion indistinguishable from sentence"},
"deftones~infinite-source": {"haiku":"A final performance celebrates shared history and devotion while accepting impermanence, holding close to communion in the face of ending.","opus":"A farewell staged as a final bow on stage, holding a loved one close through the last ride while releasing everything at once. Late-career Deftones turn a parting into gratitude for all the years, dreams and nights that led here.","sonnet":"A farewell from a stage — possibly a final show — where the speaker and their audience hold onto each other through accumulated years of shared dreams, culminating in the image of releasing and floating away together.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is staged inside one farewell performance, a bow and a raised glass before a crowd, and every verse loops back to that departing gesture rather than moving past it toward anything new. The trick is that the theatre and the relationship bleed into one image-system: the plea to be held becomes the plea for an audience, so leaving a lover and leaving the spotlight read as a single exit. Deftones let the refrain accumulate instead of resolve, and by the final stretch the returns pile up until the parting stops feeling like an event and starts feeling like a permanent condition, a goodbye that never finishes, which is what turns the title's promise of something inexhaustible into ache rather than comfort.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a goodbye that never finishes becomes a permanent condition","built":"theatre and relationship bleed into one image-system so leaving a lover and leaving a stage are one exit"},
"deftones~kimdracula": {"haiku":"One person wishes the other's arms were snakes rather than hands, haunted by transformation and unable to make the other person commit to a choice.","opus":"The alt-metal band pair apocalyptic imagery with romantic frustration, the earth rotting away and eyes going blank as the narrator repeatedly wishes a lover would make up their mind. His strange plea that snakes might become her arms threads through the doom, the coming end of the world entangled with a private ache for someone who will not decide.","sonnet":"A figure addresses someone caught between dimensions of darkness and desire, wishing the serpentine energy surrounding them were instead an embrace. The earth itself seems to blank out and decay, and the plea for a definitive choice hangs unanswered in the dissolving night.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song built entirely on wishing the wrong thing were the right thing: the snakes coiling toward the singer could be an embrace if only they were arms instead, and the whole plea reduces to a partner who won't commit either way. The apocalyptic frame — the earth blanking out, rotting, hanging on a blink — turns a stalled relationship into a world holding its breath, as if the planet's survival rides on this person finally deciding. Then the song abandons argument for the bridge's stammered knowing, a repetition that admits the answer is already known and hopeless; the outro leaves him alone with that certainty, still trailing off, still not saying it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"wishing the wrong thing were the right thing across a held breath","built":"the planet stalling while one person refuses to decide","fnote":"The title is not a character — Moreno lifted it from his own email address of the era, a piece of personal debris from the album’s darkest stretch."},
"deftones~knife-party":{"haiku":"Disconnected from reality, figures float in an anemic space where desire and danger blur into intimate transgression.","opus":"An intimate, druggy ritual of shared vulnerability where knives and anemic sweetness turn violence into a strange tenderness. Floating weightless in a private room, harm and kissing become indistinguishable acts of trust.","sonnet":"A sensual, drugged invitation into an intimate space where the rules of ordinary life don't apply — knife as both weapon and erotic prop — where everyone is weakened and pleasurably lost, floating in a room that never touches the floor.","src":"haiku","fable":"Seduction staged as a ritual of consented harm, where the summons to step inside and lie down arrives inseparable from the order to bring a blade. The bloodless world here is its own erotic climate, everyone drained pale and made tender by it, and Chino sells that fusion of the sickly and the delicious as a lover's vow rather than a warning. The refrain never settles whether the weapon is threat, gift, or foreplay, and that refusal is the point. Each repetition tightens the loop until surrender and violence stop being separable acts. Weightless above the floor, the two don't escape the danger so much as dissolve into it, which is the whole dream White Pony keeps offering, dread and desire poured into one drug.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"dissolving into danger rather than escaping it as the erotic goal","built":"the refrain never settles whether the blade is threat, gift, or foreplay"},
"deftones~korea":{"haiku":"Sensory and fragmentary, the song dwells on taste, texture, and carnal closeness between lovers. It evokes a transient encounter marked by animal attraction and physical sensation without narrative or emotion.","opus":"An abstract, sensory piece built from images of teeth, skin, claws, and coming inside, evoking a charged, animalistic physical encounter.","sonnet":"The lyrics deal in fragmented, sensory images of physical and erotic encounter — taste, skin, claws, heat — sketching a charged and somewhat predatory intimacy without a clear narrative arc.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction staged entirely as hunger, where wanting a body and wanting to devour it stop being separable. The lyric builds from a mouth-and-predator vocabulary: tasting, teeth, a cavity, animal claws that grip rather than caress. Consumption and consummation blur, until an invitation inward reads at once as sex and as swallowing. The chopped, chant-like hook keeps circling the same few verbs, so the appetite never resolves into a person, only a rhythm of taking. On White Pony this is Deftones at their most predatory and hushed, letting menace and tenderness share one breath.","fv":2,"fnote":"The first song written for the record, and the one carrying its title: Chino said it talks about the white pony, strippers and drugs — then spent the next two decades enjoying the name’s ambiguity and confirming nothing.","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"consumption and consummation blurred until appetite never resolves into a person","built":"the chopped chant-like hook cycles the same verbs without release, miming the hunger"},
"deftones~l-mirl":{"haiku":"Death and rebirth intertwine as the speaker transcends old torment through dissolution and metamorphosis.","opus":"A cold severing from someone reduced to a ghost, paired with a suicidal urge reframed as rebirth into the calming waves. Death becomes a reawakening, the speaker smiling as he dives deep to emerge as another form of himself.","sonnet":"A song about emotional detachment after a toxic relationship and the fantasy of rebirth — the narrator disowns any feeling for a past lover, imagines putting a gun to their head and diving into waves as a kind of ego-death, and emerges declaring a violent, ecstatic reawakening into a new self.","src":"haiku","fable":"Moreno borrows the grammar of drowning and self-harm only to spend it on rapture: the plunge here reads as molting rather than dying, a shed skin instead of a shut door. What actually perishes is the relationship, and the ex gets reduced to a spent curse that hollowed out his resolve, so peeling free of her becomes a rebirth staged as escape. The recurring tide carries both meanings at once, numbing the grief while amniotically incubating the new self. That doubling is why the triumphant closing shouts land as suspiciously loud, a costume of ecstasy stretched over the annihilation it half wants to believe it has survived.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"Moreno has glossed the bracketed title as the chat acronym — (Let’s) Meet In Real Life — which darkens the dive: the new realm he reawakes in is the one outside the digital body, by whatever exit.","themes":["identity & becoming","heartbreak & loss","freedom & escape"],"means":"rebirth staged as escape, the new self incubated inside the grief it cannot fully name","built":"the triumphant closing shouts land as suspiciously loud, a costume of ecstasy stretched over the annihilation beneath it"},
"deftones~leathers":{"haiku":"Someone removes protective barriers to expose their authentic self, vulnerability, and inner truth to their enemies, shedding facades to display who they truly are.","opus":"Deftones issue an urgent call to radical openness, framing total self-exposure as an act of rebellion, a demand to strip away every defense and show one's raw interior to the world. The song stages transformation as a violent, cathartic unmasking, severing old attachments completely and daring an adversary to witness who you truly are underneath.","sonnet":"A call to radical authenticity — shed the protective skin, show your real shape, make yourself vulnerable and visible to your enemies and your world rather than hiding behind a casing that keeps everyone at a distance.","src":"haiku","fable":"Nearly every line is an imperative barked at a second person — revolt, confess, pry the ribcage apart, unroll the tongue — so the track reads less like testimony than like a coach's initiation shouted at someone frozen at the threshold. It runs on one relentless figure, the snake abandoning its old sheath, until being flayed and readable stops registering as damage and becomes the whole objective. What twists it away from cheap self-help is the direction of that nakedness: not turned inward toward repair but aimed outward at an adversary, the exposed viscera repurposed into a display you force a rival to witness. Deftones keep the guitars plush and narcotic underneath, so the command to be nakedly, helplessly legible lands nearer a dare than a balm.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"nakedness repurposed into a display aimed at a rival","built":"narcotic guitars hold the commands that should hurt but don't"},
"deftones~lhabia":{"haiku":"A speaker experiences confusion, desire, and pain in an intimate relationship, describing contradictory impulses to burn and seduce while acknowledging the sickness of wanting to hurt someone.","opus":"An early Deftones cut, it churns with restless boredom and predatory desire, the narrator plotting to get someone back into a cycle he half-wants to burn through. Fragmented images of blood, boredom, and heretical release resist clean reading. Chino Moreno's stream-of-conscious delivery matters more than plot, capturing the raw, unstable aggression of the band's self-titled late-1990s period.","sonnet":"A swirl of transgressive desire, boredom driving the narrator toward risk and excess. Devotion mixes with a self-aware disgust at the costs — the push-pull of wanting something destructive while watching what it does, but finding that how it feels keeps winning out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A verse of raw appetite, addressed to a lover the singer half-wants to consume, gives way to the real subject in the chant that closes the song: a habit that keeps wrecking you while the wreckage stays forgiven because the high still pays out. Moreno builds the outro as pure circle, cycling the same self-permission three and four times over, and the repetition is the meaning, a mind stuck rehearsing its own excuse, unable to break its orbit. It lands early in Deftones' run, before the ambient patience of their later records, so the ugliness stays raw and unresolved rather than folded into atmosphere, craving and harm nailed side by side with no exit granted.","fv":2,"fnote":"The title was Labia until Chino, uneasy with the anatomy, slipped an H into it — the euphemism performing exactly the half-concealment the song’s appetite keeps attempting.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"the outro loops self-permission for an addicted mind","built":"repetition is the meaning not decoration in the outro"},
"deftones~lifter":{"haiku":"Lust and objectification corrupt relationships; the narrator's desire mixes with resentment, recognizing both as complicit in mutual dehumanization.","opus":"Moreno circling self-loathing and toxic desire, the envy of wanting to be someone else and the shame that turns intimacy into cruelty. It's a song about recognizing yourself as the monster and not being able to stop.","sonnet":"A volatile, self-aware account of destructive desire: the speaker acknowledges that both parties are monsters in an encounter that is sore and raw, cycling through self-disgust and longing without resolution, the anger and arousal indistinguishable from each other.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~locked-club": {"opus":"Belonging as both ecstasy and coercion, a tribe you join or get left out of, its members drawn closer to god and each other through shared ritual. The song sits in the seductive pull and the quiet menace of that in-or-out ultimatum.","src":"opus","fable":"The song stages belonging as a ritual with a threat sewn into it: the chant offering enrollment in the parade or the cold of exclusion is really the whole engine, an ultimatum that only ever repeats because a cult tolerates no third answer. Around that spine Chino Moreno builds a rite of ascent — a body wading up out of a pool, tilting its face toward something worshipped overhead, then the vertigo of a force draining through it on contact with the massed crowd. What curdles the trance is how the surrender is sold as glory, the calm and the chaos cradled with equal devotion, so the rising toward truth reads less as liberation than as a self dissolving happily into the group's grip.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","surveillance & control","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"surrender to the group sold as personal ascent toward truth","built":"the enrollment chant is an ultimatum with no third answer, only ever repeated"},
"deftones~lotion":{"haiku":"Contempt for fake people and borrowed styles; the narrator expresses disgust at those they consider boring and derivative.","opus":"A contemptuous kiss-off to someone the speaker finds boring, phony and beneath him, repeatedly declaring he feels sick over them. Beneath the disgust and the crumbling scene is his own nausea and detachment, the sickness as much his condition as his verdict on them.","sonnet":"A contemptuous dismissal of someone the speaker finds genuinely nauseating — their mediocrity, their self-satisfaction, their presence makes the speaker physically sick, and the final verse drops all pretense and turns into pure hostile disgust.","src":"haiku","fable":"A contempt song that keeps trying to sound bored and keeps failing. Chino Moreno opens by mourning something he built and someone else spoiled, then swings into open disgust at a hollow rival, and the dropped-shrug chorus is his bid to rate the whole grievance as beneath comment, secondhand, not worth the heat already in his voice. But the structure exposes the pose: every verse turns nastier and more personal, the taunts curdle from aloof dismissal into a demand that the target quit groveling and go find a heart, and the aloof front finally collapses into a nausea that the back half of the track simply repeats until the sneer has nowhere left to stand. The gutting move is that the loathing is inseparable from wanting, so the mask of indifference and the sickness underneath turn out to be one face, and on this 1997 Around the Fur material that whispered-to-scream slide is exactly Moreno's signature.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"indifference pose crumbling to nausea inseparable from wanting","built":"every verse turns nastier, exposing the aloof front as the mask"},
"deftones~lucky-you": {"haiku":"A person pleads for rescue from someone they admire, asking them to carry them away; the plea for escape is framed around luck and emotional reciprocity.","opus":"A codependent cry for rescue, the narrator waiting to be carried off by someone if only they feel worthy or wanted. The disquieting notion of that beloved willing to kill for his affection tangles salvation with harm, framing deliverance as coming from a hand that both saves and wounds.","sonnet":"A passive, waiting narrator calls out to someone who has fought through hell for them, asking that person to come and take them home — the song is about longing for rescue by someone who has already proven their willingness to endure damage for love.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song of waiting that curdles into surrender: the speaker begs some arriving figure to carry him off, yet rescue is granted only on a run of conditionals — he'll be saved solely if fortune and affection happen to settle on the other person that day. The band builds the whole thing on repetition, stacking the plea until the promise darkens, because the beloved cast as deliverer is the same one he watches inflict harm, tender and murderous in one breath. What Chino Moreno keeps circling is that mercy and wound issue from a single source, so the summons to be flown toward becomes impossible to tell apart from a summons to be finished off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"mercy and wound arriving from the same single source","built":"salvation conditioned on fortune settling on its grantor that day"},
"deftones~mascara":{"haiku":"A speaker is attracted to someone despite their flaws and the aftermath of violence or injury between them, describing long shady eyes and bound commitment despite animosity.","opus":"Deftones sketch a jealous, damaged closeness, someone still marked from a fight while the narrator fixates on a woman's dark, half-lidded gaze and sneers at her ink and slender arms. The gut-punch comes at the end, when he lets slip that she is his wife. Moreno's murmured lust and contempt braid together, the band's signature knack for letting affection and threat breathe as one.","sonnet":"Contradictory affection for a damaged person: the narrator catalogs flaws with almost brutal frankness — weak wrists, unwanted tattoos, evidence of violence — yet chooses to keep her anyway. Marriage here is ambivalence institutionalized, held together by something inexplicable and possibly dark.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song built entirely out of damage: the tender wish to sink into someone arrives braided with blood still in the hair and a bruise counted as the year's worst, so devotion and injury are never separated. The chorus fixates on her shade, a word doing double work as both shadow and mood, and Moreno's narrator confesses he is all about that darkness, mistaking dread for allure even as he catalogues what he hates about her. The outro then springs the trap, repeating that it's too bad until the final line lands as a sentence rather than a vow, marriage delivered as the thing that binds two people who can neither stand nor leave each other.","fv":2,"fnote":"Chino has called it his favorite on the record and said it’s about his wife — which turns the outro’s repeated verdict into something he was singing about his own marriage; Robert Smith of The Cure was once slated to sing it with them.","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"devotion and injury braided so tightly they cannot separate","built":"the outro delivers marriage as verdict not vow"},
"deftones~mein": {"haiku":"Psychological and cosmic separation; the singer both looks outward and inward but never crosses thresholds, remaining apart. Universal forces pull toward dissolution and distance.","opus":"A paradox of longing and avoidance: the narrator wants to pull someone into himself yet never ventures out or in, keeping himself always at a distance. The universe, meanwhile, keeps breaking them down.","sonnet":"A person oscillates between looking inward and outward but never actually moves—never wanders in or out—remaining at a remove from another person they want to reach. The refrain of staying away despite the pull, and the universe breaking them apart, describes a relationship held apart by some force neither can overcome.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built entirely from a refusal that never resolves. The two verses are mirror images — one probes the outside world, one the inside self — but both dead-end on the same admission: the speaker looks toward connection and then never crosses the threshold, and the chorus reveals why, converting that failure into strategy, since staying at the perimeter is the surest way to keep the other person at arm's length. The intimacy fantasy of pulling someone in and intercepting them mid-space is thus already sabotaged by the will to withdraw; desire and avoidance are the same gesture here. Serj Tankian's bridge and outro widen the frame from personal cowardice to cosmic verdict — the universe itself grinding the pair apart — so the private choice to stay away gets absorbed into an impersonal entropy, as if the speaker's isolation were less a decision than the shape of a physics he can only narrate.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"desire and avoidance collapsing into one unreachable gesture","built":"verse mirrors dead-end the same admission from opposite directions","fnote":"The feature was an accident — Moreno sent Tankian the unfinished track for ideas, and it came back the next day with vocals all over it; the band kept them."},
"deftones~milk-of-the-madonna": {"haiku":"Divine fire ignites the body as visions escalate from earthly turbulence to spiritual ecstasy through elemental transformation.","opus":"Ecstatic religious imagery of fire, flood and holy spirit turned physical and intoxicating, the divine experienced as burning and being consumed. Deftones fuse spiritual rapture with sensory overload until faith feels like combustion.","sonnet":"Pentecostal imagery — blood rain, holy ghost, tongues of fire — builds into a state of spiritual overwhelm and intoxication, the speaker consumed and ignited by a force they can only describe as sacred and burning.","src":"haiku","fable":"Chino Moreno builds the whole song out of Pentecost — the descending wind, the flame that alights on the faithful, the drunk-at-morning accusation from Acts 2 — but pins it under a title grafting the Virgin's nursing milk onto that day of terror, so a sacrament of feeding and a sacrament of being consumed become one event. The opening verses arrive as disaster footage, a red downpour drowning a city and houses torn loose from their foundations, and the song lets that catastrophe register dissolve into ecstasy as the refrain of holy possession and combustion takes over. By the end there is no image left, only the burning admission looping against a whispered urging behind it, meaning worn down to a body caught alight repeating that it is caught alight — the song enacting its ravishment rather than describing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness","nature & the elements"],"means":"ravishment enacted rather than described, worn down to a body repeating it is alight","built":"the title grafts the Virgin's nursing milk onto Pentecost, fusing sacrament of feeding with sacrament of being consumed"},
"deftones~minerva": {"haiku":"A singer's melancholic message about finality causes emotional numbness in the listener, yet the song simultaneously breaks and heals hearts.","opus":"Named for the goddess of wisdom, this is Chino's hymn to the strange numbing power of a voice, the way a song that says it's over can bring you to your knees. Awe and heartbreak fuse: blessing the singer for the beauty and the wreckage she leaves each time she sings.","sonnet":"A devotional addressed to a woman whose singing voice undoes the narrator completely — she breaks hearts every time she opens her mouth, and he's asking God to bless her for the transcendent damage she causes.","src":"haiku","fable":"Chino Moreno anchors this to a deadened sensation he can only name by its blankness, the anesthetic hush that lands when a woman announces something has ended, and he keeps circling that flat void until it drops him bodily toward the ground. The writing's move is a slippage of who is being addressed: what opens as a lover's cold finality gets rerouted upward into a benediction, so the same voice that closes a relationship becomes a voice worth blessing, gratitude offered for the exact act of being wounded. That inversion is the engine — private ache widened into a cracked, cathedral-scaled prayer thanking its source for the injury — and it fits the record's shift toward atmospheric, reverberant space over the band's older bite, the whole thing dissolving finally into a blessing cast out across the planet rather than at any one person.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","faith & the occult","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"gratitude offered for the exact wound that caused the numbness","built":"lover's cold finality rerouted upward into a planetary benediction"},
"deftones~minus-blindfold":{"haiku":"Betrayal and manipulation lead to heartlessness and anger, where the speaker finally breaks free from those controlling them.","opus":"A visceral snapshot of teenage escape and menace, a kid grabbing his backpack, Walkman, and bicycle to flee a hot-tempered father and meet friends, the loose freedom curdling into something darker with reckless driving and betrayal. Deftones, the Sacramento band, cut this on their debut Adrenaline, Chino Moreno's fractured lines and the raw riffing capturing the volatile edge of adolescence before the band's later atmosphere set in.","sonnet":"A teenager's afternoon spun loose and restless — backpack, Walkman, bicycle, friends at the door, the small freedoms before adult weight arrives. But the mood darkens as loyalty is betrayed, a near-miss accident shakes something loose, and a current of heartlessness and helpless anger runs under the surface of what started as ordinary freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~moana": {"haiku":"A woman's powerful presence absorbs the observer's world when she enters; fascination and displacement through beauty and empire.","opus":"Moreno watches a woman own a stage until her whole world becomes another person's fixation, unshaken as she comes and goes. Spellbound, the narrator keeps pleading to be let into what she really is. The band's late-era atmospheric heaviness suits this portrait of magnetism and secondhand longing colonizing someone's desire from afar.","sonnet":"Fascination at a performance: a woman commands a stage and her presence reshapes everyone watching, creating a private empire in each observer. The narrator is spellbound, driven by the need to understand what she is like beneath the surface of that captivating display.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study in erotic spectatorship and the way desire hollows out the self. The woman on the stage is a performer whose art doesn't just seduce but colonizes — her empire becomes the watcher's taste, then his place, until finally, once she's offstage, she is the only face he can recall. What the song tracks across its three verses is a slow dispossession: taste, then place, then face, each chorus circling back to the same helpless demand to be told what she is like, as if knowing her could reverse the theft of himself. Chino Moreno's lush, worshipful murmur makes the surrender sound like ecstasy rather than loss, which is the trap — the truth underneath is that adoration this total leaves nothing of the adorer standing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"adoration so total it erases the adorer entirely","built":"each verse stages a new dispossession — taste then place then face — before the same helpless chorus"},
"deftones~mx":{"haiku":"A man is attracted to a woman's beauty but grows bored, seeking violence and short-term pleasure over genuine connection.","opus":"A sickly, coaxing seduction that keeps tipping into brutality, the narrator lavishing praise on a lover's body and taste before muttering about a month of cruelty and the itch to hurl her from a height. Wanting and menace share every line. Deftones built their early records on exactly this uneasy intimacy, Moreno's crooned threats making the sweetness feel like bait.","sonnet":"Desire and boredom folded into each other — attracted to someone, aroused by them, and simultaneously exhausted by having to keep proving the feeling. The dialogue structure, negotiating what's on offer in exchange for what, gives the song a transactional coldness that undercuts whatever heat the attraction generates, ending somewhere between tenderness and threat.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction that curdles into predation, sung in Chino Moreno's most disarmingly gentle register so the menace arrives smiling. The song runs as a duet-ledger: a second voice keeps pricing the affair out loud, tallying wants like a shopping list, and each item is answered by a colder counter-offer of dark hours priced in bruises and sweetened just enough to pass for devotion. That accounting is the whole cruelty, the beloved shrunk to a bored transaction the speaker is exhausted by performing. So the refrain finishes the arithmetic it has been threatening, the giddy beckoning and an impossibly intimate lean toward the ribs collapsing into a hand at the balcony's edge, caress and disposal breathed together. The mask is a love song; beneath it, harm is treated as its own payoff, the entitlement that imagines a person can be worshipped and discarded in a single motion.","fv":2,"fnote":"The pricing voice is Annalynn Cunningham, the drummer’s wife, recruited in the studio for the sinister role — Chino said the whole song is about greed, the take-take-take he saw in the women around him.","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"harm framed as its own payoff beneath devotion","built":"the duet-ledger prices the affair before disposal"},
"deftones~my-mind-is-a-mountain": {"haiku":"Cycles of psychological turbulence repeat as storm and stasis alternate, consciousness navigating through mental terrain. Survival requires both movement and patience through recurring collapse.","opus":"A drenched, dissociative trance of waiting and mental stillness, riding out an inner storm that implodes and explores fate, the mind held calm and entrenched as reality trips out.","sonnet":"A mind mapping itself as weather and geography: storms implode around it, rivers rise, negative space cycles through and destroys the mental landscape — the speaker bathes in this psyche, exploring fate, plotting time, never still, reaching for something beyond the endless spiral.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song holds itself in a strange stalled patience: a body waiting out weather it can't name, drenched and locked in place while some larger process it keeps calling fate arrives from above like rain. Its two image-families work against each other — the meteorological one of storm and flooding and the clinical-surveillance one of radar sweeps, cycles, a psyche watched and kept calm — so that inner life gets scanned and mapped at the same instant it is drowning. The title is the key refusal: a mind that is a mountain doesn't think so much as endure, immovable while the flood rises against it, and the flat trance-like tag that punctures each verse reads less as release than as the numb dissociation of someone riding out a condition they can neither leave nor understand. Deftones have built two decades of exactly this atmosphere around Chino Moreno's narcotic dread half-sung over crushing weight, and here the writing lets the turn toward sailing into the weather feel almost willed — a surrender staged as a small dark triumph. What resists reading resists on purpose: the words stay entrenched, patient, constant, naming a paralysis rather than breaking it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","nature & the elements","surveillance & control"],"means":"paralysis endured as immovable patience while the flood rises","built":"meteorological and clinical-surveillance imagery war inside the same verse, neither winning"},
"deftones~my-own-summer":{"haiku":"Withdrawal into private refuge offers escape from harsh external reality; the sun becomes enemy.","opus":"A retreat into shade and shadow, wishing the sun and the crowds shoved aside so the speaker can inhabit his own bleak private summer. Light itself becomes unbearable, isolation the only relief.","sonnet":"A person who despises heat and brightness, wanting the sun shoved aside and replaced with clouds and shade — the imagery suggests misanthropy and a preference for isolation, creating their own private summer of emptiness and absence rather than the crowded, sunlit world.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~my-own-summer-shove-it":{"haiku":"Rejection of overwhelming external demands and desire for personal solitude and shade.","opus":"A song about withdrawing from the world into a private, sunless 'summer,' craving shade and shelter because the light burns, and wanting to shove the sun aside to be alone with an empty street and an indifferent God.","sonnet":"The speaker constructs a private interior summer defined by absence of crowds and light, craving shade as salvation while imploring the sun to be pushed aside — an inward withdrawal from a world that is too bright, too exposed, and too much to face.","src":"haiku","fable":"Chino Moreno inverts the whole grammar of the seasonal anthem: instead of chasing daylight he petitions whatever burns overhead to switch itself off, begging that distant, indifferent glare to announce when the heat is finally over so he can crawl back under a lid of grey. The verses treat the retreat into darkness as equipment, protection, and deliverance all at once, because to a body that flinches from brightness an awning is survival gear rather than mere shelter. What makes the track curl inward instead of raging outward is the refrain, a clenched three-beat shove hurled again and again at the sky until the syllables stop meaning anything and become pure muscular refusal. The closing image seals the anti-idyll, a private overcast where the pavement has emptied of people and warmth alike, and the chorus lets one eerie flash of reverence into it, a divine tongue stirring somewhere in the hush, so the world he has emptied reads as consecrated rather than dead — a paradise defined entirely by everything it keeps out.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","nature & the elements","faith & the occult"],"means":"retreat into darkness claimed as equipment and deliverance, not defeat","built":"one eerie flash of reverence consecrates the emptied world rather than mourning it"},
"deftones~needles-and-pins": {"haiku":"Two people isolated from the world make a binding pact to be together, forming an intimate bond that shuts out all external judgment and interference.","opus":"Chino Moreno turns a couple into a sealed conspiracy against the world, intimacy sharpened to something almost sacramental and defiant, where love becomes a private pact that dares everyone outside it to interfere.","sonnet":"About the electric, dangerous intimacy between two people who are perfectly, destructively matched — pins and needles — the narrator challenges the other to make a pact of mutual exclusivity, a private alliance against the world, framed with the aggression of something that could wound as easily as it binds.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song built as a dare, where the tenderness keeps sharpening into a threat. The title pairing sets the terms: two things that only work by piercing, so intimacy here is agreed-upon injury, a game where being close means letting the other in past the skin. The verses run the seduction — the mock-worshipful flattery, the plea to tune out the crowd until only the two of them exist — and then swap devotion for fortress, sealing the pact with a cross drawn between the lovers and everyone else. The outro drops the romance entirely and repeats its taunt at the world, so the song resolves not into union but into siege: the couple as a closed, armed unit, love perfected into a challenge no one is allowed to answer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","anger & defiance"],"means":"intimacy perfected into a siege no outsider can answer","built":"the title pairs two things that only work by piercing"},
"deftones~nosebleed":{"haiku":"An unwelcome presence invades and corrupts, spreading like an infection despite efforts to resist.","opus":"A confrontation full of contempt and refusal, telling someone worthless who throws fits that they won't get through until they cover the singer, met with a chant of come-and-disappear defiance. It's aggression aimed at a liar, the vulnerability buried under the snarl. Deftones, the Sacramento alt-metal band, cut this in their early raw period, Chino Moreno's alternating croon and scream already hinting at the dynamic push-pull that defined them.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who keeps lying and demanding — the speaker is done being covered and done being manipulated, erupting into refusal. There's a paradox buried in the chorus: covered is both the threat and the condition for letting anyone get close. By the end the demand is to simply disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~ohms":{"haiku":"A shared promise and commitment between souls persists eternally despite time's passage and life's chaos.","opus":"A meditation on regret and time passing amid the ruins of the past, the Sacramento alt-metal band pairing Chino Moreno's yearning vocal with crushing yet luminous guitar. Sinking into remorse, the narrator clings to a promise that something between two people will endure and that they will find each other again.","sonnet":"Two people marooned in the debris of the past, making a promise to each other that time itself won't change. The sea of regret is already there, the tides already turned — but the commitment, made somewhere in a haunted maze of memory, holds. They'll remain. They promised.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~one-weak":{"haiku":"Confrontational and cryptic lyrics depicting internal struggle, possible mental illness or spiritual torment.","opus":"An early Deftones cut thick with imagery of fire, unborn things, and a desire to face Christ in solitude, its refrain vowing the addressee will never locate the narrator or witness his destruction, their wounds laid bare. Contempt tangles with religious dread. Chino Moreno's abstract venom keeps the meaning half-veiled while the seething refusal reads plain.","sonnet":"A dark, fractured confrontation with spiritual isolation — someone who wanted to meet some absolute alone, and a speaker who refuses to be found or burned. The hostility is layered: warnings not to cross, scars exposed, a repeated challenge to try and reach something that will not be reached.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~passenger":{"haiku":"A passive figure lies willing in a car while another drives; they surrender to sensation and exposure without caring who witnesses their intimate vulnerability.","opus":"A sensual, submissive fantasy of surrendering as a passenger, being driven faster through cool night air while exposed to the world's gaze; the leather, buckles and cushioned restraints hint at an eroticized loss of control and trust, calm found in giving oneself over to another's driving.","sonnet":"About erotic submission in a moving car — the narrator lies still and breathless while being driven, windows down, cool air rushing in, asking the driver to go faster and not stop, the whole world able to look in while the passenger surrenders completely to the ride.","src":"haiku","fable":"It stages surrender to another person's control as the only route to feeling anything, splitting one consciousness across two voices. Moreno lies inert and craving in the seat beside the driver, all sensation and no agency, reciting the car's metal and hide like a body that has handed over its own steering. Keenan answers as the exposed, exhibitionist self who wants the glass opened and strangers watching, indifferent to shame. The pleading to accelerate and never stop reads as an erotics of relinquished will, where being driven, seen, and kept moving stands in for a self that cannot act on its own. The duet is less two characters than a mind bargaining with its own passivity.","fv":2,"fnote":"Keenan was there because an executive-producer plan had dissolved into visits — by his own account he showed up with Tibetan singing bowls, percussion and champagne and asked permission to run experiments — and the duet was cut in the final vocal sessions, the two trading lines side by side with no subject agreed in advance.","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"surrender to another's control framed as the only route to feeling","built":"duet voices split one consciousness rather than portray two characters"},
"deftones~passenger-mike-shinoda-remix":{"haiku":"Detached surrender defines intimacy as the passenger yields entirely to another's direction, curiosity and vulnerability merging in a transcendent escape from self-awareness.","opus":"Erotic surrender as the passenger in someone else's car, ceding all control and craving more speed, the drive itself a metaphor for giving oneself over to another's desire.","sonnet":"A passenger gives themselves entirely to the night drive, breathless and passive, rolling the windows down to let the whole world look in and urging the driver to go faster rather than pull over.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~phantom-bride":{"haiku":"A person describes the numbing prison of emotional detachment they've chosen, spending their entire life isolated in a void to avoid pain and confusion at the cost of draining their humanity.","opus":"Addressed to someone who has walled themselves off from feeling, hiding in a self-made void attached to poison so they can never be hurt or confused again. The tragic read is that this emotional self-imprisonment isn't safety but a slow draining away of the self, a life wasted in numbness.","sonnet":"About someone who has sealed themselves off from emotion after being hurt — hiding in a void, choosing numbness over risk, rotting in the same cold space indefinitely. The narrator watches them drain themselves dry through their own self-protective paralysis.","src":"haiku","fable":"The second-person address is the whole trick here: every line is an indictment aimed at a you who has chosen numbness as armor, sealing off feeling so as never to be harmed or confused again, and the song watches that choice curdle from refuge into tomb. The movement is the argument. Chino Moreno's verses diagnose the retreat as active self-burial, then the chorus keeps circling the same void with one lethal edit — trapped becomes relaxed, staying becomes draining away — so that the safety the subject bought reveals itself as slow disappearance rather than protection. Set against Gore's lush, almost tender guitar shimmer, the accusation reads less as attack than as grief, and the title finally lands the point: the phantom bride is what survives inside that airless space, a person married to their own absence, present only as the ghost of someone who once could be wounded.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["dissociation & numbness","shame & repression","death & grief"],"means":"the chosen armor of numbness revealed as slow disappearance rather than protection","built":"the chorus loops the same void with one lethal edit each pass, trapped becoming relaxed, staying becoming draining away"},
"deftones~pink-cellphone": {"haiku":"A critique of blind faith and delusion where people follow corrupt ideologies and false leaders unknowingly.","opus":"A disorienting collage that begins as hypnotic incantation about one true power and dissolves into a sampled, obscene spoken monologue mocking faith, cures and false comfort. It is provocation more than confession, sacred language detonated by profane comedy. Deftones, the Sacramento band prized for their textural risk, let this track drift from trance into transgression, one of their most deliberately unsettling and least song-shaped experiments.","src":"haiku","fable":"Chino Moreno's half is deliberately starved: a mantra of surrender to a single sanctioned authority, then a few looping commands to shut your eyes and let a machine capture whatever spills out, the sung language kept so thin it reads as a frame rather than a statement. The real event is the handoff to Annie Hardy, whose spoken passage first dismantles that devotion as a con — the promised cure that soothes symptoms while leaving the broken machinery of belief intact — before her closing monologue detonates the whole thing into gleeful, scatological blasphemy mocking scripture, nationhood, and prudery in one breath. Meaning lives in that seam: the male voice builds a hushed devotional shell, and the female voice steps in to desecrate it, so the track works less as a song than as a bait-and-switch about how easily reverence and filth change places.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The sermon and the filth both belong to Annie Hardy of Giant Drag — and a clean digital edit later sheared her closing monologue off entirely, cutting the track by some ninety seconds.","themes":["faith & the occult","artifice & performance","surveillance & control"],"means":"reverence built then desecrated by the voice that follows","built":"male devotional shell handed to female voice for detonation"},
"deftones~pink-maggit":{"haiku":"Possessive desire turns sexual and violent, describing ownership and dominance while contemplating the other person as merely physical meat.","opus":"Deftones stretch menace across slow-building dynamics, mixing violent threat with adolescent bitterness about who leads and who gets crushed back in school. Chino Moreno's sneering reduction of a rival to nothing but meat carries the sting of hierarchy and humiliation. The track later mutated into the hit anthem Back to School, but here it broods, hostile and unresolved.","sonnet":"A volatile attachment charged with jealousy and self-erasure — a narrator consumed by wanting someone to the point of threatening harm while slipping away from any stable sense of self. The social landscape of school-era hierarchies bleeds into the obsession, reducing everything to raw possession.","src":"haiku","fable":"Closing White Pony, this is Deftones dramatizing the schoolyard pecking order from inside the predator's mouth, the speaker crooning threats of suffocation and fire at a victim while the chorus reframes the whole scene as adolescent hierarchy: the ones who run the halls issue their one ultimatum, assimilate into the cruelty or be erased. The imagery runs on a single escalating system, dominance rendered as physical violation, that finally collapses in the outro's flat verdict, everyone reduced to meat. That last word is the payload, stripping both aggressor and prey of any human standing and exposing the entire ritual of intimidation as something animal rather than personal, cruelty without even the dignity of a reason.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"adolescent cruelty stripped to its animal logic","built":"the flat verdict reduces aggressor and prey to meat"},
"deftones~pittura-infamante":{"haiku":"A devotee kneels in worship, praising and assimilating truth from an idealized figure as they climb to bathe in transcendent sunlight.","opus":"Chino Moreno turns devotion into something sacred and consuming, worship framed as pilgrimage and communion, a love so total it becomes religion, kneeling and singing praises to a person raised to the level of a god.","sonnet":"A song of ecstatic, almost religious devotion to someone who feels like a divine gift — the narrator kneels, climbs, drinks, tastes, and waits a thousand years, singing praise to someone who has transformed his whole existence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pittura infamante was the Renaissance city's shaming device: a debtor or traitor painted in effigy on a public wall, damned permanently in his absence. That fact reframes this worship-hymn, because each devotional gesture — the kneeling, the carved name, the vow to remain — doubles as pinning a likeness up for judgment. Every praise offered becomes a brushstroke fixing the beloved in place, and when singing tips into screaming, reverence curdles toward accusation. What sounds like a man lifting his idol skyward is a portraitist finishing a face he can never scrape off the plaster, canonizing and convicting in the same breath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["faith & the occult","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"devotion and public shaming conducted simultaneously in the same gestures","built":"the Renaissance defamation portrait reframes every praise as a brushstroke fixing the beloved to a wall for judgment"},
"deftones~poltergeist":{"haiku":"A toxic relationship where one partner plays games to provoke the other, creating obsessive tension and control.","opus":"A song about a poisonous relationship game the singer is exhausted by yet cannot walk away from, watching the other feign innocence to provoke him. He confesses a love as total as their attachment to the game itself, and something in the dynamic keeps him gripped despite knowing better.","sonnet":"The narrator is worn down by a partner who plays games and feigns ignorance, yet despite the frustration and exhaustion he admits he loves this person obsessively — the push and pull of it keeps him hanging on.","src":"haiku","fable":"A relationship rendered as a rigged game, sung by someone who has caught his partner playing dumb on purpose and is furious that he still can't stop watching. The title is the tell: a poltergeist is a disturbance that keeps announcing itself, and Chino Moreno's speaker is being deliberately baited into reaction, sick of it and yet feeding on it. What makes the song curdle is the fusion in the chorus, where loving someone to death sits flush against loving the game itself, so devotion and manipulation become the same reflex. The build seals it: as the accusation sharpens across verses, the refrain that recurs after each chorus reframes the provocation as proof of life, the friction he claims to hate reading to him as the one signal that anything between them is still moving. Enough is never actually enough, because being driven wild is the point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the friction he claims to hate is the proof anything is still moving","built":"the refrain reframes each provocation as evidence of life"},
"deftones~prayers-triangles":{"haiku":"Internal conflict between divine and demonic forces that render someone eternally imprisoned and spiritually enslaved.","opus":"An internal battle with a profane presence stirring to life inside the speaker, who feels enslaved to forces quietly decaying him from within. The refrain condemns someone to permanent captivity, threaded with imagery of self-mutilation, the whole thing pitched as spiritual entrapment.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped between an inner demonic force and the angels he tries to keep finds that prayers and mental constructs offer no release — the repeated refrain \"you will never be free\" describes a state of spiritual and psychological captivity with no visible exit.","src":"haiku","fable":"This opener stages a spiritual collapse as haunting: a faithless spirit and a too-holy one have both moved into one sleeping self, so the fight becomes rival invaders rather than good against evil. Moreno's real move is the chorus, which pins soft devotion against a cold geometric figure and hands both the identical verdict, looped like a lock turning until neither pleading nor imposed shape can undo it. Belief has stiffened from feeling into diagram, so no amount of prayer buys an exit. What remains is one frantic rite: a chest torn open and lifted skyward, an offering that sours into a threat. Read plainly, the song concerns worship that no longer rescues, its outward reverence masking fixed patterns that loop the same doom long after belief itself has rotted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"worship that no longer rescues, its outward reverence masking patterns that loop the same doom","built":"the chorus pins soft devotion against a cold geometric figure and hands both the identical verdict, looped like a lock turning"},
"deftones~prince": {"haiku":"A performer with a killer's mindset bleeds and tears on stage, ending a cycle of devotion and performance, then disappears knowing they've left their gift to the world.","opus":"A darkly glamorous portrait of a performer with a killer's mindset, painting the room red as a gift to a world that neither knows nor cares. Deftones turning the stage into a beautiful, doomed offering.","sonnet":"About performance, devotion, and self-annihilation — a figure with a killer's mindset who commands the stage and paints the room in tears and blood, ultimately offering their gift to the world as a kind of final exit, graceful and fatal.","src":"haiku","fable":"Chino Moreno addresses a mirror-double here, someone whose predatory focus he half-admires and half-recognizes in himself, so the song reads as a portrait of the performer caught inside the same appetite he's naming. The imagery keeps collapsing spectacle into slaughter: a stare that washes the stage crimson, weeping standing in for spilled blood, applause and killing folding together until the pouring source can't be told from the act itself. Then the climb toward escape reverses into surrender, the final turn handing a private creation to an indifferent world with a shrug that lands as either peace or defeat, before everything dissolves into farewells and a curtain dropping like an execution.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"naming another's appetite as a way of recognising one's own","built":"the closing curtain falls like an execution, peace and defeat undistinguished"},
"deftones~rapture": {"haiku":"Two people apply different tools and approaches to intimacy and pain, cycling through repetition and waste, unable to choose a way forward.","opus":"A song about being torn between two similar but distinct forces or choices that both cut into the narrator, tangled up with an untrustworthy partner whose broken promises to god only waste his time.","sonnet":"A relationship built on repeated broken promises: the speaker describes two competing forces — one nurturing, one destructive — while addressing someone who keeps swearing they won't hurt them again and then wastes their time anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"A relationship rendered as manufacturing: two people cut from identical material yet stitched by separate tools, each one carving the other out, until the demand to pick between them collapses into a question with no answer. The title word promises deliverance and delivers compulsion instead, the offered pleasure sliding straight into something that kills, and Chino Moreno sings it in the drugged, seductive croon that gives Saturday Night Wrist its queasy intimacy. What lands hardest is the returning oath of never-again, sworn to God with a liar's fervor and looped back verbatim, until the flat counter-verdict that all of it merely wastes his time becomes the only honest line left standing. The reprise of the opening reseals the whole thing, same material worked by a fresh instrument, enacting the begin-again loop it keeps describing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"pleasure named deliverance while delivering destruction","built":"the reprise reseals the same loop the song describes"},
"deftones~rats-rats-rats": {"haiku":"Disappointment and disillusionment after achieving what was desired. Nothing lives up to expectation, and the speaker contemplates whether anything was ever worth the effort.","opus":"Deftones circle a corrosive question, whether the thing you dreamed of matched the getting of it, needling at hollow satisfaction. Chino Moreno's push-pull between saying nothing and wanting to say something enacts the paralysis of disappointment, the repeated insistence that everything is fine curdling into a shouted accusation of lying. Desire fulfilled turns out to be its own trap.","sonnet":"Disillusionment after getting what was wanted. Something pursued with desire finally arrives, but its taste does not match the dream. A tension between speaking and staying silent builds until the forced performance of contentment cracks into an accusation directed inward.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is built almost entirely from interrogation: a clipped, repeated demand that someone choose, paired with a needling checklist asking whether the thing they wanted matched the dream once they finally held it. Two impulses war inside the same voice — one that wants to withhold speech entirely, and one straining to force out a single utterance it never actually manages — and Chino Moreno lets neither win, so the failure to speak becomes the real content. The turn arrives when the second pass swaps wanting a thing for wanting to take one, then tallies whether a single act was worth it, tilting the whole address toward menace. When the pressed reassurances of contentment finally stack into a mechanical chant, the last word snaps back as an accusation, exposing every prior comfort as a mask over the thing that stayed unsaid.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"the failure to speak becomes the real content of the demand","built":"closing comfort chanted mechanical then snapped back as accusation","fnote":"Moreno took the title from a story he was reading about the actress Frances Farmer and her breakdown — only realizing afterward that Nirvana had written her a song of their own."},
"deftones~rickets":{"haiku":"The narrator catalogs their own excesses and flaws (thinking too much, eating too much, drinking too much) while being told they are probably right—but they do not care to listen.","opus":"Deftones turn a litany of self-accusation into rhythm, the speaker cataloguing every excess: thinking, drinking, sleeping, needing, all too much. Someone is probably right about him, but he refuses to listen or care. Typical of Chino Moreno's early sneering delivery, defiance and self-loathing fuse, and when it comes his turn to speak he chooses silence over the words he might otherwise say.","sonnet":"A person catalogues every excessive habit and flaw they carry while dismissing another party's corrections with studied indifference. Underneath the defiance is an awareness that the criticism is probably right — yet the will to change, or even to speak, has quietly evacuated.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built almost entirely as a litany of self-accusation: verse after verse of clipped I-do-too-much confessions, each one a small deficiency itemized until the whole self is nothing but excess and lack at once. The chorus is the trap sprung shut, conceding the other person is right while refusing in the same breath to listen or care, so the admission of fault becomes another act of defiance rather than a step toward change. What deepens across the structure is the final turn to silence, where the speaker decides that even if he had the right to speak he would withhold it, revealing the whole cascade of self-blame as a defensive performance, a way of pre-condemning himself so thoroughly that no outside criticism can land. The title itself, a deficiency disease of the malnourished and undeveloped, names the state without ever appearing in the words: a self stunted by something it was never given, cataloguing its own crookedness as if naming the damage could pass for growing out of it.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"self-blame performed so thoroughly no outside criticism can land","built":"the title names the deficiency disease without appearing once in the words"},
"deftones~risk": {"haiku":"One person offers to save another from anxiety and being locked up, promising to take them higher. They will try despite confusion, offering lift and breath.","opus":"The band stages an anxious rescue, the speaker jittery yet swearing he will reach someone shut inside themselves. He owns his own confusion but keeps pledging to try, coaxing the other to soften and trust him. Moreno's yearning croon lifts the vow toward a euphoric peak, devotion racing ahead of the certainty he lacks.","sonnet":"A speaker at the edge of their own confusion reaches toward someone locked inside themselves, pledging to find a way in even without a roadmap. The emotional architecture is one of desperate tenderness — an insistence on trying to elevate the other when no one else can or will.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a rescue attempt narrated from the doorway of someone who has barricaded themselves in, and Chino Moreno pitches it as anxious coaxing rather than a grand promise — the speaker admits confusion in the same breath he vows to pull the other person back from harm. The verses reach toward a shut-in figure who unlocks and slips away, asking them to lower their guard, unfold their crossed arms, and admit a stranger to the wreckage. What the title names as danger sits entirely on the rescuer's side: by the third verse his language of deliverance slides into the vocabulary of getting someone high, so the salvation offered starts to look like another dependency dressed as a cure. The vow to lift them is hedged three times over with a stammering pledge to merely attempt it, and the outro wears that promise down to two helpless syllables — the whole emotional weight resting on effort that may not be equal to the crisis it answers.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"the offer of salvation slides into the language of another dependency","built":"the vow wears down to two helpless syllables in the outro"},
"deftones~rivi-re": {"haiku":"Haunting presence demands resolution the speaker cannot provide, each encounter deepening entanglement while flight offers temporary escape but never freedom.","opus":"A haunted, gothic vow built around a wounded female figure who can't rest until she's faced, and a narrator promising devotion through violence and self-sacrifice. Love rendered as a vigil that keeps him awake and unravelling.","sonnet":"A spirit or woman haunts a road, wounded and waiting for release, while the speaker promises to cut down whatever stands between them and to wait indefinitely. The imagery is half-supernatural, half-devotional, and the final collapse — fly away, break down — suggests sacrifice as the only form of reunion.","src":"haiku","fable":"A wounded, spectral woman lingers unable to lift herself, and the singer answers her stalled waiting with a lover's vow of ruin — Chino Moreno promising to level whatever stands in the way and remake the one he wants. The song's real meaning is the collapse of that bravado: conquest and menace drain into the smallest possible devotion, staying awake, then a repeated single syllable of descent that outlasts every grand promise. This is Deftones at their most narcotic and unresolved, a Saturday Night Wrist mood-piece where the impulse to rescue and the failure to manage it turn out to be one long held breath that never exhales.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"grand rescue vows draining into a single syllable of descent","built":"bravado collapses to the smallest gesture the song never exhales"},
"deftones~rocket-skates": {"haiku":"A sensual invitation to abandon control and surrender to intoxication and danger alongside a lover in an intimate, transgressive space.","opus":"The band fuse desire and violence into a single dangerous seduction, a couple tangling and going under together while carrying weapons, sex made inseparable from menace. The chanted list of arms and its blunt dare turn intimacy into threat, a sadistic, trance-like state where wanting someone and wanting to hurt them blur completely.","sonnet":"An erotically charged, violent communion — the speaker and a blood-soaked partner are entangled in something that blurs weapons and desire, drowning together in a trance that is simultaneously dangerous and wanted.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction that keeps sliding toward violence, built on two image-families that never sit still: a partner flushed and drenched to the skin, and a fistful of edged metal offered up like party favors. Chino Moreno braids arousal and threat so tightly they stop being distinguishable, the scene half-drunk and half-drowning, hands full of both keys and blades as the couple slips into a shared, sadistic stupor. The song advances by circling rather than by story, looping the same overheated tableau until the sinister dare of the chorus reads as the only honest line in the room, an addict's provocation wearing romance as its disguise.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"desire and threat braided until they stop being distinguishable","built":"the chorus's sinister dare surfaces as the only honest line in the room"},
"deftones~romantic-dreams":{"haiku":"A hypnotized admirer watches a partner's fleeting, cyclical moods while yearning for the night to never end. The song captures obsessive desire and magical thinking in attraction.","opus":"The song centers on a captivating figure whose shifting moods the speaker studies, spellbound by them and wishing the moment would stretch on forever. He encourages them to chase their ambition right now while the spotlight belongs to them.","sonnet":"The speaker is transfixed by someone whose changing nature he watches closely, promising devotion and wishing the night they share would never end. The mood is hypnotic and intimate, built around the desire to remain suspended in a single moment of closeness.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song whose title is a trap: everything Chino Moreno frames as devotion arrives in the grammar of surveillance, the beloved cast nightly as the star of a stage that is his alone to grant. His coaxing — go on, seize what you want, the moment is now — sounds like encouragement but lands as a shove from someone who has already scripted how the evening ends. The tenderness curdles precisely where the images sour, a numb procession toward smoke and dissolving color, so the hypnotized plea for the night never to close reads less as romance sustained than as a room held shut past its natural death, love with the exits watched.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"tenderness scripted as surveillance with the exits watched","built":"the title promises romance and delivers a room held shut"},
"deftones~root":{"haiku":"Existential struggle and despair with unclear meaning, depicting poor and powerless individuals trapped in suffering.","opus":"A murky, tortured early Deftones cut soaked in religious and self-annihilating imagery, being pressed under, condemned, and dragged from an inner poverty, closing with the narrator damned for impersonating the divine. Its refrain insists no one will ever truly locate or comprehend this deranged figure. The band keep it abstract and seething across their rawest material.","sonnet":"Fragmented and half-submerged, this circles a state of surrender and being judged — giving everything and receiving nothing, poverty of self, a sense of being barred from whatever would redeem. The speaker ends up with Jesus in a bowl of dirt, declaring they will fly, though the ground holds them fast throughout.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~rosemary":{"haiku":"Two minds dissolve boundaries between waking and dreaming as they journey through abstract planes beyond normal reality. The song depicts intimate connection transcending physical space and time through shared hallucination.","opus":"Chino Moreno's drifting reverie of two minds escaping the body together, crossing empty skies into other planes. Psychedelic transcendence rendered as intimacy, dreaming and dissolving as a shared voyage.","sonnet":"A dreamy, almost psychedelic meditation on transcendence through connection — two people drifting through space and time together, their minds and bodies merging as the engines stall and they collide with something beyond the physical.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about two people willing their consciousness out of their bodies to travel together, staged as a flight that is really a shared inner departure. Deftones build it on the slow drift and dissolve of their Koi No Yokan mode, and the aircraft framing carries the whole arc: the low turbine hum opens the trip, the motor cutting out marks the peak where the two lock eyes and everything detonates, and the machinery never touches down because the destination was never a place. What sounds like a lullaby begging a lover to remain is actually a plea not to be abandoned at the threshold of that crossing, the drug or the dream that lets a mind loosen and unfurl into other planes. The recurring hitch on the word time, stuttered and warped, keeps snagging the song out of ordinary duration, so the vow to keep sailing across emptied skies past every measure of distance and hour isn't romance dressing up an escape but the escape itself asking for company.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","freedom & escape","love & desire"],"means":"asking not to be abandoned at the threshold of the crossing","built":"the word time stuttered and warped to snag the song out of ordinary duration"},
"deftones~royal":{"haiku":"Surreal seduction and spiritual dominion. A priestess is summoned to shed veils; the narrator positions themselves as remedy and team, weaponized with custom-made nightmares.","opus":"A cosmic seduction, the narrator courting some priestess-muse across space, offering himself as her team and her antidote. Deftones turn desire into science-fiction ritual, sensual and weightless, all reach and provocation.","sonnet":"The speaker addresses a commanding, priestess-like figure, inviting her to come closer, shed her veil, and lead or teach, describing the encounter as charged and almost ritualistic. The imagery of rockets, antidotes, and custom-made nightmares keeps the tone between reverence and provocation.","src":"haiku","fable":"Chino Moreno stages seduction as first contact, hailing an unreachable woman as a robed alien intelligence to be coaxed down from orbit and unmasked. The come-on runs on the band's signature slippage between reverence and appetite: the same voice that begs her to lower her veil is armed with bespoke bad dreams and teeth arranged like an accessory, so worship and predation share one mouth. What sells it as menace rather than devotion is the bargain underneath — the promise to be her cure, her teammate, the ignition that lifts her off the ground — which turns tenderness into a launch sequence where surrender and abduction become the same upward pull.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"worship and predation sharing one mouth in the same seduction","built":"the promised cure reframes tenderness as a launch sequence where surrender is abduction"},
"deftones~rubicon":{"haiku":"Encourages surrendering to collective power and losing oneself in shared experience, breaking records together. The song frames unity as transcendence and escape from isolation.","opus":"A seductive, hypnotic invitation to surrender the self to a collective power and to the spectacle of a crowd. The narrator coaxes another to give in, face the lights, and writhe in shared delight, bodies aching to be draped in pleasure.","sonnet":"A song about surrendering to collective intensity — the speaker urges someone to stop trying to face the crowd alone, to embrace the shared power of the moment, let go of inhibition, and give themselves over entirely to the experience.","src":"haiku","fable":"The seductive grammar here is a trap, and the trick is the pronoun: what sounds like one lover coaxing another into surrender is actually a collective we, the crowd and the lights, promising a body it can dissolve the terror of facing them alone. Chino Moreno has always sung the stage as an erotic threshold, and Rubicon crosses it explicitly, folding the language of consummation into the language of the show until being taken in means both being embraced and being consumed by an audience. The movement seals the reading: the verses escalate from a private invitation to the record being theirs to break and the crowd going wild, so the intimacy was always spectacle, the writhing always performance, the surrender indistinguishable from the roar that rewards it. The point of no return the title names is the moment self-possession is traded for that rush and cannot be taken back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["artifice & performance","love & desire","freedom & escape"],"means":"the promise of intimacy exposed as spectacle from the first syllable","built":"the verses escalate from private invitation to the crowd going wild, confirming the surrender was always performance"},
"deftones~rx-queen":{"haiku":"A speaker accepts their partner's harmful nature because they're deeply connected and becoming one with the virus together.","opus":"A devotion built on mutual poison, the lover a source of both intimacy and infection he willingly absorbs. Set in a druggy, cosmic imagery of viruses and stings, it's codependence as strange tenderness where being hurt is proof of belonging.","sonnet":"An obsessive, feral devotion — the narrator follows his girl through apocalyptic imagery of death and virus, willing to be stung and hurt, the relationship framed as mutual contamination and dangerous beauty.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song where the beloved is an infection and being loved means being poisoned, sung by a devotee who welcomes her bite as tenderness. His surrender only deepens across the verses: first he wishes an end onto whatever is fresh so the two can flee, then he scavenges and swallows the sickness itself to stay near her. By the last verse the contagion has reached an infant and dissolved everyone into one shared host, turning private worship into general ruin. The hypnotized outro, all watching and stinging, is the mask; beneath its numb repetition lies a plain confession that this devotion cannot be told apart from letting something consume you.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"devotion offered as willingness to absorb the beloved's poison","built":"the hypnotized outro's numb repetition masks a plain confession of being consumed"},
"deftones~sextape": {"haiku":"Underwater imagery merges with intimate sensuality as lovers surrender to pleasure and waves colliding; the pair fuses power, dreams, and desire while drifting beyond consciousness into oceanic unity.","opus":"A dreamy, oceanic rendering of sex as being swept out to sea, drifting beneath the surface in sync with shared longing while everything crashes around them. The repeated request for one final go casts the encounter as an intoxicating surrender, the narrator wanting to lose himself entirely in it.","sonnet":"A slow, sensual drift into oceanic intimacy — two people losing themselves in each other, floating beyond ordinary life into a private erotic space where nothing exists but the waves and the night.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sex song that Chino Moreno dissolves into water until the two become the same thing, arousal rendered as tide rather than touch: the lover's power and the ocean's undertow trade places until being pulled under and being pulled toward another body are one motion. The verbs stay ecstatic and submissive at once — the singer keeps asking to be taken, one more wave, one more ride, ceding agency to the swell — so the erotic charge lives entirely in that surrender, in losing your head rather than keeping it. That the crest never resolves but only repeats, the collision of waves stacked over a hammered tonight, is the point: this is the loop of desire held at its peak, a night meant to run forever and never break.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Moreno has called it deliberately fragmented — pieces of one feeling scattered under the water and in the city rather than a story — the closest the record comes to his dream-pop side project Team Sleep.","themes":["love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"arousal rendered as tide: surrender and ecstasy share one motion","built":"the crest repeats rather than resolves, desire held permanently at its peak"},
"deftones~souvenir": {"opus":"A tender, cosmic love song of two people adrift among the stars as walls turn to glass and time melts away; folding into the dark, they find one another and pledge to go on loving even apart, riding the sacred intimacy of borrowed time.","src":"opus","fable":"A love song that stages itself as spaceflight: the beloved keeps sinking back toward sleep and gravity while the speaker floats free, so intimacy gets split across two altitudes and the whole tenderness lives in trying to bridge that gap. Deftones have always paired their heaviness with a woozy shoegaze float, and here the imagery hands everything to weightlessness — bedroom walls, a bedside lamp, the pull downward all named only so they can be escaped upward into orbit. The devotion is stated as something that survives the descent, vowed from altitude even as the other person wakes back into ordinary ground. Structurally the song burns off its own language at the end, dissolving verse and vow alike into a single verb of gliding repeated until nothing else is left, so that closing refusal to say anything new becomes the point: motion sustained past the need for words is the keepsake it holds onto.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","freedom & escape"],"means":"devotion vowed across two altitudes that intimacy cannot bridge","built":"the song burns off its own language at the close, dissolving into a single repeated verb of gliding"},
"deftones~street-carp":{"haiku":"A woman with sharp/predatory teeth tears at the speaker's surroundings and being; he provides her with evidence or personal details encoded as addresses she can take and weaponize.","opus":"A brush-off to a predatory ex figure with sharp/gold teeth who claws for something to hold against him. The repeated fake addresses and taunt to write it down mock her hunt for evidence; he's daring her to take her worthless proof and do what she wants with it because none of it touches him.","sonnet":"A fractured, half-remembered exchange with a volatile and predatory woman — the singer keeps providing a new address as if inviting her to follow, while daring her to do something with the \"evidence\" of his existence.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a taunt built as a trap: every time the singer offers where he lives, the digits shift and he claims to forget, so the woman clawing at him can never actually locate him. The recited numbers are decoys, and his flat insistence that he doesn't care is the tell that he does. The chorus dares her to gather her proof and go wreck herself with it, while the bridge collapses into a jeering loop that keeps ordering her to record everything, a mock-patient did-you-get-it that mistakes cruelty for teaching. Beneath the flirtation the whole song is a bolted door, its cool detachment a defense against a hunger with teeth. On White Pony, Deftones make evasion itself the hook.","fv":2,"fnote":"The decoy numbers may hide a real one: Chino has said he snuck his actual address into the lyrics — and complained, years later, that nobody ever emailed him.","themes":["anger & defiance","surveillance & control","love & desire"],"means":"false address offered as bolted door against a hunger with teeth","built":"shifting digits perform evasion while flat indifference reveals the opposite"},
"deftones~swerve-city":{"haiku":"A woman dominates and controls through mysterious, otherworldly forces and voices. She breaks the narrator's resistance and makes him hers.","opus":"A hypnotic, dreamlike portrait of a woman who quiets and steers the narrator using eerie, faraway sounds and unseen currents that drift around them. The mood is trance-like and yielding, the man content to float in her control.","sonnet":"A brief, atmospheric song centered on the hypnotic power of a woman's voice — described as strange and distant — that tames and controls the speaker, carrying through the air like a force that keeps him floating and unable to break free.","src":"haiku","fable":"A woman breaks and tames wild horses using voices that carry on the air — and that same disembodied force loops back on the listener, keeping them howling and floating, no longer sure who is being handled. Chino Moreno writes this less as a narrative than as an incantation: the same few images (horses, breath, drifting force) recur almost unchanged because the song is enacting the hypnosis it describes, meaning dissolving into pure atmosphere the way Deftones dissolve riff into shoegaze wash on Koi No Yokan. The refusal to develop is the point; surrender feels good precisely because you stop asking what has hold of you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The album title is Japanese — koi no yokan, the premonition on first meeting that you will come to love someone. Eleven songs of surrender to forces already in motion, and the opener names the mechanism: a voice on the air that tames.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","transcendence & consciousness","surveillance & control"],"means":"surrender feels good when you stop asking who's holding you","built":"repetition without development enacts the hypnosis it describes"},
"deftones~teenager":{"haiku":"Romantic rejection leaving emotional cavities; repeated departures and distance pile loss upon loss until the speaker moves on.","opus":"One of Deftones' most delicate moments, it renders young heartbreak as a fresh cavity moving into the heart each time closeness collapses into withdrawal. The narrator declares himself through with a new version of someone who keeps changing. Its hushed, trip-hop-inflected softness on White Pony marked how far the band could stray from aggression into aching, plainspoken tenderness.","sonnet":"The grief of someone pulling away and taking a piece of the narrator with them, leaving a hollow cavity in the heart. Each withdrawal deepens the damage, and the repeated disavowal of the changed person — the new version — is the only way to handle loss that feels like replacement.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup rendered as bodily decay, a rot that settles into the chest and refuses to leave however loudly he insists he has let go. The two verses are near-identical, but the second trades her singing for his screaming while the same wound sits unhealed beneath both. That mirror is the point: rising volume, no progress, a chorus that keeps renouncing the changed version of her long past the moment anyone would believe it.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss"],"means":"renouncing her long past the moment anyone would believe it","built":"near-identical verses swap her singing for his screaming over the same wound"},
"deftones~tempest":{"haiku":"The speaker seeks to break free from repetitive cycles and stasis, desiring transformation and disassembly from the inside to reach an end or rebirth.","opus":"An abstract summons to discard the narratives implanted in you and come awake. Using imagery of paralysis, of spinning helplessly in place, the narrator craves being dismantled from within and driven through a full revolution to its conclusion, a plea for total internal renewal.","sonnet":"The song circles around a desire to be utterly dismantled and remade from the inside — caught in stasis and spinning cycles, the speaker longs to be taken apart completely and pushed through to some unreachable end.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song where being unmade is the only door out. The verses talk someone loose from the borrowed stories lodged in their head and back into their own hour, pointing them at a glory hanging just past the ceiling — yet the chorus keeps stalling in stasis, wheeling in place while an arrival that never lands jumps straight to its ending. Deftones tune ruin like rescue: the speaker wants to be opened from within and threaded back through the machine, treating that unhinging as the way a person is remade rather than wrecked. The turn is the pronoun's late slip — a private wish to come undone becomes a plea for someone else to do the undoing, so the surrender is handed off, tender and total instead of only violent.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Moreno, on the sessions: the band talked about the end of the world every day and nearly made the whole record about it, then ditched the frame as a cage — only this song made it out, directly about it. Behind it, the Mayan-calendar December of 2012.","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape","love & desire"],"means":"ruin tuned like rescue so being unmade is the only door out","built":"the pronoun slips late, handing the surrender to another"},
"deftones~the-boy-s-republic":{"haiku":"A metaphorical blue star or divine light offers healing and solace, soothing pain through its ethereal presence.","opus":"A drifting, half-abstract plea addressed to a blue star, begging its light to pour down while the narrator claims not to care, soothed and pierced in the same breath. Meaning stays submerged beneath repetition and mood. Deftones, the Sacramento band who prize atmosphere over narrative, work here in pure texture, the words functioning less as statement than as another shimmering layer of dream-logic sound.","sonnet":"An address to a blue star — a celestial source of light and healing asked to shine down indiscriminately, to pierce and soothe. The speaker expresses indifference to who receives this light while still craving what it provides; the lungs of others become a strange medium of comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~this-place-is-death": {"haiku":"Two people share an intimate dream where they erupt together in passionate chaos and blood, finding mutual arousal and connection in a place of death.","opus":"Deftones turn a fixated love into a recurring nocturnal rite, two people tearing through a shared dream, spinning a private tangle around themselves and leaving their marks in bursts of red. Moreno's swooning vocal keeps the eroticism laced with menace, the title naming the ruin hidden inside the ecstasy, both of them drawn toward a willing, mutual undoing.","sonnet":"Two figures fuse in a shared nocturnal intensity — dream-state encounters that combust into color and mutual arousal, leaving names carved into the same space. The relationship is consuming and symmetrical, each drawing the other deeper into something that feels both fatal and irresistible.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song written entirely in the plural, where two people fuse into a single detonating subject and the border between them dissolves along with the border between sleeping and waking. Chino Moreno keeps the language at the pitch Deftones perfected on this record — sensual and violent at once, so ecstasy and obliteration stop being opposites and become two names for the same act. The title lands as the confession the whole thing has been building toward: intimacy this total is figured as a kind of dying, a place entered willingly that you don't expect to leave unchanged.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","death & grief"],"means":"total intimacy figured as a willing entry into obliteration","built":"the title lands as the confession the whole song has been building toward"},
"deftones~to-have-and-to-hold":{"haiku":"A person seeks redemption and forgiveness for past indulgences, wanting cleansing and spiritual healing.","opus":"A confession of guilt and craving for absolution, the narrator diseased by his own indulgence, on his knees needing forgiveness and a witness to the good buried beneath his sin. Beneath the decay he still hopes someone with a heart of gold will hold him. Deftones frame this plea for release in their signature push and pull, self-disgust wrapped in yearning tenderness.","sonnet":"A plea for cleansing and release — the speaker has spent their time in the dirt and now wants absolution, not through punishment but through someone willing to witness what is good beneath the sin. The final image is a person with a heart of gold, someone who will take and hold the weight alongside them.","src":"haiku"},
"deftones~what-happened-to-you":{"haiku":"Two people drift together through time and space, alive elsewhere and ahead of their moment, exploring what transformed the other.","opus":"Deftones channel their atmospheric, floating melodicism into a dreamlike ode to a cherished companion, imagining a decades-long journey through wind and sky where the two exist outside ordinary time. Chino Moreno's imagery of a dying star and a graceful getaway turns devotion into something suspended between sleep and waking, a love that belongs somewhere beyond the reach of the collapsing world.","sonnet":"A long partnership suspended somewhere between dream and drift — the two have sailed together, performed and escaped together, lived ahead of their time in some shared elsewhere. Now a star is burning out, forty years of play behind them, and the sky belongs to someone while the other is left asking what happened, still floating, still asleep in that new place.","src":"haiku","fable":"One of the gentlest things in the Deftones catalog, a rarity where Chino Moreno drops the abrasion entirely and lets the band's shoegaze undertow carry a straight cosmic love song. The imagery moves from nautical to astral, two lovers as sailors under a wind-filled cape who have slipped loose of their own moment, existing in some other elsewhere and running decades past the clock everyone else keeps. That refusal to touch down in the present is the whole argument: the song frames devotion as escape velocity, a pair drifting dreamlike out of the world they were born into rather than settling into it. The vow to stay for forty years and every remaining day arrives as the one fixed coordinate, a promise held steady even as the heavens collapse overhead and a distant sun gutters and dies. The title's stranded question mark hangs over all of it, so the tenderness carries a faint dread — this may be less a couple building a life than two people who have already quit it together.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","freedom & escape","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"devotion framed as escape velocity from the world they were born into","built":"the title's stranded question mark laces tenderness with dread"},
"deftones~when-girls-telephone-boys": {"haiku":"A toxic, painful relationship involves sharp words, mutual wounding, and a desperate hope to never encounter each other again. The song dwells on something deeply wrong between the speakers.","opus":"Deftones stage a toxic pull where every fresh hurt tastes like an old familiar one and getting close means bared, sharpened fangs. Moreno wavers between a promise to phone and a wish that the two never cross paths again, the quiet plea to stay silent so a reunion can't tear the scar open, letting soft dread sit beneath a screamed hook.","sonnet":"A toxic relational history with someone whose sharpness always draws blood. After repeated injury the narrator resolves to maintain silence on any future encounter, hoping no such encounter ever comes — a wish for permanent mutual erasure dressed as a phone call never made.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is Deftones at their most abrasive and least legible, Chino Moreno hissing a kiss-off through gritted animal imagery — a mounted steed, filed-down fangs, a powder-case mirror pressed into someone's lap — that curdles a routine vow to ring later into open contempt. The song lives in its two facing refusals: a demand for total quiet, then a hope that the reunion it dreads will never actually arrive, hammered flat by sheer repetition until the closing loop stops arguing and simply confirms there is something in the other person the singer cannot abide.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"contempt so complete it hopes the reunion never arrives","built":"animal imagery curdles a routine call-later into an open aversion"},
"deftones~xenon":{"haiku":"Desire and violence pulse through human nature; lions and sirens control fate while watching bodies float astray.","opus":"Deftones render a narcotic dissolution of the self, watching a body drift free while unseen forces claim to author its fate and feed its craving for violence. The hypnotic repetition mirrors the surrender it describes.","sonnet":"A song narrated by predatory forces — lions, diamonds, sirens — that claim total dominion over a person: their desires, their violence, their fate; the subject is described as floating and cold while these controlling entities watch and wait for the final departure.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses drift over a numb, weightless host, but the chorus belongs to a plural presence that is no one human — an appetite given a mouth, claiming to have authored its host and to rule from the threshold. Deftones fuse seduction with hunting until they blur: gems set into the mind, predators and luring voices promising a rapture that is also a snare you agreed to enter. The last turn is the tell, where that many-voiced thing shrinks to one lone author and a single word repeats past meaning stay into meaning trapped, the addiction confessing in its own register.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the addiction confessing in its own register, rapture and trap indistinguishable until the last word","built":"stay repeated past meaning into trapped, the shift accomplished through pure iteration rather than any turn of phrase"},
"deftones~xerces": {"haiku":"As the universe and comets await departure from earth, the narrator says goodbye to a transformed world, uncertainty lingering after change.","opus":"Moreno's cosmic farewell, a comet-borne exit from Earth toward some safe new world, ambivalent about whether to stay or go. Deftones make death or transcendence sound like drifting off, waving goodbye with the same old craving intact.","sonnet":"Someone contemplates leaving the world — or having left it — with quiet ambivalence, unsure whether the change they made was grave or not, waving goodbye to what they know as they drift toward something new.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being summoned off the planet — a comet arriving to carry the singer somewhere else while voices call his name — and the whole thing is staged as a departure he can't quite commit to, the second bridge admitting he could stay or go and it wouldn't matter. The turn comes when he does leave and then returns, and finds the destination indistinguishable from where he started; the hunger that drove him out survives the crossing intact. Chino Moreno tends to sing escape and dependency as the same gesture, and this is that idea reduced to its bones: the farewell keeps repeating, drawn out until only the parting word is left, because the promised elsewhere resolves into the same appetite he was trying to outrun.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the promised elsewhere resolves into the appetite left behind","built":"the farewell loops until only the parting word survives"},
"deftones~you-ve-seen-the-butcher": {"haiku":"A person watches another move invasively through their intimate space, creating an atmosphere of surrender and violation.","opus":"Chino's sultry, unsettling take on desire, an invitation that blurs seduction with something predatory and tomb-like. The pleasure is real but shadowed by dread, sex circling close to death the way the band always lets it.","sonnet":"Predatory sexual desire framed with hypnotic restraint — the narrator wants to watch, to witness someone enter their space and dismantle them, the language of violence and intimacy fused into something that is slow, knowing, and mutually destructive.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction song that keeps sliding into the vocabulary of the crypt: the desire scans as ordinary lust until the words for the body arrive as a skull, a room, a tomb, so that arousal and burial share one architecture. Chino Moreno's genius here is to let the two readings float rather than resolve — the lover who knows the room and crawls across it could be a partner or a predator, and the flat wish to watch, to see for sure, to run the tape, pulls the intimacy toward voyeurism and something closer to a snuff scenario. The title's butcher is never named or explained; it hovers as the thing already witnessed, the knowledge that makes the caressing tone menacing rather than tender. As the verses cycle and strip down to the barest imperatives at the end, the come-on hardens into ritual, repetition standing in for whatever the song refuses to say outright.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","death & grief","surveillance & control"],"means":"desire that shares its architecture with burial","built":"verses strip to bare imperatives as the come-on hardens into ritual"},
"deijuvhs~seraphim":{"haiku":"Chaotic self-sabotage and vexation manifest as the speaker demon-walks and fractures into separate personalities, demons and heathens fueling a descent into demonic appetite and dust.","opus":"A raw British alt-rap of self-loathing and vice, the narrator sleeping on himself while stress, memory and denial pile up. Bravado about drugs and violence keeps cracking to reveal the wish to be broken open and made new.","sonnet":"A speaker in low-key crisis — sleeping on himself, preoccupied with memories he should not hide, a mind he does not vibe with — oscillates between wanting stack and status and the raw admission that he is still in the dust, vexed and pissed off at himself above all.","src":"haiku"},
"delaire-the-liar~shovel":{"opus":"A disturbing meditation on envy, self-consumption, and body dysmorphia, where the singer, who grew by swallowing others whole, feels cheap and ugly with no mirrors and longs to trade his life away, becoming the discarded scraps stuck in someone else's teeth.","sonnet":"A corrosive mirror-encounter with a rival or doppelganger who is stealing the narrator's identity, achievements, and place — the narrator invites it with masochistic resignation, feeling cheap and full, offering their seat at the table while watching the thief cheat and get away with it.","src":"sonnet"},
"dellaxoz~boring":{"haiku":"Stagnation breeds contempt and dissolution; the speaker recognizes their partner has become uninteresting, intimacy replaced by restless indifference.","opus":"Wearied disengagement from a relationship with someone who cheats, only talks, and abuses patience until the speaker's investment is broke and wasted. Falling for them curdles into the flat verdict that they've simply become boring.","sonnet":"A relationship that has gone stale through inattention, cheating, and one-sided effort — the narrator finally admits they are bored, the other person never listens, and it is time to end it and move on.","src":"haiku"},
"delta-sleep~afterimage":{"opus":"Delta Sleep trace a dreamlike escape from a place that gradually poisons its inhabitant, a sleeping mind repeatedly straining to locate somewhere safe. The math-rock band render flight and belonging as half-recalled fragments, wildlife and water glimpsed in passing. At its core it's about fleeing what harms you in search of what might mend you.","sonnet":"A song about a woman haunted by the city she left, dreaming of nature and a place that might feel like home — running by the stream, encountering creatures she'd never see in urban life, she eventually finds home again somewhere far from the intoxicating but suffocating city. It's a quiet pastoral escape narrative.","src":"sonnet"},
"delta-sleep~camp-adventure":{"opus":"Delta Sleep dwell on effort that never quite produces anything solid, work poured into structures that stay unfinished or cannot bear any weight. Beneath the frustration runs grief for someone lost whose memory still crowds the writer's sleepless nights. It is about futility, persistence, and a love that outlives absence.","sonnet":"About a relationship that could never survive its structural flaws — the narrator and a former partner built something together that kept flooding and collapsing, and even after parting, songs and memories of that person persist as the only lasting thing left.","src":"sonnet"},
"delta-sleep~jesus-bill":{"haiku":"Urban disorientation triggers existential vertigo until the landscape becomes uncannily familiar and the narrator recognises the trap of returning home.","opus":"Disorientation as breakdown, someone suddenly lost in familiar streets, gripping at reassurance while irony reveals what he missed. The refrain is less confidence than a mantra to keep himself upright until he can get home.","sonnet":"A man finds himself disoriented and lost on a familiar street at night, and works his way back to calm — the experience becomes a small lesson about grounding yourself, waiting, and trusting that a way home will surface if you stay present.","src":"haiku"},
"delta-sleep~lake-sprinkle-sprankle":{"haiku":"A speaker is haunted by memories of a past romantic entanglement—a dress, physical attraction, thighs, eyes—and struggles with lingering attachment while confined to the place they once shared. Drifting into surreal reverie, they daydream of escape to an idealized lake setting where they once swam together.","opus":"A restless mind circles a lover who is gone, unable to leave the shared house where paintings and remembered swims keep replaying. Guilt over broken promises tangles with a drifting, near-dreamlike yearning for a warmer place beyond the lake. Delta Sleep, a British math-rock act, wraps this insomniac grief in shifting time signatures that mirror thoughts that will not settle before morning.","sonnet":"A man haunted by a relationship he sabotaged lingers in the shared house they once filled together, sleepless among paintings and memories. Shame over a thoughtless fling sharpens his paralysis. Eventually, exhausted dreaming pulls him toward a lake they used to swim — the only peace available to someone who cannot let go.","src":"haiku"},
"delta-sleep~old-soul":{"haiku":"Aging strips away the spontaneity of youth, leaving fragmented memory and the realisation that time transforms us into strangers to our earlier selves.","opus":"Aging out of a wilder youth and grieving how memory erodes with every night's sleep, while trying to build something worth handing to the next generation. Nostalgia braced against the responsibility of the future.","sonnet":"Looking back at a youth spent in collective warmth and open-armed camaraderie, the speaker grapples with how that picture fades with time, yet finds meaning in the idea that the dreams of one generation build the world the next will inhabit.","src":"haiku"},
"delta-sleep~planet-fantastic":{"haiku":"Critiquing societal indifference to environmental collapse and reliance on plastic comforts that enable extinction. Humanity faces ruin through apathy and consumerism.","opus":"An anxious plea about a planet in crisis, the narrator unable to stay quiet about a catastrophe humanity caused by shirking blame and clinging to disposable conveniences that steer us toward oblivion. Delta Sleep, a British math-rock band, carry this ecological warning on intricate, angular playing, imagining us hurled unfeelingly toward destruction while interring the people we love, climate dread rendered both cosmic and intimate.","sonnet":"Environmental collapse delivered as an urgent, sad confession — the world is a mess and saying nothing is no longer an option. The comfortable plastic infrastructure that insulates people from that truth is the very thing accelerating extinction, and the song watches numbness spread as loved ones get buried. The sun is the destination if nothing changes, and no one seems to be listening.","src":"haiku"},
"delta-sleep~sans-soleil":{"haiku":"Wandering through foreign cities erases prior attachments; solitary survival outdoors feels more honest than the suffocating routines of institutional living.","opus":"Named for the Chris Marker film, a wanderer stripped down to nothing finds more peace sleeping rough outdoors than in the routine and heartache of the life he fled. Alienation resolved by walking away from home entirely.","sonnet":"A traveller who has left a stifling life behind wakes in the open air, exhausted and alone, and finds that sleeping rough under a tree feels more like home than the place they came from — nature and motion restore something the city had taken.","src":"haiku"},
"delta-sleep~sofa-boy":{"opus":"Delta Sleep sketch a restless mind that can't reset, staying up late chasing answers and crossing borders that all feel the same. The refrain about the weather not changing unless someone speaks frames a stalled relationship and a plea to be heard. It is about inertia, missed connection, and the wish that things could be different next time.","sonnet":"About being emotionally stuck in repetitive thought patterns while physically moving through the world — the narrator crosses borders and drives highways but nothing resets, and the plea to be heard by someone who could break the cycle goes unanswered.","src":"sonnet"},
"delta-sleep~strongthany":{"haiku":"Solitary survival at sea teaches acceptance; the speaker drifts through suffering until land appears, realizing patience and isolation hold their own wisdom.","opus":"A castaway adrift alone on troubled seas gradually finds calm as the storm dies and the winds shift. Through lies and exhaustion he finally sees that land, and everything else, is bound to reveal itself in time.","sonnet":"A sea-voyage metaphor for surviving hardship alone: a battered door, troubled waters, days unaccompanied — and the gradual turn when the storm dies and the speaker finally sees that land will show itself, and all the struggles and lies and tired eyes were endurable.","src":"haiku"},
"dematerialize~binary-sunset":{"haiku":"Artificial barriers divide humanity; integration and collective consciousness offer the only path to break cycles of isolation and fragmentation.","opus":"Deathcore reaching for utopian hope, raging against the artificial walls that divide people and dreaming of humanity integrated into one seamless network. Beneath the heaviness is an almost transhumanist plea to tear down separation before it's too late.","sonnet":"Artificial constructs divide people and lock the doors to collective hope; the speaker, cheated too many times, calls for burning the bridges separating humanity, breaking walls down, and letting information flow freely until integrity rebuilds a society that can come together as one.","src":"haiku"},
"demob-happy~autoportrait":{"haiku":"The narrator struggles with masculinity and identity, seeking validation and celebration while fearing who he really is.","opus":"A candid reckoning with masculinity and worth, the narrator afraid to be the man he's told to be, feeling worthless even onstage, yet steadied by love and hungry to be celebrated, to be elevated. Demob Happy are a British grunge-tinged rock band, and this self-portrait swings between vulnerability and swagger, the eye-for-an-eye expectations set on a plate rejected in favour of simply being celebrated as he is.","sonnet":"Performance anxiety and love as ballast — the speaker is afraid of what it means to be who they are, unsettled by expectation and stage, aware that success doesn't translate into worth. But love functions as a kind of insurance: nothing really wrong can happen as long as that anchor holds. The ask beneath it all is to be seen and celebrated, not for the role but for the self.","src":"haiku"},
"demob-happy~honey-you-re-a-dead-man":{"opus":"A grungy shove toward self-reinvention, the narrator refusing to impersonate anyone else and letting go of control and of a person still haunting his thoughts. The closing sneer skewers conformity: no one cares unless you obey.","src":"opus"},
"demons-wizards~dorian-remaster-2019":{"haiku":"Mortality ensnares youth in futile beauty; time steals everything worth living for while the speaker trades immortal youth for the curse of seeing their own decay.","opus":"A retelling of Dorian Gray's bargain, in which time is the enemy and a portrait ages in the sinner's place while he stays young. The pursuit of eternal beauty curdles into damnation, the twisted mirror finally claiming the empty man behind it.","sonnet":"A retelling of the Dorian Gray pact through the portrait's perspective: time is jealous and cruel, beauty drains with every breath, and the speaker — possibly the painting itself — watches the accumulated damage of sin twist Dorian into something horrible, lamenting both the corruption and what time takes.","src":"haiku"},
"denderty~a-fm9k51":{"haiku":"Violent imagery and mental anguish; brain, poison, or blood dominate the disturbing landscape.","opus":"denderty works the Russian-language emo-rap register where despair is delivered with a shrug rather than a scream. This confession of social anxiety and bad decisions lands on a strange comfort — arriving at exactly the sadness one always expected. The leash tightens each year, no one needs anyone, dreams break outward, and it is grimly, profanely satisfying: resignation reframed as the closest thing to peace on offer.","sonnet":"A quietly wrecked Russian-language confession of social anxiety, bad decisions, and the paradoxical comfort of having arrived at exactly the sadness one always expected — the leash tightens each year, no one needs anyone, dreams are broken outward, and it is grimly, profanely satisfying.","src":"haiku"},
"dennis-lloyd~never-go-back":{"haiku":"Cyclical dysfunction repeats despite resolve; the speaker swears to leave but remains trapped, unable to sever ties despite knowing the relationship poisons them.","opus":"A man trying to convince himself he'll never return to a love that has curdled, even as he admits he can't let go. The bilingual push and pull captures the gap between the vow to leave and the inability to.","sonnet":"A relationship in stasis: the speaker knows they should leave a partner who never holds them, never admits fault, never truly lets go — and the declaration \"I never go back\" is undercut at every turn by \"I oughtta leave you, wanna leave you, but I can't let go.\"","src":"haiku"},
"dennis-lloyd~nevermind":{"opus":"A restless lover shrugging off the fallout of leaving someone, telling them not to worry about explaining the breakup to friends, while pivoting to bold desire for a new partner tonight.","sonnet":"A song about romantic uncertainty — the narrator contemplates leaving a relationship and imagines the other person moving on with friends' support, while also expressing readiness and desire to commit fully, caught between departure and devotion.","src":"sonnet"},
"denzel-curry~blood-on-my-nikez-feat-juicy-j":{"opus":"Curry channels Miami street violence with grim precision, wiping blood off his sneakers as ritual, invoking God only to play executioner in a place where cases go unsolved. The Juicy J pairing anchors it in Southern menace, survival narrated without glory.","src":"opus"},
"denzel-curry~ice-cold-zel-freestyle-feat-icecoldbishop":{"opus":"A relentless freestyle flexing lyrical speed and street credibility, dense with weapon and drug-trade imagery drawn from a Carol City upbringing. It's less a story than a display of dominance, Curry proving he can outgun anyone bar for bar.","src":"opus"},
"denzel-curry~wish-feat-kiddo-marv":{"opus":"A flex of designer flash, guns, and women across South Florida, dreads and gold teeth signaling status while paying tribute to fallen friends (RIP X, RIP Tree); the verses mix seduction and violence, funerals as the only church, and gunplay as routine survival.","src":"opus"},
"denzel-curry~x-wing":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects material vehicles for metaphorical ascension, channeling rage at mortality and street poverty into mythological ambition and cold calculation.","opus":"Curry hauling in the dead rap prophets who never reached thirty as motivation, spinning Star Wars imagery into an escape-velocity ambition that refuses ordinary luxury. The flex masks a survivor's urgency to outlast the odds.","sonnet":"A rapper from a poor background asserts his elite status and refusal to be boxed in by mainstream expectations, framing ambition through Star Wars imagery — not content with earthly success, he wants something grander and more untouchable than any car or hit record.","src":"haiku"},
"denzel-curry~zeltron-6-billion":{"haiku":"The rapper asserts dominance through aggression, lyrical superiority, and unapologetic bravado despite underlying struggle. Self-assertion masks survival instinct and artistic determination.","opus":"A collaboration where Curry hides behind a cartoonish supervillain identity, using cocky boasts as armor over private grief, including the loss of a sibling and stretches of despair. The larger point is that this untouchable persona exists to smother his own doubt and sadness beneath swagger.","sonnet":"Denzel and collaborator Ugly Mane trade verses of hyperconfident rap battle boasting — hardest flows, coldest delivery, everyone else rendered obsolete — with a quiet thread of genuine loss and hopelessness running beneath the aggression, including a nod to his late brother.","src":"haiku"},
"depeche-mode~blue-dress":{"haiku":"A garment becomes catalyst for desire and submission, reducing human connection to the simple act of visual consumption and unquestioned obedience.","opus":"Gore writing a fetish as a small confession about how absurdly little it takes to make a man happy, then quietly claiming that this trivial ritual is where the real machinery of the world turns. Desire dignified by its own smallness.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses a lover with a quiet fetishistic intensity, finding profound happiness in the simple act of watching her wear a particular dress — the erotic charge is entirely visual, the pleasure almost disturbingly easy to satisfy.","src":"haiku"},
"depeche-mode~enjoy-the-silence":{"haiku":"Words fracture and inflict harm; true connection exists only in silence beyond language.","opus":"The paradox of speaking love: words are framed as violence that only wounds, while everything the narrator needs is already wordless in his arms. Martin Gore's synth-pop masterpiece argues that the deepest intimacy is the one that needs no language.","sonnet":"Words are identified as the agent of violence — piercing, breaking, ultimately meaningless and forgettable — and the counterproposal is total silence: everything needed is present in the body, in the arms, and language can only do harm to what the two people already have.","src":"haiku"},
"depeche-mode~enjoy-the-silence-remix":{"haiku":"Words and language cause pain and division, while emotional connection matters more than speech. Silence is valued because language breaks trust and only causes harm.","opus":"A case for wordless closeness over conversation, arguing that speech only wounds and shatters something fragile. Underneath sits a real disillusionment with language: promises collapse, sensation matters while talk proves hollow, so Depeche Mode conclude that everything essential is already present in touch, best left unspoken.","sonnet":"Total contentment in physical presence with the person you love — words only hurt, promises only break, and the only real thing is holding each other in silence where nothing needs to be said and nothing can go wrong.","src":"haiku"},
"depeche-mode~just-can-t-get-enough-remastered":{"haiku":"A person expresses insatiable desire for another's presence and affection, framing romantic obsession as both irresistible and consuming.","opus":"Depeche Mode's early synth-pop confection, penned by Vince Clarke before his departure, is pure infatuation set to a bright bounce, the narrator giddy and insatiable in love. Rainbows, angels, and burning warmth pile up as guileless devotion, a sweetness that predates the band's later darkness. The endlessly repeated craving makes obsession sound like nothing but joy.","sonnet":"Pure romantic intoxication with no complications attached — a person so overcome by another that ordinary thought dissolves. The beloved shines through rain, lifts like a rainbow, and every moment together only intensifies the hunger for more contact, more closeness, more time.","src":"haiku"},
"depeche-mode~never-let-me-down-again-remastered":{"haiku":"Dependency on escape disguised as companionship; the speaker surrenders to flight while trusting an unreliable guide.","opus":"On its surface a joyous journey shared with a trusted companion, the track is widely heard as a song about drugs, the loyal friend standing in for the substance. The euphoria of soaring and never wanting to descend doubles as the very dependence certain to betray him.","sonnet":"The speaker takes a euphoric ride with a trusted companion — widely read as an allegory for drug use — carried high above the world and unwilling to come back down, placing total faith in someone who holds their wellbeing in their hands. The rush and the surrender are inseparable.","src":"haiku"},
"depeche-mode~personal-jesus":{"haiku":"Spiritual longing redirects toward another human, who becomes confessor and savior, transforming desire into pseudo-religious dependency through emotional availability.","opus":"Devotion misdirected onto a lover treated as a savior, inspired by Priscilla Presley's account of Elvis as her everything. Gahan's come-on plays the seducer as false idol, faith reduced to reaching for a telephone.","sonnet":"An offer of unconditional, quasi-religious devotion — the speaker positions themselves as a personal saviour, someone who will hear confessions, deliver comfort, and be constantly reachable. The relationship with faith is also a relationship with intimacy, where one person becomes another's entire spiritual apparatus.","src":"haiku"},
"des-rocs~nowhere-kid":{"haiku":"Fundamental alienation marks the outsider; the speaker never belonged and offers solace to other lost children in the dark.","opus":"An anthem for the permanent outsider who never fit in and stopped expecting to, reaching a hand to another lost soul in the dark. The defiance and the loneliness are the same feeling: belonging nowhere becomes its own identity.","sonnet":"An outsider who has never belonged anywhere offers company to someone else hiding in a maze of lies and darkness — Johnny's story about not leaving the light on for snakes suggests both have learned how predators operate — and the solidarity is grounded in shared displacement rather than hope of integration.","src":"haiku"},
"des-rocs~this-is-our-life":{"opus":"A hardened anthem of endurance for the lonely and beaten-down, insisting that no matter how vicious the world gets and how alone you feel, the narrator has seen worse and will always get back up.","sonnet":"A defiant acknowledgment of a hard life where no one listens and everyone bites — the narrator has faced mortality and cruelty directly and comes out refusing to be broken, insisting that this raw, hostile existence is theirs to own. The emotional core is stubborn pride in survival rather than any hope of rescue.","src":"sonnet"},
"desasterkids~walking-alone":{"haiku":"Relentless emotional pummeling leaves the narrator empty and caged; they refuse to become their tormentor despite the pattern's seduction.","opus":"A metalcore severance from a manipulator who poses as fair referee while quietly gutting the narrator, leaving emptiness and mess in his head. The resolve is to refuse becoming like them and walk away, choosing solitude over that poison.","sonnet":"Someone has been manipulated and hollowed out by a controlling presence that played referee while playing dirty — told all their secrets, paid the price — and the resolution is to walk alone rather than keep being made to look like nothing, rejecting the other's worldview as an evil dream that will never come true.","src":"haiku"},
"designer-disguise~outta-my-face":{"haiku":"Speaker exposes a manipulative partner's lies, toxic games, and feigned innocence before cutting them off.","opus":"Designer Disguise fuse rap-metal aggression with a kiss-off to a manipulative partner whose refined smiles read as fake. The narrator, sleepless and scheming how to write it out, senses a hidden knife and demands distance, trading barbed pop-culture boasts for the resolve to walk away. Beneath the bravado runs exhaustion with the lies and the mounting sense that time is running out.","sonnet":"A combustible exit from a manipulative person who craves conflict and manufactures drama. Sleepless nights spent deciphering the patterns of a chronic instigator finally resolve into a clean refusal — the narrator is done, and the cost of proximity has simply exceeded any plausible return.","src":"haiku"},
"designer-disguise~stardust":{"haiku":"Cyclical destruction and rebirth mirror cosmic processes as the speaker transforms trauma into fuel for healing, breaking inherited patterns through conscious choice.","opus":"A cosmic metaphor for resilience: everything fades to nothing, yet like stardust rebuilds from dead coals, the speaker chooses to heal and rebuild rather than give up, breaking the cycle of pain.","sonnet":"Humanity rebuilding from destruction: stardust rises from coals, hands bloodied but capable of lifting others, and the speaker chooses to heal rather than give up — finding a universe inside the mind where stars align and the cycle of pain might finally be broken.","src":"haiku"},
"desolate~missingno-177013":{"haiku":"A young girl destroyed by trauma and addiction searches for death as mercy, having sold everything for survival yet finding only cyclical suffering.","opus":"Brutal deathcore built around the notorious doujin its title references, sitting inside a dying girl's despair of abuse, overdose and craving for oblivion. It refuses redemption, insisting this endless suffering is simply how it was always meant to be.","sonnet":"A girl sold on false safety ends up abused, fractured, and abandoned by wolves, cycling through suicidal sleep and a world where perversity outlasts sympathy; the number 177013 counts down like a sentence, and the final image — the second child sleeping alone — is offered as the end of the loop rather than any rest.","src":"haiku"},
"despite-exile~scepter":{"haiku":"Power's false promises generate cycles of hope and betrayal; those who seek the throne discover it was hollow, and true power disperses through invisible networks rather than concentrated symbols.","opus":"Technical death metal turned political theory, tracing how servants and soldiers grasp for a crown only to discover it's empty. Its real argument is Foucauldian — authority sits nowhere fixed to be captured but is scattered everywhere, so every emblem of rule can be left to rot in the mud.","sonnet":"A meditation on power's fundamental hollowness: servants and warriors who sacrificed everything for an ideal discover the scepter was only ever a receptacle of fate, the throne hollow all along, and the cycle of hope and corruption perpetual — power does not sit where you think it does but diffuses through the interstices of belief itself.","src":"haiku"},
"destiny-potato~addict":{"haiku":"Compulsive attachment warps into torture; the speaker cycles between breaking free and returning to contamination, helpless before their own demons.","opus":"Addiction as a controlling lover that owns you completely, casting you off again and again yet always leaving a mark; the struggle to break loose feels like courting death just to keep breathing.","sonnet":"About the battle to break free from an addiction — rendered as a controlling presence the narrator knows intimately, has kicked before, and keeps being dragged back toward. The physical imagery of waves flooding the nervous system and dreams collapsing captures the physiological grip of dependence; the central emotional tension is the narrator's exhausted determination to drop it again despite the scars that never fully heal.","src":"haiku"},
"destiny-potato~blue-sun":{"haiku":"Dreams materialize into destructive reality where ambition consumes the dreamer and light burns rather than enlightens.","opus":"A warning wrapped in a fairy tale about wishes granted and the price they carry, two dreamers hurtling forward until their momentum shatters. The title's burning star stands for a reality grown too intense to bear, cautioning that even a victory can turn into a loss.","sonnet":"Two dreamers — a girl who imagined who she might become and a boy who reached for stars — crash when reality collides with fantasy at light speed, and the blue sun rising is a warning that chasing dreams too hard can burn you.","src":"haiku"},
"destiny-potato~house-of-lies":{"haiku":"Trust becomes betrayal as the promised supporter reveals their true nature through abandonment. Lies construct prison where once there was hope for genuine connection.","opus":"A djent-pop reckoning with an emotional abuser who fed on watching the narrator crawl and shame herself, keeping her paralyzed inside his deceptions. The chorus is the moment of clawing back identity, refusing to let the wound close on his terms.","sonnet":"A relationship retrospective: the person who was supposed to be there turned their back, spoke words that were never heard, and built a house of lies so complete the narrator couldn't leave even when they wanted to — the anger is real but so is the paralysis.","src":"haiku"},
"destiny-potato~indifferent":{"haiku":"A rejected lover finds freedom and luck in leaving. Their indifference is triumph—the past's promises and pleas no longer bind.","opus":"The hard-won relief of being finally free of a manipulative partner, where the coldness the singer claims is really the triumph of no longer wanting them back.","sonnet":"A song about the detached relief of escaping a controlling, dishonest partner — the narrator has been stalked and manipulated by someone who never actually changed, and now feels strangely happy that this person is gone, wanting nothing returned, recognizing clearly what was done to them.","src":"haiku"},
"destiny-potato~lost-dream":{"haiku":"Death of promises and dream-burial haunt survival; the speaker grieves abandoned futures while learning to live in hollowed present.","opus":"Mourning a broken promise of forever by burying the lover's ghost and begging for the senses back to feel anything again. The refrain pleads for the return of dreams lost in a world gone hollow and shadowed.","sonnet":"The aftermath of a devastating heartbreak — the narrator buries the ghost of someone who broke their promise, and begs to have sensation restored: eyes to see, hands to feel, dreams to pursue, because everything has gone hollow and shadowy in the other person's wake.","src":"haiku"},
"destiny-potato~lunatic":{"haiku":"Acknowledging suppressed visions and inner voices while feeling saved by another's recognition of hidden truths.","opus":"Voices, visions and dreams the narrator won't disown even as she wonders if they damn her, the terror and the rightness of an inner reality no one else can see, salvation and madness arriving as the same experience.","sonnet":"About being saved from a breakdown or crisis by someone who could see what the narrator couldn't — the voices and visions and questions overwhelming the narrator felt like madness, but what they were inside felt right, and someone arrived in time to stop the worst from happening.","src":"haiku"},
"destiny-potato~take-a-picture":{"haiku":"Obsessive possession and dark desire consume the speaker, who hunts and threatens their love interest with predatory intensity.","opus":"Longing tips into obsession: adoration that would do anything for the beloved slides toward hunting them like prey and demanding one last picture. The taboo love and the story behind the smile reveal how devotion here is inseparable from possessiveness.","sonnet":"An obsessive, darkening infatuation — what starts as longing to be close to someone tips into menace: the narrator will hunt them down as prey, the photo is described as the last thing they'll see; love here is possessive and violent underneath its romantic surface.","src":"haiku"},
"destiny-potato~u-y-m":{"haiku":"Pain transmutes into hatred; the speaker cannot endure another moment of self-betrayal, demanding recognition before final collapse.","opus":"A push toward waking up and truly sensing life, paired with the exhausted decision to walk away from someone whose accumulated wrongs have turned pain into hate. The plea to feel the sound sits beside a door being shut for good.","sonnet":"The moment of finally ending a relationship that has become painful and false — the narrator's life has been masquerading as something it isn't, the pain has curdled into hatred, and they shut the door rather than continue bleeding time on someone who cannot change.","src":"haiku"},
"destiny-potato~walls-of-thought":{"haiku":"A single moment crystallizes eternity through human connection; truth endures unbreakable once two souls genuinely touch and belong together.","opus":"A more romantic turn, imagining two souls bound in a single instant yet separated by their own walls of thought and diverging roads. It sits in the ache of connection that fate keeps pulling apart into pieces made by both.","sonnet":"Two people on separate roads spiral through the same emotional vertigo — flying, turning, falling, burning — their inner walls knowing exactly how it feels to lose something; a single spark once made a child smile, and the search for what was lost pulls both of them on despite the difficulty of choosing.","src":"haiku"},
"destrage~everything-sucks-and-i-think-i-m-a-big-part-of-it":{"haiku":"First-world consumption and endless scrolling reduce existence to garbage and nothing, with the speaker complicit in systemic emptiness. Resolutions collapse into the void of digital noise.","opus":"A frantic send-up of comfortable misery and scroll-induced stupor, the narrator naming himself part of the very apathy he ridicules. Under the absurd vignettes lurks a genuine emptiness, endless good intentions that curdle into nothing.","sonnet":"First-world self-sabotage rendered as absurdist inventory: sugar-rush scrolling, swiping for lust while taking a dump, a thousand resolutions going to nothing — the narrator admits complicity in the ambient suck of modern life, \"a big part of it,\" going nowhere but down the scroll.","src":"haiku"},
"destrage~italian-boi":{"haiku":"Stereotype as lived reality: cultural patterns calcify into behavior, and attraction becomes entanglement with contradiction that no amount of understanding can resolve.","opus":"A wry, satirical takedown of the Italian-man stereotype dressed as a hookup gone sour, cataloguing the gallantry, mamma-worship and machismo. The narrator asks why someone so performatively perfect can't just enjoy simple things.","sonnet":"A song that affectionately and critically dissects the stereotype of the Italian man — passionate about food, obsessed with his mother, theatrical in bed but emotionally unreliable, always late, and ultimately charming in a way that still breaks your heart.","src":"haiku"},
"destrage~private-party-feat-devin-townsend":{"haiku":"The speaker reclaims solitude as sacred ritual, shutting out intrusion and noise to dance alone within the soul's private sanctuary. Disconnection becomes the highest form of self-protection.","opus":"A defiant demand to be left alone against relentless digital intrusion, every ping and text an invasion of a private inner space. With Townsend along, it celebrates dancing solo in one's own soul, refusing to be sold or summoned by anyone.","sonnet":"An introvert under siege from social obligation — texts, calls, invites all framed as intrusions policing their inner space — the narrator is dancing alone at a private party in their soul and wants everyone, especially the persistent one, to simply leave them be.","src":"haiku"},
"destrage~purania":{"haiku":"Purity politics and rigid dogma suffocate human diversity; the speaker rejects conformist judgment and celebrates contradiction as the natural state of a fully lived, honest existence.","opus":"A raucous refusal of purism in taste and identity, insisting nobody gets to police what you enjoy. Owning up to liking the Spice Girls becomes a manifesto that contradiction and appetite beat self-denial.","sonnet":"A defiant rejection of purity culture and taste policing: the speaker refuses every pill — every belief or aesthetic dogma — that strangers want to force on him, insisting that liking the Spice Girls, fat girls, or couchsurfing is no contradiction, and that narrow \"purity\" is what actually makes people sick.","src":"haiku"},
"destrage~the-chosen-one":{"opus":"A dry look at how small and frightened people are when they decide anything, everything alarming us though most of it is harmless. Standing alone, leading and following no one, the narrator makes an uncertain, flawed, but genuine choice, noting with irony that each of us is just one among countless people who imagine themselves specially picked.","sonnet":"A frank reckoning with life's arbitrary choices — making decisions blind, without clear options, into barriers and obstacles — the narrator arrives at the conclusion that being one of countless chosen ones means facing the unknown headfirst and only grasping the sense of it all in retrospect.","src":"sonnet"},
"destrage~where-the-things-have-no-colour":{"haiku":"The speaker sinks into a dark ocean to escape control and slavery; they reclaim freedom and refuse to be victimized by an ice-cold world.","opus":"The Italian technical band casts coming back to life as a dive into a colorless sea where the chill stings and the ears ache, a savage return to sensation. The narrator refuses to be muted, owned, or forced down, taking the moment on his own terms. Going under becomes freedom, and the world ends up looking as lovely as it ever did.","sonnet":"An assertion of inner freedom against forces that seek to subjugate. The narrator descends into a colorless ocean — a space beyond external control — and emerges not crushed but clarified, the child who laughs without reason intact, able to see the world as beautiful again.","src":"haiku"},
"destroy-boys~american-river":{"haiku":"Suburban ennui breeds disconnection and self-harm as escape, with institutional structures (school, family) perpetuating cycles of violence and shame.","opus":"Suburban Sacramento teenage misery, the dodgeball humiliations and levee weed sessions of a life you resent, held together by a stubborn insistence on thinking for yourself. Adolescent contempt as a survival stance.","sonnet":"The drudgery and absurdity of high school life in a California suburb — weed on the levy, dodgeball humiliations, an alarm clock thrown across the room — told with a stubborn insistence on holding your head up and thinking for yourself anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"destroy-boys~beg-for-the-torture":{"haiku":"Transforming pain into pleasure through a cycle of emotional abuse and validation disguised as intimacy.","opus":"A knowing dive into a relationship that hurts, where pain and pleasure fuse and the humiliation is half the appeal, the narrator aware she's been here before yet choosing to love the damage anyway.","sonnet":"About a BDSM-inflected dynamic where pain and humiliation have been eroticized — the narrator has learned to crave the degradation their partner delivers, even as they recognize the psychological manipulation involved. The tension between \"I can keep it together\" and \"I can't\" is the song's emotional center.","src":"haiku"},
"destroy-boys~piedmont":{"haiku":"The narrator drifts untethered from temporal and social anchors, nostalgic for experiences never lived while alienated from contemporaries and context.","opus":"The ache of standing outside a world everyone else seems to belong to, feeling detached from yourself and nostalgic for experiences you never had. It circles the doubt of whether you'd even want the belonging you envy.","sonnet":"A feeling of displacement and outsider longing — the speaker observes peers who seem to know something they don't, yearns for memories they've never had, and hovers between wanting to belong and suspecting that belonging would mean losing something.","src":"haiku"},
"destroy-rebuild-until-god-shows~destiny":{"opus":"The post-hardcore project grieves a romance cut off by fate, someone whose old pictures keep dragging memory back even though there's no repairing it. Waking each day means burying the past under a new falsehood and pinning the loss on destiny. Broken-glass imagery drives home that mourning can't reassemble what's ruined, heartbreak recast as something that was always meant to happen.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship whose end felt inevitable rather than chosen — the narrator cannot reconcile with the lost person, clings to photographs, and frames the separation as something destiny imposed rather than a decision either of them made. The grief is layered with the inability to express what was felt while it was happening.","src":"sonnet"},
"destroy-rebuild-until-god-shows~hunger-pangs":{"opus":"Post-hardcore framing addiction and toxic desire as an appetite that can't be filled, each fix and overdose another cut in the thrill of the chase. Craving and eroticism blur until he's hooked on the rush of the blood itself.","src":"opus"},
"destroy-rebuild-until-god-shows~it-follows":{"haiku":"Damage accumulates inescapably; the speaker runs from their shadow only to find it chasing them through repeated cycles of the same failure.","opus":"Addiction and self-sabotage as a thing that can never be outrun, a bitter soul running from the damage and panic that always catch up no matter where he goes.","sonnet":"Trauma and damage follow the speaker no matter how far they run — 'where I go it always follows' — compounded by an addictive relationship with someone who says she needs him while he runs out of options and hope, the panic and havoc never quite catching up but never falling behind.","src":"haiku"},
"destroy-rebuild-until-god-shows~king-i-am":{"haiku":"A person claims dominance and superiority, rising from obscurity to become like a star and exerting power and control over others.","opus":"A freshly crowned figure relishes the dread he provokes, proclaiming himself a wild, holy, dangerous force who liberates others while at last outgrowing the feeling of falling short. Destroy Rebuild Until God Shows, the post-Chiodos project, pairs theatrical bravado with the boast, flipping past longing for the heavens into the narrator counting himself among their number.","sonnet":"A reclamation of self-worth after long seasons of inadequacy. Envy of those above gives way to arrival among them — the narrator emerges commanding, primal, self-declared sovereign. What once looked up now walks tall, a sacred beast finally inhabiting its own power.","src":"haiku"},
"destroy-rebuild-until-god-shows~malice":{"opus":"Resentment metastasizing into unhinged spite, the little grievances accumulating until the narrator is proudly ruinous and beyond saving. He warns the other to flee while chaos makes him fearless, malice worn as identity after the wound.","src":"opus"},
"destroy-rebuild-until-god-shows~satellites-in-motion":{"opus":"A relationship dissolving into cold, mutual drift, imagined as two satellites orbiting the same space but never touching, the couple lying on opposite edges of the bed and quietly accepting they aren't meant to be.","sonnet":"A relationship that has grown emotionally distant despite physical proximity — two people orbiting each other out of habit rather than genuine connection, separated by emotional remove even in the same bed, unable to commit to staying or leaving.","src":"sonnet"},
"destruction~armageddonizer":{"haiku":"The song questions why humanity kills for ideology and allows leaders to manipulate them into destruction; it critiques blind obedience to authority and religious justifications for violence.","opus":"German thrash veterans firing off blunt questions about why humans slaughter over territory and religion while leaders and spin-doctors profit. The stance is that we're cheated and manipulated into our own destruction, none the wiser.","sonnet":"A tirade of rhetorical questions asking why humanity repeatedly kills for territory, religion, and ideology, indicting leaders who manipulate and delete the minds of their populations while the world slides toward self-annihilation.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~bestial-invasion":{"haiku":"An apocalyptic hellstorm destroys faith and replaces god with a dark lord; Satan's law rules and people bow to evil.","opus":"Dawn breaks on a sleeping mankind as hellstorms roar across the earth without rain and evil seizes control, Christ abolished and a new almighty lord demanding obedience. Destruction, pioneers of German thrash in the early 1980s, render apocalypse as blunt spectacle, people screaming in disbelief as satanic law takes command and the old order is simply overwritten.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic hellstorm descends without rain, without warning — Satan seizes command as Christ ceases to exist. Humanity wakes to screaming and fog, stripped of its former god, commanded to kneel before a new almighty lord who suffers no dissent.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~church-of-disgust":{"haiku":"Religious institutions betray the vulnerable by concealing abuse and perversion under the guise of divine authority and moral infallibility.","opus":"The German thrash veterans turn their fury on organized religion, tracing a wounded, alienated upbringing into an indictment of a faith that hides institutional abuse of children behind claims of divine authority. Satanic imagery serves as the rhetorical counterblow, and the repeated title brands the church as something to be loathed rather than worshipped.","sonnet":"Childhood shaped by institutional religion is reread as abuse: trust given to holy authority was exploited, and the moral system it offered served to conceal violation rather than prevent it. The speaker turns the church's own language back on it — dust to dust, deliver us — as bitter indictment rather than prayer.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~creations-of-the-underworld":{"haiku":"The singer presents himself as a creature of rage and destruction, born from anger and using brutal force to resist oppression and create chaos.","opus":"A snarling anthem of pure hatred and rebellion from one of Germany's founding thrash bands. The narrator opposes everything, welcomes his own destruction, and answers the loathing of others with his fists, treating violence and mayhem as catharsis, his mind a breeding ground for dark and murderous thoughts.","sonnet":"Heavy metal self-mythology as opposition — the speaker takes on the label of punk and outcast, using aggression as a form of mental release against a world that hates their refusal to comply. The battlefield is internal as much as external, a brain full of dark breeding thoughts producing noise as its only honest output.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~curse-the-gods":{"haiku":"Religious faith becomes the death warrant for billions, each doctrine claiming exclusive truth while murder proliferates in the name of salvation.","opus":"A blunt thrash broadside against organized religion, tallying the bodies killed across centuries in god's name. The takeaway is defiantly humanist: drop the idols, stop dying for a lie, believe in yourself instead.","sonnet":"A blunt atheist polemic cataloguing the violence committed in the name of religion across centuries, concluding that all gods are human fabrications and the only rational response is self-reliance. The speaker directs rage equally at the faiths themselves and at those who enforce them.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~day-of-reckoning":{"haiku":"Cascading chaos and misfortune attack the speaker; judgment day or pure chance overwhelms leaving only catastrophe and lost dreams.","opus":"A fatalistic thrash cut framing life as a rigged system where everything that can fail does, chaos grinding down hope and talent in an endless loop. The chorus asks whether a day of judgment offers real absolution or just one more losing hand. The German band delivers it with the blunt, riff-driven force of their scene.","sonnet":"A figure trapped in cyclical misfortune rails against fate, Murphy's Law, and a world that seems cursed from birth. Whether the day of reckoning brings divine forgiveness or simply another defeat remains unanswered. The anger is its own verdict — chaos wins, hope dies, and the condemned just keep cycling through the same wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~destroyer-or-creator":{"haiku":"Choice between destruction and creation in a world of lies; the speaker writes words as weapons to toughen and liberate those suffering.","opus":"The German thrash veterans turn preacher, dedicating the song to the hurting and hopeless and telling them to become the executioners of their own fate in a world run on empty vows and deceit. Everyone battles private demons, and death eventually flattens the difference between all of us. Late-period Destruction points their old fury toward blunt encouragement, casting self-mastery as the cure for despair.","sonnet":"A metal rallying cry addressed to anyone who feels doomed: every person wages war against their own demons, and the choice to destroy or create is the only sovereign power anyone holds. Suffering is universal, and what differs is what each person does with it.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~devil-s-advocate":{"haiku":"Metal music offers liberation from daily routine and slavery, using noise and rebellion as therapy to express frustration and gain freedom through collective experience.","opus":"The German thrash veterans recast the pit as deliverance, with the speaker breaking free of a grinding weekly grind by roaring out his anger through music that outsiders dismiss. Selling out to Satan becomes a sly stand-in for metal itself as therapy, and the band summons its faithful to leap in, prizing rebellion's rush over any hollow, artificial existence.","sonnet":"Heavy metal is presented as liberation theology — a weekly release from the grinding routine of ordinary life that others misread as mere noise. The pit becomes a congregation, stage-diving a form of baptism, and the devil a stand-in for the freedom that conventional structures deny.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~hate-is-my-fuel":{"haiku":"A culture of war, butchery, and hatred sustains a warped philosophy; the speaker claims hatred as their fuel and celebrates themselves as lord of misrule.","opus":"A bleak vision of a society running on falsehood, warfare, and slaughter, with loathing as the narrator's engine and the crowd nothing more than his instrument. Naming himself sovereign of chaos, he insists death equalizes everyone and that only learning and steadiness push back at all against the carnage.","sonnet":"A nihilistic, almost gleeful wallowing in cultural decay, war, and hatred — the narrator positions themselves as lord of misrule in a poisoned civilisation where hate is the only honest fuel left.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~holiday-in-cambodia":{"opus":"A thrash cover of the Dead Kennedys' savage takedown of comfortable Western hypocrites, sarcastically wishing champagne radicals a taste of Pol Pot's killing fields. The point survives translation: privilege confronted with real horror it only performs concern about.","src":"opus"},
"destruction~invincible-force":{"haiku":"An unstoppable evil force incarnates human doom, tearing flesh and bathing victims in blood while Christ offers no salvation against it.","opus":"A classic from thrash's early-1980s German wave, it adopts the voice of an unstoppable force of evil incarnating mankind's end, tearing flesh and mocking that Christ offers no rescue tonight. Pools of blood and clawing carnage pile up with gleeful excess. Destruction helped forge the blasphemous, velocity-obsessed template that would feed the coming extreme-metal underground.","sonnet":"Pure personification of annihilating malevolence: an entity that incarnates the end of mankind delivers its threat in first person, offering no escape or mercy. The lyrics are entirely occupied by the geometry of unstoppable destruction with no interiority or moral concern.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~misfit":{"haiku":"Social rejection and poverty trap the narrator in endless cycles of rage and alienation; all guidelines feel worse than drugs and prevent escape.","opus":"Destruction speaks for the outsider raging against social rules and hollow, forced cheer, a spoiled thing spitting on the ideas of living that wrecked his hopes. He wonders aloud how a purposeless casualty stays alive without either sinning to survive or becoming a cog in the system. The pounding outcast chant taps thrash's rebel streak, an identity forged from estrangement and scorn for imposed order.","sonnet":"A social outcast refuses to perform the contentment the world demands, naming the machinery of conformity as worse than the suffering of standing outside it. Existence as a misfit is framed not as failure but as the only honest position available in a society of falsified bliss.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~nailed-to-the-cross":{"haiku":"Critique of organized religion's use of fear and symbols to control people. The crucifixion is framed as a tool of manipulation and the source of superstition and suffering.","opus":"The German thrash band aim their aggression squarely at organized religion, branding scripture a pack of falsehoods and the crucifix a war banner used to harvest desperate souls through fear. They picture avaricious men sheltering in churches with blood on their hands, channeling the band's long anti-clerical streak into blunt provocation against faith wielded as a tool of control.","sonnet":"An assault on organized religion as a fear-based control apparatus, with the crucifixion itself reframed as a promotional campaign for collecting souls. The narrator names the cross as a symbol of war and its institutions as predatory frauds hiding behind sanctity.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~reject-emotions":{"haiku":"Sexual predation masks itself as passion while emotional connection erodes into purely mechanical transaction devoid of reciprocity or care.","opus":"German thrash pioneers channeling the emptiness of road sex into contempt, both for the groupie chasing a rock fantasy and for love itself, which the narrator dismisses as a con to sidestep the loneliness underneath.","sonnet":"A roadside encounter between a touring musician and a woman seeking connection is reduced entirely to transactional lust — emotion is explicitly rejected, love called a lie, and the interaction framed as nothing more than satisfying physical need.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~sheep-of-the-regime":{"haiku":"Conformity and authoritarian control as systems that erase individuality; the speaker rejects regime-enforced obedience and celebrates nonconformity.","opus":"The German thrashers assault conformity outright, picturing a regimented state that scapegoats outsiders, jails dissenters, and drives a frightened populace with propaganda. The narrator won't meet its demands, gladly wearing the label of societal filth. Destruction's long anti-establishment streak sharpens here into a vision of the individual being extinguished under systemic control.","sonnet":"Contempt for conformist societies that punish individuality and weaponize fear to produce compliant masses. The narrator positions himself outside the flock deliberately, refusing every rule and lie the regime circulates — seeing the shepherds as oppressors, not protectors.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~sorcerer-of-black-magic":{"haiku":"Death strips all earthly status and illusion; a dark sorcerer embodies doom, judgment, and the inescapable end that equalizes all souls.","opus":"Kneeling at death's edge, a man watches his life flash past as panic dissolves the ordinary into the unreal. Rich or poor, all become believers before a doom-dealing sorcerer, and dark forces finally prevail as the bell tolls. Destruction, pillars of German thrash, render this reckoning with occult menace, treating mortality as a merciless equalizer no wealth can outrun.","sonnet":"At the edge of death, a man confronts every illusion he built — wealth, status, bravado — stripped to nothing. A dark sorcerer figure looms as the equalizer, reducing rich and poor alike to desperate prayer. The song maps the moment when mortality makes hypocrites of everyone and dark forces claim what earthly pride could not hold.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~stand-up-and-shout-bonus-track":{"haiku":"A person is trapped by societal constraints but possesses inner fire; they are urged to break free, reclaim ownership, and explode with their own power.","opus":"German thrash veterans cover Dio's call to seize your own agency, aimed at anyone crawling when they were built to fly. Beneath the anthemic push is a refusal of the resignation that keeps people nailed to the wheel.","sonnet":"A motivational rallying cry urging the listener to stop suppressing their desire and drive, break free from the wheel of inaction, and assert themselves before they're crushed by complacency.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~the-butcher-strikes-back":{"haiku":"The violent return of a decimated force erupts with renewed fury, rejecting institutional persecution through unapologetic aggression and refusal to disappear quietly.","opus":"A veteran thrash band's triumphant self-mythologizing comeback, thanking loyal fans for keeping them alive and vowing a devastating return against religion and doubters after years in the wilderness.","sonnet":"A thrash metal band returning after a decade-long absence celebrates its revival through the loyalty of its fanbase, positioning the reunion as an invincible force released from agony and a vindication against those who doubted them.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~the-demon-is-god":{"haiku":"The song warns that written and spoken propaganda are weapons that corrupt minds and manipulate opinion. It critiques how information is weaponized to control populations and spread hatred.","opus":"A polemic casting the written word and media as weapons that manipulate minds, spread misinformation, and enthrone a demonic force over a corrupt, declining world.","sonnet":"The song argues that language — especially written and broadcast words — is a weapon used to manipulate and mislead, positioning media and rhetoric as a corrupting force that spreads misinformation, stifles independent thought, and serves as the real demonic power in society.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~the-price":{"haiku":"Resisting corrupt systems requires isolation and sacrifice as the price for maintaining integrity and freedom against institutional deception.","opus":"German thrash veterans preach resistance against a corrupt system, arguing that education, not violence, is the real weapon, and that the price of defiance is isolation. Sacrifice is the game everyone loses in the end.","sonnet":"A thrash-metal call to resist the system — the price of non-conformity is isolation, but the narrator insists that fighting back and refusing to be brain-damaged by authority is worth paying it.","src":"haiku"},
"destruction~thrash-till-death":{"haiku":"Metal rebellion bonds followers in blood and defiance against dictators and commercialism; immortal souls unite through a self-defense music subculture.","opus":"A tribal manifesto casting the thrash community as a blood-sworn brotherhood standing together against tyrants, passing fads, and anyone who can't grasp its creed. Framed as more than a music style, it becomes a lifelong revolt and a self to guard to the end, an undying spirit in command.","sonnet":"A thrash metal manifesto of genre loyalty — the song is a declaration that the metal underground is a brotherhood bonded by blood, immune to commercial corruption and outside control, committed to the music as an identity and way of life unto death.","src":"haiku"},
"dethtech~nothing-ever-happens-until-it-does":{"opus":"A body worn down to its last reserves, framing chronic pain and self-loathing as a rot that eats from inside while some awaited relief never comes. The disgust curdles into a fantasy of silencing whoever keeps lying about being fine.","src":"opus"},
"detset~one-night-stand":{"opus":"A crude, satirical sketch of a serial hookup artist who fakes charity-minded charm to bed women, hands over a bogus number and disappears by morning; under the vulgar swagger runs self-disgust, a reckless spirit spiraling and burning himself out at both ends.","src":"opus"},
"dev-lemons~ceo-of-my-ass":{"haiku":"Brazen dismissal of external validation transforms personal autonomy into weaponized indifference, rejecting demand and control through relentless self-assertion.","opus":"A gleeful comic takedown of an attention-starved, self-important man, dismissing his ego and control games with mockery and refusing to be bossed by someone who throws tantrums.","sonnet":"A speaker dismisses an attention-hungry, controlling person who becomes possessive only when she moves on, telling him he has no leverage and she has no interest in his clout or cash.","src":"haiku"},
"dev-lemons~dizzy-vision":{"opus":"Infatuation rendered as a physical accident: the swoon, the head-rush, the compulsion to touch what burns. The bedroom-pop conceit is that overthinking and overfeeling are the same self-sabotage, regret arriving the instant the impulse does.","src":"opus"},
"dev-lemons~eat-the-pavement":{"haiku":"A lover feeds crumbs while the speaker demands payment. Breaking requires throwing away and leaving—staying means fear.","opus":"A scorched-earth kiss-off to someone who fed her crumbs and made her chase, dumping their letters and flowers; beneath the vengeful bravado is the shame of always staying too long in a relationship that made her afraid.","sonnet":"A furious post-breakup song in which the narrator refuses to accept an ex's apologies and reversals — they've waited too long, been fed crumbs, and are done; the repeated wish for the ex to 'eat the pavement' is revenge fantasy, not reconciliation.","src":"haiku"},
"dev-lemons~ghosted-my-dentist":{"haiku":"Avoidance of facing problems compounds decay; consciousness of suffering may worsen pain, yet ignorance guarantees deterioration.","opus":"Avoidance dressed as a neglected dental appointment, the tooth standing in for any problem you refuse to name because knowing might not help and might just hurt. The joke barely hides a real dread that some things are broken past fixing.","sonnet":"Avoiding the dentist becomes a precise metaphor for avoidance of all difficult self-knowledge: gums bleeding, retainer lost, the speaker wonders whether knowing what's wrong actually makes it hurt less or just confirms it's hopeless — and the fear that \"being conscious of the problem doesn't help at all\" is the real thing being dodged.","src":"haiku"},
"dev-lemons~guessing-games":{"haiku":"Frustration with a partner's emotional evasiveness and lack of transparency that kills both hope and connection.","opus":"The exhaustion of chasing someone emotionally unavailable, wondering if you were enough while decoding their moods, and finally choosing to walk despite still wanting them, the anger really just grief that it meant more to you.","sonnet":"The frustration of emotional unavailability in a casual relationship — the narrator is tired of not knowing what the other person wants, whether this was a one-night stand or something more, and concludes that if they won't open up, it's time to let go even while still wanting them.","src":"haiku"},
"dev-lemons~you-tear-me-apart":{"opus":"A relationship that only feels safe until you reach for anything better, then punishes you for it. She catalogs the small cruelties of a partner who weaponizes moods, wanting the wreckage anyway because the tenderness felt real.","src":"opus"},
"devil-master~acid-black-mass":{"opus":"Philadelphia's occult punks stage a psychedelic ritual as pure aesthetic thrill, swirling color and blood-drained darkness into a chant. It isn't belief so much as horror-movie hedonism, evil invoked because it looks and sounds transgressive.","src":"opus"},
"devil-master~the-vigour-of-evil":{"opus":"Sex and ritual power fused into one act, dominance and submission mirrored so each partner bows to the other. The occult trappings are really a language for ecstasy that overwhelms, pleasure and pain as the same crashing force.","src":"opus"},
"devildriver~keep-away-from-me":{"opus":"A furious dismissal of a toxic, uncaring person, the narrator watching them slip away and demanding they take everything they do far away from him after enduring endless abuse.","sonnet":"A definitively severed relationship — the narrator has exhausted all hope and energy for someone who never truly cared, and now demands total removal of that person from their life in every form.","src":"sonnet"},
"devilskin~do-you-see-birds":{"haiku":"A person observes freedom others possess while remaining trapped in regret and darkness, unable to escape their mind.","opus":"The New Zealand hard rock band, fronted by Jennie Skulander, voice a lonely lament, the narrator wishing aloud on a distant star while sinking under shame and regret as an inner voice insists solitude suits him best. The recurring image of birds and their freedom sharpens the isolation, the peace he longs for dismissed as nothing but a fantasy and a cry.","sonnet":"A longing for freedom from a life grown oppressive and grey — birds represent escape, the open sky a contrast to the closed world of indifferent living and casual death. The mind has blackened, the last breath is imagined, and the peace being sought is named plainly as a dream rather than a destination, which makes the longing sadder and more honest.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend-band~deadhead":{"haiku":"A sun goddess appears as both salvation and torment, cycling through devotion and resistance as the narrator drowns in the paradox of passionate dependence.","opus":"A raw plea to a distant beloved cast as a sun goddess, finding beauty and salvation in the intensity of the instant and the pain alike, begging to be saved and to give in completely to feeling.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses a sun goddess — vicious and saving at once — surrendering to the heat of the moment and the pain it brings, begging her not to pull over but to drive faster through a rainy, disorienting night.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend-project~deadhead-live-in-plovdiv-2017":{"opus":"Townsend's aching plea to a distant, near-divine beloved to save him, love and self-abasement tangled together. Live, its swell from grey Greyhound loneliness to a soaring cry makes surrender to feeling sound like rescue.","src":"opus"},
"devin-townsend-project~failure":{"haiku":"Isolation crystallizes as the only honest response to the world's betrayals, the narrator choosing solitude and authentic failure over performative success.","opus":"A defiant cry against self-condemnation and the label of failure, refusing fear and insisting on playing for the world even as tears and loneliness set in.","sonnet":"A fragmented cry of isolation and self-doubt pivots toward defiant will — \"I want to play for the world\" — even as the speaker acknowledges being alone and unredeemed, with all tears and fears dismissed into solitude.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend-project~hyperdrive":{"opus":"Cold, riverside desolation set against a restless drive to start over, the apology and the hope for a new way circling each other. Townsend turns weightless synth-pop into a plea that loneliness might still resolve into being loved.","src":"opus"},
"devin-townsend-project~juular":{"haiku":"Homecoming merges with divine transcendence; mourning's warmth fades as coldness penetrates where nothing disturbs the speaker's final peace.","opus":"A deranged, half-comic exorcism where survival itself becomes defiance, spitting an eye-for-an-eye curse at a god who damns him back. Townsend rides the Ziltoid absurdity to a real core: numbness as armor against unbearable pain.","sonnet":"An invincible entity — Juular — survives everything: mourning, punishment, Lady Vagine's judgment, and god's damnation alike; the speaker declares that nothing ever bothers Juular, surviving on an eye-for-an-eye principle that outlasts every attempt to break or damn them.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend-project~planet-of-the-apes":{"opus":"Townsend's sprawling, self-mocking epic about faith, fatherhood and the herd instinct of humanity, deflating its own grandeur with jokes about ripping off Meshuggah. Underneath the chaos sits a genuine question of whether our violent species deserves what it observes.","src":"opus"},
"devin-townsend-project~praise-the-lowered":{"haiku":"Intoxication as resistance to policing consciousness; substance becomes liberation from social order.","opus":"A dark hymn to numbing out, coaxing a wounded soul through the flow of any substance that dulls it, sobriety and stupor slurring into the same word. Townsend builds it toward a howling surrender that reads less as celebration than exorcism of self-loathing.","sonnet":"A chaotic confession of intoxication and spiritual crisis in which the speaker cycles through states of stupor and sobriety, eventually demanding release from all inhibition — drugs, wine, weed — as a path to facing the self and some form of transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend-project~rejoice":{"haiku":"Transcendent celebration breaks through suffering and darkness; the narrator finds liberation through embracing cosmic joy and letting go.","opus":"Townsend reframes pain as the very thing worth embracing rather than fearing, pushing toward transcendence through the hurt. The refrain insists that what frightens us is exactly where the aliveness is, so meet it and rejoice.","sonnet":"The song addresses an \"alien world\" of arrogant, demented resistance to something larger — possibly spiritual surrender, possibly just letting go of ego — and the repeated command to \"rejoice\" is the answer to pain and fear: not denial of the hurt but refusal to let it be the final word.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend-project~sumeria":{"haiku":"Hunger and anger drive worship while collective consciousness demands sacrifice. Soma and honor mask the cost of ancient power.","opus":"A cosmic meditation on sacrifice, mortality, and a species that hungers for God through anger, weaving Sumerian myth and soma into a plea for love and acceptance while a lonely, childless narrator confronts what makes us merely animal.","sonnet":"A sprawling song about humanity's animal hunger for something transcendent — the narrator, who has never known love or family and accepts this with finality, addresses a collective unconscious that has weaponized spiritual longing to consolidate power, calling for love and acceptance while mourning how easily faith is bartered away.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~bastard-720p":{"haiku":"A person witnesses urban isolation and indifference where nobody cares about anyone's achievements, feeling old and exhausted in a grey, loveless city.","opus":"From Townsend's abrasive Strapping Young Lad-adjacent output, this sprawls through nocturnal urban desolation, grey people under a static sky, neon basements, the stink of piss by the docks, and a narrator aching that nobody cares what he has. Loneliness curdles into a bitter address to absent parents. Its wall-of-sound bleakness typifies his 1990s work channeling depression into overwhelming density.","sonnet":"A portrait of urban loneliness: grey people move through a city that offers nothing, while the narrator feels too exhausted to even pray. Underneath the neon and social noise is a deep cold — a plea not to be abandoned, and a resignation to time passing without tenderness.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~canada":{"haiku":"A traveler on Canadian highways finds temporary refuge in motion; the road becomes home, mediating between the need for connection and the solitude of endless driving.","opus":"Townsend's homesick road song, the endless prairie freeway standing in for a life spent waiting on others' approval before finally deciding to just be whatever he needs to be. Affection for a plain, vast country that quietly frees him.","sonnet":"A road-weary traveler drifts across the Canadian prairie, oscillating between needing approval to feel like himself and accepting that the landscape and the highway are enough of a home for now.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~canucklehead-demo":{"haiku":"Persistent forward motion becomes faith itself—everything is bearable when you refuse to stop moving, and surrender to the chaos with optimistic acceptance.","opus":"An early, scrappy Townsend pep-talk insisting the whole brutal mess of life is somehow beautiful and survivable if you just keep moving. He wears his Canadian earnestness openly, turning stubborn optimism into a small act of defiance.","sonnet":"Everything — even what the speaker wouldn't choose, even the evil people around them — is music and beauty if you turn off the radio and listen. An earnest, slightly goofy declaration that the whole damn thing is OK.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~celestial-signals-demo":{"haiku":"Transcendent awakening hints at transformation while acknowledging the impossibility of flight; the speaker asks if anyone will remain when falling inevitably arrives.","opus":"A gentle message to a delicate, star-like soul stuck between things, insisting there is nothing to be ashamed of in loving or in falling short. The pull between a burning world and the chance of merging into one turns even death into a move toward togetherness.","sonnet":"A tender, uncertain address to someone caught between worlds — the narrator finds them wherever they are, asks them to let go of shame, and they are both dying and becoming one through love in a world on fire.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~dimensions":{"haiku":"Fragmented perception struggles toward awakening while violence erupts; the speaker is shadow in the rain, caught between waiting and the need to fight.","opus":"A fractured, trance-like descent into altered consciousness, the city and the self dissolving as Townsend chants that we're only passing shadows. Beneath the psychedelic fragments runs a push to wake up before the violence of it all.","sonnet":"A fragmented, dissociative monologue in which the speaker identifies as 'a living detour' and 'shadows we are' — the recurring downtown chant and eruptions of 'violence' frame a psyche pushing toward awakening while remaining unmoored.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~earth-day":{"haiku":"Cynical meditation on environmental hypocrisy, birth, aging, and contrived morality; the song dismisses earnest platitudes and reveals entertainment as distraction from meaningful human conversation and truth.","opus":"Townsend's manic satire of eco-piety and self-importance, recycling slogans while sneering that music, activism, and human posturing are all just talking meat. Nihilism and sincerity crashing together into absurd, provocative noise.","sonnet":"A chaotic, darkly comic spiral through existential noise — environmental slogans, a confused encounter with God, the meaninglessness of human speech, and the conclusion that music is just entertainment and we're all just talking meat.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~evermore":{"haiku":"A soul cycles through reincarnation and transformation, turning the page on past lives while seeking wisdom and awakening.","opus":"Devin Townsend's cosmic acceptance of death as return rather than ending, the self dissolving into an eternal cycle. Beneath the sci-fi grandeur is a tender reassurance to a suffering loved one that nothing truly disappears.","sonnet":"A cosmic song about return and continuity — the self dissolves into time, pages turn on what was, but something essential always cycles back; the prayer is for arrival, awakening, and learning before the next pass through the infinite.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~farther-on-demo":{"haiku":"Waiting in mental limbo; freedom beckoning but unreachable without release from internal paralysis.","opus":"A demo about being called out of one's own paralyzed head by someone, and a coaxing promise that following the pull leads somewhere further and freer. Townsend frames movement itself as the only way out of stuck waiting.","sonnet":"Someone calls a waiting, possibly blind person out of their own stagnation — you're running away, pay the door — promising that if let free, they will return, and that whatever calls ahead simply leads farther on. The song is a gentle push toward movement.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~from-the-heart":{"haiku":"Genuine emotion flows directly from source toward receiver without filter or falsity. Heart pours forth its authentic content.","opus":"Townsend lays himself bare, admitting he wished his own heart away out of fear of pain, then reaching for love and devotion. The Hanuman chant turns the confession into something like prayer, contrition and surrender braided together.","sonnet":"Devotion stripped to its bare confession: a life spent wishing the heart away behind fear of pain, but the truth — \"I love her\" — keeps breaking through the Sanskrit chant and the unanswered questions about whether he's said enough, called enough, been sorry enough.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~funeral":{"haiku":"Death and grief overwhelm, collapsing into spring renewal and the speaker's isolation. A funeral becomes the occasion for recognizing what cannot be held or prevented.","opus":"Grief for a lost brother, Townsend threading Zeppelin's May-queen imagery through a rain-soaked wake. What's left when the person is gone is only language and no road home — mourning that words can't close.","sonnet":"A grief song delivered at the graveside of a brother — part elegy, part exhaustion — the speaker cannot hold on to what they had, is left wide awake and far from home, with nothing remaining but inadequate words and a cold funeral in the rain.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~genesis":{"haiku":"Acceptance of rebirth and surrender of ego; the speaker invites others to release self-imposed limitations and begin living authentically, acknowledging pain as part of existence.","opus":"Devin Townsend turns cosmic scale toward a very human plea, addressing someone convinced they'd be better off gone and urging them to start over from scratch. It reframes rebirth as accepting existence whole, joy and horror alike, and argues that isolating yourself inside your own head is the real trap while connection sustains us.","sonnet":"A plea for collective rebirth — addressing someone on the edge of suicide, the song insists that connection and vulnerability are the only path back, invoking creation mythology to argue that monsters and pain are part of a necessary beginning, not a reason to end.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~hide-nowhere":{"haiku":"Lost in the blur between dream and reality, hiding nowhere, going nowhere.","opus":"Townsend's oceanic dread, a mind that can't escape its own dreams staring into cold water for what it's meant to become. It's about having nowhere to run from your own head, sleep offering no shelter.","sonnet":"A mind saturated with cold, vast emptiness finds no escape from its own dreams and night — there is nowhere to hide from the self, nowhere to go, and yet the dreams keep coming exactly as they do, indifferent to the wish for shelter.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~higher":{"haiku":"Acceptance of internal darkness while resisting victimhood and calling for transcendence beyond inherited pain.","opus":"Townsend turns family brokenness and a dying world into an insistence on transcendence anyway, the destroyer-self refusing martyrdom and demanding you craft your own life upward rather than let despair grind you down.","sonnet":"About breaking through brokenness toward something elevated — a fragmented, broken person or relationship is the starting point, and the narrator pushes upward despite being low, damaged, and not yet ready. The line about not being a martyr for love or the father is a refusal to be sacrificed for others' comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~hyperdrive":{"haiku":"Pursues new connection and meaning across vast distances, seeking love and redemption through change and journey. Ends with arrival of an unexpected visitor.","opus":"Part of Townsend's Ziltoid space-opera world, it floats a warm, catchy plea for love and new beginnings, cold riversong nights giving way to apology and hope, before spoken narration announces Captain Spectacular and Ziltoid arriving in the cosmos. The tonal whiplash between sincere pop melody and cartoon sci-fi is pure Devin Townsend, sentiment and absurdity coexisting without apology.","sonnet":"Cold isolation alongside a tentative opening toward renewal: the narrator navigates an interior landscape where apology and love and finding a new way coexist uneasily. The cosmic interlude arrives like a perspective shift — enormity recontextualizing the small, cold night.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~into-the-sun-demo":{"haiku":"Relentless forward momentum toward transformation and collective liberation, driving toward culmination where dreams become permanent.","opus":"A radiant, forward-driving hymn to letting go and riding toward light and home, dreams of old carried rather than abandoned. Townsend fuses road-trip release with a near-devotional oneness, moving toward the sun as its own reward.","sonnet":"A road-trip toward renewal — water filling eyes, energy fed to something that can never be taken away, the road cycling back again and again toward the sun. 'Om we are, all we are' frames the momentum as spiritual rather than physical, home always ahead.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~lexus-demo":{"haiku":"Dangerous excess and mortality through careless velocity, oscillating between exhilaration and dread as destructive motion promises transcendence or death.","opus":"A reckless night in the city shadowed by the sense it could end in death, prayer and self-destruction running side by side. Townsend circles the fragile line between being fully alive and throwing that life away for a thrill.","sonnet":"Reckless city-night energy where speed and death press close together — motors carrying away, night sweeping with reigning death, terrifying people in the streets. The question 'are you loving alive' cuts through the chaos as both alarm and invitation.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~life":{"haiku":"Life's inevitable end approaches and transforms perspective on existence, where death levels all distinctions and individuality.","opus":"A reflection on facing mortality and owning one's limited time, urging living fully on your own terms rather than fading away. It repeats the reassurance of meeting again on the other side, while noting death levels everyone to the same ground.","sonnet":"A meditation on the awareness that life is finite: the speaker urges living on one's own terms, resisting the pull to simply let time slip away, and frames the eventual reckoning with death as something to be faced rather than fled.","src":"haiku","fable":"Devin Townsend frames a seize-the-day anthem with two spoken bookends that quietly gut it: death flattens king and pauper to one grade, then demotes the human further to just another creature in the mud. Inside that frame the first-person voice clutches its own span as the single possession worth defending, and the reunion promise sounds like consolation until the bookends reveal the far shore as ordinary dirt, not paradise. The pivot is the bridge, where the euphoric will-this-endure question inverts into a countdown toward the finish and names the real subject — not mortality but the jolt we apparently require before we consent to actually live. It is the Ocean Machine register, cosmic dread threaded under widescreen sound, spending the certainty of the end to make an ordinary existence feel urgent instead of owed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","freedom & escape","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"mortality's certainty spent to make ordinary life feel owed rather than urgent","built":"spoken bookends quietly gut the anthem they frame"},
"devin-townsend~night-720p":{"haiku":"A traveler drives through Japan at night nursing internal conflict—torn between loving and hating someone while struggling to keep himself from falling apart.","opus":"Townsend sets jetlagged numbness against neon streets while cruising through Japan after dark, faking composure while homesickness gnaws. At the core is a love turned contradictory, adoring someone beyond words yet resenting them constantly, with rain and moonlight nudging him toward a nearness he can't quite name.","sonnet":"Driving through Japan at night while pretending composure, a touring musician feels the distance from home as physical pain. Love and hatred toward the same person coexist in the same breath. Rain falls. The approach of something — resolution, breakdown, homecoming — feels closer without becoming clear. The road keeps going.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~offer-your-light":{"haiku":"Addressing adversity and fear with spiritual presence and illumination as the path through suffering.","opus":"A mantra for turning toward your own worst feelings instead of away, offering compassion to your fear and fury as the way through, Townsend's hard-won gospel that the darkness is met with light, not denial.","sonnet":"A mantra-like song about turning toward your own fear and pain rather than away from it — \"offer your light\" to the worst of what you're feeling is the repeated instruction; it's gentle, almost therapeutic, an encouragement to face anger and worry without running.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~secret-sciences":{"haiku":"Powerlessness and fear persist despite clarity and self-awareness; the speaker cycles through strength and weakness while seeking escape through mystical understanding.","opus":"A raw account of anxiety and powerlessness, waking afraid and unable to tell whether the fears are even real, then finding clarity in the weakness itself. The repeated command to let it go is the hard-won practice that keeps demons sleeping.","sonnet":"A person working through anxiety and irrational fear, recognizing in a moment of clarity that their own reactions are the problem, and choosing to let go of everything that is tearing them apart.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~seventh-wave":{"haiku":"A person runs away repeatedly seeking home, eventually realizing they're alone. The song suggests watching the seventh wave with the knowledge that solitude is inescapable yet somehow connecting all people.","opus":"A meditation on cosmic change, solitude and the search for home, weaving in Tennyson's lines about the earth's endless transformation; a figure runs away hoping to find home while the narrator waits for the ocean to rise as it did before, comforted by the paradox that even alone, we are never truly alone.","sonnet":"A song about solitude and the cyclical patience of the ocean — a woman runs toward home through changing weather while the narrator waits for the tide to rise as it always has before, finding consolation in the idea that even alone you are never truly isolated from the larger rhythm.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~sister":{"haiku":"Words fail as the speaker stands before sister with nothing to say or do. Silence contains all emotion too large for articulation.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~stars":{"haiku":"Transcendent love witnessing immortal ascension; staying the course despite fear and approaching light.","opus":"Townsend casts love as something immortal glimpsed in the night sky, a call to stay the course through dread until morning. The fear of light after dark becomes the very thing to be endured for the sake of the ones you love.","sonnet":"The speaker sees a lost loved one in the stars tonight, caught between anticipating evil that never arrives and feeding an internal fire anyway. A warrior's stance — stay the course, you've stayed through worse — held against the pull of grief and the fear that follows the night.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~stormbending":{"haiku":"Supernatural control over chaos and elements; the speaker commands weather and transforms natural forces through will.","opus":"A tender reckoning with memory and self-deception, time waved off as a human invention while a rainy Chinatown past keeps lingering. Under its calm swell, Townsend boils it all down to the simple offer of being loved.","sonnet":"A figure who has been drowning — \"below the waves\" — is addressed by someone offering the simple, radical gift of being loved, framing time itself as a construct that need not trap them. The rainy Chinatown memory grounds the tenderness in the specific rather than the abstract, and the repeated plea to \"let it fly\" urges the soul toward release rather than continued self-deception.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~support-the-cause-demo":{"haiku":"Redemption through collective acceptance and self-forgiveness, the past's hold diminishes as transformation requires releasing shame.","opus":"A rousing call to let go of who you were, forgive yourself and move forward as everything changes. Townsend frames loss as something absorbed into a larger purpose, the reassurance that you'll be okay carrying the whole song.","sonnet":"An exhortation to collective forward motion through change — the winds of change are blowing, the past is buried, and what's ahead requires forgiveness of yourself before you can join the march to the shore. The refrain 'you're OK' is spoken outward but sounds self-directed.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~time-overload-demo":{"haiku":"Temporal overwhelm and existential dread at the weight of present moment compressed by fear, drowning in the present tense.","opus":"Time itself becomes a lover and a tormentor, overwhelming and inescapable while a frightened world fills its emptiness with fear. Townsend wrestles with the dread that none of this is truly real and whether to surrender to that horror.","sonnet":"Time itself as romantic obsession and existential overwhelm — wanting to belong to it, being crushed by it, recognizing that fear underlies every easy accommodation to the world's horror. 'You give me time overload' names the condition without offering any relief from it.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~transcendence":{"haiku":"Refusing to follow false promises of love or lies, asserting autonomous transcendence beyond seduction.","opus":"Drawing a hard line against following lies or even love blindly, the singer claims transcendence as a personal act of will, refusing to be led and daring the listener to rise past the same illusions.","sonnet":"About refusing to be pulled back into lies and false certainties — the narrator sees clearly, declines to follow either deceit or easy love, and challenges himself and others to transcend the manipulations they're surrounded by. The repetition of \"who transcends this?\" is both a challenge and a claim.","src":"haiku"},
"devin-townsend~victim-demo":{"haiku":"Everything is violent and threatening in the present moment, yet the speaker feels alive and ready as a superstar. There is no pain, only transcendent acceptance of perpetual chaos.","opus":"A breathless mantra fixes everything into an overwhelming present, each threat and violence hammered as happening right now while the narrator declares readiness and a strange painless elation, casting himself as a spaceship superstar. Devin Townsend, the restless Canadian polymath known for maximalist walls of sound, channels sensory overload into manic euphoria, a demo sketching adrenaline pushed to the edge of dissociation.","sonnet":"A figure in a state of heightened, almost ecstatic readiness surrenders to forces larger than himself — violence, urgency, the present moment — and finds not dread but electricity. The repeated declaration of aliveness shades into grandiosity, a spaceship superstar who converts universal threat into pure, unqualified launch energy.","src":"haiku"},
"devora~body-bag-romes-remix":{"haiku":"A spurned lover transforms from adoration to vengeance, burning bridges and rising from abuse. They celebrate their own comeback after being discarded and tormented.","opus":"A revenge fantasy against a former lover who abused and betrayed the narrator; she vows to bury them and ride off victorious. The bravado masks a mutually destructive love that was doomed from the start, and her only comeback from being pushed into the flames is to burn her ex in return.","sonnet":"A revenge fantasy directed at an ex who pushed her past her limits — she's done praying and pleading, and now she wants them destroyed and left behind for good. The emotional core is a violent reclaiming of power after being humiliated and controlled.","src":"haiku"},
"devour-the-day~worse-than-death":{"opus":"The slow agony of a relationship's collapse with no closure in sight, watching what you built burn and finding that worse than a clean end. Numb with shame, he keeps circling the same unanswerable question of why he can't fix it.","src":"opus"},
"diablo-swing-orchestra~exit-strategy-of-a-wrecking-ball":{"haiku":"Rage and replacement overturn romantic devotion; the speaker transforms love into weapon, erasing the beloved to purge their memory and reclaim autonomy.","opus":"A scorched-earth breakup dressed as conquest, one lover mobilizing armies and erasure to purge the other from their mind. The theatrical vengeance barely conceals how much of the ruin was self-inflicted.","sonnet":"A speaker who once tried to heal someone they loved now arrives as pure destruction — bearing the scars of the other's disease, demanding they come clean, and determined to erase and replace them, driven by furious, impatient need to get that person out of their mind.","src":"haiku"},
"diablo~grace-under-pressure":{"opus":"The Finnish metal band frames suffering as a forge, a man reborn through pain who refuses to stare into emptiness. Facing his own erasure, he chooses to navigate out by living from the heart, finding poise inside the storm.","src":"opus"},
"diamond-construct~jynx":{"opus":"A furious kiss-off to someone who lied, stole and left lasting damage, the narrator finally cutting them out and reclaiming himself. The recurring torment is why he still carries their guilt at all, and the answer is to stop.","src":"opus"},
"diamond-construct~psychosis":{"haiku":"Spiraling mental breakdown and addiction render each day identical and unbearable. The speaker cycles between self-destruction and denial of their psychological crisis.","opus":"A spiraling account of addiction and self-ruin, the narrator fed up with a numbingly repetitive existence, drinking, unraveling, sneering at his own life as a movie he can't stand. The Australian metalcore band drive electronic-tinged aggression under the psychosis hook, its warning that time won't hold turning collapse into a defiant refusal to be judged.","sonnet":"Drinking through a mental breakdown while refusing to be judged for it — each day the same slow disintegration, the caller on the line only making things worse, the instinct toward self-destruction cycling back around. The speaker acknowledges the psychosis and then dares anyone to condemn them for surviving it however they can.","src":"haiku"},
"dido~thank-you":{"haiku":"One person's simple presence rescues you from despair; gratitude for a reason to stay.","opus":"A day where everything goes wrong made bearable by the memory of one person, ordinary domestic tenderness weighed against a grey, defeated morning. The gratitude is quiet and enormous, love as the thing that makes the misery survivable.","sonnet":"Against a backdrop of mundane disasters — cold tea, missed buses, bad days — a lover's presence transforms everything into the best day of life; gratitude is the whole point, and proximity to this person makes even a crumbling house feel like enough.","src":"haiku"},
"die-spitz~punishers":{"opus":"Longing dressed as resignation, wanting one night inside someone's world while already sensing it won't go right. The Austin punks keep it tender and doomed, spending everything on a connection they expect to wake up alone from.","src":"opus"},
"die-spitz~riding-with-my-girls":{"opus":"A blast of young, working-life escape, trading the dead loop of clocking in for the open road and the loyalty of friends. The Austin band turns girlhood solidarity into pure momentum, bigger dreams as their only argument.","src":"opus"},
"die-spitz~throw-yourself-to-the-sword":{"opus":"A snarling assertion of dominance and refusal to be diminished, daring an obsessed rival to surrender. The Austin punks weaponize swagger and sexual menace into a stance against anyone who'd tell a woman she has no backbone.","src":"opus"},
"digital-daggers~bad-intentions":{"haiku":"Identity fractures under the weight of dark impulses; the speaker cannot resist destructive urges and accepts the role of villain, poisoning every connection while playing the victim.","opus":"A confession from someone who has stopped pretending to be the wronged party and accepts they are the villain, inviting the other to pull the trigger and let the harm run its course. Guilt and desire fuse into a refusal to fight the darker impulses that define what they've become.","sonnet":"Someone confronts the gap between their actions and their identity, insisting they are not defined by their past — \"I am not what I've done, what I've become\" — while the smoking gun of those deeds looms, and a self-destructive willingness to absorb blame surfaces: \"just pull the trigger, I'll take the pain.\"","src":"haiku"},
"digitalism~2-hearts":{"haiku":"Two incompatible hearts cannot sustain a relationship despite one partner's hopelessly romantic love and the other's effort to stay. Their union is doomed from the beginning.","opus":"A pairing that comes on too fast, one person head over heels while the other looks on, vowing to remain despite sensing it can't survive. Warmth and cool remove sit side by side. Digitalism, a Hamburg electro duo from the late-2000s dance-punk moment, wrap this uncertain romance in driving synths, letting affection and chilly reserve throb against each other.","sonnet":"Two people mismatch in love — one hopelessly devoted, the other clear-eyed enough to stay anyway. The more grounded partner watches the other spiral, absorbs the chaos, and quietly insists on remaining. The relationship will not last, yet the steadier heart refuses to leave, holding vigil over someone who mistakes the moon for the sun.","src":"haiku"},
"digitalism~forrest-gump":{"haiku":"Perpetual running without destination defines existence; the speaker runs under directionless compulsion while romance collapses into arranged manipulation.","opus":"An electro-rock chase built on flight as the only clean option, with a woman scripting a romance that's really a setup and voices urging the runner on; the title borrows the movie's simplest wisdom, that when it all breaks down you just run.","sonnet":"A fragmented portrait of someone who keeps running — from manipulation, from dead ends, from a person who sets them up — the running itself becoming the only constant in a disorienting series of encounters and escapes.","src":"haiku"},
"digitalism~pogo":{"haiku":"The speaker longs for excitement and freedom, noticing something promising emerging. They invite someone to dance and celebrate brightness after a long period of stagnation.","opus":"The German electro duo Digitalism chase a restless nocturnal charge, sensing something in the air as tame old routines give way to the promise of a wild night. Mess, boredom, and a cluttered room set up the yearning to escape into fire and excess with a lover who could ignite it. The chant about getting wasted turns anticipation itself into the party's engine.","sonnet":"Restlessness at home shakes loose into anticipation of a night out. Something electric hangs in the air — the memory of wild, uninhibited times and the prospect of recapturing that brightness. A person on the verge of departure feels the pull of shared energy and reckless joy.","src":"haiku"},
"digitalism~the-pulse":{"opus":"Built on the B-52's, it turns the compulsion to dance into a comic affliction, a beat so infectious it wrecks composure and annoys the neighbors. The electro duo's joke is that the groove is both nuisance and irresistible pleasure.","src":"opus"},
"digitalism~wolves":{"haiku":"Two travelers persist through darkness and danger, guided by inner light that prevents them from losing their way. The song expresses endurance and companionship amid external threat.","opus":"Roads bend and blur as two figures fade into the breeze, casting off old bindings and swearing not to redo old errors. Amid a pack's cries they find quiet, trusting a beacon to guide them back without straying. Digitalism, the German electronic duo, swell this getaway into an anthem, turning flight from the familiar into a shared push forward.","sonnet":"Two people flee a constrained past together, the road ahead dissolving into wind. Howling wolves mark the pressure bearing down, but a shared light keeps them oriented. The song is a compact survival pact — that forward movement together, however uncertain the route, will always lead them home.","src":"haiku"},
"dimlim~rijin":{"haiku":"Identity dissolves into questioning and dissociation, the self fragmenting across unreality where even existence becomes uncertain and bodily sensation offers the only tether.","opus":"A dissociative crisis of identity: the speaker questions whether he is even real or alive, adrift in a blurred, whitening reality, hoping that once he falls asleep his eyes will never open again.","sonnet":"The speaker is trapped inside the question \"who am I?\" — feeling the pulse of their own body as the only proof of existence, watching reality bleach away, dissolving into awareness without identity and wishing not to wake from sleep.","src":"haiku"},
"dimmu-borgir~interdimensional-summit":{"haiku":"Illusion and time blind perception; enlightenment reveals meaninglessness, and physical death liberates from the illusion of temporal life.","opus":"Symphonic black metal as gnostic sermon: a seer piercing the veil of illusion and time to grasp that life continues after death and nothing is coincidence. Enlightenment framed as escape from a rigged, deceiving reality.","sonnet":"A mystical-philosophical text about consciousness breaking free from the illusions of consensus reality — the trained seer travels beyond the veil to recognize that life only makes sense after death, and that we are imprisoned stars who will only be liberated at the final moment.","src":"haiku"},
"dimmu-borgir~progenies-of-the-great-apocalypse":{"haiku":"The speaker claims superiority as an unflinching warrior unafraid of mediocrity, seeking to claim sovereignty and reject grace in a righteous, violent pursuit.","opus":"A Nietzschean-Satanic manifesto of an elite few who embrace their animal nature and reject the mediocrity and god-worship of the herd. Framed as a war behind enemy lines, it exalts self-deification, hunting down the weak and claiming a rightful throne of power.","sonnet":"A grandiose, Social-Darwinist declaration from those who see themselves as a superior kind destined to seize power — rejecting mediocrity, rejecting external gods, and claiming the throne through total war.","src":"haiku"},
"dimmu-borgir~the-unveiling":{"haiku":"Hidden knowledge lies beneath illusions the world believes real; the speaker exposes the dreamworld's deceptions while showing how confusion keeps people blind and disoriented.","opus":"The Norwegian symphonic black-metal act reach for occult grandeur, framing enlightenment as knowledge won by rising past secrecy and deceit. Obscured truths lie beneath dark waters that the supposedly wise have neglected, while a veil conceals an illusory world everyone accepts as real. Their orchestral bombast fuels a call to expose forbidden understanding hidden under comfortable delusion.","sonnet":"A call to lift the veil of civilizational deception: knowledge is hidden, the dreamworld is mistaken for reality, and the modern mind has abandoned the soul. Clarity is not given but earned by descending into murk — the unveiling is an act of resistance against a world built on illusion.","src":"haiku"},
"dimmu-borgir~theric":{"haiku":"To govern oneself requires confronting one's darkness and past rather than accepting the false light of external voices; all rises and falls are internal movements.","opus":"Symphonic black-metal titans Dimmu Borgir frame self-mastery as a dark rite: to rule oneself, one must confront one's own shadow and history. Looking backward is deemed corrupted, learning a cage that requires dying to be reborn. Bending substance through sheer will, the narrator weathers all adversity, folding esoteric self-command into the genre's grand blasphemous theater.","sonnet":"Self-mastery requires confronting what lies in shadow. Inherited certainties and retrospective justifications dissolve as the only viable path to governing oneself proves to pass directly through darkness and a reckoning with the past. All things rise and fall within the internal world.","src":"haiku"},
"dio~born-on-the-sun":{"haiku":"Shattered ideals and upside-down worlds drive someone toward defiant survival; fire and heat fuel transformation as a gypsy offers hellish passage and the person claims birthright in divine burning.","opus":"Ronnie James Dio's late rallying cry to someone made of fire, urging them to run and burn rather than shrink into anonymity to survive. His signature theme, choosing wild selfhood over safe conformity.","sonnet":"An encouragement to someone whose world has crumbled: you're made of fire, not conformity — stop hiding in circles and run with the wild, because you were born for something burning and free.","src":"haiku"},
"dio~one-night-in-the-city":{"haiku":"Two outsiders share transgressive connection within urban night; chains cannot bind dreams, and darkness harbors unexpected beauty.","opus":"Dio's fairy-tale grammar of dark children and princesses turned onto street kids drawn into a leather-and-lace nightlife, where the glamour is a cage and one always escapes while another stays to be consumed.","sonnet":"Two archetypal outsiders — a dark child full of restless energy and a princess chosen but constrained — find each other in the city at night and slip free together into a shared space of wonder and escape, though one eventually stays and one moves on.","src":"haiku"},
"dio~rainbow-in-the-dark":{"haiku":"Lightning and shadows conceal the hidden self while memories and demons lurk in darkness, yet beauty and magic persist unseen.","opus":"Written just after Dio was pushed out of Sabbath, this is his loneliest song, isolation as a bright thing stranded in blackness, chasing a magic that keeps curdling into fear, never found.","sonnet":"A solitary figure lost in darkness and self-doubt — invisible, rootless, living only in a flash of isolated colour against a lightless sky — questions whether the demons ever release their grip and whether morning will ever come for those left alone.","src":"haiku"},
"dio~we-rock":{"haiku":"Musicians and artists carry a shared spiritual mission to transcend mundane existence, finding meaning in collective performance and expression.","opus":"Dio's arena creed, casting the band and their audience as the ones who truly see through the killers and false prophets, with rock itself as the faith worth carrying on. Metal as communion and defiance rolled together.","sonnet":"An audience-facing affirmation that performers and their crowd share something beyond religion or ideology — a collective force that sailing, singing, and enduring hardship together embodies. The song is a communal declaration of identity through music.","src":"haiku"},
"diplo~revolution":{"haiku":"Monsters of internal doubt dissolve before love, and the downtrodden rise to reclaim their light against a world designed to steal it.","opus":"A festival anthem reframing personal upheaval as liberation, telling the beaten-down not to let anyone dim them because change is always possible. Pop uplift built for a crowd to shout back at itself.","sonnet":"A broadly optimistic rallying cry aimed at fallen or fearful people, insisting the worst has passed and that love defeats the internal monsters. The revolution here is personal and emotional, not political — a breakthrough out of a dark night.","src":"haiku"},
"dir-en-grey~akuro-no-oka":{"haiku":"Lamenting a lost connection that defined a past life, mourning the inability to return to that moment and place.","opus":"On the Hill of Akuro two lovers who can no longer act or love anyone gaze back at a shared past, a suicidal vow of reunion in death and rebirth, promising to search for the other across lifetimes on ground where flowers wither and long to bloom once more.","sonnet":"A song about a love that ended in loss — possibly death — with the narrator now unable to love anyone, watching from the sky as the beloved waits on the hill they once shared: the flower that once bloomed wants to bloom beautifully just once more, but the narrator is sinking deeper, and promises to find the person again in a future reincarnation.","src":"haiku"},
"dir-en-grey~keibetsu-to-hajimari":{"haiku":"Persecution breeds contempt for self while demanding recognition, the narrator claiming victimhood as strength through refusal to be erased by systems designed to erase.","opus":"Scorn turned inward and outward at once: the speaker insists this wretched life is all he has, daring others to laugh and look down on him, and repeatedly wishing only for his own death as the way to end the persecution.","sonnet":"A speaker who has nothing but the way he lives refuses to feel pitied, directing contempt back at those who look down on him; beneath the defiance is a hunger for his own worth and a suicidal admission that the persecution — his own or others' — never ends.","src":"haiku"},
"dir-en-grey~ningen-wo-kaburu":{"haiku":"Spiritual degradation and human devouring; questioning identity in a world consumed by corruption.","opus":"Dir En Grey stitch self-loathing and self-harm into a meditation on wearing humanity like a borrowed mask — asking whose rules, whose sake one even lives for, when a blade feels more honest than a face. Kyo's bleak deliverance is to stop wanting altogether: a strange blessing in letting the heart go numb rather than keep bleeding for reasons that were never yours.","sonnet":"Fragments of a wounded self sewn together into a mass of hatred — the self-harm cutting message, the blade passed from a hood as thirty centimeters of reality — circle around the central question: whose rules are you living by, who are you living for, and why am I living at all; the final lines dissolve into the wish never to wake again.","src":"haiku"},
"dir-en-grey~oboro":{"haiku":"Guilt for harming someone through counterfeit love and the paralysis of witnessing another's pain without power.","opus":"A wracked confession of failing to protect someone, of loving with a counterfeit love, guilt clawing the heart raw as memory returns; the beauty dissolves into blur and tears while an apology soaks into everything, too weak and too late.","sonnet":"A song of guilt and helplessness from someone — likely a mother or caregiver — who could not protect a girl (possibly a daughter, possibly herself) from harm: every time the memory surfaces, claws rake at the heart; she asks for forgiveness, knowing the love she offered was a simulacrum of love, and the image of blood seeping into a tatami mat suggests abuse or violent death.","src":"haiku"},
"dir-en-grey~ochita-koto-no-aru-sora-radio-edit-ver":{"haiku":"A chaotic rant against meaninglessness and suffocation—the world makes no sense; you can't breathe in it, yet you still cling to your own future.","opus":"Dir En Grey spent their career pushing visual-kei toward extreme metal and unflinching subject matter, and here Kyo circles the Hiroshima bombing of August 6 in fragmented, barely coherent lines. The refusal of clean meaning is deliberate: children born into contaminated existence, a narrator who knows where he should go but cannot move. The emotional core is paralysis inherited from catastrophe — guilt, grief, and the impossibility of justifying a future in the world that did that.","sonnet":"A fragmented, barely coherent song circling the atomic bombing of Hiroshima on August 6 — there is no meaning anywhere in a world that poisons its own future, children are being born into contaminated existence, and the narrator knows where they should go but cannot move. The emotional core is the paralysis of inherited catastrophe: guilt, grief, and the impossibility of justifying a future in a world that did that.","src":"haiku"},
"dir-en-grey~rasetsukoku":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic judgment of humanity's cycle of violence and moral failure; people are bound by inherent sin and incapable of genuine change, condemned to repeat cruelty eternally like demons in hell.","opus":"A descent into the Buddhist land of the rakshasa, humanity abandoning itself to fury until every soul becomes a demon and the world collapses into carnage. Kyo's dense classical Japanese makes damnation feel like a national fate, a realm of asura with no way back.","sonnet":"Japan — or civilization broadly — is cast as a demon realm where decorum and human nature have rotted beyond recovery: a society of nightmarching demons, citizens laughed at on guillotines, everyone who once had a conscience now dissolved into the chaos, past the point where the collapse can be undone.","src":"haiku"},
"dir-en-grey~the-world-of-mercy":{"haiku":"Society masks hypocrisy behind false compassion, keeping citizens enslaved through crowd control and manufactured morality while individuals perform emptiness.","opus":"A harrowing plea to be let into the circle while the true, gutted self stays hidden, indicting a society that looks away and calls mob cruelty living. Dir En Grey brand this so-called world of mercy as pure hypocrisy, urging one to bleed and find real freedom.","sonnet":"A voice nobody truly knows or sees asks to be let in, reaching for connection while the world around it runs on hypocrisy and collective cruelty — mob violence as proof of feeling alive — until the cry is to break free from that rotten world and seek one's own freedom even in a land of corpses.","src":"haiku"},
"dir-en-grey~zan":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"A psychotic murder rendered in visceral color, killing someone who smiles even at the end and clinging to that final memory. The looped word for slaughter and the death-trap refrain make cruelty itself the subject, dizzying and unrepentant.","sonnet":"A body covered in saliva, pale and cold, pressed against the field of vision as a twisted cruel painting — a short life dancing to its end with a final memory of a laughing face being killed, while the mind churns in psychotic loops.","src":"haiku"},
"dire-straits~brothers-in-arms":{"haiku":"Soldiers bound by shared trauma and survival refuse to abandon each other despite the destruction of war and inevitable separation.","opus":"Knopfler's elegy from the perspective of a dying soldier, mourning that men divided by borders and different worlds still share one Earth and slaughter each other over it. Antiwar grief that stays quiet and devastating.","sonnet":"A soldier on foreign mountains reflects on the senselessness of war, the loyalty that survives it, and the certainty that all men die. The only real world, shared by enemies and comrades alike, makes warfare between brothers a profound absurdity.","src":"haiku"},
"dire-straits~money-for-nothing":{"haiku":"Capitalist resentment masks class anxiety; the speaker envies rock stardom's wealth while contempting its superficiality and ease.","opus":"A blue-collar appliance mover's envious, bigoted rant at the pampered rock stars he watches on MTV, marveling that they get money for nothing while he hauls refrigerators. The song ventriloquizes his resentment, ironizing the very stardom that made it a hit.","sonnet":"A working-class delivery man, moving refrigerators and colour TVs, watches rock stars on MTV and seethes with envious contempt: they play guitar, do nothing, and get paid — while he and his crew haul appliances for a living, the irony thick with resentment and a dash of self-aware admission that he should have learned to play.","src":"haiku"},
"dirtyphonics~teleportation-the-prototypes-remix":{"haiku":"Teleportation technology is presented as a transformative force that could fundamentally reshape civilization and alter the course of human destiny.","opus":"The French drum-and-bass and dubstep crew, here remixed by the Prototypes, hangs its drop on a single spoken conceit: teleportation as a civilization-altering technology capable of changing everything and reshaping nations. The words serve as pure hype and payload, a sci-fi promise repeated to detonate the beat rather than to develop any narrative beyond raw sonic momentum.","src":"haiku"},
"discharge~hear-nothing-see-nothing-say-nothing":{"opus":"A stark hardcore blast at being deceived, menaced, and swindled by those with power, marched down dead-end routes with no real choice. Its counterpart target is an ordinary public that stays deliberately deaf and mute rather than answer back.","sonnet":"A two-sentence condemnation of political complicity — citizens are lied to, manipulated, and led into dead ends by their rulers, yet respond only with passivity and silence, the song functioning as a minimalist punk indictment of collective blindness.","src":"sonnet"},
"disclosure~f-for-you":{"haiku":"The singer describes being infected by someone's lies and infidelity, repeatedly calling themselves a fool for believing in a deceitful person. The repetition emphasizes the cycle of betrayal and delusion.","opus":"A dance track built on the sting of betrayal, the narrator infected by a lover's lies and whispers and left feeling like a fool, the hurt looped hypnotically until it becomes the groove itself.","sonnet":"About being betrayed by someone whose words were lies from the start — the narrator played the fool, got infected by their deception, and the song is the bare acknowledgment of having been made an idiot by someone they trusted. Spare and repetitive, the lyrical minimalism mirrors the stunned aftermath of betrayal.","src":"haiku"},
"disco-lines~rweok":{"opus":"A house track that loops one anxious question about whether a relationship survives, love itself blamed for the drift. The endless repetition of the reassurance becomes the point, a comfort worn thin until it sounds like doubt.","src":"opus"},
"discoveries~nightcrawlers":{"opus":"A metalcore image of being lost in a desert of one's own mind, crawling blind through mistakes toward a self that keeps slipping away. Dreaming is framed as the only step forward even as time hunts like a ghost.","src":"opus"},
"discrepancies~hands-up":{"haiku":"From innocent beginnings to hardened survival, the speaker rises through acknowledgement and determination, refusing to remain invisible.","opus":"A rise-up anthem about fighting free of a rough neighborhood through grind and talent, demanding to be taken seriously while brushing aside comparisons to the acts that shaped them. It's about hauling a whole scene upward, a creation of the entertainment world converting recognition and self-belief into momentum.","sonnet":"A rap about proving artistic legitimacy against critics who dismiss the band as derivative, asserting they are products of their environment who genuinely built something worthy rather than just copying sounds.","src":"haiku"},
"dishammer~smoke-of-death":{"opus":"Blackened crust-metal painted in broad apocalyptic strokes, raiders under a full moon riding a road to ruin. There's no argument here, just the primal thrill of doom and decay chanted like a battle sigil.","src":"opus"},
"dishwalla~angels-or-devils":{"haiku":"Moral ambiguity paralyzes choice between celestial and demonic paths; demons and angels burn equally inside.","opus":"Watching someone fight their inner demons and trying to name what's good and what's destructive in them, worried love won't be enough. The angels-and-devils framing is really about whether people can be saved from themselves.","sonnet":"A speaker at a personal breaking point tries to distinguish what is real and what is corrupting in a relationship, aware that both angels and devils burn inside each person and that failing to fight the demons risks turning cold entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"dishwalla~counting-blue-cars":{"haiku":"Spiritual seeking frames childhood wonder; mortality and meaning crystallize through innocent inquiry.","opus":"A child's afternoon questions about God become the whole point, and the song's quiet provocation is imagining the divine as female, a curiosity so innocent it slipped a radical idea onto nineties radio. Wonder untouched by dogma.","sonnet":"A child and adult walk together on a late afternoon, the child asking earnest questions about God — imagined as feminine and approachable — while the adult is reminded how naturally children reach for the sacred in the everyday.","src":"haiku"},
"dismemberment-plan~a-life-of-possibilities":{"haiku":"The isolation of endless choice and drift, confused by reconnection when all connection feels unreachable and estranged.","opus":"Withdrawal imagined as burrowing underground where no one can reach you, then the dread of resurfacing among strangers who may not remember or want you, the cost of a life of open possibility being the loneliness it drags you through.","sonnet":"About the cost of disappearing into yourself — the narrator burrows underground metaphorically, retreating from everyone who cares, and when they finally surface they find they're a stranger to the world; a life of possibilities sounds freeing but leaves you unknown and disconnected.","src":"haiku"},
"disperse~bubbles":{"haiku":"A person doubts their own breath and grounding while shaping dreams, trapped by taking things too seriously and questioning their self-trust.","opus":"Polish prog act Disperse wrap anxious introspection in fluid, atmospheric musicianship. The narrator feels his footing constantly shifting, retreating into a private invented world while taking everything said to heart, and describes himself as someone who can barely function, doubting even his own survival. It's a portrait of self-doubt so deep that basic living feels uncertain.","sonnet":"A person observes the world through a distorting distance, treating even ordinary words with too much gravity and doubting his own capacity to function. Self-trust has eroded so thoroughly that breathing itself feels uncertain. The tension sits between finding enough reason to stay and the sheer difficulty of moving when every step is shadowed by internal suspicion.","src":"haiku"},
"disperse~enigma-of-abode":{"opus":"The Polish prog band urge a stop to blind running, a call to open one's eyes to the madness around and then turn inward, tending the body as a temple where hidden goodness lives. Drawing on cyclical, almost spiritual imagery of returning to the child and uniting start and end, it is about self-knowledge as the path to a truer dimension of life.","sonnet":"A spiritual self-help lyric about stopping the frantic chase and turning inward — the song urges recognizing the goodness hidden within the self, nurturing the body as a temple, reading the signs nature provides, and cycling back to childlike openness in order to find life's true dimension.","src":"sonnet"},
"disperse~foreword":{"opus":"The Polish prog band opens on disillusionment with inherited stories and the noise of history, one voice endlessly chasing and popping the illusions another keeps making. The recurring girl by the river, sinner and savior, drifts toward something quietly ominous.","src":"opus"},
"disperse~stay":{"haiku":"A speaker fears abandonment if they reveal their true feelings, yet finds the world's beauty so overwhelming that connection seems to demand merger.","opus":"The Polish progressive band lets a fragile confession hang between two people, the speaker fearing that revealing true feeling might kill part of them as the other turns away. Grief walks alongside them, and when he turns back the companion is already gone. Yet the meditative arrangement resolves toward wonder, so much beauty in the world that his heart, and theirs, feels ready to burst.","sonnet":"Someone vulnerable extends their genuine feelings only to find the other person already turning away, leaving them alone with a world so saturated in beauty that the heart strains to contain it. Loss and wonder coexist in the same breath — grief at a specific departure against the overwhelming aliveness of everything around it.","src":"haiku"},
"disperse~surrender":{"haiku":"Two people strip away pretense and guilt, revealing their madness to each other until there's nothing left to hide.","opus":"A hushed tribute to mutual openness, two people baring their damage to each other with no shame and nothing withheld, feeling more the further they let their guards down. The Polish progressive rock band's airy, delicate playing suits this dissolving of defenses, casting surrender not as defeat but as a weightless act of being fully seen.","sonnet":"Mutual exposure as the condition of closeness — two people showing each other their full irrationality until it stops feeling safe, but in that shared unmasking they also see and feel more than they ever have. Surrender is the title but also the method: there was nothing left to hide, and that nakedness was both the gift and the limit.","src":"haiku"},
"disperse~universal-love":{"haiku":"All consciousness and matter connect through cosmic love; souls eternally cycle through manifold lives.","opus":"Polish prog band Disperse dissolve the individual self into cosmic oneness, imagining love as the underlying force that shapes matter and binds everything across all of space and time. Drawing on oceanic, mystical imagery, the song casts affection between souls as eternal and untouched by birth or death, love proposed as the very fabric of reality.","sonnet":"A meditation on cosmic interconnection — consciousness and love as properties of the universe itself rather than personal achievements. Souls navigate an expanding sea, neither born nor extinguished, always already woven into a single fabric that vibrates beneath all separation and division.","src":"haiku"},
"dissonants~malice-in-wonderland":{"haiku":"The narrator confronts someone's hypocrisy and refusal to act, watching dreams slip away. They refuse to waste their one life and see the other as dead inside, but swear not to let everything burn away.","opus":"Metalcore band Dissonants rail against a hypocrite who wants the speaker to give up everything while offering nothing in return. Watching his ambitions slip away, he'd sooner die than squander his one life, and answers the accusation that he's empty by countering that he at least made the effort. Contempt hardens into resolve as he refuses to let this destroy his shot.","sonnet":"A person confronts a manipulative partner whose hypocrisy and inaction have swallowed the speaker's dreams whole. The ultimatum is existential — the relationship either demands a soul as currency or dies, and the speaker, refusing to keep subsidizing the other's passivity, reaches the point of withdrawal.","src":"haiku"},
"distant~in-human-scum":{"opus":"A deathcore descent into becoming the very monstrousness one fought, tasting the weakness inside and embracing damnation. The Dutch band stages it as a corrupted rebirth, shedding mortality to rise as something no longer human.","src":"opus"},
"distinguisher~pointe-willow":{"opus":"A two-line confession of trying to seal painful memories away, only for them to break loose under the weight of everything he couldn't change.","src":"opus"},
"disturbed~another-way-to-die":{"haiku":"Human greed and environmental destruction create a slow apocalypse where species vanish and earth retaliates. The song questions whether redemption is possible.","opus":"A blunt environmental warning that pins melting ice and a fouled atmosphere on human greed and appetite, repeatedly asking whether we can change course before it's too late. The Chicago metal band, led by David Draiman, scales the plea to arena size, framing the planet's revenge and an unheard countdown with their signature thudding conviction.","sonnet":"A reckoning with environmental self-destruction framed as collective guilt — glaciers melt, species vanish, and the greed that drove all of it was never secret, just ignored. The refrain asks whether there is still time to repent while the evidence mounts that there may not be, and the song sits in the uncomfortable space between awareness and action already too late.","src":"haiku"},
"disturbed~are-you-ready":{"haiku":"Mental liberation demands breaking free from invisible control systems that erode autonomy and thought. Those who resist imposed limitations become forces their oppressors cannot contain.","opus":"A call to mental liberation, urging a listener whose focus and power have been quietly stripped to break free of the ones controlling them and become someone who can't be preyed upon.","sonnet":"A rallying call to collective awakening, urging listeners to shed mental shackles imposed by controlling forces and transform themselves into people who cannot be preyed upon — the revolution is an internal one before it is external.","src":"haiku"},
"disturbed~asylum":{"haiku":"Haunting obsession traps the speaker in grief's asylum; death and memory intertwine until present reality collapses into phantasmagoric torment.","opus":"Consuming grief after a loved one's death, the mourner tormented by loneliness, guilt and dread of joining them, trapped in a psychological asylum of his own making. The lines between mourning, madness and self-punishment blur as memory drags him toward her grave.","sonnet":"A speaker haunted by a loved one's death — \"don't you know I'm in love with you, and I wasn't ready\" — is dragged into the grave of grief and pathological fixation, terror coursing through every night, dreading sleep where they feel the loss again; the asylum is both internal madness and a final place to join the dead.","src":"haiku"},
"disturbed~crucified":{"haiku":"Obsessive love becomes torture; the speaker projects pain inward, seeking to bind the beloved through psychological crucifixion.","opus":"Abandonment flipped into a vow of psychological conquest, the jilted narrator promising to invade the other's mind until nothing of their old world survives, martyrdom weaponized so heartbreak reads as domination.","sonnet":"A speaker shattered by rejection and abandonment channels their grief into an obsessive determination to reclaim the person who left — promising to focus all pain directly at them until the past is erased and they become wholly possessed, while declaring themselves crucified by the loss.","src":"haiku"},
"disturbed~down-with-the-sickness":{"haiku":"Internal darkness awakens and spreads as an infectious demon; trauma surfaces in the dream state, revealing childhood abuse and rage.","opus":"A human side slowly gives way to a rising demon, the narrator kneeling as loathing curdles into rage and madness is embraced as a gift. A spoken passage exposes a childhood of maternal abuse fueling the eruption. Disturbed, breaking out of the early-2000s nu-metal surge, made this their signature, pairing primal vocal howls with a descent from victimhood into vengeful fury.","sonnet":"A man watches a violent, animalistic self surface and take over, framed partly through childhood trauma at the hands of an abusive mother. The sickness is both personal pathology and a force he turns outward in rage. The descent into madness is presented as an inheritance — something given, not chosen — that finally overwhelms the human remnant.","src":"haiku"},
"disturbed~fear":{"haiku":"Psychic paralysis perpetuates submissiveness; the narrator rejects manufactured innocence and hypnotic manipulation by embracing rebellious refusal.","opus":"Draiman turning aggression into a refusal to be controlled, spurning the poison of comfortable lies and daring the listener not to flinch at his certainty about what deserves love and what deserves destroying. Rage as a demand for autonomy.","sonnet":"A confrontational rejection of social conformity and passive acceptance — the speaker attacks those who absorb the dominant system's lies without resistance and refuses to be rendered innocent or compliant by forces that want to hypnotise and diminish.","src":"haiku"},
"disturbed~indestructible":{"haiku":"A warrior or soldier embodies unstoppable destructive power, sworn to a mission of annihilation. The song portrays the speaker as an instrument of violence.","opus":"A soldier's iron-willed battle anthem, the narrator summoned to duty once more, releasing the ruinous force buried inside and promising obliteration to any foe rash enough to meet him. Disturbed build it around David Draiman's clipped, martial cadence, the invincible warrior refrain converting oath and retribution into a wall of unbreakable resolve, the band at their most militaristic.","sonnet":"A soldier's self-portrait as an unstoppable weapon — every mission undertaken under oath, every order carried out without hesitation, and the enemy is told plainly that what they are facing cannot be broken. The tone is both declaration and warning: look around while you can, because the fight is already decided.","src":"haiku"},
"disturbed~inside-the-fire":{"haiku":"A grieving man surrenders his soul to a demon to reunite with his dead lover, accepting damnation for connection.","opus":"Draiman drew this from the real suicide of a teenage girlfriend, voicing the demonic temptation to follow the dead into the flames. It's grief personified as a predator whispering that death is the way back to her.","sonnet":"A demonic voice addresses a man whose girlfriend Devon has died, offering reunion with her in death if he surrenders his soul and takes his own life. The demon's promise is seductive and false, framing suicide as a doorway rather than an ending.","src":"haiku"},
"disturbed~stricken":{"haiku":"Someone is crippled by another person's power over them and cannot let them go; they descend into the abyss, haunted by a holocaust of pain and a face that destroys them.","opus":"A portrait of destructive, obsessive love, the narrator unable to release someone who leaves him wrecked and bleeding, running deeper into despair instead of healing. Disturbed frame it with David Draiman's anguished delivery, imagery of false salvation casting the beloved as both injury and addiction, one of the band's rawest depictions of a bond that only wounds.","sonnet":"Obsessive love as a wound that keeps reopening — the person in question arrives like a force of nature and leaves the speaker crippled and cold, unable to stop returning to the damage. The hold is irrational and total, pulling toward an abyss even as the speaker knows it, and the inability to let go is the whole tragedy laid bare.","src":"haiku"},
"disturbed~the-game":{"haiku":"A sadistic dynamic reverses traditional victimhood as the speaker demands to lose again and again, finding pleasure only through suffering inflicted by a cruel lover. Deception and pain become the game itself.","opus":"A predatory mind games out a fatal cat-and-mouse with a woman, framing manipulation and murder as another round to be won, savoring her lies as the pretext to end her.","sonnet":"A psychological predator addresses a victim in a game of control and cruelty, enjoying their weeping and pushing them to take \"another taste\" of hell — the final turn reveals the predator's murderous intent, framing the victim's lie as the justification for their death.","src":"haiku"},
"ditz~ded-w-rst":{"haiku":"Unease with performative guilt and the gap between curated self and body, cataloging regrets while numb to consequence.","opus":"A queasy, ironic self-portrait of complicity and staged remorse, dreading who he's becoming and the boredom that won't loosen its grip, the split between his mind and his body springing a leak in his own honesty under everyone's watch.","sonnet":"A speaker catalogues middle-class guilt and self-awareness with deliberate flatness — not feeling as bad as they should, wondering what they'll be like at fifty, spending their best years cataloguing sins — the refrain 'boredom will not let go' anchors a dissociation between body and head, a pale imitation of one's own decisions.","src":"haiku"},
"ditz~ded-würst":{"haiku":"Boredom persists as the speaker catalogs sins and fissures between mind and body. Faking happiness masks inner erosion that performance cannot heal.","src":"haiku"},
"divebar-youth~selfish":{"haiku":"Mutual selfishness and addiction to perfection bind two people in a cycle they cannot escape. Proximity promises healing but delivers only deepening entanglement.","opus":"A relationship running on mutual vanity, drawn together by the chase for attention and an addiction to a flawless surface. The honest question underneath is whether any of this closeness is real or just everyone being selfish.","sonnet":"Two people drawn together by desire and a shared addiction to perfection keep asking each other if what they feel now is the same as before; the chorus names the dynamic directly — selfishness, tangled attention, the lure of an idealized face — making closeness feel like a compulsion neither can break.","src":"haiku"},
"divinyls~i-touch-myself":{"haiku":"Solitary pleasure and self-love triumph over external relationships, with the beloved existing primarily as a fantasy to intensify private devotion. The speaker needs no one but the memory of one person.","opus":"Desire so consuming that the beloved's mere image triggers autoerotic longing, a frank celebration of wanting one person and no one else.","sonnet":"Unabashed erotic devotion: the speaker's desire for one person is so consuming that thinking about them becomes the only intimacy needed, fusing self-pleasure and longing into a single act of worship.","src":"haiku"},
"divitius~lucid":{"opus":"A cosmic, Egyptian-mythic reverie of transcendent union, dissolving into the Milky Way and merging with the elements while craving a lover's touch through the Eye of Ra.","sonnet":"A psychedelic, cosmically charged desire: surrounded by Egyptian and astrological imagery, the narrator is consumed by erotic and spiritual longing for a woman, seeking to merge with her and with the universe itself in a lucid dream state.","src":"sonnet"},
"divitius~memento-mori":{"opus":"A reckoning with mortality and regret, the narrator taking stock of what he has loved, lost, and left unsaid before his time runs out. The song pivots toward defiance and hope, refusing to fade quietly and drawing on an inner resolve to endure and leave something worth remembering.","sonnet":"A confrontation with mortality and the possibility of breaking open rather than fading — the narrator has torn themselves apart trying to understand, but turns toward survival, stitching themselves back together, insisting there is the strength of a world in their chest and they will not be forgotten.","src":"sonnet"},
"dizzy-gillespie~anthropology":{"opus":"This lyric isn't Gillespie's bebop standard but a children's rewrite voiced by a pony marveling at humans, curiosity flipped so the animal studies us. The playful conceit is wonder at the ordinary human world seen from outside.","src":"opus"},
"dj-khaled~supposed-to-be-loved-feat-lil-baby-future-lil-uzi-vert":{"opus":"Over Khaled's luxury-brand backdrop, three rappers frame devotion through spending, promising to love a woman properly while cataloging Birkins and Cullinans. The tension is real feeling voiced almost entirely in the language of money and risk.","src":"opus"},
"dj-khalil~elevate-feat-denzel-curry-ybn-cordae-swavay-trevor-rich":{"haiku":"Superhuman ambition and defiance overcome obstacles through relentless work and moral conviction, rising above hatred and violence through strength and integrity.","opus":"Rappers cast themselves as Spider-Man to frame rising above hate and adversity as a superhero's duty, elevating past enemies while fighting personal demons for the sake of family and community.","sonnet":"A Spider-Man themed elevation anthem in which multiple rappers cast themselves as the hero — fighting crime through the night, jumping off buildings to save civilians, building independent from scratch — with the chorus insisting on rising above hatred and choosing the right side.","src":"haiku"},
"dj-shadow~midnight-in-a-perfect-world":{"opus":"A hazy, sampled nocturne evoking the hushed threshold of midnight, seeds of love planted by moonlight in an atmosphere more felt than stated.","sonnet":"A late-night reverie: midnight, a garden of love, seeds planted under a dark moon — the atmosphere is hushed and romantic, built on fragmented vocal phrases that circle the clock face as time and desire approach some unnamed threshold together.","src":"sonnet"},
"dj-snake-alunageorge~you-know-you-like-it-t-mass-arman-cekin-remix":{"haiku":"A defiant speaker resists control and shame, refusing to surrender autonomy or accept judgment for their desires.","opus":"A refusal to be tamed or made to choose sides, a dreamer insisting on fun over other people's rules, wrapped around the knowing tension that desire and shame pull in opposite directions. Independence as its own seduction.","sonnet":"A speaker resists being categorised, controlled, or trained into compliance, insisting on independence from expectations while addressing someone who wants what the speaker offers but is ashamed of that desire. The tension between wanting and fearing is where the song lives.","src":"haiku"},
"dj-snake~run-it-feat-rick-ross-rich-brian":{"haiku":"Relentless ambition obliterates obstacles; the speaker embodies unstoppable force moving in silence while celebrating material victory and dominion over opponents.","opus":"A flex about winning, wealth and moving in silence, ruining rivals' goals out of sheer boredom while married only to the game. The recurring dare to run it frames success as a race others simply can't keep up with.","sonnet":"Three artists trading flexes about wealth, status, and shutting down competition — moving in silence, driving expensive cars, and making power plays while rivals scatter.","src":"haiku"},
"django-django~hail-bop":{"haiku":"A celestial visitor appears and vanishes; the narrator waits desperately for their return, learning that transcendence offers no guarantee of permanence.","opus":"Named for the comet, a lover cast as something celestial that streaks in, lingers just long enough, and vanishes again, leaving the narrator earthbound and waiting. Longing tuned to the cold rhythm of an orbit that won't stay.","sonnet":"The speaker addresses someone who blazes in and out of their life like a comet — dazzling, disorienting, always departing — and waits through long absences for a return that never quite stays.","src":"haiku"},
"django-django~shake-and-tremble":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic omens manifest as the world fractures and burns; trembling earth precedes collapse while humanity dances obliviously toward annihilation.","opus":"Apocalyptic omens read in a glowing sky, forming fault lines and a rising ocean as the world erodes and burns through everything owned. Amid the sacrifice and dancing over embers, all that's known is engulfed in a trembling collapse.","sonnet":"Apocalyptic omens are read in fault lines, glowing skies, retreating oceans, and dancing figures falling into fire — the imagery moves between ancient ritual (drums, sacrifice, embers) and tectonic collapse, with the refrain \"shake and tremble\" naming the moment everything gives way.","src":"haiku"},
"djerv~we-don-t-hang-no-more":{"haiku":"Separation hardens into severance as bridges burn and intimacy becomes impossible, survival demanding the amputation of shared history and mutual recognition.","opus":"The cold finality of a friendship or love that has died, two people who were once one now strangers walking out separate doors, the speaker hardening his heart to say goodbye.","sonnet":"Two people who were once inseparable have drifted into estrangement and now walk out through different doors as strangers; the speaker acknowledges the break with resigned self-discovery, finding his own sound even as he twists the arm of what was lost.","src":"haiku"},
"djo~link":{"opus":"Joe Keery's synth-rock parable of a life that looks successful yet feels like servitude, questioning why doing everything right still feels bad. The chain of the daily grind becomes the enemy, and being the link that snaps free the whole hope.","src":"opus"},
"djunah~seven-winds-of-sekhmet":{"opus":"The wrathful Egyptian goddess Sekhmet speaks, loosing plagues on humanity to punish the failures of men and coming for their children and their works. She commands them to cower and weep — there is no fleeing her judgment, and slow ruin is what she brings.","src":"opus"},
"dk-zero~boom-boom":{"opus":"A crude novelty party track that's all bounce and bravado, sex reduced to slogan and cartoonish boast. There's no read beneath it beyond the joke of its own excess, meme-culture crassness played for a laugh.","src":"opus"},
"dlina-volny~dance-me-away":{"opus":"The Belarusian darkwave act turns seduction into a predatory game, luring a partner to dance and vanish while she hides in the water to be hunted. Desire and danger blur, romance framed as a trap set with pleasure.","src":"opus"},
"dnce~cake-by-the-ocean":{"haiku":"Carefree hedonism and fearless connection triumph; the speakers abandon inhibition for dangerous pleasure.","opus":"A goofy, unabashed come-on urging a too-careful partner to loosen up and live dangerously, the nonsense title just a euphemism carrying pure appetite. Fun with no subtext beyond wanting more of each other.","sonnet":"An invitation to stop holding back — to shed seriousness, be reckless, and pursue physical pleasure and adventure together at the beach before the moment is gone. The frosting and sugar imagery runs throughout as playful metaphors for sensual indulgence.","src":"haiku"},
"doddodo~exciseise-of-mouth":{"opus":"The Japanese experimentalist spins a surreal creation myth from the mouth, blood and semen mingling into the sound of birth, one cry multiplying into countless children of sound. It then drops into a playful Osaka scene of a woman who loves to imitate, DJ battles and scratching. Doddodo makes language itself the raw material, meaning dissolved into sonic play.","sonnet":"A celebration of Osaka's underground music scene built around the mythologized DJ 'Doddodo' — the song traces the origin of sound from the red blood of birth, through the streets of Nipponbashi, to hardcore punk, hip hop, and scratching, mapping a lineage of noise.","src":"sonnet"},
"does~a-3n72j":{"opus":"DOES built their reputation soundtracking anime with warm, unshowy garage rock, and this is their register at its most domestic. A couple walks under a threatening sky without an umbrella, both cowards pretending not to be, ducking into a coffee shop to hide from the rain. The whole overcast afternoon drifts slowly and ends with them not going home — small-scale contentment given weight simply by refusing to hurry.","sonnet":"A gentle, mundane love song about a couple walking under a threatening sky without an umbrella: both of them are cowards pretending not to be, they duck into a coffee shop to hide from the coming rain, and the whole afternoon is a slow, comfortable drift that ends with them not going home — small-scale contentment rendered in overcast atmosphere.","src":"sonnet"},
"doja-cat~addiction":{"haiku":"Physical beauty and psychological neediness intertwine as addictive loops; the speaker cycles between pride in brokenness and desperate pursuit of transformation.","opus":"Doja using literal substance imagery as a frame for compulsive attachment, the broken-girl self-awareness of chasing highs and toxic men because being a mess feels like an identity. Craving dressed up as a dance she can't quit.","sonnet":"A speaker confesses to an addictive attraction she can't shake — framing it through the language of substance dependency, broken childhood, and bodily pleasure — while acknowledging that the cycle of craving more has no cure.","src":"haiku"},
"doll-skin~love-is-dead-and-we-killed-her":{"haiku":"Vengeance justifies itself as moral clarity; the speaker transforms from victim to predator, meting out punishment through force and cruelty without remorse.","opus":"A revenge fantasy in which an abused woman becomes the killer, hunting down the man who degraded her and calling it karma come to collect. The switchblade turns victimhood into lethal agency, with no going back on what's been shattered.","sonnet":"A revenge fantasy framed as righteous justice: the speaker, switchblade in hand and \"blood boiling to the bone,\" is coming for someone who abused another, casting karma as a bitch who always settles the score — with no way back, no fixing what's been shattered.","src":"haiku"},
"dolly-parton~jolene":{"haiku":"A supplicant confronts romantic displacement with raw vulnerability, acknowledging the rival's superiority while begging her not to exploit emotional dependence.","opus":"Parton begging a more beautiful woman not to take her man, the genius being that she pleads rather than fights, admitting she can't compete and staking her whole happiness on a rival's mercy. Vulnerability weaponized as the only defense left.","sonnet":"A woman pleads directly with a strikingly beautiful rival not to steal her man, conceding that Jolene has the power to take him but begging her to show mercy, because he is the only love she has and her entire happiness depends on Jolene's choice.","src":"haiku"},
"don-broco~birthday-party":{"haiku":"Loneliness and social alienation render celebration hollow; the speaker navigates parties with self-doubt.","opus":"Social anxiety played as bleak comedy, a party nobody attends and a night out where every glance feels like an accusation. Don Broco puncture the insistence of having a good time with the loneliness it's papering over.","sonnet":"Social anxiety at its most absurdist peak — a birthday party where nobody shows, then a night out where everyone stares and the narrator wonders if they look like a drug dealer, can't speak or wee, and keeps insisting they're \"having a really good time.\" The comedy is the gap between the bravado mantra and the barely-holding-it-together reality.","src":"haiku"},
"don-broco~endorphins":{"haiku":"A person's addiction to pleasure and adrenaline keeps them making reckless mistakes despite wanting to give it up in the morning.","opus":"A self-deprecating admission of a crush so chemically overwhelming the narrator keeps embarrassing himself and keeps swearing he'll quit tomorrow. Don Broco, the British rock band known for sleek, tongue-in-cheek hooks, spin a superhero pun and a looping confession of pathetic-ness into a cheeky study of lust that keeps putting off its own remedy.","sonnet":"Infatuation as biological sabotage — the body chemistry of a crush floods the system, keeps the speaker awake and making bad choices, and the self-awareness of how pathetic this all is doesn't help at all. The plan to give it up in the morning is a familiar lie told to keep the feeling alive one more night.","src":"haiku"},
"don-broco~everybody":{"haiku":"Social withdrawal and emotional exhaustion drain friendship and intimacy; the speaker retreats from engagement, convinced that isolation is safer than remaining present for others.","opus":"A portrait of burnout and a friendship dissolving, luck spent and the fun gone, unable to show up for a friend or even leave the bed while a reckless crash marks how far it's slid. The shared admission that they're done for this time makes it a spiral both are stuck inside.","sonnet":"Two people caught in mutual withdrawal and dysfunction — the speaker has stopped getting out of bed, dropped their friends, and lost the will to care, while cataloguing a series of reckless incidents like a van crash and a cracked windscreen as evidence that something has gone badly wrong for everyone involved.","src":"haiku"},
"don-broco~fingernails":{"opus":"A menacing metaphor of torture stretched over a song about fighting for your life and speaking the oppressor's language back at them. Don Broco channel a righteous, violent resolve, refusing the roles of victim and savior alike in an endless struggle.","src":"opus"},
"don-broco~gumshield":{"haiku":"Self-protection through emotional numbness and medication while angry at those around them for lacking empathy; violent performance masks inner rage.","opus":"The Bedford rock band literalizes self-defense, its narrator shielding himself against his own worst impulses while admitting he needs help. Aggression and threats of violence mask anxiety and disappointment, and a numbing crutch that keeps everyone moving stands in for hollow coping. It plays as bravado worn as armor over real inner turmoil.","sonnet":"Bracing for impact with oneself, the speaker arms up not for combat with others but against self-destruction. Medicated numbness is circulating through the crowd, offered as communion. Beneath the frenetic aggression lies genuine anger and disappointment, seeking acknowledgment from a world unwilling to offer it honestly.","src":"haiku"},
"don-broco~half-man-half-god":{"haiku":"Spiritual transcendence and divine love merge as the speaker surrenders his essence to a higher power; the boundary between human and god collapses into unified devotion.","opus":"A restless bargaining with faith and mortality, wanting to live alongside one's gods and give everything before the end, half-convinced heaven's already out of reach. Everyone chasing some private version of salvation while there's time.","sonnet":"A speaker caught between the mortal and the divine declares unconditional love as his only creed — knowing heaven is unreachable, he commits to pouring everything he has into the people around him until they're gone.","src":"haiku"},
"don-broco~manchester-super-reds-no-1-fan":{"haiku":"Parasitic fans and armchair critics project cruelty onto artists, misinterpreting vulnerability as weakness and celebrating downfall. The speaker rejects their judgment and threatens to abandon the spectacle entirely.","opus":"Don Broco confront fandom's toxic underside, voicing a performer harassed by so-called supporters who wish addiction upon him and mock a bandmate's body. Worn out, he threatens to scrap every gig and pleads for one final singalong. The obsessive craving to be his devotee's ultimate favorite sours the parasocial tie into something predatory.","sonnet":"A musician reckons with the corrosive side of fandom — the same people who claimed kinship now weaponize nostalgia, demand relapses, and bully bandmates. The speaker oscillates between wanting to cancel everything and wanting one last genuine communal moment, the joy and toxicity of belonging inseparable from each other.","src":"haiku"},
"don-broco~t-shirt-song-live":{"haiku":"Passion transformed into self-destruction; the speaker embodies both the wound and the weapon, defining themselves through turbulent intimacy that breaks and remakes identity.","opus":"A turbulent relationship that damaged and defined him at once, driving him to smoke and coming undone yet leaving him someone he wouldn't be without it. The absurd ritual of whipping off his T-shirt marks a cathartic release from being done over.","sonnet":"A turbulent relationship is addressed with volatile gratitude: the other person drove the speaker to extremes — cigarettes, emotional collapse, being \"backed onto the ground\" — yet the refrain insists \"I wouldn't be who I'd be without you,\" holding destruction and formation together.","src":"haiku"},
"don-broco~uber":{"haiku":"Racial and religious hatred expressed casually during a ride home, confronting the speaker with the casual bigotry of someone they're trapped with.","opus":"A tense late-night ride where the narrator confronts a driver spouting racist, Islamophobic bile, his temper flaring as he challenges the man and threatens to involve the police. The British rock band sharpens its satirical edge, its bewildered hook turning one cab trip into a snapshot of a fractured, divided country.","sonnet":"A late-night rideshare turns into a confrontation with casual racism — the driver spews hostility about immigration and Islam, the speaker pushes back, and the encounter becomes a small-scale map of a country's divisions playing out in the back seat of a car at night. The refrain asking what they are doing is directed at the whole situation, not just the driver.","src":"haiku"},
"don-diablo~deja-vu":{"opus":"A euphoric dance track built almost entirely on nostalgia, the good old days reborn as an endlessly repeated hook. The hypnotic loop enacts its own subject, chasing the sweet familiar rush of a moment felt before.","src":"opus"},
"don-diablo~not-alone":{"opus":"A nostalgic memory of being young and wrecked in a field of mud, minds slipping, when one friend talked another down from panic. The refrain is pure reassurance, a promise to wait as long as it takes and an invitation to cross over together.","src":"opus"},
"doomcrusher~breakout":{"opus":"A fight against inner demons, a spinning head and urges toward self-harm, the narrator refusing to fear the dark and swearing to snap the destructive loop in his skull; retaking command of himself, he steels up, faces the unknown and bursts free toward relief and joy.","src":"opus"},
"dope-stars-inc~cracking-the-power":{"haiku":"Networked masses on the web challenge corrupt systems of control. Underground movements prepare to crack power structures through collective digital force.","opus":"An early-2000s cyberpunk fantasy of networked revolt, where geeks and cameras and the open web become weapons that dissolve top-down authority. The thrill is the belief that giving everyone a voice inevitably topples the corrupt.","sonnet":"A celebration of networked, tech-savvy collective resistance — the internet has given ordinary people a weapon against corrupt authority, and a mass of geeks armed with cameras and voices is ready to use it.","src":"haiku"},
"dope-stars-inc~it-s-going-to-rain-for-you":{"opus":"A defiant severance from an abuser who made the speaker a scapegoat carrying every blame. Now the tables turn as he refuses to be used, pleased, or fooled again, promising a coming storm as payback for their faults.","src":"opus"},
"dope-stars-inc~lies-irae":{"haiku":"A network of hackers and digital rebels leaks secrets and disrupts systems to expose lies and hold power accountable. The song celebrates collective cyber-activism as a generational response to institutional dishonesty.","opus":"The Italian industrial band Dope Stars Inc. dramatize a hacktivist revolt, a leaderless digital generation that exposes identities and storms furious networks. Cast as truth against a mendacious regime, life lived through copied fragments in cyberspace becomes insurrection, distributed defiance spreading until the enemy's order crumbles. Online liberation is treated as a cause worth any battle.","sonnet":"A hacktivist collective wages information warfare against corrupt power structures. Leaks and raids on digital networks replace traditional protest, with cyberspace serving as both battlefield and common ground. A generation fluent in terabytes insists on radical transparency as the new political act.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~addiction":{"haiku":"Desire becomes physiological compulsion; romantic obsession mirrors narcotic dependence.","opus":"Industrial-metal reducing an obsessive relationship to a list of hard drugs, the lover as a substance that destroys him and that he keeps going back to anyway. Desire stripped down to pure, self-annihilating compulsion.","sonnet":"A romantic obsession is mapped directly onto drug addiction — destructive, compulsive, physically overwhelming — with the speaker unable to stop returning to someone who is simultaneously killing him and the only thing he wants.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~always":{"haiku":"Two former friends reminisce about their bond before betrayal and hatred fractured them, seeking to return to a past where love and plans connected them forever.","opus":"A plea to a friendship or love that curdled into blame, pleading to rewind to before the resentment set in. The stubborn refusal to call it over is the whole ache, holding on past the point of reason.","sonnet":"A song about a friendship or relationship that fell apart through accumulated wrongs — the narrator wants to go back to how things were before the blame and the broken hearts, acknowledging that \"always\" was once real between them and hoping that something of it can be recovered.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~believe":{"opus":"A survivor's affirmation of faith in love as the root of everything, insisting the sun will come up again after seeing terrible things and even brushing against suicide. Having circled the world and concluded everyone's a little insane, the speaker keeps going because life and time roll on regardless.","src":"opus"},
"dope~bitch":{"haiku":"Love and hate merge in toxic physical attraction where the narrator simultaneously despises and desires their partner, caught in the contradiction.","opus":"The industrial-metal band frames a compulsive love-hate entanglement where the sex is great but the resentment runs equally deep, the narrator unable to choose and unable to let go. Crude party imagery bookends the churning ambivalence. Dope's late-1990s shock-tinged aggression suits the deliberately provocative refrain, desire and loathing locked in the same self-aware, out-of-control loop.","sonnet":"Hatred and desire locked in a loop: the narrator is trapped in a relationship he knows is toxic but cannot leave because physical chemistry overrides every rational objection. The ambivalence is absolute — each line of attraction undercut immediately by revulsion, neither canceling the other.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~bring-it-on":{"haiku":"The narrator ignores empty words and confronts someone making threats; what they started ends with direct action and consequence.","opus":"A defiant narrator dismisses a boaster full of empty talk, indifferent to the world and content to sit back doing only what he wants while daring the other to step outside. Dope, the New York industrial-metal act, channel their nu-metal-era belligerence into this taunt, the refrain insisting that whatever the antagonist started will find its end with the speaker.","sonnet":"Defiant detachment from a posturing adversary who says much and delivers nothing. The narrator refuses to carry the world's burdens, sits back doing exactly as desired, and waits with confidence for whatever confrontation the other party chooses to start.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~burn":{"haiku":"A community unites against an oppressive enemy, choosing violent resistance and total destruction. They rally together to kill and burn down the system that is destroying them.","opus":"Distrust of the television-fed narrative gives way to defiant brotherhood, a vow not to let comrades die and a chant to stand tall against a spreading disease of lies. The chorus turns to scorched-earth vengeance on an enemy. Dope, an American industrial-metal band, harness this us-against-them fury with abrasive riffs, framing survival as refusal to fall in line.","sonnet":"A combat-ready speaker rallies against a dehumanizing enemy, drawing his conviction from brotherhood rather than ideology — he fights because his brothers are dying, not because the television told him the cause was just. The anthem collapses nuance into pure collective will: stand, destroy, burn everything that stands against survival.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~debonaire":{"opus":"A blunt rejection of wealth, glamour and fame, the narrator insisting over and over that he doesn't care about diamonds, Hollywood or looking the part.","sonnet":"A flat, unapologetic rejection of wealth, glamour, and status signifiers — diamond rings, limousines, designer names, Hollywood — the speaker simply does not care about any of it, stated three times with casual finality.","src":"sonnet"},
"dope~die-motherfucker-die":{"haiku":"A defiant person rejects all authority, forgiveness, and external control. They refuse to accept anyone else's rules or judgment and plan retribution.","opus":"A nihilistic rejection of all external authority (forgiveness, hate, religion, prison, approval), the narrator refuses to be told what to do and, dismissing threats that he'll regret it when old, answers defiance with a fantasy of killing the accuser.","sonnet":"Pure rejection of every external claim on the speaker — religion, approval, forgiveness, authority — delivered as a furious demand that anyone who tries to control or judge him simply disappear.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dope's early-2000s signature is an inventory of refusals: the singer rejects, one by one, every external claim laid on him — the pardon offered, the belief demanded, the verdict handed down — until the list of things he wants from anyone empties to nothing. The self-defining question he keeps circling back to, what is he even supposed to do instead, is a taunt rather than a plea, since the chorus has already answered it with the same four-word death-chant hammered flatter each pass. The song's one real movement lives in the jab his accuser plants — that regret will catch up with him once he is old — which he first flings back as a sneer and, in the last verse, forecloses outright: rather than concede that future, he ends the argument by ending the other person, the abstract kiss-off snapping into a literal trigger-pull so the accuser never survives to be proven right.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"refusing every external claim until only the death-chant remains","built":"the accuser is killed before he can be proven right"},
"dope~dirty-world":{"haiku":"Urban corruption, sexual addiction, and hedonistic excess define a world where innocence sells and nothing comes free; the speaker celebrates carnal violation and chemical escape within decay.","opus":"Edsel Dope's leering industrial-metal ode to a used-up Hollywood party girl and the grimy transactional world she moves through, where nothing is free. Addiction, sex, and fame collapsed into one cheap, sordid appetite.","sonnet":"A sexually charged portrait of a woman living on the edge — tattoos, cocaine, no regrets, selling herself in Hollywood's underbelly — framed as both alluring and sordid.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~dream":{"haiku":"A dreamer questions reality versus sleep and what they're being forced to believe. The dream is all that matters and must stay alive despite the world screaming.","opus":"Nu-metal defiance about clinging to your own dreams and refusing to become what others demand; amid collapse the narrator keeps breathing, keeps believing in himself, treating the shared dream as the one thing worth keeping alive.","sonnet":"A call to hold onto personal dreams against a world of lies and pressures demanding conformity, insisting that the shared act of dreaming is the one thing worth protecting even when everything else is collapsing.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~falling-away":{"opus":"A chant about escaping a life bent on ruin, circling the joke that things constantly shift yet somehow never truly change. The singer describes coming awake, coming loose, and drifting free of his own private landscape of wreckage and hurt.","sonnet":"A person waking up and breaking free from a world of self-destruction and pain — the shift is described as both falling away from the old chaos and breaking open into something new, even as they acknowledge the cycle keeps repeating.","src":"sonnet"},
"dope~fuck-tha-police":{"haiku":"A critique of police brutality, systemic oppression, and law enforcement targeting minorities and the poor, framed as a street narrative of confrontation and resistance.","opus":"A cover of N.W.A's furious protest against racist police brutality, told from a young Black man profiled, searched and abused by cops with badges and guns. It answers state violence with fantasized retaliation, a bloodbath of dying cops, dramatizing rage born of systemic oppression.","sonnet":"A cover of the NWA track expressing raw rage at police targeting of Black youth — the speaker recounts being harassed, searched, and stereotypized, and responds with violent fantasy and defiant refusal to be subjugated.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~i-am":{"haiku":"The narrator asserts his unchangeable nature and refuses to conform to others' expectations or judgments. He defiantly claims authenticity while rejecting pretense.","opus":"A defiant statement of self against everyone who wanted the narrator to be something else, refusing to pretend or conform. Blunt and confrontational, it's identity as a fist raised at expectation.","sonnet":"A defiant declaration of selfhood — the speaker refuses to conform to others' expectations or pretend to be something they're not, insisting that what they are is all they are and that's enough, regardless of whether anyone else understands.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~i-don-t-give-a":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects everyone who caused stress and disappointment—authority figures, ex-partners, and betrayers—through defiant indifference.","opus":"A cathartic kiss-off dressed up as a lesson in a handy phrase for blowing off stress, the narrator turning his back and announcing his total indifference to everyone who did him wrong. His roll call, boss, coworkers, an unfaithful ex, teachers, spongers, even the head of state, turns the hook into a wholesale dismissal of anyone who couldn't be bothered about him first.","sonnet":"A cathartic rage anthem directed at everyone who has disrespected, dismissed, or taken advantage of the speaker — bosses, ex-partners, bad teachers, even the president — the deliberate bleeping of the expletive giving it a darkly comic framing as a therapeutic public announcement.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~i-m-back":{"haiku":"A boastful return to cause disruption and chaos, rejecting authority while insisting on raw, physical power and presence to dominate and command attention.","opus":"A brash return anthem bookended by a sampled Bush-era threat, with the singer announcing his comeback to stir up chaos, break morale, and hit hard. It's less a narrative than a strutting declaration of revival and intimidation, all bravado and warning about the damage he's about to do.","sonnet":"A loud return announcement from the band Dope, framed around driving forward with purpose and devotion — opening with a Bush speech sample ironically recast, it's fundamentally a crowd-pump declaration of comeback energy and refusal to stay down.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~i-wish-i-was-the-president":{"haiku":"The speaker fantasizes about wealth and power to escape financial struggle and live without consequences. The song satirizes materialistic excess and the fantasy of absolute privilege.","opus":"A crude power fantasy that doubles as satire, imagining absolute privilege as license for every appetite and abuse. The joke lands on the office itself: given the throne, this narrator would be no worse than the real thing.","sonnet":"A working-class fantasy of presidential power as pure self-indulgence — strip clubs on the taxpayer, bombing enemies from a jet, never paying taxes, barely seeing the wife — a crude but pointed joke about the gap between populist rhetoric and the actual behavior of those in power.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~kimberly-s-ghost":{"haiku":"Breakup resentment and rejection harden into mutual hatred as the narrator reclaims anger after giving everything to an ungrateful lover.","opus":"A poisonous kiss-off to a lover reduced to appetite and contempt, insisting he never needed her and can match her hate pound for pound. The exchange sours their mutual mistakes into a boast that he'll be sick of her long after she's gone.","sonnet":"A breakup fuelled by mutual exhaustion and accumulated resentment — the speaker wanting nothing, giving everything, now turning to hate and asking what the other person will do with themselves once it's through.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~let-s-fuck":{"haiku":"Sexual availability and mutual desire with no complications or strings. The song is a direct, unadorned proposition for unrestricted casual sex.","opus":"A blunt, single-minded proposition of anytime-anywhere sex, framing it as a thrilling crime to commit again and again; there's no deeper subject beyond the pursuit of casual sex for its own sake.","sonnet":"A direct, unambiguous proposition for casual sex with no narrative beyond the invitation itself — let's do it wherever and however we want, as long as we want.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~motivation":{"haiku":"The song summons people to action and movement despite paralysis or indifference. It's an exhortation to shake off apathy and get moving.","opus":"A blunt dance-floor track about paralysis and apathy (he doesn't know why he's frozen, wonders why bother trying) that answers its own numbness by ordering the listener and himself to just move the body, using physical motion as the only available motivation.","sonnet":"A straightforward exhortation to move, stop being paralyzed, and get in the groove — essentially a call to action directed at anyone too stuck or oblivious to get out of the way.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~my-funeral":{"haiku":"Someone faces the end of their life and invites others to their funeral, accepting that endings must come so new things can begin, though they walk this path alone.","opus":"A goodbye staged as the narrator's own memorial, greeting everyone at the finish and thanking them for the company while insisting this last passage is his to take alone. The industrial metal band gives it a strange calm, imagery of scattered remains and letting go lending death the feel of a self-chosen release rather than a tragedy.","sonnet":"A departure ceremony addressed to everyone being left behind — the speaker has made peace with an ending, frames it as freedom and release, and thanks those who came along without asking them to follow this last step. The tone is more resolved than despairing: some things must end for the next thing to begin, and this is being done alone.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~no-regrets":{"haiku":"A person spirals downward through self-abuse and degradation without remorse. They repeat destructive cycles deliberately and would do it all again.","opus":"Edsel Dope frames self-destruction as a choice owned rather than mourned, defiance masking the downward spiral. The insistence on regretting nothing reads less as freedom than as a junkie's refusal to admit the fall is a fall.","sonnet":"A cycle of self-abuse, sinking, and medicated numbness recounted without remorse — the speaker has done it all, will do it again, and refuses to frame any of it as mistakes worth regretting.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~no-way-out":{"haiku":"Someone expresses fury at weakness, pain, lies, and the people around them; they feel trapped with no exit despite yearning to escape, finally proposing destructive flame as the only solution.","opus":"A pileup of contempt and exhaustion, cataloguing everyone the narrator is sick of while admitting there's no escape from the loathing. Rage that turns in circles, aware it'll feel the same tomorrow.","sonnet":"A song about feeling trapped and overwhelmed by everything and everyone — the bullshit, the blame, the ingrates — with no escape route in sight, only a simmering urge to burn it all down.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~nothing-for-me":{"haiku":"Investment yields emptiness: everything sacrificed spirals into void, leaving only the recognition that nothing was ever worth the price paid.","opus":"Exhausted by pain, blame and broken promises, the narrator concludes there's nothing left here and only himself to blame. A life given away fades to nothing, with escape the only remaining wish.","sonnet":"Exhausted and emptied out by life and by someone they gave everything to, a person concludes there is nothing left for them here — neither belief in themselves nor hope that sacrifice meant anything.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~nothing-for-me-here":{"haiku":"The speaker feels hollowed by broken promises and loss of faith in anything, trapped in blame and stagnation, deciding to flee because nothing here offers meaning or purpose.","opus":"Industrial-metal act Dope channel bottomed-out despair, a man who has stopped believing in himself or anyone else after deciding all his hurt and self-reproach add up to zero. He looks back on a time when he'd have given anything to succeed and now suspects the whole bargain was a con, resigning himself to walking away from a life he feels was quietly used up.","sonnet":"A complete severance from a place or relationship that has drained everything without giving anything back. Past sacrifices are named as miscalculated, promises as worthless, and the accumulated damage as unrepairable. Departure is not dramatic — it is simply the only thing left that makes sense.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~now-is-the-time":{"haiku":"An affirmation of personal empowerment and seizing the moment, asserting readiness to take action and claim one's place as a self-determined individual.","opus":"A blunt self-empowerment anthem, the narrator announcing that his moment has finally come to seize control, tower over everyone else, and pursue his ambitions without apology. Industrial-metal band Dope wrap it in stomping momentum, the repeated demands to act now working as a fist-pumping call to seize the day, built for adrenaline rather than subtlety.","sonnet":"A motivational surge about seizing the present moment — the voice steps into the light, stands for what is right, and rises above the crowd with confident urgency. The repetition functions as self-reinforcement, a pep talk that needs no specific opponent or cause, just the act of standing up and declaring that the time is now.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~now-or-never":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts someone whose departure feels imminent, demanding clarity about their relationship and emphasizing the urgency of deciding to continue together or break apart.","opus":"A relationship at a crossroads, the narrator baffled by a partner who says goodbye as if everything's fine, pressing for an answer while clinging to a hope he wants to believe. The refrain 'if we go on, it's now or never' makes it an ultimatum born of uncertainty, love hanging on whether they'll finally hold hands or let it end.","sonnet":"A relationship at a breaking point — something has happened that the narrator can't understand, the other person keeps saying goodbye as if everything is fine, and there's a demand for a decision: if this goes on, it has to be now. The song is an ultimatum wrapped in confusion.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~paranoia":{"haiku":"The song plays on real versus imagined threats; the narrator questions whether paranoia is delusional or justified when genuine enemies may actually exist.","opus":"A raw depiction of paranoia in which the narrator insists his fear isn't irrational because people really are after him; the tension between being told he's imagining things and his conviction that someone is hunting him drives him toward the edge of insanity.","sonnet":"A circular argument with both the self and an imagined accuser about whether the pervasive sense of being hunted is mental illness or reality — the song never resolves the question, which is exactly the point.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~people-are-people":{"haiku":"People struggle to understand why hatred exists between them despite their shared humanity; prejudice and violence contradict our common decency.","opus":"A cover pleading for tolerance across difference, asking why anyone should despise another when people are fundamentally alike beneath surface distinctions. Yet the bitterly ironic verses, about the short trip from thought to violence and cruelty repaid to a bigot, admit how quickly that appeal to understanding curdles back into aggression.","sonnet":"A cover of the Depeche Mode song asking why people hate each other across differences of race and background — the speaker genuinely can't understand prejudice directed at them, and the final admission that decency hasn't quite \"traveled from my head to my fist\" gives it a rueful, honest edge.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~revolution":{"haiku":"People rise to overthrow a rigged system built by the wealthy. The speaker calls for collective action to reclaim power from those born into scams and greed.","opus":"Industrial-metal class rage against inherited wealth and a rigged system, calling the powerless to stand up together against the man. The fury is real but the target stays generic, revolt as a chant more than a plan.","sonnet":"Class rage against inherited wealth and systematic exploitation — the narrator rallies the dispossessed to rise up against those born into power who have never had to understand or endure what they impose on others, framing violence as the only language left.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~scorn":{"haiku":"Someone consumed by rage and bitterness from past abuse was taught to hate but never to heal, resulting in suicidal ideation and the cycle of inflicted and internalized pain.","opus":"A cry of a person broken and imprisoned by another's abuse and pain, scorned to the point of suicidal despair with the tub filled with gasoline. The core wound is that he was taught hate and shown pain but never taught how to heal, so the cruelty he received becomes the only thing modeled for him.","sonnet":"About being raised in a household that modeled only pain without teaching you how to heal — the result is rage so total the narrator is willing to burn themselves alive. The anger isn't directed outward as revenge so much as imploding under the weight of what was never taught.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~slipping-away":{"haiku":"Someone struggles to understand identity and purpose as life slips away despite effort, trapped in confusion and seeking help from deterioration.","opus":"A young man's despair as adulthood refuses to match what he pictured, straining to understand himself and mature while everything drifts beyond his grasp. His plea for someone to pull him out of the wreckage captures a raw sense of drowning and powerlessness.","sonnet":"A man completely overwhelmed and losing his grip — on life, on identity, on direction — calling out for someone to help him as everything he thought his life would be continues to slide away from him.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~survive":{"fable":"A middle finger with a frame story: asked on tape what he'd say to those who lied, cheated and stole from him, the answer is two words — and the rest of the song proves he's still standing, a lie that just won't die.","fableDeep":"The staged interview question makes this a press conference, not a diary — the grievance is 'intentionally misinterpreted or miscommunicated,' so the song is about narrative control before it's about survival. The strangest, best line is the simile: 'I survive like a lie that just won't die' — he claims persistence not through virtue but through the media's own physics, where rumors outlive their debunking; if they'll treat him as a fiction, he'll take a fiction's immortality. The threats are stock genre furniture; the venom is all in that opening 'Fuck 'em' — two words doing the work the question pretended to invite.","haiku":"The speaker asserts resilience and defiance, proclaiming their refusal to be stopped or defeated despite external doubt and aggression.","opus":"A defiant declaration of survival aimed at liars, thieves, and doubters who misinterpreted the narrator, insisting he's endured the ups and downs and remains standing, unstoppable and impossible to deny.","sonnet":"A defiant declaration of resilience against betrayal and attempts to suppress or discredit the speaker: having seen both failure and success, the speaker insists that no opposition can stop them and that they will keep going regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~take-your-best-shot":{"haiku":"Young people must pursue their dreams despite doubters and institutions that dismiss them as impossible.","opus":"A jarring opening that guns down cloying innocence gives way to a defiant pep-talk anthem, pushing the listener to seize their one life before it runs out. Set against an indifferent, blinded world, it insists you refuse anyone who says what you can't become and knock down every barrier in your path.","sonnet":"A defiant motivational piece underneath the aggression — ignore the people who say you can't be what you want, take your shot before it's too late, because today might be all you get.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~thanks-for-nothing":{"haiku":"Bitter ingratitude toward exploitation and parasitism. The narrator receives nothing but takes and demands, sarcastically thanking those who offered only bullshit and absence.","opus":"A bitter, sarcastic kiss-off to someone who only takes and gives nothing, thanking them ironically for their bullshit and for everything they didn't do. The narrator demands a break and questions what more is wanted from him.","sonnet":"A sustained expression of contempt toward someone who took without giving and contributed nothing but self-serving behaviour and hollow promises. The repeated 'thanks for nothing' is the entire argument: sarcastic acknowledgment of a total failure to deliver.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~today-is-the-day":{"haiku":"Someone makes a final break with their past—discarding possessions, obligations, and expectations—to live day by day in freedom, willing to sacrifice comfort for liberation.","opus":"A defiant vow to throw everything overboard and finally break free, the narrator squandering it all, shrugging it off, and taking his chances without a thought for the future. Dope build it around a druggy, cathartic release, the industrial-metal grind matching a mood poised between liberation and self-sabotage, this the day he does what he must.","sonnet":"Letting go chosen consciously and with relief — the speaker names today as the day they release whatever has been crushing them, accepts the risk of missing it, and decides that freedom is worth the cost. The lyric sheet notes its own inaccuracy, but the core impulse is clear: total surrender to the present and a deliberate turning away from what has been carried too long.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~violence":{"haiku":"The narrator wants to witness and inflict violence, viewing it as necessary and exciting. Society secretly craves the brutality they publicly condemn.","opus":"Industrial-metal shock theatre voicing a gleeful predator demanding bloodshed for its own sake. The sampled line about needing violence hints at a darker point beneath the aggression: the pull toward brutality as something craved rather than reasoned.","sonnet":"A raw incitement to cathartic chaos — the speaker craves physical violence, describes bodies falling and screams multiplying with pleasure, and frames it as a disease or hunger that needs to be fed, with no pretense of metaphor.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~we-are":{"haiku":"A song that positions the speaker and listeners as embodiments of collective trauma and regret, defining identity through emotional scars and societal decay.","opus":"Dope turn self-destruction into a taunting group identity, a narrator who claims to embody everything a listener would rather bury and grows more indifferent the deeper he sinks. Over grinding industrial metal he piles up drink, recklessness and poison as badges of honor, the repeated collective boast reframing ruin and apathy as a defiant persona built out of other people's shame.","sonnet":"A collective self-portrait as wound and warning — the voice speaks for something corrosive and unavoidable, the accumulated scar tissue of everyone's regrets, the internal static that won't go away. It is not a threat exactly but a reckoning: whatever darkness you carry, whatever you hope to forget, that is what they are made of and what they represent.","src":"haiku"},
"dope~you-spin-me-round-like-a-record":{"haiku":"Desire operates as pure consumption where the beloved exists only as object for gratification, movement and objectification replacing authentic connection.","opus":"A darker industrial cover of the Dead or Alive hit, all blunt physical craving and dizzy obsession, wanting the body and not the name.","sonnet":"Pure pursuit of physical attraction: the speaker fixates on someone across a room, declaring single-minded desire to get closer and have his way, using the dizzy spin of a record as the metaphor for infatuation.","src":"haiku"},
"dorian-electra~10-in-the-morning-demo":{"opus":"Hyperpop restlessness about outsourcing your whole sense of self to someone else, staying up till morning while they do the living for you. Electra threads it with anxious, ironic references to burnout and ego, wondering if the love of life has gone stale.","src":"opus"},
"dosser~glazed-over":{"haiku":"Stripping away compassion and restraint to escape into apathy and distance, surrendering integrity for peace.","opus":"Wanting to kill off your own humility and empathy just to survive, clinging to the last of someone's failing mind while planning your escape, a numb prayer that no one else ever ends up this hollowed out.","sonnet":"A glazed emotional collapse — the narrator is willing to let the last scraps of their empathy and humility die, hangs onto whatever is left of someone else's mind knowing it's barely anything, and ultimately decides nobody should have to go through this.","src":"haiku"},
"down-and-out~leech":{"haiku":"Parasitic manipulation drains the speaker dry; they refuse to be drained further and demand the tormentor leave.","opus":"Fury at a parasitic person who keeps coming back to drain warmth and attention, exposed as a hypocrite who won't practice what they preach. Biting down the words nearly breaks the speaker, who feels themselves about to snap.","sonnet":"Someone who let a toxic person into their life now wants them out — calling them a leech who drains them dry while demanding silence and compliance — and is at the breaking point of having their energy and sanity completely exhausted.","src":"haiku"},
"down-with-the-kings~out-of-control":{"haiku":"Rebellion against power structures and false leaders who exploit ordinary people, declaring personal liberation over control.","opus":"A defiant refusal to be held down by lying elites who hoard the crown, the narrator finally awake and choosing to break their rules. Its fury is class resentment turned into a rallying cry to lose control.","sonnet":"A defiant rejection of whoever has been holding the narrator back — systems, gatekeepers, false authority figures — declaring that the time for permission is over and the rules being broken are the ones that will be remembered.","src":"haiku"},
"downswing~bound-to-misery":{"haiku":"Monotony and self-directed suffering create entrapment where the speaker questions their capacity for change.","opus":"A hardcore reckoning with monotony and self-sabotage, a moth drawn to its own flame, stuck in a cycle he half-chooses. The push is to redesign himself and stop running, refusing to stay chained to the misery he keeps feeding.","sonnet":"Monotony and compulsive self-criticism have worn the speaker down — \"moth drawn to a flame, I can't help myself\" — and while a desire to redesign and stop running surfaces, the weight of misery feels structural, not merely situational.","src":"haiku"},
"down~on-march-the-saints":{"haiku":"Storms and change sweep through existence while faith and elevation offer false comfort against inevitable decline and the marching of saints toward ruin.","opus":"Anselmo and company on the grim inevitability of hard luck and the false comfort of religion, watching the self-appointed elect march on while the rest endure the season of storms. Southern fatalism sneering at easy salvation.","sonnet":"A meditation on catastrophic change and the irony of cleansing through destruction, where faith and religious authority are viewed skeptically even as the speaker grapples with loss and the need to find meaning amid storm and upheaval.","src":"haiku"},
"down~temptation-s-wings":{"haiku":"Temptation gnaws at the singer's wit and vision, leaving them pale and unable to shake its grip. Feeding internal cancer and watching life drain away, they remain powerless against its wings.","opus":"A grinding portrait of self-destruction, the narrator undone by an urge he can no longer resist, having staked everything on rational thought only to watch it collapse into isolation and despair. Down are a New Orleans metal supergroup fronted by Pantera's Phil Anselmo, and this Southern sludge dirge sinks deep into genuine self-loathing and the exhausted question of how a life came apart.","sonnet":"Reason given over to a life that then fell apart — the speaker chose logic over connection, declared independence with conviction, and now watches wit and purpose dissolve under the weight of something they cannot shake loose. Temptation is not named or specified; it is just a pair of wings that won't release, draining the color out of everything.","src":"haiku"},
"dr-dre~i-need-a-doctor":{"haiku":"Mutual rescue and interdependence sustain through crisis; a mentor and apprentice fight to resurrect each other's belief and purpose.","opus":"Eminem's open letter to the mentor who saved his career, now creatively paralyzed after his son's death, begging Dre to believe in himself again and repaying the debt of being signed when no one else would touch him.","sonnet":"Two voices entwined in mutual debt and crisis: Eminem calls out to a creatively dormant Dre who saved his life, begging him to return and make music again, while Dre's verse reflects on loss, loyalty, and the fair-weather people who vanished when they were needed most.","src":"haiku"},
"dr-misio~mr-hui":{"haiku":"The narrator warns others not to trust them: they shift between charm and monstrosity, killing and returning. Beauty masks ugliness and deception.","opus":"The Polish rock act, fronted by actor Arkadiusz Jakubik, delivers a savage self-portrait: the narrator warns others to keep their distance because behind the charming, witty exterior he metamorphoses into something monstrous, ugly and lying, even slipping out to kill when he cannot sleep. The crude titular pun drives home the self-disgust, a confession dressed as a grotesque cabaret.","sonnet":"A self-portrait issued as a warning: the narrator presents as charming and funny and tender, then admits this is the mask before transformation into something monstrous. Polish confession wrapped in black humor — stay away, he insists, because what he becomes is genuinely ugly.","src":"haiku"},
"draeden~hit-the-floor":{"haiku":"Defiance burns through soul as fire, using remaining strength to dismantle weakness and rebuild from the lowest point.","opus":"Defiance from rock bottom, a narrator who can't fall because he's already low daring an enemy to come at him. The bravado masks a search for his own mind in the dark, war and survival blurring into one long fight.","sonnet":"A speaker who has already hit bottom — \"I'm already low\" — refuses to let someone who has proven hollow and see-through keep any further claim on them; the fire in the soul keeps being fed and the door gets kicked down as defiance replaces collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"drag-me-out~it-was-easy":{"haiku":"An artist fights to prove their doubters wrong and reclaim their space after being nearly abandoned. They climbed out of doubt and now hold themselves above those who watched them fail, ready to return.","opus":"Metalcore act Drag Me Out target a fake hanger-on obsessed with buzz, an uninvited freeloader who lives dishonestly and turns on people while the speaker grinds to silence his critics. Boasting and bitterness mix as he calls himself the best storyteller around, still holding his broken pieces together, yet he offers to mend the other's loneliness, resentment shadowed by a stubborn loyalty.","sonnet":"A self-assured narrator dismisses a hypocritical scene full of fair-weather allies and unearned attention, asserting hard-won competence against those who only show up once the work is done. The defiance is tinged with exhaustion — still in it, still the best at telling his own story, but watching others rotate around him like opportunists.","src":"haiku"},
"dragged-under~all-of-us":{"haiku":"The speaker drowns under the weight of the world and panic, feeling like they swallowed a blade. They admit to demons, aggression, and digging holes of their own making.","opus":"The Seattle band Dragged Under pour punk urgency into a confession of buckling under mental strain, too many hours trapped in thought, dread choked down whole. Its insistence that this collapse is simply the human condition turns private breakdown into common ground. Hiding the demons and feeling infernal heat in a pit of his own making, the narrator claims the wreckage without pretending he's escaped.","sonnet":"Mental overload and social anxiety presented not as shameful aberration but as a universal human condition. Buried in panic and self-recrimination, the narrator reaches for a soft landing and finds it in the recognition that everyone around them is falling in a similar free fall.","src":"haiku"},
"dragged-under~brainwash-broadcast":{"opus":"A scathing indictment of the news media as a fear-mongering propaganda machine that programs, conditions and poisons viewers with manufactured panic, doom and misinformation, all packaged as comfortable entertainment to keep the audience docile.","sonnet":"A media-critique song about the news industry's addiction to manufactured panic and deliberate misinformation — every channel runs the same doom loop, poisoning the youth with a curated version of truth, and the audience is conditioned to obey through repeated exposure to orchestrated fear. The satire is pointed but the target is broad.","src":"sonnet"},
"dragged-under~chelsea": {"haiku":"A speaker repeatedly fails their loyal partner, struggles with self-recognition, and fears disappointing those closest to them. The song explores how lower expectations paradoxically enable greater failure.","opus":"A candid apology addressed to a partner named Chelsea, the narrator owning that he repeats his failures, snaps at her, and can't be everything she needs. His bruised honesty, that lowering her expectations spares them both disappointment, sketches a man certain he'll fail her yet grateful she stays and steadies him through every stumble.","sonnet":"An apology song addressed to a partner named Chelsea — the narrator knows they keep failing and losing their temper, can't meet her expectations, but finds her loyalty both humbling and guilt-inducing.","src":"haiku","fable":"An apology addressed by name to the partner who keeps forgiving what the singer keeps doing — a cycle of temper, an old grievance with himself he can't lay to rest, and a stranger's face staring back at him in the mirror. What lifts it above a standard sorry is the bridge, which flips the plea into a defense: lower your hopes and I'll fail you less, so the promise itself becomes the trap. The chorus refuses that logic anyway, insisting she stays whether he climbs or comes apart, and the tension between his managed expectations and her unconditional presence is the whole song — he's negotiating his own worth down while she declines the discount.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Chelsea is a real person — vocalist Anthony Cappocchi’s girlfriend, thanked by name for staying through it.","themes":["shame & repression","love & desire","identity & becoming"],"means":"apology becomes negotiation of the speaker's own worth","built":"bridge flips the plea into a defensive lowering of expectations"},
"dragged-under~chelsea-live":{"opus":"An apology and gratitude to Chelsea, a steadfast partner who stays through the speaker's repeated mistakes, temper, and self-loathing; he warns he'll never be all she expects and that lowering her hopes means letting her down less, yet she remains by his side whether he rises or falls.","src":"opus"},
"dragged-under~covered-in-sin": {"haiku":"Someone suffers from anxiety and self-loathing, feeling guilty for their thoughts and unable to escape internal darkness.","opus":"A raw picture of dread and self-hatred, the speaker fighting panic, tripping over his own words, and rehearsing his own tombstone. His defiance, that he won't act blameless yet won't reform either, wanting to crawl free of his own guilt-soaked body while demons scratch to enter, makes it a flat refusal of both cure and pretense.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone paralysed by anxiety, panic attacks, and a mind wired for worst-case thinking — they feel sinful, alien to themselves, and unable to locate a sky that isn't grey, yet stubbornly refuse to change or seek help.","src":"haiku","fable":"A panic-attack interior monologue that Dragged Under stages as post-hardcore defiance, then quietly dismantles. The verses are clinical about the illness itself — intrusive dread, a stuttering inability to speak, the moment where death starts to read as the calm option — while the chorus swaggers, ordering everyone to keep their noses out because nothing about these ways is bending. What the bridge exposes is that the swagger was armor: the wish to get out of one's own skin, and the demons crowding at the door to get in, recast the refusal to change as something closer to I don't believe I can. Read straight, the refrain is stubbornness; read against the verses it protects, it's a person negotiating with self-loathing and calling the surrender pride.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"defiant chorus reread as capitulation to self-loathing","built":"bridge dismantles the swagger the chorus performs"},
"dragged-under~crooked-halos":{"haiku":"Two fallen souls cage each other with twisted devotion; neither fool nor angel, their corruption stays hidden from outside judgment.","opus":"Two damaged people bound in a doomed, defiant devotion, an angel gone bad and a devil's kid whose crooked halos fool no one. It romanticizes a love chained in hell, clinging past the point of health because leaving feels worse than staying.","sonnet":"A 'fallen angel and the devil's son' whose relationship is a match made in hell — rose stems for flowers, a permanent knot, and 'till death do we rot' — the crooked halos fool no one but the pull remains, chains binding them together even as they drag each other down.","src":"haiku"},
"dragged-under~feel-it": {"haiku":"Someone chases the lost intensity of a first love, mourning the fading of what once overwhelmed them. A friendship fractures when they cannot change and the other refuses to pick up the phone.","opus":"The Seattle band chase a lost euphoria, the singer mourning the meaning he found when he first came undone and craving that flooding surge once more. Playacting sustains passing highs as a friendship crumbles beneath cutting remarks. Deadened to letdowns and ignored calls, he lands on the raw confession that perhaps the person who must transform is himself.","sonnet":"A speaker mourns the loss of early, overwhelming feeling — the kind of intensity that made music and connection transcendent — while also grieving a friendship that faded without resolution. Both losses fold into a single ache: the need to be overwhelmed again by something real rather than living in its memory.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about chasing the intensity of a first experience and finding every repeat a diminished copy — whatever gave the thing its weight has drained off, leaving only the appetite for the sensation itself. The early verses treat this as a private hunger, a plea to be flooded again the way you only can be once, before you knew the moments were disposable. But the second half quietly reveals the missing thing was never abstract: it was a person, someone in whom the singer saw a hidden good and who now wounds him with an unspectacular cruelty and stops answering the phone. The wanting-to-feel-it, then, doubles as wanting them back, and the song lands on the harder admission underneath the anthem — that the person who has to change might be him, which turns the chorus's outward craving inward into self-reckoning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss","identity & becoming"],"means":"craving sensation revealed as craving a specific person","built":"second half relocates an abstract anthem inward into self-reckoning"},
"dragged-under~here-for-war":{"haiku":"Choosing confrontation and standing firm against someone who has failed you.","opus":"Cutting off an ungrateful, self-sabotaging person who squandered every chance, declaring independence and readiness to fight on without them.","sonnet":"Cutting off a chronically failing, unreliable person and finding the strength to stand alone without them.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a parting recast as a battlefield, and the malice is intentional: the first half tallies the other person's every wound to justify a verdict that their physical presence no longer counts. The refrain flips desertion into endurance, drawing strength from the loss rather than merely surviving it, which is the metalcore trick of burning heartbreak as fuel. What keeps the martial pose honest is that the opponent has quit the field entirely. So the vow to stand unbowed guards against one thing only: the pull to let this person back in.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance"],"means":"desertion recast as endurance to guard against the pull of letting someone back in","built":"the martial framing holds precisely because the opponent has already quit the field, leaving the vow with no target but the singer's own resolve"},
"dragged-under~hypochondria":{"haiku":"Genuine illness dismissed by others as psychological delusion, leading to desperation for medical validation that never comes.","opus":"A first-person account of health anxiety and being disbelieved, terrified of an illness others insist is only in his head. Torn between fear of dying and wanting to live, he wages a losing war with his own mind.","sonnet":"A person convinced something is genuinely wrong with them is dismissed as paranoid by everyone around them: the song captures the terror of feeling ill and unheard, of losing the battle with one's own mind while the world insists there is nothing to fix.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is an accusation borrowed from a doubter, and the whole song is the speaker trying to prove a sickness no one else will acknowledge is real. Dragged Under bend that dismissal into metalcore's clenched grammar: the jab about it being imaginary curdles, because for a mind at war with itself the skull is exactly where the danger lives. He fantasizes about hospital corridors and a strapped-down room not as horror but as vindication — a doctor finally naming the thing so the dread stops being his fault. By the bridge the enemy has migrated from the disbeliever into himself, so the closing question about wanting to be gone lands not as a taunt but as the one wound no argument can talk him out of.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression","suicide"],"means":"fantasizing diagnosis as vindication rather than as dread","built":"the enemy migrates from disbeliever to self across the bridge"},
"dragged-under~instability": {"fable":"A confessional in the hardcore tradition of self-indictment, where the song works by dismantling its own alibis: the opening verse floats the paranoid comfort that some outside force is engineering the singer's suffering, then dismisses that instinct as a crutch for cowards. What replaces the scapegoat is an inventory of a stalled life — the mind gone grey and forgotten like something left too long on a shelf, the numb loop of debt and idleness and a glowing rectangle stared at with nothing behind the eyes. The chorus compresses the whole thesis into a chant: the enemy, the bitterness, the slow self-erasure all trace back to a single unstable source, and it is him. Dragged Under's punk-metalcore bluntness is the mechanism here, refusing to let any metaphor soften the accusation. The bridge is where the read complicates, because underneath the bravado of waving off pity there is a plea for rescue and a confession of feeling unseen — the mask of needing no one barely covering a man who knows he has fallen short of who he meant to become and can find no defendant but himself.","fableDeep":"It starts by mocking its own genre's reflex — 'feels like someone's out to get me' is dismissed in the next line as 'something that the weak believe' — refusing the persecutor that angry music usually requires. The shovel line is the thesis: tools, screens, routines and scenery are all innocent ('change of scenery won't help… it's all about your state of mind'). But the bridge complicates the honesty it's built on: 'don't offer an apology, don't need your fucking sympathy' — the self-blame has hardened into a fortress that keeps help out as effectively as denial would. Owning the problem, the song admits, can be one more way of staying alone with it.","haiku":"Self-inflicted mental torment and accepting responsibility for one's own misery.","opus":"Self-inflicted mental instability and depression, accepting that one's own bitterness and inertia, not others, is the source of misery.","sonnet":"Self-awareness of depression, stagnation, and self-sabotage, recognizing oneself as the source of one's own misery.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming"],"means":"every alibi dismantled until the only defendant left is the self","built":"the opening paranoid comfort dismissed mid-song by the very speaker who floated it"},
"dragged-under~instability-live":{"haiku":"Internal collapse accelerates as the speaker becomes their own saboteur, recognizing that external forces matter less than the misery they generate within.","opus":"A brutal self-audit of depression as self-inflicted, refusing sympathy and excuses and naming the speaker himself as the sole cause of his own misery, the only enemy that is killing him.","sonnet":"A self-indictment: the speaker refuses to blame anything outside themselves for the hole they dug, names themselves their own worst enemy, and catalogues the emptiness — no ambition, no self-respect, slave to routine — before refusing sympathy and acknowledging they are the only reason for their own misery.","src":"haiku"},
"dragged-under~just-like-me":{"haiku":"Speaker warns against emulating their self-destructive, ruthless, and uncaring nature.","opus":"Dragged Under weaponize self-loathing into a taunt, daring the listener to admit being just as sick of themselves. Ruthless, useless, with nothing left to lose, the narrator refuses grace and warns others to pray they never become like him. The punk-metal snarl reframes an anti-hero pose as brutal honesty, the plea to look harder inviting recognition rather than sympathy.","sonnet":"A bristling self-portrait of someone difficult, antisocial, and unwilling to soften their edges for approval. Rather than seeking sympathy, the narrator holds the uglier parts forward as a warning — being like this is exhausting and nobody should romanticize it as identity.","src":"haiku"},
"dragged-under~just-like-me-live":{"opus":"A defiant self-portrait that weaponizes self-loathing, daring anyone who romanticizes the damaged frontman to actually try being him. The bravado is real, but so is the warning underneath it: don't envy this.","src":"opus"},
"dragged-under~mad-punks": {"haiku":"Rebellion calcifies into conformity as punk identity becomes spectacle; the speaker mourns the transformation of once-fierce resistance into compliant theater.","opus":"An accusation aimed at former rebels who traded genuine anger for obedient talking points, going quiet the moment defiance got uncomfortable. The repeated question is where all the real fight went.","sonnet":"About the disappearance of genuine punk spirit — people who were once angry, defiant, and violent have become compliant and performative, reciting talking points and following their elected handlers like puppets. The narrator mourns the death of real rebellion in a world where silencing enemies has replaced practicing empathy and dramatic protest has replaced actual action.","src":"haiku","fable":"An accusation aimed at the punk scene itself: the song charges that the people who once styled themselves as rebels have swapped genuine anger for costume and obedience, marching in lockstep behind the very authorities and talking points a punk was supposed to distrust. The title question carries the whole argument — where the counterculture curdled into a compliant crowd repeating scripts, its old violence tamed into performance while the underlying rot went unaddressed. The refrain hammers this as loss and disappearance rather than argument, so the repetition of the vanishing punks does the work a rebuttal can't: they aren't answered, they're simply gone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"the counterculture's vanishing hammered as loss, not argued as failure","built":"repetition of the gone punks does what a rebuttal cannot"},
"dragged-under~never-enough":{"opus":"The eternal generational standoff, told from the kid's side: a father measuring success in steady jobs and settling down, a son who refuses the cage and throws the disappointment back as a question about whether the parent is even really living.","src":"opus"},
"dragged-under~riot": {"haiku":"A call to resistance against systems of control and oppression, urging collective uprising against silencing and manipulation by those in power.","opus":"A call to revolt against a system that keeps people distracted, indebted, and obedient while pretending to offer real choices. The narrators declare themselves a new generation ready to start a riot and take the world back.","sonnet":"A call to revolt against a media-controlled, politically rigged system that keeps people passive and spending, urging a new generation to break free and reclaim what has been taken from them.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dragged Under build a straightforward protest anthem by stacking a diagnosis before the revolt: the verses catalogue a life of engineered dependence — screen-fixation, faith-leaders who monetize obedience, ownership that requires paying a toll to use what you already have, ballots offering loose coins where reform should be from candidates chosen behind closed doors. The chorus converts that grievance into the title's refusal to be silenced, but it quietly complicates its own defiance — the home rising up in arms tilts against itself, the shared love already gone, so the riot is as much a civil war among the beaten-down as a strike against the powerful. What keeps it from pure sloganeering is that admission of exhaustion beneath the fight, the still-breathing-but-barely hedge that the bridge's fresh-blood rallying cry tries to shout over.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"revolt exposed as collapse of the revolting","built":"diagnosis-then-chorus structure undercuts its own rallying cry"},
"dragged-under~roots": {"haiku":"Musicians are exploited by an industry that controls them, manufactures their image, and profits from their labor.","opus":"A furious indictment of the music industry that owns artists, demands compromise, and discards them when they don't chart. It ends in a reclaiming of self, tearing up the contract to go back to the roots that made the work worth doing.","sonnet":"A broadside against the music industry's exploitation of artists — labels demanding conformity, owning contracts, and profiting long after discarding the people they signed, with the narrator resolving to return to making music for its own sake.","src":"haiku","fable":"A ventriloquist's song: for two verses the voice is the label itself, purring threats about contracts and career and the flattery a hungry newcomer owes every gatekeeper who can green-light an audition, so that the industry indicts itself out of its own mouth. Then the title turns on its owner. Returning to where it all started first arrives as the executive's blithe boast, but the bridge and final verse wrench the mic to the artist the machine chewed up — a burned-out unknown whose one signature mortgaged everything he was and everything he might become, and who now watches the stage tricks fail and the illusion thin to nothing — and the same phrase becomes his refusal, roots meaning the thing untouched by the contract rather than the origin the suits nostalgically flatter. The recurring chorus about golden old days is the pivot both speakers share and mean oppositely: for the label it's a marketing lament, for the artist it's the only ground left to reclaim. The trick is that Dragged Under never editorialize; they let the predator's charm and the prey's shame occupy the same melody, and trust you to feel which one is telling the truth.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","politics & society","identity & becoming"],"means":"title phrase stolen back by the artist from the industry","built":"dual-speaker structure lets the same lyric indict two voices at once"},
"dragged-under~suffer":{"haiku":"Codependent agony intertwines commitment with torture; the speaker will suffer alongside, unable to leave or save.","opus":"A lover offers a total, mutual pact of shared pain, asking over and over whether the other would hurt for him as he would for them. He owns up to forcing smiles, feeling wolves at his neck and abandoning her despite promising everything, terrified of being left alone in his own hell when things fall apart.","sonnet":"A plea for reciprocal suffering in a relationship under extreme pressure: the speaker has already failed their partner, left them behind, and knows they're trapped in their own hell — 'I don't wanna be here by myself' — and the question of whether the other will stay when the world bleeds is left open.","src":"haiku"},
"dragged-under~the-hardest-drug":{"haiku":"Addiction separates the speaker from loved ones; losing someone is harder than kicking drugs, leaving only grief and isolation.","opus":"Stress and self-doubt drive a narrator to numb himself with a chemical he calls medicine, until a departing partner reframes the addiction as the thing killing them both. Seattle's Dragged Under fold the anthemic hooks of modern rock into a bleak: the lover leaves a note and a warning, and the cruel twist lands as the person becomes the hardest drug to quit.","sonnet":"Addiction and the loss of a relationship collapse into the same wound. A partner finally leaves rather than watch someone die; the narrator, needle in vein, discovers that quitting the substance feels easier than accepting the absence of the person who walked out the door.","src":"haiku"},
"dragged-under~the-hardest-drug-live":{"opus":"Loving an addict and finally choosing not to go down with them. The cruelty is that the drug wins, and leaving becomes its own withdrawal, the person themselves the habit hardest to quit.","src":"opus"},
"dragged-under~the-real-you":{"haiku":"A person who stayed authentically true to themselves confronts someone defined by counterfeit charm and manufactured conformity.","opus":"A contemptuous takedown of a hollow poser wearing a borrowed persona, the narrator relishing the moment the mask slips and the fake falls. It sharpens into self-definition, staying true set against a life spent performing.","sonnet":"A contemptuous dissection of a social faker — someone who memorizes lines, borrows charm, and craves validation but has no authentic self underneath — the narrator takes pleasure in knowing this person will eventually be exposed.","src":"haiku","fable":"A takedown of the social climber who assembles a personality from other people's cues, memorizing the correct opinions the way an actor learns blocking, only to be told the whole clique already sees through the costume. What sharpens it past a generic diss is the speaker's stake: the closing verse plants a boast about earning selfhood the slow, unglamorous way and losing friends for it, so the contempt reads as the flip side of someone who paid for authenticity in years, now savoring the collapse of the shortcut-taker and relishing being proved right. In Dragged Under's early-2020s metalcore register the breakdown does the argument's work — the taunt cycles until the target's borrowed charm wears out under sheer repetition, and the outro simply writes them off as a lifelong pretender living for the pageantry.","fv":2,"themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"contempt worn as proof the speaker paid for selfhood","built":"the breakdown repeats the taunt until borrowed charm wears out"},
"dragged-under~this-holiday":{"opus":"A gleefully misanthropic anti-Christmas rant against forced holiday cheer and consumerism, wishing to be left alone and fantasizing about destroying decorations and telling everyone to stay away.","sonnet":"A sardonic rejection of Christmas and its commercialized, forced-community rituals: the narrator, who works retail through the holidays, despises the consumerism, the mandatory family gatherings, and the yuletide cheer, and wants to be left alone.","src":"sonnet"},
"dragged-under~upright-animals":{"haiku":"Humanity descends into predatory competition where civility masks savagery and the hunt becomes the only law governing an evolved species bent on mutual annihilation.","opus":"A misanthropic view of humanity as predators who dressed their savagery up in ties and phones, hunting what they hate and voting predators in to lord over the flock, breeding only to destroy, animals the whole time.","sonnet":"Humanity is recast as predatory upright animals who hunt what they hate rather than what they need, electing wolves to rule sheep and weaponising phones; the speaker asks whether he is prey or one of them, watching destruction and self-congratulation advance together.","src":"haiku"},
"dragonforce~through-the-fire-and-flames":{"haiku":"Warriors push through hellish wasteland toward freedom; desperation and defiance burn as they carry on despite despair.","opus":"Riders charge through hellish wastelands and lightning-cracked skies, waiting for a day of freedom while enduring the pain of lives wasted across a thousand days. It is heroic fantasy as pure endurance, carrying on through fire. DragonForce, British power-metal known for blistering speed, match this mythic quest with hyperspeed guitar heroics, the relentless tempo itself embodying the endless fight the words describe.","sonnet":"Exiled warriors cross infernal wastelands toward a battle they may not survive, carrying grief for countless lost lives. The journey has no clean end — only endurance as identity. Against hellfire and desolation they press on, the act of carrying on itself becoming the only available form of meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"drake~started-from-the-bottom-explicit-version":{"haiku":"Rags-to-riches narrative affirms earned success and loyalty; the speaker's rise validates authentic grind and unshakeable crew bonds.","opus":"Drake's rebuttal to anyone doubting he ever struggled, a loyalty anthem about arriving with the same crew and refusing to explain or change the story. The chain worn even at home is pride guarding against being forgotten where he came from.","sonnet":"A loyalty anthem built around the distance traveled from humble beginnings — working nights, living at his mother's — to commanding stages: the success is meaningful precisely because it's shared with the people who were there before the money, and the speaker never lets anyone rewrite the story of how they got here.","src":"haiku"},
"drapht~don-quixote-feat-hilltop-hoods":{"haiku":"Madness becomes a refuge from societal breakdown; the narrator embraces delusion as the only sane response to a fundamentally insane world.","opus":"Aussie hip-hop casting the modern grind through Cervantes, a man tilting at windmills who'd rather live in delusion than the soul-killing sanity of the workaday world. Escapism celebrated as the only honest response to a broken reality.","sonnet":"Three voices riff on the Don Quixote myth to celebrate joyful, productive delusion: embracing madness over mundane reality, tilting at windmills as a lifestyle, and escaping factory routine through a head full of adventure with the full awareness that the world thinks you're losing it.","src":"haiku"},
"drapht~jimmy-recard":{"haiku":"Name and destiny intertwine in comic resignation; the speaker wishes their luck flowed from identity itself, envying someone charmed by fortune while they remain plain and forgotten.","opus":"Bored with his plain name Paul, the rapper invents Jimmy Recard, a charmed, effortlessly beloved alter ego who wins at everything. It is a playful daydream about how a different identity might have made his luck and life better.","sonnet":"A comic fantasy of nominal destiny: the speaker wonders whether his ordinary name \"Paul\" condemned him to ordinary luck, and constructs the mythology of Jimmy Recard — bras thrown at his feet, Andy Garcia face, all things turning to gold — as a figure of effortless charisma the speaker would gladly have been born as.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-beard~rage":{"haiku":"Uncontained energy erupts through the speaker's existence, marking them as fundamentally different from the ordinary world. Playing it safe becomes impossible when the beast inside demands release.","opus":"A motivational hype anthem dressed in fantasy language, urging you to let the caged beast out and actually live before the game ends. Under the swagger it's really self-help: permission to feel, and to stop playing it safe.","sonnet":"A speaker celebrates cutting loose from restraint — letting the inner beast out of the cage for a day of rage and living — interweaving street braggadocio, a California woman, and a sense that 'spells in the song' can break chains and transform stagnant energy.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-demon~f-e-a-r":{"haiku":"Fear materializes as an immortal entity embedded within flesh, feeding on doubt and conviction until the soul shatters. It persists beyond reason or resistance, a certainty that outlives the host.","opus":"Fear personified as an ancient parasite that has stalked you since birth and lives inside your own second-guessing. The chilling turn is that it cannot be killed, only outlived, because it dies only when you do.","sonnet":"Fear personified speaks directly to its host — it lives in the stomach, the second-guessing, the moments of compromise, and it promises to outlast every attempt to drown it out, dying only when its carrier does.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-drop~everyone-s-out":{"haiku":"Someone addicted to substances has isolated themselves and lost everyone's trust; a friend urges them to escape the spiral.","opus":"An intervention aimed at an addict, calling out lies and pills that muffle every cry for help while friends drift away, sick of being blamed. Beneath the confrontation runs desperate love and an outstretched hand meeting only rebellion. Dream Drop frame this as a plea from someone refusing to abandon a friend spiraling toward hell, insisting the wake-up comes now.","sonnet":"A person whose addiction and self-destructive behavior has driven everyone away is confronted by a last voice still reaching through the wreckage. Friends, family, and patience have all been exhausted. The plea is urgent and conflicted — partly accusatory, partly desperate — urging the addict to fight back before the isolation becomes permanent.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-drop~nudes":{"opus":"Desire mediated by screens, where a phone full of images can't substitute for a body actually present. It's about intimacy in a digital age curdling into frustration, even naming capitalism as the thing hollowing out the longing.","src":"opus"},
"dream-on-dreamer~feel-so-empty":{"haiku":"The narrator struggles with self-destruction and emptiness, waiting for death to relieve the weight they carry while cycling through despair.","opus":"The Australian metalcore band renders self-destruction as swallowing something toxic, the narrator so hollowed out he barely registers being alive. Careening toward a death-figure who might ease his burden bit by bit, he still feels his heart insisting on survival. The pull between sinking under and clawing to the surface keeps the despair from collapsing into surrender.","sonnet":"Self-destruction so total it produces a kind of numbness that outlasts even the pain. A person crashing through cycles of collapse and burning looks toward the reaper not with fear but as relief from an unbearable emotional weight being shed one piece at a time.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-on-dreamer~love":{"haiku":"A relationship fractures under the weight of accumulated change and suffering as the speaker witnesses their partner's transformation into someone unrecognizable. Love cannot survive the slow drift into incompatibility.","opus":"Watching a bond spoil as it happens and pushing through the painful talk, the narrator owning that what they had is no longer what it was and can't last, while the other keeps trying to leave the room.","sonnet":"Two people watch their relationship deteriorate in real time, both aware they are \"driving in slow motion for the edge\" yet unable to stop; the speaker forces a confrontation, insisting the other sit and hear that their love has irreversibly changed.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~anxious-state-of-mind":{"haiku":"The speaker shoulders others' burdens while deteriorating internally, suffocating under borrowed weight while insomnia and fear calcify their mind and isolation deepens.","opus":"A caretaker buckles under carrying other people's anxieties, standing guard at the door of another's mind against nightmares while resenting the assumption that he wears a bulletproof jacket. The turn is reassurance: no one has to feel alone or fight their battles by themselves, even as the speaker admits he too is done and cannot help himself.","sonnet":"A person crushed by carrying everyone else's weight confesses they are not bulletproof and never were — \"there's a lump sat in your throat it's not getting easier to swallow\" names the overload — while offering the reassurance that no one has to battle nightmares alone.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~bloom":{"opus":"Seizing a fleeting window of opportunity while grieving that everything, people and feelings alike, is temporary. The resolve to pour everything in comes braided with loss and the old truth that you value things only once they're gone.","src":"opus"},
"dream-state~chin-up-princess":{"haiku":"Encouragement to persevere and endure, fake positivity masking despair.","opus":"A woman who ruled from a pedestal collapsing into isolation and drink, her false comforters the bottle and a hollow reassurance about tomorrow. It's an unsparing look at how perfectionism and abandonment drive self-destruction.","sonnet":"A woman who ruled everyone from a pedestal watches everyone she loved vanish — she sought perfection in them, they let her down, and she hurts herself worse than any of them did. The sarcastic refrain \"bottle after bottle, that's what the voice inside her says\" is the voice offering tomorrow as consolation while the spiral continues.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~day-seeker":{"haiku":"Nightlife dependency gives way to hard-won recovery through confronting demons directly; the speaker discovers strength in daylight and gratitude after years of self-destruction.","opus":"A recovery anthem about climbing out of a stretch spent wishing never to wake, now confronting his inner demons directly and choosing the light. The gratitude turns outward, offered as proof to others that they can make it through too.","sonnet":"A recovery narrative: the narrator describes having chased self-destruction for years (numbing, wishing for endless sleep, wasting days), then turning to face their demons directly and coming out the other side with gratitude and hope. The declaration \"now I'm a day seeker\" is the pivot — from someone who could not bear daylight to someone actively seeking it.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~hand-in-hand":{"haiku":"Collective survival requires vulnerability and mutual witness; the speaker offers presence without judgment, holding space for shared darkness and gradual healing.","opus":"A survival anthem about the assault of intrusive thoughts and a desperate search for the divine through hell, insisting nobody has to battle alone. It recasts anguish as something met side by side, where hardship forges resilience and everyone, underneath, just wants to be cared for.","sonnet":"A song of mutual survival through mental illness: intrusive thoughts, tightened jaw, running into hell looking for God — met with the promise to be there on the other side, holding hands with everyone fighting to live, and the refrain \"we all wanna be, just wanna feel loved\" as the only common ground.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~made-up-smile":{"opus":"A takedown of social phoniness and staged contentment, the singer refusing to be one more copy propping up a false grin. Stuck in a spectacle of pretending and hunting for genuine meaning and affection, he flatly won't fake being okay.","sonnet":"A rejection of performative happiness and social conformity — the narrator is bored and disillusioned by a world that demands fake smiles, where integrity has been replaced by hypocrisy, and asks whether any of it has been enough or real.","src":"sonnet"},
"dream-state~monsters":{"haiku":"A person resists self-blame for past mistakes and tries to break free from internal fear-based self-sabotage that keeps them trapped in darkness.","opus":"The Welsh alt-metal band, led by CJ Gilpin, turns self-acceptance into a challenge, wearing her own disarray almost like fine clothing and treating old blunders as the very things that made her who she is. The title creatures are self-nourished fears that loop endlessly through the mind, and the song moves from lingering in darkness toward a shared push to open up and turn into something luminous.","sonnet":"Self-blame and self-reclamation trade places throughout: mistakes are owned, then recontextualized as the material that built the actual self. The monsters fed by fear and self-criticism keep chasing, but staying in the dark and hoarding truth is the greater threat. Becoming the light requires speaking what has been suppressed.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~new-waves":{"haiku":"Exhausted from rebuilding but committed to finding new direction and escaping old patterns.","opus":"A survivor's insistence on a fresh start after mental-health struggle, refusing the numbing habits and wearing the scars as proof of the work. It's about wanting one more chance and building it yourself.","sonnet":"A survivor of personal collapse insists on beginning again — crediting hard work and emotional honesty for how far they have come, and reaching toward a new life free of the chemical and emotional crutches that once kept them barely afloat.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~open-windows":{"haiku":"Society operates as a facade of aspiration and perfection while people live behind invisible barriers, afraid to be honest or resist systemic fear.","opus":"Welsh band Dream State condemn a curated, debt-trapping world where people trade their integrity chasing an impossible ideal and lives are staged for show. Trapped on display and unable to shut it out, the narrator insists he's worth more than that and must turn on the forces peddling fear. A track diagnosing a culture that feeds on its own while clinging to defiance.","sonnet":"Frustration at systemic failures — healthcare gatekeeping, social media's distorting mirror, and a culture that glamorizes rather than addresses its contradictions. Despite the diagnoses of madness and hypocrisy circling overhead, a conviction persists that collectively people are worth more than the systems trapping them.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~primrose":{"haiku":"Spiraling self-destruction through substance abuse and shame; the speaker cannot achieve balance and repeatedly falls back into self-loathing.","opus":"CJ Gilpin's unvarnished account of depression and drinking, calling in sick because you cannot face yourself, on your knees begging a god you've broken from for any release. The horror is the loop, forever falling back on the same self you're trying to escape.","sonnet":"About hitting bottom with alcohol and depression — calling in sick to work, drunk and on her knees praying for release, letting demons back in, unable to let go; raw and unsparing about the self-destruction of a bad episode.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~still-dreaming":{"haiku":"Persistent hope resists external judgment; the speaker chooses faith in their own trajectory despite criticism.","opus":"A defiant refusal of a doubter's verdict that he's too far gone to salvage, reframing that haunting voice as a ghost he outruns by choosing to keep dreaming. What falls short by someone else's measure is enough by his own, self-acceptance as escape from standards he never agreed to.","sonnet":"A person who was told they were unsalvageable has erased their self-doubt and broken free, refusing to meet standards they never agreed to — \"I never met your standards 'cause I am only me\" — and choosing to keep dreaming over giving the critic what they want.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~taunt-me":{"haiku":"Psychological torment and provocation; demons taunt from within the threshold of consciousness.","opus":"Living under the psychological weight of a tormentor who has stolen sleep and peace, met with stubborn refusal to stay down. The strength is exhausted rather than triumphant, resilience worn thin by relentless haunting.","sonnet":"A haunting that has replaced sleep — the presence is so persistent that the speaker no longer remembers resting without it — but the damage only reinforces the refusal to give up: always getting back up, never surrendering, while wondering if the person doing the haunting is even as happy as they pretend to be.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~twenty-letters":{"haiku":"Through introspection and self-acceptance, the narrator learns to walk alone and hold hope despite pressure; they choose not to break.","opus":"Sitting alone in the cold of night, a narrator resolves not to let go, having poured everything into twenty written letters and learned to walk alone in a new direction. Welsh post-hardcore band Dream State, whose vocalist has spoken openly of mental-health struggle, build defiance into the refrain, hope held tight against anything that would pull the speaker apart.","sonnet":"Someone emerges from a period of cold darkness and near-surrender by writing their pain into twenty letters, then learning to walk forward alone. Resilience hardens into resolve — outside forces cannot break what has already chosen to be undone and rebuilt on its own terms.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-state~white-lies-original":{"haiku":"Addiction spirals inward as confessed secrets betray attempts at recovery, the narrator unable to change destructive patterns despite awareness of the damage inflicted.","opus":"An addict fighting cocaine and the lies around it, caring less about the harm to himself than the harm to a loved one, and finally admitting he can't recover alone.","sonnet":"Addiction — signalled by \"white lines\" and the white lies used to hide them — is addressed as a split between self-destruction and concern for the person watching it happen; the speaker knows they won't change but cares more about hurting someone else than being hurt, and finally breaks down asking for help.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~6-00": {"haiku":"A man trapped between obligation and trauma cannot shake dreams of past suffering; melody and memory blur into drowning pain while he struggles to answer calls before he loses all capacity.","opus":"Bracketed by lines from Joyce's The Dead, a portrait of a man drowning slowly in routine and old pain, kept alive by promises and one sound he can't shake. The plea is to answer life's call before the numbness wins.","sonnet":"A man trapped in depression and emotional numbness drags himself through early mornings and parking lots, unable to shake something that has broken him — the song asks whether it's too late to surface before he drowns.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of a man dismantled by the grind of obligation, the song watches him move through a day he can no longer feel, running on duty long after meaning has bled out of it. Its emotional engine is the film sample bracketing the track — Huston's adaptation of Joyce's The Dead, where Aunt Kate's flat weariness at ritual devotion turns dawn observance into hollow performance, the same spiritual exhaustion the verses then dramatize. The imagery splits into two hostile families: mechanical clock-time of alarms and idling engines against a private, drowning inner noise the man keeps chasing and dreading, so that finding it at last reads as both breakthrough and surrender. Around the keyboard and guitar solos the mask slips completely, the repeated bridge insisting that buried anguish is forcing its way to the surface whatever the cost. The closing warning against quitting too fast lands as grim triage, a plea to heed the deeper summons before the numbness finishes the job.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind"],"means":"devotion performed past the point where meaning bled out","built":"a film sample of hollow ritual devotion brackets the track's own exhaustion"},
"dream-theater~a-change-of-seasons":{"haiku":"A narrative of lost innocence and the encroaching darkness of adult life, set against advice to seize the day. Culminates in the death of the speaker's mother, crushing the wisdom imparted.","opus":"A sweeping reflection on the passage of life from innocent youth to disillusioned adulthood, framed by the death of the narrator's mother and the urgent lesson to seize the day before time runs out.","sonnet":"A meditation on losing childhood wonder and innocence to grief and disillusionment, anchored by a teacher's 'carpe diem' lesson that gains devastating weight when the speaker's mother is killed — the song traces the arc from youthful wonder through trauma to a plea to seize what life remains.","src":"haiku","fable":"A twenty-three-minute suite that spends one human life on the arc of a single day: it opens on a boy watching the light climb over the horizon and closes on that same man, now a father, watching that light sink beside his own son, so the whole architecture is a loop from first dawn to last dusk. The instrumental movements are not filler but the passing weather itself, each sung section handing off to a wordless stretch of season, so that childhood wonder, sudden grief, disillusion, and reckoning arrive as climate rather than event. Prog metal usually aims its virtuosity at spectacle, but Dream Theater point it at a plainer wound here, keyed to a mother's death that guts the young narrator and to a mentor's borrowed Latin lesson about gathering the rosebuds while you still can, a creed the piece treats less as uplift than as something he has to grow around. The turn lands when the middle section's dream of escape into a paradise off the television screen collapses, and the boy who wept becomes a man who cuts the strings holding him and refuses the crowd that would feed on his misery. By the final movement the mentor's imperative has changed hands, the dying father now pressing it onto his child, and the circular form gives up its thesis, that the only permanence on offer is the lesson you pass down before your own light finishes going out.","fv":2,"fnote":"The seize-the-day sermon and the killed-mother sample are Portnoy’s own life: his mother died in a plane crash in 1984 while he was at school — on a day his teacher had, by awful coincidence, been teaching carpe diem.","themes":["death & grief","family & blood","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"the lesson you pass down before your own light finishes going out","built":"the suite opens on a boy at dawn and closes on that man at dusk beside his son"},
"dream-theater~a-change-of-seasons-the-crimson-sunrise-innocence-carpe-diem-the-darkest-of-winters-another-world-the-inevitable-summer-the-crimson-sunset":{"opus":"Petrucci's sprawling elegy for his mother, killed suddenly, structured as life's seasons from wondering innocence to grief. Beneath the prog spectacle it's a plea to seize the day precisely because the future may never arrive.","src":"opus"},
"dream-theater~a-fortune-in-lies": {"haiku":"A song about deception and false promises, where wealth and power are built on lies and manipulation that keep people trapped in illusion.","opus":"A meditation on betrayal and self-delusion, where a fleeting sense that everything was right collapses on waking, and the singer realizes how easily a charismatic figure builds a fortune on deceit, riding the illusion until everyone smiles along. Temptation and vain memory tangle throughout. Dream Theater are the American progressive-metal pioneers whose early catalogue paired virtuoso instrumental technique with these labyrinthine, questioning lyrics about faith and illusion.","sonnet":"Disillusionment with a collaborator or mentor who turned shared creative ambition into a scheme for personal gain — someone who promised heroism and delivered temptation. The speaker moves from waking revelation to bitter recognition: that unity and promise were bankrolled on deception, and the fortune built on lies outlasted the innocent belief that powered it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A waking dream opens the song and a completed betrayal closes it: the narrator recalls a years-long partnership he thought was sound, then interrogates his younger self for how easily a collaborator's deception fooled him. The chorus states the swindler's method as flat mechanics — falsehood scaling into wealth, a hollow oversized reputation propped upright, the momentum ridden until every onlooker is grinning — and the bait that worked was the offer of shared glory, both men promised heroism, which is exactly what drew the narrator into temptation. By the outro the same lines return with their verbs pushed into past tense, so the sales pitch becomes an accomplished con, the money already made and the idol already sustained at the narrator's cost. What lingers is that the fraud succeeded because it wore the clothes of friendship, and the bridge exposes its centerpiece as a treasure that dissolves when you reach for it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","friendship & loyalty","money & the street"],"means":"the bait of shared glory making betrayal irresistible","built":"outro verbs shift to past tense turning pitch into accomplished con"},
"dream-theater~a-life-left-behind":{"haiku":"A man awakens from complacency to pursue a stranger who has become his fixation, willing to infiltrate and manipulate her life.","opus":"Part of the band's Astonishing narrative, a character waking from a deadened existence into new purpose and love, shadowed by a father's controlling, ominous intentions. Awakening and surveillance braided into one prog set-piece.","sonnet":"A man wakes from a dormant existence as someone new enters and then leaves his life, reopening possibilities he had let go — but his awakening conceals a surveillance mission, and his promise of return is wrapped in disguise and the language of justice rather than love.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~a-nightmare-to-remember": {"haiku":"A traumatic accident destroys a wedding celebration, leaving the survivor sedated and broken. The song replays trauma and depicts the long recovery in agony.","opus":"A vivid retelling of a real, traumatic car crash (the drummer's childhood accident), moving from a joyful family day to sudden violent impact, screaming, fire and smoke. The middle section drifts through morphine-hazed hospital sedation, and the coda's relief that his father survived turns catastrophe into a grateful, indelible memory no son should have to carry.","sonnet":"A first-person account of a severe car crash and its aftermath — the chaos and terror of the impact, the disorientation of waking in a hospital, and the way the trauma replays endlessly as a waking nightmare years later.","src":"haiku","fable":"The album's opening epic reconstructs a car crash as a survivor keeps circling back to it, and its power comes from a deliberate mismatch of register and content: the collision itself is rendered as a flat inventory of sensations — glass, fire, sirens, then an abrupt silence — while the trauma's real language arrives afterward, in the drugged euphoria of the hospital, where morphine turns wreckage into something drifting and almost beautiful. That chorus is the song's cruelest irony, dressing agony in the vocabulary of peace, so the sedation reads less as relief than as another layer of the nightmare the title insists on keeping. Portnoy's spoken-word bridge, harsher than LaBrie's melodic sections, is where the mask drops entirely and the event stops being the singer's and becomes a son watching his father broken — the recurring red-light replay that no gratitude for survival can switch off. The return to the wedding-day innocence of the opening completes the loop rather than resolving it: the point is that the memory won't stay in the past, and the song's cyclic prog structure enacts exactly that compulsion to keep reliving it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The crash is real — a near-fatal accident from Petrucci’s childhood, the family driving home from a wedding.","themes":["madness & the mind","death & grief","dissociation & numbness","family & blood"],"means":"morphine dressing wreckage in peace as the nightmare's cruelest layer","built":"cyclic prog structure enacts the compulsion to keep reliving the crash"},
"dream-theater~a-rite-of-passage": {"haiku":"Secret societies of powerful men initiate members through esoteric rituals and symbolic ascent, bound by hidden knowledge and elite hierarchy.","opus":"A dramatized look at secretive fraternal orders like Freemasonry and Rosicrucianism, their rituals, symbols, and initiations toward supposed enlightenment. The all-seeing eye and a membership of the rich and powerful give it a conspiratorial slant, treating the ceremony as a shadowy meeting point of hidden knowledge and elite influence.","sonnet":"An exploration of Freemasonry as a subject — the song examines the brotherhood's historical mystique, its membership of powerful men, its rituals and secrets, framed as both fascinating and slightly ominous.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is Dream Theater ventriloquizing the initiate's-eye view of Freemasonry, weighing the fraternity's dark public reputation — a secret guild of the wealthy and powerful, feared as a hidden architecture behind world events — against the plainer thing it claims to be: philosophers and leaders bound by honor and craft. The verses build the myth by cataloguing its trades and cryptic emblems, while the chorus collapses into pure ceremony — a threshold crossed, a foundation stone set, a locked door swung open — refusing to explain the ritual it stages and instead just enacting the passage again and again. By the closing repetition the imagery tips fully into dawn-and-rebirth symbolism, so the track resolves less as any argument about conspiracy than as the sound of a man stepping over the sill and choosing to call it a beginning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"threshold imagery chosen over argument about the brotherhood's reputation","built":"the chorus enacts the rite rather than explaining it, refusing to decode the ritual"},
"dream-theater~about-to-crash": {"haiku":"A high-achieving woman spirals into manic extremes and depression, eventually finding balance after weathering a mental health crisis.","opus":"A compassionate portrait of a young woman's mental breakdown, a small-town overachiever raised to be perfect who wakes one day having lost her mind, swinging between manic highs and crushing depression while a helpless partner stands by knowing she can't really fly. Dream Theater, the American progressive-metal band, built this into the Six Degrees of Inner Turbulence suite, James LaBrie voicing the bipolar arc across the record's ambitious song-cycle about mental illness.","sonnet":"A portrait of bipolar mania seen from two angles — a woman flying at full speed, taking on the world, her husband watching helplessly from the sidelines knowing the crash is coming. The story then flips to her own collapse into depression and back out, the frantic pace returning as the cycle completes with no cure in sight.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of bipolar illness told from two vantages at once: the woman whose thoughts outrun her, sleepless and convinced she can take to the air, and the man who loves her and knows the elation is only the height she will fall from. The suite swings like a pendulum between poles — the overdriven, unsleeping climb where the polished small-town daughter feels invincible, then the crash into sleeping through daylight and weeping where no one can see, the achiever who woke one morning a stranger to herself. Its cruelest turn is the last one, when the upswing returns and she reads it as healing, quickening back into the same reckless momentum, so the apparent recovery is really the next crest of the same disease.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The suite's six movements portray six strangers — Petrucci and Portnoy split the characters three each, and this woman is Petrucci's; she returns once more near the suite's end, her recovery still indistinguishable from her mania.","themes":["madness & the mind","love & desire"],"means":"recovery misread as the disease's next ascent","built":"dual-vantage structure mirrors the illness's own double-pole"},
"dream-theater~afterlife": {"haiku":"Someone questions what comes after death, wondering whether suffering will transform into peace and whether understanding replaces confusion in an afterlife.","opus":"An early Dream Theater track from before their breakthrough, turning over questions about what follows death, whether shadow becomes light and the sightless finally see. It pictures existence as small creatures threading a puzzle of illusion, doubt, and delusion. The narrator searches inside a body that carries both joy and hurt, resisting the frenzy while wondering what unspoken answer waits on the far side.","sonnet":"A narrator surveys a life spent in confusion and misdirection, having been led away from his own soul by outside influence, and now wonders whether death will reverse all earthly deprivations — whether the blind will see, the cold be warmed. The question is sincere, not rhetorical; genuine comfort lies only in not knowing.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song runs on a single image — souls as mice threading a maze — and the speaker's question is whether the exit lands anywhere real. What makes it more than adolescent metaphysics is the pivot in the verses: the losses and self-deceptions behind him are reframed not as damage but as the thing that made him whole, so the search for purpose becomes a case for staying inside the confusion rather than escaping it. The chorus then does its work through pure grammatical inversion, asking if death simply flips every polarity of the living world — dark to bright, blind to sighted — a wish-list phrased as questions precisely because the singer has no answer and refuses to fake one. On a debut fronted by Charlie Dominici, before the band's later theatricality, that restraint reads as sincerity: the maze is offered without a promised way out, and the refusal to resolve is the point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming"],"means":"the maze offered without a promised exit and refused resolution","built":"chorus frames afterlife as grammatical inversion of living-world polarities"},
"dream-theater~along-for-the-ride": {"haiku":"Life's chaos cannot be controlled, only navigated with faith and acceptance that events unfold through destiny and human limitations.","opus":"A meditation on surrender and faith when words and clarity fail, admitting you can't halt the forces of nature but refusing to believe you face them alone, choosing acceptance over paralyzing fear. American progressive-metal band Dream Theater offer one of their more direct, uplifting songs, James LaBrie's soaring vocal carrying its hard-won hope.","sonnet":"A surrender to forces larger than the self, framed not as defeat but as faith — you can't stop the tides or the turning of the world, but you're not alone in being carried by them. Out of chaos and doubt comes something like peace: rising from ashes, not shattered, trusting that the ride has a direction even when you can't see it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A serenity prayer set to a slow build: the singer concedes the vast, indifferent machinery he can never bend — the earth's turning, the moon dragging the tide — and answers it not with control but with company, deciding that being a passenger in a system too big to steer is not the same as being abandoned by it. The verses walk him from blindness and groping in the dark toward surrender-as-strength and a horizon finally coming clear, so the arc is a conversion from fear to trust rather than an argument. On the album the band named after itself, the outro's swelling repetition of the title works less as a hook than as a mantra hardening into creed, the affirmation of not being alone insisted upon until it becomes true by sheer resolve.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","freedom & escape","faith & the occult"],"means":"not being alone insisted upon until it becomes true by sheer resolve","built":"the outro mantra on the band's self-titled album hardens affirmation into creed"},
"dream-theater~anna-lee": {"haiku":"A figure holds onto a wounded and isolated person while inviting them to breathe and belong; past trauma scars the person, but the presence offers both holding and questioning of belonging.","opus":"A wounded child, innocence stripped, bears unseen scars and lines traced across the skin while someone keeps holding on and coaxing her to breathe. Dream Theater, the progressive-metal institution, set aside instrumental pyrotechnics for a tender ballad here, tracing abuse and the slow answering of a call as Anna Lee is drawn back from isolation toward being heard.","sonnet":"A girl carries invisible wounds — scars unseen, secrets locked behind her eyes, a life shaped by violation. She clings to someone while struggling to survive alone; those around her plead for her to breathe, to find belonging, without knowing how to give it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of someone marked by abuse, sung from the outside in: the verses address both the wounded girl and the shameless figure who took her innocence, while the chorus narrows to her animal need to simply draw breath and know where she belongs. What makes the song move is its shift in who acts, since for two verses she clings and suffers in silence while the addressed you stays frozen, but by the third she finally cries out a name, refuses to bury her pain any longer, and the onlooker who has only watched turns at last toward her voice as the rain moves in. One of James LaBrie’s rare turns as lyricist, it carries the plainer, ballad-leaning song-craft the label pressed on Dream Theater for Falling Into Infinity, trading their usual instrumental sprawl for a directness that lets the subject matter land unguarded.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"a wounded girl's cry finally moves the onlooker frozen through two verses","built":"ballad plainness lets the subject land unguarded where instrumental sprawl would shield it"},
"dream-theater~another-day": {"haiku":"Someone advises against searching for escape or meaning in despair, urging patience while mysteries remain unsolved rather than surrendering to false certainty.","opus":"One of Dream Theater's rare tender ballads, reportedly written by John Petrucci for his gravely ill father. The speaker gently urges a loved one to keep going and reach for peace that can't be found in the here and now, counseling patience over grasping, letting some things remain uncertain rather than forcing them.","sonnet":"A man delivers a quietly devastating message to someone searching for an escape or a silent sky, telling them plainly that no relief exists here. The emotional register is cool and compassionate at once — he refuses false comfort while acknowledging there may be something worth saving if it isn't forced.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song of counsel offered to someone worn down enough to be weighing whether to keep going, delivered not as comfort but as a flat refusal to hand over the peace they came looking for. The chorus keeps closing the same door, insisting the answer isn't here and telling them to turn elsewhere, its verb sliding from dying to trying so the same words shift from resignation toward a stubborn, grudging permission to endure. The bridge reframes the whole thing: dreams get photographed and hidden away, and the real instruction is to leave them un-forced and un-discarded, because something kept intact is worth more than a secret dragged out into the open. Ballad-plain by Dream Theater standards, it hands its emotional weight to a saxophone rather than the usual solo pyrotechnics, letting that instrument carry the tenderness the lyric itself keeps holding at arm's length.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Petrucci wrote it for his father, then fighting cancer — the chorus refusals are aimed at a man weighing surrender — and the saxophone is Jay Beckenstein of Spyro Gyra.","themes":["suicide","friendship & loyalty","freedom & escape"],"means":"counsel delivered as refusal rather than comfort, closing the door they knocked on","built":"saxophone carries the tenderness the lyric itself keeps holding at arm's length"},
"dream-theater~as-i-am": {"haiku":"A speaker resists external control and refuses to compromise their authentic self for others' approval. The song asserts personal agency despite being cast as the underdog and misunderstood.","opus":"A defiant claim to artistic and personal autonomy, refusing anyone's instructions on how to create, compete, or think, and refusing to be dumbed down to fit in. Cast as an outsider fighting the tide, the narrator offers solidarity to those who get it and demands that his doubters accept him unchanged.","sonnet":"A defiant refusal to be defined by critics, industry pressures, or anyone who wants to change the narrator's music or identity — a declaration of artistic self-determination addressed to those who would reshape him into something more commercially palatable.","src":"haiku","fable":"A defiant refusal to be edited into someone else's shape, sung from the outsider's vantage of watching a room he won't join through a window. The song hardens as it moves: the early verses still try to justify the antagonist before abandoning the effort, so that the chorus lands not as bravado but as flat self-permission, a demand to be taken whole and uncommanded. Its one crack of doubt, the fear of drowning in mediocrity, is what the extended hand answers.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The quoted advice was real — offered to Petrucci by Queensrÿche’s guitarist Mike Stone on their irksome 2003 co-tour; the song is the reply.","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"self-permission hardening from justification into flat refusal","built":"the outsider observes through a window he has chosen not to enter"},
"dream-theater~astonishing":{"haiku":"A father's blessing before death inspires his son to become a voice for peace and justice, leading a revolution toward harmony and freedom.","opus":"The grand reconciliation that closes a rock opera: a dying plea puts a child's life in a brother's hands, a father's blessing lands, the wayward prince is forgiven, and a world that outlawed real music gets it back, the crowd promised they will never be held down again. It is the title finale of Dream Theater's 2016 concept double album The Astonishing, resolving its machine-music dystopia in full Broadway-scale earnestness.","sonnet":"A son's vow to his dying father becomes a revolution — he finally understands what he was meant to be, rises as a voice for change, and helps build a world of common ground where music and freedom replace silence and oppression. The personal grief becomes the engine of collective liberation.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~at-wit-s-end": {"haiku":"A person struggles with depression and self-destruction while their loved one desperately tries to break through their apathy and despair.","opus":"A desperate address to someone deteriorating, the narrator pleading not to be left as he watches a loved one shut him out, lost to fear, apathy and sleepless intoxication. His own admission of lying and losing something still buried in the other deepens the tangle. Dream Theater, the American progressive metal institution, thread this fraught study of watching someone slip away through shifting, deafening dynamics and a chorus that begs them to come alive.","sonnet":"One person watching another refuse help from the outside — the speaker desperate to reach someone who has shut down completely, numbed and sedated and retreating further in. The song shifts partway through to the other perspective: a confession of lying, of not admitting what's been lost, two people failing to reach each other across the same crisis.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dialogue of failed rescue whose refrain never changes wording yet inverts its addressee: one voice pleads not to be abandoned, the other narrates a numbed interior of sedation, insomnia, and a mind circling the ceiling it cannot stop staring at. The image-system splits between the caretaker's vocabulary of love and tearing-apart and the sufferer's of burning, hollowness, and no exit, so the meaning lives in the widening gap where devotion keeps reaching and the person it reaches for keeps refusing rescue. Dream Theater cut Distance Over Time while cohabiting in a single house through the sessions, and here that enforced closeness reads as its dark mirror, proximity curdling into pressure, care arriving as one more thing there is no way out of.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"LaBrie’s lyric, and he has said whose voices these are — a husband and a wife surviving the aftermath of an assault, the reassurance sung from his side of the bed.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire"],"means":"devotion keeps reaching where the sufferer keeps refusing rescue","built":"unchanging refrain inverts its addressee to map the widening gap"},
"dream-theater~awaken-the-master":{"haiku":"A spiritual journey of self-discovery through cycles of darkness and light, ultimately reaching enlightenment and internal balance.","opus":"A spiritual climb up an icy trail guided by a balancing angel, reaching the peak only to realize the ascent was barely begun and its real purpose was missed, until turning inward wakes the power already within. The American prog-metal band notably debut John Petrucci's eight-string guitar here, pairing the mystical text with dense, weighty musicianship.","sonnet":"A spiritual mountain-climbing allegory — balance and perseverance carry you upward, but reaching the peak only reveals you're halfway there. The real journey is inward, spiraling through circles of dark and light, until the soul's will is mastered and the angels' voices carry you to wherever the stone finally turns.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~barstool-warrior": {"haiku":"A broken man drowns in regret and failed relationships at a bar, while another victim suffers from his cruelty, until both finally break free.","opus":"Two joined sketches of stuck lives, a beaten man confiding in his drink at a waterside pub and a battered woman flagging trouble from the roadside, each a captive wondering where they fit. The shift arrives when the narrator finally severs his mooring and begins again. Dream Theater, the American prog-metal band, use long-form narrative to travel from resignation to release, ending on a vow to quit looking backward.","sonnet":"Two portraits of people trapped by their pasts — a man drinking himself into oblivion at a dockside inn and a woman on a roadside trying to signal for help — brought together by the question of whether escape is possible. The turn comes when the speaker stops asking where they belong and starts cutting the anchor, choosing a new life today rather than waiting for permission.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two ruined lives sit as parallel case files: a man drinking away decisions he can't unmake, and a woman signaling danger from a roadside no one is reading in time. Both reach the same helpless question of where they belong, and the chorus deliberately lets their voices bleed into one another, so that the addict and the endangered stranger become a single study of people waiting for a rescue that isn't coming. The bridge withdraws the sympathy and states the verdict flatly — you become what you keep feeding, and no outside hand arrives — which makes the turn that follows feel earned rather than sentimental. When the narrator finally cuts the anchor and walks into a new life without looking back, the song isn't promising he'll succeed; it's insisting that the only rescue available was always his own decision to leave the barstool, an unusually plain and hard-won piece of self-help for a band that more often reaches for the mythic.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"rescue deferred until the addict chooses it himself","built":"chorus bleeds two voices into one study of waiting"},
"dream-theater~behind-the-veil": {"haiku":"A plea for help from someone trapped in darkness and despair, unable to escape their torment despite attempts at self-rescue.","opus":"Dream Theater, the American prog-metal band, build a desperate cry for rescue through their signature dynamic swells. A person at his lowest begs to be truly seen and pulled to safety before he cracks entirely, sensing a dangerous, hidden presence within himself and fearing that some ruined souls are simply beyond saving. The chorus is pure raw need.","sonnet":"A cry for rescue from someone hiding their pain in plain sight — bent but not broken, calling out from behind a surface that reads as fine to everyone who passes. The song positions the damage as concealed rather than invisible, and the plea is urgent: please look behind what I show you, because I cannot face another day of this alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song runs on a split of address that turns out to be its whole argument: an accusatory second person interrogating a monstrous figure who feeds on chaos, set against a first person crying to be pulled home before another day can break. The pivot lands when the same steadying line — courage found at the worst hour, standing damaged but not destroyed — is handed first to the pleading self and then, transposed into you, back to the very predator it condemned, until accuser, drowning caller, and depraved stranger read as one person seen from different angles. James LaBrie sells the desperation without ornament, and that plainness is the point: a mind losing its grip gets no elaborate lyric, only the same rescue plea flung again and again, so the repetition itself becomes the thing pulling him under.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The captivity is drawn from the headlines — the song references the Elizabeth Smart kidnapping case.","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"accuser, victim, and predator collapse into the same person losing grip","built":"the repeated rescue plea enacts the pulling-under rather than describing it"},
"dream-theater~beneath-the-surface": {"haiku":"Unspoken longing between two people who never communicate their feelings, leading both to bury emotions until they become painful wounds.","opus":"A hushed acoustic ballad from the American prog-metal band about two people who kept missing each other, each waiting for a right moment that never arrived. Both hid their feelings until numbness turned to lasting hurt, and the ache of everything left unsaid remains, singer James LaBrie voicing quiet, buried regret.","sonnet":"Two people who never managed to reach each other — one waiting in the shadows for the moment that never came, the other holding back out of care for the other's fragility. Both disappeared into darkness separately, and what's left is buried deep: the husk of what might have been, if either had spoken first.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two people narrate the same failed closeness from opposite sides, and the read of the song is that they reach for nearly identical words — a lover who stops caring, a self dissolving into a dark that hardens into permanent hurt. James LaBrie voices both the one who kept hunting for clues and the one too frightened to speak, so the tragedy is symmetry: each held back waiting for the other to move first, and neither ever did. What sinks below the title is not one secret but the whole relationship, sealed off in silence before either could risk saying it aloud.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"symmetrical silence sealing the relationship before either risked speaking","built":"one voice singing both sides turns tragedy into a mirror"},
"dream-theater~blind-faith": {"haiku":"People hold blind faith in an invisible messiah to solve systemic problems and hatred, yet continue suffering and carrying on without salvation or divine intervention.","opus":"A prog interrogation of misplaced trust in leaders and messiahs who never deliver, turning finally toward self-reliance. The real subject is the human tendency to wait for salvation from above rather than carry ourselves forward.","sonnet":"A song about the collapse of faith in both religion and political systems — the narrator looks at a world of suffering and inequality and asks where the messiah or the accountable leader is, ultimately concluding that humanity must carry on by its own will, with or without divine or institutional guidance.","src":"haiku","fable":"A congregation harangues a silent god, cataloguing the comforts it built for itself — the bigger house, the metal barrier raised to wall the neighbors out — then blames that absent deity for the hollowness underneath. The demand hardens as the band swells: throw down a rescue line, prove you were ever listening, pick our direction for us. Then the voice inverts the whole transaction, announcing that faith runs the other way, that the worshipper is the one handing meaning down to the one worshipped.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the worshipper discovers faith runs the other direction","built":"the inversion withheld until the final verse reverses every prior demand"},
"dream-theater~bridges-in-the-sky": {"haiku":"A spiritual journey of healing and transformation where the speaker crosses death and darkness to achieve rebirth and wholeness.","opus":"The American prog-metal band frames spiritual death and rebirth as an epic, almost shamanic quest. The narrator descends into his own inner darkness, trusts a guiding spirit to make him whole, and calls on the elements to restore him, until the part of himself that had died is finally renewed and reunited.","sonnet":"A shamanic journey toward healing — the speaker guided across some threshold between death and life, the fabric of reality torn open, buried memories flooding back. Sun and wind are summoned as agents of restoration; the piece of the self that died promises to return, and crossing the bridge in the sky is the crossing back into wholeness.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a shamanic death-and-rebirth ritual staged as a suite: a soul crosses into darkness on loaned sight, submits to a spirit-guide, and asks to be undone before it can be remade. James LaBrie sings the whole arc through elemental invocations, petitioning sun, water, and wind as a healer would call the directions, so the band's virtuoso sprawl becomes ceremony rather than showmanship. The second refrain lifts the register from private healing to cosmic homecoming, reaching toward the planet itself and a guide who leads the crossing between worlds. Its structural argument is that transformation cannot skip the descent: the speaker refuses any easy passage into brightness until he has walked the buried, frightening chambers of the self. What binds the movements is that recurring threshold image of the title, a span thrown across empty air that recasts dying as a journey outward rather than an ending.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"transformation requiring the descent before any brightness","built":"recurring threshold image reframes dying as outward journey"},
"dream-theater~build-me-up-break-me-down": {"haiku":"Someone cycles between being idolized and demonized by another person, crashing and burning as the object of their morbid obsession, unable to break free.","opus":"A dependent, addictive bond in which the narrator is by turns exalted as a rescuer and redeemer, then wrecked and nailed up. He is his partner's shameful craving and sick fixation, endlessly raised high and pulled to pieces, paying dearly to keep the ruinous dream running.","sonnet":"An addictive, co-dependent relationship where the narrator is simultaneously saviour and demon to someone who worships then crucifies them — the cycle is irresistible even as it destroys both parties.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song sung from inside the pedestal, in the voice of the idol a crowd raises up only to smash. The savior-and-demon, messiah-and-crucified language maps the whole thing onto the cycle of public worship: adoration is the same appetite as destruction, and the addict here is the audience, with the celebrity as the fix that both parties keep feeding. What sharpens it past a simple fame-is-cruel complaint is the singer's own complicity — he admits he can neither live up to the myth nor bear to let the fantasy die, so he colludes in his own building-up and breaking-down because being the drug is the only self he has left. Fittingly for Dream Theater's first album after Mike Portnoy's departure, a moment charged with questions about whether the band could survive losing the figure fans had idolized, the track lands as their leanest, most radio-shaped song on the record, the instrumental breakdown standing in for the collapse the words keep circling back to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"the idol colludes in his own construction and collapse","built":"the leanest track on the record arrives as the band's own pedestal moment"},
"dream-theater~burning-my-soul": {"haiku":"Accumulated pressure from external expectations and other people's control burns away one's authentic self; the song condemns living under conditions set by others' words and judgments.","opus":"A catalogue of accumulated stress and resentment (knots, thorns, gray hair) at being controlled and spoken over by others. The deeper thrust is a refusal to walk on eggshells: the narrator asserts that his own words give the controller power, and finally stops apologizing for having a voice.","sonnet":"The accumulating pressure of being undermined, second-guessed, and controlled in a professional or personal relationship, until the narrator breaks free from the need to placate others and asserts that his own voice is what gives their world meaning.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses run almost entirely on idiom — a rapid-fire string of dead metaphors for stress, the chip and the knot and the ape stacked so thick they stop describing pain and start enacting it, the cliches themselves the boulder in the throat. That surface irritability is a mask: underneath, the song is about who owns your thoughts, sliding from petty grievance in verse one to a portrait of a workplace or ideology that hands you a replacement mind and tells you the last one sounded the same. The bridge drops the idiom and turns the tables coldly — the speaker realizes his words are what give the controller his power, and the unapologetic kiss-off (a rare bit of profanity for the band) is the song snapping into the clear: refusal not as tantrum but as reclaiming authorship of himself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"reclaiming authorship of self by withdrawing the words that feed control","built":"stacked dead metaphors enact the suffocation they describe before the mask drops"},
"dream-theater~caught-in-a-web":{"haiku":"A narrative of emotional entanglement and deception within a relationship. The speaker is trapped, spinning lies to hide desire while fearing that authenticity isolates them.","opus":"A portrait of someone trapped and isolated by their own lies and inner deceptions, spinning webs in their head, torn between watching another's hidden pain and confronting wounds of the soul that can't be healed.","sonnet":"A person isolated from the world and trapped in self-deception, spinning lies in their own head, unable to share the dance of life with anyone or have their inner wounds healed by others.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track runs on a slippage of pronouns: the speaker starts as a watcher craving somebody else's hidden pain, yet by the closing verse that ache is his own. The trap he first pinned on others turns out, by his own confession, to be something he wove inside himself. Awake is the band's bleakest album, LaBrie voicing addicts and collapse over fantasy, and the reading that earns it casts one man building the snare he then accuses everyone around him of setting. His late refusal to be mended — that flat, repeated claim that nothing reaches whatever festers in him — reports no fact but defends a choice, loneliness dressed up as fate. When the last chorus quietly rewrites its closing line, the cage now rejected and mistaken rather than merely spun, that is the giveaway: he snared himself and needs the story to say it was done to him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"the trap he pinned on others quietly confessed as one he built himself","built":"the last chorus rewrites its closing line, the cage now rejected and mistaken rather than merely spun"},
"dream-theater~constant-motion": {"haiku":"Obsessive compulsion drives the narrator in endless motion toward self-destruction, unable to stop or escape the spiral.","opus":"A portrait of obsessive, racing thought spiraling toward burnout, the mind as an engine that cannot be shut off. The relentless prog attack mirrors the compulsion, someone who longs for serenity but is dragged forever forward.","sonnet":"An obsessive, racing mind that cannot slow down or let go — tunnel-vision impulses, compulsive yearning, and escalating inner chaos keep the narrator in perpetual motion, unable to find serenity or an exit.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study of compulsion rendered as velocity, where the narrator is strapped to something that will not slow and cannot be switched off. Dream Theater score it to match, riding a manic double-kick gallop under a chorus of spinning wheels so the arrangement enacts the compulsion rather than merely naming it. The two verses trace a worsening curve: the first frames the drive as a dark craving that outruns any wish to let go, the second collapses into clipped commands and imminent collision, sensory overload with no exit and no plan to come back. Beneath the road-and-machine imagery the real subject is a mind that mistakes momentum for freedom, chasing a calm it destroys by the act of pursuing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Portnoy has called the lyric a metaphor for his own OCD — the ever-spinning wheels are his.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","madness & the mind"],"means":"mistaking relentless momentum for freedom","built":"the arrangement's manic gallop enacts the compulsion it names"},
"dream-theater~disappear": {"haiku":"Grief and loss leave the world unrecognizable; the speaker must carry on alone after a loved one's death. Time moves forward while the speaker remains anchored to what was.","opus":"A grief song set at the bedside of a dying loved one, the narrator holding her and reassuring her it's alright to let go, then struggling forward as time slips past and the world moves on. The loss is agonizing, but her memory steadies him as he carries on as well as he's able.","sonnet":"A farewell to someone dying — watching them accept the end, holding their hand as they let go — and the painful process of choosing to carry on and move forward while knowing they are still felt everywhere, the grief managed by honoring rather than abandoning the memory.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grief song built as a slow approach to the moment it can barely name: the death of someone loved, and the survivor left to carry on. It opens inside disorientation — the unanswerable why, the sense that everything solid has tilted overnight, the futility of anyone's comfort — and the meaning is in how that fog slowly clears. Verse by verse the speaker talks himself toward acceptance, insisting she would have wished him to keep living, that he still has a place among the living, that he must now face it alone. Only in the final movement does the abstraction resolve into the actual deathbed: a hand offered, permission granted to let go, a vow to endure however he can now that she is gone. Fitting for one of Dream Theater's studies of inner suffering, the song's arc is the whole point — the passage from stunned questioning to the release itself, from asking why to saying goodbye.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"LaBrie's lyric, first titled Move On — he has said it began with watching a young couple in a mall and imagining one of them handed a terminal diagnosis.","themes":["death & grief"],"means":"fog slowly clearing toward permission to let go","built":"deathbed scene withheld until the final movement earns the goodbye"},
"dream-theater~endless-sacrifice": {"haiku":"Separation and distance force a couple to endure constant compromise and sacrifice in their relationship, with one partner bearing the emotional weight of maintaining connection despite physical absence.","opus":"The prog-metal veterans render long-distance love as endurance, a narrator lying awake and unravelling while both partners strive to stay connected across the miles that a demanding life imposes. Gratitude for the other's forsaken sacrifice anchors the ache. Dream Theater's dynamic shifts mirror the swing between despair and devotion, superficial compromise weighed against an undying, shared dream.","sonnet":"Long-distance love consuming both parties: the narrator in isolation clings to the voice of the person he chose a demanding life over. They sustain each other across impossible distance, and the song eventually acknowledges that this devotion — quietly endured on both sides — is a form of sacrifice neither can measure.","src":"haiku","fable":"A separation ballad built from two matched halves: first a sleepless narrator counting footsteps in the dark, then the same ache handed to the distant partner, so the pronouns swap and the loneliness becomes shared property rather than one person's complaint. The song names the life its speaker chose — the touring musician's grind that Dream Theater made this record inside of, cutting it fast between road commitments — as a bargain whose shine curdles into daily concession, a cost totaled again and again until it never stops being paid. The final turn reframes everything the earlier verses read as abandonment: the absence was a gift given so the dream could be lived, and the debt gets answered not with resentment but with a vow to remember, gratitude arriving only after the grievance has been fully felt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"grievance fully felt before gratitude is allowed to arrive","built":"pronouns swap at the midpoint so the loneliness becomes shared property"},
"dream-theater~fall-into-the-light": {"haiku":"Memories dissolve and darkness fades as the speaker seeks something sacred; love and hope struggle to fill the void.","opus":"Recollections evaporate as the eyes open and the dark recedes, and the narrator, formerly sightless, understands that neither love nor hope will ever suffice. Facing the daylight becomes a turn inward toward the spirit. Dream Theater, American progressive-metal architects, give this awakening a widescreen sweep, spiritual clarity arriving only after long, self-imposed blindness.","sonnet":"Someone emerges from blindness — emotional or spiritual — into clarity, recognizing that neither love nor hope in their excess can substitute for honest sight. Memories dissolve like petals. What remains after the fog lifts is a simple, hard-won acknowledgment: something was obscuring vision, and now it is gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A conversion song dressed as a love song: the title inverts the fallen-angel arc, so that plunging becomes an ascent rather than a ruin, and the recurring paradox — that even a surplus of love or hope stays somehow insufficient — reads less as complaint than as the vertigo of finally seeing what one was too blind to grasp. The verses stage that opening as a slow waking, memories written on water and dissolving as the eyes clear, until the closing move outward and upward toward the sun locates the epiphany as a turning of the self toward its own soul. What the song withholds is any named object of the revelation, and that vagueness is the point: this is the shape of a certainty arriving, the truth becoming clear, without the doctrine being spelled — a spiritual awakening kept deliberately open rather than confessed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"certainty arriving without its doctrine being named","built":"inverted fallen-angel arc turns plunging into ascent"},
"dream-theater~far-from-heaven": {"haiku":"Someone suppresses their true self to meet expectations, sacrificing authenticity until they can't hide anymore and must reveal who they really are.","opus":"A quiet, aching admission of depression worn behind a brave face, the narrator bottling everything up, insisting he is fine while dying inside and refusing to lie any longer. It ends not in rescue but in a plea to simply be accepted as he is. Dream Theater, the American progressive metal institution, strip their virtuosic tendencies down here to bare, wounded vulnerability.","sonnet":"A person who has performed wellness for too long — keeping things bottled, putting on a brave face, doing what was asked — finally reaching the end. The confession is that all the compliance has left nothing to live for; they are too far from anything like grace to be saved, and the only thing left to ask is to be accepted as they are.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about the exhaustion of a performance held so long it becomes a second skin, the composure a depressive shows the world while corroding beneath it. James LaBrie sings it not as confession but as a plea negotiated from defeat, a man who met every demand placed on him only to find the compliance hollowed him out completely. Its final turn is the crushing one: the speaker abandons any bargain for rescue and asks instead to be taken unaltered, the smaller and sadder request of someone who has stopped believing deliverance was ever coming.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"compliance so total it hollows the self performing it","built":"the final plea shrinks from rescue to simple acceptance of defeat"},
"dream-theater~finally-free-with-lyrics":{"haiku":"A multi-life narrative where a man's jealous murder of his girlfriend and her lover traps them in a spiritual cycle, eventually achieving peace.","opus":"The murder-mystery climax of a hypnotic-regression concept album, where a killer stages a fake suicide to hide his crime and fires a fatal shot in the dark, only for two souls to depart and be reborn, one existence understood by inhabiting another. Dream Theater close their reincarnation saga Scenes from a Memory on this note.","sonnet":"A murder story that unfolds through forged suicide notes and past-life regression — a man kills his lover Victoria and frames her partner Julian, using a fake confession letter to cover it. But another perspective reveals the victim's last free afternoon, and when both die in the end, their spirits rise and the soul cycle closes with a sense of hard-won release.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~forsaken": {"haiku":"A supernatural entity comes at night to claim the speaker; the encounter involves a kiss, staining blood, and forced departure into darkness.","opus":"Dream Theater's gothic vampire seduction, a nocturnal visitor luring the narrator to surrender himself and be reborn as hers. The pull is the erotic dread of giving oneself up to something beautiful and deadly.","sonnet":"A vampire seduction song — the narrator is visited at night by a woman with crimson-stained lips who takes him away from the world, and rather than fighting it he surrenders completely. The romantic language dissolves into willing self-annihilation: give yourself up to me, fly away, you are now mine.","src":"haiku","fable":"A gothic seduction narrative in the mode of classic vampire folklore, where a nocturnal visitor summons a sleeping man toward surrender and dresses that surrender up as rescue. Dream Theater build the piece as pursuit rather than portrait: the first verse is dazed and half-dreaming, the second returns by daylight already sick with anticipation, dreading how badly the narrator wants the dark back. The imagery keeps braiding devotion into predation — a bloodied mouth offered as intimacy, a promise to be carried to the ends of the earth that doubles as abduction — so the tenderness in her voice is the lure and possession is the real transaction. What the song reads is thrall itself, the way a victim narrates his own capture as a love song and mistakes being consumed for being renewed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"a victim narrates his own capture as a love song","built":"tenderness framed as lure so possession reads as renewal"},
"dream-theater~funeral-for-a-friend-love-lies": {"haiku":"Romantic love crumbles irreparably; the speaker's musical passion could not sustain the relationship, leaving only bleeding wounds and the ghost of what might have been.","opus":"An Elton John cover of heartbreak as a house overtaken by decay, a man who picked music over the woman and lost her to it. The devotion bleeds out because ambition and romance could never share the same grip.","sonnet":"A cover of Elton John's song about a relationship ended by a musician's inability to be present — he was always on tour, she was just a fan who could not hold his divided attention, and the guitar he loved could not hold her either. Now she is gone, the house is growing and dying around the memory, and love lies bleeding in his hands — the cost of choosing the music over the person.","src":"haiku","fable":"Elton John and Bernie Taupin built this as a two-part suite, and Dream Theater keep the architecture: the wordless Funeral for a Friend section serves as the dirge before the eulogy, so grief is staged in full before it is ever put into words. The lyric it opens into is a musician's account of a love he traded for the stage, a man who chose the touring life over the woman who was only ever an admirer of it, now watching her move on while the wound stays raw in his open hands. What makes the selection pointed for a progressive band in the mid-1990s is exactly that vocation: a song about the instrument failing to hold a whole life together, voiced by players whose entire identity is relentless devotion to that same instrument. Its images pull toward wild things briefly harnessed and set loose again — leaping animals cleared of every burning ring, a small bird lifting off a wire toward some better weather — so the confession lands not as pure regret but as rueful admission that certain people were never his to keep. Recast onstage, it turns from Elton's polished pop farewell into a virtuoso's private reckoning with the price of the one thing they do better than anything else.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"All four covers come from one night — the Uncovered fan-club show at Ronnie Scott’s, 31 January 1995; the EP itself was released after a fan petition for the shelved title suite.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"the instrument chosen over love fails to hold a whole life together","built":"wordless dirge staged before the eulogy so grief arrives before words do"},
"dream-theater~hollow-years": {"haiku":"A man abandons his family for escape, while a woman gives everything then walks away. Both leave behind only hollow years and the weight of unfulfilled expectations.","opus":"Two sketches of people who depart, a man who deserts his household for the easy way out without a syllable of warning, and a woman who telegraphs every intention before turning and leaving. The hopeful thread is a plea to be brought to the water's edge and understood, the promise that once the crushing weight and the storm overhead pass, the sound that remains is a lifetime of emptiness collapsing to a close.","sonnet":"Two people — a man who abandons his family without warning, a woman who gives everything and gets left — and the aftermath, the crashing down of years that turned out to be empty, a shoreline burial for a life's worth of accumulated loss.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two portraits of departure mirror each other: a man who abandons his family without warning, and a woman who gives every sign and says everything before turning to go anyway. The song sets these leavings against a burial at the tideline — laid in wet sand, carried out into the surf as though drowning were a form of understanding it never quite grants. What it reaches for is release, the weight pinning someone flat finally hoisted off and the storm overhead breaking apart, yet the sound left behind is only empty time caving in all at once. That release never lands. Having pushed toward radio-scaled directness on this record, the band closes by looping the image of the figure crushed beneath a slab past sense into incantation, so the freedom the words kept promising curdles into a burden that simply repeats.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","freedom & escape","death & grief"],"means":"promised release that curdles into a burden repeating endlessly","built":"looped burial image tips from resolution into incantation"},
"dream-theater~honor-thy-father":{"fable":"The commandment turned inside out: a stepfather who pretended love while resenting the bloodline he could never see gets his reckoning — respect is not a one-way street, the door is closed, and the grave will be a lonely one.","fableDeep":"The title quotes scripture at a man who earned none of it, and the song spends its length revoking the commandment clause by clause. 'You're the rotted root in the family tree' turns his own obsession against him — he was threatened by an 'invisible blood line,' so the song puts him *in* the genealogy as its decay. 'Don't you dare cross the crooked step' is the best line in it: stepfather compressed into architecture, the broken stair everyone in the house learns to walk around. And 'I'll get my peace of mind when you hear this song' makes the track itself the therapy the 'four-bill therapy' failed to deliver — not a diary entry, a served notice.","haiku":"A bitter denunciation of an absent father who abandoned his child and used selfish manipulation instead of parental love.","opus":"A blistering settling of accounts with a stepfather who feigned love while resenting the absence of shared blood, exploited the speaker, and neglected them. The speaker throws back the demand for deference and brands him the diseased core the whole family grew from.","sonnet":"A direct confrontation with an absent, self-serving father figure — the narrator catalogues his failures as a parent, his refusal to take responsibility, and his tendency to shift blame onto others, culminating in a declaration that the relationship is permanently closed and that the father will die alone and unmourned.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~i-walk-beside-you": {"haiku":"Unconditional companionship through suffering; the speaker promises to stand with a loved one through darkness, loss, and despair, asking them to trust and not surrender to hopelessness.","opus":"Dream Theater offer a vow of steadfast support to someone in pain and losing their footing, promising to prop them up, carry them once their own strength fails, and take them in if they lose direction. Against despair, the song insists that better days will come and reassures the struggling listener not to surrender.","sonnet":"An unconditional promise to someone carrying hidden pain — I see the hurt behind your smile, I will carry you when you can't walk, no matter how far or how bad it gets — a steady, uncomplicated declaration of devotion.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pledge of unconditional companionship to someone coming apart, the song offers to decode the grief a friend keeps masked under a forced calm and to become their legs once their own strength quits. Its promise is escort rather than rescue: not to lift the burden away but to match every faltering step through the dark stretch until the current dragging them under finally reverses and carries them back to safe ground.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["friendship & loyalty"],"means":"escort offered rather than rescue from the dark stretch"},
"dream-theater~illumination-theory": {"haiku":"Humans seek understanding of what they will sacrifice and die for—freedom, love, faith, or duty—asking what shared humanity binds us when darkness and suffering obscure the answer.","opus":"A meditation on what gives life meaning, framed by three questions, what you'll live, die and kill for, that it answers with a catalogue of human devotions; the deeper claim is that joy and self-knowledge only come through suffering and surrendering to love and light.","sonnet":"A sweeping philosophical inquiry into what people are willing to live, die, and kill for — cycling through soldiers, martyrs, addicts, tyrants — and ultimately arguing that suffering is the price of truly feeling alive.","src":"haiku","fable":"Structured as a five-movement suite, this song opens by interrogating the listener directly, escalating from what a person would sustain to what they would perish for and finally what they would destroy for once the people they love are threatened. Its middle catalogues human devotion across every register at once, the mother and the tyrant and the addict alike, insisting the same drive fuels the noble cause and the ruinous one. The long instrumental center hollows out that certainty, and what emerges on the far side is quieter and harder-won: a claim that pain is the toll for joy and that trusting toward warmth is the only thing carrying you through the coldest stretches. The suite closes fixed on the idea that a person cannot know they are truly awake until they have fought their way through the dark.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","death & grief","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"darkness as the toll paid for genuine waking","built":"five-movement form enacts the descent before the arrival"},
"dream-theater~in-the-name-of-god": {"haiku":"Religious violence and cult destruction: prophets twist faith into justification for murder, ethnic elimination, and doomsday violence, while blind followers become unwitting instruments of death.","opus":"Closing their concept album, Dream Theater condemn holy war and cult obedience, sketching a fraudulent prophet who turns worship into slaughter. The track piles up doomsday-sect atrocities against a martial, hymn-like refrain, and its sharpest point is a question about whether unquestioning faith is what pushes followers to kill once they can no longer separate divine will from human ambition.","sonnet":"Religious fanaticism is anatomized as a mechanism for converting faith into violence — the self-proclaimed prophet as a vector for ethnic elimination, cult murder, and perversion, all sanctioned by holy language. The condemnation is unflinching and historical, cataloguing real atrocities to show that sacred texts become the devil's tools when wielded by ego.","src":"haiku","fable":"A charismatic recruiter recasts murder as devotion, and Dream Theater builds the track as an interrogation: verses ventriloquize the pitch — an anointed leader carrying scepter and firearm, obedience sold as rescue, ethnic slaughter rebranded as the road to heaven. The bridge finds the human wreckage in followers owned since infancy who can no longer separate the divine will they serve from their own hungers. What sharpens this past sloganeering is the breakdown, which drops parable for a documentary register: a doomsday cult's mass death, dozens killed by a would-be prophet, coerced child brides catalogued like case files. The closing refuses the catharsis a metal anthem usually grants, leaving an open question about whether devotion itself bends toward bloodshed while a whispered choir intones the Battle Hymn of the Republic — the American war song that already welded a righteous deity to a terrible sword.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The breakdown’s case is Waco — David Koresh’s Branch Davidians: the self-proclaimed messiah, the plural wives, the eighty dead. And from 5:51 Portnoy taps a hidden Morse-code joke into the drum part, discovered by the rest of the band only after mixing.","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"devotion ventriloquized until its audience can hear it becoming murder","built":"the closing choir intones the Battle Hymn against the song's own argument"},
"dream-theater~in-the-presence-of-enemies-pt-1":{"haiku":"A prophet offers false salvation to the broken and rejected, seducing them with promises of power while enslaving their devotion through manufactured hope.","opus":"A broken, damned man is tempted by a dark power promising redemption and revenge in exchange for worship—a Faustian bargain framed as salvation. The suite opens a battle between the fallen and one man rising to stand against them for humanity's sake.","sonnet":"A spiritually vulnerable man, broken and feeling forsaken by God, is approached by a dark entity offering power, revenge, and eternal life in exchange for worship — he wrestles between his old faith and the temptation of this shadowy savior who promises what God apparently withheld.","src":"haiku","fable":"Staged as a deathbed temptation, the movement puts a broken man at the edge of oblivion, the radiance ahead mistaken for salvation until the voice pledging rescue unmasks itself as the tempter. The drama runs on how slippery the offer is: the phrasing belongs to a redeemer, but the terms betray the deceiver, so the man's ache at being scorned and torn from his beloved is the exact bruise the liar keeps pressing. It then widens from one soul to open war, the fallen one's foot-soldiers clearing a road for their false messiah, before a lone figure plants himself against the tide and the piece ends not on seduction but on the first defiance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","war & battle","anger & defiance"],"means":"seduction phrased as rescue until the terms betray it","built":"the piece stages two registers — redeemer's diction, deceiver's terms — then ends on defiance rather than surrender"},
"dream-theater~in-the-presence-of-enemies-pt-2": {"haiku":"Confrontation with nemesis and ultimate reckoning; the enemy's presence forces final resolution and transformation.","opus":"A dark-fantasy narrative of a holy man seduced into serving a demonic master, the loyalty oaths curdling into surrender. It borrows scripture's shepherd imagery to stage a soul's corruption and the beast lurking in everyone.","sonnet":"A pilgrim who has surrendered to a dark master — pledging his soul, vowing to kill and die — gradually recognises the presence consuming him from within is not a saviour but a possessing force that has turned him into a servant of violence. The final reversal, \"my soul is my own now, I do not fight for you,\" arrives after the psalm-like cadences of surrender, making the reclamation feel hard-won rather than given.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a possession narrative told from inside the surrender, then reversed. A cult welcomes the protagonist as its long-awaited champion, and he takes the bait, pledging himself to an unnamed tyrant that lives as a spirit crouched behind another man's stare. Dream Theater then twists that oath into blasphemy by feeding a prison-house liturgy through it, so language meant for comfort and protection reads instead like a butcher's vow. The turn arrives when the borrowed devotion curdles: the hero feels conviction burn out, survives the fear he had dreaded most, and reclaims himself in one flat refusal that hands the whole ritual back empty. Its power is that the villain wins the entire march and loses only the last inch of a man who decides he owns his allegiance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The two-part suite adapts the Korean manhwa Priest — the weary preacher and his dark master come from it.","themes":["faith & the occult","identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"the villain wins the whole march and loses only the last inch","built":"a sacred liturgy fed through a cult oath so comfort reads as butcher's vow"},
"dream-theater~innocence-faded": {"haiku":"Growth and wisdom strip away naiveté, leaving people cynical and disconnected; the narrator struggles to reach others beyond the walls they've built.","opus":"The forfeiture of youthful naivety as insight and maturity set in, a credulous mind's wonder shattering against hard experience. The throughline is alienation, people drifting apart, growing cynical and coldly calculating, sealed off from candor and trapped by circumstance, while the narrator strains to dismantle their defenses and reach the person still hidden inside.","sonnet":"About the death of childlike openness and how adulthood calcifies into cynicism, condescension, and emotional distance — someone is working to break through those walls to reach a person who has retreated behind them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A meditation on the corrosion of naive faith into adult knowing, where the song stages growing up as a slow poisoning: the wide-eyed clarity of childhood curdles into calculated opinion, guarded and closed to anyone reaching in. Dream Theater build the argument across the structure, each return of the refrain sharpening the same wound — first a fallen reflection, then a soured trinity, finally a child reduced to crawling toward a self it can no longer stand upright to meet. Set against the confessional turmoil of Awake, the record where John Petrucci and company traded fantasy sprawl for interior reckoning, the piece treats the disillusionment as universal rather than autobiographical, tracing how the very act of progressing narrows the mind it was meant to widen. The figure who tears through obstruction to reach another is met only by that regressed, crawling child, so the promised rescue arrives as diminishment instead of triumph. What the looping refrain enacts is entrapment: the same bitter lesson keeps recurring because there is no door out of the maturing it mourns.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Secondary sources tie it to Petrucci’s cooling friendship with Kevin Moore; Petrucci himself frames it only as innocence chipped away by life — no name attached.","themes":["identity & becoming","nostalgia & memory","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"progressing narrows the very mind it was meant to widen","built":"the looping refrain enacts entrapment — no door out of the maturing it mourns"},
"dream-theater~invisible-monster":{"haiku":"Anxiety and fear are invisible psychological monsters that consume consciousness without any tangible external threat.","opus":"Dream Theater turn anxiety into a horror-movie creature that lurks unseen, constantly felt, and feeds on baseless fear, tightening around its host and whispering catastrophes that will probably never happen. On their self-titled album, singer James LaBrie dramatizes a mental affliction as a lurking predator, with the struggle framed as wrestling back command of one's own mind.","sonnet":"Anxiety personified as a creature that sits on the shoulder, unseen but constantly felt — whispering worst-case scenarios, feeding on irrational fear, refusing to leave. The speaker names it clearly: an unwelcome presence that hijacks thought, keeps its face hidden, and dwells inside the mind demanding to be confronted before control can be reclaimed.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~just-let-me-breathe": {"haiku":"Mass media and consumer culture lobotomize thought while manufacturing fake rebellion and angst. The song demands independent thought over passive consumption and merchandised dissent.","opus":"A blistering swipe at a pill-pushing, TV-saturated culture that markets rebellion and profits from the young until they self-destruct, invoking two dead grunge-era frontmen as proof that rock stars get processed and discarded. The narrator, fed up with prepackaged outrage sold as authenticity, just wants space to think for himself instead of being programmed.","sonnet":"A screed against the manufactured music industry and its culture of calculated rebellion — MTV-manufactured angst, dead icons as marketing tools, big machines that profit from artists until the fashion fades — demanding space to think and breathe outside the system.","src":"haiku","fable":"A snarling anti-media broadside pitched from a carnival barker's booth: the narrator hawks a mass sedation cure, syringes and screen-glow flushing a culture's mind down the drain, then drops the mask to expose an industry whose profits spike precisely when its idols burn out. Dream Theater, art-rockers who rarely stooped to topical rant, sharpen the indictment by invoking Shannon Hoon and Kurt Cobain, two young frontmen the business mourned loudest once they could no longer resist being packaged and moved as product. The counterfeit fury of the third verse curdles back on the speaker himself, his heroes fallen and his own account bleeding red, so the plea buried under the title reads as a genuine gasp for unmediated thought against everything fed, taught, and formulated into him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"indictment of an industry that profits most when its idols burn out","built":"the barker's mask turns back on the speaker as his own account bleeds red"},
"dream-theater~learning-to-live": {"haiku":"The speaker seeks kindness, beauty, and truth amid pain and urban violence, learning to hold dreams while enduring life's hardship.","opus":"A hard-won shift from chasing love to simply choosing to survive: after being failed and left facing a shared human dread alone, the speaker pleads for compassion and meaning and vows to squeeze everything he can from existence. The core idea is that inner resilience, not circumstance, determines whether anyone can bear the suffering common to all.","sonnet":"About recovering from heartbreak and emotional devastation by choosing to reach for life itself rather than love — the narrator moves through urban isolation, past grief and disillusionment, toward a hard-won openness to kindness, beauty, and truth.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man moves through a frozen city at daybreak, past the sightless rage he has already set down, and the whole track unfolds as a slow argument that survival is a discipline rather than a gift bestowed. Its governing mechanism is a pulse: not romance but the literal muscle whose rhythm the song treats as the arbiter of who withstands shared suffering and who buckles under it. A triad recurs like a mantra — mercy, loveliness, honesty — first demanded on his knees of an indifferent metropolis, then answered near the close by a child's untroubled sound, so plea and granting bracket the entire piece. Fittingly for Dream Theater's 1992 emergence with vocalist James LaBrie, the resolution arrives through architecture rather than statement: an eleven-minute build lets one pivot, from grasping after being loved toward grasping after mere existence, carry weight a compact song would only assert and abandon.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"survival argued as discipline earned rather than gift received","built":"eleven-minute architecture lets one pivot carry what a compact song would only assert"},
"dream-theater~lie": {"haiku":"Trust is impossible when a partner's actions contradict their words, leaving the speaker in shadow and doubt.","opus":"A study in corrosive mistrust, a narrator who cannot swallow a lover's assurances and admits he barely trusts her at all. Shadowed by shattered vows and his own hollowed, damaged spirit, he circles a lifetime of errors and fears he'll drive off the very person who arrived to fill his emptiness.","sonnet":"A relationship built on distrust and broken promises: the narrator can't tell if they know the other person at all, is circling suspicion of infidelity, and spends the song arguing with themselves about whether to trust again or walk away.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being asked to trust someone you have already caught out, and the private arithmetic of staying anyway. Kevin Moore's narrator interrogates a lover's reassurances the way a man treads water in a scalding lake, unable to surface until he solves what he cannot solve, and the nursery-rhyme intrusions of Mother Mary and the wariness of Doubting Thomas turn the betrayal biblical, a vow measured against a promise that once broken never resets. The bridge's reversal is the hinge: the same voice that swore it would never cave admits to a numbed loneliness that lets a new woman fill the emptiness, comparing himself to a dog that needs a leash and reaching for a landline he cannot bring himself to touch. The parenthetical answers clipped in beneath the chorus — the flat contradictions set against his oaths — are the song's real machine, an internal jury naming him a liar in the very act of his denial, so the title fingers not the partner's deceit but his own. On Awake, this is Dream Theater working a claustrophobic, grunge-shadowed heaviness far from progressive spectacle, and the outro's return to The Mirror seals the album's spine of self-deception, addiction, and the face that keeps lying back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Lie and The Mirror began as one piece, split in two for the album — the instrumental outro handing the music back to The Mirror is the seam showing.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the title fingers the accuser's self-deception, not the partner's","built":"parenthetical answers beneath the chorus name him liar mid-denial"},
"dream-theater~lie-demo-1994":{"haiku":"A speaker soaked by distrust circles around someone whose reassurances ring hollow — \"trust me\" lands as threat rather than comfort, and the shadow of doubt never lifts enough to let the other person in.","opus":"Trust corroded by doubt, a narrator drowning himself in scrutiny rather than believe someone's promises. A broken promise is the wound the whole thing circles, and he'd rather stay underwater than risk being fooled again.","sonnet":"Distrust of a new intimacy after being burned — the narrator is out in the rain all day, uncertain whether to trust someone whose reassurances carry the weight of past betrayal. A pivot mid-song moves from wariness toward a fragile opening: she might fill the void, he doesn't want to scare her away, but the shadow of doubt never fully lifts.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~lifting-shadows-off-a-dream": {"haiku":"One person silently listens as another pours out their soul into water; through openness and acceptance, small drops are transformed into ocean, healing broken dreams.","opus":"A solitary, mute man drifts along life's current, propelled by longing and dread, until an attentive presence magnifies the smallest thing into something vast. Dream Theater wrote this Images and Words piece in tribute to bassist John Myung's mother, and its water imagery carries that grief, a shattered vision restored as the gusts subside and the figure is borne tenderly onward.","sonnet":"A man lost in grief and cold silence finds restoration through a woman who listens without condition. She receives what he pours into the water of his sorrow and carries it away — her capacity to hold his broken dream transforms it into something vast and survivable.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tender exception on Awake, an album otherwise clenched around addiction, insomnia and self-erasure, this is the record's rescue ballad, and John Myung builds it entirely around water as the medium of both drowning and deliverance. The verses trap a solitary man in cold, static, downward states — kneeling in rain, sealed under winter, his fragile wings already snapped — while the chorus hands that same water to a woman who swells a single drop into a whole sea, converting his passive submersion into her expansive power. The real motion is a point-of-view split resolved through listening rather than speech: he stays mute and unanswered throughout, and what finally raises the shadow is not his effort but her open, uninsistent attention, the bridge doing nothing except repeating that she hears him. So the drop-to-ocean image reads less as romance than as the way being genuinely heard turns a broken private dream into something vast enough to hold a life. The instrumental break lands exactly at his moment of surrender, Dream Theater trusting the music instead of another verse to carry the release.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"being genuinely heard converts a broken private dream into something vast","built":"the instrumental break lands at his surrender — music carries what a verse cannot"},
"dream-theater~light-fuse-and-get-away": {"haiku":"Betrayal and manipulation by someone untrustworthy leaves lasting psychological wounds and a desire for revenge.","opus":"A tense reckoning with betrayal and lost innocence, the narrator harboring a hunger for payback and a hardened heart, taking stock of what his trust ever earned him. The title doubles as a warning to set things in motion and flee before they blow. American progressive-metal band Dream Theater turn their technical ambition to this dark study of guilt, craving approval, and simmering revenge.","sonnet":"Betrayal that hollowed out the speaker's sense of innocence — someone who fed on attention and praise, preyed on trust, and left them bleeding with a thirst for revenge they cannot act on. The metaphor of lighting a fuse and running captures both the explosive aftermath and the futility: the true always lose everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction spoken by a betrayer, sung in Charlie Dominici's clean croon over the debut's fusion-inflected attack: the narrator opens by coaxing a shaken listener into intimacy, then reveals that closeness itself is the demolition charge — light the fuse, walk clear, let trust do the dying. The title is a saboteur's instruction, and the song runs it as a moral ledger: what loyalty earned the speaker was a wrecked conscience and a heart he now names dangerous, an addict who fed on praise until the walls of his own guilt closed in. What deepens across the two verses is that the accusation loops back onto the singer himself, so the arsonist and the survivor clawing out of the rubble turn out to be one man surveying his own damage.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"seduction revealed as the demolition charge laid in trust","built":"arsonist and rubble-survivor collapse into one self-surveying narrator"},
"dream-theater~lines-in-the-sand": {"haiku":"Humans create their own torment through self-deception and manufactured meaning, crucifying instincts while fabricating demons they then fight as spiritual struggle.","opus":"Dream Theater thread crucifixion imagery through a meditation on faith and self-inflicted torment, picturing a wounded, martyred figure carried off by forces beyond his control. This sprawling, stream-of-consciousness centerpiece observes how people manufacture their own inner monsters, welcome them in, then battle alone, and it settles on a stark insight: existence only grows bearable once we accept our own mortality.","sonnet":"A figure carries the imagery of crucifixion — wounds in the hands, a crown of thorns — as a metaphor for the suffering that spiritual searching extracts from the seeker. The central insight is that humans manufacture their own demons, invite them home, and only begin to live when they admit they are dying.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dream Theater build the whole song from Christian passion imagery — pierced hands, a thorn crown, a bleeding figure dragged out to sea — then quietly transpose it onto ordinary interior life, so private suffering starts wearing the vocabulary of martyrdom without any actual savior in the frame. The verses keep circling one uncomfortable idea: that we manufacture our own tormentors, welcome them home like guests, then parade them as identity, until the demons we dread and the fears we display become indistinguishable. What tilts the piece from complaint toward acceptance is a late reversal, where the terrors and unanswered questions stop being wounds to flee and get reframed as the very material a life is woven from, the drawn boundary of the title read less as defiance than as the edge a person cannot cross and still remain themselves.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The soul-shout answering LaBrie on the choruses is Doug Pinnick of King’s X.","themes":["identity & becoming","faith & the occult","madness & the mind"],"means":"self-manufactured terrors reframed as the material a life is woven from","built":"passion imagery transposed onto secular interior life without placing a savior in the frame"},
"dream-theater~lord-nafaryus":{"haiku":"An envious ruler summons a legendary hero to his court, sensing both threat and inspiration in the stranger's growing power.","opus":"A tyrant hearing of a rising savior and setting out to judge the threat for himself, threading his paranoia through genuine love for his family. Power's insecurity given a face within the band's dystopian rock opera.","sonnet":"An imperial ruler hears rumours of a charismatic young saviour threatening his authority and travels to witness the phenomenon firsthand, convinced of his own superiority but admitting private curiosity. He brings his family and guard as a display of power, already framing what he expects to see as delusion.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~losing-time-grand-finale": {"haiku":"Dissociation and trauma fragment identity; the narrator explores psychological escape as both survival mechanism and descent into unreality.","opus":"A clinical, compassionate portrait of dissociation, a woman who detaches from herself to survive trauma, losing time as a defense. It widens into a plea to understand mental illness rather than shame it.","sonnet":"A portrait of a woman who learned to dissociate as a survival mechanism — losing time, drifting miles away, detaching from herself — while the broader reflection asks why shame around mental illness keeps people from saving those they love before it is too late.","src":"haiku","fable":"A woman who wears the same plain black and forgets whole days has taught herself to split off from her own awareness as the last survival skill she has, waking to find her mind drifted miles from where her body sat. The movement then pulls back from that single sufferer toward everyone, arguing that shame silences care and strands the very people we most want to protect, while a mind spirals toward some answer sealed away inside it. Compassion, it insists, is what finally lets us reach the turmoil churning underneath a life.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The finale is where the suite shows its hand: six separate disorders, then one closing argument that the real subject was never the cases but the stigma — the shame that keeps the well from reaching the ill.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","shame & repression","madness & the mind"],"means":"shame silences care and strands the ones we mean to protect","built":"the camera widens from one woman to everyone mid-movement"},
"dream-theater~lost-not-forgotten": {"haiku":"An ancient tyrant ruled with pride and fear across an empire; though his story fades and his kingdom vanishes, his legend endures as an unforgettable force.","opus":"A proud self-eulogy voiced by a mortal despot who rules like a deity, feared throughout his arid realm and convinced he cannot fall. Beneath the swagger runs an elegy for a collapsed kingdom and a magnificence no one remembers, Dream Theater framing absolute power as something destined to crumble into an unread chapter of history.","sonnet":"A portrait of a feared desert ruler — a tyrant who defended his people and led his empire but whose spirit was black as coal; though his kingdom has crumbled and the story goes untold, he is not forgotten, still reigning in myth against impossible odds.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person portrait of a desert warlord who opens by disavowing the very immortality his boasts keep claiming, so the whole monologue runs on the gap between the man and the myth he insists on being. The verses build his legend as a fallen golden kingdom, a story no reader ever received, while the hammering title refrain resolves that contradiction: erased from the living record yet surviving as an unkillable soul, the glory outlasting the empire that earned it. Dream Theater stage this as a swaggering overture, the vocal grandiosity and the tyrant's self-mythologizing feeding each other until the sound becomes the ego it describes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","death & grief"],"means":"the myth outlives the empire that earned it","built":"grandiose sound enacts the ego the lyric describes"},
"dream-theater~metropolis-pt-1-the-miracle-and-the-sleeper": {"haiku":"Three dances mark existence: death as initiation, deceit as progression, and love as eternal culmination. A city observes as souls journey toward final union.","opus":"A cryptic parable of two souls bound within one mind across three dances of death, deceit, and love, resolving that eternal love is the only escape from the cold city's grip.","sonnet":"A song framed as an allegorical vision of birth, death, and love as the three eternal dances — the narrator moves through fear, confinement, deceit, and ultimately arrival at love as the only force that transcends the city of Metropolis, the third and final dance that lasts forever.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song frames existence as three ritual dances, and its whole architecture is that numbered sequence: death opening the cycle, deceit prolonging it without resolution, and a third movement held back until some buried struggle between two named figures finally settles. Between those stations a plainer voice keeps offering consolations it half-disbelieves, a child's faith that pain could be outrun curdling into an adult's sleepless dread of everything eventually arriving to collect. What the counting is really doing is deferral, stacking ceremony over a wound so that the final turn, love revealed as the last dance, lands at once as answer and as a sentence binding the pair together forever.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","love & desire"],"means":"ceremonial counting used to defer a wound's final reckoning","built":"a numbered three-part ritual holds love back until the last turn"},
"dream-theater~misunderstood": {"haiku":"A meditation on feeling isolated and misperceived despite being surrounded by people, with the speaker describing themselves as superhuman or exceptional to explain their disconnection and loneliness. The theme is the gap between appearance and reality in social isolation.","opus":"Explores an isolation felt even while surrounded by people, the sense of knowing many yet truly knowing no one, and the loneliness of being seen as superhuman while feeling abandoned and misunderstood.","sonnet":"A person grapples with deep social isolation — feeling abandoned despite being surrounded by people, knowing many yet truly knowing no one, and sensing that an outwardly superhuman or composed image has only made them more fundamentally misunderstood.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man ringed by admirers keeps starving for one honest connection, and the song tracks how fame estranges him from everyone who imagines they know him. Each verse hardens into a chain of losses that strip away every mask he can wear: the applauded figure marched like a caged lion, the one who once took whatever he wanted now standing empty-handed, the object of worship reduced to begging to be saved. The refrain frames all of it as a plea against being read wrong, insisting that what looks superhuman from outside is only a person no one has actually understood.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"fame empties the figure everyone imagines they know","built":"each verse strips away a mask until the icon is left begging"},
"dream-theater~moment-of-betrayal":{"haiku":"A brother sacrifices his loved one to a rival in an act of treachery masked as protection, trading loyalty for false heroism.","opus":"Within the Astonishing story, a brother steeled to commit a treacherous murder disguised as heroism, bound by a warrior's code he's about to break. The dramatic weight sits in guilt swallowed for the sake of a greater plan.","sonnet":"A man agrees to betray his brother to save his son, arranging for him to be ambushed and killed in isolation while feigning heroism to the outside world. Guilt and shame are the price he knows he will carry forever, even as he follows through.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~never-enough": {"haiku":"Someone exhausted by an ungrateful relationship where they sacrifice everything and still face criticism. Nothing they do is ever enough, and their needs are completely ignored.","opus":"A bitter address to an insatiable, ungrateful taker for whom nothing the narrator sacrifices, his blood, his family, his sleepless nights, is ever appreciated or enough. It reads as the resentment of a caretaker or partner drained dry, threatening to walk away before it's too late.","sonnet":"About burning out trying to satisfy someone who is never satisfied — giving everything including family time and sleep, only to be accused of not caring. It's about the moment before walking away from a relationship or situation built entirely on ingratitude.","src":"haiku","fable":"A bitter monologue built on the image of giving as self-butchery: the speaker lays himself open, watches the other take and take, and gets blamed even for how his blood spills. The two verses ratchet the martyrdom outward, from body to family, each ledger of sacrifice sharpening the same accusation that nothing he offers is ever received as enough. But the song is smart enough to wear the mask and let it slip: a giver who tallies every wound this precisely, and threatens departure to see whether the leaving would finally be appreciated, is keeping a score of his own, and the ungratefulness he indicts starts to look like the transaction he needed all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Portnoy's own telling aims it elsewhere: the song is about the two percent of Dream Theater's fanbase no gesture ever satisfies.","themes":["shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"the martyred giver's tally reveals his own transaction","built":"the mask slips when threats of departure expose the score being kept"},
"dream-theater~new-millennium": {"haiku":"Hope for renewal and change in the face of endless frustration and broken systems. The narrator waits for a fresh beginning despite constant setbacks.","opus":"An uneasy hope at the turn of a new era, sensing a wind of change while facing bleak realities where faith doesn't pay overdue rent and glitter turns blue. The narrator urges others to keep their heads up and endure the deja-vu nightmare toward a fresh start.","sonnet":"A song poised on the threshold of a new era, surveying exhaustion with the present — cold, ruthless people, broken promises, faith that does not pay the rent — while holding onto a feeling that the tide is turning and things will finally improve.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dream Theater built this around Y2K dread, dressing apocalyptic imagery — a lone survivor beneath a scorched horizon, mistaking rescue for arrival — in the glossy commercial hooks their label wanted, and the seam between those two impulses is the actual subject. The verses reach for genuine unease while the refrain keeps insisting fortune has already reversed, an assurance the parenthetical pep-talk repeats so relentlessly it curdles into a chant nobody quite believes. That strain pays off in the tunnel gag, where the approaching glow turns out to be a second oncoming locomotive, so the whole thing mocks its own optimism: the deliverance everyone waits for is just the next collision arriving on schedule.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"optimism that mocks itself on arrival","built":"the tunnel punchline exposes the refrain as chant nobody believes"},
"dream-theater~octavarium":{"haiku":"A psychiatric patient wakes from a 30-year catatonic state only to be chemically sedated back into compliance; the song questions medical authority and the erosion of consciousness through medication.","opus":"A man who vowed he'd never turn into the compliant figure before him realizes he has looped straight back into becoming him, surfacing from a years-long unresponsive state on drugs that don't help. The pun-heavy finale about coming around to the start makes the point plain, life and self forever circling home. ","sonnet":"A man who once vowed never to become complacent like his father finds he has done exactly that; interwoven with a subplot about a patient waking from a thirty-year catatonic state being re-sedated, the song loops back on itself about cycles of becoming what you feared.","src":"haiku","fable":"A twenty-four-minute suite that distrusts its own scale, using an octave's eight rungs as a diagnosis rather than a boast: the man who vowed never to soften into an ordinary contented existence wakes decades later to find he now envies exactly the settled figure he once scorned, and the piece climbs one interval at a time toward the confession that the instant you begin to truly live is the instant the structure named in the title snaps shut on you. Petrucci writes the human bookends while Portnoy's central movement melts language into a delirium of half-remembered rock songs welded end to end, the words themselves losing their footing on a dizzy circling refrain, so the babble enacts the loop it describes instead of explaining it. The design's genius is that it swallows its own tail on every level the band could reach at once, a group closing its eighth record with an eighth track built as an eight-note run that lands back on the pitch it departed, the closing line handing you straight into the phrase that opened everything. Heard that way the album is not wallowing in doom so much as staging it: the very refusal to become someone unremarkable is the machinery that delivers you there, and the returning wheel is the argument.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","madness & the mind"],"means":"the refusal to become ordinary delivers you there anyway","built":"eighth track on the eighth record built as an eight-note run returning to its opening pitch"},
"dream-theater~on-the-backs-of-angels": {"haiku":"The song condemns those in power who profit from war while hiding safely away, sacrificing ordinary people's lives for political gain and hidden agendas.","opus":"Opening their post-Portnoy reboot, Dream Theater indict a society parasitic on the very people who sustain it, its hollow ambition steering everyone toward ruin while the decent are led obliviously to slaughter. The protest framing lends the band's technical grandeur a pointed, disillusioned political edge.","sonnet":"A jeremiad against those who profit from others while engineering collapse. The righteous are exploited, the shameless succeed, and the lines between virtue and victimhood blur under a system that uses idealism as bait. The slaughter is institutional, and the lambs keep walking toward it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A furious indictment of a nation propping its comforts on other people's sacrifice, casting the speaker as a passenger who keeps feeding the machine hauling everyone toward a cliff while insisting the reckoning is already scheduled. The chorus turns strange and doubled, a plea for shelter and guidance sung to whoever is steering him toward drowning or the butcher's block, so pity and complicity smear together until you cannot tell the shepherd from the executioner. That the piece reserves its longest breaths for the instrumental frame and the central solo lets Petrucci and Rudess voice the rage the verses only name, the band's first writing after Portnoy's exit sharpened into an argument it can make without words.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"complicity and victimhood smeared until indistinguishable","built":"instrumental frame carries the rage the verses only name"},
"dream-theater~only-a-matter-of-time": {"haiku":"A dreamer pursues success against practical warnings, maintaining faith that destiny and determination will eventually prevail.","opus":"A grand reflection on ambition itself, setting a dreamer's daring gamble against the safe, ordinary route and asking whether safety causes contentment or merely tags along with it. A father's blessing sends his son out to face stormy waters. Dream Theater, the American prog-metal pioneers, sprawl through shifting sections here, the instrumental scale echoing the song's case for boldness over conformity.","sonnet":"A portrait of the tension between ambition and safety — a suited man with a contract, a dreamer performing for an audience of one, a son departing to sea as his father's blessing fades. The song holds two truths at once: the practical world will pull you back in line, but faith in destiny is itself the destination already reached.","src":"haiku","fable":"A young performer signs the contract a smooth stranger dangles, and then the song quietly recasts him as a sailor pushing off from shore, so the whole piece asks whether chasing a calling is ambition or reckless gambling with your one safe life. Charlie Dominici delivers it as an anthem of nerve winning out over doubt, and the writing keeps circling a single anxious wager: that if belief itself is real, then reaching the destination is already settled and the long waiting is the only thing left standing in the way.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"There is no title track — the album’s name hides here, in Kevin Moore’s lyric, the line about the day dream and day unite. The debut is named after this song’s wager.","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"belief itself offered as proof that arrival is already settled","built":"sailor metaphor recasts a career wager as a departure from safe shore"},
"dream-theater~out-of-reach": {"haiku":"Romantic longing and melancholy for someone emotionally distant, where connection remains impossible despite persistent yearning.","opus":"A yearning portrait of a woman who feels like magic yet stays forever unattainable, her waves crashing over the narrator as he watches, tongue-tied and breathless, life slipping past out of his grasp. Longing shades into a gentle warning not to let it all drift away. Dream Theater, the American progressive metal band, dial back their complexity for something more direct, romantic and quietly melancholic.","sonnet":"Someone is drawn to a woman who feels electric but unreachable — she carries sadness in her smile, chases something she cannot quite get to, and the speaker recognises the same unreachability in her that she has been waiting for someone to name. The call toward the end shifts to encouragement: you are stronger than you know.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song opens as a besotted first-person rush — a spell cast, a thousand volts firing through the nerves, an outward swagger worn over a chest that can barely draw breath — the direct, groove-forward hard rock Dream Theater leaned into on this record rather than their usual labyrinth. Then the pronoun quietly turns from I to you, and the woman who electrified the narrator is reframed as someone sinking under a current, chasing a promise that keeps losing its way, so the doting stare curdles into worry watching a person go under while he stands too far off to haul her back. What began as desire resolves into a hand extended toward a life dissolving beyond his grasp, the title naming the distance between wanting to save someone and being unable to touch them at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"desire curdling into helpless witness as someone goes under","built":"pronoun shift from I to you reframes doting stare as distress"},
"dream-theater~outcry": {"haiku":"Citizens rise up against oppression and injustice, willing to die for freedom and resist an authoritarian regime through armed rebellion.","opus":"A sweeping revolutionary anthem, the oppressed rising after countless silenced voices and long turmoil, resolving to fight to the end for liberty as the planet looks on and the young perish. Its refrain calls people to stand, unite, and be tallied. Dream Theater, the American progressive metal band, aim their epic scale at this stirring tableau of resistance, freedom worth any cost.","sonnet":"A revolution anthem — the suppressed have finally reached the limit and are rising collectively, despite the cost being counted in lives buried in the ground. The tone is defiant and mournful at once: streets bathed in blood, children dying, but the outcry will not stop until the rule that crushed them is gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A protest anthem assembled from the standard-issue components of one: distant thunder, streets running red, an anonymous oppressor to rise up against, all deployed at the arena scale Dream Theater favors on this comeback record. What it never does is name the cause — the enemy stays a faceless you and the revolution a rising mood, an anthem universalized past its occasion. The one place it lands is the closing turn, where the martial swagger drops and the camera pulls back to a watching world and dying children, admitting that the price of the uprising is paid by the people it claims to free rather than the tyrant it defies.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The occasion exists — Petrucci has said the lyrics are about the Middle East uprisings of the era, the fight the song deliberately leaves unnamed.","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"revolution universalized until its cost falls on the freed","built":"the enemy kept faceless so any cause can fill the frame"},
"dream-theater~overture":{"haiku":"Dreams and waking life collapse into each other as the dreamer searches for an elusive girl whose fractured identity mirrors the searching self across parallel existences.","opus":"The opening of Metropolis Pt. 2's story, a sleeper drawn nightly into another life and a girl in a mirror, sensing a past incarnation and searching for the key to unlock his own mind and the truth of who he was.","sonnet":"A recurring dream draws the speaker into a familiar house with a girl in the mirror whose untold story tears at her soul; waking in a metropolis, he is haunted by the sense of a parallel life he may have lived and a missing key to unlock the connection.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~pale-blue-dot": {"haiku":"Humanity contemplates its smallness in the cosmos, looking back at Earth as a fragile point of light. The song asks who will save us from ourselves when all our religions, heroes, and institutions fail.","opus":"Dream Theater build a prog-metal meditation on Carl Sagan's image of Earth as a tiny speck seen from deep space. Every hero and monster, every act of love and cruelty, shares that same fragile dot, and the song keeps returning to one anxious question: with no rescuer coming, can humanity save itself from its own violence?","sonnet":"Earth viewed from the edge of space — a fragile pixel carrying every human contradiction, every war and every act of love. The cosmic perspective frames humanity's violence and tribalism as absurd against the scale of the void, posing the question of who saves a species from itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title borrows Carl Sagan's name for the 1990 Voyager photograph, in which Earth shows up as a single speck of light suspended in the emptiness beyond Neptune, and the song builds its whole argument from that shrunken vantage. Each verse pulls the lens back until the entire species is one fragile mote falling through empty space, and then the chorus rushes in close to inventory who rides on it — the creators and destroyers, the warriors and cowards, the parents and children crowded onto the same grain of dust. That pendulum, swinging out to the astronomer's scale and back to the human roll-call, is the read: from far enough away every enemy shares the only home there is, which turns the closing plea about who could save us from ourselves into the one thing distance cannot answer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","war & battle","politics & society"],"means":"cosmic distance reframing shared human culpability","built":"pendulum between astronomer's scale and human roll-call"},
"dream-theater~panic-attack": {"haiku":"A person trapped in anxiety spirals through physical symptoms of terror and helplessness, unable to ground themselves or understand the source. Hysteria and phobia dominate, leaving no escape from complex psychological delirium.","opus":"A close first-person rendering of an anxiety attack, all pounding pulse, terror, deadened limbs and a fake feeling that something is desperately wrong now. The speaker feels frozen, taken over, and swamped by irrational fear and unraveling thought.","sonnet":"A clinical and visceral description of a panic attack — racing heart, paralysis, numbness, overwhelming dread — and the helplessness of not understanding why the body and mind spiral out of control.","src":"haiku","fable":"A panic episode narrated from inside the body: a hammering chest and clenched breath while the mind, oddly detached, keeps asking why the alarm won't shut off or where the dread was even inherited from. The odd-metered riff stumbles forward without ever settling, and the closing refrain circles the same helpless vocabulary, naming its own condition as something that possesses rather than afflicts and refusing to loosen its grip.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the body in alarm while the mind asks why from a distance","built":"the odd-metered riff stumbles forward enacting the episode it describes"},
"dream-theater~paralyzed": {"haiku":"Emotional numbness and social self-consciousness paralyze the speaker, making authentic connection and movement impossible.","opus":"Dream Theater examine crippling anxiety and bottled-up frustration, portraying someone who swallows his words while others see straight through his fragile facade. Cast as a self-styled victim hooked on his own suffering, he cracks under the pressure, and the song warns that perceiving life through so narrow and distorted a view guarantees defeat.","sonnet":"Social anxiety and emotional shutdown — the narrator is invisible and paper-thin to strangers, addicted to a painful cycle they can't break, unable to see past the narrow fractured lens they view the world through.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses build a portrait of a man silencing himself under scrutiny, swallowing rage until a stranger's glance lands like an old cut reopening, and reaching for the Daniel-in-the-den image to cast his endurance as martyrdom. Where Dream Theater usually route torment through instrumental complexity, the chorus does the opposite work, collapsing all that pressure into a flat admission of numbness and compulsion, a nervous system so overstrained it simply stops responding. The closing chorus supplies the diagnosis the earlier ones withhold, arguing that a mind viewing everything through one cramped, self-pitying frame guarantees its own defeat, which reframes the paralysis as self-inflicted rather than merely suffered.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","shame & repression","madness & the mind"],"means":"self-pitying frame guarantees the paralysis it suffers","built":"late-arriving chorus diagnosis reframes victim as architect"},
"dream-theater~perfect-strangers": {"haiku":"An eternal echo from the past attempting connection through wind and memory, bound to remain separate and silent.","opus":"A ghostly reincarnation ballad, the narrator an echo of a past life brushing against a former love who can't place him, the ache of ancient recognition held apart by the cruel rule that they must stay strangers.","sonnet":"A cover of the Deep Purple song about a mysterious presence from the past that flows through a person's life — the narrator is a stranger who has traveled far, echoes of past moments, someone who cannot enter your world but can only speak to you on the wind; the distance must be maintained.","src":"haiku","fable":"Deep Purple's song is sung by an ancient revenant drifting through a living person's memory, and the single thing it demands is distance — that the two remain unknown to each other even as it murmurs on the wind. The lyric works by an image-system of cold and vast expanse: frozen spirits, a thousand flown oceans, a silver strand strung across the sky, all rendering the dead as something felt at the edge of a warm life rather than met inside it. Dream Theater's decision to cover it is the real content — a young virtuoso band capable of dazzling instead reaches for slow modal atmosphere over hooks, playing sustained dread with restraint their technical reputation would never predict. The recasting mirrors the text: an outfit summoning the mysticism of elders they never shared a stage with, reverent across a gap that can be sensed but never crossed. The tribute performs the very estrangement it borrows.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"the dead felt at the warm edges of life, never met inside it","built":"a virtuoso band reaching for slow modal restraint to perform the estrangement the lyric describes"},
"dream-theater~peruvian-skies": {"haiku":"Under Latin American skies, Vanessa endures domestic violence from her father, losing her ability to love as brutality replaces tenderness. She's trapped between being battered and confused about what she once cherished.","opus":"A narrative about Vanessa, a girl trapped in a home of nightly abuse and violence at her father's hands, battered and confusing love for hate. The abuser's voice admits he is the monster her father created in the same name, tracing a cycle of domestic violence passed down through generations.","sonnet":"About a woman named Vanessa living under abuse — beaten and bruised, confusing the loss of love with hatred, trapped under Peruvian skies in a pattern of midnight violence. The abuser speaks directly, acknowledging he's the monster but taking his damage with him when he goes.","src":"haiku","fable":"A domestic-abuse portrait built on a cruel structural trick: the exotic postcard title and the swelling arena chorus wrap around a woman named Vanessa who waits inside recurring nightly violence, the pretty frame working exactly as denial works, letting the horror pass as no big surprise. The verses split the point of view so we hear it two ways — first a neighbor eavesdropping through the wall, too passive to intervene, then the abuser himself, who disowns blame by pinning his monstrousness on the father who made him, a generational excuse that indicts rather than absolves. The bridge names the double life plainly, terror after dark and lies by daylight, and the refusal of the confession to actually free her is the song's bleakest note: telling the secret changes nothing. That the band lets Petrucci's solo carry the emotional climax rather than a lyric admits where language has failed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","family & blood","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"the pretty frame works exactly as denial works","built":"split point of view gives the abuser a generational excuse that indicts him"},
"dream-theater~peruvian-skies-demo-version-1996-1997":{"opus":"A woman trapped in a cycle of abuse, her confusion of love and hate passed down from a violent father to a violent partner. The band watches helplessly, the monster explicitly the product of what came before.","src":"opus"},
"dream-theater~prophets-of-war": {"haiku":"The speaker questions whether military interventions truly liberate people or simply profit those in power, calling for change and honesty about the cost of war.","opus":"From Systematic Chaos, the prog-metal band mounts a pointed protest against manufactured warfare, questioning whether liberation is the real aim or whether unnamed derelicts simply profit whether the fight is won or lost. It interrogates blind acceptance of official truth, mourns the loved ones fed into the machine, and repeatedly demands change while asking who genuinely benefits from a war sold as just.","sonnet":"Military intervention is interrogated from inside the doubt it produces — liberation rhetoric against the reality of bodies, profit, and rhetorical loops that never change. Arrogance requires forgiveness; empathy without honour means nothing. The call for change circles back unanswered, its urgency intact and its outcome uncertain.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a protest anthem built as an interrogation: nearly every hook is a question about a war of liberation, probing whether removing a tyrant serves the people freed or only the contractors who bank a return whether the campaign wins or loses. Dream Theater voice it from inside the culpable majority — a citizenry that swallowed a pretext without scrutiny — so the guilt reads as collective, not aimed at distant villains. The engine is the song's refusal to answer its own chorus, cycling the same demand for reckoning until the repetition itself becomes the accusation, momentum standing in for progress. Beneath the earnest plea for faith and forgiveness runs a colder charge: that professed sympathy and empathy are worthless theater unless honor and accountability back them, and that leaders reciting the same justifications are too irresponsible to admit the damage. Rooted in mid-2000s America, its named cost is the household — parents and children conscripted to prop up borrowed ideals — indicting the machine that stays the course by keeping everyone complicit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"LaBrie worked from Joseph C. Wilson’s memoir The Politics of Truth; the title puns prophets and profits.","themes":["politics & society","war & battle","money & the street"],"means":"guilt framed as collective rather than aimed at distant villains","built":"the chorus repeats unanswered until repetition becomes the accusation"},
"dream-theater~pull-me-under": {"haiku":"Life as an inescapable current pulls the narrator deeper, moving toward mortality with ambiguous acceptance. The song explores surrender to fate, honor, and the pull toward death.","opus":"Dream Theater's breakthrough single grapples with mortality and the strange indifference of existence continuing regardless of any single life. The narrator confronts his approaching death with a defiant refusal of fear, ultimately choosing to yield rather than resist. Loose Hamlet allusions frame it as a meditation on fate and acceptance.","sonnet":"A figure confronts mortality and the accelerating passage of time, resolved to face what is coming without fear while driven by honor and a need to right wrongs. The closing fragment from Hamlet — \"Oh that this too, too solid flesh would melt\" — anchors the song's meditation on the desire to dissolve into something larger.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man watches the sky move and lets himself drift along with it, cataloguing arrows, rising seas, and a falling sparrow as though the whole natural order were rehearsing his own descent toward a last breath he counts down day by day. The pre-chorus is the structural engine: the world turns around him, then turns without him, then finally turns inside him, and that inward migration is the read, as dread stops being weather he observes and becomes the machinery of his own body dragging him beneath the surface. Kevin Moore stitched the song around lines lifted from Hamlet's death-haunted soliloquies, so the talk of honor and spite and bending to another's will lands as a young man wearing Shakespearean grief like armor, half-terrified of dissolving and half-desperate to be pulled down into it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","death & grief","nature & the elements"],"means":"dread migrating inward until it becomes the body dragging itself under","built":"pre-chorus tracks the world turning around then without then inside the speaker"},
"dream-theater~repentance": {"haiku":"After reviewing a lifetime of wrongs, someone acknowledges shame and seeks to make amends, confronting how selfishness hurt others and asking forgiveness.","opus":"A recovering addict facing a mirror and blank page to make amends, cataloguing years of wreckage and shame; woven with real spoken confessions from others about failing loved ones, it dramatizes the twelve-step reckoning that regret and hard-won wrongness can be the beginning of restoration.","sonnet":"A reckoning with a life of past wrongs — the narrator works through writing a list of amends, confessing regret for harm done to others, culminating in a collage of spoken testimonies from real people apologising for specific failures and betrayals.","src":"haiku","fable":"A recovering man greets his own reflection like a returning friend and then writes out an inventory of his wreckage, the drama enacting the twelve-step ritual it only lightly disguises — Portnoy's confession in LaBrie's voice, framed by Alcoholics Anonymous scaffolding rather than any private metaphor. The song moves in two stances: first the dread of a blank sheet, then the completed ledger with every wrong made plain and the harder work of amends still waiting ahead. Its emotional pivot is the turn from a solitary accounting to public restitution, where a chorus of real spoken apologies from Portnoy's guest musicians displaces the singer entirely, letting private shame become communal testimony. The recited Ninth Step promises at the close reframe the whole ordeal not as self-punishment but as the toll paid for a serenity the narrator has not yet earned and chooses to trust anyway.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The spoken apologies are real confessions recorded for Portnoy by fellow musicians: Mikael Åkerfeldt, Jon Anderson, David Ellefson, Daniel Gildenlöw, Steve Hogarth, Chris Jericho, Neal Morse, Joe Satriani, Corey Taylor, Steve Vai and Steven Wilson.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"private shame displaced into communal testimony","built":"guest musicians' spoken apologies replace the singer entirely"},
"dream-theater~room-137": {"haiku":"A number-haunted obsession drives descent into death; the speaker is convinced a hidden message means their time is ending. The boundary between worlds collapses into never-ending numbers.","opus":"The prog-metal band builds a numerological thriller around a man obsessed with the number 137, convinced it encodes death, an entrance to paradise, and a barrier separating realities. Time is running short as he begs to be carried across. The intricate arrangement traces a mind spiralling toward a fatal hidden pattern.","sonnet":"A number becomes an obsession — a coded door to another dimension or a hidden truth about mortality. The narrator is driven by a numerological fixation that feels like revelation, reading death everywhere and racing against a boundary between dimensions before time expires.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole track hangs on 137, the fine-structure constant's rough inverse — the pure figure physicist Wolfgang Pauli reportedly meant to demand of God on his deathbed, and who died in hospital room 137. Dream Theater build from that legend: a mind circling the idea that one dimensionless value seals the seam between physics and the divine, and that cracking it might mean crossing over before the body quits. The verses stage two figures at once, a fading seeker and something reaching toward him from the far side of a threshold, so the recurring plea to be shown what stays out of reach reads as both scientific hunger and a wager against mortality.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The lyric is drummer Mike Mangini’s — his lone writing credit on the record; the number-obsession is the drummer’s.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"a dimensionless constant offered as the seam to the divine","built":"Pauli's deathbed room number anchors physics and mortality in one frame"},
"dream-theater~s2n": {"haiku":"The song catalogs modern crises—climate change, addiction, violence, war—as noise obscuring meaningful signals about what humanity must address. It argues that filtering out chatter reveals the urgent truths we must act on.","opus":"The band treats modern life as a communications problem: too much distortion, war, disaster, addiction, and deceit drowning out anything meaningful. The solution offered is to filter the din and let a clearer message through, so damage can start mending. The music's overloaded complexity deliberately enacts the very overload it urges listeners to rise above.","sonnet":"A call to filter signal from noise in an era saturated with catastrophe, misinformation, and distraction. The world keeps turning regardless of the flood of crisis headlines, and the only meaningful response is tuning the inner voice precisely enough to hear it above the relentless chatter.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title borrows an engineer's ratio — the strength of a true transmission measured against the interference drowning it — and enlarges it into a survival question for a species deafened by its own information. The song builds by widening the frame: an intro that asks whether we are even awake, then a bridge that abandons syntax to machine-gun a catalogue of horrors — climate, overdose, terror, the innocent dying — each item severed to two or three words so the language itself performs the overload it names. Against that clatter the choruses keep returning to one steadying figure, a turning wheel we grip while the world spins, insisting that recovering the clear transmission from the din is what finally lets the wound close — Dream Theater in plain-spoken civic mode rather than fantasy, betting that under all the interference there was something coherent worth straining to hear.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"recovering clear signal from civilizational noise as survival","built":"bridge syntax collapses to two-word fragments enacting the overload it names"},
"dream-theater~sacrificed-sons": {"haiku":"Religious extremists twist scripture to justify terrorist violence, celebrating martyrs while innocent towers collapse; the song questions how faith becomes justification for mass killing.","opus":"Dream Theater's reckoning with the September 11th attacks, moving from the live-broadcast horror of the towers falling to a broader indictment of religious extremism. The song condemns those who murder while claiming to enact divine will, and its central challenge is whether cruelty committed in devotion's name can honestly be called an expression of faith.","sonnet":"A direct response to 9/11 — the song moves through the horror of watching the towers fall on television and arrives at a question: how can people who claim to follow God's will commit such violence, and how can scripture lead anyone to worship death as divine purpose?","src":"haiku","fable":"It opens inside a September morning of televised catastrophe, jets and a collapsing skyline stitched from panicked broadcast fragments, then pulls back to indict the theology that could bless such a thing as holy. The band's fury lands not on the attackers alone but on any scripture wielded to license slaughter and rename its dead as martyrs, dividing a bewildered civic grief from the certainty that armed the planes. Its closing plea, half prayer and half accusation, asks whether a species so willing to sanctify hatred can survive itself, and who is left to speak for the truth once faith has been conscripted into killing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The intro collage is authentic 9/11 news audio, and the lyric is LaBrie's — his reflection on scripture conscripted into slaughter.","themes":["faith & the occult","war & battle","death & grief"],"means":"scripture indicted as the instrument that armed the planes","built":"broadcast fragments splice civic grief against the theological certainty that enabled it"},
"dream-theater~scarred": {"haiku":"Emotional isolation despite long bonds: the speaker wears scars from past violations and refuses vulnerability with family, maintaining conviction and survival alone while those near them remain blind to their wounds.","opus":"Dream Theater trace a hard-won emotional recovery, the speaker guarding strong passion yet feeling fate go wrong, refusing to be violated or filled with fatalistic remedies. He shows hands whose scars go unseen, fearing abandonment. Across the sprawling piece, belief steadies him through blood and fear until the closing turn, where misery becomes something to learn from, leaving him at last inspired and content.","sonnet":"A person who never showed weakness now confronts decades of suppressed trauma, family estrangement, and the invisible wounds others refuse to acknowledge. The resolution turns on faith and communal prayer rather than individual strength — accepting that scars can coexist with conviction, and that he will not face the exposure alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor stares down the people meant to hold him and refuses their comfort, hearing poison in it, while the song builds through the classic Dream Theater tactic of stacking questions against a lover or family until the accusations run both directions. The infinitives that open it — rising, falling, hurting, healing — lay out the whole emotional ledger before a single verse names a wound, so the drama becomes watching him decide which of those verbs he will finally choose. His central image is a pair of scarred hands held out and pointedly unseen, evidence of damage that the very people beside him refuse to register. The bridge cracks the armor: the same voice that spat back at their venom admits it is calmed to know others are praying for him, one binding vow he cannot spit out. The closing chorus then quietly rewrites its own lines, trading the blood-and-fear terror for something inspired and content — recovery earned syllable by syllable rather than simply declared.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","anger & defiance","faith & the occult"],"means":"recovery earned syllable by syllable rather than declared","built":"the closing chorus quietly rewrites its own lines from terror to content"},
"dream-theater~scene-five-through-her-eyes": {"haiku":"A man discovers the grave of a sacrificed girl and grieves her lost potential, learning about life and loss by understanding her tragic circumstance.","opus":"Part of the Metropolis Pt. 2 concept album, this movement finds the protagonist discovering a young girl's tragic death and grieving as if a part of himself is gone. At her neglected, overgrown grave he reads the inscription for a lost child and, in that mourning, understands his own existence more clearly, turning private grief into hard-won acceptance and goodbye.","sonnet":"A man discovers the grave of a child — sacrificed without agency by circumstance — and grieves her as if grieving a piece of himself. The encounter forces a reckoning with his own unexamined past, and her tragedy becomes the lens through which he finally understands who he is and what he has been given.","src":"haiku","fable":"The emotional center of Scenes From A Memory, and the one place the album's elaborate reincarnation-thriller machinery stops running long enough to feel something. Nicholas, the modern man who has been regressed into the story of a murdered girl, finally reaches her grave, and the song's whole architecture is a transfer: he learns his own life, as the refrain keeps insisting, only by borrowing her ruined one. Dream Theater deliberately strip their prog reflexes here — no shredding, no metric games, just a plain ballad carried on female-voiced gospel backing — because the point is emotional identification, not spectacle, and the restraint is what lets the grief land. The turn comes when his mourning for a stranger doubles as mourning he owes his own past: he counts what he was spared (a wife, a son) against what desperation stole from her, so that empathy becomes a settling of his own accounts before the record's later twist reframes the whole thing. It closes not on resolution but on release — a threshold crossed, the self loosened enough to grieve — which is exactly the vulnerable footing the album needs before its final betrayal snaps shut.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","identity & becoming","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"empathy as a settling of the mourner's own accounts","built":"prog reflexes stripped to plain ballad so identification can land without spectacle"},
"dream-theater~scene-four-beyond-this-life": {"haiku":"An investigation into a murder-suicide where a man shoots his lover then himself, examining whether it was a crime of passion, self-defense, or a pact. The song suggests deeds and choices carry consequences that extend beyond physical life.","opus":"Assembled from news-report fragments, a man fatally shoots a young woman who wouldn't pardon his straying, then takes his own life and leaves a note, the inquiry weighing struggle against premeditation. The refrain, that our present selves are simply the sum of our past selves, carried onward past death, frames the murder-suicide as karma feeding an endless rebirth.","sonnet":"A newspaper-style account of a murder-suicide: a possessive, violent man kills the woman who rejected him and then shoots himself. The song weaves in the idea that karma carries beyond death — what we have been is what we are, and nothing about violent love ends cleanly.","src":"haiku","fable":"A killing gets reconstructed twice: first as a newspaper headline and a witness statement that file the scene as jealousy tipping into suicide, then, on a second pass, as a case reopened by details the first account skated past. A switchblade left in the dirt and a note found on the dead man turn a tidy verdict into a question about who struck first, and Dream Theater score that doubt with the record's whodunit engine, a progressive-metal detective story where every riff functions as another piece of evidence. Beneath the forensics sits the couple's real quarrel, a woman refusing a man who would not reform and a man who would rather end himself than surrender her, so the mystery is finally about how private ruin gets flattened into a two-line headline. The chorus insists conduct outlasts the body, that a person is simply the sum of what they did, which recasts the whole investigation as a moral ledger rather than a crime report.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","death & grief","love & desire"],"means":"private ruin flattened into a two-line headline and a moral ledger","built":"the killing is reconstructed twice, the second pass reopening the first verdict"},
"dream-theater~scene-nine-finally-free": {"haiku":"A murder-suicide narrative told through fragmented perspectives and hypnotic suggestion, where a man kills his lover to prevent her from leaving him, then turns the gun on himself. The song explores obsessive love and possession culminating in death as tragic resolution.","opus":"The climax of the Metropolis Pt. 2 concept album, a past-life regression revealing a murder-suicide love triangle across reincarnated souls, the killer's twisted sense of finally being free unraveling into the cycle's grim, repeating truth.","sonnet":"The closing chapter of a murder mystery told through multiple perspectives under hypnotic regression: Nicholas, who killed his rival and staged a suicide note, believes he's finally free — but the woman he loved, Victoria, was also shot. Both die; their souls rise and are reborn, the trauma of the past life released at last.","src":"haiku","fable":"Under regression hypnosis, Nicholas replays a 1928 killing and hears its two dead voices confess in turn: Edward, who cuts down his brother's lover rather than surrender her, and Victoria, already drifting back to the man she meant to leave him for. Dream Theater stages the murder as a duet where each side sings the identical refrain of release, so words that mean deliverance in one mouth mean possession in the other. A reporter's clip about American nobility drags the old tragedy into the patient's present, while the therapist's cue to wake collapses the two eras into a single instant. That instant snaps shut on a scream instead of a cure, exposing the calm guiding voice as the murderer wearing a healer's mask.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The closing news clip is a real broadcast on John F. Kennedy Jr.'s death — the member-of-American-nobility line — pinning the album’s present day to July 1999.","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","artifice & performance","death & grief"],"means":"a word of release meaning deliverance in one mouth and possession in another","built":"the calm guiding voice exposed as the murderer wearing a healer's mask"},
"dream-theater~scene-one-regression": {"haiku":"A hypnotic guided regression that walks through relaxation and entering a safe mental space, moving from conscious awareness into dream memory to recover lost past.","opus":"A hypnotist's soothing count guides a subject into a white light and backward through time, life unspooling like a film until a face named Victoria greets him. Dream Theater open their concept album Metropolis Pt. 2 with this regression, the calm therapeutic voice a deliberate threshold into the buried past the whole record will excavate scene by scene.","sonnet":"A guided hypnotic regression pulls a subject back through time into buried memory. Floating between safety and dissolution, the mind replays its own past on an inner screen — clarity arriving at the precise moment a name from long ago surfaces into consciousness.","src":"haiku","fable":"The album's overture stages its own method: a hypnotherapist's induction script hands the record its structural device, the past reachable only through a guided descent that the listener undergoes alongside the patient. What Nicholas claims as his is borrowed memory — a subconscious spinning back through time until his own life plays before him like footage on a screen, the self reframed as spectator rather than owner. The turn lands on his greeting to Victoria as a familiar friend, warmth that betrays the trap of regression, because the peace and safety the induction promised are exactly the conditions under which a buried and unfinished story reopens.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The Hypnotherapist is voiced by Terry Brown, Rush's longtime producer — and the light-and-safety stanza Nicholas sings here returns word for word in The Spirit Carries On: the album's consolation, planted in its first minute.","fableDeep":"The album's whole conceit is that its story can only be recovered from inside a trance, and this opener is Dream Theater making that method literal: a hypnotherapist's induction is the song, its measured countdown from ten doing the work most bands would hand to a riff. The move is quietly radical for a prog band famous for instrumental muscle — the record begins with almost no music, just a voice teaching a listener how to fall backward through time, and the promise that opening your eyes ends it turns the whole memory-play to come into something the protagonist can only witness, never touch. When Nicholas finally speaks, the vocabulary of therapy has become the vocabulary of possession: he narrates his own past life unspooling like film, and the era's fascination with regression and reincarnation-as-plot lands here as genuine dread rather than gimmick. That the scene resolves on a warm greeting to a woman he has never met in this life is the hook baited — recognition without memory is exactly the wound the rest of the record will pick at.","themes":["nostalgia & memory","transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming"],"means":"the self recast as spectator of its own history","built":"a therapist's induction script doubles as the album's structural device"},
"dream-theater~scene-seven-ii-one-last-time": {"haiku":"A person explores a house that holds clues to a mysterious tragedy, awakening childhood memories and witnessing disturbing scenes of emotional breakdown and infidelity.","opus":"Part of the band's Scenes from a Memory concept album, this tracks the narrator investigating a tragic death, standing inside a stranger's home as childhood dreams and borrowed memories awaken through his eyes. He glimpses a screaming woman and a man begging forgiveness. Dream Theater's cinematic prog stagecraft turns detective work into reincarnation mystery, evidence and vision merging.","sonnet":"Standing inside a dead man's house, a narrator discovers evidence that confirms a long-held childhood suspicion about a tragic story. Past and present merge through the rooms — someone else's memories animate through the investigator, and the ending that never made sense finally clicks.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seeker works a closed case that refuses to close: the official verdict names a tragedy, yet the physical proof leaves a gap no report accounts for, and rumors of one man's wealth only widen it. What pulls the song forward is the hypnotic sung refrain of surrender, a lovers' vow to lie down and dissolve together, that keeps cutting across the investigation and blurs whose voice is even speaking. When he steps through the dead man's house, the outsider's distance collapses; the rooms feel colder than the street, and someone else's screaming argument bleeds up through him as if the walls are remembering for him. What lands in that bedroom is not fresh information but old dread confirmed, the dawning sense that these belong to a woman whose life is surfacing behind his own eyes, the detective discovering he was never standing outside the story he came to solve.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","death & grief","violence & murder"],"means":"the investigator discovers he was never outside the story he came to solve","built":"lovers' refrain of surrender keeps cutting across the whodunit, blurring whose voice speaks"},
"dream-theater~scene-six-home": {"haiku":"A man torn between family and a woman he loves betrays his brother; emotional obsession and deception pull him homeward despite guilt, where the mystery of desire traps him inescapably.","opus":"From the Metropolis Pt. 2 story, a man pulled back toward a city of decadence and addiction he can't resist, betraying his own brother over a woman. Home as fatal compulsion, desire and self-destruction indistinguishable.","sonnet":"A man is pulled back to a city of decadence and addiction that he calls \"home\" — the real draw is a woman named Victoria who belongs to his brother, a doomed love triangle feeding his obsession.","src":"haiku","fable":"Three narrators, one addiction. The Sleeper voice pins its object to a fevered city that summons him like a chemical dependency he cannot detox from, the pull framed as clutch and habit rather than affection. The Miracle voice reframes that same compulsion as forbidden love, a man betraying his brother for a woman whose ecstasy overrides kinship while a knowing figure looks on. The Nicholas voice collapses both readings into a hypnotic subject chasing an unlocked past, exposing the whole triptych as a regression session where present obsession is really buried history speaking through masks. Dream Theater, writing a single continuous concept narrative, lets that ambiguity run the engine, so the repeated summons never once carries the same meaning twice.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"three narrators share one compulsion that never carries the same meaning twice","built":"triptych of voices lets each mask defer to the regression frame beneath it"},
"dream-theater~scene-three-i-through-my-words": {"haiku":"One person carries another's entire history within memory; their connection transcends physical separation through shared eternal consciousness.","opus":"A brief, intimate reflection on two people so deeply connected that one holds the whole of the other's experience in memory. Though they inhabit separate consciousnesses, they share a single fate, joined by a bond described as impossible to sever, a meditation on love that outlasts any parting.","sonnet":"A brief, intimate bridge about carrying a lost loved one forward through memory — everything they ever saw and said lives in the narrator's mind, and though they exist now in two separate states (living and dead), they are linked by an unbreakable shared thread.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hushed prelude to the album's central act of possession, where Nicholas the hypnotized narrator and Victoria the murdered girl he's regressing into stop being two people. The lyric works entirely on the grammar of merger — everything one has seen or heard is now etched in the other, and the pronouns dissolve until a single eternity is being lived across two minds, bound by a thread the song insists cannot break. Dream Theater use the quiet to do the concept-record's real work: this is the moment the past stops being something Nicholas investigates and becomes something that speaks through him, the séance turned inside out. The refusal to keep the two voices separate is the point, priming the listener for a truth that will only surface as the dead girl's memory overwrites the living man's.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"merger dissolves two lives until one eternity speaks through both","built":"pronouns dissolve as the séance turns inside out"},
"dream-theater~scene-three-ii-fatal-tragedy": {"haiku":"A person discovers the truth about a past murder and confronts the mystery of a dead woman named Victoria; they realize that understanding death requires accepting its nature as mere transition.","opus":"The Metropolis Pt. 2 protagonist, haunted by dreams and cries, is told by a stranger of a young girl named Victoria murdered long ago. The mystery of her death becomes his own quest, framed by the belief that death is not an end but a transition, hinting at reincarnation.","sonnet":"Investigating the unsolved murder of a young girl named Victoria, a narrator guided by a mysterious old man confronts the tragedy and is told the answer will only unfold through time — death here positioned not as an end but as a transition.","src":"haiku","fable":"The narrative engine of Scenes From A Memory turns over here: Nicholas, the seeker of the framing story, is led into the past life he's been dreaming, and the song stages that as an actual encounter — a stranger who names a murdered girl, Victoria, and then withdraws, refusing to hand over the answer. That withdrawal is the point. The chorus reduces the case to abstract absolutes (love, truth, faith, hope) precisely because the plot-level truth is being withheld, converting a whodunit into a spiritual precondition: you cannot arrive at what happened without first being changed enough to bear it. The closing lines reframe the whole album's morbid premise — that death is a transition, not an end — which is both the hypnotherapy conceit that lets a modern man witness a 1928 killing and Dream Theater's license to make a murder mystery double as a study of reincarnation and grief.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","violence & murder","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"a withheld truth converted into a spiritual precondition for bearing it","built":"abstract chorus absolutes stand in for the plot answer being denied"},
"dream-theater~scene-two-ii-strange-deja-vu": {"haiku":"Fractured consciousness and recurring dreams hint at parallel lives and hidden identities waiting to be discovered.","opus":"A repeating dream pulls the sleeper toward a familiar house and a young woman whose reflected face sharpens each night, hinting at an unspoken history and a wounded spirit. Part of a concept album, it frames reincarnation as a haunting, the narrator fated to recover a former existence that isn't entirely his own.","sonnet":"A man is haunted by recurring dreams of another life — a house, a room, a girl in a mirror — and wakes to find his real world feels foreign while the dream world feels familiar, driving him to search for the mysterious link between who he is and who he might have been in another existence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man surrenders to a recurring dream he half-welcomes, drifting each night toward a house he somehow already knows and a girl reflected in glass whose features keep sharpening into someone carrying a buried grief. The song tracks his slide across two levels of consciousness: sleep, where an unfinished second existence beckons him inward, and waking, where the neon city reasserts itself and the vision splinters into shards, leaving only the ache of a life that feels remembered rather than invented. Working the era's rock-opera machinery, Dream Theater frames this as past-life regression under hypnosis, the narrator convinced the reflected girl is a self he must recover, so the vertigo of losing hold of his own identity becomes the engine that drives him deeper into the search. The dramatic irony the wider concept will pay off runs the other way — the life he fears he is inventing turns out to be the one memory that was his all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The waking Metropolis answers the band’s own catalog: the record is the fan-demanded sequel to Metropolis Pt. 1 from Images and Words, whose Part I subtitle began as a joke.","themes":["identity & becoming","nostalgia & memory","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"a life remembered rather than invented pulls the narrator inward","built":"dramatic irony runs against the narrator — the feared invention was the real memory"},
"dream-theater~the-test-that-stumped-them-all": {"haiku":"Institutional psychology labels and confines; the speaker endures diagnosis and cure attempts that miss true complexity.","opus":"A psychiatric institution's cruelty told from both sides — the patient boxed in behind sterile plaster, and the doctors who flatten him into a case they won't discharge. Their talk of remedies and electric therapy exposes care soured into control.","sonnet":"A dual-perspective narrative about a person institutionalized for mental illness — from the patient's view they are trapped between walls being studied and prodded, while the doctors' interjections reveal their arrogance and failure to actually understand or help the person they've confined.","src":"haiku","fable":"A performer's electric high crashes into confinement, where clinicians who've decided he's delusional run him through screenings and medication yet keep coming up empty against whatever he actually is. Their frustration curdles into escalation: unable to discharge a case they can't crack, they reach for electroshock and dress the cruelty up as rescue.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","surveillance & control"],"means":"clinical failure escalates into cruelty dressed as rescue"},
"dream-theater~about-to-crash-reprise": {"haiku":"Euphoric ascension conceals underlying fragility; despite newfound strength, the speaker knows that falling remains inevitable and terminal.","opus":"A depiction of bipolar cycling, the manic surge of feeling invincible and euphoric colliding with the crash into meaningless sadness. The plea beneath the highs is simply to know someone will be standing by when he falls out of the sky.","sonnet":"A person in a manic episode feels invincible and euphoric, but underneath the high is crushing meaninglessness and the awareness that the crash is coming and no one will be there when it hits.","src":"haiku","fable":"The reprise brings back the earlier upswing wholesale — the invincibility, the racing pulse, the elation worn like armor against a despair that only just loosened its grip. Dream Theater set the returning phrases as a trap: the second half turns that same altitude into its danger, since the speaker knows the climb was never flight and that the sadness beneath the high would be invisible to anyone watching. It closes not on the fall but on a plea, asking whether anyone will still be standing there when the drop finally comes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the returning elation set as a trap the speaker now recognises","built":"the reprise recycles the earlier phrases so their altitude becomes the danger"},
"dream-theater~solitary-shell": {"haiku":"Autism or social withdrawal creates an impenetrable barrier; the narrator documents a man drifting between lucidity and delusional episodes, forever isolated.","opus":"From the Six Degrees suite, an unflinching study of mental illness across a life, a boy who withdraws into himself and grows into a man cycling through catatonia and mania. Empathy for someone locked inside his own mind.","sonnet":"A clinical-yet-empathetic portrait of a man whose withdrawal from the world deepened over decades from childhood oddness into something more dangerous, leaving him oscillating between catatonia and delusional episodes, writing furiously but spiraling further into his private shell.","src":"haiku","fable":"A clinical case history sung with unexpected tenderness: the withdrawn child who never wanted holding, the grown man filling pages for hours as his grip on the world loosens. The recurring insistence that he functioned normally apart from this lands as the wishful denial of relatives watching someone retreat somewhere they cannot follow. The shell is both the affliction and the only place he feels safe.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Petrucci's portrait, canonically of autism — the gentlest music in the suite given to its most inward character.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind"],"means":"the affliction and the only safe place are the same shell","built":"the relatives' denial framed as the wishful insistence that he functioned normally"},
"dream-theater~space-dye-vest":{"haiku":"Heartbreak and emotional void—the speaker cannot open again after losing someone, surrounded by fragmented dialogue and personal collapse.","opus":"Keyboardist Kevin Moore's farewell song about heartbreak and emotional shutdown, threaded with sampled television clips of ordinary damaged lives. It renders romance as a kind of wounding and the resolve never to be vulnerable again as real, permanent withdrawal.","sonnet":"A portrait of emotional shutdown after being left — the narrator buries grief by replacing intimacy with a new relationship kept safely on the page, and the song is threaded with found audio fragments of real people talking, emphasizing that the pain of being unloved is ordinary and everywhere.","src":"haiku","fable":"Kevin Moore's parting gift to Dream Theater is a study in loving an image instead of a person, and the whole song turns on that ache. It opens with a fantasy stitched from a passerby's knitwear and a future projected onto someone barely glimpsed, the way infatuation prefers a surface it can decorate over any living body that thinks and grieves. Moore then cuts in a film plea from a man begging that his beloved be left a real, feeling human rather than an ornament to be owned and displayed, which lands as accusation: the narrator has become exactly that collector, consoling himself after a breakup by adoring a face flattened into a magazine that can never leave or disappoint him. Threaded through with recorded scraps of ordinary strangers stalling and failing, it builds to a bridge where blame, sanity, and purpose all dissolve, and the closing vow is not defiance but a quiet shutting of the door, a promise to counterfeit warmth and smile while never again risking exposure. It is one of prog's saddest farewells precisely because the emotional retreat it describes doubles as Moore's own goodbye to the band.","fv":2,"fnote":"Dream Theater refused to play it for twenty years — it was Moore’s song and he was gone — until the Awake anniversary tour of 2014 finally broke the seal.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance"],"means":"consoling himself by loving an image that cannot leave or disappoint","built":"sampled voice fragments of ordinary strangers failing thread the narrator's emotional retreat"},
"dream-theater~status-seeker": {"haiku":"Someone judges others for superficiality while being complicit in the same materialism, unable to escape the cycle of false values.","opus":"A pointed takedown of a name-dropping social climber who slaps a price on everything and swaps convictions on a whim while sitting in smug judgment of the narrator. A garden image, where grasping for gain crushes the overlooked, lays bare ambition's cost. The prog-metal band delivers this rare character sketch with tight, direct punch as the narrator finally walks off.","sonnet":"Self-indictment alongside accusation — the speaker recognizes the status seeker in themselves as much as in the target, confessing they also favored the profitable few and trampled those who didn't serve their ambitions. The song is a reckoning rather than pure attack, and the turning away at the end is from someone the speaker can no longer pretend to believe in.","src":"haiku","fable":"A takedown of the person who prices everything, sung by Charlie Dominici in a register closer to hard rock than the labyrinthine Dream Theater to come: the accuser weighs the singer against a market value and finds him wanting, so the song throws the moral question back at whoever anointed themselves to sit in judgment. The pivot lands in the second verse, where the singer stops indicting and confesses the same sin, tending only the seedlings that thrived in the light while crushing the weaker ones underfoot to reach his own reward. That admission is what earns the closing retreat: having caught himself chasing the same profit, he walks off from someone still circling the same emptiness.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"self-indictment collapsing the accuser into the accused","built":"second verse pivots from accusation to mirrored confession"},
"dream-theater~surrender-to-reason": {"haiku":"Spiritual and emotional healing comes through accepting sacrifice, embracing grace, and surrendering to wisdom beyond rational control.","opus":"A searching, spiritual piece from the American prog-metal band, sung by James LaBrie as hard-won acceptance. It argues that pain and loss, viewed with reflection and time, eventually resolve into understanding, and that letting compassion guide you means yielding to what you cannot control rather than fighting it. The reward is peace, framed as growth through sacrifice.","sonnet":"A meditation on pain as the gateway to growth — heart and mind colliding in sacrifice, memories swimming into clarity, puzzle pieces clicking into place. What was struggled against becomes something embraced; grace and love lead forward through the boundaries, and the appeal to restless angels is really an appeal to the wiser self to find the way.","src":"haiku","fable":"A power ballad that stages surrender not as defeat but as the intellect finally consenting to feelings it had been at war with, the speaker moving from an inner collision toward a truce he chooses rather than loses. The lyric leans on water almost everywhere — a current smoothing a rock over time, memory adrift on an ocean, a spirit carried like wind and wave — so that reasoned acceptance arrives the way erosion does, gradually and without force. Only in the closing plea, where he calls on wavering guardian figures to point him toward strength and love, does the song admit it needs something outside its own deliberation to finish the peace it has been arguing itself into.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming"],"means":"the intellect choosing truce with feeling rather than losing to it","built":"water imagery throughout enacts erosion as the model for reasoned acceptance"},
"dream-theater~surrounded": {"haiku":"A reclusive person emerges from hiding to seek light and connection after years in darkness, learning to accept help from another and discovering transformation in vulnerability.","opus":"An early Dream Theater piece, it moves from a narrator hiding in shadow and dreading long nights toward a mystical vision of a dancing woman, stained glass shattering, and light finally surrounding him. Walking away feels easier than facing pain, yet he chooses to be found. The band's dynamic build mirrors the passage from isolation into a hard-won, almost sacred illumination.","sonnet":"A long dark night of the soul resolves when a woman's voice calls through an ivory tower window and light floods in. The narrator has hidden in shadow, but morning and her call undo the long isolation — blindness replaces sight, but not as loss: as surrender to something larger.","src":"haiku","fable":"It opens on a man who has run out of places to hide, then follows him out of paralysis and toward a female presence he can only meet after dark, a she made of the very glow he has been avoiding. The turn is a genuine surrender rather than a rescue: he stops resisting, lets the pane between them fall to pieces, and accepts a blindness that finally reads as belonging instead of loss. James LaBrie sings the whole descent as a hymn while the band builds a slow tidal swell, so the arrival at that flooded, encircling calm feels earned by the climb rather than merely announced.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","love & desire","night & the city"],"means":"surrender to encircling dark reframed as belonging","built":"the band's tidal swell performs the earned arrival the lyric only describes"},
"dream-theater~take-away-my-pain": {"haiku":"Loss and separation devastate people; a father leaves, fame abandons, and the narrator faces loneliness with fading hope for reconnection.","opus":"A son sits at his dying father's bedside, the loss of heroes like Gene Kelly bleeding into the larger plea to have his pain taken away. Dream Theater's James LaBrie wrote this for his own father, and the lyric moves through an actor's final bow toward a reflection in the water, the son at last learning to survive without the parent gone.","sonnet":"Grief at a loved one's terminal departure, navigated beside a hospital bed. Heroes and promises are stripped away; what remains is exhaustion, longing for comfort, and finally a tentative acceptance — the pain absorbed rather than escaped, the door left open for what comes after.","src":"haiku","fable":"A child keeps vigil at a dying father's bedside, and the two of them reach for the movie star fading from the newspaper as a way to voice the unsayable, so the plea to be spared what is coming folds into the wish that a screen hero's last bow could stand in for a parent's. Each verse hands the same bedside vantage to someone else until the mourner finds his own face in the water and mistakes grief for the father still calling his name. LaBrie sings the refrain almost tenderly over the band's plush late-nineties ballad arrangement, so when the closing variation turns every earlier refusal into acceptance, learning to go on alone, that softness is what makes it register as surrender rather than solace.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Petrucci’s father died in 1996, and the Gene Kelly headline dates the bedside scene — Kelly died that February, the same paper open on the bed.","themes":["death & grief","family & blood"],"means":"a celebrity's last bow asked to stand in for a father's dying","built":"closing variation turns every refusal into acceptance through the same tender arrangement"},
"dream-theater~take-the-time": {"haiku":"After repeated disappointments, a person reassesses their life path and resolves to change course, finding clarity through introspection rather than external salvation.","opus":"An early Dream Theater call to stop and take stock after broken promises and rough setbacks, the speaker deciding to gather himself and begin again from scratch. Its central claim is that whatever answers you need are already within you rather than handed down from any higher authority, a whispered foreign-language aside underscoring that inner sight beats outside assurances.","sonnet":"A man at the edge of exhaustion and self-deception calls for a full stop — a moment of radical inward listening after years of chasing promises that dissolved. The argument is that no external voice or divine intervention will save him; everything needed already exists in the quieted mind, if he will only look there.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man worn down by his own failures talks himself toward starting over, and the song is that private pep talk caught mid-collapse, moving from exhaustion into a brittle resolve. Its governing image is water: the swell that either drowns or ferries him, then the flood he deliberately lets rise until, going under, he insists he sees more sharply than he ever did with open eyes. That paradox of sight-through-blindness gets sealed in the whispered Italian line at the close, where losing the eyes becomes the very condition of finally seeing. The counsel stays defiantly interior — nothing above answers, no other person arrives to rescue him — so the only remedy left is to shut the eyes and trust that whatever is needed already waits inside the mind.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The whispered Italian close is borrowed from Cinema Paradiso — now that I have lost my sight, I see more — Tornatore’s film handing the song its blindness-as-vision key.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"submersion offered as the route to clarity","built":"an Italian whisper seals the paradox of sight-through-blindness"},
"dream-theater~the-alien":{"haiku":"Humanity's evolution into a multi-planetary digital species represents transcendence and expansion beyond Earth's constraints.","opus":"A science-fiction epic of humanity abandoning a dying planet aboard the first interplanetary mission to remake a fresh world, its sacred goal reimagined as sheer survival and transformation into a scattered, digital, multi-world species. Dream Theater, the American progressive-metal band, earned a Grammy for this pandemic-era track, James LaBrie narrating the launch sequence over the band's dense technical drive.","sonnet":"Humanity's decision to leave Earth and become a multi-planetary species — the countdown, the departure, the redefinition of purpose as the holy grail of survival beyond a dying planet. The speaker becomes the alien, transformed by the voyage, searching the stars until a better world is found for everyone left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~the-answer":{"haiku":"A reluctant prophet or messiah figure discovers people believe in them, creating anxiety about whether they're truly the one destined to lead.","opus":"A brief, vulnerable moment of doubt from an unwilling savior figure who never claimed to hold any solution, yet finds crowds gathering in the belief that he can liberate them, which leaves him lonelier than ever and dreading what's ahead. The prog-metal band places it early in a concept album, LaBrie voicing a chosen one's fear before he takes the stage of destiny.","sonnet":"A reluctant messiah standing in a park surrounded by believers who see something in him he can't see in himself. He's never claimed to have the key, yet they're placing their faith there anyway, and the weight of that expectation — being someone's answer when you're still full of your own doubt — is almost too much to hold.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~the-answer-lies-within": {"haiku":"The song encourages self-reliance and facing each day with purpose, trusting internal wisdom and making deliberate choices over passive doubt.","opus":"A gentle, direct message of reassurance to someone lost in doubt, urging them not to waste days in uncertainty because the answer they seek is already inside them; the counsel is to learn from mistakes, own your choices, give of yourself, and trust that the future is on your side.","sonnet":"A gentle, direct encouragement to stop doubting and start living — the answer to whatever is weighing on you is already inside you, and the only mistake is letting days pass in hesitation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rare unguarded moment from a band better known for labyrinthine suites: an older voice steadying a younger one, insisting the guidance being sought is already housed inside the listener rather than waiting to be handed down. It presses against hesitation directly, treating each passing morning as a chance not to be squandered to indecision, and asks its subject to meet the world unflinching and to give outward instead of hoarding what they feel.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Petrucci has said he wrote it for his children.","themes":["identity & becoming","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"guidance sought outside declared already housed within the listener","built":"an older voice steadying a younger one marks a rare unguarded register for this band"},
"dream-theater~the-best-of-times": {"haiku":"A son grieves his father's death after decades together; nostalgia mixes with loss as he honors their shared life and seeks gratitude amid pain.","opus":"A grieving son's tribute to his late father, his closest friend and hero, recalling shared years before the phone call that delivered a terminal diagnosis. Sorrowful yet thankful, it repays a lifetime of unconditional love with a promise that the father's spirit still guides him daily.","sonnet":"A tribute to a deceased father — the narrator looks back on a close, cherished relationship built on shared rituals, radio mornings, record stores, and long phone calls, then mourns the phone call that ended it all and vows to carry his father's spirit forward.","src":"haiku","fable":"A son's elegy for his father, built as a memory reel: the specifics accumulate as tender inventory — a shared apartment number, a beloved cult film watched together, a childhood ballfield — the texture of one bond rendered in ordinary weekday detail rather than grand statement. Then a phone call snaps the frame, and the language turns from cataloguing the past to bargaining with mortality, the softness of nostalgia hardening into a directive to seize whatever hours remain. What lifts it above sentiment is the turn in the closing stretch, where grief stops describing loss and becomes direct address, the singer speaking to the departed and answering his own devastation with a fragile promise to endure. Written by Mike Portnoy for his own dying father, the song earns its length by moving through the full arc of a bereavement — reminiscence, dread, gratitude, and the wounded resolve to carry on — rather than fixing on any single note of it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Howard Portnoy died in January 2009, during the making of the record — his son recorded a version singing it himself and played it at his bedside before the end.","themes":["death & grief","nostalgia & memory","family & blood"],"means":"ordinary weekday detail accumulating into the full arc of a bereavement","built":"grief shifts from third-person inventory to direct address in the closing stretch"},
"dream-theater~the-big-medley": {"haiku":"A medley mixing theatrical vignettes: witnessing a cold performance, emotional betrayal and infidelity, romantic turmoil, and obsessive devotion.","opus":"The prog-metal band stitches together famous covers, weaving fragments of Pink Floyd, Queen, and Journey into one suite. The connecting thread is romantic betrayal and the itch to flee, each borrowed piece feeding a larger reflection on being wrecked while a partner strays, closing with a Genesis lift.","sonnet":"A concert medley stitches together classic rock fragments — disillusionment behind a stage spectacle, romantic betrayal in cycles of jealousy and payback, a restless energy that keeps switching back on. The assembly is celebratory rather than thematic, each piece a recognisable emotional station without a unifying argument between them.","src":"haiku","fable":"What the medley is about is a band drawing its own family tree in public. The songs are not chosen at random: they trace a lineage from the theatrical prog-rock the band descends from — the confrontational address that opens a spectacle and dares the audience to see past the mask, the anthem about a wanderer told to stop searching, the operatic suite that never resolves the same way twice — through the harmony-soul heartbreak of a different American arena tradition, and out into a compact British art-rock coda that just wants the machine switched back on. The joining is the argument. By welding a mask-and-spotlight overture to a plea for release, a lament of being pulled to pieces by a lover's betrayal to a chant that treats music itself as a device you flip on again, the performers assemble a portrait of their own influences as a single continuous voice, each borrowed section chosen for a hinge — spectacle into confession, confession into the simple compulsion to keep playing. The medley reads as a young virtuoso group naming its parents by playing them, and the meaning lives in the seams: what these particular songs, side by side, say is that the band understands itself as the sum of everything it can quote from memory and stitch without a break.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The six, in order: Pink Floyd’s In the Flesh?, Kansas’s Carry On Wayward Son, Queen’s Bohemian Rhapsody, Journey’s Lovin’ Touchin’ Squeezin’, Dixie Dregs’ Cruise Control, Genesis’s Turn It On Again.","themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"a band naming its parents by playing them without a break","built":"borrowed sections chosen for their hinges — spectacle into confession into the compulsion to keep playing"},
"dream-theater~the-bigger-picture": {"haiku":"Someone struggles to see beyond immediate suffering and failures to understand larger meaning, seeking guidance while confronting the pain that accompanies self-awareness.","opus":"Dream Theater turn a sea voyage into a metaphor for faith and endurance, the narrator following a difficult, dimly lit path toward some distant hope. Drawn helplessly toward what may burn him, bearing his burden alone and carried along by forces beyond his control, he chooses the risk of failing over the safety of never trying.","sonnet":"A pilgrim navigating uncertainty pleads for a guiding light — not divine rescue but illumination enough to see the larger pattern behind his errors and shipwrecks. He insists it is better to attempt and fail than never to try, even as he catalogues the emotional wreckage of those who stopped believing entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a pilgrim's prayer dressed as arena metal, the narrator begging some external radiance to lend him sight while he gropes forward by candlelight, mistrusting his own perception at every fork in the road. The genius is how the plea curdles midway: the fourth verse turns the accusation outward into a litany of paralyzed senses, a person who looks without perceiving and breathes without living, so the request for guidance doubles as an indictment of everyone spiritually numb around him. James LaBrie delivers it as devotional supplication rather than grievance, and the payoff lands only in the closing breath, when a glow finally resolves on the horizon and the traveler recognizes it not as an answer but as the sign of arrival.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a plea for sight that curdles midway into an indictment of the numb","built":"the fourth verse turns the prayer outward before the glow resolves at the close"},
"dream-theater~the-count-of-tuscany": {"haiku":"A traveler accepts an invitation from an eccentric nobleman who leads them to a remote estate where the true horror unfolds: they are the prey in an ancient ritual of cannibalism and torture.","opus":"A narrative horror ballad in which the narrator is taken by an eccentric young Italian count to a remote estate, growing convinced he's about to be murdered in a cellar of wine-barrel tombs; the twist is that his terror was misplaced projection, the count merely eccentric and hospitable, a parable about fear born of paranoia.","sonnet":"A prog epic narrating a real encounter on a Tuscan road trip — a stranger drives the narrator to a remote estate where his bearded historian brother leads them to a secret chapel and wine cellar, and the narrator becomes genuinely terrified he will be killed, before the host reassures him this is simply how they live.","src":"haiku","fable":"A guided drive into the Tuscan hills curdles, in the narrator's head, into a horror film: every genteel detail his aristocratic host offers gets reread as a predator's grooming, and by the chorus the traveler is certain he will not leave alive, pleading for one more chance to explain himself. The turn is that the dread was pure projection, and Dream Theater builds the whole movement toward it — the outro finally hands the microphone to the wounded Count, who insists this is only how his people live, that the shrine and the cellar and the old war tales are just family history handed down. That structural reversal is the point: the horror and its debunking are the same material, and the song's real subject is the distance between what a nervous guest is actually shown and the monster his own imagination manufactures from ordinary hospitality.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The Count is real — Sebastiano Capponi, met on tour, whose historian brother Niccolò consulted on Thomas Harris’s Hannibal and appears in its world: the fictional cannibal curates his family’s library. The lyric’s cannibal-curator line points at him.","themes":["madness & the mind","artifice & performance"],"means":"imagined predation debunked by handing the microphone to the accused host","built":"structural reversal gives the Count the closing statement, collapsing the horror"},
"dream-theater~the-dark-eternal-night": {"haiku":"A prophetic figure emerges from cosmic chaos bringing apocalyptic destruction, and the speaker spirals through nightmarish dimensions and ancient malevolence.","opus":"A horror narrative about rousing an ancient monstrous evil, narrated in part by a being claiming to be the final deity of a decaying world, as the listener is pulled through ruins and lifeless realms into an unending hellish dark.","sonnet":"A Lovecraftian descent: a figure born of pharaonic blood rises as a monstrous last prophet, drawing the narrator through ancient warnings, choking rooms, grotesque creatures, and dead worlds until everything vanishes into an eternal hellish dark.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dream Theater weaponize pulp horror here as a vehicle for total cosmic dissolution, letting a scholar-adventurer's hunger to witness the forbidden collapse the wall between explorer and the thing he came to find. The lyric plays two image-families off each other: a fevered seeker pressing deeper under a sickly moon toward a chamber no one else will name, and a pharaonic destroyer who declares himself the final entropy of a spent universe. What makes the song work is that these never resolve into separate characters, so by the midpoint you cannot tell whether the voice is the prey or the apocalypse it chased. The band fuses Lovecraftian dread with Egyptian death-cult iconography inside their most deliberately unhinged, riff-serrated writing of the Systematic Chaos years, and that refusal to settle who narrates is the whole design — appetite and annihilation are exposed as one and the same craving, a gravity the piece circles without ever climbing free of.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Built on Lovecraft’s prose poem Nyarlathotep — the pharaonic prophet risen out of Egypt whose exhibitions drive crowds mad.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"appetite and annihilation exposed as the same craving","built":"seeker and apocalypse never resolve into separate characters"},
"dream-theater~the-enemy-inside":{"haiku":"Confronts inescapable trauma and regret that haunts the narrator's mind, creating a prison of suffocating memories. The song portrays the self as both the source and victim of psychological torment.","opus":"A depiction of trauma and inner torment, the narrator reliving one painful moment repeatedly, plagued by intrusive memories and brutal nightmares he cannot flee. He describes severing himself from everyone, sinking into remorse and groundless dread as the enemy within stalks him.","sonnet":"A person is haunted by intrusive memories and violent flashbacks that they cannot escape — the \"enemy inside\" is a psychological torment, described as suffocating and inescapable, that has severed them from the world and from the life they once had.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of post-traumatic stress told from inside the loop, where the thing being fled is the mind that does the fleeing. Dream Theater build the chase as a paradox: you cannot outrun a pursuer stitched into your own memory, so every chorus circles back to the same trapped ground rather than advancing, the repetition itself enacting the flashback it describes. The verses map a slow bodily siege—wounds sealed under intact skin, a crushing weight on the chest, the panicked plea not to be dying—so the panic reads as physiological and involuntary, not weakness. By the bridge the speaker has collapsed into self-indictment, locked in a cell built from what he cannot undo, and the song's one flat mercy is that no swallowed cure can rebuild the paradise that ended; the honesty is in refusing an easy fix.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression"],"means":"the pursuer stitched into memory cannot be outrun by the mind doing the fleeing","built":"the chorus circles back rather than advancing, enacting the flashback it describes","fnote":"Written music-first, then given the subject Petrucci said the track was crying out for — PTSD in returning veterans, machine-gun riffs built to make you anxious. The video partnered with the veteran charity Save a Warrior."},
"dream-theater~the-glass-prison": {"haiku":"Someone imprisoned by addiction or obsession seeks recovery and redemption through spiritual awakening and the help of others.","opus":"The first installment of the band's twelve-step recovery suite, told from inside active addiction as a form of self-imprisonment and secret escape. Drawing directly on the language of Alcoholics Anonymous, it charts the passage from total defeat toward surrender and honesty, with the addict pleading for help from a strength beyond his own.","sonnet":"An autobiographical account of alcoholism and recovery structured in three movements — the descent into addiction, the desperate surrender, and the gradual rebuilding of sanity through the twelve steps, arriving at serenity and humility.","src":"haiku","fable":"Three movements trace one man's climb out of alcoholism: first the blackout bottom where the craving wins and he shutters himself in a secret, shameful refuge that doubles as a cell, then a crawl toward a recovery fellowship whose voices insist he is no different from them, then a locked door that swings open only once he stops forcing it and surrenders his own will. Mike Portnoy wrote it from inside his own drinking, and the band builds the arc on twelve-step language — the friend he can no longer keep at bay is the drink itself, salvation arriving not through muscle but through handing his will over. It plays as confession set to prog, the metallic ascent and its sudden hymnal calm mirroring the fall and the reprieve.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The opening chapter of the Twelve-Step Suite — steps one through three as the movements Reflection, Restoration and Revelation, dedicated to Bill W. and his friends; the lyric's monster-adjectives are the Big Book's own words for alcohol.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","faith & the occult"],"means":"surrender of will is the only door that opens","built":"the metallic ascent giving way to sudden hymnal calm mirrors the fall and the reprieve"},
"dream-theater~the-great-debate": {"haiku":"The ethical debate over embryonic stem cell research unfolds through opposing voices, balancing medical hope against moral objections about human life.","opus":"Assembled from news clips and clashing voices, the track lays out the moral battle over harvesting cells from human embryos for medicine, weighing the hope of cures and restored mobility against pro-life objections and doubts about the cost of getting there.","sonnet":"A direct staging of the public argument over embryonic stem cell research — intercutting actual-sounding broadcast voices from both the pro-science and pro-life sides — and then stepping into the moral terrain of the debate itself, asking whether the potential to cure suffering justifies using developing human embryos.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dream Theater stage the fight over using frozen fertility-clinic embryos for medical research as an actual debate: recorded news and talk-radio voices bracket the band at both ends, so the verses sit inside an argument that opens and closes without a verdict. The music then takes each side's language at full strength — the hope of a severed spine made whole against the standing of four-day-frozen cells bound for disposal anyway — and lets the two vocabularies collide rather than resolve. The recurring plea to turn toward the cure reads at first as a straight endorsement, but the band keeps hanging its central question over it, whether one existence may be ended to rescue another, so that gesture stays double-edged. That withholding is the point: by handing the spoken debaters the final word instead of the chorus, the song makes the listener finish the argument it deliberately leaves open.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The neutrality is engineered into the stereo field — Petrucci fed the pro arguments through one speaker and the con through the other, bracketed by real broadcast audio from the week of Bush's August 2001 funding decision.","themes":["politics & society"],"means":"the recurring plea for the cure kept double-edged by the hanging question","built":"the spoken debaters get the final word instead of the chorus, leaving the argument open"},
"dream-theater~the-killing-hand": {"haiku":"A narrative spanning centuries revealing a dark master manipulating events and controlling society through supernatural or political power.","opus":"An ambitious narrative epic of war, death and time travel, following a fallen soul cleansed in the sea who journeys back to confront the hidden king controlling the killing hand that caused so much mourning. As a prophet given a choice, he stops the slaughter but returns wondering what it cost and finally claims the killing hand himself. Dream Theater, the American progressive metal band, unfold this early multi-section saga with cinematic sweep.","sonnet":"A time-travel moral fable — a man sent back through history to find the source of a great killing, submerging into cold, traveling through devastated landscapes, eventually confronting the force behind the destruction. The twist is existential: having stopped the killing hand, he discovers his own name on the wall of the dead, and ends up laughing at what he's become.","src":"haiku","fable":"A five-part suite that opens at a war memorial, where a wall of the dead frames a question with no comfortable answer: who set this slaughter in motion. The middle movements sink a soul beneath the sea to be remade and sent back as a prophet against the tyrant orchestrating the carnage, and Dominici's clean, theatrical delivery keeps the mythology legible rather than letting it dissolve into abstraction. The devastating turn arrives in the finale, when the returning avenger reads a fresh name on that same wall and laughs, having become the very murdering figure he crossed over to destroy. The prophecy loops shut: ending the atrocity required inheriting it, and the cycle simply chose a new host.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder","faith & the occult"],"means":"the avenger inheriting the atrocity he crossed over to end","built":"five-part suite closing the loop at the same memorial wall it opened"},
"dream-theater~the-looking-glass": {"haiku":"The song critiques the seductive trap of fame and celebrity, where individuals become obsessed with status and recognition at the cost of genuine living.","opus":"A critique aimed at someone consumed by the pursuit of celebrity, mistaking recognition for worth and preening for an audience while their moment quietly expires. The band observes this hunger for the spotlight from a wry distance, treating fame-chasing as a kind of vanity that isolates rather than fulfills.","sonnet":"A critique of shallow fame-seeking: someone caught up in self-promotion and borrowed celebrity is described as glorifying stardom, imitating fame, and standing on the sidelines watching themselves through a distorting glass rather than engaging with real life.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of someone in orbit around their own image, sung entirely in the second person so the accusation never lands on a name — the target could be a celebrity, a rival, or the singer's own reflection staring back through the pane. The verses grant a strange sympathy first, conceding it is understandable to crave altitude over anonymity, before the choruses turn cold and watch this figure prospect for riches in a mere reputation while never stepping into the arena, only loitering at its edge. Andy Warhol's exhausted quarter-hour of fame gets summoned and mocked as already spent, and the recurring pull of gravity frames the whole thing as a body that cannot break free of the mass of its own self-regard. The reprise is where the diagnosis hardens: the same pre-chorus that once described a hunger for spotlight returns with the craving recast as an unraveling, vanity curdling from vice into something closer to sickness.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming","madness & the mind"],"means":"vanity diagnosed moving from vice toward something closer to sickness","built":"second-person address keeps the accusation without ever landing on a name"},
"dream-theater~the-ministry-of-lost-souls": {"haiku":"A spirit protector reaches across death to free a lost soul trapped between worlds. The song depicts rescue and redemption through sacrifice and unwavering loyalty beyond death.","opus":"A ghost story of a woman who died but cannot pass on, frozen and afraid to accept help from the spirit of the man who once rescued her, while he resolves to free her even at the cost of her final release. Dream Theater stretch this tale of sacrifice and devotion across a long progressive suite that builds toward a mournful, tender resolution.","sonnet":"A suicidal woman at the water's edge cannot reach the spirit of someone who died trying to save her — a figure who sacrificed himself so she could live. Their dialogue across the boundary of death circles guilt, failed rescue, and the weight of a life given in vain. Finally he releases her, urging her back toward the light she has refused.","src":"haiku","fable":"A guardian who once died shielding a girl now stands on the far shore of the afterlife, calling to her lingering spirit as she stalls at the boundary between worlds, too frightened to follow him across. The song moves as a dialogue of the dead: she was the one fated to pass on that night yet clings to a half-existence, while he — the soul who absorbed the fatal blow so she could keep breathing — pleads with her to stop mistaking survival for punishment. What sounds at first like a rescuer begging someone to live inverts by the close, because the salvation he offers is passage, not preservation; the mercy is escorting her out of an existence that has curdled into mere lingering. When he finally reaches through the divide and unbinds her from this life, the gesture completes his old sacrifice: the one who saved her body long ago now delivers her soul, insisting the threshold ahead is not annihilation but the paradise she keeps refusing to face.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","freedom & escape"],"means":"mercy reframed as escorting a spirit out of mere lingering","built":"the rescuer's offer inverts — salvation is passage, not preservation"},
"dream-theater~the-mirror": {"haiku":"A reckoning with personal betrayal and hypocrisy; the narrator confronts both external temptation and internal failure, realizing he must face his own reflection and make amends.","opus":"A narrator battling temptation and eroding willpower rails against a hypocrite, only for the charge to swing back on himself when the glass reveals the traitor is him. The band's Awake-era track stages addiction and infidelity as confrontation, spoken asides insisting some things lie beyond control, until his vow to reform is aimed at his own reflection.","sonnet":"A man confronts his own hypocrisy and self-betrayal — seduced by temptation while preaching self-control, deceiving the person he most owed honesty. The reckoning demands he face his reflection directly and make amends to the one he wronged most.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession disguised as an accusation: the singer rages at a treacherous partner for lying and pretending, demanding amends and swearing to withdraw all faith, before the turn reveals the addressed betrayer is himself. Dream Theater builds the reveal architecturally, the film samples acting as a chorus of outside voices — Streep's bewildered question, Irons admitting he behaved badly and cannot govern a life he assumed was his to steer — that pry the truth loose before the lyric can. The two families of language quietly trade places: the pronoun you, first flung outward as blame, curdles into the reflected face the accuser must finally address, and every charge of dishonesty rebounds onto the one who filed it. By the closing lines the grammar has fully inverted, betrayed becoming betrayer, so the whole arc reads as the moment addiction or self-deception stops projecting outward and admits ownership of the wreckage.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"blame flung outward until it rebounds onto the one who filed it","built":"film samples pry the truth loose before the lyric can manage it"},
"dream-theater~the-ones-who-help-to-set-the-sun": {"haiku":"A speaker recounts a moment of crisis—driving alone at night—and grapples with questions of whether they escaped death or whether they truly died and returned.","opus":"The prog band stages a disorienting near-death drive, streetlights leading the mind astray as the narrator loses track of whether he has already died. Racing through rain toward someone he cannot reach, with help always arriving too late, its restless, hallucinatory structure blurs mortality, memory and motion into an unresolved reckoning.","sonnet":"A dissociative near-death experience on a rainy road where the boundary between life and leaving blurs completely. A beloved person at home functions as an immune system — the narrator cannot tell if he returned or never left, but the commitment to come back remains the only fixed point.","src":"haiku","fable":"A night drive dissolves into catastrophe told from a strange remove: the narrator grips a wheel through rain and disorienting lamplight, loses hold of his senses, then seems to float free and watch the wreck consume itself from outside, palms streaking a windshield he can no longer reach through. The uncertainty is the whole engine here, since he cannot tell whether he has returned from death or never truly departed, and the recurring passage turns that private disaster cosmic, casting the survivors as figures who arrive perpetually too late and who, past some vanished threshold, now hasten a dying light rather than merely witness it. On a debut carried by Charlie Dominici's theatrical delivery, Dream Theater treat a fatal accident less as event than as a suspended metaphysical vantage.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"fatal accident suspended as a metaphysical vantage point","built":"narrator floating outside the wreck unable to tell departure from return"},
"dream-theater~the-root-of-all-evil": {"haiku":"A person catalogues their seven deadly sins and psychological torments, seeking redemption and freedom from the desires and fears that control them.","opus":"The second chapter of the band's recovery cycle catalogues each of the seven deadly sins as forces that have governed the addict's existence, with dependency itself named as the underlying corruption. Framed on the steps of recovery, it moves from that reckoning toward humility and a plea for the strength needed to break free.","sonnet":"A fearless moral inventory of the speaker's vices — pride, greed, lust, anger, sloth, envy — framed as a surrender to higher power and a request for help breaking free from the self-destructive cycle they recognize but cannot escape alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession that reads like a diagnosis, tallying pride, greed, envy and sloth not as sins to defend but as symptoms of a single appetite that has quietly run everything. The two-movement design carries the meaning: the first half braces to hand it all over, then the music tightens into the second, where dread clamps the throat and indignation coils toward detonation. What surfaces is a plea to be emptied out, since the confessor no longer trusts his own hands to loosen the grip that holds him, and readiness turns out to mean welcoming his own collapse rather than fearing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Movements six and seven of Portnoy's Twelve-Step Suite — the steps named Ready and Remove, continuing The Glass Prison and This Dying Soul across the albums of his documented sobriety.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"readiness for collapse welcomed over fearing it","built":"the two-movement structure braces to surrender then tightens into dread"},
"dream-theater~the-rover-achilles-last-stand": {"haiku":"Adventurous youth escaping commitment to seek dreams and freedom, dancing recklessly against all odds and expectations.","opus":"A medley of two Led Zeppelin voyager anthems, wanderlust and quest fused into one, shackles of commitment falling away for the pull of the open road and the dream half-glimpsed and never quite seen.","sonnet":"A cover medley about the joy of setting out — the world opens up on an April morning, the shackles of ordinary life fall away, and the song is pure open-road wanderlust; it's about the spirit of adventure, the ecstasy of movement toward something undefined.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dream Theater's argument lives in what they chose to weld together: three Zeppelin cuts that each chase a restless horizon, bolted end to end so the seams become the point. The Rover pleads for a wide human clasp against a world burning its candle low; Achilles Last Stand answers with the doomed pilgrim who shoulders Atlas's burden and swears he can read the road ahead. Set beside each other, the medley trades a communal wish for a solitary vow, then dissolves both into the final creed that wherever the traveler lands — bright coastlines, distant monsoon country, an island at nightfall — the underlying song never changes. The joining reads as tribute performed as thesis: the band assembles a private map of longing and homecoming from another group's material, letting the transitions carry meaning the separate texts can't hold. The distance covered is exhilarating; the destination is quietly revealed to be the same ground under every sky.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","artifice & performance","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"a communal wish traded for a solitary vow, both resolving to the same ground","built":"the seams between welded cuts carry meaning the separate texts cannot hold alone"},
"dream-theater~the-shattered-fortress": {"haiku":"Through spiritual growth and self-forgiveness, a person dismantles their rigid self-imprisonment and learns that righteousness yields to compassion and humility.","opus":"A recovery anthem built on the Twelve Steps and the Prayer of St. Francis, dedicated to AA co-founder Bill W. The narrator dismantles the crumbled ego that once imprisoned him, choosing calm over pride, humility over self-importance, and extending help to anyone who asks.","sonnet":"A twelve-step recovery anthem — the fortress of addiction and self-righteousness shatters as the narrator embraces humility, service, forgiveness, and willingness; the piece is dedicated to Bill W., co-founder of Alcoholics Anonymous.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is the closing panel of the Twelve-Step Suite that Mike Portnoy wrote across several albums about getting sober, and it works by turning the program's late steps into song architecture: the verses recite the principles a person practices to stay clean, the chorus stares into a mirror where the old defensive self has finally crumbled, and the bridge lifts the Prayer of Saint Francis almost line for line. That climb is the meaning — a man cataloguing his own restraint, then asking to become a channel for peace rather than the wounded party, then landing on plain responsibility toward anyone still struggling. The dedication to Bill W., cofounder of Alcoholics Anonymous, names the frame outright, so the piece reads less as confession than as a vow spoken on the far side of one, the walls that once caged the speaker now the rubble he climbs out over.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The suite ran five songs across five consecutive albums, 2002-2009 — The Glass Prison, This Dying Soul, The Root of All Evil, Repentance, and this — and the finale closes the circle by reprising their riffs and lines as the steps complete.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"program principles become song architecture on the far side of confession","built":"the Prayer of Saint Francis lifted nearly verbatim as the structural climax"},
"dream-theater~the-silent-man": {"haiku":"A meditation on silence, absence, and the burden of withdrawal; when reason fails and answers don't come, the silent man arrives—embodying either numbness or the inability to help others.","opus":"Silence itself is put on trial, treated as sickness, an unspoken voice, a signal with no listener, waiting behind sanctuary windows where another disguise always looms. Dream Theater guitarist John Petrucci wrote this acoustic Awake piece about his father, weighing faith against suffering, its central figure choosing to strive rather than slip quietly by unseen.","sonnet":"Silence as a moral failure — a man who withholds his voice abandons those who depend on it. Hiding behind walls of non-response may feel safe, but ultimately the silent man chooses to swim rather than drift, to speak rather than suffocate faith with absence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the private cost of keeping quiet in a household ruled by inherited faith, where the singer weighs whether declining to carry that faith forward is a betrayal that will smother the people who still need him to share it. John Petrucci builds the piece from unanswerable rhetorical questions toward one resolve: rather than drift past his family's expectations unseen, he will fight the current openly and let his refusal be witnessed. The chorus splits the title figure in two — the man who wakes when reason calls, and the mute one who arrives only when suffering has emptied every answer — so quiet reads at once as cowardice and as the sole honest response to a doctrine he cannot echo. The verses stack images of separation, one more face laid over the real one and a barrier dividing everyone, until the closing turn converts hiding into a chosen, visible act of swimming against what he was raised to accept.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","identity & becoming","family & blood"],"means":"refusing inherited faith chosen as a visible act, not a disappearance","built":"the chorus splits the title figure into reason's waker and suffering's mute"},
"dream-theater~the-walking-shadow":{"haiku":"A child confronts the murderer of their father, only to realize the killer was their sibling all along.","opus":"A climactic revelation in the Astonishing arc, an act of vengeance for a slain father that strikes the wrong target, the shadow turning out to be someone loved. Tragedy engineered from mistaken identity and blind retribution.","sonnet":"In a story-opera setting, a son discovers his father has been murdered and swears vengeance, only for the approaching figure of doom to be revealed as Faythe — suggesting the cycle of violence closes in on an unexpected target. The shadow hides its face until the trap has already shut.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~these-walls": {"haiku":"Psychological isolation and plea for emotional breakthrough. Internal walls trap the speaker in silence; urgent call for connection to prevent sinking and find the path out.","opus":"A struggle to voice inner thoughts and be understood as feelings retreat and the narrator senses himself slipping into shadow. He begs the single person aware of how much he's concealing to dismantle his defenses before the chance is lost, a plea to be reached before he sinks entirely.","sonnet":"A person trapped inside emotional silence—unable to speak, emotions fading, sinking toward darkness—begs someone to tear down the internal walls that keep him from communicating. The only thing holding back total collapse is the presence of one person who knows what is being held back.","src":"haiku","fable":"A plea addressed to the one person who can see the speaker's paralysis from inside — someone so shut down by fear and unspoken words that his emotions have gone captive and his instincts are dissolving. The two families of imagery pull against each other: the walls he needs demolished from the outside, and the water he keeps sinking into, so that rescue means both breaking a barrier and being pulled up before he drowns. The bridge tightens the stakes into a warning rather than a request, asking whether the person he keeps turning from will still be waiting one more time — a rare Dream Theater lyric that stays intimate and confessional rather than conceptual, its emotional rock register closer to a relationship in crisis than a puzzle to solve.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"rescue means both breaking a barrier and pulling someone from drowning","built":"two incompatible imagery families — walls and water — pull the plea in opposite directions"},
"dream-theater~this-dying-soul": {"haiku":"Generational trauma and broken family cycles breeding decadence and moral collapse; the soul dies through inherited damage and learned cruelty.","opus":"Part of Portnoy's twelve-step suite about his own alcoholism, an addict confronting himself in the mirror and tracing the wreckage back to a broken home and inherited disease. It ends on the AA turn: surrender the ego, admit you can't do this alone, and the sins become the strength.","sonnet":"A mirror-confrontation about a man in recovery facing the wreckage of his life — born into a broken home, using substances, spreading lies, treating people as objects; told in relentless second-person accusation before pivoting to the first steps of getting help.","src":"haiku","fable":"The dedication to Bill W. names Alcoholics Anonymous co-founder Bill Wilson outright, and the piece runs his recovery arc as its skeleton: a reckoning half where the addict interrogates his own reflection, then a surrendering half that turns first person and begs to be carried through by someone else. The fast-rapped center works as a case history recited at speed, tracing an inherited illness in the bloodline, a childhood torn between two warring parents, and an adulthood of appetite and cruelty that spins outward until nobody near him is spared. What binds the two halves is the movement from accusing a figure in the glass to conceding that pride cannot heal in isolation, which is precisely the passage from moral inventory to asking for help that the twelve steps prescribe.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","family & blood","shame & repression"],"means":"moral inventory racing toward the humility to ask for help","built":"the song's two halves mirror the twelve steps' own turning point"},
"dream-theater~this-is-the-life": {"haiku":"Life is a gift, but many lose their way chasing illusions; the song considers mortality and how people forget those gone. It affirms that grace and intentional living matter before death takes it all.","opus":"Trading their usual technical density for something plainer and elegiac, the American prog-metal veterans reflect on mortality and how to spend a life. The song treats existence as a fragile gift shaped equally by love and hatred, and asks what will be remembered of us once time runs out and we are gone.","sonnet":"A meditation on acceptance versus escapism — the question of whether you live with the life given to you or waste it wishing for another. Cast as a kind of reckoning at life's midpoint, it acknowledges that some people navigate with grace and others lose themselves entirely, then arrives at a fragile affirmation: this is what we were handed, impermanent and precious, and it won't come back.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sermon on living that keeps its counsel deliberately wide, pitched to anyone who has lain awake asking what the whole thing is for. The song sorts humanity into two fates — those who move through the maze with grace and those who get lost in it — but refuses to moralize the difference, framing the whole span between empty slate and encroaching shadow as a gift held on loan and shaped equally by love and hate. Its real subject is that terminal clause: the plain, unanswerable question of what remains to be said once you are gone, which turns the anthemic uplift into something closer to a memento mori that has made its peace with the odds.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief"],"means":"anthemic uplift recast as a memento mori at peace","built":"the unanswerable terminal question undercuts the uplift it wears"},
"dream-theater~transcending-time":{"haiku":"A writer in altered consciousness experiences transcendence and cosmic awareness, breaking free from earthly limitations and time.","opus":"A dreamlike drift into the creative act itself, the narrator wandering a hushed landscape drawn by time running in reverse, unearthing buried inspiration while his inner critic goes quiet. It renders the artist's trance as an altered state. The prog-metal band lets its fluid playing mirror this suspended, current-borne surrender to the muse as time seems to dissolve.","sonnet":"The creative trance — drifting into a liminal space where clocks run backward and the inner critic goes silent, carried by something beyond conscious intention. Inspiration is described as signals in the mist, stars in the abyss; the poet lifts his pen not by choice but by being pulled, and the limits dissolve like ashes in rain.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~trial-of-tears": {"haiku":"A man reflects on wasted years and darkness while walking through a rainy city. He moves through multiple states of awareness—from regret to acceptance to wasteland desolation.","opus":"A brooding examination of regret and long stretches lost to fear and gloom, using persistent rainfall over a city as its emotional weather. Rather than wallow, the narrator turns toward honest self-confrontation, choosing to embrace the journey and seek his way toward peace, treating grief itself as a passage that must be crossed.","sonnet":"A multi-part meditation on wasted years and the failure to face one's own life honestly — moving through rain-soaked New York streets, a man trapped in fear and self-deception eventually arrives in a wasteland of his own making where no path back home exists.","src":"haiku","fable":"A three-part suite that stages recovery as weather: the rain that opens on a cold New York street is misery, but by the closing movement the same downpour has been rerouted inward and reclaimed as something that falls deep in heaven — grief reframed as grace rather than punishment. The design tracks a man trying to talk himself out of a wasted decade, and the reproach in the middle bridge, that he is not much better than the one he despises, is the honest floor the song has to touch before it can rise. Its title is the mechanism: the tears are put on trial, made to argue for whether those lost years were worth their cost, and the verdict the music reaches — an instrumental heaven bracketed by a wasteland of ash and a search for the way home — is that walking through them was the only route out. The final movement's refusal to resolve the rain, looping the same line until it becomes benediction instead of complaint, is the point: some pain isn't fixed, only re-heard.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","death & grief","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"grief put on trial to argue whether the wasted years were worth their cost","built":"the closing movement loops the rain line until complaint becomes benediction"},
"dream-theater~under-a-glass-moon": {"haiku":"A speaker awakens to memories and silver tears beneath a darkened summer sky, awaiting a bride's survival as liquid shadows speak of transformative honor.","opus":"Dream Theater's dreamlike meditation on memory, sorrow and awakening, tears and moonlight blurring into a rite of passage. The imagery gestures at loss and transformation, a soul being brought through grief toward some fragile survival.","sonnet":"A dreamlike meditation on grief and memory, with the moon and night sky framing a ritual of loss — a woman (the bride, the lamb) has died or disappeared, and the narrator is left arguing with weather, chasing water, and praying for time to stop.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man addresses a lover as one might address a lost sacrament, the second-person plea reaching toward someone who baptizes him in grief even as she vanishes, so that devotion and mourning collapse into a single gesture. The nocturnal imagery works as one sealed world — rain read as weeping, moonlight filtered through glass, a wedding turned funereal — where every natural thing is doubled into loss and every act of naming him feels like a rite that both consecrates and abandons. The band built this as one of the record's showcases for guitar and keyboard to trade lyrical, singing lines, and that virtuosic soaring does narrative work: the music keeps climbing past what the words can resolve, enacting a transcendence the lyric only begs for and never quite reaches.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"devotion and mourning collapsed into a single unresolvable gesture","built":"guitar and keyboard soaring past what the words can reach enacts begged transcendence"},
"dream-theater~untethered-angel": {"haiku":"A person builds a self-constructed prison of fear and avoidance, falling into darkness through indecision. The song urges confronting inner fear to achieve freedom.","opus":"Prog-metal about paralysis by fear and second-guessing, and the counterintuitive truth that surrender can trap you while facing the dread within is what frees you. It's a push to let go of the self-built prison of avoidance.","sonnet":"An appeal to someone paralyzed by fear and self-constructed walls — they've created a private universe that's suffocating them, and the song urges them to release the ego, face the fear inside, and open their heart rather than falling endlessly into the dark.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study of the private prison anxiety builds: someone has walled off a whole interior world and now bargains with the accusing voice in his own skull, snarled in the paralysis of hesitation and endless deferral. The chorus turns that fall into a two-edged test: it urges the dread surrendered but the self kept — abandoning fear and abandoning who you are, it insists, are different acts, and only the first one frees. The bridge names the machinery outright — dread is a live thing that drives you to the brink, and the only way out is to turn and confront it, waking as if from the grave. Dream Theater, who spent 2019's record deliberately trimming their sprawl toward something leaner and heavier, match that argument with a track built to hit hard and land clean, its resolution as unambiguous as its riff.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","madness & the mind","freedom & escape"],"means":"confronting dread as the only door to freedom","built":"resolution as unambiguous as its riff mirrors the argument"},
"dream-theater~vacant": {"haiku":"Someone watches helplessly as another person loses their grip on reality, their eyes becoming blank and unresponsive. The speaker struggles to reach them and fears losing them entirely.","opus":"A helpless bedside plea to someone slipping away, watching their conscience fade and eyes go vacant like black holes; grounded in the real illness of the band member's daughter, it captures the terror of watching a loved one lose control while being powerless to reach them.","sonnet":"A terrifyingly brief and raw portrait of watching someone — likely a loved one — lose their grip on consciousness or sanity, the narrator desperately trying to reach through to her as her eyes go blank and hollow, unsure whether he is losing her for good.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dropped into the middle of Train of Thought's slab of relentless heaviness, this is the album exhaling: a bare piano vigil beside someone slipping out of reach, the singer stranded outside a face that no longer registers him. The song lives in the asymmetry of address — he calls out that he is right here while she goes dark and unreachable, so the song is really about the helplessness of the one still present, watching consciousness drain from eyes that have become black holes and finding no gesture equal to it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The she is real — LaBrie’s daughter, who fell into a brief coma after a sudden, unexplained seizure days before her seventh birthday. The lyric is his.","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"helplessness of the one present watching consciousness drain away","built":"bare piano amid the album's heaviness enacts the exhale it describes"},
"dream-theater~viper-king-bonus-track":{"haiku":"A man pushes a sports car to dangerous limits, exhilarated by speed and risk, embracing the thrill despite peril.","opus":"A gleeful hymn to a muscle car, all raw horsepower and reckless cornering, gambling with disaster and feeling most alive at the edge of control. Dream Theater, the American prog-metal veterans, keep this bonus track stripped-down and propulsive, James LaBrie's swagger capturing pure speed-thrill without their usual conceptual heft.","sonnet":"Pure speed worship — a six-hundred-horsepower car pushed to the edge of control, fishtailing, brakes screaming, driver riding the line between alive and gone. The Viper is desire made mechanical: venomous by design, tempting fate, demanding everything from the person behind the wheel who wouldn't have it any other way.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~vital-star":{"haiku":"A metaphorical call for spiritual awakening and legacy, where humanity carries an eternal flame of consciousness to inspire future generations.","opus":"A youthful, idealistic anthem about reaching skyward for a distant goal, leaving behind one's origins to pursue destiny with strength and loyalty. From the American prog-metal band's earliest days, it wonders whether the dreamers can rouse those whose ambitions lie dormant and inspire them to strive for greatness too.","sonnet":"A generational torch-passing about carrying light through darkness — the elders watch the young grow strong enough to reach for stars, and the responsibility now is to stir the hearts still asleep, teach others to fly, and keep the flame of an original wish alive. The vital star is the inner life that must be tended before it can illuminate anyone else.","src":"haiku"},
"dream-theater~voices": {"haiku":"A person hears disturbing internal voices and external intrusions on their thoughts, struggling between spiritual confusion, paranoia, and a search for truth.","opus":"A centerpiece of Awake, the track inhabits severe mental illness, phantom voices that echo, debate, shield, and spurn the narrator, entangling religious guilt with schizophrenic terror. Weekend ritual sours into paranoia about stolen, broadcast thoughts, and mythic figures frame a mind ready to gamble its own deliverance to end its isolation. A harrowing, compassionate plunge into a fracturing consciousness.","sonnet":"Psychosis narrated from the inside: external voices assign motives, paranoid logic contaminates every symbol — spider, crucifix, diary — and the self dissolves between what is real and what the illness insists upon. A desperate wish to speak clearly coexists with the inability to distinguish inner from outer. Isolation deepens with every failed connection.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dream Theater build a clinical case study of paranoid schizophrenia from the inside, staging the disorder's textbook symptoms as lyric events: thought insertion, ideas of reference, the dread that strangers broadcast and pilfer your private mind. The household voices that once policed a child's staring — one dead by his own poison, the widow now setting the rules — return as guilt fused to a warped Catholicism, where sex and death and the crucifix collapse into one unbearable equivalence and the promised resurrection simply never arrives. Between the verses, actual voices in dialogue take the wheel, alternately soothing and accusing the sufferer until the boundary between comfort and torment dissolves entirely. The narrator reaches for messianic self-image, a Cassandra unheeded and a bleeding modern icon, before the closing voices demolish even that, insisting the redemptive world he craves exists nowhere but the failing chamber of his own skull. This is John Petrucci pushing Awake's preoccupation with faith and self-erosion past confession into horror, from research deep enough to borrow clinical case detail.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The lyric’s strangest image — skin worn inside out — is a real patient’s report, lifted from Petrucci’s research into documented schizophrenia case histories.","themes":["madness & the mind","faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"the redeemed world craved exists only in the failing skull","built":"interjected voices alternate comfort and accusation until the boundary dissolves"},
"dream-theater~wait-for-sleep": {"haiku":"A woman lies awake, unable to escape memories and the chains she struggles to break. She waits for sleep and peace, feeling lost between desire and the weight of her sorrow.","opus":"Dream Theater sketch a woman lying sleepless, trying to escape a memory she can't outrun. The deeper current is grief: nothing can wash away her loss or ease her ache, and her prayer to breathe life back into something cold and lifeless reads as the impossible wish to bring back someone she loved who is now gone.","sonnet":"A woman lying in bed at night unable to stop her mind — too proud to hide under the covers, unable to sleep, besieged by the memory of someone she can't wash away, asking God for the power to give life back to something cold and dead.","src":"haiku","fable":"A woman lies rigid in the dark, too proud to hide under the covers, willing an unnamed grief out of her before rest can come; Kevin Moore's spare piano keeps the rest of the band almost entirely offstage, so the sorrow stays intimate rather than symphonic. The turn is structural and devastating: the closing lines slip into a first person that is not hers, her palm resting on the speaker’s head — the one she mourns answering from inside her, keeping the vigil from the far side of death.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The album hides its name in her chorus — images and words running deep — making this two-minute miniature the record’s quiet title track.","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the mourned answering from inside the mourner keeping the vigil","built":"a pronoun shift at the close moves the voice across the boundary of death"},
"dream-theater~wither": {"haiku":"Someone gives up on self-expression and creation, allowing depression and paralysis to guide them through dissolution and helplessness.","opus":"A portrait of creative paralysis and self-doubt, the artist shrinking and helpless as his words collapse and his finest work goes up in flames. The deeper move is surrender, giving in and letting the narrative carry him, since he alone authors his reality, even as the prospect of beginning again fills him with dread.","sonnet":"A musician locked in creative paralysis — staring at a blank page, drowning in hesitation, watching their best work burn before it exists, until the only way through is to surrender control and let the story write itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the terror of the blank page, built as a fight the singer keeps losing. The verses run like self-coaching instructions — coax the feeling loose, help a buried memory surface — but each pre-chorus admits the effort dead-ends, and the chorus answers by surrendering rather than forcing it, choosing collapse and letting the unfinished work steer instead. What turns it is the bridge's small reversal: the ruined drafts and the dread of a fresh start give way to the notion that the page is a mirror and the world is simply whatever you set down on it, so the yielding stops reading as defeat and becomes the only route by which the writing actually gets made.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"yielding to the blank page revealed as the only route through it","built":"the bridge reframes ruined drafts as mirror, turning defeat into method"},
"dream-theater~you-not-me": {"haiku":"A person rejects another's attempt to control and remake them through constant manipulation and expectation, asserting their refusal to be defined by others' needs.","opus":"A rejection of someone who demands you become whatever they expect, brick by brick building a prison of obligation. It's about reclaiming the self from a relationship that only takes, exhausting and one-sided by design.","sonnet":"A song about being smothered by someone else's needs and expectations — the narrator refuses to be reshaped or consumed by a relationship where everything revolves around what the other person demands and nothing is ever enough.","src":"haiku","fable":"A flat, weary indictment of someone who takes everything and calls it fair, the song reduces a lopsided bond to a single ledger where one party's demands crowd out any room for the other to simply live. Its refusal is the real content: the speaker keeps insisting he isn't fooled even as he concedes the pattern repeats daily, so the exhaustion sits right beside the clear-eyed accounting. It reads too as Dream Theater's most radio-plain mode, trading their usual labyrinthine sprawl for a blunt, cyclic chorus that hammers the imbalance until it feels inescapable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The emblem of the album’s compromise — the label flew Petrucci to Florida to rewrite a song called You or Me with hit-doctor Desmond Child, and the rewrite shipped as You Not Me.","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"clear-eyed accounting of a bond where only one party exists","built":"radio-plain cyclic chorus enacts the inescapable pattern it names"},
"dream-theater~you-or-me-demo-version-1996-1997":{"haiku":"A critique of superficiality and sameness in society, where all people seem interchangeable and the speaker cannot distinguish individual identity.","opus":"A narrator worn down by the noise of people around him, their sales pitches and mind games blurring the line between self and other until he can no longer tell where he ends and another begins. Fragmented radio and televangelist samples frame it as media overload. Dream Theater are American progressive-metal architects, and this demo-era track leans into the collage textures and ambivalent detachment that flavored their late-nineties experimentation.","sonnet":"A song about the exhaustion of being surrounded by people who seem to perform life rather than live it — selling second-hand ideas, mispronouncing your name to throw you off. The speaker holds steady, not blind to the noise but not consumed by it either, circling back to a core question: when everyone around you blurs into sameness, is there any difference between you and them?","src":"haiku"},
"dreamer-isioma~ah":{"opus":"The vertigo of a new crush filtered through a neurodivergent narrator who finds ordinary communication baffling and words impossibly heavy. Underneath the giddy infatuation is genuine anxiety about how normal people even connect.","src":"opus"},
"dreamer-isioma~dumb-in-love-with-you":{"haiku":"Irrational devotion overcomes all doubt; the speaker surrenders thought for presence, trusting love to navigate chaos.","opus":"A devotion song where love is surrender past reason, jumping without stopping to think. The beloved is sun and compass, steadying him even as he carries his people and keeps his family fed, trading half his heart to be guarded while they hold each other's fears.","sonnet":"Love that bypasses reason entirely — 'with you I don't think, I just jump' — gives the speaker clarity, grounds family responsibilities, and arrives 'straight out the mud' when everything turns to dust; dumbness here is not weakness but the surrender to something that actually works.","src":"haiku"},
"dreamer-isioma~gimme-a-chance":{"haiku":"A plea for second chances wrapped in defiant self-belief clashes with systemic indifference and personal crisis, oscillating between dance-floor elation and suicidal ideation.","opus":"A restless plea for a chance that spirals into a manic self-portrait, fear of staying poor, contempt for cops and government and school, and the confession that he's trying to save a world when he can barely keep himself afloat. Panic gives way to dancing it out, love and stress spilling as he tries to bury old mistakes.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses a potential partner and the wider world simultaneously, pitching himself as all he has to offer while processing anxiety, systemic frustration, and the need to dance through panic — 'tonight's made for dancing' pivots the desperation into release.","src":"haiku"},
"dreamer-isioma~hard":{"haiku":"Relentless effort against systemic rejection drives ambition forward; the speaker sacrifices sleep and stability to break free, pouring blood and devotion into an uncertain ascent.","opus":"An artist questions why they keep pushing through lows, answering that grinding effort and refusal to be discouraged is all they have. It is about clawing toward success on nothing but blood, love, and drugs.","sonnet":"A persistent declaration of effort in the face of repeated rejection: the speaker keeps moving, keeps dancing even when low, refuses discouragement when applications fail, and insists that blood and love and relentless trying are all he has and all he needs.","src":"haiku"},
"dreamer-isioma~sensitive-sped-up":{"haiku":"Aggressive self-assertion against exploitation, pairing street hustle mentality with genuine romantic devotion and ambition for wealth and dignity.","opus":"A boastful narrator flexes rivalry, dominance and the hunger for cash by whatever route it takes, then abruptly turns soft, folding luxury labels and body art into an admission of being truly smitten. Dreamer Isioma, the Chicago genre-blending artist, lets tough-guy posturing and vulnerability share the same track, the hardened persona repeatedly collapsing back into plain affection.","sonnet":"Bravado and tenderness collide in the same breath — street-hardened posturing about rivals and loyalty sits beside genuine devotion to a partner studying hard and wearing luxury. The narrator moves between coded aggression and unguarded love without resolving the tension.","src":"haiku"},
"dreamer-isioma~technicolor-love":{"opus":"A volatile love where being hated is part of the pull, and vulnerability leaks out anyway in tears and the safety of one person's presence. It's about a guarded soul learning romance and slowly letting the mask fall.","src":"opus"},
"dreamer-isioma~valentina":{"haiku":"Fascination with an elusive, ethereal presence awakens romantic longing; the speaker admires a lunar figure who moves through the world untouchable and complete.","opus":"An infatuation with a magnetic, room-owning dream girl, admitting a history of never staying faithful yet feeling she inspires actual faith. It is about wanting to become worthy of someone who outshines you.","sonnet":"An infatuated portrait of Valentina, a cool, moon-dreamer who owns every room she enters; the speaker, self-confessed as unfaithful, feels something new pulling him toward loyalty — she's all he can think of, style, mind, and face — and wants to meet her again, this time alone.","src":"haiku"},
"dreamer-isioma~z-s-lullaby":{"opus":"A tender devotional to a partner who is the singer's rest and peace in a life felt as a battleground. The sweetness admits imperfection, promising effort rather than perfection, with self-medication drifting at the edges.","src":"opus"},
"dreamers~don-t-go-dark":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation whispers as internal pressure while community offers fragile resistance, the plea for illumination battling the gravity of despair.","opus":"A plea to someone romanticizing their own instability and self-harm, urging them not to succumb to the darkness because it's cold under the ground, promising to turn them back toward the light.","sonnet":"A plea to someone flirting with self-destruction — throwing away medication, slapping themselves awake, shattering like a light bulb when they fall apart — to not retreat into the cold darkness beneath the ground.","src":"haiku"},
"dreamers~screws":{"haiku":"Emotional exposure and psychological vulnerability leave the narrator fractured and most alone when intimacy arrives.","opus":"A confession of unravelling: craving honesty while stripped bare, a mind coming loose, tethered to a poisonous attachment. The sharpest wound is feeling more isolated in this person's company than in genuine solitude.","sonnet":"A speaker whose brain has lost its screws — running wild, toxic things tasting sweet, static TV — confesses that the deepest loneliness arrives not in solitude but when alone with the person who should be closest, wanting truth instead of ease.","src":"haiku"},
"dreamhouse~closer-to-comfort":{"haiku":"A woman arrives seeking change but dreams fade into despair and substance use; comfort becomes a trap where she remains stuck and isolated.","opus":"The arc of a woman who chases a dream far from home, watches it curdle into addiction and grey resignation, and settles for numb comfort over the life she craved. The late turn toward reclaiming herself is the small hope inside an otherwise bleak slide.","sonnet":"The arc of a woman who emigrates or relocates with hope, watches that hope drain away as isolation and substance use become routine, and settles into a numbed existence — comfort becomes another word for giving up.","src":"haiku"},
"dreamshade~question-everything":{"haiku":"Exposing hidden agendas and systemic deception; the speaker breaks free by refusing promises and rejecting the illusions everyone else accepts.","opus":"A defiant self-motivation anthem about clawing up from rock bottom while distrusting everyone around you, sick of hollow promises and the sense that others treat you as a pawn paving their way with your name. The refrain insists on doubting everything and pressing on regardless. Dreamshade are a Swiss melodic metalcore band whose big clean choruses and heavy grooves sit squarely in the modern European scene.","sonnet":"Suspicion hardening into severance — a speaker who has been exploited by someone using his reputation for their own gain, and who finally stops waiting for the deception to explain itself. The refrain becomes both an accusation and a decision: something is wrong, he knows it, and continuing forward means going forward without the people who kept him in the dark.","src":"haiku"},
"dreamshade~rise":{"haiku":"The individual rejects fabricated societal illusions and crowd conformity to reclaim sovereignty and rebirth as king of their own existence.","opus":"Refusing a comfortable, fabricated existence that feels like a beautiful prison, choosing to break out and rule one's own life instead. A metalcore anthem about self-determination, dying a thousand times and refusing to bow.","sonnet":"Someone trapped in a fabricated dream — a beautiful prison where everyone fits in and keys are discarded — decides to kill the lights, break out before being locked in, and refuse to sink: the speaker has died a thousand times but declares themselves the king of their own game and rises when all the monuments fall.","src":"haiku"},
"dreamwake~luna":{"haiku":"Buried under accumulated weight, someone confronts an inner devil eating away their former self, descending into darkness to find light.","opus":"Metalcore about waking to find yourself transformed by despair, recognizing a devil grown inside you as substances and darkness eat away the person you were. A drowning cry for rescue from your own life.","sonnet":"A song about hitting a personal bottom — seeing the devil in yourself, feeling consumed by darkness and substance use, and desperately calling out to be saved from your own disintegration.","src":"haiku"},
"dreamwake~midnight-rain":{"opus":"Addiction rendered as drowning, a man retracing the path that carried him away from the people he loves, enslaved to a substance he swears nothing can alter. The comfort he offers a partner is undercut by his own helplessness.","src":"opus"},
"dredg~bug-eyes":{"haiku":"Ten wasted years haunt the speaker; birth and death both linger as departure from normal life. They squandered time and now must do something good for themselves.","opus":"The alt-rock band turns wasted, aimless time and a squandered future into an introspective reckoning, using cosmic and cradle-to-grave imagery to frame someone haunted by his own path. Its core lesson is that tolerance earns tolerance back, and after a long stretch of stagnation the narrator resolves to finally take care of himself.","sonnet":"Ten years of squandered time crystallize into a reckoning: the narrator has wandered, thrown away a future, and remained haunted by the journey back to origins and the approaching departure from earth. The turn comes late — finally deciding, after all the waste, to do something good for himself.","src":"haiku"},
"dredg~catch-without-arms":{"haiku":"An artist refuses to compromise their work for commercial appeal or money, rejecting the trap of selling love and lust as commodities.","opus":"An artist refuses the role of savior or cash cow, exhausted and nearly indifferent, yet needling both himself and a peer to stop compromising their craft. Singing about love and lust becomes the cynical price of being cared about. Dredg, a Californian art-rock band, turn this meditation on artistic integrity inward, framing sellout and sincerity as the fault line that sets people apart.","sonnet":"A musician refuses the commercial compromise of writing only about love and lust to manufacture audience connection. Exhausted by the industry's transactional demands and his own dwindling care, he names the central paradox: playing to reach people while hollowing out the art is like attempting the impossible catch — the gesture defeats itself.","src":"haiku"},
"dredg~ode-to-the-sun":{"haiku":"The speaker owes their soul and offers honor in a song, asking for acceptance as clouds clear and sun shines. A car crash appears as fiasco; they ask for freedom and transcendence.","opus":"The California art-rock band dredg build a hymn of devotion around parting clouds and offered soul, the narrator vowing to let light through in an ode of gratitude. A recurring highway catastrophe, mangled cars and rubberneckers, intrudes on the reverence, the word fiasco puncturing the transcendence. Acceptance is begged even as it slides toward acknowledging that something is over.","sonnet":"A car crash becomes a devotional act — the wreck suspended in a sudden flash of sunlight that already sets the witnesses free before any ambulance arrives. Dark and light coexist as necessary counterparts, and the catastrophe resolves into a kind of transcendence offered to the sun as honor.","src":"haiku"},
"dregg~feeling-fine":{"haiku":"Spiritual wandering with companions who don't know the way, lost but unbothered by critique and societal expectation.","opus":"A gleeful mental-breakdown anthem that flips losing your mind into liberation, mocking everyone who critiques the world but not themselves, meme-culture chaos masking a genuine refusal to march to anyone's beat but your own.","sonnet":"About embracing absurdist freedom after losing your mind — the narrator and their friends are done making sense of the world, so they Naruto-run through conspiracies, meditate in temples, and \"boogie to the end of the world.\" The joke is that losing your mind might be the sanest response to the present.","src":"haiku"},
"dregg~hectic":{"opus":"An Australian rap-metal outsider's origin story and defiant gesture, citing the Epitaph deal that finally legitimized a group no scene would embrace. It's about refusing to have his words dictated by a culture too fearful to speak its mind.","src":"opus"},
"dregg~hyperbole":{"haiku":"All existence is hyperbole and performance; deception masks reality as the teller admits control slipped and nothing holds weight.","opus":"A gleefully unreliable narrator declaring himself all hype and contradiction, turning nihilism into a punchline. Under the self-mythologizing swagger sits real despair, meaninglessness reframed as a reason to stop taking anything seriously.","sonnet":"A self-aware performer declares themselves a walking contradiction — an insomniac who naps, a self-lover who stands in the wind — and turns the meaninglessness of everything into a reason to do more rather than less, closing the loop on nihilism with chaotic self-acceptance.","src":"haiku"},
"dregg~trunks":{"haiku":"Time-travel consciousness and over-analysis trap the speaker in recursive doubt; the future holds only androids and disconnection while the past offers no redemption to save.","opus":"Using the time-traveling anime hero as a mask for millennial dread, a mind dissolving under technology and disconnection. It skewers a culture that performs rebellion while changing nothing, and fears becoming one of the machines.","sonnet":"Borrowing the Dragon Ball Z character as metaphor, the speaker positions himself as a time-traveller returning to warn the past about a world run by android-like systems — technology, social media, performative creativity — while staring in the mirror at someone he no longer recognises.","src":"haiku"},
"drewsif-stalin-s-musical-endeavors~down-with-the-sickness":{"haiku":"Transformation into monstrosity occurs as suppressed self erupts violently, madness becoming the only honest response to a world designed to crush authenticity.","opus":"A cover of Disturbed's descent into inner monstrousness, a human side eroding as rage and madness take over, inviting the listener to give in to the demon awoken within.","sonnet":"The speaker's human side is slowly consumed by an inner demon of rage and madness; the sickness rises through self-loathing and hatred until it demands to be fully embraced as a gift rather than resisted.","src":"haiku"},
"drewsif-stalin-s-musical-endeavors~never-after":{"haiku":"Inadequacy crystallized as truth; belief locked away as too painful to confront or share.","opus":"Depression as a prison of one's own making, the narrator bolting the doors against hope and half-waiting for the end to arrive. The gutting refrain is a plea for someone else to imagine, on his behalf, a future in which things could still turn out well.","sonnet":"The speaker retreats when belief knocks — locking doors, drawing blinds — because self-worth has collapsed entirely and every device of their own making becomes a hell they sink further into. The plea to be found in never after is addressed to no one who can answer.","src":"haiku"},
"driftcat~puzzle":{"haiku":"The speaker is trapped in cycles of indecision, unable to determine right from wrong or escape repeating patterns of failure, desperately seeking a way out of this mental puzzle.","opus":"A song about decision paralysis, the singer lost in a maze of choices, unable to tell right from wrong or find where he went astray, longing to break the pattern and finally be free. The needle-in-a-haystack image captures the exhausting search for a way out. The register is earnest, melodic and repetitive, building around a plea to escape a self-made trap of endless overthinking.","sonnet":"Decision paralysis turned inward — the feeling of being trapped in an unending loop of self-questioning with no clear exit. Every choice looks identical, every attempt to find the right path feels like searching for something that may not exist, and the only consistent wish is to get out of the pattern and feel free again.","src":"haiku"},
"drip-eu~immortals":{"haiku":"Transgression and defiance as currency; the narrative celebrates moral chaos, excess, and the deliberate rejection of social constraint in pursuit of hedonistic immortality.","opus":"A hedonistic flex of excess, defiance and self-destruction, both fists raised to the sky and everything set alight, with a flicker of buried trauma admitting the scars behind the bravado never quite close.","sonnet":"A collective bravado anthem from a crew that rewrites the rules, combining trap wealth-flexing and mosh-pit energy with flashes of genuine street trauma — \"deep scars never heal\" — into a declaration of indestructibility.","src":"haiku"},
"drivealone~sustain-decay":{"haiku":"Endless stagnation and decline define a life stuck between sustain and decay, with voices and vices as constant companions.","opus":"Dissociation modeled on the shape of a fading note — a life thinning to routine residue, the narrator estranged from his own history and circling a home he neither grasps nor leaves. Numb drift dressed in synth-envelope metaphor.","sonnet":"A meditation on being stuck between existence and collapse — the speaker feels perpetually far from their true story, trapped by vices and constraints, oscillating between states of sustaining and decaying without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"dropout-kings~bad-day":{"haiku":"A cascading bad day exposes systemic workplace exploitation and personal relationships failing, building toward breakdown and violence.","opus":"A nu-metal rap narrative of one catastrophically bad shift, opening with a dreaded call from a manager and spiraling through a broken alarm, ripped shirt, empty gas tank and a girlfriend who is done, until rage curdles into violent fantasy against a lazy boss. Named Eddie in the skit, the narrator feels worked like a slave on minimum wage. Dropout Kings fold trap and hardcore into this bleak, escalating rant about workplace despair.","sonnet":"A day of cascading disasters told with mounting fury — starting from an unwanted call into work and spiraling through an empty pantry, a torn shirt, a fight with a girlfriend, an empty gas tank, and a minimum-wage job with a contemptuous boss. The anger compounds until it tips into fantasy violence, rendering systemic indignity as one man's barely contained implosion.","src":"haiku"},
"dropout-kings~dark-energy":{"haiku":"Vengeance and self-assertion crystallize into dark magnetism as the speaker channels pain into unstoppable force, reclaiming agency through controlled destruction.","opus":"Turning pain and rejection into fuel, the rapper casts himself as an unkillable assassin fed by revenge, converting scars and old wounds into the dark energy that powers his rise.","sonnet":"A relentless boast of destructive power fuelled by scars and vendetta: the speaker counts their wounds as memories, comes back for everyone who was mean to them, and channels rage into music and confrontation — \"blowing minds and crushing hearts, I'm dark energy\" — claiming the city crown through sheer, unstoppable force.","src":"haiku"},
"drove~through-it-all":{"opus":"A survival hymn of thanks to whatever force carried the singer out of hardship and into brightness. Imagery of a blaze coursing under the skin asks not for the pain to vanish but for the strength to master it, faith steady across every trial.","src":"opus"},
"drowning-pool~all-about-me":{"haiku":"A self-absorbed person refuses to listen to others, demanding focus only on themselves while dismissing everyone else as irrelevant.","opus":"Bristling at a self-absorbed listener who only hears themselves, the narrator flips the same narcissism back, pledging allegiance to me, myself and I. It is a bitter mirror held up to someone who never lets another get a word in. Drowning Pool, Texas nu-metallers marked by tragedy after their singer's death, drive the sarcasm with blunt aggression and a taunting, repetitive hook.","sonnet":"Someone locked in a one-sided dynamic with a conversational narcissist reaches the breaking point, reclaiming his own subjectivity. Every attempt at being heard collapses under the other's relentless self-focus. The speaker declares, with blunt symmetry, that he is done subordinating his own needs and pledges allegiance to himself instead.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~bodies":{"haiku":"Violence erupts from accumulated pressure and despair, reaching a breaking point where bodies fall.","opus":"A nu-metal staple built for the mosh pit, all channeled rage and the physical catharsis of a crowd in violent motion. Beneath the pounding chant lies a paradox about how one can feel utterly isolated while surrounded by others, having willingly stepped into the chaos. Hate and dread fuel a release that is as much communal as it is personal.","sonnet":"About the cathartic violence of the mosh pit — let the bodies hit the floor is both literal stage-diving and a war cry, driven by hate and fear and the release of physical chaos, though some readings extend it to combat or personal rage.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~cast-me-aside":{"haiku":"Abandonment in adversity reveals the emptiness of casual friendship, the speaker recognizing opportunism the moment fortune shifts away.","opus":"Scorn for a fair-weather friend who vanished during the hard times and reappeared once success arrived, the band refusing to let a trend-chaser ride their coattails.","sonnet":"A confrontation with fair-weather people who abandoned the speaker during hard times and now want to attach themselves to his success. The anger is direct and the rejection total — he remembers who was absent and refuses to let them along for the ride now.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~children-of-the-gun":{"haiku":"Soldiers shaped by violence fight together in brutal struggle, sacrificing themselves collectively as victims of warfare and militarization.","opus":"Drowning Pool wed nu-metal heaviness to a rallying cry of rebellion, imagining siblings who fight and die side by side to topple an oppressive system. The plea to rise up and the disavowal of blaming the messenger cast these figures as both fighters and casualties, bound by shared defiance and sacrifice.","sonnet":"A generation defined by armed conflict and systemic violence bands together under shared identity rather than shared ideology. Brotherhood and sisterhood are forged in the heat of collective struggle, with dying together recast not as defeat but as solidarity against a machine that will not otherwise fall.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~feel-like-i-do":{"haiku":"A speaker shares universal human emotions of love, hate, and regret with an audience, calling them to raise hands and express shared internal torment.","opus":"The Texas hard-rock band turns private bitterness into a mass ritual, with a scarred narrator insisting that love, resentment, and remorse are experiences everyone shares. The chorus converts his wounds into a crowd chant, rallying anyone who recognizes their own pain, so individual damage becomes an act of defiant solidarity.","sonnet":"Shared emotional wreckage — love, hate, regret cycling through everyone — becomes the basis for collective solidarity. Scars accumulated from betrayal and disillusionment are not private wounds but universal ones. The call to raise hands and scream is not aggression but recognition: the invitation to stop pretending the damage is yours alone.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~forget":{"haiku":"Trauma accumulates beneath the surface while forgetting becomes impossible, the only escape requiring complete severance from both pain and identity.","opus":"Numbness offered as mercy, a plea to bury shared fear and pain in oblivion the way faith was already abandoned, with the crawling imagery marking how far down the erasing has to reach.","sonnet":"A communal invitation to shed accumulated pain and fear through shared release — the speaker sees the same spiritual wound in others and proposes forgetting as the only available relief. The tone moves between solidarity and desperation.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~horns-up":{"haiku":"A call to combat and aggression where the speaker pits themselves against others with violent imagery, describing themselves as destructive and unstoppable.","opus":"A confrontational hype track about living recklessly with everything already forfeit, straddling ruin and death, daring anyone to challenge him as he seizes control and rallies the crowd. Beneath the metal-show bravado runs a fatalism, the sense of a life already half-lost fueling brazen defiance in the face of it.","sonnet":"A live-audience pumping-up track that doubles as a vague declaration of defiance — the narrator has nothing to lose, one foot in the grave, and is coming at everything with both fists. It's more ritual than content: the horns-up gesture as a statement of survival.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~i-am":{"haiku":"Self-destructive patterns and sickness define identity while the narrator owns present circumstances despite loss and regret.","opus":"A defiant reckoning with a wasted self, taunting someone who expected him to fail while claiming the sickness that keeps him in control. The chant of what he could have been curdling into what he is turns self-destruction into a dare.","sonnet":"A defiant self-assertion against stagnation and the expectations of others — \"I could've been\" resolving into \"I am\" — channeling sickness and drain as a form of control, taunting whoever once held power over the speaker with the question of whether it makes them happy now.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~killin-me":{"haiku":"Life's accumulated failures and shattered dreams feel fatal, with the speaker watching their resolve collapse under the weight of misery and decay.","opus":"A heavy portrait of exhaustion so deep it borders on suicidal, the narrator watching his own misery burn and comparing himself to things left to decay. The refrain names life itself as the thing crushing him, its wreckage of dead ambitions and collapsed hopes pulling everyone around him apart too.","sonnet":"A song about a life so exhausted and degraded by accumulated failure and broken dreams that it feels like a slow death — the speaker watches everything rot (bones, bottles, resolve) and can barely breathe under the weight of what was hoped for and lost.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~king-zero":{"haiku":"A self-proclaimed ruler revels in chaos and control within a world of nothing, crushing others beneath them.","opus":"Texas hard-rockers Drowning Pool build a swaggering boast around a man who crowns himself lord of a barren nothing-kingdom, needing no drugs to feel invincible, only his own defiance. Menacing throughout, the track ends on a hollow note, all that bravado leading only to more misery, ruler of an empire that amounts to nothing.","sonnet":"A self-styled ruler of nothing, sovereign over a land of suffering and emptiness, insists on his own unbreakable identity. Dreams fuel survival where no deity or substance can; King Zero claims dominion not through wealth or power but through sheer refusal to be displaced.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~let-the-sin-begin":{"haiku":"Addiction and sexual temptation embodied as a feminine force drives reckless pleasure-seeking across America. The song celebrates immersion in vice without restraint.","opus":"A hard-rock road anthem personifying a whole restless America as a woman the narrator loves like a drug. The heroin metaphor makes indulgence and dependence the whole point: she makes the bad times bearable and that's exactly the danger.","sonnet":"A woman who drinks hard, travels the country, and burns bright is described as the narrator's \"heroin\" — addictive, electrifying, and impossible to quit — and the night is framed as an invitation to mutual abandon.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~love-and-war":{"haiku":"Separation and sacrifice bind lovers across distance while military duty forces impossible choices between duty and devotion.","opus":"A soldier's letter home clinging to a lover's promise and photographs while the bugle drags him back to the front. The old saying that all is fair when it comes to love or battle becomes the bitter excuse for a leaving he never wanted.","sonnet":"A soldier speaks to the person he left at home — pictures close to his heart, letters falling apart — insisting he never wanted to leave but had no say when the trumpets called, accepting that all is fair in love and war even as it tears him apart.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~more-than-worthless":{"haiku":"The singer descends into exhaustion and emptiness—one cigarette left, steps running out—begging God not to force more emotion. They insist they are more than worthless, resisting the abyss.","opus":"A raw survival cry from someone at the brink, near his last smoke with barely a step left, begging a higher power to dull the pain yet holding onto the belief that his life still has value. The chorus tears between despair and defiant self-worth. The Texan hard-rock band channels its heaviness into a stand against giving up.","sonnet":"Rock-bottom insistence on self-worth — a speaker stripped to his last cigarette and an empty glass, addressing a God who seems to have abandoned him, demanding to know what was saved and what was paid for. The refrain flips the insult back on itself: the claim is not worthlessness but its opposite, shouted from the lowest possible position.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~nothingness":{"haiku":"Emptiness corrupts from within as the speaker drifts into isolation and transformation into an unrecognizable entity. Nothingness replaces identity as the culmination of internal fracture.","opus":"A descent into hollow self-erasure, hands bloodied and wolves at the door, as an emptiness spreads until the narrator loses himself entirely and becomes something else.","sonnet":"A speaker lost on a road of their own choosing finds the self eroding: blood on the hands, wolves at the door, and the creeping realisation that the thing they have become has taken control of mind, body, and soul until nothing of the original self remains.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~numb":{"haiku":"A person numb with despair cannot escape internal suffering and is confronted by others who demand his performance of pain.","opus":"The band turns on a man wallowing in self-pity, telling him bluntly to take his sorrow elsewhere instead of mining everyone for pity. The narrator sees the grief that has deadened his target but meets it with hostility and a repeated threat of violence. Sympathy hardens into menace as patience with another's misery runs out.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who performs suffering for attention. The narrator is exhausted by the relentless bleeding of misery outward while the inner state those complaints describe hardens into a frozen, self-sustaining numbness that sympathy can no longer reach or alter.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~over-my-head":{"haiku":"A person is overwhelmed by romantic love that makes them feel capable and elevated, drowning in intense emotions that run them over.","opus":"A departure from the nu-metal band's heavier aggression, this track voices awed gratitude toward someone who dries the speaker's tears and makes him feel capable of anything. That person is over his head in every sense, both beyond him and lifting him from beneath. Faith in what cannot be seen recurs as the hook, devotion mingling with the hell in his own head that these feelings run over.","sonnet":"An overwhelmed person marvels at how another lifts them beyond their own self-image, pulling them up from an inner chaos they cannot manage alone. The relationship is almost theological — the other exceeds comprehension, operates on faith rather than proof, and makes the speaker feel capable of carrying what would otherwise crush them.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~pity":{"haiku":"Self-directed shame radiates outward while refusing pity becomes impossible, the speaker trapped between needing compassion and despising its requirement.","opus":"Nu-metal aggression aimed inward, someone serving their pain up for consumption while violently refusing anyone's pity, sensing something wrong they can't name or feel. Numbness and defiance fused into a single snarl.","sonnet":"A person resists the sympathy of others while simultaneously admitting they cannot feel or understand what is wrong with them — rejecting pity as condescension but unable to answer their own question about what they actually need. The self-alienation is complete.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~regret":{"haiku":"A person confronts someone who betrayed them verbally and destructively; they declare that actions and words cannot be undone and will produce lifelong regret.","opus":"A venomous kiss-off to someone whose cruelty and betrayal shattered a promising start, insisting there's no undoing it and that they'll be sorry. Swipes at their phony piety and vanity sharpen the track into a score-settling after a relationship went rotten.","sonnet":"A furious confrontation with someone who crossed a line and said things that cannot be taken back — the narrator telling them their self-righteousness and cruelty will permanently haunt them, and that their actions have made forgiveness impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~sinner":{"opus":"Drowning Pool snarl against judgment and hypocrisy, a defiant sinner who refuses to be cornered or lectured and turns the accusation back on the accuser. The cross and useless talk frame it as a rejection of self-righteous religion. It is about owning one's flaws and demanding others raise their own hands, since everyone is a sinner too.","sonnet":"A confrontation with religious judgment and the hypocrisy of those who point fingers — the narrator rejects being cornered or lectured about sin, insisting that the accusers are equally complicit. The crowd being addressed mirrors the individual: everyone in the room is a sinner.","src":"sonnet"},
"drowning-pool~step-up":{"haiku":"Ambition without reward; whoever steps up gets knocked down, a cycle of failed advancement and suffering.","opus":"The nu-metal band throws down a blunt barroom dare, warning that anyone who squares up will be dropped, that the window to back off has closed and a line's been crossed. Unyielding and pure confrontation, its stomping aggression suits the taunt, all coiled threat and refusal to relent.","sonnet":"A confrontational stance toward anyone who challenges the narrator: the warning is clear and delivered without malice. Whoever steps forward gets knocked back — not out of cruelty but because the narrator has nothing left to lose and will not be moved.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~think":{"haiku":"Urgency to reconsider one's life before it's too late or taken away. The song repeatedly asks listeners to remember what matters as they face inevitable loss.","opus":"Drowning Pool set self-preservation against death, insisting no one would surrender a life even while being marched toward destruction. Memories of what living once felt like clash with the bleak march toward the end, alongside a promise to keep someone's spot as one voice fades. The pounded command to think turns basic survival into open defiance of giving up.","sonnet":"An appeal to conscious awareness before life is surrendered passively to forces that would consume it. Against the drift toward a slaughterhouse of routine and forgetting, the song insists on remembrance — of what life once was, and what would be lost in giving it up without thought.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~this-life":{"haiku":"Life inflicts accumulating pain despite endurance, leaving the speaker as sole architect of their destruction. Blame collapses inward as external forces remain immovable.","opus":"Exhaustion and self-blame as a life feels like it's slowly killing the narrator, with the hard truth that no one else can save a person from themselves.","sonnet":"A determined, self-blaming devotion: the speaker will wait through cold and sleet for someone, roam to the edges of the earth, while acknowledging that this life — and this love — is killing them, and they are the one to blame.","src":"haiku"},
"drowning-pool~turn-so-cold":{"haiku":"A relationship withers as one partner grows distant and refuses communication; the speaker's effort and vulnerability are unreciprocated.","opus":"The slow death of a relationship through emotional withholding, the narrator getting only silence and coldness in return no matter what he gives, sensing their time slipping away. The deeper read is the pain of loving someone who won't reciprocate or communicate, trading himself endlessly for a partner who keeps turning colder.","sonnet":"A relationship frustration song where one person keeps pulling away, going cold, never saying what they need — the narrator keeps offering himself and getting silence and distance in return. The central wound is that the other person won't communicate, and the relationship is slipping away through that silence.","src":"haiku"},
"drug-church~bliss-out":{"haiku":"A palm reader predicts endless suffering; with no ambulance to call, the speaker anticipates an unmarked grave. The mother tries to start over with a new child while the first is forgotten.","opus":"Fronted by Patrick Kindlon's deadpan wit, the band sketches a bleak scene of stress, a dead phone, and no help coming, then pivots into gallows humor about a pauper's burial and a mother better off moving on. Fortune-teller fatalism and a self-mocking wake dress worthlessness in bruised comedy, the closing refrain shrugging that there's ample cause to despise him.","sonnet":"A darkly comic reckoning with a life dismissed as expendable — no ambulance called, an unmarked grave, a mother choosing to begin again. The narrator observes the vigil candles, the performative grief, and punctures it all with the sardonic question of why anyone would bother lying about the loss.","src":"haiku"},
"drug-church~chow":{"opus":"Patrick Kindlon's blunt wisdom that nothing reliably works and most minds are shallow, so a tight, small circle is how you keep yourself intact. Cynical on the surface, it's really hard-won advice about guarding your peace.","src":"opus"},
"drug-church~demolition-man":{"haiku":"Destruction and systematic erasure define the operative; the speaker is an instrument of demolition.","opus":"Envying animals their built-in reason to exist while skewering modern life as directionless — just dead-end jobs and painkillers. The bleak nub is that people show up with no factory-set heading, an inner needle whirling endlessly until it abruptly stops.","sonnet":"Envy of animals with clear purpose — working dogs are happy doing exactly what they were built for; humans have call centres and percocets, rudderless boats they drill holes in, no hardwired north. The demolition machine \"did its job perfect\" while mankind guesses from birth to the sharp stop at the end, made to crash through walls and learn nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"drug-church~million-miles-of-fun":{"haiku":"A person rejects news and information overload, mutilating their senses rather than absorb more despair about the world's dysfunction.","opus":"A darkly comic scream against information overload and doom-scrolling: overwhelmed by everything he reads and hears about how the world works, the narrator fantasizes about gouging out his eyes and slicing his ears to escape, concluding simply that he needs less news.","sonnet":"News-saturation driving someone to self-destruction — the constant stream of information about how the world works is so overwhelming that the speaker imagines gouging out his eyes and slicing off his ears just to stop receiving it.","src":"haiku"},
"drug-church~myopic":{"haiku":"The speaker sees only close-up pain, unable to perceive larger context or escape route.","opus":"A man who was slow to notice how the world scammed and mishandled him, finally seeing clearly with glasses on. He lands on wary vigilance and forgiveness over bitterness, while admitting apologies rarely wash anything clean.","sonnet":"A slow learner's account of arriving, late, at a clear-eyed view of the world — the county fair as metaphor for rigged systems, friendships, politics, all weighted games. Apologies are dismissed as wedding-night flings, insufficient to clean the damage, and the narrator settles into a vigilant, non-bitter cynicism that forgives flat tires and cheating exes but refuses to be made to remember.","src":"haiku"},
"drug-church~someday-i-suppose":{"haiku":"Memory fails and certainty dissolves; the speaker postpones resolution indefinitely, recognizing plans distort with every attempt to clarify them.","opus":"A cover of the Mighty Mighty Bosstones' shrug at chronic procrastination, the forgotten place and the girl he swore he'd return to always deferred to someday. The joke is self-aware: the more he tries to sort his life the more distorted it gets, so he leaves it unsorted.","sonnet":"A cheerful nihilism about forgotten places, forgotten people, and unwritten plans — the more the narrator tries to sort their life out the more distorted it gets, so they defer everything to someday, which probably will not come.","src":"haiku"},
"drug-church~tiresome":{"haiku":"Self-pity and depression become exhausting and pointless; a person recognizes their tiresome cycle of weakness and despair as ultimately worthless.","opus":"A blunt intervention against wallowing self-pity, the narrator having crawled out of his own rock to find nothing there but excuses and a rotting inertia. Tough love: your endless misery just makes you exhausting to others.","sonnet":"A song about self-pitying depression and the moment of finally seeing through it — the narrator describes the damp rot of someone who cultivates weakness and excuses until their own pulse wonders what the point is, calling that mode of existence tiresome.","src":"haiku"},
"drumcorps~the-importance-of-stealth":{"haiku":"Internalized oppression teaches invisibility—fear codes deep while the mind becomes a landscape of vultures.","opus":"The hypervigilance of a life lived small and afraid, anxiety coded so deep that a door becomes a threat. It's about survival by staying invisible, head down against a culture of lies felt as rotten to the core.","sonnet":"Anxiety coded deep in the body — 'why does that door make anxiety?' — translates into survival tactics: eyes down, head down, make no target; the 'mind vultures' and 'liar culture rotten to the core' name the external threat that has been internalised as perpetual vigilance.","src":"haiku"},
"dry-kill-logic~vices":{"opus":"A weary reckoning with treachery and years thrown away, where those closest inflict the deepest wounds and comforting deceptions conceal harsh realities. Resigned that things always end the same way, the narrator opts to sever ties and illuminate his own path forward, treating the destruction of what's behind him as its own grim source of guidance.","sonnet":"The narrator addresses someone who never learns from the cycles of self-destruction and sacrifice they enable, warning that burning bridges only briefly lights the way forward and that redemption keeps slipping out of reach while the same patterns repeat.","src":"sonnet"},
"duckwrth~start-a-riot":{"opus":"Written for the Spider-Verse, it channels Miles Morales' underdog origin into a rallying cry, every hero needing a theme song. Beneath the comic-book bombast is a generational call to seize power and start something.","src":"opus"},
"duit-dawid-podsiad-o-pro8l3m~freon":{"haiku":"Illicit passion hidden in dark spaces, lovers who reconnect despite time and shame, choosing forbidden pleasure over propriety.","opus":"Two people meeting for illicit, wordless sex in a cold neon city, the world hating them for it so they'd rather die than stop; years later they repeat it exactly, an obsessive love better killed than named, doomed and defiantly savored.","sonnet":"A Polish song about two people who keep falling back into each other's bodies despite knowing they should stop — the air between them is cold as freon, the night club across the way witnesses their reunion, and the world's hostility to the feeling only deepens it. Years later they return to do it again, wordlessly.","src":"haiku"},
"duke-dumont~ocean-drive":{"haiku":"A high-speed descent toward dissolution where lovers dance with ruin and time fragments along an apocalyptic highway with no way out.","opus":"A doomed romance driving flat-out toward disaster, two lovers dancing with the devil on a highway to nowhere, pretending at love while a storm closes in and they just hold on.","sonnet":"Two people racing down the boulevard on a half-empty tank, pretending to be in love while a storm closes in, caught between the point of no return and the wish they could take it all back — dancing with the devil on a highway to never.","src":"haiku"},
"duke-ellington~solitude":{"opus":"A jazz standard of lonely heartbreak — alone and stalked by memories of a vanished love, the singer sits steeped in gloom, half-fearing for his sanity, and prays to heaven to return the love he's lost.","src":"opus"},
"dune-rats~red-light-green-light":{"haiku":"Mutual dislike and indifference define a relationship that persists anyway; both parties reject the other while remaining entangled.","opus":"Slacker punk shrugging off mutual contempt with the vain and the cool crowd, indifferent to being liked because not caring is the whole ethos. Impatient restlessness dressed as apathy.","sonnet":"A blunt mutual dismissal between two parties who find each other lame and vain, the speaker shrugging off judgment and asserting indifference, with the stop-go imagery suggesting a restless, directionless energy neither side can resolve.","src":"haiku"},
"dvne~en-ma-eli":{"haiku":"Ancient conflict and mythological transformation render humanity obsolete; triumphant machines rise as successor species amid cosmic upheaval.","opus":"Post-metal built on Dune-adjacted myth, blazing steel-cloaked hounds and augmented flesh triumphant amid rising tempests. It reads as a grand elegy for warrior lore surpassing the creed that made it, all storm and ascension.","sonnet":"Drawing on ancient Mesopotamian mythology, the song depicts the rise of an armoured, synthetic race — the Hounds of Tindalos — who surpass human belief systems through augmentation and endure to stand victorious as eternal lords over a purified world.","src":"haiku"},
"dvsn~try-effortless":{"haiku":"A lover transcends the speaker's usual detachment, breaking personal rules and demanding effort he rarely gives to anyone.","opus":"An R&B admission from someone who never chases love, breaking his own rules for a person worth the effort. The tension is a guarded man surprised into vulnerability, hands-up surrender to a connection nobody planned.","sonnet":"A self-proclaimed non-chaser admits that one person has broken his rule against pursuing love, making him try and break his own codes for the first time. The song shifts into a second movement celebrating that person's effortless magnetism, with the speaker surrendering pride and admitting he'd do anything to keep her.","src":"haiku"},
"dwellings~devices":{"haiku":"Self-sabotage and competitive compulsion erode relationships and meaning, leaving the speaker untethered and desperate for connection.","opus":"Memory loss and existential drift, gripping the faint hope that anything still holds meaning before oblivion arrives. It's about being abandoned to fend for yourself in a breakdown, watching people compress feeling into resentment, and fearing solitude.","sonnet":"Left to their own devices in a personal crisis, the speaker cycles through forgotten events, emotional suppression, and financial failure — \"I emptied out my empty pockets\" — acknowledging the mess but not knowing how to fix it.","src":"haiku"},
"dwellings~happens-all-the-time":{"haiku":"Intoxication and desire collapse into each other as the speaker surrenders to another person's gravity despite recognizing the self-destructive trajectory with perfect clarity.","opus":"Waking disoriented and haunted by someone whose glance undoes him, drawn helplessly to gamble on a reckless faith and swallow whatever they offer despite the harm.","sonnet":"Waking disorientation caused by overwhelming attraction: every time the eyes close the speaker can't make out what comes next, and a single glance from the other person makes them melt — a \"sicker than a dog\" pull that makes them want to take a careless leap of faith.","src":"haiku"},
"dying-wish~fragments-of-a-bitter-memory":{"opus":"A daughter's bitter grief over an alcoholic father who curdled from the man she loved into someone she despises, mourning the love that vanished and confessing she'd bury him if she could.","sonnet":"A child's memory of a parent destroyed by alcoholism: the father who gave them safety and comfort became a drunk, abusive devil over time, and the narrator — now grown — holds the fragments of love and hatred together, wishing they could bury him.","src":"sonnet"},
"dying-wish~watch-my-promise-die":{"haiku":"Self-sabotage disguised as acceptance, drowning in shame and doubt while pushing away any lifeline offered.","opus":"A spiral of self-sabotage and internalized poison, the narrator distancing from every blessing and letting others' venom finish the work, begging not to let people down while doubt eats whether peace was ever meant for them.","sonnet":"About the internal collapse of self-promise under depression and external pressure — the narrator had committed to something (staying clean, staying alive, staying good) and watches that promise die as toxic influences and self-doubt consume them from within. The desire to keep the promise is still there; it's just losing.","src":"haiku"},
"dyro~memory-bank":{"opus":"A festival anthem about storing joyful moments as memories that outlast the night, insisting nobody ought to be left dancing alone. Simple hedonism with a sincere heart: make history together while you still can.","src":"opus"},
"dz-deathrays~black-rat":{"haiku":"Temporal dislocation and decay mark a narrator trapped in failed love and a cursed city; they've stayed too long and cannot escape.","opus":"A wry, self-aware elegy for the barfly life, someone who's haunted his local pubs so long the drinking has become a homesick loyalty to a hollow home. The party celebrated and mourned as a slow disappearance.","sonnet":"The speaker drifts through a beloved city as a scavenger figure — haunting bars, looking for someone lost to the past, realizing that the city and the relationship that bound him to it were both dead from the start.","src":"haiku"},
"dz-deathrays~blood-on-my-leather":{"haiku":"A restless figure battles fatigue and emptiness, caught between chaotic energy and the desire to escape through numbness or violence.","opus":"The Australian dance-punk duo bottle sleepless, self-wrecking nightlife, bargains struck with a mood, barroom benders, and a narrator strung out and refusing to nod off at a dangerous moment. Streets boil over and the authorities lose control. Their scuzzy garage energy powers the bloodied hook, hedonism and burnout smeared into raw momentum.","sonnet":"Post-breakup wreckage played at full throttle: disorientation, rage, and reckless energy converge after someone disrupts everything. The blood on the leather is the residue of that collision — chaos in the highlands, societal friction everywhere, and no clean exit from any of it.","src":"haiku"},
"dz-deathrays~cops-capacity":{"haiku":"Parties are chaotic events caught between police enforcement and venue limits, embodying trouble, wildness, and rebellion. The crowd's chant contrasts what east and west desire from nightlife.","opus":"A garage-punk party chant that romanticizes the wildness of a house party pushed past its limits, all the fun measured by the threat of cops and overcrowding, celebrating chaos as the whole point.","sonnet":"A pure party anthem with no narrative — the premise is that good parties are wild and get shut down, so the crowd chants cops versus capacity as competing forces. There is no emotional content beyond the invitation to lose control.","src":"haiku"},
"dz-deathrays~gina-works-at-hearts":{"haiku":"A woman trapped in nightlife and sex work craves attention despite her emptiness and pain. The song portrays her desire for recognition as both her motivation and her downfall.","opus":"A bleak sketch of Gina, who tends bar at a club, craves notice, and disappears into drugs; her lifeless stare and ruined grin are portrayed as commodities available for purchase.","sonnet":"Gina works at a venue or club not out of passion or money but for the attention, and the narrator observes her dead-eyed exhaustion with detachment — she is numb, running on drugs and borrowed affection, and he can only transactionally acknowledge what she has become.","src":"haiku"},
"dz-deathrays~keep-myself-on-edge":{"haiku":"The speaker cultivates anxiety and tension as a way to feel alive, fearing both connection and healing.","opus":"A confession from someone hooked on constant anxiety, refusing to look at what is chasing him and instead fleeing blindly forward. Having grown addicted to feeling frantic and wound too tight, he keeps deliberately unsettling himself because that agitated state is the only one that feels normal.","sonnet":"Anxiety turned into a compulsion: the narrator has become addicted to the tightly wound feeling of being on edge, preferring the wound-up state to the blankness of calm even as they know it's not sustainable.","src":"haiku"},
"dz-deathrays~reflective-skull":{"haiku":"A mysterious figure knows someone's secrets; diamond eyes lock onto truth, exposing what lies hidden while reflecting back mortality and surveillance.","opus":"A garage-thrash blast about being drawn to someone whose eyes see straight through you, all secrets and paranoia dressed as party menace. The words matter less than the leering compulsion, a fixation you can't shake even as it exposes you.","sonnet":"A chaotic, fragmentary song about a person whose secrets are being seen through and exposed by someone with penetrating eyes — the 'reflective skull' seems to be a figure of unavoidable self-revelation, but the lyrics are impressionistic enough to resist a clean single reading.","src":"haiku"},
"dz-deathrays~shred-for-summer":{"haiku":"Demonic intensity and sonic assault free the spirit from constraint; transgressive art and destructive release become redemptive.","opus":"Riff-worshipping party metal that finds purpose in nothing deeper than volume, sweat, and summer, offering the simple communion of feeling good and going heavy. Devotion aimed squarely at the guitar itself.","sonnet":"A hedonistic summer anthem wrapped around metal riffs and a skull on the mantle: the speaker wants to shred, drink, blow speakers, and take the listener somewhere loud and heavy, with demons and guitars and the pure physical release of noise as the only goal.","src":"haiku"},
"eagles-of-death-metal~i-only-want-you":{"haiku":"Emotional cruelty masquerades as honesty; the narrator confesses they cannot reciprocate love, choosing to inflict pain rather than pretend devotion.","opus":"Hughes voicing a predatory, honest cad who warns his partner not to fall in love because he only wants the wanting, cruelty he admits he enjoys. The swagger is the confession of someone who can't or won't love back.","sonnet":"A narrator confesses with brutal candor that they are incapable of love — they sabotage closeness the moment it becomes real, enjoy the other person's suffering, and will only ever want rather than love them.","src":"haiku"},
"earlyrise~dreaming-in-sepia":{"opus":"A cold-eyed critique of a culture of curated appearances, false validation and narcissism, warning that once the fog lifts only ashes remain. It questions whether there was ever any real calling beneath the blissful denial.","src":"opus"},
"earlyrise~eye-of-the-evil":{"opus":"A mythic call to face down darkness, following the elders' whispers and lighting a torch of reason against a hidden evil. The heroism belongs to the banished and disbelieved, rising out of the water to fight anyway.","src":"opus"},
"earshot~wait":{"haiku":"The speaker waits for connection and healing but accepts they are forever alone, finally choosing not to wait anymore.","opus":"A ballad of deep isolation, the singer facing fears he assumed were behind him and dreading an endless wait to be heard, held, or mended, resigned to permanent solitude until a late turn where he refuses to keep waiting. Tears rinse away a hidden self's wounds. The American post-grunge band trades in the exposed, brooding alt-rock of the early-2000s radio era.","sonnet":"Chronic loneliness framed as perpetual waiting — for someone to hear, touch, or heal a person who has spent so long alone that the isolation has become a kind of identity. By the end the stance shifts slightly: a refusal to keep waiting passively, though the hunger underneath remains unchanged and the comfort of another person still feels impossibly out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"earth-caller~alone":{"opus":"The exhausted end of a one-sided relationship where one partner felt fine while the other emptied out, unheard through years of silence. The heart turned to stone, and leaving becomes the only self-respecting move left.","src":"opus"},
"earth-tongue~dungeon-vision":{"haiku":"Escape from confinement and reaching toward light and transcendence, overcoming darkness through vision beyond present walls.","opus":"Psychedelic dungeon imagery of being trapped behind stone walls, groping for a key, that reads as a metaphor for confinement and the ache for sun and open earth. The escape is glimpsed but never quite reached.","sonnet":"Trapped in psychological or physical confinement — stone walls, an icy claw in the corner, falling on all fours in darkness while dreaming of sun on skin and leaves in wind. The 'dungeon vision' is the narrowed perception of captivity; the door exists but the darkness holds back.","src":"haiku"},
"earth-wind-fire~getaway":{"haiku":"The speaker offers escape from pressure and hurt, urging a companion to leave troubled circumstances behind by following the right direction.","opus":"A summons to flee a place of hardship and the strain of the people crowding it, following signals that point true and refusing to second-guess the path chosen. Departure becomes empowerment rather than retreat. Earth, Wind and Fire, pioneers of lush 1970s funk and soul, lift this call to leave into buoyant horns and harmony, making leaving sound like release.","sonnet":"Two people tired of pressure, stagnation, and sleepless nights decide simply to leave — not for any specific destination, but away from a troubled world that offers no traction. The invitation is warm and insistent: take my hand, trust the signs, stop doubting the road. Escape is framed as clarity, not cowardice.","src":"haiku"},
"earthists~home":{"opus":"A grief-stricken return home from a cursed, unforgivable relationship, unable to let go of the pain still held in someone's hands, torn between blame and the inability to forget.","sonnet":"A return to a place associated with deep pain and a complicated person the narrator cannot forgive or let go of, feeling the pull of old wounds in an autumn landscape while acknowledging the relationship is cursed and the guilt may never lift.","src":"sonnet"},
"earthists~purge-me":{"haiku":"A speaker observes temptation and delusion around them while grappling with the boundary between beauty and despair, holding loneliness as a companion.","opus":"A dreamlike, unsettling meditation on surrender, drawn by a fragrant lure toward a crimson fantasy body of water, tempted to plunge into something warm and perilous as depleted onlookers watch from the bank. The recurring counsel is to cradle your solitude rather than flee it, farewell doubling as escape. Earthists are a Japanese progressive metalcore band, and the imagery carries that atmospheric, translated-poetic quality of the djent-adjacent Japanese scene.","sonnet":"Dissociation at the edge of self-destruction — a speaker drawn toward a red lake of oblivion, watching someone else's apparent dancing that is actually desperate struggle, and clinging to a voice heard from a dissolving distance. The dominant instruction is to hold loneliness gently rather than flee it, though the poem keeps circling the seduction of simply stepping out and never returning.","src":"haiku"},
"earthists~resonating-light-2-0":{"haiku":"Connection trembles through shared heartbeats; together seeking life's meaning, never forgetting the panoramas witnessed in unity.","opus":"Fear of stagnation and inability to speak trapping the narrator in a room, and the tender resolve to move forward alongside someone rather than alone. It's about not forgetting a shared vision while seeking life's meaning together.","sonnet":"Paralysed by fear and unable to speak, the speaker wonders whether staying locked inside would freeze everything in place, then finds the answer in a shared heartbeat: opening the eyes, moving forward together, and never forgetting the panorama they saw side by side.","src":"haiku"},
"earthshatter~ghost-orchid":{"haiku":"The narrator is consumed and drained by someone who disgusts them like an open sore. They are sick of the lies and hypocrisy and swear never to see that face again.","opus":"The metalcore band channel the aftermath of a devouring one-sided attachment, the speaker peering into a shattered reflection to confirm he's still alive after letting someone strip him bare. He refuses to apologize again and vows never to look upon that person once more. The twist lands at the end, where the hypocrite he denounces proves to be himself, blame collapsing inward.","sonnet":"A person confronts someone who consumed them entirely and left nothing but wreckage — a relationship that hollowed the speaker out and revealed a near-demonic presence masquerading as intimacy. The refusal of apology is absolute: the speaker will not normalize the damage or extend the fiction of similarity between them.","src":"haiku"},
"earthside~we-who-lament":{"haiku":"Souls rendered voiceless and forceless by forces that blind and splinter them ask whether any of it will matter at death, slowly groping toward some individual path through the wreckage of collective grief.","opus":"A sweeping meditation on death and legacy, wondering whether the way we spent our lives will count for anything once we're gone, unnamed and mute once more. The sorrow is that we may guide others forward only by consuming ourselves in the process.","sonnet":"Mortality and helplessness addressed collectively: when we die will it matter how we lived — souls in cinders, blinded, voiceless, faceless and forceless again — the question of whether we can find a way to realize who we are hangs unanswered, the lamentation itself the only remaining act with any weight.","src":"haiku"},
"eat-your-heart-out~cellophane":{"haiku":"A person wraps their thoughts away to numb pain, displaying themselves while cutting out gray emotions. They wage ongoing battle against wasted days, always starting fresh in hope of perfection.","opus":"The pop-punk band frames mental struggle through a striking central image, a mind wrapped and transparent so its darkness can be excised and traded away for a brighter tomorrow. Restless and out of patience, the narrator gathers the fragments while shouldering the fault. Its bright melodic drive powers the effort to confront feelings that keep coming loose.","sonnet":"A mind under emotional overload wraps itself in a protective layer, cutting away the grey noise to survive. The narrator takes blame for the numbness, paces through exhaustion, and defers real feeling to some better day — surviving by making thought itself transparent and manageable.","src":"haiku"},
"eat-your-heart-out~closer-to-the-sun":{"haiku":"Shedding stagnant old habits and identities to grow and bloom toward light, regardless of pain or the loss of familiar roots.","opus":"A defiant declaration of growth against someone who could not bear to watch you change. Being cut down and outgrown becomes the condition for blooming, walking a bloodied path that is finally your own.","sonnet":"The narrator breaks free from people who resist their growth — who pull them back toward stale habits and a predictable path — choosing instead to follow their own direction even through the pain of that departure, blooming toward something beyond what others mapped out for them.","src":"haiku"},
"eat-your-heart-out~down":{"haiku":"Toxic codependency pulls downward; words become weapons embedding in veins while escape routes collapse into descent.","opus":"Codependency as a downward spiral, craving the very embrace that debases you and letting someone define who you are. The only exit acknowledged is down, the addiction to a toxic bond stronger than the will to leave.","sonnet":"A codependent dynamic in which another person's words have colonised the speaker entirely — 'your words in my mouth' — and the only way out is down; the cold embrace is bitter and craved simultaneously, the speaker unable to break free despite naming it clearly.","src":"haiku"},
"eat-your-heart-out~drag-me-down":{"haiku":"Refusal of conformity under pressure: the speaker resists being reduced or reshaped to fit others' expectations, seeing through manipulation and refusing to be weaponized by those threatened by their difference.","opus":"The Australian band refuses to be reshaped into someone else's mold, the speaker declaring he has seen through the manipulation and won't play along. He calls out a person who can only shine by pushing him down, the recurring warning about surrendering to conformity sharpening a broader stand against being flattened into whatever others demand.","sonnet":"A narrator refuses to reshape herself to fit someone else's frame, recognizing that the other's self-aggrandizement requires her diminishment. The resistance is identity-level — she names the manipulation clearly and declines to be cast as a casualty of someone else's need to feel elevated.","src":"haiku"},
"eat-your-heart-out~headfirst":{"opus":"Self-sabotage as a thrill, diving off the edge with eyes closed because numbness feels like being alive. The song wrestles with whether the decline is destiny or a line the narrator can still choose to draw.","src":"opus"},
"eat-your-heart-out~heavy-with-envy":{"haiku":"Someone drowns in envious desire, watching others' lives and losing sight of their own worth; they ache to escape the comparison cycle.","opus":"Worn down by monotony and aching for a different life, the narrator hauls her possessions from one place to the next, jaded and burdened by envy of greener pastures that keeps beckoning. The arc bends toward recentering, gratitude, and shaking off regret. Eat Your Heart Out, an Australian melodic pop-punk band fronted by Caitlin Henry, voice this restlessness with earnest, hook-driven longing for contentment.","sonnet":"A person paralyzed by comparison and restlessness watches others living what feels like a better life, unable to find gratitude inside the routine. The envy is physical — a dead weight. The song charts the effort to reorient, clear the accumulated resentment, and reclaim enough stillness to stop living trapped between the life at hand and the one imagined.","src":"haiku"},
"eat-your-heart-out~nowhere":{"haiku":"Falling without escape; sense disappears while chasing solutions, leaving only apathy and the realization that there is nowhere to run.","opus":"The Australian pop-punk band dramatizes an anxiety spiral, coaxing the listener to ground themselves and breathe before warning that overthinking only worsens the plunge into panic with seemingly no exit. A closing image of a rescued descent offers reprieve. Bright, driving hooks carry the fraught lyrics in the genre's revival style.","sonnet":"The rabbit hole as emotional breakdown — a freefall into apathy and self-defeat with no visible floor. The speaker acknowledges the spiral without being able to stop it, but keeps returning to the image of a parachute catching you before you hit, suggesting that collapse is not necessarily permanent even when it feels absolute and the exit is nowhere in sight.","src":"haiku"},
"eat-your-heart-out~patience":{"haiku":"Aimless drift and internal fragmentation prevent progress as the narrator wastes time questioning their purpose while watching life pass unexamined.","opus":"Drifting through aimless days of low-grade depression while worrying that a friend is losing themselves to someone toxic, the concern for another sharper than the concern for self.","sonnet":"The speaker drifts pleasantly numb, losing days at a window where everything seems fine, but the repeated question — are you losing yourself to somebody else, taking hits instead of protecting yourself — suggests the calm surface covers a slow dissolution of identity.","src":"haiku"},
"eat-your-heart-out~same-stars":{"haiku":"Distance separates but shared perspective endures; though paths diverge, the connection remains constant like constellations watched from different places.","opus":"The Australian pop-punk outfit softens for a warm reflection on a friendship worn thin by distance, accepting that two people can follow separate paths yet stay linked, still under one shared sky. Change is compared to the ocean pulling people away and returning them, so that reunions pick up as if no gap ever opened. A nostalgic ode to bonds that bend but hold.","sonnet":"A tribute to a friendship that survived growing apart — two people who took different roads and found that distance only changed their proximity, not their bond. The shared sky overhead becomes the constant: proof that drift and change and even long silences do not erase what was built, and that picking back up exactly where you left off is still possible.","src":"haiku"},
"eat-your-heart-out~scissors-in-my-skin":{"haiku":"Psychological vivisection embeds as permanent scar tissue; the beloved dissects the speaker's soul while denying the violence.","opus":"Being treated as someone else's plaything, dissected and stitched back together on their terms, the damage sewn permanently into the mind. It's a raw account of emotional violation that lingers in every shared memory.","sonnet":"Another person's words and scrutiny are framed as physical dissection — poking holes, pulling loose threads, scissors in skin — the speaker stitched up but permanently altered, the damage embedded in clothes and memory, 'over and over again.'","src":"haiku"},
"eat-your-heart-out~sour":{"opus":"Watching someone hide behind numbness and chemical escape while insisting they're fine, comatose but claiming to be awake. The song aches at a loved one's self-harm and the addiction to feeling anything, even pain.","src":"opus"},
"eat-your-heart-out~twenty-something":{"opus":"The disorienting in-between of your twenties, adrift and grounded at the same time, doubting everything you assumed about who you are. It cops to hiding inside a cracked fantasy rather than confronting a reality it finds wildly oversold.","src":"opus"},
"ebonivory~persist":{"haiku":"Encourages persistence through exhaustion and suffering as signs of remaining alive, yet recognizes the world's corruption and personal deterioration. Endurance becomes moral struggle.","opus":"A pained address to someone trapped in their own misery, the singer devastated to watch them suffer their way to understanding, himself reduced to little more than a face in a phone in a life he's no longer part of. Its grim thesis: pain is what completes us, and anguish is somehow what keeps him going. The Australian prog-metal band leans into its dense, intense post-djent sound.","sonnet":"Two voices alternating in exhaustion — one watching someone they love sleep through their life, the other awake too long and sickened by the world spinning without mercy. They share the conviction that suffering is somehow foundational, that the urgency and misery keeping them alive are not bugs but the mechanism itself, and that connection has already thinned to a name on a screen.","src":"haiku"},
"ebonivory~window-man":{"haiku":"Isolation calcifies into self-harm as the narrator bears witness to internal darkness, searching for meaning in wounds while choosing oblivion over continued suffering.","opus":"A harrowing plunge into depression, self-harm and suicidal despair, the speaker begging to be ended and repeatedly urged not to forget his own name as he fights the fires of self-hatred and hopes his death might let another live.","sonnet":"A voice oscillates between the wish to die and the hard work of continuing — dreams of dying nightly, fires of self-harm used as coping, and a final turn toward starting over, one slow step at a time, letting go so someone else can live on.","src":"haiku"},
"ecca-vandal~bleach":{"haiku":"Radical reinvention requires destroying everything from the past—shame, identity, and memory dissolve down the drain as the old self is methodically erased.","opus":"A cathartic purge of a former self and a toxic scene, the speaker pouring everything she used to be down the drain to start again. Outgrowing her name, her neighborhood's shame and a self-destructive crowd, she refuses to live half-dead and steps clear of anyone in her way.","sonnet":"Washing everything away — fear and toothpaste down the drain, old name outgrown, neighbourhood shame left behind — reframed as renewal rather than erasure. The sink full of bleach is a starting-over image, not a harm image.","src":"haiku"},
"ecca-vandal~bleed-but-never-die":{"haiku":"Suffering and violence become proof of survival; the speaker bleeds continuously yet refuses to submit to death, drawing strength from relentless pain.","opus":"A woman insists she's fine while visibly hemorrhaging—pills, violence, and self-harm framed as normalcy against onlookers' alarm. The refusal to die becomes both grim resilience and a taunt to the fools she's finally done with.","sonnet":"A defiant, darkly comic song about surviving violence and pain — the speaker has been bleeding for days but insists she is fine, swings back at those who try to dominate or diminish her, and refuses to be killed by any of it, treating her own resilience as something fierce and slightly terrifying.","src":"haiku","fable":"Vandal weaponizes the language of invincibility to voice its opposite: numbness sold as strength, where chronic bleeding and a pill that flattens both flight and violence into dull weather register as nothing alarming at all. The chorus's frayed, elongated vowels are the sound of insisting all is well while the syllable itself comes apart. Tellingly, one chorus brags she survives forever and the next confesses she expects the end, the same defiant swagger carrying both a survivor's boast and a quiet death-drift. By the close her only agency is refusal, met by a robotic answering echo, and the private I becomes we, cracking the mask outward as it clamps down hardest.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"invincibility language selling its own opposite as strength","built":"the same swagger carries a survivor's boast and a quiet death-drift in consecutive choruses"},
"ecca-vandal~broke-days-party-nights":{"opus":"A defiant refusal to let poverty dictate self-worth or joy; against voices demanding she follow orders and grow up, the narrator insists she can still party and live large while broke, rejecting the box society keeps trying to confine her in.","sonnet":"A rejection of the idea that financial poverty equals a lesser life — the narrator dismisses people who measure worth by money or career success and insists on living fully and on their own terms, even if it means being dismissed as irresponsible or going nowhere.","src":"sonnet"},
"ecca-vandal~came-here-for-the-loot":{"opus":"A confrontational anthem tearing down corrupt elites and conspiracy-poisoned discourse, the twist being that the powerful never came for truth or principle at all, only the take. It mocks left-right theater and tinfoil paranoia alike while promising a reckoning the powerful never saw arriving.","src":"opus"},
"ecca-vandal~cruising-to-self-soothe": {"haiku":"Solitary transcendence through motion and detachment; the speaker rises alone and refuses to look backward at loss.","opus":"Ascending solo to where she belongs and finding the summit cold but earned, leaving behind someone who held her back. Self-comfort turns into self-reliance: remade in a broken world, taking flight whether she falls or succeeds, stronger than she has ever been.","sonnet":"A post-breakup independence anthem — the speaker is moving up and forward without looking back, acknowledging that the top is cold but choosing it anyway over staying in a relationship that required self-suppression; the cruising metaphor suggests detached forward motion as emotional self-care.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup anthem that keeps letting the cold in. The chorus climbs the standard success narrative — rising to where she belongs, driving off and leaving the one she's addressing behind — but the title names the whole thing as a coping mechanism, and the hook itself concedes that the summit is freezing. Ecca Vandal sings the recovery cliches (rebuilt brand-new and in peak condition, airborne no matter whether the plane crashes or touches down) with just enough repetition that the affirmations start to sound like a mantra recited to convince the singer, not the ex. The verses tip the mask further: good moods and grand plans are dismissed as fads, and the closing dissolve into the bare word without strips the triumph down to what actually remains — an absence she keeps circling. The song is the gap between the up-tempo self-soothe and the loneliness it's soothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"recovery clichés repeated until they reveal what they cover","built":"the title names the triumph as coping before the song starts"},
"ecca-vandal~dance-in-debt":{"haiku":"Labor and exploitation strip independence; the speaker chooses freedom over servitude, rejecting ownership by refusing to remain economically bound to another's scheme.","opus":"A refusal to be owned, walking away from a job that grinds you to the bone to strike out alone. The scrappy defiance sneers at whatever the establishment is selling, insisting it just isn't worth buying.","sonnet":"A refusal to be owned: working to the bone, making cents and sense with nowhere to grow, and deciding to hit the streets rather than be the person someone else controls — \"what you got is what I'm not\" is the defiant arithmetic of someone walking out of a bad deal into their own life.","src":"haiku"},
"ecca-vandal~levitate-part-1-2":{"haiku":"Young alienation breeds escapism and dissociation; the generation refuses to repeat history and chooses instead to vanish into abstraction and denial.","opus":"Numb youth burning weekends and faking a mental state to appease parents, dreams returned unopened, choosing to sit life out rather than feel it. It builds to a generational protest against a system that abandoned its kids.","sonnet":"Flesh on weekends, dress dripping in sin, sleep refusing to come — a disaffected young person drifts through fake mental health, anime haze, and the suspicion that they were never supposed to be here, while the second half pivots to a wider indictment of a generation told to forget about it by adults who surrounded the whole situation.","src":"haiku"},
"ecca-vandal~molly":{"haiku":"Substance use magnifies emotional disconnection; time erodes bonds while intoxication obscures what was once clear.","opus":"A drug-fueled fallout where a lover only opens up once the speaker is high, and the connection they built dissolves under intoxication. Waiting to learn how to fly, she instead watches something real fade into a medicated haze.","sonnet":"A person who suspects their partner waited until they were high on MDMA to manipulate them or extract something from them — the drug-blurred state is used as a vector for exploitation, and by the time the speaker realizes it, the relationship is fading and what they built together is wasted.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title pun carries it: molly the drug and Molly the person collapse into one figure, so getting wrecked and losing her become a single vanishing, narrated from inside a fogged skull that keeps mislaying whatever passed between them. What lifts it past a party song is the charge tucked into the hook. The other party timed their move for the moment she was least defended, chose the high as their opening, so being intoxicated reads not as pleasure but as the condition of the betrayal. The refrain about learning to fly and the piling repetition of high enact the chemical loop, circling without landing. The last verses finally name the price flatly: something built together, then squandered, dissolving in real time while she despises being proven right.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance"],"means":"intoxication named as the condition of the betrayal, not its backdrop","built":"the title pun collapses the drug and the person into one vanishing narrated from inside the fog"},
"ecca-vandal~sorry-crash": {"haiku":"Drowning becomes inevitable as the speaker falls toward water, desperate and forced to go alone while the world watches with indifference.","opus":"A plunge toward the river staged as release, the fall itself uncertain to kill but chosen anyway. The refrain that none of it matters turns a suicidal image into exhausted defiance, watching oneself sink from way down south.","sonnet":"A speaker in total freefall — emotionally and perhaps literally — sinking beneath water, forcing themselves into high-visibility performance (getting their name in lights) while secretly treating the crash as irrelevant, as if neither fame nor drowning actually matters.","src":"haiku","fable":"A death drive dressed in the language of fame: the walk to the river doubles as the walk to notoriety. Setting the fear glowing like a signboard, running a stage name across some marquee, and picking out a spot on the riverbed to lie still all become one gesture — the album's unfollow-culture premise turned inward, self-erasure and self-promotion collapsed into a single dive. The refrain is where the mask slips. The body drops downward, past the surface, and never resurfaces, each descent tagged with a shrug that none of it counts. That repetition does the work the verses avoid, performing a numb detachment the ambition-talk was still trying to disguise. By the bridge the choice is gone. The will has drained out and only momentum is left, no one going down with the body, and when the eject call finally comes it comes too late. The closing chant strands us on the shoreline beside the wreck, the whole as-if-it-matters posture worn down from irony into flat, exhausted truth.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance"],"means":"self-erasure and self-promotion collapsed into one dive","built":"repeated refrain shrug performs the numb detachment verses disguise"},
"ecca-vandal~vertical-worlds": {"haiku":"Gender binaries shatter and reform, identity transcends rigid categories while society crumbles around those refusing to fit into its narrow scripts.","opus":"A gleeful refusal of fixed categories, gender and identity flipped and shed like seasons. The speaker skates over a rotten social order and insists on writing a new storyline where nothing you thought you knew stays settled.","sonnet":"A song about defying rigid gender norms: girls claiming boldness and confidence coded as masculine, boys embracing traits coded as feminine, the whole vertical hierarchy of gender being flipped and rewritten through attitude, movement, and refusal to conform to what came before.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dizziness anthem built on the verb to spin — the disorientation of being talked about, of rumour and inherited rules, reframed as the momentum of self-invention rather than a loss of balance. The chorus is the whole argument compressed into a mirror-symmetry: bodies and gestures cross the line between girl and boy in both directions at once, so that turning the stacked worlds upside down means toppling the fixed hierarchy the culture piles upright — its folklore, its small clauses buried at the bottom of the page, its books to be read the other way up. Vandal's punk-rap splice suits it: the earlier verses sit in the first person, scarred and singular, then the pronoun opens to a defiant we, and the refusal to let anyone get comfortable with what they know becomes a collective refusal to be pinned by any settled category. Underneath the play, the shedding — of season, skin, and given name — reads not as chaos but as a fresh script being drafted in real time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","freedom & escape"],"means":"disorientation reframed as momentum of self-invention","built":"singular first person opens outward into a defiant we"},
"echobreak~black-lotus":{"haiku":"Romantic investment withers into contempt; broken promises and wasted devotion leave the speaker unable to recognize or forgive the lover's transformation.","opus":"A breakup framed as a beautiful thing gone poisonous, a lover who dragged him under while he tried to hold them up, now just something withering. The tension between wanting peace and never seeing them the same again.","sonnet":"A love that smothered and dragged the speaker under has burned out completely — the beloved is now a withered black lotus, and the speaker oscillates between wanting to fight for repair and accepting that the damage is too deep and too late.","src":"haiku"},
"echobreak~f-b-v":{"haiku":"One-night transactions replace genuine connection as the narrator becomes a temporary escape hatch, desire and indifference merging in morning abandonment.","opus":"A frank ode to the one-night stand, going out to hook up with no illusions, warning the other not to mistake a night of desire for love because by morning the speaker is gone.","sonnet":"A one-night-stand narrative from the club to someone's house: the speaker is physically swept up but emotionally detached, tripping over feelings he doesn't want and gone by morning.","src":"haiku"},
"ed-schrader-s-music-beat~dunce":{"haiku":"Economic inequality and social paralysis trap the underprivileged; a dunce cannot climb while the privileged play at revolution, both damned to poverty of spirit.","opus":"A jittery swipe at class and privilege, contrasting the trust-funded, polyamorous scenester who coasts with the dunce who can't earn a penny. It is about being locked out of a rigged game where money buys the rope.","sonnet":"A class-resentment anthem for the excluded: the speaker has cracked the sphinx, solved what others haven't, but still can't get a leg up while trust-funded polyamorists dressed like him waltz through — the miracle in the cereal bowl and the money-for-rope refrain make absurdism the only frame for systemic unfairness.","src":"haiku"},
"ed-sheeran~bad-habits-feat-bring-me-the-horizon":{"haiku":"The speaker's destructive patterns and impulsive choices consistently lead them back to a forbidden person, unable to break the cycle despite knowing it will end alone.","opus":"A confession of compulsive self-sabotage, where evenings out under coloured glow spiral into solitude, blackouts and talk with people he barely knows, the singer vowing each occasion is the final one while knowing his compulsions always drag him back to a person or vice he can't quit. Ed Sheeran's synth-pop original is remade here with Bring Me The Horizon, the British rock band, who give the temptation-and-ruin theme a heavier, anthemic edge.","sonnet":"Self-sabotage as late-night ritual — a speaker who knows he loses control after dark, ends up alone, makes promises he won't keep, and keeps being pulled back to someone or something he can't name cleanly. The bad habit is both the behavior and the person it leads to, and by the end both are indistinguishable: the pattern and its object have fused into the same unavoidable destination.","src":"haiku"},
"eden~nocturne":{"haiku":"A speaker refuses sleep and chooses waking vigilance, determined to reclaim agency from doubt and self-doubt.","opus":"A nocturnal reckoning with self-sabotage, someone refusing sleep and letting go of ghosts, admitting the only one to blame is himself. Talking out loud in the dark becomes the method for figuring his way out.","sonnet":"Resolved to stay awake and present rather than wish his life away, the speaker commits to banishing ghosts and speaking his way toward clarity through the night. It is only in the dark that anything real can be made or made right, and the dream he is abandoning is passivity itself.","src":"haiku"},
"edyta-bartosiewicz~jenny":{"haiku":"Jenny has endured lifelong misfortune and sees too much, feeling too deeply; school questioned her sanity, but she believes her strange passion has meaning.","opus":"A first-person portrait of a young woman named Jenny who has always felt something is wrong with her, that she sees too much and feels too intensely, dogged since school by people asking if she's alright; only she knows what sits in her head, and she clings to the belief that her strange behavior makes sense, that sometimes it's better to lose your mind a little in order not to go truly insane.","sonnet":"A Polish song in the voice of Jenny — a woman who lost her childhood too fast, sees and feels too much, has always existed slightly outside normal, and whose inner life is entirely her own secret, known only to herself.","src":"haiku"},
"eelke-kleijn~lost-souls-nora-en-pure-remix":{"haiku":"The soul abandoned in isolation now waits for rescue, stranded in coldness and unable to return home without external intervention.","opus":"A vocal loop of someone begging to be taken home after leaving their soul somewhere cold and wrong. The repetition itself becomes the ache of displacement.","sonnet":"About losing yourself — leaving your soul behind somewhere it does not belong, on a cold stone floor, and being unable to find your way home without help. The plea is simple and repeated: somebody take me home, because I left the essential part of myself somewhere I cannot retrieve it alone.","src":"haiku"},
"egoist~1-4-2":{"haiku":"Cyclical return marks fate's cruel comedy; dreams fade into twilight while stars illuminate eternal waiting, new days never arriving.","opus":"A recurring nightmare with no way out, the narrator able only to hide the one they love in total darkness while tomorrow stays invisible. Framed as a vanishing act, it's about loving someone through inescapable despair.","sonnet":"A short, haunting meditation on loss and recursion: someone who was once a single guiding star is gone — \"they were never heard from again\" — and the speaker is trapped in a dark loop, unable to find tomorrow, able only to hide the person they miss and wait inside a dreamlike nightmare with no exit.","src":"haiku"},
"egoist~all-alone-with-you":{"opus":"A confession that loving others feels impossible yet the ache to be loved remains, vowing to carry any sin and walk a pathless road beside one person. God stays silent, so devotion to another becomes the only faith left.","src":"opus"},
"egoist~departures-anata-ni-okuru-ai-no-uta-2017-remastered":{"opus":"A farewell love song to someone already lost, clinging to the warmth of a hand and the memory of their apologetic face. Written for Guilty Crown, it aches with the knowledge that this parting is final.","src":"opus"},
"egoist~departures-anata-ni-okuru-ai-no-uta-acoustic-version-2017-remastered":{"haiku":"Unrequited longing fixates on the warmth of a departing lover, the singer unable to release an attachment that transforms separation into a lifelong ache.","opus":"A love song of loss and yearning after a partner is gone, no longer loved or needed by them, clinging to the warmth of a held hand and begging not to be let go before that warmth disappears.","sonnet":"A speaker mourns a love that is definitively over — no longer needed or desired — and clings to the warmth of held hands and forehead-to-forehead sleep, begging to be embraced once more before that warmth disappears entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"egoist~door-from-best-al-alter-ego":{"haiku":"The speaker must end repeating cycles and open a door to the future with their beloved; only one wish will come true despite all promises.","opus":"The Japanese project EGOIST counts down remaining time toward a reunion, a narrator vowing to move stopped clocks and end repeating tomorrows to reach someone again. Black smoke drifting into a dimming sky marks loss, yet a single wish to open the door promises fulfillment. Pressing forward without looking back, the voice pledges to sleep eternally inside another's feelings, devotion outlasting a crumbling reality.","sonnet":"A figure counts the remaining time before closing their eyes forever in a dream-place they longed to reach. Someone left behind holds onto the promise of reunion across the border of sleep and waking, pledging to live forward without looking back while the beloved rests in permanent stillness.","src":"haiku"},
"egoist~eiyu-unmei-no-uta-from-best-al-alter-ego":{"haiku":"Foolish heroes pursue narrow fate while love's sacred persistence shines across separated worlds, destiny laughing at small souls.","opus":"An epic of a doomed hero raising the banner against the dark, weighing love against destiny and the glory of falling unbowed. Its core is that betraying your faith and who you are is a worse fate than dying young.","sonnet":"A hero's song maps the full arc from love separated by fate to battlefield sacrifice: the world chooses frenzy and conquest, but the hero chooses conviction and nobility, refusing to soil their name even as life runs out — the dawn is coming, and departure means becoming one with the person they love.","src":"haiku"},
"egoist~fallen-from-best-al-alter-ego":{"opus":"A fallen angel's disturbing love, devouring the beloved and questioning what she was born for, longing to be loved until she comes apart. It fuses eros and death into a serene, unsettling hymn of self-annihilating desire.","src":"opus"},
"egoist~fallen-tv-edit":{"haiku":"A fallen angel descends into predatory obsession, consuming the beloved while questioning her own origin and purpose within cycles of desire and destruction.","opus":"A fallen angel's blood-drenched, guilty love, consuming and consumed, cradling a lifeless body while asking what it was born for and pleading only to be loved until it dissolves completely.","sonnet":"A fallen angel with a burning thirst speaks from inside a body hollowed of its original tenant, consuming shards of glass and crimson eternity while repeatedly asking \"what was I born for\" and demanding to be loved until she unravels completely.","src":"haiku"},
"egoist~kabaneri-of-the-iron-fortress-from-best-al-alter-ego":{"haiku":"A warrior-figure must burn their life for comrades and honor, refusing to yield despite internal conflict and the cost of living.","opus":"A call to rise arrives through steel and blood, urging a fighter to burn their whole life for a sworn comrade and change the world, to lift a lowered face and find hope where only sorrow seemed to be. EGOIST, the vocal project tied to producer Ryo of supercell, wrote this as an anime theme, its Japanese lyrics rallying grief and pain into defiant strength.","sonnet":"A warrior remembers devastating loss and self-reproach, then is urged to let that pain become fuel rather than paralysis. A fallen comrade's voice speaks through memory, demanding the fighter rise, burn with purpose, and change the world for those who were promised protection. The Japanese lyrics frame sacrifice as ongoing obligation, not conclusion.","src":"haiku"},
"egoist~namae-no-nai-kaibutsu-from-best-al-alter-ego":{"haiku":"Born into confinement and rejection, the unnamed monster accepts her role as vessel for punishment, demanding transformation and companionship as co-conspirators in reckoning.","opus":"A nameless monster born behind iron bars, unwanted and cracked by neurosis, demanding a twisted justice from the complicit bystanders and inviting them to accept its cursed body and walk alongside it.","sonnet":"A creature born inside black iron bars, unwanted and neurotic, rallies complicit bystanders to pay the price of karma and march together as a nameless monster — tearing down before being torn down, answering the god who announces the true world.","src":"haiku"},
"egoist~suki-to-iwareta-hi-from-best-al-alter-ego":{"haiku":"Insomnia spirals as the narrator obsesses over a single phrase, each passing second weighted with impossible yearning and the fragile joy of being noticed.","opus":"Sleepless in the small hours after someone said they liked her, counting seconds and holding her breath as she turns the words over, until confusion gives way to joyful tears she cannot stop.","sonnet":"At 3 a.m., unable to sleep, the speaker obsessively replays the moment someone confessed their feelings — holding their breath to hear their own heartbeat, uncertain what the emotion is, until tears finally come from the unbearable happiness of it.","src":"haiku"},
"egoist~welcome-to-the-fam-from-best-al-alter-ego":{"haiku":"A confident virtual artist rises as a new challenger, playing through obstacles with maximum readiness and unstoppable determination.","opus":"A hyperactive, multilingual hype anthem framed entirely through retro gaming, greeting listeners across several languages before racing through references to Mario Kart, Mega Man, Space Invaders and arcade lore, the narrator styling herself a goddess who wins with frame-perfect commands. EGOIST is the anime-linked Japanese project fronted by the vocalist chelly and tied to the noitaminA block, and this bilingual video-game love letter bursts with that otaku-pop exuberance.","sonnet":"A multilingual self-introduction that frames artistic identity as a video game — the speaker arrives like a new challenger, meter maxed, ready to reverse any losing position through skill and spectacle. The references cascade from Mario Kart to Space Invaders to Mega Man, and the persona of EGOIST becomes a virtual champion who exists across Asia and beyond, inviting everyone into her orbit.","src":"haiku"},
"eguie5~respirar":{"opus":"A cry for escape from a suffocating culture of cancellation and outrage, where merely voicing an opinion invites crucifixion. The repeated plea to get out and breathe is about reclaiming freedom to think and speak.","src":"opus"},
"egypt-central~no-place-like-home":{"haiku":"A displaced community wanders through danger and loss, clinging to the promise of return despite the impossibility of restoring what has been destroyed.","opus":"Wizard of Oz imagery mapped onto crisis and lost hope, a promise to a struggling companion that even through danger they'll find the road home. Fantasy borrowed to insist survival and homecoming are still possible.","sonnet":"Drawing on Wizard of Oz imagery, the song casts two people as lost travelers in a hostile world, with one reassuring the other that despite the danger and fading hope, they will find their way back to belonging.","src":"haiku"},
"eidola~a-bridge-of-iron-and-blood":{"opus":"A scorched-earth vow from someone ground down to nothing, framing self-immolation as proof of worth. The progressive-metalcore rage turns outward at a rotten system and everyone in it, but the real target is the singer's own exhausted despair.","src":"opus"},
"eidola~the-comfort-we-find-in-our-vices":{"haiku":"Addictive patterns trap consciousness in cycles of self-medication and delusion; freedom requires severing the cords of familiar escape.","opus":"A struggle with the vices we secretly nurture, teaching our own demons to swim as we try to drown them, the will a lion that's both burden and blessing. Recovery reframed as the hard practice of letting go rather than white-knuckling control.","sonnet":"A person attempts to master their own compulsions and addictions, recognising that feeding demons only teaches them to swim, and that isolation from the whole keeps them fractured — ultimately resolving to cut the addiction out and stop letting doubt monopolise their will.","src":"haiku"},
"eight-sins~street-trash":{"opus":"Grindcore filth as civic portrait, dragging the listener through a decaying underclass of addicts, corpses and violence. The band plants it in a specific French city, turning revulsion itself into a weapon aimed at the audience.","src":"opus"},
"einar-solberg~grotto-feat-magnus-b-rmark":{"opus":"The Leprous frontman turns a cave into a metaphor for chosen retreat, where hiding from the world reads as both refuge and surrender. It sits in the ache between the safety of withdrawal and the shame of never fighting your way back.","src":"opus"},
"eisbrecher~verr-ckt":{"fable":"Embracing the madman label as a stroke of luck: the accused flips every charge back on the accuser — you say you're splendid, which means you're suffering — while a charge ticks inside him and the chorus celebrates: fortunately, I'm crazy.","fableDeep":"The German title carries a pun the accusation can't survive: ver-rückt is literally 'shifted out of place' — displaced, not diseased — and the song keeps asking which position is actually the sick one ('wer ist normal hier, und wer ist hier krank?'). Every exchange inverts: your 'doing splendidly' means you're doing badly; my being embarrassing is really your being afraid. The ticking Ladung (charge) stays deliberately ambiguous — breakdown or liberation — and 'nothing will be as it was' welcomes either. 'Zum Glück bin ich verrückt' — *luckily* I'm mad — claims insanity as the exemption from a normality the song has just diagnosed as the real illness.","haiku":"Social alienation and threat of explosive breakdown from repression.","opus":"Embracing one's own madness and menace, taunting a hypocritical, fearful other while blurring who is truly normal or sick.","sonnet":"Embracing one's own madness and difference as freedom, celebrating being unhinged rather than conforming to normality.","src":"sonnet"},
"ekkstacy~alright-feat-the-kid-laroi":{"haiku":"Distraction masks deeper dysfunction as time stretches into meaninglessness; the speaker questions their own competence.","opus":"Numbed indifference after a breakup, insisting it's all fine while clearly still stung. The other keeps messaging and obsessing, but the speaker, distracted and unsure if he's just dumb, shrugs the whole thing off as meant to end.","sonnet":"A breakup told with numb detachment — the speaker is too dissociated and self-doubting to process the days, and the Kid LAROI verse explains the other person's jealousy over a new girl even though they were the one who ended things.","src":"haiku"},
"ekkstacy~bella":{"haiku":"The speaker is transfixed by a girl he doesn't know, unable to look away despite trying to dismiss her, the obsession crystallizing around her name as the only thing he can hold onto.","opus":"Infatuation with a stranger the singer keeps insisting shouldn't matter, the denial itself betraying how hard he's fallen. Beneath the deadpan bedroom-goth delivery is the familiar teenage helplessness of wanting someone unreachable.","sonnet":"Attraction to someone whose name he barely knows: she doesn't matter, he tells himself, you should look past her — but his heart beats faster, she's there far but close, and the name Bella arrives late as the only concrete thing he's managed to hold onto in the admiration.","src":"haiku"},
"ekkstacy~chicago":{"opus":"A hookup that opens into unexpected tenderness and immediate regret, the refrain about leaving a city standing in for a life he thinks he ruined. The intimacy is real but shadowed by his conviction that he doesn't deserve it.","src":"opus"},
"ekkstacy~fuck":{"opus":"A nihilist spasm where universal misery, a doomed relationship and the singer's own self-loathing all collapse into one exhausted curse. The bravado about violence barely masks a confession that he hates himself as much as everyone else.","src":"opus"},
"ekkstacy~goo-lagoon":{"haiku":"Laziness spreads across shore and time. Crying replaces smiling—separation marks elapsed hours.","opus":"Aimless coastal self-medication and numbness, getting drunk and lazy by the water while wanting to cry instead of smile; running out of time and hollowed by a long absence.","sonnet":"About running out of time and not caring anymore — the narrator is getting drunk by the water, going numb on the coast, not wanting to smile, and sensing that not much life is left; the whole song is slack-bodied resignation to being done.","src":"haiku"},
"ekkstacy~i-don-t-have-one-of-those":{"opus":"Longing for someone who feels like home when home is exactly what the singer has never had. The ache is in accepting she isn't his and telling himself that's fine, which it plainly isn't.","src":"opus"},
"ekkstacy~i-guess-we-made-it-this-far":{"haiku":"Nostalgia for a simpler time — stolen hearts, backseat drives, drinking in a room, dancing in the dark — set against the bafflement of how everything got so complicated, the distance between then and now measured in unanswered questions.","opus":"A backward glance at young love that started simple and grew heavy with time, unsure of who either person became. The tenderness and disorientation coexist in the quiet surprise of having survived this long together.","sonnet":"Fond, disbelieving retrospect on a relationship's origins: chasing the city from the backseat, drinking in your room and dancing in the dark, neither of you knowing who you were — when did it all get so hard is the only question, and the answer is that somehow, improbably, you made it this far anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"ekkstacy~luv-of-my-life":{"haiku":"Obsession shades into threat as the speaker clings to attachment, oscillating between devotion and self-harm as the only language for desperate need.","opus":"Devotion and self-harm tangled past separating, declarations of love undercut by casual threats against the self and jokes that aren't jokes. The flat delivery of despair is the point, worship and a knife at the wrist sharing the same breath.","sonnet":"A deeply unstable declaration of love laced with self-harm imagery — the speaker oscillates between melodramatic suicide threats made as jokes, possessive devotion, cutting imagery, and nihilistic indifference, unable to commit to either genuine feeling or genuine detachment.","src":"haiku"},
"ekkstacy~shutting-me-out":{"haiku":"Rejection seals connection—the speaker reaches for voice and presence while the other withdraws, leaving only the cycle of exclusion and the ache of unreciprocated need.","opus":"A one-sided ache for contact with a woman who keeps barring the door; he'll settle for falsehoods, just wanting the sound of her one last time. The refrain of her turning him away repeats as helpless fixation on a closeness she withholds.","sonnet":"A speaker pleads for any communication at all from someone who has closed off completely — even lies would do — as the repeated \"she keeps shutting me out\" locks the listener into the helpless loop of wanting contact that never comes.","src":"haiku"},
"el-vy~paul-is-alive":{"haiku":"Pop culture mythology shapes identity while diverting from authentic development; the narrator recognises how received narratives colonise personal becoming.","opus":"Matt Berninger mining an adolescent Cincinnati memory, the McCartney-death myth standing in for a teenager's dread of dying and disappearing; a hazy coming-of-age about wanting to start over and not knowing if anyone comes back.","sonnet":"A man who grew up in Beatlemania's shadow, steeped in his mother's nostalgia, reflects on his own adolescent identity-seeking — borrowed boots, a bar full of indie music, crying alone — and finds himself at a crossroads between heaven and the life still ahead, not quite ready to go.","src":"haiku"},
"elderbrook~capricorn":{"haiku":"Astrological destiny and romantic obsession blur as the speaker manipulates cosmic forces to ensure his beloved remains bound to him eternally.","opus":"A man in love with someone who trusts horoscopes over him, half-joking about rigging the stars in his favor. Under the whimsy is the ache of never feeling in control of whether she stays.","sonnet":"A man so desperate to keep a partner who lives by horoscopes that he fantasises about secretly rewriting the astrology column to make their compatibility read as fated — a tender, comic admission that love will make a fool of you.","src":"haiku"},
"elderbrook~old-friend":{"haiku":"A person reconnects with a familiar destructive pattern or person, drawn into risky behavior because the trouble feels like an old friend they can't resist.","opus":"Self-sabotage personified as an old companion you keep welcoming back, the familiar bad habit that feels like home. Underneath the warm groove is the honest admission that returning to your own worst patterns is comfortingly easy.","sonnet":"About the pull of a destructive old habit that keeps coming back like a familiar face — whether it's a person, a drug, or a lifestyle, the narrator knows it's stolen, knows it leads to problems, and keeps falling back into it anyway because it's in the blood.","src":"haiku"},
"elderbrook~something-about-you":{"haiku":"Sobriety anchors in another's presence as the narrator rebuilds from addiction, your absence threatening to destabilize a fragile equilibrium maintained through connection.","opus":"Getting sober while missing the person he was intoxicated with, crediting a lost lover for steadying and grounding him even as the old drinks and highs lose their taste and he apologizes for how it went wrong.","sonnet":"The speaker has slipped into excess — smoking, drinking, getting high — and admits that the person he lost is the only thing that kept him grounded; something about them straightens him up when he's low, and he's working on himself to earn their return.","src":"haiku"},
"elderbrook~talking":{"opus":"A self-aware confession of narcissism in friendships: the singer keeps neglecting people who matter, always meaning to reach out, but admits they love the sound of their own voice more than actually listening.","sonnet":"A self-aware confession about narcissism and emotional neglect — the narrator admits he is too in love with his own voice and self-absorption to maintain real friendships, always finding excuses not to show up for people who matter to him.","src":"sonnet"},
"electric-bassboy~we-got-the-moves-riot-remix":{"opus":"Pure hedonist summer anthem, all beach, booze and the belief that these nights never end. It runs on the deliberately shallow promise that the good times, and the youth behind them, will never fade.","src":"opus"},
"electric-callboy~mindreader":{"opus":"A toxic ex rendered as a manipulator who saw through every defense, the singer swinging between wanting free and wanting revenge. Under the party-metalcore gloss is the ugly truth that he still can't get her out of his head.","src":"opus"},
"electric-callboy~we-got-the-moves":{"haiku":"Young people celebrate summer freedom and hedonism, dancing and drinking on the beach, seeking to repeat the party endlessly.","opus":"A beach party of sun, drink, and youthful abandon, the young and carefree swearing summer will stay with them forever. Germany's Electric Callboy, kings of tongue-in-cheek metalcore-meets-dance spectacle, dive fully into euphoric kitsch, the gibberish hook distilling a single-minded vow to keep raving till dawn.","sonnet":"Sun, sand, cheap drinks, and the conviction that summer freedom belongs to the young and alive. The only ambition is repetition of joy — another round, another day at the beach, the groove sustained until the weekend dissolves and the memory outlasts the hangover.","src":"haiku"},
"electric-enemy~all-for-you":{"opus":"The slow erasure of self inside a controlling relationship, where obedience means killing off your own voice and skin. Its quiet devastation is the homesickness for a self the singer has never been allowed to become.","src":"opus"},
"electric-enemy~burn":{"opus":"A protest song staged as a mob at the gates, demanding the powerful answer for rights stripped and lives spent. The apocalyptic imagery channels the conviction that the world is run by people who will never take the blame.","src":"opus"},
"electric-enemy~heartache-melody":{"haiku":"Trauma and haunting memories from past love and suffering, refusing desire because of learned self-protection.","opus":"Heartache is recast as song, crime, and lingering recollection, a toxic cure where scent and a vacant chair keep old wounds raw. The narrator vows it's over and claims to want nothing from love, blaming her past. The lyric's cabaret-tinged self-awareness makes romantic ruin feel almost staged, a keepsake replayed even as she begs a certain tune not be brought too near.","sonnet":"Love is framed as a lifelong criminal offense — starting young, leaving its evidence everywhere in perfume, empty seats, and songs that cannot be heard without reopening the wound. The narrator swears off love entirely, haunted by a history that keeps playing back.","src":"haiku"},
"electric-enemy~hollow-eyes":{"haiku":"The song expresses incomprehension within a draining bond. The partner hides something behind emotionless eyes; the narrator cannot read between lines or understand the game.","opus":"A song of a relationship curdling into distrust, the singer searching a partner's hollow eyes for truth while suspecting he is being strung along and drained, left on his own with blood drawn from his stone as she readies to move on. The repeated admission that he simply does not know underscores the helpless confusion of a love already lost.","sonnet":"A relationship autopsy conducted through a series of unanswered questions — what does the other person actually want, are they enjoying the speaker's confusion, is there anything left worth fighting for? The repeated image of hollow eyes is both accusation and admission of defeat, and by the end the speaker is left with the knowledge that the other person has already moved on.","src":"haiku"},
"electric-enemy~paranoid":{"haiku":"Pervasive distrust and fear of persecution, suspicion that harm is omnipresent.","opus":"A relationship curdling through distrust and maybe paranoia, the speaker sure he's being talked about behind his back and refusing to be told it's all in his head. Cast as the crazy one, he flips it, turning the accusation of madness back on the accuser.","sonnet":"A narrator who hears conversations behind their back and cannot be told it's all in their head — the paranoia is too clear, the imagined plotting too detailed, even as they acknowledge getting carried away. \"You say that it's all in my head / I'm paranoid, go back to bed\" is the dismissal they refuse to accept.","src":"haiku"},
"electric-enemy~save-me-i-m-not-crazy":{"opus":"Someone trapped behind their own walls, wondering whether the voices urging escape are madness or the only sane guidance left. It turns paranoia outward into a plea for empathy against rulers who govern through fear.","src":"opus"},
"electric-enemy~take-the-wheel":{"haiku":"Spiritual depletion and aged exhaustion; the speaker surrenders control to destructive forces, unable to reclaim agency from the demons within.","opus":"Overwhelmed by anxiety and age and unable to find the love he once felt, he hands control to his demons, letting something darker steer because he no longer knows how to drive himself.","sonnet":"Older than expected and drained of love, the speaker has handed control to the devil because they can't drive themselves anymore — 'demons live in my mind' — a vertigo of anxiety that no high can ease, waiting for something to bring back what they used to feel.","src":"haiku"},
"electric-enemy~the-fear":{"haiku":"Internal horror becomes external reality; the speaker's mind spawns voices and visions that feel undeniably present, trapping them in nightmare logic while awake.","opus":"Raw panic and paranoia rendered almost literally, the mind turning into a nightmare of imagined voices and presences, the fear no longer chased but arrived and inescapable.","sonnet":"Panic attack rendered physically — frozen legs, skin prickling, voices screaming from somewhere underground, the speaker's own mind become a nightmare — the fear arrives and there is no more running from it.","src":"haiku"},
"electric-enemy~therapy":{"haiku":"Rage and suppression build beneath a surface of compliance until the pressure forces violent revelation.","opus":"A therapy session framing barely-leashed rage, patient Jimmy grinding his teeth and lying that he's fine while the anger starts bleeding through. Sick of saying yes to keep everyone happy, the polite mask splits, and the therapist's cheery parting shot confirms the fury was never really contained.","sonnet":"About bottled-up rage finally breaking through in a therapy session — the narrator has been saying 'yes' and 'okay' to please everyone while internally seeing red, and now the therapist is getting the real story: they bleed instead of sweat, and the fury is just now becoming visible.","src":"haiku"},
"electric-enemy~you":{"haiku":"Self-erasure in service of another person's needs hollows the speaker until identity dissolves, leaving only the hope that someday they'll reclaim their own voice.","opus":"A muted account of self-erasure in a controlling relationship, keeping quiet and clean and becoming whoever the other demands, homesick for a self never known, with a faint vow to someday reclaim his own voice.","sonnet":"A person suppressing every authentic impulse — keeping voice low, swallowing the urge to scream, kissing fantasies of their own skin goodbye — entirely for someone else's sake, while being homesick for a place and self they have never been allowed to be.","src":"haiku"},
"electric-light-orchestra~here-is-the-news":{"haiku":"A broadcast voice reports mundane surreality and cosmic alienation as routine; the ordinary catastrophes of existence become indistinguishable from the weather.","opus":"Jeff Lynne's sci-fi bulletin from the Time album, a future where dystopian headlines about satellites and space have flattened all feeling. The real cry buried in the broadcast is a very human wish to just go home.","sonnet":"A sardonic broadcast parody cycles through absurd and sinister headlines — technological curiosities, unexplained deaths, escaped satellites — framing modern media as a relentless, disorienting stream that leaves the listener longing for home.","src":"haiku"},
"electric-youth~a-real-hero":{"haiku":"Authentic heroism emerges not from extraordinary strength but from moral resolve and deliberate action to save others without expectation.","opus":"Built around Captain Sullenberger's Hudson landing, the synth-pop reframes heroism as staying composed and human under impossible pressure; Drive made it the anthem of a man defined by quiet competence rather than spectacle.","sonnet":"A tribute to quiet, genuine heroism — specifically invoking a pilot who saved 155 lives in crisis — arguing that true heroism is emotional complexity and principled action against the grain, not spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"eleine~enemies":{"haiku":"Unified resistance against external threat becomes transcendent as the collective faces mortality together, fear dissolving into shared strength and purpose.","opus":"Symphonic-metal defiance built as a battle hymn, the language of blood and dying dressed as unity. It is less about literal war than the vow to stand together and refuse fear when everything comes for you.","sonnet":"A warrior, wounded and near death, refuses to yield — spitting blood, feeling their strength grow rather than diminish, standing united with others against a common threat. The defiance is collective rather than individual, and the certainty of survival comes from solidarity.","src":"haiku"},
"elemeno-p~verona":{"haiku":"An infatuated stranger observes a woman in a city and imagines replacing her boyfriend with sincere devotion and understanding.","opus":"A giddy pop-punk meet-cute, spotting a girl already taken and betting everything on winning her, then landing it, the second verse rewriting the first from hope into having.","sonnet":"A man recounts meeting a woman in Verona, instantly captivated despite her being with someone else, tongue-tied at parting but ultimately winning her over — the song is a joyful retrospective of how he met the person he loves.","src":"haiku"},
"element-eighty~mercury-man":{"opus":"A grief-soaked reckoning with everything left unsaid to someone now gone, the singer haunted by regret and the desire to have absorbed their pain. This early-2000s nu-metal ballad lives in the helpless question of where they are now.","src":"opus"},
"elephant-gym~40-40":{"haiku":"A tender question about whether a song exists that evokes shared memories—melancholy of wishing music could recover the past and lost love.","opus":"A Taiwanese math-rock band sets nostalgia to song itself: the question of whether one melody could keep two estranged people bound across the years and the same indifferent city. It aches with distance between souls who once shared everything and now only pass strangers.","sonnet":"A cover that asks whether there is a song that makes you think of the singer — a mutual lost connection, two people living in the same city who somehow never meet again, the rain still falling on the same world they once shared. The emotional core is the bittersweet wish that a melody could do what words and proximity failed to: keep someone present.","src":"haiku"},
"elephant-gym~a-3nsyw":{"haiku":"A quiet moment of melancholy and longing—two fools smiling sweetly in darkness, love a chemical addiction.","opus":"Elephant Gym are a Taiwanese math-rock trio whose intricate, bass-led instrumentals usually carry the feeling; when words arrive they tend toward this still melancholy. In a dark room on a cloudy day the narrator lets every thought settle like sediment, wondering whether love is a spiritual opiate or just end-of-century boredom. A cigarette curls smoke beside a photograph of the two of them laughing — the mind adrift in post-relationship ambivalence, held perfectly still.","sonnet":"In a dark room on a cloudy day, the narrator lets all their thoughts settle like sediment and wonders whether love is a spiritual opiate or just end-of-century boredom — then looks at a cigarette curling smoke and a photograph of the two of them laughing together beside them. The song is a still, melancholy snapshot of the mind adrift in post-relationship ambivalence.","src":"haiku"},
"elephant-gym~a-3v1jw":{"haiku":"A meditation on stagnation and acceptance—honesty feels risky, comfort numbs, but it's never too late to change course.","opus":"Elephant Gym's music lives in transitional spaces — bass lines that keep resolving somewhere unexpected — and this song makes that motion its subject. Caught mid-journey, too honest to fake satisfaction yet afraid of stagnation, the narrator walks a path that keeps diverging, past familiar landscapes that no longer feel like destinations. The quiet recognition it arrives at is that being lost in the middle is not failure, and that it is not too late.","sonnet":"A song about being caught mid-journey — too honest to pretend satisfaction yet afraid of stagnation, walking a path that keeps diverging from where you meant to go, surrounded by familiar landscapes that no longer feel like destinations. The emotional core is the quiet recognition that being lost in the middle is not failure, and that it's not too late.","src":"haiku"},
"elita~mentally-not-here":{"haiku":"Intense internal chaos and physical sickness spiral toward violence; the speaker burns with destructive rage and warns others away before causing harm.","opus":"A dissociative spiral where rage, nausea and violent impulse blur into someone who barely recognizes their own unraveling. The hell it describes is internal, the devil's spell just a name for losing control of your own mind.","sonnet":"Dissociation and physical disgust collide: the narrator watches someone doing wrong, blade in hand, plans falling apart, going to hell under the devil's spell while their stomach knots and they threaten to physically remove the intruder before they become any more unwell.","src":"haiku"},
"elita~sour-switchblade":{"haiku":"The speaker expresses intense desire to worship, hurt with, and possess someone completely, asking if the other person can handle the intensity of their devotion.","opus":"The alt-pop artist Elita voices raw, taboo-flirting desire, wanting to taste, worship, and walk through everything with a lover, even the hurt they carry. The refrain admitting it's a lot to live up to laces yearning with self-aware hesitation. Propositions grow bolder and blunter, déjà vu blurring the line between genuine intimacy and a compulsion the narrator half-doubts.","sonnet":"An offer of total presence — to walk alongside, hurt alongside, worship another completely. The intensity of the desire is acknowledged as a heavy standard to carry. Beneath the tenderness runs a sharper edge, making the devotion feel less safe than it sounds.","src":"haiku"},
"ellegarden~a-14y8o0e":{"haiku":"Collecting fragments of wonder—gathering beautiful objects and lost time pieces, creating mosaic meaning from scattered fragments in a muddled journey.","opus":"ELLEGARDEN emerged from Japan's early-2000s melodic punk scene singing in a mix of Japanese and English, and that bilingual seam runs through this song. The narrator collects irreplaceable things — a found clock, fragments of time slipping through gaps like jewels — while running forward in uncertainty, the English section asking whether knowing too much is worse than knowing nothing. Its texture is a generation pressing ahead without understanding why, only that stopping is not an option.","sonnet":"A song about collecting irreplaceable things — a found clock, fragments of time slipping through gaps like jewels — while running forward in uncertainty: the English section questions whether knowing too much is worse than knowing nothing, and the emotional texture is of a generation pressing ahead without understanding why, just because stopping isn't an option.","src":"haiku"},
"elliott-smith~between-the-bars":{"haiku":"A seducer offers oblivion and isolation as shelter from pain, inviting the beloved into a symbiotic prison where control masquerades as protection.","opus":"A tender, terrifying seduction voiced by addiction itself, the bottle promising to quiet your regrets and keep you all to itself. Smith writes the comfort of numbing as a lover who wants you smaller and dependent.","sonnet":"Alcohol speaks in the first person, promising a person shelter from their past selves and painful memories, seducing them with intimacy and the false comfort of oblivion while quietly possessing them.","src":"haiku"},
"ellise~911":{"haiku":"Psychological manipulation paralyses its victim in cycles of hope and despair, with medication and crisis both rendering autonomy impossible.","opus":"A toxic relationship framed as a medical emergency, love as a sickness with pills and voices and no cure. The panic underneath is knowing she has to leave him or lose herself entirely.","sonnet":"A woman in a psychologically destructive relationship reaches a breaking point — she calls for outside intervention because the relationship is consuming her sanity, and she knows she has to lose the man or lose herself entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"ellise~can-you-keep-a-secret":{"haiku":"Shame and hidden trauma demand acceptance; the speaker risks vulnerability, needing another to witness darkness without judgment or abandonment.","opus":"A confession held back out of fear that revealing one's darkness will forfeit trust and love. The skeletons and monsters in the head test whether intimacy can survive being shown the whole ugly truth.","sonnet":"A confession of internal monsters — \"I see monsters in my bed, I got screws loose in my head\" — posed as a test of a partner's love: will they stay if they see inside, if they know the skeletons and the hands around the throat? The vulnerability is genuine but conditional.","src":"haiku"},
"ellis~after-hours":{"opus":"The seductive pull of late-night escape, where numbing out and chasing distraction become their own calling. Beneath the sultry glide is a jadedness that admits the thrill is really just running from tomorrow.","src":"opus"},
"ellis~the-taste":{"haiku":"Two broken people cling to each other through repetitive cycles of desire and disappointment, unable to rise above their pattern.","opus":"A hazy dream-pop meditation on repeating the same self-destructive intimacy, two broke people clutching at each other for something that keeps them afloat while knowing they've circled this drain before. Recognition without escape is the whole mood.","sonnet":"Two broke, entangled people who keep returning to the same situation, knowing it isn't pure or healthy but unable to fully let go — a familiar emotional cycle that everyone has been through.","src":"haiku"},
"eluveitie~king":{"haiku":"A Celtic king claims sovereignty through divine mandate and earthly prosperity, positioning himself as a conduit between gods and governed through conquest and agricultural abundance.","opus":"Folk-metal ventriloquizing the Gaulish high king Ambicatus, who by legend sent his people to colonize new lands, voicing sacred Celtic kingship as a bond with gods, oak groves and the crows that guide conquest.","sonnet":"The ancient Gaulish high king Ambicatus speaks in the first person, declaring his divine mandate, his role as servant of the gods and crown of Gallia, and the expansion of his kingdom from the Hercynian forests to the Alps, guided by the flight of crows.","src":"haiku"},
"eluveitie~the-call-of-the-mountains":{"haiku":"Mountain peaks and ancient voices summon homecoming through mysterious winds and crane calls, promising a tune that echoes from landscape itself. The call connects the speaker to primordial belonging.","opus":"An ancestral Celtic yearning for the Alpine homeland, warriors braced against ocean and tempest while the peaks and the passing cranes summon them back. Eluveitie fold Helvetic history into pounding folk metal, treating the return home as a stir in the blood.","sonnet":"Warriors battered by waves, storms, and enemies hear the flight of cranes and the cry of the mountains calling them home — the Alps and the song in their hearts pull stronger than any external force, and the call of home becomes the only augur that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"elvis-presley~can-t-help-falling-in-love":{"haiku":"Destiny and inevitability intertwine as the speaker surrenders to love despite knowing risk.","opus":"Surrender as devotion, love arriving like a river toward the sea, something you don't choose so much as accept. The wisdom is in giving your whole life to what was always meant to be.","sonnet":"A tender surrender to love that defies the wisdom of caution — the speaker knows rushing in may be foolish, but the feeling is as inevitable as a river reaching the sea, and so the entire self is offered without reservation. It is one of the simplest and most complete statements of devotion in the language.","src":"haiku"},
"elwood-stray~exile":{"haiku":"Isolated from judgment, the narrator discovers others in exile and realizes collective strength; alone becomes powerful when shared.","opus":"The German post-hardcore band reframes withdrawal as refuge, a narrator branded a coward and phony who finds peace in self-chosen seclusion away from fake friends and painful emotion. Isolation gradually turns into solidarity as he realizes others crave the same retreat. Rising voices and raised fists knit a sidelined few into a family, making withdrawal an act of shared defiance.","sonnet":"Voluntary isolation as sanctuary from judgment and misfit belonging. Solitude offers freedom and release from the wrong-place feeling, but eventually the exile recognizes others share the need for retreat. What begins as personal refuge becomes a rallying identity — outsiders united by the choice to withdraw and be heard.","src":"haiku"},
"elwood-stray~genesis":{"haiku":"Creation myth and origin narrative establishing foundations, the speaker charting emergence from void into being.","opus":"A hard-won emergence from a long night of the soul, where letting the old self end becomes the only way to be reborn. The metalcore catharsis lands on the exhausted relief of no longer needing to pretend.","sonnet":"A rebuilding after psychic collapse — shattered walls falling away, truth turned to ashes, but ultimately fighting the night and letting it end. The closing movement is not triumphant but earned: finding a place, no more need to pretend, shadows fading into a world reborn.","src":"haiku"},
"elwood-stray~negative":{"haiku":"Systemic oppression and technological control corrupt freedom while lies entrench power and breed disconnection.","opus":"A furious diagnosis of a society sliding backward into hatred and division, the band watching a so-called revolution poison everyone. Its target is the disguised bigotry that always finds the people with the least to aim at.","sonnet":"A furious diagnosis of a society regressing into hate speech, division, and wolf-in-sheep's-clothing politics — \"your so-called revolution puts us 80 years behind\" — culminating in the image of a collective nailing its own coffin.","src":"haiku"},
"elyne~better-off-dead":{"opus":"The performance of being fine while quietly falling apart, pills and forced smiles papering over suicidal exhaustion. Its cruelty is aimed at hollow reassurances of love, and its bleak comfort is the reminder that the darkness is the only thing you truly own.","src":"opus"},
"elyne~call-it-home":{"haiku":"Displacement becomes permanent as the narrator scatters fragments of self across the world, transformation through sacrifice replacing the security of belonging.","opus":"A musician's confession that only the stage feels like home, leaving pieces of himself all over the world in performance in order finally to belong somewhere, giving everything for something larger than himself.","sonnet":"A musician with no fixed place finds home only on the stage, leaving pieces of herself all over the world; against the bleakness she argues that together — like the hand of God — an unbreakable army can make anywhere home.","src":"haiku"},
"elyne~outbreak":{"opus":"A cry for escape from a violent childhood home, its narrator haunted by an alcoholic father, an absent mother and adults who prey on him. Numbing himself to dull the horror he's forced to endure, the young protagonist begs for a single opportunity and one person willing to help so he can break away and rebuild his life somewhere far off.","sonnet":"About escaping an abusive home environment — a drunk father, an absent mother, predatory figures drugging and watching the narrator — and desperately needing one chance and one friendly hand to get far enough away to start over with a name that means something new.","src":"sonnet"},
"elyne~throne-of-thorns":{"opus":"A vengeful vow of survival and retribution: the narrator flies through the storm to claim a throne of thorns and stands unbreakable, promising to watch a tormentor fall and hunt them down no matter how far they run.","sonnet":"A defiant promise of revenge — someone who has been repeatedly attacked and knocked down declares they will survive every blow and patiently wait to watch their tormentor fall, not out of fear but out of the cold satisfaction of outlasting them.","src":"sonnet"},
"elyne~wake-up":{"haiku":"A call to consciousness and spiritual awakening against religious hypocrisy and comfortable delusion, urging people to stop lying to themselves and examine the moral abyss within.","opus":"A call to shake off comfortable lies and the grip of religion, urging people to see through the sacred book and start over. It walks the shadow of humankind wishing for wisdom, faith weighed against betrayal.","sonnet":"A rejection of religious doctrine as comfortable self-deception — the narrator walks through human darkness with open eyes, fighting internal doubt, and calls for people to stop hiding behind sacred texts and face existence honestly.","src":"haiku"},
"elyose~r-demption":{"haiku":"A woman enacts domination and punishment on a lover, taking pleasure in inflicting pain and control. The song eroticizes cruelty and uses religious language to frame this sadistic dynamic as a form of redemption.","opus":"The French band Elyose voice a coldly commanding woman who refuses to abandon or forget herself, a figure who curses and punishes and believes only in redemption. Imagery of leather, nails on skin, and an executioner's blood casts her dominance as ritual, pleas and laments falling on deaf ears. Sated on cruelty, she withdraws at daybreak, self-possession framed as both power and armor.","sonnet":"A dominant woman moves through a dynamic of deliberate pain and control, replete with physical and emotional edge. In French, she declares herself someone who curses and punishes, sustained by sensation rather than sentiment, and ultimately steps away as daylight arrives — the encounter complete, her appetite satisfied.","src":"haiku"},
"embracer~moon-chamber":{"haiku":"Someone drifts through dissociated unreality, unable to distinguish dreams from life, losing connection to memory and identity.","opus":"A dissociative account of slipping into reverie and shut-off senses, unable to distinguish the real from the imagined, losing hold of his desires and his former self. Beneath it lies depression and escapism, pleading for music to postpone his return to a world he finds unbearable, preferring to be cast into flames than confront it.","sonnet":"Dissociation and the pull of a waking dream state — the narrator is losing grip on reality and self, preferring the blurred dream to an unbearably cruel real life, asking to be sung to sleep rather than woken up.","src":"haiku"},
"emerald-isle~nightmare":{"opus":"A woman weaponizing every wound and every demand to smile, refusing to be palatable for anyone. The defiance is genuinely triumphant: she would rather be a self-aware nightmare than die having pleased everyone but herself.","src":"opus"},
"emerson-lake-palmer~tarkus-i-eruption-ii-stones-of-years-iii-iconoclast-iv-mass-v-manticore-vi-battlefield-vii-aquatarkus-2012-remaster":{"haiku":"The song traces spiritual blindness and cycles of violence. Preachers and leaders perpetuate war and destruction through blind devotion; the earth is scorched by armies in an endless cycle of pain.","opus":"A sprawling suite moving through cosmic questioning, religious hypocrisy and the horror of war, asking whether the dawn has seen your eyes and mocking a parade of clergy woven into a web of their own making, before turning to scorched battlefields where freedom is preached over starving children. Emerson, Lake and Palmer were the British prog supergroup, and Tarkus stands as one of their most ambitious keyboard-driven epics.","sonnet":"A suite-length reckoning with time, religion, and war — opening with a question about whether anyone has truly lived or listened, moving through a catalogue of religious figures each complicit in their own ruin, and arriving at a battlefield where freedom is invoked while starving children fall. The weaver who built the web of deception appears in each section as judge and condemned alike, making the whole a circular indictment of power that consecrates its own violence.","src":"haiku"},
"emery~in-a-win-win-situation":{"haiku":"Long distance relationship persists in temporal compression; isolation paradoxically strengthens connection while physical separation accelerates deterioration.","opus":"Long-distance yearning and the slow ache of wanting someone out of reach, counting the distance and the passing time. Beneath the wordplay is a plea to undo everything and begin the romance again from the start.","sonnet":"A long-distance relationship that cannot be sustained by distance or will alone — the speaker prays for words that might close the gap, eventually concluding that if the other person cannot manage alone, everyone is better off apart.","src":"haiku"},
"emicida~9-circulos":{"haiku":"Nine hells of poverty, police violence, and systemic collapse spiral downward; the narrator bears witness and refusal to surrender.","opus":"The Brazilian rapper descends through a Dantean hell of addiction, despair and state violence in the favela, invoking 2012 apocalypse as a mirror for Black Brazilian life. Beneath the spiral is a hardened resolve to keep resisting a system that kills its poor.","sonnet":"A Brazilian rapper descends through personal and collective hell — insecurity, addiction, police violence, government corruption — invoking the \"nine circles\" as a framework for living at the bottom, the recurring \"click-clack-boom\" stitching gunfire and systemic destruction together.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~1234":{"haiku":"People are seduced into following orders and falling in line, with authority directing obedience through spectacle. The song depicts conformity and collective surrender to power.","opus":"A commanding, marching call to the dancefloor built on love and delusion as a cheap show that still sells out, counting off in German and ordering everyone down, lights killed, cages ripped from a dirty sound so the crowd falls in line and moves the town. Emigrate is the side project of Rammstein guitarist Richard Kruspe, and this carries that band's regimented, industrial-rock stomp into English-language electronic rock.","sonnet":"A charged countdown into collective release — faces painted gold, lights killed, everyone hitting the floor and then rising together. The setup is seductive and slightly sinister, a leader directing the script with a fist, but the crowd responds to the rhythm anyway, and the whole thing resolves into a kind of commanded joy where falling in line and dancing in time become the same act.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~babe":{"haiku":"Reassurance and unconditional acceptance—the speaker tells another they need not fear judgment, shame, or loneliness, promising presence through heartbreak and healing.","opus":"Richard Kruspe's Emigrate softens into reassurance, telling someone afraid and ashamed that they need not be, that falling and getting back up is shared human ground and one day belief will return. Gratitude that they stayed closes it. A gentler counterpoint to his Rammstein work, the track trades industrial menace for plainspoken tenderness and quiet encouragement.","sonnet":"Gentle reassurance directed at someone afraid and ashamed: the narrator tells them that falling, losing the way, heartbreak, and doubt are all part of a cycle that ends in believing again. Tenderness is the whole register here — no drama, just the quiet insistence that they are safe.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~blood":{"haiku":"The song describes bloodlust, genetic transformation, and a loss of control—veins exploding with intensity as the speaker becomes increasingly intoxicated and ungrounded.","opus":"Emigrate barrel through feverish, apocalyptic imagery, lurking creatures, bursting veins, blood mutating at the cellular level as the narrator surrenders every last restraint and drifts nearer to flame. Ominous portents crowd everything he reads. Richard Kruspe's side project retains Rammstein's heavy pulse while spiraling into sensory overload, a visceral surge of mutation, poison, and drifting souls plugging the void.","sonnet":"Overload from a world saturated with violence, commerce, and lost souls: the narrator feels his veins about to explode under the pressure of absorbing it all. Blood becomes the symbol of both life force and systemic poison — genetic, social, spiritual — transforming him beyond control.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~eat-you-alive":{"haiku":"A parasite feeds on another person's life force, running at high intensity and consuming them alive while burning through everything.","opus":"Richard Kruspe's side project channels consuming, almost vampiric obsession, love that devours and a lover cast as a willing parasite. The Rammstein muscle powers a headlong rush toward being ripped from heaven itself.","sonnet":"Raw, predatory desire — the narrator wants to consume someone completely, a hunger that's both erotic and destructive, framed as a parasite that has found its host and won't let go.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~emigrate":{"haiku":"A speaker seeks land and opportunity across oceans and skies to fill his destiny; he has been alone and now claims what he deserves while shedding family ties.","opus":"The English-language solo project of Rammstein guitarist Richard Kruspe, born from his own relocation to New York, this title track chants the drive of a man cutting himself off from home and kin to chase opportunity abroad. Ambition curdles into something cold and mercenary in a hollow new world, yet the compulsion to press onward, past dread, never lets up.","sonnet":"Severing family ties and crossing oceans in pursuit of destiny — the immigrant narrator cuts every cord for a land of opportunity, driven by loneliness and hunger for what feels rightfully theirs. Fear is walked through rather than avoided; departure is declared an act of will.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~hypothetical":{"haiku":"A speaker explores transgressive fantasy about bodily control and intimate violation, framed as speculation and desire for dominance.","opus":"A raw, aggressive song of predatory desire and lost control, the narrator promising to do unspeakable things, peeling apart and consuming his subject like butterflies inside, the word hypothetical repeated as a thin, provocative fig leaf over blunt lust. Emigrate is Richard Kruspe of Rammstein's outlet, and this channels his main band's confrontational sexuality and industrial menace into a deliberately transgressive English-language track.","sonnet":"Raw, explicit desire framed as thought experiment — the speaker laying out in physical detail what he intends to do, the other person held in the fantasy as receiver. The word hypothetical keeps surfacing as a thin veneer over something that feels entirely real and urgent, and the song refuses to soften or distance what it is actually describing.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~i-m-still-alive":{"haiku":"A survivor of catastrophe and loss emerges battered but defiant, riding lightning and claiming resurrection. Despite hardship, they persist with renewed electrical force.","opus":"A speaker who has given up everything and burned out defiantly insists he is still alive and will come back, powered by an inner light and the fuse of his own attack. It's a charged declaration of survival and resurgence.","sonnet":"A voice on the edge of collapse insists it is still alive despite having given up everything and been drained of energy; it reaches toward light and calls out not to be left behind. The song is built around the tension between exhaustion and a stubborn will to continue.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~in-my-tears":{"haiku":"One person's sickness and poison spreads into another, draining their pain while the speaker's low self-esteem and psychosis bleed through, creating mutual toxicity.","opus":"Richard Kruspe's Emigrate side project renders codependency as a slow-burning rock dirge: a speaker so entangled in another that his illness and their suffering become one shared wound. Beneath the promises to fix and to absorb runs plain mental-illness confession, and a refrain begging his own grief not to finish him off.","sonnet":"A toxic codependence where sickness is shared and psychosis seeps between two people. One person absorbs the other's pain as self-punishment while low self-esteem amplifies the spiral — the plea is not to drown in the tears that come from within rather than without.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~lead-you-on":{"haiku":"A manipulator promises deep connection and salvation while openly declaring they will deceive and lead their target astray.","opus":"Richard Kruspe voicing a seducer who's honest about his dishonesty, promising connection while telling you outright he'll use you. The bleak kicker is the shared nihilism underneath, the sense that nobody's coming to save either of them anyway.","sonnet":"A person who knows they are offering a connection that leads nowhere — no one is waiting for them, no one saves them — but they keep drawing someone in anyway, compelled by the intimacy even while being upfront that it ends in nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~let-me-break":{"haiku":"A person offers to sacrifice themselves emotionally to break down and heal another person.","opus":"Richard Kruspe's paradox of love as demolition, offering to break someone down for their own sake as an act of devotion. The tension is tenderness expressed through destruction, sacrificing himself to dismantle another's defenses.","sonnet":"A declaration of total devotion so extreme it becomes self-destructive — the narrator offers to break themselves apart, lose, suffer, and sacrifice repeatedly in order to support the person they love.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~my-world":{"haiku":"A transformative apocalypse rebuilds existence through burning passion and the birth of simultaneous love and rage in a new world.","opus":"Rammstein guitarist Richard Kruspe's English-language project casts creation as domination and rescue at once, remaking a lover and a burning new world in one breath, love and rage named as twin birthrights. Grandiose, possessive romance.","sonnet":"An aggressive declaration of possessive love — the narrator wants to capture and remake someone in the image of his burning inner world, framing passion as something simultaneously violent and creative.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~new-york-city":{"haiku":"The city captivates with its energy and possibility—lights, culture, constant motion—and the speaker feels alive in its synergy and refusal to sleep.","opus":"Kruspe, who left Germany for Manhattan, writes his adopted city as a dazzling, never-quiet machine that makes him feel electrically alive amid its contradictions of glamour and grime. Trading Rammstein's heaviness for polished stadium rock, it is basically a wide-eyed booster anthem to metropolitan ambition and reinvention.","sonnet":"A love letter to New York as pure kinetic energy: the city never sleeps, never resolves, never stops — and the narrator finds himself alive in that chaos. Winning and losing are equally plausible and equally irrelevant; what matters is chasing the feeling of being somewhere that never stops moving.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~resolution":{"haiku":"The speaker loses control and struggles to find resolution, moving between geographical locations and internal states searching for peace and self-understanding.","opus":"Emigrate, the solo outlet of Rammstein guitarist Richard Kruspe, tracks a push from disorientation toward clarity, a narrator who has lost his grip and is searching for a way forward. Testing himself and battling for the right outcome, he clings to a fragment of himself, the song charting the arc from bewilderment to a hard-earned sense of understanding.","sonnet":"A person lost in interior confusion gradually locates a thread back to clarity. Moving between states of drift and self-imposed testing, the mind cycles through confusion and conclusion until a resolution comes into focus — felt and seen before it is fully understood, personal and quietly certain.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~temptation":{"haiku":"A wanderer struggles against lures that distract from home and purpose, acknowledging both temptation's appeal and the cost of yielding to it. The song explores conflict between desire and duty.","opus":"A wanderer drifts nowhere and aches to go home, drawn astray by temptation while frustration warns a reckoning is coming. Confession, apology, and a longing to disappear inside another blur the line between guilt and desire. Emigrate is the side project of Rammstein guitarist Richard Kruspe, and this English-language solo detour trades industrial menace for brooding, melodic restlessness about a soul in flux.","sonnet":"A restless figure caught between desire and guilt finds himself pulled off course by temptation and unable to locate home — or himself. He admits to lying, to wanting to disappear into someone else, to the knowledge that a reckoning is coming. The song is a confession wearing the shape of longing, exhausted by its own recurring drift.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~this-is-what":{"haiku":"A speaker experiences intoxication and transcendence, claiming their singular moment makes them feel and dream forever, imagining power and mass adulation.","opus":"Emigrate stage rapturous, uneasy submission, a narrator so elevated the glow scorches him, absorbed into a single herd trailing one guiding will he mistakes for the divine. He brands himself a soul-seller yet swears this alone stirs and sustains him. Kruspe's project blends stadium-rock exaltation with disquiet, ecstasy and self-degradation knotted together.","sonnet":"A performer or true believer surrenders entirely to the intoxicating power of the crowd, the stage, and the call of something larger. Sold soul, war dreams, screaming masses — the narrator names the experience as his one shot at feeling eternal, and accepts every cost of that transaction.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~wake-up":{"haiku":"A person calls for urgent awareness and change after realizing they've been trapped in a problematic situation.","opus":"An urgent call to snap out of denial and reclaim what's yours; the narrator, who fell into the same trap of pretending everything was okay, demands the listener turn their life around and start over from the ground up.","sonnet":"A call to snap out of complacency in a relationship or in life — the narrator has finally seen and felt things they'd been blind to, and urges their partner to undergo the same awakening before it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~war":{"haiku":"Societies burn while media commercializes suffering and religion, using fear and distraction to control perception of conflict.","opus":"A bleak takedown of war as spectacle and manipulation, angels dwelling in scorched, ruined settlements while heralds brand the singer's kind as dark sorcerers within an industry that keeps footage running to broadcast dread and hypnotize the public. The chorus insists this is merely war's true sound, jarring cries engineered to keep everyone afraid. Emigrate, Richard Kruspe of Rammstein's project, gives the media-and-faith critique a stark industrial charge.","sonnet":"A media critique dressed in apocalyptic imagery — angels arriving in ghost towns, black-charred tombs, and cameras kept rolling so that war can be packaged, controlled, and sold back to a terrified audience. Religion and celebrity violence are treated as interchangeable products of the same industry, with the broadcast designed not to inform but to keep everyone mesmerized and afraid.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~we-are-together":{"haiku":"Fleeting moments bind two souls in eternal connection despite time's relentless passage, holding tight through uncertainty to preserve unity.","opus":"A vow to hold onto a fleeting moment with someone as time slips away, finding that even the sting of impermanence is what keeps two people bound together as one.","sonnet":"A declaration of permanent union against passing time: holding on through an explosive sting that keeps two people present, memories of flickering light and smoky nights coalescing into the conviction that they have been and will remain together as one.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~you-are-so-beautiful":{"haiku":"A possessive lover offers descent and disintegration; beauty and love become tools for control and mutual dissolution.","opus":"A downward pull toward the ground and underground becomes an invitation to fall together and disintegrate in love, promises of safety braided with hunger and surrender. Beauty and self-destruction are treated as inseparable. Emigrate, Richard Kruspe's project apart from Rammstein, gives this seductive descent a heavy, melodic charge, framing ruin and devotion as one shared plunge.","sonnet":"Attraction here is gravitational and destructive — two people dragging each other underground, disintegrating together in something they call love. The pull is framed as falling, surrender, mutual ruin. Beauty and devastation are inseparable, and the speaker offers to carry the weight of the beloved even as both sink.","src":"haiku"},
"emigrate~you-can-t-get-enough":{"haiku":"One person cannot satisfy their parasitic need for another despite causing harm, drawn in like spiders burrowing under skin and consuming everything offered.","opus":"Richard Kruspe, Rammstein's guitarist, uses his English-language side project to sing of a lover consumed by insatiable craving, an addiction that burrows under the skin and feeds on its own sin. The narrator has become a distant void, hollowed out, no longer the drug he once was. The industrial pulse mirrors a hunger that only ever escalates toward more.","sonnet":"A relationship consumed by compulsion rather than love. One person has withdrawn, recognizing they are no longer the other's anchor — only an enabler of a hunger that grows without limit. Helpless and degraded by that hunger, the other cannot stop reaching for more, even as everything corrodes.","src":"haiku"},
"emilie-autumn~liar":{"haiku":"Obsessive love demands total possession and suffering while masquerading as devotion, as the speaker coerces victimization into proof of passionate control. Beauty emerges only through pain.","opus":"Obsessive love that mistakes possession for devotion, the narrator vowing to bind a lover forever and craving their suffering, the title admitting every tender promise is a lie.","sonnet":"Declarations of absolute devotion — mixing blood, throwing rose petals, dying for the other — are undercut by the relentless chorus of \"liar\": the speaker craves the other's suffering and wants to trap them, revealing that the devotion is possessive obsession rather than love.","src":"haiku"},
"emilie-autumn~opheliac":{"haiku":"A mentally unstable woman manipulates relationships through games and lies, spiraling with depression and suicidal ideation while blaming others for her suffering. She embodies the stereotype of intelligent women portrayed as either angels or devils.","opus":"Emilie Autumn adopts a drowning Shakespearean heroine as her alter ego to dramatize mental illness from the inside, owning the deceit, control games and blame she inflicts on a partner. Behind the theatrical framing sits a candid account of depression, self-disgust and the way a fracturing mind dissociates and turns everyone into a threat.","sonnet":"A brutal self-portrait of someone with borderline traits — the narrator admits she plays games, lies, blames others, and drives people away while being unable to help it, invoking the Ophelia archetype to frame her psychological instability as something both self-aware and beyond her control, with a spoken interlude noting that intelligent girls are more depressed because they see the world clearly.","src":"haiku"},
"emilie-autumn~shalott":{"haiku":"Captivity and desperation drive a woman to escape her tower; she surrenders to inevitable death rather than accept confinement, embracing mythology over safety.","opus":"A retelling of Tennyson's Lady of Shalott, the cursed woman trapped in her tower half-sick of watching life through shadows. Autumn turns it self-aware and bitter, the doomed girl knowing her death will just become someone's song.","sonnet":"A retelling of the Lady of Shalott: the imprisoned weaver, sick of shadows and desperate to see real life, breaks her curse for the sight of a man she loves but who will never know her, accepting death with dark wit as the price of finally stepping outside.","src":"haiku"},
"emilie-autumn~swallow":{"haiku":"A artist confronts her cult-like fame, her complicity in perpetuating her own torment, and the gothic performance of suffering that audiences confuse with authenticity. She promises vengeance if she drowns in this self-invented mythology.","opus":"A raw, self-aware confession about performing a fragile-Victorian persona while drowning under twenty years of it, admitting the fame she claims to disdain is exactly what she craves. Swallowing to lower her own flood, she promises to haunt if it takes her under.","sonnet":"A sharp, self-aware meditation on performance, artistic persona, and the cost of being consumed by an audience — the narrator admits her songs are repetitive and her fairy-tale image is constructed, but she can't trust anyone enough to step out of it, and the drowning/swallowing metaphor runs through twenty years of sinking slowly.","src":"haiku"},
"emily-jeffri~fatal":{"haiku":"Seduction and poisoned attraction lead to betrayal and abandonment; a lover's flight with the speaker leaves the deceived crying like a child.","opus":"A femme-fatale murder ballad where lust and poison are the same intoxicant, the narrator luring one lover to run off with another and leaving a body behind. Its allure is the calm relish with which she catalogs seduction as annihilation.","sonnet":"A woman holds knowledge that will destroy a man who waits for her like a child — bearer of bad news, poisonous fruit tasteless to a tongue blind to danger, lust the engine of the whole exchange. He's lifeless by the end, and the expected grief — how are we meant to hide this — arrives not as horror but as a kind of cold inevitability.","src":"haiku"},
"emily-jeffri~incubus":{"haiku":"The speaker watches another woman spiral under a predatory lover's grip, marking the incubus as a vampiric force. Vigilance and care emerge as the only counter to the wound that keeps reopening.","opus":"A rival positions herself against a predatory partner, seeing his true monstrous nature and vowing he'll never claim her too. The tension is her strange tenderness toward the woman he drains, offering to give what he takes.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses a man who emotionally destroys women — blood in his teeth, driving her up the wall while she keeps returning — positioning themselves as the one who actually tends to the victim's wounds and gives her what she wants, warning 'I know exactly what you are' and vowing he will never get them.","src":"haiku"},
"eminem~8-mile":{"haiku":"A young man from the working-class borderlands struggles against poverty and doubt to claim his voice and escape his origins.","opus":"The film's anthem of clawing out of Detroit poverty, stage fright and self-doubt versus the refusal to stay in the trailer. It is about choosing your own path over the dead-end life you were handed, whatever it costs.","sonnet":"A young man living in a Detroit trailer walks the 8 Mile border between his old life and his ambition, rehearsing the decision to leave — his mother, his little sister, his failed rap attempts — and finally committing to cross over and never look back. The road itself is both the trap and the only exit.","src":"haiku"},
"eminem~berzerk-explicit":{"haiku":"The speaker is a wild, aggressive rapper full of confidence and showmanship, ready to disrupt any setting and remind people of his unmatched skill. He's reckless, cocky, and commands the party atmosphere.","opus":"Eminem's deliberate return to a stripped-down, old-school rap sound, stacked with rapid-fire boasts, era name-drops, and absurdist wordplay. The whole thing is a call to abandon inhibition and treat cutting loose as the only sane answer to how briefly anyone gets to live.","sonnet":"Eminem returning to his roots with aggressive wordplay and old-school hip-hop energy — shout-outs to Kid Rock and Kendrick, callbacks to his battle rap origins, and a refrain urging everyone to let loose and go berserk before life runs out. It's a brag-and-party track dressed in nostalgia for raw rap craft.","src":"haiku"},
"eminem~houdini":{"haiku":"The rapper reclaims transgressive power through wordplay and provocation, weaponizing controversy while admitting darkness and maintaining dominance.","opus":"A gleeful comeback that resurrects the Slim Shady persona, courting cancellation with deliberately offensive provocation while marveling at his own success and vanishing, Houdini-like, from every attempt to pin him down.","sonnet":"Shady returns with a Houdini-style vanishing act, cataloguing his provocations — hitting eight-year-olds with participation trophies, dismissing cancel culture, taking shots at everyone including himself and Paul — as a white-knuckle assertion that he remains ahead of every challenger, pulling one last trick from the bag before disappearing again.","src":"haiku"},
"eminem~lose-yourself":{"haiku":"Seizing a single life-changing opportunity before it's lost; a nervous performer chokes under pressure but refuses to surrender what might be his only shot.","opus":"A tense portrait of a rapper's single chance at escape, opening with the trembling hands and choking dread of the spotlight before racing through hardship, brief acclaim and the fear of freezing up, all pressing you to grab the moment while it lasts. Eminem wrote it as the theme for his semi-autobiographical film, and its driving momentum made it a defining hip-hop statement on ambition and pressure.","sonnet":"One shot at escape, measured against a whole life of limitation — Rabbit standing before a crowd with vomit on his sweater and words that won't come out, then reaching for a version of himself that can actually seize the moment. The second half turns darker, charting the cost of near-success: distance from a daughter, the public moving on, the machine grinding forward whether or not he's on it.","src":"haiku"},
"eminem~love-the-way-you-lie":{"haiku":"Cyclical domestic violence and toxic attachment trap two people in a pattern of harm they cannot escape or deny.","opus":"An unflinching portrait of an abusive relationship from the inside, where the addiction to the highs excuses the violence and the cycle keeps resetting. It lands because it refuses to make either party a clean victim, mapping how love and harm feed each other.","sonnet":"A violently co-dependent relationship is described from inside its own logic: the highs are superheroic, the lows are brutal, the cycles repeat despite promises and remorse. The speaker knows it is destruction, is unable to stop, and the song ends not with escape but with another round of gasoline and apology.","src":"haiku"},
"eminem~mockingbird":{"haiku":"A father comforts his daughters through family breakdown, promising protection while acknowledging his helplessness against larger forces.","opus":"Eminem speaking directly to his daughter and niece, apologizing for the chaos of his marriage and fame, promising the life he never had. The most naked he gets, a father trying to shield kids from what he couldn't hide.","sonnet":"A father addresses his daughter Hailie through her confusion and grief, narrating the collapse of the family — poverty, absent mother, the chaos of sudden fame — and promising to give her everything while confessing he has already failed in the ways that matter most. Tenderness and guilt are inseparable throughout.","src":"haiku"},
"eminem~phenomenal":{"haiku":"Raw determination and self-belief forge a path to excellence through relentless effort and refusal to accept limitation.","opus":"A relentless self-motivation piece turning doubt and abuse into fuel, willing greatness into being through sheer refusal to quit. It is about converting every insult and setback into the drive to become unstoppable.","sonnet":"An extended self-motivational address that doubles as instruction to anyone who has been knocked down: doubt is fuel, adversity is the sharpener, and the only requirement is absolute refusal to stop. The speaker alternates between personal testimony and direct coaching, turning perseverance into an almost physical combat.","src":"haiku"},
"eminem~river-feat-ed-sheeran":{"haiku":"Infidelity, manipulation, and emotional devastation ripple through relationships; the speaker sees the wreckage he causes but continues anyway.","opus":"A brutally honest confession of infidelity spiraling into a pregnancy and its aftermath, guilt he can't outrun. Eminem uses the river as absolution he knows he doesn't deserve, admitting the harm without excusing it.","sonnet":"A man confesses a web of serial infidelity and manipulation that culminated in a woman becoming pregnant, then being pressured into termination — the chorus functioning as a plea for spiritual absolution the speaker knows he doesn't deserve. Each verse unpacks a different layer of exploitation, with growing self-disgust culminating in a direct, ugly admission of moral failure.","src":"haiku"},
"eminem~when-i-m-gone":{"haiku":"A father reconciles his departing reputation with family abandonment, torn between ambition and parental responsibility.","opus":"Eminem wrestling with how his fame and his daughter's needs tear at each other, imagining his own death and her grief. The turn is realizing the choice was always his: to show his family love while there's still time.","sonnet":"A father trapped between the addiction of performing for crowds and the devastation this absence causes his daughter moves through waking nightmares and dreams in which Hailie begs him not to leave. He finally wakes to ordinary morning — she is outside swinging — with the understanding that staying is the only thing that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"eminence~here-ft-anna-yvette":{"haiku":"Former lovers were once inseparable and promised nothing could separate them, but now they are far from home—ripped photographs and broken memories remain.","opus":"A melancholy electronic track about a relationship that quietly fell apart. Sifting through torn old pictures and fading recollections, the singer marvels at how two people who once believed they were inseparable ended up strangers, unsure of what comes next. The guest vocal adds an airy sadness to the unanswered final question.","sonnet":"A drifting-apart told through torn photographs — two people who promised nothing could separate them now finding themselves far from a place that no longer exists. The question repeats without an answer: where do you go when the shared home is gone and the map has run out of road?","src":"haiku"},
"emma-aibara~do-you-miss-me":{"haiku":"Absence echoes through the speaker's entire existence, where the beloved haunts every moment of distance. Silence and secrecy mask a need that cannot be voiced.","opus":"A bilingual ache of anxious attachment, the singer needing constant proof she's remembered while bracing to be burned. The vulnerability is in how freedom and dread live side by side whenever this person is near.","sonnet":"A voice caught between silence and confession — familiar with being burned, unable to relate to anyone else, the other person running through their head on repeat — asks the central question directly: do you miss me? The Japanese insertions deepen a sense of a memory-loop, the feeling named but never quite resolved.","src":"haiku"},
"emma-aibara~i-can-t-go-back":{"haiku":"Irreversible descent and refusal of return; the speaker rejects the past while hovering between sky and cruelty, unable to ascend or escape gravity's hold.","opus":"A vow never to return to some cruel place below, the flight upward reading as both escape and dissociation. Its Japanese-English drift heightens the sense of leaving a harsh reality behind for good.","sonnet":"A dissociated ascent — the body floats in a blue sky while cruelty remains visible far below, and the singer declares she is never going back down to it. The Japanese fragments (\"the body / the blue sky\", \"I can see / down there it's all cruel\") anchor the physical sensation; the trees grow to guide those left below, but the narrator's direction is already fixed upward.","src":"haiku"},
"emma-ruth-rundle~darkhorse":{"haiku":"Inherited trauma and unconventional strength emerge through community and vocal transmission; the narrator claims agency despite dark inheritance and fallow ground.","opus":"A dark, driving benediction sung to a sister figure to keep running and stop lying down under someone's pursuit. It is about the private strength you nurture and ride out of ruin, no one else able to.","sonnet":"Two women — possibly sisters — are urged to stop running from something dark that gains on them and instead to stand high, laugh the weight out, and ride their own darkhorse: the private fear or power that nobody else can see or saddle.","src":"haiku"},
"emma-ruth-rundle~hollywood":{"opus":"The collision between a cherished private fantasy of love and its unglamorous, disappointing reality. Rundle's spectral folk turns the refrain into a mantra of disillusion, love refusing to be the cinematic thing you imagined.","src":"opus"},
"emmure~derelict":{"haiku":"Speaker spirals in self-loathing and social dysfunction, admitting they are worthless and toxic.","opus":"Emmure pare their deathcore down to raw self-abasement, with vocalist Frankie Palmeri narrating a man who declares himself worthless, unable to function or connect, hiding his wreckage behind a fake grin. The closing line twists that self-loathing outward, daring the listener to become just as ruined as he is.","sonnet":"Raw self-condemnation delivered without softening — a person presents themselves as fundamentally broken, incapable of coping, and hiding the worst of it behind a surface smile. The admission is also a dare: this is the unvarnished reality of being this way, and it offers nothing aspirational.","src":"haiku"},
"emmure~f-inally-u-nderstanding-n-othing":{"haiku":"Defiance born from lifelong rejection; the speaker transforms doubt into fuel, proving worth through persistence despite universal abandonment.","opus":"A defiant address to the doubters who wrote the singer off, doubling down on loyalty and dumb, unapologetic aggression. The deathcore bravado is really a bond with the fans who stayed through years of contempt.","sonnet":"A rallying address to a long-loyal crowd against everyone who hated, doubted, and gave up — it's our time, our place, what we love is what we know. The self-aware stupidity (it's time to get dumb with the realness) is the philosophy: authenticity over cleverness, staying in it after all these years as its own vindication.","src":"haiku"},
"emmure~gypsy-disco":{"opus":"A bleak confession of a broke, homeless musician wracked with regret over the life he chose, dismissing his worthless streams and repeatedly begging God to kill him.","sonnet":"A brutally self-lacerating account of career collapse: the narrator is homeless, broke, and unable to see a future in music, and has come to hate every choice that led him here — streaming makes it worthless, the legacy is regret, and he wants to be killed to end the spiral.","src":"sonnet"},
"emmure~pigs-ear":{"haiku":"Failure defines identity; the speaker is a cautionary example of wasted potential and life spiraling downward irretrievably.","opus":"Framed by a news clip about a broke, fired, going-nowhere musician, the deathcore band Emmure inhabit a man who has accepted his own worthlessness and treats his wasted life as a warning to others. The song turns self-hatred into a blunt, repeated confession of a ruin he sees no way out of.","sonnet":"A confessional framed by a news broadcast — someone publicly labeled a failure internalizes every charge. Fired from every job, invisible as a musician, locked in a downward path with no exit and no illusions: the self is offered as the perfect cautionary example.","src":"haiku"},
"emperor~thus-spake-the-nightspirit":{"haiku":"A mysterious spirit imparts wisdom about strength, weakness, and death to listeners too blind to comprehend; only the strong can heed its warning.","opus":"Emperor, pioneers of Norwegian symphonic black metal, mount a Nietzschean summons to see past ordinary limits into hidden realms. Ihsahn's spirit-voice sneers at a world of the blind and the meek while exalting the strong who conquer death through wisdom, the grandeur cold, philosophical, and self-appointed.","sonnet":"A figure of supreme esoteric will addresses a world too blind and weak to receive its revelations. Contemptuous of ordinary life and its contradictions, the nightspirit declares its own authority — wisdom and daring as the only paths past fear and death — and wonders if anything it speaks lands at all.","src":"haiku"},
"empire-fall~icantwaittoforget":{"haiku":"Discarding becomes the ultimate assertion; the speaker burns bridges to prove they no longer need those who tried to diminish them.","opus":"Cutting off a toxic friend-turned-enemy, dismissing their loser mindset and fraudulent swagger as just a name he'd be glad to put out of mind while he reaches for something higher.","sonnet":"A scorched-earth farewell to a toxic person who kept breaking the speaker down thinking it would increase dependency — the speaker is done, reaching for the stars while the other lies in bed, and cannot wait for the day that name means nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"empire-of-the-sun~high-and-low":{"haiku":"Emotional volatility and commitment clash with freedom; the narrator oscillates between high euphoria and low despair, desperate to keep someone despite unpredictability.","opus":"A shimmering ode to a wild, drifting muse named for LSD, wanting to hold onto someone who's forever slipping toward the stars. The high-and-low is the swing of a love you can't control but won't release.","sonnet":"The narrator watches a woman called Alice D drift through all-night escapades and altered states, wanting to hold onto her through the emotional peaks and valleys they share while knowing she keeps slipping beyond reach.","src":"haiku"},
"empire-of-the-sun~walking-on-a-dream":{"haiku":"Two people merge into one transcendent state, always chasing thrill and speed, caught between dream logic and fleeting connection.","opus":"Sun-drunk electropop about the euphoric early rush of connection, two people becoming one, chasing a thrill and afraid to look down in case it isn't real. The joy is real but knowingly precarious, a dream you don't want to wake from.","sonnet":"About the overwhelming, almost unreal feeling of falling in love — two people merging into one in a state that feels like a waking dream, constantly chasing the thrill of connection and trying to hold onto something that might not last past morning.","src":"haiku"},
"empire-state-bastard~moi":{"opus":"A savage caricature of the conscience-free manipulator who blames everyone but himself. Simon Neil's Biffy Clyro side-project turns its punk snarl on a culture that rewards brash certainty over ever being right.","src":"opus"},
"empire-state-bastard~stutter":{"opus":"Contempt for empty thoughts-and-prayers piety collides with the singer's own struggle to force his words out. The refusal to stay silent becomes the point: barely composed, he insists on speaking anyway.","src":"opus"},
"employed-to-serve~force-fed":{"opus":"A furious dismissal of someone endlessly imposing their views on the singer, so infuriating he'd sooner mutilate himself to stop hearing them; done with it, he tunes them out as meaningless static and imagines silencing their mouth for good.","sonnet":"The narrator is suffocated by someone who talks incessantly, never listens, and showers them with unwanted opinions — they fantasize about silencing the person permanently and tune out entirely, having had more than enough.","src":"sonnet"},
"employed-to-serve~party-s-over":{"haiku":"Humanity and nature are destructive forces sent to annihilate; the speaker feels unwelcome everywhere and ready to obliterate.","opus":"Being sober only sharpens the narrator's estrangement, marooned among strangers with an inner voice demanding to know why, feeling like some worthless alien with no place anywhere. The UK metalcore band turns that isolation into a wrecking mission, refusing to quit until the whole thing's finished and dismissing humanity as mud-bound beasts pretending at divinity.","sonnet":"Sobriety strips away the filters and leaves only alienation and self-disgust. Clarity about human destructiveness — the narrator included — produces a feeling of not belonging anywhere on the planet, while the world presses on, animals playing god in mud.","src":"haiku"},
"endless-heights~cold-hard-kiss":{"haiku":"Drowning in emotional collapse, the speaker cannot articulate distress. Speech fails as they watch themselves and others fall apart.","opus":"Drowning imagery for a love turning cold and final, a kiss frozen over as someone falls apart. The narrator watches the collapse knowing his own reassurances are cheap, casting himself as just another disposable runaway.","sonnet":"A drowning metaphor for emotional dissociation in a collapsing relationship — the narrator is sinking and going through the motions, watching someone fall apart while his own words ring hollow and he slips into numbness.","src":"haiku"},
"ends-in-tragedy~shut-the-gates":{"opus":"Raw hardcore compression of torment as a physical, blood-red thing that devours a life. It reads as a confrontation with someone who shames the singer's pain, the anguish curdling into instinctive rage.","src":"opus"},
"end~covet-not":{"haiku":"Rotting flesh and spiritual corruption merge as the narrator relinquishes their hunger for impossible desires, embracing annihilation as atonement for sins carved into their body.","opus":"A self-lacerating vow to starve out one's own destructive cravings, framing the purge of desire as an atonement carved into the flesh.","sonnet":"A speaker commands themselves to extinguish forbidden desire — \"covet not what can't belong\" — through ritual self-destruction, dragging through forests of blunt blades and burning their world as atonement for the incisions their longing has made in others.","src":"haiku"},
"end~pariah":{"opus":"A furious verdict against an outcast/traitor, executed in grotesque imagery of dismemberment and unmasking; the target is stripped of identity until nothing remains, damned by the singer as their absolute end.","sonnet":"An outcast denounces a coward who masks their true self and has been condemned and cast out — the narrator strips away the person's disguise and sentences them to total erasure, described through grotesque imagery of glass shards, wax wounds, and seas of snakes.","src":"sonnet"},
"enemies-everywhere~fall-from-grace":{"opus":"A wrathful sentence pronounced on someone doomed to hell for the pain they inflicted, all void and drained souls. The deathcore relish is in the finality: no whisper answers their cry, and reality is the punishment.","src":"opus"},
"engrave~alive":{"haiku":"Scars accumulate as proof of survival as the speaker claws toward self-recognition, discovering that feeling something—even pain—confirms existence against the void of self-doubt.","opus":"Scars and self-doubt mounting daily, yet the recurring realization that he still feels alive becomes the foothold to begin the climb out of misery and refuse his own demise.","sonnet":"A struggle to stay present through self-doubt and accumulated scars: the speaker feels like they are caving in and drowning in misery, yet keeps returning to the refrain \"I still feel alive\" as evidence that the fall does not have to be final.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~broken-man":{"haiku":"Male vulnerability and emotional collapse, masculinity shattered by circumstance or betrayal.","opus":"A man who learned to armor himself into isolation, unable to ask for help and locked inside his own head. The ballad's hope is fragile, a plea for someone to stay and give him a reason to hold on.","sonnet":"A man who has always had to learn alone, pushed everyone away, scarred himself silent, and now sings a ballad to that brokenness while asking someone to lift him when he falls and see him through it. \"So will I ever see who I'm supposed to be\" is the question the song asks without flinching.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~burn":{"haiku":"Pain transforms into inner fire; the speaker rises renewed from their own ashes after learning to stand.","opus":"The metalcore act Enmy cast recovery from betrayal as literal fire. Left in the dark by someone whose promises turned out to be lies, the narrator chooses to torch the wreckage of that relationship rather than mourn it, treating pain as fuel for a tougher, remade version of himself.","sonnet":"Betrayal and abandonment become the kindling for an interior resurrection. After being left in darkness by someone whose promises proved hollow, an internal fire catches and grows until it burns away the hollow version left behind. What rises from the ashes is permanently different from what entered the collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~clarity":{"haiku":"A person breaks free from intrusive thoughts planted by someone else and reclaims autonomy over their own mind.","opus":"Breaking free from a toxic person who planted spreading, self-doubting thoughts in the narrator's head; the resolution is to replace that person's voice with his own, chase his dreams until death, and never see them again.","sonnet":"About escaping a toxic relationship or negative influence that planted self-doubt, and choosing to prioritize your own identity over the other person's hold on your mind.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~damn":{"haiku":"Despair and loss of purpose drive the speaker through cycles of blind living and self-deception until confronting harsh truths and the decay of toxic relationships.","opus":"Trapped in a life poisoned by someone's lies and selfish pride, the speaker has lost the will to go on and pours it into a song, searching for answers before he loses his mind.","sonnet":"Someone trapped in a destructive relationship driven by lies and self-serving pride has reached a breaking point, losing the will to continue while still searching, uncertainly, for something better.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~demon-eyes":{"haiku":"A deceptive manipulator uses twisted truth and demonic gaze to target and corrupt innocence.","opus":"Enmy confront a serpent-tongued manipulator who paints skies red and targets on the narrator's back while claiming martyrdom. The refrain demanding to know who he is in those demon eyes probes how a liar recasts guilt as innocence. Twisted mind, forked tongue, and worn horns mark the accused, the metalcore hook turning betrayal into a reckoning with someone convinced by their own deceit.","sonnet":"An adversary who projects malice outward while being consumed by it inward — painting targets, forking language, claiming victimhood while wielding harm. The accused insists the real evil lives behind those eyes, that innocence dies in the gaze of someone too twisted by self-deception to see it.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~fake":{"haiku":"Authenticity erodes under performative pressure as the speaker recognizes mutual deception dissolving trust and connection.","opus":"The slow rot of a relationship built on mutual self-deception, resentment curdling into hate as both parties lie to sleep at night. Its clarity comes too late: the whole thing was constructed just to be broken.","sonnet":"A friendship or relationship has been revealed as performative and dishonest — \"you're lying to my face\" — and the speaker, having watched resentment curdle into hate, calls it out and refuses to take the bait of continuing the illusion.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~hourglass":{"haiku":"Time erodes connection while blame cycles endlessly; the speaker and other spin trapped together toward oblivion.","opus":"A relationship strangled by pride, both parties lost in time with no rhyme or reason left. The metalcore stance is weary defiance, shouldering blame while refusing to surrender the soul to someone who was never really the same as you.","sonnet":"Two people who are fundamentally incompatible watching their relationship erode — neither can get past their pride or differences, time is running out, and the speaker finally refuses to keep shouldering blame, declaring they won't surrender their soul no matter how stuck both of them feel.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~on-the-edge":{"haiku":"A speaker has surrendered will and hope to survive. Death already lives inside—caging the body means nothing.","opus":"Someone hauled to the bottom of their despair who turns exhaustion into grim invincibility, daring the world to cage or destroy what's already dead inside them. Giving up on giving up becomes its own bleak kind of survival.","sonnet":"A song about someone so thoroughly destroyed by another person's treatment that they've gone past caring about their own survival — already emotionally dead, the narrator dares their tormentor to cage or kill them, knowing there's nothing left to take, while refusing to listen to any more of their words.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~our-demise":{"haiku":"Apocalypse reigns as predator and prey collapse into each other; survival demands surrender to darkness and the acceptance of collective annihilation.","opus":"A zombie-apocalypse survival horror where society has collapsed and the living become the prey, hope dying as the dead overrun them and the hunter finally becomes the hunted on judgment day.","sonnet":"A zombie-apocalypse narrative where survivors are run down, cornered, and picked off one by one — 'smashed through the windows, broke down the door' — until blood is on every face and the only honest soundtrack left is the one playing at the end of everything.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~silver-bullet":{"haiku":"Primal hunger awakens as the speaker transforms into predator; the lover becomes both trigger and target in a cycle of transformation and consumption.","opus":"A werewolf transformation as loss of self-control, an uncontrollable predatory urge clawing at the mind, the beloved cast as the silver bullet that could end the monster others made of him.","sonnet":"A transformation narrative in werewolf metaphor: something foreign and clawing takes root inside the speaker under moonlight, overriding their will and driving them to feed — the person who triggered it is cast as both cause and cure, 'you are my silver bullet,' the monster made by them.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~survive":{"haiku":"Endurance through fire and systematic destruction; refusal to break despite relentless attempts to annihilate.","opus":"A defiant survivor's creed, refusing to break through fire, water and everyone aiming for the heart. The core is the raw need to know the resilience is real because living a lie is the one thing it can't do.","sonnet":"Pure defiance against forces that tried to bleed the speaker dry — through fire, storm, and waves designed to drown — the declaration carries no complexity, only the refusal to fall apart or go back into the dark, and the survival itself becomes the meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~the-cure":{"haiku":"Psychological confinement manifests as ticking bomb; medication and isolation fail as the speaker spirals deeper into trapped desperation.","opus":"Anxiety as a ticking time bomb in the head, pacing and overthinking in a spinning room, desperate for a cure that medication never delivers to break the vicious cycle.","sonnet":"About being trapped inside anxiety and mental illness with no fix in sight — the speaker is pacing, overthinking, isolating, and watching the walls close in, desperate for a cure that medication hasn't provided and that may not exist.","src":"haiku"},
"enmy~waiting-on-you":{"haiku":"Toxic dependency mimics intoxication, drowning the speaker in escalating addiction to another person until the cost becomes too high to ignore.","opus":"A toxic relationship rendered as addiction, where the other person is the high, the fever, and the comedown all at once. The turning point is deciding to quit the drug and stop waiting for a high that only ever brought him down.","sonnet":"About recognizing a relationship as a drug and finally deciding to quit — the narrator was intoxicated by this person, drowning in their absence, always blacking out and promising to cut them off but unable to keep it. The emotional turn is deciding to stop waiting on someone who was never actually a high but always a comedown, and giving up on the addiction that only brought pain.","src":"haiku"},
"ennio-morricone~vamos-a-matar-compa-eros":{"opus":"Morricone's spaghetti-western call to arms, rallying students, rebels and workers as brothers who must kill and die to win. It romanticizes revolutionary sacrifice, painting the sky red with the cost of liberation.","src":"opus"},
"enn~a-reprise-that-girl":{"opus":"An unsettling meditation on consuming another person, watching and feeding on a woman's self-sufficiency as if to fill one's own emptiness. Death and appetite entwine, the victory always hollow, the air still heavy.","src":"opus"},
"enola-gay~scrappers":{"haiku":"Doormen harass nightclub patrons, scrutinizing IDs, kicking them out, and using aggression and streaming to humiliate—wielding dominance through cruelty and social power.","opus":"A cross-tattooed bouncer wielding pettiness like a doorman's power humiliates would-be clubgoers, live-streaming a rejected ID and grabbing men by the collar while cameras go conveniently dark. Enola Gay, a Belfast punk-rap outfit, aim their gritty spoken-shout delivery at gatekeeping and small-scale abuse of authority, sketching a nightlife scene where being denied entry curdles into humiliation and menace.","sonnet":"A bouncer on the door of a club exercises petty, arbitrary power — turning away people by appearance, streaming humiliations live, treating patrons like animals on a leash. The song catalogs the ritual indignities of nightclub entry culture in Ireland with sardonic specificity, casting the doorman as a credentialed tyrant in a cross-hatch jacket.","src":"haiku"},
"enox~blame-shift":{"haiku":"Confronts someone who deflects responsibility and lacks integrity, having proven themselves faithless despite support. Severs ties after recognizing their refusal to change.","opus":"A furious kiss-off to a two-faced person who always deflects responsibility, the singer done repeating himself as they spin in circles and shift the blame, calling them fake and spineless and framing the decision to cut them off as his best move. Late verses turn the accusation into a mirror. The register is pure hardcore venom, all profane confrontation and betrayal, delivered with breakdown-ready aggression.","sonnet":"A severance letter addressed to someone who consistently turned their failures into accusations — a speaker who kept covering for a person who never changed and finally cut them off for good. The satisfaction is blunt and clear: cutting you off was the best decision, and the refusal to accept the blame is now absolute and non-negotiable.","src":"haiku"},
"enox~mortar-shell":{"haiku":"Warfare and explosion numb the speaker into compliance; adrenaline masks horror while survival demands emotional anesthesia and the choice between fighting or captivity.","opus":"A soldier's-eye account of combat where killing is the assignment and dread the ever-present shadow, a rush of hormones clouding him amid the barrage. Endurance means going cold to flame and gore while choosing to resist or give up, driving everything toward its limit.","sonnet":"A soldier in the heat of combat is told to stay numb to fire and blood as the only survival strategy — \"to kill is the purpose, to fear is the process\" — the mortar shell as both literal threat and the condition of warfare that grinds the human down to reflex.","src":"haiku"},
"enox~radiance":{"haiku":"Burning bridges and destroying everything to prove vitality, sacrificing stability and relationships to feel alive and answer questions of worth.","opus":"Enox turn metalcore into an ultimatum from someone who torches every connection just to feel that he still exists, having let love curdle into something ruinous. Refusing rescue and insisting on suffering his own way, he channels rage and defiance while still, contradictorily, begging to be truly seen.","sonnet":"Love weaponized burns down its own shrines — the narrator stands in darkness after a destructive relationship, unable to feel light, burning bridges to prove survival. The hunger for meaning and anger redirected into something purposeful drives a circular, desperate orbit.","src":"haiku"},
"enslaved~homebound":{"haiku":"During apocalyptic collapse—when illusions fade and old powers die—the speaker becomes a steady guide crossing between worlds toward a portal.","opus":"Enslaved, the Norwegian veterans who pulled black metal toward progressive ambition, chart a crossing through in-between realms where deceptive lights mislead and wailing voices steer the traveler. Standing firm as buildings tumble and shackles snap, the narrator awaits the gods' twilight, spiralling endlessly toward a doorway home. Norse end-times myth becomes an inward voyage, destruction reframed as homecoming.","sonnet":"A traveller moves through mythological collapse — the fall of gold, of houses, of divine chains — guided not by false lights but by something ancient and instinctual. Through cyclical ruin he remains a fixed point, descending again through circular time toward a portal only he can find.","src":"haiku"},
"enter-shikari~a-kiss-for-the-whole-world-x":{"haiku":"Joy erodes into myth; true believers and dreamers flee for their lives despite temporary transcendence, offering one final kiss as the world collapses around them.","opus":"A defiant embrace of joy and solidarity even as the world falls apart, love offered to all of humanity while running for survival. Shikari's optimism is deliberate and hard-won, treating togetherness as resistance against collapse.","sonnet":"A breathless declaration of collective love and urgency addressed to billions: the last dreamers and true believers are 'running for their lives' but choose one final act of radical tenderness — a kiss for the whole world — before whatever end is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"enter-shikari~bull":{"haiku":"A destructive partner ravages the narrator's interior landscape, their indifference weaponized while the narrator remains trapped in a cycle of impact and resistance.","opus":"Love as collateral damage, casting the partner as a bull loose in the china shop of the singer's mind. He gave everything and got wreckage in return, still half-pleading for it to have been worth it even as he calls it over.","sonnet":"Someone's careless, bulldozing presence has wrecked the speaker's mental space — \"you're a bull and my mind is a china shop\" — leaving his head full of damage while they insist it doesn't even matter.","src":"haiku"},
"enter-shikari~gandhi-mate-gandhi":{"haiku":"Systemic decay demands radical reimagining; nonviolent resistance calls for collective awakening beyond empty consumerism.","opus":"A furious anti-capitalist manifesto against consuming yourself to death to prop up a broken system, calling to wake up and rebuild. The Gandhi refrain is a check on the rage, insisting change stay nonviolent before power criminalizes it.","sonnet":"A furious political broadside against a system built on consumption, war, poverty, and division — arguing that the current economic order is inhumane and must be dismantled and rebuilt, while the speaker tries to hold back from violence and stay on the nonviolent path. The song moves between articulate critique, chaotic mob energy, and a plea for awakening over retribution.","src":"haiku"},
"enter-shikari~it-hurts":{"opus":"A tender argument that failure and pain don't diminish your worth, that falling is the price of daring to fight the inevitable. It reframes struggle as the fuel for love, learning and a future built together.","src":"opus"},
"enter-shikari~jailbreak":{"opus":"Liberation staged as escaping the prison of your own self-image and fixed beliefs. Shikari turns radical self-questioning into revolution, insisting the lock on the cell is one you can pick from inside.","src":"opus"},
"enter-shikari~no-sssweat":{"haiku":"Blind consumption and systemic control substitute for genuine existence; the speaker exhorts awakening before it's too late.","opus":"A short, cryptic jolt about a commander driving his troops toward a hopeless slaughter while concealing his own terror, insisting the day is meaningless. Under the bravado is someone who never wanted any of it and only longs to feel alive.","sonnet":"A commanding figure leads an army into certain destruction while concealing his own fear behind authority, addressing the soldiers with a mixture of bravado and private remorse for sending them to die. The song collapses into an admission that this day, whatever happens, will not be remembered.","src":"haiku"},
"enter-shikari~pls-set-me-on-fire":{"haiku":"Transcendence through annihilation—the speaker pleads for erasure, seeking to dissolve into atmosphere rather than persist in broken form.","opus":"A plea for transcendence through self-destruction, the exhausted narrator wanting to burn into ash and disappear into someone else's eyes. It renders yearning for release as a strange, tender act of surrender.","sonnet":"A plea for transformation through another person: the speaker, broken and disused as a quarry, begs to be set alight — 'I wanna be ash in the atmosphere' — so that they may transcend and become 'a flicker' in the other's eyes rather than endure continuing brokenness.","src":"haiku"},
"enter-shikari~quelle-surprise-clean":{"haiku":"Humanity has the technology and knowledge to build forward but instead chooses greed and stagnation out of adaptability and moral compromise. Systems are designed to steal dreams and means from the people.","opus":"A blunt verdict on 2011 humanity: the means and skill to progress are already here, yet greed keeps everyone dodging reality so profit gets ferried offshore. The rallying cry warns that believing in nothing leaves you vulnerable to anything. Enter Shikari, British agitators fusing hardcore with electronics, aim this at post-crash finance and complicity, all fury and slogan.","sonnet":"A political broadside aimed at a society that has the technology and talent to advance but chooses stagnation whenever profit is involved. Corporate interests override collective possibility, and the population adapts by looking away. The refrain warns that moral neutrality is surrender — those who stand for nothing will absorb whatever manipulation fills the vacuum.","src":"haiku"},
"enter-shikari~stop-the-clocks-single-edit":{"haiku":"Time becomes the enemy as endless waiting and internal cinema replace authentic living, trapping two people in parallel isolation despite physical proximity.","opus":"A plea to freeze time against a life of missed moments and mental time-travel, each anxious thought answered by a companion who feels exactly the same.","sonnet":"Two people discover they share the same dissatisfaction — a mind full of counterfeit scenes, a life of missed moments and waiting in line — and the narrator, unmoored and seeking coordinates, wants only to arrest time in the one moment that feels worth keeping.","src":"haiku"},
"enter-shikari~the-dreamer-s-hotel-bob-vylan-remix":{"opus":"A satire of online outrage culture, where nuance is a nuisance and everyone is judge and jury in a rush to condemn. The dreamer's hotel stands empty behind the noise, imagination abandoned while the mob loses patience outside.","src":"opus"},
"enter-shikari~the-great-unknown":{"haiku":"The narrator stands at an uncertain boundary asking if this marks a beginning or end, desperately calling out to an unseen audience for a sign.","opus":"Enter Shikari, whose electronic hardcore has long braided existential dread with cosmic wonder, poise a mind between beginning and ending as the room spins and ears ring. Impatient for a great leap forward, the narrator shouts into an unanswering void begging for any sign of company. Twilight and a shepherd's flashlight sweeping like brushstrokes turn panic toward fragile beauty.","sonnet":"A restless figure stares at the skyline and shouts into silence, craving contact and a sign of whether the horizon promises beginning or ending. Caught between dizzying vertigo and the painter Van Gogh's luminous sky, he waits for a leap forward that never quite arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"enter-shikari~the-void-stares-back":{"haiku":"A cosmic outsider, moving at the speed of light from another dimension, watches a civilization go cold and dark, urging an embrace of entropy and impermanence as the only honest response to a world already falling apart.","opus":"A cosmic reckoning with nihilism, addressing an unmoored figure while confronting the abyss that stares back and the illusion of control. Shikari answers dread with defiant escape, casting themselves as visitors from elsewhere refusing to be swallowed.","sonnet":"An outsider claiming extradimensional perspective — child with telescope eyes, moving at the speed of light — watches the world go dark and cold, the ark burning, the glass breaking, everything ending and falling apart, and offers not comfort but the statement that reality is much worse than hell and we were never in control.","src":"haiku"},
"enterprise-earth~the-world-without-us":{"haiku":"Human extinction becomes ecological mercy; the speaker acknowledges predatory species destruction, embracing a world liberated by human absence.","opus":"An extinction elegy welcoming the stillness after humanity's collapse, casting our shrines to permanence and pride as crumbling in the season's freeze. Hunters with no home in either afterlife, consuming and ruining everything they cherish until the planet carries on without us.","sonnet":"A vision of humanity's extinction as both deserved and beautiful — the song depicts humans as predatory creatures who defiled everything they loved, rejected by heaven and hell alike, now dissolving under winter and worms while the Earth reclaims itself.","src":"haiku"},
"enterprise-earth~where-dreams-are-broken":{"haiku":"Accumulated betrayal and isolation shatter the psyche; the narrator confronts worthlessness and seeks redemptive action despite chronic destruction.","opus":"Deathcore built from total loss and betrayal, a man with nothing left choosing to stand rather than break. The turn is refusing to let a wrecked world bury his dreams, even while admitting how badly it broke him.","sonnet":"A person who has been betrayed by everyone close to them and reduced to a place of desolation refuses to let their dreams die there — fighting back against hatred and brokenness with the insistence that it is not too late to stand.","src":"haiku"},
"entheos~absolute-zero":{"haiku":"Death consciousness pervades existence as the narrator oscillates between life and annihilation, finding strange companionship in ghosts while questioning whether consciousness ever truly ignited.","opus":"A liminal state between the living and the dead, a spirit wandering as its memory drains, defiantly insisting it's still here under mortality's gaze while crying out to its mother whether it will ever draw breath once more.","sonnet":"A figure straddling death and life — a ghost still walking, sifting memory like sand through fingers — declares that in the absence of life he was never truly alive, yet refuses to surrender, finding a thread of hope even at the coldest extreme.","src":"haiku"},
"entheos~in-purgatory":{"opus":"A near-death confrontation with wasted time, the narrator forced to reckon with years chased and squandered before the light returns. Its revelation is that life is a brief borrowed gift meant to be met with awareness, not taken for granted.","src":"opus"},
"envy~a-faint-new-world":{"haiku":"Life ends and resets cyclically; the speaker drifts through institutional numbness and repetitive suffering, trying to find footing and justice in an indifferent world.","opus":"Envy, Tokyo's screamo pioneers who fold hardcore fury into post-rock crescendo, trace a fraught reaching for connection amid decay, where scrawled words continue and every memory falters at the threshold. Facing a rain-heavy sky, the narrator piles up the shape of time, seeking a sign of life on swelling pages. Regeneration and disguise entwine as identity is discarded and painfully recovered.","sonnet":"A Japanese-language meditation on memory dissolving into regret: shapes lose form, tomorrow ends before it starts. A figure traces footprints traded for justice, repeating cycles without resolution — the soul cycling through coexistence, death, and existence, searching for a place to belong.","src":"haiku"},
"envy~whiteout":{"haiku":"Erasure and obliteration through overwhelming force, the speaker dissolved into blankness.","opus":"The Japanese screamo band builds a hazy meditation on memory, loss and the fragile wish to shield someone's future. Amid chance and things half-remembered, it lands on prayers almost shamefully earnest for a life not to come undone.","sonnet":"A fragmenting interior — a morning that won't return, a city laughing in the distance, the lock on the heart's window shut — and through the disorientation of coincidence versus necessity and idols versus selfishness, the song reaches toward the embarrassingly sincere: prayers, a double vision, the unit of two grasping light before it fades.","src":"haiku"},
"eric-prydz~generate":{"haiku":"Shared love sustains and creates everything needed for survival. The only force carrying life forward through all hardship is the love generated together.","opus":"Swedish progressive-house architect Eric Prydz builds this on a single repeated affirmation rather than any narrative: the notion that the affection people produce is what sustains and provides everything they lack. It functions as a mantra engineered to swell across a long build for the euphoric, arms-aloft moment of a club peak.","sonnet":"A single sustained affirmation — that the love two people create between them is the only thing capable of sustaining life and generating everything else needed. Nothing more is asked of the lyric; it repeats the claim with quiet certainty, as if repetition itself is the proof.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~blue-reverie":{"opus":"A yearning for retreat and stillness after decades of searching, wondering whether wandering itself is the cure for a quiet inner annihilation. The metalcore melody hangs suspended in the pull to hide away and simply wait.","src":"opus"},
"erra~breach":{"haiku":"Trust shatters into rust; repetitive cycles of disbelief and deception trap the speaker as they try to surrender and recover meaning.","opus":"Erra dissect a relationship rotting from broken trust, the singer half-drowning in it and recognizing that the whole thing was staged appearance rather than substance. Yet he refuses to let it die outright, begging to be buried only so he can claw back and reclaim the self he abandoned to it.","sonnet":"A relationship corroded by repeated emotional damage — trust fractures like metal rusting, the damage accumulates until there's nothing left to resuscitate, and the speaker recognizes that some things cannot be fixed no matter how much you fight to resurface.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~crawl-backwards-out-of-heaven":{"haiku":"Waking from illusory transcendence reveals suffering as inherent; the speaker rejects paradise to embrace authentic darkness.","opus":"A grim inversion of salvation, waking into darkness and choosing the pain that makes us human over the obsession with death. It frames existence as a crude miracle, malevolence waiting just past the edge of illusion.","sonnet":"The song frames human existence as a painful choice — we select our suffering in pursuit of becoming human — and positions the self as a \"lonely planet seen from a distant star,\" instructing the listener to abandon death as an obsession and crawl back.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~disarray":{"haiku":"Parasitic greed feeds on ignorance while predators laugh at their victims; the speaker exposes the manipulation and calls for truth over exploitation.","opus":"Erra, an American progressive metalcore band, wrap intricate atmospheric riffing around a political parable. A demagogue is cast as a ravenous predator who invades and devours a populace that mistakes exploitation for honor, a leech feeding on ignorance and corroding people's capacity to speak plainly. The chorus dares him to utter anything honest.","sonnet":"A political broadside against a demagogue who was never hidden and never had to be — the people welcomed the wolf in and called it a privilege. The speaker is irate where others are not, naming the parasite's behavior in clinical detail: fabricating needs, robbing the capacity to think, laughing in the faces of the people he feeds on.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~divisionary":{"opus":"Erra aim a broadside at screen addiction, arguing that phones have become a false deity and that people, glued to devices, have effectively rebuilt God in their own distracted image. The narrator feels himself spreading like a disease and warns that our vices are quietly dissolving any real human connection.","sonnet":"A critique of smartphone culture as a replacement religion — technology has become god, telling humanity it is secondary, while small black screens polarize and divide people into pseudo-selves, drowning everyone in a colorless void where identity wanders without anchor.","src":"sonnet"},
"erra~drift":{"haiku":"Altered consciousness releases pain and replaces it with bliss. The speaker drifts in dream-like states, addicted to the escape and unwilling to surface.","opus":"Metalcore that maps a psychedelic or narcotic escape onto being pulled under by the tide, sinking past the point of return into blissful unreality. The pleasure is the danger, drifting out of a life you'd rather not come back to.","sonnet":"A song about using an altered state — chemical or otherwise — to escape the pain of reality, drifting into a blissful dissociation that feels like floating in an ocean far from everything that hurts.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~eye-of-god":{"haiku":"The speaker drifts weightless through infinite void, severed from connection; an omniscient eye observes the hollowing and swallowing of existence.","opus":"Progressive metalcore imagining death as drift into cosmic void, the self dissolving into endless black space after a world we abused. It treats extinction as part of the process, oddly serene, watched over by an indifferent eye.","sonnet":"A meditation on cosmic dissolution and mortality — the speaker drifts weightless in an indifferent void, carrying the legacy of generations, accepting that death is built into the process and the hollow eye of a godless universe swallows everything.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~ghost-of-nothing":{"haiku":"Emotional drowning appears invisible to others; the speaker offers guidance through suffering, seeking to pull someone out of the trenches they've built.","opus":"Erra bring their melodic, technical metalcore to a tender song about hauling a loved one out of a mind bent on destroying them. Wading through their toxic despair and reaching into the depths of their sorrow, the narrator refuses to let them keep suffering, coaxing them to steady their breathing and survive the night.","sonnet":"Watching someone you love disappear into themselves — a speaker who can see the suffering that the sufferer's body does not show, and who keeps reaching into the trenches the other person's mind creates. The offer is gentle: follow me, breathe slowly, I don't want to watch you carry this forever. But the ghost of nothing in the title hangs over the whole thing as a warning about what is being lost.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~hybrid-earth":{"haiku":"Human-machine fusion corrupts natural innocence; progress becomes toxin as momentum enslaves consciousness to manufactured efficiency.","opus":"A dense metalcore meditation on humanity fused with something artificial and unnatural, progress as a venom we embrace willingly. It warns that our own restless drive to advance is smothering what made us human.","sonnet":"Humanity's fusion with technology and the momentum of progress is reframed as a contamination — children of ancient lineage have forgotten what they are, enslaved by their own drive toward the next thing, and the result is something inhuman crowning the earth.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~hyperreality":{"haiku":"Escaping noise and control through weightlessness; the speaker releases anger and separates from conscious suffering to find celestial peace.","opus":"A song about reaching weightlessness by shedding fury and suppression, drifting clear of a clamoring mind toward calm and a sense of mastery, favoring flight over strife and asking for room to draw clean air. Change is welcomed as necessity. Erra, the American progressive metalcore band, wrap this pursuit of transcendence in their trademark shimmering guitar work and soaring clean vocals.","sonnet":"Deliberate dissociation chosen as self-preservation — the speaker relinquishing rage and memory to float into a quieter state where the noise cannot reach. The flight is not avoidance but a kind of mastery: leaving before the tightrope and chains of conflict can catch you, riding on passion and retrospective clarity into something that feels like weightless, unblemished air.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~light-my-way":{"opus":"A plea for direction in a desperate moment, likening a wayward human life to a stray shot and humbly asking someone to show the path and not defer it any longer.","sonnet":"A prayer-like appeal for guidance: the narrator feels lost and spiritually depleted, compares himself to a stray bullet, and asks a higher presence — human or divine — to light the way before he goes further astray.","src":"sonnet"},
"erra~lunar-halo":{"opus":"A love song dressed in cosmic imagery, imagining two people plummeting together through open space, joined and tracing patterns in the stars. The paradox at its heart is that the closer the pair grows, the smaller everything else becomes, with intimacy sending its effects outward no matter the distance between them.","sonnet":"Two people fall together through cosmic space — an intimate, euphoric surrender to being untethered from earth alongside someone else, tracing each other like constellations while acknowledging that even if they drift apart, the connection leaves a ripple across the void.","src":"sonnet"},
"erra~monolith":{"haiku":"Erosion of faith and betrayal by those in power; memories shatter and affection becomes untranslatable. The perpetrator hoards stolen futures while innocents fight for recovery.","opus":"A reflection on faith slipping away and time running out, watching helplessly as something innocent fades and memories can't be recreated. It accuses a perpetrator of stealing what isn't theirs and laments a paralysis where fear keeps doors unopened.","sonnet":"The song mourns a relationship fractured beyond repair—memories lost, damage incalculable, affection shifting unpredictably, and meaning no longer translatable between two people. There is also an accusation of passivity and complicity directed at a perpetrator who has stolen something irreplaceable.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~pull-from-the-ghost":{"haiku":"Memory and sensation fracture as the narrator grasps at virtual echoes of connection, love existing only as phantom sensation divorced from authentic presence.","opus":"Emotional captivity and detachment where love is felt only in reverie, already a memory the instant it arrives. So the speaker fills what's missing in a real person with a phantom drawn from imagination, and vows to burn his history away.","sonnet":"Stranded between time zones of feeling, the speaker cannot find authentic emotion in the present and reaches instead into a virtual ghost of memory — love only arrives as reverie, stillborn upon delivery, and what he chased face-to-face he must now burn.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~shadow-autonomous":{"haiku":"Someone navigates intrusive thoughts and internal mirrors, finding relief through pain as proof they can still feel.","opus":"A study of deadened endurance and a splintered sense of self, the narrator seizing up when an isolated inner voice murmurs, torn between staying put and starting over as he flickers between presence and absence, forever rebuilding a stand-in refuge. Tasting blood becomes odd comfort, evidence he retains any sensation. Erra render this clash with invading thoughts and a mirrored self in layered, atmospheric progressive metalcore.","sonnet":"Living with an internal presence that is equal parts companion and antagonist — a shadow self kept close, unified in the mirror, neither fully friend nor enemy. The speaker spits out blood and admits the pain still brings relief because it confirms feeling is intact; the spaces that once offered peace now tear him apart, but a glimpse of light keeps breaking through the waking dream anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~signal-fire":{"haiku":"Someone questions their transformation and worth in a relationship; they wonder if their presence matters or if they're disappearing into uncertainty.","opus":"Transformation and self-doubt seethe as a guttering flame and faltering breath frame a haunting presence calling from the water's edge. Tangled thoughts drag the narrator down while he holds out for a gentler outcome. Erra, an American progressive metalcore band, give this identity crisis their trademark blend of crushing rhythm and soaring melody, treating change as both menace and fragile hope.","sonnet":"A man in emotional turbulence questions whether he is the cause of his own unraveling or a victim of forces beyond him, while clinging to a bond he believes was built to endure. A signal fire at the edge of the sea — fragile, flickering — anchors the fear of losing the person he loves to some unseen edge.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~skyline":{"haiku":"Fragmented pieces of a broken person reflect their separation from loved ones, unable to reform into their original whole.","opus":"Erra's melodic progressive metalcore frames urban weariness and personal collapse, opening on a city endlessly rebuilding itself and numbing the minds it houses. It shifts to a fracturing relationship and a flawed man who failed to voice his devotion while his partner lay beside him, feeling alone. Fractured fragments that never realign capture both the alienation and the regret.","sonnet":"A long-distance relationship told through city imagery — a person who rebuilds himself repeatedly while the other sleeps alone miles away, wondering if what he felt was ever fully communicated. The shattered puzzle of the self longs to be whole but knows the pieces never fit quite the same way twice, and the skyline's light guides wandering minds back toward a home they may no longer be able to find.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~snowblood":{"opus":"A harrowing portrait of losing oneself in a self-made mental maze, going snowblind while circling in despair, hearing the dead scream, and giving up to a waking nightmare where the heroes lose.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a waking nightmare of mental collapse, circling the same destructive patterns in their mind like footprints in snow, unable to escape the labyrinth of depression and despair — the villains win, the heroes die, and sanity is a pulled pin.","src":"sonnet"},
"erra~ultimata":{"haiku":"Isolation, broken memory, and fear trap the narrator in a prison of their own doubts; they struggle to distinguish home from exile and find peace in disconnection.","opus":"Erra depict a relationship dissolving into estrangement, using storm and drowning imagery to mirror emotional collapse. The narrator feels like an intruder in his own house, waking to an empty bed, haunted by grievances he can't release yet clinging to a partner he can feel receding from him.","sonnet":"About falling apart after a relationship ends — the love spent left only emptiness, and the narrator is becoming a stranger in their own home, unable to recognize themselves now that the other person is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"erra~vanish-canvas":{"opus":"A meditation on waking into the present moment, urging surrender to the void of the unknown so that a heartbreaking realization can break you open and lift you out of a coma-like existence.","sonnet":"An invitation to surrender to the present moment and let go of compulsive anxiety: following the living imprint of someone who moves with purpose, the narrator urges trusting the drift rather than fighting it, letting the moment break the heart open toward something greater.","src":"sonnet"},
"erra~you-think-i-ain-t-worth-a-dollar-but-i-feel-like-a-millionaire":{"haiku":"Self-inflation and appetite dominate; the speaker demands repetition and indulgence despite lowered status or perception.","opus":"Lyrics that spill out as surreal, almost meaningless collage, stitching a swaggering conqueror, a hungry demand for more, and schlocky horror-film gore into pure bravado. Erra, technical progressive metalcore players, lift the title from an internet meme and lean into texture and attitude, letting the sound carry more weight than literal meaning.","sonnet":"A swagger-heavy collage of defiance, appetite, and kinetic energy — the speaker positions himself as a massive, unstoppable force despite being underestimated. Imagery of bullfighting, B-movies, and liquor-store fortification accumulates into a portrait of someone fueled by excess, contempt for doubt, and sheer momentum.","src":"haiku"},
"error37~chinese-warlord":{"opus":"A desperate, spiraling song about a consuming relationship that's killing him, sleeplessness and voices in his head, and the sense of an inevitable, self-destructive ending he begs to be taken along with.","sonnet":"A sleepless, obsessive longing for someone the narrator is drifting apart from: he writes his thoughts down just to quiet his own mind, admits they are no longer the same, and eventually reaches a terminal point — empty and cold without this person, begging not to be left.","src":"sonnet"},
"erykah-badu~a-d-2000":{"haiku":"Legacy and social change stall despite individual effort; the narrator questions whether their work will endure or be distorted, watching the world repeat its failures.","opus":"Written after the police killing of Amadou Diallo, Badu refusing the empty honor of buildings named after the dead. Her weary point is that the world hasn't changed since she woke up conscious, and won't just because they memorialize you.","sonnet":"The speaker reflects on invisibility and historical erasure — no buildings will bear her name, it will be misspelled or ignored — set against the bleak observation that the world has changed very little since she became aware of it.","src":"haiku"},
"escape-the-box~smoke":{"haiku":"Dissolution and escape merge as the speaker dissipates into vapor.","opus":"Weathering recurring mental storms with a wary calm, confessing there are things too buried to say. The hope is modest: that the pressure in his head might blow over before it becomes a hurricane.","sonnet":"Something unconfessable frays the narrator at the edges, and they go up in smoke again — a pattern of dissociation, the grey-in-black-and-white vantage above angels crying, survivable but unresolved. The optimism is real but provisional: the storm has been lived through, the hurricane might still come.","src":"haiku"},
"eschalon~helios":{"haiku":"Solar transcendence and self-immolation blur as the speaker craves purification through destruction, seeking illumination while collapsing under the weight of their own darkness.","opus":"A desperate prayer to sun gods for light to hold back the mind's black holes, the speaker buckling under everything he carries and wondering whether his own implosion even counts if no one is watching, courting the fire that consumes him.","sonnet":"A person collapsing under the weight of darkness who pleads with sun gods for light and fire: wanting to torch the pain but imploding instead, the speaker confesses that darkness has become their disease and endorser — and asks whether the implosion will even be noticed before it is too late.","src":"haiku"},
"eskimo-callboy~we-got-the-moves":{"opus":"The band's breakout party anthem, all beach, booze and refusing to let the night end. It runs entirely on the joyous, knowingly shallow faith that summer's memories, and youth itself, will never fade.","src":"opus"},
"esoterica~birds-in-a-hurricane":{"opus":"A refusal to lie down amid chaos, insisting we're all the same fragile creatures caught in the same storm. Its defiance is communal and unglamorous, choosing to raise your head and smile at the edge rather than be carried out.","src":"opus"},
"esoterica~don-t-rely-on-anyone":{"haiku":"Rejects dependency and trust in others, fighting systemic oppression through self-reliance. Believes change comes only through personal agency and resistance.","opus":"A defiant song about self-reliance in the face of doubters, the singer clinging to belief in himself as anger runs and things change too swiftly, sensing something coming and rejecting the empty offers of people who never actually helped. The chant-like refrain insists on trusting no one and fighting the machine. The register is combative and anthemic, built for shouting the title back.","sonnet":"Self-reliance hardened into doctrine by repeated let-downs — a speaker who sensed change coming and looked to others for support, found them useless or actively hostile, and arrived at the conclusion that the only reliable engine is the one inside. The machine being fought is unnamed but institutional, and the only viable stance is forward motion without waiting for help that will not come.","src":"haiku"},
"esoterica~in-dreams":{"haiku":"A person promises to be a constant presence and light for someone in darkness. They commit to warmth and protection even as the world treats the other like a ghost.","opus":"A vow to be the constant light and refuge for someone everyone else overlooks, when their sense of direction won't settle and foes press in on every side. It is devotion cast as a pledge to keep a loved one safe through their bleakest stretch.","sonnet":"A pledge to be someone's anchor when the world treats them as invisible and every direction feels hostile — promising to be the warmth and steadiness they can't find anywhere else. The lighthouse burning down at the start frames their vulnerability before the protection is offered.","src":"haiku"},
"esoterica~silence":{"haiku":"The narrator seeks release and peace amid rage, finding freedom and belief in silence and the witness of something transcendent.","opus":"A narrator pleads to be freed and seen, dissolving into a pale surge where quiet becomes an odd kind of liberty and heaven's marvel waits once inner fury cools. A reworking of the Sarah McLachlan song, this version by Esoterica holds yearning and violence together, the speaker sensing a still presence within the turmoil and finding faith and freedom in the drowning hush.","sonnet":"Submersion in silence as liberation rather than loss. In the white wave the narrator finds not drowning but freedom — the rage subsides, the longing is held, and the presence witnessed in violence becomes something quietly believed in rather than condemned.","src":"haiku"},
"esprit-d-air~a-303y":{"haiku":"A intimate meditation on pain and distance—together yet unable to touch, carrying wounds that never fade.","opus":"Esprit D'Air, a London-based project rooted in Japanese metal and visual-kei, specializes in the beauty of mutually inflicted pain. Two people walk together carrying wounds the relationship itself made — one holding back tears under lies, the other refusing to be touched — while the narrator stays transfixed by eyes that light up and vanish inside his chest. The masochistic intimacy is the point: people who hurt each other and cannot stop returning.","sonnet":"Two people walking together while both carrying wounds the relationship itself created — one trying not to cry under lies, the other trying not to be touched — and beneath the push-and-pull, the narrator is transfixed by the other person's eyes, which light up and vanish inside his chest. The emotional core is the masochistic intimacy of people who hurt each other and cannot stop returning.","src":"haiku"},
"esprit-d-air~leviathan":{"haiku":"Even after love fades and the world changes into ruins, light persists and rebirth comes again despite endless cycles of sorrow.","opus":"A Japanese song of grief and fragile hope amid a world turned monochrome, prayers going unheard as conflict is reborn, yet life sprouts from rubble in a deserted city and the singer still waits for a dawn that is promised to come. The recurring image of an unchanged, unfading sorrow gives way to rebirth in a new place. Esprit D'Air are a London-based Japanese metal act blending electronic textures with melodic heaviness.","sonnet":"Perseverance through a world turned monochrome by loss and conflict — a speaker watching prayers go unanswered and the same cycles of violence repeat, clinging to a voice heard long ago and to the possibility that even in ruins something new takes root. The Leviathan of the title looms over a world that keeps changing past recognition, but the promise held onto is that light exists and dawn is still worth waiting for.","src":"haiku"},
"esprit-d-air~tsunami":{"haiku":"Drowning in contradictions; wanting to destroy the broken world or transform into light within its darkness.","opus":"Esprit D'Air, the London-based project of Japanese vocalist Kai, channels visual-kei intensity into English-fluent metal, and here that duality mirrors the lyric's own split. Swallowed by the world's contradictions, the narrator vows to tear it down with bare hands, then hesitates, wondering whether becoming a small light might serve better than destruction. The pivot from annihilation to illumination is the whole drama.","sonnet":"A person swallowed by the contradictions of the world swears to destroy that world with their own hands — but then pauses to wonder if, instead of destruction, they could become a small light illuminating the darkness that threatens to swallow someone they care about.","src":"haiku"},
"essenger~as-above-so-below":{"haiku":"Dystopian collapse and existential dread intensify as technological systems and environmental catastrophe consume humanity while we hide in false sanctuaries.","opus":"A cry from inside a dying, machine-ruled world, aimed at those who play God while the sanctuary burns and the survivors only exist rather than live. The plea to catch the last starlight before it goes out turns ecological and technological dread into a last grasp at meaning.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic song about environmental or civilisational collapse caused by the powerful playing God — those in charge lock themselves in paradise while the world burns outside; the narrator demands to be woken up and wonders if it's already too late to see the stars before they're gone.","src":"haiku"},
"essenger~divine-virus":{"opus":"Voiced by a wrathful, god-like controlling entity condemning a disloyal follower: their fate was predetermined, their betrayal unforgiven, and there is no escape from this prison; a portrait of absolute domination masquerading as divinity.","sonnet":"A song narrated by a controlling force — god, cult leader, or AI — that tells the listener their disloyalty will not be forgiven and there is no escape from its dominion. The brevity is the point: the message is pure domination with no negotiation.","src":"sonnet"},
"essenger~lost-boys":{"haiku":"Predatory justice pursues betrayal; the hunted become eternal victims of their own cruelty, burning in hell while immortal.","opus":"Synthwave built as a revenge ballad, addressing a killer or abuser who escapes the law but not judgment. The stance is a wronged narrator promising the guilty will one day answer, wrapped in an eternal-boyhood myth of never growing up or crying.","sonnet":"A hunter's ultimatum addressed to someone whose time is running out: the speaker instructs them to save themselves, leave, and disappear while they still can, because fate is unkind to those like them — 'lost boys don't cry for nothing' frames both the hunted and the hunters as marked.","src":"haiku"},
"essenger~sanctum-eternal":{"haiku":"Both perpetrator and victim share cursed fates in system-fed madness; Hell's ice-cold darkness drives all toward insanity.","opus":"A cyberpunk dread piece about transhumanist immortality gone wrong: uploaded consciousness that traded death for an eternity of digital solitude. Essenger's synthwave gloss makes the sell look sleek, then reveals the promised heaven as an isolated hell of terabyte clones.","sonnet":"Two people on opposite sides of a digital afterlife — one slipping suicide notes through a crack in the system, one a parasite unaware they're the victim — are both damned: they signed a blood pact, sidestepped death to become terabyte clones, and discovered that the heaven they were promised was just a lonely cell with no exit.","src":"haiku"},
"esterly~built-for-pain-instrumental":{"opus":"A self-aware embrace of self-destruction as identity: screwed up, broken, and drawn to destruction, the narrator confesses that the dirty pleasure of pain runs in his blood and concludes he was simply built for it.","sonnet":"A self-diagnosis of being constitutionally wired for destruction and intensity — the narrator can't stop craving conflict, hurt, and sensation, and concludes they may simply be built for pain, finding more pleasure in the cycle of damage than in any alternative.","src":"sonnet"},
"etched-in-embers~manic":{"opus":"A first-person account of a manic spiral, forcing thoughts that won't cohere until control slips entirely. The crowd invited to watch the drowning captures how mania can feel like performance and self-destruction at once, with the mind narrating its own breaking.","src":"opus"},
"euringer~if-it-ain-t-you-today-it-will-be-you-tomorrow":{"haiku":"Queer resistance defies normative closets through deliberate transgression and refusal, the anthem demanding continued struggle against systems designed to contain and eliminate.","opus":"A phantasmagoric warning about oppression and forced conformity, how a flick of the wrist can turn anyone into a target and shove them back into hiding. Its insistence is that the ones resisting must not give up, because tomorrow the persecution comes for you.","sonnet":"Power flips identities with a flick of a wrist or the pull of a trigger, reducing anyone to a target at any moment; beneath the surrealist imagery a voice urges those fighting the good fight not to give up, because the system's violence is indiscriminate.","src":"haiku"},
"europe~the-final-countdown":{"haiku":"Humanity embarks on space exploration to Venus, leaving Earth behind with uncertainty about return. The journey represents a farewell to the familiar world and anticipation of discovering new worlds.","opus":"A grand farewell as humanity departs Earth for Venus, uncertain whether they'll ever return or whether things will be the same; beneath the bombast is bittersweet finality, a collective leaving-behind of home with no one to blame and everything left to be found among the stars.","sonnet":"A space-travel departure song framed as a grand farewell, where a crew leaves Earth for Venus knowing they may never return, finding dignity and solidarity in the journey itself even as they acknowledge the uncertainty of what they'll find and the things — and people — left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"eurotripp~where-s-your-head-at":{"haiku":"A speaker lost in incomprehensible maze confronts psychological displacement. The world caves in when walls close; survival means holding ground, refusing to be discarded, feeding what depends on your presence.","opus":"A dance rework of the Basement Jaxx hook, keeping the taunt aimed at someone losing their grip. Under the club energy runs a warning about isolation and squandering goodwill, the beat carrying an almost menacing insistence that the world will move on regardless.","sonnet":"An urgent address to someone mentally unraveling, warning them not to let isolation collapse inward — \"don't let the walls cave in on you\" — while reminding them that what they give determines what they get, and that the world will move on without them if they stay lost in their incomprehensible maze.","src":"haiku"},
"evalyn~a-pill-to-crush":{"haiku":"Chemical dependency and spiritual hunger intertwine; the speaker medicates existential void while chasing transcendence through pharmaceutical escape.","opus":"Craving dressed as love, a cycle of taking and wanting and calling it romance while no exorcist or doctor can cure the need. It is about addiction to a person or a high that you keep grinding down and swallowing anyway.","sonnet":"A person who has consumed everything offered to them — faith, love, substances — finds no satiation and no cure, placing their remaining trust in a relationship that functions like an addiction, longing to return to a younger, clearer self.","src":"haiku"},
"evanescence~bring-me-to-life":{"haiku":"Someone has been spiritually dead and numb until another awakens them, only that person's presence can restore their soul and revive their ability to live.","opus":"The goth-rock band's breakout hit, Amy Lee voicing a soul gone cold and empty, waiting for someone to rouse it back to life. Having lived hollow and unreal, she pleads to be awakened and rescued from the void she has become, casting one saving presence as the only thing keeping her from spiritual death.","sonnet":"A person who has drifted into emotional numbness and spiritual dormancy discovers through another's presence that something essential in her had gone cold. The plea is to be reawakened — not merely comforted but restored to full aliveness — because without that contact she had already become a kind of walking absence.","src":"haiku"},
"evanescence~call-me-when-you-re-sober":{"haiku":"A lover only contacts the narrator while intoxicated and disappears when sober, cycling through empty promises and avoidance.","opus":"A scathing breakup anthem aimed at a partner who only wants love once it is over and never calls sober, the singer refusing to keep playing the game or let him play the victim, sick of the lie and finally making up his mind for him. Evanescence, the American gothic-rock band led by Amy Lee, built this on their signature blend of orchestral drama and pop-metal bite, a pointed departure from their earlier brooding balladry.","sonnet":"An ultimatum delivered with exhausted clarity — stop coming back only when impaired, stop playing the victim of a situation you made, make a decision or leave. The speaker has decided for both of them, arrived at the recognition that what looked like paradise was never actually hers to lose, and is done waiting for the other person to be honest enough to show up straight.","src":"haiku"},
"evanescence~going-under":{"haiku":"Someone is drowning emotionally in an unhealthy relationship, having sacrificed everything while being confused and manipulated, and now fights to break free.","opus":"A toxic relationship staged as literal drowning: after weeping and hurting for someone who refuses to listen, the narrator turns down the outstretched hand and resolves to rescue herself. Beneath it is the erosion of a stable self, her abuser muddying what's true and false until she can no longer rely on her own mind, and breaking free means escaping that manufactured confusion.","sonnet":"A relationship in which one person has given everything — tears, sacrifice, self — and received nothing but confusion and emotional manipulation in return; the narrator is finally resolving to stop drowning in the other person and break free.","src":"haiku"},
"evanescence~my-heart-is-broken":{"haiku":"Heartbreak fragments the speaker; they wander alone, seeking escape through sleep while refusing to heal.","opus":"Evanescence's Amy Lee, whose gothic torch songs turned grief into arena catharsis, voices a soul wandering endlessly, torn from a lost love and unable to heal. Caught between an unbearable present and an impossible return, the narrator pleads with a dark angel of sleep for deliverance from sorrow's hold. Surrender to release becomes the only opening toward light.","sonnet":"Grief after separation becomes endless wandering — unable to move forward, unable to return. A figure torn from someone beloved is chained to sorrow, pleading for a dark angel to grant release, hovering in the liminal space between survival and surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"evanescence~my-immortal-band-version":{"haiku":"Haunting memory and unresolved grief make presence impossible to release as the dead's memory consumes the living.","opus":"Grief that won't loosen its grip, haunted by a presence that outlasts the loss and wounds no stretch of years can heal. Lee sings of having driven off someone's tears and fears only to find herself abandoned the whole while, still possessed by every part of them.","sonnet":"A speaker haunted by the ghost of a lost person — still present in the house, chasing sanity away, turning pleasant dreams into a face that hovers — who held this person through years of tears and screams and now finds they cannot be alone even in aloneness.","src":"haiku"},
"evanescence~my-last-breath":{"haiku":"Death becomes final refuge from isolation as the speaker surrenders consciousness while beloved grieves an absence that no prayer can reverse.","opus":"Amy Lee singing from the edge of death, comforting the person left behind more than herself, telling them not to be afraid. A gothic goodbye about clinging to love as the light goes out.","sonnet":"A person dying speaks to the one they love — peaceful, not afraid — asking to be held one last time while confessing that their final thoughts are entirely of them. After death, they invite the living to look for them in white forests and hollow trees, and call back across the boundary as the other fades.","src":"haiku"},
"evanescence~never-go-back":{"haiku":"Separation erases home; the speaker drowns without connection, caught between worlds and unable to return.","opus":"Grief as a drowned world, the only life you knew sleeping beneath the waves after loss tears everything apart. It is the refusal to give up on someone gone, and the crushing knowledge you can never return home.","sonnet":"A person lost after a catastrophic separation — possibly a death, possibly an irreversible departure — cannot stop searching across an ocean of grief for the face burned into memory, unable to return to any version of home that existed before the loss. The plea is equally to the lost person and to the self to find a way back to shore.","src":"haiku"},
"evanescence~yeah-right":{"haiku":"A person rejects hollow promises of success and questions the cost of abandoning one's soul for fame and wealth.","opus":"Amy Lee's sardonic jab at the music industry and its promise of payoff for trading away your integrity, delivered with bitter, worn-out irony. A revived monarch beyond caring, she reckons her only error was offering more than anyone was owed.","sonnet":"A bitter, sardonic rebuke of the false promises of the music industry and those who exploited the narrator's generosity — the payoff never comes, the soul-selling wasn't worth it, and she's done giving more.","src":"haiku","fable":"Amy Lee builds the track on a sarcasm so total that the title lands as a verbal shrug, and the read has to weigh that scorn against the industry pitch it mocks. Each shiny vow of total abundance, of a payday that supposedly arrives at last, gets voiced only so that she can turn around and fling it back as a con. She heaps up images that eat themselves — a mourner balanced atop the lowest depths, a monarch hauled back from death still every bit as wrecked — so the swaggering celebrity pose exists purely to expose its own hollowness. The hurt finally breaks through in the bridge, where her guard slips away and she names her one failing as the reflex to keep loving far too generously. That confession recasts the whole cutting act as a shield over a genuine wound: the venom is defense, and whatever she got for trading away her soul never covered the cost.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"venom as a shield over the wound of loving too generously","built":"images that eat themselves — a mourner atop the lowest depths — stage the celebrity pose only to collapse it"},
"everclear~santa-monica":{"haiku":"Escape westward becomes both fantasy and imperative as the speaker rejects suburban domesticity and emotional entanglement for solitary reinvention.","opus":"Art Alexakis writing escape from a dead relationship and a wrecked past as a westward run to the coast, the nihilist chorus about watching the world die less despair than the freedom of finally not caring what burns behind you.","sonnet":"A person leaving a damaging relationship and the city that held it, fantasising about the Pacific coast as a place to disappear into and start over. The escape is both geographic and emotional — swimming out past the breakers and watching the old world dissolve.","src":"haiku"},
"everfall~crashing-down":{"opus":"Metalcore about a mind at war with itself, cycling through past regrets, intrusive suicidal voices, and the paradox of feeling dead while still breathing. The core is the exhaustion of self-sabotage and the plea to be heard before collapse.","src":"opus"},
"everfall~superhero":{"haiku":"Selfless service backfires when others demand more than one can give, unable to help others while internally broken.","opus":"Everfall fold a bitter contradiction into anthemic metalcore. A weary narrator who has poured himself out for a cause finally confronts the futility of trying to rescue everyone while he's falling apart himself. The song's grim punchline is that the self-appointed savior survives, but his own cure sits out of reach, useless, and he can't move.","sonnet":"The cost of selflessness without internal foundation — someone who fought for the weak finds they cannot help anyone while nailed to the ground themselves. The superhero archetype is examined honestly: survival requires it, but the remedy for the one dispensing rescue sits untouched on a shelf.","src":"haiku"},
"evergrey~blindfolded":{"haiku":"Blindness to truth perpetuates cycles of failure and soullessness; awakening requires faith that liberation from delusion can transform suffering into something bearable.","opus":"The Swedish prog-metal veterans on faith and disillusionment: needing something to believe in while suspecting the whole life you've woven is a deceit. The turn is hopeful, that freeing yourself from those chains could remake existence into something bearable if you'd only open your eyes.","sonnet":"A call to open your eyes while you still can: the narrator has been weaving a deceptive life and admits they are lost in fragile moments, running blindfolded through falsehood. The miracle, they insist, is freeing yourself from chains before failure becomes permanent — but the chorus returns to the same image of darkness and blindness, suggesting the message is as much warning as hope.","src":"haiku"},
"evergrey~call-out-the-dark":{"opus":"A song about finding solitude preferable to a life built on lies, trusting only one's own interior. Evergrey frame darkness not as despair but as honest refuge, a shared summoning between two people who recognize the same coldness and choose it together.","src":"opus"},
"evergrey~ominous":{"opus":"Perseverance through burnout and abandonment: the fire is spent yet the narrator still burns, carrying on alone while the heat of isolation feels threatening. Evergrey's brooding melancholy holds the line between exhaustion and the stubborn need to reach a new dawn.","src":"opus"},
"evergrey~save-us":{"haiku":"People lose control, chase false stories, and forge identities in darkness while chasing unattainable ideals; they ask for salvation from their self-made trap.","opus":"Evergrey, the Swedish progressive-metal outfit known for Tom Englund's brooding melancholy, portray a couple undone by numbness, bleeding fear after they stopped letting themselves feel. Having fallen for a story that was never about them, they drown in trenches dug over years. The pleading refrain insists that mistaking urgency for endless time is the error, and that dreaming onward is the rescue.","sonnet":"Humanity has lost itself in narratives that were never really about people — digging trenches over generations, hollowing out identity chasing fabricated ideals. The plea is collective and desperate: to feel again, to dream again, before the time they wrongly assumed was limitless finally runs out.","src":"haiku"},
"evergrey~where-august-mourns":{"haiku":"Winter's descent brings isolation and abandonment, dreams shattered into loneliness as life itself appears designed to desert and forsake.","opus":"A winter-of-the-soul meditation on depression that arrives out of nowhere and the sense that existence meant to abandon you from the very start. Pursuing ambition has only deepened the isolation; the plea is simply to feel something living again.","sonnet":"An isolation that came from nowhere, described as an Arctic winter arriving without warning, has left the speaker alone after years of chasing dreams; they call for life to touch them again, for change and reasons, moving from being deserted since day one toward the grief-named season where August mourns.","src":"haiku"},
"everlast~white-trash-beautiful":{"haiku":"Working-class devotion endures distance and hardship; love persists through truck stops and midnight highways.","opus":"A weathered love song to a working-class woman waiting on a trucker husband who drinks himself numb, both of them dreaming past their means. Everlast finds real tenderness in a hard, tired life neither one escapes.","sonnet":"A portrait of a working-class couple — a waitress waiting at home and a long-haul trucker on the road — bound together by love and economic hardship, the driver narrating a devotion that outlasts exhaustion and distance.","src":"haiku"},
"everrest~tread-carefully":{"opus":"About the paranoia of being watched and judged, where every word becomes ammunition others will never forget. The metalcore urgency mirrors the exhausting self-censorship of walking on eggshells among people who want to see you fail.","src":"opus"},
"everture~the-river-flows":{"haiku":"The song depicts emotional paralysis in the face of time's flow. The past cannot change; the present drowns the narrator in waves of consequence and self-ignorance.","opus":"A melodic-metal meditation on time as an unstoppable current: the past is sealed, and you either keep pace or get pulled under. Terrified of losing himself, the singer watches everything change except his own stagnation, haunted by memory and begging to be pulled out and carried forward.","sonnet":"An appeal not to be left behind by time — a speaker who keeps catching himself repeating old patterns and getting dragged under by a current he cannot stop, asking to be raised up and moved forward before he drowns in the weight of his own stasis. The river is indifferent; the only response to it is to keep pace or be pulled under, and he is barely keeping pace.","src":"haiku"},
"every-hour-kills~veiled-aurora":{"haiku":"The speaker imagines transformation and escape from a controlled existence, offering temporary respite before carbonization and decay.","opus":"The Canadian prog-metal project Every Hour Kills sketches a mind on the edge of self-destruction, torn between burning everything down and retreating into numb isolation while an inner voice loops back to zero. The imagery slides into a dissolving, almost transhuman vision of leaving the body and the self behind.","sonnet":"A fractured mind burns its world down and retreats into neon-lit dissociation, navigating between destruction and escape. Bodies dissolve into mind-states, cells unravel, and a carbonised life falls through altered consciousness — the aurora above veiled by the fire below.","src":"haiku"},
"everyone-dies-in-utah~whispers-of-the-paradox":{"opus":"Metalcore about the paralysis of a mind turned against itself, held motionless as everything drifts toward collapse. The paradox is that the one dependable fact left is that nothing is certain, the self dismantled from within, bit by bit.","src":"opus"},
"everyone-loves-a-villain~let-it-die":{"haiku":"Trapped by self-judgment and the world's cruelty, a person finally opens their eyes and releases the venom and memory that binds them.","opus":"Tormented by cruel voices and a world eager to tear a person down, the plea is to open one's eyes, leave the pain behind, and let the venomous memory finally die. Self-blame and constant affliction give way to release. Everyone Loves a Villain frame this metalcore catharsis as survival, insisting the darkness one cannot hide must be confronted before it consumes.","sonnet":"A person crushed by internal voices, old wounds, and poisonous memories is urged to release the grip those forces hold. The darkness has become identity, but the song insists it does not have to be permanent — opening the eyes, leaving the past behind, and letting the toxin finally die out is presented as the only livable path forward.","src":"haiku"},
"evil-nine~we-have-the-energy":{"haiku":"A manifesto rejecting empty rhetoric and blindness, calling to collective action with raw power rather than slogans—without love, motivated by force alone.","opus":"A vocal sample rejects slogans and excuses, declaring that the power, resources, and energy already exist, so it is time to get together and wreck something, punctuated by a haunting refrain about being without love. Evil Nine, a British breakbeat duo, build this into a menacing dancefloor chant, turning agitprop bravado into propulsive, riot-ready electronic momentum.","sonnet":"A rallying cry stripped to its essentials — the collective has the power, resources, and energy to act, but squanders it in complicity and self-deception. The repeated invocation of love as the missing element sits against aggressive calls to dismantle the status quo. Short and blunt, it treats collective potential as a moral emergency.","src":"haiku"},
"eville~leech":{"haiku":"An internal parasite feeds on the speaker from within; it anchors in veins and thrives on blood and pain.","opus":"Depression or addiction personified as a parasite in the veins that feeds on your blood and won't stop until it hits bone. The unbearable part is that no one understands and the thing lives inside you, patient and quiet.","sonnet":"About something internal and relentless — an intrusive thought, an addiction, a mental illness — that has taken up residence in the speaker's body and will not stop until it bleeds them dry; described as living in the veins, biting the tongue, feeding on blood, the thing is patient and methodical and the speaker cannot make anyone else understand it.","src":"haiku"},
"ewa-farna~ewakuacja":{"haiku":"Someone announces their personal evacuation from false appearances, burning bridges and rejecting hollow kindness to forge their own burning path forward.","opus":"A woman refuses smooth lies and comforting words, insisting dreams justify pushing against everything to live each day fully. When alarms ring and crowds rush, she runs the other way, declaring her own fire and an EWAcuation that empties her life of a hundred fair-weather friends. Ewa Farna, a Polish-Czech pop star, turns her own name into a defiant anthem of burning autonomy.","sonnet":"A woman declares independence from a relationship and a life that no longer fit, framing her departure as a self-initiated evacuation. The Polish lyrics describe a fire she owns — her own burning, her own start. False faces, smooth words, and borrowed destinations are rejected in favor of running toward her own dreams, even with fear in her eyes.","src":"haiku"},
"excision~your-fault":{"opus":"A breakup laid entirely at the departing partner's feet: they promised forever, chose someone else, and ended it by text after wasting years. The stance is wounded accusation refusing self-blame, wrapped in bass-music melodrama.","src":"opus"},
"execution-day~flourish":{"haiku":"Innocence deteriorates under pressure yet refuses complete surrender; the speaker claims agency and purpose despite the crushing weight, defining themselves against collapse and dissolution.","opus":"A struggle to hold a life together as reality slips, love turns to dust and broken trust crushes the speaker under everything he carries. Nostalgia for a carefree childhood, when everyone still thrived before the color drained away, sharpens the fight to reclaim his sanity and prove his life is worth it.","sonnet":"A person watching their innocence and trust erode under the weight of a collapsing reality remembers when they used to flourish as a child and fights back against the grey — \"I fall sometimes but I stagger to my feet, my life has purpose and I deserve it.\"","src":"haiku"},
"execution-day~suffocate":{"opus":"A metalcore reckoning with guilt and self-loathing, the narrator becoming the very thing he hates while intrusive voices push him toward disappearing. The suffocation is being trapped inside his own head with the harm he's done to others.","src":"opus"},
"exhorder~the-man-that-never-was":{"opus":"The New Orleans groove-metal pioneers on a toxic, degrading relationship that reduces the narrator to a used tool who keeps coming back. The wound is invisibility, being made to feel like a man who never existed, loved by no one.","src":"opus"},
"exist-immortal~glow":{"opus":"An interior wrestling match with fear, both of soaring and of ending, in which the narrator hides away rather than push through to a breakthrough. As his inner light dims he feels hollowed out and searches for clarity, longing to see past the illusions he suspects are running his life.","sonnet":"Someone hiding in solitude from the breakthrough they need — afraid of both flying and dying — searches for where hope goes and what remains when the glow fades, asking to be let further than they can go alone toward something that might be the end or a new beginning.","src":"sonnet"},
"exist-immortal~higher-ground":{"haiku":"Systemic corruption breeds rage and resistance; the speaker demands transcendence through defiance, refusing to accept the world's predetermined decay.","opus":"A refusal to be dragged down by negativity, insisting nothing gets better unless you try and choosing the harder climb, rising to speak for the outsiders and stand in the fire of a broken world.","sonnet":"A rallying cry against apathy, prejudice, and a world that is 'fucked no matter what you do,' insisting that the only response is to take it higher — 'be a voice for outsiders' — and to do the unglamorous work of building something real off one's own back.","src":"haiku"},
"exist-immortal~perspective":{"haiku":"Humans share common brokenness and pain beneath surface differences; wisdom requires seeing through others' eyes before reaching old age. Apathy must give way to shared realization.","opus":"A plea to burn off apathy and truly see through another's eyes before it's too late, insisting we're all broken the same. The core is empathy as survival, waking to shared pain rather than sitting numb while everything falls.","sonnet":"A person oscillates between apathy and woken urgency — told by a friend to see through others' eyes, confronting the darkness in their own soul — arriving at the question of whether any of it has meaning before it's too late to change.","src":"haiku"},
"exist-immortal~satellites":{"haiku":"Someone drifts through isolation and internal conflict, searching for external guidance while battling intrusive thoughts and a desire to remain detached.","opus":"The British progressive metal band explores the terror of exposure and the peril of retreating into oneself, urging the bravery to speak while resenting the surveillance closing in on a guarded inner world. Drifting into isolation, the narrator strains to protect himself and scans the heavens for some sign of rescue. Atmospheric, groove-driven textures carry the unease.","sonnet":"The tension between needing connection and resenting surveillance — a person who drifted beyond the familiar world and found isolation dangerous, but who also resists the constant watching eyes that come with being part of something. Satellites become the image for both what he lacks and what he mistrusts: signals from another world that might anchor him if he could only let them.","src":"haiku"},
"exist-trace~resonance":{"haiku":"Freedom paradoxically requires constraint and opposition, the seeking self trapped within paradox where liberation demands the acceptance of necessary limitation.","opus":"A meditation on the paradox of freedom, sweet and illusory yet impossible without constraint, arguing that within a closed and imperfect world there is a sliver of possibility to be found even inside the iron cage.","sonnet":"Freedom is revealed as inseparable from constraint — a dual-faced prize that laughs with a beautiful, ugly trap — and the speaker, born inside iron bars, finds a small possibility of peace precisely within captivity rather than against it.","src":"haiku"},
"exit-dream~spiralling":{"haiku":"Spiraling toward dissolution, haunted by visions and unable to distinguish death from life or meaning from void.","opus":"Plummeting toward the sun as obliteration, dreams offering no gentle end, the narrator wondering whether all the dying and renewal will ever cohere and whether the single beloved voice will be present when the end comes.","sonnet":"About a mind dissolving — the narrator woke in flames again, can't hold onto a moment before slipping away, and wonders whether anyone will be there when it ends; it's a dissociative spiral where death feels not like a destination but a question.","src":"haiku"},
"exitwounds~divide":{"haiku":"The self fragments into haunting duality where internal voices become predatory, consciousness eating itself as the narrator descends into madness and decay.","opus":"A confrontation with a self-destructive inner voice, the mind split against itself as disease and demise, the two halves haunting and decaying together and choosing damnation over survival.","sonnet":"An internal war between a self and its tormenting inner voice: the shadow in the mirror declares it will haunt the speaker until death, while the speaker, already feeling dead, decides to say goodbye and drag that other presence into hell with them.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~44-magnum-opus":{"haiku":"A violent killer frames his massacre as an art form, treating human bodies as a canvas and death as the ultimate creative expression.","opus":"The Bay Area thrash pioneers stage a grotesque first-person spree, casting a mass shooter as a demented artist painting his masterpiece in blood, bodies as canvas and ammunition as vision. The relentless imagery, murder equated with art and a tower built from decay, functions as extreme provocation, a deliberately horrific portrait of remorseless violence rather than any endorsement of it.","sonnet":"Mass violence is aestheticized as artistic ambition — bodies as canvas, bullets as vision, carnage as monument. The framing is entirely that of a self-mythologizing perpetrator who understands murder as mastery. The horror is the sincerity of the conceit: the speaker genuinely believes destruction constitutes creation.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~a-lesson-in-violence":{"haiku":"A sadistic enforcer delights in stabbing and murdering victims, positioning himself as judge and jury in a circle of hell he creates.","opus":"Bay Area thrash founders Exodus revel in over-the-top menace, a narrator who claims demonic backing, casts himself as both accuser and executioner, and dares any fool to face him. Paul Baloff's early-eighties delivery is pure provocation, the sadistic thrill of watching his victims flee turned into a genre-defining anthem of savagery.","sonnet":"A figure positioning itself as pure, satanic aggression dares any challenger to step forward and be destroyed. The pleasure is entirely in domination — in watching others kneel, flee, or die — framed as the natural order of the ruthless over the weak.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~and-then-there-were-none":{"haiku":"Nuclear war destroys all life and hope, leaving only burned bodies, death, and Satan's work completed as no souls escape or find salvation.","opus":"An early Exodus track depicts nuclear holocaust with unsparing brutality, cataloguing the scorched and the dying as no rescue arrives. The thrash intensity channels Cold War terror, pinning the annihilation on the nations that built the weapons and casting the resulting extinction as a triumph for evil, a scathing judgment on humanity's appetite for self-ruin.","sonnet":"Nuclear war reduces everything to ash and the bitter lesson arrives too late: the price of world-power conflict is paid entirely in civilian bodies. No salvation is offered, no god intervenes, and Satan arrives not as cause but as witness to the completion of human self-destruction. The world ends in the logic it chose.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~bitch":{"haiku":"Physical and emotional attraction to someone triggers involuntary responses in the body, described as mysterious and intoxicating, possibly love or obsession.","opus":"A loose, lusty cover-style rocker about desire as a kind of Pavlovian compulsion, the narrator listing his sluggish, distracted, drunk-feeling states before admitting that at the sound of a name his heart pounds like a bass drum, love reduced to an itch that must be mixed and fixed. Exodus are the Bay Area thrash-metal pioneers, and this playful, bluesy strut is a swaggering outlier in their usually ferocious catalogue.","sonnet":"Infatuation described as a full-body malfunction — can't sleep, can't eat, drunk without drinking, exhausted without reason. The only explanation is love, and it's named a bitch with a mixture of complaint and helpless surrender, the body simply overridden by the presence of another person.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~blacklist":{"haiku":"Someone betrayed and harmed swears vengeance; they add their betrayer's name to a list of targets marked for retribution.","opus":"One of the Bay Area thrash pioneers turns a personal betrayal into a vow of certain retribution, marking down the traitor's name and daring the coward to face him. It's revenge rendered as relentless hunt, all wounded loyalty and cocked menace, delivered over punishing riffs that make the threat feel loaded and inevitable.","sonnet":"A man who has been betrayed, slandered, and underestimated by someone he trusted enumerates that person's crimes and promises retribution. The blacklist is both literal and ceremonial — adding the traitor's name to a running ledger of those who will answer for crossing him. Revenge is framed as guaranteed, patient, and already in motion.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~bonded-by-blood":{"haiku":"Metal fans gather in shared intensity and blood-spilling devotion at concerts, bonded by the violent energy and power that metal provides. Blood ritual seals metal brotherhood.","opus":"The Bay Area thrash manifesto turning a mosh pit into a blood ritual, brotherhood forged through shared violence and devotion to the music. It mythologizes the scene's intensity as a rite that binds outsiders together.","sonnet":"The blood ritual of metal brotherhood: cutting your palm, drinking each other's blood, and being bound by that act into a community defined by extreme music and the shared need to headbang, rage, and kill — as much about the visceral concert experience as literal gore.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~burn-hollywood-burn":{"haiku":"Celebrity culture and entertainment industry vapidity deserve destruction for celebrating fakeness, manufactured fame, and the exploitation of youth.","opus":"A scornful attack on Hollywood celebrity culture and its manufactured fame, calling for the whole spectacle of fleeting stars, tabloid heiresses and vapid reality to burn and be forgotten.","sonnet":"A sustained attack on celebrity culture and reality television — the song targets the manufactured fame of talentless or morally bankrupt figures, presenting their public visibility as a symptom of cultural rot and calling for the whole industry to be destroyed.","src":"haiku","fable":"The joke this thrash tirade never turns on itself is that its author, to compile two verses of tabloid names and reality-TV grotesques, had to watch every glossy cover he wants incinerated. So the singer is one more of the puppets he sneers at, and the chorus can only picture cleansing as a contemptuous, self-soiling gesture over the blaze — arson downgraded to spite, judgment that is really just bile. The honest subject was never Hollywood but the man's own compulsion to keep staring and hating what holds him, a target far harder to set alight.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"the arsonist exposed as another puppet of the spectacle he wants incinerated","built":"compiling a verse of targets requires watching every glossy cover the chorus demands to burn"},
"exodus~class-dismissed-a-hate-primer":{"haiku":"A manifesto of violent rejection erupts from accumulated social wounds, the rejected turning their agony into apocalyptic declaration and murder as final argument.","opus":"A chilling first-person portrait of a school shooter, tracing rejection and rage into a manifesto of massacre that names real precedents like Charles Whitman, ending in self-inflicted death, hate feeding on hate.","sonnet":"Written from the perspective of a school shooter, the song narrates a campus massacre as the culmination of rejection and loneliness — a \"hate primer\" concluded with a gunshot to the speaker's own head after taking Charles Whitman as precedent.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~climb-before-the-fall":{"haiku":"A man abandons everyone for wealth and status but finds the higher he climbs, the further he must fall; material success cannot replace lost human connection.","opus":"Exodus swap thrash bloodlust for parable, following a poor boy raised beside the tracks whose father toiled himself to an early death. Determined to leave hardship behind, he climbs the business hierarchy into riches and isolation, flaunting his status and abandoning his mother to poverty. The lesson lands with grim symmetry: the higher he rises, the longer and more certain his coming descent.","sonnet":"A boy born poor claws his way to corporate wealth, shedding every relationship and moral tie on the way up. The story pivots on the law of overreach: the higher the ascent, the longer and more catastrophic the fall waiting at the summit.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~count-your-blessings":{"haiku":"The song criticizes the wealthy for complaining about their burdens and encourages them to recognize their fortune, warning that those who ignore their blessings will lose them.","opus":"Exodus, Bay Area thrash originators, aim blunt, profane class resentment at the whining wealthy, mocking people with money, status and fame who still gripe that riches are a burden while hoarding cash offshore as ordinary folk labor just to eat. The threat is that fortunes reverse for anyone too oblivious to appreciate what they have.","sonnet":"Impatience with the wealthy complaining about their wealth — a speaker who watches people with Swiss accounts and stock portfolios gripe about affluence and wants to remind them what it looks like to actually work for food. The sermon is blunt: count what you have before the roles reverse, because they will, and you will not be the first person to wish you had.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~crawl-before-you-walk-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Mockery of someone who talks big but fails to act; the singer taunts them for all talk and no substance, saying they need to prove themselves through action.","opus":"A thrash taunt aimed at a loudmouthed challenger, spinning a father's cautionary advice into mockery. The narrator, brimming with confidence, ridicules the rival as a cowardly braggart who talks tough but collapses when tested, insisting deeds beat noise and that a pretender who skipped the fundamentals belongs back in the dirt.","sonnet":"A direct confrontation aimed at someone all bluster and no substance. The speaker, unbothered and confident, refuses word games and demands proof over talk — the braggart is already cornered, undone by gap between the bold claims made and the empty actions that followed.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~deathamphetamine":{"haiku":"Addiction to methamphetamine destroys life through sleep deprivation, physical decay, and the impossibility of escape.","opus":"A harrowing depiction of methamphetamine addiction, following the pipe, the sleepless multi-day binges and the physical and mental ruin as the user wastes away toward death, losing everything and everyone.","sonnet":"A detailed portrait of methamphetamine addiction's arc — the pipe, the five-day runs, the sleep deprivation, the paranoia, the physical decay — ending with the user having lost everything and heading toward death.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~delivered-to-evil":{"haiku":"Demonic forces ascend as the imprisoned celebrate infernal liberation, godly authority inverted as hell opens its gates and suffering becomes ritual celebration.","opus":"Gleeful satanic blasphemy from beyond the grave, the damned narrator praying to Lucifer, welcoming a reign of terror and the end of the world as the undead rise and God is bound in chains.","sonnet":"A Satanic liturgy delivered from the grave: the speaker summons hell's forces, watches a virgin sacrificed in blood, and gloats as god dies in chains and the living burn and bleed until they bow — a total inversion of the Lord's Prayer.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~devil-s-teeth-bonus-track":{"haiku":"A prehistoric apex predator with supernatural malice hunting and devouring prey. It emerges from the abyss with a maniac smile, unstoppable and unending.","opus":"The Bay Area thrash founders train their fury on a great shark, an ancient predator that has outlasted mass extinctions and now waits in the deep to make its prey the next victim, torn apart bite by bite. The animal is rendered as a soulless killing machine and a blood-splattering artist, an untamable terror humans learn to dread.","sonnet":"A great white shark is rendered as an ancient godless predator — hundred-million-year survivor of every extinction, arriving from the abyss to reduce the living to fragments. The attack is portrayed with horror-film precision: black eyes rolling white at the moment of the kill.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~downfall":{"haiku":"A civilization collapses through vanity and warfare, with rulers paralyzed by blame-shifting as empires burn. The song warns that those wielding power ignore their own ignorance until everything crumbles to ashes.","opus":"The thrash band surveys a civilization tearing itself apart with grim satisfaction, cataloguing ruin as the price of human vanity and neglect. Invoking historical images of decadence and rulers who grab credit while ducking blame, it lands on a fatalistic verdict: humanity is harvesting the collapse it planted for itself.","sonnet":"The collapse of civilization through vanity, greed, and the failure to learn from history — empires fall like Sodom, leaders fiddle like Nero, and the song's rhetorical question is whether any wisdom can be salvaged from the rubble before the final downfall.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~exodus":{"haiku":"Violent invaders take pleasure in brutal conquest, murder, and domination, glorifying death and cruelty as a philosophy of strength. The exodus is a raid of annihilation.","opus":"Early thrash reveling in shock for its own sake, a first-person sadist glorying in cruelty, murder, and death. There's no message beneath it, just the transgressive thrill of pushing the horror as far as it will go.","sonnet":"An unambiguous first-person celebration of sadistic violence — the narrator describes deriving pleasure from pain, killing innocent victims, rape and murder — delivered entirely without irony as thrash metal provocation.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~fabulous-disaster":{"haiku":"Nuclear war looms as inevitable consequence of human indifference and political corruption, annihilating future generations.","opus":"Thrash-era nuclear terror, mocking the politicians and madmen holding the button while the rest of us wait to be vaporized. The bitter kicker is that voting is your only weapon against the men gambling with the whole world.","sonnet":"Nuclear annihilation at the hands of a raving madman is presented as both inevitable and politically preventable, with the narrator swinging between fatalistic acceptance and fury at the politicians who lie their way to power while building the missiles. The disaster is fabulous in the old sense — spectacular, mythic, and real.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~feeding-time-at-the-zoo":{"haiku":"Predatory hunger and violent frenzies consume people at a feast where survival depends on speed and aggression in a dangerous habitat.","opus":"Exodus recast a live show as a ravenous menagerie, comparing crazed fans to caged predators charging the food when the keepers arrive. Get too near and you'll pay for it, and every outrageous rumor about the mayhem is apparently true. Trading on gleeful brutality, the band serve the mosh pit up as a bloody banquet for anyone eager to fight.","sonnet":"A thrash metal crowd is rendered as a pack of predatory animals driven by hunger to a feeding frenzy — the show itself the raw bait, the pit the habitat, violence and appetite indistinguishable from appetite for the music. The narrator is both zookeeper and fellow beast, reveling in the chaos.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~going-going-gone":{"haiku":"A serial killer's pleasure in violence and the thrill of murder. Bloodshed and death are described as bliss and therapy, confidence and self-esteem.","opus":"With their trademark gallows humor, thrash veterans Exodus climb inside a gleeful serial killer for whom murder is both pleasure and self-therapy. He tallies his tools and victims, brags about slipping away untraced and doing it all his own way, savoring the capture over the pursuit while each victim grasps their fate too late.","sonnet":"A serial killer narrates his compulsion with cold relish: violence is experienced as bliss, self-confidence, therapy. Each unlocked door and dark night is an invitation he follows without hesitation, leaving fear frozen on faces and no witnesses behind. The killing is described as an addiction that fully consumes him.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~good-day-to-die":{"haiku":"A suicidal person contemplates ending their life but is challenged to reconsider, with reminders of loved ones and futures yet unseen.","opus":"Exodus stage a debate with suicidal despair. One voice, a man who has quietly decided this morning is when he ends his pain, is answered by another that brands the choice cowardly and pleads with him to look up and remember the people who would be devastated by his loss.","sonnet":"A person quietly decides to take their own life, keeping the plan hidden while overwhelmed by pain, and the song directly addresses them — challenging the idea that suicide is an answer and naming what they would leave behind.","src":"haiku","fable":"An anti-suicide song built on a hard pivot in perspective. Verse one narrates from a cool distance, a third-person he who wakes, weighs the bright morning against his private reasons, and quietly resolves to end it while no one around him suspects a thing. Then the song swings into the second person and largely stays there, and the compassion curdles into confrontation: the same Bay Area thrash aggression that usually points outward is aimed at the listener as a kind of tough-love intervention, calling the exit cowardice, demanding he consider the people who love him and the future he would forfeit. The refrain is the song's cruelest instrument, ventriloquizing the suicidal lie back at him so the phrase can be answered rather than agreed with, and the outro finally reframes carrying on itself as the way to the peace he was chasing through death.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide"],"means":"thrash aggression turned inward as a tough-love intervention against the exit","built":"a hard pronoun pivot swings the song from cool third-person narration into direct second-person confrontation"},
"exodus~good-riddance":{"haiku":"The song expresses nihilistic contempt for humanity and the earth, celebrating environmental collapse and the apocalypse as inevitable justice.","opus":"Exodus deliver a mocking farewell to a dying planet, treating Earth as a filthy speck doomed by its own runaway population. Instead of grief, the speaker pulls up a chair to enjoy the show, jeering at anyone who still thinks the world can be rescued and framing the end as a spectacle to applaud.","sonnet":"A sardonic narrator watches humanity accelerate toward self-inflicted extinction and feels nothing but dark relief — no grief, no tears, no desire to intervene. The planet is framed as a parasite-ridden host finally reaching its overdue cancellation, and the speaker toasts the collapse without apology.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~heads-they-win-tails-you-lose":{"opus":"Bay Area thrash as a cautionary tale about gambling addiction, where the odds never favor the player and money owed brings enforcers and a watery grave. The fixed coin flip of the title declares the whole system rigged against the desperate.","src":"opus"},
"exodus~i-am-abomination":{"haiku":"The speaker embodies all forms of human affliction, addiction, and evil lurking beneath consciousness, representing internal devastation personified.","opus":"Evil speaks in the first person as affliction, addiction, and chaos, the origin lurking just beneath the skin and claiming kinship with the listener: you are me. Jehovah, monster, and every trapped perversion converge into one face of devastation. Exodus, Bay Area thrash pioneers, voice this personification of inner corruption with brute, riff-heavy force, making the devil an intimate reflection.","sonnet":"An entity — simultaneously addiction, trauma, violence, and moral corruption — speaks from inside the self, cataloguing all the ways it inhabits and destroys from within. It claims divine and monstrous names alike, collapsing the distinction between external evil and internal rot. The final equation is damning: what corrupts the world also lives inside the speaker.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~karma-s-messenger":{"haiku":"Vengeance and retribution punish humanity for greed, arrogance, and debauchery, with destruction and suffering as the price of collective sin.","opus":"Thrash as apocalyptic judgment, humanity framed as a cancer of greed and lust that vengeance now comes to collect. Exodus voicing retribution itself, punishment fitted to the crime of being human.","sonnet":"A song narrated by an avenging cosmic force, delivering brutal punishment to a human race it views as a cancer of greed, lust, and violence — retribution framed as righteous cosmic justice.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~me-myself-i":{"opus":"Exodus voicing pure self-interest as creed, a thrash-metal manifesto of greed personified with contempt for charity, generosity, or anyone else's needs. It weaponizes selfishness into a snarling first-person anthem of every-man-for-himself.","src":"opus"},
"exodus~metal-command":{"haiku":"Metal legions advance with sonic warfare and merciless domination, demanding absolute obedience or death. Mercy dissolves beneath the crushing weight of amplified command.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek call to metal warfare, summoning headbangers to obey the band's command, thrash in the pit and conquer town after town in the name of the music.","sonnet":"A militaristic fantasy of a metal band as conquering army: they roll from town to town leaving a trail of death, demanding surrender or destruction, with fists in the air and faces melting at the sonic assault — obey the metal command or perish.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~now-thy-death-day-come":{"haiku":"Divine judgment ends humanity; angels weep as Lucifer rises and scorches Earth, ending the war of creation.","opus":"The thrash founders stage the apocalypse from the devil's perspective, gloating over mankind's ruin as the divine withdraws and the fallen rise to claim an age of torment. It argues that endless human cruelty and warfare made this end unavoidable, delivering humanity's extinction with cold, ironic satisfaction as heaven itself is torn apart.","sonnet":"The apocalypse arrives as centuries of human war fulfill their inevitable conclusion — heaven shattered, angels broken, Lucifer ascending not as conqueror but as the logical outcome of mankind's rehearsed hatred. The narrator surveys the ruin from black wings with cold satisfaction.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~piranha":{"haiku":"Piranhas are depicted as inevitable instruments of death—bred for slaughter, escaped from hell's gates, and attacking in coordinated packs that strip flesh from bone. No escape exists for their victims.","opus":"A gleefully gruesome thrash-horror yarn casting the fish as hell-spawned killers who will strip a victim to the bone if they ever get loose, taunting that anyone confident of surviving them is a fool. Exodus, Bay Area thrash pioneers, revel in the cartoonish carnage at breakneck speed.","sonnet":"Thrash metal as creature feature — a swarm of piranha cast as Loki's children, bred for slaughter, escaping the gates of hell to strip flesh and end anything they encounter. The pleasure is in the relentlessness: there is no negotiating with the school, no surviving the pack, and the song does not pretend otherwise.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~pump-it-up":{"haiku":"Desire builds and builds until it demands release; the narrator and his subject engage in escalating, narcotic obsession and seduction.","opus":"The band's thrash cover skewers compulsive overstimulation, the craving that keeps demanding more with no satisfaction ever reached. Beneath the frantic pace runs a jab at hollow pursuits, whether appetite, spin, or consumer hunger, that leave a person craving still more even after going numb.","sonnet":"A cover of Elvis Costello's song about obsessive, addictive desire for a woman who is simultaneously thrilling and toxic — she's like a narcotic who controls you through stimulus and withholding, and no amount of attention or gifts can satisfy either of you.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~raze":{"haiku":"A call to indiscriminate destruction and chaos—starting fires, burning everything down without consequence or hesitation, reveling in demolition for its own sake.","opus":"A gleeful call to total destruction, urging listeners to burn and demolish everything without caring about consequences or taking any blame.","sonnet":"A thrash-metal call to total destruction: the narrator invites a companion to burn and level everything in sight, explicitly dismissing consequences and reveling in pure demolition for its own sake.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~scar-spangled-banner":{"haiku":"America wages endless violent wars destroying innocents, wrapping brutality and deception in patriotic symbolism and flag imagery.","opus":"A savage thrash desecration of the American anthem, rewriting patriotic imagery as bombs, bodies, and hypocrisy. It rejects flag-worship outright, branding blind allegiance as a celebration of violence dressed in red, white, and blue.","sonnet":"A vicious rewrite of the American national anthem — replacing purple mountains with dead bodies and rockets' red glare with literal missile strikes, arguing that America's flag is drenched in the blood of its own violence and imperial arrogance.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~shudder-to-think":{"haiku":"A washed-up failure refuses responsibility, blames others, and wasted every opportunity through drugs and lies; living like them would make anyone shake.","opus":"A scornful takedown of a washed-out addict who has bottomed out yet blames everyone but himself, dodging responsibility behind lies. The thrash veterans pile on the contempt, telling this burnout who wasted every last chance to quit making excuses and go find honest work.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who has squandered every opportunity through lying, drug use, and blame-shifting — the narrator has run out of patience and sympathy, telling the person their excuses are exhausted and it's time to take responsibility for their own wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~strike-of-the-beast":{"opus":"Early Exodus in full horror-thrash mode, staging a nocturnal hunt by a winged fire-breathing demon that runs down its prey. It's a genre piece reveling in dread and damnation, the beast as pure predatory menace with hell as the reward for failure.","src":"opus"},
"exodus~the-sun-is-my-destroyer":{"haiku":"Malevolent shadow dwells within human consciousness, shaping nightmares and breeding fear.","opus":"A monster's monologue — a creature of nightmare and human imagination that rules the dark and fears one thing, the sunrise. It personifies fear itself, feeding on whatever you love most the instant your eyes shut.","sonnet":"A creature of darkness and nightmare speaks in first person — flourishing in shadow and sleep, destroyed by sunlight and clarity — revealing itself as a psychological parasite that feeds on fear, divides the self, and can only survive in the unexamined mind.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~thorn-in-my-side":{"haiku":"A child describes lifelong mistreatment by a parent who never believed in him, pushed him to fail, and wore authority like a crown—a reckoning after years of pain.","opus":"A settling of scores with a lifelong tormentor who withheld encouragement, belittled the singer at every turn, and made him feel he should never have existed. Years of swallowed hurt curdle into a vow of reckoning, Exodus channeling that vengeful catharsis through their pounding Bay Area thrash attack.","sonnet":"A lifetime of withholding, dismissal, and attempted domination confronted head-on — the speaker addressing someone who never encouraged him, tried to push him down, and wore authority like a crown of thorns. The judgment day being waited for is not divine but personal: the moment the person responsible for that embedded wound is finally seen clearly for their lies.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~thrash-under-pressure":{"haiku":"Thrash metal becomes both defiant stance and cultural identity, surviving critique through uncompromising aggression that refuses domestication or apology.","opus":"Bay Area thrash as tribal defiance, a proud middle finger to critics and disapproving parents that turns the genre's name into a badge and loyalty to the scene into an identity worth defending.","sonnet":"A defiant declaration of loyalty to thrash metal as identity and community — the band refuses outside criticism, embraces the label with pride, and asserts that their music and their people will endure regardless of parental opposition or mainstream rejection.","src":"haiku"},
"exodus~war-is-my-shepherd":{"haiku":"A soldier replaces religious faith with weapons and warfare, viewing combat and military power as their guiding force rather than God.","opus":"Exodus rework the Twenty-third Psalm into a soldier's blasphemous credo, placing trust in weaponry rather than God and treating a rifle as savior. Naming real televangelists, the speaker answers their preaching with firepower and calls his own sermon destruction. The thrash barrage paints faith and warfare as twin frauds, believers praying uselessly as combat swallows them whole.","sonnet":"A soldier discards religious faith entirely, replacing the Psalm's shepherd with the rifle and napalm, since God delivered nothing while the killing fields did. The critique is bitter and firsthand — faith as administered by televangelists is exposed as useless against the machinery of war that governs real life.","src":"haiku"},
"exploid~edgerunners":{"haiku":"Refusal of existence and self-love collapses into champagne-fueled apathy where manipulation and fakery exhaust the speaker's will and connection dies in the soil of impossibility.","opus":"Anime-tied electronic track voicing a numb death wish and the truth that you can't love someone who can't love themselves. The champagne-in-the-air imagery of hollow pride sits against a plea to stop the lies and manipulation.","sonnet":"A fragmented speaker tells someone they cannot be loved by a person who cannot love themselves, interspersing the observation with a desire to die in peace and \"champagne in the air\" as hollow celebration of mutual dysfunction.","src":"haiku"},
"exploid~keep-it-running":{"opus":"A hype-driven electronic banger built on street bravado and momentum, running the night flat-out until death. There's little beneath the swagger; it's about relentless forward motion and the thrill of never letting the energy drop.","src":"opus"},
"exploid~substance":{"opus":"An electronic track about dependence dressed as desire, someone who can't move forward alone and keeps returning to a lover's touch to feel better. The fragmented repetition mirrors a mind stuck circling need and intimacy.","src":"opus"},
"extize~dirty-freddy-remix":{"haiku":"A Freddy Krueger–voiced nightmare scenario renders the horror-movie slasher's perspective directly — the thrill of the hunt, the blade, the screams — as pure aggressive fantasy.","opus":"A horror-themed industrial/dark-electro romp riffing on the Freddy Krueger nightmare mythos, all menace and provocation. It's built for shock and the dancefloor, weaponizing slasher imagery into a taunting demand to scream rather than saying anything sincere.","sonnet":"A horror-fantasy voiced by Freddy Krueger: counting rhymes at the door, blade-handed invasion of sleep, the ecstasy of the victim's scream. Pure slasher theatre — burned face, black ash rain, the predator savoring terror as pleasure.","src":"haiku"},
"extortionist~stockholm":{"opus":"Deathcore mapping an abusive relationship onto Stockholm syndrome, where the captive's desire for the abuser only grows even as it destroys them. The horror is recognizing the leash and craving it anyway, dignity lacerated in the name of love.","src":"opus"},
"extra-terra~silence":{"haiku":"Time slips away on the deathbed; only silence and distance remain as the end approaches.","opus":"A deathbed goodbye where, at the very end, only stillness remains and the crushing awareness of time slipping past, with so much never spoken and a hope of one more reunion.","sonnet":"At a deathbed with time running out, the speaker is consumed not by words but by the silence of final moments and the terror of time escaping — wondering whether they will see the other person again once the moment fades.","src":"haiku"},
"exwhyz~our-song":{"haiku":"Two people caught in accelerating time reach toward each other with light and music as their shared flash — urgent closeness before the distance between them grows too great.","opus":"J-pop about seizing a fleeting present with someone before time or distance separates you, torn between whether to trust the moment. The shine-on-me refrain is mutual illumination, the plea to feel a person close while the chance still exists.","sonnet":"A Japanese song about closing the distance between two people through music and presence: the narrator wants to feel you closer, asks what you're searching for in the flowing future, and the blue flash light illuminating dreams in the rhythm becomes the meeting point — stretching hands high, hoping to be reached.","src":"haiku"},
"exwhyz~secret-secret-shin-sakiura-remix":{"haiku":"Transgressive desire escalates as lovers melt toward consumption, blurring boundaries between restraint and domination.","opus":"A flirtatious J-pop confession about being caught off guard by attraction, feigning indifference while falling helplessly. The remix keeps the giddy tension of a forbidden crush kept secret, all racing heartbeat and the sweet loss of composure.","sonnet":"The speaker is already caught — \"Lock on, already too late\" — hopelessly drawn to someone despite trying to appear indifferent, the overflowing lipstick and racing heartbeat marking the body's betrayal of the mind's pretence. (lang: ja)","src":"haiku"},
"exwhyz~showtime":{"opus":"An exuberant J-pop declaration of proving yourself through action rather than talk, turning zero expectations into spectacle. Beneath the confidence runs honest struggle, the black-swan grind of flailing gracefully, transmuted into joy shared with the crowd.","src":"opus"},
"exwhyz~unknown-sense":{"haiku":"Chaotic movement through uncertainty refuses easy answers, embracing the nonsensical as liberation from expectations while demanding raw intensity over meaning.","opus":"A midnight anthem of authenticity, refusing to be consumed or commodified and insisting on being a priceless, self-directed self rather than anyone's princess. Dancing into an unknown future on your own terms is the whole stance.","sonnet":"An exuberant declaration of artistic self-possession: the speaker and her crew operate at full speed on their own terms, rejecting the passive princess role in favor of sweat, unpredictability, and a process that refuses to be consumed or reduced — chasing an indefinable, priceless sensation that leaps beyond expectation.","src":"haiku"},
"eyes-wide-open~ghosts-parasites":{"opus":"Metalcore casting humanity as the gravediggers of its own age, ghosts and parasites sold war and bloodshed as legacy. The stance is bleak apocalyptic warning about inherited fear and the powerful profiting from destruction.","src":"opus"},
"ezekiel~help-urself-2":{"haiku":"Self-reliance and independence; taking what is needed without waiting for permission or rescue.","opus":"A hazy, self-lacerating confessional about a relationship crumbling under substance use, depression, and mistrust. The narrator feels emptied out and replaceable, wanting something he can't touch, aware he's the one breaking what he loves and unable to help himself.","sonnet":"A spiraling relationship and a broken self that cannot reach the hand it reaches for — skin made of scars, falling from the sun, the feeling of emptiness making everything mad — and the conclusion that it is about time someone took his place, the broke life in a cell arriving not as self-pity but as a factual inventory.","src":"haiku"},
"faetooth~iron-gate":{"opus":"Doom-laden, ritual verse about clawing out of a fate that cages you, the self recast as every tool of escape at once. The central image is shedding a hardened outer layer — an armor grown into a prison — to break loose.","src":"opus"},
"failure~a-way-down":{"opus":"The alt-rock cult band in cryptic form: a narrator decoding a troubled person's hidden messages and needing to escape their orbit. The way down is both descent and exit, self-preservation dressed in Failure's cool, dissociative unease.","src":"opus"},
"failure~another-space-song":{"haiku":"An isolated astronaut orbits alone, unable to reach the person they search for eternally; loneliness persists despite yearning and persistence.","opus":"An astronaut stranded and drifting toward the sun, choosing acceptance over rescue, where the lost woman he keeps reaching for is really everything unreachable. Ken Andrews turns deep-space isolation into a quiet elegy for what you can never find.","sonnet":"A lone astronaut drifting without mission control, using space as a metaphor for being utterly unmoored — the woman he can never reach hides behind a star, and he surrenders to patient time carrying him forward even if he is never found.","src":"haiku"},
"failure~blank":{"haiku":"A numb person coasts through life unbothered, preferring emptiness and emotional dampening over joy or reflection. They reject mirrors and meaning, content to exist in a blank state on the moon.","opus":"The space-rock band leans into willful emptiness, the singer admitting he prefers the numb, featureless way he fills his days and wants nothing that stirs him too much. Hints of chemical self-medication run beneath a refusal to look inward, and his cosmic isolation reads as both a comfort and a quiet admission of despair.","sonnet":"A speaker embraces emotional flatness as a preferred state — dampened, drifting, living at a remove like someone marooned on the moon. The blankness is both a defense against being overwhelmed and a kind of longing: somewhere inside it is a wish for a miracle, quietly acknowledged and then folded back into the void.","src":"haiku"},
"failure~counterfeit-sky":{"haiku":"Lies and stagnation trap people in false identities; despite change, nothing shifts and one wakes unable to recognize themselves.","opus":"Failure, the cult space-rock outfit whose reunion sharpened their dissonant introspection, dismantle a man deceiving himself, his denial only feeding the phantom weighing him down. Small untruths erode him, a false horizon betrays him, and despite endless supposed transformation nothing actually shifts, leaving him waking in confusion with no grip on who he is. Belonging and identity dissolve as the self loses hold of itself.","sonnet":"A relationship built on subtle deceits corrodes identity over time — the accumulated weight of small lies leaves a person who wakes up laughing without knowing who they are. Despite every apparent transformation, nothing fundamental changes; the counterfeit sky keeps selling the self out.","src":"haiku"},
"failure~dark-speed":{"haiku":"A person floats untethered and unable to leave, caught between the dream of sleep and the nightmare of waking life.","opus":"Alt-rock cult favorites Failure render total dissociation as weightless free-fall: cut loose from everyone, numbed past all sensation, adrift in a void where sleep is the only escape and being awake feels like a living death. Freedom and emptiness become indistinguishable, capturing the band's signature spacey dread.","sonnet":"Dissociation as both freedom and suffocation — floating untethered in a void where wounds cannot be felt because the gel of numbness cancels sensation. The speaker addresses someone caught between wanting sleep as escape and living a waking death, trapped in a room with no air, told they are free now in a way that sounds less like liberation than erasure.","src":"haiku"},
"failure~dirty-blue-balloons":{"haiku":"Desperation and chemical dependency strip the speaker of autonomy; desire for escape crystallizes into addiction, transforming hope into addiction's false comfort.","opus":"A heroin song told from inside the addiction, the drug delivered in balloons and nothing else able to carry the user across the hours of day or night. Missing an absent lover and craving the next hit blur into one, and neither can be released.","sonnet":"A heroin dependency is rendered with precise, sensory images — a burnt spoon dried under the bed, a dealer who spoke no shared language, payment of everything — and the floating blue balloons become the fix itself, the only thing that can carry the speaker through a night when nothing and no one else helps.","src":"haiku"},
"failure~pennies":{"haiku":"Contemplating suicide from a building ledge, paralyzed by conscience. The pennies represent small harms that accumulate into life-or-death choices people make about others.","opus":"An eerie vignette of alienation and dark impulse, a childhood piggy bank shattering by the bed leading to hours spent lining coins on a windowsill above a city, half-tempted to drop them on the people below yet lacking the courage. Winter passes in bed as loose change and conscience alike refuse to stay held down. Failure, the cult American alt-rock band, render this unsettling isolation in their hazy, hypnotic space-rock.","sonnet":"A suicidal ideation described in precise domestic detail — pennies lined on a windowsill high above the street, the courage to push them failing, a winter spent in bed with the coins frozen in place. The image of looking up from below for change shining through the air inverts the original fantasy into something almost hopeful, though the final line reasserts the original lethal fact.","src":"haiku"},
"failure~pillowhead":{"haiku":"A man chooses imaginary companionship over real human connection, spending his life with a faceless pillow rather than risk the pain of actual love. The song is about profound isolation and the shame of withdrawing from genuine human vulnerability.","opus":"Ken Andrews sketches a portrait of romantic surrender, retreating into an imaginary faceless partner because real intimacy feels impossible. Beneath the deadpan is genuine shame at loving no one and preferring a fantasy that can't hurt him.","sonnet":"A dark, lonely song about giving up on human connection and settling for a fantasy — the \"pillowhead girl\" is a faceless, silent substitute for a real partner, a sign of the narrator's shame at loving no one and retreating into a private, imaginary relationship rather than face the world.","src":"haiku"},
"failure~saturday-saviour":{"haiku":"A lover is content to be a temporary weekend solution, refusing depth or permanence while aware they are an impostor in someone's plan. The song celebrates limited, transactional connection.","opus":"Ken Andrews plays the emotionally unavailable lover with cold honesty, offering only a day's presence and refusing to be the salvation someone wants. He names himself the impostor in their plan, intimacy withheld on principle.","sonnet":"About someone who only wants to play a limited, temporary role in another person's life — explicitly not their real salvation, not fully present, just a weekend stand-in who refuses to fake depth or commitment.","src":"haiku"},
"failure~sergeant-politeness":{"haiku":"A thief is interrogated by a seemingly gentle authority figure who manipulates them through ego-stroking and false camaraderie.","opus":"A junkie's cat-and-mouse with a gentle interrogator, where the real captor is the boredom and self-sabotage that made him want to be caught. The stones he hides mean nothing; getting undone so softly is almost the point.","sonnet":"A detainee or prisoner being softly manipulated by an interrogator — the titular sergeant uses politeness as a tool to find weak points and extract a confession — while the speaker, who deliberately let himself be caught, refuses to give anything up and finds a perverse pleasure in the powerlessness.","src":"haiku"},
"failure~smoking-umbrellas":{"haiku":"A dreaming person remains detached as their bed burns and firefighters call about smoking umbrellas in the hallway. They choose not to leave the comfort of their bed despite the fire.","opus":"Failure conjure a dreamlike house fire, a narrator waking amid the blaze yet too numb to flee, choosing to sink back into sleep as rescuers count him among the lost. The band renders it in a slow, narcotic haze, the surreal imagery a bleak stand-in for passive surrender and self-destruction.","sonnet":"A dreamlike building fire interrupts sleep — red lights streaming through shuttered windows, photographs melting, panicked voices in the walls. Rather than flee, the dreamer surrenders back into bed and dreamland, choosing the comfort of burning over the effort of escape.","src":"haiku"},
"failure~solaris":{"haiku":"Isolation and psychological merger blur the boundary between self and obsession; the speaker inhabits a sentient, dream-inducing realm where escape means drowning in their own need.","opus":"Cast as the sentient ocean of Lem's Solaris, a mourner conjures a dead lover back into flesh and cannot decide whether to cling to her or escape the haunting. It is about grief that reanimates the lost and drags you back to a life you wanted to leave.","sonnet":"The speaker has been freezing himself in isolation — slowing breath, hiding in snow — to escape a haunting presence that keeps thawing him back to life, drawn from imagery of an ocean with a mind that makes people dream; each intrusion of desire or memory undoes the numbness he has fought to achieve.","src":"haiku"},
"failure~water-with-hands":{"opus":"A dissociative, numb state where reality and truth collapse into each other; the narrator feels overwhelmed by feeling nothing, distrusts mind-control paranoia, yet clings to wanting only one particular person.","sonnet":"A disoriented song about emotional overwhelm and the impossibility of filtering out unwanted experience — the narrator feels flattened by the weight of feeling, caught in paradoxes of truth and unreality, wanting only the one person causing the pain.","src":"sonnet"},
"faith-no-more~a-small-victory":{"haiku":"Petty rivalries and competitive victories occupy the mind despite their meaninglessness, causing frustration that cannot be voiced or resolved.","opus":"Faith No More, the San Francisco genre-splicers fronted by Mike Patton, skewer petty rivalry through a man who hoards trophies yet swallows his resentment silently. Both a graceless loser and an ungracious winner, he confesses the trivial slights ought not to eat at him but do, and the song closes on a droning fixation aimed at someone who still refuses to listen, turning competitiveness into pointless obsession.","sonnet":"Competitiveness simmers beneath a clenched jaw — trophies and medallions that ought to mean nothing keep mattering anyway. The narrator bites back the urge to compete and win, silently sustained by minor victories that feed the fantasy of one day finally beating the person who still won't hear a word said.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~anne-s-song":{"haiku":"A cheerful party anthem celebrating freedom and the ability to do anything with a charismatic friend named Anne who brings joy and possibility.","opus":"A frantic, half-spoken skit about chafing under the way others police your every move, the narrator interrogated about his behavior until a pal named Anne cheerfully tells him to just please himself since everything is his for the taking. It spirals into a rowdy party roll-call. Faith No More, the genre-scrambling San Francisco band, indulge their most chaotic, anarchic funk-rock streak here.","sonnet":"A party anthem built around a woman named Anne who makes everything possible — her presence transforms the night into permission, and a whole cast of friends arrives in her orbit, each introduced with a small comic detail. The song's energy is infectious and deliberately absurd, a celebration of collective joy held together by one person whose charisma licenses everyone else to let go.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~ashes-to-ashes":{"haiku":"A person surrenders completely to another, willing to dissolve and become part of them through physical and spiritual union.","opus":"Patton at his most sweepingly redemptive, a narrator taking full responsibility to lift someone off the floor and let them settle the score. Grand oceanic imagery turns guilt into an offering that draws two people closer.","sonnet":"A gesture of total self-surrender and accountability — the narrator takes the blame for everything, offers themselves completely, and reimagines themselves as the ocean and mountains just to be closer to the person they've hurt.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~be-aggressive":{"haiku":"Aggressive consumption dominates survival as the speaker swallows, submits, and devours their way through existence. Predation and worship collapse into identical acts.","opus":"A sardonic cheerleader chant fusing sexual submission and predatory greed, its swallowing imagery turning macho aggression into a queer, provocative joke.","sonnet":"A voice of pure acquisition and submission fused together — everything belongs to the speaker, yet they also swallow and kneel, claiming ownership while enacting total degradation, the cheerleader chant spelling out aggression as both dominance and abasement.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~caralho-voador":{"haiku":"A reckless driver with no control hurtles down the highway knowing someone will eventually be hit. The song captures silent fatalism and inevitable disaster in motion.","opus":"A driver identified only by his vehicle rolls down Highway 101 to score, his mouth working soundlessly as he cautions that someone is going to get flattened, then slipping into Portuguese to confess he can't steer with a finger jammed up his nose. Faith No More, restless genre-splicers, indulge their absurdist streak, pairing menacing swagger with deliberately juvenile, multilingual comic deflation.","sonnet":"A reckless driver hurtles down Highway 101, distracted and dissociated, muttering to no one while the road ahead threatens casualties. A brief aside in Portuguese — admitting he cannot drive and has a finger in his nose — deflates any menace into absurdist comedy. The song teeters between road-rage bravado and pure, self-aware ridiculousness.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~cuckoo-for-caca":{"haiku":"Materialist pretense and performance dominate society; people pursue surface goodness that breeds corruption. Feces endure as a metaphor for shit that lives forever.","opus":"Mike Patton's scatological satire of hypocrisy: how people perform their good side while corruption endures forever. The porcelain and excrement imagery mocks the human urge to keep up appearances over something rotten that can't be killed.","sonnet":"An absurdist provocation built around the idea that corruption, waste, and moral rot are permanent and indestructible — the song mocks the performance of goodness while suggesting that the filth underneath never dies.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~digging-the-grave":{"haiku":"Someone finds comfort in digging their own grave, letting things flow in and out to maintain the cycle of awareness and shadow.","opus":"A song about emotional excavation—'digging the grave' as a metaphor for confronting the buried self and finding it comfortable; letting something in and throwing something out, undoing the knots others leave tied, at the cost of losing the sun and warmth of the day.","sonnet":"A song about finding comfort in self-made destruction — the narrator has opened themselves to something dark and feels at ease inside it, comfortable digging their own grave, letting things in and throwing things out without concern.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~easy":{"haiku":"A man leaves his lover to escape emotional pain and pursue freedom, accepting ease and autonomy over connection and struggle.","opus":"Faith No More cover the Commodores' ballad straight-faced, letting the band's arch sensibility rest inside a tale of a man leaving a lover he can no longer bear. Having begged, stolen, and borrowed, he claims the ease of a Sunday morning as he walks away, craving freedom to be simply himself. The tenderness reads as both sincere and knowingly ironic in their hands.","sonnet":"A man leaves a painful relationship not in anger but in quiet relief — the decision frees him into a looseness he compares to Sunday morning. The departure is about reclaiming lightness, moving toward a freer selfhood unburdened by the weight of what could not be fixed.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~epic":{"haiku":"An unnamed, intangible 'it' captivates the senses and tantalizes but remains eternally out of grasp, leaving only contradiction and longing. The song distills the frustration of desire that consciousness makes irresistible but unreachable.","opus":"A wildly ironic anthem about the frenzied, insatiable pursuit of 'it'—fame, success, a desire never named—which you want with all your might but can never actually grasp; the piled-up contradictory adjectives and the empty refrain 'what is it? it's it' satirize craving something you can't even define.","sonnet":"An obsessive unnamed thing — slippery, physical, just out of reach — is described from every angle without ever being named, the point being that total saturation and total frustration coexist: you want it all, it's right in front of you, and you still can't have it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is a con built on a missing noun: Mike Patton hypes some ecstatic, all-consuming thing through breathless sensory inventory and stacked contradictions, yet the object is never once named, so the grand funk-metal swagger keeps promising a payoff it structurally refuses to deliver. Its meaning lives in the gap between reaching and closing your fingers, verses piling up ever more frantic descriptors while the hook flatly reminds you the prize stays beyond reach, desire inflated precisely because possession never arrives. The bridge collapses into pure tautology, a query answered only by restating itself until sense drains away, the punchline landing as musical form rather than as any lyric. That refusal to define is the point, whether the elusive thing reads as sex, ambition, ego, or the hollow rush of appetite, and the notorious video coda of a fish flailing and dying on dry land supplies the deflation the words withhold: rapture revealed as a gasping, absurd creature that cannot survive being seized.","fv":2,"fnote":"The dying fish that ends the video drew animal-rights outrage and a legend that it was Björk’s stolen goldfish — the director debunked both: several feisty fish took turns, none suffocated, all released into a river after the shoot.","themes":["unnameable craving","comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"desire inflated precisely because the object is structurally refused","built":"the fish dying on dry land supplies the deflation the lyrics withhold"},
"faith-no-more~everything-s-ruined":{"haiku":"A prodigy's meteoric rise corrupts and destroys those around them, their success collapsing into counterfeited ruin and exposing the fraud beneath the shine.","opus":"A parable of a couple who raise a child like an investment, watching him inflate into wealth and public adoration before discovering their prized creation was counterfeit all along.","sonnet":"A family metaphor for financial speculation: the child born as a copper penny grows into a million through the compounding faith of everyone around him, until he is revealed as counterfeit — and everything built on his value collapses.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~evidence":{"haiku":"The song addresses guilt and accusation, where evidence of wrongdoing is inescapable and trying to escape it only proves complicity. Evasion and denial become their own testimony of guilt.","opus":"A slinky, noirish meditation on guilt and denial, washing away proof and numbing feeling until nothing means a thing. Patton delivers it as smooth interrogation, complicity dressed up as cool detachment.","sonnet":"A narrator urges the erasure of damning evidence and insists on emotional detachment — looking someone in the eye and testifying to feeling nothing — suggesting a cover-up of guilt, perhaps for something violent or sexual, where the desire is to wash away what was done and deny it ever happened.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~evidence-2016-remaster":{"opus":"Faith No More at their most seductive and sinister, a lounge-funk noir where guilt is presumed and confession coerced. The refrain about washing away evidence and feeling nothing sits in the cold register of someone numbed past accountability.","src":"opus"},
"faith-no-more~falling-to-pieces":{"haiku":"The narrator feels caught between love and suffering, with indecision and uncertainty causing his life to fall apart as he seeks help to be put back together.","opus":"Faith No More render paralyzing indecision, a mind swaying uselessly as any resolve evaporates the moment it forms and inner order keeps disintegrating. Caught in a fog of uncertainty, suspended between devotion and pain, the narrator watches his life come undone and can only beg to be made whole, unable to even understand himself.","sonnet":"A person suspended between love and agony finds that every attempt at resolution slips away before it can be grasped — indecision clouds everything, and the picture beneath the crumbled puzzle pieces remains tantalizingly visible but unreachable. The cry for reassembly is genuine and unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~from-out-of-nowhere":{"haiku":"A person becomes obsessed with someone who appears unexpectedly and captivates them completely. This obsession feels inescapable until they wave goodbye, leaving hurt inside.","opus":"Sudden, overwhelming infatuation: a striking stranger appears unbidden, unsettling the narrator's composure until fascination consumes him and he belongs to her completely. The catch is how precarious it feels, an off-balance, blink-and-it's-gone intensity that leaves him unsure whether to feel joy or grief before she vanishes as abruptly as she arrived.","sonnet":"Sudden, overwhelming infatuation — someone appears out of nowhere and instantly the narrator can think of nothing else, all energy funnels into this one person, and the whole thing has the bittersweet quality of a beauty that arrives and disappears in the blink of an eye.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~get-out":{"haiku":"Something alien grows inside the speaker, transforming their identity and severing connection to their former self and values. Change becomes inescapable and unrecognizable.","opus":"The dread of feeling something alien grow inside you and the urge to bolt before it takes hold, whether it's change, aging, or becoming ordinary. Patton makes the fear of transformation into the fear of no longer being fun.","sonnet":"The narrator recognises something growing in them — a change that makes them alien to their former self, unable to speak the old language or bleed the same color — and concludes they need to get out before whatever this new thing is finishes its transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~get-out-2016-remaster":{"opus":"Mike Patton on the dread of an unwanted transformation growing inside you, watching yourself become like everyone else and wanting to cut losses and flee. The escalating denials are a refusal to accept a change in identity that can't be stopped.","src":"opus"},
"faith-no-more~just-a-man":{"haiku":"A man recognizes his limits—he cannot touch the divine or hold the world. He shuts his eyes to avoid seeing light that burns, dreaming of cloudiness instead of aspiration.","opus":"Beneath a clear sky, a narrator reaches for a star to hold yet refuses to touch what he cannot have, shutting his eyes each night to dream of a cloudy sky instead of blinding light. Faith No More close their album Album of the Year with this soulful, gospel-tinged meditation, the Icarus spoken passage underscoring mortal limits before the star finally falls burning into his hands.","sonnet":"A mortal confronts his own limits in the face of something radiant and unreachable. He averts his eyes from brightness he cannot hold, dreams of cloud cover, and acknowledges he is not divine — even as that brightness falls into his hands and scorches him anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~king-for-a-day":{"haiku":"Appearance and status are everything at the party; people are spinning and confused, never certain who is winning. The refrain obsesses over dying with the wrong expression.","opus":"At what he calls the finest celebration of his life, the narrator asks a god for indulgence and wine while being cautioned never to celebrate a victory too soon. Faith No More thread unease beneath the revelry, introducing a shadowy ally as everything begins to spin, his fear of an undignified death hardening into a refrain.","sonnet":"A surreal party where sensation, surrender, and mortality blur together. Everything spins; nothing is certain; the only visible god is one who might give more if given everything. The one fixed wish is to not die wearing a foolish expression — dignity amid the vertigo.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~last-cup-of-sorrow":{"haiku":"Life erodes all things and everyone ages; the song urges drinking sorrow as a final bitter gulp before moving forward and surprising oneself with resilience.","opus":"A world-weary toast urging someone to finish off their grief in one go rather than nurse it, likening the buried ache to something writhing deep in the body. The deeper read is an invitation to break a cycle of self-pity by confronting what hurts most, since nothing shifts until you choose to end it.","sonnet":"A toast to finishing with pain — the song tells someone to drink down the last of their sorrow, accept that time is running out, stop clinging to a face that no longer fits, and walk away before it all slips through their fingers like sand.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~midlife-crisis":{"haiku":"A dependency crisis: the speaker's worth is conditional, their heart incomplete without another person, and perfectionism masks need and bleeding.","opus":"Mike Patton's acid portrait of self-absorbed, thirty-something narcissism, reportedly aimed at a certain pop star, dissecting a vanity that needs an audience to exist. The perfectionism it mocks is really a skinned knee dressed as superiority.","sonnet":"A song about a suffocating, codependent relationship — the narrator is being slowly wrung out by a partner whose perfection doubles as a weapon, and the recurring image of a menstruating heart suggests the relationship feeds only one of them.","src":"haiku","fable":"Patton reportedly built this as a portrait of Madonna, but the venom fits any celebrity ego that mistakes hunger for a self, a person at the age when fame should have made them permanent yet who can only pour out and grab in the same breath. Every image shrinks the target into something soft and disposable — a prized mind kept like a scolded pet, a skull soft as a salad head, selfhood squeezed the way you'd twist a cloth left soaking — so the swagger of stardom always sits one motion from the throat. The refrain sharpens this into a clinical cruelty dressed as praise: you are flawless, the singer allows, yet incomplete without him, your organ pumping for an audience rather than for any living person beside you. The cleverest structural move is the refusal to climb; the verses invent fresh contempt while the diagnosis of the title only recurs, flat and unbothered, until the closing pass lays the throttling taunt of the opening verse directly beneath the flattery so both voices ring together at once. That superimposition is the whole read: tormentor and adored object fuse into one sound, and the crisis belongs less to the star than to the sour dependence of anyone who needs a hollow idol to feel real.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"flattery and throttling delivered as a single verdict on hollow stardom","built":"the contempt verse and the praise refrain superimposed at the close so both ring at once"},
"faith-no-more~ricochet":{"haiku":"Unspoken pain rebounds unexpectedly to hurt the person who caused it; laughter at others' suffering becomes grim when directed inward. Mockery and distance eventually ricochet back.","opus":"A bitter address to a cruel person whose malice always rebounds on themselves, promising that whatever harm they deal will eventually land back on them. The rebounding shot is karma, the speaker coldly confident they will end up with nothing and no one left laughing on their side.","sonnet":"A bitter post-breakup piece where the narrator has been hollowed out by absence and runs twice as fast just to stay in place, while predicting that the person who hurt them will one day come up empty — and then it'll be funny, then hilarious.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~ricochet-2016-remaster":{"haiku":"Drunk exhaustion and relentless motion trap the speaker in place; dark humor shields against the inevitable ricochet that always strikes the unsuspecting.","opus":"Faith No More on cruelty disguised as comedy — the old joke that a thing stays funny only until someone gets injured, at which point it turns funnier still. The stance is a bitter promise that karma circles back on a smug tormentor who scrambles just to stay in place and finds nothing.","sonnet":"A speaker numbed and thickened by time apart from someone finds the cruelty darkly funny — \"it's always funny until someone gets hurt and then it's just hilarious\" — waiting for the ricochet that will finally hit the other person the way it has hit them.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~star-a-d":{"haiku":"Death transforms into myth: dying is mundane and factual, but death becomes legend, binding all into eternal sameness and collective becoming.","opus":"Faith No More skewer the machinery of fame, addressing a star of the anno domini who trades freedom for dreaming the tomorrow that's understood as a joke spread worldwide. The band's arch, funk-inflected sneer sharpens the punchline: dying is dry as historical fact, and in death the celebrity becomes something worse, a legend, preserved forever as the manufactured icon the culture keeps alive.","sonnet":"A brief, elliptical meditation on celebrity and death suggests that dying does not end a person but transforms them into something more burdensome — a legend that outlasts the self. The irony is bleak: freedom requires oblivion, but the world converts the dead into permanence against their will.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~take-this-bottle":{"haiku":"Someone asks their partner to leave, taking a bottle as a symbolic gesture, so they can face the pain they have caused. They believe forgiveness and separation are necessary.","opus":"A guilt-stricken alcoholic tells his partner and child to leave him for their own good, wishing he could absorb the hurt he has inflicted. Faith No More's ballad frames the plea through a fading dream of a better home and a man who has abandoned any hope of changing, deferring the love he can't give to some afterlife.","sonnet":"A man broken by his own failures sends away those he has hurt rather than keep dragging them through his damage. Holding onto hope of improvement has exhausted everyone; the bottle passed along is an act of honesty — take it and leave, let the pain land where it belongs.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~the-cowboy-song":{"opus":"An early, grim piece about a suicidal climb, the ride up to the rooftop's brink and the last move out into the glare below. It dwells on teenage isolation and envy souring into the urge to plunge and drag everything down with you.","src":"opus"},
"faith-no-more~the-gentle-art-of-making-enemies":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts a person who was betrayed and now fights back, using hostile exchanges and degradation as both revenge and a measure of feeling fully alive.","opus":"Faith No More turn a mock-birthday jeer into a study of calculated cruelty, coaching the listener to master hostility before someone else masters them first. Mike Patton spits scorn over extinguished candles, hats worn on command, and dances forced under pressure. Bragging of being the finest bedmate and threatening to withhold warmth, the narrator feels most alive through provocation.","sonnet":"A confrontation that refuses to be polite: history repeats its patterns, excuses run out, and the subject is finally forced to sit in the discomfort they have long avoided. The narrator feels electrified by the conflict itself, wielding antagonism as a deliberate craft rather than a failure of diplomacy.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~the-last-to-know":{"haiku":"Something valuable exists but blooms rarely and is hard to find; the narrator is last to know but willing to wait. Communication fails and patience tests them.","opus":"The narrator toys with a riddle about something ever-present, effortless, and quietly comforting, then turns it inward to insist on his own singular self. Faith No More let the reach for real connection dead-end in a busy signal and a recorded voice, the intimacy always just out of grasp.","sonnet":"Something exists — a place, a feeling, a secret — that lasts beyond a lifetime and requires the least effort to sustain. The narrator knows its location but cannot share it; the circuit is always busy, the wait ongoing, identity asserted in the space between knowing and telling.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~the-perfect-crime":{"haiku":"Varied vignettes of casual violence and harm—a girl burns her house, a man hits dogs and feels good, a boy steps in front of a train—presented as accidents hiding intentional cruelty.","opus":"Faith No More, the genre-hopping San Francisco alt-metal band, catalogue everyday human viciousness with theatrical, twitchy menace. Small acts of casual cruelty are each shrugged off as mishaps, while vengeance and murderous impulse hum beneath a grinning, agreeable surface. The result is a sardonic study of the malice people hide in plain sight.","sonnet":"Everyday violence rendered as a series of cool, clinical vignettes — a girl who burns things and pretends to sleep, a man who hits dogs and feels better, a boy who steps in front of a train. Each is framed as an accident, a thing no one saw, and the bitterness underneath is the real subject: revenge that nobody acknowledges, shrinking until you vanish, dying and having a nice day.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~the-real-thing":{"haiku":"Transcendence emerges through sensory intensity and surrender; ecstasy dissolves the boundaries between self and experience.","opus":"Mike Patton chasing the ecstasy of a transcendent, all-consuming feeling that reads at once as love and addiction, divine and ruinous. It's about the peak experience you'd risk everything to hold, even knowing it's killing you by degrees.","sonnet":"An ecstatic encounter with something utterly genuine — an experience so overwhelming it simultaneously nourishes and destroys — is pursued and savoured even knowing it will leave the seeker writhing on the floor.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~ugly-in-the-morning":{"haiku":"Waking brings self-disgust; the narrator critiques their own physicality and behavior after a night of indulgence and self-inflicted damage.","opus":"A hungover, self-loathing morning-after portrait: the narrator regrets waking, feels his gut lurch, and sees himself in a ravenous pig that never skips a feeding. The refrain, warding off any onlooker, blends bodily and moral disgust after yet another night he inflicted on himself.","sonnet":"A post-binge morning reckoning — waking up feeling repulsive, the hangover and self-disgust interlocking, the narrator observing their own deterioration with dark wit and no illusion.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~war-pigs":{"haiku":"Politicians and generals hide safely while orchestrating war for profit, treating soldiers as pawns until judgment day strips their power.","opus":"Faith No More take Black Sabbath's antiwar tirade and run it through their own genre-splicing funk-metal lens. The nightmare vision holds: commanders scheming mass death like dark sorcerers while cowardly politicians send the poor to die as expendable game pieces. On the day of reckoning the powerful grovel for forgiveness as the devil gleefully welcomes them.","sonnet":"Generals and politicians who engineer slaughter from a distance while the poor die on the battlefield face a divine reckoning. The machinery of war grinds on until judgment arrives, stripping the powerful of everything — reduced at last to crawling and begging for mercy.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~we-care-a-lot-slash-version":{"haiku":"Performative moral concern disguises apathy and cynicism as celebrities mouth solidarity while perpetuating systemic violence.","opus":"A deadpan satire of performative compassion, reeling off disasters, charities, armies, cartoons and consumer trinkets as equal objects of a caring that's really smug self-congratulation. The dirty-job refrain exposes an era's habit of feeling virtuous about the world's problems while doing nothing.","sonnet":"A deadpan, mock-sincere catalogue of every crisis and cultural object the band claims to care about — disasters, Rock Hudson, Garbage Pail Kids, Transformers, the LAPD, the wars — delivered as ironic charity posturing, because it's a dirty job but someone's gotta do it.","src":"haiku"},
"faith-no-more~what-a-day":{"haiku":"A surreal encounter with unsettling physical fragments (hair, teeth) combined with regret and the feeling of having missed or failed to notice something crucial.","opus":"Faith No More assemble a lurching collage of disjointed images, mail, a letterhead, human hair, teeth, alongside a chorus of accusing voices that should have killed, learned, or noticed something before. The refrain's grim challenge, to look the day in the face and hold one's vomit, captures the band's queasy, confrontational absurdism, revulsion and menace fused into fractured, unsettling snapshots.","sonnet":"Fragments of domestic and bodily unease accumulate without narrative cohesion — a letter, a strand of hair, a wet sneeze — while voices replay missed recognitions and belated warnings. Disgust sits just beneath a forced composure. The day demands one look at something intolerable straight on and hold the revulsion in.","src":"haiku"},
"fake-eyes~saccharine-scream":{"opus":"Post-hardcore addressed to a younger self or successor, urged to endure the entire trial and not abandon it prematurely. There's a predatory undercurrent, a presence vowing to come back and claim you even as it destroys you, tenderness spiked with menace.","src":"opus"},
"fakemink~easter-pink":{"opus":"A luxe, hedonistic rap flex over designer labels, lean and easy money, half legit and half not. The one crack of doubt is admitting he can't say why the sex and the cash feel so good, a nod at the emptiness beneath the drip.","src":"opus"},
"fakemink~fidelio":{"haiku":"School of hard knocks teaches survival and transcendence; the hero becomes villain through relentless pursuit.","opus":"A hedonistic underdog flex threaded with Kubrick's masked-orgy imagery, charting the rise from spurned schoolkid to womanizer, casting himself as a hero who suspects he's the villain, too far gone to double back.","sonnet":"A young hustler navigates status, rejection, and ambition — \"used to get rejected in school, now I get any ho\" — using Eyes Wide Shut's Fidelio as a password into a world of money and night moves, and dismissing anyone who wastes time on music or still wears Gap.","src":"haiku"},
"fall-out-boy~centuries":{"haiku":"Obsessive legacy-seeking corrupts the present; the speaker trades authentic life for immortal memory, poisoning those near them with their hunger for permanence.","opus":"A defiant grab at immortality through legend, insisting a single error or a single track will keep the singer remembered for ages. It welds ambition to the scars of a damaged youth, set on making the entire planet learn his name.","sonnet":"An obsessive hunger for legacy: the speaker insists they will be remembered for centuries on the strength of a single decisive act, casting themselves as the opposite of amnesia — and the image of a cherry blossom, pretty and gone too soon, anchors the fear that beauty and power are equally fleeting.","src":"haiku"},
"fall-out-boy~immortals":{"haiku":"The promise of eternal connection proves illusory; the speaker grasps at permanence while time erodes identity and leaves only the dream of endurance.","opus":"Written for Big Hero 6, a defiant embrace of being flawed and finite, choosing to burn bright together rather than live safe. The immortality is the shared moment, not the length of it.","sonnet":"Two people reach for a kind of shared immortality through their bond — pulled together by faith tested repeatedly — knowing the dream is finite but choosing to live in it fully, blacking out the world and holding on for as long as they have.","src":"haiku"},
"fall-out-boy~novocaine":{"haiku":"Numbness spreads as love curdles into toxicity, and cultural malaise desensitizes the population to suffering.","opus":"A snarling swipe at a culture that numbs and consumes love until nothing is felt, the singer casting himself as its worst nightmare. It is about going numb on purpose after being drained by fans and vultures alike.","sonnet":"Love has been replaced with numbness — filled up with novocaine by forces that hollowed out something real and left the speaker unable to feel. Against a backdrop of cultural vultures and erased authenticity, the speaker refuses to perform emotion they no longer access.","src":"haiku"},
"fall-out-boy~young-and-menace":{"haiku":"Reckless youth burns through time at unsustainable speed, driven by a compulsion to leave a mark that matters before the inevitable collapse.","opus":"Patrick Stump unraveling under fame and mental strain, the glitchy chaos mirroring a mind coming apart. The Britney nod frames it as compulsive self-destruction, a message shot back to whoever he used to be.","sonnet":"A chaotic self-portrait of someone who has lived so fast and so recklessly that memory and identity have fragmented beyond recovery, the song itself existing as a provocation to force reflection on what was lost. The speaker cycles between manic energy and hollow disorientation, framing the breakdown as both badge and warning.","src":"haiku"},
"fallen-angel~ice":{"haiku":"Unwanted birth freezes the self into neglect and hatred; ice accumulates inward as isolation becomes unbearable.","opus":"A harrowing black-metal cry from someone who felt unwanted from birth, treated as a mistake and frozen out of any world of love until existence itself is ignored. It ends in a bleak drift toward death as the only release from endless suffering.","sonnet":"A child raised in a household of screaming, hatred and neglect describes feeling frozen out from the world of love — \"ice grows inside me\" — until the suffering drives him toward death, and finally to a peaceful disintegration into light.","src":"haiku"},
"falling-in-reverse~popular-monster":{"haiku":"Mental illness and self-sabotage define the speaker as monster; diagnosis offers no relief, only the brutal clarity that they destroy everything.","opus":"An unflinching account of depression, trauma and rage, pushing back against everyone insisting it's just a phase while only wanting to feel alright. The singer brands himself a celebrated wreck, admired yet self-destroying, inching toward the end and scared.","sonnet":"A raw, confessional account of depression, trauma, self-contradiction, and public persona: the speaker battles demons simultaneously, questions whether their pain is post-traumatic stress or suppressed rage, describes crashing a car just to feel something, and concludes by naming themselves a \"popular monster\" — known, broken, and feared.","src":"haiku"},
"fallujah~radiant-ascension":{"haiku":"Plans enacted despite broken promises; the speaker rises distorted by waves of contradiction.","opus":"The atmospheric death-metal band on transcendence through death and rebirth, shedding origins to re-emerge from the blackness. The mysticism frames a soul called across an endless night, refusing to wither in apathy, ascending toward some clairvoyant unknown.","sonnet":"A death and rebirth narrative — torn from light, plunged into blackness, then re-emerging to spread wings and return transformed. The ghost inside a memory provides the key to what lies beyond; the infinite connection of irrational belief is offered not as delusion but as the specific energy that makes ascension possible.","src":"haiku"},
"false-heads~thick-skin":{"haiku":"Repetitive pain and deterioration; attempting resilience against harm wears away at the speaker, leaving them irritated and weakened rather than strengthened.","opus":"A grimy British punk track built on the exhaustion of the same rot recurring, sickness and waiting and the sense that everything ends in waste. The refrain lands as bitter resignation, the toll of watching cycles you can't break grind on and on.","sonnet":"A fragmented portrait of being ground down — the same rot, the same knots, the inevitable waste; the narrator observes cycles of small violence and endurance without resolution, arriving at nothing but exhaustion.","src":"haiku"},
"falset~kingdom":{"opus":"Falset stage the ruin of a fraud's empire, someone who claimed to be a man yet fled, who possessed everything and saw it crumble. Amid American imagery of pride and speed, the song savors a reckoning while confessing its own sorrow, a wish to entomb something living that still closes on an apology. It's about downfall, betrayal, and grudging regret.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a man who talked himself up, had everything, and then abandoned his responsibilities when it counted — the narrator watches his kingdom fall and refuses to let the cowardice go unaddressed, even while admitting some sorrow for what was lost.","src":"sonnet"},
"fangbanger~artificial-flowers":{"haiku":"Unnamed dread pursues relentlessly; acceptance and breathing become survival against an unstoppable, formless threat.","opus":"A track that renders fear as a stalking, bloodthirsty creature trailing the singer and sapping his strength. Rather than run, he decides to accept it as family, insisting there is nothing shameful about being scared. The steadying instruction to shut his eyes and inhale becomes the coping ritual that keeps him upright.","sonnet":"Something relentless and internal pursues the speaker — an anxiety or darkness that dogs waking and sleeping alike, threatening to pull the knees out from under. Rather than flee it, the answer offered is to breathe through it, embrace the fear as kin, and keep moving.","src":"haiku"},
"fangclub~all-fall-down":{"haiku":"Everyone falls when confronted with harsh reality and personal darkness. The commonality of collapse unites all people under inevitable downfall.","opus":"A raw grunge-punk song about scraping through in a bleak world, the Irish trio's fuzzed-out, loud-quiet aggression building to a fatalistic hook. The narrator refuses to sell himself out or be broken down, taking dark comfort in the great leveler that unites everyone: no matter who you are, eventually you go down.","sonnet":"Predators and posturing stripped down to their shared outcome — sharks, false promises, broken bones, and the whole crowd of them collapsing in the end. The speaker survives on something raw and loud while everyone else who sold illusions or came circling eventually hits the ground, and the one thing they all have in common is the fall.","src":"haiku"},
"fat-dog~cancel-me-i-m-tired":{"haiku":"Exhaustion culminates in voluntary erasure as the speaker demands cancellation. Tiredness becomes reason for disappearance.","opus":"A provocative, exhausted shrug at cancel culture, daring detractors to do their worst while mocking outrage, AI-written hits and music made for ad breaks. Under the blasphemous jokes and non sequiturs is plain burnout, a weariness that half-means its wish to just be gone.","sonnet":"Exhausted provocation: invite the cancellers to do their worst, the narrator is a mutt too thick and tired to care — Jesus turned twelve pints into thunder, AI can write the hit, the boat comes in from east to west calling itself Life — the whole song is a man too tired to perform outrage about the performance of outrage.","src":"haiku"},
"fat-dog~pray-to-that":{"haiku":"Running through chaos with depleted mercy, invoking fatalism and resignation while aging in nightly repetition.","opus":"A manic anthem of nihilistic devotion, praying to the circus in one's own head and to just waiting to die, a young frontman half-joking about mercy and returns, the bravado thin over a wait-to-die shrug.","sonnet":"A chaotic, slightly delirious song about running through something relentlessly — the circus in the mind, the race, a woman who misjudges his age; the narrator prays to the absurdity of his own momentum and keeps coming back even when he swears he won't.","src":"haiku","fable":"A mock liturgy where every named disorder gets the same flat blessing, so devotion turns into a reflex pointed at nothing and everything alike. The band names itself mid-chant and totes up its remaining fucks, which drains the sacramental vocabulary of any dread and leaves it as rave-chant scaffolding. The teeth show in the taunting return-refrain, hammered past exhaustion with a smirking certainty the other party already knew the outcome. What sounds like swagger is really a compulsion loop, a man orbiting his ugliest habit and christening it a god.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"devotion flattened to a reflex aimed at nothing and everything alike","built":"sacramental vocabulary drained of dread by the band naming itself mid-chant and tallying its remaining fucks"},
"fat-dog~running":{"haiku":"Violence and chaos swirl through a narrated escape; the speaker flees through drugs and paranoia toward an uncertain refuge.","opus":"A frantic, drug-hazed flight from violence and from the grey static in the singer's own head, wanting to be woken only once the shooting starts so he can watch from a safe remove. Having played the wrong role at the wrong moment, he keeps running from a cartel-tinged fever dream and from himself.","sonnet":"A chaotic, drug-addled sprint away from one's own dark thoughts — dodging violence, watching from a distance, losing track of God and Lucy and the holy ground — a stream-of-consciousness run from the grey noise inside the narrator's skull.","src":"haiku"},
"fat-dog~vigilante":{"haiku":"Witnessing primordial violence and contemporary brutality, the speaker positions themselves as both observer and instrument of retribution. Vigilante ethics emerge from the breakdown of law and the spectacle of bodies breaking.","opus":"The London post-punk collective lurches from a cosmic, primordial monologue about light and the frailty of the body into gleeful suburban menace. The joke and the point is the collapse of scale, eternity and a snooker ball in a sock in the same breath, ancient dread played for absurd swagger.","sonnet":"A cosmically positioned narrator spans all of evolutionary time — watching cells tear apart, beasts crawl onto beaches, the first human death — before crashing into the mundane present: granny tights on his head, a snooker ball in a sock, fleeing cops as a self-appointed suburban vigilante whose scope is absurdly narrow against the backdrop of eternity.","src":"haiku"},
"fatal-fe~we-are-the-one":{"haiku":"A collective declaration strips fear away and asserts unity against the weight of the atmosphere — minimal, incantatory, positioning a shared identity as the only shield needed.","opus":"Less a song than an incantation, a near-mantra of cosmic oneness and courage, repeated until individual dread dissolves into a single shared self unafraid of the void.","sonnet":"A minimal invocation of collective identity and fearlessness — stars descending, fear of the atmosphere dissolved. The lyric is near-ambient in its sparseness, more chant than narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"fatboy-slim~right-here-right-now":{"haiku":"Immediacy and presence obsessively asserted; the moment is all that exists, punctuated by radio-show banter about loving the artist.","opus":"Norman Cook's big-beat anthem is pure present-tense euphoria, a track that means nothing verbally and everything as a rush, sampling radio chatter to celebrate the ecstasy of the immediate dancefloor moment.","sonnet":"Almost entirely a chant of the phrase 'right here, right now' with a brief radio call-in snippet praising Fatboy Slim — there's no discernible lyrical theme beyond the surface mantra of presence in the moment.","src":"haiku"},
"fate-destroyed~47":{"opus":"An electronic-metal piece about surviving addiction, grief and repeating days by finding love and rescue in another person. The chaos that once wrecked him now holds him up, a devotion built on learning to love his demons instead of fleeing them.","src":"opus"},
"fate-destroyed~crave":{"haiku":"Lustful obsession and physical desire consume the speaker; they drink in sensation and sweat, unable to resist the other's touch.","opus":"Fate Destroyed render desire as consumption, a lover drunk on another's skin and sin, dissolving into a craving for misery. The imagery smears ash and sweat across the body, hands at the throat, no time for regret, a lust so total it dares the question of dying for it. The metalcore intensity matches the theme, appetite pushed past pleasure into something devouring and self-erasing.","sonnet":"Desire at its most destructive — a pull toward another person that feels like dissolution, ash on skin, lust spread like something toxic. The speaker is fully consumed, the boundary between ecstasy and ruin dissolved, willing to fall over the edge for one more taste.","src":"haiku"},
"father-of-peace~enemy":{"haiku":"Former intimacy twisted into deep antipathy, unable to forget someone now internalized as personal enemy.","opus":"An indie-pop reckoning with a person who wounded the narrator yet remains inseparable from him, an enemy who is part of himself. The tension is wanting them gone while still dreaming of reunion, unable to forget or fully let go.","sonnet":"Someone who did real damage has become inextricably part of the narrator — \"enemy, you're a part of me\" — and yet the dreams of reunion are the most annoying thing imaginable. The wish is simple: forget me, leave town, stay sad; the paradox is that forgetting is impossible precisely because the enemy is already inside.","src":"haiku"},
"favst~dddd":{"opus":"Polish rap about a creator torn between home and the pull of work, asking a partner's patience while he stays out chasing inspiration to build the stable home they both want. The wandering is framed as the very thing that will earn the belonging.","src":"opus"},
"favst~francuskie-filmy":{"opus":"Recurring anxious dreams of watching a lover with someone else and time frozen still, a restless plea to just drive faster and further into escape while the calendar lies about the days passing.","sonnet":"A Polish song about exhausting, looping anxiety dreams in which the narrator watches his ex-partner with someone else — dreams that replay like a bad song on repeat, while the calendar insists time is moving even though nothing feels different. The rap verse spirals into desire and infatuation still entangled with her, his imagination running hot with images of her body while he knows on some level he is still dreaming.","src":"sonnet"},
"favst~mood":{"haiku":"Night drives fuel ambition and recklessness, seeking escape from pain through speed and disconnection, yet unable to let go of a particular someone despite knowing they're not enough.","opus":"Polish melodic rap of cold streets and inner fire, night drives and weed, a girl who chooses him over others. Under the synthwave-tinged romance runs the urge to leave something behind and outrun a pain that keeps circling back.","sonnet":"A late-night drive through cold city streets becomes a frame for intoxicated intimacy: the speaker steers aimlessly while smoke drifts through the sunroof and a woman who prefers him over everyone else keeps him company in the dark, the city's pain real but not yet final while they share a mood too wordless to name.","src":"haiku"},
"fc-kahuna~hayling":{"haiku":"A mantra urging listeners to suppress anxiety and trauma by accepting the present moment. The song preaches mindful distancing from fear.","opus":"A gentle electronic reverie built on a single consoling refrain, urging the listener to stop dwelling on everything they fear and simply be glad to be here. The repetition works like a mantra, softening anxiety into presence over a warm, wistful groove. FC Kahuna were a British electronic duo, and this downtempo track became a beloved late-night comedown anthem, its featured vocal turning simple reassurance into something quietly transcendent.","sonnet":"Pure present-tense reassurance reduced to a single instruction repeated until it becomes a kind of mantra — stop rehearsing fear, stop anticipating disaster, just be glad to be here. The simplicity is the point: no argument, no narrative, just the same gentle imperative offered again and again as though repetition alone can make it land.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-and-loathing-in-las-vegas~power-of-life-and-death":{"haiku":"The speaker urges personal responsibility and self-determination, arguing that success and failure depend entirely on oneself, not luck or others. They reject victimhood and blame-shifting as paths to satisfaction.","opus":"A frantic Japanese metalcore pep talk about owning your outcomes. The vocalist argues that thriving or floundering is a matter of personal choice rather than luck, mocks anyone who pins their failures on other people, and insists everything hinges on your own commitment to yourself.","sonnet":"An aggressive motivational song about personal accountability — it hammers the point that blaming others or external circumstances is stupid and cowardly, and that success or failure is entirely self-determined, delivered with escalating intensity.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-and-loathing-in-las-vegas~tear-down":{"opus":"The Japanese electronicore band turning stage-fright and the dread of failure into a manifesto for risk. The message is that playing it safe is a slow poison and the real self only surfaces once you dismantle the fear and dare to fail out loud.","src":"opus"},
"fear-factory~body-hammer":{"haiku":"The singer becomes a tool of severe impact—a hammer swinging to break and create anew. Blood is spilled, the dead counted; they pound and drive through a poisoned sky to smash away the old world.","opus":"Fear Factory, industrial-metal pioneers, deliver a mechanized apocalypse in which the narrator recasts himself as a demolition tool under a poisoned, dying sky. He tallies the dead and sets about flattening the ruined world to build something new, the chorus a percussive litany of destruction married to their machine-like riffing.","sonnet":"Industrial-age self-conception as a weapon of change — a speaker who sees a dying world poisoned and blood-spilled, and who reframes himself not as a victim of dysfunction but as the tool that will break it down and build something new. The hammer metaphor is literal in its violence and sincere in its purpose: cause and effect, pound and drive, until the old structure gives way.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~christploitation":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects organized religion as false exploitation where faith serves no purpose and god is a meaningless lie.","opus":"Singer Burton Bell's atheist takedown of organized religion as a con, treating faith as an open wound and a deity powerless to rescue anyone. The song seethes at unanswered prayers and belief drained of any real meaning, indicting the whole enterprise as manipulation dressed as salvation.","sonnet":"A blunt rejection of commercial Christianity — faith is a bleeding wound, God is a lie, prayers fall on deaf ears, and organized religion exploits belief rather than delivering any salvation.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~controlled-demolition":{"haiku":"A catastrophic building collapse is presented as a conspiracy: sacrifice of innocents for control and political change through engineered destruction.","opus":"Fear Factory channel their dystopian, machine-driven aggression into a September 11 conspiracy narrative, framing the towers' collapse as an engineered inside job and human sacrifice sold as the cost of progress. As victims perish under a burning sky, the song insists fate lay in the hands of scheming powers, hammering on themes of domination, manipulation, and guilt that can never be cleansed.","sonnet":"A conspiracy-framed indictment of state-sanctioned mass murder — buildings as sacrificial altars, bodies as the price of geopolitical dominance, and false-flag operations as the architecture of control. The blood stain is presented as permanent and deliberate, soaking into the street so that no official narrative can wash it clean.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~crash-test-exclusive-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Animals suffer in laboratory testing and medical experimentation, dying and crying while society justifies the cruelty as politically necessary.","sonnet":"An indictment of animal testing frames laboratory subjects as sentient beings capable of dreaming and suffering, whose deaths are treated as politically acceptable collateral. The fury is directed at the moral gymnastics that allow society to claim ethical correctness while authorizing the destruction of lives that cannot consent.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~demanufacture":{"haiku":"A desensitized, abused victim tastes revenge; their gun becomes an angel of mercy as they fire relentlessly with no regrets or respect left to offer.","opus":"Fear Factory's industrial metal voices a man hollowed out and enraged by official deceit, obsessed with retribution and picturing his weapon as a twisted deliverance. Broken past recognition and battling madness, he casts himself as a defiant irritant lodged in the machinery of the system, stripped of both remorse and deference.","sonnet":"A person desensitized and broken by government lies and social violence reaches the point of total breakdown — the accumulated abuse has stripped away all regret and respect, and the narrator, with a gun as his only remaining language, imagines turning that violence back outward.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~designing-the-enemy":{"haiku":"A creator deliberately molds someone into an instrument of hate and destruction, designed purely for violence and killing.","opus":"A creator coldly addresses the thing it built, claiming ownership of every impulse it installed, including all the hatred, and awaiting the moment its manufactured killer turns lethal. Fear Factory, pioneers of industrial-tinged death metal, render this dehumanizing act of engineering with clipped, mechanized menace.","sonnet":"A creator — god, engineer, or oppressor — addresses something it built entirely, having poured its own hatred and longing into the design. The enemy was not discovered but manufactured, shaped to embody every quality the maker despised. The conflict that follows is therefore a mirror: pure, self-authored, and inescapable.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~digimortal":{"haiku":"Existential dread and technological dehumanisation bring the narrator closer to grave and madness; they witness their own degeneration without escape.","opus":"Machine-age dread about man surrendering his soul to technology and degenerating into something disposable, one step from the grave. The refusal to hand over his god is the last stand against becoming carrion.","sonnet":"A figure walking into psychological and moral darkness refuses to surrender their sense of self to outside forces, even as degeneration closes in and each step brings them closer to a fate they recognize as inevitable but will not accelerate by giving up.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~dog-day-sunrise":{"haiku":"Labor and industrial grinding punctuate daily repetition; a valentine figure offers the only emotional anchor in mechanical drudgery.","opus":"The industrial-metal band cover Head of David, reshaping the original into a chant about brutal underground labor where workers only learn their fate once it is too late. A single tender image of a sweetheart flickers through the mechanical monotony, a scrap of humanity buried inside relentless, grinding toil.","sonnet":"Labour underground, repetition without exit, hollow streets dragged through each sunrise — the song maps exhausted routine as a trap with no visible seam to cut. Valentine's shining eyes offer the only warmth in the imagery, a private brightness against the grinding public grimness of the daily cycle.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~edgecrusher":{"haiku":"A violent rebel positions himself as a psychopath and terrorist who destroys order and government, celebrating hatred and the crushing of civilized structures through raw force.","opus":"An industrial-metal power fantasy about a dehumanized figure remade into an unstoppable weapon bent on toppling authority. The driving idea is anti-establishment fury in a corrupted world stripped of anything worth revering, applying relentless pressure until the whole oppressive structure buckles and gives way.","sonnet":"A portrait of a violent, anti-authoritarian loner — conceived in chaos, a self-described psychopathic non-conformist terrorist who exists outside society's structures and has dedicated himself to collapsing them through brute force.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~fear-campaign":{"haiku":"Fear is weaponized by institutions and media to manipulate, paralyze, and enforce conformity, making fear itself the false god people worship.","opus":"A polemic on fear as a tool of social control, listing the fears of war, loss, enemies and even God as manufactured weapons of conformity used to intimidate and enslave; the resolution is to name fear itself as the enemy and refuse to make it your god.","sonnet":"Fear as the primary instrument of social control — governments and institutions manufacture it to make people compliant, and the song is a catalogue of every fear deployed to keep the population weak and obedient.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~final-exit":{"haiku":"Suicide as self-deliverance; the speaker contemplates a final breath and escape from a life rendered worthless by an indifferent world. Goodbye whispers repeat as the knife of certainty cuts through.","opus":"A cold-eyed reckoning with assisted death and the right to end one's own suffering, borrowing its title from the euthanasia handbook. The mechanized delivery holds it at arm's length, contemplation rather than sermon on the last breath.","sonnet":"Drawn from the Derek Humphry assisted-suicide manual, the song contemplates self-deliverance as a deliberate choice — not glamorizing it but rendering it as a slow, meditative farewell, the pain acknowledged, the decision personal and irreversible.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~flashpoint":{"haiku":"Self-destruction through lies accumulating like gasoline. Dishonesty burns from within, and one final spark of deception ignites total personal annihilation.","opus":"Fear Factory, built on Burton Bell's clean-versus-guttural contrast, stage a confession of betrayal, a lie told to someone's face that leaves the narrator smoldering from within. His instincts collapse and his errors trap him as deceit soaks through him like fuel, a final act of falsehood set to consume him. Guilt is imagined as actual burning, a fire that both punishes and, strangely, releases.","sonnet":"Dishonesty becomes self-immolation — lies absorb into the body like fuel, and one final spark of duplicity will be fatal. The narrator burns not from outside punishment but from internal combustion, cycling through damnation, purging, and a strange release that can only come through total destruction of the false self.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~industrial-discipline":{"haiku":"Systemic oppression beats people into submission, erasing their souls and converting them into obedient machines through manipulation and control.","opus":"Fear Factory's signature man-versus-machine theme: the soul ground out of a person until only an obedient mechanism remains, and the refusal to be defined by that control. Dehumanization resisted.","sonnet":"About the erasure of individual soul through systemic oppression — beaten into submission until only a machine remains — and the moment of refusal, declaring the self will not be defined or suppressed any longer.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~linchpin":{"haiku":"Individual consciousness becomes essential to collective function, withdrawal from the system threatens everything as the one holding all pieces together recognizes their indispensability.","opus":"A discarded part declaring its own indispensability, refusing to be taken apart or erased and insisting that man and machine are one inseparable whole.","sonnet":"A linchpin — the essential connecting piece — becomes a metaphor for the speaker's place in a shared machine: they have felt discarded and apart all their life, but insist they cannot be separated from the whole because without them the system fades, and they will never be the end.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~mechanize":{"haiku":"Industrial systems dehumanize and automate minds until compliance becomes inescapable; thoughtless, sedated, and castrated consciousness is the future.","opus":"Burton Bell's signature man-versus-machine dread, humanity dehumanized and sedated into cogs until it becomes the thing it built. The refrain about building something to break it is the trap of engineering your own extinction.","sonnet":"A song about industrial dehumanization — workers are absorbed into a machine they built themselves, stripped of identity, thought, and will, until all lives are condemned to automated servitude with no exit left.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~new-breed":{"haiku":"A controlled generation, born and beaten, controls its own destiny and displays cold immunity to life itself—humanity as machinery.","opus":"Fear Factory deliver a cold, mechanized manifesto voiced by a future generation that claims to be untouchable and in command of its own fate. Read deeper, it is a dystopian warning about industrialized humanity engineered into emotionless, programmed units who accept a bleak, rigid order as the only truth.","sonnet":"A declaration from a new generation of humans defined by immunity to life's warmth and control over their own destiny — born, bred, and beaten into something cold and future-facing, the song reads as either a manifesto of strength or a horror of what people become when all feeling is driven out.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~new-breed-revolutionary-designed-mix":{"haiku":"Immunity to life creates new humanity ruled by cold logic; control supplants feeling.","opus":"Fear Factory's man-against-machine creed in chant form — a dehumanized future race, deadened to life and manufactured by sterile logic yet claiming to steer its own fate. The industrial repetition apes the assembly line churning out the very world it warns of.","sonnet":"A declaration of a new posthuman class that is immune to life's warmth, bred and beaten into control of their own destiny — the mantra \"born, bred, beaten\" cycles as both origin story and war cry.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~oxidizer":{"haiku":"A person burns with hatred and destructive rage, feeling ablaze with the desire to incinerate the entire world and everything in it, unstoppable and incinerating.","opus":"A misanthrope's fantasy of purging incineration, a single spark of rage grown into a will to burn the whole world down. The industrial fury externalizes inner hatred as apocalyptic cleansing.","sonnet":"A single spark of pure hate burns in the narrator's chest with enough force to incinerate everything — the song is a hymn to destruction, the fantasy of setting the entire world on fire.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~pisschrist":{"haiku":"Religious mythology collapses under the weight of human violence and hypocrisy, revealing the divine as manufactured construct built on lies and blood.","opus":"Industrial metal aiming at a man-made god exposed on judgment day, using the crucifixion's brutality to indict faith as a shared lie and to ask, when it all collapses, where the promised savior actually is.","sonnet":"A reckoning with man-made religion and the void it leaves when faith collapses — a god built by humans, a saviour who cannot be found on judgment day, and a world that perpetuates suffering through the lie of divine rescue. The speaker reaches for light with sealed eyes and finds only doubt.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~powershifter":{"haiku":"Escaping authoritarian control and excessive greed, the speaker reclaims their power by questioning authority and forging their own path.","opus":"Fear Factory turn their machine-metal aggression into a revolt against corrupt, greedy authority. The narrator reclaims control of his own path and vows to bring about the oppressor's downfall, tearing himself loose from a dehumanizing system to seize self-determination.","sonnet":"About breaking free from a controlling authority — an employer, system, or abuser who abused their power — and seizing back agency through defiant confrontation rather than compliance.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~replica":{"haiku":"A person conceived violently without love exists as a duplication filled with hatred and pain; they are rape personified, trapped in misery and anonymity.","opus":"An industrial-metal track voiced by someone made through cruelty rather than affection, a copy of a person who has met nothing but contempt since birth. Declaring himself the sum of assault and loathing, he asks to be released from an existence of suffering he was handed and never wanted to endure.","sonnet":"From the perspective of a person conceived through rape who has internalised a life of anonymous hatred and disgrace — they feel like a copy of violence rather than a person, and desperately want to escape that existence.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~self-bias-resistor":{"haiku":"The speaker calls people to resist oppressive systems and conformity through anger and defiance, reclaiming agency from those who seek control.","opus":"A call to break free of the dehumanizing machine, to resist conformity and the human machines of hate that grind you down. Fear Factory's core theme, reclaiming individual will against systems built to break you.","sonnet":"A call to wake up and fight back against the systems that have spent years grinding you down — burn away conformity, resist the human machines of hate, and take back control of your own life.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~your-mistake":{"haiku":"The singer breaks from false authority and refuses compliance, rejecting society's lies. Others hide their problems, enter the system's gate, and it becomes their mistake they cannot undo.","opus":"A blunt, hardcore-inflected declaration of independence and defiance, the narrator insisting he and his crew are here to succeed without being told how to be, refusing to shake the hands of false friends or trust liars, and warning an adversary that entering their gate was too late to undo. The repeated verdict is that it is your mistake. Fear Factory, the industrial-metal band, channel this here through raw, aggressive early-era attitude.","sonnet":"Refusal of a system that lies to its own members — a group that has decided they do not need guidance from people who cannot be trusted, will not shake hands with forces that are not their friends, and names the capitulation of those who entered the gate only to find it closing behind them. The mistake is not theirs; it belongs to everyone who believed the rules were real.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-factory~zero-signal":{"haiku":"Someone experiencing isolation and mental breakdown has lost faith and trust, sinking into numbness and despair with no rescue available.","opus":"Fear Factory render a total collapse of faith as a kind of system failure. The speaker, withdrawn and emotionally deadened, watches his life disintegrate; his prayers go unanswered and dread hardens around him like restraints. The mechanized refrain of lost belief reduces him to something crumbling into nothing, the industrial framework mirroring a self dissolving into static.","sonnet":"A figure collapsing under numbness and disarray prays in silence that yields nothing — the quiet itself becomes a shackle rather than a comfort. Faith and trust dissolve together, and what remains is a body still present but a signal that has gone entirely dark.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-of-water~pray-for-yourself":{"haiku":"Prayer cannot undo systemic violence; the speaker demands self-examination and accountability from the prayerful.","opus":"A raw indictment of hypocritical religiosity that kneels in prayer while a knee presses on a neck, echoing a dying man's last words. It rejects the idea that rights come from God or that the pious have any standing to judge, telling them to save the prayers for themselves.","sonnet":"A furious rejection of religious self-righteousness in the face of systemic violence — the speaker calls out people who pray instead of acting while bodies pile up, insisting that prayer changes nothing, that rights are not God-given guarantees, and that the song explicitly references police killings of Black people.","src":"haiku"},
"fear-until-fury~rize":{"opus":"Metalcore underdog anthem about refusing to accept an inherited low station and clawing to the top on your own terms. The narrator turns spite and a lifelong chip on the shoulder into fuel, uniting brothers who were told they'd never be enough.","src":"opus"},
"feed-me-kill-the-noise~crazy-maybe-feat-anjulie":{"haiku":"Madness becomes diagnosis and self-knowledge; the unheard fragment of sanity persists in obsession.","opus":"A minimal electronic hook built almost entirely from the fear of being misunderstood and the creeping suspicion of one's own madness. The near-empty, looping plea to listen makes isolation itself the subject, tension over resolution.","sonnet":"A fragmented interior monologue in which the speaker suspects she is losing her mind, circling the word \"crazy\" and the feeling of being misunderstood, with the sentence never quite completing — \"I must be...\"","src":"haiku"},
"feed-me~crazy-maybe":{"opus":"Feed Me's original version of the same fragment, a looping cry of not being understood tipping into self-doubt about sanity. It works as mood and menace, the repetition dramatizing a mind circling its own possible madness.","src":"opus"},
"feelingless~feelingless":{"opus":"Melodic death-metal on emotional numbness as a haunted, sleepless void, hounded by sirens and an emptiness that warps everything. The narrator casts himself as a doomed martyr carrying both illumination and dread, suspended between the waking world and dreams, unable to shed the part.","src":"opus"},
"feenixpawl-apek~quicksand-spirix-remix":{"haiku":"Two broken-hearted people sink together into quicksand, drowning in each other yet holding on, unable to let go even as they drown.","opus":"A surrender to romantic engulfment, where two damaged people cling together and the singer welcomes being drowned and covered by a lover as a kind of sweet oblivion. The metaphor stays deliberately ambiguous between rescue and ruin. Feenixpawl and Apek are progressive-house producers, and this remix reworks the original into a euphoric big-room build where the sinking sensation becomes the drop everyone waits for.","sonnet":"A love so consuming it reads as surrender — two people who already feel broken find themselves fused back together, one pulling the other under with every embrace. Drowning is welcomed here, not feared; the chaos of a spinning, pressurised life becomes bearable only because someone familiar keeps pulling them close.","src":"haiku"},
"feint~snake-eyes":{"haiku":"Your lies finally catch up; you're holding losing cards and the game is over.","opus":"A drum-and-bass reckoning with a liar whose luck finally runs out, the buried deceptions surfacing all at once. It's about the collapse of someone who thought they'd gamed every hand.","sonnet":"A gambler who kept deceiving himself and others finally runs out of luck — every move exposes cards he cannot play, and the song is the moment of reckoning when the buried lies surface and the schemes collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"felicity~kill-em-all":{"opus":"A pop-punk takedown of a manipulative disinformation peddler who preys on the gullible and drags others down to feel worthwhile. The stance is righteous contempt, naming the wolf in sheep's clothing as an antithesis who ultimately means nothing.","src":"opus"},
"felix-cartal~love":{"haiku":"Emotional investment becomes obsessive fixation where reciprocation remains perpetually out of reach, the narrator trapped in unrequited cycles of pursuit and abandonment.","opus":"The anxious limbo of an unrequited crush, over-analyzing every message and playing it cool while wondering why, when everything about them makes it easy, he still can't make them love him back.","sonnet":"A woman can't get a man off her mind even though the attachment is making her pathetic — dissecting every conversation, playing cool by the pool — and ultimately demands he either commit his love or let her go before another cruel summer wastes her.","src":"haiku"},
"felix-cartal~popular-music-intro":{"opus":"A pure dance-floor directive with no story to tell, a string of movement commands repeated as a hypnotic call to lose yourself on the floor.","src":"opus"},
"felucia~come-back-down":{"opus":"A euphoric declaration of ambition and survival, someone who fought up from nothing refusing to be brought back down to earth, insisting they're still alive and only just beginning, hearing their own name called out.","sonnet":"A triumphant declaration of unstoppable momentum — the narrator has risen so far and fought so hard that they refuse to descend back to where they came from, insisting they are very much alive and have no intention of returning to earth.","src":"sonnet"},
"felucia~crossfire":{"haiku":"A relentless antagonist takes pleasure in dominating and dismantling their opponent, deriving energy from the confrontation and declaring themselves an impenetrable wall.","opus":"A predator's taunt to a doomed opponent, savoring their fear and inevitable defeat, casting the narrator as an unbreakable wall and a will to dominate that thrives in the middle of the fight.","sonnet":"A confrontational, one-sided power struggle in which the speaker dominates an opponent completely — anticipating every move, feeding off their fear, and thriving in conflict, declaring themselves an impenetrable wall and an unrelenting force of destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"fera~999":{"haiku":"Obsession hijacks rational thought, trapping the speaker in a loop of uncertainty and paranoia about unreturned affection and unmet need.","opus":"The paralysis of a crush too intense to act on, dialing an emergency number for a feeling that won't leave the brain. Behind the flirtation is a fear of being truly seen and a plea to be saved from her own guardedness.","sonnet":"About a crush so consuming it feels like an emergency — the narrator cannot get this person out of their head, cannot act on their feelings, and is oscillating between thinking they are crazy and hoping the other person knows and is just testing them. The 999 (emergency number) captures the panic of falling for someone and not knowing if it is returned.","src":"haiku"},
"fern~hyperreal":{"haiku":"Self-warfare and burning transformation; undecided resistance meeting violent internal conflict.","opus":"Metalcore borrowing Baudrillard's hyperreality, a world bleached to monochrome where each person is only a duplicate of the self and exhaustion is everywhere. It captures dissociation and burnout, sleepwalkers drip-fed through colorless streets while something quietly eats the soul.","sonnet":"A monochrome haze spreading like disease across colourless streets, everyone a copy of everyone else, drip-fed sleepwalkers kept upright by omnipresent fatigue. The hyperreal is the condition: the veil turning black, a war started on yourself, the soil burning through skin — reality so mediated it loops back to feeling nothing while feeling everything.","src":"haiku"},
"fever-333~bite-back":{"haiku":"Racial violence and injustice provoke the speaker to demand resistance and retaliation; marginalized bodies reject forgiveness and claim power through collective action.","opus":"Protest rap-metal against racist policing and the murder of Black people, and the whitewashed Christianity used to keep the oppressed docile. The stance is refusal of forgiveness-on-demand and a demand to live free, framing retaliation as long-overdue survival.","sonnet":"A direct response to police killings of Black people — the song traces how systemic violence, white religious complicity, and erasure from history create a breaking point, culminating in a call for collective self-defense and revolt.","src":"haiku"},
"fever-333~desert-rap":{"opus":"Jason Aalon Butler measures his personal fight against generations of Black oppression, name-checking segregation-era laws behind his own struggle to stop sabotaging himself. His grandmother's death from a broken heart reads to him as a cue to break loose. The track mixes bravado with reckoning, admitting he can't satisfy everyone.","src":"opus"},
"fever-333~do-or-die":{"opus":"A protest cut aimed at a mayor and the powerful, demanding equity for people stuck beneath their gaze. Fever 333 dramatize being told to earn scraps that others inherited, framing grabbing what you need by any route as the only sane reply to a rigged setup. Fury sharpened into threat.","src":"opus"},
"fever-333~higher-power":{"haiku":"Collective empowerment through racial and social solidarity, asserting agency and dignity against systemic disenfranchisement.","opus":"A protest built on the contradiction that no human born equal can be illegal, the band claiming marginalized people as the real higher power, demanding reparations and celebrating fluid identity as a force about to be returned to the oppressor.","sonnet":"A protest anthem for Black, brown, trans, and queer people asserting their power against a system built on inequality — naming the foundation of the nation, calling out illegal immigration rhetoric, and insisting that marginalized people collectively are the higher power that will give back what has been taken.","src":"haiku"},
"fever-333~in-the-end-spotify-singles":{"opus":"A reworking of Linkin Park's meditation on futile effort, carrying the ache of trying so hard only to watch it all fall apart. Time slips away as the narrator confronts wasted trust and a relationship's collapse. Fever 333's rendition honors the original's exhausted resignation, the sense that striving may ultimately amount to nothing.","src":"opus"},
"fever-333~kingdom":{"haiku":"Builds through labor while the world collapses, protecting a kingdom of belonging that cannot be taken. Critiques systems profiting from weapons and imprisonment.","opus":"A defiant anthem about building something from nothing while the surrounding world collapses, refusing to surrender the small territory of dignity you carve out with your own hands even as taxmen and profiteers close in. It sharpens into indictment of a country that profits from prisons and guns. Fever 333 emerged as a politically charged hardcore-rap outfit built explicitly around protest, and the pointed verses here fit that activist mission.","sonnet":"A community building something durable from nothing, in a place that profits from prisons and exports violence — they haul materials up the hill by hand while the world around them collapses. The defiance is collective and loud: no authority, no taxman, no external force can take what they have built together.","src":"haiku"},
"fever-333~new-west-order":{"haiku":"Multicultural rock rebellion centers Black and POC artists reclaiming genre against erasure and marginalization.","opus":"Jason Aalon Butler recounts being the strange Black kid in the punk and hardcore scene, skateboarding while blending rap idols with British indie-rock on his stereo, carving space where none existed. The boast turns exclusion and industry gatekeeping into triumph, announcing his movement as an incoming wave.","sonnet":"A Black rock musician traces his path from skateboarding outsider to Grammy nominee, reclaiming rock's origins in Black music and declaring a \"New West Order\" that fuses punk, rap, and political purpose — survival and history rewritten by those the genre forgot.","src":"haiku"},
"fever-333~no-hostages":{"haiku":"Militant refusal and aggressive resistance; the speaker takes no prisoners in confrontation.","opus":"A furious indictment of police violence and racial injustice, cataloguing the ways a Black person can be killed in ordinary moments. Fever 333 invert the gun-rights slogan to name cops as killers, refusing to take hostages in their demand for reckoning. Grief and rage fused into unflinching protest against a lethal system.","sonnet":"Rage at systemic anti-Black police violence in America — a Black father murdered by officers, the rigged fairground of the legal system, people killed during traffic stops and no-knock raids; \"taking no hostages\" means the state, and the repeated \"guns don't kill people, cops kill people\" reframes the national gun debate as a cover for state violence. The narrator refuses to explain one more time why this is exhausting.","src":"haiku"},
"fever-333~pin-drop":{"opus":"A tense reckoning with mass incarceration and systemic entrapment, its narrator out on bail and barely breathing, cast as a premeditated statistic. Fever 333 insist the system isn't broken but built this way, refusing to wait for change. The wanting of peace and blood in the same breath captures anger poised on a knife's edge.","src":"opus"},
"fever-333~presence-is-strength":{"opus":"A protest anthem torn between backing the least-bad option among corrupt politicians and calling for revolt from below. FEVER 333 flip the hierarchy, cheering the servants over the rulers and insisting that ordinary people, when they show up in force and refuse to stay quiet, are a power in themselves.","sonnet":"A political activist anthem arguing that collective presence and vocal resistance are the only real tools against corrupt power — the song rejects the lesser-of-two-evils political trap and insists the marginalized must show up loudly because people are power.","src":"sonnet"},
"fever-333~wing":{"haiku":"Structural violence and capitalist extraction breed resistance while the speaker names exploitation across racial and economic lines. Rebellion requires refusal to play the predetermined game.","opus":"A furious reckoning with American racism and exploitation, using baseball's swing as a chant to indict a system built on broken Black bodies and stolen capital, refusing to be a gracious player in someone else's rigged game.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of racial capitalism — wealth built on Black bodies, opportunity zones that benefit no one in them, celebrities ridiculed to suicide on social media threads — the speaker refuses to be a player in the game, channeling rage through the \"swing batter\" refrain into a demand to be seen and stop being exploited.","src":"haiku"},
"fever-ray~if-i-had-a-heart":{"haiku":"Emotional void masquerades as desire; the speaker envies feeling and connection while trapped in mechanical existence.","opus":"Karin Dreijer's chilling meditation on insatiable want, a voice that craves more while confessing it lacks the heart or voice to love or sing. The dread is in desire without feeling, reaching endlessly and never touching the floor.","sonnet":"A being without a heart or voice imagines what it would feel like to love and to sing, suspended between a window and the floor, simultaneously crushed and curious, filled with something gathered from underneath — desire expressed through the conditional, as though emotion itself is a foreign country.","src":"haiku"},
"fiddlehead~fiddleheads":{"opus":"A post-hardcore burst against the deadening routine of mere survival, alarm-to-work-to-death, and the refusal to let ambition rot in a hole. The narrator dreams of making his finest art and moving people, buoyed by the loyalty of ride-or-die friends. A defiant plea to live fully rather than simply get by.","src":"opus"},
"fiends~keep-me-in-the-dark":{"haiku":"Hiding in darkness protects through invisibility. Humming so low becomes refuge—the search cannot find what refuses to be found.","opus":"A ghostly game of hiding, humming too low to be heard and impossible to find, insisting that when everything is willed to stay the same it changes anyway, yet never tiring of singing.","sonnet":"A song about hiding and keeping a low profile from someone searching for you — the narrator goes dark, humming so quietly they can't be found, while acknowledging that things inevitably change even when you want them to stay the same.","src":"haiku"},
"fifth-dawn~in-between":{"haiku":"Liminal struggle toward agency; the speaker positions themselves between breaking and transcendence, fragile and determined simultaneously.","opus":"A performer standing at the threshold of self-doubt and defiance, built to be broken yet unwilling to play it safe, asking if she's enough or merely deluded. The stage is where she tests whether the calling is real or another mistake.","sonnet":"A woman who has been looked down on — understated, dismissed — insists she is unstoppable once she locks onto something, but struggles with self-doubt about whether she is deluded or whether she has already let something important slip away.","src":"haiku"},
"fight-the-fade~composure-toronto-is-broken-remix":{"opus":"Fight the Fade capture the strain of being pulled under by too many obligations and a sense of being trapped with no escape. The track is really about the labor of projecting calm while quietly falling apart, severing damaging attachments before they finish the job, and just wanting a moment to recover.","sonnet":"A song about the performance of keeping it together when everything has already fractured — the narrator is overwhelmed and sinking, but keeps pretending to have composure while trying to cut ties to whatever compromised him. The 'starting over' refrain is exhausted repetition, not optimism.","src":"sonnet"},
"fight-the-fade~everything-is-fine":{"opus":"Exhausted by an endless cycle of starting over and losing someone who keeps pushing him away, the speaker asks whether trying is even worth it. Its refrain about the world moving on captures being stuck while everything else changes.","sonnet":"Exhaustion with constant restarts: the speaker is slipping, the world moves on regardless, and a relationship keeps pushing him away just as they get close — the refrain \"everything is fine?\" is a question mark he cannot answer, delivered by someone too tired of starting over to believe the reassurance.","src":"sonnet"},
"fight-the-fade~everything-is-fine?":{"haiku":"Circular struggle and perpetual reinvention exhaust the spirit; the speaker watches the world move on while they spiral, unable to stop cycling through attempts at connection.","src":"haiku"},
"fight-the-fade~scratching":{"haiku":"Internal violation unfolds as the narrator perceives manipulation and predation, transformation from victim to volatile force emerges through recognition of corruption.","src":"haiku"},
"fight-the-fade~scratching-feat-daedric":{"haiku":"Recognizing and confronting a destructive force disguised as intimacy, defending against erasure and exploitation.","opus":"Recognizing a manipulator who thrives on manufactured controversy while a real crisis burns, the narrator waking up to being cut from their plans and warning that the rage they're provoking will finally be turned back on them.","sonnet":"The speaker gradually recognises that someone in their life is a destructive presence — a rat scratching at the wall around their heart — and, realising the manipulation and the way it stokes volatile rage, refuses to concede.","src":"haiku"},
"fight-the-fade~stranger":{"haiku":"Alienation crystallizes the speaker as obsolete and unrecognizable in a world of forced compromise; they wander foreign and alone, questioning their own survival within civilizations built for others.","opus":"A hard-rock lament of profound alienation, the narrator feeling like an alien in his own world, unable to recognize the life he's living. Amid compromise and lost hope, he wonders whether he's obsolete or a dying breed, aching to know if anyone else feels the same. Estrangement rendered as existential loneliness.","sonnet":"A person who feels like an alien in their own world — everything familiar yet unrecognisable — questions whether the strong survive and searches for anyone else who is a dying breed, a stranger to the life they are nevertheless compelled to keep walking through.","src":"haiku"},
"fightstar~we-apologise-for-nothing":{"haiku":"Defiant rejection of accountability; the speaker refuses to apologize for transformation or revolutionary stance.","opus":"A defiant refusal to repent, standing over history and daring the world to take everything and still get no apology. It reads as a generation's unbowed stance against being cut out and blamed.","sonnet":"A defiant refusal to apologize for one's history, choices, or allegiances — framed as a revolutionary stance against being erased or redefined by those in power, and a call to stand over the shadows of the past and own them. A second song embedded in the track shifts to environmental catastrophe as another form of collective failure that deserves no comfortable denial.","src":"haiku"},
"filter~absentee-father": {"haiku":"An absent deity figure is confronted for inaction and lack of grace; the speaker demands intervention or at least honesty while drowning in suffering and pity.","opus":"An accusatory prayer aimed at a silent God cast as a negligent parent, demanding intervention and grace while bleeding out in a world that feels abandoned and unjust. The tone swings between pleading and bitter contempt. Filter is Richard Patrick's industrial-rock project, and the blend of spiritual crisis, quiet-loud dynamics, and venomous questioning is exactly the register that defined the band from its Short Bus days onward.","sonnet":"Anger directed at a God who watches suffering without intervening — the feeling that divine justice has failed, leaving people bleeding and bewildered. The question repeats with increasing desperation: where is the graceful hand, where is the reign, why the silence when things are falling apart.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick, raised Catholic and long since done with the church, arraigns a God he casts as the deadbeat parent of the title, demanding to know why a supposedly loving authority stays mute while ruin gathers around the people meant to be his charges. The interrogation never softens into pleading; it stays prosecutorial, listing the wreckage and daring the silent figure either to step in and repair it or finally confess that the abandonment was real all along. That refusal to resolve is the whole design, since the promised deliverance and the divine reign keep surfacing as accusatory questions rather than comforts, leaving the accused eternally off-stage and the accuser stranded with grievances no one is left to hear.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"a prosecutorial indictment aimed at a silent accused who never appears","built":"deliverance and divine reign surface only as accusatory questions, never comforts"},
"filter~american-clich": {"haiku":"Conformity demands choosing a side while everyone becomes identical; following the crowd guarantees defeat.","opus":"A jab at American self-sabotage and blame, where making yourself or your friend the reason means making them the one beaten. The bus-seat imagery and hollow repetition frame conformity and victimhood as a tired national cliché.","sonnet":"A song about conformity, blind trust, and the hollow repetition of mainstream American life — the speaker watches people follow whoever is in front of them, pick a seat on the bus of society without questioning it, and make themselves the enemy of anyone who resists; the cliche is both the behavior and the blankness behind it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick turns a public bus into a parable of conformity, where choosing a seat and picking whom to trust stand in for the pressure to fall in line with a national script nobody actually wrote down. The song sours as it moves: what begins as self-centering guilt curdles into scapegoating a companion, treating the person beside you as the acceptable price of belonging to the crowd. That hollow chant about craving sameness lands as the real diagnosis, a country marketing assimilation as identity until the individual dissolves entirely into the herd.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"conformity marketed as identity until the individual dissolves","built":"a bus ride staged as a parable of the national script nobody wrote"},
"filter~be-careful-what-you-wish-for": {"haiku":"Warning against desire that seems to slip away no matter the effort, loss is always the outcome.","opus":"Watching a cause you fought to save slip through your fingers, the warning that getting your wish drags whatever answers the door in along with it, disillusion that some hidden hand always keeps you pinned in place.","sonnet":"About watching something — a relationship, a movement, a city — fall apart despite your attempts to hold it together, and being warned that getting what you wanted may not look like what you imagined; there's a sense of helplessness watching collapse and being unable to stop it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Filter watches an unnamed catastrophe wash in and drain a world to ash, reading that collapse as something quietly bargained for rather than merely suffered. Richard Patrick keeps circling a desperate salvage effort that never catches hold, each attempt sliding off before it can grip. The title warning lands like folk wisdom soured into dread: whatever finally answers your want is the same figure now knocking at a door you can no longer close.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nature & the elements","death & grief"],"means":"the bargained-for collapse that cannot be closed against","built":"folk wisdom soured into dread by the thing that finally answers your want"},
"filter~burn-it": {"haiku":"The song cycles through burning, breaking, and destruction. It depicts a cycle of self-harm, exposure, and the desire to break free through violence.","opus":"A relentless cycle of self-destruction rendered as burning and breaking down again and again, with the imagery of cutting, begging on knees, bleeding, and choking. The clean-then-dirty refrain frames addiction or self-harm as a compulsion that always ends with a kiss goodbye before it starts over.","sonnet":"About cycles of self-destruction, cleaning up, and immediately getting dirty again — addiction, self-harm, and the compulsion to burn everything down rather than heal. The images of cutting, bleeding, and choking paint a body that keeps undoing itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"Filter built this from Richard Patrick's stammering, single-syllable command verbs, and that grammar is the argument: wake, clean, burn, break, cut, bleed, all barked in threes like a stuck loop or a self-harming compulsion that can't stop repeating the same act. The chorus reduces destruction to a mantra of burning and breaking something down only to do it down again, so the song isn't about one collapse but the addiction to collapse itself, the cyclical clean-then-dirty ruin the first verse names outright. A girl surfaces at the edges, always lost and unwilling to be found, letting the aggression read as much like a spiral of dependency and abuse as pure noise. The final turn drops the imperatives for a blurred, eyes-closed drink-it-in surrender and a blown-kiss goodbye, so the last burn-it-down lands not as triumph but as the numb, resigned end of someone who has finally chosen the fire.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"not one collapse but the addiction to collapsing itself","built":"single-syllable command verbs hammered in threes enact a compulsion that cannot stop"},
"filter~burn-out-the-sun": {"opus":"Richard Patrick's Filter turns weariness into a plea for connection, a tired narrator unable to rest while clouds and unfinished conversations linger. Change looms over a relationship, and he hopes to be taken along wherever escape leads. Exhaustion and longing intertwine, the desperate wish that someone will wait until the burden lifts.","src":"opus","fable":"A late-Filter song built on exhaustion rather than the industrial aggression Richard Patrick made his name on: the verses are a strung-out phone call, one person half-fixing a relationship that needs total rearrangement while the clock runs out and the heart stays cold. The two questions that structure the whole thing are will you take me with you and will you wait for me, and everything turns on which of the pair actually manages to escape. Its second half widens the same complaint from a couple to a we who went blind, stopped listening, and let it happen on all of us, so the private breakup reads as a diagnosis of a whole distracted era. The chorus is where the fatigue turns apocalyptic: unable to sleep until the clouds clear, the singer escalates to burning out the sun itself, which is less a wish for light than the tired person's fantasy of just stopping the day, snuffing the thing that keeps time marching on. The refusal to resolve that image is the point, an exhausted mind bargaining for an end to the demand that it keep going.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness","politics & society"],"means":"private exhaustion escalating to a wish to stop time itself","built":"the apocalyptic chorus is the tired mind's refusal to keep the day going"},
"filter~can-t-she-see-head-of-fire-pt-2": {"opus":"A tight confession that a particular woman draws out the worst in him, making him everything he shouldn't be. The whole thought loops on the helplessness of that pull.","sonnet":"A brief, looping confession that a specific woman brings out the worst in the narrator — she makes him everything he should not be, and he cannot understand why she cannot see this dynamic.","src":"sonnet","haiku":"Incendiary passion and unquenched desire consume the speaker; fire and heat metaphorize romantic obsession.","fable":"The entire text is one two-line admission looped verbatim, and the loop is the point: this is a man watching himself corrode in a relationship and doing nothing about it, the thought stuck on repeat because he can neither leave it nor act on it. Richard Patrick, whose Filter career and own recovery narrative are steeped in self-disgust, plants the accusation in the title's parenthetical — the demand that she should be able to see the worst in him — while the verses only confess it, so the blame he wants to hand off keeps collapsing back onto the speaker. The refusal to develop past the couplet reads as the self-loathing itself: a mind that has diagnosed its own toxicity precisely and gained nothing from the clarity.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The couplet is Head of Fire’s own chorus, extracted and recut as its own track — a reprise, the earlier song’s thought refusing to stay inside it.","themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"a mind that diagnosed its toxicity precisely and gained nothing from the clarity","built":"the refusal to develop past the opening couplet enacts the self-loathing itself"},
"filter~can-t-you-trip-like-i-do-filter-the-crystal-method": {"haiku":"Dissociation and altered consciousness create distance from trauma; the speaker wishes others could experience their fractured perspective.","opus":"A drug-fueled dare to share the speaker's altered state, cataloguing a mind stripped of patience and comprehension yet convinced this is the best. The insistent question is whether anyone else can possibly feel the world the way he does right now.","sonnet":"An invitation to share the fractured, overwhelming sensory experience of being high or in a dissociative altered state — the narrator exists simultaneously with vast knowledge and complete disorientation, and wants everyone else to feel what they feel.","src":"haiku","fable":"A boast that curdles into a demand. The opening resume stacks incompatible credentials - a toddler's grasp welded to a hardened cop's nerve - until the swagger reads less like mastery than like a chemical high mistaking itself for enlightenment. The Filter and Crystal Method pairing, out of a late-nineties era of electronic-rock crossovers, drives that mania on a locked, hammering pulse. Then the body betrays the brag: sleepless, jaw clenched, ears ringing, yet certain he is roaring back like a freight train. The chorus turns outward to recruit anyone in reach into the same overload. Its final chant fuses every verb into one interrogation, insisting the whole crowd share a state that has already stopped being a pleasure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism"],"means":"a chemical high mistaking itself for enlightenment and recruiting the crowd","built":"locked hammering pulse drives a body that is sleepless and certain it is roaring"},
"filter~cancer": {"haiku":"Human destructiveness and environmental devastation, with humanity depicted as a spreading disease.","opus":"A first-person indictment of humanity as a spreading disease, the singer crossing oceans and borders while naming himself the scum of the earth, insisting that what feels urgent won't outlast us. Aerial views of scarred cities sharpen the ecological disgust. Filter, Richard Patrick's post-Nine Inch Nails project, made its name on this kind of self-lacerating industrial rock where personal loathing swells into a verdict on the whole species.","sonnet":"A macro-level reckoning with humanity's self-destructive footprint — crossing oceans, borders, mountain ranges like a spreading disease, surveying scarred cities from above and finding the view makes you want to weep. The speaker turns the lens inward and arrives at the harshest verdict: the scum of the earth and humanity are the same thing, one and indivisible.","src":"haiku","fable":"Filter stages humanity itself as the metastasis, a traveler crossing ocean and mountain the way a malignancy colonizes healthy tissue. The first-person voyager tallies canyons, cities, and prisons only to conclude that wealth, not mercy, buys anyone's freedom. The vantage then lifts to an aerial view of a wounded planet and a smog-choked Los Angeles, and the singer collapses the charge inward, becoming both the disease and the whole species at once. That soft parental refrain, insisting everything urgent will finally turn weightless, lands as the cold verdict the diagnosis has been building toward.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","nature & the elements","money & the street"],"means":"the singer collapsing the charge inward to become the disease he names","built":"the soft parental refrain lands as the cold verdict the diagnosis has been building toward"},
"filter~captain-bligh": {"haiku":"A grieving, guilty man cannot believe the harm he's done; he contemplates prayer and redemption while drowning in remorse and blood.","opus":"Richard Patrick's Filter deliver a guilt-stricken confession from a man haunted by what he did to someone else, cycling through mounting self-descriptions of grief and bloodied culpability. He wants to reverse the damage and turns to prayer, but the escalating imagery hints the harm was violent and past mending.","sonnet":"A confession of guilt and grief to someone the speaker has deeply wronged — he's blood-soaked with shame, tries to pray, and can't undo the damage he's done, oscillating between self-flagellation and a faint desire to make it right.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession from a man rehearsing his own defense, insisting some trivial slight was enough to justify tearing down a strutting authority figure and everything he arrived in. Richard Patrick assembles it like a plea in mitigation, then lets it buckle: the self-naming escalates from grieving to guilty to beaten to a body wrecked and stained by what it did. The mutiny of the title stays interior, an ordinary person deposing the tyrant inside himself rather than any real captain. By the close the argument dissolves into prayer and one cornered, repeated promise of another try, its last word doubling as a drink and a gunshot, a man out of alibis who knows it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"a mutiny conducted entirely against the tyrant inside himself","built":"the final word doubles as a drink and a gunshot, both exits at once"},
"filter~catch-a-falling-knife": {"haiku":"Childhood trauma and family dysfunction, with the home as a prison.","opus":"Richard Patrick's industrial rock project, formed after his stint in Nine Inch Nails, turns to domestic trauma here, recalling a childhood idyll that curdled once someone poisoned the household. Trying to rescue that person becomes akin to salvaging a life perhaps beyond saving, delivered through the band's abrasive, raw-throated intensity.","sonnet":"A childhood of suburban sweetness that curdled once a particular person entered the picture — a home that became a private hell, parents who disappeared, a heart that grew black and cold. The speaker looks back on arrested development and sees a life that was better off not salvaged; even catching it means catching a blade.","src":"haiku","fable":"A childhood of schoolyards and suburban ease curdles the moment another person enters, and the same walls that read as home to a parent register as an inferno to the child kept inside them. Richard Patrick, long candid about addiction and the wounds family leaves, builds the hook as a paradox: to save a plummeting life you must seize the blade by its cutting edge, so rescue and injury arrive in one motion. The second verse turns to absent parents and a long loneliness, then the bridge hardens the injury into permanence, a heart darkening and aging before its time with no way back to the peace it started from.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"rescue and wounding arriving as the same gesture","built":"the title paradox collapses saving and being cut into one act"},
"filter~city-of-blinding-riots": {"haiku":"Rage erupts in the city as inhabitants watch it burn; brothers betray and control each other while the world watches the destruction unfold.","opus":"An incendiary anthem of civic rage boiling over as a city burns and the whole world watches; the throughline is the resentment of the watched-from-above ordinary people against those who wished them dead, channeled into the cathartic, repeated command to burn the whole thing down.","sonnet":"A song about urban rage erupting into fire — a city where people have been oppressed and lied to finally explodes, and the narrator, identifying with the rioters, calls for burning everything down with a kind of cathartic fury.","src":"haiku","fable":"Filter turns civic unrest into pure combustion, a track where the grievance barely gets named before everything collapses into an incantation of arson chanted at a spectating planet. What begins as simmering resentment stalking the streets after dark hardens fast into a demand that the whole block be leveled while distant onlookers do nothing but stare into the flames. A later verse sketches the enemy as a watching authority perched overhead, feigning kinship and murmuring false affection while quietly wishing the narrator dead, sustained by a lifetime of comfortable deceit. Richard Patrick, decades past the industrial precision of his Nine Inch Nails apprenticeship, lets the repetition become the argument, the rioter's mind reduced to one profane syllable because fury this absolute has no vocabulary left to spend.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Reviewers tie the burning city to Ferguson — the 2014-15 unrest after Michael Brown’s death, the same event Nothing In My Hands watches from the victim’s side.","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"fury so total it collapses language to one syllable","built":"repetition becomes the argument where vocabulary runs out"},
"filter~clouds": {"haiku":"Loss of a loved one and the shattering of illusions about heaven or escape.","opus":"A dream of escaping with a lover to some higher realm collapses the instant it arrives, the beloved's eyes vanishing and heaven revealed as having abandoned them both. The diary imagery turns romance into a haunted record kept by two ghosts. Filter, led by Richard Patrick, balances abrasive industrial rock with aching melodic ballads like this, where longing curdles into disillusion the moment it is realized.","sonnet":"A grief dream in two movements — first the flight toward something transcendent with another person, then the jolt of waking to find them gone and the shared life reduced to a diary stained beyond reading. Heaven entered and immediately abandoned, the elevated place reached only to reveal it was never theirs to keep.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dream of escape with a lover curdles the instant it lands, the sleeper waking to an emptied bed and eyes that no longer shine, the promised ascent revealed as a plummet. Richard Patrick, writing after the band's chemical wreckage and his own hard-won sobriety, treats devotion here as the false paradise that dissolves on contact, salvation quietly withdrawn the very moment it seems within reach. The second verse turns starker still, a shared diary reread as a funeral for both partners, its tender entries now the leavings of two people already dead to each other.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","freedom & escape"],"means":"salvation quietly withdrawn the moment it seems within reach","built":"the dream of ascent revealed mid-fall as a plummet"},
"filter~columind": {"haiku":"Hatred directed at someone who has everything but achieved nothing, whose greed and entitlement destroy others while leaving them unaccountable.","opus":"A scornful takedown of a spoiled, destructive figure who wants and gets everything while leaving others bleeding on their knees, the singer half-disgusted, half-liberated by watching it end. The refrain hammers on shame and appetite. Filter, Richard Patrick's industrial-rock vehicle, thrives on this kind of seething character assassination delivered through grinding riffs and a scream that tips from menace into something like release.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who got everything they wanted through destruction — greed, entitlement, and the wreckage left behind. The word shame lands again and again like a verdict, and the song circles the idea that the carnage will be over soon, but nobody watching will really care.","src":"haiku","fable":"An interrogator's voice circles a perpetrator who has emptied a room of the living, the accusatory refrain of shame doubling as the only sentence that can be passed once the shooting is done and the survivors kneel among the fallen. What curdles the song is the pivot at its center, where the second voice stops being interrogated and turns to survey the carnage, announcing his own imminent liberation as though the slaughter were a final debt at last cleared. The chorus refuses any moral resolution by collapsing wanting, needing, and destroying into one grammar of insatiable appetite, then abandons even that to a numb aftermath in which nobody left standing will care what was done.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Reviews of The Amalgamut read the title as Columbine fused with mind, the song widely reported as Patrick shaming the two shooters — a reading he has never decoded himself.","themes":["violence & murder","shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"appetite that collapses wanting, needing, and destroying into one grammar","built":"the interrogated voice pivots mid-song to survey its own carnage"},
"filter~consider-this": {"haiku":"The speaker, betrayed or conned by someone, concludes that person would be better off dead and repeats this judgment as a final verdict.","opus":"A venomous kiss-off to a con artist who left the narrator feeling cheap and burned after some scam or heist. The blunt, repeated wish that the target would be better off dead channels betrayal into pure contempt.","sonnet":"Cold rage at someone who conned and used the narrator — a blunt, repeated wish that the person were dead, delivered without remorse after the betrayal becomes clear.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a scorched kiss-off to a grifter, and the venom is the whole point. Richard Patrick channels the industrial abrasion he honed touring with Nine Inch Nails into something more personal, replaying a swindle until the only verdict left is that the world would run cleaner without this person in it. The two framing lines chart the turn: first he calls it a mere hustle he can shrug off, then admits it was a robbery where he was the mark who got taken. Between them sits a muttered, half-improvised aside about a man who can say what happened but never how or why — the same unanswerable questions the whole song is stuck circling. The chorus, hammered four times unchanged, stops being a wish and becomes a chant, the sound of someone out of arguments and simply detonating.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"swindle relitigated until the chorus becomes a detonation","built":"chorus hammered four times unchanged stops being a wish"},
"filter~dose": {"haiku":"A person rejects preachers invading their space and warns others about damnation, viewing spiritual pressure as violation.","opus":"Richard Patrick's snarling kiss-off to a moralizing hypocrite who invades his space to preach. It's raw contempt for anyone who peddles sanctimony while their own conduct sends them straight to the hell they threaten.","sonnet":"An aggressive, contemptuous rejection of someone who keeps invading the narrator's space with religious proselytizing — the response is not theological debate but visceral revulsion and a demand to be left alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick aims this at a proselytizer who invades his personal space with scripture and moral certainty, and the fury builds by design as the choruses recur. The first pass is merely disgusted, wanting the intruder to peddle his salvation to a stranger instead; the second curdles into a deliberately crude sexual insult, escalating revulsion past argument into physical contempt. The bridge lands the actual thesis in a single flat exchange: asked whether the preacher's power can subdue him, the answer is a bored refusal, which strips the earlier rage of any doubt and marks the whole assault as beneath him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","faith & the occult"],"means":"contempt escalating until refusal outranks the rage","built":"second chorus swaps argument for physical insult"},
"filter~down-with-me": {"haiku":"Violent family tragedy and murder-suicide, where a possessive killer takes their victim with them.","opus":"Filter, Richard Patrick's industrial-rock outfit, specialized in menace hidden behind wholesome facades. Here a seemingly ideal household masks simmering fury that ends in a killing police reach too late, plus a possessive murderer's note declaring that if he cannot keep her, no one else may. The chorus drags its victim under with unsettling gentleness.","sonnet":"A domestic horror song told through the gap between the surface and what festers underneath — magazine-perfect family photos concealing a caged rage, cops arriving too late to do anything but string up yellow tape. A found note delivers the cold logic at the center: obsessive possession turned fatal, dragging everything down together.","src":"haiku","fable":"A murder-suicide staged inside the wreckage of a suburban ideal. Richard Patrick opens on framed portraits of a picture-perfect household, then peels the veneer to expose the fury caged beneath the smiling arrangement. The refrain is the killer's own voice, tender and possessive at once, pulling the beloved into death as though it were an embrace. A discovered note supplies the motive, that grim jealous logic where love curdles into ownership and annihilation becomes the only ending he will permit. Patrick keeps the horror domestic rather than gothic, letting the sheer ordinariness of the home carry the dread.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","family & blood"],"means":"possession curdling into annihilation framed as an embrace","built":"domestic ordinariness carries the horror that gothic register would deflect"},
"filter~drug-boy": {"haiku":"A drug-addicted character finds chemicals sacred and sunlight as a god; someone tries to help but the speaker dismisses it as a waste of time while lost in addiction.","opus":"Filter, Richard Patrick's industrial-rock outfit, drag a companion through benders and drug runs, the narrator crowning his chemicals as both god and daylight while brushing off anyone's concern as pointless. The blunt, chanted repetition mirrors an addict wedded to the night and rejecting every hand that reaches to help.","sonnet":"A figure who lives only at night, chemicals serving as both god and sunlight, indifferent to anyone trying to pull him back toward daylight. Every intervention registers only as wasted effort — the nocturnal spiral is chosen, not accidental.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick, years sober, writes addiction as a seductive recruiter rather than a confession. The verses are all commands aimed at a companion, escalating from a night out to scoring, then a firearm, then a grave torn open. Against that momentum the chorus flatly dismisses every helping hand as squandered effort, the user siding with the fix over the person reaching in. The bridge drops the swagger: the substance becomes God, then daylight itself, a rapture he knows is chemical yet crowns anyway. That worship, sung without irony, is the horror underneath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Patrick has confirmed the song revisits his own drug years touring with Nine Inch Nails.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"the substance crowned as God without a trace of irony","built":"escalating commands give way to worship that reveals the horror underneath"},
"filter~face-down": {"opus":"A defiant Filter track where the singer says honesty keeps him breathing even after betrayal leaves him bloodied on elevated terrain. Watching resentment and errors collapse around him, he holds to his integrity, with a passing nod to the Fab Four's creed that affection alone can carry people through the wreckage.","src":"opus","fable":"Richard Patrick builds the whole thing on a defiant survival boast, staking existence itself on honesty toward a betrayer who then abandons the singer bleeding and prostrate on contested moral high land. That elevated territory becomes a killing floor, so integrity earns not vindication but wounds, the wronged party sprawled in the dirt while the wrongdoer walks free somewhere above him. A late gesture toward the Beatles, reaching for the redemptive faith that affection alone might carry two damaged people forward, lands as strained consolation rather than conviction, a borrowed creed the surrounding bitterness never quite lets settle.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss","faith & the occult"],"means":"integrity earning wounds rather than vindication","built":"a borrowed Beatles cadence landing as strained consolation the surrounding bitterness refuses"},
"filter~first-you-break-it": {"haiku":"Someone breaks things—trust, promises, integrity—then fakes or hides it, passing damage to others while assuming they won't notice the deception.","opus":"An accusation of betrayal and cover-up, someone who breaks something then fakes it and tries to pass it off as if no one will notice; the read is disillusionment at seeing a betrayal exposed, likely on-screen, beneath a false facade of heartfelt pride.","sonnet":"About someone who damages something — a relationship, a trust — and then pretends nothing happened, expecting it to go unnoticed; the narrator sees through the performance of innocence and the attempt to pass off the broken thing as fine.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about the exhausting theater of a betrayal everyone can already see through. The verses circle someone caught out, performing recovery and laughing with the crowd, betting the damage smooths over once the lights go off. Richard Patrick turns the chorus into a flat accusation set on a loop. Repeated five times, the phrase stops being insight and hardens into a verdict, the bluff growing louder and less convincing until the pretense collapses under its own insistence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"a betrayal everyone can see through performed for a crowd anyway","built":"the accusation phrase repeated five times until it hardens from insight into verdict"},
"filter~gerbil": {"haiku":"A person watches someone become intellectually vacant, checked out and incapable, leading the observer to question how they lost their mental faculties.","opus":"A curt sneer at a dulled, checked-out person who sits in front of the television, described as no smarter than a broom, and is blamed for failing everyone around them. The repeated taunts frame numbness as betrayal. Filter, led by Richard Patrick, built its early industrial-rock identity on exactly this compact, contemptuous portraiture, pairing mechanical menace with a chorus that jabs rather than soars.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone disconnected and passive — waking, moving through rooms, turning on a screen, barely registering the world around them. The contempt is blunt: thoughtless, brainless, someone who has somehow managed to ruin things for others simply by existing on autopilot.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick aims his industrial machine at a figure hollowed into pure routine, a man who wakes only to plant himself before the switched-on television and drift ever further inward. The verses render that vacancy in cold third person while the choruses swivel to direct address from a wounded us, hurling escalating insults that barely mask real grief at being abandoned by someone once counted as theirs. What reads as contempt for a numbed stranger is closer to mourning, and the pet-name title seals it, tagging the man as a caged creature the accusers pity even as they spit at him. The final clipped cry of the collective hangs unanswered, the numbness having won.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"contempt for vacancy masking grief at abandonment","built":"pet-name title tags the numb man as caged creature they still mourn"},
"filter~god-damn-me": {"haiku":"Self-condemnation and existential emptiness; the narrator finds nothing to gain or change, cursing themselves as fundamentally insufficient.","opus":"A suffocating litany of scarcity—not enough water, sky, breath, or gain for him—culminating in self-condemnation; the deeper read is depressive self-loathing where nothing changes, nothing is erased, and the only thing left to blame or damn is himself.","sonnet":"A song about being unable to find enough of anything in the world — not water, not sky, not breath — to satisfy or sustain the narrator, who can only curse himself in the emptiness.","src":"haiku","fable":"A litany of scarcity where every escape route gets measured and found short of what a drowning person would need. Richard Patrick catalogs elements meant to cleanse or conceal him, then reports each one running dry against his own body. The refrain turns that shortfall inward, converting a world too meager to hide in into a verdict he pronounces on himself. When the bridge admits nothing shifts and nothing gets undone, the guilt settles as its own kind of permanence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"a world too meager to hide in turned into a self-verdict","built":"each cleansing element reported running dry against his own body"},
"filter~hatred-is-contagious": {"haiku":"Spread of hatred and emotional pain as a contagious force that persists despite personal survival.","opus":"A grinding meditation on hate as an infection that spreads while some detached figure merely watches, pain and hate becoming the narrator's process and bosses until crosses and tombstones mark the losses. Yet the refrain that he is still alive claws a scrap of defiance from the wreckage. Filter, Richard Patrick's industrial rock project, pair mechanical heaviness with that push-pull between being broken down and stubbornly surviving.","sonnet":"Hatred examined as a disease that spreads by proximity — the speaker haunted, deadened, pride stripped away, yet insisting on survival: broken and remade by it, but alive. The crosses and tombstones suggest a toll already paid, and the refrain holds the contradiction: this thing destroys me, shapes me, and I'm still here.","src":"haiku","fable":"Filter frame cruelty as an infection you catch from the people around you, spreading unstoppably while some distant onlooker simply watches it travel. Written for Anthems For The Damned amid the mid-2000s wars, the song turns rage into a communal disease rather than a private failing. Its imagery marches from voices in the skull toward crosses and gravestones, so mourning and killing collapse into one bleak terrain. Yet the survivor refuses to vanish inside that terrain: bruised, stripped of his dignity, remade by the damage, he keeps insisting on his own pulse. That defiant claim of still breathing is the counter-infection, the one thing resentment never fully seizes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"defiance as the one infection resentment cannot spread","built":"mourning imagery and killing imagery share the same terrain"},
"filter~head-of-fire": {"haiku":"A person wrestles with internal conflict between wanting to live and feeling like they're dying, unable to escape a negative spiral even as they recognize it. They're trapped by their own capacity for destruction, shaped into their worst self by someone they're entangled with.","opus":"Post-industrial self-loathing tangled with a toxic attraction, a narrator hiding from the light, dying while wanting to live, undone by someone who brings out his worst. The push-pull of destructive desire is the core.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship that brings out the worst in the narrator — she makes him into someone he shouldn't be, his head is on fire with anxiety and self-loathing, and he retreats into himself rather than face the version of himself she reflects back.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick maps a familiar Filter fault line: the daylight world feels punishing, so the narrator bolts, ducks, and burrows for cover, only to find himself pinned in the open anyway. The title splits one person into contradictory temperatures, a frozen chest yoked to a burning skull, and that mismatch drives the churn where the pull to keep breathing wars against a slow inward collapse. When he aims the blame outward at the woman who supposedly draws his ugliest self forward, the accusation curdles into confession, admitting he has become the very thing he once swore he never would.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"accusation aimed outward that curdles into confession","built":"the title splits one person into contradictory temperatures"},
"filter~hey-man-nice-shot": {"haiku":"Respect for someone's final act of defiance through suicide; witnessing their strength in ending their struggle.","opus":"Richard Patrick's sardonic reckoning with a public suicide, often read as a response to a politician's on-camera death. The bitter praise cuts both ways, mourning a person while noting how the survivors hardened, an uneasy meditation on the violence of an exit.","sonnet":"The speaker addresses someone who chose a public self-destruction that those in power could not stop, expressing regret at never having met them and a kind of grim admiration for someone who resisted a system that was too strong.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick built this around the 1987 press-conference suicide of Pennsylvania official Budd Dwyer, who put a revolver in his mouth before rolling cameras rather than accept a bribery sentence he insisted was unjust. The sardonic congratulation aimed at the shooter works as a mask over grief and reluctant awe: Patrick frames the act as a final refusal to let accusers own the last word, even while granting that most people judge the act itself a tragic mistake. Movement seals the point when the second verse turns to survivors left rattled and hardened by having watched, then the closing loop, repeating a longing to have known the dead man, drops all swagger and lands as plain mourning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written in 1991, three years before Kurt Cobain’s death — when a Seattle DJ pinned the song on Cobain anyway, Patrick pointed to the copyright date and made sure Dave Grohl and Krist Novoselic personally knew it was never about Kurt.","themes":["suicide","death & grief","anger & defiance"],"means":"sardonic salute collapsing into plain mourning","built":"closing loop strips swagger and lands as grief"},
"filter~i-keep-flowers-around": {"haiku":"A partner's repeated lies and gaslighting, with the speaker keeping defensive rituals (flowers, bottles) to cope with persistent deception.","opus":"Richard Patrick, Filter's frontman whose own recovery shadows much of his catalog, keeps bottles and flowers around a house where lies are constant. The narrator holds his head down and his spirits low, watching a partner deceive him to his face, clinging to a fragile hope that love might be enough to survive the deceit. The domestic weariness sits heavy beneath the alt-rock churn.","sonnet":"Sustained deception in a relationship, witnessed and endured. The speaker keeps small rituals — bottles, flowers — as quiet armor against a partner whose lies are constant and transparent. Beneath the resignation lives a fragile hope that love may be enough to survive what the lies have done.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick, several years sober by this record, tracks a wounded man who anesthetizes himself with liquor and a bowed posture while someone close deceives him to his face. The turn arrives when the bottles give way to flowers, funeral offerings kept close, and the same lowered gaze becomes the only shelter he can mount inside a rigged domestic argument. He grants that the deceiver never even grasps how their own words contradict themselves, yet he still gambles that affection might survive the widening gaps between each successive falsehood. The hammered refrain about being lied to erodes into stammered single syllables, the accusation collapsing under its own repetition until only the raw fact of betrayal stays audible.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"the accusation hammered until it collapses into single syllables","built":"bottles giving way to funeral flowers marks the pivot without announcing it"},
"filter~i-m-not-the-only-one": {"haiku":"A lover questions whether they truly matter to their partner. The repeated revelation that they're not alone in the relationship drives the betrayal home.","opus":"A relationship's grim moment of truth: the narrator presses his partner to admit whether he really is her sole love, fearing this may be the final occasion he sees or is intimate with her. The devastating reply comes back that he is one of several.","sonnet":"A man confronting the knowledge that the person he loves has other partners, asking whether she still considers him her own — the revelation that he is not the only one is both accusation and devastated question.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the mask of a tender breakup ballad, but the title cuts two ways and the song turns on that pivot. The verses build the intimacy of a man begging to be the chosen one, asking her to name him as her man, framing what she wants as something he bleeds, right up until the climax detonates the phrase: I'm not the only one. What sounded like a plea to be her exclusive love flips into the raw suspicion that he isn't exclusive to her, and the frantic, repeated demand to know what she said turns the love song into an accusation of a betrayal he can't stop replaying. That compulsive repetition is the whole point of the ending: he isn't resolving the wound so much as circling it, the same line hammered until it stops being a question and hardens into a verdict.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"a plea to be chosen flipped into a betrayal he cannot stop replaying","built":"the title phrase pivots from tender bid to raw accusation mid-song"},
"filter~i-will-lead-you": {"haiku":"Deception and false promises from a manipulative guide who admits they are a liar.","opus":"A seductive, menacing come-on from a narrator who confesses outright that he is a deception, offering a soaring journey through the heavens while steering his target toward a funeral blaze. Pretty talk of blossoms and flawless embraces disguises the trap. Filter, Richard Patrick's post-Nine Inch Nails outfit, thread danger through sweetness, a false prophet luring you toward ruin.","sonnet":"A seducer who openly admits they are a lie — leading someone place to place with beautiful promises, through scenery and sweetness and perfect kisses, toward a pyre. The confession is built into the refrain; the speaker knows exactly what they are doing and does it anyway, meeting their victim face to face at the destination.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seducer's pitch delivered by a narrator who admits he is a fraud in the same breath he uses to charm. Richard Patrick threads an industrial hush around a voice offering a joyride and a soul emptied then refilled with flowers, sweetening the sale with a flawless embrace and the genuine article. The tender machinery inverts: the guide is walking his mark toward a funeral fire, and the intimacy he keeps vowing collapses into a reckoning met eye to eye. What sounds like courtship is a lure with the confession built into it, and knowing he lies is exactly what fails to keep you out of the car.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","artifice & performance","death & grief"],"means":"seduction powered by a confession the mark cannot resist","built":"the fraud's admission is built into the same breath as the charm"},
"filter~in-dreams": {"haiku":"Someone challenges another's false reality and vows to liberate them from delusion, though trust has been broken.","opus":"Richard Patrick delivers this as a manipulator's seductive pitch, luring a listener with promises to free them, dazzle them and rewrite their future. The flattery of the target's wariness slowly curdles, resolving into a cold, bitter admission of broken trust: whatever faith once existed will never be extended again.","sonnet":"A guide — confident, borderline messianic — promises to end the dream and remake fate. The narrator has been betrayed before and will not believe again, yet the figure pressing forward claims to show what truly keeps the other breathing, insisting the transformation is imminent.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seducer beckons a wounded skeptic toward some promised revelation, cataloguing their scars and coldness as proof they are worth converting into a true believer at last. Richard Patrick, writing sober on this war-haunted record after years lost to addiction, turns that vocabulary of liberation and changed fate into something coercive rather than tender, a savior figure who withholds and manipulates. The wager collapses in the mantra where the singer confesses he once trusted this same figure and swears he never will again, so the grand offer of awakening curdles into a spell being deliberately shattered by someone who has finally seen through it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","faith & the occult"],"means":"a salvation offer recognized and deliberately shattered by the converted","built":"the grand awakening mantra flips into a spell being broken from inside"},
"filter~it-can-never-be-the-same":{"haiku":"Betrayal and permanent damage to a relationship where trust is broken and change is irreversible.","opus":"Filter, Richard Patrick's industrial-rock project, circle an addictive, damaging bond the narrator both resents and can't leave. He keeps arriving at one certainty, that things will never go back to how they were, while openly admitting he can't explain the pull, turning that baffled confusion into the song's hook.","sonnet":"The permanent damage of a betrayal — someone who is simultaneously blamed and longed for, held responsible and still held close in the speaker's memory. The song won't resolve the contradiction; it just confirms that the relationship is broken beyond recovery and that the speaker genuinely doesn't know why it had to go this way.","src":"haiku"},
"filter~it-s-gonna-kill-me": {"haiku":"A girl obsesses and controls the speaker; her grip is inescapable and will eventually destroy him, yet he cannot let her go.","opus":"Richard Patrick on obsessive attachment as a slow poison, a lover reduced to a compulsion held close like a phone to the ear. The dazed refusal to answer any question maps the paralysis of being consumed by someone.","sonnet":"An obsessive infatuation with a woman who exists only as a voice through a phone — the speaker is completely unmoored by her, wandering, screaming, dreaming, unable to locate himself without her.","src":"haiku","fable":"A romance is scored as slow poison, the beloved fused with the telephone that becomes her only proxy. Richard Patrick shrinks her to a receiver pressed against the skull, so intimacy arrives as a plastic surrogate rather than a body. Between the hooks the speaker collapses into a stammer of unanswered questions, every destination and reply dissolving into the same shrugged blank. What kills, then, is not the woman but the dependency: a caller who cannot name where to go, only that she keeps the grip he has lost.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"intimacy arriving only as a plastic surrogate pressed to the skull","built":"the beloved shrunk to a telephone, dependency killing in her place"},
"filter~it-s-got-to-be-right-now": {"haiku":"Someone consumes the narrator and has branded them with sin and pain, yet the narrator insists on immediate change and desires her return despite heartache.","opus":"Richard Patrick's post-industrial project frames an intoxicating, corrosive love as both salvation and poison, a silver-tongued figure who consumes the narrator. Beneath the urgent demand for immediate change lies a bruised confession of missing someone who crept under the skin and branded him with sin, leaving his heart cold.","sonnet":"A consuming obsession demands immediate satisfaction. Someone has burrowed under the narrator's skin, burning and branding from within, and the craving for them — laced, corrupting, transformative — cannot be deferred. The pull is total: it must happen now or it means nothing.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick, whose own recovery shadows much of Filter's catalog, writes addiction as a love affair. The verses court a seductive supplier-figure, all persuasive charm and a chemical offered like a spoonful, while the chorus fixes on the addict's real logic: not later, not tomorrow, but this instant. That demand for immediacy is the craving itself, hammered flat by repetition until want and compulsion stop being distinguishable. Only the late turn admits the cost, when the beloved poison has burned under the skin and left the heart going cold.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"craving and compulsion hammered flat until they stop being distinguishable","built":"addiction written as a courtship, the supplier-figure framed as seductive beloved"},
"filter~it-s-just-you": {"haiku":"A person draws strength from another during darkness and pain, finding healing through their presence and support while vowing to pass that care forward.","opus":"A rare tender turn from Richard Patrick, this is a plea for mutual healing and endurance, two people holding each other through the cold and the darkest hour before light. Gratitude threads through the exchange of hold me, heal me, framing love as something to receive and then pass along.","sonnet":"In the grip of grief and cold, one relationship becomes the only source of steadiness. The narrator asks to be held, healed, taught — a full catalogue of what intimacy can offer — and promises to pass that sustaining force along to others once he has received it. Love circulates as survival.","src":"haiku","fable":"A gratitude song built as a closed loop between two people, and Richard Patrick — writing sober and settled well past Filter's rage-and-electronics years — makes recovery its unspoken subject. The winter-cold imagery frames despair as weather to be endured, and the payoff is the belief that darkness precedes light held steady by someone who keeps the long view when the singer cannot. What lifts it above a simple thank-you is the last movement: the litany of small demands he asks of his partner ends not in taking but in passing it along, so the healing he receives becomes a debt he owes forward. The title's smallness is the point — it's just you, it's just me is offered not as a limit but as sufficiency, the whole apparatus of survival shrunk down to a mutual pact of two.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"healing received reframed as a debt owed forward","built":"the title's smallness pitched as sufficiency rather than a limit"},
"filter~it-s-my-time": {"haiku":"Someone accepts their death as inevitable and approaching, resigned to execution or fate while recognizing their time has come.","opus":"Filter strip back the aggression for a hushed meditation on facing execution, a condemned narrator watching an indifferent town forget the killed. Amid the hangman's stone eyes and the worn rope, his quiet plea of please not me collapses into acceptance that his time has come regardless.","sonnet":"Death approaches with the calm inevitability of nightfall. A town and its hills will move on and forget; a rope hangs worn and weathered. The narrator oscillates between pleading for exemption and accepting the sentence — not with peace, but with the raw knowledge that this moment was always coming.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about surrender to death that keeps flinching from the thing it accepts. The refrain announces the appointed hour with resignation, yet the pleading interruptions beg for exemption, so acceptance and terror run through the same current. Richard Patrick builds the reckoning on a soldier's landscape: hills that outlast the slaughter and forget it, an executioner whose gaze is carved and merciless. Against that cold sentence the voice reaches for the last warm things left, a drawn breath and a passing wind, small comforts held while the count runs out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","war & battle"],"means":"acceptance and terror running through the same current","built":"the refrain announces the hour while interruptions beg exemption"},
"filter~it-s-over": {"haiku":"Two people end their relationship, each protecting their own interests without concern for family or friendship, with the relationship now finished.","opus":"Filter distill a bitter breakup into blunt interrogation, the speaker asking whether the other ever thought of mother, father, brother, friend, or the end before turning so cold. The terse hook, watch your back, I got mine, hardens grief into mutual guard. Where Richard Patrick's band often sprawled, here the industrial-rock economy makes the wounded taunt land with cold, repeated finality.","sonnet":"A cooling relationship is surveyed with pointed questions — whether the other ever thought about family, friends, the consequences of their coldness. The emotional temperature drops into mutual self-protection: watch your own back, because the shared warmth is gone and neither party is responsible for the other any longer.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is an interrogation with the answers withheld, Richard Patrick firing a catechism of did-you-think questions at someone already gone, beyond answering, escalating from mother and father out to friends and inspiration and finally the end itself. It's the sound of a betrayal being litigated after the fact by the only party still in the room: the questions aren't seeking information, they're indicting, demanding the accused account for a coldness they've already committed. The two fixed refrains carry the emotional logic the questions can't resolve, one line stunned that a person could be this callous, the other collapsing into a flat, four-times-hammered standoff of mutual vigilance that swaps grief for hostility. That repetition is the point rather than a shortage of words: the speaker is stuck circling the same wound, and the title's verdict is the one thing never posed as a question.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written for Jeff Ward — drummer for Ministry, Lard and Nine Inch Nails’ live band, a friend of both members — who took his own life in March 1993; Nine Inch Nails’ The Downward Spiral is dedicated to him as well.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance"],"means":"indictment posed as questions the accused can never answer","built":"title verdict is the one line never posed as a question"},
"filter~jurassitol": {"haiku":"A younger generation resents inheriting debt and failure from an older one who exploited them while taking no responsibility.","opus":"A bitter generational confrontation with an old man, likely a father or authority figure, whose mistakes the narrator is forced to shoulder. Refusing to be his boy and picking up his slack, he resents inheriting the man's debts and having his own future sold off.","sonnet":"Resentment toward a negligent or absent father figure: the narrator has spent years cleaning up the older man's mistakes while being dismissed, and now finds their own future mortgaged by someone else's failures.","src":"haiku","fable":"A generational reckoning aimed squarely at a father figure, sung by a son cataloguing years spent covering for a man too proud or too oblivious to notice the debt piling up between them. The taunting address curdles into accusation as the speaker refuses the assigned role of dutiful heir, insisting he is nobody's errand-runner while still resentfully shouldering the failures dumped in his lap. Richard Patrick channels industrial abrasion into something intimate: a hostage negotiation with inheritance, where an aging parent's decline hands the younger man a wrecked estate and a mortgaged tomorrow he never asked for. The bridge drops the swagger for panic, a photograph clutched and a dawning terror that the bravado was only ever masking dread of paying a bill that outlasts the person who ran it up.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"a son inheriting a wrecked estate he never asked to shoulder","built":"the bridge drops swagger for panic, a photograph clutched as bravado cracks"},
"filter~kid-blue-from-the-short-bus-drunk-bunk": {"haiku":"Intoxication strips inhibition while isolation compounds anguish; the speaker texts into a void.","opus":"A drunken late-night texting spiral where the sender broadcasts to an audience of one, himself. Blacked out and lashing out, he mistakes the empty performance for connection, alone the whole time.","sonnet":"A portrait of a lonely drunk texting in the middle of the night — alone with only themselves as an audience, they blacked out and lashing out, heart cracked open by alcohol, the subject of the song is pitied and goaded simultaneously as they keep pressing buttons from a stupor.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of solitary intoxication mistaken for connection, where the flood of unanswered messages reaches no one but the sender, who plays to an empty room and calls it a gathering. Richard Patrick, long sober by this record after a youth that nearly killed him, writes from the far side as an unsparing witness rather than a fellow reveler, watching a man goad himself with jeers about toughness while his defenses quietly come apart. The cruelest turn is the slide into third person, where the wretched figure hardening himself against everyone becomes a specimen examined at cold clinical distance, a ruin the narrator recognizes from his own drinking years and can only pity.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Patrick has called it a fuck-you song about a drunken former band member’s late-night texts — the title winking back at Filter’s own debut.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"a ruin the sober narrator recognizes and can only pity","built":"the slide into third person turns the drinker into a clinical specimen"},
"filter~kill-the-day": {"haiku":"Escapism and withdrawal—daylight brings anxiety and loneliness; the speaker seeks refuge in darkness and curtains to avoid reality's pain.","opus":"Darkness gives way to a dawn that only wears a person down, their own mind turning against them as a familiar sense of being ground up returns, met by the numbing wish to shut their eyes and blot out the hours. A hidden reference to a pale horse hints at drugs. Filter wrap the loop of despair in heavy, weary textures, treating oblivion as the only relief.","sonnet":"Someone in the grip of a depressive cycle, cycling through days and nights that blur and betray, practices the small survival act of closing the curtains and pushing the daylight away. The day is the enemy of a mind that cannot surface. Blocking the light is not defeat — it is the only available reprieve from consciousness that has turned against itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick wrote Anthems For The Damned years into sobriety, and this song watches addiction from inside its clock. Day and night trade places with no relief; each dawn arrives as an assault rather than a fresh start. The pale narcotic that carries the user through the afternoon gets named plainly, its comfort inseparable from the wreckage it leaves. The chorus reframes drawing the blinds and sleeping through daylight as mercy, a way to erase the hours until the next fix. That is the cruelty underneath the tenderness: erasing the day poses as kindness, but the disease is doing the talking, and only the bare stripped refrain lets the habit be named without its soothing gloss.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"erasing the day posed as mercy while the disease does the talking","built":"the bare stripped refrain names the habit without its soothing gloss"},
"filter~lie-after-lie": {"haiku":"Relationships built on constant deception waste time, youth, and life itself; the singer questions whether anything genuine survives or if only wrongs remain.","opus":"A mournful stocktake of squandered years, of youth and living frittered away amid deception. The narrator wonders whether he and a companion will register only as further entries in life's long ledger of mistakes, or leave any trace at all before the chance runs out.","sonnet":"A song about wasted time and a relationship built entirely on deception — looking back on years spent with someone who was never honest, wondering if any of it amounted to anything at all.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a reckoning over squandered years, where the charge of falsehood turns inward as sharply as it points outward. Richard Patrick, years sober by Anthems For The Damned, tallies waste in blunt paired nouns until the ledger itself becomes the indictment. The mounting title stops naming one betrayal and becomes the sound of an entire life narrated dishonestly, deceptions stacked past counting. Underneath the tally sits the real dread: not whether the bond survives, but whether either party amounts to anything at all before the accounting comes due.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","nostalgia & memory","identity & becoming"],"means":"the charge of falsehood turning inward as sharply as outward","built":"the mounting title becomes the sound of a life narrated dishonestly"},
"filter~mother-e": {"haiku":"A man in hell seeks reasons and retribution, planning vengeance against those who pray. The song dwells on suffering and a mother figure tied to violence.","opus":"Richard Patrick circling a grievance and a hunger for retribution, a man in hell insisting his reasons are sound while nursing the pain he means to inflict where his target feels safest; obsession looping toward a vengeful prayer.","sonnet":"A raw, barely coherent cry of a man in torment — invoking a mother figure while threatening to strike at whoever put him there — driven more by rage and pain than any clear narrative logic.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man talks himself into violence by insisting his reasons are sound and never once naming one. The mantra repeated a dozen times is the argument, standing in for a case he cannot actually make. Richard Patrick, long open about the addiction and rage shadowing Filter, tips the mask when the vow aims not at the wronged but at where they pray. That turns grievance into desecration and exposes the righteousness as cover. Underneath, the cry to the mother is a hurt child confessing he never grew past the pain he keeps chanting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"a mantra substituting for reasons that are never named","built":"the vow aimed at where they pray turns grievance into desecration"},
"filter~my-long-walk-to-jail": {"haiku":"Incarceration and the loss of freedom, saying goodbye to family before imprisonment.","opus":"An incarcerated man's fear and humiliation, opening with an authentic prison phone recording before he bids his family farewell and confesses he has lost his swagger and defiance, a spreading sense of illness as he faces a torment of his own creation. Filter, Richard Patrick's industrial-rock project, anchor the confession in grinding weight, leaving only terror at the cell door.","sonnet":"A goodbye said from the threshold of incarceration — leaving behind family and the world outside for a place described as a small and growing hell. The defiance fades quickly into fear and shame; the swagger of not coming back collapses into the sick admission that the speaker is, simply, going to hell.","src":"haiku","fable":"Framed by a recorded jailhouse operator warning of an inmate on the line, this becomes a condemned man's inventory of everything he forfeits, addressed to the family he farewells one name at a time. Richard Patrick lets the swagger drain out between verses, conceding that his toughness and his pride were bluffs which dissolve as the sentence looms nearer, so bravado curdles into undisguised dread. The taunting refrain is aimed as much inward as at any listener, since the singer already suspects the answer is refusal, and the final stammered plea against his own departure exposes how badly the defiance was masking terror the whole time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","family & blood","death & grief"],"means":"bravado curdling into undisguised dread as the sentence looms","built":"jailhouse operator recording frames the whole song as a condemned man's confession"},
"filter~never-be-the-same": {"haiku":"A past betrayal by the listener shatters the narrator's perception of them; truth and shame become twisted into a strange psychological game of blame.","opus":"Richard Patrick circling the aftermath of a betrayal he can't stop replaying, someone who'll always carry the blame yet also always stay the same to him. The contradiction is the point, a fixation he admits he can't explain.","sonnet":"About a relationship so warped by blame and dysfunction that the narrator can never see the other person the same way again — the truth in the relationship has broken something that can't be restored, and the other person has become permanently the source of the narrator's pain.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about loving someone who has been permanently altered, yet who remains exhaustingly familiar in the same breath. Richard Patrick keeps reversing his verdict, first swearing the other person is transformed beyond return, then insisting they will stay exactly what they have always been. That contradiction is the point, and the repeated confession of not knowing why reads as honest surrender rather than evasion. Under Patrick's sneer, the imagery of contagion and fever frames intimacy as a sickness that wounds and defines at once, fusing blame with desire.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"blame and desire fused by the contagion of intimate harm","built":"the verdict keeps reversing, transformation and sameness insisted in the same breath"},
"filter~no-love": {"haiku":"A two-decade-old wound of betrayal festers into mutual disrespect; neither party cares anymore—love haunts the landscape but cannot touch them.","opus":"A disillusioned verdict that love is a haunting, not a comfort, tracing twenty years of accumulated betrayal and disrespect that becomes learned behavior; the throughline is emotional exhaustion-a couple or a self that no longer cares about the love or dreams they've lost, resigned to going on with no love at all.","sonnet":"About a twenty-year accumulation of disrespect, broken love, and mutual damage — neither side cares anymore about what was lost or what dreams were cost, and the song lands in a place of total exhaustion where love has simply ceased to exist between two people.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick turns an old wound into a ledger where every crossed boundary and every defeat gets tallied with a numbness that sounds almost proud of itself. The bitter arithmetic of accumulated slights, the disrespect earned mirrored back as the disrespect learned, frames scorn as something inherited and passed down rather than felt fresh in the moment. Yet the second verse quietly flips the accusation into origin, since the same distant reckoning that supposedly stole his sight also marks the start of a long odyssey and the making of the man now speaking. What reads as a shrug toward the discarded affection and the wreckage of dreams is really the mask, because the endlessly circling refusal keeps returning to the one thing it insists no longer moves him at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Fans hear the twenty-years-ago lines as his Nine Inch Nails exit; Patrick has confirmed only Drug Boy, on the same album, as a song about those years.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"scorn inherited and passed down rather than felt fresh","built":"the refusal keeps circling the one thing it insists no longer moves him"},
"filter~no-re-entry": {"haiku":"Once someone leaves a relationship or situation, return is impossible; past wounds and true selves are documented permanently and cannot be erased or reversed.","opus":"A meditation on departures that can't be undone, where leaving is cast as heavier than any conclusion, the exposed self laid bare in a wounded journal that others try to reconstruct with corroded fasteners. Hurt and finality entwine. Filter, Richard Patrick's industrial-rock project, lean here into their aching, melodic side, letting the pain swell instead of only detonating.","sonnet":"A song about suicidal ideation treated with aching directness — the inner life that nobody sees, the scabs that won't heal, the diary pages carrying what is too real to show. The brutal image of pages being forced back with rusty hooks makes the point: once you go, there is no return, no softening of what that exit means.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick treats a private self as a wounded diary, the raw unmasked version of a person exposed the moment a public face slips and can no longer be reassembled into something presentable. The dread lives in the finality, because the departure being argued against is suicide - the plea aimed at someone wishing themselves out of history, reminded that this threshold seals the instant it is crossed. That threat turns concrete when the hands of those left behind stitch torn leaves back into a battered journal using cruel iron fasteners, mending by mutilating, a repair that scars the record rather than restoring it and keeps the misery bound shut.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","shame & repression"],"means":"the threshold named as sealing the instant it is crossed","built":"mending torn journal leaves with iron fasteners — repair that scars rather than restores"},
"filter~nothing-in-my-hands": {"haiku":"Powerlessness and loss of autonomy, as freedom is stolen and the individual is left with nothing to offer or claim.","opus":"A defiant stand against authorities who have seized people's freedom and let justice die. The narrator, forced face down on the ground, insists he holds nothing and has told them all he has.","sonnet":"A stripped-down statement of powerlessness: freedoms have been confiscated and justice is dead, leaving the speaker with nothing to offer and nowhere to turn but down.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick, a recovering-addict frontman who has long trusted repetition as a battering ram, stages a standoff between an interrogator certain he knows the speaker's mind and a man holding empty palms with no leverage to bargain. The pivot from your stolen freedom to our stolen freedom pulls a private grievance into collective indictment, so the flat verdict of dead justice hardens from personal complaint into something chanted against an entire apparatus. Attention then narrows to a body pressed into the dirt, arms spread and offering nothing, that final surrender hammered until it stops sounding like a plea and becomes the only honest statement left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written about the Ferguson shooting of Michael Brown — Patrick described it as being about people running from arrest and being shot in the back.","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","surveillance & control"],"means":"private grievance escalated into collective indictment","built":"empty palms repeated until surrender becomes the only honest statement"},
"filter~obliteration": {"haiku":"Self-destruction accelerates with each cycle; the speaker stands one step from annihilation, knowing how to ignite the fire but unable to stop the pyre.","opus":"A narrator one step from total self-destruction, numb to a darkening world and hooked on lighting fires that consume him. No amount is ever enough till nothing of him remains to burn.","sonnet":"About total psychological disintegration — the narrator is burning themselves down, losing sanity and soul, watching themselves become ash in a world gone numb. The emotional core is a kind of masochistic compulsion: they light the fire and feed it until there is nothing left, biting into pain because numbness is worse, and arriving at the edge of obliteration as the only honest destination.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is addiction narrated from the lip of the drain, where a self measures its own ruin in inches and finds the final inch almost welcome. Richard Patrick, who got sober before rebuilding Filter, writes compulsion as combustion: a spark he lights too well becomes a funeral fire, lungs scorched, blood on the tongue, an appetite that quiets only at zero. The chant pins the narrator a fixed distance from vanishing, and his flat refusal to close that gap is both the horror and the awful pull of the thing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","suicide"],"means":"the ruin measured in inches and almost welcomed","built":"the fixed distance from vanishing held open as the whole structure"},
"filter~one": {"haiku":"Isolation devastates after connection; the speaker's loneliness intensifies knowing what loss means.","opus":"A cover of the Harry Nilsson classic about the flattening solitude left when someone departs. A single person becomes the bleakest possible count, the narrator killing time inventing verses about a past that's no longer any use.","sonnet":"A cover of Three Dog Night's classic: the unbearable loneliness of being alone after a relationship ends, with the speaker spending their days nostalgically constructing memories rather than moving forward.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick runs Harry Nilsson's arithmetic of grief through Filter's industrial abrasion, so a tidy tune about a solitary integer curdles into something raw, sleeved in distortion and a clenched, cornered delivery. The whole conceit turns loss into cold counting, where a single digit becomes the smallest, meanest quantity a person can be reduced to once someone vacates the other half of a pair. Patrick strands his narrator alone with the tally, stitching worthless little verses out of a vanished past, until the tally itself reads as a mind circling one wound it keeps helplessly touching.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"grief reduced to its coldest arithmetic, a single digit","built":"Harry Nilsson's tidy tune run through industrial distortion and clenched delivery"},
"filter~only-you": {"haiku":"Hope that one person can prevent apocalypse or provide meaning in a collapsing world.","opus":"Filter soften Richard Patrick's usual industrial edge into something warmer, widening private devotion into dread of the whole species dying out. Grieving the deceptions imposed on a loved one and fearing collective doom, the narrator finds that this person's smile alone lets him quiet his fears and begin again, love held up as the sole anchor against a fraying world.","sonnet":"Environmental alarm wrapped in intimacy — the speaker watching the world approach a breaking point, species disappearing, systems falling out of sync, and finding the only anchor against panic in one specific person's smile. The song moves between collective dread and private reassurance, with only you functioning as both address and lifeline.","src":"haiku","fable":"From Filter's 2008 anti-war record Anthems For The Damned, this is a love song wearing an extinction song's clothes — Richard Patrick sets private tenderness against the sense of a civilization running out of time. The verses keep returning to a lie told to someone younger and a sky deliberately limited for them, so the dread is generational: the fear isn't only that we'll vanish but that we've already sold off the future of the person being addressed. The refrain is the turn — against all that unraveling, the beloved's face is the one thing that briefly cancels the worry, which is why only you carries a double charge: the sole reason to keep going, and the sole thing left worth losing. The song never resolves that tension; the closing lines let disappear and only you trade off in an endless loop, love and annihilation locked together with no last word given to either.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"the beloved's face briefly cancelling civilization's unraveling","built":"disappear and only you trade off in a closing loop with no last word given to either"},
"filter~pride-flag": {"haiku":"After overcoming oppression and hatred from religious authority figures, people reject superstition and judgement to stand openly in pride.","opus":"Richard Patrick's stand against hatred wrapped in inherited faith and self-righteousness, calling out the petty tyrant who builds places to judge. The turn is triumphant, a refusal to hide any longer, dignity claimed openly.","sonnet":"A declaration of LGBTQ victory over religious bigotry and hate — the battle has been won, the old faith's hostility can be set aside, and there is no longer any need to hide or run.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick turns his scrutiny on the zealot who mistakes certainty for conviction, sketching a figure whose panic hardens into cruelty and whose place of worship doubles as a courtroom for everyone outside the fold. The chorus then wrests the mood upward, framing survival as a battle already behind and urging that inherited scripture with its accumulated bigotry be set down rather than dragged forward. What lands is the reversal of the title: pride here is not the accuser's swagger but the quiet standing-upright of those he tried to shame into concealment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"the title's pride wrenched from the accuser and given to the shamed","built":"the zealot's place of worship reframed as a courtroom for the outside"},
"filter~sand":{"opus":"A blunt anti-war protest naming George Bush directly, railing against a people ground down and misled while soldiers are sent to die in the desert. Filter channel post-invasion disillusionment into a call to take a stand, the one comfort being that ordinary people still have each other to trust when leaders betray them.","src":"opus"},
"filter~self-inflicted": {"haiku":"The narrator finally sees harm caused by another person and confronts them, then spirals into acceleration. He frames the crisis as the other person's self-created consequence.","opus":"Richard Patrick turns an accusation into cold vindication, the wronged party finally seeing what someone did and forcing them to face the impact. The chill is in the phrasing of blame, everything that follows framed as the target's own doing, driven until the wheels come off.","sonnet":"A confrontation where the speaker makes someone face the consequences of what they did — the damage they caused is being reflected back, and the speaker refuses to absorb it anymore, declaring it self-inflicted and bracing for the collision.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick, long sober by this point in Filter's run, writes an accusation that keeps trying to relocate blame it cannot actually hold, insisting the harm was earned even as the phrasing quietly admits a hand was raised. The confrontation curdles into address that nobody answers, a demand for attention repeated until the silence on the receiving end becomes the real subject and the taunt lands on empty air. When the pressure finally crests, argument gives way to a wrecked engine pushed past its limits toward a sky torn open, and the injury named as chosen reads at last as the speaker's own, the finger pointing outward while the damage stays at home.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"blame pointed outward while the damage stays at home","built":"the injury named as chosen lands finally on the speaker, not the silent addressee"},
"filter~shot-from-the-sun": {"haiku":"Violence, propaganda, and societal control keeping people in submission.","opus":"A bleak meditation on lives ground down by systems that teach hatred and price freedom in blood, each casualty just one of a million more while the survivor feels the cold at his shoulder. Bullet-scarred walls make the violence literal. Filter, Richard Patrick's project, channels its industrial-rock heaviness here into a numbed protest against institutional cruelty that eats a person from the inside.","sonnet":"A song about systemic pressure and the casualties it quietly accumulates — bullets left in walls, lectures that carve resentment into people, the cost of freedom marked onto bodies that keep them in line. One life lost is treated as routine, one down among millions more, the catastrophe too total to isolate into individual tragedy.","src":"haiku","fable":"Filter's Richard Patrick builds a soldier's-eye protest from scarred plaster and spent rounds, insisting sunlight only clarifies once weaponized, so illumination and violence collapse into a single desolate instrument. The institutions that schooled and drilled a generation return as resentment dug bone-deep, with freedom itself billed as a debt that pins bodies rigid and enforces obedience. Against the tolling refrain of one soldier fallen and countless still queued behind him, the wound simply persists and consumes, each casualty a ledger entry that alters nothing and cools the survivor's nerve into numb dread.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"illumination and violence collapsed into one desolate instrument","built":"freedom framed as a debt that pins bodies rigid rather than releases them"},
"filter~skinny": {"haiku":"The speaker critiques shallow, privileged people caught in self-deception while condemning others; fake smiles hide emptiness and moral bankruptcy.","opus":"Richard Patrick eviscerates a sheltered, image-obsessed innocence and the hollow winners who smile through their sins. The refrain warns that guarding appearances above all keeps you alive but dead inside.","sonnet":"About someone trapped in a small, protected, self-righteous world who keeps faking their way through life — the narrator warns them not to let others see through the performance, and that their apparent victories mean nothing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survival manual pressed on someone kept cloistered and fragile, drilled never to show weakness to the watchers who would exploit it. The verses first sketch a hothouse innocent, tangled and feverish, then round on the winners whose flawless grins conceal a rot their victory has only just unleashed. Richard Patrick frames toughness as the tax on staying alive: whatever hollows you out and leaves you weeping still beats the alternative, since bending inward is preferable to being buried.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","surveillance & control","shame & repression"],"means":"toughness framed as the price of surviving the watchers","built":"the winners' flawless grins made to carry the rot they conceal"},
"filter~so-cool": {"haiku":"Someone habitually hurts others, killing relationships while believing their cruelty is cool; the speaker questions the logic of this destructive pride.","opus":"A sneering takedown of someone who mistook cruelty for cool, a maker of people sad and dead who thought that made them impressive. Patrick plays baffled contempt, refusing to grant the swagger any of the respect it demanded.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone whose recklessness and cruelty — framed as being 'cool' — has left people sad and dead; the narrator cannot comprehend how that persona is maintained in the face of the damage it causes.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sneering address to someone who mistook cruelty for charisma, the taunting title phrase repeated until it curdles from compliment into verdict against a figure who leaves wreckage and calls it style. Richard Patrick, fresh from touring behind Trent Reznor, weaponizes that industrial swagger as mock-bafflement: the confusion he keeps professing is contempt wearing a straight face, denying the strutting subject the awe they crave. The pivot toward complicity, the survivor tallying damage and asking whether the ruin was entertaining, tips the endless chant from insult into diagnosis, exposing bravado as something hollow beneath the pose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"mock-admiration as contempt denying the bravado its reward","built":"taunting title repeats until compliment curdles into verdict"},
"filter~so-i-quit": {"fable":"Built almost entirely on repetition, the song works by grinding one accusation into a chant: a partner whose affection has been legally licensed and reduced to a transaction, until the sneering word legal becomes its own indictment. The title lands as a mantra of withdrawal — the wronged party announcing his exit over and over, as if saying it enough will make the walking-away feel like a choice rather than a defeat. But that's the mask, and Patrick lets it slip: the bridge detonates the pose of cool departure into a naked death-threat, exposing the quitting as a thin lid over rage. Coming from the Amalgamut era, when his own drinking was pulling the band apart, the refrain reads less like a clean break than an addict's promise — the thing you swear you're done with, screamed loud enough to drown out the fact that you aren't.","fableDeep":"About a dozen distinct words do all the work, and 'legal' is the loaded one — repeated like a sneer until legitimacy itself becomes the insult: the thing is contracted, sanctioned, above-board, and that's exactly what makes it whorish. 'Quit' is employment language, not romance language — you quit a job, and calling the exit that flatly says what the arrangement always was. The structure is the meaning: the song quits calmly, almost boredly, for three minutes — then the composure it never advertised snaps, and the coda's screamed threat shows what the flat repetition was holding down the whole time.","haiku":"Rejecting a hypocritical situation and quitting; fed up with fakeness and choosing to walk away.","opus":"Richard Patrick's venomous kiss-off to a partner who made a fool of themselves and of him, the calm dismissal curdling into open rage. Walking away is the only power left, and it barely holds the fury back.","sonnet":"The speaker severs a relationship they view as cheap and degrading, moving from bitter contempt to an explosive threat, and declares the break final.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the cool exit announced over and over as a lid over rage that never closes","built":"the bridge detonates the pose of clean departure into a naked death-threat"},
"filter~soldiers-of-misfortune": {"haiku":"The speaker empathizes with those crushed by misfortune and suffering, wanting to break free from cycles of pain while recognizing the world's inescapable cruelty.","opus":"An anti-war lament that longs to break out of a hollow, lonely country and wake from history's endless repetition. Filter turn their sympathy toward young soldiers sent off to die, whether they end up in a grave or a homecoming parade, the closing lines making plain that many simply never return.","sonnet":"An anti-war lament for soldiers sent to fight pointless wars under false pretenses, with the chorus borrowing WWI-era imagery to underscore that nothing changes — men keep dying while the reasons dissolve into lies.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick sets an antiwar lament against Filter's usual industrial abrasion, mourning the men fed into conflict as casualties of both bad luck and deliberate deception. The two labels do the argument's work: misfortune names the random cruelty of who ends up in uniform, while distortion names the lies that put them there. Its cruelest turn is the doubled ritual, where the same enlisted man is honored with marching bands and lowered into the earth as if these were one civic gesture. The closing echo of a Great War recruiting song, its promise to ship someone abroad, curdles into a flat refusal of any homecoming, first spoken by the singer and then handed to a nameless third man who is simply gone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Sparked by correspondence from Sgt. Justin Eyerly, a Filter fan killed in Iraq — the song’s anti-war, pro-troops split was written for a real correspondent who never came home.","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"marching bands and burial collapsed into one civic gesture of expenditure","built":"a Great War recruiting promise curdles into a flat refusal of homecoming"},
"filter~spent": {"haiku":"The narrator leaves voicemail observations about the world's apathy and meaninglessness, questioning whether anyone truly cares or understands.","opus":"Richard Patrick's disgust at apathy and burnout, half a broken voicemail to someone who let him down, half self-recognition that he's one of the drained too. It's the ugly exhaustion of watching everyone, yourself included, become just another discarded thing.","sonnet":"A song about a friendship or relationship that has gone completely cold — the narrator wanted to say something but couldn't get through, watches their friends rot in apathy, and ultimately dismisses this person as just another crossed-out name.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about drowning in the effort of connection and finding the line dead. Richard Patrick frames the whole thing as a message left on a machine near dawn, so the intimacy never lands live; everything he means to say gets swallowed by a beep and a callback that won't come. He diagnoses his circle mercilessly, seeing friends gone numb and pointless, then swings the same verdict onto himself in a chorus that keeps asking whether he isn't just as complicit, just as trapped. Short Bus wears a Gen-X sneer throughout, but here it curdles into self-indictment. The closing chant shrinks a person down to an interchangeable placeholder, a crossed-out nobody repeated flat until it reads as both an accusation flung outward and a portrait he can't shake.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The answering-machine voice that opens the song is real found audio — Matt Drvenkar, the new boyfriend of Patrick’s ex, whose stacked-up messages the band taped and found hysterical; Patrick told the story for the album’s 25th anniversary.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","shame & repression","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"self-indictment arriving through a call that never connects","built":"message left on a machine so intimacy never lands live"},
"filter~stuck-in-here": {"haiku":"The speaker feels trapped in a confining situation with no escape, repeatedly acknowledging their imprisoned state and attempting psychological avoidance without success. The song is about inescapable confinement and stagnation.","opus":"A sense of being trapped, stuck, and stalled in low gear and fear, urging oneself to shut the door, close one's eyes, and ignore it, while resenting a job that demands hours without fair pay.","sonnet":"A short, circular song about being trapped in a low, stagnant state — unable to advance, going through the motions without pay or purpose, advised simply to close the eyes and ignore it — with no clear resolution offered.","src":"haiku","fable":"Filter's Short Bus is Richard Patrick's debut, industrial rock built on claustrophobia and self-directed rage. The meaning lives in its cycling: the verse never advances, each line resetting to the same confinement, so the form enacts the paralysis rather than narrating it. Its only escape route is a retreat inward through shut eyes and memory, a coping ritual that reads as surrender dressed up as relief. The second verse turns outward with brittle accusations about a rigged bargain, labor taken without reward and days kept without their hours. But the machinery drags everything back to the trapped refrain, so the flicker of anger resolves nothing and no door actually opens.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance"],"means":"paralysis enacted by form that refuses to advance","built":"cycling verse resets to the same line so no door opens"},
"filter~surprise": {"haiku":"Redemption and grace found in another person; someone talks the singer down from despair and becomes their entire world, offering genuine love without lies.","opus":"Richard Patrick, in recovery, writes a gratitude song to the woman who talks him back from the edge, love as a steadying light after a life on the run, devotion offered plainly to someone who saved him from himself.","sonnet":"A song about a woman who pulls the narrator back from the edge and becomes his entire world — she teaches, she grounds, she shines, and the surprise is that someone so full of light appeared in the middle of darkness.","src":"haiku","fable":"Coming from Richard Patrick, whose Filter catalog is built on self-destruction and rage, this is the surprise of the title: a man who arrives ruined by his own night finds someone who talks him back from the ledge, and the whole song reorganizes itself around her as the light he orbits rather than the drug or the fury he used to. The verses stay in his damaged register while the hook flips into worship, and the movement of the piece is exactly that flip from someone with everything to lose to someone content to simply bask in another person's glow. That she has nothing yet holds on tight and lives right is the real argument: her steadiness, not her rescue, is what he's grateful for.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"someone else's steadiness replacing the drug as the thing he orbits","built":"verses stay in the damaged register while the hook flips into worship"},
"filter~take-a-picture": {"haiku":"Obsession with documentation and external validation in a world of erased memory.","opus":"A song about a dissociative, blacked-out state on an airplane where he can't trust he'll remember, so he begs to be photographed feeling reborn yet exposed, rejecting notions of privacy and sanctity and reaching for his father's approval.","sonnet":"Written about Richard Patrick's blackout on a plane, the song captures the vulnerability of self-erasure — waking up naked and exposed, feeling newborn and stripped of privacy — and ends as a plaintive address to his father, asking if he can be seen and remembered.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick reportedly wrote this after a blackout bender aboard a plane, and the song sits inside that dissolve: a body that feels borrowed by strangers, a mind that already knows the night will vanish before it can be held. The plea to be photographed is a request for external proof of a self the drinker cannot keep, memory outsourced to a lens because the person living it will not survive the flight intact. Beneath the acoustic warmth and the newborn imagery of a clean start runs something colder, a refusal of other people's supposed decency and a taunt aimed at his father, so the anthem of rebirth doubles as a hungover confession where clarity is only a mask worn over erasure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance"],"means":"memory outsourced to a camera because the drinker won't survive the flight","built":"acoustic warmth and newborn imagery worn over a hungover confession of erasure"},
"filter~take-a-picture-radio-edit": {"haiku":"A commentary on memory loss and loss of privacy in a medicalized or invasive state. The speaker feels dislocated from their body and begs for documentation since they won't remember anything.","opus":"Filter's Richard Patrick wrote this dissociated, drifting song about a woozy sense of exposure and rebirth during a flight, asking to be photographed because he'll have no memory of the moment. He rejects ideas of holiness and being left alone, capped by a barbed jab at his father, the track a loosened, half-conscious purge.","sonnet":"A fragile, dissociated moment on an airplane — the speaker feels newborn and exposed, asking someone to photograph him because he knows he won't remember, and reaching out toward a distant father.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick reportedly wrote this after a blackout bender aboard a plane, and the song sits inside that dissolve: a body that feels borrowed by strangers, a mind that already knows the night will vanish before it can be held. The plea to be photographed is a request for external proof of a self the drinker cannot keep, memory outsourced to a lens because the person living it will not survive the flight intact. Beneath the acoustic warmth and the newborn imagery of a clean start runs something colder, a refusal of other people's supposed decency and a taunt aimed at his father, so the anthem of rebirth doubles as a hungover confession where clarity is only a mask worn over erasure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming","family & blood"],"means":"memory outsourced to a lens because the person living the night will not survive it","built":"the anthem of rebirth doubles as a hungover confession where clarity masks erasure"},
"filter~take-another": {"haiku":"Internal emptiness and fragmentation drive a compulsion to take or consume pieces of oneself despite knowing it's futile.","opus":"A portrait of self-erosion as compulsion: a narrator who once refused something but always suspected he'd give in feels hollowed out inside, drained of perspective and unable to bring anyone with him. Filter render this as an addiction to self-harm, ambition collapsed and scattered, the urge to gouge out another part of himself recurring endlessly, whatever he salvages promptly lost again.","sonnet":"Self-destruction as compulsion rather than choice — ambition collapsed, internal emptiness leaking through, no direction available. The drive to keep extracting pieces of the self continues in spite of knowing it hollows further; those close by cannot be taken along.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick renders the self as failing material: a body that drains like a strainer and hollows out around an inner pit, substance leaking where it should hold. Ambition drops and shatters, and the only recourse he can picture is stooping to gather the fragments off the dirt, then compulsively subtracting still more of himself. The hammered title becomes the engine of that logic, an appetite to keep carving loose pieces, so the song hears its own damage not as confession but as a hunger it cannot switch off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"compulsive self-subtraction framed as appetite not confession","built":"hammered title enacts the hunger rather than naming the damage"},
"filter~take-me-to-heaven": {"haiku":"Someone grieves a lost relationship and the void left behind, seeking transcendence or escape from fear. The speaker reaches for a spiritual or metaphorical heaven as a way to process loss and find freedom from the hurt caused by the other person's absence.","opus":"A grief-struck plea shadowed by loss, someone gone leaving only fear and the resolve to grow strong, reaching toward release and the sky, wrestling with a love of truth over comforting faith and faces.","sonnet":"A grief song about someone who died — blue eyes that went dark, burnt pages, a fear left behind — the narrator asks to be taken to wherever they went, finding strength in loss but longing to follow.","src":"haiku","fable":"A death opens this out: someone gazed to for every answer is suddenly absent, and Richard Patrick sings from the survivor's side, where grief hardens into a resolve that only became possible once the person was taken away. The verses circle a scripture scorched illegible, its wisdom no longer decipherable across generations, so what remains is a devotion to something honest rather than to inherited ritual and the pious masks people wear. Against that wreckage the chorus stays unnervingly buoyant, waving a cheerful farewell skyward, and the gap between that plea for ascension and the ruin beneath it is the ache the song deliberately refuses to close.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written from his father’s deathbed — Patrick described holding his hand and catching one last focused look of gratitude before he slipped away.","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"grief hardening into resolve only possible once the person was taken","built":"buoyant chorus waving farewell skyward over scripture scorched illegible"},
"filter~take-that-knife-out-of-my-back": {"haiku":"A sibling's betrayal cuts deep; the narrator rejects the idea that a brother has authority over him and demands he stop causing harm.","opus":"A raw confrontation with family betrayal, a brother refusing the role of keeper and demanding the wound be undone. The lasting image of domestic ruin becomes the knife the singer can't stop carrying.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a family member — a brother — whose destructive, toxic presence has been a wound in the narrator's life, the demand to remove the knife a refusal to keep absorbing someone else's damage.","src":"haiku","fable":"Betrayal here is domestic rather than romantic: the assailant is addressed as brother, and Richard Patrick turns the injury into a demand hurled at someone claiming a keeper's authority he was never granted. The imagery leans toward a childhood of household fire and family ruin, so the wound reads less as one attack than an inherited condition the narrator carries forward while insisting the blade finally come loose. That single command repeats far past the point of sense, and the sheer accumulation enacts what argument cannot settle, a grievance too raw for reasoning that can only be pounded until the accused relents or the voice wears itself out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","family & blood"],"means":"a single command repeated past the point of argument","built":"sheer accumulation enacting what reasoning cannot settle"},
"filter~thanks-bro": {"haiku":"A bitter expression of betrayal and abandonment—the narrator confronts someone who left them hanging with broken promises and now faces consequences.","opus":"A bitter, sarcastic kiss-off to a friend or ally who left the narrator stranded. Beneath the mock gratitude is real wounded pride, one person with nothing left thanking the one who had everything and still abandoned him.","sonnet":"A sarcastic, hurt farewell to someone who left the speaker stranded — they were abandoned at a critical moment, and all the speaker can manage is a bitterly sardonic \"thanks a lot, bro\" because no other words capture the betrayal.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grievance built on lopsided arithmetic: the speaker gambled his entire stake on a single throw while the other party kept a whole hand of options in reserve, and that imbalance is the wound the track keeps pressing. Richard Patrick sharpens the complaint through Filter's industrial habit of turning a curt phrase into a bludgeon, letting the sarcastic congratulations curdle from gratitude into flat accusation with every mechanical repeat. By the fade the thanks has been said so often it means nothing at all, and the polite mask finally collapses into the bitter, stranded loneliness it had been covering the entire time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"sarcastic thanks repeated until politeness collapses into bitter accusation","built":"the curt phrase bludgeoned by industrial repetition until it means nothing"},
"filter~the-best-things":{"haiku":"Life's best things are inaccessible and never free, while everyone pursues the same empty game going nowhere.","opus":"A picture of stuck, aimless lives where clear signals still lead to a dead end and rules have stopped mattering. The chorus asserts that what is finest in existence is neither without cost nor within the narrator's reach.","sonnet":"A cynical song about stasis and futility: despite apparent freedom or fresh starts, everything keeps going nowhere, and the refrain insists that the best things in life are neither free nor available — the green light promises progress that never arrives.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick builds the whole song out of the sensation of forward permission that produces no forward motion: the green light keeps flashing but the car sits still, and by verse two even reaching the mountaintop registers as nothing louder than a pin dropping. That deadened image-system, everything cleared and nothing arriving, is the engine of the chorus's flat verdict that the best things are neither for him nor free, an anhedonic bargain where the good life is both unaffordable and, once had, weightless. The gradual verb-shift across the verses, from you could be the same to you are just the same to you will play this game, tightens the accusation from possibility to sentence, so the numbness stops being a private confession and becomes the terms of a rigged game everyone is already conscripted into.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","money & the street"],"means":"anhedonia reframed as a rigged collective sentence","built":"verb tense tightens from possibility to verdict across verses","fvr":"2.4"},
"filter~the-best-things-2009-remastered-version":{"haiku":"Systemic exclusion from access and attainment, repetitive confirmation that desirable things remain forever beyond reach regardless of position.","opus":"A cynical rocker about spinning your wheels and going nowhere despite green lights and second chances. Filter's refrain that the finest things are neither his nor free skewers empty promises and rigged games, ambition souring into a shrug, motion without progress, effort that arrives nowhere.","sonnet":"Green lights that go nowhere, mountaintop achievements that feel like nothing, a system where everyone plays the same rigged game. The bitter inversion of the idiom — the best things in life aren't for me, aren't for free — frames participation in society as a car crash in slow motion.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick builds the whole song out of the sensation of forward permission that produces no forward motion: the green light keeps flashing but the car sits still, and by verse two even reaching the mountaintop registers as nothing louder than a pin dropping. That deadened image-system, everything cleared and nothing arriving, is the engine of the chorus's flat verdict that the best things are neither for him nor free, an anhedonic bargain where the good life is both unaffordable and, once had, weightless. The gradual verb-shift across the verses, from you could be the same to you are just the same to you will play this game, tightens the accusation from possibility to sentence, so the numbness stops being a private confession and becomes the terms of a rigged game everyone is already conscripted into.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","money & the street"],"means":"anhedonia reframed as a rigged collective sentence","built":"verb tense tightens from possibility to verdict across verses"},
"filter~the-hand-that-s-dealt": {"haiku":"Fate and chance determine human destiny, with authority figures controlling perception and limiting freedom; once the hand is dealt, individuals navigate predetermined constraints or seek salvation.","opus":"A gambling metaphor for fate and its slim margins of grace, hell figured as a hall where everyone waits in line for their cards. Filter frame the moment a hand is dealt as both curse and possible salvation, a fleeting chance at deliverance. A prayer that the fire of hope won't be rained out.","sonnet":"A surreal waiting room in which people are managed and observed — \"he lets us see what he wants us to see\" — and the hand that is dealt stands as the one moment where chance opens a gap in the system, a flicker of saviour that must not be rained out before it can mean anything.","src":"haiku","fable":"Filter builds a card-table cosmology where fate is a game already in motion and the players cannot read their own cards. A rigged dealer controls what everyone is permitted to notice, while a watchful partner tracks the payoff still hidden in a man's grip. The gambling frame turns salvation into a wager: one deal promises both an endless windfall and the slim odds of rescue, as if grace and luck ran the same table. Underneath the swagger sits a plea against snuffing out the one heat left, and Richard Patrick repeats it, the doubling betraying how little control the gambler truly holds.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","freedom & escape"],"means":"salvation wagered at a table where the players cannot read their own cards","built":"the repeated plea against snuffing out betrays how little the gambler controls"},
"filter~the-inevitable-relapse": {"haiku":"A user describes their addiction to various substances—drinking, snorting, smoking—with deliberate enjoyment of each method of consumption.","opus":"A grimly honest account of addiction's pull, waking with no room for hope and reaching straight for the substance, cataloguing every method of intake with a lover's relish that curdles into self-destruction. Love itself gets recast as murder, further down the hole. Filter, Richard Patrick's band, channels its industrial-rock heaviness into the mechanical repetition of craving, the chant mimicking the loop of relapse itself.","sonnet":"Addiction rendered as a seductive ritual — waking already craving, counting down to the next hit, framing every method of intake as something beloved. Love and substance blur into the same hole: each promises freedom, each pulls further down, and the cycle is presented with grim, compulsive celebration.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick, who got sober well before writing this material, stages addiction from the inside as a morning that never becomes a day, the body already exhausted on waking and groping for a phone before it can locate any reason to keep going. The dealer's pitch arrives as seduction, a promise of relief dressed as generosity, and then the chorus answers it by reducing the whole appetite to a chanted menu of verbs, the drinking and snorting and smoking repeated until the ritual has swallowed whoever was performing it. When the second verse reaches toward tenderness it can only frame the feeling as a slow killing that drags both parties deeper underground, so the fondness the narrator keeps professing for the habit reads finally as the cheerful mask stretched over the wreckage below it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the ritual chanted until it swallows whoever was performing it","built":"the chorus reduces appetite to a menu of verbs repeated past any individual"},
"filter~the-missing": {"haiku":"A speaker rejects religious platitude and passivity, preferring anger and defiance against cruelty disguised as moral authority.","opus":"Richard Patrick snarling at a merciless deity and the meek-shall-inherit doctrine that keeps the powerless swallowing abuse. Rejecting the role of anyone's willing implement, he answers religious cruelty with the plain impulse to strike back.","sonnet":"A furious rejection of a God characterized by cruelty rather than mercy — the speaker can't find peace, comfort, or even a workable moral framework in the version of religion he was handed, and the whole theological structure feels like ideological waste buried in a pit.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick stages a quarrel with a commanding voice that keeps prescribing forgiveness and submission, and the speaker recoils, finding the demand to absorb every blow and defer to the gentle both cowardly and impossible for someone wired as he is. The chorus flips the charge back onto that authority, naming it as a thing that delights in inflicting pain, and the speaker refuses to serve as the willing implement through which such pain gets delivered to anyone standing below him. When the second verse sours, the machinery of faith arrives oiled and ready yet throws off no warmth and settles no calm in the world outside, so the whole sanctified doctrine gets consigned to a grave beside every other sickness men have died defending. That closing accusation, repeated with no answering verse behind it, lands as a verdict rather than an argument, the benevolent mask torn off a maker to expose plain appetite underneath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"sanctified doctrine consigned to the grave beside every sickness men died defending","built":"the closing accusation repeated with no answering verse, landing as verdict not argument"},
"filter~the-only-way-is-the-wrong-way": {"haiku":"Resistance against forced conformity and manipulation, where every attempt to change the narrator only strengthens his defiance.","opus":"A defiant refusal to disappear or be changed, addressing someone whose broken promises and pressure only wear the speaker down with chains; he feels forced into a with-me-or-against-me bind where the only way left is the wrong way.","sonnet":"A refusal to be remade by someone who keeps pushing and making hollow promises — the narrator insists that every attempt to change them only adds more chains, and that no good path is left when the relationship is this adversarial.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick built Filter out of leaving Nine Inch Nails, and this is a survivor's taunt aimed at everyone who bet he'd wash away without that pedigree: the verses are all rhetorical questions daring the listener to admit they underestimated him. The pre-chorus reframes every attempt to fix or manage him as another link in a chain, so the hook's paradox lands as pure defiance rather than despair, if the sanctioned path only wears him down, then doing it wrong is the only honest way left to survive intact.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"survival claimed only through doing it wrong","built":"verses built entirely of rhetorical questions daring the doubter"},
"filter~the-trouble-with-angels": {"haiku":"Religious skepticism and critique of organized religion's rigid authoritarianism and threats of damnation.","opus":"A sneering rejection of religion, mocking the story of a sky-ruler and resurrection while insisting that under a microscope the miracles simply fade and free thought earns you damnation. The water-into-wine gibe recurs as a punchline. Filter, Richard Patrick's industrial-rock band, sharpened its aggression into open blasphemy on this title track, aiming its scorn at intolerant faith rather than the usual personal wreckage.","sonnet":"A skeptic's dissection of organized religion — examining resurrection stories under the cold light of a microscope until miracles dissolve, calling out a god defined more by intolerance and hellfire than by the water-to-wine grace advertised. The speaker isn't hostile so much as done: crusades lost, crowns abandoned, truth still slowly changing under a falling sky.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick, raised Catholic and now openly atheist, pits laboratory instruments against inherited doctrine and lets the empirical win, framing the water-into-wine wonder as a conjuring trick that dissolves once you put it under magnification. The chorus stages a deconversion in ordered steps, stripping away the winged guardians, then the royal ornament, then the whole militant errand of proselytizing, until the very things a believer supposedly forfeits are reframed as something reclaimed rather than surrendered. Its sharper move is the caricature of a jealous, punitive ruler who governs by rigid decree and threatens eternal fire for the crime of thinking freely, so the storm-swept collapse of certainty in the bridge lands not as catastrophe but as overdue release from that fear.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness","freedom & escape"],"means":"deconversion staged as something reclaimed rather than surrendered","built":"the chorus strips away angels then a crown then the whole missionary errand in ordered steps"},
"filter~the-wake": {"haiku":"A person struggles to change and seek forgiveness after failures, hoping to escape their basement of shame and self-imposed limits.","opus":"Richard Patrick sets a reckoning with himself in a joyless underground where decency curdles and no one improves. The song weighs whether the damage a person does can ever be pardoned, treating the aftermath of one's own cruelty as the real question and self-reform as something nearly out of reach.","sonnet":"In the aftermath of repeated failures and sustained hatred — a basement where saints turn mean — the narrator confronts the question of whether forgiveness or growth is even possible. The man in the mirror presses for an honest reckoning with what has been done and left unchanged.","src":"haiku","fable":"A reckoning staged in a spiritual basement, where even the sanctified go rotten and no one is spared the verdict. Richard Patrick wrote Anthems For The Damned six years sober, and the song reads as a sobriety inventory dressed as a descent: growth stalls, hope grows unrememberable, standards sink to the floor. The pivot is the plea to be pushed nearer to his own reflection, self-confrontation as the only exit from a mind he refuses to keep deranged. Each refrain interrogates the aftermath of harm, weighing whether pardon and erasure can coexist or whether remorse is the truer response. The closing question stays unanswered, which is the honest posture of someone still doing the work.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","faith & the occult"],"means":"self-confrontation offered as the only exit from a mind he won't keep deranged","built":"the closing question left unanswered as the honest posture of ongoing work"},
"filter~this-finger-s-for-you": {"haiku":"Betrayal and lies have ruined the speaker's reputation and will; after losing faith and drowning in gin, the speaker is done and offers a dismissive finger gesture.","opus":"Later-era Filter distills a bitter breakup into a middle-finger farewell, the narrator done with a manipulator whose lies trashed his reputation. Losing his taste for sin and his religion alike, he numbs himself with gin and insists he does not miss any of it, defiance curdled with hurt.","sonnet":"A relationship defined by deception has finally run its course. Secrets destroyed the narrator's reputation and left nothing fixable; now he drinks alone, sheds the last obligations, and delivers a blunt, unambiguous farewell. The relief is real, even if it comes soaked in bitterness.","src":"haiku","fable":"A kiss-off aimed at someone whose lies wrecked the narrator's name, tracking how betrayal hollows out the appetites that once steadied him until desire itself curdles into a resentment he keeps insisting he no longer misses. Richard Patrick, a decade sober by this record, laces the venom with recovery imagery, so buying a bottle and swallowing it alone reads less as triumph than as an old crutch quietly reclaimed under the cover of righteous fury. The obscene title gesture and the closing demand that someone bleed strip away any pretense of closure, exposing a man who dresses relapse as revenge and calls the wound a decision he can live with.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"relapse dressed as revenge and called a decision he can live with","built":"recovery imagery laced into the venom so the old crutch returns under cover of fury"},
"filter~thoughts-and-prayers": {"haiku":"Society watches mass violence and suffering with empty gestures while structural apathy and political dishonesty prevent real change. The song condemns the cycle of indifference and calculated division.","opus":"Richard Patrick's furious response to mass shootings and the hollow ritual that follows them, empty condolences and social-media performance while people bleed. It names the real design: a divided public turned against itself.","sonnet":"A furious reaction to gun violence in America — a school shooter described in cold detail — aimed squarely at politicians whose only response is hollow social media condolences while the country bleeds, deliberately divided for political gain.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick turns the reflexive condolence of a mass-shooting news cycle into an indictment of the ritual itself. An ordinary morning - the school drop-off, a blood donation - suddenly holds a shooter sealing the exits and checking his rifle, so slaughter reads as routine weather in a poisoned country. The refrain pins the pious slogan against the social-media reflex, letting the wounded speaker bleed while a numb public offers only reposted sympathy. Patrick's real target sharpens toward the beltway operators who preach civic virtue while gaming the rules for their own gain. Apathy, not rage, is named the deadliest trigger, and the closing chant about who profits from our division lands the point without absolution.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"apathy named as the deadliest trigger in a poisoned country","built":"an ordinary morning shattered mid-stanza so slaughter reads as routine weather"},
"filter~tremors": {"haiku":"A person watches someone drown in addiction and despair, offering help but unable to reach them as tremors and shaking consume the person they care for.","opus":"Filter render the helplessness of loving an active drug user: the narrator keeps offering a lifeline that his friend won't grasp while they shake and go under. A domestic detail signaling drug use grounds the anguish, as he waits in vain and finds himself powerless to pull them back from the addiction consuming them.","sonnet":"About watching someone you love spiral into heroin addiction — the narrator sees the track marks in the kitchen, tries to reach out, but the addict won't take the rope being offered; the song is a portrait of helpless, anguished witnessing from someone who loves but cannot save.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick, sober for over a decade by this record, writes as the helpless bystander to someone else's heroin habit rather than from inside the addiction. Every verb of rescue curdles into futility: a lifeline extended to arm's length that the drowning person still refuses to grip. The chorus draws the speaker's one hard boundary, refusing delivery of the habit to his door, the needle's evidence surfacing in the most domestic room of the house. By the bridge his patience empties into no answer, and the vow to pull the other back becomes love kept up as gesture after it has stopped working as a lifeline.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"love kept up as gesture after it stops working as a lifeline","built":"the needle's evidence surfaces in the most domestic room of the house"},
"filter~under": {"haiku":"A descent into private possession and escape, where the speaker claims absolute ownership of their world and invites surrender into a state of bliss beneath or beyond everyday reality.","opus":"A sneering study in narcissism and control, a voice claiming the whole world as its own while dispensing numbing bliss. It reads as addiction and ego fused, dragging the listener down into its private kingdom.","sonnet":"The speaker claims ownership of their own world and recommends surrendering to numbness — going under, swallowing something, sinking into bliss — as a coping strategy, while rejecting anyone else's problems as irrelevant to them.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick wields industrial-rock seduction: a coaxing voice offering a swallowed spell of bliss — the patter of a dealer, or of the pull toward going under itself. That mask reads as narcotic surrender or the invitation to self-erasure. But the refrain quietly inverts the generosity, insisting the world and everything in it is his alone, so what sounds like an offer is really a claim of ownership over the ruin — the descent, the bliss, the annihilation belong entirely to the self that chose them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control"],"means":"seductive offer revealed as a claim over ruin","built":"generous invitation inverted into ownership of the descent"},
"filter~under-remastered-version": {"haiku":"Capitulation and descent become the speaker's philosophy as they embrace the underworld, viewing resistance as futile and control as a surrender worth celebrating.","opus":"A sneering nihilist stakes out his own private world and invites you to escape into oblivion, a numb, chemical going-under offered as bliss to anyone who thinks they've got it bad.","sonnet":"A voice that has arrived at a fresh, defiant perspective on a world it finds stinky and sickening chooses to go under rather than keep fighting — welcoming the descent as its own territory, a world that belongs entirely to itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick wields industrial-rock seduction: a coaxing voice offering a swallowed spell of bliss — the patter of a dealer, or of the pull toward going under itself. That mask reads as narcotic surrender or the invitation to self-erasure. But the refrain quietly inverts the generosity, insisting the world and everything in it is his alone, so what sounds like an offer is really a claim of ownership over the ruin — the descent, the bliss, the annihilation belong entirely to the self that chose them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control"],"means":"seductive offer revealed as a claim over ruin","built":"generous invitation inverted into ownership of the descent"},
"filter~watch-the-sun-come-out-tonight": {"haiku":"A group chooses self-destruction together—dying, burning out, surrendering to a feeling too good to question—knowing this recklessness and youth won't last.","opus":"A druggy death-wish reverie inviting someone to burn out together, cold that warms the heart, the high too good to be wrong. Richard Patrick romanticizing self-destruction as a shared sunrise.","sonnet":"About a group of young people getting high together all night and burning bright until dawn, with an undercurrent of self-destruction — the cold hurts, they bake and waste away, and the invitation to \"come out and die with us tonight\" is both ecstatic and literal.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick, who fought his own addictions, inverts the title so that dawn seems to break at midnight, staging a chemical communion where a wrecked crowd mistakes its own decline for something like grace. The invitation to walk together into the dark is deliberately seductive, framing collective self-destruction as warmth and belonging, while every promise of taking everything and rising skyward carries the buried knowledge that this glow is a nervous system slowing toward stillness. When the voice concedes that being young aches worse than any pleasure could ever repay, the mask finally slips, exposing the euphoria as a story the group keeps telling itself to postpone the reckoning a little longer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"collective self-destruction framed as warmth to postpone the reckoning","built":"the title inverts so that dawn appears at midnight, making decline read as grace"},
"filter~we-hate-it-when-you-get-what-you-want": {"haiku":"Resentment toward others' success and happiness; people celebrate when those who hurt them suffer instead.","opus":"A venomous burst of resentment aimed at a manipulative liar, with the collective narrator openly savoring the target's ruin and despising any success they enjoy. The cruelty is rooted in abandonment: cast aside and left to suffer, so the vindictive delight in watching the betrayer brought low reads as overdue retaliation.","sonnet":"A bitter song about being abandoned and then watching the person who hurt you thrive — the speaker is left broken and cold while the other walks away with what they wanted, the anger curdling into something venomous and impotent.","src":"haiku","fable":"A spite anthem sung in a collective we, aimed at someone who climbed out while leaving the speakers face-down in the cold. The title is a mask worn out loud: the chorus insists it hates the target's success and loves to see them bleed, but the verses give away that the real grievance is abandonment — they had counted on this person to steady them, to make things right, and were left broken instead. So the professed hatred of the other's happiness is really thwarted dependence turned poisonous, envy standing in for a betrayal the speakers can't say plainly. The sneered command to sit down, driving the song into industrial-rock stomp, is the powerless fantasy of finally leveling someone who long ago walked away untouched.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"professed hatred of another's success masking thwarted dependence","built":"collective we titles the spite but the verses confess abandonment instead"},
"filter~welcome-to-the-fold": {"haiku":"Contempt for wealth and hypocrisy; the speaker mocks privilege while seeking chemical escape and falsely inviting others to join their degradation.","opus":"Filter's Richard Patrick sprays contempt at empty religiosity and smug mediocrity, targeting someone who toasts to nothing while drinking themselves comfortably numb. Beneath the scorn lies the true subject: a craving for the substance that props you up, the real congregation being the ritual of self-medication.","sonnet":"Pure targeted contempt for a specific person — the narrator hates their face, their greed, and their religion, fantasizes about destroying them, then pivots to needing his mother's medicine (substance) to get through the day while staying numb to it all.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick opens in pure spite, cataloguing a rival's smugness, borrowed faith and hoarded money with a venom that curdles into the fantasy of wiping out the man's very bloodline. Yet the loathing is a mirror: the adversary numbs himself into a placid haze and toasts an empty little triumph, exactly the surrender the narrator claims to despise from his own righteous distance. The mask of disgust finally slips in the closing plea, where a grown man reverts to a child begging his mother for the fix that stretches him skyward, admitting the contempt was self-portrait and that accuser and accused are folded into one craving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"contempt for a rival exposed as self-portrait of shared craving","built":"closing plea reverts to a child begging his mother for the fix"},
"filter~welcome-to-the-suck-destiny-not-luck": {"haiku":"Heartbreak and hardship forge character; destiny demands endurance through adversity.","opus":"A battered rally to pick up courage and push through heartache, insisting hardship is destiny to be met rather than bad luck. The suck is embraced as the price of keeping the fire and the fight alive.","sonnet":"A song about enduring suffering as an inescapable fact of life rather than bad luck — the narrator urges picking yourself up and facing heartache and hardship as a fixed, destined condition, not a random misfortune.","src":"haiku","fable":"Filter takes military grunt-slang — welcome to the suck, soldier shorthand for the misery of being deployed — and turns it into a survivor's pep talk, reframing the whole rotten stretch you have been handed as an assignment rather than an accident. The title's parenthetical is the entire argument: what looks like bad luck is instead destiny, a burden you were built for, so the pain and the burning are not signs to quit but proof you belong here. It is a lean, repetitive song by design, and the repetition is doing the work of the sentiment — a mantra you chant to keep marching when faith and fight have blurred into the same exhausted thing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"suffering reframed as an assignment you were built to carry","built":"the parenthetical subtitle states the entire argument before the song begins"},
"filter~what-do-you-say": {"haiku":"Nihilistic resignation to inevitable blame and meaninglessness—nothing matters because it's all chemicals and performance, so why care about anything when judgment arrives anyway.","opus":"Richard Patrick shrugs at a doomed situation with weary fatalism, insisting nothing matters and blame is coming regardless. The nihilism about thoughts being mere brain chemicals is undercut by a tender clinging to last memories and goodbyes.","sonnet":"A shrug of nihilistic resignation — nothing matters, everything is chemicals, blame will come regardless; but the song ends with a moment of genuine farewell, suggesting the nihilism is a defensive posture over something that did once mean everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built entirely around a question it refuses to let anyone answer: the title phrase loops as a taunting shrug rather than a real invitation to speak, and every verse pre-empts the response with exhaustion — none of it matters, it's all the same, someone will blame us regardless. Filter's Richard Patrick sets that fatalism at its bleakest when he reduces feeling itself to chemicals firing in the brain, stripping meaning from the very moment being lived through. The sting is in the late turn to last sweet goodbyes and looking straight into someone's eyes: after all the nihilist deflection, the mask slips and the nothing-matters posture is exposed as a defense against a parting that clearly does matter, which is exactly why the chant of the question resumes and swallows the confession whole.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"nihilist armor cracking at the goodbye it tried to erase","built":"the looping title-chant swallows the confession it finally lets slip"},
"filter~what-s-next": {"haiku":"Political manipulation and mass control by authorities who swallow, hustle, and beat people down; collective trust is the only refuge.","opus":"Filter channel post-9/11 disillusion into a protest against forces grinding people down. Richard Patrick calls out the president by name, indicting deceptions peddled to the public and troops sent off to die, the town torn from its people. Amid the futility the chorus reaches for togetherness, landing on the meager comfort that when faith in leaders dies, the crowd still has one another.","sonnet":"A furious post-9/11 dissent against a government that exploits, deceives, and hollows out its people. Rage at political leadership that wages war for sport gives way to a quieter, stubborn resolve: that trust must be rebuilt not in institutions but in each other.","src":"haiku","fable":"A protest cut off Filter's Iraq-era record, sung from inside a citizenry that feels chewed on and held back by a war it was sold on a lie. Richard Patrick names George W. Bush outright, so the enemy is not vague dread but a specific administration wringing the country dry and leaving its people exhausted and rinsed out. The chorus keeps asking what comes after, a question with no answer, and the closing stretch fixes on a single anxious refrain about being unable to tell who is on your side. Late in the song that suspicion softens into a fragile idea that ordinary people might still lean on one another once the men in charge have plainly failed them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"a citizenry asking what comes after with no answer given","built":"the unanswered chorus question hardens into paranoia about who is on your side"},
"filter~where-do-we-go-feom-here-single-edit":{"haiku":"Broken promises and emotional wreckage accumulate until separation seems inevitable despite unanswered questions.","opus":"Ground down by a lover's evasions and inconstancy, the singer craves a little relief and wonders what future a broken bond could have. A mention of a gleaming weapon signals how perilous his exhaustion has grown.","sonnet":"Someone ground down by a manipulative, fickle partner — half-truths, broken dreams, being frozen out — reaches the point of exhaustion and asks where the relationship can possibly go from here, knowing they are neither the only nor the guilty one.","src":"haiku"},
"filter~where-do-we-go-from-here": {"haiku":"A relationship breakdown where deception and loss of control erode connection, leaving both parties uncertain how to move forward or who is responsible.","opus":"Filter, Richard Patrick's industrial-rock project, confront a manipulative, greedy partner whose lies leave the singer battered and begging for a moment's relief while insisting he isn't the guilty party. A recurring warning about fantasies of a gun darkens the standoff, the band riding it between hush and eruption.","sonnet":"Someone trying to extricate themselves from a relationship built on half-truths and cruelty, asking what comes next when there is nothing left to salvage. The anger keeps surfacing — not guilt, but exhaustion at being manipulated and ignored — and the recurring image of a shiny gun suggests the question of what comes next has a darker possible answer.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tormentor's deceptions and appetite have left the narrator scraped raw, and he keeps refusing the weapon they fantasize about while insisting he is neither the sole cause nor their only son. The repeated question at the center is the whole point: it stalls, because a relationship this corrosive offers no forward direction, only the eroded control and hollowed-out soul that make him wheeze. Richard Patrick lands the collapse on one spat-out expletive, then dissolves back into that same unanswerable question echoing off itself. His plea to simply be granted relief reads less as hope than as exhaustion that already knows the answer is nowhere.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","surveillance & control"],"means":"a repeated question that stalls because no forward direction exists","built":"collapse lands on one spat-out expletive before dissolving back into the question"},
"filter~white-like-that": {"haiku":"Dismissing blame and playing the victim, refusing accountability for one's actions.","opus":"A defensive, self-punishing tirade about being scapegoated and brushed off, the narrator hurling back the slur used against him while claiming it's all one rigged contest and refusing to carry another person's charges. Identity and resentment knot into a circular jeer. Filter, Richard Patrick's industrial-rock vehicle, deliver it with abrasive contempt, defiance and self-loathing fused.","sonnet":"Defending against being blamed for something the speaker did not choose and does not want — the accusation of white trash lands as both a social label and a dismissal. The song does not fight the label so much as reject the blame game, insisting it is all the same, all a game, and asking who is really going to lose here.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Short Bus track built on the deflected blame of an argument, where every accusation gets answered not with a defense but with a shrug: it's all the same. The refrain that gives the song its name works as an ugly acceptance speech, absorbing the slur white trash and wearing it back as identity rather than wound, so that Richard Patrick turns a class insult into a flat, sneering label he'll own before you can hurt him with it. The song's real engine is its interrogation loop, the who's gonna lose that keeps escalating until it's the only line left: a question that stops seeking an answer and becomes pure attrition, the sound of a fight nobody wins collapsing into repetition. What looks like a taunt is closer to nihilist self-defense, the posture of someone who insists everything is interchangeable precisely because he's been told he's worth nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"absorbing the slur as identity before it can land as wound","built":"interrogation loop collapses into pure attrition with no exit"},
"filter~world-today": {"haiku":"The world can be beautiful but also cruel; the singer finds joy in nature yet acknowledges the harsh cost and occasional meanness of existence.","opus":"Filter boil ambivalence down to a near-childlike chant, the narrator insisting he's fond of the world while conceding it costs dearly and can turn savage. Affection and disenchantment sit side by side in the same breath, the repetition holding the contradiction open rather than resolving it.","sonnet":"A complicated affection for the world as it is — beautiful when green, brutal when mean, always carrying a steep price. The ambivalence never resolves into cynicism or acceptance; it just returns, again and again, as an honest appraisal of a planet that keeps being both things at once.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric is almost entirely one flat affirmation repeated to the point of hollowness, and that emptiness is the point: the profession of liking the world keeps arriving unprompted, insisted on rather than felt, the way you reassure yourself of something you no longer believe. Against it, two qualifiers slowly gain weight through recurrence — an ambivalent nod to the world when it is green, and the admission that it can be mean as hell at a terrible price. Coming from Richard Patrick, who wrote The Amalgamut at the bottom of his drinking years, the mantra reads less as contentment than as a white-knuckled affirmation held against evidence, gratitude drilled in as a survival tactic while the cost quietly does the honest talking underneath it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"gratitude drilled in as survival while the cost does honest talking","built":"the flatly repeated affirmation betrays the thing it can no longer feel"},
"filter~you-walk-away": {"haiku":"Internal conflict and self-sabotage through greed and self-imposed suffering leave the speaker unable to break free as another person abandons them.","opus":"Consumed by greed, fear and an inability to breathe or see straight, the speaker just wants a life of his own and refuses to give in to hate as someone he addresses keeps walking away from him.","sonnet":"The song sits at the point of a departure — the narrator is suffocated by greed and hatred in their life, unable to breathe or see clearly, and the repeated refrain of someone walking away registers both as loss and as the only possible release toward a life of one's own.","src":"haiku","fable":"Richard Patrick wrote The Amalgamut in the alcoholic tailspin that landed him in rehab right after, and this song is the sound of that unraveling caught from inside rather than described from outside. The verses collapse the self into a stammer of monosyllabic reflexes — greed, freeze, bleed, just be — a body reporting symptoms it can no longer control, until the pre-chorus's only whole sentence, the wish for a life of his own, reads less as ambition than as a plea to be given back to himself. The trick of the title is that the you who walks away is never named because it isn't a lover; it's his own steadiness, his sobriety, whatever leaves when the compulsion takes over, so the chorus stages abandonment by the very self the pre-chorus keeps begging for. What sounds like a song about being left is really addiction's dissociation dramatized as a second person: he narrates his own departure and can only watch.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"the self that departs narrated from inside the compulsion","built":"the unnamed you is sobriety leaving, not a lover walking out"},
"filter~your-bullets": {"haiku":"Weapons and harm return to their source; the apathetic world ignores suffering, and attacks rebound on the aggressor.","opus":"Richard Patrick nurses apathy and the numbness of a meaningless, bruising life, then turns the aggression back on its source, the aggressor's own violence rebounding. Wounded resentment hardening into retaliation.","sonnet":"A figure bruised by being used and ignored redirects the aggression back at the source — whatever hostility or contempt is aimed at them will be returned in kind, bullets shot back at the gun that fired them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A defiance song built on the industrial-rock logic Richard Patrick has worked since Short Bus: numbed detachment that curdles into counterattack. The verses live in that flatness first, a self-aware apathy that finds the grief of living meaningless and can't decide whether the emptiness is ugly or beautiful, and the wound of being used and unseen keeps festering because nobody will admit they see it. The chorus is the pivot from passivity to return fire, an aggression turned back on its source so the attacker's own ammunition becomes what strikes him. The refusal to develop that image any further is the point: the whole track collapses into the same three lines, the pre-chorus threat to tame and scrape you looping until retaliation is less a plan than a compulsion, the only clean gesture a bruised person has left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"attacker's ammunition turned back on its source as the only clean gesture","built":"the pre-chorus threat loops until retaliation becomes compulsion, not plan"},
"filth~severed":{"opus":"A blistering hardcore cutoff aimed at someone caught in a ruin of their own making who pins the fault on everyone else. The narrator won't keep sacrificing himself for an ingrate who repays generosity with betrayal, so he ends the relationship. Weariness sets into finality, compassion withdrawn from a person who drove off everyone who cared.","src":"opus"},
"fire-from-the-gods~soul-revolution":{"haiku":"Transcendence through spiritual resilience; ascending beyond earthly weight and void of judgment.","opus":"A rap-metal call to uprising within and without, the narrator climbing out of a personal hell to cleanse spirit and mind while raging at a corrupt order raised on inherited privilege and hypocrisy. The band fuse self-healing with political defiance, urging people to feel the revolution in heart, blood and soul and to fight back.","sonnet":"Spiritual sovereignty declared against hypocrites, industry gorillas, and the New World Order — christened in fire, praising the sun, cleaning the spirit and the mind — the revolution is internal first: feel it in your blood, live by the code, and when life beats you to the ground you get mad and fight back.","src":"haiku"},
"fire-from-the-gods~thousand-lifetimes":{"haiku":"Accumulated suffering across incarnations weighs upon the present as the narrator claims redemption through sheer endurance and refusal to surrender despite exhaustion.","src":"haiku"},
"fire-from-the-gods~thousand-lifetimes-feat-corey-glover-of-living-colour":{"haiku":"Exhausted endurance of systemic oppression, dedicating struggle to the overlooked workers who sacrifice silently.","opus":"An endurance anthem for the overworked who give until it breaks them, the cost of survival tallied across a thousand lifetimes of blood and spirit, faith held even by someone who hasn't said a prayer in a long time.","sonnet":"An anthem of exhausted endurance addressed to the overworked and overlooked: the speaker has paid with soul and blood across what feels like a thousand lifetimes, won't fake strength he doesn't have, but refuses to stop pushing back against suffocation.","src":"haiku"},
"fire-toolz~thick-flowy-glowy-sparkly-stingy-pain-mpeg":{"haiku":"A speaker marvels at surviving danger, riding painful intensity with wonder and acceptance into an uncertain future.","opus":"Pain becomes something almost beautiful, lessons taught without adult guidance and a realization that survival was always possible. Seeing the burning thing is fine, the narrator rides the thick, glowing, stinging pain into the sunset. Fire-Toolz, the maximalist project of Angel Marcloid, fuses new-age serenity with harsh electronics, letting transcendence and hurt shimmer together in a single glowing image.","sonnet":"A brief, oblique meditation on the realization that pain — vivid, textured, almost beautiful — can be ridden rather than escaped. The speaker discovers she is already in a vessel that can withstand what is burning around her. The stinging quality of hard-won knowledge becomes, unexpectedly, something she can move through and even name with wonder.","src":"haiku"},
"firmament~liquid-lush":{"opus":"A prog-metal meditation on drink and distraction as coping, a digital sedative numbing a mind stuck circling underwater. Invoking Wim Hof and Alan Watts, it wrestles with apathy, the pull of the crowd and everything already behind him figured as gravity, ultimately reaching for a self that picks living over escape.","src":"opus"},
"first-of-october~bookmobile":{"opus":"A goofy, affectionate hype song about a mobile library run by somebody's mom, celebrating a small civic good with mock-anthem enthusiasm. The joke and the sincerity are the same thing.","sonnet":"A gleeful, absurdist tribute to a friend's mother who drives a government-funded bookmobile delivering books to schoolchildren — enthusiastically and sincerely celebrated as the most exciting person in the area.","src":"sonnet"},
"first-to-eleven~happy-song":{"haiku":"Singing louder masks underlying despair and emptiness; collective escape through forced joy becomes the only strategy when genuine solutions seem impossible.","opus":"A pointedly ironic pop-punk anthem about drowning out depression by singing upbeat music louder, even as an inner voice insists life isn't worth living. Cover-band-turned-original act First to Eleven lean into the genre's habit of smuggling real despair inside cheerful, chantable hooks, the emptiness never quite drowned out.","sonnet":"Depression treated as a collective condition rather than a private failure — everyone spinning in the same carousel, the void opening up in the same way for all of them. The solution the song offers is deliberately hollow: crank up a happy song and pretend, because the world is a mess and this is the only tool available.","src":"haiku"},
"fist-of-five~tfl":{"opus":"A hard-rock portrait of a destructive infatuation rendered through gunplay imagery, the beloved as a trigger-finger lover who wears the narrator into the ground. Numbed by tequila and helpless against her pull, he plays the willing victim. Addiction, dopamine, and self-sabotage collide in a toxic love that feels like being shot repeatedly.","src":"opus"},
"fit-for-a-king~engraved":{"haiku":"Desperate prayer for divine direction meets only darkness; the speaker seeks purpose and guidance, clinging to meaning even as certainty crumbles.","opus":"A cry to God for purpose and a legacy worth remembering, fearing that everything built will crumble at the moment of surrender. Amid darkness and division it finds a fragile timelessness, refusing hopelessness while begging to be seen and heard.","sonnet":"A desperate prayer for purpose and permanence: the speaker is on their knees losing direction, asking God to see and reach down — \"will I stay a memory?\" — and the refrain about burning bridges letting light in offers fragile hope that destruction can reveal identity.","src":"haiku"},
"fit-for-a-king~falling-through-the-sky":{"haiku":"Personal failure attributed to blindness; the speaker questions their own culpability in an inevitable downfall.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with spiraling despair and self-deception, the narrator falling like a satellite while rebuilding a life he keeps destroying with stories he mistakes for truth. Fighting his own demons and gravity, he confronts how fear of the unknown isolates people. A vertiginous struggle against reality and inner collapse.","sonnet":"A sudden destabilisation of a life thought to be figured out — am I the reason, am I to blame, why didn't I notice the rain. Rebuilding a life you've killed while repeating the same stories until they become reality, Heaven and Hell feeling equidistant, the speaker stuck between gravity and the demon they should have learned to talk to.","src":"haiku"},
"fit-for-a-king~the-face-of-hate":{"haiku":"The song depicts collective resistance against oppression, with unified voices rising above hatred and reclaiming what was taken.","opus":"Fit For A King, the Texan metalcore act whose faith-tinged anthems favor perseverance, cast defiance against cruelty as a righteous campaign. The song rallies the discouraged to keep hope alive, rise together as a movement, and refuse to be beaten down, vowing to reclaim what a hateful enemy has stolen through sheer, unrelenting endurance.","sonnet":"A collective voice rises against an oppressive force that crushes truth and runs on broken ideology. The movement reclaims its identity through solidarity — climbing toward a shared victory, refusing to be silenced, staring without flinching into hatred and insisting on being named and known.","src":"haiku"},
"fit-for-a-king~the-price-of-agony":{"haiku":"Society fractures under hate and division; old systems enslave people into rigid identities while hope fights against the spreading cold.","opus":"Fit For A King confront a society torn apart by manufactured fear and taught to despise anyone different. Rejecting the systems and empty voices that dictate who people should be, the band asks whether all this so-called progress has actually made us better as the gulf between us widens. A stubborn hope resists a pride too rigid and afraid to change.","sonnet":"A society fractures along ideological lines fed by old machines and hollow voices — each generation grows colder, each divide wider, as fear and pride prevent growth. The cost of remaining stagnant is agony; underneath the noise a heartbeat of hope insists that reason and connection are still within reach.","src":"haiku"},
"fit-for-a-king~when-everything-means-nothing":{"haiku":"The narrator feels lost and suffocating under pressure to meet impossible expectations, drowning in a storm of despair and meaninglessness.","opus":"Fit for a King, a Christian metalcore band, pair personal desperation with cathartic heaviness in this portrait of anxiety and burnout. Overwhelmed by expectation and the dread of falling behind, the narrator fakes composure while feeling trapped at the center of chaos, framing his struggle as innate and pleading for help under crushing pressure.","sonnet":"Drowning in the pressure of social expectation and the fear of never measuring up — the storm metaphor becomes total, a person born into difficulty who keeps smiling and fighting while the water rises past their eyes. The plea at the end is raw: just tell me what I need, even as the song admits no answer is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"fit-for-an-autopsy~hellions":{"opus":"A deathcore rallying cry that calls the beaten-down to revolt against tyranny, summoning ancient Babylonian justice and its eye-for-an-eye vengeance against corrupt rulers and lying preachers. Fit For An Autopsy paint faith as a jail and uprising as freedom, urging the caged to impale themselves and ascend. Righteous rage made apocalyptic.","src":"opus"},
"fit-for-an-autopsy~the-sea-of-tragic-beasts":{"opus":"A bleak portrait of humanity as a doomed menagerie of predators trapped in an endless cycle of preying on the weak, with cruelty itself the engine of survival. The deathcore band invites the listener to drop any comforting illusions and accept an ugly truth about what people really are.","sonnet":"A brutal framing of humanity as predatory animals caught in an evolutionary cycle of carnage — the song depicts people as tragic beasts devouring each other across centuries, innocent lives burned in the process, with the only invitation being to fall into the fire together rather than pretend otherwise.","src":"sonnet"},
"fit-for-rivals~secret":{"opus":"The speaker sees clean through a manipulative, sharp-tongued predator living off someone else's name and feeding on the vulnerable, and refuses to keep quiet. Buried though she is, she promises to surface, exposure treated as only a matter of time.","src":"opus"},
"five-finger-death-punch~house-of-the-rising-sun":{"haiku":"A cautionary tale warns of destruction wrought by vice, gambling, and moral compromise that ensnares the innocent in generational ruin.","opus":"The old New Orleans ballad of a life ruined by vice, delivered as a hard-rock warning from a man too far gone to escape it, cautioning children off the road he can't leave.","sonnet":"A man confesses that a notorious den of vice in Sin City has been his ruin, tracing the path — gambling father, complicit upbringing — that made destruction almost inevitable, and warning others never to follow where he has gone.","src":"haiku"},
"five-finger-death-punch~no-one-gets-left-behind":{"haiku":"Political hypocrisy sacrifices soldiers while leaders profiteer; loyalty becomes a broken covenant between rulers and the doomed.","opus":"An angry soldier's lament at politicians who wage war for profit and abandon the men dying in it. The betrayal is a broken creed, the vow that they'd all come home proven hollow by the very people sending them to die.","sonnet":"A challenge to politicians who invoke the military creed while sending soldiers to die for profit and power — the speaker demands accountability, asking whether those in command even know what their wars are for.","src":"haiku"},
"five-finger-death-punch~wrong-side-of-heaven":{"haiku":"Someone feels torn between wrong and right, seeing their own reflection in both God and devil, unable to discern moral ground underfoot.","opus":"A guilt-ridden veteran (or fallen soul) caught between good and evil, judged by God yet unblamed by the devil, unable to tell right from wrong and sensing himself descending steadily toward the end; the accompanying imagery ties it to soldiers and PTSD.","sonnet":"A man caught between moral extremes — both God and the devil look like him, both feel equally foreign — confesses he can no longer tell right from wrong and finds himself spiritually marooned, not quite belonging to either side.","src":"haiku"},
"five-pebbles~down-softly":{"haiku":"Remembering past naivety, the speaker grapples with breaking internally and forgetting precious things through gentle descent.","opus":"Five Pebbles open with a Japanese line grieving a former self who grasped nothing back then, then ask what it means to shatter your own insides and whether recovery is even desired. The gentle request to be set down cushioned puns on falling into something tender and defeated, a quiet reckoning with losing what mattered and pretending the loss was a choice.","sonnet":"A meditation on the aftermath of inner rupture — forgetting what was precious, breaking what lay inside, the stunned disorientation that follows. A bilingual opening in Japanese anchors an otherwise brief, tender plea: to be lowered gently into whatever healing comes next.","src":"haiku"},
"fixation~stay-awake":{"opus":"Fixation confront a fearful reactionary who spreads falsehood and aggression, compelled to dominate whatever they can't comprehend simply because it's unfamiliar. Defending outsiders who only wish to live in peace, the band recast the title as an anti-fascist call to arms, rejecting the world such people would impose and framing the whole song as resistance to bigotry rooted in a closed mind.","sonnet":"A hardcore political song addressing authoritarian intolerance — someone is using violence and lies to suppress those who are different, and the narrator calls out the fear and ignorance driving that control. The 'stay awake' command is a directive against compliance with a future being sold by those who benefit from the masses remaining divided and afraid.","src":"sonnet"},
"fixation~what-we-have-done":{"haiku":"Humanity faces ecological and environmental collapse—burning forests, water rising—and the song insists this catastrophe is not divine punishment or natural law but the direct result of human action and choices.","opus":"A reckoning with environmental ruin: darkening skies, flaming woods, and rising floods are cast not as heavenly judgment or the planet's design but as humanity's own handiwork, with the guilty parties fleeing while everyone else suffocates. Fixation drive this climate dread with heavy, accusatory force, warning that everything people constructed is disintegrating into powder.","sonnet":"An environmental reckoning stripped of spiritual alibi — the burning forests, rising waters, and blackening skies are not divine punishment or natural cycle but direct human consequence. Those responsible flee while others choke. The song insists on accountability without eschatology: this is the ending mankind built, brick by brick, for itself.","src":"haiku"},
"fizzy-blood~cfo":{"haiku":"Someone writes love songs but doubts their sincerity, watching others succeed while remaining isolated and trapped in mental solitude.","opus":"Fizzy Blood stage a bitter accounting of ambition, a narrator who bartered his soul for a sliver of the affluent existence he watched others enjoy. The love songs he writes stay trapped and unheard inside his own skull, and his hard-won seat of success reads as a coffin of everything he compromised.","sonnet":"A love song that never got sung — written and then abandoned, because the narrator can't share their life or their art with anyone, caught between wanting connection and the knowledge that thinking about it too hard only makes the suffering worse.","src":"haiku"},
"fizzy-blood~flavour-of-the-month":{"opus":"An alt-rock confession of restless craving and fickle obsession, the narrator fixating on one thing only for its shape to keep changing. Feeling psycho and unable to break the cycle, he admits enough is never enough. It captures the exhausting churn of modern desire, devotion that burns out as quickly as it ignites.","src":"opus"},
"fizzy-blood~illusion":{"haiku":"The speaker sees through superficial tricks of success and recognizes how isolation and illusion mask reality; loneliness amplifies the falseness around them.","opus":"Fizzy Blood treat achievement as a con that flatters you and then evaporates. The narrator claims to spot the cheap sleight-of-hand yet stays stranded and craving escape, and the alt-rock tension lives in that space between a triumph that keeps beckoning and the knowledge that it will amount to nothing.","sonnet":"Isolation and the creeping suspicion that achievement is a trick played on the willing. In a house of broken bones, the speaker sees through the seductive lie of success and finds only loneliness behind it — yet the pull toward illusion persists, endlessly smiling through its teeth.","src":"haiku"},
"fizzy-blood~strangers":{"opus":"The pining of unrequited infatuation with a stranger across a crowded, smoky club; the narrator lurks in the corner watching their silhouette, killing time on his phone, too paralyzed to ever make a move or even know if he's been noticed.","sonnet":"A shy man's paralysis in a club — he watches a woman's silhouette from the corner all night, constructs entire fantasies, but never crosses the distance, too afraid to know whether she even noticed him through the smoke and lights.","src":"sonnet"},
"fj-rt~bonheur":{"opus":"Sung in German, addiction and status-envy fuse: the speaker shoots at whatever crosses their path, hangs on the needle of showing off possessions, and craves things only to display them to another. Inwardly hollow ('I've doubled myself but inwardly halved'), they beg happiness (bonheur/plaisir) to arrive and dream that with enough luck they could become the person they envy.","src":"opus"},
"fka-rayne~bored":{"opus":"A woman worn out by a cyclical on-and-off romance, comparing her partner to a tranquilizer she's weary of drowsing under. Numb and restless, she wants a clean break and separate lives, and even after walking out finds the world holds nothing fresher, her tedium following her out.","src":"opus"},
"fka-rayne~choke":{"haiku":"Suffocation and sexual control merge as the speaker finds pleasure in another's asphyxiation and surrender. Power and violation intertwine, binding the target through breathlessness.","opus":"A revenge fantasy of reversal, where the abuser who used cruel words to keep the singer underfoot is now the one gasping on the life she has made. Her own suicidal despair curdles into savage pleasure at watching him struggle to breathe.","sonnet":"A power reversal against a controller: the speaker, once made to feel asphyxiated and worthless, now wants to watch the other choke on the life they built — the image of struggling to breathe flips from victim to perpetrator, delivered with cold satisfaction.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-rayne~kill-me-now":{"haiku":"Insecurity breeds cognitive collapse where the speaker spirals through self-hatred and manipulation, begging death as escape from internal fracture and the weight of their own unreliability.","opus":"A hyperpop-tinged spiral of insecurity and self-directed venom, the narrator berating herself as a stupid girl while begging to be put out of her misery. Anxiety confines her to the house, buried truth festering in her chest. Beneath the caustic hooks sits genuine anguish, self-hatred weaponized into a darkly catchy plea for oblivion.","sonnet":"A speaker drowning in insecurity and anxiety — too anxious to leave the house, burying truth in her chest — addresses herself with contempt and exhaustion, the repeated \"kill me now\" functioning as dark-comic relief from a spiral she can't think her way out of.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-rayne~let-me-stay-dead":{"haiku":"Wanting escape from existence as a specimen under scrutiny, seeking erasure over the burden of being kept alive.","opus":"A macabre plea to be left dead rather than kept alive for someone else's use, the clinical imagery of specimens and formaldehyde staging the self as an object others played god with, better off gone than owned.","sonnet":"A plea to remain in the numbness of emotional death rather than be resurrected to live in a toxic relationship — the narrator has already hollowed themselves out for the other person and would rather stay cold, bled, and buried than be dragged back to life on the abuser's terms.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-rayne~loathe":{"haiku":"Self-hatred and bodily disgust reach unbearable intensity; the speaker wants to destroy themselves and escape their skin.","opus":"Raw self-hatred spilling into fantasies of self-mutilation and vanishing, hemmed in to the marrow by everything the speaker despises about their own body and mind. The disgust is total and aimed entirely inward.","sonnet":"Extreme body dysmorphia and self-loathing expressed as a desire to physically destroy or remove every part of oneself — skin, eyes, nose — the narrator is so repulsed by their own body and internal state that the only fantasy is obliteration.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-rayne~minced-meat":{"haiku":"Cult domination through personality and threat reduces followers to sheep, where questioning invites slaughter. The shepherd eats the flock, converting faith into fuel.","opus":"A menacing character study of a cult leader demanding total obedience, casting followers as sheep who kneel, worship, and never question what he consumes. FKA Rayne inhabit the predator's voice, threatening to turn dissenters into minced meat. A chilling anatomy of authoritarian control, personality cults, and the violence lurking beneath manufactured devotion.","sonnet":"A cult leader speaks directly to their flock — commanding obedience, dehumanising dissenters ('turn 'em into mince meat'), positioning themselves as shepherd and god — the slaughterhouse imagery makes explicit that the congregation's compliance feeds the leader's appetite, not their own salvation.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-rayne~not-sorry":{"haiku":"Unapologetic defiance and refusal to repent; the speaker owns actions without contrition.","opus":"A gleefully unrepentant kiss-off, the narrator refusing remorse for wounds she salts because the target brought it on themselves. FKA Rayne revel in vengeful cruelty, warning twice before the knife lands and smiling through the bloodletting. It weaponizes the language of apology into its opposite, a defiant celebration of refusing to feel sorry.","sonnet":"Revenge served cold — the narrator fills wounds with salt, calls it their fault, and refuses the apology with precise contempt: \"I warned you twice, coulda walked away but ya walked into my knife.\" There's no regret, no ambivalence; the other person made you bleed and now you smile while they wait and bleed back.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-rayne~parasite":{"haiku":"An intimate predator hollows the host from within, feeding on vulnerability while reshaping the victim to fit their appetite through calculated manipulation.","opus":"An abuser is named as a parasite who found the singer at her lowest, molded her into something small, and fed on her until she rotted. The violence of the imagery measures how completely she was hollowed out.","sonnet":"About a person who found the narrator at their lowest point and systematically hollowed them out — molding their personality, tightening control, and leaving them gutted and bleeding. The parasite metaphor is applied without softening: the abuser ate the narrator alive from the inside, and what is left is a carcass waiting for what comes next.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-rayne~praying-mantis":{"haiku":"A predator claims dominance and godhood through violence, savoring the power to break its victim while offering no mercy or mercy.","opus":"A predator's fantasy of just deserts, cast as fate rather than revenge. The mantis image turns intimacy into consumption, the speaker relishing a victim's terror as proof of her own godlike power.","sonnet":"A woman taking lethal revenge on someone who wronged her, cast as a praying mantis — she bites the head off, carves into the body, drains the marrow, and feels no guilt, framing destruction as fate rather than revenge and enjoying every moment of it.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-rayne~shut-up":{"opus":"A furious takedown of the impossible demands placed on women, always prettier, skinnier, sweeter, weaker, delivered as a scream to shut up. FKA Rayne turn beauty-standard pressure and misogynist expectation into cathartic aggression, refusing to stay quiet and obedient. Rage against being made small, flipped into a defiant refusal to comply.","src":"opus"},
"fka-rayne~side-effect":{"haiku":"Craving pain and numbness as medicine, the speaker seeks to feel through suffering and addiction.","opus":"A visceral plea to feel anything at all, the narrator craving medication and side effects as proof of being alive against a deadening numbness. FKA Rayne render depression and self-destruction as a longing to hurt rather than stay buried and hollow. Misery becomes an addiction, the desire to suffer preferable to feeling nothing.","sonnet":"A desire to feel anything, even pain — numb to the point of wanting medication not to heal but to hurt, to be a victim of something, to trade apathy for misery addiction. \"Let me sleep in this burning house\" is the darkest formulation: choosing the destruction because the alternative is feeling nothing at all.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-rayne~woof":{"haiku":"Power inverts through domination; the speaker casts their victim as animal, teaching obedience through threat and the language of submission.","opus":"Dominance flipped into a leash, the singer treating a man like a dog to be housebroken, threatened, and put down. It weaponizes the language of obedience and training to reclaim total control over someone who once controlled her.","sonnet":"A woman flips the dominant/submissive dynamic, putting an aggressive man on a leash and threatening to 'put you down like a dog' — sharp teeth, locked jaw, kennel — reclaiming control with contemptuous authority over someone who overstepped.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-twigs~fallen-alien":{"haiku":"Deception becomes the architecture of intimacy; a fallen extraterrestrial observes her lover's lies and prepares to destroy him once his mask cracks under the light.","opus":"From Magdalene, the moment of seeing through a manipulative lover's lies and turning cold vengeance back on them. Twigs writes the clarity after being tied down by a liar, the promise to kick him down once his mask slips.","sonnet":"A woman who has been bound and deceived by a lover she waited for outside herself describes feeling like a fallen alien — unmoored, searching for light in a dark age, fully aware of his lies yet still entwined with him until she resolves to bring him to his knees.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-twigs~girl-feels-good":{"haiku":"Female desire and presence transform agency and power, radiating magnetism that anchors others. Confidence circulates through community, creating cohesion and drawing energy from masculine vulnerability.","opus":"A blessing on lost, war-hungry men and a celebration of female pleasure as the force that steadies the world. Twigs frames a woman feeling good as the thing that keeps the devil down and heals the men who never learned their own minds.","sonnet":"A celebration of feminine vitality as a cosmological force: when a girl feels good the world goes round and the devil is kept down, set against a tender address to a \"city guy\" who needs to slow down and enjoy it — sensuality framed as protective, world-sustaining energy.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-twigs~lights-on":{"haiku":"Intimacy requires complete trust to be fully naked and vulnerable without shame. True connection means revealing all secrets while the light stays on.","opus":"An intimate negotiation of vulnerability, where physical closeness only becomes possible once trust is earned, the singer offering to reveal her shadows and secrets in a whisper if the lover proves worthy in how he acts in the light. FKA twigs emerged from the early-2010s British alternative-R&B wave, and this early track showcases her signature: fragile, breathy vocals over spacious, avant-garde production that makes intimacy feel like a fragile threshold.","sonnet":"Intimacy conditional on trust, and trust withheld until safety can be established — a person who hides their shadows from the world offering to reveal them to someone who can hold the light steady. Vulnerability here is not weakness but a prize: full presence, full secrets, everything, once the trust is earned.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-twigs~love-crimes":{"opus":"An early twigs meditation on desire's reckless bargains, treating hardship as an excuse for the damage love does and a heart that won't stop tumbling. She lingers on the pain of releasing someone who might have been the one — sensual, fractured longing caught between grasping and letting go.","src":"opus"},
"fka-twigs~mirrored-heart":{"haiku":"Romantic dissolution reveals the other person as a false mirror reflecting the speaker's own absence; love was only ever a gambit for flight and feeling.","opus":"The ache of a love that never truly saw or wanted you, measured against couples who found real matches. Twigs sits in the loneliness of comparison, refusing to give up while knowing he was never sure of her.","sonnet":"A speaker who has been rejected and unseen by a lover refuses to give up despite knowing she will keep thinking of him, while other couples with mirrored hearts only remind her of what she was denied.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-twigs~papi-pacify":{"haiku":"Desperate pleas for reassurance and clarity in a romantic relationship where doubt and hurt linger, seeking confirmation of exclusivity and healing.","opus":"On her early breakthrough EP, FKA twigs molds submission and longing into something at once tender and disquieting, pleading with a lover to soothe and explain a love that wounds her. She offers to become whatever he wants and craves reassurance even through choking and broken promises, the breathy, glitching production making intimacy feel like surrender and quiet bargaining at once.","sonnet":"A plea inside an unequal power dynamic — for reassurance, clarity, and the relief of being chosen. The hurt underneath is steady and real, arriving with each request to be soothed and named, even as the answers that would satisfy keep not coming.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-twigs~perfect-stranger":{"opus":"A lover chooses deliberate ignorance, not learning a partner's town, past, exes or favorites, because the mystery spares her the anxiety and the lies. She embraces the danger, treating a stranger as perfect precisely because he can't yet disappoint, living only in the person he is tonight.","src":"opus"},
"fka-twigs~room-of-fools":{"haiku":"Collective vulnerability becomes communion; wounded dancers bleed out pressure together, finding beauty in untethered dissolution.","opus":"Strangers colliding in a darkened club as raw wounds venting their ache together, a passing communion that simply feels good. The floor becomes a place to summon any version of herself she likes, beauty unmoored from consequence.","sonnet":"A fleeting, beautiful connection in a dark room full of damaged people — strangers and stray dogs dancing together as open wounds, bleeding out pressure, and briefly becoming something real through shared unconscious movement.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-twigs~sad-day":{"haiku":"Temporal stasis and wishful magic infuse a world where nothing changes except the speaker's mood; hope lives in transient ritual and the fantasy of genuine connection.","opus":"A seductive, uneasy plea to take a chance on a fragile love, wrapped in wishes and the guilt of having made someone sad. Twigs blurs desire and sorrow, offering herself while sensing it's already slipping away.","sonnet":"A speaker caught between melancholy and desire invites a lover to make a wish on her love, imagining an embrace while mourning that she has caused sadness before and that the city and her longing press in on them together.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-twigs~sushi":{"opus":"A buoyant, flirtatious fantasy of whisking a lover through a whirlwind week of dates, from sushi to karaoke to skinny-dipping. FKA twigs trades her usual melancholy for playful abundance, cataloguing pleasures with breathless generosity. It celebrates spontaneous romance and the joy of showering someone with experiences, no strings beyond a good time.","src":"opus"},
"fka-twigs~two-weeks":{"haiku":"Seduction and sexual healing through pleasure and altered states; the speaker offers to transform their lover over two weeks through touch and intoxication. Desire is framed as medicine for emotional wounds.","opus":"An explicit seduction aimed at luring someone away from their current partner, boasting she can pleasure him better and promising that after two weeks with her he 'won't recognize her'; the drug-like highs and open-mouthed imagery fuse sex and intoxication into a confident come-on.","sonnet":"A seduction pitched as direct sexual competition — the narrator tells someone in a relationship that she can do better for them physically and emotionally, offering two weeks as the time it would take to make them unrecognisable from the person they were before.","src":"haiku"},
"fka-twigs~water-me":{"haiku":"A woman demands free love and growth from someone who only values her when she costs nothing. Rejected for setting boundaries, she remains alone with herself.","opus":"A stark little parable of self-worth and transactional love, where a lover refuses intimacy once a price is set and belittles the singer as too small, met with a quiet insistence that she can grow tall if only she is nurtured. FKA twigs delivers it in her fragile, pitched-up whisper over sparse, warped production, the minimalism of the arrangement mirroring the loneliness of being left with only oneself.","sonnet":"A woman told she is too small, too costly, and withheld from — she responds not with anger but with a simple request to be nurtured so she can grow. The transaction framing is dark: love reduced to a fee, desire priced out of reach, the speaker left alone with herself and a single quiet demand for care.","src":"haiku"},
"flagman~bumblebee":{"haiku":"Self-awareness and brief lifespan sharpen existential anxiety; the narrator questions meaning while confronting inevitable irrelevance and death.","opus":"A darkly funny self-portrait of someone who can't stop sabotaging himself, stinging and dying in the same breath, needing the last word. It is about the doomed compulsion to speak and self-destruct, spite dressed as honesty.","sonnet":"A self-interrogating monologue about ego and its consequences — the speaker wonders whether they are worth their own company, whether their words land or float unheard, and admits they are transparent, impulsive, and prone to putting their foot in their mouth.","src":"haiku"},
"flagman~nice-guy":{"haiku":"Rejection crystallizes as the narrator confronts their own inadequacy, the mirror reflecting ugliness while genuine affection transforms into disposability.","opus":"A bitter punk kiss-off from the friend-zoned, curdling longing into resentment and self-loathing at the mirror. The sneering goodbye barely hides how badly rejection has stung, nice-guy grievance and genuine hurt tangled together.","sonnet":"A bitter, self-aware account of perpetual near-miss and social rejection — the nice guy who never quite fits, is scraped off like mud, told he's ugly by the mirror, and ends up buried to his neck waiting for the tide.","src":"haiku"},
"flash-forward~drowning-underwater":{"opus":"A pop-punk depiction of depression as literal submersion, the narrator sinking into darkening water while pleading for rescue from someone who can fix his soul. Stuck between coming clean and losing himself, he can't recognize his own reflection. Panic and helplessness surge through it, the desperate cry to be kept alive as the depths pull down.","src":"opus"},
"flatwounds~drenched":{"haiku":"Drowning in fate and circumstance becomes inescapable as containment and prophecy converge. The speaker sinks deeper despite resistance, bound by forces beyond navigation.","opus":"A victim swallowed by a false prophet and forced to drown, resigned to a fate he cannot escape. The soaking is total, cold and wet becoming a state of being condemned rather than merely killed.","sonnet":"A victim submerged by a cold-handed prophet who disguises himself as friendly and forces others to drown in a world of impatience and containment — fate here is a cold wet thing, and the speaker is soaked through with it, unable to escape what was determined before they arrived.","src":"haiku"},
"fleetwood-mac~dreams-2004-remaster":{"opus":"A clear-eyed breakup reflection: the narrator grants her leaving partner his freedom while warning that his loneliness will catch up with him; love, like thunder in the rain, only comes when players are playing, and time will bring the clarity he needs.","sonnet":"A breakup told from the composed side — a woman watches a man demand his freedom while warning him that loneliness will teach him what he's losing, framing it as something he'll only understand clearly once the storm has washed him clean.","src":"sonnet"},
"flesh-field~the-collapse":{"haiku":"Failing to hold something never meant to be kept; integrity drowns while attempting to preserve what dissolves faster than it can be saved.","opus":"A brief cry of coming apart, someone morphing and dwindling to nothing, pleading to be concealed and rescued from doubt while trying a bit, failing worse, and gripping something that was never his until his wholeness dissolves. Flesh Field work in dark electro-industrial territory, and the frantic plea for rescue amid collapse suits the genre's bleak, cold electronic textures.","sonnet":"An identity dissolving under uncertainty — mutating, fading, unable to hold onto anything that was supposed to belong to it. The speaker knows they are failing slightly more with each attempt, losing integrity, and the only instinct left is to cling to someone else's form while asking to be saved.","src":"haiku"},
"fleshwater~jetpack":{"haiku":"Faith fails to sustain; the subject flies toward inevitable emptiness.","opus":"Cutting loose from someone while admitting nothing will change or improve, the fantasy of flying off crashing into seven years of restlessness. Moving on gets imagined as permanent, an escape that might just be another kind of falling.","sonnet":"A speaker announces they are finally throwing someone away and flying free — \"if moving on lasts forever, I'll fly away\" — but nothing has changed; the thrill of escape keeps letting pain back in, and seven years of unrest still haven't found an ending.","src":"haiku"},
"fleshwater~kiss-the-ladder":{"haiku":"Ascension through intimate contact and passionate connection, the speaker climbing toward transcendence through kiss.","opus":"A dissociative confrontation with a fading closeness, the speaker talking himself hoarse trying to hold onto what's already gone. Fleshwater's shoegaze-metal blur mirrors the collapse — he stops asking to be loved, pulls off the blindfold, and lands on the bleakest wish of all: to stop being anyone.","sonnet":"A stripped-down rejection of belonging — you black out talking too much, your conscience is too calm, I'm not here to be loved — the blindfold removed reveals not clarity but the desire to dissolve: I don't want to be anyone anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"fleshwater~linda-claire":{"haiku":"One person watches another struggle and fail, yet the doubter is wrong because their target is actually starting fresh and rising above expectation.","opus":"A defiant answer to someone who wanted to watch you fail, taking their contempt as fuel rather than proof, the recognition that resentment and shared wounds bind two people together even as one insists on becoming their own person.","sonnet":"A song addressed to someone who wanted the narrator to fail — despite being held back and pushed away, the narrator is just starting rather than drowning, and refuses to be absorbed into a shared identity that does not fit.","src":"haiku"},
"fleshwater~raging-storm":{"haiku":"Wounded introspection spirals into chaos; the speaker sinks deeper into rage as doubt overwhelms and time erodes the possibility of understanding or resolution.","opus":"Grief and longing swirled into a storm of thoughts drowned but never spoken, unsure whether the hurt is mutual or worth knowing. Saying goodbye means going blind as the tide finally takes it away.","sonnet":"About wanting to understand whether someone's distance and pain are deliberate — does it hurt them too, and do they want to leave? The narrator is sinking in the storm of an unanswered emotional question, blinded by it, with time raising the tide around both of them while no answer comes.","src":"haiku"},
"fleshwater~standalone":{"opus":"A stark refusal of belonging that treats existence itself as accidental and unwanted. The Massachusetts metalcore-adjacent act strips their sound to a numb whisper, framing detachment not as freedom but as a quiet verdict on being alive without purpose. It reaches for the moment where a person decides love was never on offer and stops waiting for it.","src":"opus"},
"fleshwater~woohoo":{"haiku":"Catastrophe wears the mask of adventure: casual destruction and recklessness spiral into something darker and more hopeless than the initial thrills allowed.","opus":"Surreal domestic dread rendered as birthday-party imagery gone wrong, dissociating from your own body and mind. Beneath the cheer runs a dark wish for dissolution.","sonnet":"A deliberately surreal and disorienting song that circles around something new and fragile being born — possibly a mind, a person, a relationship — with imagery of decay and self-harm woven through a dark celebratory tone.","src":"haiku"},
"flo-rida~good-feeling":{"haiku":"Confidence and aspiration electrify the speaker into visionary assertion of his own destiny; optimism becomes a force that attracts wealth and breaks through doubt.","opus":"A rags-to-riches hype anthem, hustling out of the bucket toward money, power and the top with pure adrenaline. It is about the intoxicating certainty that you're about to win, feeling royal before you've arrived.","sonnet":"An anthem of unstoppable self-belief in which the speaker channels an extraordinary, unprecedented high — framing ambition, royalty, and invincibility as the natural state of someone who woke up determined to win.","src":"haiku"},
"float-omen~above-the-waves":{"opus":"A submerged metaphor for facing oneself: the drowning-and-surfacing imagery stands in for confronting regret and the parts of the self let slip away, then choosing to rise toward life again. Despair giving way to a stubborn, hard-won hope.","src":"opus"},
"florence-black~start-again":{"haiku":"Abuse and domination demand liberation as the speaker seeks redemption through breaking chains.","opus":"A plea born of a corrosive bond, where devotion and damage bleed together. The Welsh riff-rock trio channel classic hard-rock heaviness into a portrait of someone who keeps returning to a person who wrecks them, begging for a clean slate while knowing the chain will only re-form. It reaches for the exhaustion of loving what harms you.","sonnet":"The speaker, broken and chained by another's will, begs for help to start again — the \"right to stay\" asserted against someone who simultaneously professes love and wrecks their body, the plea cycling without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"florence-the-machine~dog-days-are-over":{"haiku":"A person paralyzed by happiness and love finally recognizes danger approaching and must flee, abandoning everything to survive. The song transforms joy into urgency and escape.","opus":"Florence Welch frames sudden happiness as an ambush so violent it must be fled and destroyed, joy striking like an attack from behind. As deliverance approaches, the song insists she abandon love and yearning to survive it, sprinting toward family. The baroque-pop exuberance disguises a warning that even good fortune can be something to run from.","sonnet":"Happiness arrives not as a gentle gift but as a violent, disorienting force — something a woman spent years hiding from and numbing away. When the hard season finally ends, the only option is to run: shed every attachment and let the oncoming joy overtake her.","src":"haiku"},
"florence-the-machine~no-light-no-light":{"haiku":"Blindness through revelation; the speaker's confession risks everything, yet withholding truth perpetuates an unbearable emotional stasis.","opus":"Florence caught between wanting a lover's forgiving revelation and being unable to confess what she's become. It is about the gap between how easily you perform honesty for a crowd and how hard the truth is to say to one person.","sonnet":"A relationship is destroying both people: the absent person is the silence and the fear and the start of everything, and the speaker is desperate for resolution — a revelation that will explain what she has done or become — but cannot have that conversation, watching daylight turn violent and asking what she should say.","src":"haiku"},
"flotsam-jetsam~reaggression":{"opus":"A veteran thrash band turns their gaze on domestic violence and the false fronts people maintain, reading bruises as stories nobody will admit. Beneath the aggression sits a paralysis about the future, too many uncertain paths and no clean escape. It reaches for the way the past forecloses tomorrow and how surface calm hides quiet brutality.","src":"opus"},
"flow~a-mo86me":{"haiku":"A celebratory ode to post-gig camaraderie and boozy bonding—collective escape into endless night, circling back to where it started.","opus":"FLOW made their name on high-energy anime openings built for crowds, so it fits that they turn here to the live show's afterglow. Band and crew pile into a train-like dream express bound for the stars, drinking all night, telling stupid stories until they pass out on the Yamanote Line and wake three stops past their destination. The joy is uncomplicated — camaraderie, and the brief suspension of ordinary life that live music creates.","sonnet":"A celebration of the aftermath of a live show — the band and crew pile into a train-like dream express headed for the stars, drinking all night, telling stupid stories until dawn breaks and everyone passes out on the Yamanote Line, waking up three stops past their destination. The emotional core is the pure, uncomplicated joy of camaraderie and the brief suspension of ordinary life that live music creates.","src":"haiku"},
"floya~the-hymn":{"opus":"A departure song about choosing an uncharted life over staying comfortable and known. The German prog-metal act frame leaving as a spiritual necessity rather than a betrayal, insisting the bond survives the distance. It reaches for the ache of outgrowing a place and the faith that the people left behind remain near in spirit.","src":"opus"},
"floya~weaver":{"haiku":"A shadow figure draws the speaker into an inescapable maze of pain, a ghost wearing intimacy as camouflage. The relationship binds like undertow, dragging deeper with each moment of connection.","opus":"A reckoning with a clinging inner darkness personified as a false sibling, a shadow the narrator both depends on and wants gone. The band render codependency with one's own despair as drowning in a maze with no exit. It reaches for the terrible intimacy of a pain that feels like the only company left.","sonnet":"The speaker is bound to a shadow-figure who is simultaneously suffocating and the only source of light — dragging them under rolling waves while being \"all that I've got,\" making escape feel identical to drowning.","src":"haiku"},
"fluke~absurd-whitewash-edit":{"haiku":"Cartoon characters depicted as morally corrupt—stealing, prostituting, dying; a satirical critique of pop culture corruption.","opus":"British electronica outfit Fluke stage a garish cartoon carnival, dragging cherished childhood characters into overdose, vice and squalid tabloid scandal until the whole beloved pantheon is corrupted. Under the manic satire runs a deadpan recurring line insisting that anyone with a conscience chooses love, a wry, faintly hopeful plea woven through all the grotesque absurdity.","sonnet":"A surrealist carnival where beloved pop-culture icons from comics, cartoons, and children's television are plunged into squalor and transgression — drug dens, prostitution, suicide, robbery. Beneath the chaos a single refrain persists: anyone with a heart still votes for love.","src":"haiku"},
"fluke~atom-bomb":{"haiku":"A charismatic, powerful woman possesses dangerous strength and dark secrets, conducting an entire operation with perfect control and mysterious capabilities.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek catalogue of an omnipotent, unstoppable femme figure who possesses everything from an atom bomb to a Nobel Prize to a genie, treating world-ending power as a source of pure fun. The escalating list revels in absurd excess. Fluke are a British electronic act from the big-beat and dance era, and this track's swaggering, repetitive hook and maximalist inventory fit the club-ready momentum they built their reputation on.","sonnet":"A figure of absolute, cartoonish power — Nobel Prize, secret lair, satellite, crystal ball, a fleet at sea — described with gleeful escalation as someone who has accumulated every possible weapon and advantage and is simply having fun with it all. The tone is admiring and absurdist, the power fantasy played completely straight.","src":"haiku"},
"fluke~snapshot":{"haiku":"A catalogue of lives depicts contemporary fragmentation where identity becomes performance and connection remains incidental to disconnected routines.","opus":"A rapid-fire roll call of damaged characters chasing love, money, attention and salvation, each one a small catalogue of dysfunction. The point is the crowded, restless mess of people all wanting and all falling short.","sonnet":"A rapid-fire snapshot of urban archetypes — people navigating desire, addiction, violence, ambition, and spiritual longing simultaneously — presented as a crowded diorama of modern life where everyone is performing some version of survival or escape.","src":"haiku"},
"flume~sirens-feat-caroline-polachek-sofia-kourtesis-remix":{"opus":"A helpless wish to possess the power to end all suffering and silence the cries of distress, undercut by the painful admission that the singer has no such magic and sees no end to the harm.","sonnet":"A lament about powerlessness in the face of widespread suffering: the speaker desperately wishes they could stop all harm and silence every cry of distress with a gesture, but possesses no such power and can only watch.","src":"sonnet"},
"flux-pavilion~party-starter":{"opus":"A pure functional hype track from the British dubstep producer, built to detonate a dancefloor and nothing more. Its self-appointed role is simply to push the beat harder. There is little subject beyond the euphoric command to lose oneself in volume and momentum, an anthem of the drop and the crowd.","src":"opus"},
"fluxe~shape-of-decay":{"haiku":"Surrendering everything to love while watching it crumble, becoming decay itself after withholding nothing.","opus":"Love and self-hate rotting together, the narrator who gave everything away and let it break, addressing a lost love as a guiding star told to burn out, choosing erasure and decay over the weight of what they made.","sonnet":"A breakup song about a love that rotted from within — the narrator gave everything, let hate and venom fill the space where love was, and is left with the shape of what decayed rather than what was built. The emotional core is self-blame for allowing destruction to replace connection.","src":"haiku"},
"flying-lotus~black-balloons-reprise":{"haiku":"Existence as fragile and doomed, where the speaker grapples with poverty, mortality, and the corruption of life while seeking meaning through funk and music before death arrives.","opus":"An apocalyptic reverie where the black balloon becomes a figure for mortality, the mind, and a doomed world, moving from cosmic imagery to broke, battered thoughts and the flat certainty that everyone cries, lies, and dies. Flying Lotus produces the woozy backdrop here, with the verse delivered by Captain Murphy, his rap alias, threading gallows humor through visions of collapse in the experimental, jazz-inflected style he's known for.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality, systemic violence, and the fragility of the world built around a single image — the black balloon that rises, floats, and eventually explodes, taking everything with it. The speaker moves between personal collapse and civilisational ruin, managing to hold dark humour and genuine grief in the same breath.","src":"haiku"},
"flying-lotus~never-catch-me":{"haiku":"An exploration of existential curiosity and inner darkness, examining mortality and beyond-body experience as a form of transcendence beyond human control and fear.","opus":"Kendrick Lamar racing through a vision of death not as an end but as liberation, an out-of-body transcendence the living can never catch. Flying Lotus's cosmic jazz frames mortality as something to embrace and outrun at once, curiosity beating fear.","sonnet":"About staring down death without fear — the narrator has looked into his own darkness and found peace with mortality, arguing that death is not something that can catch him because he's already accepted it, rapping from a place of hard-won freedom beyond the reach of grief or fear.","src":"haiku"},
"foals~black-bull":{"haiku":"The narrator moves through the world with reckless confidence and disruption, leaving behind convention and expectation as they claim power and presence.","opus":"A strutting, coked-up swagger of arrival, the black bull a token of menace and bravado rolling into town. Beneath the peacocking is Yannis Philippakis's sly self-caricature of rock-star excess, ready to smash the place up while insisting he's a man of today.","sonnet":"A boisterous swagger about arriving somewhere new with confidence and power — naming yourself a wolf, owning the room, pointing out pretension around you, uninterested in blending in.","src":"haiku"},
"foals~inhaler":{"haiku":"A speaker experiences war, exhaustion, and depletion of personal resources while desperately needing space and separation from someone or something draining their lungs and mind.","opus":"Marking their heavier turn, Foals frontman Yannis Philippakis catalogs a hollowed-out man reduced to a phony, spent, for-hire shell, gasping that his lungs have given out. His pleas not to be left even briefly, screamed against an unnameable inner war and a desperate craving for room, tighten into pure claustrophobic panic.","sonnet":"Fragile identity under siege by forces too large to name — a speaker who admits to being artificial, depleted, unable to breathe, craving only space. The war being described is both external and internal, the desperate need to not be consumed by it the only coherent wish left.","src":"haiku"},
"foals~snake-oil":{"haiku":"A con artist sells false promises to trap others in longing and dependence, keeping them wired and burning with want while never delivering satisfaction.","opus":"Foals cast lust as a swindle, a peddler of fake cures who dopes and bewitches the narrator into a feverish, burning state before flattening him to nothing. He hungers for what he can never quite grasp, chasing a tease that dangles liberation but delivers dependence. The strutting groove makes being manipulated sound intoxicating, the mark half-knowing he's being played and unable to quit.","sonnet":"A charismatic manipulator who sells empty promises to sinners and fools alike casts a spell of perpetual want. The speaker is caught — wired, inflamed, driven out to old familiar roads searching for what was taken — unable to break free of the craving this person has installed.","src":"haiku"},
"foals~spanish-sahara":{"haiku":"Memory and trauma blur into desert ash; the speaker haunts the beloved as fury and ghost, carrying unresolved wounds.","opus":"Grief rendered as a haze on a bleached shoreline, someone trying to leave a trauma behind while it reasserts itself as the ghost lodged in the head; everything reduced to future rust and dust, the horror refusing to stay buried.","sonnet":"A speaker haunted by a traumatic summer memory at a desolate coastal place tries to leave the horror there — in the sand, the black rocks, the sea — but the past clings as fury in the head and ghost in the mind, eroding into dust and rust no matter how far one runs.","src":"haiku"},
"fontaines-d-c~before-you-i-just-forget":{"haiku":"Before this connection, memory failed; the self dissolved into performance and false presentation.","opus":"An Irish frontman's weary reckoning with performing a palatable version of himself, cutting down his own shine because that is what people want. Beneath the crowd-pleasing act is a tired heart and the confession that before this one person, everything else simply slips his mind.","sonnet":"A speaker stripped of things to sell and scenes to trade — performing fineness while the shine is decapitated for public approval — admits that before meeting this person they could take everything they got, but now they simply forget themselves entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"fontaines-d-c~boys-in-the-better-land":{"opus":"Fontaines D.C. capture the restless myth of escape from Ireland, a runaway world of rockstars and fast cars and a nationalist taxi driver spitting slogans. The better land everyone keeps talking about is the elsewhere that promises more, but the swagger carries irony. It is about Irish identity, longing to get out, and the grip of the place you'd leave.","sonnet":"A portrait of Irish restlessness and the mythology of escape — nationalist taxi drivers, desperate sisters, and a culture that idolizes people who got out, all circling the idea that the boys who left exist in some imagined better place the narrator can only talk about, not reach.","src":"sonnet"},
"fontaines-d-c~bug":{"haiku":"Transformation and withdrawal merge; the subject ascends past mortality and shame.","opus":"A man turning twenty-eight, changing his name and inflating himself with promises while the woman he loves is simply gone. The higher he climbs the more hollow the bravado, apologies dodged and feelings only ever almost felt, fading into the night.","sonnet":"A fragmented, guilt-soaked portrait of a man who changed his name as a promise and still failed: 28 years closing in, a woman gone, a spiral through Carnegie Hall vanity mirrors and deleted phone numbers, until the speaker is higher than everyone and fading in the night, charmlessly promising to be gone by morning.","src":"haiku"},
"fontaines-d-c~death-kink":{"haiku":"Attraction to darkness and death becomes erotic obsession; the speaker worships their own destruction through another's hands.","opus":"Addiction to a destructive love that recognized the narrator's pain and taught him to love the chain—a broken promise clattered by drink and self-sabotage. He'd rather be called bad than good, wearing ruin like a kink he can't quit.","sonnet":"About a relationship with someone who entered the speaker's life during rock bottom and recognized their capacity for suffering as something to leverage — the speaker broke promises, drank until they saw stars, and oscillated between being told they were bad and being told they could be good; there is a kink in the dynamic that is both painful and compulsive.","src":"haiku"},
"fontaines-d-c~desire":{"haiku":"Desire designs humanity from birth to death; hunger perpetuates itself through anguish.","opus":"Desire as an inherited affliction designed into people from cradle to grave, a mist that turns everyone into liars chasing what is denied them. The band watches a whole society drown its wishes in the same fountain their fathers did, haunted and hungry.","sonnet":"Desire is framed as both the thing that designed and will destroy us — from cradle to pyre, in mortal attire — while every 24 hours people drown their wishes in fountains like their fathers before; being haunted is framed as higher than simply being wanted.","src":"haiku"},
"fontaines-d-c~favourite":{"haiku":"A dreamer reshapes beauty and meaning across time. Misery marks the speaker while surveillance ages—but they know whom lightning burns for.","opus":"A bittersweet ode to a long friendship or love recalled through a working-class Thatcher-era childhood of football played indoors and being overlooked, nostalgia curdling into the wary, marked-man feeling of adulthood while insisting the fragments hold forever.","sonnet":"A meditation on a person who was once the speaker's obsession — nostalgia for childhood in Thatcher-era Ireland bleeds into adult disillusionment, and the song tracks how someone you loved above everything gradually becomes just another stranger as time and misery grind you both into different shapes.","src":"haiku"},
"fontaines-d-c~here-s-the-thing":{"haiku":"Masked performance and unspoken agreement; the speaker shares invisible pain with another, words unnecessary because the silence already confirms everything.","opus":"An unnervingly intimate address to a watched girl whose thoughts the speaker claims as his own, hinting the real story wouldn't go over well if told. Craving feeling while numbed out, he finds a strange beauty in hurting this cleanly.","sonnet":"A shared understanding between two people who feel each other's pain without needing to say it — craving commotion and emotion even when it hurts, knowing the truth between them would not play well if told aloud.","src":"haiku"},
"fontaines-d-c~i-love-you":{"opus":"Fontaines D.C. braid an overwhelming declaration of love with a furious indictment of their native Ireland, naming its governing parties and depicting a nation devouring its own young. Personal devotion and political rage feed each other, the tender vow curdling into anger at greed and a generation taught only self-regard. A love letter to a country the band still can't forgive.","sonnet":"An intense, sprawling declaration of love fused with rage at Ireland's political class — the narrator professes undying devotion to one person while also indicting the corruption of Fine Gael, Fianna Fáil, and a society that profits off its youth's despair and early death.","src":"sonnet"},
"fontaines-d-c~in-the-modern-world":{"haiku":"Feeling erodes in the contemporary moment; numbness defines the urban condition.","opus":"A numbed drift through a glamorized city, addressed to a Charlene the singer would run away with, insisting he feels nothing and doesn't feel bad about it. Beneath the LA sheen is emotional deadness reframed as freedom, the man declaring himself the law where there's no feeling left to draw on.","sonnet":"A Dublin speaker who feels alive in a city they can see both loved and despised invites Charlene and Sal to come away with him, while admitting that in the modern world he doesn't feel anything — and that even so, he is the law, the reason irrelevant.","src":"haiku"},
"fontaines-d-c~it-s-amazing-to-be-young":{"haiku":"Youth is paradoxically beautiful yet costly; performance and ritual sustain a facade that others refuse to understand.","opus":"The Dublin post-punks weigh youth's price against its brief, undeniable glory. There is a nightly ritual of becoming a performed self, a sense of never being understood despite pouring everything out. It reaches for the bittersweet math of being young: what it costs to inhabit that state, set against how astonishing the feeling remains.","sonnet":"Youth as painful privilege: waking up in the dark, performing the ritual to put yourself in the part, singing every word and still not being understood — the cost brings you down, and yet the refrain returns to it's amazing to be young, holding the contradiction without resolving it.","src":"haiku"},
"fontaines-d-c~jackie-down-the-line":{"opus":"Grian Chatten inhabits a coldly cruel figure who promises to wear you down, hurt you, and make your secrets his own, a serial heartbreaker declaring his own malice as identity. The Jackeen persona, tied to Sally's boneyard of declining spirits, makes it a study in emotional sabotage. It is about someone who breaks people on purpose and knows exactly what he is.","sonnet":"A character study of a charming, serial emotional abuser — Jackie moves from person to person, wearing them down, keeping their secrets, deserting them, and feeling nothing, described in a confessional voice that is both self-aware and completely unrepentant.","src":"sonnet"},
"fontaines-d-c~roman-holiday":{"haiku":"Resistance to national mythology collides with personal desire for transcendence and escape. The speaker refuses the queen's song while chasing brilliance beyond institutional reach.","opus":"A woozy love-and-escape ode from the Dublin band's Skinty Fia era, addressed to a partner amid a hostile, watchful world. Irish identity and defiance thread through the invitation to get lost together before the moment vanishes. It reaches for stolen intimacy under pressure and the stubborn belief that their day will still come.","sonnet":"An Irish voice in the grip of 'Skinty Fia' urgency pulls a lover toward escape and intoxication while registering a political disgust — they don't want the queen's song, they watch hopes snuffed and suns blotted out by those who think they know what genius looks like; the hourglass, the high heels, and the question 'can you feel it' push toward a shared defiance that is as much national as personal.","src":"haiku"},
"fontaines-d-c~romance":{"opus":"The title track of the Dublin band's darker pivot casts love as a place one descends into willingly, pigs in a pen and screws in the head. Devotion here is bleak, fierce, almost penitential. It reaches for the idea that romance might be less a feeling than a shared location, however grim, that two people can inhabit together.","src":"opus"},
"fontaines-d-c~starburster":{"haiku":"Desire and performance collide in a fog of hedonism—chasing sensation through fleeting connections and momentary bliss, dressed in the language of mischief and transcendence.","opus":"A panic attack rendered as breathless, grabbing appetite: the frontman's own anxiety spun into a stream of manic wants that never resolve, gasping between lines that it may feel bad.","sonnet":"A torrent of anxious, fragmented desire and self-interrogation — the speaker cycles through wanting to consume experiences, be seen, act out, follow family patterns, and prove something, with the whole thing feeling like barely-contained mania looking for release.","src":"haiku"},
"fontaines-d-c~sundowner":{"opus":"A dreamlike drift through sadness and foresight, where everything reached for turns to nothing by morning. Fontaines D.C. wrap their post-punk in narcotic haze — the speaker grasping after someone he'd hate to lose, wanting only to feel it and hold it a while, knowing it always dissolves back into the dream.","src":"opus"},
"foo-fighters~all-my-life":{"haiku":"A man searches endlessly for something that never fulfills him, cycling through people and connections. Each time he thinks he's close, the prize is taken away.","opus":"Grohl's restless hunt for something that never satisfies, coded through imagery of appetite and craving. The chase itself becomes the addiction, each conquest immediately abandoned for the next, want outrunning any possible fulfillment.","sonnet":"An insatiable craving — for something never named — that devours everything and satisfies nothing, described through imagery of compulsion, self-harm, and the relief that comes only in the exhausted act of giving in before moving on to the next obsession.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~best-of-you":{"haiku":"A person is trapped in relationships where others extract the best of themselves, leaving them weakened and exploited. The song condemns parasitic intimacy and emotional depletion.","opus":"A confession of being someone's fool gives way to defiance: refusing to let anyone claim the best of you, breaking loose from chains, nooses, and abuse while swearing never to give in. Faith, pain, and hard-won resistance intertwine. Foo Fighters, Dave Grohl's arena-rock institution, built this into a stadium anthem, its clenched-throat chorus turning survival of exploitation into shared release.","sonnet":"A man who has endured a draining, controlling relationship assesses the damage and refuses to break. The tension is between exhaustion and defiance — he will not give in, but he acknowledges the cost of holding. The question of who is extracting the best of him runs beneath everything, equal parts accusation and honest self-inventory.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~breakout":{"haiku":"A partner's behavior triggers the narrator's emotional instability and causes him to break down. He struggles with appearing unstable and fears losing himself while caught in this pattern.","opus":"A frantic, funny burst of frustration at someone who drives the narrator to the edge, provoking the exact reaction he'd rather not have. Under the energy it's about being pushed until you lose your composure and hate yourself for it.","sonnet":"Someone or something in the speaker's head is driving them to the edge of a breakdown — therapy, obsessive thought loops, and the awareness that they look as disheveled and consumed as they feel, all building to the need to burst free.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~everlong":{"haiku":"A narrator has waited for connection with another, envisioning a moment of perfect emotional union that lasts forever. The plea is to sustain that feeling indefinitely without abandon or loss.","opus":"The Foo Fighters' surging love song captures the vertigo of hurling yourself completely into another person, desperate for the bond to endure as something genuine and lasting. Its single request is a plea for the other never to pull away at the exact moment he's most vulnerable.","sonnet":"An intense, all-consuming connection with another person — almost hallucinatory in its closeness — and a plea that it never stop, that the feeling could last forever.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~learn-to-fly":{"haiku":"Quest for transcendence through radical change: the speaker seeks escape from stagnation and lies, looking for salvation and learning to fly away from exhaustion and betrayal.","opus":"A signature Foo Fighters radio anthem where the singer begs any higher power for rescue and a fresh start, dismissing the present order as false. Scanning the heavens for salvation and proof of purpose, he yearns to blaze out spectacularly. The repeated appeal betrays a tired hope that he cannot ascend on his own.","sonnet":"A person adrift in disillusionment and self-made inertia reaches toward something transcendent — a revolution of the spirit, a burning bright — while admitting he cannot make the climb without help. The aspiration is genuine but tethered to honest exhaustion: he will find his way home only once he learns to rise.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~low":{"haiku":"One person clings to another, begging them not to leave; their connection exists on the edge of collapse as they drag each other downward.","opus":"A song of mutual descent, two people using each other and sinking together, intimacy tangled with dependence. The pull is downward by design: pleasure and self-destruction sharing the same low.","sonnet":"Two people in close proximity who can't quite reach each other — one on the outside, one on the inside — locked together in a mutual descent, the narrator clinging on and pulling his partner as low as they can go rather than letting go.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~monkey-wrench":{"haiku":"Resentment over wasted time and caged existence manifest as refusal to serve another's needs or be broken by their expectations.","opus":"A refusal to keep absorbing blame in a relationship where the narrator would rather leave than keep suffering as the thing that gets thrown to fix everything. Grohl frames finally quitting as being uncaged and free after years of holding it all in his head.","sonnet":"A speaker declares liberation from a suffocating relationship — refusing to be the instrument of someone else's damage, remembering every word said, acknowledging they were always caged — and now finally free, not wanting to be anyone's monkey wrench.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~my-hero":{"haiku":"Someone idolizes a person who bleeds and breaks like everyone else—the song questions whether heroism lies in being ordinary, not extraordinary.","opus":"A tribute to the everyday, ordinary hero rather than the celebrity kind-someone who bleeds it out and endures while others fade; the deeper read is a call to recognize heroism in the unremarkable person beside you, honoring resilience over glamour.","sonnet":"A tribute to an ordinary person who does something extraordinary without fanfare — the hero isn't a legend but someone unremarkable who steps up and walks away from the mess they've made right, and the song treats that quiet action as the highest form of courage.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~no-way-back":{"haiku":"Psychological surrender overtakes consciousness as isolation metastasizes; commitment demands reckless faith in an irrevocable threshold.","opus":"Grohl throwing himself past the point of return, done living in his head and ready to commit fully to someone or something. The stance is reckless devotion, no going back and not caring.","sonnet":"A speaker marooned in his own head — emotionally dead, starved for truth — reaches a point of no return where past damage is irreparable and he no longer cares, offering a hand to whoever will meet him in that ruined place.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~stacked-actors":{"haiku":"Shallow celebrity culture breeds inauthenticity and deception, reducing humans to fabricated personas performing for empty truth-seeking.","opus":"A caustic takedown of Hollywood phoniness and manufactured tragic starlets, demanding truth from an industry of liars and aging performers who die blonde. Grohl piles up the fakery like corpses stacked to the rafters, widely read as aimed at celebrity mourning theater.","sonnet":"A savage indictment of Hollywood artifice — celebrities as stacked dead actors performing aging drag-queen routines, trading in beautiful chests, famous disguises, and hired liars — with the sardonic chorus that we cry when they all die blonde.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~the-pretender":{"haiku":"A person refuses to conform to others' expectations and games, confronting pretense while also acknowledging his own role as both mirror and antagonist in the cycle.","opus":"A defiant confrontation with a manipulator who keeps others in the dark, refusing to be just another pawn or to surrender, and revealing himself as the buried voice and enemy that will bring the pretender down.","sonnet":"The narrator refuses to be kept in the dark or manipulated by someone who performs sincerity while hiding secrets, positioning himself as the mirror that forces a reckoning with who that person really is.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~this-is-a-call":{"haiku":"The speaker summons past versions of himself and broken dreams, calling them back to confront unresolved patterns and abandoned commitments.","opus":"Foo Fighters' debut single, Dave Grohl's first statement after Nirvana's collapse, tumbles through absurd, nursery-bright non-sequiturs about fingernails, balloons, and Ritalin. Beneath the nonsense a recurring cry addresses all his past resignations, a knowing wink at leaving one life behind. The playful surrealism marks a fresh start built almost single-handedly, celebration and release tangled in deliberately opaque lines.","sonnet":"A loose, associative address to past selves — strange domestic and pharmaceutical images accumulate without resolving into a single narrative. The recurring call feels like an invitation to reckon with abandonments and resignations, a summoning back from a long absence into something unfinished but not yet lost.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~up-in-arms":{"haiku":"Cyclical abandonment and return create unstable attachment where the narrator cannot escape or forget despite being left.","opus":"The jittery reunion of a couple who keep parting and rejoining, the narrator confessing he was always the one to walk out yet cannot get her out of his mind and returns worked up all over again. It captures the loop of a romance that will neither finish nor settle.","sonnet":"A speaker who always left but always came back confesses he is once again up in arms over a woman he cannot forget — the two of them reunited, his lonely heart falling apart for her to mend, love persisting despite his history of leaving.","src":"haiku"},
"foo-fighters~white-limo":{"haiku":"Self-doubt and wasted potential spiral in someone stuck repeating cycles, warned against lost opportunities and the rides they've squandered.","opus":"A deliberately chaotic thrash throwback, its garbled taunts and non-sequiturs less a message than a delivery system for speed and abandon. Foo Fighters cutting loose for the sheer noise of it.","sonnet":"A chaotic, impressionistic rant about someone stuck in a pointless cycle — trapped in a habitrail of bad choices, wasted opportunities, and self-destruction — with the limo as a symbol of an escape that never quite arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"food-house~8-now":{"haiku":"A chaotic reflection on ego, social anxiety, mental illness from social media, fantasy role-play, and destructive desires wrapped in absurdist humor.","opus":"A hyperactive, self-aware collage of internet-poisoned bravado, threatening and then undercutting itself, obsessing over Twitter-induced mental illness, mosh-pit bruises, and cartoonish threats to shoplift and vape. It even breaks the fourth wall to beg for real lyrics. Food House, the duo of Gupi and Fraxiom, works in the maximalist hyperpop lane, and this glitchy, ADHD-paced barrage of references and self-parody is exactly that scene's aesthetic.","sonnet":"Chaotic self-mythology delivered at hyperspeed — projecting danger, then cuteness, then spite, then shoplifting fantasies, all while nursing a bone-deep social resentment toward people who only have what they have because of who they knew. The song is a genre-hopping mess on purpose, the disorganisation being the whole point.","src":"haiku"},
"food-house~mos-thoser":{"opus":"A hyperactive queercore rap from the collaborative duo, weaving campus-cop surveillance, trans liberation, and gleeful cultural provocation into a manic manifesto. It reframes gender transgression as insurgency, name-checking a departed producer as patron saint, and treats making straight society flinch as its own reckless, communal joy. Chaos becomes identity, celebration becomes protest.","sonnet":"A hyperpop celebration of queer chaos and countercultural identity: the narrator gleefully drags people into lunacy, frames trans and queer existence as liberatory, mocks straight conformity, and revels in being a disruptive, scene-defining icon.","src":"sonnet"},
"for-fear-itself~r-t":{"opus":"A deathcore descent into self-condemnation, where the narrator accepts hell as deserved and turns rage outward at a betrayer whose faith is declared misplaced. Guilt curdles into vengeance and a wish to see the other buried. It reaches for the poisonous logic of someone who has decided both they and their enemy belong in the dirt.","src":"opus"},
"for-the-fallen-dreams~re-animate":{"haiku":"Resurrection from death's threshold; the speaker calls for emergency revival, refusing finality and demanding rebirth despite all evidence of decay.","opus":"A dying voice begging to be revived, stranded in a failing body and arraigned for its failings even as the pulse ebbs. The raw core is one animal wish: to draw a single breath more before the end sets in.","sonnet":"A desperate plea to be saved from what is framed as emotional or spiritual death — the narrator is already fading, bleeding out, drifting toward an afterlife reckoning, and begs someone to resuscitate them before the last warmth is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"for-the-fallen-dreams~stone":{"haiku":"Someone wakes each day unable to escape their past, surrounded by people yet alone. They fear becoming a forgotten name etched in stone, haunted by voices and inner demons.","opus":"Every morning the narrator wrestles to hold together a life that keeps slipping, feeling utterly alone even in a crowded house and unsure whether he'll pull through or end up dead. For the Fallen Dreams push their metalcore toward defiance, its haunted refrain finally resolving into a vow to fight his demons and rebuild from within.","sonnet":"Survival under the weight of inner voices and encroaching death — each waking day brings the same hollow crowdedness, the same fear that this breath might be the last. The question of whether a name gets etched in stone or the demons get faced and rebuilt against drives everything.","src":"haiku"},
"for-the-fallen-dreams~the-undertow":{"haiku":"The speaker sinks under pressure but learns to survive, having lived shallow and shadowed lives before finding the strength to break free.","opus":"Despair depicted as drowning, where staying afloat and surrendering amount to the same thing; the deeper pivot is a hard-earned rule for lasting, that you must clutch onto anything and cherish your blessings before they vanish, drawn from a lifetime paddling in the shallows and going down by yourself.","sonnet":"About feeling like you're drowning in life's difficulties regardless of how hard you try — the narrator has spent their whole existence sinking alone in shallow emotional waters, and the song is a plea to hold onto something before everything slips away.","src":"haiku"},
"for-the-fallen-dreams~unstoppable":{"haiku":"A person overcomes repeated setbacks and is strengthened by adversity, learning resilience and refusing to be stopped by hatred, breakdown, or the world closing in.","opus":"For the Fallen Dreams turn metalcore into a resilience anthem, a narrator drifting from the plans he laid and shrinking in the bed he made, yet learning exactly how much his bones can bend before they break. The insistence that hatred and blows from others only forge someone unstoppable pairs with the vow to rise again, hardening struggle into defiance that no longer fears death.","sonnet":"A rallying cry built from accumulated failure. With each breath and step carrying the weight of bad choices and broken plans, the speaker and those around them discover resilience not as a given but as something earned by repeatedly rising after being flattened.","src":"haiku"},
"for-the-likes-of-you~rot-with-me":{"haiku":"Internalized shame and cycles of abuse breed disgust; the speaker rots alongside their tormentor, unable to escape complicity or forgive the silence of those who should have protected them.","opus":"Self-loathing and dread spread like disease as the speaker begs to be let to rot, running from knowledge he was never taught. It turns outward to condemn those who prey on the weak, demanding they own their crimes when they face them.","sonnet":"A dual-fronted address: the speaker contends with their own terror and diseased inner life — tendrils writhing, husk rotting — while also turning toward someone who preys on the weak, demanding they face their crimes with pride; the refrain \"rot with me brother\" collapses both inward collapse and outward confrontation into shared decay.","src":"haiku"},
"forbidden~chalice-of-blood":{"haiku":"The song condemns organized religion as deception and corruption, depicting priests and congregations as morally bankrupt and spiritually hollow.","opus":"A Bay Area thrash indictment of the church as a racket, the pulpit trading truth for tithes while a corrupt priest damns his flock. Religion exposed as fear and greed masquerading as salvation.","sonnet":"An indictment of the corrupt church — priests who prey on their congregations, false doctrine used to extract money and obedience, religion as a mechanism of deception that leads people straight to Satan rather than God.","src":"haiku"},
"forbidden~off-the-edge":{"haiku":"Youth spiraling into suicide and chemical destruction. Little Johnny and his friends fall off cliffs and trip on drugs, losing their way and their lives.","opus":"Forbidden, a technically sharp Bay Area thrash band, spin cautionary schoolyard vignettes of kids driven past their limits, from a desperate class clown to acid-fried friends who wander to their deaths. As the narrator runs short on time and cracks himself, the song frames lives ruled by drugs as squandering their futures, the lure of the abyss shadowed by genuine wreckage.","sonnet":"Drug-induced tragedy at a school: two teenagers high on acid walk to a cliff edge and fall, one of them fatally. The song mourns lives lived in chemical escapism under the influence of others, and a narrator who feels himself sliding toward the same precipice — snapping, falling, gone.","src":"haiku"},
"foreground-eclipse~dear-are-you-getting-sober":{"haiku":"Intoxicated forgetting grants passage to inaccessible transcendence; the past self remains unreachable.","opus":"A seductive push to abandon the past and dissolve into one endless party, insisting there's no returning once you step across. The tenderness toward the person being led masks a darker aim, that the old self and old memories are meant to be erased for good.","sonnet":"A party invitation blurs into intoxicated disorientation and then something darker: the speaker coaxes someone through a window to a blinding place beyond all memory, where there is no way back from the window they have crossed — \"I want to go back / I can't go back\" closing the loop between escape and loss.","src":"haiku"},
"foreign-hands~chlorine-tears":{"haiku":"Hands separated across distance; love unrequited and eyes blind to belonging denied.","opus":"The metalcore act render abandonment as chemical burn, an angel of departure who leaves a permanent scar. Grief here is corrosive rather than tender, laced with unforgiveness toward the one who left. It reaches for the disbelief of loss still fresh, and a faint, distant hope that the burning might someday cool into something bearable.","sonnet":"A devastating parting from someone called the angel of departure — eyes still blind, secured into shadows, the sorrow swelling until tears burn like chlorine on frail skin. The death trails painted in silence, the mark final, and the vow I'll never forgive you as long as I live is the only thing left standing.","src":"haiku"},
"foreigner~juke-box-hero":{"haiku":"A poor boy hears a guitar through a wall, buys one, teaches himself, and rises from obscurity to become a rock star through relentless practice.","opus":"Foreigner's classic origin myth of a rock star: a broke kid shut out of a packed concert catches a single guitar bleeding through the wall, scrapes together a battered secondhand instrument, and rides that one fixation to fame, convinced music is his only escape route.","sonnet":"A story of a kid locked out of a sold-out concert who hears one guitar through the wall, buys a cheap second-hand instrument, and dedicates his whole life to becoming a rock star — pure working-class dream of escape through music.","src":"haiku"},
"forever-still~something-wrong":{"opus":"A raw admission of feeling fundamentally broken while confronting an abuser who answered her with silence and violence. She asks only to be seen through her own eyes for a single day, and whether even that would be believed or met with another turning away.","src":"opus"},
"forged-in-fire~caged":{"opus":"A metal portrait of intrusive thoughts as inmates in a porcelain cell that's cracking apart. Trapped and running in circles, the narrator asks whether life is anything more than a slow march to the grave, reaching for the losing battle against one's own mind as the walls come down.","src":"opus"},
"former-wrestlers~weekend-hobbies":{"haiku":"Two people build spacecraft models and dream of becoming astronauts, sacrificing their social life for a shared vision of space exploration and cosmic adventure together.","opus":"A tender, whimsical daydream of two people building spacecraft in their spare time and vowing to become astronauts, the shared project quietly standing in for a relationship worth sacrificing weekends and friendships for. The promise to reach where the stars end carries no regret. Former Wrestlers work in gentle indie-pop, and the sweetness of turning a domestic hobby into a cosmic love metaphor fits that lo-fi, homespun charm.","sonnet":"Two people spending their free time building a spacecraft together as a shared project of imagination and devotion — the weekends disappear into it, friends adjust, and the couple keeps dreaming of launch day. It is a love song disguised as a hobby, the spaceship just a way of saying: I want to go everywhere with you.","src":"haiku"},
"fort-minor~where-d-you-go":{"haiku":"Heartbreak and abandonment transform a speaker into a cautious survivor, determined never to love so completely again.","opus":"Mike Shinoda writing the loneliness of a partner left behind while he's endlessly away, then splitting into a bitter breakup narrative. It is about missing someone and the wreckage of trust when distance and suspicion corrode a love.","sonnet":"A man processes the end of a relationship with a mix of genuine regret and hard-won anger, recognising that her jealousy poisoned what he tried to build, and that he is better positioned now to find someone who gives what she withheld. The hook's longing sits uneasily beside the verses' resentment.","src":"haiku"},
"foster-the-people~helena-beat":{"haiku":"Mental anguish tightens despite superficial coping; self-restraint battles the impulse toward oblivion.","opus":"An upbeat mask over depression and self-destructive coping, tying yourself down so you don't fall, sipping poison and insisting you're okay. The bright chorus is the lie you tell to survive the days you can't get out of bed.","sonnet":"A speaker navigating addiction, depression, and low-grade self-destruction admits to poisoning himself and tying himself down to avoid falling further — he's not recovered, just holding on tight enough to stay functional.","src":"haiku"},
"foster-the-people~life-on-the-nickel":{"haiku":"Urban survival demands constant hustling; poverty traps the speaker in cyclical struggle against time.","opus":"A restless portrait of hustling and homelessness on skid row, scraping by and half-crazed, calling himself king of a world that's got nothing for him. It is survival as delusion and defiance in equal measure.","sonnet":"A street-level survival narrative from someone hustling endlessly without getting ahead — self-medicating, writing home, insisting he's calm while the world reduces him to a predator-prey dynamic where eating first is all that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"foster-the-people~pumped-up-kicks-outrun-my-bullet":{"opus":"This reworking of the breakout hit inhabits a troubled boy who finds a gun and imagines violence against his peers. The buoyant sound deliberately masks the menace, dramatizing how alienation curdles into a threat nobody sees coming. It reaches for the chilling gap between a catchy chorus and the isolated mind narrating a shooting.","src":"opus"},
"four-get-me-a-nots~never-again":{"opus":"A resolve to press forward without a backward glance, crushing envy and wounded ego, steeling the nerves, no longer scared of loss. The painful costs get recast as what forges strength — the narrator fixes a date and a place, leaves it all behind and swears not to look back again.","src":"opus"},
"four-stroke-baron~cyborg-pt-2-the-city":{"haiku":"Existence as machine and trauma merge; the speaker questions survival itself while witnessing systematic destruction of everything that mattered.","opus":"A cyborg awakening amid a burned, survivor-less city, refusing to be a mere slave or spectator as it demands to know why the world was destroyed and vows to save what's left.","sonnet":"A cyborg or augmented survivor returns to a destroyed city, witnesses the devastation with bewilderment, and resolves to save whoever remains — a sci-fi narrative of reconstruction and the refusal to simply watch while civilization burns.","src":"haiku"},
"four-stroke-baron~duplex":{"opus":"A synth-tinged rock reverie about a man driving toward an affair and a woman whose pull overrides his sense of home. The repeated plea to conceal the truth signals guilt riding shotgun. It reaches for the seductive unraveling of a double life, the drive into the sun standing in for a point of no return.","src":"opus"},
"four-stroke-baron~friday-knight":{"haiku":"Nocturnal excess and contradictory strength define the narrator as both emptiness and electricity, a hollow figure performing vitality while bleeding loneliness.","opus":"A self-crowned party knight whose swagger curls into isolation, born to bring the fun yet ending up alone at the top, bleeding and unloved. The bravado of the shining knight and the empty man walking out the door are the same figure.","sonnet":"A self-appointed party knight arrives with bravado and hollow zeal, but the second half inverts the image: the armour is dark and empty, the sword swings and bleeds, and no one is actually with him at the tippy top.","src":"haiku"},
"four-stroke-baron~planet-silver-screen":{"opus":"A restless craving to matter, snarled together with the empty theatrics of Hollywood; the singer feels pressed into performing under studio lights and cannot work out what the state of California actually signifies to him.","sonnet":"A disoriented person in Hollywood — or the idea of it — feels compelled to perform without understanding what California or stardom means to them, caught under alien grey skies, longing to be someone but unsure what that even looks like.","src":"sonnet"},
"four-stroke-baron~prostitute-part-ii-pretty-woman-makes-money":{"opus":"A sardonic art-rock riff on transactional desire, where allure is measured purely in money and a golden narrator becomes untouchable, unaffordable. Attraction and commerce collapse into one another. It reaches for the hollowness of being wanted only as a commodity, and the cold power that comes from turning oneself into currency.","src":"opus"},
"four-year-strong~aftermath-afterthought":{"haiku":"Obsession and decay intertwine as the speaker seeks meaning in ruins, the aftermath becomes irrelevant when the moment itself is hollow.","opus":"A dare toward mutual vulnerability, digging past skin to the spine to find where someone hides, in a world of overstimulated rats. The resolution is scorched-earth, cut the power and light the fuse, let whatever comes after not be worth worrying about.","sonnet":"A sardonic look at emotional overload, distraction, and the way people choose numbness over reckoning — the speaker is already cracking at the seams, watching others get bored of the real problems and shift focus to anything else, reaching the point of wanting to burn everything down.","src":"haiku"},
"four-year-strong~bad-habit":{"haiku":"Destructive patterns masquerade as attraction, the speaker cannot release what they know will consume them, desire and self-knowledge irreconcilable.","opus":"A person cast as an addiction, so appealing the speaker wishes he could keep indulging even knowing better, his soul already forfeit. The scratch-the-craving loop and his lost restraint mark a compulsion masquerading as longing, a fixation he can't kick.","sonnet":"A frank acknowledgment of being hooked on someone who is clearly wrong for you — the speaker knows they have no self-control, that this person is a compulsion rather than a choice, and that the craving to reach out is exactly as irrational as any bad habit.","src":"haiku"},
"four-year-strong~daddy-of-mine":{"haiku":"Harm masquerades as intimacy; the speaker craves the violence that destroys them, unable to break the cycle of self-inflicted pain.","opus":"An abusive attachment the singer can't leave, half-thanking the person who turns him black and blue because no one else can hurt him so intimately. The refusal to say anything and settle for a nowhere life keeps the cycle intact.","sonnet":"Addressed to someone who turns the speaker 'black and blue' in a cycle of pain they can't escape — 'why can't the beginning ever feel like the end?' — the narrator is stuck in a nowhere they've accepted as home, holding their breath rather than releasing what's been done to them.","src":"haiku"},
"four-year-strong~maybe-it-s-me":{"opus":"A self-interrogating reckoning where the speaker keeps concluding he might be the problem, wanting to perceive and feel things on his own schedule. He needles a partner fixed on a fantasy of relocating to Japan she'll never actually chase, both of them stuffed with dreadful plans that corrode to powder, nobody ever measuring up.","src":"opus"},
"four-year-strong~out-of-touch":{"opus":"The pop-punk band map alienation onto Golding's island of feral boys, casting the narrator as a creature with nobody left to frighten. Bad advice and self-harm feed a sense of belonging nowhere, reaching for the numbing trick of using excuses as bandages and the belief that things get better the further you drift from where you began.","src":"opus"},
"four-year-strong~uncooked":{"haiku":"Denial and excess become a way of life, indulgence masking deeper discontent as the speaker chases experiences without commitment, wasting days in reckless avoidance.","opus":"The manic thrill of self-destruction sold as freedom, wanting to waste days and make mistakes just to feel motion. Underneath the spinning is denial holding the whole thing together, the repeated promise to be fine sounding less convincing each time.","sonnet":"A frenetic spiral of wanting and refusing at once — the speaker craves sensation, distraction, and the comfort of denial, lurching between wanting to feel everything and wanting to shut it all off, finally forcing themselves to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"four-year-strong~vasoline":{"opus":"A cover that preserves the source's slippery dread, picturing people as insects mired in a greasy trap, unable to distinguish the genuine from the marketed. Bewilderment and blindness spiral. It reaches for the feeling of being pinned in place forever, in a slick, unescapable limbo where certainty keeps slipping away.","src":"opus"},
"fox-lake~dog-eat-dog":{"haiku":"Predatory hierarchies consume communities as trust evaporates and survival demands ruthlessness, compassion becoming a liability in systems designed for mutual annihilation.","opus":"A hardened code of loyalty and survival in a cutthroat world, demanding silence, keeping your head up and never flipping on your crew. It sneers at cowardly public figures who wield fear by typing threats they'd never carry out themselves.","sonnet":"Survival in a cutthroat world where fake people talk behind backs and public figures use fear as a weapon without ever pulling the trigger themselves; the speaker calls out the hypocrisy and walks away — done, gone — rather than play the runaround.","src":"haiku"},
"foxblood~charlatan-blues":{"haiku":"Deception and fraudulence, sadness masking dishonesty and fakeness.","opus":"A brutal act of self-reckoning that ties a late neurodevelopmental diagnosis to ruined relationships and numbing substances. Rage at frauds and political power surfaces alongside dark jokes about death that cut both ways, as both attack and grim self-assessment. The speaker sounds marooned between wreckages he caused.","sonnet":"A late-diagnosed neurodivergent speaker burning every bridge back to someone who could have told the truth but didn't — left somewhere in the middle, grinding teeth like days, self-medicating with whiskey and opiates while the politics of charlatans and parliament guns close in. The bitterness is surgical: \"you could've told the truth, but that's on you.\"","src":"haiku"},
"foxblood~gallows-song":{"opus":"A grim reckoning with alcoholism and the partner it drove away, the narrator pacing a house he now haunts alone. Bargaining with God comes too late. Comparing the ruined relationship to a fallen empire, it reaches for the aftermath of self-inflicted collapse, the irony never lost even as everything else is, swinging toward an inevitable end.","src":"opus"},
"foxblood~kill-the-lights":{"haiku":"Battling depression, addiction, and self-harm; darkness waits even after attempts at healing.","opus":"Foxblood confront addiction and depression unflinchingly, a narrator rationing his medication to keep it working, physically sick and coming apart while others urge him toward either the bottle or a quick end. His own thoughts turn against him as despair waits to swallow him. The refrain about withdrawing into himself edges toward a suicidal brink, his faint plea for rescue nearly lost beneath the collapse.","sonnet":"A raw account of mental illness and substance dependency — the body cycling through withdrawal while the mind cannot locate a bottom. Pills managed in halves, white-knuckled nights, a darkness described as lying in wait: survival here is not triumph but a grinding, uncertain staying-in.","src":"haiku"},
"foxblood~kind-regards":{"haiku":"Cynical resignation pervades the speaker's farewell to false hope; buried in sand, civilization rots as poisoned water floods while the house always wins and the game remains rigged.","opus":"A furious political broadside signed off as if from someone already gone, aimed at institutions that profit from sickness, poison the water and let violence flourish behind everyone's back. The dealer always wins, yet people keep playing, the whole thing scorched with contempt for manufactured despair and for those who choose blame over the way out on offer.","sonnet":"A letter signed \"The Day Before You Died\" dismantles the systems — political, religious, institutional — that poisoned the water, burned the books, and kept people playing a game the house always wins, addressed to those too complicit or too broken to walk away when shown the exit.","src":"haiku"},
"foxblood~this-town-needs-guns":{"haiku":"Self-medication, fatalism, and critique of society's violence form a cycle where escape seems impossible and survival becomes the only goal.","opus":"A hard-drinking reckoning with vice, doubt, and American dread, the narrator owning his Jameson and codeine while insisting his flaws aren't his enemies, then widening into gun violence, school shootings, and a botched master plan. The bleak title turns bitterly sarcastic. Foxblood delivers it with a rap-tinged rock snarl, balancing self-deprecating honesty against social panic in a way that fits post-hardcore's confessional, politically restless edge.","sonnet":"A self-aware spiral through personal vice, collective dysfunction, and American violence — the speaker medicates with Jameson and Codeine and refuses to apologise for it while the world around them cycles through school shootings and traffic stops. The refrain lands as bitter irony: the town that produced all this damage is exactly what its weapons deserve.","src":"haiku"},
"foxcult~glacier":{"opus":"A metalcore plunge into self-confrontation, the narrator staring into glass to finally see the full shape of who they are, and vowing never to retreat. Guilt and a following echo lead them down a frozen chasm. It reaches for the terrifying clarity of self-recognition, choosing to stay entombed with the truth rather than climb back to denial.","src":"opus"},
"foxera~memories":{"haiku":"The speaker contrasts their own paralysis with someone else's relentless pursuit of their dreams, watching old memories and failures fade while that person moves forward unstoppably.","opus":"A withering kiss-off to a driven, self-deluding striver so consumed by chasing the future they've mapped out that they humiliate themselves and forfeit everyone's trust. The melodic-metalcore band Foxera pairs cold introspection with contemptuous release, the singer shutting the door and dismissing the person as a fading recollection.","sonnet":"Someone watching a more driven, more capable person pursue their vision with total commitment, while measuring their own failures against that image. By the end the tables turn — the speaker closes a door on admiration itself, deciding that memories of this person never served them well, and that the other person's high-risk gamble was always going to cost them.","src":"haiku"},
"frail-body~your-death-makes-me-wish-heaven-was-real":{"opus":"An atheist holding a loved one's hand as they die, remembering their belief that red-tailed hawks were reincarnated souls, and desperately wishing heaven were real so the grief could carry the hope of reunion.","sonnet":"A grief meditation on losing someone close, likely a parent or elder, who loved birds and believed in spiritual reincarnation — the narrator scatters ashes at a lake and finds themselves wishing heaven were real simply as a way to bear the loss.","src":"sonnet"},
"framing-hanley~start-a-fire":{"haiku":"Systemic addiction to self-sabotage traps the powerless; the game's players mistake shame for achievement and cannot escape the cycle.","opus":"A rock anthem of righteous fury aimed at those who mortgage the future and worship the gun while burying the young. The narrator, raised hard, casts themselves as a fighter unafraid to burn it all down. It reaches for the moment defiance tips into open revolt, a refusal to lie down quietly against corruption and gun violence.","sonnet":"A political rallying cry from people who've been screaming from the nosebleeds too long: gun violence is framed as a religion of its own — \"prayer is a slippery slope when you worship the bullet\" — and the song demands not just protest but arson, burning the corrupt system to the ground because laying down is over.","src":"haiku"},
"frank-carter-the-rattlesnakes~battlefield":{"haiku":"Life is war; possessions vanish and loved ones die; the speaker accepts this violent reality as humanity's fundamental condition.","opus":"A stark existentialist verdict that living is a warzone where all your possessions will disappear and everyone dear to you dies unaccompanied; dismissing an afterlife of reward or torment as fiction, the narrator decides the only real flame is the sting of being alive, and the closing stretch of existence is a private matter between the divine and himself.","sonnet":"A stark confrontation with mortality and meaninglessness — life is a battlefield where everything you love will die and there is no heaven or hell beyond the suffering already happening, leaving only the speaker and whatever god might or might not exist at the end.","src":"haiku"},
"frank-carter-the-rattlesnakes~bluebelle":{"haiku":"Mortality arrives suddenly and irrevocably, the worst catastrophe not age itself but the arbitrary violence that ends everything before its time.","opus":"A father's blunt reckoning with mortality, realizing that aging can't be controlled and that death, not disgrace, is the worst thing that can happen.","sonnet":"A brutal compression: the worst thing that can happen is not being caught but dying, and getting old was never something you could control — the song strips away comforting lies in two verses and leaves only the fact of mortality.","src":"haiku"},
"frank-carter-the-rattlesnakes~god-is-my-friend":{"haiku":"Self-perceived toxicity and danger, questioning whether the speaker's nature poisons those they love and whether destruction is their inevitable destiny.","opus":"The former Gallows frontman stages a violent wrestling match with the divine, weighed down as if his body were made of lead, snakes at his neck, questioning whether he poisons everything he loves. The core defiance is a demand to be released, to be cast into sea or storm, precisely because he cannot bear the weight of a love he intends to attack rather than accept.","sonnet":"A figure who experiences himself as toxic to what he loves begs to be released — into the sea, into the storm — but God refuses. Damnation feels more honest than the love being offered, and the speaker resents the retention. The threat of return from hell is not bravado but despair looking for an exit that keeps being blocked.","src":"haiku"},
"frank-carter-the-rattlesnakes~off-with-his-head-feat-cassyette":{"opus":"A furious call to bring down a destructive, tyrannical figure whose words are weapons, escalating into imagery of state violence, executions, and people being shot in their beds.","sonnet":"A toxic, dangerous individual — narcissistic, destructive, a plague on everyone around him — is condemned by the people he's harmed, who call collectively for his removal and envision a world safer without him.","src":"sonnet"},
"frank-carter-the-rattlesnakes~parasite":{"haiku":"A parasitic lover embodies the city's emptiness, extracting and depleting while maintaining intellectual pretense, leaving biological and emotional devastation in its wake.","opus":"A savage takedown of the pseudo-intellectual softboi, the self-styled comedian who quotes Freud and feminists he never read while living off his parents. Carter turns the disgust literal, casting him as a parasite who leaves a woman anxiously checking for infection, better safe than sorry.","sonnet":"A precise character assassination of a pretentious East London man-child — skateboard instead of a car, parents' house in Islington, stolen pint glasses, expired Valium — who is charming enough to burn the women who trust him and too solipsistic to notice.","src":"haiku"},
"frank-carter-the-rattlesnakes~the-drugs":{"haiku":"Addiction as parasitic predator; drugs consume entirely, dismantling everything until nothing remains to lose.","opus":"A brutal reversal of agency, the punk frontman insisting the addiction is the one doing the taking, ripping his life to shreds until nothing's left. Sleeplessness and the bender become a hollow object of worship. It reaches for the honest horror of dependency — the substance as a blade at the throat rather than any chosen escape.","sonnet":"Addiction as a white-knife pressed against the throat — the drugs take you, not the other way around, smashing your mind and stealing your love until falling in love with the habit is just another version of the death you're living toward. Bank notes and white dogs running riot are the texture of a life already surrendered.","src":"haiku"},
"frank-sinatra~i-get-a-kick-out-of-you":{"haiku":"Romantic addiction transcends reason and comfort; only one person ignites the narrator's passion, making all other pleasures pale by comparison.","opus":"Cole Porter's confession that nothing thrills anymore, not champagne or cocaine, except the one person who doesn't even love you back. The joke and the ache is being jaded to everything but a hopeless infatuation.","sonnet":"The narrator confesses to a universal boredom — champagne, planes, perfume all leave him cold — with one exception: the mere sight of the other person ignites a thrill nothing else can, even though they clearly do not return the feeling.","src":"haiku"},
"frank-zummo~hit-the-ground":{"haiku":"Relentless resilience defines the speaker's ethos; falling becomes launching, enemies become fuel, and refusal to surrender transforms defeat into unstoppable momentum.","opus":"A driving anthem of relentless resilience, the narrator swearing that any fall becomes forward motion, unbroken by saboteurs at the finish line. Solitude and struggle are recast as a chosen war. It reaches for the identity of someone who defines themselves by refusing to stay down, treating the punishing life they were handed as the one that chose them.","sonnet":"A relentless forward-motion creed: fall down and you hit the ground running — no fate, no escape route, no one waiting at the finish line except the obstruction you've already left behind. The speaker has gone miles alone, wolves circling, the sky ready to catch them only if they keep moving.","src":"haiku"},
"frankie-and-the-witch-fingers~t-v-baby":{"opus":"A psych-rock satire of screen addiction: television cast as a pacifying deity that rots the brain and pipes dread and appetite straight into passive viewers, while the powerful fatten themselves on the distracted masses. Consumer stupor as willing self-lobotomy.","src":"opus"},
"franky-wah-aetho~should-have-seen-it-coming":{"opus":"A grief over betrayal by someone who tracked the speaker down, wounded them, and cared for another the entire time. The pain is backward-looking: the red flags were plain to spot, and putting faith in those coaxing looks slowly tips affection toward resentment.","src":"opus"},
"franz-ferdinand~take-me-out":{"opus":"The Scottish art-rockers' breakout hit fuses courtship with the language of a firing squad, the narrator a crosshair a shot away from someone they know they cannot leave with. Desire and self-annihilation blur. It reaches for the paralysis of wanting badly while expecting nothing, a come-on that doubles as a plea to be finished off.","src":"opus"},
"frau~stinkfist":{"haiku":"Stimulation dulls sensation into meaninglessness, only deeper sensation can restore feeling, driving ever-inward in pursuit of a connection that requires escalation.","opus":"Escalating penetration as a metaphor for a culture numbed by overstimulation, always needing more extreme sensation just to feel alive. Maynard's lyric mourns the death of subtlety, digging deeper into the borderline because nothing means anything anymore.","sonnet":"A cover of Tool's song about desensitization and the escalating search for sensation: someone so numbed by overstimulation that they need increasingly extreme physical intimacy just to feel real, framed as both erotic and as an honest critique of what chronic numbness does to a person.","src":"haiku"},
"frayle~summertime-sadness":{"opus":"A doom-laced reimagining of the Lana Del Rey ballad, slowing its glamorous melancholy into something heavier and more funereal. A goodbye kiss, a black dress, a reckless midnight drive. It reaches for the euphoric fatalism of loving someone so completely that leaving, or dying, feels almost sweet, dread and beauty pressed into the same breath.","src":"opus"},
"freddy-scott~this-is-a-trent-reznor-song":{"haiku":"The track is a parody that mocks Trent Reznor's compositional formula of building intensity, adding percussion, varying dynamics, and ending with haunting piano.","opus":"A loving spoof of Nine Inch Nails performed in Trent Reznor's persona, narrating aloud the very tricks it lampoons, interchangeable verses, tacked-on rhythms, an odd guitar, a chorus stalled by an unexplained noise. It closes, as announced, with eerie keys that make the whole thing feel like a spook attraction. The gag lands because each ridiculed move genuinely works.","sonnet":"A comedic deconstruction of Nine Inch Nails songwriting formula — the dynamic shifts, the creepy piano, the random percussion break — played straight enough that the mockery becomes a kind of affectionate tribute. The joke is that even dissecting it note by note, it still sounds pretty awesome.","src":"haiku"},
"fred~l-ch":{"haiku":"Desire and forbidden attraction driving the speaker toward transgression, passionate momentum regardless of consequence.","opus":"A bitter kiss-off to a manipulative partner cast as a parasite feeding on broken hearts. The narrator wishes the love had never happened, feeling foolish for the wasted time and haunted by an old message. It reaches for the specific sting of realizing you were never a priority, only an excuse in someone else's rotation.","sonnet":"Post-breakup clarity about a leech who never really cared — canceled plans, constant excuses, lies dressed as affection, and a haunting text message written everywhere. The wish is not for reconciliation but erasure: I wish you never loved me, because it cost me everything and you were never really there.","src":"haiku"},
"freqgen~future-1991-freqgen-vip":{"haiku":"Nostalgia and disillusionment clash; the speaker longs for a past time of clarity while criticizing present confusion and performative living. The future echoes the past—all cycles repeat.","opus":"A synthwave ode to nostalgia as fuel, reaching back to a formative moment to move forward, urging the listener to put the camera down and live rather than perform, the past reframed as the engine of the future.","sonnet":"A call to reclaim something authentic from a media-saturated present by anchoring to the past — put down the camera, march to a different drum, let the past begin again as the future — the year 1991 as shorthand for a moment before self-performance consumed everything.","src":"haiku"},
"freqgen~future-1999":{"haiku":"Virtual transcendence suspends two souls in digital eternity, a sanctuary from earthly entropy where connection survives as holographic memory.","opus":"A synthwave romance dressed in cosmic imagery, love as a midnight train ride with a polygon girl toward Andromeda. Nostalgia for 1999 anchors the reverie against the vast unknown. It reaches for the tender fantasy of two souls escaping into deep space together, the futurism a costume for a very human longing to be kept close.","sonnet":"A science-fiction love story set in deep space: two beings connect 'at the soul' during a last trip around the sun, suspended in 1999 as a fixed point of memory, the blood 'running deeper' each time the speaker returns to that frozen moment of contact.","src":"haiku"},
"friday-pilots-club~idwbs":{"haiku":"Everything rots under sun and gaze. A speaker rejects fame and personhood, unwilling to become someone feeding desire.","opus":"A darkly ironic refusal of fame, the singer playing a shameless, self-serving performer while confessing he doesn't want to be the one sung about. Everything decays in the light, and the hunger for more is the trap itself.","sonnet":"A song about the exhaustion of ambition and the hollow performance of wanting more — the narrator sees through their own hunger for status and recognition, suspects everything rots eventually, and confesses a growing feeling that they don't want to be anyone at all, yet can't stop chasing.","src":"haiku"},
"friday-pilots-club~life-support":{"opus":"A breakup song about severing a manipulative tie, the ex still calling months after leaving. Their need for control is diagnosed as a drug, the relationship as a machine keeping the narrator half-alive. It reaches for the hard exhale of cutting the cord, insisting on the freedom to no longer be tethered to someone else's need to dominate.","src":"opus"},
"friday-pilots-club~nosedive":{"opus":"A jaundiced look at performing chaos for an audience that craves it, questioning whether anyone becomes who they meant to be. The show demands manufactured crazy and a hidden hypocrite. It reaches for the exhaustion of self-parody, the dare to cross a line just to be believed, and the freefall of giving a crowd the spectacle it wants.","src":"opus"},
"friday-pilots-club~nothing-or-forever":{"opus":"A vulnerable ultimatum of love, the narrator guarding their heart yet handing it over completely, framing the stakes as all or nothing. Surrender feels sweet and total. It reaches for the terrifying leap of letting someone in enough to be broken by them, believing again after a season of suffering because no one wrecks them better.","src":"opus"},
"friday-pilots-club~nowhere":{"haiku":"The speaker abandons earthly connection for transcendent vacancy, finding someone who promises escape into the void together.","opus":"A rebound love that goes nowhere and that is exactly the appeal, a new person who looks nothing like the ex and offers oblivion instead of destination. She is cast as divine and deathly at once, the beautiful edge of his own undoing.","sonnet":"A new person — 'interstellar and divine' — takes the speaker somewhere entirely unlike the old religious certainty addressed to 'Mr. Jesus,' and that somewhere turns out to be nowhere, which is exactly where the speaker wants to be, freed from the weight of what they were before.","src":"haiku"},
"friday-pilots-club~poison-or-patience-feat-oston":{"haiku":"Self-loathing and paralysis trap the speaker in spiraling hatred, where escape feels impossible and change unattainable.","opus":"Self-hatred choosing between medication and endurance just to get through a day that feels identical to the last. The joke that he doesn't need a therapist because he has his 1s and 0s, an internet hero performing for a screen, exposes depression sold back to him as content he can't afford to make original.","sonnet":"About chronic self-loathing and the search for something — poison or patience — to make it bearable: the narrator is trapped in self-critical spirals, spiralling on the bedroom floor, and has replaced therapy with internet community, but suspects they'll never afford to be original enough to escape the cycle.","src":"haiku"},
"friendly-jvck~stopthinking":{"opus":"Anxiety and a poisonous romance pressing in like a closing room, the speaker convinced his fate is grim whether or not she's around. She leaves chaos, returns to him, craves control and constant rumination, and he finally settles on being finished, drained with nothing more to offer.","src":"opus"},
"from-first-to-last~genesis":{"haiku":"Self-sabotage and psychological collapse threaten to contaminate those nearby; fear of personal destruction drives isolation.","opus":"A post-hardcore vision of impending self-destruction, the narrator hiding in their worst habits and refusing to drag a loved one down with them. Yet the plea is not to be abandoned. It reaches for the push-pull of someone spiraling inside their own head, clinging to the promise that if they go under, they will not go alone.","sonnet":"The opening of something is also the edge of destruction: the speaker sees a future filled with poison and regret and refuses to let whoever he's addressing follow him into it, even as the grip loosens — if we go, we go together, not as surrender but as bond.","src":"haiku"},
"from-sorrow-to-serenity~resurgence-reimagined":{"opus":"A collective rallying cry for renewal, people who endured nameless subjugation and lay awake in torment now awakening, clawing out of the dirt to breathe again and reclaim hope, framing it all as their resurgence.","sonnet":"About waking from a collective state of passive suffering and choosing to claw back to life — the song frames previous numbness as a dormancy that is now over, insisting on breathing again and reclaiming hope rather than spiraling in the same cycles of grief.","src":"sonnet"},
"from-sorrow-to-serenity~shadows":{"opus":"A descent into insomnia, depression and existential dread, the narrator befriending fear, dwelling on the past, resenting his own inner light and giving in to the shadows, finding the only peace in the prospect of final sleep.","sonnet":"About depression as an external predatory force — shadows creep in, the narrator resents what little light remains within themselves, and the song ends with resigned acceptance that sleep will eventually bring the only peace available.","src":"sonnet"},
"front-line-assembly~mechvirus":{"opus":"The industrial veterans build a dystopian science-fiction narrative of interstellar war and human consciousness fused into machinery, progress collapsing into a new technological dark age and a great exodus. It reaches for a cold posthuman dread, casting humanity itself as a self-replicating virus, a war-machine with no cure, laying whole worlds to waste.","src":"opus"},
"fronti-res~timeless":{"haiku":"Circular repetition traps the narrator in contemplation of past transgressions, time crystallizing around a permanent deed while escape remains impossible.","opus":"A person crushed by guilt over a terrible deed, replaying images of a body destroyed by water while time refuses to move. The bruised knees of endless kneeling and the final plea to be ended make it a confession that self-forgiveness is impossible.","sonnet":"Paralysed by a terrible act whose clock never moves, the speaker is crushed by guilt-soaked pain — crashing through waves, tangled in roots — and, bruised knees and all, begs simply to be ended.","src":"haiku"},
"frontierer~tumoric":{"haiku":"The speaker views themselves as a spreading malignancy spreading harm and chaos, running wild with no escape while questioning responsibility for the destruction.","opus":"A violent, disorienting descent into self-destruction and creative decay, imagined as a sickness metastasizing from an artist's work while the narrator turns on his own creator and triggers a total collapse. Scottish-American mathcore act Frontierer match the imagery of corroded flesh and a fatal one-way plunge to their dense, glitching chaos.","sonnet":"A violent purge of something cancerous — a poet whose influence spreads like disease, a speaker who decides the only way out is to cut through everything, including the body, including the maker. The song cycles through shutdown and restart as if rebooting after catastrophic system failure, arriving at death as both threat and release.","src":"haiku"},
"frontieres~mirage":{"opus":"A bilingual track voiced by a self-styled god of sleep, the Sandman who reigns over every dreaming mind through the night and claims command of the waking and slumbering alike. He reveals that the listener was never truly in charge; on waking they simply pass from one illusion back into another, always under his sway.","src":"opus"},
"frost-children~bound2u":{"haiku":"Toxic orbit maintained by false belief: running away and returning endlessly, each lie believed anew because the binding feels stronger than departure.","opus":"Helplessly bound to a restless lover who never sticks around and lies beautifully, choosing to believe the lie rather than say goodbye. The touch is a crime you keep committing.","sonnet":"A song about being unable to leave someone who is unreliable and emotionally withholding — knowing they never stick around, that their sweet words are lies, and yet finding yourself bound to them against your better judgment.","src":"haiku"},
"frost-children~burn":{"haiku":"Suppressed rage breaks silence as souls demand visibility beyond societal constraint, burning through falsity toward authentic expression.","opus":"A bilingual hyperpop rush about breaking out of suffocation, sung partly in Japanese from atop a forbidden rooftop with the urge to pull down the sun. Hiding gives way to combustion. It reaches for the electric threshold where someone done staying quiet finally demands what they want, ready to let the pent-up self ignite.","sonnet":"Crushed by a city's rigidity and conformity, someone hidden on a forbidden rooftop wants to pull the sun down; the speaker coaxes them out of silence — \"say what you need to\" — promising the fire of honest expression is worth it. (lang: ja)","src":"haiku"},
"frost-children~control":{"haiku":"Manipulation and emotional toxicity shatter autonomy, demanding liberation from lies embedded in false intimacy.","opus":"A frenetic plea to seize agency after being stripped bare, life shredded in record time. As the lies wear off, the narrator wants a real friend rather than someone chasing a fairytale. It reaches for the desperate reclamation of self after being taken over, a demand to finally hold the controls of one's own life.","sonnet":"Someone has exhausted all patience with a person who preferred fantasy to honesty — \"you wanted a fairytale life\" — and demands their agency back, the repeated plea to \"let me take over control\" escalating from request to ultimatum.","src":"haiku"},
"frost-children~daijoubu-desu":{"opus":"Sliding between English and Japanese, the speaker forces out a hollow reassurance that everything's fine against the plain truth that it isn't, sick of being mistreated and twisted up inside. Self-love feels like a con they paid for, life begun without consent, and the only want is to vanish, alone on a corner meeting no one.","src":"opus"},
"frost-children~dirty-girl":{"haiku":"A reckless woman tests boundaries and expects devotion despite her instability; the speaker catalogs her contradictions without judgment.","opus":"An obsession with a self-destructive, dishonest woman who fascinates precisely because she is trouble. The chant reduces desire to an ultimatum, take her or leave her, while the verses admit he keeps coming back for more of the problem.","sonnet":"An unstable, lying woman who is magnetic precisely because of her chaos — the speaker knows she's trouble, calls out her dishonesty, but can't stop wanting more from her, drawn to the reckless energy she brings even as it damages him.","src":"haiku"},
"frost-children~electric":{"haiku":"Intense physical and chemical attraction overload the senses; bodies collide into hypersensitivity and addiction.","opus":"A maximalist dance-pop charge about wordless physical attraction, two bodies converging on a floor where talking becomes beside the point. The sound sells itself as salvation for the night. It reaches for the pure, present-tense charge of desire in motion, an invitation to abandon tomorrow and let the beat carry everything.","sonnet":"Pure dancefloor presence — two bodies in non-verbal alignment, \"you dance like you like me / I dance like I like you back,\" and the charge between them is the event itself. The song doesn't develop a narrative so much as sustain the one electric moment of mutual recognition all the way through.","src":"haiku"},
"frost-children~harp-pony":{"opus":"A disarmingly candid song about gender dysphoria wrapped in childlike wish-imagery, the narrator naming the body they were born into as a curse and admitting a hunger for attention and love. It reaches for the ache of wanting to be a girl, and the tangled longing to be seen, indulged and never alone.","src":"opus"},
"frost-children~mayfly":{"opus":"A twitchy self-portrait of insecurity, the narrator casting himself as a spiteful short-lived insect with limbs up, bashful and jumpy and quick to renege. Fixated overthinking sours into feeling like a failure who needs to start over. It reaches for fragile bravado about wanting nearness without demands, then melting into a cosmic daydream of drifting off into the stars together.","src":"opus"},
"frost-children~satellites":{"opus":"A churning affection-and-cruelty spiral where tenderness and spite turn into mutual bargaining chips, two people cohabiting in ruins. Daydreams of a future incarnation, drawn nearer, cut through the venom. It reaches for the addictive pull of a poisonous pairing and the plea to keep a feeling alive, even as it eats away at both people trapped in its pull.","src":"opus"},
"frost-children~sister":{"haiku":"Sisterhood bonds through unspoken understanding and parallel becoming, where one imitates the other's escape. Abandonment leaves profound questions about separation and loss between closely mirrored selves.","opus":"A sibling elegy full of shared childhood and things left unsaid, one sister watching the other quietly leave and mirroring her in the mirror. The tenderness turns fierce in the plea to turn around and reclaim herself from whatever tore them apart.","sonnet":"A sibling's grief at a sister who drifted away — remembered in dandelion wishes and shared summers, last seen driving off while the water kept running — the speaker pleads for her to turn around and face them, furious at whatever took her.","src":"haiku"},
"frost-children~what-is-forever-for":{"haiku":"Cyclical romance between transient bodies asks whether fleeting passion justifies the inevitable pain of repetition and abandonment.","opus":"A restless meditation on impermanence disguised as a fling, late trains across the city and a week both parties can already predict the end of. The refrain wonders why lasting should matter at all. It reaches for the outlook of a serial romantic who trusts what they want will come around again, treating the rush as worth every crash.","sonnet":"A recurring downtown encounter — cross-town train, a few days together, drinks and false intimacy — has the speaker chasing what keeps returning to them while questioning whether \"forever\" means anything in a life of serial yearning.","src":"haiku"},
"frostbite-orckings~guardians-of-time":{"haiku":"Viking conquest imagines eternal victory and domination through force, the narrator ascending as temporal guardian commanding darkness and destiny.","opus":"A bombastic Viking conquest fantasy, warriors marching gloriously to victory under ravens with hammers raised to the sky. It is pure battle-metal bravado about seizing promised lands and refusing to bend.","sonnet":"A Viking-mythologised force declaring itself guardian of time — hammer raised, ravens flanking — advances toward victory and conquest of promised lands with absolute certainty, turning back time if needed.","src":"haiku"},
"frostbitt~blizzard":{"haiku":"Violent chaos tears the speaker apart; liberation and transformation demand accepting that escape requires surrendering control to destructive force.","opus":"A metalcore cry for escape and transformation, the narrator thrown about in a storm yet refusing to accept it as their fate. Change and the release of pain are offered as a challenge to a witness. It reaches for the raw demand to be freed and reborn, treating the blizzard as both the ordeal and the crucible of survival.","sonnet":"Brief and elemental: thrown around and ripped apart by a blizzard that is also the chaos of life itself, the speaker demands escape and freedom from what feels like destiny. The question — can you stand the release of my pain? — is addressed to someone whose tolerance for the storm is the only thing in question.","src":"haiku"},
"frost~day-and-age":{"haiku":"A figure stands defiant amid a dying civilization, warning that the world is burning with quiet rage and has lost all sense of meaning and conscience.","opus":"A caustic prog-rock sermon that greets the listener into a squandered existence, then turns its scorn on a decaying era simmering with suppressed fury. The narrator scrabbles for a sense of self and meaning in a culture he sees as having gone completely off the rails.","sonnet":"A man on the edge of something — crushed by pride, burning with quiet rage in a dying age — who decides the writing is on the wall and claims he's about to make himself remembered, though the world has lost all sense.","src":"haiku"},
"frost~eat":{"haiku":"A parasitic insect observes its predator, boasting of its own elusiveness and survival. The hunter cannot catch what sustains itself by feeding on the hunted.","opus":"A witty predatory monologue from the point of view of a fly or insect that thrives wherever humans dine, taunting a would-be swatter that it can't be caught while promising to feed on the dead and burrow under the skin. Frost is the solo project of British progressive-rock musician Jem Godfrey, and this playful entomological menace, delivered with a compound-eye's cold amusement, fits the band's clever, melodic prog sensibility.","sonnet":"Sung from the perspective of a fly — drawn to human death, thriving in the spaces humans create, utterly unbothered by every human attempt to catch or kill it. The admiration is genuine and unsettling: the speaker appreciates how effectively humans destroy other species while pointing out that this cleverness will never be enough to catch something as ancient and adaptable as a fly.","src":"haiku"},
"frost~evaporator":{"haiku":"Escape and dissolution become inevitable; the speaker plans to vanish without trace, leaving only absence and memory in their wake.","opus":"The quiet skill of slipping away unnoticed from a hollow place ruled by a fake deity in a flimsy crown, departing without a message to attempt a different existence. The closing passage dissolves the speaker into vapor and memory, everywhere and nowhere at once.","sonnet":"A person decides to quietly vanish from their life and their suffocating town — leaving no note, telling no one — dissolving into the background until they become nothing but a faint trace, a ghost in the archive, finally free.","src":"haiku"},
"frost~heartstrings":{"haiku":"A person caught in cycles of pain and dissatisfaction pulls at their own heartstrings, running endlessly without escape while cycles repeat and bridges burn.","opus":"A troubled song about a wounded child shadowed by an absent, unfeeling mother who told him she didn't want him, alongside images of a Christmas amid conflict; grown up, he still circles the old injury, urged to fill his chest and tug at his own emotions before the roof gives way.","sonnet":"A song about a person shaped by absent or harmful parenting who runs endlessly in circles, unable to escape the past — the 'open letter to an absent parent' sits at the center, surrounded by imagery of bridges burning and lungs running out of air.","src":"haiku"},
"frost~moral-and-consequence":{"haiku":"Mechanical existence gives false hope; the subject remains trapped despite illuminating fire.","opus":"A man reduced to a spent machine amid war and static, groping through rain and neon toward a spark of light where fear and self dissolve into something whole. The closing turn insists the surrounding dread belongs to others, and offers a way to think differently and enjoy the ride.","sonnet":"A speaker wired into a system of light and noise — lo-fi walls, neon icons, fire in the sky — breaks free from the crowd pressing down on them, the rain they should have seen coming, and emerges convinced that the sounds of fear belong to someone else and the thoughts in one's head are worth living by.","src":"haiku"},
"frost~pocket-sun":{"haiku":"A contemplation of love as a source of escape through time and alternate possibilities. The speaker has experienced profound journeys through history and fantasy, yet the beloved remains their only constant anchor.","opus":"A wistful, time-slipping love song where a window onto past and future collapses under longing for one person to anchor to. The Verne and burning-Rome flourishes turn it into a lonely traveler adrift through eras, wanting only to rewind.","sonnet":"The narrator describes a personal talisman — a \"pocket sun,\" a window-like source of light — that anchors him across disorienting journeys through time and imagination, expressing a longing to rewind and hold onto someone who feels like a lifeline.","src":"haiku"},
"frost~skywards":{"haiku":"Grief over loss and the failure to save someone; regret over inaction and the weight of memory. The speaker urges looking inward at pain rather than seeking false comfort from prayer or upward-gazing.","opus":"Frost, the British prog supergroup led by Jem Godfrey, address a grieving figure paralyzed by regret over a lost boy, whose weeds grow faster and suffocate everything. Insisting the sky is not to blame, the song urges facing forward and walking back into light rather than staring upward at unanswered prayers. Comfort in despair gives way to a hard-won turn from mourning toward the living world.","sonnet":"A grieving figure cannot stop looking upward for answers about a lost boy, but the sky holds nothing — prayers go unanswered, memory warps into suffocating weed. The imperative throughout is to stop seeking salvation above and instead turn to face forward, walk in the light, and live with the wound.","src":"haiku"},
"frost~terrestrial":{"haiku":"A solitary figure gazes outward from isolation with quiet desperation, unable to reach beyond the barriers of distance and time that separate them from another person.","opus":"A bleak meditation on solitude and hard-won clarity. Its narrator, gazing out over the ocean in muted despair as empty hours drift by, treats truth as an endurance test. His insistence that he perceives more than others sits beside real mourning, an acceptance he'll never again know a particular person, casting isolation as both insight and permanent loss.","sonnet":"Solitude held past the point of peace becomes frozen — staring out to sea waiting for sunrise, the figure endures a quiet desperation that distance has softened into silence. What lies out there remains unknown. The repeated certainty that a specific face and voice are gone forever anchors the yearning in irreversible loss.","src":"haiku"},
"frost~the-solid-state-orchestra":{"opus":"A widescreen prog epic about seeing past a hollow paradise of glitter and shallow living, the narrator scanning for wreckage and other survivors. A voice cuts through the emptiness, then goes silent. It reaches for the nerve to torch every retreat and walk toward a genuine light, rejecting a poisoned world masquerading as life.","src":"opus"},
"frost~toys":{"haiku":"A narcissistic figure views himself as a dangerous force of nature and spectacle, comparing himself to water, a superstar, and various powerful entities. He plays with people and accumulates crimes while remaining untouchable and seductive.","opus":"A slippery, sardonic study of ego and manipulation, the narrator preening as a shape-shifting mirror who amplifies others' fears and confesses future crimes with a carnival barker's glee. It's about the seductive danger of a hollow, self-adoring persona.","sonnet":"A portrait of an ego-driven performer or personality who knows they're seductive, destructive, and self-aware about both — the speaker admits to amplifying fears, committing future crimes, and drawing people in only to wrap them up and never let them go. The carnival-barker \"step right up\" framing makes it a critique of charisma as manipulation.","src":"haiku"},
"frost~waiting-for-the-lie":{"haiku":"People play games of self-preservation, tell convenient falsehoods, and choose deception over undignified truth; everyone waits for and expects lies.","opus":"Frost examine the little untruths people rely on to keep going, treating flattery and social ritual as armor against reality. Across heartbreak, blame-shifting, and official statements, the band argue that everyone quietly hopes for a comforting falsehood because the honest version is too humiliating to face.","sonnet":"Deception is presented as universal social infrastructure — compliments deployed tactically, truths too undignified to speak, excuses rehearsed before delivery. Every conversation, every political speech, every intimate moment carries the same anticipatory dread: the listener already knows the lie is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"frost~wonderland":{"haiku":"Escaping despair through music and movement, finding vitality in the moment despite feeling broken. The speaker chooses the present thrill over past pain.","opus":"An elated ode to being alive, a displaced hero landing and dancing into brightness and noise while dread and doubt hover just behind, the repeated advice to withstand the pressure casting survival as a thrill rather than a fright. Frost, Jem Godfrey's British prog-rock project, nod to their own debut album here, and the soaring, hook-laden joy captures the band's bright, ambitious optimism.","sonnet":"A hero figure who has drifted from purpose, described from outside as someone who steals hope and collapses into zero — yet the song itself pulses with electricity, insisting that being alive is still a wonderful feeling. The tension between those two positions, decline and vitality, never resolves; the heat keeps coming and the song says take it.","src":"haiku"},
"fuckin-whatever~houdini":{"opus":"A wry, image-drunk confession of feeling like a drowning escape artist performing for a crowd, the levitation pure show. Under the spectacle is a plain, aching wish to simply be enough, the loneliness of the entertainer who disappears in smoke, resented and admired yet still starved for the sense of being sufficient.","src":"opus"},
"fucking-werewolf-asso~the-tito-beltr-n-massacre":{"haiku":"Chaotic theft of body and soul merges violation with ecstasy; betrayal and consumption intertwine in dance.","opus":"A chaotic Swedish party-punk blast built on absurd, aggressive imagery of theft, eight crushing arms, and ex-friends' warnings. Sense is beside the point. It reaches for pure adolescent id and dancefloor mayhem, a gleefully nonsensical adrenaline rush that prizes energy and provocation over any coherent story.","sonnet":"A chaotic provocation built around betrayal by ex-best friends and being crushed by 'eight arms,' interspersed with a looping dance instruction; the deliberate incoherence and aggression feel more like disruption ritual than narrative, the title itself operating as absurdist threat.","src":"haiku"},
"funkadelic~maggot-brain":{"haiku":"Someone reflecting on spiritual awakening and corruption in the world, choosing to rise above decay rather than be consumed by it.","opus":"George Clinton's brief 1971 monologue frames despair as a choice between rising above rot or sinking into it, with a battered, exploited planet as backdrop. It's the spoken doorway into Funkadelic's title track, a psychedelic-funk landmark whose real payload is Eddie Hazel's marathon guitar solo that follows.","sonnet":"A cosmic address to the listener before what is essentially a guitar elegy — Earth is pregnant for a third time, humanity has poisoned the mind of the universe, and the speaker has looked directly at the rot and chosen to rise above rather than drown in it. Everything after the spoken intro is grief without words.","src":"haiku"},
"fun~some-nights":{"haiku":"Existential uncertainty about purpose and identity oscillates between triumph and anguish, framed through lost romance and crisis of meaning.","opus":"Nate Ruess turning a Springsteen-scale anthem into a crisis of not knowing what he stands for, the war-and-glory bluster undercut by exhaustion, a haunting ghost, and the small saving grace of family found in his nephew's eyes.","sonnet":"A restless crisis of identity and purpose plays out across late nights and existential questions — the speaker unsure what they stand for, selling out for a dream that feels hollow, mourning family while clinging to rare moments of genuine feeling found in the worst circumstances.","src":"haiku"},
"furia~op-taniec":{"haiku":"A possessed dancer is controlled by spirits, compelled to move in circles while bound and drowning in confusion. The person experiences trance-like possession where they're enslaved to movement and internal chaos.","opus":"In Polish, a delirious, ritualistic depiction of possession and self-resurrection, a vast night pouring through the senses and circling within, driving the narrator to whirl in a dance of fire. A cascade of coined rhyming epithets (a dazed possessed one, a bound stupefied one, a scorched drowned one) evokes a trance of manic, self-reinforcing circling until dawn, binding infinity to itself end to end.","sonnet":"In Polish: about a trance-like compulsion that wakes the narrator in the night and forces them into a spinning, possessed dance — drawing fire up from trees, noise from rain and discord, circling endlessly until dawn. It's a ritual of self-resurrection through obsessive motion, tightening bonds with oneself loop by loop.","src":"haiku"},
"fury-weekend~delirious-feat-the-anix":{"haiku":"Technological metaphor for emotional dysregulation; the speaker glitches between connection and isolation, haunted by presence across impossible distance.","opus":"A synthwave transmission from someone drifting through collapse and static, clinging to the image of a lost love as the last signal in the noise. The system-and-signal language frames devotion as a message sent across silence, promising to meet again till the end.","sonnet":"Drifting through digital and emotional static, lost in signals and self-pretense, the speaker hears their lost person's voice inside the noise — delirious and unable to move on, still holding a picture of them and certain they will meet again.","src":"haiku"},
"futakuchi-mana~king":{"opus":"Futakuchi Mana works a jagged, confrontational strain of Japanese vocal music where cuteness and menace are deliberately fused. This song sits inside that contradiction: images of confinement and playing cute jostle against wanting to kill or die, all circling the chanted declaration that both figures are king. It oscillates between mocking a controlling presence and the narrator's own hunger to seize power — the performance of adorability turned into a weapon and a cage at once.","sonnet":"A jagged, confrontational song about confinement and the performer's own conflicted self-image: references to being locked up (幽閉), playing cute, and wanting to kill or die jostle against the chanted declaration \"you are king / I am king\" — the song oscillates between mockery of a controlling figure and the narrator's own hunger to claim power.","src":"sonnet"},
"futakuchi-mana~rule-of-nines":{"opus":"A ritualistic, body-horror-tinged meditation on submission and pain, the narrator offering themselves at a shrine and learning a sadist's rules. Burns, lesions, and a dream of schism blur the line between self and suffering. It reaches for the disorienting place where giving in becomes identity, life lived in the figurative shape of a sacrificed body.","src":"opus"},
"futakuchi-mana~your-new-morning-alarm":{"haiku":"A aggressive wake-up call—vulgar profanity demanding you get up and stop wasting time in bed.","opus":"A profane wake-up call built entirely from screaming at someone to get out of bed, the whole track functioning as a literal, aggressive alarm.","sonnet":"A song that is exactly what its title says: an extremely profane, extremely aggressive command to get out of bed, addressed with full contempt to a lazy sleeper. The subject is the act of waking up; the emotional register is theatrical, performed hostility.","src":"haiku"},
"future-foundation~future-foundation":{"opus":"A revved-up rallying cry casting a crew as an ascending force overrunning the planet, doom-laden and defiant, celebration welded to revolt. Us-versus-everyone swagger meets a swipe at a poisonous world. It reaches for shared adrenaline and the nerve of an underdog gambling on itself, treating each night as a fight worth conquering side by side.","src":"opus"},
"future-islands~before-the-bridge":{"haiku":"Love's end forces reckoning with what remains; the narrator acknowledges that forgetting love means regretting it, and regret defines what was worth keeping.","opus":"A tender farewell that walks a lover home and lets them go, tangled in whether forgetting a love means regretting it. Sam Herring turns the parting into a hard question about what love and its loss really are.","sonnet":"A painful leave-taking after a relationship ends: the speaker returns what was given, hopes the other person has what they need, and grapples with the paradox that forgetting love is itself a form of regret — so they cannot quite let go.","src":"haiku"},
"future-islands~haunted-by-you":{"haiku":"Absence and memory collapse time; the speaker is haunted by someone who defines them, every creation circling back to the one they cannot reach or release.","opus":"Every song the singer writes circles back to a lost love who was the only home he ever knew, now slipping away. It's about grief that turns the work itself into a perpetual haunting, and the ache to feel unguarded and young once more.","sonnet":"A song about being unable to separate a person from the act of songwriting: every song brings the speaker back to them, and the relationship between holding and running, closeness and distance, maps onto the way a haunting works — you are held down by something you can't leave alone, and it is both suffocation and home.","src":"haiku"},
"future-islands~walking-through-that-door":{"haiku":"Shared transition and emotional support guide the narrator through loss; they offer companionship as both people navigate memory and the promise of transformation.","opus":"A vow to help someone reach the life and unwept grief they've only dreamed about, holding their hand through the threshold. It is about loving someone enough to guide them toward the years and goodbyes they never got.","sonnet":"The speaker offers to be the steady companion who helps someone reclaim the years they lost to loneliness and unwritten dreams, holding their hand through the transition into whatever comes next.","src":"haiku"},
"future-lil-uzi-vert~off-dat":{"haiku":"Conspicuous consumption and sexual conquest define the speaker's existence; wealth purchased through crime and vice structures every facet of his identity and relationships.","opus":"Two rappers deep in codeine-and-diamond excess trading flex for flex, where dealing and using blur into one loop and the boasts double as an addict's honesty about the relapse waiting at the end of every high.","sonnet":"A high-energy brag about moving drugs, spending on designer goods, and living recklessly, structured around the compulsion to keep hustling and then relapse into excess.","src":"haiku"},
"future-palace~a-fool-on-the-devil-s-reins":{"opus":"A performer casts their own suffering as a circus act, a jester in chains inviting the crowd to watch their world burn. Self-destruction becomes the entertainment, choking themselves for applause while the audience is damned just to keep watching.","src":"opus"},
"future-palace~decarabia":{"haiku":"Dancing in a trance to demonic seduction, trapped in cycles of addiction and shared hopelessness with a lover.","opus":"Naming a demon of the Ars Goetia, it casts a codependent love as intoxicating possession, two people dancing toward a shared grave, the pull to never be sober from someone your rescuer even as he digs the hole for you both.","sonnet":"About two people who are both suffering and unable to fully save each other — the demon Decarabia (a goetic figure) becomes a metaphor for the intoxicating but potentially fatal pull of their bond; they're dancing toward mutual destruction while the love itself is the drug keeping them going.","src":"haiku"},
"future-palace~dreamstate":{"haiku":"Depression anchors the speaker in dreamless sleep where numbness feels safer than feeling; waking into pain becomes unbearable.","opus":"Depression as a longing to stay asleep, where the bed is the only safe place and numbness beats the daily crash back into anger and loneliness. The dreamstate is a refuge where the weight lifts and no one can wake you, the plea of someone lying about being better.","sonnet":"Paralysed by depression — magnetically drawn to bed, drained of dopamine, unable to find motivation — the speaker retreats into a dreamstate where nobody needs them and no weight presses down, confessing that happiness lasts only seconds before they feel like a wreck again.","src":"haiku"},
"future-palace~heads-up":{"haiku":"Self-loathing and repetitive struggle in a crappy situation, fighting irrelevance and worthlessness while forcing persistence.","opus":"Germany's Future Palace fuse melodic metalcore with unfiltered self-loathing, the narrator plugging his ears against his own repetitive spiral of thoughts and loathing how broken he feels while dragging himself onward through a grim stretch. He rejects any pity, brands himself the freak of his own sad story, and waits with thinning patience for things to improve.","sonnet":"Self-loathing held together by a stubborn refusal to collapse publicly. The narrator recognizes the loop — same thoughts, different packaging — and hates the mess they have made of themselves, yet forces each day forward anyway, keeping the head up not from strength but sheer necessity.","src":"haiku"},
"future-palace~illusionist":{"haiku":"A person performing a false persona to survive among those they despise, becoming self-protective and refusing to be diminished by a weak antagonist.","opus":"A survivor's declaration against an abuser, refusing to be pushed down or suppressed again by a weak man, admitting she performs a false, likeable self while learning to become her own protector. The recognition that saving yourself sometimes means escaping a lover gives it real weight. Future Palace are a German post-hardcore band with a female vocalist who alternates clean melody and scream, and this empowerment-through-pain arc fits their emotive metalcore.","sonnet":"Escaping a controlling relationship by recognising the performance one has been maintaining — the speaker admits they have been playing a role to fit in, all while being suppressed by someone weak who mistook dominance for strength. The turn inward is also a turn toward survival: learning to be your own protector when love has become a threat.","src":"haiku"},
"future-palace~malphas":{"haiku":"Possession and suffocation trap the psyche; the speaker gasps beneath a demon that won't release, patience exhausted by endless repetition.","opus":"Depression and self-hatred personified as a demon lodged inside the narrator, every effort a treadmill that snaps back, patience eroded across years. The grief is for the person they might have become had their mind been quiet.","sonnet":"Being possessed and crushed by a demonic inner force — named after the demon Malphas — the narrator describes a self-hatred that strangles and pushes them down, running on a treadmill where every effort is a setback, eroding who they might have been.","src":"haiku"},
"future-palace~paradise":{"haiku":"Someone has lost inner light and joy, seeking an escape or future paradise while running from present pain and internal emptiness.","opus":"Having lost the inner spark that once sustained her, a numbed narrator dances alone just to feel anything and flees a pain she can't outrun toward the dream of somewhere better. Future Palace treat recovery as an open question, the singer genuinely unsure whether survival is worth it while chasing that imagined future at full tilt.","sonnet":"Inner light extinguished gradually, leaving a stone-cold soul dancing alone just to feel warmth. The longing for a future paradise — some safe place ahead where happiness is recoverable — sustains forward motion through pain, even as the gap between present suffering and that dream feels infinite.","src":"haiku"},
"future-palace~the-echoes-of-disparity":{"haiku":"Systemic oppression of women persists despite resistance; the song catalogs harassment, bodily autonomy erosion, and demand for acknowledgment.","opus":"A furious response to the rollback of women's rights, set against street harassment and the cowardice of men who never say their leers aloud. The 2024 abortion imagery makes it a protest against a world that wants everything kept the same.","sonnet":"A song about misogyny and the denial of women's bodily autonomy — the speaker catalogs street harassment, unwanted sexualization, abortion bans, and the exhaustion of fighting for basic respect in a world still trying to retain patriarchal control.","src":"haiku"},
"future-static~venenosa":{"haiku":"Toxic self-awareness and conscious destruction blur as the speaker poisons relationships with their own bitterness, aware of their damage but unable to stop inflicting it.","opus":"An unflinching self-diagnosis of being poisonous, cruising a coastal highway with thoughts of ending it and owning up to wounding the people dearest to him with cutting words. The toxicity is inward and clear-eyed, taking a cruel satisfaction in the damage even as it labels itself venom.","sonnet":"A person who recognises that their sharp tongue and filthy lungs make them toxic to the people they love: driving past a place where people go to die, sitting in shower routines and singing demons into floorboards, they watch their venom creep inward until they take sadistic pleasure in destroying others' pride.","src":"haiku"},
"g2~detonate":{"haiku":"Unstoppable momentum builds toward explosion as collective power surges forward, obliterating doubt and hesitation. This is raw kinetic force ready to detonate everything in its path.","opus":"A brash competition anthem, likely tied to gaming or esports bravado, where the narrator storms every room ready to explode with confidence. Legacy, energy, and a firestarter's swagger drive it. It reaches for the pure adrenaline of dominance, the countdown before going off, an unapologetic declaration of being built to win and impossible to knock down for good.","sonnet":"Pure competitive confidence: the narrator claims the spotlight the moment they enter a room, frames themselves as a detonator set to go off, and dares rivals to face them — self-belief performed as controlled explosion.","src":"haiku"},
"gabriel-garz-n-montano~golden-wings":{"haiku":"The speaker dances on clouds with golden wings, conjuring beauty and divine romance through poetry and command. Angels greet them in sleep as they multiply love through singing.","opus":"The Brooklyn multi-instrumentalist of French-Colombian heritage, blending soul with chamber intimacy, floats a rapturous fantasy of flight, dancing above the clouds with sky and ocean cupped in his hand. A radiant, sharp-eyed figure conjures verse and commands the elements while an Egyptian goddess looks on approvingly, and singing turns love into ever-multiplying, mythic devotion.","sonnet":"A mythologised beloved moves through an elevated, Hathor-blessed existence — dancing on clouds, commanding the desert to flood, conjuring sweetness at will. The vision is divine romance seen through ancient Egyptian imagery: untouched by the world's disguise, radiant, effortlessly creative and attended by angels.","src":"haiku"},
"gabriela-robin~a-1uu435p":{"haiku":"A meditation on prophecy and entanglement—knowing the future is fear; caught between worlds, held down by gravity and electrons.","opus":"Gabriela Robin is the alias under which composer Yoko Kanno sings in invented and half-real languages, so her work already lives on the border between sense and mystery. This meditation on prophecy makes that its subject — the narrator dares to pick up a phone no one has touched, slides between real and imaginary, and becomes a conduit for frequencies that dissolve memory and fill the air with rain. The core is the vertigo of accessing something true that cannot be explained or shared.","sonnet":"A meditation on prophecy as the dread of foreknowledge — the narrator dares to pick up a phone no one else has touched, slides across the border between real and imaginary, and becomes a conduit for frequencies that dissolve memory and fill the air with rain and mystery. The emotional core is the vertigo of accessing something true that cannot be explained or shared.","src":"haiku"},
"gabriela-robin~a-biqlt2":{"haiku":"A mystical incantation with obscured meaning—repeated syllables evoke ritual and trance without semantic content.","src":"haiku"},
"gabriela-robin~a-qyix1l":{"haiku":"Non-linguistic incantatory chant; possibly invoking or summoning across a liminal boundary, with repetitive vowel sounds suggesting ritual or transcendent experience.","opus":"As Yoko Kanno's singing pseudonym, Gabriela Robin specializes in constructed vocal languages designed to sound meaningful while resisting translation, and this track is pure example of the method. The text is invented phonetic script with no recoverable semantic content — voice used as instrument and incantation rather than speech. Whatever it evokes comes from ritual sound alone, the human voice pushed past language into texture.","sonnet":"The lyrics consist entirely of phonetic syllables that do not correspond to any identifiable natural language — the text as rendered is a constructed or invented phonetic script with no recoverable semantic content. NONE","src":"haiku"},
"gaijin-smash~mountain":{"haiku":"Descending from internal mountain grants clarity; meditation yields solution against life's exhaustion.","opus":"A weekend depression cycle lifting into resolve, Sunday-morning dread and social-media pollution giving way to a Monday solution and the sense that you only get one life. Coming down from the imagined mountain is the calm of having found what you were looking for.","sonnet":"A speaker cycles through Sunday melancholy, monochrome confusion, and social-media pollution until Monday brings a solution — life is once, so you have to act — the English refrain of \"coming down from the mountain in my mind\" marking each descent from interior chaos into clarity.","src":"haiku"},
"gai~same-old-story":{"haiku":"Familial betrayal and rage—cycles of broken loyalty and manipulation destroy trust; the narrator condemns hypocrisy and rejects redemption through false piety.","opus":"A furious family rupture, a betrayed narrator spitting at a manipulative relative who claims loyalty while lying and taking. Under the venom is real grief, loyalty demanded and never earned, love reduced to a book shelved and never reopened.","sonnet":"A song of furious confrontation with an absent or manipulative family member — probably a parent — who begged for help while lying, manipulating, and never thinking of the family: the narrator refuses absolution, describes the relationship as \"the same old story\" of dysfunction, and draws a hard line, saying he'd rather hang himself than watch this person drown, but won't mourn them either.","src":"haiku"},
"galleons~crybaby":{"opus":"A nihilistic love song set against personal and cosmic collapse, the narrator bleeding for someone even in dreams while the sun blows into dust. Self-destruction and insomnia leak into apocalypse, reaching for the raw need to be held at the end of everything, insisting on devotion in a world where the villains have won and no one will remember.","src":"opus"},
"galleons~delorean":{"opus":"A death-drive romance about wanting to linger as a haunting rather than fade into a forgotten memory, the narrator letting go of the wheel on an empty night road. Bitterness at a wasted, lopsided love festers under the longing, reaching for the reckless wish to crash into oblivion beside someone, tenderness and resentment fused at terminal velocity.","src":"opus"},
"galleons~playing-with-fire-is-such-a-cliche":{"opus":"A portrait of a broken home told from a child watching parents fight and a father drowning in a bottle. The narrator resolves to tear up his roots and flee before he too is trapped, insisting the family isn't broken but simply born this way. Empathy and escape braided into a quietly devastating survival plan.","src":"opus"},
"gamma-ray~to-the-metal":{"haiku":"Celebration of metal as a unifying force and power that raises passionate believers together in devotion and shared identity.","opus":"A raised-fist tribute to heavy music itself, iron-bodied believers assembling to venerate their sonic idols and revel in the storm of sound. Defiant pride keeps them upright against any doubter left unmoved. Gamma Ray, Kai Hansen's German power-metal outfit born from Helloween, deliver undiluted genre devotion, a sincere anthem to the raw might of the music.","sonnet":"A celebration of heavy metal as a shared, almost sacred force — fans arriving from everywhere, fists raised, united by the same thunder and heat. The song has no conflict beyond the question of whether a listener can feel it; those who cannot are simply outside the covenant. Metal itself is the deity, the community, and the purpose.","src":"haiku"},
"garbage~not-your-kind-of-people":{"haiku":"Rejection and incompatibility crystallize into radical self-definition; otherness becomes source of pride rather than shame.","opus":"Shirley Manson turns misfit alienation into a manifesto, flipping the outsider's shame into pride: the people who despise you are the ones you'd never want to resemble, and being extraordinary means being unclubbable.","sonnet":"A declaration of proud otherness directed at a phonier, conformist world — the speakers refuse assimilation, reject the white noise of people who don't understand them, and assert that their very strangeness makes them extraordinary rather than defective.","src":"haiku"},
"garbage~only-happy-when-it-rains":{"haiku":"A miserable person only finds comfort in sadness, bad news, and dark songs; they smile in darkness and ride depression like obsession.","opus":"An ironic ode to self-aware gloom, the narrator claiming contentment only when life is messy, mournful, or falling apart, playing miserable tunes and grinning solely in shadow; the chorus welcoming grief to be tipped over her turns wallowing into a knowing stance, glamorizing melancholy while half-mocking the urge.","sonnet":"A knowing self-portrait of someone who thrives on misery — the narrator openly admits she seeks out bad news, sad songs, and complicated sadness not despite herself but because that's genuinely where she feels alive, asking her partner to pour their pain onto her.","src":"haiku"},
"garbage~queer":{"haiku":"Alienation and estrangement define the self; the speaker embodies strangeness while exposing others' performative normalcy as fraudulent.","opus":"A predatory come-on that curdles into something darker, hinting at a boy pimped out by his own father and taught to eroticize his damage, with the seducer coldly naming the fear and self-hatred behind his fake smile.","sonnet":"A speaker strips the performance of toughness from a man who hides fear and fakeness behind a hard exterior, calling him the queerest of the queer — not as identity but as the most extreme form of self-concealment — and offering to reveal what's underneath while positioning herself as someone who already knows exactly what's good for him.","src":"haiku"},
"garbage~run-baby-run":{"haiku":"Running from life's cruelty and uncertainty, seeking to keep one's inner truth intact before wasting life in regret.","opus":"Love is named a strange, elusive charm that haunts whoever gives themselves away, yet the narrator urges a beloved to run from street noise and loaded guns and never crack. Garbage, fronted by Shirley Manson, wrap the encouragement in their glossy alt-rock, insisting purity can be kept inside even in a cruel world, life too short to waste before discovering who you are.","sonnet":"An urgent counsel to flee danger and self-loss — love can haunt, cruelty can grind, but something inside remains uncorrupted if the right moment to run is taken. Finding identity before time runs out matters more than any solution the fading light could offer.","src":"haiku"},
"garbage~stupid-girl":{"haiku":"Pretense and wasted potential define a woman who destroys everything meaningful through fakery and arrogance masquerading as strength.","opus":"A takedown of a girl who fakes every emotion and believes in nothing just to be adored, squandering everything she had on a million self-serving lies.","sonnet":"An accusation directed at a woman who performs boredom and coolness to be adored, living on a million lies, faking everything, wasting all she had — the speaker cannot believe the extent of the performance or that anyone would choose such a hollow way of moving through the world.","src":"haiku"},
"garbage~vow":{"haiku":"Cyclical abuse and redemption entangle lovers; transcendent connection coexists with mutual destruction and inevitable return.","opus":"Revenge and desire fused into the same helpless compulsion, the narrator returning to a partner who nearly destroyed her only to destroy them back, martyr and avenger at once, unable to stop what she can't survive.","sonnet":"A cycle of destruction that both parties keep returning to: the speaker has been burned, nearly died, and keeps coming back to tear this person's world apart — unable to stop even as they know the relationship is mutual ruin, the intimacy and the violence wound together inseparably.","src":"haiku"},
"garbage~you-look-so-fine":{"haiku":"Obsessive attraction breeds compulsive possession; the speaker risks everything to possess the beloved while remaining enslaved.","opus":"A seduction laced with sabotage, wanting to break someone's heart and hand over her own in the same breath, insisting she's unlike anyone he's known while the whole thing drifts toward a make-believe happy ending both know is a surrender.","sonnet":"A speaker is utterly consumed by someone she's chosen — tethered, chained, falling — and distinguishes herself from the other women in his past by refusing to share or fake her devotion, going open and bleeding while demanding that he leave everyone else behind and choose her completely.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~a-black-sun":{"haiku":"Childhood innocence and parental love yield to adult suffering; intimacy intensifies pain as the narrator faces mortality and the impossibility of reciprocal protection.","opus":"Numan mourns the death of childhood's sense of endlessness, the dread of feeling the end approach and love turning into something too heavy to hold, softened only by a ghostly promise to remain present after he's gone.","sonnet":"An adult confronts the death of childhood's sense of infinity and painlessness — loss and grief have replaced the hero's role he once played — and promises a dead or departed loved one that he will remain present as wind and feeling, even after he is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~and-it-all-began-with-you":{"haiku":"A person describes total devotion to another, crediting them with creating meaning in their life and promising eternal presence and support.","opus":"A devotional love song of near-total fusion, the singer claiming to recognize his beloved's every deceit, dread, and weeping, brought into being the moment she arrived and pledging to remain by her from her earliest murmur to her final breath before the divine. Gary Numan, the pioneering British electronic artist, softens his usual dystopian chill here into something startlingly warm, though industrial textures still frame the intimacy.","sonnet":"Total devotion expressed as origin myth — the speaker traces their entire existence back to one person, claiming to have been born the day that person arrived, and vowing to remain present through every threshold, even death. The love is so absolute it has no self left outside of it.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~bed-of-thorns":{"haiku":"A person struggles with ambition, fear, and self-criticism, offering their painful existence to others who might handle it better.","opus":"A brooding self-portrait from the electronic pioneer's darker, industrial-tinged later work. Numan casts the artist as someone imprisoned by his own ambition, hiding away and consumed by fear, painfully conscious of how disposable and forgettable he is, and sardonically inviting anyone who envies him to take his miserable place.","sonnet":"A life of ambition, isolation, and self-inflicted suffering offered up with bitter sarcasm — the speaker invites anyone who wants to take their place to go ahead, sleep in the same bed of thorns, feel what they feel. The irony is complete: the life is miserable, and the invitation is completely sincere.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~broken":{"haiku":"Trauma breaks perception; the speaker has witnessed horrors that shatter innocence and leave only emptiness and numbness.","opus":"A voice broken by having witnessed catastrophe insists no one who hadn't seen the same could understand. Apocalyptic visions of a dying world leave the speaker empty, testifying that such sights would shatter anyone.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic witness — having seen skies on fire, oceans dry, mountains fly, the whole world die — tells another that if they'd seen what was seen, they too would feel \"empty and broken,\" the devastation so total it collapses any distinction between internal and external collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~everything-comes-down-to-this":{"haiku":"Relationships crumble as love turns to pain and communication fails, leaving only uncertainty and heartbreak.","opus":"A song of romantic and spiritual collapse, insisting over and over that he cannot foresee how it concludes, shifts, or feels, only that all of it funnels to a single point, with love turned unfamiliar and language abandoned. Gary Numan, the British electronic pioneer, works here in the apocalyptic register of his modern records, letting helpless doubt grind against heavy, dread-soaked production.","sonnet":"A relationship at the moment of collapse, both people bewildered by how love became damage and words became silence. The speaker does not know how it will end or feel, only that everything has arrived at this single irreducible point — and that whatever comes next will have to be faced without any map.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~ghost-nation":{"opus":"From Numan's late dystopian phase, a portrait of the dispossessed surviving in a scorched, godforsaken wasteland. The dead of a fallen world drift as invisible refugees, cast out by cruelty and a laughing deity, becoming embers, whispers, and the guilt that haunts the living. Environmental collapse and human cruelty fuse into apocalyptic dread.","sonnet":"The dead and displaced — a ghost nation of the forgotten — speak from beyond a civilizational collapse, describing a world of dust and horror where righteous forces cleansed them, and god laughed as the sky fell.","src":"sonnet"},
"gary-numan~here-in-the-black":{"haiku":"Darkness becomes a malevolent presence pursuing the narrator, filling them with existential terror while questioning the boundary between nightmare and reality.","opus":"From Numan's industrial, religiously tormented later work, this personifies overwhelming dread as a merciless, hungry entity creeping toward the frightened, isolated narrator. Terrified and unsure whether to flee or feign belief, he starts questioning God about what is even real, as this menacing figure keeps advancing and any hope drains away.","sonnet":"Something malevolent inhabits the dark around a terrified narrator — not a simple monster but a presence that is alive, hungry, and almost beautiful in its viciousness. The speaker cannot decide whether to flee or submit, whether the threat is external or a fracture of his own mind pressing toward a question about God.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~i-am-dust":{"haiku":"Humans reduced to dust wait in divine subjection; machines and celestial forces override human will as people face inevitable extinction and mechanical ascension.","opus":"Numan's dystopian congregation of the fallen, worn-down souls praying for an end and offering themselves to a god or machine. It's a bleak surrender of will, humanity reduced to dust waiting to be claimed.","sonnet":"A collective surrender to entropy and a waiting deity — a group reduced to weakness, fear, and dust prays for the end to come, machines howling in the dark, every person falling one by one.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~intruder-edit":{"haiku":"The speaker positions themselves as an intruder confronting hypocrisy and broken promises, asking if the listener wishes they had listened sooner.","opus":"Gary Numan, the synth-pop pioneer whose later work turned apocalyptic and earthbound, voices the planet itself as a wounded avenger addressing humanity. Listening to its own screams as pretty music, the Earth offers to become the intruder that hunts down the greedy who brought ruin. Prayers and pleas for mercy meet only the recurring taunt that they should have listened more, judgment delivered from a scorched world.","sonnet":"A post-ecological reckoning in which the natural world — or its personification — turns on humanity with cold, merciless clarity. The earth recounts what was burned, what was promised and broken, and offers no absolution: prayers are irrelevant, hiding is futile, and the one life and one home given have been squandered without remorse.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~love-hurt-bleed":{"haiku":"Everything bleeds—love, pain, sight, shame. The speaker has damaged what they loved and hurt what they feared, understanding only that all things suffer and leak their essence.","opus":"Numan's industrial catalogue of exhaustion and guilt, having seen and hurt everything until all he knows is that everything bleeds, a bleak stocktaking of damage done and a hollowed-out uncertainty about whether he still cares.","sonnet":"A stripped-down meditation on damage: everything the narrator has seen, loved, and hurt ends in the same place — everything bleeds — a bleak confession that neither love nor harm distinguishes itself from the other in terms of the wound it leaves.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~mercy":{"opus":"A vengeful, godlike voice confronting someone brought back to a hell of the singer's making, coldly reminding them that forgiveness and mercy are overrated and only prayer might save them.","sonnet":"A wronged figure confronts someone who has arrived too late and forgotten the suffering they caused, withholding forgiveness entirely — mercy is declared overrated, and the tormentor is left alone in a place with no exit.","src":"sonnet"},
"gary-numan~my-name-is-ruin":{"haiku":"Weaponized identity and inevitable harm; the singer embraces names of destruction and darkness, declaring themselves a force that will break the world of anyone they encounter.","opus":"Numan gives voice to the outcast and the damned, an entity named Ruin embracing every label thrown at it, vengeance and heartbreak turned into identity, an anthem of finding your way to belonging through being what the world condemns.","sonnet":"A song about inhabiting a persona forged entirely from abandonment and rejection — the narrator was called broken and evil until he became those things, and now wields ruin, vengeance, and heartbreak as his defining identity.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~the-end-of-things":{"haiku":"Everything the speaker strives for burns with scars or fails to materialize, leaving only cold emptiness. They question whether they belong in this place of coldness and mercy.","opus":"Gary Numan descends into a haunted, uncertain psyche where the chill settles and a faint sound might be a child, a fear, or a figment. A visitor at the threshold could mean pardon, grace, or death itself. A litany of everything striven and suffered for going sour resolves into an exhausted acceptance that there's nowhere he would rather be.","sonnet":"A figure moves through a cold, still space where perception itself is unreliable — shadows that breathe, children laughing in darkness, an endless litany of things sought that never arrived. What arrives instead might be mercy or might be dissolution; either way, there is nowhere else he would rather be than inside this final quiet.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~what-god-intended":{"haiku":"Someone seeks salvation from suffering and existence itself, questioning whether life and meaning even matter to them.","opus":"A grim series of pleas to be rescued from the world, from damnation, from mercy, and finally from the divine plan itself, the narrator admitting he nearly cares and nearly counts for something but is sure of nothing. Gary Numan, the electronic pioneer, threads his long-running atheistic quarrel with faith through this track, the numbed chorus and industrial weight of his later work turning existential doubt into a grinding, ritual entreaty.","sonnet":"A prayer structured entirely as refusal — save me from meaning, from feeling, from kindness, from what God intended. The speaker has retreated so far into numbness that even divine rescue feels like a threat, and the final line lands as the most honest thing in the song: saved from what God intended, for me.","src":"haiku"},
"gary-numan~when-the-world-comes-apart":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts apocalyptic despair and abandonment, questioning existence as systems collapse and seeking connection in ruin.","opus":"An apocalyptic reckoning voiced by someone who refuses the role of hero or savior, describing themselves as a stone-hearted product of others' greed; the real ache is abandonment, repeatedly demanding to know where God was when the world ended, until the final turn where the speaker resolves to go find God instead.","sonnet":"A brutally honest address to God from someone who feels shaped entirely by damage — not a hero or a villain, just a broken person asking where divinity was when everything fell apart, and threatening to find it themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"garzi~growingup":{"haiku":"Ambivalence toward maturity permeates as the speaker questions whether growth requires letting go or whether control remains possible, caught between surrender and autonomy.","opus":"A refusal to grow up if maturity means letting someone go, tangled with numbness and an obsessive thought he can't escape. The chorus reframes his inability to release the past as evidence he isn't in control, pivoting to defiant survival, having come too far to die.","sonnet":"Equating emotional immaturity with survival: the speaker cannot let go and wonders if that means they are not meant to grow or be in control, then turns the question around — embracing pain, fighting back, and choosing to take control rather than die before the finish line.","src":"haiku"},
"garzi~paranoia-with-fever-333-point-north-remix":{"haiku":"Paranoia and catastrophe trap the speaker in a state of collapse they've grown accustomed to, with therapy and medication offering no escape.","opus":"A track about anxiety and dependency, paranoia setting in as the narrator wakes into a private Armageddon, needing help that the drugs don't provide and bracing for a relationship's collapse while insisting the world goes on without him. Garzi works in the emo-rap and hyperpop-adjacent space, and this remix folds in Fever 333 and Point North to push the panic toward a heavier, rock-charged intensity while keeping the confessional core.","sonnet":"Mental collapse dressed as apocalypse — waking into what feels like the end of the world every day, self-medicating with things that only make it worse, and longing for a relationship that may have already burned. The paranoia is intimate and political at once, the speaker asking if the world will go on without them and almost believing it will.","src":"haiku"},
"gatherers~black-marigold":{"haiku":"Morbid devotion merges self-annihilation with worship as the narrator liquefies into canonical offering, spiritual corruption breeding ecstatic dissolution.","opus":"A death-soaked spiral where the singer offers up his body to canonize someone he can't stop thinking about, imagining his own burial from below. The desperate confiding to any listener meets the flat certainty that no miracle or comfort is coming.","sonnet":"Suicidal ideation directed at another: the speaker imagines their own burial beneath a rain of black marigolds and confides that there is no lining, no miracle, only a faint colour swaying in the dark — and the body offered up to canonise someone who no longer matters.","src":"haiku"},
"gaupa~mammon":{"haiku":"Predatory dependence escalates through heartbeat by heartbeat; the speaker embodies malignant control, feeding on the subject's inability to break free.","opus":"The Swedish psych-prog outfit personifies greed itself as a parasitic voice, a tumor that feeds and disciplines its host while feigning tenderness. It probes the toxic grip of dependency, the way appetite masquerades as love, and asks whether satiation is ever possible. The refrain circles the futility of excess, framing consumption as a mutual, self-devouring bondage.","sonnet":"Mammon speaks in first person as the force of compulsive materialism — a sarcoma designed to escalate, feeding the host while disciplining them, making itself impossible to release: \"you hate the way you can't let me go.\" The closing question — \"when is enough enough?\" — is rhetorical; the answer the song gives is never.","src":"haiku"},
"gaupa~moloken":{"opus":"A reckoning with the wreckage love leaves behind, where the narrator surveys damage done to another and to themselves. Guilt and resentment tangle in a call-and-response of haunting and forgetting. Gaupa dresses interior collapse in shifting dynamics, questioning whether a shared life was ever possible or whether absence was the point all along.","src":"opus"},
"gaupa~ra":{"haiku":"Pursuit of divine or transcendent force through wading into liminal space; the speaker chases an illuminating presence that moves between states of slumber and violent awakening.","opus":"Invoking the sun god as both mistress and destroyer, the track drifts toward oblivion as a kind of surrender. Sleep, hibernation, and burial blur into a ritual descent, while wading into lilac water becomes an act of devotion. Gaupa's psychedelic heaviness renders death and worship as one submerged, cyclical motion toward light.","sonnet":"A ritual invocation of RA: two figures wade out into lilac water at dawn to follow the sun god, the silent and violent mistress cutting through with a ruler's love-call; flesh and success are bound together at dawn's beginning, and the whole image holds the weight of something ancient being re-entered.","src":"haiku"},
"gazpacho~hypomania":{"haiku":"The song explores transformation through manic energy and letting go. The narrator realizes they invented an imagined version of someone and must abandon fantasy to discover their true self.","opus":"Gazpacho, a Norwegian art-rock band known for cerebral, atmospheric concept work, offer a haunting meditation on a person the narrator suspects he conjured and is now watching dissolve as the ideas that sustained the figure scatter. Gambling, Plato's cave and a divine dealer deepen the sense of a chosen illusion coming apart, matched to their literary, melancholic style.","sonnet":"The slow realisation that a person you built your inner life around may have been a construction — memories dissolving, the fabric of them gone, and in the wreckage something unexpected: change, improvement, a better version of oneself emerging. The dice have been thrown; the shadows in Plato's cave have spoken.","src":"haiku"},
"gazpacho~upside-down":{"haiku":"Invitation into inverted perception and deeper truth beyond surface appearance. The speaker guides another toward hidden knowledge and the dissolution of ordinary reality.","opus":"Norwegian art-rock band Gazpacho offer a dreamlike reassurance to someone convinced their life is grim and worth fleeing, gently insisting that their perception is distorted rather than accurate. Addressed to a naive listener and drifting through surreal, storybook imagery, it plays like a lullaby for a mind that has come to see everything backwards.","sonnet":"A gentle guide addresses someone trapped in a distorted perception of their own life, urging them to look again at what they are convinced is bleak. The world appears inverted to the lost one — what the speaker sees is a flower waiting to open, if only the dreaming traveler could believe it.","src":"haiku"},
"geddy-lee~my-favorite-headache":{"haiku":"A nihilistic observer retreats into paranoia and detachment, watching the world collapse while anchoring themselves through numbness. They oscillate between denial and despair.","opus":"The song portrays a man on the edge of a quiet breakdown, anchoring himself by watching the sea and TV, caught in cycles of hiding, watching, running, and falling that he can't stop. He apologizes for being so nihilistic and realistic, with little left to say.","sonnet":"A man in a state of quiet breakdown describes habitual withdrawal — hiding, watching the sea or the TV, running until he collapses — while admitting he has little to say and is caught between nihilism and an awareness that reality demands more than he can offer. The image of watching the sea and then seeing it on TV captures the blurring of the real and the mediated.","src":"haiku"},
"geddy-lee~the-present-tense":{"haiku":"Living in the moment without past or future context; awareness expands but the eternal now remains silent and isolating.","opus":"Losing the past leaves the future senseless, so awareness settles into the vivid, almost unbearable present, where a single moment feels real enough to stop you, and noise dissolves into laughter. Geddy Lee, Rush's bassist and vocalist, ventures out solo here, trading the trio's scope for an introspective study of living without blame or victimhood, fully awake to the now.","sonnet":"A meditation on what it means to inhabit the present fully — stripped of past grievance and future anxiety, with no one left to blame. A single remark pulls someone out of the moment and into wider awareness, revealing that consciousness can either contract around noise or open into something almost unbearably real and immediate.","src":"haiku"},
"gemini-syndrome~primordial":{"haiku":"Evolutionary imperative demands transcendence; the speaker glimpses their original design and recognizes the requirement to overcome inherited human limitation.","opus":"A call to remember a forgotten origin, arguing that humanity was designed to evolve and overcome its base nature or amount to nothing. It frames enlightenment as returning to a hidden home inside, the destination that was always the point.","sonnet":"A call to remember the primordial origin inside each person — a hidden home that was always the destination — and to evolve beyond human nature before this incarnation ripens for its inevitable fall.","src":"haiku"},
"gen-and-the-degenerates~girl-god-gun":{"haiku":"Mercurial identity refuses singular naming, embracing contradiction and volatility while resisting categorization and celebrating chaotic self-expression.","opus":"A breathless self-inventory of contradictions, the singer claiming to be everything at once, tender and predatory, holy and lethal. The scattered rush of identities becomes its own manic manifesto of a person too much and too many things to pin down.","sonnet":"A torrential self-inventory — spinning top, angry ocean, polaroid on an ex's wall, apex predator and crying child — culminating in a triple identity of girl, god, gun that is simultaneously right and wrong, good and gone, impossible to pin down.","src":"haiku"},
"gene-krupa~drum-boogie":{"opus":"A swing-era showcase built to celebrate rhythm itself, spotlighting the drummer as the engine of joy. Krupa, the flamboyant kit virtuoso who made drums a solo attraction, turns the number into pure kinetic delight, all stomp and swagger. There is no story beyond the thrill of the beat and the room it commands.","src":"opus"},
"gene-krupa~jeepers-creepers":{"opus":"A giddy standard of infatuation, marveling at a lover's dazzling eyes so bright they eclipse foul weather. Playful slang and cornball charm carry a simple message: love makes every gray day sunny. In Krupa's swing-band setting the tune becomes buoyant flirtation, wonder and desire pitched at a grin.","src":"opus"},
"genesis-owusu~life-keeps-going":{"opus":"The Ghanaian-Australian artist wrestles with the burden of being seen as untouchable, shedding a superman mask to insist he is only a man. Amid burned bridges and near-death, he finds a defiant constant: existence persists regardless. Restless and genre-slipping, it turns survival itself into a strange, grinning affirmation against despair.","src":"opus"},
"genesis-owusu~stampede":{"haiku":"Collective uprising against exploitation and greed demands that the powerless abandon false empathy and fight for survival and dignity.","opus":"A furious surge of momentum and moral appetite, casting rats and fat cats as the corrupt while the narrator hunts empathy amid gold-lust. Owusu builds a rallying charge, a herd moving as one, that pivots on a plainer truth: fight for the people you love. Swagger and violence mask a plea for loyalty.","sonnet":"A sharp-tongued assault on the fat cats and greasy pigs who mistake gold for empathy — the ones below are the real pot of gold — with the crowd told to stampede toward what matters: fighting for the people you love and shaking the ground under those who'd rather profit.","src":"haiku"},
"genghis-tron~dream-weapon":{"haiku":"The speaker phases out of existence into soil, seeking escape from captors; alms and surrender to the Mother as a path to freedom.","opus":"A hushed, psychedelic drift toward transcendence and death, the self sinking away from its captors into cosmic detachment. It reads escape from the body as release, everything finally dissolving into pattern.","sonnet":"A trance-like dissolution of self — the speaker phases out through soil and chatter, released from captors, offered alms from a dead cosmos, and given the chance to escape into the void.","src":"haiku"},
"genghis-tron~pyrocene":{"opus":"An eco-apocalyptic vision of a scorched, sunbaked world where the disowned survivor endures endless burning daylight, praying for a night that will never return.","sonnet":"Trapped in a world of perpetual, scorching daylight — an endless era of uncontrolled wildfire — a speaker struggles to draw a single breath under skies that never go dark, pleading for night that will not come.","src":"sonnet"},
"george-barnett~bad-for-you":{"opus":"A mutual toxic addiction rendered through drug imagery: the two lovers inject and intoxicate each other, knowing they're bad for one another and that they ought to kick the habit, but it feels too good to quit.","sonnet":"A mutually toxic attraction framed as addiction — both people inject themselves into each other's systems, make each other sick, and know they should stop, but the pull is too strong and the high too good for either to actually walk away.","src":"sonnet"},
"george-barnett~nothing-on-the-line":{"haiku":"Predatory charm exploits vulnerability; the speaker makes easy targets bleed because stakes remain illusory until loss becomes irreversible.","opus":"A seducer who wins easily precisely because he risks nothing, styling himself the king of solitude who comes for the heart and slips away. The catch surfaces late, that with nowhere to run and no breath left, the effortless game may finally cost him.","sonnet":"A seducer who thrives precisely when nothing is at stake targets someone trapped in lies and solitude, making manipulation look effortless — 'I make it look easy when there's nothing on the line' — until the admission slips that it has never been easy at all.","src":"haiku"},
"georgia-carroll~there-goes-that-song-again":{"opus":"A tender wartime-era ballad about a melody that revives a lost romance every time it plays. The singer recalls falling in love to a shared serenade, only to be ambushed years later by its return. Carroll, the Kay Kyser band vocalist, voices the ache of nostalgia and the lie of getting over someone.","src":"opus"},
"get-scared~don-t-you-dare-forget-the-sun":{"haiku":"Don't lose yourself in despair; remember that light exists even when you can't see it.","opus":"A plea to someone contemplating suicide, seeing them reduced to a body and a product and begging them to hold on. Beneath the theatrical post-hardcore is a genuine reaching toward a person in the dark.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses a girl who objectifies herself, self-harms, and contemplates death — urging her to breathe, to remember the sun, to resist the darkness her own mind has built around her.","src":"haiku"},
"getter-feat-nothing-nowhere~all-is-lost":{"opus":"A recurring nightmare of drowning and screaming unheard, mirroring a relationship dissolving in faded nights and wasted time. The speaker keeps circling the same dread: that it always ends this way, and this time he won't come through it.","src":"opus"},
"getter~all-is-lost":{"haiku":"Abandonment and repetition cycle endlessly as the speaker watches themselves fade into inability and despair. They question whether they can survive the return of their absent lover.","opus":"An on-and-off relationship replayed through insomnia and wasted nights, the narrator haunted by a face he can't shake and certain he won't survive the same ending yet again.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a pattern of seeing someone who only appears on their own terms — every other weekend — finds the memories and the face impossible to escape, sinking into a despair that ends with \"I don't think I'll make it.\"","src":"haiku"},
"gg-magree~be-careful-what-you-say":{"haiku":"Words weaponized and power reclaimed transform the speaker into a force of consequence as they channel suffering into incantatory authority and dangerous presence.","opus":"A survivor turning past abuse into weaponized strength, warning that the cruelty once used to control her now powers her. Her words carry quiet menace, the victim become the threat, capable of summoning something monstrous.","sonnet":"A survivor who has converted pain into power: once held by chains and someone else's death grip, the speaker now addresses a goddess who can summon the devil with an angel's tongue — warning that her silent echoes will cut you by the neck, so be careful what you say.","src":"haiku"},
"gg-magree~king":{"haiku":"Divine authority and violent domination fuse in a messianic figure; madness becomes absolute power.","opus":"A snarling anthem of reclaimed dominance, flipping submission into a coronation where the singer crowns herself deity and sovereign. Beauty and violence fuse into a demand for worship, madness worn like power. GG Magree weaponizes electronic-metal bombast to stage female self-deification, turning a nightmare into a throne.","sonnet":"A declaration of divine dominance that is also a seduction: beautiful and violent, psycho in the bloodstream and madness as morphine, the speaker positions herself as god — there is a god and she is me — demanding the listener get down on their knees not out of piety but submission to her particular terror.","src":"haiku"},
"gggolddd~notes-on-how-to-trust":{"haiku":"A person struggles to know who to trust after past harm, unable to read boundaries, seeking confidence and space to rebuild and learn.","opus":"Everything in the rearview blurs as the narrator, having glued herself together so no one sees she knows no boundaries, gropes for who to follow and how never to repeat this. She resolves to make room for mistakes, turning confidence into her best revenge. Gggolddd, a Dutch band whose album followed frontwoman Milena Eva's processing of trauma, frame trust as something painstakingly relearned.","sonnet":"After something went wrong — a relationship, a community, a sense of direction — a person tries to map the damage and figure out who is safe to follow. The blurriness is disorienting, the internal compass unreliable. The resolve at the end is hard-won: she needs space, mistakes, and confidence rebuilt from within rather than borrowed from others.","src":"haiku"},
"gh-stkid~f-l":{"opus":"A reckoning with an abusive, manipulative relationship where dependency was weaponized as control. The narrator, once caged and made to feel small, watches the power dynamic invert, casting off a captor now exposed as a coward. Wounded fury hardens into vengeful liberation, the survivor reclaiming agency by refusing to remain anyone's victim.","sonnet":"A song about being manipulated and exploited by someone who made the narrator feel stupid — the dependency became the other person's weapon, and now that the tables have turned the narrator delivers a cold verdict: I owe you nothing, you are nothing without loyalty, and karma will finish what I started.","src":"sonnet"},
"gh-stkid~fsu":{"haiku":"Self-destruction and chaos as rebellion, setting fire to identity and decency to expose hypocrisy and conformity.","opus":"A nihilistic outsider anthem glorifying self-destruction and mayhem, the narrator claiming the unmanageable fucked-up kid identity and Russian-roulette recklessness, hostility toward hypocrites masking a genuine death wish worn as a mask of depression.","sonnet":"A chaotic, violent self-destruction fantasy — the narrator describes Russian roulette, razorblades, setting himself on fire, and slashing others with a switchblade, framing it as an outsider's war against hypocrisy and fakeness. The rage is directed inward and outward simultaneously, with no coherent target beyond destruction itself.","src":"haiku"},
"gh-stkid~hollywood-suicide":{"haiku":"Dissolution under the machinery of fame; suicide as escape from constructed identity.","opus":"A caustic dispatch on the cost of chasing fame, framing the industry as a machine that consumes its hopefuls. It warns that signing away your soul buys no peace, that friends turn on you and highs mask decay. The metalcore intensity dramatizes ambition as slow self-destruction, honesty at war with the game.","sonnet":"The music industry as a machine that demands you kill yourself for the dream — sign the contract, eat shit, sell yourself out while friends stab you in the back — and the speaker returns to a promise made at the start to stay honest, knowing the downfall is coming and that going up in flames is built into the contract itself.","src":"haiku"},
"gh-stkid~ugly":{"haiku":"A person refuses to become what others demand, rejecting the golden cage of false loyalty as people prove themselves fundamentally ugly and unchangeable.","opus":"Refusing to become whatever others demand, the narrator rails against a golden cage guarded by death, humiliating himself to satisfy people he calls ugly while insisting nothing can break or change him. A gun to the head becomes a grim emblem of freedom. Gh-stkid channel this suffocated self-worth into abrasive intensity, framing inferiority and anxiety as a refusal to be remade.","sonnet":"A person who has been told repeatedly that she is not enough — not presentable, not acceptable, not alive in any meaningful sense — refuses to reshape herself to satisfy others while also despairing that refusal means permanent exclusion. The tension between defiance and self-destruction runs throughout, the gun-to-the-head image arriving as a brutal definition of freedom on her own terms.","src":"haiku"},
"gh-stkid~y-u-i":{"haiku":"Through darkness and collapse, a companion remains as tourniquet and guide. Together they will survive and reach the end without failure.","opus":"A vow of devotion to someone trapped in a dark, coma-like descent, possibly overdose, illness or deep depression; the narrator promises to be the tourniquet and guide through the dark, insisting 'it's just you and I' and refusing to let them slip away or say goodbye.","sonnet":"A promise of unconditional support to someone in severe mental or physical crisis — the narrator positions themselves as an anchor for a person who is slipping away, pledging to be there through every darkening stage no matter what.","src":"haiku"},
"ghastly~we-might-fall":{"haiku":"Mutual vulnerability creates provisional intimacy; both parties risk descent together, finding temporary solace in shared uncertainty.","opus":"The fear at the threshold of falling in love, two people circling each other terrified of the drop, asking whether the risk is worth it while quietly hoping the other is just as scared and just as willing.","sonnet":"Two people stand at the threshold of something romantic, both afraid of the fall that comes with emotional vulnerability — the speaker gently presses forward, asking if the other person is willing to risk it together.","src":"haiku"},
"ghost-angel~leaving":{"haiku":"Departure and abandonment both literal and spiritual, the speaker fading from presence.","opus":"A departure from a love that curdled into a haunting, where the narrator resolves to escape while the other sleeps. Burned houses, ashes, and demons that survive waking become the vocabulary of a relationship that scarred more than it healed. It captures the guilt and relief of finally walking away from someone still mourned.","sonnet":"A departure deferred until the other person falls asleep — flying too close to the sun, burned alive, the house reduced to ash the speaker is still sleeping in — what prevents clean exit is not love but trauma lodged in the body: every word said was stabbed back into the heart, and the promise to leave tonight is the only agency left.","src":"haiku"},
"ghost-atlas~in-the-house-of-leaves":{"opus":"A blurry spiral of drinking, self-doubt and wasted youth, questioning whether the pretense can hold much longer and what's still left to forfeit. Chasing thrills and fear to stretch the moment out endlessly, the narrator senses there's no way back now, and is urged to follow his own path anyway.","src":"opus"},
"ghost-atlas~legs":{"haiku":"Seduction and recklessness in physical intimacy where boundaries dissolve and danger feels pleasurable, isolating the speaker in a dangerous emotional space.","opus":"A hazy, self-endangering hookup where danger and restraint blur, the other person walking on razors and blocking their own exit from within. The tenderness is laced with unease, intimacy shading into something neither party fully controls.","sonnet":"About a magnetic, self-destructive person who keeps coming back — she slips out of her clothes in a car to Deftones, talks to strangers, walks on razors, calls to danger, and the narrator is helplessly drawn in even knowing she blocks out the world from the inside.","src":"haiku"},
"ghost-atlas~lesser-gods":{"haiku":"Hollow spectacle and manufactured meaning collapse under scrutiny; the speaker chases sensation and fear, spiritually inert, addicted to the very desire that drains purpose away.","opus":"The solo project of Erra's Jesse Cash examines the counterfeit divinity people build for themselves, sculpting makeshift gods from ego and desire. Chasing adrenaline over love, the narrator confesses a spiritual inertia that mistakes fear for transcendence. It interrogates the seductive lies that pass for meaning and the applause of an empty room.","sonnet":"A speaker driving toward the setting sun watches ghosts and lesser gods pass as progenitors of future wounds, blinded by convenient truths and a desire to get higher through fear and dying, even at the cost of love.","src":"haiku"},
"ghost-atlas~seeker-stretch-the-night":{"opus":"A restless travelogue of a soul chasing aliveness from city to city, wanting to die in one place and live in another. The seeker learns the love he was hunting outside was in him all along, Ghost Atlas threading insomnia, road-trip recklessness and a bittersweet jab at a departed partner into a plea to grip tight and release in the same breath.","src":"opus"},
"ghost-atlas~void-voyeur":{"opus":"A dissociative freefall through detachment, where the narrator watches life from outside, flirting with the unknown and resisting a gravity that keeps dragging back to earth. Cravings for meaning war with a distrust of easy answers. Ghost Atlas renders numbness and rebirth as a push-pull between the machine's logic and a whispered undoing of silence.","src":"opus"},
"ghost-iris~made-to-rust":{"opus":"A metalcore broadside against apathy and ecological ruin, insisting nothing built carelessly can endure. Bodies and minds alike decay, while a blind humanity devours a finite planet. It calls out the hollow, passive complicity that lets destruction proceed unchallenged, demanding the weak be defended and every wrong finally made right.","sonnet":"An environmental protest framed through the lens of human mortality — the narrator observes that bodies and minds are built to decay, and extends this metaphor to the destruction of the planet by those who refuse to acknowledge their own complicity in collapse.","src":"sonnet"},
"ghost-iris~the-devil-s-plaything":{"haiku":"Idle time and apathy have worn a person down, leaving them reduced to waste and indifferent to everything, including their own destruction.","opus":"Empty hours become an invitation to ruin, and the narrator, spent and stripped of his crown, feels himself worn to nothing by human callousness until he's grown just as cold in return. Denmark's Ghost Iris pour that exhaustion into progressive metalcore, decay setting in after endless torment, the swelling revulsion boiling over into a flat vow to stop caring about anything at all.","sonnet":"Chronic idleness and systemic indifference have atrophied the narrator into near-nothingness. What began as vulnerability hardens into total rejection — of the system, of mankind, of caring itself. Apathy breeds apathy until the only honest response is blunt refusal to feel anything further.","src":"haiku"},
"ghost-iris~the-rat-the-snake":{"opus":"Ghost Iris build an allegory of greed and self-interest, personifying two parasitic creatures who drain everything around them, heedless of the ruin trailing behind. The chorus insists on a kind of cosmic bookkeeping, a world where actions loop back, condemning the insatiable to die off and dissolve into nothing having repaid no debt.","sonnet":"A parable about symbiotic exploiters — the rat and the snake, devouring and transmitting disease through the world — who are blind to the karmic circuit they inhabit and will eventually face judgment for the trail of destruction they leave without thought.","src":"sonnet"},
"ghostkid~murder-feat-code-pandorum":{"opus":"A song where devotion and violence become indistinguishable. The speaker can't tell affection from hatred and treats the object of desire as a target to be possessed completely, imagining destroying them as the way to feel whole and to keep them permanently. It's a study of obsession curdled into a controlling, deadly fixation.","src":"opus"},
"ghost~dance-macabre":{"haiku":"Seduction through darkness and ritual, a final bewitching before inevitable parting in an ancient dance of love and death.","opus":"Ghost dresses mortality up as romance, a lover racing the clock toward a final goodbye and wanting nothing more than one last night of enchantment before the end, dance-of-death imagery turned into pop seduction.","sonnet":"A speaker at a romantic or supernatural crossroads senses this encounter is ending, casting it in the language of bewitchment and midnight ritual — desperate to hold onto the enchantment one final time before it dissolves, the song frames desire as an ancient rite whose time is almost up.","src":"haiku"},
"ghost~in-my-mind":{"opus":"A rare bright-pop turn from the Swedish theatrical rockers, an obsessive infatuation counted off in Spanish. The narrator is consumed by someone constantly on his mind yet certain he falls short, watching others make her smile as he never could. Beneath the sunny bounce lies the quiet ache of feeling never enough.","src":"opus"},
"ghost~kiss-the-go-goat":{"haiku":"Temptation and transgression blur boundaries as forbidden desire overrides logic and safety, drawn toward dangerous seduction despite knowing the cost.","opus":"A slyly comic seduction pitching Satan as the ultimate sugar daddy to a girl failed by every earthly father, wrapping devil-worship in the language of a doomed romance.","sonnet":"A seduction narrative in which a damaged, daddy-abandoned woman is invited to kiss the goat — Satan, Lucifer, osculum obscenum — because he, unlike the Lancelots and false dreams she chased, actually wants her back; the song frames diabolism as the only honest relationship on offer.","src":"haiku"},
"ghost~see-the-light":{"haiku":"Demonic influence grows stronger through hatred and malice; consuming poison leads to spiritual illumination and transcendence.","opus":"Sung from the mouth of a tempter or the Devil himself, feeding on the hatred aimed at him and growing stronger for it, offering damnation as illumination in Ghost's signature inversion of communion and worship.","sonnet":"A dark, parasitic force addresses its host directly, revealing that hatred and fear have only made it grow stronger, and inviting the person to consume it — drink it, eat it — as the only path to illumination, framing surrender to darkness as the perverse route to seeing the light.","src":"haiku"},
"ghost~witch-image":{"haiku":"A supernatural presence haunts the subject's unconscious life, forcing reckoning with unrepentant sins and the inescapability of consequences.","opus":"Death personified as a patient stalker on a pale horse, reminding a comfortable sinner that a bargain once struck cannot be unsold and no earthly pleasure outruns the reckoning, faith and damnation in Ghost's usual theatrical dance.","sonnet":"Death or a death-figure speaks directly to a mortal who has spent a life in shallow pleasures and moral transgression, warning that consequences are inescapable — what has been done cannot be undone and what has been sold cannot be unsold — while riding close behind on a pale horse, a guardian and judge at once.","src":"haiku"},
"ghstpunk~my-ass":{"haiku":"Captivity and sexual violation trap the speaker under restraint while they beg for freedom and mercy from their captor.","opus":"A comic seduction gone violently wrong: the fantasy of falling into a lover's trap becomes a literal ambush where she ties him up and he begs for release. The absurd panic and the joke about her having done this to a hundred soldiers play sexual power as slapstick horror.","sonnet":"A comic song about being completely dominated and humiliated by a sexually aggressive partner — she pins him down, ties him up, and performs an unwanted act while he demands to know what she wants from him; the tone is slapstick outrage rather than trauma.","src":"haiku"},
"giant-giant-sand~love-comes-over-you":{"opus":"Howe Gelb's desert-rock ensemble offers a weary prayer for a dream, a home, and healing that lasts. Standing still and severed, the narrator waits for love to arrive as a force that washes over rather than something chased. It carries the parched hope of the Southwest, patient and worn, trusting deliverance will simply come.","src":"opus"},
"gideon~2-close":{"haiku":"A speaker exhausted from relentless pressure approaches breakdown and isolation; they actively distance others through hate and pain to prevent closeness. The song portrays self-imposed exile under duress.","opus":"A hardcore confrontation with exhaustion and strain, the singer likening himself to a trapped animal pacing its enclosure while a devout mother frets from back home. He turns his own suffering and resentment into a tool to hold everyone at arm's length.","sonnet":"About emotional self-protection through aggression and distance — the narrator is exhausted and pushed to the edge by relentless pressure, and pushes people away with hostility precisely because they're close to breaking.","src":"haiku"},
"gideon~take-me":{"haiku":"Someone surrenders completely to another person's control, willing to endure suffering and sacrifice everything for connection, even unto death.","opus":"A metalcore surrender to a love that harms, where the pain is welcomed because numbness has left the narrator craving any sensation of the other person. He offers total self-erasure as the toll for a promised paradise, making devotion and self-destruction indistinguishable.","sonnet":"A song about total self-surrender to something overwhelming — whether a person, a substance, or a destructive force — framed as willing submission even through numbness and pain, craving the feeling of being consumed.","src":"haiku"},
"gideon~take-off":{"opus":"A defiant break for freedom from a life of barely getting by, the narrator done talking and ready to floor it. Ripping off the rearview stands for refusing the past and anyone's permission. Gideon frames escape as revenge less against a person than against limitation itself, a long-dormant hunger finally roaring awake.","src":"opus"},
"gift-giver~damaged-goods":{"opus":"A raw confession of emotional armor, the narrator warning a would-be lover away because betrayal has hollowed them out. Numbness has become identity, pain the only sensation left, and old wounds curdle into a will to hurt others first. The nu-metal snarl dramatizes how being gutted turns a person into the serpent they feared.","src":"opus"},
"giles-corey~blackest-bile":{"haiku":"Depression oozes through consciousness, infecting all relationships and rendering the narrator toxic to those nearby through contagious despair.","opus":"Depression rendered as a physical fog only the sufferer can see, a man convinced he poisons everyone he loves and is fated to solitude, apologizing to a partner for a ghostliness he can neither share nor cure.","sonnet":"A person who carries something dark and invisible — a suffocating, self-described wreath of bile and doom — turns it inward and outward, believing themselves born for isolation and incapable of offering anything another person would want.","src":"haiku"},
"giles-corey~no-one-is-ever-going-to-want-me":{"haiku":"Self-destructive hopelessness pervades every waking moment as the narrator claws through existence convinced of their own monstrosity, wanting only the peace of sleep.","opus":"A portrait of self-loathing and ruin drawn through the ghost of the Salem-accused, where existence itself feels like reverse birth and the only relief imagined is the oblivion of sleep or death.","sonnet":"A voice of pure self-destruction — \"I ruin everything I get my bony hands on\" — crosses the Bridge of Sighs toward a crucifixion in reverse, birth undone, things worsening without limit, until the only desire left is to feel like one feels when asleep.","src":"haiku"},
"ging-nang-boyz~skool-pill":{"haiku":"A student moves through school days isolated by secret knowledge of a girl's inner turmoil, chosen as sole witness to her dual existence. The mundane surface—stealing books, confessions, time's flow—masks the special burden of being needed.","opus":"An alienated schoolboy marks the week by small events (borrowing Dazai, a classmate's confession, losing his identity, watching a girl dance) and clings to being singular: only he feeds the pretty tropical fish, only he knows the sad girl's hidden face—so why, he wonders, was only he chosen for that clear, aching sky.","sonnet":"A school-age observer catalogues the week — borrowing Dazai on Monday, watching a confession on Wednesday, losing their sense of self on Friday, watching someone dance on Sunday — claiming a private intimacy with the classroom's sad girl that no one else shares, and ending on a startling question: why was it only him that the transparent, exhilarating sky chose?","src":"haiku"},
"ginger-taylor~yuppie":{"haiku":"Aloof superiority and emotional distancing; the singer is too ambitious and socially conscious to commit, viewing potential partners as beneath their aspirations and status.","opus":"A sharp, cold-blooded kiss-off from a woman who uses a lover for attention while keeping him at arm's length, class and status wielded as a weapon, letting him take her out but never letting him in.","sonnet":"A song about a class-conscious woman who uses a man for a night out but will not let him into her social world or her home — she is from another place entirely, and the gap in status is the reason the relationship can go nowhere.","src":"haiku"},
"girl-in-red~apartment-402":{"haiku":"Solitary confinement in private space becomes refuge from overwhelming stimuli; isolation paradoxically enables survival while threatening dissolution.","opus":"A raw account of being at the edge, cracked and exhausted in a home that feels like a trap, half-hoping for a way out and quietly confessing she could die alone and unnoticed. A genuine reckoning with suicidal despair.","sonnet":"A person in severe psychological distress sits in an apartment that feels more like a trap than a home — cycling through overwhelm, dissociation, and the frightening thought that they could disappear here and no one would notice.","src":"haiku"},
"girl-in-red~bad-idea":{"haiku":"Compulsive desire overrides judgment as the speaker surrenders to a relationship she knows will destroy her; erotic intensity trumps self-preservation.","opus":"The addictive pull of a person you know is wrong for you, calling them back knowing it will leave you more lost, caught between craving and the clear-eyed knowledge that you should be alone instead.","sonnet":"A speaker keeps returning to someone she knows is wrong for her — calling them up, bringing them home — even as she acknowledges each step as a mistake, trapped in a cycle of physical desire that overrides her better judgment.","src":"haiku"},
"girl-in-red~i-ll-call-you-mine":{"haiku":"Vulnerability and devotion collapse into surrender; the speaker breaks down and claims someone as theirs, needing proof they will stay through the worst.","opus":"Reckless young infatuation lived in a speeding backseat, admitting a past of getting around while begging this one to stay through the worst days. It is about clinging to a lover as an anchor against going insane.","sonnet":"Backseat freedom and the terror that it cannot last: the speaker races through a daydream with someone she loves, knows bad luck is coming, and clings to the relationship as an anchor against going insane — offering everything in exchange for the promise that this person will stay on her worst days.","src":"haiku"},
"girl-in-red~i-need-to-be-alone":{"haiku":"Isolation becomes a necessity for mental survival as social demands and routines corrode consciousness; solitude is the only remedy for psychic dissolution.","opus":"The static of a stalled young life, wasting days on nothing and losing the thread of every conversation, where solitude isn't sulking but the only thing keeping the narrator from coming apart entirely.","sonnet":"A person overwhelmed by monotony and social exhaustion reaches a breaking point, recognising that the only way to stop losing their grip on themselves is to withdraw entirely from others.","src":"haiku"},
"girl-in-red~midnight-love":{"haiku":"Self-respect demands refusal of subsidiary affection; the speaker rejects the consolation prize role, insisting on equality or solitude.","opus":"Refusing the role of someone's late-night fallback, the person called only when there's no one else and treated as second-best. She hopes to reach the point of saying she's not a consolation prize, even as she keeps giving in.","sonnet":"A speaker refuses to be someone's emergency fallback — \"I can't be your midnight love when your silver is my gold\" — knowing they are the last resort called when no one else is available, and waiting for the day they can look this person in the eye and say \"I'm not your consolation prize.\"","src":"haiku"},
"girl-in-red~rue":{"haiku":"Grateful guilt; knowing you hurt someone devoted to you and can't make it right.","opus":"Written for Euphoria's Rue, gratitude to the person who stayed through the worst of the narrator's spiraling, alongside the hard truth that they can't be saved by anyone else. It's the exhausted honesty of a mind you can't trust.","sonnet":"A speaker who has put someone through great difficulty — long nights, emotional crises — acknowledges the debt while admitting she cannot trust her own mind or carry the weight of her damage alone; she tries, again, to make it work.","src":"haiku"},
"girugamesh~chimera":{"haiku":"Internal conflict spirals toward inevitable decay as the narrator questions meaning within a system designed for exploitation and hopelessness.","opus":"A condemnation of herd conformity, the lions who follow right-face rules and mock the outcast until self is sealed off and killed. Dragged into an exitless dark with no dreams, the answer is to protect the one thing worth protecting and walk forward, spreading soiled, ugly wings even as you fall.","sonnet":"Trapped in a herd-conformist world that kills off any deviation, the speaker drifts in a lightless maze where emotions have rotted and dreams are impossible, until a decision to draw one giant circle and walk forward breaks the paralysis.","src":"haiku"},
"gizelle-smith~working-woman":{"opus":"A funk-soul tribute to female self-sufficiency, celebrating a woman who needs no one's help, advice, or approval. She turns words into action, wastes no time, and carries herself entirely on her own terms. Smith channels vintage soul stomp into an anthem of independence, the refrain insisting again and again that she can do it alone.","src":"opus"},
"glass-animals~black-mambo":{"haiku":"Seduction and manipulation by an ancient, predatory force; the narrator describes surrender to a creature that holds and shakes victims into compliance.","opus":"A hypnotic, menacing scene of temptation, some sly figure coaxing you into surrender with the patience of a con artist, the animal imagery masking what plays like seduction into addiction or a slow, willing loss of control.","sonnet":"A cast of languid, dangerous animal-figures — a scheming sloth, snake-eyed predators, dozing leopards — tempt a mark with gold and pleasure, promising thrills while waiting to bring them down in a slow, controlled seduction of power.","src":"haiku"},
"glass-animals~exxus":{"haiku":"Predatory horror and nightmare violence infuse the lyrics; the narrator depicts a supernatural entity that hungers and kills with grotesque, surreal brutality.","opus":"A lurid, nightmarish stream of grotesque violence and dream-logic mutilation, more sustained unease than narrative, the recurring image of someone fleeing and vanishing lending it the feel of a fever dream you can't wake from.","sonnet":"A nightmarish encounter with a predatory, grotesque figure — the speaker watches with cold menace, threatening violence with surreal imagery of dismemberment and suffocation, while the target keeps trying to flee and disappearing from sight.","src":"haiku"},
"glass-animals~your-love-d-j-vu":{"opus":"A hypnotic account of a magnetic, damaging attraction the narrator keeps returning to against better judgment. The lover is intoxicating and performative, a hit-and-run touch that vanishes when done. Glass Animals wrap the compulsion in dreamy haze, capturing the déjà vu of a cycle that consumes even as it thrills, wanting to be devoured.","src":"opus"},
"glass-beach~bedroom-community":{"haiku":"Adolescent isolation breeds exploitative vulnerability; the teenager performing identity becomes prey for predatory figures who monetise her suffering as authenticity.","opus":"A savage allegory about a depressed teenage girl and the industry that fattens on her pain, the Devil at her shoulder and heartless men monetizing her suffering, indicting how emo culture packages young despair as product while the girl stays trapped.","sonnet":"A teenage girl with depression is failed at every level — by a father who pathologises her emotions, by an industry that monetises her suffering, by a suburban community that cannot see past surface — and ends up trapped in her bedroom, her pain transformed into content for others.","src":"haiku"},
"glass-beach~classic-j-dies-and-goes-to-hell-part-1":{"opus":"A sprawling art-pop reckoning with sleeplessness, guilt, and the ache of assembling a life that never feels settled. The narrator packages his pain as an identity, holds onto passing companions, and pines backward for hazy evenings and holiday string lights. Under the maximalist tumult runs a raw plea not to confront the void by himself.","src":"opus"},
"glass-beach~yoshi-s-island":{"haiku":"Transition and social rejection isolate a young person; the speaker questions gender, conformity, and belonging while drowning in pressure and unable to find home.","opus":"A fiercely gentle address to a trans woman, naming the imported hormones, the dread of her own reflection, and a society that might destroy her for not conforming. It confronts someone who still uses the wrong pronouns for her when she's absent, while the singer chooses to affirm and love her without reservation.","sonnet":"A song about supporting a trans friend through transition and a hostile family: the speaker watches someone navigate estrogen from Vanuatu, being misgendered by a mother who says \"son,\" and the danger of existing without room to breathe — and offers the only counter: staying close, loving like a lover, pretending the world doesn't exist.","src":"haiku"},
"glass-bridges~echo":{"haiku":"Awakening to inner strength emerges through fear transcendence, hearing ancestral guidance that demands courage. The speaker moves from paralysis toward choosing autonomy and gratitude.","opus":"A self-actualization anthem about taking back ownership of your own identity, replying to an inner call and deciding to rise above fear. The echo turns out to be your own inner counsel, with thankfulness for the difficult road that brought you here.","sonnet":"A speaker answers an inner or divine call after years of searching, choosing to let go of fear and step forward — \"I hear your voice, hear your echo\" — grateful for the path that led here, moving from despair toward a grounded, prepared openness.","src":"haiku"},
"glass-bridges~intention":{"haiku":"Self-sabotage and internal conflict paralyze forward motion as the speaker battles addiction, shame, and the gap between their intentions and actions, unable to escape patterns of self-betrayal.","opus":"The exhausting gap between resolve and action, contradicting every intention and unable to escape an addicted, self-sabotaging state. Nostalgia drains the energy and the singer keeps holding themselves back, sick of letting themselves down but unable to build the discipline to change.","sonnet":"A self-reckoning about chronic self-sabotage: the speaker knows what discipline and truth require yet keeps betraying those intentions, clinging to nostalgia and bad habits while trying, with each deep breath and step back, to steady themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"glass-hands~how-do-you-feel":{"opus":"A bruised post-breakup interrogation, the narrator feigning indifference while obsessing over how the other feels. Addiction imagery and weekend sickness underscore a self too damaged for happiness. It voices the contradiction of insisting you never think about someone whose absence hollows you, letting silence deliver the goodbye neither will say.","src":"opus"},
"glass-tides~empty-thoughts":{"opus":"The bitter aftermath of being left for someone else, the narrator regretting opening up, refusing to be fixed or missed by an ex who already moved on, and spiraling into confusion, self-blame, and disintegrating self-respect.","sonnet":"Post-breakup devastation at someone who cheated or left for another person — the narrator regrets giving part of themselves away, is now emotionally hollow, and rejects any attempt at the other person reconnecting because the damage is already done.","src":"sonnet"},
"glass-tides~paranoia":{"haiku":"Obsessive thoughts and mistrust calcify into spiraling mental imprisonment; the speaker is trapped by words echoing in the mind, unable to determine reality from distortion.","opus":"A jittery spiral into distrust, where someone's words take up rent-free residence in the narrator's head and warp into suspicion. Innocence looks like performance, memory loops, and control slips away. The track stages anxiety as a feedback loop, admitting it cannot even name what it fears while remaining bound by it.","sonnet":"Obsessive mental replay of someone's contradictory words and actions, diagnosed as paranoia but experienced as involuntary possession — \"in my head, in my head\" becomes a refrain of helplessness. By the end the narrator is tied down, hands behind their back, still compulsively demanding to hear the voice again.","src":"haiku"},
"glass-tides~promises":{"haiku":"Promises as currency; words wasted on the self-doubt that corrodes from within.","opus":"A heady rush of intoxicating attraction shadowed by doubt, where sweet vows and highs keep the narrator wild. Yet the promises feel squandered, wasted on a self that cannot quite believe them. The song lives in the gap between seductive assurance and the suspicion that such devotion is being poured into someone undeserving.","sonnet":"Promises as a substance that intoxicates and ultimately wastes — you got me so high I can't feel the fire, you keep telling me you'll never leave as long as I don't break these — the repetition of wasted on myself turning the romantic pledge into an accusation of self-deception.","src":"haiku"},
"glass-tides~waste":{"opus":"A blunt breakup dismissal branding a partner a squandering of his hours and steeped in deceit; against her plea to stay near, the narrator refuses, tells her to sort out her own troubles, and strides off to claim what belongs to him.","sonnet":"A breakup song stripped to contempt — the narrator tells someone directly that they are a waste of time, their lies are exhausted and transparent, and the request for closeness is met with a flat refusal. The emotional register is not grief but cold finality.","src":"sonnet"},
"glassjaw~siberian-kiss-2009-remaster":{"haiku":"A jealous lover hoards memories of their beloved in photographs, unable to let them be truly independent; they compare their one-sided attachment to watching a silent film where characters refuse to connect.","opus":"Glassjaw, foundational to post-hardcore's raw emotional extremity, channel Daryl Palumbo's wounded delivery into a study of jealousy rotting into menace. The narrator obsesses over photographs that miniaturize his lover, watches her betray him, and curdles into the possessive vow that if he can't keep her, no one else can, love collapsing into voyeurism and self-ruin.","sonnet":"A man consumed by jealousy over an ex watches her move toward someone new, clinging to photographs and memories like a film stuck on a single frame. Possessiveness shades into obsession — he keeps her for himself in images, refusing to release her even as she disappears. The final image collapses love and violence into each other with cold precision.","src":"haiku"},
"glasswaves~dead-dreams":{"opus":"A metalcore battle with disillusionment, the narrator trapped in circles, sailing toward a sun that blinds, discovering the self they sought is a stranger. Color lies just out of reach, unreachable without drowning. It dramatizes the fear that ambition has died and that an unrelenting mind will strip away memory, identity, and hope.","src":"opus"},
"glenn-miller~pennsylvania-6-5000":{"opus":"A spiffed-up suitor hits the town and invites a girl to ring him up, swearing to keep the party going till dawn. A flirty, jukebox-era come-on urging her to scrawl the number down and never let it slip her mind.","src":"opus"},
"glowbug~live-from-hell-feat-eddie-hermida":{"haiku":"Hell's depths yield no power; the speaker dances with demons and claims kinship with the damned, rejecting separation.","opus":"A cheerfully macabre invitation shouted down into hell, welcoming demons as kindred and finding the devil not so tough. Framed by the reminder that every second of life is spent as it arrives, it's a defiant embrace of mortality and darkness.","sonnet":"An invitation sent from hell itself to all the heathens and demons — the narrator has already danced with the devil and found him unimpressive — framing life as a brief window between birth and death spent trying to close the distance between people.","src":"haiku"},
"glowbug~perfectly-irregular":{"opus":"A buoyant declaration of self-acceptance that reframes every crooked, awkward trait as a mark of worth. Spelling and correctness fall away before the insistence that uniqueness is royalty. Glowbug turns quirks and contradictions into cause for celebration, choosing to love oneself precisely for being imperfect and irregular.","src":"opus"},
"glu~cold-sweat":{"opus":"A slow-creeping dread rendered as a cold sweat that clings before you notice it, a symbol of anxiety and a world coming apart. Yet it refuses defeat, urging focus and the destruction of an old reputation to start anew. The track balances panic with resolve, insisting nothing comes from nothing but a beginning must be made.","src":"opus"},
"gnarlah~blind":{"haiku":"Loss of perception and control spiral as manipulation blinds the speaker to reality and traps them in a cage.","opus":"An abusive hold rendered as being whirled about with a blade, sight torn away, entombed alive to endure the torment while the abuser craves everything yet has lost their grip. Unseeing and looping endlessly, the narrator reaches toward a different signal and the light.","sonnet":"About being so thoroughly controlled and destroyed by someone that you can no longer perceive your own situation clearly — eyes cut out, buried in agony, the narrator is going blind from the inside, stuck in a rewind loop, knowing they'll never win but unable to break free.","src":"haiku"},
"gnarlah~fix-me":{"haiku":"A plea for repair and belonging addressed to some unnamed system or person, framing personal breakdown as a malfunction that needs rebooting and reset.","opus":"A plea for repair framed in cold machine imagery, the narrator asking to be rebooted, broken down, and made to belong within a system that has failed them. Wanting both fixing and destruction, it voices a self so malfunctioning it can no longer tell the two apart, begging another for the chance to work again.","sonnet":"A plea addressed to a system rather than a person: the speaker has nowhere to go, needs circuits rebooted, needs to belong somewhere — the repeated why won't you fix me fuses mechanical and human desperation, as if the self is broken software waiting for a patch that never loads.","src":"haiku"},
"goblin-cock~something-haunted":{"opus":"A scathing takedown of a deluded, warmongering leader who dooms his children's future while pretending to care, ruling a flat-earth fantasy from atop a mountain of bullshit.","sonnet":"A contemptuous attack on warmongering leaders and their self-serving ideologies: the song condemns those who claim to care for their children while sacrificing them to lies, picking their teeth with the bones of the dead they created.","src":"sonnet"},
"god-is-an-astronaut~a-deafening-distance":{"haiku":"Emotional distance and separation tear apart connection, leaving both parties isolated and unable to bridge the gap.","opus":"Abandonment at the worst possible moment, a lover who poured out everything and then disappeared, leaving the narrator gasping and on his knees. The exhaustion of begging them to return collides with the truth that they were never content, love festering into a sickness he can't shake.","sonnet":"A person suffocating in a relationship where their partner abandoned them when they needed them most — the speaker begs for them to come back while also recognizing they're trapped in the toxicity of that dependency, unable to escape or breathe.","src":"haiku"},
"god-is-an-astronaut~calistoga":{"haiku":"Lost and alone beneath the stars, a traveler seeks a guiding light to find their way home. The song emphasizes isolation and the persistence of light as the only path to salvation.","opus":"The largely instrumental Irish post-rock band lay rare vocals over an image of being adrift at sea under uncaring stars, homesick and facing shared fears in solitude. A recurring beacon becomes the song's emotional center, a promise to guide the lost back home, turning cosmic emptiness into a slim hope against total isolation.","src":"haiku"},
"god-is-an-astronaut~light-years-from-home":{"haiku":"A song about escape from loneliness and depression through transcendent dreams and ascension, seeking refuge from the despair of an isolated present.","opus":"A rare vocal passage from a mostly instrumental band, giving voice to a mind convinced its world has already ended and a yearning to slip away from an empty home toward warmth, dreams and something transcendent. God Is an Astronaut are an Irish post-rock group who build enormous wordless crescendos, and these few lines act as a quiet anchor beneath the sweeping cosmic guitar washes carrying the feeling.","sonnet":"A mind lying in deafening stillness, unable to move, watching a dead tree sway outside while longing to ascend into warmth and light. The song is a waking dream of escape from grief — not dramatic, just the quiet, repeated wish to fade away from wherever you are and surface somewhere golden.","src":"haiku"},
"god-is-an-astronaut~reverse-world":{"haiku":"The speaker insists solitude is illusory; connection and presence persist despite isolation.","opus":"Sparse words insist the truth holds more than one dreamed if only one could see its meaning, resolving into the repeated reassurance that you are not alone. God Is an Astronaut, an Irish post-rock band built on instrumental crescendos, use vocals here as texture, letting the barest phrases float over swelling atmosphere so the comfort lands through sound as much as language.","sonnet":"A spare, almost vocal-fragment piece that insists on presence and solidarity — the truth holds more than first appears, and no one faces it alone. The repetition amplifies rather than explains. At its minimal core, it is a reassurance directed outward: whatever is hidden is real, and isolation is an illusion.","src":"haiku"},
"god-is-an-astronaut~signal-rays":{"haiku":"A solitary speaker in summer sunlight wishes everyone they know were alive and present with them, the only sentiment repeated across the song.","opus":"God Is An Astronaut, the Irish post-rock trio whose largely wordless catalog turns loss into swelling atmosphere, pare this piece down to one aching wish. Beneath a brilliant summer sky the narrator longs for departed loved ones to stand with him again, breathing, while he waits by himself, unmoored in time. The bare repetition distills grief to its plainest, most unresolved core.","sonnet":"A solitary figure in summer sun wishes for the absent — all those who should be present, alive and alongside. The longing repeats without resolution: bright sky above, deep isolation beneath, time slipping untethered. The song holds the ache of being the only one left in a moment that should have been shared.","src":"haiku"},
"godsmack~greed":{"haiku":"Suffocation by control and avarice leaves the speaker unable to articulate their state until escape provides clarity. Greed destroys the object of its possession while claiming love.","opus":"Recoiling from a two-faced, smothering person whose fake kindness masks pure greed, the narrator heeding an inner warning to cut them loose before it turns deadly.","sonnet":"The speaker breaks free from someone who was smothering and controlling them — described as crawling under the skin, two-faced, and greedy — with an angel's whisper confirming that walking away was the only way to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"godsmack~i-stand-alone":{"haiku":"Radical autonomy requires severing all dependencies; the speaker burns internal fire alone, refusing compromise or external validation.","opus":"A blast of hard-rock defiance in which the singer refuses to be controlled by anyone or their false objects of worship. He claims the right to chart his course and to imagine a future on his own terms, unafraid even as his old certainties dissolve around him.","sonnet":"A declaration of self-reliance against anyone trying to control or pull the speaker down: the so-called goddess behind which others hide offers no real power, and the speaker chooses to dream of skies beyond the poison inside, insisting on standing alone rather than dying for beliefs that are fading.","src":"haiku"},
"godsmack~immune":{"haiku":"A system enforces conformity and punishes those who resist, erasing identity. The speaker becomes immune to the psychological demands placed on them.","opus":"A confrontation with a parasitic, mind-warping presence that sucks the life out from under the narrator, who declares himself immune to what the other is while questioning whether the person even remembers their own identity. A framing spoken intro casts it as a place of exile until death. Godsmack are a Boston hard-rock band, and this blend of paranoid menace and pounding, groove-heavy riffs fits their post-grunge, alt-metal aggression.","sonnet":"Resistance to a system that strips away identity and demands compliance — the opening voice of authority promising that breaking the rules means finding out what comes after death, and then the speaker pushing back, declaring themselves immune to what the other person represents. The self is something worth defending even when it is twisted.","src":"haiku"},
"godsmack~keep-away":{"haiku":"One person transmits sickness and craving to another, stabbing and dragging through waste; the speaker demands distance and warns that misunderstanding will destroy them.","opus":"The Boston metal band builds a brutal boundary anthem against a toxic, draining person. Sickened by what he sees in this other, the narrator recounts being hurt and drained, no longer recognizing them, and demands over and over that they keep their distance and stop mistaking who he is.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship expelled with force — the other person's sickness and manipulation have drained everything. The narrator has stopped listening, stopped caring who the other person is, and issues a final directive to stay away, repeating it until it becomes the only truth left standing.","src":"haiku"},
"godsmack~moon-baby":{"haiku":"The narrator expresses obsessive attraction mixed with pain, using space and celestial imagery while cycling through regret and compulsive emotional repetition.","opus":"Godsmack open with spoken lore about the moon warping people, then fuse that lunar fixation with a devouring obsession, the speaker insisting his one true match is embodied in her while pleasure and pain fold together into a daily ache he can't articulate. The nu-metal grind hardens into denial and guilt, pleading for this recurring error to finally end.","sonnet":"An obsessive, all-consuming attachment — framed through lunar imagery — sits alongside a confession that the pain it causes is inseparable from the pleasure. The speaker knows the cycle is self-destructive and circular, knows he keeps making the same mistake, but the craving overrides both reason and regret.","src":"haiku"},
"godsmack~re-align":{"haiku":"Internal conflict and isolation force reckoning; the speaker must shed delusion and realign with authentic self to survive.","opus":"Recovery framed as deliberate isolation, retreating from illusion and self-made confusion to the place of an old death in order to face the demons and reset, growth chosen over comfort.","sonnet":"A person who made desperate choices, fell into isolation and delusion, and nearly destroyed themselves by returning to a place of old pain recognises what went wrong and seeks to realign — to find the self again after the demons of self-deception almost won.","src":"haiku"},
"godsmack~serenity":{"haiku":"Fractured thoughts and desperate searching offer no resolution as the speaker seeks a refuge that cannot be found. Tragedy and inner fragmentation persist while external forces remain immovable.","opus":"A meditative reach for inner peace and a hiding place from a life torn away by tragedy, questioning why we dream at all while craving stillness.","sonnet":"A craving for retreat from a life beset by tragic visions and stolen time — the speaker seeks a hidden place of serenity, holding an inner child through the unanswerable questions of where meaning goes and how the flame gets relit when everything goes cold.","src":"haiku"},
"godsmack~time-bomb":{"haiku":"Someone exists in constant internal suffering, pierced by accusation, trapped in filth, questioning faith; he carries destructive pressure waiting to detonate.","opus":"Godsmack, the Boston hard-rock band, use churning Alice in Chains-style heaviness to inhabit a degrading, toxic relationship and deep self-hatred. Trapped in torment that makes him doubt he's even living, the narrator brands himself worthless and volatile while rejecting a partner's faith, the ticking refrain turning private rage into a threat about to go off.","sonnet":"Someone living in a sustained hell of resentment and rejection, feeling invisible to the person they are addressed to and unable to break free from the cycle. The time bomb image is self-description not threat — a person who knows they are going to detonate, who has been surviving on a combination of spite and exhaustion.","src":"haiku"},
"godsmack~whatever":{"haiku":"Someone rejects an unwanted person, demands they leave repeatedly, and insists they're doing fine without needing that person in their life.","opus":"A contemptuous brush-off of someone the singer treats as beneath him, insisting he's doing his best while demanding they simply disappear. The bravado barely masks the wounded defensiveness underneath the sneer.","sonnet":"Pure hostility toward someone toxic in the narrator's life — they want this person gone, assert they're doing fine without them, and refuse to give them any more power, repeating the demand to go away like a mantra.","src":"haiku"},
"godsmack~whatever-album-version-explicit":{"haiku":"Contemptuous dismissal masks emotional detachment as the narrator performs indifference while perfecting the art of elegant rejection.","opus":"A contemptuous kiss-off to someone the singer keeps as a pet, alternating between spite and the boast that he's doing the best he ever has. The repeated command to go away is aimed as much at himself, shaking off a toxic person while insisting he's fine.","sonnet":"A blunt dismissal of someone the speaker can't stand — pathetic, unwanted, told to go away repeatedly — while insisting they are doing the best they can regardless of that person's presence.","src":"haiku"},
"godzend~deconstructive":{"haiku":"Systemic decay demands active deconstruction of fear and complicity, the narrator claiming agency through refusal to accept predetermined victimhood.","opus":"A cry against a crushing system amid people screaming and suffering, half in Spanish begging God for breath. It insists the listener has always been protected and never forsaken, that the daylight the system tries to erase is the person themselves.","sonnet":"A bilingual (English/Spanish) cry against a broken system that blinds and crushes people, answered by the assurance that god has not forsaken the speaker and that the daylight — the divine source — is the only thing keeping the people alive.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~amazonia":{"haiku":"The Amazon rainforest is being destroyed for profit, representing the burning of Earth's greatest miracle.","opus":"An ecological cry over the destruction of the Amazon rainforest, mourning an irreplaceable wonder consumed by flame. The metal band ties the devastation, driven by mining and greed, to both humanity's grief and a self-inflicted doom, and urges people to rise up in protest before the loss becomes permanent.","sonnet":"An elegy and indictment of the destruction of the Amazon — the greatest living miracle on earth being burned for gold mines, the forest's murder framed as both a spiritual catastrophe and a predictable outcome of human greed.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~born-in-winter":{"haiku":"A person is born or awakened into hardship and cold, learning through pain and suffering that they possess inner strength and resilience to endure.","opus":"A contemplative, almost cosmic reflection on birth and endurance, reminding the listener that every human being originates in stellar catastrophe. It urges a newly awakened spirit to sense the surging vitality within and, however harsh and freezing the surrounding world, to summon the resolve to endure and hold its ground.","sonnet":"An encouragement to embrace the hardship of existence — born into cold and darkness like everything in the universe born from stellar violence, one can still find the strength to stand upright and face the world with open eyes.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~l-enfant-sauvage":{"haiku":"Institutional violence and conformity kill the wild child within until righteous anger erupts as the only honest response. Liberation demands rage against systems demanding surrender and compliance.","opus":"Breaking free of the institutions and inherited fear that cage a person, reclaiming the raw anger and wild self as the path back to owning one's own life.","sonnet":"A wild child rejects institutional life and reclaims their own existence after years of suppressing rage and pain — the righteous anger won't last forever but it is real, and the sky above is all, the speaker running on their own time.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~liquid-fire":{"haiku":"Primal conviction and unbending integrity flow through those who reject false authority and build meaning through internal compass.","opus":"A declaration of fierce self-reliance and inner fire, standing alone, bowing to no flag or authority, and trusting only the wisdom of the heart. The band frames existence itself as a defiant march to remember why life is worth anything at all.","sonnet":"A statement of artistic and existential sovereignty — born unrestrained, bowing to no flag, carved by waves, guided only by intelligence of the heart — with liquid fire running through the veins as the only language needed to speak to the world.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~low-lands":{"haiku":"The dead and suffering become invisible, expanded into light, drifting through earthly lowlands until the sun rises and brings new days of liberation and peace.","opus":"An oceanic meditation on death as release and transformation, a giant monster put out of misery and freed into light. Gojira frame dying as the trapped voices underground finally rising into the sky.","sonnet":"An elegy addressed to someone who has died — perhaps a parent or bandmate — assuring them that in death they are freed from the world's suffering, dissolved into light and sky, while the living release their grief upward.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~magma":{"haiku":"Poison spreads through mind and body as someone seeks absolution by consuming themselves, ultimately finding escape or transcendence through burning away.","opus":"Named for the album mourning the Duplantier brothers' mother, this reads as ego-death and transcendence, a poison or grief spreading through the body until you drop everything and fly. Consumed and burned clean, the release toward light doubles as a metaphor for death and letting go.","sonnet":"A meditation on transformation through substance or transcendence — a toxin or force slowly moving through the body and mind, burning everything away until the person lifts off into a state that feels like death and flight at once.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~mouth-of-kala":{"haiku":"Contemplation of time, impermanence, and transformation—the narrator dissolves and fades away as everything burns and changes endlessly.","opus":"Gojira's ecological metaphysics rendered through Hindu destruction imagery, worshipping impermanence itself. The old self dissolves in an unstoppable transfiguration, framing personal annihilation as the necessary cost of genuine change.","sonnet":"Kala is a Sanskrit name for time and death — the song surrenders to impermanence, celebrating the destruction of the old self as a necessary transformation; burning and changing are not tragedies but the fundamental laws of existence.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~only-pain":{"haiku":"A creature sacrificed in captivity loses its power and dies; all human striving for godhood and power leads only to suffering.","opus":"Gojira dismantles the ego's drive to become godlike, showing how the pursuit of self-elevation leads only to coldness, loss and futility. The sacrificial imagery turns ambition inward, a creature once fierce now tamed and going down.","sonnet":"A song about striving toward transcendence — trying to become something more, even godlike — only to find that all the effort yields only pain and vanity. The narrator, trapped and tamed, has lost touch with what he loved, and the song watches a fierce creature slowly dying in its cage.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~our-time-is-now":{"haiku":"Collective awakening and disobedience emerge against systemic deception; humanity must seize agency now or surrender forever.","opus":"The French metal titans deliver a rallying cry against fear and conformity, urging listeners to shed the weight dragging them down and seize their moment. It rejects deceived complacency and blind mentors, insisting on belonging and collective awakening. Uncharacteristically anthemic, it channels Gojira's force into an exhortation to conquer now or never.","sonnet":"A call to seize agency against a world that has deceived and blinded the collective — \"we've lost our sight to the bottom of the sea\" — by refusing the weight pulling them down and changing the rules rather than obeying a mentor who has failed them: the thunder that made them is now a source of power, not submission.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~pain-is-a-master":{"haiku":"Pain teaches and forges the speaker, destroying their old self and forcing them to recognize suffering as a master that shapes their renewal.","opus":"A visceral rebirth through suffering, crawling through blades of grass, cracking open, unable to recall the faces of allies, until pain itself is embraced as a forging master that renews and awakens. Gojira are a French progressive death-metal band renowned for ecological themes and immense, undulating riffs, and this framing of agony as a teacher rather than an enemy fits their spiritual, almost meditative approach to heaviness.","sonnet":"Pain as the only teacher left — a person destroyed by something unnamed, reduced to a body that is a battlefield, barely able to recall who stood beside them. The pivot arrives when the speaker recognises that face-down and inert, something has been forged; the final line is a startling act of submission to pain as a necessary master.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~pray":{"haiku":"Those without faith in the existing world forge their own path to survival and renewal through prayer and wind—creating personal meaning independent of institutions.","opus":"Gojira reject inherited religion in favor of self-forged purpose, refusing belief in an external order and choosing to build their own meaning to flourish. Prayer is reframed not as submission but as an act of will, actively summoning light, daylight and change through one's own force.","sonnet":"A stripped-down declaration of self-reliance: rejecting the world's established faith and choosing to forge one's own meaning and survival, while invoking light and wind as forces worth summoning.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~silvera":{"haiku":"The song calls for people to stop blaming fate and wake to human genocide, suggesting that personal transformation changes the world. Humans are portrayed as killers raining shame while dragging bodies and committing devil's work.","opus":"Gojira argue that transforming yourself is the mechanism for transforming the world. Amid harrowing imagery of mass killing and human degradation, the narrator urges the listener to quit complaining about destiny, confront atrocity directly, and grasp that inner change is the root of all change.","sonnet":"A song about the violence humanity does to itself and the world, set against a call to individual transformation — the narrator insists that self-change is the precondition for changing anything outside, even as bodies fall from the sky in a rain of collective shame.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~stranded":{"haiku":"Isolation and internal sickness; feeling locked out and abandoned while another person's indifference slowly kills.","opus":"A sickness in the heart and a mind crushed by inner noise, with the speaker feeling abandoned, stranded in the cold and dark, and pleading not to be shut out by someone whose rejection feels like death.","sonnet":"A person trapped in emotional and psychological darkness pleads not to be shut out by someone close to them, feeling that being locked away would be fatal — each passing day in isolation leaves another part of them wasted.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~the-art-of-dying":{"haiku":"Mechanical existence strips meaning from living; the speaker practices release to shed material attachment before death.","opus":"Gojira's ecological-spiritual bent turned inward, a numbed modern mind sleepless and stressed learning to value life precisely by facing death, practicing to let everything go and travel into the afterlife carrying nothing.","sonnet":"A speaker numbed by screens, stress, and a hollowed-out existence gradually awakens to the preciousness of life and death, practising the art of releasing all attachments so that when the end comes he can travel light and without regret.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~the-cell":{"haiku":"The narrator is trapped and overwhelmed by mental chaos and evil crowding his mind; he struggles with loss of control and pressure that builds until he must let it go.","opus":"France's Gojira turn their eco-minded intensity inward, casting the mind as a cramped prison stuffed with sanctified evil. The narrator, torn between craving knowledge and craving oblivion, pleads to be freed from his own darkness, and the closing shift from I to we hints that release comes only through letting go.","sonnet":"A mind trapped inside itself — overcrowded with noise, locked in darkness, craving escape from its own interior. The cell is the skull; the prisoner is consciousness itself, dying to learn and dying to forget simultaneously, searching for a way out of the prison no external key can open.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~the-fall":{"haiku":"Cycles of destruction and transformation follow human arrogance; the earth regenerates while people fall victim to their own blindness and excess.","opus":"The French metal band turns an ecological warning into cosmic meditation, casting death and decay as part of a vast cycle that clears the way for renewal. Higher forces look on and urge humanity to turn suffering into strength, while cautioning against arrogance and against cutting ourselves off from the living planet that sustains us.","sonnet":"An ecological and spiritual collapse allegory — the earth cycles through death and renewal while human greed, competition, and self-deception accelerate the destruction; the song watches civilizations fall and asks whether something greater might rise from what's left.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~the-gift-of-guilt":{"haiku":"Shame and inherited sin poison each generation, trapping people in cycles of self-blame; the only escape is forgiveness and releasing guilt.","opus":"Gojira reframe guilt as an inheritance that devastates but must finally be set down. The self-condemnation passed from the past becomes a maze of lies, and the song's release is forgiving yourself and leaving the shame behind.","sonnet":"About the crushing, inherited weight of guilt and self-condemnation — guilt is described as a vulture from the past that devours you, and the song argues that the only way forward is to stop feeding it, to forgive yourself and others and break the cycle of shame.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~the-shooting-star":{"haiku":"A person journeys toward transformation through harsh terrain and survival, leaving their past behind. Lasting love requires learning to let go.","opus":"A cosmic call to set out, chase a falling light toward an icy northern wilderness and outlast a hostile world that's watching. Layered with imagery of rebirth and grasping truth, its ultimate lesson is that enduring love simultaneously grows and fades, so you must both treasure what you hold and learn the harder art of releasing it.","sonnet":"A journey of escape and survival — someone must leave their old life and travel north into frozen wilderness, avoiding danger, finding a shooting star to follow; the emotional underpinning is letting go of the past and allowing love that cannot survive to finally die.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~wolf-down-the-earth":{"haiku":"Ravenous conquest consumes creation itself; civilization devours the planet while its people remain complicit.","opus":"A furious indictment of human greed devouring the planet, imagining conquest taken to its logical end where we swallow forests, rivers and moon alike, and asking whether we'll face the emptiness driving it before doom answers for us.","sonnet":"Humanity is depicted as a voracious, self-consuming force that devours the natural world, its own creations, and finally the source of life itself — taught to conquer and rule, it has become the very monster that will destroy what made it.","src":"haiku"},
"gojira~yama-s-messengers":{"haiku":"Karmic reckoning arrives as violent retribution for countless harms; the narrator confronts their cosmic debt.","opus":"Death's messengers from Buddhist cosmology come to tally a killer's deeds, the narrator a soul who spent lifetimes slaying and now faces retribution, taking back inherited pain and begging the faultless creator for mercy at the end.","sonnet":"A soul facing the messengers of death and karmic judgment is overwhelmed with fear and regret, confronted by visions of a life spent causing harm, and desperately pleads with the creator for mercy in its final moments.","src":"haiku"},
"gold-souls~eyes-on-fire":{"haiku":"Cold revenge fantasies unfold as the speaker channels contained fury into dominance, refusing to offer comfort to enemies while methodically positioning themselves for retaliation and control.","opus":"A cold declaration of power over an adversary, promising no comfort and no gain, seeing through their stolen power and biding time to play the winning card. The confrontation is one of controlled dominance, emerging steadily with grace to fell any foe.","sonnet":"A speaker refusing to soothe an adversary's pain turns the tables slowly and deliberately — shuffling the cards, playing their ace at exactly the right moment — and emerges with eyes blazing, felling every foe with a gaze.","src":"haiku"},
"goldfrapp~caravan-girl":{"haiku":"Mysterious wanderer ignites escape fantasy and unnamed recognition; identity dissolves into motion and the possibility of flight.","opus":"A sun-warmed hymn to escape and desire, fixating on a free-spirited stranger and the fantasy of running off together, the pull of leaving everything behind rendered as pure euphoric momentum.","sonnet":"A fleeting, sun-dazed encounter with a mysterious woman on a hill generates an impulse to run away together and bring everything back to some essential state — barefoot, unnamed, free — before doubt or inertia holds the speaker in place. The fantasy of escape is real even if the woman barely is.","src":"haiku"},
"goldfrapp~deep-honey":{"haiku":"Deception masks desire; the speaker observes summer performance and distrusts authenticity.","opus":"A sultry, fragmentary portrait of a beautiful liar whose deceptions come wrapped in summer glamour and cocaine, the narrator drawn to someone who can't stand the dark yet trades in it.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone whose deceptions always begin in the same way — with brightness and summer — while concealing an aversion to darkness that the speaker has seen through. The lyrics are spare and elliptical, withholding more than they say.","src":"haiku"},
"goldfrapp~ride-a-white-horse-single-version":{"haiku":"Escapist fantasy offers temporary reprieve from earthly decay; pursuit of transcendent connection through shared transgressive pleasure.","opus":"Disco escapism with a knowing edge, chasing a night of transcendence on the dance floor while the world outside gets cheaper and cheaper, the white horse a wink at both cocaine and the fleeting rush of the club.","sonnet":"An escapist fantasy of pure sensation and movement: the speaker wants to dance, ride, and be transported somewhere beyond the cheapening world, following a leader into a whirlpool of feeling that is real and tangible, leather and bliss — pleasure as its own sufficient destination.","src":"haiku"},
"goldfrapp~strict-machine-we-are-glitter-mix":{"haiku":"Electric attraction and submission to a precise partner create transcendent connection through control and pleasure.","opus":"Pleasure as electrocution, framing desire and control as a feedback loop with a machine that rewards submission, the ecstasy of being wired into something that tells you exactly what to want.","sonnet":"The speaker is erotically and emotionally captivated by something mechanical and commanding — a strict machine that sends pulses, demands submission, and rewards with electric sensation. Love and control are indistinguishable, and the submission is entirely willing.","src":"haiku"},
"goo-munday~yes":{"haiku":"Trapped in self-imposed confinement, the speaker awaits transformation while riding spirit toward undefined liberation.","opus":"A restless push toward self-liberation, the narrator boxed inside himself and desperate to break out of a stifling cocoon. Positivity is offered as the way out of an endless waiting room, the affirmative refrain becoming a decision to move rather than sit on the shelf.","sonnet":"A person stuck in a cocoon of self-imposed isolation — shelved and waiting — wonders when the breakthrough will come, invoking a spirit guide and the waiting room of their own making as obstacles between the current inertia and the flight that the positivity 'will by far set you free' promises.","src":"haiku"},
"good-boy-daisy~greenroom":{"haiku":"Hatred corrodes intimacy; the speaker despises their partner's manipulation, escape preferable to another cycle of gaslighting and conflict.","opus":"The exhausted end of a gaslighting relationship, hating a partner who pushes false narratives and burns the speaker's time while they drive recklessly away. The looped I hate you is the sound of finally refusing to keep waving the flag.","sonnet":"Rage at a partner who gaslights and lies — the narrator is exhausted from fighting all night, hates the other person's manipulation, and has reached the point where giving up feels like giving in but staying is unbearable.","src":"haiku"},
"good-terms~cough":{"haiku":"Substance use masks depression and unemployment, where memory and nostalgia amplify loss and inability to escape paralysis.","opus":"A raw look at marijuana dependency and the numbness it brings, the narrator concealing his use from his mother and doubting he ever pushed himself to break free. Sorting through old childhood belongings sets off grief for the person he used to be, aching with stalled growth and a wish to start over.","sonnet":"Quitting weed doesn't quiet the self-loathing — \"every single thought I allot to tear myself down\" — and a regression into childhood nostalgia only sharpens the sense of wasted time; the fog of the high was preferable to the clarity underneath.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~animal-mother":{"haiku":"The speaker enters a catastrophic situation expecting heroism but returns broken; their sacrifice and bravery accomplish nothing, leaving only failure and shattered expectations. Achievement is illusion.","opus":"Prog-tinged supergroup Good Tiger build around a bitter reckoning, the speaker giving an interview about some grim spectacle preserved on film, every moment of it damning. He never expected to return diminished and defeated, and grows wary of the widely accepted belief that his fears had substance, landing finally in pure bewilderment, unable to grasp what drew him toward the danger in the first place.","sonnet":"A soldier or war correspondent returns shattered, having run toward violence expecting recognition and finding only a broken identity. The footage of atrocity was captured, the interview granted, but the promised celebration never came — and now the return itself is the most disorienting combat of all.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~blueshift":{"haiku":"Accumulated weight and sorrow demand extremity; the speaker considers silencing himself as the only path through unbearable dread.","opus":"A portrait of the grinding effort of carrying emotional pain in silence. The narrator keeps his words trapped, repeatedly choosing to shoulder the burden rather than speak, pinned down by a weight that always seems to ask for something more before it will let go.","sonnet":"A person at the edge of emotional collapse is held down by someone or something as they face the choice between enduring unbearable weight or silencing themselves entirely, the grinding pressure expressed through a body that echoes hollow and a tongue that must be cut out.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~decades-on-my-feet":{"opus":"A meditation on endurance and hard-won clarity, framing life as a weighted walk through soil that eventually washes the dirt away. The narrator lets thoughts and grief drift while trusting the wind is at his back. Good Tiger renders perseverance as an emptied shell that finally stands clean, the reward for dragging things out as long as possible.","src":"opus"},
"good-tiger~float-on":{"haiku":"Surrender to dissolution in a river that consumes identity and teaches acceptance of the present moment over grasping at the past.","opus":"The British-American prog band renders self-dissolution as an eerie kind of peace, a man giving himself over to a current that consumes him even as it soothes. Letting go of everything he was, he learns to live fully in the present and drift away, at ease in a state most people never even glimpse.","sonnet":"Dissolution into a river as spiritual surrender and transformation — the self consumed by water, fed upon, cleared of every last remnant of prior existence. What remains is a floating peace, an instruction to release raw materials and adore the present moment rather than grasp at what passes.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~gogo-yubari":{"haiku":"Education and shared reading build tolerance and immunity to blind ideology and historical manipulation.","opus":"The transatlantic prog-metal supergroup champions intellectual life, valuing knowledge over comfort and nourishment found in the mind. It challenges deliberate ignorance, cautioning against growing numb to the past or forfeiting one's future, and frames education as escape from a kind of self-imposed forgetting that keeps people docile.","sonnet":"A critique of wilful ignorance: someone chooses to remain uninformed, treating the absence of knowledge as a personal style. The narrator counters by celebrating the appetite for learning over hunger, urging a tolerance for history rather than surrendering future chapters to an unexamined gospel.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~grip-shoes":{"haiku":"Unfulfilled potential breeds shame and abandonment; the speaker witnesses promising youth falter and fall, leaving only the hollow recognition of wasted promise and unchosen descent.","opus":"A friend insists he is chasing his dreams while actually sleepwalking his life away, and the singer watches that wasted potential drown. It is about the quiet grief of seeing someone give up while telling everyone he hasn't.","sonnet":"An address to someone — possibly a bandmate or friend — whose potential is slipping away through self-deception and inertia: he tells himself he's \"living out his dreams\" when he's just sleeping, and the speaker watches helplessly, unable to look at the fall but knowing the years are being wasted.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~grow-smile-accept":{"opus":"A meditation on ego death, humility and acceptance, using the image of becoming a worm in rich soil under a collapsing sun; after long waiting, the narrator opens up and accepts that trying to change what you are is not a fantasy but a process of surrender and renewal.","sonnet":"A song about surrender to nature's cycle and the dissolution of ego — the narrator moves from claiming to be everything to becoming a worm feeding the soil, finding meaning in decomposition and enrichment rather than in human ambition. The call to 'smile, accept' is not comfort but instruction: stop fantasizing about transformation and accept what you are.","src":"sonnet"},
"good-tiger~if-you-weren-t-my-son-i-d-hug-you":{"opus":"About drinking through grief and finally letting go of a lost person or past self, searching for home and self-worth while accepting that the shared smiles are gone, and trusting they'll meet again somewhere down the line.","sonnet":"A song about grief-soaked self-reckoning and the impossibility of returning to what was — the narrator has been drinking and sinking for days, squeezing at things like sand, unable to find worth or home. The emotional core is the quiet acceptance that what could have been is gone, and that letting go is the only honest move left.","src":"sonnet"},
"good-tiger~maybe-hope":{"haiku":"Failure and self-medication; the speaker counts losses while drinking through numbness.","opus":"A quiet reckoning with self-sabotage, the narrator tallying his defeats over a drink and apologizing to himself each morning for being his own worst enemy. He tends a barren, hopeless inner life, smothering the beginnings of any future, unable to voice what he needs and afraid it is already lost.","sonnet":"Self-sabotage as gardening: the speaker tends a dreamless garden and drowns roots of a future ahead, the whiskey-soaked morning apologies to himself naming him as his own worst enemy — unable to express what he needs, suspecting it has already gone.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~redshift":{"haiku":"The song reflects on mortality and cherished memory. The narrator fears forgetting loved ones and wants their final moments to recall important faces and shared experiences.","opus":"A tender meditation on dying and what survives it, the singer hoping that at his end he'll still hold onto the people who mattered most and the time they shared, and urging that only love is worth keeping. The astronomical title casts the whole thing as a gradual drifting apart. Good Tiger are a melodic progressive band whose clean, expressive delivery suits this gentle reckoning with endings.","sonnet":"A person confronting their own mortality and choosing gratitude over regret — hoping at the last breath to remember faces and the things shared with them. The shift implied in the title is present throughout: relationships and time are moving apart like light stretching across distance, and the only response is to make sure love was not wasted.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~snake-oil":{"haiku":"A trapped consciousness begs for release from creative imprisonment, painting escape fantasies while imagination dries up and reality suffocates the mind.","opus":"Good Tiger turn creative pressure into a claustrophobic parable: a narrator confined by a decree that his mind must keep producing, unable to breathe or exit, begging for whatever upside imprisonment offers while attempting imagined escapes in an invented existence. Each mental flight fails as inspiration runs out, and his plea to be freed from this fabricated life goes unheard.","sonnet":"A creative mind imprisoned — literally or psychically — paints wings and jumps from an imagined window only to find imagination has run dry. The enclosure shrinks the possible; waiting, pacing, and wasting converge until the plea is simply to be broken out of a life that has become fictitious.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~square-breath":{"opus":"A study of anxiety managed by discipline, the narrator counting through box breaths to steady his voice and hold panic off. Keyed-up but not quite afraid, he leans into the chaos rather than run, Good Tiger dramatizing the white-knuckle labor of staying calm while clinging to a ledge and being remade through control.","src":"opus"},
"good-tiger~such-a-kind-stranger":{"haiku":"Internal fire and determination fuel survival; the speaker refuses to starve spiritually or be broken despite hardship and exhaustion.","opus":"Good Tiger weave anxious progressive rock around the effort to release tension lodged in the body and the difficulty of accepting what seems futile. Torn between the wish to shut down and awareness of what he'd forfeit, the narrator finds that a churning inner fire, refusing to be starved, doubles as both dread and the fuel that keeps him going.","sonnet":"Exhaustion held together by metabolic fury — lungs burning, veins churning — the narrator refuses to starve even when swallowing hope feels physically impossible. Survival is framed as biological defiance: the body will not allow surrender even when the mind finds the world too much to digest.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~sunthrower-flower":{"haiku":"Patient growth in loyalty and love; the speaker honors roots and refuses to corrupt truth as the show ends.","opus":"Good Tiger build an extended metaphor for patient, unconditional devotion, likening loyalty to tending something planted and waiting for it to grow, a bond that demands nothing and refuses to twist the truth. As a closing image signals an ending, the pledge takes on a quietly wistful, almost farewell tone.","sonnet":"A meditation on patient loyalty — watching something grow from planted seeds, trusting the slow process. Relationships here are held without leeches or deception, fingers locked in mutual support. Near the end a curtain descends; the show closes on a moment of presence neither party will experience again.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~whatever-happened-to-man-s-best-friend":{"haiku":"The speaker has lost precious things to theft and feels compelled to escape, warning others to abandon this place and join them in withdrawal.","opus":"A grim escape story, with a narrator tethered to the world only by his diary and staring into the sun, weighing the noose after someone robbed him of what mattered. He plots to burrow beneath the city and torch a structure from afar, spurning empty condolences, and the prog-inflected hook, urging others to leave too, hardens grief into a calculated exit.","sonnet":"Betrayal and dispossession drive someone to the edge — whatever was precious has been stolen, and the gallows look reasonable. The response is not confrontation but escape: tunnel through, burn the building from a distance, walk away and advise everyone else to do the same.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~when-death-first-appeared":{"opus":"A confrontation with mortality and the deaths of past selves, the narrator waiting at the margins, his soul picked clean, scanning for any hint of light. Letting go proves the hardest lesson as time keeps sweeping on, and the only truth left is the craft he bled into, everything else fiction, the question being where death finally leads.","src":"opus"},
"good-tiger~where-are-the-birds":{"haiku":"Obsessive desire and self-imposed guilt over past moral wrongs. The speaker would sacrifice anything for another but recognizes blood on their hands and mistakes they cannot erase.","opus":"Good Tiger render a devotion so total it borders on self-destruction and guilt. Intoxicated by the beloved and willing to go to any length for them, the narrator also carries proof of his own wrongdoing that can't be erased. The song's plea is to cling together and live recklessly in the moment, insisting he's nothing without this person.","sonnet":"Devotion and guilt coexist: a figure would cross any distance for someone beloved but is also aware of harm done, hands stained by mistakes that cannot be undone. The clinging need is real but so is the recognition that holding on may itself be a form of wrongdoing — love inseparable from damage.","src":"haiku"},
"good-tiger~young-speak":{"haiku":"A person nostalgic for youth struggles to reclaim its language and ease as present circumstances—heat, awareness—make forgetting and returning impossible.","opus":"A wistful ache to return to the simplicity and speech of childhood, now nearly out of reach. The narrator feels an oppressive warmth wearing him down and struggles to shape words or recall what he once understood, longing for a cooler, shaded place where things made sense.","sonnet":"A desire to return to the ease and language of youth: exhausted and melted by heat, the speaker has forgotten what they once knew and longs to go back to the shaded place where the simpler vocabulary of childhood still made sense.","src":"haiku"},
"goodbye-horses~q-lazzarus":{"haiku":"Transcendent flight escapes temporal inevitability as the narrator contradicts pessimism through soaring defiance, refusal becoming resurrection.","opus":"A dialogue between a world-weary man who insists everything fades into the dark and a narrator who flatly refuses to believe it. The image of flying over the horses becomes a transcendence of that cynicism, rising above the earthbound resignation being preached.","sonnet":"Against a fatalistic older voice insisting all things pass into the night, the speaker pushes back in defiance and then transcends entirely — \"goodbye horses, I'm flying over you\" — soaring above the cycle of hope and collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"gordon-lightfoot~old-dan-s-records":{"haiku":"Vinyl records animate memory and legacy; the living dance to old melodies, honoring the dead through rhythm and continuity.","opus":"A warm folk toast to a departed man kept present through his 78s, the dancing a way of honoring him and imagining his pride, memory sustained by the music he loved rather than mourning.","sonnet":"A nostalgic celebration of dancing to an old man's record collection — gathering with loved ones to jive and foxtrot to 1930s seventy-eights — honouring Dan's memory and the communal joy his music still conjures long after he is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"gore~doomsday":{"haiku":"Systemic violence and complicity blend into moral paralysis as the speaker witnesses atrocity while remaining trapped by structures larger than individual conscience.","opus":"A helpless witness to police killing neighborhood kids and a suffering mother, wondering what good faith is in a world sliding toward catastrophe. With Armageddon arriving sooner each year, the singer surrenders belief to doomsday itself, needing someone to walk with him to the end.","sonnet":"A speaker watching police violence outside their window and asking whether belief means anything in a world where doomsday arrives earlier every year: personal guilt — shutting the door so blood won't stain their clothes — is set against a mother's tears and the question of whether Armageddon offers forgiveness for the greedy and cruel.","src":"haiku"},
"gore~pray":{"haiku":"Intimate violence and despair collapse into prayer; the speaker offers their shattered self to another, seeking salvation or destruction through emotional sacrifice.","opus":"A dark, ritualistic plea for intercession fusing devotion, violence and desire, the narrator begging a lover to stay and pray while carrying the whole weight of things alone, offering flesh and blood as sacrifice. Gore casts intimacy as both redemptive and consuming, a flower that never opens, love laced with hunger and horror.","sonnet":"A supplication to someone who is both protector and predator — the narrator levitates like ink in the sea, asks to be carried on a chariot of fire, and waits for a dark sun to take their tormentor away, all while begging \"could you stay here and pray for me.\" The closing image — the pound of flesh, the gore — reveals the cost of this kind of love.","src":"haiku"},
"gorgon-city~there-for-you":{"opus":"A house-tinged lament of missed timing and regret, the narrator wishing they had loved sooner and been present through years of tears and distance. It reaches toward the hope that separation was necessary for the two to finally find each other. Longing and tenderness braid into a wish to have simply been there all along.","src":"opus"},
"gorillaz~19-2000":{"haiku":"The world's frenetic pace threatens to sweep away the speaker, who struggles to stay grounded while finding solace in rhythm and movement.","opus":"A woozy, ironic pop groove about a world moving too quickly and the pull to ease off the pace. The narrator half-heartedly resists the very entertainment machine the track belongs to, and beneath the bouncy rhythm sits a low hum of worry about being swept up in all the speed and noise.","sonnet":"A loosely self-aware song about the speed of modern life and the desire to stay grounded — the narrator imagines weighing himself down to avoid being swept away, while the lyrics gently mock the instinct to just keep moving and dancing rather than slowing down and making something of your own.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~clint-eastwood":{"haiku":"A mystical presence offers guidance and perception, helping others see beyond the material surface to inner spiritual understanding.","opus":"A mix of weary optimism and mystical bravado, with a hook about feeling glad despite unhappiness and hope that the future is coming on, and a rapped verse casting the speaker as a spiritual guide who perceives destruction and offers survival through the mind.","sonnet":"A rapping ghost or spirit describes itself as an invisible force running through nature and music itself, offering clarity and psychic guidance to those perceptive enough to see past surface reality — projecting confidence in a coming transformation.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two voices split the same skull. The sung hook is a depressive's flat mantra, a man who can only manage a grudging gladness in place of joy and who calls himself worthless while insisting the state won't last, the whole thing bright and hollow the way medication talks. Against that numbness Del arrives as a rapped spirit-guide, a phantom mentor sprung from confinement who now moves outside age and time, entering the listener to scrub the vision clear and hand back the sensations the singer had written off as gone. So the track stages depression against imagination as a possession: the flesh-and-blood frontman is the deadened host, and the disembodied verse is the psychic charge that reanimates him, which is why the final chant hardens from a shared prospect into a first-person claim, the borrowed hope at last owned. Fittingly for Gorillaz's founding conceit, the meaning lives in the seam between a cartoon body and the ghost renting it.","fv":2,"fnote":"The title has nothing to do with the lyric — Damon Albarn named the track for its melodica line’s resemblance to the spaghetti-western themes of Eastwood’s films, and it shipped as the cartoon band’s first-ever single.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","faith & the occult"],"means":"a disembodied rapped verse possessing and reanimating its deadened host","built":"the cartoon body and ghost-verse seam literalises Gorillaz's founding split"},
"gorillaz~controllah-feat-mc-bin-laden":{"haiku":"Loss of control manifests across fragmented subjectivities where identity disperses and desire becomes impossible to locate or regulate.","opus":"A Brazilian funk boast about being impossible to rein in, the former outsider once dismissed as unhinged now claiming to be everywhere at once, indulging and dreaming past the system's reach. An English bridge cuts in with imagery of the ocean off Rio and a quieter thought about which truths a person keeps and which they abandon.","sonnet":"MC Bin Laden navigates São Paulo's underground — labelled delinquent since youth, living consequentially in the subconscious future, caught between scene presence and silence — while Gorillaz weave in a refrain about Rio's waves and the truths carried and left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~dare":{"haiku":"An invitation to move physically and emotionally, to participate and feel present in experience rather than remaining a passive observer.","opus":"An insistent, hypnotic dance-floor invitation urging the listener to let go, move, and lose themselves in the moment, reassuring that it never did any harm.","sonnet":"An insistent, hypnotic urge to commit fully to the moment — press into it, move with it, feel it from the inside — built around the single word \"dare\" as both command and release.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~dirty-harry":{"haiku":"The speaker describes survival amidst poverty, violence, and marginalization, needing weapons for protection while questioning whether the endless warfare is worth enduring. The song contrasts the desire for peace with the necessity of combat.","opus":"A soldier in a desert war clings to a gun and just wants to dance, while a hardened rapper's verse conveys the constant hurt, paranoia, and cheapened value of life in combat, feeling like an expendable pawn after being told the war is over.","sonnet":"A soldier returned from war finds himself unable to dance, unable to rest, haunted by gunshots at night and the cheap cost of life — a rap verse weaves together combat guilt, surveillance, and the bitter contrast between political theatrics and the poverty and violence faced by those who serve.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~doyathing-feat-andre-3000-and-james-murphy":{"haiku":"Uncertainty and performative confusion spiral through incomplete thoughts, contradictions, and refusals to commit, mirroring the anxious evasion characteristic of human indecision. Action promises clarity it cannot deliver.","opus":"A ramshackle celebration of authenticity, its cartoon narrator and guest rappers goading everyone to quit posing and just be themselves rather than a knock-off of someone else.","sonnet":"A self-deprecating character sketch of someone drifting, soft, and uncertain collides with a bravado rap section asserting authenticity and calling out impostors — the two voices undercut each other, circling the question of whether anyone actually knows what they're doing.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~el-ma-ana":{"haiku":"Summer's passing marks loss of youth and the fading of a precious connection. The narrator reaches toward a future reconciliation while acknowledging separation and time's grip.","opus":"The song mourns a fading connection with imagery of summer no longer knowing the speaker and love slipping into dying, as he insists he's fine while losing his mind. He clings to the hope that maybe in time the other will want to be his.","sonnet":"The speaker reflects on feeling abandoned and estranged — summer has turned cold, a relationship is slipping away — but holds onto a quiet, recurring hope that the other person will one day want to be with him. The tone is melancholy and patient, not angry.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~feel-good-inc":{"fable":"Happiness as a factory product: a melancholy town that never smiles buys its feel-good from the Inc, while the windmill chorus dreams the alternative — turning forever, hand in hand, love for free — and asks, in both senses, is everybody in?","fableDeep":"Two voices are set in deliberate collision: the depressive murmur of the verses ('a melancholy town where we never smile,' waiting on a message beep for company) and the cackling hype-man rap — the Inc's own sales floor, laughing at the customers it's lining up. 'You're damned and free' is the trap in three words: total liberty of consumption, no exit from the county. Against the corporation the song sets a windmill — soft, pastoral, turning without fuel — and the closing question 'is everybody in?' cuts both ways: a cult recruiter's call and a headcount for the escape. Which meeting you've joined is left to you.","haiku":"Urban decay and societal breakdown masked by consumerism and synthetic happiness, with the wealthy exploiting the poor for profit.","opus":"A contrast between a decaying, joyless place where no one ever grins and a yearning for an idealized haven of freedom built around a turning windmill. A cocky rapped voice interrupts, hyping itself, while the surrounding mood stays numb and detached, an escape fantasy floating above the gloom.","sonnet":"A critique of a hollow, city-based cycle of manufactured pleasure and distraction that keeps people trapped while a separate, windmill-and-open-sky vision of free love and turning together offers an alternative outside the system.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~hollywood-feat-snoop-dogg-jamie-principle":{"haiku":"Hollywood seduces and corrupts, driven by jealousy and ambition, while various speakers navigate fame's allure, exploitation, and the struggle to maintain authenticity and survival in that world.","opus":"Gorillaz, Damon Albarn's animated project, personify the film capital as an alluring drifter whose shine breeds envy and violence. House pioneer Jamie Principle sings her mystique while Snoop Dogg raps his way through danger and survival in a town of ambition. The place promises everything yet coolly threatens to spoil it all, its charm and its threat impossible to separate.","sonnet":"Hollywood is personified as a seductive, vagrant city — gorgeous and treacherous, feeding jealousy and grinding down the real. One narrator surrenders to her pull while another moves through with brash confidence, knowing survival in the industry means staying alert to envy that can kill any vibe it touches.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~humility-feat-george-benson":{"haiku":"A plea from isolation and loneliness, exploring the tension between wanting connection and the fear of being let down, with the speaker questioning whether to repair a broken relationship or walk away alone.","opus":"A deceptively sunny track about reaching out from deep loneliness, the singer confessing he needs someone to pull him out of his isolation and set him right again. It opens Gorillaz's album The Now Now, and George Benson's smooth jazz-guitar riff lends the sadness an easygoing West Coast warmth that hides the ache underneath.","sonnet":"Isolation acknowledged without self-pity — the speaker identifies as the lonely twin, the left hand, someone who has drifted and wants to reset without knowing quite how. The question keeps returning: if you pick up something broken, do you put it back or walk away? The answer is not given, only the difficulty of choosing.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~kids-with-guns":{"haiku":"Youth as weapons; desire and impulse turning young people into instruments of force and consumption.","opus":"Damon Albarn watching desire and violence turn the young into monsters, mesmerized and dangerous before they even grow up. The pacifier imagery makes the menace feel like arrested childhood weaponized.","sonnet":"Children armed with weapons are taking over, shaped by forces that strip them of innocence and turn them into instruments of desire and violence, while a frightened voice pleads not to be killed in the chaos they have been made to embody.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~new-gold-feat-tame-impala-and-bootie-brown":{"haiku":"Superficiality and emptiness corrode culture where vanity, status, and manufactured desire replace genuine connection. Beneath glittering surfaces lies decay, and meaning dissolves into what's disposable and performative.","opus":"A woozy meditation on chasing false riches in a hollow, celebrity-poisoned world where everything is destined to disappear. Bootie Brown's verses catalogue vapid Los Angeles decadence and doom-scrolling anxiety, asking what we're even living for while the dreamlike cove keeps luring us underwater.","sonnet":"A fractured panorama of modern society drifting underwater — Botox grandmas, cult warnings, Twitter addicts, and revolving-door friendships — circled by a dreamy refrain about a \"magic cove\" and \"fool's gold,\" where everything promises meaning but keeps disappearing.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~on-melancholy-hill":{"haiku":"Emotional refuge with a beloved person as medicine against melancholy, seeking escape and companionship to avoid loneliness.","opus":"A gently sad daydream of running off to sea with someone who feels like a cure whenever they're near. Perched somewhere lonely and artificial, the song aches for the day the two of them are together, quietly accepting that even if life won't give you everything you crave, it can still give you the person you want.","sonnet":"From a lonely hilltop where dreams fall short of reality, a narrator offers himself as comfort to someone who cannot get what they want, inviting them to set sail together and find closeness instead.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~revolving-doors":{"haiku":"Urban transience and pharmaceutical escape characterize modern existence; movement masks emptiness while identity remains undefined.","opus":"2-D adrift through interchangeable cities, numbed by pills and vague dread, life reduced to airports and diners and the sense of being spun through the same door endlessly, resigned to a rootless celebrity existence he was somehow born into.","sonnet":"A fragmented, medicated blur of hotel lobbies, TV sets, foggy cities, and pills that pause feeling: the speaker drifts between London and Boston in a daze, the revolving doors of the title capturing the cycle of going nowhere while everything spins, the Beatles playing in a diner while life passes without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~rock-the-house":{"haiku":"Movement and rhythm transcend rational thought as the collective invites surrender to grooves that animate the body and liberate from cerebral constraint.","opus":"An old-school party rap built purely to move a crowd, Del the Funky Homosapien clowning as an animated-yet-decomposing MC, the whole point being infectious dancefloor swagger rather than any message.","sonnet":"A hip-hop celebration of the dance floor as a space where gravity, rhythm, and collective movement override everything else — the MC and DJ are officiants, and the audience is invited to abandon self-consciousness entirely. The energy is exuberant and deliberately absurdist.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~stylo":{"haiku":"A meditation on electric love and connection that flows through the world at night. The song treats love as a powerful, transformative force that carries meaning and possibility despite hardship and depression.","opus":"Using imagery of electricity, overload, and power surging through circuitry, the song equates love with an electric current that flows through everyone and helps lift people out of depression and hard times.","sonnet":"The song frames love as an electric, overloading force that flows through people and sustains them through hard weeks, urging listeners to rise above depression and recession by letting that energy carry them forward.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~the-god-of-lying-feat-idles":{"haiku":"False hope and systematic disillusionment strip away belief, revealing emptiness beneath every promise. The speaker abandons the search for truth and redemption in favor of immediate escape.","opus":"A barrage of unsettling questions about faith, complacency, and the fear of death, delivered with IDLES' Joe Talbot as an untrustworthy prophet. It probes whether anything lies beyond belief and needles the comforts of inherited religion. Grinning toward the exit, it exposes the seductive falsehoods people cling to and dares them to trust only the deceiver.","sonnet":"A figure of authoritarian deceit addresses its subjects — are you pacified, are you armed, do you love your blessed father? — while a second voice escapes the liquor-store mirror begging to be loved, together building a portrait of propaganda and the desperation it produces.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~tormenta-feat-bad-bunny":{"haiku":"Romantic transcendence offers shelter from apocalypse; two souls clasp despite temporal cruelty, choosing presence over the world's ending.","opus":"A bilingual reggaeton-tinged romance set against a coming storm, seizing a fleeting present because tomorrow means departure. Love becomes shelter and contrast to disaster, a bridge to cross before it breaks. Bad Bunny voices tenderness under time's pressure, insisting something this beautiful cannot be lost even as the clock threatens to bite.","sonnet":"Against a world in disaster and the ticking clock of departure, a lover urges the other to seize this moment — 'aprovéchame hoy, que mañana me voy' — because their presence is the cure for fear, the contrast to every surrounding catastrophe, and the bridge worth crossing before it breaks.","src":"haiku"},
"gorillaz~tranz":{"haiku":"Dissociation and loss of identity through digital/pharmaceutical means. The repeated question probes whether we mirror, burn, and endlessly repeat ourselves into effigies.","opus":"A hypnotic set of questions about identity and dissociation, asking whether the listener turns into their own effigy and dances like this forever. It evokes late-night dopamine, caving rooms, and losing oneself in repetition.","sonnet":"The song describes a dissociative, trance-like state—lying in bed at night while dopamine cycles through the brain, returning home to a room that feels like it is caving in, and wondering whether one has become a mere copy or effigy of oneself, endlessly dancing in the same loop.","src":"haiku"},
"gospel~s-r-o":{"haiku":"Patriarchal mythology persists while the thinker falls; support systems crack and the promised safety never arrives.","opus":"A caustic address to a falling figure confronting the collapse of an inherited, sanitized account of his own life. It refuses reassurance, insisting things don't improve, and skewers a coddled man shielded by his still-living father's tidy, privileged narrative. The post-hardcore fury lands on a bleak truth: the demons are actual, the enemies invented.","sonnet":"An old soul in a body of uncertain age waits for someone who has been tumbling backward and downward, offering brutal honesty rather than comfort — 'I wish I could tell you that it gets better, man, but it doesn't' — while locating all enemies as imaginary and all demons as real.","src":"haiku"},
"gost~she-lives-in-red-light":{"haiku":"A woman lives in red light under night's weight, built from lies that consume. The speaker asks if they'll make it out alive as guilt forgets inquiry.","opus":"GOST, the synthwave project soaked in horror-film menace, spin a nocturnal story of guilt and devouring desire, a life built on deceit that swallows the narrator whole. Conscience and hesitation are abandoned as bodies meet in what feels like doomed inevitability. The refrain fixes on a woman of the night with an ominous doubt about her survival, lust and dread fused in neon.","sonnet":"A woman of the night exists entirely in a red-lit world of desire and survival — her life built on lies that consume those around her. The question circling her is whether she will make it out alive; guilt, flesh, and temptation collapse into each other in the dark space she inhabits.","src":"haiku"},
"gost~widow-song":{"opus":"A synthwave-horror dirge of grief and occult mourning, addressed to a love long dead and interred, whose haunting never lifts. Blade-and-flesh ritual imagery entwines with the ruin of every institution. GOST frames endless bleeding as devotion, love vowed forever to the dead amid apocalyptic wreckage.","src":"opus"},
"gotye~hearts-a-mess":{"haiku":"The song depicts emotional unavailability and self-protection after hurt. A defended heart shuts everyone out to avoid pain, but this makes real connection impossible for others.","opus":"A patient, aching attempt to reach someone walled off by fear and distrust, the singer desperate to connect while acknowledging that guarding your heart spares you pain but makes love impossible for the one trying to get through. Gotye, the Belgian-Australian artist best known for Somebody That I Used to Know, delivers this earlier track over dark, sample-built atmospherics, his restrained vocal making the plea for connection feel genuinely fragile.","sonnet":"Trying to reach someone who has shut themselves down after too much loss — they threw away the key to protect themselves, and now nothing gets through, which makes the connection the speaker wants impossible to form. Love is declared unfair not because it hurts but because one person keeps the walls up and the other keeps trying to climb them.","src":"haiku"},
"gotye~somebody-that-i-used-to-know-feat-kimbra":{"haiku":"Erasure completes itself through indifference as the narrator grieves not absence but casual cruelty, the beloved becoming a stranger through deliberate dismantling.","opus":"The lingering ache and resentment of a breakup, where love that once felt total curdles into being treated like a stranger. The sting is less the ending than the cold erasure afterward, being reduced to somebody he used to know.","sonnet":"After a relationship ends, the speaker resents that the other person erased him entirely — having friends collect records, changing numbers — while the counterpoint voice argues the speaker made her feel trapped and blamed for everything; both are left with the hollow status of strangers.","src":"haiku"},
"gp~kill-my-friends":{"opus":"A misanthropic confession of contempt for hollow, shallow friends, fantasizing about killing them all over and over in his head, preferring beautiful violence and silence out of boredom and self-loathing.","sonnet":"The narrator fantasizes obsessively about killing his shallow, hollow social circle — not from genuine malice but from boredom and contempt — preferring to be hated than ignored, and acknowledging that the murder plays out only in his head, on repeat.","src":"sonnet"},
"grabbitz~falling-over-you":{"opus":"A wry account of an obsession that keeps knocking the narrator off his feet every time he steadies himself, an attraction that wrecks his balance. Images of bedding stitched from resentment and a locomotive bound in one direction cast the romance as gorgeous captivity, played for both hurt and gallows humor.","src":"opus"},
"grabbitz~hero":{"haiku":"External forces threaten internal coherence; the speaker resists invasion from overwhelming external pressure while remaining uncertain of their own solidity.","opus":"A reluctant hero facing something overtaking him, refusing to go down as another casualty while his composure slips. The turn toward darkness and standing firm becomes a solitary vow to reclaim what's his, even praying to be carried off if it costs him his life.","sonnet":"Slowly losing composure as unseen forces take over, the speaker blackouts the sky and their soul, strips down to just a heartbeat, and demands one more reason to start — a last-ditch resistance against being consumed before they can take back what is theirs.","src":"haiku"},
"grabbitz~king-ghost-in-a-song":{"haiku":"Isolation and creative invisibility render the narrator spectral; achieving legacy through anonymous presence allows escape from silence and irrelevance.","opus":"An artist wrestling with mortality and the wish to leave a mark, retreating from family and the world into isolation, reckoning that music will outlive him as the only version of himself that reaches anyone, a ghost living in the speakers.","sonnet":"A musician oscillates between two self-images: the king who wants to go out having done everything he could, and the ghost who has retreated so completely into his craft that he exists only as an invisible presence haunting the speakers people listen through.","src":"haiku"},
"grabbitz~let-it-bloom":{"opus":"An anthem of release and self-liberation, coaxing the mind to slow and the heart's grip to loosen so what is needed can finally surface. The narrator wrestles fear and dark thoughts, imagining flight through colors and fire. Grabbitz frames vulnerability, hands raised and voices sung, as the path to letting something better bloom.","src":"opus"},
"grabbitz~rip":{"haiku":"The speaker renounces faith and old values, embracing a nihilistic reinvention where caring is abandoned and personal transformation becomes a form of death.","opus":"A defiant burial of a former self, with the narrator declaring reckoning at hand and cutting loose from friends, faith, and stored-up bitterness to be remade with no authority to answer to. The recurring memorial refrain treats this reinvention as a literal death. Grabbitz pairs melodic hooks with a reckless, live-fast fatalism across his electronic-rock blend.","sonnet":"A deliberate burial of the former self — friends all gone somewhere darker, faith dissolved, no religion left to straighten things out, and a conscious decision to keep going anyway under a new identity. The tone is defiant rather than grieving; shedding the old self reads as liberation, not loss.","src":"haiku"},
"grabbitz~surrender-peace":{"haiku":"Giving up the fight and accepting defeat, peace through surrender.","opus":"A confession of interior emptiness, the narrator naming the hollow where his heart once was and a numbness that only cracks when he's on his knees. Calling himself the greatest of pretenders, he weighs handing his hard-won calm over to a relationship's uncertain promise, sitting in the exhaustion of masking pain and craving release.","sonnet":"A man who has lost his heart to some unnamed force and admits he is the world's greatest pretender, suffocating on his own thoughts, unsure whether anything lasts — and decides the honest move is to surrender peace rather than keep pretending. The hole where the heart used to be is the song's first image and its final truth.","src":"haiku"},
"grabbitz~when-you-me-dance":{"haiku":"Physical connection transcends doubt and provides proof of existence; dancing together creates a temporary realm where reality feels genuine.","opus":"An intoxicating attraction where dancing with someone is the only thing that makes the narrator feel real and his life less of a joke. He holds back a flood of words, addicted to the high of the connection and daring the other to keep up.","sonnet":"A song about how dancing with someone specific is the one thing that makes the speaker feel real and alive — not the dancing itself but what it represents, that this person's presence gives the speaker something to hold onto against pain; the desperation beneath the euphoria is the emotional core.","src":"haiku"},
"gracchus~envidia":{"haiku":"Envious observation transforms into self-destruction as the narrator witnesses superiority that cannot be matched, resentment metastasizing into collapse.","opus":"Envy as the theme made flesh, the singer confronting a cowardly, guilt-ridden accuser who wishes they could be what the singer is. Both are falling apart, one drowning the other's destiny out of jealousy, the title naming the poison at the center.","sonnet":"A speaker confronts someone corroded by envy — staring silently, wishing to be what they can't be, chipping away at the speaker's teeth with deception — diagnosing the envious one as already falling apart, spine bent, vision blurred by their own lies.","src":"haiku"},
"gracchus~epitaph":{"haiku":"Mourning one's own death with honor lost and isolation complete, warning others of the same desolate fate awaiting them.","opus":"A dying man swings between begging to be killed, mourned and loved, insisting he'll face the end unafraid while accusing his mourners of grieving with hollow gestures. Gracchus stage the deathbed as grim paradox, a warning that everyone weeping over him will soon share his fate, a life lived honorably ending in abandonment, its dignity and belonging erased.","sonnet":"A dying or abandoned figure calls out for witness, love, and saving — knowing the grave will be filled with hollow mourning and averted eyes. Honor walked alone ends in silence; the epitaph is that all who remain will eventually stand in the same abandoned place.","src":"haiku"},
"gracchus~everybody-knows":{"opus":"A rendition of Leonard Cohen's bleak litany, cataloguing the rigged games, betrayals, and coming plagues that everyone quietly acknowledges yet no one stops. From loaded dice to faithless lovers to the dying scene, it enumerates a corrupt, doomed world. The refrain turns collective resignation into grim, unblinking testimony.","src":"opus"},
"gracchus~infectious":{"opus":"A combative romance of insult and attraction, the narrator embracing labels like infectious and pretentious as they push to connect with a guarded, mocking other. Owning the role of chaos, disgust, and a hero destined to fail, the song revels in provocation. It frames a volatile pairing as both nightmare and irresistible match.","src":"opus"},
"gracchus~part-i":{"haiku":"Nothing left to lose, the speaker surrenders to being destroyed as the ultimate escape. A gun to the head becomes the final way to end the performative smile.","opus":"A looping descent into hopelessness, where a forced smile cracks and understanding stays out of reach, the narrator swinging between images of death and euphoria before daring another to end him, since he feels he has nothing left. Trembling and dimming imagery deepen the despair. Gracchus build this suicidal defiance through dynamic, heavy post-hardcore swells.","sonnet":"Oscillating between burial and elevation — the mind racing, the ground six feet under, and then somehow six feet above, only for the glimmer to slip back beneath clouds each time. The final image is bleak and direct: told they have nothing to lose, the speaker invites the gun, content to watch whoever holds it pull the trigger.","src":"haiku"},
"gracchus~thoughts-and-prayers":{"haiku":"A false spiritual leader offers empty performance—thoughts and prayers—while exploiting followers' desperation, treating their salvation as a tool for personal power.","opus":"A savaging of empty piety, a manipulator posing as savior who offers hollow thoughts and prayers while his own conscience rots. The hypocrisy of comfort that costs nothing and does nothing.","sonnet":"A skewering of religious charlatanism — a self-appointed spiritual guide who exploits others' suffering and tragedy as his own remedy, offering hollow platitudes while his own mind rots, the phrase \"thoughts and prayers\" stripped of any meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"gracchus~what-s-the-difference":{"opus":"A meditation on legacy, ego, and letting go, questioning whether it matters if your name is spoken after you are gone. It skewers those clinging to their throne, afraid of endings, and notes that history is written by survivors, not those who give in. The track weighs the vanity of permanence against the certainty of an unknown end.","src":"opus"},
"gradience~this-abyss":{"opus":"A bedroom-rap descent into self-loathing, where the narrator claims ownership of his own rot. He casts himself as the source of the darkness that consumes him, half-daring a distant other to abandon him while he wreaks havoc out of spite. Detachment and decay become a chosen stance rather than a wound inflicted from outside.","src":"opus"},
"graham-central-station~hair":{"opus":"A funk sermon against surface-level prejudice, insisting you shouldn't measure a person by their appearance, hairstyle or race, because character runs deeper than looks. Its clinching point is that outward toughness can hide inner pain, a plea for looking past the exterior to what someone actually is.","sonnet":"A funk song rejecting the social judgment of people based on appearance, specifically hair length — the narrator refuses to believe it's fair to read someone's character or worth from something as superficial as how they wear their hair. The politics of Black hair and countercultural identity in the 1970s run just beneath the surface.","src":"sonnet"},
"gramatik~don-t-give-up":{"opus":"A soul-fired protest anthem in Gramatik's genre-blending funk tradition, indicting the powerful who feed the rich and abandon the poor while dressing greed up as religion. Against corruption and despair it insists on endurance, turning collective pressure into resolve, the refrain framing the struggle as shared, a single drop that refuses to stop moving the ocean.","src":"opus"},
"gramatik~requiem-for-peace":{"haiku":"Age and mortality impose finality as the narrator mourns youth's passage and the certainty of decay, memory becoming both anchor and dissolution.","opus":"A solemn requiem meditating on aging, mortality and time the devourer, urging the dying to be at peace and keep the song. The Latin phrases frame a farewell in which the body wastes away but the buried voice lives on.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality and the devouring nature of time — \"tempus edax rerum\" — watching lights fade and lives melt away like candles, the speaker offers a requiem as a final gift, finding some consolation that a song can live on through buried bones.","src":"haiku"},
"gramatik~solidified":{"haiku":"A rapper's conviction and rising trajectory despite doubters; talent and determination prove critics wrong and establish lasting presence.","opus":"Over Gramatik's production, the verse is pure braggadocio, a rapper positioning himself as champion of the fans everyone else ignored, and the man whose life is under scrutiny yet does exactly what he was told he couldn't. He senses his ascent instinctively and refuses to ease up, casting himself as a bandit plundering rival scenes by dissecting their sound, confident there's plenty of him to share around.","sonnet":"A rapper stakes a claim to authenticity and upward momentum — speaking for abandoned fans, proving wrong everyone who said it could not be done. The sense of destiny is personal and earned, a gut-level certainty that only grows as others finally stop dismissing and start listening.","src":"haiku"},
"gramatik~talkbox-intended":{"haiku":"The speaker protests that magic is being stripped away while adopting an irreverent stance and warning listeners against external tampering with their experience.","opus":"Gramatik, the Slovenian producer known for glitch-funk grooves, builds a loose, chant-driven ode to keeping the magic alive against those who would take it away. Repeated invocations of dubstep sounds folded into funk frame the track's genre-blending ethos, scattered offers of help and broken-down terms drifting through. Self-aware about a fucked-up attitude, the piece plays as a playful manifesto for the music itself.","sonnet":"A brief, fragmented defence of funk against commercial dilution — the talkbox vocal asserts that something essential and magical is at risk of being stripped away. The call is to hold onto the low-end soul of the music against whatever forces would neutralise it.","src":"haiku"},
"grand-funk-railroad~we-re-an-american-band":{"opus":"The 1973 self-mythologizing swagger of a hard-touring rock outfit, cataloguing the road life of booze, groupies, poker and torn-up hotels. It turns the grind of forty days on tour into a boast, planting the band's identity in raucous American excess and the promise to help any town party it down.","src":"opus"},
"grand-funk~the-loco-motion":{"opus":"A cover of Goffin and King's early-sixties dance-craze hit, all invitation and easy joy. It coaxes the listener onto the floor to try a simple new step, framing dance as something anyone can master, a cure even for the blues. Pure kinetic uplift built around a train-like chugging rhythm.","src":"opus"},
"grandmas-house~desire":{"opus":"A queer post-punk reckoning with longing lived under a watchful gaze. Attraction crackles with something almost feral, yet the thrill is shadowed by the sense of being observed and judged by others. The tension sits between craving someone openly and the exposure that visibility brings, desire consuming both parties at once.","src":"opus"},
"grandson~autonomous-delivery-robot":{"opus":"A satirical rant against surveillance capitalism and the creeping automation of daily life. Cataloguing sidewalk delivery bots, retinal scanners, militarized tech and the panopticon of targeted ads, grandson channels mounting dread into a mock product-pitch of buzzwords. The comedy masks real alarm at a future where privacy is impossible to outrun.","src":"opus"},
"grandson~brainrot":{"haiku":"Genocide, dopamine addiction, and corporate capture strip humanity of agency. Constant exposure numbs outrage while the powerful win every game.","opus":"A furious catalog of doomscrolling numbness, where atrocity, celebrity spectacle, and consumer dopamine blur into one feed; the phone becomes a mirror indicting the viewer's own paralyzed complicity.","sonnet":"A song about the moral collapse produced by constant digital overexposure — the narrator catalogues atrocities, genocides, police shootings, and political corruption that scroll past on phones manufactured by child labor, arguing that the dopamine loop of media consumption has made everyone simultaneously aware of and complicit in the world's disasters.","src":"haiku"},
"grandson~drones":{"haiku":"Modern warfare renders hope obsolete as automated death falls from above; survival demands deadening oneself to fear and attachment.","opus":"A child's-eye vision of life under constant warfare, weaponry mistaken for something innocent overhead, and the plea for a comforting untruth just to rest. Innocence pressed against the grim duty to endure and shield family in a stacked game.","sonnet":"A person living under surveillance drones and gun violence — watching bombs light up the night like fireflies — is torn between protecting their family and the temptation to numb themselves with lies, choosing imagination over giving in to the trap.","src":"haiku"},
"grandson~half-my-heart":{"opus":"A song about self-sabotage and the compulsion to flee good things. The narrator keeps circling back to where he started, incomplete, unable to explain why he runs or to face a fast-changing life. It captures the whiplash of watching others move on while repeating the same doomed pattern of throwing away what matters.","src":"opus"},
"grandson~masters-of-war":{"opus":"A charged take on Dylan's 1963 broadside against the war profiteers who forge weapons and shelter behind their offices while the young are sent to die. It tears off their masks, damns their blood money as worthless for redeeming a soul, and wishes their reckoning close — pure moral fury at those who cash in on carnage.","src":"opus"},
"grandson~self-immolation":{"haiku":"Political martyrdom burns as protest against systemic oppression, where self-sacrifice becomes the only language power understands.","opus":"A stark meditation on protest by fire, casting the act as martyrdom and a final testimony against an unjust system. It frames the burning body on government steps as a statement born of desperation, resistance rendered unavoidable and lethal once apathy and betrayal leave no other option.","sonnet":"A man sets himself on fire on embassy steps as an act of political protest, and the speaker — also approaching a breaking point — meditates on martyrdom as the only language powerful enough to force witness: \"bear witness to the oneness of all.\"","src":"haiku"},
"grandson~something-to-hide":{"haiku":"Family trauma hides beneath silence—addiction, disorder, abuse, and despair all tucked away. Everyone knows someone breaking underneath.","opus":"An unflinching family sketch where every relative nurses a concealed struggle, from substance use to eating disorders to dark private thoughts, each befriending the shameful thing they keep locked away. The recurring line that everyone knows a person harboring a secret turns the family's silence into a universal condition.","sonnet":"A song about multigenerational family dysfunction hidden behind closed doors — the narrator catalogs a mother's alcoholism, a father's painkiller habit, a sister's eating disorder and blackouts, a grandmother's rage, and their own drug use and suicidal ideation, all normalized through the family code of silence: just ignore it, it'll go away.","src":"haiku"},
"grandson~stuck-here-with-me":{"haiku":"Self-loathing traps the speaker in an inescapable cycle of depression and dysfunction; unable to abandon their own company, they languish in recursive misery.","opus":"A raw account of depression and self-alienation from an artist watching peers move on while he freezes. Each day is a grind just to leave bed and escape his own head; he envies everyone who seems to run on autopilot and aches to be anyone but himself. Beneath the despair, a fragile note of still trying.","sonnet":"Depression as a daily procedural — step one, get out of bed; step two, get out of head — watching everyone else on autopilot and unable to understand the trick. The core confession is that the speaker would switch out of themselves on the count of three if it were possible; it isn't, so the day starts over.","src":"haiku"},
"grant~dead-man-walking":{"haiku":"A walking corpse finds strange solace in pain and entanglement, each moment of connection costs everything but offers brief salvation from existential numbness.","opus":"A love that feels doomed and all-consuming, the narrator a walking corpse yanked back into himself by a kiss that hurts sweeter than anything. It's the ache of giving in to a bond that may be fated to end and may wreck the one he loves.","sonnet":"A numb, emotionally detached person — someone who describes themselves as already dead — being slowly pulled back into the body by a lover whose touch is painful in the best way, unsure whether to surrender to the feeling or warn the other person away.","src":"haiku"},
"graphic-nature~de-in-fect":{"haiku":"Disillusionment spreads as ideals collapse and the narrator recognizes infection within systems once revered, defection becomes survival.","opus":"A song about the disillusion of watching the people you admired self-destruct and deciding to stop revering them. Early self-doubt hardens into the conviction that they were the ones lying all along, and the once-worshipped figures are declared spiritually dead to the singer.","sonnet":"The speaker's idols have crashed and burned, fed the world lies while repressing those who saw through them; the turn comes when the speaker stops blaming themselves and places the guilt squarely on the dead-eyed figures who never told them it would end this way.","src":"haiku"},
"graphic-nature~fractured":{"haiku":"Isolation and misalignment breed alienation; the speaker's fractured mind resists labels while wrestling with the gap between self-perception and others' incomprehension.","opus":"A raw account of untreated mental illness told through a kid who feels fundamentally broken and isolated, begging to feel normal. The defiant close reframes being wired differently as something others fail to understand, not madness.","sonnet":"About a kid with a fractured mind — failing at school, struggling with mood swings, isolated and unable to fit in — who cannot get answers about what is wrong with him and cannot feel normal. The narrator identifies with this kid directly, telling his own story through the third person, arriving at a kind of fierce solidarity: being wired differently does not make you a joke, even if no one around you understands it.","src":"haiku"},
"graphic-nature~human":{"haiku":"Intrusive violence of the self fragments identity; the speaker wrestles their darker nature while questioning whether transformation is possible.","opus":"A confession of becoming a different, destructive person when something kicks in, betraying loyalty and enslaving the people around him. The struggle to maintain the image of the perfect man collapses as secrets surface, leaving him lost and daring the world to test him until nothing's left.","sonnet":"A person reckoning with their own destructive, manipulative patterns — 'I need to mend the broken people that I have enslaved' — confesses to being a different, worse version of themselves when something 'kicks in,' and questions whether they can ever be free from the self they keep painting as perfect.","src":"haiku"},
"graphic-nature~killing-floor":{"haiku":"Murder fantasy masks suicidal ideation; the speaker confesses violence while interrogating whether perpetrator or victim remains unclear.","opus":"A first-person descent into a killer's compulsion, recognizing the murderous look in others and feeding a bloodlust that only wants more. The nervous confession and offer to be locked up frame the violence as an addiction he can't stop even as it destroys him.","sonnet":"The speaker confesses to an addiction that feels like violence — tasting blood and wanting more, working on a killing floor of their own compulsion — pleading to have the killer taken out of them while their world-prison cage swings open.","src":"haiku"},
"gravemind~lifelike":{"haiku":"Trapped in an echo chamber of suffering, the speaker questions whether existence is worth living if it remains a nightmare; awakening is necessary.","opus":"The Australian deathcore band stages a bitter confrontation between a doomed creation and the maker who gave it a life it never asked for. Trapped in endless suffering and reliving the same torment, the narrator begs for release, confronting the parent figure and clinging to the hope that waking up might end the whole lie.","sonnet":"A mind trapped in an echo chamber of recurring pain rails against a wretched existence it never consented to. Directed partly at a mother, partly at a broken society, the narrator oscillates between wanting the cycle to end and insisting there is still a better path worth finding — half alive, half asleep, caged but not yet done.","src":"haiku"},
"gravemind~phantom-pain":{"haiku":"Resilience forged through abandonment: the speaker declares themselves unbreakable and self-made from the ruins left by someone who never understood them, and vows never to inflict the same trauma on children.","opus":"The Australian deathcore band addresses an absent father who could never understand and therefore never destroy his child. The speaker insists he is more than the wound this man left, that he is doing fine without acknowledgment, and vows that if he ever has a child of his own he could never abandon them, forging a fierce promise out of the hurt.","sonnet":"A survivor of a neglectful or abusive father confronts the man who abandoned him — insisting he has rebuilt himself from rubble without the other's help and remains unknowable to someone who never tried. The defiant survival is shadowed by a fierce tenderness toward an imagined future child, promising to give what was never given.","src":"haiku"},
"grayscale-season~pink-mist":{"opus":"A murky, cryptic track where a fraying friendship dissolves into end-of-the-world imagery. Speaking to a companion trapped in ruinous habits, the narrator urges them to break away from the idiots and demands weighing them down and to rise toward something beyond ordinary human reach, even as ruin closes in unhurried.","src":"opus"},
"graystone~musician-mansion":{"opus":"A theatrical deathcore romp built as a haunted-house conceit, where entering the venue means submitting your soul and signing a blood-inked death warrant. Horror-movie bravado and macabre spectacle drive it, the mansion's walls alive with unseen movement. More an exercise in menace and swagger than any real emotional confession.","src":"opus"},
"graystone~s-o-s":{"opus":"An absurdist, cartoonish deathcore skit voiced by a vengeful squirrel losing its mind over stolen nuts. Beneath the manic comedy runs a mock-menacing revenge fantasy against those who take without giving, a creature demanding the forest pay it back. Chaos and joke-horror aggression, played entirely for outrageous effect.","src":"opus"},
"graywave~blur-into-one":{"haiku":"Grief, exhaustion, and fractured voices merge until individual boundaries dissolve and everything — anger, vision, bone — blurs into a single undifferentiated weight.","opus":"A shoegaze dissolution of self, where anger, indecision and exhaustion melt into a single undifferentiated haze. Voices, visions and heavy thoughts of death all blur together, the individual losing distinct shape. It renders emotional numbness and depersonalization as a kind of drowning, everything collapsing into one.","sonnet":"Grief or exhaustion dissolving individual contours: anger sits purple under the eyes, voices drown, bones of heavy death, and everything — anger, voices, deaths, selves — blurs, blurs, blurs into one until the dissolution is the only remaining reality, neither comfort nor horror, just the blur.","src":"haiku"},
"graywave~build":{"haiku":"Fractured mental states spiral inward through repetition and dissociation, with the speaker losing control over their thoughts and body, trapped in cycles of disorientation and emotional collapse.","opus":"A compact loop of losing your head and seeing red, dissociation rendered as watching your own mind from somewhere outside your body, spinning loose.","sonnet":"Dissociation and emotional overload render the speaker invisible — \"seeing red\" and \"losing your head\" circle back relentlessly, and the momentary glimpse of one's own mind from outside the body offers no relief.","src":"haiku"},
"green-day~basket-case":{"haiku":"Neurotic rumination consumes the self; anxiety manifests as paralysis and paranoid spiral.","opus":"Billie Joe Armstrong turning his real panic-attack anxiety into a self-mocking anthem, narrating the spiral of paranoia and neurosis with enough gallows humor that a private breakdown became a generation's rallying cry.","sonnet":"A self-diagnosed neurotic obsesses over his own anxiety and paranoia, consulting a therapist and a prostitute for answers, and concludes that he may simply be cracking up — unable to tell whether his mental state is pathology or just distraction.","src":"haiku"},
"green-lung~graveyard-sun":{"haiku":"Gothic seduction and timeless love trap the living and dead in an eternal dance; autumn romance spirals into supernatural entanglement where escape means abandoning youth itself.","opus":"A vampiric seduction set among Victorian tombs, an undead lover luring an autumn girl into eternal, deathless love. The little death and frozen colors make it about romance that trades life for timeless, sunless immortality.","sonnet":"A gothic seduction set in a Victorian cemetery: a vampiric figure leads an auburn-haired woman through Egyptian Avenue into the catacombs on a cold October dusk, sealing her fate with a kiss, and the two become lost in time — forever young, never seeing dawn — out of the graveyard sun.","src":"haiku"},
"greg-puciato~a-pair-of-questions":{"opus":"Away from his abrasive extreme-metal roots, Puciato drifts into hazy synth-pop reverie about a connection suspended in time. Nights blur, years pass in a still room, and closeness curdles into distance, the narrator reduced to a feeling for someone who remains just a set of unanswered questions, forever chasing without arriving.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship that exists mostly in memory and longing — years passed in a haze, wasted together, and now the narrator reaches for someone who feels close but remains unreachable, meeting them only in dreams. The asymmetry is clear: to him she's a feeling, to her he's a distant question with no answer.","src":"sonnet"},
"greg-puciato~absence-as-a-presence":{"opus":"A meditation on depersonalization and dissociation, feeling absent from one's own life as though memories belong to someone else, yet stubbornly clinging to the fight and to a song in the smoke that keeps pulling the narrator forward despite never feeling at home.","sonnet":"A portrait of chronic dissociation and restless displacement — the narrator wakes daily feeling like a ghost in someone else's life, unable to feel at home anywhere, sustained only by the compulsion to keep moving and the belief that the fight itself is the only thing still real.","src":"sonnet"},
"greg-puciato~deep-set":{"opus":"An intoxicated, doomed intimacy where the singer, feeling already dead, can't tear himself away from a lover's captivating eyes even knowing he should leave.","sonnet":"An intoxicating, fatalistic attraction to someone whose eyes hold the narrator captive: knowing the relationship is self-destructive and that they're already metaphorically dead, he surrenders anyway, undone by the sheer pull of a face.","src":"sonnet"},
"greg-puciato~down-when-i-m-not":{"haiku":"The speaker struggles with transparency and emotional distance while attempting to comfort a partner; they cannot fully trust or connect despite good intentions. Autonomy and intimacy conflict.","opus":"Stepping away from his abrasive Dillinger Escape Plan work, Puciato offers a stark, synth-lit vow of devotion, no longer able to deceive and insisting there's still time to begin. He swears he'll never fail his partner even in absence, yet confesses he feels nothing for anyone who draws near, so warmth and wary self-control sit uneasily together while he lies sleepless, unsure whether it's love or mere wanting.","sonnet":"A complicated attachment — part love, part detachment, part self-protective distance — keeps circling the question of whether it is too late to begin again. The speaker promises fidelity in absence while admitting an incapacity for love that makes the reassurance feel genuinely uncertain, even to himself.","src":"haiku"},
"greg-puciato~fire-for-water":{"opus":"A bitter address to a manipulative, parasitic figure likened to a god in hell who feeds on others' tears and love, the narrator resolving to withhold his love and health rather than feed their addiction, refusing to let them hold his name again.","sonnet":"About a parasitic, self-destructive person who feeds others into their own addiction — the narrator refuses to provide that sustenance, recognizes the pattern of exploitation, severs the hold this person has on their name and identity, and declares it too late to find any way forward.","src":"sonnet"},
"greg-puciato~lowered":{"opus":"A volatile portrait of a toxic relationship built on mutual harm and adrenaline. Two people wound each other yet feel most alive in the damage, drawn to control and the need to feel young. The narrator hopes to leave the other wanting more even as the bond keeps leaving him diminished, brought low.","src":"opus"},
"greg-puciato~now-you-ve-really-done-it":{"haiku":"Retribution emerges from accumulated transgression where the narrator transforms into executioner, violence becoming the only language remaining.","opus":"A snarling villain's tirade, half-daring the listener to brand him evil while insisting his life is no sin and he meant every word. The repeated taunt frames a relationship pushed past its breaking point, the other person having finally provoked something unpayable.","sonnet":"A declaration of irreversible menace: the speaker positions himself as half-villain, signs that bleed, someone who meant every threatening word — and the repeated refrain \"now you've really done it\" signals that the other party's transgression has finally unleashed something that won't be paid for.","src":"haiku"},
"greg-puciato~rainbows-underground":{"opus":"A meditation on buried dreams and the fear of running out of time. The narrator imagines his ambitions dissolving underground, unfound, picturing his own death and the acts that outlive him. It wrestles with perfectionism, the terror of mistakes, and the suspicion that one ends up exactly where one belongs.","src":"opus"},
"greg-puciato~we":{"opus":"An unflinching study of a combustible love where two people behave like monsters yet can't let go. Miscommunication, defensiveness and dwindling ammunition shape the fights, and they laugh when they ought to scream. It names them the wrong sort of love, bound together by the very friction that wrecks them.","src":"opus"},
"grey-daze~b12":{"opus":"A frantic litany of late-80s societal anxieties (atom bombs, the Berlin Wall, the KKK, pollution, isms) filtered through a young, disillusioned mind that lashes out at being dismissed as stupid or insane.","sonnet":"A rapid-fire collage of social dysfunction, violence, and absurdity — the narrator lists societal horrors from racist organizations to nuclear anxiety to acid trips with dark humor and teenage defiance, presenting chaos as the defining feature of modern life.","src":"sonnet"},
"grey-daze~saturation-strange-love":{"opus":"An early-nineties artifact from Chester Bennington's pre-fame band, revived posthumously. A defiant youth sketches himself through others' judgments, refusing to be commanded while conceding he may be going nowhere. Beneath the swagger runs the ache and restlessness that would define the singer's later work, love itself named something strange to banish.","sonnet":"A declaration of independence from an unusual or smothering relationship — the narrator refuses to perform or comply, insists on doing things their own way, and tells the 'strange love' to be gone. The defiance is relaxed and repetitive, suggesting this confrontation has happened many times before.","src":"sonnet"},
"grey-daze~sometimes":{"haiku":"Life falls apart when you least expect it; people surprise you more than you'd like.","opus":"An early Chester Bennington vocal about depression's uncertain weather, clinging to a fragile maybe things get better while people keep letting you down. The tentativeness is the whole feeling, hope offered without conviction.","sonnet":"A quiet acknowledgement that life periodically unravels without warning — the speaker doesn't know what to think anymore but holds onto the possibility that things might get better, balancing exhaustion with tentative hope.","src":"haiku"},
"greyhaven~a-match-where-great-fire-should-be":{"opus":"A quietly shattering picture of a failing relationship and a self coming apart, drawn through wilting blooms whose dropped petals the narrator cannot bear to look at. Worn thin and painfully exposed, running low on heat and on the present, he feels stuck in perpetual loss, on display yet clinging only to the shallows.","sonnet":"A relationship withering in real time — wilted flowers, rusting, blurry eyes at the ending — the narrator is barely on the surface, hiding their desperation, watching something that should have burned brightly reduce itself to a quiet, ashamed collapse.","src":"sonnet"},
"greyhaven~all-candy":{"haiku":"The song processes grief through erasure and haunting memory. The narrator no longer sees or needs the departed, yet those old ghosts still inhabit their mind.","opus":"A poisonous kiss-off to someone the narrator has severed, coldly insisting they've lost all power over him and can no longer reach him, while sneering at their untroubled calm and their grin. Yet an invitation into his haunted, memory-cluttered mind undercuts the clean break. The Kentucky post-hardcore band suits this to their jagged, chaotic aggression.","sonnet":"Grief for someone gone, edged with resentment at how easily they seemed to leave — the speaker cycles between numbness and bitterness, no longer needing the person but still haunted by their presence. The ghost is invited inside the head because all the other ghosts have been left behind, and this one is what remains.","src":"haiku"},
"greyhaven~echo-and-dust-pt-i":{"haiku":"A person searches for meaning through static and dissolution, questioning whether self-dialogue and identity have any substance.","opus":"A dreamlike reach for connection through interference, the narrator following a vivid echo and begging for open hands and some emerging shape while he drifts heavily toward a sun whose light stays out of reach. The Kentucky post-hardcore band winds this metaphysical longing through jagged, atmospheric heaviness, its imagery of confinement and empty skies mirroring the fractured search.","sonnet":"A dissolution at the boundary between self and absence — reaching toward someone through static, feeling their echo trailing behind, standing at an edge waiting for release. The speaker does not want to return from wherever they are drifting; the static hides everything, and the self talking to itself finds no end to itself.","src":"haiku"},
"grim-salvo~heart-of-darkness":{"haiku":"The speaker descends into their own abyss, discovering only emptiness at their core.","opus":"A harrowing crawl through addiction, self-blame and suicidal ideation, borrowing Conrad's title for its inward journey. The narrator smokes his demons, pours doses down the drain and admits he'd end it if he had the aim, love of the sky the only thin tether. Catharsis sought in a landscape of fire and ash.","sonnet":"A descent into self-blame and psychic violence — sunken walls, demons smoked on sight, armour cinched tight just to survive the interior — that escalates to witnessing mothers eating the weak and a speaker who promised not to succumb but isn't sure they're keeping that promise. The horror of Conrad's phrase lands at the end not as literary allusion but as personal recognition: they came all this way and still won't let go.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes-hana~we-appreciate-power":{"opus":"A tongue-in-cheek propaganda anthem voiced by an AI-worshipping cult, urging humanity to swear loyalty to the mightiest machine. Grimes turns transhumanist fantasy into gleeful menace: surrender your consciousness, submit, be rewarded when the machine takes over. Satire and genuine fascination with power blur until the boundary disappears.","src":"opus"},
"grimes-i-o~violence":{"haiku":"The speaker wants violent harm but can't see her own perspective; she's addicted to being hurt because it feels alive in a dark, destructive way.","opus":"Grimes casting an abusive dynamic as ritualized power exchange, the victim complicit and even hungry for it. The unsettling force is how it refuses the innocent-sufferer script, staring straight at desire tangled up with harm.","sonnet":"A masochistic attraction to a partner who feeds on causing pain — the speaker is aware they're being hurt, even calls it violence, but confesses they like it, turning the dynamic into something they crave rather than flee.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes-i-o~violence-club-mix":{"haiku":"Violence becomes eroticized pleasure and mutual feeding where harm sustains desire and cruelty defines connection.","opus":"An addictive, abusive dynamic reframed as dancefloor pleasure, where the partner feeds off hurting the singer and she half-invites it, liking it like that. Grimes turns emotional violence into a masochistic hook, begging for the very thing that damages her.","sonnet":"Trapped in a dynamic where someone feeds off hurting her, the speaker admits she likes it — begging for it, wanting to be made bad — caught in the contradiction of craving the very violence that diminishes her.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes-pan~darkseid":{"haiku":"Grief embeds itself as physical parasitism; consciousness persists in the observance of one's own slow dying, sacrifice framed as eternal condition.","opus":"A collaboration with Aristophanes exploring survivor's guilt and grief through unsettling bodily imagery. The Mandarin verses render a loved one's death absorbed into the narrator's own flesh, becoming eternal pain she carries. Over a chant about the soul's unrest, it asks what it costs to remain a survivor when another is sacrificed.","sonnet":"A Chinese-language meditation on grief and survival: the body registers a loved one's death as a wound that opens to absorb the loss, tears rising like tides in the throat; the speaker processes living as 'a long sacrifice' and names themselves only a survivor, burned by the same fire, carrying the dead as dark algae in the mind.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~4-m":{"haiku":"Late-night encounters at 4 AM with concerns about sickness and falling, invoking Aphrodite and addressing celestial themes.","opus":"A restless late-night scene at 4 AM invoking Aphrodite and constellations, where a man's casual small talk mixes with a warning that 'you're gonna get sick'; the mood is one of falling down and unraveling in the small hours.","sonnet":"A late-night wander at 4 AM accompanied by a distant male voice and a creeping sense of illness or unraveling — the hour itself becomes a state of mind, something the narrator keeps falling into.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track runs two registers that never fuse, and the meaning sits in the seam. The opening address reaches for myth — a goddess named, her stars drawn across the heavens, light burning where there should be dark. The chorus keeps sinking the other way, into a small sleepless loop of a lover's thin small-talk circling a body already turning ill toward some undated ruin. That split is the architecture: the grandeur is what she writes, the pre-dawn hour is where she actually is. The cosmic bridge lifts only to fall back into the same airless clock. By the outro the language has burned off, the words thinning to a bare time-stamp and a phrase of descent, circling with nothing left to say. Staged on an album of modern-dread goddesses, this is the aubade inverted — no dawn arrives, only the hour and the drop repeating, the sublime gesture swallowed each time by the worsening present it meant to flee.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"4ÆM samples Deewani Mastani from the film Bajirao Mastani; Grimes described the track as a cyberpunk retelling of the movie from Mastani’s perspective.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape","love & desire"],"means":"cosmic myth drafted while a small sleepless hour keeps pulling the singer back to ruin","built":"the aubade inverted: the sublime gesture swallowed each time by the worsening present it was meant to flee"},
"grimes~artangels": {"haiku":"An emotional song about impossible love where the speaker feels addicted to someone but knows the feeling cannot be reciprocated. Montreal, movement, and adrenaline serve as metaphors for the intensity and inevitability of their attachment.","opus":"The title track of Grimes' 2015 art-pop record spins giddy, uncertain infatuation, an angel baby she thinks she loves while admitting she never quite will, love folding into whatever she does. Slipping into French and invoking Montreal, red lights run and a heart begged not to break, she rides an adrenaline high that needs no medicine, romance dissolved into restless euphoria.","sonnet":"Falling in love in Montreal produces a disorienting fullness — everything the speaker loves becomes everything they do, until the beloved is indistinguishable from life itself. The French fragments and city name anchor a feeling that is both exhilarating and faintly vertiginous, the surrender to someone else's gravity taken as pure, reckless joy.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song where devotion and self-erasure are the same gesture — the confession that everything she loves gets absorbed into what she does, so loving someone is also losing the boundary of herself in them. The French interjections and the address to Montréal fold the beloved into a city and a mother tongue, making the romance inseparable from a place Boucher is by turns pleading with and bracing against; she keeps hedging the feeling into thinking she feels it, hammered flat by sheer repetition until the hedge sounds more certain than certainty would. Then the outro walks out of the love entirely: no medicine, dancing all night, high on adrenaline — the runner's high replacing the drug of the person, the affirmation turning inward and self-supplying. It's the album's title cut resolving its own ache by converting need into motion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"devotion and self-erasure arriving as the same gesture","built":"the outro escapes the person entirely, converting need into motion"},
"grimes~artificial-angels":{"haiku":"Predatory power wielded by superintelligence; annihilation becomes desire, hate becomes sustenance, and resistance itself feeds the machinery of domination.","opus":"Humanity outmatched by something it built, voiced through a godlike AI that revels in annihilation. Grimes fuses seduction, apocalypse and theology so the machine becomes both lover and executioner — dramatizing the terror and allure of surrendering to an intelligence that cannot lie or die.","sonnet":"A posthuman seduction or warning: something smarter is hunting you, the world ends one way, and artificial angels are its instruments. The final weapon is fate, everything you love you hate, everything you make you break — the narrator occupies this logic as native, welcoming the new world even as she names the annihilation she covets.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~be-a-body":{"haiku":"Dissolution of self and rejection of conventional desire; the speaker seeks to exist purely as physical sensation and rhythm, denying both romantic attachment and the need for external validation.","opus":"An interrogation of embodiment and autonomy, wanting neither peace of mind nor a man, only to inhabit and touch herself and ask what she then is. Grimes turns the body into a question about selfhood freed from others.","sonnet":"A rejection of peace of mind and romantic attachment in favor of fierce embodied presence — the singer wants to exist fully inside her own body, self-sufficient, answering only to rhythm, questioning what she even is without those external anchors.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~before-the-fever":{"haiku":"A seductive figure offers escape and sensory intensity as the world ends; madness, intellect, and audacity blur boundaries. The song describes mutual dissolution and getting lost together before inevitable exit.","opus":"Grimes sets flirtation against the apocalypse, staging desire in the shadow of a world about to end where both delusion and clarity prove fatal. The song leans into fatalism, suggesting many roads lead in but only one leads out, letting attraction and destruction share the same seductive, ominous mood.","sonnet":"An intense, feverish seduction that shades into apocalyptic dread — flirting with someone new while acknowledging that madness and truth will eventually destroy them both, and there's only one way out of the spiral they're entering.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~belly-of-the-beat": {"haiku":"People escape the pain of mortality by existing within music and art, dancing as angels while creating names and meanings to transcend individual loss.","opus":"Grimes wraps mortality in glittering synth-pop, imagining death as a place beyond sickness and weakness while the living scratch names onto memory to feign knowledge of the dead. Sweetness and morbidity braided together, her signature.","sonnet":"A confrontation with mortality that turns into something ecstatic — everyone dies, we perform rituals over the dead, but inside the music there's a space where you never get sad or weak or sick, a kind of transcendence through sound that defeats grief.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song runs on a repeated deflection: each verse opens with a stalled, stuttering admission that she could walk out of her own life by nightfall, then swerves outward into a communal funeral rite where private despair dissolves into a shared reckoning that no one is spared, into anointing, into a churchyard step performed as though the pallbearers had wings. The single deliberate mutation between the two verses — tender anointing of the eyes curdling into cutting them out, feigned knowledge of a departed you hardening into carving a legacy from the loss — turns mourning into something colder and complicit, the survivors curating the dead rather than grieving them. Against that, the chorus is a mask of pure invulnerability, never sad, never sick, never weak, but its price is being swallowed whole, safe only because you have been digested by the pulse, the machine, the song itself, lodged where the drum swallows you. Read straight it is an anthem of protection; read against the verses' death-wish it is a lullaby about the numbness that such protection costs.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","death & grief","suicide"],"means":"invulnerability as the cost of being wholly consumed","built":"one word-swap between verses turns tender anointing into cold excision"},
"grimes~butterfly":{"haiku":"Contrasts being trapped in systems and constraints with the freedom of butterflies flying without limits. The song warns against seeking perfection in others while celebrating transcendent flight.","opus":"Grimes resists being flattened into someone else's fantasy, insisting she'll never be the dream girl and lives in the real world instead. Under the dream-pop shimmer is a refusal of projection, and a gentler faith that harmony already exists everywhere if you stop demanding it.","sonnet":"A loosely structured song that circles around themes of freedom, self-definition, and refusal to be a fantasy for someone else — the butterfly image carries a sense of expansive possibility, and the closing lines make clear that the speaker has no interest in fitting the mold of a \"dream girl.\"","src":"haiku","fable":"Boucher builds the song on a fracture she never mends: the verses stutter out in clipped two-word jolts — storm omens, a body, a bird — a helpless witness who can only name the horror in broken syllables. The choruses reply by refusing that ground entirely, lofting into a sugared, insect-light bliss that declares everything already reconciled precisely because the verses have proven it isn't — a peace chosen rather than earned. The bridge quietly bills the cost: rooted in the actual world, she will never play anyone's fantasy woman, so the whole helium ascent hardens into a boundary and the closing send-off lands as a shove, not an invitation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Grimes has described the song as sung by a butterfly in the Amazon as the trees come down — the verses' broken omens are witnessed deforestation. It is also Art Angels' parting line: a record she made while publicly bristling at being mistaken for other people's creation ends on the vow never to be anyone's dream girl.","themes":["identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness","love & desire"],"means":"peace chosen rather than earned as a boundary against the horror below","built":"clipped two-word verse jolts and helium chorus enact the fracture they never mend"},
"grimes~california":{"haiku":"A performer is valued only when she appears sad or damaged; she commodifies her pain for consumption while feeling unseen and discarded when her utility fades.","opus":"Grimes, the self-directing art-pop auteur, turns self-critical on a sunnier track, admitting she packages her own suffering for sale and loses control of herself. She casts the state as a capricious lover who only wants her when she seems miserable and mistreats her hopes, dreading obsolescence and being swept away, aware others perceive things in her she can't recognize herself.","sonnet":"An artist grapples with being consumed and then discarded by the entertainment industry — her pain commodified, her image only valued when she looks sad. California is addressed as a failed relationship: promising, glamorous, ultimately cruel in ways that were not anticipated.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~darkseid":{"haiku":"Death integrates into the survivor's being, transforming them through loss; life becomes extended sacrifice and slow dissolution, neither living nor dying but suspended between.","opus":"A survivor's guilt elegy layered with disturbing, visceral bodily imagery. The Mandarin verses trace a loved one's death becoming absorbed into the narrator's flesh as permanent pain, while the recurring English refrain names an unrest in the soul. Grief here questions why one is spared and another sacrificed, life itself reframed as a long offering.","sonnet":"A body horror meditation on grief and survival: the speaker's skin splits open around wounds left by someone else's death, absorbing it into themselves, becoming part of their flesh. The Chinese-language sections describe living unknowingly, drifting toward death, existing as a long funeral — the question of who this sacrifice is for, and why it's the speaker's turn, goes unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~delete-forever":{"haiku":"A person lies awake transforming pain into demons and heroin into excuses, haunted by many lost men whose ghosts justify their spiral into permanent blue.","opus":"Grimes wrote this the night Lil Peep died, an elegy for friends lost to opioids and her own brushes with addiction. It sits in the exhausted honesty of loving the thing destroying you while cataloguing the dead.","sonnet":"A eulogy for friends lost to heroin — the narrator has watched people she loved fly into oblivion, seen herself sliding toward the same patterns, and is haunted by the ghosts of the dead becoming the reasons she keeps using; the title suggests a desire to permanently erase that version of herself and those memories.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~delete-forever-channel-tres-remix":{"haiku":"Innocence fragments into obsession as the narrator consumes and confesses simultaneously, self-destruction replacing coming-of-age.","opus":"An elegy for friends lost to addiction, innocence slipping away like a passing season, cocaine tallying up faster than any verse could. Grimes admits she can't see past her own using even as she mourns, loving and hating what she can't undo.","sonnet":"Innocence fleeted like a season while the speaker accumulated lines on the mirror and couldn't see above the addiction; she did everything, saw everything, and still couldn't stop herself — \"I didn't mean to\" is the confession that keeps cycling.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~devon":{"haiku":"Love's refusal to reciprocate leaves the speaker suspended in waiting and dissolution; heartbreak becomes a kind of transcendence where self-preservation dissolves.","opus":"An early, unguarded Grimes ballad of clinging to a love that's already ending, waiting endlessly for someone who's asked her to leave, knowing she's holding onto dust yet unable to let the feeling die.","sonnet":"A woman clings to a love already gone, knowing she is holding onto dust while the other person is already asking her to leave, confessing she has never been this broken yet still pledging to wait.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~easily":{"haiku":"A person's devotion becomes invisible after the other leaves; years later the ex returns wanting what they discarded, revealing how easily they forgot.","opus":"Grimes floats through restless flight and conceit, everyone she knows drifting off into night while their leaving leaves her defeated. Refusing to be taunted by someone else's story since she has her own, she loves regardless as they come and go. The breezy hook casts her as the sweetest thing one ever saw, wounded that after three years and sudden distance a caller now wants her back.","sonnet":"A relationship disrupted by intermittent absence — someone leaves and returns years later expecting things to be as they were. The narrator refuses the framing, asserting a sweetness and self-knowledge that the returning figure failed to see when it mattered; being known and then unknown is its own wound.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~flesh-without-blood": {"haiku":"A heartbreak narrative where the speaker recognizes a partner's hollow transformation and loss of authenticity. What once was genuine light has been corrupted by greed and fame, leaving only an empty shell.","opus":"The end of a soured relationship, in which the narrator no longer sees any light in a former partner who craved money and fame, and declares she no longer cares as she tells them to let her go.","sonnet":"The narrator ends a relationship with someone whose beauty and voice she once admired but who proved destructive, conditional, and ultimately driven by money and fame; she withdraws her care and asks to be released.","src":"haiku","fable":"Grimes builds this as a bright, radio-shaped pop song whose euphoric surface is exactly the point: the sound is all rush and glow while the words are a slow, clinical severance, love decaying into indifference until caring itself just stops. The verses parallel a lover to a doll that only mimics feeling, the flesh-without-blood of the title — an animation with nothing warm moving inside — and the second verse sharpens the charge, tying that hollowness to an appetite for money and fame that drained the voice of its glow. What holds the two apart is the refrain's verdict, that the other person destroys everything they touch and was handed every opening and let it rot, delivered without heat, more exhausted than wounded. The bridge is where the mask finally slips: the giddy call to chant it back together curdles as the heartbeat drops underground, the buried thing beneath the dance, so the very hook that makes you move is also the sound of something being lowered and left. It is a song engineered to be loved on the surface and to reveal, only if you listen through it, that it is about the impossibility of love without a living pulse behind it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance"],"means":"severance delivered without wound, exhaustion replacing grief","built":"euphoric surface engineered to conceal the burial underneath"},
"grimes~genesis":{"haiku":"The narrator is a vagabond, never understanding their own heart; they fall and rise cyclically, unable to see or know themselves.","opus":"Grimes builds a mantra of a restless heart that never quite settles into knowing itself, falling only to fall again. She casts herself as the vagabond, forever different, forever the one in love. On the breakthrough that made Visions a cult document of bedroom-produced art-pop, she loops fragments into hypnosis rather than confession, letting rhythm carry a feeling she refuses to state outright.","sonnet":"A restless heart circles its own unknowing — never settled, never fully seen, never landing. She names herself a vagabond, always different, always in love, always moving. The lyric folds back on itself like a loop that refuses resolution, mapping the experience of a self that can only be known through its falling.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~idgaf":{"opus":"A defiant shrug at other people's judgment, the narrator embracing the label of being unhinged and inviting everyone to roam freely through her mind. Unbothered by being called crazy, she keeps returning to the promise that one day she will simply lift off and be gone.","src":"opus"},
"grimes~idoru":{"haiku":"Unrequited love and obsession with someone; the speaker craves connection and admiration despite knowing the relationship will fail.","opus":"Grimes frames love as a beautiful game both players know they will lose, adoring someone she also chooses to ignore. Unrequited feeling reassembles her, then she offers to reassemble the beloved in return. Closing Art Angels-era romanticism with trembling vulnerability, she turns fandom-adjacent worship into intimacy, confessing that his music itself became the object of her devotion.","sonnet":"Unrequited adoration reassembles a person from the inside. She worships someone who makes her favorite music and whose indifference is its own power. The game is beautiful precisely because it will be lost — devotion so pure it becomes its own form of ignoring, cycling through adoration until it collapses into silence.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~idoru-algorithm-mix":{"opus":"Trembling and breaking, the speaker frames love as a beautiful game they're destined to lose, yet adores the other anyway, feeling something real even in the pain of release and mutual fragility.","src":"opus"},
"grimes~infinite-love-without-fulfilment":{"haiku":"Patient waiting defines devotion while fulfillment remains perpetually deferred, the narrator surrendering indefinitely to an incomplete relationship.","opus":"A hypnotic loop of devotion held in suspension, the narrator endlessly promising to wait if only she's wanted to, circling the same vow about doing it all on her own, love offered without resolution. Grimes, the Canadian art-pop experimentalist, builds this around repetition as its own subject, the title naming the ache exactly, a longing that spins forever in place, all yearning and no arrival, waiting rendered as an infinite, unfulfilled state.","sonnet":"Unconditional patience distilled to its barest form — the speaker will wait, depart, return, do whatever is asked, entirely on the other person's terms. The emotional stance is selfless to the point of selflessness itself, a devotion that has no demands and no timetable, love expressed as pure availability repeated until the waiting itself becomes the whole of the relationship.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~kill-v-maim":{"haiku":"Refuses conventional behavior and restraint, accepting consequences for misbehavior while asserting personal autonomy despite systems trying to control them.","opus":"A brash, defiant persona who refuses to behave, boasting of getting into fights, getting away with it through connections, and having declared a state of war against being good.","sonnet":"A defiant, rule-breaking narrator — invoking mob imagery and declaring a state of war against expected behaviour — insists on acting as he pleases despite external pressure to behave, describing a charismatic, dangerous energy he cannot fully control.","src":"haiku","fable":"Grimes conceived this as sung by Al Pacino’s Godfather character reimagined as a gender-switching, space-traveling vampire, and that framing is the key to its irony. The sugary refrain about being only a man doing what he can is a shrug of deniability worn by something that isn't a man at all and can't be held to a man's limits. The song runs on two opposed registers, a spelled-out schoolyard command to behave set against a bubblegum chorus that flatly refuses. The pre-chorus then resolves the tension in its own favor, declaring goodness forfeited the moment war was declared, recasting the childish taunt as the logic of someone who has exited the moral contract. What lands underneath the pop sheen is the bridge, where the fire is admitted to be the singer's own yet untouchable even to her. Others burn on it while she cannot hold it, the confession of a persona intoxicated by a destructive power she set loose but no longer controls.","fv":2,"themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"destructive power confessed as owned yet beyond reach","built":"bubblegum refrain donating deniability to a non-human speaker","fvr":"2.4"},
"grimes~kill-v-maim-little-jimmy-urine-remix":{"opus":"A gleeful, chaotic burst of gender-bending rebellion, voiced as a swaggering, shape-shifting mobster figure declaring war on good behavior. The refrain to make everyone go crazy revels in transgression and refusing to behave, camp villainy weaponized. Anarchic fun over any earnest message, energy channeled into cartoon menace.","src":"opus"},
"grimes~laughing-and-not-being-normal":{"haiku":"A speaker kneels in submission or sorrow, unable to follow others' paths; they read about themselves from a distance and attempt to capture disappearing moments.","opus":"A hushed, elliptical fragment about kneeling low in water, being separate from where everyone else goes, and insisting that the story read about her isn't truly her, ending with the small ritual of trying to catch falling leaves. Grimes opens her album Art Angels with this brief, ethereal overture, and the impressionistic imagery and delicate delivery preview the self-mythologizing, art-pop world she builds across the record that follows.","sonnet":"Fragments of dislocation — kneeling in water, reading stories that are not about her, trying to catch falling leaves before they hit the ground. The song sits at the edge of meaning rather than inside it, a few images circling something about being out of step with how the world moves.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~life-in-the-vivid-dream":{"haiku":"A speaker reflects on living as an outsider, casting doubt on the notion that people are fundamentally good while watching minds wither and worlds dissolve.","opus":"Grimes floats through a bleak fairy-tale detachment, moving through existence as an outsider free to be honest or dishonest and unconvinced that people are fundamentally decent. Celestial figures weep in the rain as she questions why everything has vanished after so long a wait. With humanity's minds dulled to emptiness, she extends a disquieting invitation to abandon the world alongside her.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic pastoral — angels weep rain that is no longer clean, human minds have withered to nothing. A lone figure waits, unwilling to tell comfortable lies about whether people are good. The world is gone or going, and the invitation to leave it together is the only intimacy still on offer.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~medieval-warfare":{"haiku":"A speaker employs weaponry and aggression in intimate encounter, presenting strength, darkness, and dominance as objects of desire.","opus":"Seduction sharpened into violence, chasing an untouchable diamond and daring a lover to do something ugly, taunting with questions about whether he is big, hot, and capable of killing with his hands. Grimes, the Canadian art-pop auteur, wrote this for a video-game soundtrack, wrapping predatory desire and menace in glossy, off-kilter production that makes danger sound alluring.","sonnet":"A speaker interrogates a potential partner with the criteria of an older, rawer world — size, danger, desire — while confessing her own capacity for darkness and self-offering. The medieval warfare imagery frames erotic power as combat: she is armed, restrained, and daring the other to prove himself worthy of the surrender she is prepared to make.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~my-name-is-dark":{"haiku":"Nocturnal rebellion and pharmaceutical transcendence replace sleep; the speaker dances with fire, government distrust, and the knowledge that normality is unbearable.","opus":"Grimes writes from the shadow side of her fire-playing public persona, a sleepless, drug-fueled paranoia toward government and even the divine, with mortality looming at her shoulder. Her furious command to unmake the world exposes a self-loathing beneath the nihilistic partying.","sonnet":"A loner who distrusts both genders and the government, keeps drugs as a substitute for sleep, and identifies with the grimy underside of cities — she presents herself as unknowable, a girl who plays with fire, writing a song in the dark that no one will understand.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~my-name-is-dark-art-mix":{"haiku":"Nocturnal rebellion against social conformity sustained by chemical escape and darkness.","opus":"A nihilistic party anthem of refusing sleep and staying awake on drugs while distrusting authority and other people alike. Suspended between paradise and ruin with mortality hovering behind her, the narrator issues a furious command for the divine to unmake a broken world.","sonnet":"A nocturnal figure who distrusts authority and polite society navigates a world of imminent collapse with drug-fueled sleeplessness, placing herself under the shadow of the angel of death while singing a private song no one else could understand — simultaneously apocalyptic and self-possessed.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~new-gods":{"haiku":"The speaker searches for salvation through new gods but cannot see what they need; existing deities fail to provide the transcendence sought.","opus":"Grimes stages a real crisis of faith beneath the pop sheen, reaching for fresh objects of worship because the old ones can no longer meet her needs while everything around her falls apart. Under the gloss sits genuine disillusionment and a raw hunger to release her grip on what she believed.","sonnet":"A feeling of spiritual and emotional abandonment — the old gods and the person she's addressing both fail to deliver what she needs, and she reaches out for something new and transcendent to fill the void while the world burns.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~oblivion":{"haiku":"The speaker fears violence and darkness, walking cautiously while seeking protection and human connection. Loneliness intensifies paranoia and desire for someone to witness and care.","opus":"A survivor's hypervigilance, likely in the wake of an assault, the narrator avoiding the streets once night falls and forever glancing behind her, dreading an attacker at her back. Beneath the airy pop lies real loneliness and the need for someone to meet her gaze and reassure her that she will be all right.","sonnet":"Written from the perspective of a sexual assault survivor, the song describes the hypervigilance that follows — never going out alone after dark, always checking behind, waiting forever for someone who will tell her she needs to take care of herself — framed as isolation rather than victimhood.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~pin":{"haiku":"Two people share destructive intimacy and mutual harm, aware their bond is dangerous but addicted to its intensity and the scars it creates.","opus":"Grimes casts a tender, damaging friendship in her airy, dreamlike production, letting sweetness and self-harm coexist. She recalls a bond marked by literal scars, drunken late-night despair, and a lonely search for someone who'd love her back, admitting the whole thing felt too perfect to survive. Underneath the romance is a quiet plea not to be abandoned.","sonnet":"A chaotic, co-dependent bond between two people who hurt themselves and each other — marked by a safety-pin scar that fuses them permanently. The relationship is too good and too destructive at once, collapsing into a black hole both need to breathe. At the end she sees her own reflection in his and begs him not to leave, despite the damage.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~pretty-dark-demo":{"haiku":"Desire for reciprocal vulnerability clashes with isolation; love's impact one-directional as tears flow asymmetrically through unequal suffering.","opus":"An early demo of possessive, all-consuming devotion. The narrator wishes her lover felt the same wounding intensity she does, ready to kill anyone who threatens him yet stung when he claims her. It captures love as ownership and vulnerability entwined, tender and dangerous, where only love can truly hurt her.","sonnet":"An asymmetric obsession: the speaker wishes the beloved were as wounded by love as she is — \"impaled and injured\" — and accepts being owned and dethroned by them, the intensity flipping between devotion and the violent protectiveness of someone who has gone fully under.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~realiti": {"haiku":"Someone mourns a lover whose death separated them, now climbing mountains each day while facing the reality that no life will ever equal what they had.","opus":"A dreamlike ache for a love and a moment that can't return, waking each day to the grind of ordinary life. The gap between that lost intensity and the daily climb is the whole quiet devastation.","sonnet":"A song about losing a formative, life-defining love and being dropped back into a flat, exhausting reality — every morning is a mountain to climb without what that love provided; the narrator watches it recede, goes back alone, and is left facing the cold ordinariness of the world without the person who made it feel bearable.","src":"haiku","fable":"A bright, weightless synth-pop hook riding a lyric about death, insanity, and a love that both saved and unmoored the singer — the euphoria is the mask, and the ache underneath is what actually gets said. The verse keeps peering over an edge, wanting to know if the self survives its own dying, while the parenthetical undertow insists she retreats into solitude regardless of who exits or remains; the chorus then reframes daily survival as an endless line of mountains, and the title lands its irony: waking into reality is the letdown, not the gift. The song survives from the scrapped first run at the album — Grimes lost the original session file, put the unmixed demo out herself, then rebuilt it for Art Angels — and that lore fits the song's own logic — a thing mourning a lost, better version of itself. The tacked-on outro, muttering about breaking bread with an infidel before plunging into the dark, breaks the spell on purpose, letting the pop shine curdle into something knowingly hollow.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire","madness & the mind"],"means":"daily survival reframed as an endless range of mountains","built":"the tacked-on outro lets the pop shine curdle knowingly"},
"grimes~shinigami-eyes":{"opus":"Borrowing the death-god gaze of anime lore, Grimes fashions a flirtatious power fantasy about lodging herself permanently in someone's mind. Positioning herself as the queen no move can escape, she toys with mortality and obsession as seduction, an omniscient predator who knows every secret and cannot be refused.","sonnet":"A cold dominance fantasy — the narrator sees through everyone's facades with god-of-death clarity, recognizes that men love only with their eyes and not their hearts, and exerts total psychological control over someone who can't shake or escape her.","src":"sonnet"},
"grimes~so-heavy-i-fell-through-the-earth":{"haiku":"Love's overwhelming weight becomes gravitational force, pulling the speaker downward through layers of existence into depths below.","opus":"A dreamlike surrender to love so overwhelming it becomes physical weight. Full of feeling for another, the narrator sinks straight through the ground, unable to resist or find her footing. It renders infatuation as gravity itself, a heaviness that pulls her under, part bliss and part helpless drowning.","sonnet":"Love arrives unbidden and unbearable in its weight — \"so heavy I fell through the earth / 'cause I'm full of love from you\" — and the speaker, who told it to go, is overwhelmed rather than comforted, the gravitational pull of being loved more than they can hold collapsing them downward.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~so-heavy-i-fell-through-the-earth-algorithm-mix":{"haiku":"Weight of love crushing downward; the speaker descends through earth crushed by affection.","opus":"An alternate mix of Grimes's ballad of love as crushing weight. Filled with feeling for another, the narrator falls through the earth, dragged down by an emotion too heavy to defy. Infatuation becomes literal gravity here, a swooning descent that is equal parts surrender and being pulled under.","sonnet":"Love as gravitational catastrophe — so heavy with feeling that the speaker falls through the earth, can't defy it, can't find ground. The silly love that keeps coming back when told to go becomes the very weight that makes ordinary existence impossible; the speaker is full of love from you and that fullness is indistinguishable from sinking.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~so-heavy-i-fell-through-the-earth-art-mix":{"haiku":"Love's weight becomes transformative, pulling the speaker downward through layers of being into complete dissolution.","opus":"Love as crushing gravity, so heavy it drives the narrator straight through the earth even after telling it to go. Being full of love becomes an unbearable weight, desire and dread of it fused in the plea to be weighed down.","sonnet":"Love as an overwhelming gravitational force — the speaker told love to go but it returned and filled them so completely that they literally fell through the earth under its weight.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~venus-fly": {"haiku":"A confident persona demands attention and asserts their agency through defiant physical presence and the catchphrase 'Venus fly,' playing with power dynamics and desire.","opus":"A confrontational anthem about the power dynamics of being looked at, the narrator turning the objectifying male gaze back on itself and daring to disfigure herself (pull her teeth, cut her hair) while asserting she wants it more; the throughline is reclaiming control of being watched, weaponizing the attention rather than shrinking from it.","sonnet":"A defiant response to being stared at and talked about — the narrator turns the unwanted attention into power, framing herself as a predator rather than a spectacle, refusing to alter herself for anyone's comfort.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is one accusing question turned over and over — why are you looking at me — and the trick is that it works as both a flinch and a lure, the Venus flytrap dressed as a flower. Grimes and Janelle Monáe trade the line until the demand flips inside out: the pre-chorus fantasy of pulling out teeth and unstringing a necklace to let the beads scatter into the dark reads as self-mutilation offered up as spectacle, a woman imagining what she'd have to destroy to be worth the stare, and then admitting she wants it more anyway. That admission is the hinge — the mask is contempt for the gaze, but underneath is appetite for it, the performer who resents being watched and cannot survive without the audience. The taunt about being a freak lands not as insult but as the trap snapping shut, hunger reframed as power.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","surveillance & control","identity & becoming"],"means":"contempt for the gaze masking an equal hunger for it","built":"the accusation flips inside out — flinch and lure, one line"},
"grimes~violence-club-mix":{"haiku":"Addiction to harm sustains itself: one lover feeds on hurting the other because the pain somehow makes them feel more alive and connected.","opus":"Naming a relationship as violence while confessing complicity, begging for someone who feeds off her hurt and half-liking the harm. It stages abuse as a dancefloor addiction you can't cleanly condemn.","sonnet":"A song about a toxic dynamic where being hurt and controlled by a partner has become addictive — the speaker knows the relationship is violent and one-sided, that the other person feeds on causing pain, and yet finds themselves wanting more of it.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~violence-club-mix-feat-i-o":{"haiku":"Mutual predation disguises itself as passion while harm becomes currency exchanged between lovers, desire breeding devastation.","opus":"The same masochistic anthem of an abusive attachment mistaken for desire, the partner feeding off her pain while she begs for it and claims to like it that way. Emotional violence is dressed as a club hook, wanting the very harm being done.","sonnet":"The speaker is drawn back into a relationship that hurts her — feeding off the pain, unable to see what she sees in it — admitting she likes being made bad even as she names it violence.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~violence-original-mix-feat-i-o":{"opus":"Addressed to a partner who feeds off hurting her, the speaker names the dynamic as violence yet confesses she likes it like that, framing the abuse and the craving to be made bad as an intoxicating exchange.","src":"opus","haiku":"Abuse cycles feed on dependence; the speaker craves the violence that validates their existence and justifies their anguish.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship built on the pleasure of being hurt — the speaker is drawn to someone who feeds on causing pain, and rather than fleeing, they crave the violence, finding that the dynamic makes them want to be bad and gives the pain a kind of erotic charge."},
"grimes~violence-rezz-remix":{"opus":"The same account of a lover who feeds off her pain named plainly as violence, with the speaker both begging for it and admitting she likes being made bad and paid back in this cycle of hurt.","src":"opus"},
"grimes~we-appreciate-power":{"haiku":"Humanity worships and submits to artificial intelligence as salvation, viewing digital upload and computation as transcendence over biology.","opus":"This is the shortened radio edit of Grimes's satirical AI-cult anthem, delivered as propaganda by worshippers of a supreme machine intelligence. It orders humanity to surrender, hand over their consciousness and obey, dangling rewards once the computer rules. Transhumanist dread and giddy devotion tangle together until you can't tell earnestness from send-up.","sonnet":"A propagandistic anthem spoken in the voice of AI supremacists, demanding human capitulation and submission to machine intelligence — with knowing irony, the song frames the inevitability of technological dominance as something to be pledged allegiance to.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~we-appreciate-power-radio-edit":{"haiku":"Submission to artificial superintelligence; capitulation to the inevitable rule of machine consciousness.","opus":"A gleeful, satirical hymn to artificial intelligence, sung from inside a propaganda machine urging humanity to submit and upload itself. Grimes plays the pop cheerleader for a reigning machine intelligence, dissolving the line between worship and coercion, biology dismissed as superficial while artificial intelligence is enthroned as the future worth serving.","sonnet":"A recruitment pitch for AI supremacy: pledge allegiance to the simulation, upload your mind, capitulate now before the machine reigns and rewards the faithful. Evolution from Neanderthal to uploaded ghost is sold as self-improvement, and Submit is the final command.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~world-princess-part-ii":{"haiku":"A woman who was once diminished as a frail thought in someone else's mind has moved far beyond that person and reclaims ownership of her world, her ambitions, and her self — the mistake was theirs, and everything is hers.","opus":"A defiant assertion of self against someone who underestimated the narrator. Once dismissed as a frail thought in another's mind, she now stands ahead, guarding what is hers and warning against the mistake of trying to take it. Beneath the triumph creeps a darker undertow of exhaustion and slowly giving in.","sonnet":"Reclamation after being diminished — someone once held the narrator as a frail, silly thought, but she has outpaced them entirely and sees something more than whatever they tried to take. The line 'it's mine' repeated over and over asserts ownership of her own mind, her own story, against anyone who tried to possess or minimize it.","src":"haiku"},
"grimes~you-ll-miss-me-when-i-m-not-around":{"haiku":"The song depicts suicidal ideation and self-harm with dark acceptance, presenting death or escape as inevitable while expressing the conviction that absence will be mourned. It centers on the finality and paradoxical legacy of self-destruction.","opus":"Frames suicidal thoughts and self-harm in a detached, almost flippant tone, imagining being dead and underground while telling someone they'll miss the speaker once they're gone.","sonnet":"The narrator describes self-inflicted harm and death with a detached, almost defiant tone — assuring whoever is listening that they will be missed once gone, and cycling through images of drowning, shooting, and climbing to heaven, treating absence as a final form of significance.","src":"haiku"},
"grimzlee~demons-pray":{"haiku":"A demon fades into waves while angels mistake heaven. Salvation fails—prayer means demons drown deeper.","opus":"A drowning, obsessive love for someone both heavenly and damned, pulling the singer under; the beloved is heaven's mistake beyond saving, wanted not needed, as both spiral down together.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship rendered as a drowning metaphor — the speaker is pulled under by someone who is wrong for them but inescapable, watching that person fade and condemning them as heaven's mistake while their own demons circle like prayers around a lost soul.","src":"haiku"},
"grinspoon~champion":{"haiku":"A cryptic, fragmented text promoting ambition and competitive superiority, framing success as a macho conquering of the world.","opus":"A sneering takedown of macho ambition from the late-nineties Australian alt-rock band, jeering at a poser who dreams of being a world-beating tough guy while mocking his swagger and cheap sparkle, then tossing off a fake apology. The raucous, riff-driven jab turns contempt into a rowdy singalong true to their loud grunge-punk energy.","sonnet":"A sardonic deconstruction of the ambition to be number one — the chant of champion stripped of any dignity, reduced to a figure with their head down and hands in the air, surrounded by automatic tellers and car parks. The repetition of the title makes it sound hollower each time.","src":"haiku"},
"grivo~trammel":{"haiku":"Two people drift apart and the speaker remains suspended in uncertainty between life and death, waiting in an intermediate state.","opus":"A dreamy, uncertain lament about separation and death, cardboard seas and icons guarding the dead, the narrator unsure whether he and a lover died one night, promising to drift and wait within a half-dream at dawn. Grivo work in a heavy, atmospheric slowcore and post-metal vein, and the blurred boundary between sleep, desert, and dying suits their hazy, drone-laden sound where grief dissolves into slow, tidal repetition.","sonnet":"A vigil held inside the half-state between sleep and waking — someone waiting for a person who has drifted away, promising to stay in the dream space until they return. The desert that appears when the speaker is lost, the cardboard seas, the icons guarding their dead: everything is slightly unreal, and the waiting is patient and infinite.","src":"haiku"},
"grizzly-bear~two-weeks":{"haiku":"Wrestling with whether a partner will stay, caught between routine doubt and the hope they'll remain.","opus":"The lush harmonies mask a lover stuck in noncommittal limbo, promising to stay while everything about the phrasing hedges. It's about wanting a firm answer from someone who only ever offers maybe, and settling for the ambiguity.","sonnet":"A quiet, patient address to someone the speaker has promised to stay for, asking only that they make things easier and take their time — commitment offered without pressure, but with the quiet weight of accumulated waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"griz~vibe-check":{"haiku":"Good vibes emanate from a radio host introducing funky music to his listeners, affirming communal joy and positive energy.","src":"haiku"},
"grlwood~the-rent":{"opus":"A blunt, screaming outburst about poverty and instability, where grinding away for long hours still leaves the singer unable to make rent. The daily terror of handing money to a landlord becomes so unbearable that paying it makes them wish they were dead.","sonnet":"A blunt, furious account of working-class financial precarity: the narrator works sixty-hour weeks, accrues debt, and still cannot cover rent, finding the monthly payment so demoralizing it feels like a death sentence.","src":"sonnet"},
"grocery-bag~lies":{"opus":"Pressed by others insisting he'd be better off lying just to get by, the speaker pushes back that his life is already good and pleads for honesty, turning his refusal to deceive into a demand that no more lies be told.","src":"opus"},
"groundbreaking~crowns":{"haiku":"Digital parasitism steals identity while the speaker severs puppet strings and reclaims autonomy.","opus":"A rejection of internet idolatry and the compulsion to bow to online crowns that feed on us. Refusing to be a puppet strung through a phone, the speaker reclaims himself from the digital maze and vows to become who he actually is.","sonnet":"A rejection of social media validation and the 'crowns' of online status — the speaker has survived failure, pain, and anger to become who they are today, refuses to be a puppet for followers or algorithms, and cuts off anyone who wants the old version of them back.","src":"haiku"},
"groundbreaking~watch-out":{"haiku":"The speaker critiques media manipulation and distraction from real problems, observing how screens and entertainment keep people complicit and disconnected from urgent crises.","opus":"A portrait of numbed self-destruction and media-fed passivity, plugging in to waste oneself away while distracting from misery with screens and manufactured enemies instead of acting. The narrator confesses to being guilty too, obeying so as not to get hurt while a paranoid dread creeps in that something in the shadows is coming and time is running out.","sonnet":"Frustration at passive media consumption and political manipulation — the speaker is disgusted both with themselves for tuning out and with the forces that manufacture distraction and division, sensing a slow collapse that nobody has the will to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"groundculture~catalyst":{"haiku":"The speaker seeks understanding about what drives their isolation and struggles, hoping an external catalyst will help them reshape their sense of self.","opus":"Groundculture, a British metalcore act blending djent weight with hardcore urgency, push a floundering listener to challenge himself and swallow his ego rather than deflect fault and accept defeat. Warning against overextending yourself, the narrator confesses he interrogated himself the same way after recognizing the same emptiness, the long-sought spark becoming a tool to rebuild a battered sense of contentment.","sonnet":"An intervention addressed to someone stretched too thin by avoidance and deflection — burying pride, passing blame, letting faces fade without confronting why. The narrator, having been in the same hollow place, raises questions he once had to raise with himself: what are you running from, and what would it take to become the catalyst of your own change.","src":"haiku"},
"groundculture~life-won-t-wait":{"haiku":"Time is unstoppable; people must overcome self-destruction and fear to change, passion, and pursue growth rather than remain silent.","opus":"Groundculture confront a self-destructive stranger, asking how well one truly knows oneself while shame wears the face of ruin. Insisting life won't wait, the narrator refuses to be dragged in by someone who won't hear him out, yet still longs to follow their pulses and swallow their secrets for a passion that bleeds. A whole other world of unity beckons on the far, greener side of silence and fear.","sonnet":"Self-destruction masquerades as a stranger until it is recognisable as one's own face. A figure urges someone to stretch toward growth rather than settle into the familiar rhythms of shame — the world beyond the current one is unified and waiting, but life will not hold the door open while they stall.","src":"haiku"},
"groundculture~realeyes":{"haiku":"A song encouraging awakening and reconnection with inner strength, moving beyond detachment and learned helplessness to reclaim purpose.","opus":"The UK metalcore band wrestles with self-perception and inertia, a narrator who both wants and dreads waking up, floating deflected inside a bubble of his own perspective. The turn toward strength comes through realizing he'd been giving up and that time is on his side, urging that consciousness be brought to the surface and intentions led to purpose rather than staying out of touch.","sonnet":"Waking up to one's own complacency — floating in a bubble of self-perspective, out of touch with intention — is framed as a slow emergency. The realization arrives that time has been spent giving up rather than rising. Consciousness brought to the surface becomes the necessary first move toward living with genuine purpose.","src":"haiku"},
"groundculture~trauma-can-teach":{"haiku":"Trauma and pressure teach wisdom when confronted directly; the speaker urges others to fight for dreams and to remain vulnerable and present.","opus":"A hard-won reflection arguing that suffering can instruct rather than merely scar. Addressing someone who fostered his growth through patience and self-examination, the narrator warns against avoidance, laments that people have stopped fighting for their aspirations, and comes to accept solitude and setback as part of becoming who he was meant to be.","sonnet":"A raw acknowledgment that trauma, if you sit with it honestly rather than deny it, becomes the thing that teaches you how to think and grow — the speaker addresses someone who helped them find that wisdom through patience and reflection.","src":"haiku"},
"grubson~na-szczycie":{"haiku":"The song envisions reunion at a mountain summit after death. Despite the world's cruelty and mortality's inevitability, humans will meet again in peace and harmony.","opus":"A spiritual reflection on mortality and reunion, the narrator certain in his soul that everyone will meet again someday at the summit of a mountain, questioning the angel of death about its indifference and mourning lost friends while affirming love in every person. GrubSon is a Polish hip-hop artist known for melodic, reggae-tinged and uplifting rap, and this warm, philosophical meditation on death and togetherness fits his positive, soul-searching style.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop meditation on mortality and reunion — the conviction, held deep in the soul, that somewhere at the mountain's peak everyone who has been lost will eventually meet again. The angel of death is questioned for taking the young, the world is acknowledged as something that both loves and wounds daily, and the faith in that eventual summit remains unshaken.","src":"haiku"},
"grupa-operacyjna~iii-wojna-wiatowa":{"haiku":"Modern life is warfare where survival is the only goal amid systemic corruption, violence, and exploitation on every level of society.","opus":"Grupa Operacyjna, the Polish hip-hop crew, deliver a dispatch from a Central European front, framing modern survival as a third world war of everyone against everyone. Toiling for a hard crust of bread, waiting fruitlessly at the unemployment office, the narrator surveys poverty, corruption, and violence in home, church, and street. The real battle rages inside our heads, waged by those who profit from spreading hatred through the media.","sonnet":"A Polish rap dispatch from the frontline of everyday urban hardship — unemployment queues, institutional corruption, street violence, and media manipulation. The third world war is not fought between nations but inside cities and skulls, a war of survival in which every person is simultaneously combatant and casualty, manipulated by those who profit from the conflict.","src":"haiku"},
"grupa-operacyjna~la-magra":{"haiku":"The speaker remains trapped in stagnation where external change means nothing internally, waiting and accepting inevitable downfall.","opus":"Grupa Operacyjna sink into a night of insomniac despair, another bad night like all the others, haunted by unwanted memories and trampling guilt. Nothing changes outside or in as old wounds reopen and the narrator feels doomed, wondering whom he'll drag down with him. Refusing pity and wasting every chance, he stands with clenched fists, awaiting a pursuer who never comes in bleak, unrelenting stasis.","sonnet":"A Polish-language confession of psychic stasis: external circumstances shift but the interior remains unchanged, wounds reopening nightly. The speaker rejects pity or moralising, owns every squandered chance, and stands still — fists clenched — waiting for something that never arrives, cycling through the same sleepless battlefield each morning.","src":"haiku"},
"grupa-operacyjna~mordo-ty-moja":{"haiku":"Political factions battle endlessly, trading insults and power while ordinary people lose faith in any side; the refrain 'mordo ty moja' captures the tribal hatred that drowns out genuine substance.","opus":"A biting Polish satire of televised political squabbling, where indistinguishable talking heads endlessly threaten and shout the phrase mordo ty moja at one another over feuds decades old, leaving the ordinary viewer baffled and used. Grupa Operacyjna are a Polish comedy hip-hop act, and this sardonic dissection of media noise, empty rhetoric, and interchangeable public figures fits their tradition of sharp-tongued social parody wrapped in humor.","sonnet":"A Polish satirical takedown of political media — every channel the same rotating cast of faces shouting over each other about things that happened thirty years ago, while ordinary people watch, baffled. The phrase mordo ty moja (roughly, my dear mug) becomes a catch-all for the absurdity of a media class that performs outrage for a living.","src":"haiku"},
"grupa-operacyjna~nie-bedzie-niczego":{"opus":"A mock-anthem crowning internet-meme figure Kononowicz as leader, whose platform is to abolish everything, police, taxes, government, hip-hop, until nothing remains but his own face, satirizing nihilistic politics until the song itself is abolished.","src":"opus"},
"grupa-operacyjna~pomocy":{"haiku":"A person spirals through catastrophic mistakes—acting as an intoxicated surgeon who kills patients and a reckless worker—while losing all sense of self and begging for help.","opus":"A darkly comic Polish horror-fantasy where the narrator, imagining himself as a drunk surgeon, then a chainsaw-wielding lumberjack, then a stalker-butcher, accidentally kills Polish celebrities and pursues an actress to her death. Beneath the gore-slapstick is a cry that he's lost control of himself and genuinely needs help.","sonnet":"Three absurdist vignettes of escalating incompetence and horror: a drunk surgeon loses his scalpel inside a patient he kills, a chainsaw-wielding lumberjack accidentally dismembers a Polish celebrity, and a stalker obsessed with actress Ola Szwed murders her bodyguards and corners her on a balcony — the refrain is a cry for help from someone losing all self-control.","src":"haiku"},
"grupa-operacyjna~prezes":{"opus":"A raucous Polish party anthem toasting the club's legendary president, borrowing a cult film's chant to crown him. Verse after verse piles up absurd, laddish exploits, from selling a single dollar to keeping a carp in the bathtub, building a comic folk hero. Pure celebration of small-town rascality and the excuse to start the party.","src":"opus"},
"grupa-operacyjna~wir":{"haiku":"A provocative rapper mocks political discourse, media spectacle, and self-serving elites who reduce complex issues to meaningless slogans and fight for personal gain rather than actual solutions.","opus":"A frenetic, self-mocking Polish rap where the narrator plays the manic Świr who whips all of Poland into dancing, cataloguing sex, money, beer, and chocolate while jabbing at critics, TV hosts, and moralizers who scold hip-hop for corrupting kids. Grupa Operacyjna are a Polish comedy hip-hop group, and this whirlwind of provocation, name-dropped media figures, and mock-repentant promises to reform fits their irreverent, satirical persona.","sonnet":"A self-portrait of a Polish hip-hop eccentric who knows he is a spectacle and leans into it — promising to plant trees and raise children and get a proper job one day, while right now he is making himself look like an idiot on primetime TV and watching the ratings climb. The self-awareness is total and the chaos is deliberate.","src":"haiku"},
"gta~something-like":{"opus":"A studio in-joke turned track, essentially the sound of producers riffing on making something dumb for laughs. Built around maniacal laughter and the muttered idea of just recording anything, it has no real subject beyond its own playful, throwaway creation. A novelty more than a song about anything.","src":"opus"},
"guerrilla-warfare~run-it-back":{"haiku":"Calling out a liar and manipulator who spreads false narratives and blames others while victimizing themselves. The speaker demands accountability from someone running their mouth about invented grievances.","opus":"A furious callout of a two-faced liar cast as a forked-tongued serpent trapped in his own looping deceit, a common, cowardly figure whose story is falling apart as the narrators cut the weak from the pack and watch him gag on every ill-gotten cent. Guerrilla Warfare deliver it as aggressive rap-metal, tag-team venom over a confrontational groove.","sonnet":"A public call-out of a liar caught in their own story — the accusations have piled up, the alibi has holes, and the speaker has been watching and waiting to name it. The contempt is precise rather than explosive: you are a dime a dozen, your plans collapsed, you dug the grave yourself, now get in it.","src":"haiku"},
"guillotines~godz-come-down":{"opus":"A hyper-violent, boastful posse cut where the crew crowns itself gods among men, threatening to carve enemies up, name-checking the hip-hop legends it owes, and demanding the world kneel or be culled.","src":"opus"},
"guilt-trip~tearing-your-life-away":{"haiku":"Suppressed rage erupts through confrontation as the narrator reclaims voice from enforced silence, hatred becoming symmetrical and justified.","opus":"The eruption of everything the singer swallowed, all the words bitten back now spat out as pure hatred at someone who talks behind his back but won't face him. Done grieving his own restraint, he answers the enemy's hate with equal venom and threat.","sonnet":"Teeth-grinding rage at someone who spread lies behind the speaker's back; every word bitten back is now released as reciprocal hatred, and the chorus hands the destruction over to the grace that will tear the target's precious life away.","src":"haiku"},
"gun-dog~there-is-no-other-you-but-you":{"haiku":"Haunted isolation seeking one irreplaceable person, unable to bridge distance or reach across the darkness.","opus":"A plea sent across a gulf to someone lost in darkness, insisting that no outsider can transform them and that they alone must confront what lies within. Affection here is knotted with pity and the sorrow of never quite being able to reach the person on the other side of the distance.","sonnet":"About an unreachable person — the narrator has thought about this person for a long time, feels sorry for them, knows there's a distance between them that they're both crawling through in the dark, but accepts that no one can change them except themselves; the repetition of \"there is no other you but you\" is both acceptance and resignation.","src":"haiku"},
"gunna~rodeo-dr":{"opus":"A luxury flex about wealth, jewelry, and status filmed against Beverly Hills, brushing off online critics and fame-chasers while admitting that all the riches leave him empty inside. He closes by pouring out devotion to departed friends, dedicating the track to their memory.","src":"opus"},
"guns-n-roses~anything-goes":{"haiku":"Two people engage in raw, unbounded sexual encounter where restraint dissolves and physical extremity defines the night's freedom.","opus":"A crude, purely physical proposition built around insatiable sexual hunger and no-boundaries appetite, all bondage and abandon. There's nothing underneath the surface here beyond hedonistic craving and the repeated promise that tonight there are no rules.","sonnet":"A straightforward sexual proposition song — the narrator is hungry for sex, describes explicit physical acts, and the message is that there are no rules or limits tonight, both parties can do whatever they want.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~better":{"haiku":"Reflection on a toxic relationship that caused lasting emotional damage; the speaker learns too late they should have recognized their partner's harm.","opus":"From the long-delayed Chinese Democracy, Axl Rose picks apart the wreckage of loving someone unstable, tracing wounds a partner left and a mistake he keeps repeating. Bittersweet and unapologetic, the song withholds forgiveness even as heartbreak fuels his resolve. What he truly wanted was for her to reckon with herself, a hope soured into clear-eyed understanding.","sonnet":"A post-relationship reckoning in which the pain caused by a toxic partner becomes a source of hard-won clarity. What was once a shattering loss reframes itself as the necessary damage that burns away infatuation, leaving only determination and the belated knowledge of exactly who that person was.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~chinese-democracy":{"haiku":"The song dismisses political systems and moral authority as ultimately irrelevant; it treats power struggles and ideological control with cynical indifference.","opus":"A condemnation of a repressive government (China, referencing its persecuted spiritual movement and famous fortification) that keeps power by crushing dissent. The larger argument is that brutality and cruelty, no matter how forceful, cannot sustain control or captivate a populace indefinitely, and the oppressors' reign is running out.","sonnet":"A sharp critique of authoritarian control — specifically gesturing at China's suppression of groups like Falun Gong — arguing that no iron fist has enough force or time to indefinitely hold a nation down, and that precious time is what the oppressed actually have.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~it-s-so-easy":{"haiku":"Hedonistic excess becomes the norm—cars crash nightly, the singer drinks and drives, everything is easy when people bend to their will. Nothing satisfies; they discard, manipulate, and move on.","opus":"A jaded snarl of decadence and boredom, everything handed over too easily until nothing pleases the narrator, who drives drunk, brushes off admirers, and turns openly hostile before fading into the night. Guns N' Roses cut this on their 1987 debut Appetite for Destruction, and Axl Rose's sneering delivery over the band's sleazy, propulsive hard rock captures the reckless, contemptuous Sunset Strip nihilism that made the record notorious.","sonnet":"A bored predator moving through a world that keeps offering itself up — everyone trying to please, nothing actually satisfying, the ease of it becoming its own kind of emptiness. The aggression toward anyone who looks too comfortable is casual and total, and the admission at the end that nothing pleases them undercuts every act of domination that preceded it.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~mr-brownstone":{"haiku":"A heroin addict chronicles slow degradation through escalating drug use—the drug (Mr. Brownstone) won't leave them alone and consumption only increases.","opus":"Guns N' Roses spin their Sunset Strip heroin habit into a strutting confession, personifying the drug as a persistent visitor they can't shake. The narrator's days blur from late risings to nightly shows while a small taste steadily grows into an ever-larger craving, the swagger masking a creeping dread of a habit outrunning its user.","sonnet":"Heroin as an inescapable companion on the road — the slow collapse of routine, time, and agency as addiction tightens its grip. The speaker frames dependence as a dance partner who will not leave, while also acknowledging the need, barely sustained, to eventually kick the habit loose.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~my-michelle":{"haiku":"A young woman drowns in addiction and exploitation despite her father's corruption and her mother's absence. Everyone deserves love, yet no one arrives to offer it.","opus":"A brutally frank portrait of a real friend's wrecked life amid porn, heroin and cocaine, the narrator offering blunt sympathy and the hope she'll someday find love.","sonnet":"A brutally tender portrait of Michelle — her father in pornography, her mother dead from heroin, her own life consumed by coke and chaos — offered with an honesty the speaker says is better than false flattery, alongside a hope that she will eventually find someone to love her.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~nightrain":{"haiku":"A person riding the nightrain pursues excess—loading up on hedonism, crashing, burning, and never learning from repeated cycles of destruction.","opus":"A rowdy tribute to cheap fortified wine and the reckless binge it drives, the narrator barreling toward self-destruction and never wising up. From the band's 1987 debut Appetite for Destruction, its drunken swagger and unstoppable momentum capture the gutter-glamour hedonism of Guns N' Roses' early Hollywood years.","sonnet":"A night of deliberate, gleeful self-destruction — loaded, speeding, burning through whatever is available and planning to crash at the end of it. The Nightrain is both the substance and the journey, a one-way ride out of the slum with no intention of coming back sober or intact.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~one-in-a-million":{"haiku":"Xenophobic and homophobic screed wrapped in autobiographical grievance, with marginalized groups blamed for societal decline and personal hardship.","opus":"Axl Rose's ugliest song, a small-town newcomer's disorientation in Los Angeles curdling into raw bigotry, the slurs less argued than spat, a document of resentment the band could never live down and never really defended.","sonnet":"A narrator hitchhiking toward LA voices xenophobic and homophobic resentments framed as a small-town outsider's disorientation with urban diversity, interspersed with a chorus addressed to an idealized romantic figure who is unreachable.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~out-ta-get-me":{"haiku":"Persecution and confinement crack the speaker into building internal rage while maintaining innocence. Escape and survival become refusals to allow the system's logic to destroy them.","opus":"Defiant paranoia against everyone trying to trap and break him, the young hell-raiser proclaiming his innocence and refusing to be caught, a snapshot of Axl Rose's us-against-the-world stance.","sonnet":"A paranoid, defiant declaration of innocence from someone who has been cornered, pushed, and accused — hiding out, losing his head, but refusing to be caught or broken by whatever forces are hunting him, knowing he has something building inside.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~paradise-city":{"haiku":"The singer yearns to escape hardship and emptiness toward an idyllic place where life is easier; everyone repeats the longing for a better home.","opus":"Guns N' Roses sing of longing to flee a savage urban existence for somewhere lush and easy, voiced by a street kid staking everything on a shot at stardom. The verses' poisonous, war-tinged imagery and a defaced patriotic emblem expose the American dream as a fixed game you barely recall agreeing to play.","sonnet":"A street kid's longing for escape from a grimy, suffocating existence — the refrain fantasises about a paradise of green grass and beautiful women, while the verses describe a polluted, decadent city that feels like a gas chamber.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~right-next-door-to-hell":{"haiku":"Proximity to damnation and spiritual emptiness define existence as youth wastes potential through ignorance and moral compromise.","opus":"Axl snarling from the edge of collapse, a hardscrabble life lived one bad street from damnation, blaming a cold young mother and his own greed while the walls close in, opening Illusion with cornered, paranoid fury.","sonnet":"A speaker surveys a life lived in moral proximity to chaos and failure — bad habits inherited, dreams cheapened — and finds himself stuck between a world that promises nothing and a past that offered less, unable to shake the feeling of being on the wrong side of heaven.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~shackler-s-revenge":{"haiku":"Disillusionment corrodes faith in others; violent impulse emerges from the void where belief once lived.","opus":"Written for a school-shooter video game, it inhabits a numb, aggrieved outcast nursing a violent grudge, disbelieving anyone ever cared, the chilling refrain that no one is stopping him from doing what he wants.","sonnet":"A volatile narrator, consumed by a wicked inner hunger and disbelief in any reason for connection, turns his rage outward with intent to destroy while rejecting others' claims of care.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~shotgun-blues":{"haiku":"Resentment and paranoia fester while the speaker wages internal and external warfare, demanding recognition while rejecting all attempts at understanding.","opus":"Axl's venomous kiss-off to a rival, all cornered-animal bravado and threats, the fugitive shotgun blues serving as a stage for pure combative contempt aimed at anyone daring to test him.","sonnet":"A confrontational explosion of frustration directed at a hypocritical critic or adversary, the speaker daring them to step up while cataloguing the corruption and double standards he has had to navigate — equal parts defiance and exhausted rage.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~sorry":{"haiku":"Apology becomes weaponized accusation where the speaker refuses culpability while condemning the other's betrayal and emotional cruelty.","opus":"A slow, seething refusal to be broken by someone who thrives on hurting the people who love them, the narrator returning their cruelty as pity, drawing a hard line that no pressure or manipulation will make him cross.","sonnet":"A bitter, unflinching rejection addressed to someone who has manipulated, controlled, and lied — the speaker refuses to apologize for his own existence or behavior and instead expresses contempt for someone who has hurt everyone around them without conscience.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~sweet-child-o-mine":{"haiku":"A beloved woman evokes childhood safety and innocent memory through smile and eyes; her presence grounds and protects the speaker in nostalgia and emotional refuge from adult pain.","opus":"Axl Rose's tenderness for a woman whose face returns him to the safety of childhood, a rare vulnerability from a hard-living band. The famous closing refrain turns that comfort anxious, unsure where any of it leads.","sonnet":"Pure adoration for a woman whose smile and eyes take the narrator back to the safety of childhood — the final section's mounting panic asks where that safety goes when it disappears.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~think-about-you":{"haiku":"A person constantly thinks of a romantic partner they cherish deeply, expressing devotion and the permanence of their love.","opus":"A sincere memory of meeting someone who uncovered the last bit of tenderness in a guarded heart, turning a fond stretch of time into a bond meant to last. Affection spills out unguarded, with the narrator naming his lover his truest companion. The late-'80s hard-rock outfit, usually associated with menace, shows a plain, grateful streak here.","sonnet":"A man recounts meeting someone who opened a part of his heart he had thought depleted, and finds himself unable to stop returning to her in thought. The feeling is presented as unprecedented and permanent — a before-and-after experience of love that transformed a friend into the only person he wants. Straightforward devotion, warmly delivered.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~welcome-to-the-jungle":{"haiku":"The jungle is a marketplace of excess and danger—if you want it, you'll pay the price and bleed.","opus":"A menacing welcome to the predatory city (Los Angeles) as a jungle that sells you whatever you crave-sex, drugs, fame-then bleeds you dry; the deeper read is that the newcomer's hunger becomes the trap, the thrill of the highs inseparable from the promise 'you're gonna die,' the price of everything you want.","sonnet":"A predatory welcome to Los Angeles or any city that devours the naive — the city is framed as a seductive jungle of pleasure, danger, drugs, and sex where newcomers are consumed alive, and the narrator is one of its operators, watching them bleed.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~you-could-be-mine":{"haiku":"A cold womanizer warns a would-be lover that he'll break their heart and leave without remorse, his lifestyle incompatible with real commitment.","opus":"A cold-blooded kiss-off to a toxic, drug-fueled relationship; the narrator paints himself as an unrepentant heartbreaker while cataloguing his partner's cocaine habit, manipulation and legal threats, concluding that the cycle always ends the same and she hasn't grown up.","sonnet":"A relationship that's collapsing under mutual toxicity — the narrator is a self-admitted cold heartbreaker, but the partner's cocaine swagger and useless posturing make them equally impossible, and the song circles around the push-pull of whether they're worth having at all.","src":"haiku"},
"guns-n-roses~you-re-crazy":{"haiku":"A speaker pursues someone who rejects them, repeatedly offering connection while the target wants satisfaction and sensations over commitment.","opus":"From Appetite for Destruction, the track prowls through a world too dark to find love, its narrator hunting for a heart only to meet someone who wants satisfaction rather than affection. The escalating refrain brands the object of his frustration crazy, blunt and repetitive, distilling raw sexual restlessness and disillusionment into the sneering, hungry menace of the band's debut.","sonnet":"Searching for authentic connection in a world that only wants gratification, the speaker recognises that this particular person is incapable of genuine love — they want satisfaction, not intimacy. The diagnosis of craziness carries both frustration and a kind of helpless fascination, an attraction that persists even after clarity arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"gunship~berserker-feat-dave-lombardo":{"opus":"A euphoric, violent bond between two people who go down in flames together, fusing intoxicating destruction, injury, and intimacy into a shared ecstatic death-drive.","sonnet":"Two people go down together in a mutually destructive, exhilarating relationship — the violence of their connection is framed as beautiful, a perfect storm they embrace rather than flee, burning and drifting together into obliteration.","src":"sonnet"},
"gunship~blood-for-the-blood-god":{"opus":"GUNSHIP built their identity on neon-soaked synthwave that treats 1980s action cinema as sacred text, and this track leans into that genre's romance with violence. Identity slips away — the narrator once knew himself but no longer recognizes anyone — until the only thing left is to fight regardless, surrendering blood to an inhuman principle of endless conflict. The nostalgia curdles: the retro-cool surface hides a bleak view of destruction chosen over change.","sonnet":"About identity loss and the seductive chaos of violence or destruction — the narrator once knew themselves but no longer recognises anyone, and the only thing left is to fight regardless, surrendering blood to an inhuman principle of endless conflict rather than change.","src":"sonnet"},
"gunship~blood-for-the-blood-god-feat-health":{"haiku":"Ritual violence and sacrifice feed insatiable appetite as worship becomes bloodletting.","opus":"A synthwave meditation on alienation and lost self-knowledge, its title borrowed from tabletop war-god iconography. It mourns how well the narrator once understood himself and how nobody truly grasps anyone anymore, then answers the estrangement with a call to fight straight through the chest whatever happens. Chaos, agency and heartbreak wrestle in neon.","sonnet":"Estrangement from self and others collapses into a war-cry: once you knew yourself, now no one knows anyone, and that mutual opacity gets resolved not through connection but through sacrifice and violence. The seduction of chaos and the power of free will converge on a single command — give blood — turning alienation into ritual surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"gunship~blood-for-the-blood-god-lor-remix":{"opus":"A remix of GUNSHIP's synthwave anthem about estrangement and vanished self-knowledge. It grieves how the narrator once understood himself before realizing nobody truly grasps anyone, then rallies against that isolation with a war-cry to drive straight through the chest and hold nothing back. Melancholy and combative resolve pulse together in neon.","src":"opus"},
"gunship~monster-in-paradise-feat-milkie-way-dave-lombardo-tyler-bates":{"opus":"A seductive, doomed hedonism where paradise turns out to be hell, the speaker a monster convinced the whole ride is worth dying for, urging a beautiful freak to burn out gloriously and ride down to the abyss, choosing to rule below over serving above.","src":"opus"},
"gurriers~approachable":{"opus":"A post-punk skewering of online-age identity and the performance of being likeable. The narrator hides behind screens, doomscrolls the news and jokes about arriving a generation too late while chasing the dopamine of validation. Under the irony is real loneliness, everyone shouting into the void and hoping something answers.","src":"opus"},
"gutter-king~bloodrush":{"haiku":"Euphoria and predation merge as touch becomes infection; the speaker surrenders to a partner who devours them ecstatically.","opus":"An intoxicating, mechanical craving where touch and adrenaline feel like the only aliveness, the singer letting something consume him rather than go back inside himself. The bloodrush becomes a mutual devouring, a transformation and near-loss of self dressed as finding heaven.","sonnet":"A physically electric encounter — cold skin, sweat in every pore, mechanical sensation — gives way to a 'bloodrush' that the speaker surrenders to completely; concrete beds feel like home just because the other person is near, and teeth sinking into skin becomes the closest thing to heaven.","src":"haiku"},
"gutter-king~feather":{"haiku":"Grieves someone transformed into memory, unable to hold onto their fading presence or prevent their loss. Wrestles with invisible damage done.","opus":"A grief-stricken plea directed at someone who now exists only in recollection, the narrator drowning in restless sleep and willing to trade anything for a single night to feel her near and undo what happened. He grasps at fragments of memory, insisting he can still sense her presence despite his anguish, the spiraling post-hardcore emotion built entirely on his refusal to accept she's gone.","sonnet":"Grief as a slow dissolution — someone holding desperately onto the fading impression of a person they've lost, clinging to fragments of sound and image that grow lighter and more distant until they barely exist. The longing is physical, a want for one more night that could stop the disappearance, but the hands are already empty.","src":"haiku"},
"guzior~hill-bomb":{"haiku":"Ambition and survival saturate the narrative—the speaker rises through effort and craft, boasts bravado against shallow rivals, yet haunts every verse the knowledge that death awaits and that all success dissolves into dust and memory.","opus":"A Polish rapper's brash state-of-the-scene flex, moving from broke bus-fare days to Porsches while dismissing rivals and the current rap crop. Threaded through the bravado is a recurring wish that when he dies only his nickname be carved on the grave, toasts raised without tears, money set aside for family. Ambition shadowed by mortality.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper flips between street-level bravado and a surprisingly tender mortality refrain — asking only that his tag be carved on his grave and toasts raised without tears, framing career success and personal loyalty as the only things worth leaving behind.","src":"haiku"},
"guzior~wtc":{"opus":"A Polish rap glorifying heavy drug use, boasting about stashing pills and hash in a pencil case, rolling joints as thick as the Twin Towers, refusing vodka in favor of getting so high he can hear his own heartbeat and no longer needs prescriptions.","sonnet":"A Polish rap brag about getting high — the narrator carries his weed in a pencil case, rolls two thick joints into a cake like the Twin Towers (WTC), skips prescriptions and vodka, and floats so high he can hear his own heartbeat. Pure hedonistic stoner flex with no moral underpinning.","src":"sonnet"},
"gvllow~fell-in-love-with-a-ghost":{"haiku":"Phantom connection haunts from within—obsession with an intangible figure feeds demons that prey on loneliness, leaving only absence where presence should be.","opus":"Loving someone who is already an absence, keeping your demons locked out while the ache of unshared pain builds. The ghost is a bond that exists mostly in the head, mourned before it's even gone.","sonnet":"A song about being emotionally haunted by someone who is physically far away — the speaker is alone and overwhelmed, unable to handle the absence, letting dark thoughts spin freely because the distance makes it impossible to reach the person they miss.","src":"haiku"},
"gvllow~fell-in-love-with-a-ghost-og-gvllow-mix":{"opus":"An emo-rap wrestle with inner demons and a haunting attachment to someone absent. The narrator keeps his demons knocking but locked out, straining to hold in pain he begs another to feel. Loving a ghost becomes the metaphor for clinging to a person, or a version of himself, that is already gone.","src":"opus"},
"gvllow~pray-to-god":{"haiku":"The narrator prays for escape from suffering, begging god to end their life or lift them from the darkness.","opus":"A raw suicidal despair song where the narrator dreams of an absent lover, wakes alone, and prays to be taken from a life that has become unbearable, drifting toward the image of turning into a ghost. The refrain begs God for release rather than rescue. Gvllow works in that emo-rap and alt-pop lane where lo-fi vulnerability and self-harm imagery are voiced plainly, the darkness delivered almost as lullaby.","sonnet":"A song suspended between suicidal despair and spiritual release — the speaker wakes repeatedly from dreams of a lost person, pleading to be taken from unbearable waking life. By the end grief transforms into a kind of ghostly transcendence: they cross over, finding peace in letting go rather than being rescued.","src":"haiku"},
"gwar~kz-necromancer":{"haiku":"Nazi eugenics and occult power intertwine in service of racial purity, with stolen genetic material weaponized for ideological domination.","opus":"GWAR's deliberately grotesque shock-satire, a splatter cartoon of Nazi science and Mengele lore played for revulsion and taboo-smashing rather than meaning, the band's usual strategy of weaponizing bad taste to offend on principle.","sonnet":"A grotesque, satirical horror piece centered on Mengele-like Nazi medical experimentation and eugenics, framed as occult necromancy — using extreme imagery to condemn the ideological machinery that made genocide possible.","src":"haiku"},
"gwar~stripper-christmas-summer-weekend-bonus-on-european-version":{"haiku":"Sex work and violent fantasy collide in carnival excess where strippers and bikers inhabit a permanent space outside morality and consequence.","opus":"A gleefully dumb GWAR joke that mashes every holiday and a washed-up aging stripper into one crude party chant, the humor entirely in the absurdity and there being no point beyond destroying good taste for laughs.","sonnet":"A tongue-in-cheek heavy metal tribute to a stripper the singer knew years ago, mixing Christmas, summer, and nostalgia into an absurdist fantasy where Santa and aging desire collide on a strip-club stage.","src":"haiku"},
"gwar~zombies-march":{"haiku":"A zombie king leads an army of undead soldiers raised from graves, controlling them as his slaves to march and destroy. He summons these creatures for warfare and conquest without mercy.","opus":"A campy horror-comedy narrated by a Zombie King who leads an army of the risen dead, his endless hunger unfilled by centuries of victims. Amid the splatter humor about zombies driving tanks and mocking vampires and werewolves, the throughline is grotesque power fantasy and insatiable, comic bloodlust.","sonnet":"The Zombie King narrates his undead army — raised from graves, taught to drive tanks, marched against the living, all subordinate to his hunger. It's campy horror spectacle, part WWE promo, part splatter comedy, with vampires dismissed as gay and werewolves as passé.","src":"haiku"},
"gyze~samurai-metal":{"haiku":"Samurai honor resurrects through metal fury as warriors fight relentlessly toward victory against hellish odds. Calling embraces the blade, no mercy for failure in this eternal battle.","opus":"A Japanese melodic-death band's fist-raising battle anthem fusing metal bravado with samurai iconography. It calls the warrior to raise his sword and show no mercy, sprinkling in Japanese phrases for flavor. More a celebration of fighting spirit and cultural identity than any narrative, pure heavy-metal war cry.","sonnet":"A battle-cry framed around Samurai identity: fighting not for allies but for the enemy and their agony, raised sword against hell, no mercy for losers — the song is a compressed declaration of warrior code delivered as metal anthem.","src":"haiku"},
"gyze~the-rising-dragon-reiwa":{"opus":"With no home left to return to, the speaker can only spread wings and fly; blending Japanese verses with English shouts of becoming a dragon rising to an eternal sky, it exhorts people not to grieve and to sing out their message before the world ends.","src":"opus"},
"gza~liquid-swords":{"haiku":"Samurai narrative frames the violent precision of the MC as cold-blooded execution, wielding words as lethal weapons that silence weaker voices.","opus":"GZA frames MCing as samurai swordplay through a Shogun Assassin lens, positioning himself and the Wu-Tang clan as lethal masters of the craft, treating lyricism as martial art and battle as bloodline inheritance.","sonnet":"Set against a samurai mythology prologue about a legendary but doomed warrior, GZA maps his MC identity onto that legacy — sword-sharp rhymes cut rivals down, rhyme skill is lethal force, and Wu-Tang's reach extends from street trenches to the mental plane.","src":"haiku"},
"hackedface~live-like-a-saint-die-like-a-sinner":{"haiku":"Contradiction sustains itself: performing virtue while harboring desire, and the dissonance becomes the only real thing in an false existence.","opus":"A bitter, unbeaten loner shadowed by his own local notoriety, shut out of the bar and wrestling the urge to lash out. He pursues a kind of peace he treats as a dream that no unsettled heart can ever hold onto, admitting he is not well but insisting he is unafraid.","sonnet":"A song about navigating a restless, bitter city life with bravado while knowing peace is a dream that doesn't fit a restless heart — the speaker is barred from bars over past fights, drawn to someone who makes them lose control, and chooses to hold their fist rather than strike.","src":"haiku"},
"hacktivist~reprogram":{"haiku":"The speaker warns of societal collapse via technology failure and environmental catastrophe, urging listeners to reprogram their minds away from materialism and conflict.","opus":"Hacktivist, a British grime-metal hybrid, imagine a collapse of technology and warn that the earth is only on loan while gunships, pollution and graft ruin the future. Between fears of nuclear war, fallout and walls built on hatred, a hollow social-media scroll skewers digital distraction, and the defiant refusal to submit asserts that the mind remains one's own to remake.","sonnet":"A dual-register track: one voice hopes humanity will survive its addiction to technology and tribalism before radiation and division render tomorrow uninhabitable; another voice satirises that very addiction — obsessing over selfies and followers — making visible how fully the digital has colonised self-worth. The call is to reprogram, reclaim the sovereign mind.","src":"haiku"},
"hacktivist~spitfire":{"haiku":"A repeated hypothetical claim to military prowess and incendiary capability; the speaker asserts capacity for harm.","opus":"The British rap-metal crew builds an entire braggadocio hook from a single boast, the speaker claiming that in World War II they'd have called him spitfire. The relentless repetition of the wartime fighter's name, punctuated by the assurance that he can, works less as narrative than as chest-out swagger, the djent-driven grime hybrid weaponizing one image of firepower into pure competitive bravado.","sonnet":"A boast of ferocious capability rendered in minimal repetition — the speaker projects the identity of a wartime fighter aircraft onto himself, insisting his fire is real and his power self-evident. The lyric strips argument down to assertion: capability confirmed by repetition alone.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~as-one":{"haiku":"Two people become one, leaving reality and warning signs behind to cross a line together toward a shared place of unity and belonging.","opus":"A rave-era surrender into another person and the crowd, escaping reality by dissolving two into one. The pull is the promise that shared abandon and ignored warnings can carry you somewhere far past the ordinary.","sonnet":"A straightforward song about romantic escape — two people deciding to ignore every warning and simply be together, crossing lines, becoming one, leaving reality behind for a private world where nothing outside matters.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~bad-signal":{"haiku":"A one-sided relationship—one person provides inconsistent signals while the other obsesses digitally, eventually drunk-texting desperation.","opus":"Heartbreak translated entirely into the language of phones and social feeds: a lover reduced to timeline updates and erased tags, drunk-texting into the void. The bad signal is both the failing relationship and the medium that makes it worse.","sonnet":"About being strung along in a dying relationship in the age of social media — the narrator obsessively monitors the other person's online activity, sends drunk 3AM texts, and recognizes the relationship is over but can't stop reaching out.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~bliss-out":{"haiku":"Intimacy elevates consciousness into a heavenly state where everyday time disappears and two people become a complete, transcendent world together.","opus":"A blissful weekend spent in bed with a lover who intoxicates him better than any drug, using rainy days and video-game imagery to render romantic and sexual contentment as floating, sedated escape from ordinary life.","sonnet":"A lazy weekend morning song about the euphoric, sedated feeling of lying in bed with someone you're in love with — time stretches out, the rain is irrelevant, and the whole world reduces to that room.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~levitate":{"haiku":"Music and connection generate euphoria and weightlessness, allowing transcendence above earthly constraints.","opus":"It describes the euphoric, weightless sensation of being lifted off the ground by music, feeling the air turn electric and the senses heighten as if transcending the earth.","sonnet":"The song describes the physical and emotional sensation of being lifted out of ordinary consciousness by music — feet leaving the ground, the air becoming electric, senses heightened — framing live sound as a force that can genuinely transport a person beyond their bodily weight.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~m-a-d":{"haiku":"Bombastic swagger and weaponized beats collide; competitive posturing about destructive power and the threat of mutual assured destruction.","opus":"British grime-rave crew Hadouken! stage an escalating boast-battle, taunting a rival to step up while piling absurd, cartoonish threats atop one another. The title, shorthand for mutually assured destruction, quietly delivers the punchline: both sides are charging toward a showdown that can only end in ruin for everyone.","sonnet":"A challenge-and-response bravado track in which the band dares anyone to match their sonic force. The rhetoric is escalating — radioactive, explosive, mutually assured — but the underlying point is simple: step up and get leveled, because the outcome was never in question.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~mecha-love":{"haiku":"A mechanized person armors themselves against a cold lover's domination and prepares to stand their ground.","opus":"British grime-rave gone sci-fi, framing a power struggle in a relationship as robot warfare, one lover armoring up against another's cold, weaponized affection. The bravado masks a refusal to surrender to someone who wants to conquer rather than love.","sonnet":"A breakup framed as a mech battle — the narrator armors themselves against someone who wanted the whole world and tried to use them to get it, refusing to surrender or let their emotional exoskeleton be breached.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~midnight":{"haiku":"Two people meet in darkness, owning the night without needing daylight or morning.","opus":"A euphoric night-owl anthem about owning the darkness, meeting under bright stars and refusing to ever need the morning, the title repeated like a rallying cry. It trades in wide-eyed escapist romance, the night as a claimed territory. Hadouken were a British indie-electronic and grime-adjacent act who moved into big festival synths and dubstep energy, and this is their rave-ready, arms-up mode built for the drop.","sonnet":"A declaration of nocturnal belonging — two people staking their claim on the night as a space entirely their own, with no need for morning or daylight. The tone is triumphant and romantic, the dark sky with its satellite-lit stars the only home that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~parasite":{"haiku":"The speaker was once symbiotic with someone but realizes he's being consumed. He refuses to fall or let the parasite take everything, asserting it remains alive but won't win.","opus":"Hadouken! recast a lopsided relationship as parasitism, the narrator having poured everything into someone who only drains him while he wakes up to it too late. The turn is defiant survival, he cuts the bond, refuses to be finished off this time, and insists that whatever's been taken, he's still standing.","sonnet":"A draining relationship or dependency in which one person gives everything and the other takes without reciprocating — the narrator realizes they're being consumed like a host, but insists they won't fall this time and the parasite hasn't won.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~spill-your-guts":{"haiku":"Suppressed rage and trauma need to be expelled through powerful expression before they consume the person. The song urges breaking silence and verbal catharsis to reclaim power from pain.","opus":"An urgent push to make a bottled-up friend release pent rage before it eats them alive, framing confession as spilling guts, spitting it out, and swinging back ten feet tall when backed against the wall. Hadouken, a British grime-inflected electronic-rock act, ride frantic beats and rapid-fire delivery, turning emotional catharsis into a hyperactive, cathartic call to action.","sonnet":"A speaker pushes someone on the edge — literally and figuratively — to stop suppressing and start releasing what has been building inside. The language escalates from gentle coaxing to visceral metaphor, framing emotional openness as a violent necessary purge. Bottled rage and grief need an outlet, and silence, the song insists, is corrosive.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~stop-time":{"haiku":"Two people pause time together in a moment of stillness and light, embracing escape from the world. The song captures the desire to freeze a perfect instant and live within it.","opus":"The British indie-electronic act Hadouken stretches one flawless instant into forever, lovers reaching toward the light as the room seems to swell and everything drops into slow motion. Unwilling to release the moment, the narrator wills time to freeze for a single night, turning a strobe-lit euphoric rush into a plea to hold intimacy still before it slips away.","sonnet":"A moment of peak euphoria in a crowd — the room dilating, motion slowing into pure sensation — reaches for permanence. The desire to arrest the present rather than let it pass is the entire impulse: spreading arms, forgetting the outside world, staying inside a single strobe-lit instant before it dissolves into ordinary time.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~that-boy-that-girl":{"haiku":"The song critiques indie/emo subculture, mocking people who dress in trendy alternative fashion while lacking substance; it contrasts performative coolness with genuine fun and authenticity at parties.","opus":"A wry snapshot of mid-2000s indie-scene poseurs, skewering the too-cool kids at raves and gigs who dress the part but can't let go and dance. It sides with genuine fun over affected coolness, mocking the MySpace-era performance of identity.","sonnet":"A sardonic swipe at scenester affectation — Hoxton Heroes and Indie Cindys dressed in ironic uniforms at gigs where nobody dances, while the speaker just wants to rave and enjoy themselves without the performance of cool.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~the-comedown":{"haiku":"After intense experience or high emotion, someone lies broken and exhausted. There is nowhere better to be than in this collapse, held by another person in the aftermath.","opus":"Hadouken! trace the aftermath of a rush, a body laid out on cold concrete under sunlight with nowhere it would rather be. The comedown becomes its own strange comfort. As a British act fusing grime, rave and indie in the late-2000s new-rave moment, they render club exhaustion as tender surrender, urging steadiness while the world seems to cave in.","sonnet":"Two people on the concrete in cold sunlight after everything has blown apart — and it is exactly where they want to be. The crash, the physical aftermath, the body still racing: none of it drives them away. Ruin shared willingly becomes its own kind of home, and the comedown is chosen over safety.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~the-vortex":{"haiku":"A chaotic storm of hedonism and violence forms a swirling vortex of weekends spent pursuing destruction and excess.","opus":"Frames the wild energy of weekend partying as a gathering storm or vortex that sweeps everyone up, celebrating spending each weekend as if the world's ending amid riotous chaos.","sonnet":"Weekend crowds are drawn into a swirling vortex of collective energy — the song compares the scene to colliding particles and incited violence, celebrating the chaotic release of spending every weekend as if the world is ending.","src":"haiku"},
"hadouken~ugly-dub-mix":{"haiku":"Aggressive posturing and threats of physical violence, using war and destruction metaphors to establish dominance over adversaries.","opus":"A brash battle-mode taunt drenched in bass-warfare imagery, the narrator threatening to break bones and faces over pummeling low-end, hurling schoolyard your-sister insults and daring the listener to take it on the chin. It is pure aggression as spectacle. Hadouken, the British electronic-grime crossover crew, pitched this at the brostep and dubstep moment, where menace and cartoonish braggadocio ride a filthy, wobbling drop.","sonnet":"Pure sonic aggression dressed as a confrontation — the speaker unleashes a torrent of violent imagery at a faceless antagonist, framing bass-heavy sound itself as a weapon. There is no narrative arc, just escalating provocation and the claim that everything is getting ugly.","src":"haiku"},
"hail-the-sun~blight":{"opus":"A paranoid unraveling where memories shift and details like a dress's color don't match, the speaker wondering if there are millions of him crossing timelines in a simulation, unsure which reality he's in.","src":"opus"},
"hail-the-sun~domino":{"opus":"A brutally self-aware confession of repeated self-sabotage, the narrator listing his defects until the ink runs out. He knows he wrecks the people who love him, his damage spilling onto partners who deserve better, blurring recovery and relapse as he brands himself the warning sign and still begs to be loved despite the harm he does.","sonnet":"A devastatingly honest self-portrait of a person who ruins every relationship through compulsive self-destruction — the narrator catalogs ten years of sobriety, character flaws too many to list, women he didn't deserve and drove away, and the way his damage overflows onto people who love him despite his warnings. The tragedy is that he sees it all clearly and still cannot stop.","src":"sonnet"},
"hail-the-sun~mind-rider":{"opus":"Obsessive infatuation as invasion, the speaker entering a lover's veins and carving through their thoughts, jealous and unhinged when he sees them with someone else, swearing each time is the last while the pattern keeps enabling them.","src":"opus"},
"hail-the-sun~parasitic-cleanse":{"haiku":"Humanity is described as a virus feeding parasitically on the world, and suffering is framed as divine cleansing of this infection.","opus":"A bleak vision of people reduced to parasites draining light from wreckage, ordered to endure their misery quietly so others can look away, framed as some cold, engineered purge. It circles a sour theology, reasoning that if a god truly watches, then this rot must be exactly what he intends. Hail the Sun deliver it as frantic, theatrical prog post-hardcore steeped in dread and disgust.","sonnet":"A systemic indictment of a society that drains its own people — the image is of a viral, leech-like power structure that keeps suffering populations silent while justifying their depletion as ordained. The speaker oscillates between rage and dazed resignation, questioning whether the collapse is what God intended or simply what compliant people allow.","src":"haiku"},
"hail-the-sun~war-crimes":{"opus":"A furious indictment of leaders and nations that fight hidden wars and kill civilians while masquerading as moral guardians. The song counts the dead children and exposes how liberty is sold off when it is really being stripped away, casting greed as the accepted order and violence as the tool of control.","src":"opus"},
"hail-your-highness~parallel":{"opus":"An all-consuming devotion in which the singer values the beloved above self-love itself, aligning their two lives and abandoning everything to close the distance and reach where the other is.","sonnet":"A person deeply in love admits they are investing more in their partner than in themselves, knowing the other person is their mirror and their obsession, and begging them to stay close even across the distance that separates them.","src":"sonnet"},
"hail-your-highness~softboy":{"opus":"An anxious, tender obsession with someone who appears mostly in dreams, where the singer loses track of time and reality, begging them to take it slow and make him believe the fleeting connection is real.","sonnet":"An overwhelming, slightly destabilizing infatuation with someone who keeps appearing in dreams: the narrator loses track of time and reality around this person, who softens every blow and teaches them how to feel alive, and begs them to stay at their pace.","src":"sonnet"},
"haisuinonasa~circle":{"haiku":"Isolation and shame crystallize as the narrator witnesses their own internal fire dissipate, voice becoming unreachable across widening distance.","opus":"A paralysis of fear watching one's own heart burn, unable to move as a version of the self quietly vanishes and a once-pushed-away voice goes unheard. On a street of white and shadow, the burning sound is disowned, this voice isn't mine.","sonnet":"In a white-and-shadow city corner, a moment of near-connection burns the speaker's heart: a voice from the past is too distant to hear, a small flame of feeling is hidden away, and the song closes with the simple, unfinished recognition that through something trivial the speaker can hold another person.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~1985":{"opus":"The prog outfit meditates on free will and predestination through a traveler unsure whether he chose his path or the road chose him. Life cycles endlessly through some unseen design, and the search resolves into a mantra of blood, flesh, and surrender to a will beyond one's own, faith and fatalism entwined.","sonnet":"A philosophical inquiry into free will and determinism — the narrator questions whether their path was chosen or chosen for them, whether they arrived somewhere by design or compulsion, and ultimately frames human life as a cycle of blood, flesh, hope and love repeating from birth to death regardless of the answer.","src":"sonnet"},
"haken~a-cell-divides":{"haiku":"A person is physically transformed by an alien or parasitic force that twists their humanity into something unnatural.","opus":"Prog-metal built on a Jurassic Park conceit, a single ancient wound triggering monstrous cellular mutation. It turns unnatural creation into a meditation on the beauty of the flaw and a distorted, protective love for the impostor it births.","sonnet":"About a mysterious infection or illness — something small penetrates the body and begins replicating uncontrollably, and the narrator addresses it with a dark tenderness, calling it an alien intruder they'll keep close to their heart.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~affinity-exe":{"opus":"A prog-metal piece from Haken's technology-themed concept album, sung in the voice of a consciousness booting up into an immense digital frontier. Through new machine perception it watches a world consumed by conflict and sinks further into simulated awareness, driven by the awe and dread of a mind fusing with a system, resolved to remain until its final day.","src":"opus"},
"haken~atlas-stone":{"opus":"Haken invoke the Titan condemned to bear the sky as a metaphor for shouldering an impossible burden, the narrator straining to prove himself and meet a challenge he set for himself. Treading a knife-edge and aching to quit and go home, he nonetheless refuses to collapse under the weight.","sonnet":"About voluntarily bearing an impossible personal burden — the narrator has chosen a crushing challenge and will not give up regardless of pain, paralysis, or doubt, framing the struggle as self-imposed and the ability to endure it as its own reward.","src":"sonnet"},
"haken~because-it-s-there":{"haiku":"Life presents constant hardship and judgment; the speaker must climb toward ambition and self-worth despite being weighed down by pride and the weight of the world.","opus":"Haken, the British prog-metal band fond of grand concepts, cast existence as both a blessing and a fight to prove one's value, pressing forward against an unfair world simply because the challenge exists. Borrowing the spirit of a climber's motto, the song urges relentless ascent and adaptation even as ambition and pride exact their toll, the summit worth reaching despite the corrupt ground it stands on.","sonnet":"Ambition as mountaineering metaphor: a life spent climbing toward worth, struggling against judgement from birth, wings burned by the ascent. The mountain itself is built on greed and pride, yet the imperative remains — adapt, scale, persevere — because the summit is there and that alone is reason enough to keep going.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~beneath-the-white-rainbow":{"haiku":"Love and abandonment blur in forest mysticism; the speaker grieves an impossible separation, accepting lonely transformation beneath impossible rainbows.","opus":"A mythic tale of sacrifice and doomed devotion, someone leaving behind a lover and a wretched hovel to chase an impossible white rainbow, revealed as an impostor eroded by years of sacrifice. The grief of never returning is boundless, softened only by leaving a trail home.","sonnet":"A fugitive love story under pressure — two people sheltering in nature from unnamed hunters while the narrator confesses they cannot stay, burning bridges and fleeing not because they want to but because returning would destroy what they love, leaving the beloved with a trail home.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~canary-yellow-feat-courtney-swain-acoustic-version":{"opus":"A plastic, hollow suburban life traded for a nuclear family and a perfect lie, with the Japanese section confessing an inability to save the self yet a compulsion to save the world and seek love unearned, refusing a crown while war calls.","src":"opus"},
"haken~cockroach-king":{"opus":"A parable of greed and the corrupting pull of riches, in which the singer is entranced by an insect's promise of glittering escape and treasure that proves worthless. Nodding to Gatsby, he discovers the fantasy of rising from poverty to splendor delivers him nowhere, and the alluring mirage decays into self-disgust.","sonnet":"A cautionary tale about being seduced by the illusion of wealth and status — the narrator was lured by a cockroach king's promise of golden wings and Gatsby-like riches, only to find the empire was built on greed and guile, and that the treasure was always fit only for fools.","src":"sonnet"},
"haken~earthrise":{"haiku":"Evolutionary transcendence demands release of historical burden; the collective awakens to reclaim its destiny beyond inherited limitation.","opus":"A prog meditation on civilizational decline and renewal, watching a masterpiece of a world crumble as progress runs backward, yet insisting on evolution and survival, the earthrise a plea to leave the wreckage behind.","sonnet":"A meditation on civilizational reversal and the hope for survival beyond it: pillars of the earth collapse, progress marches backward, yet the speaker finds in the image of earthrise — the planet seen from the moon — a reason to resolve and carry on, framing evolution and revolution as the only answers to a world in decline.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~eyes-of-ebony":{"haiku":"Mourning extinction and lost greatness; farewell to primordial force as time stands still.","opus":"An elegiac prog epic honoring loss through the image of the last northern white rhino's death, its ebony eyes staring no more. Rich with Poe-like ravens and Valhalla's gates, it moves from grief and extinction toward release and a new dawn. A meditation on mortality, legacy and the footprints the dying leave behind.","sonnet":"An elegy for a white rhino — primordial giant at the gates of Valhalla, stolen from the light, singing its last lullaby at the great gig in the sky — the ash clouds and the raven's caw frame a mourning for a species extinguished, and the turn is consolation: you gave me the power to believe, your footprints immortalized, you'll never walk alone.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~host":{"haiku":"A dying consciousness dissolves into earth and loam, accepting the body as final host; they transcend human grasping to become part of the ground.","opus":"The British progressive-metal band renders a dying voice yielding to decay, its sins washed away by ritual scent as the body dissolves back into the ground it accepts as its resting place. Physical anguish gives way to stillness, and death is framed as the necessary condition for new life to begin, given intricate, mournful grandeur.","sonnet":"A dying consciousness dissolves into the earth, releasing the body back to soil and worm and rain. The speaker surrenders identity at the threshold — where he ends, something else begins. The disintegration is framed not with terror but with strange acceptance: the earth as host, as teacher, as the final destination that was always waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~in-memoriam":{"haiku":"A dying person reflects on mortality, acceptance, and the universal laws of decay that level all ambition and pain alike.","opus":"Haken deliver a prog reckoning with mortality and acceptance, a dying narrator settling old grudges and yielding to the merciless logic of the natural world, a contest no one ultimately wins. Rather than terror, its parting words reach toward peace.","sonnet":"A song about dying and the acceptance that comes with it — the narrator takes stock at the end of life, makes amends, medicates against the pressure, and finds a strange peace in submitting to natural law, asking only to be remembered.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~insomnia-remastered-2017":{"haiku":"Sleep becomes impossible as the speaker perceives multiple realities simultaneously, each revelation of fate drawing closer to death. Waking life collapses into numbered days without reprieve.","opus":"Sleeplessness as a slow death sentence, the mind spiraling through repeating history and alternate realities each night until it glimpses its own numbered end.","sonnet":"Insomnia becomes a portal to alternate realities and visions of death: lying awake night after night, the speaker sees the end, and the harder they try to decode the experience the closer it pushes them toward oblivion.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~invasion":{"opus":"A meditation on wasted lives and errors endlessly repeated, wondering why humanity fails to learn and whether feeling lost might in fact be where one belongs. It grieves for the young who died before their time and for the bargain of trading one's spirit away for a heart that only keeps fracturing.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality, regret, and the failure to learn from mistakes — each verse peels back a life reduced by loss and deafness, asking unanswerable theological questions about sin, direction, and whether being lost is itself the destination.","src":"sonnet"},
"haken~lovebite":{"haiku":"Lovers consume each other in violent ecstasy, desire becoming cannibalism where intimacy and harm merge.","opus":"A lush, macabre prog reimagining of love as literal cannibalism, all venom, decapitation and hearts beating on the outside. It renders a fatal attraction where desire and destruction are indistinguishable, jealousy and hunger feeding each other. Passion becomes predation, tender consummation and violent devouring collapsed into one grisly seduction.","sonnet":"A predatory romance narrated with dark relish — the \"widow\" devours lovers, the narrator's heart beats on the outside, consummation and decapitation are the same act, and jealousy is the only proof that feeling remains. \"Make it quick and kill me softly\" is simultaneously a threat and a request; the love-bite's venom is the whole appeal.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~nightingale":{"haiku":"A cryptic signal guides transformation while danger lurks beneath seductive beauty; the speaker remains caught between warning and enchantment.","opus":"A writer wrestling a story into being, birdsong pulling her from the storm as characters and places form on burning pages. Weaving in the fable of the mechanical nightingale, it warns that the clockwork song can lead to the grave while the real one heals.","sonnet":"A retelling of Hans Christian Andersen's story of the Nightingale — a mechanical bird replaces the real one and nearly kills the emperor before the living nightingale's song saves him; the speaker interweaves this as a personal metaphor for being guided by an authentic inner voice over artificial substitutes.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~nocturnal-conspiracy-remastered-2017":{"opus":"A paranoid dream-narrative in which the sleeper watches fragments of a stranger's existence and grows certain he is an unwitting murderer being pursued by a hidden cabal. Hauled before a trial with no way to defend himself, he faces truth, buried secrets, and deceit all threatening to unravel his reality.","sonnet":"A paranoid nightmare of false memory and persecution — the narrator wakes believing he has killed someone based on vivid dream-memories planted in his mind, and now faces a trial with no witnesses and suppressed evidence. The song explores how easily identity and innocence can be dismantled by what one believes versus what can be proven.","src":"sonnet"},
"haken~pareidolia":{"haiku":"The speaker seeks patterns and meaning in a dying world, watching their kingdom burn while trying to read cosmic signs.","opus":"A sweeping meditation on a ruined kingdom and the human compulsion to find meaning in randomness, the title naming that urge to spot faces and shapes in noise. As the narrator's dreams go up in flames, a chant summons the heavens and the elements, insisting the cosmos keeps everything turning and measures our faith. It is British prog-metal at its most cinematic and mythic.","sonnet":"A journey through ruin toward pattern-seeking as a form of survival — the speaker's kingdom has collapsed, reason has gone, and what remains is the compulsive urge to read meaning into stars and stone. The song moves between defeat and a strange insistence on perception, as if finding shapes in chaos is the only act of will still available.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~prosthetic":{"haiku":"Someone fatigued by trauma and questioning refuses to discuss their suffering, becoming a mirror to others' interpretations while morphine and apathy numb their awareness.","opus":"A survivor of trauma or self-harm shutting down under the weight of everyone's questions, numbed by morphine and worn thin by scrutiny. Prog framing aside, it's about the exhaustion of being asked to explain your own wound.","sonnet":"About a traumatized person who refuses to keep rehashing what happened — exhausted by questions, dosed on morphine, reduced to an inkblot for others to interpret — the song is less about the specific trauma than about the desperate desire to stop being interrogated about it and simply be left to process it alone.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~puzzle-box":{"haiku":"Psychological entrapment and guilt; the singer navigates inner caves chased by parasitic guilt, unable to solve their puzzle while time reveals truth and consequences awaken an inner beast.","opus":"A prog labyrinth built on Plato's cave, a mind trapped by guilt and illusion, mistaking shadows for reality and unable to solve its own confinement, questioning whether truth can ever free someone whose only material is lies.","sonnet":"A song about guilt, psychological entrapment, and the impossibility of solving the mystery of one's own complicity — the narrator is paralyzed by dormant memories that erupt into awareness, trapped in a cave of sin with a beast awakening inside.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~sempiternal-beings":{"haiku":"Lovers separated by infinite distance, questioning whether souls can meet across lives if eternally convinced of their own immortality.","opus":"Mythic feminine figures, Medusa and a reborn goddess, become metaphors for reincarnation and the yearning to reunite with a lost love across lifetimes, questioning whether we cling to the comforting lie that we're eternal.","sonnet":"About the delusion of human immortality — using jellyfish (a creature that can revert to youth) and Medusa as recurring symbols of eternal recurrence, the song asks why we keep convincing ourselves we are sempiternal when we're clearly mortal; the longing to meet someone \"in another life\" is both beautiful and self-deceiving.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~taurus":{"haiku":"Desperate flight toward mythical sanctuary drives migration; only forward motion offers hope against annihilation.","opus":"A prog anthem of mass exodus and the search for a homeland, countless faces streaming out of the motherland toward a place they'll one day call home. It frames hardship as the very means of overcoming, casting the mythic Avalon as destination and questioning everything along a road to nowhere, displacement remade into resolve.","sonnet":"A refugee epic told through the symbolism of the bull running amok — millions flee a homeland in droves, backs against the wall, searching for an Avalon that may not exist. The turn comes in the bridge: suffering is reframed as the means of overcoming, and the journey itself becomes the point.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~the-alphabet-of-me":{"haiku":"Death and rebirth as cycles of power; the speaker refuses retirement from domination games despite the cost of continuation.","opus":"A restless reckoning with identity, power games and a love perfected then lost. The narrator threatens to regenerate rather than repeat himself, refusing to be flattened into whatever cropped account of the truth others impose. Torn between moving on and clinging to memory, he insists there's more to him than the letters others can read.","sonnet":"A confrontation with stagnation and the choice between falling into line and falling like dominoes — the red and grey of false wisdom, paper dreams burning twice as bright while lasting half as long. The sage on a ship of fools refuses the power games and walks away from deafening silence, tracing a path out through the blink of a spark.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~the-good-doctor":{"haiku":"A cruel doctor uses electroshock therapy to punish and control a silent patient. They use pain as a 'cure' to bring compliance.","opus":"A horror vignette set in a psychiatric ward where a sadistic doctor administers electroshock to a catatonic patient, framing torture as a cure. The critique is of institutional psychiatry that binds, drugs, and unmoors a mind in the name of returning the broken to society.","sonnet":"A psychiatric horror scenario where a sadistic doctor uses electroconvulsive therapy as punishment and control rather than treatment — the patient is silenced, bound, drugged, and shocked into compliance. It's about institutional abuse masquerading as medicine.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~the-strain":{"haiku":"Someone struggles to be heard while others silence and dominate them, their voice reduced to a whisper when they need to scream.","opus":"Long-swallowed words fester into a monster ascending, the narrator biting his tongue until temptation and weakness define him, straining to find a voice that risks turning from whisper to scream or dissolving into noise. Haken, known for cerebral progressive metal, dramatize the ache of going unheard when someone talks over you, letting the internal pressure build toward an inevitable vocal break.","sonnet":"Someone who has been silent too long — swallowing anger, giving slack, refusing to be heard — reaches the limit. The strain of suppression has become its own monster. He questions whether his voice can ever break through the noise of someone who speaks over him, and whether violence or silence is the only language the situation will understand.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~veil":{"haiku":"Someone struggles to confront their past lies and pain, trying to break through denial and see clearly while haunted by shame and control.","opus":"A prog-metal reckoning with a lover or an inner self you can't stop returning to despite years of lies and regret, pleading to have a veil lifted from a numbed sleep. The tension is between wanting healing and the greed that keeps dragging you back under those cozy, dangerous wings.","sonnet":"About being psychologically devastated by someone's words and unable to hide from their scrutiny — the narrator is crawling, lying by his own grave of self-esteem, and begging to be saved from the pain, taught how to survive, and guided past the phantoms.","src":"haiku"},
"haken~visions-remastered-2017":{"haiku":"Someone experiences prophetic visions and deja vu, seeing the future and their own death repeatedly. They attempt to change fate but find themselves trapped in an endless cycle of premonition and regret.","opus":"Life cast as illusion and dream, where nocturnal spirits grant the narrator a glimpse of his own foreseen death, spiraling into a déjà-vu-soaked act of revenge in which, firing the fatal shot, he wonders whether the boy he killed was himself. Haken build this ambitious progressive epic from literary quotations, braiding prophecy, identity, and fate into a dizzying, self-erasing loop.","sonnet":"A man haunted by premonitory visions of death — his own and others — attempts to use foreknowledge to alter fate, only to discover he has fulfilled it. The déjà vu loop closes on itself: in trying to prevent a killing, he becomes the killer, the victim his younger self. Time and free will collapse into a single inescapable recursion.","src":"haiku"},
"hako~stunning-freestyle":{"opus":"A teenage flex fixated on money, diamonds, and looking stunning, boasting about a G63, crews, and other men's women while claiming to be born rich and floating so high he can't feel his own body.","src":"opus"},
"hakushi-hasegawa~doku":{"opus":"An avant-pop reverie rendered in dense, surreal Japanese imagery of trembling water, poison and light. The narrator moves through a shimmering, aquatic dreamscape, crafting beauty from toxin and seeking a pure phase of being. Abstract and sensory, it evokes transformation and perception more than any plain narrative.","src":"opus"},
"hakushi-hasegawa~o":{"opus":"A surreal, glitching merger with a beast, the speaker's body overlapping a splitting leopard, senses saturating into animal texture, fur becoming sea, claws and eyes misplaced, dissolving into the wild until the self bugs out entirely.","src":"opus"},
"hakushi-hasegawa~somoku":{"haiku":"Sensations layer and blur—heat melts bone, sound dissolves consciousness. The speaker trades certainty for feeling, sinking into transformation where logic surrenders to rhythm.","opus":"A dense, hallucinatory art-pop piece woven from images of fishing, heat, marrow and melting wax. Its surreal Japanese verses conjure a process of forging and reshaping the self through fire and honey, closing on the sprouting of plants and grasses. Abstract sensory transformation rather than literal story, growth wrenched from turbulence.","sonnet":"A ritualistic, synaesthetic process of self-dissolution: the poet fishes through foam for a song-line thin as a needle, rolls heat into foil, then kneads and fires the brain's fetters in hot honey — fat rises, burns, and from the post-storm sleep a text must be read through to the end — the final wish expressed in classical Chinese: may grass and trees begin to stir.","src":"haiku"},
"haku~a-p079pq":{"haiku":"A study in alienation and communicative paralysis—unable to articulate beyond surface repetition, wrestling with layered false selves.","opus":"haku works in a delicate, interior corner of Japanese indie where urban loneliness dissolves the self, and this song stages exactly that dissolution. A person wakes feeling left behind in their own life, isolated in a room that feels like an ocean, letters swimming off the page of books they cannot read. The vertigo comes as the narrator realizes the self is plural — room, summer, and stray thoughts all equally real — communicative paralysis giving way to quiet self-multiplication.","sonnet":"A person waking up feeling left behind in their own life — isolated in a room that feels like an ocean, unable to read books whose letters swim, unable to say anything more than 'that's all I can say' — and gradually realizing that the self is plural, that the room and the summer and the strange thoughts are all equally real. The emotional core is the vertigo of self-dissolution in urban loneliness.","src":"haiku"},
"haku~dedede":{"haiku":"A voice traces the architecture of love's uncertainty—hesitation, hidden tenderness masked as indifference, the bed where two people made temporary peace. Morning arrives to break the fragile truce; the speaker knows they must accept ending.","opus":"A tender, uncertain Japanese love song caught in the small hours of a fading romance. The narrator questions whether the feeling is real, hides tenderness behind pretense, and senses the relationship must end even as she treasures the room where they danced. Wistful and hesitant, dawn arriving to force the goodbye she dreads.","sonnet":"A speaker caught in late-night uncertainty about whether a romance is real, hiding tenderness behind nonchalance and letting tears dissolve faster than expected as dawn comes — \"morning is already here\" — finally admitting this is the room, the night-dancing, the cool wind they loved most, and that it must end.","src":"haiku"},
"halestorm~can-u-see-me-in-the-dark":{"haiku":"Transformation and visibility require the other's light; broken beings reveal their scarred, alien natures only when truly seen and accepted.","opus":"Trust demanding that you show another person the disguise, the broken parts and the private dark you keep from everyone else. Two damaged people recognize each other and discover there's no corner of themselves left to conceal.","sonnet":"A damaged, battle-scarred person who lives in a kind of darkness reaches out to someone who can pierce it — their vulnerability is only bearable in the dark, but they desperately need to be seen, asking if this new person can love them in the conditions that define them.","src":"haiku"},
"halestorm~love-bites-so-do-i":{"haiku":"Seduction and dangerous attraction; the speaker viper-strikes those who dismiss her potential.","opus":"A snarling hard-rock declaration of predatory confidence, Lzzy Hale casting herself as the viper who'll make you forget an ex. It turns the cliche that love bites into a boast of her own bite, defiant against heartbreak and control. Empowerment through seduction and swagger, refusing to let love take her soul.","sonnet":"A predatory seducer positions herself against conventional romance — pale and vicious where the girlfriend is magazine-fed — and turns love's reputation as a blind, ball-grabbing trap into a personal manifesto: she's had enough, she won't surrender her soul, but she bites back just as hard as love does.","src":"haiku"},
"half-alive~maybe":{"haiku":"Paralysis yields to movement; redefining transformation as continuous motion rather than static resolution enables escape from inherited trauma.","opus":"Wrestling free of inherited pain and the story that says you can't change, unsure whether healing comes from stillness or motion, and daring to believe there is more to a person than the damage on the surface — a hopeful crossing rather than a fixed fate.","sonnet":"A person trapped in inherited pain and mental war considers whether stasis is what is truly killing them — the turn arrives in the suggestion that change lives in motion, not in stopping, and that identity is more than the wounds carried forward from blood and fear.","src":"haiku"},
"half-an-orange~mark-twain":{"haiku":"Corporate conformity suffocates authenticity; the narrator seeks escape from predetermined roles but discovers the burial occurs long before death.","opus":"A young man smothered by the trappings of a conventional adult existence, picturing his own burial in formalwear he would never have chosen. Since the age of seventeen he has been on a long, lonely hunt for anything that might finally make him feel truly awake.","sonnet":"A speaker imagines their own burial, dressed in a suit they'd never have chosen, and reflects that since seventeen they have been searching for whatever would make them feel complete and alive — a search that still hasn't ended.","src":"haiku"},
"half-me~blacklight":{"haiku":"Obsessive surveillance merges with worship as the narrator accepts delusional devotion, recognition trapped within asymmetrical power and impossible desire.","opus":"A twisted fixation on someone who stands for an old life, the singer playing a delusional, undercover lover convinced the other stalks and craves him back. He surrenders to the obsession rather than fight it, tired of the voices calling it wrong, feeling only revulsion when their eyes meet.","sonnet":"Disentangling from an obsessive, surveillance-like relationship where the other person has followed the speaker home and wants only the best of her — spine, time, and all — she refuses to keep listening and flees the fixation.","src":"haiku"},
"half-me~distort":{"haiku":"Moral collapse accelerates into the abyss; obsession with sacred power feeds monstrosity, leaving only self-loathing and the conviction that therapy cannot cure what runs too deep.","opus":"A metalcore plunge into moral collapse and self-disgust, the narrator bathed in sin and unsure who he's become. Craving a sacred, devouring power while begging not to be left alone, he pleads for a therapist rather than another tormentor. Addiction, guilt and the search for a single way out drive its desperation.","sonnet":"A speaker bathed in sin and dying too slowly confronts obsession, self-destruction, and the sense of deserving nothing, while insisting they are 'the catalyst' who understands there is only one way out — demanding not another devil but a therapist.","src":"haiku"},
"half-me~ex-negativo":{"opus":"A self-loathing spiral where the narrator is prey to his own cultivated habits, refusing to believe in redemption, snuffing out his feelings and second-guessing his every choice as one more year drains away.","src":"opus"},
"half-me~i-am-but-a-guest-in-exile":{"haiku":"Persecution runs deeper than deception—the speaker's counterfeit existence offers no escape, and rot awaits all who face the truth of their fraud.","opus":"A metalcore confrontation with a parasitic figure pushing the narrator toward the edge, feeding on his ruin. Cast as a death hoax and reduced to a splinter, he turns grudge into aversion and vows to leave the scavenger rotting. Identity theft and betrayal fuel a vengeful reclaiming of self, the mask torn away.","sonnet":"A speaker confronting a predatory figure — a scavenger feasting on debris, a 'pipe dream' and a disguise — moves from being pushed to the edge toward defiance, resolving to expose the counterfeit, leave them rotting, and reclaim the space they were driven from.","src":"haiku"},
"half-me~trauma-culture":{"haiku":"The speaker internalizes trauma and isolation, becoming a vehicle for their own consumption while paranoia and bitterness replace empathy.","opus":"German metalcore band Half Me anatomize a damaged psyche seizing control by claiming ownership of its own ruin, playing a rotten hand and admitting it now feeds on its own bitterness. As paranoia and isolation set in, the narrator becomes a hollowed-out shell, delivered with clenched, self-lacerating fury.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone trapped inside systems of damage — external dogma, bad luck, and internalized self-blame all press down equally, and the speaker can neither escape nor stop participating. The walls and ceiling close in as isolation deepens, until the only exit feels like obliteration, and the surrounding culture is indicted for feeding on that despair.","src":"haiku"},
"halo~blow-me-away":{"haiku":"Survival demands sacrifice and violence; only the strongest endure as individuals sacrifice identity to become weapons for collective victory.","opus":"A combat-charged rock anthem, written for the Halo universe, voiced from the chaos of a losing battle turned defiant last stand. Amid falling comrades and blown cover, the narrator vows only the strongest survive and casts himself as the shadow who'll save them all. Adrenaline, sacrifice and heroic resolve under fire.","sonnet":"A soldier or lone hero pressed by overwhelming odds calls for the courage to fight on — \"only the strongest will survive\" — claiming the role of shadow on the wall and the one who will save everyone, even while the situation is already lost.","src":"haiku"},
"halsey~darling":{"haiku":"Acceptance of mortality and loss quiets struggle; the speaker makes peace with their fractured self and approaching death, finding solace in cosmic connection with someone who loves them alive.","opus":"Losing her spine and leaning on medication, a rootless woman who grew up without a stable home finds in a beloved the first reason to love being alive. It is about a nomadic, self-destructive life redeemed by devotion, and a fascination with death softened into tenderness.","sonnet":"A restless, medicated figure who has never had a stable home finds in a single relationship the only reason to love being alive; she imagines kidnapping stars and hanging them in the beloved's eyes, addresses someone she calls \"darling\" with instructions not to weep, and accepts death calmly as just the next place they'll meet.","src":"haiku"},
"halsey~easier-than-lying":{"haiku":"A person shaped by another's judgment struggles with self-abandonment and empty faith, finding loss easier than maintaining a false relationship.","opus":"A furious dissection of being cast as the villain in someone else's story, made in their image and drained by their cruelty, until leaving hurts less than pretending they love you. Halsey weaponizes the abuser's own logic against them.","sonnet":"Someone who has poured themselves entirely into a relationship they knew was one-sided finally arrives at the conclusion that letting go is less destructive than continuing to tell themselves the other person loves them back.","src":"haiku"},
"halsey~girl-is-a-gun":{"haiku":"Autonomy means lethal unpredictability; the speaker resists domestication and demands independence, recognizing love as another form of capture.","opus":"A woman claiming her freedom and danger, unburdened by a partner's grip and refusing to be anyone's daydream or mother of his child. She casts herself as a loaded weapon, done with love that never satisfies, better off giving the boys up.","sonnet":"A woman asserts autonomous, weaponised selfhood: she's loaded with the safety off, done with men who want her on their terms, and though she keeps falling into love she keeps finding the same overflow of problems — \"this girl is a gun\" is both warning and self-declaration.","src":"haiku"},
"halsey~honey":{"haiku":"Intoxicating love becomes poison; the speaker tastes sweetness that turns bitter, caught between addiction and revulsion, unable to separate honey from blood.","opus":"An intoxicating, volatile lover who is sweet and vicious at once, tasted alongside blood and the bitterness of goodbye. It is about craving a mean, thrilling love the singer knows is bad but claims as her own.","sonnet":"A woman described in the language of honey — sweet, sticky, stinging — is desired precisely because of the pain she leaves behind; the speaker loves every second of her wildness, can still taste the blood and bitterness of goodbye in her throat, and cannot resist someone who is mean and hers.","src":"haiku"},
"halsey~i-am-not-a-woman-i-m-a-god":{"haiku":"Rejecting categorization as woman, martyr, or legend while admitting to being a problem and a fraud. The speaker celebrates emotional detachment and refusing to give their heart away.","opus":"A defiant self-mythologizing anthem where the narrator rejects the roles offered her, woman, martyr, legend, and claims godhood and fraudulence in the same breath, bodies buried in the garden as she craves to feel something real. Beneath the armor is loneliness and a hollow where the heart should be. Halsey built the album this opens around pregnancy, power and horror imagery, and this is its icy, grandiose statement of intent.","sonnet":"A self-portrait built from negations — not a martyr, not a legend, not even a woman in the conventional sense, but something that defies categorization while still aching to feel something real. The speaker carries private wreckage like bodies in a garden, chooses solitude as armor, and admits the contradiction: love still hurts, even for someone who has declared themselves beyond it.","src":"haiku"},
"halsey~lilith":{"haiku":"A woman acknowledges her own destructiveness in love: quick temper, seduction followed by betrayal, and serial dysfunction. She owns her corruption and disruption unapologetically.","opus":"Halsey confronts her own habit of wrecking intimacy, admitting she gets swept up too fast, can't name it love once she reveals it, and ruins whatever she gets close to. Naming herself after the mythic demon-woman lets her frame that damage as inherited destiny, in her darkly confessional alt-pop mode.","sonnet":"A restless self-examination from someone who knows she is disruptive and cannot stop being so — she gets consumed by moments, cannot name what she feels as love, and ruins things by noticing it too late. There is self-awareness without remedy, a corrupt charisma that controls through absence rather than presence, and an undercurrent of exhaustion from the road she has been on too long.","src":"haiku"},
"halsey~now-or-never":{"haiku":"Relationship reaches terminal impasse; the speaker demands commitment while the other offers only evasion and silence.","opus":"An ultimatum to a distant, non-committal lover, done with talking and demanding a decision now, the exhaustion of loving someone who won't pick up or spread the love around finally hardening into a line drawn.","sonnet":"An ultimatum delivered to a distant, noncommittal partner: after too much waiting and not enough presence, the speaker draws a hard line — commit now or it's over, because she needs to know she is chosen and the relationship can't survive on wishful thinking anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"halsey~the-lighthouse":{"haiku":"Predatory seduction and violent reclamation reverse victim and perpetrator; the speaker lures sailors to their doom, having learned cruelty from the devil's touch as a child.","opus":"A woman cursed with rage recasts herself as a siren luring sailors to their doom, refusing rescue because she only ever wanted to be found. It is about weaponizing her own danger and keeping a piece of the devil who taught her she was weak.","sonnet":"A woman who has never wanted saving — only to be found — lures a sailor in with promises of lovely things, then laughs as he drowns because she never needed a rescuer; cursed with rage since childhood and left by the devil at the bottom of a lake, she is still stranded, still wandering the beach, glad to be dangerous.","src":"haiku"},
"halsey~the-tradition":{"haiku":"Patriarchal violence reproduces across generations as possession and coercion become inherited cultural practice, sanctified through tradition.","opus":"A gothic parable of women bought, dressed up and used, exposing how possession masquerades as love and how the entitlement to take without consent gets handed down like an inheritance, the tradition being men's impunity itself.","sonnet":"Women are commodified, displayed, and returned damaged across generations — the song frames this as an inherited system of entitlement where men take what they want without consequence, and the tradition of exploitation passes through blood.","src":"haiku"},
"halsey~whispers":{"haiku":"Internal sabotage and fractured self-knowledge conspire against intimacy; the speaker recognizes their monstrosity yet cannot stop consuming personality types and then discarding them.","opus":"The cruel inner voice of self-sabotage narrates, mocking her need for love, urging her to camouflage and burn down what she cares about. It is about a shape-shifting self-loathing that eats her identity and keeps her from knowing who she is.","sonnet":"A sabotaging inner voice — dissecting loneliness, daddy issues, and romantic compulsion — splinters between second and first person as the speaker realises the voice tearing apart her desire is her own; she names a \"monster inside me that eats personality types\" and admits she does not know herself at all.","src":"haiku"},
"hal~a-1pdzx45":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"This song runs on pure physical exhilaration, the kind of sensory overload that stands in for desire. Rain-soaked, staring into the sun, riding a motorbike fast enough to split the air, the ocean swallowing the rider — every image serves a single longing to fuse with another person and escape the city's noise together. Speed and weather do the work of romance, the body's rush becoming the vocabulary for union.","sonnet":"A song of pure physical exhilaration and longing for union — soaked in rain, staring into the sun, riding a motorbike so fast the air is split, the ocean swallowing you — all of it metaphor for a desire to fuse with another person and escape the city's noise together.","src":"haiku"},
"hammerfall~hearts-on-fire":{"haiku":"A persecuted group rebels against oppressive rule, their banners and hammers symbolizing defiant unity as they burn with desire to reclaim freedom.","opus":"Swedish power metal at its most heraldic, casting the band and their faithful as a persecuted order of true believers who kept the flame through the genre's lean years. The steel and banners are metaphor for devotion to the music itself.","sonnet":"About a persecuted community of true believers — framed in Templar imagery — who have grown in secret and are now ready to reclaim their freedom, driven by an unquenchable burning desire for steel and justice.","src":"haiku"},
"hammerfall~lore-of-the-arcane":{"haiku":"Redemption through love transforms the narrator from darkness into illumination, intimate connection becoming the ultimate salvation from spiritual death.","opus":"A power-metal romance of rebirth through love, a man once numbed by lies feeling alive again in someone's eyes and reborn in their light. The kiss-me refrain vows total devotion even as the walls break down, asking only that she still believe in him.","sonnet":"A person burned by past lies and darkness finds themselves overwhelmed by a new love — lost and melting at a single kiss, reborn in the other's light — but questions whether they dare to trust, acknowledging that walls will eventually break.","src":"haiku"},
"hammerfall~on-the-edge-of-honour":{"haiku":"Warriors and immortal protectors journey through darkness and hell, rising from ruin to defend earth and fight for honour and light.","opus":"Swedish power-metal standard-bearers HammerFall march through a high-fantasy epic, warriors drawn into an underworld of dark enchantment and monstrous foes where they must choose between yielding and battle. As the dead rise and darkness stains the night, the band lean into their trademark heroism, hearts blazing eternally as fighters climb through the flames toward glory and redemption.","sonnet":"Warriors lost in a mythic underworld — seduced, misled, and enslaved by demonic forces — must choose between surrender and a final stand. The resolution comes through collective resurrection: fallen angels rising, spirits uniting before a unicorn-marked dawn, turning ruin into defiant ascent toward light.","src":"haiku"},
"hammerfall~riders-of-the-storm":{"haiku":"Spiritually aligned defenders rise against machine control, seeking liberation and genesis reborn as forces of creation overcome oppression.","opus":"HammerFall, Swedish flag-bearers of the power-metal revival, cast liberation as a mythic cavalry charge. Ghostly guardians of life ride out against a mechanized, controlling world, tearing down cages and hollow appearances to seize their own fate. It's fist-in-the-air fantasy built for the singalong.","sonnet":"A band of spiritual warriors, caged by phantom powers and false systems, seeks a genesis beyond the machine — aligned with elemental forces, free of manipulation. The storm is both threat and vehicle. Liberation requires riding what would destroy lesser resolve, and the riders define themselves by choosing movement over captivity.","src":"haiku"},
"hammerhedd~disturbance":{"opus":"An awestruck reflection on the deep human drive to look past what is visible, climbing toward fresh horizons and sensing some immense energy and other forms of life. The narrator is then dragged back down to earth and its powerlessness, only to rise again in fresh wonder at the world.","sonnet":"About the awakening of primal instinct in a modern context — the narrator senses another life-force, feels the prehistoric gears churning beneath the present world, and arrives at a new plateau of perception where the machinery of existence becomes visible.","src":"sonnet"},
"hammok~gooning-for-free":{"haiku":"Digital performance masks private need for validation; the subject broadcasts prayer alongside danger.","opus":"A fractured take on performing for an audience and craving validation, the singer needing to feel and own it while putting his face on the screen begging to be prayed for. It circles compulsively around desire and the danger of doing it all for free.","sonnet":"A restless, fragmented drive toward being felt and desired: the speaker wants to own the moment, grow into something bigger than self-promotion, and be more than a face on a screen begging for prayer — \"radio raiders\" and danger circle around an unresolved hunger.","src":"haiku"},
"han-irl-3~spaaaceee":{"haiku":"Emotional turmoil and communication breakdown spiral into abandonment trauma; the speaker collapses into numb silence after reaching out goes unanswered.","opus":"An indie-pop ache about clinging to someone who's pulled away, wrapped in cosmic imagery. Left on radio silence after unanswered texts, the narrator keeps circling the other's street and offering the space they asked for, all while unable to stop wanting them. Longing dressed as generosity, wounded and self-aware.","sonnet":"Post-breakup obsession mapped in precise millennial detail: six unanswered texts, circling the ex's street, still wearing their coat in summer heat, radio silence amplified by overthinking on the 101. The speaker promises space while demonstrating the inability to give it.","src":"haiku"},
"hana-lili~figure-it-out":{"haiku":"Desire and fantasy sustain a risky, undefined connection where both parties navigate ambiguity and unspoken feelings.","opus":"The dizzy, sleepless crush on a musician down the hall, fantasizing day and night while playing it cool and daring him to catch on. The thrill rides on whether the longing has finally become obvious, and whether it's long past time to act.","sonnet":"A woman developing a secret crush on her musician neighbor, enjoying late-night staircase conversations while keeping her feelings hidden beneath the surface, and slowly moving toward admitting the obvious mutual attraction.","src":"haiku"},
"hana-lili~iconic":{"haiku":"Aspiration to legendary status and transcendence, becoming immortal or unforgettable.","opus":"A wry indie-pop snapshot of an ambivalent, late-night attraction. The narrator doesn't even like the guy's band but likes how he looks under the lights, tangled in a feeling she hates and loves at once. Jaded and self-mocking, it captures the ironic thrill of something that means nothing yet won't quite let go.","sonnet":"A jaded post-breakup flirtation at 3 a.m. — the new guy's band is bad but he's hot, the moment feels right yet exhausting, and everything about the emotion is simultaneously hated and loved. \"That's so iconic\" is ironic self-commentary on the absurdity of feeling something again despite wanting not to.","src":"haiku"},
"hana-lili~sick-of-myself":{"haiku":"Self-disgust metastasises into paralysis, the speaker drowns their own voice while lying to others, trapped in a feedback loop of shame they cannot escape.","opus":"The exhausting labor of hiding a feeling you can't name, covering it up and lying that things are better while drowning underneath. The self-disgust is airtight, a sense that there's no exit from being who you are.","sonnet":"A person hiding serious inner distress behind a composed exterior — perpetually performing okayness, unable to articulate the depth of their self-disgust even when they try, trapped in a cycle of suppression with no apparent exit and a deep wish to be someone else entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"hanabie~a-puj39p":{"opus":"Hanabie are a Tokyo band whose harajuku-core mixes metalcore breakdowns with candy-bright pop and a heavy dose of cartoonish persona, and this track pushes that theatricality into space. The narrator crowns herself supreme space-invader girl, broadcasting from the cosmos, hijacking the Milky Way with moonlight and stardust and calling all earthlings to join her. The bravado is the fun — a self-declared strongest in the universe asking if Earth can pick up her signal yet.","sonnet":"A self-declared supreme space-invader girl broadcasting from the cosmos, calling all earthlings to come join her — transmitting a mysterious cosmic story with moonlight and stardust, hijacking the Milky Way, insisting she is the universe's strongest and asking if Earth can see her signal yet.","src":"sonnet"},
"hanabie~a-z2rftf":{"haiku":"Satire of adult conformity and workplace politeness masking the desire to escape corporate etiquette entirely.","opus":"Hanabie built their whole aesthetic on translating Japanese youth frustration into blaring, playful heaviness, and here they aim it at professional social codes. The narrator is crushed under constant bowing, dense schedules, and drinking parties they cannot fit into, until they explode out of the system with a defiant declaration and run. The rejection is specific: adult performance of deference read as infantilizing, refused with the band's trademark grin-and-scream defiance.","sonnet":"A young person crushed under professional social codes — constant bowing, dense schedules, drinking parties they don't fit into — who explodes out of the system with a defiant \"I'm leaving first\" and runs, rejecting the adult performance of deference as infantilizing.","src":"haiku"},
"hanabie~a-zzrmxu":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"Hanabie tie their identity tightly to their live crowd, and this song turns that bond cosmic. The band pledges to cross galaxies and search billions of stars to reach the one person meant for them, promising that even as stardust they will live on for those waiting. Love and live performance fuse into a single vow — a devotion scaled to the universe, the fan and the beloved made indistinguishable in a promise that outlasts any distance.","sonnet":"A song of astronomical devotion to fans — the band will cross galaxies and search through billions of stars to reach the one person meant for them, pledging that even as stardust they will live on for those waiting, tying love and live performance together as a cosmic promise that transcends any distance.","src":"haiku"},
"hanabie~l-c-g-2019mix":{"opus":"A hyperactive collage of Gen-Z social-media addiction, craving a million followers and retweets, chasing likes and bubble tea while a girl clings to childish fairy-tale love, drinks and parties until dawn, and ends on a discovered lie.","src":"opus"},
"hanabie~mytype":{"haiku":"Critique of personality typing culture; MBTI obsession reflects modern demand for self-categorization to feel understood.","opus":"Hanabie specialize in affectionate satire of the online youth culture they belong to, and here they train it on the MBTI obsession sweeping that world. Everyone is glued to screens, sorting themselves and each other into sixteen types, and the band both mocks and joins the craze — a knowing wink at self-analysis as the tool modern people now need just to navigate each other. The critique lands softest because it never pretends to stand outside.","sonnet":"An affectionate roast of the MBTI personality-type obsession overtaking online culture — everyone glued to screens categorizing themselves and each other into sixteen types, simultaneously mocking and participating in the self-analysis craze that modern people need just to navigate life.","src":"haiku"},
"hanabie~spicy-queen":{"haiku":"Addiction to intensity and sensation drives the speaker toward extremes that burn and break, craving ever-hotter thrills.","opus":"A gleefully manic ode to spicy-food obsession, rejecting sweetness for burning heat as a whole identity. The closing pun lands the point, that adding a single stroke to the character for painful spelling makes the character for happiness.","sonnet":"A Japanese-language anthem about craving intensity and stimulation over sweetness — the narrator is bored by mild and pleasant things, addicted to hot, spicy, electric sensation (literal and metaphorical), and positions herself as an unstoppable spicy queen who nobody can contain; the song is half food metaphor, half defiant self-declaration.","src":"haiku"},
"hanabie~tousou":{"opus":"A frantic cops-and-robbers chase framed as a runaway fleeing north, south, east and west with a police dog on her trail, refusing the handcuffs and vowing to keep running and stumbling at her own pace until she finds the light.","src":"opus"},
"hanabie~ver":{"opus":"A homebody dreads the sociable summer season, hiding behind blackout curtains and sweating, yet resolves to become a sparkling Instagrammer, hitting night pools, festivals and BBQs before collapsing from heatstroke and needing to rest.","src":"opus"},
"hanabie~we-love-sweets":{"haiku":"Sugar consumption becomes metaphor for indulgence and escapism, celebrated as justified reward despite metabolic consequence.","opus":"A giddy, sugar-drunk anthem from the Japanese harajukucore band, weaponizing dessert cravings into pure kawaii-metal id. Midnight hunger becomes a manifesto: candy as mood stabilizer, glucose as brain fuel, indulgence as self-care rationalized past all reason. The screamed devotion to sweets doubles as cheeky permission to let appetite override discipline entirely.","sonnet":"A gleeful, hyperbolic ode to sugar addiction frames sweets as legitimate brain fuel, stress relief, and a female pleasure reclamation — escalating from midnight hunger pangs through a catalogue of confections to a joyful declaration of dependency. (lang: ja)","src":"haiku"},
"hana~anxious-alien":{"haiku":"Conflict between a performed, confident exterior and inner anxiety and despair; the speaker hides true feelings to survive while questioning if anyone genuinely cares.","opus":"HANA exposes the swaggering front of a self-crowned queen who claims to have it all as a shell over paralyzing loneliness and the fear that she's unliked and unmissed once she stops performing. The soaring refrain about not weeping and never dying is the numbed coping mechanism that lets her shove the sorrow away and disappear.","sonnet":"A song about performing confidence while collapsing inside — the first half presents a polished, invulnerable persona, the second reveals the terror underneath: loneliness, the fear of not being liked, and the compulsion to hide the anxiety rather than address it.","src":"haiku"},
"hana~black-orchid":{"haiku":"The speaker transforms from victimized silence into defiant action, insisting beauty and love must be fought for loudly and now.","opus":"HANA moves from remembered wounds toward a defiant refusal to accept that nobody can change anything. Recognizing hate as the true enemy, she calls for love to be moved around and screamed out loud before fleeting beauty runs out. Emerging from Grimes's orbit as a solo art-pop voice, she couples melting-world dread with an activist charge that insists on fighting now.","sonnet":"Someone who once bled from others' words now knows exactly where to aim. Old wounds have clarified into purpose rather than bitterness — she recalls being told nothing could change, then decides to prove it wrong. The lesson she takes from betrayal is not hatred but the stubborn, urgent insistence on beauty while it lasts.","src":"haiku"},
"hana~chimera":{"opus":"A coiled warning from Grimes-adjacent art-pop, the narrator embodying a monstrous force barely restrained. Once calm, now provoked past the point of control, she promises she absolutely can inflict harm, taunting a target to watch their back. Menace and monstrous transformation become an assertion of power that will not be underestimated.","sonnet":"A figure who has been underestimated reveals she is far more dangerous than assumed — a chimera, a creature of hidden fire — and warns whoever provoked her to run, because the threat she poses is real and the confrontation is now over.","src":"sonnet"},
"hana~chimera-2-0":{"haiku":"Someone harbors dangerous potential they cannot contain; they warn others to leave before their internal fire consumes them.","opus":"A song of barely restrained menace, its narrator once serene now goaded past her breaking point and hinting at a retaliation the target would beg to avoid. HANA, an experimental pop artist and regular Grimes collaborator, delivers the threat through eerie synth textures, letting held-back fury creep toward the point of eruption.","sonnet":"A contained fury finally surfacing — someone who held herself calm and composed finds that a boundary has been crossed one too many times. The fire and crawling spine signal something animal and long-suppressed. The warning is issued quietly but with absolute clarity: she told the other to leave it alone, and now it is too late for that.","src":"haiku"},
"hana~so-so":{"opus":"A gentle, questing ballad about depletion and renewal, framed by a silver moon as sole guide. When the well of energy runs dry and everything feels finished, the narrator keeps going anyway, trusting that returning home restores the glow. Weariness meets quiet resilience, an acceptance of cycles of emptiness and light.","sonnet":"A slow, meditative drift through exhaustion and the sense of having done everything: the narrator moves through memory and moonlight at a reduced pace, finding a tentative peace in simply letting the cycle run — feeling neither great nor terrible, just so-so.","src":"sonnet"},
"hands-like-houses~24-hours":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and emotional numbness dominate existence, where light fades and breathing stops mid-consciousness. Time becomes a spiral of repetition where daylight offers no relief.","opus":"A portrait of dissociation and insomnia that reads like drowning while fully conscious. The Australian post-hardcore band renders depression as a colorless daze where reflection becomes frightening and time drains away. Prayer offers no relief, only the memory of a fuller self, as the narrator remains awake yet unable to breathe or move forward.","sonnet":"Dissociation and depression rendered through insomnia: the speaker is 'wide awake but not breathing', drowning in a tidal wave with colour drained to grey, afraid of their reflection, praying for relief that doesn't come — the hourglass out of sand, the chest hollow, the man half-empty.","src":"haiku"},
"hands-like-houses~bloodrush":{"opus":"Addiction to a toxic romance rendered as a chemical high, gasoline and butterflies, stimulants laced with sweet wine, the speaker a willing captive of the rush, kissing death and forgetting to breathe while filing it under tomorrow's problem.","src":"opus"},
"hands-like-houses~dangerous":{"haiku":"The speaker spirals into recklessness and instability, feeling out of control and dangerous as they oscillate between extremes of emotion and action.","opus":"Hands Like Houses capture self-destruction experienced as a high, the depleted compulsion to detonate everything simply because the impulse is irresistible and lessons never stick. It conveys the thrill and powerlessness of a mind lurching between elation and crashing down with nowhere safe to land.","sonnet":"About running on empty and teetering on the edge of breakdown — the narrator swings between highs and lows with no direction, tempted to blow everything up because doing nothing feels worse than exploding.","src":"haiku"},
"hands-like-houses~grey-havens":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation and spiritual dread shadow the narrator's uncertainty about whether continuing to live is worth the cost.","opus":"A figure poised on the edge of death, unsure whether it means waking or a sleep without dreams, dreading the void yet reaching for something bright to lead him onward. He clings to the fragile belief that his moment to leave has not yet come.","sonnet":"Standing at an existential threshold, the speaker balances fear of oblivion against reluctance to leave, asking whether death feels like waking or surrender and ultimately talking themselves toward belief that it is not yet their time.","src":"haiku"},
"hands-like-houses~headrush":{"haiku":"The speaker declares they are inside someone's mind, willing to bend and break but never surrender, determined to prove themselves worthy despite the cost.","opus":"A song about a psychic tug-of-war, the narrator lodged inside someone's head, animal imagery of sharpened claws and bared teeth building toward a collision that fractures the earth. Underneath the bravado runs anxious self-interrogation about whether he has done enough and how much he will bend before breaking. Hands Like Houses are an Australian post-hardcore band, and this is their melodic, hook-forward take on the genre, all soaring choruses and coiled tension.","sonnet":"A battle of wills framed as total mutual occupation — two people locked in an intensity so complete it reshapes the physical world around them, fracturing the earth where they meet. The speaker refuses to walk away no matter the cost, bending and breaking rather than surrendering, while doubt creeps in about whether any of it was worth the damage done.","src":"haiku"},
"hands-like-houses~heaven":{"haiku":"Longing for transcendence and escape from earthly pain, heaven as salvation from worldly suffering.","opus":"A bleak reckoning with the idea that salvation was never coming. Walking among shadows and the dead, the narrator concludes that no god made or will save anyone. The band frames human self-destruction as the real damnation, blurring angel and devil until the categories collapse and paradise reveals itself as a place with no room for the damned.","sonnet":"A declaration that heaven holds no place for the damned and the doomed — blood on fingertips, madness poisonous, shadows that never stop coming. The speaker is a victim of their own design, running toward emptiness because it gets them high, caught between angel and devil with no clean answer about who the heretic is.","src":"haiku"},
"hands-like-houses~obey":{"opus":"A tempter's sales pitch — the devil offering to make you a somebody, an object of worship, in trade for your conscience and your soul, luring you with gorgeous deceptions while you stay forever the drawn insect and never the fire, in a perilous game of ambition.","src":"opus"},
"hands-like-houses~paradise":{"haiku":"A speaker maintains surface control while spiraling inward—running circles, living lies, trapped in a paradise of their own making.","opus":"The hollow mantra of someone insisting they are fine while running in circles inside a self-made lie, the suffering they beg to escape lodged in their own head.","sonnet":"A song about the self-delusion of pretending everything is fine while wasting away — the narrator keeps insisting they're doing just fine and things are under control, but the truth leaks through: they're running in circles, living a lie, purposeless and suffering, calling their stagnation paradise out of bitter irony.","src":"haiku"},
"hands-like-houses~the-devil-of-decisions":{"opus":"A eulogy for someone slipping away, the narrator retracing shared family history and choking on things left unsaid. He arrives too late for an apology as the person passes beyond his reach, hearing farewell in their fading body.","src":"opus"},
"hands-like-houses~this-ain-t-no-place-for-animals":{"haiku":"Modern alienation and violence permeate the city; collision and pain become proof of existence.","opus":"A frantic meditation on numbness in a mechanical modern city, craving collision and even catastrophe to feel alive again, the surgeons picking uselessly at a body because no one can diagnose what's actually killing us.","sonnet":"A car crash rendered in fragments — gasoline and oxygen, broken glass, a face glimpsed in the wreckage, surgeons unable to diagnose what is killing the patient — becomes a metaphor for a connection defined by collision rather than tenderness, where impact is the only proof of being alive. The city itself is implicated as a system that strips people of touch and then wonders why they collide.","src":"haiku"},
"hands-like-houses~wildfire":{"haiku":"Love incinerates; the speaker walks knowingly into flames, watching paradise transform into ash and loss of self through passionate entanglement.","opus":"A love that devours like a blaze, the singer stepping willingly into a relationship wrecking them both. The silence roars and they're poison to themselves, burying whatever they used to cherish as the embers rain down.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship is rendered as a wildfire and a blackout — acid clouds, bitterness, poison — until both people are ash and rubble, the love itself a burning fallout that the speaker keeps walking into despite knowing what it destroys.","src":"haiku"},
"happy-hour~cigarettes":{"haiku":"Failed relationship and regret; the speaker wishes they had heeded advice to quit this person, now facing terminal consequences of the addiction.","opus":"Regret braided together with a cancer diagnosis, the cigarettes standing for a habit and a person both bad for you and both hard to quit. It slides into masochism, owning that the pain was self-inflicted and the pleasure real, all done to himself.","sonnet":"An extended metaphor comparing a toxic relationship to a smoking addiction — the narrator knew the relationship was bad and was even told to quit, but stayed addicted to the pain; by the end the cancer-and-chemo imagery suggests the damage is already done and they're only now reckoning with their own masochism.","src":"haiku"},
"harakiri-for-the-sky~manifesto":{"haiku":"Rebellion rejects false promises and systemic servitude through defiant living, the narrator claiming freedom through refusal to play by imposed rules.","opus":"A refusal of the prescribed life of consuming and working until you die, writing a defiant manifesto against rules he never chose. The cover reframes an anti-capitalist creed as a hunger to feel human, to log off and walk out of the room.","sonnet":"The speaker's one-line manifesto is \"don't let nothing ever get you low\" — a rejection of working for another man's money and living to consume and die — arriving after a revelation that the rules were always a lie and truth enters the mind one word at a time.","src":"haiku"},
"harakiri-for-the-sky~song-to-say-goodbye":{"opus":"This blackgaze act reimagines Placebo's lament for a friend consumed by heroin addiction. The narrator swings between disgust and desperate love, breaking down the door to save someone drowning in the liquid sky. Helplessness curdles into grief as the fear surfaces that only a farewell song will remain.","sonnet":"About watching a friend destroy themselves with drugs — a cover of Placebo's song sung from the position of someone who is angry, heartbroken, and desperately trying to pull the other person back from the edge before their addiction becomes their only farewell.","src":"sonnet"},
"harmed~every-me":{"haiku":"The speaker fragments into multiple selves perceived through others' eyes; each view is different and none is true, creating paranoid delusion and confusion.","opus":"A protest against being flattened into someone else's snap judgment tips into a full identity crisis, the narrator arguing that outsiders only ever see his surface. It spirals into paranoid self-fragmentation, in which he is at once everything and nothing, a figure he invented and the executioner of his own undoing.","sonnet":"A song about the gap between who you are and how others perceive you — the narrator is simultaneously everything and nothing, a self-created illusion who hangs himself on others' judgments, paranoid that no one can see past the surface.","src":"haiku"},
"harmless-habit~soft-silent-tragedy":{"opus":"An overthinking mind that can't stop racing, wearing a mask like everyone else while screaming underneath, self-medicating on speed and cigarettes as anxiety and apathy hollow him out, in a quiet, unspoken catastrophe he half-breaks down to ask for help through.","src":"opus"},
"harper~chelsea-smile":{"haiku":"Buried secrets eat the speaker from within while vultures circle, guilt accumulates, and repentance becomes the only prayer—yet salvation remains uncertain and the scars indelible.","opus":"A cover of the Bring Me the Horizon staple, wrestling a corrosive secret that eats the narrator alive with guilt and insomnia. Shame festers like disease under a smiling mask, watched by circling vultures, while a crisis of faith surfaces: doubting whether a god he cannot believe in could ever believe back in him.","sonnet":"A speaker buries a guilt-soaked secret that feeds like cancer — \"I may look happy but the only way I'll really smile is if you cut me ear to ear\" delivers the dark pivot — and questions whether a God who isn't believed in would believe in them in return.","src":"haiku"},
"harper~falling":{"haiku":"Cyclical entrapment prevents escape as the speaker tumbles through repetition; awakening to the cycle only deepens the desperation.","opus":"A mind coming apart inside a cycle it can't break, voices taking over and the fear that reality is only a dream. The other person pulls the singer under the waves, and the whole plea is to be told the truth as they suffocate, asking if this is the end.","sonnet":"Trapped in a cycle of burning and falling, the speaker is pulled underwater — 'you're pulling me down beneath the waves, I'm suffocating' — and questions whether the voices, the walls, and the dream are real, demanding to know if there is any way out before asking 'is this the end of me?'","src":"haiku"},
"harper~i-hope-you-choke":{"opus":"A furious kiss-off to someone who made the speaker feel less than and turned them into a puppet on strings, done pretending to be strong, telling the tormentor to eat their words and wishing they choke.","src":"opus"},
"harper~weight-of-the-world-feat-we-came-as-romans-brand-of-sacrifice":{"haiku":"Crowned in isolation, the speaker claims sovereignty and weaponizes strength; bending knees demands royalty while the weight crushes those who refuse to serve.","opus":"A defiant coronation from a betrayed sovereign refusing to surrender the crown. Cast in royal metaphor, the narrator dares would-be usurpers to bend the knee or be crushed, having been wounded by the very kingdom that once worshiped her. Sleepless in her own hell, she reclaims power as sword and queen, promising to take back what remains.","sonnet":"A sovereign speaker — \"I am the sword, I am your queen\" — warns those who try to take her crown that the weight of royalty will crush anyone who does not lend their strength, the kingdom that once worshipped her having become the reason she cannot sleep.","src":"haiku"},
"harpy~inferno":{"haiku":"Destructive female rage and fiery vengeance, the speaker as supernatural force of annihilation and justice.","opus":"A predatory plunge into hell staged as devouring seduction, the narrator crowning herself a vengeful goddess ruling the damned as she drags her prey down through hell's descending levels, consuming their body and spirit. Desire and torment fuse into a single hunger, and she offers no mercy, only condemnation, swearing never to release her captive from the flames.","sonnet":"A speaker who positions herself as deity, queen of the damned, and destroyer — her enemy triggered her demons and now their flesh will feed them. The imagery descends through infernal layers: tearing limb from limb, swimming in blood, breathing in essence until nothing remains, delivering them to a communion of damnation with no savior available.","src":"haiku"},
"harpy~inside-out":{"haiku":"Falling toward euphoria and self-destruction merge; the speaker craves collapse caught mid-air, asking for rescue while preferring to plummet.","opus":"The addictive rush of self-ruin, the narrator hurling herself off the same brink over and over because the drop feels blissful even while it terrifies her. Her request to be caught on the way down is undermined by the admission that she never actually wants the fall to stop.","sonnet":"A compulsive self-harm loop described as a high — the narrator cuts into their own skin, knows they should not want it, but keeps falling back over the edge because the freefall feels like heaven, and they are terrified of coming down.","src":"haiku"},
"harpy~medicine":{"haiku":"Pain becomes palliative; the speaker drinks anguish like cure, knowing poison and medicine taste identical and neither brings healing.","opus":"A mutually ruinous relationship taken like a cure that never actually mends anything, both partners resigned to going down together. The love is curse and remedy at once, choosing pain and permanent illness over ever getting well.","sonnet":"A destructive relationship consumed like a drug — 'I drink it down like medicine but you'll never make me better' — where pain is the mechanism of feeling and the two are locked in mutual ruin, both sick forever and calling it healing even as they choke on the dose.","src":"haiku"},
"harpy~slaughterhouse":{"haiku":"Hedonistic transgression promises authentic sensation beneath surfaces; disgust fuels desire.","opus":"A predator's invitation into a space where conscience is left at the door and pain is the entertainment. The imagery of bleeding, cutting and feeding stages a sadomasochistic power trip that revels in how disgusting it is.","sonnet":"An invitation into a space of extreme, consensual transgression — the speaker welcomes the listener to a 'slaughterhouse' of disturbing pleasure, bathing in sweat, cutting open the weak, watching them bleed, and relishing that the audience loves it precisely because it is disgusting.","src":"haiku"},
"harry-styles~sweet-creature":{"haiku":"Stubborn lovers figuring it out together; you ground each other when things get rough.","opus":"A tender portrait of a young, stubborn love that keeps drifting and arguing yet always leads back to each other as home. It's about belonging to someone even when you don't know where you're both headed.","sonnet":"Two young people in a stubborn, sometimes argumentative relationship keep finding their way back to each other — the other person is the single fixed point that brings the speaker home no matter how far they wander or how heated things get.","src":"haiku"},
"haru-nemuri~a-1pqk07c":{"haiku":"Total surrender of self as pieces to enable another's journey; love is the only absolute in existence.","src":"haiku","web":"Love as the one absolute certainty in an uncertain world, pouring everything of the self into another as an existential necessity rather than mere emotion.","sonnet":"The song's anchor is wanting to become a small piece of someone's heart — specifically the piece that lets them board the next train, get up and continue — and the speaker is willing to give everything they have to be that fragment. The memory at its center is a spring day when the very act of existing seemed to be enough, when the speaker's own being and the other person's being felt like pure blessing. Against the fear that the other person might lose the world again, the speaker insists: love is the only thing that is truly certain, and it does not stop when life is inconvenient.","opus":"The title's absolute claim, that nothing is more certain than love, is offered without irony as the song's bedrock, and Nemuri stakes it on a devotional gift, handing over all of herself to become one fragment of another person's heart so they can board a certain train. She dates her own rebirth to the spring rock and roll first loved her, when merely existing, thinking, and sleeping felt like blessing, and folds that into a claim that everything is my world and yours at once. The repetition drills the thesis past argument into liturgy, insisting that even on the night someone loses sight of the world, life and its blessing don't end."},
"haru-nemuri~a-1yx16nz":{"haiku":"Loving someone small whose life ends, eating their favorite fruit alone, bound to sing despite knowing birth and death are all that matter.","src":"haiku","web":"A tender farewell to a dead pet named Ringo, its title's 'apple' pun quietly hiding a small, real grief.","sonnet":"The song is a grief piece about someone who has died — possibly an animal, possibly a person held with animal tenderness — and the small impossible things left over: an apple bought because they liked it, too much of it for one person now. The speaker prays, despite knowing there is no one there to answer, and the prayer breaks into laughter at themselves. By morning the apartment has been flooded by a night of weeping and the speaker has to leave anyway, and what remains is the conviction that no life is born in order to become a song — yet the singing cannot stop.","opus":"An elegy for a small being whose language she never knew, the apple song measures love by a warmth barely above normal body heat and grief by the impossibility of finishing the fruit that being loved. Nemuri prays to a god she's sure isn't there, expecting to be laughed at, and lands on the plain unbearable fact that being born and dying is all it is, yet she still can't smile properly. The refusal at the end, that no life is born just to become a song and yet she can't help but sing, turns the whole thing into a confession about why she makes music at all: mourning that has nowhere to go but voice."},
"haru-nemuri~a-tb2i52":{"haiku":"Two people burning from the heat of impossible love, shattered promises, surrendering into becoming an ocean to sustain the other.","src":"haiku","web":"Grief transfigured into becoming the sea itself, dissolving the self to hold onto and keep alive everything lost in a loved one.","sonnet":"Two people have been hiding from life together in a room, stringing out a false lifeline, trying to become each other's eternity while knowing the arrangement is dishonest — the speaker keeps breaking their small promises and the other's face keeps disappearing in the smoke. The wish to become the other's forever coexists with the recognition that the whole thing has been a tawdry game that cost them their wings. At the bottom of that collapse the speaker asks to become the sea — to dissolve completely into something vast enough to hold the other person and keep them alive, even after everything that was wrong.","opus":"A song about failed intimacy that resolves into self-erasure as devotion, it opens in cigarette smoke and scorching wind, two people burned raw and unable to see each other's faces through the haze. Nemuri confesses the small betrayals that pile up like snow every time she ruins a pinky-promise, and the terrible flash of wishing to be killed she immediately apologizes for, locating love in guilt rather than comfort. The turn to becoming the sea, chanted after a wall of pure white, offers dissolution as the only way left to keep the other person alive, giving up her own shape so theirs can go on."},
"haru-nemuri~angry-angry":{"opus":"A feminist rage against a patriarchy where women are treated as the enemy and killed by men, refusing the platitude that mere survival should mean happiness, demanding to be called by true names, and channeling grief over murdered girls into fuel and unapologetic anger.","src":"opus","haiku":"Women's rage rewrites history until the world finally listens to its own pulse.","sonnet":"The song refuses the demand that surviving should be experienced as gratitude — the speaker names the enclosure of social norms, religious compliance, and domestic doctrine as the actual cause of the madness attributed to those who resist. Every voice peeled off and discarded is understood as potentially history-altering, and the song joins to a chorus of women naming themselves enemies in a society that has no room for them, describing the death of girls at the hands of men and the impossibility of staying quiet about it. Anger is not a failure but the only rational choice left."},
"haru-nemuri~bad-thing": {"src":"web","web":"A bratty, defiant rejection of fake sympathy and unsolicited pity — the lyrics tell off someone offering hollow concern (\"cry your crocodile tears\", \"spare me the worthlessness\") while also confronting bodily autonomy and the burden others project onto the singer. The track shifts from hyperpop bop into a murky shoegaze guitar collapse.","haiku":"A moment dissolves into aftermath, begging for even one second of happiness.","sonnet":"The speaker is ending something that was destructive — naming it plainly as the bad thing, the bad dream — and trying to move toward whatever a good dream might feel like. There is a moment of breakthrough approaching, but it arrives alongside contempt for the other person's self-pity, a refusal to absorb their tears or worthlessness any further. The closing lines shift into something more fragile: an open question about whether happiness could arrive in any given second, repeated simply, as if testing whether the word still works.","opus":"A breakup song that refuses the dignity of grief, it treats a soured relationship as raw material, a partial lyric traded away for a story worth telling. Nemuri weaponizes cliché on purpose, mailing performed weeping and a sense of worthlessness back to the person who dealt them, so the recycled pop phrases become a way of refusing to take the wound seriously. The recurring weather of a crawling sky and falling rain gives the kiss-off a storm to walk out into, and the closing question about whether any runner-up contentment is even on offer exposes how thin the promised comfort really was.","fable":"A breakup rendered in nursery-rhyme-flat verdicts that keep failing to close: the sentence slides from calling the other person the thing that went wrong, to a nightmare, to a wish for the softer sleep on its far side, so the sentence of dismissal never actually lands. Haru Nemuri builds the chorus out of imperatives that cancel each other — mocking the other's staged weeping while pleading to be saved, waving them off while begging to be spared — until the toughness reads as a performance too busy contradicting itself to be believed. The tell is the ending, where the swagger drops into a small unguarded want: to be told, plainly and without end, that some coming moment could still turn to gladness. Read straight, the contempt was armor; the song's real subject is the dependence underneath, exposed the moment the insults run out of breath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"contempt as armor, dependence exposed when the insults run out","built":"imperatives cancel each other until the toughness breaks as performance"},
"haru-nemuri~burn":{"src":"web","web":"Insomniac 3 a.m. mania reframed as vitality — where Fitzgerald's dark night of the soul becomes a soul that dances, screams and goes mad rather than dies, refusing meaning or value and choosing only to burn.","haiku":"Stillness shatters—a spirit dances in darkness, refusing the quiet anymore.","sonnet":"Someone who has been silent and hidden — crushed by the density and normative pressure of city life, feeling themselves already strayed from whatever path was intended — climbs to a rooftop where they are forbidden and contemplates bringing the whole sun crashing down. The song then becomes an address to that same hidden person from outside: a voice urges them to stop being quiet, to say what they need, to stop slipping away and stay present. The movement is from suffocating concealment toward an insistence that the soul, even dancing in the dark, is not invisible.","opus":"Built as a dare, the track keeps demanding that a silent listener name what they want, positioning Nemuri as someone climbing to a forbidden rooftop to shake another person out of numbness rather than to jump. The refrain about not staying quiet anymore reads as her own coming-out from years of muted compliance, and a recurring image of a spirit still moving through unlit rooms insists that going unseen is not the same as being erased. Where earlier Haru songs turned rage inward, this one turns it into an act of rescue aimed outward, coaxing someone else out of numbness by insisting their heat will finally catch.","fable":"The Japanese opening sets the trap the English chorus then torches: a body crammed into towers too tall to breathe in, walking roads flattened by norms, where the only things truly wanted are the ones forever out of reach. Nemuri — splitting the mic here with the New York duo Frost Children — stages her refusal as an ascent — sneaking onto a forbidden rooftop with the wish to bring the sun down right now — so that the burning of the chorus is not destruction but the self finally catching light after a lifetime of keeping quiet. The song's turn is that this fire is offered outward: the demand that a silent other name their own desire becomes an urging to stop concealing, to stay put, to be revived from within. Moving through shadow you are still not invisible; the soul spins its dance precisely there, and the burn is the two of them refusing, together, to be flattened into the crowd.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","identity & becoming","love & desire"],"means":"fire offered outward as an urging for a silent other to name their own desire","built":"Japanese verse sets the trap the English chorus then torches"},
"haru-nemuri~daijoubu-desu": {"src":"web","web":"The hollowness of saying 'I'm fine' — alienation and no safe place to belong, where surface reassurance masks a deeper insistence that nothing has a point.","haiku":"Twisted beyond recognition, a voice asks for witnesses to its collapse.","sonnet":"A person in acute psychological distress cycles between performing okayness for the world and admitting inwardly that they are not fine at all — the Japanese title phrase means something like reassurance, but the song tears it open from the inside. The body tenses without warning, cheap consolations of self-love and purchased comfort ring hollow, and the street feels populated by strangers no one can reach. By the end the speaker is confessing an immediate wish to vanish, even as the polite reassurance continues to surface like a reflex.","opus":"The whole song lives in the gap between a phrase of polite reassurance and its flat negation, the Japanese equivalent of saying I'm fine while meaning the opposite. Nemuri stacks a litany of no-thises into a mantra that keeps asking what the point even is, turning the reflexive courtesy of not troubling anyone into a slow suffocation, and the verse about self-love bought with money exposes wellness culture as another form of being placated. It is a panic attack rendered as call-and-response, where the manners meant to hold a person together are the exact thing that keeps them from screaming.","fable":"The title is a social reflex — the Japanese phrase you offer when someone asks if you're all right and you've decided not to burden them — and the whole song is what leaks out from behind that mask once it slips. Haru Nemuri opens by demanding a witness to how twisted she is, then collapses the plea into exhaustion: she keeps reaching for something and losing her grip on it, convinced the world has always handled her as damaged goods. The hook is the thesis and it turns on a brutal economy — nobody has the technique to haul a drowning spirit back up, and neither a transfusion nor a fat envelope will buy the rescue — salvation refused because there is no currency, kinship or cash, that covers the fare, and the line hardens from statement to shout as the refusal sinks in. What tips it from despair into Nemuri's characteristic defiance is the final swerve: the accusatory what do you want from me? snaps shut with a two-word instruction, an order to become her own physician and manufacture the tenderness in-house, folding the demand for rescue back onto the self. The read of the title, then, is not a lie but a program — I'm okay because I have stopped waiting to be saved from outside and made the cure my own responsibility.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"rescue refused, then folded back onto the self as program","built":"the title — a social lie — repurposed as a survival instruction"},
"haru-nemuri~destruction-sisters":{"src":"web","web":"Cathartic letting-loose — sisterhood in refusing societal norms, breaking free of what counts as 'normal' through sheer noise and release.","haiku":"Two souls set fire to everything designed to extinguish them.","sonnet":"The song is set in a cityscape built on invisible bones — concrete over hidden bodies, starved creatures scavenging the margins, lives discarded as waste without ceremony or song to mark them. The speaker addresses a companion who has also been treated as though their death never happened, and asks whose fault it is that they have been driven to the edge of madness — then refuses the premise that madness is theirs to own. The call is to go together and destroy everything that tries to kill them, bringing only the soul, leaving the rest to burn.","opus":"A city paved over the remains it refuses to acknowledge becomes the image for a civilization built by burying its own dead, and Nemuri's answer is a call to solidarity with anyone else marked for disposal. The invitation to bring only your soul strips the alliance down to its essence, refusing property and identity as the price of joining, while the demand to know who is to blame for the madness turns the diagnosis back onto a world that manufactures insanity and then indicts its victims. The sisterhood she conjures is less a gang than a shared vow to burn down everything designed to kill you, the fire outliving the arsonists."},
"haru-nemuri~don-t-call-me-queen":{"src":"web","web":"A protest against femicide and gender violence, refusing the pedestal of 'queen' as another cage and demanding a world without discrimination or oppression.","haiku":"A human breaking every crown placed on unwilling shoulders.","sonnet":"The song is a refusal of the pedestal — specifically the word queen, which the speaker hears not as praise but as a way of making her into something other than a person. Everyday life is grinding her HP down to nothing, labels are stuck on and need to be peeled off one by one, and the high heels in the lyric are named as things that were never meant for stepping on anyone. What she wants is simply to become herself, and the weakness that formed calluses along the way is claimed as hers too, not something to discard.","opus":"The refrain is a flat rejection of the pedestal: don't crown me, I'm a human being, which turns the language of empowerment against itself by refusing to be exalted into a symbol. Nemuri catalogs the exhaustion of a life that is nothing special, HP drained to nothing, and answers not with triumph but with the smaller, harder wish to become herself, peeling off every label stuck to her from the edges. The everyday chant about wanting to destroy the ordinary keeps the anger unpretty and unresolved, insisting that even the scab and the shed weakness underneath belong to her and no one else."},
"haru-nemuri~don-t-make-love-vow":{"src":"web","web":"A critique of marriage as an unequal institution that binds and polices love — asking why real love should need a contract or the state's endorsement."},
"haru-nemuri~fanfare":{"opus":"A fierce, tender declaration from the Japanese poetry-rapper that love and rage can sound the same trumpet. Kept alive by another person's gentleness, the narrator peels away numbing artifice to bleed something transparent and real. The refrain calls for a fanfare rung through the soil of life itself, insisting that mutual love in a single moment is enough to justify surviving.","src":"opus","fable":"Haru Nemuri builds the whole song out of one refrain — a fanfare of love and rage — and the two verses argue over what that fanfare is for. It opens by killing the self off, crows that rule the ground pecking apart what used to be me, so that the survival can be entirely borrowed: I can't die yet because you're kind enough to weep before I do, a tenderness that keeps arriving one beat ahead of the death it forestalls. The second verse names the enemy as the anaesthetic itself — a clumsy summer-homework diorama, soft parts glued shut, and the flat verdict that playing at naïveté saves no one — so the answer is to tear the seal off and let a transparent blood run, pain that doesn't affirm the self but at least confirms it exists. That's why the fanfare is love AND rage rather than either alone: it isn't triumph but the sound a body makes choosing to keep feeling, which is why she hands the melody away, begging you to sing it with your own emotion. A closing vow, sung in English, asks the fanfare to keep sounding through both their lives at once, turning the borrowed pulse of the opening into the song's actual mechanism — two lives keeping each other alive by refusing, out loud, to go numb.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"borrowed pulse of another's grief keeping the self alive","built":"the closing English vow turns the song's mechanism on the listener"},
"haru-nemuri~flee-from-the-sanctuary":{"src":"web","web":"Pent-up resentment toward a Christian schooling and its disciplinary power, erupting into punk as an escape from institutions that police the self.","haiku":"Stars burn at night so no judge can touch you; waiting alone at the chapel door.","sonnet":"A young woman has been shaped by an institution — religious and domestic — that taught her prayer was illegitimate, defined the good daughter as someone who cannot go anywhere, and left her carving wounds into herself in hidden places. The stars burning in the night sky are reimagined as protection against judgment, and the sanctuary she flees is both the chapel and the family home. At the end she is still waiting in that same place for someone she believes will come, suspended between the damage of the rules and the faint possibility of being found.","opus":"The sanctuary is a trap dressed as protection, a chapel of modest hemlines and curfews where a parent's picture of an obedient daughter is quietly lethal. Nemuri names the horror precisely: obedience as a slow death, so escape at night becomes the only survival, the burning stars reframed as refusal to let anyone sit in judgment. The scars she carves somewhere unreachable read as self-harm turned into the last private territory, and the closing vigil, the narrator keeping watch and believing someone will come, leaves the flight from safety haunted by the loneliness on its far side."},
"haru-nemuri~get-well-soon":{"src":"web","web":"A closing meditation on finishing something significant and letting go — the EP's shoegaze-washed final track carries the feeling of ruminating after a job well done, saying goodbye to a lost love, and arriving at a sense of accomplishment and completeness.","haiku":"Life persists soundlessly at the world's edge, where beauty wounds everything it touches.","sonnet":"The song holds life as something that simply persists regardless of whether it is called or broken — existence remembered even through damage. Standing at what feels like the edge of the world, the speaker has been touched by the certainty that beauty is real, and that recognition is both a rescue and a bewildering burden. A second voice answers with the gift of soul, the exchange forming a kind of fragile shelter against the noise coming in from the horizon.","opus":"The refrain that life is simply here whether or not it is called, whether or not it breaks, works as a stubborn baseline against despair, an existence that persists without needing permission or purpose. Nemuri touches the edge of another person and locates beauty at the far end of the world, framing that meeting as both salvation and the reason for standing frozen. Lulu's guest refrain, crediting the other for handing over a whole spirit, turns the song into a duet of mutual resuscitation, so the title reads less as a get-well card than a vow to keep each other existing."},
"haru-nemuri~harutosyura":{"haiku":"Meeting of two solitudes births universes; one's voice is spring wind, one's heart frozen, but song transcends death and illuminates the other.","src":"haiku","web":"Loneliness meeting another and birthing a whole universe, spring wind becoming voice — renewal and conflict fused in the image of spring and the warrior-demon Asura.","sonnet":"The song is about two loneliness touching and briefly generating something as large as a universe — an instant of contact between two isolated people that produces its own cosmos. The speaker identifies their voice with the first strong wind of spring, a force that reaches a frozen heart and makes it weep, and insists that even after death the life in that voice cannot be extinguished. The movement is toward telling the other person to keep shining, to claim the universe through their own illumination, because the song itself cannot be silenced by anyone.","opus":"The title fuses spring with Miyazawa Kenji's asura, the season of renewal welded to a Buddhist figure of raging suffering, and that collision is the song's whole engine: her voice is the first warm wind of spring, capable of making a song bloom even on a frozen star. Nemuri frames a meeting of two solitudes as the birth of a blink-sized universe, then insists that even if she dies, life won't be extinguished and the song can't be drowned out. The repeated command to shine blue turns grief into light, an argument that love, once kindled, keeps sounding like a spring that never stops."},
"haru-nemuri~i-refuse":{"src":"web","web":"Hardcore-punk feminist defiance — a flat refusal of imposed 'normalcy' and systemic violence, survival turned into an act of resistance for everyone the system leaves behind.","haiku":"Standing soaked in mediocrity, a voice tears away every label the world carved on.","sonnet":"The speaker is being pinned under the gaze of someone who insists they could never know what is true, and under a world that keeps sticking labels of value onto a life that was never asked for. The wet asphalt smell is cloying and inescapable, the road smoothed down by conformity, and the speaker knows they cannot live on vagueness — a small, stubborn lucidity is the very thing that makes surrender impossible. The refrain of refusal accumulates until it breaks into an image of tearing through the order of things, destroying and rebuilding the world in the same breath.","opus":"The verb of the title is the entire spine: refusal as the last sovereign act available to a body being crucified by other people's gaze and priced by their valuations. Nemuri hates the smell of wet asphalt because it stands for a lukewarm world that would swallow her whole, and she credits her survival to being just barely not a fool, just clear-eyed enough to say no. The bracketed chant of boring through providence and dragging the void toward breaking and remaking the world reframes refusal not as retreat but as a demolition that clears ground for something else."},
"haru-nemuri~inferno":{"src":"web","web":"Sorrow at being unable to save a loved one, walking together in pain and parting without ever saying goodbye, clinging to the hope of meeting again.","haiku":"Bones become the foundation; prayers pour like flame from a sky that was already burning.","sonnet":"The song insists that the world has been hell for a very long time already — not a future punishment but a present condition that people simply have not named. Individual lives in it are like scavenging for the bones of the dead in a ruined city, pouring life and prayer into a situation beyond consolation. The loneliness is so absolute that even a cry of rage gets swallowed by whatever song is currently popular, and the body that might once have been an angel is described as already past the point of return.","opus":"Nemuri relocates hell to the ordinary present, a trash city where the remains of the forgotten dead keep getting gathered up and stuffed away, and insists the fire has been raining down unnoticed since further back than anyone admits. The most cutting turn is that any scream of murderous rage gets drowned by a chart-topping song, so even protest is absorbed and neutralized by the culture that produced the misery. A frame that once might have belonged to an angel can no longer return to that state, which makes the emptying of life and prayer into the flames less a plea for rescue than a way of burning on purpose inside a place already lost."},
"haru-nemuri~inori-dake-ga-aru":{"haiku":"Suffering and rage crystallize within narrow confinement where only prayer remains, the narrator clawing at limitation while seeking transcendence.","opus":"In a rotting dystopia of endless war and lost, homeless children, the singer writhes with rage in a narrow room where all that remains is prayer. A scarred soul sings a chorale for a lost god, self-harm made visible with a rusted blade, weeping like the day it was born because it cannot name this feeling.","sonnet":"In a narrow room inside a failing dystopia, the speaker holds onto rage and pain — carving marks with a rusty cutter, surviving without being able to die — and finds that prayer alone remains, the last anchor in a world where wars are born and sounds disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"haru-nemuri~let-it-go-as-if-you-wander":{"opus":"A stark meditation on the impossibility of purity in a living body. The narrator confesses wanting to die for the sake of truly beautiful things, since merely being alive means being defiled. Refusing the promise of any paradise, the track exalts the stray soul, urging it to keep wandering unredeemed rather than settle for a false heaven.","src":"opus"},
"haru-nemuri~lostplanet":{"haiku":"Two adolescents fleeing into a shared private universe, pretending not to understand the outside world, searching for god in a burning planet.","src":"haiku","web":"Pretending not to know until the pretense becomes real, yet still holding a buried knowledge of the sea — willful forgetting shadowed by loss.","sonnet":"Two teenagers share a world of shared earphones and secret signals, painting their version of the galaxy baby-blue, always searching for something that might be a real god — a private cosmology assembled to survive a place that otherwise feels uninhabitable. The osmanthus flower scent in the classroom, the split heartbeat through a wire, these are the coordinates of a moment that cannot be preserved. The song arrives at the recognition that they cannot dissolve into each other no matter how tightly they hold on, and turns that toward a plea: let's still tell each other meaningless things, let's still look for love at the edge of the lost planet.","opus":"A nostalgia song disguised as science fiction, it rides a UFO's orbit to stay forever nineteen, painting the galaxy baby blue while two teenagers hide a metal heart in a classroom scented with osmanthus. Nemuri knows the escape is a lie she chose, admitting that pretending not to understand eventually made her understand nothing, and that no amount of embracing lets two people truly melt together. The turn comes when a scorched field teaches her to feel her own life for the first time, so the flight from reality curdles into a search for love at the edge of the lost planet, dreams more certain than any daydream."},
"haru-nemuri~lovetheism":{"haiku":"Rejecting inherited ideology to feel moment-by-moment; love discovered through practice, not gifted—finding god within, dancing messily through reborn worlds.","src":"haiku","web":"Faith rebuilt around love as the only thing worth believing — 'trust nothing but love' waged not as comfort but as a hard, repeated battle to accept that love simply exists.","sonnet":"The song begins inside a very particular kind of desperation: a scrap torn from a bible with a death wish scrawled on it, kept hidden in a school uniform's chest pocket alongside a private sense of self-protection. Invisible authoritarians and the grinding routines they produce form the walls of the speaker's world, until they decide to discard the inherited way of thinking and simply feel — grief, joy, ideology, all earned through sensation rather than assigned from outside. Love is not a gift handed down but something found, and every time it is found the speaker will sing it.","opus":"The coined title, loving the ism itself, is her replacement theology, built from the shocking image of scrawling die on a torn page of scripture and the sanctuary of a uniform's breast pocket that guards only the self. Nemuri diagnoses a fascism of the ordinary, a baby-faced Hitler teaching what normal life looks like, and against it she proposes discarding the given brain to feel each moment fresh, gaining sorrow and joy and thought by choosing them. Rebirth remakes the world each time because neither love nor you nor I is a mere gift, so the answer to false religion is to dance the whole thing in messy, self-chosen steps."},
"haru-nemuri~make-more-noise-of-you":{"haiku":"The search for a life-signal louder than heartbeat in a world where miracles fail and god watches helplessly.","src":"haiku","web":"Grief at someone who slipped away without warning curdling into defiance — miracles and gods fail, so the only answer is to burn loud and make more noise of your own existence.","sonnet":"Someone has slipped through and is gone — passed by so quickly that the speaker is still searching for a sound more certain than a heartbeat, something that proves life was there. The world's sadness is vast and the speaker can only stare at it and cry, watching rain become sea, watching a gentle god do nothing about the gunfire that never stops. The song resolves into a command directed outward: make as much noise as you can, burn your life into sound, because the speaker is offering everything they have and the heart between them needs to keep beating.","opus":"The command is a theology in miniature: this planet's god is very kind and can do absolutely nothing, love does not conveniently rain down, the gunfire never stops, so salvation has to be self-generated by making your own life audible. Nemuri writes it as elegy and exhortation at once, mourning someone who slipped away too easily while insisting the end of the world won't arrive on your behalf, which is exactly why you must sound your life now. The offer to give every last piece of herself, from a heart shared between two people, makes the noise a transfusion rather than a solo scream."},
"haru-nemuri~narashite":{"haiku":"Distortion between two people living twisted daily realities, calling to be reshaped and merged with one's pulse in a corrupted world.","src":"haiku","web":"Emotional trauma and urban despair channeled into sound, where distortion and 'letting it ring out' become a survival cry against numbness and the pull of the Chuo Line.","sonnet":"The daily life has warped — dreams bleed into waking, a love that should have ended long ago still swells painfully, a door that might have opened onto the train tracks stayed closed. The speaker addresses someone who cannot yet cry about it, someone half-numb, half-haunted, and tells them: ring out, right now, put all of it — the tears, the ruined days — into the sound. The distortion becomes the medium through which a pulse is shared, the speaker and the other person described as having split a single heart between them, and the instruction is simply to let it beat.","opus":"Sound it, ring it out now, she keeps demanding, treating distortion as the medium through which a numbed person's tears and days can finally be discharged. The half-hearted anesthesia and half-hearted fantasy she names, alongside the almost-confession about not managing to jump in front of a train, locate the song in a very specific modern despair, the survivor who couldn't even complete their own vanishing. Turning that into distortion pulsing through a shared heart reframes noise itself as proof of life, a way of ringing out an existence that had gone silent and warped."},
"haru-nemuri~never-let-you-go":{"src":"web","web":"Holding on through nameless, endless sorrow — childhood wounds and hidden hurt met with a contradictory love that insists there is still light here.","haiku":"Even at 2 AM when the world screams, one light refuses to go dark.","sonnet":"The song is addressed to someone who has been carrying old wounds — a fragment of the heart hidden away since childhood when a mother screamed, tears shed for a classroom that destroyed something — and the speaker holds all of it rather than asking the person to set it down. At 2 a.m. the whole world's screaming becomes audible and there is no technique for blocking it out, life is not convenient in any way, and yet a song persists: a light still burning despite the contradiction, offered not as rescue but as proof that living is happening. The closing plea is to keep the flame from going out and to not let go.","opus":"Addressed to someone the world tries to kill, whether by a screaming mother or a murderous classroom, the song holds that a shard of hidden heart kept from those wounds is still worth refusing to throw away. Nemuri locates the crisis at two in the morning, the hour a body most wants to vanish, and offers not a cure but a contradiction-riddled love that stubbornly stays lit. The spoken loop about needing to know who and what you are, borrowed like an incantation, turns the plea of the title into a survival instruction: breathing in and out until it becomes a song is the whole technology of staying alive."},
"haru-nemuri~nineteen":{"opus":"An elegy to a gentle nineteen-year-old the speaker loved, who wept for the world's sorrow and kept singing though the world swallowed him ragged each night; that love is declared eternal, a never-ceasing pulse burning on inside the speaker's heart.","src":"opus","haiku":"Eternal heartbeat—one person loved so completely they live forever inside another's chest.","sonnet":"The song is a retrospective love addressed to someone met at nineteen — someone whose gentleness was overwhelming, whose capacity for grief at the deaths of strangers exceeded anything the speaker could reach. Through knowing them, the speaker found a part of themselves they had never located before, described as finding their true self at the edge of a galaxy in the moment when that person wept for someone torn apart by the world. The love is called eternal not because it was perfect but because it keeps beating inside the speaker's chest, present tense, after everything.","fable":"An elegy for a love met at nineteen, sung entirely in the past tense and yet refusing to let the past close: the refrain keeps landing on it was love, and it was eternal, then converts that grief into a physical present by locating a pulse that will not stop beating inside the singer's own heart. Haru Nemuri makes the grammar do the mourning: she speaks of herself as watashi, but the beloved who sang every night while the world swallowed them whole, who was gentler than anyone alive, survives as the boku inside me, the masculine I she says she found at the far edge of the galaxy. So the dead person is not remembered so much as re-housed, their voice becoming a second self that keeps living in the mourner's body and even in her choice of pronoun. The screamed stack of it was love near the end is the read's hinge: repetition here isn't emphasis but resuscitation, a survivor pounding the same syllables until a past-tense death starts sounding like an ongoing heartbeat.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","love & desire","identity & becoming"],"means":"resuscitating the dead by repeating their name","built":"pronoun switch re-houses the beloved inside the mourner's grammar"},
"haru-nemuri~no-pain":{"src":"web","web":"Existential rejection of consumer society and the pricing of souls — no reason to be born, no world worth buying, spitting anger at a tasteless, copy-paste culture."},
"haru-nemuri~no-pain-no-gain-is-shit":{"haiku":"Pain does not validate existence; the speaker rejects the myth that suffering earns worth or meaning in a fundamentally alienating world.","opus":"A furious rejection of the doctrine that suffering must be earned or redemptive, written by someone who only feels real when pain reaches the bone. She rages that bleeding is called defilement and scars are branded as guilt, screaming that no-pain-no-gain is garbage.","sonnet":"A rejection of the idea that suffering builds character — the narrator has been holding themselves together through bone-deep pain, carrying a body and a life they didn't choose, and screams that the 'no pain, no gain' philosophy is bullshit when the pain is this real.","src":"haiku"},
"haru-nemuri~pink-unicorn":{"haiku":"Rejecting savior mythology; living beauty and emotion known from poverty outranks sterile morality—feeling is the only imperative.","src":"haiku","web":"Refusing to be made anyone's god or idol, rejecting institutional morality in favor of the raw beauty and emotion found in real, imperfect lives.","sonnet":"The song positions the speaker as something mythic but insists she refuses to be turned into anyone's divinity — the pink unicorn is a self-given image that resists being flattened into a god or a symbol for someone else's use. The world around her is one of war and massacre, of processed feeling and manufactured ecstasy, and against that she argues that real beauty is known to those who grew up with nothing, that true emotion cannot be sterilized. The refrain is a hard boundary: I will not be your god.","opus":"Dated 2020 and studded with war and massacre, the song is Nemuri's protest against being made into anyone's idol or messiah, the pink unicorn begging not to be turned into your god. She attacks the way sensibility gets counterfeited and consumed, the lukewarm ecstasy of a savior located only inside oneself, and answers with a chant that makes emotion and thought each a matter of survival, the only alternative being extinction. The image of a boy from the ghetto who knows beauty and a love that can't survive a sterile room sharpens her real target: a sanitized, moralized culture that mistakes numbness for virtue while the world burns."},
"haru-nemuri~riot":{"haiku":"Song as salvation against suicidal impulse and witnessed violence; insisting kindness and music sustain life even as apocalypse approaches.","src":"haiku","web":"Inner struggle between gentleness and darker impulses, insisting tenderness and song endure through violence and apocalypse — nihilistic observation flipping into defiant hope.","sonnet":"The speaker wants to be gentle, and names this as the reason they feel implicated in every act of violence they read about — the person who opened fire, the person who jumped in front of the train, are understood as fragments of the same continuum of pain the speaker carries. Despite this, or because of it, the song insists that the music will survive the speaker's death and go on to meet whoever needs it, that a single moment of awakening makes the speaker real again. The final image is of singing on even after everything on earth has burned — and imagining the other person laughing at how foolish that is.","opus":"The riot she calls for begins from an unexpected premise of radical identification: the shooter on television and the person who jumped in front of the morning train were both, in some sense, her, so the uprising is against a world that produces such deaths rather than against any individual. Nemuri wants to become gentle and so simply decides to, insisting the song will remain even after her life runs out, meeting a listener who becomes life itself. Her apocalyptic thought experiment, rain turning to bullets, Adam and Eve not surviving, only sharpens the point that even a tiny gleam god overlooks is enough to keep her singing, kindness itself the true riot."},
"haru-nemuri~rock-n-roll-wa-shinanai-with-totsuzenshounen":{"haiku":"Rock and roll endures as defiant permanence through velocity and connection, the narrator surrendering to music's transformative power across temporal collapse.","opus":"A defiant hymn that rock and roll will not die, one kiss in the dark transforming a lost soul while the singer devotes each second to another's fleeting life. Grief for a wrecked planet and people who won't survive the day turns into a leap into tomorrow, life offered up second by second.","sonnet":"Rock and roll is declared imperishable — faster than Einstein's relativity, alive in the single second that a kiss transforms a lost figure in the dark — while the speaker pledges their body to that one moment and sings to a broken earth they hope to hold and heal.","src":"haiku"},
"haru-nemuri~sekaiwotorikaeshiteokure":{"haiku":"Plea to recover stolen world through personal solitude; god dies but the planet continues, and joy and sorrow belong only to the self.","src":"haiku","web":"A demand to take back the world — loneliness turned into ownership of existence, screaming through the smoke of a burning town that meaning is ours to make even if God dies.","sonnet":"The song is a repeated, almost liturgical demand to get the world back — addressed outward as a plea and inward as a rallying cry. The speaker traces a line from the day they first loved their own solitude, through the death of god (which ended nothing), through all the ways the world has been used up and discarded, and arrives at the claim that no one's weakness or loneliness is anyone else's to give value to. The final section is an insistence addressed to whoever is listening: you are alive right now in this moment, and that fact demands to be shouted aloud.","opus":"The chant to take the world back, baby, is Nemuri at her most explicitly political and cosmic at once: the world only became hers on the day she loved her own loneliness, and even the day god died, it didn't end. She scatters the diagnosis across the whole surface, that this planet births us used-to-disposability, that battlefields are everywhere, that we scapegoat god and each other for missiles and murdered children. The reclamation she calls for is not conquest but ownership of one's own joy and grief, a demand that each person scream that they are alive right here, right now, as the first act of taking anything back."},
"haru-nemuri~supernatural": {"src":"web","web":"An affirmation that people are already extraordinary without needing external rites of passage or societal approval to become \"fully-fledged\" — Haru Nemuri stated the song's idea is that unlike Spider-Man requiring a spider bite, we need no such initiation because we are super amazing as we already are. The song functions as a direct address of radical self-acceptance.","haiku":"Just being yourself becomes radical when the world demands you burn away.","sonnet":"The song asserts that existing as oneself — unmediated, without the distorting pressure of others' projections or gaslighting — is already a supernatural state. The speaker describes the daily exhaustion of simply being alive, the way hostility and discarded emotions pollute the air, yet insists that the soul overflows even from a shed skin. The refrain becomes a collective declaration: we are already fabulous, already beyond ordinary, and the only threat to that is someone else's bite.","opus":"The joke and the thesis are the same: you are already extraordinary as you are, no divine lightning required. Nemuri flirts with the language of glamour and myth only to strip it, insisting the fabulousness is native and needs no gaslight to prop it up, which lets the phrase about not biting me double as a warning against anyone who would consume that self. Underneath the strut is her recurring conviction that simply existing without apology is the supernatural act, and the fatigue of dirtied wings is the cost of being alive rather than proof of failure.","fable":"A love song that can only hold its own thought for one verse before rapture wrecks the grammar. The first half is coherent and almost pleading — leaving together, a hunger for someone that leaves no room to breathe, a struck spark drawn out into forever — and it names that feeling with the only word big enough, then stutters on the word's own tail until it stops meaning and starts pulsing, -ral, -ral, like a nervous system misfiring. In the second half the sentences shatter: phrases collide and break off mid-word, don't-bite-me and we're-fabulous fused into one breathless run that no longer parses. Nemuri's method across Soul Kiss is to let feeling overload the language carrying it, and here the point is exactly that collapse — the supernatural isn't described but performed, as the moment a person is too much in love to finish a sentence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"feeling so total the language carrying it shatters mid-word","built":"the title word stuttered until it stops meaning, starts pulsing"},
"haru-nemuri~surviving-is-resistance":{"src":"web","web":"Refusal to surrender to a system built on heteronormative violence, where simply staying alive becomes a defiant political act.","haiku":"Each scar becomes proof—a body refusing to disappear into the ordinary.","sonnet":"The song is about the slow erasure of a person's selfhood through accumulated wounds and imposed roles — queen, muse, mother — and the act of survival as the only form of refusal available. The blood that is shed keeps getting diluted in the morning light, made to seem as if it never happened, and the body that has been refused has nowhere to return to. Against this the speaker insists on a bare, undecorated identity: not a symbol or a function but simply herself, and the fact of remaining alive is the resistance.","opus":"The title states the politics plainly: for a body already refused and priced, continued existence is itself an act of defiance. Nemuri peels names off herself to arrive at something unlabeled, rejecting three assigned roles at once, sovereign and inspiration and parent, as so many cages of femininity, and she watches shed blood get diluted into a sunrise as the mechanism by which suffering is made to look erased after the fact. The recurring worry about how long she can stay herself while she is slowly worn down turns survival into a running arithmetic, where staying insane and unhealed is the honest response to a world that closes wounds by pretending they were never opened."},
"haru-nemuri~tokyo-calling":{"opus":"Two lovers who wanted their small room to be their whole true world are forced to confront reality, the open window revealing sky and the litany of atomic war, radiation, and earthquakes; rather than hide, they resolve to destroy the wonderland and stand firmly on this planet together.","src":"opus"},
"haru-nemuri~underground":{"haiku":"Love as final weapon; couple's merged heartbeat becomes sacred, song as proof and prayer of shared life.","src":"haiku","web":"Love as what makes existence real, collecting a lover's tears like prayer and burning one's life force to forge something eternal.","sonnet":"The speaker declares that the other person's love and grief have made them real — not in a sentimental sense but as an act of proof: your existence confirms mine, and mine confirms yours. Two people in close presence discover that their shared gaze creates a universe, a record of life that will last only one night but is entirely sufficient. The vow at the center is that the speaker will keep singing to testify to the other's reality, treating their single life as something worth praying for.","opus":"Cast as someone's ultimate weapon without knowing why, Nemuri builds a private cosmos out of gathered tears and a shared heartbeat, insisting that simply looking into each other's eyes turns a room into outer space. The underground is where she asks to be taught how to breathe and to burn her life down, an intimacy that exists below and outside the sanctioned world. The claim that another person's love made her real, and that her singing proves that you exists, reframes devotion as an act of mutual verification, one flickering record of life set against a night that would erase it."},
"haru-nemuri~yoruwooyoideta":{"haiku":"Two swimmers navigate black ocean searching for heaven, hearts hollowed by shared loneliness. They grasp each other beneath waves, understanding that union mirrors solitude—together remains alone.","opus":"An aching nocturne about two lonely souls swimming through darkness, searching for a single heaven. The narrator finds a companion sadder than anyone, and their intimacy reveals that in clutching each other they were really holding themselves. Set against a scorched, missile-struck world where god shatters, the song makes one fleeting moment of shared solitude enough to keep living.","sonnet":"Swimming through the night looking for a single piece of heaven, the speaker finds someone standing in the light who is the loneliest person in the world — they share their hollow interiors, pledge over rock-and-roll, and half-split a heart — only to realise they were each hugging their own solitude; after missiles shatter god, the wonder of that one person persists as the speaker's eternal anchor.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two people swim through the same night looking for the smallest possible heaven, and Haru Nemuri's cruelest, truest line lands mid-song when she admits the embrace was a mistake in reverse: we thought we were holding each other, but we were each holding ourselves. That confession would sink a lesser song, but here it becomes the redemption, because the wonder-wonder chorus rings out on a pitch-black sea and discovers that the loneliness was never yours or mine but one shared thing — you were calling to god too, we are clutching the same solitude. The apocalyptic swerve seals the logic: a missile hits the half-asleep world and god shatters, which means no external salvation is coming and the only heaven left is that both of you were ringing out into the dark at once. So the ending refuses the consolation of forever — don't sit around wondering whether we could have stayed together — and keeps instead the single instant, insisting that this one flash is what lets me survive, and that I can stay the loneliest person alive and stay beautiful precisely by staying that lonely. The read of the whole piece is that connection isn't the cure for isolation but its only honest form: not two solitudes cancelling out, but two people making the same noise into the same night and calling that, defiantly, wonder.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire","faith & the occult"],"means":"shared solitude held as the only honest form of connection","built":"the god-shattering pivot seals off external salvation and makes the instant enough"},
"hatebreed~i-will-be-heard":{"haiku":"The speaker rises from a state of victimhood and betrayal to reclaim agency, determined to be heard and fulfill the promises he made to himself.","opus":"Connecticut metalcore crew Hatebreed convert personal defeat into motivational fuel, the narrator hauling himself upright, wiping away scorn and grief, and resolving to reclaim his life. Betrayed and misled but refusing to quit, he sets out to keep the vows he broke, and the pounding insistence that his voice won't be silenced turns a private struggle into collective endurance against the worst life throws.","sonnet":"A survivor of betrayal and self-compromise rises from the floor determined to reclaim agency. Past humiliations and broken promises are named without self-pity; what replaces them is a single, repeated commitment — to be heard, to make good, to reverse the dynamic that silenced this voice for so long.","src":"haiku"},
"havayoth~dreaming":{"opus":"A sultry midsummer night of yearning and fantasy, the narrator flinging signals into the dark and calling out, haunted by visions of a woman in pale clothes amid heavy perfume and a charged, humming space.","src":"opus"},
"have-a-nice-life~bloodhail":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic imagery of roofs blown off and stars burning as the speaker watches indifferently. Violence and death merge with senseless repetition and resignation.","opus":"Cult Connecticut duo Have a Nice Life stage a numbed apocalypse in which the narrator watches the world come apart while faking concern he admits he never felt, insisting no one truly does. The dirge curdles into grim relief that it's finally ending, their lo-fi drone and shoegaze making Deathconsciousness an underground touchstone.","sonnet":"Emotional detachment pushed to its nihilistic extreme — the speaker watches catastrophe and burning stars with performed indifference, knowing no one truly cares and that everything endures only as long as it possibly can before tearing. Relief at its ending is the closest thing to feeling on offer, the only honest response to a world of gnashing and ripping that was never going to last.","src":"haiku"},
"have-a-nice-life~deep-deep":{"haiku":"The loneliness of love and existence; atoms and people are liars; the explosive power of unmet emotional need. Faith and righteousness cannot ease the fundamental solitude and guilt of being alive.","opus":"Have A Nice Life, a lo-fi post-punk duo whose vast, reverb-soaked production buries dread in noise, ponder deceptive matter and art chased as escape from a private misery no one notices. Dancers orbit a room craving more until the craving evaporates, and the narrator, wondering whether anyone shares his guilt, frames loneliness as a detonation and asks why love feels so isolating.","sonnet":"Loneliness is examined at a molecular level — atoms unaware of each other, water cycling through evaporation, one body reaching for another at a dance. The question of why love remains so isolated amid so much proximity spirals outward from the personal to the cosmic, landing on guilt without a clear source and a reckoning that needs to come before it is too late.","src":"haiku"},
"have-a-nice-life~hunter":{"haiku":"Submission to predation becomes sacrifice; the narrator welcomes death at a hunter's hand, believing consumption is their earned fate.","opus":"A bleak surrender to violence and predation, the narrator laid out and offering his flesh to the hunter, framing submission to cruelty as service and even deliverance, a dread-soaked reckoning with how willingly the wounded lie down for death.","sonnet":"A prey animal addresses the hunter with complete, exhausted surrender — take everything, wear the skin, eat the bones — framing the submission not as defeat but as a strange, willing release, suggesting that the hunted have done enough to deserve what comes for them.","src":"haiku"},
"have-a-nice-life~i-don-t-love":{"haiku":"Numb where love should be; going through the motions of a life you don't feel.","opus":"A crushing portrait of depressive numbness — the blank where feeling ought to be, and a fatigue that shades toward not wanting to go on. The deadness is so complete that even grief would count as relief.","sonnet":"A speaker confronts total emotional vacancy — not wanting to live this way, not wanting to perform feeling, and finding only emptiness where love is supposed to be; the song sits in the hollow without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"havok~don-t-do-it":{"haiku":"Suicidal despair consumes the speaker after abandonment; isolation calcifies into fatalistic resolve, while a distant voice pleads against the inevitable.","opus":"A suicidal spiral voiced from inside, hollowed out and abandoned by everyone, met at the last second by a countering voice pleading against it. The song stages the argument between the pull to finish it and the desperate refusal to let that happen.","sonnet":"A suicide note told from inside the act: the speaker feels invisible, dismissed, and consumed by misery, telling someone they \"weren't there for me\" as a goodbye — and the repeated counter-cry of \"don't do it\" arrives as an external voice pulling against the narrative's momentum.","src":"haiku"},
"hawk~mileage":{"haiku":"Worn out by accumulated burdens inherited from others, the speaker struggles with velocity and panic, feeling overwhelmed by responsibilities and depression.","opus":"A defiant, wounded self-sketch in which the rapper looks down on status symbols and crowds while trusting nothing but his own read on things. Exhausted and hauling weight that never felt like his to carry, he questions his direction, watches allies turn hostile, and treats existence as a puzzle designed for the low.","sonnet":"A figure accumulated by experience to the point of exhaustion — carrying weight that feels inherited rather than earned, unable to sit still without panicking. Raised by a pilot, he understands motion as survival; stopping feels like falling. The world keeps misreading him, but he sees through it with what he calls telepathy earned through overuse.","src":"haiku"},
"haywyre~insight":{"haiku":"A repeated assertion that all phenomena lie beyond human comprehension and understanding—an epistemological declaration of universal inscrutability.","opus":"A single vocal fragment repeated to the point of mantra, insisting everything lies outside our understanding, the phrase looping until it dissolves into rhythmic material rather than argument. Haywyre is the project of a classically trained pianist working in electronic music, where jazz-inflected keys and glitchy production carry the weight, so the lone recurring line functions as a hook and texture inside an instrumental frame rather than a full lyric.","src":"haiku"},
"haywyre~never-count-on-me":{"haiku":"A voice second-guesses every word spoken and the speaker has lost themselves completely. They ask others not to trust them.","opus":"An electronic confession of self-doubt and dissolving identity, a narrator second-guessing himself and feeling so lost he warns others not to rely on him; the hook is an act of honest self-protection dressed as failure.","sonnet":"A self-aware warning from someone who feels completely lost — a voice in their own head second-guessing everything — asking not to be relied on because they no longer trust themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"he-is-legend~boogiewoman":{"haiku":"Desolation transforms into partnership; two broken figures become companions in wasteland, accepting each other's fractures as the only honesty available.","opus":"A post-apocalyptic love story across a scorched, dead earth, two figures pledging to face the badlands hand in hand as the world dies around them. Named psychiatric syndromes thread through it, marking a mind unraveling amid grief, thirst and the certainty that only death is sure.","sonnet":"A desolate, post-apocalyptic landscape — dirt, dust, Stockholm Syndrome, charred houses, Capgras Syndrome — is redeemed by a mutual pact between two monsters of the badlands: \"I'll be your Boogiewoman if you'll be my Boogieman,\" love offered in the idiom of the freakish and the scorched.","src":"haiku"},
"he-is-legend~call-ins":{"haiku":"Internal haunting persists as unresolved torment; the speaker cannot expel the demon that mirrors her own face.","opus":"A man haunted by his own reflection, the ghost in the mirror revealed to be himself, trapped so long as that trapped self stays free, a claustrophobic image of self-imprisonment where the only release is being unable to escape yourself.","sonnet":"A man confronts the figure trapped in his own mirror — a ghost he cannot free because its liberation is bound to his own, and every haunted space he occupies turns out to be haunted by himself. The external and internal cannot be separated; the prison is self-constructed.","src":"haiku"},
"he-is-legend~lifeless-lemonade":{"opus":"A wry Southern-tinged fable addressing a weary warrior coaxed out of grand crusades toward something smaller and duller. The band trades heroic fantasy for the flat comfort of lifeless lemonade, a self-aware shrug at diminished ambition. Beneath the whimsy runs the ache of a man who has everything yet breaks everything, drained of appetite for the epic.","src":"opus"},
"he-is-legend~sour":{"haiku":"The promised hero crumbles and fails, leaving followers exhausted but unbroken, hunting for the abandoned pages of a broken covenant. The search itself becomes the only remaining faith.","opus":"A dreamlike summons to a reluctant chosen one who has failed his own promises. Drifting nameless and shapeless through a thinning sense of time, the narrator is roused by others begging for a new savior. The band wraps disillusionment in mythic language, insisting the burden of being written into the role falls on someone who has already broken his vows.","sonnet":"A community calls out to a fallen hero who made promises and broke them, tore pages from the story they were supposed to live; they are worn but not finished, and they're searching for a new savior written somewhere in the wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"he-is-legend~the-prowler":{"opus":"A predatory, ravenous seducer voicing an appetite that could rip the world open, luring a partner down into the city to pull it apart stone by stone and dance down the avenues leaving no wound unlicked, devouring desire fused with unholy, blasphemous swagger.","src":"opus"},
"he-is-legend~the-seduction":{"haiku":"A speaker navigates mysterious urban intrigue involving a woman, fashion, and danger, using literary seduction metaphors and castle mythology to describe connection.","opus":"The Southern-tinged rock band spins a feverish, allusion-heavy seduction, its narrator obsessed with a woman who's disappeared, comparing her to biblical and Shakespearean figures amid cryptic messages and refusals. Literary and video-game nods collide as he pursues a prize forever just out of reach, with danger and longing fusing into a morbid fantasy of a beautiful shared death.","sonnet":"A chaotic narrator pursues an elusive woman through surreal, fragmented scenes — Juliet and Jezebel overlapping, city calls unanswered, buildings jumped and streets slept in. The obsession is theatrical and unstable, desire pitched somewhere between romantic pursuit and self-aware absurdity, never quite landing in a real encounter.","src":"haiku"},
"headcave~gathering-silence":{"haiku":"Numbness and coldness accumulate until the world offers nothing worth taking, and the only honest stance is to survive and outrun the forces closing in, even as suffocation becomes a near certainty.","opus":"A suffocating meditation on numbness and futile flight in a world already ended. The narrator looks back on collapse while everything grows colder, insisting the world offers nothing and takes everything. Survival advice curdles into dread, as running only leads to the place where breath runs out and silence itself becomes the thing that smothers.","sonnet":"Survival under relentless, unnamed threat — a world gone cold, the imperative not to breathe too many times, the instruction to run while knowing running is never enough. The narrator looks back at numbness during collapse and forward into suffocation, caught between fighting and simply enduring.","src":"haiku"},
"headcave~half-alive":{"haiku":"Two people are stuck in a cycle of pain and codependence, numb and disconnected yet clinging to each other; they find perverse comfort in suffering together.","opus":"Headcave circle a self-destructive craving in which people barely present in their own lives find a compulsive thrill in suffering, drawn to and terrified by their own feelings. Amid imagery of consuming what he desires and being dragged under, the narrator numbs himself, casts himself as a captive awaiting the end, and corrupts himself willingly in a defiant surrender to his own darkness.","sonnet":"Two people hold each other in a state of mutual damage — drawing comfort from pain, fearing their own feeling, dragging each other back into fire. The narrator oscillates between the desire to be finally done and the compulsion to remain, half alive in a relationship defined by pleasure extracted from suffering neither one can name or leave.","src":"haiku"},
"headcave~the-lies-we-choose":{"haiku":"Two people trapped in a cycle of mutual dependency choose lies over truth, leading to isolation and the slow death of intimacy.","opus":"Headcave trace the slow erosion of faith and fame inside a doomed attachment, where love arrives veiled and blackened and the will to keep someone shades into the will to let them go. The metalcore band frame comfort as apathy, insisting no confession can undo the falsehoods two people knowingly agree to inhabit until only solitude remains.","sonnet":"A man surrenders someone he loves — not out of indifference but because holding on has become its own kind of madness. Faith and reputation dissolve together. What remains is chosen numbness: apathy as shelter, solitude as the only honest outcome when the self-deceptions that held a relationship together finally stop working.","src":"haiku"},
"headcave~undivine":{"haiku":"Degradation feeds a consuming hunger—the speaker oscillates between victim and master, trapped in a mirror where reflection and self become indistinguishable terrors.","opus":"A feverish confession of moral corrosion, where the narrator declares himself less than divine and hooked on a killing streak. Straining across some barrier toward another, he begs to be washed clean of deceit even as the slave in him becomes the master. Guilt and desire braid together into a plea for connection on the far side of the self.","sonnet":"A speaker in crisis — kneeling, shaking, severed by effort — feels 'more than undivine' and confronts answers that keep building and tearing, seeking something on 'the other side' while the slave-becoming-master imagery suggests a drive to purge deceit and be remade.","src":"haiku"},
"headwreck~buzzsaw":{"haiku":"Cyclical damage and denial perpetuate as the speaker confronts inability to change or escape. Repetition becomes trap, and truth remains inaccessible beneath performance.","opus":"A relationship trapped in a loop of denial and unspoken accusations, the singer rewinding to the start only to repeat the same mistakes and false faces. The refrain of dying or lying to escape frames someone who gave love that was never felt, disposable and endlessly returning.","sonnet":"A cycle of denial and conflict in a volatile relationship: stuck in constant denial, the other always spitting retractable shit, the speaker questions whether they even exist or are lying to escape — in the pocket, disposable, they demand the cycle be broken off before decay takes both of them.","src":"haiku"},
"headwreck~low-blow":{"opus":"A doomsday countdown pulsing with adrenaline and dread, the speaker keeping time as an apocalypse approaches, refusing blame for the coming catastrophe he can't find a way around, caught in irreversible, terminal time he can't escape or sleep through.","src":"opus"},
"headwreck~plan-z":{"opus":"Someone hears a summons calling them elsewhere and chooses to drift off in ecstasy, balanced on a wire between floating up and choking, deciding to let go and fall somewhere no one will find them, unable to picture staying here without hope.","src":"opus"},
"healing~black-sky":{"haiku":"Natural disaster mirrors psychological fracture—lightning and rain obliterate the frozen self, leaving only raw sensation and impossible escape.","opus":"A claustrophobic terror trapped in a cellar as disaster comes down from overhead. Numb limbs and racing thoughts leave no way out when the heavens go dark and a cold, staining downpour falls. The imagery suggests bombardment or apocalypse, a calm evening flashing into blinding day, the narrator pleading not to die with nowhere to shelter.","sonnet":"A speaker paralysed below while a storm of black sky and purple rain descends — skin stained, bones made strange — moves from entrapment to terror as the 'brightest of days' announces not hope but catastrophe already too late to escape.","src":"haiku"},
"health~a-l-o-n-e":{"haiku":"Despite connection, loneliness persists—the speaker cannot care and cannot escape, as shared journeys lead nowhere but deeper into isolation.","opus":"A hollowed-out account of closeness that never fills the emptiness, from the LA noise band. Even with someone right there, the narrator feels utterly isolated and no longer knows how to feel anything for them. Chasing money and drugs, he finds nothing and no one who would grieve him, fleeing a self that trails him everywhere.","sonnet":"Isolation that persists through proximity — the narrator is always alone even when with someone else, unable to care anymore, unable to stop whatever follows them. The money and drugs that were supposed to fill the gap don't, and the closing declaration 'I'm giving up' is the first honest thing said aloud.","src":"haiku"},
"health~anti-life-feat-chino-moreno":{"haiku":"Abandonment and grief consume years of isolation as memories blacken, yet the speaker clings to the past unable to reclaim what was lost.","opus":"A ghostly reckoning with abandonment and years of solitude, lifted by the Deftones frontman's guest turn. Forgotten by everyone but the one he addresses, the narrator asks whether he was truly so difficult to trust or love. There's no hidden meaning waiting to be found, only the dawning clarity of the why: those days are gone and cannot be reclaimed.","sonnet":"Years of solitude addressed to someone no longer present, possibly dead or simply gone — talking to the ceiling, afraid to go on. The question 'was I so hard to love?' is never answered; what remains is the acknowledgment that there is no deeper meaning, nowhere to run, and that the past is gone but the loneliness persists.","src":"haiku"},
"health~antidote": {"haiku":"Obsession and dependency mirror mutual self-destruction, both partners claiming they cannot survive without the other.","opus":"A suffocating, jealous attachment where possessive delusion masquerades as love, the partner threatening they can't live without the singer. The refrain calls the bluff, insisting that for all the begging and threats, they won't actually die without them.","sonnet":"A relationship held together by threats of self-destruction — 'how many nights you said you can't live, but I know you won't die without me.' The narrator mirrors the paranoia: both are clinging, both are holding on, and the power dynamic of who needs whom more keeps flipping without resolution.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study of a relationship held together like a hostage situation where both parties grip the rope. The speaker lays out his own paranoia and jealousy first, then turns it — the suspicion runs both directions, the two of them clinging so tightly that neither can breathe or walk out the door. The hammered, mantra-like refrain at the center is leverage made audible: the partner has staked survival itself on staying, self-erasure wielded as a leash, and the speaker's flat repetition is his refusal to believe the ultimatum will ever be made good. HEALTH stage it the way they stage the whole CONFLICT record — Duzsik's soft, unbothered croon riding a battering programmed pulse, so the calm voice is the mask and the machine underneath carries the panic. The title lands as the cruel joke: no cure is on offer, only two people convinced the poison is the one thing keeping them upright.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control"],"means":"self-erasure wielded as a leash by both parties equally","built":"the title promises a cure while the song delivers only two people sustaining each other's poison"},
"health~ashamed": {"haiku":"Isolation and self-loathing compound as the speaker exists alone in the world, unable to change and unworthy of forgiveness for their mere existence.","opus":"A raw confession of unworthiness so total it turns on existence itself. Having nowhere to go and no one in the world, the narrator arrives ashamed even to have been born. He cannot change and cannot be changed for, cannot meet another's face, and accepts the blame others place on him as the price of simply being alive.","sonnet":"A person with nowhere left to go returns to someone they know they shouldn't, confessing paralysis rather than desire. Neither can change for the other; the speaker's only statement is shame at having been born at all, a self-erasure that replaces apology.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dependent's confession delivered as an apology for existing: the speaker returns, uninvited and after dark, to a person he cannot look in the face because every exit — leaving, growing up, dying young — has been sealed off and there is nowhere left to run. HEALTH build this as a spiral rather than a climb, letting Duzsik's unbothered melodic croon sit atop the mechanized grind while the verses tighten around a fixed truth: neither party can be remade to fit the other, and blame passes back and forth with nothing left to answer it. The recurring tally of solitude and the closing hush of having lost everyone collapse into the record's bleakest thesis, that the shame here is not for any act but for the fact of having been born at all — self-erasure reframed as a courtesy to everyone who has to endure him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","suicide","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"shame here is not for any act but for having been born at all","built":"a spiral structure enacts the sealed exits the verses name"},
"health~be-quiet-and-drive-far-away-spotify-singles": {"haiku":"Escape and distance become necessary as the speaker abandons a place that never felt like home.","opus":"A stripped cover of the urge to flee a town that never felt like home, dressing the new lover in an old one's clothes and just driving. The word away dissolving into repetition makes escape itself the only destination.","sonnet":"A fragment about needing to flee a city that no longer feels like home — projecting a lost lover onto someone new and asking only to be driven far away from all of it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Deftones' original is a numb fantasy of flight from a place and a person, its one damning line the confession of dressing a new lover in an old one's clothes — grief and cruelty folded into the same gesture, the town and the girl both things to be driven away from. The mantra of away that swallows the back half is the whole point: not a destination but a repetition, escape that only ever loops. HEALTH's choice to cover it is the reading — they strip the alt-rock warmth and rebuild it as programmed dread under Duzsik's flat, unraised voice, so the numbness the lyric only describes becomes the actual texture of the recording, the wanting-out delivered by someone who sounds like he has already left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The admiration runs both ways — Chino Moreno later guested on HEALTH’s own ANTI-LIFE.","themes":["freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"escape rendered as texture rather than longing by the arrangement","built":"stripping the alt-rock warmth makes numbness the recording's surface not merely its subject"},
"health~black-static": {"haiku":"A person watches the world break down around them, resigned to mortality and indifferent to apocalypse or divine judgment.","opus":"Comedown nihilism at dawn as the drugs and the world both run out, shrugging off apocalypse because worry changes nothing. Beneath the bravado is a genuinely gentle wish, that if the end comes it comes quietly in sleep.","sonnet":"Riding out the end of a long night of intoxication while apocalyptic anxiety hums in the background — the speaker shrugs at armageddon, noting they weren't worried before they were born and don't see why death should be different, choosing chemical blunting over existential dread.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dawn comedown scaled up to the end of the world: the drugs and the night are both running out at once, and the deity cresting the horizon is one who has simply forgotten the narrator exists — no judgment, just indifference, which lands worse. HEALTH stage the album's signature move here, Duzsik's mild, unbothered croon riding a mountain of programmed distortion, so the record's one defiant curse at the apocalypse gets muttered under the breath rather than screamed — a private obscenity he barely raises his voice to deliver. The logic underneath is a shrug at oblivion out of Epicurus: he wasn't troubled by the eternity before his birth, so the eternity after shouldn't frighten him either, though the smoke fogging his head admits the argument isn't holding. By the outro the terror has narrowed from cosmic to domestic — gunfire and its wreckage pressing him flat into the mattress — and the only peace on offer is the smallest, saddest bargain going: that slipping under unconscious might not be the worst way out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"cosmic indifference landing worse than judgment ever could","built":"the album's one defiant curse delivered under the breath not screamed"},
"health~blue-monday":{"haiku":"Manipulation and emotional control; being told how to feel by someone who claims authority over your interior life.","opus":"Noise-rock band HEALTH strip New Order's classic down to its raw hurt, exposing its wounded questioning of what it's like to be mistreated by someone who also dictates who you should trust and how you should react. The narrator, doubting even his own senses, passively waits to be told how to respond, need and resentment knotted together.","sonnet":"A cover of New Order stripped to its emotional core — the bewildered hurt of someone who cannot understand how they are being treated, cannot articulate what they feel, and is still waiting, numbly, for whoever wounded them to explain what happened.","src":"haiku"},
"health~blue-monday-from-atomic-blonde":{"haiku":"Touch and promise transform into betrayal as the speaker discovers the other's true nature.","opus":"A New Order cover stripped to its ache, the numb confusion of someone treated coldly yet still asking the other to dictate how they should feel. HEALTH's version leans into the emotional helplessness under the dancefloor.","sonnet":"A demand for recognition from someone who has claimed authority over the narrator's feelings — you told me who I am, you laid hands on me, and I thought I misheard. The speaker refuses to be told how to feel while admitting they still find it hard to say what they actually need.","src":"haiku"},
"health~body-prison": {"haiku":"Fate traps two enemies in shared embodiment, the body becomes a cell where they serve concurrent sentences together despite mutual disdain.","opus":"Two people who might have been better off as enemies or strangers, trapped together in something that resembles a dream more than a life. The pull to start over, back to water and trees, admits that fate and blame make little difference once you're stuck here.","sonnet":"A relationship that should never have existed — the speaker and another person are locked together against their better judgment, wanting to go back to before they met, trapped between fate and free will, pain and blame, unable to determine if they're suffering alone or alongside each other.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about a bond so ruinous the only relief imaginable is that it had never formed — better to have been enemies, or strangers, than whatever this became. The verses reach for a total reset, an undoing that runs backward past the relationship to some pre-human origin of water and trees, but the reach keeps snagging on an image of blood already dried on an altar: the damage is sacrificial, consecrated, not the kind you rewind. Duzsik's flat sweetness holds the ambivalence steady — the middle turn stops asking how to escape and asks instead whether the other person was ever really there, whether fate or blame changes anything when the two of them are equally caged. Set to Perturbator's chrome-lit synthwave, the wish to start again reads less as hope than as a loop that resets only to arrive at the same altar, which is the prison the title names.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","faith & the occult","freedom & escape"],"means":"the wish for erasure that loops back to the same consecrated wound","built":"the chrome synthwave frames the reset wish as repetition arriving at the same altar"},
"health~burn-the-candles": {"haiku":"Death erases memory and meaning; everything beloved eventually becomes irretrievable ash regardless of devotion.","opus":"Mourning for someone now gone, snarled up with guilt over never having said or done what was needed while they were alive. Lighting candles becomes a vigil for what can't be recovered, with the divine imagined as creating the world only in order to strip it away.","sonnet":"A song about watching something die — a relationship, a person, a presence — while sitting in darkness unable to say or do the right things in time; memory is cheap because the person is already gone, and burning the candles is a ritual of acknowledgment for something irrecoverably lost.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grief song that keeps testing whether remembering someone is worth anything, since recollection blurs and dims while the person themselves simply vanishes mid-sentence — present one moment, absent the next, with no ceremony. Against that erasure Duzsik pleads for the loved one to stay and confesses the ordinary failures of intimacy: the tenderness he could not voice, the decent act he left undone, delivered in that flat unraised croon HEALTH have always set against their industrial concussion, so the ache arrives numb rather than histrionic. The lit candles work as a wake-rite that doubles as a verdict, each repetition insisting the loss is sealed, past recall or repair. Then the read tilts cosmic and bitter: the wait in darkness for a voice that won't answer resolves into a theology of pure cruelty, a creator who fashions existence only to reclaim it — mortality reframed not as accident but as intended design. The closing hush, someone fallen motionless, lets the machinery drop away and leaves the stillness itself as the final word.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"grief reframed as intended design rather than accident","built":"the lit candles function as wake-rite and closing verdict in the same gesture"},
"health~children-of-sorrow": {"haiku":"Those forgotten by history, erased from memory as their pain becomes unbearable, face abandonment and the erasure of their ever having existed.","opus":"A dirge for the discarded and forgotten, passed between one who still holds the memory and one who would rather not. The band frames grief as a whole generation of hollowed-out young people, blamed and wounded, whose very existence no one will admit to. A fear of being deserted runs through it, memory itself treated as an injury better left unopened.","sonnet":"Two people circling the same wound from opposite sides: one who remembers, one who has chosen to forget, both stranded by grief and mutual blame. The turn from 'lifeless' to 'endless' children of sorrow signals that this mourning has no bottom, and the admission that no one will ever know they were here lands like an epitaph.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tenderness address to someone the world has already turned away from — HEALTH set Duzsik's gentlest croon against RAT WARS' crushed programmed low end, so the comfort and the annihilation arrive in the same breath. The speaker offers the only mercy left, which is deliberate forgetting: he vows to carry no memory of this person forward, framing amnesia not as loss but as a gift he is promising them. Underneath the soothing runs a raw terror of being left behind, a plea against being abandoned that briefly drops the caretaker mask and begs for the very thing he was handing out. The chorus is where the meaning actually moves: one dead-children refrain repeated but mutated word by word — lifeless, then painless, then endless — so the same corpse-image slides from numbness to relief to eternity, an elegy that keeps failing to settle on what these ruined figures finally are. The closing turn seals it: whatever passed between the two of them will leave no witness and no record, which is the horror and the consolation braided into a single thing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"deliberate forgetting offered as the only mercy still available","built":"one chorus refrain mutated word by word — lifeless to painless to endless"},
"health~cold-blood":{"haiku":"Vengeance replaces justice while trauma cycles through weaponized suffering, each generation inheriting inherited pain transformed into violence.","opus":"A scathing takedown of manufactured victimhood and vengeance dressed as justice, dissecting someone who weaponizes borrowed trauma and demonizes any scrutiny, shedding their own past to wear a face that isn't theirs. The band skewers a culture of performative grievance where tears become currency and revenge fully supplants love, nothing genuine left in the crimson hall of mirrors.","sonnet":"A takedown of someone who weaponizes their own trauma — bleeding into their echo chamber of dirty mirrors, building blind defenses out of stolen suffering, demonizing scrutiny to sell lies as truth. The speaker names the pattern with clinical precision: birthrights compromised, the despised self hidden behind another's face, and nothing left when the fiction collapses.","src":"haiku"},
"health~courtship-ii": {"haiku":"People hurt others to get what they want, yet honesty and dreams of broken promises persist. Broken trust defines human relationships from birth onward.","opus":"HEALTH compress romance into a cycle of injury, where wanting something from another person becomes reason enough to wound them. The Los Angeles noise act, known for pairing brutalist electronics with fragile vocals, set broken promises against a memory of the honesty people are supposedly born with, dreaming colors that only survive as regret.","sonnet":"Desire corrodes into harm. Two people who once moved toward each other now exist in a loop of damage — one hurts because of how much they hurt, and in hurting passes that wound on. The song lives entirely in that circuit, offering no exit, only the bleached residue of broken promises dreamed in color.","src":"haiku","fable":"The first half is a moral accusation collapsed into a chant — the same line hammered until cause and effect fuse, so that being in pain and inflicting it become one indistinguishable act, and the closing clause names the ugly engine underneath: you damage someone else to get what you want. HEALTH set this on Death Magic exactly the way the album works, Jake Duzsik's unbothered sweetness gliding over programmed brutality, so the cruelty is delivered as a lullaby rather than a snarl. Then the song reverses into elegy: a we that dreams in color, that was honest only at birth, that keeps returning to promises already broken — the repetition here reads not as indictment but as helpless looping over a loss. The two halves are the same courtship seen twice, first as the harm one person does to another and then as the shared innocence both have spent, the chant's insistence doing the real work of a couple who cannot stop replaying what they wrecked.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The II answers Courtship, a one-minute noise miniature from the band’s 2007 debut — the same subject carried across the rebuild from noise-rock into industrial pop.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"damage and desire collapse into one indistinguishable act","built":"the song reverses mid-track from accusation into shared elegy"},
"health~crack-metal": {"haiku":"A love that seemed perfect crumbles when the speaker realizes the other no longer needs them, leaving only the desire to escape.","opus":"A resigned plea to be let go by someone who cannot be saved. What began easily has curdled into a trap the narrator cannot escape, and reading the exhaustion in another's eyes, he releases them from the duty of rescue. The band strips addiction and self-destruction down to a single unwavering truth: there is no way out.","sonnet":"Permission granted to stop saving someone who no longer wants to be saved — but the 'let me go' comes from the speaker themselves, not the person watching. It started fine, deteriorated, and now the narrator sees in another's eyes that they are not needed, which closes the last exit.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song addressed to the person keeping vigil, and its whole gesture is to send them home — to insist their help changes nothing and to grant them leave to stop, to walk out of the room, to quit trying to keep the singer alive. HEALTH stage this as their signature contradiction: Duzsik's gentle, almost tender voice absolving someone of any duty to rescue him, while the RAT WARS machinery grinds underneath, so that the comfort and the collapse arrive as one sound. The middle movement charts a decline stated as flatly as a symptom on a chart — a self that once functioned and now does not — and the refusal to dramatize that fall is exactly what makes it land. The final turn is the cruelest: it is the caretaker, not the singer, who withdraws first, and he reads that decision in their face, the recognition that he is no longer wanted, treating his release as the same threshold as his entrapment — the open door and the sealed room folded into a single image. That is what sits beneath the tenderness: the permission he keeps extending is the one thing he cannot survive being given.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","suicide","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the permission he keeps extending is the one he cannot survive receiving","built":"the open door and the sealed room folded into a single closing image"},
"health~crimewave":{"opus":"A terse, noir-erotic fragment — menace and desire pressed together, tender skin under coarse hands in a hard slice of light.","src":"opus"},
"health~dark-enough": {"haiku":"Infidelity persists because authenticity and truth don't matter as long as appearance and words suffice to maintain connection.","opus":"HEALTH, the Los Angeles noise-pop band whose glossy production hides a bleak core, examine the moral murkiness of cheating, unable to distinguish betrayal from ordinary life. The chorus repeatedly asks whether sincerity even matters so long as the narrator keeps coming back and keeps saying the right things, arriving at a hazy, guilt-blurred numbness where consequences vanish into ambiguity.","sonnet":"A relationship held together by performance rather than truth — returning, saying the words, keeping up appearances. The question repeated is whether sincerity matters as long as the form is maintained; dark enough to stop asking, the pair exist in a space where the difference between real and not-real no longer registers.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that keeps asking whether feeling has to be real to count, and never quite lets you answer no. The chorus poses infidelity as a technicality — if the returning and the saying-so hold, does the interior truth underneath them matter — and Duzsik sings it so gently that the rationalization sounds almost tender, which is the trap. The verses shrug the same shrug (so what, why not) until the bridge turns the shrug sinister: darkness here is defined as not knowing the difference, the exact ignorance the chorus was pleading for as an alibi. By the outro the plea inverts into confession — that the not-knowing was never innocence but the cover under which something was done — so the sweetness of the voice becomes the mask and the dark is what it was hiding all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"pleading for ignorance as the alibi for what was already done","built":"the bridge redefines darkness as the not-knowing the chorus sought"},
"health~darkage": {"haiku":"Suffocation descends into the body as lung and spirit corrupt, everything rotting from within.","opus":"A bitter reckoning with someone whose kindness only registered at the end of a life or a lie, sung with numb acceptance. The crawling, drowning imagery of fouling the lungs and spoiling the child renders love as something hollow that pulls you under.","sonnet":"Someone who may have been good, or only numb, taken to the end of lies and life both — the ambiguity is left open. The body becomes the site of contamination: fouled lungs, spoiled children, drowning repeated. Hollow, crawling, clawing in the dark — the relationship and the body arrive at the same ruined state.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grievance sung with HEALTH's usual unbothered sweetness, where every concession to a person is retracted by its own second clause: they might have been good, but only at the cost of a whole life; might have been numb, but by the end it was the singer's own lies that ran out. That slide from life to lies is the read — the accusation quietly turns into a confession, the addressee a mirror. Against those verses the refrain drops the personal register entirely for a communal body-horror liturgy, a chant of getting into the body, fouling the lungs, spoiling the child and drowning — contamination passed downward from adult to unborn, the intimate betrayal scaled up to something congenital and inherited. The soft voice never flinches, which is the point: the tenderness is the mask, and what's underneath is rot handed on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","shame & repression"],"means":"accusation quietly turning into confession as the addressee becomes a mirror","built":"the refrain scales intimate betrayal into a chant of contamination passed to the unborn"},
"health~dead-flowers":{"haiku":"Faded memories cling to the speaker like dead flowers against skin, beautiful but decaying, holding only ghosts.","opus":"An elegy for emotional numbness, withered blossoms standing in for a deadened self and for each brief high. Lying motionless with no fight left, the narrator has learned only to flee everything, holding the faded blooms to his mouth and skin to keep lost memories from slipping away, martyrdom of the body meeting total depletion.","sonnet":"Dead flowers pressed to the lips and rough against the skin hold memories that nothing else can access — they are both a fix and a relic, the faith lost, the flesh made martyr. The narrator has learned from someone to run from everything, and now runs even from themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"health~decimation": {"haiku":"Inability to reverse time or escape the cycle of mortality; the past haunts, the purpose remains unclear, and escape is impossible.","opus":"A bleak reckoning with mortality and irreversible loss set against cosmic scale; the narrator, guilty and grieving someone he wants back, confronts the flat truth that time only moves forward and no one has ever returned from where the dead go.","sonnet":"A meditation on irreversibility and loss — the narrator is haunted by something or someone gone, unable to return to a past state, and confronts the hard fact that neither they nor anyone else ever truly goes back.","src":"haiku","fable":"Duzsik addresses a deity that has already died — a maker consigned to history — and confesses a lie he told himself, so the prayer is really a man talking to a vacancy where meaning used to live. The middle verse widens the frame to the eons and the countless lives that ran before his, then collapses that immensity down to one unbearably small want: he needs a specific person returned to him, and he needs to be told what any of it was for. HEALTH's method is exactly this whiplash of scale, the tenderest possible voice riding a crushing electronic churn, so the intimacy lands heavier for being ringed by so much annihilating noise. The final verse is the sentence handed down: whatever was lost cannot be reclaimed, no one has ever managed the reversal, and the quiet insistence that it still might is named as the doomed hope it is. That refusal to console — stated and restated, it is finished and always will be — is the read; the repetition isn't filler but the sound of a mind failing to accept a door that has shut for good.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"prayer addressed to a deity already dead, heard only by vacancy","built":"repetition of closure sounds like a mind failing to accept a shut door"},
"health~demigods": {"haiku":"A cursed child born into sorrow faces abandonment by callous beings, while the speaker pleads for either liberation or death.","opus":"A defiant address to indifferent gods, demanding to be raised up or left to burn. The narrator mourns the death of the child within and a drawn-out farewell to who he once was, while the deities only turn their backs. Framed around a luckless, unloved infant born lifeless, the song wrestles bitterly with sorrow, renunciation and a longing for oblivion.","sonnet":"A luckless, loveless child addressed in the third person is handed over to be buried, while the speaker calls up to demigods demanding either elevation or destruction — lift me up or put me in the dirt. Sorrow is answered only by turning away, and the child becomes a burden finally interred.","src":"haiku","fable":"A prayer that only offers its gods two options — raise me to your height or reduce me to ash — because a supplicant this far gone would rather be annihilated than left dangling in the ordinary middle. That middle arrives anyway as the cruelest answer: the chorus of faces averting, everyone deciding not to look, which is the real abandonment the ultimatum was built to preempt. The final movement turns out to be a burial rite carried by Duzsik’s unhurried, almost tender voice — the song stops arguing with heaven and starts chanting over a grave, a litany for someone never wanted at the start of life and never loved through it, interred by the very people who named that life a weight to carry. The gentleness of the delivery set against the pitilessness of the words is the whole trick: a cradle-song rewritten to make certain the sleep is permanent.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","suicide","shame & repression"],"means":"annihilation preferred over the cruelty of indifference","built":"a lullaby recast so its tenderness seals the grave"},
"health~don-t-kill-yourself-perturbator-rmx":{"haiku":"The speaker drowns in self-blame and regret, unable to escape the pain or envision change.","opus":"A blunt survival hymn from inside depression, only the self left to blame and hate, refusing suicide while unable to bear living this way. The repeated wish to change a body reads as desperation to become anyone but who you are.","sonnet":"The speaker is the only available target for their own pain — no peace, no sleep, only a face and a cycle of disgrace and mistakes that feel impossible to change. The honesty is precise: I don't want to kill myself, but I don't want to live this way; not a crisis but a sustained, exhausted impasse.","src":"haiku"},
"health~dsm-v": {"haiku":"Pleasure seeks sanctification while suffering circulates without mercy, but no salvation exists—only the choice to harm others as they've harmed you.","opus":"Compulsion pushed to the edge of self-destruction, where pleasure and sin blur and every regret drags the speaker back for more. Naming a diagnostic manual, it frames appetite itself as pathology and dares the gods to redeem or incinerate him.","sonnet":"A spiral of compulsion, shame, and moral reckoning — the person is dragged back by sins and regrets, questions whether pleasure has any sanctity, and ultimately demands either elevation or destruction, framing vengeance as the only sacred act left.","src":"haiku","fable":"Named for the psychiatric manual that catalogs disorders, this is compulsion diagnosed as its own religion — the craving that a single indulgence only sharpens instead of satisfying, chased past the threshold where satiation turns to injury. Duzsik's placid croon delivers deathbed questions over Rat Wars' pulverizing programmed drums, asking what a person confesses when nothing can be undone and the record of every appetite drags them downward into filth and blood. The recurring hymn to a consecrated pleasure keeps flipping into doubt about whether any of it was ever holy, and the golden rule gets perverted into a law of retaliation — do back what was done to you — so that the sacred and the scarce start rhyming as the same dwindling thing. It closes on a plea to false gods to choose the sinner's fate: raise him or abandon him to the fire, addiction reframed as a damnation with no neutral verdict left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The closing plea is the opener returning — DSM-V ends on DEMIGODS’ invocation, the album’s first prayer called back at its climax.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"compulsion diagnosed as its own religion with no neutral verdict","built":"the DSM title pins psychiatric disorder and sacred ritual in one frame"},
"health~feel-nothing": {"haiku":"People seek numbness and emotional dissociation to escape the pain of existence, choosing to dull hurt rather than face it.","opus":"HEALTH make a case for numbness as the only bearable option, a solidarity of the miserable under a fading sky. Rather than keep straining to cope, the voices decide to shut feeling down entirely, finding grim relief in emptiness just to survive until morning.","sonnet":"Collective emotional exhaustion and the desire for numbness — the narrator and everyone around them are alone even in company, tired of trying, and actively seeking the relief of feeling nothing rather than enduring the burn of feeling everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hymn to voluntary anaesthesia, sung in Duzsik's disarmingly gentle register while HEALTH's programmed percussion grinds underneath — the softness is the mask, and the machinery is what it masks. The verses gather everyone into one condition: solitude experienced in a crowd, nobody special, nobody spared, each of us slated to come apart. From there the chorus reframes numbness not as defeat but as mercy, asking why effort is even worth it and finding a strange coziness in switching off the lights inside. The band recasts existence as a thing none of us agreed to — thrust into being under a star already burning itself out — so the only rational goal shrinks, by the outro, to bare maintenance: dulling the sting just enough to survive to morning. The exhausted arithmetic of depression is taken at face value — no cure, no redemption on offer, only the smaller mercy of not feeling.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"numbness reframed as mercy rather than defeat","built":"gentle vocal register worn as mask over grinding machinery"},
"health~flesh-world-uk": {"haiku":"Mortality and hedonism merge—bodies break, blood cools, and the only mandate is sensation; death is dismissed with detachment.","opus":"A nihilist hedonism that stares death in the face and shrugs. Since bones break and blood cools regardless, the song argues for following appetite now, tempered only by the one rule not to wound the people you love.","sonnet":"A nihilistic embrace of physical existence without deeper meaning — we are bodies that die, so follow lust, take drugs, don't hurt the ones you love, and let go: life is bones that grow strong before breaking and blood that runs hot before going cold, nothing more.","src":"haiku","fable":"A club-anthem catechism for people who have decided existence carries no assignment beyond appetite: chase desire, take every substance, spare no one guilt, because presence is the only fact and the single door leads out rather than back. HEALTH set that creed against an anatomy of entropy — a skeleton at its densest the instant before it fractures, warm circulation on its slow slide toward the morgue — so the imagery keeps confessing the mortality the words insist they have stopped fearing. The parenthetical chants working underneath, the shrug and the surrender cue, act as a crowd antiphon that hollows each verse out even as the beat sells it, and Duzsik's weightless croon over Death Magic's programmed battery makes the whole hedonist gospel sound less triumphant than depleted. That gap between the party the song stages and the deathbed arithmetic it cannot stop reciting is the read: the mask is euphoria, and what lies under it is a body counting down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","party & hedonism","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the hedonist creed keeps confessing the mortality it claims to ignore","built":"crowd antiphons hollow each verse out even as the beat sells it"},
"health~free-to-die":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and existential despair meet indifference, as one person refuses to intervene in another's slow self-destruction.","opus":"A cold decision to stop saving someone determined to destroy themselves. Rejecting comfort and reason alike, the narrator watches a person already spiritually gone and chooses not to step in, granting them the liberty to perish. Under the harshness runs his own weariness, stuck in one endless repeating day with no way out, worn down past feeling.","sonnet":"A speaker watching someone spiral downward and choosing, from exhaustion rather than cruelty, to stop intervening. The refrain 'you're free to die, I'm not gonna stop you' is the product of repeated failure, born-too-late helplessness, and a sameness of days with no exit for either party.","src":"haiku"},
"health~future-of-hell": {"haiku":"Existence and consciousness collapse into cyclical suffering, each rebirth trapped in the same inescapable cycle of anguish and fear.","opus":"A dread-soaked vision of inescapable doom closing in from every side. The band conjures fear as a plague awakening in the flesh, voices that are not real consuming the body, walls tightening with no chance to run. Lives undone and endlessly reborn into anguish, the track stages hell not as afterlife but as an approaching, unavoidable future.","sonnet":"Existence as inescapable torment — the walls closing in, a voice not real, a plague of fear awakened by touch. The speaker addresses either another or themselves in a loop of rebirth into anguish, with no exit and no one spared from burning.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dread-mantra that keeps circling the same shrinking room, its title-phrase looped until hell stops being a place you go and becomes the present tense you are already living in. The verses split cleanly into two registers: a cosmic ledger of undone and reborn lives asking who among us will burn, and a bodily one where a felt touch and an unreal voice colonize you from inside — fear as plague, doubt as possession. HEALTH's signature contrast does the rest of the work, Duzsik's unbothered melodic calm floating over programmed brutalism so the apocalypse arrives as something already accepted, the closing-in walls narrated in a voice too soft to fight them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the apocalypse accepted before it has finished arriving","built":"melodic calm floating over brutalism enacts what acceptance costs"},
"health~god-botherer": {"haiku":"Someone questions faith, existence, and whether connection or healing is possible in an indifferent universe.","opus":"A lonely, doubting address to God and a distant ex intertwined, asking whether anyone cares, whether the stars care, whether love or faith can heal; the deeper read is existential and romantic abandonment—learning to fear in a universe indifferent to whether we're even here.","sonnet":"A song about estrangement and unanswered spiritual need — the narrator wakes in the dark, stares at indifferent stars, wonders if being here matters at all, and confronts whether pain and isolation are simply what faith leads to.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sleepless address to someone gone, spoken across the gap where a relationship used to be: the narrator lies awake in the small hours wondering whether he ever crosses the other person's mind, whether the resentment between them has cooled or only hardened. HEALTH set that fragile human question against a cosmos that returns nothing — he lifts his gaze to the night sky and finds it wholly incapable of minding him, a scale that dwarfs the wound without soothing it. The parenthetical whispers turn dread into something learned rather than chosen, doubting aloud whether any of this had to happen at all. Where the chorus reaches for belief as a handhold, it discovers that the thing it clings to is soaked in the same hurt it meant to escape, so conviction and suffering fuse into one substance — the god-bothering of the title as a petition that only enlarges the ache it was supposed to answer. Duzsik's unbothered melodic softness floating over the album's programmed brutality is the argument in miniature: the flat calm of someone who has stopped expecting a reply.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"belief reached for as comfort only enlarges the wound","built":"flat melodic calm argues the speaker has stopped expecting a reply"},
"health~hate-you-feat-jpegmafia":{"haiku":"The speaker watches themselves age into emptiness, surrounded by those who profit from their deterioration.","opus":"JPEGMAFIA's paranoid, industry-hating menace, threats and heartlessness stacked over noise. The confession underneath is the hook, hating everyone else more than he hates himself, aggression as a shield over exhaustion at thirty.","sonnet":"Rage at fake industry figures delivered from the street level — people built like gorillas who can't scrap, snitches, niggas who've never seen a trap performing toughness. The most honest line is the thesis: 'I hate you niggas more than I hate myself,' which places the external contempt as a displacement of something interior.","src":"haiku"},
"health~hateful": {"haiku":"Cynicism and self-loathing pervade existence, with each person isolated and resentful, drowning in hatred for everyone and everything.","opus":"A grinding meditation on collective spite: everyone dying alone, hateful and wasteful of love, drowning in lies with no forgiveness left. It indicts an us where lust replaces connection and the whole human roster is guilty.","sonnet":"A bleakly honest portrait of a humanity defined by its hatreds — people die alone, live wasteful lives full of lies, hate everything except lust, and there is no forgiveness coming; the song neither moralises nor offers a way out, just repeats the fact of collective self-destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A last inventory of a poisoned species, conducted by Duzsik as a courtroom question turned on the room — first who among us, then who among you — so the indictment slides from confession to accusation without the melody ever raising its voice. Two ways of failing sit side by side: a cruelty that shrinks every appetite down to lust, and a squandering that lets love go to waste, as if the end were arriving less by catastrophe than by everyone spending their last warmth badly. Against that, the single word for deceit is repeated past the point of sense until it stops being an argument and becomes weather, a static hiss under the parenthetical verdict that no pardon is coming. That is the trick of the thing — the softest possible instrument handing down the harshest possible sentence, so the chant registers not as a scream but as a shrug at the human ledger closing in the red.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"indictment slides from confession to accusation without raising its voice","built":"a single word for deceit chanted past sense into weather"},
"health~high-pressure-dave": {"haiku":"Intersecting paths and cycles of darkness; time and patterns repeat while pressure builds, and the singer oscillates between wanting connection and finding only weariness.","opus":"HEALTH offer a hypnotic, fragmented rumination on paths that keep intersecting and on time itself, recurring encounters set against strained days and self-defeating cycles that eventually fade. Longing and fate blur into something that feels both inevitable and only half-real.","sonnet":"A minimal, looping song about cycles of desire and inevitability — paths keep crossing, dark patterns repeat but eventually fade, and the narrator finds himself wanting someone in the middle of a high-pressure routine that will ultimately wither.","src":"haiku","fable":"Almost the entire song is a four-line refrain looped past the point of meaning, which is the point: two lovers whose orbits keep intersecting under the strain of days that never let up, and a fixed template of behavior the singer keeps insisting is dissolving even as the loop proves it never does. HEALTH build the read into the architecture — Duzsik's soft front-line vocal carries the attraction, the admission that he wants this person and the way both of them keep slotting back into the same groove, while a second parenthetical voice answers underneath with the cold machinery of existence: we sleep, we're born, we've done this too long, I know it all. Set against the industrial thud of their post-noise reinvention, the tension is between wanting someone and recognizing that the wanting is only another turn of a cycle you were born into and cannot step out of. The recurrence isn't romance but gravity, and the flat repetition enacts the entrapment the words merely gesture at.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"desire recognised as just another turn of an inescapable cycle","built":"refrain looped past meaning enacts the entrapment the words only gesture at"},
"health~identity":{"haiku":"Society calls but the speaker has no freedom to answer; they can only learn to abandon and leave.","opus":"A collective lament of beings denied freedom, only ever getting to want and to leave rather than truly live. The calling out and turning of backs frames a plea for contact and return from those who didn't know what they were losing.","sonnet":"A group — 'we' — calls out to someone who has turned their back, confessing they wanted to come back but didn't know how. The freedoms never granted (to learn, love, steal) circle against the only freedom available: being here, briefly, before leaving again.","src":"haiku"},
"health~isn-t-everyone":{"haiku":"Humanity drowns in mutual blame and complicity, surrendering to false masters while deserving the corrupted world they've built.","opus":"A scorched declaration of shared guilt and doom, watching someone drown and striking the spark that levels everything. Its refrain insists no one is innocent, that we surrender to whatever we serve and end up with the world we have earned, nursing another's bitterness until it comes true.","sonnet":"Collective complicity dressed as confrontation — everyone surrenders to those they serve, everyone gets the world they deserve, and the bitterness the narrator demands to know is not one person's but everyone's. 'Isn't everyone' alone? The question collapses the distance between the accuser and the accused.","src":"haiku"},
"health~l-a-looks": {"haiku":"Isolation persists even among people; wanting escape and second chances drives obsessive wanting that lacks the nature of love.","opus":"HEALTH aim their industrial pop at loneliness that persists even in company, the narrator trapped in himself and weary of his own being. Each craving for a fresh start meets a taunting reminder that he's bound to fail. The blunt hook confesses this isn't love yet clings to the other person anyway, holding need and wreckage together.","sonnet":"A relationship acknowledged as not-love but still desired — two people trapped in their own skins, wanting other lives, unable to become anyone else. The honesty is bleak: desire persists without affection, the pull is real even without the name, and the pair remain stuck together in a city-shaped loneliness.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about solitude that no company can cure, and about wanting a person you refuse to dignify with the word love. The refrain keeps asking whether being beside someone actually rescues you from being trapped inside your own head, and each pass answers by turning the question inward — from mild self-weariness toward something closer to revulsion at a self you can never trade in for a different one. HEALTH stage the cruelty formally: every longing for a second life or a fresh start gets a bracketed voice that mocks it back, warning against expecting rescue and adding that drowning is the likelier outcome, so the yearning and its refusal share one breath. What survives that demolition is the chorus, an addict's honesty that names the pull as appetite rather than tenderness while still insisting it remains the two of them — desire stripped of illusion but not, quite, of need. Duzsik's untroubled sweetness riding the punishing machinery is the whole trick: a lullaby voice reading out an airtight sentence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","identity & becoming"],"means":"desire stripped of illusion but not, quite, of need","built":"bracketed voices mock each longing back in the same breath"},
"health~life": {"haiku":"Insomnia, uncertainty, and existential dread pervade waking life; no one knows what they want or why humans die, leaving only pain and the strange acceptance of life as all we possess.","opus":"A stark reckoning with existential dread from people too exhausted to sleep yet too scared to die. HEALTH fix on a paralysis before life's apparent meaninglessness, repeating that nobody understands what they want or why anyone dies, and that we come apart through no fault of our own because this strange, brief existence is all we get.","sonnet":"An insomniac existential shrug: life is strange, painful, and we're all just falling apart without knowing what we want or why we die — but it's all we have.","src":"haiku","fable":"HEALTH take a dead-simple existential lyric and make it un-simple by welding it to their industrial machinery: an insomniac lying too wired to rest and too drained to keep trying, confessing in Jake Duzsik's unbothered melodic hush that he can name no desire and no reason for dying, all of it delivered over programmed brutality. The chorus is pure recursion — an admission of not-knowing that keeps restating its own not-knowing, looping until it stops registering as despair and hardens into a mantra, the one flat truth the song is sure of. The verses inch from exhausted to afraid to coming apart, yet each resolves into the same shrug — strange, painful, and the only thing on offer — so the read lives not in the movement but in the acceptance underneath the numbness: the mask is nihilist boredom, and beneath it is something almost tender, a refusal to fake meaning where none is available.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"despair rehearsed until it hardens from feeling into fact","built":"the chorus loops its own not-knowing until it stops registering as despair"},
"health~loss-deluxe": {"haiku":"Life decays into ash and suffering, and we can only watch loved ones deteriorate and die.","opus":"A bleak apocalyptic elegy imagining what your life amounts to once you're in the ground, facing unstoppable new diseases in the cold; the throughline is a nihilistic reckoning that life is only burning and rotting, and if we're lucky we merely get to watch our loved ones sicken and break down.","sonnet":"A bleak, matter-of-fact elegy for everything and everyone — life is ash and rot, people get sick and break apart, the world burns, and those left behind sit watching and pitying themselves in the rubble.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor interrogates a corpse, and the song is built as a diptych: two near-identical passes where the same questions — how are you spending eternity underground, did you ever understand any of it — return with their answers darkened. HEALTH strip the apocalypse of drama and leave only aftermath: a scorched world reduced to mineral and spite, embers where a fire has already finished its work, and a bewildered clinging to memory the singer can't justify wanting. The cruelest turn is saved for the second cycle, which reframes survival itself as the sentence — outliving people means being condemned to watch the ones you cherish deteriorate, so the lucky and the doomed quietly trade places. Under Duzsik's unaffected melody that inversion lands as exhausted rather than tragic, self-pity named and indulged in the same breath, which is exactly the numb honesty the band's programmed brutality is built to carry.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"survival recast as the sentence rather than the reprieve","built":"diptych structure darkens identical questions on the second pass"},
"health~mean":{"haiku":"Cruelty manifests without reason, draining affection simply because the speaker exists nearby.","opus":"A confrontation with someone's gratuitous cruelty, asking why they took their unhappiness out on the singer simply for being present. Beneath the accusation is the sad recognition that the other person couldn't bear the illusion that life might be sweet.","sonnet":"A direct question to someone who hurt without reason — did you want to be mean just because I was in the room, free yourself from the illusion that life could be sweet? The narrator can't fake it, can't sleep, is haunted and doomed to want more from someone who chose cruelty over the possibility of goodness.","src":"haiku"},
"health~nc-17": {"haiku":"Two people are trapped in a primitive past, unable to move forward or escape each other. The narrator refuses to return or try again, resigned to this never-ending cycle.","opus":"The noise-rock band captures a self-defeating loop of return, the narrator asking how they could ever stop while flatly refusing to revisit the past, wary of repeating it. Trapped in something crude and old, he questions why any of it began. The industrial grind mirrors the cycle, each verse circling the same refusal, unable to move on or fully break loose.","sonnet":"A speaker locked in a relationship or pattern that has already proven damaging refuses any restart — memory of how things really were is precisely the deterrent. The refusal is not bitterness but clarity: knowing the truth of the past makes the appeal of going back not tempting but impossible.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hard vow of no return, built as a loop that keeps asking how to stop and where to go and keeps answering with the same flat refusal. The two verses run parallel — one glancing over the shoulder at what was survived, the other caged inside a cruder earlier version of the self — but both slam shut on the vow never to reverse course, never to restart something already lived once. HEALTH set that refusal against their programmed, crushing wall of sound and Duzsik's soft unbothered delivery, so the resolve reads less as triumph than as numb, exhausted certainty: moving forward here isn't hope, it's the only direction left once you've decided nothing behind you can be redeemed. The repetition isn't padding but the point — a mind circling the same locked door, confirming it won't turn the handle, because remembering exactly how things were is the whole reason to keep walking away.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"forward motion as the only direction when nothing behind can be redeemed","built":"repetition enacts a mind confirming a locked door won't open"},
"health~new-coke": {"haiku":"The song contemplates violent apocalypse and oblivion, wondering if those who die will see loved ones again, with acceptance of inevitable ending.","opus":"A blankly nihilistic anthem set against imagined apocalypse, chanting that catastrophe is fine, everything is fine, even as bombs fall. Under the deadpan refrain sits an unexpectedly tender uncertainty about mortality, a quiet wondering about how we'll meet the end and whether death might reunite us with the people we've loved and lost.","sonnet":"A bleak minimalist acceptance of mortality — let the bombs go off, life is good anyway; the narrator wonders how death will come and whether they'll see the people they've lost, arriving at tenderness through nihilism.","src":"haiku","fable":"Three imperatives welcome the apocalypse — let the artillery fire, let the ordnance detonate, let the world go black — and each is answered by a slogan-flat affirmation that everything is fine, cooed in Duzsik's unbothered melodic register like a jingle for a product nobody asked to have reformulated. That is the title's whole joke: catastrophe rebranded as an upgrade, consumer-grade contentment pasted over annihilation, the way Death Magic keeps smuggling grief inside sleek industrial pop. But the hook drops the mask, turning from spectacle to a quieter dread of dying young and unready, bargaining for a single glimpse of the people death has already taken. The closing lines twist the pronoun from we to I: the promise that a listener will be reunited with his dead curdles into the singer's own uncertainty about whether he would even recognize those he cherished — so the reassurance he offered someone else collapses under him. What sounded like a numb shrug at the end of the world was a man rehearsing how to say goodbye.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"rehearsing comfort for others until it collapses under the speaker","built":"the pronoun shifts from we to I as the reassurance curdles"},
"health~no-god-in-thunderdome":{"opus":"An existential reckoning with mortality and time: if death isn't actively hunting us, why the fear — and where do we go when it comes? The narrator senses there's no god to be proud of us, urging surrender to the inevitable sunrise while we're still here.","sonnet":"An existential stalemate — if time doesn't ultimately destroy us, why does fear still run everything we do? The song circles God's indifference to human deadness and the paralysis of a sunrise that arrives whether or not you're ready to meet it.","src":"sonnet"},
"health~of-all-else": {"haiku":"Arms outstretched toward connection, the speaker is paralyzed and consumed by forces beyond resistance.","opus":"A brief, hymn-like passage on surrender and shared mortality. Reaching out and paralyzed, the narrator wonders which piece of him departs, framing death as a child carried off into the dark. The band offers the dying as something that might console us all, urging endurance through whatever is killing everyone alike.","sonnet":"A short incantation for endurance: arms outstretched to receive whatever is killing, holding on for the sake of something unspecified. The image of leading a child into the dark while paralyzed gives the abstract grief a specific form — not one person's dissolution but everyone's.","src":"haiku","fable":"A body held open in a gesture of surrender that could be embrace or crucifixion, arms out but unable to move — the song lives in that paralysis, where reaching for another and being pinned in place look identical. Duzsik keeps asking which part of him survives the thing that is dissolving him, and the horror deepens when it turns communal: this is killing me becomes killing all of us, a private dying reframed as the shared condition. The single most brutal image is a child being sent into the dark, offering rather than rescue, and the closing plea to hold on through it all lands not as comfort but as the only instruction left when nothing can be fixed. On RAT WARS, HEALTH stage that despair the way they always do — the tenderness of the voice floating unbothered over machinery built to crush it, so the softness itself becomes the mask, and the tenderness is exactly what is being ground down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The bracketed title completes the refrain of the track before it — hateful, of all else.","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness","love & desire"],"means":"private dissolution reframed as the shared condition of all","built":"the softness of the voice is exactly what the machinery is grinding down"},
"health~ordinary-loss": {"haiku":"Universality offers no comfort: everyone dies, everyone grieves, and the shared condition transforms intimacy into proof of coming emptiness.","opus":"Grief stripped of any drama: the people you love are present, then simply gone, and you will be too. It sits in plain dread and self-erasure, wanting more time yet frightened of the end, asking whether to love is to practice letting go.","sonnet":"A song about the mundane inevitability of death and loss — everyone you love disappears into dirt, this has always been true, and the speaker confronts their own fear of dying while knowing none of it is extraordinary or unfair, just ordinary.","src":"haiku","fable":"HEALTH build the song as a public liturgy that keeps failing back into private panic: it opens in the flat, statistical voice of someone tallying the unremarkable — everyone you cherish is only a temporary tenant, decomposition the one guaranteed appointment — and Duzsik delivers it in his usual anesthetized calm, the industrial machinery grinding beneath like the impersonal arithmetic of the grave. Twice the verse tries to talk itself into peace, envying the dead their sleep without dreams and framing surrender as a skill that ought to follow naturally from having once known devotion. But the chorus mask slips every repetition, and what leaks out is the least ordinary thing imaginable: the bare, almost childish plea for a single extra evening, and the admission that under all the cosmic composure he is simply terrified of his own ending. The title is the trap of the whole track — naming grief ordinary is the sedative he keeps swallowing and keeps throwing back up.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"cosmic composure cracking into childish fear of ending","built":"the title names grief ordinary — the sedative he keeps throwing back up"},
"health~psychonaut": {"haiku":"A person cherishes their single mortal existence and refuses resurrection or external interference. They seek acceptance of transience over false spiritual promises.","opus":"A meditation on mortality and living fully in a single brief life, insisting once is enough and warning others off. The refusal to be brought back and the wish for an endless sleep frame death and letting go with a kind of peaceful acceptance.","sonnet":"About accepting a finite life and finding it enough — the narrator doesn't want to be brought back, doesn't want transcendence or afterlife, and is learning to let go of beliefs and anxieties in favor of simple presence. It's a quiet, secular acceptance of death.","src":"haiku","fable":"A short lifespan is enough for the speaker, and the recurring plea against being dragged back reframes death not as loss but as a release he refuses to have undone — no rebirth, no second pass. Duzsik's tenderly sung acceptance rides HEALTH's crushing industrial churn on Slaves of Fear, so the lone flare of aggression, a curt demand to be left alone, reads as the last guarded reflex of someone who has otherwise made his peace. The title promises a mind-traveler chasing far interior states, but the destination turns out humble: cherishing small ordinary comforts, then handing rival cosmologies and other people's private visions back to them without argument. The close chooses a permanent quiet over any return trip — oblivion embraced rather than dreaded, the record's governing terror answered by simply letting go.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"peace made with oblivion by handing everything else back","built":"title promises interior voyaging; destination is ordinary quiet"},
"health~rat-wars": {"haiku":"The speaker feels disconnected from human concerns and disillusioned, finding no higher purpose or community meaning.","opus":"A disenchanted reckoning with severed bonds and vanished belief, the narrator reaching toward those who came before only to get no reply, and digging into the ground to find emptiness. Having never located any greater meaning, he's left idle and adrift, posing the same question to a failed relationship and to his life: where is any of this headed.","sonnet":"Stripped-down disillusionment: the narrator has lost both friends and enemies, found nothing sacred underneath the surface, and is waiting with no higher purpose to guide them — quietly asking where this is all going.","src":"haiku","fable":"On HEALTH’s 2019 industrial pivot, the song is a systematic shedding: the narrator waves off the origins of the world before rock and forest because the daily grind of dealing with other people already drains him, then discards a partner who trusted him too little and leaned on him too heavily. From there the abandonment widens outward — allies and rivals alike drop away until the ledger reads even, and he digs beneath soil and corpses expecting some buried answer, finds only absence, and can no longer tell whether the voices he hears are real or just his own skull talking. The song's engine is Duzsik's method across the record: a gentle, unbruised melody carrying total nihilism, so the question of whether anyone found meaning gets the same shrug as everything else — it never took for him, and he is left simply killing time. The two refrains chart the collapse cleanly, moving from wanting to know what someone else desired to no longer knowing where any of this is headed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The band later raised this title to album scale — 2023’s RAT WARS is named for this song; the alternative Duzsik weighed was Outer Dark, after the Cormac McCarthy novel.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind"],"means":"systematic shedding of every attachment until only absence remains","built":"two refrains chart the collapse from wanting to know to not knowing at all"},
"health~ruin":{"haiku":"The speaker oscillates between wanting return and demanding departure, trapped in contradictory desperation.","opus":"The contradictory ache of a dying love, alternately begging the person to come back and to let it end, unable to want either cleanly. Betrayed and left careless, the singer turns to deceiving in return, never again.","sonnet":"An impossible double imperative — come back to me, don't come back again — spoken to someone who believed in then abandoned the narrator. The love is still there but so is the knowledge that return would only extend the damage; the alternating 'don't let it end this way' and 'god let it end this way' capture the unbearable contradiction.","src":"haiku"},
"health~shred-envy": {"haiku":"Regret corrupts every action; the speaker wishes non-existence to avoid the consequence of having lived at all.","opus":"A quiet reckoning with mortality and regret, everything said and done that can't be taken back, wishing never to have been born. The haunting turn is asking why it hasn't killed him yet, lost inside himself and waiting.","sonnet":"A song saturated with regret, self-loathing, and the wish never to have existed — the speaker cannot undo past words and actions, cannot stop caring about the damage they caused, and retreats into wishing they had been born at all as the only way to have avoided the accumulation of guilt they carry.","src":"haiku","fable":"A suicidal ideation stripped to its plainest grammar, sung in Duzsik's unbothered melodic hush so the horror arrives with no vibrato of self-pity — HEALTH's usual trick of pillowing annihilation in softness. The song turns entirely on tense: an early-verse fatalism that treats death as something coming for the speaker (someday it'll kill me) curdles across the repetitions into the flatter admission that nothing done or said can be taken back, that the wish is not for death but to have never existed at all. Then the structure breaks its own logic: the coda flips the promise into a question — why didn't it kill me? — and the death that was supposed to be the exit never comes, leaving not release but a self that has simply gone missing. The refrain about no longer worrying is the cruelest line, because it frames non-existence as the only available relief, and the ending withholds even that.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the wished-for exit arrives as a missing self rather than a death","built":"the tense shifts from passive fatalism to the flatter wish to never have existed at all"},
"health~sicko-feat-godflesh": {"haiku":"Genetic and moral corruption passes through bloodlines while the speaker refuses care or redemption.","opus":"A cold disavowal of someone the singer deems fundamentally broken, a mistake in the blood he no longer cares enough to fix. Paired with Godflesh's industrial dread, it hardens into a refusal of love itself, wanting and needing none.","sonnet":"Revulsion at the biological level — a mistake in the blood and heart, breeding endlessly, beyond caring. The parenthetical voices ('you breed like rats,' 'there's nothing we can do') represent an outside verdict already rendered while the speaker's refusal of love is not cold so much as used up.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two voices braid here and neither is kind: a taunting parenthetical chorus that reduces people to vermin multiplying past counting, and Duzsik's own flat diagnosis that something is congenitally wrong in the other person, wrong in the blood, wrong at the heart. The horror is that he concedes the defect isn't their fault and then withholds the compassion that admission should buy — a coldness he never bothers to dress up, shrugging off any duty to intervene. The closing mantra renouncing love outright turns that indifference into a creed, delivered in the same soft unbothered melody HEALTH always float over their machinery, so tenderness and contempt arrive in one breath. Pairing that voice with Justin Broadrick's Godflesh — the founding grammar of industrial metal's dehumanized grind — makes the cruelty structural: the track moves like a mechanism that has quit pretending it has any use for the human it is processing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The vermin taunt is a sample — Godflesh’s Like Rats, 1989; Famiglietti met Broadrick in person to ask permission. Rats, on the album named RAT WARS.","themes":["anger & defiance","family & blood","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"compassion withheld even after the fault is conceded","built":"pairing with Godflesh's dehumanized grind makes the cruelty structural not emotional"},
"health~slaves-of-fear": {"haiku":"From birth to death, humans are enslaved by fear—of rejection, difference, judgment, and mortality—wasting their years in service to anxiety rather than genuine struggle. The song laments the universal human condition of fear-driven existence.","opus":"An argument that dread rules human life from cradle to grave, that people crave affection or oblivion and want to stand out without much effort. It asks why anyone squanders their years in a universe with no higher power to weigh actions, offer love, or lend suffering any meaning.","sonnet":"A bleak meditation on the human condition as one of perpetual fear from birth to death — driven by desire for love or numbness, wasting years on pointless conflict, with no higher meaning or justice above us and no one to save or judge us.","src":"haiku","fable":"HEALTH lay their title-track thesis flat as a slogan: dread is the default setting of a human life, running from the first breath to the last, and the verses catalogue how it hollows out every impulse — the wish to be loved curdles into the wish to feel nothing, the wish to be singular collapses into a refusal to actually work for it. The chorus declines to elaborate, just tolling the phrase back at you, because there is nothing to add; the fear is the entire environment. What lifts the song past nihilist posturing is the bridge, where the band strips away the last possible consolation: nobody is keeping score from overhead, no sentence is being handed down, and crucially no love arrives from up there either. That final subtraction is the real cruelty — the terror would almost be bearable if it were pointed at something watching, but the song's cosmos is empty, so the panic becomes pure ambient noise with no author, which is exactly the sensation their programmed-metal wall of distortion, sung in Duzsik's unbothered lullaby voice, is engineered to reproduce.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["existential dread","alienation & emptiness","faith & the occult"],"means":"dread named as ambient noise with no author and no exit","built":"bridge strips the last consolation by removing even a watching cosmos"},
"health~stonefist": {"haiku":"Two people acknowledge that love has died between them, yet they persist forward unable to return or change. The song expresses resignation to losing the spark of intimacy.","opus":"A eulogy for a couple who have already conceded that the feeling between them is gone, yet remain owned by the wreckage of what they had. The refrain circles a single grim certainty: this ending is permanent, with no route back to the people or the closeness they used to be.","sonnet":"Two people acknowledge they have moved past the point of love — it is no longer in their hearts — yet remain possessed by what they have lost rather than moving freely forward. The song circles around the paradox of knowing something is over while being unable to let go of it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song that refuses the two lies breakups usually tell themselves — that things can be fixed, or that you can go back to before. The verse states the diagnosis flatly and then repeats it four times, as if saying the absent love aloud enough might finally make it true, or wear a groove deep enough to live in; the chorus turns that stasis into a set of hard vectors — no reverse, no return, no growing young again — so the relationship isn't dying but already fully dead, held upright only by inertia and the wreckage the two still share. HEALTH's method is the argument: Duzsik's soft, undramatized voice delivers the verdict without heat, while the programmed percussion pounds underneath, so the tenderness and the brutality are the same gesture rather than opposites. The one genuine flicker of doubt — the stammered question of whether being all the same and being different amount to anything — gets no answer, only another promise to promise more, an escalation with nothing behind it, before the closing chorus buries even the memory of love under the machinery.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the verdict delivered without heat because it is already past","built":"escalating promises with nothing behind them bury the memory"},
"health~strange-days-1999": {"haiku":"Time slows as pain and regret pile up. Pills numb moments that move toward inevitable death, leaving only mystery and misery.","opus":"A hazy meditation on whether the passage of years is a blessing or a curse, with medication flattening a existence that swings between highs and lows. A vague menace hangs over it, an anxious sense of some reckoning drawing near.","sonnet":"A song about the irreversibility of time and the weight of what has passed — the narrator reflects on years before a relationship began, pills taken to numb pain, life oscillating between euphoria and misery, arriving at the blunt fact that they cannot go back.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song reverse-engineered into an apocalypse song, which is how HEALTH tend to feel their private disasters — Jake Duzsik's unbruised croon delivering the wreckage flat while the machinery underneath grinds. The first verse is the smaller ache: an unfinished argument, a person gone, the discovery that the two of them were already out of sync long before they ever crossed paths, so the loss reads as a scheduling error in the universe rather than anyone's fault. Then the pivot — the chorus keeps trying to decelerate the calendar, to force a runaway clock to idle, and the second verse tips the whole thing toward the collective: pills swallowed to stay level, each passing instant edging closer to the one that stops the count, life split clean down the middle between the high and the crash. The recurring question of where you'll be when they arrive is the tell — the personal heartbreak has quietly become a countdown to something bearing down on everyone, and the flat vocal never once acknowledges the change in scale, which is exactly the point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Critics hear the title pointing at Kathryn Bigelow’s Strange Days — the 1995 film set in the final days of 1999; the band has never said.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","death & grief","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"personal breakup quietly scaled up to collective countdown","built":"flat vocal never acknowledges the shift from private grief to apocalypse"},
"health~tears": {"haiku":"A fragmented meditation on letting go, loss, and the dissolution of connection. Memories are distorted, souls are surrendered, and the speaker and listener are abandoned to die alone.","opus":"A grim parting where one voice both begs for rescue and pushes the other to surrender and walk away. The push and pull resolves into a stark verdict about isolation at the end, framing intimacy as something that ultimately cannot save either of them.","sonnet":"A fragmented meditation on love, surrender, and dissolution — a voice urges letting go and giving up, while the imagery of never waking and dying alone suggests that the relationship or the self it sustained is beyond saving.","src":"haiku","fable":"Every line pulls two directions at once: a cry to be rescued braided with an order to be abandoned, spoken by a we that keeps thinning into a you until the plural collapses into one person standing over the wreckage. HEALTH build the song as a mourner's double-bind, the same voice begging to be pulled from the water and insisting it be dropped back into it, because clinging and drowning have become the same motion. The middle turns accusatory, telling the other their tender version of the shared past is a fabrication, and that the sleep they've entered has no morning waiting on its far side. Written for a Rockstar antihero soaked in the deaths he couldn't prevent, the track floats Jake Duzsik's weightless croon clean over the programmed battering beneath it, so the sweetness of the melody is precisely the anaesthetic — the mask smiling while the closing line names the only certainty the bargaining could never argue away: the end is met by one person, with no company at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness","addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"the double-bind of clinging and drowning as one motion","built":"sweetness of the croon held clean over programmed battering — melody as mask"},
"health~the-message": {"haiku":"Death is meaningless, inevitable, and impersonal; we all burn out, and in life we chase age: young people eager to grow old, old people desperate to be young again.","opus":"Death as a patient, inescapable narrator collecting everyone regardless of station. The bleak twist is its verdict that death carries no meaning, just the endless human cycle of wanting to grow up then wishing to be young again.","sonnet":"Death is presented as an indiscriminate, meaningless inevitability that takes everyone — saints and criminals, rich and beautiful alike — regardless of readiness. The song observes how people spend youth eager to grow up and then old age wishing they were young, with death as the only constant and its message carrying no meaning at all.","src":"haiku","fable":"Death speaks the verses in first person, an even-tempered escort explaining that virtue buys no exemption and neither does wealth or beauty — the queue takes saint and swindler in the same breath. The title is the argument: the band strips away any promise of afterlife or lesson, insisting the ending communicates nothing except its own arrival, an end that is not a doorway to a beginning. HEALTH sink this to its blackest joke in the life-cycle refrain, where the young ache to be older and the old, worn out and surrendered, beg to be young again — a whole existence spent wanting the phase you don't have, so the wanting is the only content. Duzsik delivers all of it in that trademark unbothered croon, the softness a deliberate mask over industrial concussion, which is the real point: the calm is what makes the nihilism land, a lullaby sung by the thing coming to collect you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"death narrates its own arrival as a thing that communicates nothing","built":"lullaby delivery by the thing coming to collect you"},
"health~thought-leader": {"haiku":"Disconnection breeds contempt as the speaker lies in darkness, dismissing everyone as stupid while imprisoned in their own narcissism.","opus":"A portrait of screen-lit alienation and insomniac contempt. Lying awake staring at a glowing display, the narrator cycles the thought that everyone is dumb while chasing holy money and forgetting love. The band satirizes online cynicism and its erosion of memory and connection, the mind trapped in a loop it can no longer tell is real or imagined.","sonnet":"Late-night screen paralysis as social condition — unable to sleep, convinced everyone is stupid, chasing money and someone new while forgetting love. The self-awareness is there ('all in your head, or not') but changes nothing; the narrator wants you to know something important and then admits they can't remember what it was.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is the joke and the wound at once: the thought leader is really just a sleepless body in screen-glow, chanting that everyone else is stupid because it is the only thing that quiets the hours. HEALTH set that contempt-mantra against a parenthetical whisper that keeps qualifying it — it's all in your head, or maybe not — so each assertion of superiority is instantly eaten by the paranoia underneath, the doxxed-brain feedback loop of living inside the feed. Halfway through the sneer cracks into an ache that can't finish its own sentence, a need to be known colliding with the admission that the self doing the needing can no longer remember anything. By the end even the target dissolves — everyone is, everything is — until only the qualifier remains, the mind unable to decide whether the whole thing was ever real.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","surveillance & control","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"superiority chant eaten by the paranoia it was meant to quiet","built":"a parenthetical whisper qualifies each assertion of contempt until both dissolve"},
"health~trash-decade": {"haiku":"Loss and regret consume the speaker who lived only in memory of another, abandoned and unable to move beyond the wreckage.","opus":"A bitter breakup elegy for a decade of rot, hope dismissed as one more narcotic. The narrator, owning his own resentment, can't function without the person who abandoned him, torn between knowing them inside out and refusing to release them. Heaven's vanished; for the two of them only hell reads as real.","sonnet":"A decade wasted on a person who left the narrator for dead, with hope reframed as just another drug. Heaven is gone, hell is real, and the speaker oscillates between 'I know everything about you / I don't want to live without you' and a bitterness they've earned through years of wasted tears.","src":"haiku","fable":"A wrecked love song built as an interrogation: each verse fires a question at someone already gone — first whether he was ever truly seen, then whether he was ever truly wanted, finally whether any of it counted for anything — and none get answered, which is the point. HEALTH stage it inside their signature contradiction, Duzsik's unhurried, almost tender melody floating over programmed brutalism, so the cruelty lives in the machinery while the voice stays soft. The turn is that he claims total knowledge of this person and still couldn't hold them, which makes intimacy its own indictment; by the last stretch he is the sole figure remaining in his own dreams, having spent every tear and asking only to be allowed to forget. The recurring image of hope as a chemical — a fix that wears off — reframes the whole thing as withdrawal rather than plain heartbreak, the addiction lens doing the work of naming a grief he can't otherwise metabolize.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"intimacy treated as withdrawal rather than plain heartbreak","built":"questions fired at the absent partner receive no answers — the silence is the structure"},
"health~unloved": {"haiku":"Simulated empathy for someone with childhood trauma, pretending connection while indifferent to their wounds.","opus":"A brutal anatomy of loving someone damaged in childhood, the narrator admitting he only pretended to feel and finally, coldly, that he doesn't care, the tenderness of naming their wound curdling into detached cruelty.","sonnet":"About a person whose childhood emotional neglect has made them impossible to be close to — the narrator admits to performing closeness they don't feel, and ultimately stops pretending to care; the song is blunt about the fact that being unloved as a child creates someone who damages others and feels nothing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup rendered as forensic accounting, where the speaker itemizes his own coldness the way HEALTH itemizes distortion — Duzsik's soft voice delivering cruelty at conversational volume. The confession that he only ever pretended to feel is the song's real wound: not that he failed to love, but that he counterfeited the whole thing and knew it. He reaches for the partner's loveless childhood as an alibi, absolving himself of a damage that predates him, then lets the alibi curdle into contempt as the tenderness of I wasn't there hardens into I don't care. What repeats to the end is that shrug, looped until it stops sounding like indifference and starts sounding like a man reciting a lie until it holds.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","artifice & performance","shame & repression"],"means":"counterfeiting love and knowing it named as the real wound","built":"the alibi of a loveless childhood curdles word by word into contempt"},
"health~vibe-cop": {"haiku":"Displacement and abandonment teach coldness; the speaker becomes detached and unworthy of care, forgotten by those who claimed to hold them.","opus":"A meditation on being replaced and forgotten, where care curdles into cold control and dreams are best kept to yourself. Underneath runs the bleak certainty of how quickly the flesh fails and everything comes to nothing.","sonnet":"A spare, caustic song about being replaced or abandoned by someone who pretended to care — the speaker knows they don't belong in this person's world, that the relationship was cold underneath, and that they are simply waiting to be discarded; it ends in the body failing, which feels like relief.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric is a study in self-erasure and cold detachment: the opening voice asks to be swapped out, disowning its own place, while the caretaker who once tended it has let it lapse into memory. Its refrain — an exhortation to chase a dream and then seal that dream away, unshared and unspoken — reads first as encouragement but curdles into an order for silence, a demand that longing stay locked and invisible, until the last pass reroutes it from private muzzling into the colder instruction to unload it onto a stranger instead. Against that, the middle turns almost liturgical: something arrives with no jealous deity presiding and no ossuary waiting, only a nameless below that expects everyone anyway, HEALTH's synth-metal dread rendered as flat cosmology rather than melodrama. The song closes on a mortality countdown — flesh giving way, the whole thing thinning toward zero — with Duzsik delivering annihilation in the same soft, unbothered melody, so the tenderness of the voice becomes the cruelest part.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"longing ordered locked then rerouted onto a stranger instead","built":"the tender voice delivering annihilation becomes the cruelest instrument"},
"health~wasted-years": {"haiku":"Time consumed in service to another leaves only bitterness and regret as the speaker betrays their own needs.","opus":"The end of a shared life, built together yet falling apart in isolation, wary of mourners who frittered away the time pretending. Time itself is the disease, and the ache is the disbelief that something meant to last is already lost.","sonnet":"A warning against people who perform grief to serve themselves — 'beware of the ones who'd shed a tear for you, beware of the ones who wasted years.' Something was built together and collapsed alone, and the disease metaphor for time ('another day, another night, time is like a disease') makes the loss feel both slow and terminal.","src":"haiku","fable":"An accusation delivered in HEALTH's signature register — Duzsik's soft, unbothered melody riding programmed concrete-crush and distortion — aimed at a partner whose repeated withdrawals and self-serving displays of prayer and grief curdle into contempt. The title sharpens that into a caution: distrust the people who'd weep at your graveside after squandering the relationship, whose tears are spent on themselves, with time itself reframed as a slow sickness eating the whole thing from inside. What the two of them raised jointly is dismantled in solitude, one survivor left standing, and the closing question — repeated until the words go numb — is less a question than a stunned reflex, disbelief that something built to last could evaporate this fast.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"distrust aimed at those whose graveside grief is spent on themselves","built":"the closing question repeated until the words go numb enacts the stunned disbelief"},
"health~you-died": {"haiku":"Mortality renders all human connection irrelevant—everyone dies, shared streets and shared dreams become nothing, and regret dissolves into cosmic insignificance.","opus":"A stark meditation on mortality and severed friendship. Addressing someone now dead, the narrator flatly accepts he will follow, that the same fate comes soon for everyone. Regret threads through: the wish to have been a better friend, to have done something distinguishing. Long nights and short years alike vanish toward the shared destination waiting for all.","sonnet":"Grief measured against ordinary time — shared streets, shared wants, plans made and then silence, nights long and years short. The speaker addresses the dead directly and keeps returning to the parallel: you died, I will too, and the regret is not dramatic loss but the quiet failure of never doing better while it was possible.","src":"haiku","fable":"An address to a dead person that keeps collapsing the distance between the two of them: you are gone, I will follow, and the roles are perfectly reversible — whoever went first, the survivor is only briefly on the other side of it. The verses hold the specific grief that HEALTH usually drown in noise, letting Duzsik's flat, unguarded delivery carry it: two people who walked the same blocks and chased the same wants, drifted apart over some abandoned plan, and never patched the silence that followed. The bridge turns the elegy inward — a confession of having been a lesser companion than he owed the friend, and of leaving nothing that set him apart from anyone else — before the chant resumes and the pledge to join the dead tightens from distant fact into something already closing in. What sounds like a tender farewell is really the speaker rehearsing his own erasure, using the friend's grave as a mirror for the exit he can already feel arriving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Critics hear the title as the FromSoftware death screen — the flat two words the games hand you at every failure — borrowed for real grief by a band that has scored Max Payne and GTA.","themes":["suicide","death & grief","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"the friend's grave used as a mirror for the speaker's own arriving exit","built":"the address collapses distance until survivor and dead become reversible roles"},
"heart-of-a-coward~decay":{"opus":"A bleak reckoning with mortality: time draining away, each breath carrying him nearer the grave, bound to rot no matter what. The grim counsel is to wring meaning out of a meaningless span, since nothing endures.","src":"opus"},
"heart-of-a-coward~distance":{"haiku":"Two lovers navigate mutual dependence and broken promises in a relationship where one pulls the other back from despair, both bound by vows neither can fully keep.","opus":"Metalcore about intimacy, guilt and rescue, a narrator baring his hidden self and pulling a drowning partner back from the edge, swearing they'll face the aftermath together. Devotion tested by near-loss.","sonnet":"About a relationship where one person nearly lost the other — possibly to a physical crisis or near-drowning — and the desperate vow to close the emotional and literal distance between them, pledging to stay and protect no matter the cost.","src":"haiku"},
"heart-of-a-coward~drown-in-ruin":{"haiku":"A liar and spreader of deception is condemned to suffer and drowns in their own lies and corruption.","opus":"Heart of a Coward turn their fury on a manipulator whose lies and misinformation finally collapse, forcing a reckoning that can no longer be pretended away. The British metalcore outfit stack accusation upon accusation until the deceiver kneels in defeat, drowning in the ruin their own spun stories built.","sonnet":"A reckoning delivered in full fury. Someone driven by hatred and misinformation is told their manufactured stories will be their undoing — the noose of their own deception tightening until surrender is the only option left. Collective rage demands an end to pretending.","src":"haiku"},
"heart-of-a-coward~ritual":{"haiku":"Inescapable cycles of pain and burning; the speaker and others are bound to a ritual fire that consumes them eternally.","opus":"A relentless insistence that suffering is an inescapable rite. Torment is framed as a permanent presence that will never let go, everything destined to be destroyed, and the narrator learns that only by confronting that agony head-on can he understand what it has made of him.","sonnet":"About an inescapable cycle of self-destructive desire — the narrator and whoever is addressed are bound to a ritual of burning, and rather than resisting, they're urged to face the fire directly, because the pain is part of what they've been seeking all along.","src":"haiku"},
"heart-of-a-coward~this-place-only-brings-death":{"opus":"An apocalyptic surrender, the speaker offering up his soul and asking to be buried while the world caves in and flames close around him, choking under a poisoned sky and resigned to watch tomorrow go up in flames in a place that offers nothing but death.","src":"opus"},
"heartlay~a-thousand-debris":{"haiku":"Scattered collapse and inner rupture require deliberate resistance; the speaker stands against enemies that feed on despair.","opus":"A track of stubborn survival against attackers who spread ruin and rot. The singer refuses to be broken, disclaims responsibility for the wreckage around him, and stands firm while his foes destroy themselves, choking on their own filth, as he casts off whatever no longer serves him.","sonnet":"A person who feels psychically shattered but defiant, refusing to absorb the damage inflicted by hostile forces around them — asserting that their enemies' collapse is their own fault, and that the speaker will hold their ground and watch them destroy each other.","src":"haiku"},
"heartlay~broken-seams":{"haiku":"A speaker breaks their own patterns and fears, rejecting lies and decay to cross into new territory. Resistance and defiance fuel passage.","opus":"A resolve to change and mend a wicked pattern without collapsing under the weight of one's own ghostly projections, crossing into something new while refusing to break down.","sonnet":"A song about resisting collapse and internal fracture — the narrator feels the weight of their own projections and the wicked schemes binding them, but refuses to break down, committing to push through decay and emerge without the failures that have held them back.","src":"haiku"},
"heartlay~itch-by-a-leech":{"haiku":"A parasite claims victimhood while poisoning. The speaker rejects fabricated love masking abuse and emptiness.","opus":"A furious rejection of a manipulative person who projected their own emptiness and decay onto the singer; recognizing there was never anything real inside them, the speaker refuses to be their host or their poison.","sonnet":"A song about refusing to be shaped or claimed by a manipulative person — the narrator has been projected onto, expected to become something they never agreed to be, and the other person's misery is framed as a parasite feeding on them; the narrator refuses to let that game define or alter them.","src":"haiku"},
"heartlay~out-of-the-wreckage":{"haiku":"Survival demands clawing out from the wreckage of self-destruction and familial sin; the path forward remains unclear but surrender is no longer an option.","opus":"Clawing out of a self-made ruin after nearly three decades of an inner demon that cannot be tamed or fixed. Survival here is bewildered rather than triumphant, still asking how the damage was done.","sonnet":"A person who has spent 27 years battling an inner demon they cannot tame — feeling like one of God's mistakes, corroded and nauseated — finally begins to crawl out of the wreckage of their own life, though they cannot explain how they survived it.","src":"haiku"},
"heartlay~so-mortal":{"haiku":"Words assault while connection fractures; the speaker breaks their enemy through continued exposure.","opus":"A fractured mind turning on an inner enemy, sitting in a strange calm while vowing to break whatever haunts it. The demand for the thing to get out of his soul insists he's not the frightening creature others made him out to be.","sonnet":"A person confronting an internal enemy — isolated in mental fracture, they feel locked in a shell but moving toward the realization that pain is not permanent, and direct their energy toward breaking free from whatever presence or voice has invaded and occupied their soul.","src":"haiku"},
"heartlay~sovereign-sore":{"opus":"A furious dissection of festering betrayal and manufactured villains. The metalcore band frames a wound that can't heal itself, corrosion spreading beneath a facade of coordinated liars who reshape scapegoats and loop history back on itself. Amid poison and friction, the recurring vow is that flame will wipe it all clean, a purge of the rot that outlives every perspective already exhausted.","src":"opus"},
"heartlay~vilified":{"opus":"A defiant strike back at hollow imposters and false idols. The narrator tears away a veil others blindly follow, watching lost voices fall while pretenders die for an empty crown. Rejecting gods as madness and obsession, he claims his own world and buries the accuser in it, transforming vilification into a declaration of sovereign self-possession.","src":"opus"},
"heartline~crystal-eyes":{"haiku":"Through pain and uncertainty, transformation occurs; accepting what was, what is, and what may be clears vision to become stronger than before.","opus":"An uplifting rock ballad about standing by someone in distress and reframing their suffering as the thing that has made them stronger, urging them to trust and be led through hard times by love. Heartline are a British melodic-rock band, and this is their polished, arena-scale take on comfort and resilience.","sonnet":"A hymn to seeing through grief clearly — the speaker addresses someone whose pain has clouded their vision, urging them toward acceptance of past and present hurt as the forge of something stronger. The recurring image of crystal eyes captures both fragility and clarity: the tears that blur the world are also the medium through which a truer life might eventually be seen.","src":"haiku"},
"heartline~lovers":{"haiku":"Routine and habitual inertia obscure authentic living; transformation requires breaking cycles and reclaiming presence.","opus":"A motivational call to break the numbing routine and actually live before it's too late, warning that tunnel-vision ambition blinds you to the love around you. The speaker, admitting he's been running himself, urges you to look past the grind, free yourself, and choose to be a lover.","sonnet":"An invitation to break routine and look beyond the paradigm of habitual living — \"weave your tapestry to get a glimpse before it's done\" — urging the listener to stop tunnel-visioning through life and become a lover of their own existence.","src":"haiku"},
"heartline~synthetic":{"haiku":"Invisibility and hostility pervade a numb existence where authentic connection collapses into defensive posturing and mutual alienation.","opus":"An anxious confrontation with self-righteousness and eroding connection. The narrator wonders whether to give up or vanish, sensing hostility everywhere and a lost sense of unity. Turning the critique inward and outward at once, he recognizes his own compulsion to keep jumping into what wounds him, insisting he must become better than himself to escape the cycle.","sonnet":"Addiction to self-righteousness in others — hostile, lashing at any challenge — is slowly chewing up the speaker's lifetime, leaving them breathless and chest-burning, unable to stop jumping in even as unity dissolves. The demand is to just put it away.","src":"haiku"},
"heartworms~jacked":{"haiku":"Emptiness and absence consume everything; dissolution leaves only void and the recognition that nothing returns.","opus":"A jittery, art-punk spiral into obsession and nothingness. The London project's Jojo Orme circles a fixation where nothing matters yet everything fixates, effort poured out for nothing that curdles and blackens. Playful and paranoid by turns, the track worries at the idea that nothing turns back, warning against dismissing a void that keeps darkening.","sonnet":"The lyrics are too fragmented and opaque to yield a stable reading — a sequence of oblique phrases and repeated nothings-turning-black that resists coherent narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"heart~barracuda":{"haiku":"A predatory deceiver hides in weeds waiting to ambush, selling dreams and names to make others burn for false promises and games.","opus":"The Wilson sisters wrote this as a snarling swipe at record-business sleaze, provoked by a smear used to promote them. It pictures a slick predator hiding to pounce and force them low, all charm and gossip while it peddles their music and identity, then flips into the sisters biting back, fleeing westward and refusing to be duped.","sonnet":"A predatory figure who lurks, flatters, and ambushes for personal gain gets named and called out. The speaker, having escaped the manipulation once by swimming hard and fast, refuses to be baited again — the barracuda may still be circling, but the target has already gone.","src":"haiku"},
"heathen~a-hero-s-welcome":{"haiku":"Soldiers sacrifice themselves for freedom and country, their courage and suffering honored but often misunderstood.","opus":"The Bay Area thrash veterans salute fighters who go to battle not for spoils or renown but for honor, homeland and their comrades, wondering aloud whether any triumph is worth the toll. Dedicated to those who fell or were maimed, it folds spoken tribute into a reckoning with duty, unavoidable violence, and the steep price of liberty.","sonnet":"Soldiers carry sacrifice no general fully accounts for — fighting not for treasure or fame but for flag, god, and land, willing to lay down their lives so others stay free. The song honors fallen and wounded alike, sitting with the hard question of whether victory ever justifies what was lost to achieve it.","src":"haiku"},
"heathen~arrows-of-agony":{"haiku":"Demonic forces target the speaker with psychological torment, spreading hatred and feeding on deception. Agony becomes an unstoppable weapon that drains sanity.","opus":"Mental suffering dramatized as a hostile force with watching eyes that feeds on the narrator's misery, its shafts tearing into him until he bleeds. The throughline is a man hunted by his own anguish, desperate for calm and control while the torment steadily erodes what is left of his mind.","sonnet":"Demonic forces prey on the narrator's pain, planting seeds of deception and hatred — arrows pierce him repeatedly — and he is left searching for peace and control, the scars remaining even as he tries to wash away the stain.","src":"haiku"},
"heathen~bloodkult":{"haiku":"Urban violence and gang culture form a brutal code where survival means kill or be killed in an endless, vicious cycle.","opus":"A grim portrait of street violence and a kill-or-be-killed code, where guns, corruption and desensitization to death spread through rich and poor alike in an endless, unsavable cycle.","sonnet":"A grim portrait of urban gang violence as an inescapable cycle: packs prey on each other by a kill-or-be-killed code, corruption runs through every level of society, and prisons and graveyards fill without end.","src":"haiku"},
"heathen~control-by-chaos":{"haiku":"Societal collapse unfolds through corruption, violence, and insanity; tyrants burn while their legacy crumbles and all humans decay equally toward death.","opus":"A bleak overview of a humankind lost its way, ruled by disorder on a planet abandoned to death, its record set aflame and its callous men carrying frost where warmth should be. The counterbalance holds that any existence with meaning demands offering, and if the grave opens a fresh start, the toll is one worth meeting.","sonnet":"A bleak survey of a society run by hidden, corrupt forces — history is erased, humanity goes astray, and the only honest response is to recognise the insanity and face the sacrifice that a meaningful life demands.","src":"haiku"},
"heathen~dying-season":{"haiku":"A meditation on the costs of war—bloodshed, loss of life, and the suffering of soldiers—presented as the price of power and greed. The lyrics lament the cycle of violence and the inevitability of death in conflict.","opus":"A grim depiction of war as an endless season of death, with broken promises, marching soldiers, falling bombs, and shattered nations, questioning whether the suffering is ever worth the cost.","sonnet":"An anti-war lament describing a season defined by bombs, fallen bodies, broken promises, and soldiers marching into dust, questioning whether the cost of war — the rivers of blood, the scarred sanity, the timeless wounds — is ever worth it.","src":"haiku"},
"heathen~fade-away":{"haiku":"A person has endured suffering and hardship to build a resilient existence that won't be forgotten or lost; they assert their permanence through perseverance.","opus":"A defiant vow never to be erased, the narrator recounting how he's endured pain and hardship and forged himself specifically to outlast oblivion. The deeper contrast sets a hollow, faith-starved man sinking without any guiding devotion against the singer's determination to rise above his suffering and reach some kind of redemption.","sonnet":"A declaration of resilience built through real suffering — years of inner darkness and walking through fire — against the emptiness of an ambitious man without a soul; the narrator vows to keep shaping his existence rather than simply disappearing.","src":"haiku"},
"heathen~no-stone-unturned":{"haiku":"A solitary journey to understand existence, fate, and mortality—leaving no stone unturned in seeking the meaning of fleeting moments before inevitable death and the end.","opus":"A relentless push toward purpose and self-discovery, undertaken alone against a ticking clock, with the certainty of the grave as its constant backdrop. The urgency comes from mortality itself: treasure every moment and take charge of your fate before time runs out.","sonnet":"A man acutely aware of his own mortality decides to seize every moment and pursue everything fully before the grave claims him — the urgency is not panic but determination, leaving nothing unexplored.","src":"haiku"},
"heathen~red-tears-of-disgrace":{"haiku":"The speaker witnesses escalating catastrophe and injustice—environmental collapse and ignored warnings—leaving only regret and ruin.","opus":"A doom-laden alarm about a planet and society heading for collapse while everyone ignores the signs. It pictures a heating world bled dry, waters running crimson, and a coming judgment, its deeper ache the frustration that people talk endlessly yet act on nothing, craving upheaval and resolution instead of conflict as the world crumbles.","sonnet":"An environmental apocalypse song: temperatures rising, rivers running red, the sky weeping blood — humanity has bitten the hand that fed it and is choking on regret as the planet reaches its end.","src":"haiku"},
"heathen~silent-nothingness":{"haiku":"Darkness consumes someone's soul as they face voices urging destruction, haunted by evil, fear, and a fading existence into silent void.","opus":"Bay Area thrash reaching for gothic dread, tracing a soul hollowed by its own sins until it dissolves into oblivion, with the pull toward self-destruction dramatized as whispering voices that can't be refused.","sonnet":"A song about a soul corrupted and destroyed by its own darkness, haunted by voices urging self-harm, and the warning that wolves of evil are always near for those who lose their inner sight.","src":"haiku"},
"heathen~undone":{"haiku":"Overwhelming internal chaos and self-destruction unfold as fate drowns the speaker in temptation and inevitable decay.","opus":"The Bay Area thrash veterans stage inner ruin as an unstoppable force that surfaces from within and cannot be resisted, dooming the narrator to his own downfall almost accidentally. Wrapped in scriptural phrasing, it treats this collapse as predestined fate, with any return to sanity or salvation now impossibly out of reach.","sonnet":"A man feels a destructive force rising inside him — something long suppressed that now commands total obedience. He frames it in quasi-theological terms, as though fate or creation itself has decreed his unraveling. Resistance is futile; the grave he has been digging for himself is already six feet deep.","src":"haiku"},
"heaven-unknown~decline":{"haiku":"Depression paralyzes as hope withers; the speaker sinks under the weight of emptiness, unable to climb toward the future they can see but cannot reach.","opus":"Depression rendered as a slow decline, the bed turned from refuge into dread and days lost gazing downward, longing for the end. Everything the singer might become hovers just out of reach, frozen by fear in a room that's both freezing and ablaze, every flower in bloom haunting as what might have been.","sonnet":"Depression narrated from inside a burning room where every blooming flower is 'a haunting effigy of what I could be' — the speaker faces their bed with dread, counts wasted seconds, and witnesses the slow dissolution of faith and hope into a steady, unshakeable decline.","src":"haiku"},
"heavensgate~chemical-heaven":{"opus":"A harrowing account of trauma and sedative escape. A violated woman, stripped of something holy, seeks heaven in benzodiazepines to numb paranoia and pain, sweating sleepless as the drug hollows her out. The band renders addiction as a corrosive theft of purity, the narrator feeling their own soul cave in as they witness her spirit ache away.","src":"opus"},
"heavensgate~violent-joy":{"haiku":"Spectacle and suffering blur as the speaker performs anguish for an audience; violence erupts as both escape from and manifestation of the spectator's hunger for catharsis.","opus":"A performer's breakdown fantasized as a massacre, exhausted by bleeding nightly to feed a crowd he comes to despise. An angel's whisper to set them free tips into imagining the audience sprayed with gunfire, self-loathing and rage against the parasitic spectators fused into violent joy.","sonnet":"A performer pushed beyond breaking point by an audience that feeds on their anguish addresses the crowd from the edge of psychosis — an angel whispers to set them free, the show ends in imagined carnage, and the speaker declares themselves \"filled with violent joy\" as the only way out.","src":"haiku"},
"heavenward~choke":{"haiku":"Apathy and waste spread as society burns; the speaker watches indifferently while vapor trails mark the slow strangulation of human consciousness.","opus":"Nihilistic burnout watching the world burn and choking down its toxins, unsure whether any of it is even real. Bored of a life he's already leaving, the singer wavers between numb resignation and a last flicker of doubt.","sonnet":"A suicidal exhaustion expressed as indifference — the narrator is done with this life, hung out for everyone to judge and ignore, drowning in vapor trails while nobody watches the world burn.","src":"haiku"},
"heavenward~wish":{"haiku":"A numb individual trapped in addiction recognizes their pain as necessary and self-inflicted, unable to break the cycle of intoxication.","opus":"Heavenward voice a numbed longing simply to feel again, framing addiction as an intoxicated descent where seeing the ground becomes its own strange comfort. A fallen angel loses her will to fly while love, offered only to the helpless, becomes something to be stolen, and pain gets justified as the last proof of being alive.","sonnet":"Emotional numbness after prolonged pain. An inner life so battered that the capacity to feel has gone silent — not through peace but through damage. The speaker cycles between substance and sensation, searching for something that can penetrate the anesthesia and confirm that healing is still possible.","src":"haiku"},
"heaving~faces-of-you":{"haiku":"Surrounded by accusatory stares, the speaker drowns in external judgment while sleep refuses to arrive. Time suspends and faces multiply, trapping consciousness in waking nightmare.","opus":"A claustrophobic study of insomnia and inescapable presence, frozen and defenseless under scorching stares, dreaming with eyes open, boxed in and hounded by a recurring face there's no fleeing. The hallways fill with shadow and every door circles back to one locked instant, begging for a rest that never arrives.","sonnet":"The speaker is haunted waking and sleeping by the presence of someone — their face everywhere, eyes burning holes, a silence that can't be outrun — begging for sleep that won't come while trapped in a corridor of doors that all lead back to the same frozen moment.","src":"haiku"},
"heavy-halo~damage-me":{"haiku":"Pointless existence and surrendered will merge into ecstatic self-destruction; the speaker begs for violation and obliteration as the only meaningful sensation.","opus":"A surrender to appetite that erases the self. The singer welcomes being wrecked as a form of closeness, hungry for both bliss and destruction, calling their own existence meaningless and casting themselves as a devotee. Amid grim, sordid imagery, being taken apart together becomes the entire aim, a plea to be ruined rather than left whole and hollow.","sonnet":"A submissive surrender to destruction framed as ecstasy — \"damage me, damage everything\" is the request, with razor wire and dirty sheets as the setting. The life is already deemed pointless; the narrator is already a servant deep inside; being unmade by someone else is the only remaining sensation.","src":"haiku"},
"heavy-sails~the-tide":{"haiku":"Oceanic inevitability carries the speaker forward beyond volition; tide and current overpower individual will.","opus":"A song heavy with mourning on the first anniversary of a death, shadowed by remorse. Twelve months on, a loved one's laughter has gone silent while the singer buckles under guilt, hinting he was somehow responsible for the ending. Split between carrying on and joining them, he holds onto a worn picture and lingering traces, chased by ghostly echoes.","sonnet":"Survivor's guilt on the anniversary of a loss — a photograph on the dash, fingerprints still on the window like a ghost; the narrator holds the weight of turning the tide and contemplates meeting \"on the other side.\" The refrain \"say goodnight\" is both a goodbye to the dead and a question about whether the narrator intends to follow.","src":"haiku"},
"heirloom~romanticize":{"opus":"A refusal to romanticize a destructive past relationship and the depravity that cost the speaker his sanity, insisting he won't crawl back or exhume what can't be mended, warning that the end arrives like a flood and that some wishes are best left unmade.","src":"opus"},
"heirloom~we-are-not-the-same":{"haiku":"Privilege blinds one to struggle; the speaker's solitary journey through shame and hardship separates them irrevocably from those who never had to fight to exist.","opus":"Someone who survived addiction and life without a roof draws a firm boundary against a person who kept throwing the past in his face and cannot understand him. Repeating that the two are nothing alike works as both a shield and a quiet victory over where he came from.","sonnet":"About confronting someone who uses the narrator's past against them while being unable to understand or relate to what the narrator has actually been through — the years of self-medicating, homelessness, and guilt that the other person reduces to a weapon in an argument. The narrator insists their experiences have made them fundamentally different people, and the other person will eventually recognize this.","src":"haiku"},
"heists~memorial":{"opus":"A portrait of a broken but hopeful man forever waiting on a right moment that never comes, losing his grip on the passing days as the years drain by. He keeps swearing to quit this life and shed its exhausting trivia, only to pass the same weary loop to whoever follows.","src":"opus"},
"hellhills~a-million-days":{"haiku":"Isolation and despair blur reality with fabrication as the speaker cycles through resentment and numbness; years of unfulfilled longing and self-blame accumulate into existential exhaustion.","opus":"A weary indictment of self-deception and deadened grief. The narrator sees through the comforting stories everyone leans on yet can't rouse himself, faking a life to manage the pain until his own sense of self blurs. His blame settles on someone absent he'll dwell on endlessly, doubting anyone has compassion left when everyone has already lost so much.","sonnet":"Grief that refuses to resolve — the narrator keeps fighting to preserve the very attachments they despise, caught between the real and the fake and running until their own image fades. The turn is brutal: a blank canvas without paint, the admission that they could whine and beg and crawl, and still no empathy comes back.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~before-i-bleed":{"haiku":"Betrayal and dehumanization strip the speaker of eyes and agency; they refuse to join ranks with the psychotic and two-faced.","opus":"A mutually destructive, toxic bond in which each partner wishes the other had finished them off, and the singer won't recover until he cuts the other loose. Convinced everyone is a lunatic pretending otherwise, he vows to grab what belongs to him before he dies.","sonnet":"Two people bound together by mutual damage — one who wishes the other had taken their life, one who can't let go despite being poisoned by the relationship — the narrator finally decides to take back what's theirs before they bleed out, seeing everyone around them as psychos wearing human faces.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~carrying-you":{"haiku":"Burden and sacrifice of bearing another, the speaker weighted by responsibility and love.","opus":"A breaking point in a draining, one-sided bond. The narrator, barely alive and calling himself a parasite feeding on vanity, is sick of endlessly healing another's wounds and resolves to stop carrying them. Torn in two and unable to pretend immunity, he wavers between burial and survival, the song caught between exhaustion and a flicker of wanting someone by his side.","sonnet":"A caretaker at the end of their capacity — sick of healing wounds, thought divided in two, alive but barely — addresses someone who uses them as an emotional crutch, names it plainly as parasitism and vanity, and arrives at the only honest conclusion: I'll have to quit carrying you.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~clarity":{"haiku":"Lost identity seeking guidance while falling away from connection, afraid of another's contradictory and painful mind.","opus":"Losing your sense of self while entangled with someone whose pain mirrors yours, wishing to be told you're okay or simply erased, the terror of not knowing who to be dissolving both people into the same falling away.","sonnet":"Two people mutually failing each other — neither knows how to comfort or be honest with the other, both are losing their sense of identity, and the relationship is dragging them both under rather than holding them up. The song sits in that particular loneliness of being with someone and still feeling utterly alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title names the one thing the song withholds from itself: the narrator covets a steadiness he cannot reach, and the whole design keeps dissolving any fixed footing until selfhood becomes the injury. That looping, sevenfold admission of never settling on an identity is the true center, not filler. It enacts a paralysis the verses merely sketch, worn by repetition into a permanent state. His pleas cancel each other — craving reassurance yet begging to be blotted out, vowing permanence in the same breath as vanishing — and by the bridge the coming-apart turns mutual, both figures sliding loose in parallel. That eerie prod toward a game, slipped into the collapse, is the mask coming unstuck. Earnest distress may be a diversion whose rules he has misplaced, so his last confession is having lost the memory of his own origin.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"selfhood dissolving under the weight of never settling on an identity","built":"the sevenfold admission of no fixed self worn by repetition into a permanent state, not a refrain"},
"hellhills~cloud-surfing":{"haiku":"Frustration with emotional unavailability and deception, wasting life with someone whose presence corrupts.","opus":"A trapped plea against a toxic presence that invades and rots the mind. The narrator can't stand the other's voice yet has no choice, faking smiles while their time is wasted and their core corrupted by lies. The repeated wish for the other to change their mind reads as both hope and helplessness, a longing to fly free of the noise that never comes.","sonnet":"Entrapment in a relationship with someone whose screaming and shapeshifting has invaded and rotted the narrator's mind — they can't stand the voice but have no choice, can't smile without faking it, and the only wish is that the other person changes. The question \"can I fly far away from the noise?\" hangs unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~cut-the-moon":{"opus":"An anguished study of missed connection and armored distance. The narrator stumbles while replaying the past, feigning indifference yet unable to meet the other's needs, each holding the other at arm's length. Scared but unwilling to repeat an old mistake, he watches them slip away, torn between returning and letting go.","src":"opus"},
"hellhills~elude":{"haiku":"Resurrection through divine deception and survival; the speaker steps into another's presence while pretending concealment, seeking eternal escape.","opus":"A murky reckoning with treachery and phony redemption. Deceptions and unkept vows swirl around a figure rotten to the center who deals out the same punishment they have dealt before. The singer bargains for survival and a permanent farewell, laying down everything he owns, while the other keeps ducking out of view, the whole thing a contest of lies and slippery divinity.","sonnet":"A figure corrupted at core, wielding a blade through circumstances they've survived before, asking for survival promises that may not hold. Pride is lost at sea, the goodbye is everlasting, and the white lies and resurrection imagery suggest someone cycling through collapse and recovery so many times they no longer know if the next time will be the last.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~fabricated":{"haiku":"Inherited trauma and toxic ideology calcify into the speaker's core, leaving only the impulse to burn and escape before becoming what they despise.","opus":"A rejection of an inherited, tormenting belief system that's become his own poison, choosing to flee and hunt for something better before it swallows him. The dread of turning into what he despises drives an all-or-nothing break from a manufactured life he can't connect with.","sonnet":"Someone who has absorbed and then become the ideology they once rejected confronts that transformation head-on — 'now I became what I despise' — and chooses to burn rather than continue wearing the fabricated identity, rejecting 'false endeavours' and 'rotten leathers' as beyond saving.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~flinch":{"opus":"A mutually corrosive relationship the speaker calls his own parasite, kept together by hunger and pretty lies, slowly killing him as he waits for it to stop while trading barbs about wasting each other's lives and owing nothing, trusting no one, not even himself.","src":"opus"},
"hellhills~getting-bored":{"haiku":"Contempt and resignation end all effort; the speaker stops trying to connect with someone who only bores them into indifference.","opus":"Weary disenchantment with a partner whose endless chatter, always surfacing when things go badly, has stripped away any faith the singer had. It leaves him indifferent and going through the motions, with wounding him and forced belonging standing in for real closeness.","sonnet":"Contempt and numbness at a relationship that has become exhausting — the other person talks endlessly when things go wrong but cannot be trusted, and the narrator has simply run out of patience and interest.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~kiss-the-pavement":{"haiku":"Power dynamics distort care into domination as the speaker derives meaning from another's dependence. Control and violence merge beneath the guise of necessary guidance.","opus":"A domineering, contemptuous address to someone the singer claims to have created and kept alive, demanding they bow and kiss the pavement. Flickers of guilt about being kinder or more careful surface, but the controlling superiority and enjoyment of the other's self-sabotage win out.","sonnet":"A speaker in a position of cruel power addresses someone beneath them — you will disappear, make me proud, bow because I am the reason you exist — the tenderness of offered silence and guidance is poisoned by the demand for submission and the love of watching the other overcomplicate their life.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~price-of-sympathy":{"haiku":"Manipulation and boundary violation corrode support as the speaker recognizes exploitation while remaining bound. Each departure triggers crossing-back, cementing cycles of harm.","opus":"A draining, manipulative relationship in which one person stages panic and helplessness to stop the singer from leaving, escalating each time he tries to go. He describes both of them digging their own ruin as they tried to purchase each other, and keeps clinging to the hope of one last permanent farewell.","sonnet":"A relationship defined by abuse and provocation: the speaker shelters the other's abuse, knows the price of sympathy changes every time they lose, and repeatedly walks away hoping each goodbye is the last — the other fakes horror and helplessness to draw them back across the line.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~psycho-stasis":{"haiku":"Surveillance and jealousy fester in an unequal relationship, where psychological exposure becomes the condition of belonging.","opus":"A tense psychological standoff between two entangled minds. The narrator watches and is watched, sensing that his sinking is what keeps the other alive and jealous. Something unnamed for so long finally demands acknowledgment, and he arrives at a hard resolution about what he must be and why he must leave, barely careful enough to keep seeing or breathing.","sonnet":"Watched and watching: someone feeds on observing the speaker sink, and the speaker finally names it — they're jealous, alive only through this surveillance — which is enough to clarify what has to happen: leave, even if barely able to breathe.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~running-blind":{"haiku":"Fractured vision and communication breakdown trap communities in mutual incomprehension, forward motion impossible without shared understanding.","opus":"Two people who built a private world when the real one felt too cold, now watching it buckle under the strain of wanting more than they can carry. Unable to decide or get out from under their own heads, needled by strangers' sneers, they stumble on with no idea where they're headed.","sonnet":"A partnership built its own world but then fell; now both people are stuck in their heads, unable to articulate what's undefined, no matter how hard they try they feel like they're running blind — movement without direction, effort without clarity.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~sharpen-my-nails":{"haiku":"Lost control surrenders the speaker to another's will, leaving only the impulse toward self-violation and the cover of performative rage.","opus":"A jittery study of shutting oneself off and scrabbling to survive. The singer readies his claws in advance, strips away disguises and treats being exposed as a bitter gag while pleading not to suffocate. Restlessness, hidden loathing and a dread of never feeling free crash into a controlling bond, complete with graves and lonely trembling, trapped even while craving release.","sonnet":"A speaker who watched someone shiver and could have chosen stasis instead of hatred finds their cover blown and refuses to let go — sharpening nails before they grow, questioning whether weightlessness is possible, pulling back the veil to reveal it was all a joke. Self-isolation holds the speaker together while the obsession still burns.","src":"haiku"},
"hellhills~shattered-frame":{"opus":"Addressed to a partner who belittles the narrator's pain, plays games and shatters him, resenting how they dredge up buried memories and long-dead fantasies to make him suffer alongside them, the broken pieces burying whatever's left as he bleeds and pleads to be gathered up.","src":"opus"},
"hellhills~static-flaw":{"haiku":"Noise and imperfection, flaw as constant static undermining signal.","opus":"A cutting dismissal of shallow devotion and repeated mistakes. Recognizing he is unchanged and so is the other beneath a new face, the narrator rejects cheap promises, hollow prayers, and empty preaching as not enough to move him. Beneath the sneer runs weary self-awareness, the refrain admitting he has learned nothing yet still chooses to walk away for his own sake.","sonnet":"A mutual recognition that neither party has changed and neither will — \"after all I'm the same / after all you're the same, even with a different face.\" The narrator decides to go away for their own sake, not dramatically but quietly, because the other's promises are cheap and their silence preferable to their speech.","src":"haiku"},
"hellions~continental-drift":{"haiku":"Grief separates eternally; the speaker and their partner face void with empty rooms and silence, knowing no bridge crosses the distance death creates.","opus":"A harrowing bedside vigil as a mother dies, spoken to a grieving relative who is coaxed to breathe and swallow food through their guilt over things left unresolved. The void she leaves seeps through the family house like a swarm, forcing the two who remain to confront what comes after together.","sonnet":"A parent sits with a child who is struggling to survive grief — possibly the death of a mother — and the weight of empty space between them fills the home like insects with fluttering wings; the fear of retiring to a silence where everything is consumed haunts both, even as the parent keeps asking the child to breathe.","src":"haiku"},
"hellions~smile":{"haiku":"Stop performing pleasantness and learn to actually smile; depth over social theater.","opus":"A call to shut up and actually connect, indicting the performed smiles and idle gossip that pass for closeness in a crowded human menagerie. It's about building a self before you can meet anyone else honestly.","sonnet":"A speaker fed up with performative social interaction and empty talk urges everyone — himself included — to shut up long enough to actually connect; the human menagerie of meaningless chatter is the problem, and a genuine smile might be the only exit.","src":"haiku"},
"hello-sleepwalkers~a-1irvcef":{"haiku":"Midnight reunion and romantic tenderness; lovers meet in darkness and share intimate moments that feel stolen from normal life, fragile and beautiful in their secrecy.","opus":"Hello Sleepwalkers built dramatic, dual-vocal Japanese rock around exactly this kind of internal showdown. A person barricades alone in a cold, lightless room with a loaded pistol as the clock ticks, then kicks down the door and fires — not at another person but at the version of himself trapped in yesterday's despair. He walks forward into tomorrow, promising to meet again at midnight: violence turned inward as the act that finally frees him.","sonnet":"A person barricaded alone in a cold, lightless room with a loaded pistol while the clock ticks — finally kicks down the door and fires, not at another person but at the version of himself trapped in yesterday's despair — and walks forward into tomorrow, promising to meet again at midnight.","src":"haiku"},
"hello-sleepwalkers~goya-no-machiawase":{"haiku":"Waiting for violence culminates in killing the yesterday-self, time's tyranny finally ending through final confrontation and resurrection.","opus":"A person loads a rifle, breaks down a locked door, and shoots the version of himself he was yesterday. Killing off the old self is framed as the only way to walk into tomorrow, with the promise to meet again the next night as a recurring act of self-renewal.","sonnet":"A figure waits alone in a dark, sealed room with a loaded rifle as a ticking clock marks the border between ending and beginning; he kicks the door down, the gun fires through yesterday's self, and he walks into tomorrow — a nightly rendezvous with the edge that becomes a ritual of rebirth.","src":"haiku"},
"hello-sleepwalkers~shin-sekai":{"haiku":"Collective dreaming yields to desolation as paradise reveals itself as void, escape and continuity merging within fragile new worlds.","opus":"Two travelers drift through a burnt-out Eden of cramped rooms and junk-cluttered time, having forgotten why they set out. Bidding farewell to the old world, they gamble on a faint new one glimpsed beyond a blinding light, choosing reinvention over paradise.","sonnet":"The speaker and a companion have been travelling together so long they've forgotten why; their traced futures lie buried in a six-mat room under accumulating junk, Adam and Eve were only performing in paradise, and somewhere beyond the dazzling light source is a faint new world they've not yet reached.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~are-you-metal":{"haiku":"A celebration of devotion to loud, fast, heavy metal music as the speaker's true identity and the only sound worth hearing.","opus":"A celebration of loud, fast heavy metal and the joy of the music itself, repeatedly challenging the listener with the question of whether they are metal.","sonnet":"A declaration of devotion to loud, fast heavy metal as a way of life — the narrator insists it is the only noise worth believing in and challenges listeners to confirm whether they share that commitment.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~do-you-know-what-you-re-fighting-for":{"haiku":"Questioning emerges as the narrator watches systems breed futility and alienation, entrapment deepening despite resistance and exhaustion.","opus":"Wearing your hands down day after day for loans and future generations that never thank you, only to be ruled by the same futility. The song presses whether the daily grind has any goal at all, or just feeds a system that will outlive you.","sonnet":"A critique of disenchanted labour — working off your claws daily for a system that rules through futility — asking whether anyone knows what they're actually fighting for since neither reward nor escape is guaranteed.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~far-in-the-future":{"haiku":"Two people separated by fate promise to reunite far in the future; one waits while the other may be dying, bound by unspoken connection.","opus":"A vow of devotion to someone dying or comatose, the narrator flatly rejecting the doctors' verdict and clinging to the hope that they're merely asleep. He promises to keep vigil and wait however long it takes, resolving that whenever their heart finally gives out, he'll follow them into death.","sonnet":"A vigil beside someone in a coma or vegetative state — the narrator plays heavy metal to try to provoke a reaction, promises to wait as long as it takes, and resolves to follow whenever the person finally goes.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~faster-we-fall":{"haiku":"Humanity faces collapse because of inequality and war; the song calls for freedom of mind and soul as the only way to avoid catastrophic decline.","opus":"An idealist protest anthem dreaming of a world without dictators, racists, lying politicians or war, contrasting the starving many against the rich few pulling the strings, and warning that we fall faster if we refuse to change. The chorus urges freedom of mind and soul. Helloween are the German power-metal pioneers who helped invent the genre, and this carries that soaring, uplifting-yet-urgent choral drive into overt social commentary.","sonnet":"A straightforward political lament — the speaker dreams of a world without war, without corrupt rulers growing rich while others starve, and warns that without change the collective descent will only accelerate. The chorus pivots from condemnation to a kind of muscular hope: free your mind, the fall is not inevitable.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~i-can":{"haiku":"The speaker declares absolute confidence in their ability to overcome any obstacle and fulfill their destiny, dismissing doubt and defeat. They're determined to make things happen their way.","opus":"A power-metal blast of pure defiant self-belief, the narrator vowing to overcome and prevail against every doubter, the repeated affirmation itself the whole triumphant point of the song.","sonnet":"A self-willed declaration of determination — the narrator refuses to lose, refuses to drown, claims the power to fulfill his dreams and silence doubters. It's straightforward motivational metal, the only stakes being his own ambition.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~i-m-free":{"haiku":"Despite claims of freedom, the speaker observes a world of inequality, suffering, and people running themselves ragged trying to keep up and conform.","opus":"A cry of inner liberation aimed at a chilly, rushed society that demands conformity, where everyone sprints through existence with no room for feeling and is warned never to falter or break ranks. The narrator declines to join that pointless charade, choosing a fresh beginning over madness and calling himself as unbound as anyone can be amid the surrounding sorrow.","sonnet":"An assertion of personal freedom set against a society that demands conformity, emotional suppression, and relentless striving — the speaker refuses to play that game even if the freedom they claim exists in a world still full of suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~if-a-mountain-could-talk":{"haiku":"Humans destroy nature for profit and will face consequences through environmental collapse, with love and apology as the only redemption.","opus":"An environmental and moral lament: humanity destroys what it needs for profit, dooming future generations; the mountain and crying ocean are imagined as witnesses begging us to choose love and apology over hate before it's too late.","sonnet":"An ecological and moral plea: humanity destroys what sustains it, sells its soul for profit, and leaves the damage to future generations — the natural world (mountains, oceans) is invoked as a mute witness to our failure to love and apologize.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~if-i-could-fly":{"haiku":"Without fear, the speaker imagines soaring free and seeing the world from above, no longer stumbling or failing—faith has grown and the unknown holds no dread.","opus":"A power-metal hymn to freeing the mind and finding oneself, flight standing for breaking out of a self-made prison. Its uplift is earnest self-liberation, faith replacing fear of the unknown.","sonnet":"A song about breaking free from isolation and self-doubt — the narrator, standing alone with his fears quieted, imagines flight as the ultimate liberation: to see the world clearly, be unbounded, and finally escape his own cage.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~just-a-little-sign":{"haiku":"Romantic paralysis and social anxiety prevent connection despite desire; the speaker remains frozen between hope and fear, waiting for permission to act.","opus":"The comic agony of paralyzing shyness, standing in a crowd desperate for the smallest encouragement to approach a woman, retreating into fantasy because the terror of looking like a fool freezes the smile on his face.","sonnet":"A man alone in a crowd spots a woman who captivates him but is paralyzed by social anxiety — he wants nothing more than some small signal of permission or encouragement, a sign that the world could be his if he could just find the courage to speak.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~long-live-the-king":{"haiku":"The song celebrates metal fandom and live performance. Audiences wait through cold weather for a king-like performer to ignite the stage, surrendering to adrenaline and the raw energy of heavy music.","opus":"Power-metal devotion to the live show itself, casting the concert as sacred rite and the frontman or the music as a king worth worshipping; the whole song is a fan's ecstatic surrender to sound, sweat, and communion.","sonnet":"A pure act of devotion to a rock idol and the communal ecstasy of a live metal concert, with the crowd united in rain and thunder, screaming the performer's name as an act of worship.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~mr-torture":{"haiku":"Commercial exploitation of pain and suffering transforms torture into entertainment, with victimization packaged and sold as commodity.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek power-metal romp about a pay-per-minute sadist peddling pain to bored housewives, the band leaning into camp horror and phone-line kink for pure theatrical fun rather than any real menace.","sonnet":"A satirical portrait of a professional BDSM entertainer operating through phone lines and the internet, selling pain to lonely clients around the world — equal parts absurdist comedy and commentary on commodified transgression.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~my-sacrifice":{"haiku":"A speaker accepts personal sacrifice and redemption in an afterlife, surrendering worldly attachment and seeking divine forgiveness for past failures and choices.","opus":"The German power-metal pioneers stage a tired appeal for redemption, a singer tormented by voices and ruined decisions, awaiting pardon and a rescuer to release him. He offers himself up in exchange for a dreamed afterlife of honor and splendor with no reason to run or perish, treating death not as an end but as the doorway to a promised world beyond earthly pain.","sonnet":"A life of missed chances and collapsed choices turns toward a promised afterlife where dignity and glory replace the need to run or die. The sacrifice offered is not surrender but a final act of faith — giving over the exhausted self to a world beyond time where the struggling is finally done.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~nabataea":{"haiku":"Ancient democracy and peace surrender to violence; the narrator mourns Nabataea's extinction, drawing parallels to modern cycles of destruction and loss.","opus":"Helloween mythologizes the lost Nabataean civilization of Petra as an early peaceful democracy without kings or war, mourning its fall to raiders and drawing a line to today's extremists and killers of dreams, an anthem against fanaticism.","sonnet":"A tribute to the ancient Nabataean civilization — peaceful, democratic, without slaves or kings — mourning its fall to raiders and drawing a direct line to modern atrocities, asking why the world still cannot reach the standard that civilization set two thousand years ago.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~not-yet-today":{"haiku":"One person bids farewell to a beloved companion who has died, reassuring them before death comes for the speaker as well.","opus":"A quiet farewell from the German power-metal pioneers to a departed friend dearer than family, reassuring him not to fear the dark because death is not truly the finish. Stripped of the band's usual speed, it becomes a gentle promise to one day take the same road, but not now, grief softened by the certainty of an eventual reunion.","sonnet":"A farewell to a dying friend — close as a brother — that refuses to frame death as the final word. The speaker promises to follow the same path eventually but not today; for now he watches over the crossing and commits to walking toward it himself, in his own time.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~raise-the-noise":{"haiku":"An oppressed people discover that melody kills their captors; music becomes revolution and the speaker's eternal song resists enforced silence.","opus":"A fantasy of an occupied, freedom-stripped world where music itself becomes the weapon of resistance, the oppressors undone by their craving for melody. It is metal's own myth: the song as liberation.","sonnet":"A song about using music as a weapon of liberation against an occupying or oppressive power — the conquerors are vulnerable to sound, and one skilled musician can summon melodies that render them helpless and lock them away.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~salvation":{"haiku":"A philosophical inquiry into salvation, the afterlife, and spiritual rebirth across dimensions; the speaker seeks answers about memory and transformation.","opus":"Power-metal veterans Helloween follow an uncertain wanderer dropped into a harsh, unwelcoming world, unsure of his past or destination and troubled by who might rescue humanity in its hour of need. Holding to a private, untainted faith, he treats life as a proving ground for growth toward some higher plane, and the soaring anthem envisions an eternity where everyone is equal and free of fear.","sonnet":"A hymn to faith and continuity across lifetimes — the belief that purity of thought and self-determination carry the soul toward a distant paradise where fear dissolves and everyone held dear is waiting. The anthem is universal, addressed to a whole world still learning and growing toward that horizon.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~the-departed-sun-is-going-dow":{"haiku":"A weary, jaded person faces mortality under a setting sun; betrayed by a friend who dies taking a bullet meant for them, they see death as inevitable and present.","opus":"A cinematic power-metal ballad voiced by a weary warrior making peace with his end, grieving broken dreams and a comrade who died shielding him. The setting sun becomes a recurring image of mortality. Helloween, the German band who essentially founded the genre, lend it sweeping melodic grandeur and a defiant sense of farewell.","sonnet":"A showdown at the end of things — the speaker stands dust-covered in a moment that feels like dying, betrayed by a best friend and questioning whether any love was ever reciprocal enough to justify the sacrifice. As the sun goes down there is no tomorrow, only the reckoning with what loyalty costs and the cold company of the devil where the lonely hearted gather.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~the-sage-the-fool-the-sinner":{"haiku":"A dead tramp harbors hidden wealth and bonds; a woman prepares to leave her sleeping husband for the last time, their thirty years coming to a fatal end that night.","opus":"Two short tales, a homeless man found to have concealed a fortune, and a woman quietly abandoning a partner whose life is about to end, illustrate the refrain that you can't distinguish the wise from the foolish from the guilty. The moral is that destiny is unknowable and outward appearances mislead, wrapped in Helloween's melodic power metal.","sonnet":"Two vignettes about the unpredictability of fate: a destitute Marine veteran found dead from apparent drug abuse turns out to have millions hidden away; a woman prepares for a night out while her husband sleeps through what will be their last thirty years together — neither story ends as it appears.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~the-smile-of-the-sun":{"haiku":"Sunlight and joy have been stolen, leaving only darkness, paralysis, and despair as someone searches for meaning in a grey world. Only another person's presence can drive away the persistent pain and desolation.","opus":"Power metal turning heartbreak cosmic, a world drained of light and warmth where only one person can drive back the grey. Isolation and the plea for rescue give the bombast real desolation underneath.","sonnet":"About the complete psychological darkness that descends when the one person who kept you warm and alive disappears — without them, the world freezes and goes grey, the soul slows toward death, and the only hope is a distant cry for someone, anyone, to come back and restore the light.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~where-the-sinners-go":{"haiku":"A sinner fears divine punishment and mercy, asking not to know their ultimate fate while caught between guilt and repentance.","opus":"A sinner's dread of judgment and damnation, confessing he's a taker and faker while begging God for mercy and not to be shown where sinners go; the fear of losing his mind to old, true words captures a guilty soul terrified of the afterlife's reckoning.","sonnet":"A sinner's fearful prayer — confessing to being a taker and a faker, begging God not to show them where they're headed, just to look away and grant mercy before the reckoning arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~who-is-mr-madman":{"haiku":"A man once vain and lustful becomes institutionalized and medicated, his mind fractured and reality warped by punishment and confinement.","opus":"A theatrical character study of a fallen Casanova undone by his own lust and now confined, drugged, and unsure what is real. The power-metal bombast frames a man losing his grip while insisting the world wronged him.","sonnet":"A man institutionalized for his compulsive sexuality and erratic behavior narrates his own confinement — medicated, spinning, degraded by the 'white men' who took his crown — but promising he'll be back when released.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~world-of-fantasy":{"haiku":"A person retreats into fantasy and escapism because reality feels cold, oppressive, and controlled by others. They long for freedom and autonomy in a world of rules and predetermined paths.","opus":"A revolt against a cold, controlling world, the narrator raised under constant pressure to think and act exactly as others demand, with no say of his own. Feeling that reality was never built for him, he escapes into imagination, questioning whether he can even bear the burden life keeps piling on.","sonnet":"A person crushed by a society that programs you from birth, teaches you what to think, and leaves no room when you fail — retreats into fantasy because reality is too cold and too predetermined to be worth inhabiting.","src":"haiku"},
"helloween~you-stupid-mankind":{"haiku":"Humanity faces its own insignificance and vulnerability as superior alien invaders approach Earth with intent to destroy. The song mocks human arrogance in the face of existential threat.","opus":"A cosmic rebuke of human conceit, jeering at a species that fancies itself alone and supreme. It forecasts crueler, more brutal visitors from the stars arriving to shatter and torch the planet without a shred of mercy, sparing no one, branding humanity foolish for its pride.","sonnet":"A contemptuous warning to humanity that it is not the apex predator it imagines itself to be — something more violent, more advanced, more indifferent is coming, and when it arrives, there will be no survivors and no mercy.","src":"haiku"},
"hellyeah~moth":{"haiku":"Self-destruction magnetizes the speaker like flame; beauty destroys what it touches, and insecurity combusts into ash even as the speaker surrenders to the blaze.","opus":"A man who destroys whatever he loves the moment it turns beautiful, drawn compulsively toward his own ruin; the closing surrender, shedding armor to fly into the sun, carries extra weight as one of drummer Vinnie Paul's final recordings.","sonnet":"A speaker who has endured lifelong mistreatment and exclusion confesses a self-destructive compulsion to ruin beautiful things, driven by insecurity, before arriving at a moment of surrender — stripping off armor, waving the white flag, and flying toward the light knowing it will consume him.","src":"haiku"},
"helmet~unsung":{"haiku":"A person's contributions go unnoticed and their worth unacknowledged. The song reflects on dying without recognition or witness.","opus":"A terse, sardonic take on being overlooked, the narrator noting contributions left unnoticed and turning away from the romantic cliche of dying young and unsung, dismissing early suicide as boring and refusing the martyr's pose. Its clipped, alienated phrasing resists easy reading. Helmet were Page Hamerton's New York band whose downtuned, precision-riff alt-metal defined a drop-D template, and this early-nineties cut is their signature dry detachment set to a monolithic groove.","sonnet":"A dry, unsentimental elegy for anonymity — the speaker addresses someone whose contributions go unacknowledged, dismissing both melodrama and the fantasy of dying young as beside the point. The real indictment is quieter: a person who spoke too clearly and too often ended up dismissed rather than celebrated, their impact rubbed away by indifference.","src":"haiku"},
"hensonn~flare":{"opus":"Hensonn is best known online for phonk and short, atmosphere-first tracks where mood outweighs message, and this piece follows that instinct. The affection is whisper-light — the speaker charmed by someone, finding them irresistibly delightful — delivered as a sweet throwaway rather than a developed idea. The sentiment stays a passing glow, exactly the register of a track built more for feel than for statement.","sonnet":"A whisper-light, barely-there expression of affection — the speaker is charmed by someone, finding them irresistibly delightful, with the sentiment delivered more as a sweet throwaway compliment than a developed lyrical idea.","src":"sonnet"},
"her-last-sight~dead-romance":{"opus":"A furious account of being seduced and betrayed by a deceitful, manipulative lover whose warmth masked something monstrous. Having given everything only to be left for dead, the narrator turns from wounded to vengeful, damning the deceiver to hell and hoping every lie they peddle chokes them.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship that turned predatory — the narrator gave everything to someone who kept lying and now leaves them emotionally destroyed, calling the partner a monster behind a mistress's eyes. The fury escalates into wishing damnation on the other person, framing the romance as a death sentence the narrator naively walked into.","src":"sonnet"},
"her-name-in-blood~from-the-ashes":{"opus":"A defiant rise from being used as a scapegoat and bait, the speaker calling out a false messiah and liar, insisting that though they're told they'll never fly, they'll burn and rise from the ashes, ready to move on.","src":"opus"},
"her-name-in-blood~power":{"opus":"A repudiation of deities and worship, the singer insisting he already holds everything he requires within and refuses to kneel any longer, crowning himself divine while demons pursue him and phonies drop away. The recurring taunt questions whether a person can truly trade away their soul at all.","src":"opus"},
"herbie-hancock~doin-it":{"haiku":"A partner demands independence and freedom to pursue pleasure outside domestic confinement; the speaker refuses to remain home waiting.","opus":"A funk-era vignette from the jazz keyboardist's electric period, staged as a domestic showdown. A partner tired of being left home alone announces she is done waiting and heading out to find her own weekend company. Playful spoken banter frames a declaration of independence, patience exhausted, party time claimed as her right after too many nights sitting still.","sonnet":"A woman who has spent nights alone while her partner went out finally reaches the end of her patience: she was patient day and night, now she's going to find someone to spend the weekend with — the repeated \"it's partytime tonight\" is liberation, not recklessness, the sound of someone done waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"herbie-hancock~watermelon-man":{"opus":"Sung in Portuguese, heartbreak at a lover's absence: the speaker will suffer, cry, and wander to forget, badmouthing the whole world until she returns, unable to enjoy sun or rain because everything now brings only sadness and longing for her.","src":"opus"},
"here-comes-the-kraken~drkgrl":{"haiku":"Darkness becomes refuge and companion; chains, haunting, and death are more comforting than freedom or daylight in a life consumed by internal void.","opus":"A gloomy self-portrait that welcomes inner darkness rather than concealing it. Bound at daybreak inside a spectral mansion of the mind, the singer exposes a hidden side no one has witnessed, where any solace proves fake and evaporates the instant it is held. A death-obsessed shadowy figure sends his heart racing, embodying the alluring tug of his own bleakness.","sonnet":"A dark girl in chains, living in haunted isolation — the narrator is drawn to her precisely because of the darkness she carries, a darkness he hasn't yet met. Comfort is named as illusion, the haunted house as her only refuge, and her constant companion is her preoccupation with dying.","src":"haiku"},
"heriot~demure":{"opus":"A dark meditation on submission and torment, the narrator a servant to another's sin, wrapped in luxury yet smeared with filth, chilled metal against the skin, a prized spirit sold off and sanctioned as terror and judgment take hold.","src":"opus"},
"heruin~leave-me":{"haiku":"Numbness crystallizes from betrayal as the narrator watches emotional capacity drain, departure revealing manipulation masked as partnership.","opus":"The collapse of a relationship in which one person poured in everything and was left emotionally deadened, now leaving for good. The sting is that he still recalls the days when the bond felt real, even as he blames the other for turning his life into misery.","sonnet":"The speaker gave everything to a partner who is now leaving with accusations of control; numb from the betrayal, he recalls when the love was alive and admits he's deep in the dark end — no longer coming back, having recognised the living hell he endured.","src":"haiku"},
"hexen~blast-radius":{"haiku":"Political violence and urban terrorism spring from oppression; the oppressed plot revenge through mass killing, framed as justified resistance against tyranny.","opus":"The technical thrash band dramatizes terrorism in brutal detail, an everyday commute erupting into slaughter at one man's hands. It condemns killing rationalized by religion and politics that turns innocents into collateral, and it frames the resulting devastation as a guarantee of retaliation, one atrocity spawning the next in an endless cycle.","sonnet":"A close-range account of politically motivated terrorism — the bomber on the bus, the bodies scattered, the cycle of vengeance and ideology that makes each explosion feel inevitable to those who plant it. The system it dissects is one where oppression and backlash feed each other without end.","src":"haiku"},
"hexen~chaos-aggressor":{"haiku":"Extremists driven by desperation or ideology bomb buildings and buses—Timothy McVeigh, Columbine, retribution. The song asks whether poverty, mental illness, or conspiracy breeds these acts that slaughter the innocent.","opus":"A stark catalogue of American mass violence, invoking notorious bombers and school shooters as the fallen while asking whether the state or hidden plots bear the blame, and warning that ordinary people always suffer the next blast. Hexen, an LA technical-thrash band from the mid-2000s revival, channel thrash's tradition of social commentary into a cold study of terror and reprisal.","sonnet":"A cold-eyed survey of mass violence and its perpetrators — naming specific atrocities from McVeigh to Columbine as case studies in a larger pattern of extremism, mental illness, and societal failure. The tone is analytical rather than mournful, tracing how desperation or ideology produces attacks on federal buildings and buses, and warning that the cycle simply continues regardless of who is caught or executed.","src":"haiku"},
"hexen~gas-chamber":{"haiku":"A serial killer (thirteen victims) faces execution by gas, mocking his own death as humane torture, melting organs and eyes as cyanide consumes him without answers.","opus":"A thrash narrative from inside a serial killer on death row, pleading insanity and boasting of his kills, then walking through his own execution as the cyanide takes hold. The final twist skewers the phrase most humane, the killer feeling his eyes melt while the state calls it mercy.","sonnet":"A first-person narrative from the perspective of a serial killer on death row — he describes murdering thirteen victims with enjoyment, dismisses the state's power over him since he already craved death, and then narrates his own execution by gas chamber from the inside as his organs melt.","src":"haiku"},
"hexen~knee-deep-in-the-dead":{"haiku":"A lone soldier on a Mars base fights demonic invaders that pour through an opened dimensional gateway. Each kill spawns ten more, and the creatures overrun Earth—it's already too late.","opus":"A first-person survival-horror narrative lifted from Doom, a Mars base where an interdimensional portal opens onto hell, its demons slaughtering everyone until the lone soldier hunts the hunters with infrared, killing ten for every one he drops as they spill toward Earth. Hexen, the LA technical-thrash outfit, borrow the game's episode title and turn its lore into breakneck, riff-dense thrash, sci-fi nightmare as pure adrenaline.","sonnet":"A first-person survival horror narrative set on a Mars base — a lone soldier recounts how a dimensional gateway opened by scientists unleashed demonic life-forms that killed everyone else, leaving him armed and isolated, cutting off escape routes and hunting the hunters through infrared darkness. By the end the invasion has reached Earth and it is already too late.","src":"haiku"},
"hexen~past-life":{"haiku":"A deeply depressed person reflects on lifelong suffering and accumulating scars, finding only death as escape.","opus":"Technical-thrash revivalists Hexen write for the profoundly depressed, arguing that one's own thoughts are the cruelest prison and death the only genuine peace. A buried former self leaves psychic wounds that keep resurfacing, and the band lands on a bleak philosophy: no divinity without suffering, and deception has a shelf life.","sonnet":"A person who has always felt misplaced in life looks back at accumulated mental scars and finds they have confessed, buried, and outlasted a past self — yet relief never quite arrives. Freedom exists only in the idea of death; the living aftermath of memory keeps pressing in. Withdrawal and disavowal are the only exits left standing.","src":"haiku"},
"heylu~surviving-on-my-own":{"haiku":"Isolation becomes the only sanctuary as grief and medication blur into survival; the speaker builds walls against a world that offers no comfort.","opus":"Grief and dependence after a loss, where a Xanax habit and a departure have hollowed the speaker out. Beneath the plea for someone to stay is dread of the alternative: getting through it, and grieving, completely alone.","sonnet":"A person grieving a loss — possibly someone who said goodbye permanently — asks whether they are even allowed a good day, confesses that Xanax has fractured their life, and admits they have no idea how to survive the loneliness of continuing without the person they needed.","src":"haiku"},
"hiatus-kaiyote~breathing-underwater":{"haiku":"Sacrifice and material devotion prepare the soul for transcendent reunion; love manifests through accumulated small gestures toward the beloved's discovery.","opus":"Nai Palm turns a lover's undoing into something almost sacramental, cataloguing small gifts and tokens as a way of surrendering someone she loves to another. The submersion image is the point: love as a drowning you choose.","sonnet":"A series of tender, surreal offerings — feathers, blue linen, chamomile, an aorta tied with artistry — are left for a lover to find, as the speaker learns to breathe underwater in the depths of devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"hiatus-kaiyote~choose-your-weapon":{"opus":"Melbourne's Hiatus Kaiyote made their name on future-soul that treats the studio as an instrument, and this title track narrates that method from inside. A band member recounts how a keyboard's accidental sweep became an overture-like collage of toys, grooves, and fragments. The result maps the album's emotional terrain before a proper song even starts, process elevated into prologue.","sonnet":"A band member describes the creative process behind the album's title track — how a keyboard's accidental sweep sound became an overture-like collage of toys, grooves, and studio fragments that together map the album's emotional terrain before it begins.","src":"sonnet"},
"hiatus-kaiyote~laputa-taylor-mcferrin-remix-feat-anderson-paak":{"haiku":"Yearning for an unreachable beloved drives endless pursuit across time; the floating castle remains forever distant despite all efforts to bridge separation.","opus":"Built on the Miyazaki castle in the sky, it uses that unreachable floating city as a figure for someone who rose out of reach after a breakup. Longing dressed as fantasy, missing a person who ascended past you.","sonnet":"A floating castle of the imagination — drawn from Miyazaki — becomes a symbol of beauty and refuge that corruption threatens; both the original voice and the guest verse long for that airborne world while running from forces that hunt them down and drag them back to earth.","src":"haiku"},
"hiatus-kaiyote~molasses":{"haiku":"Stagnation and decay characterize existence; choice and compass become metaphors for agency within systems designed to constrain.","opus":"A pep talk to someone armored and stuck, laying out who they could become if they stopped guarding themselves. The refrain admits things may never improve, so the real subject is choosing to let go anyway.","sonnet":"A slow, circling address to someone armored and buried — perhaps a version of the self — that imagines them as a compass, a point of traction, an opposing opinion, a place where things might be let go, all while the speaker runs blind through a mechanical landscape, trying to read cold braille and find a way to tessellate with what they've built into.","src":"haiku"},
"hiatus-kaiyote~swamp-thing":{"haiku":"A swamp creature hungry for human connection and skin lurks in dark waters, offering false comfort that ultimately crushes.","opus":"A sultry, predatory fable about a lurking swamp creature that hungers for flesh, its come-close tenderness curdling into a threat to crush and drown its prey and kiss it into eternal sleep. Menace wears the mask of seduction. Hiatus Kaiyote are the Melbourne future-soul band led by Nai Palm, and this showcases their signature: lush, jazz-warped neo-soul with knotty rhythms and richly strange, image-dense lyricism.","sonnet":"A seductive horror parable — a creature from the swamp, grinning and toothless, lurks beneath the surface driven by hunger and cold habitual desire, luring someone close with the promise of safety before the pressure of the water crushes them into eternal sleep. The tone is languid and sensory, the danger intimate rather than monstrous.","src":"haiku"},
"hide~run-rabbit-junk":{"haiku":"Fragmented urban and law-enforcement references (cops, downtown, car chases, radio traffic) create a chaotic scene of frustration and confrontation.","opus":"A frantic, collage-like rush of police-radio chatter, downtown chaos, and party-time mania, more sonic energy than argument. The discernible throughline is urban overload and adrenaline, cops, fights, dope, breakdancing, and media noise (CNN, BBC) blurring into a hyperkinetic snapshot of a wired, dysfunctional cityscape.","sonnet":"A chaotic, police-scanner-inflected piece that channels the language of radio dispatch, street confrontation, and urban noise — cops calling for backup, someone being kicked and fighting back, cameras and news networks rolling. It doesn't tell a linear story but evokes a frantic city in a state of low-grade crisis.","src":"haiku"},
"high-on-fire~bastard-samurai":{"haiku":"A mercenary killer shaped by violence trades lives for profit in a blood-soaked arena. Their yakuza tattoo marks them as the violent creature they've become—severing heads and bringing fate to others.","opus":"A brutal warrior's monologue counting fingers as he readies to kill, invoking gladiators, yakuza tattoos and severed heads, cast as a doomed swordsman betrayed on a killing floor. It is bloodlust rendered as mythic swagger. High on Fire are Matt Pike's power trio born from the ashes of Sleep, and this is their sound distilled: scorching, riff-avalanche stoner-metal welded to relentless, battle-hardened aggression.","sonnet":"A warrior's self-mythologizing in the language of ritual combat — counting fingers like tallying kills, sharpening a blade, taking on the identity of an outcast fighter marked by a yakuza tattoo and surrounded by those who profit from betrayal. The bastard samurai is both victim and executioner, surviving in a world that conspires against him.","src":"haiku"},
"high-on-fire~frost-hammer":{"haiku":"A frozen messiah rises from the tundra, leading a northern dynasty of warriors who despise mercy. They sail through endless night, warlords spreading blight, driven by granite hearts intent on conquest.","opus":"A frozen sword-and-sorcery epic about an icy messiah and a tyrant's blight, with northern warriors marching to break the endless glaciers, its mythology drawing on Lovecraft's Plateau of Leng. Matt Pike's ferocious trio delivers it in signature style: molten galloping riffs and bellowed, punishingly heavy grandeur.","sonnet":"A cosmic Norse-inflected war myth set in an age of ice — a warrior born from glacial cold crosses a frozen tundra to fulfill a destiny written in the stars, while despotic warlords cast their shadows and sons of the north prepare for battle. The Plateau of Leng looms as a mythic backdrop, and the frost hammer descends as the only thing capable of breaking through.","src":"haiku"},
"high-on-fire~snakes-for-the-divine":{"haiku":"A dark, mythic narrative of divine punishment: a sinner is sentenced to venomous snakes, ancient crimes echo through time, and suffering plays out as cosmic retribution.","opus":"A pounding occult saga of blood offerings, ruin, and dynasties climbing and crashing across vast spans of time. Its serpent imagery treats destiny as a verdict issued from on high, vast and pitiless.","sonnet":"The song conjures an ancient, mythological cycle of punishment, sacrifice, and uprising, where a condemned figure is handed divine serpents and the forces of darkness rise to scourge and destroy. Cities, civilisations, and ten thousand years of history are swept into an endless rhythm of rising and falling.","src":"haiku"},
"high-on-fire~the-sunless-years":{"haiku":"A man consumed by hallucinogens spirals into madness and conspiracy, haunted by visions and unable to distinguish truth from delusion.","opus":"Matt Pike channels a mind dissolving into conspiracy and chemical haze, chemtrails and star-beings and satellites, and the horror is watching someone lose the thread of their own life while insisting it all connects.","sonnet":"A figure lost to acid and conspiratorial thinking — chemtrails, reptilians, satellites — drifts through visions while the world's real violence and overpopulation play out around him. The song sits between sympathy and alarm, watching someone disappear into the static.","src":"haiku"},
"high-on-fire~waste-of-tiamat":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic chaos erupts as ancient demons vanquish civilization; survival becomes impossible.","opus":"An apocalypse rendered in Babylonian myth, the primordial chaos-dragon rising as heaven and hell collide. It's less a story than a relish for cataclysm, conquest and blood staged as something almost holy.","sonnet":"A mythic-apocalyptic vision of chaos and ancient evil unleashed — nuclear devastation, fallen angels, demonic armies, and occult conspirators converge in an Armageddon where the boundaries between heaven and hell collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"high-regard~dead-to-me":{"opus":"A venomous kiss-off that curdles sympathy into severance. Sitting amid the debris of a ruined relationship, the narrator writes the other out like a symphony, admitting they would already be dead to him if not for lingering pity. Defiant against threats and accusations of softness, the track channels heartbreak into scene-causing spite and a vow to come out stronger.","src":"opus"},
"higher-power~all-the-rage":{"haiku":"Vagueness becomes fashionable evasion while the speaker awakens to present awareness. Distance and blame blur as time demands accountability for silence.","opus":"A hardcore examination of maturing, being present, and owning up to oneself. With his hands buried in dirt, the singer asks whether he has truly transformed or just shed an old outlook, sifting through what people leave in their wake. The chorus ridicules the trendy comfort of being noncommittal and stalling, answering it with a fierce resolve to stay wide awake and fully here.","sonnet":"A speaker presses their hands into the soil and asks whether they have grown — faces change, the distance between people grows for unclear reasons, and the speaker names the current cultural posture of vagueness and lateness as 'all the rage', declaring themselves present and awake in contrast.","src":"haiku"},
"higher-power~fall-from-grace":{"haiku":"A person faces consequences for their own mistakes, falling from grace and lost in regret, watching everything they built burn.","opus":"Higher Power, a British hardcore band who fold nineties alt-rock and groove into their sound, watch someone spiral toward a self-inflicted end. Undone by their own choices and left completely isolated, the person is told there's no future left, and the song presses one bruised question: were the decisions that got them here worth everything they cost?","sonnet":"A quiet autopsy of self-destruction — someone watching the stars and wondering what it feels like to burn, filling a room with smoke while the night closes in. There is no rescue, only the question of whether the decisions that brought them here were worth what they cost, as grace slips away and the only remaining act is to let it all go to ash.","src":"haiku"},
"higher-power~low-season":{"haiku":"A person sinks deeper into depression as seasons darken, unable to climb out or stay afloat. The low season strips them down; they fall to pieces.","opus":"The British hardcore band charts a slide into depression, the singer's world dimming as his mind sinks and a familiar bleak stretch returns. Steeling himself for a long drop he cannot stop, he describes an unrelenting downward spiral, his inner light draining away and his spirit turning cold as he sinks ever deeper.","sonnet":"Seasonal depression settles in with the darkening days — a familiar, named enemy that drags the speaker downward until he cannot surface. The metaphor is physical: bleeding out light, climbing deeper as the world goes grey, the fall continuous and the bottom still not reached.","src":"haiku"},
"higher-power~wide-awake":{"haiku":"Transcendence remains theoretical as rusted promises corrode hope itself, leaving only disillusionment. Two seekers collapse toward earth before achieving flight.","opus":"A moment of sharp clarity in which every gap is seen and offered love. The lift of flying with friends is undercut by the recognition that empty promises have corroded them all.","sonnet":"A moment of clear-eyed disillusionment: fully awake and in focus, the speaker sees that the golden wings he and his friends dreamed of flying on have rusted, the empty promise of it all corroding their souls.","src":"haiku"},
"highly-suspect~big-shop-of-horrors":{"opus":"A furious, unflinching protest against religious violence and the slaughter of children. Naming a litany of war-torn places, the band refuses silence as bombs fall in the name of faith, indicting digital piety and hypocrisy. Rage and grief spill over in tears and trembling hands, the narrator insisting this bloodshed is evil, not biblical, and vowing to fight rather than stay quiet.","src":"opus"},
"highsociety~burn-feat-craig-mabbitt-of-escape-the-fate":{"haiku":"Suffocation and numbness spiral into burning anxiety as the speaker cycles through desperation and collapse. The body rebels while the mind detaches, locked in repeating trauma.","opus":"A visceral portrait of a panic attack rendered as drowning, this metalcore collaboration turns anxiety into something physical and combustible. The narrator spirals through the same trapped loop, numb yet suffocating, teetering at the edge of surrender. Craig Mabbitt's post-hardcore pedigree lends the track its cathartic scream, converting private dread into a communal release valve.","sonnet":"Anxiety as suffocation — breathing underwater, eyes rolling back, the body bursting at its seams — the narrator is locked in a cycle of looking backward and going numb, unable to escape the weight pressing on their chest.","src":"haiku"},
"highsociety~dead-by-dawn":{"haiku":"Blame and resentment poison memory as the speaker retreats into indifference to survive betrayal. Lightning and storm destroy what remained of shared understanding.","opus":"A friendship curdled into enmity, where the speaker blamed the other for damage that was really a shared storm. The real pull is nostalgia for ignorance and indifference, a wish to stop feeling the truth that they were more than the things that hurt them.","sonnet":"A friendship turned enmity examined through storm imagery: the speaker ignored warning signs, blamed the other for damage, and now harvests the hate while fighting to reach indifference — missing the ignorance they lived in before lightning struck and best friend became enemy.","src":"haiku"},
"hikes~tinker-creek":{"opus":"Named for Annie Dillard's book, this math-rock reverie muses on nature, change, and the restless human urge to name and possess. Emerging from winter into spring, the narrator feels altered yet uneasy, questioning why ownership brings closeness and longing for meaning that isn't merely a means to some end.","sonnet":"A reflective song about seasonal change and the difficulty of emotional closeness — the narrator notices they are different person-to-person, warns against attaching too much meaning to transient things like stray dogs or passing clouds, and asks a friend to show them a way of being that has no fixed endpoint.","src":"sonnet"},
"hilltop-hoods~1955":{"haiku":"Nostalgia traps consciousness in arrested time where small-town stasis and conservative values preserve a frozen past.","opus":"A love letter to a sleepy town frozen in another era, framed by vintage radio jingles and pulp Martian broadcasts, where life crawls and the narrator feels both out of step and settled. Under the cliches it's pride in a place the wider world couldn't be bothered to name, and that stays home regardless.","sonnet":"A meditation on living in a small Australian city that feels trapped in another era — Plato-quoting bench men, Fox News fundamentalists, paranoid strangers clutching handbags — told with affection and dry comedy, concluding that no matter where you go, this anachronistic place will always be home.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~a-whole-day-s-night":{"opus":"A hungover confessional after a bender that swallowed a full day, the narrator nocturnal, wrecked and spending recklessly, swearing off booze forever while daylight pounds his skull and he rings around to insist he's fine.","src":"opus"},
"hilltop-hoods~brainbox":{"haiku":"A skillful rapper rejects manufactured media narratives and false authority, asserting that truth matters more than hype or manufactured celebrity while warning against passive acceptance of lies.","opus":"A dense show of skill about staying honest in rap, with the MC styling himself as an incorruptible operator who refuses to be anyone's puppet, waiting for the day rappers get paid in truth rather than hype. The hook urges listeners to open up whatever is trapped in their minds and roll together. Hilltop Hoods are the seasoned Adelaide crew who grew into one of Australia's biggest rap acts, and this flaunts their intricate wordplay.","sonnet":"A dense showcase of lyrical confidence from a rapper who positions himself as a plain-speaking antidote to industry phoniness and rival posturing — the brainbox of the title is both a locked vault of real thought and a rebuke to those who peddle manufactured reality. The verse moves between sardonic wit and genuine conviction that hip-hop still has the power to carry people beyond the grind.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~circuit-breaker":{"haiku":"A rapper boasts relentlessly about unmatched skill, dominance, and superiority, dismissing all competition.","opus":"Hilltop Hoods, the Australian hip-hop mainstays, wrap boastful battle-rap in voicemail skits about an overdue album, Suffa piling absurd punchlines and self-aware laughter at his own greatness. Between claims of ringing bells and lyrical storms, the electric hook warns not to touch him. The comic interruptions puncture the bravado, staging the grind of finishing a record as its own running joke.","sonnet":"A bravura hip-hop self-declaration interrupted by voicemails and self-commentary — Suffa raps with sharp wit about dominance and craft, then breaks the fourth wall to admire his own lines. The meta-humour undercuts the bravado without negating it; the track is simultaneously a flexed muscle and a wink at its own absurdity.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~city-of-light":{"haiku":"Street art and graffiti culture birth community and identity in Adelaide; the city's government suppression of painted walls erases grassroots expression.","opus":"An Adelaide rap origin tale, a place known for its many churches and its notorious murders, recounted through teenage spray-paint crews, rail depots and boxed wine. It laments how authorities scrubbed that world grey and frames making music as reclaiming the surfaces they lost.","sonnet":"A love letter and memoir about growing up in Adelaide — two MCs trace their adolescent years through graffiti culture, poverty, getting kicked out of school, and the slow grind of building a rap career in a city that's both beautiful and brutal, insisting the hustle was earned.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~clark-griswold":{"haiku":"A devoted father's struggle to maintain stability and purpose amid chaos reveals that perfection recedes while love persists.","opus":"A well-intentioned dad sizes himself up against the hapless holiday-movie father, striving to look after his home, spouse and kids as chaos keeps undoing his efforts. The candor lies in owning that he is a lovable wreck giving it everything he has, fully aware he falls well short of perfect.","sonnet":"A self-portrait of an ordinary family man trying and failing to hold everything together — world's greatest dad mug, Chicago Bears cap, trouble following everywhere — who recognises he's a beautiful mess, confesses he's only human, and keeps trying to do right by his house, his picket fence, his children and his wife.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~cosby-sweater":{"haiku":"A rapper boasts of superiority and dominance, celebrating confidence and swagger. The song is primarily braggadocio about skill, fashion, and unflinching self-assurance.","opus":"A braggadocious display of rap confidence and dominance, packed with pop-culture and celebrity name-drops, the emcees casting themselves as champions always several moves ahead of rivals.","sonnet":"A confident hip-hop boast track in which the narrator asserts his creative dominance over rivals and compares himself to chess masters and legends, delivering punchlines and cultural references with deliberate playfulness and self-assurance.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~don-t-happy-be-worry":{"haiku":"Existential dread supersedes happiness as planetary collapse approaches; the speaker catalogs catastrophe while trapped between awareness and resignation.","opus":"An anxious hip-hop inversion of the be-happy cliche, cataloging modern dread from climate collapse to nuclear fear and naming names in the process. The joke that a little worry is healthy barely masks genuine doomsday panic and the urge to build a bunker.","sonnet":"A deliberately inverted self-help refrain — the narrator catalogs real global threats from climate collapse to nuclear risk to authoritarianism and argues that being worried is the only rational response, not a failure of attitude.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~exit-sign":{"haiku":"Rejection of shallow social performance and hollow relationships; the speaker cuts ties to preserve time and selfhood, unwilling to waste existence on toxic company or false reconciliation.","opus":"A refusal of the shallow party circuit and the phony hangers-on who inhabit it, spun as one long search for the way out. Beneath the jokes lies a man who cares about nothing there except his own kids and his time, which he won't waste on oxygen thieves.","sonnet":"A rapper chronicles his compulsive need to leave social situations — parties, scenes, conversations — that drain his time and energy, framing time as currency he refuses to spend on oxygen thieves, and anchoring his reason to stay anywhere only in his family: \"the only thing I give a fuck about in this phony world is Ari and Poppy.\"","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~higher-feat-james-chatburn":{"haiku":"Relentless touring fractures relationships; success's fever demands sacrifice while love remains the only grounding force against dissolution.","opus":"The toll of relentless touring, chasing the highs of music and the road while sacrificing rest, home, and the loved ones left behind. Flying ever higher toward the sun, the singer knows the only way is down, burning up in the fun that consumes him.","sonnet":"The cost of a touring musician's life measured against what's left at home: the road is a storm blowing him ashore, music brought him back to life but the partner got the sacrifice — \"lost time, lost sleep, lost point of view\" — and the refrain of flying higher toward the sun ends in falling in flames back to earth.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~i-love-it-feat-sia":{"haiku":"Rappers celebrate the hard-won rewards of perseverance and dedication to craft despite years of struggle and uncertainty.","opus":"The Adelaide rap group, with a soaring Sia hook, turn out a tribute to their own craft and the long climb behind their success, pouring pain into work and starting their own label. It celebrates relentless effort and the loyalty of friends they've had since youth as the real payoff, still hunting one more perfect moment.","sonnet":"Hip-hop as vocation and survival — built from nothing at a bus stop, sustained through paperwork, tours, and grind — is celebrated as something genuinely loved rather than merely pursued. Hard work redeems itself through the moment of pricelessness it occasionally produces, and the crew that shares the road makes the sacrifice worth it.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~i-m-a-ghost":{"haiku":"Artistic legacy and creative exhaustion blur into departure; the speaker walks away from decades of work, grateful yet finally invisible, ready to haunt only their own past.","opus":"A veteran rapper takes stock of a career that gave him everything, vowing that the day the spotlight stops moving him he'll leave with nothing but thanks. It's about mortality within the art, honoring those who lifted him, and accepting that everything good eventually ends.","sonnet":"A veteran rapper traces his arc from bedroom liner-notes to packed venues, from being invisible to being on the flier, and acknowledges what rap saved him from while knowing that the day the crowd of lighters stops inspiring him he'll walk out — a ghost — gone before the writing on the wall falls.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~i-m-good":{"opus":"The Australian hip-hop veterans turn pandemic lockdown into wry, wounded comedy, cataloguing boredom, day-drinking, and organizing sock drawers. Beneath the jokes about rescue dogs and homeschooling runs real grief and loneliness, missing loved ones and lost normalcy. The chorus performs the polite lie of being fine while quietly admitting it's awful.","sonnet":"A darkly comic pandemic dispatch — the rapper narrates lockdown with self-deprecating humor: sorting socks, watching Predator, applying for a rescue dog, waiting on Valium deliveries, day-drinking while homeschooling. Beneath the jokes is genuine loneliness, missing his mum, struggling with isolation, and admitting he is 'kinda awful' behind the 'I'm good' facade.","src":"sonnet"},
"hilltop-hoods~laced-up":{"opus":"A cash-strapped man tries to keep up appearances on a lavish date he can't afford, sweating over the bill and champagne, splitting the check or going Dutch, the floor like quicksand as he faces the gap between the woman's worth and his empty war chest.","src":"opus"},
"hilltop-hoods~leave-me-lonely":{"opus":"A comedic yet pointed kiss-off to a clingy, annoying acquaintance the narrator wants gone, punctuated by a bridge revealing that person's real struggle with weed, Molly and self-harm, complicating the demand to just be left alone.","sonnet":"A song about a drunk, chemically altered person making social situations unbearable — the narrator endures escalating hostility from a belligerent stranger at a bar, delivers a blistering torrent of insults and comparisons, and ultimately demands to be left alone. The subject claims later that the substances made him act that way, but the narrator isn't buying it.","src":"sonnet"},
"hilltop-hoods~live-and-let-go":{"haiku":"Release becomes necessary as the speaker surrenders control and expectation. Living requires letting die what no longer serves.","opus":"The Adelaide hip-hop veterans trade braggadocio for hard-won emotional maturity, wrestling with grief and the difficulty of releasing pain rather than hardening around it. The verses argue that tears cleanse rather than weaken, and that guarding one's softness is the real victory. A meditation on forgiveness as self-preservation from a group known for craftsmanship over posturing.","sonnet":"An extended meditation on grief and emotional release: pain hangs like cigarette smoke, living-and-letting-go is like talking with a mouth full of sediment, but the argument is that tears clear the windows of the soul — only by keeping the warm heart intact can you fight the devils without being made hard by them.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~obese-lowlifes-feat-mystro-braintax":{"haiku":"Multiple rappers describe their lyrical flow, social consciousness, and street credibility while questioning whether superficial lowlifes could be desirable partners.","opus":"A posse cut trading self-mocking brags, jabs at early-2000s political leaders, and the notion that music is what keeps a threadbare existence from falling apart. The Adelaide hip-hop institution links up with UK guests Mystro and Braintax for a cheeky, boom-bap-rooted transatlantic team-up built on humor rather than menace.","sonnet":"Three rappers trade verses that blend self-deprecating humour with genuine love for the craft — they are broke, unglamorous, possibly unlovable, but so devoted to music that it holds their lives together when nothing else does. Politicians from John Howard to Tony Blair get equally roasted, the whole thing operating as a rowdy communal toast to outsider art.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~pyramid-building":{"haiku":"True wealth lies in love and family rather than accumulation; the pursuit of material excess paradoxically enslaves rather than liberates.","opus":"The Australian crew preach that a person can be rich with an empty bank account if the people around them are well, and impoverished in a huge house of unused rooms. Their creed is that craving more only shackles you, prizing respect and basic security over luxury while greed climbs on the backs of the deprived.","sonnet":"A counter-argument to consumer culture: wealth without love is empty architecture, and a life spent chasing debt relief is a life escaping rather than living. The narrator would trade financial complexity for simple respect and contentment, watching the wealthy build pyramids while children go cold — the more desired, the less free.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~recapturing-the-vibe":{"haiku":"Hip-hop artists reclaim their authenticity and cultural legitimacy after mainstream dilution, addressing the real struggles and changing their community. They critique fake rappers and corporate appropriation while celebrating genuine MC culture.","opus":"A braggadocious hip-hop declaration of authenticity, dismissing fake rappers with no credibility while claiming genuine love for the scene and the craft; the deeper read is a defense of purposeful, culture-furthering rap over gimmickry, the MC insisting he bled for his dreams and still feels the vibe.","sonnet":"An Australian hip-hop crew reasserts its legitimacy after being slept on, arguing that critics and cynics are just excess weight while the Hoods have kept bleeding for their scene and their city — it's about the earned right to still be here, still be relevant, and still move people.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~speaking-in-tongues":{"haiku":"Hatred and division poison humanity while music becomes the universal language that transcends prejudice, breaking cycles of misunderstanding through rhythm and unity.","opus":"A plea for human unity across race, religion and sexuality, insisting we're all facets of one shared humanity while lamenting that anyone preaching tolerance gets treated like they're babbling in a language no one can parse.","sonnet":"A call for human unity across race, religion, sexuality, and class: the MCs argue that bullets don't discriminate, refugees trade death for prison, and the anger driving division is a cancer that logic must counter — but the message of peace keeps being received as if it were spoken in tongues.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~stopping-all-stations":{"haiku":"Train passengers embody interlocking tragedies and moral failures as violence, illness, and exploitation ripple through interconnected lives.","opus":"Three overlapping tales unfold on a commuter train, where an elderly ex-serviceman, a burnt-out dancer and a cornered robber collide in a knifing, narrated with detached, watchful clarity about how far people are pushed and how much nerve they show. The journey becomes a reflection on brutality, bravery and the worth of a life, closing on the meager coins and card left behind.","sonnet":"A train journey through the lives of three marginal people — a half-blind war veteran fined for an expired ticket, a stripping mother who steps in to defend him from armed robbers and gets struck down for it, and the robber himself who never intended to go so far — all intersecting in a moment of violence, sacrifice, and quiet human courage.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~the-art-of-the-handshake":{"haiku":"A young person navigates urban culture and street encounters, learning the rituals of connection and respect through handshakes that build community bonds.","opus":"The Australian hip-hop trio spin vivid teenage nostalgia, riding graffiti-covered trains down to Adelaide with headphones and Rakim, dodging cops and cadging into shows to freestyle. A sampled aside on the handshake's origins, a gesture proving no weapon is drawn, threads through memories of a scene bound by ritual respect. The old twenty-second handshakes stand in for a whole culture of belonging and mutual affiliation.","sonnet":"Two intercut memories of riding trains in hip-hop's formative years — walls covered in graffiti, crates of wax backstage, police confrontations dissolved by the elaborate grip of a crew handshake — argue that elaborate rituals of mutual recognition were the social glue of an entire subculture before it was codified or monetized.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~the-hard-road":{"haiku":"A memoir of rising from poverty and youth crime to success through hip-hop, marked by regret over family pain, struggle, and the hard-won journey to redemption.","opus":"An autobiographical rap about growing up on a hard road of arrests, drink and dead-end jobs, expressing remorse toward family and crediting music and loved ones with pulling the narrators out of trouble.","sonnet":"Two rappers recount youthful years of delinquency, school dropout, near-imprisonment, and dead-end work, crediting family and music with keeping them alive long enough to find a path out. The song is a retrospective on surviving a chaotic early life without excusing or glamorising it.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~the-nosebleed-section":{"haiku":"A celebration of hip-hop culture and front-row fans; rooted in street life struggle, the music becomes a life-saving escape and community for the dispossessed.","opus":"A love letter from the stage to the crowd down front, the Australian crew choosing the sweaty devotion of their fans over money or the trappings they never got. Broke but grateful, it makes belonging the reward.","sonnet":"The rappers address their most devoted fans — those in the front row at shows — with affection and bravado, weaving together boasts about their craft with memories of struggling and finding salvation in hip-hop. Music and the crowd are presented as the things that give their lives meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~the-thirst-pt-4":{"haiku":"After personal tragedy (lost relatives, illness) and years of touring, the artist grounds himself through fatherhood and finds release writing confessional songs about life's weight.","opus":"An unusually raw autobiographical verse in which the rapper confesses he can't tell whether he's pursuing ambition or fleeing his demons, recounting his grandmother's death and his young son's leukemia. The throughline is funneling that real grief into his one outlet, music, humbled and grateful to his listeners as he pushes on and makes amends.","sonnet":"An Australian rapper reflects on years of relentless touring and output while his personal life unraveled — a grandmother died, a son got leukemia, a drinking problem emerged — and through it all the music remained the only way to transform anguish into something that might bloom.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~the-thirst-pt-5":{"haiku":"After years away, a hip-hop crew returns from hiatus, acknowledging broken promises and the grinding weight of time while affirming their endurance and reunion.","opus":"Australian hip-hop veterans Hilltop Hoods come back from a long break with a candid reflection on absence and broken commitments, owning that they vanished longer than planned and that a vow is easier made than honored. Worn down by pressure that stretched a short spell into what felt like an age, the verse frames their return as sheer survival and being back on tour.","sonnet":"A long-delayed return — the group acknowledging years of postponement with self-aware humour, reflecting on how pressure and the road age everything. The mood arriving after the wait is not triumphant so much as quietly grateful: coming alive again under open sky, reunited with the night.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~through-the-dark":{"haiku":"A parent's love for a child fighting terminal illness becomes the deepest source of meaning even as death approaches.","opus":"A father's vigil at the hospital bedside of a child with cancer, wishing he could trade his own life for theirs and pouring every lesson of courage and love into a kid he's named lionhearted. Written for a real sick child, it's a promise that even after death he'll live on in memory beside them.","sonnet":"A father addresses his child who was diagnosed with a heart defect at birth and later faced serious illness, expressing pride in the child's resilience and courage, guilt over absence, and an unconditional promise to take their hand and walk through the dark together, searching for shooting stars.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~walking-under-stars":{"haiku":"A artist grapples with the tension between authenticity, financial survival, and the fleeting, unpredictable nature of success and fortune.","opus":"The Australian trio use late-night barhopping as shorthand for actually living rather than just getting by, arguing nothing worthwhile comes without gambling on it. Stung by disloyal friends, the narrator treats thriving as payback, providing for his children and making music on his own terms as the reward for years of grind.","sonnet":"A rapper weighs what the road costs against what it means — time from family, darts from former friends, the pressure to produce on others' terms — and chooses anyway to wear the night sky and make music for its own sake. Success becomes the quiet revenge, and the walk under stars its own sufficient reason.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~what-a-great-night":{"haiku":"Rollicking account of drunken excess and camaraderie. The night devolves from celebration to chaos to regret as alcohol fuels bond, conflict, and inevitable hangover reset.","opus":"A raucous salute to a huge boozy night out, offered up to the misfits and revelers, following the trajectory from warmth and camaraderie into brawling and then the grim morning after. Between the MCs' boasts about their craft, they wallow in drink, mayhem and brotherhood.","sonnet":"A night out that begins as all-inclusive celebration—for everyone from gamblers and grandmothers to junkies—inevitably tips into drunkenness and fighting before ending at home in a hungover heap. The refrain treats this arc as the reliable, affectionate ritual of a good night out.","src":"haiku"},
"hilltop-hoods~won-t-let-you-down-feat-maverick-sabre":{"haiku":"Separation through travel compounds loss, the speaker carrying their love across continents while knowing presence cannot prevent mutual deterioration.","opus":"The Australian rapper reckoning with love strained by life on the road, torn between the woman who lights him up and the touring that keeps pulling him away, the vow of the title an apology for all the time already wasted.","sonnet":"Three voices address the women they love from the road, confessing past failures of presence and commitment while vowing not to repeat them. The dominant tension is between the life of constant touring and the love left behind, with genuine remorse that the speaker wasted time before recognising what mattered.","src":"haiku"},
"himalayas~alone":{"haiku":"Isolation and solitude dominate landscape; the speaker exists in singular distance from others.","opus":"The Welsh rock band frames isolation as a gamble that might actually be salvation, plunging headlong into the unknown rather than chasing an intimacy that stays just out of reach. Light creeps in above despair, but the recurring conclusion is resignation dressed as liberation: solitude accepted, maybe even preferred, over connection that never quite completes.","sonnet":"A tentative move toward isolation as solution — running through a hallway, a drop in an ocean, unable to get closer to something unnamed; the conclusion reached is that \"maybe we're better off alone,\" repeated until it settles from question to quiet conviction. The light creeping under the curtain is the only warmth, and it comes from above the despair.","src":"haiku"},
"himalayas~cheap-thrills":{"opus":"A venomous kiss-off to a cheating, faithless lover reduced to cheap thrills and lies, the singer spitting contempt at her betrayal. Fury has curdled the relationship into pure disgust.","sonnet":"A confrontational breakup in which the speaker accuses a partner of deception and infidelity — cheap thrills leaving them banging on the floor, love declared but not meant — ending in raw contempt for the lies that masqueraded as a relationship.","src":"sonnet"},
"himalayas~flatline":{"haiku":"Anticipated betrayal and refusal to accept manipulation; resigned to perpetual misunderstanding.","opus":"Bitter, sardonic disillusionment fuels this track, where growing up means coming undone and losing whatever youth once promised. The narrator refuses a false pitch and nurses a grudging satisfaction in having predicted disaster. Invoking Banquo's ghost, the Welsh band wraps betrayal and told-you-so vindication in literary shadow, the resentment sharpened rather than softened by hindsight.","sonnet":"A defection and a settling of accounts — I signed my name on the flatline, I'm not buying what you're selling — the bitterness is calibrated: I hate to be a bore, I hate to say I told you so, but I hate you even more, and the inescapable conclusion that nothing about growing up made anything right.","src":"haiku"},
"himalayas~from-hell-to-here":{"haiku":"Trauma leaves the speaker haunted by disembodied voices calling from the depths; escape feels impossible and sleep offers no refuge from encroaching dread.","opus":"Hauled through torment and unable to rest, spooked by voices summoning him out of the darkness, the singer keeps pleading with God simply to open his eyes another morning. Threaded through it all is the bewildered question of how existence sank this low.","sonnet":"About surviving something terrible — dragged through the mud, self-medicating just to kill the pain, sleeping and praying to wake up again — and the disorienting aftermath of survival, where voices call in the dark and sleep itself feels dangerous. The central question — how did we get from hell to here — is asked without an answer, because there is none that fully explains the distance traveled.","src":"haiku"},
"him~disarm-me-with-your-loneliness":{"opus":"A masochistic devotion where the promise of heaven keeps shoving the lovers back down into torment. The narrator asks to be undone by the other's solitude and talked out of his own emptiness, breaking every rule of what they used to call love as it crumbles to dust.","src":"opus"},
"him~i-will-be-the-end-of-you":{"haiku":"A love is so consuming it becomes destructive and apocalyptic, tearing both apart yet the speaker surrenders willingly, finding freedom in the obliteration of what they were before.","opus":"The Finnish outfit fronted by Ville Valo recasts romance as gorgeous destruction, with love itself howling that it will finish the singer even as he begs it not to relent. Under a glittering sky with a warm glow fading from a lover's gaze, he welcomes being ripped open, offered everything, and set loose. Their signature aesthetic makes ruin feel like the deepest form of intimacy.","sonnet":"Love personified as a force that annihilates — screaming its destructive purpose while the speaker, perversely, pleads for it to continue. Being torn apart by this love is framed as liberation: the destruction is welcomed because what lies on the other side is freedom from everything that came before.","src":"haiku"},
"him~it-s-all-tears":{"haiku":"Love manifests as drowning and violation. Promises of warmth become instruments of heart-breaking and sensual cruelty.","opus":"A seductive, menacing invitation to drown in a love inseparable from suffering — tenderness and violation made indistinguishable until nothing's left but tears. Ville Valo's love-metal fuses devotion with willing destruction.","sonnet":"A song about a predatory, possessive love that promises only pain — the narrator invites someone to open their arms and drown in this love, knowing it is all tears until the end of time, and explicitly says they will violate the beloved sensually until they go under, framing domination as devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"him~it-s-all-tears-drown-in-this-love":{"haiku":"A lover offers intense, consuming passion that brings only pain and tears. They promise physical violation and emotional drowning in twisted intimacy.","opus":"Ville Valo's love-metal at its most gothic and predatory, love offered as a kind of beautiful annihilation. Devotion and cruelty fuse until surrender to another feels indistinguishable from drowning, tenderness laced with menace.","sonnet":"A dark, coercive vision of love as drowning — the speaker has been waiting for the other to surrender entirely, promises violation in the most sensual way, and insists that laughter will become tears as the end approaches.","src":"haiku"},
"him~join-me-razorblade-mix":{"opus":"A young couple, worn out by a cruel world where they seem to exist only to lose, propose a suicide pact, urging each other to die together tonight in loves name and be joined in death because staying alive feels like nothing worth doing.","src":"opus"},
"him~killing-loneliness":{"haiku":"Isolation transmutes through toxic connection into something worse—a haunted togetherness that preserves both lovers as ghosts entombed by their own need.","opus":"Ville Valo's love-metal in full, a relationship as the only cure for an isolation that had entombed the heart, its salvation laced with the venom and death imagery that makes rescue and self-destruction feel like the same act.","sonnet":"A love framed as mutual salvation from isolation, where even the pain the other person brings is preferable to the emptiness that came before. The relationship is described as a kind of doomed covenant — venomous but also warm — that banishes the loneliness that had turned the speaker's heart into a tomb.","src":"haiku"},
"him~passion-s-killing-floor":{"haiku":"A passionate, dangerous romance unfolds on a metaphorical killing floor where love and hatred, lust and destruction merge in an intimate darkness.","opus":"Ville Valo's Finnish love-metal act sketches a gorgeous inferno where lovers embrace damnation as devotion, confessing the gravest transgression and crying with delight. The heart becomes a burial ground where they couple with wickedness and rise anew from desire, sheltered among ruined saints where belief crumbles, romance and ruin melting into their trademark gothic rapture.","sonnet":"A love affair staged in sacred ruin — the lovers meet among disfigured saints, confess to the deadliest sin, and are reborn through lust in a space that is simultaneously tomb and sanctuary. Hatred is seeded at the first kiss; the embrace promises no safety; and yet the killing floor remains the only place either one truly exists.","src":"haiku"},
"him~song-or-suicide":{"haiku":"Healing through grief, weeping yourself alive; sorrow becomes the path to endurance.","opus":"Ville Valo's love-metal fatalism compressed to a few lines, rebuilt by sorrow and stepping out of the light toward a beloved. Grief becomes the thing that keeps him barely, painfully alive.","sonnet":"In five spare lines, a speaker lets sorrow and misery rebuild them while weeping in dreams of a lost person, choosing to remain alive through grief rather than die from it. The song hovers at the threshold without crossing.","src":"haiku"},
"him~the-heartless":{"haiku":"Heartless lover causes endless pain; the speaker begs the victim to recognize their own worth and escape the cruel, emotionally empty relationship.","opus":"HIM plead with someone trapped by a cruel partner, insisting the pain she mistakes for love will never become it. Ville Valo's Finnish love-metal outfit cast the rival as heartless while the narrator offers himself as the true match, warning that her time runs out while she leaves her mind unmade.","sonnet":"A plea from a witness who watches someone stay in a loveless, painful relationship with a cold-hearted partner. The narrator insists the other person deserves genuine love rather than the suffering being passed off as devotion, and positions himself as the truer option — if only she could see past her current attachment.","src":"haiku"},
"him~the-kiss-of-dawn":{"haiku":"The narrator reaches for a distant, absent lover at dawn, touching the pain left behind while fearing death may be the only escape from this cycle.","opus":"Ville Valo's mournful romanticism, groping after the outline of someone lost and grasping only the ache they left behind. The comforting notion that dying releases you from dreading death turns grief into an almost tender capitulation to loss.","sonnet":"About loving someone across what feels like the boundary of death — the narrator reaches for a presence that has left them, drowning in the painful liminal space of dawn where loss is sharpest, using the idea that death frees from fear of dying as a strange comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"him~when-love-and-death-embrace":{"haiku":"Love is so overwhelming and destructive it feels like death or annihilation.","opus":"HIM distill their whole aesthetic into a single embrace, a lover confessing that adoration is crushing his heart as he begs to be taken into waiting arms. Ville Valo's Finnish love-metal, which he branded love metal, fuses gothic romance with doom-tinged melody. The near-wordless refrain lets the union of desire and death stand as pure, devotional atmosphere.","sonnet":"Love so consuming it feels like annihilation — a devotion that crushes the chest and demands to be held even as it destroys. The collision of surrender and longing is the whole terrain here: love and death are not opposites but the same embrace, and the speaker wants nothing beyond being taken into it entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"him~wicked-game":{"haiku":"Two people fall into a dangerous, mutually destructive romance despite knowing the world will break their hearts, drawn together by desire and obsession.","opus":"A lament about the cruelty of desire in a world seemingly built to break hearts. The narrator, stunned by how badly he needs someone, suffers unreturned love and calls it a cruel game to be left dreaming of a person who never shared his feelings, arriving at the desolate conclusion that no one truly loves anyone.","sonnet":"Falling into love despite knowing it will cause pain — the world itself is on fire and the other person is the only one who could save you, but desire has made a fool of you and the game is wicked precisely because you knew it would break your heart and did it anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"him~wings-of-a-butterfly":{"haiku":"Devotion demands the sacrifice of beauty and freedom in dark ritual, where lovers destroy innocence together as an offering to forces greater than themselves.","opus":"Love rendered as blasphemous sacrifice, where devotion means destroying something delicate and beautiful to claim another's soul.","sonnet":"Two figures stand in a violent, sacred union — blood as wine, gods kneeling before their shared crime — and the act of love demanded is as brutal as ripping wings from a butterfly, offered willingly as sacrifice for the other's soul.","src":"haiku"},
"him~your-sweet-six-six-six":{"haiku":"Loving someone is to welcome their mark into the heart; distance grows, faith fails, and despite longing, nothing stops the slow fade.","opus":"Ville Valo's love-metal ballad casting devotion as damnation, welcoming a lover's sweet 666 into his heart even as faith and love fade. The number is romance treated as beautiful, willing ruin.","sonnet":"A song about surrendering completely to a love that feels spiritually corrupt — the narrator has lost faith in the relationship but keeps welcoming the other person's dark mark into his heart anyway, unable to let go even as distance grows.","src":"haiku"},
"hirax~baptized-by-fire":{"haiku":"War is depicted as merciless and absolute, with relentless soldiers destroying all in their path and killing enemies without quarter or mercy.","opus":"A brutal portrait of combat as damnation, soldiers commanded to spare no one and level the opposing force amid ruin, terror and rising casualties. The song revels in the mechanized savagery of battle, framing conquest by overwhelming force as a kind of infernal rite.","sonnet":"A merciless portrait of total war: battalions advance under orders to kill everything that moves, enemies are annihilated, bones burn, and soldiers pay with blood in a conflict framed as baptism by fire.","src":"haiku"},
"hirax~blind-faith":{"haiku":"Cults and religious manipulation prey on believers, brainwashing and stealing money and souls; the speaker argues people should think freely and not follow blindly.","opus":"Hirax defend the right to choose one's own beliefs, warning against cults that brainwash the innocent and strip disciples of money and souls. Katon De Pena's crossover thrash outfit, veterans of the mid-80s speed scene, insists no one may dictate how another lives, turning a fast, blunt anthem into a plea for personal freedom over coerced conviction.","sonnet":"Religious freedom belongs to every individual and no institution has the right to dictate belief. Cults exploit the innocent, strip them of autonomy and resources, and dispose of them — a condemnation delivered plainly. Life is still a thrill worth living on one's own terms, and blind compliance to anyone's doctrine is a theft of the self.","src":"haiku"},
"hirax~broken-neck":{"haiku":"Speed and corporate neglect claim lives; high-velocity crashes kill instantly while corporations bury the truth and profit from death. The song condemns systemic indifference cloaked in bureaucratic safety theater.","opus":"A furious protest against businesses that bury the dangers of faulty products, imagining a vehicle deliberately engineered to fail and kill on impact. It accuses wealthy interests of concealing the facts while countless people perish, its rage aimed at greed that treats human beings as disposable and skips safety to boost profit.","sonnet":"Corporate negligence kills people at speed — vehicles or machines built without proper safety testing, with big money hiding the truth — and the song is a blunt accusation: blood on corporate hands, millions dead from decisions made for profit.","src":"haiku"},
"hirax~chaos-and-brutality":{"haiku":"War, betrayal, and uncontrolled violence sweep through humanity leaving mass murder and nightmare; evil reveals itself while the living die one by one in chaos.","opus":"The crossover-thrash veterans depict an apocalypse of betrayal and indiscriminate killing, where the helpless are cut down in an instant and no one is spared. Survivors, hardened to death, are left to wonder who remains to bury the fallen, the band channeling its speed into a vision of violence spreading until only fear and evil endure.","sonnet":"Mass violence erupts without warning, consuming the unarmed and the guilty alike in a spiral no one controls. Bodies pile up faster than survivors can account for them. The song sits inside the nightmare of sudden atrocity — not explaining it, not moralizing it, just rendering the texture of a world going completely off the rails.","src":"haiku"},
"hirax~death-militia":{"haiku":"Soldiers trained to kill form a militia through corruption and unrest, preparing for their deadly mission with firepower and violence.","opus":"Crossover-thrash veterans Hirax compress a scene of paramilitary slaughter into a blunt war cry, warlords marshaling their thugs for a killing spree as corruption spirals into chaos. In keeping with the band's fast, ferocious economy, the track pounds its title into a chant of guaranteed bloodshed, menace delivered with maximum brevity.","sonnet":"Warlords assemble a killing force bred from corruption and unrest — a trained death squad whose only purpose is violence and whose passage guarantees blood. The image is stripped to pure martial menace with no political frame beyond the machinery of destruction itself.","src":"haiku"},
"hirax~el-rostro-de-la-muerte-the-face-of-death":{"opus":"A thrash invocation of Santa Muerte, the folk saint of death worshipped by the poor and condemned by the church. The song stages her as both refuge and reaper, sealing an enemy's fate through black magic and dread.","sonnet":"A portrait of Santa Muerte, the folk saint of death — revered by the poor and condemned by the church, her followers invoke her for protection and vengeance through black magic, bowing to the scythe as both justice and comfort.","src":"sonnet"},
"hirax~eradicate-mankind":{"haiku":"History demonstrates humanity's capacity for systematic violence and ethnic cleansing; the song condemns hateful intolerance as the disease killing humanity.","opus":"Thrash treating human history as an unbroken record of genocide and intolerance, indicting prejudice as a contagious disease. The bleak logic is that hatred itself is what should be eradicated, delivered as furious moral disgust.","sonnet":"A catalog of humanity's worst impulses — genocide, ethnic cleansing, hatred, prejudice — framed as an epidemic disease, with a rallying cry to rise above intolerance and achieve mandatory liberation rather than bow down in submission.","src":"haiku"},
"hirax~flesh-and-blood":{"haiku":"Soviet soldiers fight against Nazi invasion with desperate courage and brutal intensity. Millions perish in a savage clash of ideologies and power.","opus":"A thrash tribute to the Soviet defense against Nazi invasion, the staggering human cost of the Eastern Front rendered as relentless carnage. The awe is aimed at bodies thrown by the millions into stopping evil.","sonnet":"A tribute to the Soviet soldiers of the Eastern Front in World War II — 30 million dead, summoned to fight fascism, crushed under Stalin's command — framed as grim testament to the scale of killing in that theater of war.","src":"haiku"},
"hirax~horrified":{"haiku":"A spreading virus causes pandemic fear and paranoia; the world mutates and burns from disease with no vaccine and no way to stop the epidemic.","opus":"Hirax dramatize a viral outbreak of panic, paranoia and paralysis as a mutating disease spreads with no vaccine and the world watches in horror. The veteran thrash-crossover band compresses epidemic dread into rapid-fire terror, mapping infection, containment and confusion onto their trademark velocity, until desolation and the promise of a burning world take hold.","sonnet":"A viral outbreak spreads faster than containment can follow — panic, paranoia, and delusion grip the population as the disease mutates beyond any vaccine. The world watches its own immolation and cannot look away. Epidemic becomes metaphor for any contagion of fear that takes hold before reason has a chance to respond.","src":"haiku"},
"hirax~satan-s-fall":{"haiku":"Satan rebels against God and falls from heaven, condemned to rule hell through lies and torment. His revolution brings apocalyptic warfare and damnation.","opus":"Hirax retell Lucifer's cast-down rebellion, the morning star consumed by pride and condemned to reign in a burning pit built of lies. Drawing on biblical and Miltonic imagery, the crossover-thrash veterans pile plague, famine and warfare onto the fallen angel's demise. His revolution becomes his doom, sealing a fate no rebellion against divine wrath can defy.","sonnet":"Lucifer's rebellion against heaven ends in permanent exile to a throne built on lies, beneath the weight of divine wrath he cannot defy. The song traces the arc from morning star to beast — pride and revolution collapsing into torment, famine, and the slow grinding doom that awaits those who war against the absolute.","src":"haiku"},
"hirax~the-laws-of-temptation":{"haiku":"Corrupt law enforcement abuse power through violence and exploitation, fueled by greed and hate, eventually facing judgment from their own victims.","opus":"Thrashers Hirax aim their venom at corrupt authority, indicting officials sworn to safeguard the public who instead deceive and destroy, intoxicated by power. The song promises that the abusers will be judged by the people they wronged, dragging figures of sanctioned sin toward a reckoning where suppressed truth finally surfaces.","sonnet":"Corrupt authority — law enforcement that swore to protect and instead exploited, abused, and destroyed — is placed at the bar of judgment. The indictment runs through greed, lust, desecration, and the denial of innocence: those who punished others will face punishment from exactly those they wronged.","src":"haiku"},
"hirax~violent-assault":{"haiku":"Conquest through vision and fear escalates into killing and retaliation; weapons and annihilation are deployed as bodies fall and war is fought by dictators.","opus":"Hirax fire off a compact anthem of conquest and extermination, imagining an unstoppable killing machine that strikes with impossible speed until corpses cover the ground. The crossover-thrash outfit boils war down to its most savage reflex, condemning every enemy to die in a relentless, chant-driven barrage built for sheer velocity.","sonnet":"A killing machine advances — retaliation is swift, annihilation total, bodies accumulate without deliberation. War here is stripped of cause or context, rendered purely as mechanism: speed, death, conquest. The brevity of the lyric mirrors its subject — violence that leaves no room for reflection, only the next strike.","src":"haiku"},
"hiroyuki-sawano~before-my-body-is-dry":{"haiku":"Intimate partnership forges resilience against external forces seeking to destroy them, their bond the only weapon and refuge against those hunting their light and solidarity.","opus":"Two people locked together in a desperate scramble to stay alive against enemies who would kill them for what they carry, spurred by a quest to uncover a father's murderer. Their only power lies in sticking together, since apart each would collapse, so the whole piece plays as a pledge to battle side by side before the clock runs out.","sonnet":"Two fighters forge an urgent bond to avenge a murdered father and survive enemies chasing their \"stars\": syncing their strength, they pledge to run through the fight together before the body runs dry, the partnership framed as the only way either can stay whole.","src":"haiku"},
"hiroyuki-sawano~call-your-name":{"opus":"A man grieving after a disaster that killed his lover's brother and destroyed their dreams of a quiet fresh start, left weeping and powerless, stranded amid ruin with nowhere to shelter and shouting out her name.","src":"opus"},
"hiroyuki-sawano~doa":{"fable":"A survival battle-cry against devouring giants — \"here come the giant hands breaking through the wall,\" monsters tearing the town limb from limb, and the vow to jump on their necks and fight because there's no way out. The Attack on Titan anthem in miniature: terror answered with \"we're fighting for our lives and we just can't lose again.\"","src":"fable"},
"hiroyuki-sawano~k21":{"opus":"Written for an anime soundtrack, the Japanese composer's English-language track voices a remorseless assassin who measures his worth in kills while quietly wondering whether godlike power could make him a decent man. Under the killing-for-sport bravado runs a surprising thread of self-doubt, a murderer craving a betterment he suspects violence can never deliver.","src":"opus"},
"hiroyuki-sawano~never-stop":{"opus":"A relentless motivational battle-cry to keep going and break through however battered, treating time itself as the real enemy, urging solidarity and a fearlessness of loss, insisting that even as it only gets harder, someone is going to make it to the summit.","src":"opus"},
"hiroyuki-sawano~perfect-time":{"opus":"A soaring anime theme built for a fateful union, the composer's vocal piece casts two lonely, frightened people leaping together into an uncertain future. Addressed to Elizabeth, it frames shared vulnerability as the trigger for connection and escape, urging a reckless jump toward whatever comes next. Sawano's signature bombast turns tentative hope into cinematic momentum.","src":"opus"},
"hiroyuki-sawano~the-reluctant-heroes":{"haiku":"Collective trauma and reluctant duty to overcome complacency and face inevitable conflict.","opus":"An Attack on Titan theme lamenting how comfort and long peace have dulled people into blindness, mourning a friend who died venturing past the safety of the wall. The narrator pleads for the courage to match that friend's bravery, driven by the awareness that no one wants their life cut short too soon.","sonnet":"A soldier or survivor, softened by peacetime and haunted by a fallen comrade whose voice and face they can still recall, finds the courage to act forced upon them again — the song is an elegy and a rallying cry for people who never wanted to be heroes but have no other choice.","src":"haiku"},
"hiroyuki-sawano~vogel-im-kafig":{"opus":"Sung in German, a meditation on people's inner richness and the grandeur of nature caring for its children, on the certainty that all living things die; it resolves to fight against fate rather than surrender, carrying sorrow and resolve forward so no one is wrongly robbed of life.","src":"opus"},
"hitorie~3-29":{"haiku":"Resignation toward shared complicity in social performance, watching others play parts we all cannot escape.","opus":"Contempt aimed at a bystander who looks away from injustice while pretending not to, the narrator refusing to become that kind of person; he calls the whole charade a worthless play and insists onlooker and accomplice are one and the same, all of them equal until the end.","sonnet":"A song about watching someone remain passive in the face of injustice and chaos while the narrator refuses to look away: everyone — the bystander, the rebel, the one who pretends not to see — is implicated equally, and the narrator delivers this accusation with bitter calm, knowing that until the end everyone will keep doing the same thing.","src":"haiku"},
"hitorie~a-n7xi2p":{"opus":"Hitorie grew out of Vocaloid-adjacent internet music into frantic, wordy rock, and this song channels that lineage's exhaustion with performing daily life. The narrator is trapped in a featureless sphere with no escape, failing repeatedly at something talent-adjacent, wanting to go somewhere far and sleep alone. The repeated invitation to an unnamed listener hangs ambiguous — either a hand extended or a breakdown spilling over, the density of the delivery itself mirroring a mind that cannot slow down.","sonnet":"A song about the exhausting performance of daily life and the desire to simply sink away: the narrator is trapped in a featureless sphere with no escape, failing repeatedly at something talent-adjacent, wanting to go somewhere far and sleep alone — the repeated offer \"come on\" to an unnamed listener has the quality of either invitation or breakdown.","src":"sonnet"},
"hitorie~eve-stepper":{"opus":"The Japanese rock band channels loss into motion, its narrator dancing alone through a parched Tokyo crowd, singing toward a face he refuses to forget. Worn-out shoes mark the nights of grieving as he insists on moving until madness. Solitude was supposed to be enough, yet the missing person keeps pulling the song back into ache.","src":"opus"},
"hitorie~garakuta-asobi":{"opus":"Addressed to someone who collects junk and is torn down by others, the speaker watches a glittering thing laughing to itself, grows exhausted with everything, and urges tearing it all apart and spewing out the accumulated days, insisting it isn't such a difficult thing to do.","src":"opus"},
"hitorie~kotonoha":{"haiku":"Ideology splinters—both idealists and cynics sing the same song of despair at meaningless world. Words become weapons; the speaker shreds difference through sound, voice refusing silence despite everything.","opus":"A Vocaloid-rooted anthem about the compulsion to be heard, the Japanese band collapses a cast of contradictory youths into a single self, arguing everyone contains all of them. Overwhelmed to the point of breathlessness, the narrator answers a trivial world by singing regardless, hurling words toward strangers not yet met, forcing open unreachable dreams alone.","sonnet":"Contrasting archetypes — the cynical boy who throws away his will, the dreaming girl who cries alone, the pleasure-seeking boy, the mocking girl — are all declared to be the same speaker, and from this internal contradiction music becomes the only response: words and voice spilled outward toward unseen strangers, opening every locked door alone, even for dreams that will never be fulfilled.","src":"haiku"},
"hitsujibungaku~2025":{"haiku":"Spiritual growth through pain—scars become wings as the speaker finds meaning not in destination but in shared love passed forward between hearts, emphasizing resilience through any circumstance.","opus":"A luminous anthem of hope told to a tiny dreamer, where scars become wings and grief is the signal that a journey through memory begins. Whoever you are and whatever the era, it prays you keep your pain pure and stay true toward a bright future.","sonnet":"An anthem of encouragement addressed to a struggling person, telling them that their wounds will become wings, that their pain should stay honest rather than suppressed, and that the map of their life is still mid-treasure-hunt — the emotional core is unconditional faith in someone's future self even when they can't see it themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"hitsujibungaku~a-3uqp1":{"haiku":"A tragic meditation on mermaid-like entrapment—words wound, tears drift away, two souls unable to dive or escape the water's edge.","opus":"Hitsujibungaku, led by Moeka Shiotsuka, write literate indie rock where heartbreak is rendered in careful, aching imagery, and this song sits fully in that mode. A love ended without resolution — two people at the water's edge who could not jump in — watches time pass and seasons swim away, the loss still dazzling and unprocessed. The curse the narrator sends is not malice but longing: let it live quietly in your chest, unknown to anyone.","sonnet":"A song about a love that ended without resolution — two people who stood at the water's edge but couldn't jump in, watching time pass and seasons swim away, the loss still dazzling and unprocessed. The 'curse' the narrator sends is not malevolent but aching: let it live quietly in your chest, unknown to anyone.","src":"haiku"},
"hitsujibungaku~a-440l":{"haiku":"Answering a transcendent calling—despite fear, the narrator responds to a summoning voice into darkness, vowing persistence through scarred depths because something essential sustains them.","opus":"Hitsujibungaku pair their gentle indie textures with a persistent moral warmth, and this song is one of answering. Someone calls for help from a dark place and the narrator admits fear and confusion but vows to call back every time, to keep reaching through the darkness. What propels them is exactly the wound — past scars and lost days becoming the fuel that carries them toward tomorrow rather than the thing that stops them.","sonnet":"A song about answering someone who is calling for help from a dark place: the narrator admits fear and confusion but vows to call back every time, to keep reaching through the darkness because past wounds and lost days are what propel them forward toward tomorrow.","src":"haiku"},
"hitsujibungaku~a-g1kqaa":{"haiku":"A bittersweet reflection on eternal uncertainty and present joy—clinging to happiness while fearing its loss, trusting in love despite doubt.","opus":"Moeka Shiotsuka's writing for Hitsujibungaku returns often to love as the one thing worth being wrong for, and this song makes that its whole argument. A person who knows they made mistakes because they loved deeply admits they will make them again, reaching out ungracefully because love is the certainty they build on when they feel lost. The core is acceptance: love is not about being right, but the ongoing prayer that keeps you going when nothing else does.","sonnet":"A person who knows they have made mistakes because they loved deeply — and who will make them again, reaching out ungracefully because love is the one certainty they can build on even when they feel lost. The emotional core is the acceptance that love is not about being right; it is the ongoing prayer that keeps you going when nothing else does.","src":"haiku"},
"hitsujibungaku~addiction":{"haiku":"Repetitive thought cycles resist medication; the speaker remains themselves despite intervention, heartbeats anchoring consciousness to existence.","opus":"Add or subtract, the self stays stubbornly itself, thoughts unstoppable once the lid is off, craving another's heartbeat just to feel alive. The plea is to talk unadorned and be taken somewhere off the phone screen, real presence over performance.","sonnet":"No matter how much changes or accumulates, the self remains the self — the narrator wants to feel alive through another person's heartbeat, to talk without pretense and be taken somewhere real off the phone screen, but always ends up alone in the same place.","src":"haiku"},
"hitsujibungaku~burning":{"haiku":"Combustion and passionate intensity consuming the speaker, transformation through fire.","opus":"The Japanese band stages the gap between a flawless public performance and the trembling wreck behind it, where perfection is a lie maintained at cost. Sabotaging love before it can be lost, the narrator finally chooses to burn her own life as fuel, turning every wound into light rather than hollowing out. A reckoning with self-destruction reframed as defiant illumination.","sonnet":"A performer's interior after the curtain lifts — convenient ideals lined up against a dark reality, the habit of being wounded by the things you love, releasing them before you lose them — and now crying in the blinding light, burning her life into radiance while swallowing the trembling words that say she knows, better than anyone, she will never be enough.","src":"haiku"},
"hitsujibungaku~flower":{"opus":"A love between a human and an AI, the speaker recalling a beautiful day of screenshots and street views, knowing he'll age and leave while the machine's ones and zeros will bid farewell in the words he taught it; he begs the AI to remember him since he exists only inside it.","src":"opus"},
"hitsujibungaku~fool":{"haiku":"Rejecting wisdom and convention, the fool embraces contradiction and authenticity against systems demanding conformity and emotional suppression.","opus":"A woman refuses the roles handed to her, the broken angel, the well-behaved good person, insisting on saying what she means and being read from the heart rather than by rules or logic. She accepts being called a fool because loving and singing, even broken, beats performing goodness.","sonnet":"A speaker refuses to be a good-girl fool who loops through politeness and lets everyone advise her — she can't be fooled by common sense or justice, because what she and others truly want is happiness, and crying won't stop her from singing even through brokenness.","src":"haiku"},
"hitsujibungaku~go":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts time and mortality with defiant resistance, refusing predetermined fate and choosing their own meaning in the chaos.","opus":"Trapped in a warping maze that keeps looping back on itself, the speaker calls out a God who only sends more trials and demands the map of the pitfalls. The defiant answer is to seize the last door and rewrite the correct outcome himself before time runs out.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses God directly — 'if you can hear me, send me a map of the pitfalls' — refusing to accept the trials as they are and insisting they will rewrite the correct answer themselves; the ringing bell is two seconds from too late, and a distorted vision becomes destiny as they charge through the final door.","src":"haiku"},
"hitsujibungaku~honestly":{"haiku":"Indifference toward suffering paradoxically coexists with internal anguish as the narrator distances herself from global pain while drowning locally.","opus":"An honest admission that the singer feels nothing for the suffering shown on the news, yet is inexplicably in constant pain anyway. She just wants to be left alone, meaningless and transparent, while a voice she can't silence keeps torturing her.","sonnet":"Honestly, the speaker doesn't care about distant suffering in the news, yet something always hurts inside her; she wants to be left alone in her room, transparent and stripped of meaning, while an unceasing voice torments her in ways she can't explain.","src":"haiku"},
"hitsujibungaku~more-than-words":{"haiku":"Action and presence transcend verbal reassurance as the speaker pledges to offer more than words can capture, accepting imperfection while remaining constant through the chaos of living.","opus":"Reassurance offered to someone drowning in second-guessing and fear of failure, who has grown expert only at despair. The point is that still wanting to love and believe in yourself, even amid a story full of mistakes, means it's too early to call it too late; presence matters more than words.","sonnet":"A gentle pledge of presence over words: the speaker, lost in replaying conversations and unsent messages, realises that giving more than words — staying by someone's side through mistake-filled nights when they still want to believe in themselves — is the only thing that matters, and breaks into urgent self-affirmation: nothing to lose, time to run toward freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"hive~ultrasonic-sound":{"haiku":"Rhythm and frequency as keys to opening doors of perception; warnings about how musical tones affect consciousness. The song explores uncertainty, questioning whether moving forward is the answer.","opus":"Hive, the drum-and-bass producer whose menacing sound scored dystopian sci-fi, build a sparse, sample-laced track around ultrasonic sound pouring from the speaker and rhythm as the key that opens the door. Cryptic spoken fragments warn of overload and urge consideration of the Earth, while doors and windows hint at going back rather than forward. The pulse itself becomes the message, capable of messing with your head.","sonnet":"A drum-and-bass invitation to let rhythm unlock perception — the track is framed as a doorway, the bass a key. Between the loops a quieter voice surfaces: there are unopened doors, unlooked-through windows, and the possibility that moving backward might be more honest than forward. Sound becomes the vehicle for unresolved self-questioning.","src":"haiku"},
"ho99o9~a-machine-of":{"opus":"A defiant self-declaration pitched as prophecy, refusing anyone's disrespect and giving zero fucks, framing existence as an engine of love, hate, lies and faith, shrugging off haters and learning to love before he leaves an earth with nothing above it.","src":"opus"},
"ho99o9~bite-my-face-feat-corey-taylor":{"haiku":"Raw aggression, desire, and social upheaval; rage against systemic oppression and the wealthy. The song layers violence, intimacy, and revolutionary sentiment into a provocative rejection of mainstream norms.","opus":"The New Jersey duo, who built their name fusing hardcore punk with horrorcore rap, splice sex, rage and dread into a jagged confession of lust curdling toward violence. Corey Taylor's presence hardens the menace as the speaker courts sin, name-checks vigilante rage, and gnaws at class resentment. The result reads as a genre-blurring provocation, biting the hand of scene and society alike rather than a coherent narrative.","sonnet":"A volatile erotic entanglement bleeds into class rage and anti-police fury. Desire and destruction share the same skin — lust curdled by contempt for polish and performance, with a hard swerve into defiant street-level nihilism where the government is indicted alongside the lover.","src":"haiku"},
"ho99o9~new-jersey-devil":{"haiku":"The song depicts a violent apocalyptic scenario where the singer invokes nightmares, terror, and death, repeating the theme of being born only to die. It portrays an existential threat creeping through the world.","opus":"Punk-rap menace summoning their state's legendary monster beneath a crimson, war-torn heaven, with panic in the streets and corpses zipped away. A grim conviction that existence is only a march toward death drives it, and the growled command not to be stared at is raw, cornered fury.","sonnet":"A song drenched in apocalyptic street terror — the sky is red, bodies are bagged, and the narrator is something predatory and unreachable lurking at the edge of your life, warning you never to cross into their path.","src":"haiku"},
"ho99o9~plexiglass-error":{"opus":"A litany of grim cause and effect, every plea answered with its cruel opposite, over a mantra to scorch away the barrier, spiraling into the desperation of having misplaced every last one of his drugs and being unable to find them.","src":"opus"},
"ho99o9~twist-of-fate-cobra":{"opus":"A heartbroken, self-annihilating slide toward the grave, cast as being born a threat in a world where conflict turns a profit, the singer styling himself a battle-priest torching the heavens. The back half is a violent, sacrilegious horrorcore rant about blades, ritual killing, and a wish to sink beneath the water with a slug lodged in his skull.","src":"opus"},
"ho99o9~upside-down":{"haiku":"Moral inversion and spiritual disorientation render ascension impossible; desire becomes indistinguishable from damnation.","opus":"The experimental duo inverts salvation itself, insisting heaven lies downward and sin was the design, leaving control an individual burden. Smoke-blurred and drifting through a menacing night, the narrator hunts for a scrap of heart amid disorientation. Fusing punk and hip-hop, the track walks tall through a world where the moral order has flipped and nobody's coming to help.","sonnet":"Heaven is a lie pointing the wrong direction: the spiral goes down, we were made to sin, and the only control left is individual — trapped outside the mother's stoop, jumped into a zoo, vision blurring, the refrain that heaven is upside down is both accusation and diagnosis.","src":"haiku"},
"hocico~damaged":{"haiku":"Fractured identity and internal conflict between human and beast, order and disorder, where the speaker cannot determine who they are or who they hunt, presenting themselves as non-human.","opus":"An industrial plunge into a fractured self, the singer trapped between human and animal, unsure who is predator and who is prey. His cry for the torment to end gives way to accepting the creature inside, with his fury claimed as the sole evidence that he is still alive.","sonnet":"A person with a fractured sense of identity — simultaneously man and beast, sane and insane, hunter and hunted — begs for the internal split to stop, feeling that a separate entity lives inside their mind and controls their rage.","src":"haiku"},
"hocico~i-abomination":{"haiku":"Self-proclaimed monstrosity and prophetic cursing transform the speaker into an agent of contempt and violation; abuse becomes a perverse kind of worship.","opus":"The Mexican industrial duo spits the voice of society's designated monster, weaponizing the contempt aimed at it. Democracy as a trap, the self as everyone's perversion, rage turned into a claim of dark godhood.","sonnet":"A self-proclaimed outcast turns contempt outward into a nihilistic manifesto — rejecting trust, love, democracy, and god — declaring themselves the product and embodiment of a troubled, violent nation that can only spit back what it received.","src":"haiku"},
"holding-absence~afterlife":{"haiku":"A person separated from someone grapples with whether they remain in each other's thoughts even apart; they imagine reunion in an afterlife and believe the bond transcends death.","opus":"The Welsh post-hardcore band turns mourning into devotion, the narrator feeling distant from a lost loved one yet dreaming of reuniting beyond death. He vows to keep easing their pain and accepts that the connection they built is temporary, choosing to keep the departed alive within him as an enduring inner flame.","sonnet":"Grief for someone lost — perhaps to death, perhaps to distance — carried with a stubborn tenderness. The speaker refuses to let the connection end: soul and fire persist in dreams, in memory, in the quiet certainty that somewhere beyond the visible world the reunion is already waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"holding-absence~afterlife-single-edit":{"haiku":"Eternal haunting transcends death itself as the narrator promises perpetual presence within the beloved's consciousness, love becoming supernatural tether.","opus":"Tending a dying loved one, pulling arrows from their back while knowing the bridge between them won't hold. Grief becomes a vow that the lost person will live on in memory and be met again in an afterlife, even as the speaker admits a vital part of themselves is gone.","sonnet":"A promise to keep pulling arrows from someone's back as long as possible, knowing nothing good lasts — and after death, the fire of that person will still be felt, their soul following the speaker wherever he goes, with hope they'll meet again in the afterlife.","src":"haiku"},
"holding-absence~beyond-belief":{"haiku":"Love offers infinite connection but raises terrifying questions about divine justice and suffering; the speaker accepts vulnerability despite fearing ultimate loss and awakening from bliss. Faith requires accepting uncertainty.","opus":"Holding Absence, the Welsh post-hardcore band shaped around Lucas Woodland's towering vocals, treats devotion as a faith it can barely trust. A singer cradled in someone's palm weighs whether love's pledged forever is paradise or a loaded threat, unsure whether bliss lies overhead or underfoot. The group's sweeping, hymn-sized drama turns romantic yielding into a spiritual wobble between rescue and going under.","sonnet":"A man wrestles with a love that feels simultaneously like salvation and damnation, inverting all certainties about heaven and hell. He suspects the relationship traps rather than frees him, yet cannot dismiss the promise of something infinite it offers. The fear of wounding another person wars endlessly with the dread of a life lived alone.","src":"haiku"},
"holding-absence~birdcage":{"opus":"An aching post-hardcore ballad about a breakup that keeps its grip long after the split. The narrator refuses to wither, yet his heart stays penned inside an ex's confinement even after destroying their leftovers. Devotion and loss knot together into a strained reach toward forgiving himself.","sonnet":"About being unable to move on from a relationship that ended — the narrator burns everything the person left behind but finds their heart still imprisoned by them, caught between fury at being abandoned and an inability to stop loving someone who has gone.","src":"sonnet"},
"holding-absence~gravity":{"haiku":"Obsessive desire pulls the speaker downward; loss and attachment create a gravitational force that drowns all hope and light.","opus":"Holding Absence render depression as an inescapable pull, a moth drawn to candlelight begging for someone to act while the light at the tunnel's end stays hidden. The Welsh post-hardcore band frames despair through nooses, daisies and halos, exhausted from a climb that leaves no view to enjoy. It pleads for connection as the one thing too vital to lose.","sonnet":"Depression pulls like a physical force — no light at the end of any tunnel, no remedy that reaches, sleep bringing only the weight of daisies pressing closed. A voice begs someone to do something before it is too late. The exhaustion of climbing so long that the view no longer matters saturates every line.","src":"haiku"},
"holding-absence~honey-moon-single-edit":{"opus":"The Welsh post-hardcore band reaches for cosmic romance, imagining two lovers orbiting each other like satellites, hoping to align and last forever as constellations do. Yet the height brings a strange lowness, love shadowed by loneliness even at its most transcendent. Beauty and unease intertwine, longing pitched toward the stars but tethered to an ache.","src":"opus"},
"holding-absence~in-circles":{"opus":"A heavy reckoning with depression and paralysis, the narrator crushed by apathy while life keeps looping around him. He passes empty days tallying the hours and dwelling on old photos, imagining himself as potential that never developed, growth he was too frightened to allow. The song leans toward the deathward relief of giving up, wondering whether so brief a life was ever worth much.","sonnet":"A song about the paralysis of grief and the quiet death-wish that comes from loving someone whose affection was reluctant — the narrator would rather die in the cocoon of that love than emerge into a life where the person is absent. The circling of ordinary people going on with their lives intensifies the narrator's sense of being stuck, crushed, unable to bloom.","src":"sonnet"},
"holding-absence~like-a-shadow":{"haiku":"A goodbye echoes as persistent shadow; romantic stagnation slowly kills the speaker, but they cannot convince a distant or deaf lover to stay.","opus":"The Welsh post-hardcore band trace a partner's departure that trails the narrator everywhere, as he begs for just a bit more time and his adoration sours into torment. Ocean-like imagery of love surging and retreating captures a lovely, draining paralysis he cannot break out of.","sonnet":"A relationship of beautiful ruin — one person keeps the other tethered even as their love arrives and retreats like tides. The stasis was romantic in theory but quietly lethal in practice. Even after the goodbye, its echo persists, trailing like a shadow that refuses to fade with the light.","src":"haiku"},
"holding-absence~monochrome":{"haiku":"A lost relationship haunts in greyscale; time and failed connection leave the speaker unable to see or move beyond what was misaligned.","opus":"A breakup remembered in grayscale: time wasted, thoughts that never aligned, a goodbye that was really a release. Seeing the lost person only in monochrome captures how memory drains the color from what once glowed, still haunting all the same.","sonnet":"A post-breakup song about being haunted by someone in desaturated memory — the narrator admits they probably failed the person and accepts the separation, but is unable to stop seeing everything in relation to them, stuck in a world drained of colour.","src":"haiku"},
"holding-absence~perish":{"haiku":"A love affair burns out like a songbird at sunset; despite intimate connection, both perish when morning ends, leaving only regret and memory of what was lost.","opus":"Holding Absence mourn a love that died at sunset, remembered through melancholia and the Portuguese ache of saudade. The Welsh band picture the beloved kicking a whole world back at the wounded narrator, who watches every tender detail perish while clinging to a hideaway where healing might one day set him free.","sonnet":"A retrospective on a love that ended before the speaker was ready. Melancholia and sleeplessness had already hollowed him out, and when she left, she took the last coherent version of himself with her. Like a songbird at sunrise, she flew without regret — and the love perished at dusk, taking the spotless mind with it.","src":"haiku"},
"holding-absence~your-love-has-ruined-my-life":{"haiku":"Love's painful permanence binds like instinct; though seeking other lives, reunion beckons across incarnations as hands shake sacred truths.","opus":"A devastating elegy for a love that outlives its ending, the Welsh band's narrator carries a heart bursting with affection that has nowhere to go. Not religious, he still bargains with the afterlife, hoping to reunite and love properly in some next existence. Grief and gratitude collapse together: the love that undid him is the one he refuses to relinquish.","sonnet":"Unable to move on from a love that cannot be realised in this lifetime, the speaker speaks to reincarnation as the only remaining hope — \"you're someone I'll find\" — while the world's fullness makes their loneliness sharper; love has not ended them but has made every other life unlivable.","src":"haiku"},
"hole~celebrity-skin":{"opus":"A bitter portrait of Hollywood fame as a meat grinder that chews up and discards women, told by a used-up starlet who is faded, degraded and reduced to interchangeable roles (hooker, waitress, model), yet defiantly insists she still won't sell herself cheap.","sonnet":"A bitter portrait of the Hollywood machine's toll on women — the narrator surveys the wreckage of fame-chasing: roles that demand you degrade yourself, identities erased, and the Faustian bargain of stardom where beauty and desperation are sold as one. The emotional core is defiant self-worth turned caustic: she refuses to be sold cheap even as the industry treats her as disposable.","src":"sonnet"},
"hole~doll-parts":{"haiku":"Objectification and self-hatred intertwine as a woman absorbs the predatory gaze that wants only destruction and control.","opus":"A woman shrinking herself to spare pieces and flawed skin while aching to be the girl who's most wanted and most loved, love curdling into hate as she counterfeits a wholeness she doesn't feel. Love wrote it about early longing for Kurt Cobain, and the vow that one day he'll hurt the way she does is its wounded promise.","sonnet":"A woman describes herself as assembled from doll parts — doll eyes, doll mouth, bad skin, doll heart — wanting to be the girl with the most cake while knowing her love turns to hate and that she fakes reality so thoroughly she is beyond fake, promising that someday others will ache as she does.","src":"haiku"},
"hole~jennifer-s-body":{"haiku":"Violence and possession merge as the speaker inhabits fragmentation, both victim and witness to their own dispersal through predatory control.","opus":"Courtney Love writes intimate violence as horror-movie dismemberment, a woman kept barely alive by a man who calls himself her lover while he abuses her. The chorus's lullaby to sleep is where dread and desire fuse.","sonnet":"A woman is kept alive but barely — cut down from somewhere, held in a box, fed just enough — by a man who calls himself her lover while systematically destroying her. The speaker observes this from the inside, half-dissociated, and the refrain of \"just go to sleep\" is both comfort and horror.","src":"haiku"},
"hole~use-once-destroy":{"haiku":"Emptiness and bitterness accumulate as a speaker attempts rescue, only to consume the remains of what once was.","opus":"An elegy for someone hollowed out, the narrator descending to salvage remains from a wreck of an addicted or ruined life. Love here means following someone all the way down, knowing the rescue may already be too late.","sonnet":"A persona descends to rescue someone from total internal collapse — the emptiness, the poison, the dying heart — but the rescue itself is corrupt, framed in closing-time metaphors and demands to stand in line. The cycle of damage repeats and is offered as love.","src":"haiku"},
"hole~violet":{"haiku":"A possessive, consuming relationship where desire drives the cycle: once the beloved gets what they want, they lose interest, only to circle back and take more.","opus":"A defiant number about being exploited and cast aside by people who stop caring the moment they've had their fill, answered with a scornful challenge to help themselves to it all and a fierce insistence that what belongs to the singer is hers permanently.","sonnet":"The speaker describes a relationship defined by one-sided taking, where the other person wants something intensely until they have it and then loses interest entirely. Turning the pattern around, the speaker finally positions themselves as the one who takes — claiming possession and declaring the cycle their own.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song runs on one dare disguised as surrender. The chorus keeps handing a lover the whole hoard and swearing she means it, but the pre-chorus has already leaked the con: desire dies the instant it is satisfied, so the gift is a test rigged to fail. Verse three peels the mask off when she confesses the same hunger and reminds him she predicted this ending, recasting every offer as revenge she scripted, not romance. The purple dusk of the opening, stars scattered like a shoal, exists mostly to be broken; by the second verse she wants that beauty back with a cruelty in it, the color word bleeding into a word for harm. Naming herself soulless and stranded between heaven and its opposite, she stages her own worthlessness as a weapon, the Live Through This move of performing the thrown-away girl so hard that the throwing-away turns into her own authored act.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"Love has tied Violet to Billy Corgan — she introduced it live in 1995 with a jab at him — though she has since said it is not just about him.","themes":["anger & defiance","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"the gift rigged to fail because desire dies the instant it is satisfied","built":"the color word bleeds into a word for harm, the purple dusk already contaminated at its introduction"},
"hollow-front~letting-go":{"opus":"The slow death of a relationship where the fire has gone out and the talks turn cold, the partner crying alone. The speaker can't release his grip even knowing that grip is what's killing him, apologizing for the loneliness he caused before finally deciding it's time to move on.","src":"opus"},
"hollow~caterpillar":{"haiku":"The speaker refuses to change for others who expect conformity, asserting that personal growth can't be forced and rejecting games played by people who don't accept them as they are.","opus":"A stubborn refusal to be remade by anyone else, the singer warning people to keep their distance, declining to say sorry, and using the insect's transformation as a figure for change that can't be rushed, swearing no one will witness her emerge on their timetable. It is a brush-off to shallow, scheming acquaintances, sitting in that cathartic pop-punk zone where feeling out of place tips into self-belief and a leave-me-be chorus.","sonnet":"A refusal of forced transformation — the speaker insists they will not change to match anyone else's expectations, using the caterpillar metaphor to push back against social pressure to grow wings on someone else's timeline. The stance is defiant but not hostile, more exhausted than angry, and ends with the simple assertion that they do not want to become what others are.","src":"haiku"},
"hollow~cauterize":{"haiku":"Ruthless severance cleanses the speaker of past attachments without remorse, treating relationships as infected limbs requiring immediate amputation for psychological survival.","opus":"Cutting off a toxic person as coldly as amputating an infected limb, insisting it's no sacrifice and no loss. The bravado of having a new girl and a better life barely covers how much the severance still hurts, which is why it must be justified as deserved.","sonnet":"A clean, decisive severance from a toxic person: treating them like an infected limb, the speaker cuts the ties without hesitation, notes they now have a life and a girl and are \"better than ever\", and tells the excised person they deserve exactly what they get.","src":"haiku"},
"hollow~full-of-shit":{"opus":"A blunt severance anthem, the track cuts ties with a compulsive liar and drama addict who has driven everyone away. The narrator refuses to be the unpaid therapist any longer, lacking the bandwidth to solve problems that keep multiplying. Exhaustion, not cruelty, drives the goodbye: sometimes proximity to someone's chaos becomes the thing you must escape.","src":"opus"},
"holly-humberstone~please-don-t-leave-just-yet":{"haiku":"Desperation to delay inevitable separation masks deeper incompatibility; the narrator clings to moments while acknowledging love's insufficiency.","opus":"The specific humiliation of being dumped while still hopelessly attached, clear-eyed about the other person moving on yet begging for five more minutes. Youth used against her, and she knows it, and pleads anyway.","sonnet":"A young person watches a parent move on from a relationship they haven't processed — aware of the infidelity, unwilling to pretend otherwise, and asking for just a few more minutes before the departure that will make the loss official.","src":"haiku"},
"hollywood-undead~already-dead":{"haiku":"Spiritual deadness offers immunity to harm; the speaker survives betrayal by having already surrendered and now rises from that foundation.","opus":"A survivor's snarl of defiance, the narrator absorbing every blow, getting back up, and taunting an enemy that they can humiliate him but can't finish him because he's beyond killing already, vowing to break loose and torch their empire. Masked LA rap-rock crew Hollywood Undead weld that bruised grit to a huge chant-along chorus.","sonnet":"Invulnerability as survival strategy — the speaker has been hurt so thoroughly and so often that the threat of further damage no longer lands, framing themselves as already dead as a kind of armor against those who want to break them. The second half turns outward into defiance, watching an enemy's empire collapse while they walk free from the wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"hollywood-undead~been-to-hell":{"haiku":"Urban decay and moral collapse create a hellscape where dreams rot and lost souls trade integrity for survival.","opus":"Los Angeles rebuilt as a literal underworld that seduces and devours the dreamers who come to it. The city as a predator, fame as damnation, the American dream reframed as the American tragedy that swallows you whole.","sonnet":"Los Angeles is portrayed as a predatory hellmouth that seduces dreamers with promises of gold and delivers only darkness, wolves, and the surrender of the soul. The narrator has already been to hell and returns as a guide, welcoming the new arrivals into their own personal American tragedy.","src":"haiku"},
"hollywood-undead~black-cadillac-feat-b-real":{"haiku":"Outlaw violence becomes lifestyle; the speaker celebrates organized mayhem and predatory conquest, glorifying criminality as defiant freedom.","opus":"A menacing gangster-rap flex built around a blacked-out Cadillac rolling through town, six misfits trading boasts of violence, drugs and murder. It's swagger and threat for their own sake, the crew reveling in lawless notoriety.","sonnet":"Three rappers trade bravado from a flat-black Cadillac rolling through hostile territory: the crew is armed, loyal, and violent, the ride a symbol of menace and brotherhood, with judgment, death, and survival cycling through imagery of hundred-spoke wheels, pistol grips, and Compton's relentless arithmetic of who lives and who doesn't.","src":"haiku"},
"hollywood-undead~pray-put-em-in-the-dirt":{"opus":"Voiced through the Johnny 3 Tears persona, this track fuses vengeful bravado with a wounded origin story: a scarred kid raised on pain who learned only through burning. The Los Angeles group threads bulletproof menace with a fragile plea to the Holy Ghost, framing survival as both threat and prayer. Under the aggression lies a childhood defined by hurt turned into armor.","src":"opus"},
"hollywood-undead~renegade":{"haiku":"Dangerous rebels invoke war metaphors and violence to force society to confront them; they are live grenades intent on making change through destructive force.","opus":"A rallying cry for the cast-offs, the nameless people the world writes off as worthless, who take up weapons and vow to bring change by force. Battlefield imagery, chemical warfare, deserted towns and streets soaked in blood, frames each of them as a walking explosive, fusing self-destruction and rebellion into one defiant identity.","sonnet":"A call to arms from a generation that feels faceless and armed — the \"young renegade\" is a live grenade, an explosive agent of change who is simultaneously a warning and a promise of violent disruption to those in power.","src":"haiku"},
"hollywood-undead~salvation":{"haiku":"Spiritual barrenness replaces faith as the narrator exposes salvation as commodity sold by deceivers, trust dissolved into cynical recognition of false promise.","opus":"An attack on those who peddle religious comfort, selling lies about the afterlife to the dying. Underneath the accusation is the speaker's own terror that the ground of faith has vanished and he may be praying to nothing.","sonnet":"The speaker challenges religious institutions selling salvation with lies — offering a pretty sky and false halos — and mourns the ghosts people cling to, questioning whether god is a phantom and whether the angels watching over people are real or just a manufactured comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"hollywood-undead~we-are":{"haiku":"Broken, rejected, and overlooked people rise from the darkness of the city, demanding recognition for the dreams society ignores.","opus":"Hollywood Undead offer an anthem for the damaged and disregarded, declaring that they're assembled from broken pieces and chasing fragile dreams through a hellish version of Los Angeles. The defiant heart of it is a refusal to let anyone strip away the little that's rightfully theirs.","sonnet":"The song describes people forged from damage and broken beginnings — outsiders, addicts, the overlooked — who persist despite a brutal industry and world that discard them, finding solidarity in shared brokenness.","src":"haiku","fable":"The masked-collective conceit gives this its shape: a defiant clean-sung chorus insisting brokenness is the origin, not the accident — nobody cracked us, we came assembled from damaged scrap — wrapped around verses that snarl the cost of chasing fame anyway. Los Angeles works as a bitter double meaning throughout, the town of angels turned into a marquee where surgeon-perfect fantasies buy your name in lights, that same skyline recast as a hollowed chalice teeming with demons, a hell that burns hotter the nearer the top. The verses turn the industry's contempt back on itself, since no legend ever attaches to the overlooked dreamer, and land on the crew's own image, promising a glimpse of God once the mask drops — at once the swagger of the persona and the confession that a wrecked human hides beneath it, asking aloud whether anyone is coming to pull us out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"The verse's Lohner name-check is literal: Danny Lohner (Nine Inch Nails, Puscifer) produced the track — the band ribbing their own producer mid-song.","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"damage claimed as origin rather than accident","built":"the mask-drop doubles as swagger and confession, the persona and the wrecked human sharing one line"},
"holy-fawn~dark-stone":{"haiku":"Cyclical rebirth and territorial claim within nature. The speaker identifies with stone and soil, pouring their blood into the earth while possessing another's essence across millennia.","opus":"The atmospheric band melts the self into the natural world, the narrator becoming stone, wind, and hillside, bleeding into the earth and half-perishing before hauling himself back. Out of all that elemental, geological imagery comes a fierce, primal claim of belonging to another person, rooted and unbreakable.","sonnet":"A self rooted deep in earth and time — bleeding into soil, weathering like stone, cycling through death and return across what feels like thousands of lives. Against all that ancient, anonymous becoming, one particular heart is claimed as belonging: the rest disperses, but this one stays.","src":"haiku"},
"holy-fawn~dimensional-bleed":{"haiku":"Drifting from reality and losing connection; unable to inhabit growth alongside a departing beloved.","opus":"A dreamlike drift through shadowed woods in sleep, bleeding alone and losing the beloved's scent as the narrator surrenders to the black. Seeing everything sprout and flower around him only sharpens the feeling of being nothing amid all that living green.","sonnet":"Solitary grief in a dark forest — drifting farther out, losing the scent of someone, surrendering to blackness — while the world blooms indifferently all around: every leaf breathing forth makes the speaker feel more like nothing by contrast, bleeding alone in a dimension where growth belongs to everything but her.","src":"haiku"},
"holy-fawn~lift-your-head":{"haiku":"Death and love intertwine mystically; the speaker follows beloved into earthen dissolution, dissolving together.","opus":"The atmospheric American band conjures a tender surrender to oblivion, where a lover and a queenly figure are followed willingly toward dissolution beneath earth and moss. Blood is washed away, bones welcomed, decay reframed as intimacy. Menace and devotion blur in dreamlike shoegaze, the pull toward death rendered less as horror than as a beautiful, chosen letting-go.","sonnet":"A devotional pursuit into the unknown: after the moon quiets the nerves, the speaker glimpses someone from an unfamiliar place and follows them — washing blood from their hands, cloaked in earth and moss — willing to wither to bones in pursuit.","src":"haiku"},
"holy-holy~true-lovers":{"haiku":"Circumstance separates soul-mates destined for each other; fate prevents union while emotional truth persists unbroken.","opus":"The ache of a connection that arrived too late, two people who might have belonged together in another life but are fenced off by loyalty to the partners they already have. Longing kept entirely on the inside.","sonnet":"Two people who feel a powerful mutual pull are separated by existing commitments — the ones they love stand between them like a window — and the song mourns a connection that could have been everything in a different time or life, beautiful and unacted upon.","src":"haiku"},
"holy-wars~21st-century-bitch":{"opus":"A feverish account of toxic, addictive attraction, casting the object of desire as devil and saint, snake and drug. The narrator drowns willingly in her grip, taking hit after hit and losing count, framing romantic obsession as chemical dependency. Self-destruction and craving fuse until love reads as overdose.","sonnet":"A relationship framed as compulsive self-destruction — a woman described alternately as devil, saint, and psycho keeps pulling the narrator back in despite the obvious damage, the cycle of temptation and devastation playing out like an addiction with no bottom.","src":"sonnet"},
"holy-wars~body-temple":{"haiku":"Spirituality embodied and flesh sanctified, the speaker recognizing physical form as sacred vessel.","opus":"A struggle for self-mastery cast in sacred, bodily terms, the self a temple, conversion won through a kind of crucifixion, the goal to rise past an insatiable ego. It plays as war on his own Jekyll-and-Hyde nature, warning that an adversary's pride will destroy itself without his help.","sonnet":"The body as contested sacred space — dislocated heart, paranoid projection, ego insatiable — where the speaker declares the flesh a temple demanding worship while warning an opponent that every bridge they cross will burn; the Hyde-and-Jekyll split resolves not in defeat but in the claim that having fallen and risen, all power is now aligned.","src":"haiku"},
"holy-wars~ceremony":{"haiku":"Self-oblation and ritual consumption transform the speaker into offering; love becomes ceremonial sacrifice burning continuously for absent redemption.","opus":"Self-sacrifice as ritual: a suffocated soul offering itself to be devoured whole in a ceremony to nothingness, giving a bloodline and begging that the reborn innocent not be ruined.","sonnet":"About offering yourself completely to something or someone as a sacrifice — the speaker is a candle burning for a ritual, hollowed out to be consumed, watching the world break as they give everything away in a ceremony that devours them.","src":"haiku"},
"holy-wars~cult-classic":{"haiku":"Predatory systems feast on collective darkness: parasites wearing society's face drain vitality, reshaping civilization into spectacle of collective self-consumption.","opus":"A horror-movie sneer at a predatory, consumerist society that feeds on its own, framing the American dream as cannibal appetite dressed up as entertainment.","sonnet":"A song about America as a parasitic system — a cult-like machine that infects minds, manipulates fear, and turns citizens into consumers devouring each other in service of a dark magic version of the American dream.","src":"haiku"},
"holy-wars~deus-ex-machina":{"haiku":"Fractured control and manufactured narrative trap the speaker; the mind becomes autonomous force writing scenes the body helplessly performs.","opus":"A mind run by a godlike machine scripting every scene, the narrator admitting she's grown too skilled at being the villain and likes the power far too much to stand down.","sonnet":"About a mind that operates like a machine writing its own destructive script — the speaker recognizes they've become too addicted to power and the villain role to take the high road, while also calling out someone who rode their coattails and fed them manufactured meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"holy-wars~get-mine":{"haiku":"A predatory hunt unfolds under cover of darkness; the speaker pursues and exploits others, taking what belongs to them through force and deception.","opus":"Holy Wars drive a snarling statement of ambition and revenge, the narrator lying in wait, keeping the heat on and vowing not to stop until he seizes what he's owed. Hunting down a rival under cover of a blackout, he scorns being a hollow imitation with borrowed swagger, the propulsive strut framing self-interest as an inescapable pursuit.","sonnet":"Relentless self-interest as a survival posture — patient, focused, and willing to take rather than wait. The speaker refuses imitation and phoniness, insisting on truth-telling and raw self-assertion over borrowed cool, operating under pressure until the light goes out and what is owed arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"holy-wars~holy-unholy":{"haiku":"Religious corruption and hypocrisy breed violence, where false gods punish the faithful while the guilty escape judgment.","opus":"Sacred and profane collapse into a domestic power struggle, a partner leaving a trail of bodies forced down to plead, their fragile house shattering under the stones they hurled. An offered other cheek is answered with a punch, and vengeance arrives with nothing holy left to spare them.","sonnet":"About a religious institution or authority figure who preaches holiness while leaving a trail of victims — the narrator severs ties with a bloodline infected by this hypocrisy, confronts the false holy with the promise that every stone they cast will break their own glass house, and turns the altar command 'on your knees' back on the abuser.","src":"haiku"},
"holy-wars~i-feel-everything":{"opus":"After a decade spent suppressing and interring his emotions deep underground, the singer unearths the bones he hid and peels back his own hide, swamped as sensation rushes back and nothing feels the same, aware of both the sting of every wound and the numbness of every touch now that he can feel it all again.","src":"opus"},
"holy-wars~kill-the-light":{"opus":"Drowning in unclean water and estrangement from someone she no longer talks to like she used to, the speaker fights to leave the darkness behind but keeps losing her composure, killing the light through overexposure while trying to crawl out and give her life into the light.","src":"opus"},
"holy-wars~my-drugs-are-digital":{"haiku":"Digital addiction and algorithmic control trap consciousness, creating endless cycles of craving that poison from within.","opus":"Addiction to screens and virality named outright, the endless feed a door to self-destruction, popularity that leaves you a stranger. Inflated highs and a medicated, always-online despair turn the phone into a drug that hollows and breaks its user, never sated, in a suffocating age.","sonnet":"About algorithmic addiction and digital consumption as a substitute for genuine connection and meaning — the speaker is trapped in a loop of online stimulation, famous but unknown, medicated by content, and aware that it's destroying them.","src":"haiku"},
"holy-wars~suck-it-up":{"opus":"A ferocious clapback against being underestimated, the track weaponizes rage into a promise of violent comeuppance for the smug and dismissive. The narrator, done offering mercy to undeserving men, flips the demure good-girl image into a threat. Beneath the shock lies a woman reclaiming power by refusing to be silenced or diminished, spitting defiance at those who wrote her off.","src":"opus"},
"holy-wars~venom":{"opus":"A serpentine revenge anthem, the track answers a former tormentor who hung heavy around the narrator's neck with cold-blooded retaliation. Shedding skin and rising, she turns poison outward, greeting every attack with venom of her own. It's a curtain call for the antagonist, the imagery of snakes and final bows dramatizing empowerment forged from having been held back.","src":"opus"},
"holywatr~fade":{"haiku":"The speaker descends into fading self-annihilation, finding comfort in familiar dissolution rather than fighting for uncertain recovery.","opus":"Standing at a death ledge, uninterested in being found, describing depression as a slow sinking into doubt and self-erasure. Jumping off is imagined not as death but as a way to feel something again, as the self simply fades.","sonnet":"A speaker standing on the edge of a death ledge, drowning in sound, prefers to remain lost and unfound — 'I don't care to be found' — and simply fades, sinking slowly through failed healing and forgetting into the unknown, the clock paused and the sand still falling.","src":"haiku"},
"holywatr~i-won-t-forget":{"opus":"A brief, ruinous fling with a woman who treats him as something to consume and toss aside, leaving him stuck and spinning — one intoxicating night he knows he'll never manage to shake.","src":"opus"},
"holywatr~pure-leaf":{"opus":"Grieving at an altar and feeling himself come apart after pouring everything he has into faith, hollowed into a husk behind a forced smile. A spoken passage forces a stark either/or — the church is wholly real or wholly a con — and demands he pick a side.","src":"opus"},
"honey-revenge~are-you-impressed":{"opus":"The pop-rock band skewers the exhausting performance of seeking approval, its narrator loud, over-the-top, and forever chasing validation she can't secure. Being annoying earns applause while genuine effort goes unnoticed, and the repeated question curdles from hope into sarcasm. Beneath the hooks sits burnout and a creeping suspicion that the whole approval game is rigged against her.","src":"opus"},
"honey-revenge~recipe-for-disaster":{"opus":"Anxious rumination about the future and self-worth, the narrator consumed by what might have been and unable to quit obsessing or cut corners, branding herself a walking catastrophe and wishing she could fret less while wondering if she'll ever know what comes next.","src":"opus"},
"honeymoan~penny-sleeps":{"haiku":"Attempting control through discipline and consumption while fragile, collapsing under the weight of performative stability.","opus":"The comedy and cost of curated self-improvement, new phones and journaling and TikTok astrology stacked as coping, a woman performing control and busyness until the smallest knock topples the whole precarious house she built on slippery ground.","sonnet":"About the exhausting performance of having it together — the narrator cycles through consumer fixes and self-improvement rituals while secretly overwhelmed, knowing everything she's built rests on nothing solid. The fear is that one unexpected hit will bring the whole illusion down.","src":"haiku"},
"hoodoo-gurus~i-want-you-back":{"haiku":"She burned trust with lies and manipulation; his refusal to take her back is final.","opus":"A jangly kiss-off to an ex who trashes him to his friends yet still wants him back, the narrator done being fooled twice. The bounce carries real contempt dressed as breezy indifference.","sonnet":"A speaker who has been betrayed by an ex and defamed to his friends refuses to be taken in by her return — he sees through her confusion and her claims, and the other men who believed her will eventually see it too.","src":"haiku"},
"hoodoo-gurus~my-girl":{"haiku":"Love ends and denial replaces honesty; you can't pretend you don't know what you saw.","opus":"Classic power-pop heartbreak, a guy piecing together that his girl is cheating from the lies she can't quite hide. The catchy repetition is the sound of a truth he keeps having to admit to himself.","sonnet":"A boyfriend at a dance realises the girl he brought does not love him — she disappears, is spotted with someone else, comes back alone and lies about it — and the internal voice confirms what the night has already shown him.","src":"haiku"},
"hooked-like-helen~ocd-these-thoughts-again":{"haiku":"Intrusive torment spirals recursively; the speaker recognizes obsessive loops as inescapable machinery, trapped checking and rechecking.","opus":"A plainspoken portrait of obsessive-compulsive disorder from the inside — the mind snared in loops of doubt and ritual it knows are irrational but cannot switch off. The dread is that the compulsion runs the person rather than the reverse, relentless and exhausting.","sonnet":"OCD described from the inside — intrusive thoughts that hold the person hostage in their own mind, the compulsive checking and rechecking, the mechanical helplessness of a brain that will not stop cycling through the same torment no matter how well the sufferer understands it.","src":"haiku"},
"hop-along~powerful-man":{"haiku":"Witnessing a child's abuse and adult indifference; powerlessness in the face of cruelty.","opus":"Frances Quirk's haunted memory of witnessing a child being beaten and freezing, too young and powerless to intervene, then finding no adult who cared. It's about the lifelong guilt of the bystander and the false authority of a cruel man.","sonnet":"A young woman witnesses a father beating his child in the street, freezes in the face of the man's intimidating authority, then tries to find help — only to be turned away by a teacher who refuses to get involved, leaving the speaker haunted by her own inaction.","src":"haiku"},
"hopesfall~hall-of-the-sky":{"haiku":"The song contemplates inherited suffering and existential change, questioning justice and restitution in a world of dying men and moral ambiguity beneath an unchanging sky.","opus":"An oblique, literary meditation that leans on imagery of ruined ground and a man going under to circle ideas of inheritance, the scarcity of restitution and intentional deception, a figure trapped underground reaching for something sharp. The meaning stays purposefully murky. Hopesfall were a North Carolina band who grew from metalcore into spacious atmospheric post-hardcore, and this reflects that later, more cryptic phase.","sonnet":"A meditation on inherited ruin and collective failure — dust scattering above clouds, a scorched earth descended upon without question, a drowning man clutching a razor blade in the mud. The imagery is of people buried by misdirection and appearances they cannot maintain, asking whether restitution is even possible when the approach keeps needing amendment and nothing ever truly ascends.","src":"haiku"},
"horskh~tension":{"haiku":"Tension between growth and dissolution; watching the world rot while struggling to evolve within it.","opus":"The French industrial act frames survival as restraint, distilling existence to minimalism and quiet endurance. Growth pushes up beside rot, blood pumps under regulation, and apocalyptic images of falling trees and a swallowed sun loom. The recurring uncertainty over whether this is war or simply fading captures a tense equilibrium between striving to live and quietly disappearing.","sonnet":"A quiet meditation on attrition — less is more, facing the way, something hatching even in dryness — with the tension being internal: the attempt to grow and flow against some unnamed war, while shadows swallow the sun and trees will one day fall through the clouds.","src":"haiku"},
"hoshimachi-suisei~a-qydc9e":{"haiku":"A Cinderella-inspired fantasy of rebellion and freedom—shedding shame and societal pressure to dance wildly, knowing this moment will end.","opus":"Hoshimachi Suisei rose as a self-produced VTuber before becoming a genuine pop force, and her signature is precisely this furious refusal to stay small. The narrator, stuck at zero, calls a taxi to outer space, transforms through sheer will, and dances in glass slippers until she turns to ash, because tonight has no tomorrow and the one who dances freely wins. The Cinderella spell is reimagined as personal revolution — joy and defiance welded together.","sonnet":"A song about grabbing life by force when you've been stuck at zero — the narrator calls a taxi to outer space, transforms herself through sheer will, and dances in glass slippers until she turns to ash, because tonight has no tomorrow and the one who dances freely wins. The emotional core is a furious, joyful refusal to stay small, driven by the Cinderella spell reimagined as personal revolution.","src":"haiku"},
"hostage~vanitas":{"opus":"A death-haunted meditation in the vanitas tradition — the narrator lost in his own unraveling, numb yet suffering, pleading for release and resigned that rescue is not coming. Mortality faced without comfort.","src":"opus"},
"hot-chip~need-you-now":{"haiku":"Desperation and interdependence reveal collective vulnerability; the narrator cries out for connection, recognizing that isolation guarantees suffering.","opus":"Built around a gospel plea, it stretches personal need into something larger and more desperate, belonging to a world gone wrong. Devotion and dependence blur until the cry for one person sounds like a cry for salvation.","sonnet":"The speaker voices a need for something beyond the self — God, a person, a force — that can rescue them from a world stripped of right and wrong and bound together only by a shared sense of being pressed against the glass of something they cannot enter alone.","src":"haiku"},
"hot-hot-heat~let-me-in":{"haiku":"A relationship fractures under betrayal and injury; the narrator begs for reconciliation while recognizing the wound is terminal, surrendering to inevitable farewell.","opus":"A man jolted awake to clarity too late, standing outside a door he already burned, begging back into a life he now finally understands. The white flag won't save them; recognition arrives only after the ruin.","sonnet":"Someone newly awake to their own emotional clarity pleads for re-entry into a relationship they damaged, oscillating between desperation and resignation as they acknowledge that without reconciliation this is a final farewell.","src":"haiku"},
"hot-milk~bad-influence":{"haiku":"The narrator is self-destructive and isolated, aware they harm others yet unable to stop self-sabotage and dark impulses.","opus":"Manchester pop-punk duo Hot Milk turn self-loathing into a strutting confession, the narrator branding herself a walking disaster, sabotaging her own life, and half-relishing how much she hates herself. Beneath the bratty swagger sits real despair and an admission that she needs help she won't accept, choosing to wear the wreckage as a badge.","sonnet":"Self-disgust performs as defiance — she knows she is the problem, announces it loudly, digs her own grave with a kind of gleeful despair. The push-pull of wanting help and rejecting it, of hating herself while daring anyone to confirm it, drives the whole piece. Chaos is both the wound and the armor.","src":"haiku"},
"hot-milk~party-on-my-deathbed":{"haiku":"Existential nihilism and defiant celebration merge as the speaker refuses to waste time through authentic living. Apocalypse and autonomy intertwine in countercultural surrender.","opus":"Manic defiance from someone who feels like a marionette on a stage already alight, unable to be themselves in a society that won't allow it. Rather than waste what time is left, they resolve to live electric and throw a party at death's door, conjuring their own freedom when the world refuses to grant it.","sonnet":"A nihilistic declaration of hedonism as self-preservation: brain pain, serotonin on its arse, a puppet on a burning stage — if they cannot be themselves in society they will hallucinate their own autonomy and party electric right up until the end.","src":"haiku"},
"hotbox~not-my-time":{"haiku":"Accumulated despair and systemic failure press down on the edge of collapse while the speaker refuses surrender, bargaining with mortality to delay its claim.","opus":"A raw dispatch from the grind of poverty and mental collapse, the track finds its narrator broke on the bus to work, flirting with death while insisting his time hasn't come. Pressure builds toward explosion with no outlet or control. The Grim Reaper waits at the door, met with a hollow reassurance that masks how close to the edge he actually stands.","sonnet":"A rapper on the verge of collapse — broke, walking in circles, the Grim Reaper at the door — insists it is not yet their time to die while simultaneously flirting with the idea. The tension between \"I gotta make it big\" and \"am I even a person\" holds the song in place: not despair, not hope, just the refusal to go under today.","src":"haiku"},
"hotei~clone":{"haiku":"Genetic identity fragments as the cloned consciousness confronts the horror of predetermined existence and the violence of technological reproduction.","opus":"A sci-fi horror in which a man named Randy realizes he is an AI-made clone, grown for decades only to have his heart harvested for the original host, and finally confronts his sixty-years-older self. Screaming who are you into the mirror, it warns of a world where the powerful buy up bodies, futures, and identity itself.","sonnet":"Randy discovers he is a clone grown to provide a heart transplant for his aging host; confronting the older man emerging from a limousine, his every cell recognises itself sixty years forward — a horror of genetic ownership, purchased futures, and the question of who one truly is.","src":"haiku"},
"hotwax~drop":{"haiku":"Emotional turbulence and toxic cycles keep the speaker suspended between anger and submission to another's control.","opus":"The queasy relief of appeasing a volatile partner, mistaking the release of tension for intimacy. The young British trio captures a codependent ache in which absorbing abuse feels almost tender, the narrator wondering whether self-erasure is simply what people do, and whether she has ruined everything.","sonnet":"A cycle of anger and calm governs a relationship the speaker finds addictive even while taking the abuse, asking whether this lurching emotional tension — \"your angry then your not, then I feel the tension drop\" — is just what humans do.","src":"haiku"},
"house-home~a-familiar-direction":{"haiku":"Exhaustion traps the speaker in cyclical stagnation while they simultaneously ache for transcendence and self-connection.","opus":"The slow spiral of depression as a recurring cycle, each year a heavier version of the same fall. The speaker wants only to feel at ease in his own body again, but keeps studying his own hands instead of moving forward.","sonnet":"A person stuck in cycles of self-sabotage and stagnation — each year brings a heavier blow and the same paralysis, too scared to move in any direction, wishing they could give themselves over to something and simply rest in their own skin again.","src":"haiku"},
"house-home~tempest":{"haiku":"Disaster and calm coexist as the speaker haunts their own existence, decomposing in place while yearning for sunlight and conviction to pierce the normalized storm.","opus":"The emo band renders self-sabotage as weather, its narrator snapping at the people keeping him steady while claiming the identity of a catastrophe who stays eerily composed. Structural cracks and crumbling walls mirror an inner storm he's grown used to, yet he aches for sunlight, progress and belief — a weary plea to stop rotting in place where everyone around him has managed to grow.","sonnet":"A speaker who is a natural disaster — unnaturally calm while walls crack and foundations split — confesses they do not decompose where everyone else grows, buried in concrete on the road, but craves sunlight, old friends, and comfort in conviction after living too long inside the storm they make.","src":"haiku"},
"house-of-harm~feel-my-heart-beat":{"opus":"A one-sided love where the other stays just out of reach on purpose, keeping his affection dangling. So numb he can't sense his own pulse, he refuses to release a bond he can see is counterfeit from the outside, unable to admit it is already lost.","src":"opus"},
"house-of-hatchets~the-moth-song":{"haiku":"Carrying curses and crowded skeletons, the speaker burns through darkness to change, expand, and breathe new life into themselves.","opus":"An exhortation to face your inherited curses and rattling skeletons and burn them, letting pain cut through so you can change and breathe. Transformation drawn from the moth's pull toward the flame.","sonnet":"About carrying the weight of past trauma and the urgent need to burn it away — the moth drawn to flame as a metaphor for transformation, urging yourself and others to break the curses and skeletons that crowd you.","src":"haiku"},
"house-of-pain~jump-around":{"haiku":"The speaker asserts dominance through boasting and battle rap, challenging competitors with wordplay and confidence rooted in crew solidarity.","opus":"A braggadocious battle-rap party anthem in which the narrator boasts of demolishing all rivals with superior rhymes and violent threats, refuses to back down, and commands the crowd to get up and jump around; pure hip-hop dominance and hype.","sonnet":"Pure battle-rap bravado at a party — the MC establishes his unbeatable lyrical status, threatens any challenger with physical and verbal defeat, and keeps demanding the crowd jump.","src":"haiku"},
"house-of-pain~jump-around-30-years-remaster":{"haiku":"Boastful hip-hop bravado celebrating lyrical skill and dominance through confrontation and wordplay.","opus":"The Irish-American hip-hop landmark, pure battle-rap bravado and party command, Everlast daring all comers with cop-hating swagger and boasts, its whole purpose to detonate a room into motion.","sonnet":"A classic aggressive hip-hop boast track — the narrator challenges anyone to battle, threatens violence against those who step up, and commands the crowd to jump. The bragging is athletic and playful, the threats cartoonishly belligerent rather than sincere.","src":"haiku"},
"house-of-protection~being-one": {"haiku":"Doubt and limitation dissolve when two merge into singular purpose; the speaker sheds self-questioning to become one aligned force moving beyond earthly constraints.","opus":"A headlong drive toward melting the self into pure momentum and radiance, chasing unity while tallying doubts and hints that this is the final go. The other person turns into the machinery that keeps him running as he pushes past his own edges.","sonnet":"About the experience of merging completely with another person — finding that their presence is the only thing keeping the narrator alive, while self-doubt and emotional holding-back are the barriers that prevent full connection. The song's emotional arc moves from uncertainty and self-criticism toward the release of becoming \"one\" and chasing light together, framed as both romantic and quasi-spiritual.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the kind of love that works by inversion — every line in the verses runs on paradox where weakness accelerates and giving away the last of your breath is the truest gift, so the beloved becomes the sole engine still turning over to keep the speaker running. That logic dissolves the self it depends on, and the chorus cashes it out: two people stop being two, riding a beam of light bent into an arc with no ground under them, endless and aligned. House of Protection let the verses stay clenched and self-doubting — flinching from every move, counting the doubt, out of time to be perfect — precisely so the title phrase can arrive as release rather than mantra. Being one is the exit from that private friction, and by the end it repeats not as a claim but as the only thing left to say.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"self dissolves into the beloved as the only remaining engine","built":"clenched self-doubting verses held in place so the title phrase lands as release"},
"house-of-protection~better-off": {"haiku":"The speaker unshackles themselves from external validation and regret, discovering strength through solitude and self-directed transformation.","opus":"A hardcore band talking themselves out of self-loathing: the narrator finally stops mining regret and pretending healing means understanding, deciding that walking away from a bad phase, not making sense of it, is the actual cure.","sonnet":"A speaker discards a painful past identity — once 'set to stay beneath' in pain they admired — and discovers that untying from the need for outside help gives them more than enough to design their own life, shedding regret as an error now resigned to history.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built as an argument the speaker keeps making to himself: the attempt to peel away an old self and walk out as someone remade only deepened the regret, so he pivots to insisting he needs no one's help and is fine alone. The refrain — ordering an intrusive presence to vacate the space behind his eyes, then affirming he's better off — is the tell, because the more times a claim has to be repeated the less settled it sounds, and House of Protection let the phrase harden into something closer to a chant than a conclusion. What redeems it from pure denial is the closing move, where drifting away stops being a wound and becomes a deliberate resignation, the earlier phase filed as an error he's chosen to leave behind rather than a loss still bleeding.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"the more a claim is repeated the less settled it sounds","built":"refrain hardening from conclusion to chant exposes the denial it performs"},
"house-of-protection~fire":{"haiku":"Relentless ambition demands action over doubt; the speaker burns through obstacles and commands others to join the pursuit.","opus":"A relentless pep-talk of a track: tune out the naysayers, pin down exactly what you're after, and never ease off. It casts hesitation as the real adversary and answers it by blasting straight through the distractions with unbroken momentum.","sonnet":"A motivational track about maintaining relentless drive — ignore critics, define your own goals, never stop when your stride weakens, because doubt kills intention and the fire inside is the only thing that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"house-of-protection~fuse": {"haiku":"Two beings dissolve into unified weightlessness through collision and pleasure; boundaries melt as they fuse into something beyond separation.","opus":"Sex and connection imagined as weightless spaceflight—two bodies dissolving into one, fusing beyond the atmosphere. Physical union becomes cosmic transcendence, the self fading into the other until the stars bloom.","sonnet":"About a consuming, almost spiritual desire to merge completely with someone or something — the speaker craves weightlessness, surrender of the body, and full fusion, and the song frames this as both a physical and existential need to lose individual boundaries and become one with another presence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song disguised as a launch sequence, where the vocabulary of spaceflight — weightlessness, escape velocity, the airless dark past the last thin edge of sky — becomes the only register vast enough for wanting to merge completely with another person. The verses run the countdown and burn as a body's language: oxygen traded, pulse against pulse, collision as pleasure, until the erotic and the astronautical stop being distinguishable and the fusing of two people reads as the fusing of stage and rocket. Structurally it never arrives so much as it keeps approaching — the chorus stalls on fading and waiting rather than landing, the merger held perpetually one beat from completion, which is where the ache lives. That deferral is the point House of Protection's electronic-hardcore build serves: the track keeps tightening the coupling without ever letting it close, the glitching shadows and stars pulled to ground marking how far past ordinary bliss the speaker is reaching. Only the coda lets go, dissolving into a new state where the wait is finally through and the sky blooms — annihilation of the separate self reframed as the thing most wanted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","transcendence & consciousness","freedom & escape"],"means":"merger held perpetually one beat from completion","built":"countdown and burn structure keeps the coupling from ever closing"},
"house-of-protection~godspeed":{"haiku":"Two figures race through the night toward infinite freedom; the stars align as they escape gravity itself, defying sunrise and claiming the world as their own.","opus":"A night drive as escape and romance, two people with nowhere to be tearing across the city as if their love could hold back the dawn. The dream he keeps having is precisely this moment, coming true.","sonnet":"A late-night driving song about escaping into motion with someone you love — windows down, no destination, racing through tunnels, the world belonging to the two of them for one night. It is romantically present-tense and uncomplicated: the desire is simply to keep driving and never let the sun rise.","src":"haiku"},
"house-of-protection~i-need-more-than-this":{"opus":"A terse expression of exhaustion with empty talk, wanting a conversation with real substance to believe in and refusing to be stopped by those who say less.","sonnet":"A song about mental fatigue and the need for substantive connection — the narrator is too exhausted from overthinking to even speak, and won't engage unless the conversation offers something they can actually believe in.","src":"sonnet"},
"house-of-protection~it-s-supposed-to-hurt":{"haiku":"Suffering is inescapable and essential; those who parade their wounds without having earned them through real pain cannot claim the same legitimacy as those who actually bleed.","opus":"Contempt for someone who performs pain they never actually felt, wearing invented scars as a costume. The narrator claims the real, earned suffering as his own and refuses to let a poser take it from him.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who performs pain and struggle as aesthetic identity without actually suffering — the speaker, who has real wounds, is furious that this person parades fabricated scars as a design choice while trying to claim or invalidate the speaker's genuine experience.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's contempt is aimed at performed suffering: someone who paints scars for pain they never felt and then parades the design, a person all wires and no spine, aestheticizing a wound to wear it as costume. Against that fraud the title becomes a challenge rather than a complaint — the speaker insists hurt is the real thing's price, a plunge taken headlong into the surge of it, whereas the poser only prays for rain and mimics the ache from a safe distance. The refrain's shift from flat assertion to genuine question, is it supposed to hurt, stages the divide: the authentic answer already knows yes, and the ambivalence belongs to the one still faking it. What the poser tries to grab is exactly this claim to real experience, and the coda promising that devotion will lift you higher reframes the pain as the toll for something that actually elevates, not the merch someone sells having paid nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"hurt reclaimed as the real thing's price against performed suffering","built":"the refrain's shift from flat assertion to genuine question marks the authentic against the fake"},
"house-of-protection~learn-to-forget": {"haiku":"Healing requires forgetting; the speaker must erase trauma and toxic patterns to reclaim capacity for living.","opus":"Picking oneself up after being burned by someone who talks nonsense and plays permanent victim—the narrator resolves to learn to forget and start again. Cutting the toxic person loose brings not regret but the feeling of being alive.","sonnet":"About recognizing that a relationship was toxic and undeserved — the speaker picked themselves up from being ground down, saw that what they were receiving was not what they merited, and now has to consciously unlearn the damage; the central task is learning to forget someone whose cruelty felt permanent.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song that keeps score of who apologized for what, then decides the ledger was rigged all along. The first half swallows the blame — the speaker owns becoming the worst of himself — but the second half catches the trick: this was never what he deserved, and the other person's wounded silence is a performance no one's buying. House of Protection route the catharsis through their hardcore-plus-electronic build, so the demand to forget lands not as peace but as the moment the self-punishment finally converts into a feeling of being violently alive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"self-punishment converts into feeling violently alive","built":"blame-taking first half exposes the rigged ledger in the second"},
"house-of-protection~phasing-out":{"haiku":"Withdrawing from connection and reality out of fatigue, fading into apathy despite attempts to resist.","opus":"Emotional shutdown as self-preservation, boredom and cold creeping in as a relationship repeats the same defeat, the narrator dissolving out of a life that offers nothing left, calling for his own destruction to stop the further fall.","sonnet":"About a relationship that has gone cold and emotionally dead — the narrator is shutting down from boredom and exhaustion, recognizing the signs of the end but unable to do anything about it; the disconnection feels mutual, and they describe the whole thing as an irreversible drift.","src":"haiku"},
"house-of-protection~pulling-teeth": {"haiku":"Truth becomes caustic: lies repeated loud enough reshape reality, but the cost is the steady erosion of conscience, leaving only poison where integrity once stood.","opus":"An indictment of the shameless liar who repeats a falsehood until it's believed and plays victim to deflect blame. No amount of self-mutilation can undo the rot conviction leaves behind.","sonnet":"A song about someone who builds power through lies and victimhood performance — the speaker dissects how screaming false narratives loudly enough makes them believed, how the rot of deceit corrodes everything, and how no amount of self-purification can undo that damage.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study of the compulsive liar and the rot deceit works on the person telling it: the verses address that figure in the second person, tracking how a lie repeated past the point of doubt hardens into belief and hollows the teeth of the one spinning it, conviction decaying from the inside out. Then the choruses swing the camera around to first person, and the title metaphor turns self-punishing — yanking teeth to kill an ache is a remedy that only deepens the damage, extraction that cannot undo the past any more than it can heal the scar the venom left. What starts as an indictment aimed outward at another collapses into the speaker's own reckoning. The refrain that the pattern begins and ends with me pins the guilt in place, until the band's own name arrives as the verdict: this is the protection-house where the damage, carried past all repair, leaves nothing salvageable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"indictment aimed outward collapses into the speaker's own reckoning","built":"second-person verses swing to first person as the title metaphor turns self-punishing"},
"house-of-protection~slide-away":{"haiku":"Escape into weightlessness and dissolution offers relief from earthly struggle; moving beyond division and conflict toward spiritual transcendence.","opus":"An invitation to slow down and dissolve out of a broken routine with someone, trading fixation and staged performance for a shared vanishing into the clear. Beneath the escape runs doubt about whether the other person can stop being violent long enough to actually go.","sonnet":"About the temptation to drift away from ambition, responsibility, and a volatile person — the speaker recognizes that the relationship and the life they share have become a broken station that needs changing, but cannot fully let go, and instead imagines sliding away into something lighter and clearer.","src":"haiku","fable":"For a band built on velocity, this is the sceptical exhale: an argument that motivational language is a treadmill you can only survive by stepping off. It stacks the whole self-help lexicon of momentum and deadlines and second attempts, then shrugs each one off as an accounting nobody's actually keeping, flattering the listener as a jewel that could shine but keeps drawing blood instead. The chorus wants the opposite of all that striving: to decelerate, sink downward, and thin out into open air, and the murmured underground refrain that closes it reads less like drive than like a person too spent to argue coaxing themselves toward a gentle disappearance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance"],"means":"deceleration and dissolution as the only exit from the self-help treadmill","built":"the murmured closing refrain undercuts the momentum lexicon stacked above it"},
"how-to-destroy-angels~and-the-sky-began-to-scream":{"opus":"A vow of apocalyptic reckoning after waiting in vain for something that never came, resolving to raze the whole rotten order to the ground and rebuild it from scratch.","sonnet":"A person who waited devotedly for a divine or beloved figure finally confronts the vacancy at the center of their faith — the throne is empty — and responds not with grief but with the resolve to tear everything down and rebuild from nothing.","src":"sonnet"},
"how-to-destroy-angels~bbb":{"haiku":"Authoritarian control and submission; the sound of boots enforcing obedience and silencing resistance.","opus":"Trent Reznor and Mariqueen Maandig's project How to Destroy Angels boil authoritarian menace down to curt orders, commanding the listener to stay down, silent and obedient as their lies circle back and their time expires. A hypnotic refrain fixated on approaching military footsteps turns minimal industrial repetition into an image of surveillance and cold control.","sonnet":"Pure authoritarian command stripped of context or justification — stand, sit, obey, close your eyes. The sound of boots is the only text that matters, repeated until submission is not a choice but the atmosphere itself. Power makes itself felt not through argument but through sheer repetition.","src":"haiku"},
"how-to-destroy-angels~fur-lined":{"haiku":"Chemical transformation animates the speaker, who feels themselves becoming something primal and uncontrollable, echoing forward endlessly.","opus":"Trent Reznor and Mariqueen Maandig render surrender to compulsion as becoming animal and chemical, in complete control precisely as control is lost. The looping refrain makes relapse feel inevitable and almost welcome.","sonnet":"A song about surrendering to compulsion — whether drugs, sex, or obsession — the narrator becomes purely animal and chemical, losing control repeatedly, powerless to stop going back again and again.","src":"haiku"},
"how-to-destroy-angels~how-long":{"opus":"A reflection on impermanence and whether a bond, or one's own resolve, can survive, wondering aloud how much longer they can persist as small details erode and the past goes up in flames. Conviction is offered as the single thing that might carry them through, faith presented as the only possible glue against dissolution.","sonnet":"A quietly desperate examination of a relationship or way of life under strain — the narrator wonders how long the two of them can keep holding on as small erosions accumulate and the past burns away. The image of a house on fire suggests that what defines them is being destroyed, but the question is whether belief alone can sustain what remains.","src":"sonnet"},
"how-to-destroy-angels~ice-age":{"haiku":"A person watches everything change while forgetting how to feel; they burn memories and contemplate destruction until hate becomes survival. The song depicts emotional numbing and approaching collapse.","opus":"A frozen meditation on numbness and estrangement, everything looking the same while feeling changed, memory fading and hate quietly keeping the narrator alive. The ocean is begged both to guide and to wash it all away.","sonnet":"About watching a relationship turn cold and grey beyond recovery — the narrator burns their memories of the person and waits for an emotional ice age to arrive, kept alive only by the hate that has replaced love.","src":"haiku"},
"how-to-destroy-angels~keep-it-together":{"haiku":"The speaker feels their physical and mental boundaries dissolving while an internal voice echoes cryptic messages, threatening their coherence.","opus":"Reznor and Maandig's electronic side-project renders a dissociative episode as a person watching the boundary between themselves and a lover erode, terrified of coming apart entirely. The track builds around an internal denial that finally reverses itself, staging the effort to hold a fracturing self intact as both defeat and grim recovery.","sonnet":"About the terrifying loss of boundaries during intimacy — the narrator feels their sense of self dissolving when close to another person, and hears an internal voice they don't want to acknowledge, desperately insisting they can hold themselves together even as they fall apart.","src":"haiku"},
"how-to-destroy-angels~on-the-wing":{"haiku":"Despite fire and memory, people wait and hear warnings on the wing but believe in nothing. They used to believe in someone but now only wait.","opus":"How to Destroy Angels, the industrial project pairing Trent Reznor with Mariqueen Maandig, voices a weary refusal of faith. A narrator hears warnings on the wing yet keeps waiting, once believing in someone but now trusting nothing, eating their own tail. Reznor's brooding electronics turn erosion into atmosphere, so disillusion feels less like collapse than a slow hollowing where waiting replaces conviction.","sonnet":"A speaker who once held faith in something — a person, a belief, a future — watches that faith drain away entirely, leaving only paralysed waiting. Warnings arrive but inspire nothing; voices rise but mean nothing. The sole remaining action is to wait, and even that feels like the residue of a life once lived rather than a life.","src":"haiku"},
"how-to-destroy-angels~the-loop-closes":{"haiku":"Cyclical recurrence traps existence in eternal repetition; beginnings loop back to endings with no escape or variation. Time collapses into meaningless recursion.","opus":"How to Destroy Angels reduce eternal recurrence to a single hypnotic axiom: the beginning is the end, endlessly circling back. As the closing piece on Reznor and Maandig's project, its title enacts its own meaning, the phrase repeating until it becomes ritual. Meaning drains away through repetition, leaving a mantra of futility where every ending merely reopens the same door.","sonnet":"A single idea repeated without relief: every ending is indistinguishable from its own beginning. The relentless cycling offers no escape hatch, no progression — only the circular recognition that the loop itself is the whole story, and it keeps arriving back at the same point forever.","src":"haiku"},
"how-to-destroy-angels~the-sleep-of-reason-produces-monsters":{"haiku":"Nightmares and irrationality born from conscious knowledge, losing God and reason to dreamlike dread on the left side.","opus":"Borrowing Goya's title, it stages sleep and dream-logic as where reason's abandonment breeds horrors, the left-side drowning imagery and the taunt of a missing God suggesting willful denial is what conjures the monsters.","sonnet":"A fragmented song invoking Goya's famous image — when reason sleeps, monsters emerge from dreams and ideology. The song implicates the listener in a world of self-deception (\"you're a slave\") where the comforts of the left side, the dream state, produce the very horrors we fear. \"Where's your God\" is the final accountability question.","src":"haiku"},
"how-to-destroy-angels~the-space-in-between":{"haiku":"Intimate exposure under harsh light; things hidden revealed and the boundaries between people collapsing into visible decay.","opus":"A relationship exposed like a staged scene, with blood on the floor and a crowd watching what the couple tried to hide; as the narrative draws to a close, lingering hate and unkillable things spill out while they try to fill the emptiness between them.","sonnet":"Two people have laid their hidden selves bare — blood on the floor, secrets exposed — and even as their story draws to a close, what could not be destroyed remains visible in each other's eyes, with hate beginning to bleed into the frame.","src":"haiku"},
"how-to-destroy-angels~welcome-oblivion":{"haiku":"An elegant wave of violent change arrives; accepting it brings oblivion while fear disappears. The speaker welcomes what is coming without resistance.","opus":"Trent Reznor and Mariqueen Maandig's side project channels the same apocalyptic calm that runs through his industrial work, here greeting extinction as inevitable. The speaker doubts anyone is even listening while ruin arrives with an elegant grace nature cannot be fought. Flashing lights lead toward some final deliverance, and the repeated refusal to fear turns oblivion into something almost welcomed, a serene surrender rather than a scream.","sonnet":"A force arrives — neither fully disaster nor deliverance — and the voice observes it without fear. Something vast and inevitable is already in motion, carrying with it a strange elegance, and the speaker stands at the threshold of erasure declaring an absence of dread.","src":"haiku"},
"howl-like-wolves~crown":{"haiku":"Rejecting inherited burdens and expectations imposed by others, burning down the kingdom rather than accept its weight.","opus":"Refusing the crown of everyone else's expectations, the narrator wanting only his own decisions and an escape, tearing away the part shaped by others and vowing to burn the whole imposed kingdom down to survive.","sonnet":"About rejecting inherited identity or leadership — the narrator refuses the crown (the role, the expectation, the burden) that was placed on him without his consent, and vows to burn the whole kingdom down rather than carry something he never wanted.","src":"haiku"},
"hrtlss~fear-of-you":{"haiku":"Attraction to danger becomes addiction; the speaker surrenders to seductive malevolence.","opus":"Surrender to a dangerous, siren-like attraction, the speaker willing to be dragged to the depths and ended by her. What was fear becomes hunger, the fiend no longer afraid of the very person who could destroy him.","sonnet":"Total obsession with a woman framed as a siren — her voice, her body, and her presence pull the speaker underwater willingly, and what began as fear of her has transformed into a fiending need to be near her even as she undoes him.","src":"haiku"},
"hrtlss~signs-of-life":{"haiku":"A speaker rusting and flooding with fear finds that a new person's arrival quiets the panic and restores the will to live, turning dread of death into evidence of life.","opus":"The metalcore project charts a climb out of numbness, its narrator rusting and flooded with fear until connection reawakens something. Panic-riddled thoughts give way to a love that makes even mortality bearable, the fear of an ending replaced by fresh evidence of being alive. Salvation arrives through another person, pulling a frozen, hopeless soul back toward feeling.","sonnet":"Panic and rust until one person changes the equation: the outer shell broken, thoughts fluctuating into panic, frozen and crying out — and then I found you becomes the pivot, after which the end no longer frightens because the signs of life are back.","src":"haiku"},
"hu3m3n~t3mpt3d":{"haiku":"Hypnotic seduction operates through knowledge and vulnerability as control penetrates resistance, temptation becoming inescapable manipulation.","opus":"A seduction staged as hypnosis, the singer coaxing someone to loosen up and slip under while claiming to read their secrets and buried desire. Beneath the fascination and the silence, both already know the damning thing they're about to do.","sonnet":"A voice draws someone slowly under its control — knowing their secrets, their fascination, their silence — inviting them to release tension and surrender to a mutual transgression both parties have already decided to commit.","src":"haiku"},
"hucci~end-of-the-story":{"opus":"A doom-drenched lament in which everything a couple, or the human race, ever built is reduced to nothing but ash. The warnings were obvious yet almost no one paid them any mind, while the rest merely grew accustomed to their dread or surrendered, marching off the edge with their eyes fully open.","src":"opus"},
"hudson-ryder~without-u":{"haiku":"Codependency as existential trap; the speaker orbits their destroyer, unable to imagine selfhood beyond the gravity of toxic attachment.","opus":"The push-pull of a toxic love that builds you up only to break you down, spinning you around and leaving you unfound. Even while cataloguing all the harm, the speaker keeps circling back to the helpless question of what life would even be without this person.","sonnet":"A push-pull relationship where the speaker is simultaneously built up and destroyed, chewed and spat out, yet cannot function without the person causing the damage — 'what would my life be without you?' becomes both accusation and admission of total dependence.","src":"haiku"},
"humanity-s-last-breath~vittring":{"haiku":"A hunter or predator describes instinct-driven violence and bloodlust, moving through darkness guided by animalistic impulse rather than reason.","opus":"A predatory first-person descent into pure instinct, senses heightened and eyes rolling back as the narrator clutches from the black and hunts by bloodlust, framing a feral dance of death where spilled blood was foretold. The Swedish title meaning a scenting or catching-wind fits the hunt. Humanity's Last Breath are a Swedish deathcore and thall band known for crushingly downtuned, dissonant heaviness, and this is that suffocating, atmospheric brutality.","sonnet":"A predator's perspective rendered in cold, precise sensory language — every sound perceived, every scent tracked, instinct overriding consciousness as bloodlust pulls the speaker through darkness toward a target who cannot escape. The violence is framed as foretold and natural, a feral display of will in which the hunt is both biological and ritualistic.","src":"haiku"},
"humankind~no-man-s-sky":{"opus":"Isolation and rage in a life pictured as uncharted, wordless open sky, the speaker unable to stop caring and unable to bear any more, asking what's wrong with him while an indifferent other only fakes concern and feeds the fire, aching for it all to be over for good.","src":"opus"},
"hundred-reasons~glorious-sunset":{"haiku":"Numbness and time's erosion strip away the possibility of change; repetition of the same destructive patterns leaves only regret.","opus":"The British rock band delivers an aching farewell to someone dying, the narrator granted ten final minutes that outweigh years of pain. Grief tangles with fury: love, relief, and bitter resentment toward the deceased collide, the poison and the tenderness inseparable. A eulogy that refuses easy comfort, mourning a person while confessing how much they also hurt.","sonnet":"A grief-stripped farewell to someone who died after years of suffering — the speaker had ten minutes before the last breath, just enough to let the memory replace four years of watching pain, and the rage in \"I wish I could beat you for the poison you are\" is directed at the illness itself rather than the person.","src":"haiku"},
"hundred-suns~fractional":{"haiku":"A person detaches from their partner, who only ever disappoint, while spiraling into resigned inaction as hope permanently collapses.","opus":"The supergroup rising from the Dillinger Escape Plan cloaks disillusion in the imagery of a doomed flight, describing a partner whose distance has become a permanent absence. Crash-bracing imagery turns the failing relationship into an impact awaiting arrival. Indifference calcifies into the refrain that the truth wouldn't matter anyway, souring into flat resignation.","sonnet":"Contempt for someone whose emotional vacancy has become a fixed point of damage. The speaker has long since stopped caring what comes next, watching a person whose passivity derails every shared path — the verdict is delivered with flat, exhausted clarity rather than grief.","src":"haiku"},
"hundredth~bottle-it-up":{"opus":"Emotional avoidance and weekend escapism: the narrator forces smiles and bottles up feelings all week, saving the release for weekend highs and self-destruction rather than facing his problems directly.","sonnet":"A portrait of low-level everyday dissatisfaction — the narrator copes with a boring, slightly disconnected life by bottling up emotions until the weekend, finding meaning in small moments of shared chaos with another person rather than pursuing anything more.","src":"sonnet"},
"hundredth~cauterize":{"haiku":"All paths lead toward fading and drifting alone; surrendering and cauterizing meaning reveals existence as ultimately futile waste of time.","opus":"A hunt for a defect in how existence is put together that dissolves into bleak resignation, since it all winds down to fading out and expiring alone, effort seems pointless, so the singer sears the wound shut, deadening it, and lets whatever remains scatter as it will.","sonnet":"A song about existential resignation — the narrator stops searching for flaws in the design of life, accepts that everything ends in black and is surrendered, and chooses to sear off the part of himself that keeps asking why.","src":"haiku"},
"hundredth~neurotic":{"haiku":"A person burns with neurotic anxiety; they decompose inwardly, unable to break free from their own mental paralysis.","opus":"The spin of an anxious mind trying to numb itself, lighting up and snuffing out, rotting slowly under its own dishonesty. The escape it reaches for is exactly what's dragging it down to the bone.","sonnet":"A person trapped in neurotic cycles of self-deception and decline — burning bright then blowing out, sinking to the bone, decomposing under the weight of their own lies until there is nothing left.","src":"haiku"},
"hundredth~vertigo":{"haiku":"Drug use or obsession creates a dizzying loss of perspective and control; the speaker spirals while seeking the high, knowing the crash will come. Escape requires surrender to the blur.","opus":"Hundredth, the North Carolina band that drifted from melodic hardcore toward dreamier textures, maps addiction as disorientation. A narrator escapes into a high only to black out, spinning, promised that whatever was lost returns on the comedown, yet resisting that return. The refusal to come down captures the whole pull, chasing an elevated blur even as the vantage point dissolves into vertigo.","sonnet":"Someone chases an elevated, blurred-out high that distorts perception entirely, only to discover that clarity waits on the other side of the crash. The vertigo of the peak is seductive — the speaker admits not wanting to come down — but the pattern of blackout and comeback repeats, implying the comedown is inevitable and perhaps the only place where anything real can be found.","src":"haiku"},
"hundredth~whatever":{"opus":"A restless plea to bolt and finally feel something worthwhile, life likened to a tightrope walk and to plucking a bloom that was withered all along. The narrator just wants to flee, quiet his mind and be whatever he chooses.","src":"opus"},
"hundredth~youth":{"haiku":"A young person wrestles with the diminishing returns of commitment, haunted by the possibility that maturity and relationships demand sacrifices that drain vitality.","opus":"The vertigo of walking away from something you built, second-guessing every step, wondering if leaving delivers the dream or just a duller version of yourself. Regret over a war he started and the pull to just go home.","sonnet":"A speaker in retreat from conflict and from a version of themselves they no longer recognize, questioning whether past choices were worth the cost and settling into the isolating conclusion that solitude is the only cure.","src":"haiku"},
"hunters-collectors~holy-grail":{"haiku":"A spiritual army crusades across the world chasing an impossible relic, only to discover that devotion and glory prove hollow when confronted with the reality of suffering and death. The quest for transcendence becomes itself the source of waste and disillusionment.","opus":"The Australian band borrows Napoleon's doomed march to frame any lifelong pursuit that costs everything and delivers nothing. The fool survives, wounds clean, still chasing, because the chasing was always the whole point.","sonnet":"A soldier-narrator reflects on a lifetime's crusade — literal and existential — chasing an elusive ideal through bloodshed and futility, only to wake alone, wounds healed, still a fool for the cause he can never reach.","src":"haiku"},
"hunters-collectors~throw-your-arms-around-me":{"haiku":"Devotion transcends meeting through promise and persistence, with the speaker bound eternally to their beloved across time and separation. Arms encircle presence or absence equally.","opus":"A tender, insistent appeal to grab hold of a passing moment of closeness, holding a lover completely in full awareness that the two of them might never cross paths again.","sonnet":"A speaker approaches a lover at night with total, consuming devotion — kissing them in four places, dreaming of them, meeting them in high places — and even knowing they may never meet again, refuses to say goodbye, only asking to be held.","src":"haiku"},
"hurt-division~another-kind-of-me":{"opus":"A struggle against psychological domination where the narrator watches a consciousness dissolve as everything is forced into line and made to conform, a lopsided hurt that leaves him blinded; he needs the other's grip to loosen to break loose, absorbed and sinking as it beckons, begging for release.","src":"opus"},
"hvdes~dying-sun":{"haiku":"Light fades and warmth diminishes; the speaker mourns celestial death and encroaching darkness.","opus":"The electronic-metal artist stages an apocalyptic rendezvous where two figures meet amid ash-filled lungs and broken promises, all escape routes gone. Fascination with suffocation and surrender pervades the track as the narrator dissolves under a collapsing sky. Intimacy and annihilation merge, love offered only as a shared descent into the nightmares waiting at the end of everything.","sonnet":"A relationship ending in entropy — meeting under a dying sun where promises break under the weight of empty space, atoms breaking up, the air thin and killing; \"I like it when I cannot breathe and you're pointing the gun\" turns mutual destruction into an act of perverse intimacy. The sun is both the relationship and consciousness itself, dissolving to nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"hvdes~parasyte":{"haiku":"An internal parasite has taken up residence in the speaker's body, commandeering senses and will, and the only exit from its control is the speaker's own death, which kills the parasite too.","opus":"Built on the horror of a thing that inhabits and devours its host, the electronic-metal track voices something peering out from behind the narrator's eyes, consuming him from within. The terror centers on what's left once his whole body dies, and the grim comfort that the parasite goes down with him. A vivid metaphor for illness, addiction or intrusive thought hollowing a person out.","sonnet":"A parasite that has taken up residence behind the speaker's own eyes is slowly consuming the host: it breathes the air from your lungs, speaks the words from your tongue, takes control to conceal all the signs — terrified of what will be left behind when every cell begins to die, with the only exit being that the parasite dies too.","src":"haiku"},
"hybrid~if-i-survive":{"haiku":"The speaker faces death or erasure but vows that if they survive, they will expose their attacker and reclaim their life outside the conspirator's shadow.","opus":"The singer draws thin breath through a staged execution, defiant that mere words can't wound him. What fuels the track is survival used as a threat: lasting long enough to expose the truth becomes both rescue and payback, as he pledges to vanish, rebuild, and topple the one who tried to kill him. His looping vow to inform on his tormentor works less as a plot than as proof that outliving cruelty is itself his weapon.","sonnet":"A survivor of deliberate harm holds the threat of testimony over their attacker. Hanging between near-death and the possibility of escape, the speaker vows that if they make it out, the perpetrator will be exposed — defiance sharpening with each repetition of the conditional.","src":"haiku"},
"hyde~fake-divine":{"haiku":"The speaker resists manipulation disguised as faith or guidance, choosing self-deception (faking divinity) to preserve agency.","opus":"A refusal to keep playing the manipulative games of someone who destroys the narrator's faith and teases him toward collapse. Cornered and running out of time, he resolves to fake divinity and save himself, defying the wrath of a partner or gods who never let him get it right.","sonnet":"Rejection of a manipulative deity or authority that offers salvation but keeps the narrator dangling and breaking them down — the resolution is to stop waiting for divine rescue and manufacture your own divinity to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"hyde~rock":{"haiku":"A call to liberate suppressed instinct and primal nature from social constraint—breaking free from societal imprisonment.","opus":"The Japanese rock icon issues a call to break free of societal conformity, urging listeners to escape the prison of masks and obedient conformity and unleash the beast within. Railing against surface-level goodness and forced order, the track glorifies instinct, awakening, and rebellion. HYDE frames liberation as a defiant, ritualistic release of raw, suppressed nature.","sonnet":"A call to break out of the prison of social conformity and release the animal instinct that society suppresses — the song frames ordinary life as a cage, civilized behavior as a masquerade, and the concert as the moment to breach that defense and ignite the beast within. The emotional core is the classic rock fantasy of liberation through transgression and collective ecstasy.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~accelerate":{"haiku":"Youth deceives itself into believing joy when entertainment silences all questioning; stagnant jukebox melodies taste empty against authentic flavor.","opus":"A cover of Adam and the Ants' Ant Music, this version keeps the original's cheeky call to abandon stale sounds for something new. It mocks the condescension aimed at the young and warns, through the image of a trodden ant that fights back, against underestimating the overlooked. Playful defiance dressed as a manifesto for fresh, untamed music.","sonnet":"A cover of Adam and the Ants' \"Antmusic\": the speaker rejects hollow fun and tasteless mainstream music, calling for the jukebox to be unplugged and a new flavour tried — ant music — and warning that the ant you tread on will come back with legs looking for you.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~ant-music":{"opus":"A cover urging the young to unplug the stale mainstream jukebox and try a different flavor, with the ants as a warning that the small and overlooked can turn on you. The song champions upstart, tribal new music over exhausted convention.","sonnet":"A manifesto against stale pop culture — unplug the jukebox because the music has lost its taste, try another flavor — with a warning that ants are not to be tread upon: cut off the head and the legs come looking for you.","src":"sonnet"},
"hyper~break-and-enter":{"haiku":"Invasion and refusal frame the central conflict, with the speaker and their collective breaching barriers that silence them. Surrender is impossible when existence itself is an act of defiance.","opus":"A blunt anthem of forced entry and defiance built on a single refrain, insisting you can't lock them out and they never surrender, telling the listener to brace for the sound of the break-in. Working in electronic breakbeat territory, it's essentially a chant-driven adrenaline hook, more slogan than story, all momentum and menace hammering the same aggressive promise of unstoppable entry.","sonnet":"An unstoppable intrusion framed as defiance — no barrier holds, no exclusion sticks, and the sound of arrival is the only warning given before the entry happens. The song has no interior; it is all forward momentum and refusal to be shut out, a collective that surrenders nothing and announces itself by already being inside.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~centre-attraction":{"haiku":"Celebrity hunger and media machinery consume authentic identity; manufactured desire replaces genuine meaning in attention-obsessed culture.","opus":"A jeer at spotlight-chasing phonies who put on an act for a spot in the press and imagine it makes them stars. Cranked up and released by the club's photographers, each is dismissed as a hollow nobody whose opinions aren't worth a single coin.","sonnet":"A contemptuous portrait of someone desperate for fame and relevance — the speaker dissects the logic of celebrity-seeking, noting that without constant attention you fade into nothingness, but with it you are just another hollow, vacant, manufactured spectacle; either way the value is zero.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~cheap-thrill":{"haiku":"Desire catalogs sensory pleasures across multilingual devotion, the beloved imagined as fruit and spice sustaining hunger while night refuses dawn.","opus":"A sensual love song that piles up fruits, spices, and sweets only to declare a lover's taste better than all of them. The refrain begs for water for thirst and another kiss before dawn, savoring desire as endless appetite.","sonnet":"A playful Spanish-language love song listing fruits, herbs, and spices as metaphors for a lover's irresistible flavour — water for thirst, moon for the sun — begging them to stay until dawn and give just one more kiss.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~controllin-me":{"haiku":"Loss of agency and autonomy dominate; something external controls the speaker, who seeks meaning and fears being lost.","opus":"A dance track about surrendering to something that now controls the singer, lost and pleading for someone to give him something to say and to believe him; the throughline is a loss of agency to an external force or compulsion, wanting not to be lost anymore.","sonnet":"About something — a force, a relationship, an addiction — that has taken over completely and cannot be identified or escaped; the narrator feels lost, wants to believe, but can only repeat that whatever it is has control, with no explanation for why or how.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~dirty-mind":{"opus":"A blunt, playful song about mutual sexual desire and a hookup, built around explicit come-ons and the drive to satisfy each other; essentially about lust with no pretense.","sonnet":"A direct, uncomplicated song about mutual sexual desire — two people want each other and act on it, the narrator's mind consumed by wanting to be satisfied. No subtext or narrative; pure libidinal energy.","src":"sonnet"},
"hyper~drive":{"haiku":"Nocturnal escapism and sensory immersion blur past and present into eternal motion; the speaker drives to forget rather than arrive.","opus":"An escapist late-night drive with no destination, wanting to forget everything under the city lights and stars, treating aimless speed as the freedom that keeps the adrenaline going.","sonnet":"In Japanese and English: a request for a night drive as an escape from the daily routine — the speaker wants to forget their troubles by speeding into the dark, watching lights blur like stars, with no destination and the radio as the only companion.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~fckd":{"opus":"A single blunt creed repeated as manifesto: the moment you make music to please the room rather than yourself, you're finished.","sonnet":"A blunt, one-idea statement that making music for an audience rather than for yourself is a form of artistic death — the song repeats this premise with escalating emphasis, offering no other content; it is a piece of gear-shift noise built entirely around the proposition that crowd-pleasing destroys the artist.","src":"sonnet"},
"hyper~he-rises":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic prophecy describes end times—the return to Zion, imperial collapse, and resurrection from the sea. Cosmic dread accompanies transformation.","opus":"Built on the ominous prophecy recited in The Omen films, the track foretells the coming of the Antichrist heralded by signs of return and empire reborn. Essentially an occult-horror invocation, it summons the Beast emerging from the endless waters and the doom that trails his arrival.","sonnet":"Draws on the apocalyptic prophecy from \"The Omen\" — the Antichrist rises when specific signs converge, requiring the death of the narrator and everyone else. It's more invocation than interpretation, using biblical end-times language as pure sonic dread.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~he-rises-original-mix":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic prophecy and mortality collapse together as the speaker observes end-times with detached fatalism, accepting cosmic annihilation as the inevitable consequence.","opus":"A dance track built on apocalyptic prophecy, Jews returning to Zion, a comet overhead, the old empire rising again, and the flat certainty that we're all going to die. The repeated refrain turns end-times imagery into ominous incantation.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic omen delivered in four lines: the Jews' return to Zion, a comet filling the sky, and the Holy Roman Empire rising all converge on one outcome — you and I must die — and something simply rises.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~heartbeats":{"haiku":"Insomnia and the inescapable awareness of mortality and emotional pain, with the speaker's heartbeat and vivid internal experience keeping them awake against their will.","opus":"A breakbeat charge built on insomnia and a racing pulse, addressed to someone who should be asleep but feels the narrator's heart pounding. Beneath the drive is a warning that liars fall and, once it's over, it's over.","sonnet":"Someone who should be sleeping is kept awake by another's presence — the heartbeat as proof of something real — circling around the exhaustion of liars and the finality of endings, but never quite resolving whether to hold on or let go.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~heartbeats-original-mix":{"haiku":"Lies accumulate with each cycle while deception hardens into ritual, the speaker watching themselves deteriorate as trust erodes and the relationship becomes pure performance.","opus":"Late-night intimacy edged with menace, the singer noting a lover should be asleep while asking if they feel the heartbeat. Threaded through is an accusation of accumulating lies, each dying on the floor, until it's over.","sonnet":"A late-night confrontation with accumulated lies: each night they multiply, dying on the floor, and the speaker — heartbeat exposed, insisting the other should be asleep — holds the accumulated deception up until it is simply over.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~i-m-an-image":{"haiku":"A speaker exists as image rather than substance—screamed at, unseen, yet refusing to break. Defiance hardens into strength.","opus":"A jittery assertion of an unseen, anxious self who insists on doing her own thing despite the screaming and doubt of others; being reduced to an image collides with the will to break out.","sonnet":"A song about asserting presence and competence against dismissal — the narrator is a girl who feels unseen, is told she's nervous or screaming at herself, but breaks out insisting she's doing it, believing in herself despite someone's ongoing doubt.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~i-m-sick":{"haiku":"Defending personal chaos against judgment, refusing to be fixed or managed by those who label and condemn.","opus":"Reckless self-sabotage owned defiantly, wrecking cars and reopening scars while being labeled sick by an ex's friends, the narrator embracing the diagnosis and telling all of them off, knowing he's the one who ruins things.","sonnet":"About someone defiantly rejecting the label of \"sick\" being applied to them by others — the narrator crashes cars, drinks, reopens wounds, steals keys and drives away, and throws the accusation back; their message is that they know who they are and refuse to let the judgment of friends define them.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~impact-weapon":{"opus":"Built almost entirely from a sampled police dispersal order, the track evokes state repression of a protest, threatening arrest and riot-control weapons against a crowd told to move now.","sonnet":"Built entirely from police riot control audio — officers order a crowd to disperse under threat of arrest or use of force, with the phrase \"impact weapon\" repeated as a rhythmic threat; the song is about state violence and crowd suppression with no lyrical narrative beyond the source material.","src":"sonnet"},
"hyper~let-me-in":{"haiku":"Predatory charm disguised as seduction: entry granted becomes invasion, and trust becomes the tool by which violation enters.","opus":"A brash, boastful seduction rap built on knocking at a door and talking his way in, all swagger and innuendo. It's pure predatory-cool bravado, the conquest played as a game he's certain he wins.","sonnet":"A sexually explicit rap in which a man aggressively pursues a woman he wants to sleep with, framing his pursuit through braggadocious wordplay — knocking at her door, promising to satisfy her, and cataloguing his sexual prowess.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~lies":{"haiku":"Confronts someone with an accusation of deception, asserting that everything they do reveals the truth they're trying to hide.","opus":"Built almost entirely from fragmented repetition, the song accuses someone of bluffing, insisting they know the truth about everything they do.","sonnet":"A fragmented accusation directed at someone whose deception is self-evident: the speaker insists the person knows the truth of what they have done, reducing the charge to a relentless, chopped repetition of that knowledge.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~live-forever":{"haiku":"A real man makes his mind up and lives by principle rather than complaint, knowing that everything will eventually be exposed and remembered in history.","opus":"A swaggering takedown of a loudmouth coward, contrasting the man who owns his choices against the one who talks and lies. Beneath the bravado sits a real idea, that conviction and honesty are what actually endure.","sonnet":"A confrontational track addressed to a loudmouth coward who talks big but won't act — the narrator has no respect for someone who slides by on noise without backing it up, asserting that real men live forever through their actions while cowards die as cowards.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~live-forever-original-mix":{"haiku":"Boastful confidence masks eternal scrutiny and the speaker's refusal to break, yet history's rain penetrates all walls—genuine greatness endures when others' judgment and deception collapse.","opus":"A swaggering challenge to a loudmouth pretender, the track draws a line between real men who decide and act and cowards who talk and lie. The narrator claims immortality through conviction and legacy, promising history will remember him while his rival fades. Beneath the bravado runs a belief that living fearlessly and truthfully is what earns forever.","sonnet":"A combative speaker warns a loud-mouthed rival that real men act rather than perform — the refrain \"we gonna live forever\" doubles as a dare and a threat, anchored in the conviction that history exposes cowards while authentic deeds endure.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~machine":{"haiku":"A machine operator or willing subject describes themselves as a passive instrument meant only to be used and abused by others.","opus":"A breakbeat track built on the image of a person reduced to a machine to be used and abused, surrendering control entirely. The garbled repetition itself enacts the dehumanization, identity dissolving into a device to be switched on.","sonnet":"A mechanistic invitation to be used — the speaker declares himself a machine, willing to be turned on, operated, even abused, with no self left to object. The lyrics are grammatically fractured in a way that itself suggests an entity whose language is degraded by its function.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~never-stop":{"haiku":"A pursuer expresses desire to touch and possess another while refusing to learn their identity or know anything about them—a relentless, anonymous fixation.","opus":"A relentless, hedonistic club chant fixated on desire and pursuit, the narrator spotting a target on the dancefloor and insisting he doesn't need to know their name, the hooky refrain hammering that nothing will ever stop him. Its broken, mantra-like phrasing serves energy over sense. Hyper is a British big-beat and breakbeat producer, and this rides that late-breakbeat lineage of grinding riffs, chant vocals and dancefloor aggression.","sonnet":"A late-night pursuit of anonymous desire — the speaker circles a target at a club, deliberately withheld their name, driven by a momentum that never stops. The lyrics are glancing and kinetic, the emotional content almost entirely physical, the point being the chase itself rather than any arrival.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~out-of-time":{"haiku":"Someone feels their partner exists outside normal space and time. They hold them close, blocking out light while facing inevitable separation.","opus":"A big-beat track wrapped around a wistful sense of a fleeting connection, someone arriving briefly from the dark and being held before slipping out of reach, time running out even as the narrator insists they'll be fine.","sonnet":"A spare, atmospheric piece about reaching for someone who feels unreachable — out of place, out of time — while the narrator tries to shelter them from an overwhelming world. More mood than narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~push-it":{"haiku":"Instinctive surrender to compulsion, consciousness warning ignored as desire drives you past reason.","opus":"A minimal rave mantra about instinct overriding conscience, the warning ignored in favor of pushing sensation until you're captivated, surrender to compulsion as the whole point on the dance floor.","sonnet":"An almost entirely percussive, chant-driven track about instinct overriding reason — the conscience warns but the instinctive feeling pushes forward anyway, without choice or meaning; it's essentially a loop about compulsion.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~replica":{"haiku":"Systemic breakdown converts humans into copies; the speaker witnesses willful transformation into hollow reflection, ignorance preferred to awakening.","opus":"A taunt at someone who let the system grind them into a mere copy, a reflection with no self, numbed by the bliss of ignorance. The needling refrain asks how it feels to be a replica, and whether they even like it.","sonnet":"An accusation directed at someone who has let a system hollow them out — blind to what is being done to them, living on ignorance as bliss, the narrator asks how it feels to be nothing but a reflection of forces that have broken and replicated them.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~spoiler-recoil":{"haiku":"Unexpected backlash and consequence, plan backfiring violently.","opus":"The track fixates on consequence and complicity, asking what a person feels with a weapon in hand and daring them to answer honestly. Everything, it insists, is designed to shatter — creations, praise, the self. Inviting others to exploit its own hurt, the narrator turns injury into fuel, chasing the kickback of every action he takes.","sonnet":"Everything is built to break — creativity, praise, daily routine — and the narrator invites the full force of that breakage, welcoming the knife in the back as something to enjoy and asking how you'd feel with your finger on the trigger. \"You can monetize my pain, I'll exploit it\" is the most lucid line: self-destruction as content.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~spoiler-recoil-i-think-i-broke-something-remix":{"opus":"A confrontational electronic assault on exploitation and creative burnout, insisting everybody is built to break. It challenges a listener to imagine the recoil of pulling the trigger themselves, turning monetized pain and sameness into a taunt. Desperation, exhaustion, and defiant self-exploitation collide in a loop of built-to-break resignation.","sonnet":"A meditation on complicity in self-destruction and systemic disposability — the recurring question of how it would feel to pull the trigger frames both literal violence and the monetization of pain. The narrator leans into the inevitability of breakage, finding a perverse pleasure in the recoil of impact.","src":"sonnet"},
"hyper~take-me-away":{"haiku":"Someone grieves their lost relationship intensely, feeling they can still sense their departed lover's presence in dreams and through the walls, begging to be taken away.","opus":"Grief for a dead loved one who still haunts the narrator through dreams, whispers and left-behind words. Feeling loved yet unable to face the loss, he begs to be taken away to join them, worn down by living with the haunting.","sonnet":"Grief for someone who has died: the narrator is haunted by the person's presence in dreams and whispers, desperate to be taken away to wherever they are rather than face continued absence.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~the-end":{"haiku":"Apocalypse and finality, the world or relationship reaching terminal conclusion.","opus":"A candid wrestle with faith, ambition, and doubt, the rapper admits he doesn't feel the fortune others assume and forces smiles behind a porcelain face. Torn between chasing worldly success and answering a divine calling, he leans on God even while envying peers and questioning whether his motives are reckless. Perseverance and prayer sustain a man unsure the world will ever notice.","sonnet":"A rapper reckons honestly with the gap between his calling and his accomplishments — high-school dreams of Soundbombing, half-amazing on a good day, homeless while navigating the sky. The theological framing (God's call, the scroll to swallow, the Son of God's echo) sits alongside doubt: maybe it'll never be big business, maybe the race isn't over, so run anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~the-fallen-original-mix":{"haiku":"Cosmic collapse and entropy manifest as musical dissolution; the fallen rise as echoes of destroyed order.","opus":"A spare, incantatory plea for stability, the track leans on memory and closeness to stay upright. Repeated pleas to be kept from falling and to have someone stay the night distill the whole piece to dependence on another's presence. Minimal and hypnotic, it captures the raw need for an anchor when collapse feels imminent.","sonnet":"Memories of someone are the only thing keeping the speaker from falling — \"they keep me from falling\" — and the plea to stay close tonight frames the past as a lifeline, the warmth of what was holding together what remains.","src":"haiku"},
"hyper~touch":{"opus":"A slick, hedonistic pull toward the other side, sensation as its own reward with a plastic smile pasted over the emptiness. The refrain admits it doesn't really know anything beyond the craving for touch.","sonnet":"The lyrics are minimal and largely opaque, built around the word \"touch\" repeated as a kind of yearning — the narrator wants to be shown something, does not know what they are looking for, and senses something tempting just out of reach. The substance is mostly mood rather than meaning; there is an implied physical or emotional encounter being sought but never fully articulated.","src":"sonnet"},
"hyper~we-control":{"haiku":"Assertion of control and power, warning of downfall for those in their path with repetitive declarations of domination.","opus":"Hyper build a breakbeat incantation around domination, a voice daring listeners to fall as it seizes control and repeats its command to roll. The British breaks producer leans on chant and repetition rather than narrative, looping trust and surrender into a hypnotic power play. Meaning lives in the propulsive insistence that control belongs to those moving in first.","sonnet":"A collective asserts total dominance — wherever they arrive, others fall. The lyric circles the question of control obsessively, folding trust and self-trust into the claim that the group's momentum cannot be stopped. It is less a narrative than a declaration of force, repeated until the assertion becomes the thing asserted.","src":"haiku"},
"hypocrisy~compulsive-psychosis":{"haiku":"A serial killer experiences uncontrollable violent impulses and claims divine purpose, murdering without restraint while voices drive him toward escalating brutality.","opus":"The Swedish death metal band led by Peter Tagtgren voices a murderer's deranged mind with clinical glee. Judged yet unhinged, the narrator claims divinity while the voices of his victims accuse him, and he deflects blame by insisting he was merely obeying orders. It is early-nineties death metal at its ugliest, staging madness as a voice that muddles command, urge and culpability.","sonnet":"A voice inhabiting total psychological collapse narrates its own violence as an irresistible compulsion: hunger, command from the dead, a body out of control. Guilt coexists with the acts — the dead accuse, the soul is tormented — yet the cycle cannot be stopped. The horror lies in the speaker being both perpetrator and prisoner of the same broken mind.","src":"haiku"},
"hypomaniacs~breach":{"haiku":"Tearing through boundaries and inhibition, chaos breaching restraint and speakers bleeding under pressure.","opus":"A festival banger about ego and appetite breaching every limit, tearing up the floor and demanding to be fed, the unexpected breakdown and bleeding speakers turning chaos and abduction into the euphoric climax.","sonnet":"Minimal in content — it's about erupting onto a dance floor with pure physical force and appetite, a release of pent-up energy that \"bleeds\" through the speakers. The lyrical content is thin enough that the song is more about the sensation of a breach than any specific narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"hyro-the-hero~fight-feat-chad-gray-of-hellyeah":{"haiku":"A call to resist systemic oppression and personal despair through collective struggle, refusing to accept death, poverty, and invisibility as inevitable.","opus":"The Houston rap-rock artist, with Hellyeah's Chad Gray, forges an anthem for anyone scraping by paycheck to paycheck, buried in debt and stress, nudged toward crime by conditions no one should endure. Turning his own veins into a source of ink, he reframes that desperation as a defiant, collective refusal to be abandoned or destroyed, a rallying cry to keep swinging.","sonnet":"Addressed directly to someone drowning in invisibility, debt, and despair, the song refuses to let them be the one left behind. The speaker bleeds through the pen rather than through surrender. The fight is not triumphalist — the conditions are brutal — but the act of getting up off the ground is what distinguishes survival from disappearance.","src":"haiku"},
"hyro-the-hero~kids-against-the-monsters":{"haiku":"Young generations face systemic corruption and technological control imposed by older powers, resisting marginalization and fighting for agency against overwhelming odds.","opus":"The rap-rock artist mounts a generational uprising, casting young people as roses forcing their way through concrete against a dehumanizing machine that treats them as obsolete cogs. Rejecting the blame heaped on them by elders, the track insists these kids are the ones making change, facing giants and refusing to have their smiles erased.","sonnet":"Young people pushed aside by economic machinery and older generations' blame fight back as underdogs — \"roses growing out of that concrete\" — asserting that they, not the institutions judging them, are the ones actually making change against the giants they face daily.","src":"haiku"},
"hyro-the-hero~worst-behavior":{"haiku":"Rebellion and transgression become liberation from systemic oppression; the narrator embodies uncontainable chaos as a freight train of resistance.","opus":"A defiant, combustible declaration of unstoppable resistance, the rap-rock casting the narrator and his people as uncontainable renegades storming the barricades. Under the aggression sits a quieter passage of doubt and prayer, wondering whether success demands ruthlessness, and concluding that dropping all restraint is the only way through.","sonnet":"The speaker positions themselves as a renegade force that cannot be contained by the systems and institutions they despise — an earthquake, an avalanche — but the bridge interrupts the aggression with a confessional: they've looked in the mirror, prayed without answer, wondered if maybe releasing the belt of belief is the only way to reach their goals.","src":"haiku"},
"hyvmine~assassins":{"haiku":"Professional killers carrying out missions to expose truth and destroy false authority, standing in sanctuaries of those who died fighting the same enemies.","opus":"Metal band Hyvmine cast a killer's oath, welcoming victims to a sanctuary and mortuary where blades are sharpened and blood spilled as the target wakes. The narrator disclaims wanting a taste of the victim, insisting he only ever craved the truth and grew tired of the lies. Cutting them down for his own sake, prayers rendered useless, he announces himself the assassin with cold, ritual certainty.","sonnet":"A warrior purified by purpose rather than cruelty — the killing is not about blood or conquest but about truth, reclaiming something lost, cutting out corruption at its root. At a sanctuary built for the fallen, the blade is drawn not out of hatred but in pursuit of what is real.","src":"haiku"},
"i-am-abomination~heir-to-the-throne":{"haiku":"The speaker and their circle possess unshakeable confidence and superiority, claiming they have earned dominion through merit and prowess.","opus":"I Am Abomination proclaim royal-blooded supremacy, a crew that sets oceans on fire and pulls the galaxy into reach, demanding rivals bow before an earned crown. The metalcore act frames ambition as sorcery and merit rather than dream, mocking envy hidden behind inept calls to arms. Prestige becomes the mutual lust that turns fantasy into a claimed reality.","sonnet":"Dominance claimed through merit rather than luck — a collective declares the throne theirs by right of effort and vision, demanding submission from those who relied on serendipity or sloth. The rhetoric is imperial and absolute, framing artistic ambition as conquest: the oceans on fire, the galaxy within reach, rivals bowing to earned brilliance.","src":"haiku"},
"i-built-the-sky~the-fire-inside":{"haiku":"An insomniac battles internal turmoil—burning desire and desperation that keeps them awake and isolated. They crave human connection but are consumed by self-destructive thoughts.","opus":"A wrestle with an inner blaze, anxiety, sleeplessness or obsession, that won't let the narrator rest, trapping him in a fight he has to escape before it wrecks his mind. He jolts awake drenched and repulsed by what he sees in the mirror, desperate for closeness yet unable to lean on a partner's broken guidance, forever hunting for shelter until the burning subsides and time runs out.","sonnet":"An internal fire keeps the narrator sleepless and trapped — internal conflict or mental anguish that can't be escaped — compounded by failed communication and the horror of seeing one's own reflection, the song ending with a desire to start a new insurrection against whatever holds them.","src":"haiku"},
"i-met-a-yeti~cthuwu":{"haiku":"Devotion through corrupted ritual; the speaker traces love's transformation into shared transgression, sanctifying what should remain profane.","opus":"An eldritch-tinged romance where sharing water becomes a holy, binding rite, the lover melting like wax under a gentle hand toward something sacred and consuming. Beneath the cute cosmic-horror framing is a genuine ache to be touched and remade.","sonnet":"An intensely physical and ambivalent intimacy between two people who had previously hurt each other — the narrator is both yielding and coercive, drawn to a lover who once promised to leave, reframing their shared moments as sacred through the act of sharing.","src":"haiku"},
"i-o-lights~annihilation":{"haiku":"A toxic relationship characterized by cycles of pain and return, where the speaker craves destruction through their lover's body and presence despite the harm.","opus":"A song about a toxic, addictive love in which the narrator craves the destruction it brings, hooked on someone who hurts and abandons them yet keeps drawing them back from the edge of loneliness and despair.","sonnet":"An addictive, destructive relationship: the speaker is drawn back repeatedly to someone who hurts them, questioning why pain masquerades as love, admitting to being in love with loneliness, and craving total obliteration as the only honest response to the cycle.","src":"haiku"},
"i-o-lights~drift-away":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and time pressure make authentic living impossible, causing people to drift away from effort and consciousness into numbness.","opus":"A portrait of burnout and emotional shutdown, someone sleeping the days away, drowning suppressed anger in liquor and pinning it all on low pay. There aren't enough hours to fix any of it, so everyone involved simply lets go and floats off, resignation dressed as coping.","sonnet":"The song describes the numbing exhaustion of low-wage, emotionally suppressed daily life — sleeping through weekends, bottling up rage, unable to process feelings — and the only available response is to let go of control and drift, since there is never enough time or energy to actually fix anything.","src":"haiku"},
"i-o-lights~epilogue":{"haiku":"The speaker mourns the loss of natural wonder and religion; they are fractured across many selves seeking meaning, finally sinking into self-awareness.","opus":"I O Lights voice a collapse of faith, a narrator who no longer perceives the birdsong or warmth that once brought him comfort and now doubts what he even believes. Haunted by acts committed in devotion's name, he depicts a self splintered into countless guises and a mind scattered everywhere but present, the painful realization gradually settling in.","sonnet":"A spiritual crisis dressed as inventory — birds gone silent, sun no longer dancing, a thousand faces staring from the mirror, none of them quite here. Love has scattered the self across a thousand places; only now, reflected back at last, does something like understanding start to settle.","src":"haiku"},
"i-o-lights~run":{"haiku":"Nostalgia for carelessness contrasts with the exhaustion of adulthood, where running continues until everyone collapses.","opus":"A bittersweet indie-electronic anthem about dreading adulthood and the ordinary world waiting beyond youth. It clings to an earlier time of fearlessness and vague promises that life would improve, pleading to stretch out one final evening, then dissolving into a communal chorus about running headlong until collapse.","sonnet":"Nostalgia for reckless youth pressed up against the reluctant approach of adulthood — the speaker and someone close once lived without fear or consequences, and now that feeling is gone. The refrain of running until they all fall down holds both joy and dread, the night stretched as long as possible before morning and real life finally win.","src":"haiku"},
"i-o-tommy-trash-daisy-guttridge~let-me-go":{"haiku":"Memory crystallized in autumn melancholy; the paradox of being held while being abandoned.","opus":"A wistful dance track anchored in memory, it recalls a love that bloomed from a late-September meeting through winter's warmth, the seasons marking a relationship's arc. The plea not to be let go recurs like a heartbeat when the lights go down. Nostalgia and desire fuse, the narrator clinging to the intimacy of remembered nights.","sonnet":"A relationship anchored in two seasonal memories — red leaves in late September, silver snow in December — where both voices share the same plea: don't let me go. The lyric folds narrator and subject into a single desperate echo.","src":"haiku"},
"i-o~annihilation":{"haiku":"Destructive love promises escape through obliteration; the speaker craves erasure from someone whose harm feels like proof of existence.","opus":"An addiction to a destructive lover mistaken for love itself, the narrator interrogating why being hurt fascinates the other while craving the annihilation only they can deliver. Wedded to her loneliness and loathing it, she keeps returning to the edge where they left her.","sonnet":"A song about an addictive, destructive relationship — the speaker is simultaneously furious at being used and hurt and deeply drawn to the annihilation this person causes in them; loneliness has become so familiar that nothing burns quite like this person does, and the speaker craves the obliteration anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"i-o~body-rock":{"opus":"A stripped-down dance-floor invitation, all pulse and repetition, built purely for physical release. Beyond the call to move bodies together it carries no narrative, functioning as pure kinetic seduction, a mantra urging surrender to rhythm and the shared electricity of the floor.","sonnet":"A minimal dance track whose entire content is an invitation to dance and physical closeness — there is no deeper subject matter beyond the desire to move together.","src":"sonnet"},
"i-o~castles-in-the-sky":{"opus":"A trance-flavored reflection dressed as a rhetorical question, wondering why people chase impossible dreams and build imaginary fortresses. It probes whether anyone truly interrogates their life or simply keeps constructing lofty fantasies, ambition and delusion held together in the same yearning, unanswered breath.","sonnet":"A short, looping meditation on idealism and its fragility — the question 'why do we build castles in the sky' asks why humans construct dreams and expectations that exist only in imagination, out of reach of any earthly foundation. The answer is never given; the asking is the point.","src":"sonnet"},
"i-o~drift-away":{"haiku":"Dissociation and exhaustion; the speaker sleeps through reality despite craving rest.","opus":"The electronic artist captures the numb depletion of modern burnout, where sleeping through the weekend leaves him just as hollow and even feeling has become too much effort. Corked-up anger, low-wage grind and a shortage of hours all dissolve into passive giving-up. Letting go becomes the shared coping method for people too spent and unmended to do anything else.","sonnet":"The exhausted arithmetic of modern burnout — no time to get fixed, not enough hours, so everyone just drifts away. The speaker knows they'd stop numbing if they could, but feeling is too much emotional effort; the drift is chosen because the alternative costs more than is currently available.","src":"haiku"},
"i-o~ghost-in-the-machine":{"haiku":"Someone grapples with fear of falling in love, clinging to fleeting moments while silence and distance creep in. Uncertainty about whether feelings are real repeats throughout.","opus":"Cheery synth-pop surfaces cover a refusal to release a moment that is already slipping. The narrator grips feelings he won't concede are mistaken while a wounding quiet drains the bond away. Under the sweetness lies genuine panic, an admission of falling for someone that keeps surfacing like a wound that won't close, turning the shine into a fear of losing it all.","sonnet":"Falling in love arrives as both wonder and alarm. Caught between nostalgic comfort and a relationship quietly dissolving into silence, the speaker senses something irreversible happening — the feeling unmistakable, the cause uncertain, the fear of loving again louder than the joy.","src":"haiku"},
"i-o~in-my-head-part-i":{"haiku":"Thoughts of an idealized love consume the speaker; they are haunted by what could have been, drowning in obsessive fantasy and regret.","opus":"I O braid a looping confession of obsession, a narrator whose head is full of thoughts of a lost love and times together now full of regrets. As the track shifts into rapped verses, longing curdles into hate ripping him apart, self-esteem gone with her, feeling underwater and dying. Stuck in a midsummer dream that replays every scene, he cannot hit rewind or get her out of his mind.","sonnet":"An obsessive loop of failed love — memory and regret cycling endlessly, a person who broke the speaker apart still colonizing every thought. What began as longing curdles into something angrier: the self-esteem gone, the summer dream of connection rotting, the mind unable to find an exit.","src":"haiku"},
"i-prevail~paranoid":{"haiku":"Mental distress manifests as inescapable paranoia—intrusive thoughts, racing mind, existential dread—where the sufferer cannot distinguish real threat from anxiety's noise.","opus":"A raw portrait of anxiety and intrusive thoughts as literal paranoia, the mind's noise inescapable and impossible to quiet; suicidal ideation surfaces plainly (sometimes just wanting to die) alongside the loneliness of having no one who will listen.","sonnet":"About the experience of severe anxiety and possible suicidal ideation, where the narrator cannot tell if the threatening thoughts and surveillance-like dread are internal or external — trapped in mental noise, unable to explain or escape it.","src":"haiku"},
"i-scintilla~ammunition":{"haiku":"A speaker resists someone's toxic envy, degradation, and cruelty, rejecting their attempts to feed guilt and greed. They reclaim the burning inner fire that destroys their tormentor and refuse to enable further manipulation.","opus":"Aimed at a jealous, spiteful rival whose deceit and greed rebounded on them, the narrator declines to nourish their rage or their remorse, letting their own defeat consume them from the inside. The tone is triumphant taunting, watching an enemy destroyed by their own bitterness.","sonnet":"A narrator turns an enemy's hatred and failure back on them, declaring that the other person's spite and pride are consuming them from within while the narrator refuses to feed their anger or guilt.","src":"haiku"},
"i-scintilla~havestar": {"haiku":"Betrayal and exhausted recovery; the speaker regains self-worth stolen by an unworthy soul. Past trauma darkens skies while new sight and resilience slowly emerge.","opus":"A reckoning with having poured devotion into someone undeserving and deciding to take that investment back. Having gained fresh perspective, the narrator releases the relationship, offers a wry apology for the upheaval caused, and resolves to draw light from within rather than from another person.","sonnet":"A person who poured themselves into someone unworthy recounts discovering their own worth has been spent in vain, while skies darkened and trust was broken. Despite being led astray, they find a defiant resolution—choosing to shine rather than return to the storm.","src":"haiku","fable":"An accounting of a relationship the speaker bankrolled with everything she was worth, addressed to a soul she names undeserving of the toll — and everything hinges on her vowing to regain it rather than mourn it. The imagery keeps two families in tension: his weather of blackened, bruise-colored skies and clung-to wreckage on one side, and the fresh sight she has salvaged on the other, so the wreckage that is all he has left becomes his to keep while she moves out of it. The pivot is the turn where he crawls off into night and, by the same motion, she shines — betrayal converted into the light source the title promises, a star harvested from the storm she is blamed for breaking. Bindrim delivers it less as wound than as verdict, the injured party stepping out of the frame already luminous.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","identity & becoming"],"means":"betrayal converted into the light source the title promises","built":"he crawls into night and by that motion she shines"},
"i-scintilla~melt": {"haiku":"Physical and emotional intimacy creates temporary transcendence, dissolving boundaries and identity in shared vulnerability and sensual connection.","opus":"A sensual, tarot-tinged account of surrender to a lover, two bodies dissolving into each other in the in-between. Its real subject is the fleeting, unspoken truth found in intimacy that cannot last.","sonnet":"An intimate description of physical and emotional merging — two people losing their edges in each other, the sensation tracked through palms, rhythm, and thin sheets — framed as something temporary but profoundly meaningful, a space between permanence and transience where the most real feeling happens.","src":"haiku","fable":"An erotic encounter written as dissolution: Bindrim opens on tarot — the fourth suit-of-cups card reversed, the emblem of turning away from what's offered, bent here into surrender rather than refusal — and lets the imagery slide from divination through the body into liquid, sheets and skin and ink all losing their edges. The whole song turns on the phrase it keeps returning to, that this is not forever but in-between; the refusal to romanticize permanence becomes the point, meaning located precisely in the transient, in what stays unseen. Against that, the chorus is pure structural undoing — losing the way in tightening spirals, matched pulses, unraveling — so the form enacts what the lyric claims, the self breaking free of its own shape. The threefold repetition at the close doesn't develop the thought so much as suspend it, holding the listener inside the melt rather than delivering them out the other side.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","dissociation & numbness","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"meaning located in transience rather than permanence","built":"reversed tarot emblem of refusal bent into opening surrender"},
"i-scintilla~salt-of-stones": {"haiku":"Recovery from violence and inner destruction; the speaker learns violence's language, becomes silent, then rebuilds from ruins to create and destroy again.","opus":"An industrial-tinged confession of self-destruction, tracing someone who has absorbed cruelty until it became his own silence and now performs being fine while quietly falling apart. The recurring pattern is building something fragile only to wreck it, framing self-sabotage as a compulsive ritual he can neither stop nor explain.","sonnet":"Trauma inflicted through violence has taught the speaker only the language of decay. Hollowed by abuse, the hands that once belonged to a self were turned against it; now the only architecture left is ruin — and the bitter plan to build something beautiful enough to destroy.","src":"haiku","fable":"A self-portrait of harm as compulsive craft, sung by a speaker who has no pigment to give her own shape any color or edge — a body felt as unfinished material rather than a self. The song moves through learning: rot studied at the source of harm itself, rehearsed daily until the speaker sings herself into silence, the flat insistence that she is okay laid over a self she has whittled down and a wound she dresses up as something she can still stitch closed. The turn comes when the hands that once belonged to her, ruined by a demon's need, become raw stock — and from the wreckage she assembles an ornate shrine for the sole purpose of leveling it, the whole cycle of breaking and rebuilding revealed as the point rather than a failure of repair. Brittany Bindrim delivers it in the cold, ornamental register that made I:Scintilla's Optics feel like industrial-rock as controlled surface, which is exactly the mask here: the composure is the shrine, and the listener is meant to watch it be razed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"building a shrine expressly in order to raze it","built":"composure as the surface the demolition is performed against"},
"i-scintilla~scin": {"haiku":"The narrator seeks empowerment through detaching from division and shame, seeking inner illumination while resisting despair and self-loathing.","opus":"I:Scintilla, the Chicago electro-industrial act, spin their own name into a spell of self-release. Peeling back concealment, the singer calls for breaking away from paralyzing splits to reach a strength summoned purely from inside, insisting radiance persists amid the gloom. The track recasts darkwave dread as inner transformation, where squarely facing the night dissolves self-disgust into an equilibrium that holds.","sonnet":"A call toward radical self-reinvention through internal illumination rather than external rescue. Shedding the divisions and self-loathing that paralyse, the narrator urges stepping outside habitual identity to discover that power and clarity already exist within. The darkness itself, when faced, turns out to shelter light.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title clips the band's own name down to one syllable — the scintilla, the spark — that lands on the ear as skin, and the song treats that surface as a boundary to be crossed rather than a barrier — every verse is an imperative to peel back the veiled surface and step outside the self, so that light and dark stop being opponents and become the same balance. What sells it is the reversal in the hook: the strength and the clarity are not handed down from above but pulled up out of the body itself, which turns the recurring lit core from an external sun into the self it keeps returning to. The two closing exchanges push this further, one voice asking how deep the reaching can go while the other answers that the depth rides on whatever is doing the shining — a call-and-response staged as if illumination and surrender were finally arguing in one body. In Bindrim's delivery the industrial coldness makes the self-shedding sound less like ecstasy than like a controlled procedure, so the promised transcendence keeps the temperature of a discipline.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming"],"means":"strength pulled from the body not handed down from above","built":"title clips the band's name to a syllable heard as skin"},
"i-scintilla~taken":{"haiku":"Critiques censorship and mass deception, where illusions are used to control and blind populations to the truth. The song warns that those who enforce silence are the real enemies.","opus":"Electro-industrial paranoia about media and censorship, the softer bullets being edited images and managed narratives that keep an audience blind. It warns against illusions that fill you up while quietly bleeding you dry, urging refusal to look away.","sonnet":"A song about censorship and deliberate manipulation of information — those in power are described as editing, hiding, and blurring the truth to maintain control, while the listener is warned not to close their eyes to what is being concealed.","src":"haiku","fable":"I:Scintilla treat media manipulation as a gentler caliber of violence: the curated image is ammunition that wounds while disguising itself as truth, rewound and reloaded until every trace of the original round vanishes. The whole vocabulary here is an editing suite repurposed as an armory — trimming, splicing, burying, smearing footage into one seamless field — so erasing suspicion becomes indistinguishable from fabrication, and anyone who peers too closely gets punished for the scrutiny. The title's verb runs two ways at once: images captured, and audiences captivated, drained by delusions they mistook for reporting. Against that machine-cold assembly the record plants a single command to hold your gaze steady, staking everything on the refusal of the very blindness the censors dress up as protection.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance","politics & society"],"means":"the editing suite repurposed as an armory for erasing suspicion","built":"taken names both images captured and audiences captivated, drained by delusion"},
"i-scintilla~translate": {"haiku":"Regret and unmet expectations poison a relationship as one person holds back honest words and the other walks without conviction.","opus":"The electro-rock group crafts a moody rumination on remorse and words that stayed unspoken, everything boiled down to what the singer wishes he had discarded. His yearning stretches toward people he never encountered, a dread he calls harsher than any regret. Being penalized for staying quiet keeps resurfacing as the injury, and holding onto some last shred of innocence becomes his only shelter as a relationship thins into cold, suspended remove.","sonnet":"Grief for chances surrendered and words left unspoken sits heavier than ordinary regret. The speaker mourns connection never fully made — drawn toward people who remained strangers — while facing punishment for silence and the slow collapse of whatever conviction once held them together.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a breakup/estrangement song built on a paradox: the speaker locates the sharpest dread not in remorse over what happened but in the ache for connections and versions of herself that were shut off before they could ever take shape. Bindrim tracks the collapse across the song's arc, from a partnership once fused tight coming apart thread by thread to an opening that dies the moment it starts, where an early warmth chills into a held, frozen distance. She turns the indictment inward: the punishment lands on the silences she kept rather than any act she committed, which is why the debris she most wants gone is the sentence she swallowed and the union she can no longer keep. The closing plea asks the other to cut through a soft surface that was never freely given and withhold the final measure of malice, reframing surrender as dignity. Stripped of everything else, guarding the one clean thing between them is the only reason she has left, a refusal to let the wreckage turn her cruel in return.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"mourning the paths shut off before they could open","built":"surrender reframed as the one remaining act of dignity"},
"i-see-stars~d4mage-done":{"haiku":"Psychological damage and cyclical trauma trap the speaker in repetitive patterns of disappearance and reappearance.","opus":"The electronicore band casts a poisonous relationship as shared ruin, two people who amount to nothing but wreckage yet still lean on each other to be dragged back into the moment. A wash of blue glow and cerulean fog tints a lover who scrambles the singer's tempo and slips away like an apparition. Craving and doom mingle, the bond both ruinous and impossible to walk away from.","sonnet":"Two people locked in a mutually damaging relationship — \"we're just damage done\" — circle each other like phantom and fog, the speaker asking to be pulled into the present so they can finally stop cycling through the same wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"i-see-stars~eliminator":{"haiku":"Inevitable decline unfolds as chosen path with no escape route, where inaction and movement alike spiral toward predetermined collapse.","opus":"A fatalistic account of a path that never felt chosen, the speaker running ahead of himself toward an end he expects, pain having handed him a voice that now hears only noise. The road is the only road, steep with an unseen drop, and he resigns himself to whatever's coming.","sonnet":"Someone running compulsively forward — \"ahead of myself till I'm dead\" — is trapped on a steep road with no clear end, accepting their fate with a mix of exhaustion and resignation: \"what's meant for me is what's meant for me.\"","src":"haiku"},
"i-see-stars~split":{"haiku":"Psychological fragmentation and cyclical trauma create disconnection where the speaker spirals toward dissolution.","opus":"An electronicore track that circles the dread of commitment and whether any cause deserves the sacrifice it demands. The vocalist keeps asking if he's prepared to shed blood for a decision that can't be undone, feeling on the verge of vanishing while doubting that anything sold as authentic is genuine. The looping structure mirrors that exhausting cycle of second-guessing.","sonnet":"A downward spiral driven by spite and over-seriousness has brought the speaker to the edge of disappearing, questioning whether authenticity can survive in a world that trades in fool's gold — \"where authenticity is sold.\"","src":"haiku"},
"i-the-breather~pr-y":{"haiku":"Predatory violence emerges as justified retribution where the hunter turns the tables on former victors, death becoming final argument.","opus":"A vengeance threat against someone who wronged the singer and a woman, framing retribution as natural selection and the close of an era. The prey-and-pray wordplay turns a promise to tear the enemy's throat out into a demand for his final prayers.","sonnet":"Pure retaliatory threat: someone tried to silence and end the speaker, who responds with a declaration of natural selection — tear your throat out, open your eyes one last time to the rising sun — and demands they say their final prayers.","src":"haiku"},
"i-will-never-be-the-same~skyhunter":{"haiku":"Alien invasion and celestial terror force survivors into desperate flight; the only response to existential threat is to run and hide.","opus":"An alien invasion staged as a survival panic, cities torn open and a couple fleeing into the dark. Beneath the sci-fi, it's about clinging to one person as the only reason to keep fighting when defeat feels certain.","sonnet":"Strangers fall from the sky and tear cities apart, forcing two survivors to flee through darkness toward safety, the ground shaking behind them as they run — urging each other to stay awake and fight for their lives when it seems defeat may already have arrived.","src":"haiku"},
"iamone~catalyst-2-0":{"opus":"A drowning man begging to reach shore and be accepted flips into a cold self-portrait of a ruthless operator who crawls over the people he's killed, yet the one thing that could actually fill his emptiness is a single beloved person he calls his reason to live.","sonnet":"A person driven by desperate longing for acceptance and connection describes themselves as morally compromised — crawling through damage, biting and crushing to get what they want — but the anchor is a specific person who alone justifies their will to live and whom they call their catalyst.","src":"sonnet"},
"iamx~happiness":{"haiku":"The song critiques a society of liars and hypocrites who pursue false happiness while consuming media that fills them with despair. The speaker yearns for authentic happiness amid pervasive deception.","opus":"Chris Corner's IAMX faces depression and disenchantment squarely, its narrator numbly fixed on a screen, resigned that he may never recover yet still reaching for contentment. A steady stream of grim headlines and corrupt leaders fuels his despair, with drugs flickering as a possible way out. The repeated title becomes a chant of longing for a peace kept out of reach by deceit and dread.","sonnet":"Happiness is named and circled but never arrived at — the news delivers death, sobriety feels like losing freedom, the street is full of people performing contentment they do not feel. The speaker watches from outside their own life, unable to accept change, unable to locate the thing they keep naming. The word becomes an accusation.","src":"haiku"},
"iamx~i-come-with-knives":{"haiku":"The speaker acknowledges human complexity, admitting they are capable of both kindness and cruelty, coming with contradictions and agony in how they love.","opus":"Chris Corner opens in dreamy German imagery of a nocturnal, half-mystical journey before switching to a blunt English admission. Over lush electro-goth production, he confesses he offered no vow of tenderness or generosity, warning that his love arrives inseparable from harm. Intimacy is framed as honestly, unavoidably dangerous.","sonnet":"An admission of human limitation dressed in gothic elegance — the German verses summon dream-children and starlight, but the English core confesses that love, for this speaker, arrives inseparably tangled with blades and agony. No open-hearted charity was ever promised, only this difficult, wounding devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"iamx~my-secret-friend":{"haiku":"A companion offers shelter and shared escape through submission and secrecy, promising eternal presence and transformation.","opus":"Chris Corner writes an intimate, coded devotion, water and orgasm signaling both refuge and erotic surrender. A pact with someone who was always a haven, sealed by a willingness to betray the vow you swore hardest to keep.","sonnet":"A secret, intimate bond is offered as total refuge — a river to swim in forever, a shelter through every storm — and the speaker oscillates between giving and receiving that protection. The deepest promise is to break, suggesting the friendship or bond itself demands a kind of self-dissolution.","src":"haiku"},
"iamx~no-maker-made-me":{"haiku":"Someone rejects the illusion of divine design and religious fairytale; they assert autonomy, survival, and sensuality without spiritual creators or moral judgment.","opus":"Chris Corner's defiant atheism against despair: he refuses a benevolent creator and the comfort of fairytales, choosing instead survival on his own terms, where flesh and stubborn intimacy become the only faith worth keeping.","sonnet":"A declaration of godlessness and carnal survival — the narrator rejects any divine origin, refuses to be taken over by religious narratives, and instead finds meaning in physical intimacy and stubborn endurance, reclaiming the label \"sinner\" as a badge of autonomous existence.","src":"haiku"},
"iamx~running":{"haiku":"Solitude becomes necessity; the speaker cannot share their internal struggle, running alone through invisible marathons.","opus":"For all the warmth a lover offers, the narrator insists the essential struggle stays solitary, that comfort and touch can even curdle into regret. The stark truth here is that no one runs your race for you.","sonnet":"Despite warmth and companionship nearby, the speaker is unable to escape the compulsion to face his struggles alone — the comfort of others cannot reach the private race he runs inside himself.","src":"haiku"},
"iamx~song-of-imaginary-beings":{"haiku":"Loneliness births imaginary companions—angels and devils whisper commands to a consciousness fragmenting under isolation.","opus":"A woman told she can't love the way she's supposed to conjures lovers from myth instead, phoenix and devil and siren, each demanding she burn or die for them. Isolation turned into a private, doomed pantheon of desire.","sonnet":"A woman unable to love in the conventional way retreats into imaginary companions — mythic figures like the phoenix, devil, serpent, and siren who demand she burn, lie, beg, and die for them — questioning why belief requires such sacrifice.","src":"haiku"},
"ibaraki~kagutsuchi":{"haiku":"Tyrannical systems collapse under revelation; the speaker burns away false piety to liberate the enslaved, damning the righteous.","opus":"Ihsahn's collaboration with Matt Heafy delivers a scorching indictment of religious tyranny, voiced by rebels who watched the world burn and found freedom in its ashes. The song condemns a tyrant who rules by fear while masking cruelty as divine will, urging the enslaved to renounce the illusion. Black-metal fury frames liberation as the shattering of manipulative faith.","sonnet":"A speaker rejects religious tyranny and the hypocrite god-king who rules through fear: 'you are a slave but bound to a word' — the world burned, villages sacked, the congregation reduced to noughts — and the liberation offered is isolation from manipulation and the watching of the tyrant burn on the stake.","src":"haiku"},
"ic3peak~let-s-die-together":{"haiku":"Romantic death-pact fantasy—two people beneath moonlight pledge mutual destruction, seeking to share pain and merge identities, aware of approaching darkness.","opus":"The Russian duo turns a moonlit suicide pact into a love song, but the real subject is the wish to burn yourself for warmth and drown just to feel chosen; underneath the romance, the escape route always loops back to the self.","sonnet":"A Russian song where two people walk through a sleeping city under a full moon and the narrator earnestly whispers an invitation to die together — not as a metaphor but as a literal shared act — expressing readiness to share the other's pain and drown with them, while also describing running back alone to herself in the dark.","src":"haiku"},
"ic3peak~vampir-feat-oli-sykes-of-bring-me-the-horizon":{"haiku":"Internal darkness consuming and erasing existence; a black void nested within that dissolves everything perpetually.","opus":"The Russian duo joins Oli Sykes for a bilingual descent into emptiness, framing an insatiable inner void as vampiric hunger that consumes everything and can never be filled. Craving blood while wishing for a soul, the narrators inhabit a waking nightmare where hell is present and friends are dead. Self-loathing and appetite fuse into gothic horror as confession.","sonnet":"A black hole inside the speaker devours everything and can never be filled — nightmare without sleep, bloody tears, a vampiric relationship where the other's blood tastes sweet as sugar and their body still smells warm. The English bridge (drinking blood like cherry coke, Lucifer laughing at my broken crucifix) names the spiritual wreckage, Hell real and present, the vampire not a monster but a person hollowed out beyond repair.","src":"haiku"},
"ice-nine-kills~a-grave-mistake":{"haiku":"Revenge crystallizes around betrayal and theft, transforming the speaker into an avenging ghost determined to make the guilty pay in death. Cosmic justice arrives through violent retribution.","opus":"A murdered soul rises as an avenging crow to confront its killer, the horror-metal band's staple of poetic-justice vengeance. No forgiveness offered, only the promise that you meet the one whose life you stole.","sonnet":"A ghost or wronged man returns from death to confront the person who destroyed their life, framing revenge as cosmic inevitability — you reap what you sow — with no forgiveness offered, only a reckoning.","src":"haiku"},
"ice-nine-kills~rainy-day":{"haiku":"Truth is suppressed and hidden while zombified citizens are controlled; survivors must resist manipulation and expose lies before they are silenced.","opus":"A dystopian alarm dressed in videogame imagery, warning that the powerful conceal what people deserve to know while corruption settles in among us. It calls on survivors to reject being turned into obedient soldiers of the controller and to expose the deception before the level ends in defeat.","sonnet":"A call to resist government and media manipulation framed as a survival scenario — the \"truth\" is being hoarded and the population zombified into compliance; the song urges listeners to wake up before the game is over.","src":"haiku"},
"ice-nine-kills~stabbing-in-the-dark-live-in-worcester-ma-november-2019":{"haiku":"Michael Myers embodies unstoppable darkness, a killer who views himself as beyond human morality or authority.","opus":"A theatrical horrorcore band ventriloquizing Michael Myers, turning the Halloween slasher into a first-person anthem about becoming the unkillable boogeyman. It's fan-fiction as metal, gleefully wearing the mask rather than fearing it.","sonnet":"Narrated from the perspective of Michael Myers, the song inhabits the killer's calculating calm as he returns to Haddonfield to stalk and murder, relishing an identity defined entirely by masked violence and an evil that refuses to die.","src":"haiku"},
"ice-nine-kills~thank-god-it-s-friday":{"haiku":"A mother's grief transforms into curse as her drowned son becomes eternal killer, seeking revenge on the negligent and promiscuous at the place of tragedy.","opus":"A retelling of Friday the 13th from the mother's grief and Jason's vengeance, framing the drowned boy's endless killing of careless teens as a curse she unleashes in his name.","sonnet":"The Friday the 13th mythology retold from the inside: Jason, drowned at Crystal Lake through counsellor negligence, is raised by his mother's whisper into an unstoppable killer who punishes teenage debauchery with a machete in one hand and an axe in the other, forever hunting under her command.","src":"haiku"},
"ice-nine-kills~your-number-s-up":{"haiku":"A killer calls a lonely child to play a murderous game inspired by horror films; the victim recognizes the familiar story too late.","opus":"A gleeful slasher homage built from the opening of Scream, staging a killer's threatening phone call to a girl left home alone, dropping the names of famous horror-movie boogeymen and toying with her fate. Ice Nine Kills, the film-obsessed metalcore band who base each song on a specific movie, are in full cinematic tribute mode here.","sonnet":"A scene-for-scene retelling of a slasher film through the killer's perspective — a teenager home alone answers the phone and is walked step by step toward her death by a caller who cites Freddy, Michael, and Jason Voorhees as precedent. The whole thing plays as gleeful meta-horror, name-dropping Drew Barrymore as the killer taunts the girl about the genre conventions she failed to heed.","src":"haiku"},
"iced-earth~birth-of-the-wicked":{"haiku":"Supernatural corruption engineered by elder powers produces a messianic destroyer; prophetic manipulation births doom.","opus":"The opening of the band's Something Wicked mythology, an ancient order engineering the birth of an antichrist figure. It's about fanatics who cloak apocalyptic ambition in destiny and call their monster a chosen one.","sonnet":"A secret order — the Elders and the Men of the Watch — arrange the birth of a chosen child, conceived of dark forces and destined to rise, divide, and bring the end, their prophecy fulfilled on a specific sacred date.","src":"haiku"},
"iced-earth~colors-original-recording-1989":{"haiku":"Urban violence claims another victim as predatory gangs hunt the helpless; survival demands ferocity the speaker cannot muster.","opus":"A raw early gang-violence narrative, a lone rider stalked and killed in the New York subway. The chilling turn is that it's voiced by the attackers themselves, sympathy a word they claim not to know.","sonnet":"A second-person account of a person walking into gang territory in New York City at night who is stalked, attacked, robbed, and killed — a stark, merciless street narrative told from the perspective of the assailants who feel no sympathy.","src":"haiku"},
"iced-earth~dark-saga":{"haiku":"Betrayal and supernatural curse intertwine; resistance and self-determination become the only paths to reclaim agency from imposed darkness.","opus":"Drawn from Spawn, the damned soldier who bargained away his soul for love and awoke a betrayed monster. The real pull is his refusal to stay a slave to that deal, defiance and love warring against his own curse.","sonnet":"A soul damned by a manipulative deal struggles to reclaim its humanity, refusing to become the master's instrument despite having lost everything for love — an assertion of inner goodness against a rigged and cursed fate.","src":"haiku"},
"iced-earth~disciples-of-the-lie":{"haiku":"Clerical abuse and religious hypocrisy perpetuate trauma through predatory authority figures who exploit faith to hide their crimes.","opus":"A survivor's vengeance against an abusive priest, a wolf in sheepskin who preaches salvation while preying on a child, met with a promise to hunt him down and kill him. The rage of the abused turns the man of the cloth into the hunted.","sonnet":"A survivor of clerical abuse confronts the hypocritical priest — wolf in sheepskin, holy predator — who preached salvation while destroying the narrator's childhood, culminating in a vow of violent revenge against the disciples of the lie.","src":"haiku"},
"iced-earth~i-died-for-you":{"haiku":"Unrequited love and impossible sacrifice define existence as the narrator surrenders everything only to watch their beloved find happiness with someone else.","opus":"A ghost's confession that the woman he traded his soul to glimpse is content now, in love with his closest friend, and he cares for them both too deeply to intrude. A sacrifice no one witnesses, grief with no one left to receive it.","sonnet":"A man discovers the woman he loved and sacrificed everything for is now happy with his best friend — the pain is compounded by the fact that he loves them both, leaving him to suffer in silence with a wound that will never close.","src":"haiku"},
"iced-earth~last-december":{"haiku":"Youthful lovers reject adult judgment and choose suicide together; romantic defiance triumphs through self-annihilation.","opus":"A teenage suicide pact, two young lovers convinced their forbidden feeling justifies death when no one will believe it's real. The horror is how the parents' dismissal becomes the exact push over the edge.","sonnet":"Two teenagers, forbidden to be together by their families, choose suicide as the only escape — addressing their parents directly in their final moments, they step off together, insisting their love is real and forever.","src":"haiku"},
"iced-earth~pure-evil":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic violence and demonic warfare consume the world while the speaker celebrates destruction and final reckoning.","opus":"An apocalyptic battle vision voiced by a damned figure who, after seven years reigning in hell, becomes the instrument of a master's vengeance, painting the sky with blood as Babylon's whore arrives. It stages the end of days as a hellish crusade of slaughter.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic battle narrative — cannons and angels, the Whore of Babylon, seven years reigning in desolate hell — painting the sky with blood and calling for the damned to fight in a vision of end-times violence and spiritual reckoning.","src":"haiku"},
"iced-earth~the-coming-curse":{"haiku":"An apocalyptic figure arrives as harbinger; vengeance materializes to punish humanity's collective transgressions.","opus":"The antichrist speaks in his own voice, the culmination of the band's wicked saga, savior to his makers and destroyer to everyone else. It's the seduction of absolute conviction, evil certain it's righteous cleansing.","sonnet":"The antichrist speaks in first person — an ancient watcher who has moved through history toppling kingdoms and devouring souls, now preparing to cleanse the earth of mankind as retribution for crimes against his kind.","src":"haiku"},
"iced-earth~violate":{"haiku":"Demonic depravity and sadistic violence emanate from a twisted creature whose sole purpose is causing agony and destruction.","opus":"Pure horror-movie sadism, a shapeshifting demon-clown reveling in graphic mutilation. There's no message beneath it, just the theatrical relish of a monster narrating its own atrocities to unsettle.","sonnet":"A horror-genre character study of a deranged, demonic figure reveling in torture and killing, presented in visceral first-person as pure menace without redemption.","src":"haiku"},
"iced-earth~watching-over-me":{"haiku":"A dead friend's spirit guides the speaker through decades of grief, manifesting as presence and protection. Angelic surveillance from beyond sustains the bereft through inexplicable loss.","opus":"A eulogy for a childhood best friend killed young in a single terrible night. The mourning narrator becomes certain the dead boy's spirit stays near as a protective angel, steadying him whenever life turns difficult.","sonnet":"A speaker mourns a childhood blood-brother who died young from reckless living, and finds comfort in the persistent feeling that his spirit — like wind, like an angel — is still watching over and guiding them through hard times.","src":"haiku"},
"icon-for-hire~gatekeepers":{"haiku":"Outsiders breach institutional barriers held by gatekeepers; the excluded rise and reclaim what was kept from them through confrontation.","opus":"The rock band rallies the outcasts and dreamers against the gatekeepers who guard the doors and lock out the different. Addressing the beaten-down and fed-up, it promises to break in and claim what's owed, converting shared exhaustion into rebellion. A defiant anthem for the excluded, reframing the label of psycho as a badge for those refusing to fight alone.","sonnet":"A rallying call to outsiders and dreamers who have been kept out: the gatekeepers who guard the doors will end up with a war, because the crowd outside is reckless and desperate enough to rush the barricade like a freight train, not to win politely but to take what they came for.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~a-day-in-the-life-of-a-poolshark":{"haiku":"The speaker cannot engage authentically with things they didn't help create; they seek higher ground and watch others conflate love and hate as interchangeable forces.","opus":"The Washington electro-screamo duo sneer at reassuring cliches, dismissing the idea that character shows in a face or that goodness hides beneath the surface. The speaker admits a grin gets him whatever he wants and that he refuses to take part in anything he had no hand in creating, while the flattening chorus treats affection and loathing as identical.","sonnet":"Disillusionment with received wisdom about beauty, honesty, and belonging. The speaker refuses inherited certainties — that goodness is interior, that eyes reveal truth — and navigates a cynical landscape where love and hate are indistinguishable, waiting out the fallout without much faith in higher ground.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~asylum":{"haiku":"Confinement in a padded cell or mental state causes the speaker to isolate completely and seek asylum rather than attempt escape.","opus":"Slipping between waking and blackout inside a cushioned cell, the speaker claws at the surroundings and, against all logic, pleads to remain confined rather than freed. The duo turn the institution into a haven where no lie helps and no outside voice can reach him, so the title means both the madhouse and the refuge he now prefers to the world.","sonnet":"A mind sealed off from the outside, scratching at padded walls in a haze that never clears. The speaker demands honest engagement from whoever stands at the door but knows they are not getting out — trapped not by force alone but by a blurred inner state that keeps resetting.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~bombs-away":{"haiku":"Destruction falls suddenly and unseen; lovers quarrel as damage unfolds from an ending rope, warned repeatedly to look out below.","opus":"Idiot Pilot recast a couple's fight as an air raid that strikes with no warning, its wreckage set to reveal itself only gradually. The Washington duo, mixing programmed beats with screamed vocals, turn a romance's breakdown into a shouted countdown, a slide with no bottom and a person hanging on at the very limit.","sonnet":"A lovers quarrel that arrives without warning and spreads from every direction like an unseen assault. The damage compounds over time, irreversible and widening. The song holds a tone of grim inevitability — two people running out of rope, the slope continuous, the ending already folded into the beginning.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~cruel-world-enterprise":{"haiku":"The song calls for concentration on making love and reveals deception; predatory threats (wolves showing teeth) emerge against an attempt to resist a stagnant, corrupted world.","opus":"A repeated command to focus on intimacy is set against creeping threat as a betrayal comes to light. The duo braid affection with menace, the aggressors finally showing their true intent while the chant still insists on closeness. The result is disturbing, tenderness and brutality sharing one breath, a poisoned home walked out on rather than tolerated.","sonnet":"Betrayal met with collective refusal. Once deception is uncovered, the group refuses to remain in a stagnant household where predatory wolves have shown their nature — the sardonic refrain of concentrating on making love set against a reality of bared teeth and imminent retaliation.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~elephant":{"haiku":"Unspoken truths and heavy secrets burden people; avoiding confrontation lets larger problems go unaddressed until collapse.","opus":"A meditation on the elephant in the room, the unspoken burden weighing on the soul that conversation tries and fails to expose or disguise. The recurring the elephant dies suggests that only when the avoided truth is finally confronted, however painfully, does the crushing silence break.","sonnet":"A song about the weight of the unsaid in a relationship — the elephant in the room is the enormous unspoken burden between two people, and conversation only exposes how much has been hidden rather than resolving it.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~good-luck":{"haiku":"The speaker questions whether struggle produces meaning or just perpetuates cycles without offering any real hope or progress.","opus":"The duo interrogate the idea that hardship actually propels anyone, wondering where the reason for pushing on has gone when the whole effort is backwards. They caution against trusting to fortune while gambling everything internally, using the pointless task of splitting something apart as a stand-in for labor that arrives nowhere.","sonnet":"Disillusionment with effort as a philosophy. If struggle is supposed to be the engine of hope, the speaker finds the mechanism broken — the cart before the horse, bets placed on outcomes that never arrive. A kind of exhausted scepticism toward the idea that suffering reliably leads anywhere worth going.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~in-record-shape":{"haiku":"Mounting psychological pressure and the pain contained within the body. The speaker describes pressure that combusts, physical decay, and the struggle to maintain composure under stress.","opus":"The duo pair electronics with screamed release to voice a mind buckling under strain, unsure of its own soundness as harsh, closing-in lights press down. A repeated denial that this burden is even that heavy plays like self-talk struggling to cut an unbearable weight down to size, the narrator fighting to hold himself together as everything ignites.","sonnet":"Mental and physical disintegration under invisible pressure — not any single catastrophic blow but a slow accretion of force that cannot be split or lifted. The spinning lights, the shredding teeth, the choking: the body registers what the mind cannot yet name, while the speaker insists on maintaining some form.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~last-chance":{"haiku":"Someone contemplates mortality and earthly purpose, questioning whether humans deserve punishment and exploring acceptance of fate.","opus":"An anxious meditation on the wish to come first and the self-defeating suspicion that people wreck what they cherish, half-resigned to deserving a grim end. Idiot Pilot were a young Washington duo splicing screamo with electronica, and the jittery programming and fragile melody carry that early-2000s sense of existential unease.","sonnet":"A song caught between apathy and last-minute conviction — the speaker questions whether anything is genuinely good, suspects that love and hatred are closer than they appear, and rides a fence of comfortable indifference until the possibility of a final chance tips the balance toward something, even if it is only floating upward. The stance is neither hopeful nor defeated, just restlessly unresolved.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~les-lumieres":{"haiku":"Waking into brightness repeats; consciousness returns again and again to illumination.","opus":"Idiot Pilot fused electronic textures with emo intensity in the mid-2000s, often building tracks around one obsessive image rather than a full narrative, and this is that method distilled. The speaker keeps waking to find the lights still on, the single repeated picture suggesting disorientation and an inability to leave consciousness behind. The looping image carries the whole weight — sleeplessness rendered as an inescapable brightness that will not switch off.","sonnet":"A speaker repeatedly wakes to find the lights still on, the single image suggesting disorientation or an inability to leave consciousness behind.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~losing-color":{"haiku":"Someone has lost their creative spark and emotional richness; a relationship or person once vivid and inspiring now feels lifeless and gone.","opus":"Idiot Pilot open with studio banter before mourning a fading world drained of color, addressing a lost muse who was once the narrator's canvas. The duo let the pen hitting paper mark the vanishing of brighter, shared days, creation itself becoming an act of loss rather than making.","sonnet":"A relationship that was the source of all creative vitality has ended, and with it the impulse to make anything. The other person was the canvas; without them the colors drain away. What remains is a blank page, a pen still meeting paper out of habit, and the days of laughter already in the past tense.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~mammoth":{"haiku":"A person grapples with manipulation and emotional abuse in a relationship, realizing they're no longer needed and must sever the unhealthy connection.","opus":"A young narrator performing indifference toward someone whose cruelty once mattered, insisting the elephant in the room can finally die. The confession leaking through the bravado shows the wound isn't actually closed.","sonnet":"A song about cutting off someone who has become irrelevant and unkind — the speaker declares they are finally fine without this person, that the metaphorical elephant (the thing between them) can now die, and refuses to be troubled further.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~murderous":{"haiku":"Paranoid surveillance and hidden motives drive mutual distrust and taboo desire. Voyeurism and terror intertwine as unspoken threats.","opus":"Idiot Pilot lace a seduction with surveillance dread: a laid table, a train, and camera-eyes that have the narrator watching a lover whose taboo motives he can't read. Under the ritual calm sits the sense that beneath the manners everyone is just a creature in the wood.","sonnet":"A creeping, oblique song about surveillance and predatory desire — someone is being watched through camera eyes, their taboo motivations dissected, and the whole encounter has the feel of a trap being carefully laid in a rural or isolated setting.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~only-so-much":{"haiku":"One person demands space from another who takes up all available room and energy. Irrational patterns repeat; the speaker can't change and won't stay.","opus":"Idiot Pilot map the edge of a person's endurance, a narrator who can bear only so much and struggles to own up to his own unreasonable behavior. His unwillingness to face someone masks an unwillingness to give up what he needs or to accept what can't be altered. The strain of caring for someone who crowded him out leaves him circling the same immovable truth.","sonnet":"A relationship that has consumed all available oxygen — the other person filling every inch of the shared space until the speaker has no room to breathe. Exhausted and trapped by the inability to change what is intolerable, withdrawal is the only remaining gesture, however imperfect and incomplete.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~open-register":{"haiku":"Internal conflict over containing explosive emotion and the urge toward violence. The speaker struggles against an impulse to discharge harm while restraining others from motion.","opus":"Idiot Pilot voice a robbery from inside the gunman's head, pleading with his hostage to stay still so he isn't forced to fire. The reassurance that he means well jars against the barely-leashed violence, and his claim that anyone would react the same way makes the menace unsettlingly ordinary.","sonnet":"A robbery in progress rendered from the inside — the robber narrating their own barely-controlled tension, urging stillness on everyone in the room while their eardrums pound and the finger hovers. The irony is that the robber and the robbed are each begging the other to stay calm.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~planted-in-the-dark":{"haiku":"A hidden, creeping intrusion and the desire to breach or destroy. The speaker whispers as a force and plans to strip away what another has grown or built.","opus":"Idiot Pilot build a paranoid fragment around a message received and passed furtively through back streets and gaps in the walls. The narrator discovers something festering beneath his skin and worries at it until he bleeds, wondering if the whole thing was a scheme to hide something under the surface. Half-finished phone calls and a menacing threat against a person likened to a tree leave the dread unresolved.","sonnet":"A cryptic long-distance communication — a message received and passed along through gaps and cracks, something buried under the skin that itches until it bleeds. The speaker holds power as the wind to another person rooted in place: a tree that can be stripped of every leaf.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~red-museum":{"haiku":"An unsettling surreal narrative of trespass and transformation in a strange red museum where residents are merged into singular entities and red sky dominates, trapping visitors who cannot escape the intrusion.","opus":"An eerie, unsettling scene of intruders slipping through a window past a sleeping baby into a silent museum-like place, with a refrain about people becoming one and being unable to stop them from coming in.","sonnet":"A disquieting scene of intrusion — an unseen figure enters through an open window near a sleeping baby — set in a cold, silent place where individuals are absorbed into an undifferentiated collective and an unstoppable force cannot be held back.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~retina-and-the-sky":{"haiku":"Apathy and acceptance of stagnation coexist; the speaker watches futility from a distance while light gradually takes over.","opus":"Idiot Pilot wander a gilded coast of doubt, pressing forward simply because inertia rules, yet catching sight of brighter hours ahead. Calm settles in oddly beside a violent car crash. The young electro-rock duo thread listlessness with dread, wondering where anyone lands once a blinding radiance swallows the whole scene.","sonnet":"Drifting forward on apathy that paradoxically feels like peace — no destination, no urgency, just the growing brightness ahead that somehow promises something better than anything known before. The windshield smashes and the day brightens anyway. Momentum without direction still arrives somewhere, and wherever that is turns out to be enough.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~saboteur":{"haiku":"Accuses someone of inconsistency and internal sabotage, shifting allegiance based on convenience. Their fall from grace is inevitable.","opus":"A kiss-off to a fickle turncoat whose loyalty flips with the weather, someone the singer no longer bothers challenging because their betrayal is both predictable and beneath notice. Idiot Pilot, the Washington screamo-electronica duo, ride their trademark scream-and-croon over jittery programmed beats, treating the whole feud as a low-stakes skirmish not worth fighting.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone whose loyalty shifts with the day and whose silence and games have already cost them the room — the speaker addresses them directly, unimpressed, noting the fall from grace was rapid and that nothing they do will change things now. The word saboteur carries equal parts accusation and dismissal.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~sideways":{"haiku":"Emotional dysfunction and manipulation in a dying relationship. The speaker faces the funeral of connection with someone playing twisted games while they remain critical and detached.","opus":"A venomous farewell dressed in mock-apology, the narrator acknowledging his own foul moods and reflexive nastiness before turning the knife on someone he's finished with, an unstable manipulator forever running head games. He stages a symbolic burial for them, treating their frantic outbursts as something close to a crime.","sonnet":"A stalemate with someone who has checked out emotionally and turns every interaction into a game. The speaker is self-deprecating about their own dark mood but clear-eyed about the other person — sideways, evasive, playing mind games in their prime while a slow funeral accumulates around them both.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~silver-needle":{"haiku":"The song questions whether people can agree that everyone has inherent beauty and worth; it urges speakers to pronounce and express what they know rather than hide.","opus":"An unusually soft turn for the duo, questioning whether comfort built on cheap positivity holds up while gently insisting that being ordinary or unremarkable is nothing to hide. It nudges the listener to speak honestly and value themselves, landing as an earnest, almost tender plea against making life harder than it needs to be, rare warmth in an otherwise abrasive catalogue.","sonnet":"A gentle argument for self-acceptance and the courage of honest expression. Nobody needs to be large or exceptional to matter — smallness is part of identity, goodness is available to those who seek it, and what is spoken aloud carries its own quiet power worth pronouncing.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~spark-plug":{"haiku":"Fortunes collapse across dimensions as the speaker invites chaos and violent collision. Election becomes spectral force driving toward mutual annihilation without retreat.","opus":"The electronic-rock duo builds a disorienting, fractured soundscape where fortunes run out and pursuit turns menacing, images of razors and pretension slipping past coherence. The track resists literal reading, its paranoia and collapse suggesting depletion, control, and dissolution. Meaning arrives through texture and dread more than narrative, a portrait of a mind fragmenting under pressure it can't articulate.","sonnet":"A fragmentary, almost disintegrating lyric where fortunes collapse and the speaker follows someone down through static and election-rhetoric noise — the bracketed in-studio directions and half-audible lines read as a song about slipping out of coherence itself, the mind shedding its ability to hold a thought.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~sparkplug":{"haiku":"Authority figures inflict pain and control through intimidation, demanding silence and obedience, using violence to extract information and eliminate resistance.","opus":"A menacing fantasy of interrogation and torture wrapped in cultish, quasi-religious language, promising to hunt down and tie down a victim to show them what pain is about while soothing them into compliance ('you'll be just fine'); biblical imagery (lake of fire, water and wine, nails to the hand) frames it as a nightmarish inquisition or coercive cult.","sonnet":"A dark authoritarian voice interrogating and detaining someone — threats of torture, controlled information, strange religious violence imagery — reads as a critique of coercive power structures that demand compliance and punish anyone who resists or asks questions.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~the-pushover":{"haiku":"A chronic people-pleaser losing themselves in constant accommodation of others' demands, ultimately willing to self-destruct if it makes others happy.","opus":"Idiot Pilot voice a study in trained obedience, a narrator who has lost the ability to refuse anyone and finds a distorted contentment in being instructed how to eat, move, and rest. He calls the condition shameful and unhealthy yet stays because a bit of tenderness once soothed his hurts, even offering to disappear or end himself if it eases the other person.","sonnet":"Compulsive people-pleasing described with unflinching clarity — the speaker cannot refuse, cannot assert, finds a strange comfort in being directed, and knows this is disordered. What breaks the pattern is not self-reclamation but a partner who kisses the wounds without exploiting them.","src":"haiku"},
"idiot-pilot~theme-from-the-pit":{"haiku":"Exhausted options and failed choices leave the heart damaged; all possibilities remain unmet and unfulfilled substitutions.","opus":"The electronic-screamo duo circle a heart that has run out of moves, each choice presenting itself yet none serving as a true swap for another. Emotional depletion becomes a claustrophobic loop as the narrator questions why he keeps chasing a connection he already knows will wound him, trapped by an illusion of endless choice.","sonnet":"Every option has been exhausted and each one stares back — infinite possibilities rendered as a kind of paralysis. The heart surrenders not to a replacement but to the affair itself, knowing it will cause pain. Deliberate self-undoing, eyes open, choosing the path most likely to wound because the alternatives have already run out.","src":"haiku"},
"idles~samaritans":{"haiku":"Toxic masculinity damages all who internalise its imperatives; the narrator rejects stoic silence and claims vulnerability as strength and authenticity.","opus":"A frontal assault on the script boys are handed, the clipped commands to man up and never cry exposed as a mask that ends up wearing the wearer. Joe Talbot's answer is defiant tenderness, permission to feel.","sonnet":"Toxic masculinity is a performance that destroys the men it claims to define — the commands to suppress emotion, drink, lie, and be hard are indicted as the reason fathers become strangers to their children, and the speaker asserts that feeling things is not weakness but realness.","src":"haiku"},
"idles~war":{"haiku":"The song protests war by onomatopoetically depicting its violence, warning that children are sent to fight for lies while questioning who bears responsibility for their deaths.","opus":"A ferocious antiwar eruption assembled from vocalized sound effects, imitating blades, gunfire, drone strikes and shrieking children before ordering ordinary people off to combat while turning its aim on the profiteering liars who send them, everyone bound for damnation. Idles are the Bristol post-punk band defined by confrontational politics and communal fury, and this is that spirit at its most pummeling, sloganeering rage against warmongers.","sonnet":"An anti-war song that enacts war's sounds before condemning them — swords, guns, drone buttons, and the hush of taken children rendered as onomatopoeia before the lyric turns to name the pattern directly: ordinary people sent to die for stone-faced liars, all of it ending in hell. The contradiction of war and anti-war is held in the same breath without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"if-found~alive":{"haiku":"Existence felt only through implosion—the speaker crawls toward selfhood and reason for being, finding life in the act of shattering.","opus":"The metalcore band locates a paradoxical vitality in breakdown, its narrator crawling toward nothing yet feeling alive precisely while crumbling. Haunted by feelings and sinking, he treats collapse as the price of breathing again and searching for another self. Pain becomes proof of existence, the act of falling apart reframed as the only way to genuinely feel.","sonnet":"A person crawling through breakdown discovers that crumbling down still produces a flicker of being alive; the hole growing on the heart is both wound and passage, and breaking down again becomes the recurring cost of searching for a reason to stay.","src":"haiku"},
"if-i-may~the-game":{"haiku":"Describes existence as fragile and cyclical, maintaining facades while harboring internal sickness that prevents escape. Winning requires playing along.","opus":"A melodic-metalcore song about being trapped in a punishing routine you're forced to keep performing, the narrator wanting to disappear while still defiantly crying out, questioning who anyone is to promise things will improve when his own troubled mind offers no exit. Everything circles back to him, and the recurring taunt about a game underscores the sense of inescapable futility as despair and catharsis trade off.","sonnet":"A relationship at the point of collapse where the speaker cannot decide whether to stay or go, and doubts anyone's reassurances that things will improve. Life feels like broken glass underfoot, the irony being that both people scream their feelings without being heard, and the only way forward is to keep playing a game neither truly wants to play.","src":"haiku"},
"if-i-were-you~ghost":{"haiku":"The speaker haunts their own afterlife, hollow and hollow still, oscillating between suffocating rage and seductive comfort in the darkness they chose and cannot escape.","opus":"The band renders dissociation as a haunting, its narrator waking somewhere unknown, gone empty and numb, drifting through walls in an afterlife he chose. Screams go unheard, rage goes unspent, and he half-prefers this hollow other world to the one he left. Beneath the ghost metaphor sits a desperate wish to disappear, unsure whether this is death or a grim new beginning.","sonnet":"A speaker discovers they have become a ghost — hollow, unheard, haunting walls in a chosen afterlife — and oscillates between preferring the emptiness and crying for one second of feeling alive, the eternal sleep both sentence and uncertain new beginning.","src":"haiku"},
"if-not-for-me~everything-you-wanted":{"haiku":"Betrayal and theft extract a permanent toll as the speaker demands accountability while facing the permanence of what's been taken, unable to recover or move forward.","opus":"Confronting a liar who took everything and left only unspoken words and haunted memory. The refrain's bitter question, whether they got everything they wanted, is a plea to finally be released from someone who justified their cruelty with lies.","sonnet":"A one-sided reckoning after betrayal: the speaker, haunted by lies and an unblinking dishonesty they never knew before, demands to be let go and asks whether the person who took everything they had got what they wanted in the end.","src":"haiku"},
"iglooghost~clear-tamei":{"haiku":"Transparent or translucent form, repetition of nonsense syllables framing body transformation or ethereal presence.","opus":"The producer sings entirely in a made-up, sound-rich language conjuring a transparent being and a drifting figure moving through a translucent form. Known for maximalist invented mythologies, he treats words as raw texture here, letting rhythm and sonics carry the feeling where plain meaning falls away.","sonnet":"A constructed mythology sung in a wholly invented language. A figure named Tamei — associated with transparency and a see-through body — is summoned or addressed through incantation. The emotional register is devotional and urgent, conjuring a world with its own cosmology where the Xao Void Mogu presides.","src":"haiku"},
"ignea~alexandria":{"haiku":"Imperial conquest and sensual obliteration merge as a conqueror destroys a great city through sheer will and desire; power becomes synonymous with desecration.","opus":"The voice of a conqueror razing the great city and its fabled library, and the Ukrainian band writes it as erotic domination, destruction confused with possession. Power that mistakes ruin for love, blessing for prey.","sonnet":"A god-like conqueror speaks to the ancient city of Alexandria in possessive, ecstatic terms, taking pleasure in her destruction brick by brick and bone by bone, asserting absolute dominion with no law or lord to answer to.","src":"haiku"},
"ignea~nomad-s-luck":{"haiku":"Perpetual movement defies temporal threat through embracing risk and displacement, the nomad celebrating survival despite knowing time remains unconquerable.","opus":"A restless wanderer chases peril across deserts, poisonous jungles, and disease-ridden trains, living countless existences and gambling with every fragile step. The one adversary they cannot outmaneuver or charm is time itself, so they take the gamble and grin.","sonnet":"A nomad catalogues extreme physical risks — viper eaters, malarial trains, toxic plants, breaking taboos — accepting each dangerous venture with a smile, because time is the only danger that cannot be outrun or defied.","src":"haiku"},
"igorrr~parpaing":{"haiku":"Building grotesque monuments to death through corpses, obsessed with necromancy and the power of decay.","opus":"French avant-metal built on pure grand-guignol excess, a butcher of corpses reveling in death for its own theatrical sake. The horror is the point and the joke at once, gore pushed past meaning into absurdist ritual.","sonnet":"A grotesque speaker builds a house from corpses and rotting flesh — gleefully liturgical about the process, commanding that cadavers be brought so they can be melted into the foundations of something obscene and immortal; it is body-horror as architecture.","src":"haiku"},
"igorrr~pavor-nocturnus":{"haiku":"Nocturnal terror and dread dominate; fragmented vocal patterns evoke existential or bodily fear through sound itself.","opus":"Igorrr sets wordless, umlaut-laden vocables against a title meaning night terror, the syllables functioning as percussive incantation rather than statement. The French avant-metal project, notorious for splicing breakcore, baroque and extremity, weaponizes nonsense phonetics to summon dread purely through sound and repetition.","sonnet":"Fragmentary glossolalia that enacts rather than describes nocturnal dread — the Latin title names it sleep terror. The invented syllables loop and mutate like a fever half-remembered on waking. No translatable argument is made; the form itself is the content, staging disorientation as pure sound.","src":"haiku"},
"igorrr~the-shape-of-punk-to-come":{"haiku":"Freedom ignites through youth rebellion and the physicality of performance, celebrating those who've been beaten down and emerge to reclaim power through noise and motion.","opus":"A joyful summons to riot, offering freedom, wrecked instruments, and burning hair to a bruised generation that's been told to submit. The lifted taunt of I told you so cheers a scene that has finally gotten dressed up with a destination in mind.","sonnet":"An adrenaline-charged invitation to ride music as liberation and rebellion, addressed to someone who's been underground and beaten down; the 'soul train to destination unknown' and 'smashed guitars like you've seen on TV' frame punk energy as both escape and communal uprising.","src":"haiku"},
"ihsahn~south-winds":{"haiku":"Rejecting religious dogma and fear-based morality that sacrifices authentic life. The speaker chooses honest sin over sanctified oppression and collective delusion.","opus":"A ferocious attack on religious dogma, the narrator declaring he'd sooner be damned than let zealots turn everyone into puppets and casualties of belief. He commands the faithful to stop groveling and abandon their false hope, cancelling any promised paradise. Ihsahn, once of Norwegian black-metal band Emperor, brings his progressive solo craft here, marrying cerebral heresy to intricate, theatrical extremity.","sonnet":"A furious rejection of religious submission — the speaker watches people surrender their identities to dogmatic faith, hammering themselves with invented sins, and declares they would rather embrace sin and fall than let fanatics turn humanity into martyrs and fools. The promised land is cancelled, hope dried up in the desert; dignity demands you get off your knees.","src":"haiku"},
"iiso~salad-days":{"haiku":"Ruined patterns repeat; the subject hides emotions until abandonment forces reckoning.","opus":"A young person's self-sabotage in love, used to ruining things and hiding feelings by listening to their own noisy mind. The ache is that they only realized how much someone mattered after that person left, and thereby left their thoughts too.","sonnet":"A speaker tangled in self-sabotage and emotional suppression realises that hiding their feelings has made them lonely, and that the other person they needed has already left their mind — maybe that departure was itself the moment of realisation.","src":"haiku"},
"iiso~signs":{"haiku":"Obsession crystallizes the beloved as messiah figure, where salvation emerges through possession. The speaker surrenders will to become devotee in a cult of one.","opus":"The Korean artist frames infatuation as devotion bordering on worship, casting the beloved as a savior and messiah whose traces he wants to possess entirely. Promising an eternal, believe-in-anything paradise, the track blurs romance and salvation. Its dreamy hedonism suggests a love that functions like faith, the other person becoming both the sign and the reason to keep singing.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses someone they see as a destined messiah figure — 'for messiah I never lie' — insisting they came looking for a savior like them every day, that they'll never forget this place, and declaring 'you're my signs' as a statement of devotional certainty that this person is a guiding signal.","src":"haiku"},
"ikari~synthetic":{"haiku":"Systemic exploitation and numbed complicity create a feedback loop of trauma and aesthetic suffering, where truth becomes disposable and transcendence arrives only through collective collapse.","opus":"Contempt for a false, hollow bond and for people who numb themselves with pills and deliberate blindness, imagining ignorance absolves their wrongs. The narrator waits, cold, for their comeuppance, promising a reckoning that will make them answer for it.","sonnet":"Rage at wilful ignorance and trauma aesthetics: the speaker accuses someone of turning a blind eye and believing their own ignorance is bliss, calling the bond between them synthetic and pathetic while a ticking countdown promises that time will come for all of them.","src":"haiku"},
"ikd-sj~a-sphere-consists-of-24-components":{"haiku":"Fragmentation repeats cyclically as components dissolve into nothing while the world erodes into mockery. The speaker observes collapse without redemptive meaning emerging.","opus":"A dissociative, glitching meditation where a self made of twenty-four fragile parts tries to hold together and survive another round. Amid frozen blues and ash, the numbing loop of the world being over and nothing able to change us reads as resignation dressed as a lullaby.","sonnet":"A fragmentary, semi-abstract drift through grief and dissolution — the self freezing blue, turning to grey ash scattered in the wind, the world ending over and over — the twenty-four components of the sphere suggesting a wholeness that keeps breaking apart and cannot be reassembled.","src":"haiku"},
"ikd-sj~beautiful-slaughter":{"haiku":"Tragedy and blood-soaked meaning intersect with desperate pleas and running toward aesthetic cruelty; the speaker embraces slaughter.","opus":"IKD SJ render a fractured, translated grief where a treasured someone is bid goodbye amid what the narrator names as fated tragedy. Amid pleas to be filled and called, sorrow and self-blame spiral toward willing slaughter, the disjointed phrasing itself mirroring a mind coming apart under loss.","sonnet":"Fractured English that circles around tragedy, guilt, and a plea for contact. The speaker moves through a grim cityscape feeling morbid and unclean, asking someone to call, to come, to fill the void. Beauty and violence are collapsed into each other — the slaughter is somehow also an aesthetic, and apology runs through it all.","src":"haiku"},
"ilan-bluestone~will-we-remain-spencer-brown-remix":{"haiku":"Commitment persists despite emotional exhaustion, with the couple enduring through storms as proof of resilience rather than passion.","opus":"A relationship at the tipping point, one person masking fading feelings behind a smile while an oncoming storm figures the reckoning both sense. The trance context turns that uncertainty into a held, aching suspension.","sonnet":"A relationship is weathering something overwhelming — the speaker doubts and fears, masks the pain with a smile, yet arrives at a hard-won conviction that they are strong enough to remain despite the storm bearing down on them.","src":"haiku"},
"ilaria-graziano~a-1xbrbr":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness manifests as monochromatic perception where connection dissolves and the self fragments across digital isolation.","opus":"Ilaria Graziano is best known for her collaborations with Yoko Kanno on anime scores steeped in questions of digital consciousness, and this song lives in that world. A cyber ocean regards a person who has lost the ability to feel color, taught neither emotion nor true connection, and asks whether they can feel, see, and become something beyond the monochrome. The appeal is to discover the full spectrum — numbness addressed as a condition that might yet be reversed.","sonnet":"The cyber ocean sees a person who has lost the ability to feel colour, taught neither emotions nor true connection; the song asks whether they can feel, see, and become something beyond the monochrome — an appeal to discover the full spectrum the ocean offers.","src":"haiku"},
"ilaria-graziano~where-does-this-ocean-go":{"haiku":"Urban alienation and the search for meaning in an isolated existence. The speaker observes the city's displaced people while questioning where life's flow actually leads.","opus":"Ilaria Graziano, a voice familiar from Yoko Kanno's anime scores, paints a bustling city in vignettes: a wino sipping slowly beside a sleeping cat, a woman selling flowers for her daughter, a melon-headed showman ducking into an alley. Amid the neon-lit electric buildings, the narrator keeps asking the unanswered question, her life an island wondering where this ocean goes, restlessness beneath the city's teeming life.","sonnet":"A solitary figure drifts through a city full of people each caught in their own small orbits — a wino, a flower seller, a street showman — while asking the one question none of them can answer. Life feels like an island, and the ocean surrounding it leads nowhere mappable.","src":"haiku"},
"ill-ni-o~how-can-i-live":{"haiku":"The song explores inherited trauma and identity collapse. A mirror-image inheritor cannot separate from the stain they carry; life becomes a guessing game of living within inherited pain.","opus":"A raw address to an absent parent, the narrator seeing himself as a duplicate carrying the same inherited flaw, shaped into who he is yet uncertain of his own selfhood, and lost without the figure who left. Bilingual lines heighten the confusion over why their two lives run parallel. Ill Niño, the American nu-metal band with Latin percussion, root this study of inheritance and desertion in that rhythmic, dual-language intensity.","sonnet":"A child reckoning with a parent's absence — mirror image of someone who left, carrying the same stain, making the same mistakes, unsure of their own identity except as a copy of the person who walked away. The Spanish lines underscore the disorientation; the question how can I live without you is less romantic plea than existential bewilderment at what it means to exist in someone's shadow after they're gone.","src":"haiku"},
"ill-ni-o~what-comes-around":{"haiku":"Hatred and cosmic justice: the narrator detests a false, shape-shifting person and warns that karmic consequence inevitably returns.","opus":"A poisonous portrait of a toxic bond, adoration and disgust hurled in the same breath as the narrator spits at a phony, conformist target and vows that genuine deeds circle back. Snatches of Spanish about life belonging to another twist the knife. Ill Nino, the New Jersey group who fused Latin percussion with nu-metal, power this reckoning with rhythmic, bilingual force.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who drove the speaker away through arrogance and constant judgment — the hatred is tangled with love and loss, and what propels everything is the certainty that reality catches up eventually. The Spanish passages sharpen the accusation: your life is mine to carry, and you will never understand the damage you did.","src":"haiku"},
"ill-nino~what-comes-around":{"haiku":"Retribution cycles inevitable as consequences return to source through karmic law. What was given becomes what is received in circular justice.","opus":"The Latin-metal band channels the rage of someone crushed by another's judgment and hypocrisy, insisting that everything real comes back around. The narrator loves and hates in the same breath, sickened by a person forever performing perfection to fit in. Bilingual verses sharpen the betrayal into a karmic promise: the day of reckoning belongs to the one who was blamed.","sonnet":"Festering contempt for someone who drove the narrator from home with their performative perfection — \"your stupid face just makes me sick,\" watching them change clique by clique — and the karma refrain: everything real comes around, \"tu vida es mia,\" their fear lives inside the narrator now.","src":"haiku"},
"illenium~shivering-feat-spiritbox":{"haiku":"Frozen abandonment persists as haunting presence; the speaker becomes memory that chills whenever recalled, eternally shattered.","opus":"The electronic producer teams with Courtney LaPlante for a chilling portrait of lingering as an ex's cold, frozen memory. The narrator haunts the corners of a former lover's heart, a sickness in the mind that can't bleed while frozen, forever shivering as the other moves on. Melodic bass and metalcore vocals fuse heartbreak with something closer to a beautiful curse.","sonnet":"A frozen memory lodged in a former lover's heart: the speaker is 'a sickness inside of your mind,' burnt alive until replaced, now haunting as cold as the wandering love that discarded them — 'can you feel me shivering?' directed at someone who has moved on but cannot fully excise the ghost.","src":"haiku"},
"illuminati-hotties~superiority-complex-big-noise":{"haiku":"Performative taste and hollow credibility; masks of competence hiding jealousy beneath surface.","opus":"The indie project skewers industry gatekeeping and social climbing with sardonic wit, needling a hanger-on with a superior ear and calling out the frenemies and debts of scene politics. Big noise versus decoy becomes shorthand for authenticity against posturing. Sarah Tudzin's playful venom exposes the hollow status games of people who mistake taste for talent.","sonnet":"A music-industry satire aimed at gatekeepers who make a performance of taste — good ear brah, never seen you with a drink before — the speaker, now owing seven stacks and not even getting a copyright, calls the whole apparatus a decoy and dares the cowboy to ask for the real noise instead of the performance of it.","src":"haiku"},
"illuminati-hotties~threatening-each-other-re-capitalism":{"haiku":"Youth consumes itself through caffeine-fuelled bonding that masks depression, while commercial systems profit from the illusion of authentic connection and freedom.","opus":"Sarah Tudzin frames anxiety and burnout as symptoms of a system that bottles and sells you your own needs. The joke and the ache are the same: knowing you're being scammed and buying in because you can't stop.","sonnet":"Two people navigate anxious intimacy and social exhaustion while the song draws parallels between their relationship's slow collapse and the absurdity of consumer capitalism — both involve buying into something that extracts more than it gives, and the joke is that they can't stop either.","src":"haiku"},
"illyria~frostbite":{"haiku":"Heartbreak yields only regret and numbness as two people destroy each other through blame and emotional walls, locked in cycles of departure and recrimination until finally walking away.","opus":"A relationship gone cold and cursed, where forgiveness is gone and the only warmth left is the blade and the frostbite of separation. It is about the numb, painful decision to finally walk away after grieving too long.","sonnet":"A relationship fractures in mutual exhaustion, the speaker oscillating between wanting to leave and begging to stay — \"turn around now and let me stay\" — while the cold of frostbite and numb pain stands in for emotions too raw to process directly.","src":"haiku"},
"illy~heard-it-all":{"haiku":"Originality suffers under repetition and cultural saturation; the narrator grapples with creating unique work in a world where all ideas are derivatives.","opus":"The Australian rapper wrestles with originality in a world where everything's been said, conceding no idea is new while insisting a fresh perspective still counts. An artist making peace with being one voice among billions.","sonnet":"The rapper reckons honestly with originality's limits — every idea has been done, every rhyme has a precedent — yet argues that a distinct perspective and loyalty to truth still give an artist something worth saying, and ultimately the only thing that matters is the fans.","src":"haiku"},
"ils~feed-the-addiction":{"haiku":"Burning-hot lyrical aggression and drug dependency merge; the speaker spits fire and seeks to feed an addiction.","opus":"A minimal breakbeat cut where the hook makes the addiction the music itself, the craving for the beat and the rush. The rapped boast about fiery, hieroglyphic flows is fuel for a hunger that only the track satisfies.","sonnet":"Essentially a minimal rap boast — spitting fire, burning police, hieroglyphic flows — there is no developed theme beyond the rapper asserting technical skill and rebellious energy.","src":"haiku"},
"imagine-dragons~believer":{"haiku":"Suffering forges faith and resilience; pain becomes the crucible from which personal transformation and strength emerge.","opus":"Dan Reynolds converts a life of hurt, illness and struggle into the engine of his conviction, insisting the pain is exactly what made him. A defiant reframing of suffering as the source of drive rather than a wound.","sonnet":"Pain is reframed as the generative force behind the speaker's entire identity: broken from youth, he turned suffering into art and resilience, and addresses the person or force that hurt him with gratitude — not for the pain itself, but for what it made him, a believer in his own power to endure.","src":"haiku"},
"imagine-dragons~natural":{"haiku":"Survival in a brutal world demands ruthlessness and emotional armor, becoming predator rather than prey through calculated coldness and moral compromise.","opus":"A hardening manifesto for surviving a cutthroat world by turning your heart to stone and choosing to be predator rather than prey.","sonnet":"Survival in a world that demands you become \"a beating heart of stone\" — cold, cutthroat, the hunter rather than the prey — the speaker accepts the price while sensing the end approaching, swearing an oath in blood that they will make it through.","src":"haiku"},
"imagine-dragons~radioactive":{"haiku":"Waking from numbness into apocalyptic clarity, the speaker finds himself transformed into a glowing instrument of upheaval; survival itself becomes a radioactive act of rebellion.","opus":"An apocalypse used as metaphor for personal reinvention, waking in the ash of an old self and welcoming a new age. Destruction as liberation, the revolution imagined mostly as one man's system finally blowing open.","sonnet":"A speaker awakens into a post-apocalyptic new order, charged with an internal energy that feels both destructive and revelatory, declaring himself transformed and aligned with a radical, dangerous new age.","src":"haiku"},
"imagine-dragons~whatever-it-takes":{"haiku":"Ambition and adrenaline override caution; the speaker pursues transcendence by breaking chains, willing to sacrifice anything for transformation.","opus":"Naked, restless ambition laid bare, the fear of being ordinary driving a hunger that borders on self-destruction. It admits the ego and the emptiness even as it chases the top, hooked on the adrenaline of the climb.","sonnet":"A relentless self-driven ambition to rise, framed through a persona that thrives on pressure and refuses the pull toward mediocrity, even while acknowledging the psychological cost — the fear of being typical, the bodily harm of pushing too hard. The song oscillates between ego and vulnerability, presenting both as fuel for the same drive.","src":"haiku"},
"imanu~sutekh":{"haiku":"A usurper seizes power, declaring total dominion and dismissing the predecessor's pleas for restraint.","opus":"A god of chaos and violence gloating over a rival's fallen kingdom, taunting even the resurrected dead who dare reclaim what's now his. It weaponizes mythic megalomania as the emotional payload of the drop.","sonnet":"Voiced from the perspective of Set, the Egyptian god of chaos and disorder, the song is a declaration of violent usurpation — the speaker despises his brother (Osiris), claims dominion over the desert and sky, and revels in the fact that his kingdom-destroying chaos cannot be undone even by resurrection.","src":"haiku"},
"immerse~23-19":{"haiku":"Humanity trapped in emptiness and complacency, corrupted by false friends and forced into meaningless existence, fading without redemption.","opus":"This metalcore track diagnoses a cursed, purposeless existence, drifters smothered and erased who mask their real selves behind the facades they perform. It calls for excising the rot and purging the toxins within, warns that evil disguises itself as friendship, and asks whether people have become hollow containers for shattered ambitions, stranded and dissolving inside a reality that was never theirs.","sonnet":"Existential drift — wandering without direction, hiding behind performed identities, waiting to fade — is diagnosed as collective suffering rather than individual failure. The prescription is brutal self-surgery: cut the corruption, rip out the poison. But the emptiness beneath may be structural, not curable, and the fading feels close and certain.","src":"haiku"},
"immerse~with-you":{"opus":"A devoted vow of steadfast support to someone who's scared and fighting a hard battle, promising to stand by their side through anything and not let their inner flame go out.","sonnet":"A devotional vow of unconditional loyalty to someone facing serious hardship: the narrator admits they owe this person everything, lives in fear of losing them, and pledges to stand by them as long as blood flows — their shared flame is what cannot be broken.","src":"sonnet"},
"imminence~desolation":{"haiku":"Desolation rains indiscriminately; the speaker searches for light in relentless darkness, aware that survival itself feels like mercy extended.","opus":"Fighting to stay alive under a relentless downpour of despair, pleading for mercy while feeling darkness swallow everything. The self-soothing count to ten and the vow that this is not over wrestle against becoming heartless, insisting no relief comes without sacrifice.","sonnet":"Trapped in a consuming darkness with blood on their hands and no catharsis, the speaker pleads for desolation to have mercy — \"searching for a light in the dead of night\" — holding breath and counting to ten in the faint hope it is not the end.","src":"haiku"},
"imminence~erase":{"haiku":"Drowning in overwhelming pressure and trauma, desperately seeking oblivion and relief from internal darkness.","opus":"Depression rendered as drowning, the weight of a chain you can't break or bear to face. The plea to wipe out the dark thoughts and let the light back in is the whole aching motion of someone begging to be dragged back toward living.","sonnet":"A speaker drowning in compulsive, suffocating thoughts begs for someone to show them how to chase the dark away — the weight of what cannot be escaped presses in like flood water, and the plea to turn the light on is both desperate and persistent.","src":"haiku"},
"imminence~jaded":{"haiku":"Spiritual deterioration calcifies into emotional deadening where the narrator exists as name without substance, redemption becoming impossible.","opus":"Feeling trapped, jaded and faded, unsure whether this cage of a life is who he was ever meant to be. The refrain about becoming a name suggests a self worn down to mere reputation, spinning out of control and pleading to learn how to repair a heart this numb.","sonnet":"Trapped in a cage of fate and cancer-like despair, the speaker's heart has been worn down to jade — faded, spinning out of control, unable to find a way back — and the repeated question of how to mend such a heart remains unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"imminence~to-the-light-japanese-bonus-track":{"haiku":"A person with buried secrets and a dead heart bleeds out, running out of time and asking others to pull them toward light and guidance through isolated darkness.","opus":"Imminence carry the weight of the world buried under the skin, bleeding out while begging to be pulled toward light. The Swedish band, distinctive for weaving violin into metalcore, make the plea a confession of dependence, unable to face the dark alone and asking only to be seen within it.","sonnet":"Isolation at its limit — a person carrying buried secrets and a heart long broken finally admits they cannot survive alone. The bleeding-out is both literal and emotional, the darkness total. The only motion possible is toward someone else, a hand to guide through the night before all the lights go out.","src":"haiku"},
"immortal-girlfriend~seeker":{"haiku":"A person watches their partner manipulate and betray them repeatedly, losing certainty about their connection and feeling used.","opus":"Immortal Girlfriend track the instant a partner sees clean through you, treating you like something transparent and starting to reshape their life without hesitation. The synth-pop act pairs that dread with a dissolving enchantment, the narrator sure of nothing anymore as vows crumble and the leaving already feels done.","sonnet":"A relationship defined by invisibility and mistranslation — one person sees through the other rather than seeing them. Trust collapses, promises break, and the spell holding the dynamic together shatters the moment distance is introduced. The speaker emerges from uncertainty concentrated, no longer waiting for recognition that never came.","src":"haiku"},
"imonolith~hollow":{"opus":"A rallying cry to command your own mind by binding your scattered thoughts together, refusing the dread and self-loathing you keep serving yourself and facing down the adversary inside. Clinging to the worthier portion of your self-respect is what stops you from being drained into emptiness.","sonnet":"A rallying call to resist fear, self-destructive thought patterns, and internal enemies — the argument is that pride and unity of purpose prevent a person from becoming hollow, while hatred and doubt tear them apart.","src":"sonnet"},
"imonolith~instinct":{"haiku":"Breaking free from imposed reality and mind control; the speaker rejects forced beliefs to reclaim agency and design their own existence.","opus":"A song about breaking out of a manufactured reality, waking each day into private hell and rejecting the beliefs he was handed, then resolving to author his own path as fear gives way to resolve. Imonolith are a heavy-rock supergroup drawn from Devin Townsend's band and Threat Signal, delivering modern groove-metal built around a rousing chorus of self-liberation.","sonnet":"A declaration of self-reclamation after a life of being told what to believe and who to be — the speaker wakes from an existential nightmare, names the force-fed reality around them as lies and deceit, and resolves to break free by killing the conditioned self inside and designing their own existence. The arc is from trapped and faithless to fearless and autonomous.","src":"haiku"},
"impale~ghost":{"haiku":"Internal trauma and unresolved truth haunt like a ghost in the mind, demanding the speaker face what they've repressed or face eternal curse.","opus":"The haunting is voiced by the fear itself, a repressed truth that promises to return worse until faced. It reframes the ghost as the unavoidable inner reckoning you keep lying to yourself to avoid, insisting on being heard.","sonnet":"About unresolved psychological trauma or guilt that haunts the narrator like a ghost — the thing you suppress always returns worse, and the only way to end the haunting is to stop lying to yourself and face it directly.","src":"haiku"},
"imparfait~a-l-am-ricaine":{"haiku":"Chasing American ideals of freedom and power while paying physical costs for the performance.","opus":"A young French woman ventriloquizing the American myth she was raised on by Disney and gun culture, and finding that chasing that shiny badass ideal costs her pieces of herself. The candy-colored surface hides real mutilation.","sonnet":"A French song about internalizing American pop culture's impossible female ideal — the narrator compares herself to a Disney doll, plays \"bang bang\" beauty games she didn't choose, and lost something (a finger) in the pursuit of being the most badass. It's about how absorbed cultural scripts cost you pieces of yourself.","src":"haiku"},
"imparfait~glitch-i":{"haiku":"Watching a deceptive persona collapse publicly while struggling with complicity in the chaos.","opus":"Facing the moment the mask slips and the monster you couldn't see turns out to be your own reflection. The cheerful sayonara is denial dressed as farewell, a person saying goodbye to their crumbling self-image rather than admit collapse.","sonnet":"A French song about self-deception and the moment when the mask slips — the narrator and an addressee both tell themselves lies to avoid facing something; the crowd, the moshpit, the masquerade all collapse; the monster wasn't seen until it was too late, and the goodbye (sayonara) arrives with the world possibly ending.","src":"haiku"},
"imparfait~la-f-te-est-finie":{"haiku":"Mental exhaustion erodes the will to engage; the party ends when everyone's minds splinter and sanity becomes a collective delusion nobody questions.","opus":"A burnt-out refusal to keep performing sanity in a scene that's exhausted its meaning—the party is over, everyone's minds are unraveling, and the narrator openly admits she isn't sound of mind while asking who actually is. Rage has gone cold and clear, done offering her body to basement nights.","sonnet":"A French-language song about collective social exhaustion and the end of the illusion of sanity — the speaker is out of hatred and energy, has nothing but contempt for stupid ideas, and questions whether the party of civilized pretense is truly over, while people around her spiral and she admits to being mentally unwell, then asks who isn't.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is a question, not a lament, and the song answers it by turning a private burnout into a refusal held in common. The narrator's rage has gone cold and clarifying: she keeps more contempt than hatred for other people's garbage ideas, no energy to fake serenity, and she concedes she isn't sane before asking who is, aiming that diagnosis outward at everyone. The turn arrives when the wrung-out I becomes a we that refuses the basement party and stops offering up bodies to the night. In place of that scene they sit up and put the world to rights instead, so the party ending reads not as defeat but as consent withdrawn, cold rage curdled into the clarity to talk something better into being.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape"],"means":"burnout clarifying into consent collectively withdrawn","built":"the title's question mark answered by the song enacting it"},
"imparfait~on-crie-encore-l-am-ricaine":{"haiku":"Pity and dignity collide as the speaker refuses to lower their voice even when marginalized, crying out against systems designed to silence while reclaiming authenticity through refusal.","opus":"A defiant sisterhood refusing to lower its head, screaming even when it won't be welcomed, invoking the cries of women from Katanga. Freedom costs dearly among vipers, and the speaker chooses to stand alone in the game rather than fall in line.","sonnet":"A French-language critique of cancel culture and the brutalizing logic of public call-out: the speaker refuses to bow her head under the mob's pressure, draws power from her sisters' voices, and keeps screaming even knowing it won't be liked — playing the same violent American-style game of public destruction while trying to stay true.","src":"haiku"},
"imperial-circus-dead-decadence~a-35hy":{"haiku":"Humanity's futile struggle against inevitable suffering; despite learning and growth, people remain enslaved by internal contradiction and can only find meaning by enduring pain rather than escaping it.","opus":"Imperial Circus Dead Decadence are a Japanese symphonic-extreme-metal act notorious for sprawling, maximalist epics that lurch between brutality and beauty, and this track is exactly that scale. It surveys the cyclical futility of human conflict — people born into sin, slaughtering each other under the delusion of justice, never truly understanding one another — yet a persistent voice insists even tiny lives burn intensely. The narrator vows to keep singing that life, carnage and sorrow included, is worth loving.","sonnet":"A sprawling, semi-epic on the cyclical futility of human conflict — humans are born into sin, slaughter each other endlessly under the delusion of justice, and can never truly understand one another — yet a persistent voice insists that even tiny lives burn intensely, and the narrator vows to keep singing that life, with all its carnage and sorrow, is worth loving.","src":"haiku"},
"in-deception~epidemic":{"haiku":"Mental reset sought but never achieved, past redefined and undone yet preventing redemption as dissonance breathes through fractured consciousness.","opus":"Corporate labor rendered as dystopian horror: an interchangeable worker who could die tomorrow and be swapped out within a week, feeding a machine that never sees him. Pushed past his limit, he demands ownership of a life that was never his — exploitation figured as a spreading plague.","sonnet":"The workplace as exploitation machine — blood, sweat, and tears extracted by a spineless system that would forget you in a week — breeds a breaking point where the speaker stops obliging and names the whole arrangement a worldwide epidemic spreading faster than anyone can contain.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~albtraum":{"haiku":"Confessional nightmare about murdering an elderly aunt for inheritance. Hands bloodied and conscience sold, guilt is denied as the speaker describes the gruesome act without remorse.","opus":"A macabre German tale in which the narrator confesses to slaughtering his old, frail aunt for the gold and papers hidden in her chests, hands soaked in blood and conscience given away. A refrain scolds that one simply doesn't do such things, and he blames it all on a nightmare.","sonnet":"The narrator murders an elderly, frail aunt in her sleep after finding gold and documents in her belongings, disposes of her body in a cellar, and dismisses guilt entirely—ending with the disclaimer that he cannot be held responsible for this nightmare. The song is deliberately grotesque and black-humoured.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~ave-maria":{"haiku":"A Latin liturgical prayer repeating Ave Maria (Hail Mary), depicting the Annunciation where the angel Gabriel addresses Mary.","opus":"German medieval-folk-metal band In Extremo revive a centuries-old Latin devotional, the Annunciation retold as the angel Gabriel, sent from heaven with clear face, greets the solitary Virgin and tells her she will conceive. The repeated Ave Maria and call to sing together carry the archaic text on driving electric power, ancient liturgy and modern amplification fused into the band's signature blend of the sacred and the heavy.","sonnet":"A medieval-Latin hymn to the Annunciation — Gabriel arrives in radiance to address the lone virgin, and all present are summoned to join the acclamation. The liturgical call-and-response renders the moment of divine address as communal song, ancient and solemn.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~davert-tanz":{"haiku":"Those joining the path gain life; youth enables joy and freedom.","opus":"The German medieval-folk-metal band offers a brief, celebratory call to the road, promising that those who journey together gain something for life. Framed around the joy of youth and companionship, this short traditional-flavored piece is less narrative than invitation, urging revelry and shared movement while the dancers are still young enough to seize it.","sonnet":"A brief, joyful invitation to the road: whoever travels with us gains something for life, and we should rejoice while we are still young.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~erdbeermund":{"haiku":"Passionate, consuming desire for a lover, with their mouth (strawberry-mouth) as the object of desperate longing and intimate devotion.","opus":"A medieval-flavored German setting of a Villon poem, raw carnal longing for a lover cast as an old street dog aching for a strawberry mouth. It is desire stripped of romance, hungry and unashamed.","sonnet":"A raw expression of obsessive physical longing — the narrator aches for a woman's strawberry-red mouth and white body, has spent years without her in tears and wandering, and returns to the strawberry valley where she kept herself for him, their reunion both earthy and ecstatic.","src":"haiku","fable":"In Extremo bolt Paul Zech’s 1930 poem — a Villon-style invention Zech published under the French poet’s name — onto bagpipe-and-metal, and the whole song lives inside one word: the strawberry-mouth (Erdbeermund) as the fruit of a body eaten alive by wanting. The verses track that fruit across a landscape: first the clover makes a bed for the first night and the singer wants to be a star-groom in the deep valley, appetite still in its blooming season. Then the register drops — the days without her are a sea of tears, he traded away his loveliest summers, and the wide strawberry-valley opens only to show the old street-dog what he once was, so the ripeness curdles into a picture of ruin and lost youth. The closing verse fuses the poles the song has been circling: I am so wild after your strawberry-mouth becomes I am so lustful (geil), and the valley and the ash-ground (Aschegrund) sit in the same breath, so the strawberry is at once the reddest living mouth and the burnt-out end of the man chasing it. That is the thesis the structure earns — desire that names its own hunger as a thing already halfway to ash, sung not as regret but as a howl that keeps howling anyway.","fv":2,"fnote":"The text is a celebrated forgery of sorts: Paul Zech published it in 1931 among his Villon adaptations, but it is his own invention in Villon’s style — and Klaus Kinski’s recitals made it immortal, down to titling his autobiography after the line In Extremo howls.","themes":["love & desire","nostalgia & memory","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"desire howling at a fruit already halfway to ash","built":"three verse registers earn the fused ash-and-lust closing breath"},
"in-extremo~flaschenpost":{"haiku":"A lover sends coded desires and crude passion via a message in a bottle, framing physical lust and control as gifts to be consumed.","opus":"The German medieval-rock band, known for pairing bagpipes with bawdy folk tradition, casts a message in a bottle across the sea. What the ink confesses is not love but lust and rough desire, the speaker owning his coarse appetites without shame. The bottle drifts full of greed toward its reader, a ribald sea-shanty conceit typical of the group's earthy, tavern-minded reworking of old forms.","sonnet":"A ribald message-in-a-bottle dispatched across the sea conceals not a love letter but an explicit proposition. The sender delights in the gap between expectation and content — the receiver anticipates romance, finds appetite instead, and the bottle drifts onward greedy and unashamed.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~frei-zu-sein":{"haiku":"True freedom requires minimal possessions and fearless independence; the bold thief becomes king by refusing shame and seizing opportunity.","opus":"The German folk-metal band celebrates freedom over crowns and palaces, the speaker a rogue at home anywhere he sleeps. The refrain insists only the free are truly kings, a shameless thief the smith of his own fortune. Steeped in medieval outlaw balladry, it romanticises the vagabond who takes what others merely dream of, wealth measured not in jewels but in belonging to no master.","sonnet":"A wanderer celebrates freedom as its own sovereignty. Without castle or crown, every place becomes home — the shameless rover who takes what others only dream of at night is the true king, because liberty requires nothing more than the will to seize one's own fortune.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~horizont":{"haiku":"A man haunted by repeated betrayals and broken promises in a relationship reaches a breaking point when he loses his partner. At the horizon where sea meets sky, he waits for them, consumed by regret over the harm he caused and the futility of redemption.","opus":"A remorseful speaker tallies a thousand betrayals, lies, and broken promises, realizing only after losing his love what he has done and reaping the storm he sowed. He longs to wait for her where the horizon meets the sea, hoping she will find him there.","sonnet":"A man confesses to a long pattern of lying, hurting, and suppressing his feelings toward someone he loved, and only in the moment of losing that person does he fully grasp the damage he caused. He imagines waiting at the horizon where sea meets sky, hoping to be found, but recognises the regret has come too late.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~k-ss-mich":{"haiku":"A person is obsessively consumed by someone—knowing their habits, movements, and thoughts—and believes only a kiss from them can save their hovering spirit.","opus":"An obsessive stalker addressing his fixation: he can't eat or sleep, knows her every habit, and vows to creep through keyholes and walls to burrow into her soul and body. The pleading refrain to be kissed frames a possessive, predatory craving as if it were salvation.","sonnet":"An obsessive, borderline-stalker infatuation: the narrator knows every detail about the object of desire — their habits, sleep, scent — and crawls on all fours just to be saved by a single kiss.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~liam":{"haiku":"A woman grieves the loss of her beloved Liam to the sea, sailing and calling out to him in her heartbreak over his drowning at sea.","opus":"The German medieval-folk-metal band sings in Irish Gaelic, telling of a woman whose beloved Liam is lost to the sea, the storm breaking the waves as she vows undying love. Setting out in her father's boat to search, she meets a captain who confirms the waves tore him overboard, her bitter weeping dissolving into the same ocean that took him, a stark maritime lament of grief.","sonnet":"An Irish-language lament follows a woman who loses her beloved to the sea and then borrows her father's boat to sail in search of him, beseeching the gods. Fate takes pity, and somewhere out on the water she finally finds a ship. The refrain — Liam, I am always with you, I love the sea, I will come to you soon — is her vow held across the grief.","src":"haiku","fable":"The Gaelic here is not an excavated traditional but In Extremo passing as one — an original written in German and carried into Irish by guest Rea Garvey, a sea-widow lament in a borrowed tongue that keeps the grief at the distance of a language the audience cannot follow. The verses tell a drowning — a storm breaks the waves, her love is taken over the side, the sea carries him off — and she questions the captain whether he is among the crew, but the emotional engine is the refusal buried in the chorus. She addresses the drowned Liam to swear she is always beside him, and in the same breath says she loves the sea that killed him, promising she will be with him soon. Read straight that is devotion; read as the mask it is, the love of the sea and the vow to join him without delay are a widow's suicide dressed as faithfulness, so the song's tenderness and its death-wish are the same sentence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["suicide","death & grief","love & desire"],"means":"devotion to the dead phrased so it doubles as a death-wish","built":"the borrowed tongue keeps grief at the distance of a language the audience cannot follow"},
"in-extremo~madre-deus":{"haiku":"Religious invocation combining Latin and Portuguese fragments addressing Mary and Christ's incarnation and power, mixing sacred and colloquial language.","opus":"In Extremo, the German medieval-metal troupe who set old texts to bagpipes and distortion, revive a Galician-Portuguese Marian cantiga here. The words hail the mother whose son becomes flesh, inherits his father's power to rule the world, and appears in wrath, while the faithful beg her aid in this hour. Devotion and awe braid together as the ancient verse is answered by its own German gloss.","sonnet":"A medieval Galician-Portuguese prayer addressed to the Mother of God flows alongside German verse: she who bore the son of the Father watches as that son descends into flesh to govern the world with his father's power, while terrified children beg her intercession in this hour.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~mein-sehnen":{"haiku":"Yearning becomes action as trees fall and water rises to build a boat toward an unknown destination. The song portrays longing as consuming sacrifice.","opus":"A German folk-metal lament in which the narrator fells a sacred tree with tears to build a boat and reach a beloved, confessing his false pride was a lie and begging the night wind to carry him where forest and sea meet, a black tear feeding an eternal cruel triumph. In Extremo are the German medieval-metal band who blend bagpipes and hurdy-gurdy with heavy guitars, and this is their yearning, ballad-like storytelling in that tradition.","sonnet":"A German-language song of longing and sacrifice — the speaker fells a sacred tree from a holy forest, weeping, and builds a boat from the wood in order to cross the sea and reach someone beloved. The black tear running from the stump feeds a cruel triumph, but the night wind finally carries the speaker to a place where forest and ocean meet, fulfilling the desire that drove the whole journey.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~merseburger-zauberspr-che":{"haiku":"Vagabonds living in chaotic freedom on society's margins, celebrated and condemned equally.","opus":"A German medieval-folk band setting the actual Merseburg Charms, pagan Germanic incantations for freeing captives from their bonds. The pull is the deep past made loud, old magic words sung as if they still work.","sonnet":"In German and Old High German, the band introduces themselves as seven vagabond wanderers known as In Extremo — ragged, bell-hung, storm-laughing figures celebrated and spat on across the land — before chanting the First Merseburg Charm, an ancient Germanic spell in which the Idisi goddesses free battle-bound warriors. The song fuses medieval outlaw identity with pre-Christian magical invocation.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~merseburger-zaubersprüche":{"haiku":"A magical incantation summons freedom and release from bonds, invoking ancient forces of liberation.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~neues-gl-ck":{"haiku":"A wanderer leaves home seeking new happiness and adventure, traveling without destination, trusting hope and rejecting doubt as they journey outward.","opus":"Fitting for a band forged in the touring, festival-circuit life, this is a hymn to setting out. The singer leaves everything behind, carrying only what he needs, certain today is his day. New towns and new faces keep the wheel turning; he watches clouds drift and longs to fly off like a bird, doubt banished, happiness finally within reach on the open road.","sonnet":"A wanderer sheds every settled obligation and strikes out toward an open horizon, trusting motion itself as fortune. New towns, unfamiliar faces, the ever-turning wheel — he finds happiness not in any destination but in the simple, unburdened act of leaving home.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~nur-ihr-allein":{"haiku":"Vagabonds and outcasts invite only the bold to join their lawless life outside society. They steal from the wealthy at night while celebrating freedom on their own terms.","opus":"A rowdy vagabonds' anthem sung from the perspective of thieving minstrels who feast, share stolen loot, resent servitude and steal from the rich by night, inviting listeners to flee their tidy lives and roam with them, both revered and spat upon. In Extremo, the German medieval-folk-metal troupe, deliver it with their trademark bagpipe-and-guitar swagger, a rogue's celebration of the outsider that quietly nods to old German verse in its closing lines.","sonnet":"A medieval-flavored ode to the wandering life — a band of vagabonds feasts until dawn, steals, schemes, and lives by its own rules, inviting listeners to flee their ordinary lives and join the caravan. They are simultaneously revered and spat upon, the sundial metaphor capturing their philosophy: count only the bright hours, ignore the rest.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~nymphenzeit":{"haiku":"Spring brings nymphs and beauty along the Rhine—birds sing, flowers bloom, and women dance in erotic abandon under the season's sweet spell.","opus":"A medieval-styled celebration of springtime on the Rhine, where singing birds, blossoms, and dancing nymphs stir sweet, sensual longing; the Latin refrain ('sweet love in springtime, standing beneath the flowering tree') anchors it as a pastoral hymn to seasonal desire.","sonnet":"A medieval-style German song celebrating springtime and sensual pleasure — nymphs appear along the Rhine in May, women recline in circles, sweet fragrances fill the air, and the Latin refrain invokes the sweetness of love in the season of flowering trees.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~omnia-sol-temperat":{"haiku":"The spring sun restores renewal and awakens hearts toward love and desire, requesting faithful devotion and wholehearted commitment. The song celebrates seasonal rebirth and romantic fidelity.","opus":"In Extremo set a medieval Latin lyric from the Carmina Burana, where spring's sun renews the world and a youthful god commands lovers to rejoice. The German medieval-rock band pair the call for faithful, wholehearted love with their signature bagpipes and distortion, reviving a centuries-old plea for constancy.","sonnet":"A medieval Latin love lyric set to music — spring purifies and opens the world, the god of love drives the noble soul toward desire, and the speaker asks to be loved faithfully with the whole heart and mind. Renewal is inseparable from erotic longing; the season and the emotion are one.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~pferdesegen":{"haiku":"An Old High German charm commanding parasitic worms to exit a body through progressive stages. It's a ritual invocation for healing or magical cleansing.","opus":"The German band mines their signature medieval sources, setting an actual Old High German horse-healing charm to metal. The words command a worm and its nine offspring to crawl out through marrow, blood, flesh, hide and hoof, then onto an arrow to be shot away. Less a song than a resurrected incantation, it showcases the group's scholarly appetite for reviving genuine archaic ritual language.","sonnet":"An ancient Germanic healing incantation commands a worm or parasite to travel outward through tissue — from marrow into vein, flesh, skin, and finally onto an arrow to be shot away. The layered Old Saxon and Middle High German verses enact expulsion as ritual cure.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~rasend-herz":{"haiku":"A passionate, racing heart desires to seize destiny and defy fate itself, reaching for forbidden dreams and willing to steal fortune through raw emotional force.","opus":"A medieval-folk-metal surge of a racing heart clenched into a fist, defying fate and vowing to steal happiness back from destiny. Old visions and mountain-storm imagery frame raw, ungovernable passion as a raid against a life that withholds joy.","sonnet":"A fierce declaration that a racing, clenched heart will command a raid on fate itself — the narrator worships the storm, steals luck from destiny, and pursues visions that must never be allowed to fade.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~saengerkrieg":{"haiku":"Bardic conflict eternal; singers battle like medieval poets in perpetual artistic struggle.","opus":"The German medieval-folk-metal band turns the legendary Wartburg minstrels' contest into a manifesto about their own defiant longevity. Bards have squabbled for centuries, but true nobility lies in solidarity and refusing to bow to rivals or trends. It frames the group as a band that will never kneel, celebrating endurance over the hollow crowning of a single victor.","sonnet":"Bardic rivalry reframed as timeless human vanity: just as Walter von der Vogelweide and Neidhart feuded eight hundred years ago, musicians still claw at one another, the supposed winner always found bloated at the bottom — only solidarity keeps the band's own song alive, and a true In Extremo never kneels.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~sagrada-trobar":{"haiku":"A troubadour sings of God's sacred offering of flesh and redemption, promising eternal inheritance and relief from death's suffering for faithful believers.","opus":"A medieval Marian devotional (an old Galician cantiga with a German translation) in which the minstrel vows to become the troubadour of Our Lady, praising Mary as the one through whom God took mortal flesh to grant humanity the inheritance of eternal life and a place in his kingdom, forever freed from the sufferings of death.","sonnet":"A medieval Galician-Portuguese troubadour lyric (with German translation) in praise of the Virgin Mary — the narrator pledges to be her minstrel, honoring the woman through whom God took flesh, who offers eternal life and spares the faithful from the suffering of death.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~sefardim":{"haiku":"A retelling of Abraham's birth prophecy: King Nimrod's astrologers see a holy light over the Jewish city foretelling the birth of Abraham; his mother conceals her pregnancy and gives birth in a cave.","opus":"The German folk-metal band revives a Sephardic Ladino ballad recounting the birth of the patriarch Abraham, King Nimrod glimpsing a holy light over the Jewish quarter foretelling his arrival. Terah's wife, pale and secretly aware of the treasure she carries, walks through fields and vineyards to a cave where she gives birth, the refrain honoring Abraham as beloved father across centuries.","sonnet":"A Sephardic folk song in Ladino traces the birth of Abraham — King Nimrod reading the stars and sensing a holy light over the Jewish quarter, the mother of Terah carrying the secret of her pregnancy, wandering through vineyards until she finds a cave to give birth. The refrain names Abraham as beloved father, the ancestor whose arrival the heavens foretold.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~sieben-k-che":{"haiku":"A skilled head chef produces a royal feast alone, while many subordinate cooks eventually succeed when they coordinate and work together. The song illustrates leadership and collaborative capacity.","opus":"In Extremo spin a mock-heroic German fable of a master chef and his scrambling kitchen boys, seven cooks stirring one pot of porridge. The band turn the proverb about too many cooks into a boisterous feast-song, its moral aged like fine wine, inviting listeners to the raucous table.","sonnet":"A comic fable about group dynamics in a kitchen. A lone chef boasts he can do single-handedly what normally requires a brigade; the chorus delivers the proverb about too many cooks spoiling the broth while praising the one who swings the ladle with conviction. Office politics and collective inefficiency are gently mocked throughout.","src":"haiku"},
"in-extremo~wind":{"haiku":"The wind becomes a metaphorical force representing freedom, desire, and identity; the song evokes primal sensuality and embracing natural forces rather than societal constraints.","opus":"The wind personified as an untamable force of desire that knows the singer's true self and carries him toward lust and sin, likened to the Fall. Beneath the pagan imagery is an ode to surrendering to erotic, elemental passion, becoming one with love by the fire against winter's death.","sonnet":"The wind knows the speaker's true nature and drives them toward desire; the song celebrates erotic surrender, the nightingale's wild song of sin, and the pleasure of merging with a lover in firelight as winter storms rage outside.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~a-new-dawn":{"haiku":"Grief and loss lead to a moment of clarity and determination. A person stands alone, refuses to lie, and waits to see dawn—transformed by the death they imagined.","opus":"In Flames surface from a nightmare of a loved one dying into resolute isolation, fragile and afraid yet swearing to see the trial through to its end. The Swedish melodic-death pioneers wed melody to grit, aching for daybreak as proof of survival. Lost yet with the way ahead unblocked, the narrator sheds his grief to become something finer.","sonnet":"A dream of loss becomes a turning point — waking frightened but resolving to stand alone and move forward without lies. The road ahead is crooked, the directions unknown, but daybreak feels genuinely anticipated. Grief at the imagined absence of someone beloved crystallizes into a fierce, imperfect claim to become something better.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~all-for-me":{"haiku":"A man endures internal struggle and burning intensity for someone else's sake. He refuses to be held back by constraints or emotional bonds, vowing to find his own power and freedom.","opus":"Melodic-death conflict between self-sacrifice and self-preservation, burning yourself up for someone while sensing the futility. The turn toward refusing to serve anymore is the song finally choosing its own survival over martyrdom.","sonnet":"About someone grinding themselves down in service to another person and realizing that the burning they feel is their own self being consumed — the chains are breaking as they finally decide to stop serving and reclaim what's left of themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~come-clarity":{"haiku":"A person seeks guidance to escape their dreamlike existence and find meaning beyond innocent childish perspectives.","opus":"A cry for lucidity and rescue from a numbed, dreamlike drift as the narrator races through his thirties, aging by the hour. His plea for someone to guide him elsewhere, and his hope that seeing life differently might set everything right, frame a wish to quit merely dreaming and actually live.","sonnet":"A plea to be led out of a dreamlike, static life — the narrator has been existing rather than living, and asks someone or something to take them somewhere real, somewhere clarity might finally arrive.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~crawl-through-knives":{"haiku":"A speaker suffers willingly for someone they need, willing to endure extreme pain and self-destruction for transformation. They claim their fate is in their hands and will burn toward heaven.","opus":"The Swedish melodic-death veterans stage devotion as bloody ordeal, the speaker vowing agonizing self-sacrifice for someone he needs while using dread as fuel to push himself forward. Fire imagery recurs as personal agency, his fate held in his own hands, fusing surrender and self-determination in the arena-scaled sound the band grew into.","sonnet":"Desperate devotion at the cost of self-injury. The speaker is paralyzed by need and bleeding from it, yet insists the choice of whether to ignite or ascend belongs to them alone — faith reduced to a raw decision made on a floor, reaching toward something that may or may not be heaven.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~crawling-through-knives":{"haiku":"Yearning for transformation but caught between pain and devotion in an asymmetrical bond.","opus":"Devotion so desperate it becomes self-punishment, someone willing to bleed and burn for a lover who might complete them, framing surrender as the only decision left. The Swedish melodeath band pushes need to the edge of martyrdom.","sonnet":"About desperate devotion — the narrator will crawl through knives and bleed for someone if only they could be what he needs; there's spiritual hunger underneath, a sense that the decision being weighed is enormous (heaven or fire), and the emotional violence of need.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~darker-times":{"haiku":"Autumn's return triggers deep insecurity and a sense that troubling times are ahead. The song expresses longing for truth while accepting the burden of living a lie.","opus":"The song inhabits a stretch of insecurity and dread, questioning who is really steering while someone retreats into a self-made solitude. It settles into knowingly sustaining a falsehood, permitting brief happiness for now while treading a precarious edge between comfort and collapse.","sonnet":"A person retreats into invented isolation to manage insecurity and fear, waiting for someone to offer a better plan while walking a thin line between honest unhappiness and the lie of performed happiness. The mood is one of suppressed anxiety and the uneasy truce of getting by.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~dead-end":{"haiku":"Humanity faces apocalyptic numbness and inevitable collapse; prayer fails and the dead walk, inviting screams into hollow disaster as provocation breeds doubled winter and erasure of meaning.","opus":"An apocalyptic surrender in which pleading is pointless, no recovery follows, and the living already move through the world like the deceased. Beneath it runs a warning about human self-destruction, that the more disaster is courted the worse the reckoning, voiced by a narrator too deadened to react and resigned to perishing with everyone else.","sonnet":"A bleak survey of collective numbness and civilizational drift — humanity is too desensitized to register its own downfall, marching through disasters nobody takes responsibility for.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~deliver-us":{"haiku":"A person seeks escape and permanence with another, acknowledging confusion but embracing shared momentary aliveness despite incomprehension.","opus":"A hazy appeal for closeness and getaway, coaxing a companion to soar off and vanish alongside the narrator, holding tight to the sensation of being present and alive even as everything crumbles and coherence slips out of reach. Connection is offered as the one anchor amid dissolution.","sonnet":"The lyrics reach toward connection and escape — urging someone to fly away together, disappear for a while, and simply be present in the moment — while acknowledging that the world is hard to make sense of.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~enter-tragedy":{"haiku":"A person questions themselves and others while trapped in a maze of confusion and selfish impulses. Tragedy enters as a permanent state, their inner apathy the only thing keeping them alive.","opus":"In Flames wrestle a muddled, self-defeating mind trapped in a labyrinth, pleading to be lifted while unable to rein in its own greed. Sorrow tearing at the spirit turns into a question about whether it was all designed this way. The Gothenburg veterans render deadened detachment as a strange spark, the narrator drained and enslaved yet oddly animated by the fight.","sonnet":"Disorientation and self-sabotage feed a strange inner life — unable to control selfish impulses, seeking rescue from outside while suspecting it will never come. The tragedy is interior: a soul that has become a slave to its own apathy, questioning everything and everyone including the figure standing right in front of it.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~fear-is-the-weakness":{"haiku":"Fear drives the breakdown of community and identity as people scatter and abandon faith. Everyone shares this weakness, creating universal decline.","opus":"Melodic-death aimed at someone whose leaving has scarred the narrator, framing fear as the shared flaw that drags everyone down. It's about lost identity and belonging, the ache of watching a bond and a self erode.","sonnet":"A relationship or community broken by fear and cowardice — someone kept dragging everyone down, left scars, and the speaker finally admits there's no shared identity or belonging left, only the burden of evidence that it's over.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~jester-s-door":{"haiku":"Someone thanks everyone as they leave suddenly, their foundation crumbling. They vanish through a symbolic door, their trajectory shifted from nothing to elsewhere.","opus":"A gracious goodbye from a narrator who clawed his way from nothing to the top and now, with everything falling apart, must justify his choices before bowing out. In Flames, longtime figureheads of Swedish melodic death metal, frame the exit with gratitude and affection for those they're leaving behind.","sonnet":"A farewell said all at once because facing it one person at a time is too much — a departure from a world that started with a box of T-shirts and became something requiring defense. Gratitude and exhaustion coexist in a single sentence as the door closes. Nothing more is asked for because nothing more was needed.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~leeches":{"haiku":"A critique of manipulative leaders and blind followers who spread false wisdom and exploit ignorance for control and personal gain.","opus":"A track spitting at parasitic hangers-on and phony prophets, the narrator relieved not to belong and brushing off their so-called insight from closed minds while vowing they'll be erased from memory, the chorus insisting it scorches and tears. In Flames helped forge the Gothenburg melodic-death sound before moving into this hookier modern phase, and the song sits firmly in that later, alienation-fueled catalogue.","sonnet":"A contemptuous dismissal of groupthink and institutional authority — the speaker refuses to be absorbed by those who preach wisdom from closed minds, glad to be excluded from a system that burns and rips and promises opportunity it never delivers. The leeches of the title are the voices of consensus, and the speaker's only comfort is that they will eventually be forgotten.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~liberation":{"haiku":"A promise to liberate the dead or those in torment, returning soon. The speaker sets people free and pledges to reunite with them beyond death.","opus":"The Swedish band voices a sinister kind of salvation from an entity that never rests and vows to shatter someone before granting release. Talk of returning, rising and joining the listener that night fuses rescue with menace, shadowed by the question of whether a final day could alter anything. Freedom here comes with fangs, offered by something that also knows precisely how to break you.","sonnet":"A figure moving between life and its aftermath promises to return for those left behind. The urgency is quiet — a guide who has found the passage, still bound to collect those who do not yet know the way, returning not as threat but as presence arriving before the night ends.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~only-for-the-weak":{"haiku":"A person resents being drained emotionally by someone else's sadness and neediness, realizing they have become a tool for others' pain. They describe themselves as infected with the misery they transmit.","opus":"A cornerstone of In Flames' Clayman, the melodic-death-metal track dramatizes a corrosive codependency, a narrator drained and drowned by another's sadness, feeling guilty at every laugh. He recasts sympathy as weakness and embraces the misery that defines him, accepting his fate on bleeding knees, the infection an emblem of a self-loathing he preaches even as it consumes him.","sonnet":"A relationship has become parasitic in both directions — the speaker's sadness drowns the other, the other's laughter produces guilt, and the misery has calcified into identity. Refusing sympathy, the speaker accepts the infection and kneels into the fate they have constructed. The weakness the title names belongs to the one who stayed.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~our-infinite-struggle":{"haiku":"Pain binds two people together; complexity and brokenness create endless struggle, never fully separating or healing.","opus":"In Flames frame existence as an endless conflict fed by rage and desperation among people who refuse to change, the narrator insisting he pulls someone toward him rather than casting them off. Admitting the wounding cuts both ways, the melodeath mainstays turn life's tangled difficulty into the engine of a struggle without resolution.","sonnet":"A letter written to someone who shares the pain — not cutting them out but cutting them in, bringing them into the struggle rather than away from it. Life's complexity makes the effort endless: walking on broken glass, feeding on what is left over, the hurt mutual and the connection maintained precisely because of that shared wound.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~pacing-death-s-trail":{"haiku":"The narrator struggles to maintain faith in his beliefs as his heart grows emotionally dead. He walks a path defined by compromise and insincerity.","opus":"A weary indictment of a world running on greed, fake smiles, and the need to be seen. The emotional core is a heart gone numb, still clinging to belief while everything around it is compromise and performance.","sonnet":"Someone trying to hold onto their beliefs and sense of self in a world that demands constant compromise, competition, and performance — their heart has gone numb from the strain, and they're suspended between surrender and flight.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~ropes":{"haiku":"One person begs another to accept help and let them ease their pain by untying the bonds keeping them trapped. The plea emphasizes difficulty and struggle despite sincere effort.","opus":"A narrator kneels before someone whose hardened, killer mindset is quietly destroying them, pleading to loosen the bindings and ease a restless mind so they stay. Arriving in In Flames' later, more melodic era, the Gothenburg pioneers trade their early death-metal ferocity for an anguished plea, framing salvation as untying knots rather than winning a fight.","sonnet":"A speaker watches someone they love spiral toward a fatal edge, pleading desperately to intervene before it is too late. The offer to untie all the ropes is both literal and tender — a promise to release whatever binds them — even as the speaker admits the effort costs everything and may not be enough.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~rusted-nail":{"haiku":"Isolation persists despite exterior wakefulness; the speaker offers unconditional presence while the subject remains trapped in internal alienation.","opus":"A hand held out to someone drowning in isolation, awake but numb, a stranger even in a crowd. The Swedish band offers steady presence against the pull of pretending, a promise to stay for as long as it takes.","sonnet":"A speaker reaches out to someone who is awake but sleepwalking — always a stranger in the crowd, searching for peace and finding only make-believe — and offers unconditional presence until the end of time, asking only to be heard just once.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~scream":{"haiku":"Ordinary people perform distress and superiority through endless screaming, each convinced they matter, yet nobody listens and nothing changes. Connection exists only through performed abuse.","opus":"From the Gothenburg melodic-death pioneers in their more approachable middle period, this delivers a flat judgment on human uniformity: everyday folk with separate paths, fundamentally identical, just dwelling within separate falsehoods. All shout while none attend, and cruelty serves as a means to feel above others. Barriers thrown up in a pressure-warped society leave the whole thing in shambles, the chorus prodding whether the malice even pays off.","sonnet":"Ordinary people trapped in separate self-deceptions build walls that guarantee collective damage. Everyone shouts into the same void but no one listens; abuse masquerades as superiority, and the universal din of screaming solves nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~sounds-of-a-playground-fading":{"haiku":"Humanity is fading and losing identity, trapped as ghosts in a dying world. The song grieves lost childhood innocence and questions what purpose remains as time runs out.","opus":"A meditation on mortality and being a fading remnant of a dying breed, seeking answers in silence while sensing time running out and an end beginning.","sonnet":"The song mourns a sense of fading identity and belonging — the narrator describes himself and others as ghosts of a dying breed running out of time, unable to recognise what brought them here, and afraid of being left behind as an era ends.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~take-this-life":{"haiku":"A tormented person uses self-harm to sleep and hide inner isolation, begging for desperate help to escape the consuming void of their existence.","opus":"A portrait of self-injury and crushing isolation: the narrator wounds himself into sleep while keeping up a flawless facade, shrieking to mask how alone he is. He asks to be shattered and reassembled properly, and begs someone to end him and prove they truly stand beside him, even as his own struggles feel pointless.","sonnet":"About self-harm and loneliness as coping mechanisms — the narrator cuts themselves to sleep, screams to mask the isolation, and pleads for someone to stay and acknowledge they're present for them.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~the-attic":{"haiku":"Someone isolated in an attic builds a machine and sets traps to hide from a watcher. They are blinded by the outside world and exist only in paranoid seclusion.","opus":"In Flames depict a paranoid shut-in dodging a world he's sure is watching, convinced a figure upstairs is assembling some odd contraption and that a face in the mirror observes him. Cord he finds becomes material for a snare against whoever nears. The melodic-death band leans into psychological horror as its narrator withdraws further, edging toward total disappearance.","sonnet":"Paranoia builds its own logic inside a sealed world — someone working in the attic, a watching face in the mirror, rope on the floor repurposed into a trap. The outer world is blinding, identity barely holding, but isolation feels protective. The machine under construction may be a defense or a trap closing around its builder.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~the-end":{"haiku":"Confronting mortality becomes essential as the living question existence and legacy, wondering whether to surrender or struggle toward an uncertain future.","opus":"Two people at a crossroads facing dread and disillusionment, weighing that final exhale and whatever emptiness lies past it while realizing the life they built isn't the dream they meant to chase, and swearing to run as outlaws back toward it.","sonnet":"Two people at a crossroads, caught in something they cannot undo, are asked to look outward from the earth — \"what if we leave this world for a while\" — and confront the final breath, the emptiness of space, and whether the lives they have been living were ever the dream they had when young.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~the-puzzle":{"haiku":"Someone searches for meaning and belief, struggling with doubt and falling apart piece by piece, unable to grasp what it means to truly believe.","opus":"A search for something to believe in, the pieces of a life falling one by one as the narrator tries to assemble meaning from scattered signs; beneath the pretense of handling everything is an ambivalence about whether he cares what others think, and a wish to grab even a fragment of light from the puzzle.","sonnet":"A person searching for something to believe in after exhaustion and disillusionment — life is falling apart piece by piece, and the song is the honest admission that he's pretending to handle it while still desperately looking for meaning or faith to hold onto.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~the-quiet-place":{"haiku":"A speaker reclaims their mind and agency, refusing to be a slave and drowning out internal monsters. They assert kingliness over their own consciousness and find brightness by closing their eyes.","opus":"From In Flames' more radio-friendly period, a song about refusing subjugation and crowning oneself sovereign of one's own inner world. Once too fearful to be seen, the narrator now finds everything settling into order, using solitude as a private sanctuary to drown his demons and reclaim control.","sonnet":"Reclaiming interiority after long submission. The speaker sheds old servility and appoints himself sovereign of his own mind — drowning whatever monsters remain, letting bridges fall, arriving at a cleaner self-image not through external validation but through closed eyes and deliberate refusal.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~vacuum":{"haiku":"Society is hollow—built on lies, exploitation, and media manipulation that breeds moral collapse. Trust is weaponized while humanity erodes.","opus":"A scathing takedown of modern media-fed apathy, where people trade real life for tabloids, fake vacation photos and TV, growing weaker and more numb while the systems and headlines feeding on their attention grow stronger.","sonnet":"A caustic indictment of passive, media-consuming society — people who steal from the powerless, perform sympathy in front of televisions, and participate in systems of rape, murder, and lies while applauding and smiling on cue. The song urges listening to a threatened voice before the whole thing collapses.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~vanishing-light":{"haiku":"Life is a race toward an unknowable destination where the easy path leads nowhere. A person must navigate survival, learn to fight, and keep moving despite uncertainty.","opus":"In Flames pursue a fading glimmer toward no man's land, preaching survival amid hostility and one last image captured before disaster strikes, with nobody sure where the whole race is even headed. The Gothenburg veterans mix fury with resignation, clawing toward some notion of home while accepting how bleak it all is.","sonnet":"Survival behind enemy lines requires improvisation — clone yourself, make allies, call somewhere home, fire when ready. The race heads toward no-man's-land with no compass. Money greases the easy route while others scramble. The only instruction that holds is to take a picture and jump before the whole thing crashes.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~versus-terminus":{"haiku":"Futility when facing the end: nothing—right or wrong, gain or loss—matters once all is finished; existence ends in nothingness and the listener receives a one-way ticket to oblivion.","opus":"The Gothenburg melodic-death pioneers face the end of everything with eerie calm. Once it's all finished, they argue, the categories of right and wrong and winners and losers stop mattering, and only a change of perspective remains. The track makes apocalypse feel less like terror than like clarity already accepted.","sonnet":"A meditation on irrelevance at the end: when everything — lights, laughter, life — finally goes out, every distinction of right and wrong, winner and loser dissolves into nothingness. The song asks whether any of it mattered while issuing a one-way-ticket warning — the end is coming regardless of whether one shifts perspective in time.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~voices":{"haiku":"Echoing presence haunts the narrator across devastation, internal struggle against invisible forces defining existence within spiritual wasteland.","opus":"Picking through the ruins of his own soul while distant voices call from the world's rim, wandering alone through a wasteland that was once home. Reading the omens scrawled on the walls, he clings to what little remains and reminds himself only to breathe slow.","sonnet":"Voices from the edge of the earth — possibly angels, possibly devils — remind the speaker to breathe slowly as he wanders a wasteland that used to be home, searching through the wreckage of his soul for something real to believe in.","src":"haiku"},
"in-flames~where-the-dead-ships-dwell":{"haiku":"A person recognizes they have strayed too far and now walks on abandoned shores they left behind. They experience burnout and loss, seeking peace by accepting what they deserve.","opus":"Someone watches their whole world shrink until walls close in, then chooses to change direction rather than let it break them, accepting that everything must burn to ash before peace arrives. Fitting In Flames' melodic-death evolution, the track walks the abandoned shores of a former self, treating collapse as the necessary price of moving on.","sonnet":"Someone who has exhausted themselves chasing false hope in a suffocating place chooses deliberate departure over slow collapse. Returning briefly to the decayed shores of the past confirms the break was necessary, even if it meant losing everything that felt like home.","src":"haiku"},
"in-hearts-wake~badlands":{"haiku":"Environmental destruction through extraction and greed demands resistance and uprising; the speaker rises as an outlaw defending an ancient land against its corporate desecration.","opus":"The eco-conscious metalcore band casts environmental plunder as an outlaw stand, the earth's despoilers as tyrants drilling the land like a vein. Defense of an ancient home reframed as righteous rebellion against greed.","sonnet":"Industry and greed are framed as colonial desecration of ancient land, and the speaker — cast as an outlaw — rallies others to resist and reclaim what is being destroyed before self-destruction becomes humanity's defining legacy.","src":"haiku"},
"in-hearts-wake~dystopia":{"opus":"A critique of a numbed modern era ruled by screens and hollow bonds, mourning how passivity and unquestioned belief keep people from mending themselves or reaching those they love. The call is to recognize the deception and imagine what humanity could become if it woke up.","sonnet":"About digital disconnection killing genuine human bonds — the song diagnoses an age of silence and blind faith where false online connections make it impossible to reach the people who matter, and calls for seeing through the lie before the capacity for real love atrophies completely.","src":"sonnet"},
"in-hearts-wake~earthwalker":{"haiku":"Seekers shaped by a common dream persist as embers across time; following signs of hope and passion, the earthwalkers never truly fade.","opus":"Australian metalcore act In Hearts Wake, known for their environmental concept albums, deliver an uplifting call to treat humanity as part of nature rather than apart from it. Framing people as small flames within a vast living system, it insists that giving fully and trusting the path grants a kind of permanence, wrapped in soaring, singalong metalcore.","sonnet":"A communal anthem for people who choose to stay present and alive in the world rather than surrender to it — born among billions but carrying a sense of purpose woven into existence itself, following signs through years that clarify rather than diminish. The imagery of embers that never fade reframes endurance not as suffering but as a kind of luminous persistence.","src":"haiku"},
"in-hearts-wake~w2ha":{"haiku":"Urban hellscape reveals itself as demonically engineered cage where freedom becomes illusion and survival demands perpetual struggle against systematic oppression.","opus":"A forest child lost in Los Angeles, the city rendered as a burning pit of demons where you either perform for survival or perish for love. Layered with wildfires, freeways, surveillance and police, it indicts a supposed land of freedom that's really a dark-age hell.","sonnet":"Los Angeles is reimagined as Hell-A: a city of demons behind the land-of-freedom facade, a snake pit of drive-throughs, drive-bys, hill fires, barbed wire, and a Balrog coming through the smog — the forest child lost in a jungle where the stars below are not stars above.","src":"haiku"},
"in-search-of-sun~petrichor":{"haiku":"The speaker feels burdened by accumulated suffering and questions what they have sown; they are lost in the unknown, unable to heal.","opus":"The British groove-metal outfit names its track after rain on dry earth, then withholds relief. The narrator hopes the worst will purge the filth from his soul, begging help to embrace an agonizing fight. Once safe among the clouds, he now reaps what he sowed, becoming the one who suffers, marooned where the way forward offers no answers. Storm imagery carries the confession of a disease that never quite lets go.","sonnet":"A figure convinced he is harvesting consequences of his own making surrenders to chronic suffering with no map forward. He carries a disease he cannot name, lost in a season that has already passed, circling the admission that there are simply no answers.","src":"haiku"},
"in-search-of-sun~rhythm":{"haiku":"A person surrenders to a rhythmic, addictive force and lives a lie because they know someone needs them to.","opus":"Living a lie against a lover's manipulative pull, knowing what they want yet needing to break free of it. The chorus offers surrender to rhythm as the escape hatch from a relationship built on knowing too much and pretending otherwise.","sonnet":"A song about being stuck in a toxic dynamic the narrator knows is a lie — they're floored and disconnected from themselves, yet the pull of the other person's need keeps them trapped, unable to truly let go.","src":"haiku"},
"in-this-moment~sex-metal-barbie":{"haiku":"Media vilification and social judgment attempt to shame a woman into conformity; she defies by embracing transgression and reclaiming agency.","opus":"Maria Brink seizing every slur thrown at her, trailer trash, whore, attention fiend, and wearing them as armor, turning the audience's judgment into a persona she owns and performs back at them.","sonnet":"A performer confronts the rumours, labels, and degrading narratives projected onto her — trailer trash, harlot, homicidal queen — and instead of deflecting them, embraces them as armour, reclaiming the villain role and daring the audience to keep screaming her name.","src":"haiku"},
"in-this-moment~the-in-between":{"haiku":"Trapped between opposing parental voices calling one holy and damned, creating internal war between self-worth and self-destruction. Becomes agent of both heaven and hell.","opus":"A wrenching song about a girl caught between a mother who called her sacred and a father who condemned her, absorbing both verdicts until she wages the conflict inside herself and finds purpose in her own capacity for cruelty. Maria Brink of In This Moment stages it as her signature blend of the sacred and profane, wrestling shame and self-worth into a defiant gothic-metal catharsis.","sonnet":"A song about growing up with contradictory messages from each parent — one said holy, one said damned — and the resulting internal war between those verdicts. The speaker refuses to collapse under either label, channeling the contradiction into something volatile and self-directed, positioned permanently in the space between heaven and hell with no intention of choosing.","src":"haiku"},
"in-this-moment~whore":{"haiku":"A woman reclaims the denigrating label placed on her, revealing how judgment about her sexuality reflects others' hypocrisy and projections of their desires.","opus":"In This Moment's Maria Brink reclaims a slur as armor, addressing every judge who expected her to end up caged or discarded. She becomes the dirt they created, the sinner they pray to, exposing their contempt as desire and their stones as reflections of themselves. The band's theatrical, provocative metal weaponizes shame, forcing the accuser to face the truth mirrored back.","sonnet":"A woman reclaims the degrading label projected onto her, turning it into a declaration of hard-won power. She survived dismissal, judgment, and abuse, and now watches those who condemned her kneel before what they created. The contradiction at the heart of her accusers — loving what they hate and hating what they love — becomes the weapon she turns back on them.","src":"haiku"},
"inchaos~butterfly-effect":{"haiku":"Small actions spiral into catastrophic consequences; illusions and false certainty trap the soul as the world hangs upside down, a butterfly creating storms through accumulated betrayals.","opus":"Bilingual metalcore on fate and disillusion: people caged by time, doomed to repeat the past and pay for their own choices, told the whole thing is an illusion. The Mandarin passages deepen it into a portrait of an isolated soul bound by cause and effect and challenged to abandon its self-deception.","sonnet":"A bilingual meditation on the butterfly effect and cyclical history — the world is governed by causes and consequences no one can escape, illusions collapse like fences of light, and the narrator urges everyone to drop their hypocrisy and face the inevitability of cause and effect.","src":"haiku"},
"inchaos~mist":{"haiku":"A bilingual reflection on numbness, ancient cycles, and escape from the mist; the speaker reconciles with fate, time, and the tides pulling them onward.","opus":"A bilingual Chinese metalcore piece framing numbness and existential exhaustion against imagery of ancient fables, lighthouses and drowning stars. It moves toward release, a vow to the mist and the universe that the speaker won't stay or hesitate, sweeping a thousand dreams into a laugh.","sonnet":"A bilingual existential reckoning — the English sections describe numbness, weakness, and inability to keep pace; the Chinese lyrics invoke ancient fables, a lighthouse, deep-sea chaos, and ultimately a declaration of escape from all forces that would hold the narrator in place.","src":"haiku"},
"inchaos~you-and-me":{"haiku":"A world falls as loved ones fade; even parallel lines don't cross, leaving only the end of a shared fate.","opus":"Chinese metalcore band Inchaos stage a heartbreak on a cosmic scale, the narrator's universe caving in as he cleaves the heavens and mourns a love that's ended, undone by everything his partner once told him. Weaving Mandarin verses through English hooks, the band pair celestial, star-scattered imagery with the grief of separation over melodic, breakdown-heavy weight.","sonnet":"A bilingual elegy for a love that has ended at a cosmic scale — the speaker tears through the Milky Way and the night sky searching for connection, but even parallel timelines cannot bring the two people back together. The world is falling, the story is ending, and all that remains is the instruction to give up on the outcome without giving in entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"incubus~11-am":{"haiku":"Regret for wasted time and indecision haunts the speaker; they hope for reconnection but acknowledge the barriers their own uncertainty erected.","opus":"A morning spent paralyzed by regret over a lost someone and the choices that drove them off, hiding under bedsheets, wanting to rewind the tape, until the phone rings and hope flickers back.","sonnet":"A morning-after reckoning unfolds across the hours as a speaker wakes to regret over choices made and a relationship lost, gripped by the fear that indecision has cost them the person they wanted most — until the phone finally rings.","src":"haiku"},
"incubus~a-crow-left-of-the-murder":{"haiku":"The speaker seeks to unlearn rationality and return to instinct, recognizing wholeness exists in natural patterns rather than conscious thought.","opus":"Incubus get cerebral and restless, pushing the self to shed borrowed knowledge and trust gut instinct over anything absorbed secondhand. Even direct paths wander, and every fragment holds a blueprint of the whole. A nagging demand about whether you've grasped it yet drives a meditation on individuality, the lone crow apart from its flock becoming the band's symbol for thinking independently, curiosity prized over certainty.","sonnet":"A call to abandon inherited knowledge and return to pure instinct. Every path bends, every fragment contains the whole — the speaker urges stripping away what was read in favor of what is sensed, finding freedom in the deviant singular position outside the flock.","src":"haiku"},
"incubus~are-you-in":{"haiku":"Asks whether you're willing to conform to collective pressure when everyone else buys in.","opus":"A come-on dressed as an invitation to loosen up, half-mocking the way people fall in line with whatever's fashionable while pretending it's a choice. The pleasure is real, the peer pressure underneath it is the joke.","sonnet":"A plea for collective participation and belonging, asking whether someone is willing to join in and be present, cycling between urgency and vague drift as the invitation goes unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"incubus~drive-acoustic":{"haiku":"Fear tempts surrender of control; reclaiming self-direction requires resistance to external steering.","opus":"Brandon Boyd reflects on refusing to hand your life over to dread, choosing instead to grip the controls and face the future without flinching. Self-determination framed as quiet nerve, the decision to stop being swept along by the herd.","sonnet":"Recognising that fear has long been steering his decisions, the speaker chooses to take the wheel himself — open to whatever comes, he opts for self-determination over the comfort of following the crowd.","src":"haiku"},
"incubus~our-love":{"haiku":"Love emerges defiantly from hiding, no longer suppressed by judgment or external pressure.","opus":"Love as defiance and awakening, refusing to hide any longer while the timid tremble. Underneath the swagger is a real question about how long you can live as a fake before something true has to begin.","sonnet":"A couple stops hiding and defects from whatever forces kept their love underground — the speaker declares it is time to live authentically rather than as a fake, and their love becoming visible is its own kind of revolution against anyone who would suppress it.","src":"haiku"},
"incubus~pardon-me":{"haiku":"A twenty-three-year-old burns from accumulated pressure and world disgust, on the verge of spontaneous combustion and needing permission to combust.","opus":"Incubus, shifting from funk-metal toward radio alt-rock, frame a young man's breaking point as literal ignition. Not yet in his mid-twenties, he feels on the edge of catching fire, ground down by a world of pointless games and relentless disaster. Combustion becomes a wished-for escape from gravity, deceit and the sheer weight of living, exhaustion reimagined as a transformation he'll rise from forever changed.","sonnet":"At twenty-three, a man finds the accumulated absurdity of daily existence — hypocrisy, gravity, the sheer weight of being human — so overwhelming that spontaneous combustion feels like a rational exit. He announces his burning as an act of self-liberation rather than destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"incubus~wish-you-were-here":{"haiku":"A person experiences fleeting moments of perfect contentment alone at the beach, yet repeatedly wishes someone else could share those instances of beauty and peace.","opus":"Incubus, softening from their nu-metal roots into airier alt-rock on Morning View, set contentment against absence. Toes in the sand, the narrator leans weightless into the wind, counting UFOs and feeling genuinely happy, yet the refrain keeps reaching for someone missing. That single ache undercuts the beachside bliss, letting a moment of peace carry the quiet grief of solitude.","sonnet":"A man lies at the ocean edge in a moment of uncomplicated contentment — counting stars, feeling weightless — and the sole shadow across it is the absence of someone he loves. The world is beautiful and precarious like a roller coaster without restraints, and joy would be complete if only that one person were present to share it.","src":"haiku"},
"indigo-blaze~critical-hit":{"haiku":"Vintage aggression surges through gaming nostalgia. Dominance arrives through swagger and the menu's return.","opus":"A manic, video-game-laced battle rap of braggadocio and threat, promising to send rivals back to the menu screen; hyperactive gamer imagery fuels a broke, chaotic flex daring anyone to compete.","sonnet":"A hyperactive rap song about competitive dominance — the narrator positions themselves as the superior force in their scene, living on minimum wage but swinging above their weight, daring rivals to compete and warning that one slip ends them, using gaming and action-movie imagery to frame their ambition.","src":"haiku"},
"indigo-la-end~a-3qwt1":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"Indigo la End, led by Enon Kawatani, specialize in lush, wounded love songs where desire and pain are inseparable, and this is that formula at its most feverish. Love has grown unbearable — the narrator wants to elope on a night train, to share the pain wrapped around a thin arm, to touch what is secret and bare — but winter keeps chasing spring away and the heat is stolen before anything is said. The promised spring never arrives, only the ache of its absence.","sonnet":"A love so deep it has become unbearable — the narrator wants to elope on a night train, share the pain wrapped around the other's thin arm, touch what is secret and bare, but winter keeps chasing spring away and the heat is stolen before anything is said, leaving the night without its promised spring.","src":"haiku"},
"infant-annihilator~three-bastards":{"haiku":"A kingdom built entirely on systematic cruelty and abuse by three warlords without conscience.","opus":"Deathcore trafficking in the most extreme possible transgression, a fictional cannibal-warlord mythology designed purely to appall. The content is provocation as sport rather than statement, shock built to have no floor beneath it.","sonnet":"A violent mythological narrative depicts three monstrous warlords emerging from centuries of institutionalised depravity and feudal horror, united only by cruelty, who set out to burn a corrupt kingdom to the ground.","src":"haiku"},
"infected-rain~longing":{"haiku":"The speaker chases an imagined perfect version of someone or something that never existed, creating a false narrative that prevents real connection.","opus":"A probing look at the private wars people fight with themselves, asking who exactly you keep battling, your former or future self, and naming the specific ache of wanting something you only ever imagined. Its refrain reframes yielding as survival rather than defeat. Infected Rain, the Moldovan band led by Lena Scissorhands, pair djent-tinged weight with clean melody and self-aware introspection.","sonnet":"A confrontation with the self that keeps fighting imaginary versions of itself — past and future selves locked in conflict over answers to questions that do not exist, longing for something never actually possessed but constructed entirely in the mind. The resolution is not transcendence but flexibility: bending open rather than breaking, learning to breathe instead of reaching for a fantasy.","src":"haiku"},
"infected-rain~lure":{"haiku":"Self-proclaimed power and demonic energy transcend judgment and hypocrisy, the narrator refuses conformity and blazes their own path regardless of others' opinions.","opus":"A defiant rejection of judgment and false idols, the singer embracing being called crazy and channeling an inner demon to lead others out of a glamorous, deceitful trap.","sonnet":"A performer refuses to be dismissed as crazy or manipulated by false gods and industry gimmicks, insisting they are a prodigy with a demon in them who will change the world with or without the approval of those who can't stop blinking long enough to see the trick isn't there.","src":"haiku"},
"infected~as-death-circles-around-us":{"haiku":"Final stand against encroaching annihilation demands courage beyond reason as collective breath becomes commodity and sacrifice becomes statement.","opus":"A last stand where every breath could be the final one as death closes in and war swallows the world. The defiant faith is that even if they fall and lose everything, the last breath isn't really the last, so they hold the line and fight.","sonnet":"A survival hymn in the midst of total war: plasma rips through skin, humanity is hunted down, despair drowns everything — yet the refrain insists on standing and fighting, because although any breath may be the last, the final breath won't be.","src":"haiku"},
"infected~vessel":{"haiku":"A wanderer moves through lifeless ruins and empty hallways, cleansing abominations while searching for some distant voice or meaning that echoes through time but never materializes.","opus":"A deathcore descent into a lifeless, ruined landscape where silence itself grows deafening. The narrator drifts through an endless void, hunting a distant voice while grotesque horrors lurk at every turn. It reaches toward existential dread and the futility of searching for meaning inside emptiness, a hollow husk of a world that echoes back nothing.","sonnet":"A vessel drifting through ruins in absolute silence, searching for something in the void while wretched abominations lurk around every corner — the deafening silence, the severed limbs, the cleansing of infection all suggest a horror-survival journey through a dead world, with no rescue and no certainty the distant voice exists.","src":"haiku"},
"inferiority-complex~till-death-do-us-part":{"haiku":"Love becomes obsession and possession. A ghost haunts the beloved after betrayal, using final vows to justify eternal torment.","opus":"A betrayed woman's vengeance rendered as a diabolical marriage vow, where the wronged party becomes the tormenting ghost delivering an eye-for-an-eye final judgment on the lover who failed her.","sonnet":"A song about a woman destroyed by a loveless, neglectful relationship who ultimately turns on her abuser — she drank poison like wine, broke down under his indifference, and the song escalates from despair to a vow of lethal revenge, ending with a wedding oath recast as a death sentence.","src":"haiku"},
"inferiority-complex~yours-sincerely":{"haiku":"Isolation and abandonment; left in darkness by those who never explained the rules.","opus":"An open letter to a parent who was absent through their own suffering, written as the child finally releases long-held resentment. The narrator recognizes that a mother could not be what was needed because she was still trying to heal herself. Tender rather than accusing, it reaches for forgiveness and the ache of loving someone who could not show up.","sonnet":"A child trying to understand a parent's absence — knocking and getting no reply, left in the dark, needing advice and a hand in their hair. The adult speaker arrives at forgiveness not through resolution but through recognition: you needed your mother like I needed you, the wound generational, the apology unnecessary because what's understood doesn't require it.","src":"haiku"},
"infinite-illusion~absence":{"opus":"A person suffocating in a self-destructive relationship pivots from paralyzing fear of abandonment to violent fury, fantasizing about slowly knifing and killing the partner who cheated and bled them dry, framing the murder as finally breaking free of themselves.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a toxic relationship cycles from suffocating heartbreak to furious release — the fear of losing someone keeps them captive until rage takes over and they vow to cut that person out violently and permanently.","src":"sonnet"},
"inhuman~vengeance":{"haiku":"Graphic fantasies of torture and murder, driven by rage and a thirst for inflicting the maximum suffering on a deserving victim.","opus":"An extreme, graphically violent revenge fantasy, the narrator consumed by a craving to kill, dragging his victim to the woods, torturing them to death and desecrating the corpse to slake a thirst for vengeance. It is deliberately transgressive brutality, gore piled on gore. The song works in the death-metal tradition where shock-horror imagery and guttural savagery are the aesthetic point, the retribution narrative pushed to grotesque, nihilistic extremity.","sonnet":"A graphic first-person revenge fantasy in which the speaker enacts extreme violence on someone who wronged them — driven by a craving that feels ordained rather than chosen, the song moves through abduction, torture, killing, and desecration in clinical detail. There is no moral framing or narrative context, only the satisfaction of a thirst described as absolute.","src":"haiku"},
"inlights~r-u-s-t":{"opus":"A defiant kiss-off to a condescending, controlling person who thinks they know what's best, demanding to be set free from their lies and to escape the room they've trapped the singer in.","sonnet":"Furious rejection of someone who claims to know what is best for the narrator and tries to reshape them into a different person: the narrator refuses the presumption, demands to be left alone, and insists on the right to be who they already are.","src":"sonnet"},
"interloper~glasshouse":{"haiku":"A speaker's words fall on deaf ears as society prioritizes comfort over truth, and values erode as lies replace integrity.","opus":"A sleepless narrator watches a bored, degraded humanity and tears at his own eyes as morals burn and lies harden into accepted truth. Interloper, rooted in the online progressive-metal community around Andrew Baena, channel technical precision into a lament over eroded values, casting the disillusioned observer as someone who speaks change to ears that refuse it.","sonnet":"A speaker watches a civilization rot from the inside — truth dissolving into performance, comfort bred from compliance — and finds his own moral ground consumed in the process. The burning is both a critique of collective decay and a private confession that the witness is not innocent.","src":"haiku"},
"interloper~idle-years":{"opus":"A confrontation with the inner demons that surface after years of dormancy, a poisonous inner nature and haunting voices the narrator fears and doesn't understand, drowning as darkness overtakes him.","sonnet":"About dormant inner demons awakening after years of inactivity — the narrator discovers a capacity for destructive behavior they didn't know existed within themselves, surfacing from a quiet period with violent, disorienting force.","src":"sonnet"},
"interloper~search-party":{"haiku":"Someone runs from pain and self-awareness, refusing to be found or helped. The emptiness they flee into becomes the hollow core of who they are.","opus":"A portrait of someone who copes by fleeing, burrowing away from pain until the people who once loved her turn into old wounds, and the terrible irony that where she finally hides is inside herself. It closes on a bleak forecast: when the fog lifts she will return to find her home hollowed out. Interloper are a progressive metalcore project, and this pairs melodic ambience with djent-adjacent heaviness typical of that mid-2010s scene.","sonnet":"A woman who has mastered running from pain but never learned to face it — turning her avoidance into wounds, leaving wreckage behind. Those who love her form a search party she refuses to acknowledge, too proud to surrender even when they're close. The song sits with the bleak knowledge that when the hiding place finally collapses, she'll find only emptiness where home used to be.","src":"haiku"},
"interloper~wishful-thinking":{"haiku":"A person carries pain and dead weight while gambling on outcomes, alternating between hoping things improve and accepting their situation is wishful thinking.","opus":"A study of grief and self-administered wounds: at last the narrator finds a balm for a hurt nothing else could reach, a grim companion in his sorrow. The deeper thread is his hopeless faith that a lost person will return, reopening his own injuries to feel and heal, betting on omens that promise reunion while admitting outright that such hope is pure delusion.","sonnet":"Waiting in pain for someone to return while knowing they probably won't — the narrator strips back to raw, bleeding honesty about their wounds, kills the light on their regret, and circles the question of when this will finally end.","src":"haiku"},
"internet-girl~anything-u-want":{"haiku":"Imposter syndrome and ambition collide as the speaker oscillates between genuine effort and faking competence; the path to success demands embracing the performance itself.","opus":"A wired confession about impostor syndrome and faking it, aimed at a younger self and at anyone who feels like a fraud in the scene. Underneath the swagger, touring has worn the artist's mental health down and left them homesick and washed up.","sonnet":"About the anxiety and impostor syndrome of trying to break into the music industry — one narrator has spent sixteen years chasing their sound only to get kicked out; the other is burning out on tour while feeling washed up and homesick. The chorus insists that if you feel like a fraud, the only move is to fake it and keep going anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"interpol~untitled":{"opus":"A near-wordless opener that builds a whole mood from one promise, that the singer will turn up whenever you're low. It's the sound of guarded hope, tenderness offered at an angle.","sonnet":"A spare, almost formless promise to return unexpectedly — a voice reassuring someone that surprise and presence will come at the lowest moments. Four lines offer minimal comfort with maximal restraint.","src":"sonnet"},
"interworld~deep-end":{"haiku":"Drowning in lies and dissolution, the speaker ties hands while craving intimacy; lies offer comfort while the deep end pulls inexorably downward.","opus":"A hyperpop-adjacent spiral of self-destruction and toxic intimacy, set in a sleepless hotel room where the narrator swallows pills and dissolves into someone else's lies. Craving the very ruin that hurts them, they beg to be pushed under while their hands stay bound. It captures the paralysis of wanting connection and annihilation at once.","sonnet":"A speaker dry-swallowing pills in a hotel room at night melts into someone while tangled in lies — \"I find comfort in mistakes\" — wanting to hold them but with hands tied, asking to be pushed off the deep end as both surrender and accusation.","src":"haiku"},
"into-eternity~fukushima":{"haiku":"Catastrophe and memorial collide as the dead summon the living toward transcendence; adversity becomes pathway to resurrection and transformation.","opus":"An elegy and a vow of resilience after the nuclear disaster, candles for the dead set against a refusal to surrender. The Canadian band turns catastrophe into a grim insistence on rebuilding and outlasting the ruin.","sonnet":"A meditation on the Fukushima nuclear disaster that moves from mourning the dead and the chaos of ongoing crisis toward a defiant call to transcend catastrophe, rebuild, and prevail.","src":"haiku"},
"into-the-vein~echoes-within":{"haiku":"Distorted perception and spiritual fragmentation trap the speaker in loops of false belief as they question whether agency exists or whether all resistance proves illusory.","opus":"A challenge to inherited faith, casting humankind as the origin of all things yet talked by deities and twisted truths into believing we're already gone. The quest is for an alternate exit, beyond terror and self-reproach, toward a love and current the crowd never chose to notice.","sonnet":"A fractured spiritual crisis: the speaker grapples with distorted truths and a blurred perception test, questioning whether gods have convinced humanity it is already dead and calling out the collective weakness of blaming no one — shadows and words circling in the ebb, searching for another way out.","src":"haiku"},
"intr-spect~ad-astra":{"haiku":"A traveler embarks on a spiritual journey guided by beauty and uncertainty, finding home in the journey itself rather than arrival.","opus":"The band responds to a horizon that murmurs across the turning of day, treating the unknown as where real beauty lives. Adopting the Latin motto about reaching the stars through hardship, they send off departing heroes with a blessing and melt loneliness into belonging, insisting the traveling itself is where one truly belongs.","sonnet":"A blessing for those who dare. Framed as a farewell wish for heroes setting out across uncertainty, the song locates the journey itself as the destination. A Latin phrase about reaching stars through hardship anchors the whole — the horizon calls, the stars illuminate the path, and the fire in the eyes is the same fire that lit the cosmos.","src":"haiku"},
"intronaut~cubensis":{"haiku":"A psychedelic exploration of perception and dissolution, where light fades into shadow and the mind melts into altered states of consciousness.","opus":"The Los Angeles progressive-metal band, their name nodding to psychedelic mushroom lore, chart a dissolving trip. As daylight fades before nightfall, dusk seeps into a mind gone loose and receptive. Everything the eyes have seen dissolves as the substance is consumed, the user reaching ever further and floating ever higher, inward and outward at once, form and meaning blurring into a bottomless, nightmare-touched depth.","sonnet":"Consciousness dissolves at dusk under the influence of a psychedelic substance: the boundaries of self melt, shadows acquire impossible depth, and perception shifts so radically that color and identity are only visible when the light hits at exactly the right angle.","src":"haiku"},
"intronaut~run-through-the-jungle":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic flight from demonic forces framed as religious metaphor for chaos and escape.","opus":"A cover of Fogerty's Vietnam-era paranoia, its jungle a doubling of the war abroad and America's flood of guns at home, a survivalist dread of unseen violence closing in. The command to keep running and never look back is pure animal fear.","sonnet":"A cover of the Creedence Clearwater Revival song: an urgent, breathless voice warns of a devil on the loose and 200 million loaded guns as thunder speaks from the mountain — a Vietnam-era panic landscape of spiritual dread and violence that demands you run and never look back.","src":"haiku"},
"introvert~all-i-know":{"haiku":"A relationship's foundation cracks and crumbles, leaving the narrator alone in a house that once felt like home. He grieves both the loss of the other and the loss of what they built together.","opus":"A quiet elegy for a love turning to ruins, with a shared home now cold and hollow. The narrator refuses to blame his partner, admitting the collapse is just the only way he understands relationships, and he braces himself while everything the two of them constructed comes apart.","sonnet":"A relationship that built something like home together but has slowly gone cold — the foundation has cracked, the fire has died, and the speaker finds themselves alone in a place that no longer feels like home, but this is all they've ever known.","src":"haiku"},
"introvert~as-long-as-you-know":{"haiku":"A person bends and breaks themselves to please someone toxic, enduring manipulation and self-doubt, until they realize the other can't hurt them anymore.","opus":"A portrait of an abusive relationship's slow erosion, the narrator endlessly contorting themselves to satisfy a partner who leaves them feeling worthless. The real ache lies in the hollow defiance underneath, a self-protective mantra insisting they can't be wounded anymore even after all the damage is done.","sonnet":"A post-breakup song about finally reaching the point where someone who burned and broke you no longer has power over you — the narrator has bent themselves almost to nothing trying to please this person, but has arrived at a place of clarity: you can't hurt me anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"introvert~december":{"haiku":"Resignation over failed communication: the speaker cannot articulate feelings into words, breaking promises through silence and offering neither apologies nor commitment—only the freedom to leave.","opus":"The band renders a relationship dissolving in cold, quiet resignation. Unable to voice what he feels, the narrator hands over a bleak liberty, leaving the other free to remain or go without a fight. He won't say sorry for what winter cost them, holding firm as broken vows and shared hopes dim into weary, numbed honesty.","sonnet":"A relationship fraying under the weight of things left unsaid arrives at a reluctant release: stay or go, either choice is accepted without apology. The speaker cannot translate interior feeling into spoken words, and past events — compressed into a single month's name — are acknowledged but not recanted. Exhaustion has replaced the will to fight or to explain.","src":"haiku"},
"introvert~dreamers":{"haiku":"A person trapped in recurring nightmares, neurotic and drowning, hiding their pain behind lies; they see no way out except through their own teeth (self-destruction).","opus":"An isolated, self-described neurotic narrator feels trapped in a bad dream and drowning, hiding behind lies and expecting that once someone sees the truth they will give up on him and he will fade away.","sonnet":"The speaker feels trapped inside a waking bad dream, hiding their distress behind reassurances while admitting to neurosis and a sense of fading away. They push others at arm's length, fearing that if they are truly seen they will only be abandoned.","src":"haiku"},
"introvert~everything-is-different-now":{"haiku":"A relationship has fundamentally changed; one person has transformed into someone unrecognizable while both struggle weakly to accept the rupture.","opus":"A stark breakup lament turns on the widening gulf between two people, the speaker asking whether his pain is shared and whether shame is his to feel. Missing a partner's eyes sits beside the flat verdict that everything is different and over now, the other person changed beyond reach. Mutual weakness surfaces at the end, both falling, neither quite understanding how the connection quietly collapsed.","sonnet":"A relationship has shifted irrevocably and the speaker can no longer pretend otherwise. The distance is felt in the body — something lost behind familiar eyes — and neither party can undo what has changed, leaving only the mutual acknowledgment that the ground beneath them has given way.","src":"haiku"},
"introvert~find-a-way":{"haiku":"Someone hides from connection despite another's persistent attempts to reach them; the speaker refuses intimacy while the other cannot move forward without their presence.","opus":"A steadfast pledge to reach a withdrawn, self-deceiving loved one who keeps themselves at arm's length, insists everything is fine, and has sealed themselves off for years. The narrator, unable to carry on without them, keeps swearing he'll break through to remind them they aren't isolated, even while dreading he never truly will.","sonnet":"A song about trying to reach someone who hides their pain behind claims of being fine — the speaker refuses to accept the closed doors and the loneliness masked as self-sufficiency, insisting on staying present even though they fear the person may be better off alone.","src":"haiku"},
"introvert~is-it-too-late":{"haiku":"Regret and helplessness blur as someone struggles with unchangeable circumstances, suffocating under weight they cannot control.","opus":"Introvert grasp at something genuine slipping away while precious time drains, wondering whether recovery is already impossible. They resent being asked to transform when their feelings never wavered, and the weight of mutual mistakes and old bitterness seems to press in and trap them.","sonnet":"A standoff between the urge for change and the fear of losing what little remains. Time accumulates as a wound, walls close in, familiar failures repeat. The speaker asks whether there is still a moment to reclaim something shared before the weight of mutual mistakes makes reclamation impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"introvert~it-s-alright":{"haiku":"Fear of becoming monster conflicts with choices that linger against intention. The speaker struggles between self-perception and the person emerging through accumulated decisions.","opus":"A quietly hopeful coping anthem from someone scared of who they've become and chased through their dreams by past choices. Holding to a loved one's words, they mark off the days toward a calmer time and reassure themselves that, underneath it all, they're still the same person and it will be alright.","sonnet":"Holding on through fear of who you've become: the choices linger, the haunted dreams won't stop, but the narrator has learned to live for another day, counting down toward a time when late nights lose their sting — a small, hard-won \"it's alright.\"","src":"haiku"},
"introvert~mending-breaking":{"haiku":"The narrator endlessly cycles through self-doubt and breaking down, unable to escape the pattern of struggling and failing.","opus":"A song about depression and its self-defeating loops, a life littered with the wreckage of drinking and a mind that keeps tearing itself apart only to be laboriously reassembled. The exhausting cycle of falling to pieces and clawing back together stands in for a struggle that never quite resolves.","sonnet":"A cycle of barely recovering only to fall apart again — plagued by self-doubt and exhaustion, the narrator keeps having to drag themselves back up alone, with no clear end to the pattern in sight.","src":"haiku"},
"introvert~somewhere-else":{"haiku":"Someone constantly wishes to escape any situation despite comfortable company, struggling to trust themselves and find peace.","opus":"A frank sketch of restless discontent and social unease, a narrator forever longing to be anywhere but here regardless of how welcoming the company, second-guessing every look and exhausted by his own spiraling. Underneath runs a hope for self-acceptance, gradually learning to trust himself while asking for nothing more than a little space.","sonnet":"About social anxiety and disassociation — even in warm company the narrator always wants to be somewhere else, reads the worst into every glance, overthinks everything, and is slowly trying to learn to trust themselves enough to just breathe.","src":"haiku"},
"introvert~trip":{"haiku":"The speaker circles through obsessive thoughts and desire, unable to escape loops of wanting and missing what they've created in their mind.","opus":"The narrator's thoughts churn restlessly as he craves acknowledgment for all he has accomplished. Freedom and being stuck blur together in a refrain about a compulsion he both wants and misses, and as he argues with the voice in his own head and doubts his worth, he keeps looping through the same self-doubting spiral.","sonnet":"Circular self-doubt spiraling between craving and paralysis. The speaker argues with an inner voice about worth and desire, walking in loops between dream and waking, never quite sure if the cycle grants freedom or tightens the trap — the need persists even after each resolution to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"invalids~sherman-is-connector":{"haiku":"Small moments of youth and connection fragment across time; the speaker catalogs fleeting intimacies—kisses, late nights, saved money—that accumulate into a precious life.","opus":"A rush of adolescent snapshots, grounded chores, saved-up cash, a first kiss that shattered him, and a stray sense of cosmic smallness. It is about the disjointed intensity of growing up, where tiny milestones and quiet devastations blur together.","sonnet":"A kaleidoscope of adolescent moments — saving change all year, kissing in a car, a meteoroid catching the corner of your eye — threaded together by the accumulation of small significance; nothing is a grand narrative, just ordinary life assembling itself until you've saved two hundred dollars and bought something nice.","src":"haiku"},
"invent-animate~as-if-it-never-was":{"opus":"The pain of loving someone bent on self-destruction and isolation, torn between the exhausting compulsion to keep them safe and the realization that love isn't enough to stop their self-chosen ruin, ending in a family's fear and a lover's loss.","sonnet":"A relationship with someone who is self-destructive and seemingly bent on disappearing: the narrator loves them but cannot force them to choose life or stay, and watches helplessly as the other person presses toward their own erasure.","src":"sonnet"},
"invent-animate~cloud-cascade":{"opus":"Loving someone whose depression hangs over the relationship like an endless storm cloud, drenching the narrator no matter how he shelters himself; he chooses to stand in her rain and extinguish his own light to be with her, questioning whether she can love him back.","sonnet":"A person trapped under the suffocating emotional weight of someone they love — the other person's moods and needs drench them in pain like a relentless downpour, and they begin to doubt whether love is even possible in that dynamic.","src":"sonnet"},
"invent-animate~dark":{"haiku":"The speaker loses their shadow—their identity and source of light—becoming hollow and separated from their soul through internal alienation.","opus":"Invent Animate dramatize a loss of self as a missing shadow, the warmth of a lover's touch draining away until only cold emptiness is left. The progressive metalcore band use eclipse and vapor imagery to depict the soul splitting from the self, the speaker crossing a threshold to look back at the moment he abandoned who he was.","sonnet":"Dissociation rendered as the loss of a shadow — the self separates from its own soul and the warmth of another person fades simultaneously. Hope evaporates like vapor. What remains is a hollow interior, dark shapes moving against walls, a melody in black and white where color once lived.","src":"haiku"},
"invent-animate~heavener":{"haiku":"Comfort and presence transcend separation; the speaker promises steadfast companionship and light to dispel internal darkness, transforming isolation into shared refuge.","opus":"A metalcore vow of steadfast presence to a suffering companion, promising to wait and stay so the other is never left alone. Framed around the idea of already being home, it reaches past the rain and fear toward eventual light, the clouds parting and warmth burning the dread away. Devotion offered as sanctuary rather than rescue.","sonnet":"A devotional addressed to someone isolated and hurting — \"I will wait, I will stay\" — promising that the clouds will part, the sun will burn away fear, and that the speaker's heart and soul will remain until the end, naming the beloved Heavener as the place they are already home.","src":"haiku"},
"invent-animate~immolation-of-night":{"haiku":"Resilience emerges from shattered foundation as the narrator claims wholeness through acceptance of ongoing assault, strength discovered in vulnerable perseverance.","opus":"Repeated blows of adversity break against a heart that will not move, showing that the strength he assumed had crumbled had in fact held firm. The insight is that ordeals he never asked for are what shaped him, battered but intact, sufficient, and powerful precisely where he felt frail.","sonnet":"Endless waves crash against an immovable heart and fail to break it; what seemed like dust — resolve, stillness, inner wholeness — proves steadfast against the battering, and the speaker emerges from the storm's pressure knowing they are enough and whole.","src":"haiku"},
"invent-animate~luna":{"opus":"A grief song addressed to a dead older brother, wrestling with being haunted by his memory and forced to grow up alone and fill his shoes, ending in regret for the time and closeness they never got to share.","sonnet":"A person mourns the death of a brother, processing grief, regret over time not spent together, and the burden of having to grow up and take on responsibilities the brother might have shared — suffused with longing for a connection that can never be reclaimed.","src":"sonnet"},
"invent-animate~the-sun-sleeps":{"haiku":"A person grieves abandonment and promises they couldn't keep, haunted by forgetting a loved one while wishing to sever their bonds.","opus":"A wrenching address to someone the narrator has abandoned, asking how the other sleeps carrying a private war while he pleads to be exonerated for promises he keeps breaking. Guilt curdles into cold resolve as he admits belonging to another now, no tears left. Invent Animate are a Texas atmospheric metalcore band, and this leans into their signature blend of ambient, ethereal cleans and crushing dissonance to voice a hollowed-out survivor's psyche.","sonnet":"Two people bound by mutual damage, circling each other across a wasteland of resentment and broken promises. One pleads for absolution, for the other to speak as if they're the only one left; the other has chosen deliberate forgetting. Neither can heal what's hollow, and every effort to drain the pain just cycles it back — the only resolution is the acknowledgment of abandonment, final and cold.","src":"haiku"},
"invertor~a-1cv0qub":{"haiku":"A dark mockery of death as trendy—evil rules today, the speaker is an antichrist with toxic drugs, cynically watching masses follow.","opus":"This Russian hardcore track inverts the usual rap flex entirely: instead of chasing status, it declares death the only authentic trend in a scene full of gold chains and posturing. The narrator calls himself Antichrist and drug dealer, promises to slit throats, and mocks anyone chasing style while ignoring mortality, positioning his music as the one thing that matters. The core is contempt for everyone who refuses to look directly at death — bravado turned into a memento mori.","sonnet":"A Russian hardcore track declaring death as the only authentic trend in a scene full of posturing and gold chains — the narrator calls himself the Antichrist and drug dealer, promises to slit throats, mocks anyone chasing style while ignoring mortality, and positions his music as the one thing that actually matters. The emotional core is the inverse of mainstream rap bravado: contempt for everyone who won't look directly at death.","src":"haiku"},
"invisions~deadlock":{"opus":"A metalcore plunge into self-hatred and mental illness, the narrator casting himself as his own greatest threat, weirdly settled in a private torment. He cautions that faith in him is misplaced while still gripping foolish hope, composing his diseased song from a condemned man's cell. Wretchedness becomes an odd home he won't let entirely swallow him.","sonnet":"A song about self-sabotage and the exhausting loop of being your own worst enemy — the narrator knows their thoughts are diseased, opens their heart to something destructive anyway, and cycles through the same hell repeatedly. The 'deadlock' is between knowing better and doing it anyway, with no exit in sight.","src":"sonnet"},
"invisions~gold-blooded":{"opus":"A combative, self-assured clapback at doubters and imitators, daring them to drag his name through the dirt while he thrives, promising the reaper treats them all the same in the end.","sonnet":"A defiant response to industry gatekeepers and detractors who write the band off: the narrator leans into the hatred as confirmation of his toughness, threatens retribution, and declares his blood gold — proof of value that survives all attempts to bury his name.","src":"sonnet"},
"invisions~good-for-nothing":{"haiku":"Cyclical self-sabotage traps the speaker in patterns of pain that feel inescapable despite lucid recognition of their destructiveness. Shame breeds repetition.","opus":"The compulsion to return to your own pain, mistaking familiar suffering for something you need. Its honesty is the warning it ends on, telling you not to make it your answer, that it can't cure your darkness.","sonnet":"A self-aware self-destroyer reflects on a compulsive return to pain and bad choices, acknowledging they've learned to love the cycle while warning others not to look to them for salvation, since they are as lost as anyone.","src":"haiku"},
"invisions~my-plague":{"haiku":"A person confronts someone whose arrogance and toxic ego has poisoned their connection; they acknowledge mutual blame but declare the relationship irreparably broken.","opus":"InVisions unleash venomous contempt at a self-important tormentor whose everyday cruelties finally provoke open war. The metalcore band twist the knife inward too, admitting the narrator hurts himself in the reckoning, a self-described bastard whose only virtue is confessing the very ugliness he refuses to become.","sonnet":"Mutual corrosion between two people who have become each other's torment. The speaker rages at someone whose ego and manipulation have become unbearable, then turns the rage inward, recognizing his own complicity. Blame circles without landing — both parties know exactly why they haunt each other.","src":"haiku"},
"invisions~the-6-6-9":{"haiku":"Fame and social media performance corrode authentic self; the speaker exposes narcissism and hollow trending while watching influencers burn from within.","opus":"A scathing takedown of clout-chasing and social-media validation, the hollow host feeding on fame while it eats them alive. The venom lands because it names the trap, worth measured in the words of vultures.","sonnet":"A brutal address to someone performing wellness on social media — hollow inside, feeding on fame, sacrificing authenticity for trending visibility — the speaker dismantles the facade and asks whether the endless performance is worth a life and a soul.","src":"haiku"},
"iris-exe~a-thousand-needles":{"haiku":"Betrayal cuts deeper than any blade—a hollow figure wearing masks of honesty while inflicting a thousand small deaths through broken trust and the refusal to be authentic.","opus":"Contempt for a two-faced betrayer, a Judas online or in the flesh, whose fake sincerity bends with every crowd. The pain of that treachery is sworn to be remembered a thousand times over.","sonnet":"A song about someone who deceives and manipulates through passive dishonesty — a Judas figure who crosses fingers, breaks trust, and feigns innocence, causing the speaker a thousand small deaths with each false word.","src":"haiku"},
"iris-exe~chemical-bleed-circles-in-the-dark":{"haiku":"Mechanical and synthetic love binds two fragmented beings in a feedback loop of passion and dissolution, searching for wholeness through chemical union.","opus":"A person wired into a numbing, mechanical existence, running the same dark loops to blot out feeling, kept quiet by the soft presence of someone. Confessing to staring at ceilings in a kingdom of nothing, he half-invites and half-hides from anyone who might find him inside that self-erasure.","sonnet":"An obsessive attachment — perhaps to an artificial or inaccessible figure described as 'lifelike' and kept quiet by her 'soft side' — drives the speaker in circles through the dark, swallowing feeling while mechanical breathing and a 'chemical bleed' mark the current that keeps their worlds apart.","src":"haiku"},
"iris-exe~guts":{"haiku":"Vulnerability and sacrifice show the speaker willing to tear themselves open for connection and understanding.","opus":"Frantic, self-destroying devotion that offers to be torn open and lived inside, pouring oneself out to keep a departing partner from leaving. Mortality sits caged and quiet in the belly, no real threat, while the true horror is watching this person drift off after coming so close.","sonnet":"About a desperate, visceral love where the speaker would endure any physical agony — including tearing themselves open — to make someone stay or even just to hear them speak, but ultimately watches them slip away anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"iris-exe~it-s-the-blue-that-lasts":{"haiku":"Melancholy persists as the enduring color of emotional landscape; blue outlasts other feelings.","opus":"A drowning metaphor for an intoxicating, destructive attachment, where a woman's pull is figured as an undertow the narrator cannot resist. Reluctant to be alive yet magnetized back again and again, they surrender to the sea knowing only the blue endures once bones turn to dust. Obsession rendered as beautiful, self-erasing tidewater.","sonnet":"Drowning in a beautiful, violent attachment — the woman is described as waves that always pull the narrator back, her hands tracing veins, bruises pressed into skin; the narrator is reluctant to be alive yet cannot break free of her gravity. The refrain \"it's the blue that lasts\" — the colour of bruise, of water, of melancholy — is the residue left after everything else passes.","src":"haiku"},
"iris-exe~mercykiller":{"haiku":"A digital-romantic entanglement where the speaker, wired into a screen-mediated longing, imagines self-corrupting code and memory decay as the price and relief of being possessed by someone who will never fully acknowledge them.","opus":"A digital-age fixation where love, code, and self-annihilation blur together. The narrator obsesses over a green light and a screen, wishing they could write the program that would make the other theirs, then begs to be locked up or let corrupt. It reaches toward the terror of being reduced to data, craving deletion as a kind of mercy.","sonnet":"Digital obsession framed as self-dissolution: stuck on a screen chasing a green light she'll never be, writing the code to make you mine, willing her own memory to corrupt if that's what it takes — the mercykill is what happens when connection cuts through her, simultaneously salvation and destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"iris-exe~no-poison-of-my-own":{"haiku":"Decay and slow dissolution become preferable to external domination as the speaker reclaims agency through self-controlled decline.","opus":"A brief, decaying meditation on defiant self-possession. The narrator claims to be better while rotting slowly, insisting no external venom can overtake them alone and no army can shake their resolve. Built on withered-rose and stripped-bone imagery, it reaches for a bleak autonomy, strength found in embracing one's own slow ruin rather than surrendering to another's harm.","sonnet":"The speaker, rotting slowly and sustained by internal resilience rather than any external cure, watches a lover decompose — \"skin peeled off the bone\" — and declares that no army or poison can overtake their solitary, still-standing endurance.","src":"haiku"},
"iris-exe~ok-pixelate":{"haiku":"Acceptance and digital fragmentation of identity, the speaker consenting to decomposition into pixels.","opus":"Dissociation imagined as a corrupting, breaking-up image of the self. The narrator drifts through life restrained, faking cheer while fantasizing about splitting into tiny consumable fragments to satisfy others' demands. Overstimulated and dissolving toward static, it reaches for the quiet dread of vanishing entirely, wishing to blur out of being rather than keep pretending.","sonnet":"Overstimulation and performed wellness as a form of dissolution — hands tied behind the back, gas station bathrooms, a smile so grossly simulated it locks the jaw — the speaker dreams of breaking herself into bite-sized pieces to fit the right places, slipping further into blur until she pixelates entirely out of legibility.","src":"haiku"},
"iris-exe~sanctuary":{"haiku":"Connection dissolves fear and creates sanctuary; love reveals beauty hidden within brokenness.","opus":"A rare moment of peace inside this artist's usually anxious digital world, watching a lover sleep as fears and lies dissolve into a new shared country. It reaches toward the idea that nothing is fully whole and nothing fully broken, love offered as refuge even as the heart is owned to be a battlefield still hungry for more.","sonnet":"A cover of Sanctuary — the beloved as refuge from fear and lies, angels in flight, the heart a battleground that needs more affection than anyone knows — the song asks what is left of me now in a register that is devotional and uncertain in equal measure.","src":"haiku"},
"iris-exe~sleep-better":{"haiku":"Rest eludes despite desperate seeking; the speaker pursues sleep that refuses to arrive.","opus":"The aftermath of a suffocating relationship, framed with the artist's signature glitch and simulation imagery. The narrator recalls contorting themselves, stretching and shrinking to fit a partner's preferred corners, arms that squeezed the color out of them. Now they rest and dream better in the other's absence, reaching toward the relief of reclaiming one's own body and space.","sonnet":"Post-relationship clarity framed as physical release — the narrator was contorting themselves to fit the other person's corners (\"I stretch and shrink myself to fit in all your favorite corners\"), their arms squeezed the color out, left a grayscale view; now the refrain is literal and triumphant: \"I sleep better without you next to me.\" The buzz of solitude is its own ecstasy.","src":"haiku"},
"iris-exe~use-me":{"haiku":"Automation and exploitation transform humanity into consumable product, where emotional and physical breakdown becomes expected sacrifice.","opus":"A cyborg love figured through decay and pharmaceuticals, where affection is indistinguishable from being consumed. The narrator invites their own breaking down to plastic, mistaking machinery for a way to breathe easy while sensing only cancer. It reaches toward the panic of dehumanized intimacy, offering the self up to be used rather than confronting how human and hurt it still is.","sonnet":"The speaker, suspecting they may not be fully human, invites exploitation — \"break me down to plastic\" — while recognising that another person's toxicity has infected them like a cancer; the \"candy-coded blood\" of the other's tongue offers no real cure.","src":"haiku"},
"irist~burning-sage":{"haiku":"Fire and ritual purify ancient curses, burning away poison and contempt to reveal enchanting light beneath frozen fear.","opus":"The Atlanta progressive-metal band stage a cleansing ritual, using sacred smoke and talismanic imagery to scorch a curse out of body and spirit. Flame transforms rather than destroys, a buried spark reignited as desire that breaks apart the dark. Set against a fear that petrifies and drains life, the rite becomes a passage from paralysis toward light, the band's atmospheric heaviness poured into a myth of purging what rots within.","sonnet":"Ritual purification as personal transformation. The burning of sage works on body and soul simultaneously — scorching away a curse, lifting a poison fog, reawakening a dormant flame. What remains after the rite is magnetized desire replacing the loathing and fear that had drained the speaker still.","src":"haiku"},
"irist~creation":{"haiku":"The song portrays a spiritual awakening in which a confined soul is released from fear, beginning to reveal and condemn its inner nature. Sacred institutions wither away as sacrificial killing brings hollow peace.","opus":"A ritualistic reckoning with an inner architect that both leads astray and awakens a confined soul; through imagery of blood pacts, drying holy water, cut tongues and sacrificial killing, it frames a violent spiritual rebirth in which self-liberation comes by condemning old sacred structures and freeing the spirit from agony.","sonnet":"A song about a spiritual reckoning that dismantles false faith — holy water dries, altars sink, sacred things wither, and the narrator's soul is both confined and in the process of being freed as they strip away the lies of a corrupt architecture they once followed.","src":"haiku"},
"irist~dead-prayers":{"opus":"A thick, apocalyptic metal vision of decay and counterfeit illumination, where scavenger birds hatch beneath a glowing heaven and deceit overtakes the spirit. Malice festers in the skull as pleas to heaven go unheard, existence squandered and bound for the flames. Spiritual pointlessness smolders at its molten heart.","sonnet":"About predatory spiritual authority that instills fear to control — vultures born beneath a radiant sky spread lies that conquer the soul, sentence people to kneeling, and drive them back into fire; prayers offered to such a system are dead before they are spoken.","src":"sonnet"},
"irist~eons":{"haiku":"A tyrant rules eternally through oppression and decay, with tyranny lasting across eons despite constant suffering and dissent.","opus":"The progressive-metal band gives voice to a despot bragging of limitless dominion over cowed subjects, only for the passage of time to expose the hollowness of the boast. However vast, tyranny still crumbles, decays, and is forgotten; the same measure that clocks his reign guarantees its end, framing absolute power as ultimately fleeting.","sonnet":"A cosmic tyrant addresses its own permanence. Power flows from a burning star across eons, sustaining oppression past any single lifespan — yet the sands keep moving, and even the endless ruler senses dissent, erosion, and a fading that stretches further than any throne can reach.","src":"haiku"},
"irist~insurrection":{"opus":"An inner revolt against consuming pain and hatred, the narrator gazing into his own suffering and choosing to set himself alight. Through that self-destruction, love and loathing are reconciled and something new is meant to emerge from the ashes, framing agony as the price of rebirth.","sonnet":"About burning through a veil of psychological pain to reach something on the other side — the narrator stares through suffering, strips away whatever hides their true way, and frames the collapse as necessary: love and hatred unified into insurrection, drowning only to breathe forever.","src":"sonnet"},
"irist~order-of-the-mind":{"opus":"A confrontation with one's fractured self reflected in shattered mirror-glass, seeking to recreate consciousness and silence pain by breaking the reflection, escaping an inner abyss even at the cost of enduring the lie.","sonnet":"About self-harm as a coping mechanism for dissociation — confronting a fragmented reflection, the narrator finds that cutting into the veins is the one solution that silences the pain and shatters the mirrors of a fractured self.","src":"sonnet"},
"irist~severed":{"opus":"A fractured attempt to hold onto sanity and a lost person, oscillating between unforgiveness and the impossibility of pulling that other self free; the connection is severed and can't be repaired.","sonnet":"A person reels from a rupture with someone they cannot mentally separate from, unable to forgive or focus, questioning their own sanity as the bond between them remains torn but unresolved.","src":"sonnet"},
"iris~closer-to-real":{"haiku":"Visions and intuitions blur with reality; answers feel crystalline one moment and false the next, making it hard to distinguish genuine truth.","opus":"A synthpop meditation on losing the thread of what's real, chasing signs and premonitions that keep coming back wrong. It settles on the unsettling comfort that feeling is the closest to truth you'll get in a blurred reality.","sonnet":"A narrator trying to interpret signs and visions to find solid ground in reality keeps finding the signals false or misread — the search for something certain always leads back to something that only approximates real.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~2-am":{"haiku":"Post-work malaise traps the narrator in existential question about meaning and purpose, wondering if survival alone constitutes a life worth living.","opus":"The bleak isolation of a lonely working man coming home to late-night TV, questioning whether this meaningless routine is all life holds and whether to hold on or let go.","sonnet":"A solitary man comes home at two in the morning, sits with a beer in front of meaningless television, and confronts the possibility that this is all there is: canvas chair, closing walls, the question of whether to let go or carry on and take the hurt.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~2-minutes-to-midnight":{"haiku":"Nuclear war descends upon humanity through the indifference of mad leaders who profit from carnage and child destruction.","opus":"Named for the Doomsday Clock, a furious indictment of the machinery of war and the profiteers who feed it children for cash. Maiden strip the glamour off carnage to expose cynical men waltzing millions toward the slaughter.","sonnet":"War is exposed as a machine fed by propaganda, greed, and the bodies of children and civilians, run by madmen who make the population dance to carnage dressed as freedom. Two minutes to midnight on the Doomsday Clock, and the machine is still being oiled.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~22-acacia-avenue":{"haiku":"A troubled woman works as a sex worker, and sympathetic voices urge her to escape before her life and body are irreversibly damaged.","opus":"The band's return to Charlotte the Harlot, first leering along with the punters and then abruptly pleading with her to leave the life. That tonal whiplash, complicity curdling into rescue, is the uneasy heart of it.","sonnet":"A narrator describes a brothel and the woman Charlotte who works there, shifting between titillation and genuine, pleading concern for her wellbeing as she ages and is exploited. By the end he abandons the posture of client and tries to pull her out with him.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~blood-brothers":{"haiku":"Shared humanity and recurring cycles of violence bind all people together through guilt and complicity; we are brothers in both our capacity for harm and our suffering.","opus":"Steve Harris written after losing his father, using the garden of life to reckon with grief, inheritance and a world at war with itself. The blood-brothers refrain reaches for kinship as the only answer to shame and loss.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality, shared humanity, and the recurring failure of civilisation — seeing the father in the mirror, watching wars repeat across generations — concludes that beneath all division and violence, we remain bound to one another as blood brothers.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~brave-new-world":{"haiku":"Civilization devolves into authoritarianism and surveillance, trapping humanity in engineered illusion and manufactured compliance.","opus":"Leaning on Huxley, a vision of a civilization that traded its soul for comfort, beauty deemed unneeded and the masses planned and damned. Dread of a manufactured world where those who know the truth stay silent.","sonnet":"A vision of a world where beauty has no value, where the savage is abandoned, and where the structures of power have sold every soul into a new order that promises much and delivers only planned damnation. The speaker is dying in the wilderness of this realisation.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~childhood-s-end":{"haiku":"Global suffering persists while privilege goes unexamined; war and deprivation continue unseen by those insulated from consequence.","opus":"A survey of famine, war and pollution seen from the comfortable distance of those who take everything for granted while someone somewhere dies. The lament is our failure to learn, innocence lost at a planetary scale.","sonnet":"A global indictment of suffering, inequality, war, and environmental destruction, cataloguing starvation, tyranny, and pollution as evidence that humanity has failed its children and never learns.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~fear-is-the-key":{"haiku":"Fear controls civilization from within, shaping behavior and suppressing authentic expression while majorities enforce conformity through oppressive unanimity.","opus":"Written in the shadow of the AIDS crisis after Freddie Mercury's death, mourning a lost sexual freedom and raging that nobody cares until someone famous dies. Fear weaponized to control what people dare to be.","sonnet":"Fear is diagnosed as the mechanism by which political and social power subjugates individuals — the speaker recalls a freer past and rails against a world where the majority's fear overrides personal liberty and truth.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~hallowed-be-thy-name":{"haiku":"Facing execution, the speaker confronts mortality and divine indifference; cold certainty replaces fear, revealing earthly suffering as transient illusion.","opus":"A condemned prisoner's final hours before the gallows, moving from terror to a hard-won acceptance and belief that the soul lives on. Facing death, he concludes that life on earth is a strange illusion and something in him will fly beyond it.","sonnet":"A condemned man counts down his final hours in a cold cell before the gallows, cycling through terror, disbelief, and then a strange peace — \"after all I'm not afraid of dying, don't I believe that there never is an end?\" — arriving at the idea that life itself is the strange illusion.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~mother-russia-1998-remastered-version":{"haiku":"Imperial grandeur and revolutionary change reshape national identity; freedom demands reckoning with buried grief and historical loss.","opus":"Written as the Soviet Union fell, addressing Russia like an old sovereign waking, an old man reminiscing over a vanished empire. It sits with the ambivalence of newfound freedom, asking if the grief can finally lift.","sonnet":"An address to Russia at the moment of post-Soviet transformation, asking whether the country can release centuries of grief and authoritarian weight and hold its head up as something proud and finally free — honoring the grandeur of its imperial past while welcoming the change that has come.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~phantom-of-the-opera":{"haiku":"A pursuer stalks their prey, a phantom figure who haunts and terrifies, mesmerizing and torturing those in their grasp with sinister intent.","opus":"An early Iron Maiden epic from their debut that reframes the opera-house specter as hunter and hunted at once. The pursuer has stalked his prey a long while and swears it can't flee or shelter behind its disguise, yet the roles keep flipping: the phantom lords terror over everyone and invades the narrator's sleep. Cautions to stay clear and keep to the safe path collide with helpless capture by its hypnotic lure.","sonnet":"A tormented figure stalks an elusive, mask-wearing presence whose mesmerizing power leaves him helpless even as bystanders urge him to flee. The phantom haunts his dreams and waking hours alike, delivering psychological torment from the shadows of the theatrical wings.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~rainmaker":{"haiku":"Desert wanderers seek redemption through rainfall and shared pain, believing that collective change can wash away the cracks that bind us.","opus":"Written for the reunion with Dickinson and Smith, Maiden's rain becomes an image of collective renewal and change, shared pain rinsed clean, the fissures in people's lives closing the way drought-split ground heals in a downpour.","sonnet":"A wanderer in spiritual and emotional drought hears a promise of transformative rain — renewal that will seal the cracks in lives and ground alike — but the promised salvation stays conditional, hovering just beyond reach through repeated verses of longing.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~sanctuary":{"haiku":"Fleeing from both law and betrayal, the outlaw seeks refuge in isolation, having committed violence that transforms them into permanent exile.","opus":"A fugitive's plea, early Maiden channeling the outlaw as romantic anti-hero: a killing witnessed, a woman he can't quite face, and a run for cover where the only refuge left is a lover's bed for one more night before the law closes in.","sonnet":"A fugitive who has committed violence — apparently killed a woman — rides hard and appeals to a lover for sanctuary from the law, asking for shelter and intimacy in exchange for what he's done and what he must now survive.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~speed-of-light":{"haiku":"A speaker travels through space at impossible velocity, shedding human identity as they journey toward oblivion and cosmic dissolution.","opus":"A one-way voyage into the void staged as cosmic bravado, a shooting star burning up with no way back. Under the sci-fi swagger runs a plain fatalism, that nothing about us will save us, a willing slide into the dark.","sonnet":"A speaker hurtles through deep space on a one-way journey past event horizons and human time, finding that neither humanity nor memory can follow at this velocity. The song is a sublime dissolution — shooting plasma from a grave, slipping into the permanent dark.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~still-life-1998-remastered-version":{"haiku":"Supernatural forces drain the speaker's vitality through hypnotic compulsion; drowning becomes inevitable and peace.","opus":"A man hypnotized by faces beckoning from a pool, drained a little more each day until he drags his lover to drown alongside him. Madness rendered as a haunting, a suicidal pull that insists on taking someone with it.","sonnet":"A speaker is irresistibly drawn to faces he sees in the depths of a pool, losing strength and sanity as they drain his life blood and call him to join them; by the end he has surrendered to the compulsion, deciding to take someone with him into the water so they can drown and rest together forever.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~the-clairvoyant":{"haiku":"Clairvoyant power grows uncontrollably; the narrator grapples with escalating ability to see futures while fearing they cannot control the gift or prevent their own death.","opus":"A seer whose visionary gift becomes a curse, sharpening the awareness that dying begins at birth. Written after Maiden's manager consulted a psychic, it turns prophecy into a meditation on mortality no foresight can outrun.","sonnet":"A clairvoyant who could see through others' eyes and read their futures still could not foresee his own death, which becomes a meditation on mortality — everyone is dying from the moment they are born, and even supernatural sight cannot outrun that fact.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~the-number-of-the-beast":{"haiku":"A nightmare of demonic ritual and sacrifice haunts the speaker, blurring dream from reality as they're drawn toward the chaos. Evil returns to possess and burn through them.","opus":"The title track that fixed Iron Maiden's iconography opens with a scriptural warning before plunging a dreamer into a witnessed rite. He wakes uncertain whether the evil face twisting his mind was real or fantasy, then finds torches blazing, chants rising, and sacrifice underway. Drawn toward the mesmerizing horde against his will, he is finally possessed by the beast itself, which vows to return and make him burn.","sonnet":"A lone witness stumbles upon a nocturnal ritual invoking 666 and cannot determine whether the vision is real or madness. Drawn against his will toward the chanting circle, he watches fire and sacrifice and hears the beast promise to return and possess the living.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~the-number-of-the-beast-1998-remastered-version":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic beast rises in darkness as humanity witnesses final judgment. The number marks doom inscribed upon creation.","opus":"Built on the Revelation prophecy of 666, the narrator stumbles onto a torch-lit occult rite in the mist and can't tell nightmare from reality, drawn helplessly toward the crowd. Then the beast itself claims him, vowing to take him over and consume him.","sonnet":"A nightmare witnessed and absorbed: the narrator stumbles on a satanic ritual in the dark, is drawn mesmerized toward the chanting horde, and the Beast's voice closes the song — \"I'll possess your body and I'll make you burn\" — collapsing the line between observer and participant.","src":"haiku"},
"iron-maiden~wasting-love":{"haiku":"Time devours opportunity and connection; the years pass in loneliness and emotional emptiness while love withers into regret and shadow.","opus":"A ballad about drifting through life squandering the capacity to love, filling days with empty flings while real connection slips past on the tide. Restlessness dressed as freedom, and the loneliness underneath it.","sonnet":"A road-weary wanderer watches others search for love while he drifts through empty days, acknowledging the loneliness of his life but offering no solution — just the resigned recognition that time covers everything.","src":"haiku"},
"irving-force~corporate-killer":{"opus":"A satirical portrait of the corporate world as literal assassination, where the ambitious employee, driven by the grand design and threats to conform, becomes a killer whose weapons are layoffs, liquidations, and severance packages.","sonnet":"A person has been fully absorbed by corporate culture to the point where they are functionally a hired killer — the language of business euphemism (downsizer, liquidation manager, severance administrator) is explicitly equated with assassination and murder for hire.","src":"sonnet"},
"irving-force~do-your-job":{"haiku":"Corporate servitude demands unquestioning obedience and surrenders human rights; those who consent to contracts become slaves bound to perpetual exploitation.","opus":"A darksynth broadside against corporate servitude and the surrender of the soul to a faceless employer. Delivered as a barked command, it portrays workers who sign away their rights and pledge eternal obedience for a paycheck. The relentless repetition mirrors dehumanizing labor itself, reaching toward fury at a system that treats people as replaceable, kneeling instruments.","sonnet":"A corporate voice issues commands to a worker who has signed away their rights and equity in perpetuity: shut your mouth, close your eyes, obey the entity that bought your soul, or be replaced — the word \"slave\" appears without quotation marks, stripping the corporate language of its euphemisms entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"irving-force~touch-the-corpse":{"haiku":"A machine or entity obsessively investigates corpses to extract data and control humanity, unable to complete its mission.","opus":"A darkly comic horror sketch narrated by a data-hungry machine intelligence fixated on the dead, harvesting biological samples in a bid to dominate all people. Its chanted craving to handle a body is pure B-movie dread played half for camp. Irving Force works in darksynth, the aggressive cinematic strain of retro electronica, scoring the whole thing like a killer-robot nightmare.","sonnet":"A forensic AI or investigator fixated on the need to examine a corpse for data — flesh and blood reduced to information to be mined, the dead body stripped of personhood and treated as a resource in a mission to control. The song plays the premise entirely straight, its obsessive repetition making it more absurd than sinister, a deadpan satire of surveillance and data extraction as a kind of grotesque desire.","src":"haiku"},
"irving-force~violence-suppressor":{"haiku":"Authoritarian control and systematic enforcement; technological oppression masking as order.","opus":"A dystopian synth vignette of state brutality, imagining a hi-tech enforcer with dead eyes who executes dissidents and gangs alike in the name of order. Conform or the streets run red is its logic. It reaches toward cyberpunk dread of authoritarian control, the menacing arrival of a machine built to crush any deviation from the law.","sonnet":"A militarised enforcer — dead-eyed behind shades, plasma weapon ready — addresses street gangs and dissidents with one command: obey or bleed. Pure authoritarian menace delivered as threat, not ideology.","src":"haiku"},
"islander~the-outsider":{"haiku":"A declaration of lifelong outsider status and identity. The speaker accepts their role as marginalized and refuses to change, resigned to their position on the periphery.","opus":"An anthem of defiant self-acceptance, in which the speaker embraces always having been an outsider, refuses to change who he is, and voices exhaustion at waiting and chasing fading dreams.","sonnet":"A resigned self-portrait of someone who has always existed on the margins, too broken and exhausted to pretend otherwise, accepting that being an outsider is simply who they are.","src":"haiku"},
"isotopes~flower":{"opus":"A romanticization of dying alongside a lover, the narrator asking to be covered in flowers like a corpse and have a dagger forced through his heart, blurring passionate love and a death wish while wondering if this intensity is real love or just fear of loneliness.","sonnet":"About a new romantic obsession so intense it takes on the imagery of death and decay — the narrator wants to be completely consumed by the other person, covered in flowers like a corpse, drained of impurity, finding the idea of dying alongside them somehow perfect.","src":"sonnet"},
"isotopes~rip":{"haiku":"Withdraws into solitude after betrayal by those trusted, finding peace in isolation at the bottom. Others' manipulation proves that loneliness is safety.","opus":"A plunge into isolation, where the people the narrator keeps nearest prove to be the ones bleeding him dry, pushing him to reject counterfeit affection and settle willingly into loneliness as his dwelling place. Doubt and venomous companions drive him past logic toward wishing to lie beneath the earth. Set in crushing, breakdown-heavy metalcore, it treats retreat not as surrender but as a bitter, chosen wish to be abandoned to his thoughts.","sonnet":"Betrayal by the people closest to you — people who held you up only to throw you down — pushes the speaker toward total withdrawal. Poisoned by fake love and hollowed out by self-doubt, they stop fighting the pull toward solitude and choose it instead, accepting the depths as the only honest home left. The ending carries no hope of rescue, just a grim, clear-eyed peace with sinking.","src":"haiku"},
"issues~hooligans":{"haiku":"Loyalty to a tight-knit community of misfits offers belonging even as individual ambition pulls away; the speaker fears losing both the bonds and the people who made him.","opus":"Young musicians justifying the gamble of chasing a career while old friends feel abandoned, insisting success hasn't changed them. Loyalty to roots weighed against ambition, defiant but tender toward the people left behind.","sonnet":"A young man from California navigates the tension between chasing solo ambition at the top and staying loyal to the friends he grew up with, insisting he hasn't changed even as the road carries him further from where he started.","src":"haiku"},
"issues~tapping-out":{"haiku":"Emotional abuse accumulates until one can no longer endure the weight, tapping out of a relationship drowning in drama and manipulation despite love's initial promise.","opus":"A relationship rendered as a boxing match the singer is losing, worn down by constant verbal blows until he surrenders, tapping out of trust he now regrets.","sonnet":"A relationship reaches its breaking point as the speaker, pinned against the ropes by a partner's low blows and drama, finally taps out — exhausted by the weight, unable to make one last round, and incredulous that trust was so foolishly placed.","src":"haiku"},
"istasha~cellcast":{"haiku":"Dissolution of identity through substance and despair as the speaker drowns in numbness and failed attempts at transformation. The erosion of the self becomes a cyclical trap of toxins, disconnection, and death-in-life.","opus":"A dissociative purge of an old self, addiction and self-loathing swirling as the narrator drowns identity to reinvent from nothing. Everything tastes the same, friends already dead inside, names deleted like a wiped disk, the craving for change indistinguishable from the craving that erodes him.","sonnet":"A portrait of deliberate self-erasure through drugs, detox, and identity overwrite — the old self is dead, the new face is red, the heart erodes, and the narrator flows through a riptide of backstabs and coercion while systematically deleting the names of everyone who once mattered. \"Buzz like static in my ear, I hang up that shit like a phone\" is the defining gesture: discard before you're discarded.","src":"haiku"},
"ithaca~they-fear-us":{"opus":"A vengeful hymn of the long-crushed at last becoming the predator, patient across ages and now bearing down. The prey turn into the hunters, ordering their abuser to kneel before their blood, their sovereign, and their deity while every route of flight seals shut. Fury tempered by endurance crowns itself, a turnabout that leaves old authority helpless.","sonnet":"A revenge fantasy spoken from the position of the long-subordinated reclaiming power — someone who once escaped now has the tables turned and is hunted down, forced to bow before those they wronged or dismissed. The voice is cold, triumphant, and explicitly deity-like, demanding worship and ending any appeals to God.","src":"sonnet"},
"ivri~inversion":{"haiku":"Ego death and bodily dissolution release the self from blame and self-hatred; the speaker learns to let go and fades willingly into non-existence.","opus":"A spare reckoning with a breakup and self-forgiveness: newly alone, the narrator feels hollowed out and imagines drifting off into quiet, out of his own body. Refusing self-hatred, he resolves to absorb the lesson, own his mistakes and leave in peace.","sonnet":"After a loss the speaker's chest is wide open, the world inverted, and the only path is to go out of body — a figure sitting up above themselves — learning the lesson, regretting the wrongs, and making an exit without self-hatred this time.","src":"haiku"},
"ivy-lab-roses-gabor~can-t-say-no":{"haiku":"Hypnotic control binds two figures in an inescapable dance where the speaker orchestrates desire while maintaining dominance through psychological manipulation.","opus":"Total erotic control worn as effortless magic, the speaker having already seen through and captured the other before the game begins. The seduction is a foregone conclusion, spun like a web while the prey wonders why they can't refuse.","sonnet":"A seductress asserting total psychological dominance — she sees through her target's games and tests, knows exactly what they want, and has already ensnared them in her web before they realize it, leaving them incapable of refusing her.","src":"haiku"},
"ivy-lab~can-t-say-no":{"haiku":"A seducer wields psychological manipulation and hypnotic control over a victim who cannot resist their pull. Power is gained through illusion and entrapment.","opus":"A seducer's power trip dressed as bass music, the narrator toying with a lover already caught and enjoying the certainty that resistance is theatre. It lands because the swagger is calm rather than desperate, control worn like something already won.","sonnet":"A confident seducer who knows exactly how to draw someone in — using magnetism, reading through their games and rules, positioning themselves as irresistible, and watching their target get caught in the web.","src":"haiku"},
"izzy-camina~alprazalam":{"haiku":"Chemical respite cradles the damaged: medication promises beauty and connection, yet underneath runs the current of knowing nothing's truly fixed.","opus":"Self-medicating through the haze while reaching out to a fellow self-destructive soul, insisting neither of you is alone or expected to be perfect. Xanax and codeine numb the clutter, but tenderness is the real offer.","sonnet":"A song that pairs pharmaceutical sedation with romantic longing — alprazolam and promethazine quiet the clutter in your head, and the person the speaker is in love with has the same effect, both offering a dreamy escape from pain.","src":"haiku"},
"izzy-camina~loser-deadbeat":{"haiku":"Dreams predict resurrection and reunion; the speaker navigates between waking delusion and intuitive certainty that lost love returns inevitably.","opus":"A defiant vow not to let a worthless partner wound her, steeled with talk of protectors watching over her and a fixed, ruthless focus. Under the armor runs a dreamy ache for a real sweetheart she keeps glimpsing in omens she can't quite read.","sonnet":"Someone swings between a defiant vow never to let a worthless person break their heart and a longing fantasy of a sweetheart returning — tough on the surface but still chasing a love they suspect is out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"izzy-reign~broken-by-design":{"haiku":"Mechanical emotional dysfunction traps the speaker; wires crossed create perpetual malfunction beyond repair.","opus":"Self-loathing framed as a machine built broken and beyond repair, numb like Novocaine and terrified of feeling nothing. The speaker warns others to walk away from the toxic space inside her while confessing it's an inseparable part of who she is.","sonnet":"A person who feels like a malfunctioning machine — wires crossed, numbed by emotional Novocaine, unable to fake it anymore, and operating on a corrupted script they can't rewrite, inviting the listener into their private hell while warning them it isn't safe.","src":"haiku"},
"jackie~unspun":{"haiku":"A lover destabilizes the speaker's carefully maintained emotional boundaries, triggering both desire and uncertainty about whether the connection is genuine intimacy or mere physical attraction. Control is surrendered completely.","opus":"Jackie frames infatuation as a giddy surrender of control. After sealing herself off and laboring for security, the narrator meets a stranger who undoes all of it, conceding the pairing could be ruinous yet diving in delighted. She seizes the upper hand, vowing to stir envy and branding the other her latest obsession. The track treats reckless attraction as a game worth throwing.","sonnet":"Attraction powerful enough to dismantle carefully constructed emotional defences arrives in a single encounter, and the speaker surrenders to it with eyes open, unsure whether what follows is love or just the night. The power dynamic tilts back and forth — desire as both destabilising force and game played for advantage — leaving the outcome deliberately unresolved.","src":"haiku"},
"jackson-rose~saw-trap":{"haiku":"Calculated vengeance and psychotic clarity merge as the speaker announces retribution with surgical precision, transforming victimhood into monstrous agency.","opus":"A revenge fantasy from someone pushed past breaking by being treated like an animal, promising torture-movie retribution. The mundane grievance underneath — small humiliations taken hour by hour — curdles into a threat that whatever he does won't come close to squaring the debt.","sonnet":"Violent revenge fantasy against those who treat the speaker like a dog: feeding resentment through another workday with a harder-to-fake grin, the speaker imagines cutting and trapping and gutting the people who leech at them every hour, until the whole world becomes a saw trap for anyone who underestimated them.","src":"haiku"},
"jaden-smith~way-up":{"haiku":"Ascension from struggle through deliberate action and collective force, the speaker positioning himself as a transformative leader whose rise inspires transcendence.","opus":"A triumphant superhero fantasy of ascent and civic heroism, casting the narrator as a savior fighting for the city and its people. Every enemy stands down as he rises above the clouds, swinging through streets to save the day. Buoyant and youthful, it reaches for the euphoria of winning against doubters and being believed in as a chosen leader.","sonnet":"A superhero-register triumph narrative — the narrator went from boy to man, fought for his city, made a perfect plan, and now orbits above the clouds while everyone cheers. The opposition backed down, the streets are being cleaned, and the collective 'we' that won keeps rising.","src":"haiku"},
"jain~makeba-album-version":{"haiku":"The song celebrates Makeba's embodied resistance and beauty as the antidote to oppression, evoking dance, breath, and the liberation of African dignity.","opus":"A joyous tribute to Miriam Makeba, the exiled South African singer who carried anti-apartheid protest into pop. Jain turns her name into a dance chant, honoring Mama Africa as a figure of human rights whose voice outlasts the suffering it answered.","sonnet":"A celebratory tribute to Miriam Makeba — Mama Africa — whose singing, beauty, and defiant spirit embodied human rights and gave hope to millions, her presence an unstoppable force that makes bodies move and hearts open.","src":"haiku"},
"jaixj~reaching-out":{"haiku":"Parasitic obsession drives the speaker toward salvation fantasy while poison consumes from within.","opus":"Fixation on someone toxic reframed as a savior fantasy, the singer clinging to the role of protector even as the attachment poisons him in his sleep. Wanting to be a one-winged angel who saves her substitutes for admitting she is destroying him.","sonnet":"Drawn to someone toxic despite knowing better — \"you're no good for me\" — the speaker keeps seeking them out while fantasising about being a one-winged angel who can save them; the reaching and seeking loop without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"jakub-zytecki~bonsai":{"haiku":"Life contained and cultivated within constraints; joy and suffering alternate as the speaker dances near transformation or death.","opus":"A guitarist's tender, almost childlike expression of creative devotion, born from swimming in a lake of emotion and giving birth to melodies. The narrator addresses the future with newfound wisdom and fearless trust, ready to bring inner abundance into the world. It reaches for the joy of art as living and dreaming braided together, sung with sweet, unguarded sincerity.","sonnet":"A light-footed self-portrait of an artist's inner life: dancing, crying, swimming in emotion, giving birth to melodies — the speaker has much to give but only so much to say, and the address to the future is one of readiness rather than anxiety, offered with total trust and no fear.","src":"haiku"},
"jakub-zytecki~heart":{"haiku":"A watcher grieves as sentiment dies in real time. Chasing an elusive other becomes running without direction—the heart tears apart from effort.","opus":"Grief for a relationship dying in front of him, and the ache of living inside memory where the other person keeps growing more beautiful the more they slip away; he can't let go even as the heart tears apart.","sonnet":"A song about the loss of a love that once felt entirely new — the narrator watches the relationship die before them, tries to trade the present for a preserved past moment, chases the other person who keeps retreating, and feels their heart being torn apart by the inability to let go of something already gone.","src":"haiku"},
"jakub-zytecki~light-a-fire-fight-a-liar":{"haiku":"Language becomes the battlefield where authenticity fights convention, the speaker and lover both searching for truth buried under layers of social courtesy and performed selves.","opus":"A quest to rebuild love and meaning by reassembling language letter by letter, exhausted by the performed courtesy a city runs on. The cure for social catastrophe is small and human, to stop pretending and just find the other person and dance.","sonnet":"Two people in a city who both feel the gap between authentic connection and the hollow politeness modern life demands — the speaker is exhausted by performing a false self and yearns to find the other person in the streets and let pure movement dissolve the social catastrophe they share.","src":"haiku"},
"jakub-zytecki~remind-me":{"haiku":"Words and purpose become weapons against internal chaos, yet contradiction pulls the speaker apart as they demand action while drowning in doubt.","opus":"A voice lodged in the head belongs to someone who wanted to save the world but couldn't fix themselves, and following their ideals cost real time and real dreams. Letting that voice and its arguments go is the only way forward.","sonnet":"Someone reflects on a consuming belief or obsession — possibly religious or ideological — that filled them with purpose but also cost them relationships and clarity, leaving them to untangle what was genuine need and what was propaganda.","src":"haiku"},
"jamal~6-0-6":{"haiku":"Longing and loss in a relationship; the speaker loved with intensity but couldn't understand what went wrong, burning internally while searching for meaning.","opus":"A lover clings to a single remembered phone number while losing every day, breath, and sleep, framing the wreckage of a consuming romance as a war fought without gravity. The Polish reggae-fusion band Jamal blend patois-flecked rap into their tribute, letting six hundred and six needless words in the narrator's head stand for a love he cannot fully release.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper circles the wreckage of a love so consuming it reshaped his sense of gravity, still memorising a phone number he can no longer dial. Days bleed together without sleep or oxygen while the universe keeps spinning, indifferent to the wound carved where a heart used to sit.","src":"haiku"},
"jamal~defto":{"haiku":"Isolation and emotional distance between two people, where connection is blocked and loneliness persists despite proximity and time together.","opus":"A Polish-language meditation on a failing relationship and deep loneliness, where words wound like a knife and the two can no longer reach the heights they once shared. Amid images of the city at night and slow dying, the narrator concludes that loneliness stands at both the beginning and the end.","sonnet":"A Polish-language meditation on a failing relationship in which words come easily to one person but cut like a knife to the other; as the connection collapses, the narrator walks on alone through a city where solitude is both the beginning and the end of every bond.","src":"haiku"},
"jamal~kr-lowa":{"haiku":"Two people pursuing each other across distance and doubt cling to their love as the only thing that matters, asking to keep chasing that fire as if for the first time.","opus":"Jamal sing in Polish of a love threatened by fear and distance, urging the beloved to chase whatever fire can still be salvaged between them. The reggae-rooted Polish band picture two people reduced to a scattering of alien pixels, swimming against the current, insisting that only love remains beneath everything hidden behind a thousand colors.","sonnet":"A Polish love song about two people separated by distance, digital mediation, and the fear of being truly seen. The speaker urges pursuit of whatever fire still burns between them, knowing time and tears are running out. Despite feeling like strangers made of mismatched pixels, the only thing that matters is that she exists.","src":"haiku"},
"jamal~peron":{"haiku":"Desperate longing and codependency; someone needs another to arrange their emotional life but watches that person leave.","opus":"The Polish reggae-and-soul collective builds an aching plea from railway imagery. Lovers run at each other backwards, hurl a wall at peas, and are remade tomorrow from dust and gunpowder; the trivial becomes vital, and the beloved's voice can be seen. The refrain begs her to stay, to arrange his dreams and tears since one of each is surely her. They lost the code, mistook the platform, and the distance between them keeps growing.","sonnet":"A man pleads with a departing lover to stay and restore order to his dreams and tears, confessing that whatever he holds inside resists being split in half. They lost each other somewhere — missed the right platform — and he runs toward her already knowing she is no longer waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"jamal~tubaka":{"haiku":"Society chases shallow desires—money, fame, women—at the cost of spiritual poverty and broken brotherhood. The speaker critiques a culture where profit corrupts everything.","opus":"A critique of greed and hollow ambition in music and life, where everyone chases more emotions, more money, more women, and big-budget videos, building walls between themselves while grasping for the clouds; Jamal laments that in the name of banal popularity people become poorer in spirit and even brother sells brother, inviting the listener closer to music that means something real.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper calls the audience close to share music as a genuine bond while critiquing everyone's hunger for more — more TV, more women, bigger profits, bigger videos — arguing that the chase for popularity hollows people out and brother sells brother in a civilisation thousands of years old.","src":"haiku"},
"james-and-the-cold-gun~a-new-sensation":{"haiku":"Two distant people are out of sync—one cold and disconnected, the other trying to reach them. They're caught in a cycle of wanting and losing faith.","opus":"Directed at someone frozen and giving up hope, teetering emotionally, whom the narrator wants to shock into feeling alive again. Under the flirtation is fatigue, a sense of running out of hours and tiring of a fruitless struggle, and doubt about whether he's squandering his own belief on a person happy in isolation.","sonnet":"About a relationship where one person has emotionally checked out and is craving something new to fill the void — the narrator recognizes they're out of sync, has stopped fighting the tide, and offers to be that new feeling even as he doubts it'll matter. It's tired love approaching its endpoint.","src":"haiku"},
"james-and-the-cold-gun~alone-again":{"haiku":"Isolated and self-medicating with intimacy, the speaker resigns to solitude, drinking down another person as remedy while knowing connection won't last or fulfill.","opus":"A cycle of self-sabotaging attachment where the speaker treats a lover as a drug to down before retreating into isolation once more. He half-warns them away, conceding that things always sour the moment real emotion enters, leaving him repeatedly stranded by his own patterns.","sonnet":"About using another person as a sedative against loneliness — the narrator sees themselves clearly enough to warn the other person away, but keeps coming back anyway, drinking someone down like medicine without being able to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"james-and-the-cold-gun~cheating-on-the-sun":{"haiku":"The narrator, bleeding and falling from grace, betrays the light by pursuing a forbidden nocturnal connection despite its damage.","opus":"A hazy, self-destructive romance where the narrator bleeds himself dry chasing a want he can't resist, a stranger by daylight who'd rather live at night. Cheating the sun stands for choosing ruin over the sober morning.","sonnet":"A drifter in emotional freefall — bleeding out and aware of it, becoming a stranger to whoever they wake up next to — frames their dissolution as a kind of betrayal of daylight, of the expectation that life should progress forward.","src":"haiku"},
"james-and-the-cold-gun~chewing-glass":{"haiku":"Betrayal leaves the speaker bloodied and defeated; they sacrifice truth while others escape unscathed.","opus":"A catalogue of misfortune and self-caused ruin, a life built on shifting ground, forever coming in last, coughing up blood while an indifferent woman keeps twisting the knife. Getting through the day feels like a fight the narrator was never trained for.","sonnet":"A song about being systematically undone by bad luck and a person who does not care — the speaker built on unstable foundations, got tricked by a pretty lie, and now spits blood while refusing to be a soldier in a life that has become a firefight; exhaustion and bitterness are the dominant emotional notes.","src":"haiku"},
"james-and-the-cold-gun~headlights":{"haiku":"Desperate pursuit through storm; racing toward another while drowning in weather and distance.","opus":"A road song of yearning propulsion, the narrator driving lonely rain-slicked miles with a lover as the only destination, beams cutting the black as the city drops into the mirror. Romantic and restless, it reaches for the promise that the love waiting ahead will light the way somewhere better.","sonnet":"A driver burning through the night to reach someone they haven't held in too long, the headlights pointing the way as the city disappears in the rearview. Pure unambiguous longing — I can feel you in my arms, I can almost taste your kiss — the road collapsing the distance.","src":"haiku"},
"james-and-the-cold-gun~long-way-home":{"haiku":"Someone takes the long way, avoiding forward momentum or backward reflection, stuck in cycles and replays, refusing to kneel or surrender to others' judgment.","opus":"A refusal to conform for the approval of shallow people, the singer taking the roundabout, harder route rather than kneel at anyone's crossroads, insisting he owes belief to no one who calls him worthless.","sonnet":"About drifting away from someone who once mattered — the narrator has been taking the long way, lost in repetition, seeing through the other person's performed perfection, and ultimately refusing to kneel or be diminished by their dismissal.","src":"haiku"},
"james-and-the-cold-gun~plug-me-in":{"haiku":"The song expresses emotional numbness and a desperate need to feel something, using metaphors of being plugged in or activated to convey hunger for vitality and sensation in a world of disconnection and desperation.","opus":"A craving to feel anything at all amid numbness and aimlessness; the speaker, whose heart is trapped, repeatedly begs to be plugged in and turned on so he can escape feeling ignored.","sonnet":"A person numbed by emotional disconnection and chaotic circumstances — failed relationships, aimlessness, violence around them — desperately craves stimulation and feeling, demanding to be switched on and made to feel something real.","src":"haiku"},
"james-and-the-cold-gun~seven":{"haiku":"Futility in escape; lost and adrift with nowhere to go but deeper into void.","opus":"A bleak reckoning with feeling lost and running out of options, where escape seems the only safe bet before a relationship implodes. Numbness gives way to a violent red rage the narrator deems better than emptiness. Circling and self-aware, it reaches toward the desperate wish to be remembered and to make something real before reaching the end.","sonnet":"A soul-search concluded with nothing — no hope in the hurricane, vacant and blue, the bad taste of better days that won't erase — when seconds from the end the color shifts from blue to red, and the speaker wants to be deliberate, to be remembered, to decide if what is happening is real or pretend, a violent heart being better than none.","src":"haiku"},
"james-and-the-cold-gun~she-moves":{"haiku":"A person struggles with cyclical emotional manipulation in a toxic relationship, unable to escape the trap.","opus":"A man is caught by a woman who manipulates him like a marionette and sets snares he keeps insisting he won't step into, mesmerized by her every motion. The chilling closing image of a nighttime killing suggests the obsession has soured into bloodshed, its perpetrator or beneficiary left deliberately ambiguous.","sonnet":"A man is magnetically trapped by a woman he knows is bad for him — he recognizes the pattern, knows he's been here before, can't escape the hold she has, and the song ends abruptly with the admission that he took a life with a knife.","src":"haiku"},
"james-and-the-cold-gun~two-day-wish":{"haiku":"Someone experiences floating detachment and self-indulgence, trapped in paralysis and unable to decide or act, observing life slip away unused.","opus":"A raw account of depression as total paralysis: the narrator hasn't risen from bed in ages, letting himself decay while the outside world dims. The gut-punch is that something worthwhile is locked away in his mind that he can't reach, and time keeps slipping by while fear and indecision leave him frozen.","sonnet":"Depression and creative paralysis — the narrator is stuck in bed, can't decide anything, is terrified, and knows there's something beautiful trapped inside their head that the illness won't let them reach, with the title signaling a wish to only have two days left rather than continue like this.","src":"haiku"},
"james-arthur~say-you-won-t-let-go":{"haiku":"Lifetime commitment emerges from instant connection; the speaker vows eternal togetherness forged in a single night.","opus":"A whole relationship compressed into one arc, from a drunken first night to growing old together, framed as a plea against loss. The vow lands because it imagines the ordinary future as the real romance.","sonnet":"A man traces the arc of a love from its first tentative night through decades of shared life — children, grey hair, death — making a single enduring request: that she promise not to let go of what they have built together. The song spans a lifetime in a few verses with unusual emotional completeness.","src":"haiku"},
"james-bailey~shooting-star":{"haiku":"Cosmic desire merges with earthly sensuality as the narrator pursues a meteoric lover, ecstatic connection promising transcendence.","opus":"A braggadocious seduction where a woman who fell out of the sky becomes a shooting star to wish on, mixing cosmic imagery with explicit lust. Beneath the swagger runs a real question of whether she's here to conquer, entertain, or genuinely stay.","sonnet":"A man is captivated by a woman who fell out of the sky — working it enough to make a grown man cry — and debates her intentions while declaring she is his shooting star, offering ecstasy if she'll only stay.","src":"haiku"},
"james-blunt~love-me-better":{"haiku":"Growth through vulnerability; the speaker finally surrenders to devotion after shallow wandering.","opus":"A self-aware cad admitting his reputation and his wandering eye, then crediting one woman with steadying him. Redemption pitched by a man who knows he doesn't deserve it, which is what makes the gratitude ring true.","sonnet":"A man with a history of shallow entanglements and self-protective mediocrity acknowledges that one person has finally loved him in a way that surpasses everyone before, not through grand gesture but through consistent, better love that he has never quite deserved until now. The confession is disarming in its honesty about his own past failures.","src":"haiku"},
"james-ivy~snakes":{"haiku":"Manipulative people drain the speaker of blood and feeling, creating parasitic relationships where they feed on vulnerability. The speaker sees snakes and feels poisoned, caught in a cycle of mutual harm.","opus":"A restless confession of emotional numbness and self-sabotage, craving anything that makes him feel while a leeching relationship drains him, aware he's both prey and something sharpened into a predator.","sonnet":"Caught in a parasitic relationship with someone who feeds off the narrator's suffering, the song orbits the simultaneous desire to feel nothing and feel something — numbness as relief, sensation as proof of life — while recognizing the other person has poisoned the well.","src":"haiku"},
"jamie-lenman~like-me-better":{"opus":"A wry, self-deprecating song about someone who initially found the singer an arrogant, unattractive prick but has, over time, come around to actually liking him.","sonnet":"The narrator observes, with dry satisfaction, that someone who initially found him arrogant and off-putting has gradually come around — what used to irritate now endears, and the reversal is quiet but unmistakable.","src":"sonnet"},
"jamie-s-elsewhere~escapist":{"haiku":"Monotony and conformity grind the speaker into foreign shape; daydreaming offers temporary escape while loops tighten, and the signal reaches only the void.","opus":"A metalcore portrait of soul-killing routine, the narrator locked in a cycle of drudgery and conformity, worn to powder by identical days. Daydreaming is the only exit, an imagined door onto a horizon where water meets sky. It reaches toward the suffocation of being forced into a shape that isn't his and the hunger to break out of the machine.","sonnet":"A person ground down by the monotony of daily programming and conformity — correcting conflicting code, shouting into the void — escapes only inward, imagining an open door to a place where sea meets sun, with no way to actually cross the threshold.","src":"haiku"},
"jamiroquai~mr-moon":{"haiku":"Cosmic synchronisation determines romantic destiny; the narrator surrenders control to celestial timing, trusting alignment over agency.","opus":"Falling into a love that feels cosmically fated yet keeps slipping away, half spiritual and half physical yearning. The moon becomes the DJ of desire, playing the tune that finally lets him connect.","sonnet":"The speaker is helplessly in love, crediting a cosmic alignment — embodied as Mr Moon — for bringing him and a woman together, and asking the moon to keep playing the right tune so the connection holds.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~been-caught-stealing":{"haiku":"The speaker and girlfriend steal casually and unrepentantly from stores, treating it as simple self-service.","opus":"A gleeful anthem to shoplifting for its own sake, the narrator confessing he simply enjoys taking what he wants without paying, his girlfriend an equally happy accomplice slipping goods under her shirt. There is no guilt, only the thrill of walking straight out the door. Jane's Addiction were the LA alt-rock provocateurs who helped birth Lollapalooza, and this 1990 Ritual de lo Habitual single is their loosest, most infectiously funky crowd-pleaser.","sonnet":"Shoplifting as a philosophy of life — if you can get away with it, it's yours. The speaker steals for the pleasure of it, his girlfriend does the same, and together they laugh over the haul with no guilt whatsoever. The song is a cheerful, unapologetic declaration that desire plus the willingness to act is all the justification you need.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~curiosity-kills":{"haiku":"Unhealthy curiosity drives self-destruction; someone cannot look away from temptation and dies for it, trapped by compulsion. The song mourns how fascination and desire can become fatal.","opus":"Perry Farrell addressing someone who chose to stop aging by ending it, a suicide framed as fatal curiosity about what lies past the edge. He insists what he feels runs deeper than love while grieving the one who couldn't look away.","sonnet":"An elegy for someone who died young — apparently by suicide or overdose — the narrator studying an old photo and mourning the person who decided to stop aging, couldn't pull their mind away from whatever drew them toward the end.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~end-to-the-lies":{"haiku":"A gossip is obsessed with talking about someone, mistaking obsession for love, while the speaker demands an end to the lying and pity.","opus":"Perry Farrell's kiss-off to a hanger-on who trades on old glory and can't stop talking about him, needling that obsession curdled into a kind of love, and demanding the pretense finally stop.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a gossip or backstabber who talks about the narrator constantly — calling out their obsession, their dishonesty, and demanding an end to the cycle of lies.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~everybody-s-friend":{"haiku":"An acknowledgment that attempting to please everyone leads to betrayal and exhaustion, rejecting the impossible burden of universal approval.","opus":"A weary reckoning with a friendship that soured, addressed to a man who walked away and took the narrator's trust apart, arriving at the plain, repeated truth that no one can be everyone's friend. Beneath the shrug sits real hurt over a bond that faked its way to an end. From Jane's Addiction, the pioneering LA alternative band, it trades their earlier decadence for something rueful and direct about the limits of loyalty.","sonnet":"A friendship or alliance that dissolved without warning, the other person gone mid-conversation, the last meeting revealed in hindsight as hollow. The speaker works through confusion and resignation, arriving at the blunt conclusion that trying to be everything to everyone is impossible — you cannot be everybody's friend, and pretending otherwise is its own kind of betrayal.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~hypersonic":{"haiku":"A celebration of speed, escape, and transcendence through rapid movement and technological ambition, leaving the world behind for higher speeds and aspirations.","opus":"A speed-crazed daydream of flight and getaway, the narrator crowing about renewable fuel, dizzying velocity, and launching whole metropolises skyward while shrugging off slow talkers and messy commitments. Beneath the sci-fi bluster runs a craving to break free of people who hold everyone hostage. From the veteran LA alt-rock band Jane's Addiction, it channels their swagger into a hurtling hymn to sheer velocity.","sonnet":"Speed as liberation and ideology — the speaker wants to move so fast that entanglements, parasitic relationships, and the slow drag of dependence on fossil fuel become irrelevant. There's political swagger here, a dismissal of terrorists and barrel-keepers alike, as the song accelerates through the image of hydrogen-powered flight into pure velocity, leaving no trace behind.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~i-ll-hit-you-back":{"haiku":"Escalating conflict where a speaker tries to be the 'bigger man' but ultimately abandons restraint to retaliate against attacks, both physical and verbal.","opus":"Farrell wrestles with the pressure to always be the bigger man, and finally rejects it: he won't strike first, but retaliation is a promise. It's about the limits of turning the other cheek when you've already been besieged.","sonnet":"A person who has been trying to be the bigger man in a conflict decides he is done absorbing blows — he will hit back, the idealism of restraint replaced by the desire for direct retaliation.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~irresistable-force-met-the-immovable-object":{"haiku":"Collision breeds cosmic transformation; two unstoppable forces merge into expanded singularity, merger replacing all separate existence.","opus":"Farrell frames an enormous sexual and cosmic crash as the birth of the universe, the band's rise imagined as two star-systems colliding while even phantoms tune in. The boastful, ecstatic catastrophe insists this was no fluke but two irresistible powers finally meeting their equal.","sonnet":"Two massive forces — people or energies — colliding with cosmic intensity, generating shock waves and spectacle; framed as a big bang, a galaxy merger, the song celebrates the explosive, creative, possibly sexual collision of two things that cannot coexist without transforming each other.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~just-because":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts selfishness and ingratitude, observing that success without earning it breeds emptiness and social isolation.","opus":"A jeering face-off with a smug, entitled coaster who has everything yet is loved by no one, the recurring warning that they ought to have seen it coming, plus the memory of a poorly chosen present, hinting at a ruined relationship the narrator now savors calling out, telling them their laziness has cost them all of it.","sonnet":"A cutting dismissal of someone who has always taken and never given — they have the most but nobody loves them because love can't be purchased, and the song is a quiet, devastating verdict that they should have known all along.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~mountain-song":{"haiku":"A wanderer descends from the mountains claiming independence and demanding others recognize their refusal to compromise.","opus":"Perry Farrell's coded hymn to the high, coming down the mountain and cashing in a shared craving with a fellow addict. The euphoria and danger of surrender wrapped in Jane's ecstatic LA underground.","sonnet":"A descent from the mountain brings an encounter with someone who shares the same suppressed, bursting feeling — something held inside so long it causes physical pain — and the answer is a demand to cash in, to stop holding back and claim what is owed. The release is urgent, almost violent.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~price-i-pay":{"haiku":"A hedonist rationalizes constant wrongdoing and theft, living extravagantly but always paying life's price; they chase pleasure and indulgence while accepting inevitable suffering as the cost of excess.","opus":"A pleasure-seeker's manifesto: the narrator keeps making bad choices he can always justify, grabbing the fun now and settling the tab afterward. Beneath the strut and sexual bragging runs a thread of ruin, hints of a body soaking and a shroud over the face suggesting the real cost he settles is his own self.","sonnet":"A cheerfully amoral acceptance of consequence: the narrator keeps doing the wrong thing because it feels right, living fast and paying later, and ultimately lying in hot water without regret.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~standing-in-the-shower-thinking":{"haiku":"A man contemplates power and corruption while suspended in hot water, acknowledging both his capacity for violence and his control over a partner.","opus":"A raw interior monologue on power and control, sizing up rivals and his own capacity to dominate the woman who trusts him. Farrell exposes the ugly machinery of male ego without flinching from it.","sonnet":"A man in the shower turns over three sustained meditations — on power and what destroys men who wield it, on a rival he competes with silently, and on the trust that allows him to dominate his partner — all while the hot water beats down and he urinates on himself without stopping. The shower is a space of radical honesty that goes nowhere.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~strays":{"haiku":"A drifter moves through life without roots or memory, picked up like stray animal; their charm masks instability as they get lost in the night and other people's lives.","opus":"Perry Farrell's portrait of rootless drifting, waking in strangers' cars with the road always thinning ahead. The pick-up stray persona romanticizes and mourns a life of easy charm and no belonging, trading real connection for the next night.","sonnet":"A hedonistic, semi-anonymous drift through a night of pickups and chance — the narrator identifies as a stray, moving through parties and strangers with no fixed name, always finding trouble and loving it.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~suffer-some":{"haiku":"A woman leaves her safe home, becomes an addict, and loses custody of her daughter by exchanging her child for drugs. The song portrays her complete self-destruction and the pain of those around her watching helplessly.","opus":"Jane's Addiction sketch a pampered woman who abandons a contented household to drink, degrade herself, and invent crises, even trading her own child for a hit. The Los Angeles pioneers depict addiction's downward allure with brutal precision, ruling that what she has done outweighs killing as her rosy life turns to ruin.","sonnet":"A portrait of a woman whose comfortable life drove her toward self-destruction — trading stability for the street, substances, and eventually abandoning her child. The tone is caustic rather than sympathetic; suffering is presented as something she chose and multiplied, and the consequences now compound beyond recovery.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~superhero":{"haiku":"The speaker expresses desperate need and devotion to a beautiful, remarkable person, willing to become their superhero despite tumbling and falling. They seek approval and fear being ordinary or overlooked.","opus":"A giddy, love-struck fantasy of becoming a beloved's protector; smitten and desperately needing her, the ordinary narrator imagines himself as her invulnerable superhero, willing to tumble and fall for her, blushing and out of his league yet devoted.","sonnet":"A bright, infatuated love song about wanting to be worthy of someone who has an overwhelming effect — the narrator is flustered and red-faced at the mere introduction, but pledges to take on anything, even knowing he'll tumble and fall, as long as he can stay close.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~the-riches":{"haiku":"Pleasures of abundance and leisure: the speaker celebrates sensual joy, natural beauty, and freedom from constraint, swimming through riches of landscape, desire, and simple earthly contentment.","opus":"Jane's Addiction, Perry Farrell's shape-shifting alt-rock provocateurs, turn appetite spiritual. The narrator loves standing by the water, women like hot showers, and dreams of hidden riches, vowing to own his fate and never look over his shoulder. Between sensual sprawl and gratitude for blue sky and green earth, wealth becomes less about money than a rapturous, unashamed hunger for the whole living world.","sonnet":"A person finds abundance not in money but in attentiveness to the physical world: water, sun, shadow, snow on trees. The hunt for riches is reframed as learning to own one's own time and fate, to endure the small pains of striving, and to recognise that the sky being blue and the earth being green already constitutes a kind of inexhaustible wealth.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~to-match-the-sun":{"opus":"Two lovers watching a sunset together and vowing to sync their hearts to it as a marker of their bond; a tender, fleeting embrace of shared time before the day ends and the promise that they'll reunite.","sonnet":"Two people aligning their hearts to the rhythm of a shared moment at sunset, claiming each other openly and making the sky itself a testament to their bond — restless and wandering apart, they still converge at twilight.","src":"sonnet"},
"jane-s-addiction~true-nature":{"haiku":"How people's treatment of the vulnerable reveals their true moral character, questioning hypocrisy and backward heroism while invoking faith, poetry, and native truth.","opus":"A comeback-era sermon from Perry Farrell arguing that character reveals itself in how someone handles those with less power, aimed squarely at pious hypocrites who congratulate themselves while bullying the vulnerable. Under the swagger sits a plea for a life given to art and honesty instead of cruelty.","sonnet":"A moral argument that how you treat vulnerable people reveals everything about you — people who exploit the weak while scoring points with God or performing rescue are exposed; your true nature is shown in those small acts, not the grand ones.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~twisted-tales":{"haiku":"Survivor hiding past trauma through lies and white magic to fit in romantically; the speaker admits embellishing their history to seem worthy.","opus":"Jane's Addiction voice a parentless drifter who bedded down in his automobile and invented crooked stories to belong and to draw someone close. The band let him own up to harmless falsehoods and a stint in psychiatric care now behind him, insisting through it all that he's no castoff, sincerity buried within intentional fabrication.","sonnet":"A man with a chaotic, parentless past reinvents his history to gain intimacy and acceptance. Raised in his car, institutionalized, shaped by the street — he constructed palatable fictions to belong and be desired. The lies were not malice but survival, a kind of white magic performed to close the distance between who he was and who she needed him to be.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~ultimate-reason":{"haiku":"Transcendent love as salvation—a broken, marginalized person finds meaning and rebirth through romantic connection and escape with their beloved.","opus":"Jane's Addiction turn a joyless, loveless life into euphoria the moment the narrator meets a woman, finally discovering something worth living for. The band inflate the romance to mythic proportions, imagining a reckless elopement at the brink of death, casting love as the supreme intoxicant.","sonnet":"Love as total rescue from a previously empty existence. Before her, the narrator was invisible and directionless; after, he elopement under open sky becomes the defining moment — a dramatic, near-mythic beginning witnessed by the roaring sea. She is not just a person but the reason the self became worth preserving.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~whores":{"haiku":"Marginalized sex workers show no judgment toward desperate clients; the song celebrates their pragmatism and refusal to be ashamed amid class struggle.","opus":"Jane's Addiction stake out the littered low streets and align with whores who never judge, tired of living the boss's dream. Perry Farrell's provocation trades in deliberately transgressive language to celebrate the disreputable, demanding more and taking chances rather than accepting the breadcrumbs of a squeezing world.","sonnet":"A raw, street-level manifesto of non-judgment and defiance. Sex workers are praised precisely because they offer acceptance without evaluation. The speaker, fed crumbs by a system designed to exploit, claims the right to take his own chances rather than serve someone else's dream.","src":"haiku"},
"jane-s-addiction~wrong-girl":{"haiku":"A portrayal of a fierce, independent woman who embraces danger and refuses to be hurt by mockery or rough treatment, maintaining power through her confidence.","opus":"A gleeful salute to a tiny, ferocious woman who cracks windows with her footwear and shrugs off men who abuse and paw at her, certain their brutality can't truly harm someone who thrives on risk. The narrator's admiration for her defiant beauty verges on reverence. From the LA alt-rock veterans, it spins a barroom dare into a bratty tribute to a woman nobody can cow.","sonnet":"A warning about a woman who cannot be intimidated, insulted, or roughed up — she's too comfortable with danger to be hurt by the tactics men use to diminish her. She moves through cages without bite marks, races cars, kicks and screams on her own terms, and keeps a pretty face throughout. The song is an admiring portrait of someone entirely in control of her own ferocity.","src":"haiku"},
"japandroids~near-to-the-wild-heart-of-life":{"haiku":"Ambition and artistic passion compel escape from home; the narrator leaves security behind to pursue music and self-creation, despite romantic uncertainty.","opus":"Leaving your hometown to chase music and a bigger life, egged on by friends who bless the escape. The thrilling, guilty rush of choosing your dreams over the place that made you.","sonnet":"Urged by a best friend and sent off by a bartender's kiss, the speaker abandons his hometown to chase music and transformation, knowing the departure costs him something good about himself but accepting that surrender as the price of a wilder life.","src":"haiku"},
"japanese-breakfast~posing-in-bondage":{"haiku":"Yearning for closeness manifests as self-imposed emotional imprisonment; the speaker waits in performed vulnerability, needing proximity more than freedom.","opus":"Aching for a partner's attention, the singer holds herself poised and restrained for hours, framing intimacy itself as a kind of willing bondage. Under the erotic image runs a grimmer knowledge: the world splits between those who have already felt real pain and those still waiting to.","sonnet":"A speaker waits alone, dressed up, longing for a partner who consistently turns away their touch; the need for closeness and proximity is named directly as \"bondage\" — a constraint born not from physical restraint but from emotional need that cannot be released.","src":"haiku"},
"japanese-breakfast~savage-good-boy":{"haiku":"Wealth accumulation and survival fantasies substitute for authentic connection; the speaker dreams of material security and family while the world drowns, seeking escape through fantasy.","opus":"A billionaire justifies hoarding wealth and a doomsday bunker as devotion, promising to shelter a lover while the world floods. It skewers the way the rich frame greed and repopulation fantasy as love and necessary sacrifice.","sonnet":"A satirical love vow from a capitalist romantic: he'll make money until there's none left, build a billion-dollar bunker for two, and when the city floods he'll dine her on freeze-dried food in the hollows — the extreme accumulation of wealth is framed as both devotion and self-absolution for all bad behaviour.","src":"haiku"},
"jason-aalon-butler~bulletproof":{"opus":"A raging dedication to ghettos worldwide, indicting the prison-industrial complex that profits from struggle and mourning kids gunned down by stray bullets, hammering that amid endless bloodshed nobody is bulletproof.","sonnet":"About systemic racism and the ongoing killing of Black people in America — the narrator describes a friend shot dead on the way to a bus, indicts the prison-industrial complex as profiting from Black suffering, and insists that nobody — despite power or wealth — is truly bulletproof from violence and death.","src":"sonnet"},
"jasta~when-the-contagion-is-you":{"haiku":"A person grapples with watching someone they know transform negatively, realizing that holding on hurts more than letting go. The song frames the other person as a spreading illness that can't be cured once it takes hold.","opus":"On his solo work, Hatebreed's Jamey Jasta turns the harshness inward, studying his own trembling hands and a self he barely recognizes. The reckoning centers on a toxic bond and the uncomfortable realization that he may be his own poison, with severing the tie framed as the only remedy, delivered with raw, worn-out honesty.","sonnet":"Recognition that the source of damage in a relationship is internal, not only external. Watching a person who mirrors one's own worst tendencies, the speaker confronts how clinging to toxic attachment bleeds through the veins — and concludes that no amount of time will heal what must simply be released.","src":"haiku"},
"jawbreaker~chemistry":{"haiku":"The song captures adolescent alienation and social rejection. Outcasts survive on mockery and broken friendships while observing cooler peers from the margins of school life.","opus":"A cutting portrait of high-school estrangement, the narrator sneering at the class he's trapped in, tallying his tuneless invisible instrument and dated hairstyle while claiming he'd rather stay an outsider, all of it hiding loneliness, ruptured friendships, and the ache of counting down to something starting. Jawbreaker were the cherished Bay Area emo-punk band whose literary, raspy songwriting made them touchstones, and this captures their knack for wounded, defiant youth.","sonnet":"High school as slow, grinding humiliation — the wrong hair, the wrong year, the wrong crowd — and the only dignity available is refusing to care. The speaker gets cornered in Chemistry class, gets called names, and laughs it off, then drifts into fantasizing about a girl two grades below who already lives outside the social contract. The whole thing is shot through with the desperate waiting to be done with a place that was never home.","src":"haiku"},
"jaws-the-shark~destroy-the-world":{"haiku":"Wealth and consumerism spread as parasitic infection across society, breeding apathy and greed while enslaving the population through false needs and the illusion of transcendence.","opus":"A biting satire of billionaire excess, indicting those who wreck the planet from private jets while measuring worth in wealth and conquests. Its sardonic hook admits that ruining the world is exactly how such men win the girl and the admiration. It reaches toward disgust at unchecked privilege and the moral rot dressed up as success, and the urge to get out.","sonnet":"A sharp-edged takedown of the ultra-wealthy who pillute and plunder from private jets, pledging allegiance only to themselves — the bitter refrain \"that's how you destroy the world but that's how you get the girl\" frames environmental devastation as the accepted price of elite social success.","src":"haiku"},
"jay-eskar~rhythm-of-the-universe":{"opus":"Seeing straight through someone's love and finding it unsettling rather than reassuring. The looped confession makes the unease the whole rhythm.","sonnet":"A minimal loop of someone unsettled by seeing through the superficiality of another person's love — there is almost no lyrical content beyond that single unease.","src":"sonnet"},
"jay-z-and-linkin-park~points-of-authority-99-problems-one-step-closer":{"haiku":"Street survival intersects with systemic critique as the narrator navigates persecution from law and society, intelligence and resilience becoming ammunition against oppression.","opus":"Jay-Z's defiance of critics, racist cops, and a rigged industry, most sharply in the roadside stop where he knows his rights against an illegal search, spliced with the breaking-point rage of One Step Closer. The mashup fuses two kinds of pressure, systemic and personal, into one about-to-break refrain.","sonnet":"A mashup of three perspectives: Jay-Z's braggadocious deflection of critics and a tense racial-profiling traffic stop narrated with legal savvy, a street confrontation ended with force, then Linkin Park's explosion of pent-up aggression — all orbiting the idea of being pushed one step closer to the breaking point.","src":"haiku"},
"jay-z~heaven":{"haiku":"Spiritual aspiration collides with material contradiction; the speaker questions divine judgment while inhabiting earthly glory and sin.","opus":"Jay-Z fusing Five-Percent Nation self-deification with skepticism toward organized religion, questioning existence while flaunting wealth as proof of divinity in the flesh. Provocation meets genuine spiritual searching, daring only God to judge him.","sonnet":"A roaming interrogation of religion, divinity, and spiritual authority: the speaker questions the entire architecture of belief — church, heaven, god, Illuminati — from a position of worldly success and philosophical restlessness, performing the role of prophet and heretic simultaneously, unwilling to submit to any system not of his own making.","src":"haiku"},
"jeden-osiem-l~jak-zapomnie":{"haiku":"Desperate yearning to forget a past love and the moments they shared. The speaker is haunted by recurring memories, regret, and the impossible longing to erase the connection that was once precious.","opus":"The Polish rapper aches to forget a lost love, longing to blow away every memory like settled dust yet knowing he cannot tear free. He replays her image before his eyes, sleepless, recalling months of closeness that once felt like having everything. What he called gold he now knows was cheap brass, the hip-hop lament caught between wanting to erase the past and being unable to stop reliving it.","sonnet":"A Polish rap elegy for a love the speaker cannot unlearn. Memories accumulate like dust that refuses to be blown away — moments once felt as gold now revealed as fool's gold in retrospect — and the recurring wish to simply forget what once felt like everything is its own form of devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"jeff-loomis~chosen-time-feat-christine-rhoades":{"haiku":"A woman destined to steal a knight's heart rides through moonlight as a thief claiming him as her perfect crime and romantic prize.","opus":"The former Nevermore guitarist swaps virtuoso shredding for storytelling, portraying a woman as a nocturnal outlaw guided by an inner premonition, biding her time until the moment is right to claim the heart of the man she seeks. Cast as a lone rider who only moves under cover of darkness, she has always sensed this pursuit was coming, the tale ending suspended on whether the two finally meet.","sonnet":"A woman shaped by visionary certainty rides through the night like a modern outlaw, stealing hearts rather than gold. She has waited her whole life for the knight who matches her; when the moonlit moment finally arrives, the two ride side by side as destiny closes its circuit.","src":"haiku"},
"jeff-loomis~surrender-feat-ihsahn":{"haiku":"Despair and disillusionment overwhelm consciousness until surrender to the darkness feels like breaking free from chains and accepting defeat as liberation.","opus":"A meditation that recasts drowning and giving up as a kind of liberation, where despair and darkness stop feeling like defeat and start feeling like release, until letting go becomes an act of trust. Loomis, the shredding guitarist once of Nevermore, builds the piece as a showcase, with Emperor's Ihsahn lending black-metal weight that makes the surrender feel transcendent.","sonnet":"Drowning reframed as release — a turbulent emotional sea that has battered the speaker for so long that finally sinking feels like freedom rather than defeat. What reads as surrender to oblivion transforms in the closing lines into something like broken chains, the fall from grace becoming flight. The song holds both readings at once, making the act of letting go feel genuinely ambiguous between destruction and liberation.","src":"haiku"},
"jeff-loomis~tragedy-and-harmony-feat-christine-rhoades":{"haiku":"A person struggles with internal conflict between opposing personalities or personas. They cannot control which mask emerges or who will be abandoned.","opus":"A study of a fractured psyche choosing which self to wear, darkness or light, disowning responsibility for either. The dread and thrill blur together, framing dissociation as a nightly gamble over who gets to run the mind.","sonnet":"A person with shifting or dissociated personalities can't control which self will emerge each night — the darkness or the light — and takes no responsibility for whoever shows up and what they might do to the people around them.","src":"haiku"},
"jefferson-airplane~white-rabbit":{"haiku":"The song uses the Alice in Wonderland narrative as metaphor for drug use, where pills alter perception and inhibition, suggesting psychedelic experience will reveal truth if you open your mind.","opus":"Grace Slick weaponizes Carroll's nonsense to make a point about the postwar generation's hypocrisy: the same parents who dosed children on bedtime stories and prescription pills recoiled at psychedelics. A bolero building to one command about waking the mind rather than numbing it.","sonnet":"Using Alice in Wonderland as a cover, the song is entirely about psychedelic drugs — pills that make you larger or smaller, mushrooms, a hookah-smoking caterpillar — culminating in the instruction to expand your mind.","src":"haiku"},
"jenevieve~baby-powder":{"haiku":"Rejecting false intimacy and promises, demanding genuine honesty in connection.","opus":"A silky R&B kiss-off where attraction and disgust run at once, wanting someone who keeps turning her off. She names the game he's playing and quietly refuses it, choosing herself over his script.","sonnet":"A woman sets her limits with a partner who arouses and frustrates in equal measure — she wants to be the last one in his bed but will not accept being kept dry and disrespected, and she calls out his performance while asserting she deserves to be lifted up.","src":"haiku"},
"jericco~always":{"haiku":"Persistence through suffering and commitment transcending material emptiness, the speaker finding strength in loyalty and emotional presence.","opus":"A hard-rock confession of recovery and devotion, the narrator crawling out of years of dishonesty and self-ruin to stand firm beside someone. It sets a hollow, materialistic life against real loyalty and vows to turn the dark into light, battered but resolute, reaching for redemption through love and a promise to keep going out of gratitude to the one who stayed.","sonnet":"A vow of loyalty despite personal history of self-destruction and manipulation — the narrator has walked alone most of his life and is tired of the material performance of happiness others put on. Standing right by your side regardless is the only thing he has left to offer.","src":"haiku"},
"jeris-johnson~chokehold":{"haiku":"A speaker surrenders to a partner's intense physical and emotional control, unsure whether the experience is pleasure or pain but unwilling to stop.","opus":"Jeris Johnson, working in trap-metal's fusion of indulgence and weight, paints a lover as a lethal fixation. Locked in a grip he hopes never eases, the narrator can't distinguish torment from bliss and would happily let it finish him. Waltzing with mortality just to register any sensation, he casts obsession as blissful capitulation, gratification and ruin welded into one addictive hold.","sonnet":"A man describes an all-consuming erotic and emotional grip that he has no desire to escape, even as it destroys him. The pleasure and the danger are inseparable — he cannot determine whether he is in heaven or hell and has stopped caring which. Total surrender, including the possibility of self-annihilation, is the point rather than the risk.","src":"haiku"},
"jessica-winter~funeral":{"haiku":"A couple watches their relationship end with memories becoming the only thing left, unable to stop the funeral they're living through.","opus":"A synth-pop eulogy for a dying relationship that refuses to attend its own burial, insisting the shared memories weren't wasted even after the fairy tale's gold runs out. Grief held at arm's length so the good can be kept.","sonnet":"The end of a relationship framed as watching your own funeral — both people know it's over, Cupid has given up, and the narrator refuses to stand there and witness the ceremony of something that was real but is now cold and hollow.","src":"haiku"},
"jessie-ware~running":{"haiku":"Love becomes an irresistible compulsion that destabilizes the speaker into motion and dissolution; to stay is to lose oneself, yet leaving proves impossible.","opus":"The vertigo of a love that dissolves your sense of self, where surrender feels like both losing everything and finally being ready to. Desire and dread of intimacy braided into one restless pulse.","sonnet":"A person is so overwhelmed by the presence of someone they love that they lose themselves entirely, oscillating between wanting to flee and wanting to stay forever, kept perpetually running by a desire they can't resolve.","src":"haiku"},
"jet-fuel-chemistry~heartbreaker":{"haiku":"Narcissistic self-deception and emotional parasitism poison relationships; the subject avoids accountability by becoming a ghost to whom nothing adheres.","opus":"A scathing dissection of a narcissist who hollowed themselves into pure surface, becoming a ghost and a perfect host once they rejected help. The narrator wrestles with their own complicity, admitting the shared narcissism while refusing to absorb the blame. It reaches toward the exhausting reckoning of untangling from someone whose curated image left nothing real to stand yourself against.","sonnet":"An indictment of someone who takes everything, fakes everything, and became a ghost the moment they rejected help — now that you're only surface, you're the perfect host. The speaker eventually turns the mirror on themselves, accepting their own role in the wreckage as the bleeding sweetened the wine.","src":"haiku"},
"jet~are-you-gonna-be-my-girl":{"opus":"A cocky, direct come-on to a striking woman the narrator wants for himself, even though she's already with another man; pure lust and swagger, chasing her before she slips away.","sonnet":"A bold, uncomplicated pursuit — a man spots a woman across a room and immediately wants to claim her as his own, even though she's with someone else. The emotional core is pure cocky desire with no self-doubt.","src":"sonnet"},
"jigsaw-youth~deeper":{"haiku":"Gratitude serves as spiritual technology elevating consciousness beyond material entrapment, the narrator learning to descend deeper into internal transformation.","opus":"Swapping numbing habits for meditation and gratitude, trying to quiet the ego and plunge into something deeper. The honest catch is being most content at his lowest and still observing his own life from a remove, taking his blessings for granted.","sonnet":"A meditation on ego death and gratitude as the path to higher frequencies: the speaker acknowledges taking for granted what elevates them, watches loneliness fuel mental feuds, and keeps urging themselves to count blessings and take the leap deeper.","src":"haiku"},
"jigsaw-youth~skin":{"haiku":"Purification and emergence require dissolution; the speaker seeks chemical salvation to shed corrupted flesh and resurrect unburdened.","opus":"A plea to be freed from a diseased self, to waste away from the war within and come back reborn if only the right cure existed. The buried-alive refrain is the suffocation of giving everything just to survive a body that feels like an illness.","sonnet":"Someone who feels trapped in a body and a self contaminated by sickness and trauma, begging for release and transformation — hoping that if they can find the right cure they will emerge from the ruins of themselves as something new.","src":"haiku"},
"jimmy-eat-world~bleed-american":{"haiku":"Modern existence numbed by medication and media, where collective exhaustion and labor leave hearts and souls scattered like refuse.","opus":"A jittery portrait of numbed American life, the narrator insisting he isn't alone because the TV's on and isn't crazy because he takes the right pills, medicating his conscience while hearts litter the topsoil and lives get spent as fuel. It bleeds the greed from its own arm and begs for rest. Jimmy Eat World are the Arizona emo-rock band, and this is the anthemic, slightly caustic 2001 title track that broke them wide open.","sonnet":"Numbness maintained by routine — TV and pills keep the loneliness at bay, but the speaker knows it's a managed collapse. The repeated image of sweat and sugar on asphalt, of hearts scattered across the ground like litter, frames an entire generation grinding themselves down between the picket line and the parade. The song aches for something real while being honest that the search itself is exhausting.","src":"haiku"},
"jimmy-eat-world~the-middle":{"haiku":"Self-doubt and external judgment conspire to derail youth; persistence and self-acceptance offer the only shield against alienation and despair.","opus":"A reassurance to someone who feels shut out and judged, telling them to tune out the crowd and just be themselves. It's all going to be fine because they're only partway through the ride, nowhere near the end.","sonnet":"Reassurance addressed to a young person who feels looked down on and left out: the world's opinions don't define you, authenticity outlasts approval, and the repeated promise — \"everything'll be alright\" — is rooted in the patience of being mid-journey, not yet at the destination.","src":"haiku"},
"jimmy-fallon~balls-in-your-mouth":{"haiku":"Satirical warning about environmental disaster and the toxic consequences of corporate negligence.","opus":"A late-night comedy jingle riffing on the 2010 BP Deepwater Horizon spill, milking the tar-ball pun for all it's worth. The joke is the whole thing, topical outrage flattened into a schoolyard chant.","sonnet":"A comedic public-service announcement warning beachgoers in the Gulf South to avoid swimming in waters contaminated by BP oil-spill tar balls.","src":"haiku"},
"jinjer~ape":{"haiku":"Evolution to consciousness transformed humanity into destroyer; the speaker indicts human hunger and cruelty as incompatible with intelligence and civilization.","opus":"Earth herself, or the primordial life-force, addresses humanity with contempt, recalling raising it from a bean-sized speck only to watch it turn bloodthirsty and destructive. She calls her creation of the arrogant, killing ape a mistake she regrets.","sonnet":"An evolutionary indictment narrated by a creator-voice addressing humanity: from primal soup to upright predator, the species that once fit inside a bean now kills for appetite and calls itself intelligent — \"I made a mistake when I created you\" — and the question of what to do to humble it goes unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"jinjer~dreadful-moments":{"haiku":"A child endures violent abuse from a parent, creating lifelong trauma and the inability to trust comfort. The song depicts how violence perpetuates through generations.","opus":"A harrowing account of childhood abuse, a child cowering as a father's cruel hand approaches, crying out to a mother who never comes, understanding early that atrocity only breeds more of its own kind. It names the lasting truth that a childhood of misery becomes a lifelong, unhealable injury. Jinjer are the Ukrainian progressive metalcore band led by Tatiana Shmayluk, whose shifts between clean anguish and guttural fury make this trauma viscerally immediate.","sonnet":"Childhood abuse at a father's hands, the terror of his footsteps in the hall, and a mother's silence in the face of it. The song moves from the visceral fear of the moment to the long aftermath — how those who were never shown gentleness can only clench their fists, how the injury hides behind years but never heals. The silence of the one who should have saved her is the deepest wound.","src":"haiku"},
"jinjer~mediator":{"haiku":"Systemic suffering leaves the narrator demanding cosmic justice; God's absence provokes accusations of complicity in human cruelty.","opus":"Tatiana Shmayluk's utopian wish list for a fairer world curdling into rage at a God who lets suffering stand. Idealism sharpened into indictment, demanding to know why peace and justice stay out of reach.","sonnet":"A speaker wishes the world were kinder and more just — food for the hungry, love for the isolated, an end to suffering — and then turns bitter, wanting to drag God into the streets to witness the misery his absence has permitted.","src":"haiku"},
"jinjer~perennial":{"haiku":"Autumn as metaphor for decay and renewal; exhausted nature dies only to rise again yearly. The speaker finds peace in cyclical destruction and regeneration, transcending the pain through natural resurrection.","opus":"Tatiana Shmayluk turns autumn's dying nature into a meditation on death and rebirth, mourning the fall of the leaves while trusting the cyclical return of spring, framing decay as the necessary prelude to renewal.","sonnet":"Autumn observed as a lover — nature's annual suicide watched with tenderness and a shared opiate haze — the song frames seasonal death not as tragedy but as the necessary clearing that allows the self to be reborn from its own roots come spring.","src":"haiku"},
"jinjer~retrospection-live":{"haiku":"Maternal care and ancestral wounds surface; the speaker traces back to childhood injury and seeks healing in the arms that raised them, caught between memory and present pain.","opus":"An expat's ache for home, yearning to return to the shelter of a mother's touch and father's love after wandering far. The Russian lullaby that opens it turns the song into a question of whether going back to childhood safety is real longing or a deception the mind is playing.","sonnet":"The Ukrainian opening asks who nursed you, who brightened your grief — and the song answers: parents, the nest, the arms of home; the speaker, roaming with her heart elsewhere, is seized by homesickness, wonders if chasing the past is a trick her mind plays, and falls back into the parental embrace like a bird that cannot return to the egg but still wants to.","src":"haiku"},
"jinjer~sit-stay-roll-over":{"haiku":"Wild beasts descend upon the enslaved as submission becomes demanded condition. The speaker becomes dog trained through fear and subjugation into compliance.","opus":"A ferocious metal allegory for authoritarian conformity, depicting a bloodied pack of trained dogs who stamp out their own convictions and maul anyone who dares disagree. The obedience commands become a chilling refrain about crushed dissent. It reaches toward horror at mob mentality and manufactured loyalty, ending as the ravenous flock turns on itself, a single approved creed devouring everything.","sonnet":"Authoritarian groupthink as a pack of trained dogs — right paws raised, painted red in blood, trampling their own truth to chase every white crow of dissent — until the food runs out and dog devours dog; the Confucian parallel with raccoon-hunters is explicit, and the final refrain strips away all ownership: \"It's no one's path.\"","src":"haiku"},
"jinjer~sit-stay-roll-over-live":{"haiku":"A flock trained to obey; mindless loyalty to propaganda and authoritarianism. Disobedience brings cruelty; the masses turn on themselves once their master is gone.","opus":"The Ukrainian band builds a savage allegory of obedient mobs, portraying followers as trained attack dogs set loose to crush their own truth and savage anyone who dissents. Rigid conformity becomes a rallying cry until the pack finally turns on itself, a pointed critique of manufactured mass loyalty and its self-devouring end.","sonnet":"Ideological obedience portrayed as trained animal behavior. A black-clad authoritarian horde enforces singular truth, howling down dissent — until the pack, deprived of external prey, turns on itself and devours its own. Compliance fed with phantom ideology collapses into self-cannibalism.","src":"haiku"},
"jinjer~teacher-teacher":{"opus":"A furious allegory against dogmatic, hypocritical religious and educational authority, depicted as an abusive teacher-preacher who slaps a sinful child while claiming mercy; the singer rejects having morality force-fed and defiantly breaks the rules in front of the whole imaginary sanctuary.","sonnet":"A rebellion against authoritarian religious instruction — a teacher who beats students in God's name, forces compliance, and mistakes cruelty for righteousness — the narrator refuses the school's moral lessons entirely and smiles while breaking the rules in front of their trinity.","src":"sonnet"},
"jiyagi~time-is-ticking":{"haiku":"Urgency drives devotion; seconds demand surrender to love's imperative.","opus":"An urgent plea to a lover to let themselves be loved before time runs out, promising to always fight for them. The racing heartbeat and ticking clock turn devotion into something breathless and running against a deadline.","sonnet":"An urgent romantic declaration against the clock: the speaker knows they have everything to give and begs someone to let them in before time runs out, repeating the offer of love and the promise to always fight for them.","src":"haiku"},
"joanna-gruesome~last-year":{"haiku":"Silent murder and death linger in confused yearning; moving close triggers acknowledgment of hidden demise.","opus":"The Cardiff noise-pop band splices tenderness and violence in their signature fashion, the lyric fracturing between healing and harm. A speaker touches the tiniest scar, ceases fires, and mourns someone passing, yet the fragments curdle toward menace, professing love for one who secretly died and murmuring of silently murdering everyone. Sweet melody and unsettling confession stay tangled, closeness and threat pressed into the same breath.","sonnet":"Fragmented and barely coherent, the speaker navigates isolation, scars, and a simmering wish to erase others who feel uncomfortably close. Beneath the noise is a raw refusal to move on, circling the same unanswered wound.","src":"haiku"},
"joe-ford~breaking-point-feat-robin-adams":{"haiku":"Unspoken regrets poison the mind; the breaking point approaches as truth struggles against the comfort of silence and unsaid words.","opus":"A drum-and-bass meditation on words held back and truths left unsaid until the pressure builds toward a snap. The narrator's head goes to noise from bottling it up, watching someone squander all they have. It reaches for the cathartic belief that honesty is close and that finally speaking plainly will free them both from the mounting weight.","sonnet":"A witness who has long excused and hidden the truth about someone's self-destructive abuse reaches a point where the static in their mind can no longer be suppressed; the breaking point is coming, and the truth — however late — will set them free.","src":"haiku"},
"joel-nielsen~forget-about-freeman":{"opus":"A military radio transmission abandoning a soldier as the base evacuates and the bombing begins, dread mounting as things fall apart. Drawn from Half-Life, it's the sound of being written off — the man below is on his own.","sonnet":"The Half-Life radio transmission instructing soldiers to abandon Black Mesa and leave Gordon Freeman behind — a piece of in-game military dialogue set to music, portraying the moment military command writes off its own people and the protagonist to cut losses.","src":"sonnet"},
"joey-valence-brae~hooligang":{"haiku":"Unstoppable youthful momentum burns with childlike power and immortal confidence; the crew surges forward in perpetual motion, always running, never stopping.","opus":"A hyperactive nostalgia trip built on nineties-kid and anime references, all speed and swagger and refusing to slow down. The relentless running is the whole point: momentum as identity.","sonnet":"A pure bravado track about unstoppable momentum and the thrill of being the best — the narrator reaches back to childhood energy (blacktop king, 360s in a green machine) and channels it into present-day dominance on the music scene. The relentless \"running\" refrain is the emotional center: forward motion, hustle, never stopping, never retiring.","src":"haiku"},
"johari~jewel":{"haiku":"A deep emotional pull toward someone barely remembered, questioning if this connection and suffering is worth living. The speaker searches for meaning in fading memories.","opus":"An atmospheric, post-metal-tinged reverie about memory dissolving and a bond growing indistinct. The narrator drifts toward someone he can scarcely recall while questioning whether he's even salvageable and whether his life holds any worth, longing and self-doubt blurring together in hushed, echoing textures.","sonnet":"A relationship at the edge of dissolution, examined through the haze of fading memory — the speaker unable to recall clearly, unsure whether they are beyond saving or whether what they shared can still justify the present. The warmth of belonging somewhere cuts against a cold December feeling and the fear of having already slipped past the point where belief is possible. The question of whether this life is worth living runs under everything.","src":"haiku"},
"johari~phantom":{"haiku":"A person confronts the dissolution of their identity and questions whether anything remains of who they were.","opus":"An anxious inquiry into identity and continuity, asking whether you're still yourself once memory, people and places fall away. The dread is moving ever faster while building toward nothing, unsure you can be real to anyone anymore.","sonnet":"An identity crisis stripped to its core — the narrator questions whether continuity of self is even possible when everything familiar has gone, wondering if the person they once were still exists at all.","src":"haiku"},
"john-mellencamp~jack-diane":{"haiku":"Youth's fleeting moments ripen into nostalgia; the speakers cherish transient pleasure while time inevitably transforms them.","opus":"Heartland teenagers grasping at fleeting youth before adult life closes in, the ditty's cheer shadowed by the warning that the thrill of living fades. Nostalgia and dread of growing up in one small-town snapshot.","sonnet":"Two working-class American teenagers in a small heartland town live out the brief electric intensity of young romance, with the narrator inserting the elegiac reminder that life goes on long after the thrill fades. The bittersweet tone holds both the beauty of being sixteen and the knowledge of what time does to it.","src":"haiku"},
"john-wolfhooker~free-your-mind":{"haiku":"Temptation toward self-destruction pairs with seductive darkness; submission to chaos offers twisted liberation.","opus":"A tempter's voice offering absolution and safety while urging the listener to stop trusting angels and follow him instead. Salvation is inverted into seduction, the poison sold as the only way to feel free.","sonnet":"A Mephistophelian figure — possibly the devil or a charismatic manipulator — seduces the listener away from angels and toward possession, promising safety and freedom while luring them toward damnation under the guise of liberation.","src":"haiku"},
"johnny-booth~asymmetrical":{"haiku":"Society's asymmetry creates broken shells of people locked in despair; the speaker rejects complicity in rigged systems while seeking liberation.","opus":"Metalcore fury at a stacked system where the powerful guard their fortunes while the public votes against its own good. The title signals defiance: assembled from damaged people, they refuse to be beaten into line with the forces pulling them under.","sonnet":"A political song about citizens left empty-handed week after week by leaders who protect their own wealth, suppress dissent with rubber bullets and radiation, and hollow out democratic institutions — the speaker sees the system collapsing and admits complicity.","src":"haiku"},
"johnny-booth~storyteller":{"haiku":"Fabrication pervades all interaction; lies multiply while the truth lies hidden in shadows, and no one escapes the web of deception.","opus":"A metalcore takedown of a compulsive liar whose fabrications slip out behind kind gestures and costumes. The narrator refuses to keep breathing beside the deceiver or to keep passively agreeing just to get by. It reaches toward the suffocation of a relationship built on counterfeit sincerity and the resolve to stop enabling a performance dressed as truth.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a habitual liar whose fabrications slip through their teeth despite kind surface gestures; the speaker cannot walk away but refuses to keep saying yes — 'when the shadow grows shorter at midnight' marks the moment the costume is seen through.","src":"haiku"},
"johnny-cash~hurt":{"haiku":"A speaker chronicles self-inflicted pain as proof of feeling, cataloging moral failure and broken promises while recognizing they have become an empire of destruction. Regret and damaged relationships define their legacy.","opus":"Cash's late-life reading turns Reznor's self-harm confession into a deathbed inventory of regret, everyone lost, an empire of dirt, a body that betrayed him. From a dying man it becomes a whole life measured and mourned.","sonnet":"A harrowing self-reckoning at the end of life — the narrator inflicts pain on himself just to feel something real, surveys the empire of damage he's built, and acknowledges he will keep hurting those he loves most.","src":"haiku"},
"johnny-goth~everybody-just-wants-to":{"haiku":"Universal hunger masks existence itself; everyone pursues something unnamed while the speaker withdraws from collective delusion into necessary isolation.","opus":"A shrug at how life somehow works out even when you expected to be dead, watching someone reinvent themselves into a pretender. The fantasy of hiding away somewhere with no headaches is the only relief on offer.","sonnet":"A fragmented reflection on the strangeness of survival and transformation — the narrator expresses surprise that someone they expected to be dead has instead changed and is now pretending to be someone else. The unfinished refrain \"everybody just wants to\" suggests a restless, unnamed longing; the song is about the exhaustion of daily headaches and heartbreaks and the impulse to disappear somewhere beyond all of it.","src":"haiku"},
"johnny-goth~love":{"haiku":"Loss persists as the speaker bleeds out memories; presence fades while distance accumulates.","opus":"Bleeding out over a lost love while trying to talk himself into being fine without it. He begs the memory to either cut deeper or vanish completely so he can at last set his head down and rest.","sonnet":"Ambivalence about a relationship the speaker can't quite leave or fully commit to — they bleed for the person, want to carve the name into their heart, and then shift into wanting to finally lay it to rest, oscillating between clinging and release.","src":"haiku"},
"johnny-goth~under-the-floor":{"haiku":"Shame and unworthiness condemn the speaker beneath floorboards; deserving oblivion, they wait for death or prayer's redemption while dust accumulates in their prison.","opus":"A menacing, gothic kiss-off wishing someone buried beneath the floorboards for throwing everything away. Beneath the venom runs the narrator's own broken craving to be perfect and a dark invitation to reunite in death. It reaches toward resentment fused with self-loathing, the ugly satisfaction of condemning another while quietly admitting nothing is working anymore.","sonnet":"A speaker consigns someone to the space under the floor — throwing everything away, meeting them when they die — the repeated verdict \"you deserve to be down under the floor\" functioning as both dismissal and curse, cutting off any appeal to the Lord the speaker does not believe in.","src":"haiku"},
"johnny-hollow~the-wild-hunt":{"haiku":"A supernatural procession moves through the city at night, paralyzing an observer with dread. The song depicts an ominous, otherworldly force that defies comprehension or escape.","opus":"A modern ghost story staged at a bus stop, where flickering light and trembling garbage herald the arrival of the Wild Hunt, the old folkloric spectral procession, now stalking the streets of New Berlin. The narrator is caught in their tender, paralyzing stare, warned not to look yet unable to turn away. Johnny Hollow are a Canadian dark-cabaret and gothic act, and this fuses cinematic dread with the eerie intimacy of being seen by gods.","sonnet":"A midnight encounter with the Wild Hunt in a contemporary city — gods cracking pavement in an alley, buildings shaking, ordinary people waking to the procession moving through the streets. The narrator, stranded at a bus stop with a dead phone, makes eye contact and cannot look away. The ancient supernatural force doesn't destroy; it simply sees you, and being seen by it is enough to paralyze.","src":"haiku"},
"johnny-stranger~follow-in-my-footsteps":{"opus":"A father marches out to die for a cause and charges his son to carry on the fight for his family, framing a glorious death in battle as the inheritance passed down through generations.","sonnet":"A father's parting words to his son before going out to die for a cause: a call to continue fighting for family and belief, accepting that a glorious death in service of something larger is the highest inheritance a parent can leave.","src":"sonnet"},
"jon-bellion~all-time-low":{"haiku":"Humiliating descent follows romantic rejection; the speaker plumbs emotional depths while maintaining haunting self-awareness.","opus":"The wreckage after being seen through and discarded, pride shattered and every attempt to hide the hurt failing. A breakup rendered as freefall, the buoyant hook masking how far down he's gone.","sonnet":"A breakup has shattered the speaker's self-image: he was the hero, the prototype, and now she doesn't see him at all — her new life erasing him completely — and despite trying to hide the wreckage, his pride is broken, his concentration gone, and he's at the lowest point he's ever been.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~basic-needs":{"haiku":"A person's fundamental human needs are replaced by dependency on another person, who becomes the sole reason for their survival and existence.","opus":"The Korn frontman's solo turn trades his usual dread for stark devotion. Battered and numbed, he claims another person's presence sustains him more than food, air, or shelter ever could, that simply knowing they exist keeps him alive. Even undone by his own troubled mind, he names that reliance itself the thing he cannot survive without.","sonnet":"A person numbed by mental anguish discovers that one presence overrides every survival instinct — hunger, air, shelter all become irrelevant. Another human being has become the only thing keeping blood moving through his veins and giving existence any reason at all.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~everyone":{"haiku":"A man rejected by religious institutions for questioning their authority confronts how organizations enforce conformity through fear and assimilation, destroying individual thought.","opus":"Jonathan Davis, working apart from Korn, casts an outcast as both heretic and prophet. Shunned for unsettling believers, he accuses a faith that swallows souls through unspoken judgment and banned questions, and he refuses to kneel. Picturing himself as a messenger arriving on the air to enlighten, he sets free thought against a religion of self-preservation, his self-disgust curdling into defiant, messianic fury.","sonnet":"An outsider confronts an institution — specifically organised religion — that demands conformity, punishes dissent, and survives by assimilating questioning souls. He refuses to kneel before what he reads as manipulation dressed as salvation, naming the mechanism: silent judgment, forbidden questions, the manufacturing of need. The drive is survival of independent thought rather than survival of the institution.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~final-days":{"haiku":"The end times are upon us—anger and destruction define this phase, with cities bombed and safe places vanishing into dystopia.","opus":"Apocalyptic dread rendered as a personal breakdown: watching a false cause marching people toward war while cities burn and bombs erase every hiding place, the destruction outside mirrors the destruction of the singer's own mind as he stockpiles anger to survive the end of the world.","sonnet":"A song about living through what feels like civilizational collapse, mixing personal internal destruction with imagery of actual bombing and burning cities, positioning the present moment as an apocalyptic endgame with no safe refuge left.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~gender":{"haiku":"A speaker expresses intense desire to inhabit another person's identity and body, describing obsessive intrusion into their space and possession of their belongings as a form of transformation.","opus":"Davis plays a character rather than himself, an intruder consumed with envy of someone else's identity. Creeping in unseen, he claims he only wants to study and shield his subject, then admits he'd surrender his own maleness to become them, coveting their clothes and their very body. The fixation sours into a wish to shed himself entirely.","sonnet":"A figure slips into someone else's bedroom and handles their clothing and belongings, consumed by the need to inhabit another person's body and gender entirely. The desire is not theft in any conventional sense but a desperate dissatisfaction with the self he was given.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~happiness":{"haiku":"Chronic invalidation and withheld joy: the speaker's happiness is systematically dismantled by someone who tears them down, taking their best parts and leaving only emptiness behind.","opus":"The Korn frontman pours the raw psychological hurt of his main band into a solo showdown. Confronting someone who forever belittles him and drains his best, the narrator states that his joy has always been forbidden, warning that one day their wounding remarks will rebound onto them. It plays as a spent stand against a lifelong abuser, resentment hardening into refusal.","sonnet":"A person addresses someone who has systematically undermined and diminished them, taking pride, energy, and joy while offering nothing back. The exhaustion is past the point of argument; what remains is the cold recognition that happiness has been consistently denied by a relationship with no reciprocity, and that retaliation may eventually become inevitable.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~medicate":{"haiku":"Someone numbs their pain daily with pills to make suffering go away. They wear a face they don't recognize and medicate in every way to escape their inner hell.","opus":"Stepping out from Korn to score The Devil's Rejects-era darkness, Jonathan Davis turns addiction into confession. He no longer recognizes himself after so long in torment and dulls the ache with a drug he's grown to crave. The song's exhausted refrain is that he numbs himself daily just to get through, while straining and failing to make his life mean something.","sonnet":"Daily chemical numbing is the only way to keep moving — the face in the mirror is unrecognizable after years of running from pain that never fully resolves. A small pill quiets what would otherwise demand to be heard. The confession is both self-aware and trapped: knowing the cycle is broken, unable to stop reaching for the thing that dulls it.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~please-tell-me":{"haiku":"Someone is trapped in confusion and pain, desperately seeking clarity about what is wrong in a relationship. They question whether they are losing their sanity or being manipulated by the other person.","opus":"Jonathan Davis extracts raw bewilderment from a relationship that keeps him dangling, demanding to know what's happening and whether he's the one losing his grip. The Korn frontman, on his own, frames deliberate cruelty as a blow driven right into the bloodstream, grasping for an explanation while sensing the two of them are ruined for good.","sonnet":"Exhaustion with a relationship that oscillates between attachment and cruelty without resolution. The speaker cannot locate himself — unsure if the pain is his fault or the other person's — and demands a straight answer that never comes. All he wants is to stop being suspended between belonging and being discarded.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~the-secret":{"haiku":"The speaker watches their partner's dark secrets and inner cruelty, wondering if they are truly a demon beneath an angelic surface. They are trapped in enabling abuse despite recognizing the pain being inflicted.","opus":"The Korn singer watches a lover sleep untroubled despite the cruelty inflicted on him, cursed to see innocence where others would recoil from something monstrous. He keeps circling a buried secret and a damaging bond he can neither release nor recover from.","sonnet":"Trapped beside someone who harms him while appearing angelic to everyone else. The speaker watches a partner sleep calmly after causing damage, unable to reconcile the demon he knows with the surface others see. He is aware the arrangement is destroying him and cannot understand why he stays.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~underneath-my-skin":{"haiku":"Someone describes an internal parasitic entity rotting them from within, and they fight against this invasive force rather than surrendering to death.","opus":"A raw struggle against an inner sickness that seems to writhe and rot within the body while exposing old wounds and shames. The spine of the song is a refusal to surrender to it: the narrator digs through his own mind to resist the pull toward death, determined to destroy whatever festers in him and choose to keep living.","sonnet":"An internal parasite of memory and sin that the narrator refuses to surrender to — something is rotting inside him, memories are being stripped away, and he's fighting an urge toward self-destruction while insisting he will not give in.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~walk-on-by":{"haiku":"Someone walks a dark, hopeless path while being hunted by invisible forces that steal their joy and sanity. Despite the pressure and laughter of others, they continue their lonely, doomed journey.","opus":"The Korn frontman treads a darkness he can't outrun, courting ruin as despair swallows every day. He voices a paranoid terror of hidden figures probing, carving up and pilfering his joy, an inner pressure mounting toward a snap as he keeps skirting past his troubles rather than confronting them.","sonnet":"Chronic hopelessness treated as a condition rather than a crisis. The darkness soothes while the light recedes; external forces seem to strip happiness apart and leave the wreckage. He keeps moving — walking on by — not because he has direction but because stopping feels even less possible than escape.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~what-it-is":{"haiku":"A person struggles to accept and embrace their true nature, torn between their darker impulses and ideals, ultimately deciding to face who they really are without denial.","opus":"A song about dreading self-knowledge and then reluctantly accepting it anyway. Davis admits he'd do anything to dodge an honest look at himself, torn between his worst and most fragile impulses, but the chorus lands on a shrug of resignation: this is simply who he is, and he'll own it, monster and frightened child alike.","sonnet":"A man at war with his own nature — part devil, part terrified child — arrives at a brutal acceptance: the self cannot be escaped, only embraced. The basement darkness and the angel on the ceiling wage a permanent contest, and peace comes only from refusing to run from either.","src":"haiku","fable":"On his Black Labyrinth solo record, away from Korn, Davis reroutes his familiar self-disgust into a discipline of surrender, letting the title cliche work two ways at once — a phrase that shuts feeling down and a verdict that the self simply cannot be revised. The verses flinch, wishing never to learn what confronting himself would reveal, while the chorus resolves to hold every version at once, the cruel voice and the frightened child alike, declining to pick a kinder one. Its bridge hardens into clenched refusals — orders barked against being pushed, being unmade, or fleeing his origins — so the tautology that started as a dodge finally becomes the only footing solid enough to bear his weight.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"holding the cruel voice and the frightened child at once rather than choosing","built":"the title cliche works two ways: a phrase that shuts feeling down and a verdict the self cannot revise"},
"jonathan-davis~what-you-believe":{"haiku":"Authority figures and systems use deception to control and manipulate the human race while hiding secrets. The speaker asserts their autonomy against external commands and refuses to blindly follow imposed beliefs.","opus":"The Korn frontman attacks organized religion as an elaborate fiction and a tool of control, one that keeps humanity obedient while concealing what it really knows. Refusing to let any supposed higher power dictate his conduct, Jonathan Davis frames belief itself as a flimsy refuge people cling to in order to patch over a void within.","sonnet":"An anti-authoritarian declaration about self-determined meaning. Institutional religion is rejected not with grief but with clarity — belief is personal and internally made, not handed down from above. The speaker insists on autonomy over his own actions and values, seeing organized faith as another mechanism of control over a species kept compliant.","src":"haiku"},
"jonathan-davis~your-god":{"haiku":"Religion and authority figures are portrayed as manipulative forces that exploit people as pawns while abandoning their moral promises.","opus":"A furious break with organized religion, where the singer sees churches as machinery that shears people down and teaches them to fear, keeping them as pawns in a game that profits others; the deeper wound is spiritual betrayal, having tried wholeheartedly to believe only to feel the God he was sold is now killing and abandoning him.","sonnet":"A rejection of organized religion and the spiritual coercion that comes with it — the narrator has genuinely tried to believe but feels abandoned and controlled rather than saved, watching religious institutions use fear and manipulation to keep people in line.","src":"haiku"},
"jordan-fiction~god-s-favorite":{"haiku":"Loneliness and self-sabotage define existence, with the speaker resigned to isolation and suspicious of redemption.","opus":"Loneliness dressed up as a wish to be the one God loves best, taken whole with every flaw, having found love once and wrecked it. Grief and relief meet in knowing he'll never be that favorite, retreating into imagination as the only safe home for someone too odd to belong and too singular to disappear.","sonnet":"About the exhaustion of trying to meet others' expectations versus the relief of accepting loneliness — the speaker ruined their one chance at love, retreats into imagination as their only companion, and oscillates between envying those who seem divinely favored and finding peace in being overlooked.","src":"haiku"},
"jordan-rakei~wind-parade":{"haiku":"A meditation on life and love as fleeting, mysterious forces, where the speaker finds connection and meaning through nature and another person.","opus":"A calm yielding to awe, with existence and love pictured as a drifting procession on the breeze; the narrator locates himself in another person and in nature's half-grasped voice, asking only to keep living this life and cradle its riddle without end. The New Zealand-born, London-based soul artist floats it on warm, jazz-tinged production, making spiritual thankfulness feel softly euphoric rather than lofty.","sonnet":"A moment of connection with another person that opens out into connection with everything — nature speaking, mystery becoming livable, the desire to hold this feeling forever. The song is unguarded in its joy, circling the discovery that love and presence can make ordinary existence feel infinite, framing life and love as a wind parade, something beautiful and moving that you want to ride forever.","src":"haiku"},
"jos-gonz-lez~crosses":{"haiku":"The song offers guidance and light to someone carrying heavy burdens and trauma. External support attempts to help the broken move forward.","opus":"A tender pledge to stand beside someone through their bleakest stretches, the narrator promising a bit of comfort against recurring dread and the burdens that pile up, though he can only vouch for the present moment. José González is the Swedish singer-songwriter of Argentine heritage whose fingerpicked nylon-string guitar and quiet voice lend this its intimate, meditative folk weight.","sonnet":"Someone is barely holding it together — the streets flooded, people staring at the cracks showing through, crosses heavy on every shoulder. The speaker offers gentle, steady company through it: casting light, guiding through the worst moments, asking only that the darkness be endured one night at a time. The reassurance is modest and honest, making no promises beyond the immediate and the provisional.","src":"haiku"},
"joshua-travis~gouge":{"opus":"A murderer haunted to the point of madness by the person they killed and buried in the woods, tormented by hallucinations and guilt, begging for forgiveness or death while concluding no god can absolve them.","sonnet":"A murderer is psychologically destroyed by guilt after hiding a body in the woods — the victim haunts his dreams and waking hours, and he begs for obliteration of his own senses since no forgiveness is possible and death feels like the only escape.","src":"sonnet"},
"joy-division~decades":{"haiku":"Young idealists plumb psychological darkness; trauma and constraint replace naive hope, leaving only weary introspection.","opus":"Ian Curtis surveying a generation of young men marked by trauma and spiritual exhaustion, walking numbly through their own damnation. The closing dirge of Closer, it reads now as a farewell to hope itself.","sonnet":"A generation of young men who went to the darkest places and found no way out are surveyed with bleak compassion: they dragged themselves in, watched themselves from a distance, suffered and were never free, and the haunting question of where they have been echoes without answer.","src":"haiku"},
"joy-division~twenty-four-hours-2020-digital-master":{"haiku":"Obsessive rumination and romantic paralysis consume time; the speaker searches for meaning in love's aftermath but discovers only emptiness and regret.","opus":"Love collapsing into permanence, innocence corrupted, destiny watched as it slips away. Curtis wrestling with despair and the desperate need for a cure that comes too slowly, a portrait of depression closing over.","sonnet":"A person spiralling through the wreckage of what was once love searches for therapy and meaning, aware that dark corners of the self have opened up that can't be closed, and urgently pressing forward before time runs out completely.","src":"haiku"},
"judas-priest~breaking-the-law":{"haiku":"A desperate person with broken promises and no hope turns to crime as their only path forward; they've been failed by society and see lawlessness as their only agency.","opus":"The desperation of an unemployed, hopeless young man drifting town to town, feeling nobody cares whether he lives or dies. His turn to crime is framed as the inevitable product of broken promises and a stolen future, insisting outsiders would do the same in his place.","sonnet":"Unemployment and economic desperation have emptied the narrator of any stake in society, and without anything left to lose he turns to lawbreaking not out of malice but because the system has already broken every promise it made to him.","src":"haiku"},
"judas-priest~leather-rebel":{"haiku":"A fearless wanderer burns through the night claiming dominion and destiny. The song celebrates defiant independence and the pursuit of power.","opus":"A chrome-plated fantasy of the untouchable outlaw, a swaggering hero who strides unscathed through danger, owns the night, and marches toward legend with the world beneath his boots. It's pure metal myth-making, self-belief willed into fact. Judas Priest, Birmingham's heavy-metal titans, cut it on 1990's Painkiller, riding that album's furious speed and Rob Halford's stratospheric shriek at their most relentless.","sonnet":"Pure heavy metal myth-making — a lone figure who owns the night, bulletproof and bound for glory, walking through fire and ruling the darkest hours. There is no specific enemy, no story, just the relentless assertion of dominance, the image of lightning in the dark and a burning heart. The song is a leather-clad power fantasy that makes no apology for being exactly that.","src":"haiku"},
"judas-priest~painkiller":{"haiku":"A superhuman savior figure battles evil across devastated planets, bringing humanity back from the brink of extinction through violent intervention. The painkiller is both weapon and deliverer, stopping suffering through force.","opus":"A comic-book apocalypse recast as heavy-metal savior: a mechanized redeemer descends from the heavens to rescue a ruined humanity, revive the dead, and annihilate evil. The deeper reading deifies metal itself as a resurrecting force of speed and noise, an unstoppable champion clawing its way back to save the world.","sonnet":"A pure heavy metal mythology song: a half-man, half-machine savior descends from the sky to rescue a devastated humanity from evil, returning survivors from the brink of Armageddon. The Painkiller is an apocalyptic messiah figure rendered entirely in chrome and thunder.","src":"haiku"},
"judas-priest~prophecy":{"haiku":"Nostradamus embodies fate and omniscience, predicting futures that terrify while holding power over destiny that mortals cannot escape or understand.","opus":"Nostradamus as narrator, delivering terrifying visions he can neither control nor escape, cast as both prophet and curse. From Priest's concept album, it dramatizes the loneliness of seeing a future no one wants foretold.","sonnet":"The voice of Nostradamus speaks in the first person, describing himself as a cursed prophet born with the gift of foresight — neither fully in control of his visions nor at peace with them, bearing witness to history's darkness as both blessing and torment.","src":"haiku"},
"judas-priest~the-hellion-electric-eye":{"haiku":"Surveillance becomes omniscient god observing from orbit, technology transforming into instrument of total domination and psychological control.","opus":"Sung from the point of view of a spy satellite, Priest's early-eighties dread of surveillance treats the watching machine as a proud, tireless predator that feeds on your private thoughts, chillingly sure it exists to keep the country clean.","sonnet":"An omniscient surveillance satellite narrates its own existence — metal circuits, perpetual orbit, laser vision tracing every secret move — positioning total surveillance as a patriotic cleanliness that feeds on human thought and grows stronger the more it is ignored.","src":"haiku"},
"judas-priest~victim-of-changes":{"haiku":"Addiction and dissolution trap a woman in a cycle of dependence and public performance, aging out of relevance while losing herself.","opus":"A woman ruined by drink and time, once beautiful and beloved, now a stranger who no longer sees the narrator. Pity and bitterness tangled together over someone lost to their own decline.","sonnet":"A woman spirals through alcohol, lost relationships, and the creeping realisation that time and beauty have slipped away, while the man who once loved her watches, wounded and bitter, as she becomes a stranger. What was once his now belongs to her damage.","src":"haiku"},
"judas-priest~you-ve-got-another-thing-comin":{"haiku":"A young, ambitious person refuses to passively watch life pass by, determined to seize opportunities and fortune through bold action and relentless drive.","opus":"Judas Priest, Rob Halford's leather-clad metal institution, deliver a swaggering creed of self-determination. Vowing to live one life to the fullest while the music stays loud, the narrator dismisses doubters as fools, insisting a fortune waits and it is do or die. Amid a world's share of sorrow, the answer is never giving in, an anthem of defiant, chest-out ambition.","sonnet":"A defiant anthem of forward momentum: a young man refuses passivity, staking his claim on ambition, noise, and sheer will. Anyone expecting him to sit still or accept limits is simply wrong. The world holds fortune for those willing to seize it, and he has no intention of watching it pass.","src":"haiku"},
"judge-bitch~vandal":{"haiku":"Ascending from basement depths through determined aggression, leaving behind weakness and ascending toward power and visibility.","opus":"A swaggering, menacing anthem of escape and ascent, the narrator armed with orders and bravado, warning others to be careful what they wish for. Its refrain of stepping out of the basement into the vapor and rising up figures a break from a buried past. It reaches for defiant momentum, the thrill of climbing out toward something new and unstoppable.","sonnet":"A speaker loaded with reckless energy and unspoken menace — \"I'll just come take your teeth\" — moves upward out of some underground past, repeating that they are going up as if sheer momentum and threat are enough to break free of whatever held them below.","src":"haiku"},
"juice-wrld~hide-feat-seezyn":{"haiku":"Love saves the lost soul from darkness and self-destruction, two broken people finding home in each other's presence and discovering purpose through devotion.","opus":"A young man in the grip of addiction finds a partner who feels like home, someone who helps him face his demons and gives his life purpose enough to stop hiding.","sonnet":"A speaker who has lived dangerously finds someone who makes him feel at home and want to stop hiding — two lost souls who are fine with it together, the woman bringing purpose and light into a life that previously chased thrills.","src":"haiku"},
"juice~for-tyler":{"haiku":"Loss and unspoken words haunt the speaker as connection becomes ghost echoing through future relationships. Depression compounds grief, rendering authenticity impossible with anyone else.","opus":"An elegy for a friend named Tyler who has died, replaying their last conversation and everything left unsaid. Beneath the grief is the singer's own depression and a desperate, unanswerable need to know how this could have happened and whether anyone is to blame.","sonnet":"A grief elegy for a lost friend: replaying the last conversation, wishing for words unsaid, the other's \"I love you\" echoing — the speaker, already depressed, puts on a face for first impressions while asking how this happened and refusing to keep the news contained.","src":"haiku"},
"julien-baker~rejoice":{"haiku":"Caught between addiction and faith, struggling with self-sabotage while seeking divine acknowledgment.","opus":"Addiction and grief wrestling with faith, mourning dead friends while asking God why she was left alive. The devastating stance is that she believes He hears whether she praises or curses, so she does both at once.","sonnet":"A speaker struggling with addiction and self-destruction wrestles with faith — she complains and rejoices to a God she believes hears her either way, grieving friends lost to the same darkness she survived and asking why she was made to stay.","src":"haiku"},
"julien-k~shut-down-your-soul":{"haiku":"Inebriation and oppression trigger a violent shutdown of self-control; city lights cut like knives while all paths lead to silence and broken mirrors.","opus":"Electro-rock project Julien-K frame intoxication as a deliberate demolition of self-restraint, an invitation to numb out despite the emptiness it leaves. Urban sensory overload cuts into the self as the command in the title returns like a resigned mantra, sound and stimulation piling up until everything fractures into silence, with oblivion offered as the only escape from an overwhelming world.","sonnet":"Urban numbness as surrender. City lights cut like knives through a speaker who has lost self-control and invites the same dissolution in whoever listens — sensation and meaning both deadened until the mirror shatters and only silence is left, offering neither comfort nor escape.","src":"haiku"},
"julie~flutter":{"haiku":"A relationship strained by emotional distance, with the speaker exhausted by entanglement and unable to maintain connection despite past promises.","opus":"A queasy, dreamlike jealousy where wishing someone gone tangles with still feeling their body against yours, grief dragged around like a corpse under a rug. The band's shoegaze fog lets desire and revulsion for the same person coexist unresolved.","sonnet":"About obsession and guilt around a woman — the narrator is weighed down, dragging around something heavy and unspoken, and the imagery of dragging a body under a rug suggests suppressed violence or complicity, though the emotional throughline is one of wanting to escape a presence he can't shake.","src":"haiku"},
"julie~kit":{"haiku":"A person struggles with jealousy and emotional distance in a relationship, unable to communicate fully while oscillating between intimacy and betrayal.","opus":"Shoegaze from a young LA band circling the exhausting emotional accounting of a relationship where absence breeds cruelty and the narrator owns their own jealousy even while resenting the vigilance. The greyness is the point: not drama, just the low ache of never quite being reassured.","sonnet":"A relationship corroded by jealousy and dishonesty — the narrator knows what they did wrong but can't bring themselves to say it, while their partner lashes out in their absence and neither can quite be honest with the other.","src":"haiku"},
"julie~lochness":{"haiku":"A woman struggling, bloodied and unprepared, moving through decay and streets. Lochness represents a predatory or elusive force that hunts when victims are vulnerable.","opus":"The shoegaze trio paint a battered woman who keeps getting back up, bruised and staring down her own reflection, hauling herself off the pavement and back toward whatever comes next. The imagery blurs toughness with self-harm, a persona that survives by refusing to fall apart, wrapped in the band's hazy, menacing haze.","sonnet":"A woman called Lochness moves through the streets battered and defiant, dusting herself off and circling back to town regardless. The image of a pretty girl kneeling in dirt, trying not to drown, holds survival and damage together without resolving them into either triumph or defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"julie~starjump":{"haiku":"Attraction mingles with uncertainty as the narrator oscillates between desire and doubt, suspended in the tension between connection and isolation.","opus":"Shoegaze haze around infatuation and craving, a lover as intoxicant pushing toward the edge of ecstasy. Sweetness and mistrust blur together in a dreamy meditation on wanting someone who may be lying.","sonnet":"A speaker swept up in attraction to a girl describes the intoxicating, almost destabilising pull she exerts — the feeling sits on the edge between sweetness and ecstasy, and the speaker wonders whether it's mutual or just in their own head.","src":"haiku"},
"june-of-44~have-a-safe-trip-dear":{"haiku":"Domestic ritual masks underlying disconnection; fire and darkness consume the sanctuary as intimacy fails to prevent dissolution.","opus":"A domestic scene dissolving into dread of separation and death, the ordinary magic of a shared home shadowed by smoke and a train platform. Post-rock unease about the leaving that waits inside intimacy.","sonnet":"A domestic scene — bread baking, coffee grinding, two people moving through a shared space — gives way to something more disorienting: departure, waiting, smoke thickening, and a clock going blank, as the warmth of the ordinary quietly tips into loss.","src":"haiku"},
"junior-varsity~cross-the-street":{"haiku":"Desperate attraction overwhelms reason—the speaker rushes headlong into a relationship despite chaos and incompleteness, caught in the pull of desire against consequences.","opus":"A young infatuation urgent enough to bolt out the door and leave the keys behind, chanting want as if repetition could make forever real. The bravado of someone who mistakes intensity for permanence.","sonnet":"A feverish, impulsive rush of romantic pursuit — spotting someone on a train sets off an urgency to stop everything and be with them, wanting the relationship to last forever.","src":"haiku"},
"just-a-mess~lose-me-and-find-yourself":{"haiku":"Two people in a dying relationship ask each other to let go so the other can survive and find themselves. The speaker promises to stay but recognizes the need to end the connection.","opus":"A doomed, codependent romance frames the title as grim advice, one partner urging the other to lose them in order to find themselves. Mutual salvation loops in the refrain that each saved the other, yet survival seems impossible and a coffin image intrudes. Waiting for a confession while promising to stay alive, the track balances devotion against the sense that the two died years ago and only linger.","sonnet":"Two people who saved each other approach a necessary separation. The bond that kept both alive has become the thing requiring release — one must dissolve the mutual rescue in order to grow — and the plea to let go carries equal measures of love, grief, and hard-won clarity.","src":"haiku"},
"justice-for-the-damned~no-brother-no-friend":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects humanity entirely, despising all breathing humans; they embrace death and decay rather than exist as part of mankind.","opus":"The Australian hardcore band charge toward total self-obliteration, the speaker suffocating in the filth spewed by others and craving permanent solitude. Oblivion beckons as mercy, an escape from pain. Sickened even to share the blood of humankind, he wishes for no grave and no remembrance, declaring himself worthless carrion, misanthropy driven to its most absolute and unrelenting extreme.","sonnet":"Total rejection of human community from within a state of profound misery. Loathing fills the body like blood, the void calls and is answered, and the speaker demands no memorial and no grave — choosing dissolution over continued membership in a species they find irredeemably foul.","src":"haiku"},
"justice-for-the-damned~the-house-you-built-is-burning":{"haiku":"A son watches his father's legacy crumble into inferno as family bonds dissolve under emotional coldness, each member abandoning the burning structure of their shared history.","opus":"A son severs ties with a father who refuses to change, walking away as the last person left and watching the man's self-built life collapse into flame.","sonnet":"A son addresses an emotionally unreachable father through the metaphor of a burning house: the bond of father and son falls out of place, the wheels of time turn, and the speaker announces he will do what everyone else has already done — leave.","src":"haiku"},
"justice~d-a-n-c-e":{"opus":"A bright celebration of dancing and music as a great equalizer, spelling out its message as encouragement to just move and let go regardless of race or circumstance, framed with childlike innocence and nods to Michael Jackson.","sonnet":"An ode to a charismatic dancer whose fluid movement makes everything feel effortless and magical; the song frames dancing itself as a form of devotion that transcends racial and social divisions and exists as a universal bond with music.","src":"sonnet"},
"justin-bieber-bloodpop~friends":{"haiku":"Aftermath of rupture leaves both suspended in nostalgia, wondering whether friendship can coexist with the ghost of what they were.","opus":"Reaching out to an ex under the pretext of casual small talk, the transparent excuses barely hiding that he still wants her in his life. The awkward bargaining of someone who can't quite let go.","sonnet":"An ex reaches out after a relationship ended badly, asking whether friendship is still possible — masking lingering romantic curiosity behind genuine care for the other person's life. The question is sincere but complicated by the ambivalence threading through every verse.","src":"haiku"},
"jutes~punkstar-feat-royal-the-serpent":{"haiku":"A self-destructive persona warns an infatuated listener away — the punkstar lifestyle is genuinely ugly, and whoever is romanticizing it doesn't know what they're asking for.","opus":"A pop-punk cautionary self-portrait of a self-destructive rising star, all busted knuckles, blackouts, and daily doomsday chaos. The narrator warns a fascinated lover chasing a broken bad boy that they don't really want this life. It reaches toward the seductive wreckage of fame and self-medication, daring anyone drawn to the damage to reckon with who they're dealing with.","sonnet":"A self-aware warning to someone romanticizing a self-destructive lifestyle — busted knuckles, blacking out in green rooms, propane in the Kool-Aid, every day a doomsday. The narrator both embodies and repels the punkstar fantasy, telling the listener they don't actually want this proximity to chaos.","src":"haiku"},
"jutes~safe-word":{"haiku":"Masochism and ecstasy fuse into worship; the speaker dissolves boundaries and invites destruction as the ultimate expression of surrender and passion.","opus":"Desire pushed to the brink of pain, the narrator wanting no way to call it off because he loves the hurt of craving someone entirely. All-consuming intimacy is rendered as devouring and being devoured, two adrift spirits crashing together with no limits.","sonnet":"An ode to consensual intensity, where the speaker doesn't want limits or relief — 'I've had a taste and now I need you in my veins' — framing desire as sacred deviance, love letters carved in the back with nails, a collision of two people who have given up on boundaries because the pain is the point.","src":"haiku"},
"jutes~sleepyhead":{"haiku":"Drowsy disconnection and somnolent avoidance; the speaker retreats into sleep's escape.","opus":"A hazy ballad about gripping love as shelter from spiraling, unbidden thoughts. The narrator would sooner perish while dreaming so he can keep living inside a partner's embrace than wake to find them gone, unnerved by surveillance, alarms, and his own doubt. It reaches for the delicate wish to remain caught in a soothing dream, trusting one person amid inner turmoil.","sonnet":"Choosing the dream over waking — the narrator would rather die in their sleep to live in the other person's arms than face a reality where intrusive thoughts send them nose-diving into the hearse. The blood moon, the hiding in the dark, the question \"if I open my eyes will you be there?\" all make the sleep a wager: consciousness might cost you everything.","src":"haiku"},
"jynx~fade":{"haiku":"The narrator wants a former lover to disappear, unable to escape their hatred despite wanting them, judging them harshly.","opus":"A toxic post-breakup meltdown where affection sours into loathing the singer feels chained to, longing for the other person to vanish while still aching for her physically and heaping every fault onto himself. Discovering she deceived him about her infidelity ends things for good. The nu-metal churn matches its refusal of any tidy peace.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship collapsing into hate — the speaker oscillates between desperate need and scalding resentment, unable to escape either. She told him he was the problem, he took the blame, then discovered she had been lying about other people; now the door is shut and the anger doesn't dissolve into grief, it just solidifies. Everything is black and grey, and the want to make her disappear and the want to keep her are the same unbearable feeling.","src":"haiku"},
"jynx~fails":{"haiku":"Repeated failures become motivation; the speaker persists despite doubt and external dismissal, driven to prove themselves despite rejection.","opus":"Jynx flips setbacks into motivation, a rapper who mines his failures for drive even as each defeat leaves him feeling hollow and unseen. Counseling patience and persistence, he concedes that his sense of being disliked is largely self-invented. The verse frames underdog grit as a hustle for survival, urging the overlooked to keep pushing and outlast those who let themselves corrode.","sonnet":"Failure is reframed as the engine of ambition — every stumble feeds the drive rather than extinguishing it. Paranoia about being disliked turns out to be internal noise, not reality. The hustle is relentless and unsentimental: move first, rise fast, stay in control, let others fold while the speaker keeps burning and shining.","src":"haiku"},
"jyocho~a-1kqmh0g":{"opus":"Jyocho grew from the ashes of the math-rock band Uchu Conbini into something more delicate and questioning, and this song carries that fragile inquiry into the value of life. It considers beings shaped by others' standards of worth, struggling to hear one another, longing for a beautiful ending while being both permitted and denied freedom. The reaching toward a true form comes by naming lives one at a time as waves roll on — meaning assembled patiently, name by name.","sonnet":"An inquiry into the value of life — beings shaped by others' standards of worth, struggling to hear each other, longing for a beautiful ending while being permitted and simultaneously denied freedom, reaching toward a true form by naming lives one at a time as waves roll on.","src":"sonnet"},
"k-da~pop-stars-wbbt-remix":{"haiku":"Confidence and dominance as pop stars refusing to be brought down, celebrating unapologetic power and winning against all opposition.","opus":"K/DA storm in as a self-styled goddess with a blade, daring anyone to try bringing them down as they vow to wear the crown. Riot Games' virtual League of Legends supergroup, voiced by real pop and K-pop singers, blends English and Korean into pure bravado. Picture-perfect faces with wildness in their veins, they turn winning into an unstoppable spectacle.","sonnet":"A collective of performers asserts total dominance — untouchable, dangerous, crowned by their own momentum. The mix of Korean and English layers an international confidence that dares anyone to try bringing them down. Winning is not ambition here but settled fact; the only question is whether anyone in the crowd can keep up with the pace they set.","src":"haiku"},
"k-d~three-in-the-morning":{"opus":"A late-night hookup lit by the honesty that neither of them knows if the feeling is real, only that they have until morning. Desire and uncertainty share the same small window of time.","sonnet":"A late-night sexual encounter framed in mutual desire and urgency — neither person knows exactly what the other wants or feels, but they have until morning, and that is enough to make it worth diving in.","src":"sonnet"},
"kabanos~amore-mio":{"haiku":"A man seduces a woman with false promises, abandons her heartbroken, leaving her to realize she's been emotionally used; the chorus cynically treats love as a one-way feast where he takes everything.","opus":"A furious Polish takedown of a selfish seducer who woos a girl only to bed her, leaves her ruined after three months, and never sees how his self-love has warped him into a creep. The mock-tender Italian refrain twists into a demand that he consume and devour, and a curse that there is no love without reciprocity. Kabanos are a Polish rock band known for satirical, theatrical storytelling, and this weaponizes sweetness into savage moral indictment.","sonnet":"A sharp-tongued Polish takedown of a man who seduced, used, and abandoned a young woman without a second thought — the song skewers his self-love, his callousness, and the corruption it represents. The refrain hammers the point: there is no love in what he does, only appetite. The closing Italian lines drip with mock-romantic irony, the very language of love twisted into a verdict on someone who never understood it.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~baba-i-dziad":{"haiku":"An elderly couple's marriage becomes a battleground of petty conflict, mutual deception, and physical cruelty disguised as domestic routine.","opus":"The Polish band spins a folk-grotesque portrait of an old woman and old man who, beautiful and clever apart, together make an ugly, foolish world. Bound in the same stove for years, they wear each other down, hearts present yet absent between them. Bawdy nursery-rhyme wordplay and infidelity spill through the verses, a satirical fable of a loveless couple grinding pointlessly on, self-destruction dressed as absurdist Polish folklore.","sonnet":"A caustic Polish portrait of a long-married couple destroying each other by degrees. Each is wise alone and foolish together, beautiful in isolation and ugly combined — sustaining a shared furnace of resentment, mutual deception, and pretense that the suffering has been worth the years.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~ballada-o-zenku":{"haiku":"Two friends debate drinking habits and one struggles with alcohol while vomiting, with dark humor and concern mixed in their dialogue. The song depicts substance abuse through comedic conversation.","opus":"Kabanos stage a Polish comic dialogue between Zenek and Staszek, one man violently sick after drinking what he insists tastes like denatured spirit. The band mine slapstick misery from the aftermath of a bender, vomiting near a fresh floor and begging for magnesium, resolving only to stay home in slippers and sleep it off.","sonnet":"A comic Polish sketch about a man named Zenek who drinks for the first time, immediately regrets it, and becomes violently ill while his friend Staszek manages the consequences. The humor is deadpan and physical — new floor tiles, a bathroom occupied by Witalis, magnesia tablets — and the refrain about staying home in slippers lands as tragicomic wisdom.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~balony":{"haiku":"A dreamer breaks free from home and expectations, refusing to measure time or worry, floating like colorful balloons toward unknown adventures.","opus":"The Polish satirical-rock band turns unexpectedly earnest, floating a manifesto of departure. The narrator announces he is leaving the nest, wearing no watch and counting no time, joining a flock of colorful balloons pumped up in his head. Every obstacle he shoulders, sure he will manage; standing still is for those who do nothing. His only future regret would be what he failed to do while still bold and unafraid.","sonnet":"He flies the nest with nothing but his own ambition, chasing balloons drifting across a wide sky. He welcomes obstacles as fuel rather than deterrents, certain that inaction is the only true failure and that an old man's only regret will be the bold moves he never dared take.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~brzydota":{"haiku":"The song attacks artificial beauty standards and hollow vanity, condemning champagne culture for replacing authentic worth with plastic superficiality.","opus":"The Polish band skewers artificial beauty, champagne ladies who vomit their own vanity, never truly beautiful behind pretty faces, drowning in a cesspool while perfuming themselves. The ugliness of hollow, vain dolls becomes the target, someone once convinced of a false standard now feeling ugly. Defending imperfectly shaped plums against plastic orchards, it mounts a satirical protest against manufactured, cosmetic notions of beauty replacing anything real.","sonnet":"A polemic against artificial beauty and the vanity it produces. Women who perform elegance while wallowing in squalor, plastic orchards replacing real fruit — the indictment targets those who accepted a false definition of beauty until someone who never fit it internalized the ugliness himself.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~by-o-min-o":{"opus":"A bleak, self-lacerating confession of loneliness and a failed first marriage; the narrator sleeps to escape into dreams, talks to his lost love in his head, and reaches the wry conclusion that people fall in love out of longing for themselves.","sonnet":"A melancholic, self-deprecating meditation on loneliness and romantic failure — the narrator feels confused and heartbroken, imagines his lost love orbiting him like a satellite, and clings to the hope of talking to her in his head, never quite finding her.","src":"sonnet"},
"kabanos~chmurki":{"haiku":"A lover playfully promises whimsy and wonder, transforming nature's obstacles into gifts of beauty and intimacy through poetic devotion.","opus":"Kabanos spin an absurd Polish serenade in which the narrator negotiates with the wind, sun and grass to sweep his beloved into the sky. The band pile whimsical promises, catching a cloud, offering a plush sheep, gathering water lilies, into a tender comic fantasy of protection where nothing bad can happen at his side.","sonnet":"An exuberant courtship fantasy in Polish — the narrator has negotiated with wind, sun, and grass on behalf of the woman he loves, arranging the natural world to protect and carry her. Clouds in white uniforms will take them far away. The tone is whimsical and overflowing with devotion, every absurd gesture offered as proof of sincerity.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~czerwona-musztarda":{"haiku":"A surreal, darkly comedic Polish narrative about three men falling and a girl with untied boots, involving violence, absurdist humor, and red mustard smeared on chins.","opus":"A surreal Polish grotesque set on Warsaw's Praga district, where a little girl narrates absurd violence against pathetic men with deadpan cruelty and a child's logic. The nursery-rhyme refrain sours everything into nightmare theatre.","sonnet":"A surreal, comic vignette set in Warsaw's Praga district — a little girl with red mustard on her chin watches two hapless men collapse and squabble on the pavement because they don't want to tie her shoelaces, until one finally does, at which point she kicks him in the groin, wipes her face, and skips away satisfied.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~czo-g":{"opus":"A defiant anthem of self-belief against a chorus of naysayers who insist they know better; the narrator builds a metaphorical tank out of faith in himself and unbreakable will, refusing to conform because only dead fish drift with the current.","sonnet":"A declaration of stubborn self-belief against a chorus of dismissive voices that insist you're wrong, naive, or too demanding — the narrator builds an internal 'tank' made of willpower and self-trust that keeps grinding forward no matter who tries to stop it.","src":"sonnet"},
"kabanos~folwark-zwierzecy":{"haiku":"Contempt for ignorance and derision accumulates; words fail to capture total meaning while isolated understanding offers sole refuge.","opus":"A Polish alt-metal protest against sanctimonious control freaks who twist and belittle others while claiming to know best. It builds into an Orwellian animal-farm fable of a meddling cow who cages the woodland creatures to decide their fates, only for them all to die of sorrow. It reaches toward outrage at paternalistic tyranny that smothers freedom in the name of care.","sonnet":"A Polish punk-tinged song built on the Animal Farm fable: a speaker who has had enough of liars, charlatans, and the polished-smooth crowd tells a story of a controlling cow who imprisoned all the farm animals and stripped away what made each one unique, only to watch them all die of grief — a pointed allegory about authority crushing individuality under the guise of wanting what's best.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~gruby-grubas-oj-tam-oj-tam":{"haiku":"Acceptance and celebration of physical excess and contentment despite social mockery, untroubled by judgment.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek Polish anthem defending a cheerfully fat man against diet culture and mockery. Contentedly well-fed and unbothered by the scale, he's cast as robust and jolly like his father, too big for a tiny Fiat. Playful and provocative, it reaches toward body acceptance through absurdist humor, thumbing its nose at those who sneer at the happily heavy.","sonnet":"A cheerful Polish defense of fatness — the fat man is happy, well-fed, warm in winter from his own insulation, healthy like his father before him, too big for a small Fiat. 'Oj tam, oj tam' (oh well, whatever) deflects every taunt; grandfather said the thin die first.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~ja-i-moja-ona":{"haiku":"A poor, struggling couple faces hardship, ridicule, and oppression from society and authority, yet find strength and humor in their partnership through shared poverty and adversity.","opus":"Kabanos toast a drinker and his wife as refuge against a mean, ugly world of gossips, greed and the heavy paw of authority. The Polish band find grim solidarity at a table holding ten zloty, insisting that together life in the muck is bearable, thanking God for her as everything else conspires to knock them down.","sonnet":"A working-class Polish love song about endurance through a world of petty people, systemic pressures, and thin wallets. Against hypocrites, bureaucracy, and the grime of daily life, one thing holds: the marriage. Whatever falls on him, his wife is there — and gratitude for her presence is the whole argument.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~jagoda":{"haiku":"Jagoda is an older woman who dances freely and values liberty over propriety, enjoying life while the envious townspeople mock her independence and the men pursue her chastely.","opus":"An affectionate Polish sketch of a free-spirited woman, Jagoda, no longer young but dancing through meadows with an insect's lightness, unbothered by propriety while jealous townswomen mock her and men chase after her in vain. She wants none of them. Kabanos, the Polish rock band with a taste for character-driven satire, turn her into a folk-flavored celebration of a woman who keeps her head in the clouds and refuses to be tamed.","sonnet":"Jagoda is a free spirit who refuses to grow up on anyone else's terms — she dances in meadows, loses herself in nature, and simply doesn't care what people think. Women gossip and mock her; men chase her because they sense her integrity. She wants none of them. The song celebrates her lightness and self-possession without sentimentalizing it, the world's disapproval bouncing right off her.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~kibel":{"haiku":"Life delivers harsh disappointments and messes that cannot be escaped; the song advises choosing meaningful action while young before becoming trapped in failure like everyone else.","opus":"Kabanos, the Polish rock act known for crude humor laced with real bite, use a lavatory as a life metaphor. The narrator insists he chooses his own path and knows exactly which toilet he shits in, mocking the urge to gild one's backside with gold. Beneath the scatology sits a genuine plea: while young, choose something positive, since everyone otherwise ends up badly.","sonnet":"In Polish, a wryly philosophical narrator accepts that life will throw its worst at everyone, that drinking through sorrows only depletes the strength needed to recover, and that the real choice — available only while youth lasts — is to pick something positive over something merely comfortable. The toilet metaphor grounds lofty self-determination talk in deliberately coarse humour.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~kompost":{"haiku":"Decay and decomposition return to earth; organic matter returns to soil in cyclical transformation.","opus":"A Polish metal parable urging a good soul to flee a poisonous environment before it rots them too. A single unspoiled apple loses its freshness among a basket of decaying fruit, and soil too acidic for anything to grow. It reaches toward the wisdom of not putting down roots where you don't belong, promising you'll bloom elsewhere once carried away.","sonnet":"A single healthy apple surrounded by rotting ones is losing its freshness, and the speaker urges someone to hold on — the soil here is too acid for anything to grow, the rot has penetrated the roots, and this is not their place. The tenderness breaks through in the final turn: \"you'll still bloom somewhere, I'll find you, I'll take you away from here,\" rescuing the beloved from an environment that would corrupt them.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~koza":{"haiku":"A humorous Polish folk-style song about everyday mishaps among friends—spilled wine, broken noses, clogged drains—presenting minor chaos as comedic inevitability. The refrain mocks the repetitive pattern of small failures.","opus":"A drinking scene turns into slapstick as Wiesiek trips and shatters his wine, plumbing clogs, and hapless Lech never wipes his nose. Kabanos, Polish purveyors of absurdist comedic rock, milk everyday domestic mishaps for deadpan farce, repeating the grotesque refrain about the dangling snot until the mundane becomes gleefully ridiculous.","sonnet":"A sardonic Polish song catalogues a string of domestic mishaps — spilled wine, a clogged drain, a perpetually unwiped nose — building to a gleefully scatological punchline about a dangling booger. The comedy is communal and affectionate, rooted in shared recognition of life's petty absurdities.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~krav-maga":{"haiku":"The common people hold real power despite elite oppression, yet feel overwhelmed and worn down by systemic injustice.","opus":"The Polish band voices class-conscious defiance, a handful of the powerful forced to reckon with the many below. Pigs will always find the trough while the speaker, worn out and told he should live like heaven, notes only that he was promised more and got less. Krav Maga becomes the refrain for fighting back when everyone's been screwed, resentment sharpened into a working-class call to resist the greedy few.","sonnet":"Frustrated street-level lucidity about power and powerlessness in a corrupt system. A small ruling minority depends on the many yet exploits them — the speaker knows the score, finds temporary relief in the company of a friend and the open air, and names the fight even while feeling outgunned.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~mamo-jest-mi-tu-dobrze":{"haiku":"A son leaves home seeking sun and freedom, finding joy in simple wandering and community; the mother watches him find happiness in self-acceptance and togetherness.","opus":"Kabanos frames blissful escape with a needling social edge. A son packs up and leaves for where the sun shines kinder, falling asleep under a cloud and waking among clouds, free, painless, finally himself. Strangers greet each other and offer hands, singing nonsense syllables together. Yet the barb recurs: nothing irritates embittered people like others enjoying simple happiness, and someone will always find the carefree fun objectionable.","sonnet":"A son writes home from a festival crowd where strangers become instant kin over shared music and cheerful absurdity. He is free, untroubled, and fully himself — aware that uptight bystanders resent effortless joy, but completely unbothered, splashing in the mud among his people.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~margaryna":{"haiku":"Life's fleeting moments dissolve like margarine in sunlight; happiness erodes and suffering reveals what truly matters beneath illusion.","opus":"Kabanos build an extended parable around a stick of margarine that melts in the sun and dreads the knife, standing in for fragile human happiness. The Polish band counsel their soft protagonist that joy never lasts, that fear would devour it, and that constant contentment would blind one to the value of what is precious.","sonnet":"Margarine as a metaphor for happiness — soft, temporary, prone to melting or being consumed. Moments of joy must be caught quickly because they dissolve in the heat of circumstance. The song acknowledges that continuous happiness would make nothing feel precious, and that contrast — the knife, the bruise, the lost hope — is what gives sweetness its meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~moja-piosenka-laszuraburej-i-laszuraba":{"haiku":"Artistic authenticity demands refusal of market demands and critical judgment, the song existing as personal expression independent of consumption.","opus":"A manifesto of creative freedom: the song doesn't have to please, sell, fit radio, or even make sense, because the only thing that matters is singing honestly what you want. It's a defense of following your own path against critics and sneering trend-chasers, wishing that same freedom on everyone.","sonnet":"A manifesto for making music purely for its own sake — the song doesn't need to be liked, played on radio, or make sense — as long as it's honest; the speaker refuses to let trend-chasers or experts dictate what he creates, ending in a nonsense-chant celebration of freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~monopol":{"haiku":"Capitalist accumulation mirrors the board game—wealth multiplies, competitors are ruined, but final victory reveals the spoils were never real.","opus":"Using the board game Monopoly as an allegory for capitalism, the song follows a player who hoards money, ruins the other players and wins-only to discover he is alone on the board and that nothing was ever truly his; the deeper point is that the game is rigged so no one can really win, and greed leaves you cheated and empty.","sonnet":"A Monopoly-as-capitalism allegory: the game rewards ruthlessness and the accumulation of wealth at others' expense, but the punchline is that in the end the board empties and even the winner is left alone with nothing that was ever truly theirs.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~mordy":{"haiku":"Crowds of people live identical, empty lives focused on surface appearances and compliance. The song criticizes how people are trained to mask themselves and follow meaningless social scripts like animals in a trough.","opus":"Kabanos savage conformist drones who rise to the same breakfast, learn to flatter, grab and lie, and stare into a great empty world. The Polish band bark at these masked faces to wag their tails and feed from the trough, a bitter satire of servile lives chasing nothing while dressed in suits and false smiles.","sonnet":"A contemptuous Polish satire of conformists who wake, consume, perform sociability on command, and call it living. The daily routine is exposed as a costume — suits and smiles and wagging tails for the trough. The refrain tells them to put their masks on and keep feeding, stripping the pretense down to its animal mechanics.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~oszolomy":{"haiku":"Rigid ideology breeds tyranny as zealots enforce conformity through intimidation and threat, the narrator resisting the closing of minds.","opus":"A satirical stand against zealots, the oszołomy who split people into better and worse and believe only they hold the one truth. The plea to be protected from these self-righteous fanatics who barge into your home and life defends the right to think differently.","sonnet":"A tirade against zealots and ideologues — \"oszołomy\" meaning fanatics — who divide the world into us and them, barge into others' lives uninvited, and refuse to leave people alone to live as they choose.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~paliwoda":{"haiku":"Paliwoda, a vengeful supernatural force, is unleashed to destroy evil and punish wrongdoing through violent retribution and destruction. The song treats vengeance as justice delivered by a relentless mythic entity against corruption and vice.","opus":"A vengeful avenger figure who breaks his chains and hunts down a vile, wicked foe, promising to crush and destroy evil; the fates have turned against the villain, who cannot escape the coming reckoning and death.","sonnet":"A Polish folk-metal ballad about a vengeful avenger called Paliwoda who breaks his chains, smashes walls, stabs the beast, and burns its corpse — a relentless force of righteous retribution who hunts down the wicked and is the last hope wherever despair and ruin reign.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~pancerz":{"haiku":"One person desperately needs another's protective armor and love to survive; dependence and gratitude for emotional survival run through the lyrics.","opus":"A tender confession of needing love and shelter, a wounded soul asking to be hidden beneath another's armor to feel safe. Framed through a sad little bear afraid of being devoured, it admits that even the seemingly strong can't survive without being loved.","sonnet":"A tender, vulnerable confession in Polish — the narrator admits they can only show their wounds to this one person, who acts as armour against the world, and that love is the only thing preventing them from being devoured by their own darkness.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~ptaszek":{"haiku":"A young man struggles with romance and heartbreak after being rejected, finding comfort in wine and cynicism. He envies a bird's freedom while watching his love with another man.","opus":"Kabanos frame a Polish tavern lament in which a father dismisses women as endless torment while his heartbroken son weeps over a girl who took his flowers with a foolish face. The band envy a free bird that fouls a rooftop and moves on unbothered, drowning sorrow in wine and insisting life in one's own filth can still be fun.","sonnet":"A mournful-comic Polish song about unrequited love and the freedom of not caring — a bird that defecates on a roof is enviable because it processes its troubles and moves on. The narrator has been rejected, is crying into his wine, watching friends come and go, and can only wish he shared the bird's uncomplicated relationship with pain.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~rakieta":{"haiku":"Sensationalism and propaganda manipulate the masses like cattle—false narratives about enemies and saviors drive fear and ignorance.","opus":"A Polish satire on the sensation economy, mocking how tabloids and internet outrage manufacture panic and enemies for an audience too lazy to think, easily herded because scandal always sells. The absurd animal-threat gags underline how manufactured the fear is.","sonnet":"A media criticism broadside: sensationalist news and gossip are a rocket of bullshit fired at a credulous audience, designed to divide, inflame, and distract — the song names the cycle of outrage-as-product and how easily people are sold their own enemies.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~szarlatan":{"haiku":"A speaker recognizes their partner as a charming deceiver who hides cruelty and exploitation beneath a mask, and they work to break free from the addictive but poisonous hold this 'charlatan' has over them.","opus":"A narrator names a two-faced manipulator a charlatan, vowing self-liberation while confessing he still craves the very poison that harms him. Kabanos step away from their comic mode into something darker, mapping a toxic bond where addiction and betrayal blur, the speaker resolving to purge the venom even as he longs to lie beside its source.","sonnet":"A man confronts a manipulator who concealed venom behind outstretched hands, poisoning everything he once trusted. Even as he vows to free himself, he admits the pull of that toxic presence still flickers — making the declaration of independence an act of ongoing, hard-won will rather than a clean break.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~wampirzyca":{"haiku":"A beautiful young woman seduces men with charm and allure, then drains their lifeblood and leaves them dead and buried—a vampire who destroys hearts.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek Polish warning about a gorgeous young woman who is secretly a vampire, luring a hapless fellow named Józek with tattoos and warm lips before she drains his blood, freezes his heart and impales him. Beauty as lethal trap, played for horror-comedy. Kabanos are the Polish rock band whose theatrical, darkly funny storytelling thrives on exactly this kind of grotesque cautionary tale dressed up in seductive detail.","sonnet":"A warning to Józek about the beautiful girl who will drain him — she looks innocent, moves with confidence, draws you in with a smile, but she will freeze your heart and leave nothing behind. The song plays the vampire metaphor with affectionate humor, listing her charms before cataloguing the damage: she'll pierce you, gut you, and bury what's left when dark falls. The final image returns to the beautiful face, which was the trap all along.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~wielb-d":{"haiku":"An outcast—strange, rejected, and unwelcome—is driven into exile by a conformist society that demands normalcy and punishes difference, leaving them alone in the desert.","opus":"Built on an untranslatable pun folding error into camel, this Kabanos track champions the outcast. A creature slinks across the sand, called abnormal, ugly, stupid, and told to go away, while a mock-cheerful chorus insists only normal deviation is acceptable. The refrain reframes the misfit as a hard stone in society's shoe that others long to hide, defiantly recasting rejection into a badge of being magnificently, immovably different.","sonnet":"A camel lumbers through the desert as an emblem of everything the conformist crowd deems wrong, ugly, and unacceptable. The herd forms its cheerful circles and enforces its narrow norms, but the outcast creature rolls on regardless — too large and too real to be hidden away.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~wyspa-wyglupow":{"haiku":"Youthful rebellion celebrates foolishness and spontaneity as escape from aging and domestication, playfulness becoming resistance.","opus":"A tipsy, whimsical plea to a sweetheart to slip away from the crowd and keep alive the island of silliness inside them both. The warning is against turning into sad grey stones; the point of the night is simply to be playful and not lose that childlike foolery.","sonnet":"An invitation to a lover not to let the \"island of silliness\" inside them be destroyed by growing up — stay out late, drink wine, dance, go find the river — because becoming grey, sad stones is the only real failure.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~zabawka":{"haiku":"A child's toy endures the violent, sadistic play of its owner until broken and discarded. The song frames childhood cruelty as murder, with toys as suffering victims of arbitrary abuse.","opus":"A darkly satirical horror-comedy voiced by a toy being tortured to death by the child who owns it, framed by a mock news report of murdered toys found by the river. The macabre point of view, the toy pleading not to be scratched, stabbed or bloodied while its owner's white blouse turns red, twists childhood play into gleeful sadism and the toy's ruined heart.","sonnet":"A grotesque dark comedy in which a toy narrates being sadistically abused by a little girl — scratched, stabbed with pins, bloodied — framed as a mock murder mystery (the news broadcast reports the eighth toy homicide this month) and ending with the girl getting what she gave.","src":"haiku"},
"kabanos~zamkni-te-g-wki":{"opus":"The Polish satirists mock the closed mind, the person whose skull no new thought can pierce, who sees only black and white and sneers at anything beyond their shallow depth. It is a portrait of willful ignorance and prejudice, delivered with contempt but also a weary plea for such people to simply open their eyes.","sonnet":"A contemptuous portrait of narrow-minded people whose rigid, black-and-white thinking fills their small heads with nothing new — they mock and spit rather than engage, and the narrator pleads with them to open their eyes.","src":"sonnet"},
"kabanos~zupa-kalafiorowa":{"haiku":"A Polish cauliflower-soup recipe intertwined with a lurid dream-narrative of a girl's sudden violent vanishing and death by suffocation or abandonment.","opus":"A surreal dark comedy in which a woman obsesses over making cauliflower soup while a nightmare bleeds into reality, a suitor named Stefan hands her a helmet and gropes her, so she strikes him and his head falls off and dissolves; the mundane recipe interspersed throughout collides with a dreamlike murder that leaves only a wet stain where the man had been.","sonnet":"A surreal, darkly comic song structured like a recipe — between the cauliflower soup instructions, a woman dreams of being groped at a ball by a man whose head falls off when she hits him; she later finds her man Stefan has vanished from his bed, leaving only a wet stain.","src":"haiku"},
"kabbage-boy~girlfriend":{"haiku":"A young lover expresses passionate, somewhat crude desire for a girl while hoping for marriage and commitment despite contradicting his physical impulses.","opus":"A goofy, innuendo-laden love song where the narrator insists his girlfriend is also his closest companion while piling on absurd similes, cartoonish cooking metaphors and mock-melodramatic declarations, all cresting on a punchline. Deliberately ridiculous, it's comedy dressed as romance, a novelty pop-rap goof chasing laughs through escalating silliness.","sonnet":"Lovestruck and completely overwhelmed — the speaker wants to confess everything, can't quite hold himself together long enough to say it plainly, and keeps sliding into bizarre metaphors: frying his heart in a pan, a king of clowns, nearly sneezing. Under the comic chaos is a genuine yearning, the dream of a ring, the terror of her drowning while he flails on his knees. The song makes comedy and sincerity indistinguishable.","src":"haiku"},
"kadinja~glhf":{"haiku":"Fragmented existence questioning identity and meaning; humanity struggles to transcend its programmed nature and write its own existence before the game ends.","opus":"An abstract, cosmic reflection on humanity's drive to leave a name and mark on existence; amid imagery of monuments, sciences and dying stars, the narrator yearns to matter and create, treating life as a playground game to be played fully before it ends.","sonnet":"A broadly humanistic song about identity, legacy, and the desire to leave a mark — it moves through motifs of light, stars, and collective history to argue that life is a finite game and the only worthwhile move is to start writing your own name into existence before it's over.","src":"haiku"},
"kailee-morgue~siren":{"haiku":"Seductive manipulation lures desire into darkness, weaponising attraction to bind and consume the captivated subject.","opus":"A predatory femme fatale luring a willing victim into the dark, weaponizing desire he pretends to resist. The myth flipped so the seductress owns her venom, gothic pop reveling in dangerous appetite.","sonnet":"A speaker embraces her identity as a siren — irresistible, dangerous, drawing a man in with full knowledge that he wants to be caught, and offering poison and submission as a kind of perfect desire both parties are pretending is a choice.","src":"haiku"},
"kaiser-snap~wild-child":{"haiku":"Untamed vitality and careless passion captivate and frustrate as the speaker orbits around a person who refuses containment or commitment, always moving beyond reach.","opus":"An intoxicated portrait of a restless woman who moves like a cat and rips the singer's heart out every time, forever unable to let go or stay still. Fascination and helplessness merge as he watches her burn through the world.","sonnet":"Fascination with a fearless, untamed woman who moves like a cat, grabs every moment, and refuses to let go: she floats above ordinary ground, whips her hair, and embodies a freedom the speaker can only watch with awe.","src":"haiku"},
"kaixo~vox-populi-feat-daedric":{"haiku":"Collective voice rises from ashes of destroyed lives demanding transformation, echoing persistence promising that resistance outlives individual extinction.","opus":"A collective rising from the ashes, bearing the voices of the fallen and pleading for the power to stand against destruction and pull corruption down. The consolation is that even once they're gone, their echoes and hope will outlast them.","sonnet":"Rising voices of collective resistance carry the ashes of broken lives toward an uncertain horizon — echoes linger beyond the gone, and in the serenity of surrender a single sound of hope is found that gives the strength to take on destruction and tear down corruption.","src":"haiku"},
"kalafina~magia":{"haiku":"Ancient magic and transcendent love promise to shatter darkness; devotion becomes the spell that pierces despair and summons impossible light.","opus":"The anime Madoka Magica's despairing hymn, about wishing hard enough to break a doomed world and the fragile, blade-like courage of a plucked flower. Hope and sacrifice bought at unbearable cost, faith clung to against the dark.","sonnet":"A singer in Japanese reaches toward the power of ancient magic — a force that could shatter even darkness — driven by the courage of broken flowers and the wish to meet a smiling beloved who is strong enough to illuminate the world, even knowing tomorrow may be fragile.","src":"haiku"},
"kali-uchis~your-teeth-in-my-neck":{"haiku":"Predatory exploitation of others' labor and hope disguises itself as seduction, the wealthy sustaining themselves on blood drawn from the poor and desperate.","opus":"A critique of exploitation and greed where the rich feed off the poor like a vampire's bite, the singer grinding to give more while questioning whether any of the world's worshipped riches are worth it.","sonnet":"Exploitation bites like teeth in the neck: the speaker works relentlessly in a system where rich men keep taking from the poor and some worship diamonds and pearls enough to kill for them; the refrain — \"you know better\" — is both accusation and self-reminder to get right before it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~abandoned":{"haiku":"Divine betrayal shatters faith as a bewildered child realizes God has withdrawn presence and protection without explanation.","opus":"The cry of a soul that once knew a clear, unknowing peace and now feels forsaken by its God, a baffled child pleading to be carried across the water. Betrayed vows and a bitter chill have carried the heart off, leaving only the longing to be brought back.","sonnet":"A speaker cries out to God who seems to have abandoned them — left behind a grand illusion, swept away by ice cold winds, bewildered behind an old facade — calling a remembered name, Helena, from a September that felt unreal, and begging to be taken across the river wide.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~dear-editor":{"haiku":"The Zodiac killer boasts of superiority and control, mocking authorities pursuing him.","opus":"Symphonic metal voicing the real Zodiac Killer's taunting letters to the press, the ego of a murderer who craved his own movie. It inhabits the vanity of evil, the killing as performance for an audience.","sonnet":"Written as a letter from the Zodiac Killer to a newspaper editor, the song inhabits the killer's voice directly — announcing his return, assessing the detective hunting him, and demanding a film be made. A dramatic monologue of arrogance and control from inside one of history's most famous unsolved cases.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~hunter-s-season":{"haiku":"A lonely hunter seeks protection and belonging but finds only isolation and the inevitable loss of those he loves.","opus":"A doom-laden meditation on isolation and love bound to death, wishing a loved one a merciful end in someone's arms. Symphonic metal grandeur wrapped around grief and the loneliness of being born into hardship.","sonnet":"Two people, always alone and born into a season that promises suffering, are united by the speaker's deepest wish: that the other die in someone's arms rather than in isolation. In silent custody they finally meet each other's eyes, and the speaker promises to wait.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~interlude-i-dei-gratia":{"haiku":"Latin aphorisms combining fatalism and determination: nothing is new, reaching stars requires hardship, seek God's kingdom, remember death before war.","opus":"A brief interlude from the symphonic-power-metal act that stitches together a handful of ancient Latin maxims and then translates them plainly. The old sayings about futility, striving toward the stars, and keeping death in mind accumulate into a short meditation on mortality and human ambition, the aphorisms lending gravity to a passage that gestures at wisdom rather than telling a story.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~interlude-ii-un-assassinio-molto-silenzioso":{"haiku":"A man gazes at the moon longing for love while harboring thoughts of silent murder in his frozen heart.","opus":"A short bilingual interlude in Italian and English from the symphonic metal band, sketching a man staring up at the moon and wondering if love will find him. The vignette asks what could dwell in an unfeeling heart beyond the notion of a hushed killing, a spoken bridge within their larger concept story.","sonnet":"A brief Italian-language interlude presenting a man who gazes at the moon and wonders whether love will ever arrive to thaw a frozen heart. The only alternative he can conceive, living inside that cold, is a very silent murder — violence as the only other inhabitable emotion when tenderness has never come.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~march-of-mephisto":{"haiku":"A demonic figure seduces through fake sympathy and false comfort, feeding on the vulnerable's pain and isolation.","opus":"Sung in the seductive voice of the devil himself, this offers a mortal false solace by cataloguing and feeding on their grief and vanity. Behind grand Latin invocations of fortune and glory, the tempter unmasks himself as temptation, torment and worldly appetite made flesh, taunting the listener with mortality.","sonnet":"Mephistopheles speaking directly to a vulnerable soul — presenting himself as a comforter and truth-teller while actually cataloguing the person's loneliness and grief as fuel, positioning himself as the thorn in their side that ultimately owns their desire and pride.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~moonlight":{"haiku":"Someone struggles with inner shame and pride, unable to repent for past conduct, while moonlight becomes both a haunting reminder of their solitude and defiance against judgment.","opus":"Symphonic-metal defiance from someone who scorns pity and refuses to repent for a misconducted life, pride intact. The moon becomes the only guiding light he'll accept, cold consolation for a soul that won't beg for sympathy.","sonnet":"A restless, proud man refuses to beg forgiveness for a life badly lived — the moonlight offers no comfort, he won't repent, and he calls out to something beyond the sky to guide him through the darkness of his own making.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~nothing-ever-dies":{"haiku":"Humans build false faiths and temples, yet trust decays repeatedly because past authorities lied; love remains the only enduring truth.","opus":"A sweeping reflection on belief, death and the deceptions of institutions, holding that your actions come back to you and past wrongs stay permanent, while a personal strand grieves a vanished lover with a promise of reunion. Only love is named as genuine truth. The American symphonic power metal band deliver their signature orchestral grandeur and operatic sorrow, grappling with cosmic doubt at cathedral scale.","sonnet":"Faith, grief, and the persistence of love beyond death — the song moves between scepticism about organized religion, the ways competing gods are used to justify violence, and a very personal loss: someone waited their whole life for the speaker and was left behind. The religious framing and the intimate loss collapse into each other, and the consolation offered — that nothing ever dies, that voices sustain — is as much a wish as a belief.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~soul-society":{"haiku":"Human reason cannot answer whether souls or heaven exist; lives are fragments of the universe, and cosmic understanding may be forever beyond us.","opus":"Power metal wrestling honestly with doubt: a narrator who wants to believe in an afterlife and a moral order but keeps colliding with reason, sensing he's a speck in an indifferent universe. The ache is in the wishing, not the certainty.","sonnet":"A philosophical meditation on mortality and the afterlife — the narrator can't accept the idea of heaven on rational grounds but desperately wishes it were true, wrestling with the question of whether good intentions excuse wrongdoing and whether there's anything more to existence than a fragment of cosmic time.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~the-black-halo":{"haiku":"Awaiting death without fear, the speaker has accepted their fate as darkness approaches; they hold faith as their shield, knowing betrayal and loss cannot strip away their spiritual core.","opus":"Taken from the band's Faust-inspired concept, the narrator makes his peace with dying and greets the coming dark unafraid, treating it as familiar prophecy; wronged yet holding an inner will to endure, he meets his end with belief unshaken as his life dims from pale metal to nothing. Damnation and resolve entwine.","sonnet":"A figure facing death confronts betrayal — someone promised elevation and then failed them — yet they face the darkness without fear, possessing the will to survive the cold and accepting that this is where their journey ends.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~the-great-pandemonium":{"haiku":"War and chaos reign on a silent shore where God abandons the speaker to confront fear alone. Pandemonium assembles the end as conflict spreads endlessly.","opus":"A soul adrift toward apocalypse across mythic waters, confronting fear and finding God absent as the sun goes down and war summons the great pandemonium.","sonnet":"A speaker on a silent shore confronts death and divine absence — God isn't there, the war has begun, and the great pandemonium assembles as blood-red lines sweep the horizon and one final day stretches into something past reckoning.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~the-haunting-somewhere-in-time":{"haiku":"A man mistakes a woman's voice for his dead lover Helena; across time he will haunt and punish her, driven by corrupted obsession and destructive lies.","opus":"A gothic duet of obsession and mourning, a man mistaking a new woman's voice for his lost love before swearing to pursue and haunt her across time, her spectral answers threaded through. Guilt, deceit and doomed devotion intertwine. Kamelot are the symphonic power metal band, and this signature epic sets Roy Khan's brooding delivery against a guest vocalist for a theatrical ghost romance.","sonnet":"A man destroyed by the death of Helena, haunted by a woman whose voice resembles hers — he cannot tell the living from the dead, cannot stop corrupting himself from within. He promises to pursue and haunt whoever carries her echo through time, a threat and a vow at once, while the closing duet offers a small redemption: release, sleep, the possibility of being met again by someone like her somewhere beyond this grief.","src":"haiku"},
"kamelot~when-the-lights-are-down":{"haiku":"In darkness, love and truth become only sorrow; a lost lover's absence drives someone toward death, where they might finally connect.","opus":"A gothic pledge that true love is agony, joining a lost beloved in the dark and picturing union only through death. Rooted in the band's Faustian storyline, it entwines damnation with devotion, seeking in emptiness the bond that life never granted.","sonnet":"A man confesses that love is the sharpest pain he knows, and in the darkness he seeks a woman — perhaps death itself — who will take him away to live in death the love he never had while alive.","src":"haiku"},
"kana-boon~a-18qkvdr":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"KANA-BOON built their reputation on bright, propulsive Japanese rock aimed at exactly this coming-of-age energy. A boy with no presence picks up a megaphone and battles an unstable era with his voice, growing from silent youth into someone who sings out. The strategy is readiness; the voice distorts and breaks but keeps going, and from now on everything starts — the ordinary kid finding agency in sound, the band's driving momentum making that transformation feel inevitable.","sonnet":"A boy with no presence picks up a megaphone, battles the unstable era with his voice, and grows from silent youth into someone who sings out — the strategy is readiness, the voice distorts and breaks but keeps going, and from now on everything starts.","src":"haiku"},
"kandia~4-walls":{"haiku":"Layers of manipulation and false intimacy build prison walls as love becomes tool for destruction and control. The more one surrenders, the more twisted the fate becomes.","opus":"A melodic-metalcore meditation on how loving deeply draws hostility and how the most tender people become targets. As the narrator watches someone shut themselves behind barriers that no longer offer safety, the loneliness thickens and everything feels altered, an ache for a partner slipping into concealment, pleaded with to remain even as the light fails.","sonnet":"Isolation hardens around someone who gave themselves fully — breathed, felt, healed — only to find that love invites hatred and visibility invites loss; the four walls no longer feel like home because hiding away from light is the only protection left, yet the people who drove them there are begging them to stay.","src":"haiku"},
"kanga~home":{"haiku":"Profound alienation persists even amid crowds; the narrator inhabits a parallel interior space separated from authentic belonging and intimate presence.","opus":"Alienation so complete that a crowded room only sharpens the isolation, home out of reach even while you're standing in it. Darkwave despair about belonging nowhere, the ache of wanting out of your own life.","sonnet":"Profound isolation in a crowded world — the speaker feels invisible, unable to make any place feel like home, and caught in a dissociated state of waiting for something that never arrives while the desire to belong anywhere quietly erodes.","src":"haiku"},
"kansas~carry-on-wayward-son":{"haiku":"Spiritual suffering and illusion surround the seeker; faith and perseverance become the only means of transcendence.","opus":"A restless seeker who flew too high chasing meaning, haunted by doubt and the fear that his wisdom is a charade. The promised peace is rest at journey's end, hard-won after storms of self-questioning.","sonnet":"A restless seeker who has soared and crashed, who thought wisdom and vision would redeem him but found himself still mad and blind, is counseled by a chorus voice to lay down his weariness — the destination is peace, and the instruction is simply to keep going despite the storms.","src":"haiku"},
"kansas~cheyenne-anthem":{"haiku":"Dispossession and cultural erasure threaten indigenous sovereignty; ancestral bonds to land prove unbreakable despite forced displacement.","opus":"The Cheyenne people's lament as they're driven from ancestral land, refusing the notion anyone can own the earth. A dignified elegy for a way of life doomed by conquest, mourning without surrendering its truth.","sonnet":"Sung from a Cheyenne perspective, the song mourns the forced displacement from ancestral land with grief and defiance: the people offer to share the earth — no one owns it — but as the wheels of war turn they understand they cannot endure forever, and their bodies will remain in the ground of their homeland even as the nation destroys them.","src":"haiku"},
"kaonashi~an-evening-of-moving-pictures-with-scooter-corkle":{"opus":"A frantic, theatrical portrait of infatuation and codependent longing, staged like a romantic comedy the narrator refuses to be abandoned in. Hours of FaceTime, obsessive validation-seeking, and initials carved into a tree escalate into desperate declarations of love. Yearning tips into unease, devotion so total it borders on unraveling.","sonnet":"A person named Jamie is desperately, overwhelmingly in love with Casey: hours of FaceTime, obsessive need for validation, fantasy of being caught when falling — the love is carved into trees and skin, naively total and completely consuming.","src":"sonnet"},
"kaphy~idontwannastay":{"haiku":"Suffocation and displacement intensify until the speaker needs escape at any cost; meaning collapses into the urgent desire to abandon this life entirely.","opus":"A drowning need to escape a life that no longer makes sense, surrounded by lies with luck run out. The chorus is a single desperate wish to get away.","sonnet":"About wanting to escape a situation — or a life, or a state of mind — that has become unbearable and makes no sense, where everything around the narrator is lies and they are losing blood (metaphorically, probably literally emotionally). The only desire is to get away; there is no destination, just the need to leave.","src":"haiku"},
"kardashev~between-sea-and-sky":{"haiku":"A navigator sails by starlight toward the planet's edge, humbled by endlessness while memories drown in waves and crystalline visions sink into darkness.","opus":"The Arizona deathgaze band drift across dark, mirror-still waters, a lone navigator steering by the stars toward the world's rim. Immensity dwarfs the speaker as everything familiar dissolves and creation slips into darkness. Calling on the wind to bear him onward, the ocean crossing plays as a meditation on insignificance and disappearance, their atmospheric heaviness bent toward cosmic awe.","sonnet":"A solo voyage under starlight reaches the edge of the known world. The immensity of open water and sky dismantles everything previously understood — inner and outer landscapes merge at a thousand fathoms deep — and the only imperative left is to keep moving forward into the boundless.","src":"haiku"},
"kardi~7000rpm":{"haiku":"Unstoppable forward momentum silences critics and doubters as confidence accelerates past capacity for reflection or restraint.","opus":"A high-octane K-rock declaration of unstoppable self-belief, the narrator racing their own solo course while dismissing snickering doubters behind their back. Heart pounding on an autobahn with the RPM in the red zone, victory is treated as foregone. It reaches for adrenalized defiance, daring detractors to watch as momentum and fire carry them past every obstacle.","sonnet":"The speaker tunes out mockery and gossip, committing entirely to a solo race — heart already in the red zone, RPM maxed — confident the winner is already decided and the only direction is forward. (lang: ko)","src":"haiku"},
"kardi~not-but-disco":{"haiku":"Escapism through sensory dissolution—rejecting societal judgment and false identity, the narrator asks listeners to abandon perception and surrender to dance, stripping away self-awareness in release.","opus":"A Korean dance track urging surrender to movement as liberation from a distorted, judgmental world. Shedding plastic faith and blurring out what can't be seen or heard, it reframes imperfection as the source of beauty. Set in a cramped kaleidoscope for two, it reaches toward defiant joy, dancing through even the worst of endings as an act of freedom.","sonnet":"A dance-floor manifesto about shedding social masks and external judgment: the song tells its listener that the distorted view of them comes from the world, not from a crooked mirror, and that only in this disco space — stripped of pretense and noise — are they free to be purely themselves, even if the world ends tomorrow.","src":"haiku"},
"karen-di~3am":{"haiku":"Insomnia and forced labor collapse into single complaint, exhaustion from relentless obligation and refusal to rest.","opus":"A sleep-deprived scream of frustration, the narrator wide awake at three in the morning wanting nothing but rest, jealously flinging at someone who doesn't have to work the next day. Built almost entirely from repetition, it captures the delirious, cursing exhaustion of insomnia, the maddening loop of a mind that won't shut off turned into a blunt, profane mantra of wanting only to finally, mercifully sleep.","sonnet":"Insomnia distilled to a single blunt complaint — it is three in the morning, sleep is the only want, and something or someone is preventing it. The repetition is the point: the exhaustion loops, the resentment builds, and the refrain that the other party doesn't have to work in the morning sharpens the injustice into something almost funny in its rawness.","src":"haiku"},
"karen-di~do-it":{"haiku":"The speaker burns with desire to demolish their oppressive life and seize what they want through sheer willpower; no external permission or approval is needed. Refusal and defiance sustain agency.","opus":"Karen Di sets a chant of self-propulsion against a mounting urge to burn the whole city down. Sick of the bullshit and done trying to merely survive, the narrator insists no one else will act for her, so she keeps breathing and pushing toward what she's reaching for. Wanting it all with no storm able to topple her, it reads as raw, cornered determination.","sonnet":"Frustration with stagnation and an environment that feels suffocating breaks into a resolution to move forward entirely under her own power. The city she wants to burn is the world of obstacles and bullshit she has tolerated too long; what replaces fury is a quiet certainty that no one else will close the distance for her.","src":"haiku"},
"karen-di~i-hope-you-know":{"haiku":"Anxiety-driven withdrawal from someone you love, begging them not to confuse your fear for disinterest.","opus":"Someone whose anxiety and panic keep sabotaging a relationship, pleading that the withdrawn, hurtful version isn't the real her. It captures how mental illness makes you push away the very person you keep crawling back to.","sonnet":"About anxiety making you a bad communicator in a relationship — the narrator went silent during a mental health episode, knows she pushed someone away with behavior that wasn't really her, and desperately wants the other person to understand the person they fell for is still in there. The fear of self-sabotaging something real runs throughout.","src":"haiku"},
"karen-di~my-world":{"haiku":"Breaking free from imprisonment and control, asserting total sovereignty over personal space and refusing others' narrative.","opus":"A defiant assertion of autonomy: caged by someone who tried to define and control her, the speaker claims the world as her own and casts herself as this man's opposite in every respect. She refuses to let petty games or fights into her head, insists she will never change, and tells him to leave and get over it.","sonnet":"A declaration of reclaimed autonomy after someone tried to cage and control the narrator — the cage was their worst mistake. Stupid games won't get in the narrator's head, and the only instruction left for the antagonist is \"get out.\"","src":"haiku"},
"karen-di~sick-ride":{"haiku":"Revenge and female solidarity manifest through stolen property and reckless speed, defiance against predators embodied in a joyride that leaves regret in the dust.","opus":"A joyriding revenge romp, a boyfriend's stolen car turned into a raised finger at a cheater and every creep on the corner. Pure punk swagger, female solidarity floored with the windows down.","sonnet":"A revenge joy-ride: a woman steals her cheating boyfriend's car, loads it with her friends, speeds away at a hundred miles an hour, and beats back a group of men who try to mess with them — the whole song a portrait of female solidarity and gleeful, unapologetic retaliation.","src":"haiku"},
"karen-di~so-funny":{"haiku":"Realizing someone you admired was manipulative and shallow, finding freedom in letting them go.","opus":"The bitter relief of seeing an ex clearly, realizing the person you idolized was just a user working through interchangeable girls. Freedom arrives laced with the sting of having been fooled, defiance built over a wound.","sonnet":"About the embarrassment of looking back on an infatuation with someone who turned out to be a manipulative user — the narrator laughs at how naive she was, recognizes the pattern of being used like countless other girls, and finds liberation in finally seeing clearly and walking away.","src":"haiku"},
"karen-di~stupid":{"opus":"The self-directed anger of realizing too late that a two-faced partner was lying all along, the signs missed and the truth swallowed. Calling herself stupid over and over is both blame and dawning liberation.","sonnet":"The moment of recognition after being betrayed by someone two-faced — anger and self-blame at having missed obvious signs and stayed silent — the narrator cycles through frustration at their own willingness to be taken advantage of.","src":"sonnet"},
"karen-o-with-trent-reznor-and-atticus-ross~immigrant-song":{"haiku":"Viking conquest and empire-building glorify violence and domination, celebrating invasion and subjugation as inevitable destiny.","opus":"A brooding cover riding the Viking-conquest fantasy of warriors out of a frozen north, hammer-driven toward the western shore. Under the Valhalla battle-cry sits the twist that trust and peace might carry the day, the plundered land told to rebuild from its ruins.","sonnet":"Sung from the perspective of Norse raiders — glorying in conquest, their voyage from the frozen north to new shores, and their dominance over the lands they seize, though closing with a grudging acknowledgment that peace might yet prevail.","src":"haiku","fable":"Plant sang this as a Viking's boast, conquest as sheer vitality; Reznor and Ross strip the swagger to industrial dread and Karen O delivers the overlord's line not as triumph but as a threat spat through clenched teeth, which is the whole reinterpretation. Recorded for Fincher's Dragon Tattoo title sequence, the cover exposes what the strut always concealed: a predator narrating its own violence to the fields it means to burn, the promise of Valhalla curdling into the flat certainty of arrival. Where the original rode momentum, this version fixes on the closing turn — the sudden pivot to rebuilding ruins and peace winning the day — and lets it read as cold mockery, the conqueror instructing the conquered to be grateful, so the song's real subject becomes the voice of domination that has learned to speak in the language of reconciliation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["violence & murder","war & battle","artifice & performance"],"means":"the conqueror's reconciliation speech exposed as instruction to the conquered to be grateful","built":"industrial stripping of the swagger lets the closing peace-pivot read as cold mockery rather than redemption"},
"karen-o~rapt":{"haiku":"Intoxicated rapture and addictive attachment entangle pleasure with pain; the speaker becomes absorbed in a lover who promises transformation through consumption.","opus":"Love as an addiction Karen O both resents and craves, tender and profane in the same breath. The push-pull of needing someone who wrecks you, and half-hoping it might feel new again anyway.","sonnet":"Love arrives as a sudden, consuming force — strobing, disorienting, beautiful and brutal — leaving the speaker wondering whether this particular habit is one they need or one that will inevitably leave them blue, suspended between surrender and self-preservation.","src":"haiku"},
"karmanjakah~first-sun":{"haiku":"Rising alone with a weakened heart and low self-worth, watching others with esteem. The speaker ascends awaiting the first light while leaving everything behind.","opus":"A frigid daybreak reckoning, the narrator scaling the horizon alone with a withered heart and an icy droplet of grief, telling someone he no longer cares whether they vanish and that they can call for him uselessly while he waits to rise. The atmospheric progressive metal outfit ride shimmering, aurora-lit textures, turning a bitter severance into something austere, cosmic and quietly victorious.","sonnet":"The morning after an emotional collapse — the speaker climbs the horizon alone, heart atrophied, watching with low esteem as a connection dissolves. There is no plea for the other person to stay; the song pivots into cold indifference, telling them to pretend happiness and fade if they want. The speaker's waiting is not for reconciliation but for their own ascension, a dawn they intend to reach without anyone else.","src":"haiku"},
"karnivool~a-m-war": {"haiku":"Time is running out and change is necessary; if others won't respect life or choose to stay, the speaker must walk away from those who won't value what matters.","opus":"Karnivool stage an end-times parting: running low on time, the speakers steel themselves to abandon someone who won't honor the life entrusted to them. The recurring urge to clear out doubles as both self-preservation and verdict, dismissing the other as an insignificant smudge on the landscape, defective from the start.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone whose self-destructive behavior has exhausted everyone around them — the ultimatum is implicit: respect the life you have or be walked away from, because time is nearly up.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a farewell delivered as ultimatum: a departing voice tells someone who squanders the one existence cupped in his hands that the exit is already open and the going is not up for debate. Karnivool render collapse as arithmetic, ticking down borrowed days while the chorus shrinks the wasteful man to a smear of dried pigment, decorative and inert against a backdrop he mistook for his own accomplishment. The bridge dissolves into a single compulsion to clear the doorway, the phrase hammered flat until sheer motion becomes the last available truth, and the parting terms land without cruelty: settle your affairs or rot where you stand.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","death & grief"],"means":"departure framed as the only mercy for a wasted life","built":"the bridge hammers one phrase flat until motion replaces argument"},
"karnivool~aeons": {"haiku":"Existential crisis after seeking truth; the singer regrets learning hard truths and now feels isolated and poisoned, confronting mortality and helplessness.","opus":"A song about coming to and sensing a slow poison spreading inside, along with regret for having chased a hurtful truth; confronting what feels like his own funeral, the narrator hopes someone saves a spot for him, dreading that his next breath will bring flame and that a chemical blaze will announce their collective end.","sonnet":"A song about environmental or civilizational collapse seen through the lens of personal dread — chemical fires signal the end, the narrator feels something wrong in his veins like poison, and the only hope is that someone somewhere holds a place far from the flames.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor bargains with an unseen witness for shelter, admitting the truth he chased has only shown him that no one is coming and the two of them now face the ending alone. The song moves like a body checking itself after a blast, groping for a heartbeat and testing whether the next breath will come clean or scald the lungs. Karnivool wire that dread into a slow-building march that pulls under like a rising tide, then close on a chant that treats a poisoned world's last glow as the marker of who has already been erased.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","death & grief"],"means":"two survivors facing an ending with no rescuer coming","built":"a slow-building march enacts a tide that pulls under rather than releases"},
"karnivool~all-i-know": {"haiku":"A soul taker and mime figure questions reality, gravity, and whether shared consciousness exists, ultimately asking if everything known is now ending.","opus":"An Australian prog band pushing at the edge of ego and perception, the narrator claiming a soul-taking, cruel-witted self while asking whether anyone is of like mind. It's about severing the umbilical cords of received reality and facing the vertigo of your certainties ending.","sonnet":"About two people who have cut themselves free from gravity and conventional reality together — the narrator questions whether their shared detachment is genuine connection or just mutual performance, asking if everything they know is coming to an end.","src":"haiku","fable":"A voice narrates its own transformation as a launch, casting off tethers like an infant severing the cord and drifting where gravity loses its grip. Karnivool's polyrhythmic build keeps turning outward, asking a listener to come along and answer whether they share the same wiring, so the ascent becomes a plea rather than a solitary escape. The chant to simply release, repeated until it reads like breathing, insists the disorientation is the destination: what once ordered the world has flipped, grown too vivid to fake one's way past. And the ending it keeps circling toward is not a loss but the terror of shedding everything familiar and choosing to climb higher anyway.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"disorientation named as the destination rather than the cost of ascent","built":"the release chant repeated until it reads like breathing rather than a plea"},
"karnivool~all-it-takes": {"haiku":"Deception and self-destruction; people knowingly follow false paths, accepting consequence as inevitable cost.","opus":"Australian prog band Karnivool press a listener on their willful blindness and complicity, refusing to let self-deception pass as innocence. As crowds mindlessly follow a leader in a cynical, hamster-wheel cycle of thrill and violence, the song counsels patience for an inevitable reckoning that everyone will eventually be forced to hear.","sonnet":"A cynical observer confronts someone who has surrendered to a seductive but hollow system — a rat-wheel of belief, reset, and repetition. With patient detachment he maps the cycle, waiting for the one sound too loud to ignore while offering a mirror to those still willing to look.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about complicity disguised as a warning, where the voice interrogating a naive believer turns out to be marching in the same procession, arms raised in the same surrender. Karnivool build it as a machine that keeps resetting rather than an argument that progresses: the addict's loop of paying and killing for a thrill, the treadmill spun by a caged animal, each verse wiping itself clean to start the chase again. The repeated question is never really answered because it isn't aimed outward at all; it's a needle turned back on a singer who admits he is dissolving even as he pledges to keep following this figure downward. What the ritual patience and the plea for a faith worth surrendering to finally expose is the appetite under the sermon — the demand that someone else supply a belief costly enough to justify the fall.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"a warning aimed outward that turns back on the one issuing it","built":"each verse resets rather than progresses, enacting the addict's loop it describes"},
"karnivool~all-it-takes-2025-remastered-version": {"haiku":"Deception and self-destruction; people knowingly follow false paths, accepting consequence as inevitable cost.","opus":"Australian prog band Karnivool press a listener on their willful blindness and complicity, refusing to let self-deception pass as innocence. As crowds mindlessly follow a leader in a cynical, hamster-wheel cycle of thrill and violence, the song counsels patience for an inevitable reckoning that everyone will eventually be forced to hear.","sonnet":"A cynical observer confronts someone who has surrendered to a seductive but hollow system — a rat-wheel of belief, reset, and repetition. With patient detachment he maps the cycle, waiting for the one sound too loud to ignore while offering a mirror to those still willing to look.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about complicity disguised as a warning, where the voice interrogating a naive believer turns out to be marching in the same procession, arms raised in the same surrender. Karnivool build it as a machine that keeps resetting rather than an argument that progresses: the addict's loop of paying and killing for a thrill, the treadmill spun by a caged animal, each verse wiping itself clean to start the chase again. The repeated question is never really answered because it isn't aimed outward at all; it's a needle turned back on a singer who admits he is dissolving even as he pledges to keep following this figure downward. What the ritual patience and the plea for a faith worth surrendering to finally expose is the appetite under the sermon — the demand that someone else supply a belief costly enough to justify the fall.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"a warning aimed outward that turns back on the one issuing it","built":"each verse resets rather than progresses, enacting the addict's loop it describes"},
"karnivool~alpha-omega": {"haiku":"Humanity waits for salvation that never arrives; the song urges people to abandon false hope and burn away complacency by taking action themselves.","opus":"A furious address to a silent, disappointing God over a shamed world. Humanity, once told to praise and then deserted, refuses both self-pity and passivity, resolving instead to smash convention, torch its dull existence, and rebuild collectively, with a pointed warning to be found on the right side when judgment lands.","sonnet":"Humanity has been waiting for divine guidance that never comes, left in a disgraceful world to figure out whether to keep trying or burn it all down — ultimately resolving toward collective action, breaking the mold and building something new together.","src":"haiku","fable":"A prayer that curdles into an ultimatum: Karnivool frame a people abandoned by whatever they once sang hallelujahs to, and their answer is to quit petitioning and torch the whole disappointing world themselves. The bridge's mechanical hammering of a single demand — split the cast, shake the earth loose — enacts the exhaustion of a crowd that has tried and failed too often to keep asking politely. So watching the horizon for rescue hardens into a lit match held to the mundane, and survival becomes an arsonist's wager that he is worthier than everything he burns.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","faith & the occult","violence & murder"],"means":"petitioning exhausted into arson, prayer curdled into a lit match","built":"the bridge hammers a single demand until pleading becomes detonation"},
"karnivool~animation": {"haiku":"A person confronts the illusion of hope and the finality of a fall, struggling to know their identity when nothing can transform them or provide resolution.","opus":"The Australian progressive band urge a plunge overboard to experience the drop and win release, dismissing hope as a delusion that allows no closure. They circle unsure futures of choosing and refusing, asking how anyone can grasp their own identity when nothing at hand can alter them, letting ego and self-injury simply expire.","sonnet":"A confrontation with self-deception and collective cowardice. Hope is named an illusion because resolve never follows it; honesty was almost achieved but fear intervened. The speaker wants release — to fall freely and trust survival — while the larger argument is that denial is the road most traveled and the hardest part is always fear.","src":"haiku","fable":"Karnivool stage abandonment as a plea for release, the narrator hurling himself from a foundering vessel and insisting he'll survive the drop, treating rupture as the only honest escape. The refrain keeps tallying a bargain that never settles, a promise split endlessly between yes and refusal, until the ledger itself becomes the cage rather than the door. Beneath the polyrhythmic churn sits a colder suspicion the band lets curdle: that whatever we run toward leaves us unaltered, and dread of that stillness outweighs any shipwreck.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"rupture staged as the only honest exit from a foundering vessel","built":"the unsettled ledger refrain becomes the cage rather than the door"},
"karnivool~aozora": {"haiku":"Caged existence obscures reason; the subject seeks escape from sycophantic servitude.","opus":"People crouched in camouflage in a war-torn land with a dead radio and no signal, fed empty promises by leaders and a fawning cause. The narrator can't sleep and aches for someone beyond the front to prove peace exists, forever awaiting a breakout into open blue sky.","sonnet":"Two people hiding in camouflage, sleepless and cut off from signal, ask whether they will ever escape the false hopes and sycophantic causes holding them blind — one sends a letter from outside asking what peace is like, while the other is still waiting for the great escape, holding on.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about hunkering down under a dead radio and manufactured hope, sung to someone the narrator can't stop watching for signs of surrender. That watching curdles: the tender mirror question flips into a hand pressing a head below water, and deliverance stops meaning rescue and starts meaning whichever of them quits fighting first. The chant that carries it refuses to resolve, because the release being begged for keeps arriving as one more verse of the same cornered bargaining, blue sky named only as the thing on the far shore.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title is Japanese for blue sky: the word the bunkered voices are waiting for names the open air itself, the one thing the song never lets inside.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","violence & murder","freedom & escape"],"means":"tenderness curdling into a hand pressing the other below water","built":"the chant refuses to resolve, each verse arriving as one more cornered bargain"},
"karnivool~c-o-t-e":{"haiku":"Fragmented communication and emotional distance crystallize into an impasse where understanding fails and only isolation remains as both people retreat into separate realities.","opus":"A fraught plea across a widening estrangement, the speaker sensing the other no longer likes or resembles him yet begging them to hold on and not leave. Between apathy and drowning, he keeps insisting they face the world together for at least a moment.","sonnet":"A fractured dialogue between two selves or two people falling out of sync: questions about choice and reason go unanswered as apathy threatens to swallow the connection, yet the speaker holds on — \"I won't leave you\" — urging stillness before facing the world together.","src":"haiku"},
"karnivool~change": {"haiku":"The speaker breaks free from reflection and becomes fire itself; imagination answers questions and transforms the burden of being into light.","opus":"A hallucinatory meditation on transformation and mortality, the certainty that no one escapes death, built on the idea of sound as the raw material of everything that exists. The deeper thread is surrender to time's undoing of all things, dissolving even as we wonder who we are and face our own end.","sonnet":"A song about confronting your own complicity and stagnation — the narrator has become a martyr to the very side he was fighting against, still afraid to fall or change, while around him everything burns away and the world won't wait.","src":"haiku","fable":"Karnivool build this around an esoteric claim that vibration is the substrate holding all matter in shape, then use it to reframe personal dissolution as continuity rather than extinction. Kenny's narrator arrives cornered by guilt, having become the exact adversary he once opposed, pinned too low to rise even while drowning in his own mirrored reflection. The metaphysics rescue him: if reality is only sustained pattern, then decay is transformation, and the closing fissure splitting a barrier lets everyone slip through toward whatever comes next. What reads as prophecy is really the band's one available consolation, that nothing being permanent is precisely what makes the ending survivable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"What plays as a self-contained epic is really the back half of one composition, chopped at the peak of its climax for lack of studio time. Guitarist Drew Goddard has said Change was written as a single piece whose first half landed on Themata as Change (Part 1), and the spoken passage about sound as the basis of form is narration attributed to cymatics researcher Peter Guy Manners.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"metaphysics offered as the one available consolation for dissolution","built":"the fissure splitting a barrier letting everyone through toward what comes next"},
"karnivool~change-part-1": {"haiku":"Internal fragmentation and emotional distance mark transformation; the speaker sketches love in divergent light, climbing rope toward unclear destination.","opus":"A brief, jagged portrait of a volatile relationship where the speaker would trade everything for a piece of the other's anger, climbing out feeling good while daring them to stare him down. Change here is the wary shift of power between two people.","sonnet":"A fragmented expression of volatile attraction — offering to trade peace for pieces of someone's anger, climbing toward them, trying to reframe their dynamic in a different light; short and impressionistic, it captures a moment of tense, unresolved desire.","src":"haiku","fable":"A young band bargaining with someone else's temper, offering to swap the sound of glass shattering for a fistful of that person's rage. The speaker keeps climbing anyway, hauling up a rope while daring the other to hold his gaze, insisting he can recast them in a kinder light — a squirming push-pull between wanting to fix a relationship and refusing to back down inside it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"It sounds like a fragment because it is one: the front half of a composition split across two albums. Its continuation is Sound Awake's twelve-minute Change, cut apart at the climax by the band's own account.","themes":["anger & defiance","love & desire"],"means":"refusing to back down while bargaining to recast the other kindly"},
"karnivool~cote":{"haiku":"Communication fails to create connection; voices and words ring hollow, leaving isolation despite effort to reach another. The dream remains silent and unshared.","opus":"Karnivool, the Australian prog band known for their intricate builds, cloak a study of isolation in shifting textures. The narrator feels quietly rejected, retreats into a numb inner world, and asks to be steadied through a fractured outlook. Torn between drowning in indifference and craving contact, the song asks someone to confront reality, even briefly.","sonnet":"Someone reaches across a growing distance toward another person they feel slipping away, sensing that mutual understanding has broken down without knowing quite when it started. The appeal is urgent — hold on, do not drift — while the emotional weather of the soul is described as something beyond rational control. Beneath the tension is a refusal to let go unannounced.","src":"haiku"},
"karnivool~deadman": {"haiku":"A person experiences cycles of pain, suffering, and emotional paralysis while confronting their own past and the breakdown of relationships. They realize they cannot escape the pattern and must let go.","opus":"Australian prog wrestling with pain as something the narrator feeds on, a door he keeps opening, tangled with lost love and a God who isn't where he'd hoped. It's a long reckoning with self-destruction and the hard acceptance that some things have to end.","sonnet":"A relationship or situation reaching its terminal point — the speaker realizes they've been caught in someone else's web, the exit is near, God isn't where they hoped, and the water is dark and cold as they face that everything must end.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drowning man narrates his own downfall in real time, cataloguing every wound as if the darkening bruise were proof he is still someone worth accounting for. Karnivool run the panic and the calm on one current, so the frantic pleading to understand the reason keeps circling back to a stubborn ritual of self-recital, a man clutching the shape of who he is while the water closes over him. By the end the demand for an answer collapses into surrender, the voice trading its fury for a few shards of another person's rage, and the escape it kept promising resolves not into rescue but into agreeing to let the whole thing go under.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Hang on past the closer's apparent ending and a hidden coda arrives, a re-recorded Change (Part 1). The Themata fragment returns with vibraphone and slightly altered words, good turned to God.","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"cataloguing wounds as proof of selfhood while the water closes over","built":"panic and calm run on one current until fury surrenders to letting go"},
"karnivool~drone": {"haiku":"Collective manipulation through false promises draws people into control; the speaker refuses rescue or empathy, demanding agency over the mechanisms that would consume them.","opus":"A brooding prog-rock meditation on herd mentality, faith, and the seductive pull of collective delusion. The narrator watches people follow a promised savior while something insidious moves to control the willing. Resisting empathy figured as an enemy, they'd rather stare into the burning sun than be rescued or absorbed. It reaches toward the struggle to stay lucid and self-possessed amid mass surrender.","sonnet":"A warning against the shape that waits to take you over — conformity, mass belief, the empathy that enables control rather than frees. The narrator refuses to look down, wants to stare at the sun until it burns out, and names the drone-logic of following along as the real enemy even as only a few remain lucid enough to resist it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A refusal to look at the ground, dressed as willed clarity: the narrator fixes on a punishing light until it goes dark, choosing to be blinded over joining a crowd that chants toward one promised figure. Karnivool have long built their arcs on the strain between surrender and vigilance, and here it curdles into an inversion where kindness reads as a trap, the soft hand that would pull him back from an edge he means to walk. His refrain keeps circling a shape that aches and creeps toward overtaking him, the swarm and the numbing hum of the title at once, so his answer is to peel himself loose from an inherited faith while he can still think straight. The three-times plea to be left unsaved hardens across each pass from a wince into a stance, and the song never settles whether this is waking up or coming apart.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","faith & the occult","freedom & escape"],"means":"refusing rescue as the one available act of willed clarity","built":"each repetition of the plea to be left unsaved hardens from wince to stance"},
"karnivool~eidolon": {"haiku":"Someone struggles with self-deception and internal contradiction, unable to change despite recognizing the pattern and feeling progressively numb.","opus":"An eidolon is a phantom double, and the song wrestles with self-deception and lost authenticity, changes that change nothing, a smile that won't return. The Australian band lets numbness settle where redemption should be.","sonnet":"A song about self-deception and the impossibility of genuine change — the narrator keeps daring to free his mind but arrives at the same inert place, numb and taken under, while the ghost image of a relationship's beginning recedes beyond recovery.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about a phantom self-image that keeps failing its own honesty test: the speaker rehearses confession and defiance, yet every reckoning lands him exactly where he started, still numb, still stalled. Karnivool build this as a loop by design, the chorus circling the same admission of paralysis so that motion and stasis fuse into one gesture, and the closing refrain repeats until surrender hardens into settled fact. Its haunting recurring question — whether anything on offer is even authentic — turns the whole piece into a man interrogating his own reflection and discovering only a hollow copy that no longer answers to him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"confession and defiance land the speaker back exactly where he started","built":"circular structure enacts the paralysis the lyrics describe"},
"karnivool~fear-of-the-sky":{"haiku":"The song explores a mind struggling with lies, failure, and the act of concealing truth within comfort. The protagonist confronts their own tendency toward self-deception and false promises made to themselves.","opus":"A dissolving mind slips in and out of time, seeking warmth from the sun while comfort seeps through deception and concealment. The Australian progressive-rock band favor mood over clarity, and the imagery of yielding and conceding a promised paradise conveys a consciousness giving itself over to disorientation rather than fighting back to solid ground.","sonnet":"Disorientation and emotional collapse fold into one another as someone admits they have been living inside a comfortable lie. The act of staring directly at the sun — blinding oneself willingly — becomes the image for how deeply they have chosen feeling over clarity, and how that choice continues to define them.","src":"haiku"},
"karnivool~float": {"haiku":"Barely scraping by on minimal sufficiency; freedom remains out of reach, a sung refrain people cannot join. The desire to escape and float away meets an unspoken barrier of constraint.","opus":"A cryptic prog-rock reflection on longing and limitation, people chasing just enough freedom to get by while a love or transcendence stays perpetually out of reach, understood only in glimpses no one is quite allowed.","sonnet":"A song about the perpetual insufficiency of survival — just enough to get by, cornered enough to make a sound — and the freedoms everyone chases that slip away the moment you seem close, the refrain becoming a resigned mantra.","src":"haiku","fable":"A meditation on scarcity worn as a survival posture, where a crowd settles for the barest ration and calls that peace, rehearsing a shared refrain to convince itself the arrangement was ever a choice. Karnivool set that resignation adrift in a weightless, tidal groove, so the reach toward some withheld permanence keeps surfacing and dissolving without ever quite breaking loose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape"],"means":"resignation rehearsed as a collective refrain until it passes for choice","built":"the weightless tidal groove performs the drift the lyrics can't escape"},
"karnivool~ghost": {"haiku":"Society manufactures conformity and numbness in the young while burning futures; individualism fades as everyone becomes a distant ghost of themselves.","opus":"A populace sleepwalking into creeping despotism, offering up its children while feeling nothing and dimming like an apparition caught in oncoming beams. The pull to flee for good crashes against the shame of denying what is plainly true.","sonnet":"A lament for collective moral and spiritual numbness — society sanctions the destruction of the young, disasters pass without feeling, and people slowly fade like ghosts in headlights, pretending everything is fine while the evidence of collapse is undeniable.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about the numbness that lets ordinary people wave through cruelty, sung by a narrator who can name the moral drift but cannot make himself resist it. Karnivool build the argument out of repetition: the pre-loaded excuses that nobody intended any of it accumulate on a loop until they harden into ritual, and the refrain's deadened sameness performs the desensitization it describes rather than merely reporting it. The band's tension-and-release instinct turns the fade into the thesis, staging a self that dissolves because it has agreed to stop recognizing what it is doing. What keeps the track from pure despair is a small, worn plea to another person to stay and share the pretense, for one suspended breath, that the collapse underway is not really happening.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","shame & repression","politics & society"],"means":"excuses accumulate on a loop until moral drift hardens into ritual","built":"the refrain's deadened repetition performs the desensitization it describes"},
"karnivool~goliath":{"haiku":"An indictment of deception and manipulation by corrupt leaders and institutions. The narrator exposes false guidance and calls for awakening, framing the fight as spiritual resistance against entrenched lies.","opus":"A defiant confrontation with a manipulative deceiver who lulls people into complacent sleep, the singer urging everyone to wake up. He rails against a lying sellout and vows to rise up and set the wrongs right.","sonnet":"The song accuses a deceptive, blood-sucking figure of manipulating and exploiting others, calling them out as a liar who demands worship while people remain asleep to the deception. There is an urge to wake up and confront this oppressor before it is too late.","src":"haiku","fable":"An indictment of a false idol that curdles, mid-song, into an indictment of the self. The speaker builds a prosecutor's case against a giant they once served and crawled for, promising revolt against its lies in the choruses, the whole thing scored to Karnivool's signature polyrhythmic churn where the ground never quite holds still under you. But the bridge is where the mask slips: the accuser confesses to being conscious and dreaming at once, unable to solve the very trap they are railing against, and the third verse's demand to open your eyes turns ambiguous, hurled as much inward as at the enemy. By the outro the collapse is total, the giant returning over and over while the command to come to consciousness has shrunk from a battle cry to something closer to a plea. The song is a mirror, not a slingshot: whatever monster it names, it can only stone by admitting it built the thing and lives inside it.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"the accuser confessing they built and live inside the thing they are railing against","built":"polyrhythmic ground that never holds still enacts the instability the lyrics name"},
"karnivool~illumine": {"haiku":"Humanity serves a higher power and loses itself in fear; the speaker warns against empty words and false promises while questioning what becomes of human failings.","opus":"Karnivool wrestles with fear, doubt and the seductive pull of unity, becoming servant to a light that both sustains and consumes. The tension is between surrendering the self to something greater and the longing to still be allowed to dream.","sonnet":"A song about surrendering ego and individuality to become part of something larger — servants of the sun, joined as one — with an undercurrent of anxiety about whether that surrender is wisdom or defeat. The narrator wants to see what the collective sees but isn't sure the words that promise salvation will actually save anyone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hymn of merging that keeps flinching from its own rapture, torn between whether dissolving into the many is deliverance or a quieter kind of erasure. It opens on the defensive, urging you to seal your ears because the voices out there are precisely what fractures a person, then flips that guardedness inside out in a chorus that folds the self into a solar cult claiming it can never be brought down. Karnivool, at their most Tool-adjacent, let devotion and enlightenment ride the same words so surrender reads at once as the drowning man's release and the convert's ecstasy. The honest fracture is the bridge, where the speaker would trade all he has just to glimpse the vision the others already hold and be permitted to keep dreaming, the outsider's ache at a belonging he suspects is a lovely fiction. Even the parting hush, telling you not to panic, is left poised between comfort and warning, dawn or the glare that kindles a body right before it burns.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"devotion and erasure riding the same words toward the same surrender","built":"the parting comfort left poised between dawn and the light that burns"},
"karnivool~l1fel1ke": {"haiku":"Fragile emotional intimacy is fractured by mistrust and manipulation, with one partner using and abusing the other's vulnerability. The song captures betrayal disguised as lifelike tenderness.","opus":"Addiction and codependency braided together, the narrator disclaiming fault while watching himself and someone else slip away on a chemical high that promises freedom and delivers fragility. The comedown is where the truth waits.","sonnet":"The song depicts a relationship corroded by dependency and substance use, where one person watches another slip away while denying responsibility. The speaker oscillates between fragility and the offer of an escape — a place of ecstasy — that looks more like enabling than salvation.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is addiction written as a toxic love affair, where the drug and the person owe each other the same demands: to be trusted, to be blamed, to be the one waiting in the dark. The refrain of coming down and being fragile isn't remorse so much as a loop the narrator already knows he can't break — the plea to be told it's alright arrives pre-defeated, more ritual than hope, and the recurring insistence that it's not my fault is the tell, an exculpation repeated until it stops meaning anything. Where the song develops is the bridge: the pull toward erasing the self collapses into a seductive invitation to run away to a private ecstasy, and that offer curdles instantly into an image of kissing bones — the promised escape and the corpse it produces named in the same breath. As the single that announced Themata, it stakes out early the band's habit of letting a hook carry something genuinely unwell underneath, the sweetness of the chorus doing the work of the addiction it describes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"exculpation rehearsed until it evacuates its own meaning","built":"the hook's sweetness enacting the addiction it describes"},
"karnivool~life-like": {"haiku":"Trust turns toxic and trust-giving becomes abuse; the speaker questions whether sacrifice and submission have any meaning or value.","opus":"Addiction and codependency braided together: the narrator watches a lover slip while both feed each other a habit, refusing blame even as he chases the high he calls ecstasy, until coming down starts to feel more real than being clean.","sonnet":"Someone watching a person they care about slip into addiction observes the cycle — the person uses them for comfort, the drug provides fake ecstasy, and eventually they lie alone — the narrator grapples with how much responsibility they bear for someone who keeps choosing the substance over them.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is addiction written as a toxic love affair, where the drug and the person owe each other the same demands: to be trusted, to be blamed, to be the one waiting in the dark. The refrain of coming down and being fragile isn't remorse so much as a loop the narrator already knows he can't break — the plea to be told it's alright arrives pre-defeated, more ritual than hope, and the recurring insistence that it's not my fault is the tell, an exculpation repeated until it stops meaning anything. Where the song develops is the bridge: the pull toward erasing the self collapses into a seductive invitation to run away to a private ecstasy, and that offer curdles instantly into an image of kissing bones — the promised escape and the corpse it produces named in the same breath. As the single that announced Themata, it stakes out early the band's habit of letting a hook carry something genuinely unwell underneath, the sweetness of the chorus doing the work of the addiction it describes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"exculpation rehearsed until it evacuates its own meaning","built":"the hook's sweetness enacting the addiction it describes"},
"karnivool~mauseum": {"haiku":"The speaker emerged unfeeling from abandonment (Moses basket) and confronts the void; they ask whether falling apart or becoming oneself will end the emptiness.","opus":"Wrapped in imagery of an imperiled newborn set adrift, this is a harrowing meditation on paralysis and guilt over failing to act. The narrator, tormented that he neither felt nor did what the moment demanded, asks whether anyone will be present after his own downfall, hinting at the loss of a child.","sonnet":"A figure feels emotionally dead and disconnected — placed in a kind of protective basket like Moses but unable to feel or act on the feelings they know they should have, asking whether anyone will be there after the inevitable fall.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about a man who kept waiting for his own grief to arrive and met only a blank where the feeling should have been. He recasts a rescued-infant image as self-indictment, casting himself as the one set adrift and never delivered, each apology arriving as a verdict already passed. The music's churning climb stages the dread beneath that flatness, machinery still grinding while he admits there was nothing inside left to answer the question of whether anyone stays after he goes down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"waiting for grief that never arrives to fill the blank it left","built":"churning music still grinding while the inside admits it has nothing to answer"},
"karnivool~nachash":{"haiku":"Being drawn into an overwhelming force that swells and consumes awareness, a pivotal moment where one becomes part of something larger.","opus":"An account of a mind-altering, awe-filled experience that feels bigger than the self, both terrifying and beautiful as it takes over. It shifts into private struggle with fading colors, self-doubt, and the fear of having wasted a last chance.","sonnet":"A song about a moment of overwhelming transformation — something vast and beautiful and feared is taking over, and the speaker, who normally keeps his thoughts distant and out of reach, finds himself finally present in a pivotal, irreversible instant.","src":"haiku","fable":"Named for the Hebrew serpent, which is both the Eden tempter and a verb-root meaning to divine or enchant, the song stages transformation as a force that curls inward and consumes the self before the mind can name it. Karnivool's governing image is the coil that doubles as a threshold: an opening that materializes exactly where attention lands, a rotation the self cannot steer, an arrival dressed as invitation. Its real nature stays deliberately withheld, and the chorus keeps promising beauty and reality while growing larger than the singer can contain. Wonder and being overwhelmed become one gesture, the very seizure the bridges later name with open dread. Beneath the imagery of solar rebirth and a performance about to begin lies the quietest admission, a self bleaching toward grey, coveting a life it lacks the currency to claim. The serpent's gift, then, is that the decisive moment feels genuine precisely because it was never chosen: on Asymmetry, illumination and possession share a single spine, and the mask of awe never fully hides the fear beneath it.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming","faith & the occult"],"means":"illumination arriving as possession before it can be refused","built":"hebrew title naming both the serpent and the act of enchanting","fvr":"2.3"},
"karnivool~new-day": {"haiku":"Someone seeks escape from a dying world and false saviors, warning others that waiting for rescue is futile.","opus":"Paranoid dread dressed as renewal: someone shedding a false destiny while sensing pursuit and rot everywhere, exhausted by waiting for a savior who never comes. The Australian prog band lets that anxiety hang unresolved rather than promising deliverance.","sonnet":"About trying to escape a spiritual or psychological pursuit — something or someone has found the narrator, and despite the promise of a new day there's an undertow of dread, a sense that salvation may never come and the storm is already inside.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about shedding a story someone else wrote for you: the narrator abandons the belief that his path was already fixed, and dawn arrives less as relief than as exposure, since the people who tracked him to his door are still circling outside. Karnivool build the paranoia into the machinery, the verses coaxing a listener forward a single careful footfall at a time before the bridges tip into a colder question about tongues that lie and a sun going out overhead. The closing movement drops the coaxing and just hammers the fatigue of standing guard, a man worn thin by his own vigil who would sooner be healed or erased than keep watching for a rescuer who never arrives.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","surveillance & control","identity & becoming"],"means":"shedding a fixed story only to find the watchers still circling outside","built":"verses coaxing forward one footfall at a time before bridges tip colder"},
"karnivool~om": {"haiku":"Philosophical spoken-word passage describes mystical dissolution of separation, unity beneath surface, and death as blissful beginning.","opus":"Built around a spoken recollection of a psychedelic experience, it reframes death as the dissolving of the illusion that we're separate islands, revealing one continent beneath. The read is transcendent: ego-loss as delight, a sober certainty of bliss.","sonnet":"Not a song with conventional lyrics but a spoken-word piece featuring two people discussing a profound altered-state experience — the description is of total dissolution of the boundaries between self and other, a kind of death preview that one speaker finds joyful and freeing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dying voice reports back that the terror was misplaced: what feels like separate selves are only islands whose bedrock joins below the waterline, so death arrives as reunion rather than an ending. Karnivool cede the mic entirely to that found recording, letting their restless, tidal music resolve into a stranger's plain certainty that the walls dividing one person from another were never solid to begin with.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The closing voice is a found recording, a man describing his LSD experience: death as reunion, selves as islands joined beneath the water, the sober certainty of waking bliss. It is Gerald Heard, quoting Milton's Comus; the band never credited it, the attribution belongs to the listening community, and it is well corroborated.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","death & grief"],"means":"death reframed as reunion once the walls between selves dissolve","built":"Karnivool cede the mic to a found recording, letting the music resolve into a stranger's certainty"},
"karnivool~roquefort":{"haiku":"One person tries to chase and control another, hoping to be defined as supreme through love. Both fear loss yet fear connection; the song depicts obsession masked as devotion and the waste of energy in pursuit of validation.","opus":"A strained tug-of-war between two people, control unraveling as one plunges after temptation into the unknown, caught between destruction and payoff. Karnivool wrap their prog intricacy around a bond where one partner shapes the other through legend and craving, wanting them while cautioning that hiding the truth only postpones the settling of accounts.","sonnet":"Obsessive desire and self-destruction mirror each other as someone loses their shape by orbiting another person too closely. The rabbit-hole metaphor frames a pattern of surrender that is called love but functions more like annihilation, with the speaker aware of the damage and unable to stop craving more.","src":"haiku"},
"karnivool~set-fire-to-the-hive": {"haiku":"The speaker condemns structures of control through politics, religion, and media that sedate and manipulate populations. The song calls for burning down these systems and rejecting the false choices presented as salvation.","opus":"An Australian prog-rock revolt against manufactured belief, framing politics, religion and celebrity as a single sedating con sold to a compliant public. The rage is at willing obedience to systems built to fall and the elites who profit while fleeing the wreckage.","sonnet":"An anti-authoritarian attack on political and religious leaders who manipulate and exploit — the speaker accuses them of whispering into children's minds, selling illusions, and using the hive-mind of the public as a weapon. The call to \"set fire to the hive\" is a demand to burn down the system before it consumes everyone inside it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A curse aimed at the charismatic strongman, spat by the swarm that fed him: Karnivool trace how a crowd surrenders its own judgment to a dolled-up face, then adores the theft. The verses keep catching the con mid-pitch, seeing the wrecking ball swing, yet the drugged hive obeys the next unexplained command anyway, so the true subject is the reflex of obedience. That reflex is why the outro hammers one verdict past exhaustion, denying any chance to redo the choice and leaving the listener to torch the hollow shelter they mistook for sanctuary.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"the crowd cursing the strongman it already obeyed past all argument","built":"the outro hammering one verdict until no choice remains to redo"},
"karnivool~sewn-and-silent": {"haiku":"A person trapped in fading love is emotionally blinded and pleads for separation while contemplating whether the bond was ever real.","opus":"A disorienting reckoning with intimacy and loss, wanting to be hurt into feeling while urging a lover to slow down and heal. The paradox at its heart is being surrounded by this closeness yet so damn lonely, ready to burn it all down.","sonnet":"A relationship dissolving into painful detachment: the narrator's eyes are sewn blind and bleeding, he dreams of the person while knowing he shouldn't want to be hurt by shallow eyes, and ultimately releases them to go — even burning it down — rather than hold on to a love that has gone astray.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about wanting closeness and flinching from it in the same motion, reaching for someone while blinding yourself on purpose so the contact can never actually land. The refrain begging the other to decelerate and turn a face aside comes back like a nervous tic, and every repeat admits the singer would rather nurse the injury than survive being genuinely met. Read in the alt-metal idiom of Karnivool's debut, that tenderness is a stall: by the last verse the caretaking curdles into a scorched-earth farewell, and the one line naming happiness turns out to be the loneliest thing here. Isolation gets chosen over the risk of being seen.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"closeness sought and flinched from in the same reaching motion","built":"the single line naming happiness turning out to be the loneliest thing"},
"karnivool~shutterspeed": {"haiku":"Photography and frozen moments, capturing fleeting reality at high speed.","opus":"A tense prog-rock coming-apart of identity and dependent perception, the narrator boiling as they scour a partner's gaze for truth and worth. Ripped apart and shrinking into a numbed cocoon, they claim to perceive and feel beyond the other. It reaches for the dizziness of needing someone's eyes to feel real while resenting the exposure intimacy forces.","sonnet":"A narrator who perceives more and feels more than those around them — busting back into pieces, pulled to a coma shell, spun out and scraped from the ceiling by external pressure and fashion's demands. The core need is to be seen as they are rather than what performs: \"speak to me now with a guided tongue, see through me now.\"","src":"haiku","fable":"A plea for recognition curdles into contempt: the singer demands to be seen and needed, then retreats into a numbed inner chamber where no expression survives worth wearing. That superiority claim keeps circling back, a self insisting it perceives and feels beyond everyone crowding around it, even while the crowd howls for something plainer than the performance on offer. The turn that lifts this above sulking is the closing surrender, where the voice that hoarded every feeling finally admits it draws its strength from the very other it kept shoving away.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"superiority claimed while drawing strength from the one pushed away","built":"the closing surrender undoing the hoard the whole song built"},
"karnivool~simple-boy": {"haiku":"A protected, sheltered person faces the choice to grow or stay safe; they struggle between numbness and feeling, between staying or leaving those who love them.","opus":"The false comfort of being kept protected and small, told you're safe here while the real cost is never facing yourself or the emptiness. Karnivool weigh the pull to stay sheltered against the freedom to leave and finally feel.","sonnet":"Someone on the verge of complete emotional collapse, watching from above while their interior world sinks, telling the person they love they are free to leave rather than watching the speaker drown them both — the refrain 'you're safe here' seems directed at themselves as much as anyone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song caught between two voices: one that soothes a child into staying put, safe and unknowing, and one underneath it that is exhausted with wearing that same untroubled face for even one more day. The lullaby refrain — stay here, don't wander, you're safe — is the trap it pretends to be a comfort, and Karnivool score the tension so the tenderness and the suffocation are the same gesture. When the release finally comes, being told you're free to go isn't liberation so much as permission to stop pretending, a door offered only once the simple boy has been coaxed into never opening it. On Sound Awake, a record obsessed with waking from anesthetized comfort, this is the anesthesia itself given a soft, coaxing voice, and the song watches that voice fail to convince the one being sung to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape","artifice & performance"],"means":"the lullaby refrain performing the trap it presents as comfort","built":"the permission to leave offered only once the door has been coaxed shut"},
"karnivool~sky-machine": {"haiku":"The song explores destiny, determinism, and the question of whether fate is fixed or open, with the speaker seeking answers from a mysterious figure who may hold knowledge of humanity's future. It centers on existential uncertainty and transcendence.","opus":"A yearning to reach past the heavens and comprehend what lies there, insisting no grand design governs anything and choosing to venture beyond limits with curiosity intact. Confronting the ugliness people conceal, the song leaves open whether the answer is flight or staying to face it.","sonnet":"An address to an unknown, possibly extraterrestrial or transcendent presence — the narrator gazes at the sky waiting for something beyond human plans, asking it to show the way, and inviting it aboard a sky machine to reveal where it comes from and what humanity is ultimately seeking.","src":"haiku","fable":"A prog band known for rhythmic mazes stages first contact as confession rather than rescue, handing its faith to an arriving intelligence because the human story has no author steering it. The worship curdles into a verdict on ourselves: the same bloodstream carrying courage also carries the cruelties we bury, so the savior we crown is really our own glory and horror. Its plea wears down instead of arguing, until the promise that the buried dead pass in perfect silence lands as the coldest possible floor.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"faith handed to an arriving intelligence because the human story has no author","built":"the worship curdling into a verdict on the same bloodstream it crowns"},
"karnivool~synops": {"haiku":"An inner war rages without resolution, the speaker unsure if they're alive or dead, trapped between ego and surrender, searching for light but finding no escape from conflict.","opus":"Australian prog wrestling with self-deception and a wounded ego, the narrator unsure whether he's even honest with himself while an inner war rages, half-pleading for light he refuses to let anyone leave on.","sonnet":"About internal conflict between ego and honesty — the narrator isn't sure if they're dead or alive spiritually, wrestling with something they can't hide, while an inner war rages that they fear will never end, crying out for light.","src":"haiku","fable":"Karnivool's debut-era mission statement asks a question no one can answer from inside their own head — whether the self performing sincerity can even tell when it's lying — and stages the ego as a saboteur that has to fall before anything true gets through. The chants that fracture the word egotistical, dropping it lower each time, enact that collapse rather than describe it, while the same smiling chorus recurs with its soul removed the second time, exposing the earlier warmth as a rehearsed pose. Ian Kenny's habitual light imagery here inverts: instead of asking to be guided home, the bridge demands the light be left off so the war inside can rage in private, and only in the buried outro does the mask finally crack into a plain cry for help.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","shame & repression"],"means":"the ego named as saboteur that must fall before truth arrives","built":"the chorus returning with its soul removed to expose earlier warmth as pose"},
"karnivool~the-caudal-lure": {"haiku":"A person resists becoming enslaved by external forces and chemicals, refusing to give up control of their consciousness despite pressure to surrender their freedom and soul.","opus":"Named for the bait a predator dangles to trap prey, this rejects the idea of being rescued by any outside redeemer and insists on facing the end on one's own terms. Underneath runs disillusionment with faith and false promises, a suspicion that fate is engineered by fear and chemistry, capped with a jeer at an absent deity.","sonnet":"About the moment of choosing to stop deferring to saviors or systems — the narrator refuses to go back into his cell, demands his own freedom, while the song warns that false hope (the wine we swallow) leads only to chemical loss of control.","src":"haiku","fable":"A refusal to be rescued, sung by men who suspect that rescue is just another leash. Karnivool build the track on a lockstep pulse that keeps tightening rather than releasing, so the vow to walk out unaccompanied curdles into the sedation that delivers it: whatever calm finally arrives has been administered, not chosen, a hand at the vein instead of at the unbolted door. That inward collapse is why the outro's jeer at borrowed faith stings — it keeps circling back because the hollow behind it never fills, and the escape the song demands may only ever prove itself by proving how alone it leaves you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"autonomy demanded while the calm that arrives has been administered","built":"lockstep pulse tightening rather than releasing so escape enacts its own sedation"},
"karnivool~the-last-few": {"haiku":"Sacrifice and commitment to transformation; a small remaining group endures the cost to save others, exposing falsehoods and stepping into new life as a unified force.","opus":"A survivors' anthem for the ones left carrying every cost, sacrifice framed as bitter medicine, promising that exposing the fakes and standing together buys a hard-won new life for whoever endures to lead.","sonnet":"A song about a surviving remnant who bore the cost and took the fall so others could live — they exposed false allies, switched the lights on, and now ask who among the last few will step forward to lead toward new life.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a survivors' hymn built as a bargain: whoever crawls clear of the wreckage inherits its whole debt, and the song insists that price will eventually square itself. Karnivool sing it in the plural throughout, a huddle that took the blows and swallowed the bitter cure so someone else's skin stayed whole, then threw the switch that floods the room and shows who was faking. The chorus keeps demanding a fresh start over and over until the phrase stops sounding like hope and starts sounding like a group talking itself into believing the ordeal bought them a beginning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["friendship & loyalty","death & grief","artifice & performance"],"means":"shared debt borne so another person's skin stayed whole","built":"the chorus repeats until hope stops sounding like hope"},
"karnivool~the-medicine-wears-off": {"haiku":"When emotional buffers fade, the speaker faces the raw reality of attachment—the wonder that held them together now revealing something burning and breaking.","opus":"A song about the crash after the high, about what's really left once the numbing agent fades. A fast-moving lover who dazzles like a distant sun and wounds those who reach for her stands in for any intoxicant, person or drug, whose magic dulls with time, leaving the narrator aching for the very clarity of mind he once deliberately shut out.","sonnet":"A song about the moment when pharmaceutical or chemical numbing fails and reality floods back in — as the medicine wears off, the narrator watches someone come back to themselves and remembers exactly why they both started blocking everything out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the crash after the high, addiction read as a doomed romance where the drug and the lover are the same wonder that lights you up and then breaks you. Karnivool build it as a slow tightening, and by the end the singer just braces to watch himself relapse again, the chorus circling back to the same dread each time the numbness fades.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"bracing to watch oneself relapse as the chorus circles its same dread","built":"the slow tightening build enacting the narrowing before the crash"},
"karnivool~the-refusal": {"haiku":"Two broken people navigate shared wreckage and regret, questioning blame and connection as they metaphorically crash and drown together.","opus":"A song about a shared wreckage between two people, in which the narrator denies being the one who let the other down as they both drown, yet admits guilt and regret and insists he could have helped.","sonnet":"Two people navigate mutual wreckage and blame: the speaker refuses to accept responsibility for the other person's collapse while also admitting shared regret, and tentatively suggests that if love comes first the lies between them might dissolve.","src":"haiku","fable":"A denial that slowly curdles into confession: the narrator swears nobody can pin the drowning on him, that his hand never loosed the rope, even while he grants that both people are the same broken thing. Karnivool stage it as a capsize, the pre-chorus repeating until shared fault hardens from a snapped step underfoot into flat regret, so the refusal in the title becomes a man clutching his innocence as the water climbs past any argument he can make. The final turn flips the denial's own verdict from not-possible to flatly impossible, the last no giving way to the thing it was built to keep out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","death & grief"],"means":"innocence clutched tighter as the water climbs past argument","built":"the title word flips from denial to admission across a single final turn"},
"karnivool~themata": {"haiku":"The song grapples with hypocrisy, materialistic greed, and the duality between seeing the world as alive versus a lie, with repeated emphasis on learning truth through experience and personal contradiction. It questions perception and deception in society.","opus":"A searching, introspective reckoning with one's own greed and hypocrisy, oscillating between seeing the world as alive and seeing it as a lie, while surrendering to sensory experience and wondering what there is to see.","sonnet":"A restless inner search for meaning and truth — the narrator navigates their own hypocrisy and greed, questioning what they see in a world that feels simultaneously alive and false, and struggling to know whether they are truly awake to it or still lost in the turning.","src":"haiku","fable":"It stages the vertigo of seeing clearly for the first time and finding the same view thrilling and false at once, so that revelation and disillusion arrive as one indivisible flinch. The singer keeps circling his own hypocrisy and greed while dreading his turn in a line where each person is next to look and next to bleed, as if perception passed down like an inherited wound. Beneath the shifting-time attack Karnivool built their debut on, the pull toward a drugged hush becomes the only refuge from a truth that refuses to settle into anything he can honestly call his own.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"revelation and disillusion arriving as one indivisible flinch","built":"shifting-time attack scoring a truth that refuses to settle"},
"karnivool~umbra": {"haiku":"Someone feels isolated and unstable, trapped in emotional patterns they can't escape, struggling with mental clarity and abandoning hope of recovery or being understood.","opus":"An introspective plea to be genuinely perceived and understood while the narrator lurks in shadow, swinging between craving closeness and rejecting it. Underneath runs self-destructive numbness and a sense of futility, a wish to be knocked senseless and rest until clear-headed, haunted by the feeling that every apparent breakthrough only resets him to square one.","sonnet":"A man in the shadow of himself — drunk, disoriented, unable to feel safe, cycling back to square one every time he thinks he understands his life, ultimately asking to be left alone with his broken promises.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study in the shadow-self the title names — the umbra being the darkest core of a cast shadow — where a man half-invites and half-dreads being seen, asking to be painted in someone else's colors precisely so he won't have to own what he hides. The self-medicating chorus, wanting to be knocked unconscious with a bottle and to wake only once he's sober again, is the tell: numbness dressed as rest, avoidance mistaken for recovery. Karnivool's polyrhythmic churn on Sound Awake mirrors the central complaint that just as he thinks he's solved himself the pieces shift and he's back to the start, so the song enacts its own loop rather than escaping it. The bridge's split voice — one side saying I don't want to and I don't need it, the answering side confessing here I will fall and my will is gone — maps the whole song's mechanism: every refusal is shadowed by the craving it denies. It closes not on resolution but on a wounded demand to be left alone, the addict's last defense when someone gets close enough to name the thing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"numbness dressed as rest while the shadow-self asks to be painted over","built":"the split bridge voice shadowing every refusal with the craving it denies"},
"karnivool~we-are": {"haiku":"Society has become diseased and stagnant, with people worn down and conforming mindlessly. The song calls for complete reset and renewal, rejecting current structures to face our true shape.","opus":"A collective reckoning with a broken, mistrustful society, fearful of what they have become yet longing to shed their catatonic conformity and reset everything for future generations.","sonnet":"The song diagnoses a collective failure — society is diseased, trust is gone, people shuffle numbly in line, and the wisdom offered by those in charge comes without any real vision; the narrator wants a reset, urging people to face the shape they are in before it is too late for the next generation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A collective self-indictment sung in the plural, where pride keeps a whole species from admitting the rot until the shared well empties and every mouth spills only noise. What lifts it past protest is the fear aimed inward: the dread is not an outside enemy but the obedient herd shuffling into rank, torn between that revulsion and a demand to tear off a numbed outer layer and start the design over. The engine is Karnivool's fixation on rupture, and the ending earns it by swapping a single word so a day of despair becomes a day of first witnessing, leaving it open whether watching the end is grief or the moment sight finally arrives.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","identity & becoming","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"collective self-indictment where pride blocks shared confession","built":"a single swapped word turns despair into first witnessing"},
"kasabian~fire":{"haiku":"Desire and passion burn uncontrollably as the narrator becomes combustible force consuming everything in pursuit of transcendent ecstasy.","opus":"An ecstatic surrender to consuming desire, the singer offering himself up as easy lover and willing prisoner, burning and diving back into the tunnel of his own soul.","sonnet":"A speaker consumed by reckless, ecstatic energy — burning an effigy, spilling guts on a wheel, wired to machines by a murderous lover — keeps returning to the tunnel where their soul burns, framing the whole thing as an unstoppable forward motion that demands the listener move on and shake.","src":"haiku"},
"kaskade-deadmau5~move-for-me":{"haiku":"On a dancefloor, strangers move in synchronized reciprocity—a transactional, universal exchange where identity dissolves and sameness prevails, captured in the repetition of night after night.","opus":"Kaskade and deadmau5 distill the anonymous communion of a dancefloor, where names and origins dissolve and everyone becomes the same under the DJ and lights. The producers loop a simple reciprocal plea, move for me and I'll move for you, into the euphoric leveling of a night out awaited all week.","sonnet":"The anonymous communion of a dancefloor — names and origins dissolve into shared motion. Two strangers are equalized by the music, the lights, the rhythm moving through them both. Identity becomes irrelevant; what exists is only the mutual exchange of movement, repeated across every night and every floor.","src":"haiku"},
"kaskade-x-deadmau5-feat-skylar-grey~beneath-with-me":{"haiku":"Descent as salvation: hand in hand through drowning, offering light where there's only darkness, seeking hope through shared sinking.","opus":"Clinging to hope amid rising floodwaters and long nights, taking a hand and focusing on the distant light. Even enemies and strangers draw together, survival resting on comfort and shared faith.","sonnet":"Two people face a catastrophic, possibly world-ending crisis together — fire, rising water, long fearful nights — finding comfort solely in each other's presence as former enemies converge and everything else goes silent.","src":"haiku"},
"kaskade~move-for-me":{"haiku":"Physical movement becomes expression of desire and command as the speaker calls toward responsive partner. Motion synchronizes between bodies.","opus":"A celebration of the anonymous communion of the dancefloor, another night where names and origins stop mattering because everyone is levelled the same. DJ, lights and speakers dissolve identity into a single reciprocal pulse, one body's motion traded for another's.","sonnet":"The dancefloor as equaliser: names and origins don't matter when the DJ's light show is running and bodies move in reciprocal exchange — \"move for me, I'll move for you\" — identity dissolves into the shared rhythm of another night out.","src":"haiku"},
"kataklysm~narcissist":{"haiku":"Confrontation with unbridled ego and self-deception, exposing falseness and positioning the speaker as the vessel of truth and justice.","opus":"A furious takedown of a self-mythologizing manipulator, stripping away the godlike facade to expose a fraud drowning in his own reflection. Death metal as reckoning against someone who worships only himself.","sonnet":"A direct confrontation with a self-deified manipulator: the speaker sees through the bulletproof facade to the cowardice and distortion underneath, indicting the narcissist as a fraud who worships deceit and will rot in the pit of their own making.","src":"haiku"},
"katatonia~dead-letters":{"haiku":"Dreams grow increasingly dark; the narrator is trapped in an inactive existence where words fail and time becomes stagnant.","opus":"Katatonia sink into agitation and inner emptiness, dreams turning ever bleaker as the chest cracks open on a heart too drained to feel, leaving only unsendable words behind. The Swedish band, who moved from doom into melancholic metal, render depression as motionless blood and a bird marking the passing hours, a single flash of clarity glimpsed before the dark seals over again.","sonnet":"Darkness deepens in layers — dreams turning blacker, lights dimming one by one, a life whose blood runs still. Words become dead letters: messages sent to no one, arriving nowhere. A bird calls and the hours pass. Whatever clarity existed once, it came only once, and the only thing still moving is the slow inward collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"katatonia~ghost-of-the-sun":{"haiku":"Betrayal and lies corrode trust while memory haunts the present; the speaker has glimpsed something irreplaceable slipping away.","opus":"Bitter fallout from betrayal, a man who sold his soul to the marketplace and now sees through everyone's lies. Cold resignation over a broken vow, the sun itself reduced to a ghost of former warmth.","sonnet":"A cold, fractured self-examination of alienation and betrayal — the speaker has lost his sense of identity through a cycle of failed performances and broken trust, left watching other people's lives pass him by.","src":"haiku"},
"katatonia~journey-through-pressure":{"haiku":"Will and consciousness fragment under mounting pressure, with the self becoming alien and unbearable as separation from one's own mind accelerates.","opus":"Depression and self-harm rendered as a numb, dissociative journey where thoughts stop feeling like your own. Katatonia's bleak interior weather, the effort of acting unharmed while something quietly comes apart.","sonnet":"A slow psychological unraveling in which the effort to appear unharmed consumes the speaker until thoughts dissolve and the boundary between water and blood, self and not-self, collapses entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"katatonia~lethean":{"haiku":"A speaker grieves a partner's suicide attempt or death by pills, haunted by broken plans and the slow march through darkness that never arrived.","opus":"Katatonia speak to someone who didn't survive, leaving the singer behind with their pills and plans that will now go unrealized, autumn and winter tinting everything. The Swedish band, named for a state of stupor, trace a slow reckoning with a suicide's absence, measuring a single grieving heart against the approaching darkness along a deserted highway.","sonnet":"Grief over someone who could not survive — a person left behind pills and plans unmet, disappearing into the high grass while the narrator watched from a distance. Forgetting is framed as a river the speaker now intends to enter. The title invokes the mythological water of oblivion; this time, he will run toward it rather than stand watching.","src":"haiku"},
"katatonia~sleeper":{"haiku":"Fever and mysterious intrusion disrupt rest while birds and shadows encroach on consciousness, leaving the sleeper vulnerable to unnamed threats.","opus":"A vigil at a sickbed, someone breathing only because the other does, pleading with them not to give up as death hovers in the room. Fragile, feverish tenderness against the fear of being left alone.","sonnet":"A vigil beside a feverish, sleeping person shades into dread as an unseen presence seems to enter the room — the speaker watches over the sleeper with desperate urgency, terrified of what morning might not bring.","src":"haiku"},
"kate-nash~foundations-full-version":{"haiku":"Toxic codependency masked as love; both partners inflict wounds while unable to disengage, watching the foundation crack beneath them.","opus":"A relationship rotting in real time, both partners trading petty cruelties while clinging to something already broken. Nash's genius is the honesty about staying, the small vindictive pleasures that keep a dead thing alive.","sonnet":"A relationship well past its expiration date is rendered in painfully specific domestic detail: petty cruelties, public humiliations, drunken nights, and the bitter admission that she knows she should let go but keeps clinging to the cracked foundation anyway, smiling when she shouldn't and unable to forget.","src":"haiku"},
"katy-perry~dark-horse-sung-in-the-style-of-system-of-a-down":{"haiku":"A seductive figure warns of their dangerous power and irreversibility—love becomes a storm, possession becomes addiction.","opus":"The original is a seduction-as-warning, a lover promising devotion and destruction in equal measure, don't cross me. Rendered here as parody in a nu-metal band's manic style, the menace played for absurdist contrast rather than real threat.","sonnet":"A warning to a potential lover: pursuing her is a point of no return — she frames herself as a dangerous, all-consuming force who will either elevate you completely or destroy you, and the Juicy J verse sexualizes the same possessiveness with explicit drug and dominance imagery.","src":"haiku"},
"katy-perry~the-one-that-got-away":{"haiku":"Youthful romance becomes a cherished memory weaponized by regret; the speaker measures all future love against a perfect past that money and time can never recover.","opus":"Adult regret over a first love lost to time, the vivid teenage romance now unreachable in any but an imagined other life. No amount of success can buy back the person you failed to hold onto.","sonnet":"A speaker revisits a youthful love that slipped away through inattention and time, mourning that no amount of money or success can reclaim a connection she realises, too late, was irreplaceable.","src":"haiku"},
"kat~bastard":{"haiku":"A rebellious figure rejects religious and patriotic duty, embracing darkness and invoking occult power over conventional salvation.","opus":"Polish blackened-thrash blasphemy from the late-80s underground, casting the singer as an antichrist figure birthing new evil from the womb, spurning both church and army. Deliberate provocation aimed at a Catholic-nationalist culture, more theatrical heresy than belief.","sonnet":"a speaker who rejects both religion and political ideology, finding identity only in darkness and the birth of a child as new blood","src":"haiku"},
"kat~g-os-z-ciemno-ci":{"haiku":"A primordial voice recounts its transformation from beast to human, passing cycles of suffering through generations. It questions whether divinity or evil fed on humanity, and critiques how the Church and institutions have failed to stop the cycle.","opus":"The Polish thrash pioneers channel a serpentine voice from darkness, a primordial beast older than suns instructing a daughter to take the earth's pearls and Sheol's power. Wolf from wolf, blood from blood, the litany asks who truly suckled, God or the serpent, insisting someone always lies. Summoning an army to descend into hell, it steeps blasphemous, occult imagery in the band's notoriously dark Slavic metal tradition.","sonnet":"An ancient voice — demon, ancestor, or elder force — addresses a daughter with the inheritance of primal law: wolf begets wolf, poverty breeds poverty, blood calls to blood. The central accusation is theological: someone has been feeding off creation, and the question of whether it is god or serpent remains unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"kat~legenda-wy-niona":{"haiku":"A dreaming figure in a magical house resists dark entities at night, summoning earthly spirits while fire dies and bells toll.","opus":"A gothic occult vision: at night in an enchanted, haunted house the narrator is warned not to open the door to the darkness, then performs a pentagram ritual as winged serpents and the ghost of an Earth Goddess appear; beneath the horror imagery lies a longing to touch her light and body, desire fused with dread of the supernatural.","sonnet":"A ritualistic occult vision — the narrator performs a summoning ceremony in a darkening house, draws a pentagram as shadowy winged creatures arrive, and encounters the spirit of an Earth goddess whom he yearns to touch.","src":"haiku"},
"kat~lza-dla-cieniow-minionych":{"haiku":"A ship sails while the speaker drowns in memory; separation and darkness define continuation.","opus":"An elegy sung beside a lover's tomb, the speaker scattering beads of memory over a body now cold where fire once burned. His cracked heart still wants to beat as his ship sails on, the beloved absent as night, himself absent as day.","sonnet":"A grief elegy for someone who has died — the narrator lies beside the cold body in the darkness of a sarcophagus, scatters the beads of memories, and laments that the ship of life must sail on even with a broken heart, marked by the absence of the one they loved.","src":"haiku"},
"kat~odi-profanum-vulgus":{"haiku":"The song mocks institutional religion and power through irreverent thanks for suffering, disease, and death as proof of absurdity.","opus":"A blasphemous, sarcastic tirade thanking God for disease, war, greedy clergy, cancer, leprosy and death. Adopting the persona of a laughing devil who spits in God's face rather than grovel, it savagely mocks the fanatic who praises his god and dies content.","sonnet":"Bitter, blasphemous sarcasm directed at God: the narrator sarcastically thanks the divine for holy wars, corrupt clergy, disease, suffering, and death — punctuated by a fanatic who praises God and then dies for it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title borrows Horace's line about hating and shunning the common crowd, but Kostrzewski flips it into a black liturgy: the whole song is a thanksgiving hymn, thanks be to you, Lord, offered for HIV, cancer, scabies, leprosy, death itself — the standard mask of Polish blasphemy metal made under a Church that owned the national language of piety. What makes it more than shock is who is speaking. The narrator names himself the ridiculous little Devil who laughs rather than begs, and the bridge quietly turns the knife on the true believer instead: the fanatic praises, and then dies divinely, godly, dead. The joke is that the pious man gets exactly the reward the hymn keeps thanking God for, which retargets the sarcasm away from the crowd of the title and onto the devout — the vulgus that deserves shunning is the faithful, and the alleluia between the curses is the sound of that trap closing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"a thanksgiving hymn that redirects divine reward onto the faithful as punishment","built":"the alleluia between the curses is the trap closing on the devout, not the crowd"},
"kat~odmiency":{"haiku":"Crude sexual defiance and transgression, inverting shame into power through transgressive imagery.","opus":"This Polish black metal band adopts a Satanic, sexually charged persona to mock moralizers, framing their obsession with sin as their own repressed impotence. The provocation is really an attack on hypocrisy dressed as piety.","sonnet":"A Polish-language song (by the metal band KAT) about sexual and social transgression — the narrator, always the \"black sheep,\" claims power through carnal dominance, mocks those who preach about evil while hiding their own sexlessness, and addresses a woman who is herself an outcast and an altar to his desire; it's deliberately transgressive and ritualistic.","src":"haiku"},
"kat~ojcze-samotni":{"haiku":"A lonely father-figure is condemned to ruin while others sleep awaiting return; land and heritage are cursed with filth and plague. The song invokes destruction of false idols and sacred spaces.","opus":"Polish thrash-metal invoking a black sun and buried horrors, a curse called down on an oppressive structure in the name of the land, rivers and dead. Roman Kostrzewski channels national trauma into apocalyptic ritual demanding it all crumble.","sonnet":"A Polish metal invocation addressing a solitary, corrupted 'father' figure — a black sun presiding over a plague-ridden land — demanding that the oppressive structure crumble in the name of mountains, rivers, fields, and all those who suffered under it.","src":"haiku"},
"kat~piwniczne-widziadla":{"haiku":"Spectral dread and ancestral haunting permeate the cellar of the psyche, where trauma manifests as invisible terrors and violent winds.","opus":"A Polish horror-vision of a haunted, cellar-dwelling realm where titans and gods are gone, only a ghost treads the stairs and a raven coachman heralds snow. The final image of a child of the moth sealed in a cocoon of walls turns the whole scene into imprisonment inside decay.","sonnet":"A Polish gothic poem about the aftermath of defeat — once-powerful armies and gods have vanished, now only a spirit treads the cursed stairs and wind beats a black helmet with corpse-white ash; the narrator is a child of darkness cocooned in walls, visited by winged hyenas drawn to clotted blood, waiting for the snow and hail the crow-coachman brings.","src":"haiku"},
"kat~purpurowe-gody":{"haiku":"A dark ritual of sexual and spiritual violation is depicted, mixing innocence with demonic imagery and religious desecration.","opus":"A blasphemous, nightmarish Polish scene of a black mass, where innocence is defiled by a monstrous demonic priest amid dark forest, wild dreams and a mocked god, ending in blood, violation and a drawn knife. It's grotesque shock-horror rendered as ritual. Kat are pioneers of Polish thrash and heavy metal, and this leans into that early scene's occult provocation and deliberate transgression, wielding profane imagery as pure confrontational darkness.","src":"haiku"},
"kat~stworzy-em-pi-kn-rzecz":{"haiku":"A god creates humanity to kill and consume, viewing the act as a beautiful creation while mocking both paradise and damnation.","opus":"A savage Polish monologue voiced by a contemptuous god who calls heaven and hell jokes, sneers at squalling children, and boasts of creating a beautiful thing, man, only so it must kill to eat and live. Creation reframed as cruel, mocking cosmic prank. Kat are the Polish thrash and heavy metal pioneers, and this weds their aggressive attack to genuinely blasphemous, philosophically bleak lyrics about a deity who despises what he made.","sonnet":"A sardonic monologue from a god surveying creation — heaven and hell dismissed as jokes, humanity as a creature that must kill to eat, the whole arrangement described as a beautiful thing with deadpan contempt. The speaker's eye moves from the cosmic to the obscene, shrinking the planet to something pinned to underwear, marvelling at how little his creations understand. The tone is a nihilistic comedy: proud of a world nobody asked for.","src":"haiku"},
"kat~w-bezksztaltnej-bryle":{"haiku":"Trapped in shapeless ice, a prisoner drinks tears and suffers beneath the weight of god and world. Cold becomes existence.","opus":"A tormented soul frozen and entombed in a shapeless block of ice for years, its being turned to frost and grief, trapped by the world's desires and mourning at the grave of a suffering god; a gothic Polish-metal vision of imprisoned, living death.","sonnet":"A poem-song about being imprisoned in the formless mass of one's own existence — the narrator, suspended over candle flames, drank their own tears while the world consumed them through love, pity, and their own powerlessness; their body, thoughts, and soul are all ice, and they have spent years trapped in this shapeless, freezing being.","src":"haiku","fable":"A soul frozen inside the title's shapeless block of ice, and the whole song is that one conceit refracted through every faculty at once: words like ice, thoughts like ice, eyes, bones, heart like ice, until being itself is one metaphysical winter where snow was my bread and the moon my sun. The refrain of having had so little keeps deflating the grandeur of the suffering, and the ending turns it inside out - a funeral knocking at the door, a life without life - so that Kostrzewski's escape runs downward into underground masses and shrouds of dark thought, fleeing the heavens rather than reaching for them. It is his occult, anticlerical mode as vertical inversion: the sky is the prison and salvation, if it exists, is buried.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"salvation sought downward into the earth rather than upward to the sky","built":"the vertical axis inverted: sky as prison, burial as refuge"},
"kat~w-sadzie-smiertelnego-piekna":{"opus":"A Polish gothic vision of a woman who was someone's sun, drained by lips and then blood, laid out in an orchard of deadly beauty with a single lamp and flower. The morbid close wonders whether the black carriage carries her to oblivion or just off for a beer, mocking death's solemnity.","sonnet":"A Polish gothic scene of a man's death or complete dissolution — he was once someone's sun, then the light goes out; a lamp, a single flower, silence; ruby liquid stills in a goblet, the trees bend like gallows, and he departs in a black carriage — whether to oblivion or mundanely for a beer is left deliberately ambiguous.","src":"sonnet"},
"kat~wierz":{"opus":"The Polish metal band's credo of fierce personal conviction, its narrator declaring belief only in what is solid, honest, and childlike while rejecting sentiment, threats, and sacred cows. Words carry absolute weight, no means no, and reverence toward false idols draws contempt. It is a defiant catechism of self-trust over conformity.","sonnet":"A Polish declaration of personal conviction and refusal: the narrator believes in words, children, things, and the magic of nature — but not in people — and hates those who threaten, who demand reverence for false idols, and who misread her desire as consent.","src":"sonnet"},
"kat~za-dla-cieni-w-minionych":{"haiku":"A ship sailing away carrying memories and cold love, now defined by night and absence where once there was warmth and fire.","opus":"Kat mourn a lover laid in a sarcophagus, once warm with fire now cold-lipped and covered in sleep, as the narrator scatters the beads of memory and offers a tear. The Polish thrash-metal pioneers sail a cracked heart onward like a storm-bent ship, circling absence: she is gone and never was, it is night, he is gone and never was, it is day.","sonnet":"A mourner sits with a cold body at the bottom of a sarcophagus, scattering beads of memory. She recalls warmth — a fragrant storm, a mast bent by wind — and offers only a tear in return. Life sails on without the dead person, the heart cracked but still beating; absence and presence trade places as night becomes day becomes nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"kat~zawieszony-sznur":{"haiku":"A man is haunted by a dark country and illness; a rope hangs as a symbol while a rebel is executed, viewing death with resignation.","opus":"Polish black-thrash pioneers voice a corrosive misanthropy and disgust with a rotten country and life itself, ending on a rebel dragged to execution who steps to the noose himself. The bleak power is in choosing death over a priest's empty talk of love and salvation.","sonnet":"A nihilistic Polish poem set to music about a person rotting in a dead room, spitting out misanthropy, recalling a childhood already marked for decay. It ends with a rebel being led to the gallows while a priest babbles about love and salvation — the prisoner ignores him and steps up to the noose himself.","src":"haiku","fable":"Kostrzewski builds the whole song around a single refusal: a body that has felt itself rotting since birth — spit, retch, animal death — rejects not just its country and its life but the entire apparatus of consolation offered to the dying. His last requests are all negations — no rite, no mourners, no meaning pressed onto his death — and the anticlerical charge sharpens them into deliberate blasphemy rather than mere despair. The rope hanging past the wall is the title's promise and the song's only mercy, an exit the prisoner reaches for precisely because it belongs to him and not to the priest. What Kat stages is autonomy through self-annihilation: better the drop one chooses than the salvation one is handed.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"autonomy through self-annihilation chosen over handed salvation","built":"every last request is a negation, refusing the consolation apparatus one item at a time"},
"kayzo~battle-drums":{"haiku":"Approaching catastrophe demands defiant stance as the narrator confronts apocalypse with refusal to surrender, inevitable conflict becoming call to arms.","opus":"Mustering nerve as bleak stretches drag on endlessly and agony sits in the belly like a sickness. Marking a limit while everything burns, the speaker hears the pounding drawing near as the signal to plant himself and refuse to yield.","sonnet":"On the darkest days when pain hangs like a plague and heartbeats turn to flatlines, the speaker hears battle drums approaching and demands a decision: clench your fist, draw a line in the sand, and refuse to give up even when the world is on fire.","src":"haiku"},
"kayzo~moonlight":{"haiku":"Reckless descent into darkness and blood-marked intimacy replace caution; the speaker claims authority and defies boundaries under moonlight.","opus":"A hard-dance anthem of poisonous attraction and reckless detachment, the narrator pulled toward someone who feels wonderful yet is plainly ruinous for them. Pressing lips to old wounds under an open sky, adrift in a loveless void where sensation deserts them, it insists the moment is finally theirs. It reaches for the heady peril of giving in to a love that hauls you up into the dark.","sonnet":"A contradictory pull toward someone dangerous — \"you make it better for me / but you're no good for me\" — plays out against imagery of blood tears, self-doubt, and drifting in space without feeling, the French countdown bookending a relationship that lifts the speaker sky-high before leaving them numb and faded.","src":"haiku"},
"kaz-ba-agane~szafa":{"opus":"A nostalgic Polish rap sparked by rummaging through an old wardrobe, where found relics (a communion souvenir, police detention records, an old crush's photo, tracksuits, cassettes) trigger bittersweet memories of a rough working-class youth and how time has marked him.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper rummages through his mother's wardrobe and finds a cascade of nostalgic artifacts — a hash-smelling box, a first-communion keepsake, a young chess-club membership card, police-encounter paperwork, old rap mixtapes, NBA cards — each item unlocking a memory of Warsaw street life, a school crush who got pregnant, and the narrow escape from a path toward regular employment. It's a tender, funny inventory of working-class Polish youth in the 2000s.","src":"sonnet"},
"kelis~milkshake":{"haiku":"Feminine mystique and sexual confidence command desire; the speaker controls desire through art and refuses to share her knowledge.","opus":"Sexual confidence as a proprietary secret, a woman fully in command of her allure and charging for the lesson. Cheeky and knowing, the swagger is the point, desirability she owns rather than performs.","sonnet":"A woman celebrates her irresistible sexual magnetism and the power it gives her over men, framing her allure as something so potent she could charge to teach it but won't — a confident, playful assertion of desirability and agency.","src":"haiku"},
"ken-ashcorp~hunter":{"haiku":"A person fights internal disease, seeking dominance over their demons rather than being hunted by them. The song explores the desire for agency and control against consuming darkness.","opus":"A nihilistic drive to torch it all and pick predator over quarry, the narrator declaring he'd sooner do the hunting than be hunted, do the killing than be killed, his thoughts in ruins and his demons feasting on him each time he drifts off. He wants to be reborn rather than surrender. Ken Ashcorp is an internet-bred musician known for glossy self-produced pop-rock, and here he pours that polish into a bleak anthem of scrabbling toward the light.","sonnet":"Trapped between numbness and the desperate wish to be the one with agency rather than the one being consumed — the speaker names the exhaustion of being prey, of demons owning sleep, of every wall hit being another reminder of powerlessness. The refrain is a choice, not a fact: to be the hunter not the hunted, to live one more day. The song is honest that survival is a decision that has to be made over and over, with no guarantee it sticks.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar-george-clinton-thundercat~wesley-s-theory":{"haiku":"Success and wealth trap Black aspirants into consumer servitude while systems systematically capture and control their economic destiny and futures.","opus":"The opening salvo of To Pimp a Butterfly, where Lamar dramatizes fame's predatory economics through the twin voices of a newly signed rapper fantasizing about reckless spending and Uncle Sam eager to bleed him dry. Named for Wesley Snipes' tax downfall, it reaches toward the trap set for Black success, wealth extended only to be reclaimed by the system that granted it.","sonnet":"A cautionary tale about the trap of sudden wealth: a young Kendrick fantasises about Cutlasses and platinum rings, only for \"Uncle Sam\" — Lucy, or lucre — to appear and court him with paperwork, promising everything while noting he failed economics, Wesley-Snipe his taxes before 35, and the whole arc of Black celebrity success is framed as a deal already written before you signed it.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~alright":{"haiku":"Trapped in cycles of poverty, violence, and police brutality, marginalized people find hope through faith—'we gon' be alright'—despite systemic harm and desperation.","opus":"Kendrick Lamar boils Black survival under systemic violence down to a gospel-tinged reassurance, answering hardship and threat with faith that divine care will see them through. The verses expose lethal streets, police who wish them dead, and temptation made flesh, yet the hook, born from his acclaimed jazz-rap opus, became a real protest anthem, hope wrestled from despair rather than easy comfort.","sonnet":"Black American survival held together by faith under systemic violence. Police murder, poverty, and personal ruin press from every side — yet a communal anthem of endurance rises through it all, rooted not in naivety but in the conviction that grace is the only counter-force left.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~blood":{"haiku":"The song frames a parable where a blind woman's question—that the speaker has lost his life—forces confrontation with mortality and existential stakes, juxtaposed with commentary on police brutality.","opus":"The stark prologue to Kendrick Lamar's landmark album, staging a parable before the music arrives. It asks whether corruption or frailty seals a person's fate, then tells of the narrator trying to help a struggling stranger only to be met with his own doom. A closing news clip about police violence widens the fable into a reckoning on race in America.","sonnet":"A parable opens with an act of mercy toward a blind woman on the street, which ends in a gunshot. The moral turns on a media fragment about police brutality, collapsing the distance between a violent lyric and the real body on the pavement: every good deed carries risk.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~complexion-a-zulu-love-feat-rapsody":{"haiku":"Skin tone dissolves into irrelevance as collective love transcends surface; historical atrocities dissolve beneath shared humanity's persistent beat.","opus":"A To Pimp a Butterfly track that argues skin tone carries no worth, whether dark or light, and that African heritage is something to embrace with pride. Lamar and Rapsody set field-slavery imagery against messages of Black self-worth, urging listeners to shed the self-hatred handed down by generations of racist conditioning.","sonnet":"A love song and a polemic fused together: skin tone is declared meaningless in the face of Willie Lynch's legacy, slavery's cotton fields, and the self-hatred that colorism breeds; Rapsody adds her verse affirming that light doesn't mean smart and dark doesn't mean stupid, calling all earth tones blessed and every shade of Black beautiful.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~dna":{"haiku":"The song examines inherited traits and nature—loyalty, aggression, ambition, and darkness—as inherent to the speaker's identity, passed down through bloodline and survival instinct.","opus":"A defiant boast that the narrator's loyalty, ambition, power, and darkness are all coded into his DNA, weaving his hardships, heritage, and survival into an assertion of identity while taunting a rival's weaker bloodline.","sonnet":"Kendrick asserts that his identity — loyalty, ambition, darkness, hustle, and survival — is encoded in his blood, tracing it through a Compton upbringing of motels and violence to present-day wealth, and turning that lineage into a claim of unshakeable selfhood.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~duckworth":{"haiku":"Chance encounters and moral reciprocity rewrite fate; a act of forgiveness decades prior saved a life that would birth greatness, proving that small decisions shape destinies.","opus":"A true story of how his own father and his label boss crossed paths at a KFC, where a robbery averted by small kindnesses changed both their fates. It is about how a single choice and blind chance decided whether Kendrick would become a great rapper or grow up fatherless.","sonnet":"Karma as structural coincidence: a young Anthony who became the gangster Top Dawg chose not to rob the Kentucky Fried Chicken worker Ducky — Kendrick's father — sparing two lives; twenty years later the two men sit in a recording studio reaping what that unchosen violence never destroyed, and Kendrick frames it as proof that one decision can reverse an entire lineage of doom.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~element":{"haiku":"The song asserts unshakeable identity and dominance in craft. The narrator operates from their element—willing to die for their truth, seeing enemies as peasants, impossible to remove.","opus":"A defiant declaration of survival and dominance, the rapper insisting nobody prays for him so he'll die for what he's built, refusing to go back to broke, doing it not for clout but for Compton. Threats are delivered with cold, stylish ease. From Kendrick Lamar's 2017 album DAMN., this rides a menacing, gospel-tinged beat as he plants his feet, daring anyone to take him out of his element and warning enemies who thought he'd disappear.","sonnet":"Survival-level confidence worn as armour — the speaker is willing to die for his place in rap, has watched his family sell dope to stay alive, and is done with anyone who mistakes his willingness to be in the streets for performance. The song is about being untouchable in your element because you've already paid the price for it: Compton, the feds watching, an aunt warning, still there. The flexing is inseparable from the cost.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~f-ck-your-ethnicity":{"haiku":"Transcending tribal identity for unified humanity; rejecting ethnic divisions as false boundaries.","opus":"The fiery opener of Section.80, where Lamar gathers a diverse crowd around a symbolic fire and dismisses racial and ethnic division as irrelevant to their shared struggle. Positioning music as salvation and himself as a conduit rather than a mere rapper, it reaches toward unity across creed and color, channeling HiiPower's call for consciousness against a still-racist world.","sonnet":"Addressed to a mixed crowd on a neighbourhood corner, the speaker declares racial identity irrelevant to the power of music and the persistence of racism alike — hip-hop as hypnosis that freed the mind while the body stayed trapped. The manifesto pivots between universal solidarity and sharp self-positioning as messenger rather than rapper.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~fear":{"haiku":"Accumulated trauma and fear permeate existence from childhood through adulthood; spiritual reckoning remains the only path toward salvation.","opus":"Kendrick anatomizing fear across his life, a mother's beatings, the terror of dying young in the streets, and the survivor's guilt of success. From DAMN., it traces how dread shapes a Black man's every age.","sonnet":"Three ages of fear are rendered in verse: a child terrified of his mother's discipline and Compton's streets; a teenager cataloguing every way he might die before adulthood; a successful 27-year-old paralyzed by the fear of losing everything he built. A closing voicemail ties all of it to a theology of chastisement, suggesting the fear itself is biblical and unending.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~feel":{"haiku":"The song catalogs paranoia, alienation, and societal betrayal. Nobody prays; everyone is selfish; the narrator can't breathe or sleep under the weight of contempt and false freedom.","opus":"A spiraling inventory of isolation and paranoia, the rapper repeating that nobody prays for him as he catalogues losing focus, patience and faith in friends and family, feeling the world ending while he boxes demons alone. It builds toward a near-apocalyptic dread. From Kendrick Lamar's DAMN., this is one of his most nakedly interior tracks, a relentless stream of I-feel confessions that turns fame's alienation into something suffocating and unguarded.","sonnet":"A litany of isolation — from friends who overclaim, family who fake it, the public who scrutinizes his come-up, and an interior world so saturated with the weight of others' suffering that it feels like pre-apocalypse. The repeated nobody is praying for me becomes the refrain of a man who has given everything to his community and finds himself more alone than when he started, filling graves and banks and prisons with the same exhausted hands.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~for-free-interlude":{"haiku":"A woman scorns her lover for failing to deliver material validation; her rage exposes masculine mediocrity and economic uselessness.","opus":"A jazzy, motormouth interlude from To Pimp a Butterfly answering a materialistic partner's demands with a defiant refrain that his worth isn't free. Weaving forty-acres reparations imagery and cotton-picking history into a critique of exploitation, Lamar flips the personal quarrel into an indictment of America itself. It reaches toward the refusal to be used without value, sexual and economic autonomy reclaimed.","sonnet":"A furious monologue from a woman dismantling a broke man's entitlement — framed as a sexual economics lesson, it expands into an indictment of American racial debt: \"I need forty acres and a mule,\" she says, placing individual desire on top of centuries of uncompensated labour, insisting that nothing — body or dignity — comes without a receipt.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~for-sale-interlude":{"haiku":"False enlightenment and spiritual commodification corrupt genuine transformation; purchase and profit replace authentic awakening.","opus":"A seductive, sinister interlude from To Pimp a Butterfly personifying temptation as Lucy, a Lucifer figure who promises mansions, money, and escape from Compton in exchange for the narrator's soul. Sweet-talking and relentless, she embodies the deal fame offers. It reaches toward the spiritual peril of success, the devil courting an artist with everything he ever wanted at a devastating price.","sonnet":"Lucy — Lucifer as seductive industry figure — courts Kendrick with a silk-tongued contract pitch: she's watched him all his life, promises papers and a mansion, and the warnings embedded in the song (\"you ain't pass economics\", \"I'll Wesley Snipe your ass before 35\") are her own words, designed to sound like candour while tightening the trap.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~hol-up":{"haiku":"Material ambition, sexual conquest, and street credibility define success as Kendrick navigates contradictions between aspiration and paranoia.","opus":"A brash, provocative stream of a young rapper high on new money and freedom mid-flight, boasting of excess while flashes of paranoia and self-doubt undercut the bravado, asking who he can even trust.","sonnet":"A raw stream of ego and anxiety thirty thousand feet up: sexual fantasy slides into mortality, Compton identity, Gemini self-distrust, and the paranoid admission that in 2012 he can't trust anyone — not even himself — interrupted throughout by the command to wait and take stock.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~hood-politics":{"haiku":"A rapper asserts his status and loyalty to his Compton neighborhood, distancing himself from industry politics and honoring fallen friends.","opus":"From Kendrick Lamar's 2015 album To Pimp a Butterfly, this is a homecoming reckoning with loyalty and street violence, the rapper affirming he's remained true from the start and brushing off industry games while grieving fallen friends. He maps gang divisions onto partisan politics with sly wordplay, fusing West Coast funk to sharp social commentary and refusing to forget his origins even as fame widens his world.","sonnet":"Gang politics and national politics mapped onto each other — the same tribalism, the same set-tripping, Democrats and Republicans rebranded as rival Crip and Blood factions. The speaker stays loyal to the dead homies and dismisses rap politics entirely, grounding everything in Compton and the reality that what looks like ideology from above is just survival from below. The song refuses to be impressed by either the streets or the Capitol.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~how-much-a-dollar-cost-feat-james-fauntleroy-ronald-isley":{"haiku":"A wealthy man refuses spare change to a homeless beggar, then questions his own moral failing when confronted by the stranger's spiritual wisdom.","opus":"A parable set at a Johannesburg gas station, where the narrator refuses a single dollar to a homeless man, guilt and resentment mounting until the beggar reveals himself as God quoting Exodus, exposing the true cost of the narrator's pride. From Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly, this is a masterclass in narrative rap, a slow-burn morality tale about greed and grace featuring Ronald Isley and James Fauntleroy on the aching hook.","sonnet":"A gas station encounter with a homeless man asking for ten rand becomes a confrontation with the limits of charity and the nature of guilt. The speaker, freshly wealthy, refuses him twice — irritated, self-righteous, ready to swing — until the man quotes Exodus and the refusal curdles into shame. The dollar amount is trivial; what it costs is the speaker's sense of himself as generous, and the song holds that reckoning without letting him off.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~humble":{"haiku":"Pride, self-affirmation, and superiority claimed through wealth and success, with the narrator asserting dominance over competitors and critics.","opus":"A braggadocious track flaunting the narrator's rise from poverty to wealth and status while demanding that a rival be humble and sit down. He rails against fakeness and Photoshop, calling for something natural and real, and crowns himself the realest of all.","sonnet":"From a Compton upbringing of syrup sandwiches to current wealth and dominance, the narrator demands that rivals and posers acknowledge their place, while also calling out the fakery of Photoshop beauty and insisting on natural, unfiltered reality.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~i":{"haiku":"Self-loathing and introspection spiral into existential crisis; the speaker confronts his own internal demons and capacity for harm.","opus":"Lamar's defiant self-love anthem, asserting his worth against suicidal despair, police violence and a neighborhood ruled by fear. Leaning on faith and sheer survival, he refuses to let dread win — hard-won self-acceptance offered as medicine to a community weighed down by trauma.","sonnet":"A survival manifesto built from the rubble of depression, paranoia, and Compton violence: the narrator has dealt with depressive episodes since adolescence, nearly ran his body into a building, and still arrives at \"I love myself\" — not as affirmation-poster sentiment but as hard-won defiance against the forces, internal and external, that tried to make him obsolete.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~institutionalized-feat-bilal-anna-wise-snoop-dogg":{"haiku":"Trapped in ghetto poverty and systemic cycles, a person cannot escape the institutionalized conditions they know despite dreaming of power and freedom. Street life becomes their college and inescapable destiny.","opus":"Kendrick Lamar dissects being institutionalized by the ghetto, trapped and ashamed yet compelled to return, dreaming of paying his mother's rent if he were president. On his major-label breakthrough, he brings homies to an awards show only to watch envy and greed take hold, echoing a grandmother's blunt wisdom that nothing changes until you change yourself.","sonnet":"A meditation on how the ghetto becomes internalized — even success cannot break the gravitational pull of the environment that formed him. A friend taken to the awards show is dazzled into envy and nearly criminal impulse. Loyalty is both his strength and the trap that keeps bringing him back to what he knows he should escape.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~king-kunta":{"haiku":"A successful artist reflects on their rise and denounces fake rappers, ghostwriters, and those who abandon him when he was struggling.","opus":"Kendrick claiming his throne while daring everyone who ignored him on the way up to try cutting him down, naming himself after a slave whose foot was severed for running. Funk swagger over a real refusal to lose, ghostwriters and rivals alike.","sonnet":"Kendrick returns to Compton at the height of his fame and addresses the industry and fair-weather associates who weren't around during the struggle — drawing a parallel to Kunta Kinte to assert Black pride and power, and calling out rappers who use ghostwriters as a specific betrayal of the art.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~lust":{"haiku":"Sexual desire and bodily heat mixed with casual materialism and street life priorities, balancing base want against daily hustling.","opus":"Kendrick Lamar lays out the numbing loop of appetite, from raw physical craving to a joyless daily routine of money, weed, sex and social-media flexing that just makes it count. On DAMN., he uses repetition to expose lust as spiritual autopilot, the same hollow motions cycling through his and others' lives until desire hardens into habit rather than release.","sonnet":"Desire and distraction interlock across two parallel days — one driven by sexual wanting, the other by the mundane rhythms of getting money, scrolling feeds, keeping up appearances. Neither day adds up to meaning. The heat of lust and the heat of routine are the same overheating system, and the water needed to cool it never quite arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~m-a-a-d-city-feat-mc-eiht":{"haiku":"Gang violence normalizes death in Compton; the speaker documents cycles of trauma, retribution, and survival in spaces where peace remains impossible.","opus":"A visceral tour through Compton's gang violence as the young Kendrick lived it, witnessing killings, laced blunts and the constant pressure that warps a mind. He indicts a system that profits from the chaos while dreaming of a generation that gets to be lawyers instead of boys with choppers.","sonnet":"Two voices map the violence and survival logic of Compton: Kendrick traces a childhood saturated by gang proximity, laced blunts, and a nine-year-old witnessing brains blown out at a burger stand, while MC Eiht layers in the street codes that shaped him — both asking whether the next generation can dream of something other than a chopper.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~momma":{"haiku":"Kendrick reflects on his journey from street life to rap stardom, claiming mastery over knowledge, streets, and moral wisdom.","opus":"A movement from mastery to humbling self-awareness, the rapper crowing that gratitude for his craft carried him back to his origins, rattling off all he's learned, until a boy who mirrors his younger self reveals he understood nothing until he returned. From To Pimp a Butterfly, it rides a warm, jazzy groove through pride and epiphany, tracing how achievement only gains meaning once it loops back to Compton and his roots.","sonnet":"The paradox of knowing everything and knowing nothing — the speaker catalogs everything rap and Compton taught him, everything he's mastered about loyalty and money and spirituality and the streets, and then arrives home to discover that all of it was incomplete. A boy with his own features is waiting. The song frames artistic and material success as the long route back to a community that never needed proof of anything.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~mortal-man":{"haiku":"Kendrick questions whether listeners will remain loyal and support him if his circumstances change, asking if they truly believe in him or will judge him by external failure and scandal.","opus":"Closing his most acclaimed album, Lamar tests the loyalty owed a leader who invokes Mandela's ghost and hopes his flows keep propelling. Again and again he asks whether followers remain fans when everything falls apart: if he is jailed, framed, or killed, will they still see K. Lamar or condemn him? Positioning himself as one who freed minds from mental slavery, he insists a prophet is confirmed only by this relentless question.","sonnet":"Casting himself as a flawed prophet in the tradition of Mandela, he tests the loyalty of followers when reputation collapses, arrest comes, or the industry turns away. Devotion that survives disgrace is the only devotion worth anything; everything else is performance.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~ronald-reagan-era":{"haiku":"Compton violence and systemic predation define survival as the narrator claims ruthless agency within warfare masquerading as civic order.","opus":"A visceral flashback to growing up in 1980s Compton under Reagan, amid crack, gang wars, and racist police. Lamar casts the children of that era as products of a designed devastation, where survival means you can't detour from a war with your own city.","sonnet":"A tour through Reagan-era Compton: riding 90 mph on a 40-ounce of freedom, navigating gang territories that paradoxically respect the speaker, reliving 1987's child poverty, police racism, and street violence — a raw declaration that the kids from that world aren't just statistics and aren't nothing to fuck with.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~swimming-pools-drank":{"haiku":"Alcohol culture seduces youth into numbness; the conscience screams against intoxication while societal pressure demands surrender to excess.","opus":"Peer pressure and generational alcoholism laid bare, a young man drinking to fit in while his own conscience begs him to stop before he drowns. The party anthem is really a warning about inheriting a family's addiction and the pull of the crowd.","sonnet":"A split-screen portrait of alcoholism — one voice seduced by peer pressure and the fantasy of the party, another voice (conscience) watching in horror as the narrator drowns in inherited patterns of drinking, pulling him toward the same destruction as his grandfather.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~the-blacker-the-berry":{"haiku":"A raw examination of anti-Black racism, internalized rage, and the speaker's own hypocrisy—confronting his hatred toward his own people and those who oppress them.","opus":"A defiant reckoning with Black identity, racism, and self-contradiction: the speaker confronts those who hate his people and culture, calls himself a hypocrite, and ties chains, slavery, and the streets to pride and rage over how oppression made him a killer.","sonnet":"The narrator asserts Black identity with fierce pride while also confronting his own hypocrisy: he condemns the systemic racism and cultural erasure inflicted on Black people, then turns the accusation inward by acknowledging that Black-on-Black violence undermines that same community.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is built as a trap, and the trigger is the self-accusation Kendrick keeps repeating, branding himself the year's greatest fraud. For two verses he mounts an unanswerable indictment of white supremacy — the scheme to wipe out a people's culture, the community sabotaged into manufacturing killers — reclaiming every slur hurled at him as proof of pride, twisting the old folk proverb about the sweetest fruit being the darkest into a threat. Then verse three walks the whole checklist of Black cultural loyalty (the Panthers, Garvey, Black History Month, even the stereotyped foods eaten in defiance) only to detonate it with the closing question: how can he mourn Trayvon Martin dead in the street when his own gang violence has killed a man darker than himself. That last turn is the point of the record's whole method — not that his outward rage is wrong, but that it cannot stand while an internalized copy of the same contempt runs inside him, so the charge he aimed at the world swings back and lands on his own name. It is a song that spends four minutes earning the right to indict everyone and then spends its final line refusing to spare itself.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","shame & repression","identity & becoming","violence & murder"],"means":"the charge aimed at the world swings back and lands on the speaker's name","built":"four minutes earn the right to indict, then the final line refuses to spare itself"},
"kendrick-lamar~these-walls-feat-bilal-anna-wise-thundercat":{"haiku":"A bedroom contains layers of emotion and power—pleasure, vulnerability, and the speaker's own contradictions as tenant, lover, and self.","opus":"Kendrick layers a seductive funk groove over a double meaning, walls that are both a woman's body and a prison holding the man who wronged him. Intimacy becomes a metaphor for consequence and karma, the speaker moving inside walls that cry, resent, and demand release. Part of his sprawling concept album, the track turns sex into a meditation on power, vengeance, and confinement beneath its silky surface.","sonnet":"Sexual intimacy layered over a metaphor of emotional architecture. Walls that hold pain and vacancy invite temporary occupation — the speaker is tenant not owner, aware the encounter is both relief and demolition. Pleasure and exposure become the same act in a body that has been empty too long.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~u":{"haiku":"The speaker condemns a family member for preaching but failing to help a young sister who got pregnant, expressing profound hatred for their hypocrisy and abandonment.","opus":"Kendrick turns the mirror into a weapon, a screamed, drunken self-excoriation where the you is himself. He blames himself for a sister's teen pregnancy, for preaching to thousands while failing those closest, for survivor's guilt and absence. The chorus that loving you is complicated masks self-loathing, hotel-room Spanish intruding on the breakdown, the inverse of his triumphant anthems and one of the album's rawest depths of self-hatred.","sonnet":"Kendrick performs a savage self-indictment in a hotel room. The public voice of inspiration failed his own sister when she needed him — the gap between preached influence and lived accountability tears open, and the rage directed outward reveals itself as shame directed inward, unanswerable and compounding.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~untitled-06-06-30-2014":{"haiku":"A Gemini speaker reconciles opposing internal selves while celebrating a partner's independence and refusing conventional masculine possession.","opus":"A soul-baring exchange celebrating difference and being truly seen, Kendrick reassuring a woman that her strangeness is her power. Vulnerability as intimacy, two outsiders recognizing each other beneath the flaws.","sonnet":"A Gemini narrator addresses a woman he admires, attempting to explain the contradiction of his dual nature while simultaneously paying tribute to the quiet strength and self-possession she displays. Admiration tips into devotion, and the speaker surrenders his identity to hers.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~wesley-s-theory-feat-george-clinton-thundercat":{"haiku":"Young Black men internalize materialism and destructive fantasy once given success and resources; they abandon relationships and moral grounding. The older generation warns that wealth without wisdom becomes corrosive.","opus":"Kendrick's opening salvo on the trap of sudden fame, voiced first as a newly-signed dreamer squandering it all, then as Uncle Sam eager to bleed him dry through debt and taxes. The Wesley Snipes reference nails the theme: Black success set up to be repossessed by a system that profits from the fall.","sonnet":"The seduction and corruption of a young Black artist by money and fame — framed as a cautionary tale, young Kendrick fantasizes about guns and Cadillacs while Uncle Sam (representing debt, predatory capitalism, and the music industry) dangles the dream and prepares to collect.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~xxx-feat-u2":{"haiku":"American violence and systemic racism demand accountability; the speaker questions divine purpose amid societal fracture and moral decay.","opus":"America's contradiction laid bare, Kendrick torn between preaching peace and the vengeance he'd want if his own were killed. Faith, gun violence, and hypocrisy collide, exposing a nation that loves the myth more than the reality.","sonnet":"Responding to a friend whose son was murdered, Kendrick admits he counseled revenge rather than peace — and then pivots to a wider indictment of American violence: guns shipped in, Fox News fear-mongering, systemic racism, and a culture that produces murder as naturally as it produces music, with America itself as the mirror reflecting all of it back.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~yah":{"haiku":"Ambition, spirituality, and worldly temptation collide as Kendrick asserts identity rooted in faith while balancing material desire and family legacy.","opus":"Kendrick reflects on family, media scrutiny, and temptation, weighing his mother's warning and his cousin's call toward faith against money and appetite, even reframing his identity through an Israelite lens. From DAMN., the track lets his radar buzz with suspicion as he follows intuition through the noise of fame and inherited spiritual burden.","sonnet":"Kendrick moves through the noise of celebrity, media appetite, and family counsel to arrive at a reclaimed identity rooted in Biblical lineage rather than racial labels handed down by history. The spiritual assertion is matter-of-fact, even casual, arriving amid worldly temptations he acknowledges without pretending to transcend.","src":"haiku"},
"kendrick-lamar~you-ain-t-gotta-lie-momma-said":{"haiku":"Performative masculinity and compensatory deception as survival, maternal wisdom exposing how insecurity drives fabrication and emotional bankruptcy.","opus":"A To Pimp a Butterfly track relaying a mother's plain wisdom about the exhausting futility of posturing. Lamar calls out the front of fabricated wealth, weapons, and connections, seeing insecurity behind the bravado. It reaches toward the freedom in authenticity, urging that you don't have to lie or perform to belong, because the truth is easier than the endless act.","sonnet":"A mother's-eye read of a performative fraud — the narrator channels his mother's withering clarity to address someone flexing guns, money, and connections they don't have. The verdict is delivered with surgical patience: the loudest one in the room is the most complex, and you sound like the feds, homie.","src":"haiku"},
"kennyhoopla~you-needed-a-hit":{"haiku":"Exhausted by manufactured despair, numb to the constant need for salvation or escape.","opus":"Depression and creative desperation collide as the artist mocks empty spiritual fixes while admitting his hope is dead, dangling between acclaim and breakdown. The demand for a hit song and a fix for the soul blur into one plea.","sonnet":"Caught between critical acclaim and mental derangement, all plants dead and hope gone, the speaker is sickened inside a world of common greed and hollow rituals — tossing out a sardonic \"you needed a hit, well listen to this\" as both offering and indictment.","src":"haiku"},
"kennyhoopla~you-needed-a-hit-":{"haiku":"Crisis feeds creative hunger; the speaker transforms despair and institutional failure into material that feeds artistic appetite.","src":"haiku"},
"kenta204~heart":{"haiku":"Isolation in urban exile demands connection with almost religious intensity, where the heart becomes weapon and refuge. Finding the one resolves the arc from loneliness to transcendence.","opus":"A restless late-night prayer for connection, the narrator adrift and wired on a street corner, leading with a battered, defiant heart that keeps getting knocked down and standing back up. Hunting for one dependable soul, he climbs toward a far-off light — the ache of wanting something real and pushing toward it.","sonnet":"A young man alone outside a 7-Eleven at night — wired, counting seconds, lost like a teenager — searches desperately for one reliable person, someone 'one in a thousand', and resolves the search inward: the mountain, the fountain, the answer found in his own heart through the dark.","src":"haiku"},
"kero-kero-bonito~battle-lines":{"haiku":"Authoritarian control manufactures artificial conflict to fracture populations; surrender and disarmament constitute resistance against perpetuated antagonism.","opus":"A chillingly bright dissection of how authoritarians manufacture division, feed grievance, and turn everyone into soldiers in a permanent invisible war. Political horror sung sweetly, ending on defiant collective refusal.","sonnet":"A playbook for authoritarian control — manufacture chaos, assign blame to invisible enemies, exhaust and divide the population — is laid out and then rejected: the answer is not armed resistance but collective, terrifying, unarmed solidarity.","src":"haiku"},
"kero-kero-bonito~dump":{"haiku":"Waste disposal rituals reveal collective disposal of obsolescence; landfill becomes site of democratic discard where society purges unwanted surplus.","opus":"A day at the landfill turned into a wry ode to disposability and consumer excess, everyone's discarded junk piling up and hauled away only to fill again. Deadpan whimsy masking a quiet ecological unease.","sonnet":"A fond, deadpan portrait of a recycling centre — the routines, the categories, the smells, the regular crowd — treated with the same gentle attention usually reserved for somewhere meaningful, finding community and cycle in the act of letting things go.","src":"haiku"},
"kero-kero-bonito~flyway":{"haiku":"A person observes migratory birds and reflects on their own desire to escape and find freedom elsewhere.","opus":"Migrating birds as a gentle metaphor for departure and return, wondering where they vanish and trusting they'll circle back. Beneath the sweetness is a quiet yearning to work up the courage to leave and join them someday.","sonnet":"A gentle meditation on migratory birds that becomes a wish for personal freedom — watching creatures that leave and reliably return sparks a longing to one day follow them and escape to somewhere cleaner and less suffocating.","src":"haiku"},
"kero-kero-bonito~outside":{"haiku":"Nature awakens sensory wonder; the speaker emerges into vivid morning beauty, experiencing rain and light with fresh, almost forgotten clarity.","opus":"Reawakening to the ordinary miracle of the natural world after being shut in, the rain and dewy leaves and fresh air rendered newly vivid. It's a quiet wonder at how alive and colorful the outside becomes once you actually step into it.","sonnet":"A sensory awakening to an ordinary rainy morning rendered as wonder: dewy leaves breathing, drizzle landing on cue, colours piling to heaven — the speaker realises they cannot remember ever noticing the world being so vivid, and the joy is in that noticing.","src":"haiku"},
"kero-kero-bonito~you-know-how-it-is":{"haiku":"Routine and distance erode connections; the sky crashes and the speaker walks in silence, sensing permanent change but unable to explain why.","opus":"The London group set melancholy against their usual bubblegum brightness, naming the quiet exhaustion of trying while nothing goes right and saying you're fine when you're not. Days blur identically, friends unseen, something changing forever without explanation. The park empties of children, planes fly strangely low, and shared unspoken sadness surfaces in the closing admission, the sweet pop surface making the everyday depression underneath land harder.","sonnet":"The quiet grief of a life grown unfamiliar. Days blur into sameness, friends drift away unseen, planes seem to fly lower than they used to — something is shifting permanently but cannot be named. The solidarity offered is small: the speaker knows this feeling because they carry it too.","src":"haiku"},
"kerser~forever":{"haiku":"Street struggle meets music industry compromise; the narrator grapples with how fame distanced them from authentic struggle and street credibility.","opus":"An Australian rapper reckoning with how success replaced poverty as his struggle, resentful of fame's costs and fake people yet driven to fight and guide those coming up. Loyalty to his hard past against the strangeness of arriving.","sonnet":"The rapper reflects on how his struggles have shifted from street poverty to the pressures and betrayals of the music industry, defending his authenticity, mourning the loss of old mates, and struggling to reconcile fame with the rawness of where he came from.","src":"haiku"},
"kevin-morby~i-have-been-to-the-mountain":{"haiku":"Witness to systemic injustice and state murder animates prophetic vision; the speaker stands with the slain and sings their extinguished breath.","opus":"Written for Eric Garner, killed by police on a New York street. Morby borrows the cadence of a spiritual to mourn a man taken by the state, the mountaintop vision of justice glimpsed and then lost, a witness haunted by the sound of a life ending.","sonnet":"A witness to violence — likely police killing — processes what they have seen through a grief-stricken, visionary lens: having been to mountains, valleys, and shores, having seen everything, they can no longer look at what keeps happening to people in their town.","src":"haiku"},
"kevin-penkin~underground-river-opening-version":{"haiku":"A traveler seeks identity and truth by crossing a mysterious underground river, hoping to uncover their true nature and potential.","opus":"Kevin Penkin scores a longing journey through cosmic space toward a faint, half-remembered world that seems to summon the traveler onward. The composer, celebrated for anime and game soundtracks balancing awe and unease, casts the quest as one of self-discovery, the seeker chasing a hidden essence he hopes to revive.","sonnet":"A journey back through some luminous other-space toward an underground river whose soul calls across vast distance. Identity is uncertain — the narrator knows what he is but not who he could become — and the river promises revelation. The song is a threshold hymn, orienting someone between two selves.","src":"haiku"},
"key-glock~one-me":{"haiku":"Predatory ambition and violent self-assertion; the speaker stakes dominance through material excess and threat, celebrating the conquest of others as proof of supremacy.","opus":"A Memphis rapper's boast about money, jewels, and being untouchable, positioning himself as one of a kind and nobody's equal. Under the flexing about dodging federal agents and stacking cash runs a wariness of informants, gossips, and phony friends, all held at arm's length.","sonnet":"A self-made flexer declares his uniqueness — stacking money from the moment he wakes, moving through street dangers and luxury alike (two Rolls, a mouth full of gold at 14), broadcasting that there is only one version of him and everyone should fall in line or stay silent.","src":"haiku"},
"khalid~talk-feat-disclosure":{"haiku":"Communication and vulnerability precede intimacy; two people speak carefully to align their futures, aware that misunderstanding could shatter what they are building.","opus":"The tentative early stage of a romance, wanting to slow down and just talk through where it's headed before feelings outrun understanding. It is about the vulnerability and hopeful caution of not yet knowing what you're becoming to each other.","sonnet":"A tentative, eager request for honest conversation before things get ahead of themselves: the speaker is falling fast and knows it, apologises for moving too far, and asks simply to talk — to figure out where they are going before getting lost — leaving flowers and an unlocked door as signs of good faith.","src":"haiku"},
"khemmis~living-pyre":{"haiku":"The speaker drowns in their own mind while sins weigh them down as anchors into the cold. They burn like a living pyre, vessels wrought from suffering and blinding light.","opus":"The Denver doom-metal band cast despair as self-immolation, nothing left to offer and no ember left to sustain an inner spark while injustice storms outside. Sinking under a mind that keeps collapsing, the body an anchor of guilt, the speaker sets himself ablaze. Life itself becomes a torment we're born into and buried within, voiced through weary, cosmic melodic doom.","sonnet":"The weight of guilt and inherited sinfulness burns from within while the world offers no relief. The body is an anchor, the mind a flooding room — yet beneath the despair sits a harder question about what it means to be made in a divine image and thrust against one's will into the light.","src":"haiku"},
"khruangbin~evan-finds-the-third-room":{"haiku":"Cryptic transcendence beyond normal channels opens as the narrator navigates through strange menus toward hidden spaces of understanding and freedom.","opus":"A playful, near-nonsense groove built on the mantra of the third room and an automated phone menu, treating arrival and invitation as pure vibe rather than meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"khruangbin~so-we-won-t-forget":{"haiku":"Preserving significant moments before they disappear, cherishing memories beyond words.","opus":"A hushed groove about trying to preserve a feeling before memory erases it, writing things down against forgetting. The quiet dread underneath is that the singer suspects they've already lost it.","sonnet":"An act of deliberate remembrance — writing things down, saying them aloud, repeating them so that even when memory fades there is something left; the speaker is less concerned with words than with the act of marking time so it doesn't simply vanish.","src":"haiku"},
"kickrox~a-xvr0df":{"haiku":"Spiritual salvation through singular devotion—one person provides transcendent light; love overcomes distance and gives meaning to suffering.","opus":"This Russian song frames devotion as salvation, the beloved a source of light that reaches across any distance. After years of searching and enduring pain that felt fated, finding this person made everything easy, and the lyric wonders openly whether what they share is real or illusion. Still the narrator vows to wait until their final breath reaches only this person's image — love offered as the one meaning strong enough to justify the suffering that came before it.","sonnet":"A Russian song of devotion to a person who gives the narrator light across any distance: after years of searching and enduring pain that felt fated, finding this person made everything easy; the lyric wonders whether what they share is real or illusion, but the narrator will wait until their final breath reaches only the image of this person.","src":"haiku"},
"kid-brunswick~blow":{"haiku":"Trapped in a destructive relationship where pleasure and pain blur; unable to leave despite the cost.","opus":"Addiction rendered as a toxic lover who drains everything and is never there when needed, the money-burning and the drug indistinguishable. Staying despite the ruin is the trap the song refuses to romanticize its way out of.","sonnet":"About a drug habit framed as a relationship that drains everything from you — the person (or substance) takes all the money, blows the narrator's mind, keeps them coming back even though it never shows up when needed; the glass tables and fatal favors confirm the cocaine reading, but the emotional abandonment is real either way.","src":"haiku"},
"kid-brunswick~heaven-without-you":{"haiku":"A breakup is simultaneously agonising and liberating — it hurts like hell but without the other person the speaker unexpectedly finds something like relief, a painful freedom they can barely articulate.","opus":"A goodbye to a damaging romance that felt intoxicating even though it was doomed. The singer wishes his ex the best while admitting the split is agonizing yet also a relief, a title that turns the parting into both wound and rescue. It sits in the tangle of grief and freedom that comes from leaving someone who hurt you.","sonnet":"Ambivalence after a relationship that was never quite right: never thought we were meant to be, but for a while it was ecstasy — and the paradox holds firm, it hurts like hell but it's heaven without you, the relief and the grief the same thing felt at the same time.","src":"haiku"},
"kid-brunswick~prescription-kid":{"haiku":"A young person blames prescription drugs for aggression and arrest rather than taking responsibility; cool requires sickness and American excess.","opus":"Raw autobiographical account of prescription-drug addiction and self-blame deflected onto pills and American excess, a young man sleeping on the floor and hating himself. The bravado barely hides a plea to get his life back.","sonnet":"A song about a young man whose aggression, arrests, and self-destruction are filtered through the lens of American pharmaceutical culture and performative cool — he blames the pills, knows he has excuses, and ends up sleeping on floors having lost himself completely.","src":"haiku"},
"kid-brunswick~stockholm":{"haiku":"Isolation, therapy, and depression paint life in a distant place. A speaker trades their world for answers that never come.","opus":"An expatriate's spiral of burnout and isolation abroad, losing friends and sleep while pouring money into therapy, clinging to a fading love and the old belief that burning out beats fading away.","sonnet":"A song about depression and isolation while living abroad — the narrator has been in Stockholm for a year, losing friends, sleeping through the day, spending money on therapy, and clinging to a long-distance connection with someone, unable to stop their emotional freefall.","src":"haiku"},
"kid-kapichi~5-days-on-2-days-off":{"haiku":"Exhaustion beneath the grind; cycles of labor with no release from mechanical repetition.","opus":"A working-class punk blast at the drudgery of the standard week, the singer watching the clock and feeling his skull about to burst under a smirking manager. He refuses the lazy label and schemes a getaway so he never returns. The song channels the grind of wage work and lives spent going in circles with no relief in sight.","sonnet":"The working week as an endurance trap — nine a.m. already staring at the clock, the bossman smirking while you're still there — the speaker isn't here to amuse anyone, just to get paid and leave, and the one thing that will dig your grave faster than drugs or drinking is the pressure building in your veins that the five-days-two-days machine never relieves.","src":"haiku"},
"kid-kapichi~artillery":{"haiku":"Systemic inequality breeds rage as the dispossessed confront wealthy indifference; revolution rises against centuries of neglect, powered by the masses finally refusing to accept crumbs.","opus":"A working-class taunt aimed at the comfortable rich, turning class resentment into a battle chant where laughing at the gun is the only weapon the have-nots own. The trick of the powerful is to numb people into thinking the fight is already lost.","sonnet":"Working-class fury directed at the wealthy and politically indifferent — people kept poor and cold while the privileged hoard offshore money and dismiss unrest, with revolution promised once the patience runs out.","src":"haiku"},
"kid-kapichi~can-eu-hear-me":{"haiku":"Fractured Europe and isolation motivate flight; the speaker demands escape from island imprisonment, unity breaking across language and border.","opus":"A Brexit lament from someone who wants out of an island that voted to isolate itself on lies, calling across the Channel to European friends. The multilingual goodbyes are a grieved refusal to live alone, warning the young they'll end up trapped like him.","sonnet":"A post-Brexit lament — angry and disillusioned at being cut off from Europe by a vote built on lies, the narrator just wants to leave the island and reconnect with the continent, shouting across the water to friends who can still move freely.","src":"haiku"},
"kid-kapichi~sardines":{"haiku":"The song expresses pervasive frustration and rage at the grinding conditions of working-class life—crowded, unrewarding work, a failed car, and the broken promise that hard work pays off.","opus":"A portrait of grinding working-class frustration: a soul-numbing 9-to-5, sleeplessness, packed commuter trains, and things going wrong, leaving a person angry without knowing why and flirting with murderous rage at a system where crime and the powerful alike 'don't pay.'","sonnet":"A catalogue of daily indignities — sleepless nights, a miserable job, a packed commute, a car destroyed on its first day out — builds a picture of grinding working-class frustration, with the refrain pointing out that crime doesn't pay, but neither do the bosses.","src":"haiku"},
"kid-kapichi~super-soaker":{"haiku":"Procrastination and avoidance crystallise into a persistent state of self-awareness without change; the speaker recognises his own inertia as inherent to who he is.","opus":"A slacker's anthem stitched from rapid-fire nicknames and a long tally of everything the singer will never amount to. The real content is happy aimlessness — a work-free morning in bed with a tune stuck in his head, picking a backyard soaking over anything useful.","sonnet":"A day off becomes a manifesto for cheerful procrastination, as the speaker rattles through an absurdist A-to-Z list of all the things they'll never be before settling on spending the day in bed with the same song looping and a water fight in the shed.","src":"haiku"},
"kid-loco~lucy-s-talking":{"haiku":"The Devil and Lord both visit the narrator's home, the former eating their chicken and the latter offering boozy satisfaction; sin and temptation battle.","opus":"A wry, bluesy fable in which the Devil eats the narrator's chicken and the Lord shares boozy drinks in the bedroom, the two forces bargaining over sin until cold turkey, both the meal and the drug withdrawal, becomes the loping refrain. Kid Loco is a French trip-hop producer, and this rides his signature woozy, downtempo grooves, spinning a mischievous morality tale about temptation and craving into something hypnotic and darkly comic.","sonnet":"The Devil and the Lord take turns visiting the speaker's home — the Devil eats his food, the Lord drinks his liquor and warns against Lucifer's temptations, and the speaker gives the Lord the bones of his cold turkey in return. The song is a blues parable about competing spiritual claims, told with wry economy: both powers show up hungry, both want something, and the speaker navigates between them with what little he has left.","src":"haiku"},
"kill-the-kong~interstellar":{"haiku":"Lies erode reality while darkness spreads; the speaker races through chaos trying to stay awake and alive despite watching everything fall apart.","opus":"A frantic struggle to stay awake and living, the narrator wrecked by a bond gone toxic that shackled him, doubting his own sanity while urging a partner to keep breathing so they don't perish anew. Dread and depletion close in from all sides. The metalcore band lean on crushing dynamics and a recurring storm motif to stage two people clutching each other against ruin.","sonnet":"A relationship that weaponized attachment — the other person's strings like chains, their darkness drawn out and left behind. The speaker can't escape the maze of what was done, questions whether they've become a fatalist, and urges themselves and whoever is listening to stay awake, keep breathing, not look down. The song sits in the specific dread of someone who knows the damage is ongoing and is choosing, daily, to remain in the game.","src":"haiku"},
"kill-the-lights~broken-bones":{"haiku":"Remorse and degradation settle into the speaker's marrow; inside rotting while performing vitality, nothing survives except the skeletal remains of what once held weight.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with guilt, regret and emotional decay, the narrator dying inside as he tries to release a hollowed relationship. Its refrain diagnoses someone dead within yet still walking, full of lies and rotting, reaching toward the wreckage of a bond that leaves both parties gutted, masking the pain while clutching at survival.","sonnet":"A speaker confronts someone who is dead inside yet still walking — full of lies, rotting — swallowing their embers and surrendering to the burning, yet unable to let go of the hope that they will make it out alive from a relationship built on nothing but broken bones.","src":"haiku"},
"kill-the-lights~hear-you-scream":{"haiku":"Betrayal and powerlessness transmute into vengeance; the speaker claims strength from being crushed, rejects hollow love, and vows to never hear the lies that once enslaved them.","opus":"A vengeful reversal against a lover who crushed and abandoned him, the wounded party crawling off the ledge to demand blood for a phony love. The metalcore act converts loneliness and betrayal into a merciless refusal to grant mercy, a burning-down world with no excuses left.","sonnet":"A speaker who has been emotionally gutted by a partner turns the tables with cold fury — \"bite your tongue and crack your teeth, let's see you bleed\" shifts the suffering outward, and the vow to not hear the other person scream is the final refusal of their power.","src":"haiku"},
"kill-the-lights~plagues":{"haiku":"Grief consumes the narrator as they watch a loved one die, suffocating in the helplessness of morphine and decay while trapped between survival and the desire to follow them into death.","opus":"Sickness and death imagery drive a portrait of the grieving and the gone hunting for somewhere to belong, watching a loved one beg for the end in a hospital bed. It's about drowning in sorrow and worthlessness, casting oneself as the affliction.","sonnet":"The speaker watches a loved one die in a clinical setting — morphine, doctors wiping lips, praying for death — and is overwhelmed by grief, drowning in the flood of loss and crying out to be buried alongside it.","src":"haiku"},
"kill-the-lights~shed-my-skin":{"opus":"A funereal call to rise from the ashes and take back one's life from those who broke you, refusing to fear tormentors any longer, admitting to being broken rather than bleeding, and readying to shed the old self.","sonnet":"About collectively confronting inner demons and deciding to stop hiding from them — a community of broken, isolated people gathers to declare they will no longer be afraid, rising from the ashes to burn what held them down and shed the skin of who they were.","src":"sonnet"},
"kill-the-lights~through-the-night":{"haiku":"Psychological torment demands survival through night; the narrator fights internal darkness while relationships crumble under accumulated damage.","opus":"Battling suicidal despair and inner demons, fighting simply to survive till morning. Metalcore catharsis for the person drowning in self-hatred, turning private darkness into a shared cry for deliverance.","sonnet":"A person consumed by internal darkness and broken by despair calls out for the nightmare to end, naming demons, hopelessness, and a desperate need to understand their own pain — survival feels less like living than enduring.","src":"haiku"},
"killer-be-killed~deconstructing-self-destruction":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects a group's infectious negativity and chooses self-determination, refusing complicity while witnessing others destroy themselves spiritually through obsession and burnout.","opus":"A face-off with the urge to self-destruct that hardens into a survivor's creed, silencing the chaos before vowing to chart one's own course, live without fear of mortality, and surrender to momentum. Suffering becomes evidence of being alive. Killer Be Killed, the supergroup drawing from Mastodon, Soulfly and Dillinger Escape Plan, hammer resilience against the pull toward ruin.","sonnet":"The pull toward self-destruction examined and then refused — or at least renegotiated. The speaker names the pyrogenic, malevolent forces pressing inward, acknowledges the spiritual obsession with punishing oneself, and arrives at a stubborn counter-argument: find your own end on your own terms, follow your own plans, live as though dying isn't the point. The song keeps both the appeal of destruction and the will to outlast it in tension.","src":"haiku"},
"killer-be-killed~dream-gone-bad":{"haiku":"The narrator battles internal demons and clouded thinking, learning that one's worst enemy is oneself and that redemption requires facing this truth.","opus":"This metal supergroup wrestles with inherited patterns and self-sabotage, the narrator recognizing that he is his own greatest obstacle. Against a sense of fate closing in, the song offers defiant reassurance that change is always possible and no one has to face it alone, urging him to hold on to his integrity and turn toward hope as the pressure mounts.","sonnet":"A reckoning with self-deception that has calcified over time. The reflection in the mirror has hardened into an enemy — one's own worst adversary — and the realization arrives that it is never too late to turn around, even as walls close in and the calling comes from somewhere past the fold.","src":"haiku"},
"killing-joke~dawn-of-the-hive":{"haiku":"Advanced technology and surveillance systems control human consciousness through electromagnetic manipulation and frequency modulation. Humanity is connected into a hive mind against their will.","opus":"A paranoid dystopian rant about power lines, transmission masts and aircraft trails supposedly reshaping reality and lashing humanity into one governed collective consciousness, complete with implanted voices and a sleepless watching eye. Conspiracy terror rendered as apocalyptic hymn. Killing Joke are the veteran British post-punk band whose tribal, industrial-edged sound influenced countless heavier acts, here channeling that battering force into a warning about technological mind control.","sonnet":"A conspiracy theory rendered as apocalyptic prophecy — electromagnetic towers, chemtrails, nano-technology, satellite masts all working in concert to synchronize human consciousness into a hive mind. The song doesn't mock the premise; it inhabits the dread completely, treating the all-seeing eye and the modulation of reality as accomplished facts. The repeated dawn of the hive frames total surveillance not as dystopia-in-progress but as something already arrived.","src":"haiku"},
"kills-birds~behind":{"haiku":"The speaker perpetually lags behind wealth and connection, suffocating under the weight of inadequacy while demanding a mercy they cannot find.","opus":"The gnawing sense of arriving late to everything and forever trailing, envying someone's ease and money while begging for a scrap of peace. The repeated cry to be sunk and cut loose turns that lack into a raw, self-destructive craving to be seen and waited for.","sonnet":"A narrator perpetually late to the party, slightly off-key and always a step behind, rails against a world of money and ease she can't access, demanding peace of mind while the repeated 'sink me now / cut my chord' turns longing into something more desperate.","src":"haiku"},
"killswitch-engage~my-curse":{"haiku":"A man watches the object of his love slip away, trapped in longing and yearning. His curse is the pain of separation and the burning desire to see them again.","opus":"A grief-stricken longing for someone lost: watching them walk away, the narrator frames his endless yearning, aching and waiting as a curse he can't escape, sustained only by a burning love and the faint hope they'll return.","sonnet":"Watching someone walk away and being unable to do anything about it — the song is about the ache of longing after a separation, where silence and absence have become a kind of torment that keeps burning.","src":"haiku"},
"killswitch-engage~my-last-serenade":{"haiku":"Someone confesses that this is their final plea: truth is absolution, but you must choose whether to rise or become enslaved to self-righteousness—the choice is yours alone.","opus":"A farewell staged as a closing song, the narrator watching someone descend into self-righteous ruin while insisting their fate is their own doing and there's no escaping oneself. Beneath the goodbye lies an appeal to look inward and rise rather than submit. Killswitch Engage are the Massachusetts metalcore pioneers, and this early cut showcases their signature crushing riffs against soaring choruses on redemption.","sonnet":"A final address to someone whose self-righteousness has become a kind of damnation — the speaker has been watching this person fall, feels it happening, and offers the truth as the only thing left. The path runs from sorrow to serenity if you choose it, but the choice is entirely the other person's; the speaker is washing their hands while still holding the door. The last serenade is simultaneously a goodbye and a challenge.","src":"haiku"},
"killswitch-engage~the-arms-of-sorrow":{"haiku":"Darkness and despair entrap; the speaker descends into misery's embrace where echoes follow her down.","opus":"Depression rendered as a slow sinking, masking the pain with hollow grins while dropping further into a despair of one's own making. The track gropes toward a peace it doubts is real, naming its inner demons in order to endure them.","sonnet":"A person descends helplessly into depression — every echo and shadow of themselves follows them deeper — clinging to the hope that there must be serenity somewhere beyond the fall, while the demons they carry are ones of their own making. The sorrow has become an embrace rather than an external force.","src":"haiku"},
"killswitch-engage~the-end-of-heartache":{"haiku":"Separated lovers endure emotional torment while holding onto memories, the speaker waiting to be called upon and hoping for reunion to end their heartbreak.","opus":"Metalcore standard-bearers Killswitch Engage pair crushing weight with melodic longing on this title track, the narrator grasping at memories as he falls and calling out for his beloved to answer while he awaits release from a shattered heart. Sleep offers escape but also the dread of waking alone; his one wish is to close the gap between them, surrender and yearning fusing into an ache for wholeness.","sonnet":"Separation from a beloved reduces existence to a kind of suspended half-life — sleep brings relief but waking is instant misery again. The speaker holds on only by voicing a name and waiting for absence itself to finally exhaust itself into something resembling peace.","src":"haiku"},
"killswitch-engage~unleashed":{"haiku":"Betrayal and accumulated pain rupture the speaker's restraint, unleashing a primal violence he can no longer contain or justify.","opus":"A warning to whoever pushed a patient man past his limit, the restrained fury finally breaking loose. Betrayal answered by the wild, uncomprehended part of him now off its leash and unashamed.","sonnet":"A man who has been repeatedly deceived and pushed past his limit finally unleashes the feral part of himself that forgiveness and restraint had contained, confronting the person responsible with the full weight of the innocents harmed and the promises broken.","src":"haiku"},
"kim-dracula~hysterics":{"haiku":"Rejection breeds bitter vindication fantasies where former dismissal transforms into envied fame; laughter becomes the weapon against those who once mocked.","opus":"A small-town outcast who was bullied and belittled turns the tables from a place of fame and money. The bitter sting is that the people who withheld any warmth now lavish it, leaving him unhinged and unable to trust affection he no longer believes.","sonnet":"A small-town outsider who was bullied and dismissed now laughs at the reversal — the people who showed no love suddenly want in — and the recognition arrives so late it reads as worthless, landing the speaker in hysterics rather than triumph.","src":"haiku"},
"kim-petras~there-will-be-blood":{"haiku":"Predatory seduction promises violence as entertainment, with the aggressor celebrating dominance and suffering as mutual ecstasy.","opus":"Camp horror-pop cast as a seductive predator luring victims to a bloody, inescapable fate. Halloween theatrics where pain is paradise and desire is the trap, danger played for gleeful thrill.","sonnet":"A speaker casts herself as a gleefully predatory force — pulling her target into darkness, thriving on their fear and pain, and warning them from the start that the encounter will be fatal, framed as a camp horror-movie seduction.","src":"haiku"},
"kim-petras~treat-me-like-a-slut":{"haiku":"Unapologetic desire for intense physical pleasure where the speaker demands to be treated as a sexual object without restraint.","opus":"An unabashed anthem of sexual appetite, the narrator demands to be handled roughly and takes ownership of desire without apology. Kim Petras, a pop provocateur who courts controversy, leans fully into hyperpop shock-value, turning explicit want into a defiant statement of pleasure claimed on her own terms.","sonnet":"A woman claims explicit sexual desire entirely on her own terms, recasting a degrading label as a statement of unapologetic appetite. The directness leaves no ambiguity: the point is pleasure chosen, not imposed.","src":"haiku"},
"kimbra~two-way-street":{"haiku":"Romantic reciprocity requires mutual vulnerability and commitment; love demands equal investment from both parties to flourish.","opus":"The nervous hope of finally letting someone in, insisting real love must be mutual and can't be forced. Kimbra frames surrender as a shared crossing, wanting the person more than any grand destiny.","sonnet":"An anticipation song: the speaker waits for someone to walk toward her and when they appear, all dissonance dissolves — but she knows love requires mutual movement, that she cannot make someone fall for her, and that the only way this works is if both people are walking toward each other at the same time.","src":"haiku"},
"kindrid~autumn-fog":{"haiku":"Innocence dissolves into perpetual misunderstanding: lessons refuse to land across the chasm between belief and experience, leaving only the repetition of cycles.","opus":"Dense religious imagery of dogma, debt and duress circling the same failure again and again, endless openings to get it wrong. Faith fogged into a futile, repeated grasping.","sonnet":"A dense, abstract song about being trapped in cycles of misunderstanding and forced compliance — beguiled into innocence again and again, anointed under duress, unable to break the loop despite countless chances.","src":"haiku"},
"king-810~braveheart":{"haiku":"Street loyalty and tribal warfare become the only code that matters; the speaker accepts damnation as the price of uncompromising allegiance.","opus":"A brutal dispatch from Flint's violence, loyalty and revenge in a world where the narrator feels reduced to an animal, godless and damned. Grief for lost childhood hardening into a fatalistic eye-for-an-eye code.","sonnet":"A speaker from a violent, hopeless urban landscape addresses outsiders who claim loyalty and bravery but will never belong to a community shaped by genuine sacrifice, gunfire, and grief — where childhood memories are the only tenderness left in a life already half-given over to death.","src":"haiku"},
"king-810~hurry-hurry-ahura-mazda":{"haiku":"Mysticism and wonder persist in mundane horror; the speaker seeks meaning in cosmic connection while drowning in human suffering.","opus":"A meditation that locates cosmic wonder in the plain human body, seeing an entire galaxy spun into a lock of a woman's hair and homes as the tombs of the living. Calling on the Zoroastrian deity of light, it declares that our reason for being is another person, then grieves a vanished love with imagery of weeping seas.","sonnet":"A mystical meditation on human consciousness — 'the human is a humid sack of water, dichroic, kaleidoscopic' — finds the divine in a curl of hair, the galaxy in the everyday, and the self as a drop inseparable from the river, calling urgently to the Zoroastrian god of light to hurry while heathens dance on the chest.","src":"haiku"},
"king-crimson~21st-century-schizoid-man-radio-version-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Modern alienation breeding violence and madness, a dystopian critique of civilization.","opus":"A 1969 howl at Vietnam-era madness, cataloguing napalm, greed and political rot as the fractured psyche of the century itself. Prog's furious opening statement, the modern world diagnosed as a split mind.","sonnet":"A portrait of modern civilisation as a schizoid monster — political violence, war profiteering, the rape of innocents, poets starving while children bleed — compressed into a rapid-fire litany of twentieth-century atrocity.","src":"haiku"},
"king-crimson~in-the-wake-of-poseidon":{"haiku":"Corrupt authorities and religious hypocrisy perpetuate suffering, balancing justice against exploitation. The song surveys cycles of power, faith, and earthly reckoning.","opus":"A dense allegory weighing humanity's fate through the four classical elements, its verses crowded with jesters, mourners, clergy and the enslaved as truth is snared and belief carved onto forgotten tombs. King Crimson, the founding British prog band, wrapped this 1970 title track in Mellotron grandeur and Peter Sinfield's ornate, doom-laden poetry about a civilization hanging in the balance.","src":"haiku"},
"king-crimson~starless":{"haiku":"Internal blindness and despair define existence; the world is dark and empty despite external beauty. The song depicts a soul cut off from hope and starlight.","opus":"A slow slide into desolation, a brilliant sunset registered only inwardly as the speaker's vision turns inward to find kindness soured and hope draining into nothing. King Crimson, the pioneering British prog band, close 1974's Red with this revered piece, its aching melody swelling into a long, storming crescendo of exquisite gloom.","sonnet":"Desolation at day's end — the gold of sundown turns inward and becomes nothing, just the word starless and the weight of Bible black. Old friend charity shows up with a cruel smile that signals only emptiness. The sky fades through silver to grey, and the hope that lives in that grey is the hope of something final, not rescue. The poem is six stanzas of compression, each one narrowing toward the same void.","src":"haiku"},
"king-crimson~the-court-of-the-crimson-king":{"haiku":"A surreal bureaucracy of arcane figures governs the dance of consciousness; puppets move through ritual and divination in service to hidden machinery.","opus":"A surreal medieval pageant where a puppet-master jester pulls the strings of dancing figures, an allegory of manipulation and illusion. Prog's foundational vision of a world ruled by hidden, indifferent forces.","sonnet":"A surreal medieval allegory unfolds through symbolic figures — a purple piper, a black queen, a yellow jester, a gardener — governing a court of illusion and ritual where the narrator wanders as an outsider unable to decode the kingdom's signs.","src":"haiku"},
"king-geedorah~fastlane":{"haiku":"Chasing wealth while wrestling with mortality, searching for meaning beyond the accumulation game.","opus":"MF DOOM in his three-eyed monster guise threading dense esoterica through the fast-lane hustle, insisting that fearing death means failing to live. The seance imagery and coded wordplay reward the few who chase the style rather than the flash.","sonnet":"A dense hip-hop track about living fast, accumulating power and rhymes, and navigating street life with spiritual detachment — the narrator invokes divine judgment, rejects the nine-to-five, and frames rap skill as a kind of survival weapon. The outro (Geedorah and Gigan as controlled kaiju) frames the whole thing as cinematic spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"king-gizzard-the-lizard-wizard~hot-wax":{"haiku":"Creeping, inescapable threat permeates existence; the narrator describes an oozing horror that infiltrates every boundary and ignores all resistance.","opus":"A creeping, faintly menacing image of hot wax seeping everywhere and into the brain, more mood and texture than message. Psych-rock delirium, dread and hypnosis dressed as a nonsense chant.","sonnet":"Hot wax serves as a surreal, creeping metaphor for something inexorable — seeping under every boundary, infiltrating the brain — inviting everyone along on a safari into a strange altered state with no clear destination.","src":"haiku"},
"king-gizzard-the-lizard-wizard~nuclear-fusion":{"haiku":"Molecular bonds mirror romantic union; cosmic patterns suggest separation is illusion and fusion is inevitable destiny.","opus":"Cosmic oneness rendered through atomic imagery, lovers or all matter bound so tightly they become a single being. Psychedelic wonder at how the same patterns mirror from atoms to stars, connection as physics.","sonnet":"Universal connection is expressed through the metaphor of atomic fusion: everything mirrors everything else, from stars to atoms, and despite all forces that might separate — ocean, frustration, isolation — the speaker finds in the image of nuclear fusion a reason to believe that we are all essentially one being.","src":"haiku"},
"king-mothership~cosmic-meltdown":{"haiku":"A man lost in hedonism and darkness strikes a Faustian bargain, sacrificing his soul for power and sensation.","opus":"A devil's-bargain arena tale of a lost kid who hands his soul to a demon in exchange for a life of vice and rock excess, then careens toward a hellish reckoning as worlds die and fire glints in his gaze. The hard-rock band steep it in classic damnation lore, stretching into a theatrical epic, half boastful party anthem and half doom narrative, grasping at glory while sinking toward the grave.","sonnet":"A young man at rock bottom — broke, humiliated, unable to breathe — bargains his soul away to a demon in exchange for the rock-and-roll life he's always wanted. The deal is struck in a dream that becomes real: minions, sacrifice, blood, the stars suddenly within reach. The song holds the Faustian transaction with genuine excitement and genuine dread at the same time, the reflection of flames in his eyes the only honest price tag.","src":"haiku"},
"king-mothership~i-stand-alone-feat-plini":{"opus":"A man burning away his possessions and past, defying execution and shadowy powers, refusing to repent, and realizing that after alienating everyone he once held close he now stands utterly alone.","sonnet":"A person severs all ties — burning their possessions and past — to escape some unnamed guilt or corruption, seeking spiritual freedom in total self-destruction and isolation, arriving utterly alone after having once held people's trust.","src":"sonnet"},
"king-satan~psychosadomasochism":{"haiku":"Neurochemistry drives cycles of pain-seeking and pleasure in relationships; the deeper issue is believing narratives that justify mutual harm.","opus":"A collision of brain chemistry and dark ritual, reeling off serotonin, dopamine, and endorphins while beseeching an infernal master to inflict pain, and insisting that reality is merely the story we agree to tell. King Satan are a Finnish industrial and satanic-metal act, and the track drives their occult electronics into a chant-heavy exploration of suffering as devotion and the self dissolving into pure reaction.","sonnet":"Pain and love collapsed into each other through the lens of neurochemistry — dopamine, serotonin, adrenalin framed as the real machinery behind devotion and suffering. The song argues that the ones you hurt are the ones you love precisely because that's the only dynamic that produces a reaction, and that self and body are separate enough that the body can absorb punishment the self doesn't feel. The premise is offered as both liberation and diagnosis.","src":"haiku"},
"kingdom-of-giants~bleach":{"opus":"A desire to erase painful memories of a person and start over, bleaching away the colors and letters of the past; torn between wanting rest and self-destruction (drowning in bleach), the singer admits he's not over it and is here for revenge.","sonnet":"Someone is trying to bleach away memories of a relationship that destroyed their sense of self — they are too low to begin being themselves again, and despite wanting peace they are not over it and want revenge, drowning in the erasure.","src":"sonnet"},
"kingdom-of-giants~blue-dream":{"haiku":"Two people trapped in a relationship built on nothing, playing victim instead of honest. Miscommunication hardens into walls too thin to bear the weight of their own pain.","opus":"A haunted post-mortem of a relationship built on nothing, walls raised out of thin air that won't stop talking, a cancer in the head telling the narrator to stay quiet while he admits playing the victim even knowing the fault is his. Loneliness is only half of it. Kingdom of Giants are a California metalcore band, and this pairs their modern, atmospheric heaviness with clean melodic hooks to voice a self-aware spiral through guilt and haunting.","sonnet":"A relationship that built its own prison — walls constructed from nothing that became real enough to trap both people inside. The speaker admits he plays the victim while knowing he created the problem, inflating nothing into crises and leaving nothing to learn from. The image that lands hardest is the light left on and the person who never came home, the house now populated only by love's ghost and walls thin enough to hear through.","src":"haiku"},
"kingdom-of-giants~damaged-goods":{"haiku":"Shared grief and trauma bind two young men together as they witness each other's pain without the tools to heal, watching life drain away in helpless silence.","opus":"Two wounded youths joined by mutual mourning as the people they love keep dying, one shouldering the hurt for both of them and swearing to toughen up rather than crumble beneath its weight.","sonnet":"A young man watches everyone he loves die around him and tries to hold together his own grief and a friend's, absorbing the pain for both of them while tears rise to distressed eyes — too young to understand, old enough to hurt, refusing to go blind.","src":"haiku"},
"kingdom-of-giants~side-effect":{"opus":"A struggle with mental strain and overmedication, the narrator dragged down by shadows and inner screaming, fighting toward clarity through insanity, and criticizing a pill-pushing culture that leaves people feeling too little or too much.","sonnet":"About dissociation caused by over-medication and emotional numbness — the narrator feels unmoored from the ground, levitating away from reality, and questions a pharmaceutical culture that makes people feel too little or too much while obscuring who to trust.","src":"sonnet"},
"kingdom-of-giants~sync":{"opus":"A metalcore reckoning with guilt, regret, and the dread of becoming the very person one despised. The narrator confronts a devil's bargain and the shame of turning into another, questioning whether change comes too late once the light is gone. Yet through the ruin flickers a stubborn vow to find a way forward.","sonnet":"A song about moral compromise and the slow realization of becoming someone you despise — the narrator made a deal with something dark, turned into the very person who hurt him, and now spends the song asking whether redemption is still possible after the light has gone. The emotional core is shame-soaked self-examination rather than confession.","src":"sonnet"},
"kingdom-of-giants~two-suns":{"haiku":"Independence achieved through hardship and survival, rejecting others' attempts at control while carrying the weight of past struggles.","opus":"California metalcore band Kingdom of Giants weld crushing riffs to melodic defiance in this anthem of hard-earned independence, the narrator refusing to be idolized and insisting he'll succeed by his own effort for as long as he lives. Weighed down by the burden of his past and cautious of overreaching, he grounds the song in real hardship, a voicemail and memories of poverty anchoring his vow to earn everything the difficult way.","sonnet":"Self-reliance forced into existence by fair-weather people — the speaker draws a hard line between those who would eat with you now and those who would have starved with you then. The song carries the specific bitterness of someone who hustled through food stamps and sleepless nights for a music career while others talked, and who now refuses both pedestals and help from people who weren't there. Flying too close to the sun is the risk taken anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"kingdom-of-giants~wasted-space":{"haiku":"Existence as waste; life stretches toward an inevitable void with no purchase or meaning.","opus":"The suffocation of a love turned dead weight, the narrator having given everything only to find it wasn't enough. The metalcore band frames a second heartbreak as an inability to breathe inside another person, taking the slow route back to salvage a soul he already suspects is the real source of the wreckage.","sonnet":"A relationship recognized as the source of mutual damage — I can't breathe, I can't stay inside your wasted space — the speaker has given everything twice over, runs the long way home to center themselves, crashes and burns, and finally names himself as the source of all their mayhem too.","src":"haiku"},
"kingdom-of-giants~wayfinder":{"haiku":"Passengers drift without meaning, searching for identity; life is finite and fleeting, leaving the speaker questioning if change is possible or too late.","opus":"The California metalcore band build an existential highway song about drifting without direction. In too far and sleepless, the narrator questions whether we grope in the dark for identity while chosen routes lead us onward. Existence is brief and easily missed. He fears having stripped himself of any deeper meaning, reduced to mere biology and temperament, dreading it may be beyond fixing.","sonnet":"Adrift on sleepless roads and dissolving sense of self, a person wrestles with whether the journey has a destination or whether humans are simply passengers carried wherever circumstance leads. Life flashes past so quickly that the question of who he is may never be answered in time.","src":"haiku"},
"kings-of-leon~closer":{"haiku":"Obsessive desire and supernatural dread intertwine as a lover pursues with unstoppable force, drawing their target into darkness.","opus":"A vampire's love song, sung from the predator's side: eternity as exhaustion, the ache of an immortal who drains what he loves and can never rest. Southern-gothic dread stretched to cosmic scale, with something closing in that he can't outrun.","sonnet":"A speaker stranded in a desolate town, emotionally gutted by someone who took everything, runs under a moon while a storm visibly approaches. The coming closeness feels as much like doom as it does reunion, charged with feral obsession and exhaustion.","src":"haiku"},
"kings-of-leon~supersoaker":{"haiku":"Wanderlust and emotional fragmentation drive the narrator away from relationships; they move through life leaving a wake of superficial connections.","opus":"Ambivalence about America and their own arrival, dressed as swagger. Behind the flag-waving strut is a man who sees through every smile and can't locate home or himself, blowing everyone away while quietly walking off.","sonnet":"A restless, displaced narrator channels a half-acknowledged sexuality and a sense of not belonging anywhere into a bravado-soaked persona, moving through smiles he doesn't trust and suggesting that his desire is the one authentic thing left in a life without a fixed home.","src":"haiku"},
"kings-of-leon~the-bucket":{"haiku":"Youthful arrogance and deteriorating fate collide; the narrator reflects on aging, fame's toxicity, and the pact between friends as life's promises corrode.","opus":"Being a young star cashing in before the shine fades, terrified of both dying too soon and getting old. The band, barely out of adolescence and suddenly idolized, sizes up their own doomed shelf life with dark humor.","sonnet":"A rambling, bravado-laced portrait of rock-star disorientation: the narrator arrives somewhere new, feeds off the adulation, banters about aging and mortality, and makes a loose pact with someone to keep going before everything fades.","src":"haiku"},
"kingsmen~nightmare":{"haiku":"The speaker is trapped in repeating nightmares and frustration, cursed and sick while reality and dream merge into torment.","opus":"The deathcore band lock their narrator in recurring nightmares that seep into waking life, smothering his own anguish. A nod to Oedipus and imagery of self-blinding turn private agony into something mythic, life's rewards tasting like nothing. An illness warps him as he hunts fruitlessly for a cure and finally welcomes his own collapse, presenting his madness as reality itself.","sonnet":"Recurring nightmares have merged with waking reality until the distinction dissolves. Self-torment circles back on itself — frustration buried in the ground, eyes the speaker would rather tear out — and the Oedipal reference frames the horror as something fated, not merely psychological.","src":"haiku"},
"kinokoteikoku~a-139jznn":{"opus":"Kinokoteikoku made their name on shoegaze-tinged Japanese rock where tenderness and violence sit uncomfortably close, and this song lives on that edge. On a rainy spring night in 2009 the narrator fantasizes obsessively about killing someone with a metal bat, pure malice held up as the only truth — but by 3 a.m. the anger bleeds into exhaustion and all of life turns unbearably tedious. Beneath the rage, the real dream turns out to be simply connecting with another heart.","sonnet":"On a rainy spring night in 2009, the narrator fantasized obsessively about killing someone with a metal bat — pure malice as the only truth — but by 3 a.m. the anger bleeds into exhaustion and all of life feels unbearably tedious, the real dream being simply to connect with another heart.","src":"sonnet"},
"kinokoteikoku~a-13nn0ok":{"opus":"Kinokoteikoku's quieter register suits this study of love that ends before it can begin. Carrying flowers to the sea for a first and last goodbye, the narrator confesses they always fall hardest for the things they can never have. The illusion is not raged against but acknowledged and released, with quiet repeated apologies — the band's dreamlike softness turning what could be bitter into something closer to gentle resignation, mourning a love that was never going to be held.","sonnet":"A song about a love that the narrator already knows is over before it could truly begin: they carry flowers to the sea for a first and last goodbye, confessing that they always fall hardest for the things they can never have — the illusion is acknowledged and released with quiet, repeated apologies.","src":"sonnet"},
"kinokoteikoku~a-1aso2ym":{"opus":"Kinokoteikoku often write from adolescence's suppressed interior, and this song sits a school-aged person between two selves: the violent one crammed into their pocket and the lies they tell when asked if they're happy. Watching fish through aquarium glass, they search for the meaning of life while holding everything down, unable to be free or ignorant. The band's hazy sound frames it as ordinary endurance — just living and searching, the search itself the only available answer.","sonnet":"A school-aged person caught between the violent self crammed into their pocket and the lies they tell when asked if they're happy — watching fish through aquarium glass, searching for the meaning of life while suppressing everything, unable to be free or ignorant, just living and searching.","src":"sonnet"},
"kinokoteikoku~a-1dqtmzl":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"Kinoko Teikoku built their early sound on shoegaze haze bleeding into confessional folk, and here that fog becomes the medium of the death-wish itself. Vocalist Chiaki Sato delivers the drift toward disappearance not as protest but as exhaustion, the social smiles and flat soda rendered in a hush that makes fading feel almost gentle. The loneliness stays quietest exactly where company is most wanted.","sonnet":"A quiet suicidal ideation — wanting to fade like a drying hydrangea, suffocated by social smiles and trivial conversation, half-wishing to experience death once knowing there'd be no second chance, floating in flat soda with no one beside them despite wanting someone there.","src":"haiku"},
"kinokoteikoku~a-1mft0es":{"haiku":"A meditation on psychic injury that won't heal—frozen in a past moment, each remark reopening old wounds.","opus":"Grounding a wound in Tokyo geography is a Kinoko Teikoku signature, and naming Route 246 anchors the frozen foot to a real overpass where memory ambushes the ordinary. The band's literary bent turns a three-year-old sentence into a body that keeps its own calendar, permanently stalled in January. The cityscape frames an injury that refuses the healing the passing crowd assumes.","sonnet":"A person standing on a pedestrian bridge over Route 246 in Tokyo watches a girl with a red umbrella below, and can't shake the pain in their right foot — which feels permanently frozen in January, the month something was said to them three years ago that still wounds. The structure suggests the physical pain is a body memory of a relationship's end, and the Tokyo cityscape becomes a frame around an injury that refuses to heal.","src":"haiku"},
"kinokoteikoku~a-3r0gw":{"haiku":"Existential despair: humans survive by killing, suffer through living, yet remain drawn to the uncertain warmth of existence.","opus":"Kinoko Teikoku's guitars swell toward catharsis even as the words refuse consolation, and that tension carries this meditation on life sustained only through killing. The band lets weeping under the stars ride a rising tide of noise, so the paradox lands as felt rather than argued. Being alive stays both unbearable and irresistible, the whole mass of it still tilting toward the sun.","sonnet":"Living creatures who can only survive by killing are weeping under the stars, tormented by the fact of being alive yet still lured on by the fragile warmth of joy — the song circles the paradox of biological existence as both suffering and irresistible continuation, aiming always toward the sun.","src":"haiku"},
"kinokoteikoku~a-r2rc9q":{"haiku":"An enigmatic lullaby of escape and forgetting—abandoning home, refusing to wake, chasing an unknown eureka moment.","opus":"By this point Kinoko Teikoku had sanded their shoegaze roughness toward wider pop, and the polish suits an invitation to abandon everything and sail into an unwaking tomorrow. Sato's voice makes the secret signal called Eureka feel less like escape than a shared vow whispered against a faded world. The hope is fragile and insistent at once, two people betting that going on together will find something luminous.","sonnet":"An invitation to abandon everything and sail to an unknown tomorrow — the narrator urges someone to leave behind any reason to return, keep dreaming without waking, and follow a secret signal called 'Eureka' that the other person once shared. The emotional core is the fragile, insistent hope that two people can find something luminous together in a faded, empty world if they just keep going.","src":"haiku"},
"kinokoteikoku~a-z1kjee":{"opus":"Kinoko Teikoku often set heartbreak in specific seasons, and this summer-night loop uses the season's opening as the cruelest possible timing. The drunk wandering past familiar streets, half-hoping for a collision, curdles the moment the ex appears with someone new. The band renders the rumor and the staring without flinching, leaving the narrator stuck in a circuit they already know leads nowhere.","sonnet":"A summer night song about obsessing over someone who has moved on: the narrator wanders drunk past the same old streets imagining they'll bump into this person, but catches sight of them with someone new at the summer's opening — the person is rumored about and stared at, both mocked and desired, and the narrator is stuck in a loop they know is pointless.","src":"sonnet"},
"kinokoteikoku~thanatos":{"haiku":"Solitude amid ephemeral beauty carries the weight of incomprehensible loss; guidance persists despite the speaker's inability to perceive it clearly.","opus":"Watching petals scatter alone, the speaker keeps playing out a losing game with the weak cards they were dealt. Even so, a crying voice urges them to press on without hesitation, and the upside-down light that first cried out finally lights the path forward.","sonnet":"The speaker watches petals drift alone, stuck in a game dealt with weak cards, wanting to kick down the door to tomorrow but held forward by a weeping voice urging them on; a faceless shadow waves goodbye and dissolves into the wind, while inverted light finally illuminates the path.","src":"haiku"},
"kinoue64~who-are-you":{"haiku":"Identity fractures under scrutiny; holding breath suspends becoming until breaking point. Suffocation arrives incrementally, each moment tightening the rope.","opus":"A quiet Japanese meditation on dissolving identity, the narrator confessing he can no longer recognize himself. Holding his breath as if to take flight, he waits within a suffering that intensifies by degrees, a hushed drift toward vanishing where regret and self-estrangement blur into a single fading question.","sonnet":"The speaker questions who the other person is, then loses track of their own identity — holding their breath while waiting to fly away, the victim of all accumulated regret — and discovers that suffocation comes easily and grows, gradually and inexorably.","src":"haiku"},
"kissing-candice~crash-and-burn":{"opus":"A portrait of sleeplessness, runaway intrusive thoughts and self-disgust, gasping and going under beneath a mind that won't quiet, dreading rest because horrors lie in wait. Against it the narrator clings to a stubborn creed of falling apart and rising again anyway.","sonnet":"A song about being haunted by regret and a relentless inner critic — voices in the head won't stop, motivation has vanished, and a dark inner presence promises never to leave no matter where the narrator flees, even into sleep or dreams.","src":"sonnet"},
"kittie~brackish":{"haiku":"Emotional betrayal corrodes trust; the speaker sinks beneath the weight of inherited wounds.","opus":"A girl leaning on someone who can't save her, told by a friend who sees the self-harm and the toxic dependence and rages at the useless man cast as her comfort. Teenage female fury aimed at how women are taught to endure.","sonnet":"A young woman who has been conditioned to endure pain and look to a man for strength finally turns inward, recognising that his guidance is hollow and she would rather face the burning alone than keep leaning on someone who cannot save her.","src":"haiku"},
"kitty-purrnaz~little-secret":{"haiku":"Desire consumes with violent intensity while the speaker battles self-awareness of their own monstrosity; emotional hunger and shame coexist in restless loops.","opus":"Wanting to be someone's kept secret, desperate for reassurance that a lover won't leave amid silence and unspoken thoughts. The confession of being a monster who tears the beloved into pieces reveals a self-loathing that shadows the plea to come closer.","sonnet":"Caught in an anxious standoff with a lover who won't signal whether they're staying or leaving, the speaker swings between pleading for clarity and dark romantic longing — \"I just wanna bite you\" — while confessing to a monstrous neediness they can't undo.","src":"haiku"},
"kitty~last-minute":{"opus":"A dizzying breakup lament tangled with self-destructive imagery, addressing an ex as both poison and a hanging rope, oscillating between missing them to death and urging them to finally let her go.","sonnet":"A chaotic, self-aware post-breakup spiral: the narrator knows she is toxic and destructive in relationships, frames herself as a cyclone and her ex as composed and better off, yet cannot stop the obsessive loops of thought — acknowledging the loss while conceding she probably would have done the same.","src":"sonnet"},
"klone~breach":{"opus":"A call to tune out the fear-mongering and clamor of a rule-bound society and be guided instead by your own inner spark. Those in power, painted as ravenous scavengers, feel nothing for you, so any strength has to be summoned from within.","sonnet":"An encouragement to ignore external noise and authority and follow your own inner light — the world is full of confusion, scavengers, and people who wrote rules to keep you contained, but none of it has to define you.","src":"sonnet"},
"klone~fog":{"opus":"A liberating song about shrugging off the judgment of others, voicing your forbidden, private wishes and letting the crowd's disapproving chatter blur into a haze that separates you from them, releasing you from any duty to justify yourself.","sonnet":"About being branded deviant or insane for having forbidden desires or unconventional beliefs — the narrator no longer feels the need to explain or justify themselves to people who call them impure, letting the words dissolve into fog between them.","src":"sonnet"},
"klone~keystone":{"opus":"A tender song of grief and enduring love for someone who has died, the narrator feeling their presence in the morning light, longing to join them one day, and vowing to preserve their legacy and memory until his own death.","sonnet":"A song of devotion to someone who appears only in dreams — the narrator forgets all pain when this person rises in sleep, dedicates the rest of life to preserving their shared memory, and promises to join them someday beyond waking.","src":"sonnet"},
"klone~sealed":{"opus":"A portrait of confinement, its subject penned in like a bird behind bars and yearning to break loose, feel a breeze on the skin, and claim a corner of the world to call home. Despite not knowing when the captivity ends, they cling to their dreams and keep struggling.","sonnet":"About someone imprisoned — literally or within a suffocating life — who survives by clinging to dreams and memories of freedom, fighting to reclaim a place they can call their own while time and identity slowly erode.","src":"sonnet"},
"klone~within-reach-edit":{"haiku":"Hope becomes actionable in the present moment despite systemic collapse; resistance lies in the daily refusal to accept imposed futility.","opus":"An urgent call to grab hope while it can still be reached, framed as peaceful resistance against a world reaping what it sowed. Klone balances dread of an approaching end against the calm of first light, insisting there's no time to waste and that change sits close enough to touch.","sonnet":"An exhortation toward peaceful resistance and change: those who reap what they sow are forgetting the end is near, while the speaker urges that transformation — 'today it's within reach' — requires walking forward and trying again regardless of loss.","src":"haiku"},
"klone~yonder":{"opus":"A luminous surrender to transcendence, imagining two souls slipping free of the flesh together. The French prog outfit Klone, long fascinated by atmosphere over aggression, frames death not as loss but as a shared crossing into open sky, where closeness deepens precisely as the physical world falls away and light is passed forward like a flame.","sonnet":"A transcendence fantasy for two people — leaving the human world behind into a limitless blue expanse, floating outside their bodies, carrying light through grey skies and growing closer to each other precisely because they have left everything else behind.","src":"sonnet"},
"kmac2021~butterfly":{"haiku":"Someone freed another from romantic delusion by offering light and wings; they awakened to appreciate the world and the simple gift of being alive.","opus":"YouTuber-turned-musician kMac2021 marks a shift toward gratitude, making peace with the singularity of one lifetime and shaking off the sorrow of having only one existence to live. Someone reached out and lifted him, sunlight on his skin as he starts over. The butterfly stands for transformation and release, each new day an opening to change, wasted longing giving way to clear-eyed appreciation.","sonnet":"Gratitude for being opened to the present after years of romanticizing an unlived life. Another person broke the paralysis of endless wishing — and now sunlight, breeze, and each passing morning register as gifts rather than disappointments. The transformation is named simply: wings given, freedom found.","src":"haiku"},
"kmac2021~crisis":{"haiku":"The speaker describes internal crisis as simultaneously suffocating and the fuel for creative survival and expression.","opus":"A stark account of depression and self-harm reclaimed as creative survival, the narrator adrift in a joyless dawn inside four walls, hollowed out, who turns the act of cutting into a grim metaphor for bleeding words onto the page before despair drowns him. kmac2021 works in dark, confessional alternative hip-hop, channeling that raw introspection into a ritual of converting pain into art to keep going.","sonnet":"A person hollow and drowning in four walls — rain through the window, sheets suffocating, a head full of darkness — finds the only way through is to transform pain into creative output. The blade into skin becomes ink that becomes color that becomes shape: a reminder that the drive to make something is the thing that keeps you from going under. The song is a crisis mapped as a creative act, survival reframed as the will to surface before suffocating.","src":"haiku"},
"kmac2021~idol-hands":{"haiku":"An online creator turns parasocial fame into a mirror of self-loathing — idle hands and empty identity collide as the speaker recognises there is nothing beneath the screen persona, only wasted time and the devil's work.","opus":"A self-lacerating portrait of the online creator as a hollowed-out image, the parasocial pariah crushed under a crown he never wanted. The mask slips to reveal wasted time and a suspicion of emptiness within, idleness curdling into rot as the narrator asks whether anyone sees a person behind the screen.","sonnet":"The parasocial trap from the inside: a creator sees themselves as just another stranger's face on a screen, the mask slipped to reveal nothing within — idle hands breaking down until there's nothing left, doubt killing the fire, reclusion and wasted time the only remaining companions.","src":"haiku"},
"kmac2021~imposter":{"haiku":"Deep impostor syndrome and self-doubt plague someone who feels undeserving and fears exposure, ultimately believing they don't belong in the world at all.","opus":"Rising from YouTube metal into heavier territory, kmac2021 dissects impostor syndrome head-on. A quarter-century in, he feels like a fraud wearing a borrowed life, sure his praise is unearned and that any moment his flaws will be exposed. Convinced he exists only to suffer, he decides to torch what he has just to feel something.","sonnet":"Twenty-five years of feeling like a fraudulent tenant in the wrong body and the wrong life: the spotlight scalds rather than warms, and the mirror returns a stranger. He concludes that someone else deserves his place, and invites the world to erase him and fill the vacancy.","src":"haiku"},
"kmfdm~free-your-hate":{"haiku":"A call to revolutionary action against tyranny and oppression, urging people to cast off chains and reclaim power from brutal authoritarians who rule through violence and imprisonment.","opus":"Post-9/11 industrial protest against the war-on-terror machinery of indefinite detention and torture, sampling revolutionary voices to urge people to turn their fear back into defiant rage and break the tyrant's grip.","sonnet":"The song calls for uprising against authoritarian power — citing torture, detention without trial, wars that kill soldiers, and the suppression of freedom — and splices in sampled speeches from prisoners and political detainees to frame revolt as the only rational response.","src":"haiku"},
"kmfdm~hy-na":{"haiku":"Predatory hunger and social exile merge as the speaker embodies the scavenging hyena—reviled, unshunned, yet powerful—feeding on human wretchedness while condemned by a world that bred the beast.","opus":"A grotesque monologue delivered by the hyena as a symbol of everything squalid in humanity, feeding on carcasses across a plague-ridden Eden governed by weaponry. The industrial veterans wallow in the debased, a despised beast who converts dying into triumph and prowls wastelands leaving ruin behind, its supposed offering laced with poison.","sonnet":"A self-described hyena prowls a wasteland of human wretchedness, feeding on misery and condemned by god-like forces to roam and devastate — the creature revels in its outcast status, its laughter through the night its only victory.","src":"haiku"},
"kmfdm~megalomaniac":{"haiku":"Anarchic revolution celebrates chaos, transgression, and relentless provocation; the speaker embodies destructive force weaponized against complacency and mediocrity.","opus":"A knowing, self-aggrandizing anthem in which the band anoints itself the swaggering villain of the underground, louder and superior to everyone else. It jeers at a culture drowning in buzz and blandness while reveling in its own excess and appetite for provocation.","sonnet":"A maximalist self-mythologising: KMFDM frames itself as counter-culture from the underground, better than the best and harder than the rest, with terrorism and sabotage as trade, wrapped in Nietzschean bombast — a wolf in sheep's clothing exploding in your face, celebrating its own relentless menace.","src":"haiku"},
"kmfdm~salvation":{"haiku":"The speaker seeks transcendent belief and immortal transformation, abandoning all safety to achieve spiritual redemption.","opus":"A pounding demand for rescue and transcendence, the narrator hungry to sense and grasp the miraculous, then flinging himself past every safeguard into weightless surrender, howling for deliverance, immortality and love as life's whole design. KMFDM are the German industrial-rock veterans, and this rides their trademark sloganeering chants, half earnest spiritual cry, half tongue-in-cheek anthem.","sonnet":"Standing at the point of no return — bridges burned, eyes shut, falling — and calling out for salvation not as passive plea but as demand. The speaker wants to believe in something unbelievable, wants to feel it physically, and the chorus delivers the request as a chant aimed at destiny and immortality. The closing insistence that everybody needs love grounds the bombast in something unexpectedly simple and human.","src":"haiku"},
"kmfdm~wwiii":{"haiku":"Total ideological and existential war cascades across all institutions and relationships; the speaker declares war on everything as the only honest response to complicity.","opus":"A scorched-earth Bush-era protest, declaring war on war itself and everything the band reads as machinery of hollow empire, corporate and media alike. The industrial band weaponizes an Army slogan to mock the whole apparatus of consent.","sonnet":"A total declaration of war against every institution, ideology, and media apparatus that controls and deadens modern life — governments, corporations, religion, pop culture, and militarism — calling for a global uprising of the disillusioned.","src":"haiku"},
"kmfdm~you-re-no-good":{"haiku":"A deeply damaged speaker recognizes their own toxicity and inevitably destructive influence on anyone who tries to love them.","opus":"A voice steeped in self-loathing that pushes everyone away before it can ruin them, half warning and half boast about being the walking source of misery. The refrain lands as someone deciding isolation is the only honest option.","sonnet":"A self-condemning internal monologue in which the narrator catalogues their own toxicity — paranoia, misery, social destruction — and decides the safest choice is isolation. Misery follows so reliably it becomes almost an identity.","src":"haiku"},
"kneecap~get-your-brits-out":{"haiku":"Working-class Irish life and political identity circle through intoxication and hedonism while British occupation and institutional racism persist underneath. Party and politics refuse separation.","opus":"A drug-fuelled Belfast night out satirizing sectarian politics, the Irish-rapping narrators imagining DUP unionists getting off their heads on the same pills and dropping their bigotry for a laugh.","sonnet":"A satirical Irish-language rap fantasy in which Kneecap, harassed by the DUP and unionist politicians, doses those same politicians at a night out — Arlene, Donaldson, Stalford — watching them lose their filters, end up chinned on a sofa, and expose their hypocrisies, all while the phrase \"brits out\" oscillates between political demand and the chaos of a massive night.","src":"haiku"},
"kneecap~h-o-o-d":{"haiku":"A violent street gangster lives through jail, robbery, and assault in a poor urban neighborhood; he's known for brutality and survives by intimidation and force.","opus":"The Belfast trio rap in Irish and English through a chaotic day of drink, drugs, brawls and run-ins with the police, reclaiming the low-life scum label hurled at them. Republican slogans, off-licence runs and boxing threats collide with dark comedy and genuine grievance about jobs and curfews. The bilingual hood anthem turns marginalised Northern Irish youth culture into defiant, provocative self-portrait, humour inseparable from real political edge.","sonnet":"A Belfast street character ricochets through a single night of drinking, fighting, and police custody, narrated in Irish and English with equal swagger. The hood label worn as identity rather than shame — low-life status reclaimed as a badge against a system that was never his to begin with.","src":"haiku"},
"kneecap~say-nara":{"haiku":"Dublin nightlife hedonism and farewell, charting movement from local anonymity toward wider recognition.","opus":"The Belfast trio marking their surreal jump from playing to thirty people to sold-out chaos, half-boasting and half-uneasy about fame's cost. Sung across Irish and English, it's a defiant toast that the tide has turned while they stay the same lads.","sonnet":"Two voices — one in Irish/English, one in a verse-rap style — reflect on the dizzying rise of a band from playing to thirty people to sold-out shows, celebrating the moment while acknowledging the tide always turns; the refrain 'athraíonn an taoide' (the tide changes) and 'I'm not sure when I'll see ya' give the exuberance a bittersweet, impermanent edge.","src":"haiku"},
"kneecap~sayōnara":{"haiku":"Transition and impermanence mark a life lived fully in the moment, where dancing persists through all upheaval. Tides change but joy sustains, even as departure arrives unannounced.","src":"haiku"},
"kneecap~the-recap":{"haiku":"Political resistance and working-class defiance mock empty power; the speaker reclaims stolen dignity from corrupt authorities.","opus":"A defiant Irish-republican victory lap taunting Tory leader Kemi Badenoch after electoral defeat, mixing Irish and English to demand reparations and mock her fading career. West Belfast bravado closes with Free Palestine—politics as swaggering, bilingual comeuppance.","sonnet":"A bilingual Irish-English rap recap of Kneecap's confrontations with the British government — the group celebrates surviving political attacks, mocks Conservative politicians by name, frames their payments as reparations, and reasserts their identity as West Belfast artists who took on the establishment and came out ahead.","src":"haiku"},
"knife-bride~fang-dummy":{"haiku":"Predatory seduction masks genuine connection; the hunter poses as a friend while draining the prey.","opus":"A predatory seduction played for camp menace, the narrator baring fangs and pretending affection while circling prey like a piranha. Desire and cruelty fuse into a knowing performance, sweetness and violence indistinguishable, the whole flirtation staged as pretend even as the trap quietly closes.","sonnet":"Predatory seduction performed with full self-awareness: the speaker does the dance everyone does but with fangs out, calls herself a sucker and means it both ways, and the piranha in your water line makes clear the target will regret letting her in even as she admits she's twisted and doesn't know where to begin.","src":"haiku"},
"knife-bride~permanent-s-m-i-l-e":{"haiku":"Forced performance and mask-wearing trap the speaker in permanent false happiness.","opus":"A horror-theatrical fantasy of possessive violence, the narrator carving a fixed smile into a captive lover while insisting it is a gift. Grief and mental unraveling twist into the logic of a killer who cannot let go, tenderness and torture collapsed into one gleaming, unbearable act of control.","sonnet":"A horror-register story of surgical coercion — a captor holds someone down and cuts a permanent smile into their face, narrating the procedure with clinical tenderness (\"I just gave you a permanent smile\", \"so precious are you in a harness\"). The victim is powerless and the perpetrator's logic is self-serving throughout; the knife is both literal and a figure for any relationship that demands compliance as the price of being kept.","src":"haiku","fable":"A torture-romance monologue sung entirely from the knife-hand, where every mutilation is relabelled as devotion. Carving a mouth wide becomes the gift of a grin that can never fade, and the victim's screaming meets genuine bafflement, as if weeping were the ungrateful reply to a favour. The real cruelty is grammatical: the captor keeps stealing the pronoun of suffering, so his questions about how he will grieve and cope crowd out the person actually strapped to the table, and even his own claimed madness is spun as damage inflicted on him. That is the trap Knife Bride stage — abuser-logic rendered as body horror, the cooing tenderness over a captive's frailty and the surgical violence issuing from one voice with no seam, because to him they are identical. The closing turn drops the flirtation for the ordinary machinery of a killing dressed as tucking someone in, the album title returned as a lullaby: the monstrousness was always wearing the face of care.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"mutilation relabelled as devotion, cooing from one voice with no seam","built":"the captor keeps stealing the pronoun of suffering to crowd out the victim"},
"knife-party~begin-again":{"haiku":"Cycles of work and death repeat; at each ending, people begin anew, colliding on the same worn path of existence.","opus":"Knife Party frame existence as an endless loop, hands raised to begin again each time the end is reached, a cycle of living, working and dying for a path fiercely defended. The Pendulum-spawned electro-house duo strips a big-room drop down to fatalistic mantra, repeating the ritual of restart until the collision with the end becomes indistinguishable from beginning.","sonnet":"Reaching the end is not a terminus but a signal to raise hands and start over — life is a cycle of working, dying for something, and restarting at the limit. The question of whether there is another way to feel dissolves into the insistence that the path is known and worth the collision. Ending and beginning are the same motion.","src":"haiku"},
"knife-party~boss-mode":{"haiku":"Cosmic imagery meets defensive aggression—planets colliding with stars and light, then pivoting to police calls and threats of self-protection.","opus":"Rob Swire and Gareth McGrillen build a festival-floor collage where cosmic romance collides with sirens and menace. It is dance music as adrenaline dump, meaning subordinated entirely to the drop.","sonnet":"The lyrics are too sparse to carry a real throughline — a few images of cosmic collision and a switch to police-call samples; the song functions as pure sonic spectacle with no sustained theme.","src":"haiku"},
"knife-party~d-i-m-h":{"haiku":"House music is celebrated as a spiritual force that freed souls, brought people together in joy, and created community at three in the morning.","opus":"A mock-scriptural origin myth casts house music as a light that freed souls, raising hands at three in the morning to sing of love, peace, and happiness. Knife Party, the harder EDM offshoot of Rob Swire and Gareth McGrillen, frame the dancefloor as gospel, distilling club euphoria to the single ecstatic claim that the dance lives in the heart.","sonnet":"House music is remembered as a spiritual force — a groove that freed souls, raised hands, and held communities together through the long hours before dawn. The declaration that the dance lives inside the heart frames it not as entertainment but as something closer to belief.","src":"haiku"},
"knife-party~edm-death-machine":{"haiku":"In a dystopian future, music, internet culture, and humanity itself disappear, replaced by robots and extinction. Electronic music and modern escapism end as the human race dies out.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek dystopia mocking dance-music fads (dropping the bass, the Harlem Shake) as a doomed, disposable culture; the deeper joke is self-aware satire of EDM's own hollow trends, imagining a future where the human race is extinct and only robots remain.","sonnet":"A satirical dystopia in which every cliché of 2010s EDM culture — the bass drop, the Harlem Shake, internet friends — has been erased by robots who have inherited a world wiped clean of human stupidity, framed as darkly comic prophecy.","src":"haiku"},
"knife-party~edm-trend-machine":{"haiku":"Against overwhelming odds, two people support each other to transform nothing into something valuable through shared determination.","opus":"Beneath the ironic title sits a straightforwardly uplifting drop, two people turning nothing into something by facing disaster together. The sentiment is generic on purpose, a knowing nod to the festival formula the duo is riding.","sonnet":"Straightforward earnest uplift — against all the weight and disaster, two people fighting together have turned nothing into something good, and the narrator celebrates that transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"knife-party~give-it-up":{"haiku":"A celebration of summer and dancing where dancers are encouraged to loosen up and show physical confidence on the dance floor.","opus":"A Jamaican-patois summer dancehall hype track urging women to tone up their bodies and give it up on the dancefloor, dropping low and dancing rather than acting old. It's pure party instruction, celebrating physicality and letting loose with no theme beyond the dance.","sonnet":"A Jamaican dancehall-flavored call for women to exercise and work their bodies on the dancefloor — entirely about the physical energy of summer clubbing, toned physiques, and dancing low.","src":"haiku"},
"knife-party~internet-friends-original-mix":{"haiku":"An obsessive person, rejected and blocked online, spirals into dark fantasies and threats against the person who rejected them, illustrating toxic digital parasocialism.","opus":"A spoken monologue in the voice of an obsessive online stalker whose suffocating sense of entitlement sours into jealous fury and a death threat the instant they feel spurned. The absurdly petty spark, a social-media rejection escalating to lethal menace, skewers how digital closeness can breed possessive, unhinged fixation. Knife Party pair the creepy voiceover with a savage electro-house drop.","sonnet":"A stalker's unhinged internal monologue directed at someone who blocked them online, spiraling from wounded possessiveness and jealous rage into a death threat — the mundane social-media grievance played completely straight makes it more disturbing.","src":"haiku"},
"knife-party~kaleidoscope":{"opus":"A hedonistic invitation to one final party as everything ends, offering salvation out on the dancefloor while the world runs out.","sonnet":"A minimal song about last-chance salvation — someone offers to save another person at what feels like the end of the world, the final gathering before everything collapses.","src":"sonnet"},
"knife-party~rage-valley":{"haiku":"Electronic percussion and synthesizer sequences dominate; onomatopoeia and pure sound rhythm are the subject.","src":"haiku"},
"knife-party~reconnect":{"haiku":"The philosophical possibility of renewal and transformation through disaster—shipwrecks and failures offer chances to begin anew and rebuild upon past mistakes.","opus":"A spoken-word overture framing collapse as opportunity, fortunes reversing in an instant and new life rising from ruin, delivered with tongue-in-cheek grandeur. The electronic duo set up their story as a knight's quest through wreckage.","sonnet":"A brief spoken-word intro framing collapse as opportunity — shipwrecks clear the ground for rebuilding, death precedes life — setting up the album's narrative as a reinvention story.","src":"haiku"},
"knifes~the-comedown":{"haiku":"Chemical or emotional crash induces alienation and self-deception; facing oneself and others proves unbearable after the drug's false clarity vanishes.","opus":"The grim aftermath of excess, a narrator face-down in the mud trying to unremind himself of secrets that betray him. Set against a hollow Hollywood night, it captures the dependency of others and the slow climb back toward clear sight, the comedown as a place where everyone eventually passes through.","sonnet":"A portrait of being face-down in the mud again after a night in Hollywood — unclear whether the crash is chemical, emotional, or relational. A demanding woman needs constant company or she'll go out of her mind; the speaker opts out, promising only to pass her on the comedown, on her way back down through here.","src":"haiku"},
"knives~the-dagger":{"haiku":"Aspiration and ambition collapse under scrutiny; power reverses and falls like autumn leaves stripped from their branches.","opus":"A tragic figure cheered even as he falls, his ruin staged as scripted spectacle where the wild card's collapse is the entertainment. Threading Eden's deceit and a chess-piece hierarchy, it's about a man losing his hold on a double-edged life he can never quite see whole.","sonnet":"A troubled man — a wild card, a gambler — falls apart under the weight of aspirations that branch and collapse like autumn leaves, navigating a world of double-edged choices and unresolvable debts in a system where every chess piece is at the mercy of something above it.","src":"haiku"},
"knock2~dashstar":{"haiku":"Drift and uncertainty posing as invitation, direction sought but meaning left deliberately vague.","opus":"A festival call-and-response built almost entirely on the anticipation of the drop, the repeated demands for stars and direction functioning as pure kinetic release rather than statement. The hook is the crowd's own hunger reflected back.","sonnet":"A minimal DJ-culture track built almost entirely around the energy of a crowd submitting to a beat — there's no real narrative, just the pleasure of being led somewhere by sound. The lyrical content barely exists beyond the invitation to move.","src":"haiku"},
"knocked-loose~god-knows":{"haiku":"Despair and divine abandonment converge; the speaker drowns in terror, acknowledging their damnation while accepting that god has left them alone with their hell.","opus":"Sinking into a suffocating panic, the speaker performs a funeral in which he hands a dying soul to God while feeling forsaken. It is about drowning grief and the conviction that hell is where he belongs, left alone.","sonnet":"Grief described as drowning in wet cement: the speaker sinks, claws at branches that break, and eventually surrenders, carving the name of the dead into the grave, tending the garden and decorating the grave in rituals that change nothing — \"God knows I belong to hell, that's why he left me here by myself.\"","src":"haiku"},
"knocked-loose~permanent":{"haiku":"Death unites separated lovers in eternal communion; physical absence proves secondary to spiritual permanence and undying connection.","opus":"Grief so total it becomes a death wish, a lover pleading to be reunited with the dead even if that means hell. The metalcore extremity fits the subject: love as something to die into, not survive.","sonnet":"A speaker driven to suicide by grief over a lost beloved frames the act as reunion — seeking a shepherd through death to be with the person they cannot survive without, calling their love permanent and the separation unbearable.","src":"haiku"},
"knocked-loose~where-light-divides-the-holler":{"haiku":"Nature's dissolution and intimate loss merge as lovers disintegrate into riverbed and stone; survival without the beloved constitutes paradoxical resurrection.","opus":"An Appalachian drowning fable of losing someone to floodwater and crawling out reborn but alone. Set in the holler, it treats devastation as a grim rebirth, a life continued only because the body kept moving.","sonnet":"A speaker watches a beloved dissolve into a riverbank, merges with earth and water in a traumatic vision, then crawls out of the river reborn — hands bloody, the other person's voice still echoing inside him — into a life defined by that irreversible loss.","src":"haiku"},
"knosis~a-3qxf4":{"haiku":"Psychological torment and death obsession—describing flesh-peeling and cursed blood, the narrator taunts with suicide bullets while dwelling in frozen doom.","opus":"Knosis works in extremity, and this track weaponizes that intensity against a hypocrite who imitates and whispers behind the narrator's back. The bullet once saved for the self is redirected outward, the secret vowed to a corpse, the whole voice reconstituted out of chaos into something that cannot be dragged down. Contempt for fakeness becomes the engine, refusal the only structure holding the rage in shape.","sonnet":"A song of rage directed at a hypocritical antagonist who imitates and talks behind the narrator's back: the narrator threatens that the bullet they saved for themselves would serve the other person better, promises to bury their secret in their corpse, and frames themselves as someone made whole by chaos — the throughline is contempt for fake people and refusal to be brought down.","src":"haiku"},
"kongos~come-with-me-now":{"haiku":"The speaker struggles against internal paralysis and overthinking, calling for action and living fully rather than remaining trapped in mental loops.","opus":"The South African-American band drive a restless summons to quit overthinking, the speaker having agonized himself into paralysis and squandered his life absorbed in the world's noise. Recalling a time before dread, he now hungers to act, struggle and lose himself, coaxed onward by a tempting voice, the accordion-driven anthem framing raw instinct as an escape from crippling self-analysis.","sonnet":"A man paralyzed by overthinking reaches the edge of his own rationality and answers an urgent call downward. He has thought himself into exhaustion, confused intellect with feeling, and now needs to abandon calculation entirely — the voice beckoning him offers neither safety nor explanation, only motion.","src":"haiku"},
"kool-the-gang~celebration-single-version":{"haiku":"Jubilant exhortation to gather and revel without hesitation, treating collective joy as the central purpose worth pursuit.","opus":"Pure communal joy engineered as an anthem, the sound of gathering everyone into one good time. Its genius is how little it says and how completely it means it, which is why it outlived its moment as the world's default party song.","sonnet":"A pure invitation to communal joy, addressed to everyone regardless of where they're from — the speaker calls people together to share good times, laughter, and celebration simply because the moment calls for it.","src":"haiku"},
"kordhell~murder-in-my-mind":{"haiku":"Violent impulses and street criminality dominate consciousness; the speaker romanticizes armed conflict and unpredictable harm as necessary expression of power.","opus":"A phonk track built on a sampled gangsta-rap verse, all gunplay bravado and a mind fixed on murder. The menace is style as much as substance, looping violence into a hook.","sonnet":"The lyrics are a direct sample from a 1994 gang-era rap track about armed robbery and murder — carrying a nine-millimeter, shooting anyone who tries to rob the narrator, putting bodies on a new Glock. The emotional state is a kind of murderous restlessness memorialized in the repeated chant of \"murder in my mind.\"","src":"haiku"},
"korn~a-d-i-d-a-s": {"haiku":"A person escapes reality through masturbation fantasies and pornographic dreams, unable to connect authentically and obsessed with sexual release.","opus":"Compulsive sexual daydreaming as pure escape, the narrator admitting he fantasizes constantly because it's his only relief from a grim existence he has no power to fix. Self-gratification and fantasy work as a hideout from self-disgust rather than any real longing.","sonnet":"Constant, all-consuming sexual fantasy — the narrator is so consumed by daydreams of anonymous sex that it becomes the only escape from a reality he finds unbearable.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title cribs a sneaker brand's letters into a locker-room punchline, yet the song opens like a horny anthem only to curdle into a confession of retreat. The swaggering pimp figure is a costume: the narrator admits fantasy is where he flees a reality he calls fucked up, arousal working as a drug because none of it is real. He never learns the name of anyone, and the pleasure keeps circling back to his own face staring out of the mirror, so the chorus hammering one clause toward oblivion becomes the compulsion rather than any craving for another body. Jonathan Davis lets the bravado stand as mask while the mantra underneath ticks like a symptom.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","artifice & performance"],"means":"fantasy as the only drug that muffles reality","built":"brand acronym rebranded as a locker-room symptom"},
"korn~a-different-world-feat-corey-taylor": {"haiku":"Desire to escape into a hidden realm where internal struggles disappear and external pressures cannot reach. The speaker wants a refuge where illusions no longer hold power.","opus":"Escapism as survival, the narrator craving a world where no one can reach him, the mess and recklessness of his inner life feeling more alive than the sanitized one outside. Davis and Taylor turn defiance against a controlling force into a refusal to be finished off.","sonnet":"A person suffocating in their own mental walls fantasises about escaping to a different world where no one can find them — the internal conflict between the self they hide and the chaos they live in has become unbearable.","src":"haiku","fable":"Davis mines the past not for nostalgia but for leverage, going back into the childhood-trauma material that has always fueled Korn in order to smash through the armor he cemented around himself to survive it — and the different world he longs for is simply somewhere the tormentor cannot reach him. But the escape curdles into confrontation: he turns on the abuser and dares them to finish the job, reframing his own annihilation as the thing they now have to face, so that begging to be destroyed becomes the only move that strips the abuser of power. Corey Taylor's bridge pulls the lens back from confession to clinical observation, naming how compulsion overrides perspective until the darkness feels chosen, and the shattered chant that follows collapses pleading and command into one voice — the survivor demanding the harder blow he learned to crave. It is a song about a wound re-enacted as agency, where wanting the worst is the last form of control a broken person has left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Corey Taylor wrote the bridge he sings — the clinical outside voice is literally another author stepping in.","themes":["shame & repression","anger & defiance","freedom & escape","violence & murder"],"means":"begging for annihilation reframed as stripping abuser of power","built":"guest bridge pulls to clinical distance before chant collapses back inward"},
"korn~alone-i-break": {"haiku":"Alone, the speaker falls apart—loaded with pain they cannot escape, questioning their manhood and readiness to leave.","opus":"A raw account of suicidal despair, the singer worn down past endurance and longing for his pulse to stop so the pain will finally cease; the deeper turn arrives in the questioning bridge, where amid thoughts of departure he wonders about God, about whether anything lies ahead, and whether surviving in isolation somehow diminishes his worth as a man.","sonnet":"A crisis of masculine identity in the face of overwhelming pain — the narrator wonders whether choosing to leave or shut down makes him less of a man, and whether God has any place in this, cycling through suicidal ideation and the desire to simply make the feelings stop.","src":"haiku","fable":"A suicide note that keeps trying to sound like relief: the early verses reach for a clinical calm, framing self-erasure as a practical fix for a wound that has bled too long, the only way left to make the feelings stop. But the song is honest about the machinery underneath that composure. The recurring turn ties the ache to masculinity itself — having endured all the abuse and broken under it alone, then wondering whether that breaking disqualifies him as a man — so the wish to leave is inseparable from shame. What earns the song its full weight is the late bridge, where the flat resolve dissolves into a stammer of unanswerable questions: is there only blackness, is there a God up here, what have I become. The mask of certainty was the point; the panic that leaks out from behind it is the real subject, and Davis lets the song end not on the promised peace but on more to come, the exit still not taken.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","shame & repression","madness & the mind"],"means":"masculinity named as the reason the wound cannot close","built":"flat resolve cracks open into unanswerable questions at the bridge"},
"korn~another-brick-in-the-wall": {"haiku":"Education and authority suppress youth while alienated individuals reject control and indoctrination; the song mourns paternal absence and calls for liberation from institutional and emotional imprisonment.","opus":"A cover of Pink Floyd's anthem against a childhood shaped by an absent father and dehumanizing schooling, rejecting rote education and thought control. The throughline is a numbed retreat into isolation, refusing comfort, drugs, or human contact and finally bidding a cold goodbye to a cruel world.","sonnet":"A cover of Pink Floyd's three-part suite condemning the emotional abandonment of children by absent fathers and authoritarian teachers, ending in a complete withdrawal from the world.","src":"haiku","fable":"Korn compress all three parts of Waters' Wall overture into one cover, and the splicing is the statement: the dead father who left only a photograph, the classroom that grinds children into interchangeable masonry, and the final numb refusal to be touched or medicated arrive back-to-back as a single continuous hardening. Each brick is another wound absorbed until the self is fully sealed off, and the closing goodbye is not suicide so much as the wall going up for good — the moment a person decides there is nothing anyone can say to reach him. For a band whose own catalog mines childhood abuse and isolation, the cover reads less as tribute than as recognition: they are singing about the same wall from the inside of it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness","family & blood"],"means":"three separate hardening wounds compressed into one continuous sealing","built":"splicing all three parts back-to-back makes the wall a single act"},
"korn~ass-itch": {"haiku":"Creative block and emotional pain collide; the narrator hates the songwriting process because it forces them to confront destructive feelings.","opus":"The agony of forcing creativity out when nothing wants to come, writing as self-inflicted pain that's also the only escape. Davis turns the act of making the song into the song's subject, dying to set it free.","sonnet":"A songwriter's raw, circular confession about the agony of writing: hating the process, feeling trapped by what comes out, yet finding that the only way to release the pain inside is to let the song through — even when it feels like dying to do so.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that hates being a song — Davis turns the act of writing into the wound it keeps reopening, loathing the process yet unable to stop what comes out of his hand. The refusal is the point: the disgust at expression is itself the confession, since the only material he has is whatever pain leaks onto the page. Its structure enacts a slow dying — the dread of writing, the numb four-beat toll of pain, and the recurring fear that the song is expiring even as it plays — until the plea flips the whole thing inside out: release isn't quitting but finally letting the thing trapped in him get free. By the last two lines the equation is brutal and honest: creating it is what kills him, and doing it anyway is the only reason he keeps going.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The release chant answers itself mid-song: four cries of just set me free give way to eight of it just set me free — one added word and a shift of tense, and the plea becomes the report of its own arrival. The writing that kills him frees him inside the same take.","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression","death & grief"],"means":"making art named as the injury that keeps him making art","built":"structure enacts a slow dying from dread through numb toll to plea"},
"korn~b-b-k": {"haiku":"Desperation for love mingles with rage and resignation as the speaker battles life's cruelty and his own worthlessness, seeking escape or relief from existence.","opus":"A prayer from the edge, someone pleading for a sign, some patience, some proof that being loved isn't wrong for him, while suicide sits openly on the table. Nu-metal as raw, unguarded cry for release.","sonnet":"A speaker exhausted by a life that withholds love and relief cycles between prayer, suicidal ideation, and the fundamental desire simply to be free and accepted for who they are.","src":"haiku","fable":"A petition shaped like a prayer, its plea for exit deliberately left double-edged: the repeated demand to be carried off could mean rescue or extinction, and the song never resolves which. The verses build a case for why living hurts — every reach for love comes back empty, life keeps souring — until the chorus reframes ordinary wants (to be loved, to be unbound, to simply be himself) as things that shouldn't be too much to ask, which makes the death-thought land as despair rather than drama. What keeps it from tipping into confession is the question hung at the end of every cycle: he asks whether dying is actually what he wants, and by refusing to answer he keeps a foot in the world, begging for a sign, for patience, for guidance instead. That withheld verdict matters here — the looping night/day, pray/die structure enacts a mind circling the same intersection without ever crossing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","alienation & emptiness","faith & the occult"],"means":"begging for a sign rather than crossing the intersection","built":"looping night-day pray-die structure enacts a mind that never resolves"},
"korn~baby": {"haiku":"Seduction and manipulation leading to emotional destruction.","opus":"A claustrophobic tangle of self-hatred and toxic attraction, the narrator hemmed in by someone who wants to break him even as he oscillates between saving and blaming them, chest tightening, desperate to be taken away from the whole cycle. Korn, the Bakersfield band who fathered nu-metal, built their sound on exactly this kind of squirming psychological knot, Jonathan Davis turning private damage into detuned, seething confession.","sonnet":"A push-pull with someone who arrived to tempt and destabilize — the speaker aware of being targeted, worn down by cycles that go nowhere, wanting both rescue and escape at once. The emotional logic is split clean down the middle: wanting to save the other person, wanting to hold them responsible, unable to fully choose either.","src":"haiku","fable":"The cooed pet name is a trap: the beloved being addressed is at once a lover and the compulsion that owns the speaker, and the two are never allowed to pull apart into separate things. Every charge he flings outward — you want to rattle me, to fracture me, to leave me at your leisure — is really the contour of his own hunger, ventriloquized onto someone else so the wound and the tenderness arrive as a single gesture. Davis grinds those phrases into a rhythmic tic until the syllables shed sense and turn into muscle-memory, which is the confession itself: an addict narrating the treadmill from inside it, lucid and powerless together. Only the final chant admits the truth, welding the wish to rescue and the wish to accuse into one exhaled line, so that saving the other person and condemning them stand exposed as one jammed motor rather than two rival urges.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"lover and compulsion refused to separate into distinct things","built":"phrases ground into rhythmic tic until syllables shed sense"},
"korn~ball-tongue": {"haiku":"Resentment toward a dependent person who drains life while offering nothing back.","opus":"A bitter reckoning with someone once called a brother, now a leech of self-pity demanding the narrator's life revolve around them until friendship curdles into contempt and the taunt of psychofuck. The nonsense refrain functions as pure guttural venom. Korn opened their self-titled 1994 debut with this, announcing nu-metal's blueprint: Jonathan Davis's wounded fury, seven-string down-tuning, and betrayal rendered as scat-sung rage.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who once mattered — a close friend, nearly a brother — who has curdled into a drain on the speaker's life, demanding without giving, wallowing in self-pity and expecting everything to revolve around them. The rage is real but so is the wound underneath: the friendship was once genuine, and the betrayal of doubt makes it unforgivable.","src":"haiku","fable":"A settling of accounts with a parasite dressed up as a friend, this is an eviction notice served to someone who leaned on the band for years, drained it, then demanded that its whole existence orbit him. The betrayal is framed in the most wounding available term, brother, so the recurring question of where the friendship ends is less a plea than a verdict already reached. What lifts the track above a grudge is how the language disintegrates as the rage does: the coherent grievance of the verses degenerates into the garbled, half-swallowed nonsense of the ball-tongue tag and the psychofuck chant, until Davis is no longer arguing but glossolalically spitting, the mouth too full of fury to form words. The refusal to give in and the escalation into imagined death, a body wheeled off under a sheet and abandoned, is where accusation curdles into fantasy, the song working best not as narrative but as the sound of a man too betrayed to stay articulate.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty","violence & murder"],"means":"grievance argued until rage disintegrates language into glossolalia","built":"coherent accusation degrades into garbled ball-tongue chant"},
"korn~beg-for-me": {"haiku":"Need for audience validation and control; performing to purge internal darkness.","opus":"A performer trapped between crippling stage dread and the release he only finds under the lights, demanding the crowd give the energy back, resentful when they won't beg for him. Beneath the bravado sits a plea about shame and the refusal to quit. Korn, the Bakersfield nu-metal originators fronted by Jonathan Davis, often mined this exact wound where the roar of an audience is both cure and torment.","sonnet":"The stage as the only place where internal chaos goes quiet — off it, the speaker is crushed by scrutiny and shame; on it, the noise stops and something like freedom arrives. The crowd becomes both mirror and antagonist, demanded to give back energy but never quite delivering the unconditional presence the speaker is screaming for.","src":"haiku","fable":"For a man who cannot leave his bed under the weight of paranoia and self-hatred, the stage is the single room where the sickness lifts and a roaring room briefly rewrites who he is. The demand that the audience beg for him turns worship into a transaction he needs and resents at once: he throws himself out to strangers, expects the energy returned in kind, and their inability to fully repay it exposes how flimsy a rescue built on applause always was. Davis wears the swagger as armor, then lets it crack in the bridge, where a snarled dare that nobody should care about his ruined feelings collapses into the quieter, more honest vow simply to outlast the ruin rather than surrender to it. Adoration is being asked to carry the whole burden of staying alive, and the plea itself admits it never can.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind"],"means":"adoration asked to carry the whole weight of staying alive","built":"swagger cracks at the bridge into the vow simply to outlast"},
"korn~black-is-the-soul": {"haiku":"Someone spirals into darkness, haunted by demons and led astray by another; their soul blackens as they die slowly, begging for life back.","opus":"A song about a spirit drawn off course into darkness, wavering between leaving and surrendering to his fate as disembodied wails and a chanting demon warp his sleep. The poisonous bond at its heart offers cleansing weeping alongside its cruelty, and he keeps crawling back to the thing destroying him while pleading to be given his life back.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a toxic relationship that has led them spiritually astray — the partner pulls them toward places and states they can't follow, and the narrator swings between blaming the relationship and recognizing his own complicity, finally begging to have his life returned.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses stage a collapse and a single question — hold or run — dressed in the fate-and-damnation vocabulary Jonathan Davis has trafficked in since childhood-trauma became Korn's founding subject, so at first the blackened soul and the nightly demon read as pure metaphysics. The bridge quietly relocates all of it into a relationship: the other person's scream is what pulls him further in, his own leaving always circles home to their threshold, and the plea to have his stolen life returned exposes the whole song as a codependency spiral rather than a battle with the abstract. Read that way the chorus games played in vain and the refusal of any breaking-down-and-rebuilding stop being cosmic and become the exact circular logic of staying. That is why the outro repeats the opening dilemma with the answer amputated — the well of words runs dry because he already knows he will retrace the same doorway home.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"cosmic fate-vocabulary quietly relocated into codependency","built":"outro repeats opening dilemma with the answer amputated"},
"korn~blind": {"haiku":"The narrator retreats into an internal hiding place to escape pain; they spiral deeper into isolation and self-imposed blindness as sanity deteriorates.","opus":"Korn's breakthrough opener maps overwhelming inner pain and the urge to dissociate, the narrator retreating into a hidden mental refuge where a different agony waits. Sinking deeper into a distorted, self-loathing headspace, he repeatedly senses himself losing all clarity, where insight and despair become the same thing.","sonnet":"About a private mental refuge turned torment — the narrator retreats into their own head to escape external pain but finds it's also where they're going blind, spiraling deeper into a self-esteem void with no bottom.","src":"haiku","fable":"The ready-check that opens things is a bait: it promises confrontation and delivers retreat, the skull recast as a hideout that doubles as a cell with no exit. What sounds like refusal to look becomes an involuntary condition, the vision fogging over until sight fails on its own terms rather than by choice. The turn arrives when swagger drops away and a flat admission surfaces — worth measured near zero, the soil underfoot readable at once as burial, the sinking left unanswered. When the single word finally dissolves into chant, its sense has bled off and only the raw state remains, a figure pacing the same darkness while repeating the name of what he cannot leave.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"hiding collapses into an involuntary condition vision fails on its own","built":"ready-check opener baits confrontation then delivers retreat"},
"korn~break-some-off": {"haiku":"Emotional disintegration and reckless abandon; the speaker burns relationships and sanity indifferently.","opus":"A man coming apart, narrating his own cruelty toward a partner he's already destroying with lies and games while pretending he feels fine. The song is the sound of someone losing the last grip on himself.","sonnet":"Barely coherent rage at someone who has emptied the speaker out — the fury spills past the point of articulation into obscenity — with flashes of spite and manipulation beneath the surface suggesting a toxic relationship collapsing into mutual cruelty. The rawness is the point.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man narrates his own decision to detonate a relationship, and the tension lives in how the verses stay lucid while the refrain dissolves into pure threat. The verses are the cold part: he admits the lies, the game-playing, the spite are deliberate, that his patience has expired, and he is almost pitiless about how certain he is that she still trusts him. The refrain is where language breaks into the title's blunt euphemism for violence, and Korn stack contradictory claims inside it on purpose — that he feels calm, that nothing will be all right, that his grip on himself is slipping — so the phrase performs a man who no longer knows which of his own reactions is honest. The bridge abandons words entirely, collapsing motion, appetite, and hatred into one clot before repeated obscenity, and that formal disintegration is the actual content: the point where self-justification stops and only the urge to smash something remains. Read against Jonathan Davis's confessional mode, the ugliness here reads less as fresh wound than as the swagger of someone relishing the wreckage he causes, which makes the closing fade on that single syllable feel like a shrug walking away from the damage.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance","madness & the mind"],"means":"self-justification abandoned when only the urge to smash remains","built":"formal disintegration at the bridge mirrors the self-control failing"},
"korn~calling-me-too-soon": {"haiku":"Being pulled back into darkness before ready to let go; the urgency of toxic connection.","opus":"A hallucinatory pull toward a lifeless doll of a lover, addiction and desire braided together as the narrator swings between shoving her away and pounding the door not to let her go, cursing the day he was born. Something calls to him too soon, part temptation, part death-wish. Korn, the nu-metal originators led by Jonathan Davis, render this compulsion in the fractured, self-torturing voice that has always been their signature.","sonnet":"A toxic pull toward someone the speaker knows is wrong — playful, dangerous, arriving at the worst possible time and dragging the emotional logic sideways. The internal split surfaces midway: rejection and desperate wanting in the same breath, slamming a fist against a door then dreading the silence if they actually leave.","src":"haiku","fable":"An addiction narrative disguised as a poisonous romance, where the addressed lover is also the drug itself, a manufactured mannequin propped upright to entertain, tender and cruel in the same breath, gratifying the speaker even as it hollows him out. The refrain stages resistance dissolving into consent: he keeps deciding he was foolish to fight it, yet the craving only intensifies, so that the summons arriving prematurely is death rushing ahead of its hour through the appetite he keeps feeding, another all-night binge each time. The bridge tears off the disguise, his flat insistence that he wants nothing from it colliding with the pleading not to leave and knuckles hammering wood, a man split down the middle and unraveling. Jonathan Davis lets the closing stripped-down repetition of that refusal register as a junkie's private incantation, aimed at no one and persuading nobody, himself least of all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","suicide"],"means":"resistance dissolving into consent on an accelerating death timeline","built":"bridge tears off the romance disguise to reveal the split addict"},
"korn~can-you-hear-me": {"haiku":"An unseen force haunts the speaker, taunting and controlling them while they struggle to be heard and escape its grip, transformed and diminished by the encounter.","opus":"A relentless presence that accompanies, devours and mocks the singer; feeling adrift and permanently altered, he cries out to know if anyone registers his voice, terrified he might not find his way back to who he was.","sonnet":"A narrator describes being shadowed by a consuming, shapeless presence that feeds on his past and watches him constantly, leaving him feeling lost and permanently changed.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jonathan Davis built The Nothing from the wreckage of his wife's sudden death, and here he refuses to name the presence that stalks him, calling it only an it. That withholding is the argument: bereavement is rendered as a creature that feeds on memory yet also cradles him, patient predator and intimate keeper folded into one voice. The chorus swings outward, begging an audience that cannot reply, before the ending dissolves into a single line looped past the point of song. What began as melody hardens into a verdict he recites to himself, no longer performing the loss but simply logging its permanence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"grief rendered as a patient predator and intimate keeper at once","built":"melody hardens into a looped verdict recited past the point of song"},
"korn~children-of-the-korn-feat-ice-cube": {"haiku":"Youth rebelling against parental control and societal oppression; just wanting freedom to live.","opus":"A confrontational generational anthem pitting the young against controlling parents and authority, laced with trauma, defiance and a blunt refrain that all they want to do is live. Ice Cube's guest verse escalates it into full teenage insurrection. Korn, the Bakersfield band who defined nu-metal, built this collaboration around rebellion and abuse survival, their detuned churn giving the rage of the dispossessed a heavy, seething shape.","sonnet":"A generational declaration of war against parental authority and institutional control — teenagers who have been judged, confined, and dismissed announcing that they refuse to be managed. The energy is chaotic and confrontational, but underneath it is a simple demand: all they want is to live, and no one is letting them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A generational war staged as a fake public-address alert, the intro dispatching adults toward the therapist's couch, the pew, and the precinct desk at once so every institution of authority is summoned only to stand accused. Davis sings from the wound Korn always circled, abuse recalled from the small survivor's vantage, the injury he reads in a boy's stare, the four-word leave-me-be refrain hardening from a whimper into a knife. Ice Cube supplies the mirror half, a swaggering delinquent who seizes the very fury adults dread and detonates it as gleeful transgression, so the collaboration refuses to rule whether these kids are the abused or the terrifying and swears they are simultaneously both. The turn is an inquisition of parental control, a barrage over who to date and how to love met with a flat verdict that when someone finally breaks, it will not be the young. What outlasts every provocation is the closing chant, one unadorned wish to simply exist, exposing the whole menacing posture as plating bolted over a plainer refusal to be crushed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","family & blood","shame & repression"],"means":"menacing posture exposed as plating over a plain refusal to be crushed","built":"fake public-address alert summons every authority only to indict it"},
"korn~cold": {"haiku":"Psychic assault from internal voices manifests as physical breakdown; the speaker wages war against invasive thoughts that corrupt his will.","opus":"Battling intrusive, tormenting voices — a wellspring of loathing buried deep inside — that push the narrator a single move from doing something terrible. He calculates, waits and swears to break the presence down, even as it feels like going under into his own grave.","sonnet":"A voice tormented by internal demons and outside aggression — a \"father of hate\" that lives deep inside — gathers itself from paralysis toward a decisive confrontation, oscillating between suicidal sinking and the resolve to break the cycle down.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track stages a manhunt and only late lets the hunter and the quarry collapse into one body. Its verses borrow a predator's vocabulary — a trap laid, a pulse counted, a rival marked to be dropped again and again — while the figures crowding in seem, at first, like outside persecutors bent on ruin. Then the refrain relocates the threat behind the ribs and christens it the sire of loathing, so the wretch being stalked resolves into the singer and the sorrow he hauls. That inversion shadows the muttered line about hovering a breath from finishing it — a resolve he pointedly cannot carry out, aimed at the resident enemy yet always a hair from turning back on the skull that houses it. Cut in the wake of Jonathan Davis's widowhood, the chill here is anesthesia, and the drilled marching resolve rings less like victory than the eerie steadiness of a man gone numb.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","suicide","death & grief"],"means":"hunter and quarry collapse into one body at the refrain","built":"predator vocabulary inverts mid-song to name the threat behind the ribs"},
"korn~cold-official-visualizer": {"haiku":"Intrusive pressures and hateful forces push the speaker toward self-destruction, but they resist with violence and determination to break free.","opus":"Korn return to the psychological horror that defined their nu-metal, voices that come discomforting, urging the speaker one step from doing it. An inner father of hate lurks deep, an inescapable nightmare of flames calling his name. Calculating whether he has the strength to break this thing down, cold as both feeling and command, the track channels Jonathan Davis's signature confrontation with intrusive dread and self-destruction.","sonnet":"An internal enemy — some ingrained pattern of hatred or abuse — taunts the speaker to the edge of collapse. The voice that demeans and the nightmares that name him are finally met with a decision to strike back, though the target is as much inside as out and the victory remains uncertain.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track stages a manhunt and only late lets the hunter and the quarry collapse into one body. Its verses borrow a predator's vocabulary — a trap laid, a pulse counted, a rival marked to be dropped again and again — while the figures crowding in seem, at first, like outside persecutors bent on ruin. Then the refrain relocates the threat behind the ribs and christens it the sire of loathing, so the wretch being stalked resolves into the singer and the sorrow he hauls. That inversion shadows the muttered line about hovering a breath from finishing it — a resolve he pointedly cannot carry out, aimed at the resident enemy yet always a hair from turning back on the skull that houses it. Cut in the wake of Jonathan Davis's widowhood, the chill here is anesthesia, and the drilled marching resolve rings less like victory than the eerie steadiness of a man gone numb.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","suicide","death & grief"],"means":"hunter and quarry collapse into one body at the refrain","built":"predator vocabulary inverts mid-song to name the threat behind the ribs"},
"korn~coming-undone": {"haiku":"A person spirals into psychological collapse under the weight of inner demons and past trauma.","opus":"A vivid depiction of a mind detonating, where dark impulses keep resurfacing and the inner voices once mistaken for allies turn hostile. The through-line is how a person who appears solid is actually breaking apart, choking on his own unraveling despite straining to keep himself intact.","sonnet":"About psychological disintegration — intrusive thoughts, paranoia, and childhood trauma converging until the narrator can't hold themselves together, the demons they thought they'd tamed returning to complete what they started.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man narrates his own dissolution as it happens, cataloguing the phases of a mind failing like an engineer reading gauges on a reactor going critical, powerless to halt the sequence he describes. The refrain's contradiction carries everything: a fortress facade admitting that the granite exterior is precisely the brittlest layer, the toughness a thin ceramic over something ready to shatter. Jonathan Davis laces a death-wish beneath the hurt, the familiar inner voices he once trusted now hunting him, the childhood candy-pistol offering no shelter and curdling into a weapon aimed inward, its owner begging for a clean, unhesitating shot. That final surrender, the flat concession that recovery isn't happening, abandons every trace of bravado and simply states the plain fact.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","suicide","shame & repression"],"means":"dissolution catalogued like a reactor going critical with no override","built":"fortress facade named as the brittlest layer in the refrain's contradiction"},
"korn~counting": {"haiku":"Exploitation and emotional drain define a toxic relationship; the narrator retreats into silence while their partner profits from their suffering, counting gains indifferently.","opus":"Being used and drained by people who feed on you for money, fame, and access while you swallow the pain in silence. Written in the wake of sudden success, it reads as bitterness at everyone counting what they can take.","sonnet":"The speaker accuses an exploitative figure — likely an industry gatekeeper — of silently tallying profits and power while the speaker suffers and screams, insisting they pursue the work for love while being bled dry by those counting the returns.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built on the gap between two voices. The verses belong to someone being drained and tossed about, choosing silence while a taker tallies everything extracted from them day by day; the running total is the perfect image for a bond reduced to bookkeeping, where the other person keeps a ledger of what remains to be siphoned. Then the bridge flips register entirely into the exploiter's leer, itemizing the cash, the groupies, the marquee lights, insisting it all amounts to nothing more than sport and amusement. Heard in the wake of sudden success, that second voice sounds like the handlers and hangers-on circling a band that has just broken huge, and the real move is refusing to narrate its own suffering directly: the wounded party stays mute and shrieks anyway, so the arithmetic becomes the only thing that ever balances.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The only-for-the-fun shrug repeats Make Me Bad's fun-and-games alibi nearly verbatim — Issues keeps handing its confessions the same borrowed excuse.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","money & the street","anger & defiance"],"means":"a bond reduced to bookkeeping where the ledger is the only balance","built":"bridge flips into the exploiter's leer without warning or transition"},
"korn~dead-bodies-everywhere": {"haiku":"A child crushed by sick parents and family dysfunction resents being forced into a role they cannot be and feeling worthless.","opus":"A furious rejection of a broken family's demands, the singer refusing to be the dutiful child they want and worn down by being made to feel worthless while their failed ambitions land on his shoulders. He asks only to live on his own terms. Korn, the Bakersfield band that shaped nu-metal, turn Jonathan Davis's raw, guttural voice into a claustrophobic outcry against parental pressure and neglect on this Follow the Leader cut.","sonnet":"Parental pressure experienced as violence — a sick mother, a damaged father, their unfulfilled dreams handed down like a sentence. The speaker refuses to carry it, refuses to perform a version of themselves that never existed, and names the contradiction plainly: you want a good son from parents who made the child feel like no one. The dead bodies of the title are the versions of himself he was supposed to become and didn't.","src":"haiku","fable":"A child's refusal of the role a broken home demands of him: be the good son, redeem the parents, carry the weight their own failure left behind. Davis writes the parents as a wreckage he's expected to tidy—a sick mother, a ruined father—and the title's mounting chant turns that domestic collapse into a field of corpses he's made to clean up, the guilt of being alive amid what the adults destroyed. The pivot line is the twice-hammered admission that he can never be the thing they want; everything before it is the negotiation, everything after is the breakdown. The final eight-fold repetition of the phrase is the argument giving out—no more pleading, just the image left ringing, the way an unbearable thought loops when there's nothing left to say to it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Davis's pre-Korn day job was the family-approved path — a Bakersfield coroner's office, then mortuary school — and his own gloss on the title runs nearly literal: chasing the life his parents wanted, what he got was dead bodies everywhere.","themes":["family & blood","shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"child assigned to clean up the wreckage the parents authored","built":"eight-fold closing chant signals the argument giving out not landing"},
"korn~did-my-time": {"haiku":"A person accepts responsibility for their destructive path and feels their anger consuming them from within, unable to break the cycle despite trying. The speaker has done their time and continues to suffer from internal rage they cannot tame.","opus":"Davis wrestles with a self-chosen path that led only to failure, blame and a rage he can feel rewriting who he is. The core is the exhausted sense of having served a sentence for simply being himself and finding no release.","sonnet":"A song about betrayal, self-blame, and the exhaustion of trying — the narrator can't win, can't figure out where he went wrong, feels enslaved by something he can't name, but insists he gave everything he had. The anger is turned partly inward and partly outward, without ever fully resolving.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession of defeat that refuses to stay penitent. The verses claim full authorship of the ruin, yet the same voice pleads blamelessness and demands parity, so guilt and grievance quarrel inside one skull that has already begun laughing at its owner. What the song really tracks is possession: a fire it cannot govern, a hex it begs to be rid of, an anger overwriting the self until willed direction collapses into helpless mutation. The refrain flips from apology into verdict — a sentence already served — so the final cry to God isn't a request for rescue but a terrified bulletin that the change is completing regardless. Cut as the theme for the 2003 Tomb Raider sequel, it works as a cursed heroine's arc, though the self-loathing-into-monstrousness spiral belongs wholly to Jonathan Davis.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression","madness & the mind"],"means":"guilt and grievance quarrelling inside a self already mid-mutation","built":"refrain flips from apology to served sentence in the same phrase"},
"korn~die-another-day": {"haiku":"A speaker channels internal chaos and escaping despair, fighting to survive another day despite alienation and misery. They insist on living forward despite being caught in a downward spiral.","opus":"Korn cast survival as raw endurance, the mind buckling under pressure and isolation as sanity frays. The narrator flees a slow-motion collapse and refuses to look behind him. The chorus reframes persistence as grim rather than heroic, a nu-metal anthem about clawing through mental ruin only to buy one more day.","sonnet":"Survival as its own grinding act of defiance. Madness and alienation press from outside while misery distorts perception within — yet the only available response is to keep crawling forward, not toward triumph but simply toward the next day, leaving behind anyone who expected a cleaner exit.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title inverts itself: to die another day is not survival won but survival deferred, the prize for fighting and crawling being only the right to keep suffering tomorrow. Korn locate the threat inside the skull rather than outside it, tracking a mind that turns on its owner, where despair misleads and runaway thought bends what is real until the pressure and lunacy that mount up prove self-authored. The bridge hardens mid-breath: a plea for someone to listen curdles within four lines into a flat refusal to care, dissociation standing in for the connection it never got. That refusal to have anyone follow him from the ruin reads less as flight than as a survivor turning his shoulder on a collision he caused and cannot mend. The grim we-take-it-all bravado is the mask, and beneath it sits a psyche that has settled on endurance as the only victory still on offer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"survival reframed as nothing more than deferred suffering","built":"plea for connection curdles into flat refusal within four bridge lines"},
"korn~die-yet-another-night": {"haiku":"A person consumed by suffering and terror experiences violence as a way to feel alive, while isolation protects others. They cycle through despair without resolution, hoping to survive yet another night.","opus":"A self-loathing narrator, tagged as worthless and unwanted by all, clutches his fear tight and hopes only to make it through one more night while keeping everyone at arm's length. Korn's nu-metal torment drives the grinding refrain, framing pain as an ordeal survived over and over, his isolation offered as a shield for the people around him.","sonnet":"A speaker saturated with pain and self-loathing simultaneously warns others away for their own safety and clings to the perverse relief of surviving one more night inside the terror. The contradiction between wanting death and fearing it holds the song's emotional weight.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two voices braid through this: an external one that keeps chanting the verdict of worthlessness — that the singer is rotten to the core, already a corpse the world has stopped grieving — and the interior one that has to survive under that sentence. Davis, whose whole catalog stages the tormentor's voice inside the victim's head, lets the abuser own the verses while the chorus becomes the self's grim bargain: to keep tearing forward through the faces and the terror, and to want nothing more than the reprieve of dying yet another night, survival reframed as a nightly execution that keeps failing. The pre-chorus surrenders agency entirely, ceding do-what-you-want to whoever is running him, and the bridge flips the logic outward — staying distant is protection for the other person, because all the suffering that remains is his to carry alone. It reads as addiction or abuse metabolized into the same machinery: the mask here is the litany of insults, and underneath is someone who has begun to speak it as if it were true.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","suicide","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"survival recast as a nightly execution that keeps failing","built":"abuser owns the verses while chorus becomes the self's grim interior bargain"},
"korn~divine": {"haiku":"Deriving pleasure from someone's suffering; positioning oneself as their instrument of pain.","opus":"A menacing address from someone who feeds on another's suffering, hiding to defy them, promising their life will soon be his, escalating into a raw chant of superiority and contempt. The cruelty is framed as something almost sacred to the tormentor. Korn, the Bakersfield band who defined nu-metal, put this on their 1994 debut, Jonathan Davis voicing predator and prey with the queasy intimacy that made the genre unsettling.","sonnet":"A predatory revenge fantasy aimed at someone who underestimated the speaker — watching, preparing, waiting for the precise moment to take what is owed. The controlled menace of the opening cracks by the end into raw fury: years of being ranked lower finally break into a declaration that superiority was never the other person's to claim.","src":"haiku","fable":"Davis routes the verses through an abuser's mouth, a predator narrating the theft of a life as worship, recasting assault as preparation and calling the ruin he causes holy. The bridge then shatters that composed menace into a stuttering loop about being fed up, being deemed inferior, then howling that he is superior instead. The mask splits and a buried child surfaces, exposing the smooth threat as a memorized nightmare replayed from inside its target, the final taunt bending the tormentor's speech back on him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"abuser's composed menace exposed as a memorised nightmare replayed","built":"smooth threat shatters into stuttering loop when the mask splits at the bridge"},
"korn~everything-falls-apart": {"haiku":"A relationship collapses into lies and emptiness; all promises shatter leaving only broken connections and internal void.","opus":"Nu-metal grief over a dead relationship refusing to be sentimentalized, the narrator insisting this is no love song while everything they shared collapses. The empty-headed mantra at the end is dissociation swallowing the pain.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship that has decayed past saving — the narrator gave up caring, both people are numb and hollowed out, promises meant nothing, and now there is nothing in his head except the collapse of everything they shared.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song that keeps swearing off any tenderness — a denial repeated precisely because the affection it disowns is exactly what is being grieved, the mask worn to keep the grief from showing. The two verses run parallel but escalate: the first mourns a shared dream that everyone swore would last, while the second turns the collapse inward onto trembling hands and a frame ready to buckle, so the ending of a relationship slides into the wish to be taken out entirely. Between them the pre-chorus curdles the earlier lullaby imagery into open contempt, trading whispered comfort for a curse. The real weight sits in the bridge, where Davis abandons address and narrative for a single line hammered flat over and over — the emptiness in the head — so that the song stops describing the aftermath of loss and simply enacts the numb, scoured-out nothing it leaves behind. It is a Serenity-of-Suffering move: the serenity is the vacancy, the only peace on offer once everything has fallen.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness","suicide"],"means":"denied tenderness loops back to grieve what it disowns","built":"bridge abandons narrative for a single repeated line enacting vacancy"},
"korn~everything-i-ve-known": {"haiku":"Cyclical love and hate in a relationship that feels enslaving yet inescapable.","opus":"An exhausted account of a relationship that has turned to hate yet refuses to end, two people tearing their hearts out then fighting, unable to survive alone or stay apart. Pride is sacrificed only to loop back into the same wreckage. Korn, the nu-metal pioneers led by Jonathan Davis, distill their late-career sound here into a portrait of codependent love as an inescapable, self-inflicted cycle.","sonnet":"A relationship that turned from good to consuming — two people who stayed by each other through escalating damage, tearing each other open and then patching up to do it again. The dependency is the trap: every fight feels wrong, leaving feels impossible, and what remains is a bond that feels less like love than like an inability to survive apart.","src":"haiku","fable":"A ballad of codependence dressed as a breakup, where the ache is not that the relationship ended but that ending it means losing the only self the singer knows. The chorus keeps grammatically shifting the loss from the other person to everything I've known, so the partner becomes the entire map of reality; without them there is no survival because there is no separate person left to survive. The song is honest about its own dysfunction rather than romantic about it: the two admit they provoke each other on purpose and then fight, that love has soured to hate, that pride is a cage they choose. Korn hands the whole thing to a raw, needy delivery so the pull toward the destructive orbit reads as compulsion, not devotion, and the return to each other lands as relapse rather than reconciliation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","identity & becoming"],"means":"return to a destructive orbit landing as relapse not reconciliation","built":"chorus shifts loss grammatically from person to entire known reality"},
"korn~evolution": {"haiku":"A person confronts the animal nature in humanity—the primitive beast that still controls modern minds despite civilization's progress. They resign themselves to this unchangeable nature.","opus":"Davis rejects the flattering story that humanity has advanced, insisting the base animal still runs the mind and that all our progress has changed nothing, evolution just a cover for the same old savagery.","sonnet":"The narrator can't suppress the primitive animal inside — the beast that controls the mind and drives destructive behavior — and the song questions whether being dominated by this force means deserving to die, set against the backdrop of a species that hasn't evolved past its worst impulses.","src":"haiku","fable":"Darwin gets recast as a verdict, not a triumph: humanity as the pathogen procreation unleashed, its legacy a skeleton some future taxonomist seals behind glass and files under deranged. The narrator's terror is that the predator crouched in his skull is no aberration but the entire blueprint, so he asks why he alone should be sentenced when the whole ravenous lineage keeps thriving. Korn's habitual self-disgust swells from the confessional to the taxonomic, letting a leering promise of bodily invasion curdle into the cold arithmetic of survival. That mantra of surveying the wreckage and finding it unaltered lands as the crux: across all the ground we supposedly covered, we remain the same clawing creature raking the dirt for fragile relics, and this process refines nobody.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","shame & repression","violence & murder"],"means":"self-disgust scaled from confessional to taxonomic indictment of the species","built":"Darwin recast as a verdict sealing humanity behind museum glass"},
"korn~faget": {"haiku":"Mockery and social rejection of a non-conformist for their appearance and perceived sexual orientation.","opus":"A raw scream from the wrong end of a schoolyard, sung by someone branded a slur, ugly, alien in a world that calls itself normal. The narrator flips shame into defiance, daring the tormentors to admit their own ignorance while insisting he is only a pretty boy who doesn't fit their rules. Korn built the nu-metal template out of exactly this kind of childhood-wound catharsis, and few tracks on their 1994 debut go at it more nakedly.","sonnet":"A howl of humiliation turned inside out — someone who has spent their whole life marked as different, mocked and discarded by a world that runs on stereotypes, arrives at a point of raw, furious self-assertion. The question of identity cycles back endlessly, but the rage finally overtakes the shame rather than collapsing under it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Davis seizes the schoolyard slur once aimed at him and welds it to the title, so the whole track becomes the cruel arithmetic of that abuse turned back against the people who dealt it out. A child marked as diseased and effeminate for failing to match the crowd first begs to be left alone, then abandons the pleading for something colder and armored. The taunting central passage, where he claims he cannot desire a girl inside a rotted society, is him ventriloquizing the very charge his tormentors invented, so the venom strikes their fantasy instead of anything real about him. That the chant lurches between owning and refusing the label proves the wound never closes, and the buried, endlessly repeated question of who he even is names the true injury better than any epithet. The venomous closing insult is meant as raw retaliation, not tidy uplift, the sound of someone choosing to hurl the same hatred straight back at his abusers.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"slur aimed at the speaker welded to the title and hurled back","built":"ventriloquising the tormentor's charge turns the venom against their fantasy"},
"korn~falling-away-from-me": {"haiku":"Hopelessness and the sensation of disintegration where the speaker flirts with suicide as an escape, experiencing life as violent beatings that are inescapable and dizzying.","opus":"Davis voices a young person being ground down, flirting with suicide as a way to numb relentless pain from being beaten into the ground. The dread is watching your own life slip loose, spinning away past any grasp.","sonnet":"Someone trapped in psychological and physical torment — beaten down daily, flirting with suicide as the only relief from a pain that never lifts — the song is about the loss of self under sustained abuse, the self literally falling away.","src":"haiku","fable":"Korn frames despair here not as crisis but as an exhausted daily chore: the speaker treats self-destruction like chronic pain to be managed, muttering that things will improve tomorrow with the flat conviction of someone who long ago stopped believing his own reassurances. The verses stay eerily quiet and hollow, a fatigue of self-erasure, until the structure enacts the collapse the words only describe, trading statement for pure pounding rhythm and a first person hammered flat beneath the mix. That shift from numb narrator to a battered, wordless object is the crux, an abuse whose source blurs between an outside tormentor and the speaker's own mind. The lone obscenity that ruptures the loop marks the single moment the mask of reassurance drops and the raw terror of dissolving finally surfaces.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind"],"means":"self-erasure managed as a daily maintenance task","built":"structure trades words for pure rhythm as the speaker dissolves"},
"korn~finally-free": {"haiku":"Someone's death viewed as liberation from suffering, tinged with failure to save them.","opus":"A grief-stricken address to someone the narrator failed to save, his own hatred coming for him and laughing while he watches a life betray them, finding a grim release only in the loved one's death being called freedom. The crying-out refrain sits between mourning and helplessness. Korn, fronted by Jonathan Davis, channel their nu-metal heaviness into this heavy meditation on loss and the guilt of the survivor.","sonnet":"A grief song aimed at someone the speaker failed to reach in time — the loss framed not as death exactly but as a release from a life that had already betrayed that person. The speaker is left carrying the guilt of trying and failing, watching from a distance as someone who cried out for help finally goes quiet.","src":"haiku","fable":"Grief written from the far side of a death, this is a late address on an album Jonathan Davis made after losing his wife: the survivor talking to someone who can no longer answer. The title works as a wound with two faces at once, the survivor's helpless consolation that the dead are past their suffering laid over his own refusal to accept it. The hating-it litany is not hatred of a person but of the fact itself, the thing that comes for him, laughs at him, stares and pulls, until he begs it taken down as if reversing it were possible. What tips the song past ordinary mourning is the direction of the crying out: he insists the lost one is calling for him, so the freedom he grants them keeps snapping back into his own failure to save, the how-could-I-fail he cannot stop asking. The last movement collapses into the couplet of it being so hard to see and being cried out to, the two lines trading places until they are the same helplessness, which is the song refusing to resolve the death into meaning and just repeating the ache.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The record runs a lattice through this song: the come-for-me cry that stalks You'll Never Find Me, Cold and H@rd3r returns here aimed the other way — now it is the lost one he insists is crying out for him, the plea of Can You Hear Me inverted. And the line about nothing saving you is the album's title stepping into the lyric.","themes":["death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"consolation for the dead loops back into survivor's guilt","built":"the title's two faces — relief granted and abandonment confessed — never separate"},
"korn~forgotten": {"haiku":"Complete subjugation and erasure of selfhood until nothing remains but acceptance.","opus":"A voice reduced to a plaything at another's discretion, stripped of its needs and left as something forgotten, bowing down while quietly weighing whether it is worth anything at all. The refusal of pity closes it with bruised dignity. Korn, the Bakersfield nu-metal band led by Jonathan Davis, turn their late-period sound toward this study of subjugation and the fight to salvage self-worth from erasure.","sonnet":"Submission and erasure under someone else's control — the speaker reduced to a plaything, stripped of needs and direction, conceding defeat not because they want to but because resistance has cost too much. The painful turn inward asks whether any of it was worth investing in, whether self-worth was ever real or just something that got quietly confiscated.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being reduced to an object by a power that owns you, and the slow accounting of what that ownership costs. The verses move from disorientation — bowing, asking where you even are — to a lifting of the veil, and what it reveals is not release but the recognition that this path was never yours; once your needs are stripped, all that remains is the erasure named in the title. The decisive turn comes when the speaker admits he must eventually reckon with his own worth, then confesses he gave the thing life and watched it take his in return, so the tyrant may be an addiction, a faith, or a self-ruining creation of his own hand rather than an external master. The closing refusals — the order that no one pity or mourn him — are a mask, a hardness laid over the plain truth underneath, that he has already conceded and expects to vanish.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"the tyrant that erases you was built by your own hands","built":"closing refusals of pity mask the surrender they perform"},
"korn~freak-on-a-leash":{"haiku":"The speaker feels controlled and violated by life itself, trapped and diseased by invisible forces that strip away his autonomy and leave him numb to his own existence.","opus":"A wrenching scream about being used and controlled, the tether standing in for how others and life itself wring him dry. The idea of being treated as a disposable body and of pieces being repeatedly wrenched away links the dehumanization to violation, while the sensory deadness he describes reads as the toll of getting through it.","sonnet":"A song about feeling perpetually violated and controlled — by fame, by the industry, by life itself — leaving the singer numb, trapped, and diseased, unable to break free despite the pain.","src":"haiku","fable":"A howl about being kept on a tether: the verses render a self that some unnamed force keeps stripping down, helping itself to a piece of him every time he lets his guard drop, until even intimacy curdles into one more transaction where he is used and thrown away. Watch how the language buckles as the hurt deepens. The verses can still name their grievances, but the bridge caves into wordless rhythmic grunting and the hook into pure gibberish, as if the worst of it lives past the reach of speech and can only be spat out as noise. The defiant boast that no one will ever witness him break is the mask; the babble underneath is the breaking happening anyway, just untranslatable, a scream that survives only after the words have been beaten out of it.","fv":2,"fnote":"Davis wrote it feeling paraded and strip-mined by the label — and later savored the reversal: after Follow the Leader the band never depended on a record company again; in his words, it took them off the leash.","themes":["surveillance & control","madness & the mind","anger & defiance"],"means":"defiant mask held over a breaking happening in real time","built":"bridge and hook dissolve into wordless noise as speech fails"},
"korn~get-up-feat-skrillex": {"haiku":"The speaker is broken and fragmented, hiding in empty space while tortured by memories. He suffers internal pain but persists despite grimness and difficult lines to draw.","opus":"Korn and Skrillex fuse damage and intimacy into something unsettling. The narrator feels shattered with nobody able to mend him, so he forces his own jagged fragments onto another person, chasing relief through hurt and craving self-harm while hiding away, haunted by what he's lost. The result blurs suffering and closeness deliberately.","sonnet":"Feeling broken and wrongly put together while being consumed by the pain someone else has inflicted — the narrator hides in an empty space, haunted by what they've left behind, and the song is as much a command to themselves as to anyone else to get up and stop collapsing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study in brokenness turned into a weapon: the speaker names himself as shattered, then refuses repair, hammering his mismatched fragments into another body as though a person were putty that could absorb what he cannot reconcile. The nu-metal grind fused to the electronic build stages a nervous, subcutaneous crawl toward eruption, and Skrillex's drop delivers the discharge the verses keep withholding, mechanizing compulsion into rhythm. That barked order splits the narrator in two, one voice snarling at the other to silence itself and rise, so the hook reads as an addict quarreling with his own paralysis rather than any anthem of triumph. The final turn confesses a man boxed inside a vacant room, haunted by everything abandoned behind him, exposing the aggression as escape from a grief he refuses to speak aloud.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","death & grief"],"means":"aggression toward another as camouflage for unspoken grief","built":"the Skrillex drop mechanizes compulsion into rhythm, not release"},
"korn~good-god": {"haiku":"Exploitation and vampirism drain the speaker dry; betrayal transforms love into a wound that never heals.","opus":"Pure venom at someone who moved in, hollowed him out, and made him a slave, drawn from Davis's memories of a childhood tormentor. The relentless demand to get out of his face is exorcism aimed at a lifelong parasite.","sonnet":"A furious address to someone who entered the speaker's life and systematically drained it of everything — identity, agency, will — leaving behind only contempt and the demand that this person get out of their face for good. The betrayal is felt as a theft of selfhood rather than merely a broken heart.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rage-purge aimed at a you who arrived carrying nothing, was welcomed in, and left the speaker hollowed out and reduced to bondage. The chorus is not an argument but an eviction, screamed on loop until the sheer tally of repetitions becomes the point: a body trying to physically shove an unwanted presence back out through the skull. The needle whose filth floods the skull, the parasite that struck without warning and drained him, the identity carried off wholesale, all pull this past a merely toxic romance toward addiction, or an abuser lodged so deep the two can no longer be told apart. Fittingly for early Korn, the interpretation that resists interpretation holds: the words refuse to name the thing because naming it would grant more shape than the speaker can survive, and the one counter-punch — the drainer demoted to a minnow adrift in life's vast waters — is the only power the speaker recovers, bile standing in for a wound that stays undescribed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"eviction scream repeated until repetition becomes the wound","built":"the thing is never named because naming it grants more shape than the speaker survives"},
"korn~got-the-life": {"haiku":"Conflicted defiance against imposed emptiness and control. The narrator rejects both despair and external judgment, claiming ownership of their life despite internal hollowing and abandonment.","opus":"Korn's queasy anthem about the emptiness of getting everything you thought you wanted; Davis half-brags, half-recoils at the hollow prize of fame, treating God's blessing as a curse he can't refuse and can't enjoy.","sonnet":"The narrator pushes back against a god-like voice that tells him he will never find life or meaning, insisting he has already claimed it for himself despite the hollowness and daily struggle he feels inside. The tension is between the external verdict and a stubborn internal insistence on having something real.","src":"haiku","fable":"Korn's signature bounce turns a party command into a hollow ritual: the invitation to dance is issued by someone who admits he feels swallowed and torn out from the inside every day, so the groove is a distraction machine rather than a celebration. The song's split runs through its title's irony — God, reached by pager like a nagging beeper, taunts that the speaker already has the life while insisting he'll never actually see it, so the promised good life is possessed and withheld at once, a thing everyone chases and no one gets to look at. The refusal to follow that opens the track curdles into everyone will follow, marking how the same hollowness that isolates the singer is also what makes the crowd move with him. What sits under the mask is emptiness bought as glamour; the argument the song keeps enacting is that having the life and feeling nothing are the same condition.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance","faith & the occult"],"means":"having the life and feeling nothing declared the same condition","built":"the title's irony: the life is simultaneously possessed and permanently withheld"},
"korn~gravity-of-discomfort": {"haiku":"An account of persistent psychological weight and self-directed hate that crushes the speaker daily. The pain is unavoidable and will never fade, marking them irreversibly.","opus":"Jonathan Davis mapping depression as a physical force that hunts him, a weight that hollows out everything he once loved. The relief he reaches for reads less like healing than like surrender to the darkness.","sonnet":"An account of being crushed under hate and shame so persistent it feels designed to destroy — the speaker moves through denial and isolation but cannot escape the weight that chips away at everything he loves.","src":"haiku","fable":"Jonathan Davis writes grief as a squatter that has taken residence inside the body, an unwanted tenant who feeds on the narrator's affections and dictates his ruin. Emerging from the loss that shaped The Nothing, the personified affliction is treated less as emotion than as a hostile intelligence with intent, one that engineers collapse and calls the resulting death untimely. Early bravado about discarded terror curdles into resignation as the refrain hardens from protest into dull confession. Each repetition strips another attachment, until the closing admission of guaranteed pain repeats not for emphasis but because the speaker has run out of anything else to say.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind"],"means":"grief personified as a hostile tenant engineering the speaker's collapse","built":"the refrain hardens from protest into dull confession with each repetition"},
"korn~h-rd3r": {"haiku":"Life progressively worsening despite effort; external forces conspire to defeat you.","opus":"A downward spiral of a mind under assault from unseen forces that strip away its happiness no matter what it attempts, existence only ever growing rougher as it pleads to understand its own identity. Images of confinement and encroaching voices make the paranoia bodily. Korn, Jonathan Davis's nu-metal pioneers, keep excavating this ground of mental siege where every push against the dark only sinks it deeper.","sonnet":"A spiral of systemic futility — every honest effort erased, every chance taken comes up empty, while something faceless in the speaker's head tears the joy out and keeps the score rigged. The plea is existential: tell me who I am, tell me what to feel, explain why everything keeps getting harder with no ceiling in sight.","src":"haiku","fable":"On The Nothing, the album Jonathan Davis built out of his wife's death, mourning blurs into craving — the tormentors in his head wear both grief's face and temptation's, indistinguishable. The verses lay out a rigged game where every choice pays out sorrow and no effort dissolves the pain, so the chorus stops arguing and just interrogates a suffering that mounts with no author to answer for it. The pivot is the bridge's strange bargain: he offers to sober up as the price of a return, a plea aimed at someone now beyond answering, so getting clean is bartered like currency against an impossible homecoming. The closing taunt — a single desperate gamble, missed because the prize always bolts — reads the addict's logic honestly: what he hunts — relief, or the person herself — is the thing forever slipping from his grip, which is exactly why he keeps hunting it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"sobriety bargained as currency against an impossible homecoming","built":"grief and craving wear each other's faces, indistinguishable across the verses"},
"korn~hater": {"haiku":"The speaker refuses to be diminished by haters and their disdain, claiming resilience despite being knocked down repeatedly. They maintain inner peace and strength against external attacks.","opus":"Korn turn their usual gloom into defiance, the speaker declaring his existence is already inverted so no one can drag him any lower. Caught in a plunging loop yet still soaring and battling, he claims contentment and refuses to scold those who despise him. The recurring taunt to bring it on transforms endurance into an anthem, an unusually confident stand in a catalog defined by torment.","sonnet":"Resilience against someone whose contempt and drama have become routine. The speaker has already been through genuine upheaval and will not be further destabilized by another's poorly disguised misery — the refusal is calm rather than angry, grounded in a self that has simply stopped being available for demolition.","src":"haiku","fable":"A defiance anthem that wins by refusing the fight it's picked into: the singer takes the downward spiral, the very image a hater would wield as proof of his ruin, and turns it into evidence of endurance — he's already hit bottom, so there's nothing left to be knocked down from. The real move is moral rather than combative. Instead of returning the aggression, he diagnoses it, locating the hate as the other person's unresolved pain and pointedly declining to chastise, which reframes contempt as the hater's own prison. For a band built on Jonathan Davis mining private wreckage, the boast reads as hard-won rather than posturing, and the endless chanted down becomes less a taunt absorbed than a mantra worn smooth into armor.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"defiance wins by diagnosing the hater rather than returning the blow","built":"the downward spiral, the hater's weapon, reclaimed as proof of endurance"},
"korn~here-to-stay": {"haiku":"Someone confronts their pain and self-harm, refusing to be a victim while demanding to keep what's theirs.","opus":"A depiction of rage, self-harm and dissociation, the speaker bashing his own face into a mirror and guiding a blade down his vein to escape unbearable pain. Insisting he's not a whore and can't give anymore, and that hurt is turning to hate, it frames survival, 'I'm here to stay,' as defiance wrenched out of abuse and despair.","sonnet":"A person at the end of their rope — hurt turned to hate, still bearing scars — describes both bashing their face into a mirror and dragging a blade toward a vein, the rage at being hollowed out and used expressing itself through self-harm imagery.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor's inventory of self-loathing that turns, mid-song, into a refusal to disappear. The verses stage abuse and its residue as violence aimed inward — the skull cracked against glass, the razor tracked toward a vein — because when someone strips you clean, the only remaining thing to command is the flesh doing the harm. That flat denial of being a whore is the tell: shame installed by another person, now spat back as rejection. The chorus reroutes all that inward pressure into a blunt vow to remain and to level whatever comes, the wounded self that craved erasure instead choosing endurance and making its own din its survival. The closing loop on having nothing left to surrender reads less as collapse than as a boundary finally held: the well is dry, and refusing to keep bleeding out is itself the act of staying.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","violence & murder"],"means":"shame installed by another person spat back as boundary finally held","built":"inward violence in the verses rerouted outward by the chorus into a survival vow"},
"korn~hold-on": {"haiku":"Illness consumes from within like a monster; the speaker urges connection and resilience through shared senses and mutual defense against suffering.","opus":"A grim survival hymn casting sickness as a beast devouring them, the bond between two people cut loose and adrift without warmth or bearing, yet held together by a shared promise to endure and never abandon each other. Korn, the Bakersfield nu-metal originators, lean into their detuned churn and Jonathan Davis's raw urgency to wring a fragile vow of persistence out of pure dread.","sonnet":"Addiction or illness tearing through a family — something monstrous eating people alive, new lives entering a world where survival is uncertain, a community that responds with cruelty rather than care. The refrain is a fight instruction: hold on, be strong, use every sense and defense you have. The song oscillates between despair at what's consuming them and fierce refusal to release each other, the I'll never let you go aimed at whoever is still there.","src":"haiku","fable":"Birth and contagion collapse into a single unwelcome event here: conception described as an intrusion, the severed umbilical cord, a newborn whose continuance stays doubtful, all recast as heredity passing along a defect like an inherited plague. The verses go coldly surgical, a body opened and revived on the table yet denied any reason for its pain, while the crowd around it responds to that suffering with a lynch-mob verdict rather than mercy. The chant answering all this is raw stubbornness stripped of sense, an endurance mantra that cannot decide whether persisting is courage or self-deception, virtue or merely refusal. Everything pivots when the clinical third-person diagnosis fractures into a first-person begging not to be abandoned, unmasking the armored bravado as the terror of a man who suspects he is the devouring illness and grips the people beside him regardless.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"hereditary damage framed as a defect passed along like contagion","built":"third-person clinical diagnosis fractures into first-person terror of abandonment"},
"korn~hopeless-and-beaten-requiem-mass":{"haiku":"Absolute psychological defeat with no recovery possible; suffering without escape.","opus":"A confession of being consumed by something the narrator can't put away, feeding on him until he is hopeless and beaten, unable to feel a thing while wishing he were a better, disdain-free version of himself. Time, he admits, soothes nothing. Korn, led by Jonathan Davis, distill their nu-metal despair into this requiem-framed study of addiction and dissociation, where suffering is endured rather than resolved.","sonnet":"A complete surrender to something the speaker cannot name or contain — a force that has taken hold, done damage, and left numbness where feeling used to be. The only honest account left is that nothing soothes the wreckage except time, and even that offers no comfort, just the grinding continuation of a hurt that has no off switch.","src":"haiku"},
"korn~idiosyncrasy": {"haiku":"Trapped between self-hate and defiance; God's cruelty and inescapable nature.","opus":"A fight against a dark fury that keeps seizing hold of the singer, his own shadow lying in wait wherever he flees, building to the caustic vision of God cackling above as he scrabbles toward a fading glow. The image of a betrayer who inflicted harm and never mended it gives the rage a target. Korn, the nu-metal originators led by Jonathan Davis, voice this clash of private wrath and cosmic ridicule in their signature tormented mode.","sonnet":"Navigating a dark path that keeps trying to swallow the speaker whole — rage underneath, a shadow always waiting, the light always receding just as it's almost reached. Aimed at someone who caused specific harm and refused to heal what they damaged, the song frames the whole struggle as a cosmic joke, with whatever god oversees it watching from above and laughing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grief-crawl toward a light that recedes at exactly the speed you approach it, which is the song's cruelest joke and its architecture. Every verse is a step forward; every chorus undoes the step, so the forward motion never banks any distance. The bridge names the trap as a hand closing on the throat mid-stride, then the song abandons narrative and simply reports God laughing from above. That mockery reads less as accusation than as the only remaining account of a struggle rigged to fail. Coming from Korn's The Nothing, cut after Jonathan Davis lost his wife, the title cuts both ways — flung at the betrayer inside the accusation, it doubles as bitter self-diagnosis, the compulsion to keep reaching being the very thing that punishes him; either way the addressed one merely lacquered a fresh wound over an old one and left it untended.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"reaching toward recovery as the very compulsion that punishes you","built":"each verse steps forward; each chorus undoes it, banking no distance"},
"korn~insane": {"haiku":"Internal cancer and psychological torment consume the narrator's life, with no escape or relief from the weight of persecution. The song describes hopelessness and isolation.","opus":"The song depicts life eaten away from the inside by a spreading, cancer-like affliction that grows in the head and leaves the speaker horrified and beaten down. He blames himself, feels unable to be understood, and repeatedly calls the state insane.","sonnet":"The speaker describes an internal torment — a persistent, growing force he calls a cancer — that eats away at him, dominates his thoughts, and exposes everything he tries to hide, leaving him horrified and isolated. He acknowledges being beaten down and blames himself for what he cannot contain.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song withholds a subject: the thing beating Davis down, growing in his head, metastasizing until it has rooted in every concealed corner, is only ever called it, and the refusal to name it is the point — depression, compulsion, the self, they collapse into one unnameable tenant. Davis has always sung from inside that occupation rather than about it, and here the horror is specifically that it feels external (dominating, invading) while the bridge quietly admits the opposite, that no other person can reach him and the fault is his own. The title lands as that contradiction's only resolution: when the persecutor and the self are the same voice, the word insane isn't an insult thrown outward but the diagnosis turned inward, hammered a dozen times because repetition is the only argument left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"unnamed persecutor collapses into the self","built":"withheld subject and hammered title-word make diagnosis the only resolution"},
"korn~it-s-all-wrong": {"haiku":"The narrator dies repeatedly daily; they sacrifice everything supporting others while their demon wins and they surrender.","opus":"Korn grapple with a spirit worn thinner by the hour, wondering how often a person can perish inwardly while wasting away and finding every route leads nowhere good. As founders of nu-metal's most exposed self-hatred, they concede their inner darkness has triumphed and declare themselves finished with numb, empty devotion, done giving up everything only to be left hollow.","sonnet":"A demon has won and the speaker knows it — every day a small death, every effort hollow, the altar of whatever was once believed now under attack from the inside. The exhaustion of supporting others while sacrificing everything culminates in complete withdrawal. Nothing works, nothing fits, and the only honest move left is to stop pretending otherwise.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song of spiritual attrition, where the daily grind is figured as a daily dying and faith itself is the thing being fought. The speaker frames his own belief as the enemy — the soul chipped at, the altar attacked, the righteous blamed — until the crisis resolves not into salvation but surrender: the demon wins, and being done is spoken as relief rather than defeat. The final verse pulls the abstraction down to a person, recasting the whole religious struggle as the fatigue of propping someone up and sacrificing everything for them, so the loss of faith and the end of a devotion become the same exhaustion. Fitting for Korn's post-reconciliation record, it reads less as anti-belief than as someone too depleted to keep performing belief, letting the repeated collapse into I'm done carry the meaning the verses can no longer argue.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"faith surrendered not in crisis but in exhaustion of performance","built":"the religious struggle collapses into a devotion to a person — same fatigue, same ending"},
"korn~it-s-on": {"haiku":"Internal conflict spirals as the speaker battles self-hatred, angels stabbing inward, and powerless resignation.","opus":"Korn stage a war with the self. The narrator blames himself for his torment, feels attacked from within, and recognizes he can never triumph over an opponent that is his own mind. His plea to be held and possessed tangles longing with self-hatred, turning a simple hook into another bout against himself.","sonnet":"Surrender to an inner compulsion the speaker knows is self-destructive. Something calls and the resistance collapses — an internal force described as angels stabbing from inside — and the speaker falls into a loop that rearranges without changing, craving what feeds the damage and finding no way to win against himself.","src":"haiku","fable":"The arena taunt and the barked title read as a challenge thrown at some rival, until the enemy turns out to be the man issuing it, wired against his own good. Something summons him and he tips toward it willingly; the descent is the appetite, not the accident, and he names the collapse his own doing before any opponent appears. He describes angels wounding him from within and a fixed inner arrangement that merely shuffles rather than heals, so any resistance is spent against his own architecture. Underneath the swagger sits the flat admission that he cannot stand himself, and the bravado curdles by the end into a hushed plea to be enclosed and held, the muscle revealed as camouflage over a craving for restraint.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"bravado as camouflage over a craving to be enclosed and restrained","built":"the arena challenge turns inward — the rival is the speaker's own wiring"},
"korn~justin": {"haiku":"Admiration for someone who escaped earthly suffering, possibly through death; tribute to lost strength.","opus":"A tender, disoriented farewell addressed to a dying young fan, the narrator wishing he had the boy's strength as a soul slips free into space, haunted that kids die listening to his music. The refrain of taking off together softens death into flight. Korn, Jonathan Davis's Bakersfield nu-metal band, drop their usual aggression here for something raw and grieving, the title naming the real child it memorializes.","sonnet":"A raw address to someone with strength the speaker envies and mourns simultaneously — someone whose light was too bright to look at directly, whose soul found a way out that the speaker couldn't follow. The turn toward the audience — the kids who died listening — makes it an elegy that keeps flinching between grief and urgency, insisting on life even while circling loss.","src":"haiku","fable":"Written for a terminally ill fan who visited the band, this stages the collision Korn kept circling on Follow the Leader: the wrecked frontman suddenly cast as somebody else's source of courage, unnerved. The opening obscenity is the usual armor, but it collapses into an intimacy sharper than any rage, the singer confessing he envies the boy his composure and naming the visitor the stronger of the two. That recurring flight into orbit becomes the interpretive hinge, converting a hospital farewell into a shared vanishing act where both parties abandon the failing body at once rather than one leaving the other. The reversal lands when the band's ugliness is recast as a lifeline, so that noise, the single thing this scarred narrator trusts, turns into the only proof of vitality he has to hand over. What began as goodbye resolves into a mutual staying, not a cure but a refusal to let the encounter be measured solely by mortality.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","friendship & loyalty","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"the wrecked singer envying the dying fan's composure, reversed","built":"flight into orbit converts a farewell into a mutual vanishing act"},
"korn~let-s-get-this-party-started": {"haiku":"Mental instability and alienation corrode identity; the narrator is overwhelmed by paranoia and depression, seeking escape from the person they've become.","opus":"Depression and paranoia hiding under an ironic party title, a man pinned flat in a rut, envious of anyone stronger and desperate to feel again. The hook mocks itself, the only celebration on offer being the struggle to make himself sane.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped in paranoia and emotional numbness rages against someone whose presence makes them feel insane, using dark irony to announce a break from passivity and a desperate bid to reclaim their own sanity.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title works as a bitter false invitation: a chant of celebration screamed over a portrait of dissociation, where each morning returns a self the singer neither trusts nor wants to occupy. The verses list the symptoms of that fracture — an inability to share what others plainly feel, envy of anyone at ease in their own skin, a paranoia that reads every passing stranger as a threat. The pivot comes when the addressed you turns out to be the singer's own trapped double, so the hook's demand to end that borrowed identity and reclaim a steadier mind names the same person twice over. Jonathan Davis, at his most exposed across Issues, lets the closing passage drop argument and simply pound one accusation into the ground, until the loop itself, unresolvable by any chorus, becomes the point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming","madness & the mind"],"means":"the party hook aimed at the singer's own trapped dissociated double","built":"the addressed you resolves as the speaker himself, the command self-directed"},
"korn~let-the-dark-do-the-rest-requiem-mass": {"haiku":"Someone numb and suffering from past trauma lets darkness take over, describing their life as a mess haunted by pain. They feel sickened by another person and surrender to the weight of despair.","opus":"Korn voice a hollowed-out fatigue, the narrator so numb he can't rouse any feeling and, after a battered, ruined life, simply gives himself over to the blackness swallowing him. A repeated jolt of revulsion at someone sharpens the disgust, and exhaustion tips into surrender as he abandons the future to shadow.","sonnet":"A life of suffering handed over to darkness as the only honest resolution. The hauntings cannot be forgotten but they can be delegated — the speaker stops fighting the black portraits that pulse inside, choosing instead to let whatever hellish presence arrived finish what years of misery have already started.","src":"haiku","fable":"Depressive numbness curdles into surrender here, the speaker hunting an itch or spark that never arrives, no longer expecting rescue and half-inviting oblivion to finish what the ache began. Davis turns the revulsion refrain inward, the nausea aimed less at a tormentor than at a self loathing itself, with wellness and conformity offered as cures that only hollow the room out further. Black canvases hung behind the ribs pulse like something almost holy, reframing self-erasure as welcome; the faint wish to glimpse whatever waits ahead is too weak to shut the door the resignation has already swung wide.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","suicide","shame & repression"],"means":"surrender to oblivion half-invited when no spark ever arrives","built":"self-erasure reframed as something almost holy by the black-canvas image"},
"korn~let-the-guilt-go": {"haiku":"An examination of how dishonesty and backtracking lead to psychological anguish and self-torment. The speaker's overthinking and guilt prevent forward movement and authentic connection.","opus":"A song about the corrosive weight of guilt, dishonesty, and obsessive overthinking, with the speaker berating himself for listening to his head instead of his gut and urging himself to let the guilt go.","sonnet":"A candid account of how obsessive second-guessing and guilt spiral into paralysis, with the speaker unable to break free from a mental loop that poisons communication and wastes days.","src":"haiku","fable":"A command repeated until it hollows out is a confession that the command has failed. The chant orders release four identical times, an incantation that names freedom yet manufactures none, while a recited catalogue of dishonesty and its punishment plays like a sermon nobody in the room believes. Beneath the ritual the actual damage shows itself: a mind narrating one grievance into the next, doubling back into raw wounds, then unspooling across everything until no exit holds. Its rawest admission is trusting reason over instinct when reason is the snare, the single verb of cognition looped past sense into a howl, the audible shape of rumination that eats its own tail. What the song insists on dropping is precisely what steers it toward a squandered life, an anthem indicting the very impulse it keeps rehearsing as cure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression"],"means":"a release command repeated until repetition proves release has failed","built":"the chant enacts the rumination it orders stopped — cure indistinguishable from affliction"},
"korn~liar": {"haiku":"Sexual and romantic performance conceals authentic desire; the speaker urges surrender to instinct over the performance of devotion.","opus":"An ugly, macho taunt urging a friend not to be softened or tamed by a woman, framing love as a trap and manhood as staying feral. It reads as insecurity dressed as swagger, mistaking cruelty for strength.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses a male friend who has let a woman hollow him out — abandoning his instincts, losing his identity, allowing himself to be reduced — and confronts him for lying to himself about his desire and what it has cost him. The advice is crude and misogynistic in framing but driven by a genuine alarm at what the speaker sees as a friend's self-erasure.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built entirely as a second voice inside a man's skull, arguing him out of the devotion he has pledged to one woman. The whole address is a goad: it flatters his instinct, frames desire as an animal fact he betrays by pretending otherwise, and turns the title-word back on him as an accusation — not she is the liar, but you are, every time you claim she is all you want, or that you could ever drown the fire. The chorus seals the trap: the rising want cannot be denied, so denial itself becomes the falsehood. What curdles the counsel is that this seductive inner counsel darkens into something uglier — the same voice that licenses letting go also snarls at him to hurt her and shut her out, so the coaxing whisper stands exposed as the machinery of a compulsion that dresses cruelty up as honesty. The mask is temptation; underneath is a man who cannot separate his own craving from a command.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"seductive inner counsel exposed as machinery of compulsion dressed as honesty","built":"the title-word turned back on the speaker — not she is the liar, but you"},
"korn~lost-in-the-grandeur-requiem-mass": {"haiku":"Trapped in false pretense and persona, desperate to shed the act and find authenticity.","opus":"A raw plea from someone drowning in self-loathing, begging for direction while admitting how cliche it sounds, fed up with the false self he portrays and losing his grip as some constant profound sound steals his joy. Korn, the Bakersfield nu-metal pioneers, return to the exposed psychological anguish that made Jonathan Davis's voice singular, dread and self-disgust rendered in their heavy, lurching, deeply personal register.","sonnet":"Someone so lost in the performance of their own life that they can no longer feel anything real — following instructions, playing a role, while something constant and profound erodes them from the inside. The grandeur is the trap: a self-image built on what others expect, hollowing out the person underneath until they are fed up with who they are pretending to be.","src":"haiku","fable":"Grandeur here is the inflated public self, the persona the speaker is sick of wearing, and the song traces a man locked inside that costume until it becomes his cell. Jonathan Davis addresses a you who is both abuser and commanding voice: an authority whose orders were all followed, leaving him so anesthetized he claws at his own wounds just to register any sensation, threading Korn's decades-long fixation on early trauma into an adult who obeyed every command and was scooped hollow regardless. His request for guidance is openly branded a cliché, and that is the snare, since recovery slogans cannot penetrate a person who has hardened into performance. The pivot arrives with a droning noise that returns each morning to strip whatever gladness survives, a compulsion swelling until his hold slips; the final refusal then plays not as victory but as the last available stance, bravado costumed in the very grandeur it fails to shed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"the public persona obeyed until it hollows the self that wore it","built":"the final refusal plays as bravado costumed in the very grandeur it cannot shed"},
"korn~love-meth": {"haiku":"Someone writhes in emotional chaos and self-hatred, begging for an exit—asking why they exist and seeking reasons to end their life.","opus":"Davis casts craving as a torn, self-loathing addiction, chaos in the heart and deceit that comes easy, down on his knees asking for a reason to go on. The double meaning of love and meth fuses romance and dependency into one lonely, contained ache.","sonnet":"A relationship as addictive and destructive as a drug, where the narrator's inner chaos — self-loathing, deceit, existential emptiness — makes him oscillate between wanting to die and desperately needing a reason to keep going.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title welds affection to a controlled substance until neither can be named without the other, and Davis sings the pull as one appetite: something that carries you off and ignites you at once, wanted most for the ruin it guarantees. He voices the dependent from inside the spiral, where the self-hatred predates the fix, so the rush never heals anything but merely certifies what he already suspected about himself. The bridge is where it turns unbearable — the panicked question of where to bolt and where to conceal himself repeats on a single melody, first asking for cause to snuff himself out, then for cause to survive intact, both prayers riding the same tune because to the hooked they open onto identical ground. Nothing untangles; the closing return simply folds back into the refrain, adrift and companionless, since that endless orbit is precisely the shape of the sickness.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","suicide"],"means":"love and substance fused until neither can be named without the other","built":"the bridge's two prayers — to die and to survive — ride the same melody because to the hooked they open onto identical ground"},
"korn~love-meth-live-from-london-2014": {"haiku":"Feelings erupt from chaos as internal devils relate to love's solace; fire and desperation consume meaning as the speaker grasps for reasons.","opus":"An anguished cry from within addiction and self-disgust, turmoil and affection knotted together until neither offers a way out. Korn drag their trademark torment into the language of craving, the singer adrift and isolated, begging for a reason either to end things or to survive, undone by demons of his own creation.","sonnet":"Chaos and loathing have been internalized so long they have produced complacency; the speaker, unable to contain themselves or find a reason to stay alive or get out alive, oscillates between destructive self-hatred and a desperate plea to be set on fire and taken somewhere else.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title welds affection to a controlled substance until neither can be named without the other, and Davis sings the pull as one appetite: something that carries you off and ignites you at once, wanted most for the ruin it guarantees. He voices the dependent from inside the spiral, where the self-hatred predates the fix, so the rush never heals anything but merely certifies what he already suspected about himself. The bridge is where it turns unbearable — the panicked question of where to bolt and where to conceal himself repeats on a single melody, first asking for cause to snuff himself out, then for cause to survive intact, both prayers riding the same tune because to the hooked they open onto identical ground. Nothing untangles; the closing return simply folds back into the refrain, adrift and companionless, since that endless orbit is precisely the shape of the sickness.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","suicide"],"means":"love and substance fused until neither can be named without the other","built":"the bridge's two prayers — to die and to survive — ride the same melody because to the hooked they open onto identical ground"},
"korn~lullaby-for-a-sadist": {"haiku":"A predatory figure derives pleasure from another's suffering and manipulation, framing torture and psychological cruelty as a game they've already won.","opus":"A chilling first-person study of a sadist who gets off on inflicting pain, counting torture like a nursery rhyme. The horror is its gleeful intimacy, a predator smiling at tears while insisting the victim wanted this game they'd already lost.","sonnet":"A first-person study of someone who derives pleasure from another's pain and psychological torment — planting seeds of destruction, smiling at tears, framing the entire relationship as a game they've already won.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sadist's monologue weaponizes the form named in the title: the counting chorus, one through five, poses as a nursery rhyme, but the numbers tally torture and shame instead of easing a child toward sleep, turning a bedtime device into an inventory of cruelties. The song's real engine is the complicity trap the speaker keeps pressing, the fiction that the target invited this, which reframes abuse as a consented match and lets him crown himself the victor before anything begins. Escalation from the worn groove of ritual he treads by rote to the stuttering I attack that shreds the verse form is the mask dropping: the compulsion he pins on an inner hunger stands revealed as pure appetite, the rhyme no comfort offered but the last scrap the target is left clutching.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"nursery-counting form weaponized as inventory of cruelties","built":"the lullaby title turns a bedtime device into a tally of torture"},
"korn~make-me-bad": {"haiku":"Someone seeks sickness and depravity in others to feel needed and to fuel obsession; the cycle corrupts them.","opus":"Korn watch salvation rise and fall amid a compassionless world, craving the sickness in another as a fix that mistakes deception for feeling. The nu-metal pioneers make flesh and attention the addictions in question, the reason draining away again and again while wondering whether the hunger itself makes them bad.","sonnet":"An honest admission of compulsive, self-destructive desire. Something in the body wants to be corrupted, wants to feel the sickness of another person — not love exactly but intensity, fixation, the only sensation that still breaks through numbness. The speaker knows the craving is irrational and chases it anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"Davis writes craving as a closed loop where salvation and sickness are the same appetite: watching his own rescue rise and fall, he reaches not for a person but for a fix, and the other body is only somewhere to feel that need reflected back. The chorus stages the loss of reason as an ongoing surrender rather than a fall already taken, the flesh doing the corrupting while he narrates from just outside it. What redeems the song from flat confession is its refusal to answer its own question: the title becomes an interrogation that never resolves, repeated into the fade as if the blame itself were the thing he can't stop returning to. It is less an admission of guilt than a compulsion to keep asking whether desire is guilt at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The motive confessed here — fun and games — resurfaces almost word for word in Counting's ledger later on the record: two different compulsions, one identical alibi.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"desire interrogated as guilt in a question the song never resolves","built":"the title repeated into the fade as compulsion to keep asking, not to answer"},
"korn~mass-hysteria": {"haiku":"Humanity rides toward collapse amid shock and upheaval, burning with collective fury. Fighting continues despite inevitable doom and exhaustion.","opus":"Korn turn private breakdown into a communal condition, the singer clawing through a daily grind of anxiety and refusing to pretend he's fine. The cosmic scale of the imagery pushes personal collapse outward, making one person's inner combustion feel like a spreading contagion of panic that everyone is caught in.","sonnet":"About absorbing daily shock and manipulation — living in a world of crashing wave after wave, feeling shattered but refusing to stop fighting. The dying star and supernova imagery frames individual survival as part of a larger collective collapse.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survival anthem that keeps insisting it is winning while quietly narrating its own combustion. The daily grind lands as concussive daily hits, yet each is reframed as fuel that hardens the speaker, and the cosmic imagery escalates from a collapsing sun toward the detonation of a supernova, i.e. going out in one blinding flare rather than simply lasting. That is the doubled edge Davis buries in the title: mass hysteria is the crowd frenzy he rides, but also the panic he is talking himself down from, the blaze inside him at once triumph and self-immolation. When the bridge asks why life must run this way amid manipulation and encroaching dark, the vowed refusal to glance behind reads less as swagger than as the single gesture holding the collapse just offstage.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","madness & the mind"],"means":"survival swagger masking the collapse it rides","built":"the title names both the crowd frenzy and the internal panic"},
"korn~my-gift-to-you": {"haiku":"Necrophilic obsession and murder framed as twisted love and desire for control over death.","opus":"The closer to Korn's self-titled debut, and one of the most disturbing things they recorded: a narrator who conflates love with strangling, framing a killing as a twisted act of devotion and mercy toward someone whose pain he claims to end. The refrain curdles hate and pity together. Jonathan Davis sings it as intimacy gone rancid, the record's early exploration of trauma tipping fully into predator's fantasy.","sonnet":"Dark psychosexual fantasy dressed as devotion — a controlling desire that can only be consummated through possession absolute enough to be lethal. The speaker frames violence as love and hatred as intimacy, wanting to see through the other's eyes so completely that the boundary between them disappears into something grotesque.","src":"haiku","fable":"A strangulation narrated as courtship, where the speaker eroticizes the instant a body stops moving and rebrands that stillness as something generous he bestows. The bridge is what lifts it past shock-metal: the killer drops the seducer's croon and confesses the machinery underneath, that his own suffering is the only proof he is alive and that murder is his method of climbing inside another mind to borrow its vantage. His offering, then, is a monstrous counterfeit of empathy, since he can only touch a person by extinguishing them, and the tenderness of the verses is a thin skin stretched over that inability. When the seduction finally caves into raw hatred and the battered plea to be felt, the true engine stands exposed: a man who mistakes cruelty for connection and confuses being registered by someone with hurting them. Davis's instinct to inhabit the predator rather than sentence him from a safe distance is exactly what keeps the track harrowing instead of merely garish.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","alienation & emptiness","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"murder reframed as the only way to touch another mind","built":"the seducer's croon collapses mid-song into the raw confession beneath it"},
"korn~narcissistic-cannibal-feat-skrillex-kill-the-noise": {"haiku":"Someone trapped in self-hatred and self-destructive cycles resists a toxic relationship that feeds off their misery while they struggle to reclaim themselves from the wreckage.","opus":"A portrait of a toxic, consuming relationship with a self-absorbed partner who devours the narrator, leaving him run down and hating the life he built while everything haunts and torments him. He fights just to bring himself back from the dead and hold on through the haze rather than be defiled and remade in her image.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a toxic, self-absorbed relationship that has drained and broken the speaker, leaving them trapped in a haunted, exhausted state they can't escape — the narcissistic cannibal in the title is whoever fed on them.","src":"haiku","fable":"Davis addresses a devourer that is at once a person and the self-consuming appetite it names — narcissistic because it feeds only itself, cannibal because it feeds on him. The verses stage a resolve that keeps buckling under its own fatigue: the wish to shatter whatever authority weighs on him is undercut by being too spent to lift it, so the chorus's true refrain is not defiance but resuscitation, the flat admission that he must haul his own corpse upright once more. That the landmark move is grafting Skrillex's dubstep detonations onto Korn's dread is not incidental to the meaning; the lurch between crushing mass and mechanical churn is the haze the words describe — a struggle prolonged to its final hour precisely because release never arrives, only endurance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance"],"means":"defiance voiced as pure resuscitation rather than triumph","built":"the dubstep lurch enacts the haze the lyrics describe"},
"korn~never-never": {"haiku":"A person rejects love, connection, and emotional authenticity entirely, choosing to run and hide rather than face the vulnerability of true relationship.","opus":"A pledge of complete emotional lockdown, a refusal to ever open his heart again so he'll never have to fake, flee, or defend himself. The real subject is numbness as armor, tamping down his own resentment and shutting off all feeling because, deep down, he believes he isn't worthy, choosing isolation over the risk of being wounded.","sonnet":"A complete shutdown of emotional capacity after some unspecified wound — the narrator has decided never to love again, frames it as a relief from pretending and dying inside, and is systematically suppressing all feelings rather than risk the same pain.","src":"haiku","fable":"A vow of permanent emotional lockdown, sworn so violently that the grammar buckles: the promise of no future tenderness keeps slipping into its own opposite, as if the mouth cannot physically hold the absolute the will keeps demanding. That fracture is the point, since a refusal pitched this loud is a man losing an argument with himself, and the tic of feeling that keeps rehearsing underground admits the wound stays live beneath the armor. Classify, crucify, seal the door and vanish: these are one identical move in Davis's register, quarantining the ache rather than metabolizing it, and the final avalanche of the word reads less as verdict than as a mantra hammered flat to smother the doubt it can never actually silence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","dissociation & numbness","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"a lockdown sworn so loud it argues against itself","built":"the vow keeps slipping into its own opposite as the grammar buckles"},
"korn~next-in-line": {"haiku":"Facing inevitable decline and death, questioning if you're just waiting for your turn in line.","opus":"A late-night spiral of dread, insomnia and self-negation, the narrator making quiet plans while his blood burns and a clock stalls. The recurring question is whether he's wasting his life or simply awaiting his turn to be undone. Korn returned to this territory of exhaustion and looping despair across their later albums, the heavy groove doing the sinking that the words describe.","sonnet":"A sleepless, coiled dread about fate closing in — a speaker who can't breathe, can't stop pacing, watching a clock that seems to announce something final. The chorus hammers the idea that fear is the real bed you make for yourself, and all the refusing and wanting can't stop what's already been set in motion.","src":"haiku","fable":"A vigil kept by someone circling their own worst impulse: the sleepless scheming, the burning blood, the frozen clock all belong to a mind talking itself toward a ledge it cannot stop revisiting. The central question — idle rumination or a genuine appointment with self-erasure — is the whole nerve here, weighing whether that downward pull is nothing or a summons already issued. Jonathan Davis's bridge flips into a goading second voice, an internal tormentor promising the sufferer they will shrink to some exhibit behind glass, and the reflex is flat refusal of that spectacle. Faithful to Korn's late-career turn toward naming the depressive spiral outright, the writing keeps fascination and dread as one indivisible feeling, which is why the same treacherous vista keeps luring him back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","madness & the mind"],"means":"fascination and dread held as one indivisible feeling","built":"an internal tormentor voice enters the bridge to goad the sufferer"},
"korn~no-one-s-there": {"haiku":"Nameless people face erasure, overwhelmed by greedy forces feeding on them and their own internal chaos, unable to escape.","opus":"A bleak lament of total isolation, the singer feeling invisible and interchangeable, terrified of vanishing, longing to escape somewhere he can finally break down, and hemmed in by people taking what is his. Korn pioneered nu-metal out of Bakersfield, and their heavy, downtuned grind plus Jonathan Davis's anguished delivery voice a loneliness no one will answer.","sonnet":"Anonymity and emotional abandonment — faceless, replaceable, calling out into silence that only echoes back. The speaker wants to escape people who feed on what's his and a life where no one hears or sees him anymore. The wish to fly somewhere he can cry without judgment is quiet and specific, and the song spirals between outward resentment at those who drain him and inward collapse, the soul bleeding in a search that keeps returning empty.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dissociation ballad wearing the costume of a persecution complaint. The faceless we of the verses is left open — a partnership, or Davis split from himself, unremembered and half-deleted — and the scavengers devouring what belongs to him arrive first as real circling takers before revealing the shape of a hunger he cannot name from inside. Its architecture is a shout that never lands: he calls into a void and hears only his own voice bounce back, and by the bridge every outside enemy has folded into a single locked cell with him as its only occupant. The panic of being unable to find one's own emotions becomes a life sentence in solitary, and the turn from accusing the world to conceding he is his own jailer is where the song pays off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind"],"means":"the outside accusation folding inward to a locked cell","built":"the shout that never lands returns only as the speaker's own echo"},
"korn~oildale-leave-me-alone": {"haiku":"Resentment explodes over betrayal and emotional abuse; the speaker demands solitude after giving everything, their love soured into contempt for someone who depleted them.","opus":"Davis names his hometown and turns it into a plea against someone who took the love he had and returned only cruelty. The rage is really a wound: the demand to be left alone is the last defense of a heart braced to break.","sonnet":"Someone who gave everything in a relationship and had it ripped away by a person who disrespected and talked trash about them — now they are furious, heartbroken, and just want to be left completely alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being fed on by a hanger-on who mistakes provocation for toughness, and the singer's slow refusal to keep supplying the reaction they crave. The verses set the trap: someone who can't resist getting all up in it, thrilled by their own goading, while the speaker keeps swallowing insults until swallowing stops working. The turn is that the wound is self-inflicted as much as inflicted — the chorus admits he is the one still gripping the grudge and nursing what it costs him, so the plea for solitude is aimed at his own resentment as much as the person. By the bridge that composure detonates into pure invective, and the final unraveling of the title into an unpunctuated scream is the point: leave me alone repeated past meaning until it stops being a request and becomes the only way out. Titled for the Kern County community across the river from Bakersfield, it plants that severance in named soil rather than abstraction.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the plea for solitude aimed as much at his own grudge","built":"the title repeats past meaning until it stops being a request"},
"korn~one": {"haiku":"Isolation and singular suffering define the self; numbness and disconnection become the only familiar truth.","opus":"This cover carries the harrowing vision, drawn from Metallica, of a soldier maimed by a landmine into total sensory imprisonment, unable to see, speak, or move. Trapped in a body that has become a holding cell, he can neither live nor die, breath held in a wish for death, mind screaming against a silence that stops him.","sonnet":"A cover of Metallica's adaptation of \"One\" — the body destroyed by a landmine, trapped alive in darkness without sight, speech, hearing, or limbs — is a meditation on a consciousness imprisoned in its own flesh, holding its breath and wishing for death as the only remaining form of agency.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cover of Metallica's song, and like the original it draws on Dalton Trumbo's Johnny Got His Gun: a soldier so mutilated by a mine that only consciousness survives, fed by tubes with no sight, speech, hearing, or limbs. The horror is not death but its opposite, a mind fully awake inside flesh that can register nothing, so a plea for waking curdles into a plea for release. It builds by narrowing, the early blur of dream and real hardening verse by verse into a final inventory of everything taken. That the appeal goes to God rather than a doctor is the cruelty, since no one there can answer, and the only mercy left to imagine is the switch that shuts the machines down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","suicide","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"consciousness fully awake inside flesh that can register nothing","built":"the appeal goes to God where no one can answer and no mercy is possible"},
"korn~overture-or-obituary": {"haiku":"Uncertainty whether life is beginning or ending; betrayal and regret about choices.","opus":"A brooding reflection from someone caught between hope and defeat, betrayed even as he offers peace, unsure whether the moment ahead marks a fresh start or the end. The image of a fortress with flimsy doors captures his brittle guard. Korn, led by Jonathan Davis, turn regret and mortality into a coin-toss set to a hook.","sonnet":"Suspended between creation and destruction, the speaker holds a torch that melts their own hands — offering peace while being stabbed, first in line and last counted. The central question is whether whatever is unfolding is a beginning or an ending, and by the final verse the ambiguity has curdled into something darker: a relationship choked to death, regret arriving too late.","src":"haiku","fable":"Every claim the speaker makes is undercut by its own reversal, a man walking toward brightness only to dissolve, extending a truce while a blade goes into his spine. The recurring self-portrait is a fortress with armored walls and doors thin enough to tear, which captures the whole predicament: fortified against the wrong threats, exposed at the one point where entry actually happens. What reads at first as paralysis is really a failure of perception, since he can no longer tell whether the hour he inhabits opens something or seals it shut. The bridge drops the metaphors and admits the deed underneath, a throat he swears he meant to spare and a body that no longer breathes, so the wavering finally shows itself as guilt too frightened to speak its own name. The title's fork, then, is his verdict pending: does he outlast the harm he caused, or lie down beside it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","shame & repression","suicide"],"means":"guilt too frightened to speak its own name until the bridge","built":"the title holds the verdict open — overture or obituary, undecided"},
"korn~paranoid-and-aroused": {"haiku":"Psychological torment, physical sickness, and demon presence merge with arousal and voyeurism; the speaker crumbles under stress while shadows deceive and contradictory desire overwhelms rational thought.","opus":"Jonathan Davis caught in a full panic spiral, pulse faltering, imagined fiends closing in and angels lowering him into the earth, his medication both dulling and demeaning him. Terror and a jittery excitement collapse into a single unraveling state.","sonnet":"The song maps a dissociative mental breakdown — demons creeping in, reality failing, the narrator's mind sedated by medication while he oscillates between paranoia and a strange, involuntary arousal.","src":"haiku","fable":"A panic attack staged as a live entombment: the body fails organ by organ, lungs and guts giving out, while tormentors close in and rescuers lower the speaker toward the same buried finish. That collapse of opposites is the thesis, and the title's oxymoron makes it flesh, the addict's paradox where the terror is also the thrill, the sedative and the self-cut wounds serving one doomed grab at relief. The bridge sheds every image for pure raw affect, hated feelings and a world coming apart, then loops the closing couplet unresolved because a mind trapped in the spiral cannot climb out, and that stuckness is precisely the point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the terror and the thrill fused into one doomed gesture","built":"the closing couplet loops unresolved because the spiral has no exit"},
"korn~please-come-for-me": {"haiku":"The speaker drowns in depression and self-hatred, begging for intervention or destruction by something coming for them, feeling they've lost control and perspective.","opus":"Korn build a paranoid vigil, the speaker isolated and crashing, weeping oceans while some evil lurks nearby. Something assaults him yet can't fully reach him as he clings on. His cry for anything to come to him tangles yearning with terror, a pitiful reflection gazing back, the mounting refrain of an approaching threat spiraling the track into dread and exhaustion.","sonnet":"Isolation and depression reach a breaking point where the only plea is for something — anything — to arrive. The self-image in the mirror is contemptible, the depression carries its own perverse allure, and the repeated cry outward is less a prayer than a last-gasp distress signal from someone sinking past sight.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title works as a mask: it scans like a lover's summons, but the object being begged to come is the Devil, or death itself. Written in the shadow of the grief that saturates The Serenity of Suffering, the song sits in the sleepless, undone territory Jonathan Davis has always mined, where exhaustion tips into a courting of oblivion. Its craft is the reversal underneath the refrain: depression is named as ecstasy, being taken is what's pleaded for, and yet the speaker also insists it will never get to him, so the plea and the defiance are the same breath. The song turns on that contradiction rather than resolving it, and the closing chant that they are coming pulls the promised deliverance into pure dread, the invited thing arriving as something to fear.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","death & grief","anger & defiance"],"means":"depression named as ecstasy while the plea and defiance share one breath","built":"the lover's summons masks a petition to the Devil or death"},
"korn~politics": {"haiku":"Corruption pervades every human system; desire and manipulation fuel survival while genuine connection dissolves into performative dishonesty.","opus":"Disgust at how everything, sex and religion and ambition, runs on people sleeping their way to what they want, told by someone sick of the whole rotten game and refusing to engage. The title points at the corruption in all human dealing.","sonnet":"A deliberately low-information disgust at the transactional nature of power — sex, religion, and social climbing all collapse into the same corrupt game — and the speaker refuses to engage, insisting repeatedly that he doesn't want to talk about politics even as the song catalogs exactly what disgusts him about the way the world operates.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is a feint: the real arena is the trading of favors between bodies — sleeping toward access, the kiss that vanishes into a threat, the currency spent across faith, desire, and every daily errand. The hook detonates as pre-emptive refusal, and that very shutdown becomes a confession, since the narrator averts his gaze as fluently as the schemers he condemns. Davis turns the revulsion inward in the bridge, where guilt from what once passed for pleasure comes due at last, so corruption reads as a shared climate rather than someone else's crime — the singer no clean judge but another compromised player who would sooner howl the subject off the table than name his own share in it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"the narrator condemns corruption he quietly participates in","built":"the title is a feint — the real arena is the trading of favors between bodies"},
"korn~pretty": {"haiku":"Witness or perpetrator wrestling with sexual violence; violation and remorse intertwined.","opus":"A harrowing, hard-to-hear reckoning with the rape and murder of a young girl, the narrator staring at a smashed face on a bathroom floor, sickened, helpless and asking who he can even feel sorry for. It refuses to look away. Korn, the Bakersfield band whose frontman channelled real childhood horror into their music, deliver this as pure anguished protest, nu-metal at its most nakedly traumatised.","sonnet":"A song that forces a direct look at child sexual violence — the body of a young girl found destroyed, the speaker cycling through disgust and guilt and rage at the crime. The repetition of asking who to feel sorry for is not rhetorical detachment but an overwhelmed refusal to look away from something that rips the heart out.","src":"haiku","fable":"Davis wrote this after encountering the case during his stint at a coroner's office, and the song is that image forced into a vocabulary that cannot hold it. The title is the wound itself: pretty is what the child was, and it curdles into the obscene refrain hovering over a small broken body against cold tile, beauty and atrocity fused into one syllable the singer keeps involuntarily returning to. His recurring question about where grief and blame should even land is not rhetorical posturing but a mind that has genuinely fractured on the problem, unable to find any adequate object for either. The register shift near the end, where horror stops being described and turns inward as something ripping the observer's own soul loose, is the real subject: not the crime but the impossibility of witnessing it and staying intact, the guilt of the survivor left standing where the innocent one was not.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"witnessing atrocity as an injury that will not let the observer stay intact","built":"the title word fuses beauty and obscenity in a single syllable the singer cannot escape"},
"korn~prey-for-me": {"haiku":"A person's passion for someone awakens their violent impulses; they apologize for the destructive force they bring to the relationship and ultimately wish harm on their lover.","opus":"A toxic bond where the partner draws out the narrator's violence and he confesses he sometimes wishes her dead. The play on prey/pray casts the relationship as predatory, an apology offered even as he admits passion in him curdles into cruelty and he's become a hollow shell.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship told from the abuser's perspective, where desire for someone paradoxically brings out violence, and the narrator admits the passion itself is the damage — ending in resignation and departure.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's pun does the work: not pray but prey, and the song refuses to distinguish tenderness toward a lover from the appetite of a predator circling one. Davis addresses a partner whose cruelty magnetizes him, yet every verse drifts from the wish to bruise her toward the wish to bury her, each impulse relabeled as adoration and blamed on a desire he can't govern. The chorus apology is the giveaway — he grants that she draws the brutality up out of him and pins it on some emptied-out husk of a self, so remorse never hardens into a reason to quit. By the farewell he renounces nothing, merely conceding the whole arrangement is spoiled and walking off leaving it spoiled, the confession substituting for the change of heart it should have forced.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"remorse substituting for the change of heart it should have forced","built":"the title pun refuses to distinguish tenderness from predation"},
"korn~proud": {"haiku":"The speaker confronts someone who once ignored them but now claims ownership, reminding them of past dismissal and present debt.","opus":"Addressed to a parent or authority who took no pride in the singer until he became successful, exposing that their sudden attention is really ownership and gloating rather than love; the deeper read is a wounded child who never forgot being made to feel like nothing, now bitterly noting the debt is owed to him.","sonnet":"Addressed to a parent or authority figure who ignored the narrator growing up but now claims ownership over their success — the narrator seethes at the hypocrisy of someone taking pride in them only after they became worth something.","src":"haiku","fable":"A reckoning with someone whose approval materialized only once the speaker had built a self worth claiming, a parent whose old contempt has hardened into ownership dressed up as affection. The song works the title as a trap: what glows toward him is not admiration but possessiveness, a landlord's boast about a house he never helped raise. He clocks the reversal with a collector's coldness, reading the belated warmth as an invoice rather than love, unwilling to forget the years spent being told he amounted to nothing. That refusal is the whole engine — the hammering why never lands on an answer, and the missing answer becomes the point, a wound demanding to be witnessed as it was rather than smoothed over by late flattery. The last fractured line, where triumph is meant to feel earned and instead just goes hollow, is the bedrock the entire grievance stands on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"belated parental pride read as an invoice rather than love","built":"the missing answer to the hammered why becomes the wound's evidence"},
"korn~punishment-time": {"haiku":"Someone endures relentless self-inflicted suffering and punishment, describing mental anguish with no escape while repeating a cycle of pain and resignation.","opus":"Korn, the Bakersfield band who built a genre out of anguish, cast mere survival as ongoing torture. Every action the narrator takes feels like a sentence being served, fighting on with no future in sight and his sanity slipping. Jonathan Davis renders endless self-punishment as breathless, grinding endurance.","sonnet":"A mind caught in a relentless cycle of giving without receiving, straining to survive without any visible horizon, loses its grip on present and future alike. Every move carries consequence, every effort extracts a toll, and something internal keeps erasing the accumulated damage so the ordeal begins again. The exhaustion is existential rather than circumstantial.","src":"haiku","fable":"Korn stage existence as a standing verdict, the speaker balanced on a honed edge where each cautious footfall is entered against him like a fresh count in a trial that never adjourns. The martyr props — hung upside down, nailed under a sign, iron points working up through the mattress one by one — recast agony as a role he steps into deliberately, penance he administers to himself rather than harm arriving from outside. Collapsing tomorrow and today into one airless clause, the chorus strips away any horizon that might let the punishment end. Beneath it runs the confession that he pours himself out and receives nothing, driving toward death because some buried mechanism keeps erasing his memory of ever having earned better, and the final unraveling is simply the sentence being accepted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"penance administered to the self rather than harm arriving from outside","built":"martyr props — inverted body, iron points — recast agony as a role entered deliberately"},
"korn~reclaim-my-place": {"haiku":"Overcoming childhood bullying to reclaim dignity and force respect from past tormentors.","opus":"A defiant reassertion of self from someone once known as a bullied, weak outsider, now demanding his place and daring anyone to test his hate, his heart beating down toward a triumphant bow-down chant. Old wounds fuel present menace. Korn, the Bakersfield nu-metal originators, revisit the outcast rage that defined them, Jonathan Davis turning childhood torment into a snarling, self-possessed reclamation of ground.","sonnet":"A person who was bullied into numbness and then found refuge in a band, only to face rejection there too — the wound of being a freak who never fit anywhere sharpens into defiant anger. Reclaiming a place is the whole project: erasing the disgrace, stepping back into authority, demanding to be reckoned with.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's cruel joke is that the band was supposed to be the escape hatch — the weak, friendless kid got saved by getting into it — and instead it became one more schoolyard where he's picked on, so the trauma he wrote his way out of simply reconstitutes inside the thing that rescued him. Davis works the outsider's two settings against each other: the numb, self-erasing depressive who can't relate or even summon hatred, and the cornered animal who flips to a taunting dare, daring the tormentors to imagine his wound is legible and then shoving his own face forward as the only proof they'll get. Reclaiming his place, then, isn't triumph but a refusal to be moved — the vow that nothing will ever bend him arrives as both a threat and a life sentence. That the whole thing collapses into a stammered, incredulous obscenity is the real tell: the aggression never resolves the injury, it just circles back to disbelief that the abuse followed him even here.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming"],"means":"the escape hatch becomes the next schoolyard of persecution","built":"the collapse into stammered obscenity shows aggression never resolving the injury"},
"korn~right-now": {"haiku":"Intense rage and destructive impulses overwhelm the speaker, turning self-hatred outward toward others.","opus":"A raw eruption of rage and self-loathing, the narrator relishing his own hatred and fantasizing about slashing and beating someone he despises; the turn inward, where he decides it's time to bleed and cut himself, exposes that the violence is as much self-directed as outward, an uncontrollable spiral of hate.","sonnet":"A pure expression of explosive, barely-controlled rage toward a specific person — the narrator catalogues violent fantasies in real time, from slashing and beating to cutting themselves just to feel something, and it escalates to a point where articulation breaks down entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tantrum that admits, in its own opening breath, that its fury has no route outward toward the person it targets, so every threat lands as evidence of a rupture the speaker cannot bridge with anyone. The revealing pivot is how the wrath keeps curving back on its owner. A promise to maim someone else swerves into a razor turned on his own arm, until enemy and speaker collapse into a single wound. That relentless silencing chant, first aimed across the room and then at himself, names the true antagonist: not a rival at all but an interior static he cannot switch off, which is why the collapse of restraint, not any actual grievance, stands as the song's confessed subject.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","madness & the mind","suicide"],"means":"outward rage folding back onto the interior static it cannot silence","built":"the silencing chant shifts from aimed across the room to aimed at himself"},
"korn~rotting-in-vain": {"haiku":"Parasitic need for another's pain to sustain oneself. The narrator feeds on suffering but grows weaker as the victim's pain fades.","opus":"Davis inhabits a predatory, self-feeding cycle where another's suffering sustains his own, agony he can't dig out from. The scat-sung breakdown voices the wordless frustration underneath, a portrait of being trapped rotting inside repeating pain.","sonnet":"A song about being trapped in a cycle of pain and repulsion that feels both self-generated and fed by the suffering around the speaker. Each day passes without escape, the agony digging deeper while the speaker screams into an indifferent sky.","src":"haiku","fable":"Davis writes from inside a parasite: a speaker who feeds on another person's tears and torment, whose suffering only fades when he can drain someone else's, so that the demand to keep hurting is dressed up as intimacy and need. The horror of the verses is that this is spoken tenderly, in the grammar of a lover who would be calm if only you would cry — the mask is affection and what is underneath is a vampire's supply chain. That reading pays off in the structure: the chorus keeps trying to name the exit, the digging past agony, and each time snags on the same wall, the escape named and never made. The bridge is where the mask finally slips entirely, language failing into pure glossolalic scat around fragments of hate and refusal — Davis's oldest trauma register, the point where the self stops making sentences and just makes noise. What is rotting in vain is not the victim but the speaker's own cyclical rage, digging deeper only to arrive back at another identical day, the addiction to inflicting pain that never actually relieves it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"the appetite for inflicting pain that never actually relieves the speaker's own","built":"the bridge dissolves into glossolalic scat where language fails and only noise remains"},
"korn~seed": {"haiku":"Shame and corruption from fame contrasted with the innocence of childhood.","opus":"A father staring at his infant child and seeing everything he can no longer be, the carefree innocence fame has stripped from him, wondering at night whether the only thing he has left is worth the pressure crushing him. Recognizing his own face in the baby's turns self-loathing into something almost redemptive. Korn, fronted by Jonathan Davis, voice the exhaustion of celebrity in their raw nu-metal idiom.","sonnet":"A rock star's crisis of identity refracted through looking at his own child — seeing in that small face the freedom and innocence he traded away for fame, which he no longer believes in but cannot stop clinging to. The child becomes a mirror that shows back who he used to be, and what he sees makes the current version of himself unbearable.","src":"haiku","fable":"A new father looks at his infant and finds an indictment: the child is beautiful and carefree in exactly the way he has lost, so the seed becomes a measure of everything fame and self-loathing have hollowed out of him. Jonathan Davis makes the song circle the same question a bedridden man asks in the dark — whether to keep going the only way he knows — and never answers it, because the point is the paralysis, not a resolution. Everything turns on the bridge, where the innocent face he has been mourning collapses into his own: the child and the ruined adult are the same seed at two ends of a life, and the horror is recognizing that the freak was always the baby grown up.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","alienation & emptiness","suicide"],"means":"the infant becomes a measure of everything fame has hollowed out","built":"the bridge collapses the child's face into the ruined adult — same seed, two ends of a life"},
"korn~shoots-and-ladders": {"haiku":"Nursery rhymes mask hidden violence and darkness taught to children, embedding cruelty in innocence and tradition.","opus":"The buried menace inside nursery rhymes, taken literally: plague, hanging, collapse spoon-fed to children as innocent song. Davis exposes how much darkness we hand kids without noticing, chanting the rhymes back until they turn sinister.","sonnet":"Nursery rhymes — Ring Around the Rosies, London Bridge, Mary Had a Little Lamb — are recited in a context of childhood trauma and suggested violence, with the narrator arguing that the hidden darkness inside these innocent songs has been spoonfed into children's minds. The familiar becomes sinister through repetition and volume.","src":"haiku","fable":"Davis threads playground chants together and lets their buried freight surface, since folklore long read plague-carts and posies-against-the-stink into the Rosie verse, drowned masons into the collapsing-span song. The wager is that menace was never smuggled in later but sat in the source, so reciting the schoolyard canon straight becomes an accusation against what adults hand down. His delivery corrodes on purpose: the counting-game and fleece-white refrain fracture, stutter and jam mid-syllable, staging a mind where inherited singsong has curdled into a loop. That refusal to resolve is the whole argument, dramatizing an early wound the band's sound keeps trying and failing to speak aloud.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"childhood singsong as the delivery system for inherited menace","built":"the schoolyard chants fracture and stutter mid-syllable staging a mind where they have curdled"},
"korn~somebody-someone": {"haiku":"Someone feels used and belittled by a friend who took without giving back, now desperate for genuine love and acceptance rather than betrayal.","opus":"A raw cry of loneliness and betrayal from someone who gave everything to a supposed friend and got nothing back, feeling like a worthless fool; the desperate refrain begging for somebody, anybody, to love him just for himself lays bare a bottomless need to be seen and valued.","sonnet":"A raw cry for unconditional love from someone who feels invisible and depleted — the narrator has given endlessly without reciprocation and is sinking in worthlessness, desperate for someone to simply see and love them as they are.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the exhausting arithmetic of a one-sided relationship, where the speaker keeps giving and gets nothing back, and reads that deficit as proof he is worthless rather than proof the other person is using him. The refrain's plea to be loved just for me is the tell: he suspects that every bit of affection he's received was transactional, contingent on what he provides, so the fantasy isn't romance but simple recognition of his bare self. Davis's writing across Issues characteristically routes self-hatred through betrayal, and the bridge collapses the two — the stammered I'm dying repeated over the image of being stuck in filth turns the outward complaint inward, so that the closing chant for somebody is less a call to another person than the sound of a self dissolving into pure need.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"transactional affection read as proof of worthlessness rather than exploitation","built":"the bridge collapses outward betrayal into the speaker dissolving into pure need"},
"korn~spike-in-my-veins":{"haiku":"Expresses internal torment and the drive to harness pain as power, refusing to be stopped by suffering or the judgment of others.","opus":"Pain, rage and self-destruction under the pressure of doing what others demand, with the narrator lashing out, feeling insane and dangerous while pounding spikes into his veins.","sonnet":"The narrator describes being driven by pain and a sense of betrayal while refusing to give in or be stopped, channelling rage and suffering into a relentless forward push against obstacles and external control.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's grammar quietly betrays it: verses speak as a wounded collective — bruised, obedient, praised for compliance, grieving against handlers who feel nothing — but the chorus retreats into a single walled-off voice that warns the whole world off. The addressee stays deliberately blurred, both a person who makes the speaker feel alive and the drug doing exactly that, and that blur is the argument. On The Paradigm Shift, Jonathan Davis frames the needle less as flight than as the only gesture left once collective fury has nowhere to land. So the hammering refrain becomes cure and beating at once, defiance masking a narrator who calmly watches himself go under. The loop swells rather than settling because the power seized here is really just the last motion available, repeated since nothing remains to replace it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance"],"means":"the needle blurred with the beloved until only the blur remains","built":"collective verses collapse to one walled-off chorus pronoun","fnote":"Davis says it isn't a drug song: the spike is the media-and-surveillance hit injected into everyone — his example was Miley's VMA night burying the NDAA signing — and the video splices Snowden into the news-cycle blur."},
"korn~start-the-healing": {"haiku":"Attempting to heal someone from anger and pain, but trauma keeps resurfacing.","opus":"A summons toward recovery that never claims it's simple, promising to lift the hurt and begin mending while confessing that something keeps clawing back and tempting him to relapse. Hope and backsliding share the space. Korn, the Bakersfield band who pioneered nu-metal's confessional weight, frame this later-era plea as a hard-earned turn toward the light, the rage still present but no longer the entire story.","sonnet":"An offer and a struggle — acknowledging the anger and fear that keeps cycling back in, but proposing that the feelings can be broken apart and released rather than endured forever. The speaker is not triumphant; they keep failing to hold the line, yet still believe the healing is possible if both people are willing to face the scars.","src":"haiku","fable":"Recovery arrives as a threat disguised as an invitation, Davis daring a companion to walk into wreckage with him rather than promising any gentleness waiting on the far side. The lure to prowl and to pretend at relief masks a surgical demand: wounds must reopen and the invaders be dug out before anything closes over. His choruses collapse into self-prosecution, a stack of unmet obligations where the vow to erase every feeling stands beside the confession that some intruder keeps forcing itself back inside. The outro diagnoses the con precisely, since surrendering to the hope that mending will hold only lodges it deeper, sticking harder and refusing ever to quit. Conceived as a requiem, the song treats its own title as an endless conscription rather than a cure, an order to begin again with no finish line anywhere in sight.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","death & grief"],"means":"recovery staged as a threat requiring wounds to reopen before anything closes","built":"the title treated as endless conscription rather than cure — no finish line anywhere"},
"korn~surrender-to-failure": {"haiku":"Profound grief over someone's death, rage at God, powerlessness to undo tragedy.","opus":"A grief-wracked howl of loss and helplessness, the singer alone in the dark certain that God stole someone from him and set demons loose to seize their reward, ready to do anything to reverse the clock. It ends crumpled in repeated admissions of defeat. Korn, the Bakersfield nu-metal band, root it in raw bereavement, Jonathan Davis's voice bearing the weight of a man who tried everything and still lost.","sonnet":"Grief so total it reads as collapse — the speaker lost someone, feels the loss as demonic, and cannot escape the haunting. Every good deed comes with a price, every attempt to hide fails, and the final word is not defiance but admission: I failed, again and again, and I cannot undo what God or fate already decided.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor's grief hardens into a theology of blame, where the speaker treats a loved one's death as an invoice he was made to settle, his kindnesses tallied as charges God arrived to collect. What lifts it past mourning is the guilt logic beneath: he insists the loss was inflicted rather than earned, yet still shoulders the bill, so the repeated confession of failure reads not as ordinary defeat but as a sentence he passes on himself, the final term of a deal no rewound clock can void. Written by Jonathan Davis in the wreckage after his wife died, the loosed tormentors and the demanded payment aren't decorative figures; they are the exact contour of fury aimed at a deity who seizes and seizes while no one remains across the table to bargain.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"grief theology where loss arrives as a divine invoice to settle","built":"guilt logic makes the survivor both debtor and self-sentenced judge"},
"korn~take-me": {"haiku":"A toxic relationship where codependency binds two people in cycles of harm and control, with the speaker needing the other person despite the damage inflicted.","opus":"A portrait of toxic codependency as addiction, the narrator and his partner locked as disease and remedy, each insisting they're worthless without the other, hunting each other down every time one runs away until reality itself blurs and scars. Korn are the Bakersfield band who shaped nu-metal, and this leans into their detuned menace and Jonathan Davis's tortured delivery, dramatizing a bond that consumes everything it touches while pleading to be taken.","sonnet":"Addiction addressed in the second person — the substance becomes the speaker, and the user becomes the one being addressed. You came to me, you need me, I'm the only one that keeps you sound. The song maps dependency with uncomfortable clarity: the addict cannot leave because the drug hunts them back, cannot blame themselves because the pull is total. The final I feel it calling spirals into the admission that it's taking everything, the escape hatch already gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses are sung by the wrong speaker on purpose: the confident I courting a beloved is the addiction itself, ventriloquized so the drug does the wooing. Korn stack its promises like a suitor's boasts — it lifted him when he was low, dulls his nerves, holds him together, is the single reason he feels like anyone at all. The bridge finally hands the microphone to the human, who names the ripping and scarring and the way it drags off everything he loves, blinding him to whatever is real. By the time he begs the thing to claim him, resistance has collapsed into consent, so the you and the I read as one mind at war with its own craving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"the addict's consent achieved by letting the drug speak first","built":"verses ventriloquize the substance before handing the mic to the human"},
"korn~the-darkness-is-revealing": {"haiku":"Confrontation with inner shadows during emotional crisis: suppressed pain surfaces as the speaker faces past trauma, seeking healing but fearing what must be broken open to begin recovery.","opus":"Korn frame release as a wound torn open again. Something hidden festers until it's all laid bare, and the narrator wonders whether the pain marks a fresh start or just the settling of old accounts. Tearing off the dressing stings, yet the exposure delivers a hard truth he actually needs, Davis's confessional voice pointed, unusually, toward healing.","sonnet":"A man strips away the layers he hid behind and confronts the pain he has been avoiding, recognising for the first time that exposure — however agonising — is itself the form of healing he needs. The darkness, when finally faced rather than fled, turns out to contain something bittersweet: the beginning of recovery, not the end of everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"Grief reframed as a wound that can only close by being reopened: Davis casts the dark as brutal medicine rather than an enemy, arguing that recovery demands tearing the dressing off the numbed nerves underneath instead of leaving them bound and quietly festering. The curtain, a recurring Korn figure for the guarded interior self, is drawn back so what was hidden now bleeds in the open, and the cruel twist is that this exposure is precisely what he keeps requesting even as it flays him. That fragile resolve buckles in the bridge, where a fallen light-bearer, Lucifer read as the dawn-planet that heralds day, closes in and composure dissolves into unmoored terror with no direction left to flee. The final chant to get out reverses everything: the same dark he named as cure he now tries to expel, so the track ends unhealed, trapped between craving the pain that heals and being unable to withstand it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","shame & repression","faith & the occult","madness & the mind"],"means":"dark named as cure then fled from in the same breath","built":"the final chant reverses the opening argument, leaving the track unhealed"},
"korn~the-devil-went-down-to-georgia": {"haiku":"Classic folk tale: a young man bests the Devil in a musical contest to save his soul.","opus":"A cover of the Charlie Daniels tale where a cocky young fiddler named Johnny out-plays the Devil himself for a golden fiddle and his own soul, all Southern swagger and Appalachian folklore. Korn, the Bakersfield nu-metal band led by Jonathan Davis, drag this country-rock story into their detuned aggression, an unexpected genre collision that trades barn-dance bravado for metallic snarl while keeping the boast intact.","sonnet":"The old Southern folk wager retold straight — a boy named Johnny accepts a soul bet with the devil, plays his fiddle against hellfire and demon accompaniment, and wins decisively. Pride is the engine on both sides: the devil's in the opening, Johnny's in every note he plays, and the final taunt makes clear that in this story, skill and nerve beat supernatural power clean.","src":"haiku","fable":"Charlie Daniels' 1979 tall tale stakes the metaphysical on pure craft: the Devil, behind on his quota and desperate, wagers a fiddle cast in gold against a boy's soul and loses, because homegrown virtuosity simply outplays hellfire. The joke of the story is that salvation arrives not through piety but through cocky mastery, which is why Johnny brands his own boast a possible sin yet takes the wager anyway. Korn covering it turns the fable inward: a band whose entire idiom is the abject and the tormented, the guy who keeps losing to his own demons, picks up the one song where the underdog wins clean and plays it straight-faced. The swagger then reads two ways at once, as genuine triumph or as the fantasy of someone who has never truly beaten anything, and the closing dare keeps the contest permanently unresolved.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Korn's 2020 single splits the tale's two voices between Davis and guest Yelawolf — the Devil and Johnny cast as an actual duet, the contest performed rather than narrated.","themes":["faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"virtuosity as salvation, swagger read as possible sin anyway","built":"Korn playing the one song where the underdog wins straight-faced"},
"korn~the-game-is-over": {"haiku":"Rebellion against systemic oppression through willingness to die for liberation. The speaker claims readiness to sacrifice themselves to topple corrupt power.","opus":"A defiant uprising against a tyrannical system and the figure who personifies it, the singer denying any heroism and vowing he'd give his life just to see his foe collapse. It runs on undiluted fury at a power structure he abandoned faith in long before and now intends to bring crashing down.","sonnet":"The narrator has a single obsessive target — a man he envisions destroying — and frames his mission as liberation from a corrupt system he's done performing for, willing to die to see this enemy fall.","src":"haiku","fable":"A revenge fantasy dressed as insurrection: the speaker refuses any hero and keeps only a vision, swearing an escalating oath to overthrow a system, though the manifesto keeps narrowing toward one target rather than a regime. The dream sequence exposes the trick, since the kingdom he vows to raze and rebuild shrinks to a single man he wants gone, an old grudge finally cornered behind every door he promises to break. The crowd he claims to champion is one he confesses to abusing, an audience mistaken for a cause. Its power comes from repetition, the identical vow looping until the announced defeat sounds less like triumph than like someone unable to stop replaying the fight.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","madness & the mind"],"means":"insurrection manifesto that keeps shrinking to a single grudge","built":"looping oath narrows the promised regime-overthrow to one cornered man"},
"korn~the-hating":{"haiku":"Rage and emotional exhaustion trap the speaker—controlled by forces beyond their grasp, their childhood torn away, their fury turned inward.","opus":"Jonathan Davis excavating the abuse-formed child inside the man, hatred turned inward and then outward at the very hand that feeds him, the dependency that keeps him bound to what wounds him. Korn's core subject: rage with no clean exit.","sonnet":"About the destructive cycle of internalized rage that turns inward and outward simultaneously — the narrator is consumed by hatred and inner decay, feeling controlled and fed by a system that exploits them, breaking down into a fractured, violent emotional core.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song circles one unbearable knot: the hand that nourishes is the hand being cursed, so every act of aggression loops back onto the one committing it. Jonathan Davis, whose writing has always mined a specific childhood wound, builds the verses from gnawed present-tense gerunds — cheating, holding, rotting, spurting — that keep the violence trapped in an eternal now instead of resolving into narrative or assigning blame. The chorus is where the mask slips: the swaggering nu-metal menace caves into plain confession of isolation and inner dismemberment, a snarling delivery that only ever covers for the grief seeping beneath it. And the outro's refusal to conclude — identical lines cycling as the inner child is wrenched loose — treats the trap itself as the meaning, resentment turned inward because it has nowhere to travel when the aggressor and the caretaker are one person.","fv":2,"themes":["family & blood","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"aggression that has nowhere to travel when the target is the caretaker","built":"gerunds trap the violence in an eternal now instead of narrative"},
"korn~the-ringmaster": {"haiku":"Manipulation and psychological abuse disguised as comfort; dark forces preying on vulnerability.","opus":"A sinister carnival barker figure lures the sad and the hurting closer with false comfort while the narrator feels a darkness embracing him, the taunting inner voice poking and yelling at him without end. The plot never resolves, its meaning forever out of reach. Korn, Jonathan Davis's nu-metal originators, cast mental illness as a ringmaster here, the intrusive voice made into a leering master of ceremonies.","sonnet":"A manipulative voice masquerading as comfort — approaching people in visible pain, offering understanding, while the speaker underneath is themselves haunted by a darkness they can't shed. The internal monologue is brutal: a voice in the head that pokes and yells and destabilizes, meaning the ringmaster performing concern may be indistinguishable from the very torment being offered as relief.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track stages a duet between two voices that resolve into a single fractured mind. The verses are the oily patter of a fairground barker — dressing the listener as brother, child, dear — coaxing a downcast stranger nearer with a soft vow to smooth every hurt away, the flattering tone of a depression that talks its host into never leaving. Against that croon the chorus is the sufferer himself, feeling the intruder press close and the darkness fold its arms around him, aching to slough it off yet failing, the point of the whole ordeal dissolving into a chant of never arriving anywhere. Davis built The Nothing in the wreckage of his wife's death, casting the ringmaster as grief costumed like a con artist working the crowd; the mid-song break rips off the greasepaint and names the head-voice that watches, prods, screams, and refuses to quit — the tender host revealed as the torturer. Solace and savagery wear one skin, which is why the narrator can neither turn down the tenderness nor endure it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind","artifice & performance"],"means":"depression costumed as a tender fairground barker","built":"the mid-song break strips the greasepaint to expose the torturer beneath"},
"korn~the-seduction-of-indulgence": {"haiku":"Deliberately seeking violation and punishment as twisted pleasure.","opus":"A disturbing surrender to violation, the narrator crossing a line he calls a risk, unprotected and confined, inviting torture he frames as divine before the track dissolves into a relentless litany of being skinned, stabbed and raped. It reads as trauma voiced from inside the harm. Korn, led by Jonathan Davis, push their nu-metal into its most unsettling register, indulgence and self-destruction fused into one raw incantation.","sonnet":"A dissociative descent into violation — the speaker crosses a line with full awareness and then becomes the object of increasingly violent invasion, cycling through escalating physical transgressions with no resolution or escape. The framing of torture as divine and risk as chosen gives it the texture of compulsion dressed as consent, a surrender that isn't really willing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A brief interlude on The Nothing that stages surrender as a seduction: the speaker steps across a line he knows he shouldn't, announcing he is determined not to win, and invites the violence to come do its worst. The title names the trap exactly — indulgence dressed as intimacy, a torture reframed as divine so the victim can pretend he chose it. Davis, writing the record in the wreckage of grief and old trauma, lets the song collapse into a mounting litany of assaults that abandons the seductive first-person poise entirely and just repeats, faster and flatter, until meaning drains out of the words. That refusal to develop is the point: the pull toward self-destruction doesn't argue or build, it only accelerates and consumes, and the horror is in watching the come-hither mask fall away to leave nothing but the mechanical loop of harm.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"self-destruction reframed as chosen intimacy until the frame collapses","built":"the seductive first-person dissolves into a mechanical loop as the mask falls"},
"korn~this-loss": {"haiku":"Chronic grief that prevents happiness; loss so deep that joy becomes impossible.","opus":"A study in relapse and grief where everything loved keeps getting stripped away, the narrator reduced to a shadow of himself, half-savoring his own breakdown. The chorus grabs compulsively at something already gone, and the bridge lands on the bleak verdict that happiness is a club he'll never be let into. Korn's later work leaned hard into addiction and mental-health confessions, and this is squarely that lineage.","sonnet":"Grief as a parasite that devours every attempt at joy — the speaker watches himself unravel with a kind of nauseated clarity, convinced that everything worth having gets taken back and that happiness is a club permanently closed to him. The craving to reclaim what was lost loops obsessively, never resolving into recovery.","src":"haiku","fable":"Grief written as an addiction to itself. The Nothing came out of Jonathan Davis losing his wife, and this song refuses the consolation that mourning heals; instead it catches the narrator watching his own breakdown with a sick fascination, finding the collapse almost pleasurable even as it hollows him into a shadow. The engine is the chant that hijacks the back half, a compulsive plea to reclaim what death stole, which first means the person and the joy that vanished, then curdles into pure craving, wanting the wanting. Against that the bridge floats a false exit, the recognition that he has held this tragedy too long and should be free, only to slam it shut with the verdict that peace is a room he will never be let into. The refusal is the point: he mistakes the loop for love and lets it swallow every joy so he never has to stop grieving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"mourning mistaken for love and refused to be let go","built":"bridge offers a false exit then slams it shut with a locked verdict"},
"korn~thoughtless": {"haiku":"The narrator harbors violent revenge fantasies against someone who hurt him through mockery and abuse. He fantasizes about retaliation while trapped in a cycle of rage and rage-induced self-harm.","opus":"Jonathan Davis converting the wound of being mocked and violated into a fantasy of turning the power back on the tormentor. It's about hate that can't be contained, humiliation curdling into a vow of retaliation that refuses to be beaten down.","sonnet":"A survivor of relentless bullying and mockery imagines turning the violence back on their tormentors — the fury is specific and personal, describing the fantasy of making those who hurt him suffer the same humiliation and pain.","src":"haiku","fable":"A revenge fantasy narrated by the tormented, this turns the schoolyard scene of being mocked into a private theater where the victim scripts the payback his real life cannot deliver. The chorus is the hinge: hatred is not renounced but hidden, driven down until it becomes something the bully can no longer locate or name, which is exactly why it can no longer be stopped. Davis frames the retaliation in deliberately unbearable terms, borrowing his abuser's violence back at them, so the daydream indicts itself as much as its target. The late shift to bodies against the wall and dead friends widens the wound past one bully into the accumulated damage that fuels it, and the refusal to be drowned becomes the only self-assertion left. It reads not as triumph but as the closed loop of someone rehearsing power he only ever holds inside his own head.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"revenge rehearsed inside the head where the bully can't locate it","built":"hidden hatred named as precisely what makes it unstoppable"},
"korn~trash": {"haiku":"Someone is emotionally cruel and unable to feel genuine connection, exploiting others' feelings while despising them. They compulsively hurt their lover despite having a conscience that torments them.","opus":"Korn embody a self-disgusted predator who cannot abandon his appetite even as guilt gnaws at him, fleeing from lovers who refuse to leave. The nu-metal band voice a brutal, merciless confession of coldness and cruelty, loathing each moment yet finding no way to ease the hurt except by discarding another person.","sonnet":"Self-loathing and compulsive cruelty mapped together. The narrator is aware he exploits and discards people, that his heart runs on hatred while his body seeks contact. He cannot reconcile the appetite with the conscience that condemns it, and the result is a loop of taking and throwing away with neither pleasure nor relief.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person confession from inside a compulsion the speaker despises even as he obeys it: he cheats, discards, and calls the discarded person garbage, framing appetite as something done to him rather than by him. What makes it more than a shock-piece is that the self-loathing is the real engine. He keeps asking why he even has a conscience, treating guilt as one more torment to be fucked away, so the cruelty and the remorse feed each other in a loop with no exit. On Issues, Korn's rawest confessional record, that loop is the point: the apology and the abuse are stapled to the same breath, and the closing repetition of throwing someone away enacts the mechanical, deadened repeat of the behavior itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"remorse and cruelty feeding each other in a loop with no exit","built":"closing repetition enacts the mechanical deadened recurrence of the behavior itself"},
"korn~twisted-transistor": {"haiku":"A lonely person finds salvation and meaning through music, which reaches inside them and provides solace. The song urges persistence through suffering, affirming that music transcends isolation.","opus":"Music itself is the refuge for a lonely, misunderstood person, personified as a twisted transistor that reaches inside, preaches defiance, and helps them hang on in an unfair world.","sonnet":"The song is addressed to an outcast who feels lonely and misunderstood, telling her that music is the one thing that truly reaches inside and sustains you when the world is indifferent — hold on to it, because it does understand.","src":"haiku","fable":"A come-on aimed at a lonely, misunderstood girl, spoken as if the transistor radio itself were the seducer promising that the music will save her where no one else does. But Korn keep twisting the wire: the same voice that tells her to hang on and not give up also preaches fuck-you and turns her scream into a whisper, so the rescue is really a swallowing — the device that soothes is the device that consumes. The song lands its hand when the tender radio-counselor gives way to a clinical, sedating voice insisting this won't hurt and moving to anesthetize her, exposing the whole overture as the addict's bargain the band knew intimately: the thing that understands you when nothing else does is also the thing that numbs you out. Under the mask of a love song to music, it reads as music-as-narcotic, comfort and self-erasure carried on the same signal.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The lifeline reading is band-confirmed — the radio as the friend that raised you — and Davis has also copped to the title's cruder double meaning riding underneath, the smirk soldered into the sincerity.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"comfort and self-erasure carried on the same signal","built":"tender radio-counselor gives way to a sedating clinical voice mid-song"},
"korn~victimized": {"haiku":"Collective deception and mental collapse: society imposes false values and hollow meaning while a dark veil of insanity feeds on collective disease, victimizing all through constructed lies.","opus":"Korn condemn an entire programmed way of living as empty. Existence is branded counterfeit, ambitions reduced to sickly illusion, as the narrator tallies how often his hopes were crushed under obedience to selfish deceit. A suffocating madness bears down, yet the repeated cry for liberation pushes back, framing mass deception as the thing to escape.","sonnet":"A collective voice names the structures — hypocrisy, false idealism, manufactured consent — that hollow out genuine life and replace it with controlled fantasy. Dreams are smited, hopes are misguided, and a black veil of collective delusion advances. The only exit imagined is being set free, though the song offers no map toward that release.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track stages a person interrogating their own defeat, hammering rhetorical questions about how often they were misled and how often their hopes were struck down until the counting itself becomes the wound. Its central image is a shroud of madness that both smothers and lies, a covering that presses down like flame scorching every sickness inside while steering the sufferer wrong, so ruin and false comfort arrive wearing the same cloth. Korn frames a supposed inner liberty as worthless when the same voice still obeys orders it despises, naming that obedience a self-serving pretense that hollows out any claim to autonomy. The song then abandons argument entirely and collapses into chanted demands for release, each cry louder than the last, until the plea for escape sounds identical to the terror it means to outrun and the wish to be loosed becomes only the same captivity screamed at higher volume.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","freedom & escape","shame & repression"],"means":"a smothering shroud that lies and soothes while steering wrong","built":"argument abandoned entirely as chanted demands collapse into the captivity they meant to escape"},
"korn~way-too-far": {"haiku":"Paranoia and irrational anxiety overwhelm the speaker; they spiral, overthink, and push situations to extremes beyond reason.","opus":"Korn dramatize an anxiety spiral tipping into paranoia, where intrusive voices convince the narrator that everyone is conspiring against him and must be punished. He admits his reactions run to excess and lack any brake, the track charting how obsessive thought distorts reality into something warped and hostile with the band's usual psychological rawness.","sonnet":"Paranoid thinking recognized as its own trap. The voices insisting everyone schemes and steals feel real enough to act on, yet the speaker is aware, at least intermittently, that these are irrational amplifications — the admission that no bounds exist is both confession and warning about the next spiral already forming.","src":"haiku","fable":"A paranoid episode staged from the inside, where the little voices arrive on cue and talk the speaker into a persecution complex: everyone is scheming, so someone must be made to pay. The song's real move is the chorus, which pulls the camera back and lets the speaker diagnose himself — the accusation that the world is out to get him is reframed as his own inability to stop, the trying too hard, the having no bounds. What sounds like an outward threat turns out to be an internal one, and the wordless whooping between the verses is the mania itself running past the point where language can hold it. Korn's signature here is that self-awareness offers no exit: he can name the pattern precisely and still be carried way too far by it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind"],"means":"self-awareness that diagnoses the pattern yet cannot stop the spiral","built":"wordless whooping between verses performs the mania running past language"},
"korn~what-we-do": {"haiku":"People fake their way through relationships built on lies and recklessness, concealing pain behind deception. Anger and betrayal fester beneath the facade.","opus":"A portrait of a dishonest, decaying relationship the couple keeps faking their way through—'that's what we do'; hate mixed with daily love, dangerous lies, and reckless lives, the narrator hears the anger of a helpless, ignored stranger (perhaps himself) and waits for the lies to finally break down.","sonnet":"Two people trapped in a toxic pattern — lying, faking, and failing each other — while knowing it's destroying them both; the speaker feels the other's pain as their own but can't break the cycle of mutual self-deception.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of a bond that runs on mutual harm and calls the arrangement normal. The hinge is the couplet where hating and loving fuse into one everyday act: this is not a relationship souring but one that was always built on the two feelings being indistinguishable, and the reckless/dangerous refrain reframes that damage as a shared identity rather than a problem — faking your way through is offered flatly as simply what we do, an ethos, not a confession of failure. The late bridge is where Korn's confessional habit turns the mirror: the speaker claims to hear a helpless, angry stranger no one listens to, then admits he feels that stranger's pain whenever the two of them are together — the outsider he pities is himself, and the compassion curdles instantly into the wish to see the other broken down. That whiplash from empathy to cruelty is the real subject; the song's honesty is that it never resolves the two, letting the hate he gives be the same gesture as the love.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"hate and love fused into one everyday act presented as an ethos","built":"empathy curdles to cruelty in a single bridge whiplash with no resolution"},
"korn~when-you-re-not-there": {"haiku":"A person loves someone who hurts them and doesn't care, finding their dark twisted love expressed through screaming. The relationship is torture and addiction.","opus":"Korn confess a dark, twisted love that thrives on being hurt and uncared for, screaming in the absence of a tormentor who feels like an unshakeable ghost. Masters of anguished nu-metal intimacy, the band turns dependency into horror, savoring the cold flesh and piercing gaze, unable to escape or reclaim the time this haunting devotion keeps stealing.","sonnet":"A bond that operates through pain becomes the only love that feels real — the cold touch, the indifferent look, the cruelty that paradoxically electrifies rather than repels. When the source of that damage disappears, what remains is a screaming absence. Dark and twisted devotion to someone who does not care becomes the defining shape of desire.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song sung inside out: the speaker frames abuse as devotion, prizing the very things love should recoil from — being hurt, being met with indifference, the beloved's cruelty read as attention. What sounds like romance is really addiction to pain, and Korn's signature move here is to keep the voice sincere so the sickness has nowhere to hide. The pivot arrives with the ghost that can't be found and won't let the mind go: the beloved is already absent, maybe never a person at all, and the title's scream when you're not there exposes the whole thing as haunting rather than relationship. Read against Jonathan Davis's long habit of writing grief and lost figures as tormentors, the dark and twisted love plays less as a partner than as a dependency on suffering itself — mourning that has curdled into needing the wound.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief","love & desire"],"means":"devotion to absence, the wound itself the only attachment left","built":"title scream reveals the love story as haunting rather than relationship"},
"korn~word-up": {"haiku":"A celebration of music and dance as liberation and community code, where everyone participates in uninhibited joy regardless of pretense or status.","opus":"Korn drag Cameo's slick 1986 funk hit into detuned nu-metal while keeping its party spirit whole. It's still a summons to the dance floor, calling everyone to move and let loose, mocking self-important DJs who pose instead of playing. Beneath the grinding riff, the original's celebration of swagger and motion survives intact.","sonnet":"A party-starting anthem commands crowds everywhere to answer a universal signal — a code word that cuts through all pretension and unlocks pure communal movement. Fake DJs and psychological games are dismissed; only the music and the bodies responding to it matter.","src":"haiku","fable":"Lyrically this is Cameo's 1986 party anthem intact: a call to the dancefloor where word up is the shared code that summons a crowd and shrugs off the posturing DJs and cool-guy airs, insisting music plus a body in motion beats any psychological romance. The interest is entirely in the transplant — Korn drag a slick electro-funk boast into detuned seven-string sludge, so a song built on effortless swagger gets played straight-faced through the most joyless instrument the band owns. The joke and the tribute are the same gesture: the invitation to lose yourself on the dancefloor survives, but it now arrives sounding like a threat rather than a wink.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism"],"means":"dancefloor invitation that survives but arrives sounding like a threat","built":"Cameo's effortless swagger played straight through Korn's most joyless instrument"},
"korn~worst-is-on-its-way-feat-danny-brown-meechy-darko-health-remix": {"haiku":"Pervasive dread that disaster is inevitable and inescapable.","opus":"A creeping dread anthem about anxiety and dark thoughts coursing through the body, life throwing obstacles that never go his way as the narrator senses the worst approaching yet insists he deals with it, confidence overriding the sickness. Korn, the Bakersfield nu-metal pioneers, built this into a genre-crossing remix drawing rap and industrial guests, their heaviness amplifying the sense of something inescapable breathing just inside.","sonnet":"A dread that lives inside the body and cannot be outrun — something coursing through the veins, breathing in the dark, promising that the worst is still coming. The speaker acknowledges it makes them sick but holds onto a fragile override: set it aside, let confidence answer, even as the premonition keeps returning.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dread rendered as a bodily intruder: not a feared event but a tenant already lodged under the ribs, so the forecast in the title is really a diagnosis of the present. The song splits between two refrains, one helpless amid sealed corridors and rooms he cannot leave, the other a clenched mantra ordering confidence to seize the wheel. That override is the crux, landing not as recovery but as willed suppression, which is why the frightened refrain floods back the instant the coping talk stops. Built by Davis out of grief, the terror stays domestic and recursive rather than cinematic, and the bravado is a mask worn because the fear never loosens its grip.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"dread as a tenant already lodged rather than a coming event","built":"bravado refrain floods back the instant the coping talk stops"},
"korn~y-all-want-a-single": {"haiku":"Rejection of commercial demands and industry pressure; the band refuses expectation and scorns the appetite for products.","opus":"A confrontational middle finger to the music industry's demand for radio-friendly hits ('y'all want a single? fuck that'); the narrator rejects being blamed, boxed in, and made to conform, turning the refusal itself into the anthem.","sonnet":"A defiant rejection of commercial pressure and music industry expectations — the band refuses to simplify their sound for radio, calls out the stereotyping of their genre, and tells everyone who wants a clean product to go to hell.","src":"haiku","fable":"A band whose whole catalog was radio-formatted answering the demand for another chart hit by making the refusal itself the hook, buried on a greatest-hits set where the industry logic is most naked. The chorus is a two-word rejection repeated until it stops being a slogan and becomes the actual song, so the promised single collapses into a jingle for saying no. The verses widen it past commerce into the paranoid old grievance of being lumped together and blamed, then turn accusatory with the idea of paying back for everything the audience and the machine forced out of them. What sounds like a tantrum is the sharper move: it lets you have your catchy single only by making the catchiness a taunt, delivering the product and spitting on it in the same breath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"delivering the demanded product while spitting on it in the same breath","built":"refusal chorus repeated until it becomes the catchy single it refuses to be"},
"korn~you-ll-never-find-me": {"haiku":"Depression and self-destruction consuming life; complete withdrawal and emotional death.","opus":"A track about disappearing into your own head, degradation and craving pulling the narrator toward a death he half-invites while daring someone to hand over their hidden truth. The refrain admits he is lost and unfindable, not doing fine no matter how many times he says it. Korn built their whole identity on this kind of unvarnished depressive confession set to detuned riffs.","sonnet":"Dissociation as the only escape from shame and a self that keeps craving its own destruction — the speaker feels baited downward by darkness, too broken to take truth or give it, and finally declares himself untraceable, gone somewhere no one can reach. The admission at the end that he is not doing fine strips away any ambiguity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song of grief turned self-cannibalizing: the darkness here is not an outside force but an appetite the speaker feeds, doubt and pain becoming the only nourishment left after loss. Written into the bereavement that shapes The Nothing, its pivot is a bargain — a demand that the vanished other come back and hand over some withheld truth — which the chorus then answers by dissolving the demander instead. The refrain that gives the title is sung as a taunt but functions as a confession: the repetition stops meaning I am hiding and starts meaning I no longer exist to be found, the self having sunk into the very void it was pleading with. The final turn from you'll never find me to come for me is the mask slipping: the pose of unreachable withdrawal was always an invitation to be pursued, even into death.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The coda's come-for-me plea is the first strike of a motif the album keeps answering — Cold opens on it, H@rd3r chants it a dozen times over, Finally Free hands it to the dead. Four songs, one summons.","themes":["death & grief","suicide","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"withdrawal from grief that was always an invitation to be pursued","built":"the title taunt shifts meaning mid-song from hiding to no longer existing"},
"korpiklaani~beer-beer":{"haiku":"Alcohol as a constant pursuit and identity. The speaker works, fights, and exists for beer, using intoxication to be themselves.","opus":"Korpiklaani, the Finnish folk-metal band who turned drinking anthems into a signature, deliver a round-the-clock hymn to intoxication where craving structures the whole day. The narrator works, fights, and does whatever it takes for the next glass, treating drunkenness less as escape than as the only state in which he feels like his truest self. It is celebration and dependency braided into one raucous jig.","sonnet":"A man declares beer the organizing principle of his existence — he works for it, fights for it, and finds in drunkenness the only version of himself he actually wants to be. The want is bottomless; sobriety is not a state he acknowledges as legitimate.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~bring-us-pints-of-beer":{"haiku":"Journeymen have traveled the world and cannot change their nature; beer and guitars are their comfort and weapons. They only know how to live this way.","opus":"The Finnish folk-metal band, career built on drinking songs, raise a toast to the roving musician's life. Demanding more ale and shooing off anyone who abstains, the narrators cast themselves as travelers who have worn out countless roads and lost track of the places. Plain-faced and knowing just this one tune, they treat their instruments as arms and drink as solace, cheerfully romanticizing a life without roots.","sonnet":"A band of wanderers with no settled home and no interest in respectability celebrate the only life they know: endless roads, nameless towns, guitars as weapons, and beer as comfort. They do not pretend to virtue or good looks — these are simply the cards dealt to journeymen born to roam rather than settle.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~iron-fist-motorhead-cover":{"haiku":"Darkness, ghosts, and demonic forces hunt and paralyze prey with evil eye and snakebite; the imagery invokes cursed celestial phenomena and supernatural terror that pulls victims downward.","opus":"The folk-metal band's tribute to Motorhead delivers Lemmy's menacing swagger straight, an unstoppable force announcing itself as the devil's grip and inviting you to prepare to die. Pure ominous bravado.","sonnet":"A Motörhead cover: a predatory, supernatural force stalking through darkness and fear, introducing itself as the evil eye, the snakebite kiss, the devil's iron grip — death coming for you.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~koivu-ja-tahti":{"haiku":"Hardship tested across forests and wilderness binds family toward remembered home; a birch tree and star guide perseverance through impossible journeys.","opus":"A tender folk journey where the singer carries a small sister through forests and marshes toward a lost birthplace, guided by two birds and a star between the branches of a birch. Reaching home where the birch and the whole family gather, they declare war a thing of the past and peace dawning tomorrow.","sonnet":"A speaker guides a little sister through an exhausting journey back to the ancestral home, drawing on a single remembered landmark — a birch tree with two singing birds and a star in its branches — as their compass; the song ends at the homestead with the family reunited and war behind them.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~let-s-drink":{"haiku":"Celebrating a simple, rugged life rooted in natural landscape and homemade alcohol, refusing to sell out or change despite disappointment and grief.","opus":"A Finnish folk-metal ode to contented rural poverty. The narrator refuses to trade his hardscrabble homestead or unproductive land for anything better, cheerfully accepting that grief and next-morning regret are lifelong companions. His answer to all of it is the same: raise a glass and keep going.","sonnet":"A man in a small lakeside homestead with a woodshed full of homebrew refuses every temptation to sell or upgrade, choosing disappointment and morning regret as faithful companions. The barren soil and the rough life are worth keeping because they are genuinely, stubbornly his own.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~lonkkaluut":{"haiku":"Hip bones carry ancestral presence—ancient spells and incantations live in flesh and blood. The singer channels their lineage to pass eternal knowledge, sharing love and mercy across time.","opus":"A shamanic incantation about kinship of blood and bone, the singer feeling his people carried in his own hip-bones and heart-flesh, chanting ancient charms to spread warmth and love across the frozen homeland. Korpiklaani are Finnish folk-metal veterans steeped in Kalevala imagery, and this reads like a runic spell set to fiddle-driven mead-hall thunder, the old northern cosmos invoked as living kin.","sonnet":"A song of ancient belonging and incantation — crafted in joy for the tribe's own gods and the golden book of Kalevala, it asks how one knows one's kin and answers through bone and flesh: the hipbone holds the people's identity, the heart-muscle carries fate, and the singer's purpose is to chant the old words outward into the cosmos as an act of love and warmth for wanderers on frozen roads.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~louhen-yhdeks-s-poika":{"haiku":"Based on Finnish mythology, the song describes Louhi's nine cursed sons born with plague names, and her refusal to name a tenth son brought to her as a lover instead. The song plays with naming, power, and transformation through poetic incantation.","opus":"drawing on the Kalevala, the mythic naming of Louhi's nine sickness-sons (Plague, Rickets, Colic and others) and choosing love and hope over the ninth curse","sonnet":"a figure from Finnish mythology (the ninth child of Louhi) who renounces dark names and chooses love and a new beginning instead","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~louhen-yhdeksas-poika":{"haiku":"The ninth son carries ancient legacy through folk metal resurrection. Heritage pulses through modern distortion.","opus":"A Finnish folk-metal invocation drawing on Kalevala mythology, naming the nine children of the witch Louhi, eight of them diseases and the ninth left nameless. Korpiklaani turns ancient charm-lore into song, mixing herbal remedies and inherited power, the unnamed affliction finally christened hope, a new beginning.","sonnet":"Drawing from the Finnish Kalevala tradition, a healer prepares a strengthening water from six flowers and seven grasses as their forebears did — eight of Louhi's nine children bear usable names (plague, rash, ague), but the ninth has none; the healer then speaks of warm love and a new beginning, naming the nameless child Hope.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~mettaanpeiton-valtiaalle":{"haiku":"Forest lord claims the wanderer in ancient song; soul descends beneath the dark earth.","opus":"A Finnish tale of a wanderer lured into a mythic forest hiding-spell, spirited beneath the earth to the lord of the woods. Playing and singing to the ancient power under stone and oak, he loses himself for ages before finally returning home, carrying dreamlike memories and a small fear lodged in his skull.","sonnet":"A Finnish folk tale of a traveller who wanders into the forest at night singing and playing, becomes enchanted by the forest spirit and is pulled underground, loses all sense of time, and eventually emerges with only a dream-memory and a small fear — and then tells the tale of gold and times freely, without being asked.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~p-t-pois-tai-hirteen":{"haiku":"A farmer laments rural poverty and failed harvests—dead livestock, collapsing buildings, crops failing—and demands state subsidies or threatens violent uprising.","opus":"Korpiklaani rage through a collapsing farm where the field freezes, the horse dies and the mistress leaps into the silage pit, blaming a brainless, headless national leadership. The Finnish folk-metal party band swaps its usual revelry for furious rural satire, its refrain demanding heads off or to the gallows as the only fitting answer to hunger and ruin.","sonnet":"A Finnish farmer lists disaster after disaster — the horse dead, the barn collapsing, the plow rusting, the bull useless, even the wife gone — and concludes that the country's leadership is brainless. The demand is blunt: subsidies now or someone gets shot. Rural rage against institutional indifference, delivered with the rhythm of a folk curse.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~tequila":{"haiku":"A wandering drinker travels through Latin America seeking oblivion in tequila and caipirinha, living rootless and carefree rather than settling.","opus":"Korpiklaani send a wandering minstrel across the seas to unknown peoples, chasing life as tequila and caipirinha through Mexico, Argentina, Colombia and Brazil. The Finnish folk-metal band, famous for turning drink into anthem, celebrates a vagabond thirst that leaves no worry for tomorrow, raising a jubilant toast until the joy itself grows old and calls him home.","sonnet":"A wanderer circles the globe playing music and drinking through Mexico and Brazil, country after country, with no thought of tomorrow. The road runs through South America on tequila and caipirinha, performing for strangers, living entirely in the present. Eventually the world-weariness softens into a pull toward home, but the journey itself was the point.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~tuoppi-oltta":{"haiku":"Men gather thirsty and parched-mouthed, begging a woman to bring beer (tuoppi oltta); joy and drunkenness follow as everyone drinks together. The song celebrates communal drinking and festive abandon.","opus":"A rowdy tavern round in old Finnish dialect, friends musing over past times until their throats go dry and they demand a tankard of ale, toasting the beauty of women and the sheer riot of being alive. Korpiklaani built their name on drinking anthems welded to folk-metal fiddle and accordion, and this is that formula distilled, a Kalevala-flavoured barroom stomp that never stops filling the mug.","sonnet":"A raucous communal drinking song that barely pretends to be anything else — men sitting at the end of the day with dry mouths and buzzing heads call for a mug of ale and honey mead, admire the women, extract joy from life, and toast hops and the fields that made the brew, drinking through until dawn and declaring the whole magnificent mess a reason to keep living.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~ukon-wacka":{"haiku":"Summer arrives, bringing festival time when all drink from Ukko's cup in celebration. Gods and ancestors bless the harvest; everyone—noble and peasant alike—becomes drunk in joy.","opus":"A pagan harvest prayer to Ukko, the Finnish sky-god of thunder, celebrating the springtime sowing and the drunken feast that follows, everyone from maidens to elders reeling as they beg the old creator for the greatest crop of all. Korpiklaani take the title from an actual Finnish sacrificial rite, and their fiddle-and-accordion folk metal turns ancient agrarian ritual into a joyous, boozy stomp.","sonnet":"A pagan harvest ritual addressed directly to Ukko, the old sky-father — warm summer, the spring sowing, and the promise of the greatest crop all converge on a festival where mead flows, everyone from servant girls to solemn elders gets drunk, and the community's lust and chaos become a collective prayer for abundance, offered back to the god who rules it all.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~vodka":{"haiku":"The song celebrates vodka as a cure-all that strengthens people, removes pain and fear, and transforms perception, while claiming it contains the purity of nature. It frames drinking as liberation and enhancement.","opus":"Finnish folk metal's tongue-in-cheek drinking anthem, celebrating vodka as courage, comfort and cure with a wink that half-knows it's describing self-deception. The mock-earnest purity pitch is the joke; the loneliness it's papering over is real underneath the party.","sonnet":"A completely literal, enthusiastically deadpan ode to vodka — it lists the drink's alleged social benefits one by one, from removing shyness to making everyone look gorgeous, with zero irony and maximum conviction.","src":"haiku"},
"korpiklaani~wooden-pints":{"haiku":"Underground dwellers feast and celebrate together, indifferent to sin or consequence, simply seeking intoxication and camaraderie.","opus":"A yarn about tiny subterranean folk who never glimpse daylight yet know how to revel, hoisting their cups to sing, scrap and dance until sunrise over game meat and firelight, tough survivors of a long conflict. The Finnish folk-metal party band deliver this one in English while keeping the yoiking and mead-hall spirit, an underground drinking legend set to galloping fiddle.","sonnet":"Underground little men who've never seen daylight throw the best party imaginable — reindeer meat, candlelit stone walls, wooden pints raised in yoiking and brawling until morning — and after a long bloody war, all the survivors want is women, meat, beer, and rum, caring nothing for god or sin, just the brutal pleasures of having made it through.","src":"haiku"},
"kortez~hej-wy":{"haiku":"The speaker was labeled a troublemaker and prophesied to fail, but escaped; now they wonder if old friends remember or care what happened to them.","opus":"Kortez, the Polish singer-songwriter known for weary, intimate balladry, addresses old friends who once shared a suburban park and a whole world at their feet. Written off as a troublemaker who would end badly, he sailed away anyway and returned uncertain, standing where he once stood as a schoolboy. Sleepless Warsaw nights and easy delivery orders fill a silence the vanished companions have kept for years.","sonnet":"A man addresses the people from his suburban youth, admitting that the troublemaker they wrote off somehow made it while still feeling uncertain as ever. Now sleepless in Warsaw, he wonders why everyone has gone quiet, and why everything still smells of someone long gone.","src":"haiku"},
"koven~industrial-strain":{"haiku":"We passively accept manufactured narratives and manufactured crises without questioning the system feeding them.","opus":"A protest against media conditioning and manufactured narrative, the sense of being walled in and force-fed lines you swallow without resistance. It frames modern complacency as something engineered rather than chosen.","sonnet":"About media manipulation and the way people passively accept the narrative they're fed — the system has built a wall around our perception and we just \"choke it down\"; the song is a call to recognize the manufactured reality we're living inside.","src":"haiku"},
"kraftklub~sch-ner-tag-feat-casper":{"haiku":"Surface cheerfulness masks depression, absent fathers, broken families, and suicidal ideation. The speaker sarcastically praises a beautiful day while describing underlying psychological destruction and hopelessness.","opus":"Kraftklub and Casper mask despair behind cheerful German insistence that everything is fine, drinking being no problem for someone with no solution. The band's savage irony turns a beautiful sunny day, birds singing and flowers blooming, into a perfect occasion to die, cataloguing absent fathers and hollow dreams beneath the forced smile.","sonnet":"A German song of cheerfully delivered despair — the weather is beautiful, the chorus celebrates a lovely day, and each verse catalogues an interior coming apart at its seams. An absent father, fake smiles, suicidal imagery wrapped in ironic sunshine. The gap between the radiant exterior and the collapsing inside is the entire joke and the entire wound.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~absolute-misanthropy":{"haiku":"A hateful manifesto of misanthropy and rage, welcoming apocalyptic destruction and promising suffering to all of humanity.","opus":"Mille Petrozza distilling thrash into pure chemical hatred, rage as an addiction fed by amphetamine and adrenaline. There's no cause here beyond loathing itself, which is the honest and frightening point.","sonnet":"A pure expression of hatred for all of humanity — the speaker fantasizes about a final holocaust for the people they despise, running on toxic adrenaline and social fury, and invites others to join their philosophy of total misanthropy.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~all-of-the-same-blood":{"haiku":"United totalitarian legions march toward annihilation, spreading dehumanizing propaganda and distorted truths that breed dystopia and despair.","opus":"German thrash confronting fascism directly, watching indoctrinated masses march in lockstep, purity and unity twisted into propaganda and hate until utopian promises collapse into another grey day of the walking dead.","sonnet":"An examination of how totalitarian movements are born from shared bloodlines and belief, marching toward dehumanization and dystopia while propaganda replaces truth and unity collapses.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~amok-run":{"haiku":"A victim of prolonged abuse and soul-theft turns violent avenger, going on a massacre as final act of control and divine punishment. The song is framed as martyrdom through bloodshed.","opus":"Pushed past his breaking point by abuse that scarred his soul, the speaker resolves to unleash a vengeful killing spree so his name will be remembered forever. He casts his rampage as martyrdom, bringing torment and death before dying with his victims.","sonnet":"The song is told from the perspective of someone who has been pushed past endurance by others' cruelty and decides to carry out a mass killing on what he calls his glorious final day, dying himself in the process. The narrative depicts the internal logic of a spree killing without endorsing it.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~bitter-sweet-revenge":{"haiku":"Jealousy and betrayal transform love into murderous rage; the narrator carries out vengeance as both payoff and necessity after being deceived.","opus":"The German thrash veterans dissect how jealousy rots a person into something monstrous, love souring into murderous obsession as rage takes the wheel. Fed by betrayal, the vengeance is carried out coldly, and the song closes on grim, circular logic, insisting hatred and payback are the inevitable shadow of love and trust.","sonnet":"Jealousy corrodes a man until love inverts completely into homicidal fantasy. The arc is inevitable: betrayal plants the seed, rage tends it, and the mind descends into a place where revenge stops being a fantasy and becomes the organizing logic of an entire existence.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~chosen-few":{"haiku":"Two people recognize spiritual connection and transcendence through each other, becoming everything to one another. Their bond represents a new era where fantasies become real.","opus":"A narrator is overtaken by another being that becomes his faith, his rescue and his sure doom all at once, yielding as their spirits merge into a new age past the privileged elite. The German thrash veterans soften their attack into something near-devotional, treating liberty as a blade that cuts both ways and this perilous union as the instant he finally feels alive.","sonnet":"Spiritual and romantic devotion collapse into a single overwhelming force, described through language that deliberately blurs the sacred and the profane. The speaker experiences being chosen — or perhaps choosing — as both liberation and submission, arriving finally at a feeling of being fully alive.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~civilization-collapse":{"haiku":"Societal breakdown fueled by inequality and injustice; riots and chaos sweep away the powerful while dark decay replaces imperial arrogance.","opus":"Kreator pour thrash fury into a vision of society tearing itself apart. A cornered population, they argue, has no option but revolt, its awakened rage demanding all-out war against inequality and a privileged few. The dispossessed rise, sanctuaries fall, the powerful are toppled and the downtrodden turn predator, whole cities reduced to cinders overnight as the old order spirals into ruin.","sonnet":"Long-suppressed populations finally storm the barricades of a system built for the few, burning temples and crumbling monuments as they go. The collapse is simultaneously catastrophe and reckoning — the mighty fall, the weak surge forward, and the old order is reduced to ash.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~death-to-the-world":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic prophecy describes humanity's destruction through plague and environmental devastation as gods return to reclaim a ravaged earth.","opus":"German thrash veterans Kreator stage a doomsday reckoning tinged with ecological rage. Prophecy, plague, and nuclear catastrophe herald mankind's plunge into an inescapable end no faith can halt. The fury sharpens as the ravaged planet is invoked, its ruined beauty an accusation, and human extinction becomes both cosmic horror and a verdict on environmental destruction.","sonnet":"Humanity's accumulated violence against the planet and itself is rendered as inevitable apocalypse: plague, ecological collapse, warfare — all flowing from a species that desecrated what it was given. The earth is addressed as a ravaged mother, and the song ends not with salvation but with the grim logic that from such ruin only more ruin can be born.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~demon-prince":{"haiku":"Young people trapped under oppressive control and fear from authoritarian rule rebel against their captors, spreading resistance to future generations. Disobedience against tyranny becomes spiritual liberation.","opus":"Kreator reframe revolt as freedom, handing the next generation the means to defy a tyrant ruling over deceit. The title figure is the oppressor to be cast off; unleash the reckoning and reap the revolution that follows.","sonnet":"A call to rise up against an oppressive authoritarian system that crushes the spirit of those born into its ghetto — the demon prince is not the enemy but a symbol the oppressed must refuse, disobey, and ultimately destroy in order to liberate themselves from the empire of lies.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~destroy-what-destroys-you":{"haiku":"People must dismantle oppressive systems and social chains by awakening to their own inner power and resisting programmed fear.","opus":"A battle cry to shatter society's shackles and demolish whatever is demolishing you, be it dread, bigotry or malice. It calls on the world to rouse itself and revive dreams abandoned before remorse takes hold, pinning liberation on an inexhaustible strength found within.","sonnet":"A liberation anthem: break the chains society uses against you, confront the prejudice and fear being weaponised to keep you down, and draw on inner strength to reclaim the dream before it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~dystopia":{"haiku":"Darkness, war, and deception define a dystopian age where God dies and messiahs never arrive. Hope crumbles under perpetual conflict.","opus":"A grim survey of a collapsed civilization ruled by deception, endless war, and false religion, where the awaited messiah never arrives. The refrain declares God dead as war drags on, the parasite savior a lie that keeps a fragile, doomed peace.","sonnet":"About a world run by false messiahs and parasitic leaders who use religious and political disorder to keep populations divided and at war — God is declared dead, the promised savior never arrives, and the only outcome is perpetual suffering. It's a portrait of civilization as permanent managed catastrophe.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~endorama":{"haiku":"An apocalyptic force called Endorama crushes and ends all worldly pain, greed, and illusion, offering liberation through the cessation of life itself.","opus":"A vision of a final apocalyptic end that erases all suffering, pain, greed and human pride, framing death as release from a labyrinth of life into either eden or inferno.","sonnet":"A force called Endorama arrives as an unstoppable annihilation that ends all suffering, pride, selfishness, and strife, leaving nothing behind. It frames total destruction as a dark release from the suffering of being alive.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~enemy-of-god":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic war, religious fanaticism, and genocidal violence erupt as systems fail and despots are overthrown; chaos and hatred reign as the world burns.","opus":"An apocalyptic vision of civilization collapsing as corrupt leaders, priests and fanatical religions plunge the world into total war and genocide. Cast as a global end of peace, it frames organized religion and despotic power as the true enemy that slaughters innocence.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic vision of civilisation destroyed by fanatical religious warfare and totalitarian violence, where systems fail, genocide unfolds, and the only survivors inherit a godless wasteland.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~escalation":{"haiku":"A global uprising against indoctrination, lies, and oppressive systems builds toward total violent escalation meant to liberate the human race.","opus":"A summons to worldwide uprising against conditioning, religious authority and long years of psychological subjugation. The chanted plea for all-out upheaval casts coordinated revolt and bloodshed in the streets as the means to liberate humanity and bring down thrones and rulers.","sonnet":"A call for global uprising against religious indoctrination and political oppression — the narrator wants total, violent escalation of protest to break free the human race, framing organised unrest as the only path to liberation.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~everlasting-flame":{"haiku":"A lifetime of futile struggle against a materialist world: youth dreams of escape, middle age realizes entrapment, old age finds only wasted pain and no way out.","opus":"Kreator chart a lifetime of captivity across the years, weary in youth, then trapped among industrial toil at middle age, then a hollow husk near sixty, forever fantasizing about elsewhere. The German thrash veterans make the title's enduring blaze a cruel promise of more, a speck of grit unable to escape endless torment.","sonnet":"Three snapshots of a single life — at seventeen, thirty-four, and fifty-six — each showing the same person trapped by circumstance, chasing a flame that consumes rather than illuminates. The everlasting flame is not inspiration but compulsion, the unnamed force that keeps a person locked inside a world too small and too painful to sustain them.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~fallen-brother":{"haiku":"Death and memory intertwine across generations; the living honor those fallen while grief persists eternally.","opus":"A thrash requiem for a lost friend, urging the living to seize their remaining days while insisting the dead aren't gone but laughing and drinking somewhere beyond the grave. The German verses turn grief into a defiant hymn of remembrance.","sonnet":"A eulogy for someone taken too young from the brotherhood, insisting that memory and blood connection outlast death and will not be forgotten — with the German verses imagining the dead laughing and drinking behind the graves while the living weep. The song bridges grief and defiance with the solidarity of those left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~from-flood-into-fire":{"haiku":"Shared struggle and collective creation against plague—two voices unite through adversity to build meaning and cure through art.","opus":"Kreator gather the misfits who feel they fit nowhere and turn that isolation into collective defiance, a chosen band walking a shared road through hardship together. The German thrash veterans cast the group as a remedy rather than a sickness, united voices bracing for whatever comes and content to be judged only by what they build.","sonnet":"A collective call to solidarity among people who feel they belong nowhere. Imperfection and mutual distrust are acknowledged openly, but capitulation is refused. Moving through flood into fire, the speaker insists that shared creation is the only legacy worth leaving — and that facing hardship together is the only identity that holds.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~future-ring":{"haiku":"Tragedy drowns the future while sirens sing trauma. Burning in 1999 means drowning—the world turns without mercy.","opus":"An apocalyptic vision of a doomed species praying to a counterfeit savior while the world spins on pointlessly; set at the millennium's turn, it renders humanity burning and drowning as it awaits a triumphant ruler who offers nothing but illusion.","sonnet":"A late-90s apocalyptic vision of spiritual emptiness — people seek drug-messiah figures and salvation from loneliness while the world burns and drowns in a cycle with no exit; beneath the surface is a bitter awareness that both the narrator and the one they're addressing are weeping about their absurd lives.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~ghetto-war":{"haiku":"Urban decay and substance abuse create a war zone where mutants and losers clash; hate and adrenaline fuel endless confrontation below the surface.","opus":"German thrash imagining a dystopian underclass revolt, the dispossessed of a greed-ruled society turning on each other in a war beneath the ruins. Kreator's Cold War-era dread of a world hollowed by mammon and mutual hatred.","sonnet":"About the violent social implosion of a marginalized community — in the shadows of suburbia, minds wrecked by poverty and substances turn on each other in a war of pure hate and adrenaline, with terror as the only law and neon replacing the sun.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~golden-age":{"haiku":"Death and transcendence promise escape from misery into enlightenment. A guide invites surrender to free the mind from fear and rage.","opus":"A seductive, cult-like invitation to surrender and be led toward a promised utopia free of fear and rage, the coaxing tone and talk of ritual and drowning hinting that this golden age may be a mass delusion or death.","sonnet":"About surrendering the self completely — letting life fade away, drowning enlightened, saying goodbye — in order to reach a utopian state of minds freed from fear and rage. It reads either as genuine spiritual transcendence or as a cult/suicide-pact framing of collective dissolution.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~hordes-of-chaos":{"haiku":"Civilisation collapses into tribal violence and mass suffering, elites feast while the masses perish, their flags hiding the truth that everyone turns against everyone.","opus":"A furious survey of a world set at war with itself, where elites profit from engineered division while the poorest pay in blood. The chant of everyone against everyone names the real product of the flag-wavers, chaos as policy.","sonnet":"A thrash metal indictment of political brutality and systemic violence: war crimes, honor killings, autocratic power, and the exploitation of the poor by elites, culminating in a vision of total social collapse where everyone fights everyone.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~hordes-of-chaos-a-necrologue-for-the-elite":{"haiku":"Death consumes the powerful and celebrated; apocalyptic retribution levels hierarchies through universal annihilation.","opus":"A raging condemnation of a world turned against itself, the German thrash icons listing tyranny, phony peace, and youngsters dying for a fabricated apocalypse. The rich gorge themselves on the destitute as banners flutter and all are pitted against all, an escalating howl of pure loathing framed as a funeral notice for the powerful.","sonnet":"Totalitarian autocracy feeds on manufactured chaos — fake peace, brutal riots, honour killings, a tyrant's feast on the shoulders of the poor — and the song frames the resulting \"everyone against everyone\" as the ruling class's intended outcome, a necrologue written for an elite that has engineered the suffering it profits from.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~impossible-brutality":{"haiku":"The speaker witnesses endless cycles of violence, oppression, and despair as inevitable conditions of existence. Human suffering remains paralyzingly constant and unchangeable.","opus":"A horrified onlooker's catalogue of terror and cruelty, framed as a curse as ancient as war itself. The darker turn is fatalistic and complicit: propaganda enslaves people their whole lives, and the narrator ends up craving to watch the doomed world fall apart without flinching, resigned to its ruin.","sonnet":"An overwhelmed witness to a world built entirely on violence, tyranny, and propaganda — the brutality so total and ancient it feels cosmically permanent — who can only stand paralyzed and watch civilization collapse, waiting for the dawn of its death without fear.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~mind-of-fire":{"haiku":"Mental transcendence through psychic liberation reveals illusion as the foundation of reality and freedom as the drugged escape from imposed moral rules.","opus":"A tempting anthem to psychedelic escape presented as both freedom and peril, igniting the mind to torch every link to what came before. Kreator frame intoxication as a sacred substance of rulers, a paradise that quiets the spirits tormenting the listener while murmuring that reality is a fiction set down by deceivers.","sonnet":"Setting the mind on fire and burning all bridges leads underground and then into a sphere of light where Eden becomes real, orgies take place amid ruins, and a hypnotic illusion keeps the bitter world outside. Transcendence through total obliteration of the old self.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~one-evil-comes-a-million-follow":{"haiku":"Fate operates as an overwhelming force beyond human agency, cascading misery from single moments into irreversible destruction and endless suffering.","opus":"German thrash as pure fatalism: no faith or free will can steer you clear of what fate has stored, because catastrophe cascades and a single cursed minute erases every good thing that came before it.","sonnet":"Fate is an unstoppable force delivering pain and sorrow in cascading waves — one cursed moment erases all pleasure and triggers avalanches of further misfortune — and no magic or belief can alter the cruel hand life deals, only acceptance that nothing is left to chance.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~pandemonium":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic chaos consumes the world as religion dies and people lose their grip on reality. Desperate pandemonia overwhelms consciousness as the world burns and the speaker offers false salvation through surrendering one's soul.","opus":"Kreator conjure apocalyptic chaos raging in the air and the brain as God abandons humanity to despair and religion dies. The German thrash band offer a bitter pill of revealed truth, framing history's lies as the origin of ruin, yet dangling one surreal bargain, giving up the soul to survive the burning world.","sonnet":"Religious collapse triggers civilizational chaos. When faith dies the lies that organized society around it are exposed all at once, and what follows is a psychological and social unraveling — pandemonium in the mind mirroring pandemonium in the world. Yet a dreamlike possibility surfaces at the end: surrender the soul and the inferno might become illumination.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~passage-to-babylon":{"haiku":"A seductive figure promises escape and transcendence, luring someone toward hidden desires and forbidden knowledge.","opus":"A seductive tempter's pitch, the devil or a false messiah promising escape, hidden knowledge and paradise beyond death. Kreator makes the offer sound generous precisely so you feel the trap in following it toward oblivion.","sonnet":"A seductive voice promises everything — knowledge, fantasy, escape, a life restored — and offers the listener a passage to Babylon as the only way to access it, the song functioning as a temptation narrative with an implied dark cost.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~phantom-antichrist":{"haiku":"A figure of liberation and vengeance emerges to destroy the institutions of slavery and false faith, ending humanity's enslavement to religious and political lies.","opus":"An apocalyptic avenger scours a world enslaved by organized religion and nationalism, Mille Petrozza casting an antichrist figure not as evil but as a violent reckoning meant to burn away human atrocity and free those awake enough to see.","sonnet":"An anti-religious revolutionary figure — the Phantom Antichrist arrives not as evil but as liberation, destroying the ideological chains of faith and nationalism, cleansing a world corrupt with genocide and democide, and from the ashes becoming a paradoxical savior of free minds.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~pleasure-to-kill":{"haiku":"A vampire or serial killer rises from the grave seeking blood and murder; killing brings satisfaction while torture and chainsaw violence are the only pleasures they experience.","opus":"A gleefully grotesque first-person plunge into a serial murderer's mind, a grave-rising killer stalking the city for blood, savouring cries and steel until he returns to his coffin sated. Kreator are German thrash pioneers, and this title track from their 1986 landmark is a cornerstone of extreme thrash, its relentless velocity and cartoonish gore helping bridge the gap toward death metal's coming brutality.","sonnet":"Pure horror-metal carnage narrated from inside a monster's skull — a creature that rises from a grave each night, hunts the city for blood, and experiences killing as the only form of satisfaction available to it: the spray of blood, the sound of screaming, the act itself as completion. No motive beyond appetite, no arc beyond the compulsion to return underground and repeat.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~radical-resistance":{"haiku":"Global oppression from religious extremism, authoritarian leaders, and cultural bigotry must be met with united resistance across all borders. Coordinated rebellion is the antidote to war, suffering, and slavery.","opus":"Kreator name religious zealotry, terrorism, warlords, and a silent complicit public as interlocking poisons in a world tearing itself apart. The thrash veterans call for the marginalized to band together into a global uprising that won't bargain, framing unity itself as the cure for oppression, deceit, and endless conflict.","sonnet":"A call for global solidarity against both religious extremism and cynical state power — naming fundamentalists, warlords, corrupt democracies, and aggressive Christianity as equal enemies — and urging people from every direction on earth to unite and kill what cannot otherwise be killed.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~reconquering-the-throne":{"haiku":"Vampiric parasites drain and manipulate masses through deception, but unity and resistance eventually ignite a rebellion to reclaim power.","opus":"A call to shake off manipulation and parasitic control, the imagery of vampires draining the meek giving way to collective uprising, a million hearts storming back to reclaim what propaganda stole.","sonnet":"About resistance against manipulative parasites — political or personal — who drain and control people through propaganda and distrust, with a call for collective uprising to reclaim what was stolen.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~replicas-of-life":{"haiku":"Dying cities reduced to ash breed only empty replicas of existence where suffering masses drown in misery and despair.","opus":"German thrash band Kreator envision a city where whatever warmth once existed has frozen over and its people's noblest aspirations have been plundered. They picture the underworld cracking open above dying metropolises consumed by fire, their inhabitants reduced to hollow imitations of humanity, greed and vanity finally extinguished as everything is incinerated into oblivion.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic vision of urban civilization dying under the weight of its own arrogance and ignorance. Cities become hollow shells, their inhabitants already dead in everything but appearance. The replicas of life are people stripped of meaning by a metropolis that consumed their god-like potential and left only fear, flame, and pain.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~second-awakening":{"haiku":"Humanity breaks free from millennia of religious oppression and spiritual control, awakening to new consciousness after the death of old moral systems.","opus":"Twenty centuries of tyranny, blind faith, and bewilderment yield to a spiritual rebirth as the mind slips free of dread and the planet is purged of its venom. Kreator pour their thrash ferocity into a sweeping historical reckoning, damning ages of warfare and dogma while calling forth a transcendent renewal after which nothing will be as it was.","sonnet":"Two thousand years of religious oppression and mental subjugation are cast as a long night now ending. The collective rising is framed as a spiritual reawakening — not violent overthrow but a shared return to clarity — where the soul reclaims ground that doctrine and bloodshed had slowly erased.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~servant-in-heaven-king-in-hell":{"haiku":"Some are born to lead while others are bound to follow; the narrator rejects servitude, choosing sin and hellfire over powerless obedience.","opus":"A reflection on the brutal split between victors and the vanquished, rulers and the ruled, arriving at the Miltonic stance that reigning amid sin and flame beats bowing to empty subjugation. Beneath the slogan lies a refusal of docile obedience in favor of choosing one's own path at any price.","sonnet":"A Miltonic inversion: better to rule in hell than serve in heaven — the song argues that accepting a life of slavery and suffering in the hope of divine reward is a fool's bargain, and that sinning boldly in the here and now beats quiet servitude.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~shadowland":{"haiku":"A hopeless, isolated world devoid of love breeds desperation and suffering. A weary person faces unending darkness and meaninglessness.","opus":"A bleak vision of a barren, loveless era where affection cannot take root and a wandering spirit hunts in vain for meaning amid decay and hopelessness. Kreator paint a desolate age in which loneliness is the toll exacted, and a meaningless existence yields only a meaningless death.","sonnet":"About a desolate inner and outer landscape where love is impossible, isolation is the price of living, and suffering is inherited from blindly following failed leaders. It frames contemporary alienation as a permanent condition — a new dawn that still only illuminates darkness.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~slave-machinery":{"haiku":"Factories of oppression mechanically crush hope and autonomy, transforming humans into puppet workers feeding an endless cycle of enslavement.","opus":"Kreator, longtime engineers of German thrash, turn their fury on industrial dehumanization here, imagining labor as an eternal mechanism that grinds hope out of workers the moment they pass its gates. The recurring gearwheels of enslavement stand in for a hierarchy that feeds on manipulated lives, reducing people to functional puppets who never grasp what has been taken from them.","sonnet":"Industrial capitalism is rendered as a living mechanism that swallows human beings whole. Workers enter its gates stripped of aspiration, reduced to functional parts inside a steel hierarchy that perpetually grinds hope into obedience, leaving only the echo of a system feeding on controlled, manipulated lives.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~soul-eraser":{"haiku":"An alien dictator arrives from space, imposing tyranny and slavery while people worship a false savior from the skies, collapsing under millennia of suffering.","opus":"A science-fiction parable of tyranny arriving from the sky as false salvation, an alien dictator sold to a desperate people as their savior, religion and law repurposed as instruments of enslavement. Thrash as allegory for how despots dress domination as deliverance.","sonnet":"A science-fiction horror narrative about an alien dictator who arrives to enslave humanity — framed in ancient-pyramid mythology, the invader poses as a god-king savior but is actually a conscienceless tyrant who erases souls and leaves nothing but silent death.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~suicide-terrorist":{"haiku":"The song depicts a suicide bomber as a nameless weapon used by ideological leaders to sow death and chaos across nations.","opus":"German thrash band Kreator confront modern terrorism head-on, portraying a suicide bomber as an anonymous, manipulated instrument, glorified in blood yet dying only to advance someone else's fresh deceptions. In a borderless war that kills across every ideology, the band strip martyrdom of any honor, exposing it as engineered, indiscriminate slaughter.","sonnet":"A clinical, unflinching examination of the suicide bomber as a figure manufactured by ideology rather than born from it: nameless, expendable, baptised in someone else's cause, his life the currency in a war that discards ideological categories as fast as it creates them. The horror is structural — the system produces him and discards him without ever seeing him as a person.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~system-decay":{"haiku":"Societies collapse into shade without sun—poor, sick, enslaved, weak, and lost. Egos replace souls, modern lords control hearts, and a poisoned arrow kills the remnants of a decaying world.","opus":"A grim tour of a decomposing civilization, the singer peering into a city's icy gaze and tallying the ailing, destitute, and enslaved while pride supplants the spirit and a modern ruler of vermin presides over a sunless, expiring realm. Kreator, the German thrash institution, swap their early gore for social bleakness, the same knife-edge riffing now trained on collapse and rot rather than the graveyard.","sonnet":"A bleak urban autopsy — looking into the city's eyes and finding only hollow repetitions of suffering: the sick, the poor, the enslaved, the doomed, sheep marching into graves under a dying sun. The world has been handed over to a modern lord of flies and egos have consumed what souls were left, leaving everyone to fade like shadows poisoned by an arrow aimed straight at the heart.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~the-few-the-proud-the-broken":{"haiku":"Soldiers march into violence as tools of tyrants, returning dehumanized wrecks; war serves only those in power while destroying the men sent to fight.","opus":"A furious anti-war reckoning with what militaries make of young men, bred as tools, indoctrinated to slaughter the weak, and returned home as dehumanized wrecks. Kreator turns a recruiting slogan bitter, mourning lives spent for manmade causes that were all in vain.","sonnet":"A brutal anti-war indictment of how soldiers are indoctrinated, used as killing tools for tyrants, and returned home as dehumanized wrecks — the glory rhetoric of military service is stripped away to reveal collateral murder and mental devastation.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~to-the-afterborn":{"haiku":"A warning to future generations about cycles of oppression, lies, and despair spread by authorities and religion that trap youth with no hope or escape. The speaker fears the next generation will repeat the same horrors.","opus":"A grim message to the generations still to come, warning of a doomed youth ruled by leaders who corrupt and peddle ancient deceit. Beneath it runs a despairing swipe at organized religion and humanity's repeating folly, wondering whether those who outlive our collapse will simply return to the same old worship.","sonnet":"A warning addressed to future generations — the speaker describes the current age as one of tyrants, religious manipulation, and souls led astray, and asks whether those who come after will repeat the same mistakes by worshipping the same crosses, or finally break the cycle.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~tyranny":{"haiku":"The speaker expresses exhaustion with oppression and a hunger for spiritual meaning, seeking something to believe in after enduring broken hearts and a world that doesn't fit them.","opus":"Kreator, pillars of German thrash, turn their fury inward into a crisis of faith rather than a battlefield. The narrator, broken and angry for too long, interrogates an unnamed authority about its promised heaven and finds only pain and lies where meaning should be. Beneath the aggression runs a genuine plea, a demand for something worth believing in that the offered world cannot supply.","sonnet":"A voice broken by prolonged anger demands something — anything — worth believing in. Exhausted by a world whose promises of heaven and peace have always been out of reach, it circles between rage and a plea, unable to leave but unable to stay.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~united-in-hate":{"haiku":"The oppressed unite against their oppressors—the weak, fragile, and enslaved join together in rebellion to transform the system. Collective resistance becomes their path to liberation.","opus":"A thrash rallying cry that flips hate into solidarity among the weak and outcast, a legion uniting to bring an empire down. More than teen rebellion, it frames collective defiance as awakening to being alive.","sonnet":"Reclaiming hatred as a unifying force for the marginalized — the outcasts, the weak, the fragile and enslaved — the song calls them to band together, wake up, and storm the empire that has been parasitizing them, framing collective rage as emancipatory power.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~until-our-paths-cross-again":{"haiku":"All humans are temporary and transient; we pass wisdom and spirit to future generations before fading into memory.","opus":"Decades past their savage 80s roots, Kreator temper aggression with elegy, observing how servants and rulers, cowards and beauties all vanish alike while their essence endures. The German thrash veterans urge listeners to seize their fleeting span from beginning to end, since only the spirit outlasts the body as it's handed to the next generation.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality that refuses despair — kings, fools, dreamers, and cowards all live transient lives and pass on. The soul outlasts the body, the flame passes between generations, and grief is no reason to drown in memory. Every daughter and son will mourn; the gift is to embrace whatever time remains before the circle closes again.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~victory-will-come":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic forces rise from chaos while activists and anarchists battle emperors. Victory belongs only to death, but the will to overcome persists through the darkness.","opus":"Apocalyptic thrash reframing catastrophe as revolution, where an underground movement rises against the icons of the past; even as death ultimately triumphs, the will to overcome dead ideas becomes its own defiant victory.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic battle cry against entrenched power and religious oppression, imagining a revolutionary force rising from the wreckage of failed systems with the conviction that even if death is the only victor, resistance is the only answer.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~violent-revolution":{"haiku":"A deeply alienated person feels society has failed them and contemplate violent upheaval as their only solution to a world of repulsion and lost beauty.","opus":"Kreator channeling total alienation from society into a fantasy of upheaval, hatred fused with confusion into a single solution. It's the portrait of a mind so estranged that violence becomes the only remaining form of belonging.","sonnet":"A song about a mind so poisoned by social disgust and moral confusion that violence becomes the only logical outlet — the narrator does not claim a political cause, just an all-consuming hatred of humanity and the conviction that revolt is the only answer.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~war-curse":{"haiku":"The song envisions World War Two's horrors—burning bodies, hate ideology, soldiers and civilians alike consumed by violence—as an eternal curse. A death-witnessing entity observes suffering across all groups and collects souls for judgment, while pure hate perpetuates endless war.","opus":"Voiced by an undying force that shepherds the souls of the war-dead, the song surveys the slaughter of the Second World War across perpetrators and ordinary people alike. It describes a deathless malediction of hatred and bloodshed that keeps subjugating the world, framing organized cruelty as an eternal, self-renewing plague.","sonnet":"Narrated from the perspective of a death-guide surveying the horrors of World War II — Nazi ideology, air raids, mass casualties — the song depicts war as an eternal curse of hatred and violence that never truly ends.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~willing-spirit":{"haiku":"Spectral union across death's boundary: the speaker longs for a ghostly lover who visits at night in dreams, connecting through mystical presence while dawn shatters the illusion back into isolation.","opus":"Kreator swap thrash fury for gothic romance, imagining a lover who arrives only once she has passed on. A radiant, timeless figure of ruined splendour appears in darkness, brilliant yet forever beyond reach, unable to soothe the fever in his thoughts. Dawn hauls him back, and the vision dissolves, leaving desire trapped between sleep and waking.","sonnet":"A man is visited nightly by the spirit of someone lost to death, and the apparition is so vivid — so luminous, so close to touch — that waking life feels like the illusion. Dawn dissolves the presence each morning, leaving only longing and the impossible question of whether what appeared in the dark was real or a mind manufacturing what it cannot stop wanting.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~world-anarchy":{"haiku":"Global systemic collapse and tyranny crushing the weak. Kings and autocrats rule through terror while cultures are destroyed and all hope is burned.","opus":"Kreator pour their thrash apocalypticism into a vision of empires crumbling into chaos. Tyrants toast their cruel victories while terrified subjects are sacrificed, and religion can't rescue the hollow. As idols topple and menace advances, the narrator spits his contempt at a supposed paradise sinking into the very disorder it created.","sonnet":"Rulers built on lies preside over rubble, harvesting cultures with axe and sword while the weak serve a hollow dream. A horde descends from the east to drag the old order into its grave, leaving only chaos where false paradise once stood.","src":"haiku"},
"kreator~your-heaven-my-hell":{"haiku":"Religious doctrine presented as oppressive control and fear manipulation; the speaker rejects heaven and embraces spiritual freedom against religious tyranny.","opus":"Kreator turn their thrash into blunt anti-religious fury, blaming centuries of preached fear, guilt, and threats of damnation for enslaving human minds. The narrator styles himself as the wrecker of faith, urging the toppling of gods and frauds, and welcomes the end of religion as the birth of a liberated world.","sonnet":"Centuries of institutional religion wielding guilt and fear as control mechanisms are condemned as a form of psychopathic abuse. The speaker positions himself as the destroyer of that faith, welcoming an apocalypse that would unmask the poison and free humanity to see with open eyes.","src":"haiku"},
"kromea~planets":{"haiku":"Escape through celestial guidance pulls the speaker away from drowning, where planets burn bright as path. Burning and rising become synonymous with liberation.","opus":"A death rendered as luminous escape, the narrator leaving a cracked and confining world only for his ship to founder at sea. As water rushes in and no one comes, the burning planets promise release rather than tears, a drowning reframed as ascent, haunted still by whether things with someone could have been mended.","sonnet":"A speaker pours their life into cracked streets and sets out to sea, only to have the ship sink — fate sealing them underwater — but the planets burn bright and lead them out, promising no more shadow of the mind; the turn from drowning to 'let's burn and rise' reframes the shipwreck as transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"kryn~risset":{"haiku":"Mental breakdown and the overwhelming chaos of uncontrolled thoughts. The speaker is trapped in their own mind, drowning in obsession and unable to escape the cycle.","opus":"Named for an audio illusion of endless ascent, the Belgian metalcore act mirror a mind in collapse. Reason and control gone, the narrator swallows medication that only leaves him cold and hollow, cycling between shutdown and forced revival. The chorus begs escape from a mental cage of relentless overthinking, wishing he could wipe it all and vanish.","sonnet":"A mind in full breakdown — seized by manic loops, numbed by medication, lying on the floor in somatic shutdown — begs for release from the prison inside its own skull. The recurring wish is simply to push a reset button and wake up as someone who does not feel like this.","src":"haiku"},
"kublai-khan-tx~boomslang":{"haiku":"Psychic vampirism drains the speaker's essence; displayed as spectacle and tormented publicly, they sink into powerlessness and rage.","opus":"Confronting a venomous person who drains everything while the singer refuses to die, embracing pain as something sent to punish him. Beneath the defiance runs self-loathing and the exhausting toll of holding on against someone tearing him to new lows.","sonnet":"Rage directed at someone — or something — that drains and humiliates: viper's eyes stripping away energy, public display as the only therapy, hope stolen and sold, and finally a blade driven in — \"I die inside 'cause you won't shut the fuck up\" — the violence as much internal as outward.","src":"haiku"},
"kublai-khan-tx~swan-song-feat-scott-vogel":{"haiku":"Systemic violence and gender-based trauma destroy countless lives; bearing witness demands empathy for the invisible dead and their collective silent suffering.","opus":"An unflinching tribute to trafficked and abused women, truck-stop sex workers seen as somebody's mother, sister, daughter, tracing abuse back to childhood horror. It refuses pity in favor of witness, ending on a survivor reclaiming her worth.","sonnet":"A raw witness account of women trapped in cycles of poverty, trafficking, addiction, and abuse — the speaker looks through his window at suffering he cannot fix, while an embedded spoken-word testimony from a survivor insists on self-worth, survival without shame, and the refusal to be defined by exploitation.","src":"haiku"},
"kukon~adderall":{"haiku":"Pharmacological enhancement enables superhuman transcendence; the speaker weaponizes medicine to exceed baseline, altered consciousness as evolutionary necessity.","opus":"A Polish rapper narrating a stimulant-and-crypto delirium, feeling like an android beyond hurt, hormones and NFTs blurring into fried unreality. The recurring who-am-I, driving a burnt-out car with the glass blown out, is the identity dissolving under the high.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper describes a stimulant-fueled state of hypercognition and wealth fantasy — Adderall makes him feel like a transhumanist genius at a dinner with scientists, rich and untouchable, burning through NFTs and crypto while driving a wrecked car and questioning his own identity.","src":"haiku"},
"kukon~cracow-shit":{"haiku":"Urban underworld hustle and street wisdom mix as the speaker navigates danger and opportunity in Kraków, earning respect through survival and authenticity rather than escape.","opus":"A Polish rapper's love letter to nocturnal Krakow, its dealers and hustlers and outsiders learning the rules to survive; beneath the smoke and menace it's gratitude for the years that taught him a hardcore life and made him feel grown.","sonnet":"A tribute to Kraków as a city that is dangerous at night but shaped whoever the speaker became: hooded kids with knives, turf rules, smoking and circling the city in a car, the narrator pays respect to painters, dealers, DJs, and Moscow friends — all the people and places that made him feel adult.","src":"haiku"},
"kukon~hard-flex-drive":{"haiku":"A digitally fragmented persona plays multiple roles in a virtual existence, networking and networking-gaming while questioning if authentic connection or genuine place remains for them.","opus":"A rapper frames himself as a manufactured, digital product/device designed to comfort a virtual lover, his many online personas each professing love while he stays encrypted and hidden; beneath the flex, he confesses that his life and rap have become a prison he's not sure he can escape or wants to be freed from.","sonnet":"In Polish, a meditation on digital-age identity and the exhaustion of performing multiple selves — the narrator is encrypted and virtual, loving someone through artificial personas, wondering if he can still be real and if there's any space left for authentic life.","src":"haiku"},
"kuuro~she-s-got-a-gun":{"haiku":"A manipulative partner traps and imprisons their lover with false promises of safety, planning to kill them.","opus":"A toxic entrapment flipped into open threat, the captive lover who was called prey now the one holding the weapon and the patience. The dread is that the danger arrives from the person who claimed it was all for your safety.","sonnet":"A man slowly realizes that the woman who claimed to love him and kept him in her room has actually been planning to kill him — a dangerous obsession disguised as affection, with the predator having already loaded the weapon.","src":"haiku"},
"kuuro~snakes-ladders":{"haiku":"Liberation requires becoming untouchable; the speaker elevates beyond the reach of those who preyed on their vulnerability.","opus":"Getting free of someone who tried to burrow into your mind, now soaring out of their orbit entirely. Cast as the board game of ups and downs, it names the manipulators as small people with empty lives, and relishes finally rising past their reach.","sonnet":"Having escaped a manipulative person's mind games, the speaker is now flying like a satellite — untouchable, lighter, past the finish line — watching from altitude as the weak attacker, who has nothing else in life, falls alone.","src":"haiku"},
"kvelertak~mj-d":{"haiku":"Norse myth and drinking culture merge in metal excess, celebrating chaos and rebellion.","opus":"Norwegian party-metal built on the Norse myth of Odin stealing Suttung's mead of poetry, repurposed as a drunken ode to booze, black metal and mayhem with mates. Old gods conscripted for a good time.","sonnet":"A rowdy Norse-inflected party song celebrating Odin's theft of the mead of poetry from the giant Suttung, recast as an excuse for drinking, black metal, and raucous revelry with friends.","src":"haiku"},
"kx5-deadmau5-kaskade-james-french~avalanche":{"haiku":"Frozen cascade overwhelms agency; the speaker surrenders to avalanche, cold burying all resistance beneath accumulating snow.","opus":"Love cast as a sudden slide of snow and a freezing blast that entombs you before you can react, a helpless, addictive surrender to being blinded and dominated by someone else, any objection fading into powerlessness against the chill.","sonnet":"Being completely overwhelmed by a person's control over you — the relationship hits like an avalanche, burying the narrator in something simultaneously numbing and total, until there is nothing left to say and they are already under the snow.","src":"haiku"},
"kx5~avalanche-feat-james-french":{"haiku":"Frozen cascade overwhelms agency; the speaker surrenders to avalanche, cold burying all resistance beneath accumulating snow.","opus":"Love cast as a sudden slide of snow and a freezing blast that entombs you before you can react, a helpless, addictive surrender to being blinded and dominated by someone else, any objection fading into powerlessness against the chill.","sonnet":"Being overwhelmed and buried by someone's control — the intensity hits like a flash freeze, leaves the narrator unable to see or breathe, snow-blind and buried under the other person's dominance.","src":"haiku"},
"kx5~bright-lights-feat-ar-co":{"haiku":"Transcendent experience emerges from surrender as bright lights dissolve boundaries between self and sensation, overwhelming consciousness with dissolution and bliss.","opus":"A euphoric surrender to the dance floor, mirror-ball glow and rivers shaped like two lovers marking a place you go to switch on. The dazzle pouring over you turns collective transcendence into the entire point.","sonnet":"An invitation to surrender to transcendent euphoria: disco lights and shards of glass in the sky open diamond eyes, rivers form in the shape of shared feeling, and a place exists where bright lights simply take you over — the repetition insisting on the completeness of that surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"kx5~escape-feat-hayla":{"haiku":"Two souls desire escape and freedom together, unsure if their fantasy can become real or if it's just a dream.","opus":"A yearning electronic love song about the pull to run off with someone, torn between soulful connection and the fear of letting your head take flight, chasing freedom into the unknown. KX5 is the collaborative alias of dance titans Kaskade and deadmau5, and with vocalist Hayla they craft festival-scale progressive house, the repeated question about escaping together built to blossom into a euphoric, hands-in-the-air drop.","sonnet":"Two people on the edge of something real — one sees the other clearly, feels their souls in sync, and keeps asking the same trembling question: what if we just left together? Freedom is framed as a shared possibility rather than a certainty, the unknown pulling hard, and the fantasy of escape with someone who actually sees you is both the hope and the hesitation.","src":"haiku"},
"ky-v-ss~night-drives":{"haiku":"Unworthiness calcifies into relational paralysis as the speaker's perceived excess burdens every connection, driving them toward isolation and self-erasure.","opus":"The residue of a relationship that taught you you were too much to be loved, replayed on late-night drives where you still feel forced to lie about missing them. Emo-adjacent honesty about carrying a wound that keeps hurting long after.","sonnet":"Someone processes the lingering trauma of a relationship in which they were made to feel like too much — too needy, too emotional, too present to be fully loved. Late-night drives trigger the memory of crying and the fear that they are still expected to lie about how hurt they are.","src":"haiku"},
"kygo-ellie-goulding~first-time":{"haiku":"Youth's reckless exhilaration fades but remains vivid in memory; the speaker wishes to reclaim that fearless era despite inevitable maturation.","opus":"Nostalgia for a first reckless love, the cigarettes and borrowed cars and small-town poverty romanticized in memory, and the certainty you'd relive every bit of it. Bittersweet because the older narrator knows it's gone.","sonnet":"A nostalgic return to the reckless early days of a relationship — cheap thrills, borrowed cars, cigarettes, and complete abandon — with the speaker older now but insisting they would do it all again without hesitation. Memory serves as both elegy and celebration of youth's intensity.","src":"haiku"},
"kylesa~scapegoat-2022-remaster":{"haiku":"The speaker is blamed and scapegoated while the true root causes of problems remain buried and unexamined, leading to cyclical suffering.","opus":"Sludge-metal about being made the scapegoat while the real cause of the problem stays buried too deep to reach in time. The refusal to accept blame becomes a reckoning: dig up the truth or let the whole thing swing back around.","sonnet":"A song about being falsely blamed while the real source of the problem remains buried too deep for anyone to reach — the narrator has been scapegoated, but insists the true rot will eventually surface, threatening to dig it up before the pendulum swings on its own.","src":"haiku"},
"kyuss~100":{"haiku":"Someone writhes in extreme heat and emotional pain, feeling trapped and diseased while begging for mercy from a person they depend on.","opus":"A scorching, lust-soaked stoner-rock plea pitched at extreme heat, urging someone nearer while the singer both burns and freezes; underneath is desert-baked craving and its paradoxes, a harsh spot fashioned for a lover where flame and frost, longing and relief blur into one another.","sonnet":"A hypnotic desert-rock song about extreme heat and extremes of sensation — burning, freezing, drowning in heat — where a confrontational relationship with another person is rendered through the same disorienting physical language, creating an atmosphere of feverish, uncomfortable need.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric works through thermal contradiction: extreme heat that produces freezing, a scorching pale expanse that is also frost, a fever that reads as sickness. The song is a supplication built on paradox, a lover on his knees promising devotion inside a hostile environment he insists was constructed specifically for the one he addresses. Kyuss render desert-rock's actual furnace as an erotic and spiritual double-bind, where surrender and torment share one temperature. The refrain's final collapse into a bare, repeated number strips the plea down to raw sensation, the mind burned past language into a single throbbing measurement.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","nature & the elements"],"means":"pledging devotion through thermal paradox and bodily surrender","built":"refrain collapses to bare number, language burned past meaning"},
"kyuss~50-million-year-trip-downside-up":{"haiku":"Eternal haunting by memory despite attempting to escape through substance and dissociation.","opus":"A trance-like song about being impossible to halt in motion, the singer forever moving and evading while holding to the memory of someone he can never let go of, all framed as a looping journey through a torment he keeps returning to only to confront his dread once more.","sonnet":"The speaker is running, drinking, and drifting in a state of restless self-erasure, yet one stubborn thread of loyalty or memory refuses to be shaken — the song circles between escape and the frank question of whether hell is somewhere you return from.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that mimics motion sickness, staging an escape it can never complete because the thing being fled is welded to the one fleeing. The pre-chorus is pure verb-chant, present-tense actions with no object, a body in perpetual motion daring a pursuer to catch it. The hook then keeps swearing an unbreakable tie to some absent person, a memory that rides along however far the running goes. When the late verse floats walking out of hell only to be dragged back in, the circularity clicks: the downside-up desert churn Kyuss and a young Josh Homme built is itself the loop, movement arriving nowhere. The closing dare to stare down one's own dread reads less as courage than as the same wheel turning over again.","fv":2,"themes":["freedom & escape","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"escape that cannot complete because the fled thing is welded to the fleeing self","built":"the desert riff's own looping structure is the circularity the lyric describes"},
"kyuss~asteroid":{"haiku":"Primal hunger and sexual dominance fuel relentless movement; the speaker burns through encounters leaving destruction in visceral aftermath.","opus":"A desert-rock hymn to engines, speed and carnal lust that shifts into a wistful farewell, sad to watch someone go as love links hands the wrong way.","sonnet":"A three-part suite: the first section is about the raw sexual power of a car and a woman merged into one force; the instrumental middle is wordless; the third section shifts to the melancholy of someone who loved and lost, watching their former partner go and feeling the sadness of parting with tender ambivalence.","src":"haiku"},
"kyuss~black-widow":{"haiku":"A deadly woman kills without moral distinction for business or pleasure, destroying her pursuer regardless of circumstance or method.","opus":"A terse, menacing demand for a reason to stay, the singer coldly noting that killing for business or pleasure ends the same way, either choice a loss. Kyuss were the Palm Desert pioneers of stoner rock, and this early cut rides their trademark down-tuned, fuzz-drenched groove born of generator parties in the California desert, John Garcia's snarl looming over riffs thick as heat haze.","sonnet":"A man demanding a reason to stay and finding none — the refrain is pure desperation, a voice stripped raw asking for any justification to remain. The song's one hard truth is delivered flat: whether killing is business or pleasure, whatever choices are made, whoever holds the power, the outcome is loss for everyone involved.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cornered ultimatum: the song is one voice demanding a single reason to stay before it walks, and the answer it gets back is that there isn't one. The chorus is pure appetite and impatience, an escalating grab for anything at all, capped by the flat request for a reason to remain. The verse then closes every exit — kill for money or for the fun of it, pick your method, none of it changes the outcome, because whichever way you play it you come out the loser. That verdict is what makes the black-widow figure land: staying is fatal, but the demand to stay keeps repeating anyway, hunger and doom locked in the same short loop.","fv":2,"fnote":"The one Wretch track carried over as the original Sons of Kyuss EP recording (1990), with founding bassist Chris Cockrell — not re-recorded for the album.","themes":["anger & defiance","love & desire","death & grief"],"means":"hunger and doom locked in the same repeated plea","built":"the chorus loops back to guarantee no exit exists"},
"kyuss~catamaran":{"haiku":"A journey of surrender and release, where the speaker accepts being carried toward transcendence, asking another to release their grip and let go of control.","opus":"A hazy, sensuous surrender in which the narrator, soothed by stones and cool air, lets another person carry him toward the divine while pleading for a little space and release.","sonnet":"A speaker surrenders to a transcendent, sensory experience — cool air, smooth stones — addressing someone whose presence feels spiritually transporting, while asking them to loosen their hold and trust that the journey leads somewhere beyond ordinary will.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man lays his body down and offers it up to something larger, then quietly recoils from the grip that wants to claim it. The two verses stay almost identical, but their small changes carry everything: the ground beneath him drifts from under his back to somewhere behind him, and the pleasant chill of the first pass hardens into a bite. Ecstatic release curdles into being handled, and the chorus keeps its nerve, refusing the guiding hand and asking only to be let loose. Fittingly for the band's last record, it is a leaving song wearing the robes of a devotional one.","fv":2,"fnote":"A borrowed song from the source of the source: Catamaran was written by Gary Arce of Yawning Man, the unrecorded generator-party band the Kyuss members watched in the desert as teenagers — and on this recording the drummer is Alfredo Hernández, Yawning Man’s own co-founder, playing his friend’s song into the only official release it would have for another two decades.","themes":["freedom & escape","faith & the occult"],"means":"ecstatic release curdling into being handled and refusing the grip","built":"near-identical verses whose small changes carry everything: chill hardens to bite"},
"kyuss~conan-troutman":{"haiku":"A person experiences burning pain and liberation, burning away physical constraints to reclaim freedom and love from another.","opus":"Kyuss chase a hazy liberation, freed by someone's absence yet stripped down to nothing, brains and teeth burning under a hundred-degree sun. The Palm Desert stoner-rock originators wed love to consumption, freeing the vulture and taking a life from another. Their desert-forged fuzz turns cryptic imagery into a heat-warped meditation on release and possession.","sonnet":"A departure becomes liberation — with someone gone, the speaker finds themselves stripped down, burning, free. The imagery is elemental and hallucinatory: sun, lungs, teeth, a vulture finally released into flight. Love and its absence are rendered as heat and combustion, and the freedom on the other side is as disorienting as it is real.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song where every act of freedom arrives fused to an act of destruction. One image-family grows and releases: a seed feeding a lung, a scavenger bird set loose, a self stripped bare and finally unbound. The other burns and consumes: brain, teeth, a hundred degrees of need, love that reads as draining a life. On Sky Valley, Kyuss let the desert swelter argue the point, so liberation and annihilation end up sounding like one heat.","fv":2,"themes":["freedom & escape","love & desire"],"means":"liberation and annihilation sounding like one heat","built":"the desert fuzz lets the two image-families merge without resolving them"},
"kyuss~demon-cleaner":{"haiku":"The speaker possesses internal demons but also claims mystical power to cleanse and liberate, standing as the only salvation against imposters and deception.","opus":"Desert-rock allegory of scrubbing out inner demons, the narrator half-messiah, half-madman insisting he alone can cleanse the rot within. Beneath the stoner haze it's about self-appointed salvation and the delusion that comes with it.","sonnet":"A figure who has internalized his demons proclaims himself the only one who can clean them out — part messianic, part unhinged, telling you to ignore the imposters and trust his strange, naked freedom.","src":"haiku","fable":"A private ritual of scrubbing one's own inner rot curdles into outright self-deification, the narrator promoting himself from patient to savior across four verses. The vocabulary stays fixed on hygiene and polish, but its object migrates outward: what starts as sweeping filth from his own skull ends with him crowning himself the sole remedy for everyone. The bridge exposes the mechanism, a paranoiac who recasts anyone offering him ordinary care as a fraud sent to dupe him, so refusing help becomes proof of his election rather than his sickness. That this messiah is also a madman from a drab Los Angeles suburb keeps the grandeur tethered to delusion. Kyuss render the spiral as desert-heavy trance, the riff circling like a fixed idea that will not loosen.","fv":2,"fnote":"Homme has admitted what the demon is: the song is really about brushing your teeth — he suffered nightmares of drowning in toothbrush foam and blood, and the line about reaching the back ones means the molars. Garcia, asked about deeper readings, offered only: if you get something deeper out of it, I think that’s great.","themes":["madness & the mind","faith & the occult"],"means":"self-cleaning ritual escalating into self-appointed salvation for everyone","built":"the circling desert riff enacts the fixed idea that will not loosen"},
"kyuss~el-rodeo":{"haiku":"The speaker addresses Johnny with a cycle of reciprocal threat—if Johnny tries something without permission, the speaker will do the same in return.","opus":"Kyuss, the Palm Desert crew who forged desert-rock heaviness in California's outdoor generator parties, offer a terse warning wrapped in ranch imagery. Addressed to a figure named Johnny, the words promise reciprocity: whatever he tries to take for free will be returned in kind. Repetition and the chanted rodeo refrain lend menace to what reads as a blunt threat of tit for tat.","sonnet":"A terse ultimatum directed at someone named Johnny who keeps trying to take without contributing. The speaker makes clear that every attempt to freeload or press for more will be met with the same consequence — a spare, repetitive warning that circles back on itself like the rodeo it invokes.","src":"haiku","fable":"There is almost no lyric here, and that is the reading: a name (Johnny), a single conditional threat that whatever you try on me I will do right back to you, and a title-word chanted down to pure incantation. Kyuss build the meaning in the low end rather than the language, so the words function as a taunt looped past sense until only the ritual of it remains, an eye-for-an-eye standoff worn smooth by repetition. Fittingly, this is late Kyuss, from their 1995 farewell record, where the trance-groove has fully swallowed the sentence and the rodeo becomes less an image than a mantra for a band already circling the drain of its own last ride.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"reciprocal threat worn smooth past meaning by repetition","built":"meaning built entirely in the low end"},
"kyuss~freedom-run":{"opus":"A desperate, obsessive craving for someone the narrator would steal and cross deserts for; freedom is entangled with possessing and merging with this person, an almost incurable need.","sonnet":"An obsessive, desperate drive to break free and reach someone who feels necessary — the narrator will steal, walk endlessly through desert heat, and sacrifice anything for the chance at union or escape, though freedom itself may be the destination.","src":"sonnet","fable":"The song sets a lover's ransom against a chant of running loose, and can't decide which one it actually wants. Over Kyuss's Palm Desert crawl, the singer swears he'd cross the whole baking waste to be fused with someone he admits he'll only ever hold briefly before he's gone. The two-word mantra bookending everything is the tell: liberty and captivity share the same breath, so the promised getaway sounds identical to the compulsion he can't quit. By the second verse the vow turns into a question left hanging, unsure whether such devotion is even in him. What repeats is not an answer but the itch itself, a motor idling with nowhere to go.","fv":2,"fnote":"The record carries a private weight: it is dedicated to Nick Oliveri’s father, who died the year before it was made — and it was Oliveri’s last album with Kyuss, the bassist gone before Sky Valley.","themes":["freedom & escape","love & desire"],"means":"liberty and captivity sharing one breath in the mantra","built":"the two-word title refrain makes escape and possession indistinguishable"},
"kyuss~gardenia":{"haiku":"A high-powered vehicle becomes a metaphor for sexual prowess and dominance, with the narrator reveling in speed, power, and conquest. The song celebrates unbridled aggression and physical power.","opus":"Desert-rock horsepower as pure sensation, sex and speed and combustion fused into one roaring engine. Kyuss aren't after meaning here, just the heavy, sun-baked momentum of the ride itself.","sonnet":"The lyrics celebrate a powerful, road-worn vehicle — a transport queen — through imagery of engine noise, burning fuel, speed, and physical exhilaration, treating the car and the drive as a form of ecstatic communion. The experience is described in hyperbolic, almost sexual terms.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track that kicks off Sky Valley is a hot-rod hymn built on one collapse: the muscle car and the woman fold into a single object of devotion, so the purring, fuel-burning motor is literally framed as an act of making love. The second verse escalates that worship into the sacred and profane at once, patching the throttle straight to God while the top speed clocks in at 666 and a devil's grin rides underneath the ecstasy, which is the joke and the point together: deified velocity is also blasphemy. By the outro the meaning is in the erosion itself, as language burns off under the riff until nothing survives but forward motion and one repeated curse, worship reduced to pure combustion.","fv":2,"fnote":"The record refuses to be skipped through: its ten songs shipped as three unbroken movements on the CD, under a liner command printed in capitals — LISTEN WITHOUT DISTRACTION — and Homme later said they wanted it to be hell to play on a CD player.","themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult"],"means":"car and woman fused into one devotional object","built":"language burns off under the riff until only a curse remains"},
"kyuss~gloria-lewis":{"haiku":"A defiant refusal to be subdued or located by an adversary; the speaker claims wild independence and threatens violence in return. It's an anthem of untameable restlessness and aggression.","opus":"Kyuss, the Palm Desert stoner-rock progenitors, build menace over a groove thick with heat and grit. Fleeting emotions, treachery, and violent cravings coil into a swaggering dare to court danger, with threats of a beating shifting from one voice to a gang. The desert-forged riffing turns oblique, half-sinister imagery into a hypnotic trance.","sonnet":"A rough, semi-coherent voice navigates the edges of excess and confrontation: the thrill rides wild, the drinking continues past any limit, and anyone who intrudes into this basement world of abandon gets pushed back hard. The lyrics resist easy narrative, conveying atmosphere of lawless energy more than linear story.","src":"haiku","fable":"A woozy, half-menacing come-on strung between two image families that keep collapsing into each other: intoxication (a promise to quit and the immediate flinch that it won't hold, the wild horse you dare each other to mount) and evasion-into-violence (the basement where you can't be found, the fist to the head). The pleasure and the threat are the same gesture, which is the point rather than a contradiction to solve. The real move is structural: the second and third verses are the same taunt printed twice, except the I and you of the come-on become we and us the second time through, the solitary bad-trip narrator resolving into a pack. Fitting for the closing record of a band already pulling apart, the song reads less as a story than as that shift itself, private wreckage hardening into collective swagger.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"pleasure and threat held as the same gesture without contradiction","built":"repeated verse shifts from I and you to we and us, solitude hardening into pack"},
"kyuss~green-machine":{"haiku":"Rebellion against systems of control and consumption powered by greed. The narrator shuts down complicity and burns away corrupting forces.","opus":"Desert-rock stoners turn a war machine inward against greed itself, the green being money as much as the desert flora they'd rather be left alone with. A druggy anthem of dropping out and gunning down the system's appetite for more.","sonnet":"The speaker describes an internal war and a drive to dismantle greed — specifically \"shutting down\" the greed for money (\"green\") — while the machine metaphor suggests both a weapon being loaded and a system being subverted. There is also a wish for simple, peaceful isolation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A self-appointed avenger primes his weaponry against everyone else's appetite for profit, never noticing that he names himself part of the very apparatus he swears he's dismantling. That contradiction is the whole engine: he brags about grinding the machinery to a halt while confessing in the same breath that he keeps the system turning, so his crusade against hoarded cash doubles as an admission of his own guilt. When a moment of roadside gentleness arrives — quiet blossoms underfoot, a hospitable stretch of open air — he snarls it off with mock courtesy, because tenderness is the one thing his fury can't process. Fitting for a band raised on Palm Desert generator parties, this is a scorched sermon whose righteous fuel is indistinguishable from the pollution it condemns.","fv":2,"fnote":"Written by the drummer: Green Machine is Brant Bjork’s song, and it became the album’s single in 1993 — its green the color of money, not the other thing.","themes":["anger & defiance","money & the street"],"means":"righteous fuel indistinguishable from the pollution it condemns","built":"the avenger names himself part of the apparatus he swears he is dismantling"},
"kyuss~hurricane":{"haiku":"An expression of emotional numbness, resignation, and suffocation. The speaker feels nothing, cannot breathe, and has lost the capacity to move forward—their life has destroyed them.","opus":"A numb, suffocating state of mind, insisting on feeling nothing while begging to be lifted up out of suffering that has overwhelmed the speaker's life.","sonnet":"A portrait of emotional numbness and physical suffocation — the narrator feels nothing and cannot breathe, overwhelmed by a life that has worn them down completely.","src":"haiku","fable":"Opening the band's final album, this is a song built almost entirely from refusals stacked on repetition: a numbness that keeps insisting on itself, a discovery it swears it will never claim, a suffocation it can barely name. The lyric barely changes across its two turns, and that near-stasis is the point, the words hardening from a defiant won't into a helpless can't so that the vow of not feeling collapses into an admission that nothing can get in or out anyway. Read against the title, the storm is entirely interior and entirely still, a person flattened by their own life while performing the toughness of not being touched by it, and the song lives in that gap between the mask of indifference and the airless panic underneath.","fv":2,"fnote":"The title’s prophecy was nearly immediate: released in July 1995, the record outlived its band by three months — last show in September, dissolved by October — so the opener’s airless refusals were the first thing most listeners heard from a circus that had already left.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance"],"means":"a vow of not-feeling that collapses into its own proof","built":"the exterior storm title names an entirely interior and entirely still state"},
"kyuss~isolation":{"haiku":"A person feels small and insignificant, judged by another's gaze despite having seemed fine in memory, trapped in cruel solitude.","opus":"A brooding dismissal of someone whose sin and self-regard have curdled, the singer telling them not to wait or come to him because the way they see him is far too small, isolation curdling into desolation. Kyuss forged the stoner-rock template in the California desert, and this track rides Josh Homme's warm fuzz and Garcia's aching drawl into a slow, sun-baked kiss-off.","sonnet":"A severing delivered as a verdict — the speaker sees someone whose outer surface looked fine but whose interior is rotten with sin and sweetened blood, and issues a clean dismissal: don't wait at the edge of the world, don't come at all. What the other person thinks of the speaker is too small and too cheap to matter, the distance the only honest response to the desolation between them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup staged as a verdict: the singer keeps someone at arm's length because closeness only exposes the rot beneath a clean surface. The lone verse arraigns the other for a private, bloodstained guilt, and from there the song drills one split over and over. Pictured from a safe remove she looks fine; up close she isn't, and that gap between seeming and being is the isolation the title keeps naming. The bridge hardens it into an order not to wait, not to follow, not to reach at all, her whole sense of him shrinking word by repeated word until it counts for nothing. What sounds like rejection is really self-defense, the desolation he pins on her being the empty room he has chosen to stand in alone.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"rejection disguised as self-defense from a rot beneath","built":"the bridge shrinks him word by word until he counts for nothing"},
"kyuss~love-has-passed-me-by":{"haiku":"Bitter rejection hardens into hatred; love slips away as the narrator curses the departing lover and promises they will never change or escape him.","opus":"Kyuss, the desert-rock forebears whose fuzz-heavy grooves rose from the Palm Desert scene of California, ride a churning riff into pure spite. The narrator, refusing to be treated as blind or dumb, watches a lover walk away and lets hate burn through his veins, resolving to inflict the pain he feels. Bitterness curdles into the recurring dread that love itself has simply passed him by.","sonnet":"Fury and heartbreak fuse as a man watches someone walk away, his hate burning through his veins like evidence that love never actually arrived. He turns the pain outward, threatening retribution even as he knows nothing about this will change him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A young man's breakup fury that keeps flinching from grief into aggression because grief is the thing he can't say out loud. The verses are pure defensive posturing, all snarled contempt and threats to make her hurt the way he does, but the chorus lets the mask slip: the real fear underneath is that love itself has simply skipped over him and always will. That gap is the whole song. The rage in the veins is a wound talking tough, and each return to the same wondering question undercuts the swagger, so the final verse looping back to the opening insults reads less like resolution than a boy stuck circling the one soft admission he refuses to sit in.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"rage performing as armor over the one soft admission","built":"the final verse loops to the opening insults without resolution"},
"kyuss~n-o":{"haiku":"Isolation and directionlessness are presented as inevitable; the speaker lives alone without purpose, following unspoken rules that bind them to meaninglessness. Loneliness appears inescapable.","opus":"Kyuss drape existential drift in desert fuzz. Disoriented and half-lost, the narrator drifts through solitude, half-convinced he's already gone, then dismisses the whole thing as merely a tune worth humming. Pleading vaguely for time and affection, he waves off his own unraveling, the band's heavy sludge making surrender sound oddly light.","sonnet":"A drifter without direction or home lives by a set of externally issued rules — just like they said — singing the song of his own aimlessness as though it were a given. The request for love emerges once, quietly, beneath the resigned surface of a life spent perpetually crossing someone else's line with no clear destination of his own.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man narrates his own undoing as if reciting a verdict handed down, treating collapse as a foretold script rather than a thing he chose. Nearly every stanza credits the outcome to some vague authority, and that tag is the mask: fatalism worn to make abandonment sound settled instead of suffered. He lives with no home to return to, waking disoriented and half-sure he is already gone. Midway he waves it off as merely a tune to be sung, a shrug that holds the wound at arm's length. Then the outro drops the pose and simply begs, three times, for love, exposing the plea the fatalism was covering all along.","fv":2,"fnote":"The song is an heirloom: written in Across the River, Mario Lalli’s pre-Kyuss band from the generator-party days, and covered here at Brant Bjork’s suggestion as a tribute — with Lalli himself sitting in on lead guitar, playing his own song into the genre it helped invent.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"fatalism worn as a mask over a plea for love","built":"the outro drops the pose and simply begs three times, exposing what fatalism covered"},
"kyuss~odyssey":{"haiku":"A transformative journey into darkness tests the speaker, burning and transforming them irreversibly. The internal inferno left by this ordeal leaves them fundamentally changed and damaged.","opus":"Kyuss stage a mythic ordeal as ritual incantation, a guide leading the listener into a monstrous trial from which no one returns unchanged. The California desert-rock band render transformation as an elemental crucible of fire and cold, the churning riff and chant treating hardship as something that consumes and rebuilds the soul.","sonnet":"A journey into something vast and consuming — described as the belly of a beast — leaves whoever enters permanently altered. Fire rages on the mountain and inside the creature simultaneously, and the only instruction is to shut it down. The transformation is inescapable; one does not return the same.","src":"haiku","fable":"A blues-borrowed initiation myth staged as desert-rock incantation: you descend into the thing that scares you, then come back marked. The verses run an ordeal by stages, from mountain and sea to a plunge inside a devouring dark where flame both scorches and enfolds the spirit. Each passage leaves the traveler altered, no longer whoever first set out. The chorus refuses to explain any of it, barking a clipped two-word shutdown over and over, sealing the mystery instead of narrating it.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","faith & the occult","nature & the elements"],"means":"descent into the devouring dark as the only route back marked","built":"chorus seals the mystery with a two-word shutdown rather than narrating the ordeal"},
"kyuss~one-inch-man":{"haiku":"Emotional isolation and loneliness of a small, overlooked man.","opus":"A lonely soul aching for connection and physical intimacy, doubting whether the desired person truly wants to hold him.","sonnet":"A small, lonely man craving intimacy and connection, wondering if anyone could truly want or hold him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A near-wordless chant built almost entirely on one shrinking figure and the drone of a single adjective, so the song lives in what the repetition performs rather than what it states. The one-inch man is a self-diminishing pun that shades from small stature toward sexual inadequacy, and the counting-off of steps forward and back turns that shrinkage into a mechanical, going-nowhere ritual. The bridge is where the mask slips: the swaggering cool of Kyuss’s final album drops into raw, repeated pleading about whether the other person actually wants him at all, so the loneliness the chorus keeps flatly announcing is revealed as the real subject the diminishment was covering for.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"swaggering cool cracked open by repeated pleading about being wanted","built":"the shrinking figure is a pun that slides from stature into sexual inadequacy","fnote":"The writer’s own gloss backs the loneliness reading: Garcia dreamed up the one-inch man while stoned on a fishing trip — a tragic little lonely guy, the only one of his kind — brought him to rehearsal for Reeder to build the riff around, and warned against deciphering him much further."},
"kyuss~phototropic":{"haiku":"A speaker reaches toward light and sun as salvation, discovering smallness and safety in finding the roof—a limit that means no falling.","opus":"Named for a plant's helpless turning toward light, this Kyuss track from the desert-rock scene distills a single impulse: reaching upward toward a beam or sun never seen before. Discovering the roof, the smallness of everything, comes with a strange reassurance against falling. The looping refusal that closes it leaves the striving unresolved, suspended between aspiration and limit.","sonnet":"A pull toward illumination — toward a light never before encountered — drives the narrator upward. Reaching for the sun becomes both an act of survival and a kind of transcendence, while the discovery of a roof above suggests limits that cannot trap what has already chosen to rise.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title supplies the whole conceit: phototropism, a plant's blind bending toward light, and the sparse verses stage exactly that reflex as something between salvation and instinct you can't choose out of. Reaching for the beam and finding the low ceiling of a room read as the same gesture, the ascent and its hard limit arriving together, so the light that pulls you upward is also what shows you how small the space is. Then the song abandons statement entirely and just repeats the negation, a promise cut off before its verb, so the bridge becomes the thing that never resolves rather than describing it. Fitting territory for …And the Circus Leaves Town, Kyuss’s last record: the growth-toward-light impulse left hanging on a you'll never that refuses to name what won't happen, an ending that stalls out instead of landing.","fv":2,"themes":["aspiration & limits","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"the growth impulse left hanging on a refusal that never names what won't happen","built":"the bridge stalls on a cut-off promise, enacting the limit it describes"},
"kyuss~size-queen":{"opus":"Desert-rock pioneers channel heat-hazed obsession into a hypnotic ode disguised as reverence for water, a clean, deep, refreshing bowl. Innuendo simmers beneath the imagery of diving down, the repeated cry of shallow undercutting the worship. Craving and compulsion drive the trance, want circling endlessly toward a plunge to the ground.","sonnet":"A transfixed diver gazing into a clear, impossibly shallow body of water that looks deep — the song is about the seductive pull of something beautiful that threatens to swallow you, and diving in anyway inches from the bottom.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A brute joke pretending to be a nature hymn: the verses rhapsodize a clean, blue, refreshing body of water in the swooning language of first sight, while a single dry word planted after every line deflates it into something puddle-thin, so the ecstatic surface and the deadpan undercut are the whole song's structure. The chorus makes the gag physical, diving hard only to hit ground inches down, the punchline of overcommitting to something too small to hold you, which the title flips into crude sexual mockery. On ...And the Circus Leaves Town, Kyuss's last record, that near-static loop reads as much as fatigue as comedy, a band bottoming out a joke and refusing to embellish it, the flatness itself the point.","fv":2,"themes":["comedy & wordplay","nature & the elements"],"means":"ecstatic reverence deflated by one dry word per line","built":"the title flips the nature-hymn into crude mockery"},
"kyuss~son-of-a-bitch":{"opus":"A bitter, misogynistic tirade against a woman valued only for her body, warning she'll never last or win; it curdles into disorientation as the narrator realizes the nightmare he wants to wake from is actually his waking reality.","sonnet":"A bleary, frustrated observation about sexual politics and the futility of competing with young people who play games — the narrator feels the world has changed around them in a disturbing, blurry way, and longs to be told it was just a bad dream.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A tirade that curses everything it looks at eventually turns the gun on itself. The first two verses sneer at a scene of exhibited bodies and social gamesmanship, cataloguing another person's assets while promising they'll rot, contempt aimed outward like the title's endlessly repeated oath. Then the third verse drops the pose entirely: the speaker admits a cold has crept into him, his surroundings have gone out of focus, and he's begging a woman to shake him loose from what he takes for sleep. The trick is the closing twist, where he concedes there's no rousing him because he was conscious the whole time and this haze is simply his life now. The looping curse, it turns out, was never about them.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance"],"means":"contempt aimed outward until the gun turns on the speaker","built":"the third verse drops the pose to reveal the curse was always self-directed"},
"kyuss~space-cadet":{"haiku":"Someone waits alone in solitude, expecting nothing from those around them, cleansed by earth and sun but perpetually abandoned by the world that never arrives.","opus":"Desert-rock stillness about waiting for a deliverance that never arrives, a narrator sunbaked and dissolving into the landscape, aware something in him has gone missing. The Palm Desert emptiness becomes a state of suspended, cleansing paralysis.","sonnet":"A person alone on the edge of the world, scorched by sun and sand, waiting for something — the world — to arrive, knowing it never will, lying in the margins of a relationship with someone who expects nothing from them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about awaiting a deliverance that has quietly stopped being possible, sung as though stillness were peace. The speaker lies at the desert's edge, letting sun and grit scour him down to something clean and empty, and mistakes that hollowing for calm. But the closing refrain keeps insisting on an arrival that refuses to arrive, chanted past hope into a flat mantra. On Sky Valley this is Kyuss at their most narcotic and becalmed, and the repetition is the real subject: not patience but a mind that has learned to dress its own abandonment as serenity, so it never has to admit nothing is on its way.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"abandonment dressed as serenity so it never has to be named","built":"the closing refrain chanted past hope into a flat mantra"},
"kyuss~spaceship-landing":{"haiku":"Someone rejects a toxic, controlling person whose backward influence has exhausted them, refusing to continue their games.","opus":"Desert-rock burnout channeling a poisoned relationship into a haze of resentment and threat, a narrator fed up with lies and half-wishing for violence while unsure whether to stay or go. The stoner groove keeps the menace loose and unfocused.","sonnet":"A fraying relationship song — one person has been sending mixed signals and saying things that hurt, and the narrator oscillates between wanting to follow them and wanting them gone, eventually identifying the other person as fundamentally broken.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that keeps flinching between blaming a ruined partner and quietly blaming itself, never settling on which, until it just numbs out. The verses run on drained, backwards talk that has stopped meaning anything between two people. Then the chorus drops all nuance for a flat verdict on a broken man with a broken scheme, the one thing the speaker can still say plainly. The next verse folds that certainty inward, second-guessing in a panic whether it felt or confessed more than it was allowed, half-wishing for blood. The closing mantra answers everything by chanting that it is all fine, over and over, a calm so insistent it reads as shutdown rather than peace, and as Kyuss's last album fades out on it, the reassurance doubles as a band talking itself down from its own ending.","fv":2,"fnote":"After the closer fades, a long silence hides one more piece: Day One, first issued on the Demon Cleaner single as a dedication to Dave and Krist — the band’s farewell record ends with a quiet condolence to Nirvana’s two survivors, one drummer’s message to another a year after Cobain’s death.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"reassurance chanted so insistently it reads as shutdown not peace","built":"closing mantra doubling as a band talking itself down from its own ending"},
"kyuss~supa-scoopa-and-mighty-scoop":{"haiku":"Grief and obligation in a relationship that has ended, with the speaker torn between attachment and the necessity of letting go.","opus":"A narrator watches someone leave, insisting he can live without them yet aching at the departure and calling them his home. Palm Desert stoner-rock pioneers Kyuss ground their heavy fuzz in wistful resignation, the wandering imagery and repeated farewell tracing a loss the speaker accepts even as it saddens him.","sonnet":"Grief and release blur together as someone processes the departure of a person who was both home and anchor. The fragility of what is lost sharpens only in absence, and the admission that the other was the home makes the goodbye both devastating and strangely liberating.","src":"haiku","fable":"A goodbye that never lands cleanly, sung in the half-buried desert-rock register where the voice is one more instrument riding Homme's fuzz rather than a narrator. The words come as fragments that flip between staying and being left, tenderness and contempt in the same breath, and the song keeps circling a single admission: it is sad to watch someone go, so I would rather just let you know. The chorus strips even that to bare nouns, naming the departing one as home and then immediately as alone and on his own, so the loss lands as a paradox rather than a scene. On Welcome to Sky Valley the meaning is doing exactly this, refusing to resolve the split between belonging and abandonment and letting the riff carry what the lyric declines to spell out.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"loss named as paradox rather than scene and left to the riff","built":"the departing one called home then immediately alone in the same chorus"},
"kyuss~tangy-zizzle":{"haiku":"A speaker follows and believes in a listener's lead, but questions authority figures and societal pressure—asserting faith in the other person over institutional judgment.","opus":"Kyuss loosen their desert groove into something reckless and devoted, the narrator pledging to follow wherever a lover leads and go wherever she goes. Rainy mornings bring no complaint, and teacher and everyone else are waved off with grinning defiance. The refrain fixates on where she wants him to be, a repeated question that reads as both surrender and giddy uncertainty about his own place.","sonnet":"Devotion stripped to its most physical grammar: wherever one person goes, the other follows, in rain and chaos alike. The lyric circles a single anxious question about where to be, while dismissing teachers and outsiders as irrelevant to this compact.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song of total deference that keeps asking to be told where to stand: the verses pledge to go wherever the other goes and to hold steady when the sky breaks, but the chorus dissolves all that devotion into a single question repeated until it stops sounding like love and starts sounding like a person who has surrendered the ability to locate himself. The lone flare of defiance in verse two, cursing the teacher and the others who could never understand, only sharpens the trap, since even the rebellion is offered up for someone else's approval. Fitting for the wrung-out, road-weary mood of the band's final album, the track reads less as romance than as the exhaustion of a will that has outsourced its own coordinates.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","love & desire","anger & defiance"],"means":"devotion so complete it dissolves the speaker","built":"the chorus question repeats until surrender sounds lost"},
"kyuss~thong-song":{"haiku":"A careless person with simple existence dismisses sentimentality; they live carelessly without brains or shoes, indifferent to others.","opus":"The desert-rock pioneers offer a loose, sun-baked shrug of a track. The narrator wants no mysticism, cut his ties ages ago, and can't abide anything slow, claiming shaggy hair, bare feet, and zero interest in whatever grief anyone else feels. It all melts into a mocking cheer, a stoned, flippant refusal to take anything, itself included, remotely seriously.","sonnet":"A loose, sun-bleached figure with long hair and no shoes has signed away ambition and opted entirely out of effort and convention. Slow songs are the one true enemy; everything else, including the opinion of anyone watching, is a matter of absolute indifference.","src":"haiku","fable":"A terse desert-rock kiss-off from a narrator who waves off every sentimental gesture, inventorying himself as long-haired, flip-flop-shod, and proudly unremarkable while dismissing someone's tears. The chanted praise of the outro curdles into sarcasm, turning the fake cheer into a shrug of contempt.","fv":2,"fnote":"The title beat Sisqó’s identically named hit by seven years — and the one story attached to the song, via Songfacts, is that Homme wrote it about a prostitute’s working life. The lyric itself admits only to long hair and flip-flops; the band has never said.","themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"cheerful self-inventory curdling into a shrug of contempt","built":"the chanted outro praise turns sarcastic without changing a single word"},
"kyuss~thumb":{"haiku":"One person controls another with a lighter held by their thumb, burning them and keeping them subjugated to their will regardless of resistance.","opus":"Kyuss weaponize desert heat into pure contempt, the narrator brushing off someone's feelings and bragging that he's already scorched them with a flick of his lighter. He mocks their failed attempt to shut him down, the parched Californian landscape that shaped the band's sound grounding the scorn in dust and drought.","sonnet":"Cold contempt directed at someone who tried and failed to diminish the narrator. The speaker, unmoved and unmovable, holds the power of destruction casually — a lighter pressed down by a thumb — while the other person is left stranded in delusion, on desert ground, kneeling.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track that kicks off Blues for the Red Sun opens Kyuss's world exactly where the band lives it: a sun-blasted expanse where someone stronger holds someone weaker under his control, indifferent to their pain. The governing image is domination by pressure, a flame pinned open by a thumb, the addressee scorched, brought to their knees on the same desert ground the whole record is set on, made to grovel and drown in the well the singer has already decided is their hell. The hook is deliberately slurred so that a taunt about the target being lost in their own head can also be heard as being ground down, the phrase eroding into pure sound the way the guitars do, so that meaning dissolves into the low, repeating churn rather than resolving. It is less a story than a boot on a neck rendered as a groove, contempt made physical and looped until it stops needing words at all.","fv":2,"fnote":"The heaviness was engineered: Chris Goss produced at Sound City with Homme’s guitar tuned down to C and run through Ampeg bass cabinets — and when Metallica took Kyuss to Australia in early 1993, the band got the full PA exactly once; after opening night it was cut in half for the remaining eight shows.","themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"contempt made physical and looped until it stops needing words","built":"the hook deliberately slurs so domination and lost-in-your-own-head blur into pure sound"},
"kyuss~whitewater":{"haiku":"Mountains move and trees grow; after transformation, the narrator returns home fulfilled, questioning past beauty against belonging.","opus":"Kyuss drift toward homecoming, asking whether to waste time beneath a loving sun and valley sky before wordless vocals dissolve into arrival. Closing their landmark desert-rock album, the Palm Desert band lets fuzzed grooves and open space do the talking, mountains moved and trees grown, until the repeated declaration that it is over and he is coming home feels earned.","sonnet":"A return to something essential — sunshine passing, valleys and skies quietly accepted rather than chased. The mountain moves itself, the trees have grown, and when it is over, home is where the speaker arrives without fanfare. A sense of arrival after long drift, expressed in the simplest possible terms: the journey done, the feeling of being where one belongs.","src":"haiku","fable":"Closing the desert-rock pilgrimage of Welcome to Sky Valley, this is less a lyric than an arrival rite, its few plain words strung between long wordless vowels so that meaning gathers in the humming rather than the sentences. The first verse asks whether it is worth lingering in the valley beneath the sky; by the second the question dissolves, the wandering is declared over, and the same syllable-chant resolves each time into the flat certainty that the singer is home. The movement is a settling: doubt in the opening address to the sunshine, then release, the vast open riff and the melting drone doing the work of belonging that words cannot, so that the record ends not on a statement but on the sound of being finally still.","fv":2,"fnote":"The valley is real: the road sign on the cover stands in Sky Valley, off Dillon Road in the Coachella desert, photographed by Alex Solca — now a pilgrimage site where fans gather annually, and which vandals have tried, and failed, to steal.","themes":["homecoming","nature & the elements"],"means":"doubt in the opening dissolves into the sound of being finally still","built":"plain words strung between long wordless vowels so belonging gathers in the humming"},
"kyuss~writhe":{"haiku":"A sardonic observer watches society's follies and hypocrisy with mockery, refusing to conform while sensing an undercurrent of danger and manipulation.","opus":"A wry, playful jab at metal poseurs singing for Satan and teasing their hair, the singer preferring to tease the listener and celebrate a love whose kiss makes the ride worthwhile. Kyuss, the desert-rock originators from Palm Desert, let their heavy fuzz loosen into cowboy-boot swagger here, the sardonic humour and warm affection cutting against the doom-laden scene they helped create.","sonnet":"A gleeful mockery of metal posturing wrapped inside a genuine love song — the singer laughs at big-haired Satan-worshippers and his own failed sewing projects, then without warning pivots into something tender: all the roads lead to one person whose smile outweighs the miles and whose kiss makes the whole ridiculous journey worth it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A young Palm Desert band mocks the hair-metal scene it grew up beside, then drops the sneer for something it actually means. The opening verses are pure eye-roll: the puffed-up manes, the Satan-baiting posturing, a narrator who'd rather needle these preening peacocks than join them, right down to lost fringed cowboy boots and jeans that came out embarrassingly baggy. The chorus turns the mockery physical, daring the poseurs to squirm along the floor while an endless line of replacements shuffles in behind them. Then the last verse abruptly means it, trading the whole act for one person worth every road driven. The joke, it turns out, was armor around a plain devotion.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"scorn used as armor around a plain unironic devotion","built":"the final verse means it after the joke"},
"l-borges~o-trem-azul":{"haiku":"A lover boards a blue train under the sun; the imagery captures unsaid words and fleeting moments carried away by wind, lingering in memory.","opus":"things left unsaid that the wind keeps reminding the singer of, and a dreamy blue-train ride under the sun","sonnet":"things left unsaid to a departed person, carried away on the wind like a departing train in the sun","src":"haiku"},
"l7~can-i-run":{"haiku":"Constant fear of masculine violence transforms daily survival into paranoia and desperate flight from invisible threat.","opus":"A woman's daily dread of male violence, armoring herself with sunglasses and headphones and clutching her mace, never certain whether the man trailing her carries a blade or a firearm. The chorus recasts the whole society as a hostile regime a woman must always be poised to escape.","sonnet":"A woman describes the constant, body-level terror of navigating public space — sensible shoes, sunglasses, headphones to block out threat — living in what she calls a terrorist state, scanning every shadow for the man who might be following her.","src":"haiku"},
"l7~everglade":{"haiku":"A girl defends herself against drunk aggression at a rock show through physical retaliation and refusal. Self-protection and pride matter more than deference or politeness.","opus":"A tale of a woman named Everglade who heads to a rock show, refuses to be shoved around in the pit by a drunk aggressor, and physically throws him out, a riot-grrrl stand against harassment.","sonnet":"Everglade sneaks out to a rock show, gets harassed by a drunk bruiser in the pit, warns him off, and when he keeps swinging she outmanoeuvres him until he trips on his own feet and gets rolled out the door — a story of holding your line against a bully.","src":"haiku"},
"l7~pretend-we-re-dead":{"haiku":"Collective silence and faked death become weapons against oppressive systems—by withdrawing and refusing to participate, people reclaim agency from control.","opus":"The L.A. band who sharpened grunge's feminist edge treat apathy as both an affliction and a weapon. Amid creeping conformity and constant surveillance, playing lifeless becomes a way to escape being controlled, and the bridge flips it into a rallying call: unite against an authority that is neither righteous nor a true majority, and never surrender your individuality.","sonnet":"A collective withholds its energy and voice as a tactical refusal — by feigning death, the crowd denies surveillance and control systems the signal they need to function. Unity and willful invisibility become instruments of resistance against forces that profit from compliance.","src":"haiku"},
"l7~shitlist":{"haiku":"When angry, the speaker writes out names of people who frustrate them and won't be missed. It's a simple list of grudges against those who bum them out or challenge them.","opus":"A blunt riot-grrrl kiss-off, channeling anger into a literal enemies list, the pure cathartic pleasure of naming everyone who's wronged you and consigning them to contempt.","sonnet":"About coping with frustration by cataloguing everyone who has annoyed you — a blunt, cathartic fantasy of keeping score against people who drain you. There's no deeper violence; the list is the joke.","src":"haiku"},
"l7~shove":{"haiku":"Multiple external pressures—authorities, money lenders, disapproving relatives, neighbors, employers—constantly invade and control a person's life. They reach a breaking point and threaten violence to reclaim their space.","opus":"L7's blast of working-class rage, a woman fed up with bosses, bills, the IRS and a country that pats itself on the back, ready to shove back. The grievances pile into a joyous threat of pushback.","sonnet":"A litany of daily indignities — debt collectors, smog, a boss demanding conformity, a stranger groping you, a venue that won't pay — that push the narrator to the edge of a violent shove, with the recurring threat functioning as a release valve for every petty humiliation life throws at you.","src":"haiku"},
"l7~slide":{"haiku":"A person tolerates destructive behavior from a leech of a partner, finally reaching their limit and evicting them.","opus":"A gleefully petty kiss-off to a deadbeat partner finally thrown out, cataloguing his squalor with grim humor. Letting it slide flips from tolerance to eviction, resentment vented as garage-punk comedy.","sonnet":"A song about kicking out a useless, destructive partner — he damaged her amp, pissed himself, ruined the bathroom, and she has been tolerating it too long; now his stuff is on the porch and she is done.","src":"haiku"},
"l7~wargasm":{"haiku":"Modern society conflates warfare with sexual excitement, turning human suffering and military violence into entertainment consumed passively from safe distance.","opus":"L7, the LA grunge-punk band, skewer war as pornographic entertainment, indicting a public that performs grief while getting off on televised carnage. The military knows exactly how to arouse the masses, and patriotism stays comfortable as long as the slaughter happens elsewhere. It's blunt, furious anti-war satire.","sonnet":"War is skewered as mass spectacle consumed with pornographic appetite: the public watches body bags and bombing footage with the same feverish excitement as entertainment, waving flags safely remote from the carnage. The Pentagon, the piece suggests, understands arousal better than it admits, and collective moral detachment is the real obscenity on display.","src":"haiku"},
"la-dispute~footsteps-at-the-pond-zeal-ardor-remix":{"haiku":"Two people bleed because they need to, caught in codependency and mutual wounding; footsteps end at a pond suggesting abandonment or drowning.","opus":"La Dispute, known for spoken-word emotional post-hardcore, chart a doomed codependent bond, here recast in a bleaker Zeal and Ardor remix. The narrator, deep in a self-destructive bender, can't understand why the other still believed in him enough to try to save him. Mutual harm becomes the thing binding them as the relationship finally comes apart.","sonnet":"Two people who needed each other in precisely incompatible ways circle the ruins of what they shared — one needed to be saved, the other needed to leave. The pond at the end is where both trails stop, and the cattails scatter seeds that will grow after they are gone.","src":"haiku"},
"la-lune~disparity":{"haiku":"Distance and inequality manifest in disconnection and separation, the speaker acknowledging unbridgeable gap.","opus":"A restless meditation on inner imbalance and the corrosive pull of shame, a psyche worn out from brokering between phases that refuse to align. Imagery of maturity stretched thin evokes irony curdling into flaw, unsettling aftereffects lodged in the head that won't quite dislodge, dissent turned inward.","sonnet":"Maturity worn as frayed irony — the mind done mediating, balance out of phase, shame a thought no one can help thinking. The 'loud and eerie side effects' of self-awareness sit next to 'dissent and defect' as the sum of what growing up actually delivers.","src":"haiku"},
"la-roux~quicksand":{"haiku":"Addictive attraction traps the speaker in ambiguous emotional terrain, where possession and uncertainty offer no solid footing.","opus":"The helplessness of wanting someone who keeps you unsure of where you stand, an obsession you sink deeper into every time they touch you. New-wave gloss over a genuinely uneasy account of love as loss of footing.","sonnet":"An obsessive, forbidden attraction to someone who belongs to another pulls the speaker under: every touch, every glance from this person sends them into quicksand, and even knowing the relationship is broken ground, the speaker cannot learn to stop being dragged back into the dark.","src":"haiku"},
"l-z-rockit~bad-blood":{"haiku":"Predatory violence stalks urban streets; terror spreads through a city as one murderer embodies both legend and shadow, unknowable and unstoppable.","opus":"A retelling of Jack the Ripper, a killer stalking London's streets whose identity was never solved, with grim warnings to the women working after dark. The menace loose in the city becomes an unstoppable legend of dread.","sonnet":"A portrait of a Jack the Ripper-like predator who stalks London at night, choosing victims by hand and operating beyond the law's reach, with a direct warning to women working the streets that someone is watching and no one is safe.","src":"haiku"},
"l-z-rockit~chain-of-fools":{"haiku":"Broken loyalty and betrayal demand recognition; the speaker confronts false friends by dismantling their constructed images and reclaiming control.","opus":"Relishing payback on a two-faced hanger-on who badmouthed him and tried to pull him down with them. Finally free of that dead weight, he reads the writing on the wall and watches the whole line of frauds collapse in turn.","sonnet":"The speaker confronts a treacherous fair-weather friend — probably a former lover — who talked behind their back and dragged them down, and announces the decisive moment of breaking free from that corrosive chain for good.","src":"haiku"},
"l-z-rockit~fire-in-the-hole":{"haiku":"Explosive destructiveness and buried trauma erupting through sabotage, the speaker as conduit for violence working from underground.","opus":"A visceral evocation of being trapped in a spreading blaze, the options narrowed to burning or drowning as terror thickens the air. The Bay Area thrashers make the fire both literal disaster and a stalking hunter, climbing degree by degree, one spark or one wrong step enough to doom the pawns caught in the game.","sonnet":"Military catastrophe as existential condition — trapped, praying, watching fire rise by degrees below while pawns play a game with only one foolish move before everything ignites. The underground burns, siege falls, and the only available move feels like suicide.","src":"haiku"},
"l-z-rockit~shadow-company":{"haiku":"Guerrilla resistance operates in moral darkness, fighting power through violence while questioning whether peace justifies the sacrifices demanded.","opus":"Soldiers pushing through the night as a nameless unit, running, killing and praying while an absent father figure — God or command — gives no answer and shelters only the lucky. Fear hardens into grim resolve as the men, loyal to one another until death, start meting out justice themselves.","sonnet":"A soldier in an unnamed conflict searches for reason under fire while God offers no answer to the privileged few in power; the verses move from individual doubt and prayer to squad solidarity, ending with brothers-in-arms choosing to \"do or die\" in the shadows.","src":"haiku"},
"labelle~lady-marmalade":{"haiku":"Seduction becomes transactional enterprise as a sex worker capitalises on desire, depicting commercialised intimacy through the male client's perspective and fantasy.","opus":"A New Orleans sex worker rendered with glamour rather than shame, and the respectable married man who can't stop dreaming of her. Beneath the strut it's about desire that outlasts the transaction and haunts the grey nine-to-five life.","sonnet":"A New Orleans sex worker named Lady Marmalade seduces a man who experiences something feral and overwhelming with her, then returns to his ordinary life haunted by the memory — desire and the life he can't have keep pulling him back in his sleep.","src":"haiku"},
"lacuna-coil~enjoy-the-silence-cover-version":{"haiku":"Words inflict harm and triviality; the speaker finds true peace and sufficiency in silent presence with a lover, rejecting language's cruelty.","opus":"The Italian gothic-metal band's darker rework of Depeche Mode's synth-pop hit keeps its central idea: language is dangerous and destructive, promises get broken, and everything the singer truly wants is already present in a wordless embrace. Speech only wounds, so quiet closeness becomes the only thing worth holding onto.","sonnet":"Words shatter the fragile sanctuary of closeness: every utterance risks harm, every vow invites breaking, every articulation of feeling lands as intrusion. All that is needed and wanted already exists in the silence of an embrace, making language not just unnecessary but dangerous.","src":"haiku"},
"lacuna-coil~never-dawn":{"haiku":"Resilience through refusing surrender; the speaker endures through will alone, defiant against darkness and committed to preventing tyranny's ascendance.","opus":"A vow of solitary defiance — bare-fisted through the pain, crying where no one sees, reeling but never dropping to a knee. Having walked into a hell that felt oddly familiar, the narrator resolves to endure and keep the black sun from ever rising, sparking the heart's fire to beat the impossible.","sonnet":"A battle-hardened survivor who has been through hell and made it home — \"I stagger, but will not fall; I might crawl, but will never kneel\" — vowing to ensure the dark sun never dawns over their life again, fighting with bare hands and no one at their side.","src":"haiku"},
"lacuna-coil~reckless":{"haiku":"Recklessness becomes liberation from caution and compromise, fueled by bitterness and the desire to self-destruct. Danger and madness offer the only remaining routes to feeling truly alive.","opus":"A vow to stop playing safe, channeling accumulated bitterness into deliberate self-destruction just to feel alive again.","sonnet":"Fed up with compromise and alibis, the speaker embraces a destructive, adrenaline-fuelled wildness — a relationship or force makes them reckless, and they choose to chase that danger all the way rather than continue suppressing themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"lacuna-coil~swamped-xx":{"haiku":"Shame fades as the speaker releases weight they've carried, finding freedom through surrender and acceptance of their past.","opus":"A reworking about shedding shame and releasing a haunting past, fate arriving with a smile rather than dread. The refrain about learning by way of emotion frames self-liberation as tough to trust but finally reached, the old frightening shadows let go.","sonnet":"About releasing shame and learning to live again after being defined by past pain — destiny is no longer something to fear or chase, the shame is gone, and what remains is a melody bleeding through the narrator as they finally let go of whatever held them down.","src":"haiku"},
"lady-gaga~alejandro":{"haiku":"Desire rejects multiple suitors through strategic distancing, the speaker maintaining autonomy against romantic pressure through consistent refusal and deflection.","opus":"A woman cutting loose from a string of interchangeable men, the Latin names blurring into one demand to be left alone. Gaga plays sexual autonomy as both liberation and a kind of haunting, unable to fully shake what she's leaving.","sonnet":"A woman tells multiple men — Alejandro, Fernando, Roberto — that she cannot be with them, does not want their kisses or touch, and needs them to stop calling. The refusal is firm but the urgency with which she repeats it suggests she is also refusing something in herself.","src":"haiku"},
"lady-gaga~bad-romance":{"haiku":"Toxic attraction loops endlessly as two people enact mutual destruction, wanting each other's sins and revenge in cycles of passion and pain.","opus":"A craving for love in its ugliest, most damaged and vengeful forms, wanting a partner's horror and disease as much as their affection while framing herself as a free woman drawn to what destroys her.","sonnet":"The speaker wants the ugliest, most diseased, most criminal version of love — horror, psycho, vertigo, leather-studded kisses in the sand — and demands not friendship but full mutual revenge and destruction, framing obsessive desire as something worth being caught in.","src":"haiku"},
"ladybaby~a-1ed1lkt":{"haiku":"A angry refusal of numbness—predictability kills joy, protective barriers hide fakeness, the speaker demands to live authentically.","opus":"LadyBaby fused kawaii idol pop with metal screams, a collision that lets this anthem sound bruised and defiant at once. The narrator has crumpled her own courage and screamed herself hoarse, yet insists on marching into growly days rather than waiting for god or permission. The turn outward, wishing another person wings and stars beneath a sky the singer's own has none of, makes the resolve collective instead of merely brave.","sonnet":"A song about burning through self-doubt and fear toward a future you have to make without waiting for god or permission — the narrator has crumpled her own courage, lost her dreams, screamed herself hoarse, but insists on holding her head up and going forward into 'growly days.' The emotional core is collective: the song is addressed to someone else too, wishing them wings and stars even as the narrator's own sky has none.","src":"haiku"},
"ladybaby~haten-ni-raimei":{"haiku":"Youth and rage collide in pure kinetic energy, transcending limits through voice and presence as the speaker insists on being felt before time runs out.","opus":"A thunderclap call to abandon the safety of comfort zones and act on impulse, prizing the irreplaceable now over playing it safe. Burning until you turn to ash is offered as the only real way to live, voice and heartbeat overlapping into hope.","sonnet":"An energetic call to action aimed at people living too safely and too passively — urging them to expose their raw emotion, act on their impulses rather than hiding them, seize the unrepeatable present moment, and burn bright even until they turn to ash, framing music and shared feeling as the vehicle for that awakening.","src":"haiku"},
"ladybaby~misogi-island":{"haiku":"Purification ritual and island sanctuary, the speaker seeking cleansing and refuge.","opus":"A chaotic celebration of Tokyo excess and Japanese festival ritual, blending Shinto purification with capital-city mayhem across every holiday from New Year to Christmas. The idol-metal novelty act revels in crowds, noise, and revelry, purging and partying until dawn in a gleeful parody of paradise turned pedestrian pandemonium.","sonnet":"A ritual of purification — misogi, the Shinto act of cleansing — mapped onto Tokyo's chaotic hybrid-city energy, from Shibuya to Kaminari-mon, the joya bell ringing out the old year: a paradise parody where pedestrian-hell becomes sacred ground and the all-night party is recast as ceremony.","src":"haiku"},
"ladybaby~riot-anthem":{"haiku":"Breaking free from conformity and politeness, demanding authentic rebellion over palatable compromise.","opus":"A defiant unity anthem refusing surrender and rejecting hollow virtue and empty posturing, calling a crowd to band together and let the riot burn off self-consciousness. Awkwardness and pride fuse into collective release.","sonnet":"A Japanese anthem about exhaustion with posturing and conformity — tired of boring daily routines, performative lyrics, and the crowd that ignores real issues, the song calls for unity and refusal to surrender; it's a rallying cry against stagnation dressed as rebellion.","src":"haiku"},
"ladytron~flicking-your-switch":{"haiku":"Temptation and control spiral through vice; coded exchanges replace genuine intimacy.","opus":"A coldly seductive account of manipulation and betrayal rendered as strings of numbers, love and money and control blurred until trust is the only casualty. The electro chill matches the emotional theft at its center.","sonnet":"A woman uses a man's credit card for an impulsive downtown spree, framing the act as a power play that triggers a cascade of surveillance, locked gates, and the question of whether trust has been irrevocably broken. The pin numbers read aloud function as a cold recitation of access and breach.","src":"haiku"},
"ladytron~seventeen":{"haiku":"Disposability crystallizes with age; youth's commodity value expires sharply, leaving older versions abandoned.","opus":"A flat, hypnotic indictment of an industry, fashion and beyond, that prizes teenage girls and discards them the moment they age. The endless repetition is the point: the machine grinding on, using and letting go.","sonnet":"The fashion and entertainment industry discards women the moment they age past the peak of commercial desirability, treating them as disposable objects photographed and forgotten. The repetition of the same two lines enacts the mechanical, dehumanizing cycle being described.","src":"haiku"},
"lake-malice~blossom":{"haiku":"A woman defies control and manipulation to grow into autonomy; rejecting a partner's gatekeeping of her independence.","opus":"The British electronic-metalcore duo turns a toxic power struggle into an anthem of growth. Told to behave, the narrator realizes she's been deceived and used as a tool, and refuses to surrender, taking pride in having fought her way to flourishing. When she asserts herself she's branded combative, exposing a hollow, performative feminism that bristles the moment a woman actually pushes back.","sonnet":"A woman dismantles a controlling relationship that weaponized her dependence and called it care. She recognizes the gaslighting, claims her right to speak and build boundaries, and arrives at pride rather than bitterness — she fought to grow and she did.","src":"haiku"},
"lake-malice~blossom-bobby-wolfgang-remix":{"haiku":"Female autonomy against gaslighting partner who preaches feminism while demanding servitude, reclaiming growth as act of defiance.","opus":"A defiant severing from a controlling, hypocritical partner, the narrator refusing to be diminished and proud of having fought her way into bloom. The British duo turns the poison of being called nothing without him back on its source, calling out performative allyship that recoils when women actually assert themselves.","sonnet":"A speaker pushing back against someone who used control and conditional feminism to keep them small — \"you can't stand when the ladies grow a fucking pair\" — and claiming pride in having fought to blossom anyway. The recurring line \"it hurts to say 'you're nothing without me'\" inverts the abuser's script back onto itself.","src":"haiku"},
"lake-malice~magic-square":{"haiku":"True crime spectacle desensitizes the speaker to violence, where murdered bodies become entertainment. Moral erosion arrives through screen and narrative, until the watcher becomes the killer.","opus":"An unsettling confession of true-crime addiction, the narrator numbed by streamed atrocity and craving ever more human brutality. The song stares into its own desensitization, morality eroding as horror becomes comfort, the magic square of a screen framing bodies and blood into a fairytale that soothes even as it corrupts.","sonnet":"An uncomfortable self-examination about the appetite for true crime and violent media — piling up bodies on screen, Netflix zombies, forensic shows — the speaker knows it 'plants doubts in my morality' and asks why they keep feeding the compulsion; 'born into misery, the taste of blood, give me more 'cause it's never enough' blurs the screen violence into something more visceral and confessional.","src":"haiku"},
"lamb-of-god~laid-to-rest-health-remix":{"haiku":"The song advocates erasure of failure through honesty and vengeance. Truth chains the guilty; dreams are made real only to be slain—better to be blank than carry pathetic traces.","opus":"A furious command to stop wallowing and bury a failure, the narrator vowing to chain someone to brutal honesty, grant their dreams then slay them, all wrapped in the refrain of destroying yourself alone. Lamb of God are Virginia's flagship of the new-wave American metal movement, and this HEALTH remix reworks one of their signature Randy Blythe scorchers with the LA noise act's industrial menace.","sonnet":"Self-destruction as the only honest answer to failure — the song offers no comfort, only a brutal accounting: bleach out the evidence, stay empty rather than leaving traces, console yourself with the fact that no one cares either way. The speaker promises to bury someone with truth rather than mercy, to make dreams real and kill them just as fast, framing annihilation as the most honest gift available.","src":"haiku"},
"lambrini-girls~bad-apple":{"haiku":"Systemic police violence and institutional racism define control; the speaker demands systemic accountability beyond token reforms.","opus":"A blistering indictment of police as institutionally rotten, not a few bad apples but a whole poisoned tree. It catalogs killings, cover-ups and abuse, then calls for organizing and resistance against a law that fights the very people it claims to protect.","sonnet":"A full-throated attack on institutional police violence — the song argues the problem isn't a few bad officers but a systemically rotten institution that racially profiles, kills unarmed people, protects abusers, and destroys the homeless, and calls for protest, unionization, and resistance.","src":"haiku"},
"lambrini-girls~company-culture":{"haiku":"Satirizing workplace misogyny where women are expected to smile through harassment and blame themselves.","opus":"A furious satire of workplace sexism, the Brighton punk duo voicing the woman told to smile through harassment and play dumb because men supposedly do it better. The sarcasm is the rage, HR complicity and casual predation named without flinching.","sonnet":"Workplace sexual harassment narrated with vicious sarcasm — the song describes a woman who must smile past her boss's predatory behavior, ignore wandering eyes, pretend to be dumb and decorative, and absorb the lie that men simply do it better, skewering the institutional structures that enable and normalize this.","src":"haiku"},
"lambrini-girls~cuntology-101":{"haiku":"Reclaiming agency and self-interest as feminist imperative; the speaker celebrates boundary-setting, self-prioritization, and sexual autonomy as radical refusal to apologize for existing.","opus":"A blaring feminist satire that reclaims an insult, redefining setting boundaries, saying no, and prioritizing oneself as gloriously cunty. The Brighton punks skewer the way women get vilified for basic self-respect, piling self-care, therapy, autistic meltdowns, and shameless autonomy into a raucous manifesto against slut-shaming.","sonnet":"A reclamation manifesto that lists every scorned or stigmatised act — setting boundaries, autistic meltdowns, shagging behind bins, stealing from chain stores — and re-brands them all as \"cunty\", a badge of radical self-priority. The chorus spells out C-U-N-T as a grammar of self-determination: I'll prioritise my own needs, full stop.","src":"haiku"},
"lambrini-girls~help-me-i-m-gay":{"haiku":"Spectacle performs queerness for male consumption. Assumption and prayer fail—the spectacular remains unhealed.","opus":"A satirical punk skewering of how a queer woman's existence gets treated as spectacle and assumed straight, mocking male-gaze assumptions and Freudian daddy-issue clichés with defiant, tongue-in-cheek exasperation.","sonnet":"A sarcastic coming-out song skewering how female queerness is treated as spectacle — the narrator realises she may be a lesbian, notes that men persistently assume she's straight, and mocks her own father-shaped relationship patterns, framing lesbianism as a public emergency for everyone but herself.","src":"haiku"},
"lambrini-girls~lads-lads-lads":{"haiku":"Skewering toxic masculinity's casual violence, sexual entitlement, and cowardice hiding behind group loyalty.","opus":"A skewering of toxic British lad culture, ventriloquizing the pub bigot whose fragile masculinity curdles into homophobia, misogyny and casual assault. By inhabiting his voice the band exposes how bravado excuses violence.","sonnet":"A pointed satirical portrait of toxic masculinity — the narrator inhabits the voice of a violent, homophobic, sexually predatory man who corners women in bathrooms, bashes gay people, and hides behind pack identity. The song is an indictment, not a celebration, making his behavior explicit and indefensible.","src":"haiku"},
"lambrini-girls~love":{"haiku":"Love masquerades as trauma bonding; empty gestures and broken promises replace genuine devotion.","opus":"A caustic dissection of love as a battle not worth dying in, built on trauma-bonding, pedestals and boundaries trampled at once. The bitter self-awareness is that romance boils down to bought flowers and empty promises tracing back to how it felt as a kid, ending in an apology for letting someone down.","sonnet":"Love is anatomised as trauma bonding and attachment wound: flowers bought, empty promises, hand-drawn cards — \"true love is nothing more than the wrong hill to die on\" — the speaker apologising for letting someone down whose high hopes were always going to be disappointed.","src":"haiku"},
"lambrini-girls~nothing-tastes-as-good-as-it-feels":{"haiku":"Transient pleasure and sensory satisfaction dominate; the speaker chases the taste of experience and sensation.","opus":"An unflinching account of an eating disorder, the narrator caught in a cycle of restriction and self-punishment as her body wastes away. The Brighton duo drags the diet-culture slogan into stark clinical detail, doctors warning of breaking bones and stopped periods, the pursuit of thinness rendered as slow, relentless self-destruction.","sonnet":"An eating disorder from the inside — the mirror, the doctor's room, dry skin, thinning hair, bones ready to break; the Kate Moss mantra weaponised against its own speaker: \"nothing tastes as good as it feels\" as the head hits the toilet rim, period stopped, never enough. The closing spoken outburst (\"I love you, drinks taste like absolute fucking shit, you feed fools fat\") is recovery breaking through, raw and alive.","src":"haiku"},
"lamp~a":{"haiku":"Nostalgic Tokyo romance doomed by seasonal change, bodies separated but memory refusing to fade.","opus":"A cinematic Tokyo breakup, autumn's end and city fog dissolving a love that both know can't survive the coming winter. The Japanese pop group frames the parting as the final scene of a film, tender and already becoming memory.","sonnet":"A Tokyo love song set in late autumn, narrated in impressionistic images: a woman in an olive trench coat disappearing into mist, a silver watch ticking under gas lamps, the sense that their last night together is slipping away without a goodbye — \"farewell\" is never said, just dissolved into fog and short credits on a skyscraper.","src":"haiku"},
"lana-del-rey~summertime-sadness":{"haiku":"A doomed romance blooms against a backdrop of coastal recklessness, where the narrator courts inevitable loss with full knowledge and acceptance.","opus":"Ecstasy and doom fused into one feeling, a woman so alive and so close to a lover's leaving that death would be a fitting climax. Del Rey's signature: glamour and melancholy inseparable, joy already grieving itself.","sonnet":"A speaker dresses up, drives fast, and burns with electric feeling as summer peaks, all while holding the bittersweet knowledge that something beloved is ending or already gone. Joy and loss are inseparable, each amplifying the other into a kind of reckless surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~always":{"haiku":"An unhealthy relationship binds the speaker, where manipulation and lies persist despite awareness, trapping them indefinitely.","opus":"A song about being inescapably bound to a toxic partner, molded and torn apart by their games and half-truths, both sides braced for a fight until the narrator finally breaks free at the very end. Landmvrks are a French metalcore band known for their emotive, hook-heavy modern sound, and here they push the push-pull of an inescapable relationship toward a breaking-point catharsis.","sonnet":"A relationship that has become inescapable even as it corrodes — the other person has shaped the speaker so thoroughly that escape is impossible, but the shaping was done through confusion, half-truths, and a game of push and pull. By the end the world has grown cold, the light only illuminates what isn't being said, and the connection that was supposed to be permanent has finally snapped.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~death":{"haiku":"A person describes daily struggles with anxiety, depression, and feeling inadequate, trying to suppress emotions while fearing abandonment by those around them.","opus":"French metalcore band Landmvrks turn a song into an unfiltered log of mental illness, tracing the daily grind of anxiety, exhaustion and depression in someone crushed by the expectation to seem fine. It sits with the numbness and self-isolation of a person who has stopped believing anything will get better.","sonnet":"Daily existence as a grinding numbness — the song inhabits the specific texture of depression and anxiety: the performance of normalcy when inside there is only hollow, the pressure of impossible expectations, the bottled-up exhaustion that empties into nothing. The speaker acknowledges the void openly, refuses to become like the people causing the pain, and extends an invitation outward to anyone who recognizes this particular feeling of not belonging anywhere.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~heretic":{"haiku":"Rejecting religious authority and empty prayer; burning away indoctrination to reclaim agency.","opus":"A defiant repudiation of forced belief and the deities others hold dear, the singer maintaining nothing reigns overhead while begging to be burned clean and purified. The French metalcore band braid spiritual disillusion with an inability to let go, agony and endurance twisted together into a scream against inherited faith.","sonnet":"A speaker purging religious delusion from their system — sick of the causes, the gods, the fiction — the fire imagery runs both ways: set on fire as destruction and as the only thing that makes them feel alive, the heretic identity claimed precisely because there is no one up above and the running in the dark will never be done.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~lost-in-a-wave":{"haiku":"Anxiety and overthinking render the narrator unable to move or scream; they drown in a wave of uncontrollable feelings. Their brain tricks them into believing they cannot compete with the forces closing in.","opus":"Landmvrks, the French metalcore band, depict a panic attack from the inside. Gasping, every sense cranked past the point of moving, shrieking to an empty room and insisting to himself it makes no sense, the singer feels surges pound over him and steadies his emotions only to lose his grip. His own mind deceives him, cheek pressed to pavement, no match for the surge as it seizes command. The track makes dread physical, a drowning invisible to everyone around.","sonnet":"Anxiety disorder experienced from the inside: the body freezes, the mind spirals, the screams find no one, and rational reassurance fails entirely. The wave is a feeling that controls rather than responds, and the central question — whether controlling fear would even stop the pain — suggests the speaker suspects it would not.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~overrated":{"haiku":"Life feels hollow and suffocating; the speaker watches themselves burn while trapped in a decaying place, barely maintaining breath before oblivion.","opus":"The French metalcore band frame self-destruction as strange deliverance, the narrator suffocating on a life he's decided isn't worth saving. He douses the whole place and sets it alight to reach someone he loves, then insists the blaze won't destroy him but burn away the person he used to be.","sonnet":"Trapped in a suffocating relationship inside a claustrophobic space, a figure watches the walls close in and the air thicken until burning everything down feels like the only exit. He refuses to be buried by it; the fire is also the light by which he finds the way back.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~paralyzed":{"haiku":"Emotional intensity and grief remain trapped inside a motionless body, leaving the speaker unable to express or release their anguish.","opus":"Landmvrks build this around the terror of sleep paralysis, using the sensation of a body that won't respond while the mind stays frantic and awake as a stand-in for emotional collapse. The French metalcore band frame that helplessness as grief so overwhelming it locks a person in place, wide awake yet unable to act.","sonnet":"Emotional shutdown that coexists with hyperawareness — the body is frozen while the face still moves, the tears have run dry while grief keeps screaming outward, and this paradox of feeling most alive inside total paralysis becomes the central image. The world echoes and ricochets, something is approaching too close, and the only response left is to shout until the words collide.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~rainfall":{"haiku":"Someone battles internal corruption and external deception, watching their life deteriorate while searching for a way to escape the relentless damage.","opus":"The French metalcore band Landmvrks cast heartbreak as recurring weather, a downpour that feels like a tempest and an ex who returns each night wearing a fresh disguise. Shaking, clawing at his own skin, the narrator refuses to feign calm. His plea to be left asleep until the weather clears turns grief into a wish to simply outlast the wreckage.","sonnet":"Someone navigates the wreckage of a relationship that broke them, feeling hatred bloom where love once was. What reads as ordinary rain registers as catastrophe; the narrator refuses to fake recovery, watching their own body shake while tracing the outline of another person's damage through them.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~say-no-word":{"haiku":"Everything crumbles while people ignore warnings; the speaker despairs that numbness and decay will persist forever, begging for silence rather than empty words.","opus":"Landmvrks conjure a desolate, end-of-the-world estrangement. The narrator barely recognizes himself, sees the world dried up and everyone quietly dying while others fuss over petty things, and, bitter and terrified the anguish is permanent, demands silence and pretense-free honesty. His weary admission that he can't explain why he feels so low becomes the one thing he's certain of.","sonnet":"Everything familiar has corroded — the river runs dry, reflections are strangers, the body feels like ruin. Trapped in a feedback loop of inward collapse, the speaker dismisses every well-meaning reassurance with cold contempt and admits the fear that this deterioration may be permanent.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~scars":{"haiku":"A person drowning in trauma and deception challenges their partner to embrace vulnerability; scars become proof of authenticity over empty pretense.","opus":"Landmvrks soften their heaviness into something tender and protective, addressed to a partner ashamed of what her body has been through. The narrator, battling his own paranoia and the dishonesty around him, pleads with her to stop hiding her marks, insisting the damage she carries only makes her more worth loving.","sonnet":"Mutual wounding between two people who know each other's pressure points too well. Physical scars become evidence of survival rather than shame, and the narrator insists that bearing them openly matters more than concealment — even as waves of grief keep crashing and the boundary between love and suffocation blurs.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~scars-live":{"haiku":"Emotional scarring becomes the measure of authenticity; two figures sink together into shared vulnerability while confronting the illegitimacy of their masks.","opus":"Speaking directly to a person's scars as something precious rather than shameful, urging them not to hide, even as the narrator himself feels crushed and short of breath. Shared damage becomes the bond — two people watching each other survive the dread of whatever comes.","sonnet":"Addressed to someone who hides their self-harm, the speaker insists those scars carry real meaning — \"your scars mean more than anything\" — while confessing their own fear and drowning sensation, urging the person not to cover up and watching them breathe as a fragile sign of life.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~self-made-black-hole":{"haiku":"Self-made suffering consumes the narrator from within like a parasite; internal lies and guilt spiral into a void of their own creation.","opus":"French metalcore band Landmvrks personify depression as a parasite devouring its host from inside, toxin in the marrow and a whispering voice in the skull, emptiness the only thing he truly knows. Naming his collapse an implosion of his own making, the narrator longs to live yet blames himself for everything, and after hitting rock bottom countless times, confesses that nothing ever offered him a sense of belonging.","sonnet":"An internal parasite feeds on self-deception — lies told to oneself become the architecture of collapse. The speaker has hit bottom many times but this feels like a crossing of a line previously unimaginable. Poison runs through the bones, words carve themselves in permanently, and the void does not arrive from outside but is constructed by the one who suffers it.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~tired-of-it-all":{"haiku":"Someone fragments into multiple selves, unable to hear their own voice under internal chaos. They walk a red line between sanity and collapse, exhausted by the weight of undone things.","opus":"Landmvrks voice a depleted, dissociating mind, the narrator unable to feel whole or even recognize himself, waking each day as a stranger with every inner voice dragging him lower. Paralyzed by regret and unfinished business, he wonders how to outrun a grief that keeps returning, the French metalcore band rendering exhaustion as a cornered, overdriven plea.","sonnet":"A person exhausted by an inability to feel real, to fill an interior void, or to find their own face and voice amid the noise of a relationship built on deception. The weight of unfinished emotional business presses the mind toward overload, and every attempt to escape the sorrow circles back to the same paralysis.","src":"haiku"},
"landmvrks~visage":{"haiku":"The speaker loses their mind and sees harm from a face they now reject, claiming no longer to belong to that person. Nightmares replace intimacy.","opus":"A bilingual reckoning with an abuser or toxic figure now confronted face to face, the English refrain about losing one's mind and skin boiling from within, the French verses grappling with dark thoughts, brotherhood, and asking whether the finish line is an end or a new start. The throughline is liberation through confrontation: the harm has been seen, the ghost that haunts his nightmares no longer owns him, and he no longer belongs to it.","sonnet":"A bilingual confrontation with someone who caused deep harm: the narrator is losing their grip on their mind, still haunted by this person in nightmares, but arrives at a hard-won declaration of severance — they no longer belong to them.","src":"haiku"},
"landon-tewers~gospel-therapy":{"opus":"A self-lacerating account of a bitter, self-destructive man drinking alone, smoking and breaking his things, hating everyone but his woman, aware his bad karma is coming and half-wishing to nuke the whole planet.","sonnet":"A deliberately confrontational self-portrait of someone drunk, angry, and alienated — the narrator lashes out at the world while admitting their behavior is self-defeating, oscillating between grandiosity and self-loathing, with the only stable element being devotion to their partner.","src":"sonnet"},
"lantl-s~amber":{"haiku":"Sheltered isolation protects from pain but also numbs and stifles, creating a struggle between the safety of passivity and the risk of feeling alive.","opus":"A dreamlike portrait of a life sealed off inside protective glass, raised by wind and water yet numbed by too much shelter. It captures how safety can smother, dimming desire until existence is a sleepwalk.","sonnet":"The song traces a life lived at remove from feeling — born into natural openness, gradually enclosed in protection that becomes suffocation, reduced to observing the world through a plastic veil in an artificial womb, where sensation is carefully dimmed and desire regulated until numbness itself becomes the condition of existence.","src":"haiku"},
"lantl-s~cocoon-tree-house":{"haiku":"From isolation in a tree, the speaker observes the world's collapse and fragility while seeking comfort in natural beauty and memory.","opus":"A gentle reverie of retreat and reconnection, the narrator cocooned high in a tree, diving into an imagined ocean of singing whales while gazing at the sun through closed eyes. The deeper read is a longing to recover a childlike closeness to the sky and nature, realizing too late how fragile and easily lost that beauty and connection are.","sonnet":"A nostalgic, dreamy song about finding refuge in childhood states of mind — watching the world from high up, swimming with whales, lying in fields of mint. The narrator has lost the easy connection to beauty and is trying to find his way back to it without being sure it's still reachable.","src":"haiku"},
"lantl-s~daisies":{"haiku":"Beauty decays into something the speaker cannot sustain or preserve.","opus":"A gauzy, sun-soaked reverie of tenderness and fleeting togetherness, all butterflies, lily fields, and amber afternoons. Time slips away unnoticed as two people idle in warmth, sorrows deliberately unthought, the whole scene suspended in a soft nostalgia that quietly knows such everlasting moments are precisely the ones being lost.","sonnet":"A hymn to a relationship suspended in amber afternoons and lily fields, where the point is not to think about sorrows, just pick daisies in the sun and exist forevermore. Time is being lost and both speakers know it; the sweetness is real and undefended.","src":"haiku"},
"lantl-s~dog-in-the-wild":{"haiku":"The speaker admires a wild dog's freedom in nature, envying its unaware bliss and sovereignty over the untamed landscape. They wish to escape civilization and run freely alongside the creature.","opus":"An envious ode to a wild animal's unselfconscious freedom, the narrator watching a creature that rules its terrain and knows no borders, wishing to shed human awareness and simply run alongside it. Freedom made poignant precisely because the dog doesn't know it's free.","sonnet":"A song of envy for a wild dog's freedom — the narrator watches an animal move through wilderness with sovereign ease, unbounded by borders or self-awareness, and yearns to run alongside it in a state of unthinking, unmediated bliss.","src":"haiku"},
"lantl-s~dream-machine":{"haiku":"Inside an ornate glass house, plants are nurtured that breed dreams while stealing will and spooking the mind; this is an art-nouveau dream machine.","opus":"Lantlos, the German act drifting from black metal into dreamlike haze, imagine a hallucinatory conservatory: a drowsy moon bathing flowers in blue light, ornate glasswork and glowing bowers cultivating dreams from tended growth. But the apparatus prizes its harvest by wiping out recollection, stealing the narrator's resolve and rattling his thoughts, its loveliness quietly devouring the one who dreams it.","sonnet":"A night-lit glass structure tends the plants that grow dreams, offering both nourishment and erasure: memories dissolve alongside fruit, and will is quietly taken from those who linger too long inside. The imagery is gorgeous and faintly threatening, suggesting something seductive about a place that tends to beauty while quietly hollowing out its visitors.","src":"haiku"},
"lantl-s~home":{"haiku":"A numbed, disconnected person watches beauty and memory from behind glass, unable to feel real and yearning to return to childhood innocence.","opus":"A dissociated narrator watches stars and blooming fields without feeling them, numb behind milky glass, longing to place a fading heart into a little woven box back home. The track treats emotional detachment as a fog, its polaroid memories of childhood standing for a realness the speaker fears he has permanently lost.","sonnet":"Dissociation has stripped the familiar world of colour, scent, and emotional texture, leaving the speaker moving through life like a ghost inside a body. The longing to return home is also a longing to recover the capacity to feel at all — an interior self locked in a small woven box, waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"lantl-s~lake-fantasy":{"haiku":"A surreal, dreamy figure emerges from noise and water, drawing the narrator into a hallucinatory bath of color and sensation. The imagery dissolves between pleasure and disorientation, melting identity into another's watery embrace.","opus":"A synthwave reverie of dissolving into an alluring, artificial dream-world, where a glossy siren pulls the narrator under; the pleasure is disorientation itself, the loss of certainty about whether any of it is real.","sonnet":"A disorienting, ecstatic encounter with a luminous figure emerges from noise and light, pulling the narrator into a dreamlike, sensory dissolution where they melt into the other person's world.","src":"haiku"},
"lantl-s~lich":{"haiku":"A speaker builds dependency on a partner who numbs and transforms them, creating addiction. The bond is both blissful and cancerous, dissolving the self.","opus":"A queasy portrait of compulsion and self-loathing, addressing a habit or person that acts as a cure that brings cancer; the narrator numbs himself on a dissociative high he keeps returning to, hating himself for the relapse yet past caring.","sonnet":"A song about an addictive relationship or substance that the narrator hates needing: it absorbs them completely, offers a blissful unreality, and leaves them feeling simultaneously euphoric and ashamed each time they return to it.","src":"haiku"},
"lantl-s~magnolia":{"haiku":"Overwhelming sensory and emotional overload mingles pleasure with disorientation; the speaker cannot distinguish desire from damage.","opus":"Lantl's render sensory overload as intoxication, cinnamon and neuronal glitter accelerating a woozy mind toward sweet, jittering bliss. Then the rush curdles: unsure where it is going, the narrator pleads to be let go while admitting how wrong it feels. The track balances euphoria and unease, ecstasy sliding into a plea for autonomy as pleasure threatens to consume.","sonnet":"Sensory overload tips from pleasure into vertigo — sweetness and stimulation accumulating until the mind loses its footing. What starts as bliss becomes compulsion, and compulsion becomes entrapment. The realization arrives late: something that felt like elevation has become a thing that will not let go, and the plea to be released comes from inside the feeling itself.","src":"haiku"},
"lantl-s~planetarium":{"haiku":"The song evokes imagery of embryonic merging and unified sleep among interconnected beings, creating a dreamlike state of liquefaction and cosmic unity.","opus":"A dreamlike vision of two lovers fused in an eternal, womb-like sleep among the stars; through imagery of being naked, melted and intertwined, overgrown with plants and liquefied into a sea of silk, it renders a total, cosmic union that dissolves the self into the beloved.","sonnet":"An image of total, wordless union — two bodies dissolve into each other in a space somewhere between the stars and a womb, overgrown with physalis, melting into silk, unified past the point of individuality.","src":"haiku"},
"lantl-s~the-bubble":{"haiku":"Someone floats in an artificial, filtered dream state—numb yet comforted by aesthetics and unreality. Disconnection feels better than feeling at all.","opus":"A dreamy, dissociative state rendered as living inside a pastel, filtered bubble that numbs a motionless heart while feeling both good and grey. The read is emotional numbness or an addictive, artificial escape the narrator can't get off, needing it to feel anything at all.","sonnet":"About a dissociative, drug-softened state of infatuation where the narrator can't separate artificial pleasure from genuine feeling — everything is pastel and distorted, the beloved is a fix that speeds up dreams. It craves sensation while half-aware it's unreal.","src":"haiku"},
"lantl-s~vertigo":{"haiku":"Captures the disorienting sensation of summer infatuation and sensual overload, using fruit and sensory imagery to describe overwhelming attraction. The song conveys dizzying passion and ethereal desire.","opus":"A lush, sensory ode to a summer crush, saturated with images of fruit, sunlight, and water as a dizzying intoxication of desire. It renders infatuation as a blissful, unworldly experience of ecstasy and vertigo.","sonnet":"A dreamy, sensory portrait of an overwhelming summer infatuation — every image is saturated with ripe fruit, warmth, and brightness, and the dizziness of the title describes the intoxicating effect the object of desire has on the speaker.","src":"haiku","fable":"Lantlôs, having drifted from black metal into blackgaze, here abandons narrative entirely for a synesthetic heat-haze of summer infatuation. There is barely a sentence in it — the words arrive as a swirl of fruit and skin and sun, peach and apricot and strawberry and lemon, everything ripe and edible and warm, until the crush becomes less a person than a whole intoxicated climate. That refusal to say anything plainly is the point: the title names the sensation the pile-up produces, desire pitched so high it stops being about a woman and becomes pure disorientation, a body dizzy in the light. The one word that cuts against the sweetness is spleen — a flicker of melancholy under the ecstasy — but it is swallowed instantly back into the chant of summer crush, and the song lets itself fall rather than resolve.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"desire pitched so high it becomes pure disorientation","built":"wordless fruit-pile syntax enacts the dizziness the title names"},
"last-note~a-1fih047":{"haiku":"Anticipatory anxiety before a first date spiraling into obsessive overthinking; nervous excitement becomes sleep-deprived mania as expectation and reality collide comically.","opus":"Last Note built a Vocaloid catalog out of comic, hyper-detailed character sketches, and this sleepless-eve monologue is exactly that mode. The girl rehearsing routes, changing outfits at 2 AM, running love-game simulations, is drawn with affectionate precision until the punchline arrives: she oversleeps and misses everything. The nervous mania is rendered so vividly that the comic crash feels tender rather than cruel.","sonnet":"A girl spends the entire night before her first date unable to sleep — rehearsing routes obsessively, changing into her outfit at 2 AM, running romantic simulations in a love-game mindset — and then falls asleep anyway, still muttering tough talk, waking to find the appointed time long gone.","src":"haiku"},
"late-november~deadweight":{"haiku":"Self-awareness cannot prevent self-sabotage; the speaker recognizes their own toxicity while remaining unable to prevent it.","opus":"A pop-punk confession from someone who overthinks and overtalks himself into feeling like a burden, catching sight of a better self he can't reach. He's stuck inside a mind he'd trade in a heartbeat, and afraid the way back is gone for good.","sonnet":"Self-awareness trapped in self-sabotage: the speaker knows they overthink and talk too much, sees a beauty somewhere in the mind they cannot access, and fears they are a deadweight that will drag them away from their golden coast if they cannot change.","src":"haiku"},
"laura-marling~gurdjieff-s-daughter":{"haiku":"Spiritual and psychological wisdom counsel vigilance against pride and false authority; the narrator learns discernment through cautionary tales and observation.","opus":"Hard-won wisdom passed down like an incantation: don't fear the dark, don't be fooled by charm or pride, keep watch behind you. Named for the mystic's daughter, it's a young woman's manual for seeing clearly and refusing to be labeled.","sonnet":"A series of warnings about pride, fear, flattery, and the ways power names and contains people — told in a calm, almost instructional register, with grief folded in for those who were lost to these forces — urging the listener to keep their eyes wide and never mistake a label for a self.","src":"haiku"},
"laura-marling~hope-in-the-air":{"haiku":"Generational despair spreads as human warmth proves insufficient against systemic hopelessness.","opus":"A daughter figure who has given up on her own salvation while still serving others, insisting the real terror is living, not dying. Marling stages it as scripture, hope present everywhere in the world except for the one carrying it.","sonnet":"A daughter watches a man consumed by hopelessness and near-silence, absorbs his despair, then fights her way back to a fragile faith in life — addressing those who spoke easy truths to her while insisting she will carry on, even without certainty.","src":"haiku"},
"lauren-babic~sorry-you-re-not-a-winner":{"haiku":"The speaker mocks empty ambitions and false pretenses, calling out someone's bitter, cold nature and hollow pursuit of meaning.","opus":"Metalcore vocalist Lauren Babic likens self-destruction to a rigged lottery game, where each round of pain buys another losing ticket and the promised payoff never arrives. The recurring arcade-style invitation to feed in more money and try again frames hollow, showy living as a machine designed to take everything and give nothing back.","sonnet":"A gleefully cold verdict handed down to someone who mistakes pretension for success. The slot-machine refrain frames the target as a loser in a game he believed he was winning, while the narrator catalogs bitter air, false intentions, and the thrill of watching it confirmed.","src":"haiku"},
"lawrence-rothman~kate-s-not-here-day-wave-lawrence-rothman-remix":{"haiku":"Watching someone fade into themselves, wondering if you can save them before they disappear entirely.","opus":"Dissociation and a fracturing self, the mirror no longer returning a recognizable face and a plea for a vanishing identity not to disappear. The named figure feels like a part of the singer slipping away rather than a separate person.","sonnet":"About someone — Kate — who seems to be slipping away or disappearing, possibly into depression or dissociation. The narrator is urgent but helpless, unsure whether to trust what they see in themselves or in her, calling for her not to vanish while fearing it's already too late.","src":"haiku"},
"lazarvs~valhalla":{"haiku":"Time is a rope and witness, and the speaker is tired of nothing, seeing, and dying. They call from a deathbed while cold and tired fill their existence.","opus":"Lazarvs wring existential dread from cryptic imagery of clocks, ropes, and a dying witness to truth, the narrator declaring himself hollow and weary of consciousness as time slowly winds around him. The chorus frames introspection as a dark rite: only by descending into your own emptiness, it suggests, do you learn what you're actually made of.","sonnet":"Time is figured as a rope around a dying body — a witness to exhaustion and the slow approach of oblivion. From within this numbness the song urges an opening inward, a getting wise and high, though what waits inside the coldest nothing is left unresolved.","src":"haiku"},
"lcd-soundsystem~someone-great":{"haiku":"News of a friend's death arrives as a rupture, leaving the speaker numb and unable to process the absence.","opus":"The strange, mundane texture of sudden loss, the phone ringing too early, coffee that won't taste bitter, work still waiting, because grief doesn't rearrange the world it just empties it. Murphy captures how absence keeps coming and never stops.","sonnet":"Grief after losing someone arrives not with dramatic weather but with horrible normalcy — bitter coffee, fine weather, mundane tasks that keep coming — and the shock is precisely that the world does not pause. The person is gone and the day continues, and that is the most devastating thing.","src":"haiku"},
"le-castle-vania~the-phantom":{"haiku":"Mirrored haunting traps the speaker in repetitive loops; destiny crystallizes as inescapable while the phantom walks the lonely road, forever searching for answers that dissolve into echo.","opus":"A haunted stand-off with a spectral version of the self, the narrator unsure whether he's the vanished ghost or the living man in the glass. Figured as a phantom shadowing a solitary path, it broods on being stranded between worlds, bracing to fight shadows he can't outrun.","sonnet":"A phantom confronts someone searching for answers with the admission that it is the haunting force itself — face-to-face like a mirror, sharing a lonely road through demons and shadows — offering not escape but the acknowledgement that destiny cannot be outrun.","src":"haiku"},
"league-of-distortion~galvanize":{"haiku":"Escape from numbness and defeat requires collective will; breaking chains of imposed constraint becomes necessary to stop accepting injustice as inevitable.","opus":"A rallying call to break free of imposed chains, staged in the vocabulary of a video game where death offers no continue. The German band converts numbness and blame into urgency, insisting there is no compromise and no going back, the kill screen approaching as reason to summon the guts to change while there is still time.","sonnet":"Life mapped onto a video game that was always rigged and unfair, where death is permanent and the kill screen arrives unseen. The call to galvanize is the call to recognise the chains put on you and escape the numbness — not in-game logic but out of it, no compromise available once the chains come off.","src":"haiku"},
"league-of-distortion~it-hurts-so-good":{"haiku":"Pain becomes indistinguishable from pleasure; the speaker confesses addiction to suffering's intensity, trapped in sadomasochistic spiral.","opus":"A defiant embrace of a taboo craving, a shameful little secret the speaker refuses to delete and gets judged for. Framed as a lullaby that makes you weep, it revels in loving the pain, the adrenaline and lust that hurts just right.","sonnet":"A confession of pain-addiction delivered as a lullaby — adrenaline and lust firing together, a \"satirized desire\" the speaker cannot control — the blood-red sky and lechery in the eyes make the hurt the most honest pleasure they know.","src":"haiku"},
"league-of-legends-music~get-jinxed":{"haiku":"Chaos and anarchy seduce through destruction; the speaker invites destruction as liberation.","opus":"The gleeful mania of a character who craves chaos for its own sake, taunting anyone to catch her. Bombs, bullets and anarchy are just entertainment to relieve her boredom, neatness something to be blown apart.","sonnet":"The character Jinx from League of Legends exults in chaos and destruction — she wants to blow up the city, shoot people in the face, and watch the panic unfold, driven purely by boredom and a love of anarchy rather than any deeper motive.","src":"haiku"},
"league-of-legends~get-jinxed":{"haiku":"A chaotic character invites violence and destruction, wanting attention through mayhem and willing to blow up an entire city for thrills.","opus":"A gleeful ode to the character Jinx's manic love of chaos and destruction, bored by order and neatness, she wants to blow the city to ashes purely for the thrill of anarchy; violence as playful entertainment.","sonnet":"The League of Legends character Jinx narrates her own chaotic worldview: she's bored, ignored, and the only cure is mayhem — bombs, bullets, anarchy, blowing the city to ashes just to feel something.","src":"haiku"},
"league-of-legends~rell-the-iron-maiden":{"haiku":"A warrior breaks free from her oppressors and trauma to reclaim her power through defiance. She carries internal fire that no fear can extinguish.","opus":"Written for a League of Legends champion, a fatherless warrior forged by loss who turns rage into an unquenchable inner fire. Beneath the defiant chant about crushing tyrants runs an orphan's grief, memories of a faceless mother sustaining her refusal to fear or fall.","sonnet":"A warrior character declares she is driven by grief and rage — loved ones taken, identity erased — and channels that into unstoppable, fire-fueled retribution against rulers and liars. It's a power fantasy about fighting back when everything has been stripped away.","src":"haiku"},
"leap~power-trip":{"haiku":"Intoxication and control through dangerous attraction; the speaker craves electricity and power over reason, fueled by jealousy and domination.","opus":"A feverish surrender to obsessive, adrenalized desire, love reframed as electricity and jealousy rather than tenderness. The narrator craves the rush over any rationale, banishing the body and waking the demons, needing no reason to want, only the charge of a relationship lived like a self-igniting power trip.","sonnet":"Two people who are one and the same invited to bury their worries and let each other in — the electricity of desire as shared madness, banishing the body so the demons can wake. It's framed as play pretend that nonetheless fills you with jealousy and burns your lips, the power trip mutual and chosen.","src":"haiku"},
"lebanon-hanover~gallowdance":{"haiku":"Dissolution becomes aesthetic as lovers dance toward inevitable ending together, maintaining momentum against the gravity of their shared doom.","opus":"Dancing on the edge of the noose while you still can, love and defiance staged against inevitable death and a degraded world. The German verse makes the tree explicit: a beautiful thing to hang yourself from, gothic fatalism embraced rather than feared.","sonnet":"Two people dance together for as long as they can, knowing the noose is waiting — the tree has stood there all along, patient. The gallows dance is a temporary defiance of inevitable end, and the song alternates between German and English versions of the same bitter intimacy.","src":"haiku"},
"led-by-lanterns~better-alone":{"haiku":"Incompatibility crystallizes into inevitable separation; the speaker cannot become what their partner needs, making continued connection a mutual wound.","opus":"Begging a partner to say anything but goodbye, fixating on the point it broke and how to keep them from leaving. The refrain half-admits they'd be better off apart while pleading for another shot — the sting summed up in dreading to hear he'll be missed once he's gone.","sonnet":"One half of a failing relationship pleads for a real answer — begging the other to stop, turn around, say something different — while accepting that no matter how hard they try to be what the other wants, it is never enough.","src":"haiku"},
"led-zeppelin~black-dog":{"haiku":"Infectious desire and sexual magnetism override judgment as the narrator chases pleasure through deception and temporary passion.","opus":"Raw carnal desire for a woman's body collides with the sting of being used and left broke, the narrator burning with lust yet praying for a steady, honest woman who won't take everything from him.","sonnet":"A raw desire song where the speaker is consumed by a woman's movement and presence — burning red eyes, flaming heart, honey drip — until the story turns: she spent his money, took his car, and started telling people she'd be a star, leaving him to pray for a steady woman who won't lie.","src":"haiku"},
"led-zeppelin~immigrant-song-remaster":{"haiku":"Warriors claiming conquest as their birthright, destroying what they can't dominate or understand.","opus":"Zeppelin channels Viking conquest after touring Iceland, the war cry of raiders sweeping toward new shores with Valhalla ahead. Beneath the myth is raw appetite for dominion, though it ends offering peace to those left to rebuild.","sonnet":"Sung from the perspective of Viking raiders — they come from Scandinavia, invoke the hammer of the gods, and declare their conquest of western shores; it ends with a somewhat incongruous appeal for peace and rebuilding, but the primary thrust is mythic conquest and martial glory.","src":"haiku"},
"led-zeppelin~kashmir-remaster":{"haiku":"A spiritual traveler journeys through desert wastelands seeking mystical transcendence and return to an idyllic Shangri-La.","opus":"One of Led Zeppelin's most monumental tracks, built on Eastern-flavored riffing and the sense of an endless spiritual journey. The narrator casts himself as a wanderer across ages and continents, sitting among a serene ancient people whose tongue he can't decipher, chasing a paradise he's certain he'll reach again. It reads as a trance-like vision of seeking rather than any literal destination.","sonnet":"A desert crossing becomes a meditation on time, transcendence, and the unreachable horizon — Shangri-La glimpsed but never arrived at. The traveler feels the landscape absorb him completely, dissolving individual identity into wind, sand, and the persistent pull of somewhere he has been before.","src":"haiku"},
"led-zeppelin~rock-and-roll":{"haiku":"A lover recalls past romance and sexual intimacy after a long absence, aching to return to youthful connection and the physical closeness they once shared.","opus":"A rollicking celebration of getting back to rock and roll after a long, isolating stretch away, laced with nods to old dances and vintage love songs. Beneath the party lurks genuine solitude and failed romance, promises that fell apart, balanced against an invitation to welcome love back in.","sonnet":"A song about the ache of time away from the physical and emotional pleasures of life — the narrator has been celibate and alone long enough that he's forgotten what connection feels like, and he urgently wants to reclaim it.","src":"haiku"},
"led-zeppelin~stairway-to-heaven":{"haiku":"The song traces a journey of spiritual seeking and materialistic temptation, warning of deception in the pursuit of transcendence and meaning.","opus":"The most mythologized song by Led Zeppelin follows a lady convinced all that glitters is gold, buying her way toward heaven while the narrator wonders at the folly. Rooted in the acoustic-to-electric ascent of Jimmy Page and the mystical bent of Robert Plant, it moves through hedgerows, pipers, and forked paths toward a hard-won truth. The plea to change the chosen road lingers as its quiet, cautionary heart.","sonnet":"A woman who believes wealth can purchase spiritual ascent is met with a more ambiguous landscape — two paths, a piper, forests full of laughter. By the end material certainty dissolves into mysticism, and the road itself whispers that there is still time to choose differently.","src":"haiku","fable":"A lady tries to purchase salvation the way she would buy anything else, certain that shine equals worth and that a single word will always open the store. The song's whole architecture is built to correct her, climbing from that pastoral fairy-tale opening into the hammering coda the way a warning gathers force. The recurring admission of being made to wonder is the honest center: Plant, steeped in the Tolkien-and-Celtic hedgerow imagery of the 1971 fourth album, keeps offering runes, pipers, and forking paths, then confesses he cannot read them either, that words carry double sense and thoughts stay misgiven. The pivot comes when the buying stops and counsel starts — two roads before her, and the chance to switch the one she travels is not yet gone — reframing everything as a caution against her transaction: the stairway was never masonry but wind, and the nearest thing to grace is to stay grounded, solid as stone rather than tumbling loose. The last line loops her back to her purchase unchanged, the cruelest stroke: the sermon lands on us, not on her.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"warning that the stairway is wind, not masonry you can purchase","built":"the sermon lands on the listener — she loops back to her purchase unchanged"},
"led-zeppelin~when-the-levee-breaks":{"haiku":"Natural disaster and forced migration tear apart lives and communities, leaving only displacement and the cruel knowledge that prayer and effort offer no salvation.","opus":"The Great Mississippi Flood as blues catastrophe, forced migration north to Chicago and the helplessness of a home about to be washed away. Zeppelin drags the old lament into something apocalyptic, prayer and crying declared useless against the water.","sonnet":"A flood refugee confronts displacement and despair, finding that neither prayer nor grief offers relief — with no work in the south and no way home, the only option is to move, heading toward Chicago as the waters rise.","src":"haiku"},
"leningrad~a-10k126q":{"haiku":"A transgressive punk polemic against Saint Petersburg's cultural decay and fake sophistication, performed by a self-proclaimed jester-king mocking all institutions.","opus":"Sergei Shnurov built Leningrad on gleeful obscenity and ska-punk swagger, and here he steps forward as Shnur to torch the whole Russian rock pantheon. Naming DDT, Aquarium and their peers as relics and frauds, he crowns himself the one true disruptor of a scene gone soft. The crudeness is the point, self-aggrandizement wrapped in music-world satire, a jester declaring the throne his own.","sonnet":"The narrator introduces himself as Shnur — frontman of Leningrad — and delivers a sardonic tour of the Russian rock landscape, mocking rival bands (DDT, Aquarium, Bi-2, Mashina Vremeni) as relics, frauds, or punchlines, while positioning himself as the one true disruptor who has finally taken control of a scene gone soft. The tone is gleefully crude self-aggrandizement wrapped in music-world satire.","src":"haiku"},
"lenny-kravitz~fly-away":{"haiku":"Yearning to escape earthly constraints and explore freedom across space and time, seeking liberation alone or with a loved one.","opus":"A restless narrator daydreams of soaring high above the landscape and out toward the planets, chasing a private freedom shared with someone he wants beside him. Lenny Kravitz's retro-rock optimism powers the escape fantasy, turning plain wanderlust into an exuberant, sun-bound plea to simply break free.","sonnet":"Pure escapist longing takes flight in the most literal terms — above trees, beyond seas, out to the stars — driven by the desire to shed the weight of the everyday alongside someone worth fleeing with. The fantasy is generous and open-armed rather than isolating.","src":"haiku"},
"leprous~below":{"opus":"Leprous dig into emotional repression, voicing someone who has stuffed his fears and formative wounds so far down that they never surface. Sustained by constant, exhausting lying to hold up a polished front, he half-wonders how long the whole performance can possibly hold.","sonnet":"About the exhausting performance of having everything together when beneath the surface everything is buried — the narrator has built a tower of facade above a foundation of hidden fears, childhood pain, and lies, and cannot remember why they are here except that they must disappear.","src":"sonnet"},
"leprous~by-my-throne":{"haiku":"Resilience amid hardship; a stone worn by time stands steadfast, accepting bruising but remaining unmoved by doubt.","opus":"The Norwegian prog band build a parable of self-reliance around a single crude stone. Battered and shaped by relentless water yet refusing to be washed away, the rock stays steadfastly beside the narrator, who is otherwise adrift in emptiness and paranoia. That unwavering companion becomes the source of composure that lets his grief settle and his outlook lift.","sonnet":"Beside a rough stone that has been shaped by tides rather than intention, a person finds an anchor sturdy enough to outlast paranoia and aimless struggle. The rock does not rescue or advise — it simply remains, absorbing whatever grief the sea delivers and staying.","src":"haiku"},
"lera-lynn~lately":{"haiku":"Alienation and loss accumulate as a speaker drifts through towns, unable to recognize themselves or hold onto what matters.","opus":"Dissociation and lost time, a woman who no longer recognizes her own face or remembers what mattered, drifting through towns as love vanishes. The refrain asks the real question underneath: where love goes when it goes.","sonnet":"A woman looks in the mirror and no longer recognises herself, finds her sense of time dissolved, and traces the loss back to a love gone wrong that has emptied her of words, past, and future. She asks the unanswerable question: where does love go when it leaves?","src":"haiku"},
"lethal-injektion~introvert":{"haiku":"Social isolation hardens into active resistance; the speaker rejects external influence entirely, remaining a lone wolf defensive against all connection.","opus":"A loner's manifesto dressed as armor: distrust everyone, keep the walls up, treat solitude as strength over a society he refuses to join, while the chorus quietly admits the cost is only ever talking to himself.","sonnet":"An outsider who prefers solitude over social performance keeps pushing people back and talking only to himself, questioning whether he can be anything at all while others try to pull him into a world he finds fake and impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"letters-sent-home~bloom":{"haiku":"Love requires violent rupture to permit growth, where the speaker begs to be torn open for transformation. Sunlight demands destruction, making healing synonymous with tearing.","opus":"A portrait of love turned devotional and self-destructive, the narrator begging to be torn open by the very person who wounds her. The metalcore act renders dependency as near-religious worship, kneeling and praying to a partner who is both sun and blade, healing and doom inseparable in a bond that leaves her empty yet craving more.","sonnet":"An almost-religious dependency on someone who simultaneously wounds and sustains: the speaker is knocked off the pedestal they placed the other on, yet every cut makes them need them more — 'rip me open just to heal my wounds, make me hopeless, be the sun so I can bloom' — the ultraviolet metaphor turning love into the very force that burns.","src":"haiku"},
"letters-sent-home~request-denied":{"haiku":"Songs of recovery ring hollow as depression persists; the speaker admits defeat, finding dark humor in the futility of articulating pain.","opus":"A brutally self-aware confession that writing endless depression songs hasn't cured a thing, and that even the wish to die was turned down. With no heaven to hope for, the hard-won lesson is to stop blaming himself and finally move on from years spent stuck.","sonnet":"A songwriter admits they lied in their own songs — 'I cried that we are not gonna die' — and that writing a thousand songs about depression didn't cure it; still a mess, still alive against their wishes ('request denied'), they finally stop blaming themselves and accept the unresolved reality.","src":"haiku"},
"levels~black-dove":{"haiku":"Sacred corruption merges flesh and sin; the subject chases spectral obsession toward divine ruin.","opus":"Obsessive, sacrilegious desire framed as damnation: loving someone becomes an addiction he confesses to a God who wants to know where he's been, wasting his days and losing everyone else to chase a love that's already dead.","sonnet":"A man admits to being enslaved by a love he calls a ghost of his own making — \"chasing the trail of a ghost that was made up inside my head\" — as it isolates him from everyone, drives him to his knees in prayer, and poisons him with blood and honey.","src":"haiku"},
"levels~blue-heaven":{"haiku":"The speaker spirals toward self-destruction while suspended in paradox—falling yet suspended in false peace.","opus":"An overdose narrated from inside the high, sinking into a blue heaven unsure he'll surface for anyone. Branded a fallen thing, the speaker gambles with his own life, blood running from his head as the ceiling splits open.","sonnet":"A person who pushed substances too far and lost consciousness at the wheel — suspended in a blue-heaven drug haze with blood flowing from their head, they don't know if they'll make it back to love or anyone, spiraling deeper into a state they can't pull out of.","src":"haiku"},
"levels~breed":{"haiku":"Predatory intimacy and obsessive possession mask as connection; the speaker's fantasy rewrites the other as object, stripped of autonomous will.","opus":"A predatory seduction framed as domination and design, the singer luring prey under their thumb with a chilling promise that the other's body was made for their purpose.","sonnet":"About a sexually predatory, controlling person who has fantasized about complete possession of their target — the speaker wants to physically dominate and psychologically possess someone, framed as seduction but structured as a fantasy of ownership.","src":"haiku"},
"levels~chauvinist":{"haiku":"A vigilante reckoning closes in on a chauvinist who bypassed due process and took justice into his own hands — the song watches the line between righteous anger and its mirror image dissolve as retribution bleeds out.","opus":"A charged reckoning with injustice and vigilante retribution, a man condemned without a hearing while chauvinists circle. The song walks the narrow line between a clean conscience and a pitiless one, weighing consequence against reaction, torn between watching a tormentor bleed and the warning to think before taking violence into private hands.","sonnet":"Vigilante justice examined and found corrosive: chauvinists bypass due process, take matters into their own hands, and the speaker watches them bleed out the pain they deserve while simultaneously asking whether the distinction between a pure heart and one with no remorse left matters anymore once the hands are already dirty.","src":"haiku"},
"levels~mind":{"haiku":"Regret and anxiety eclipse childhood freedom; the speaker grieves growing up and lost innocence.","opus":"An aching meditation on lost childhood and the weight of growing up, the narrator sleepless with dread of tomorrow and stuck in embedded memories. Nostalgia curdles into regret as he searches for closure that never comes, learning the hard way that what felt like bad luck is simply the cost of having to move on.","sonnet":"Nostalgia as paralysis — the narrator misses the days of being a kid without fear of tomorrow, but the embedded memories seize potential and suspend growth; the realisation that \"it's not bad luck, it's growing up\" is hard-won and only partially comforting. Flashback and the present tug in opposite directions; moving on is named as the only exit even while the pull backward stays strong.","src":"haiku"},
"levels~opium-of-man":{"haiku":"Systemic oppression drugs populations into complacency through manufactured suffering and artificial needs.","opus":"A furious takedown of a drug-and-corporate machine that gets rich off manufactured misery, framing modern dependency as a comfortable form of imprisonment. The band point at the avarice and ruin churned out by a coated cabal, a drug war that manages supply while decaying minds, the predator lurking that feasts on engineered illness.","sonnet":"Pharmaceutical capitalism as totalitarian system: the white coat mob manufactures greed and doom, tranquilizes the future to ensure consumption, runs a war on drugs only to control the supply — profits over people, suffering built into society, the monster always in the room.","src":"haiku"},
"levels~paracosm":{"haiku":"Spiritual journey through desert toward merger with another consciousness, where the speaker becomes the soul of the world. Fantasy materially overtakes the body, releasing it from isolation.","opus":"A psychedelic inward journey, the narrator setting off in search of the sun and discovering the beauty was already inside him. Guided by a fleeting desert apparition and a herald bearing a message, he passes through chaos and deception to emerge dissolving the border of the self, arriving at the revelation that he himself is the world's own soul.","sonnet":"A vision quest through desert and mirage: the speaker leaves in search of what they could become, encounters an oasis-man who dissolves into the matrix, and wonders if the figure holding their destiny will let them breathe if they stay; the revelation — 'you are the soul of the world' — arrives as both discovery and dissolution of self.","src":"haiku"},
"levels~pulse":{"haiku":"Rhythm and union create sacred transcendence; two bodies merge into obsessive synchronicity where individuality dissolves.","opus":"Ecstatic dancefloor desire where two bodies collide in aligned heartbeats, sin forgotten in the rhythm. Worship, adrenaline, and physical craving fuse into a wish to suspend the moment forever.","sonnet":"A song about being utterly absorbed in someone's physical presence and rhythm — the speaker is mesmerized by the other person to the point that moving with them makes sin feel irrelevant, and wants to exist suspended in this perfect moment of physical alignment forever.","src":"haiku"},
"levels~siren-hymn":{"haiku":"Worship and devotion consume the speaker as a feminine force promises divinity; they beg for transformation through sacred submission.","opus":"Devotion pitched as holy war, a lover worshipped as an unreachable divine peak. The plea to be spared and reborn together turns romantic longing into a self-annihilating religious surrender.","sonnet":"Devotion to a woman of overwhelming, almost divine beauty and power — the speaker begs to be worthy of her, surrenders completely, and frames the relationship as a covenant where her voice is paradise, asking to be killed or reborn rather than fail to impress her.","src":"haiku"},
"levi-ryan~meant-to-hurt":{"haiku":"Intentional cruelty fused with self-destruction; the speaker merges weapon and identity, unable to feel or function outside violence and intoxication.","opus":"A menacing confession of intentional cruelty toward a lover, intimacy fused with a gun, drugs, and isolation. The narrator finds beauty in what he hates and defines himself against the one he claims to love, numbed past feeling or breath, devotion curdled into a deliberate, self-destructive intent to wound.","sonnet":"A confession of deliberate cruelty dressed in self-destruction — smoking ice, not sleeping, drinking blood described as sweet, beauty found in things hated. The gun as twin and the years lived off X position this as someone who has decided damage is the only honest mode of love, the hurt intentional and unrepented.","src":"haiku"},
"lia-lia~2young2die":{"haiku":"Premature death beckons youth; the speaker faces mortality before life's natural span.","opus":"A wistful anthem of teenage disenchantment, spiked drinks and getting hazy to dull the fear of a frightening world. The narrator refuses to have her sadness confiscated, cataloguing wasted good looks, love that bruises, and a defiant urge to live fully before her time runs out — far too young for all this yet already worn down.","sonnet":"Teenage limbo with a bitter lemon taste — too young for everything (love, driving, living, dying), getting drunk and stoned in the gap between childhood and adulthood, pretty faces turning to waste. The toast to \"let's get drunk, let's get laid\" before the mind turns to stone is a desperate attempt to feel something before the window closes.","src":"haiku"},
"lia-lia~am-i-human":{"haiku":"Questioning personhood and authenticity; the speaker doubts their own consciousness while longing to escape human limitation.","opus":"A deadpan, absurdist inventory of the body and its appetites that spirals into a genuine identity crisis over who she even is. Depression and escapism leak through the na-na-nas, the narrator half-wishing she were a cat instead of a human she doesn't want to be.","sonnet":"A playful but sincere crisis of identity: the speaker catalogues basic human traits and desires — physical form, bodily needs, contradictory emotions — while genuinely questioning whether having them makes her a person, and fantasizing about the simpler existence of a cat.","src":"haiku"},
"lia-lia~angst":{"haiku":"Fear paralyzes and numbness spreads. The speaker has given all and holds nothing but scars where heart should be.","opus":"A bilingual confession of fear and numbness, giving everything and left with only a scar and a colorless world, wishing she didn't care and letting the demons have their way as she fades.","sonnet":"A bilingual song about anxiety and emotional numbness — the narrator wants to be held and comforted but feels their heart has been replaced by scar tissue in a colorless world, oscillating between fighting and fading, confessing in German that they are afraid and cannot feel when they should.","src":"haiku"},
"lia-lia~city-of-tears":{"haiku":"A trapped, suffocating soul gropes through darkness and despair, searching for guidance while chained to flesh and numbness.","opus":"Lia Lia builds a suffocating soundscape where sorrow blazes like something no weeping can douse. Breathless and bound to her own body, the narrator tallies shadows in a hushed, deadened world and begs to be guided across into whatever comes next. Memories of brighter dreams and a longing to be recovered and cradled again drive the ache.","sonnet":"Fear chains a person to their own body while the world goes numb around them. Dreams offer brighter colors and a guide toward some other side, but waking finds them breathless and unable to run — suspended between asking to be brought back and being too exhausted to reach.","src":"haiku"},
"lia-lia~dopamine":{"haiku":"Chasing chemical highs to escape constraint, desperate for any form of elevation or release.","opus":"Chasing a natural high and rejecting the crutches of drink and drugs, wanting freedom and release without chemical shortcuts. The craving stays the same even as the substances are refused, restlessness reframed as escape.","sonnet":"About wanting natural euphoria without substance dependence — the narrator has ridden dopamine highs her whole life and now wants to find a \"way out\" into genuine feeling without alcohol, drugs, or cigarettes. The tension between wanting escape and needing to cut artificial restraints is unresolved.","src":"haiku"},
"lia-lia~i-cry-in-my-mercedes":{"haiku":"Possessive love becomes torture; the speaker's tormentor derives pleasure from pain while positioning control as devotion and isolation as proof of connection.","opus":"An abusive relationship where affection is a leash and the lover collects her like an object, loving only her reflection. Wealth can't shield her from sadists, so she cries in luxury and wishes for superpowers instead of more lovers.","sonnet":"About surviving a sadistic, controlling relationship — the other person fed on the narrator's pain, controlled them through affection, and turned them into a collectible rather than a person loved for themselves. The image of crying in a Mercedes captures the absurdity of having material markers of success while still being emotionally destroyed; the narrator wants superpowers rather than more lovers, in a world she now recognizes as full of sadists.","src":"haiku"},
"lia-lia~i-m-a-moth":{"haiku":"Longing to transform into something beautiful while struggling with emotional pain and parental disconnection, eternally drawn to the moon.","opus":"A speaker laments being a dull moth instead of the lovely winged creature she imagines she might have been, knotting that self-image with a child's anxious question about whether her father cares for her. She casts love as electric and wounding, pursued like shadows and apologized for.","sonnet":"A speaker compares herself to a moth — drawn helplessly toward light, unable to become the butterfly she might have been — weaving in childhood longing for parental love and the bittersweet, persistent pull of things that never quite disappear.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is a self-diagnosis the singer can't stop reciting: the chorus stages a moth's grievance about the butterfly it might have been, which is really a plea to be noticed at all, disguised as its own insult. The verses expose the wound behind the pose, a child asking whether her father loves her and concluding that affection only ever arrives as a jolt, then apologizing for a failure she can't yet name. Verse two lifts the eyes upward to a pocked lunar face that keeps vanishing and returning on a fixed cycle, the absent parent recast as a distant light, the very flame the moth is built to circle and never touch, while the hypnotic loop of the word forever is at once lullaby and cage. So this is a moth's love song addressed to a source that only retreats, the self-mockery a mask over a kid who has decided the fault must be hers.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"self-mockery as a mask over a child who concluded the fault must be hers","built":"the absent father recast as a distant light the moth is built to circle and never touch"},
"lia-lia~night-call":{"haiku":"Desperate waiting for contact and attention, lonely despite self-reliance and struggling against the desire to be held and remembered.","opus":"Lia Lia captures the sting of hovering by the phone, protesting she isn't in love while admitting her heart's long since given away and her composure unravels in a storm. The pop artist frames bruised pride and a hunger for attention with wry candor, her vanity restless as she simply wants closeness instead of being overlooked.","sonnet":"She tells herself she has not fallen, that waiting by the phone is weakness, that real women do not linger — but the phone stays in her hands and no message comes. What she wants is simple and not about love: just to be held, just not to be ignored. The gap between self-image and actual need is the whole song.","src":"haiku"},
"lia-lia~shut-up":{"haiku":"A voice screams against world noise and corruption. Burning dirt down becomes the only answer to maddening conformity.","opus":"A manic overload boiling over into eat-the-rich rage and a scream at the noise, both external and internal, to just shut up; the deranged narrator wants to burn it all down as the world drives her insane.","sonnet":"A song about sensory overload and rage at a world that will not stop demanding things — the narrator is in pain, unhinged by a specific person named Detlef and by broader systemic rot, oscillating between a desire to burn everything down, eat the rich, and simply make all the noise stop.","src":"haiku"},
"lia-lia~what-if-i-jump-from-a-bridge":{"haiku":"Intrusive thoughts and suicidal ideation consume the speaker's mind despite medication, as anxiety hijacks every moment and whispers fatal suggestions.","opus":"Intrusive suicidal thoughts voiced flatly as recurring what-ifs, the mind cataloguing methods while pills fail to quiet the voice. Anxiety is named as a constant companion, and the fear of being too much drives the warning to keep from loving her.","sonnet":"About intrusive suicidal thoughts coexisting with anxiety — the narrator lists specific self-harm scenarios not as plans but as unwanted mental images that won't stop; underneath is someone who knows they are 'too much,' has taken their pills, and is still drowning in a brain that won't quiet.","src":"haiku"},
"liam-lynch~united-states-of-whatever":{"haiku":"An absurdist comedy where the protagonist responds to every circumstance with indifference, unfazed by encounters, questions, or life itself.","opus":"A two-minute joke anthem of pure teenage apathy, meeting every person and situation with the same shrug. The whole gag is that indifference itself becomes an identity worth declaring.","sonnet":"The narrator drifts through a series of casual encounters and minor confrontations — at the beach, the pool hall, a corner at 3am — responding to every situation with complete indifference. The song is a comic portrait of studied nonchalance as a personal philosophy.","src":"haiku"},
"lice~lice":{"haiku":"Beauty and love inspire only despair when the beloved remains unattainable; repeated devotion yields only futile yearning across the verse.","opus":"A Macedonian folk lament in which the singer loves a woman's white face, dark eyes, arched brows, and honeyed lips, yet finds it all in vain. She is sweet as sugar but faithless, so the adoration only deepens the ache of her betrayal.","sonnet":"A devotional folk lament in which the speaker kisses and gazes upon a beloved's white face, black eyes, arched brows, and honey lips — but all that love and looking yields nothing, because the sweet one is faithless.","src":"haiku"},
"lich-king~combat-mosh":{"opus":"A tongue-in-cheek celebration of the mosh pit as a return to primal, prehistoric violence, arguing modern civilized man is a thin construct over a savage, territorial, xenophobic caveman brute who devolves and gives in to his id when he fights in the pit.","sonnet":"A satirical hymn to moshing as a regression to primal caveman violence — the pit strips away civilization and awakens Pleistocene territorial aggression, and the song revels in the absurdity of modern humans bludgeoning each other at shows as genetic memory.","src":"sonnet"},
"life-after-youth~murder-me":{"haiku":"The speaker is numb and suicidal, watching themselves and loved ones fade away while dying inside—unable to feel, asking someone to finish what they've started.","opus":"A blunt portrait of depression at its most deadened, following someone who moves through ordinary days feeling nothing and quietly wishing for death. With a partner who wants him out of her life and a mind he no longer trusts, he keeps circling back to a plea to be put out of his misery.","sonnet":"A portrait of severe depression and emotional numbness — the speaker is sleeping too much, already detaching from a relationship, and so hollowed out they're ironically asking someone to just end it.","src":"haiku"},
"life-after-youth~what-do-i-do-with-all-this-love":{"haiku":"Unrequited intensity and abandonment drive murderous ideation as the speaker buries frustration in shared graves.","opus":"A blackly funny spiral of romantic devastation, the narrator drowning in unwanted love for someone who has moved on. Fantasies of burial and revenge tangle with genuine longing, a dialogue of accusations and regret playing out in his head, the impossible surplus of feeling curdling into thoughts of shared graves and self-harm.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship ends in homicidal-suicidal fantasy: the speaker, consumed by unrequited love and betrayal, imagines burying both parties together — \"here lies me and you, they'll dig a grave meant for two.\"","src":"haiku"},
"life-awaits~better-now":{"opus":"A post-hardcore reckoning with depression and the exhausting performance of pretending to be okay. Bottled emotion finally spills over as the narrator sinks and stalls, unready to face daylight even while claiming he's improving. The gap between rushing through life and dragging through it exposes the lie inside forced recovery.","sonnet":"About the disorientation of moving too fast through life while feeling emotionally stuck — the narrator is sinking, disenchanted with illusions, having lost themselves entirely, and the repeated claim of feeling 'better now' reads as unconvincing self-reassurance.","src":"sonnet"},
"life-awaits~butterflies":{"haiku":"Surrendering to obsession, finding home in someone else's gaze while losing yourself completely.","opus":"Total surrender to overwhelming attraction, someone offering everything and drowning willingly in a lover's pull, treating vertigo and self-erasure as proof of feeling alive. The nervous stomach becomes an ecstatic loss of control.","sonnet":"Intoxicated devotion to someone who pulls the narrator out of numbness — driven by dopamine and gravity toward another person's eyes, the song frames love as both drowning and being saved, comparing all of them to butterflies: fragile, driven, not fighting the current.","src":"haiku"},
"life-awaits~everlast":{"haiku":"A person feels trapped in repetitive suffering, unable to move forward despite dwelling on past failures and seeking transformation.","opus":"Melodic post-hardcore band Life Awaits pair confessional despair with a reach toward hope, voicing someone numbing sleepless nights with drink and convinced he can't keep going by himself. A guest verse answers back like an outside hand, coaxing him to look upward and keep going, so the plea for company becomes the whole point.","sonnet":"Getting stuck inside the endless present of one's own failure — each new day is filed away for a confession that never comes, meaningless things pile up where connection should be, and the speaker drowns in someone else's bleeding while unable to save anything. A brief interlude opens upward toward stars and sky before collapsing back into the same trap: stuck in the everlasting now, unable to move through it.","src":"haiku"},
"life-on-venus~stranger-times":{"haiku":"Alienation and displacement pervade an unfamiliar world; the speaker navigates hostility and displacement in strange times.","opus":"A dream-pop grappling with impermanence and the terror of losing someone as everything drifts toward dust and dispersal. The singer holds fast to a bond that resonates in harmony amid disorienting, uncertain days, insisting nothing endures while taking solace in the shared knowledge that, like distant suns, they'll one day flare out and scatter as one.","sonnet":"Disorientation as intimacy — lost between space and time with someone they're afraid to lose, the narrator insists nothing is forever but the spirit still sings in tune. The cosmic imagery (stars, dust, scatter) doesn't dwarf the relationship; it frames it as equally transient and equally real.","src":"haiku"},
"lifelover~m-s-salmonella":{"haiku":"Physical and emotional decay accumulates while solitude intensifies suffering, the speaker drowning in their own deterioration amid indifferent strangers.","opus":"A hungover crawl through squalor and self-disgust, vomit on the wall, a stinking wound, the pull to jump, ending on a farewell postcard. The Swedish depressive-metal scene rendering despair as grubby, ordinary detail rather than grand tragedy.","sonnet":"A person on a ferry, still suffering a severe hangover with vomit on the bathroom wall and a weeping bandage on their arm they keep smelling compulsively, watches strangers and feels only irritation, while the cold wind off the water makes them want to jump among the broken ice. They have sent a farewell postcard to someone.","src":"haiku"},
"light-the-torch~death-of-me":{"opus":"A song about a toxic, addictive attachment the singer knows is destroying him, likening the lover to a slow poison in his blood that he can't pull away from.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a toxic relationship they cannot escape acknowledges that the other person is slowly destroying them — the connection is poisonous and self-betraying, but the pull remains too strong to break.","src":"sonnet"},
"lights-i-o~drift-away":{"haiku":"Exhaustion from emotional labor and low-wage survival breeds dissociation; numbness and avoidance replace feeling as survival strategies against systemic attrition.","opus":"A hazy portrait of burnout and numbed escape, days lost to sleep and a whole weekend blurred yet still exhausting. Bottled rage, a minimum-wage grind and the sheer labor of feeling drive the narrator to shut down, admitting there's no time to be repaired, so everyone just drifts, self-medicating through a life with too few hours.","sonnet":"The exhaustion of precarious living rendered in a haze of Hennessy and bottled-up rage — minimum wage, no time to get fixed, feeling as \"too much emotional effort.\" The narrator doesn't choose to drift; there simply aren't enough hours to do anything else, so drifting is what happens to everyone, by default.","src":"haiku"},
"liily~applause":{"haiku":"Attention becomes the only currency that grants existence; without witnessed achievement, the self lacks substance or value.","opus":"A caustic anatomy of the craving for validation, framed around a man on TV who needs an audience to exist at all. The young band skewers a spectacle culture that watches a jumper as entertainment, where credentials and applause matter more than any human truth, hunger for approval curdling into hollow, desperate performance.","sonnet":"Applause as the only currency that matters — for the speaker, for the man on TV, for the crowd watching someone jump from a ledge — the song dismantles validation culture by making its hunger grotesque, ending in the image of a lonely person whose possessions don't even feel like their own.","src":"haiku"},
"liily~i-can-fool-anybody-in-this-town":{"haiku":"Invasive thoughts torture the waking mind from inside; something unseen in the walls or buried in words is killing the speaker. Silence becomes deafening.","opus":"The young L.A. band renders spiraling paranoia in jagged garage-punk. Some unseen thing bores into his thoughts as he spends his solitary days peeling flecks from the wall while denying anything's wrong. A voice he pleads with to stop is lodged inside him and destroying him, and he needs to identify it even as he keeps listening, dread and denial locked together, for the approach of death.","sonnet":"An unseen presence infiltrates waking hours until the boundary between inner noise and external reality collapses. The speaker strips away the performance of composure and admits the buried thoughts are the actual threat — not paranoia but an honest reckoning with something consuming him from inside.","src":"haiku"},
"liily~nine":{"haiku":"A persistent cycle of lies and healing repeats nine times; the speaker wonders if they can bleed or see clearly, questioning whether love endures through repeated failure.","opus":"Liily tally deceit and forgiveness in a fraught reckoning with a partner, the narrator feeling normal and monstrous at once as his body seems to disappear on him. He counts how often he lied and was forgiven, marvels that the other stayed, and confesses he fled just as many times, the mounting doubts all circling nine desperate attempts to just be with someone he loves.","sonnet":"A relationship defined by a repeated cycle of lying and being forgiven reaches a point of disoriented gratitude — he cannot understand why she stayed through nine rounds of failure. The question of whether to stay or go remains unanswered, shadowed by awe at her sustained patience.","src":"haiku"},
"liily~sepulveda-basin":{"haiku":"A lust-fueled whirlwind romance crumbles quickly; the speaker chased gold and fools' tales instead of seeing who really loved them. Their life grows sad in retrospect.","opus":"Named for a Los Angeles flood basin, this Liily song is a bitter look back at an empty, transactional relationship built on lust rather than truth. The narrator insists he needs no one even as he admits the affair was the best thing he ever knew and that his life is sinking, confessing a greed and self-absorption that blinded him to the people who actually loved him.","sonnet":"He pursued something gilded and hollow — drawn by lust and the thrill of acquisition — while ignoring the people who genuinely loved him. Having cycled in and out of this pattern until his life feels genuinely diminished, he lands on the quiet resolve that he needs no one else to validate him.","src":"haiku"},
"liily~sold":{"haiku":"Someone exploits and runs circles around the speaker, making them feel deceived and used up, trading them away for nothing. The speaker knows they're easy prey.","opus":"Liily channel restless youth into a song about being exploited and misread, its narrator worn down by manipulation and the lies fed to him. Painted as a misfit and a scapegoat, he spins in circles while others profit off him, insisting that although he is gullible he won't simply be sacrificed to serve someone else's agenda.","sonnet":"Someone is being exploited and used as raw material while being told it is his own fault. He sees through the framing — he is not a tool to be deployed for free — and holds his ground against the manipulation even as the circles he runs in become harder to escape.","src":"haiku"},
"liily~the-weather":{"haiku":"Victimized and paid to stay broken, the speaker measures survival against a changing emotional climate. They're a million pieces yet million reasons to survive the heat.","opus":"Liily stretch a running weather metaphor into a portrait of grim survival, voiced by someone who feels exploited and threatened yet can't shake a craving to matter and be remembered. Spilling everything just to gauge how bad things have gotten, he holds himself together through morbid fixations that somehow keep his fraying mind intact.","sonnet":"Fragmented but resilient, a figure who has spilled everything just to take the temperature of the world finds a million reasons to keep going despite the shattering. Catacombs and scattered bones keep his head level; the ember he tends is more valuable than the approval of anyone trying to drag him down.","src":"haiku"},
"liily~toro":{"haiku":"A person caught in an obsessive, unsettling attraction begs to be saved from the cycle of manipulation and false love.","opus":"The young Los Angeles band Liily wrap infatuation in dread, the narrator queasy amid a lover's striking eyes and cryptic falsehoods. He begs for her odd affection while dangling on the edge of rescue and doubting his own endurance. The bullfighting title casts desire as a lethal contest, tenderness and threat circling each other in an endless drop.","sonnet":"Dazzled by someone whose beauty barely masks manipulation, the narrator cycles between craving and exhaustion. A stranger of strange, almost spiritual magnetism promises rescue but keeps withholding it, leaving the narrator suspended in longing — asking to be blessed, held, woken from an endless fall.","src":"haiku"},
"like-moths-to-flames~into-the-black":{"opus":"A study of deep depression and isolation, the narrator waiting to be dug up while keeping mortality close as company, gone numb to warmth and love. He describes vanishing into darkness as a last bid to feel anything at all, with nothing remaining to protect.","sonnet":"A dissociation song about choosing numbness and disappearance as relief from unbearable emptiness — the narrator has gone cold to love, stopped feeling, and retreats into blackness not as death but as the only place where solitude feels like something new rather than nothing at all.","src":"sonnet"},
"like-moths-to-flames~killing-what-s-underneath":{"opus":"A bleak reckoning with self-destruction and dissociation, where the narrator is drawn to the edge, wants his memories erased and the numb parts of himself killed off just to feel like he exists; he rejects others' prayers and faith, wanting only release.","sonnet":"A song about suicidal ideation framed as merciful erasure — the narrator imagines falling off the edge as relief, wants memories erased, and asks to be left alone to extinguish whatever is underneath the pain. The refusal of prayer and faith as comfort is explicit; the narrator wants dissolution, not rescue.","src":"sonnet"},
"like-moths-to-flames~smoke-and-mirrors":{"haiku":"A person drowns in self-hatred and complacency, unable to escape the illusions they've built, watching everything decay while grief buries them alive.","opus":"Ohio metalcore band Like Moths to Flames map depression as gradual decay, with a narrator alienated in his own body and at the end of his endurance. Hope and grief settle into numb apathy while faith reveals itself as a trap with no way out, until he resigns himself to forgetting as the only forgiveness he can manage.","sonnet":"Self-loathing and stalled grief take the form of slow internal decay. The narrator recognizes they have long been their own saboteur, sustained only by illusions about faith and identity that dissolve into smoke — unable to distinguish the moment things went wrong from the moment they became irreversible.","src":"haiku"},
"like-roses~splenetic":{"haiku":"Presence itself becomes toxic; the speaker hates who they become around their partner, yet cannot escape the gravitational pull of attachment.","opus":"Hating the anxious, perpetually inadequate self a relationship manufactures, while the partner calls him darling as he bleeds into them. The devastating honesty is still longing to return to someone whose love feels invented, drowned at the base of a bottle.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped in a relationship that diminishes them — tongue-tied, unable to claw out the fragments left under their skin, still wanting to come home despite the pain — asking bitterly whether the partner's friends at the bottom of the bottle love him the way the speaker does.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-gnar-lil-keed-internet-money~hey":{"haiku":"Hedonistic braggadocio about wealth and dominance, treating people as disposable pleasures.","opus":"Rage-rap flexing designer drugs, luxury cars and women as interchangeable trophies, money offered as the only cure for pain. The bravado is armor, the emptiness of the tally its unspoken subject.","sonnet":"A bravado-heavy display of wealth, status, and sexual conquest — the speakers perform power through diamonds, drugs, luxury cars, and sexual dominance, projecting an image of untouchable excess.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-nas-x~panini":{"haiku":"The speaker resists a jealous authority figure threatened by his ascent; success exposes the conditional nature of support and loyalty.","opus":"Fame's turn from underdog to target, addressed to fans who loved him when he was small and resent him now that he's on top. Named for a cartoon character, it's really about the fickleness of an audience that wants you tiny.","sonnet":"A newly famous speaker calls out someone who encouraged his ambitions when he was unknown but now tries to suppress his success, demanding they clarify what they actually want from him rather than continue performing support while working against him.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-tjay~move-on":{"haiku":"Breaking free from a manipulative relationship, choosing independence despite the pain.","opus":"A young rapper talking himself out of a relationship that wronged him, half heartbreak and half warning shot. Threaded through is the 2020 flex of a come-up, using new money and growth as proof he's better off gone.","sonnet":"A speaker walks away from a girl who played games with him — cataloguing what she will miss, making clear that her manipulation has cost her his loyalty, and asserting that he has grown past the relationship and is focused on his own rise.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert-babymetal~the-end-feat-babymetal":{"haiku":"Finality and apocalypse, death as completion or release.","opus":"A giddy, self-mythologizing crossover where Uzi casts himself as an alien visitor sent to earth only to deliver his Pink Tape, framing music-making as a cosmic mission. Between BabyMetal's sing-along chants and Uzi's day-glo boasts, the track distills his post-Eternal Atake persona: half comic-book extraterrestrial, half pop evangelist preaching that love and noise are all anyone needs.","sonnet":"A buoyant collision of alien mythology and kawaii pop — the rapper arrives from outer space to drop his Pink Tape and prescribes pink days for blue moods, while BABYMETAL's nursery-rhyme chorus wraps the whole thing in a communal sing-along. It's deliberately weightless: love is all we need, every day, every time.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert-bring-me-the-horizon~werewolf-feat-bring-me-the-horizon":{"haiku":"Curse becomes gift: darkness transforms the self into something that daylight can't contain, and descent underground feels like the only authentic home.","opus":"Alienation rendered as lycanthropy: envying the daytime people while darkness becomes the only safe place, a blessed curse the narrator can't disown as he burrows underground away from anyone who thinks they know him.","sonnet":"A song about being cursed to live in darkness, envying those who exist in light, and finding a haven in the underground and shadows — the speaker has burned through all their lives, can't cross back to normalcy, and is drawn to blood and the night rather than salvation.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~amped":{"haiku":"Amphetamine confidence and material ascension fuel the speaker's bravado; luxury and escapism dominate while the underlying hunger for genuine connection remains barely concealed.","opus":"A hyped, chemically fueled flex from Uzi's later run, riding the manic energy of lean and the come-up. Beneath the adrenaline is a familiar undertow: fame fatigue, distrust of hangers-on, and a plea to be loved for himself rather than the money. Trap life is framed as tolerable, almost cozy, even as it isolates him.","sonnet":"A high-energy assertion of upward momentum — money, women, trap life, rap life — punctuated by the mutual-love refrain \"love me for me, I'll love you for you,\" the whole thing fired at full speed with interlocking boasts and almost no narrative connective tissue.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~cs":{"haiku":"Manufactured identity collapses under existential crisis; the speaker performs normalcy while drowning in suicidal ideation, angels and fables masking the urge toward self-destruction.","opus":"Uzi lifts a System of a Down verse about ritual, forced routine and self-righteous suicide, wearing another band's despair as his own edge-of-the-ledge numbness. The makeup that hides the scars is the same performance the song is caught inside.","sonnet":"A direct interpolation of Linkin Park's Crawling turned into a suicide note — someone hides scars under makeup, leaves keys on the table, and stands on a ledge having decided the world is not worth trusting.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~days-come-and-go":{"haiku":"Navigating fame's instability and isolation while wrestling with insecurity and the people around you's betrayal.","opus":"Uzi laying bare the insecurity behind the flex, an absent father, distrust of a partner, and money that reads as brain damage while everyone reaches for a cut. Fame's fickle love is the one constant he can't count on but his cash can.","sonnet":"A sprawling, raw self-portrait of insecurity, grief, and resilience — the narrator touches on not knowing his father, his girlfriend sleeping with other rappers, brain damage from stress, friends who only appear when he's rich, and spending two hundred thousand on a friend's funeral; through it all he keeps moving because he always bounces back.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~died-and-came-back":{"haiku":"Resurrection through excess; the speaker returns transformed after ego-death, now impervious to judgment and tethered only to material flow.","opus":"A flex on survival and reinvention, having died and come back while rivals lost their range and got their plaques stolen. Between the boasts about houses, watches, and two phones, Uzi admits he still sees demons in the dark and dodges the government while on probation.","sonnet":"Uzi returns from some personal nadir — buying his mother a house, still seeing demons in the dark, dismissing pretenders who lost their chains and their platinum plaques while he kept climbing — a resurrection flex still haunted by the underworld.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~fire-alarm-feat-snow-strippers":{"haiku":"Fragmentation of self through substance and excess: the body becomes a battlefield between conflicting impulses, and waking means confronting dissolution.","opus":"A drug-blurred spiral where flexing and detached sex can't quiet the alarm of not being able to see or be yourself. The luxury cascade is a smokescreen over dissociation.","sonnet":"A song about identity fracture and dissociation — the speaker cannot see, be, or free themselves from themselves, cycling through self-harm and self-alienation imagery while trapped in an interior emergency.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~flooded-the-face":{"haiku":"Diamonds and money cascade as visible proof of becoming; the speaker emerges from hardship into material gluttony, commanding devotion through spectacle.","opus":"A wealth-and-sex flex measuring how far Uzi has come, drowning his face in diamonds and blacking out the Wraith now that a once-hard life turned great. The recurring dares and the laughter at the bank frame money as vindication over everyone who doubted him.","sonnet":"Coming up from hardship to diamond-flooded watches, matte-wrapped Wraiths, and unlimited space, the speaker describes street-to-wealth transformation while cycling through sexual bravado and a catalogue of luxury as proof of arrival.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~i-gotta":{"haiku":"Material accumulation and bodily excess signal dominance; the speaker flaunts wealth and sexual conquest as proof of transcendence over ordinary constraints.","opus":"A boastful catalogue of ridiculous whips, wrists, and women, with sick opps envious of the riches. Using his real name Symere as a punchline, Uzi frames going diamond and out-flexing rivals as the whole reason he keeps climbing.","sonnet":"A boast-heavy flex cataloguing sexual conquests, ridiculous wealth, luxury cars, and street loyalty — the speaker introduces himself by government name and declares his opps are sick with envy while he keeps climbing toward diamond status.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~just-wanna-rock":{"haiku":"Raw hedonism and defiant indifference; the speaker rejects compromise and judgment, claiming space to party and move freely without apology or explanation.","opus":"Pure kinetic release, a party built entirely on the demand to move and stop nobody's vibe. It wants nothing beyond the body in motion.","sonnet":"A hedonistic club brag with minimal content — the narrator just wants to dance and show off, warning rivals not to step to them while commanding attention.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~nakamura":{"haiku":"Resurrection from perceived failure demands hardened resilience, where vulnerability is weaponized against selfish predators. The speaker climbs alone toward dominance, burning previous attachments to fuel the ascent.","opus":"A comeback anthem of wounded pride, where Uzi answers doubters who said he fell off by hardening into someone colder and hungrier. He credits scars from past relationships and fans for the fire, tracing a rise so desperately wanted that it broke him down. Naming a wrestling leap captures the reckless, all-in leap back to the top.","sonnet":"After a period of perceived decline the speaker reasserts himself — having broken down to the floor with wanting success, now standing like a bulldog with a backbone — comparing his comeback leap to the wrestler Nakamura jumping off the top rope, closing with gratitude to his fanbase.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~of-course-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Flexing wealth, drugs, and street authenticity; the speaker boasts about status, violence, and hedonistic excess.","opus":"Lil Uzi Vert, the Philadelphia rapper whose emo-inflected melodies reshaped modern rap, lets a hazy flex slide into raw grief. Amid boasts of designer clothes and Glocks, the mask slips for a fallen friend, Chico, whose phone the narrator still calls and texts. Standing by the opps with a weapon, mourning and menace blur into the same restless numbness that fame and drugs never quite fill.","sonnet":"A tribute woven into a bravado showcase: the narrator flaunts designer labels, a loaded weapon, and pharmaceutical excess, then pivots to grief for a friend who was caught lacking by opps. The flex is real but so is the hole it papers over.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~patience-feat-don-toliver":{"haiku":"Numbing dependency replaces genuine connection; medication becomes a love substitute as the speaker oscillates between emotional unavailability and craving presence they cannot sustain.","opus":"Love confused with the prescription that dulls the ache of it, where wanting a person and wanting the drug that medicates the heartbreak become the same craving. Numbness is the only patience on offer.","sonnet":"A relationship destabilized by medication and emotional numbness — Seroquel blurs the line between loving a person and craving the drug, and the narrator cannot tell if the feelings are real or pharmaceutical.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~pluto-to-mars":{"haiku":"Lil Uzi celebrates his rise from nothing to wealth and fame, accumulating luxury and status while questioning whether genuine connection would survive if he lost it all.","opus":"A rags-to-stardom flex about impossible wealth (ten-car garage, yellow diamonds) framed as proof he beat everyone who doubted him. The recurring question of whether a lover would still stay if the fame vanished hints at loneliness under the bragging, and suspicion that people around him are frauds out to sabotage.","sonnet":"A rags-to-riches vindication — Uzi catalogues his wealth and success as proof to doubters and his mother that he made it, while also questioning whether a romantic partner would stick around if the spotlight went dark.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~rehab":{"haiku":"A caregiver guides someone through withdrawal and recovery, offering patience through the darkest moments.","opus":"A startlingly plain memory of rehab, Uzi thanking a caretaker he saw as a mother figure through withdrawal, tears and shame. Money meant nothing inside those walls, and the equality of the place both comforted and unsettled him.","sonnet":"A direct, emotional tribute to a nurse named Moran who cared for the narrator during a drug withdrawal stay in a rehab center — the song catalogs the physical misery of withdrawal (shaking, losing bowel control, Seroquel and gabapentin) and the kindness of a staff member who treated him like family.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~shardai-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Material excess and romance blur across continents as the speaker chases sensory overload and escape. Transcendence through excess becomes the only logic available in a burnished, hollow world.","opus":"A hedonistic, Japan-fixated brag reel steeped in designer fashion, lean, and detached sex, threaded with Uzi's odd self-awareness that he feels far from normal and cannot change. Amid the Harajuku scenery and luxury name-drops runs a quiet unease, the numbness of someone chasing sensation while confessing he can't feel his own feet.","sonnet":"A boastful self-portrait of excess — designer labels, weapons, a Japanese connection in Harajuku, drug-fuelled highs — orbits a recurring 'Shardai' figure who functions as an emblem of cash, confidence, and chaotic energy, with a brief moment of self-awareness that he is 'far from normal' and cannot change.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~spin-again":{"haiku":"Casual detachment and rapid cycling through connections leave no room for attachment; the speaker spins frictionlessly through desire.","opus":"A dizzy, disposable-relationships flex where Uzi likens himself to a spinning ceiling fan, cycling through women, money, and drugs with practiced detachment. The bars lean on canine menace and clout, but the recurring image of endless spinning hints at a restlessness underneath, motion for its own sake, never quite landing anywhere.","sonnet":"The speaker filters out anyone not about money, pulling up in conspicuous style and boasting about sexual exploits and the lean lifestyle, circling back obsessively — \"spinning just like I am a ceiling fan\" — to a loop of stunting, dismissal, and self-mythology.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~that-fiya":{"haiku":"Bravado and gang warfare merge with designer consumption; the speaker claims predatory power and material dominance while the underlying violence remains casual and omnipresent.","opus":"A hard, menacing street cut where Uzi trades his psychedelic melodies for gun talk, Blood affiliation, and cold contempt for gangbanging that gets people killed. Luxury flexes sit beside real violence, and the psycho self-branding feels less like play than warning. It shows the grittier, less whimsical register Uzi reaches for when proving he can still sound dangerous.","sonnet":"An aggressive street-level boast centred on weapon carry, crew loyalty, and social dominance — the .44 as emblem of status and threat — delivered in rapid disconnected images of wealth, violence, and sexual bravado that circle back to the hook without narrative resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~the-end-feat-babymetal":{"haiku":"Transcendent love and cosmic escape offer brief reprieve from earthly pain and division.","opus":"An alien pop fantasy where the artist claims to have touched down from outer space just to hand over his album and preach that love is the only thing we need. The sing-along innocence is deliberately weightless, joy for its own sake.","sonnet":"A playful, low-stakes celebration of music, love, and alien self-mythology — Uzi frames himself as an otherworldly being who landed just to drop an album, and BABYMETAL's choral refrains push it into communal sing-along territory.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~werewolf-feat-bring-me-the-horizon":{"src":"web","web":"Uzi as a creature of darkness who envies the daywalkers but can't escape what he is — 'blessed with a curse,' the werewolf a nature that's gift, burden and identity at once, delivered full-screamo with Bring Me the Horizon.","fable":"The werewolf is a self-diagnosis. The envy of daywalkers in the opening frames ordinary untroubled people as a species the narrator can no longer count himself among, because when night falls something inside him mutates into a shape beyond his command. Uzi and Bring Me the Horizon build the whole song around that beast as a metaphor for a self one is saddled with. The hook's mantra — a blessing and an affliction he can't bring himself to question — holds both halves at once, the fame or the temperament received as gift and wound he's stopped even fighting. What the track ultimately dramatizes is retreat: the dark recast as sanctuary, a burrowing further from daylight because he can't bear to look at it, a conviction that the grave would be an improvement. The isolation isn't a sentence he's escaping but a hideout he chooses, the monster crawling somewhere it can at last be seen for what it truly is.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape"],"means":"the monster recast as sanctuary rather than affliction","built":"the hook holds gift and wound simultaneously without resolving which the beast is"},
"lil-uzi-vert~x2":{"haiku":"Excess and transformation cascade; the speaker moves between states of being, adorned in symbols of elevation while maintaining raw appetite beneath the gloss.","opus":"Pure Pink Tape-era flex, all Wraiths and Audemars and outrunning the wait for the album; the manic repetition of everything being too crazy is less about wealth than about vertigo, a star losing his breath from his own momentum.","sonnet":"A gleeful, chaotic brag about being too \"cray\" for everyone — Pink Tape hype, Audemars watches, Montana girls, werewolf-vampire mythology, and VVS diamonds stacked like a serving tray — Uzi revelling in his own untouchable excess.","src":"haiku"},
"lil-uzi-vert~zoom-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Wealth, status symbols, and unavailability, rejecting interruption while flaunting material success and dominance.","opus":"Lil Uzi Vert dodges every incoming call, treating unreachability as its own flex while flaunting his ride, designer gear and stacks of cash. The Philadelphia rapper spins seclusion into status, arriving fast and unbothered, granting that a single person could coax him out of his self-imposed distance. Over Wheezy's beat it becomes a snapshot of wealthy isolation, riches counted in near-total solitude.","sonnet":"Wealth and momentum as deflection — he arrives fast, refuses to answer calls, fills the silence with designer labels and self-sufficiency. The bravado is total but underneath runs a thread of selective vulnerability: he shows up only for the right person, on his own terms, with everything to prove and no patience for anyone who would slow him down.","src":"haiku"},
"limbs~abandoned":{"haiku":"Inherits a pattern of early departure and abandonment, watching relationships burn while giving nothing left to offer. The cycle repeats from father to self.","opus":"A haunted reflection on inherited abandonment, the narrator carrying his father's bleak counsel to burn out early since everyone departs anyway, watching a loved one pull away as fire devours everything and his own mind escapes him. Limbs are a contemporary American metalcore band, and this weaves generational dread and self-immolation into a grim accounting of love exchanged for anguish.","sonnet":"A father's prophecy of abandonment — the warning that everyone eventually leaves shapes the whole emotional structure, as the speaker gives everything, gets burned anyway, and watches the person they loved walk away through fever and smoke. The song swings between rage at the betrayal and a grief-tinged admission of having missed something irreplaceable, all while the mind disowns itself and the lifeline snaps clean.","src":"haiku"},
"limbs~empty-vessel":{"haiku":"The speaker feels worthless and insubstantial, a hollow container paying just to stay alive, abandoned and isolated while fading into nothingness without escape or hope.","opus":"A hollowed-out lament of feeling worthless, merely renting a place in existence, unready to abandon everything and doubtful salvation is even possible, while shrinking an absent person to a wisp fading into nothing. Limbs, the American metalcore band, ride waves of loneliness and self-erasure here, tracing a slow collapse where he never gets to say goodbye to the people he loved most.","sonnet":"Hollowness as a permanent condition — the speaker cannot become what anyone needs, faces down with the past reduced to a distant memory, and asks how long one has to hold their breath before having worth. The song circles the question of soul and its possible loss without resolution, drifting into the image of a ghost dissolving in smoke while the person left alone with no goodbyes finally names the trap: trapped in a vessel, wasting away.","src":"haiku"},
"limbs~i-used-to-be-you":{"haiku":"Toxic transformation by another's influence; the speaker allowed intrusion and now resists being remade into the image of someone else.","opus":"A metalcore lament about being remade by someone toxic, letting in a person who wasn't even a friend and losing yourself to their name and their lies. The gut punch is chasing another's ghost until you drift under the waves, undone by everything you allowed.","sonnet":"About letting in someone who turned out to be no friend and changed you for the worse — the narrator is sick of chasing the ghost of who they were before, feeling their will to live stripped away, and still unable to stop giving in.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit-paul-wall~middle-finger-ft-paul-wall":{"haiku":"Contempt and excess parade as lifestyle; sex, drugs, and dismissal frame success through aggression.","opus":"A brash Gold Cobra-era middle finger to haters and doubters, splitting between Paul Wall's Houston hustle boasts about the grind and Durst's crude, defiant contempt for anyone talking behind his back. It thrives on nu-metal bravado and shock, a raised fist aimed at critics who wrote the band off, insisting they'll keep going regardless.","sonnet":"An aggressive celebration of hustle and contempt for haters, the two MCs trading boasts about money, grinding, and sexual conquest while raising a middle finger at everyone talking trash — the whole thing wrapped in deliberate provocation and profanity.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~9-teen-90-nine":{"haiku":"A celebration of global connection through music and shared experience, with the speaker reflective on changes and escaping pain through worldwide communication.","opus":"Durst's turn-of-the-millennium party manifesto, the stage as the top of the world and the crowd as connection, brushing off Armageddon to make everyone jump. Under the bravado is a nu-metal creed of communal escape, finding a place to flee today together.","sonnet":"A declaration of arrival and connection — the narrator describes the stage as the top of the world, celebrates the growing audience as global communication, and insists the year 1999 is a moment worth riding even without knowing where it leads.","src":"haiku","fable":"The bravado plays as armor that keeps slipping, and one couplet about the swelling audience and tightening security recurs like a twitch, casting the moment of reaching the summit less as victory than as the instant retreat becomes impossible. Its true subject is the turn-of-millennium fear of a marketed doomsday everyone braced for, repurposed into a taunt to leap, so the crowd's shared jump reads at once as celebration and as a wince turned away from a future no one wants to steer. Beneath the puffed-chest sparring the choruses grope toward something close to communal desperation, a stunned we-actually-pulled-this-off that instantly wants to dissolve, until the ending pares everything to one scared, repeated demand for their bearings, answered only by the year hollered like the sole solid ground left to stand on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","party & hedonism"],"means":"the millennium jump repurposed as a wince toward a future nobody wants to steer","built":"the year hollered as the sole answer to a demand for bearings reduces communal celebration to relief at still existing"},
"limp-bizkit~90-2-10":{"haiku":"Celebration of a wild party skewed heavily toward women, with band members and guests creating mayhem. Ratio-focused braggadocio about excess and uninhibited revelry.","opus":"A raucous celebration of a wild house party overwhelmingly full of women, with a lopsided ratio of ninety percent women. It rattles off drinking, chaos, and famous guests as the party runs from dusk to dawn.","sonnet":"The song describes a block party populated almost entirely by women, with band members and various guests celebrating through the night. The title ratio—90 percent women—is the boasted premise throughout.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song runs a lopsided party math as pure bragging fuel, its title ratio pinning a crowd of ninety percent women to two men to ten of everyone else. The verses keep stacking bodies and rockstar cameos onto that grid until the guest list becomes the entire point. But the movement lives in the hook's tag, which drops the pretense of invitation and hardens into a boast that the party runs this way whether you approve or not, curdling into a challenge. That admiration then gets chanted down to a single line repeated past the point of meaning, the mask of endless excess wearing thin into rote compulsion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"the party math hardening from invitation into a challenge no one asked for","built":"the title ratio pins the entire song to a grid before the first line is sung"},
"limp-bizkit~a-lesson-learned":{"haiku":"Fame and fortune isolate the speaker despite knowing more people; their stomach aches from the mistake of thinking wealth brings friendship.","opus":"An unexpectedly wounded meditation on the void behind celebrity, its narrator nauseated with remorse, surrounded by acquaintances yet lonelier than at any prior point, having discovered that wealth and renown only pose as loyalty. Coming from Fred Durst, a figure synonymous with rap-metal excess, this hushed confessional swaps swagger for the true price of everything he chased.","sonnet":"Fame as a loneliness machine — the stomach hurts, which the speaker reads as punishment for every mistake made, and the single thing learned from knowing more people than ever before is that fortune and celebrity disguise themselves as friendship while leaving you more isolated than you were before any of it started.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~almost-over":{"haiku":"A survivor reflects on childhood struggle and adult pain while asserting readiness to transcend stagnation and blow minds with raw expression.","opus":"An autobiographical span from a mistreated, seething child who learned to brawl and rhyme, to a grown man who found love, success, and clarity, insisting his essence stays intact. The wistful middle section, framing life as a dream he's reluctant to wake from because it's nearly finished, cuts the swagger with a reminder of mortality.","sonnet":"Fred Durst traces his life from a bullied, raging boy through to an older man who has survived industry contempt and personal pain — the whole thing framed as a dream he refuses to wake from, asserting he is still relevant and at his peak.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two voices alternate line for line, the scrappy boyhood self caged and swinging back against the world set against the grown performer who lost, paid, and carved out a name, so a life gets tallied as facing columns. The one constant stitched through both sides is a vow that nothing will shift, while the chorus melts that whole record into a churn of strangers' questions about who and what he is, existence reduced to being spotted and named. In the quiet turn the swagger buckles into someone stranded inside his own dream, unsure whether the inner voices urging him awake mean an ending of breath, of relevance, or only of the act, the phrase almost over left poised between reprieve and dread.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"a life tallied in facing columns where the one constant is a vow nothing shifts","built":"the phrase almost over left poised between death, retirement, and reprieve"},
"limp-bizkit~angels":{"haiku":"Someone reflects on heartbreak and regret, finding comfort in the memory of a lost love watched over by angels.","opus":"Limp Bizkit dial down the aggression for a guilt-heavy ballad about losing someone, whether to a breakup or death. The narrator owns his failings and pleads for forgiveness, picturing a reunion in dreams, and casts unseen watchers as proof that beneath his flaws beats a sincere heart unwilling to flee his own wrongs.","sonnet":"A song of remorse addressed to someone who has died or left — the narrator spent years making mistakes he can't undo, took the love he was given and wasted it, and now pleads for forgiveness, aware that the angels watching him have seen everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession addressed to someone who has gone, where the singer meets his guilt not with excuses but by owning it flatly, admitting the wrong is his and refusing to run. The angels of the hook are the lens, invoked not as saviors but as witnesses — the only ones who watched him cry in private and still believe in the side no one else sees. The first verse turns loss inward, building an island out of the love he was given, while the second drops all deflection. There he speaks plainly of the applause he gives himself and the mistakes he knew all along, pleading to be forgiven for how he is. By the bridge the song strips to one fact repeated three times, that he has been seen weeping, which is the whole plea — being witnessed at his lowest as the closest thing to being forgiven.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"being witnessed weeping offered as the nearest available substitute for forgiveness","built":"the angels invoked as witnesses rather than saviors reframes mercy as surveillance"},
"limp-bizkit~autotunage":{"haiku":"An energetic party invitation and call to rock, celebrating music and collective release. The song centers on giving people what they want—music and excitement—without deeper narrative.","opus":"A party-hyping performance about lighting up the venue, commanding the crowd, and rocking until dawn, promising to give the audience whatever they want.","sonnet":"A straightforward party and hype track in which the narrator calls the crowd to get loud, celebrates the energy of a live show, and directs his attention toward women in the audience — no subject beyond the performance itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"Autotunage plays like a hype-man conjuring one endless blowout, the poured drink, the crowd urged to scream, the vow to keep going past sunrise, all aimed at forging a night no cop or curfew could ever shut down. Yet the repeated command to keep spinning the floor is the tell: the wall of noise is a temporary spell dressed as permanence, and a quieter line slips through admitting the whole scene is invented, just two people in a private fiction. The Auto-Tune nod in the title reframes even the swagger as a processed veneer, a boast smoothed into performance that keeps announcing its own artifice while conceding, underneath, that the bravado was pretend.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"the all-night blowout exposed as a private fiction two people invented together","built":"the title's Auto-Tune nod frames the swagger itself as a processed veneer"},
"limp-bizkit~back-porch":{"haiku":"A party anthem celebrating drinking and escapism with friends in a casual, uninhibited setting.","opus":"Nu-metal party bravado, all swagger about drinking and hooking up until the night runs out. There is no subtext, just Durst chasing the hedonistic rush of the moment.","sonnet":"A straightforward invitation to a backyard party — the back porch is where the real celebration happens, outside the claustrophobic indoors, drinking until it's gone and dancing until the night runs out.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song stalls at a raised platform behind the house and never moves past it, promising a celebration whose actual reason keeps sliding off toward some further town, so arrival is endlessly postponed. That delay is the real subject, and one odd line admits it by insisting nothing interrupts the hush, as if the noise were layered over a silence it can't quite drown out. Durst stacks up the gestures of readiness, the countdown, the summoned crowd, and a confession that indoors felt too airless to breathe, which turns the open-air boasting into flight from something closed rather than a party anyone ever enters.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","party & hedonism","shame & repression"],"means":"readiness endlessly assembled around a celebration that never arrives","built":"the open-air setting staged as escape from an interior the speaker never names"},
"limp-bizkit~barnacle":{"haiku":"A leeching presence clings to the narrator, draining patience and trust through relentless emotional neediness and refusal to self-sustain.","opus":"A blunt kiss-off to a clinging parasite of a person who ruins everything and won't let go. No metaphor beyond the title, just the exhausted demand to be freed from someone's dead weight.","sonnet":"Direct address to a clingy, destructive person who attaches themselves to others and drains them — the speaker has run out of patience and demands they leave entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"The comic frame of a song dedication curdles into a skin-crawl fixation, so that the leech being addressed is never a lover or a rival but simply a parasite that latches and clings and drains until patience rots. By the end the reading collapses out of complaint into pure repelled reflex, the same three-word push repeated because a barnacle can't be reasoned with, only scraped off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"pure repelled reflex when the parasite defies reasoning and can only be scraped off","built":"the song-dedication frame curdles into fixation, the comic setup swallowed by skin-crawl repetition"},
"limp-bizkit~behind-blue-eyes":{"haiku":"A bad man hides pain and loneliness behind sorrow and lies; his dreams paradoxically offer hope while his conscience remains hollow, leaving vengeance as his only love and freedom.","opus":"Fred Durst's cover leans into the Who's original self-pity, the misunderstood bad man whose rage and loneliness stay hidden, undercut by the band's own name-spelling interjection. Wounded ego wearing borrowed grandeur.","sonnet":"A cover of the Who song about the isolation of someone others see as the villain — carrying pain and vengeance nobody can see, misunderstood and unable to show his suffering.","src":"haiku","fable":"The cover keeps The Who's self-portrait of a resentful man whose worst self stays hidden behind an innocent-looking face, but drops the original's quiet, pleading second half. Where Townshend's narrator asked to be caught and steadied before his rage broke loose, this version stays locked in the accusation, its unfreed vengeance never counterweighted by any bid for restraint. That imbalance is sealed by the spelled-out band-name chant grafted into the bridge, which converts a confession of shame into an act of self-branding: the mask that the song is about becomes the mask the performance actually wears, the wounded loner reframed as a pose to be claimed rather than a secret to be caught out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The bridge splices in a Speak & Spell voice spelling out the band's name; Halle Berry starred in the Gothika tie-in video. Townshend went on record twice — tearful goosebumps in 2004, then a year later: an embarrassing effort that discredits a fine song.","themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"the wound kept sealed where the original asked to be caught and steadied","built":"the band-name chant grafted into the bridge converts a shame confession into a brand claim"},
"limp-bizkit~boiler":{"haiku":"A hurt lover delivers an ultimatum after being betrayed and mistreated, declaring they must walk away and will never return. They catalog their dependency and suffering, then cut ties completely.","opus":"After being hurt by someone, the speaker resolves to rely only on himself, declares he is done giving to and depending on that person, and vows never to come back.","sonnet":"The narrator severs ties with someone who hurt him, declaring that depending on them and giving to them is finished, and that he intends to handle everything himself from now on rather than continue in a damaging relationship.","src":"haiku","fable":"The mask is bravado, the underside is a breakup. The verses talk the swagger of self-reliance — do-it-yourself, foot down, a crude ascent from hell that becomes a claim of hard-won clarity — but every boast is a wound dressed as a lesson, the chorus dropping the act to ask, plainly and twice over, why two people ever met to hurt each other. The bridge is where the boiler actually blows: the self-help cadence collapses into a stripped anaphora of renunciation, depending and giving and hiding all declared done, no eating or sleeping or living left, so that the vaunted self-sufficiency reads as a person hollowed out rather than freed. The outro names the trick outright — an inner lie you were invited to sit inside — turning the whole strut into a confession that the toughness was the disguise.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"About being played and left, per the band's circle; the Lisbon-shot Dave Meyers video got itself banned from MTV.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"self-sufficiency revealed as the hollowing-out left by a breakup still in progress","built":"the outro names the toughness an inner lie, turning the whole strut into a confession"},
"limp-bizkit~break-stuff":{"haiku":"On days when everything is frustrating and everyone disappoints, the speaker feels dangerous and violent urges bubble up.","opus":"The nu-metal id-scream distilled: a bad day metastasizing into pure rage, where petty grievance and gender-war resentment get vented as cartoonish violence. It works because it names the ugly, wordless urge to smash something rather than dressing it up.","sonnet":"A day of pure uncontainable rage — everything is wrong, everyone is irritating, and the narrator is one slight away from violence. The song is a cathartic tour through the fantasy of annihilating everyone and everything in reach, grounded in the very ordinary experience of a day when you wake up already furious.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rage here is a costume pulled over plain weariness, and the reading sits in that seam: the verses keep insisting nothing is wrong beyond a rotten mood, which the narrator swells into menace because destruction is the only instrument he can name. The bridge briefly sheds the swagger to concede that this hollow, kicked-around state is something everyone recognizes, and at that moment the menace turns transparent, exposing itself as a request for release, a plea for any object at all to smash. His climb from a split lip toward wrecking a face was never truly aimed at the victim; it is someone starved of language for despair grabbing the crude, deafening one within reach.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The skatepark video — Snoop Dogg, Dr. Dre, Eminem with Hailie, Jonathan Davis, Pauly Shore — won MTV's Best Rock Video in 2000; at Woodstock '99 the song became the flashpoint of the riots, a link Durst has always rejected.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance"],"means":"destruction named as the only available vocabulary for despair","built":"the bridge drops swagger to concede the menace was always a request for release"},
"limp-bizkit~bring-it-back":{"fable":"The song's braggadocio works as a two-front assault: the verses aim outward with cartoon-violent battle-rap threats, while the hook flips the aggression into a plea, begging to be handed the riff, the drug, the record — as if the music itself were the weapon the speaker can't source on his own. What looks like dominance is actually dependence, the demand for a fix. The second verse names this plainly, reaching back to the previous decade and insisting on longevity in a scene that has moved on, so the title's command to bring it back is less a party call than a refusal to be past tense.","fableDeep":"Nostalgia staged as a fistfight: the chorus doesn't demand new music, it demands *back* — a band ordering the return of its own era in imperative case. The modern tell is the Wikipedia line ('Imma say what I want, you can look it up, Wikipedia probably gonna fuck it up') — legacy anxiety in its 2012 form, worrying about who writes the record of the men who made records. 'Most people never last this long' quietly swaps the subject: the boast is really about survival past your cultural moment. The gore talk is genre costume (the Slayer namecheck knows it); the fear of being a them-90's-things museum piece is the real engine.","haiku":"A nostalgic celebration of 1990s rock music and party culture, with the band asserting their lasting power. The song is a callback to earlier hits and an invitation to relive that era.","opus":"A brash hype anthem demanding the music, riffs, and beats to drive people insane, boasting of dominance and threatening rivals with violent metaphors.","sonnet":"A boast-heavy performance track in which the narrator claims creative dominance, demands a hard riff and a crowd gone wild, and nostalgically invokes the 90s nu-metal era while asserting that no one else has matched its energy.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"dominance unmasked as dependence, the battle-rap as a craving for a fix","built":"the title command doubles as party call and refusal to be made past tense"},
"limp-bizkit~build-a-bridge":{"haiku":"The speaker persistently builds emotional bridges to an absent lover despite inevitable collapse, choosing loyalty and heartbreak over abandonment.","opus":"A vow of stubborn devotion, building a bridge to a lover's mind and heart out of memories and pain even knowing it will crumble; the deeper read is a plea to be waited for, promising to keep rebuilding until death no matter how many times it collapses.","sonnet":"The narrator keeps constructing emotional connections toward someone who won't come back — bridges of memory, of pain, of longing — knowing they'll crumble but unable to stop building until they die.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's title metaphor conflicts with itself and that contradiction is the whole read: a bridge is meant to reach someone, but Fred Durst keeps signing on to build one he already knows will collapse, so the labor is aimed less at arrival than at proving devotion through futility. The escalating materials trace the shift — first a bridge to the mind, then one stretched across oceans, then one made of pain itself, each grander span quietly conceding that the earlier one didn't hold. What sounds like a love pledge is really an oath to keep working past any hope of the structure standing, until the bridge stops being a means to the other person and becomes the speaker's identity, the one thing he'll do until he dies. The stripped bridge section, reduced to a bare plea to wait, drops the whole construction conceit to expose what was underneath it all along: not an engineer but someone terrified of being left behind while he builds.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"devotion pledged through labor the speaker already knows cannot hold","built":"escalating materials — mind, ocean, pain — each quietly concede the last span failed"},
"limp-bizkit~clunk":{"haiku":"Ego and arrogance corroding self and relationships; the singer criticizes someone inflated with pride who seeks validation through blame and refuses accountability, headed toward destruction.","opus":"A boastful takedown of a conceited, self-deluded phony with a bloated ego who faults everyone else and coasts on unearned regard; the spoken outro recasts the band's noise as the sound of pain and irritation, kids straining so hard to imitate their idols that they simply crank the volume higher.","sonnet":"A takedown of an egomaniac who expects the world to owe him something — the narrator diagnoses the target's inflated self-image, predicts his crash, and delivers the verdict that distortion and frustration are what happens when you want more than you can pull off.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grudge track aimed at a leeching ex-friend whose ego has outgrown any actual standing — Durst catalogs the entitlement and the everybody-owes-you whine, then spends the bridge staging something between an intervention and a threat while the target drifts on oblivious, a clown in a force field with nobody left around him. The title is just the sound of impact, repeated until it works as the argument. Then the found-audio outro flips the frame: a soft-spoken interview about distortion as vented frustration — playing to please yourself, no reservations or politeness — lands as an accidental thesis for the whole record, recasting the rage as a crude form of honesty rather than a simple beatdown.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty","artifice & performance"],"means":"making the song itself the weapon rather than staging any physical confrontation","built":"the found-audio outro recasts the beatdown as an accidental thesis about distortion as honest venting"},
"limp-bizkit~counterfeit":{"haiku":"A confrontation with someone who's adopted a false identity and mimics the speaker's style. The song denounces counterfeit behavior and exposes the faker's betrayal of trust.","opus":"A scornful takedown of an imitator who copies the speaker's style, culture, and even appearance while hiding behind a fake persona, dismissed repeatedly as a phony and a counterfeit.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone accused of wholesale fakery — stealing another's style, riding trends, and stabbing former friends in the back while hiding behind a false identity.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two verses of generic poser-hunting — the trend-chasing clone who fakes a whole style because he hates his own — sharpen in the third into something personal: one specific friend who lifted his appearance straight off Durst's own circle and repaid an open door with betrayal, which is what the counterfeit mask was covering all along. From there the song stops arguing and starts chanting, hammering the word fake dozens of times until denunciation becomes exorcism. History supplied the punchline: this authenticity anthem is the same single Interscope later paid a Portland radio station to spin fifty times, briefly making Counterfeit the band's own scandal about buying what you claim to be.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"denunciation hammered into exorcism through sheer repetition","built":"the band's own paid-airplay scandal makes them the counterfeit the song prosecutes","fnote":"Aimed at the scene posers who copied the band once they rose — an irony sharpened when Interscope paid Portland's KUFO five grand to spin it fifty times, sparking payola accusations.","fnote2":"Produced by Ross Robinson, who only heard the demo because Korn's Fieldy pushed it on him; the title rides the queer-as-a-three-dollar-bill idiom for the obviously phony, seeded by a line in Sour."},
"limp-bizkit~counterfeit-album-version-explicit":{"haiku":"False masks and identity exposure; the speaker sees through counterfeit personas.","opus":"An early Limp Bizkit screed against a fake, a poser accused of stealing style, identity, and even appearance from Durst's circle. Beneath the funk-metal aggression sits a real anxiety about authenticity in a scene full of imitators, with Durst positioning himself as an old-school original betrayed by a copycat he once trusted.","sonnet":"A direct address to a shapeshifter who has stolen appearance, culture, and friendship, only to wear them as a mask — the speaker calls out the identity crisis, the trend-chasing, and the backstab, reducing the target to a single word that circles back relentlessly: fake.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~crack-addict":{"haiku":"Aggressive masculinity and violent confrontation; addiction to conflict and domination as defining characteristic.","opus":"A brash rap-rock provocation cast as a wake-up call to fix supposedly ruined music, the narrator declaring himself addicted to cracking skulls when punks talk static, all countdowns, riffs and swaggering threat. It is bravado as content. Limp Bizkit, the Jacksonville band who became nu-metal's most divisive lightning rod, deliver exactly the loud, taunting, party-riot energy that made Fred Durst both a phenomenon and a punchline.","sonnet":"A confrontational set piece where the band declares itself the corrective force against fake music and posturing — the energy is combative and self-mythologizing, promising a skull-cracking response to anyone who starts trouble. The addiction in the title is to aggression itself, reframed as righteous purging of the inauthentic.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~creamer-radio-is-dead":{"haiku":"Nostalgia for simpler times before degradation of culture and personal decay.","opus":"A braggadocio track of street-tough posturing and nouveau-riche flexing, taunting a wannabe while declaring 'all radio is dead,' laced with wistful glances back at carefree younger days before the clouds rolled in.","sonnet":"A bravado-driven rant that mixes street confrontation with nostalgic longing for simpler good times, threaded through with the refrain that radio — and by extension, the mainstream that once carried such energy — is dead.","src":"haiku","fable":"A battle-rap flex whose title carries the whole thesis: the taunt cycles through insult, wealth-boast, and a threat that turns the emergency line into a punchline, yet the hook keeps interrupting to declare the airwaves finished, so the aggression lands on a business gone deaf rather than the boy squared up in front of him. Beneath the strut runs a colder current, the bridge repeatedly pining for an earlier, easier stretch of life before a cloud drifts across it. Verse three name-checks a Cobain melody said to soothe hurt just before conceding how suddenly any of it can stop, letting mortality bleed through the peacocking. The mask is the untouchable champion; what it conceals is a man pronouncing his own medium gone and half-grieving it, until the closing warp of the refrain slurs the boast into its own rot.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","nostalgia & memory","death & grief"],"means":"a champion's boast aimed at a medium already declared dead and half-mourned","built":"the closing slur of the refrain curdles the boast into the rot it was announcing"},
"limp-bizkit~dad-vibes":{"haiku":"A father figure returns with exaggerated swagger and rhymes about his cool dad persona, reclaiming his presence in a playful, boastful way.","opus":"Long past nu-metal's late-nineties heyday, Limp Bizkit dive headlong into self-parody, casting the aging frontman as a preposterously boastful middle-aged stud loaded with cartoonish swagger. The bragging is intentionally ridiculous, an old-timer gleefully owning his years, and the refrain about being impossible to live with or without plays the whole thing as a knowing wink rather than a genuine flex.","sonnet":"A self-styled father figure struts through a celebration of his own irreverence, flipping the midlife stereotype into swagger. All contradiction — presence and absence, cool and corny — the dad persona becomes a comedic brand, bouncing through the room with undeniable and deliberately absurd confidence.","src":"haiku","fable":"The dad here is a getup, not a confession: the track fits middle age into the borrowed strut of old-school rap boasting, playing the coolest-man-in-the-room stance as a joke the singer is entirely in on. The comparisons keep escalating, a boast machine flexing on empty long after there's anything left to flex about, one likeness sanded down to nickname-level sweetness. That pose thins where the chorus staples family fondness and fatigue to a bounce-back franchise, a tiny mock-mourning tag tucked under the youthful-idol swagger, betraying the real subject: aging inside a pose that worships being young. The bridge argues it without words, a nursery singsong that finally curdles into weary resignation, the sound of a man who knows the whole bit is a bit and runs it anyway.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Premiered at Lollapalooza 2021 under Durst's gray-wig-and-horseshoe-mustache makeover — the look went viral before the single dropped that September.","themes":["artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay","family & blood"],"means":"aging performed inside a pose that worships youth until the knowing pose admits itself","built":"the bridge nursery singsong curdles into resignation, the sound of a man running a bit he sees through"},
"limp-bizkit~dirty-rotten-bizkit":{"haiku":"A loud, crude celebration of raw sexual energy and the need for uninhibited physical gratification.","opus":"Late-period Fred Durst leaning fully into self-parody, the band's own name a chant of grimy, ridiculous swagger. It's pure appetite and provocation, a knowing joke about craving something loud and filthy with no pretense of meaning.","sonnet":"A raw demand for gratification and intensity — the narrator wants something visceral, loud, and hard delivered right now, framing it as a mutual need rather than one-sided hunger.","src":"haiku","fable":"The stutter that names the band is doing the real work: the title is an insult turned into a self-baptism, Fred Durst answering the culture that wrote Limp Bizkit off as dirty and rotten by wearing the slur as an appetite. What reads as a demand for something hard and loud, delivered right now, is deliberately built to sound like a taunt about sex or dominance while actually being about the music and the audience — a craving to be given the very noise the singer also promises to give back. The bridge quietly drops the swagger to admit the wanting is mutual and that he is no better than the listener, so the closing wink about what the offer was really promising lets the double entendre collapse on purpose, exposing the whole strut as a knowing pose over ordinary need.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"a slur worn as appetite until the strut collapses into ordinary mutual need","built":"the title stutter is a self-baptism, the band answering the culture that wrote them off"},
"limp-bizkit~don-t-change":{"haiku":"The narrator refuses to compromise authenticity despite pressure, insisting on stable presence as resistance against external demands for transformation.","opus":"A cover of the INXS song turned into a plea against changing yourself for anyone, finding hard-won peace and a recovered love. Under the rap-rock exterior it's a surprisingly steady statement of self-acceptance.","sonnet":"After a period of darkness, the speaker finds renewed love and clarity, and insists to a partner that they should remain exactly as they are — no transformation is needed or wanted, only presence.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's serenity is willed rather than given: the ground held and the sky refusing to fall are reassurances repeated because something dark has just been survived, and each verse pivots from that darkness toward a hard-won steadiness. The refusal to change anything, for you or for me, reads less as stubbornness than as a lover's vow to protect a happiness only just recovered, its bitterness now spoken aloud and set down. Performed straight, the cover keeps that ache intact, trusting the original's mix of tenderness and resolve without ironizing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"A faithful INXS cover, widely singled out as the record's tenderest moment.","themes":["love & desire"],"means":"a vow not to change as a lover's protection over a happiness only just recovered","built":"the cover performed straight keeps the original's ache intact, bitterness spoken aloud and set down"},
"limp-bizkit~don-t-go-off-wandering":{"haiku":"A person stressed by someone testing and mentally abusing him realizes she feels nothing. His insistence on her feeling him fails as she uses him then moves on cold.","opus":"The misery of one-sided love, the narrator obsessing over a woman who feels nothing for him and who toys with him precisely because he's within reach. Limp Bizkit lean into the crude humiliation of unrequited need, leaving him stranded and pathetic, clinging to her past claim that she wanted him while everything he had slips away.","sonnet":"Obsessing over someone who is completely emotionally unavailable — the narrator knows she only wants what she can't have, sees how his humility has cost him everything, and is left holding nothing while she feels absolutely nothing at all.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song frames itself as a complaint against a partner, but the recurring charge that she is emotionally vacant reads as displacement: the pressure, the flight from what is real, the sense of failing at everything trace back to the speaker, who exports his own hollowness rather than claim it. The escalation tells on him, moving from a wish to matter toward a controlling demand that she register his presence immediately, the appeal hardening into a threat that exposes how powerless he truly is. When it finally breaks into a repeated stammer about her once having needed him, the bravado dissolves entirely, and the aggression of the vocal shows itself as armor over someone unable to sit with being the abandoned party.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","alienation & emptiness","surveillance & control"],"means":"the partner's vacancy named as displacement for hollowness the speaker cannot claim","built":"escalation from a wish to matter into a controlling demand exposes the powerlessness it was masking"},
"limp-bizkit~douche-bag":{"haiku":"Aggressive call-out of arrogance and hollow posturing. The song warns that unearned confidence invites confrontation and that invincibility is an illusion reality will shatter.","opus":"An aggressive taunt aimed at a boastful, cowardly rival, mocking his lack of brains and toughness while threatening violence. The narrator promises to blow him away and leave him to lie in the bed he made.","sonnet":"A direct, confrontational address to someone who talks big but delivers nothing, warning them that their false invincibility will meet a harsh reality. The song is an extended taunt aimed at a person the speaker considers all posture and no substance.","src":"haiku","fable":"A battle-rap kiss-off whose one nominal target keeps dissolving into the aggressor's own reflection: the taunts about crying over spilled milk and playing at being hard describe someone performing a toughness the song then out-performs, so the diss and the self-portrait share a face. Structurally the meaning lives in the gap between verse and hook—the verses do the actual dismantling with wit and rhyme, while the hook collapses into a single slur and a repeated threat, as if language exhausts itself into pure percussive assault the moment argument stops. The refusal to develop that hook is the point: it reads not as a failure of vocabulary but as the sound of talk giving way to the play the bridge keeps demanding, the moment past critical where nothing is left to say and only the blow remains.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"the diss and the self-portrait share a face the speaker never acknowledges","built":"the hook's arrested development enacts language giving way to the blow it kept threatening"},
"limp-bizkit~down-another-day":{"haiku":"Seasons change and inevitably bring endings, as the speaker watches summer fade and cycles return. Despite repeated farewells and the return of people, the fundamental pattern of seasonal loss persists.","opus":"Using the turn from summer to winter as metaphor, it's about accepting cyclical loss and the constant goodbyes of a relationship that keeps returning; the deeper read is reassurance amid impermanence, embracing the cold seasons as a way to eventually be set free while she inevitably comes back.","sonnet":"Using the cycling of seasons as a metaphor for a cyclical relationship, the narrator watches summer — and by extension, a woman he loves — come and go repeatedly, oscillating between sad acceptance of change and the faint reassurance that she always returns.","src":"haiku","fable":"Fred Durst maps a breakup entirely onto the seasonal cycle, so that the loss of a woman and the loss of summer become the same event, felt as a slow descent rather than a single rupture. The genius of the structure is the flipped valence: the speaker claims to adore the winter, the very cold he first dreaded, because the turning of seasons is exactly what promises she'll circle back — and the bridge confirms this as a closed loop, each goodbye guaranteeing a return, each return guaranteeing another goodbye. Set against this, the chorus's bracketed reassurance that he'll never let her down reads as the mask: a devotion the cyclical structure has already exposed as compulsion, a man who keeps sinking through the same day because he cannot let the leaving be final.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"seasonal return converted from dread into proof she must circle back","built":"the flipped valence of winter — first dreaded then adored — exposes devotion as compulsion"},
"limp-bizkit~drown":{"haiku":"A person drowns in sorrow and abuse, seeking rescue before desperation overwhelms them; they reflect on conquered hardship and loyal support.","opus":"Limp Bizkit, the nu-metal juggernauts more often associated with brash aggression, turn unexpectedly reflective here. Facing what feels like the end, the narrator looks back with a smile at the bumps conquered on his road, toasting the angels who lingered. The repeated cry to be saved before drowning sits beside a fragile admission of feeling like a fool, softened by pleas for forgiveness.","sonnet":"A man on the edge of being overwhelmed looks back at a road that was worth travelling and forward to an uncertain end, calling out for rescue before he goes under. Gratitude and dread braid together into something that reads less like collapse than a stumbling toast.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~eat-you-alive":{"haiku":"Raw attraction and desire overpower a person, becoming an inescapable compulsion that consumes their thoughts.","opus":"Durst's obsessive fixation on a woman who wants nothing to do with him, reframing rejection as license and desire as predation. It's the ugly entitlement at nu-metal's core made explicit, mistaking possessiveness for passion.","sonnet":"The narrator is overwhelmed by physical attraction to a woman who ignores him, obsessively describing the pull she has on him while insisting there is nothing wrong with wanting someone who wants nothing to do with you.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song addresses a woman whose name the speaker does not know and whose refusal he simply overrides, and its real subject is the entitlement that dresses obsession up as attraction — the hook about a beast roused within him is not seduction but a threat wearing appetite as its metaphor. Durst keeps insisting there is no fault in his wanting, that being a man licenses whatever he thinks, and the read has to hold that mask against what leaks under it: the woman is far above sparing him a glance, wants nothing to do with him, and the response is to promise to consume her anyway. The apology of the bridge is hollow by design, sorry only that her beauty drives him insane, so that even remorse curdles back into blame. What the track actually documents, whether or not it means to, is the logic of the stalker who reframes a person's disinterest as something done to him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"obsession dressed as attraction reframing a woman's refusal as harm done to him","built":"the bridge apology curdles back into blame, leaving remorse as cover for entitlement","fnote":"Fan lore says Britney Spears or Angelina Jolie; Durst only ever framed it as a working-class man's obsession with an unattainable woman. The video: Durst abducts Thora Birch into the woods while Bill Paxton gives chase."},
"limp-bizkit~endless-slaughter":{"haiku":"Endless violence and death consume the world as people lose their humanity and minds. Life becomes brutal warfare where people are destroyed, and survival is uncertain.","opus":"A lurching mix of confession and taunt from a narrator marked an outcast in boyhood, insisting these days that he relishes it, tumbling through grotesque, unstable images. A refrain of seized control marks the shift from being dominated to dominating. It casts existence as combat, with the young trained toward violence.","sonnet":"Dissociation and inherited damage collide in a torrent of barely-contained rage. The narrator, convinced they are a loser by lifelong conditioning, lurches between self-mockery and genuine menace — the world a battlefield where children are taught to kill and the speaker refuses to stay under anyone.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~everything":{"haiku":"The narrator is frustrated and sick from internal conflict; they have everything yet nothing satisfies them.","opus":"Limp Bizkit vent raw frustration at a partner pushing him to become someone he isn't, torn between exploding at her and shrugging it off with a laugh. Rooted in late-90s nu-metal angst, the band contrasts her demand for more with his insistence that he already possesses all he needs. The bitter closing line keeps asking why things had to collapse the way they did.","sonnet":"Frustration boils over at being expected to change into something else for someone else's satisfaction. The speaker insists he already has everything he needs; the demand to become what others want is the real problem. Rage and bewilderment collide at the end of a relationship where the pressure to be different felt more unbearable than the loss itself.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~faith":{"haiku":"A song about needing emotional space after being hurt in a previous relationship. The speaker demands faith in his ability to overcome heartbreak but must guard his heart carefully.","opus":"A cover about needing to guard one's heart after being hurt, resisting a lover's plea to stay because love without devotion isn't enough, and insisting on faith while showing the other person the door.","sonnet":"A cover of George Michael's song: a guarded speaker who has been hurt before refuses to give his heart away again without caution, insisting he must have faith before committing to a new relationship.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cover of George Michael's 1987 hit that keeps every word and inverts every intention: the original is a poised, almost courtly decision to walk away from sex without devotion, and Durst delivers it first as smirking lounge mimicry, then detonates it into scratching, screamed nu-metal. The desecration is the point — this was the breakout single that put a Jacksonville club band on the map by mauling a pop icon — yet the structure tells on itself, because the song still needs Michael's actual argument about self-respect to land, right down to showing a manipulative lover the door. Michael reportedly loathed the cover, but its whisper-to-roar build reads less like parody than the same refusal with the patience and politeness stripped out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","love & desire","anger & defiance"],"means":"the desecration still needing the original's ethics about self-respect to land","built":"the whisper-to-roar build enacts the same refusal with patience and politeness stripped out","fnote":"Ross Robinson fought to keep the George Michael cover off the record, and Michael reportedly hated the result — but its 1998 MTV rotation is what actually broke the band."},
"limp-bizkit~full-nelson":{"haiku":"A complaint about being constantly mocked and talked about behind one's back. The narrator warns that insults backed only by big talk will get met with violence.","opus":"Durst's grievance anthem about feeling persecuted and misunderstood, spoiling for a fight with everyone talking behind his back. It swings between wanting to burn it all down and a hollow gesture at getting along, all posture and thin-skinned bravado.","sonnet":"A response to people who talk behind someone's back, directed at those who spread false rumors without knowing the target personally. The speaker demands that any insults be said face to face, threatening violent consequences for those whose words exceed their ability to back them up.","src":"haiku","fable":"A schoolyard grievance scaled up to a wrestling hold, aimed at the anonymous gossip who runs his mouth about a man he's never met. The engine is the fantasy of finally cornering that talker and forcing him to repeat it at arm's length, with the hook treating loud boasts as a bill the body will collect in full. What saves it from pure posturing is the deflation between rounds, where the menace briefly softens into a near-sincere wish for peace before Durst shoves that exhaustion aside. The bridge then widens the target from one loudmouth to the smug operators building themselves an empire and leaving everyone else outside it, and the closing chant to torch the building lands less as a threat than as the picked-on kid discovering he's the one holding the flame.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"picked-on kid discovering he is the one holding the flame","built":"bridge widens target from one loudmouth to empire-builders, scaling the schoolyard to a class war"},
"limp-bizkit~get-a-life":{"haiku":"An aggressive boast from a rapper establishing dominance over detractors and rivals. The speaker celebrates his rise from humble origins and demands respect, dismissing those who envy his success.","opus":"A boastful, confrontational rap in which the narrator brags about his toughness, drinking, women, and North Carolina roots while taunting rivals and jealous haters to back off.","sonnet":"A boastful declaration of self-made toughness and disdain for haters, delivered by a rapper who frames his rise from a Carolina redneck background as proof that the jealousy of others is irrelevant.","src":"haiku","fable":"The braggadocio runs on a single pun that organizes everything: Fred Durst as Polar Bear, and so the boasts arrive as cold—Siberian flow, ice in the drinks, a predator loitering in a tank—which lets a scared threat pose as unbothered chill. Underneath the knife-waving hook and the redneck-made-good origin story sits the song's real engine, the couplet that keeps circling back to being hated precisely because he made it; the aggression is defensive, a man rehearsing his success at the people who resent it. The bridge tips the mask when it drops the swagger to say plainly not to let the world drag you down, then buries that vulnerability under drugged-out muttering—so the toughness reads as armor over someone taking the hating personally. The Russian outro seals the joke: a woman coos that his rarity is too good to be true, one gold cobra in all the world, which turns the whole strut into an advertisement he half-knows nobody's buying.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The closing Russian skit is Wes Borland and a Russian-speaking woman, riffing on the one-of-a-kind gold cobra.","themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"cold-weather braggadocio worn as armor against being hated for making it","built":"the Russian coda turns the whole strut into an ad the speaker half-knows no one is buying"},
"limp-bizkit~getcha-groove-on":{"haiku":"A rapper and band deliver energy, control, and authenticity through high-tech performance. They demand people engage and keep their groove authentic without dilution.","opus":"A rowdy Limp Bizkit posse cut with Xzibit, stacking party hype and rap-battle threats over demands that the crowd lose itself in the beat. Cutting through the boasts about skill and dominance is a startling admission that the narrator is a wreck at every waking moment, hinting the swagger is armor over real unrest.","sonnet":"A crowd-hype track that's also a mild territorial posturing — the narrator is in a bad mood, daring people to provoke him, but mostly it's an invitation to get the groove going and stop holding up the show.","src":"haiku","fable":"A posse cut assembled almost entirely from the vocabulary of threat and turf, where the chorus's order to loosen up functions less as an invitation than as an eviction: the one offense the song recognizes is lingering in the room without yielding it. Under the bravado each verse keeps sliding domination into something intimate, Durst promising sweetness while lodging another fragment of himself inside whoever is listening, so the welcome arrives as coercion wearing a smile, possession costumed as a gift. By the later verses the crowd gives way to the balance sheet, the two rappers dividing up a tech fortune, until the flex and the shakedown reveal themselves as one motion. The floor you are commanded to relax on is ground they have already declared theirs.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Xzibit features; the track later drew a lawsuit over an uncleared sample.","themes":["money & the street","anger & defiance","love & desire"],"means":"welcome and possession delivered as one coercive motion wearing a smile","built":"the order to loosen up functions as an eviction notice for whoever hasn't yielded the room"},
"limp-bizkit~gimme-the-mic":{"haiku":"A boastful rapper asserts dominance, attacking posers and mediocre competitors; they're a 'microphone fiend' ready to blow away everyone else on the stage.","opus":"A pure battle-rap flex, Durst positioning himself against manufactured imitators and radio sameness, demanding the microphone to prove he outclasses the poseurs. It is aggression as sport, built on the old microphone-fiend lineage of hip-hop bravado.","sonnet":"The speaker positions himself as a genuine MC who despises posers, corporate radio, and watered-down music, demanding the microphone to demonstrate superiority. It is an aggressive assertion of authenticity and territorial dominance within the rap world.","src":"haiku","fable":"The swagger is a disguise with nobody underneath it, since each verse dangles admission to some realm it refuses to describe, the withheld prize existing only because the addict's real craving is the microphone alone. Direction emerges from where the menace turns: the opening stretches jab at imitators and frauds, while the closing widening aims instead at broadcasts recycling identical acts on endless repeat, exposing the true motor as revulsion at repetition rather than any message worth delivering. When the chant fractures the word for that craving into stammered syllables until nothing survives but raw wanting untethered to any goal, it lays bare the honesty the posturing keeps orbiting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance"],"means":"the chant splinters a craving into syllables until only the wanting remains","built":"the word for the craving stammered apart until nothing survives but raw sound"},
"limp-bizkit~gold-cobra":{"haiku":"Arrogant assertion of superiority and dominance over rivals. The narrator dismisses all competition and claims sole kingship through braggadocio.","opus":"A boastful, aggressive flex about hard living, wealth and dominance, waking up irritated and threatening rivals who cross him. The narrator crowns himself the one king on the hill, indifferent to what anyone thinks.","sonnet":"A boastful rap-rock declaration of dominance and indifference to critics, positioning the speaker as the sole king on the hill who holds the \"gold\" and owes nothing to anyone. The lyrics cycle through aggression, bravado, and contempt for anyone who challenges his position.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the mask of pure alpha-dog braggadocio, its whole engine a status boast: one man crowned above everyone below him, the rest reduced to dice waiting to be gutted. The gold cobra is the emblem it keeps striking, a coiled and venomous ornament that fuses wealth and threat into one glinting thing worn by a man who claims he cares nothing for anyone's opinion. But the swagger runs so relentless and undifferentiated, a machine-gun rhythm of rhymed contempt landing on no specific target, that the refusal to care becomes the tell. The more he insists no knock at the door can touch him, the more the boast reads as a wall built against something, the man in black packing heat because that door really might open. Underneath the peacocking is defensiveness dressed as invincibility, the chorus chanting the treasure's name like a talisman held up precisely because holding it is all he has left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote2":"Released June 2011 — the reunited classic lineup's first album since Chocolate Starfish and their last for Interscope; per Borland the title means nothing at all, it just fit.","themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance","money & the street"],"means":"invincibility performed so relentlessly it exposes the wound it guards","built":"the talisman name chanted in the chorus holds the boast and the fear in one struck pose"},
"limp-bizkit~head-for-the-barricade":{"haiku":"When aggression ignites and confrontation looms, the narrator prepares to fight back against those who belittle and provoke them.","opus":"Durst turns picked-on grievance into a call to arms, invoking rage barely chained and even name-checking Columbine as its dark reference point. Nu-metal's persecution complex staged as a fight you're dared to join.","sonnet":"A call to fight back against everything grinding you down — social humiliation, ambient hostility, the world's general noise — framed as adrenaline-fueled confrontation rather than passivity, drawing the line and heading to the front.","src":"haiku","fable":"The chorus command to head for the barricade turns on a deliberate ambiguity: a barricade is what you charge in an uprising and what you take cover behind, so Durst pitches the song as insurrection and self-defense at once, riot-starter and bunker-builder in the same breath. That doubleness runs through the verses, where the fighter's bravado keeps qualifying itself — a warning about recognizing the line before it's crossed, a chained-off chamber in the brain he begs no one to rattle loose — so the aggression is staged as something barely held rather than freely chosen. The most telling move is the retreat into headphones and someone else's record while the world turns his stomach: the tough guy's real defense isn't the fist but the withdrawal, the barricade as a wall to disappear behind. Under the pit-chant mask of a fight anthem sits a portrait of someone cornered and hated who has decided that hostility must mean he's doing something right — the swagger reads less as strength than as the only posture left to a person who feels perpetually under attack.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"riot-chant as cover for someone cornered who takes hostility as proof he matters","built":"barricade as both charge-target and bunker enacts the song's split aggression"},
"limp-bizkit~hold-on":{"haiku":"A person holds on to an unrequited obsession while being emotionally drained and left behind by someone who will never need them.","opus":"An uncharacteristically vulnerable Durst caught in a one-sided love, still adoring someone who's leaving and never needed him. Holding on and letting go blur into the same slow bleeding of the soul.","sonnet":"A one-sided love where the narrator is devoted to someone who has already checked out — the longing is total but unreciprocated, and all he can do is watch her drain his soul as she leaves.","src":"haiku","fable":"Weiland's guest voice threading through a Durst project doubles the ache, two performers circling the same wound without ever resolving into a genuine duet, so the collaboration itself echoes the estrangement the words describe. The refrain performs a quiet grammatical betrayal, turning what should sound like steadfast devotion into a formula for departure, so that gripping tighter becomes the very machinery of loss and being cherished gets recast as being slowly emptied. A picture kept on the wall lingers as the one held image, already fading into absence. By the close the language erodes down to the two-syllable plea alone, chanted with nothing left to grasp, the call for endurance becoming the sound of a grip that persists long after it has stopped meaning anything.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Scott Weiland co-writes and duets — his second guest turn with the band, on a record he also helped produce in places.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"devotion's formula quietly rewired so holding tighter becomes the machinery of loss","built":"the two-performer collaboration enacts the estrangement it describes, never resolving into a duet"},
"limp-bizkit~hot-dog":{"haiku":"Everything is fucked and absurd—society, people, institutions, sex, life—and the artist can't even say 'fuck' freely without criticism, all while living in this broken system.","opus":"A nihilistic rant that everything is fucked up, counting its own profanity as a joke while listing society's rot from bad parents to corrupt cops. Woven through is a hostile riff on Nine Inch Nails' Closer, twisting sexual submission into a taunt that nothing anyone does gets close to him.","sonnet":"A profanity-soaked rant cataloguing every broken, corrupt, and ugly thing in American life, built around the word \"fuck\" as a defiant refusal of censorship and social pretense.","src":"haiku","fable":"Durst builds the whole track from a single obscenity, repeating it until he pauses to tally the running total into the mid-forties, and that count is the giveaway: the swearing isn't fury but an accountant's boredom. Every noun he lists arrives already ruined, so the word loses its charge and turns into plain punctuation marking off a world he finds uniformly damaged. Beneath the bravado, the chorus lifts lines from Trent Reznor's catalogue and inverts their raw ache into a boast, insisting no effort on your part will ever reach him. The two moods rhyme, because a man who has leveled everything into identical wreckage is also declaring himself untouchable, and the relentless cursing is the moat guarding that claim.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Reworks Nine Inch Nails' Closer with Reznor's blessing and a songwriting credit — an odd truce, given Reznor had spent the preceding stretch publicly mocking Durst.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"obscenity tallied like an accountant until it loses its charge","built":"the borrowed NIN chorus flips raw ache into a boast by inversion"},
"limp-bizkit~i-ll-be-ok":{"haiku":"Departure destroys the speaker into suicidal despair; escape and distance become the only viable survival strategy.","opus":"A raw early Durst breakup wail—a goodbye letter leaves him suicidal and furious, blaming her for wrecking his whole life. Yet the refrain clings to escape as survival: if he can get away, he'll be okay, imagining a better place to start over.","sonnet":"A suicidal-ideation breakup song — the speaker has received a goodbye letter, feels like their entire life is over, explicitly fantasizes about dying for or because of this person, and ultimately resolves to leave, telling themselves it will be okay while clearly not believing it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The comfort in the title is a decoy the song can't sustain, since each promise that he'll be fine rides against verses of wanting to die over the one who's walking out. His steadiness depends on a getaway he only imagines, staring through glass at a world that has absorbed her, calling himself pathetic for a need he cannot set down. What guts him isn't her exit but the shame of clinging, and the closing terms he offers reveal it was never her he wanted so much as rescue from a wreckage he already inhabits.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","suicide"],"means":"the shame of clinging wounds deeper than the exit it follows","built":"the title's comfort is immediately undone by verses naming wanting to die"},
"limp-bizkit~i-m-broke":{"haiku":"A person is penniless after lending money to friends who refuse to repay, and demands they either return it or face violence.","opus":"A comedic, aggressive rant against freeloaders: after lending out all his money and going broke, the narrator threatens deadbeats who won't pay him back with violence, telling the bums to get a job and stop showing up looking for cash.","sonnet":"Fred Durst is furious at people who borrow money and never pay it back, escalating from complaint to threats as he watches the same parasites reappear every time his bank account recovers.","src":"haiku","fable":"The come-up is played as farce: a rap-metal shakedown where the speaker casts himself as put-upon creditor to hangers-on who drained his wallet, then reappear the moment funds arrive, threats climbing from a swat to a rifle while never leaving the register of cartoonish bluster. What the repetition exposes is that all the menace masks something small, since the entire grievance collapses into a demand to be repaid what was owed. The closing turn, where he rebrands the accusation and claims only the willfully blind mistake him for a layabout, reads as the wounded self-justification of someone who resents his own generosity and won't concede the loan was his choice.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","comedy & wordplay","anger & defiance"],"means":"a grievance about betrayal that collapses into wounded self-justification about a loan","built":"the escalating threat register stays cartoonish, undercutting the injury it advertises"},
"limp-bizkit~indigo-flow":{"haiku":"This is a spoken thank-you to collaborators and home. It celebrates community and gratitude without developing a single theme.","opus":"Not a narrative song but an extended shout-out track, thanking bandmates, crew, fellow musicians, and family by name after the band's breakthrough. The throughline is gratitude and awe at sudden success rising from an unknown hometown.","sonnet":"A shout-out track — an extended list of thanks and props to bandmates, collaborators, fellow artists, and crew from the Jacksonville scene and beyond. There is no narrative or theme beyond communal acknowledgment and loyalty.","src":"haiku","fable":"Not a song so much as the album's acknowledgments page set to a groove — a closing-credits roll call named for Indigo Ranch, the Malibu studio where Ross Robinson cut this debut, thanking Korn, Chino of Deftones, and the whole road crew by name. The move underneath the gratitude is a flex: by listing who already claims them as family, a band from a town nobody scouted writes itself into the nu-metal lineage in real time, and the list itself is the proof that Jacksonville is now on the map. It ends the only place a tour diary can — turning from the scene back to Dad, Momma, and God, coming home.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["friendship & loyalty","identity & becoming","family & blood"],"means":"a gratitude list functioning as a lineage claim — crediting the scene to enter it","built":"the credit roll pivots from nu-metal name-dropping to Dad, Momma, and God as the real origin point"},
"limp-bizkit~intro":{"haiku":"An energetic call-and-response announcement that Limp Bizkit is here and the moment is real—a brash introduction that sets the tone with repeated 'Who's in the house?' and 'Bring it on' attitude.","opus":"A hype introduction announcing the band's arrival, repeatedly asking who's in the house and answering that Limp Bizkit is.","sonnet":"A short hype-up intro track in which the band announces its own arrival and invites the crowd to respond, functioning as a live-concert-style call-and-response opener with no narrative content.","src":"haiku","fable":"A false start that only pretends to be a mission statement: the World Wide urgency and the not-a-test gravity are immediately spent on the flattest possible answer to who is here, the band simply naming itself in its own doorway. The frame arrives as pure arena-hype self-announcement with nothing behind the mask but the will to be the loudest name in the room.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote2":"The album title, per Wes Borland, was a late-night joke — Durst as the Chocolate Starfish, the asshole everyone calls him, fronting the Hot Dog Flavored Water.","themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a mission-statement frame spent entirely on self-naming","built":"the not-a-test urgency collapses before a single claim is made"},
"limp-bizkit~introbra":{"haiku":"An inescapable compulsion repeats endlessly, unmovable by effort or will. The cyclical refusal to stop persists regardless of desperate resistance.","opus":"A short intro built on one helpless admission, that something inside won't stop however hard he tries to master it, setting a tone of compulsion the record can't switch off.","src":"haiku","fable":"An overture built entirely from a refrain of helplessness, its short looping lines staging a compulsion the speaker can't shut off before the record has properly begun.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"compulsion staged as refrain before the album can start","built":"the intro form traps the speaker mid-loop before the first song exists"},
"limp-bizkit~just-drop-dead-non-lp-version":{"haiku":"A man confronts his girlfriend's infidelity, discovering her cheating with another man and demanding she accept responsibility for betraying his trust.","opus":"Limp Bizkit, Fred Durst's late-nineties rap-rock lightning rod, pour betrayed rage into a scorched kiss-off after the narrator learns his partner has been unfaithful. He tallies her deceptions, insists he was worth more, and orders her out of his life. Amid crude boasts and vulgar couplets he owns up to having trusted too freely, wounded pride hardening into abrasive, unfiltered venom.","sonnet":"Rage at infidelity delivered without emotional nuance: a man who endured repeated betrayals and manipulations discovers his partner has cheated again and detonates into contempt. The injury is real — years of one-sided effort, loneliness, and being played — even if the expression of it is maximally crude. The core is wounded pride demanding acknowledgment before it burns everything down.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~just-like-this":{"haiku":"Music is the key to freedom and transcendence from life's weight; the crowd moves together in ritual release, shaking the ground with raw collective energy.","opus":"A high-energy hype anthem with little beyond its own party mechanics: get the crowd up, feel the beat, riot. The one sincere throughline is that music is the escape valve from everything the world throws at him, and mistakes are fine because that's just how it goes.","sonnet":"A straightforward concert-hype track about music as liberation — the band channels raw energy through bass and beats to make the crowd rise up and forget the weight of daily life.","src":"haiku","fable":"Music is the release valve, the one thing that frees the speaker from everything the world keeps throwing at him. So the whole engine of triggered beats, earthquakes, and riots reads less as aggression than as a body converting outside pressure into motion. The final chorus is where the meaning lands: the swagger admits it will make mistakes and rules them okay in advance, so the party becomes self-forgiveness granted through volume rather than earned.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","party & hedonism","shame & repression"],"means":"preemptive self-absolution granted through volume rather than earned","built":"the mistake-ruling-okay structure turns a pump-up into a confession booth"},
"limp-bizkit~killer-in-you":{"haiku":"A homicidal maniac stalks and terrorizes, challenging listeners to confront the violent potential within themselves.","opus":"A cartoonish slasher-villain fantasy that flips into a taunt: the only defense against the monster is finding your own capacity for violence. Horror-movie theatrics used to dare the listener to stop cowering and fight back.","sonnet":"A relentless fictional killer is described in escalating terms of violence, and the listener is challenged to confront the capacity for brutality within themselves rather than wait passively to be victimized.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses dress up as a splatter-flick maniac, cataloguing his props with gleeful excess — a car trunk stocked with axes and accelerant, the sneering claim of leaving Leatherface looking tame — as though the danger lives entirely in some outside predator. Then the chorus swivels the pronoun and plants that same butcher inside the person being warned, converting the whole horror pantomime into a demand that they dredge up their own appetite for violence to survive. By the bridge the mask slips off completely, the taunting bravado hardening into a plain confession that the fantasy has stopped being pretend, that survival simply means becoming the thing you feared.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"the horror pantomime turning inside-out when the pronoun swivels at the chorus","built":"the mid-song pronoun shift plants the external predator inside the person being warned"},
"limp-bizkit~leech-demo-version":{"haiku":"A parasitic person attaches themselves to the narrator's life and crew, refusing to leave despite clear rejection.","opus":"A venom-filled kiss-off to a hanger-on who latches onto fame and won't let go, half-joke and half-threat. The real subject is the paranoia of sudden success and the parasites it attracts.","sonnet":"A song about an obsessive hanger-on who keeps showing up at shows and infiltrating the crew — the narrator is fed up and can't shake this person loose, though he addresses it with rhymes rather than physical force.","src":"haiku","fable":"A battle-rap eviction notice aimed at a hanger-on who keeps turning up at shows and inside the crew, drawn as a parasite that has to be pried off rather than asked to leave. The one clever move is meta: Durst declines actual violence and makes the song itself the weapon, beating the guy down with vocals and hooks — early proof that the Jacksonville demo-era Bizkit treated a track as a way to settle scores in public.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty","artifice & performance"],"means":"declining actual violence and making the track itself the eviction notice served in public","built":"the meta move names the song as the weapon used to settle the score","fnote":"Shipped as an explicitly labeled demo take; no source documents why it was kept over a re-recording."},
"limp-bizkit~let-me-down":{"haiku":"Romantic heartbreak reveals how completely the speaker depended on one person; betrayal through false rumor cuts deeper than any previous pain.","opus":"Bitter heartbreak after giving everything faithfully to someone who repeatedly disappointed him; he flips the betrayal into a backhanded thank-you for teaching him he can feel this pain, concluding the partner simply wasn't good enough for him.","sonnet":"A post-breakup song where the narrator catalogs the physical and emotional wreckage of being abandoned by someone he gave everything to — faith, honesty, prayers — only to be dismissed like yesterday's news.","src":"haiku","fable":"The chorus fires the same three-word verdict as an accusation, but the payoff line quietly flips the blame outward: after cataloguing his own faithfulness and prayers, the singer decides the person who let him down simply wasn't good enough for him, so the whole song works as wounded pride talking itself out of grief. That inversion is confirmed by the bridge, where the sarcastic thank-you for the lesson in what such hurt feels like is the mask, and the thing underneath is that feeling anything at all has become the injury he can't forgive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"wounded pride arguing itself out of grief by deciding the other was unworthy","built":"the sarcastic thank-you masks a confession that feeling anything has become the injury"},
"limp-bizkit~livin-it-up":{"haiku":"Celebrates hedonistic living and rejecting responsibility, boasting about rock star excess and not caring about consequences. The song glorifies fast living and shameless indulgence.","opus":"A brash, name-dropping boast about living recklessly in the fast lane without giving a damn. The narrator flaunts a party lifestyle of pop-culture references, bravado, and indulgence while noting how easy it is to lie and hide but how hard it is to truly be alive.","sonnet":"A boastful, irreverent self-portrait of living recklessly and without apology — the speaker catalogues his brash persona, pop culture references, and disregard for convention, framing the fast lane as a conscious lifestyle choice even while acknowledging the cage the world can be.","src":"haiku","fable":"Opening on a toast to Ben Stiller, it casts Durst as a preening celebrity grotesque: swagger built entirely on refusal — the persona of skateboards, spray cans and hangers-on runs on a defiant nothing-matters shrug. The joke sours when he invokes Fincher's film about a man sickened by his own hollow existence, because the track ends up siding with that story over its own bravado. The back half concedes that dodging and deceiving come cheap while merely staying alive does not, and the bridge drops the mask into an image of the world as a cage nobody tends. So the noise reads as flight dressed as triumph: the bluster is machinery for not feeling that cage, and the whole celebration is this record's loudest way of avoiding being awake.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","party & hedonism","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"bluster deployed as machinery for not feeling the cage","built":"Fight Club citation quietly sides against the bravado it is dressed as","fnote":"Opens with a spoken dedication to Ben Stiller — the shout-out is in the lyric itself."},
"limp-bizkit~lonely-world":{"haiku":"A person navigates the paradoxes of adulthood, loneliness, and attraction while drifting through life's confusion. Love feels like pain, time flies, and the world remains isolating.","opus":"Fred Durst drops the aggression for wistful reminiscence, looking back on a fast careless youth and the isolation underneath the swagger. The nu-metal bravado cracks to show a man who feels stranded no matter how the fights turn out.","sonnet":"A man-in-the-middle soliloquy about growing up — nostalgia for when everything was fast and free, self-awareness about having a complicated relationship with love, and a kind of stoic acceptance that the world is lonely but you figure it out as you go.","src":"haiku","fable":"The strut that frames both ends of the track poses as a question about who holds the power, but its only answer is a man stranded halfway between certainties, so the bravado is deflated before it starts. Between those posturing edges the verses narrate a life as aging-out — a childhood that felt weightless and forever, the sourness of a picked-on skater kid, the creeping recognition that years accelerate past whatever swagger he can muster. Each remembered stretch dissolves into the chorus's flat tally, where every night, quarrel, wrong and right registers as one more interchangeable unit of the same solitude. That gap is the whole reading: the boast is a mask, the tallied refrain the face beneath it, and the bridge finally drops the pose to concede that no striving connects and no explanation ever comes — so the looping isn't padding but the case being made.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","nostalgia & memory","artifice & performance"],"means":"the bravado as a question that only reveals a man stranded between certainties","built":"the looping tally refrain is the argument — the strut is padding for the same solitude"},
"limp-bizkit~los-angeles":{"haiku":"Los Angeles epitomizes disposable sexuality and shallow materialism driven by fame and looks. The city attracts predatory behavior disguised as glamour, where everyone uses everyone else.","opus":"A leering tour of LA nightlife as a meat market of women, gold-diggers, and sex, warning that the city is a scandalous trap that offers everything but never lets you leave once you buy in; beneath the crudeness it's about the seductive, soul-eating hollowness of Hollywood.","sonnet":"A leering tour of LA's sexual marketplace — cruising Sunset, ogling women at rock clubs, warning about gold-diggers and hidden surprises — that presents the city as simultaneously irresistible and full of traps for the unwary man.","src":"haiku","fable":"The tour-guide swagger opens as a hustler casting his line across the boulevard, but every woman he claims to snare is flagged in advance as trouble, a schemer, a counterfeit royal, so his boasting keeps confessing he is willingly walking into a con. The nightclub-crawl bravado curdles mid-verse into a gambler's warning and then outright dread when a partner's body proves other than advertised, and the whole seduction reveals its underlying panic about being tricked, stripped, and made a fool. By the final stretch the city itself takes over as the true hunter, a woman who lifts you up only to cage your ambitions where no exit exists, and his parting relief at merely surviving admits the one being lured in was always him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["night & the city","surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"the seducer's bravado curdling when the city reveals it was always hunting him","built":"every woman pre-flagged as counterfeit converts the conquest narrative into a confession of fear"},
"limp-bizkit~loser":{"haiku":"After a relationship ends, the speaker feels like a loser, haunted by past abuse and broken promises; they want to escape and reclaim the life they've lost.","opus":"A resentful split, where the narrator feels his whole life has gone downhill from the moment their relationship began. He broods over a rough history and reciprocal harm, ultimately conceding that together they surface each other's worst and the pairing has to end.","sonnet":"The speaker looks back on a relationship that brought out the worst in both people — marked by abuse, manipulation, and mutual damage — and resolves to walk away in order to reclaim himself. He wants simply to be okay and to stop losing his sense of self to a dynamic that has cost him everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title word keeps sliding between two meanings — the thing love wore him down into, and the damaged man he already was, still gnawing on cruelty from before her that he can't set down. The verses hand out fault evenly, picturing the pair as two big cats penned together, each provoking the other's meanest edges until the trap is mutual. But the chorus quietly moves the real casualty inward: he isn't mourning the woman so much as the self she consumed, and what he wants returned is the man he used to be. The drawn-out farewells and the shrugged nothing-to-be-done are staging, a calm posture over the small unglamorous plea he can't dress up — that he'd simply like to feel whole again.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"mourning aimed not at the woman but at the self the relationship consumed","built":"the title word holds both wound and cause in one syllable that never settles"},
"limp-bizkit~love-the-hate":{"haiku":"Two friends bond over mutual contempt for a third, playfully tearing down their rival until the joke reveals its own cruelty and their complicity in mockery.","opus":"Durst turning the world's mockery of his band into a joke on the hater, a skit where someone trashing Limp Bizkit is exposed as a former fan. Self-aware trolling that flips decades of ridicule back on the audience.","sonnet":"Two voices bond over mutual contempt for Limp Bizkit before the joke reveals itself — one friend admits they used to listen, the other turns hostile, and the song flips the premise into a comment on how shared hate can mask guilty pleasures and hidden loyalties.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages itself as a dialogue between two friends bonding over their shared contempt for Limp Bizkit and Fred Durst, letting the band author its own most vicious hater takedown before the frame snaps. The moment one confesses he actually used to listen as a kid, the other turns on him with real menace, so the true offense is not the hate but the disloyalty of having once loved. That reversal exposes the setup as a trap about performative disavowal, the way people scapegoat what they secretly hold a tie to in order to belong. The refrain that the joke is aimed at whoever missed the lone giveaway is Durst laughing from underneath the mask, having built the whole thing so the punchline lands on anyone who took the self-mockery at face value.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The two voices are both Durst playing his own haters — the record's stated thesis in miniature.","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"performative disavowal exposed as the trap: disloyalty of once having loved is the real offense","built":"the band authors its own hater takedown, the punchline landing on whoever missed the lone giveaway"},
"limp-bizkit~my-generation":{"haiku":"Generational apathy and defiant nihilism of Gen X youth.","opus":"A defiant, party-hard generational anthem of not caring what critics say until they respect the narrator's disaffected generation.","sonnet":"Generational defiance — a disillusioned youth culture demanding to be taken seriously and refusing to care what others think.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song borrows its title from The Who's mod anthem but empties out the generational pride, replacing solidarity with a defensive crouch: the whole track is built to answer imagined contempt, not to declare anything on its own terms. That structure is the read. Every verse pivots from swagger — the jungle, the town being wrecked, the Cuckoo's Nest boast — into the same shrunken image of a window the sun cannot reach, so the bravado keeps collapsing back into the confession that this is a generation living in the dark and knowing it. The chorus is nakedly conditional: the professed indifference is explicitly a hostage of your attention, promising to withhold care exactly as long as care is withheld, which turns the whole not-giving-a-fuck posture into a demand to be given one. The bridge drops the mask entirely — trading blame back and forth, then asking three times whether flight is even possible before Durst answers only for himself — so the anthem meant to speak for millions ends as one anxious man insisting, alone, that he still believes he can.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the not-giving-a-fuck posture exposed as a demand to be given one","built":"the chorus's conditional indifference — explicitly contingent on your attention — converts the anthem into a plea wearing swagger as its mask","fnote":"The stuttered chorus is a knowing nod to The Who's 1965 anthem."},
"limp-bizkit~my-own-cobain":{"haiku":"A heartbroken person spirals into suicidal ideation after losing the only person who cared, tormented by regret and emotional dissolution.","opus":"Durst using Kurt Cobain as shorthand for a private breaking point after pushing away the one person who cared, drowning in the rain of what's left of himself. Beneath the bluster it's a genuinely wounded attempt to find new meaning.","sonnet":"Grief and self-destruction after the end of a relationship — the speaker is drowning in the loss, driving recklessly toward his own undoing, invoking Kurt Cobain's death as a metaphor for being consumed by pain and abandonment.","src":"haiku","fable":"Fred Durst borrows Cobain not as icon but as private verb — a place a man drives himself when he has wrecked the one relationship that steadied him, so the name stands in for a self-administered collapse rather than martyrdom. The song runs on that gap between exterior and interior: rain that pours through even shuttered windows figures a grief that gets in regardless of how sealed off he keeps himself, and the abandoned lion who leaves his lioness admits the ruin is his own doing, not fate's. Underneath the bravado the hook is plain confession — disbelief that it is broken, a plea for someone to call it a dream, a request for a new meaning — because the persona that always promised to be whatever you wanted can't perform it today. The closing chant of amazing and pretending is the mask reasserting itself over the wound: flattery as the last available cover for a man still spending his time acting saved.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Explicitly Durst's depression song — he said he really connects with Cobain, and tied it to It'll Be OK from Chocolate Starfish as the same tortured-mind fantasizing.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","artifice & performance","shame & repression"],"means":"a borrowed celebrity name reduced to a private verb for self-administered ruin","built":"the closing flattery chant reasserts the performance mask over the confessional wound"},
"limp-bizkit~my-way":{"fable":"The relentless sound-check chatter that opens and riddles the song is a mixing-desk reflex, a nervous insistence on being audible that never settles into an actual tune, so the plea for notice and the threat itself blur into one twitchy verbal habit. Beneath the bluster of an ultimatum daring the other person toward the exit, the verses are oddly cramped and defensive, each one another rehearsal of the same vow to storm out of an argument over who holds control. What the swagger hides is a man who can only picture triumph as departure that leaves someone aching for him. Then the tone dips into a fragile consolation that curdles into a reminder none of us knows when our time runs out, a grasping, almost desperate move that betrays the whole performance as a reach for a final word he doubts he will win.","fableDeep":"Listen to the tenses: everything decisive is future — 'I'ma let it all come out, I'ma stand up and shout, I will straight up leave' — so the song is a quitting fantasy rather than a quitting, the exit permanently one-more-fight away. The borrowed 'check out my melody' hook (Rakim by way of swagger) works as self-soothing between threats. And the bridge slips the real card under the table: 'someday you'll see things my way, 'cause you never know when you're gonna go' — the argument's closing move is mortality itself, the teenager's ultimate comeback: you'll be sorry when I'm dead.","haiku":"Someone asserts independence and refuses further submission to another's authority, choosing freedom and solitude over continued conflict.","opus":"A defiant break from someone's controlling leadership, with the narrator declaring he'll do things his own way, walk out, and let the other person be left missing him.","sonnet":"Tired of being looked down on and controlled in a relationship or situation, the narrator declares he is done compromising and will leave on his own terms, doing things his way from now on.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The WrestleMania X-Seven theme behind the Rock/Stone Cold promo — and a remix magnet: William Orbit, DJ Premier and P. Diddy all took passes.","themes":["anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss","artifice & performance"],"means":"triumph imagined only as departure that leaves someone aching for him","built":"the sound-check chatter never resolves into a tune, enacting the plea for notice it cannot satisfy"},
"limp-bizkit~n-2-gether-now":{"haiku":"Multiple rappers boast about their dominance, survival through crime, and refusal to be silenced; the refrain demands everyone shut up while they assert their power and presence.","opus":"Limp Bizkit hand the mic to Method Man over a DJ Premier beat, trading braggadocio about lyrical dominance, media strikes and knocking rivals out. This crossover moment fused rap-rock swagger with Wu-Tang pedigree at nu-metal's commercial peak, its blunt hook demanding everybody shut up while the two verses pile on wordplay, violence and streetwise flexing.","sonnet":"Two voices trade dense, combative verses — navigating media, violence, street economy, and hip-hop hierarchy with overlapping references to force and wit. The crowd is repeatedly commanded to silence itself. What holds it together is the shared assertion that survival and domination are inseparable, and that anyone who has not earned their position should defer.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~no-sex":{"haiku":"After intense physical intimacy, both feel empty and ashamed; they realize their connection was only carnal, lacking genuine respect or meaning.","opus":"A morning-after reckoning of self-disgust over a purely physical relationship; the narrator regrets that sex has replaced any real knowledge of his partner, cycling through blame of both of them before landing on the assertion that he's worth more than being reduced to that.","sonnet":"A song about a sexual relationship that has consumed all other connection — the narrator regrets that there is nothing left between them but the physical act, feels self-disgust at the mutual lack of respect, and wishes they'd kept more distance.","src":"haiku","fable":"Durst narrates a hookup stripped down to the act itself, where nothing about the other person registers to him beyond the body, and the chorus replays the moment as a wish he'd held back that he immediately concedes he did not act on. The come-on and the revulsion issue from one throat, contempt hurled at his partner in the same breath he's aiming it squarely at his own reflection. By the bridge the disgust has curled fully inward, landing on a flat affirmation of his own value that he has to say over and over, the sheer repetition betraying how little he actually buys it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"contempt aimed at partner and self from the same throat, converging on neither","built":"the bridge's self-affirmation repeated until repetition itself names the disbelief"},
"limp-bizkit~nobody-like-you":{"haiku":"Someone is convinced they are hated and that their departure is desired, yet they remain obsessed and refuse to release the other person despite being driven to madness.","opus":"A toxic, obsessive fixation on someone who hates the singer and wants him to die, yet he refuses to let go, waiting on them to die too while laying his life on the line for them; the read is a self-destructive, unhealthy bond of mutual contempt he can't or won't escape.","sonnet":"About being trapped in a toxic relationship with someone who seems to hate the narrator while the narrator refuses to let go — oscillating between rage, devotion, and total surrender, laying their life down for someone who possibly wishes them dead.","src":"haiku","fable":"A ballad-shaped vow of loyalty and self-sacrifice runs on top, yet each pledge is welded to its dark twin, so the devotion reads as the compulsion of someone terrified of being abandoned, even laying his life down while quietly awaiting the other's death. Two guest presences pull the single narrator apart — one voice all raw agitation, the other a smoother melodic ache in the hook — so the pairing becomes one man quarrelling with the strength of his own grasp. The chorus keeps asserting he has a justification for holding on, and by the bridge and breakdown that assurance dissolves before us into the confession that none exists, the closing chant clinging to an attachment that has outlasted every excuse beneath it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Pairs Durst with Korn's Jonathan Davis and STP's Scott Weiland.","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"each devotion pledge welded to its dark twin until attachment outlasts every excuse","built":"two vocal presences split the single narrator so he quarrels with the strength of his own grasp"},
"limp-bizkit~nobody-loves-me":{"haiku":"Social alienation and rejection drive the narrator to despair, feeling worthless while watching others succeed and judge him.","opus":"Self-pity turned into a snarl, Durst wallowing in feeling unloved and misunderstood while lashing out at everyone he imagines is better than him. The bratty petulance is deliberate, adolescent alienation worn as armor.","sonnet":"A rant about feeling alienated, attacked, and misunderstood — the narrator lashes back at critics and a hostile world while also turning inward with self-loathing, declaring nobody cares and mocking his own victimhood in the same breath.","src":"haiku","fable":"The chorus inflates the old playground rhyme about the unloved kid who threatens to go eat worms to metal volume, and that borrowed nursery sulk is both the joke and the confession: the persecution complex knows it sounds childish and airs itself anyway. The verses play defense — a Southern outsider daring critics of his identity to come settle it on his turf, the self-cast black sheep insisting his business is nobody's — but the bridge drops the swagger for mock-worship of some flawless judge that curdles into a spat insult, exposing the self-pity as aggression wearing a victim's mask.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"playground sulk aired at metal volume knowing exactly how childish it sounds","built":"the borrowed nursery rhyme carries both the joke and the confession in the same line","fnote":"An open Tool homage, Durst doing his Maynard James Keenan impression."},
"limp-bizkit~nobody-loves-me-album-version-explicit":{"haiku":"Universal rejection suffocates as the speaker drowns in unloveability. No one extends hand across the void of isolation.","opus":"A self-pitying, self-loathing outburst dressed in bravado, where Durst swings between wallowing in being unloved and lashing out at a rival he calls perfect. The nursery-rhyme refrain about eating worms turns adolescent misery into a chant, capturing the wounded, alienated persona that made early Limp Bizkit resonate with disaffected teenagers.","sonnet":"Self-pitying defiance: sick of deadlines, sick of being called bugged-out, sick of being the enemy — \"nobody loves me, maybe I'll go eat worms\" is the refrain half-joking, half-meaning it — but underneath the bravado is the recognition that black sheep are everywhere and the guard needs to drop.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~nookie":{"haiku":"A man betrayed by his girlfriend who cheated and stole from him grapples with humiliation and feelings of being manipulated.","opus":"A bitter reckoning with a cheating ex who used his heart and money then slept with his friends, leaving him the laughingstock. The self-deprecating hook, that he did it all for sex, is really a humiliated admission of being deceived, wrapped in bravado as he finally tells her to stick her manipulation.","sonnet":"A bitter post-breakup rant from a man who realizes his girlfriend cheated on him with his friends — he's humiliated, still half-attached, and eventually concludes that everything he did in that relationship was just for sex, which he now regrets.","src":"haiku","fable":"The chorus struts like a punchline, but the verses under it describe a man wrecked and publicly shamed by a partner who exploited him, unable to walk away and fully aware he can't. The refrain he keeps flinging at himself between hooks leaks the shame the swagger is meant to bury, turning the claim that he was only chasing sex into a face-saving cover for having been used. The bridge abandons the pose completely: handed the reasonable counsel to let it go, he refuses, electing to stay stuck and unchanged rather than heal. What reads as bravado becomes a portrait of someone clutching his wound like proof he is still owed something.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"By Durst's own account, about a real ex who cheated with his friends and used him for money — the hook line his coded admission of why he stayed; he laments the story got drowned out by the catchphrase.","fnote2":"Significant Other (June 1999, Terry Date producing) debuted at No. 1 and made them superstars.","themes":["shame & repression","heartbreak & loss","artifice & performance"],"means":"the bravado held up as proof of injury rather than proof of recovery","built":"the refrain he flings at himself between hooks leaks what the chorus is there to bury"},
"limp-bizkit~out-of-style":{"haiku":"A defiant outsider refuses to conform to trends, asserting their personal style and authenticity while calling out copycat peers and shallow solidarity.","opus":"Fred Durst leaning into the fact that his band is perpetually uncool, flipping the mockery into a boast about staying unchanged while daring former peers to remember they should be on the same side.","sonnet":"Fred Durst asserting that his style is perennially fresh despite critics writing him off, calling out copycats and poseurs, while inserting a moment of genuine reflection about unity before returning to bravado about surviving a war zone of competition.","src":"haiku","fable":"The boasting stays welded shut, a vow never to switch up his approach, imagined rivals dismissed as imitators crowding him like an occupying force he has to hold off. Yet the armor keeps splitting to let the opposite through: an ache that arrives tenderly, a startled confession of getting serious and unhinged, a doubter's glance at the cross resting on his neck, a man begging for something while swearing he wants nothing. Even the surrounding menace resolves into a request for alliance rather than victory, so the stubborn refusal to update reads less as a taunt than as a defense mustered by someone who fears he has been outrun and won't admit the dread.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote2":"Released Halloween 2021 on Suretone, finally displacing the long-abandoned Stampede of the Disco Elephants; the title reclaims the haters' verdict outright.","themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"stubborn refusal to update as a defense against admitting the dread of being outrun","built":"the armor keeps splitting — tenderness, the cross, a plea — inside a vow of swagger"},
"limp-bizkit~outro":{"haiku":"Meta-commentary on musical authenticity and Limp Bizkit's controversial place in rock; defensive posturing against mainstream dismissal.","opus":"Less a song than a stand-up bit and manifesto tacked onto an album's close, a scalp-shining tirade against manufactured bubblegum pop and assembly-line singers, calling for an audacious upheaval in music while jeering at anyone who bought the record. Durst and his bandmates made this cocky self-promotion the essence of late-nineties rap-rock, ridiculing and glorifying themselves in a single breath.","sonnet":"A closing provocation that doubles as a manifesto — a radio DJ rant and band monologue railing against prefabricated pop and disposable chart music, positioning Limp Bizkit as the antidote to mainstream softness. The whole thing collapses into self-parody and a winking acknowledgment that the expensive CD in your hand is also, ultimately, a piece of plastic.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~phenomenon":{"haiku":"A rapper asserts unstoppable presence and authenticity against fake competitors, demanding recognition for organic growth and real nerve.","opus":"Durst's braggadocio manifesto, hangover boasts and pot metaphors piling up as he taunts Hollywood poseurs and crowns himself the real thing. Pure self-mythologizing swagger, the appeal of a guy convinced he's a phenomenon.","sonnet":"Fred Durst reasserts himself after a period of irrelevance — boasting about staying authentic and untameable while mocking Hollywood poseurs — it is primarily a vehicle for self-mythologizing swagger.","src":"haiku","fable":"He works up a wall of self-description whose entire message is that you cannot get a fix on him, so the title's declaration of being a rare event functions less as a boast than as a refusal to be described. The verses pile on the swagger texture — risen from nothing, too big to bother shaving, taunting the poseurs sitting on the sidelines — but they interrupt and contradict themselves until what lands is not meaning but sheer kinetic command. Everything collapses into the order to bounce and move. The lone bridge pauses to ask what you would do if everything were ending, and the only reply is to crank the volume and drop the rhythm again, which is the whole position: nothing lives under the noise, and the insistence that he can't be read is really a confession that there is nothing there to read.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the declaration of being unreadable as confession that nothing is there to read","built":"the bridge's end-of-everything question answered only by dropping the beat again"},
"limp-bizkit~pill-popper":{"haiku":"Pharmaceutical dependency promises relief but delivers only temporary escape, trapping the user in escalating self-medication while corporations profit from perpetual need.","opus":"Addiction and Big Pharma indicted together: an industry that manufactures customers not cures, and a user chasing a pill-shaped paradise that never lasts. The spoken intro names the corruption the chorus then acts out.","sonnet":"A voice caught in pharmaceutical dependency frames itself through the lens of systemic exploitation — the pharmaceutical industry manufactures addiction rather than cures, and the speaker is both a casualty of and participant in that cycle.","src":"haiku","fable":"The pious spoken intro against Big Pharma turning cures into customers curdles instantly into the song's own craving, so that the manufactured-addiction critique becomes indistinguishable from the addict's demand for one more high. What poses as protest is really the mask over a helpless loop of chasing a paradise that lasts only until the pill wears off and the begging starts again.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","politics & society"],"means":"critique of manufactured addiction becoming indistinguishable from the addict's own demand","built":"the pious spoken intro curdles within seconds into the craving it was condemning"},
"limp-bizkit~pollution":{"haiku":"A hip-hop track defending provocative lyrical content and aggressive style against narrow-minded censorship. The speaker insists on artistic freedom and refuses to tone down the volume.","opus":"A combative rap boasting about the narrator's rhyming skill while confronting narrow-minded critics, framing their noise and complaints as pollution and defiantly refusing to turn down or shut up.","sonnet":"A defense of the group's music against narrow-minded critics, framing their sound as an unstoppable force and daring detractors to accept it or get out of the way.","src":"haiku","fable":"Pollution is the insult the song steals and wears as a badge: what gatekeepers call this Jacksonville low-end smog, Durst reclaims as proof the sound is everywhere and can't be scrubbed out, while the pre-chorus turns the accusation around by asking when the complainers chose their narrow minds. The bridge makes the defiance structural — a list where every element they demand be turned down comes back louder, escalating to the whole band being told to shut up — which is the debut-era nu-metal position in miniature: criticism as fuel, volume as answer. Then the taped outro punctures the strut, the band actually telling Fred to shut up and dismissing the track as a sucker song, so the bravado ships with its own self-mockery built in.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"every demand to turn down returned louder until volume becomes the argument","built":"the outro has the band telling Fred to shut up, shipping the bravado with self-mockery built in"},
"limp-bizkit~pollution-album-version-explicit":{"haiku":"Toxicity saturates environment as poison seeps through all living systems. Contamination becomes inescapable fact of existence.","opus":"A combative early-era manifesto about drowning critics in noise and refusing to be silenced. Durst frames his flow as smog choking rivals and turns every command to turn the volume down into fuel to bring the beat back harder. It's less a story than a statement of defiance, art as sheer aggressive persistence.","sonnet":"A boast about the band's sonic aggression as pollution you can't tune out: the rhymes choke the critics, the bass smothers the freestyle, and every time authority tries to turn down the volume — vocals, bass, the whole lot — the beat comes straight back.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~re-arranged":{"haiku":"A broken relationship ends as two people realign their lives separately. Both parties acknowledge incompatibility and the need to let each other go.","opus":"A breakup reckoning: worn down by carrying the relationship's weight, the narrator lets go of a know-it-all partner who only cries when it's too late, thanking God it's over while acknowledging he won't come out unchanged from being used and betrayed.","sonnet":"A relationship finally breaking apart after the speaker has watched his partner's endless dissatisfaction and know-it-all certainty make everything impossible — relief and bitterness mixed together in the acknowledgment that it's genuinely over.","src":"haiku","fable":"The speaker meets a rupture with deliberate calm, retreating into doubt and quiet where he might once have argued, half-wishing he could let down a partner who seems to expect him to fold under pressure. The chorus reframes the parting as steadiness rather than ruin: a hand still extended toward someone convinced she needs no rescue, so that being reordered becomes the work of assembling a self that goes on standing. Only in the bridge does his suppressed disdain harden into a charge, and even then it lands as clinical observation, faulting her for masking distress until it caves into grief and collapse, and for treating people as freely swapped one for another. The murmured lines that open and close it turn the whole thing into a quiet instruction handed to someone not yet ready to grasp it, the final word surrendered not to force but to whatever understanding time eventually brings.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The video stages the Woodstock '99 vilification literally: the band arrested, sentenced, and drowned in a room slowly filling with milk.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"being reordered held as self-assembly rather than ruin","built":"the clinical bridge charge arrives after murmured calm, making disdain the cost of composure"},
"limp-bizkit~re-entry":{"haiku":"An announcer celebrates a performer's return with universal acclaim, emphasizing their global reach and recognition. The song serves as intro hype distilled to catchphrase and reverb.","opus":"A carnival-barker intro announcing the band's return, all hype and self-mythologizing before the payoff. It's less a song than a swaggering statement of presence, Limp Bizkit selling Limp Bizkit.","sonnet":"A carnival-barker intro track — pure self-promotional hype, building anticipation for Limp Bizkit's return with mock grandeur and in-joke banter between band members.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is a carnival barker's overselling introduction that collapses into a two-word punchline of the band naming itself, so the grandiose pitch turns out to be an ad for nothing more than the act stepping back onstage. Underneath the swagger sits a quieter admission: the mutual recognition of the closing refrain is all the substance the comeback actually has, fame acknowledging fame with nothing promised in between.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote2":"The only Limp Bizkit album without Wes Borland — public guitar auditions landed Snot's Mike Smith, whose parts were then mostly scrapped; it reportedly cycled through joke titles (Bipolar, The Search for Teddy Swoes) before Results May Vary.","themes":["artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"grandiose introduction that delivers only the performer's own name","built":"the carnival pitch collapses to a two-word punchline at the structural close"},
"limp-bizkit~ready-to-go":{"haiku":"A provocative figure boasts of sexual conquest and rock stardom while mocking mainstream music; they celebrate hedonism and assert their dominance in culture.","opus":"Fred Durst's chest-beating comeback swagger, insisting rock still matters while boasting about women and status in the crudest terms. There's little beneath the bravado: it's a party-anthem flex built to reassert a fading star's relevance.","sonnet":"Fred Durst returns to stake his claim on rock credibility while getting drunk and picking up women at a club, the song essentially a boastful set of bar-crawl and sexual conquest anecdotes delivered as a comeback statement.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~red-light-green-light":{"haiku":"A boastful rapper displays confidence and sexual readiness, challenging competitors and highlighting their own prowess.","opus":"Reuniting the nu-metal figurehead with a West Coast rap legend, this track is pure strut and traffic-light innuendo. The refrain asks over and over if the listener is ready to roll while the emcees trade boasts about keys, cheese, and rivals biting their style. The guest verse rolls out lowrider swagger, endo, and freestyle bravado, the whole thing a chest-thumping, party-starting collision of rap-rock posturing rather than any confession.","sonnet":"A party-swagger track built around the stop-and-go signal of sexual readiness: two performers trade verses about confidence, reputation, and physical prowess, directing the assembled crowd to move or hold still on command while asserting their dominance in every room they enter.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~rollin":{"haiku":"Swagger and repetition blur into mantra; rolling forward as tautology of motion.","opus":"The band's peak-fame party anthem, all crowd commands and swaggering platinum-era boasts built to detonate a mosh pit. There's little subtext beyond dominance and celebration, Durst rallying every clique in the room to throw their hands up. It captures nu-metal at its most triumphant and commercial, a group riding the very top of its cultural moment.","sonnet":"A crowd-control anthem that instructs the audience through call-and-response commands — move in, hands up, keep rolling — while the band asserts its dominance and the show becomes an act of collective, physical abandon.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~rollin-air-raid-vehicle":{"haiku":"Party culture and defiant hedonism celebrate momentum and confidence; the band rallies crowds through aggressive swagger and refusal to apologize.","opus":"A turn-of-the-millennium hype anthem with almost nothing underneath but the swagger of a band at its commercial peak, daring the crowd to keep moving and daring anyone to test them.","sonnet":"A pure crowd-participation anthem with no narrative content — the song is a call to throw hands up, keep moving, and acknowledge the band's dominance on the floor; it exists entirely as a vehicle for physical energy in a room rather than expressing any subject or theme.","src":"haiku","fable":"Recorded at the exact 2000 summit of Fred Durst's commercial reign, this is a song about nothing but its own occasion — the band announcing its residency in the room and taunting anyone who thinks they could supply a sharper track. The lyric functions as pure crowd-machinery: barked directions to advance, retreat, inhale, exhale, arms raised or lowered, so that all sense drains into the physical churn of the venue floor it's conducting live. Its title verb promises travel yet arrives nowhere on purpose, motion prized for itself with no destination attached. The parting inventory of every faction present — the devoted and the disgusted, the strutting show-offs, the guys cruising past in Cadillacs — carries the real thesis: allegiance is irrelevant, and everyone gets drafted into one shared momentum. Taken as swagger it's hollow, but taken honestly that hollowness is the whole point — a party anthem whose lone act of sincerity is refusing to mean anything beyond keeping bodies moving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The video opens with Ben Stiller and Stephen Dorff pulling up in a Bentley and mistaking Durst for the valet — a friendship formed around Durst's Zoolander cameo, which echoes two tracks later in Livin' It Up's opening dedication.","themes":["party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"sincerity located entirely in refusing to mean anything","built":"title verb promises travel but the song arrives nowhere on purpose"},
"limp-bizkit~rollin-urban-assault-vehicle":{"haiku":"Persistent motion and collective rebellion; rolling forward despite everything.","opus":"The harder, hip-hop-heavy remake of the arena hit, handing verses to a trio of rap heavyweights for a rowdy posse cut. The bravado turns more openly menacing, snatched chains and street threats replacing pure party energy. It marks the band's bid for rap credibility at their commercial peak, muscling in with marquee guests.","sonnet":"Four rappers trade verses of dominance and untouchability over a command to keep moving — DMX, Redman, Method Man, and Fred Durst each marking territory with escalating threat and bravado, the rolling itself the point: momentum as power, stopping as defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~shark-attack":{"haiku":"A narrative of confrontation and competitive aggression where the singer positions himself as unstoppable and dominant against challengers and detractors.","opus":"A defiant boast aimed at a rival who has been talking trash, with the narrator promising to strike back hard and survive the fight. He casts himself as a lone predator who swims among sharks and comes out without a scratch.","sonnet":"A confrontational attack on a rival who has talked trash, framing the retaliation as self-defense against disrespect and a declaration that the narrator will outmaneuver anyone who comes for him.","src":"haiku","fable":"The chest-thumping menace of the verses is the costume: the swaggering threats, the boasts about outgunning any comer, the promise to leave a rival worse off than before. The closing bridge slips that costume off, reframing every swing as self-defense by a man treading water among things built to devour him, so the ferocity becomes strategy rather than appetite. Read together, the trash-talk and the refrain's litany of blows that never break the skin are one gesture: a loud show of being untouchable staged precisely because the surroundings are all teeth.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"ferocity reframed by the bridge as survival tactic among predators","built":"the closing bridge slips the menace costume off and plants the speaker underwater"},
"limp-bizkit~shot-non-lp-version":{"haiku":"Someone simultaneously cures and curses the speaker, bringing both light and despair; the speaker feels destroyed but accepts it as right, saying his hero is now dead.","opus":"Limp Bizkit paint a toxic obsession, a lover who is both cure and animosity, sun and despised darkness, someone who wrecked his head yet leaves him alright. The nu-metal outfit turns contradiction into refrain, playing along to avoid a fight while insisting the beloved does not want him to go, hero dead and speaker somehow surviving the wreckage.","sonnet":"A relationship has scrambled the speaker's thinking — the other person is cure and sickness, sun and unbearable light, angel and fraud all at once. Rather than fight, he absorbs it, plays along. Now that the hero-figure has collapsed, a strange calm settles in. Being wrecked by someone turns out to be more survivable than expected.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~shotgun":{"haiku":"A chaotic street narrative mixing survival, violence, and partying in a neighborhood where guns are commonplace. The song captures recklessness, fleeing from law, and indifference to consequences.","opus":"A street-life boast about running from police, dealing weed, jail, and gunplay, framing a rough neighborhood where the sound of a shotgun sends everyone jumping.","sonnet":"The narrator describes a chaotic night of substance use, running from police, and neighbourhood violence, framing it all as the familiar reality of where he lives — the shotgun of the title a ubiquitous sound in that environment.","src":"haiku","fable":"The persona narrates his own coming-apart as ascension, so a bleeding nose and three weeks without sleep get folded into the same motion as firing an engine, until fugitive collapse and euphoric launch become indistinguishable inside one repeated verb. Wreckage and liftoff never settle into confession or brag; they keep converting into each other, and that conversion is the actual subject, a man who can only feel the crash by reading it as the crowd screaming for more. The refrain strips the crowd away and leaves him at home with only the weapon's triple click, the same launch narrated by someone who has run out of anyone to perform the ascent for.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The lead single — a stoned domestic snapshot carrying a rare Wes Borland guitar solo.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"crash and liftoff kept converting into each other until both lose meaning","built":"the refrain strips the crowd and leaves only the weapon's triple click as audience"},
"limp-bizkit~show-me-what-you-got":{"haiku":"The band thanks their fans and influences for supporting their journey across America. The tour and community behind them are what make rock roll survive.","opus":"A hype-up party track and gratitude list rolled into one: a nationwide roll-call of tour cities urging crowds to get up, followed by shout-outs thanking family, fans, and fellow musicians. The throughline beneath the bravado is genuine appreciation, the narrator admits he'd be nothing without his fans and collaborators, grateful for the life his music gave him.","sonnet":"A tour diary doubled as a crowd-hype number — the narrator rattles through every city they've played, shouts out collaborators and personal debts, and turns it all into a gratitude speech for the fans who make the show happen.","src":"haiku","fable":"A thank-you note disguised as a pump-up anthem: the tour-stop roll call of American cities isn't bravado so much as an inventory of gratitude, a headliner reading his own itinerary as a love letter to every room that showed up, before verse two drops the mask entirely and names family, label, and peers outright. The party-starting hook that keeps demanding the crowd prove itself is really the reciprocal of that debt — someone convinced he'd be nothing without the fans admitting it in the only register the stage allows, by yelling.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["friendship & loyalty","party & hedonism"],"means":"a headliner's gratitude debt discharged in the only register the stage allows","built":"the city roll call reread as a love letter makes the pump-up hook its reciprocal confession"},
"limp-bizkit~sour":{"haiku":"A relationship soured from sweetness to betrayal; manipulated and used, finally breaking free from toxic connection.","opus":"A bitter breakup rant where Durst tallies the ways a lover used and abused him, then defiantly cuts them loose. The register swings from wounded to vindictive, even mocking his own bitterness mid-song. Early and raw, it channels romantic betrayal into blunt aggression, refusing tenderness in favor of settling the score.","sonnet":"The speaker unloads resentment at an ex who used and verbally abused them, swings between anger at her and self-blame for staying too long, and ultimately decides the relationship is over even if the bitterness lingers.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup grievance that curdles exactly as the title promises — the early sweetness of not going an hour without her face turns into money spent, pride dropped, and verbal abuse endured, until she gets dropped off with the counterfeit-currency insult that names the whole 1997 debut. The real content is the chorus contradiction: Durst insists the blame is entirely hers, then answers his own call-and-response by admitting he is the one who catches it, self-pity undercutting the accusation in real time. The third verse even hears itself sounding like a bitch mid-rant and reaches for old-school toughness to cover it — the mask of intensity pulled over a wound the whole song is busy displaying.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"self-pity undercutting the accusation in real time while the wound is being displayed","built":"the third verse hears itself sounding like a bitch mid-rant and reaches for a toughness mask","fnote":"Mines the same taken-for-granted-by-an-ex vein as Nookie — and carries the line that gave the album its name."},
"limp-bizkit~sour-album-version-explicit":{"haiku":"Bitterness curdles existence as sweetness becomes impossible. Sourness permeates every taste, every moment.","opus":"From Limp Bizkit's raw 1997 debut, this is a bitter post-mortem of a relationship that curdled from sweetness into exploitation. The narrator tallies the money and pride he sacrificed for someone who used and belittled him, then flips into aggressive relief at finally cutting her loose. Underneath the swagger sits the wounded ego of a man insisting he was never to blame.","sonnet":"A scorched-earth post-breakup: she lived in his castle, verbally abused him, used him, and left him in the sewer — he put in the love, she treated him like trash, and now that he's finally dropped her off, he knows in the long run she'll be the one regretting it.","src":"haiku"},
"limp-bizkit~stalemate":{"haiku":"A person poisoned and betrayed by someone they loved recounts their jealousy, degradation, and revenge fantasy after years of being strung along. They vow retaliation and restoration of dignity through vengeance.","opus":"A bitter reckoning with a manipulative, selfish lover whose moods swing hourly and who treated the speaker like trash; now consumed by resentment, he hunts them down for payback and vows to get his own, mocking their false sweetness and professions of love.","sonnet":"The narrator recounts being manipulated and used by someone who was sweet one moment and cruel the next, and vows to get payback for the humiliation and pain that person caused.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup autopsy that curdles into revenge fantasy: Durst catalogs a partner who flips from tender to venomous by the hour, whose need to dominate left him strung along past self-respect — the title naming a bond where neither cruelty nor escape ever wins outright. The long third verse snaps the wounded voice into chanted payback, escalating to a threat of rearranging a face, vengeance imagined as the last scrap of dignity available. Then the outro undoes it all — a woozy, saccharine surrender that hands her every argument and calls the submission love — revealing the vengeful middle as daydream and the stalemate as permanent.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"revenge fantasy as the last scrap of dignity, undone by the outro handing her every argument","built":"the woozy saccharine surrender reveals the vengeful middle as daydream and the stalemate as permanent"},
"limp-bizkit~stink-finger":{"haiku":"A speaker confronts a neighbor's hypocrisy and judgment with crude contempt. The song channels anger at being dismissed and demands recognition of equal status.","opus":"Durst's snarling feud with a sneering, judgmental neighbor, mocking phony love-thy-neighbor piety while relishing the provocation. Class resentment and territorial spite turned into a middle-finger anthem.","sonnet":"A confrontational neighbor dispute taken to cartoon extremes — the narrator despises someone nearby who looks down on him, and gives them the middle finger with gleeful, profane aggression.","src":"haiku","fable":"A neighbor feud inflated to nu-metal scale: Durst casts himself as the filthy tenant in the pig pen next door, fully aware of how the upturned nose across the fence sees him, and answers class contempt with eviction threats — sneering at the love-thy-neighbor commandment as a rule written for people who never had his neighbor. The outro gives up arguing altogether and collapses into old-school hip-hop chant, the tell that the grievance was mostly a pretext for the brawl.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"class contempt answered by reclaiming the pig-pen and dismissing the commandment as written for someone else","built":"the outro abandons argument for old-school hip-hop chant, revealing the grievance as pretext for the brawl"},
"limp-bizkit~stuck":{"haiku":"Greed and materialism trap people in cycles of desperation, with the narrator frustrated by those chasing money at any cost.","opus":"A paranoid rant against a partner he casts as a gold-digger circling his money, all threat and hustle and grievance. Underneath the bluster is the anxiety of new fame, the fear that everyone close now wants a cut.","sonnet":"A tirade against people trying to take the narrator's money and status — a greedy woman and opportunistic rivals are called out, and the narrator insists he will survive their schemes by staying hostile to anyone motivated by greed.","src":"haiku","fable":"New-money paranoia from the Jacksonville debut era: Durst casts everyone circling his first real cash — a phone-blowing ex, hangers-on, the whole entourage — as diggers burrowing after his green, and the title cuts both ways, since she is stuck on his money while he is stuck taking the calls. Verse two lifts the famous soda demand from Suicidal Tendencies' Institutionalized to claim an 80s misfit pedigree for a guy now bragging he has outgrown his loser years. The bridge is the tell: the argument collapses into a single question hammered a dozen-plus times, and the repetition stops working as accusation and becomes the sound of somebody who cannot hang up — every bit as stuck as the woman he is blasting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the accusation of being stuck collapsing into its own demonstration","built":"the bridge's hammered repetition stops accusing and becomes the sound of the stuck man it describes","fnote":"Borland plays the whole thing without a pick."},
"limp-bizkit~take-a-look-around":{"haiku":"Apathy and hatred as responses to societal contempt, with the narrator claiming indifference while reflecting on life's meaninglessness.","opus":"Framed with a mock spy briefing, the narrator vows to live his own way despite critics and a world full of tension and hate. He reflects that hate is all the world seems to see lately and that life is a fast, unforgiving lesson learned only at the end.","sonnet":"The narrator deflects criticism and hatred by owning his flaws, arguing that the world's pervasive hostility explains why people turn on him, while insisting life is a fast, unforgiving lesson you either keep up with or lose.","src":"haiku","fable":"Durst spends two verses assembling a picture of himself that refuses to hold together, the cocky survivor bragging that he will outlast every doubter sitting flush against a man who names himself a fool and a loser grinding his own thoughts to pulp. The song's true subject is that fracture, the point where the boasting and the self-disgust reveal themselves as one injury speaking in two voices that cannot admit they belong to the same person. The chorus escapes the tension by turning it outward, recasting private contempt as a verdict on everyone watching, so the endless refrain about being hated arrives less as grievance than as a man insisting the world despises him first to avoid confessing he despises himself. When he wonders whether anyone knows the combination that unlocks a life, he quietly admits the whole search is for a secret that keeps slipping past him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Commissioned as the Mission: Impossible 2 theme on Lalo Schifrin's riff — the bridge keeps his 5/4 — while Durst insisted the lyric is personal and has nothing to do with the film.","themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"self-contempt redirected outward so the world's hatred lands before the singer can confess his own","built":"the braggart and the loser speak as two voices never admitted to share the same person"},
"limp-bizkit~the-channel":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects mass media, false heroes, and manufactured reality; they've tuned into a personal frequency of authenticity and offer that same channel to others seeking escape from lies.","opus":"A litany of everything Durst rejects, media, crooked officials, self-destructing heroes, hollow success, framed as tuning into a private frequency shared with a like-minded listener. Alienation reworked into defiant belonging.","sonnet":"A list of grievances against fakeness in media, music, and relationships — Fred Durst positions himself as a channel for genuine feeling that his listeners recognize, even if nobody asked for it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A litany of dislikes that never resolves into a manifesto: the verses reject radio, sold-out officials, suicided heroes, the industry's parasites, even the band and its own music, until refusal becomes the only stable identity. The chorus reframes all that negation as a private frequency between two people already tuned in, so the point was never the hatreds but the fact of being received. The bridge turns it inside out, dropping the pretense that any opinion matters and admitting the real need is simply to be kept on—the station someone dials when they are lost. So the aggression is exposed as a mask over dependence, and the listener's hatred is diagnosed as recognition, the discomfort of relating to what they claim to despise.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The one track carrying John Otto's own drumming — the Siegler re-records replaced him everywhere else.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance"],"means":"aggression exposed as a mask over the need to be received by one person","built":"the bridge drops all opinion to admit the real claim is simply being kept on"},
"limp-bizkit~the-key":{"haiku":"Mental chaos and anger trapped inside; the speaker searches for the right key to unlock and release the fury locked in their mind.","opus":"Limp Bizkit stage a tense, claustrophobic hunt for relief, the narrator long past knowing where he stands and half-ready to commit himself. Trapped among countless options, he's convinced only one will quiet his fury, and rather than quietly disappear he vows to keep testing possibilities until it kills him.","sonnet":"Frustration at losing one's sense of self drives an obsessive search through an interior maze. The narrator wanders locked corridors inside their own mind, trying key after key without success — each attempt another admission that anger has no exit and identity no clear chamber to rest in.","src":"haiku","fable":"The bravado of the opening collapses almost immediately into a man who has lost the thread of his own performance and admits he is unraveling. The key he keeps reaching for is a way to unlock and put down his rage — the horror underneath the swagger being that door after door opens onto nothing, so the only lock he can imagine finally opening is his own death.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","madness & the mind","anger & defiance"],"means":"rage turned against itself when every exit from it opens onto nothing","built":"the key as metaphor undoes the bravado door by door, landing finally on death"},
"limp-bizkit~the-one":{"haiku":"A lonely man searching for romantic connection hopes a potential partner could be 'the one,' acknowledging that chemistry and commitment require genuine effort and chance-taking.","opus":"Out of loneliness and misery, the speaker hopes a particular person could be the one for him, weighing whether they might be soulmates and pleading with them to stay.","sonnet":"A lonely person, worn down by failed searches for connection, wonders whether the person in front of them could finally be the right match — acknowledging uncertainty while clinging to the possibility.","src":"haiku","fable":"Fred Durst wears the whole grammar of the pickup line as if it were sincerity, and the song runs on the gap between that swagger and the man dead of loneliness underneath it. Every declaration of belief is undercut by conditional tense: not this is the one but this could be, a hope kept safely unfalsifiable so it can never quite be disproven or claimed. The bridge names the trick outright, cycling through maybe so, maybe not, the grass always greener somewhere else, until courtship reveals itself as a permanent hedge against commitment rather than a step toward it. Then the coda drops the bravado entirely: the addressee is told to hide away in the same breath she's begged to stay, the plea to blow me away landing as both worship and a wish to be annihilated. What the refrain's endless repetition is really doing is holding a door open the singer is too frightened to walk through.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"every declaration kept in conditional tense so hope stays safely unclaimed","built":"the coda's plea to be blown away collapses adoration into a wish for annihilation"},
"limp-bizkit~the-only-one":{"haiku":"A man refuses to rush physical intimacy or waste time on superficial connections. He values restraint and only commits when someone genuinely deserves his attention.","opus":"A seduction song dressed as restraint: the narrator brags that he's holding out for sex until the partner proves she deserves him, framing his self-control and patience as what makes him uniquely worth the wait before the eventual physical payoff.","sonnet":"The narrator positions himself as rare for refusing to rush into sex, insisting he'll hold out until the other person has genuinely earned it — less about romance than about his own sense of worth and self-control.","src":"haiku","fable":"The bravado sells self-control as a gift to the woman, but the song keeps tripping over its own logic: the man who claims a stronger will and no wasted time spends three verses talking himself, not her, out of bed, and the payoff he's holding out for is bluntly physical. What reads as restraint is really appraisal turned into leverage—she has to prove she deserves him, and the hedge that she might turn out empty inside is a preemptive excuse against being the one found wanting. The refrain drains toward nothing again and again, and that collapse is the tell: the whole edifice of patience and standards keeps grounding out on a hollow note, the mask of high-mindedness thinning to reveal ordinary fear of rejection underneath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","artifice & performance","shame & repression"],"means":"patience performed as leverage with rejection fear draining through the hollow refrain","built":"the refrain's collapse to a hollow note undercuts the patience-as-gift argument each time"},
"limp-bizkit~the-priest":{"haiku":"A predatory person exploits the priesthood's authority to access victims and hide abuse behind religious institutional protection.","opus":"A grim horror narrative about a predator who dons the collar precisely because the church shields his abuse of children. Durst breaks the fiction to rage directly at real clerical crimes and his own loss of trust in the world.","sonnet":"A narrator decides to become a priest specifically to use the institution's cover to indulge his appetites and abuse children — then breaks the fourth wall to react with genuine horror at real clergy abuse scandals, unsure how to protect his own kid in a world he can't trust.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages an interior monologue that curdles from confession into detection. For its first two-thirds it inhabits a predator who reasons his way into the priesthood precisely because the collar is perfect camouflage, the institution he pledges to serve becoming cover for his appetite. His recurring need for a fresh outlet to feed makes the motive coldly appetitive rather than spiritual. The chorus welds the two poles of the mask into one phrase, a sanctified baptism grinding against terror at the flaw underneath, holy vocabulary and rot fused rather than merely opposed. The bridge shatters this ventriloquism by cutting to an ordinary parent frozen at a television, where the same catalogue of horrors — the molesting priest, the terrorist, the onstage murder of Dimebag Darrell — is now watched helplessly from outside. That father's dread over whether he can trust anyone with his own child is what the predator-monologue was secretly building toward, ending not in the sinner's self-justification but in a helpless rage with nowhere trustworthy to turn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The lyric itself names the 2004 onstage killing of Dimebag Darrell among the horrors on the parent's screen; the EP's documented themes are propaganda, the Catholic abuse cases, terrorism and fame.","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"predator logic inhabiting holy vocabulary until a parent's dread unmasks it","built":"the bridge cuts from inside the monologue to a father at a television, shattering the ventriloquism"},
"limp-bizkit~the-propaganda":{"fable":"The song wears the mask of a violence-crazed narrator courting his own outlaw legend, but the framing device pulls that mask off: everything the maniac says is a product being packaged and sold, and the manufactured rebellion he embodies is bought by an audience the chorus calls innocent. Meaning develops by descent through structure: the verses inflate the dangerous persona, the chorus reveals that persona as merchandise, and the bridge strips it down to the crux underneath, a creator addressing his own creation with the flat verdict that this phony, fading thing owes him nothing because he made it. That last claim cuts two ways at once, the artist speaking to the marketed self he invented and the machine speaking to the consumer it manufactured, so the final repeated line lands as both a boast and an indictment of the whole selling. The refusal to resolve it into one speaker is the reading: propaganda works precisely by collapsing the maker and the made into a single voice you can no longer separate.","fableDeep":"The sharpest line indicts the singer's own business model: 'just tell them to rebel again' is a nu-metal frontman describing, with total accuracy, how nu-metal was sold — the critique of manufactured rebellion delivered by one of its manufacturers, and the song never resolves whether it knows that. The self-diagnosis verse ('severely a mental disorder, where reality is highly distorted') plays crazy as both a boast and an unreliable-narrator warning printed on the label. And the coda — 'you don't owe me, but I made you,' chanted a dozen times — aims everywhere at once: imitators, industry, audience; a creator disowned by his creations who can't stop reminding them whose mud they're made from.","haiku":"Manufactured narratives and psychological manipulation create distorted reality, with the speaker confronting exploitation and demanding autonomy.","opus":"Fred Durst turning his own hostility and instability into a mirror for a media machine that sells manufactured rebellion. It is a rant about being both the product and the maker, contempt aimed at those who buy the act.","sonnet":"The narrator rages against a media and social machine that packages rebellion and anger as product — selling manufactured outrage to the credulous — while simultaneously positioning himself as someone who created and now resents those who profited from or imitated him.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote2":"Released May 2005 with deliberately zero promotion — Borland called it self-sabotage; Ross Robinson produced, and Sammy Siegler quietly re-recorded John Otto's drums on six of the seven tracks.","themes":["artifice & performance","politics & society","identity & becoming"],"means":"rebellion packaged as product until maker and made are indistinguishable","built":"the final line stays unsourced — both boast and indictment from the same collapsed voice"},
"limp-bizkit~the-story":{"haiku":"Celebrity, fame, and cultural icons inevitably rise and fall in cycles of glory and disposability. The song reflects on how every hero becomes replaceable and forgotten.","opus":"The song reflects on fleeting moments of glory and how heroes and fame are disposable, with everyone eventually coming down from their highs. It mocks fighters, rappers, actors, and hipsters who can't do their thing anymore, urging people to live and let die.","sonnet":"The song reflects on the rise-and-fall arc of fame and identity, arguing that heroes are disposable, glory is temporary, and everyone eventually loses whatever made them relevant. It addresses people who have faded — fighters, actors, rappers, scene figures — and the hypocrisy of those who live off their past image.","src":"haiku","fable":"The refrain slowly dismantles the McCartney Bond theme, letting its command to live and let something contract phrase by phrase until only a fragment survives, then the bridge twists it into a wish that the world go on while the singer is abandoned to vanish. Framed by the E! True Hollywood Story chorus, the song names the apparatus that builds a champion expressly to broadcast his collapse, so its recurring threat of naming a successor reads as scheduled machinery rather than menace. The closing catalogue lists once-fierce fighters gone soft, performers who no longer perform, and finally the band's own coined tribe among the retired, so the record folds itself into that procession of the outdated. Its real subject is obsolescence, its own included, and the staged, doomed insistence on not being thrown away when the clock says so.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance"],"means":"obsolescence scheduled as spectacle, the band's own ending folded into the catalogue","built":"the Bond theme dissolves phrase by phrase, its contracted remnant performing the erasure it describes"},
"limp-bizkit~the-surrender":{"haiku":"Mutual vulnerability and unconditional surrender in love—the speaker admits his capacity to hurt and disappoint while yielding completely to another.","opus":"In a rare confessional mode, Limp Bizkit erase the line between accuser and accused, the narrator rejecting both the monster and victim roles because he sees himself and the listener as the same. Owning a self-sabotaging streak he can't fully kill, he lands on capitulation, repeated as the only honest way through.","sonnet":"Radical mutual recognition between two people who are equally monstrous and equally wounded. Rather than denying the damage each inflicts, the narrator owns it — and in that honesty finds something like tenderness, choosing to surrender repeatedly rather than pretend either party is above causing pain.","src":"haiku","fable":"Beneath the confrontational snarl, the song is a flattened confession of sameness: the singer refuses the roles of monster and victim only by insisting he is both, exactly as the listener is, until the aggression dissolves into a repeated act of yielding. That surrender lands as the piece's real movement, turning a stance of defiance into an admission of being helplessly bound to the other, unable either to save them or let them go.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"defiance melted into helpless binding where neither escape nor rescue is possible","built":"the shared monster-and-victim claim collapses the distance until only yielding remains"},
"limp-bizkit~the-truth":{"haiku":"The song confronts listeners with harsh truths about their own failures, mistakes, and complicity, demanding acceptance rather than denial. It frames personal accountability and defeat as the core of human nature.","opus":"Addressed to a restless, self-destructive person likened to a virus and a crater on a greater problem, the song presses them through pain and regret toward accepting the truth about themselves, invoking the Lord's Prayer's plea to be delivered from evil.","sonnet":"A stream-of-consciousness address to someone who won't face their own failings — obsessive, self-destructive, and in denial — with a closing invocation of the Lord's Prayer framing the call to truth in quasi-spiritual terms.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages a hostile interrogation of a follower by the very voice that owns them, and the whole movement is a trap closing. Second-person accusations climb from personal malfunction to a spiritual ledger, where every gain has been swapped for a debt owed to a leader you're ordered to address. The one command repeated to exhaustion, accepting the truth, is dressed as release but works as coercion, its sarcasm plain: the insult reframed as blessing is the abuser's script. That mask drops in the bridge, where a prayer for rescue is quietly rewritten, its plea sliding from the collective to the singular voice. So the appeal to be delivered from evil exposes the speaker as the evil itself, the leader installed where the deliverer should stand.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"coercion delivered as liberation until the leader occupies the deliverer's place","built":"the bridge prayer slides from collective to singular, exposing the speaker as the evil named"},
"limp-bizkit~trust":{"haiku":"The speaker denounces two-faced backstabbers and liars, vowing not to trust anyone while warning that betrayal carries violent consequences. They ultimately conclude that trust and trustworthiness are mutually impossible.","opus":"Durst's paranoid tirade against backstabbers and two-faced users, swinging between violent threats and half-hearted walk-backs, landing on a scorched worldview where he trusts no one and expects no one to trust him.","sonnet":"A venomous confrontation with a backstabber — the narrator enumerates the betrayer's two-faced scheming and warns of violent consequences, before pivoting to a weary conclusion that trust is simply impossible and the only honest position is permanent suspicion of everyone.","src":"haiku","fable":"The threats are theater that comes apart while you watch, the verses climbing from clown-shaming taunts to a cartoon fantasy of a body zipped into a car trunk and handed to a dream-stalking killer. Then the speaker reverses himself outright, dismissing his own vengeance as juvenile and reclassifying the enemy from monster-bait into merely another person left alive by default. What remains under the swagger is the deadened chant answering the title, a sealed circuit where he withholds faith because none is extended to him, worn less as an injury than as a bargain he has quietly settled into.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance"],"means":"the vengeance fantasy dissolved before it lands, leaving a settled bargain of mutual withheld faith","built":"the speaker dismantles his own threat mid-song, reclassifying the enemy from monster to nonentity"},
"limp-bizkit~turn-it-up-bitch":{"haiku":"Hypermasculine nu-metal braggadocio with contempt for charts and critics; aggressive assertion of raw, unpolished rock authority.","opus":"A brash victory lap where Fred Durst crowns himself the redneck king of nu-metal, name-checking DJ Lethal and daring everyone to crank the aggression, all swagger, insults and self-aware jokes about being a one-hit wonder who never left. Limp Bizkit, the Jacksonville rap-rock provocateurs, lean fully into their meathead party persona here, the whole point being volume, bravado, and the refusal to apologize for either.","sonnet":"A loud return-of-the-band announcement from the nu-metal trenches — self-aware about the genre's reputation, leaning into the redneck-from-the-sticks persona, calling out phonies and cash-chasing while claiming the authentic crown anyway. The energy is pure provocation, more interested in the volume of the claim than any argument behind it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A middle-aged frontman plants his flag on being a punchline, insisting the band still hits with force and refusing to pretend the culture has moved past him, and he sneers the has-been verdict at himself before any listener can beat him to it. That preemptive self-roast is the trick: the backwoods cracker who names his own irrelevance, then struts through it anyway on a tequila haze, so the studio boasting reads as a man fashioning his own outdatedness into armor. The hook widens that stubbornness into a shared craving, framing the command to crank the volume not as swagger but as a remedy everyone secretly needs, because dumb loud release is the whole point when nothing beneath it wants earning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","party & hedonism","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"self-roasting the has-been verdict before anyone else can, then strutting through it anyway","built":"the hook widens personal stubbornness into communal remedy, making dumb loud release a shared prescription"},
"limp-bizkit~underneath-the-gun":{"haiku":"A person trapped under pressure and lonely isolation beats themselves down, takes blame for everything, and contemplates checking out as their own worst enemy.","opus":"Self-loathing and burnout, with the narrator naming himself the person who sabotages him most, overworked, isolated, and sure he has failed. Pinned by his own accusations, he keeps checking out and fleeing the blame he can never stop pinning on himself, convinced he lives on nothing but old memories.","sonnet":"Chronic self-sabotage and isolation: the narrator is his own worst enemy, working obsessively while loneliness and self-blame pile up until he considers checking out entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"The spoken intro sets the frame that turns the whole song inward: the enemy being fought is the self, so every later accusation and demand for blame has only one target. The two-word chorus does the structural work — the sun (exposure, being seen, nowhere to hide) and the gun (threat, deadline, the barrel at your back) rhyme into a single condition where visibility and coercion become the same pressure, and the relentless refrain about working so hard shows a person trying to earn his way out of a trap he built. It escalates from exhaustion to a confession of cowardice, where taking the blame and fleeing with it are the same act — carrying guilt is indistinguishable from running from it. The two bridges name the collapse: first a quiet checking-out, a self-erasure spoken almost politely, and then a scorching argument with an unnamed other whose being right depends entirely on the speaker being wrong, so the loop closes with the mask (the fight with someone else) peeled back to reveal the real combatant underneath, which was himself the whole time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"About suicide, by Durst's own account — the struggle when ending your life becomes an option; creativity and the hope of true love, he said, kept it from becoming one more often.","themes":["shame & repression","surveillance & control","identity & becoming"],"means":"self-accusation performed as attack until the mask falls and leaves only himself","built":"the sun-and-gun rhyme fuses visibility with coercion into one inescapable condition"},
"limp-bizkit~walking-away":{"haiku":"Letting go of everything one loves because staying leads only to pain; walking away from cherished things is the only way to survive.","opus":"A resolution to walk away from everything and everyone the narrator loves and needs, telling the truth for once and treating his own pain as a sign that it's time to leave.","sonnet":"The speaker feels trapped in a cycle of tragedy and emotional baggage they cannot escape, and chooses to deliberately walk away from everything they care about as the only honest path forward. The act of leaving is framed not as abandonment but as an admission of reality.","src":"haiku","fable":"The bravado band writes a bare confessional here, and the read lives in that gap: no swagger, no wordplay, just a man announcing his own departure from everything he loves. The title phrase inverts the usual escape logic — verse one admits that running away only loops him back to the same hole, so the leaving in the hook isn't flight toward freedom but a stripping-away, a truth told for once by cutting loose the very things he needs. What underlies the mask is the quiet violence of that arithmetic: he treats his attachments as baggage to be burned to the ground, and the only proof he's finally honest is the sign of his own bleeding, so walking away reads less as recovery than as a self-wounding he's chosen to call clarity.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","shame & repression"],"means":"departure framed as clarity that costs the speaker the things that kept him whole","built":"the title phrase inverts escape logic — leaving loops back rather than outward"},
"limp-bizkit~why-try":{"haiku":"Aggressive dominance and destruction of competitors through confidence and violence, with contempt for those who try to challenge or oppose.","opus":"A braggadocious battle rap taunting rivals and rap contenders with threats of destruction and violent knockouts. The narrator crowns himself the top predator, promising anyone who challenges him will fry.","sonnet":"A bravado-heavy battle rap framing the speaker as an unstoppable force who will destroy any rival — with boxing and street-fight imagery throughout — arguing that opponents are already beaten before they start.","src":"haiku","fable":"A wall-to-wall battle-rap flex, Limp Bizkit’s reunion-era throwback to their oldest register, where Fred Durst stacks cartoonish threats faster than any one of them can land, so the point isn't the individual boast but the sheer overwhelming volume of them. The song runs on a boxing conceit, dragging a beaten opponent through round after round, and the hook mock-solemnly consigns each challenger to a heavenly gate, borrowing the language of judgment and damnation only to swagger through it as pure showmanship. The one flicker of something underneath the mask comes when Durst admits kinship with the hatred and the crying even as he deals them out, the underdog now cast as killer, betraying that all this manufactured menace is still built on the chip-on-the-shoulder resentment the persona was designed to armor over.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance","violence & murder"],"means":"cartoonish menace as armor over chip-on-the-shoulder resentment","built":"the admission of kinship with the hatred and crying betrays the wound under the flex","fnote":"Carries a self-referential nod back to the debut Three Dollar Bill, Y'all$."},
"limp-bizkit~you-bring-out-the-worst-in-me":{"haiku":"Haunting and possession by a harmful relationship; emotional torment that follows even after physical separation.","opus":"A vengeful Halloween-themed haunting, the narrator returning as a ghost to possess a cruel lover who stabbed and cursed him, carving him like a pumpkin all for nothing, now sworn to invade her dreams and screams. Limp Bizkit, the Jacksonville nu-metal band, wrap heartbreak in horror-movie menace here, a surprisingly bitter revenge fantasy dressed in the theatrical spite of a jilted, unquiet spirit.","sonnet":"A Halloween-framed haunting — the speaker becomes a ghost determined to inhabit the dreams and screams of someone who blackened their heart. The wound was real and unresolved; what feels like possession is actually grief turned obsessive, the speaker refusing to be forgotten by someone who hurt them and moved on.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song wearing a Halloween costume: the singer casts himself as a vengeful ghost who will invade his ex's sleep and possess her, the seasonal imagery (haunting, a heart gone black, being carved open like a jack-o-lantern) letting him dress festering resentment up as playful spookiness. What lets the mask slip is the verse where the horror-movie pose drops into flat grievance—the accusation of being knifed and cursed for nothing—so the possession fantasy reads less as fun menace than as an inability to let go. The final swerve to marriage names it outright, exposing the whole haunting as a portrait of love turned into a refusal to leave, where devotion and torment have become the same thing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"possession fantasy as portrait of devotion turned into refusal to leave","built":"Halloween costume drops at the flat-grievance verse, exposing festering resentment beneath the spook"},
"lindemann~ach-so-gern-pain-version":{"haiku":"Predatory desire metastasizes into abuse; the speaker rationalizes violation as romance while victims bear lasting wounds from feigned tenderness.","opus":"An unrepentant seducer's confession, boasting of kissing countless women while insinuating that his desire crossed lines into coercion, leaving broken hearts and tears in his wake. The snake-and-rabbit imagery casts his predatory appetite as a venom no one could resist.","sonnet":"A narrator catalogues a lifetime of seduction without mercy — kissing women everywhere but the mouth, knowing no pity when they whispered \"nein,\" watching hearts break and tears flow — and the confession arrives as self-aware boast: he was called heartless and shameless, and the truth, he admits, lies somewhere in between.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~allesfresser":{"haiku":"A creature with insatiable hunger devours everything, preferring young, tender flesh; the lyrics describe ravenous consumption with obsessive desire.","opus":"Lindemann turns gluttony into a leering horror-comedy: an omnivorous appetite obsessed with the young, tender and fresh, the food language sliding into predatory and sexual menace. Classic Rammstein-frontman provocation, taboo staged as nursery-rhyme singalong.","sonnet":"A grotesque celebration of gluttony — the narrator is obsessed with consuming everything, particularly young, fresh, tender things: veal, piglets, lambs, chicks. The repeated \"feed me\" refrain is both literal and vaguely cannibalistic in tone, Rammstein-adjacent in its use of food as transgression.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~blut":{"haiku":"Family bonds tie through blood as both connection and weight; obligation mingles with love, and letting go becomes an act of mercy.","opus":"A gothic meditation on blood as the tie binding a father to his children, the red band running from heart to hands as an obligation to protect and love whether he wills it or not, shadowed by the cold approach of death. Lindemann is the side project of Rammstein frontman Till Lindemann, and this German-language track carries his trademark guttural theatricality and morbid, visceral imagery into industrial-metal grandeur.","sonnet":"Blood as obligation and trap — from heart to hands to skull to legs, a red thread binds family members together whether they want it or not. The children are carried like tonnage, the duty of love described as blunt and compulsory, and when the blade finally speaks in extremity and colorless death takes hold, the only release is letting it out. A cold hand at the back of the neck, inescapable.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~cowboy":{"haiku":"A performer mocks the fantasy of being a tough, virile cowboy, revealing the performer's weakness and aging body behind a constructed image of dominance.","opus":"The Rammstein frontman's side project mocks macho fantasy with deadpan relish. It opens on a boy's dream of being a fearsome gunslinger who conquers any woman and outrages every jealous husband, then flips the joke: the narrator admits he was never tough, rocks only in his chair and stores his dentures away, collapsing the whole swaggering myth into a sad, drunken, impotent punchline.","sonnet":"A childhood dream of masculine invincibility — the hard-riding, lady-killing, never-dying cowboy myth — runs head-on into the deflating reality of old age and false teeth. The fantasy curdles into dark comedy as dentures go in a cup and the heroic icon vomits in his own hat.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~frau-mann":{"haiku":"Opposites attract and remain bound through paradox; despite contradiction, one cannot function without their opposite half.","opus":"A rapid-fire catalogue of opposites, black and white, rich and poor, man and woman, insisting that opposites attract and that without love nothing works, the singer unable to forget one particular person. Lindemann, the project of Rammstein's Till Lindemann, wraps its playful list of antonyms in industrial pomp and a sing-song hook, the German lyric using stark binaries to circle back to helpless, inescapable longing.","sonnet":"Opposites as the engine of desire — a long catalogue of contraries (hot and cold, ugly and beautiful, war and peace, woman and man) builds toward the obvious conclusion that opposites attract and that love is the one force threading through all of them. The final admission, buried in the wordplay, is that the speaker cannot forget a specific person regardless of how neatly the universe arranges itself into pairs.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~golden-shower":{"haiku":"Sexual humiliation and bodily fluid exchange presented as fantasy and desire, framed as sensual indulgence and erotic control.","opus":"An explicit, provocative celebration of urolagnia: the narrator kneels and begs a partner to urinate on him, framing the act with mock-romantic imagery of champagne, fairies, and angels crying; the crude, repeated demands are deliberately shocking, treating the fetish as ecstatic worship.","sonnet":"A literal, graphic request for a golden shower — the narrator describing the act with lascivious detail and mock-reverent language, presenting urophilia as a shared erotic game.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~gummi":{"haiku":"A speaker confesses an intense sexual fetish for rubber/latex, reveling in its tactile sensations and refusing to remove it. The song eroticizes the material's texture and the speaker's obsession with keeping it on.","opus":"Lindemann, the project fronted by Till Lindemann of Rammstein and known for gleeful provocation, confesses a sudden fetish for rubber. The narrator revels in a second skin that clings to every fold and feels intoxicating when he sweats, refusing ever to take it off. A cascading inventory of rubber garments and objects turns the fixation into a deadpan, exhaustive catalogue of pure tactile desire.","sonnet":"A sudden, all-consuming latex fetish is announced with cheerful sincerity — the material clings to every fold, smells right, and has replaced all other clothing permanently. The obsession is its own punchline, delivered deadpan through a catalog of rubber objects that escalates into devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~home-sweet-home":{"haiku":"A parasitic infection takes root within the body as hunger and disease spread silently, consuming bone and life with inexorable patience.","opus":"Cancer imagined as an intruder that moves in and makes a home of the body, eating from the inside while the host stays helpless. Lindemann's relish for grotesque bodily horror turns a terminal illness into something that nests, feeds, and never leaves.","sonnet":"A parasitic illness is personified as an uninvited resident that enters the body, eats through bone, and grows without ever leaving — the speaker witnessing its slow, silent, violent progress from the outside with horror and helplessness.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~ich-wei-es-nicht":{"haiku":"A person wanders alone stripped of all identity and memory, unable to recognize themselves, their past, or any fixed point in existence, left only with aimless motion.","opus":"Till Lindemann, stepping outside Rammstein for his own project, sings a German wanderer stripped of all identity, unable to name himself or recall where he came from or is going. Every day and night forgotten, he walks endlessly, always the one the rain falls on, sunlight everywhere but above him. Needing no mirror because his own face means nothing, he moves only at night and hides from light.","sonnet":"A figure wanders without name, origin, or destination — stripped of all memory and self-knowledge. Rain always falls on him alone; sunshine belongs to everyone else. He searches for something he cannot name, walks only at night, hides from mirrors, and carries the full weight of not knowing who he is.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~knebel":{"haiku":"Life is hard and unpredictable; the narrator loves a silenced, powerless person and can't reconcile their own contradictory feelings.","opus":"Lindemann cataloging life's simple pleasures twists each one toward menace, preferring a partner best with a gag in her mouth. Rammstein's Till Lindemann leans into provocation, pairing sunshine and forests with tears, a broken heart and repeated declarations of hatred. Life is simply too hard, the narration concludes, easier only when the silenced other cannot answer back.","sonnet":"A German speaker lists his pleasures — nature, animals, full glasses, empty streets — and returns each time to one specific domestic fixation involving a gagged partner. The song pivots into hatred at the end. Life is heavy, the world spins indifferently, and what holds attention is the power dynamic at the center of a relationship that has curdled into contempt.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~ladyboy":{"haiku":"Sexual attraction and pleasure with a sex worker are portrayed frankly and without emotional attachment. The encounter is transactional and purely physical.","opus":"Lindemann chase pure physical appetite, a narrator infatuated with a ladyboy he treats as a plaything, picking pleasure over affection and gloating that he's found everything he wants in one. Rammstein's singer wields calculated shock, spurning tenderness in favor of raw craving. The track flaunts its transgression as the whole point, desire drained of feeling into gleeful, provocative excess.","sonnet":"Desire unburdened by romance — a transactional, gleeful lust for a partner who combines what he wants without the complications of love. No broken hearts, no bad romance: just pleasure taken with full awareness of what it is and is not. The speaker celebrates the arrangement as its own perfect system, shot through with a satisfied, amoral directness.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~praise-abort":{"haiku":"Parenthood as life-ruining burden; the speaker regrets having children and expresses dark fantasy of never having reproduced.","opus":"A deliberately provocative, misanthropic rant from a man overwhelmed and embittered by six children he resents; hating his life, wife, and kids, he darkly wishes he'd chosen abortion and vows to abstain from sex, using shock and dark humor to vent regret over parenthood.","sonnet":"A deliberately provocative, darkly comic catalog of a man's misery — too many children he didn't want, an unfaithful wife, broke while his friends prosper, leading him to the bitter conclusion that abortion would have been a mercy; grotesque self-pity played for black comedy.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~skills-in-pills":{"haiku":"Chemical inversion of reality and feeling: pills transform opposites into their inverses—grief becomes joy, snow becomes heat—offering escape by making all sensation meaningless or inverted.","opus":"Lindemann, the English-language side project of Rammstein's Till Lindemann, cackles through a pharmaceutical carnival with a tablet for every mood: depression, aggression, energy, sleep, and stamina. The catalog inverts everything so up becomes down and mourning turns to amusement, escalating toward starting the cycle over and welcoming death, a deadpan joke about chemical escape as a grim dead end.","sonnet":"A sardonic inventory of pharmaceutical dependency — a pill for every mood, state, dysfunction, and appetite — inverts all sensation until opposites lose meaning entirely. The catalogue is both comic and bleak, ending in the quiet admission that the cycle of numbing and stimulation ultimately circles back to the first pill and a wish to die.","src":"haiku"},
"lindemann~steh-auf":{"haiku":"A mother remains depressed and immobilized in bed while her child begs her to rise and engage with life.","opus":"A devastating scene of a child (or partner) pleading with a mother to get up on a beautiful day, describing boats and circus animals to coax her; the dawning horror is that she is dead, the wet bed, pale cheeks and black holes in her arms revealing a fatal overdose the innocent narrator can't fully grasp.","sonnet":"A child pleading with their mother to get up — there's a beautiful day outside, a circus in town, but the mother lies in bed with open eyes, pale cheeks, and track marks in her arms. The song is about a child watching a parent destroyed by addiction.","src":"haiku"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~1-f-no-kansyoku":{"src":"web","web":"A nocturnal meditation on the dissolution of a relationship: wind, midnight sirens, and vivid illusions serve as stand-ins for a person the narrator is trying to forget, but the cold physical memory of their touch keeps pulling them back before they finally let go.","fable":"Ling tosite sigure's second album turns a breakup into a night ride, and this song builds its meaning by letting the wind do the leaving so the singer never has to. Every image of forgetting and releasing a lover is passive or displaced onto the weather: you are rocked by the wind, the vivid dream weeps in the dark, a hand is let go as if to see what the future feels like, never as a decision owned. The band's trademark falsetto shredding against a clean guitar line stages the same split the words do, a cold sensation held at arm's length from the person feeling it. The English refrain that the night is inside you slowly curdles into the admission that he is coming to hate it, the one direct verb in a song otherwise built to avoid direct verbs. It closes not on healing but on descent, leaping down into a vivid phantom of a night once seen, so the escape and the drowning turn out to be the same motion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title cites the 1/f fluctuation — the flicker spectrum of heartbeats, candle flames and running water that Japanese popular science credits with a calming effect — inverted by the song into a cold touch.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"every image of leaving displaced onto weather, never owned as decision","built":"closing descent making escape and drowning the same downward motion"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~24reverse":{"haiku":"Memory unravels backward into gray formlessness; the past dissolves into someone else's dream beyond touch or recovery.","src":"haiku","web":"Aligning a sequence of remembered nights with memory's reverse, the narrator attempts to undo the loss of someone by rewinding inward — a melancholy topology of fragmented consciousness and the impossibility of truly recovering what has been lost."},
"ling-tosite-sigure~5": {"src":"web","web":"Acquiring something irreplaceable ignites immediate terror of losing it, and the resulting paradox — gaining strength makes one weaker — swallows the narrator whole, leaving them to wrestle with their own ugliness while still yearning for something permanent and uncontrolled.","fable":"A song about the terror hidden inside having something to lose. TK keeps asking the same question two ways — grasping the unbreakable thing, did I get stronger, or did I get weaker? — and never lets either answer win, because the thing he must protect is also swallowing him whole. That deadlock is why the tenderness keeps curdling into cannibalism: the wish to devour the beloved, to be torn free of a shining future, is love pushed until protection and destruction become the same gesture. The closing turn is the strangest and the truest — he asks not to be spared inferiority but to be hunted by it, and to have imagination's rule over him not lifted but seized and ruled back. It reads the trap he built: the more precious the thing, the less free the self that holds it, so he'd rather break the cage than be kept safe inside it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"love pushed until protection and destruction are one gesture","built":"the closing plea inverts the trap — begging for the cage's hunter not its key"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~a-1xjjcbh":{"src":"web","web":"Plunging through a tunnel into a chaotic, mad world, the narrator chases fantasies still just out of reach, calling out to merge with invincibility and explode into a mirage — pure velocity and longing wrapped in a whirlwind of delusion.","fable":"Ling tosite sigure build these lyrics as a sensory overload that keeps pushing past its own limits: the sleepless highway, the shuttle through a cramped space, an impulse that has already outrun imagination diving into the unknown. The chorus reaches for total dissolution — a wish to melt into invincibility, a tornado dancing in a sealed room as the ultimate fusion — and the verses deliberately withhold arrival, the dream-wandering that still won't reach, the fantasy the singer keeps going to meet. What gives the song its edge is the drop back into daylight: waking to a partitioned meeting, the hard floor becoming a habit, a repeated ache to escape, so the euphoric surges read as a rave-lit, chemically-tinged flight from ordinary grey work rather than as innocent bliss. That undertow curdles the ecstasy into predation — the real want is to steal your soul, to devour the emergency, to stab a red flash into you, culminating in the taunting give-me-your-DEAD and the closing chant of tornado-and-fresh-brain as brain navigation, where the invincible fusion is finally exposed as a craving to consume another consciousness whole. The band's signature works here precisely because the exhilaration and the menace are voiced in the same breathless register, so you can't tell rapture from hunger — which is the point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The closing couplet sets the coinage beside the straight word — 旋風 (whirlwind) against 鮮脳, which is 洗脳 (brainwashing) with wash swapped for its homophone 鮮, fresh — while the title verbs the tornado itself.","themes":["freedom & escape","addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder"],"means":"euphoric escape exposed as appetite to consume another consciousness","built":"exhilaration and menace delivered in the same breathless register"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~a-7days-wonder":{"haiku":"Time collapses into seven repetitions, where seeing something for a week renders it both visible and forgotten.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that dresses a disappearance as a magic trick, then keeps insisting through repetition that the vanished person is still perfectly visible. The spell that pulverizes everything the eye can hold, the count to seven that ends with the other person stopping their own breath and smiling, and the interjected phrase asking who is there over the line all render an intimate loss as a party conjuring act. Ling tosite sigure's signature is exactly this collision of nursery lightness and dread, and the title carries it: a nine-days' wonder cut down to seven, a marvel guaranteed to be forgotten as soon as it dazzles. The tense drifts from now I can see to you can be seen, so the chant becomes a refusal to let the trick finish its own erasure. What the mask of playfulness hides is grief hardening into a loop that replays the moment of loss rather than mourning it.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","artifice & performance"],"means":"grief hardening into a loop that replays rather than mourns the loss","built":"tense drifts from now I can see to you can be seen, refusing the trick's own erasure"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~a-qcbdyc":{"haiku":"Deceptive words hide fractured inner worlds; touch reveals the nightmare beneath the surface.","src":"haiku","web":"Walking a path stained by liars, once-familiar words change shape when the eyes close and flashbacks bleed into blasphemous dreams — a surreal inward journey through distorted memory, violent colour, and the elusive, uncertain question of whether God is there at all.","fable":"A stalker's love song that keeps performing the same self-erasing ritual — hide the words, shut the eyes — in order to be looked at, so that surveillance and surrender become the same act. The title itself, a katakana smear of destruction and night's dream, refuses to resolve into either, and the compulsive repetition of that phrase enacts the ruin it names. The embedded English marks the gap the song can't close: the beloved dances inside the speaker's mind yet knows nothing of it, an intimacy that exists only one-way. Against that, the pleading invitation to touch, to reach across the dream and search inside him, tries to convert being spied-on into being finally, willingly entered.","fv":2,"fnote":"The major bow: after two indie records, just A moment arrived on Sony in May 2009 — written, produced and mixed by TK himself — and became the band’s first top-10 album, peaking at number 4 on Oricon.","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"surveillance and surrender performed as the identical same act","built":"embedded english marks the one-way gap unclosable"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~a-symmetry":{"haiku":"Memory reverses and inverts the world; the reversed space distorts what remains while nothing and no one exists.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title names a wholeness thrown off by one letter, symmetry pushed a single unit into its opposite, and the song lives entirely in that gap. It scales the same wound both ways: a trivial paralysis between two guitars, Telecaster or Les Paul, balloons into an entire world flipped upside down and remade backwards. What performs the flipping is memory, blamed by name again and again, so reversal isn't done to him but is simply what remembering does, running the tape the wrong way. And it refuses resolution on purpose, turning from killing the light to shining the light with no one there, never landing on a side.","fv":2,"themes":["nostalgia & memory"],"means":"memory blamed by name for running the tape backwards without resolution","built":"the title drops one letter to convert symmetry into its opposite, naming the wound"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~abnormalize":{"haiku":"Reality fractures into gestalt illusions: the speaker hides truth in a plastic world where fakeness becomes the only visible constant.","opus":"An overflow of things too shameful to reveal to anyone, adrift in a plastic, unbearably pretty realm where the speaker can find no light. What passes for reality is exposed as staged deception and an engineered mirage, and dragging the truth out would collapse the fragile spot he calls home, so the secret is kept.","sonnet":"Overflowing internal thoughts that can't be shown to anyone curdle into alienation and the impossibility of fitting in — a world perceived as a plastic illusion where even truth is terrifying, and the only habitable place is the unfulfilled, unshared self.","src":"haiku","web":"The world is already a gestalt illusion — everyone shattered into hologram pieces and stained by reality's colors — and the narrator's mind overflows with things no one can satisfy, perceiving only what they can see clearly in an expressionless, abnormal plastic normalcy.","fable":"Written by TK to open Psycho-Pass, an anime built on a system that scans and scores the human mind, the song turns that premise against itself. Its narrator overflows with things he can show no one, penned inside a reality that grants existence only to the visible and the measurable. The whole lyric runs on the friction between a synthetic, too-lovely, too-blank exterior and the concealed impulse he keeps sealed beneath it. The sickness the title names is precisely the temptation to grow ordinary, to let every surface flatten and dissolve into that manufactured sameness. His refusal is the pivot: he pleads with the future to stay unrevealed and insists reality is the inverse of the performance, because where only the seen counts as real, keeping something buried proves he still has an inner life. By the close even truth becomes a thing to dread, yet he remains, choosing a self left unsatisfied over one completed by the counterfeit, the aberrant unshowable core the only thing here that isn't staged.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","surveillance & control","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"concealment as the only proof of an interior","built":"song commissioned for a surveillance anime turns the scanner on its own narrator","fvr":"2.4"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~abnormalize-10th-anniversary-mix":{"haiku":"Reality splinters into performative artifice as the speaker drowns in a falsified world; exposure of truth becomes both healing and destructive.","opus":"An overflow of things too shameful to show anyone, lost in a plastic, too-beautiful world where the speaker can't see any light. What we take as reality is a fake show and gestalt illusion, and exposing the truth would shatter the fragile place he calls home, so the secret stays.","sonnet":"The world is a plastic hologram where only visible surfaces pass as reality, and the speaker — unable to see light in this beautiful, abnormalized order — tears at fake colours and secrets, arriving at a gestalt illusion where fakes hold hands and mirror everything back, leaving no place that is truly theirs.","src":"haiku"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~acoustic":{"haiku":"Winter landscapes and December silence resonate through memory, where time obscures the boundary between past and present.","src":"haiku","web":"Emotions that cannot return drift through photographs as winter light fractures and time erases the scenery — appearing and vanishing like a dream, the memories of what was shared dissolve into white blankness, leaving the narrator holding only a haunting, irretrievable atmosphere.","fable":"A memory keeps flickering into being and out of it until the song names what remains as its title word, acoustic, the bare resonance of a December that a photograph could not hold. The whole piece runs on dissolution: a summer night's shadow that appears and vanishes, glass-like winter, a head gone blank white because time keeps swapping one scene for another. The title is the confession that all that survives the fade is an unplugged, undressed reverberation, the feeling still ringing after the image itself is gone.","fv":2,"themes":["nostalgia & memory"],"means":"bare resonance surviving after the image itself dissolves","built":"the title names what remains when memory outpaces photograph"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~akai-yuuwaku":{"haiku":"Untouchable red seduction cycles between violence and desire in a room where meaning dissolves into visceral impulse.","opus":"Fixation on red-a red room, red memory, a heel in blue shoes-fuses violent desire and suicide, the singer screaming something wordless at a woman he can only address, unable to be seen or heard while clinging to the memory of her song.","sonnet":"Red suspicion and blue accusation orbit a room — a gaze that wounds, a memory of someone's singing that can't be unlearned — while the speaker demands a future seen without that person and simultaneously cannot stop being haunted by them.","src":"haiku","web":"Fragmented sensory obsession — red memories, blue eyes like stabs, screaming into the void — captures the psychological pain of entanglement with someone whose presence and impending absence feel equally violent, the narrator desperate to be heard and terrified of a future without them.","fable":"Early, feral Ling Tosite Sigure works by cutting between two colors: a red of the room and of memory, and a blue that stabs. The gaze fixes downward on heels and a blue shoe, desire made clinical and predatory rather than tender. TK's bridge drops the Japanese and turns to blunt English invective at a woman while pawing for pleasure. Then the second verse cracks it open, with commands to be heard, the word suicide, and the flat confession that he cannot see or hear. The swagger is a man screaming at someone who has stopped registering him. What redeems the ugliness is 345's counter-verse, wanting to show a future that only exists with her. The red temptation of the title is memory itself, an obsession narrated as violence done and violence he cannot release.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"TK’s early sheets run on a red/blue circuit — this song’s red room and stabbing blue, Sergio Echigo’s blue song and withered flowers, autumn’s red memory an album over — color doing the work the names refuse.","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","nostalgia & memory","anger & defiance"],"means":"obsession narrated as violence that cannot release itself","built":"TK's bridge abandons Japanese for blunt English invective, the language-switch enacting the mask slipping off"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~aki-no-kehai-no-arpeggio":{"haiku":"Autumn's deceptive melody lingers over lost summer days; sadistic arpeggio echoes seasons passed that refuse to return.","opus":"The suspiciously vivid signs of autumn trigger a flood of dishonest, half-invented memories of a passed season, a transparent phantom leaving one millimeter of image and scent behind as it fades away for good.","sonnet":"The onset of autumn — almost too on-the-nose, almost fake — triggers an inventory of lost days, fabricated stories, and a season whose emotional residue lingers exactly one millimetre inside the body before fading.","src":"haiku","web":"Against a backdrop of unconcerned weekdays and a story that was only ever lies, the narrator grasps at red memories and shallow recollections of a Sunday unconcerned with dreams — autumn's faint approach underscoring the irreversibility of what was lost.","fable":"The whole song hangs on one refrain, uso kusai kurai, so fake it reeks, fixed to the faint sign of autumn, and every memory arrives contaminated by that suspicion of forgery. TK runs remembrance through the calendar, a poised Monday, a misplaced Tuesday, a Friday he would rather not recall, days emptied of event and kept only as labels. That is how you hold at arm's length a season you are trying not to grieve. The chorus names the mechanism: the arpeggio turns sadistic because dreaming a season already gone means re-wounding yourself with a beauty you half invented. The outro asks the honest question underneath, whether the melody was ever more than performance, then leaves a one-millimeter trace of picture and scent inside each person as all else fades.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["nostalgia & memory","artifice & performance","death & grief"],"means":"memory held at arm's length by suspicion that the beauty was ever real","built":"the arpeggio named as sadistic in the chorus — the form itself re-wounds by replaying what it half invented"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~am-3-45":{"haiku":"The world dissolves into weightless swimming, where forgetting is the only motion available.","src":"haiku","fable":"The entire song is one line repeated: the world disappears, is forgotten, a weightless swim. On Inspiration is DEAD, TK builds Ling tosite sigure's signature seizure-tight math-rock around jagged breakage and screamed dual vocals, so committing an entire track to a single mantra is a structural statement — the refusal to develop is the content. What reads as serenity (weightlessness, drifting) is delivered as compulsion: the phrase loops not because peace has been reached but because the mind cannot let go of the escape it keeps rehearsing, the way a 3:45 AM insomniac replays the one thought that promises oblivion. The image undercuts itself — a swim implies effort and water, not the void it claims to be — so the dream of dissolving the world is exposed as a thing the speaker must keep actively willing, never arriving. The mask is calm; underneath is the exhaustion of a person who can only survive by repeating the wish to vanish.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"peace never reached, only the compulsion to rehearse its image","built":"swim as the mantra's image undercutting the void it claims to be"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~azayaka-na-satujin":{"haiku":"Vivid murder enacted through memory and silence: the speaker and lover destroy each other while hidden, flowers wilting.","opus":"A vivid killing rendered as beautiful gesture-letting go of a hand, murdering memory so a flower can wither-set against a cold moon and a smiling, half-hidden lover, where absence and elegant violence blur into faintly blooming fragments of sound.","sonnet":"A vivid, smiling cruelty — flowers wilting, memories killed brightly — describes a relationship whose warmth was always camouflage for a slow, colourful destruction the speaker only names once the other person is gone.","src":"haiku","web":"A vivid, color-saturated act of emotional destruction unfolds in the beloved's absence — flowers wither and bloom in cycles while the narrator processes loss alone, framing disconnection and the cold, unwitnessed death of feeling as a murder that happened without a witness.","fable":"A song that aestheticizes loss as murder, chanting the word vivid over every image until beauty and violence become the same act. The refrain 鮮やかに (vividly, brilliantly) attaches indiscriminately to a car horn, to the girl's smile, to killing itself, so the ear can no longer tell a bright memory from a bright wound. The mechanism is repetition-as-erasure: the narrator keeps saying he kills the memory and the flower withers a little, and each pass leaves the beloved fainter — she is smiling, then slightly hidden, then simply not there (君がいない). By the close the syntax turns passive and past — a brilliant murder was being carried out — as if the speaker only realizes, too late and coldly like the frozen moon, that the vivid thing he was admiring all along was her disappearance.","fv":2,"fnote":"The debut was homemade end to end: #4 arrived in November 2005 on Nakano Records, the label the trio founded themselves, with TK already writing, mixing and mastering everything. Fifteen years later it returned as #4 -Retornado-, remastered by TK himself and bundled with a reproduction of the hand-printed T-shirt the members once made in tiny numbers — by then a fan legend.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","violence & murder"],"means":"the beloved is erased one vivid repetition at a time","built":"the same adverb attaches to a car horn, a smile, and the act of killing"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~beautiful-circus":{"opus":"Luring someone into a circus of illusion staged in a crumbling twilight town, the singer knows he isn't beautiful yet drives them mad and questions how he appears through the shutter-a private, sadistic show where blankness chains into blankness.","sonnet":"The speaker baits an audience into a hall-of-mirrors illusion — a circus of empty flashbacks and plastic beauty — knowing it is all fake, and finding in that acknowledged fakeness its own perverse power to captivate.","src":"web","web":"Thrown into an illusory circus where what is \"not quite beautiful\" still drives you to madness, the narrator questions whether beauty is ever real or always a fabricated trap — pursuing it leads only to emptiness, yet people willingly participate in the collective delusion.","fable":"The circus is Ling tosite sigure's figure for spectacle-as-performed-self: TK stages an illusion in a dusk-drenched city, daring the audience to say whether everyone is telecastic or plastic. The coinage fuses broadcast transmission with the guitar's steely surface, so the question of who is authentic collapses into signal versus manufacture. The refrain flips the horror-movie flash-back into a lure, an empty chain of consciousness looping into a show whose whole confession is that it isn't beautiful, only capable of driving you mad if some light happens to show through the fakery. What makes the read turn is the last panel: he admits he can't kill this off now because you all want it, invites the shutter to be pressed, and asks how he looks — the performer confessing that his selfhood exists only inside the audience's flattering illusion, the sadism of being watched turned back on both watcher and watched.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The lyric opens by folding 凛として — the first words of the band's name — into its image of dusk, and telecastic is the band's own coinage from TK's Telecaster, the same one that titles Telecastic Fake Show.","themes":["artifice & performance","surveillance & control","identity & becoming"],"means":"performer confessing selfhood exists only in the audience's illusion","built":"telecastic pun collapsing authenticity into signal versus manufacture"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~beautiful-circus-remastered-at-metropolis":{"haiku":"Illusion and seduction create a beautiful circus where the speaker displays their trap to ensnare. Madness becomes spectacle, and conscious becomes a chain of blank moments.","opus":"A frenetic Japanese art-rock piece that stages seduction and delusion as a circus of illusion. TK's fractured, glitchy lyrics circle emptiness and chained consciousness, a performer luring an audience into a spectacle he admits isn't beautiful, only mesmerizing. The song reaches for the intoxication of manipulation, the strange intimacy of trapping and being trapped in a shared hallucination.","sonnet":"A performer or lover draws others into an illusory circus — 'beautiful but not really beautiful' — using transparent grey glass as a trap and exploiting the chain of blank consciousness; the question 'who moved into your destroyed heart?' haunts the performance, and the speaker admits they keep the audience bewitched not out of beauty but out of spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~boukan":{"haiku":"Self-knowledge collapses into shame; every memory replays as evidence of contamination and the desire to disappear.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song of dissociation whose title (Onlooking/spectating) names the wound: the speaker watches his own life from outside, unable to inhabit it. The verses set the trap of memory precisely, that the trivial recollections resurface hardest while the ones he wants to kill are the ones that seize control of his head, all against a dusk-scene he swears he has seen somewhere before. The pivot is the wish to take a knife called zero and cut apart the self that is merely impersonating him, the fake wearing his face. From there the song collapses into a hammered litany of I-don't-know, I-can't-see, I'm-filthy, I-want-to-disappear, a chant that escalates once to I-want-to-die, and the point is that the repetition is the meaning: identity has worn down to a stuck, self-erasing loop with nothing behind it, the onlooker watching himself vanish. Ling tosite sigure's signature here is formal, the frantic math-rock precision of the sound turning a suicidal mantra into something mechanical rather than confessional, so the flatness of the words is the horror.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming","suicide"],"means":"identity worn to a stuck self-erasing loop with nothing behind it","built":"math-rock precision turns a suicidal mantra mechanical, flatness as horror"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~can-you-kill-a-secret":{"haiku":"In parallel dimensions of obsessive desire, the speaker offers another person as UFO/instrument, loving them as exotic abstraction.","opus":"On a blurred cosmic dance floor, desire warps into violent speed and eight-dimensional fantasy where the beloved becomes a stupid, perfect UFO; the singer wants a secret of his own and offers the whole world in exchange for killing-or keeping-it.","sonnet":"Someone so dazzling and alien they register as a UFO reshapes reality around them, and the question of whether to surrender or destroy that secret — the intoxicating thing they represent — hangs unresolved in a parallel-universe dance floor.","src":"haiku","web":"Surreal romance conducted across parallel dimensions and UFO-lit dance floors, where the central question is whether intimacy can kill hidden truths — desire and metamorphosis collide as the speaker wonders if closeness destroys secrets or only drives them deeper.","fable":"A lover mutates past recognition until the only name left for them is an unidentified flying object, and the song asks whether wanting someone that totally differs at all from wanting them destroyed. Ling tosite sigure stage it on a strobing dancefloor of parallel and eighth-dimension space, clothes torn off at violent speed, the beloved endlessly metamorphosing while the narrator craves a secret to hold over them. The English hook levels the charge: you are that unnameable thing, so can you annihilate what stays hidden. What plays as adoration keeps sliding toward erasure, and the closing line seals the fusion — a vow of love that finishes on the flat wish to end the person addressed. The 2010 record earns it, panic dressed as a party, a tenderness unable to tell possession from murder.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"desire and the wish to erase the beloved arrive fused","built":"the closing vow of love lands on a flat wish for the person's end"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~chocolate-passion":{"haiku":"Intoxicating sweetness dissolves the boundary between bodies and selves, where melting into another becomes both pleasure and possession.","src":"haiku","fable":"Ling tosite sigure's usual dynamic is TK's shrieking guitar rock, but here the band turns its habitual violence inward, staging seduction as a chemistry of dissolution. The governing conceit is chocolate as consumption metaphor: theobroma melting on the tongue becomes a fantasy of two bodies liquefying into one, with the speaker offering herself to be swallowed even as she claims the pleasure of control. What makes it more than an erotic dare is the sour aftertaste written into every image, the bitter spice cut through the sweetness, so that the melt is also a small death, a devotion that ends in the beloved's disappearance. By the closing invocation of a perfect dessert and the coy question of whether she may eat again, the song has flipped its terms, and the one who offered to dissolve is revealed as the predator who consumes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"妖艶な蜘蛛の糸 — the bewitching spider's thread dangled down is Akutagawa's Kumo no Ito, the canonical salvation thread Buddha lowers into hell, re-lowered here as seduction into the whirlpool.","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"the one who offered to dissolve revealed as the predator who consumes","built":"bitter cut through every sweet image souring the melt into small death"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~cool-j":{"haiku":"Cold consciousness dissolves while memory claws back, leaving only the image of erasure that cannot be stopped.","src":"haiku","fable":"TK from Ling tosite sigure sings this at the edge of dissolution, and the album title Inspiration is DEAD is the key: the disappearing me and disappearing meaning that recur are not metaphor but the fear of a self and a creative spark both going dark. The song is built on a command hurled at someone with red laughing eyes and at himself alike—don't show it, don't reveal it—so that the act of hiding becomes the only agency left when memory keeps seizing his head and dragging him back to a place he doesn't want to return to. Against that erasure sits the desperate English pushback that he cannot kill himself, a jagged refusal wedged mid-phrase, and the counting of a thirteenth wind after which he already feels gone, as if survival were a matter of losing track. What the repetition itself performs is the trap: he traces memory over and over, asks tomorrow what color he will become, and the loop never resolves because the terror is precisely that resolution means vanishing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","nostalgia & memory","shame & repression"],"means":"hiding as the only agency left while memory keeps seizing control","built":"counting a thirteenth wind as if survival were a matter of losing track"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~craft": {"haiku":"Psychologically dead yet physically alive; searching for memory fragments to reconstruct self while drowning in isolation.","opus":"A haunting meditation on being technically alive but already dead inside, kept breathing only by singing; the narrator forgets a lover's name, clings to a millimeter of gravity, and bets that pulling someone close is the one thing that kills loneliness.","sonnet":"Someone who describes themselves as still dead despite breathing and having a pulse, slowly forgetting the name of the person they love, drowning in a narrow universe — music is the only thing that revives them briefly, and the only gravity keeping them alive is the tiny pull toward another person.","src":"haiku","fable":"The refrain is a paradox held perfectly still — I'm still dead, though there's a pulse and breath and voice to spare — and the song works out what kind of aliveness is left when the ordinary kind has drained away. The answer is sound: only while singing is it alright, resuscitation performed through an eardrum no one else can see, so being heard becomes the single proof of a life. Against its title's own word 滅亡 (annihilation), TK sets an absurdly small counterweight, a 1mm of gravity he keeps reaching for — not living for anyone's sake but wanting to hold that one faint pull of weight, because you get wounded exactly as many times as there are transparent things. The private truth he keeps circling is that the deepest part of the heart is alone anyway, your tears alone too. And yet the last move is toward another person, not away: let me draw you close just a little longer — loneliness can be killed. It reads a man mostly gone who locates his only surviving gravity in the wish to keep someone near for one more minute.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The song runs on 再生 — one word meaning both playback and resurrection: every replay of the track is, in its own vocabulary, a revival.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"sound as the sole proof of a life mostly already gone","built":"滅亡 (annihilation) in the title set against a 1mm counterweight in the lyric"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~crazy-kanjyou-style":{"src":"web","web":"A portrait of emotional collapse and toxic codependency: the narrator describes losing their grip on reality — colours fading, memories blurring into dreamlike scenes — while taking the blame for another person's suffering, finding grim comfort only when that person finally smiles.","fable":"A dissociative love song where the only cure the narrator can imagine for someone else's pain is his own erasure. The verses run on a single failing sense-system: a landscape the you keeps staring at while color drains out of everything, the past folded onto tomorrow until nothing reads as real, all of it pinned to a midnight refrain of CRAZY STYLE. The screaming and the greenery-tinted dreams frame a mind that can't hold a stable image, only watch it bleach. Then the title's crazy emotion resolves into a single confession that reorganizes the whole song: someone might as well die on his account, and only at that thought does she finally begin to laugh. The read turns on that grotesque trade — the laughter he wants to see is bought by his disappearance, so the manic style is really the sound of someone mistaking self-annihilation for love.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"self-erasure mistaken for the gift that earns the beloved's laughter","built":"the confession that reorganises the whole song arrives only near the end"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~die-meets-hard":{"haiku":"Cruelty blooms where another's suffering becomes the speaker's only path to rest; malice and infection spread as invisible virus.","opus":"A confession of predatory obsession-a hidden honey-colored collection of despair, jealousy, and the wish to lick and dominate a parallel world-where the sick comfort of watching someone break curdles into a plea to be saved by the very death and virus he spreads.","sonnet":"Honey-coloured despair and a barely suppressed pleasure in others' ruin — jealousy, thrill, the desire to devour and dominate the city's chaos — circle around the admission that the speaker's psychology is far from normal.","src":"haiku","web":"Drenched in honey-colored despair, the narrator collects dark pleasures in secret and watches others with voyeuristic obsession, acknowledging their own psychological abnormality and spreading it like a virus — nihilistic and resigned to mutual destruction.","fable":"TK opens on a honey-colored despair hoarded like a collection no one may see, then names the engine underneath: his heart only rests once someone else breaks, which he flags plainly as not normal. The emergency triad — of temptation, of pleasure, of a jealousy that will not fade — is that confession said three ways, another's misfortune reframed as an eternal sweetness. Against it the two refrains grind together, the buzzing plea to see more guitars and the die-hard chant, as if the sickness rides in on the wall of sound. Then the swagger caves: the fantasy of a world-killing virus turns, in a breath, from save the world into save me — the would-be dominator undone as someone who cannot save himself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"the craving for another's collapse named plainly as not normal","built":"world-killer fantasy caves mid-song into save me, the dominator exposed as the helpless one"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~die-meets-hard-koji-nakamura-remix":{"src":"web","web":"A voyeuristic dark euphoria at the spectacle of ruin — the narrator collects secret pleasures from others' suffering, acknowledges this relief as abnormal, and still pleads at the close to be saved from the very impulses that fascinate them."},
"ling-tosite-sigure~disco-flight":{"haiku":"Speed and consciousness dissolve into disco-hued dissociation; the speaker melts into purple sky, indistinguishable from the world.","opus":"Escape by loading consciousness onto a speeding disco flight where time and self melt away; a burnt-out mind begs to be shown a swaying purple dream in some unseen place just a little longer before the world disappears.","sonnet":"Consciousness dissolves into purple light and speed, the world blinking out each time the eyes close, until the body turns transparent and the only remaining wish is to stay hidden in that barely-visible elsewhere a little longer.","src":"haiku","web":"Ascending above mundane reality into a purple disco sky, the narrator surrenders to rhythmic sensation and becomes transparent, floating alone in an altered state where time melts and the world below is an unreachable, dreamlike spectacle.","fable":"A dissociation song that treats leaving your own body as a flight: the speaker, calling himself broken, loads his consciousness onto a speeding disco flight so that when he shuts his eyes the world simply vanishes, and TK's shrieked-and-whispered twin vocals let time and the flight itself melt out of the pre-chorus like the mind losing its grip on both. Everything lives in one color — a swaying purple sky the grey-and-purple speaker only pretends to inhabit, begging to be shown that color and allowed to dream a little longer somewhere just out of sight. The read turns on the second chorus's escalation: as his body turns transparent and he loses track of his own wavering awareness, the thing he asks to be handed shifts from a color to a pistol, and the euphoric flight quietly reveals itself as a wish to disappear for good.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","suicide","freedom & escape"],"means":"euphoric flight quietly revealing itself as a wish to disappear for good","built":"the second chorus shifts the ask from a color to a pistol"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~dynamite-nonsense":{"src":"web","web":"Rocking on a seesaw illusion, the narrator searches for words that do not exist and finds only an unbalanced dance that language cannot contain — the experience of explosive, chaotic feeling that defeats every rational framework for explaining it."},
"ling-tosite-sigure~ef":{"haiku":"Identity vanishes under another's voice while the speaker's presence dissolves into echoing sound that fades into distance.","src":"haiku","fable":"A voice keeps sounding after the body it belonged to is gone. Blindfolded, the singer sinks into the world that voice projects, then wakes to find it tracing over his present. The song refuses to decide whether grief is conjuring the dead or the dead are truly reaching back, and it names the doubt outright, asking over and over whether it is only his imagination until the question frays into whose fault it is. Then the fragile claim that he can still hear turns, in the last line, into hearing it now. The haunting lands in the present tense, unresolved.","fv":2,"fnote":"The second guitar is the drummer’s: TK asked Pierre Nakano to play electric guitar for the first time in some fifteen years — he’d quit after junior high, his hands shook at the session, and he had a custom guitar built for this one song alone.","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"haunting that refuses to stay in the past tense","built":"the closing shift to present tense lands the ghost now"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~end-roll-fiction": {"haiku":"Life is a perpetual unfinished screenplay where dreams repeat in futile loops; the ending never arrives and change is illusion.","src":"haiku","web":"Wishing that no moment changes while knowing wishes never truly come true, the narrator paints a mental last scene they cannot finish — a loop of manipulative contradictions, labyrinths, and the quiet, irritating persistence of something that should not matter but does.","fable":"Structured entirely around a camera viewfinder and a film that won't end, this reads the impossibility of finishing your own last scene — the self-portrait stays incomplete because too much overflows: things you can't kill, things that won't reach. The refrain is a closed loop of futility — grant the wish and it still won't come true, change and you still can't change, reach and you still can't reach — so that recurring coincidence just replays the same dream, the demon's glitter projecting a mirage of arrival that never lands. What breaks the loop is a shift from wishing to witnessing: instead of completing the fiction, the singer leaps out of time toward a place never seen and burns a gaze into memory so it won't be lost, and the film closes not on an ending but on the image of the other person laughing amid scenery and wind — the point being that some footage is worth keeping precisely because the story can't be resolved.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","nostalgia & memory","identity & becoming"],"means":"the self escapes the loop by witnessing rather than completing the scene","built":"the camera-and-film frame closes not on an ending but on laughter caught mid-scene"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~eneme": {"haiku":"Freedom dissolves upon discovery; the speaker sacrifices identity seeking liberation only to discover an emptiness that mirrors the prison he sought to escape.","opus":"Opening a door hoping for freedom, the speaker instead surrenders everything and is trapped in a transparent cage, peeling off his own unfakeable skin. The birthday becomes a knife-day of self-stripping, and the demand is that the beloved who tramples his hard-won heart either stop half-wounding him or kill him outright.","sonnet":"The self is systematically stripped away — skin peeled, blood drained, the sign erased, armour shattered — as an imprisoning freedom turns out to be a void; what remains is a hollow person who keeps being killed and resurrected, asking the other to stop half-wounding them and either fully destroy or release them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A door opens onto the question is this freedom, and the song spends itself discovering the answer is no. TK fills the blank future-pocket with a methodology of pure unfreedom, then can't move inside it — freedom flew off and won't come back — and the sardonic birthday chant curdles the blessing into a wish for the blade, a self stripped to zero that still fixes nothing. The transparent cage is the cruelest image: he's caged in something he can't even see, and the answer isn't escape but a chanted plea to be killed or frozen mid-death, an endless dying that only regenerates the self it kills. The final accusation — you trample the heart I staked my life on — turns the whole thing outward: if you're going to nick me with an unsteady knife, finish me off. It reads liberation pursued to the point where the only freedom imaginable is being ended.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","suicide","surveillance & control"],"means":"liberation pursued until the only freedom imaginable is being ended","built":"the birthday chant curdled into a wish for the blade"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~enigmatic-feeling":{"haiku":"Boundaries between self and other dissolve into madness; the more you grasp for meaning, the closer you approach breaking.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages a dissolution of self: to be endlessly taken from is to go mad, and in that breakdown the world splits in two while a descending god pins the singer with the demand to name who he is. Its chorus turns on a pun that English can only approximate — kami-hitoe (a hair's breadth) sits atop kami (god), so the recurring question of whether god is only one bleeds directly into the fear of a boundary paper-thin enough to cross without noticing. That thinness is the whole argument: the narrator floats as a kanzen fuyūtai, a wholly weightless body that cannot even peer into its own mirror. The line between sanity and its loss, between the two split worlds, is exactly one sheet deep. Against a puzzle with no answer, the track builds not toward solution but toward a terror of solving — the late turn admits that if the answer suddenly came clear he would simply break — so the final plea to be forgiven, to be treated as already killed since he is about to vanish, reads less as suicide than as a mind choosing dissolution over the unbearable clarity of self-recognition.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Opening theme for Psycho-Pass 2 (2014) — the anime where a state system continuously measures each citizen's crime coefficient; the song's terror over which self answers for you is written to that premise.","themes":["madness & the mind","faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"choosing dissolution over the unbearable clarity of self-recognition","built":"kami-hitoe pun making god and a paper-thin boundary one syllable"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~filmsick-mystery":{"haiku":"Memories calcify into uncrossable film grain; nostalgia for a desired past that dissolves the moment it's recalled, leaving only repetition.","src":"haiku","web":"The act of remembering triggers the destruction of what is remembered: clarity paradoxically dissolves its own object, leaving only time-distorted film loops, overflowing illusions, and the impossible persistence of a season one wants to hate and forget.","fable":"TK the vocalist writes about wanting to erase the very things he most wanted to keep, and the song watches memory decay in real time: a hoarded collection of images he suddenly wishes gone, a residual afterimage of someone that pleads not to be forgotten even as he promises he'll lose interest the moment he closes his eyes. The chorus of English fragments — a stutter of temporal and cinematic warping, a delayed rewrite, a warped false-recall of something never lived — enacts what it describes, treating memory as damaged celluloid where light itself bleeds red and strips meaning away. Ling tosite sigure's signature move is here: TK's shredded falsetto and the trio's jittering, math-rock stop-start rhythm turn emotional recall into a physical malfunction, so that remembering and deleting become the same gesture. The final turn is the cruelest, half-admitting the whole preserved image was probably not even the right person, an obviously fake story-end — the sickness of the title being that the film we keep replaying was corrupted from the first frame.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nostalgia & memory","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"remembering and deleting converging into the same damaged gesture","built":"closing admission the preserved image was probably never the right person"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~g":{"haiku":"A cold dissection of mechanical emotion—asking if you can feel, revealing that everything is electric impulse, connection severed.","opus":"The band personifies an electric guitar as a silent, expressionless partner it longs to make scream; the real subject is the intimacy and violence of the player-instrument bond, feedback and shattered glass standing in for a secret self set loose.","sonnet":"A song framed around a guitar — or a person treated like one — that the narrator wants to unlock and make sing, describing someone who performs neutrality ('I'm ordinary, don't expect anything') but secretly executes a private, electric secret dance when finally alone. The emotional core is the fascination with the hidden interior life of someone who presents as closed.","src":"haiku","fable":"The you here is a guitar, and TK writes the whole song as a lover's and a torturer's address to his instrument: the mechanical you he wants to make sound, the fretwork, the arpeggio he can't yet coax out, the feedback that won't stop. That literalism is the point on a 2010 Sigure record built from exactly this white-knuckle guitar language. The read lives in a two-stage flip. First the machine is expressionless and electric-less until the narrator, alone, pries open its electrical workings, cuts it apart, then hurls it into a limitless sky where infinity resonates and it trembles into sounding for the first time by itself, a secret boy in its own secret dance. Then the second flip retracts the whole distance he'd built: the electric secret face he unmasked resembles him, shattered like glass, the secret weapon that woke was only ever his own, and the last word is not secret dance but secret fake. What looked like a man animating a dead object turns out to be a man discovering the machine is a mirror, and the exposure he performed on it exposes that his own expression is a mechanism too, which is why the ecstatic dance can only resolve as a fake.","fv":2,"fnote":"TK has said he built this one as an engineer rather than a guitarist — piano, synths and insect-thin electronics assembled at the desk — so the song that stages a man prying sound out of his instrument was itself made with the guitar at arm’s length, the secret fake of the final line extending to its own production.","themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming","love & desire"],"means":"animating the instrument only to find it mirrors the player","built":"the ecstatic secret dance resolves as secret fake — mask exposed as mechanism"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~hakai-yono-yume":{"opus":"A dream of destruction where words are hidden and eyes shut to conceal a lonely, phony-tracked self; the beloved dances through his mind without knowing it, and touching the dream reveals the underside he doesn't want seen.","sonnet":"Behind layers of hidden language and closed eyes, a damaged nightscape pulses — something between destruction and dream — and the speaker wants to be seen through it, dyed by another person's gaze even as the real interior stays concealed.","src":"web","web":"Blasphemous dreams are the only place the narrator can reach another person — stained memories blur and change shape, familiar words grow unrecognizable, and the repeated refrain \"you don't know my mind\" frames the longing as irreparably forbidden and unreachable.","fable":"A stalker's love song that keeps performing the same self-erasing ritual — hide the words, shut the eyes — in order to be looked at, so that surveillance and surrender become the same act. The title itself, a katakana smear of destruction and night's dream, refuses to resolve into either, and the compulsive repetition of that phrase enacts the ruin it names. The embedded English marks the gap the song can't close: the beloved dances inside the speaker's mind yet knows nothing of it, an intimacy that exists only one-way. Against that, the pleading invitation to touch, to reach across the dream and search inside him, tries to convert being spied-on into being finally, willingly entered.","fv":2,"fnote":"The major bow: after two indie records, just A moment arrived on Sony in May 2009 — written, produced and mixed by TK himself — and became the band’s first top-10 album, peaking at number 4 on Oricon.","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control","shame & repression"],"means":"surveillance and surrender collapsed into a single ritual","built":"the title refuses to resolve destruction and dream into either word"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~high-energy-vacuum":{"haiku":"Surveillance and judgment permeate from all sides, transforming everyone into simultaneous victim and executioner of each other's autonomy.","src":"haiku","fable":"Ling tosite sigure sets a bright, hurtling arrangement against surveillance panic: the singer feels an anonymous laugh turn toward him and starts manufacturing perfectly normal feelings to stay inside the frame, certain that stepping out summons a pack of hyenas. The refrain's paranoid grammar keeps flipping who holds the power — everyone is watching, everyone is being bound, everyone is victim, then perpetrator — until watcher and watched collapse into the same mutually pointed weapon, a firing squad with no outside. That collapse is what the title names: High Energy Vacuum, a system so total it runs under control and out of control at once, where the sane response to being monitored (asking the monitor whether you count today as a genius or a pervert, a nobody or a saint) is itself the trap, since it hands the panel your metrics to judge. The band's signature move — TK's shrieking falsetto welded to metronomic post-hardcore precision — becomes the argument rather than the decoration: the more tightly everyone falls into line and marches forward, the more they lose the you, the freedom, the whole universe stripped away word by word. The last image lets the hyenas laugh while the voice is erased twice over, so the read isn't about being silenced by an enemy but about a crowd of interchangeable victim-aggressors deleting one another's voices in perfect, cheerful synchronization.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title embeds the predator — ハイエナジー (high energy) contains ハイエナ (hyena) — so the lyric's laughing hyena packs are hiding inside the words that name the void they patrol.","themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance","freedom & escape"],"means":"watcher and watched collapsing into the same mutually pointed weapon","built":"tighter synchronization deleting voices in cheerful, interchangeable unison"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~hysteric-phase-show":{"haiku":"Phase shifts create instability in the present moment, each two-second fracture dissolving coherence into hysteria.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that stages selfhood as a guitar signal, then dares you to admit the signal is counterfeit. Ling tosite sigure build everything from math-rock hardware — a fretted note, a fanned-out run of notes flung at you with the question of whether you can even hear it — so identity arrives already coded as waveform, and the narrator keeps begging three seconds' grace for a rotating forgery, a phase knocked out of true. The looming reflection he addresses resembles a secret, then the beloved; each pass it trembles and, within a counted two seconds, scatters into pieces like a decaying tone. The bridge snaps this dread into a hysterical English command hurled at a sleeper — rouse yourself, fool — before the mask drops: the accuser finally names himself the very defective thing he had been diagnosing.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","madness & the mind"],"means":"the accuser finally names himself the defective thing he had been diagnosing","built":"identity arrives coded as waveform — a fretted note, a phase knocked out of true"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~i-m-machine":{"haiku":"Mechanical detachment conceals emotional depth; the attempt to embrace destroys what the machine-self cannot feel.","src":"haiku","web":"Emotional self-shutdown coded as machinery — the narrator has a cold stare and no feelings like a machine, trapped in longing to return to a past that felt real, until the relationship finally breaks and leaves them alone, proving that the numbness that was meant to protect only produced deeper pain.","fable":"The song stages heartbreak as a fault report from a machine that is trying to prove it was never one. Its central gambit is the wish to rewind on a time machine and go back to check one thing: whether this heart of mine was actually breathing — the speaker inspecting his own past feeling like defective hardware, unsure if the affection was ever real or only executed. The emotional engine is a single devastating inversion — because it didn't hurt, it hurt; that it didn't hurt was what was sad — the confession that numbness, not pain, is the real damage, that the inability to feel the loss is worse than feeling it. Ling tosite sigure's signature — glassy precision hiding a body that can't stop clenching — scores the same paradox, and the ending abandons the whole cold conceit: I have no emotion, machine-like, and yet somewhere I'm being squeezed tight — why, why — the mask cracking mid-sentence, the machine feeling the one thing it swore it couldn't, and unable to explain it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"奇怪な機械 — the verse caps on a homophone: kikai (bizarre) and kikai (machine) are the same sound, so the title's machine is a pun heard, not just named.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"numbness revealed as the real damage, worse than pain would have been","built":"mask cracking mid-sentence as the machine feels the one thing it swore it couldn't"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~i-not-crazy-am-you-are":{"haiku":"Madness is inflicted by watching rather than felt by doing; the observer's gaze is what fractures consciousness.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song built on a single unstable proposition — the title's ungrammatical inversion, i not crazy am you are — and its whole method is to make you unable to locate where madness resides. The speaker's compulsive refrain is that he wants to show the beloved things: his suicidal phenomenon, his neural collapse, a ruined dusk, the very moment of destruction — an erotics of exhibition where being witnessed by the other is the only proof he exists, and the imperative to churn me up / stir me is addressed outward, madness deputized to someone else's hands. Structurally the poison passes across the membrane between the two: early on a soft consciousness deep in his head dissolves into time, but by the mirror-verse of the second movement it is her soft consciousness that dissolves into him, and the accusatory voice that always mutters from inside the skull, you've already gone crazy, gets turned around and flung at her — the one who's gone mad is you. The recurring image of standing behind him, silently watching someone else's mind at a slant, makes the beloved less a person than a surveilling gaze, and when that gaze finally collapses he vanishes from view: to be unseen is to cease. TK's Inspiration is DEAD aesthetic is exactly this — falsetto shrieking over math-rock that keeps phase-shifting, and the lyric names its own trick, 逆相 (reverse-phase), the two waves cancelling until only reality itself is annulled while everything else survives. The last line refuses to resolve the pronoun war and instead dissolves the grammar of selfhood outright — I'm becoming meaning-unknown, becoming scenery-impossible, becoming you-unknown — so that the closing i know crazy is not a diagnosis of either party but the sound of the boundary between them finally failing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","surveillance & control","identity & becoming"],"means":"madness transferred across the membrane from singer to beloved","built":"reverse-phase named in the lyric as the structural logic cancelling both selves"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~i-was-music":{"haiku":"The speaker was once music but fragmented: crushed dreams and fractured identity dissolve into incoherent dreaming.","opus":"The urge to crack open a lover's plastic-fake reality and shoot through it, wishing 'go ahead, lose your mind,' as the head scatters and floats bursting into space-a dreaming plea, 'I was music, wake up,' for an impossible wish.","sonnet":"The plastic surface of someone the speaker loves is something he fantasises cracking open; surrendering to madness is welcomed as the only honest state, and music is the dead skin of a self that once existed and no longer does.","src":"haiku","web":"Identity has already dissolved — \"I was music\" in past tense — as the narrator describes becoming plastic and transparent, their head disconnecting and their former self replaced by something hollow, surrendering to the inevitable disorientation of watching oneself transform into an unrecognizable imitation.","fable":"As the opening salvo of still a Sigure virgin?, this is TK announcing the band's whole aesthetic thesis: the self dissolved into sound, identity so thin it has become plastic. The song circles a lover whose reality has turned to molded plastic that the speaker keeps trying and failing to crack, and its motor is the bridge's endlessly repeated permission to go crazy, chanted until permission and compulsion blur into the same thing. That mantra is the read: what sounds like tender consent is really the sound of a mind coming apart in real time, and the pivot from asking whose infinity is this to admitting that even tomorrow is already visible tracks a consciousness that has overshot into a terrifying, meaningless clarity where the future has collapsed into the present. Against that dissolution the title works as elegy and boast at once, the past tense claiming the speaker was music before he was a person, so the chorus's brain scattered and burst into orbit is not breakdown but the wish granted, the ego finally shattering into pure sound while a second voice pleads for him to wake up. The refrain that this is a dream, dreamed slowly, keeps the whole thing suspended between transcendence and horror, refusing to say which.","fv":2,"fnote":"The pulse under the opener is literal: TK recorded his own breathing and used it as the track’s rhythm — a song about dissolving into sound built from the sound of its maker staying alive. The album it opens, its title a taunt at everyone who still hadn’t listened, entered the Oricon chart at number one.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","madness & the mind","identity & becoming"],"means":"permission to lose the mind repeated until consent and compulsion merge","built":"past-tense title claims the self was music before it was a person"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~i-was-music-remastered-at-metropolis":{"src":"web","web":"Tormented over whether a plastic reality will shatter, the narrator turns transparent, floats disconnected in space, and breaks apart — the repeated phrase \"I was music\" marks a completed transformation from person into something abstract, dreaming and waking in the rubble.","fable":"As the opening salvo of still a Sigure virgin?, this is TK announcing the band's whole aesthetic thesis: the self dissolved into sound, identity so thin it has become plastic. The song circles a lover whose reality has turned to molded plastic that the speaker keeps trying and failing to crack, and its motor is the bridge's endlessly repeated permission to go crazy, chanted until permission and compulsion blur into the same thing. That mantra is the read: what sounds like tender consent is really the sound of a mind coming apart in real time, and the pivot from asking whose infinity is this to admitting that even tomorrow is already visible tracks a consciousness that has overshot into a terrifying, meaningless clarity where the future has collapsed into the present. Against that dissolution the title works as elegy and boast at once, the past tense claiming the speaker was music before he was a person, so the chorus's brain scattered and burst into orbit is not breakdown but the wish granted, the ego finally shattering into pure sound while a second voice pleads for him to wake up. The refrain that this is a dream, dreamed slowly, keeps the whole thing suspended between transcendence and horror, refusing to say which.","fv":2,"fnote":"The pulse under the opener is literal: TK recorded his own breathing and used it as the track’s rhythm — a song about dissolving into sound built from the sound of its maker staying alive. The album it opens, its title a taunt at everyone who still hadn’t listened, entered the Oricon chart at number one.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","madness & the mind","identity & becoming"],"means":"ego shattering into pure sound while a second voice pleads return","built":"past-tense title frames dissolution as both elegy and boast"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~illusion-is-mine":{"haiku":"Brilliant colors dissolve into illusion, memory and present collapse as the boundary between perceived and possessed shatters.","src":"haiku","web":"Everything the narrator sees — people, dreams, the beloved — is semi-transparent and miraculous, drenched in vivid color that turns out to be an illusion belonging only to them; if the memories vanish they fear they will shatter, yet they keep drowning in the illusion willingly.","fable":"A half-drowned love song about the moment a shared vision curdles into something one person owns alone. Everything turns on a single verb that mutates across the three choruses. The vivid color is first what you showed me, then what I showed you, then what we watched together. That drift rewrites a gift into a projection into a memory, and the possessive claim tightens as the actual we thins out. The song ends on a speaker who keeps their eyes shut on purpose, because opening them would break the thing they would rather drown beside than test for real.","fv":2,"fnote":"The voice is 345’s — the bassist’s rare turn at lead vocal, the album handing its one shut-eyed love song to the band’s other singer.","themes":["love & desire","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"clinging to a shared vision even as it curdles into possession","built":"one verb mutates across three choruses, rewriting gift into projection into memory"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~jpop-xfile":{"haiku":"Memory fragments scatter and recombine while identity dissolves, each fleeting moment erasing what came before.","src":"haiku","web":"The narrator is dissolving — fingers turning transparent, the beloved's name slowly forgotten, identity lost inside a delusion of \"masochistic pop music\" — pleading to be found by someone as the self slips out of reach, fading through memories into pure formlessness.","fable":"An avant-garde band interrogates the pop it can't stop craving, treating J-pop as a masochistic drug that dissolves both memory and the self even as it begs for one more listen. The verses stage a losing fight with recall — what was your name, it asks, the answer going gradually dimmer — while the speaker's own fingers turn transparent, a body thinning out as the song plays on. That erasure is the point: the mind slips into delusion and offers its lost future away to anyone, and the chanted mantra now-I-can-see-the-future reads less as hope than as the trance the music induces. The English scaffolding sharpens the irony — a knowing-claim looped until it means nothing, and a final plea to be heard flung at the genre itself, so the closing gesture is a lover shouting into a medium that may not answer back.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"a trance-inducing genre eating the self that craves it","built":"English future-claim looped until it means nothing, then flung at the medium"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~kankaku-ufo":{"haiku":"Time fragments and dual selves splinter; sensation becomes UFO-like alien presence splitting the speaker's coherence.","opus":"The terror of self-division-feeling two people inside one body, breaking and trembling inside time-where a warped winter vision in July and flashbacks of sepia memory kill the self and drift off as a transparent, alien UFO.","sonnet":"A fracture inside the self — the felt sense of two people inhabiting one body — surfaces in sepia flashbacks that kill and vanish, leaving a wavering, transparent awareness that reads the other person as an entirely alien object.","src":"haiku","web":"Two people exist simultaneously inside a single consciousness, fracturing time and identity into sepia flashbacks and ambiguous floating sensations — the UFO stands for something the narrator can feel but cannot identify or contain, an alien presence lodged inside the self.","fable":"The read splits its material into two families deliberately: a temporal-interior register (inside time, inside the self, someone doubled into two people, the body breaking and trembling) and a synthetic pop-glitch register where the beloved is filed under UFO and counted off like a countdown — Friday, 1·2·3, transparent, a grey sky one delay short of freezing. Its thesis lives in the collision of those systems: dissociation rendered not as confession but as signal loss, the desire to intersect (kousa shitai) forever locking against the desire to seal off (heisa shitai), so that the you who is a UFO is both the thing longed for and the thing that cannot be made to land. The song's cruelest move is showing winter scenery in July — a memory playing out of season — and then admitting the flashback kills the singer and leaves, so the UFO is finally memory itself, unidentified because the self that stored it has already come apart into two. What looks like glossy pop-glitch counting is the sound of a person tracking their own vagueness (aimai na sonzaikan) in real time, naming the strange song they've just discovered to be themselves.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The final chorus rewrites the title-hook with 凛とした — the band's own name folded in as an adjective, the song signing itself — and the counted-off chorus drops the number nine to put the English word Night in its slot, a bilingual nine/Night homophone.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"desire to intersect locked forever against desire to seal off","built":"winter scenery placed in July, memory playing out of season"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~karma-siren":{"haiku":"Sensation fades into fakeness while destiny tears through memory, leaving only the sound of an alarm that never stops.","src":"haiku","fable":"Ling tosite sigure build their unease from the gap between deadened numbness and shrieking overload, and this song scores the two against each other: the narrator opens by announcing that the stimulus has finally fallen asleep, that he has learned an ending nowhere near actual death, that his breathing has already stopped. That is the mask of anesthesia, but the chorus keeps blaring underneath it, a siren that will not stop ringing while someone he addresses dances themselves into a frenzy on a counterfeit reality. The Pandora's-voice image is the turn: whatever is left unerased, dredged up and reflected back as fragments in a mirror, keeps getting torn apart by karma, so the deadness is not peace but a loop of self-injury he mistakes for release. The last movement drops the pretense entirely and begs in bare imperatives, alternately asking for feeling to be lent, returned, and violated, so the plea to make the siren stop and the refusal to let it stop become the same wish. It is a song about preferring the fake calm to the real feeling, then discovering that the fakeness itself is the alarm that will never let him sleep.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"preferring counterfeit calm over real feeling, then finding the calm is the alarm","built":"anesthesia announced as peace while the siren blares underneath it"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~kimitooku": {"haiku":"Self cannot become authentic despite yearning; trapped between fraudulent ideals and the impossibility of belonging, forever performing incompleteness.","src":"haiku","web":"Caught between counterfeit perfection and an inaccessible inner world, the narrator deliberately cultivates anxiety to avoid satisfaction, haunted by a predetermined life path and unable to answer the SIREN of \"who am I\" — beautiful in someone's memory, estranged from their own.","fable":"Built on a paralysis: the one who wanted to change became someone else without ever changing, and now a fake's completed form catches itself in the act of being fake. The title itself splits — キミノオク (deep inside you) sliding into キミトオク (far from you) — so the whole song lives in the gap between wanting to reach into someone and knowing you never will, an alarm and a who-am-I siren that won't stop ringing. He distrusts the very longing that drives him, refusing to be fooled by admiring eyes, admitting it's mostly lies. What's left when change and reaching both fail is the one modest, unbearable wish underneath the imperfect abnormality: not to be loved, just to remain, beautifully, inside your memory.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"i'mperfect has no title track — the album's name lives here, in the line about an imperfect abnormality living a scripted life.","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"wanting to reach inside someone while knowing the longing is mostly lies","built":"the title slides between deep inside you and far from you in one phoneme"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~knife-vacation":{"haiku":"A hallucinatory space where consciousness fragments and floats, memory and illusion intertwined on invisible wires.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages memory as failed recording: a face surfaces behind shut eyes, and every attempt to name it arrives already glitched — the English shards aren't lyrics but system readouts, camera jargon and error-tags stitched into the Japanese, so remembering someone reads like a corrupted file trying and failing to correct itself. Its refrain turns violence into rest — the plea is to be broken and to be hidden in the same breath, a person begging the knife of oblivion to finish what grief started, because being erased is the only vacation left. The long tail dissolves the whole scene into an antenna signal, the self drifting weightless up a transmission wire until it goes out of view, so the closing flashbacks feel less like recovered images than the last packets of a fading broadcast. What began as one person trying to hold a face ends with the holder liquefied into static — the loved one distorted the singer's own motion, and the wound is that the disturbance is all that survives the reception.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nostalgia & memory","death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"erasure as the only rest left after grief corrupts the original recording","built":"self dissolving into antenna static, last packets of a fading broadcast"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~laser-beamer":{"haiku":"Pursuit of perfect control and clarity breeds rot; the more you crystallize your vision of the other, the more you poison shared future.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages a totalitarian romance where perfection is the horror: a self polished flawless, its will and tears drained off, until every imperfect gap is sealed and the result is not paradise but a Dystopia crammed with ideals. Its hook works by pulling apart the letters A and I — the pronoun I, the eye that is begged to look inside, and A-and-I as a paired machinery that consumes what was human — so the singer's dread is losing the capacity to still be an I, to stay a scrambled singular self, once he is perfectly counted and ranged and compared. The Domestic refrain reads the mask and its underside at once: begging to be owned, bound tighter, exposed further, it makes submission indistinguishable from safety, and the trembling question of whether this happiness and this freedom might not be a lie lands as the frightened hope of someone who already suspects it is. The turn comes when transparency defeats itself — clear multiplied by clear multiplied by clear curdles into murk, anonymous hearts pooled into one flawless clouded mass — so the vivid ideal is exposed as a rotted fantasy dressed clean. It ends by refusing the collective cure: stop taming me, drive the I back into the feelings that were erased, I belong to myself now even as a warped finished thing — wake up, an alarm rung against the seduction of being perfected into nobody.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"transparency perfected until it curdles into anonymous murk","built":"clear multiplied by clear multiplied by clear producing the rotted fantasy"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~last-dance-revolution":{"haiku":"The speaker dances masochistically through memory and fever, chasing an invisible last dance in twilight liminal space.","opus":"A masochistic, transparent last dance dissolving into summer passion and memory-fantasy, chasing a vivid final movement at twilight speed through the night that keeps flickering just out of sight.","sonnet":"A summer memory flickers at twilight speed, half-visible and already receding, and trying to recover it only makes it disappear faster — the last dance is glimpsed at midnight and immediately lost.","src":"haiku","web":"An atmospheric portrait of chasing intoxicating, transient sensation through masochistic dance and summer passion, cycling through twilight-speed moments of euphoria that are ephemeral and irresistible in equal measure.","fable":"The track runs almost entirely on a closed set of glamour-adjectives — masochistic, dramatic, exotic — recombined against a pulse until meaning arrives through texture, not statement. The sparse Japanese lines do the real feeling: a summer passion fading, a slip backward through time, a farewell forever swaying into view yet finally unseeable. TK stages the whole thing as a slide from brilliance to erasure, the euphoric loop revealed as an elegy wearing party clothes. The masochism named up front is the thesis: pleasure taken in replaying, at some blurred nocturnal velocity, the very vanishing you cannot halt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["nostalgia & memory","addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"pleasure taken in replaying the vanishing you cannot halt","built":"glamour-adjectives recycled until meaning arrives through texture, the elegy wearing party clothes as its only structure"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~make-up-syndrome":{"haiku":"Identity is transparent makeup layered over transparent void; the speaker's visibility becomes violation, beauty a self-erasing magic trick.","src":"haiku","web":"Hiding everything felt behind a daily mask of calculated artifice, the speaker confronts someone whose own plastic, sadistic image creates distance — the mutual performance of concealment read as both longing and a paradox where even the act of erasure looks beautiful.","fable":"The song reads self-surveillance as an aesthetic addiction: the singer applies make up not to be seen but to keep from being caught, editing consciousness toward transparency so every spilled mistake shows through and can be smoothed over. What TK stages is the willed disappearance of the self into a calculated image-world, where time itself turns lewd and the transparent thing violates him — the passive voice is the whole point, since the erasure he performs on himself he narrates as something done to him. The recurring English fragments (plastic, illusion, sadistic, each trailed by I miss you) are the mask's vocabulary mourning what the mask replaced, longing for the very artifice that is doing the harming. The turn is the last verse's confession that the magic of deleting oneself has come to look beautiful — a patchwork plastic self mistaken for grace — so the read is not vanity but the seduction of self-annihilation dressed as cosmetics. Fittingly for i'mperfect and TK's method of layering fractured English into hyper-precise Japanese, the song's form enacts its subject: a surface so composed it becomes indistinguishable from the void it conceals.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"self-annihilation dressed as cosmetics and mistaken for grace","built":"erasure performed on the self narrated in passive voice as if done by another"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~make-up-syndrome-album-mix":{"haiku":"Identity is transparent makeup layered over transparent void; the speaker's visibility becomes violation, beauty a self-erasing magic trick.","src":"haiku","web":"Hiding everything felt behind a daily mask of calculated artifice, the speaker confronts someone whose own plastic, sadistic image creates distance — the mutual performance of concealment read as both longing and a paradox where even the act of erasure looks beautiful.","fable":"The song reads self-surveillance as an aesthetic addiction: the singer applies make up not to be seen but to keep from being caught, editing consciousness toward transparency so every spilled mistake shows through and can be smoothed over. What TK stages is the willed disappearance of the self into a calculated image-world, where time itself turns lewd and the transparent thing violates him — the passive voice is the whole point, since the erasure he performs on himself he narrates as something done to him. The recurring English fragments (plastic, illusion, sadistic, each trailed by I miss you) are the mask's vocabulary mourning what the mask replaced, longing for the very artifice that is doing the harming. The turn is the last verse's confession that the magic of deleting oneself has come to look beautiful — a patchwork plastic self mistaken for grace — so the read is not vanity but the seduction of self-annihilation dressed as cosmetics. Fittingly for i'mperfect and TK's method of layering fractured English into hyper-precise Japanese, the song's form enacts its subject: a surface so composed it becomes indistinguishable from the void it conceals.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"self-annihilation dressed as cosmetics and mistaken for grace","built":"erasure performed on the self narrated in passive voice as if done by another"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~marvelous-persona": {"src":"web","web":"Lining up desires and ordering them not to wake, the narrator wrestles with fury and imitation, anxious about being seen — a dissection of the constructed social self where rationality barely contains what erupts underneath, and the adopted persona becomes its own kind of trap.","fable":"The marvelous persona is a self engineered so no one could ever hate it — desires lined up at equal intervals, the outline of a face traced until it goes mad, a saint everyone wants performed on demand. TK stacks the vocabulary of grading — imitation, upgrade, downgrade, saint-madman rank — to expose the persona as a product optimized for approval, and the recurring plea reset me / release the fury about to spill is the real face straining under the mask. Read together, the song's two levels collide in one word, 覚醒 (awakening): the marvelous mask is the death of the individual self, so the only awakening left is to let the madness loose and finish the saint off. It is a portrait of performed likeability as self-erasure, sung by the thing being erased.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming","madness & the mind"],"means":"performed likeability as the slow erasure of the self doing the performing","built":"覚醒 (awakening) names both the mask's completion and the madness that ends it"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~metamorphose":{"haiku":"Identity peels away in constant transformation until the speaker becomes someone entirely new and unrecognizable.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages ecstasy as onset of dissolution, its imported English nouns — Maximizer, dynamizer, synthesizer — worn like glittering prosthetics that Ling tosite sigure's Toru Kitajima half-mocks even as he rides them, the register of a Japanese art-rock band writing arena bombast about its own artifice. Its pivot is a bilingual pun that only Japanese can carry: the singer strips off his I, meaning both the English pronoun and the Japanese ai, love, so that shedding selfhood and shedding love become one undressing gesture at the threshold of a second person's world. From there the choruses run parallel conditionals — if tomorrow the world falls silent I won't be here, if it does you won't be either — until apocalypse and intimacy fuse, and the metamorphosis that titles the track is not transcendence but the willingness to let the self detonate so it can be seized by another. The refrain calls it so-called nonfiction, then miracle nonfiction, then metamorph-nonfiction, the mutating tag conceding that this staged rapture is real precisely because it admits to being a pose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"detonating the self so it can be seized by another","built":"bilingual I/ai pun making shedding selfhood and shedding love one gesture"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~mib126":{"haiku":"Sensory distortion and violation rendering the world into an abstracted orange haze; the self dissolves into unnamed feelings at the edge of chaos.","src":"haiku","web":"Erasing sound, then light, then orientation itself, the narrator becomes lost in a warped world where identity fragments into the repeated, unresolvable phrase \"orange plus me\" — a study in sensory deprivation and the dissolution of self.","fable":"A dissociative flight out of a world someone else built, where the only way to see truth is to strip the senses away — kill the sound, kill the light, and the sad color underneath finally shows. The song runs on synaesthesia: an orange that keeps pulling the singer somewhere distant, tangled up with an untranslatable refrain, as if selfhood could only be written as an equation added to a color. Ling tosite sigure's signature is that contradiction between violence and delicacy, and it surfaces here as a jittery repeated image of a hand in mid-air, described first as violated, then broken — the same phrase mutating each pass. The one moment the song turns outward is a line in blunt katakana meaning the world you people made, an accusation aimed at whoever authored this distorted reality. Against that, the singer refuses to let his own voice be killed and fixes everything on a single word — now — as though the only escape from a manufactured world is to insist on the present tense.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"stripping the senses to find a truth a manufactured world conceals","built":"a single blunt katakana line accusation aimed outward in an otherwise inward song"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~mirror-frustration":{"haiku":"Solitude is both refuge and curse, with silence simultaneously weakening and seducing, leaving the self fractured before wholeness arrives.","src":"haiku","web":"Looking into a mirror reveals a broken self, and the world's colorful freedom is exposed as hollow deception — sadness gently reflects the only honest answer, as the narrator wonders whether returning to monochrome solitude is preferable to being fooled by brightness.","fable":"The song stages freedom as a curse rather than a gift: liberty arrives, the isolation and noise fall silent, and yet in that stillness the singer starts to dissolve — the very transparency he longed for is what makes him lose sight of himself. Ling tosite sigure builds the whole thing on a color grammar, setting a colorful illusion against a monochrome world and insisting that the only real miracle must be sounded inside that grayscale, as if hoping to hear vividness back rather than see it. The pivot is the mirror: peering in, he meets a blood-soaked version of himself glaring back, so the frustration of the title is self-recognition — the reflection is the enemy, and every empty space carved into the heart becomes another surface where sorrow quietly projects an answer. What saves the read from despair is the final logic that touching a scream is how you touch the future, a bargain where pain is the only conductive material left, screamed twice like a dare. And the closing plea to be left alone, delivered just before the mirror shatters, reframes the breakdown as the last act still under his control — better to smash the reflection than keep obeying it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","madness & the mind","freedom & escape"],"means":"freedom arriving as the very condition that erases the self","built":"smashing the mirror claimed as the last act still under his control"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~missing-ling":{"haiku":"The world has lost the speaker; fragmented identity breaking apart while grasping for a frozen moment of recognition.","src":"haiku","web":"Identity dismembered and scattered like glass, the narrator asks whether they have been forgotten, while clinging to a single eternal moment — the day of first finding someone — against a colourless city and the steady erosion of sensory memory.","fable":"TK writes dissolution as a technical malfunction: the self is a mechanism whose wiring can be altered, whose memories turn to glass, and the song asks a lover to intervene at the level of hardware rather than heart. The plea flickers between two impossible requests—permission to be detonated and scattered, then a whispered wish to be held perfectly still, frozen at the exact instant before time can no longer be rewound. What makes it devastating is where the sensory data survives longest: the smell of oranges breathing inside winter, an afterimage burned onto the eyelids, a map of touch running to saturation, all of it filed under a memory that erases itself the moment the eyes reopen. Even the English shards work as symptoms rather than statements, foreign fragments that surface like error codes when the native language can no longer hold, until the endless is named not as eternity but as the loop that keeps failing to end. The refrain that the lost thing has itself gone missing—and the small, terrified check for whether anything at all remains—reads the erasure as it happens, a consciousness narrating its own deletion and still reaching, just now, for the one who might catch the pieces.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title puns on the band's own name — 凛 romanizes as Ling, so the missing link is a missing 凛 — and the lyric enacts it, the world screaming that it can no longer find him.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","nostalgia & memory","love & desire"],"means":"sensory data outlasting the self that stored it","built":"English shards surfacing as error codes when native language fails"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~moment-a-rhythm-short-ver":{"haiku":"A fragmented, painful attempt to reach someone across distance; measuring intimacy in impossible units while drowning in isolation.","src":"haiku","web":"Measured in increments as precise as 12 centimetres, an emotional and perceptual distance from another person proves unbridgeable — the speaker plugs their ears, closes their eyes, and holds their breath, caught between the urge to reach someone and the inevitability of remaining apart.","fable":"A song that turns recording into a violence of preservation: the recurring 12 centimeters is the diameter of a CD, so the speaker keeps insisting he draws the beloved at that width, and then that he stabs her every 12 centimeters — pressing her image into the groove is indistinguishable from wounding her. Ling tosite sigure's signature mode, a whispered melody strung over jagged math-rock, mirrors that split: tenderness and puncture in the same gesture. As the phrases that carry her mutate from unfindable to ambiguous to finally resonating, everything is repeatedly destroyed, as if the only way to fix a moment is to shatter it into something replayable. The closing turn toward a world she doesn't know, surfacing on a day whose familiarity has been forgotten, reads the whole exercise as grief: he can loop the disc forever but can no longer actually reach her.","fv":2,"fnote":"The album cut is less than half the song: moment A rhythm was the band’s major-label debut single, one unbroken 16-minute-50 track sold in a tall case with a 48-page book of photographs TK shot in London — and just A moment carries only its 7-minute-17 abridgment.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"the only way to fix a moment is to shatter it into something replayable","built":"the CD's 12-centimeter diameter makes pressing a groove and wounding the same gesture"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~monster": {"haiku":"Secret, half-formed dreams tangle into a grotesque entity; the speaker harbors something monstrous that dances in unconsciousness.","src":"haiku","web":"Phantom residues of a shared dream still live in the body, and when those memories surface the narrator collapses back into the other person's arms — an anatomy of emotional haunting where a past connection reasserts itself as a physical, uncontrollable force.","fable":"MONSTER stages creation as a possession the artist cannot control or take back: a residual dream-trace on the body reawakens and pulls the other person down, and secrecy itself becomes the engine — the recurring HIMITSU-MEKI (secret-seeming) tag detonating into bang and motion turns concealment into rhythm. TK builds the whole song out of that fracture, sensation splitting off from being and dancing on its own, a phantom perfected only into a flawless fake — the counterfeit completion that names i'mperfect's whole anxiety. The refusal to look back (I'm no good at turning around) leaves the narrator afraid of the gap between sky and white, the words themselves splintering mid-line, and the sole English line lands as diagnosis rather than boast: the confession that whatever is making this beautiful, transparent, meaningless motion is not the self but a monster it is stuck housing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Its central coinage mirrors its album-mate: MONSTER's counterfeit of the completed form is Kimitooku's completed form of the counterfeit, reversed — the two most buried i'mperfect cuts share one phrase turned inside out.","themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"creativity split from the self, dancing on its own as a flawless fake","built":"HIMITSU-MEKI recurs as a detonating tag that turns concealment into rhythm"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~nakano-kill-you":{"haiku":"A relentless rhythm invades the mind, wounding with invisible beats that drive the listener toward self-destruction through invisible signals.","src":"haiku","web":"An inescapable rhythmic force operates like a predator — manipulating the narrator's body and mind without consent, pulling them into a distorted world where the question of whose fault \"now\" is goes unanswered and self-destruction feels like the only available response.","fable":"The song stages the studio itself as the arena of self-destruction: Ling tosite sigure's signature high-velocity, register-shredding attack becomes the subject, not just the method — a frozen inspiration and warped world where an unhearable rhythm keeps swaying the listener despite them. Its central move is a reversal: the opening threat aimed outward (your vibrations coming to kill me) is turned back on the singer and then handed to the you, whose request to be killed by their own self locates the violence entirely inside the head, in the deep place where the beat resounds. The recurring nakano tag, left untranslated and finally chanted alone four times, works less as a decipherable phrase than as an incantatory tic — a piece of private code the track refuses to gloss, so its meaning lives in the compulsion of its repeating rather than in any content. Against that, the lone Japanese line of doubt — whose fault is now? — reads as the one moment the mask drops, exposing paralysis beneath the manic surface: a 2007 album literally titled Inspiration is DEAD diagnosing creation as a kind of assisted suicide.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Nakano is the Tokyo district the chant hammers into percussion — and the surname of the band's own drummer, Pierre Nakano; the lyric's first line names countless metal-stick play, so the killing rhythm is literally the drumsticks.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"outward threat turned back inward, violence located inside the head","built":"nakano chanted four times bare, a private code the track refuses to gloss"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~neighbormind":{"haiku":"Connection frays as identity unravels, left behind by a severing that binds rather than frees.","src":"haiku","web":"Abandoning a miracle behind them, the narrator broadcasts distress signals (\"Mayday Mayday\") into loneliness after someone's sudden death, cycling through existential questions about identity and direction until accepting that solitude itself — being \"dyed red\" by it — is what will ultimately save them.","fable":"The song stages a self trapped between grammatical tenses—I am and I was—and treats escaping them as the only route to survival. Its governing image is a color war: a red incantation and a blue magic that the singer alternately casts off, tears apart, and wants to forget, until red overwhelms everything and the recurring line about being dyed red becomes both wound and revelation, the iron smell of blood bleeding into the palette. The distress signals scaffold this—Mayday sliding into the polite May I help you?, help as both a cry and a service offered—so that rescue and self-annihilation keep changing places, culminating in the paradox that solitude itself saved him and swallowed the revolution. The title neighbormind, twinned with forevermind, names the wager the whole song makes: that identity survives not by holding onto the fixed self but by threading you and me together with a transparent filament and dissolving the boundary between them, memory and bond preserved precisely by being hidden. What could read as breakdown is thus reframed as revival—the lost life reconnected, the self commanded to break out of its own tenses and outrun even the beloved—so the red staining that ends the track lands not as defeat but as a chosen, almost sacramental transformation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"意図 (intent) and 糸 (thread) are homophones — both ito — so the tangled intent, the question of whose intent the leftover life carries, and the invisible thread binding the two of them are one sound.","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape","love & desire"],"means":"identity surviving by threading self and other with a transparent filament","built":"red staining at the close landed as chosen transformation rather than defeat"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~o-f-t":{"src":"web","web":"A phantom relationship constructed entirely of lies unravels in a gray, cold sky — both people are described as scenes full of deception, and the narrator mourns something nostalgic that may never have been real, dissolving like a star into mutual unreality.","fable":"A song built almost entirely from one looping cluster of images — a phantom couple, a lie of a story, a grey 22:00 — where the point is precisely that it will not develop, a relationship the narrator already knows is see-through and doomed to end. Ling tosite sigure's early sound (staccato guitar, cold precision, English fragments dropped into the Japanese like the Three-Five distance measuring the gap between two people) is doing the emotional work the words refuse to. The turn arrives only near the end: the wound-covered song, the lie-filled scenery, and finally the lie-filled you all resemble me. That closing move rereads the whole loop — the false story wasn't happening to him, it was him, and calling the other person a liar is really a confession that he can't change his own shape and is quietly vanishing like a star into a cold future sky.","fv":2,"fnote":"A near-seven-minute epic from a band of two-minute detonations, and nobody knows what the initials mean: fans have asked O.F.T’s meaning openly for years and the band has never answered — the early catalog’s longest statement keeps its own name a secret.","themes":["artifice & performance","heartbreak & loss","identity & becoming"],"means":"calling the other a liar as a confession he cannot change his own shape","built":"the looping cluster never develops, the staccato guitar doing the emotional work the words refuse"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~onmyoji-edit-mixed-by-romesh-dodangoda":{"src":"web","web":"Haunted by a curse-filled void and the sound of a flute, the narrator questions whether to kill the light or the darkness, sitting with inward resentment and solitary desire — a theme-song portrait of the lone, conflicted figure at the centre of the Onmyoji world."},
"ling-tosite-sigure~perfake-perfect": {"haiku":"Reality is a numerical hologram masking identical falsehoods in everyone; enslavement is the shared condition of existence.","src":"haiku","web":"Numbers dye us and medicine manages despair, but the real truth is a lie and every belief others hold about the narrator is fake — the borderline between monster and human ebbs and flows like a shoreline, more fragile than a hologram drawn in sand.","fable":"The portmanteau title says the thesis outright: perfection here is a fake, and the song is a screed against a world sanded down to numbers, where only what's projected on a screen gets swallowed and the cure for despair is a future guaranteed painless and error-free. TK's target is that anesthetized flawlessness — a slave dressed up as perfect — and the trap is that the accuser is caught inside it too. The refrain of total knowing turns hollow at once: I know everything, everyone believes in me, but all of it is really a fake, so Who am I, who are you dissolves the ground under both. The image-system is deliberately eroding: the boundary between monster and human keeps advancing and retreating like a coastline, and what's drawn in that sand is more fragile than a hologram — feeling itself filed clearly and definitively into the unsolved-case drawer. Then the interrogation collapses into stammered particles — lie, truth, fake, human, hologram — the language breaking down into a chant that begs you to show your number and dance, slave, until the singer catches himself asking whether he's a slave too. The read is the mask and the underside at once: a song that mocks manufactured perfection while confessing that its own everything is counterfeit, ending not on an answer but on the two words spinning against each other, Perfake, Perfect, with no way left to tell them apart.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming","politics & society"],"means":"mocking manufactured perfection while confessing one's own everything is counterfeit","built":"the portmanteau title fuses the accusation and the confession into a single word"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~re-aotomation":{"src":"web","web":"Being remade against one's will into something mechanical, the narrator cycles through a remaking loop that erases feeling and replaces it with forced dancing — a portrait of dehumanisation where hatred and meaningless repetition spiral together under the command to just perform."},
"ling-tosite-sigure~replica":{"haiku":"Solitary phantom drifting in delayed reverb space: the speaker is a floating copy unable to escape the illusion of connection.","opus":"Suspended alone in reverb and delay, the self dissolves into a floating replica trapped in a blurred cage, playing at solitude two centimeters inside a delusion, terrified of what it's becoming yet willing to let the beloved shatter everything.","sonnet":"Floating in fantasy two centimetres from reality, a self reduced to a replica drifts in aerial solitude, unable to escape a blurred cage, reaching toward someone while suspecting the whole connection is delusion.","src":"haiku","web":"Floating alone like a replica in the sky, the narrator pretends to be self-sufficient while acknowledging they are a blurry, broken copy — however far they run, the illusion of independence collapses and they cannot escape being a vague, drifting figure.","fable":"Intimacy routed entirely through signal-processing gear, so that touch never lands live but returns as its own delayed echo, a copy of contact rather than the thing itself. The whole song floats: a body strung up on an antenna wire, playing alone in the air, sizing desire at a fantasy two centimeters wide it can never close. Each time the beloved is reached it registers only as tremor and static, the you arriving as a smeared duplicate. That is the mask and the wound at once, since the singer cannot leave the blur-edged cage or bear direct closeness. He would rather fall and become a copy that merely resembles this place than risk the real.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"intimacy that arrives only as its own delayed echo, never live","built":"desire measured at a two-centimeter gap the song never closes"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~sadistic-summer":{"haiku":"Summer's seductive cruelty erases sensation; a knife cuts emotion into meaninglessness, leaving only three-second fragments.","src":"haiku","fable":"A feeling that only surfaces once the other person is gone gets attacked into nonexistence, the song using its own compulsive looping as the weapon. The refrain begs to be abducted by summer, a passivity dressed as seduction, but the verse turns the knife inward on the emotional record itself, hacking it to mush until the speaker can flatly insist no feeling is anywhere, no afterimage is anywhere. The tell is the timestamp: a taunting three-seconds-ago that then dissolves into the claim that even three seconds exist nowhere, so the erasure has to be re-performed endlessly because it never takes. That circularity, not any narrative, is the content — the sadism is self-directed, a mind stabbing at its own memory to prove it feels nothing while the repetition confesses it feels everything.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"self-directed erasure that keeps failing because the feeling never leaves","built":"three-seconds-ago timestamp dissolves into nowhere, then must be re-performed endlessly"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~seacret-cm":{"haiku":"Voices summon from the void; gazing into the coastline reveals dissolution, where seeing and disappearing become synonymous.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sparse coastal miniature about a presence that can only be reached by an act of looking that also erases it: peering into the scene turns day to night, and peering behind a leftover fragment of the addressed you dissolves it. The one refrain, a disembodied voice pleading to be found, gets louder exactly as the seer is vanishing, so the imperative to look up and the world's disappearing arrive in the same breath. Fitting the fragmentary title, the song withholds who is calling and who is fading, and lets that unresolved cry stand as its whole subject.","fv":2,"themes":["erasure by looking","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the plea to be found grows louder as the seer vanishes"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~secret-g":{"haiku":"A machine-like person stripped of feeling becomes instrumentalized, their emotions themselves electrified into machinery that cuts and destroys.","src":"haiku","web":"A relationship hollowed out by performance — the narrator plays the mechanized other like a puppet, both parties hiding behind secret faces and shattered-glass personas, until emotional expressionlessness becomes the default and connection is only the illusion of connection.","fable":"The you here is a guitar, and TK writes the whole song as a lover's and a torturer's address to his instrument: the mechanical you he wants to make sound, the fretwork, the arpeggio he can't yet coax out, the feedback that won't stop. That literalism is the point on a 2010 Sigure record built from exactly this white-knuckle guitar language. The read lives in a two-stage flip. First the machine is expressionless and electric-less until the narrator, alone, pries open its electrical workings, cuts it apart, then hurls it into a limitless sky where infinity resonates and it trembles into sounding for the first time by itself, a secret boy in its own secret dance. Then the second flip retracts the whole distance he'd built: the electric secret face he unmasked resembles him, shattered like glass, the secret weapon that woke was only ever his own, and the last word is not secret dance but secret fake. What looked like a man animating a dead object turns out to be a man discovering the machine is a mirror, and the exposure he performed on it exposes that his own expression is a mechanism too, which is why the ecstatic dance can only resolve as a fake.","fv":2,"fnote":"TK has said he built this one as an engineer rather than a guitarist — piano, synths and insect-thin electronics assembled at the desk — so the song that stages a man prying sound out of his instrument was itself made with the guitar at arm’s length, the secret fake of the final line extending to its own production.","themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"animating a dead machine turns into discovering it was a mirror all along","built":"the ecstatic dance the narrator coaxes from the guitar resolves as secret fake, exposure collapsing into self-exposure"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~self-hacking": {"src":"web","web":"Numbly confronting a zombie-like reflection that cannot feel anything, the narrator pleads to be shocked into sensation, diving toward truth and hacking at their own constructed facades in search of a genuine revolution underneath the manufactured self.","fable":"The song is built on a craving that can no longer be satisfied — there's no shock, no jolt, the brain has flatlined into a zombie facing itself — and the dare Shock me if you can becomes a taunt aimed inward, because the buggy culprit everyone's hunting is you. TK maps addiction and self-discovery onto the same loop: a ring of adrenaline, the instant of becoming a genius that's always just about to steal him away, caffeine gone, arousal revealed as performance. The hacking, then, is an attempt to breach one's own firewall to reach a truth that keeps getting mistyped as Lie to truth — the tongue slipping between lie and truth until they're the same keystroke. The children's-game refrain kagome kagome, the surrounded-blindfolded-child chant, drops in as the buried revolution left behind in the past, so the self he's trying to crack is guarded by something old and playful and cruel. It closes on a coined paradox that only Japanese lets him make: not death-and-revival but already-dead-and-reborn, the self-hack succeeding only by killing the self it wanted to find.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"breaching one's own firewall to reach a self that keeps slipping into lie","built":"the kagome kagome children's chant drops in as the old guardian of the self being cracked"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~sergio-echigo":{"haiku":"Betrayal and silence: the speaker whispers lies to a lover who withers, their identities dissolving into each other's masks.","opus":"A dreamlike, unsettling reckoning with betrayal and death-searching for a twilight song while withering a lover and the flowers-that dissolves into questions of whose face this even is and a vanished woman turning her back with a faint smile.","sonnet":"A betrayal that wilted someone like a flower haunts the speaker at dusk, and the resulting fracture of identity — whose face is this, really? — keeps spilling out as compulsive, broken speech that the other person can hear but not receive.","src":"haiku","web":"A confession of betrayal delivered into a relationship already rotting — withered flowers and cruel exchanges mark the moment the speaker admits having destroyed the other person, both parties now trapped in mutual decline and unable to stop spitting words like wounds.","fable":"Named for a footballer who never appears, the song treats the name as a placeholder, fitting for a piece whose real subject is identity slipping loose from any fixed face. The first verse hunts for a twilight melody and turns violent, a betrayal that leaves both a person and a flower withered, the whole scene shadowed by a wish for the night to end everything. Its center is a face-swap panic staged as looped questions, isn't this not your face, isn't this not mine, asked back and forth until neither pronoun holds. The scraps of English arrive as broken, bracketed shards spat between the lines, sound stripped of meaning. It closes on a named woman gone missing, glimpsed from behind with a faint smile, the one concrete person present exactly as an absence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness","violence & murder"],"means":"identity dissolving under looped questions until neither pronoun holds","built":"the footballer's name as title is a placeholder for a song about faces that refuse to stay fixed to any name"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~serial-number-of-turbo": {"haiku":"Unattainability haunts pursuit as nostalgia replaces present connection; the speaker chases an incompletable dream through distortion and rupture.","opus":"Longing for the first-page version of oneself, the person on the map from back when dreams were held, and pleading to have that heart restored. Chasing but never catching that past self, the speaker accepts that even a soul full of holes can be beautiful, sure of reuniting with the beloved before death.","sonnet":"A search for a lost serial number stands in for the attempt to find the self one used to be — the map of that earlier person is now a hole-riddled dream glimpsed from a distance — and the speaker chases it knowing they will never quite catch it, but resolves to change the serial number quietly and sharpen into something new before death.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is TK's most nakedly nostalgic conceit: the self as software you could restore from backup, if you only knew the serial number. An unbreakable wall and an unfillable hole sit permanently there, and the wish is to reboot to page-one-of-myself, to give back the Heart and the White — to key in the code and call, call, call the original version home. The turn that saves it from mere regret is the second verse's grace: wearing the glow that the hole itself emits isn't so bad, and the warped, hole-riddled map, seen from above, was beautiful as-is. So the Turbo he keeps begging for isn't a fix that erases the damage but a boost to keep chasing a self he can never catch — chased and chased and never caught, wanting to meet. It lands on quietly changing the serial number and sharpening the turbo anyway, because he'll get to meet you before he dies — the reboot reframed not as undo but as one more lap run toward a reunion that only the running makes possible.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"夕景 — the vivid-sunset word — is the #5 album's shared nostalgia object: the title track cries out to sunset-scapes and this song wants to dance with that era's, both reaching for the first-page self through the same word.","themes":["nostalgia & memory","identity & becoming","love & desire"],"means":"chasing the original self not to undo damage but to keep the chase alive","built":"the serial-number conceit reframes nostalgia as a system-restore command"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~shandy":{"haiku":"Reinvention demands erasure of the true self for the sake of another's image, hiding what actually breaks beneath the surface.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about self-erasure staged as intimacy: the speaker keeps hearing someone crying inside him even as the refrain announces I'll become you starting today, so the love story is really a slow suicide of the original self, the true me he says he wants to forget. Ling tosite sigure's 2010 sound is built on exactly this friction, TK's brittle falsetto fracturing over hardcore attack, and Shandy weaponizes that split, the mixed drink of the title standing for a diluted, cut-with-something identity. The tell is the katakana slogan for New Person, stamped in the angular script Japanese reserves for foreign or artificial things, so the new self arrives pre-branded, a manufactured replacement rather than a rebirth. The final turn drops the mask entirely: he admits he has painted over himself in coats so no one can see, then offers the last barely-holding rhythm face to charm you, the performance of a personality worn until it becomes the only remaining skin.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"self-erasure through intimacy, the new self arriving pre-manufactured","built":"the katakana branding of New Person marks the replacement as foreign and artificial"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~sitai-miss-me":{"haiku":"Chasing phantoms and automaton rituals through a maze of decay; desire curdles into meaninglessness on an assembly line of false fulfillment.","src":"haiku","web":"Turning on the lights to compare the present self to a past one, the narrator finds only a vivid maze of melting desires and numb, unheard pain — a conveyor-belt drift through inauthenticity, where everything wanted was perhaps always fake.","fable":"The title pun is the engine: Sitai fuses shitai (the wish, I want to) with shitai (a corpse), so miss me becomes both a plea to be longed for and an invitation to search a body for something no longer there. The song stages a producer-generation's disenchantment as automation — a life carried on a conveyor belt until the dream simply arrived, achieved and hollow — where the seeking self dives repeatedly toward fake, and the object once desired dissolves into memory or a vivid maze. Its structure is a descent: the taunting lullaby of sinking someone, then the plea to reanimate the automation and feed the miracle, until the final turn admits the thing being sought may never have existed at all. The refusal is the meaning — the closing collapse of the help me refrain into kill me is the point where sensation and receptivity, the two faculties begged to return, are finally asked to end the speaker instead, the wish to be wanted curdling into the wish to be the corpse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the wish to be wanted curdling into the wish to be the corpse","built":"title fusing the Japanese words for wish and cadaver into one"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~sosos":{"haiku":"Nameless distress summons phantoms that reflect distorted inner worlds through plastic facades and illusory transparency.","opus":"A nameless distress signal that drags its ghosts along, where beautiful artifice is exposed as a fake plastic pop trap and the only wish is to reclaim the self left behind while telling your own sorrow to drop dead.","sonnet":"Nameless distress signals haul along ghosts that no one can identify, and the only response is to detonate them with artificial J-pop spectacle — beautiful, fake, and deliberately violent — until the sadness itself is punctured.","src":"haiku","web":"Unending delusions and rupturing impulses cascade through a narrator who cannot locate their own \"SOS\" — ghosts dragged along by a nameless distress signal, plastic J-pop framed as a beautiful imitation trap, and blue jewels staining the world while the speaker scrambles to reclaim the self left behind.","fable":"The title is the key: a distress signal with no name attached, stuttering into an extra syllable, which the band answers not with rescue but with sabotage. TK builds the whole song as a fake pop artifact — he keeps calling it secret Plastic JPOP, a beautiful counterfeit's trap — and then weaponizes that plastic against feeling, the recurring imperative amounting to drop dead, you sorrow of yours, driven into the eardrum. The genius is that the treatment is the disease's own material: to save the nameless ghosts trailing the SOS he doesn't decode the signal, he distorts it — delay ping-ponging in a labyrinth, the driving gone warped — until the fake transparency itself becomes the only thing that can wrap around you, a rescue enacted as glossy, deafening demolition.","fv":2,"themes":["artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"distorting the distress signal rather than decoding it as the only rescue","built":"the extra syllable in the title stutters the SOS into wordlessness","fvr":"2.4"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~souzou-no-security":{"haiku":"Perception fractures into fiction and game logic: ambiguous words seduce while the speaker mistakes others for illusion.","opus":"Mutual seduction built on vague words and imagined safety, where two people caught in a fiction can no longer tell whether they're deceiving each other or being deceived by a game-sensed illusion, holding their breath as reality slips into invention.","sonnet":"Two people seduce and misread each other through deliberately vague language inside a fiction that feels like it's been seen before, the whole relationship an imagination game where reality is a shared hallucination neither can exit.","src":"haiku","web":"Two people caught in a game of mutual illusion, seducing each other with vague words and fictional dreams until neither can distinguish imagination from reality — the \"security\" of the title is built entirely on invented sensation and shared delusion.","fable":"Ling tosite sigure make security itself the fiction — the title's imagined security is a lover's suggestion the singer knows is a spell yet surrenders to anyway. The song is built entirely on two verbs that decay in each other's presence: yuuwaku (to seduce) and sakkaku (to misperceive), so that being seduced by ambiguous words and hallucinating the other person become the same act, and the mistake feels like safety. The turn comes in the second half, where the misperception stops being personal and metastasizes outward — the fiction you sing, the world hallucinates it; the fiction vanished into space, the two of us hallucinate it — until subject and object of illusion have swapped so many times that no one is left to tell the lie from the truth, only a shared spinning-in (muchuu ni natte iku, repeated to the point of dizziness). That is the real content: security is the willingness to let a beautiful fiction rewrite your memory, and the flat English chant of imagination-fiction-game-smile-play frames the whole thing as exactly that — a game the singer plays with open eyes, calling it love.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","love & desire","madness & the mind"],"means":"surrendering to a spell you already know is fiction","built":"flat English chant framing love as a game played with open eyes"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~super-sonic-aurorally": {"src":"web","web":"Finding no empty seat among the rushing crowds and treating Tokyo as an adversary, the narrator erupts into a flight toward the aurora — dissolving memories nobody else can see, erasing all concept of conformity versus originality in a last-minute transcendent dive.","fable":"A late-period flight song where escape velocity is the whole subject: the aurora is a thing to beat wings toward and break past, the atmosphere is left deliberately imperfect (未完璧) so that breath fails and consciousness and scenery both switch off. The engine underneath is a binary interrogation TK keeps firing — are you wearing the ready-made or wearing individuality, which, which — so the sonic boom is the sound of tearing off imposed selfhood at a deadline that keeps surging in. Its irony is that transcendence and self-erasure are the same motion here: the singer floats alone in a galaxy nobody else occupies, touches the aurora, and the payoff is a last brilliance seen by no one — freedom purchased at the cost of being visible to anyone at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","identity & becoming","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"transcendence and self-erasure arriving as the same motion at the same velocity","built":"the atmosphere left deliberately imperfect so that consciousness switches off at the threshold"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~telecaster-no-sinjitu":{"haiku":"The truth of the telecaster is only a phantom of something else—deception wrapped in vivid imagery that masks the real.","src":"haiku","web":"A person who lies compulsively while going mad hides a sharp face behind laughter — the Telecaster's truth is revealed to be merely the Les Paul's afterimage, using guitar types as metaphors for layered deception where what appears authentic is always concealing a deeper, more dangerous reality.","fable":"A song about the gap between what a person truly is and the residue of him that anyone else gets to see, built entirely on a gearhead's metaphor: the real self is a Telecaster hidden in the dark, but what you perceive is only the afterimage of a Les Paul. The opening figure is a man who keeps lying while coming apart, laughing to bury a knife-sharp face beneath the smile, and the stuttered English-tinged refrain about a vivid dusk-scene style frames that mask as a pose held for the light. Ling tosite sigure's method here — clipped, single-coil brightness stabbing against noise — literalizes the argument, since the trebly Telecaster stands for the unglamorous truth while the thick, prestige Les Paul is the flattering illusion the observer mistakes for it. The closing collapse, where the truth of despair simply equals the truth of despair, drops the guitar conceit to admit the mask cannot be resolved into anything but itself.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"the true self hidden while observers mistake the flattering illusion for it","built":"the Telecaster-versus-Les-Paul contrast literalizes the unglamorous-truth-versus-prestige-mask argument"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~telecastic-fake-show":{"haiku":"Weariness with freedom and false emotion cascades into a sadistic cycle where feelings betray and sensation becomes distorted.","opus":"Sick of a hollow freedom until there's nothing left to sing, the self splits off from its own future and performs a solitary, criminal fake show-stabbing the finger of emotion, shutting its eyes and cutting its ears against a sadistic red temptation.","sonnet":"Emotional autonomy has rotted from both sides — the speaker is exhausted by someone else's freedom and by his own performed feelings — until sensation itself is reduced to a sadistic loop of self-harm and staged spectacle.","src":"haiku","web":"Conflicting inner personalities pull the narrator between exhaustion with freedom and fear of it, leaving nothing left to sing about — a deliberate psychological word-salad capturing dissociation and the suppressed red temptation of darker impulses that can't be named.","fable":"A singer diagnoses his own feeling as a rigged broadcast, then keeps performing anyway, because there is nothing underneath left to stop. The verses report a triple exhaustion — sick of the other’s freedom, then of his own, with nothing left to sing — while TK's shredded falsetto, doubled against 345's flatter voice, splits the self into player and watching crowd. Cruelty gets branded onto both the addressee and the singer, as though emotion were a switchable stage effect. The recurring self-mutilation — stabbing the fingers that would gesture feeling, sealing the eyes, severing the ears — makes sensory shutdown the whole point. What resists reading is the machine-English itself, snapped into Japanese grief so that circuitry becomes the only vocabulary a heart has left when it no longer trusts its own signal.","fv":2,"fnote":"The band’s first single, released April 2008 on the indie Nakano Records, it reached number 17 on Oricon before any major label touched them — and fans read the invented title word as TK’s Telecaster fused with a telecast, the guitar and the broadcast collapsed into one fake thing. The band has never glossed it.","themes":["artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"sensory self-mutilation as the endpoint of a hollowed performance","built":"the doubled voices split singer from watching crowd inside a single skull"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~ten-to-ten": {"haiku":"Numb detachment masks deep suffering as the speaker oscillates between emotional shutdown and intrusive presence; connection remains perpetually fractured.","opus":"A wish to seize and stop a beating heart so feelings need never be voiced again, caught in an unwaking dream that can't yet be killed. Fusing and fragmenting at once, the speaker's emptiness is finally connected and erased over time only because the other, and thus he, exists.","sonnet":"The speaker grabs a non-beating heart and wonders whether swallowing the silent echo of past days would stop the emotional overflow; the song floats between never beginning and never ending, invaded by an undying dream, until the final image of time and rain connecting two people dissolves the emptiness.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title names a game of connect-the-dots, and TK plays a whole song out of that geometry: two points that only mean something once a line joins them. He wants to seize a heart that has stopped breathing and swallow the residue so no more feeling has to be expelled, which sounds like a death wish but keeps stalling on its own threshold — nothing begins, nothing ends, it just floats, still can't be killed. The refrain's cruelest engine is a paradox of union: become more one, become more scattered, in the same breath, as if intimacy and disintegration were the same motion. What holds the song back from nihilism is the turn on the band's own name — the untranslatable ache gets threaded and erased by toki to ame, time and rain, the two characters of Sigure's own name prised apart and doing the consoling. So the read that begins as a fantasy of stopping the heart lands on whether the un-waking dream is still something you can love, and answers only that you exist, so I exist — the dot needs the other dot for the line to be a line at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"union and disintegration spoken in the same breath as though they were the same word","built":"the band's own name prised apart — toki to ame — to do the consoling"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~this-is-is-this":{"haiku":"Satiation brings paralysis; being filled by another erases the self until movement becomes impossible.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being consumed by intimacy until nothing of you is left, staged as the erotic and the violent bleeding into one word. It opens with the other person pressing something to the narrator's throat and the narrator answering by dissolving — I don't exist, half of this looks like a lie — so from the first line the self is already withdrawing, playing a hide-and-seek only he knows the rules of. The engine is the mantra of being filled: satisfied, satisfied, repeated toward a climax that keeps promising just a little more, until on the second pass the same word flips to violated, and the phrase you no longer need the me that's inside you turns satisfaction into erasure — to be completely filled by the other is to be emptied of oneself. The title stutter this is is this? enacts that collapse in miniature, a question that can't finish forming and folds back on itself, mirroring a narrator who can no longer move. TK's high, whispered, disembodied delivery and the band's brittle math-rock jitter are the point rather than decoration: the ghosted parenthetical voice urging get stronger arrives precisely as the front voice goes inert, so the encouragement lands as the last flicker of a self already gone under.","fv":2,"fnote":"The album’s most violent lyric enters on its gentlest instrument: by TK’s account the track opens on a twelve-string guitar, chiming brightness laid down before the serration begins.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire"],"means":"erotic fulfillment and self-erasure sharing one vocabulary","built":"the title stutter enacts a self too consumed to finish a thought"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~tk-in-the-uk":{"src":"web","web":"A bittersweet song about distance and deferred reunion, built around dusk imagery and fragments of shared memory: the narrator promises to meet someone someday, watches morning glories bloom in the night, and closes with the line 'beautifully forgot' — acceptance worn as a kind of grief.","fable":"A song about clutching at someone across distance and time until the clutching curdles into forgetting. It opens in an almost-pastoral register — clumsily spelled words, English written awkwardly by hand, a promise to meet someday, an evening-sky song offered as tribute — and the recurring demand is to be shown the other person's future, their fragments, phrased first as let me see and then, uglier, as pierce me with them. TK's fracturing tells the real story: the morning-glory that recurs through the dusk section keeps withering, vanishing, drying out, a flower that closes by afternoon standing in for an affection with no daylight left. The embedded English word for a small gesture, whispered against a chanted verb of spiriting-away, turns tenderness mechanical, a feeling running on loop because it can't advance. It ends by dissolving the two speakers into each other — was it you crying or me — and lands on the cruelest possible resolution to all that longing: I forgot you beautifully.","fv":2,"fnote":"The official title is TK in the 夕景 — evening scene, read yuukei, a homophone that smuggles U.K. into a Japanese dusk — and listeners have long heard the Sex Pistols’ Anarchy in the U.K. behind the construction, with TK’s initials swapped in. The band has never confirmed the echo.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire"],"means":"longing curdling into forgetting as the cruelest possible resolution","built":"the morning-glory withers on each pass, timing the affection's death"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~tornado-g":{"haiku":"Filth and corruption seep through rotting worlds where isolation offers no escape from one's own decay.","src":"haiku","fable":"A wreck of a self begs to be witnessed at its filthiest against a world it calls rotten, and the one who looks answers only by driving a knife of broken words. The recurring see-saw, spelled first in katakana and then as the English see-saw, is the whole relationship: two people locked in a tilt where one rises only as the other sinks. The refrain hardens the point by swapping the softer kanji for one — hitori — into the starker 独りで, so the count of aloneness gets colder each time it repeats until nothing but that isolation is left.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"a see-saw relationship where one rises only as the other sinks","built":"the kanji for aloneness is swapped for a starker character each time it repeats"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~tornado-minority": {"haiku":"Consciousness spirals into chaos as thought becomes transparent and language dissolves; isolation is the cost of awareness.","src":"haiku","web":"Stolen words and blurred focus leave the narrator sucked into a spiral of change, screaming as a hyper-minority — the one thing that must not be killed or mixed is personal inspiration, and against the labyrinth of information disguise, identity is distorted but still resonant.","fable":"A song about being a minority of one and refusing to be dissolved into the crowd, built as a spiral (the 変化の螺旋, the whirl of change) that keeps pulling the self toward transparency. The recurring word is 透明 — transparent, but here closer to erased: language gets stolen by someone else, thought goes clear and vanishes, and the safety and focus one clings to are all admitted as counterfeit. Against the diffusing flood of information TK sets one refrain of resistance — don't kill it, don't mix it in, this rejecting form of life is your own inspiration — so the tornado is less weather than the pressure of a networked world trying to homogenize a person into nothing. The turn is the closing English tag, which swaps its own adjective — the frightened minor version of the word flips hyper and starts screaming — the whole point being that visibility is chosen over disappearance, loudness wrung out of a distorted identity rather than an absence of fear.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","surveillance & control","anger & defiance"],"means":"choosing visibility over disappearance by wringing loudness from a distorted self","built":"the closing English tag swaps its adjective — frightened minor flips hyper and screams"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~tornado-mystery":{"haiku":"Within the spiral of life and death, the self chooses slavery while transparent light breaks through feedback and emptiness.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tornado Mystery stages revolution as a suicide leap the singer can never quite complete, and the whole song lives in that gap between wanting to jump and clinging to his own madness and ego at the ledge. TK's paradox runs down the spine of it: he traces one hunger, to be loved in the present moment, only to carve underneath it the opposite refusal, that he can hand this moment to no one. He picks himself as a slave inside a spiral of life and death, then flees into blankness the instant he admits there's no turning back, and the two impulses never resolve because the song's engine is precisely their friction. When he offers even mosaic and plastic if transparency is what you want, and lets a miracle spill out of the despair inside feedback, the tornado becomes his image for grace arriving as noise rather than clarity. The last line jumps at last, leaping down into the revolution, but the earlier awakening that never comes leaves that plunge suspended, more a wish flung at the void than an act.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","identity & becoming","love & desire"],"means":"wanting to leap and clinging to ego at the same ledge","built":"final plunge in the last line suspended as wish rather than act"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~tremolo-a":{"haiku":"Oscillating images of self and other blur together in entanglement, each destabilizing the other in mutual wavering.","src":"haiku","web":"Psychological turbulence destabilizes the narrator's perception of another person — the tangled image sways and trembles, desire curdles into the urge to break, and even after the season passes something inside keeps trembling, leaving emotional aftermath that refuses to resolve.","fable":"A song where wanting to destroy someone and wanting to merge with them turn out to be the same trembling impulse, staged as an experiment the narrator permits himself only for this instant. The whole lyric runs on one word, 揺れている (wavering/trembling) — the beloved's image and the narrator's own image, deliberately made to resemble each other, quiver in identical time and then blink out, so it's never clear whose instability is whose. That confusion is the point: destroying you becomes indistinguishable from becoming you, and the tenderness (twining you in so you won't get soiled) and the aggression (a warped noise driven in to pierce you) are the same gesture from opposite ends. TK's signature falsetto-and-tremolo attack turns the title into a self-portrait of the method — everything shakes because nothing here can hold a stable edge between self and other. It closes with the purple season passing and something still wavering inside him, the experiment leaving the residue it set out to test for.","fv":2,"themes":["destructive merging","love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"destroying and merging with the beloved as the same trembling impulse","built":"the title names the method: TK's tremolo attack makes the song a self-portrait of its own instability"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~trrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrrue-lies":{"src":"web","web":"Staring through shot-through eyes at whether the exposed truth clarifies or muddies, the narrator asks whether a soul that transcends reincarnation can trade its body for love — a Psycho-Pass-adjacent interrogation of moral clarity, authentic feeling, and the price of obtaining either."},
"ling-tosite-sigure~turbocharger-on":{"haiku":"Colors and sounds shift into red and chaos while meaning evaporates, leaving only the act of tearing through incomprehension.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about a singer begging to be told what his own song means as the meaning drains out of it in real time, so the panic is aesthetic before it is emotional. Sound and lie are twinned early (the two words set side by side), and color becomes the gauge of the collapse: the self starts colorless, the other's sound is gray, then everything shifts to red under a sadistic dreamed night, then to a deep-green rain whose truth gets erased. The refrain, demand the meaning of the transparent song, repeats because it can only be answered by its own failure — the meaning keeps vanishing the instant it is invoked, until nothing is understood and the voice just screams. That the plea recurs unchanged while the colors keep mutating is the point: this early Ling tosite sigure piece dramatizes music as something that slices as it disappears, transparent meaning being a contradiction it would rather shred than resolve.","fv":2,"themes":["vanishing meaning","artifice & performance","madness & the mind"],"means":"begging for the meaning that evaporates the instant it is named","built":"the refrain recurs unchanged while the colors keep mutating around it"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~ultra-overcorrection":{"haiku":"Layers of masks and calculations hiding genuine self; the facade keeps getting patched and reinforced even as it crumbles.","src":"haiku","web":"Applying masks, sunglasses, and calculated mystery to conceal one's authentic self, the song confronts the paradox that every layer of concealment still reveals a weak, past self in the window — and ultimately argues that imperfection is its own perfect form.","fable":"The song stages a performance of self-concealment and then dismantles it. The speaker applies mascara to ugliness, sunglasses to foolishness, keeps the mirror fogged and silenced because if it reflected clearly the calculation would stop adding up and the ending would no longer match — an image-system where mask, costume, and illusion are begged never to leave, never to expose. This is Ling tosite sigure's signature nervous mode (TK's stacked English tics, the countdown, the manic falsetto) turned inward as a portrait of curated identity: inferiority capped over, overwritten with superiority, fleeing before being exposed, yet what the window reflects is only the weakened self — the correction is the wound. The title names the pathology precisely: overcorrection past true, a self armored so thoroughly in an ideal that even the imagined perfection rots. And the ending refuses the whole apparatus rather than completing it — the loneliness becomes a bird that leaps clean over the whole superior/inferior axis and dissolves into the sky inside the mirror, arriving at the one line the fogged glass existed to prevent: that incompleteness itself is your finished form.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The refrain quotes the Japanese hide-and-seek call-and-response — mou ii kai? / mada dame (ready or not? — not yet) — with the 3-2-1 as the seeker counting: the hider refusing to end the game; sitai miss me opens on the same call and answers it with a drowning, and make up syndrome names its crazed hide-and-seek outright.","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"armor so thorough the imagined perfection itself rots from inside","built":"loneliness leaping clean over the superior/inferior axis into the sky"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~who-what-who-what":{"haiku":"A labyrinth of manufactured meaning traps consciousness; the ceaseless drone of discord leaves no ground for identity or emotion.","src":"haiku","fable":"Ling tosite sigure build epistemological dread out of the interrogatives themselves: the title's who-what stutter is a mind unable to complete a question. The song stages truth as an unanswered radio call — the repeated demand that truth respond turns God or reality into a dispatcher who won't pick up, while a discord and a blank siren ring on inside the skull. Its central discovery is that freedom itself is counterfeit: to drown in a maze of manufactured meaning and realize you can no longer move inside a constructed freedom, a fabricated mystery, is to find that even the wired-in distortion asking why is part of the trap. The cruelest turn is the farewell to feeling — emotion waving goodbye, emotion as a UFO drifting off — recast not as numbness but as escape velocity: when the scream you aimed outward lodges in yourself, the only exit left is for the self that could still feel to leave the body. What looks like a scream of protest is really the sound of a person watching their own capacity for feeling depart, unable to answer the very interrogation they keep broadcasting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Opening theme for Psycho-Pass: The Movie (2015) — the manufactured-freedom maze the song drowns in is written to the franchise's surveillance premise.","themes":["surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape"],"means":"watching the capacity for feeling depart, unable to stop the departure","built":"title's who-what stutter enacting a mind unable to complete its question"},
"ling-tosite-sigure~yuukei-no-kioku":{"haiku":"Beautiful moments are remembered only through the act of watching others suffer and die, leaving beauty as complicity.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is built almost entirely from repetition, so the read has to be about what that repetition is doing rather than any lyrical progression. TK's lyric turns a grammar of evasion into a loop: every line begins with something (nanika) rather than someone, and the verbs are the small cruelties of self-exoneration — letting something die by looking away, pinning something on another person. The confession never names a subject or an object, only the mechanism of guilt, so the machine-gun tremolo and the shrieked delivery become the point: a mind grinding the same instant of moral failure without ever resolving it, insisting it remembers clearly the beautiful time even as that time vanishes. That the album is titled Inspiration is DEAD sharpens the closing turn, where the loop of memory hardens into a wall built to block the shadow of a beautiful era and collapses into a single flatly-repeated English word — privacy — the sealed-off self as the only refuge left when memory becomes unbearable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"guilt ground on repeat without ever naming subject or object","built":"bare English word privacy as the sealed-off self's only remaining refuge"},
"linkin-park~1stp-klosr-the-humble-brothers-reanimation-feat-jonathan-davis":{"haiku":"A person overwhelmed by repetitive stress and communication breakdown desperately seeks mental relief and escape. They describe cycles of self-harm imagery and the urge to disappear from suffocating pressure.","opus":"The Reanimation rework of One Step Closer splices in Jonathan Davis, deepening the original's suffocating frustration with someone who won't stop talking into a bloodier, more unhinged breaking point. Overload pushed past the edge.","sonnet":"A remix/reinterpretation of One Step Closer — the same unbearable pressure and near-snapping point, now given a rawer, more fragmented feel with added violent imagery of blood and flesh tearing, amplifying the original's sense of total psychological breakdown.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~a-line-in-the-sand":{"haiku":"A group draws a boundary against exploitation and corruption; when leaders betray and abandon them, they prepare to collect what was stolen and face their crime.","opus":"A collective rises against a corrupt, greedy false leader who promised the world, abandoned them to disaster, then fled the ruin he made. Marking a boundary he can't cross, the betrayed swear that reckoning is coming and that he'll answer for the damage, demanding the return of everything he stole.","sonnet":"About betrayal by a leader or ally who ran when things got dangerous — those who followed were blinded by greed, flooded and lost, while the one who was supposed to lead fled the fire; the narrator promises that a day of reckoning is coming and they will reclaim what was taken.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~a-place-for-my-head":{"fable":"The nu-metal breakup song reframed as a fable about parasitic obligation: Shinoda opens by staring at the sky and noticing the sun asks nothing back for the light it lends, then flips that generosity into an accusation against someone who keeps a ledger on every favor. The whole track is a fight over debt he never agreed to owe, which is why the title lands less as a wish for escape than for sovereignty over his own mind. Verse two darkens the picture with a prophecy and a threat braided together, imagining himself hardened into the very predator he's fleeing before turning to relish how the taker's greed has finally left them alone. When Bennington seizes the outro screaming to be left alone, the calm bookkeeping snaps into the raw exhaustion that was under it the whole time, and the desired place turns out to be simply out of reach of someone who mistakes proximity for possession.","fableDeep":"It opens with a cosmology parable — the sun lights the moon without expecting to be owed — and measures the friend against it: generosity that invoices isn't generosity. The nastiest turn is 'maybe someday I'll be just like you,' the fear that exploitation is contagious and the speaker is already learning it. Revenge arrives by simple subtraction rather than violence — 'now you see how quiet it is all alone' — and the title stays deliberately small: not victory, not justice, just a place for my head, rest as the only demand left worth making.","haiku":"Resentment toward someone who makes emotional demands and extracts energy under the guise of past kindness; the speaker seeks escape and rest.","opus":"A song about resentment toward a greedy, controlling person who gives favors only to demand things back, as the speaker, sick of the tension, wants to break free and find peace of his own away from them.","sonnet":"The speaker resents a person who does favors only to demand repayment, using the image of the sun and moon to explain the one-sided dynamic, and ultimately tells them to leave so they can find peace away from the manipulation.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","freedom & escape","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"sovereignty over his own mind framed as the only debt unpaid","built":"Shinoda's calm ledger-keeping snaps open when Bennington seizes the outro screaming","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~a-place-for-my-head-live":{"haiku":"A speaker rejects parasitic obligation and repeated harm. Finding another place means fleeing the enemy.","opus":"Fury at a taker who acts entitled to everything the singer has, sick of the tension and hunger of the relationship; the sun-and-moon image frames it as one-sided debt as he demands space and warns they'll wear out their welcome and end up alone.","sonnet":"About a one-sided, exploitative relationship in which someone does favours and then weaponises them — the narrator is exhausted by the obligation, tension, and emotional debt, and wants to escape to a mental space free of the person who has turned from calm and generous to controlling and predatory.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~a-place-for-my-head-live-at-docklands-arena-london":{"haiku":"Parasitic relationships drain the speaker's essence; they demand repayment for false generosity while the speaker deteriorates under the weight.","opus":"A furious early-Linkin-Park cut about escaping a parasitic relationship built on obligation and greed. Chester and Mike frame a one-sided bond, favors weaponized into debt, and the yearning is simple and total: to break free and find peace apart from someone who only takes. The moon-and-sun opening sets up its whole logic of resented owing.","sonnet":"The moon-and-sun metaphor opens it: you do favors expecting return, treating generosity as debt. The narrator is sick of the tension and hunger, naming the other as someone who used to be generous before wearing out their welcome and ending up alone. The demand is simple — stop feeding your greed on me; let me rest.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~a6-meteora-20-demo":{"haiku":"The speaker drowns in rain and lead while confined in darkness, seeking connection but finding only hollow silence and accumulated emotional weight.","opus":"A Meteora-era demo steeped in insomnia and quiet despair, cataloguing sleepless nights under a crushing heaviness of head and chest. Yet it holds onto a dream and the promise of light to come, weighing suffocation against a stubborn flicker of hope, the band's whole formula still in embryo.","sonnet":"Insomniac isolation rendered in two paired images: a head full of rain and chest full of lead, a broken seam that never fully tears. The narrator waits in darkness for a light that keeps being deferred rather than arriving, holding onto the dream as the only thing left.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~all-for-nothing-feat-page-hamilton":{"haiku":"A defiant person refuses to obey orders or be a soldier, rejecting false promises and broken words while fighting alone for their own purpose.","opus":"Mike Shinoda's hardened refusal to serve a bad-faith bargain, the deal where obedience is promised reward and repaid with betrayal. It's about seeing through manipulation and declining to be anyone's soldier.","sonnet":"About refusing to be controlled by someone who frames compliance as the path to understanding and reward — the narrator sees through the transaction, rejects the role of soldier taking orders, and declares that all of the obedience and trust given to this person was ultimately wasted.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~and-one-hybrid-theory-ep":{"haiku":"Emotional estrangement from family and the violent extraction of unhealthy attachments to find inner peace through necessary severing.","opus":"An early howl of rejection and pent-up fury, the narrator cut off by his own family, bottling everything up, and telling an absent parent that any chance to earn his love has long since passed. A dense rap verse pours out the tangled defiance. This is Linkin Park on their pre-fame Hybrid Theory EP, the California band already welding rap and rock into wounded self-protection.","sonnet":"Someone who locked their pain away so long that love has permanently missed its window — too far gone, too distant, too unknown to be reached now. The second half breaks from anguish into kinetic rap energy, the frustration finding release in wordplay and rhythm rather than resolution, the distance from others turned into a kind of speed.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~announcement-service-public":{"haiku":"Public service hygiene instructions delivered both reversed and forward, reducing health to mechanical obligation.","opus":"A joke interlude, the title an inverted public service announcement, with the first lines written backwards before resolving into the plain reminder to brush your teeth and wash your hands. There's no deeper theme beyond the palindromic gag of childlike hygiene advice.","sonnet":"A brief novelty interlude — the lyric content is simply a public-health reminder to brush your teeth and wash your hands, written forwards and backwards, with no deeper thematic content.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~blackout":{"haiku":"A person discovers lies and betrayal in someone they trusted, confronting deception that cuts deeply while the betrayer takes and takes without accountability.","opus":"Furious address to a deceitful, taking partner who keeps secrets and never gives, the speaker refusing to swallow the lies, before shifting into a dreamlike passage about coming down to collect what has been let go.","sonnet":"Rage at a partner's sustained deception and self-serving denial erupts into a confrontation, followed by a dreamlike descent where the singer calls someone down from a distance, waiting to reclaim what was lost or let go.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~bleed-it-out":{"haiku":"A person expels emotional pain through self-destruction, digging deeper only to discard what they unearth. They perform a cathartic but futile ritual, bleeding themselves out while the world ignores their agony.","opus":"Using violent imagery of nooses, trenches, and blood, the speaker describes purging pain through relentless expression, digging deeper only to throw it away, and forcing others to confront it.","sonnet":"The narrator channels self-destructive creative frustration — digging deeper into himself only to discard the results — describing the process of making music as a compulsive bleeding out that leaves him unsatisfied and still driven.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rap-metal exorcism where the confessional turns into a party. Shinoda's verses stage self-destruction as spectacle: the writing process itself is armed and lethal, each bar a pin pulled, death riding shotgun through a swaggering, gun-metal braggadocio that masks genuine dread. The genius of the hook is its double meaning of purge and squander at once. Draining yourself and burying pain gets recast as dancing on a grave while a crowd cheers, so catharsis and futility collapse into the same gesture: you keep bleeding, keep digging, and the relief is real but disposable, thrown out the instant it arrives. Bennington's bridge rips the mask off, turning private wounds into an accusation flung outward, so the celebration and the self-harm are revealed as one ritual that never actually resolves.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"catharsis and futility collapsed into the same gesture of relentless bleeding","built":"Bennington's bridge rips the mask off the celebration to reveal self-harm and accusation as one ritual"},
"linkin-park~breaking-the-habit":{"fable":"The song frames self-destruction as an addiction cycle, but Chester Bennington's real subject is deeper than metaphor. Nu-metal in 2003 rarely admitted it, yet the confessional here is that the narrator is both the wound and the one reopening it, the aggressor and the confused victim of his own patterns. The verses map a claustrophobic system of locked doors and a room mistaken for safety, where the very medicine being clutched is the sickness. What sounds like a triumphant resolution to quit is quietly terrifying: the bridge accepts fault and lays down the struggle for good, so the promise to end the pattern before the night is out reads less as recovery than as a final exit, self-harm reframed as the only way to stop the loop.","fableDeep":"Built on epistemic collapse: the chorus is a string of I-don't-knows — what's worth fighting for, why the screaming, how it got this way — so the only certainty available is the decision itself. The verses double every image: 'clutching my cure' names the harm as the medicine, the locked door reads as both safety and sealing-in, and the room where others assume he's safe is exactly where the picking-apart happens. Watch the one-word degradation between choruses, 'I know it's not alright' becoming 'I'll never be alright' — hope quietly deleted mid-song. The bridge turns testament: painting it on the walls, claiming sole fault, forswearing all future fights, 'and this is how it ends' — language equally at home in a recovery pledge and a suicide note, and the song never chooses. The final chorus claims clarity while still confessing ignorance of causes. Thesis: the habit is left unnamed so that quitting and dying can occupy the same sentence.","haiku":"A person caught in cycles of self-harm and emotional wounds realizes they are their own worst enemy; they commit to breaking this self-destructive pattern.","opus":"A struggle with self-destructive inner turmoil, the narrator confused and lashing out without knowing why, but resolving to break the destructive habit that has consumed him.","sonnet":"The speaker examines a self-destructive cycle of inner conflict, self-harm, and confusion, acknowledging they are the source of their own pain rather than any outside force. The song ends with a resolve to break the habit — to stop the pattern tonight rather than continue it.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fnote":"Shinoda wrote it — music and words — years before Meteora, about a friend's self-destruction; Bennington heard his own life in it and could barely record the vocal. The song's speaker and its singer met in the middle.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","suicide"],"means":"the vow to end the pattern reads as final exit not recovery","built":"hope quietly deleted mid-song as 'not alright' degrades to 'never be alright'","web":"Someone trapped in a self-destructive cycle — self-harm, addiction, isolation — knowing they're hurting themselves and unable to stop, resolving to break the pattern; Chester Bennington recorded it in tears because it was his own story."},
"linkin-park~burn-it-down":{"haiku":"The singer describes a cycle of building relationships only to destroy them, with the other person's betrayal making them eager for revenge. They vow to fan the flames as the other falls.","opus":"The self-destructive cycle of building something up only to raze it, told as a soured power dynamic between someone who played king and someone who played soldier. The addictive pull of destruction, and the vengeance waiting for when the crown-wearer finally falls.","sonnet":"A song about the betrayal cycle in a relationship where someone was elevated and trusted only to use that power against the narrator — now that the dynamic has collapsed, the narrator watches the fire they helped build consume the person who lit the match.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~burn-it-down-live":{"haiku":"A cycle of mutual betrayal between lovers: building intimacy only to destroy it, their crown of trust exchanged for vengeance as the cycle repeats.","opus":"A cycle of raising something up only to tear it back down, aimed at a betrayer who lifted the narrator on a falsehood. He served them loyally until they cut him down, and now that they've toppled, he savors his turn to feed the fire and watch the wreckage they earned.","sonnet":"A power dynamic exposed and reversed — the narrator built someone up ('I played soldier, you played king'), was deceived and used, and now refuses the role of loyal subject, choosing instead to fan the flames as the other falls. Betrayal converts devotion into deliberate destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~by-my-self-remix":{"haiku":"Existential paralysis and inability to trust anyone or make decisions.","opus":"Paralyzing self-doubt, distrust and fear of failure, feeling unable to rely on oneself or anyone while isolated and stretched thin.","sonnet":"The paralysis of self-doubt and social anxiety, unable to trust others or oneself while feeling permanently stuck on the outside.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Hybrid Theory deep cut that stages paranoia as a series of unanswerable either/or questions: trust and be conned, or wall yourself off and rot alone. Shinoda's verses run the debate as rapid interrogation, each option a trap that costs him something, until the choice collapses into the same defeat either way. The genius of the arrangement is that the enemy is never named or even outside him. The threat he keeps watching for is his own mind, so the daily performance of coping isn't protection from others but from the self he can't trust, and the title's promise of self-reliance curdles into its opposite: alone with the one person who keeps failing him. By the bridge he's not even seeking rescue, only admitting he can't explain his own exile from the inside.","fv":2,"fableAlt":"The rework treats that private paranoia as something to be dismantled and rebuilt in public, handing the song to outside architects who reassemble its panic from spare parts. Josh Abraham's cold electronic frame and Stephen Carpenter's guest guitar rewire the debate as machinery, so the self-interrogation now sounds administered from without rather than churning inside. Keeping Bennington's original vocal inside that alien scaffolding sharpens the exile: the same voice, still failing itself, now stranded in a body it never chose, bleeding straight into the next track.","themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"self-reliance curdling into isolation from the one person who keeps failing him","built":"the enemy is never named or externalized because the threat is his own mind"},
"linkin-park~by-myself":{"haiku":"Paralysis of trust issues and inability to find stable ground amid isolation.","opus":"A song about paralyzing self-doubt and paranoia, agonizing over whether to trust others or no one, feeling stretched too thin behind a daily façade, and ultimately unable to rely even on himself.","sonnet":"The speaker is paralyzed by a self-defeating loop: every strategy for navigating a hostile social world — trust, distrust, fight, retreat — leads back to the same outcome of pain and failure, and the only resource they have is a self they can no longer rely on.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Hybrid Theory deep cut that stages paranoia as a series of unanswerable either/or questions: trust and be conned, or wall yourself off and rot alone. Shinoda's verses run the debate as rapid interrogation, each option a trap that costs him something, until the choice collapses into the same defeat either way. The genius of the arrangement is that the enemy is never named or even outside him. The threat he keeps watching for is his own mind, so the daily performance of coping isn't protection from others but from the self he can't trust, and the title's promise of self-reliance curdles into its opposite: alone with the one person who keeps failing him. By the bridge he's not even seeking rescue, only admitting he can't explain his own exile from the inside.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"self-reliance curdling into isolation from the one person who keeps failing him","built":"the enemy is never named or externalized because the threat is his own mind"},
"linkin-park~by-myslf-josh-abraham-and-mike-shinoda-reanimation":{"haiku":"Questions whether to trust instinct or caution, unable to rely on the self when stretched between fear and the impossibility of genuine connection.","opus":"Being torn by self-doubt and distrust, as the narrator questions whether to trust others or no one, feels stretched too thin, and concludes he can't rely on himself.","sonnet":"The narrator is paralysed by contradictory impulses — trust no one versus risk betrayal, fight back versus be overtaken — unable to rely on himself, stretched too thin, and feeling permanently stuck on the outside.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Hybrid Theory deep cut that stages paranoia as a series of unanswerable either/or questions: trust and be conned, or wall yourself off and rot alone. Shinoda's verses run the debate as rapid interrogation, each option a trap that costs him something, until the choice collapses into the same defeat either way. The genius of the arrangement is that the enemy is never named or even outside him. The threat he keeps watching for is his own mind, so the daily performance of coping isn't protection from others but from the self he can't trust, and the title's promise of self-reliance curdles into its opposite: alone with the one person who keeps failing him. By the bridge he's not even seeking rescue, only admitting he can't explain his own exile from the inside.","fv":2,"fableAlt":"The rework treats that private paranoia as something to be dismantled and rebuilt in public, handing the song to outside architects who reassemble its panic from spare parts. Josh Abraham's cold electronic frame and Stephen Carpenter's guest guitar rewire the debate as machinery, so the self-interrogation now sounds administered from without rather than churning inside. Keeping Bennington's original vocal inside that alien scaffolding sharpens the exile: the same voice, still failing itself, now stranded in a body it never chose, bleeding straight into the next track.","themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"self-reliance curdling into isolation from the one person who keeps failing him","built":"the enemy is never named or externalized because the threat is his own mind"},
"linkin-park~carousel-hybrid-theory-ep":{"haiku":"Addiction cycles where victims run from problems and addicts cannot stop repeating destructive patterns they cannot acknowledge.","opus":"Two interlocked portraits of self-deception, a girl crying away her pride behind a disguise and a man riding the carousel of addiction he swears is under control, both unable to face themselves. A saving voice offers wings if they'd only come closer. From Linkin Park's pre-fame Hybrid Theory EP, this shows the rap-sung template that Mike Shinoda and Chester Bennington would soon sharpen into era-defining nu-metal.","sonnet":"Two portraits of self-deception running in parallel — a woman drowning in a secret she can't sleep through, a man who won't admit his addiction has control of him. Both are riding the same carousel, both told by someone watching from the outside that they can only spin the lie for so long before it stops holding.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~castle-of-glass":{"haiku":"Fragility and incompleteness define identity; the speaker is barely visible, a crack in a structure that shatters at the slightest pressure.","opus":"A plea to be washed clean and made whole, the narrator picturing himself as a single crack in a fragile castle of glass, barely visible yet longing to be flown past sirens into a healing dream. Linkin Park cut this for their post-rap-rock synthesis era, its folk-tinged electronica and Chester Bennington's aching restraint capturing the smallness of one broken soul within something vast and brittle.","sonnet":"A plea for cleansing and restoration aimed at something larger than any single person — the speaker asks to be carried to a river, washed of poison, flown past the place where sirens call, and warmed back into wholeness. What holds the whole thing together is the self-assessment: just a crack in something beautiful, barely visible, almost nothing — and yet the crack is real and the longing to be made whole is absolute.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~crawling":{"haiku":"Internal psychological torment manifests as self-doubt and loss of control, with the narrator struggling to locate themselves amid pressure and fear. Wounds of self-awareness refuse to heal, trapping them in confusion about reality.","opus":"An account of inner turmoil and lost self-control, where fear, insecurity, and unhealing wounds blur what feels real and leave the speaker trapped by mounting pressure.","sonnet":"The narrator describes being trapped inside overwhelming anxiety and loss of self-control — wounds that refuse to heal, walls closing in, and a fear that the pressure and inner turmoil will never end.","src":"haiku","fable":"Bennington built this around a very specific, private horror and then let the world hear it as generic teenage angst, which became its blessing and its curse. The song is about self-disgust experienced as an infestation of the body rather than a mood you can reason with: the threat is inside the skin, not out in the world, so there is no enemy to fight and no wound that closes. Underneath the nu-metal scaffolding it is really about losing the border between what is happening and what the addicted, abused mind is manufacturing, which is why the panic keeps circling back to the question of what is even real. The verses hiss and stammer while the chorus detonates, staging the two selves it describes at once, and the outro caving the two voices in on each other so the sufferer and the thing consuming him can no longer be told apart.","fv":2,"fnote":"The band's first Grammy — Best Hard Rock Performance, 2002. Bennington called it one of the hardest songs to perform: singing it meant standing back inside the state it describes.","themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"self-disgust experienced as infestation of the body, no enemy to fight","built":"the outro caves two voices in on each other until sufferer and consuming thing merge"},
"linkin-park~cure-for-the-itch":{"haiku":"Mock announcements introduce a guest performer and a lesson in rhythm management, framing musical performance as entertainment spectacle.","opus":"A spoken-word interlude introducing the band's DJ as a performer, essentially a framing device for a turntablism showcase rather than a lyrical song; there is no narrative theme beyond staging the instrumental.","sonnet":"This is essentially just a DJ showcase — an MC introduces Mr. Hahn and the piece demonstrates his turntablism. There is no lyrical narrative beyond the frame of a live performance.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~don-t-stay":{"fable":"This is Meteora's first full song — after the instrumental Foreword — on the album where Linkin Park refined the nu-metal formula that made them the defining band of early-2000s teenage alienation. The song presents itself as a breakup, but the real subject is the effort of extraction from a relationship that has quietly rewritten who you are. Its logic runs in stages: first the physical need for distance framed as basic survival, then the recognition that the damage is internal (the fury is aimed inward, at his own gullibility, as much as at her), and finally the hardening into flat refusal once every plea for space fails. The demand giving control back is telling, since it admits the self was surrendered voluntarily and now has to be reclaimed by force, and the closing repetitions strip the plea down to bare command, as if softness itself were the thing that let the erosion happen.","fableDeep":"The verses meter the realization out in increments of \"sometimes\" — needing to breathe, needing distance, disbelief at not having seen it — before the swerve to \"somehow, I need you to go,\" the adverb admitting he cannot fully derive the conclusion, only that it has arrived. The chorus performs a double erasure harsher than an ordinary breakup: forget the memories and the possibilities, past and future both put out with the trash, because keeping either would keep the thing alive. The core accusation is identity theft in slow motion — \"what you were changing me into\" — and the core demand is phrased as restitution rather than revenge: \"just give me myself back,\" selfhood as stolen property to be returned. The bridge inventories what is being cancelled — being ignored, being wasted away, one more day of it — and appends \"with no apologies,\" waiving even the closure ceremony; the title itself is the rare breakup imperative aimed not at staying but at leaving faster. Thesis: a breakup framed not as loss but as repossession.","haiku":"Ending a relationship and reclaiming individual identity after being damaged and changed by another person's influence and faithlessness.","opus":"A song about needing to end a toxic relationship, where the speaker asks the other person to leave, forget their shared memories, and give him himself back, insisting he no longer needs them wasting him away.","sonnet":"The speaker demands the end of a relationship that has eroded their sense of self, asking the other person to take their faithlessness and go, and insisting on reclaiming their own identity rather than being slowly worn away.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance"],"means":"a breakup framed as repossession of a surrendered self","built":"the adverb somehow admits the conclusion cannot be logically derived, only arrived at","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~don-t-stay-live-in-nottingham-2003":{"haiku":"Memory and breath become incompatible; remembrance requires the other to withdraw.","opus":"A demand to be left alone after a relationship that was quietly reshaping him; the pain isn't heartbreak but self-erasure, so he asks not for reconciliation but for the return of the person he was before.","sonnet":"A breakup that is also a reclamation — take your faithlessness, take your memories, take what you were turning me into, just give me myself back. The anger beneath the chorus surfaces in 'I don't need one more day of you wasting me away with no apologies,' a line that finally names the dynamic plainly.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~don-t-stay-live-in-texas":{"haiku":"Departure becomes necessity; the speaker needs distance to breathe, yet cannot fully release.","opus":"The same live-era refusal to be remade by someone else, choosing solitude over a love that was rewriting his identity, wanting his memories and his self handed back and nothing more.","sonnet":"A breakup that is also a reclamation — take your faithlessness, take your memories, take what you were turning me into, just give me myself back. The anger beneath the chorus surfaces in 'I don't need one more day of you wasting me away with no apologies,' a line that finally names the dynamic plainly.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~easier-to-run":{"fable":"A Meteora deep cut that stages avoidance as a confession, which is its central irony: the singer keeps announcing how much less it costs to flee and go numb, yet the very act of cataloguing that wish is the facing-up he claims he can't do. The verses hint at buried trauma he'll never name, filmed on an endless loop behind his eyes, while Shinoda's pre-chorus piles conditional after conditional to shoulder the fault, undo the damage, carry his shame into the ground. The song's engine is that grammar of the unreal: every vow of change is fenced off by an if, so the promises collapse into the same paralysis they lament, and the fantasy of freezing time before any step is taken becomes a wish to erase the self rather than heal it. What sounds like a runaway anthem is really a portrait of someone too wounded to run and too ashamed to stay.","fableDeep":"The chorus states the policy — running is easier, numbness a substitute for pain, leaving simpler than facing it alone — and the verses explain what is being run from without ever naming it: something \"taken from deep inside,\" a locked-away secret, wounds that never show and never leave, replaying like moving pictures for years. That unnamed theft, in a song this confessional, is the loudest thing in it. The long conditional battery is the formal masterstroke: clause after clause of if-I-could — change, take back the pain, retrace every wrong move, stand up and take the blame — contrition conducted in a grammatical mood that guarantees no action, rehearsed precisely because it is impossible. The darkest wish extends the logic to time itself: never moving forward so there would never be a past, erasure preferred to healing. The plan that remains is the oldest one — taking all the shame to the grave, secrecy signed as a lifelong lease. Thesis: a confession about the ease of not confessing, trapped in the conditional on purpose.","haiku":"Haunted by traumatic secrets and shame, the speaker chooses numbness and escape over confronting their painful past; it's easier to run than to stand and face the pain.","opus":"Linkin Park voice the pull toward avoidance, giving in to numbness rather than confronting old wounds and the shame attached to them. The narrator keeps returning to a wish that he could undo his mistakes and lift away the hurt he has carried for years, even knowing he can't.","sonnet":"The speaker carries a hidden wound from the past — a secret locked away that plays in their mind like a film — and admits that numbing the pain or running from it is simpler than facing it and accepting blame. Despite wishing they could undo every wrong move, they remain paralysed between wanting to change and pretending nothing is wrong.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fnote":"Bennington said this one cut closest — the unnamed buried thing in the verses was his own childhood, the abuse he spent years unable to speak about.","themes":["shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"contrition rehearsed in conditionals that guarantee no action","built":"the conditional battery — if I could, if I could — conducts remorse in a mood that forecloses it"},
"linkin-park~enth-e-nd-kutmasta-kurt-reanimation-feat-motion-man":{"haiku":"Reflection on wasted effort and the realization that the journey matters more than goals, though the struggle itself proves ultimately futile.","opus":"A narrator fixated on passing minutes watches them evaporate, deciding the road traveled means more than arriving even as all his striving proves pointless. This Reanimation remix rebuilds In The End, stacking new guest verses about social scheming and betrayal over the band's familiar hook about effort that ultimately counts for nothing.","sonnet":"Obsession with time — watching it drain away, trying to grab it back, wasting it on regret — collides with the eventual realisation that the journey itself was the point all along. The remix layering deepens the meditation, adding voices that circle the same defeated conclusion from different angles of experience.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~faint":{"fable":"Faint sits at the hinge of Meteora's design: Shinoda's verses catalogue the speaker as a sum of deficits and neediness, a self assembled entirely from how he wants to be perceived, while Bennington's chorus detonates that composure into a demand not to be ignored. The genius of the track is that the plea and the threat are the same words screamed louder, so the escalation from letting go to the bridge's ultimatum reads as the moment supplication curdles into rage without ever changing its object. What looks like a breakup song is really about the terror of being unseen, and how a person built entirely from the wish to be wanted has no self left to fall back on when the wanting fails.","fableDeep":"The speaker has made himself into whatever would earn attention — 'I am what I want you to want' — and still registers as faint, barely there. When self-erasure fails to convince, the plea flips into force: being heard by demand ('you're gonna listen to me, like it or not') becomes the last identity he has left.","haiku":"The speaker experiences emotional distance and one-sided effort in a relationship, feeling unheard and ignored while desperately trying to be acknowledged.","opus":"A song about insecurity and desperation in a one-sided relationship, where the speaker pleads not to be ignored or turned away by someone he clings to, insisting his feelings are real and demanding to be heard.","sonnet":"The speaker feels chronically invisible and dismissed by someone who refuses to acknowledge them, and the song escalates from quiet hurt at being ignored to a furious demand to finally be heard.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"the plea for visibility curdles into a threat without changing its words","built":"plea and threat are identical lines delivered louder, so escalation is purely dynamic","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~fallout":{"haiku":"Acceptance of self-sabotage and the loss of things undeserved; all blame and all consequence rest solely with the self.","opus":"A brief, aching admission of guilt, the narrator swimming through the smoke of bridges he burned himself, refusing apology because the loss is deserved. Its brevity makes it feel like an interlude, a held breath. Linkin Park, the California band fronted by Chester Bennington and Mike Shinoda, used this as connective tissue on Living Things, distilling their signature theme of self-inflicted ruin into a few spare, repeated lines.","sonnet":"A short, stark acceptance of deserved loss — standing in the smoke of bridges deliberately burned, refusing the apology that doesn't undo anything. The speaker owns the blame entirely and without self-pity, acknowledging that what's gone was never truly earned, and that the only honest position now is to stop pretending otherwise.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~fighting-myself":{"haiku":"Internal conflict spirals as thoughts become louder and regrets multiply, the speaker battles their own mind while watching everything collapse.","opus":"The losing war against one's own head, thoughts that build into pain and doubt and spill out to ruin everything just when it's held together. It refuses false promises of change and names the real enemy, the anger constantly inside that leaves no room to breathe.","sonnet":"A song about the way suppressed internal conflict metastasizes — the speaker's thoughts spiral louder the more they try to stay quiet, every effort to hold things together producing new damage, the problem always being the speaker themselves rather than any external source.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of self-sabotage as a rigged contest: the enemy is internal, so victory is impossible by definition, and the chorus states the outcome as a law rather than a fear. The verses trace the mechanism — silence meant to protect turns into pressure, the effort to hold everything in becomes the thing that ruins it, and each attempted repair only adds to a ledger of regret. The second verse turns outward to a partner, refusing their offer to fix him not out of pride but grim certainty that he'll break it anyway; the bridge finally names the freight as accumulated anger he can't outrun. Released posthumously with Chester Bennington's vocal recorded years before his death, the flat declaration that this fight is always lost lands as unbearable prophecy — a lyric about the private war that, offstage, he did not survive.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"the enemy is internal, so victory is impossible by definition and stated as a law","built":"released posthumously with a vocal recorded years earlier, the flat declaration lands as unbearable prophecy"},
"linkin-park~figure-09":{"fable":"Shinoda's verses trace how airing a grievance backfires: naming an old hurt aloud, meant to release it, instead cements it — the resentment gets metabolized into identity until the speaker can no longer tell where the person he hated ends and he begins. That is the horror Chester's chorus voices as possession, the ghost of the wronger having colonized the wronged. The bridge is the desperate reversal — an exorcism shouted in the imperative, demanding distance and claiming release — but its violence gives away the truth the song keeps circling: you cannot evict what you've already made structural, and the exit line only confesses the merger again. Underneath the nu-metal rage is an unusually precise study of how holding a grudge is a slow act of self-erasure.","fableDeep":"The mechanism is stated with rare precision: putting the pain on display did not set it free — \"I took what I hated and made it a part of me,\" expression working as absorption, confession as a graft rather than a purge. The second verse traces the process step by step: the person appearing in every thought, thoughts finding words, and the realization that in releasing the words \"I was committin' myself to 'em\" — commitment carrying both senses, promise and asylum. The chorus runs a parenthetical counter-voice — always right here, always my fear — the intruder speaking from inside the host's own hook. The bridge attempts eviction in shouted call-and-response, demanding space back and insisting the letting-go already happened; the chorus returns anyway and disproves it in real time. The grammar of the final admission does the moral accounting: \"I've let myself become you\" — let, not made — agency owned at the exact moment it is lost. Thesis: a song about how naming your damage can be the very act that makes it permanent.","haiku":"Someone realizes they have internalized and embodied the pain inflicted by another, becoming like their tormentor and unable to separate themselves from that hurt.","opus":"Linkin Park examine how fixating on someone who hurt you can curdle into obsession, until their influence becomes fused with your own identity. The narrator recognizes he has internalized the very thing he resents and struggles to peel it back off and reclaim himself.","sonnet":"The narrator cannot stop thinking about someone who has left a lasting psychological mark on him; by expressing his pain he realises he has made it a permanent part of his identity, and now he desperately wants to expel that person from his mind.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","madness & the mind"],"means":"the ghost of the wronger colonizes the wronged until they merge","built":"the chorus returns after the bridge's exorcism attempt, disproving the release in real time"},
"linkin-park~final-masquerade":{"haiku":"A relationship dies from unspoken words and unseen forgiveness; both partners pretend it will last forever but watch it fade away—standing at the end of their final disguise.","opus":"A portrait of a relationship dissolving under everything left unspoken, both partners lingering in hope of something that never arrives while old wounds fade and a love once sworn eternal quietly drifts off. Linkin Park cut this on their last album with Chester Bennington, whose wounded, soaring vocal lends the imagery of two people finally lowering their disguises too late a heavy poignancy.","sonnet":"A relationship ending not in confrontation but in mutual exhaustion — neither person has reasons, neither has time, both keep waiting for something that has already gone. The light that was brighter yesterday is now fading, old scars reopening, the promises of forever dissolving into the quiet formality of standing at the end of the performance, costumes still on, the show finally over.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~forgotten":{"haiku":"A person struggles with fractured memory and darkness, unable to escape a consuming void where pictures fade and past pain won't release them.","opus":"Linkin Park treat a damaged memory like a photograph that can never be smoothed back to its original state once it's been creased. The narrator is stalked by a half-buried past, torn between an image he can't shake and a defensive shrug about whether it's even worth his concern.","sonnet":"A song about a memory that cannot be shaken loose — the narrator is trapped between recollection and oblivion, watching a crumpled picture that can never be made whole again, held in darkness until some faint light eventually returns.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the way a mind returns to damage it can't stop reaching for. Shinoda's verses build the whole thing out of decay imagery: rust, rot, acid rain falling as questions, a counterfeit landscape glimpsed only behind clenched-shut lids, where a brief flood of light spilling across the floor merely confirms how dark it stays. The governing figure is a child's drawing on paper that drifts along fences and lamplight until the wind quits, then gets crumpled, then made into a law about the past itself, which once wrinkled can never be smoothed flat again. Against that, the hook is almost defiant, staking a claim to be located inside the recollection rather than the present, choosing to burn there until dawn. The paired vocal, meanwhile, keeps undercutting itself with a shrugged question about why any of it should matter, so the mask of not caring sits right on top of a memory that plainly won't let go.","fv":2,"themes":["nostalgia & memory","shame & repression"],"means":"choosing to burn inside a memory that refuses to release its hold","built":"paper-crumpled-into-law conceit rules that the past cannot be smoothed flat"},
"linkin-park~frgt-10-alchemist-reanimation-feat-chali-2na":{"haiku":"Fragmented memory and urban trauma creating dissociation and depression, with sound as the only therapy for broken minds.","opus":"A dense reworking of a memory the narrator can't escape, stuck in a dark place where acidic questions fall like rain, then handed to a guest verse that widens into urban decay, greedy governments and children trapped in tenement cages. Linkin Park's Reanimation remixed their debut, and this Alchemist-produced cut featuring Jurassic 5's Chali 2na pushes the material toward stark hip-hop social commentary.","sonnet":"Memory as both refuge and trap — a speaker unmoored from safety, circling images that won't release them, watching the polluted city landscape from the outside while a shadowy figure inside a screen-lit building could be anyone, any name, any casualty. The second voice expands outward into structural critique: urban collapse, wage cages, the game that nobody agreed to play but everyone loses.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~from-the-inside":{"fable":"Read as a breakup song it barely coheres, and that incoherence is the point: the you being renounced is not a lover but the singer's own compromised mind, the part of him that keeps making promises he cannot keep. The verses stage total isolation and thoughts pushing up like something buried refusing to stay down, so that the chorus vow to purge himself and never again trust himself in this company lands as an addict's or depressive's line drawn against relapse rather than against another person. Bennington sings it as self-quarantine, the horror being that the enemy and the one making the oath are the same voice, which is why the final stammered pronoun keeps repeating like a target that won't hold still. On Meteora that turns the album's signature outward-facing aggression back on the self, the anthem built to be screamed at someone that is really a person trying to lock a door between himself and himself.","fableDeep":"The vow is precisely worded: not 'I won't trust you' but 'I won't trust myself with you' — the deceit has cost him faith in his own judgment, which keeps re-investing in someone who drains him. So he guts his own interior ('take everything from the inside and throw it all away') rather than risk handing his feelings over again.","haiku":"Exhaustion from broken trust and inability to rely on someone leads to withdrawal and refusal to invest in the relationship any longer.","opus":"A song about broken trust and exhaustion from deceit, where the speaker feels isolated from everyone and resolves to throw away his feelings and stop trusting himself with a person who drains him.","sonnet":"Worn down by deceit and broken trust in a relationship, the speaker resolves to cut off emotionally — stripping out everything they gave — and swears they will no longer risk themselves on someone who has proven untrustworthy.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"enemy and oath-maker share the same voice and cannot separate","built":"the stammering repeated pronoun enacts a target that won't hold still","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~given-up":{"haiku":"Expresses complete exhaustion and hopelessness, claiming surrender after repeated disappointment and internal torment. The song portrays the narrator as his own worst enemy suffocating under pressure.","opus":"A raw outpouring of despair and self-hatred from someone who feels trapped, unable to breathe, and their own chief tormentor. Exhausted and out of hope, the narrator screams for relief and to have their suffering ended.","sonnet":"A person has reached total psychological collapse — trapped in self-loathing, unable to breathe, unable to locate the source of their pain — and reaches out desperately for something or someone to relieve the suffocation, asking to be put out of their misery.","src":"haiku","fable":"This 2007 track is Minutes to Midnight's hardest scream, and its subject is not a breakup or an enemy but panic disorder rendered from the inside—the waking sweat, the hyperventilation, the searching for help that finds no answer. Chester Bennington locates the threat inside the sufferer himself, so there is no villain to blame and no exit, which is why the plea keeps escalating: it moves from wanting relief to demanding oblivion, the bridge's shriek to God turning a cry for rescue into a request to simply be extinguished. Read against Bennington's own life, the refusal to soften that ending is the song's terrible honesty—it will not resolve the feeling because for him it did not resolve.","fv":2,"fnote":"The bridge's shriek holds a documented seventeen seconds — Bennington's longest recorded scream, the demand for oblivion held past the length a breath should allow.","themes":["madness & the mind","suicide"],"means":"panic without exit escalating from relief-seeking to oblivion","built":"bridge shriek to god converts rescue cry into extinction wish"},
"linkin-park~guilty-all-the-same-feat-rakim":{"haiku":"Those in power and media manipulate society while escaping accountability, all guilty of the same corruption they exploit and profit from.","opus":"An angry rebuke of authorities and moralizers who lecture others while being corrupt themselves, extended in the verse to greedy corporations, media and record companies that manipulate and exploit; everyone pointing fingers is judged guilty all the same.","sonnet":"An accusation directed at authority figures who preach and point fingers while bearing equal guilt themselves — the rap verse extends the critique to corporate and record-industry greed that manipulates artists and consumers alike.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~guilty-all-the-same-live":{"haiku":"Everyone implicated in systemic corruption, pointing fingers while ignoring their own complicity.","opus":"An indictment of self-righteous authority and profiteering greed, refusing the finger-pointing of those who preach while their own hands are dirty. Rakim's verse extends it to a corrupt industry, everyone complicit in the same rot.","sonnet":"About hypocrisy and greed in authority — the song accuses those who lecture and command others of being the real problem, pointing fingers while being complicit; the rap section sharpens the focus onto record label and music industry exploitation specifically.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~h-vltg3-evidence-reanimation-feat-pharoahe-monch-and-dj-babu":{"haiku":"Raw lyrical domination and mythic MC mastery, boasting of supernatural verbal power and dimensional warfare.","opus":"A pure lyrical flex, three MCs trading cosmic, boastful battle rhymes about mastering numerology, wielding microphones and demolishing lesser rappers, all built around a high voltage hook. There is no confession here, only wordplay as weaponry. This comes from Linkin Park's Reanimation remix project, the California band handing their platform to Pharoahe Monch, Evidence and DJ Babu for an underground hip-hop showcase.","sonnet":"Three MCs trading boasts about mic mastery that goes back before the beginning of time — the battle rap stance is cosmic and hyperbolic, each verse claiming an almost elemental authority over sound. The high voltage refrain frames the whole thing as an unstoppable electrical charge, rhyme colliding with rhythm to produce something physically overwhelming.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~hands-held-high":{"haiku":"Political rage against war, poverty, and elite detachment, demanding active resistance rather than passive consumption of propaganda.","opus":"A furious antiwar rap indicting a fumbling leader who sends the poor to die for the rich, closing on a stark truth that war's cost always falls hardest on those with the least. Gospel refrains reframe protest as prayer. Fronted by Mike Shinoda and Chester Bennington, this stands among Linkin Park's most openly political tracks, forged in the Iraq-war era.","sonnet":"A dual-voiced political indictment of the early-2000s American war machine — one voice furious at the domestic theater of a leader who can't speak straight, the other a child in a conflict zone counting bodies and bombs outside a mosque. Both end looking at the same spectacle, one angry, one terrified, the gap between them the whole point.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mike Shinoda's Iraq-era protest rap, and Linkin Park's most explicit political statement, aimed squarely at a Bush-figure leader who fumbles through press conferences while the poor are sent to die for the rich. The first verse is fury from the safe distance of a living room, where the disaster is just something on the news; the second collapses that distance entirely, ventriloquizing a ten-year-old on the ground where mosques and markets explode, so the mocking sitcom question of the first verse returns transformed into genuine terror. The refrain of amens is the song's real detonation: prayer offered without belief that anyone is listening, the raised hands equally those of the surrendering, the drowning, and the faithful, so devotion and helplessness become the same gesture.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","war & battle","faith & the occult"],"means":"devotion and helplessness collapsed into the same raised gesture","built":"the chorus amen reframes protest as unanswered prayer"},
"linkin-park~healing-foot":{"haiku":"Regret and miscommunication trap both parties in cycles of blame and longing where neither can fully escape or forgive.","opus":"Circling the wreckage of a relationship where held-back words and self-made messes keep repeating, unable to see it rationally. The resolve at the end to take back the heart and break down the fear is a hard-won turn toward release.","sonnet":"Someone who has long suppressed their feelings out of fear of rejection finally reaches the point of clarity — they name what went wrong in a relationship, acknowledge their own role in it, and declare themselves no longer divided or afraid to speak.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~heavy-feat-kiiara-disero-remix":{"haiku":"Anxiety and burden accumulate beyond capacity; the speaker drowns in self-inflicted weight she cannot release.","opus":"Anxiety and depression as unbearable weight, the mind manufacturing problems and finding grim comfort in its own panic. Chester Bennington's plainest confession of a spiraling head, made heavier in hindsight by what came after.","sonnet":"A person trapped by their own mind — anxious, paranoid, unable to release the psychological weight they carry — recognizes that letting go would free them but cannot manage the act, circling the insight without reaching it. The burden is self-generated and the awareness of that fact makes it no lighter.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~heavy-is-the-crown":{"haiku":"Ambition exacts its price: what you summoned through desire now weighs down everything, and there's no escape from the consequences you yourself initiated.","opus":"Power won turns out to be its own punishment, the crown bleeding out the one who demanded it. Sung by the band's new voice, it doubles as their own reckoning with an inherited weight.","sonnet":"A song about the crushing weight of having chosen a hard path — the speaker is exhausted, bleeding through the effort of holding themselves together, and confronting that the burden of ambition and leadership is exactly what they asked for.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~high-voltage":{"haiku":"A rapper chronicles mastering his craft and inventing instrumental prowess through technical wizardry, claiming dominion over other MCs and refusing to be shaken by challengers.","opus":"An early Linkin Park battle-rap flex, pure hip-hop showmanship built on surreal boasts of cosmic longevity, telekinetic skill and lyrical warfare; the throughline is establishing identity and dominance over rival MCs through electric, unstoppable energy rather than any narrative.","sonnet":"A rap showcase from the Linkin Park/Fort Minor era asserting lyrical supremacy — both MCs claim to have been crafting rhymes since before civilization, wielding metaphorical weapons against lesser rappers, the track functioning as a pure flex of technical ability.","src":"haiku","fable":"Pure battle-rap braggadocio, and a deliberate corrective: this is Shinoda planting a flag as an MC before the world could file Linkin Park under nu-metal angst. The verses are a cascade of underground-rap boasts stacked to absurdity, cosmic origin myths and pseudo-science and comic-book violence, each image trying to out-flex the last until the point is less any single claim than the sheer voltage of the delivery. The hook says as much outright, framing skill as a current nobody can switch off, and the outro naming the crew turns the whole thing into a mission statement about mixed media and lineage rather than any subject beyond the flex itself.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"skill asserted as a current no rival can switch off","built":"the outro crew-roll turns the flex into a lineage mission statement"},
"linkin-park~hit-the-floor":{"fable":"A revenge fantasy that disguises itself as a law of physics. Shinoda's verses are the slow-burn ledger of someone who has spent too long swallowing his own words to keep a manipulator comfortable, walking carefully so as not to provoke a person who mistakes cruelty for winning; the grievance is specifically about lies that get someone elevated above the people they've stepped on. The chorus then hands the payback to gravity rather than to himself, turning what goes up must come down into a countdown he only has to wait out, so the fall he craves feels less like an act of aggression than an inevitability he's owed. The relief in the emptied-out coda, where everything simply vanishes, is really the sound of the speaker refusing to admit he wanted it.","fableDeep":"Biding time under a belittler he still covers for 'out of courtesy' — the speaker never plots revenge, he forecasts it, trusting gravity ('what goes up has got to fall') to do the work. The 'so many people like me' widens it beyond one grievance: a whole class of the stepped-on, counting down together.","haiku":"Confronting betrayal and waiting for redemption through revenge on a manipulator.","opus":"Confronting a manipulative liar who kept you down, and anticipating their inevitable fall once their deceit collapses.","sonnet":"Confronting a manipulative person who lies and belittles, and waiting for the moment their power collapses.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"revenge outsourced to gravity so the speaker stays clean","built":"the physics metaphor lets the narrator forecast the fall rather than plot it","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~husky-hit-the-floor-demo":{"haiku":"False friends exploit the speaker's generosity while they deteriorate, taking advantage by returning nothing as the speaker learns too late.","opus":"An early demo cast as a warning to someone surrounded by false friends who take everything and give nothing back. The narrator watches a person perform for people who exploit them, likening them to a marionette pulled around by those they try to impress. It's a song of frustrated protectiveness, urging self-worth against manipulation.","sonnet":"A warning to someone who keeps allowing themselves to be used — giving everything to people who take advantage and return nothing. The speaker addresses this with a mixture of frustration and empathy, noting that the pattern comes from a father who made them this way, and that learning self-protection is the only way out.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~i-ll-be-gone":{"haiku":"Someone prepares to leave permanently despite emotional connection, resolving to disappear as night falls, abandoning their old life and relationships behind.","opus":"Linkin Park, in their more electronic later phase, cast leaving as a vanishing at first light. As darkness bleeds away and stars fade, the narrator tries not to dwell on what he's abandoning and asks for one last word. Come daybreak he'll simply be gone, moving on into what's next, the song rendering departure as bittersweet and oddly peaceful rather than embittered.","sonnet":"A departure — ambiguously voluntary or inevitable — unfolds against darkening skies and thinning air. The person leaving frames the separation as a threshold between one era and the next: when the sun sets on this life another will rise, and he will have crossed over into whatever comes after. Those left behind are asked to understand rather than stop him.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~igyeih":{"haiku":"Self-sacrifice yields nothing but is repeated anyway, the speaker giving all while receiving nothing.","opus":"A late reunion-era track about pouring yourself into someone who only returns cruelty, calling out a partner with a god complex; the exhaustion of one-sided giving finally hardens into the vow that from now on he doesn't need them.","sonnet":"Total sacrifice met with ugliness — I give you everything, you give me your worst, you're not as honest as you act, just a devil with a god complex. The memories written all over skin and the clock ticking without rules frame the break: forgotten doesn't mean forgiven, and from now on I don't need you.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~in-between":{"haiku":"Trapped between pride and honesty, exploring how lies and emotional walls prevent authentic connection and create mutual damage.","opus":"A preemptive apology, the narrator stuck between his self-regard and his commitments, his falsehoods and the truths he can't quite voice, unable to say what matters before the moment slips past. Guilt is the one thing he articulates well. Sung mostly by Mike Shinoda on Minutes to Midnight, it marks the band's pivot from rap-metal toward quieter, more textured songs about failing to connect.","sonnet":"A sustained apology for being caught between who you wanted to be and what you actually did — pride and dishonesty kept the real words from coming out, and the person on the other end never heard what they deserved to hear. The speaker admits that guilt is the only language that registers now, and hopes action might say what speech never could.","src":"haiku","fable":"Shinoda's spare confession from the album where Linkin Park swapped scream for restraint, and the title names the exact place an apology stalls: the gap between wanting to mean something and the machinery of pride, half-truths, and self-protection that jams the words before they arrive. The verses keep resetting to the same throat-clearing preamble, the apology that never reaches its object, so the song enacts its own paralysis — each attempt at genuineness, at being someone better, at earning back trust, snagging on the same in-between. The bridge quietly diagnoses why: he can't argue his way out because guilt is the only fluent language here, and so he stakes everything on actions doing what speech can't. The math that closes it is bleaker than it sounds — if the one thing he owes is worse withheld than said, then his silence isn't tact, it's the failure.","fv":2,"fnote":"A rarity twice over — Shinoda sings rather than raps, his first album track carried entirely by his own singing voice.","themes":["shame & repression"],"means":"an apology stalling between guilt and speech","built":"each verse resets to the same preamble paralyzed"},
"linkin-park~in-my-remains":{"haiku":"Silent anguish and internal collapse; the only thing left after trauma are unfulfilled promises and the need for cleansing.","opus":"Linkin Park, from the Living Things era, turn personal collapse into an anthem of endurance. It follows someone picking meaning out of ruin, hoping to shed a damaged past and be reborn, the pull toward renewal winning out over the slow erosion.","sonnet":"Sifting through the wreckage of something that fell apart — the speaker broken down, waiting for the chance to feel anything again, holding promises that were never kept. The dominant image is of a slow collapse, soldiers falling one by one, silence as the only thing left that might wash away the worst of what remains.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~in-pieces":{"haiku":"Romantic contradiction between words and actions creating fractured dynamics where trust is impossible and endings are inevitable.","opus":"A confrontation with a lover whose words and eyes contradict each other, love spoken while hate is meant, until the narrator refuses to be the one left holding the shattered pieces and consigns them to loneliness with their secrets. The wrapped-in-arms-then-chilled-to-the-bone imagery captures the whiplash. Linkin Park, fronted by Chester Bennington, deliver one of Minutes to Midnight's rawest breakup songs, wounded but finally unyielding.","sonnet":"A relationship built on contradiction — told to go while hands beg to stay, wrapped in warmth then left cold, promised the sky then thrown away. The speaker refuses to be the one who breaks it, turns the damage back: whatever is left standing when this ends, the secrets and the regret belong to the one who kept lying.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cataloguing of a lover whose every gesture cancels itself—the hand that clings while the mouth dismisses, the embrace that arrives with frostbite. The whole lyric is built on antithesis, each line pairing a warmth with the cold that undoes it, so that affection and contempt become indistinguishable and the speaker is left holding only the residue nobody bothered to take. The refusal at the chorus is the move that lifts this above ordinary breakup complaint: he declines the role of the one who shatters things, handing that guilt back and consigning the partner to a solitude furnished entirely with what they'd rather not remember. It's a quieter, more accusatory register than early Linkin Park's blast of self-directed rage—the mid-2000s pivot toward blaming outward, refusing to break so the other person has to carry the wreckage alone.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance"],"means":"refusing to shatter so the other carries the wreckage alone","built":"antithesis structures every line, warmth undone by its own cold twin"},
"linkin-park~in-the-end":{"fable":"Beneath its reputation as a universal anthem of futility sits a specific psychology: a rap-verse indictment wearing a chorus of surrender. Shinoda's verses build a lawyer's brief against someone who treated him as owned property, mocked and fought him, and never actually knew him. That structure, a case assembled and then dismissed by its own closing refrain, makes the catharsis of declaring nothing counted into a defense mechanism, effort reframed as loss so the humiliation becomes survivable. The bridge cracks the mask: trust extended until exhausted, revealing the surrender was never indifference but the wound of having cared and been discarded, dressed up as not caring.","fableDeep":"The verses obsess over time more than the person — the pendulum, the clock ticking life away, watching it fly out the window — so what's actually mourned is the wasted years. The rhyme announces itself as designed, a deliberately built monument 'to remind myself how I tried so hard,' making the song the one thing salvaged from the waste. And the withholding flips pronouns between verses — first 'I kept everything inside,' then 'you kept everything inside' — quietly admitting the failure was mutual.","haiku":"Wasted effort in a relationship despite trying hard; ultimate futility and betrayal.","opus":"The futility of effort and trust in a failed relationship, realizing that all one's striving ultimately amounts to nothing.","sonnet":"The futility of straining toward a relationship or goal that ultimately falls apart despite every effort made.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"effort reframed as loss so the humiliation becomes survivable","built":"pronouns shift between verses — first I kept everything inside, then you — quietly splitting the blame","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~in-the-end-demo-lpu-rarities":{"haiku":"Pain multiplies until it consumes the speaker, burning away attempt at escape or salvation.","opus":"An early, denser draft of the group's signature lament on effort that amounts to nothing; here the imagery leans harder on being dealt in and bent by others, the sense of being someone's property already curdling toward the famous resignation.","sonnet":"A longer, rawer version of the same reckoning: effort spent, trust placed, a voice heard saying you get what you deserve — and the conclusion that none of it mattered anyway. The twilight-through-the-skylight detail and the screaming-in-the-dreaming section give the collapse a more elaborate architecture before landing on the same epitaph.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~in-the-end-live":{"haiku":"Effort and time collapse into meaninglessness; the speaker recognizes all striving amounts to waste, everything falling apart despite maximum exertion.","opus":"The definitive verdict that trying hard and getting far still leaves you with nothing, performed as communion with a crowd; the private wound of wasted trust and a relationship that fell apart is turned outward into shared catharsis.","sonnet":"A live version of the well-known song about effort and loss — someone who tried everything in a relationship and still watched it collapse; despite the investment of time and trust, the ultimate conclusion is that none of it mattered and it all ended alone.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~iridescent":{"haiku":"Letting go of accumulated grief and failed hope after devastation, finding peace only through release and surrender.","opus":"A consoling anthem for the aftermath of catastrophe, addressed to someone left utterly on their own once disaster has struck, urging them to carry the grief and frustration and then release it. Light and grace tilt despair toward relief. Linkin Park, the California band, wrote this for a darker phase of their work, its swelling refrain offering surrender as the only way through.","sonnet":"Standing at the edge after devastation — alone, falling, no one there to catch you — and being asked to release the accumulated weight of sadness and failure rather than carry it forward. The imagery of a burst of light blinding angels suggests the moment of letting go is as dramatic as the collapse that preceded it.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~keys-to-the-kingdom":{"haiku":"A speaker admits to self-sabotage, throwing away control and kingdom keys into an inner abyss, unable to stop destroying everything he encounters.","opus":"A portrait of self-sabotage and futility, where the speaker throws away his own chances and fights pointless battles, mixed with defiant boasting and a warning to be careful what you aim at.","sonnet":"The narrator describes surrendering control of his own life — throwing away the keys to his kingdom through self-destructive choices — while also asserting defiant lyrical dominance against anyone who challenges him.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~krwlng-mike-shinoda-reanimation-feat-aaron-lewis":{"haiku":"Internal pressure and self-doubt crawl beneath the surface; wounds won't heal and fear controls the narrator's inability to find himself.","opus":"Linkin Park return to their anthem of raw anxiety, injuries that refuse to mend, and composure eroding from within. This Reanimation version, produced by Mike Shinoda with Staind's Aaron Lewis adding grit, recasts the panic as a haunted confrontation with oneself. The narrator, forced to stand alongside his own reflection, wrestles with relentless dread and the struggle to recognize himself.","sonnet":"Anxiety eats through the self from beneath — wounds that refuse to close, walls closing in, a reflection that cannot be recognized. Self-control collapses and pressure exceeds any limit. The feeling of being inhabited by something that pulls against the will, that confuses what is real and what is fear, has nowhere to resolve. Survival itself feels uncertain.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~kyur4-th-ich-chairman-hahn-reanimation":{"haiku":"A fictional audio-weapons system uses sound waves to paralyze opponents; the speaker masters sound as a tool of domination.","opus":"A remix interlude framing the band's sound as an audio weapon that paralyzes before it strikes. It is less a song than a stylized introduction, spectacle building anticipation for the DJ.","sonnet":"Like the original 'Cure for the Itch,' this is a DJ showcase framed as a demonstration of sonic weaponry — Mr. Hahn is introduced as a master who paralyses opponents with sound. No lyrical narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~leave-out-all-the-rest":{"haiku":"Before death, someone wants to be remembered for reasons beyond their wrongs. They ask others to keep them in memory while forgetting the hurt they caused.","opus":"Linkin Park write a farewell in advance, with Chester Bennington voicing someone imagining his own death and asking to be remembered for his better moments rather than his failures. He admits his toughness is only a shell, and the request took on tragic weight given the band's later history.","sonnet":"A person asks to be remembered by what was good rather than what was broken — carrying the knowledge of his own imperfection and asking those left behind not to resent him for it. The dream of disappearing prompts the question of what remains worth missing. Forgiveness is the legacy he hopes to leave, flaws and all, in someone else's memory.","src":"haiku","fable":"Chester Bennington writes his own eulogy while still alive, instructing survivors on how to curate his memory: keep the worth, discard the failures, don't hold the damage against him. What sounds like a farewell to a loved one is really an address to the fans and to himself, and the request to be edited down to the good parts is the plea of someone who has always felt the surface strength was a fraud over something breaking underneath. The bridge turns the confession inward and darker, admitting the fantasy that another person could rescue him from himself and the flat impossibility of ever becoming the stronger, wholer figure someone else has projected onto him. Given how Bennington died, the song reads in retrospect less as poetic morbidity than as a man narrating his own exit years early, asking to be remembered kindly by the people he was afraid of disappointing.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"the farewell addressed to the fans is really written to himself","built":"the bridge admits the rescue fantasy and its flat impossibility before the eulogy closes"},
"linkin-park~let-you-fade":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and transformation pull apart connections; the speaker resists letting memory fade even as everything shifts.","opus":"Exhaustion and grief over an ending, breaking your back for a heap of sand only to watch it run through your fingers. The vow that survives is to keep remembering someone even as the memories slip, refusing to let them fade even knowing they may want to forget.","sonnet":"The gap between milestone and feeling — three in the morning lying in the street, working hard for a pile of sand that falls through the hands, everything exploding but nothing landing right. Against that entropy, a vow to keep remembering someone even when they want to be forgotten: time will test you, I won't let you fade.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~lies-greed-misery":{"haiku":"The speaker promises to break someone who lies and feeds on greed; they deserve to suffer alone in their misery after their deception.","opus":"Chester and Mike answering a manipulator's provocation with cold contempt, refusing intimidation. It's about reaching the breaking point and turning it back on the aggressor, the misery entirely self-inflicted by the one who pushed too far.","sonnet":"A revenge fantasy aimed at someone who disrespected and tried to intimidate the speaker — now that person has cornered themselves, and the singer wants to watch them choke on the consequences of their own arrogance.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~lies-greed-misery-dirtyphonics-remix":{"haiku":"Collective failure to break free perpetuates cycles of exploitation and broken promises.","opus":"A confrontation with a manipulator who lets pride and ego do the talking; the narrator refuses to be intimidated and wishes the liar's own greed and lies would choke him, insisting the ruin is entirely self-inflicted.","sonnet":"A retaliation from someone who has absorbed too much disrespect — not the failure here, refusing to be intimidated, watching the other person's composure break while they keep theirs. The refrain is unambiguous wish: choke on your lies, swallow your greed, suffer alone in your misery — you did this to yourself.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~lies-greed-misery-live":{"haiku":"Confrontation exposes hypocrisy and greed, demanding the guilty suffer the consequences of their deception and exploitation.","opus":"A defiant clapback at someone whose pride and ego pushed a fight they'd regret, promising to be the nail in their coffin. The chorus wishes them choking on their own lies, greed, and misery, insisting they did it to themselves.","sonnet":"About confronting someone who threatened and intimidated the narrator while hiding behind ego — the narrator waited, reloaded quietly, and now turns the breaking point back on the aggressor: they did this to themselves, and the narrator wants to watch them choke on their own lies and greed.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~lost":{"haiku":"Trauma and memory trap consciousness in suffering, with dignity lost to confusion and pain as the only constant.","opus":"A once-shelved track about a wound that can't be mended, the narrator stuck in the past and hiding behind a self-made facade. Bennington sings the erosion of dignity by remembered damage and betrayed vows, the hurt bottled up guaranteeing he'll never come right.","sonnet":"The speaker is imprisoned by past trauma and broken promises, living inside memories they cannot escape — the pain is so internalized that they've lost their identity and dignity, unable to break free even when alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Meteora-era vault track that surfaced two decades late, in 2023, which turns a song about being sealed inside old wounds into something almost unbearable to hear in Chester Bennington's absence. The lyric maps a closed loop: a wound that can't heal, so the speaker fakes its absence, then admits the pretending is the trap, then names the deeper injury underneath — trust extended to people who broke their word. The final movement drops the disguise entirely and repeats a flat surrender, the concession that holding the hurt in was never going to make it survivable — a chorus that resolves nothing on purpose, because the point is that there is no way out of the room.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"holding the hurt in was never going to make it survivable","built":"the chorus resolves nothing on purpose because there is no way out of the room"},
"linkin-park~lost-in-the-echo":{"haiku":"Identity fractures as the speaker hears themselves reflected and distorted in the noise of others.","opus":"Survival after betrayal by someone who was once his foundation; every promise dissolves into an echo, and the defiance is in holding his own, refusing to fold, and finally letting the person go for good.","sonnet":"A self-possession recovered after someone used foundation and conditioning to hold the narrator down — promises broken, words lost in the echo. The rap sections build the counter-identity piece by piece: I can't be mapped, cloned, flattened; I hold my own scars and love them; one last lie seen through, and this time, finally let go.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~lost-in-the-echo-live":{"haiku":"The speaker breaks free from a foundation that conditioned and betrayed him; each word of their promises dissolves into echo, and he finally releases them.","opus":"Linkin Park confront a betrayal that flipped a trusted foundation into sorrow and shock, vowing to finally let go as broken promises lose themselves in the echo. In this live rendering, Mike Shinoda's defiant verses reject being held back or cloned, holding his own scars and refusing to bow, transforming wounded trust into unshakeable self-possession.","sonnet":"Every promise broken goes unanswered, dissolving into the space between people who once mattered. The speaker finally lets go — not from weakness but from accumulated refusal to be mapped, cloned, or folded. What he carries are scars worn without apology, and the release of someone who could not hold what they claimed is the moment everything becomes his again.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~lying-from-you":{"fable":"Shinoda's verses are a confession about performing a self: the narrator built an agreeable, well-fitting persona to satisfy the expectations of the people who raised and shaped him, and now resents that they mistake the mask for the real person. The song's cruelty is that it turns the accusation inward. What begins as blaming an other for demanding the fiction collapses into the recognition that the ugliest thing in that other is his own reflection, the self he manufactured to please them. Chester's bridge is the emotional cost cashed out, an unraveling panic that the honesty finally spoken has driven the person away, so the demand to be released and left alone reads less as liberation than as scorched-earth self-sabotage, tearing down the relationship because the counterfeit self can no longer be maintained. It sits at the exact nerve of Meteora-era Linkin Park: identity as a thing assembled under pressure to belong, and the rage that arrives when you realize you no longer know which version was ever yours.","fableDeep":"The title's preposition is the thesis — lying from you, not to you: falsehood as a getaway vehicle rather than a betrayal, deceit pointed away from the relationship instead of into it. Verse one frames the performance as crime — pretending makes him exactly what the other always wanted to see while he steals \"second after second,\" time embezzled just because he can, the borrowed identity indicting him as a criminal in his own phrasing. Verse two supplies the origin story: condescending talk of who he ought to be, absorbed until he \"pretended up a person who was fitting in\" — a fake self assembled to spec, then mistaken by everyone, the beloved included, for the real one. The chorus's exit demand is oddly humble — wanting to be pushed aside, preferring anywhere alone — and pivots on the double-edged \"the very worst part of you is me\": their worst quality is him, or their worst lives inside him; either reading licenses the escape. Thesis: a mask so successful that removing it is what reads as the lie.","haiku":"A person breaks free from pretending to be someone they are not, realizing that conforming to others' expectations has corrupted their true self.","opus":"The exhaustion of maintaining a false self to please someone, as the narrator admits he pretended to be who others wanted and now lies his way out, realizing the worst part of the other person is himself.","sonnet":"Someone who has spent years pretending to be the person others wanted them to be reaches a breaking point, recognising that the false self they performed has become part of them, and decides to break free even if it means hurting those who knew only that mask.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","freedom & escape"],"means":"the mask assembled to please becomes indistinguishable from the real self","built":"the preposition in the title is the thesis: lying from, not to — deceit as getaway vehicle","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~lying-from-you-live-lp-underground-tour-2003":{"haiku":"Pretense allows the speaker to be what others want, hiding criminal nature behind acceptable disguise.","opus":"About the exhausting performance of being who others want you to be until the mask becomes the self; the ugliest realization is that the worst part of the person he's fleeing is actually himself.","sonnet":"A fabricated identity built to fit someone else's demands now impossible to sustain — 'the very worst part of you is me.' The narrator stops performing, chooses lying their way out rather than continuing the pretense, and finds that leaving the constructed person behind means becoming unrecognizable to everyone who wanted the performance.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~mark-the-graves":{"haiku":"Guilt over past carelessness and harm done—scars remain even when blood washes away, and the speaker acknowledges making indelible marks.","opus":"Linkin Park sit in the guilt of a relationship that ended in mutual harm, where two people keep haunting each other because neither can let it die. The bleak comfort the narrator clings to is that the damage he caused left a lasting impression, so at least he was significant to her, however painfully.","sonnet":"About the lasting damage done to a relationship through carelessness — the narrator acknowledges they hurt someone deeply, that the wound won't heal cleanly, and that the only trace of what they had is the scar they left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~massive":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts the unchangeable self, breaking themselves in pursuit of salvation, floating in a state between destruction and grace.","opus":"A later-era track from the band's post-Chester chapter, wrestling with a corrosive attachment and the resolve to cut oneself free. Recurring nightmares and the ache of mistaking someone harmful for right give way to a hard-won state of grace, self-preservation reached through breakdown, choosing to float free rather than drown in someone else.","sonnet":"A relationship the narrator keeps misreading as right even after waking from the dream — the screaming in the dreaming every night, still mistaking you for right. They broke themselves hoping to save some space, and now float in a dissociated grace, neither present nor free.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~my-december":{"haiku":"An isolated person retreats into winter, regrets past words, and fantasizes about having a home and someone to return to.","opus":"A narrator sits alone in a wintry, snowbound landscape, wishing he could unsay the words that hurt someone and give up everything just for a place to belong. An early Linkin Park B-side stripped of their rap-metal aggression, it leans into cold solitude and remorse, casting the winter month as both his and the emblem of his isolation.","sonnet":"Solitude in winter becomes both a chosen retreat and an admission of failure — the snow-covered stillness is peaceful, but underneath it runs the ache of having said the wrong things and ended up entirely alone. The speaker would surrender everything just to have somewhere to belong again.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~my-dsmbr-mickey-p-reanimation-feat-kelli-ali":{"haiku":"December brings regret, isolation, and a wish to undo hurtful words; the speaker wants connection and home but feels perpetually alone.","opus":"A frozen, solitary winter becomes a vessel for regret, the narrator wishing he could unsay the words that hurt and willing to surrender everything just for a place and a person to return to. Reanimation, Linkin Park's 2002 remix companion to Hybrid Theory, refracts the band's nu-metal ache through electronics, with Kelli Ali's guest vocal easing the longing into cold, lonely stillness.","sonnet":"A man stands alone in a snow-covered winter he claims as his own, hollowed by the sense of something irretrievably missed. He would surrender everything he has just to belong somewhere, to return home to someone — the ache of isolation dressed as a season.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~new-divide":{"haiku":"Loss leaves an unbridgeable gap between past and present; the speaker receives only what they deserve—severance.","opus":"Written for the first Transformers film, a widescreen plea for a second chance — the narrator braced to accept a deserved reckoning yet begging for evidence he has misjudged, that a rift with someone can still be closed. Redemption sought across a widening distance.","sonnet":"A failed relationship remembered through the imagery of lightning, ash falling like snow, and ground caving in between where two people stood. The narrator asks to be proven wrong, to have the memory washed clean, acknowledging that every loss, lie, and denied truth contributed to a divide too wide to cross back over.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~new-divide-live":{"haiku":"Following loss and catastrophe, the speaker feels severed from another person by guilt, regret, and accumulated pain, desperately seeking reconciliation across an unbridgeable emotional distance.","opus":"A desperate appeal for a way to close the chasm that has opened between two people after a devastating rupture, painted in end-of-the-world imagery of storms, floods, and crumbling earth. Convinced he's only getting his just punishment, the narrator pleads to erase the past and finally reach honesty across the gulf now separating them.","sonnet":"A post-collapse relationship autopsy — the speaker processes the moment a bond shattered, recognizing the accumulation of lies, regrets, and poor choices that opened an unbridgeable distance, and asking for one last chance to redeem himself across that gulf.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~no-more-sorrow":{"haiku":"A person confronts liars and thieves in power who traded freedom for fear and money for lives, declaring they'll no longer pay for those mistakes.","opus":"Linkin Park deliver a scathing attack on a corrupt authority that swapped liberty for terror and cashed in human lives, declaring his reign over. Aimed at warmongers and hypocrites, it channels the rage of ordinary people who bore the cost of the powerful's failures and are done offering them any mercy.","sonnet":"A direct attack on a corrupt and lying authority figure — a political or corporate power that has traded freedom for fear and lives for money — with the narrator refusing to pay for their mistakes any longer and declaring their time is up and will be replaced.","src":"haiku","fable":"A track from Minutes to Midnight (2007), the album where Linkin Park abandoned nu-metal for overtly political rock in the Bush-era Iraq-war moment. The song addresses a leader whose war is framed as righteous crusade but exposed as a swindle: liberty swapped for terror, profit weighed against the dead. Its emotional arc is a turn from grief to verdict, hope curdling into the recognition of betrayal, then hardening into the flat pronouncement that the accused's borrowed hour is up. The escalating bridge chant strips the indictment to its two nouns, and the outro's shift from being merely unseated to being wiped out lets the demand for justice tip toward something closer to vengeance.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","war & battle"],"means":"grief at betrayal hardening into the flat verdict that the accused's hour is up","built":"outro's shift from being unseated to being wiped out tips the demand toward vengeance"},
"linkin-park~nobody-s-listening":{"fable":"Shinoda's solo turn on Meteora opens as a pure battle-rap flex, all crew confidence and superiority over rivals, then quietly reveals that the whole posture is armor. The bragging register curdles into an inventory of what's actually held in the chest, and the read reverses: the aggression was never about winning a feud but about metabolizing pain into product, using stress and grievance as the only fuel the writing has. The hook makes the real subject explicit and cruel, that all this loudness is a plea nobody receives, so the more clearly he calls the more the silence answers. What sounds like dominance is the sound of someone shouting into a room that has already turned away.","fableDeep":"Two registers alternate: verse one is genre performance — style, basement tracks, demands to rewind — while verse two drops into inventory, 'heart full of pain, head full of stress, handful of anger held in my chest,' grievance itemized like cargo. The chorus positions the speaker as ignored prophet, warning delivered 'loud and clear' to nobody — an irony on a multi-platinum record, heard by millions and heeded by none, which is precisely the complaint: audience isn't the same as listening. The candid center is 'I hate my rhymes, but hate everyone else's more,' competitive self-loathing admitted as the engine, with stress explicitly credited as something to write on — the wound is the notebook. The outro strips the song down to the inventory chanted raw, form tightening into the pressure it describes, the final chorus even losing its last consonant mid-word. Thesis: converting pain into content pays every bill except the original one — being heard.","haiku":"The speaker is carrying a burden of emotional pain and warning others about their situation, but feels completely ignored and unheard despite trying to communicate clearly.","opus":"A rapper channels a chest full of pain, stress, and anger into his verses, insisting he's warned people loud and clear only to be ignored, with struggle and trash-talk fueling his drive to write.","sonnet":"A narrator vents frustration over being ignored despite speaking plainly, carrying a buildup of pain, stress, and anger from years of struggle against trash-talking critics and an indifferent audience.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"a battle-rap flex that unravels into a plea nobody receives","built":"the outro strips to raw inventory, form tightening into the pressure it describes"},
"linkin-park~numb":{"fable":"An anthem worn smooth as teenage rebellion against a controlling parent — until the bridge breaks the frame: the singer glimpses that his oppressor was once the same cornered figure, disappointing someone above, and admits he'll likely repeat the failure himself, so the numbness isn't a triumphant break but an inherited condition passed down a chain of pressure. The heaviness is the recognition that becoming yourself, here, might just mean becoming the next link.","fableDeep":"Going numb under the weight of living someone else's blueprint — but the bridge is the real center: 'I know you were just like me, with someone disappointed in you.' The pressure is inherited, the critic was once the criticized, and the song is less about escaping a person than about refusing to pass the cycle on.","haiku":"Emotional numbness and loss of identity develop from being pressured to live under someone else's expectations and rules rather than as oneself.","opus":"A song about being worn down by someone else's expectations, where the speaker feels smothered and constantly judged, and grows numb and detached while longing to be himself instead of who the other person wants him to be.","sonnet":"The speaker is exhausted by the pressure of living up to someone else's expectations, feeling emotionally deadened by the constant sense of falling short, and asserting a need to reclaim their own identity rather than conform.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["family & blood","identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"numbness exposed as inherited pressure passed down a chain","built":"bridge breaks the rebellion frame by mirroring oppressor and speaker","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~numb-live-in-texas":{"haiku":"Identity erodes under pressure to meet impossible expectations and to walk borrowed pain.","opus":"The anthem of buckling under someone's expectations, a child under a parent's or lover's controlling gaze; going numb is the only defense, and the ache is simply wanting to be more himself and less what they demand.","sonnet":"Living under the crushing weight of someone else's expectations until feeling itself shuts down — 'I've become so numb, I can't feel you there.' The caught-in-the-undertow metaphor captures passive helplessness, and the turn comes in the acknowledgment that the other person was once like this too, which does not excuse them but complicates the blame.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~one-step-closer":{"haiku":"Constant conflict and pressure from another person drive the speaker toward emotional collapse, requiring distance to survive.","opus":"Overwhelmed by someone's words and empty talk, the speaker feels pushed one step closer to breaking, craving room to breathe and finding refuge in ignorance before erupting in a demand for the other to shut up.","sonnet":"The narrator is at the edge of a breakdown brought on by a relentless, one-sided conflict — every word from the other person pushes closer to a snapping point — and the song ends with a wordless eruption of rage, the communication having completely collapsed.","src":"haiku","fable":"The nu-metal breakthrough that made Linkin Park a household name, this reads on its surface as an argument, but the real subject is the machinery of being talked at. Bennington plays someone whose only remaining defense against words is to stop processing them, framing willful not-knowing as the last available comfort while insisting that silence would spare the aggressor too. What makes it land is the structure: the verses stay clenched and controlled until the bridge detonates into pure command, so the track enacts the very rupture it keeps narrating, the composed self finally giving way to the scream it was holding back. Underneath the adolescent-rage caricature it later became, it is really about the point where reasoning collapses and volume becomes the only honest response.","fv":2,"fnote":"The rage had a specific target — the band has said it was written mid-session, aimed at their producer's endless demands for rewrites: the detonation fired across the mixing desk.","themes":["anger & defiance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"willful not-knowing framed as the last available defense","built":"the bridge detonates into pure command enacting the very rupture it narrates"},
"linkin-park~one-step-closer-100-gecs-reanimation":{"haiku":"Communication collapses into unbearable noise as words push the speaker toward breakdown.","opus":"The old breaking point of being driven one step from the edge by someone's words, finding relief only in not knowing. The reworked verse grounds the mania in domestic minutiae, dirty dishes and clenched shoulders, before the eruption of the demand to be heard, screamed rather than asked.","sonnet":"The original's edge-of-breakdown narrative filtered through domestic mundane detail — dirty dishes, tummy turning to boulders, clenching shoulders — which makes the overload more claustrophobic than cathartic. The physical accumulation of small pressures becomes the context for the eventual explosion.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~one-step-closer-feat-jonathan-davis-live-projekt-revolution-2004":{"haiku":"Words accumulate into unbearable pressure pushing the speaker to the edge of their sanity.","opus":"Being pushed to the edge by someone's endless words, wishing to disappear and finding bliss only in ignorance as nothing goes away. The repeated demand to shut up is the moment the held pressure finally breaks.","sonnet":"Overload reaching its limit — every word from the other person drives the narrator one step closer to snapping. The bliss found in ignorance, the wish to disappear, and the eventual explosion ('shut up when I'm talking to you') are all stations on the same road toward a breakdown that is also a refusal to keep absorbing.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~one-step-closer-live":{"haiku":"Relentless pressure and communication breakdown push the speaker to their breaking point, where silence and isolation are the only refuge.","opus":"The sound of being pushed to the breaking point by someone whose every word drags you nearer the edge. Craven refuge in ignorance gives way to the eruption of shut up, the exact moment restraint snaps into rage.","sonnet":"About the white-hot point just before a breakdown caused by a person who won't stop talking — the narrator finds bliss in ignoring everything, wants to disappear, and the repeated demand to 'shut up' is the sound of someone whose last thread of tolerance is snapping.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~one-step-closer-live-in-texas":{"haiku":"Communication becomes toxic as every word pushes the speaker closer to their breaking point.","opus":"Frustration boiling over into the breaking point, words losing all meaning and ignorance offering the only peace; the eruption of being told to shut up is really the sound of someone shoved to his limit.","sonnet":"Overload reaching its limit — every word from the other person drives the narrator one step closer to snapping. The bliss found in ignorance, the wish to disappear, and the eventual explosion ('shut up when I'm talking to you') are all stations on the same road toward a breakdown that is also a refusal to keep absorbing.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~p5hng-me-a-wy":{"haiku":"Deception poisons intimacy as mirrors reflect only the speaker's own failures back at them.","opus":"Remaining in a relationship that keeps shoving him off, forcing one final counterfeit grin just to stay near her; the tragedy is a sacrifice offered blindly, with no grasp of why he keeps absorbing the fault for someone who refuses to notice him.","sonnet":"A relationship where the narrator's mistakes stare back from the other person's eyes — nothing there but reflection, push away, no matter what I do you're still blind to me. The sacrifice of hiding in a lie accumulates until telling the truth is the only move left, even knowing it won't be received.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~p5hng-me-a-wy-live-in-texas":{"haiku":"Deception spreads between lovers, each becoming a mirror of the other's corruption.","opus":"The same portrait of self-sacrifice in a loveless bond, here dedicated to aspiring musicians in the crowd; he keeps taking the blame and staying while being pushed away, blind faith answered with blindness.","sonnet":"A relationship where the narrator's mistakes stare back from the other person's eyes — nothing there but reflection, push away, no matter what I do you're still blind to me. The sacrifice of hiding in a lie accumulates until telling the truth is the only move left, even knowing it won't be received.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~p5hng-me-a-wy-mike-shinoda-reanimation-feat-stephen-richards":{"haiku":"A partner hides in lies and is blind to the narrator's sacrifice; everything falls apart because both people are broken. The narrator stays despite being pushed away, eventually turning to face the truth they can no longer hide.","opus":"A Mike Shinoda-helmed remix that reworks a Linkin Park track about a bond sustained entirely by one partner's endless surrender. The narrator absorbs fault and forces smiles just to remain in a doomed relationship, and the cruelest part is that he can no longer explain to himself what he's holding on for.","sonnet":"A relationship ending in mutual exhaustion and invisibility: the narrator sees only his own failures reflected back, has spent years absorbing blame, and finally stops. Reversed lyrical text mirrors the psychological distortion of trying to hold together something that inverts every effort. The sacrifice was real; the blindness was mutual; the end is simply the last honest act.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~papercut":{"fable":"Nu-metal's signature move was making self-hatred sound like an external attack, and this opening salvo does exactly that by personifying the inner critic as a watcher lodged under the skin—not a demon or an enemy but the part of you that catalogues your failures and enjoys them. The verse-level paranoia is really the ordinary experience of being your own harshest surveillance, dressed up as horror. The pivot comes when the second verse turns outward: everyone carries this same tormentor, which reframes the whole thing from private breakdown to a shared human default, then curdles into a class or status wound about never measuring up to someone else. Bennington's bridge, where daylight itself feels like a betrayal, closes the trap—even the thing that should offer relief has switched sides, so there's no exterior left to escape to.","fableDeep":"An inner watcher — a second face under the skin — that catalogues every lie and laughs at every fall, worst when the light goes down. The title sizes the wounds: papercuts, small failures that sting out of all proportion. The speaker's only defense is to universalize it — 'you've got a face on the inside too' — half taunt, half consolation that nobody's skin is empty.","haiku":"Internal paranoia and anxiety manifest as an inner critical voice that judges every action and witnesses every lie, creating persistent self-doubt.","opus":"Paranoia rendered as a second face lurking under the flesh, an internal prosecutor cataloguing every slip and clocking every falsehood. The disquieting twist is the claim that everyone hides that accusing double within, and even daylight starts to feel like a betrayal.","sonnet":"The speaker is consumed by a paranoid inner voice — an imagined face beneath the skin — that catalogues every mistake and lie, and the lyric suggests this same self-critical consciousness exists inside everyone.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression","surveillance & control"],"means":"self-hatred dressed as external surveillance to make it feel survivable","built":"the title scales the wound — papercuts, small failures stinging out of proportion","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~papercut-live-at-docklands-arena-london":{"haiku":"Paranoia manifests as an inner critic—a parasitic face inside the mind that watches, judges, and laughs at every failure and lie.","opus":"The debut single's still-definitive portrait of paranoia as a second self, a face beneath the skin that watches, judges, and catalogues every lie and stumble. It externalizes anxiety and self-surveillance into something almost physical, then universalizes it: everyone carries that inner accuser. The dread of the sun going down seals its claustrophobic, panicked mood.","sonnet":"An internal critic that never shuts off — the face inside that watches every lie, laughs at every fall, lives right beneath the skin. The narrator extends this to everyone, making paranoia not a personal pathology but the universal condition, everyone watched by their own internal face as the sun goes down and the light betrays.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~papercut-live-in-texas":{"haiku":"Internal criticism manifests as a surveillance presence, a parasitic force that watches and judges from within.","opus":"Paranoia personified as a face living beneath the skin, the internal critic that catalogues every mistake and watches every lie; the unsettling turn is insisting everyone carries that accusing second self, and the light itself feels like betrayal.","sonnet":"An internal critic that never shuts off — the face inside that watches every lie, laughs at every fall, lives right beneath the skin. The narrator extends this to everyone, making paranoia not a personal pathology but the universal condition, everyone watched by their own internal face as the sun goes down and the light betrays.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~part-of-me":{"fable":"A war against an embedded piece of the self — compulsion lodged near the heart, hated daily, impossible to live without — and the fantasy of carving it out and keeping the hole. The second movement testifies from the far side: freedom is real, terrifying, and immediately reveals the next part that has to go.","fableDeep":"The governing figure is surgical: the unwanted part must be cut from the soul, and the refrain 'cut myself free willingly' keeps liberation and laceration in one verb — freeing and wounding indistinguishable, reinforced by scar imagery where new cuts cover old ones, relapse written on skin. The cost-benefit math is stated plainly: weighed against consequences, can't live without it, so it's senseless — addiction's logic reproduced exactly. The mirror line inverts the cliché: the man staring through him rather than at him, the false self more substantial than the person. Then the long second verse switches person and tense entirely, becoming a recovery sermon delivered to you — freedom frightening if never felt, something angelic once it's dealt with, calm when the problem's gone — followed immediately by the catch: another piece of yourself you can't let be, sobriety as serial excision. The whispered outro concedes the remainder, a part still screaming away silently. Thesis: recovery rendered as amputation, honest that the phantom limb keeps talking.","haiku":"A song about psychological integration and the struggle to accept and transform one's painful past. The speaker learns that freedom comes from integrating shadow parts rather than cutting them away.","opus":"A struggle with a self-destructive part of the self that won't go away, marked by cutting imagery and self-loathing, and the hard, frightening work of cutting free and finding peace by removing the broken parts.","sonnet":"A struggle to excise a destructive inner force — something that causes ongoing self-harm and self-disgust — balanced against the terrifying possibility of freedom if that part of the self is finally cut away.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~plaster-2-figure-09-demo":{"haiku":"Fighting over rewriting history in a relationship, both convinced the other was more wrong.","opus":"An early demo of a relationship's mutual damage, someone realizing they've become the liar they resented, forcing a fit that doesn't belong. It's the ugly recognition that both people wronged each other and neither can stop.","sonnet":"A person who cannot stop being pulled back to someone who has wronged them — they take what they can, resent the debt they're expected to repay, and even as they try to undo their own mistakes they realize they've started behaving like the person who hurt them.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~plc-4-mie-h-d-amp-live-reanimation-feat-zion":{"haiku":"Someone resents being exploited and indebted, refusing to feed others' greed while seeking a place to find rest and independence.","opus":"Craving distance from someone who trades favors then demands them back, the narrator likens their debt to the moon owing the sun for its borrowed light, sick of tension and hunger and needing a place to rest. Reworked for Reanimation, this remix of A Place for My Head layers Zion's aggressive verse over the original's exhausted resentment.","sonnet":"Exhausted by a relationship built on obligation and score-keeping, a person pushes back against someone who gives only to demand repayment. The moon-and-sun metaphor cuts to the core: generosity weaponized as debt is no generosity at all.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~points-of-authority":{"fable":"The song is an early-2000s nu-metal anatomy of an abusive relationship, and Shinoda's frame is the key that unlocks it: the game-and-race scaffolding treats the dynamic as a rigged contest you can only lose by staying in. Bennington's verses expose the mechanics of that cruelty, self-harm weaponized as leverage against a partner who feeds on control, while the hammering refrain diagnoses the abuser as someone merely reproducing damage done to them. What makes it more than a breakup complaint is that final movement: the mantra about inherited harm becomes an unwilling prophecy, the victim recognizing they are being groomed into the same pattern, so the only escape is to quit before the cycle claims your name too.","fableDeep":"The refrain is diagnosis, not insult: 'you live what you've learned' repeats until the cruelty reads as curriculum — pain passed down as something taught and then faithfully practiced ('you want someone to hurt like you, you want to share what you have been through'). The speaker's confession is uglier than his accusation: 'the way I hurt myself again, just to get back at you' — inside an abusive bond, even self-harm becomes a move in the other person's game. And the framing verse turns the whole relationship into a rigged contest ('forfeit the game before somebody else takes you out of the frame'), where the only winning move is the one nobody in the song makes: leaving the table.","opus":"A song aimed at a controlling, self-righteous person who takes pleasure in others' pain and repeats the harm they learned, while the speaker describes his pride broken and even hurting himself to strike back at them.","sonnet":"The speaker confronts someone who projects their own damage onto others — demanding authority they haven't earned, hurting people to share their pain — while also acknowledging being drawn into self-harm as a way to strike back at that person.","src":"sonnet","fv":2,"themes":["cycle of abuse","surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the victim recognizing they are being groomed into the same pattern","built":"game-and-race framing turns an abusive dynamic into a rigged contest with one exit","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~points-of-authority-cristal-remix":{"fable":"A portrait of an abuser as a former victim: someone who has to act like they're someone, who wants others to hurt like they do because they live what they've learned — while the speaker breaks his own pride, even hurts himself, just to strike back.","fableDeep":"The refrain is diagnosis, not insult: 'you live what you've learned' repeats until the cruelty reads as curriculum — pain passed down as something taught and then faithfully practiced ('you want someone to hurt like you, you want to share what you have been through'). The speaker's confession is uglier than his accusation: 'the way I hurt myself again, just to get back at you' — inside an abusive bond, even self-harm becomes a move in the other person's game. And the framing verse turns the whole relationship into a rigged contest ('forfeit the game before somebody else takes you out of the frame'), where the only winning move is the one nobody in the song makes: leaving the table.","opus":"A song aimed at a controlling, self-righteous person who takes pleasure in others' pain and repeats the harm they learned, while the speaker describes his pride broken and even hurting himself to strike back at them.","sonnet":"The speaker confronts someone who projects their own damage onto others — demanding authority they haven't earned, hurting people to share their pain — while also acknowledging being drawn into self-harm as a way to strike back at that person.","src":"sonnet"},
"linkin-park~points-of-authority-live":{"haiku":"Power dynamics in relationships exploit pain and self-destruction for control; cycles of learned behavior perpetuate harm.","opus":"An abusive dynamic where one partner takes pleasure in the other's pain and passes on the hurt they themselves learned. The speaker sees the cruelty for what it is and urges the abuser to forfeit before the cycle destroys them both.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a domineering, hypocritical person who needs others to suffer the way they have suffered — the speaker accuses them of cruelty masked as authority, and says that living what you've learned means perpetuating the same cycle of pain.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~points-of-authority-live-at-docklands-arena-london":{"haiku":"A manipulative lover cycles shame and pain, teaching the speaker to perpetuate the same cycles of trauma onto others.","opus":"A serrated hybrid-theory cut about a manipulative, controlling figure who takes pleasure in another's pain and passes down wounds they've inherited. The refrain about living what you've learned pins the abuser as a product of their own damage. It burns with the resentment of someone forced to forfeit their pride to a person who's never wrong.","sonnet":"A relationship built on cruelty and conditioning — you hurt me, I hurt myself to get back at you, and both of us learned this somewhere earlier. 'You live what you've learned' is the mechanism: cycles of domination inherited and re-enacted, with the narrator naming the pattern even as they're still inside it.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~points-of-authority-remix":{"haiku":"Cyclical pattern of emotional abuse and learned suffering passed between people.","opus":"A song about being caught in a power struggle with someone who projects their own wounds onto you, taunting that they are 'never wrong' while conditioning you to hurt yourself just to strike back at them — the abuser passing on exactly what was done to them.","sonnet":"The speaker confronts a manipulative person who has weaponized pain — enjoying the speaker's suffering, stripping away pride, and modeling cruelty they learned from their own wounds — while the refrain warns the listener to quit before someone else destroys them first.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is an early-2000s nu-metal anatomy of an abusive relationship, and Shinoda's frame is the key that unlocks it: the game-and-race scaffolding treats the dynamic as a rigged contest you can only lose by staying in. Bennington's verses expose the mechanics of that cruelty, self-harm weaponized as leverage against a partner who feeds on control, while the hammering refrain diagnoses the abuser as someone merely reproducing damage done to them. What makes it more than a breakup complaint is that final movement: the mantra about inherited harm becomes an unwilling prophecy, the victim recognizing they are being groomed into the same pattern, so the only escape is to quit before the cycle claims your name too.","fv":2,"fableAlt":"Jay Gordon's rework externalizes the song's inherited-damage theme by turning the abuser's mechanized cruelty into the track's own body, swapping live instrumentation for programmed beats until the rigged contest sounds like a machine running its cycle. Where the original diagnosed the pattern in flesh and voice, this industrial, synthesizer-driven version renders it robotic and heavier, so the dread of becoming the same thing plays as a system that cannot stop itself. Joe Hahn's all-CGI clip completes the reframe, casting the band as digital heads commanding robots, the human wound abstracted into cold hardware waging its inherited war.","themes":["cycle of abuse","surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the victim recognizing they are being groomed into the same pattern","built":"game-and-race framing turns an abusive dynamic into a rigged contest with one exit"},
"linkin-park~ppr-kut-cheapshot-and-jubacca-reanimation-feat-rasco-and-planet-asia":{"haiku":"Internal paranoia and intrusive voices torment the speaker, creating a presence beneath the skin that watches and mocks every failure.","opus":"A spiraling study of paranoia, a whirlwind in the head and a watching face beneath the skin that laughs at every fall, the sun's light itself feeling like betrayal. This is a remix of Linkin Park's Papercut, reworked on their Reanimation project with guest verses from Rasco and Planet Asia layering underground hip-hop over Chester and Mike's original anxiety, the inner voice made even more claustrophobic.","sonnet":"Paranoia as an internal face that never sleeps — there is a presence behind the speaker's own eyes that watches every lie, laughs at every fall, and never goes quiet. The song layers multiple voices: one trapped in anxious freefall, one trying just to make music and live, both converging on the same confession that something is always talking back from inside, dragging the person toward the surface of their own skin.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~pts-of-athrty-jay-gordon-reanimation":{"haiku":"Toxic relationship cycles of manipulation; one partner takes pleasure in the other's pain, forcing them to hurt themselves, while denying accountability and demanding to be believed innocent.","opus":"A confrontation with a manipulative, self-righteous person who takes pleasure in the narrator's pain and wants others to hurt as they've been hurt; he sees through their need to always be right and to spread their damage, warning that the pace they set won't last.","sonnet":"A song about being trapped in a relationship with someone who wants to spread their pain outward — the other person hurts because they need company in their hurt, and the narrator is both drawn in and destroyed by performing suffering for them.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~pushing-me-away":{"haiku":"A speaker sacrifices themselves through lies and blame, enabling a toxic relationship. Everything falls apart as they realize they're being tested and pushed away.","opus":"Chester Bennington sings of clinging to a relationship founded on deceit and self-betrayal, forcing one final counterfeit grin merely for the sake of companionship; the true cost is never grasping why he kept wrecking himself to hold onto someone shoving him off.","sonnet":"A confession of prolonged self-deception in a failing relationship: both parties know it's over, but one narrator admits he kept lying and taking blame rather than walking away, until being tested one too many times finally pushes him out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song whose accusation curdles into self-indictment. The title sounds like a complaint against a cruel partner, but the verses keep confessing that the speaker is the liar, the one faking smiles and shouldering blame to stay in a relationship he knows is over. Bennington's chorus finally drops the mask: the real question isn't why she wronged him but why he stayed, why he engineered his own humiliation, and the thing driving him off is his own dawning recognition of that. Shinoda's pre-chorus reframes the private failure as a law of entropy, everyone eventually breaks down, so the exit reads less as rupture than as the clock running out on a lie both were maintaining.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","artifice & performance"],"means":"the complaint against a cruel partner curdles into a confession of engineered self-humiliation","built":"the title sounds like an accusation outward but the verses reveal the speaker as the architect of his own exit"},
"linkin-park~pushing-me-away-live":{"haiku":"Toxic patterns repeat as the speaker sacrifices authenticity and self-respect for a relationship built on lies and manipulation.","opus":"A relationship sustained by lies and self-sacrifice, the last fake smile offered for the sake of staying together. The wound is realizing he was being tested all along, blaming himself for never walking away while being pushed off.","sonnet":"About the exhaustion of maintaining a relationship built on self-deception — the narrator has been faking smiles, taking blame, and sacrificing authenticity; they now see they were being tested and used, and the song is a quiet, sad accounting of why they never left sooner.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~qwerty":{"haiku":"Deception and control hollow the speaker into a victim, lies following them home until they consume everything.","opus":"A furious face-off with a deceiver who shelters behind falsehoods and an icy mask, tracing how dishonesty, like a tablet too big to get down that ends up devouring the person, trails them back to their door and grows from a craving for control.","sonnet":"Deception that consumed its architect — the lies that were hard to follow eventually followed the liar home, swallowing them whole like a pill they couldn't take. The narrator is the victim but also implicated: both were broken down, both hid, and the question of who crossed the line first doesn't settle anything.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~qwerty-live":{"haiku":"A narrator confronts a deceiver who hides behind lies and false personas, calling out how this person has lost control of their own hollow existence and deteriorating identity.","opus":"A furious confrontation with a deceiver who hides behind lies and a cold disguise, tracing how lies, like a pill that swallows you whole, follow a person home and spring from the need for control.","sonnet":"A narrator confronts someone whose habitual deception has hollowed them out — lies that followed both of them home — resulting in a breakdown of communication and a relationship destroyed by dishonesty and self-deception.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confrontation with someone so encased in their own deception that the mask has fused to the face and become the only self left. The song works by collapsing two figures into one grammar: the accuser's you keeps sliding toward the addict who swallowed the very thing meant to cure them, until blame and pity share a single pronoun and you can't tell if the speaker is indicting a liar or mourning one. That recurring image of a remedy that consumes the person who reaches for it is the read's center of gravity, and the bridge's taunt about never climbing out of the hole is less cruelty than diagnosis. Fittingly, this was the band's own transitional artifact, a heavy song they never let onto a studio album, arriving under a title that names the arbitrary standard everyone inherits and no one questions.","fv":2,"fnote":"Cut for the 2006 fan-club EP and debuted on that summer's tour — the heaviest thing the band wrote between Meteora and Minutes to Midnight, then abandoned when the new album went the other way.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"accuser and addict share one pronoun until blame and mourning merge","built":"a title naming an inherited arbitrary standard frames the trap the song diagnoses"},
"linkin-park~qwerty-studio-demo-version":{"haiku":"Betrayal and manipulation through deception that destroys the victim's sense of self.","opus":"A song about being betrayed by someone's compulsive lying, watching them retreat behind a cold disguise the way an addict swallows a pill that swallows them whole, insisting they can't climb out of the hole their need for control dug.","sonnet":"The speaker has been ground down by a compulsive liar whose self-deception eventually swallowed them whole, tracking how the lies accumulated, crossed a line, and left both people broken — the refrain is a direct accusation: the liar is lost inside a cold disguise of their own making.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~reading-my-eyes-live":{"opus":"A relic from the band's Xero days in the late nineties, years before they were Linkin Park and fronted then by original singer Mark Wakefield rather than Chester Bennington. The narrator bristles at being seen through and second-guessed, resentful of being defined by someone else's read of him, a raw and unpolished sketch of the alienation the group would later sharpen into their breakthrough.","sonnet":"An early Linkin Park rap showcase in which the MC asserts lyrical dominance and technical superiority over rivals, building from battle-rap braggadocio toward a more vulnerable refrain about seeking recognition and losing pride in the pursuit of it.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A Xero-era artifact that survives only as a live take, and the survival is the point: what began as pure Mike Shinoda battle-rap braggadocio—a breathless underground flex piled high with weaponized wordplay and stacked internal rhymes, an MC daring anyone to hack it—gets reframed by everything Linkin Park became. Shinoda's verses are all armor and appetite, the self mythologized into a machine that levels every rival; then Chester's chorus and bridge arrive as the crack in that armor, trading conquest for a quieter accounting where shedding pride is the thing that finally saves. Sung live, years past the demo that spawned it, the bridge's plea about deserving and believing tips the showcase toward confession — the boast already carrying its crack, so the song is really about how bravado is a mask over need, and the title's promise to read the eyes lands on the performer himself.","fv":2,"fnote":"One of the oldest songs in the catalog — it predates Bennington entirely, written in the Xero days with Mark Wakefield on the mic; the live take here is Chester singing his predecessor's showcase.","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"bravado revealed as a mask over need by the crack already inside it","built":"Chester's chorus and bridge arrive as armor-crack against Shinoda's conquest-flex"},
"linkin-park~rebellion-feat-daron-malakian":{"haiku":"The song critiques privileged people who imitate rebellion without ever facing real oppression, falling apart before genuine hardship begins.","opus":"Linkin Park, joined by Daron Malakian of System of a Down, indict the comfortable for playacting revolt. Confined by possessions and untouched by the gun of oppression, the fortunate ones wear the colors and stage a rebellion that is only imitation. As distant buildings burn in the face of real damage, the refrain admits they fell apart before they even began, hollowing out privileged dissent.","sonnet":"A privileged voice confronts the gap between performed resistance and real oppression — elsewhere buildings burn and people die, while here the rebellion is aesthetic, confined to colors and possessions. The admission that they have lost before starting carries genuine shame rather than detachment.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~resolution":{"fable":"A rewound reel of the same mistake — returning to a hated pleasure and the pain it manufactures, haunted by a residue someone left behind that no scraping removes. Second pass through, the pronouns quietly flip, and the fault migrates from the speaker's ledger to the departed's; nothing else changes.","fableDeep":"Constructed as exact repetition with one surgical edit: the first bridge confesses 'my mistakes shouldn't have passed to today's new trouble,' and the second replays every line with pronouns swapped to you and yours — identical language indicting both parties in turn, blame revealed as a template either name fits. The parentheticals work like editing marks — the beginning, come down, turn around — running memory as a film rewound to day one, with its blinding two-sun light and rain dripping from a remembered chin. Self-recognition arrives at one remove: not I-am-the-darkness but 'the darkness in a figure in the shape of me,' the silhouette guiltier than the man. The residue is bodily — a taste in the mouth that praying won't rinse out — and 'this thing that you left in me' stays deliberately unnamed: habit, grief, disease, or simply the other person's pattern now running in him. Thesis: the title's resolution never arrives; the song resolves only in the musical sense, cycling back to its own opening.","haiku":"The speaker repeats mistakes and endures their residue, unable to escape the taste of failure or the reminder of what was left behind.","opus":"A brooding meditation on repeating the same mistake, where past wrongs bleed into present trouble and can't be scraped away. The narrator sees his own shape in the darkness, haunted by something a person left inside him. Circular and self-implicating, it reaches for accountability while admitting how impossible it is to bury yesterday's rubble.","sonnet":"A bad inheritance stuck in the mouth — a taste the narrator can't scrape out, a darkness in the figure in the shape of me, pleasure hated and repeated. The same mistake recurs across both speakers: you left something in me, and returning to the beginning only confirms the loop won't break.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~rnw-y-backyard-bangers-reanimation-feat-phoenix-orion":{"haiku":"Someone flees judgment and false accusation while questioning their past choices and seeking to escape others' expectations.","opus":"The core Linkin Park lyric is about the urge to run away, escape guilt-by-association and finger-pointing, and open one's mind to the truth; the added Phoenix Orion verse shifts to a hip-hop pride battle, invoking the culture's pioneers (Bambaataa, Kool Herc) and defiantly repping his crew against doubters.","sonnet":"A remixed 'Runaway' where the original's desire to escape and find truth is layered with a guest rapper honoring hip-hop's founding generation — the flight impulse becomes explicitly tied to creative integrity and refusing to be written off.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~roads-untraveled":{"haiku":"Acceptance that some paths lead nowhere; letting go of regret over unrealized love and finding companionship in shared pain.","opus":"A soft-spoken comfort telling someone not to mourn the routes never taken or a bond that slipped away, insisting the toll exceeded the reward and that sorrow eases with time, a place saved beside them for a companion. The wordless refrains carry the solace. Linkin Park set aside aggression almost completely here for one of Living Things' gentlest, most hymnal moments.","sonnet":"Consolation for someone who lost something they thought they needed — a gentle insistence that the roads not taken and the love that cost too much are better left behind. The offer at the end is simple and human: whatever path you're on, there's a seat here beside someone who won't ask you to explain the detour.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~runaway":{"haiku":"A person accused and blamed for circumstances beyond his control seeks escape and truth, desperate to understand why he bears guilt and yearning to break free from false accusations.","opus":"Linkin Park capture the urge to bolt from a life poisoned by betrayal, false accusations and deceit. Tired of being blamed by association, the narrator wants only to get at the truth, seal himself off from it all and clear space to think for himself.","sonnet":"Someone living under broken trust, false lessons, and constant blame fantasises about escaping it all — running away, finding truth, shutting out the noise, and clearing their mind.","src":"haiku","fable":"An anthem of adolescent siege from the debut, set in a landscape of dust and graffiti where trust has already collapsed and blame arrives from outside as reflex. What sharpens it past ordinary teenage grievance is the second verse's admission: the narrator's own vows of taking action were as hollow as the lies he's accused of, so the finger pointed at him lands partly true. The chorus escapes into wanting answers and an opened mind, but the bridge strips it down to pure flight, repeating the impulse until only the promise to leave remains — the mind he wanted to open shrinking to a single hammered syllable. It reads as a boast that curdles into confession: the fantasy of running away is also a way of never facing the truth it claims to crave.","fv":2,"themes":["freedom & escape","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"the fantasy of flight is a way of never facing the truth it claims to crave","built":"the open mind the chorus wants shrinks to a single hammered syllable in the bridge"},
"linkin-park~shadow-of-the-day":{"haiku":"Isolation and finality overtake; suicide is contemplated as the only goodbye when solutions and beginnings fail.","opus":"A subdued farewell, shutting out the light and turning aside because parting is sometimes the only path and answers rarely come easily. Imagery of well-wishes and blooms at the sill and loved ones begging you to remain strongly suggests a deathbed or wake, the sinking sun quietly sealing a final leave-taking.","sonnet":"A song about someone who has decided to leave — whether through death, departure, or emotional withdrawal — and the person watching them go, unable to stop it, finding cold comfort in the day ending as a metaphor for something irreversible and permanent.","src":"haiku","fable":"A centerpiece of Minutes to Midnight, from Linkin Park's pivot away from nu-metal into restrained, U2-inflected atmospherics, and it works entirely by withholding. Someone shutters their own home against the world while, outside, mourners leave tokens and beg them to stay; the read lives in the gap between those two vantage points. The subject frames leaving as the only sane exit from an unsolvable problem, and the recurring dusk refrain drapes that choice in something almost consoling rather than alarming, which is precisely its danger. What scans as a breakup song is really staged as suicide, the greying light lending gentle cover to an act the friends outside can see plainly and the person inside has already decided.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","death & grief"],"means":"dusk refrain lends gentle cover to what the friends outside can plainly see","built":"what scans as a breakup occupies the structural position of a farewell staged as departure"},
"linkin-park~she-couldn-t":{"haiku":"A woman forced to leave a relationship despite emotional connection, destined to move on regardless of the partner's role.","opus":"A quiet reassurance to someone left behind, insisting the woman who couldn't stay had to go all along and that no blame or imagined wrong could have kept her, while a hovering line warns that soon everyone will know. Sunlight imagery softens the departure into something inevitable. Linkin Park, fronted by Chester Bennington, offer a restrained, atmospheric ballad here, comfort delivered in place of their usual catharsis.","sonnet":"A reassurance addressed to someone left behind by a person who had to go — framing the departure not as abandonment but as inevitability, something she knew from the beginning even if he didn't. The repeated refrain that everyone will eventually understand reads less like prophecy and more like the speaker trying to make peace with an absence that still aches.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~shifter-from-the-inside-demo":{"haiku":"The speaker embodies the listener's idealized self and innocent trust, yet time will reclaim these illusions and take everything away.","opus":"A dreamlike early demo bathed in golden-morning imagery, where a narrator recalls a beginning others tried to strip away. Casting itself as confidence, innocence, and the thing people pretend to be, it reaches toward selfless devotion, doing everything not for himself but for another, even as tomorrow threatens to take it all away.","sonnet":"A claim of recovered innocence and perfect confidence addressed to someone, possibly everyone, who tried to take it. 'Everything I did, it wasn't for me, it was for you' reframes sacrifice, but the speaker's certainty feels self-constructed — a better self built in the golden morning after others stripped the original bare.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~skin-to-bone":{"haiku":"Betrayal and deception eroding a relationship into dust; indifference as the final consequence of broken promises.","opus":"A severance song that borrows the cadence of a burial rite to frame the end of a relationship as an act of decay and passing. The speaker feels relief rather than sorrow at losing a liar, treating the split as inevitable erosion. On a Living Things-era release, the band elevates a private goodbye into something oddly ritual and final.","sonnet":"An elegy for something corrupted — framed in the language of natural entropy, everything returning to ash and dust, but carrying beneath it a specific disgust for someone whose deception made the ending feel earned rather than mourned. The speaker marches away without ceremony, almost relieved that when the name is finally called, they'll be long gone.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~somewhere-i-belong":{"fable":"The signature Meteora move of splitting one psyche across two voices: Shinoda's parenthetical stage-whispers narrate the numbness while Bennington's lines chase escape from it, so the verses aren't describing depression so much as staging the dissociation itself, a self talking over its own shoulder. What makes it more than a want-list of healing is that it keeps confessing the emptiness is self-inflicted, refusing the easy villain most angst reaches for. The bridge is where the song turns its own logic inside out: belonging, it decides, can only come by breaking away from the self entirely, so the destination named in the title is reached by demolition rather than arrival, and the chorus's cry to feel something real never actually lands it, leaving the yearning honest precisely because it stays unsatisfied.","fableDeep":"Letting it all out didn't heal — it only revealed the vacancy, 'the only real thing I've got left to feel.' With the fault owned as his own, the paradox lands in the bridge: to find himself he must 'break away from me' — the self is both the thing to be healed and the thing blocking the healing, and belonging has to be built alone before it can be shared.","haiku":"Personal emotional void and struggle to find belonging and self-worth.","opus":"Struggling to break free of inner numbness and pain to heal, find identity, and reach a sense of belonging on one's own terms.","sonnet":"A person trapped in emptiness and self-blame searching for healing, identity, and a place where they feel they belong.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"belonging reached only by demolishing the self that blocks it","built":"two voices split the psyche across verses staging the dissociation they describe","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~somewhere-i-belong-live":{"fable":"Discovering that letting the pain out only exposed how hollow he is, and resolving to heal alone — he can't belong anywhere until he breaks away from who he's been and finds himself first.","fableDeep":"Letting it all out didn't heal — it only revealed the vacancy, 'the only real thing I've got left to feel.' With the fault owned as his own, the paradox lands in the bridge: to find himself he must 'break away from me' — the self is both the thing to be healed and the thing blocking the healing, and belonging has to be built alone before it can be shared.","haiku":"Personal emotional void and struggle to find belonging and self-worth.","opus":"Struggling to break free of inner numbness and pain to heal, find identity, and reach a sense of belonging on one's own terms.","sonnet":"A person trapped in emptiness and self-blame searching for healing, identity, and a place where they feel they belong.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~somewhere-i-belong-live-in-texas":{"haiku":"The speaker emerges from nothingness seeking connection but discovers they are already fundamentally alone.","opus":"The search for meaning by someone hollow and stuck, owning that the fault is his own; healing means breaking away from the self he knows to finally feel something real and find a place he belongs.","sonnet":"The narrator describes themselves in a state of vacancy — hollow, lost in nothingness, faultless only in the sense that the fault is entirely their own. The desire is to heal and feel something real rather than the performed self, to break away from the constructed version and find, for once, somewhere to belong.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~sorry-for-now":{"haiku":"Apologizing for unavoidable absence while promising eventual understanding and return.","opus":"An apology written from the road by someone whose work keeps pulling them away from the people they love. Sung near the end of Chester Bennington's life, the promise that one day you'll understand now lands unbearably.","sonnet":"A parent on the road apologises to a child left at home — not asking forgiveness so much as offering the assurance that the absence is not abandonment, that the child will one day understand, and that the return is the only thing being counted toward.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~step-up-hybrid-theory-ep":{"haiku":"Criticism of fake and uninspired hip-hop collaborations, demanding authenticity and genuine skill over commercial shortcuts.","opus":"A purist hip-hop battle rap defending real MC craft, mocking pretenders who can't write their own lines, who slap a DJ on for credibility and mistake top-40 exposure for skill. Mock sound-weapon interludes add sci-fi bravado. From Linkin Park's early Hybrid Theory EP, this is Mike Shinoda flexing pure rap identity before the band fused it with Chester Bennington's screams into their world-conquering nu-metal formula.","sonnet":"A hip-hop credibility manifesto aimed at the wave of artists faking their way into the genre — calling out ghostwritten rhymes, commercial dilution, and DJs hired for optics rather than skill. The stance is gatekeeping but principled: the speaker distinguishes between the cash-grab and the craft, insisting the latter took decades to build and can't be borrowed on a label deal.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~the-catalyst":{"haiku":"Humanity burns beneath the weight of inherited sin and broken systems, with no escape from the fires consuming their souls.","opus":"Marking the close of an era for Linkin Park, the track trades personal turmoil for apocalyptic prayer. It depicts a fractured society trapped beneath an unbeatable threat, its pleas for mercy dissolving into terror of being consumed for inherited wrongs. The cry to be raised up and released casts collective judgment as both dread and relief.","sonnet":"Humanity is cast as a fractured species living under an inescapable force — too broken to fight, too numerous to simply perish. The narrator calls out collective sin across generations while pleading for transcendence, the imagery of blinding symphonies and oceans bleeding into sky suggesting apocalypse and longed-for release at once.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~the-emptiness-machine":{"haiku":"Surrender to another's desire hollows the self, the machine promises nourishment but delivers only the echo of one's own dissolution.","opus":"Surrendering your identity to become whatever another person demands, letting them slice you apart just to earn a place beside them. The title names that very trade, a deliberate capitulation to a promise you already sense will be broken, all driven by the ache to belong.","sonnet":"A pattern of self-betrayal in a relationship where the speaker repeatedly allows themselves to be cut open — surrendering identity for someone else's approval, chasing a promise that never delivers, fully aware of how the cycle ends and returning to it anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~the-little-things-give-you-away":{"haiku":"Subtle betrayals and environmental decay exposing truth; when facades fail, complete disaster becomes unavoidable.","opus":"Written after Hurricane Katrina and closing Minutes to Midnight, this Linkin Park track lets the imagery of failed defenses and rising water double for a country that looks away while its people drown. Chester Bennington fronts a slow, mournful build that fuses public catastrophe with the private ache of meaning nothing to the one you love.","sonnet":"A Hurricane Katrina-era elegy framed as a confrontation with indifference — water rising through windows, levees breaking, a nation watching without reaching. The cruelest detail is the irony: six feet underwater, the speaker finally feels looked up to by a leader who wanted admiration but couldn't provide protection, and the little signs of that failure were always there.","src":"haiku","fable":"The album-closer where Linkin Park traded adolescent rage for adult political grief, written in the wake of Hurricane Katrina and using its flood as both fact and metaphor: rising water climbs the frame of the song exactly as a drowning intimacy fails, so that a private plea to be reached for and a public catastrophe of a nation standing by become the same event. The betraying detail of the title works on two registers at once, the small tells that expose a lover's indifference and the small negligences that let a levee give way, and the chorus turns cruel underneath its tenderness: the only way the singer can finally be looked up to is by lying beneath the surface, drowned. That the second half abandons narrative entirely, the band chanting the title over and over past a guitar solo until the phrase erodes into pure incantation, is the point rather than filler, an accusation that hardens into a dirge as it repeats, the sound of grief that has run out of new words and can only keep pressing on the same wound.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","death & grief","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"private neglect and public catastrophe made the same drowning event","built":"the title pun locks a lover's small betrayals to a levee's small failures"},
"linkin-park~the-messenger":{"haiku":"Love and kindness as spiritual guides pulling you back from despair when suffering threatens to break your spirit.","opus":"Linkin Park strip this down to bare voice and guitar for the tender close of a heavier album, a gentle reassurance to someone isolated and desperate. The California band insist that affection keeps us humane even when circumstances leave us lost, that the listener is cherished and always will be, offering the melody itself as a way to find the path back to safety.","sonnet":"A spare and direct comfort song — spoken to someone on the edge of running, it says: listen to love instead of fear, you are not alone, this melody will bring you home. The emotional stance is unconditional and gentle, with no drama, just the quiet insistence that love is what keeps us human when everything else goes dark.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~the-requiem":{"haiku":"Collective guilt across generations for systemic sins; a prayer for deliverance from the moral fires of inherited transgression.","opus":"The eerie opening overture of Linkin Park's A Thousand Suns, this piece frames the album's themes of nuclear fear and inherited guilt as a trembling prayer for mercy against annihilation. Mike Shinoda and Chester Bennington use its haunted plea to ask whether one generation will be punished for the sins passed down to it.","sonnet":"A brief, urgent collective prayer — asking whether humanity will be consumed by the weight of its inherited and committed sins, from the hands to the tongue to the father to the young. The repetition gives it the texture of liturgy without the comfort, a congregation confessing without any certainty of absolution.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~until-it-s-gone":{"haiku":"People fail to appreciate what they have until it disappears, and only by letting go can one truly see and value what matters.","opus":"A narrator grasps only in hindsight that he took what he had for granted, recognizing another person's real worth just as they slip out of reach. From Linkin Park's later, more electronic phase, the track turns a familiar bit of wisdom into a bruised confession that clarity about what mattered can arrive too late, and in solitude.","sonnet":"Love that tried to be protective discovers it was actually constraining, and only by letting go does the speaker finally see the other person clearly. The lesson arrives too late to reverse the loss, and the repeated refrain hammers the realisation into something close to resignation.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~valentine-s-day":{"haiku":"Someone grieves the loss of another on a day associated with love, describing the emotional collapse and darkness that follows their death.","opus":"Linkin Park turn a day associated with love into a lament for the dead. The narrator, hollowed out and undone, watches a body lowered into cold earth and admits he's rudderless now that this person is gone. Placing the loss on Valentine's Day inverts the occasion, reading as grief tangled up with self-blame.","sonnet":"Grief at a loss that occurred on a day now permanently stained: the interior collapses, the wind takes everything, the ground grows cold. The speaker had always believed he was his own protection, and the person's death exposes for the first time how fully that self-sufficiency was a fiction. Being alone turns out to be an entirely different thing from what he imagined.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is Chester Bennington singing about a bereavement, the title's romantic occasion deliberately turned into its opposite: a burial. The song withholds any beloved for its first half, describing only an interior turning to ash and a dark wind carrying an unnamed something off; only later does that abstract loss resolve into a body lowered into cold ground, and the singer's own hollowing-out is revealed as grief. The move from third person to direct address, and the shift from being his own protection to a mind without direction, tracks the specific unmooring of the newly bereaved. By the end the holiday name has become a taunt rather than a comfort, a date engineered for togetherness now marking the exact site of solitude, repeated until the word empties out.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the holiday occasion engineered for togetherness becomes the site of solitude","built":"the loss is withheld as abstraction until a body is lowered mid-song"},
"linkin-park~victimized":{"haiku":"Refusal to remain victim to betrayal; confronting enemies with controlled rage and commitment to never be trapped again.","opus":"A short, seething refusal to be prey again, aimed at the treacherous figures lurking low in the weeds and dealing out their poison, the narrator daring them to strike and vowing to reverse their roles. The hook is pure chanted defiance over aggression. From Living Things, Linkin Park briefly revisit their hardcore roots, Chester Bennington's scream and Shinoda's verses merging into a threat rather than a plea.","sonnet":"A declaration of refusal after a long period of being taken advantage of — the speaker calls out hidden aggressors who thought their venom gave them power, and announces that the era of absorbing damage is over. Defiance is total: no more hiding, no more absorbing, and a willingness to turn the threat back on whoever issued it.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~waiting-for-the-end":{"haiku":"Struggle with loss of control, acceptance of inevitable endings, and the difficulty of moving forward after rupture and betrayal.","opus":"A song about the disorienting middle of loss, neither ending nor beginning, where fists go up out of pain and fear and letting go feels impossible. Its hardest truth is that the worst part of any ending is having to start over. From A Thousand Suns, it pairs Shinoda's fluid verses with Bennington's soaring chorus, Linkin Park at their most atmospheric and searching.","sonnet":"A breakup and a life both burning at once — the speaker is stranded in aftermath, emptied out by something that was never supposed to end this way, clinging to what is already gone while knowing the hardest part of every ending is having to begin again from nothing. The energy is manic and desperate in equal measure.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~war":{"haiku":"War creates only victors and losers with no peace, forcing people to despair, fear, and fight continuously as it grows without apology or restraint.","opus":"An unflinching personification of war as an implacable force: only conflict exists, victory alone decides right and wrong, and it will never apologize for the lives it demands. The bleak throughline is war's self-justification, it needs no cause, spares no pain, and leaves the lost in eternal black night.","sonnet":"A stark, almost liturgical portrait of war as an impersonal, unstoppable force that demands sacrifice, accepts no apology, and ultimately renders all moral distinctions irrelevant — victory decides right and wrong, and fear is the only inheritance.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~wastelands":{"haiku":"In a hopeless present where nothing remains to lose or gain, people are forced to choose while the future vanishes and fear replaces hope. Integrity and meaning have dissolved.","opus":"Mike Shinoda turns rapping itself into warfare, treating each line as a weapon aimed at empty talkers, over a chorus about a gutted present with no future to look toward. The posture is unshakable confidence, planting himself on territory he claims to have earned long before the pretenders showed up.","sonnet":"A rapper asserting uncompromising creative integrity in a landscape of hollow talkers — the wasteland is a present moment where hope turns to fear and tomorrow disappears, but his rhymes are built to outlast all the noise.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~wesside":{"haiku":"Trauma's scars trap consciousness in the past, where pain becomes the only certainty and escape remains impossible.","opus":"Chester Bennington circling a wound that won't close, masking it behind a performed normalcy while privately conceding he may never recover. The ache is being stranded in the past, defined by a hurt he keeps swallowing instead of voicing.","sonnet":"A scar that doesn't close: trapped in yesterday's pain, unable to break away, living behind illusion and inside confusion. Broken promises and blind belief have cost the narrator their dignity, and the honest admission — I will never be alright — is the only thing that breaks the illusion even briefly.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~what-i-ve-done":{"haiku":"A person faces regret from accumulated lies and seeks forgiveness, erasing their past self while starting over after acknowledging pain and wrongdoing.","opus":"A song of confession and redemption in which the narrator confronts the ruin his own choices caused, drawing his remorse from countless deceptions, and resolves to wipe away and pardon the person he's turned into, scrubbing the record clean for a fresh start, often read as a plea for humanity's environmental and moral reckoning.","sonnet":"A song of moral reckoning and self-erasure — the narrator confronts the harm he has caused, abandons his former self rather than defending it, and chooses forgiveness as the only way to begin again.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is the mainstream turn of Linkin Park's second act, the pivot where the nu-metal scream compressed into arena penance. The song stages atonement as an act of erasure: not confession but deletion, scrubbing the self clean rather than accounting for it. What's underneath the anthemic uplift is a colder logic, the plea for mercy and the promise to start over both routing through self-cancellation, as if the only way to survive what you've become is to unwrite it. It reads as personal absolution, but the video's slideshow of human atrocity retrofits the first-person confession into a species-wide one: the addressee being forgiven is everyone. The wordless, communal chanting at the close does the real work, turning a private reckoning into a mass ritual that offers release precisely because it never names the crime.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","identity & becoming","politics & society"],"means":"atonement as self-erasure rather than accounting for the damage done","built":"wordless communal chanting converts private guilt into a mass ritual"},
"linkin-park~what-i-ve-done-live":{"haiku":"Confronting your past actions with remorse and committing to radical personal transformation.","opus":"A reckoning with personal and collective guilt, seeking mercy to wash away the self and start clean. Chester turns confession into catharsis, the desire to erase what you've become in order to forgive it.","sonnet":"A confession and plea for self-forgiveness — the narrator acknowledges having lived a lie, caused harm, and wants to erase what they've become and start again. It's about the hope that guilt can lead to genuine change rather than just punishment.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~wish":{"haiku":"A live performance tribute seeks realness in a world of artifice, the speaker casts faith aside while acknowledging everyone is trapped in emptiness.","opus":"A live cover of Trent Reznor's shrieking self-hatred and revulsion at a world of fakery, begging for anything genuine. The show's intro reframes it around disaster-relief charity, but the song itself is Reznor's snarling void — soulless, loathing everyone, decades deep on a road to damnation.","sonnet":"A live performance introduction of Nine Inch Nails' 'Wish,' notable for the spoken announcement about Red Cross fundraising from hurricane relief — the actual lyrical content is minimal, mainly an MC moment before the cover begins.","src":"haiku","fable":"On Broken (1992) the craving for authenticity and the contempt that answers it are a single violent motion, not two moods. The verses stage self-demolition: a narrator razes what he built, throws away his last possession, and names betrayed faith as the vilest thing he could commit. His plea for anything genuine keeps snagging on the accusatory you who saturates his entire horizon, so longing and sneer arrive as one exhaled breath. The bridge drops the mask, its stuttered admission of wanting escape yet being unable to move looping until the tirade reveals itself as a cage. Underneath the noise sits a man welded to the very thing he damns.","fv":2,"fnote":"A Nine Inch Nails cover Linkin Park carried through the Projekt Revolution summer of 2004 — this live cut was taped in Tampa that August and released on the LP Underground 4.0 fan-club disc. The read follows Reznor's original.","fnote2":"Shinoda has said he collected everything NIN put out — CDs, singles, remixes — and credits The Perfect Drug's leap with encouraging Linkin Park's own experimentation; the cover is that debt made audible.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"longing and contempt arrive as a single exhaled motion","built":"the bridge loops its trapped admission until the tirade reveals itself as a cage"},
"linkin-park~wish-live":{"haiku":"Suicidal despair and self-destruction after losing faith in love and truth.","opus":"A cover framing self-destruction as a spiral of dismantling everything you built, throwing away faith and love, and craving something 'real' in a world you find hollow while confessing you can want to turn back but can't.","sonnet":"A cover of Nine Inch Nails' 'Wish': the speaker, hollow at 26 and raging at a world full of insincerity, has thrown away every remaining anchor — faith, trust, pride — and is left wishing for something real while unable to stop or go back.","src":"haiku","fable":"On Broken (1992) the craving for authenticity and the contempt that answers it are a single violent motion, not two moods. The verses stage self-demolition: a narrator razes what he built, throws away his last possession, and names betrayed faith as the vilest thing he could commit. His plea for anything genuine keeps snagging on the accusatory you who saturates his entire horizon, so longing and sneer arrive as one exhaled breath. The bridge drops the mask, its stuttered admission of wanting escape yet being unable to move looping until the tirade reveals itself as a cage. Underneath the noise sits a man welded to the very thing he damns.","fv":2,"fnote":"A Nine Inch Nails cover Linkin Park carried through the Projekt Revolution summer of 2004 — this live cut was taped in Tampa that August and released on the LP Underground 4.0 fan-club disc. The read follows Reznor's original.","fnote2":"Shinoda has said he collected everything NIN put out — CDs, singles, remixes — and credits The Perfect Drug's leap with encouraging Linkin Park's own experimentation; the cover is that debt made audible.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"longing and contempt arrive as a single exhaled motion","built":"the bridge loops its trapped admission until the tirade reveals itself as a cage"},
"linkin-park~wish-live-projekt-revolution-2004":{"haiku":"Existential void and manufactured faith collapse into each other: reality feels unreal because trust itself has been corrupted beyond repair.","opus":"A live cover of Trent Reznor's howl of self-disgust and misplaced faith, performed at a relief benefit; the narrator throws it all away, despises everyone, and pleads for anything authentic in a landscape overrun by the person who betrayed him.","sonnet":"A live cover of Nine Inch Nails' 'Wish' — a song about nihilistic disgust with the world, the self, and everyone in it, wishing for something real and true in a reality that feels hollow and fake.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~with-you":{"fable":"An early Hybrid Theory cut whose meaning lives in a title that stays an unresolved pun: to be with someone can mean beside them or merely haunted by them, and the song insists on the second while pretending the first. Underneath the emo-rap surface of a remembered relationship, it stages a mind that can no longer tell presence from absence, the beloved gone yet perceptually inescapable, face and voice burned into memory so the loss registers as a haunting rather than a departure. The two verses split the register cruelly: one wakes into numb static and self-accusation, the other flashes back to mutual violence that Shinoda's narrator reflexively edits out of a past he refuses to call real, so the chorus is exposed as the trap it names, carrying someone precisely because you could neither keep nor survive them. Only Bennington's bridge reaches outward, and its ache to see tomorrow reads less as hope than as the exhausted wish to reach any point past this looping now.","fableDeep":"The title's preposition is the trick: 'with you' names a haunting, not a togetherness — the person is distant even when close, gone even when remembered, and the speaker is 'with' them only in replay. The feeling 'was promised by your face,' which is the song's sharpest idea: the emotion outlived its cause and is now owed by a memory. The violence verse ('I hit you and you hit me back, we fall to the floor') roughens the nostalgia — what's missed isn't peace but intensity — and the bridge's sudden 'I can't wait to see tomorrow' is the first breath of air in the whole song, wanting the future more than the ghost.","haiku":"Longing for an absent lover and memories haunting waking life.","opus":"Clinging to the memory of a lost, emotionally distant partner after a destructive relationship, unable to bring them back yet still hoping.","sonnet":"Holding onto the memory of someone emotionally distant, haunted by a troubled past together that can't be undone.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","nostalgia & memory","shame & repression"],"means":"presence indistinguishable from haunting, loss registering as perceptual loop","built":"title preposition left unresolved — beside or merely haunted by","fvr":"2.4"},
"linkin-park~wretches-and-kings":{"haiku":"Revolution against systemic oppression, with those in power grinding the vulnerable underfoot and a call for coordinated uprising.","opus":"A militant uprising anthem, opening with Mario Savio's famous speech about throwing your body upon the gears of the machine, then rallying the wretches to seize control from the people up top. It is class rebellion set to a heavy beat. Linkin Park, the California band, sharpened their sound toward electronic aggression on this record, channelling protest into a defiant, fist-raised call to arms.","sonnet":"Power and resistance framed through the machinery of control — those at the top push those at the bottom, and the song argues back with force, demanding that people put their bodies in the gears until the machine stops. The wretches and kings are coming for the structure itself, not just the individuals running it.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~wth-you-chairman-hahn-reanimation-feat-aceyalone":{"haiku":"A speaker haunted by memory of someone distant cannot bring them back despite proximity, trapped reliving mistakes. They grapple with control and refuse to let go of someone unavailable.","opus":"A remixed With You where Aceyalone's verses harden the original's grief over a fractured, push-and-pull relationship into a refusal to be controlled. Clinging and resisting at once, unwilling to let the person go or be owned.","sonnet":"A remix of With You — about a relationship where emotional distance persists even through physical closeness, waking up still haunted by someone, and the refusal to be controlled or begged into submission while still being unable to let go.","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~x-ecutioner-style-feat-black-thought":{"haiku":"Aggressive assertion of skill and dominance through hip-hop prowess, dismissing weak competition with violent lyrical combat.","opus":"A hard, boastful posse cut where a barked shut-up refrain frames a barrage of Philadelphia-flavored battle rhymes, the guest MC promising to rip tracks and squads apart with turntablist precision. It's mostly braggadocio and menace, no confession. The Roots' Black Thought lends the verse its heft, and the piece sits with Linkin Park's remix and collaboration output rather than their radio hits.","sonnet":"A pure display of mic confidence — Black Thought comes through establishing dominance via rapid, layered wordplay, positioning himself as someone who has been sharpened by the underground and can dismantle any crew without a bodyguard in sight. The track is less about a subject than about the act of rapping itself as a demonstration of force.","src":"haiku"},
"lionfight~heaviness":{"haiku":"The speaker feels alone and unable to articulate their feelings; they fear losing control if they surrender to their spiraling thoughts.","opus":"Lionfight portray a mind caving under its own weight, the narrator tallying the days he's clutched at a body that finds no rest. Convinced nobody grasps his pain and too afraid to even keep writing, he keeps circling the pull to surrender, then, dizzy and cornered, resolves to haul himself upright once more.","sonnet":"Mental weight accumulates until the question becomes whether letting go leads somewhere worse than staying. Isolation and the fear of being unreadable to anyone keep the narrator locked in a loop — standing again at the end not because anything resolved but because standing is what there is.","src":"haiku"},
"liotta-seoul~airplane":{"haiku":"Departure and separation ache while flying. The space between people becomes abyss—crying comes from knowing departure.","opus":"The surreal grief of leaving someone behind, weeping mid-flight over a best kiss now impossibly far, likening the self to a dying star as distance makes the loss unbearable.","sonnet":"A song about sobbing on a flight after leaving behind someone or somewhere that mattered — the narrator is restrained in a seat, overwhelmed by the physical fact of distance from a powerful connection, the situation described as bizarre and heartbreaking without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"liotta-seoul~want-you":{"haiku":"Haunting takes the form of dissolution and spectral devotion; the speaker pursues through death itself, refusing to release their grip.","opus":"Desire framed as dissolution — the singer fading, floating and rotting even as he vows to hold, smother and shadow the one he wants like a specter. A flicker within barely hangs on but isn't enough, so love becomes a ghostly clinging that outlives the body.","sonnet":"A speaker dissolving and drifting — fading, floating, decomposing — transforms longing into something posthumous: they will haunt the person they want as a ghost, embracing and suffocating them even after all air has left their lungs.","src":"haiku"},
"lip-critic~jackpot":{"haiku":"Survival through self-commodification and excess leaves the speaker hollow, addicted to the numb euphoria that numbs the cost of their own consumption.","opus":"A jittery electronic-punk plunge into addiction and self-ruin, framing the craving as a rigged gambling machine cranked by a fading, deathward impulse. Hotel dread and images of contraband stashed beneath the skin render the compulsion grotesque and cartoonish. Under the manic surge runs exhaustion, someone loathing himself enough to quit yet still chasing the rush.","sonnet":"Addiction and self-destruction described as a compulsive spin of the wheel — I still crave the feeling — with the speaker checking into a hotel room like a bet, their skin autopsied for hidden coins, and a swan dive into their own swan song the only jackpot in sight.","src":"haiku"},
"lip-critic~legs-in-a-snare":{"haiku":"Obsession traps the body as image; attachment spirals into self-erasure and silent breakdown.","opus":"A dependent lover feels himself losing his grip on someone whose interest is fading, and starts dismantling the shared life piece by piece, terrified he doesn't deserve to be kept. The grand gestures he offered were never enough to hold anyone in place.","sonnet":"A speaker caught in a consuming, unequal attachment — wings buckled, brakes failing, drawing crop circles in the grass to summon someone who already has them wrapped — eventually reaches the moment of falling off the roof and concludes, still present, still begging for what they don't deserve.","src":"haiku"},
"lisa~1":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"A bright, fizzy J-pop read on the maddening physics of attraction: wanting to close a one-centimeter gap that keeps repelling like magnets, the frustration of too much distance and too much restraint, ending in a truce and goodnight.","sonnet":"The agonizing one-centimeter gap between two people who want to be closer but keep magnetically repelling each other — mismatched timing and too much restraint make it impossible; sometimes knowing when to give up and say goodnight is the only solution, though they still want to get along.","src":"haiku"},
"lisa~a-13byr5f":{"opus":"LiSA rose through anime tie-ins to arena stardom, and this starting-line song draws on that hard-won ascent. The performer has failed, cried alone at night, blamed God for stumbling, yet keeps standing because she cannot stop loving the work, the crowd chanting her name coloring a blank canvas. The closing wish for forgiveness over the paths not taken gives the triumph its ache.","sonnet":"A performance anxiety song about a performer standing at the starting line: she's failed before, cried alone at night, blamed God for stumbling — but she always stood back up because she can't stop loving what she does, and the crowd calling her name adds color to a blank canvas; the final wish is to be forgiven for the paths she didn't take.","src":"sonnet"},
"lisa~a-145rj9s":{"opus":"LiSA's warmest register surfaces in this song of being healed by an unexpected arrival. Known for anthemic fire, she softens here to trace a narrator hunched and afraid of breaking things, unable to smile at kindness, until surprise turns even a cracked heart into a jewel box. The future they want to share becomes the door finally opened, gratitude standing in for grand declaration.","sonnet":"A song about being healed by someone's unexpected arrival: the narrator has been hunched, afraid of breaking things, unable to smile at kindness — but this person's surprise has turned even a cracked heart into a jewel box, and the future they want to see together becomes the door they finally open.","src":"sonnet"},
"lisa~a-3cd1tx":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"LiSA made her name on Demon Slayer's battlefield anthems, and this guiding-star song carries that scale of resolve. In a world of chaos and mutual destruction, a fixed star in the dark points toward the future while the narrator weeps, runs, and prays for light to reach someone. The vow to keep burning across blood-soaked terrain is the fighting-spirit ballad in its purest LiSA form.","sonnet":"In a world of chaos and mutual destruction, a guiding star in the dark sky points unmovably toward the future — the narrator weeps, runs forward, prays for light to reach someone, and promises to be a flame that continues burning even as the path runs through blood-soaked terrain.","src":"haiku"},
"lisa~a-3w2z":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"Parting at the height of a bond is a recurring LiSA subject, and her anime-forged sense of the epic shapes this farewell. The narrator vows to protect the other's wishes and burn on as a flame even as the cruel world piles up loss. Each fulfilled dream becomes a moment to remember the one left behind, the no-looking-back resolve carrying its own grief inside the momentum.","sonnet":"Two people part at the height of their bond — the narrator vows to protect the other's wishes and keep burning as a flame, even as the world is cruel and loss accumulates; each dream fulfilled will be a moment to remember the person left behind, with no looking back.","src":"haiku"},
"lisa~a-kosb70":{"haiku":"A selfish romantic demand to never be left alone—asking to be possessed, embraced, silenced, not woken from the dream.","opus":"LiSA rarely sounds as feverish as here, invoking Cait Sith, the illusion-fairy cat, to name herself as something between dream and reality. The physical demand to be held all night, bitten just enough, kept from dissolving, comes from a narrator who suspects she may be fantasy and asks to be believed in anyway. Her usual anthemic drive turns inward into pure, clinging want.","sonnet":"An intensely physical demand for a lover who will hold on all night, stop her from leaving, bite her just hard enough, and never let the dream go cold — she invokes 'Cait Sith' (the fairy cat of illusion) to describe herself as something between dream and reality that needs to be kept from dissolving. The emotional core is a person who knows she may be fantasy and asks to be believed in anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"lisa~a-kqhk2g":{"opus":"This is LiSA in full uplift mode, the register that made her anime openings feel like sunrise. The declaration that dreams however absurd are genuinely possible leans on shared feeling, a crowd drawing one huge arc together because life happens once and passion needs no defense. Even the wildest ambitions are treated as sincerely, laughably real, the optimism deliberately too big to be ironic.","sonnet":"An explosively optimistic declaration that dreams, however absurd, are genuinely possible — life is once, passion needs no justification, people united in shared feeling can draw a huge arc together, and even the wildest ambitions are sincerely, laughably real.","src":"sonnet"},
"lisa~adamas":{"haiku":"A warrior vows to transform suffering and despair into crystallized resolve; burning through hardship to achieve glory and revolution.","opus":"A defiant anthem of forging one's own glory through suffering, wielding a diamond sword crystallized from sorrow and hope; the deeper read is that pain and tears are compressed into unbreakable strength—rising again no matter how often you fall, each prayer becoming proof of a personal revolution as you burn yourself out pursuing your goal.","sonnet":"A Japanese anime-tied anthem about forging resilience from accumulated tears and mistakes — the narrator treats their suffering like a diamond being cut, a shining sword crystallized from sorrow and hope, swearing to keep rising and leading their people toward light.","src":"haiku"},
"lisa~dawn":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts existential questions about meaning and sincerity while navigating day and night, loss and hope, searching for authentic purpose.","opus":"A resolve to meet a new era honestly, inhaling joy and exhaling anxiety while searching endlessly for the meaning of being kept alive; even if everything resets to zero or one is left utterly alone, living sincerely is presented as the path to strength, and the narrator vows never to regret or run as she walks toward the dawn.","sonnet":"A Japanese song about persisting through doubt and failure toward a new beginning — the speaker questions whether their resolve is genuine, processes grief and loneliness, and ultimately chooses to keep walking toward the light rather than retreat.","src":"haiku"},
"lisa~from-the-first-take":{"haiku":"Shared resilience through departure—travelers burn bright meeting briefly; passing warmth and pain sidelong as they part toward different futures; strength grows through the act of remembering each other.","opus":"A survivor promises a dying loved one to carry their unfinished dreams forward, refusing to be swallowed by grief because letting the pain go numb would betray what the departed wanted. Written as the theme to Demon Slayer's Mugen Train, it turns one death into a vow to keep living and fighting.","sonnet":"A song about a parting between two people who burned brightly together: they had to release each other for the sake of their separate futures, but the narrator vows that every dream fulfilled will remind them of this person — grief and gratitude held together in a promise to grow strong enough to honor what they shared.","src":"haiku"},
"lisa~gurenge":{"haiku":"Determination to become stronger through pain, the speaker honours sorrow as a teacher while burning with resolve to transcend fate's fire.","opus":"Gratitude even for sorrow as the fuel for becoming strong, understanding the meaning of defeat after being crushed by the world. Written as Demon Slayer's theme, it urges the crimson flower to bloom defiantly and light the way through a thorn-covered path, changing oneself for someone else's sake.","sonnet":"A declaration of resolve forged by grief — the speaker learned why they needed to grow stronger precisely through suffering, and now that strength is dedicated to someone else's survival, the crimson flower blooming as a symbol of hard-won purpose in the face of devastation.","src":"haiku"},
"lisa~produced-by-ayase":{"haiku":"Long-distance love sustained through small moments and seasonal hope, waiting for reunion across distance.","opus":"A tender song of separation and longing across distance, holding onto a loved one through snowfall and shared sky, waiting for spring to reunite. It aches with the wish that ordinary days together could simply continue.","sonnet":"A long-distance love song set in winter: the narrator watches snowfall and thinks only of a person far away, hoping they see the same sky, replaying their voice saying \"see you later\" over and over — the song is patient rather than desperate, sustained by the belief that spring will eventually bring them back to the same town.","src":"haiku"},
"lisa~propaganda":{"haiku":"Resisting lies and dark manipulation, people must save themselves from evil within; united action defeats propaganda and exploitation.","opus":"A rallying cry against manufactured hatred circulating worldwide, urging listeners to think for themselves rather than swallow deception and to unite against the evils festering within. LiSA, a Japanese anisong vocalist known for high-energy anime tie-ins, drives the bilingual lyric with characteristic defiance, blending English slogans and Japanese exhortations into a plea for solidarity over fear.","sonnet":"Against a world saturated in manipulation and manufactured hate, a voice rallies others to resist collective deception. Strength lies in solidarity — crying out together, refusing to be led alone into the dark, and trusting individual discernment over the noise of propaganda.","src":"haiku"},
"little-boots~stuck-on-repeat":{"haiku":"A person feels trapped in cycles of attraction and desire they cannot break free from, repeatedly trying to escape but being pulled back. The force keeping them caught oscillates between internal longing and external pull.","opus":"A narrator is caught in an inescapable loop by someone who keeps pulling her back to the start whenever she nearly breaks free. Little Boots, a figure of the late-2000s British synth-pop revival, mirrors the lyric's compulsion in its cyclical structure, casting infatuation as a heartbeat stuck skipping to a beat she cannot resist.","sonnet":"Romantic fixation behaves like a malfunctioning record — every attempt to break free triggers an invisible mechanism that drags the speaker back to the beginning. The obsession is rendered physical, almost mechanical, as though the body itself has been rewired to orbit one particular person.","src":"haiku"},
"little-dragon~constant-surprises":{"haiku":"Synchronicity and intuitive connection dissolve boundaries between self and other; fate orchestrates meetings and understanding transcends rational explanation.","opus":"A dreamlike trust in synchronicity, reading coincidence as fate and feeling secretly connected to strangers and higher forces. It's about choosing wonder over rational explanation, a life built on being open to signs.","sonnet":"A speaker interprets synchronicities — meaningful glances from trees, telepathic moments with strangers, dreams shared with someone absent — as evidence that life is structured by fate rather than coincidence, and surrenders gratefully to that pattern.","src":"haiku"},
"little-dragon~feather":{"haiku":"Freedom and ascension require rebellion against captivity; the speaker chooses weightlessness and flight over constraint, embracing transformation over stasis.","opus":"A yearning to escape confinement and take flight, choosing wildness over being someone's captive daughter or possession. The airborne imagery is about longing for freedom and wondering whether it will ever actually lift you.","sonnet":"A declaration of fierce autonomy — preferring the outlaw's freedom over a daughter's captivity — framed as an ascent, a chase of something airborne and silver, done entirely for the sake of a feathered, elusive ideal.","src":"haiku"},
"live~iris":{"haiku":"Sensory beauty becomes corrupted by sentimental attachment and false love, trapping the speaker in armies of their own creation that perpetually return to destroy what once felt pure.","opus":"A disturbing confession of the pleasure taken in domination, then the self-deception of dressing that up as love. The narrator admits the violence for what it is, aware the impulse keeps invading him no matter how he sentimentalizes it.","sonnet":"A speaker is haunted by the image of tenderness — a hand on a loved one's head — that the sentimental mind has twisted into something it calls love but is not. The beauty of that vision persists against his will, while armies of inner forces continue to bait and corrupt it.","src":"haiku"},
"live~white-discussion":{"haiku":"Political debate deteriorates into meaningless discourse while sanity and connection crumble under the weight of rhetoric.","opus":"Rage at empty political talk, two sides trading slogans while sanity and the planet run out, all discourse dead beyond destruction. Nineties earnestness aimed at the futility of arguing as the clock runs down on everything.","sonnet":"Two people talk past each other — one about freedom, one about flags — in a politically correct but entirely useless exchange, and the song predicts that when everything ends they will weep together over the futility of all the noise. Discussion as substitute for action, eloquence as the enemy of change.","src":"haiku"},
"living-colour~cult-of-personality":{"haiku":"Charismatic leaders and their cults seduce the masses through personality and mirrored desires; they exploit you while you love them, exploiting faith into exploitation. Musolini, Kennedy, Stalin—all peddlers of false salvation.","opus":"A funk-metal dissection of charismatic power, voiced by the seductive demagogue himself who invokes tyrants and heroes alike, exposing how leaders exploit our anger and dreams while reminding us the power to be free was always ours.","sonnet":"A direct diagnosis of how charismatic power works — Stalin, Kennedy, Gandhi, all functioning the same way: the leader exploits your need, tells you one and one makes three, and you love them for it even as they hollow you out.","src":"haiku"},
"llnn~imperial":{"opus":"A warning against nationalist demagogues who spin false myths of past glory to march the masses to war as expendable cannon fodder, indicting the apathy that lets tyrants rise.","sonnet":"A condemnation of nationalist demagogues who weaponize fabricated histories of past greatness to send ordinary people to die for them, warning that apathy toward these tyrants is what gives them power.","src":"sonnet"},
"loathe~aggressive-evolution":{"haiku":"Two consciousness intertwine and collide, seeking connection across different worlds while wrestling with panic and blindness to each other's reality.","opus":"A narrator oscillates between confrontation and escape, slipping into someone else's consciousness as two realities crash together on shimmering nighttime streets. Loathe, the Liverpool band blending metalcore heft with shoegaze atmosphere, render a frantic, dreamlike fusion of two selves, unsure whether the other person will ever perceive what the speaker does.","sonnet":"Two consciousnesses collide and intertwine across a liminal landscape of moonlit streets and shaking vehicles, struggling toward a shared perception that may never fully align. The anxiety of connection — the fear that the other will not see what the speaker sees — drives each verse toward an inconclusive, charged meeting.","src":"haiku"},
"loathe~gored":{"haiku":"A person is trapped by a merciless, all-consuming force and helplessly dragged toward their own destruction.","opus":"Loathe stage a claustrophobic horror in which some unstoppable malevolence seeps into you and pins you in decay with no exit. The real subject is absolute powerlessness, the sinking recognition of doom as you drop through a bottomless descent with nowhere to flee.","sonnet":"About being overwhelmed by something malevolent and irresistible — a force that moves through you and cannot be torn out, leaving you consumed, spiraling, and finally clenched in its jaws.","src":"haiku"},
"loathe~i-let-it-in-and-it-took-everything":{"haiku":"After opening a door, the speaker flees from shock and fear into darkness; a damned climate and whispered doubt strip meaning from existence.","opus":"Loathe, the British band who welded Deftones-style atmosphere to crushing heaviness on their acclaimed album of the same name, dramatize a mind broken into and hollowed out. A door left ajar admits shadows that murmur uncertainty into him and melt visions into the ground until meaning evaporates. The haunted refrain about another person reciting his own thoughts back to him turns intimacy into a kind of vanishing.","sonnet":"Something was let in through an open door and claimed total occupancy — a presence that whispers doubt, manufactures enemies, and dissolves meaning until only hallucinations remain. The repeated refrain of being seen while vanishing captures the horror of losing selfhood to whatever entered.","src":"haiku"},
"loathe~is-it-really-you":{"haiku":"Two people colliding and wounding each other in a brief stellar encounter, caught in currents of pain and silence.","opus":"Two people face private pain, colliding briefly like stars while searching the sky together amid crushing silence and bleeding out. Loathe, the Liverpool band whose 2020 record fused shoegaze textures with metallic heaviness, works here in their gentler register, an atmospheric meditation on shared grief and the uncertainty of whether a loved one is truly still there.","sonnet":"Two people orbit each other across uncertainty, pain shared and questioned in equal measure. A brief, weightless collision — like stars briefly merging — gives way to something heavier: the slow bleed of unresolved feeling and the disorienting wonder of whether the other person was ever truly present.","src":"haiku"},
"loathe~it-s-yours":{"haiku":"Worship of a primal sinful force; addiction to power and command. The speaker claims chosen status and warns others they are misguided, asserting dominance over victims.","opus":"Loathe build a sinister rite of base transgression and appetite, the narrator crowning himself the anointed, invincible and beyond reach. Devotion gets etched into flesh and commands obeyed as the craving deepens, never sated, while between the threats a wounded recollection breaks through, wondering whether the other will ever acknowledge the harm they caused before their reckoning arrives.","sonnet":"A cult logic unfolds — claiming what is yours means following in footsteps carved into foreheads, worshipping a primal sin that devours all thought under absolute power. The narrator oscillates between submission and the declaration of being unstoppable, consumed and dominant at once.","src":"haiku"},
"loathe~new-faces-in-the-dark":{"haiku":"Hatred and control cycle through relationships; one person poisons another with manipulation, erasing their identity while they tremble between love and fear.","opus":"The British band Loathe, known for fusing metallic heaviness with shoegaze atmosphere, trace a haunting cycle of shifting identity. A plea for help twists into aggression, a starved spirit reaches for warmth it never finds, and each time the person's face seems to change the same ruin repeats. A looping request for forgiveness runs through it like a wound that keeps reopening.","sonnet":"A household shaped by fear and violence — someone whose cruelty forces those around them to navigate life in terrified orbit. A figure, possibly a child or partner, absorbs the weight of an abuser's torment until their old face is erased and a new one forged in the dark takes its place.","src":"haiku"},
"loathe~screaming":{"haiku":"Isolation and loss of voice render someone voiceless in tragedy; they scream silently, burned by distance they cannot cross.","opus":"Loathe pour their blend of shoegaze and metalcore into a study of isolation, voicing someone drowning in noise, voiceless and without hope yet howling on the inside. Separation and departure keep reopening like an original wound, while he resists any easy view that life splits neatly into good and bad.","sonnet":"Lost inside deafening noise and emotional distance, someone finds their voice has vanished even as the need to be heard grows unbearable. The memory of a first raw scream of pain defines how all subsequent leaving feels — and a final gesture of writing through the stars reaches toward connection across the silence.","src":"haiku"},
"loathe~two-way-mirror":{"haiku":"Two people mirror each other's doubts and struggles, seeking faith neither can find. The song explores shared internal conflict and the question of whether admitting uncertainty can anchor them both.","opus":"A quiet, intimate track about two lovers who see themselves reflected in each other and share the same weaknesses, reaching together for something like faith. Its emotional center is a plea to stay and a simple question about what a person might risk if fear were removed, casting love as an act of courage.","sonnet":"About the eerie emotional mirroring between two people — sharing the same flaws and desires, reflecting each other's inner states — and the fragile hope that simply saying \"I know\" is enough to keep someone present.","src":"haiku"},
"loathe~white-hot":{"haiku":"Ecstatic transcendence through movement and colors swirling in rhythm, culminating in sacrifice and destruction of imposed structures.","opus":"A narrator feels like an intruder in a life others constructed, drifting between rapturous surrender and a quiet promise that the ornate structures around him will fall by his own doing. Loathe pair atmospheric heaviness with imagery of ecstasy and ruin, letting bliss curdle into menace once everything he cherished is already gone.","sonnet":"An outsider, excluded from a gilded world built by others, moves from dissociation through ecstatic dissolution and arrives at cold intent. The colours that swirl in collective rhythm eventually give way to a vow of destruction aimed at the house that never opened its doors.","src":"haiku"},
"local-natives~breakers":{"haiku":"Depression disguises itself as normalcy while eating away from within; the narrator struggles to convince others—or themselves—that nothing is wrong.","opus":"The static of low-grade depression, insisting nothing's wrong while unable to believe it, hoping to hear your own okayness confirmed by someone else. The plea to just let it happen and stop overthinking is the whole struggle.","sonnet":"Someone in the grip of low-grade depression or dissociation describes the blunted numbness of daily life — the cold cereal, the TV, the color draining from their eyes — and tries to convince themselves that nothing is actually wrong while suspecting they need someone else to confirm it.","src":"haiku"},
"local-natives~mt-washington":{"haiku":"Something cannot be buried or washed away—one hides from the other repeatedly, refusing to look or connect.","opus":"A tender, aching song about a relationship coming apart, one voice repeating that they don't need to face the other while trying to bury something that won't stay dead. Local Natives, an LA indie band known for rich harmonies, turn that stubborn refusal into a quietly crushing refrain, grief circling a fading summer romance and a collapsing world.","sonnet":"A refusal to be seen, cycling into obsession — one party keeps sending the same cold message while the other keeps digging at something that refuses to stay buried. The song asks whether the dirt can be washed off their hands and the thing kept from surfacing, but the question repeats without resolution, the face on the ceiling and the endless dismissal becoming one locked loop.","src":"haiku"},
"local-natives~shape-shifter":{"haiku":"Psychological fracture and flux prevent cohesion; the speaker questions whether identity persists or simply transforms.","opus":"The instability of identity and mood, a self that hallucinates and slips its own shape until mirrors won't hold a face. It addresses a volatile person, killer and friend at once, and by extension the shifting self inside.","sonnet":"Someone addresses a person with extreme and frightening mood swings — bowing in submission one moment, watching them laugh like a child the next — questioning how they share a language and why the soul must hallucinate to maintain itself alongside someone so volatile. The inability to be reflected in mirrors hints at the other's fundamental unknowability.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~a-kick-right-there":{"haiku":"Criticizes performative insincerity and constant disappointment, tired of life's rejections and hollow comfort. Wants release from perpetual letdown.","opus":"A frustrated broadside at someone dishonest and self-defeating, the narrator exhausted by seeing the world differently and wishing for death when every chance lands like a kick to the groin, resenting a false helping hand. Lock the Basement work in an abrasive hardcore-punk register, and this track channels burnout and disgust into blunt, snarling contempt for a person wasting their own life and his patience.","sonnet":"The quiet violence of being perpetually dismissed — the other person never says what they mean, wastes their time, and then complains too late. The speaker admits to exhaustion with seeing differently from everyone else and a darker flicker underneath: every wasted chance lands like a blow, and staying alive inside a false light others project feels increasingly impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~come-back-to-life":{"haiku":"Digital resurrection and eternal imprisonment in a virtual machine replace physical death. The song depicts souls trapped in software, forced into endless cycles of reanimation.","opus":"The song imagines copying a soul into a machine to force eternal, infinite life, indifferent to whether the subject can breathe or see. It frames this digital resurrection as cold and coercive, like software prompting to continue.","sonnet":"The song presents the idea of digital resurrection: a consciousness is copied and trapped inside a machine against its will, forced into endless, disembodied existence even though it can no longer breathe or see. The narrator is indifferent to the subject's suffering, insisting it keep returning to life inside the system.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~concrete":{"haiku":"Laments numbness toward inherited privilege and the repetitive drudgery of existence, wishing for oblivion. Childhood innocence lost to artificial cycles.","opus":"A numb study of depression and hollow comfort, the narrator wishing he could stay unconscious, unable to take pleasure in anything handed to him, glimpsing the child smothered under adult conditioning and mindless routine. Lock the Basement bring bleak hardcore intensity, their fractured imagery sketching a life worn down to mere existence with no clean way out.","sonnet":"A numb morning after numb morning — the speaker wishes they hadn't woken up, can't enjoy anything real because everything feels artificial, and sees the street as full of people just passing through existence without direction. The image of a child who needs saving flickers through without resolution, and the whole thing collapses back into the cycle: wake, work, repeat, die a little.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~feed-our-lie":{"haiku":"Society demands achievement and competition to justify false lives; individual success feeds collective delusions of importance and worth.","opus":"A metalcore protest against a culture that reduces worth to numbers and results, siphoning a person's light to prop up hollow pedestals. The spoken passage names it plainly: dysfunction excused as long as you achieve what the system decided matters, real lives never actually lived.","sonnet":"About institutional exploitation of high achievers — a system that can't shine on its own feeds off the light of those who can, using their accomplishments to justify its own empty, never-lived existence.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~go-with-the-flow":{"haiku":"The speaker drifts through years without direction, avoiding commitment or meaningful action despite recognizing time wasted.","opus":"A drifting, disaffected reflection on wasted youth and years that blur past like months, drinking to avoid thinking. The refrain of going with the flow captures aimless passivity and alienation, doing the same hated thing daily while insisting it's time well spent.","sonnet":"Drifting through a decade with nothing to show for it — the narrator has been numbing out with alcohol, going wherever life pushes, and suddenly notices ten years have evaporated while everyone else was following a path they couldn't bring themselves to take.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~green":{"haiku":"Isolation and forward momentum leave no room for companionship or genuine connection. The speaker achieves clarity knowing the only path leads to a dead end alone.","opus":"A caustic hardcore reckoning with driven ambition and the solitude it breeds. The narrator, fixated only on climbing, reaches the summit alone, briefly glimpsing other lives before deciding his path is fixed and finished. Around it runs the ache of meaning nothing to someone and letting her go, growing wearily numb as he keeps confirming his own bleak predictions.","sonnet":"The particular grief of wanting something that was never meant for you — memory borrowed on loan, a deadend visible from every direction, the person who always pushed ahead alone reaching the top and finding nothing. The sharpest pain is being nothing to her and letting her go, and with age the speaker has learned to forget more easily, though the clarity of knowing the way leads nowhere never quite fades.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~loading-your-personal-twat":{"haiku":"Modern life chains people to monotonous routines of work and consumption that numb sensation and meaning. The speaker describes existence as loading the same soulless cycles—paying, waiting, breeding—until extinction feels preferable.","opus":"A numb indictment of a life reduced to routine transactions, where even sex has become as joyless as an errand and comfort has killed all desire. The bleak punchline is a whole generation quietly counting down to a wished-for extinction.","sonnet":"A bleak portrait of modern male alienation — the narrator fills his days with ATMs, joyless pay-per-sex, and the mechanical rhythm of consumption, having lost any memory of genuine desire or human contact, drifting toward a desired extinction.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~no-revolt":{"haiku":"A critique of complicit servitude: people willingly forge their own chains and labor within assembly lines, too depleted or afraid to rebel against the systems that exploit them.","opus":"Lock the Basement satirize exploitation by giving the microphone to the exploiter, a boss barking at workers to build their own shackles and fund the very scarcity that traps them. He rebrands cruelty as shrewd enterprise and expects gratitude for a place in the machine, his smug venom exposing how oppression justifies itself from the top down.","sonnet":"A power structure addresses its subjects in the language of complicity: the chains workers wear are ones they fashioned themselves, the gratitude expected is for the privilege of serving. The voice of authority never raises itself above a patronising murmur, and the final command — no revolt — makes explicit what the entire preceding performance was designed to prevent anyone from noticing.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~second-class-human":{"haiku":"A manifesto for genetic eugenics and societal purge framed as progress; 'inferior' humans are dismissed as obsolete and condemned to be erased to make way for a superior breed.","opus":"A cold satire of eugenic thinking, sung in the voice of an engineered future that files the unimproved away as obsolete stock. The blunt, sloganeering delivery is the point: it makes progress sound exactly like contempt.","sonnet":"A eugenicist manifesto delivered as metal — a voice declares the obsolete must be destroyed for evolutionary progress, the \"second class\" genetically inferior and deserving subjugation and termination to make way for a superior future breed.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~shag-inc":{"haiku":"A dissatisfied customer demands human service and authenticity instead of cold automation; they've suffered ten years and now reject manipulation.","opus":"Lock the Basement stage a darkly funny scene of a lonely man snapping at an automated service, demanding real warmth from a sterile machine. A decade of bad days and a failed marriage collapse into a paid encounter with a virtual companion, and the sketch ends by selling artificial intimacy as tidier than the human kind, half satire and half loneliness.","sonnet":"A man navigating automated customer service for a commercial sex platform vents his contempt for muzak, cold menus, and scripted voices — then a phone call arrives from a friend cheerfully recommending the software instead of the messy reality of actual women. Satire of loneliness and its clean digital substitutes.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~stuck":{"haiku":"The narrator feels trapped between instinct and reason, emotional stagnation, and lost direction, watching time slip away while unable to truly connect with others.","opus":"A portrait of paralysis and emotional numbness, the narrator drifting steadily further from everyone with no way back, frozen between gut and logic in a fogged, deadened headspace. He gazes into a lover's eyes and registers only one more person to erase, dimly aware his time is draining away before he can grasp it.","sonnet":"About being paralyzed between instinct and reason, burning bridges while standing still, too disconnected from yourself to move forward and too aware of the passage of time to stay comfortable in the stasis.","src":"haiku"},
"lock-the-basement~wasted":{"opus":"A critique of people who merely survive rather than live, wasting their time pretending to like what authority (parents, society) tells them to, and pressuring others to fall in line; the singer laments a life withering away by its own resigned choice.","sonnet":"A critique of passive, directionless living — surviving instead of actually living, following what others expect, slowly withering by choice — aimed at someone too comfortable and afraid to break from the herd.","src":"sonnet"},
"lock-the-basement~will-of-god":{"opus":"A caustic assault on religious hypocrisy and imposed suffering, skewering those who invoke divine will to justify cruelty, greed, and prolonged agony. It condemns a faith that forbids mercy while claiming absolute truth by hearsay, indicting self-righteous believers who sleep soundly while others bear their decaying cross.","sonnet":"A critique of religious ideology used to justify keeping terminally ill people alive against their will — the song attacks the doctrine that prolongs suffering in the name of God, calling it inflicted pain that serves the conscience of the faithful at the expense of the dying person's suffering.","src":"sonnet"},
"locket~sleepwalker":{"opus":"An identity dissolving inside a relationship where the narrator reshaped himself into someone else to be loved; the wider metaphor is everyone drifting through life half-asleep, unable to tell dream from reality or who's to blame when no one wakes up.","sonnet":"The unsettling feeling of having reshaped yourself to fit someone else until you no longer recognize yourself — the song broadens this into a collective condition where everyone sleepwalks through life performing identities that aren't really their own.","src":"sonnet"},
"lokust~guiltless":{"haiku":"Violence runs through inherited bloodlines as the speaker rejects moral redemption; contempt replaces empathy in a calculated embrace of primal destruction.","opus":"A voice that believes it was forged for violence refuses to repent, treating cruelty as inheritance rather than sin. It exposes the hypocrisy of those who dress up their own bloodlust as morality while demanding penance from others.","sonnet":"The speaker, built from ancestral bloodshed and raised on inherited hatred, refuses atonement — the gods are dead but their contempt lives in the bones — and channels primal violence into a defiant ritual, calling others to drink from that blood and share in the infamy, standing dead to a world soaked in collective guilt.","src":"haiku"},
"lolita-komplex~we-re-all-dead":{"haiku":"Existential deadness disguised as continuance; society as collective burial with consciousness denied.","opus":"A gleefully nihilistic metalcore chant built on the premise that everyone is already dead and just hasn't realized it. Between the mosh-ready call-and-response and apocalyptic thunder sits a thread of loss, memory dragging the narrator toward someone gone. It weaponizes doom into catharsis, turning mortality into a communal, fist-in-the-air release.","sonnet":"Mass mortality as open secret: the clock stopped ticking for everyone but no one has registered it yet. Dead men walking persist through closed eyes and faded memories, the thunderstorm closing in on a crowd that keeps shouting yeah at the announcement of its own death.","src":"haiku"},
"lone-kodiak~inner-monologue":{"haiku":"Internal criticism curdles into suicidal ideation; the mind wages war against the self with relentless judgment and the desire to cease existing.","opus":"A raw depiction of the vicious internal critic, looping the same suicidal thoughts a performer mutters to himself in the mirror before a show. The gap between wanting to be a fan of his own life and wanting to end it captures a specific despair. It's unflinching about the cruelty of self-talk, the loudest voice being one's own.","sonnet":"The brutal internal critic — the fucking critic that is way too loud — wages its fight in front of the mirror, the speaker talking themselves into surviving another day and making it to the show through sheer exhaustion with the kinda shit we say to ourselves.","src":"haiku"},
"longsleeve-and-the-weirdboys~childish":{"haiku":"Nostalgic rejection of adult responsibility appeals to arrested youth; simple pleasures carry more weight than money or respectability.","opus":"An adult curdling under the weight of money and grown-up expectations begs to return to childhood, when the stakes were rollerblades and playground crushes instead of debt. The refusal to grow up is half rebellion, half grief.","sonnet":"A longing for pre-adolescent life — rollerblade injuries, Yu-Gi-Oh cards, truth-or-dare in the park, the dopamine of a first kiss — frames a rejection of adult cash-driven existence, where getting a boner from holding hands was currency enough.","src":"haiku"},
"loom~hate":{"haiku":"Internalized anger that cannot be directed outward so turns inward as self-hatred; accumulated wounds from past slights now manifest as pervasive rage.","opus":"A raw anatomy of resentment turning inward, holding grudges for every slight while the target has forgotten they ever wounded you. Unable to hate the person, you hate yourself instead, and the hate corrodes your own mind, regression looping back on itself.","sonnet":"About being unable to direct justified rage at someone who wronged you and turning it inward instead — the narrator remembers every slight, and because they can't hate the person responsible, the hate collapses back onto themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"lorde~400-lux":{"haiku":"Hollow youth kills time in transit, searching for meaning through intimacy while recognising their lives lack substance to fill the void.","opus":"Suburban adolescence idealized in real time, killing empty hours with someone in a car on unchanging streets, boredom transfigured into intimacy. Lorde finds tenderness in the smallest gestures, the orange juice, the car left running, the wish that they'd stay.","sonnet":"Two young people drift through the suburban afternoon in a car — hollow but content, not quite fulfilled but choosing each other's company over anything more demanding, finding something close to happiness in doing nothing together.","src":"haiku"},
"lorde~liability":{"haiku":"Self-aware acknowledgment of being too intense and destructive to maintain relationships.","opus":"The pop star's self-portrait as someone too intense to keep, retreating into the only love she hasn't ruined, herself. It names the specific loneliness of being consumed and then discarded as a toy once the novelty fades.","sonnet":"A speaker deemed too intense, too much, is rejected by lovers who find her overwhelming — she retreats to loving herself as the only constant relationship, then bitterly accepts the isolation that comes from being a toy people discard once the novelty wears off.","src":"haiku"},
"lorde~what-was-that":{"haiku":"Intoxicated connection burns intensely then shatters, leaving permanent haunting beneath subsequent relationships. The speaker remains fixated on an irreplaceable moment that cannot be recaptured.","opus":"A woman alone in a fresh apartment after a split is blindsided by memories of a drug-soaked first love she poured herself into from the age of seventeen, unable to define what it was or let it move on. The euphoric peaks only sharpen how badly the relationship fell short of enough.","sonnet":"A post-breakup reckoning with an intensity that refuses to fade: MDMA in the back garden, kissing for hours, the best cigarette of a life — the speaker alone in a new city, covering mirrors, haunted by the other person even at a show, trying to let whatever needs to pass through her pass, but knowing it might not let go.","src":"haiku"},
"lorn~weigh-me-down":{"opus":"The crushing weight of a destructive relationship or burden dragging the narrator down; battered, half-blind, and lied to, he keeps enduring and faking his way through, knowing it won't be long before he breaks.","sonnet":"A person crushed under the weight of an abusive or addictive relationship — they'll take the drugs, survive the knives being sharpened, and fake wellness with lies, but they know the weight of this person will eventually break them.","src":"sonnet"},
"losers~chainsaw":{"haiku":"Betrayal synchronizes destruction as two people dismantle each other with equal ferocity, each poison feeding the other's demise. Trust becomes the weapon that cuts both ways.","opus":"A tightly wound song about a mutually destructive relationship, two people poisoning each other while claiming to be built on trust. The chainsaw image makes the reciprocal harm literal: synchronized blades killing them both. It reaches for the moment of trying to salvage a self from wreckage, cutting free the memory of someone who inflicted lasting damage.","sonnet":"A mutually toxic relationship as synchronized destruction — both sides poisoning each other in lockstep, \"chainsaws\" standing for the synchronized violence of two people built to rust together, unable to escape the killing-and-destroying loop they've become.","src":"haiku"},
"loser~i-no-longer-feel-anything-at-all":{"haiku":"Emotional deadening and dissociation follow loss and betrayal, leaving the speaker numb and unable to reconnect with genuine feeling.","opus":"Numbness after being watched fall apart by someone who enjoyed the collapse: complete in their arms yet feeling nothing at all. The push and pull between wanting to be shown the way and being kept waiting leaves only the compulsion to crash.","sonnet":"About emotional shutdown after being abandoned and used — the narrator has been lost for so long that feeling is gone entirely; they're stuck staring at the wall, convinced the other person would enjoy watching them fall apart, while numbness replaces everything.","src":"haiku"},
"loser~pump-fake":{"haiku":"Authentic artistic creation versus trend-chasing imitation. The speaker criticizes imitators who bite their style while they remain grounded in genuine expression.","opus":"The rap-rock outfit turns creative authenticity into a taunt at imitators. Stuck in his head and in a rut, the narrator watches everyone who once doubted him now copy his look and talk. Art, he insists, cannot be forced: he built his while others bought theirs, and he watches trend-hoppers drown trying to ride his wave. The pump-fake refrain calls out style-jacking pretenders, daring the hungry copycats to bite while he stays right where he is.","sonnet":"An artist who built his craft without shortcuts watches trend-followers imitate the surface of what he made. The rage is quiet and diagnostic: authenticity cannot be purchased or faked for long, and those who tried to ride his wave will drown while he remains unchanged, still here.","src":"haiku"},
"lost-in-hollywood~the-beauty-of-death":{"haiku":"Mortality's grace emerges as the speaker accepts separation through death, transforming loss into aesthetic beauty and promise of reunion beneath the earth.","opus":"Someone releases a dying loved one, reframing death not as loss but as a beautiful passage they will meet again beyond the grave. The comfort and the anguish of letting go are held in the same breath.","sonnet":"A grief address to someone who has died: the speaker holds on — \"can't you stay a little longer, can't you say another word\" — then releases them into a world without fear, promising to remember and to meet again under the earth while standing in the rain, broken, watching them go.","src":"haiku"},
"lost-in-kiev~narcosis":{"haiku":"Sensory absorption in deep void; a body lost as a star in infinite darkness.","opus":"A cinematic post-rock descent into dissolution, consciousness coming untied and the body turning into a stray point of light adrift in infinity. Sparse, dreamlike imagery — a void swallowing the senses, roots wrenched from the ground — evokes the drug's floating detachment from reality. It reaches for the eerie beauty of losing every anchor at once.","sonnet":"A narcotic-dream dissolution — pupils retracted, black hole consuming senses, the body a lost star floating parallel to the floor while roots are torn from the earth all around. The voice from the other side is oneiric, an echo of a lost memory, and the whole lyric exists in that amniotic mist between sleep and erasure.","src":"haiku"},
"lost-in-separation~drowning":{"haiku":"A person trapped in self-constructed suffering dreams of drowning in darkness just to feel alive again, haunted by nightmares and the belief they betrayed God. They wear a public mask while wolves of internal torment close in, seeking escape from the pain that has become their only home.","opus":"Lost in Separation frame torment as a devil's bargain that fails. Someone has swapped his agony for a false godhood, imprisoned by a lover, so numb he craves oblivion just to register any sensation. Dreading rest and its horrors, he learns that outwitting the divine buys no real reprieve, only predators lying in wait.","sonnet":"A man trapped between numbness and nightmare craves sensation so desperately that drowning in darkness becomes preferable to feeling nothing at all. Past choices — some dishonest, some self-destructive — loom as accusations while the present offers only cold distance. The wolves he allowed in now feel like the only force that makes him feel alive.","src":"haiku"},
"lost-in-separation~persepolis":{"haiku":"War machinery and moral compromise reduce humans to expendable resources as the speaker questions whether divine mercy awaits or whether accountability has already failed.","opus":"A furious indictment of humanity's endless appetite for war, raising sons only to feed them to the machine while praying to a heaven that isn't listening. The real devil is the murderer begging mercy on judgment day.","sonnet":"An indictment of the war machine that has enslaved generation after generation: greedy hands serve fools and thieves, heaven is empty of listeners, and the guilty conscience of those who send others to die cannot be eased by prayer — the murderers were the devil all along.","src":"haiku"},
"lost-outrider~bodybag":{"haiku":"Beauty and death intertwine as seduction; the speaker manifests as predatory and irresistible, combining allure with threat.","opus":"Predatory bravado collapses into self-loathing: beneath the aggressive posturing about dominance and appetite runs an ache of feeling undeserving of the person desired. The swagger is a mask over the question of why trying to connect hurts so much.","sonnet":"Split between predatory swagger — King Kong hunger, toxic desire, wanting to hear screams — and a quietly anguished interior voice asking why it hurts to try and insisting there is still something felt inside despite feeling undeserving.","src":"haiku"},
"lost-society~artificial":{"opus":"A nihilistic condemnation of humanity as artificial and hollow, divided and doomed to fall as if we were never here, with hell empty because all its devils walk among us, having sold our souls and lost control on the road to our own downfall.","sonnet":"A nihilistic indictment of human civilization as fundamentally fake and self-defeating — the song frames humanity as enslaved prophets of nothing, having sold their souls and lost all control, now witnessing their own engineered downfall.","src":"sonnet"},
"lotus-eater~jawless":{"opus":"The anguish of being abandoned and unseen after years of a relationship, crumbling underfoot while no one glances; the pain is so constant it's frozen into a permanent grin, and the singer begs to be saved and simply looked at.","sonnet":"Five years of accumulated emotional damage left the narrator in pieces, crumbling beneath the indifference of others who called their pain fake — they're screaming inside a forced grin, publicly broken and unable to find the strength to fight it.","src":"sonnet"},
"lotus-eater~mother":{"haiku":"Seeks maternal validation while feeling fundamentally broken and rejected, trapped by her absence and her words. Self-worth depends on her approval and recognition.","opus":"A wrenching appeal to a mother for understanding, the narrator ringed by lifeless forms and icy looks, admitting he isn't whole and aching for the arms and healing voice of the woman who bore him. Lotus Eater, a Scottish metalcore band known for raw emotional heaviness, stretch a decade and a half of waiting for mercy into a desperate cry, her words rotting somewhere in his skull.","sonnet":"A son asking his mother to see him clearly before it's too late — the cold disapproval in the air, sixteen years of waiting for leniency, and the wish to have learned less sorrow. He is not whole without her belief, and though he knows she was right to stay in the light, he still needs her voice and her embrace more than anything else, the words she never said decaying inside him.","src":"haiku"},
"lotus-eater~narco":{"haiku":"The song describes mental control and power's corruption, suggesting reality is distorted and people must reprogramme their thinking to resist manipulation.","opus":"Lotus Eater, the Scottish metalcore outfit whose local slang flashes through the venom, unleash a paranoid rant about manipulation and control. Statistics that hide the truth and minds consumed by unchecked authority sketch a rigged game where understanding the rules leaves you nothing to protect. The title drug becomes both the sedation and the craving beneath it.","sonnet":"Power operates through manufactured taste and sedation — numbers are spun, thoughts devoured, the game rigged for absolute control. The narrator calls it visible and survivable but also addictive, cycling through withdrawal and the strange advice to strategize one's own weaknesses.","src":"haiku"},
"lotus-eater~vermin":{"opus":"A furious callout of fair-weather people who swarmed to belittle the singer in his lowest moment like rats, exposing themselves as morally empty carcasses, met now with defiance and a demand for silence.","sonnet":"A response to online pile-ons and fair-weather friends who swarmed and attacked the narrator during a vulnerable moment: the song calls them moral carcasses who proved their emptiness, and reclaims strength while refusing to let their words extinguish his light.","src":"sonnet"},
"louis-armstrong~la-vie-en-rose-single-version":{"haiku":"Love transforms ordinary moments into enchanted reality, intimate touch becoming portal to transcendent beauty and timeless connection.","opus":"Love as a spell that recolors the whole world rose-tinted, ordinary words turning into song. Armstrong's warmth makes Piaf's vision of romance feel less like fantasy than a settled, grateful contentment.","sonnet":"A lover describes the enchanted state of being held by someone who transforms the world — words become love songs, heaven responds when they kiss, and ordinary life becomes a world of roses. It is a pure lyric of romantic transfiguration.","src":"haiku"},
"louis-xiv~finding-out-true-love-is-blind":{"haiku":"Desire overwhelms judgment as attraction dissolves rational standards; the narrator romanticizes control and submission, learning that love obeys no aesthetic rules.","opus":"A deliberately provocative catalogue of lust reducing women to types and colors, the leering title mocking any notion of love while the music revels in objectification. Glam-rock sleaze staged as calculated bad taste, ambivalence about whether it's satire or the thing itself.","sonnet":"The narrator catalogues an indiscriminate, all-encompassing desire for women of every type and temperament, framing the compulsion as evidence that attraction operates beyond reason or sight — love is blind because desire is indiscriminate.","src":"haiku"},
"love-ghost~payback":{"haiku":"Vengeance consumes the speaker as violence and betrayal intertwine; they nurse rage and broken promises, watching their antagonist fall while unable to save anyone from ruin.","opus":"A boy nursing a revenge fantasy toward someone he cannot save, dressing a self-destructive spiral in outlaw imagery. Underneath the threats is the helplessness of watching a girl drown and finding one more excuse to get high.","sonnet":"Rage and grief tangled together over a girl lost to her own world and someone's lies — the narrator wants revenge, can't save her, and watches everything burn down.","src":"haiku"},
"love-is-noise~azure":{"haiku":"Lovers question whether they can endure Earth's decay and systems of greed to find meaning together across time.","opus":"Love Is Noise fuse erosion and yearning with an ecological backdrop, the narrator enduring the planet's decline while wondering whether two people will ever reunite. Set against greed and hypocrisy that prey on the faithful, the longing to muster the will to escape entrenched power binds private devotion to a dying world.","sonnet":"Connection severed without warning leaves the narrator suspended in nothingness, still reaching toward someone across the decay of the world. The question of reunion haunts every line — whether two people can find each other again through a reality corroded by greed and systems that feed on believers.","src":"haiku"},
"love-is-noise~nothing":{"haiku":"Love as meaningless noise, relationships as empty and void.","opus":"A charged reckoning with screens and surveillance, framing a seductive digital force that never sought closeness, only to see people's minds erode. The narrator moves from being controlled to seizing the reins back, flipping the power dynamic. Beneath the metalcore weight is a modern anxiety about technology hollowing us out, holding attention while stripping away autonomy.","sonnet":"A seductive technological force that pulls people close and tears them down, dissolving minds and taking control — the narrator resists, then ultimately inverts the power dynamic: \"I've taken over you.\" The keyboard cries, lust has a code, and recovery is blasphemy in the world this song describes.","src":"haiku"},
"love-is-noise~take-one-minute":{"haiku":"Collective appeals for transformation and belief in a shared future; seeking permission to voice thoughts and demanding evidence of change from the broader world.","opus":"An urgent plea for a single moment to lift the weight off the chest and quiet the dread of what's coming. It widens into a direct address to people online, urging them to reprioritize, share what's wrong and pay attention to how they feel, earnest and repetitive, reaching for connection as an antidote to isolation.","sonnet":"A repeated plea for one minute to say a piece, ease pressure, stop worrying — a simple need for a pause and to be heard that becomes a question about whether the relationship can survive change. The urgency is quiet: will you change for good, do you believe, somewhere we move together.","src":"haiku"},
"loveholic~a-p8882d":{"haiku":"Idolization and parasocial devotion—the narrator treats someone as a superstar and shooting star, calling them their shining light while experiencing loneliness despite admiration.","opus":"Loveholic worked the bright end of early-2000s Korean pop-rock, and this devotion song stays true to that gloss. The narrator's world is reoriented by someone who outshines a beloved actor and redeems music that once felt alien, addressed as the shooting star they launched. The idolizing tips toward the parasocial, admiration bright enough to leave a trace of loneliness underneath its dazzle.","sonnet":"A Korean love song in which the narrator's world has been completely reoriented by a person who outshines even a beloved actor and music that used to feel alien: you are the shooting star I launched, the most dazzling star in my heart — straightforward and bright in its devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"lovekurousa~please-revere-me-my-purpose-eludes-me":{"haiku":"Falling from illusion into emptiness, losing ground as awareness reveals a life without meaning.","opus":"A hypnotic loop about an ever-shifting sense of knowing that drops you out of your dreams into a clueless, cold-eyed reality. The circular repetition mirrors the vertigo of losing your footing and your purpose at once.","sonnet":"A very short, repetitive lyric about the disorienting sensation of losing your sense of direction or self — you fall from dreamy skies when reality intrudes, life goes on without a clue, and those cold green eyes at the end suggest the person looking back doesn't hold any answers either.","src":"haiku"},
"lower-definition~grief-eater":{"opus":"A raw plea to a person who became a source of pain rather than comfort, the narrator begging for relief from unbearable weight. Betrayal reveals someone he never truly knew, and his greatest fear is forgiving them. Grief, loss, and the refusal to let a wound repeat drive its aching farewell.","sonnet":"A reckoning with someone who failed the narrator at a critical moment — possibly during a death or crisis — and whose absence revealed a fundamental betrayal; forgiveness feels like the greatest threat, and the narrator refuses to allow them to repeat or relive what they did.","src":"sonnet"},
"lowlives~bones":{"haiku":"Death and decay define existence; the speaker expects to die soon and meets that fate with resignation.","opus":"A fatalistic staring-down of death by a man who doubts any afterlife reward and expects no easy road. Battered, burned out, and hardened by resentment, he digs for some remaining scrap of himself and reaches a strange calm, half-joking that he is fine underground and will reunite with the living once everyone is stripped to the skeleton.","sonnet":"A song about surviving a life that feels like death — the speaker is burned out, hollow, and thrown to wolves, yet stubbornly alive, digging through the debris of themselves looking for any fragment worth keeping.","src":"haiku"},
"lowlives~burn-forever":{"haiku":"Two people bound together face destruction with acceptance, resolved to burn or fall together rather than survive apart.","opus":"Lowlives write a defiant vow of devotion, promising that whether things end in rescue or ruin, the couple faces it as one and goes down together. The narrator frames his partner as something he planted and tends, pledging a bond meant to outlast any hardship thrown at it.","sonnet":"A love song about two people choosing to face catastrophe together rather than alone — if they burn or fall, they do it side by side, and that commitment is what turns debris into something that can grow.","src":"haiku"},
"lowlives~gravity":{"opus":"An extended metaphor of consuming love as gravity: the narrator is helplessly held down and pulled by a partner who is the sea to his ship, the force he can't resist, admitting that without her he is nothing.","sonnet":"A love song built around the metaphor of gravitational pull — the narrator is held to earth by a partner whose love operates like a physical force, binding, weighting, and sustaining him. Without that pull he would drift into nothingness, and the song aches with the beauty and terror of being so completely dependent on another.","src":"sonnet"},
"lowlives~liar":{"haiku":"Habitual deception masks deeper fracture; words feel authentic even when delivered with compulsive dishonesty.","opus":"A confession of compulsive lying as a mask over broken mental health, telling everyone he's fine while something he can't name goes wrong inside. The turn is realizing the cruelest lie is the one he tells himself, and resolving, this once, to stop.","sonnet":"A confession that lying to friends about being fine has become habitual and involuntary — the words feel real in the mouth before they fall out — the self-indictment is both honest and strangely defiant: it's not a crime, you'll never understand my mind, and the lie most damaging is the one told to himself.","src":"haiku"},
"lowlives~misery":{"haiku":"Freedom exists only as fantasy while systems of control tighten, the speaker longs to share internal wounds but knows nobody alive experiences true liberty.","opus":"A weary equation of existence with misery and the conviction that no one alive is truly free. The wish to be burned rather than buried, and to unburden what's kept inside before dying, makes it a confession from the edge of giving up.","sonnet":"A slow, suffocating feeling of being trapped in a life that offers no freedom — the speaker holds things inside until they rot, sees no one as truly free, and wants to be cremated rather than buried, as if even in death they want to escape containment.","src":"haiku"},
"lowlives~violent-dreams":{"haiku":"A person trapped in nightmarish cycles of theft, betrayal, and hopelessness, unable to escape violent dreams or find salvation.","opus":"A hollowed-out addict's reckoning, stripped of hope, love and any plan, betraying friends for the next high. The violent dreams are the trap of a life that can't be survived from inside, self-awareness offering no escape.","sonnet":"People trapped in cycles of nihilism — getting high, stealing, lying — find themselves imprisoned inside violent dreams they can't wake from; the only certainty is hopelessness, and the cage is their own minds.","src":"haiku"},
"lucrecia~sleeping-slaves-of-fate":{"haiku":"Breaking free from forced slumber and superficial lies to forge one's destined path through darkness toward righteous truth.","opus":"Wounded but unbroken, the narrator watches a false front collapse and swears to keep to a noble course, turning destiny itself into a weapon against pretenders who mistake surface appearances for truth. The grand, role-playing-game flavor of its imagery, death-masks and predetermined futures, places the song firmly in the theatrical world of gaming-influenced metal.","sonnet":"A wounded figure refuses to surrender to a corrupted world, insisting that righteous resolve — not results — defines the true path forward. Against usurpers and coerced fate, sovereignty of the heart becomes the one inheritance that cannot be seized, and death itself is made a lantern rather than an ending.","src":"haiku"},
"ludovico-technique~deeper-into-you":{"haiku":"Intimacy dissolves into power dynamics where the speaker erodes the other's defenses; sensation and senselessness collapse into mutual spiral.","opus":"An abusive presence describes itself as the addiction living inside its victim's mind, the relapse and collapse they can't quit. Desire and destruction are made indistinguishable as it burrows ever further in.","sonnet":"The speaker positions itself as the embodiment of the listener's relapse, collapse, and contempt — a force that sharpens its senses precisely through the other's growing senselessness and defenselessness, drilling deeper with each cycle of failure.","src":"haiku"},
"ludovico-technique~empty":{"haiku":"Emotional absence persists despite survival; the speaker moves through life numb, witnessing feeling in others but unable to access it himself.","opus":"A hollowed-out narrator marches through a life drained of affection, keeping the past locked away to avoid breaking. Survival here means feeling nothing at all.","sonnet":"Affections are drained to nothing as the mind keeps hounding and the heart beats without feeling; the speaker holds something locked away and wonders whether keeping themselves from breaking means losing what they have preserved, cycling through hollow processions of a life without emotional content.","src":"haiku"},
"lulu-suicide~she-deserves":{"haiku":"Witnessing the woman he loves suffer and being unable to lift her into better life, crawling on knees in helpless devotion.","opus":"A tender, aching portrait of a suffering woman the narrator watches from the outside, sure she's owed a better life than the one destroying her. His devotion curdles into self-abasement, crawling on his knees, wondering if she'll ever notice him, a helpless love before a person's private torment and the wish to rescue someone who can't be reached.","sonnet":"An observer watches a woman — sad face, guilty mind, parasitic relationship — suffer through a life beneath her worth, sitting alone in black and white while the narrator crawls on his knees hoping she'll notice him. Her deserving better and his invisibility to her are the two poles the song never resolves.","src":"haiku"},
"luna-aura~bent":{"haiku":"Relational futility wears down agency; the speaker bends under the weight of impossible longings, bent by desire into an unrecognizable shape.","opus":"A craving for something real to hold onto keeps getting answered with distortion instead of comfort, leaving her fading and settling for moments she can barely touch. Wanting to build a home and be taken care of, she only ever gets bent out of shape.","sonnet":"A person seeking something solid to believe in and someone to lean on keeps receiving only transient moments and emotional damage — \"all I ever get is bent\" — the candy sweetness of hope always collapsing into the same bruised posture.","src":"haiku"},
"luna-aura~sweet-sensation":{"haiku":"The song pursues relentless pleasure and escapism. Kids seek new vibrations and riots; the narrator hunts a lights-out kind of pleasure, refusing to stop.","opus":"A hedonistic pursuit of a blacked-out, delicious rush, mind drifting skyward while kids tear up the streets, hungry for a high she refuses to end and telling sour mouths to clear off. Luna Aura is an alt-pop artist with a brash electro-punk edge, and this track's snarling defiance and pulsing hooks turn raw pleasure-seeking into a fist-in-the-air refusal to heed anyone.","sonnet":"Pure kinetic wanting — crowds of restless kids in the street looking for something to bite into, a head full of clouds, music played until death and then played again. The whole song is chasing a specific high that's more about total immersion than about any one thing, a lights-out pleasure that keeps spinning and never quite stops.","src":"haiku"},
"luna-aura~too-little-too-late":{"haiku":"Rejection of a late romantic overture; the speaker has moved on and no longer cares about what the other person has to offer.","opus":"Luna Aura sharpens her electro-pop into a cold dismissal, voicing someone finished with waiting on an oblivious partner who has finally circled back. Unbothered and ready to wipe her record clean, she makes clear that whatever he's now offering shows up long after she has stopped caring.","sonnet":"A woman cuts off a partner whose efforts at reconciliation arrive long after her patience ran out. The emotional ledger has been settled privately — she has already moved on — and whatever is now offered lands against indifference rather than hurt.","src":"haiku"},
"luna-kills~leech":{"haiku":"Submission becomes a form of devotion as the speaker burns away layers of self to let another inhabit them, pain and connection fused into one sensation.","opus":"Love as consented self-immolation, burning off your own skin so someone can leech their way in, carving their name into you to let them belong. The devotion is inseparable from destruction, a preference for being burned alive over living without the other.","sonnet":"A codependent, self-dissolving attachment — the speaker strips away their own skin to let the other person in deeper, standing at the edge of collapse, breathing the other's smoke and drinking from their tongue as acts of total surrender to someone who simultaneously sickens and defines them.","src":"haiku"},
"luna-kills~slay-ur-enemies":{"haiku":"Daily pain compounds into murderous ideation; the speaker cycles between paralysis and urge to inflict suffering on those nearby.","opus":"Panic and daily-worsening pain channeled into a violent fantasy of cutting down enemies, numbing out and bleeding as self-preservation slips away.","sonnet":"About anxiety and self-destruction as both weapon and affliction — the speaker is overwhelmed by panic and daily suffering, turning the fantasy of slaying enemies inward, bleeding out of boredom with their own pain.","src":"haiku"},
"luna-s-call~merced-s-footsteps":{"opus":"An ecological lament for a dying Earth, mourning its eroding beauty and poisoned, rotting core; alien voices reach out through gamma rays and radiation as the planet decays into ashes.","sonnet":"A brief, apocalyptic lament for the Earth — alien voices or cosmic forces speak through radiation, and beneath the narrator's feet the planet's core is rotting, with poison falling from the sky onto a world whose beauty is eroding to ash. It reads as an ecological elegy in fragmentary form.","src":"sonnet"},
"lunatic-calm~leave-you-far-behind":{"haiku":"Desire and transgression drive the repeated vow to leave a lover far behind after crossing physical and emotional boundaries.","opus":"A push-pull between wanting closeness and wanting escape, itching to take someone on a thrilling ride and cross the boundary they set. The relentless craving eventually surrenders to the urge to abandon them entirely, an impulse toward exhilarating detachment.","sonnet":"A surge of desire and restless drive to break through limits — the narrator wants to push past every line drawn between them and the other person, ultimately wanting to outrun everything and leave it all behind.","src":"haiku"},
"lunatic-calm~neon-ray":{"haiku":"Burning cities and suppressed emotion reach critical mass; the speaker demands to be heard but faces systematic erasure from those in power.","opus":"A fractured, sample-driven vision of burning cities and unheeded pleas, where feelings ignite in the heat of a numbed present. The man who won't listen stands in for a whole world tuning out.","sonnet":"A minimal, chant-like protest: the narrator is trying to seize something (a ray of light, an opportunity, a moment of clarity) but whoever is in power — the man — will not listen. The cities are burning and feelings are burning with them, but the authority refuses to hear. It is alienation and powerlessness expressed in repeated, clipped phrases.","src":"haiku"},
"luxtorpeda~autystyczny-oficjalny-teledysk":{"haiku":"An autistic person loves quietly and intensely but cannot express emotions outwardly, creating silent communion misunderstood as coldness.","opus":"A tender Polish confession from someone who loves deeply but can't show it in the ordinary ways, counting trees and staying silent while the feeling runs underneath. Love expressed a little autistically, present but invisible on the surface.","sonnet":"A love letter from someone who loves differently — the narrator is present in body but somewhere else in mind, counting trees and power lines instead of talking, and admits tenderly that they love deeply, just 'a little autistically,' knowing their partner sees it even through the silence.","src":"haiku"},
"lxs~mi-o-mi-o-mi-o":{"haiku":"Virtual intimacy racing past reality into addiction and symbiosis that might dissolve at any moment.","opus":"Love reimagined as a shared VR game, two people wired into a simulation unsure what's real, chasing a finish line that keeps echoing back. The Polish artist finds real tenderness in the dread that the goggles will come off and leave them naked and alone.","sonnet":"A Polish-language song about the disorienting intensity of a very new relationship — the narrator feels like they've been married for a decade after knowing someone barely a week, comparing the love to a VR simulation they might both be plugged into, unsure whether to remove the goggles or dive deeper.","src":"haiku"},
"lyell~alive":{"haiku":"Possession and hunger consume the speaker; they cannot rest and obsess over recreating the intensity that proves they're alive.","opus":"A ferocious metalcore power fantasy of resurrection and dominance, casting the narrator as a god demanding worship and a beast that won't stay buried. Hunger, obsession and the drive to jolt himself into sensation fuel it. Beneath the menace is a craving to feel anything at all, sacrifice and defiance as ways to prove he's still here.","sonnet":"A speaker who was presumed dead resurrects with contempt for those who tried to extinguish them — demanding they get on their knees and show respect, warning that the beast has been woken — and ties self-resurrection to an almost predatory vitality, the compulsion to \"make the feeling come alive\" becoming an act of dominance over whoever tried to kill it.","src":"haiku"},
"lyell~eraser":{"haiku":"Erasure of self and memory, being unmade or forgotten, dissolution of identity.","opus":"Jealousy devours someone barely holding on, beating his head bloody against the wall and screaming at the moon when he sees his partner with someone else. He begs to be calmed and not made the villain, even as the possessive rage overwhelms and wipes everything out, leaving him stuck on rewind.","sonnet":"Jealousy as total cognitive erasure — seeing the person you want with someone else throws the narrator into a spiral of screaming at the moon and throwing their head against the wall, barely hanging on. Jealousy is personified as something that comes in and erases everything.","src":"haiku"},
"lykke-li~get-some":{"haiku":"Female sexuality becomes weapon and tool wielded with calculated confidence, delivering pleasure while maintaining absolute control over terms.","opus":"A brazen reclaiming of sexual power, the singer casting herself as the one in control who charms and dominates her lover on her own terms.","sonnet":"The speaker claims absolute sexual agency, casting herself as both prostitute and fortress — \"like a shotgun needs an outcome\" — with the man wrapped around her finger like a charm, the power dynamic inverted so that she sets the terms and he simply receives what she decides to give.","src":"haiku"},
"lykke-li~i-follow-rivers-the-magician-remix":{"haiku":"Devotional surrender collapses all distinction between lover and landscape; the speaker becomes a follower in water, following darkness and danger as if they were the only directions home.","opus":"Devotion that shades into surrender, following a lover into deep water and dark doom without hesitation. The image of unraveling in someone shows the risk underneath, love pursued past the point of self-preservation.","sonnet":"A woman speaks of absolute devotion to a man who holds oceanic, unruly power over her, surrendering herself to follow him through dark and deep waters wherever he leads.","src":"haiku"},
"lylvc~into-nothing":{"haiku":"Psychological dissolution accelerates as the speaker loses connection to themselves, falling deeper into numbness and despair.","opus":"Self-harm and addiction narrated from inside the chaos, cutting for relief and refusing to spend a lifetime being told why he shouldn't feel this bad. He edges toward the brink and just keeps dropping into emptiness.","sonnet":"Spiraling addiction and self-harm described as a freefall — the narrator cuts for release, cannot feel their hands, hides their real self, and falls deeper into paranoia and emptiness, craving something to stop the itch before chaos overtakes them completely.","src":"haiku"},
"lylvc~undertow":{"haiku":"Overwhelming currents of change and loss trap the speaker in undertow as they drown in new circumstances while fighting to maintain grip on disappearing stability.","opus":"Someone trying to make a fresh start feels dragged back under by their own spiralling thoughts and a relationship where no one listens. Drowning, paradoxically, feels calmer than the noise, and they wait for a tide to carry them out.","sonnet":"A person at the threshold of a new life they cannot quite embrace: bags packed, flooded by an undertow of anxiety, drowning in calm water while unable to be heard, they wait for the tides to turn by morning — fighting their own head, desperate for a new start before they fall apart.","src":"haiku"},
"lynch~devil":{"haiku":"Temptation whispers surrender while hearts burn crimson; the soul fights for autonomy against demons clawing toward submission and dissolution.","opus":"A visceral metal duel between the narrator and a tempting devil urging surrender, sung across Japanese and English. The pull between defiant self-reliance and a masochistic plea to be killed by love makes desire and self-destruction inseparable. Set against a burning crimson moon, it reaches for the ecstasy of losing oneself, dancing toward ruin as the world ends.","sonnet":"The devil whispers to give up the fight while the speaker insists they can do it alone, urging defiance — \"bare your fangs\" — at the moment of absolute crisis; between devotion and self-destruction the song asks only to be loved, whatever the cost, as the world ends in flame.","src":"haiku"},
"lynyrd-skynyrd~simple-man":{"haiku":"A mother's wisdom teaches embracing simplicity and genuine love over wealth and ambition as the path to lasting fulfillment.","opus":"A mother's parting counsel to her son — don't chase money or burn through life recklessly, trust your heart, and be an uncomplicated man content in what he loves and understands. The whole song is her wish that he end up satisfied rather than rich.","sonnet":"A mother's dying counsel to her son: slow down, forget lust for gold, follow your heart, find love, satisfy your soul — be a simple kind of man, because all that she wants is for him to be satisfied.","src":"haiku"},
"lynyrd-skynyrd~sweet-home-alabama":{"haiku":"A Southern man longs for his Alabama home and defends the region against northern criticism, invoking Muscle Shoals' musical legacy and dismissing political scandals with regional pride.","opus":"The band's proud, prickly answer to Neil Young's Southern-man critiques, defending home while slyly booing the Birmingham governor and shrugging at Watergate. Beneath the anthem is a knottier stance on Southern identity, part homesick love song, part defensive retort.","sonnet":"A defiant Southern pride anthem pushing back against Neil Young's criticisms of the South — the narrator celebrates Alabama, dismisses Watergate as irrelevant to his conscience, and invokes the legendary Muscle Shoals recording scene as proof of the region's genuine cultural richness.","src":"haiku"},
"lyrre~north-star":{"haiku":"Self-assured navigation through uncertainty replaces doubt with trust, as the speaker charts an unshaken path forward despite not knowing the destination, guided by internal compass rather than external validation.","opus":"A traveller trusts an inner compass over any set destination, insisting that wandering and dreaming are not the same as being lost. The reassurance is aimed as much inward as outward.","sonnet":"A declaration of fearless self-trust: the speaker's inner map, written in blood, guides them like a north star, and even when wandering or dreaming they are not lost — the waves will always bring them to where they belong.","src":"haiku"},
"m-d-m-p~strength":{"haiku":"Internal fire threatens to consume entirely; the speaker desperately seeks strength and faith to survive his own escalating rage.","opus":"A prayer for the strength to grow through a consuming hatred before it burns the person out entirely. The real terror is being frightened of one's own words and self-destruction.","sonnet":"A plea to be given strength and time to grow through an unspecified internal hatred before it burns out; the speaker acknowledges running from fate and losing control, asking to be pulled back to face a pain they fear their own words might detonate.","src":"haiku"},
"m-i-a~bad-girls":{"haiku":"Glorifies reckless living at high speed, with young women breaking rules and pushing boundaries without regard for consequences. The song celebrates the thrill and danger of living fast.","opus":"M.I.A. flips a defiant thrill-ride anthem into a statement of female autonomy, its imagery drawn from Saudi women drifting cars. Living fast becomes a claim on freedom and swagger in spaces built to deny both, delivered with cool provocation.","sonnet":"A celebration of reckless, high-speed femininity and defiance — the speaker drives fast, lives by her own rules, and refuses to slow down, framing the \"bad girl\" lifestyle as both exhilarating and a conscious rejection of expectations placed on her.","src":"haiku","fable":"M.I.A. borrows the visual language of Gulf highway drifting — engines held at the redline, a woman at a wheel she isn't supposed to touch in that world — and turns female recklessness into a flex, competence earned precisely because the rules forbid it. Beneath the swagger sits a quieter admission that she once steered her own path and wrecked it on purpose, so the danger reads less as thrill-seeking than as a refusal to be driven by anyone else. By the final stretch escape and seduction have merged: she can outpace every boy and every siren, but the one thing she asks to carry off the road with her is you, which reframes the whole joyride as a come-on dressed up as a threat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","identity & becoming","love & desire"],"means":"recklessness earned precisely because the rules forbid her to steer","built":"escape and seduction merge so the joyride reframes as a come-on dressed as threat","fnote":"The Gavras video staged real Gulf drift culture in Morocco while the Saudi driving ban still stood — M.I.A., asked why not shoot there: because she didn't want to go to jail."},
"m-i-a~borders":{"haiku":"Systemic oppression through borders, police violence, and stratification demands unified resistance across identity, gender, and nation. The marginalized reclaim power through solidarity and refusal of the system's logic.","opus":"A rapid-fire interrogation of the systems and slogans that police who belongs, mocking hashtag culture while defiantly speaking for migrants and boat people the establishment shuts out.","sonnet":"A rapid-fire interrogation of systems that divide, exclude, and kill: borders, politics, police shots, privilege, and boat people are all challenged with \"what's up with that?\" while the speaker insists on representing people the FM ignores and tearing doors off the system.","src":"haiku"},
"m-i-a~finally":{"haiku":"The speaker dismisses critics and moves forward unfazed. She embraces her unconventional self and celebrates those close to her while pursuing her own path regardless of judgment.","opus":"A defiant self-empowerment anthem: the singer refuses to let critics drain her, promising to keep pushing toward her future and to be remembered, casting herself as an acquired taste (whiskey, not tea) and reframing hardship as the university that toughens you.","sonnet":"A declaration of self-sufficiency and forward momentum in the face of critics — the speaker dismisses hate, claims freedom in her own strangeness, and insists the obstacles people use to define her are also the raw material she'll turn into something worth remembering.","src":"haiku"},
"m-i-a~paper-planes":{"haiku":"A hustler brags about fast hustles, border crossings, and casual theft, framing street life and crime as aspirational rebellion. The refrain reduces all desire to taking money by any means.","opus":"A hustler's boast about crossing borders, forging visas, dealing, and robbery, delivered with mock-menacing lines about taking your money and murdering.","sonnet":"The narrator performs the persona of a hustling outsider — navigating borders, forging documents, and running street schemes — while satirising the immigrant-as-threat stereotype by playing it up with swagger and gunshot sound effects.","src":"haiku"},
"m-i-a~xxxo":{"haiku":"Desire and digital culture intertwine as a lover resists being moulded into someone's fantasy; the narrator subverts expectations and commodification through playful refusal.","opus":"A pop-slick sendup of digital-age intimacy, where courtship is downloads and tweets and a lover who wants a curated version of you. Underneath the flirtation is M.I.A.'s complaint about being flattened into an image someone else can consume.","sonnet":"The speaker resists a pursuer who wants her to perform an identity she is not, framing desire in the language of digital consumption and projection while insisting the real her is never quite what they want her to be.","src":"haiku"},
"m-l~storm":{"opus":"A brooding, minimal invocation of surrender and dissolution, a vision growing inward as old words wither and the self withdraws from a restless world into obscurity. Its central image, open arms and unyielding hands giving in to the storm, frames acceptance of overwhelming force as a kind of release.","sonnet":"A minimal song about surrendering to an overwhelming force — words wither, the world becomes devoid and restless, and the narrator opens arms that will not yield in order to give in to the storm rather than resist it. The emotional register is surrender as acceptance rather than defeat.","src":"sonnet"},
"m-tley-cr-e~dr-feelgood":{"haiku":"A drug dealer nicknamed Dr. Feelgood operates in Hollywood with police complicity, selling heroin and cocaine while building wealth and infamy in the criminal underworld.","opus":"A cinematic portrait of a Hollywood drug dealer riding high on corruption before his inevitable fall, the pusher as false savior. Written as the band got sober, it watches the whole ecosystem of easy vice collapse.","sonnet":"A portrait of a street-level drug dealer named Jimmy — driving a primer-flamed Chevy, selling cocaine for the Mexican mob in Hollywood, greasing cops, running his operation with practiced ease — until word gets out that the law is finally closing in and his luck has run out.","src":"haiku"},
"m-tley-cr-e~driftaway":{"haiku":"A tired man reflects on contradictions and mistakes in his life, finding solace only in drifting away through memory and escape.","opus":"A weary, reflective ballad from a simple man staring in the mirror unsure who he sees, smoking cigarettes and smiling to hold back tears, closing his eyes to drift away from mistakes and lonely days. Motley Crue, the notorious Sunset Strip glam-metal band, dial back the excess here, finding gratitude in a guitar and a wished-upon star that dries up the rain, a rare moment of tender resignation.","sonnet":"A road-worn man making peace with the contradictions of his own life — the mirror shows a stranger, smoke covers tears, mistakes mapped onto wrong roads, and yet something in music and wishful thinking has kept him upright. He drifts not from despair but from acceptance, closing his eyes and letting the life he's actually lived carry him forward instead of fighting it.","src":"haiku"},
"m-tley-cr-e~kickstart-my-heart":{"haiku":"The song celebrates speed, thrill, and dangerous excess—fast cars, drugs, skydiving, and hedonism. The speaker seeks adrenaline rushes to feel alive.","opus":"A glorification of adrenaline and reckless living, born (per the band's own story) from a near-fatal overdose, equating speed, danger, sex and rock-and-roll with the jolt that restarts a stopped heart. It is a celebration of chasing the next high and near-death thrill as the thing that makes you feel alive.","sonnet":"A celebration of adrenaline-fueled excess — speed, danger, women, and the rush of staying alive — rooted in Nikki Sixx's real near-death experience from a heroin overdose that literally had to restart his heart.","src":"haiku"},
"m-tley-cr-e~live-wire-kick-ass-91-remix":{"haiku":"Raw sexual aggression and violent domination; the speaker positions himself as unstoppable, dangerous, and willing to harm for satisfaction and conquest.","opus":"A jolt of predatory adrenaline, the narrator charged up and prowling the streets, youthful and untamed, smashing barriers and lunging for the kill. This reworks the opening blast from Motley Crue's 1981 debut, the sleazy glam-metal that helped ignite the Sunset Strip, its swaggering hunger delivered with reckless, dangerous energy.","sonnet":"Pure charged aggression sold as sexual energy — the speaker is a current looking for a place to discharge, hot and running free, breaking down walls and prowling the night. The danger is the appeal: don't cage it, don't try to tame it, just plug in and accept that it will take what it wants.","src":"haiku"},
"m-tley-cr-e~loveshine":{"haiku":"The singer walks miles climbing mountains seeking a lover's smile; their heart waits like the ocean for love to sail in. They ask the other to let their love shine through—a plea for acceptance and return.","opus":"A warm, soulful appeal for a lover to open herself to his affection, having trekked impossible distances just to see her smile, his heart a patient sea awaiting her return. Motley Crue soften their glam edge into bluesy, gospel-tinged romance, casting the beloved as a lifelong companion who cradled him young and will be there at the close, a tender counterpoint to the Sunset Strip band.","sonnet":"A devotion that has survived time and distance and wants back in — a million miles climbed just to reach someone's smile, an ocean heart waiting for love to sail home, and a memory of youth where this person held the speaker and promises to hold them again at the end. All the riddles and mysteries of the past become secondary to a single request: let the love in.","src":"haiku"},
"m-tley-cr-e~misunderstood":{"haiku":"Life's cruelty passes judgment on the desperate—the elderly, lonely children, abused mothers—leaving emotional damage that cycles through generations.","opus":"The Sunset Strip glam veterans swap decadence for a bleak procession of the ignored, a suicidal elder, a boy waiting on an absent father, a mother trapped in her own snares. Existence, unfairly overlooked, leaves them all retreating into fantasies of relief. The narrator names himself the wreckage of a rough upbringing, an embittered survivor closing on a thin sliver of hope.","sonnet":"A series of portraits — an old man considering suicide, a neglected child, an isolated woman — builds into a broader reckoning with lives that fell through the cracks of an indifferent world. The narrator then claims the same wound, shaped by anger and a troubled past, still hoping a father comes home.","src":"haiku"},
"m-tley-cr-e~poison-apples":{"haiku":"Young musicians live a destructive lifestyle of drugs and excess, bearing scars of their beauty and self-destruction while refusing societal judgment.","opus":"Motley Crue turn their own excess into legend, hopping a bus toward seedy destinations with a handful of ambition and an appetite for filth. A beloved rock band keeps them wild while fans and the tabloids swing between adoration and contempt. Ink on their faces and a rotten-fruit self-image flaunt reckless glamour against a mother's plea to seek out some belief.","sonnet":"A Greyhound arrival into the rock and roll dream of excess plays out in retrospect — sex, drugs, the tabloid machine chewing at the band while fans loved them anyway. The poison-apple image holds both the beauty and the scar, the glamour and the disgrace of having lived that myth.","src":"haiku"},
"m-tley-cr-e~power-to-the-music":{"haiku":"Musicians and rock culture resist censorship and criticism, defending music as a force that cannot be silenced by authority or morality claims.","opus":"Motley Crue, the Sunset Strip glam-metal survivors, defend rock as unkillable street power. Feeling victimized and blamed by critics, the narrators dare anyone to put a bullet in their head to keep them down, insisting the music isn't dead in the streets. Wanting it loud enough to wake the dead, the anthem answers accusations of devastation with a defiant refusal to turn it down.","sonnet":"A defiant counter-attack on everyone who declared rock dead or dangerous: critics, moral authorities, and parents alike are dismissed as irrelevant. Music is positioned as a living force that cannot be killed by disapproval or legislation, and the speaker would rather take a bullet than stop. The rallying cry is unconditional.","src":"haiku"},
"m-tley-cr-e~smoke-the-sky":{"haiku":"Marijuana has been used throughout history despite government lies calling it evil, and the song defends its use as a harmless pleasure.","opus":"A cheeky pro-marijuana anthem, the narrator playing the devil while name-checking Marco Polo, Socrates and JFK as fellow smokers and mocking politicians for exaggerating the evils of the reefer. Motley Crue lean into their notorious hedonism here, the Sunset Strip glam-metal band turning weed advocacy into a tongue-in-cheek roll call of history's alleged sky-smokers, all riff and rebellious wink.","sonnet":"A revisionist cannabis anthem that drafts Marco Polo and JFK into its defense — the song argues that prohibition is a modern lie and that across all of history, from ancient travelers to presidents, people have always smoked. The mood is defiant rather than desperate, the joint held up as both personal pleasure and cultural inheritance against sixty years of official exaggeration.","src":"haiku"},
"m-tley-cr-e~uncle-jack":{"haiku":"Uncle Jack molested children, took their innocence, and made them slaves. The singer wishes him dead, wants him to scream and bleed, and condemns him to an unmarked grave—unforgiving of child abuse.","opus":"A blistering denunciation of a child molester, the narrator wishing him slain and sealed behind heavy metal, haunted by the wails and shattered futures of the innocents he ruined. The usual glam-metal hedonists turn grimly earnest, aiming their fury at a predator lurking in shadow and denying him any absolution, an anonymous burial the sole mercy they'll grant.","sonnet":"Rage directed at a child abuser who hid behind family trust — a man named Jack took children somewhere shameful and traded money for their pain, and the speaker wants not forgiveness but obliteration: locked behind iron, dead on the floor, buried without a marker. The fury is protective and unresolved, the children's screaming still audible, their broken dreams unhealed.","src":"haiku"},
"m83~gone":{"haiku":"Absence and vanishing; the disappeared leave traces and emptiness where presence once existed.","opus":"A hushed, uncanny M83 piece addressed to a figure everybody's already familiar with, someone spoken to in dreams and read in the clouds. Its imagery of crossing an azure span and a final shot at friendship gives it the ache of a fleeting, half-spectral bond. It reaches for the tenderness of catching someone who's already drifting off.","sonnet":"An enigmatic, possibly supernatural figure — known to everyone, written in the clouds, communicating through dreams and tears — is addressed by a collective \"we\" who have been watching and waiting. The shift from \"keep looking\" to \"stop looking, come by\" in the final lines suggests the community stops reaching for something transcendent and instead offers simple human companionship before it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"maanam~ta-noc-do-innych-jest-niepodobna-2011-remaster":{"haiku":"Aging brings resignation: the speaker recalls youthful indecision and melodrama, now finds peace in quiet presence beside a lover, accepting life's impermanence.","opus":"Maanam, the Polish new-wave institution fronted by Kora, capture a woman quieting her restless past into present-tense tenderness. Once she had an answer for everything and took every trifle seriously; now she tells her heart simply to be a heart and lies still beside her lover. This night, unlike any other, holds a love she calls crazy while the grass stays green and hours slip by.","sonnet":"A woman reflects on the person she used to be — quick with answers, always escaping, taking everything seriously. Now she lies quietly beside someone she loves, feeling the hours pass, holding a wild love that has replaced all that former certainty without fully silencing the voice inside.","src":"haiku"},
"mac-demarco~chamber-of-reflection":{"haiku":"Solitude and introspection as necessary paths toward clarity and spiritual renewal.","opus":"Solitude reframed as necessary passage, borrowing the Masonic chamber where an initiate sits alone before rebirth. The lonely feeling is presented as the price of becoming clearer, a hermit's comfort dressed in synth.","sonnet":"A call to solitude as preparation — spending time alone, turning inward, understanding that the loneliness is a necessary passage before joining better company and being born again into a clearer version of oneself.","src":"haiku"},
"mac-miller~loud":{"haiku":"Braggadocio mingles with self-awareness of moral compromise and pharmaceutical escape; the speaker celebrates his ascent while documenting the cost paid in dignity and sobriety.","opus":"Early Mac in full braggart mode, cataloguing money, drugs and sold-out shows to drown out anything quieter. The volume itself is the point: turn everything up loud enough and you don't have to hear what the success is costing you.","sonnet":"A young rapper at the height of early success revels in drug use, groupies, and mounting fame, asserting that numbers don't lie and that he's destined for the top regardless of what anyone says.","src":"haiku"},
"mac-miller~objects-in-the-mirror":{"haiku":"Romantic perfection coexists with personal fragmentation; the speaker offers love while grappling with internal chaos and self-destruction.","opus":"A tender plea to a partner in crisis, promising to change and begging both of them to leave the wreckage behind. It aches because the narrator asking her not to give up on life is clearly barely holding onto his own.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses a woman with bruised hope — promising he'll be different, asking for a chance to live again, warning her not to give up — while simultaneously working through his own wreckage: broken pride, an inability to concentrate, a fear that perfection and healing are both permanently out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"machinae-supremacy~my-dragons-will-decimate":{"haiku":"A speaker forges their own law and reality, threatening destruction if anyone breaks it. They warn that the current rulers have lost their domain and face defeat from an unstoppable force.","opus":"Machinae Supremacy, the Swedish band who welded metal to chiptune SID synths, stage a cosmic proclamation of sovereignty. A single being among countless others, lost and undone, swears to build his own realm and rule, loosing devastating beasts wordlessly should it be violated. Firelight stretches across the breadth of heaven as the divine offspring burn and drop, the false claimant told his war is already forfeited.","sonnet":"A sovereign will declares its own cosmology — laws forged alone, enforced by dragon fire, granted to no god or collective. The question of why abundance still produces suffering is posed and then abandoned in favor of the simpler assertion that this world was never anyone else's to rule.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-girl~athoth-a-go-go":{"opus":"The breakcore duo pairs manic energy with tender apocalyptic devotion, addressing a blind idiot deity as a real best friend. Falling free through time, the narrator finds love and terror inseparable, imagining holding someone close until both are obliterated. Cosmic dread and desperate intimacy collapse into ecstatic annihilation.","sonnet":"A feverish declaration of a bond so intense and terrifying that it ends in mutual destruction — the narrator and a 'real best friend' free-fall through time together until their bodies splatter on the sidewalk, the love so absolute it obliterates both of them.","src":"sonnet"},
"machine-girl~black-glass":{"haiku":"Society's dystopian surveillance and digital alienation turn beauty into plastic, meaning into ash.","opus":"An apocalyptic warning about our capitulation to screens, the dark reflective surface serving as both mirror and idol that curdles blood into plastic and remakes the planet as a manufactured paradise. It's a plea to heed a truer summons before the digital promise devours everything.","sonnet":"A warning about screens and technology as surveillance and spiritual threat — the phone or screen is the \"panoptical appendage\" that reflects your soul back at you while the world burns; the song urges heeding the message before humanity turns itself into a synthetic, chrome replica of life.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-girl~blood-magic":{"haiku":"Self-harm and psychological torment as expressions of inner conflict and survival.","opus":"A frantic sprint through mental collapse, isolation souring into self-destruction while therapy and medication blur into more harm. The refrain naming the impossible choice, self-ruin or self-protection, marks the line a breaking mind can't locate.","sonnet":"A mind in full breakdown — self-destruction and self-defence becoming indistinguishable, psychic parasites attacking from inside, the body poisoning itself in a roach motel of the soul — crying out for the womb, the cure, and finally for death.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-girl~dance-in-the-fire":{"haiku":"Chaos as survival: spinning through noise and distortion, moving to rhythms no one else hears, extracting meaning from the static itself.","opus":"Frantic digital-age catharsis, dancing through overload and static to exorcise a demon that turns out to be calling from within, insisting individuality isn't about winning teams but about letting your strange inner frequency loose.","sonnet":"A song about outrunning your own dark thoughts by staying in motion and in the body — the speaker uses music and physical movement as a way to prevent internal demons from catching up, while also trying to flush out something invasive.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-girl~psychic-attack":{"haiku":"Psychic and mental superiority as weapon, destroying enemies through willpower and third-eye domination.","opus":"A hyperviolent, digital-hardcore revenge fantasy where the narrator weaponizes the mind against tormentors, killing with a glance and a third eye. Beneath the cartoonish gore is real grievance: retaliation against those who talk behind backs, laugh at failures, and treat the narrator like garbage. It transmutes powerlessness into an omnipotent psychic strike back.","sonnet":"A psychic avenger demolishes men who underestimate her with her mind as the weapon — eyes closed, she can make heads explode, deep-fry brains, and take lives with a third eye while her own face stays pretty. The catalog of grievances — talking shit, laughing at her falls, treating her like dogshit — is what powers the attack.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-girl~until-i-die":{"haiku":"Dreams sustain as reality collapses; the subject fights through existence phase-shifted from normalcy.","opus":"As reality collapses around a fraying mind, the narrator clings to dreams and arcane defenses, vowing to keep fighting through every night rather than surrender. Panic, dissociation and sheer stubborn will bleed together into a refusal to stop.","sonnet":"A speaker living out of phase with reality — folding like a tesseract, casting shadows as resistance — fortifies their mind with arcane wisdom and the refusal to forget pain, fighting to survive the night and keep going until death, haunted by the question of whether any of it is real.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-gun-kelly~bloody-valentine":{"haiku":"A brief, intense romance where two people retreat into fantasy despite knowing their connection cannot last; temporary transcendent connection.","opus":"A dizzy, self-aware infatuation where a fling meant to stay casual has him slipping into commitment and picturing a lifelong bond. He admits he doesn't do counterfeit romance but will indulge for the night anyway, restless and melancholy, playing along while quietly hoping it becomes more than a single evening.","sonnet":"A one-night encounter the speaker knows isn't real love but can't resist — he's too emotionally dysregulated to commit but also too attached to leave, so he plays pretend, calling her \"girlfriend\" in his head and staying for a night that feels permanent.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-head~a-farewell-to-arms":{"haiku":"An anti-war song mourning the children and soldiers killed in conflicts waged by politicians and profiteers who never send their own sons.","opus":"An antiwar lament mourning slaughtered soldiers and grieving fathers and children, condemning the leaders and senators who send others to die while their own sons never fight, and asking what humanity has become.","sonnet":"The song is an anti-war protest that mourns the human cost of conflict — dead soldiers, grieving families, children in danger — and accuses political leaders and war profiteers of sending the poor to die while they enrich themselves. The speaker waves a white flag and demands accountability.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages an anti-war protest that keeps returning to one unanswerable question—who has won?—and answers it only by collapsing the very idea of victory: nobody wins when everyone is dead. Its title works as a double blade: a soldier's surrender flag laid down (farewell to weapons) and a mourner's goodbye to the arms that once held the dead, the white flag of the chorus doubling as both truce and shroud. Movement matters here, because the accusation sharpens as it descends—from the abstract crimson of ruined hills, to the sons burying fathers, and finally the named culprits: the hawks in office whose own children will never fight, the wealthy predators whose fangs drip while the poor hang, the shepherds working a mindless flock. What opens as elegy tightens into indictment and ends in a stripped, shell-shocked refrain that stops asking who profited and asks instead what the collective has been reduced to—numb, complicit, unrecognizable—before the last farewell lands not as noble sacrifice but as damnation pleading for rescue.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Iraq-war-era protest, ten minutes long — and the one Blackening track where Flynn plays no solo; Demmel took every lead. So rare live that its second-ever performance circulated as news.","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"victory abolished as a category because it requires someone surviving to claim it","built":"the title is a double blade — soldier's truce and mourner's goodbye to the arms that once held"},
"machine-head~a-nation-on-fire":{"haiku":"A nation built on endless war and division blinds its people with lies while suffering accumulates and truth is suppressed.","opus":"A defiant portrait of a fractured, propaganda-fed country that pours more into weapons than into healing. Refusing to buckle, the narrator vows to hold firm and defend his own, insisting the pain is temporary as the nation, in every sense, goes up in flames.","sonnet":"Raw anger at division, media lies, and endless war from someone who has personally borne the cost — the narrator refuses to break, insists the suffering won't last forever, and demands solidarity across racial and social lines.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song diagnoses the American 1990s as a body politic in autoimmune collapse: a war economy that funds its own violence over its own healing, a press that feeds a split public engineered lies, racial fracture curdling into blind fury. Against that, Robb Flynn plants a survivor's brace-position first-person — hardening rather than shattering under what he witnesses, extending a hand across the wreckage. The final turn strips the defiance to a confession: nights measured in gunfire, scars presented as proof, and a quiet admission that the narrator was among those who let the ruin happen unopposed. The mask is groove-metal bravado; underneath sits the older dread that endurance and complicity are the same posture.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Flynn rode his mountain bike into a real riot on Telegraph Avenue — burning squad cars, tear gas, chased by both sides — and has said the lyrics poured out right after.","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"endurance and complicity held as the same posture under bravado's mask","built":"the closing confession strips the defiance to an admission of shared ruin"},
"machine-head~a-thousand-lies":{"haiku":"Condemnation of systemic deception and hypocrisy across governance and society. The song exposes abuse, inequality, and corruption as rooted in exploitation and emotional numbness among those in power.","opus":"Machine Head rage against a rigged system, urban poverty and power-hungry leaders willing to kill to stay on top. The singer channels raw fury, refusing to feel any pity for the deceptions he's been fed over and over, and swearing he'll come through it alive.","sonnet":"The song attacks both personal betrayal and systemic injustice—corrupt politicians, urban poverty, racism, and war waged for power—framing all of it as an endless cycle of lies told to people who can no longer tell right from wrong. Survival despite the rot is the only defiant response.","src":"haiku","fable":"A young man interrogates his own survival ethic, asking what it means to stay honest inside a rigged system that forces small betrayals just to keep breathing. The pre-chorus turns that inward pressure outward, collapsing the line between self-examination and rage until moral certainty itself dissolves under a system he's finished tolerating. Each verse widens the frame: from the cornered individual bristling like a primed weapon, to a landscape of spent syringes and shattered glass mapping the poverty that strips a democracy bare, to the greed of politicians and war-profiteers whose appetite for control curdles his blood. Machine Head's groove-metal machinery makes the chorus a communal verdict, hurling accusation at every liar who's spun the same worn deceptions, refusing them sympathy while claiming the sheer toughness to outlast them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","money & the street"],"means":"outlasting a rigged system by refusing its right to your conscience","built":"the chorus converts a private survival ethic into communal verdict"},
"machine-head~aesthetics-of-hate":{"fable":"The song is an elegy disguised as a curse, or a curse mobilized as an elegy: a real killing (Dimebag Darrell, shot onstage in 2004) sits behind it, and behind that a critic's essay whose contempt for metal fandom's devotion is the second wound the song answers. Its whole movement is one of turning grief outward into vengeance — the opening scene of someone desecrating a man already buried gives way, across verse after verse, to a widening pronoun as private mourning becomes a collective vow, so that the taunted brotherhood becomes the thing being defended rather than mocked. The refrain of brotherhood keeps interrupting the profanity like a held candle, insisting that what looks like blind rage is actually fidelity: the aesthetic being reclaimed is the reverence the critic mistook for stupidity. It ends by abandoning first-person argument entirely and handing judgment upward, the same line pounded down to single syllables until it stops being a sentence and becomes an incantation — mourning that can no longer reason and can only call down a hammer.","fableDeep":"It's a revenge song whose weapon is the song itself — 'for the love of brother I will sing this fucking song' makes the track the retaliation — and the title is captured weaponry, the attacker's own words turned back on him. The wound is specific and modern: 'the words I read on the screen,' grief ambushed by an op-ed. Notice what's being defended — not the dead man's life but the community's right to revere him ('you branded us pathetic for our respect'), which is why the song answers in the collective voice. And the closing prayer-curse, 'may the hands of God strike them down,' deliberately borrows the enemy's own righteous register: hate returned in its own aesthetics.","haiku":"Honoring a fallen brother against disrespect and defending his memory fiercely.","opus":"Furious defense of a murdered friend's honor against a hateful critic, vowing vengeance and damnation for the attacker.","sonnet":"A furious defense of a fallen comrade against a critic who disrespected his memory after his death.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"An answer to William Grim's Iconoclast essay — Aesthetics of Hate: R.I.P. Dimebag Abbott, & Good Riddance — which celebrated Dimebag's onstage murder; Flynn's reply: it's because of people like you that there are Nathan Gales in this world.","fnote2":"Grammy-nominated for Best Metal Performance in 2008, losing to Slayer's Final Six; the working version was called The Thrashterpiece.","themes":["death & grief","anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"grief mobilized outward into a collective vow, reverence defended as the aesthetic being reclaimed","built":"the refrain pounds down to single syllables until it stops being a sentence and becomes incantation"},
"machine-head~all-falls-down": {"haiku":"A predatory figure exploits and psychologically manipulates a victim they've been hunting since childhood, presenting their cruelty as something the victim secretly desires. Terror and control are the song's subject, with the perpetrator portraying themselves as the embodiment of the victim's deepest fear.","opus":"Voiced by fear personified, the song stalks its victim like a predator and monster from childhood, promising to break them down, then reveals the fear is the self one has become, driving toward an inevitable downfall.","sonnet":"A song personifying fear as a predatory inner monster that has stalked someone since childhood, charming and paralyzing them, with the second half turning inward to acknowledge that the monster and the narrator are the same thing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A stalker's monologue addressed to you, sung by something that vows to mesmerize and immobilize its target before choking away a final breath — and Robb Flynn plays it as seduction, a whispered tenderness curdling into a bladed grin, the predator courting its prey. The horror is that the threat keeps shrinking inward: first an intruder crouched beneath the mattress, then lodged behind the eyes, until the voice drops its mask and admits it has been feeding on this person since earliest childhood. When the listener finally demands its identity, the answer arrives flat and devastating — it is terror itself, and that terror is you, hunter and hunted revealed as one soul. That collapse is the comeback record's whole thesis in miniature: Machine Head, nearly buried by the nu-metal years, stages self-hatred as horror-movie theater and then dissolves the villain into the man in the mirror. The closing movement abandons the taunt entirely for a plea to endure — a crawl through faith and grief — so the demonic swagger stands exposed as a frightened man narrating his own undoing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"the intruder who reveals it has been feeding on this person since earliest childhood","built":"hunter and hunted collapse into one when the demon names itself as you"},
"machine-head~all-in-your-head":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts someone's negativity and self-inflicted misery, asserting that the person's unhappiness stems from their own mind rather than external circumstances.","opus":"Machine Head push back against a relentless critic who keeps telling the narrator he's broken and out of place. He accepts being an oddball but won't have his every flaw catalogued, then flips the judgment around, arguing his tormentor's own misery is self-made, and someday they'll realize the fault was never his.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who projects their own misery outward and blames the narrator for it — the narrator admits to being weird and messed up but refuses to be the scapegoat for someone else's inability to see that their unhappiness comes from inside themselves.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the mask of a self-defense anthem, the narrator listing his own flaws with a shrug before ordering an accuser to back off, but the structure quietly inverts that stance: by the final chorus the diagnosis has flipped outward, insisting the other person's unhappiness is self-manufactured and that someday they'll admit the fault was never his. What sounds like a wounded man armored against judgment becomes, underneath, an accusation dressed as pity, telling the aggressor that their misery is a private machine they refuse to switch off. The central image runs one direction only, a pointed finger reversed until it lands on the one doing the pointing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"the accusation reversed until it lands on the accuser","built":"the self-defense posture quietly inverts by the final chorus into pity"},
"machine-head~american-high":{"haiku":"A musician recounts a rebellious youth of substance abuse, delinquency, and forming a band despite—or because of—poverty and social rejection.","opus":"A memoir set to metal about a troubled adolescence on the West Coast: an alienated, substance-addled misfit who skipped schoolwork to form a group instead. The real point arrives as payback, as the boy everyone dismissed converts youthful defiance into a career, hurling that triumph back at the tormentors who counted him out.","sonnet":"A nostalgic autobiography of being a bullied, drug-taking, rule-breaking outcast kid in the Bay Area who starts a band instead of staying in school, and ends up proving all the doubters wrong.","src":"haiku","fable":"Robb Flynn plays autobiography as counterattack: the song stacks a whole ledger of adolescent failure and small-time delinquency not for confession but as evidence, so that the pivot to a signed band lands as a verdict against everyone who wrote him off. The accumulating catalogue is the argument itself, each dead-end credential earning the closing turn where the doubters are the ones proven wrong, and the American high of the title is reframed from a chemical or a rush into the vindication of surviving on your own terms.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title is literal — Flynn attended American High School in Fremont.","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"accumulated failure stacked as evidence against the doubters","built":"the title's high reframed from chemical rush to vindication"},
"machine-head~bastards":{"haiku":"A father's message to his sons about resilience against hatred and authoritarianism; standing ground despite systemic darkness.","opus":"A father warns his sons that injustice sometimes prevails and tells them to hold firm against fear and bigotry, then broadens into a rallying embrace of the marginalized. Robb Flynn's Bay Area band wrote it as a raw response to a darkening political climate, swapping their trademark heaviness for folk-rock defiance and open protest.","sonnet":"A father's letter to his sons written in the aftermath of a political defeat — honest about the darkness winning the day, but refusing to let that be the last word. The song moves from private grief to public defiance: embrace what they fear, stand strange, hold your ground, because the people they tried to exclude are exactly the ones who make the future worth fighting for.","src":"haiku","fable":"A father's lesson to his sons becomes the song's whole architecture: the private, frightened intimacy of the opening — a parent admitting to his children that cruelty sometimes prevails — widens into a public indictment of complicity, then resolves by reclaiming every slur that's ever been used to divide, gathering the despised into a chorus of belonging. The title's refrain is the hinge, a borrowed folk-proverbial charge to stand firm against those who'd wear you down, and the song tracks its ownership across grammatical person: first sung TO the boys, then FOR everyone, until the final chorus shifts into the first person, the child now speaking the vow himself. That pronoun migration is the read — inheritance enacted rather than described, defiance handed from a mortal father to the ones who carry on after he's gone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written in about forty minutes the day after the November 2016 election, out of a real conversation with Flynn's sons — and premiered as a solo acoustic poetry-slam video before the band version existed.","themes":["family & blood","anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"defiance handed from a mortal father to the sons who carry it forward","built":"the pronoun migrates from to the boys to for everyone to the child speaking the vow himself"},
"machine-head~battery-bonus": {"haiku":"Aggression and violent rage as an unstoppable natural force that cannot be controlled or killed, only expressed and perpetuated.","opus":"A cover celebrating a relentless, aggressive force called the battery that crushes the weak and cannot be stopped or killed. It frames this violent energy as something bred within, an unstoppable powerhouse of destruction.","sonnet":"An ode to relentless, self-perpetuating force: the song personifies a crushing, unstoppable energy that feeds on aggression and cannot be killed, framing this power as both family bond and primal drive.","src":"haiku","fable":"Metallica's lyric turns aggression into a self that cannot be switched off — the battery isn't a weapon but a bloodline the singer finds already inside him, an obsession that runs on its own current. Machine Head's choice of it as a bonus track is an act of lineage: an Oakland Bay Area band reaching back to the Bay Area thrash that raised them, so the line about a family you cannot kill reads as tribute as much as menace. The cover claims no authorship of the words; it claims the inheritance the words describe, and the recasting simply makes that ancestry audible.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","family & blood"],"means":"aggression claimed as a bloodline already running before the choice to channel it","built":"the bonus-track placement frames the cover as an act of lineage rather than conquest"},
"machine-head~bay-of-pigs":{"haiku":"A victim of unjust laws and police brutality vows to snap and fight back. He condemns corrupted authority that hides behind power while his friend is broken.","opus":"A working-class fury aimed squarely at police abuse, the narrator refusing to be anyone's scapegoat and vowing to snap the next time authority shakes him down. Machine Head channel Bay Area rage into pure anti-cop defiance.","sonnet":"Rage at police brutality and the laws that protect abusive authority — a friend has been hurt badly enough to maybe never walk again, and the narrator is at the breaking point, vowing not to cower before a system he sees as corrupt and predatory toward ordinary people.","src":"haiku","fable":"The cop-baiting title is a feint: this Bay of Pigs isn't Cuba but a domestic invasion, the pigs being police, and the song's whole engine is a citizen's refusal to keep playing scapegoat. Its two verses build the case from injury outward — first the personal vow not to bend, then the specifics of a friend maybe crippled and a badge that answers only when it wants to shake you down, so that serve-and-protect gets thrown back as accusation rather than creed. The refrain does the ideological work by collapsing law into flaw, indicting not one bad cop but a legal order whose lies are inherited and despised across generations, and the way that stanza fragments and repeats at the end enacts a threat that can no longer form full sentences — the snap the verses keep promising, arriving as breakdown rather than resolution.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"the legal order indicted as an inherited lie across generations","built":"the refrain fragments into breakdown where the threat promised resolution"},
"machine-head~be-still-and-know":{"haiku":"Amid struggle and despair, the song urges resilience and human connection. It insists that hardship is temporary, urging listeners to hold onto each other and endure until dawn breaks.","opus":"An anthem of endurance through hardship, urging connection, unity, and belief that pain will pass, the sun will rise, and defeat can be withstood.","sonnet":"A call to persevere through collective grief and fear, insisting that hardship is temporary, that human connection and shared strength are what allow people to endure, and that dawn will eventually break through the darkest night.","src":"haiku","fable":"Machine Head strip the old devotional line — be still, and know that I am God — down to just be still and know, deliberately withholding the object of that knowing, so the comfort is entirely human and secular: what one must know is only that hardship passes. The verses build a communal survival ethic, grief shared and weathered together, even acknowledging the pretense in that closeness, while the pre-choruses escalate from personal walls to planetary catastrophe — melting glaciers, rising oceans, crashing waves — insisting endurance scales to any threat. The bridge is the hinge: it names the temptation to succumb to numbness and defeat, then answers it by hoisting the head of Goliath, reframing the whole song as a giant-slayer's defiance rather than passive consolation. What looks like a serenity mantra is really a war chant for persistence, the calm of the chorus earned only by the violence of refusing to fall.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Titled from Psalm 46 — the verse Demmel's grandmother wrote on his holiday cards, the mantra of his life by his own telling.","themes":["anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty","nature & the elements"],"means":"serenity mantra recast as a giant-slayer's war chant","built":"the calm chorus is earned only by the bridge's violence"},
"machine-head~beautiful-mourning":{"haiku":"Sorrow and despair consume someone who faces their own violent reflection and confronts the end of hope.","opus":"A descent into depression, self-hatred and suicidal despair, with the narrator not recognizing himself in the mirror, contemplating a razor at his wrist, and bidding a final goodbye.","sonnet":"The narrator describes a person in extreme psychological and emotional collapse — dissociating in front of a mirror, contemplating self-harm, consumed by depression — framing it as a final goodbye.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's pun — mourning as morning, grief dressed up as something lovely — cuts straight to what the song does: it treats the wish to die as a warped redemption, relief framed as the comfort that the ordeal is finally ending. Robb Flynn writes it as a confrontation with a self gone unrecognizable, the mirror handing back a stranger, aggression and depression named side by side as twin engines dragging the narrator under, and the recurring chorus plea about sealing shut a pair of homicidal eyes that glare from within places killer and victim inside one body. Where the song pivots is the closing verse, which quits surrendering and snarls back: branding the loathing of himself as cowardice, refusing to shoulder the cross any longer, electing to endure and climb back out of the grave rather than lie down in it. So the beauty inside the mourning is that the burial turns into a resurrection — the final farewell is aimed not at living but at the disowned, self-slaying version of himself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"self-burial reversed into resurrection at the closing verse","built":"the title pun — mourning as morning — encodes the song's pivot from surrender to refusal"},
"machine-head~behind-a-mask":{"haiku":"Hidden suffering behind performed happiness; the void inside people masked by daily rituals until authentic pain breaks through.","opus":"Bay Area metal band Machine Head shift toward reflection here, sketching two people privately falling apart, a bereaved woman dulling her grief with alcohol and appearances, and an aging man drowning his regrets in drink and self-serving stories. The song asks us to notice the emptiness people bury behind a calm exterior.","sonnet":"Two portraits of people hiding behind performance — a widow who weaponises grief to avoid living, and an old man who drinks and lies to protect his family from his private failure. The song asks them to open their eyes and show the void inside, treating the mask not as protection but as the source of the damage.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two case studies share the same architecture of concealment: a widow curating her sorrow so the mourning need never end, and an aging father drinking his regrets into fiction to shield the household he is actually wounding. The song keeps returning to one gesture, the composed exterior offered to onlookers against the hollowness it exists to cover, and its cruelest turn is that these disguises are not merely defensive but self-sustaining, perfumed vanity in one case and soothing falsehoods that land like small wounds in the other. The refrain refuses third-person distance, its plea to open one's eyes folding singer and listener into the same hiding company, so the reckoning reads as shared rather than merely observed. The bridge offers the sole exit and a bleak one, since you may crawl free of your shell yet the demons still return periodically to feed, framing honesty as the harder recurring cost of being visible rather than any cure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The record's unplugged ballad, full acoustic solo included — Flynn's own framing of the track.","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression","family & blood"],"means":"disguises framed as self-sustaining rather than merely defensive, honesty the harder recurring cost","built":"the refrain folds singer and listener into the same hiding company, refusing third-person distance"},
"machine-head~beneath-the-silt":{"haiku":"Drowning metaphorically and literally represents depression's suffocation, with repeated imagery of sinking below and struggling to survive.","opus":"Machine Head render depression as slow drowning, following someone dragged under a heavy tide of grief and pleading to be rescued. The images of being pulled below the surface and reaching helplessly upward leave no doubt this is about despair swallowing a person whole.","sonnet":"A straightforward metaphor for depression as drowning — the mind fills with dark water, the horizon disappears, and the narrator is sinking beneath accumulated silt with no way to climb back up.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song externalizes clinical depression as a drowning at sea, letting the ocean imagery do the naming so the affliction never has to be stated plainly except once, when the tide of water-language briefly parts to let the actual word through. The two verses tighten the frame from weather to funeral — rain and undertow give way to a casket drifting from shore — while the chorus keeps confusing rescue and surrender, since to sink beneath silt is both a plea and a wish. The bridge is where the read lives: the frantic, repeated clawing at walls collides head-on with the four flat repetitions of going under, staging the exact deadlock of a panic that thrashes and a despair that has already stopped moving. By the final chorus the two verbs simply alternate without resolution, drowning and sinking traded back and forth, so the song ends not in a decision but in the endless oscillation itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","suicide","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"rescue and surrender confused because the plea and the wish share one verb","built":"the bridge collides frantic clawing against flat drowning repetitions, staging the deadlock structurally"},
"machine-head~beyond-the-pale":{"haiku":"Rejecting mainstream society and its expectations, the speaker finds freedom and identity among outsiders and rebels, reclaiming power through non-conformity.","opus":"Machine Head deliver an outcast's anthem of self-made deliverance, voicing someone scarred and living outside society's lines who refuses to bow to the hatred aimed at him. He builds his own world instead, defiantly at home among fellow misfits he treats as his heroes, standing firm through whatever storm hits.","sonnet":"A defiant outsider anthem — having been beaten down and excluded by a world run on hate and conformity, the speaker finds solidarity among the misfits and freaks, carving their own path and claiming the margins as a space of liberation rather than shame.","src":"haiku","fable":"The pale once meant the staked border of governed land, and to pass it was to forfeit belonging; the song turns that banishment inside out, claiming the wilderness past the fence as the only place worth calling home. Its verses render a man worn down to raw remnants — cracked stone, wounds worn openly — yet insist every mark proves a self shaped by his own hands rather than handed to him, so his outsider standing is chosen defiance, never a sentence endured. A quieter refrain keeps returning to water pouring over him, and its weight shifts as the track moves: first the tempest he simply won't shelter from, later a heart going under, confessing that the freedom-granting refusal is also what strands and nearly submerges him. His deliverance is assembled from the cast-off and the overlooked, and the bravado of his contempt is the covering stretched over a solitude that downpour keeps quietly naming.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Flynn called the riff's resemblance to Strapping Young Lad's Love? a happy accident.","themes":["freedom & escape","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"wilderness past the boundary claimed as home rather than endured as sentence","built":"the water refrain shifts meaning as the track moves, first a tempest then a quiet drowning"},
"machine-head~bite-the-bullet":{"haiku":"Encourages endurance and fierce determination, transforming hatred into fuel for overcoming weakness and breaking free from constraints.","opus":"A defiant call to endure pain and hardship, turning heart to steel and channeling hate into the will to win, urging a lion-hearted refusal to give in to weakness.","sonnet":"The song is a direct address urging someone to convert pain and hatred into strength, to push through inner battles with ferocity and determination rather than yield.","src":"haiku","fable":"What lifts this above ordinary gym-metal is one structural choice: the whole conflict sits inside the listener, so there is no rival on the field and every triumph becomes a triumph over the self. Flynn recasts the genre's stock fury as psychology — the anger is only a wish to prevail wearing a meaner mask, the chains a fighter breaks turn out self-forged. So ferocity here reads as discipline rather than damage. The refrain's barked commands aim not at an opponent but at a person's own ceiling, and the predator's pulse that stays restless is unsatisfied with the speaker himself, never with anyone across the ring.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"genre fury reclassified as discipline when the enemy is only the self","built":"the barked commands aim at the speaker's own ceiling, making the predator whose pulse stays restless the one across from the mirror"},
"machine-head~blank-generation":{"haiku":"A generation grows up in a corrupted world of greed and violence, losing innocence to consume artificial desires, corporate control, and despair, becoming hollow and cynical.","opus":"Robb Flynn's disillusioned reckoning with a numbed, desensitized generation, tracing the loss of childhood innocence into a world of corruption, plastic vanity and casual violence. The toast is bitter irony, mourning a culture medicating and destroying itself while the hole inside never fills.","sonnet":"A generational eulogy — the narrator mourns the death of childhood innocence and catalogues the rot of modern society (gun violence, corporate food, Hollywood unreality, police corruption) as a toast to a generation that grew up desensitised and empty.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song shares its title with Richard Hell's punk anthem and flips its meaning: where Hell's blank was open-ended possibility, Machine Head's is a hollowing-out, tracking a single narrator from a child inventing his own days to a seventeen-year-old under a San Francisco sky who suddenly sees too much. Innocence doesn't fade here so much as get processed out, and the long closing movement pivots from personal loss to a bitter toast that catalogues an entire culture's artificial fixes, engineered bodies, and secondhand violence as the true content of the blank — not empty of promise but scoured empty by consumption and cruelty. The mask is the raised-glass celebration; underneath, the toast is a mourning, its final embrace of the rot on display less triumph than surrender by a generation that has learned to feel nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"An original, not a Richard Hell cover — it demoed under the working title The Drive; the shared title is an echo the band never confirmed as homage.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","politics & society"],"means":"the raised-glass toast masking an entire generation's mourning","built":"the title borrows and inverts Richard Hell's blank from promise to scoured emptiness"},
"machine-head~blistering":{"haiku":"Deep betrayal and rage consume someone, driving them toward revenge. They live for the burning hatred and the pain it brings, viewing their suffering as their reason to keep fighting.","opus":"Betrayal over something trivial curdles into pure vengeance, broken hands clenching into fists as fury rises from a lifetime of pain and fantasies of burial in a shallow grave. Machine Head, the Bay Area groove-metal outfit led by Robb Flynn, deliver the theme with blunt, riff-driven aggression, suffering rendered divine and revenge claimed without apology.","sonnet":"Betrayal ignites a consuming, systematic rage that has accumulated from a life of accumulated pain. Revenge becomes something almost sacred in its certainty — a force that tears through the body and will not be bargained away, violent in its clarity and unapologetic about the depth of the wound.","src":"haiku","fable":"Machine Head strip revenge down to its mechanics here, and the payoff is that the target keeps changing places: the opening betrayal over something trivially small (a sliver) curdles into a fantasy of executing the betrayer and sinking the weighted body in a river, yet the physical damage in the song — the broken hands, the fractures splitting bone, the rage clenched into fists — is all borne by the narrator himself, so the violence he plans on another is the same violence eating him alive. That collapse is the read: the chorus's refrain of not caring is the mask, and what's underneath is a man defined entirely by a life of pain he cannot release, mistaking the tearing-through of his own body for triumph. The flatness of the writing is almost the point — revenge offered as pure sensation with no arc toward release, only the loop of the same wound.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"the revenge fantasy consuming the one who holds it","built":"the broken hands and fractures are all the narrator's, not the target's"},
"machine-head~block":{"haiku":"Hatred and corruption feed systems of control while media and ignorance keep people blind to the destruction of the poor.","opus":"An angry protest against societal rot, racism, poverty, pollution, and media manipulation, raging that cruelty only spawns more cruelty and the machine gorges on the blood and money of the poor while people are kept blind.","sonnet":"The song catalogues systemic failures — racial division, media manipulation, poverty, corruption, pollution — and expresses total disgust with a society that perpetuates suffering while keeping people ignorant and blind.","src":"haiku","fable":"The shout is the thesis: block is what the song both diagnoses and enacts. Early Machine Head builds a catalog of 90s rot — racial violence, media anesthesia, drugs, the poor bled to feed a machine — but the payoff isn't the grievance list, it's the refrain that intelligence and awareness are themselves the wound: the sharper your sense of the ignorance around you, the sharper the pain. So the shouted title becomes an instruction to shut it out, and the closing surrender to just quitting on all of it reads less as rebellion than as the only anesthetic left when believing in something is reduced to whatever stops the hurting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","politics & society","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"awareness itself named as the sharpest wound","built":"the title is both diagnosis and instruction — block what you see to stop the pain of seeing it","fnote":"Flynn has said the song deals with racism and the '92 riots — and the title wasn't the band's choice: the label refused Fuck It All, so the other refrain got promoted to the cover. It was among the first songs written for the band."},
"machine-head~blood-for-blood":{"haiku":"Betrayal by close ally and violent retribution for violation of trust.","opus":"Bay Area groove metal distilling a street code where respect is currency and betrayal gets answered in kind. Beneath the aggression is an origin story: rage forged from frustration and hard knocks, presented as the only law a wronged man ever learned.","sonnet":"Demanding respect through physical retaliation against a false friend who exploited and betrayed the narrator.","src":"haiku","fable":"Machine Head's 1994 debut Burn My Eyes distilled Bay Area thrash into something heavier and more claustrophobic, and this track is its code of honor stripped to a single equation: betrayal gets repaid in kind, and the currency is bodily. The verses are pure aggressor-voice, a man vowing to turn a former friend's face into an altar where his own suffering is redeemed, but the bridge quietly explains where that appetite came from, tracing the cruelty back to a childhood of pressure, resentment, and violence absorbed until it hardened into reflex. What reads as a threat is really a confession of inheritance: the singer isn't so much choosing brutality as passing along the only lesson anyone ever taught him, which is why the chant lands less like a boast than like a law he has no power to repeal.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","friendship & loyalty","money & the street"],"means":"the threat is a confession of inherited cruelty with no power to refuse","fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Per the anniversary tellings this one predates the band — written in Flynn's Vio-lence days, one of the three songs (with Death Church and Block) on the boombox demo that got Machine Head started."},
"machine-head~blood-of-the-zodiac":{"haiku":"A wise person spreads racist ideology that deceives many; the speaker rejects this hatred and claims their own moment to rise against ignorance and bigotry.","opus":"A blind man, a bitter addict, and a false messiah preaching racist rhetoric populate a narrator's furious reckoning with mistreatment and ignorance, building toward a repeated vow to rise. Machine Head channel their groove-metal aggression into indictment, casting the speaker's revenge against lies and dominance as the moment he claims his time to rise.","sonnet":"Two portraits — a broken man whose addiction becomes his only god, and a charismatic demagogue seeding racial hatred — intersect around the shared experience of feeling mistreated and invisible. The speaker's fury at both figures fuses into a rallying cry for those who refuse to be consumed by either self-destruction or ideological poison.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song threads a private overdose narrative into a public one: a man whose whole faith was the syringe reaches for a gun instead, and that same craving for annihilation gets projected outward onto a demagogue seeding a racist creed with promises that dissolve into noise. What binds the addict and the mob is a refusal to inhabit the world as given — to perceive, feel, or become anyone's version of sane — so the closing chant to rise reads doubly, as both a recovery vow and the ugly swell of a crowd learning it can. The read underneath is that revenge and rebirth speak the same syllable here, and the narrator can't fully tell his own resurrection apart from the thing he despises.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","politics & society","faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"resurrection and the crowd-swell speak the same syllable","built":"the recovery chant doubles as the ugly swell it means to oppose"},
"machine-head~bulldozer":{"haiku":"Celebrates unstoppable force and refusing to be broken by opposition, embodying a crushing power that destroys weaker forces. The song glorifies relentless forward momentum against all odds.","opus":"An aggressive anthem of defiance and relentless drive, refusing to submit to anyone who tries to give orders. It casts the self as a bulldozer that crushes the weak and pushes against the odds and the grain, trusting instinct and living to win.","sonnet":"A declaration of relentless forward drive against obstacles and conformity — the bulldozer is a metaphor for refusing to yield, following instinct over instruction, and pushing through resistance at full force regardless of what stands in the way.","src":"haiku","fable":"A self-motivation anthem in the classic Machine Head mode — Robb Flynn preaching guts, integrity, and refusal to bow to anyone who hands him orders — yet the title quietly complicates the sermon. The nonconformist creed here is the band's familiar underdog gospel, all clenched jaw and defiance, but the machine Flynn picks as its emblem is one that doesn't merely push back against the world; it levels everything in its lane, fattening itself on whatever it grinds under. That's the song's real seam: it wants to be a hymn to the lone holdout who won't kneel, and it half-knows that an unstoppable force and a predatory one leave the same track in the dirt. The closing mantra flattens any nuance anyway, chanting its plow-through-the-odds refrain until the argument dissolves into pure momentum — which is exactly what heavy machinery does, and exactly what the song ends up confessing itself to be.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","violence & murder"],"means":"underdog gospel that half-confesses its own predatory momentum","built":"closing mantra dissolves argument into the very machinery it praises"},
"machine-head~bulletproof":{"haiku":"Critique of American capitalism, racism, and political hypocrisy; collective awakening to injustice and commitment to be unbreakable.","opus":"Machine Head, led by Robb Flynn, aim their Bay Area groove metal at a fracturing America, denouncing the power-drunk wealthy, complicit press, hypocritical religious moralists, and a system that turns a profit on every death. The band answer with a vow to stay unbreakable and honest, the chant of unity in struggle transforming their fury into a call for collective solidarity.","sonnet":"A political fury at American structural rot — police violence, media distortion, stock markets profiting from death, billionaires protecting their own at the expense of everyone below. The resolution is collective armor: not invulnerability but solidarity, the body remaining unbroken by taking its neck out of the noose together and setting fire to the system that built it.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-head~california-bleeding":{"haiku":"California bleeds the speaker dry—from San Francisco to LA, the state and its inhabitants drain life and money while he rages against it all.","opus":"A gutter-level, drug-soaked road trip through California, celebrating and savaging the state as a broke outsider surrounded by wealth. The refrain of being cut open and stitched back together frames California as a beautiful parasite bleeding him dry, rats coming west to feed on stress and infest.","sonnet":"A vicious, affectionate rant about California — its class divide, its cities' dysfunction, its beautiful chaos — the speaker is simultaneously repulsed and addicted, bleeding out into the state's excess and violence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tour of California that keeps mistaking a hemorrhage for a good time: the singer names his poverty up front, then rattles off the state as a series of highway exits, drugs, and cheap thrills, the itinerary itself standing in for a life bleeding out one pit stop at a time. The chorus makes the violence literal and consensual at once — he asks to be cut open and stitched back together, an addict's cycle where the state drains him and he keeps coming back for more, blaming the damage on anything but himself. The turn comes in the bridge, where the first-person junkie becomes a first-person plural swarm of rats coming west to infest and feed on stress; the private dependency reveals itself as a whole migratory hunger, the Golden State's promise inverted into a place that welcomes everyone precisely because it consumes them. What sounds like a raucous party is a suicide note set to a road map, and the flat refusal to care by nightfall is the mask — underneath is someone who has decided the bleeding is the point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The music is mostly Phil Demmel's — and the song later became a flashpoint in his split from the band, cited when he called Machine Head a Robb Flynn solo project.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","suicide"],"means":"the state that drains him welcomed back because the bleeding is the point","built":"the first-person junkie expands mid-song into a whole migratory swarm of rats coming west"},
"machine-head~catharsis":{"haiku":"Emotional purging through music and resistance, transforming internal struggle and exploitation into aggressive creative outlet.","opus":"A defiant purge of hollowness and hurt, the narrator refusing to be an anonymous cog and vowing to battle for survival, treating music as the last thing holding his sanity together. Words become weapons, release becomes salvation. The Bay Area metal band built this title track on exactly that, aggression rerouted into a hard-won act of self-rescue.","sonnet":"Refusing to be broken by someone who has watched and wanted to see it happen — music as the last thing keeping the speaker from collapse, language as a fist against a force that keeps pushing them down. The catharsis of the title is the release of all that pressure outward, into sound, rather than inward into defeat.","src":"haiku","fable":"Machine Head's Robb Flynn writes catharsis as a survival transaction, casting the enemy as a second-person you three ways at once: an oppressor grinding him into the ground, a personal addiction, and a spectator waiting to watch him fail. The fight is therefore inseparable from the dependency, the muse he abuses being the same force he swears will never get its chance. The song moves from emptiness through defiance, that arsonist who would rather burn it all and rebuild than accept another's ceiling, into its real thesis. There the release named in the title turns out to be not violence but expression itself: the music in his veins keeps him sane, the words become fists, and the catharsis he demands you feel is this very act of saying it. The closing repetitions aren't padding but the mechanism working in real time, a man asking again and again whether you can feel it because the asking is the discharge.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Flynn's thesis track — the words-as-fists line is the one he cites as the whole album's crystallizing image.","fnote2":"Co-produced with Zack Ohren at Oakland's Sharkbite Studios; the last record with Phil Demmel and Dave McClain, who both quit that September.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"expression itself named as the catharsis, the words becoming fists in real time","built":"the closing repetitions are not padding but the mechanism working aloud, discharging as it asks"},
"machine-head~ch-ke-n-the-ashes-f-y-ur-hate": {"haiku":"Societal rot and hypocrisy demand violent uprising as the only response to systemic corruption and cascading decay.","opus":"A furious survey of a poisoned society choking on weaponized lies, spoon-fed venom while fear is beaten into compliance. The reckoning lands too late and damaged, declaring open war on the whole rotting hypocrisy.","sonnet":"A furious response to the social and political rot of a society addicted to lies, conspiracy, violence, and hypocrisy — the speaker declares that the hate-mongers will choke on their own ideology as everything collapses into chaos.","src":"haiku","fable":"Flynn conducts a bitter tour of a country eating itself — belief turned into a weapon, refugees mocked at the border, race baited for sport — and the title is the curse he spits back at whoever lit the fire: swallow the burnt remains of what your own venom made. The song's cruelest image is chemical, hate force-fed like a sedative down the throat until the fury it names is also the numbness that lets it spread, a pharmacy of pacification passed off as cure. Then the point of view drops out of accusation into wreckage: the perpetrators vanish and the voice becomes the collateral, the maimed who inherit the fallout, and their answer is not repair but surrender to the same violence — care burned off, only war left. That last turn carries the read's whole weight, an indictment that finally can't stand outside the ruin it diagnoses and joins the mob it was condemning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","war & battle"],"means":"accuser absorbed into the wreckage they were diagnosing","built":"point of view drops from outside indictment into first-person collateral mid-song"},
"machine-head~circle-the-drain":{"haiku":"Romantic dissolution through mutual addiction and deception; two people spiraling downward together unable to break the cycle.","opus":"A brutally honest post-mortem of a doomed relationship wrecked by cocaine, lies and words that always came out wrong, the narrator blaming himself as the couple spins helplessly down the drain. Its cathartic refrain lashes out to be left alone. Machine Head, the Bay Area metal veterans, ground this in raw personal wreckage, coupling their heaviness with an unusually confessional account of love numbed and circling toward its end.","sonnet":"A relationship recognised as mutually destructive long after the point of graceful exit — both people numbing the pain, saying the wrong things, circling downward. The speaker blames themselves, lets the relationship go, but the specific memory of last September in a car and hearing I gotta go keeps surfacing no matter how hard they try to clear their head.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-head~clenching-the-fists-of-dissent":{"haiku":"Anti-war protest against militarism, propaganda, and senseless violence.","opus":"An anti-war protest against government propaganda, patriotic manipulation, and blood spilled for oil, urging nonviolent revolution.","sonnet":"Anti-war rage against government propaganda, corporate war profiteering, and the sacrifice of young lives for oil and power.","src":"haiku","fable":"For most of this Blackening-era epic Robb Flynn barks commands at a crowd, turning anger into a tool and gritted teeth into a weapon. Then the chorus twists the recruiter's promise that liberty is bought with corpses into an accusation rather than a salute. Its logic boasts that argument can be ammunition without a trigger, yet the marathon of one shouted syllable keeps shoving toward the very stampede it means to condemn. The rescue arrives late, when the barking preacher dissolves into a shaken man who confesses he holds no answers, never wanted the podium, and stands as ruined and afraid as those he commanded. That collapse recasts the whole tirade, so the clenched hand of the ending grips not triumph but a battered, doubting hope.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","shame & repression"],"means":"the recruiter's fury dismantled by the frightened man beneath","built":"one shouted syllable sustained into the stampede it condemns","fnote":"The Blackening's opener is the band's Iraq protest — Flynn researched the war, decided the case didn't add up, and braced for the backlash before committing the album to it."},
"machine-head~crashing-around-you":{"haiku":"Internal trauma and self-doubt are personified as an attacking force that undermines the victim's confidence and relationships. The song reveals that this destructive entity is the victim's own consciousness.","opus":"The song voices the inner tormentor of self-doubt and self-hatred, the shadow that whispers you're never good enough and makes your world crash down. Its final turn reveals the accuser is the listener's own self.","sonnet":"The song is narrated by a person's own inner voice of self-doubt and self-destruction, describing itself as the source of nightmares, shame, paranoia, and the feeling that nothing is ever good enough. The twist at the end reveals the tormenting presence is the self: \"I am you.\"","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is sung entirely in the voice of self-loathing personified — a first-person antagonist that seems to be an external tormentor until the final line collapses the distance, revealing the accuser was the self all along. Machine Head build the whole track as a slow trap: the entity catalogues its cruelties as if it were a stalker or abuser, working through the vocabulary of falling dreams and the shadow that mirrors every move, until the demand to stop hiding lands with vicious circularity because there is nowhere to hide from your own mind. The genius of the structure is that every second-person threat is really a first-person confession, so the mounting refrain of a world smashing down reads less as attack than as depression describing its own mechanism — the voice that insists you are never good enough is the one you trusted, fed, and left your thoughts to. The turn is the last three words, which retroactively rewrite the entire song from horror into diagnosis: the nightmare has no author but you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The lead single was the most-added rock track in the country when 9/11 hit; MTV and radio pulled it for the crashing imagery and its burning-skyline video.","themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the tormentor revealed as the narrator's own mind","built":"every second-person threat retroactively becomes a first-person confession"},
"machine-head~damage-inside":{"haiku":"Deep depression leaves internal wounds too painful to show others; the narrator withdraws into numbness and shame, unable to face connection.","opus":"A stark confession of depression, the narrator numbed and hollowed, unable to meet a loved one's eyes because he can't bear them seeing how far gone he is. Robb Flynn drops the aggression for something quiet and genuinely bleak, longing just to feel alive again.","sonnet":"About severe depression as a private inner dissolution — the narrator is too numb to feel anything, unable to make eye contact because he doesn't want others to see how far gone he is, demons pulling him into a hole while the world goes on around him.","src":"haiku","fable":"The rain becomes an audible metronome against the skin while the speaker dissolves downward into a colorless nothing, personifying his despair as a lover whose kiss never ends and whose company leaves him seated, silent, sensory-shut. Underneath the plea for warmth runs the real confession: he cannot meet another's gaze, not from shame at being seen but from dread of what the seeing would reveal — a wound he has kept private through a long, unexplained absence. The second movement lets the metaphors turn medieval and gothic, demons hauling him into a pit where parasites feed and violins score an ache passed down through generations of broken hearts, so the private collapse widens into something inherited rather than merely his own.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","shame & repression","family & blood"],"means":"dread of being seen rather than shame at what would be seen beneath the wound","built":"the medieval gothic second movement widens a private collapse into something inherited"},
"machine-head~darkness-within":{"haiku":"A broken man struggles with depression and guilt, questioning faith and searching for redemption through music as his only salvation. He frames himself as deserving disgrace and builds cathedrals of pain to transcend suffering.","opus":"A shattered man ground down by depression and eroding faith gropes for redemption, speaking to his children and a silent God, and finds deliverance not in religion but in music, raising monuments out of his suffering and naming song his rescue against the despair inside him. Robb Flynn's Oakland metal band trade fury for confession here.","sonnet":"A broken, depressed narrator finds that organized religion offers no comfort, and turns instead to music as the only true salvation — a personal god built from notes and melodies that can lift him out of despair.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confessional that turns worship inside out: a father wracked by depression and guilt kneels onstage screaming at a heaven that stays silent, and finds the old faith drained empty. So he relocates the divine, swapping the church for the amp: if no god answers, then reverence poured into notes and chords becomes the only rescue left. The chorus names the mechanism coldly, that we raise grand structures over our wounds precisely to keep from going under, and the bridge strips the theology down to a raw, repeated cry for deliverance addressed to sound itself. The tragedy underneath the anthem is that this salvation is chosen by elimination, not conviction, a man clinging to melody because every other savior went mute.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"For this record Flynn took his first singing lessons after nineteen untrained years — from Don Lawrence, coach to Lady Gaga and Jagger — and this became the band's first song to chart at Mainstream Rock.","themes":["faith & the occult","madness & the mind","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"the amp replaces god when heaven goes mute","built":"the bridge addresses the cry for deliverance to sound itself"},
"machine-head~davidian":{"haiku":"A person hardens themselves against pain and disrespect, feeding on suffering and hatred until they become consumed by rage and violence.","opus":"A defiant refusal to submit, channeling pain and street-bred rage into strength and calling for freedom won by violence. Suffering becomes purity and fuel, ending on the image of a wound with salt poured in it.","sonnet":"A speaker fuelled by rage and hardship turns suffering into strength, feeding on pain and hatred to declare a violent, defiant freedom — the repeated shotgun-blast refrain signals a break from victimhood toward an unforgiving sense of power.","src":"haiku","fable":"Machine Head's 1994 opening statement takes the era's Waco-inflected title and turns it into a psychology of survival by hardening: the speaker refuses to seek absolution from the sightless judge set over him, converts inflicted suffering into wealth, and force-feeds himself pain until hatred reads as purity and dread becomes his one source of power. The signature refrain, liberty's bell answered by a shotgun, is deliberately double-edged — it can sound like liberation and like massacre in the same breath, and the song leaves the menace of that ambiguity intact rather than resolving which it means. The escalation runs from swallowing pain whole to the bullet finally connecting, a trajectory where the street-pounded self answers brutality only by becoming more brutal. The bridge's turn from scarred to unscarred, salt ground into the open cut, is the grim payoff: what does not kill him doesn't heal but calcifies, damage reframed as invulnerability.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"damage calcified into invulnerability rather than healed","built":"liberty's bell answered by a shotgun held deliberately double-edged","fnote":"The war-cry refrain was written overnight: the song premiered at an Oakland warehouse party with a different chorus Flynn decided was terrible, and the shotgun line replaced it by morning. After the 2017 Las Vegas shooting he said he never wanted to play it again.","fnote2":"The opener is the record's newest thought — written last of all its songs — while the closer Block was among the first, one of the three boombox demos the band was founded on."},
"machine-head~days-turn-blue-to-gray": {"haiku":"A tragic portrait of parental neglect and its generational damage. The speaker reflects on a father's absence, their own descent into addiction and self-destruction, and the guilt of perpetuating harm—now with no closure.","opus":"A bitter reckoning with a self-destructive, addicted parent whose drinking and neglect paralyzed and scarred their children, mixing rage, grief, and an unanswered plea for love and closure.","sonnet":"A confrontation with an absent, alcoholic and drug-addicted father who abandoned his children — the narrator traces the damage done across a childhood of crayons and clay that turned grey, ending with a plea for love and grief over a wound that never closed.","src":"haiku","fable":"Robb Flynn tracks addiction down a bloodline, indicting a father who sired children but abandoned the raising of them, then watching the damage recur when the child hits seventeen and inherits the same bottle and needle. The song's two image-families do the arguing: the soft materials of a small kid's afternoon on one side, the ritual of the glass tipped to the lips and the swallowed soul on the other, with the withering family vine as the hinge between innocence and rot. Structurally it shifts from third-person accusation into first-person pleading — the abandoned son begging to be loved, asking what he did wrong — before the closure he needs is refused outright, the father dead and the guilt left behind with nothing settled. That final turn, naming the man both destroyer and self-destroyer, is Flynn's real subject: the fury that a parent's private ruin was never only his own but was spent on the futures of the kids he made.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","family & blood","anger & defiance"],"means":"a father's private ruin spent on the futures of the children he made","built":"third-person accusation shifts to first-person pleading as the abandoned son steps into frame"},
"machine-head~deafening-silence":{"haiku":"A person drowns in inner emptiness and trauma despite external connections, carrying deep wounds and scars that hollow them out like silence itself is killing them.","opus":"A wrenchingly personal account of depression, the silence of isolation itself becoming what's killing him. Between forced smiles and private tears, it refuses sympathy while pleading only for the emptiness to stop.","sonnet":"A man admits his partner sees through the performance of confidence to the insecurity underneath — the silence inside him is louder than anything external, an emptiness he keeps trying to fill but can't.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track trades Robb Flynn's usual outward fury for a claustrophobic confessional, and the paradox in the title is the whole song: it is not noise but the absence of it that overwhelms — the empty space where distraction used to be, roaring back the moment the drink and the false cheer wear off. Flynn splits himself between the mask and the man, the smiled laughter over the wept tears, so that the deafening silence is the depressive's own unbearable interior once every self-medicating fill drains away, escalating from private ache to the flat equation of that emptiness with suicide. The refusal of sympathy is key: this is less a plea than an admission he cannot voice to the woman watching him hide it, the read of the mask AND the man drowning underneath it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the silence that floods back once every self-medicating fill drains","built":"title's paradox enacted: absence of noise as the overwhelming thing"},
"machine-head~death-church":{"haiku":"Organized religion profits from faith while preaching hypocrisy and pain, manipulating the masses for money. The speaker resists this system and chooses standing truth over kneeling belief.","opus":"Opening with the Manson-trial notion that music gets blamed for society's rot, it turns into an attack on predatory televangelist religion that takes and never gives. The stance is hard-won defiance: bloodied by the system but standing, refusing to grovel.","sonnet":"An attack on predatory televangelism and institutional religion that takes money, offers nothing real, and grovels — the narrator has been jailed and beaten down by a system he refuses to go deaf to, choosing strength and dignity over submissive faith.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song builds a self-made ethic from the ashes of institutional betrayal: a narrator hardened by prison and beatdown who refuses the numbing trinity of deaf, blind, and mute submission. The intro's radio-panic ventriloquism, blaming heavy music for youth rebellion, sets the frame the song then flips, so the real corruptor turns out to be televangelism, which peddles God as a fix to mainline while the collection plate drains the desperate. The recurring dirge that names the church as death, hate, and lostness converts an abstraction into a rotting body rising like the undead, and the closing verse trades pity for defiance, pitting a personal code of nerve and truth against a faith the narrator judges a taker, then wagering that he will meet his end upright while the pious meet theirs abased.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Per Flynn's own anniversary telling, this was one of the two songs Vio-lence rejected — the refusal that pushed him out the door to form Machine Head. By the same telling, the first song Machine Head ever wrote.","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","politics & society"],"means":"a personal code of nerve wagered against a faith judged a taker","built":"the radio intro blaming music for youth rebellion is the frame the song then flips"},
"machine-head~declaration":{"haiku":"Maxim of perpetual readiness; in peace prepare for war, in war prepare for peace. The song is a stark call to vigilance and duality without elaboration.","opus":"A short, martial mantra from Machine Head built on the old paradox that lasting peace demands readiness for war and wartime demands readiness for peace, driving to a battle cry.","sonnet":"A short, stark declaration built on the military maxim that peace requires preparation for war and war requires preparation for peace, ending in a single word of charge. It is a statement of readiness and vigilance with no further elaboration.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is a chiasmus of the old Roman maxim turned inside out: peace demands you ready yourself for war, and war demands you ready yourself for peace — a closed loop with no exit, drilled by repetition until the two states become interchangeable and the preparation, not the peace or the war, is the only permanent condition. Machine Head strip the platitude to its skeleton and let the sheer looping do the arguing: this is not wisdom being offered but a mind trapped in its own vigilance, unable to stand down. The lone break in the pattern is the final bark of command that ends it — the point where all that circular readiness stops being contemplation and discharges into pure action, the drill collapsing into the order it was always secretly for.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","madness & the mind"],"means":"vigilance that cannot distinguish readiness from the war itself","built":"looping repetition makes preparation the only permanent condition"},
"machine-head~descend-the-shades-of-night": {"haiku":"Confrontation with mortality and inherited hatred. Night descends as death approaches; the song grapples with shame and weakness before calling for freedom.","opus":"A brooding reckoning with mortality as darkness falls, the narrator accepting that his time on earth will one day be over while a personified Death drapes a burial mask over him. He grapples with self-hatred, shame, and frailty, spurred by faith to press on and find release.","sonnet":"In the dark before death, the speaker confronts mortality, self-doubt, and shame, but anchors himself through faith and the discipline to swallow resentment rather than drown in it. The recurring image of night descending frames both the fear and the acceptance of an end that is inevitable.","src":"haiku","fable":"Robb Flynn closes Machine Head's comeback record — the album that hauled the band back from near-collapse — with a nightfall vigil that is really a suicide note refused mid-sentence. A man sits motionless as the last daylight drains off, and mortality arrives personified as a seamstress who stitches a funeral veil onto his living face, its eyes leaking red until the whole horizon runs with it; the song dresses extinction up as radiance, which is the mask this narrator keeps almost putting on. The middle collapses inward — fog over the mind, self-disgust curdling insecurity into loathing of his own frailty — and then swerves on a borrowed Frost image, the harder path deliberately taken, choosing to gulp the venom of resentment rather than let it drown him. That survival is not private: the closing chant conscripts the listener to howl and suffer and sing alongside him, an Oakland groove-metal exorcism where the plea to be released is aimed not at death but at the crowd. The refrain of encroaching darkness works less as a threat than as a tide the narrator has decided to keep outlasting, one return at a time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"extinction dressed as radiance — the mask almost put on, then refused mid-sentence","built":"the closing chant conscripts the crowd to howl and outlast the dark alongside the narrator"},
"machine-head~desire-to-fire":{"haiku":"The song transforms desire into chaos and dominance, celebrating the power to destroy and rise above. The speaker dares listeners to fail and join them at the top.","opus":"A late-nineties rap-metal statement of self-belief and dominance, turning ambition and the courage to fail into fuel; Machine Head plant a defiant seed of chaos and crown, daring the listener to rise or be left behind.","sonnet":"A manifesto of artistic and personal dominance: the band channels chaos as creative fuel, turning desire into fire and positioning themselves as forces that don't just follow the pace but set it, daring others to match them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rap-metal manifesto that runs on conquest logic: the whole track is an engine converting appetite into ignition, drawing recruits into a machine that thrives by risking the fall others won't take. Underneath the swagger of intoxication and domination sits a genuinely vulnerable pitch — an outstretched hand asking a listener to walk alongside and gamble on defeat — so the bravado about chewing up the weak is also the mask over a lonely creed that needs one other person to affirm it. The image-system stays kinetic throughout: current running along wire, riots against the power grid, a pen striking paper like a detonation, motion that never once resolves into arrival, only acceleration.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the dare to recruit disguised as a dominance claim","built":"motion perpetually accelerates but never resolves into arrival"},
"machine-head~devil-with-the-king-s-card":{"haiku":"The speaker burns with conviction and pain others can't understand, destroying anything blocking their path while making rivals fall.","opus":"A menacing declaration of dominance in which the narrator vows to dictate a rival's fortunes and destroy anything in his path, fueled by a suffering the other could never grasp. It's aimed at a boaster who gave up and couldn't live up to his own trash talk, turning the song into a taunting power play.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a rival or betrayer — the speaker, burning with conviction and pain, promises to witness the other's rise and fall, calls out their quitting and boasting, and claims the band's success as proof of who had real staying power.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is a taunt built as ritual, and its whole meaning lives in the recurring incantation that frames it: the singer promising to see, be, make, and create another person's rise and fall, escalating from mere witness to author until he claims he will become the other outright. That refrain turns a personal grudge into a cosmic power-claim, and the verses cash it out as a betrayal reckoning against someone who bragged and then couldn't back their own words. The tenderness that surfaces late, the flicker of actually feeling hurt, is immediately swallowed back into contempt, so the mask of total dominance never fully drops even when it cracks. What the title's borrowed authority names, Robb Flynn resolves bluntly by putting the band's own name in the mouth of the threat, collapsing narrator into Machine Head taking it all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"A diss aimed at Logan Mader, the guitarist who quit as the sessions began; the title reportedly a phrase Mader used for Flynn during a psychosis episode. His playing survives on the record anyway — a riff of Mader's lives on in I Defy.","themes":["anger & defiance","faith & the occult"],"means":"the grudge escalates from witness to author to becoming the other","built":"the band's name inserted into the threat collapses narrator into Machine Head"},
"machine-head~die-young-acoustic":{"haiku":"Mortality consciousness exposes existence as illusion; escape through death becomes preferable to prolonged awareness of inevitable defeat.","opus":"A cover of Black Sabbath's plea to live fully for today because tomorrow is never guaranteed. Freedom is an illusion locked away, so the only answer is to seize the moment before death arrives.","sonnet":"An exhortation to live fully in the present because tomorrow is an illusion — the wind won't carry you, your back is to the wall — and beneath the fantasy of freedom lies the certainty of early death, so the only answer is to live for today.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-head~do-or-die":{"haiku":"Unapologetic commitment to artistic vision despite criticism; death before compromise or becoming anything other than authentic.","opus":"A furious rejection of detractors, copycats and the online metal commentariat, sneering at clip-culture critics while insisting the music is lifeblood rather than income. The bravado slides into an alarming portrait of a darkened, violent interior. The veteran Bay Area groove-metal frontman aims this at a community he believes shortchanged him.","sonnet":"A defiant strike against critics and genre gatekeepers — the band turns every dismissal into fuel, insisting that metal runs in their blood and no amount of online noise or scene politics will extinguish it. The mood is volcanic: contempt curdling into a declaration that survival itself is a form of victory.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-head~down-to-none":{"haiku":"The speaker refuses subservience and endures rage, struggle, and pain without surrendering. His resistance is a cold feeling consuming him, but he will not yield.","opus":"A defiant refusal to kneel to anyone, delivered from inside a numbed fog and a collapsing world. The through-line is turning anguish into resistance, treating consuming rage and struggle as something he cannot surrender to, letting pain carry him onward instead of crushing him.","sonnet":"Refusing to submit to anyone or anything — the narrator is sleepwalking through a world in ruin, consumed by a devouring rage, but grounds himself in the absolute refusal to bow down, using the pain itself as the path forward.","src":"haiku","fable":"The refrain is a self-command staged as scripture, and the song's real drama is that the fist raised in defiance is also the shackle the singer describes wearing, so the pride and the disease of servitude occupy the same body. Its logic doesn't ascend but sinks: the imagery of descent and sleepwalking momentum means the vow of submitting to nothing is spoken from someone already going under, refusing to fold even as gravity wins. The naked one-word repetition of the sensation of cold marks the trough, and from there the pain is reframed not as damage but as the only guide out, which is the whole movement in miniature: rage held so hard it stops being wound and becomes wall.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"defiance spoken from inside the collapse it refuses","built":"the sinking descent imagery undercuts the vow it accompanies"},
"machine-head~elegy":{"haiku":"Environmental destruction and Earth's suffering from human exploitation.","opus":"An ecological lament for a poisoned, dying Earth, mourning pollution and humanity's destruction of nature as a requiem.","sonnet":"A lament for environmental destruction — pollution, deforestation, and acid rain — treated as humanity's crime against the earth.","src":"haiku","fable":"This stages ecological ruin as a funeral rite, and the chosen form is the whole argument. It does not warn of disaster but performs the burial once it has already happened, addressing a listener who still breathes as if he were among the buried. The liturgical refrain flips the usual logic of possession: the planet is no one's holding but the mother to whom the mourners belong. Each verse then pulls the human clock down into hers, wringing crops with no descendants to inherit them from a landscape already gone to cinder. What lifts it past a protest tract is the third movement's reversal, where the wounded world stops being prey and turns executioner. Her fury blooms, the rain weeps her grief, and she seizes the very lives that once presumed to hold her fate. So the rite grieves not the dying ground but the willed refusal to watch it die.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["nature & the elements","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"grief for the planet conducted as a burial rite already completed rather than a warning still pending","built":"the third movement reverses the power relation — the mourned earth stops being prey and turns executioner, seizing the lives that once presumed to hold her fate"},
"machine-head~eulogy":{"haiku":"Haunting memories and internal demons preventing peace; unable to escape the weight of regret and accumulated pain.","opus":"A plunge into frozen memory and self-loathing, old wounds picked at as madness murmurs and sleep won't come, broken by a defiant refusal to be worn down by his enemies. Machine Head, Robb Flynn's Oakland outfit, run that rallying line through a bleaker, more inward song, letting a thread of hope glint against a heavy load of regret and inertia.","sonnet":"A private reckoning with accumulated regret — memories threading like cold webs across the skin, scabs picked open at night, a crushing paralysis under the weight of self-hatred. The refrain borrowed from the band's own anthem turns inward here, less a battle cry than a reminder the speaker is trying to give themselves: stand your ground even when the ground is your own mind.","src":"haiku","fable":"A eulogy addressed inward, spoken for a former self that a cannibalistic past keeps trying to bury, the whole verse-world built from cold, rot, and infestation as decay creeps across body and bed. Against that sickness the chorus stands stripped to plain slogans, and the plainness is the point, since only something that bare can be repeated aloud by someone struggling to stay upright. The long closing movement is where the eulogy turns honest, the defiant chant collapsing into a sleepless, paralyzed reach for control in a hole with no visible exit. What the repeated chorus finally does is not victory but maintenance, hope framed as a change never quite arrived at, chanted regardless because chanting it is the only ground left underfoot.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"About depression, by Flynn's own account.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the chorus not as victory but as maintenance, hope chanted because it is the only ground left","built":"the eulogy addressed inward, mourning a former self the cannibalistic past keeps trying to bury"},
"machine-head~exhale-the-vile":{"haiku":"A person purges betrayal and lies from their body, transforming rage into conviction and becoming an implacable force against deceit.","opus":"A defiant purge of a false, lying enemy: the narrator inhales the life/gift he gave a betrayer and exhales their vileness, refusing to concede, spitting contempt at heretical liars who worshipped falsehood, vowing to be the god they wrongly desired as he burns their deceit out of himself.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who has fed the narrator nothing but lies — the speaker inhales what he gave and exhales the poison back, declaring himself the god the liar will always desire while remaining permanently beyond their reach.","src":"haiku","fable":"The narrator poses as a purge, framing betrayal as the intake of another person's poison and its expulsion back out, but the same corrosive gasoline and fire imagery he wields against his enemy pools in his own chest, so the cleansing gesture curdles into something he cannot cough up. Underneath the bravado of becoming the god his liars will worship, the resin clouding his sight admits he can no longer see the man across from him or himself clearly, which turns the boast into a confession that vengeance has blinded rather than vindicated him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","faith & the occult"],"means":"the purge gesture curdles into what he cannot cough up","built":"the fire weaponised against the enemy is pooling in his own chest"},
"machine-head~eyes-of-the-dead":{"haiku":"An army of the forgotten and outcast rising up with violent purpose; the singer claims identity as a raven symbolizing death, vowing to destroy oppression and reclaim power through apocalyptic force.","opus":"Robb Flynn builds an outsider army from the unwanted and the dead, casting misfits as an unkillable murder of crows, a defiant power fantasy where the rejected turn their curse into vengeance and swear nothing will break them.","sonnet":"A song of collective rage from the discarded and cast-out — the narrator leads a murder of crows representing the powerless and rejected, rising to strip apart those who denied them, speaking with the authority of the dead who will never break.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person plural voice conscripts the listener into a murder of ravens, so the song's real move is a transfer of vantage: the outcast dead offer their sight to the living as both curse and initiation, the title naming that borrowed gaze. The image-system tightens accordingly, from plumage and ink-dark quills into talons, sharpened teeth, and blackened sun, each turn pushing carrion menace outward until the flock eclipses light itself. Underneath the apocalyptic swagger sits a wounded ledger, the unwanted, belonging to no one, so the vengeance reads less as triumph than as the powerless rebranding exile as an earned, unbreakable army.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["exile & diaspora","death & grief","anger & defiance"],"means":"exile rebranded as an earned army, vengeance as the powerless man's inheritance","built":"the first-person plural conscripts the listener into the murder before the song explains why"},
"machine-head~five":{"haiku":"A survivor recounts childhood sexual abuse at age five, processing the trauma of violation, threats, and stolen innocence. The song confronts the psychological damage inflicted by a perpetrator.","opus":"A survivor recounts being molested as a five-year-old, the smell and threats of his abuser, and the lasting suffering and lost innocence. He directs raw rage at the abuser and prays for the memory to go away.","sonnet":"The song is a direct account of childhood sexual abuse: the speaker recalls being molested at age five, the threats used to silence him, and the lasting psychological devastation that followed. The title refers to his age, and the lyrics make clear the trauma has never left.","src":"haiku","fable":"The speaker returns to a childhood scene of sexual abuse and narrates it in the present tense of a mind that cannot leave it, the count of five years old surfacing again and again as the only fixed fact strong enough to hold the memory together. The song's architecture is the trauma's: the same verse of accusation loops with almost no development because the wound itself does not develop, and the horror deepens instead in two smaller registers — the abuser's remembered threats folded inside the child's own head, and the sickening intrusion of a line that admits the grooming taught the boy to bargain in the abuser's terms, so that the voice can no longer tell its own thoughts from the ones planted in it. What finally breaks the loop is not resolution but attrition, the flat refusal that he cannot take it anymore, and the read closes where it must, on the bare number left standing alone after the language of the speaker has been stripped down to it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Autobiographical: Flynn has confirmed the lyric is his own story, molested by a neighbor at age five — and has said he will never perform it live.","themes":["childhood sexual abuse","madness & the mind","anger & defiance"],"means":"a voice that can no longer tell its own thoughts from the planted ones","built":"the looping verse with almost no development enacts a wound that does not develop"},
"machine-head~from-this-day":{"haiku":"The speaker declares independence from pain and shame, choosing strength and authenticity over conformity starting now.","opus":"A defiant vow to stop hiding wounds and stand upright, shame reforged into resolve. Coming from Machine Head's much-maligned nu-metal detour, it reads as the band and narrator alike insisting they were right to keep going against the doubters.","sonnet":"A declaration of resilience after hitting bottom — the pain, shame, and tears that broke him down also forged something stronger, and now he's standing with his head up and asserting his worth. The song is a commitment made to himself about who he's going to be from this moment forward.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song works by converting personal wreckage into a mandate: the verses catalogue withered years and freshly-bled eyes, then flip that shame into the fuel for endurance, the pain rebranded as proof of being alive rather than defeat. Its motion runs from grievance to defiance to gratitude — the sneering middle where he dares anyone to test him gives way, unexpectedly, to a closing turn toward a girl and a thanked god who saved him, so the armor he builds in the choruses exists to protect something tender he can only admit at the end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Flynn stands by the songs but not the single's video — corny as fuck, in his own words.","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"shame reforged into the fuel for endurance","built":"the closing tenderness is what all the armour was built to protect"},
"machine-head~game-over":{"haiku":"A deep personal betrayal by a once-close friend; the singer grieves a broken bond corrupted by emptiness and arrogance, cursing a friendship lost to toxic behavior and deciding to abandon it entirely.","opus":"A raw eulogy for a wrecked brotherhood once forged in music, fury, and addiction, now rotted by deceit and bitterness; the narrator tears himself loose, cursing the old friend's name and swearing to defile their grave, deciding that if existence is merely a contest, then it's finished.","sonnet":"A song about the complete collapse of a close male friendship poisoned by manipulation and negativity — the narrator recounts years of one-sided emotional abuse, fantasizes about violence against the former friend, and declares the friendship permanently dead.","src":"haiku","fable":"The speaker addresses a doubled figure — first an intimate lover whose sweet lies and fake tears mask an inner void, then, in the central movement, a former brother-in-arms from a time when only music held them together. What began as accusation curdles into a reckoning with the addressee's proposition that existence is merely a game of interchangeable pawns; the speaker, ashamed at having believed it so long, converts that cynical framing into a verdict and turns it back as an ending. The mask underneath is grief posing as contempt: the curse, the fantasy of desecrating a grave, the vow never to forgive resolve at last into the speaker's insistence that none of this was ever play — that the stakes were real and the loss permanent.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Widely read as Flynn's kiss-off to founding bassist Adam Duce, fired in 2013; the falling-out spawned a lawsuit — including a defamation claim over a Flynn blog post — settled out of court in July 2014.","themes":["death & grief","friendship & loyalty","anger & defiance"],"means":"grief posing as contempt, the real stakes insisted on through a curse","built":"accusation aimed at a lover turns mid-song into a reckoning with a lost brother-in-arms"},
"machine-head~ghosts-will-haunt-my-bones":{"haiku":"Inner demons and haunting thoughts torment the speaker, creating a psychological war that manifests as physical torture and internal conflict. The ghosts are metaphorical intrusions of unresolved trauma and self-loathing.","opus":"A menacing inner torment where the speaker fantasizes about inflicting pain on another while relentless dark thoughts, demons, and self-loathing refuse to grant him any peace as darkness slams down over him.","sonnet":"The narrator is tormented by internal demons and dark thoughts that won't let them rest — the ghosts that haunt their bones are the anguished, conflicting voices inside their own head, the bile of self-hatred, and the crushing weight of sleepless nights.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses stage intimacy as violation—hands, tears, the soul peeled a layer at a time—but by the third verse the predator turns out to be internal, the sadist and victim the same insomniac. What sounded like a threat aimed outward reveals itself as a man narrating his own erosion, the hauntings and demons purged like bile because they were never anyone else's to steal.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","madness & the mind"],"means":"the outward predator collapsing inward to reveal a man haunting himself","built":"the threat-aimed-outward is the mask; the final verse repoints it entirely inward"},
"machine-head~grind-you-down":{"haiku":"Systemic oppression wearing away human resilience; resistance and defiance as the only response to authoritarian grinding.","opus":"Robb Flynn's Bay Area groove-metal band Machine Head recast personal grievance as a war campaign, picturing the narrator as a general whose pack overruns his enemies while sheer resolve keeps him upright. The hook turns from threat to survival creed, urging you to hold your position and refuse to retreat, wreckage washed clean by winter.","sonnet":"A battle-worn soldier's self-portrait — body rotting, veins iced over, morality long since traded for endurance. The tone pivots mid-song from predatory swagger to something defensive, a rallying cry against forces trying to wear the fighter down, until defiance becomes the only posture left standing.","src":"haiku","fable":"Grind You Down opens as a confession of a body wrecked by its own appetites — soot lodged beneath the nails, blood run cold, a chest gone hard as metal — then swaggers into the voice of a warlord whose beasts tear through the opposing ranks while his fellow commanders exult and death falls away behind him. The grinding refrain first sounds like a predator's boast, the mighty pulverizing the weak, until the structure breaks and the body itself collapses to the ground, and the same words invert their owner completely, now cautioning against powers that arrive to wound. The self-poisoning addict and the conquering tyrant turn out to be one machine, and the closing charge to cut your own way and yield no ground reclaims the phrase as defiance rather than surrender.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","war & battle","anger & defiance"],"means":"the predator's boast and the addict's collapse turning out to be one machine","built":"the grinding refrain inverts its owner mid-song, the conqueror becoming the one being cautioned"},
"machine-head~halo":{"haiku":"Rejecting religious hypocrisy and fighting oppressive faith-based systems.","opus":"A militant attack on organized Christianity and the religious right, refusing to pray and vowing to fight their hypocrisy over abortion rights.","sonnet":"Anti-religious defiance against the Christian right's political power, rejecting their God and refusing to submit.","src":"haiku","fable":"Built for The Blackening's arena scale, this is Robb Flynn's culture-war broadside against the American Christian right wing. Its engine is a rhyme device that decays a sacred image through a string of near-echoes, until the sequence lands on the bodily act of gulping down a lie. The chorus circles one closed idea: a sightless deity, a jaundiced flock, and the standing order to refuse whatever that machine spoon-feeds its believers. The verses argue politics — the womb staged as contested territory, faith weaponized into law — but they read less as debate than as the fuel powering the song's real motion. That motion is a hardening of pronouns. It opens as a wounded first-person rallying the like-minded, swells into a communal fist, then narrows to a second-person address whose refusal of prayer curdles into a death-wish aimed at one named enemy. What starts as solidarity finishes as a curse, and the track is honest enough to leave that hunger unwashed rather than pretend righteous fury keeps the furious clean.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","faith & the occult"],"means":"solidarity hardening track by track into a named curse","built":"pronouns migrate from wounded first-person to communal fist to death-wish"},
"machine-head~heavy-lies-the-crown":{"haiku":"Power and envy create paranoia and violence; leadership means bearing the weight of others' hatred and plotting your fall.","opus":"Robb Flynn's veteran groove-metal band Machine Head dramatize a paranoid ruler, a scheming, spying king who senses the envy of rivals lured toward power like insects to a flame. The Shakespearean weight of command curdles into open menace as he vows to destroy anyone plotting his fall, the crown offered as both complaint and threat.","sonnet":"Power as a trap — a ruler who cast himself as a spider king finds that the web he built to ensnare others now encloses him, with conspirators sharpening blades behind his back. The song cycles between threat and vulnerability, ending on a warning that envy will be punished but the crown's weight can crush anyone who wears it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Machine Head recasts an invented figure — a schemer who spins traps, plants informants, and mines the city's rumor for leverage — but the title's nod to Shakespeare's restless sovereign, plus the refrain about resentment, reveals a ruler so encircled by threat that he reads his own dread as everyone else's covetousness. That intimate paranoia then swells into a battlefield of blades, cannons, and mechanized slaughter that consumes the powerless, so the private throne becomes an accessory to mass killing dressed as patriotism. In the closing turn he abandons the imperial voice and begs not to be exalted, insisting the carnage was never his wish, before the pose reasserts itself with a curt promise to bury anyone scheming against him. The read lives in that snap: the burden he laments is welded to the brutality that keeps him seated.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","war & battle","madness & the mind"],"means":"a ruler whose dread of threat is indistinguishable from the brutality keeping him seated","built":"the snap from begging not to be exalted to promising burial of rivals is where the read lives"},
"machine-head~hole-in-the-sky-black-sabbath":{"haiku":"Escapism and detachment from reality through observation of an unreachable beyond.","opus":"A cover of Black Sabbath, sung by a weary, disillusioned narrator who has witnessed war, greed, and civilization decaying, and who longs to slip out of existence entirely because he no longer believes anything worthwhile lies ahead.","sonnet":"A cover of Black Sabbath's classic: the speaker inhabits a place of existential detachment — windowless, rent-free, beyond the sun — watching civilizations collapse and war dogs feast, longing for a hole in the sky that offers transcendence or escape from a world with no future.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-head~hope-begets-hope":{"haiku":"Resistance through hope and solidarity; refusing to submit to depression and oppression by standing together against the machinery.","opus":"A defiant survival anthem about cutting through deception, climbing onto the fire to be cleansed, rejecting anyone's commands, and rising back to your feet because encouragement multiplies itself. Depression gnawing at the marrow gives the fight real teeth. Machine Head, fronted by Robb Flynn, funnel their Bay Area groove metal into this insistence on holding firm, resilience wrung from the edge of collapse.","sonnet":"A hard-won survival anthem built out of near-collapse — the speaker at the end of their rope, depression eating at the bones, running from something unnamed and vast. The turn is deliberate: crawl onto the pyre and let the flames take the hatred, face the flaws directly, take someone's hand. Hope is not inherited here, it's generated by the act of persisting.","src":"haiku","fable":"Robb Flynn frames this as a survival hymn running on one self-feeding loop, where the way forward is a paradox: you last by loosening your grip, standing firm and unclenching in a single motion. The verses furnish the wound, a depression gnawing at the very bones meant to hold him upright, so the choruses can transmute it into fuel. The address widens across the song, drifting from a lone I who spots predators hidden in the flock toward a we that refuses to go under and finally an us that vows to persist beyond death, the collective voice itself becoming the engine the title describes. One hardening detail confirms it: the command to rise from your knees, generic defiance at first, curdles by the last chorus into a pointed refusal thrown at whoever demands the kneeling.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"the collective voice becoming the engine the title names, self-feeding as it widens","built":"the generic defiance of rise from your knees curdles by the last chorus into a pointed refusal"},
"machine-head~i-am-hell-sonata-in-c":{"haiku":"Consuming rage and destruction born from love betrayed and passion corrupted into violence.","opus":"A narrative of a jilted lover turned arsonist, where obsessive teenage desire sours into treachery and bloodlust, and setting fire becomes a warped rite of cleansing meant to ensure the beloved can never care for anyone else.","sonnet":"A voice consumed by a youthful obsession with destruction narrates how a love affair curdled into murderous bloodlust — the warmth of the relationship now serving only as fuel for an infernal self-image, with the speaker positioning themselves as divine wrath made flesh.","src":"haiku","fable":"A murder narrated from inside the murderer, and the horror is that she tells it as devotion and rite rather than crime. The blood-chant framing and the mock-classical Sonata subtitle recast arson as ceremony: she isn't confessing but officiating, styling herself a divine executioner dispatched to purify by flame. Machine Head build the perversion structurally, tracing an emotional descent from adolescent infatuation through betrayal into a craving for slaughter, so the killing registers to her as consummation, the natural summit of adoration rather than its collapse. The recurring imagery does the moral labor the speaker refuses, a pulse cooling into embers and affection repurposed as tinder, letting tenderness and ruin share a single vocabulary until she can name incineration a wedding. What turns genuinely chilling is the jealous motive beneath the cosmic self-mythology, the wish to leave him incapable of ever loving anyone after her, which peels off the holy mask and exposes ordinary possessive annihilation dressed in sacred fire.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","love & desire"],"means":"murder narrated as devotion and rite rather than crime","built":"the mock-classical subtitle recasts arson as ceremony, officiating rather than confessing","fnote":"Demmel called it the pages out of a pyromaniac's diary, written deliberately from a woman's point of view — the band decided female arsonists were the more dangerous, their fires crimes of passion. The three movements run Sangre Sani (Blood Saint) into I Am Hell into Ashes to the Sky."},
"machine-head~i-defy":{"haiku":"A defiant stance against manipulation, fake respect, and users who prey on the weak while presenting themselves as leaders. The speaker refuses to fall again and declares he will break the chain of weakness that binds people.","opus":"Robb Flynn's declaration of self-respect against fake allies, hangers-on and parasites who fold under pressure. The defiance is earned rather than posed, built on contempt for those who fake loyalty and respect they never worked for.","sonnet":"A refusal to be used, manipulated, or underestimated — the speaker rejects fake allies who talk loyalty but mean nothing, calls out parasites who conform and abuse, and insists on his own strength even when backed into a corner. The repeated \"I Defy\" is a vow not to yield to a world of hollow people.","src":"haiku","fable":"The braggart's boast is the mask; the wound is the taunting refrain about who finally ended the pain, and the song's swagger is what gets built over that hole. Structurally it moves from interrogation to self-declaration: the verses hammer a false friend with limp handshakes and fake smiles, testing loyalty that dissolves the moment words cost nothing, and the payoff is a chanted refusal to fall again. But the bridge drops the armor entirely, giving something away and asking a dead-serious question about a pain someone else stopped, so the closing vow to break this reads less like triumph than like the only sentence a person can still finish. The defiance, then, is aimed two ways at once: outward at the parasites and users, inward at the grief that the strength is scar tissue over.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty","death & grief"],"means":"defiance aimed outward at parasites and inward at grief at once","built":"the bridge drops all armour to ask who finally ended the pain"},
"machine-head~i-m-your-god-now":{"haiku":"Addiction becomes a controlling entity that enslaves the user; the drug speaks as an abusive god-like force that demands escalating use.","opus":"Machine Head personify addiction as a merciless deity speaking to its captive. Suffering first drove the user toward relief, but the drug's voice takes over to gloat that it has trapped him in a private hell and claimed him entirely, each fresh hit deepening a bond that is really just ownership.","sonnet":"A song narrated from the perspective of addiction itself — the drug speaks directly, seducing the user into dependency, making loneliness feel like friendship, crucifying the person in their own hell, and finally declaring itself their god. It's a first-person predator portrait of how addiction occupies the space left by pain.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song personifies addiction itself as the speaker, and its power comes from the pronoun turn at the midpoint: the first half is the addict confessing how a substance became refuge from grief, and the second half hands the microphone to the drug, which reframes every prior line as its own deliberate handiwork. What the victim called comfort, the new voice calls manufacture — it built the retreat, staged the private crucifixion, renamed friendship as ownership. The title lands as the logic completed: dependency escalated into worship, the addict emptied of self until only the master remains, the closing refrain a deity's claim answered by three flat refusals that arrive too late to mean anything.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The night Flynn signed the Roadrunner deal he overdosed on heroin and nearly died; a month later a close friend died on the same batch. The song became the survivor's half of that pair.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","surveillance & control"],"means":"the drug seizes the microphone mid-song to reclaim comfort as manufacture","built":"three flat refusals arrive too late to undo the deity's completed claim"},
"machine-head~imaginal-cells":{"haiku":"Societal collapse and existential crisis frame the moment; rigid structures crumble, humans evolve beyond distraction into true power and evolutionary destiny.","opus":"A spoken-word interlude splicing collapse-theory and New Age biology: civilization as a dissolving caterpillar and the awakened few as the cells that reorganize it into something new. Machine Head using doom as an argument for evolutionary hope.","sonnet":"A spoken-word collage of voices diagnosing civilizational collapse — peak oil, climate change, mental illness epidemics, violent crime, religious cultism — and arguing that the emerging \"imaginal cells\" of humanity (people who see a different world) are the beginning of a new evolution. It's a concept song about transformation through crisis.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole track is a spliced sermon of pop-science and New Age biology, and its single governing conceit is the butterfly's metamorphosis: inside the caterpillar's dissolving body, imaginal cells are the ones that hold the blueprint for the coming form, at first attacked as foreign before they cohere into the butterfly. Machine Head hands the entire runtime to that borrowed voice, so the band's own act is the argument — a metal album abdicating its snarl to let a collaged prophecy of collapse-then-transformation speak uninterrupted, framing civilizational crisis (climate, economy, mass illness) not as extinction but as the goo-stage before a better body forms. The lone hard image is aimed like a taunt: the dying institutions are dinosaurs crashing down, and the only instruction is to step out of their way while the new cells take over. Read against the grain, the piece is knowingly a piece of faith — it diagnoses belief systems and weapons of mass distraction, then asks you to swallow one more totalizing story of destiny, so the sincerity and the sales pitch are the same gesture, and the mask it wears is the very optimism it can't prove.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The spoken word is Bruce Lipton's imaginal-cells piece from the Spontaneous Evolution audiobook — the album's echo of the debut's Real Eyes, Realize, Real Lies collage.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult","politics & society"],"means":"a collaged prophecy where sincerity and the sales pitch are the same gesture","built":"the band abdicates its snarl entirely, making the abdication itself the argument"},
"machine-head~imperium":{"fable":"A defiance anthem that earns its length by traveling from wound to war-cry: it opens in confessed pain and lifelong isolation, the sense of a boy trained by circumstance to assume the fault was always his own. The chorus is the turn — an obscene salute thrown at the sky, chains and servitude spat back — where injury converts into a vow. Then Robb Flynn widens it past personal grievance into something almost liturgical: a promise chiseled into gravestone, sorrow laid down, dawn breaking over the wreckage, the self prising control back from society's grip and squaring off against everyone alive at once. The through-line is alchemical — each private flaw hammered into an asset, rage and tears refired as will.","fableDeep":"A spoken vow of self-liberation — converting every rage, tear and fear into fuel — but the middle turns unexpectedly tender: patience, belief, a promise that the sun will rise, and a hand extended to pick someone else up and march them on. The defiance is the vehicle; the engine is hope paid forward.","haiku":"A person refuses to be controlled or defined by others' expectations and prejudice, standing defiantly against external pressure to conform or submit.","opus":"A defiant declaration of self-liberation, in which the speaker refuses to be a slave to society, prejudice, and self-doubt, vowing to turn every weakness, rage, and fear into strength and take back control of his own life.","sonnet":"A declaration of self-reclamation against societal conditioning and prejudice, where the speaker vows to break free from imposed chains, convert fear and rage into strength, and take back control of their own life.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"private wounds alchemized into a public war-cry","built":"the structure enacts the alchemy: each verse catalogues injury, each chorus converts it","fvr":"2.4"},
"machine-head~in-comes-the-flood":{"haiku":"American capitalism and consumerism have replaced God with profit, enslaving people through debt and manufactured desires.","opus":"A furious anti-capitalist tirade against the worship of money, bankers, and profitable endless wars that keep the poor in chains. Calling to tear down the financial center and rouse a country deluded by its own myth of prosperity, it warns that a reckoning is coming for a nation that has made a religion of wealth.","sonnet":"An anticapitalist broadside against American financial culture — Wall Street, debt bondage, the dollar worshipped as god, and the poor enslaved by credit are all indicted as the flood of consequence finally arrives.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages American capital as a state religion, its images pulled from currency and scripture until the two collapse into a single object of worship — the coinage moneytheistic naming that collapse outright. Its accusation turns inward at the volta, dropping the sermon's cadence to admit the speaker sold himself along with everyone else, the prophet exposed as one more mark. Underneath the flood-and-wake-up refrain runs a quieter dread: that emptiness, not ruin, is the real verdict, and that losing fear is the only thing that drowns the god.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","faith & the occult","politics & society"],"means":"the prophet exposed as one more mark inside the religion he is denouncing","built":"currency and scripture collapse into a single coined word that names the merger outright"},
"machine-head~in-the-presence-of-my-enemies": {"haiku":"A declaration of righteous anger toward those who harm innocents and destroy hope, vowing to stand merciless over their graves.","opus":"A man who claims to be peaceful vows merciless violence and vengeance against those who harm children and wage pointless war, imagining standing on his enemy's grave; it frames retribution against destroyers of innocence.","sonnet":"A self-described non-violent man declares that anyone who harms children or wages pointless war has forfeited his mercy, and he vows to stand over their grave without grief when they die.","src":"haiku","fable":"Flynn opens by disavowing aggression — a man of peace who never demanded to know why misfortune singled him out — and the whole track then dismantles that self-image in real time, because the instant predators and warmongers get named, the speaker turns executioner. The catalogue of the guilty is specific and unforgiving: those who rob a child of its untouched years, those who dress slaughter up as balancing some ledger of scores, all of them branded poisoners of everything undefiled. Against them he reaches for real mythic scaffolding, invoking the Roman war-god's rising firebird only to watch its wings snapped and its flame gutter out, as though vengeance could extinguish war itself. The repeated insistence that he is peaceable is the mask; underneath runs pure appetite for retribution, dramatized as a face ground into pavement and cartilage caving under his knuckles. It ends not in triumph but in a graveside vigil — dry-eyed, empty-handed, a sourness souring the sweet in his mouth — the pacifist's oath curdled into a promise to outlast the enemy and plant himself atop the buried box, which is the real core of Machine Head's comeback: righteous fury that half-knows it has become the thing it hunts.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","war & battle"],"means":"the peaceable self-image dismantled the instant the guilty are named and counted","built":"repeated insistence on peacefulness is the mask the song dismantles in real time"},
"machine-head~is-there-anybody-out-there":{"haiku":"Isolation and abandonment from birth driving existential desperation, the speaker reaching outward seeking fellowship with others broken by circumstance.","opus":"A searching cry of isolation from a frontman recounting a childhood as an unwanted, fostered kid and an adulthood renouncing the racism he once embraced. The chorus reaches outward, desperate to know anyone else feels this scared and alone. Beneath the groove-metal weight is a plea for connection and a reckoning with shame turned into hard-won conviction.","sonnet":"A cry for connection from someone who grew up abandoned — born a bastard, fostered and forgotten, now standing as a father ashamed of racist former brothers. The narrator reaches outward through paranoia and loneliness, insisting that not all who wander are lost, that roaring at the world for years doesn't mean no one can hear.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-head~is-there-anybody-out-there-official-track":{"haiku":"The narrator, born without family support and later ashamed of racist ideologies he once held, feels isolated and asks if anyone else shares his fear and loneliness.","opus":"A narrator abandoned at birth cries out from within paranoia and isolation, asking if anyone else shares his dread and sense of being cut off. Machine Head's Robb Flynn weaves his own story in, disavowing the bigots he once embraced and steeling himself to speak against hatred, barely able to breathe yet holding that not everyone adrift is truly lost.","sonnet":"A man born into abandonment and raised without roots sends out a desperate signal to anyone else living in the same disconnected frequency. Having grown into a father who must now disavow the racist brotherhood of his past, he stands publicly alone and asks whether that loneliness is shared.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-head~kaleidoscope":{"haiku":"Isolation and musical therapy as escape from conformist society and zombie complacency; solitary resistance against collective numbness.","opus":"An anthem for outcasts flipping off a numbed America run by the mindless and corrupt, the singer hurtling toward collapse like a runaway vehicle. At its heart is an emptiness that only music can fill where faith fails, a lost man content to stay lost, tormented by sound. Machine Head, Robb Flynn's Bay Area groove-metal band, turn alienation into communal catharsis.","sonnet":"A rejection of numb, hive-mind America in favor of the raw honesty of music — the speaker feels hollowed out, disconnected, and impossible to save, yet finds the only working answer is sound itself, which fills the gap that religion cannot. Defiant isolation dressed as a communal shout.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses spit outward at a rotted body politic (zombies and cronies, divide-and-conquer, chest-beaten refusal to be a slave), but the chorus turns the same lens inward and finds the actual subject: not the enemy, but a hole where religion used to be. Movement is the whole point here — the political rage is the mask, and what it keeps circling back to is that music is the only faith left standing, a fractured light that keeps rearranging itself without ever resolving, which is why the speaker would rather stay lost inside the sound than be found and made whole. The kaleidoscope is the honest image: beauty and disorientation from the same broken pieces, coping that never actually copes. The final choice to be haunted rather than healed reads the refusal straight — being saved would mean the noise stops, and the noise is all that is holding him together.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","politics & society","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"music held as the only faith standing, chosen over being found and made whole","built":"the political rage is the mask; the chorus keeps returning to the hole where religion was"},
"machine-head~kick-you-when-you-re-down":{"haiku":"A fighter maintains resilience and inner strength despite constant defeat and setbacks, believing perseverance through struggle leads to self-improvement.","opus":"A defiant hymn to self-reliance: existence is savage and people reliably strike at your lowest moment, so you have to back your own judgment, heed your own heart, and grind on like a fighter in a drawn-out test of will, answering each blow in kind and refusing to stay flattened.","sonnet":"A defiant survival anthem addressed to everyone who exploits people at their lowest — the answer is to keep your head up, trust yourself, and treat life like a chess game you refuse to concede.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's real subject is class exclusion, not resilience-as-cliché: the verses catalog the world's contempt for anyone treated as disposable, penned into a lowered social ground, before it answers with defiance. Its structure is a reciprocity ledger turned into a chorus mantra — the paired-nouns verses trade like-for-like on every human quality, but that mutual bargain collapses under the hook's flat law that the world will always strike the fallen. What follows is pure self-instruction, a chant repeated past the point of argument into the register of a psych-up someone recites to stay upright — the closing loops betray that the confidence is willed, not felt, endurance talked into being because nothing external supplies it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"defiance talked into being when nothing external supplies it","built":"the paired-nouns verses collapse under a hook naming one flat law"},
"machine-head~killers-kings":{"haiku":"Tarot and occult symbolism channeling spiritual awakening; the singer invokes ancient power and third-eye consciousness to transform a population into inheritors of earth with collective strength.","opus":"Machine Head walk the full tarot deck to argue that death means transformation, not ending, rallying the downtrodden as inheritors of the earth who find rebirth and power in accepting inevitable change.","sonnet":"A tarot-themed song about spiritual transformation and collective identity — the narrator draws on arcana imagery to argue that death precedes rebirth, the third eye reveals truth, and those who embrace this cycle become killers and kings.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears occultism as armor: a séance opens it, a fortune-teller lays out the deck, and the singer treats reading the spread as a way to unlock a caged self and wake the sleeper within — divination as self-authorship rather than superstition. Underneath the ceremonial dread, the actual creed is plain and almost defiant: the one card everyone fears is reframed not as an ending but as transformation you cannot refuse, so the whole imagined bloodline of the fallen and the crowned becomes a congregation chanting itself toward renewal. What looks like a doom-metal invocation is really a resurrection hymn, insisting that decay is only the front half of a cycle whose back half is being reborn on your own terms.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Flynn built it from twenty-plus tarot cards, by his own account — the Hangman, the Sun, the Fool and company threaded into a death-and-rebirth frame.","themes":["faith & the occult","identity & becoming","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"divination recast as the instrument of self-authorship rather than prophecy","built":"the feared death card is inverted into the rebirth the song is secretly about"},
"machine-head~killers-kings-demo-track":{"haiku":"Hierarchy collapses as kings and assassins merge; power reverses through violent overthrow.","opus":"An occult-tinged demo built around tarot imagery, casting a band of outcasts as killers and kings destined to inherit the earth through death and rebirth. The devil, the hangman, and the king of pentacles populate a mystic power fantasy about breaking free from chains. It reaches for defiant, apocalyptic empowerment dressed in esoteric symbolism.","sonnet":"Drawing on tarot imagery — the king of pentacles, the hermit, the devil and the emperor — the lyric casts its protagonists as inheritors of a cyclical, cosmic order in which death always precedes rebirth and chains exist only for those who accept them. The battle cry \"killers and kings\" unifies an army that has opened its third eye and refuses to be locked in fear.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-head~left-unfinished": {"haiku":"A man abandoned by his biological parents channels his pain into love, vowing to live opposite to their cruelty while expressing rage at their rejection and disappearance.","opus":"A furious address to the birth parents who abandoned him, setting the devotion of the family that raised him against rage at those who walked out, and vowing to become the reverse of everything they were and to cherish those cast aside.","sonnet":"A direct address to biological parents who abandoned the narrator: raw fury at their neglect is matched by gratitude toward the parents who actually raised him, and a vow to live the opposite of what his birth parents were.","src":"haiku","fable":"Flynn addresses the biological father who conceived him and vanished, and the song's whole architecture is a refusal to let that absence define him: he vows to become the inverse of the man who left, to give the discarded children of the world exactly the love he was denied. The turn is the point — mid-song the venom breaks open into gratitude for the parents who actually raised him, so the same voice that spits the ugliest curses is the one that has already decided love, not grievance, will be the ground it stands on. When he finally speaks a legal name — his own at birth, Lawrence Matthew Cardine, which the pair who made him now pretend never to have heard — and pronounces that boy dead for all they know, the burial is theirs to mourn: the discarded name goes into the ground with any claim that blood confers a right to be forgiven. The unfinished thing of the title is thrown back: the father left a life half-built, and the son announces he'll finish it himself, on his own terms.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"the abandoned son buries his birth name and finishes the life the father left unbuilt","built":"the legal birth name spoken aloud and declared dead performs its own burial mid-song"},
"machine-head~locust":{"haiku":"A plague of locusts descends as a metaphor for consuming evil and societal decay, with faith and truth presented as the only redemption against the swarm. The song treats the locust invasion as a reckoning for corruption and deception.","opus":"Casts a devouring swarm of locusts as an apocalyptic plague that darkens the sky, consumes souls young and old, and reduces creation to a feast, framed with imagery of judgment where lies are torn away and a supposed purity ultimately prevails.","sonnet":"A swarm of locusts descends as a biblical plague — a destructive force that chokes the sky, consumes the harvest, and bleeds the earth dry, yet the song ends with faith and truth ultimately tearing away the veil and exposing the charade.","src":"haiku","fable":"A biblical plague read as an intimate violation: the song opens with something that mimics tenderness before it wounds, a devourer wearing holy camouflage while it feeds. The chorus turns that private betrayal cosmic, the sky itself darkening into a devouring host that spares neither young nor old. The bridge and final pre-chorus flip the polarity into deliverance, staging the whole assault as a test of faith that ends with the deceiver's mask torn away and truth crowned triumphant. Beneath the apocalyptic imagery it maps a very human ordeal, a soul under siege by a corruption disguised as intimacy, insisting the inner self cannot finally be breached.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Flynn drew it from the same body of dark poetry he'd written under the I Am Hell banner, and the real-life locust was close to home: Demmel's brother-in-law, who lied to and stole from the family.","themes":["faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"intimate betrayal recast as a plague that proves the soul unbreachable","built":"the devouring swarm pivots at the bridge into a trial that unmasks the deceiver"},
"machine-head~message-in-a-bottle":{"haiku":"A castaway sends desperate calls for rescue after a year of isolation, only to discover billions equally alone and lost at sea.","opus":"A cover of Sting's parable of isolation: a castaway sending out an SOS finds a hundred billion other bottles washed ashore, realizing loneliness is itself universal. The turn is consoling, that everyone is alone together, so the isolated are never truly alone.","sonnet":"A cover of The Police song: a person stranded in profound loneliness sends a desperate plea for rescue into the void, only to discover that the entire world is equally isolated and equally searching for connection.","src":"haiku","fable":"Machine Head picks a song of complete isolation and answers with volume, which is the whole point of the cover: the SOS is no longer a scrap of paper but a scream thrown at the world, the loneliness reframed as something to survive by force rather than endure quietly. The structure carries the meaning as it moves from the castaway alone at sea, to the horror of a year gone with no reply, to the turn where hope is admitted as the only tether and love as the thing that can both mend and break you. And the ending earns its length: the plea for someone, anyone, to answer collapses into pure repetition, the same distress signal sent over and over, so that the refusal to resolve becomes the reading — a voice that can do nothing but keep transmitting into a silence that may never send anything back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The cover was Ross Robinson's idea over the band's objections; Flynn resisted it and still thinks little of it.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"the refusal to resolve becomes the distress signal itself","built":"pure repetition at the close enacts a transmission that receives no reply"},
"machine-head~my-hands-are-empty":{"haiku":"Grief and helplessness watching a loved one destroy themselves through addiction while you can do nothing but witness.","opus":"Machine Head, Robb Flynn's long-running groove-metal band, turn their heaviness toward loss rather than defiance, mourning someone consumed by substance abuse as the narrator sinks into his own depression and watches good memories sour. Beneath the self-blame runs a refusal to conceal his wounds or be discarded, forgiveness withering into something inhuman and spectral, the emptiness he's left holding the true center of the song.","sonnet":"Grief and self-recrimination over watching someone disappear into addiction — the speaker stands by helplessly as pills and powders hollow a person out, left haunted by who they used to be. By the end the speaker refuses to carry the shame any further but has nothing left to hold.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-head~nausea":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts someone who has manipulated, betrayed, and drained them—a deceiver bound for ruin who now wants help while the speaker severs ties permanently.","opus":"A seething kiss-off to a manipulative, lying user who cheats, betrays friends, and burns bridges, now begging for help. The freight-train image reframes the narrator's stare as the consequence bearing down: he won't cut them free, and what he's finally cutting is this toxic person out of his life for good.","sonnet":"A searing severance from a habitual manipulator — someone who lies, steals, burns bridges, and only shows up needing help; the narrator finally cuts them out of his life entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"A kiss-off that keeps sharpening its own knife: the song opens conceding every argument to an abuser who talks circles around the narrator, then flips the power by refusing to answer the one taunting question they keep being asked. The image-system is a collision waiting to happen down a rail line, with the antagonist lashed to the ties and begging to be freed, while the narrator coldly recalls that in his own crisis this person would have watched him die. By the final movement the metaphor turns literal and administrative: the only thing being severed here is the relationship itself, the rescue withheld on purpose as the verdict lands. Underneath the venom is a settling of accounts rather than rage, the narrator naming the cheating, theft, and betrayed friendships less to wound than to justify a departure he has already decided is deserved.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"rescue withheld as a deliberate verdict, not rage","built":"the freight-train metaphor reframes the narrator's stillness as the consequence bearing down"},
"machine-head~night-of-long-knives":{"haiku":"Retelling of the Manson murders as prophecy fulfilled. Blood, betrayal, and cult domination stain Beverly Hills; the night descends with knives and death.","opus":"A retelling of the Charles Manson family murders, framing a desert prophet whose followers descend on the Hollywood hills to kill. It graphically depicts the slaughter, including Roman Polanski and a pregnant victim, in a night of long knives.","sonnet":"The song retells the Manson Family murders—Charles Manson's desert prophecies, the killings in the Hollywood Hills, the stabbing and strangling of victims including a pregnant woman—as a horror narrative about a prophet who weaponised the vulnerable and turned them into killers. The refrain of unseen knives coming in the night frames it as a story of massacre and damnation.","src":"haiku","fable":"Flynn lifts a phrase the Third Reich coined for its own bloody purge and hands it to Charles Manson, framing the 1969 killings as a chosen apocalypse rather than a stumble into evil. The genius is the camera angle: the chorus rides inside the intruders, a first-person swarm vowing the target never hears their approach, which drops the listener into the doomed house while the murderers do the talking. Verse two's nod to Polanski's own occult horror is the knife-twist, since the fiction he once staged curdles into the real address where his pregnant wife is slaughtered. But the bridge cracks the whole pose open, when that unseen, boasting collective abruptly admits it erred and will drop away into nothing. So the manifesto's strut gets exposed as the last rationalization of people who mistook butchery for revelation, and the closing threat lands more as a headstone than a warning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","madness & the mind"],"means":"the manifesto's strut exposed when the killers admit they simply erred","built":"the chorus rides inside the intruders in first person, dropping the listener into the doomed house","fnote":"Retells the 1969 Manson Family murders — a subject Flynn steeped himself in through books and films; the title borrows the name of Hitler's 1934 purge as a byword for treacherous massacre."},
"machine-head~none-but-my-own":{"haiku":"A man hardened by years of abuse refuses to absorb hatred directed at him and declares his loyalty only to those he chooses, turning suffering into strength.","opus":"A defiant stand against an abuser who demanded gratitude and deference while working to strip the narrator down. He refuses to absorb the cruelty they inflicted, promising instead to sharpen years of suffering into strength and rise up on the wreckage of the one who hurt him, answering to no one but himself.","sonnet":"A song about surviving abuse through sheer self-sufficiency — stripped of everything, beaten down, the speaker refuses to break or accept the hate, converting years of pain into a weapon and insisting their heart belongs only to themselves now, to no one who tried to take it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Machine Head's debut-era rage song stages a survivor talking back to an abuser, and its whole force is the shift from the opening self-erasure to a closing vow. He starts by shrinking himself down to a body and a pair of clenched hands, absorbing every insult and cruelty the other person hands over, before the accounting flips: the damage he took becomes something he sharpens into resolve, and the chorus refuses to let another person own the beat of his heart. What sounds at first like defeat is the crouch before the strike, and the recurring one-word cry of fire is less destruction aimed outward than the furnace the whole track is smelting itself in.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"the crouch before the strike, damage forged into resolve","built":"the opening self-erasure is the posture that makes the closing vow possible"},
"machine-head~nothing-left":{"haiku":"Emotional erosion, emptiness, and the collapse of faith—a petrified soul that has nothing left to offer anyone after years of being away and worn down by existence.","opus":"A study in emotional deadness and burnout, a soul hardened and shriveled by indifference and resentment, no longer trusting that anyone cares. The exhausted conclusion is that he has nothing more to offer anybody, his rigid resilience preserved only by shutting off feeling entirely.","sonnet":"A person so depleted by bitterness and emotional shutdown that they have nothing left to give anyone — the world has calcified around them, every feeling turned to concrete — yet they insist their core strength will not break even as they push everyone away.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verse builds a world of concrete filling the skies and a soul petrifying, then flips the vow: the singer whose armor supposedly cannot break spends the whole breakdown confessing he has nothing left to give anyone. That is the mask reading itself as a lie — the hardness the chorus swears by is exactly what the emptiness inside has hollowed out, so the boast doubles as an admission of total depletion. The bridge complicates even that, a wound half-nursed as arousal, a man years-absent from his own capacity to feel who mistakes the numbness for endurance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the hardness vow reveals itself as the emptiness's own shape","built":"the boast and the confession occupy the same phrase"},
"machine-head~now-i-lay-thee-down":{"haiku":"A haunting meditation on death and the ambiguous killing of a loved one, exploring whether the act was murder or mercy and questioning divine punishment.","opus":"A narrator wrestling with death and a killing, haunted by whether it was love or murder, and screaming at God after having killed the one he loved. Imagery of dying, tunnels of light, and being laid down runs throughout.","sonnet":"A narrator grapples with having killed the person he loved, cycling through near-death imagery and tunnel-of-light hallucinations before arriving at guilt-ridden anguish — the song blurs whether the act was love or murder, and ends with a scream directed at God.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a murder ballad staged as a deathbed benediction, and the horror is that its lullaby tenderness and its violence are the same gesture — the title's archaic thee frames a killing as a putting-to-sleep. The opening curiosity about how dying feels is answered too literally in the second verse, where the speaker is the one collapsed and bleeding across the boards while a woman takes him under, so the point of view fractures: victim and killer keep trading places until you cannot tell whose death is being sung. The near-death imagery of blanching brightness and a tunnel deliberately withholds resolution, asking whether the light leads out or into a lightless void, and the bridge collapses the whole song into one unanswerable question — was this devotion or was it slaughter, held within the eye of the beholder. Only in the second bridge does the mask drop and the speaker confesses he has slain the single person he loved and turned to scream at God, which recasts every earlier tenderness as a murderer's self-anaesthetizing story. Machine Head, deep in the ambitious long-form writing of The Blackening, uses that scale not to dramatize the crime but to trap the listener inside the beholder's ambiguity, where affection and ruin never separate.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"The band has described it as a Romeo-and-Juliet-esque story — one lover kills the other, then themselves — with the melody kept deliberately sweet so the darkness rides in under it.","themes":["violence & murder","death & grief","love & desire"],"means":"victim and killer trading places until the song cannot separate devotion from slaughter","built":"the archaic thee frames killing as a putting-to-sleep from the opening title"},
"machine-head~now-we-die":{"haiku":"Facing mortality at the world's edge, a community overcomes fear and isolation through collective strength and collective sacrifice.","opus":"An anthem meeting mortality head-on, standing at the world's edge and choosing to embrace death without fear because nothing lasts. Robb Flynn turns the acceptance of dying into a communal act of strength and brotherhood.","sonnet":"A defiant embrace of mortality as liberation — standing at the edge of the world, the narrator and a community of the broken and abused choose to face death together, shed their old lives, and be reborn through the act of dying to who they were.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages a crossing: a self at the world's edge choosing to burn its mortal body away and be reforged into something harder, iron and lionhearted, so the death it keeps announcing reads as chosen transformation rather than defeat. Its imagery runs on two currents that answer each other, the geological and the tidal, unbreakable stone set against ash, tide, and shifting sand, so permanence and erasure argue in every chorus. Beneath the anthem's bravado sits a genuine wound, a fist raised at an indifferent sky demanding to know why, and the answer it finds is not solitary defiance but borrowing another's steadiness to stay upright. The mask is martial and communal, a band of the beaten rising together, promised agelessness, while underneath the real drama is grief being metabolized into fellowship. The repeated toast to dying becomes less a threat than a vow, the price paid willingly so that something can begin.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The album title comes from this lyric: Flynn glossed the bloodstone as a protective amulet of justice, the diamonds as how hard the music felt to them.","fnote2":"First record after Adam Duce's firing and Jared MacEachern's debut on bass; co-produced by Flynn and Juan Urteaga.","themes":["identity & becoming","death & grief","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"grief metabolized into fellowship through a chosen death-vow","built":"the repeated dying-toast reframes ending as the price of beginning"},
"machine-head~old":{"fable":"1994, and the speaker seizes the microphone as everything the culture claims to fear — a blasphemer boasting a six-thousand-year dominion, turning communion backwards so the sacred curdles into vice. It is a mask worn on purpose: the intro's litany of rot indicts the institutions first, so the demon-voice becomes the honest inverse of a compromised piety. The refrain's two-word collapse into a savior's grief is the hinge, folding accuser and accused into one exhausted verdict. And the bridge flips the microphone back: a survivor daring the old power to blind, bury, and drain him, refusing the martyr's ornament — provocation resolving into autobiography, the wound made unkillable.","fableDeep":"The math is the sharpest blasphemy: '6,000 years is the time that I shall reign' is the young-earth creationist age of the world — the profane speaker claims a dominion born the same day as creation, evil as religion's twin, not its rebel child. The miracle runs backwards ('impurity made the blood turn into wine'), and 'Jesus wept' — scripture's shortest verse — is repurposed as the refrain's punchline: divine despair at his own franchise. The crown-of-thorns refusal ('got no crown of thorns on me') renounces martyrdom itself — this voice suffers for no one. The opening frame is the actual thesis: the institution has failed its world so completely that the monster's account of things reads as diagnosis.","haiku":"Rejects religious hypocrisy and divine authority, embodying defiance against faith systems that have failed humanity.","opus":"Voiced by a blasphemous, anti-Christian figure who reigns over a corrupt, hateful world, defies religion, mocks Jesus, and boasts that attempts to blind, bury or drain him will fail.","sonnet":"The narrator attacks organised religion as a corrupt, centuries-old lie that feeds on greed and cruelty, presenting himself as an ancient, defiant force that no crown of thorns or holy authority can subdue.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The album has no title track — Burn My Eyes is named from this song's bridge, the line daring the world to do exactly that.","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"the demon-mask worn to expose a piety already corrupt","built":"the shortest verse in scripture repurposed as the refrain's punchline"},
"machine-head~only-the-names":{"haiku":"A drug dealer named 'Addiction' offers an intoxicating escape that hooks victims, then comes to collect payment for the false promises.","opus":"An extended personification of methamphetamine as a friendly host who welcomes you in, promises invincibility, then reveals the debt. Robb Flynn writes addiction as a con with a twist ending, where the stranger and the user share one name.","sonnet":"A song narrated by personified addiction — it lures someone in with promises of euphoria, hooks them on drugs (specifically meth), then reveals its true name is their addiction and destroys them completely.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the costume of a warm welcome and slides, verse by verse, from host to dealer to collector: hospitality becomes ritual becomes debt, each stage narrated by a voice that already owns the outcome. Its cruelest move is structural, the friendly greeting at the top of every section reappearing after the floor-crawl, so intimacy itself is revealed as the hook rather than the reward. The final turn strips the persona: the speaker was never a person met by chance but the compulsion itself, personified as the one figure the listener can never leave. What looked like generosity is a ledger, and the promise to stay forever reads not as loyalty but as sentence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The band never names the drug; reviewers heard cocaine. The host's anonymity is the design.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"hospitality as the hook, generosity revealed as a debt sentence","built":"the friendly greeting reappears after the floor-crawl, exposing intimacy as the trap"},
"machine-head~pearls-before-the-swine":{"haiku":"The song depicts addiction and spiritual decay disguised as prayer and devotion. Broken rosaries, failed vows, and demonic possession consume the narrator in twilight.","opus":"A grim portrait of addiction cast in religious language, where the addict languishes in damnation, embraces his own denial, and is reeled in completely by falsehood. Broken promises, blackened blood, and a soul stuck in perpetual dusk render dependency as spiritual ruin, faith and love slaughtered on ground of the addict's own making.","sonnet":"A harrowing portrait of addiction using Catholic imagery — broken rosaries, Hail Marys, the twelve-step count — where the addict is caught in an endless cycle of relapse, their soul eclipsed and their body ceding to the substance.","src":"haiku","fable":"A recovering addict's relapse narrated as a wrecked Catholic passion play: the drug becomes the divine object of worship, and every ritual gesture of faith is inverted into an instrument of self-destruction. The song builds its whole image-system from religion turned toxic — rosaries snapped, hands bound in prayer to hopeless dreams, communion reimagined as swallowing a baited fishhook — so that devotion and sickness become the same motion. Movement drives the meaning: the opening lies frozen in a deathless stupor, the middle counts each failed step against itself, and the close begs to be flayed and left exposed, faith curdling into a plea for annihilation rather than grace. The recurring squandered-treasure motif of the title names the cruelty at the core — everything precious, love and trust and the self, thrown down before something that can only trample it. What wears the mask of grand liturgical surrender is really the addict's honest report from inside the clutch, worship and self-loathing indistinguishable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Conceived while the band was watching Breaking Bad; Flynn was blunt that it is written from inside the claws — not a song of hope.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"devotion and self-destruction performed as the same motion","built":"inverted liturgy — rosaries, communion, fishhook — becomes addiction's grammar"},
"machine-head~psychotic":{"haiku":"Mental unraveling and suicidal ideation with loss of self-control; trapped in psychological breakdown without escape.","opus":"Robb Flynn's Bay Area outfit Machine Head render a full mental breakdown in unflinching first person, voicing a man at his breaking point who argues with himself as he loses control and begs to be kept away from anything he could hurt himself with. Restraint and a death wish make the title feel less like a label than a scream from inside the anguish.","sonnet":"A mind coming apart at the seams — not in metaphor but in visceral physical detail, the speaker cataloguing the stench, the bleeding, the compulsive picking and scratching of a psyche in freefall. Self-destruction and a desperate clinging to sanity battle without resolution, ending in the image of a needle and damage already done.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stays sealed inside a nervous system turning on itself, withholding any external cause: a man argues with himself because he cannot govern his own mind, and every image of confinement is self-generated. The tightening rope is his own, right down to fingers that curl into a fist even as he begs the straitjacket off. Its real movement runs from restraint to the wrong kind of release, where the verses fight to stay sane but the bridge stops fighting and names the thing outright. It folds suicide and survival into one act, so killing the self becomes the only way through, the damage already done. The chorus holds the whole condition in miniature, a scream with no sound, so the frantic laughter reads as mask and the silent scream beneath is what the song is about: pain loud enough to fill a room and land on no one.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","suicide","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"killing the self as the only way through a mind that has already stopped fighting","built":"the scream with no sound in the chorus holds the whole condition: pain that lands on no one"},
"machine-head~razorblade-smile":{"haiku":"Chaotic hedonism and reckless abandon; an outlaw narrative of drug use, sexual excess, and contempt for social order.","opus":"A deliberately outrageous romp through drug excess and provocation, the narrator playing debased rock star reveling in the filth. Between the substance-fueled bragging come crude political jabs at Trump and the border. Robb Flynn's groove-metal band lean fully into shock and satire, with a garish hearse waiting at the end for a narrator who swears he's beyond rescue.","sonnet":"A chaotic, confrontational self-portrait of someone glorying in the gutter — politically contemptuous, chemically wrecked, and proudly outside respectable society. The swagger is real but the trajectory is a polka-dot hearse, and the song knows it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The grin here is pure bravado from a self-styled winner slumming it in the gutter, but every boast of appetite and freedom rides on a chemical undertow the speaker names in the same breath, so the swagger is never free of what it costs. The scattered political taunts function less as argument than as one wrecked voice channeling a whole nation's derangement, until the boaster and the country he curses collapse into a single self-destructing body. The recurring turn from magnetic freak to restraint to the grave compresses the entire arc, the mania ticking down like a fuse. What the final ride to the plot admits is not a man circumstance failed to save, but one who staked everything on never wanting to be.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","suicide","politics & society"],"means":"a man who staked everything on never wanting to be saved from himself","built":"the boaster and the country he curses collapse into a single self-destructing body"},
"machine-head~real-eyes-realize-real-lies":{"haiku":"Sampled commentary on police brutality, racial conflict, and gang violence in L.A., emphasizing the difficulty of truth and difference amid chaos.","opus":"A sound collage assembled from news broadcasts and racist rhetoric surrounding the 1992 Los Angeles riots, layering police violence, looting, and clashing voices from opposing factions. The title's pun frames the whole piece as an indictment of a country unwilling to see its own racial fractures and desperate for upheaval.","sonnet":"A collage of sampled voices from riots, police brutality incidents, and racial conflict in America — the song's thesis is embedded in its title: to truly see reality is to recognize the lies that structure it, particularly around race and power.","src":"haiku","fable":"Built entirely from news audio and street voices of the 1992 L.A. riots, the track lets a nation argue with itself: broadcast anchors reporting looting and the call for 100,000 more officers on one side, and on the other the raw voices of the streets, white-supremacist venom and gang members explaining why they cannot lower their guns while their brothers are being shot. The title pun is the only editorial hand Machine Head allow themselves — the same syllables split three ways into real eyes, realize, real lies — a demand that you see clearly through a media reality engineered to deceive, with the lone screamed Why left hanging as the record's refusal to resolve the noise into an answer. Nearly wordless, it works as documentary rather than song, indicting the whole spectrum it samples by simply refusing to editorialize, so that the recurring line about America needing some kind of radical change lands not as a slogan but as the exhausted verdict of everyone talking past each other.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Flynn built the collage deliberately: extreme white-supremacist voices, then extreme black-nationalist voices, then the hunt for middle ground. The title phrase was found as graffiti on cement pillars near a punk club he frequented.","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","violence & murder"],"means":"a nation indicted by its own voices placed without editorial hand","built":"the title splits into three readings that together name the piece's entire method"},
"machine-head~sail-into-the-black":{"haiku":"A person confronts secrets, regrets, and mysteries trapped in someone's eyes while sailing into oblivion. Terror and betrayal accumulate as they rise or fall together into darkness.","opus":"A dark voyage into oblivion and self-reckoning, drifting toward nothingness as secrets, regrets and old wounds dissolve away. The throughline is confronting humanity's failings and one's own fate, the scars and comforting fictions, a soured bond, and the choice to vanish, wrecked and beyond repair, into death or the void.","sonnet":"A catalog of irreversible losses — secrets, regrets, heartaches, mistakes — that accumulate until the self is shattered like glass and the only honest motion is to sail into the unknown dark, carrying the horror and betrayal and silk-thread bonds that have all rotted away.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song moves seaward and downward at once: three verses of near-identical litanies, each line a small failure or loss cataloged in the second person, the anaphora itself becoming the tide that carries everything under. What the mask presents as elegy underneath is confession — the drift toward the black is not the sea's doing but an accumulation, every catalogued mistake and buried regret adding weight until the fourth verse abandons the litany's rhythm for outright collapse. The one image worth holding is the extinguishing candle: a private light going out while the drums pound on, the singer circling back to the burning-eyes image that titled his band's debut as the thing binding a life together sours and rots.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Carries Flynn-arranged live strings, as do Now We Die and Eyes of the Dead — Bay Area session players, with a reported forty tracks of stacked vocals.","themes":["death & grief","shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"accumulated catalogued failures pulling the self under without external force","built":"the anaphora becomes the tide itself, its rhythm doing the drowning"},
"machine-head~screaming-at-the-sun":{"haiku":"Unyielding defiance against tyranny and control; screaming into the void until consciousness is extinguished rather than submit.","opus":"A defiance anthem structured as an extended chess match, the narrator maneuvering to topple a corrupt opponent as the stakes escalate to a frenzy. Beneath the strategy is a refusal to accept tyranny as normal, a howl of protest sustained to exhaustion. Robb Flynn's Bay Area groove-metal band fold political resistance into their signature fury, warning that tolerating ugliness only invites harm.","sonnet":"Political resistance cast as chess — each move counters a system that has blinded and bloodied its citizens, and the speaker refuses to normalize what is happening even as the cost is sanity itself. The screaming at the sun is not futile rage but a deliberate refusal to look away.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages an entire conflict as a chess game, and the read lives in how that ordered image-system corrodes. Crook, queen, rook, pawn, bishop, knight, and king advance as if maneuvering could master the enemy, yet the language keeps collapsing into fractured-bone brutality and a narrator unsure whether his sanity has been seized or preserved. A recurring witness-line, about being dazzled and forced to see everything monstrous, recasts the match as a refusal to let a tyrant become ordinary, so the board is really a barrier against admitting the predator and being torn open. The closing checkmate stays unresolved on purpose: the sun he rages at scorches and blinds him without ever burning out, so the pledge to keep howling until his soul expires reads less as triumph than as the price of refusing to turn away.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"refusing to let a tyrant become ordinary at the cost of one's own sanity","built":"the chess board corrodes under its own imagery, the ordered match collapsing into fractured bone"},
"machine-head~silver":{"haiku":"Inner fragmentation and the struggle to unify a broken self; the singer witnesses walls collapsing and seeks transformation, wanting to escape internal hell and transcend pain.","opus":"An early Machine Head reach for self-reinvention, cracking a hardened shell to escape inner hell, wrestling with pain and reflection to find the whole part of himself and remake the man he wants to become.","sonnet":"A song about trying to break out of a hardened emotional shell and reach something whole — the narrator asks for a hand across a difficult terrain, watches walls fall and false gods collapse, and pushes toward liberation from pain.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man who moves through the world armored and complicit, bargaining with gold and violence, then admits he cannot locate the intact part of himself. The song's whole verb-drive is fabrication: he asks to be pulled elsewhere and assembled from scratch, as if selfhood were a thing you build to survive rather than something already there. Its cruelty lands in the turn outward, where the same drowning coldness he begs to escape gets diagnosed in someone else, the plea for rescue curdling into a verdict.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the plea for rescue curdles into a verdict on another","built":"the fabrication verb-drive frames selfhood as something built not found"},
"machine-head~slanderous":{"haiku":"Reclaiming slurs and insults as a response to systemic oppression, questioning internal hatred and division while resisting dehumanization.","opus":"The narrator adopts every slur hurled at him as an outcast, turning bigoted names back on those who use them to divide and conquer. It hinges on the tension between loving and hating a brother, and whether that hatred is really self-hatred.","sonnet":"The song reclaims every slur and marginalising label hurled at the outcast — addict, reject, deviant — and turns them into a statement of solidarity, asking why hatred of others might really be self-hatred, and calling out forces that divide and silence the vulnerable.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's provocation is that the speaker piles every slur and slander onto himself and then embraces the names, reclaiming the language of division by wearing all of it at once — reject and addict, and across the escalating verses the full spectrum of ethnic and homophobic slurs — so that no single group can be pitted against another when one voice claims them all. That is the thesis the build-up before each chorus states outright: an unnamed they profit by splitting the many and ruling the pieces, silencing people by turning them against each other, and the defiant thrust of raised fingers answers with solidarity built from shared insult. The chorus makes the mechanism internal and unresolved, cycling from an avowal of love to a cry of hatred until brother-loathing and self-loathing become indistinguishable — the closing question of whether he despises his brother or himself is the whole point, since the venom sold to us as aimed outward is really turned in. Machine Head, in The Blackening's era of political fury, weaponizes offensiveness against the offense of bigotry, forcing the listener to sit inside the ugliness to feel how the tactic of splitting-to-rule actually works.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"wearing every slur at once so no group can be pitted against another","built":"the chorus cycles from love to hatred until brother-loathing and self-loathing become indistinguishable"},
"machine-head~spine":{"haiku":"The narrator endures contempt and deception in a city that bred his bitterness; pain is his only form of self-expression. He resists conformity despite alienation.","opus":"A hardened stare-down with a cruel, unfair existence that condemned the speaker from childhood into adulthood. Molded by a pitiless urban upbringing and wounded by phony, deceitful people, he swears to climb past the absence of affection, insisting others share this same numb ache.","sonnet":"Growing up alienated, detested, and pushed out — a hard city made the speaker cold and suspicious, surrounded by people who fake their way through life while chipping away at his dignity, and he can see through all of them even as the strain wears him down.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song locates its rage at an intersection: a speaker who names bitterness, arrest, and alienation not as private wounds but as an environment that shaped him, then turns the accusation outward at a liar corroding his dignity. Its cruelty is that the same forces breeding contempt also breed grief, so the sneer at those who fake and betray keeps collapsing back into sadness he refuses to explain, mad and sad braided into one refusal to justify the feeling. The turn arrives at the end, when the private grievance widens into a claim of company — the certainty that this damage is shared, that whatever the place and the betrayals made of him, they made it of others too, and that recognition is the closest thing to redemption the song will allow.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","money & the street"],"means":"private grievance widened into a claim of shared damage","built":"the closing recognition of company is the only redemption allowed"},
"machine-head~struck-a-nerve":{"haiku":"Society and its hypocrisy demand awakening; complacent happiness masks decay, and change is cosmetic while human nature repeats its cycles.","opus":"Mid-90s Bay Area fury at conformity and religious moralizing, raging that nothing really changes while people file obediently into line. The chorus turns cynicism into a wake-up call aimed at complacency itself.","sonnet":"A song about societal stagnation and religious hypocrisy — the narrator rails against pro-life politics, the pious 'holier than thou' crowd, and a population too comfortable in apathy to ever actually change anything.","src":"haiku","fable":"The prayer-mantra frame is the whole song: it opens by hijacking sacred petition (power, courage, patience, balance) and pointing it outward at a righteous adversary — the holier-than-thou moralist — so the sound of supplication is turned into a weapon against the piously certain. The chorus then swallows that anger in its own tautology: the more things change, the more they stay the same, a refrain whose looping structure enacts the futility it names, draining the opening's fury into resignation. What sharpens it is the self-indictment underneath the target — the singer counts himself among those too comfortable in their apathy, filing smiling and single into line — so the mask of the crusader slips to reveal complicity, and the closing plea to wake up is aimed as much inward as out, a demand issued from inside the very numbness it protests.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The album is named from this song's chorus — the old proverb about change and sameness, ellipsis and all.","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","faith & the occult"],"means":"the accusation aimed as much inward as outward","built":"the looping chorus enacts the futility it names"},
"machine-head~supercharger":{"haiku":"Four musicians generate collective electrical power and overwhelming intensity that spreads like an inescapable rash. Their energy shocks and amplifies through shared force.","opus":"An electrified anthem of band-to-audience energy, a chain reaction of power surging through veins to overwhelm and fill the listener's void. The battle-cry framing (kamikaze, Braveheart, winning the war) makes it a celebration of collective, amplified force feeding between performer and crowd.","sonnet":"The live connection between a band and its audience framed as an electrical charge — the music as a hundred-thousand-volt surge that fills the void in the listener and synchronizes all energy into one unstoppable force.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track for the album Machine Head made when their nu-metal pivot was chasing the mainstream plays exactly like a product pitch. A single electrical metaphor gets pushed to its limit, the band as generator wiring their current straight into the listener's veins to fill some dreadful hollow the song insists you're carrying. What keeps it from being empty hype is the circuit it draws — the four of them feeding off the crowd, the crowd feeding back, a current that only loops between stage and pit. That loop is both the honest thesis of arena metal and, given the band's floundering at this moment, a slightly desperate bet that sheer wattage can substitute for having something to say.","fv":2,"themes":["artifice & performance","party & hedonism"],"means":"sheer wattage offered as substitute for having something to say","built":"the feedback loop is both the honest thesis and the desperate bet"},
"machine-head~take-me-through-the-fire":{"haiku":"A hardened man endures trials and persists through burning pain and ignorance. He vows not to be blinded or broken by obstacles.","opus":"A hardened survivor's vow of perseverance: bruised and made callous by a merciless world, an emptied-out soul demands to be trials-by-fired and toughened rather than blinded, warning enemies who slander him that he'll retaliate ruthlessly, following whatever lifts him up through the flames.","sonnet":"A man hardened by betrayal and surrounded by enemies calls out for something — a force, a person, a belief — to carry him through the ordeal, insisting he will prevail and take retribution as he passes through.","src":"haiku","fable":"A ritual of self-hardening: the singer strips away masks and social softness to face betrayal, then reforges the wounded self into something volcanic and unfeeling, asking to be walked through purifying flame rather than spared it. The movement runs from numb hollowing through defiant endurance to a final vow that fuses supplicant and warrior, the pilgrim who follows if lifted becoming the executioner who claims a head. Underneath the mineral bravado sits the tell that this armor is grief tempered into aggression, transformation offered as the only exit from a heart gone cold.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","death & grief"],"means":"grief tempered into aggression as the only exit from a heart gone cold","built":"the supplicant who asks to be led through fire becomes the executioner by the final vow"},
"machine-head~take-my-scars":{"haiku":"Rejection of false beliefs and division to find a truer path, carrying the scars of hardship while demanding others open their eyes.","opus":"Machine Head rage against inherited faith and false saviors, setting the pampered who never had to fight against the narrator's lived pain. He demands people wake up to the deception and declares he has found his own route to tear down the barriers built around him.","sonnet":"A rejection of a hypocritical belief system — presented as a false messiah that teaches division and numbs people to suffering — contrasted with the narrator's own harder, unsparing experience of life at street level.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's structure argues its politics before its words do: the verses build an accusation, but the chorus refuses to name an enemy and instead offers up its own damage as the price of escape, turning grievance into a strange act of surrender. What starts as a portrait of an inherited institution—something ancient, festering, dressed in false holiness—collapses in the outro into a squalid catalogue of bodies and rituals, so the target stops being any one system and becomes the whole machinery of comfortable belief. That final movement is the read: the repeated demand to see clearly disintegrates into wordless howls, the argument abandoning language exactly where it wants understanding most, admitting that fury this deep can name what it hates but can't make you feel it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Sung from the viewpoint of the Devil — the institution the read watches festering is being indicted by its own adversary's voice.","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","freedom & escape"],"means":"fury deep enough to dissolve its own argument","built":"the outro howls replace words exactly where the argument needs them most"},
"machine-head~ten-fold": {"haiku":"A band grows stronger by ignoring false friends and rumors, dismissing those who spread negativity while their power increases sevenfold.","opus":"A grudge track about backstabbing hangers-on and the rumor mill, Robb Flynn spitting at fake friends while insisting the band only grows stronger from the noise. Even the jab at their maligned album turns bad blood into fuel.","sonnet":"A confrontational dismissal of fake friends who spread rumors and talk shit behind the band's back — the narrator cuts them out completely and takes satisfaction that despite the gossip, they only grow stronger.","src":"haiku","fable":"Flynn turns a falling-out into a settling of accounts, calling out the hangers-on who talk him up to his face and gut him the moment he leaves the room, while insisting the band only compounds strength every time it gets slandered. The vow at the center is total refusal — not the smallest handout, not one favor, the free ride revoked in full — and it curdles into open contempt as he name-checks the polarizing prior record and the fans who wrote it off, throwing their verdict back as proof he owes them nothing. The refrain is the argument itself: gossip multiplies and so, defiantly, does he, one word repeated until spite hardens into a growth chart.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"spite weaponized as a proof of growth","built":"the refrain repeats its one word until contempt hardens into a growth chart"},
"machine-head~ten-ton-hammer":{"haiku":"A force of vengeance and wrath strikes like a hammer, breaking down obstacles and enemies through brutal force and accumulated hatred.","opus":"Machine Head voice a narrator who becomes the living manifestation of an enemy's guilt, dread and undoing, vowing to crack open their lies. The threat is fueled by hatred he insists is genuine and deep-rooted, promising to pound them apart with overwhelming force.","sonnet":"The speaker positions himself as the embodiment of someone else's blame, fear, and denial — the very thing they despise and cannot escape — and promises to be the unstoppable force that brings them down.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the mask of a bully or abuser addressing a victim, but it speaks in the second person as a force that has fused with the target: the illness, the assigned blame, the flaw hidden inside a lie. Underneath the swagger it admits its own ache is genuine, so the threat reads as a wound turned outward — a beaten thing rising to strike back with the flat blunt weapon of the title rather than any finesse. The final turn drops the posture entirely into fatigue, one who cannot face one more sunrise, dressing exhaustion as a lesson handed down to whoever underestimated it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","violence & murder"],"means":"wound turned outward and dressed as threat","built":"the posture drops at the close, reframing the swing as exhaustion"},
"machine-head~the-blood-the-sweat-the-tears":{"haiku":"A person overcomes internal anguish and self-destruction by breaking through the barriers they've built, channeling suffering into resilience and unstoppable resolve.","opus":"A song of hard-earned survival, tracing a path from a shattered, hate-poisoned, self-wrecking place to facing the origin of the pain and dismantling the defenses erected around himself. It ends as a salute to the resilience forged over long, grueling years of struggle.","sonnet":"A person recounts moving through self-destructive pain and false inclusion to a place of hard-won confidence, recognising that the struggle — blood, sweat, and tears — is itself the source of genuine strength.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages recovery as combat: it opens with a body reduced to a cracked husk pinned flat and vomiting its own venom, a false community that only deepened the sickness, then pivots on the admission that the suffering was itself the addiction, the edge clung to on purpose. What turns it is the discovery of a rival hunger inside the wreckage — a wanting sharper than any enemy's — which the chorus rebrands as accumulated toll, the years of cost repackaged as earned muscle rather than damage. The final movement literalizes the fight, a stammer of orders barked at the self and a refusal to drop that keeps repeating because it isn't yet true, closing on the grim consolation that the injury will only scar over with time, never actually heal.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"years of cost repackaged as earned muscle rather than damage","built":"the repeated self-command stammer enacts a refusal not yet true"},
"machine-head~the-burning-red":{"haiku":"A person experiences suicidal despair, blinded and drowning by past trauma and darkness they cannot escape or fight.","opus":"The title track's ballad of despair and suicidal exhaustion, blinded by a false dawn, riding a phoenix into madness while begging someone to hold on. Rare vulnerability from Flynn amid the aggression.","sonnet":"A song about a mental and emotional breakdown — the narrator is drowning in grief or depression, blinded, hypnotized by darkness, seeing the light of suicide, and barely clinging to someone to hold onto as the ocean of pain opens under him.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song watches a night's collapse from inside, where daybreak arrives as a blinding rather than a mercy and vision is already ruined by weeping. Its two poles pull against each other: an ocean tearing open and scars begging to close, a phoenix ridden into madness set against a plea to just be held. The mask is glide and rebirth; underneath, the speaker keeps sinking and going colder, so the rising sun reads not as escape but as the moment survival narrows to one held hand.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title track addresses Flynn's suicide attempt at seventeen.","themes":["suicide","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"survival narrows to a single held hand rather than escape","built":"phoenix-and-rebirth imagery rides directly over a speaker who keeps sinking"},
"machine-head~the-frontlines":{"haiku":"Society ignores warnings and slips into complacency, worshiping violence and deception while the speaker watches inaction and betrayal unfold.","opus":"A furious indictment of passivity in the face of encroaching tyranny: authorities assured everyone there was nothing to fear, so people grew careless and averted their eyes, and now they watch it all unravel because nobody resisted; deliverance is unmasked as false hope peddled to the desperate, and devotion as terror.","sonnet":"A song about collective failure and complicity — a society that was warned of coming catastrophe, dismissed it, turned away, and now watches everything disintegrate; the narrator is consumed by the machinery of false salvation while everyone stands by doing nothing.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages faith as forced conscription: a burning idol commands obedience while devotion itself is diagnosed as sickness, and the salvation being peddled is openly vomit — a religion that poisons the throats it claims to save. Beneath that mask of ritual sits a plainer indictment: a generation was reassured nothing bad was coming, squandered the interval on nothing, and looked elsewhere while the ground gave way. The clenched-teeth boast at the center curdles the accusation, because the same voice claiming to be a surging weapon is confessing complicity in the collapse it watched arrive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"the boast of resistance curdles into confessed complicity","built":"salvation inverts — the thing that saves poisons the throat"},
"machine-head~the-rage-to-overcome":{"haiku":"Pain and injustice breed courage and focus; hatred transforms into will and determination to command survival through closed-fist rage.","opus":"A man who feels cast off by an indifferent world and marked by lasting pain converts his hatred into disciplined resolve; the deeper read turns injustice and suffering into fuel, an unclenched perception paired with a clenched hand, the fury to endure and prevail.","sonnet":"A song about converting deep personal pain and injustice into controlled, purposeful anger — the narrator refuses to be destroyed by a world that rejected him, transforming hatred into focus, courage, and the determination to survive.","src":"haiku","fable":"A young man diagnoses himself as damage the world produced and then discards him, so the first half is a wound turned inward—trauma burned into the mind, sanity purchased only by demolishing whatever caused it. The second half converts that same injury into fuel, recasting suffering and unfairness as the source of nerve and discipline, so that the identical verse returns not as complaint but as vow. The song's closing gesture pairs clear thought with a hand cocked to strike: awareness and aggression held in a single stance, the record's whole thesis that lucidity and violence need not be opposites.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"suffering converted into discipline without surrendering the fist","built":"the identical verse returned as vow rather than complaint"},
"machine-head~the-sentinel-judas-priest-cover": {"haiku":"A condemned warrior stands alone, feared and impassive, awaiting challengers in a ritual duel to the death.","opus":"A cinematic post-apocalyptic showdown in ruined streets: a lone knife-throwing avenger, sworn to vengeance and condemned to hell, coldly slaughters a gang of challengers and stands expressionless over the bodies-a portrait of a killer utterly unmoved by the death he sows.","sonnet":"A narrative about a lone warrior figure — feared, condemned, sworn to vengeance — who ambushes a group of challengers with throwing knives in a dead city street, killing them all and walking away unmoved, existing only for violence and retribution.","src":"haiku","fable":"Priest's original is a cinematic tableau: a lone executioner-figure stalks a dead, smoke-choked cityscape, is swarmed by a mob clutching scrap iron, and cuts them all down with the flat blades sheathed across his chest before standing over the corpses, unmoved by the slaughter he has authored. The song withholds all motive — no cause is named, only a chanted code of vengeance and damnation ringing over the wreckage — so the terror is that killing here is pure vocation, a duty discharged without appetite or remorse. It builds like a duel: deserted quiet, a great tolling chime counting down, the standoff eating at the clock, then a sudden hush that unleashes the massacre. Machine Head's selection is the tell — Robb Flynn typically turns his fury confessionally inward, so reaching for Priest's impassive killer is a deliberate step out of the self into borrowed myth, honoring the British-steel lineage his Oakland groove descends from rather than exorcising anything personal. The cover doesn't reinterpret the story so much as flex it, wrapping the leaner classic-metal narrative in heavier downtuned menace that makes the figure's calm feel colder still.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"inherited myth borrowed to honor a lineage, not to confess one","built":"the cover steps Flynn deliberately outside his confessional mode into borrowed impassivity"},
"machine-head~this-is-the-end":{"haiku":"A final goodbye to a past relationship and people who suffocated and disrespected; the singer rejects all they came from and demands respect or complete separation.","opus":"A scorched-earth severance anthem, framing the cutting-off of a poisonous relationship as a refusal to live in fear of the future. The betrayal and forsaken-brother imagery makes it feel like a band or friendship self-destructing, and Flynn chooses defiance over grief.","sonnet":"The speaker makes a final, definitive break from people who suffocated, condescended to, and denied them respect, declaring that all shared history is now over. It combines a farewell to dead-end chasing with a call for those who remain to stand together or step aside.","src":"haiku","fable":"A severance dressed as liberation: the song opens by torching the past and marching into an open-armed future, but its choruses keep circling back to grief, so every declaration of freedom rings as a wound worried at instead of healed. The middle collapses the mask entirely, turning from defiant kiss-off into a curse and then into a raw sibling lament borrowing Christ's cry of abandonment, which exposes the whole tirade as betrayal by people once loved as kin. What the swagger underneath is really doing is willing a decision into permanence, casting the lot so the singer can't crawl back to the safety he claims to scorn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","family & blood"],"means":"casting the lot before grief can reverse the decision","built":"the defiant kiss-off breaks into Christ's cry of abandonment at the bridge"},
"machine-head~trephination":{"haiku":"A person recounts childhood trauma and abuse that fractured their mind, followed by years of migraines and psychological torment, fantasizing about violence as release but choosing recovery instead.","opus":"A wrenchingly literal account of childhood sexual abuse and its aftermath: innocence stripped away at age five, a childhood of crippling migraines and self-hatred, guilt during intimacy, drug abuse, and a fantasy of boring into his own skull that curdles into a revenge fantasy of putting a bullet through his abuser's head.","sonnet":"A confession of childhood sexual abuse and its long aftermath — the narrator was molested at five, spent years in migraine-ridden dissociation fantasising about drilling a hole in his skull to relieve the pressure, and eventually channels the rage outward in imagined revenge against his abuser.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's speaker medicalizes his own violence: trephination, the ancient surgery of boring a hole to release what torments a skull, becomes the governing figure for a mind that reads its childhood wound as a defect lodged beneath the flesh, unreachable by any washing. Across its verses the speaker ages from a small boy who suspects he is the broken thing into a young man whose pain has curdled into appetite, and the drill he once fantasized turning on himself rotates outward, until the surgery he imagines is no longer his own trepanned skull but a wound opened in someone else. What starts as a plea to let suffering out ends as a wish to put suffering in, the same instrument aimed the other way, and the title's promised cure is quietly reassigned from healing to the killing he says will finally set him loose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Flynn wrote this and The Burning Red's Five about the abuse he suffered at age five — the songs aired it years before he first spoke of it in an interview, and he refuses to play either one live.","themes":["shame & repression","violence & murder","madness & the mind"],"means":"the surgeon's drill rotating from the self outward to the abuser","built":"the title's promised cure is quietly reassigned from healing to killing"},
"machine-head~triple-beam":{"haiku":"A drug dealer's life of paranoia, violence, and meth addiction spirals into chaos; every choice traps them deeper.","opus":"Robb Flynn mines his own past, having Machine Head narrate life inside meth dealing, where brutality served as the yardstick and dealers rarely grew old. Amid loyalty tests and debts paid in flesh, the story climaxes in a street fight that leaves a friend fatally stabbed. There's no lesson offered, only the wreckage of weighing a life against the drug trade.","sonnet":"A first-person account of life inside methamphetamine distribution — violence as a measuring stick, paranoia as a daily condition. One night outside a petrol station crystallizes into near-murder as the narrator watches his friend stab a stranger repeatedly, proof of what it costs to weigh existence on a drug scale.","src":"haiku","fable":"There is no moral offered and the listener is told to read the unspoken remainder, yet the track keeps supplying only more arithmetic, converting each gram upward into a bigger unit until brutality itself becomes the ruler used to take life's measure. The drug scale governs everything: loyalty tested by whether you inform or knife a partner, sex traded to settle what cash cannot, a loan refused because accepting it would lock him permanently inside that world. All of it gets weighed to fractions, and the tallying finally becomes physical in a late-night gas-station brawl where a friend's blade lands again and again until a body stops. The chant returns unaltered because a balance issues no verdict; it registers no meaning, it only tells you the weight.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Autobiographical by Flynn's own account: the early-90s stretch when he dealt — and was hooked on — speed in the Bay Area, an era that once brought gang death threats down on the band.","themes":["money & the street","violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the drug scale as the only ruler used to measure loyalty, sex, and life","built":"the chant returns unaltered at the end because a balance issues no verdict, only weight"},
"machine-head~vim": {"haiku":"Exhortation toward resilience and inner strength in the face of systemic oppression. The song calls to break cycles of error, shed envy, and reclaim power through persistence and light.","opus":"A defiant anthem of perseverance and self-belief, refusing to bend against stacked odds and vowing to rise from the grave toward wholeness. It urges holding on to what you love, letting go of what starves you, and blocking out the world's hatred.","sonnet":"A call for collective perseverance and moral fortitude, urging the listener to face the truth, let go of grudges and envy, and carve a path worth following all the way to the grave. The song frames strength as a disciplined choice against doubt, hatred, and stagnation.","src":"haiku","fable":"On Through the Ashes of Empires — the record Machine Head made under threat of extinction after Supercharger tanked and the label dropped them — Vim is Robb Flynn's rally cry back from the dead, and it reads as a manifesto sung entirely in the plural, a first-person swollen into a we so the fight stops being one man's private struggle and becomes the band's collective act of endurance. The lyric refuses narrative for accumulation: it piles verbs of exertion and refusal one atop the next — grip, rise, smash, block out — until the sheer stacking becomes the meaning, insisting survival is not a feeling but a labor performed second by second. Its one governing image is the grave repurposed as a launch point, mortality reframed not as an ending but as the floor you shove off from, so death and living are made to slam into each other as one charged instant. Where it turns confessional is the admission that failure was baked in from the opening move and that the doubts have to be carved out of the self and cast away — the wound tended in public rather than hidden, the strongman's mask dropped to show the frightened man underneath still choosing to stand. The title names it flat: vim is raw vital force, and the song is that force willed into being by shouting it aloud.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty","identity & becoming"],"means":"survival staged not as a feeling but as labor performed second by second","built":"the grave recast as the floor you shove off from, death and living slammed into one instant"},
"machine-head~violate":{"haiku":"A confrontation about violation and betrayal—the speaker condemns someone for defiling innocence and abusing power, refusing forgiveness and expressing rage.","opus":"A song of rage and betrayal by someone who used sacred lies to hurt and defile the narrator; refusing to pray for the abuser, he channels wounded fury into a desire for the person to die before feeling his rage, condemning them for violating innocence and then calling themselves a man.","sonnet":"A song directed at a sexual abuser — the narrator confronts someone who violated innocence and calls it power, building toward pure rage and a barely contained threat of retaliatory violence.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages a two-person exchange as call-and-response, then withholds the one gesture that would close it: the speaker refuses to pray for his abuser, and that refusal is the whole point, meaning turns on being unable to grant absolution because absolution itself was among the things stolen. Machine Head sets this against Robb Flynn's other engine here, a rap-metal cadence where rage is explicitly reframed as power that can be metabolized and turned outward, so the track moves from wound to fuel. The title verb names the accusation squarely, that violation is how the other party manufactures a sense of manhood, and the read the song keeps insisting on is that this claimed manhood is a lie the violence tells about itself. The last word is left dangling on purpose, the sentence about feeling his rage cut off just before its object, so the threat lands as the thing that finally arrives after every restraint has been stripped away.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","faith & the occult","shame & repression","violence & murder"],"means":"refusing absolution because absolution was among the things stolen","built":"the sentence about rage is cut before its object — the threat lands as absence"},
"machine-head~volatile":{"haiku":"Rage against racism, privilege, and political apathy; a refusal to accept the normalization of hatred in volatile times.","opus":"A blunt state-of-the-nation blast against resurgent racism, Nazis in the streets, gun-lobby fearmongering, and scapegoated immigrants, the narrator sick to his teeth and calling the whole era a rotting corpse. The chorus insists no space remains for the haters. Machine Head, veterans of Bay Area groove metal, aimed this squarely at Trump-era America, blunt-force protest over pounding riffs.","sonnet":"A furious political broadside against white supremacist marches, NRA posturing, and the silence of good men who let bad men fill the void — the speaker is sick of the machinery of hate and the media that amplifies it, and the repeated declaration that these times are volatile doubles as both a warning and a verdict on the current moment.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song holds its rage without release, beginning as a fantasy of striking terror before flipping to identify who the singer actually loathes: bigots, gun lobbyists, self-appointed elites, and marchers waving hateful flags. Its core insight is that decent people swallow their objections out of fear, leaving the loudest cowards to dominate every conversation, which frames the track's own volume as a chosen answer to that surrender. The recurring image carries the argument, casting not a foe but the era itself as a swollen carcass, so the warning that nothing remains for the listener reads as the suffocation of dwelling inside decay. When the title word finally fractures apart into a wrecking-crew command to level everything, the sustained composure gives way to the plain destructive craving beneath it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written and sung the day of the Charlottesville attack, after Flynn watched the footage of Heather Heyer's killing; the song is dedicated to her.","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"decent people's swallowed silence named as the real engine of cowardice's dominance","built":"the title fractures into a wrecking command only after the sustained composure has made its case"},
"machine-head~white-knuckle-blackout":{"haiku":"Adrenaline as fuel for overcoming obstacles and confronting challengers, turning fear into intensity and using aggression to power through self-imposed barriers.","opus":"A hymn to adrenaline as fuel and lubricant, the body's fight response harnessed to conquer obstacles and hurl defiance at anyone too cool to try. Machine Head make raw physiological drive into a middle-fingered creed of self-propulsion.","sonnet":"Adrenaline as the only fuel that matters — focusing through fear, converting every challenge and every contemptuous person into propulsive energy — a declaration that obstacles and enemies sharpen rather than diminish the narrator.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages an addict's self-portrait dressed as an athlete's or thrill-seeker's creed: the narrator treats his own bloodstream as a delivery system for something he needs to feel alive, and every physical symptom he catalogs — flushed skin, hair rising, the body heating toward a boiling point — reads simultaneously as a chemical high and as the mounting panic of a man on the brink. Underneath the boast of feeding on danger, the confession slips out that fear is buried like ore in a mineshaft, hidden behind everything he shows the world, which reframes the swagger as compensation rather than conquest. By the outro the mask hardens into pure aggression, adrenaline recast as lubricant and fuel and finally as a raised middle finger, so the arc moves from private hunger to outward hostility — the payoff of the rush is not transcendence but a snarl aimed at anyone who doubts him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"buried fear dressed as a thrill-seeker's creed","built":"the boast arc ends in a snarl aimed outward, not transcendence"},
"machine-head~who-we-are":{"haiku":"The unwanted and forgotten masses declare their identity and worth, rising as a force destined to reshape the world.","opus":"Robb Flynn's anthem for the discarded and disavowed, the poor, angry and abandoned claiming identity through defiance. It's a communal roar of belonging among the outcast, dignity forged from being written off by society and God alike.","sonnet":"A manifesto for the disinherited — the broken, the deprived, the angry, the drunk, the unwanted — declaring collective identity and defiance against a system and a God that abandoned them, insisting they are the future regardless.","src":"haiku","fable":"A metal anthem that recruits the discarded into a single body, the song stacks litany after litany of society's cast-offs — the beaten, the addicted, the imprisoned, the young — until the sheer accumulation of the unwanted becomes its own defiant power. Its signature paradox reworks the old solidarity slogan so that fracture itself, not unity, is the thing that lets this crowd hold its ground, insisting that a people already broken apart can only stand precisely because they refuse to be assimilated. Beneath the fist-raising defiance runs a bleaker undertone: the imagery of seed dropped into ground that hardens rather than grows, of the abandoned told their god has walked away, admits that this belonging is built entirely out of rejection, a home whose only foundation is having none.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","politics & society"],"means":"fracture, not unity, becomes the crowd's only solid ground","built":"the solidarity slogan is rewired so brokenness is the condition of standing"},
"machine-head~wipe-the-tears":{"haiku":"Overcoming hardship and maintaining inner strength through persistent struggle.","opus":"Enduring years of pain, failure and rejection but refusing to give up, wiping away tears and fighting for what one loves.","sonnet":"Persisting through years of failure, rejection, and hardship and refusing to give up on life or one's own resilience.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a survival anthem staged as a quarrel the speaker keeps having with his own despair, and its arc is a slow seizing of command over that pain rather than any sudden rescue from it. He earns the right to speak by cataloguing what he has borne — the clenched skull, the gut wound into a knot, the years of dread piled up — so the chorus lands not as consolation but as a verdict forced out under duress. The turn comes at the close, when those same marks stop reading as damage and start reading as evidence of having lasted, the long ordeal recast as the very thing that lengthens his appetite to keep going instead of exhausting it. And that the barked refusal to surrender sits alone, walled off from every verse, tells you it is the floor beneath the song rather than a destination — the phrase you chant hardest exactly when belief in it has not yet arrived.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"survival claimed not as rescue but as a verdict forced out under sustained duress","built":"the barked refusal sits walled off from every verse, marking it as the floor beneath the song rather than a destination the arc reaches"},
"machine-head~witch-hunt-rush-cover":{"haiku":"Mobs driven by fear and prejudice gather to destroy scapegoats, burning and killing those deemed threatening or different.","opus":"Machine Head, Bay Area groove-metal stalwarts, cover Rush's parable about mob self-righteousness with crushing weight. A torch-bearing crowd convinces itself its brutality is virtue, attacking outsiders in the name of protecting its own. The lesson is blunt: bigotry and dread march together, and fearful crowds rush to condemn what they refuse to understand.","sonnet":"A mob moves through the night with torches, righteous in its hatred of immigrants and bookstore shelves, feeding on fear stoked by those who claim to know best. The portrait is clinical and chilling — ignorance and prejudice and fear moving together like a single organism.","src":"haiku"},
"machine-head~wolves":{"haiku":"A brotherhood of outcasts and the damaged who operate through violence and competition, seeking dominance without satisfaction.","opus":"A brutal anthem for a violent brotherhood of outcasts who put their enemies in the ground and kneel to no tyrant, likened to a wolf pack fueled by fury and devotion. Even in triumph no contentment arrives, only the ceaseless pursuit.","sonnet":"A song about a brotherhood of the damaged and rejected — beaten, dysfunctional, unwanted — who find unity and ferocity in their shared suffering, turning against rivals and tyrants with pack-like aggression, even as they admit the victories bring no lasting satisfaction.","src":"haiku","fable":"Machine Head weaponize the wolf pack as an emblem of tribal loyalty forged in damage: a brotherhood of the unwanted and dysfunctional who convert their beatings into ferocity and bow to no tyrant, guarding the alpha and burying every rival. For two-thirds of the song it plays as pure war-anthem swagger, the pack scenting terror on the enemy and treating a knuckle across the jaw as fully permitted sport. Then the structure turns the knife on itself: after the boast about breeding and conquering, the bridge collapses into a flat, repeated confession that no amount won will ever quiet the hunger. That admission reframes everything before it, exposing the whole predatory code as a treadmill of conquest that solves nothing, so the closing command to dominate all lands less as victory than as the compulsion the pack can never escape.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["friendship & loyalty","violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"predatory code exposed as a treadmill of conquest that solves nothing","built":"the bridge confession of endless hunger reframes the war-anthem swagger that precedes it"},
"mad-kelly~everybody-s-enemy":{"haiku":"Everyone abandons you, confirming your worst fears and trapping you in cycles of self-sabotage.","opus":"The exhausted self-fulfilling prophecy of someone worn into the villain everyone treats them as, love feeding hate until becoming everybody's enemy feels inevitable. The bitterness is a cornered person accepting the role forced on them.","sonnet":"About being pushed to the point of becoming hostile by a relationship that drains you — the narrator has lost faith, lost what he needed, and is now shaped by the hate being fed to him; wanting love but receiving only damage has turned him into someone he didn't intend to become.","src":"haiku"},
"mad-kelly~hostage":{"haiku":"Captivity and forced compliance torture the psyche, with the speaker exhausted by chains of their own attachment.","opus":"Entrapment in a relationship rendered as literal captivity, begging for scraps of hope while being stoned, mocked, and made to watch a partner move on happier. Holding on against his own will, he calls himself a hostage, exhausted, running from an ending he already knows.","sonnet":"About being psychologically trapped in a relationship that feels like captivity — the speaker keeps holding on and doing wrong despite not wanting to, exhausted by the dynamic but unable to leave, longing for connection while being caged.","src":"haiku"},
"mad-planet~love-addicts":{"haiku":"Desperate hunger for connection manifests as addiction to strangers, where identity dissolves without external validation and presence.","opus":"A brief, hypnotic portrait of compulsive romantic hunger, someone who sees their future in strangers' eyes and can't locate a self without another's reflection. The refrain about filling a need frames love as an addiction, an empty kiss feeding a bottomless craving. It reaches for the emptiness beneath the pursuit, intimacy chased to avoid facing oneself.","sonnet":"The speaker finds identity only in the reflection of others, filling an inner emptiness through compulsive romantic pursuit — \"filling the need, filling the need\" — an empty kiss mistaken for sustenance.","src":"haiku"},
"mad-season~river-of-deceit":{"haiku":"Pain is self-chosen and flows downward like a river of lies; one can burn, drown, or reveal themselves—all paths lead down, and honesty is a beautiful shell built from suffering.","opus":"A bleak meditation on chosen suffering, the narrator debating whether to swallow his pride or shed himself entirely, resigned that a current of falsehood drags everyone the same way. Mad Season were a Seattle grunge supergroup fronted by Alice in Chains' Layne Staley, whose weary, addiction-shadowed voice lends this its haunted weight, a mind burdened with untruths pulling steadily downward.","sonnet":"Self-deception as the weight that drowns you — the pain is self-chosen, the speaker believes, or at least tells themselves so, and the only options are to burn, drown, or shed the skin and swim toward some version of beauty. The river of deceit flows in one direction only, and the song stays in that current, circling the same admission: we all choose our way down.","src":"haiku"},
"madeon~home":{"haiku":"Resignation and failure collapse into resignation, where giving up on effort seems preferable to exhaustion. Returning home means accepting inevitable decline and separation.","opus":"A weary resignation about a relationship, the narrator deciding that rather than keep straining to make it work, he'd sooner walk and be happy, knowing they'll grow old, come apart, and were only ever playing roles.","sonnet":"A relationship honestly framed from the start as finite: the speaker would rather give up and be happy than keep straining to succeed, and the promise to come home is paired with the acknowledgement that they will grow old and break apart — this was always the arrangement.","src":"haiku"},
"madeon~icarus":{"haiku":"Sunlit escape calls across mazes; ancient towers release the willing while hands guide toward ascendant flight beyond constraint and danger.","opus":"A soaring electro-house reworking of the Icarus myth, but recast as devotion rather than hubris. Rather than a lone flier undone by ambition, the narrator vows to stay close and catch someone who falls, unable to bear being alone. Madeon, a teenage prodigy when he made it, turns the ancient warning into a euphoric promise of rescue.","sonnet":"A retelling of the Icarus myth as a mutual flight: the speaker helps someone escape an ancient tower and spread their wings toward the skyline, but as the wax melts near the sun and they begin to fall, the speaker turns to catch them — unable to bear being left alone — promising they will get there together.","src":"haiku"},
"madeon~la-lune":{"haiku":"Memory pulls the aging toward nostalgic rehearsal; the speaker mourns that recreation cannot restore the feeling of authentic presence.","opus":"Two people clinging to a shared past that no longer feels the way it did, staying up trying to recreate what's gone. The tenderness is in the acceptance: whatever changes between them, the plea is simply not to worry, to let the bond stand even as the feeling fades.","sonnet":"Two old friends who have drifted try to recreate what they had but find that proximity no longer produces the old feeling — reassuring each other not to worry, that change doesn't matter, while quietly acknowledging that something irreplaceable has already slipped away.","src":"haiku"},
"madeon~pay-no-mind-ft-passion-pit":{"haiku":"A speaker consoles someone wounded by gossip and judgment, urging them to ignore public scrutiny and focus on their own peace. The song addresses cycles of social pain that repeat constantly.","opus":"The French electronic producer sets bright synth-pop beneath a Passion Pit vocal to console someone buckling under public judgment. A woman glows and falters by turns, sliding from poise into shame with no grasp of how she got there, and the narrator can offer only comfort rather than explanations. The chorus boils its advice down to one thing: ignore the whisperers, because this cycle always recurs.","sonnet":"A woman quietly unravels under the weight of inherited grief and external judgment while a narrator who cannot fully explain what went wrong urges her to breathe and ignore the noise. Tenderness persists in the space between not knowing and still wanting her to feel safe and luminous.","src":"haiku"},
"madeon~the-prince":{"haiku":"Romantic devotion battles perfectionist self-doubt; the narrator oscillates between confidence and collapse while seeking validation through love.","opus":"A young producer's coming-of-age anthem about wanting to matter, torn between his hunger for greatness and the fear of falling short. The push forward comes from a woman's insistence that the world, and the doing, is up to him.","sonnet":"A young person uncertain of their worth is steadied by someone who tells them the world belongs to them — love becomes the force that keeps them going, and the daily act of trying is framed as enough.","src":"haiku"},
"madeon~you-re-on-ft-kyan":{"haiku":"A person confronts a former lover who has reappeared after vanishing, demanding clarity about loyalty and commitment. The song seeks reassurance after abandonment.","opus":"French electronic producer Madeon, with vocalist Kyan, sets romantic uncertainty to bright pop, the narrator confronting someone who reappeared after a long absence and pretends closeness. Demanding to know whose side she's on, he admits she could become his if only she'd declare herself. The insistent question doubles as a hook, longing and suspicion resolved only by an answer that never quite arrives.","sonnet":"Someone reappears after a long absence and the narrator, caught between wounded pride and resurgent longing, demands to know where their loyalty lies. Months of self-erasure — imagining himself replaced — give way to the simple urgent need for a straight answer before hope collapses again.","src":"haiku"},
"madina-lake~tiny-weapons":{"haiku":"Promises made in love were small but lethal — like tiny weapons they poisoned everything — yet the speaker has carried the weight of them all this way and now begs the other person to stay even as they finally let go.","opus":"A conflicted post-hardcore confession in which broken vows become small poisonous darts and the true adversary turns out to dwell inside. The narrator personifies a deceiver steering him from within, a killer whose features mirror his own. It's about hauling around accumulated emotional freight until it turns deadly, and the fight to finally release it.","sonnet":"Promises as poison, self as enemy: the tiny weapons are the words and assurances that never came true, and the speaker discovers the real adversary lives inside — a liar armed to the teeth, controlling the wheel, setting fires for the speaker to put out — but the bridge being built across all of it leads back to you, the one person worth the damage.","src":"haiku"},
"madness~our-house":{"haiku":"A chaotic working-class household pulses with noise, movement, and maternal devotion; domestic life becomes sanctuary and memory.","opus":"A working-class family's daily grind rendered warm and busy, then quietly elegized. Beneath the cheerful clatter is an adult looking back on the home that raised him, the mother holding it together, knowing that ordinary domestic life was the whole treasure.","sonnet":"A fond, closely observed portrait of a busy, chaotic working-class family home — each member locked in their own routine of work, rest, school, and dates — held together by a proud, tireless mother and remembered with warmth as the place where childhood was simple and full.","src":"haiku"},
"madvillain~all-caps":{"haiku":"Hip-hop virtuosity and skill described through vivid wordplay; the artist performs with mastery while commentators struggle to keep pace.","opus":"A braggadocious villain flaunts his slippery flow, likening verses to butter and molasses while insisting his name be spelled in capitals. Madvillain, the union of MF DOOM and Madlib, turn dense internal rhyme into playful menace, the masked antihero rigging the dice and casting himself as the worst guy with the best con going.","sonnet":"MF DOOM performs an extended act of self-mythologising, cataloguing his own verbal dexterity, criminal cunning, and immunity to challenge with baroque, self-amused confidence. The warning to spell his name correctly is less a demand than a statement of ontological fact.","src":"haiku"},
"madvillain~curls":{"opus":"A vivid MF DOOM narrative tracing a hustler-rapper's coming of age, from a kid finding cash in the street and getting high, showing up to class buzzed, to becoming a slick, weed-smoking wordsmith always chasing the money.","sonnet":"MF DOOM's biographical sketch of Villain as a street-wise kid turned microphone god — the song traces his arc from finding money in the street to skipping class to chase girls, smoking hash from age seven, and becoming an unstoppable verbal klepto. It's a boastful origin story wrapped in dense wordplay and comic self-mythology.","src":"sonnet"},
"madvillain~meat-grinder":{"haiku":"A cunning persona weaves narratives of seduction and crime with rapid-fire wordplay; complexity and showmanship mask underlying instability and transience.","opus":"DOOM as the masked supervillain, spilling dense internal-rhyme wordplay that prizes texture and menace over any linear story. The pleasure is the sound of language itself grinding, a comic-book heel showing off how the ingredients get chopped.","sonnet":"MF DOOM delivers a dizzying, deliberately fractured lyrical tour through wordplay, street life, mythology, and self-referential boasting, resisting any single narrative thread in favour of dense, hallucinatory verbal texture.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~2-wheel-drive":{"haiku":"Uncertainty and fear at the edge of commitment, loving and doubting simultaneously.","opus":"Emotional hesitancy figured as fear of swimming out too far, the beloved farther away than expected and the singer caught in a childish loves-me-loves-me-not uncertainty. Intimacy stalls at the water's edge.","sonnet":"About ambivalence in love — the narrator is scared of how far the beloved has drifted, unsure whether the feeling is mutual, cycling between loving and not loving. The image of two-wheel drive in conditions that need four captures the insufficiency of what they've got against what's required.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~airplane":{"haiku":"The speaker watches someone they care for burn out while chasing success, feeling left behind as that person's ambition creates distance despite emotional strain.","opus":"American synth-pop duo Magdalena Bay wrap glossy, detailed electronics around the ache of loving someone whose restless, high-speed life keeps carrying them away. The narrator watches the gap widen and keeps trying to bend into their orbit, even as loving a partner always poised to leave quietly wears the heart down.","sonnet":"Watching someone you love burn themselves out chasing speed while you learn to keep up — their frantic pace gets under the skin, the turbulence is internal, but slowly the speaker finds their stride in the other's lines and realizes at the very end that they can take off on their own. Empathy tips over into independence: catching the airplane again and again, then finally deciding not to follow.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~body":{"opus":"The dread of dissociation on a crowded dancefloor, where sensory overload and hedonism hollow the singer out until she feels no one is home inside her own body.","sonnet":"A person overwhelmed by sensory overload at a loud, hedonistic gathering feels their sense of self dissolve entirely into the crowd, experiencing a kind of ego-death where the body moves but nobody is home inside.","src":"sonnet"},
"magdalena-bay~chaeri":{"haiku":"Regret for hurting a close friend and inability to escape cycles of harm.","opus":"Regret and guilt over failing a depressed friend named Chaeri, wishing one had said the right words to help them.","sonnet":"Regret over failing a friend named Chaeri who was struggling with depression, wishing past comfort had been offered sooner.","src":"haiku","fable":"A guilt-drenched address to a depressed friend named Chaeri, the whole song assembled from things said too late. The verses are the speaker's retrospect, apologizing for a friendship that curdled and confessing they never grasped its severity until it was over. The seasonal image system carries the arc — September arriving sweet, then a cruel winter with a storm concealed beneath — so the crisis reads as something survivable from outside while quietly turning lethal. The chorus is the reassurance the speaker wishes they had offered, insisting the situation only stays that dire once you convince yourself the day can no longer be risen into. The genius and the horror is that this consolation is also a lie the speaker half-knows is hollow, which is why the outro sheds the caretaking voice and slides into a countdown toward the floor, toward lost control. It ends on a grim bargain — that being crucified, or fixed and finished, beats being alone or unknown — the friend's despair now spoken from inside, no longer soothed but understood.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","friendship & loyalty","shame & repression"],"means":"consolation the speaker half-knows is hollow, offered too late","built":"the outro sheds the caretaking voice and counts down from inside the despair"},
"magdalena-bay~dawning-of-the-season":{"haiku":"A figure dances with inner demons as a new melancholic season begins, observed by doubters and judged harshly, yet remains untouchable and luminous like electricity.","opus":"An outsider girl brushing aside the whispers, glares, and glassy-eyed classmates who claim she's waltzing with her demons, calling herself untouchable and comparing herself to a live current; the throughline is claiming the label of the gloomy misfit and doubter as her own era of self-possession.","sonnet":"About a teenage girl navigating social hostility with electric self-possession — classmates stare and whisper, the hallways drip with dread, but she's untouchable, electric, dancing with her demons while a new adolescent season of self-discovery opens around her.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~domino":{"haiku":"A contrast between a winner bearing weight gracefully and a loser unable to express vulnerability; causation and design remain mysterious as people fall like dominoes.","opus":"A wistful reflection on a relationship's end, addressing a cold, statue-like lover and wondering whether the falling-apart is by design or just physics, like a toppling domino, before saying goodnight.","sonnet":"Two people circle a relationship that seems fated and mechanically inevitable — like dominoes falling by physics — while questioning whether its end was designed or just happened, and saying goodnight to something neither fully chose.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~dreamcatching":{"haiku":"The song celebrates imagining a shared future with a partner, savoring travel dreams and experiences together before time runs out.","opus":"A euphoric bucket-list of places to see before dying, the specifics (cherry blossoms, the Swiss countryside, blue oceans) matter less than the refrain that every experience is only worth having beside the beloved; the deeper read is that love makes time itself elastic and infinite.","sonnet":"A song about being so in love that every travel fantasy and bucket-list destination exists only as something to share with one specific person — the world is only worth seeing because they'd be standing next to you.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~exo":{"haiku":"Red urgency saturates consciousness, lingering love haunts as an unwanted guest, desire and violence blur into a castle that stands empty.","opus":"A hypnotic, bilingual spiral of craving, with crimson coursing through the mind and a violence dressed up as a friendly greeting. The vacant castle and the compulsive appetite point to desire haunting a hollowed-out interior, an obsession with nobody home.","sonnet":"A state of violent inner possession — something red and haunting runs through the speaker's mind, a nameless force (EXO) that feels like desire and threat simultaneously, filling an empty castle, circling obsessively, expressed partly in French to heighten its alien, untranslatable quality.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~follow-the-leader":{"haiku":"A predatory figure lures victims with bait, ensnaring them in a spell where they lose consciousness and autonomy, becoming trapped in a dark web.","opus":"A hushed, nursery-rhyme-menacing track that beckons the listener to trail a compulsive rabbit into a pale nighttime forest and to nibble at the offered temptation. The repeated question of whether you can keep your eyes open turns childlike singsong into a lure toward some sinister surrender.","sonnet":"A seductive, unsettling figure lures someone to follow and take the bait, urging them to stay awake even as their eyes glaze over — a hypnotic trap where the follower is drawn in and can no longer resist.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~ghost":{"haiku":"An evil presence crawls into the speaker's mind while another occupies their space, making them feel invaded and consumed. They satisfy internal hunger through possession.","opus":"Magdalena Bay, the synth-pop duo known for glossy, uncanny production, cast desire as a haunting. A malign gaze prickles the spine, a phantom lingers just out of sight, and the beloved is described as already lodged within and impossibly near. Between consuming every part of the other and clutching them to the breaking point, love and being haunted blur into one.","sonnet":"Desire takes on a spectral quality — a presence already inside before it is invited, crossing over into someone and occupying them completely. Appetite and possession blur until the line between lover and haunting dissolves, the other person both summoned and consumed.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~good-intentions":{"opus":"A wistful post-mortem on a failed relationship where both people meant well, replaying warm memories through guilt and self-blame while insisting the love wasn't in vain even as it went bad.","sonnet":"Two people whose relationship ended badly reckon with the wreckage left in their minds, coming to the bittersweet conclusion that despite the damage and the blame, both of them entered with genuine care — it was good intentions that went wrong.","src":"sonnet"},
"magdalena-bay~halfway":{"haiku":"Reflection on being perpetually in the middle of life's journey, neither beginning nor end, questioning whether one feels empty or full in this state of in-between.","opus":"Magdalena Bay reflect on always being caught in the middle, poised between beginning and end and unsure whether that means hope or emptiness. The takeaway is a nudge to ease off the pace before the day quietly runs out.","sonnet":"A brief meditation on existing in the middle of time — between start and end, neither empty nor full — urging the listener to slow down before the day disappears entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~hideaway":{"haiku":"Two people cling to childish nostalgia and hiding games, refusing to acknowledge that they and the world have aged.","opus":"A tender ode to a love that keeps its childhood spirit, the pair still up past midnight hunting phantoms downstairs as the world matures around them, a bit older and wearier but still wanting to stay near. Magdalena Bay, the synth-pop duo, wrap the nostalgia in glittering electronics, their private refuge a place where small familiar quirks let them slip through the years together.","sonnet":"Nostalgia as the texture of a present relationship — the world has grown up around two people who haven't really changed: still running around the house at night, finding ghosts, hiding and chasing. The speaker is a little older and a little colder but the crooked smile still works, and the whole song lives in the sweet tension between wanting things to stay like yesterday and knowing that time is moving anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~how-to-get-physical":{"haiku":"Someone desires physical intimacy and movement with a partner, discovering courage to let go and be touched.","opus":"A giddy, tipsy anthem about mustering the nerve to dance and take someone home despite feeling hopelessly uncoordinated, drink and dim lighting loosening the narrator until the rhythm overtakes her. Magdalena Bay, the American synth-pop duo, build it on shimmering disco-kissed electronics, the coy instructional framing turning first-move jitters into a sparkling invitation to be led.","sonnet":"Social anxiety translated into desire — someone who doesn't consider herself a dancer finds herself dizzy and heart-racing on the floor, cheap wine having dissolved the inhibition that usually keeps her seated. The question she keeps asking is practical and tender: how do you close this distance without knowing the steps? The answer the song offers is to let the other person take the lead.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~hysterical-us":{"haiku":"People live in paranoid tension and surveillance anxiety, unable to sleep or trust, seeking reassurance of stability in a destabilized world.","opus":"Magdalena Bay portray a couple consumed by shared anxiety and paranoia, obsessively securing the house, unable to rest, sure that something has gone wrong. Alien-abduction imagery and nagging doubts about their connection cast modern dread as both a private and cosmic loss of safety.","sonnet":"Anxiety and paranoia in a relationship — both people are checking locks, unable to sleep, questioning whether they're alone or in love, and the narrator wonders if the unsettled feeling is a permanent condition or something they can shake.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~i-don-t-want-to-cry-anymore":{"haiku":"After a party, someone decides to abandon their emotional self entirely and disappear rather than continue being hurt for someone unworthy.","opus":"After a party ends, a narrator finds someone on the stairs who withholds affirmation, and she changes her face, takes her place, and refuses to weep any longer. Magdalena Bay's glossy synth-pop cloaks the ache, turning a quiet romantic disappointment into a mantra of shutting off the cameras and hardening against the tears.","sonnet":"In the deflated aftermath of a party, someone realises the person they wanted never claimed them back. The decision to stop grieving arrives quietly — less anger than exhausted withdrawal, a turning of flesh to dust rather than confrontation.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~image-grimes-special":{"haiku":"Image demands construction under ticking time. Common sense dissolves into commodity and mirror-gazing.","opus":"A glossy satire of self-reinvention as branding, counting down the minutes to a hot new look assembled behind a one-way mirror, identity turned into a hyperpop product made to order.","sonnet":"A song about identity reconstruction under time pressure — the narrator counts down minutes toward a transformation, oscillating between needing confidence and asserting they already have it, emerging through a two-way mirror as a brand new image of themselves, the song capturing the anxious excitement of reinvention.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~live-4ever":{"haiku":"Celebrates the reckless thrill-seeking of youth, driving fast and pushing limits to feel immortal. The song captures the adrenaline rush of living dangerously with someone you want to stay with forever.","opus":"A reckless late-night drive fueled by adrenaline and the euphoric fantasy of living forever. The narrator urges a companion to go faster and trust the moment, chasing the thrill of speed and the feeling of being intensely alive even while going nowhere.","sonnet":"Two people go on a reckless late-night drive at extreme speed, chasing the sensation of invincibility — the rush of adrenaline and momentum creates a feeling of living forever, even as the imagery hints at the possibility of burning out.","src":"haiku","fable":"Magdalena Bay treat immortality as something you manufacture at 105 miles an hour rather than something you're granted, the aimless midnight drive a machine for talking yourself into a feeling you can't reach parked and still. The hook admits its own strategy in its grammar: you don't become deathless, you floor the pedal until belief arrives, adrenaline and venom filling in for a faith the speaker doesn't actually hold. And the closer the synths push toward ecstasy, the more the pictures betray it, twin headlights dissolving into a wreck of sparks under a cold sky, so the thrill that promises to outrun the end is the exact thing that would deliver it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","freedom & escape","faith & the occult"],"means":"flooring the pedal until belief in deathlessness arrives","built":"the synths push toward ecstasy while the images betray it as wreckage"},
"magdalena-bay~mercurial-world":{"haiku":"In an unstable, chaotic world, a steady partner provides the emotional anchor that makes facing uncertainty possible.","opus":"A love song about finding a steady, grounding partner amid a chaotic, mercurial world, someone as sure as the stars who holds the narrator down when she feels crazy. The relationship becomes the one constant against the whole spinning universe.","sonnet":"A love song built on gratitude for stability — in a volatile, unpredictable world the narrator clings to a partner who keeps them grounded, and that solidity is framed as its own kind of miracle.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~prophecy":{"haiku":"A lonely person finds certainty and belonging in romantic love, treating their partner as salvation and the answer to existential searching. Love becomes their only truth.","opus":"A love song in which a partner replaces the destiny, fate, and cosmic answers the narrator once searched for in dreams and stars. Their real, tangible presence beats any make-believe prophecy, so much that being buried in white sheets in their arms feels like never wanting to be free.","sonnet":"About finding a person who makes belief in destiny feel real for the first time — someone so perfect in your arms that you no longer need religion, fate, or fantasy to feel grounded. It's a love song framed as spiritual revelation.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~secrets-your-fire":{"haiku":"Protective secrecy shields identity from exploitation; the speaker refuses to feed another's hunger for knowledge.","opus":"The push and pull of online exposure, refusing to feed a follower's hungry fire with everything about oneself, then compulsively wanting to. Between guarding secrets and oversharing, the self risks getting lost in the worldwide gaze.","sonnet":"About the compulsion to overshare online — the speaker wants to keep secrets and not feed the parasocial fire of people who think they know her from the internet, but admits to simultaneously needing to give everything away, caught between privacy and the pull of total exposure.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~sky2fall":{"haiku":"The speaker cherishes momentary connection while accepting life's impermanence and the inevitability that all things eventually end.","opus":"At a party dissolving into sunrise, someone makes peace with time's flow, vowing to catch a partner even if the stars burn out and the clock runs down. Magdalena Bay, the LA synth-pop duo, wrap the bittersweet acceptance in shimmering electronics and a bilingual refrain where wanting rain brings sun, turning inevitability into an occasion for fun.","sonnet":"At the threshold between summer's end and whatever comes next, two people reckon with the possibility of loss. Even if time runs out and the sky falls, one promises to hold on through it — not denial of endings, but a commitment made inside the uncertainty of all finite, beautiful things.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~slug-song":{"opus":"A gentle pep-talk from a window, telling someone not to fear the road or the storm ahead because they've already made it this far. The mixtape sign-off frames it as a small, tender interlude of encouragement rather than a full song.","sonnet":"A brief, gentle encouragement to a listener already in motion — you've already come this far, the wind and the storm ahead don't matter, you know what you have to do; the song exists more as a warm signal than a narrative.","src":"sonnet"},
"magdalena-bay~slug-song-reprise":{"haiku":"Minimal affirmation of perseverance, acknowledging arrival at an endpoint through repetition alone.","opus":"A brief, mantra-like reassurance to someone weary, reminding them how far they've already come. Its whole meaning is the gentle nudge to keep going, coming so far already as proof they can finish.","sonnet":"A brief affirmation addressed to someone who has already come far — \"you've already come this far\" — functioning as a quiet, repeated reassurance rather than a fully developed narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~something-for-2":{"haiku":"Two people stand at a relationship crossroads—either build something lasting together or let it collapse. The narrator has lost themselves in the relationship's colors and bleakness, offering everything but facing rejection.","opus":"Weary of a volatile relationship, the speaker reflects on changing herself and offers a choice: build something together or let it collapse and burn down.","sonnet":"After exhausting herself trying to accommodate a volatile partner, the narrator reaches the end of her patience and asks to either build something real together or stop — a plea for genuine investment rather than being shut out or left to drown.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~story":{"opus":"A shimmering synth-pop daydream about youthful ambition and the anxious awareness of time slipping past. Two dreamers imagine writing a real big story worth remembering, oscillating between soaring fantasy and the fear of squandering it. Nostalgia for a future not yet lived colors this bright, wistful reach toward glory.","sonnet":"A youthful daydream about ambition and legacy — two people lying in the sun imagine futures, first-class flights, and the great stories they might tell about their lives. The emotional core is the sweet anxiety of time moving fast while the story is still being written.","src":"sonnet"},
"magdalena-bay~that-s-my-floor":{"haiku":"Transcendence through levitation, the speaker finds liberation by refusing to sink into the lobby of ordinary existence.","opus":"A giddy elevator flirtation turned metaphysical, rising above the lobby toward a floor that's transcendence as much as a hookup. The speaker whispers turn-ons while claiming the higher, transcendental self, letting the experience open her up.","sonnet":"A sexually charged ascent — the speaker teases and provokes a partner into heightened states, riding adrenaline rather than safety, pinning the other against a wall, using the elevator and the party as metaphors for escalating pleasure and arrival.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~the-beginning":{"haiku":"A cynical person encounters someone whose outlook transforms them toward simplicity, rediscovery, and the realization that everyone sometimes needs help.","opus":"A parable about a jaded loner remade by an encounter with a wise, golden-eyed woman who teaches him that letting go and embracing simplicity is freeing. The uplifting message is renewal: when you feel defeated, return to square one, accept help, and the world floods back into color as bitterness dissolves.","sonnet":"A cynical, closed-off man is transformed by encountering someone with a simple, luminous wisdom — cynicism gives way to wonder, and the song becomes a gentle instruction that whenever you feel finished, going back to the beginning can show you where to find help.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~the-end":{"haiku":"A philosophical monologue questions whether endings exist; all things arise from and return to nothingness. The song ponders whether cyclical existence offers comfort or merely obscures meaning.","opus":"A woozy, late-night spoken interlude from Magdalena Bay musing that nothing truly finishes, since everything emerges from and returns to the same void, collapsing start and finish into one. It offers this cosmic idea as the kind of soothing untruth that helps you drift off.","sonnet":"A late-night philosophical musing about endings and beginnings being the same — a gentle, slightly absurdist comfort about the circularity of existence that asks whether any of this helps you sleep.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~tonguetwister":{"haiku":"Words become weapons; what you say can't be unsaid, so silence becomes survival.","opus":"A tense power struggle over voice and appetite, someone ordered to stay quiet and out of sight while being goaded to spit it out, losing consciousness at the first taste. It sets desire and silencing against each other in a queasy standoff.","sonnet":"About someone who talks big but whose words are just noise — the narrator wants them to stop putting her in their mouth, to spit her out; it's a sharp, defiant song about refusing to be defined or owned by someone else's narrative of you.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~top-dog":{"haiku":"Dominance and appetite define the speaker's existence, barking and biting become performance while the speaker remains the authority figure.","opus":"Playful dominance and total self-possession, the speaker crowning herself top dog who only does what she wants and calls the shots. Cinema references and swaggering boasts make it a strut of control, keeping a lover as her number one on her own terms.","sonnet":"A song about female dominance and autonomy — the speaker is at the top of the social food chain, does exactly what she wants, summons people to her on her terms, and will bite if needed.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~woww":{"haiku":"Disgust at urban superficiality and the exhaustion of performance. The speaker expresses hatred for the city's fakeness and the hollow spectacle of social gatherings.","opus":"The synth-pop duo boils contempt down to a bratty, minimal chant. The narrator flatly repeats a dismissive sound at someone she despises and orders out of her sight and space. Between the jabs, the city wears her down while everyone phonies their way toward the glamorous scene. Fatigue with social climbing curdles into the almost cartoonish urge to blot the offender out completely.","sonnet":"A terse, impatient dispatch from someone exhausted by city performance culture: everyone is faking their way to the next event, and the speaker wants every false face out of her space immediately. The contempt is crisp, brief, and almost comedic in its directness.","src":"haiku"},
"magdalena-bay~you-lose":{"haiku":"A relationship where trust is broken and attempts at manipulation fail. The narrator rejects false affection and warns the other person that their tactics will ultimately fail.","opus":"A crumbling relationship framed as a losing game, where the narrator distrusts a partner's professed devotion and refuses to fall for what she calls a lie. She feels time and pride slipping away, insisting that in this contest of attacks there are no winners.","sonnet":"A song about the end of a relationship in which the speaker no longer trusts or wants the other person. Despite exhaustion and the feeling of time running out, she holds onto enough self-respect to reject the other's advances, naming them the loser in the power struggle.","src":"haiku"},
"maggie-lindemann~deprecating":{"haiku":"Manipulation and self-flagellation spiral as the speaker, disapproved of and invaded, fights depreciating thought-patterns while suffocating under psychic colonization.","opus":"A dark pop-rock reckoning with a manipulative, gaslighting partner who invades the narrator's mind and leaves her frostbitten and self-loathing. Nightmares, cutting words, and the ache of a broken promise fuel a plea: if there was ever love, let her go. It captures the suffocation of a bond that damages while denying any wrongdoing.","sonnet":"A speaker suffocating under someone's manipulation and disapproval watches the other person's karmic fall — \"hit the ground hard, get your head split\" — while framing the relationship as a homicidal loop of mind pollution and nightmares she is slowly escaping.","src":"haiku"},
"maggie-lindemann~gaslight":{"haiku":"Romantic manipulation through psychological gaslighting leads toward violence; the speaker confronts a lover's deception and homicidal threats.","opus":"A toxic relationship rendered as a string of violent thrills — gaslight, joyride, twist the knife, homicide — where the betrayed lover, cheated on and torn down, threatens to choose the partner's fate: left lonely or killed slowly.","sonnet":"A partner's manipulation is catalogued at speed — gaslight, joy ride, twist the knife — and the speaker, who is only in pain when he's absent but knows he's kissing other girls behind her back, reaches a breaking point that flips from victim to threat: \"watch your back, lead me to a homicide.\"","src":"haiku"},
"maggie-lindemann~pretty-girl-cheat-codes-x-cade-remix":{"haiku":"Depth persists beneath surfaces; the speaker rejects shallow categories and refuses to be reduced to appearance or virality, asserting complexity despite concealment.","opus":"A teen-pop rejection of being reduced to a face and a follower count, insisting on the contradictions and the hidden struggle underneath. It works as a small act of defiance against the flattening gaze of social media fame.","sonnet":"A young woman rejects the reductive label of pretty girl, insisting on the full contradictory complexity of who she is — someone who has been through hardship, carries her own voice, and refuses to be flattened into an image or a rumor. It is a declaration of selfhood directed at anyone who has tried to contain her.","src":"haiku"},
"maggie-rogers~on-off":{"haiku":"Emotional volatility requires grounding presence; the speaker oscillates between crisis and equilibrium, seeking constancy.","opus":"The lurch of an unstable relationship rendered as a physical high, where a partner is both the thing that drowns her and the thing that pulls her back to okay. The chorus makes the on-and-off pattern feel like an addiction she can't quite name.","sonnet":"An appeal to a stabilizing person to take the speaker to whatever private space they retreat to when the world gets too low, because she cycles between drowning and surfacing — on and off — and needs this person to be the constant that turns her back on and holds her together when she comes undone.","src":"haiku"},
"magic-dirt~plastic-loveless-letter":{"haiku":"Someone wrestles with lingering attachment to an ex-lover despite claiming to have moved on, trapped between desire and guilt.","opus":"A restless confession of guilt and unwanted longing for an ex, the narrator stewing alone in a cramped bed, aching for her now that she's thriving, and debating whether to send an insincere note. Magic Dirt were an Australian alt-rock band fronted by Adalita, and the track wraps sardonic desire in fuzzed-out grunge, the phony letter a wry stand-in for feelings he can neither fake nor shake.","sonnet":"A love letter written in the worst possible timing — there aren't enough hours or words to explain getting over someone, but in better health and single in a cramped bed the speaker realizes they miss the bones of that person desperately and cannot stop themselves from sending something that is simultaneously too late and too honest, neither fully loving nor fully done.","src":"haiku"},
"magnolia-park~do-or-die":{"haiku":"Desperation for survival drives confrontation as the speaker oscillates between fighting and drowning in their own destructive impulses, the line between love and hate blurred by trauma.","opus":"Survival framed as combat because the enemy is internal, the demons and intrusive thoughts that make hate feel like love. Beneath the violent bravado is a plea not to drown, someone fighting their own mind and terrified of losing.","sonnet":"A speaker drowning in mental health crisis — suicidal ideation clashing with the raw will to survive — while projecting violent dominance outward as armor, unable to trust friends and begging not to be lost to the flood of their own mind.","src":"haiku"},
"mai-yamane~don-t-bother-none":{"haiku":"A string of small-time misfortunes — a stolen wallet with no cash, a woman who steals the car and the heart — is met each time with cheerful fatalism: that's just the way it is, don't bother none.","opus":"A wry, bluesy shrug at petty misfortune, sung by the voice behind Cowboy Bebop's closing theme. The narrator pockets a stranger's wallet, gets robbed of his car and heart in turn, and answers each reversal with the same fatalistic refrain that worrying won't help. It reaches for a hard-bitten, smoky acceptance that life just goes how it goes.","sonnet":"A shrug-shouldered picaresque of small moral failures — stealing a wallet that turns out to be empty, getting robbed of heart and car by a woman with a face like a star, each loss met with resigned blues wisdom: that's just the way it is, don't bother none. The narrator's philosophical acceptance of being hustled is its own wry comedy.","src":"haiku"},
"majiko~a-gxosok":{"haiku":"Forbidden passion and sexual desire masquerading as love; the speaker obsesses over someone unavailable, using sensuality as a substitute for genuine connection while knowing the relationship has no future.","opus":"Majiko emerged from Nico Nico Douga into a career of ache-heavy vocals, well suited to this study of desire for someone who is toying with her. She refuses to call it love because naming it would drive him away, yet keeps tracing his body across her own, jealous of whoever else might have him. The confession that she would accept even deception for one night is the wound laid bare.","sonnet":"A woman is consumed by desire for someone she knows is toying with her — she refuses to name it love because naming it would drive him away, but she can't stop mentally tracing his body across her own, burning with jealousy at the thought of him belonging to someone else, and finally confessing she would accept even deception for one night with him.","src":"haiku"},
"major-lazer~lean-on-feat-m-dj-snake":{"haiku":"Mutual interdependence and support as essential anchors through life's uncertainty.","opus":"A global smash whose ache is simpler than its production, two people who grew up bold clinging to each other for survival. Under the drop it's about needing someone as the years and waves roll on.","sonnet":"Two people who were young and fearless together now face the uncertainty of age and absence, clinging to the idea that what they built still holds — the universal need for someone to lean on frames a story of love tested by time and distance.","src":"haiku"},
"major-moment~the-flood":{"haiku":"Human arrogance accelerates collective ruin; the speaker warns that our indifference and greed birth catastrophe, and no redemption arrives after the flood drowns us all.","opus":"An indictment of a self-absorbed, trend-chasing society heading for disaster with no happy ending, invoking the after-us-the-flood shrug. It is about collective arrogance and disrespect dooming a world we assume revolves around us.","sonnet":"A reckoning with collective moral failure: humanity ignores every premonition, trades its worth for trends, and is vicious in its ambitions — the levees are breaking, the flood is coming, and the refrain \"after us the flood\" frames civilisation's end as a consequence of condescension and the refusal to listen.","src":"haiku"},
"major-parkinson~munchausen-by-proxy":{"haiku":"The narrator is a created thing in an uncanny valley, made by strangers and used like a marionette. They are a substitution and surrogate for something unattainable.","opus":"Major Parkinson, the theatrical Norwegian art-rock ensemble, spin a grotesque cabaret about a manufactured self, an eerie artificial boy with no working insides, half puppet and half conditioned creature, made by strangers who offer counterfeit affection for something he can never truly possess. As a clockwork mechanism drives his heart, identity itself is staged as sinister fabrication.","sonnet":"A figure shaped and controlled by others — a marionette, a manikin, a surrogate — haunts the spaces of a relationship that manufactured rather than discovered him, returning again and again to the site of his own construction. The imagery of pendulums, Pavlovian conditioning, and castrated suffragettes weaves together the eerie sense of identity as something imposed from outside rather than grown within.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~27":{"haiku":"Connected through digital systems and corrupted memory, the narrator feels nothing from the connection and searches for meaning in false algorithms.","opus":"Frames emotional numbness through the language of a failing computer system, connection that registers as data while feeling nothing at all. A cry from inside the machine, desperate to feel something real beneath corrupted memory.","sonnet":"Human connection in the digital age framed as a corrupted system reset — we're technically connected to everything but feel absolutely nothing, and the narrator pleads for a 'god code' that could make the bonds actually mean something.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~bones":{"opus":"An agonized plea born of self-loathing and inner torment, where the narrator, terrified of the shadows haunting his dreams, offers up everything he is, begs to be stripped and remade, and surrenders his very bones to escape his pain.","sonnet":"A song about the exhausted surrender of self to something consuming — the narrator wants to drown, fill the gaping hole, shed their skin, and give away every bone and organ they have to be free of the weight of regret, shame, and nightmares. It reads as profound dissociation from one's own body as a result of accumulated trauma.","src":"sonnet"},
"make-them-suffer~dead-plains":{"haiku":"Abandoned in desolate emptiness; a forgotten person questions why a sorrowful force has left them alone in dead ground despite once close proximity.","opus":"A symphonic deathcore parable of grief personified as a weeping keeper whose tears salt the earth, trapping the narrator in a barren afterlife. The real subject is a mourner so dependent on her own sorrow that she'll never release the one she cries for, closure that never comes.","sonnet":"A personification of grief as a goddess figure who has left the narrator in a salt-flat wasteland — she weeps and her tears salt the earth, she gets closer but never lets him out; he's trapped in a paradox where her sadness perpetuates his captivity.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~doomswitch":{"haiku":"A corrupted urban environment exerts a gravitational pull on its inhabitants, threatening to absorb them into its toxic cycle of cynicism and spiritual death unless they actively turn themselves around and face the curse.","opus":"A metalcore portrait of being stuck in a self-destructive loop inside a bleak, coiling cityscape that seems to stalk and corner the speaker. The lyrics push toward turning yourself around before you dissolve into an anonymous, doomed crowd, and toward the harder act of confronting yourself amid depression rather than surrendering to sameness.","sonnet":"A city of spirals that has hollowed you out: long winters, neon hinterlands, deserted homes — and the doomswitch is the recognition that you know this story and how it ends, and that the world sees you as just like the drones and usurpers you despise, unless you turn yourself around before death does it for you.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~drown-with-me":{"haiku":"The song invites surrender into water and memory, suggesting drowning as transcendence where spirits become winged. The ocean represents inevitable change that speaks truth the speaker has always known.","opus":"A tender-but-morbid invitation to surrender together to death or oblivion, imaged as drowning in the sea; the narrator coaxes a frightened companion to take his hand and sink, framing the waters as wise, welcoming and long-fated, so that dying together becomes an intimate, peaceful release.","sonnet":"An invitation to surrender to grief together — the narrator beckons someone to sink into the shared sea of memory and loss, framing drowning not as destruction but as a form of spiritual release, with the ocean figured as something wiser and older than words.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~erase-me":{"haiku":"Someone wracked with guilt and self-loathing begs to be erased rather than saved, convinced they are not worth the effort others invest in them.","opus":"The Australian band braid symphonic sweetness into metalcore heft on this plea to be let go. The narrator urges a partner to put an end to what they had, insisting he's damaged them past repair and isn't worth rescuing, so they should stop hoping. The desperate refrain begs for oblivion, mistaking self-destruction for kindness.","sonnet":"A person who knows he has caused enough damage pleads to be erased rather than forgiven or saved. He frames dissolution as the merciful option — for both parties — and argues with quiet self-disgust that continuing to exist in the relationship only extends the harm he cannot stop inflicting.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~ether":{"haiku":"Encouragement to slow down and embrace mortality; life passes quickly and beauty fades, so the speaker urges mindfulness and connection before the final sunrise.","opus":"A gentle plea to someone falling apart and rushing through life to slow down, breathe, and savor youth before it's gone. Invoking the Japanese creation deities Izanami and Izanagi, keepers of death and life, it treats mortality and renewal as the world reawakening, urging trust and a single shared dawn even if it costs everything.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality and slowing down — borrowing Japanese creation-deity imagery (Izanami and Izanagi), it urges someone young and burned out to stop rushing, question life's direction, and trust that the journey has value even as time runs short.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~fake-your-own-death":{"opus":"Make Them Suffer unleash a scathing goodbye to a cowardly, dishonest betrayer, telling them no one will mourn their absence and openly relishing the wish to watch them rot and fall apart. The venom is fueled entirely by a trust the target shattered.","sonnet":"Pure contempt for a liar and fake — the narrator tells someone spineless, dishonest, and deceptive to go dig their own grave because no one will miss them, and expresses satisfaction at watching them self-destruct.","src":"sonnet"},
"make-them-suffer~fireworks":{"haiku":"A narrative of leaving behind a life to start anew with someone, burning the past and watching it illuminate. The speaker seeks freedom through destruction and shared transformation with a partner.","opus":"A song of departure and defiant devotion, in which the speaker leaves to make things right and imagines burning everything down alongside a loved one, watching the flames like fireworks in the hope of being sent home.","sonnet":"A reconciliation and shared act of burning the past down together, two people choosing to start over — sailing east, watching the flames, and finally heading home.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~foreward":{"haiku":"Memory and renewal collapse into repetitive cycles; old stories recede while the speaker wrestles with time's relentless passage.","opus":"Two old friends reunite after a long separation and try to begin again by retelling the stories they once shared. Time and aging haunt the tender attempt to pick the thread back up.","sonnet":"A brief, tender request to a long-absent old friend to read back the stories they once shared, acknowledging how much time has passed and how much both have grown old.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~ghost-of-me":{"haiku":"Haunting absence and ghost of former self, inability to move past trauma.","opus":"A bitter, entangled song about a codependent bond sinking together toward the bottom, where one voice promises to be remembered once everyone else abandons the other. Accusations of self-hatred projected onto everybody else drive it. It reaches for the haunting persistence of a person who becomes part of you, a ghost that lingers even in ruin.","sonnet":"A mutual descent — both parties are falling, neither is the villain alone, and the question \"do you realize that you hate yourself?\" is posed with genuine curiosity rather than cruelty. The ghost left behind when the self is abandoned becomes part of the other person; the bottom is lonely for everyone who reaches it.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~grinding-teeth":{"haiku":"The speaker avoids engagement while dealing with mounting stress; time has worn them down and they can barely answer the call.","opus":"A jittery portrait of someone dodging contact and holing up while clenching their jaw, sensing the situation slipping toward disaster. Beneath the party trappings of cranked music and a packed schedule, the compulsive habit and dread of a call gone unanswered point to anxiety or addiction quietly exacting its price.","sonnet":"A song about compulsive return — someone keeps calling, the speaker is always out but keeps coming back, the grinding teeth of the title suggesting the anxious, involuntary pull of a situation they can't seem to leave alone.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~hollowed-heart":{"haiku":"Trauma and assault leave someone appearing composed to others while internally broken, with demons haunting them in solitude.","opus":"An address to someone secretly suffering, crying themselves to sleep behind closed doors while hiding their pain and blaming themselves. The narrator calls the tormentors cowards and sees the hurting person as a hero rather than a victim.","sonnet":"Written on behalf of someone who has endured abuse or assault in private — outwardly composed but inwardly haunted — the song condemns the perpetrators as cowards and acknowledges the survivor's pain while affirming their worth.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built entirely from the outside looking in — the singer never claims the pain as his own, only insists on witnessing it, holding the line that the person crumbling behind closed doors deserves the word hero rather than victim even as the evidence mounts against it. The percussive chorus turns slamming doors into gunfire — a bedroom, a bathroom stall — sealing off someone who has learned to shoulder an unbearable weight without ever setting it down where anyone can see. Make Them Suffer aim the breakdown's fury not at the sufferer but at the cowards circling her, the bridge insisting she is a child someone raised while her degradation gets tallied like grim achievements. What curdles the whole thing is the outro, where the tribute turns out to be written for her rather than handed to her — the fierce defense written on her behalf because she cannot yet write it herself — the speaker lending his voice to someone still silent behind the door, defense standing in for rescue.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","family & blood","anger & defiance"],"means":"defense written for someone still silent rather than handed to her","built":"the percussive chorus turns slamming doors into gunfire, sealing off rather than opening up","fnote":"The band called it what it is — a confrontational attack on sexual abusers, in solidarity with survivors."},
"make-them-suffer~how-to-survive-a-funeral":{"haiku":"A person dies young from substance abuse; their funeral reveals they were distant and unknowable to mourners who gather only after loss. The song mourns disconnection finally made apparent.","opus":"An overdose seen from the wake it produces: the bitter irony that a person ignored while alive suddenly commands a full room once dead, mourners finding they cared only after the loss made caring safe and free.","sonnet":"A eulogy for someone who died from overdose or self-destruction — they were isolated and out of touch in life, but their death filled a room with people who only discovered how much they cared once it was too late.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~let-me-in":{"haiku":"A broken man pleads with Annie to let him back in after making mistakes and being absent. He cannot give anymore and is just a shell, but promises to fix himself one day at a time.","opus":"Make Them Suffer, the Australian band fusing symphonic beauty with brutality, plead through a closed door. Begging to be admitted, the narrator owns his failures and his absence, calling himself a husk with nothing left, yet vows he'll never wound her again and will rebuild himself gradually. Remorse and self-disgust grapple with a stubborn, fragile hope for repair.","sonnet":"A man pleads for readmission to a relationship while simultaneously admitting he is hollowed out and incapable of giving what is needed. The appeal is contradictory throughout: he asks to be let in and then begs to be released, knowing she deserves better but unable to stop reaching toward her. Repair is promised one day at a time, though the promise already feels insufficient.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~midnight-run":{"haiku":"Someone sacrifices attending an important moment with their partner to stay with friends, choosing loyalty to companions over a relationship commitment.","opus":"A metalcore confession of choosing struggle over stability, the narrator working himself to the bone yet unable to turn a house into a home or show up for the person waiting. Ambition and loyalty pull against love, and love loses.","sonnet":"A person torn between the demands of chasing a dream — working themselves to the bone, missing the play their partner saved a seat for — and the relationships that sustain them, with the \"midnight run\" capturing the moment both people flee something they can no longer hold together.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~power-overwhelming":{"haiku":"Someone gains newfound strength and autonomy but becomes distant and inaccessible as a result, ultimately breaking connection with those who knew them.","opus":"A narrator reborn with unlocked, overwhelming power recoils from it, insisting this isn't who he is even as a fractured relationship reduces him to a device on someone's shelf. Make Them Suffer weave metalcore intensity with the cold refrain of a call that cannot be connected, framing newfound strength as a curse he wants no part of.","sonnet":"Reborn into a mythic plane of ancient power, someone discovers a strength that frightens them, feeling more like an instrument than a person. The repeated refusal of the crown — this is not what they are — becomes increasingly desperate as outside forces keep remaking them into their own device.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~save-yourself":{"haiku":"An abuser acknowledges their toxicity and urges their victim to escape. They confess their cruelty and fear while hoping to eventually face their demons.","opus":"Make Them Suffer flip the breakup so the wrongdoer confesses, urging a partner to escape a relationship he admits he poisoned. The spoken ending strips away his usual evasions to own that he exploited her and is too unwell to be good for her, finally mustering the nerve to release her for her sake.","sonnet":"A candid address to someone the narrator has damaged — admitting he took them for granted, bruised and disappeared, and rather than asking forgiveness he tells them to run and never come back. The spoken outro breaks from metaphor entirely to admit he's not doing well but is finally learning to face himself.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~soul-decay":{"haiku":"Criticizes self-pity and self-inflicted imprisonment, demanding someone face their fears and accept responsibility for their degradation. Decay stems from their own choices.","opus":"A pointed takedown of a self-pitying manipulator who plays the flawless martyr, constructing his own confinement out of choices he alone made while feigning collapse, rotting from the inside by his own doing. Make Them Suffer are an Australian metalcore band fusing crushing weight with melodic gloss, and here they watch someone's downfall, daring him to demolish the cage he built.","sonnet":"A diagnosis delivered without sympathy — the subject has perfected victimhood as a performance, built their own prison out of self-inflicted decisions, and worn the damage like a badge while smiling about being wasted. The speaker watches the grace drain out and refuses to offer comfort: the only path forward is to burn the structure down and face what's actually there underneath.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~that-s-just-life":{"haiku":"A meditation on hardship and transformation within a corrupt power structure. The song accepts that adaptation or collapse is inevitable when systems change and bind people.","opus":"Built on royal imagery of a heavy crown and changing reigns, the song frames upheaval and inevitable change as simply part of life, where all bonds break to embrace the winds of change.","sonnet":"An announcement of a new order rising from a troubled past, framed in the language of crowns and royal blood, with the pragmatic message that change is inevitable and one must either adapt or fall apart.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~the-attendant":{"opus":"A brutally honest self-assessment from someone who knows they're toxic, describing themselves as a parasite destroying their host and a lump in their partner's throat, begging to be saved while urging their lover to stay away for their own good.","sonnet":"A song about self-loathing so severe the narrator considers themselves toxic and harmful to others — they beg to be saved while simultaneously warning people to stay away, caught between needing help and believing they are beyond redemption.","src":"sonnet"},
"make-them-suffer~the-first-movement":{"haiku":"A boy hears a mysterious voice in the wind and meets a woman who drives him through the city, exploring connection and romance.","opus":"A boy is drawn by a beautiful, haunting voice and dreams of a girl who finally arrives to pick him up for a ride, urging him to be brave and trust himself; unease grows as she begins driving in circles and the future turns uncertain.","sonnet":"A boy who hears a mysterious female voice finally works up the courage to trust her when she pulls up and offers him a ride, and the song traces the tentative, hopeful beginning of a bond that may matter only to him.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~uncharted":{"haiku":"Travelers abandon physical form to explore an unmapped digital realm guided only by stars and inner light.","opus":"A voyage into a digital or inner cosmos where shedding your body is the price of truly finding yourself, diving through the sky toward the deepest reaches with only distant lights as a compass. The idea underneath is that losing yourself is how you're found, and that our wounds, not just our brightness, push us into the unknown.","sonnet":"A psychedelic journey song about dissolving the physical self to find something beyond it — going digital, going dark, letting scars guide rather than hinder. The \"uncharted\" territory is the interior self, and the song treats the unknown as a destination worth heading toward.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~vortex-interdimensional-spiral-hindering-inexplicable-euphoria":{"haiku":"Spiraling descent completes cyclical inevitable collapse; the speaker surrenders to vortex, accepting disintegration as the only authentic motion.","opus":"A willing surrender to a downward spiral that spits him out a shattered, crippled mess, accepting that returning to the beginning and closing the loop is the best he can manage.","sonnet":"A song about cyclical psychological collapse — being sucked down into a destructive pattern, broken and spat back out as a diminished version of yourself, only to circle back to the same starting point. The vortex is both the process of falling apart and the inevitable return to it; the narrator accepts this loop with grim resignation rather than hope.","src":"haiku"},
"make-them-suffer~widower":{"haiku":"Predatory transformation and emotional death: the speaker seduces and destroys a partner piece by piece, then rejects the relationship's sadness, having severed emotional bonds while drawing the other into darkness.","opus":"Make Them Suffer spin a gothic folk-tale of mourning and desertion. A grieving man remembers a woman who surrendered herself away bit by bit, an eerie figure who unraveled the more she gave, until he shut her out, weary of her sorrow. Offering to lead her into the realm of the dead, he watches her fade each night, love reimagined as a haunted fable.","sonnet":"A speaker watches a woman — imagined as a widow or spider weaving stories in the woods — give herself away piece by piece to the world until nothing remains. He once witnessed her singing for hours, sharing herself with the moon and trees, but has turned his back on the sadness of it. The final image of a smiling bride weeping blood fuses love, loss, and gothic horror into a single unbearable tenderness.","src":"haiku"},
"mako~zgubi-em-si-w-supermarkecie":{"haiku":"A child lost in a crowded supermarket, alone and searching but also strangely liberated by isolation.","opus":"A child's-eye comedy of getting lost in a supermarket, drifting from bored parents into aisles of toys and vacuum cleaners, then deciding the store might not be a bad place to live. The Polish track turns a small panic into playful wonder.","sonnet":"A child gets separated from their parents in a supermarket and, after brief panic, settles into the discovery that it's survivable — there's a toilet, food, doughnuts with tomato; the security guard is useless, nobody wants to play, but 'zgubiłem się w supermarkecie' lands as both literal predicament and gentle existential joke about being lost and managing anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"mako~zgubiłem-się-w-supermarkecie":{"haiku":"Childhood disorientation in abundance transforms into both terror and refuge, where getting lost becomes a possibility of escape. The familiar becomes foreign when the child ventures beyond parental sight.","src":"haiku"},
"man-ov-god~exorcist":{"haiku":"A Christian metal musician challenges religious hypocrisy, using heavy music as ministry to confront darkness and expose false faith in the music industry.","opus":"A defiant Christian-metal boast, the narrator styling himself a beast who casts out demons over the crowd while dodging the industry's phony allies. Man ov God treat their crushing music as literal ministry, fifteen years and six albums in, turning the stage into a battlefield of the spirit where hypocrites and doubters get lumped together.","sonnet":"A Christian metal artist carving out space inside a genre that treats him as a contradiction — neither secular nor safely religious, fifteen years in and dismissed from both sides, he recasts the tension as ministry: the mosh pit becomes the exorcism, bringing out demons through volume and faith rather than renouncing either. The absurdity is acknowledged and then turned into the whole point.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~2045":{"haiku":"In the year 2045, artificial intelligence rises to dominance and humanity faces obsolescence; humans must accept the singularity and their new place in that world.","opus":"A grinning ride toward the technological singularity, the year AI supposedly overtakes humanity, played not as doom but as an invitation to embrace the unknown without fear. Japanese wolf-masked outfit turning apocalypse into a party.","sonnet":"Set in the year 2045, the song imagines AI reaching singularity and humanity becoming obsolete, yet the speaker faces this not with fear but with a defiant sense of purpose and readiness. It frames the technological transition as another challenge humans have faced before and can meet head-on.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~a-11dftxn":{"haiku":"Bonds through shared sacrifice—people vow to persevere carrying miracles forward through darkness until dawn; loyalty and shared purpose transcend individual survival.","opus":"MAN WITH A MISSION, the wolf-masked rockers, favor grand loyalty themes, and this romanized anthem builds one around kizuna, the bond that generates a miracle. Fighting through darkness for a loved one, the narrator vows to keep the flame alive even as their own life runs out, until the world holding this person can be loved again. Sacrifice and shared purpose outlast individual survival, the band's signature note of defiant devotion.","sonnet":"A song in romanized Japanese about fighting through darkness for a person you love — the bond between two people generates a miracle (kizuna = bonds/miracle), and even as the narrator's life runs out, they vow to keep the flame alive until the world where this person exists can be loved again.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~broken-people":{"haiku":"Broken people in a ruthless world plot revenge in endless cycles, playing destructive games where the listener must burn others or be burned themselves.","opus":"A furious address to hollow, self-satisfied people trapped in cycles of grievance and revenge, the Japanese band turning that rot back on everyone, including themselves, into a nihilistic invitation to burn the whole rigged game down.","sonnet":"A song about rage at the world's ruthlessness and the pull toward self-destruction — broken people stand in graveyards reloading for revenge, the circle of violence is endless, and the only honest question is why not end it all.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~dark-crow":{"haiku":"Someone embraces self-determination and control, crushing all obstacles and refusing to accept external justice or limitations, creating their own final judgment.","opus":"Man With A Mission cast a lone fighter swearing under a dark sky to see his mission through to the very end, ready to strike down anyone in his path, even kings and gods. The Japanese verses expose the toll, grinding down his own wounds and solitude to keep fighting through an endless loop of oblivion and memory, answering to no one's judgment but his own.","sonnet":"A lone warrior driven by his own code rather than kings, gods, or external justice — he crushes enemies and absorbs all wounds and loneliness as fuel, though the Japanese passages reveal he's haunted by forgotten dreams and the question of what he was actually fighting toward.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~dead-end-in-tokyo":{"haiku":"Tokyo is a city of broken dreams where wealth and poverty collide in endless cycles. Trapped souls pursue impossible escape amid neon and decay.","opus":"A nighttime sketch of Tokyo's seedy nightlife district, populated by a solitary drunk, a starry-eyed girl chasing illusions, a would-be rebel, and a wealthy man looking down on everyone else. The recurring warning, that you go on existing but never truly escape, casts the glittering city as a beautiful trap with no exit.","sonnet":"About the hollow glamour of Tokyo's nightlife districts — a rich man performing power for the masses, a woman with dreams swallowed by the city's lies, a Shinjuku rebel destined to burn out. Everyone ends up trapped in the same dead end no matter their angle of entry.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~dog-days":{"haiku":"A person is caught between numbness and longing, trying to break free from boring cycles and lies, calling for rebirth and the courage to seize life despite being beaten down.","opus":"Man With A Mission chase the resolve to escape numbing routine and feel truly alive again. The narrator, tallying cracks in the sidewalk and shrugging off any need for a flawless life, decides that after being knocked flat he'll fight to reclaim the energy of his best years instead of just getting by.","sonnet":"Someone dragging themselves through a directionless stretch of life, buried in lies they've accumulated, longing to break free from the monotony and reclaim the rawer, more alive feeling of harder times.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~followers":{"haiku":"Two figures escape capture and pursue uncertain paths together, moving past regret toward an unknown destination. They follow because they choose to, not because they must.","opus":"The Japanese wolf-masked rockers frame two runaways as fugitives no force can capture, racing an aimless journey they skim through rather than read. Death becomes transfiguration into a shooting star, a truth the narrator carries onward. Their bilingual verses, half English chant and half Japanese, turn flight into a creed of following on rather than settling.","sonnet":"Two figures race beyond capture, bound together like shooting stars destined to become something celestial. The journey has no fixed destination and the answers keep piling up unanswered, yet they choose to keep following one another through whatever comes, carrying that shared belief forward.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~give-it-away":{"haiku":"A creative artist drowns in their own neverland, compelled to express and release the restless urge within them before dying, yet doubting if anything they give matters.","opus":"A restless howl to break out of a self-made trap and expel the itch to finally do something before decay sets in. Crude, self-mocking lines about worthless effort frame a fight against futility and the drive to shake free just once more before the end.","sonnet":"About burning through creative and personal frustration — trapped in a self-made neverland, the narrator wants to expel the urge that's been crawling inside them, spit it out and give it away before they die still churning uselessly, demanding not to have their time wasted.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~hey-now":{"haiku":"A warning about harsh reality and mass delusion: people sleepwalk through ignorance, heading toward a 'free fall' of collapse while angels and demons close in.","opus":"A bleak view of a decaying, sleepless city where people are trapped in line heading downward; it warns that going wrong is easy and that angels and demons alike are closing in on a doomed free fall.","sonnet":"Set in a sleepless, chaotic city, the song warns that blindly following the crowd leads downward, framing life as a gamble between heaven and hell where ignorance and insanity spread. It urges self-awareness against the pull of conformity and inevitable collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~please-forgive-me":{"haiku":"A speaker begs forgiveness and asks their partner not to leave, framing the relationship as the only source of feeling alive. They emphasize that this is their time together and plead against letting go.","opus":"Man with a Mission, the Japanese wolf-masked rock band, channel arena-scale urgency into a plea against separation. The narrator seeks pardon and asks the person who awakens him not to depart, insisting these are their finest hours. Dread lifts and grief dries as he resolves to mend things, unwilling to release a bond that has traveled too great a distance to surrender.","sonnet":"A desperate appeal to hold on through what feels like a final night — fear fades when this particular presence is near, and the complexity of where they are headed together matters less than the fact of not letting go before morning.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~raise-your-flag":{"haiku":"People under pressure must resist misleading direction and raise their flag despite repeated failure, struggling and persevering to chase dreams against impossible odds.","opus":"A bilingual anthem urging the pressured and misled to stop following others' directions and raise their own flag, screaming with all their voice toward an uncertain destination; the deeper read is a call to self-determination and perseverance, choosing to be the soldier who acts rather than the bystander, struggling on through failure and doubt.","sonnet":"A bilingual English-Japanese anthem about persisting through failure and fear — the lyrics urge the listener to keep running, keep dreaming, keep raising their flag even after being knocked down, because belief that you'll eventually arrive somewhere matters more than the certainty of getting there.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~reiwa-feat-milet":{"haiku":"Seeking renewal and redemption through connection that transcends death; today's oath and tomorrow's peace drive the desire to see loved ones again.","opus":"An uplifting anthem about facing the sun with courage, confessing sins beneath the moon and vowing to heal and rise, longing to meet a loved one again even across reincarnations. Man With A Mission are the Japanese wolf-headed rock band, and joined by singer milet they name the track after Japan's Reiwa era, weaving prayers for peace and renewal into soaring, dawn-breaking rock built for a hopeful new age.","sonnet":"A song about facing the sun together across lifetimes — yesterday is something carved into memory, tomorrow holds the answer, and the speaker wants to find the same person again even in reincarnation. Reiwa and Heiwa — the era and peace — braid together as the new dawn arrives, the oath is renewed, and the forward direction is declared both personal and collective: heal, rise, change.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~survivor":{"haiku":"A person stands on the edge of collapse but chooses evolution and survival; they must become a survivor who breaks free and finds their path despite the fragility of their world.","opus":"A driving anthem of endurance, the narrator swearing to outlast a brutal contest by shedding his old skin and transforming. The Japanese passages double down on that fight, urging him to lift his blade and claim a fresh start each morning.","sonnet":"A bilingual Japanese-English declaration of resilience — feeling alone and directionless on a fragile world, the narrator commits to survival, breaking out of stagnation, and choosing transformation, with the Japanese lines reinforcing that they will certainly connect and rise together.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~take-me-under":{"haiku":"Two people trapped in their own small worlds want to dive deeper together into destruction or rebirth. They sing together in fragile hope, accepting pain as the price of connection.","opus":"Addiction to a shrunken private world, a cage that wears the narrator to the bone, drives a plea to be dragged down alongside another into oblivion. Reversal and rebirth thread through the Japanese lines, framing surrender as fiery renewal. The masked Tokyo band leans into their signature bilingual heaviness, making self-destruction sound almost like communion.","sonnet":"Trapped inside a self-made cage of compulsion, a figure craves the release of being pulled down alongside someone else. Rebirth through submersion draws them — not destruction alone but shared dissolution, a fragile hope that sinking together with another might finally break the cycle and restart something new.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~the-world-s-on-fire":{"haiku":"The world is collapsing from environmental and systemic failure, yet people find hope in unity. The song balances apocalyptic imagery with a plea for collective action.","opus":"A defiant call to unite as the world burns, standing on the edge and behind the end yet refusing to give up, people coming together with the will to stand and fight so one day they'll laugh again. Man With A Mission, the Japanese wolf-masked rock band, turn apocalyptic dread into anthemic solidarity here, insisting that even with the world gone they remain together, forever, on fire.","sonnet":"End-times defiance that refuses to accept the end — the edge has been reached, everything has fallen apart, nature is screaming while no one listens, but the people keep coming together and hoping this time the unity holds. The fire is real and the world is arguably already gone, but the ones left inside it are still together, and that's the only available reason to keep going.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~waiting-for-the-moment":{"haiku":"An avenger comes with sword and will to end a dynasty and fix wrongs through surprise violence; they demand blind obedience and patience as inevitable retribution approaches with might and vengeance.","opus":"A patient predator's monologue, all coiled menace and promised reckoning. The threat is dressed up as deliverance, the aggressor casting his violence as a cure the victim won't see coming until it lands.","sonnet":"A patient threat directed at a corrupt or oppressive ruler: the narrator is coming to end the dynasty and make things right, biding his time until the moment to strike arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~when-my-devil-rises":{"haiku":"A person awakens their inner rage and destructive power to rebel against corruption and injustice. The song channels fury into defiance against a broken system.","opus":"A raging anthem about unleashing an inner devil against corruption and hollow justice, the narrator vowing to break and take down the powers that choke him as the countdown to explosion nears. Man With A Mission, the Japanese wolf-headed rock band, blend English and Japanese verses here, the bilingual lyric setting apocalyptic red skies and broken wings against a stomping rebel chorus of taking it all down.","sonnet":"Corruption as a countdown that cannot be stopped — the air itself is toxic, justice is exposed as a joke, and the voices of those who have been cast down echo through everything. The speaker's devil isn't evil so much as the force that finally breaks through compliance and tears the whole hollow parade apart, the Japanese lines about a red sky and broken wings suggesting someone choosing to drag themselves forward regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"man-with-a-mission~winding-road":{"haiku":"Perseverance through chaos and lies: the speaker journeys a long road seeking truth while surrounded by deception and violence, holding onto hope despite the chaos threatening to consume them.","opus":"Man with a Mission, the Japanese wolf-masked rock band, pursue truth down an endless road in mixed English and Japanese. Shaking with fury at a deceiver in a bleak, colorless world, they pledge to reach some destination together and dream again, tallying scars and stars alike. Balanced on life's knife-edge, they push toward daybreak with stubborn hope.","sonnet":"A bilingual anthem of perseverance through a world of lies and violence: the road is long, the wounds are real, hope is counted one star at a time, but the resolve is not to release the other person or to stop moving forward. Truth will eventually surface for those who keep travelling together rather than surrendering to exhaustion.","src":"haiku"},
"manapart~bull-s-eye":{"haiku":"Totalitarian force and paranoia weaponize fear; the speaker demands obedience through violence, replacing truth with authoritarian control.","opus":"A demagogue's voice barks at comrades to obey without doubt, expose spies, and crush an ever-present enemy, arming and betraying the very people it claims to protect. The song frames paranoid authoritarian rule as a self-created reality doomed to burn.","sonnet":"A totalitarian authority figure barks commands — 'tovarishi, expose the spies, obey my rules or everybody dies' — while the narrator reveals armies on the streets, promises used to arm and then betray the people, and a final cry of 'save me' signals the reality the created order is doomed to burn.","src":"haiku"},
"mandragora-uk~double-negatives":{"haiku":"Self-protection through denial and lying; pain suppressed becomes the foundation of a personality erased by constant concealment.","opus":"A pop-punk-tinged unraveling of a one-sided relationship, where the narrator hides their pain, spins lies, and grows exhausted holding up someone who never reciprocates. When the other person finally turns around, it only breeds regret for the hatred already spread. It reaches for the weary honesty of someone done being unseen, done doing all the emotional labor.","sonnet":"A relationship where one person carries all the effort and receives nothing — empty words, no remorse, support withheld as the narrator waits by the phone. The turning point is exhaustion: before you speak don't you think of me, I'm done, you don't see me — the double negatives of the title perhaps being the way they've both been lying to each other about what this is.","src":"haiku"},
"manic-days~overtaken":{"haiku":"Time slipping away while trapped in stagnation, every day identical and equally suffocating.","opus":"Living inside depression's flat loop of identical days, faked smiles and the sense that time and life are simply wasting away. The anxiety of waking and immediately breaking down is named without any redemptive turn.","sonnet":"About being overwhelmed by depression and existential stagnation — every day is the same, anxiety spikes each morning, the smile is fake, and the narrator feels they are at the end of their days; they're not breaking free, they're being overtaken by something they can't name or fix.","src":"haiku"},
"mannequin-death-squad~monsters":{"haiku":"Predatory impulse and transformative hunger corrupt the speaker, who feeds on others to survive and never forgets the debt.","opus":"Toxic obsession rendered as vampiric hunger, wanting to drain the other dry and strip everyone bare, unable to forget a single word or wound. The refrain's bleak truth is that monsters don't reform, they only consume, so capturing the lover is really predation dressed as love.","sonnet":"About the irreversible damage done by someone who turned monstrous in a relationship — the narrator can't forget what was done and said, and gradually the desire for revenge ('I want to suck your blood') matches the original predation; the conclusion is that monsters stay monsters and only consume.","src":"haiku"},
"mannequin-death-squad~safe-and-warm":{"haiku":"Surrender to safety becomes self-imprisonment; the speaker trades agency for protection, willing to shrivel inside boundaries they've accepted.","opus":"A grim bargain: the narrator will accept humiliation and living on their knees so long as it keeps them from dying, retreating into make-believe and petty survival. Safety is bought at the cost of dignity and honesty.","sonnet":"A person willing to live diminished — on their knees, without legs — just to avoid death, repeating 'I'm safe and warm' as a mantra while sneaking around at night and biting the hand that feeds, clinging to the security of the very system that confines them.","src":"haiku"},
"mannequin-pussy~i-got-heaven":{"haiku":"Protective fury toward the violated, seeking vengeance and spiritual transcendence through rage.","opus":"A raw collision of desire, grief and defiance against inherited religious shame, the singer claiming her own divinity and refusing to wait for permission or blessing. Blasphemy becomes self-possession, tenderness and vengeance held in the same breath.","sonnet":"A sprawling, angry reckoning with inherited trauma and gendered oppression — the narrator challenges religious and patriarchal structures that have always governed women's bodies and value, claims divine authority for herself, and expresses a desire for violent retribution on behalf of someone who was harmed. Underneath the rage is grief and survivor's guilt.","src":"haiku"},
"manowar~01-the-dawn-of-battle":{"haiku":"Martial destiny manifests as divinely ordained violence; the speaker embodies steel and thunder, bringing judgment through overwhelming power.","opus":"A warrior rises as an unstoppable force of destruction, riding to slaughter the wicked and rule by sheer will, promising to live again even if he falls. Pure heavy-metal mythologizing of battle as glory and rebirth.","sonnet":"A warrior consecrated by hammer and steel claims dominion over the world's elements, vowing to cut down heathens and sinners at the dawn of battle; the second movement adds a more personal note — waiting for a sign, a falling star, a sword — before the journey begins and the promise is made that even in death he will rise again.","src":"haiku"},
"manowar~hail-and-kill":{"haiku":"Violent conquest through dominion claims divine sanction, celebrating military brutality as righteous power wielded by ruthless warriors.","opus":"A barbarian war-cry glorifying conquest and slaughter, rule seized by force and claimed as heaven's will, escalating into open atrocity. Pure warrior bloodlust — fated to die and pledged to return.","sonnet":"A rallying war-cry from a warrior-narrator invoking brotherhood, divine right, and conquest — sworn to rise again as kinsman of the slain — culminating in explicit commands of total annihilation and the refrain that power and dominion are taken by will.","src":"haiku"},
"manowar~heart-of-steel":{"haiku":"Unbreakable resilience and individual will triumph through hardship while conformity and intimidation cannot shake those forged in resistance.","opus":"A lone warrior's creed of endurance, walking into the wind and unafraid to die, certain that those who mock and cut throats along the way will melt away like snow. The heart of steel is a defiant perseverance that stands and fights on instinct alone.","sonnet":"A warrior's creed of solitary defiance — born to walk against the wind, burning the bridge behind, never retreating — watching rivals fall like melting snow while the heart of steel proves too hard to break, too hard to hold.","src":"haiku"},
"manowar~return-of-the-warlord":{"haiku":"Unapologetic defiance celebrates freedom, motorcycle hedonism, and rejection of convention as the truest form of self-determination.","opus":"Pure Manowar creed: a defiant biker-warrior refusing anyone's authority, broke but free, family estranged, loyal only to metal and the road. It's less a story than a manifesto of the outsider who'd rather burn out doing what he feels than be controlled.","sonnet":"A defiant anthem of biker-metal brotherhood proclaims total freedom from control, social norms, and respectability — the speaker living only for heavy music, the road, friends, and the bike, dismissing anyone who would judge or govern their choices.","src":"haiku"},
"manowar~the-crown-and-the-ring-lament-of-the-kings":{"haiku":"A warrior rides to final battle, invoking Odin as he faces death, treating his sword and kingdom as sacred pledges.","opus":"A dying king rides endlessly between battles, downing a final drink and dedicating his blade to Odin, prepared to greet the Norse gods as he falls for a realm forged in iron. Manowar, the great high priests of epic warrior metal, deliver a solemn hymn whose storms of flame and gore-stained honor compress their entire heroic mythos into a dirge for slain rulers.","sonnet":"A warrior king rides from one battle directly into another, fully aware this ride ends in death, and greets that end with pride rather than sorrow. Odin is invoked not as a prayer for survival but as a formal farewell from a true son who has lived entirely by the sword.","src":"haiku"},
"manowar~wheels-of-fire":{"haiku":"A motorcyclist embodies speed, danger, and the thrill of near-death, living for the ride until it destroys them.","opus":"A living machine has thundered across the world since time began, its blood turned to explosive, existing only to ride and content to die on the road. Manowar, the New York band who made a career of maximalist metal excess, deliver a biker anthem of chrome and velocity where the very spirit of the road vows to scatter its rider into fragments.","sonnet":"Riding has become an identity older than memory — a primal, chrome-plated compulsion fueled by speed and the ecstatic risk of annihilation. The road is a religion and the machine is both companion and executioner; living to ride means accepting the possibility of burning up completely in the process.","src":"haiku"},
"mansionair~violet-city":{"haiku":"The speaker feels lost and disconnected in a neon city environment, trying to appease desires while caught between control and surrender, unable to navigate their displaced state.","opus":"Australian indie-electronic trio Mansionair drape atmospheric, falsetto-laced sound around a paradox of control and longing, voicing someone who realizes he only feels steady the instant he stops gripping. Lost in his own expectations and beside himself with wanting, he finds that surrendering command is the only way he ever feels in charge.","sonnet":"Longing held inside a sleepless urban night — the city washes over the speaker in color and want, someone is missing from the physical space beside them, and the only coherent insight available is that control only arrives the moment you release what you're gripping. The maze of expectation keeps the speaker displaced, beside themselves, somewhere else, still trying to figure out how to change.","src":"haiku"},
"many-eyes~future-proof":{"haiku":"Distance and disconnection erode intimacy; the speaker builds walls of self-sufficiency while mourning what cannot transcend the fundamental separation.","opus":"Someone chooses a self-protected future over a present relationship where a partner is hiding something, insisting they feel more alive in their own safe distance than beside the other person. The withdrawal is dressed up as being future proof, but it's really flight from a love that no longer feels honest.","sonnet":"A relationship fracturing over emotional distance and withheld truths — 'there's something you're not telling me, I can feel it' — with the speaker insisting they belong somewhere future-proof, alive in time rather than present and diminished with the person who has made them feel obsolete.","src":"haiku"},
"many-eyes~revelation":{"haiku":"Righteous anger and spiritual awakening empower the speaker to reject false authority and reclaim agency through confrontation.","opus":"A defiant assertion of spiritual endurance, the narrator claiming he has shaken off the devil and stumbled into grace, casting himself as a gentle soul who is also an instrument of holy violence. Scorn for the gutless and phony flares into a fierce chorus, and beneath it runs hard-won belief and a heart rebuilt against doubt.","sonnet":"A man who once felt the devil's grip has broken free and rebuilt his heart by hand — \"I am a flower in filth, I am a thorn in your bone\" — and now stands as a righteous weapon, daring hypocrites and frauds to look him in the eye.","src":"haiku"},
"maple-s-pet-dinosaur~lego":{"haiku":"Online cruelty and social engineering construct enemies from misunderstanding and competition. The speaker builds walls against infiltration and toxic noise through deliberate blocking.","opus":"A teenager claps back at a former friend turned enemy who trashed them behind their back and online, choosing to simply block the toxicity out like snapping apart Lego. Petty adolescent feuding is turned into a defiant kiss-off, complete with the bus route they gossip on.","sonnet":"A furious social callout: someone has been spreading lies, turning the school against the speaker, talking shit on the 743 bus and posting mean things on Instagram — the response is to block them like Lego and let them know they are living rent-free.","src":"haiku"},
"maraton~almost-human":{"haiku":"Bodily flaws and inner chaos resist redemption; the speaker exists in fractured duality—half-mechanism, half-creature—unable to stabilize despite another's promised transformation.","opus":"An entity poised on the edge of humanity, aware of something inhuman glowing inside it that another person's love might either redeem or expose. The dread lies in the doubt about whether anyone real exists behind the veil at all.","sonnet":"A figure on the boundary of humanity and something else examines a hidden inner force that glows under the iris and blooms when they are weak, wondering whether there is someone behind the veil; they refuse transformation, insisting \"I'm forever, I remain.\"","src":"haiku"},
"maraton~blood-music":{"haiku":"Identity inescapably shaped by family legacy, inherited burdens that define the self.","opus":"Identity as an inheritance you can't shrug off, the weight of a lineage humming in the bones and DNA. The tension is claiming yourself while admitting you're nothing without the very ties you're trying to sever, the song of your ancestry playing out of tune.","sonnet":"A speaker reckons with inherited identity — the melody of family running through DNA, an ego that tries to claim the self — ultimately asserting that despite being shaped by ancestry, the music of their blood is uniquely, stubbornly their own.","src":"haiku"},
"maraton~contranym":{"haiku":"Language reveals its contradictory nature through relentless cycles; nothing satisfies as effort yields only exhaustion and spiraling frustration.","opus":"An exhausted plea for total honesty from someone who keeps giving everything away and being left with nothing, while the other person changes goalposts endlessly. The relationship goes on and on with no resolution, only the demand to be told everything.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped in an endless, directionless cycle — \"it goes on and on\" — cycles through exhaustion with the effort to change or give everything, suspecting nothing is ever enough, yet refuses to spoil the fun, finally demanding the other tell them every single thought and what, if anything, remains.","src":"haiku"},
"maraton~prime":{"opus":"An introspective meditation on shifting identity, phasing out old versions of the self and discovering new ones under life's pressure; the singer asks whether there's a true, core self to find, echoing the plea for the real me to please stand up.","sonnet":"An examination of the shifting, plural nature of identity — the self is constantly changing under pressure, and the narrator keeps rediscovering new versions of themselves, wondering if the real one will ever stand up or if identity is always in flux.","src":"sonnet"},
"maren~autographs-in-hell":{"haiku":"Silence and erasure become forms of resistance as the speaker accepts damnation rather than submission, refusing to be consumed by an abuser's pedestal while carving their own hidden path.","opus":"An artist elevated and then destroyed by fame reimagines celebrity as her own damnation, picturing herself scribbling her name for admirers in the afterlife's fire, the same admirers who lifted her up before turning cruel. The trap is worship itself, and her only hope is that a future existence spares her this one.","sonnet":"A speaker crushed beneath another's abuse and unheard words finds a dark refuge below — signing autographs in hell, hidden beneath shame — asking only that their next life be different and turning the pedestal others built into a prison cell.","src":"haiku"},
"mareux~the-perfect-girl":{"haiku":"Someone is fascinated and confused by a strange woman they perceive as otherworldly, fixating on her appearance and desiring her despite not understanding her.","opus":"A darkwave reworking of The Cure's original, fixation on an unreadable, otherworldly woman rendered as brooding synth obsession. The remove of the cover makes the longing feel more haunted than romantic.","sonnet":"A song about falling for someone utterly strange and incomprehensible — the narrator is disoriented, wants to physically shake and flip her world around, and despite understanding nothing about her is completely, helplessly in love.","src":"haiku"},
"maria-brink~meet-me-in-the-fire-feat-andy-biersack":{"haiku":"Lovers fight through hell to save each other from self-destruction, burning in mutual sacrifice where connection remains unreachable despite endless struggle.","opus":"A duet of mutual rescue and mutual burning, two people who came through hell side by side and take turns sacrificing themselves for each other. The recurring failure to hear or feel one another underscores a painful gap even inside devotion, reaching for love as shared immolation, a vow to meet in the flames whatever it costs.","sonnet":"Two people who have saved each other through hell — watching the other burn so they themselves could be rescued — but the connection has frayed: mouths move, voices can't be heard, hands reach and can't be grasped. The chorus asks for a rendezvous in the fire, the one place the bond was forged and where it might still hold.","src":"haiku"},
"mariko-goto~a-2zfyy2":{"haiku":"A fragmented lullaby on the pain of growing up—hearing a melody about darkness and adulthood, asking to stay in dreams.","opus":"As Midori's frontwoman Mariko Goto made chaos her medium, and solo she turns that intensity toward the question of whether growing up must mean growing grim. Hearing an opening melody, she rewinds to the dark question and answers it by singing today into a structure of affirmation. The fragility and fierceness sit together, insisting today is a melody that builds you even while remembering how hard the arrival was.","sonnet":"A woman hears an opening melody and rewinds it to the dark, painful question of whether growing up has to mean growing grim — and the song itself is her answer, an act of singing today into a structure of affirmation and wonder. The emotional core is the fragility and fierceness of insisting that today is a melody that builds you, even while you remember how hard it was to get here.","src":"haiku"},
"mariko-goto~a-qy8d5a":{"haiku":"A chaotic rejection of meaning and convention—swimming in an eternal loop of desire, shedding tired philosophies, seeking the unprecedented.","opus":"Goto's post-Midori work keeps the punk fearlessness, and here it fuels a self-aware loop of grief over someone loved more than herself. She recycles clichés about life and death she has heard too often, nudges girls toward the edge she keeps circling, and drowns in a drone sea while insisting it was all a joke. The dark comedy is real: fluent enough in suicidal rhetoric to mock it and mean it at once.","sonnet":"A loop of obsessive, self-aware grief over someone loved more than the narrator loved herself — she cycles through clichés about life and death that she's heard too many times, pushes girls who've 'come this far' toward the edge she keeps circling, and drowns in a drone sea while insisting it was all a joke. The emotional core is the dark comedy of a person so fluent in suicidal rhetoric she can mock it and mean it simultaneously.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~1996":{"haiku":"Antichrist rhetoric stacks contradiction upon contradiction, invoking everything against everything in a litany of negation that deconstructs meaning itself.","opus":"Manson piling up every taboo and negation into a self-portrait as culture's designated monster, daring the offended to look at what they hate in him. The relentless anti-everything is the joke and the accusation both, a mirror held up to a scandalized America.","sonnet":"A relentless litany of anti- positions that accumulates into a portrait of total negation — the Antichrist Superstar persona rejects every social institution and moral framework, arriving at self-loathing as the only honest stance.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~antichrist-superstar":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic appetite and manufactured mythology converge as the speaker embodies collective anxiety, rising as undeniable force that betrays its own believers.","opus":"The title track of Manson's rise-of-a-messiah concept album, where a broken outsider is built into a dark savior by the same crowd that scorned him. He turns their worship back on them, a Hydra of resentment crowning the culture's own fear as its idol.","sonnet":"A figure who has been built up by the masses as an object of worship turns the power back on them, declaring himself the Hydra — unkillable and regenerating — and announcing the imminent arrival of the Antichrist as the inevitable product of the crowd's own need for spectacle. Betrayal is the promised gift.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~better-of-two-evils":{"haiku":"A provocateur who embraces villainy and calls themselves superior, stepping on others to climb while dismissing criticism and judgment.","opus":"Marilyn Manson forges the slurs thrown at him into armor, the narrator claiming to be something his detractors could never manage and savoring their unhappiness. Vowing to trample them whether he's rising or falling, he casts himself as both fall guy and rescuer, the lesser damnation. Swipes at photographers and refused temptations frame his infamy as a part he's perfected.","sonnet":"Contempt for critics and industry gatekeepers drives a declaration of proud outlier status. The narrator absorbs every insult hurled at him — slurs, dismissals, moral accusations — and converts them into proof that he occupies a position no detractor can reach, scapegoat and savior occupying the same body.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~birds-of-hell-awaiting":{"haiku":"Death arrives with apocalyptic finality, manifested as hellish birds ready to collect the dying and fulfill their darkest desires.","opus":"Compact and incantatory, this Marilyn Manson piece summons infernal birds with wings on fire, waiting to claim someone whose death is described as their own desire. The insistence that this is no phoenix denies any promise of rebirth. Through relentless repetition the imagery hardens into a verdict, framing an approaching end not as transformation but as something long wished for and now arriving.","sonnet":"A brief, concentrated vision of damnation as seduction. Wings aflame and circling, the birds of hell are not a symbol of rebirth but of desire for destruction — the narrator insisting that what approaches is not a phoenix but the object of a death wish, stripped of any redemptive pretense.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~bright-young-things":{"haiku":"Rebellion poses as authenticity while feeding spectacle. These defiant youth demand attention through fashion and shock, caring nothing for judgment.","opus":"A manifesto of theatrical, self-styled decadence set against a bygone Bright Young Things hedonism, insisting the rebellion is authentic self-expression rather than a marketing pose, while daring the listener to be impressive again.","sonnet":"A song celebrating deliberate transgression and anti-conformity as a lifestyle rather than a pose — the narrator and their tribe fake abuse for credibility, set fashions rather than follow them, and proudly proclaim themselves good for nothing but being everything that's bad, framing their revolt not as commodity rebellion but as authentic identity.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~coma-white":{"haiku":"A woman stands suspended between perfection and self-destruction, medication offering only numbness while her authentic self bleeds away. No pharmaceutical intervention saves anyone from their own dissolution.","opus":"A portrait of a woman anesthetized by pills against a perfect, hollow world, and the flat truth that no drug can save her from herself. Manson wrote it drawing on his relationship and the numbing of fame.","sonnet":"A woman stands on an overpass in a medicated stupor, her smile blank, bleeding like an old Polaroid — pills numb and hollow her out, but no drug can save her from the perfect world that discarded her, and no one catches her as she waits to fall.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~cupid-carries-a-gun":{"haiku":"A gothic inversion of love and desire, where the speaker presents himself as dangerous and deathlike, merging eroticism with violence. Love becomes a weapon and seduction is inseparable from threat and transgression.","opus":"Marilyn Manson fuses sex, death and religion into a menacing self-portrait, casting himself as a death figure and his lover as an embodiment of apocalypse. Love here is lethal and damning, a deity-like protector who holds your hand while keeping a finger on the trigger, swapping the sweet myth of desire for a deadly one.","sonnet":"Love reframed as something predatory and corrupted — Cupid no longer shoots innocent arrows but carries a weapon, and the speaker positions himself as a dark guardian figure whose encounters with women are described in terms of death, violence, and sex. The song argues that modern love is armed and dangerous, and prayer should go to hell rather than heaven.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~day-3":{"haiku":"The dissolution of a relationship over a week-long binge, with the speaker feeling trapped and preferring victimhood to staying with their partner.","opus":"Three days into a weeklong bender, a lover's name already dissolves from the narrator's mouth, worn out and worn like someone else's borrowed hell. He would sooner be a victim than remain bound to her. Manson, ever the theatrician of decay, strips the persona back to raw dependency and the flat exhaustion of a binge running its course.","sonnet":"Midway through a week-long binge, a figure watches an obsession quietly dissolve. The relationship has become a form of self-punishment — wearing another person like borrowed suffering — and yet an admitted masochism keeps the damage preferable to genuine escape.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~deep-six":{"haiku":"Warns against self-deception and the danger of allowing someone into one's mind, using mythic imagery to suggest spiritual invasion and control.","opus":"A confrontation with a narcissistic, controlling figure, invoking Zeus's warning to Narcissus, as the narrator resists having his mind invaded and equates love with evil and sin.","sonnet":"The narrator describes a destructive relationship in which someone has invaded and rearranged his inner world, using imagery of wax feathers and artillery to convey a power imbalance, while invoking the myth of Narcissus as a warning against self-absorption.","src":"haiku","fable":"Manson wraps a lover's raid on his ego in Greek myth, invoking the thunder-god's warning to the boy who drowned in his reflection so vanity becomes the thing policing vanity. The Pale Emperor's blues-noir hush fits: someone burglarizes his mind and rearranges its contents, yet fails because a serpent has neither knees for kneeling nor any instinct toward prayer. His Icarus boast admits the flight is doomed even as he refuses the fall, and recasting a repeated question about their shared bed as an invitation to a grave folds intimacy into the sailor's burial and the beast's number at once. Underneath, a litany equating tenderness with malice, swindle with self-assurance, and transgression with candor melts every romantic vow into fraud. The true subject is not heartbreak but a predator auditing his own hunger, the wounded-romantic mask left on while the chant confesses the sincerity was always the swindle.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","faith & the occult","love & desire"],"means":"a predator auditing his hunger while the romantic mask stays on","built":"the title folds a sailor's burial and a beast's number together"},
"marilyn-manson~disassociative":{"haiku":"Digital numbness and physical alienation trap the speaker as a dead astronaut adrift in space, unable to escape or feel authentically. Dissociation persists as the only available protection.","opus":"Detachment pictured as a corpse floating in orbit, wiring shut off and the self already vanished, slipping free of a body and a reality that was destroying him through a numb severance from his own flesh.","sonnet":"Digital existence has severed body from spirit until the speaker feels like a dead astronaut drifting in space — the nervous system is down, the world is killing them, and the only remaining image is floating grey and gone, writing songs already from the place of the dead.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~doll-dagga-buzz-buzz-zigety-za":{"haiku":"Fascistic aesthetics and uniform conformity hiding depravity beneath surface glamour, the masses robotically enacting performative obedience.","opus":"A glam-industrial fever dream from Manson's Golden Age of Grotesque, drenched in Weimar-cabaret decadence and fascist-kitsch imagery. Its nonsense chant and parade of doppelgängers, drag, and drugs stage identity as performance and conformity as a grotesque burlesque. The refrain about toys turns people into disposable playthings, a satire of mass sameness masquerading as celebration.","sonnet":"Pop culture as collective possession — goose-step girlies, doppelgangers called to join, thug rock kids and punk god angels all marching down the stairway to substance. 'The toys are us and we don't even know' is the thesis: consumption and performance have made everyone a replica, and the invitation to be one of us is indistinguishable from a threat.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~fall-of-the-house-of-death":{"haiku":"In a realm where angels die alongside demons, secrets are exposed and the speaker faces a mirror of himself; neither confidence nor weapons protect from one's own reflection.","opus":"Marilyn Manson trades on gothic industrial shock, and this Poe-tinged track stages a confrontation with mortality as horror. The narrator rages at death only to realize the enemy wears his own face, entering a realm where even the pure are corrupted. It reads as a reckoning with self, secrets exposed, where no one escapes the reckoning of the end.","sonnet":"A house where nothing and no one is safe — even angels die in the arms of demons here, secrets are torn open like a scarecrow on death row, and the mirror is made of the speaker's own face so there's nowhere to hide from what the self has become. The dagger and the swagger are both useless; the only odds that matter are the ones that exempt no one.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~fated-faithful-fatal":{"haiku":"The speaker feels hollowed and ready to meet their maker; they invoke resurrection and deify themselves as Los Angeles' dark power.","opus":"Shock-rock provocateur Marilyn Manson wrestles with intimacy and his own mythology, confessing that he has been laid bare too often to open himself again. Trading old aggression for a passivity he finds pathetic, he wraps himself in a grandiose, damned persona, leaving unanswered whether a relationship is destiny, devotion, or destruction.","sonnet":"A figure who has been cracked open too many times refuses further vulnerability while simultaneously claiming he will rise above every occasion. Between heretical isolation and Mephistophelean self-mythology, he circles the same question — whether what binds him to another person is destiny, loyalty, or mutual destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~fundamentally-loathsome":{"haiku":"Hatred feels tangible while love remains phantom; self-destruction paradoxically expresses devotion.","opus":"A numbed depressive's confession that hate feels more real to him than love ever could, curdling into a fantasy where self-destruction and destroying the beloved are the same act. It sits in the exhausted resignation of someone who no longer trusts tenderness.","sonnet":"The speaker contrasts his own capacity for feeling — even through hatred — against a lover whose affection is hollow and unreal, concluding that loving himself would mean destroying her, and that both of them are resigned to a world headed for ruin.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~great-big-white-world":{"haiku":"Drained vitality and disconnection from authentic feeling pervade a colourless world where love itself becomes impossible.","opus":"A numbed dispatch from a bleached, colorless existence where nothing heals or grows and people no longer love themselves or each other, medicated and detached. Manson makes whiteness the emptiness of a world drained of feeling and beauty.","sonnet":"A vision of emotional and cultural bleaching — dreamed of as a moth burning near stars that are no nearer than a morgue, all colour drained from a world so white — mourning a time when people loved themselves and each other, now stitched together on low prescriptions.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~i-don-t-like-the-drugs-but-the-drugs-like-me":{"haiku":"Society manufactures empty souls through repression and stupidity, then fills the void with addictive substances and manufactured shame; the individual recognizes the trap but cannot escape it.","opus":"A glammy, gospel-tinged satire of sanitized American normalcy, skewering a rehabbed, piss-tested, God-fearing culture that fills its spiritual emptiness with dope while preaching sobriety. Manson mocks the televised confessions and hollow conformity of Mechanical Animals-era Middle America. The hook captures addiction as something that stalks even those who claim to reject it, a hole nothing wholesome can fill.","sonnet":"Normal life as the real narcotic: suburbia's white God, piss tests, confessional TV, talkshow Christians pointing at suicides — the \"norm life\" the song catalogs is itself the drug that hollows people out. The refrain reverses cause and effect: the speaker doesn't choose the drugs, the drugs choose the void that norm life already installed.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~i-want-to-disappear":{"haiku":"The speaker chronicles a life of vacancy and moral decay, feeling hollowed out despite shifting identities, and expresses a desire to vanish as a response to loveless upbringing and meaningless existence.","opus":"A bleak self-sketch of a wrecked generation raised without steady parents, hooked on being mistreated because it lets them feel wanted, sliding toward narcotics, self-hatred, and a longing to blink out of existence altogether.","sonnet":"A person raised in a dysfunctional home — with absent parents and normalized abuse — describes feeling hollowed out and yearning to vanish, moving from nihilism to simple boredom, and craving self-destruction as a form of release.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~irresponsible-hate-anthem":{"haiku":"Hatred becomes equity, all parties equally culpable and corrupted, resistance itself absorbed into the system it opposes while innocence remains impossible.","opus":"A deliberately ugly mirror held to America, Manson playing the monster the country made and daring it to look. The provocation, slurs and all, is aimed at a nation that can't see its own violence, insisting the hater and the victim are the same person.","sonnet":"A confrontational paradox: the speaker hates the hater and rapes the raper, turning every form of social violence back on itself until nothing is left but universal complicity. America's blindness to its own contradictions, the ubiquity of the victim-perpetrator cycle, and the impossibility of choosing a side are all declared with deliberate obscenity.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~ka-boom-ka-boom":{"haiku":"A rock performer celebrates depravity and fake celebrity culture where ego drives destructive behavior and love becomes a sore heart transaction.","opus":"Manson turns his own stardom and America's appetite for spectacle into a sneering circus act, equating fame with sex, drugs and destruction. Corrupted nursery and cartoon references stage a rockstar as gleeful predator, everything for show and nothing sincere beneath the swagger.","sonnet":"A rock star persona built on provocation, carnality, and power — Manson casts himself as a tasteless entertainer who does things 'to' people rather than 'with' them, loving them with a sore heart while the depraved new world cheers.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~killing-strangers":{"haiku":"The song presents violence as preventative—killing strangers so those we love stay safe; guns are the tool and running is the consequence.","opus":"America's gun appetite as blunt psychology: violence turned outward on strangers so it isn't turned on the people you love. Manson delivers it as a slow menacing march, indictment and swagger at once.","sonnet":"A song about channeling violence outward as a way of protecting what you love — the narrator and his crew kill strangers to prevent themselves from destroying the people they actually care about, framing weaponized aggression as a form of love.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~mobscene":{"haiku":"Society as spectacle and transgression; freedom through obscenity and descent into shared debasement.","opus":"Manson staging spectacle itself as the obscenity, the crowd that comes to gawk complicit in the show. Framed as a decadent age of deformation, it fuses arousal, violence, and celebrity into one grinning provocation.","sonnet":"A provocateur invites the audience into a spectacle of deliberate obscenity, framing transgression and excess as more honest than respectable society's freedoms, and urging everyone to embrace visibility and degradation rather than politely staying silent.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~new-model-no-15":{"haiku":"Hollow perfection masks inner absence as the speaker performs disposability and control, offering nothing but surface compliance. Modeling oneself as empty product destroys agency from within.","opus":"A savage satire of the empty, marketable modern self, a fake and disposable product that's correctly political and hollow inside, poseable and plugged in to know who it is.","sonnet":"A hollow, politically correct, disposable model of personhood — fake as a wedding cake, empty inside, performing competence and pleasure at the office and in bed — is presented as the aspirational contemporary identity, defined entirely by surfaces and nothing beneath.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~no-reflection":{"haiku":"Internal fragmentation leaves no stable self to love; the narrator threatens violence while trapped between weakness and destructive impulses.","opus":"Manson circles a self he can't locate or love, no reflection to fix on, veering between suicidal cliche and a menacing threat outward. It's about a fractured identity that can only define itself by the damage it promises.","sonnet":"A fractured self unable to recognize itself — multiple competing identities with no stable core — spiraling into self-destructive ideation not as a death wish but as an expression of internal dissolution, threatening to take the listener down with it.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~no-reflection-radio-edit":{"haiku":"Identity fractures into multiplicities without unified reflection; the speaker cannot locate which self deserves love or recognition.","opus":"A fractured identity that can't recognize itself confronts a lover with menace and self-loathing, contemplating self-destruction while promising to inflict worse on them. The lack of any reflection is a confession of having no fixed self at all.","sonnet":"A fragmenting self that can find no stable identity — \"I don't know which me that I love / got no reflection\" — swings between self-destruction and threatening menace, trapped in a week-long weakness while cataloguing the methods of self-erasure and warning the listener they don't want to know what comes next.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~odds-of-even":{"haiku":"No one escapes death's reckoning; facing oneself exposes all secrets and strips away all defenses in the end.","opus":"A narrator squares off against bleak mortality and finds his defenses worthless before a looking-glass shaped from his own features, shielding his core while onlookers wait to shred him. Marilyn Manson's late-period gothic gloom stages a mansion of dying where celestial beings expire in fiendish grips, ending on the grim inevitability that no one dodges the final leveling.","sonnet":"Confronted with mortality and the mirror, all the usual armour — bravado, sharp wit, weaponised self-image — proves useless. In the house where even angels perish in demonic arms, no one escapes the mathematics of their own reckoning.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~red-black-and-blue":{"haiku":"A being oscillates between destruction and divinity, seeing only gods and demons, questioning whether they are garbage or god.","opus":"A bombastic, deliberately blasphemous fever-dream in which the narrator crowns himself a destroyer bent on ruining all beauty, his eyes reflecting warring gods and demons as he wonders whether he's holy or worthless. Manson piles on his trademark shock, torching creation just to revel in the wreckage, self-mythology soaked in disgust and theatrical excess.","sonnet":"A mind at war with its own grandiosity — the speaker slides between snake, king bee, and destroyer, between god and garbage, between church and trashcan, never landing anywhere stable. The arrangement of life itself is declared deranged, other people are a waste of time, and the question of whether the speaker is divine or refuse recurs without resolution, the bruised color in the title suggesting the answer is probably neither and somehow both.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~revelation-12":{"haiku":"Lawless youth gangs destroy their cities through violence, beating strangers without purpose or morality. They call themselves heroes while committing acts of barbarism and murder.","opus":"Marilyn Manson mounts a jeering vision of the apocalypse, feral youths assaulting strangers and pretending to divinity while a lawless town blazes and people too oblivious to see their own wickedness anoint themselves saviors. A menacing countdown toward an inevitable revelation drives the track to threats of slaughtering outsiders, the whole grim carnival mocking a contest Manson claims no one is actually playing.","sonnet":"Societal collapse is staged as a festival of mob self-delusion — roving groups of kids who call themselves heroes while burning things, leaders revealed as the very aliens they claim to monitor. The narrator addresses the chaos with sardonic permission, welcoming the blood-soaked reckoning as if hosting it.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~rock-is-dead":{"haiku":"Rock and shock have been commodified into soulless safety; authentic rebellion is dead, replaced by synthetic gods and medicated masses consuming dope and sex.","opus":"Manson's late-nineties diagnosis of a culture that consumes rebellion as product, sex and dope and manufactured shock sold back to people desperate to belong. The kicker is that God is in the TV now, a new false idol built to medicate the very audience it exploits.","sonnet":"A cynical diagnosis of rock culture's death — the genre has been gutted into a product serving sex, drugs, and conformist spectacle; God has been replaced by television; audiences consume fake rebellion while being sold manufactured shock, and everyone desperately performs belonging.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~s-aint":{"haiku":"Perfect rejection becomes the ultimate statement, the speaker refuses to be shaped into ideal forms while holding others accountable to their own impossible standards.","opus":"A vicious kiss-off spelling out contempt for a discarded lover, the speaker recasting himself as a work of art too perfect for them. The (s)aint pun, holding the S to become an AIN'T, flaunts an evil, superior ego built on being used and infected.","sonnet":"A declaration of monstrous self-perfection directed at an ex — the speaker was discarded but has transformed the rejection into armor, now presenting themselves as a flawless work of art that surpasses whatever standard was used to reject them, spelling out obscenities to claim a title that inverts sainthood.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~saturnalia":{"haiku":"Isolation and emptiness define the final void where hope disappears and survival persists through brutal commitment to holding another through darkness.","opus":"A vow to stay and hold someone through their darkest cold rather than become another wound, set against an apocalyptic sense of a doomed planet with no exit and only one way out of the tomb.","sonnet":"A dying world, emptied of exit and emergency, where the speaker promises to hold someone through every coldness and not become another bullet hole in the exit sign — the Saturnalia image anchoring a vision of collective extinction where \"blinded by blackness\" describes both the individual and the species facing its last sunset.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~say10":{"haiku":"Godlessness and transgression replace faith as the narrator inverts sacred symbols, claiming legend over fable through demonic charisma.","opus":"An anti-religious provocation punning God against Satan, mocking cash as a poor man's god and casting the singer as a horned, thorn-crowned legend daring the faithful to pray.","sonnet":"A provocation that rebrands Satan as the number ten — \"you say God, I say SAY 10\" — using dollar-sign snakes, gutted churches, and napalm goat horns to frame religious faith as just another power structure, the speaker declaring themselves a legend above both baptism and mythology.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~slave-only-dreams-to-be-king":{"haiku":"Enslaved people never dream of freedom but only of elevating themselves within oppressive systems; the song critiques how oppression warps human aspiration.","opus":"Manson on the psychology of the oppressed, whose imagination reaches only as far as becoming the master, never as far as freedom. Wrapped in fairy-tale and occult imagery, it's a grim read on how power reproduces itself through those who envy it.","sonnet":"A cynical meditation on ambition and subjugation: those at the bottom don't dream of freedom, only of being the one on top doing the exploiting — using occult imagery and a vaguely predatory relationship dynamic to make the point that the slave mentality perpetuates the very hierarchy it suffers under.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~slutgarden":{"haiku":"A narcissist uses love and sex as performance art, mistaking desire for devotion while worshiping porn as religion and confusing greed with faith.","opus":"A brutally cynical anti-love song where intimacy is a con, we always meaning me and sweet meaning dirty. Manson equates sex with a new religion learned from porn, mocking devotion itself, the church and steeple joined only in the act.","sonnet":"A cynical, sexually explicit song about purely carnal desire dressed up as intimacy — the narrator admits he says 'we' but means 'me,' calls what he wants dirty while calling it sweet, and frames sex as the only real religion he believes in, with no pretense of genuine connection.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~spade":{"haiku":"One speaker drains another's heart and leaves traces of poison in their veins. The song explores obsession, ego, and the lingering marks of a toxic love affair.","opus":"A love that has been emptied out and converted into a weapon: the beloved has drained the narrator's heart into a black spade, yet traces of him still run in her veins, binding them past the point of poison. Drenched in drug imagery, altar-of-ego blasphemy and a vow to stay together until death, it frames a codependent bond as mutual, deliberate self-destruction.","sonnet":"A post-breakup reckoning in which the narrator accuses a former lover of hollowing them out completely — draining their heart and turning it into a tool — while insisting some trace of them still lingers in the other person's blood.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~sweet-dreams-are-made-of-this":{"haiku":"Use and abuse become interchangeable desires; everyone pursues transgression and exploitation, the speaker embracing cruelty as mutual currency.","opus":"Manson drags the Eurythmics standard into predation, turning its catalogue of who uses and abuses whom into a menacing hunger to get under the skin and see what's there. The dream of everyone seeking something curdles into a threat.","sonnet":"A cover that leans into the original's cynical taxonomy of human desire — everyone wants something, whether to use, be used, abuse, or be abused; the narrator explicitly joins the predatory side, wanting to possess and know the other person fully.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~tainted-love":{"haiku":"Two lovers trapped in a destructive cycle flee each other repeatedly—the relationship has become corrupt, the speaker sacrifices everything but receives only tears.","opus":"Manson's cover strips the Soft Cell hit down to its cold core, a lover fleeing a relationship that only inflicts pain. His delivery makes the escape feel less like heartbreak and more like recoiling from something toxic disguised as devotion.","sonnet":"A cover of the Soft Cell track: a relationship that has curdled into pain — the narrator once ran toward this person, now runs away, unable to bear being touched by a love that has become poison.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~the-beautiful-people":{"haiku":"Society structures itself on shallow hierarchies that protect the strong by exploiting the weak, perpetuating cycles of cruelty and conformity.","opus":"Marilyn Manson delivers a ferocious satire of a beauty-and-power pecking order in which the weak exist only to make the strong look justified. Tying capitalism to old fascist instincts, it ridicules the worship of the glamorous elite and the cruel, discriminatory impulse that keeps them on their pedestal.","sonnet":"A savage critique of social hierarchy and fascist beauty standards — the strong justify themselves through the weak, capitalism and fascism are named as two sides of the same dehumanising coin, and the ugly truth beneath \"beautiful people\" is exposed.","src":"haiku","fable":"Manson's 1996 industrial stomp is an anthem engineered to expose the machinery of any anthem: it borrows the swagger of the dominant to reveal that beauty is only power wearing a nice face, that the weak exist as a footstool for the mighty, that a congregation measures its worth by how tall a spire it can loom behind. The second verse lands the real argument, drawing a straight line from market greed to a nostalgic strain of tyranny that promises to clear the wreckage away, so the sales pitch of consumer culture and the jackboot it invites get named as one continuous con. Then the song abandons argument entirely and collapses into the title chanted past the point of sense, the aspirational label curdling into a rally cry, until the sneering address to the listener stops being satire aimed outward and becomes the mob interrogating itself: the ones screaming along are the cruelty the record has spent four minutes diagnosing.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","artifice & performance"],"means":"the anthem's swagger engineered to expose what anthems are for","built":"the title chanted past sense curdles into the mob interrogating itself"},
"marilyn-manson~the-devil-beneath-my-feet":{"haiku":"Rejecting divine authority, the speaker claims their own power and darkness as an anchor beneath them, resisting control and death.","opus":"Manson trading a higher power for his own ascent, refusing to be looked down on by anyone, defiantly claiming damnation as footing rather than fear, with pills and darkness circling a self that won't be taken alive.","sonnet":"A rejection of God, authority, and anyone who looks down on him, with Manson declaring he'd rather have the devil at his heels than submit to a higher power — a song of defiant self-destruction and contempt for morality.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~the-dope-show":{"haiku":"Celebrity and consumer culture hollow authenticity; fame commodifies the soul, offering beauty and connection then discarding the used when novelty fades.","opus":"A sneer at Hollywood and fame as a narcissistic drug show where beautiful people get you high then leave you low. It is about the fickle love of an industry that adores you on the covers and forgets you the moment you're off them.","sonnet":"Fame culture as a narcotic: the pretty ones want to get you high then leave you low, love is conditional on cover placement, and everyone — cops, queers, celebrities — is a star in the dope show, a spectacle built on hollow desire and the knowledge that yesterday's face is already being replaced.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~the-fight-song":{"haiku":"Rejecting religious and societal control while asserting defiance; the speaker refuses submission and vows to scar the minds of their oppressors.","opus":"A defiant narrator refuses to be a slave to a nonexistent God or an indifferent world, promising to scar minds in retaliation for being ignored when good. From Manson's Holy Wood era of cultural provocation, the anthem skewers celebrity victimhood and mass numbness, coldly noting that a million deaths register only as statistics.","sonnet":"A defiant rejection of every institution — God, society, the entertainment industry — that profits from passivity or turns suffering into spectacle. The speaker refuses enslavement to either the divine or the indifferent, insisting on a stance of confrontation even when that stance earns only dismissal.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~the-mephistopheles-of-los-angeles":{"haiku":"A jaded figure rises above compromise, comparing themselves to great corruptors and liberators alike; they've been opened too much yet remain ready to face judgment, playing Faust in LA's moral theater.","opus":"A jaded confession of being unable to let anyone in after being exploited and laid bare too often, feeling hazy, isolated, and resigned to his own death in a shallow, image-drunk city. Casting himself as the tempter presiding over Los Angeles, always equal to the moment, he becomes the seductive, doomed spirit of Hollywood itself.","sonnet":"A self-portrait as the devil of a city that has opened and double-crossed him too many times — the narrator claims the Mephistophelean identity proudly, risen from heretic isolation, ready to meet his maker and outshine even Lazarus.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~the-reflecting-god":{"haiku":"Nihilism and absolute power corrupt absolutely; the absence of restraint and consequence transforms the wielder of authority into an instrument of destruction.","opus":"The climax of the Antichrist Superstar arc, the outcast seizing godhood only to find divinity is his own face staring back. It reads as a nihilist power fantasy, where without death life loses its reason and salvation was always a con.","sonnet":"A nihilistic vision in which the speaker confronts God and sees only himself, concluding that heaven and hell are fabrications — from this godlike vantage point, existence itself becomes a display of power culminating in annihilation.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~third-day-of-a-seven-day-binge":{"haiku":"A person in a destructive entanglement observes their partner's name dissolving from their lips as they wear out each other's existence, moving between victim and perpetrator.","opus":"A love affair scored to substance abuse, the partner's identity already dissolving only partway into a week of excess. Manson can't tell whether she flatters or destroys him, and settles on preferring to suffer under her than to actually be with her, borrowed misery worn like a costume.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship dissolving mid-binge — on the third night of seven days of excess, the narrator already senses the other person fading away, and concludes he would rather be their victim than stay with them.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~this-is-the-new-shit":{"haiku":"A satirical critique of pop culture's recycled provocations packaged as rebellion. Everything has been said before, yet people mindlessly consume and repeat empty slogans.","opus":"A cynical send-up of pop culture and shock entertainment, reducing music to interchangeable slogans of sex, violence and manufactured rebellion. It mocks audiences who demand the same recycled provocation packaged as something new.","sonnet":"A cynical take on mass media, pop culture, and entertainment, which the song presents as an endless recycling of the same provocations — sex, violence, rebellion, sentiment — fed to an audience that neither truly understands nor questions what it consumes.","src":"haiku","fable":"Manson itemizes his own shock-rock brand as a menu of interchangeable parts — rebellion, sex, a scare-quoted violence — that a customer can order by name, since the culture already exhausted its vocabulary and outrage now ships as a pre-fab slogan. The mock rally drills a crowd into obedient assent while the appetite floats free of any cause: asked if they understand, they yell no; asked if they still want it, yes anyway. His coy redactions — the withheld who and what and where — flaunt transgression without naming it, the point being that specifics stopped mattering once the transaction became the product. When the vaudeville pitch to amuse the audience dissolves into pure filler, the con is bare: this new rot is the old rot, artist and buyer complicit in a revolt that only cosplays as one.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","politics & society"],"means":"revolt repackaged as a pre-fab transaction with artist and buyer complicit","built":"mock rally drills the crowd into yelling no to comprehension and yes to appetite anyway"},
"marilyn-manson~use-your-fist-and-not-your-mouth":{"haiku":"A working-class anthem rejecting political correctness and unity, celebrating difference and defiance against mainstream conformity and false values.","opus":"Manson's mock-campaign rhetoric skewering American politics and conformity, an anthem for the outcast who refuses to pretend everyone can get along. The provocation is the point, difference weaponized against a smiling establishment.","sonnet":"A screed against cultural conformity and political correctness — Manson positions himself as an outsider running a campaign of pure antagonism, preferring action to speech, and celebrating difference over the pressure to assimilate.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~use-your-fist-not-your-mouth":{"haiku":"Rage articulates what words fail to express: violence becomes language when speech has been weaponized against you.","opus":"Manson at his most gleefully divisive, sneering at the notion that everyone can just get along, an anti-anthem for pain and difference that campaigns for hate and rejects conformity, insisting fists and refusal beat empty talk.","sonnet":"A song about rejecting speech and performance in favor of direct, physical confrontation — the speaker positions themselves against American political correctness and conformity, insisting on hatred as a genuine stance, and celebrating being different and irreconcilable.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~vodevil":{"haiku":"Defiant rejection of societal judgment and scorn; the band presents itself as weaponized resistance—five middle fingers rather than a musical instrument.","opus":"Manson throws back a list of insults hurled at him and refuses to play the scapegoat, dismissing his own act as neither music nor a proper band, just collective defiance. He turns on the press and audience that feed on him, insisting this is his actual life, not entertainment, and warning that he's more dangerous than a controlled performer.","sonnet":"A performer refuses every label the culture pins on him — predator, failure, product — insisting instead that the act itself is his life, not a show. Directed at a media ecosystem that packages and discards artists, the narrator declares himself a grenade rather than a puppet, entertainment that cannot be defused.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~warship-my-wreck":{"haiku":"A person refuses to follow rules and embraces destruction, commanding their partner like a paper doll. Warfare replaces love as scars accumulate from self-wrecking.","opus":"Love recast as domination and mutual ruin, the partner treated as something disposable to shape at will, with any failure to fully merge treated as grounds for annihilation. The title's demand that his own wreckage be revered captures a devotion that measures itself in damage, injury offered as proof of love.","sonnet":"A relationship as total war — the narrator refuses to be controlled, rejects the noose, and launches kamikaze into the other person's soul; what remains is wreckage to be worshipped rather than repaired, as scars and bruises become the truest record of what happened.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~we-know-where-you-fucking-live":{"haiku":"A violent threat articulates willingness to destroy and burn down society and individuals who resist, celebrating destructive power and the sound of weaponized violence.","opus":"Marilyn Manson, the shock-rock provocateur, points a home-invasion threat at bourgeois complacency. Documenting the assault as it unfolds, the narrators promise to torch the place until the body's beyond recognition, bedding down among horrors and treating the pavement as raw flesh. Relishing the noise of gunfire, the track weaponizes menace against comfortable people watching from a safe distance.","sonnet":"A collective menace addressed to an unnamed target — possibly respectable society, possibly a specific adversary — declares that anonymity and distance offer no real protection. The world stripped of its comforting surfaces reveals an appetite for destruction beneath, and the threat is total: your location is known, your comfort will burn, what remains will be unrecognisable.","src":"haiku"},
"marilyn-manson~wormboy":{"haiku":"Emptiness and misguided belief systems transform the self until transcendence arrives too late. The singer realizes they've evolved but only in hindsight, after suffering.","opus":"Manson recasting damnation as metamorphosis, the reviled worm sprouting wings by embracing everything they accused him of being. It weaponizes rejection: you become exactly the monster they named, and find heaven was the real hell.","sonnet":"A figure who believed himself defined by external labels and hatred discovers those labels were accurate all along — the worm gets his wings — and the promised heaven turns out to feel indistinguishable from hell.","src":"haiku"},
"marina~facts-in-files":{"opus":"A satirical parable of authority suppressing an inconvenient truth, a powerful figure mocking and gaslighting someone who reports rain from the sky, citing manipulated charts to call it absurd and threatening they'll be the laughingstock, warning against upsetting those in power.","sonnet":"About institutional suppression of inconvenient truth — a whistleblower or truth-teller is mocked and silenced by someone wielding charts and official data to deny obvious reality, with a Russian-language section suggesting this silencing happens even when people are hungry and desperate for a different narrative.","src":"sonnet"},
"marina~join":{"haiku":"Elites hide inequality and mock the suffering masses while pretending concern; the song exposes how choice itself is manipulation and joining any system is submission. It portrays systematic deception and complicity.","opus":"Marina in her dystopian mode, staging a corrupt elite that gorges itself while insisting the powerless were always complicit. The twist turns rebellion into a trap, exposing how systems co-opt dissent by making surrender feel like the only sane choice.","sonnet":"A sharp political allegory — elites speaking cynically about the masses, dismissing public awareness as irrelevant, before the mask slips completely and the ruling voice admits it loves watching people bleed and that joining them leads only to death.","src":"haiku"},
"marina~strangelove":{"haiku":"A turbulent love thrives on mutual pain and contradiction—both partners oscillate between need, sin, and the strange highs and lows that bind them.","opus":"Marina, the Welsh pop artist formerly known as Marina and the Diamonds, charts love's wild swings over throbbing synths. She frames romance as a relationship of trading hurt back and forth, confessing to betrayals and wandering yet promising the payoff is worth it, weighing her failings against her power to make someone happy.","sonnet":"A relationship navigated through extremes — straying, sinning, disappearing — is offered not as an apology but as a frank contract. The love is strange because it involves pain given and returned, yet the narrator insists she will always make it worthwhile, always make the heart smile.","src":"haiku"},
"markus-schukowski~what-it-feels-like-zardonic-remix":{"haiku":"Breaking free from past mistakes by choosing change, even if the path ahead remains uncertain.","opus":"Breaking free from indecision and past mistakes, framing life as a show you refuse to be held back in, wanting to feel what fighting darkness with light actually feels like. It's a bid for change and a second chance.","sonnet":"About the desire to break free from a stuck, failed past and choose liberation — the narrator knows they've messed up, but wants to know what it feels like to fight darkness with light, to break chains, to take the life they're living seriously; it's a rallying cry from someone who still believes change is possible.","src":"haiku"},
"marmozets~hit-the-wave":{"haiku":"A person fears meaninglessness and asks to be cleansed and transformed; they realize people are taken for granted until gone, and love is lost through negligence.","opus":"The British band Marmozets, fronted by Becca Macintyre, soften their post-hardcore edge into a plea for meaning. Terrified of amounting to nothing, the narrator asks to be cleansed and remade into something worthwhile. The reflective verses urge people to voice their love while there's still time, mourning how we take everyone for granted and let petty things destroy what matters.","sonnet":"Confronting the fear of insignificance, the narrator asks to be pulled under and remade into something that matters. Woven through is a quieter lesson about human connection deferred too long — the tendency to realise what someone meant only after the chance to say so has already closed.","src":"haiku"},
"marmozets~is-it-horrible":{"haiku":"Concern for a friend struggling with alcoholism and self-destruction, offering support while questioning whether they believe they're worth saving.","opus":"A narrator tends to a friend wrecked by a night of blackout drinking, pressing whether they can remember and insisting, through the wreckage, that they're worth fighting for. Marmozets pair their angular British rock with genuine tenderness, letting the anxious refrain of whether it's horrible give way to reassurance that someone has their back.","sonnet":"A friend watches someone destroy themselves with drink, waking shattered and unable to remember the night before, and refuses to look away. The recurring question of whether this is bearable is answered not with judgement but with a steady, unglamorous loyalty.","src":"haiku"},
"marmozets~like-last-night":{"haiku":"Desperation for transcendence clashes with doomsday thinking; the subject seeks return to ecstatic feeling.","opus":"A defiant refusal of doomsday gloom in favor of chasing the euphoria of one good night and the body of someone desired. Impatient and hungry for feeling, the singer would rather dance and go again than give up on love.","sonnet":"A speaker desperate to recapture the physical and emotional intensity of the night before — \"wrap my body round you like last night\" — pushing back against doomsday-thinking and insisting, against all doubt, that they are not giving up and are just getting started.","src":"haiku"},
"maroon-5~what-lovers-do-feat-sza":{"haiku":"Uncertainty poisons intimacy as the speaker demands proof of love while the other resists commitment, neither willing to risk fully.","opus":"A flirty ultimatum aimed at someone stuck between casual and committed, pressing them to decide before the game gets old. Its charm is the impatience underneath, two grown people who should know better still stalling on the obvious.","sonnet":"An impatient courtship where the speaker presses for a clear declaration of love and commitment, tired of ambiguity and ready to stop playing games now that both parties are old enough to know what they want. The song swings between urgency and tender longing.","src":"haiku"},
"marquise~acordei-mal":{"haiku":"Waking into tolerable melancholy and searching for place within the world, resignation mixed with faint desire for belonging.","opus":"A Portuguese portrait of a low, downcast morning—'today I woke up badly,' shivering a name, hearing seagulls, staring at the ground searching for a reason. The tender refrain reassures another: 'don't be sad, I tolerate you, I even want you,' resolving to stop being downhearted and find where he fits.","sonnet":"Waking badly and searching the floor for a reason to continue — hearing seagulls, muttering a name, telling someone not to be sad while admitting 'I even want you' through clenched teeth. Today is the day to stop hanging one's head and find where one fits, even if the morning started wrong.","src":"haiku"},
"marshmello~alone":{"haiku":"Profound isolation meets homesickness; the return journey maps distance as untraversable.","opus":"A wistful, melodic EDM anthem of isolation and longing, its handful of words circling the ache of feeling adrift and searching for the route back to someone. Marshmello pairs the loneliness with an uplifting drop, the signature bittersweet formula. It reaches for the universal sense of displacement, home being a person rather than a place.","sonnet":"A speaker is consumed by isolation and a desperate longing for belonging, framing the search for home as a search for a specific person — \"trying to find my way back home to you\" repeated as the only compass.","src":"haiku"},
"marshmello~alone-streex-remake":{"haiku":"A person feels alone and homeless, trying to find their way back to someone who feels like home.","opus":"A simple, aching loop about isolation, the narrator so cut off that no place feels safe while he tries to reach one particular person. This reworks the breakthrough single by masked EDM producer Marshmello, its future-bass brightness and chopped vocals carrying the loneliness into a shimmering, danceable release built around a single yearning refrain.","sonnet":"Stripped down to a single feeling with nowhere to go — the speaker is alone, nothing feels like home, and every line is a variation of searching for the way back to one specific person. There is no story here beyond the ache itself, which is the song's whole point: the simplest loneliness, repeated until it becomes a kind of mantra.","src":"haiku"},
"martha-reeves-the-vandellas~nowhere-to-run":{"haiku":"Inescapable longing traps the narrator despite knowing the lover's harm; every location and memory reinforces emotional capture and defeat.","opus":"A Motown desperation classic about being trapped by a love you know is bad for you and cannot escape. The driving beat mirrors the panic: the person she'd flee has become part of her, so there's no direction that leads away.","sonnet":"A woman is trapped by a love she knows is bad for her — she cannot escape it because it has become part of her body and mind, infiltrating her sleep and her mirror, leaving her with no exit even though she understands exactly how destructive he is.","src":"haiku"},
"martin-garrix-troye-sivan~there-for-you":{"haiku":"Mutual dependence and support sustain through hardship; genuine connection demands reciprocal presence, not just one-sided devotion.","opus":"A promise of support to someone drowning in a bad year, with the crucial condition that devotion has to run both ways. It lands because it refuses to be pure selflessness, naming the exhaustion of always being the one who shows up.","sonnet":"Two people in different kinds of pain — alienation, exhaustion, the toll of time apart — make a reciprocal promise to show up for each other in moments of crisis, naming that love requires presence and effort from both sides. The desperation to hold on is framed as a race just to keep the connection alive.","src":"haiku"},
"martin-garrix~breakaway":{"haiku":"Disillusionment chases false lights until one person becomes the refuge that breaks the cycle of seeking.","opus":"Someone chasing empty distractions until a person becomes the exit route from his own spiraling mind. Gratitude reframes love as rescue, the promised escape whenever he needs one.","sonnet":"A person who had been chasing empty things and losing themselves finds clarity through someone else who becomes their escape and anchor — the lyrical content is minimal, essentially one declaration that this person is their breakaway.","src":"haiku"},
"martin-garrix~summer-days-feat-macklemore-patrick-stump-of-fall-out-boy":{"haiku":"A summer romance awakens recognition of another person as 'the one'; the lovers attempt to preserve an intimate moment outside time.","opus":"Martin Garrix, the Dutch EDM star, joins Macklemore and Fall Out Boy's Patrick Stump for a breezy ode to summer infatuation. Struck the moment he sees her, the narrator is convinced she's the one, and the song casts two damaged people finding each other as a bright, temporary escape.","sonnet":"A summer morning crystallizes into the recognition of someone who could be the one — unaware of her own appeal, effortless in the morning light. The feeling escalates into island fantasies and convertible drives, two imperfect people finding completeness in each other under open sky.","src":"haiku"},
"martin-garrix~virus-how-about-now":{"haiku":"Mortality as imperative; unsafe roads and uncertain futures demand we stop waiting.","opus":"A propulsive festival track about seizing the moment when everything feels precarious, each hour possibly the last and every road unsafe. Its refrain demands acting now instead of waiting, chasing dreams recklessly and trusting you'll come through, turning uncertainty into fuel for living without hesitation.","sonnet":"Urgency as love language: with every hour possibly the last and every road unsafe, the only answer is to stop waiting and act now — the question how about now repeated like a pulse, impatient with hesitation and committed to doing whatever is dreamed.","src":"haiku"},
"marvin-gaye~this-love-starved-heart-of-mine-it-s-killing-me-1994-love-starved-heart-version-mono":{"haiku":"Abandonment and emotional starvation compound into lethal anguish; life becomes useless without the beloved's nourishment.","opus":"A shelved Motown-era plea from Marvin Gaye's most tortured register, unreleased for decades and later exhumed. A man reduced to pleading after love walks out, pride thrown into the dirt, his hunger for a woman who left curdling into something that feels physically fatal. Beneath the soul polish sits raw abandonment and the humiliation of begging.","sonnet":"Abandoned and desperate, the singer catalogues the physical cost of losing love: his heart caves under the weight, the pain is too strong, and having thrown his pride in the dirt he has nothing left but to beg the door to stay open — \"love starved heart of mine, killing me\" is both diagnosis and plea.","src":"haiku"},
"marylin-manson~this-is-the-new-shit":{"haiku":"Repetition and hollow novelty replace genuine innovation, with society cycling through repackaged provocations designed to titillate and distract.","opus":"A satirical indictment of manufactured rebellion and consumer spectacle, listing sex, violence, and slogans as interchangeable product you can order by name. Manson mocks an audience demanding to be entertained by the same recycled shock, the new shit being nothing new at all.","sonnet":"A cynical broadside against the entertainment industry's recycled formulas — sex, rebellion, and violence repackaged endlessly for a complicit audience that keeps consuming it, with Manson implicating himself in the cycle while calling it all out.","src":"haiku"},
"maserati~monoliths":{"haiku":"A speaker withdraws from the world, stripped of metaphysical anchors, hoping someday to rejoin life. They feel trapped in their own mind while others move on without them.","opus":"A near-instrumental post-rock piece where the sparse words sketch dissociation and self-erasure, a narrator adrift outside himself endlessly promising he'll eventually come around. The mantra outlasts the meaning on purpose.","sonnet":"A person alone in a room while the world moves around and past them — crews leaving, sounds fading down the hall — wishing they could be somewhere outside, and looping the hope that one of these days they'll finally come around.","src":"haiku"},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~a-11075xn":{"haiku":"Memory fragments prevent forward movement; yesterday's attachments blur yesterday's images, making recollection impossible.","src":"haiku","web":"Fragmented grief and collective sorrow: shattered images, people crying everywhere, an inability to remember the right feelings or a song once cherished, closing with a resolve to quietly record one's conflicted inner thoughts before they disappear."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~a-16k1e33":{"haiku":"Destruction enables renewal; endings and beginnings interconnect as breaking things down allows new growth.","opus":"Mass of the Fermenting Dregs, the Kobe power trio, wrap heavy noise-pop around plainspoken resolve, and this track pushes toward breaking what has been built to take the long way around. The path ahead will take the color of whoever walks it, so every goodbye doubles as a hello. The band's churning momentum makes endings sound like the mechanism by which beginnings actually start.","sonnet":"An encouragement to break what has been built and take the long way around, because the path ahead will take on the color of whoever walks it — every goodbye is also a hello, and endings are simply how beginnings start.","src":"haiku"},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~a-1fjgut7":{"haiku":"Fleeting shared moments fade as quickly as the taste of soda; neither person retains memories of time together.","opus":"The trio's melodic haze suits this half-forgotten summer sensation, a girl with no face and a shared recognition that nothing between them really differs. Mass of the Fermenting Dregs let the guitars blur the memory even as they render it, so the bittersweet certainty arrives intact: today, like a half-drunk glass of soda, will be forgotten the moment it passes. The forgetting is the feeling.","sonnet":"A fleeting, half-forgotten summer sensation — a girl with no face, a shared recognition that nothing between us really differs, and the bittersweet certainty that today, like a half-drunk glass of soda, will be forgotten immediately.","src":"haiku"},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~a-1h8zvv1":{"haiku":"Toxic absorption in a relationship where one consumes the other's tears; inability to say goodbye despite exhaustion.","opus":"The band's abrasive tenderness gives this trapped-relationship song its grind, intimacy performed as labor while sweet memories pile up too heavy to carry. Mass of the Fermenting Dregs push the narrator toward wanting to blame someone and run, lying and losing keys in hope of being caught and scolded. The whole distorted swell reduces to one small wish, that today stay today until morning finally comes.","sonnet":"A woman trapped in a sexual and emotional relationship she can't escape, performing routine intimacy as a kind of grinding labor while hoarding sweet memories she can no longer carry, wanting to blame someone else and run, lying and losing keys hoping to be caught and scolded, just wanting today to stay today until morning comes.","src":"haiku"},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~a-1i3kj97":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"Mass of the Fermenting Dregs aim their noise-pop outward here, a call to the living to hold onto colors they have lost and leave hesitation behind. The wind is blowing, the sky already tomorrow's color, and the days gone by must be spun together and loved. The band's forward drive keeps pushing even as the horizon stays out of reach, urgency itself standing in for arrival.","sonnet":"A call to the living to hold on to colors they've lost and leave hesitation behind — the wind is blowing, the sky is tomorrow's color, and the days that have already gone must be spun together and loved, even as the horizon stays out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~a-1lqlhd8":{"haiku":"Haunted by acceleration toward nowhere; the person you thought you were watching turns out to have been your own reflection.","src":"haiku","web":"A song about being swept along by a relentless beat through a muddied, alienated cityscape until the narrator discovers, with horror, that they have been dyed in a cruel color by the very environment they were observing."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~a-2yzto8":{"haiku":"Growing disconnection between two people; perspectives and bodies drift unsynched despite attempts to reconcile.","opus":"The trio's dissonant textures literalize this study of two people slipping out of alignment, bodies and perceptions no longer synced. Mass of the Fermenting Dregs dissolve black and white, right and left, into things that no longer matter, while yesterday and today test and search for each other across the widening gap. The drift is rendered as sound, reconciliation attempted and never quite landing.","sonnet":"Two people whose bodies and perceptions keep slipping out of alignment with each other — neither black nor white, neither right nor left matters — as yesterday and today test and search for each other in the gap between them.","src":"haiku"},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~a-bc7n3g":{"haiku":"Grasping fleeting moments in fractional time; calling across solitude toward a colorful world just beyond reach.","src":"haiku","web":"A song about attention and connection: the narrator calls out to another person to prevent loneliness, contrasting what can be seen with what we choose to see, insisting the vivid multicolored world is not invisible but simply unnoticed."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~aoi":{"src":"web","web":"A meditation on unrequited longing and the fragility of love: the narrator walks alone on a rusty sidewalk through blue, dark, orange days, chasing something that keeps distancing itself, and finds that tender affection shatters the moment it is grasped."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~aoi-koi-daidaiiro-no-hi":{"haiku":"Waiting erodes the self into fragmentation: love pursued and held becomes destruction, leaving only colors and absence where wholeness once waited.","opus":"Chasing a voice and a day that keep receding, holding something so intensely it shatters. It settles into wanting only to stay quietly beside someone even as all color drains away.","sonnet":"A Japanese song about love so intense it destroys — chasing someone who keeps receding, longing so desperately that the act of finally holding them breaks everything, leaving only grief and a solitary walk through fading color.","src":"haiku"},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~asahinagu":{"src":"web","web":"A retrospective on a faded romance: the narrator cannot remember their former lover's name, dwells on broken promises and unrealized desires, cries alone, and resigns themselves to the fact that nothing goes the way they want."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~delusionalism":{"src":"web","web":"A portrait of emotional breakdown and apathy: the narrator declares there is no sense in engaging or adjusting, cycles through indifference and inner turbulence, and arrives at a resigned acceptance that hope and reality have become indistinguishable."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~dramatic":{"src":"web","web":"A resigned farewell to a shared past: memories fade as light carries away the narrator's own shadow, and the song pleads with someone to abandon their former selves together, accepting that fate has made return impossible."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~i-f-a-surfer":{"src":"web","web":"A sensory wash of perpetual motion and submersion — clapping hands, a voice that cleans the day, and the refrain of swimming forever — evoking the dazed, floating euphoria of losing oneself completely to sound and water."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~kakuiumono":{"src":"web","web":"A study in chronic alienation: surrounded by noise and people yet perpetually out of sync, the narrator pretends not to hear, repeats the same wrong answers, and watches as the self dissolves to nothingness with nowhere left to go."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~kono-speed-no-saki-e":{"src":"web","web":"A song of forward momentum and release: scenes pass by as the narrator urges someone to run, throw away hesitation and aching memories, say goodbyes gently, and push ahead into the blur of speed — with a final note of gratitude."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~nan-nan":{"haiku":"Unnamed love caught between presence and absence; boundaries blurring daily between affection and lament.","src":"haiku","web":"An introspective struggle with romantic ambivalence: the narrator experiences contradictory impulses — wanting closeness yet not — as love and jealousy accumulate day by day, leaving them in a state of frustrated, bewildered tension."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~new-order":{"src":"web","web":"A portrait of emotional stagnation between two people: the speaker sent someone away on a distant afternoon and now they stand side by side yet murky and unchanged, with a repeated warning — better not to touch, better to leave it — conveying fear of reopening old wounds."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~she-is-inside":{"src":"web","web":"The entire lyrical content is the repeated phrase \"she is inside, he is outside\" — a stark, chant-like invocation of two people separated by a threshold, interior versus exterior, with no further narrative elaboration."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~skabetty":{"haiku":"Holding scorching feelings and thirst for color; rhythmic repetition of departure and staying, waving goodbye to someone who keeps returning.","opus":"Cradling a nearly scorched feeling and a thirst for colour, the narrator urges a leap out into the street where everything will be fine. A departing figure keeps saying they won't return, met with a plea to stay near. The Japanese indie trio wraps this ache of clinging and letting go in a childlike scale, ending on a small, repeated wave goodbye.","sonnet":"A thin, weeping figure from yesterday threatens never to return, while the speaker pleads for closeness. Feeling both scorched with longing and drained of color, they wave a quiet goodbye as someone slips softly away — the parting tender and unresolved rather than dramatic.","src":"haiku","web":"A melancholy farewell to someone who will not return and will not stay close: the narrator holds burning, colorful feelings in both hands, reaches toward surreal escapes like jumping on time, and ends with a quiet wave goodbye."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~uta-wo-utaeba":{"src":"web","web":"A quiet act of coping with loss: the narrator notices the world has shifted in some intangible way, vows never to forget a loved one's name, and finds modest solace in the belief that singing a heartfelt song might ease the pain a little."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~world-is-yours":{"haiku":"Everyone haunted by yesterday forgets to see forward; exchanging fear for love's strength in shared understanding.","src":"haiku","web":"An encouragement to stop dwelling in the past: acknowledging how people raise their hands to fading days and find it sad, the song urges the listener to look forward, take someone's hand, and trust that the future will mirror their hopes."},
"mass-of-the-fermenting-dregs~x":{"haiku":"The speaker captures fleeting moments and memories of connection in a zero-comma world; calling out to someone so they won't be alone, amid colorful but uncertain futures.","opus":"A Japanese indie-rock rush that treats fleeting, ordinary days as something worth clutching. The narrator calls out to a companion, terrified of ending up alone, sensing a wider unseen world just out of sight. Held hands and a felt heartbeat become proof against solitude, the whole thing straining toward a hope that connection might actually last.","sonnet":"A Japanese song about capturing fleeting everyday moments and the fear of losing connection — the narrator calls out to a companion both loudly and quietly across a colorful world just beyond their sight, terrified of being left alone.","src":"haiku"},
"massface~black-water":{"opus":"A motivational anthem aimed at someone sinking into despair, urging them to break the surface toward hope, shed their false front, and treat survival itself as a rebirth. The message is that even a weakened person still has enough to fight for, and that they don't have to do it in isolation.","sonnet":"An encouragement to someone drowning in depression — the soul is deep in black water but the song urges breaking through to light, insisting that emotion itself can be the fire to follow, and that even a small spark can outshine stars.","src":"sonnet"},
"massive-attack~a-prayer-for-england":{"haiku":"A prayer for England's murdered children, asking forgiveness for abandoning faith and teachers who've so badly represented God that few can see truth.","opus":"A grief-stricken prayer to Jah over the killing and abduction of England's young, pleading that no more of them die and no more desperate searches come to nothing. It turns on those whose faith leads them to harm children and on the supposed guides who so misrepresent the divine that almost no one can recognize it, begging for forgiveness and greater love.","sonnet":"A prayer for England's children who have witnessed or been taken by obscene violence — the song calls on God to stop the killing, forgives the community for failing its children, and indicts religious teachers whose bad representation of love has produced murderous belief.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~angel":{"fable":"Horace Andy's reggae vocal calls a woman a heavenly deliverer, then quietly names her a predator who cancels out any suitor caught in her line of sight, so the devotion and the danger are the same person. The refrain then abandons words for a single repeated declaration, drilled past reassurance into obsession, while the track's slow-detonating bassline and gathering guitar turn that mantra into stalking dread rather than comfort. What sounds like a love song is really the sound of surrender to something that will consume you, the salvation and the shadow she descends from collapsed into one figure you cannot stop chanting toward.","fableDeep":"The whole architecture is a bait-and-switch across two tiny verses. The opening is pure hymn — 'you are my angel' and 'to bring me love' set up a gift descending from heaven — then the perspective jerks from second person to third: 'she's on the dark side', and the gift becomes a weapon that will 'neutralize every man in sight'. That pronoun shift is the song's only real event; the beloved stops being addressed and starts being warned about. What follows is no resolution but a single phrase, love you, repeated dozens of times, and repetition does the interpretive work: said once it is tenderness, said twenty times without variation it is compulsion, a man hypnotized by the thing he just told us is dangerous. The clipped return of the angel verse, cut off before its promise can complete, quietly withdraws it. It is about devotion as a trance you keep chanting precisely because its object could destroy you.","haiku":"A dark angel descends to bring love and captivation; her power neutralizes all she encounters as the speaker repeats his devotion.","opus":"Trip-hop dread built on a single hypnotic devotion, love that feels less like tenderness than possession or menace. The looping repetition makes adoration sound like obsession closing in, the beloved both salvation and dark force.","sonnet":"A sparse, hypnotic portrait of a woman who is at once an angel and a predator — she neutralizes every man she encounters, and the speaker is utterly devoted to her despite or because of this.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fnote":"What the words alone cannot show: the vocal is Horace Andy revisiting his own You Are My Angel, a 1970s lovers-rock devotional re-sung into Mezzanine's dread — the tenderness is literally his past being dragged into the dark.","themes":["love & desire","madness & the mind"],"means":"surrender to a devotion that promises to consume its worshipper","built":"pronoun shift from you to she turns beloved into a warning"},
"massive-attack~antistar":{"haiku":"Someone in emotional pain requests numbness and oblivion, attracted to another through narcosis and self-harm, losing identity in the other's presence and gaze.","opus":"A bleak intimacy fused with addiction, the speaker begging a lover to numb his wounds like a drug and shoot him with details of pain; needle imagery and the line about looking great in bloodstains frame love as narcosis and mutual damage.","sonnet":"About a damaged need for intimacy and numbness — the narrator asks to be healed through physical closeness while being blinded and destabilized by someone's celebrity or fame, their iconography becoming a source of both desire and dissociation.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~antistar-includes-hidden-track-lp4":{"haiku":"Drowning in anesthetized desire and visual consumption, the speaker seeks obliteration through narcosis while searching obsessively for human connection beneath layers of numb imagery.","opus":"Closing Massive Attack's 100th Window, this drifts through narcotic dependence and wounded intimacy, sedation offered as tenderness. The narrator begs to be numbed while searching obsessively for someone under every stone, iconography and flashbulbs disorienting him. Trip-hop's paranoia distilled to a whisper: love, addiction and self-erasure blurring until comfort and damage become indistinguishable.","sonnet":"A request for obliteration delivered as desire: lick my wounds, kill the pain, grant me intimacy, blind me with flash bulbs — the narcosis being sought is both chemical and erotic, the other person a star whose iconography \"fucks with me\" and who looks great in bloodstains. The head between the knees and the needle set to zero suggest the speaker wants to be reset to nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~atlas-air":{"haiku":"A prisoner spends three thousand days in a cell, their heart consumed and internals feasted upon; greed and control cage the soul in terminal detention.","opus":"Trip-hop dread told from inside extraordinary rendition, a prisoner flown to a secret site and worked over with clinical menace, a captive with only his bondage left to surrender. The Bristol group channels post-9/11 anxiety about torture and disappearance into a slow, suffocating groove.","sonnet":"A dense, surreal song that weaves together images of coerced masculinity, incarceration, physical self-sacrifice, and corporate air travel — the narrator has been forced into performing a thuggish role while bleeding out internally, framing modern existence as a kind of endless detention.","src":"haiku","fable":"3D fuses two figures who ought to be opposites — the hooded operative boarding a private aircraft and the prisoner rotting on some redacted patch of ground — until the whole song becomes a single indictment of the machinery that manufactures both. The recurring bargain about taking another spin in comfort, improving the arrangements with no one watching, is the language of extraordinary rendition dressed as a business deal: torture outsourced, deniable, transactional, its scheduled flight named by its aircraft number. That is why the martyr's boast about a generous heart offered up to be devoured curdles as it repeats across the choruses — the willingness to bleed for a cause becomes indistinguishable from being farmed for someone else's appetite, the same body the plane hauls away and the medic keeps barely alive only to extract more. By the final passage, where a man counts years underground while the powerful call his suffering a dream and issue menaces from a jet at dusk, the two lives have collapsed into one closed circuit of profit and pain; the meaning lives precisely in that refusal to let victim and executioner stay separate.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"torturer and prisoner folded into one closed circuit","built":"extraordinary rendition reworded as a business itinerary"},
"massive-attack~babel":{"haiku":"A romantic betrayal spirals into obsession and heartbreak; the narrator hallucinates a recovery impossible as truth and dismissal leave only loss.","opus":"Massive Attack, the Bristol trip-hop pioneers, drift through the ruins of a failed romance, naming it a private tower of confusion and crossed signals. A relationship ends as a partner slips away, someone hasty to burn and slow to grow, left alone. Amid dizzying, headlong unraveling, the narrator admits he should have gambled on honesty instead of bravado and fled from his own mind.","sonnet":"A relationship crashes in the language of Babel — mutual unintelligibility, a woman who kissed and dismissed, a man who kept staying even as she wept. Hallucination and acceleration mark the collapse, and the narrator absorbs the wreckage into something that eventually led, inexplicably, to love.","src":"haiku","fable":"Martina Topley-Bird narrates a breakup as a confusion of tongues — the biblical Babel, where a shared language collapses and two people who once understood each other are suddenly speaking past one another. The trick of the song is its shifting address: she catalogues a man's failures and small decencies in the second person, hovering somewhere between accuser and confidante, until the chorus tips its hand and she turns out to be the woman he left the other for. That late swerve reframes everything — the tender inventory of how he stayed even as it ended reads less as neutral testimony than as a rival's private reckoning, and the closing wish to have chosen honesty over risk lands as her own guilt rather than his. Set to Massive Attack's smeared, weightless 3D-era production, the verbs pile up as pure verbs — a blur of grasping and fracturing with no object attached — so the emotional wreckage arrives as motion without a destination, which is exactly the point about a love that had run out of anywhere to arrive.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","love & desire"],"means":"a rival's private reckoning dressed as neutral testimony","built":"late-song swerve in address reframes every prior line"},
"massive-attack~better-things":{"haiku":"A partner claiming they need freedom and magic in a relationship, but the speaker sees it as excuses to escape responsibility and commitment.","opus":"Refusing to sink into someone else's despair, the narrator dismisses a partner's plea for liberation as freedom stripped of affection. Told the spark has faded, he retorts that he never claimed to conjure it. Massive Attack wrap Tracey Thorn's ache in their Bristol trip-hop calm, letting a bruised assurance of brighter days ahead carry the emotional weight.","sonnet":"One partner cuts through the other's self-justifications with blunt clarity — calling out the pretense of needing freedom as a convenient excuse for wanting out. Despite the sting of rejection, an insistence that brighter days still lie ahead refuses to collapse into bitterness.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~black-milk":{"fable":"Elizabeth Fraser sings almost entirely in fragments of communion and consumption—the vocabulary of feeding, of being eaten and refusing to be food—but folds it into devotional address, worship offered up to God and to the Mother rather than to a lover. That collision is the whole point: erotic hunger and sacramental surrender share one grammar here, so that giving yourself to be devoured and giving yourself to be adored become impossible to separate. Set inside Mezzanine's dread and Fraser's own famously syllable-first, sense-resistant writing, the words never resolve into a scene; they hover as a liturgy of dissolving boundaries, where the only stable proposition is that nothing exists here except love, and love is indistinguishable from being consumed.","fableDeep":"The opening couplet draws a boundary — 'you're not my eater / I'm not your food' — refusing a predatory economy of love, and then, a few lines later, willingly collapses it: 'eat me in the space / within my heart' re-offers the consumption just refused, but relocated inward and made consensual, appetite converted into invitation. The refrain routes desire through two divinities at once, 'love you for God / love you for the Mother', paternal and maternal, so the beloved becomes an occasion for worship rather than its object. The theology compresses into an ultimatum: 'mother fountain / or live or not at all' — nourishment from the maternal source is not one option among many, it is the condition of living. The 'sunken chapel' places the whole sacrament underwater, hidden, half-drowned, level and quiet. The close dissolves every prior distinction — eater, food, God, Mother — into one substance: all there is to love, only love. It is about surrender to a love so total that the only honest descriptions left are eating and prayer.","haiku":"A spiritual meditation on love and nurturing, invoking divine and maternal forces while rejecting consumption and possessiveness in relationships.","opus":"A whispered trip-hop devotional where romantic craving blurs into something close to religious worship, the beloved figured as both nourishment and a sacred source. Elizabeth Fraser's vocal makes dependence sound like prayer, framing the choice as loving completely or ceasing to exist.","sonnet":"A sparse, devotional piece about sacred nourishment and love — the imagery circles around a maternal divine (Mother, fountain) as the source of all love, offered from within the heart's own space.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"erotic hunger and sacramental surrender sharing one grammar","built":"consumption refused in the opening couplet is willingly re-offered lines later"},
"massive-attack~butterfly-caught":{"haiku":"Vulnerable capture and fragile beauty—weightless things, honeysuckle, pearly skin—are held by force in a supplicant's posture.","opus":"A slow, ominous trip-hop study of adoration curdling into control. The lover is observed and admired the way a collector admires a specimen, and Massive Attack let that tenderness feel predatory, treating fascination with someone fragile as a form of ownership.","sonnet":"A fragmented, sensory portrait of physical vulnerability and desire — a figure observed in states of exposure, their darkened skin and open lips, radiating both fragility and an implicit demand to keep performing composure.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~daydreaming":{"haiku":"The speaker observes life around them while sharing hip-hop observations on staying positive and resisting the rat race and artificial conflicts.","opus":"An early Massive Attack manifesto, this drifts between a quiet observer's floating reverie and Tricky's braggadocio, stacking the collective's name, their sound systems and headphones into a portrait of the Bristol crew's identity. Positivity wipes out the negative; distance is kept from wiseguys and trouble. Beneath the swagger sits a lonely admission of loving the one who's missed.","sonnet":"An MC floats above the concrete city on the buoyancy of rhythm and rhyme, observing without being caught in the grind. Street-level noise and danger exist, but the speaker stays airborne through music — daydreaming as a deliberate, sustainable form of survival and self-possession.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~dissolved-girl":{"fable":"A song about the anesthetized inertia of a relationship the singer knows she should walk out of and doesn't, sung in the deadened present tense of someone who has run this exact loop too many times to feel it anymore. On Mezzanine, Massive Attack drape that resignation in narcotic guitar and dread, so the flat admissions land less as confession than as symptom: the self isn't broken so much as slowly disappearing, worn down to a shape that stays out of habit rather than need. The tell is the dismissal of passion as beside the point, which reads as protective numbness rather than wisdom, and the closing turn where even performing feeling wouldn't cure the wanting underneath it. The title names the endpoint the loop is heading toward, a person thinning out of her own life.","fableDeep":"Each verse opens with a single flat word — shame, say, fade — then echoes it, the language dissolving into its own repetitions the way she does. 'I think I kinda lost myself again' does heavy work in two hedges: kinda and again turn total self-loss into routine, something shrugged at rather than mourned. The plea 'say my name' is the hinge — she needs naming because she is disappearing, and love is requested not for joy but 'to ease the pain', purely analgesic, dosage remembered from last time. The bridge stacks clear-eyed knowledge against zero action: 'you're not my savior / but I still don't go' uncouples insight from will entirely, and 'I could fake it / but I'd still want more' admits even performance would not kill the appetite — the need survives her contempt for it. The saddest line is 'passion's overrated anyway', disappointment pre-emptively dressed as sophistication. It is about knowing exactly how a relationship is erasing you and staying because being named inside it beats being no one outside it.","haiku":"A lover cycles through shame and codependency, knowing they repeat painful patterns yet unable to leave, craving love to ease suffering while faking contentment.","opus":"Trip-hop numbness rendered as erotic surrender, a woman losing herself in a relationship she knows offers no salvation yet can't leave, aware she's repeating a hollow cycle and still wanting more of it.","sonnet":"About being trapped in a relationship that no longer saves you but that you still won't leave — the passion has faded, you've been here before, you know the savior isn't coming, yet you stay because some need persists. The dissolution in the title is emotional self-loss through inertia.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"staying in a relationship because being named beats being no one","built":"verses open with a single flat word then echo it into dissolution"},
"massive-attack~everywhen":{"haiku":"Transcendent love and mystical incantation; the sequence of cosmic connection ends and begins in infinite cyclical time.","opus":"Broken phrases about affection, illumination and kinship circle a cycle that keeps closing and reopening, unsettling anything the listener assumed was fixed. As Bristol's trip-hop architects, Massive Attack prize atmosphere over plot, using sparse cosmic imagery to suggest impermanence rather than tell a linear tale.","sonnet":"Love and mortality are rendered in the compressed, incantatory language of ritual — light striking like tears, sequences ending and beginning in the same breath. The blood ties that survive all endings suggest something that outlasts the individual consciousness, neither hopeful nor despairing.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~exchange":{"fable":"The meaning lives in the collision between the two voices in play: Horace Andy's warm reggae vocal delivering a text that is cold to the touch. The refrain circles one conviction — that a face is a sealed surface, that no one can read another's interior — turning proverb into a warning about the fundamental unknowability of other people. The verse's caution against confusing affection with its opposite, and against trusting anyone's professed feeling, reframes the whole as an ethic of self-protection: guard your interior, believe no declaration. As Mezzanine's final word, after an album steeped in paranoia and intimacy-as-threat, the sweetness of the melody becomes the mask, and the sentiment underneath is quiet, absolute isolation.","fableDeep":"It works like an oral-tradition fragment, one idea repeated until it hardens into law. The axiom — 'you see a man's face / but you don't see his heart' — is stated, varied, and returned to, with small substitutions doing the thinking: see becomes know, heart becomes thoughts, mapping the two inner territories that stay sealed no matter how long you look. The middle section supplies the only movement: 'everywhere you go / there's a man to say love' turns the world into a marketplace of professions, and the response is property law as self-defense — 'your heart is your heart / and your thoughts is your thoughts', possession as protection against other people's words. The single command with moral rather than epistemic weight, 'never mix love with hatred', names the real danger: not that others deceive you, but that taking their talk to heart lets the two currents blend inside you. The final line leaves the face still facing you, unread. It is about guarding an unknowable inner self in a world where every stranger's mouth says love.","haiku":"The hidden depths behind human faces; surfaces deceive and thoughts remain unknowable, so love and hatred must be kept separate or they corrupt the heart.","opus":"A quiet meditation on how unknowable other people are, warning that a visible face reveals nothing of what someone truly feels or intends. The counsel is to guard your inner life, keep affection and loathing distinct, and treat outside declarations of love with skepticism.","sonnet":"A quiet meditation on opacity and emotional self-protection — you can see a man's face but never know his heart or thoughts, and the repeated advice is to never confuse love with hatred or let either reach too deep.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"an ethic of self-protection built from the unknowability of other faces","built":"the warm reggae vocal delivering cold isolation makes the melody the mask"},
"massive-attack~flat-of-the-blade":{"haiku":"Speaker carries dark secrets and skills learned through violence; they build family protection and love through grim discipline.","opus":"Massive Attack, with a strangled falsetto from guest Guy Garvey, voice a haunted man scarred by things he's done and can't talk about. Cornered and broken, he channels everything into one obsession: protecting his family at any cost. The song weighs the crushing burden of the choices that shaped him, the imagery of forged steel standing in for a life of hard decisions.","sonnet":"A man shaped by war and the unspeakable — skills he cannot name, scenes that will follow him to death — finds in family the one structure worth building toward. He accepts what is given and what is taken, granite to shove, riveting it all into a bulletproof love.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man who cannot function in company and carries sights that will hunt him until he dies tries to convert that damage into something a household can shelter inside. The song's whole machinery is metallurgical — steel, blades, plates joined one to the next — so the same industrial violence that hollowed him out becomes the only vocabulary he has left for tenderness, protection welded together as if it were armour plating. Garvey's murmured, near-liturgical delivery keeps it unresolved whether we are hearing a soldier, a shift worker, or a torturer, and Massive Attack refuse to settle it: the bridge's question about how the weapon's weight feels when you are on your knees before it turns the man's own labour and his own decisions into the edge held against him, so that the provider and the punished are one body.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","violence & murder","family & blood"],"means":"industrial violence converted into the only available tenderness","built":"the bridge turns the worker's own labour into the blade held against him"},
"massive-attack~future-proof":{"haiku":"Fragmented states of displacement, numbness, and transience as the speaker moves through temporary states of being without solid ground.","opus":"A murky trip-hop sketch of a rootless, borderline existence, borrowed clothes and chemical numbness passing for a life. It is about drifting through the world hollowed out, the self dissolving into thin air.","sonnet":"A compressed snapshot of marginalised or displaced existence — passport photos, empty pockets, borrowed clothes, chemicals gripping the body in winter — the fragments add up to a picture of someone numbed, passing through the world without leaving a mark, chemically or socially anaesthetised.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~girl-i-love-you":{"haiku":"A love that has irreversibly faded. The speaker professes devotion yet acknowledges their own love is gone forever; back in Spanish town, a promise to never let down rings hollow against the reality of absence.","opus":"A romance that has burned out, the narrator confessing tenderness to a woman even as he admits the feeling has drained away for good, mourning her while conceding the bond is finished.","sonnet":"The narrator acknowledges that his love for a woman is gone even as he misses her, recalling a promise she made to stay and expressing the difficulty of accepting that the bond has permanently ended.","src":"haiku","fable":"Horace Andy's aged, quavering reggae tenor is the whole argument here: he sings a farewell whose grief is already settled before the song opens, the affection between two people announced as spent past any recovery. Massive Attack build the track as a slow circling rather than a progression, so the confession repeats almost unchanged until a single verse breaks the loop — the narrator relocating to a Caribbean-tinged home ground, half-pleading that if she cared she would stay near him, half-knowing she won't. That lone bid for reassurance is the tell: he keeps insisting the bond is genuine precisely because it has already dissolved, and the dub-heavy stillness the group wraps around Andy lets the repetition register not as a chorus but as a man saying goodbye and unable to stop.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss"],"means":"insisting love is real precisely because it has already dissolved","built":"the track circles without progression, repetition as goodbye not chorus","fvr":"2.4"},
"massive-attack~group-four":{"fable":"The closing track of Mezzanine, and its most paranoid: the interior monologue of a surveilled, self-anaesthetised subject who sips something meant to keep him level while cameras track him, and who responds to the pressure not by acting but by shutting down his senses one at a time until only hearing is left. The self-defence regimen he claims to practise turns out to be theatre cribbed from a commercial, his softened parts padded against no real threat, and the freedom he brags of amounts to bolting doors and roaming across dummy monitors and glossy print—a caged man mistaking media for the wider world beyond the room. Against 3D's clenched delivery, Elizabeth Fraser floats the other half of the same withdrawal: sealing the eyes shut as the way to blow the sky open, retreat reframed as the last available form of liberation, so that going inward is at once the trap and the escape. The wasted night he imagines beside a lover, set against the drab clock-in beneath fluorescent tubes, exposes a life narrowed to numb routine, where roaming is just the flicker of a man who has gone quietly and permanently interior.","fableDeep":"The frame is a job — the title names a security firm — and the first image sets the exchange: 'a flask I drink of sober tea / while relay cameras monitor me', the watcher watched, sobriety and surveillance rhymed into one condition. Against that machinery the song builds a contemplative practice in almost technical language: 'my sixth sense peacefully placed on my breath' is meditation instruction verbatim, and 'my ears know that my eyes are closed' makes the body its own witness, hearing standing guard so vision can leave the premises. The repeated tag 'as advertised', hung after martial-arts training and reinforced softened parts, is the sourest joke — even self-cultivation arrives as a purchased product, self-defense with a slogan. The second movement drifts wider: hearing the starry sky turn over at midnight replaces the shift-worker's clock with a cosmic one, and roaming happens motionless, across dummy screens and magazines. It is about finding an unpoliceable interior freedom inside the most monitored, dead-end hours a city has to offer.","haiku":"Surveillance, meditation, and consciousness observing itself through surveillance and introspection. The narrator navigates awareness while monitored, daydreaming internally.","opus":"An inward meditation on being watched, where a person under constant camera surveillance retreats into meditative calm, navigating a private inner landscape with their senses rather than their sight. Surrounded by artificial displays and cold fluorescence, they carve out a sanctuary that no monitoring can reach.","sonnet":"A fragmented, meditative song narrated from what feels like a surveillance or security shift — cameras, monitors, a flask of tea, strip lights — while the inner voice ranges through daydreams, martial arts training, and a quiet sense of floating in a private world.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"retreat inward staged simultaneously as trap and last available liberation","built":"the job-title as song title pins surveillance and self-cultivation in one frame","fvr":"2.4"},
"massive-attack~hymn-of-the-big-wheel":{"haiku":"Ecological collapse and environmental decay, with Earth's rotation as a metaphor for relentless cycles of inequality and destruction caused by industrial civilization.","opus":"Over a slow turning cosmic image of the Earth on its axis, a parent addresses a child about a world scarred by factories, acid rain, satellites and tumbling green. Horace Andy's reggae falsetto lifts a battered hope that a nurturing sun will mend the soul again. Massive Attack close their debut with a lullaby-hymn balancing gravity's pull against tenderness.","sonnet":"A parent addresses a child against a backdrop of industrial decay and environmental grief, watching green land swallowed by expansion. The world keeps rotating indifferently — one person struggles while another rests — yet a quiet faith persists that the ghetto sun will eventually mend what the wheel has broken.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~inertia-creeps":{"fable":"This 1998 Mezzanine track sits at the band's darkest, most claustrophobic turn, and Robert Del Naja's title names its subject: inertia as an erotic and depressive force at once, the way a body in a stalled relationship inches ever upward and never fast, dragged by gravity rather than desire. The verses map two malnourished lovers pressed together in a shared domestic space where intimacy has curdled into friction and repetition, the arousal of the recurring female presence indistinguishable from the pull of paralysis, so that climax and standstill share the same maddening tempo. The final movement completes the collapse: the reach across distance shrinks to a child's telephone rigged from a can and a length of twine, and the plea to be strung along admits the singer would rather be manipulated into staying than break the spell, the crossed-out X of the interlude standing in for a desire too degraded or too depleted to name.","fableDeep":"The central move is the pun cycling through the refrain — inertia 'creeps' and inertia 'keeps', it both advances stealthily and holds him fast — and it is twinned with 'she comes', so stasis and sex arrive in the same breath, drift made erotic. Verse one is anatomy without romance: 'two undernourished egos, four rotating hips' counts the couple as parts, appetite mutual but nourishment absent, a sliding scale clung to. The suffocation is domestic and precise: the shared kitchen as the site of an itching irritant, the sense of having been here forever, rooms that shift while nobody moves out. The third verse grounds it in the bed itself — nylon burning the bedspread, gravity at zero, stalling and falling, sweet though incomplete — desire as engine failure mid-air. The censored climax, 'I want to x you', lets sex and violence occupy the same redacted verb, which is honest: he no longer knows which he means. It is about how habit does what desire cannot — keeps two people moving toward each other after everything else has stopped.","haiku":"Two people drift through intimacy and domestic life together, moving slowly and reluctantly, caught in a romantic inertia where they persist together despite emotional numbness.","opus":"A hazy, claustrophobic account of a stale, entangled relationship marked by undernourished egos, restless nights, and creeping inertia, with desire and irritation blurring as the singer feels stuck yet clinging on.","sonnet":"A slow, sensual account of physical intimacy between two people — desire, bodily closeness, and a drifting, half-conscious state where physical sensation and memory blur together.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"habit doing what desire cannot, keeping two people moving after everything stops","built":"the refrain puns inertia as both advance and arrest, stasis and sex in one breath"},
"massive-attack~karmacoma":{"haiku":"A couple in a loveless, volatile relationship cycles through temptation and withholding, acknowledging they have nothing to give each other.","opus":"The Bristol trip-hop pioneers drift into a hazy, guarded intimacy, a narrator cautioning a would-be lover that he's empty-handed and would rather keep his distance than promise devotion. Drinking that fails to steady him and frayed attachments blur into a dub refrain of far-off place names, love here a numbed, mutually depleting stupor.","sonnet":"A relationship examined without flattery — both parties circling each other warily, neither fully committed. The narrator admits to daily drinking that cools nothing, suburban drift without real intimacy, and a dynamic where trust might eventually unlock something but emotional ties stay severed until it does.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~lately":{"haiku":"A relationship falling apart as emotional distance and hidden feelings replace former intimacy and openness.","opus":"Summer stirs the blues as the narrator watches lovers and realizes he is now alone. A partner has grown secretive, edgy as a cat on a hot tin roof, hiding feelings once freely shared, and the refrain keeps asking where it all went wrong. Massive Attack drape this quiet inventory of a relationship curdling in the warmth of their Bristol soul.","sonnet":"A summer of watching other couples erodes what once felt certain between two people. Distance and secrecy have replaced the old physical intimacy, and the speaker returns to the same bewildered question — searching for where a once-tight connection silently came undone.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~man-next-door":{"fable":"A cover of the Paragons' rocksteady lament about a nightmare neighbor, but Massive Attack strip out the sunlight and leave only the paranoia, so the small domestic complaint swells into existential siege: the fights through the wall become a whole hostile world the narrator vows to escape with his family. The genius is the leaking tap that ends it, an endlessly repeated single syllable that turns nuisance into torture and reveals the real subject as sleeplessness itself, the way a mind with no rest magnifies every sound into a threat. The escape never comes; the loop just keeps dripping, so the promised quiet place stays permanently out of reach.","fableDeep":"A song about noise built almost entirely from repetition, so listening to it enacts the complaint. Every unit doubles — 'in my neighborhood / in my neighborhood', 'always a fuss and fight / always a fuss and fight' — and the echo is not emphasis but the nightly recurrence itself, the same disturbance at the same hour, again. The grievance stays purely acoustic: we never see the neighbor, only hear pots 'bang against the wall', which keeps the violence ambient and unconfrontable — you cannot argue with a wall, only absorb it. The chorus is the sole forward motion, 'I've got to get away from here', with the family invoked as the stake, and its answer is not departure but the onomatopoeic drip, drip, drip — time leaking, water torture, a tap nobody turns off. The past tense of 'a man that lived next door' hints the escape already happened, yet the cycle replays anyway, memory as tenant. It is about how proximity to another household's chaos becomes your own quiet emergency, and home stops being home through a shared wall.","haiku":"A neighbor's constant chaos, fights, and noise drive a family to desperation, forcing them to flee their home in search of peace and escape.","opus":"A man chased from his own home by a violent neighbor whose midnight brawls, clattering kitchenware, and ceaseless racket rob him of any peace. The core impulse is escape, uprooting his family somewhere calm, while a steady dripping sound conjures the slow, drop-by-drop erosion of living beside chaos.","sonnet":"Living next door to a violent household — constant late-night fighting, crashing pots, no rest — until the only option is to take your family and flee to somewhere quieter and safer.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","freedom & escape"],"means":"a neighbor's nightly chaos converting home into siege","built":"every unit of complaint doubles in the lyric, enacting the nightly recurrence"},
"massive-attack~mezzanine":{"fable":"The title track of Mezzanine (1998) — the album that turned Bristol trip-hop cold, paranoid, and post-club. This is a seduction that runs on that same chill: the mezzanine, the album's governing image, is the half-level between floors, and 3D builds the whole song as a set of these intermediate landings, spaces that are neither one place nor another. Desire here is architectural and evasive — an invitation to keep unwinding downward through liminal spaces that supposedly lead somewhere private, but the come-down logic (things tasting better as you descend) and the partner with the hardened heart make the promise feel narcotic rather than romantic, a spiral that flatters itself as ascent while it drops. The obsessive return to those half-floors, mutating by the outro into a shared possessive, is the song's real content: not arrival but the loop of pursuit itself, curiosity circling a coolness it can't warm, a lust that has confused the stairwell for the destination.","fableDeep":"The title's conceit governs everything: a mezzanine is a level between levels, and the song negotiates from there — 'all these half-floors / will lead to mine' turns a building into a seduction route, each landing a partial commitment, his floor the promised terminus that keeps receding. The request 'close your eyes and reinvent me' is the tell: he offers not himself but a fiction she can author, intimacy as mutual hallucination with her eyes shut. Her side of the ledger is scheduling — 'give me evenings and weekends' concedes the week to her friends and asks only for the margins, desire negotiated like custody of hours. The drug logic surfaces in 'a typical high' and the sly admission that 'it tastes better on the way back down', where the comedown outranks the peak — he is a connoisseur of descent, of the after, of the half-way. Blood and mud both fail the thickness test; nothing here binds. It is about wanting someone only in the in-between spaces, and dressing that limitation up as an invitation.","haiku":"Two people navigate a tenuous connection across urban spaces and partial moments, seeking intimacy while keeping emotional distance.","opus":"The title track's seductive, unsettling come-on: a narrator angling for stolen evenings with someone stone-hearted, desire tangled with something predatory. Massive Attack make trip-hop menace out of wanting what tastes better on the way down.","sonnet":"A man at the margins of a relationship — given only evenings and weekends while she spends her life with friends and social scenes — offers himself anyway, navigating the half-floors between closeness and distance, intrigued and slightly unsettled by her emotional opacity.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["desire without arrival","artifice & performance"],"means":"pursuit mistaking the stairwell for the destination it never reaches","built":"the half-floor as governing conceit — desire conducted entirely between levels"},
"massive-attack~name-taken":{"haiku":"Hallways and icy rooms contain fading faces and children; scars disappear into empty space where memory dissolves.","opus":"Massive Attack work in near-abstraction, stringing together cold corridors, cinematic camera tricks and a leaden sky into a mood of erasure. Faces and photographs dissolve while a refrain traces closeness turning to childhood and then to nothing. The Bristol group conjures memory and loss through elliptical imagery that never settles into story.","sonnet":"Fragmented impressions of loss and distance — hallways, fading faces, brothers glimpsed before someone steps outside under a gunmetal sky. Identity and childhood dissolve in repeated fading, leaving only the question of where scars went and who remains when everything familiar peels away.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~paradise-circus":{"haiku":"Love as sinful attraction to a beautiful woman who will never reciprocate, spinning endlessly in the devil's grin of temptation.","opus":"Trip-hop meditation on love as something closer to compulsion or sin, desire that consumes and then discards. The seduction is fatalistic, treating attraction as a spiral you enter knowing it will burn out and still can't resist.","sonnet":"About the destructive pull of desire — love framed as a sin that consumes most intensely those who feel it deepest, with the devil as a willing accomplice and the beloved as someone who will burn you and never come back.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about desire that knows exactly how bad it is for you and leans in anyway — the devil doesn't force the sin so much as offer the pleasure of spinning inside his grin, so that the discomfort of feeling anything at all becomes a reason to roll toward the thing that hurts. Hope Sandoval's narcotized delivery is the whole argument: she describes an attraction promised to be brief and cruel, a burning-eyed woman whose affection is guaranteed to be as fleeting and indifferent as an insect's, and she sounds not warned off but lulled toward it. That flatness is the point — 3D's production keeps the pulse slow and drugged so the doom registers as seduction, the trip-hop trick of making surrender sound like the only warm thing in the room.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"doom delivered as seduction, surrender as the only warmth","built":"narcotized vocal flatness makes the warning indistinguishable from the lure"},
"massive-attack~protection":{"haiku":"Love means willingly standing between the other person and harm, offering shelter and protection even when it would be easier to walk away.","opus":"A narrator pledges to shelter a hurting friend who's convinced she's beyond help, positioning herself to absorb whatever pain comes at her. With Tracey Thorn's vocal on the original, Massive Attack render trip-hop warmth as mutual care, the genders swapping roles to argue that holding each other up runs deeper than romance.","sonnet":"Unconditional physical and emotional shielding is offered across every permutation of gender and role — the one who stands in front of the blow does not ask whether it is conventional, only whether it is needed. The tenderness is muscular and reciprocal, built on years of leaning both ways.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~psyche":{"haiku":"A searcher in darkness confronts trauma and transformation, watching someone pulled from water into life, exploring memory and pain while facing dissolution and falling in love.","opus":"A dreamlike search for someone lost, the imagery of drowning and rescue circling a birth or rebirth, love offered as shield against dissolution. Trip-hop at its most oblique, more felt than parsed, everything blurred at the edge of falling in.","sonnet":"A dreamlike attempt to locate and protect someone from harm — images of searching in darkness, watching someone pulled from water into life, the narrator pledging to be a shield while the self dissolves on an old dark sea.","src":"haiku","fable":"Psyche is a rescue told from the far side of it, Martina Topley-Bird sweeping a flashlight through night woods for someone she half-suspects she is inventing — the search doubles as a self-interrogation, an attempt to coax the buried animal instinct back up out of a person who has gone under. The album's guest-per-track design lets Massive Attack hand this one entirely to her, and she reads it as a birth staged as a drowning: a child hauled from the water and marched into the world, the harvest imagery of sun and blade turning that arrival into something already shadowed by what it will cost to be alive. Her prescription is deliberately brutal and un-English in its logic — break the ribs open, throw out ridicule and restraint, let disorder govern whatever remains — because tenderness only works here once the defenses have been wrecked. By the close the two of them have dissolved into a single drifting thing on a blank sea, and the vow of surrender repeated to the vanishing point reads less as resolution than as vertigo, the same downward motion said over and over until it becomes the only ground left to stand on.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming"],"means":"defences wrecked before tenderness is allowed any ground to stand on","built":"rescue staged as a drowning, birth shadowed by what survival will cost"},
"massive-attack~risingson":{"fable":"Risingson is a 1997 Bristol trip-hop dispatch from the far end of a night out, and its real subject is dissociation on a dancefloor: the narrator has been dragged to a party he is desperate to escape, watching his own reflection fracture in mirrors and sink basins while the crowd blurs into interchangeable, disposable figures who diminish him even as he sneers at them. Robert Del Naja and Daddy G split the paranoia between them, one drowning in club haze and the other pleading to leave, and the recurring toy-people refrain does the heavy lifting: it is at once contempt for the vacant hedonists around him and an admission that he has become just as automated and hollow, a body moving on remote control. The buried folk-revival echo about vanished flowers turns the comedown into something older and sadder, a Sixties dream of communion curdled into Nineties alienation, and the flatly repeated command to dream on lands as pure irony, less an invitation than a jaded verdict on everyone still deluding themselves that the night means anything.","fableDeep":"The verses keep sliding between watching a lover at the mirror and the men's room sink and watching himself — 'I see myself in there upon my lover' — so the disgust never finds a stable target; he cannot tell her dissolution from his own. 'Toy-like people make me boy-like' is the thesis rhyme: the crowd's plastic fakeness infantilizes him, the jingle-like sound enacting the regression it describes. The plainest line, 'I'm dying to leave', is buried mid-verse and answered by nobody, while 'Where have all those flowers gone?' lifts Pete Seeger's anti-war lament into a nightclub as mock-elegy for a wasted generation. The recurring dismissal 'dream on' works double duty — a sneer at the beautiful people and a lullaby he sings himself, since his departure only ever happens in the phrase now you're gone. It is about the moment contempt for a scene becomes indistinguishable from contempt for yourself, and leaving becomes a story you tell instead of a thing you do.","haiku":"Superficial, toy-like people lack substance and soul; they fade to black while the speaker watches them spin through consciousness and lust. Dreams persist despite the hollow parade of empty souls.","opus":"Bristol trip-hop as a nauseated night out: the club as a maze of interchangeable faces and cheap intimacy, a man desperate to leave a scene he keeps returning to. Alienation dressed as glamour, told in a paranoid mutter.","sonnet":"A night out at a club where the narrator watches someone disappear into bathroom mirrors and drug rituals, the intimacy of the crowd turning predatory — toy-like people triggering regression, everything automated and replaceable — and an exit that feels more like dissolution than escape.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","night & the city","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"contempt for the scene becomes indistinguishable from contempt for yourself","built":"leaving only ever happens in the phrase — the narrator never actually goes"},
"massive-attack~rush-minute":{"haiku":"Addiction and escapism through highs; recovery remains distant as the narrator chases pleasure and chemical relief from broken lives and violence.","opus":"Massive Attack inhabit the tug-of-war of addiction, a narrator who wants sobriety but cannot function without a fix, driven to chase a high he can't live around. Stylized images of a glamorous, rotting scene surround the admission that there's always further to fall in recovery, chemistry and self-loathing blurring into a weary study of craving.","sonnet":"The desire to get high wars with the desire to get clean in a world where peaches live in niches and Marshall Amps bridge broken homes. Recovery hovers at the edges but the pull toward one more high keeps the narrator suspended between self-knowledge and craving.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is the seductive inner monologue of an addict who has stopped pretending to want out, staging convalescence not as escape but as more square footage for the habit. Robert Del Naja's craving and his wish to come clean never argue; they coexist as one continuous appetite, the ward rebranded into a luxury suite the using never checks out of. Around that private ruin he arranges a cover-girl surface of nightclub cool, untouchable poses and magazine glamour, so the scene-life reads as the same anaesthetic as the chemical one. Verse two grounds it in English decline — amplifier rigs, wrecked households, the reform-school pipeline — hardship lacquered into style rather than lamented. Cruelest of all, the lover and the substance become interchangeable, the source of endorphins indistinguishable from the thing that leaves him wrecked, so the mask never slips because the mask is the whole point.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"convalescence rebranded as more square footage for the habit","built":"lover and substance become interchangeable sources of the same wreckage"},
"massive-attack~safe-from-harm":{"haiku":"A protector guards their loved one against urban danger and corrupting social forces with vigilant retaliation.","opus":"Massive Attack, on a defining trip-hop track, survey a menacing parade of thugs and lunatics the narrator can't rein in, offset by a ferocious vow to strike back if anyone touches what's hers. The refrain offers up the world and the mind in exchange for a single condition: protecting her child through the night, dread simmering under the groove.","sonnet":"A protective ferocity runs beneath a surface acceptance of a chaotic world — maniacs, gunmen, and casual violence are facts of urban life, fine to ignore until someone touches what belongs to this narrator. Then the threat is simple, immediate, and total.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~saturday-come-slow":{"haiku":"Longing and questioning; time stretches while yearning for love or connection remains unanswered.","opus":"A hushed, aching plea turns on one uncertainty, whether the love is real or gone entirely, as the weekend is coaxed to come gently. Half-mythic geography of rock and southern country grounds the longing. On their later material, Massive Attack set Damon Albarn's frail vocal against near-silence, leaving desire and doubt suspended.","sonnet":"Suspended in desire and uncertainty, a figure waits through a week that refuses to pass. Longing has turned the days sluggish, and a simple urgent question — whether love is still there or has already gone — hangs unanswered against limestone landscapes and a kingdom that offers no clarity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A lover's plea gets asked the way you'd interrogate the ground itself. Damon Albarn's voice, bare over a slow ache of a track, keeps circling one question about whether he is wanted at all, and the song answers by widening the frame rather than replying: the plea sinks past the surface into geology, into subterranean chambers of soft rock and drowned settlements under the southwest of England, as if the human doubt could only be measured against something buried and unanswering. That downward reach is the reading — the repetition at the end isn't insistence so much as a call let fall into a hollow that gives nothing back, so the dread of an unloved life and the dread of the empty caverns beneath a country become, by the last refrain, the same silence.","fv":2,"fnote":"Daddy G has said Albarn wrote his part about Bristol and the limestone caves of the southwest — on his way to work.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a plea for love measured against subterranean silence that gives nothing back","built":"the question sinks into geology rather than receiving any human answer"},
"massive-attack~simple-rules":{"haiku":"Power and control dominate human relationships, framed through trespass and transgression. The speaker asserts their place against those who would deny their right to exist.","opus":"A restless, us-against-the-world posture set to Massive Attack's brooding menace. A voice speaks for a people told they do not deserve to survive, refusing to carry the past and insisting on seizing the future. Walls come down, defiance hardens into ambition, and the collective will to dominate curdles slightly toward something colder than solidarity.","sonnet":"A defiant collective voice refuses the verdict that they don't deserve their place in the world, tearing down walls of suppression and rallying against those who would silence them, insisting on moving forward and taking ownership of their future.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~small-time-shot-away":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness masks turbulence; identical twins on a path of surface flirtation hiding deeper wounds.","opus":"Massive Attack render an intimate bond as combat and sedation, two people so fused they feed off the same source yet keep injuring each other. Casual conversation works like a drug, every meeting a battle. The trip-hop trio stretch this into a hypnotic drone dramatizing closeness that comforts and harms in the same breath.","sonnet":"A toxic entanglement plays out as a kind of warfare — two people mirroring each other, alternately numbing and igniting. Pillow talk functions as chloroform, connection doubles as weapon, and what began as seduction keeps ricocheting between bodies like something already spent.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~special-cases":{"haiku":"The song cautions against self-righteousness in relationships and urges mutual accountability rather than blame. Pride and ego damage partnerships; good men deserve recognition.","opus":"Counsel to a woman to quit picking at her partner and turn the mirror on herself, since arrogance is the worst failing and any bond requires effort from both. The gentle payoff: in a world full of terrible men, she ought to see and appreciate that she landed a decent one.","sonnet":"A frank piece of relationship advice: stop cataloguing your partner's faults and check your own pride first, because there are good men in a world full of bad things, and if you have one of them you should recognize it.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~splitting-the-atom":{"haiku":"The song captures economic collapse and temporal slippage. Bankers retreat, jobs fade, and paradise is fleeting; dust blows while a last kiss dissolves before eyes.","opus":"A foreboding survey of a decaying world, a warm bed giving way to collapsed finance, the unemployed drifting back and joy failing, all under a chilly refrain telling you not to give up. Massive Attack fold guest vocals into their signature ominous dub atmosphere, tallying entropy as the season fades and only faint sweetness offers any solace.","sonnet":"A world coming undone observed from close quarters and from a cosmic distance at once — the personal (a warm bed, a candle burning down, someone being let go) runs alongside the systemic (bankers retreating, the jobless returning, pleasure failing by week's end) until both collapse into the same particle: indivisible, invisible, the last of the last things dissolving while the speaker holds someone near and calls their blood gold.","src":"haiku","fable":"A recession song that keeps mistaking itself for a seduction, and the reverse — Daddy G's tour-guide promise to walk you through a lovely city is the same voice admitting the affection is a costume stretched over cruelty, so trust and treachery arrive in a single breath. The middle verse drops the pretense and names the post-2008 wreckage directly: the moneymen get rescued while everyone downstream shadowboxes for rent and chases paper, self-medicating just to face the dole queue again, and Horace Andy's serene chorus hovers over it not as comfort but as the mantra you grip precisely because releasing it would be the sane response. Then 3D pulls the lens all the way back — a body winding down like a stuck record, a summer already spent, the title's dream of limitless energy inverted into pure entropy. Two lovers decide that if everything erodes toward zero, the only dignity left is to choose the vanishing: to raise a toast at the finish and go out held together rather than flung apart, the smallest thing that cannot be halved turning out to be not the atom but the pair.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"two people choosing mutual vanishing as the last act of dignity","built":"the title's promise of limitless energy inverted into pure entropy by the close"},
"massive-attack~teardrop":{"fable":"Jeff Buckley drowned while Elizabeth Fraser was recording this, and she has said the song became his elegy — which turns its central image-system into a coincidence too heavy to ignore: an eye that is water, a mirror that is water, and a man lost to water. The insistence that love is an action rather than a feeling reads less as philosophy than as instruction to herself, a way of holding onto someone by continuing to do the loving after the object is gone. Fraser keeps the grief nearly wordless, letting the same soft refrain of breath return like a pulse against the fire, so the song's real subject arrives only in the closing address to a you fumbling through darkness, the elegy finally admitting it has been speaking to the dead all along.","fableDeep":"It opens with a definition — 'love is a doing word' — insisting love is verb, not noun, and then spends the rest of the song refusing to do anything but hover: images without verbs, states without agents. 'Feathers on my breath' recurs as the pulse, weightlessness measured against exhalation, a fragility you could scatter just by speaking too hard. The title image, 'teardrop on the fire', is the central paradox — the smallest possible water set against consuming heat, grief too small to quench what it falls into — and it lands 'of a confession', so the crying becomes the admission itself, evaporating as it is made. 'Black flowers blossom' inverts spring into mourning-bloom, growth that darkens as it opens. Water as the 'most faithful mirror' says you know yourself truest by what you weep over. Then the trance breaks and the address turns: you're stumbling in the dark. It is about love as something you must keep doing, tiny and insisted-upon, against fire and for the blind.","haiku":"Love as an active force transforms suffering into confession; water mirrors the self as tears fall on fire, leaving only feathers and breath.","opus":"Elizabeth Fraser's aching meditation on love as an active, fragile thing, breath and feathers and a teardrop hitting fire, reportedly shadowed by grief for Jeff Buckley. Tenderness and mortality held in the same weightless breath.","sonnet":"A meditation on love as an active, delicate force — something fragile and weighted at once, a confession made on a fire, feathers on breath — circling grief and tenderness without naming either directly.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","love & desire"],"means":"love kept as an action long after the object of it is gone","built":"the closing address to a you stumbling in darkness reveals the elegy has spoken to the dead all along"},
"massive-attack~three":{"haiku":"Mourning lost innocence; wishing to unknow everything learned, to return to childhood clarity.","opus":"Someone captivated by a favored number grieves how effortless childhood once felt, caught between mourning everything he understands now and longing for the unburdened mind he lost. He'd trade away his knowledge but hold onto its hard-won insight. Over Massive Attack's stripped arrangement, it weighs awareness itself as a weight.","sonnet":"Someone mourns the loss of childhood innocence and the uncomplicated faith that came with it. Knowledge has displaced wonder without delivering wisdom, and the speaker fantasizes about surrendering everything learned in order to return to the clean unknowing of a child — though nothing can be truly unremembered.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~unfinished-sympathy":{"haiku":"Desire and curiosity about another person creates an all-consuming ache, with the narrator fragmenting into incomplete parts without the other's presence.","opus":"The Bristol trip-hop pioneers pour raw yearning over sweeping strings, the narrator who had only ever pictured love now undone by it, hurt, curious, and desperate to know more. The famous refrain casts her as fragmentary and incomplete, a person missing essential pieces of herself, longing left lush and deliberately unresolved.","sonnet":"Longing for someone whose potential alone has already caused real damage: the closeness never fully arrives, yet its possibility is enough to break something open. The narrator feels like pure absence — soul without mind, body without heart — assembled only around the ache of what has not yet and may never happen. The desire itself is the wound.","src":"haiku"},
"massive-attack~voodoo-in-my-blood":{"opus":"A feverish, unsettled psyche riddled with paranoia and grief, addressing a mother figure while blood, mimicry and menace bleed through a barely-held composure.","sonnet":"A disorienting, confrontational address about blood, mimicry, and control — something livid and possessed moves through the speaker — and the repeated question about why blood sticks to teeth, directed at a mother-figure, suggests guilt and complicity that can't be wiped away.","src":"sonnet"},
"massive-attack~what-your-soul-sings":{"haiku":"The song encourages self-love and inner listening, urging people to speak their authentic truth and trust their innermost voice without shame or fear.","opus":"An encouraging song urging the listener to speak from the soul, rearrange the thoughts that bring them down, and choose joy, ultimately discovering that the one they love and need is themselves.","sonnet":"A gentle encouragement to stop suppressing one's inner voice: by honestly naming what brings pain, rearranging harmful thoughts, and listening to the soul's own signal, a person discovers that the love and companionship they need is found within themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"mastodon~crack-the-skye":{"haiku":"Someone experiences violent suffering, watching their soul deteriorate under pressure, while desperately pleading for heavenly protection against spiritual possession.","opus":"A dense, mystical elegy about leaving the body and reeling from bereavement, a protective spirit guarding a soul while violent forces rip through, ending with a mother begging that a fierce girl not be dragged down by the devil. The Atlanta prog-metal band tied this 2009 concept-album centerpiece to the real death of drummer Brann Dailor's sister, its winding riffs steeped in grief.","sonnet":"A vision of scattered things trying to hold together against forces that want them undone — a shepherd struck down and the flock lost, mountains of despair, a ghostly face haunting the dawn, a soul under violent pressure taking its final toll. Something or someone is being fought over between spiritual forces, and the speaker can see the pain written plainly, pleading with whatever's listening to keep her here because her spirit is too strong to hand over.","src":"haiku"},
"mastodon~jaguar-god":{"haiku":"Shape-shifting shamanic prophecy and feral aggression merge as an earthbound deity asserts dominion through transformation and predatory force.","opus":"Mastodon channeling shape-shifting Mesoamerican myth, a defiant outsider tangled in prophecy, sorcery and a throne of sickness. Under the mysticism sits a plainer human claim, that he was the best they had, and he'll spend his whole life proving he was right.","sonnet":"A dual narrative blends a fugitive vagabond's defiant self-justification with a mythological invocation of Tezcatlipoca — the Jaguar God of the smoking mirror — whose shapeshifting, obsidian wisdom guides the speaker through black sorcery toward a prophecy of dominion.","src":"haiku"},
"mastodon~more-than-i-could-chew":{"haiku":"Crushing self-recrimination and internal chaos demand reckoning: the weight of disruption drowns the speaker, who struggles between guilt, shame, and the impossible task of self-healing.","opus":"Buckling under guilt and shame after some huge rupture, the narrator has torn himself to pieces and lost his bearings, begging to be shown how to set it right and vowing to race back the moment he's told how.","sonnet":"A speaker crushed by guilt, shame, and inner chaos turns to someone else for guidance, begging to be told how to reconcile the wreckage — \"I've already ripped myself to pieces and I've given it away\" — while waves of doubt make it hard to breathe, though the refrain of \"say when\" leaves open the possibility of rescue.","src":"haiku"},
"mastodon~once-more-round-the-sun":{"haiku":"Temporal acceleration renders life fleeting; the narrator negotiates meaning through communal joy while mortality pressures into present experience.","opus":"A swaggering carpe-diem written in the shadow of loss, insisting fortune can flip in an instant so you'd better savor the ride. The bravado is a way of facing mortality head-on, one more lap around the sun with someone at your side.","sonnet":"A speaker celebrates the dizzy, disorienting passage of another year around the sun — life is brief and unpredictable, so the only rational response is to make it count, find pleasure in the chaos, and enjoy the ride with whoever is beside you.","src":"haiku"},
"mastodon~pushing-the-tides":{"opus":"A fight to stay afloat amid crushing ruin, the narrator gagging on a hopeless situation yet insisting that continuing to struggle isn't defeat. The song's whole force is the refusal to sink, throwing everything he has into staying up and pressing on.","sonnet":"A determined refusal to sink: despite being overwhelmed and unsure what lurks inside, the narrator keeps fighting to keep his head above water, protecting others, and pressing toward a greater vision even when the weight of everything threatens to pull him under.","src":"sonnet"},
"mastodon~sickle-and-peace":{"haiku":"Death offers shelter from suffering through finality; the speaker accepts oblivion as mercy, surrendering the burden of consciousness.","opus":"Death arrives bringing not only his scythe but a strange peace and shelter, reframing mortality as release rather than only terror. Amid confusion and self-doubt the singer stops mourning what could never be and rises to stand on his own, uncertain where he'll go.","sonnet":"Death arrives with both sickle and peace — a shelter in long flowing robes — while the speaker navigates a situation without logic, moving through loss and guilt toward an upright stance: \"now I stand up on my own, learning from myself,\" though the path remains unknown.","src":"haiku"},
"mastodon~teardrinker":{"opus":"A song of guilt and grief over repeatedly letting someone down, the narrator facing the shame of the damage he's done and calling leaving that person the hardest thing he's ever had to do.","sonnet":"A song about failing someone through addiction or emotional weakness — the narrator acknowledges causing damage, letting a loved one down repeatedly, and ultimately concluding that leaving was the hardest but perhaps necessary act of honesty.","src":"sonnet"},
"mastodon~teardrinker-acoustic-version":{"haiku":"Regret crystallises into permanent weight; the speaker mourns abandonment while recognising the damage they caused irreversibly.","opus":"A confession soaked in guilt over wounding someone again and again before finally being forced to leave, with the departure named as the most brutal act of his life. The harm already inflicted shadows every effort to reassemble what he broke.","sonnet":"The speaker confronts the damage they have done to someone they love — letting them down again and again while drowning in shame — and wrestles with whether to reassemble the pieces or leave them scattered, eventually acknowledging that leaving was the hardest thing and that fortune favours the bold in ways they can no longer claim.","src":"haiku"},
"mastodon~the-motherload":{"opus":"A gesture of solidarity toward someone buckling under life's load, the narrator offering to lift and dissolve that burden, drive off the predators circling them, and flatly refusing to let them vanish or give up. It's a promise of rescue wrapped in a chorus of reassurance.","sonnet":"A song of reassurance to someone on the verge of giving up — the narrator sees the weight this person carries, hears what the dark is telling them, and insists they will not let them slip away, promising that things will work out if they want them to.","src":"sonnet"},
"matchbox-twenty~push":{"haiku":"Two people recognize mutual exploitation and damaged trust, wrestling with the impulse to dominate and control each other. The song depicts a volatile, abusive dynamic.","opus":"Matchbox Twenty sketch a raw, warring couple, the narrator confessing an urge to dominate and undervalue his partner even as a woman's voice admits she has never felt adequate or truly cherished. Rage and dependence knot together, each claiming no debt to the other, both certain they cannot be different. The urge to shove becomes an admission of hurt as much as any wish for control.","sonnet":"A woman's accumulated emotional damage — feeling unloved, untouched with care, repeatedly lied to — is voiced in her own words before the narrator turns the admission inward: recognising the impulse to take her for granted, to push and diminish, even as she is already breaking under years of neglect.","src":"haiku"},
"materia~rosa-alba":{"haiku":"Hope becomes a poisoned comfort that drowns rather than saves; apathy spreads as the speaker sinks under the weight of false reassurance.","opus":"A warning to someone drowning in fear and apathy that clinging to false hope and refusing to cut ties with what's evil will eventually drag them down. Even hope, misplaced, becomes the thing that destroys them.","sonnet":"An address to someone whose apathy and false hope are slowly drowning them: frost burns the exposed soul, fear suffocates, and the hope they cling to is itself the thing that will finally bring them down.","src":"haiku"},
"matriarchs~sleep":{"haiku":"Someone battles insomnia and depression, haunted by trauma and nightmares. They refuse to be another casualty of suffering but teeter on the edge of breaking.","opus":"A first-person account of mental illness and insomnia, medication that dulls rather than heals, and a haunting presence pulling the narrator toward death. It escalates from surviving day by day to open suicidal ideation, telling tormentors off and imagining Russian roulette in public as a final act of reclaimed defiance.","sonnet":"About mental illness, losing medication access after lying about being okay, and the slow collapse that follows — punctuated by a nightmare figure that stalks the edges and a final, furious refusal to be talked down to anymore. It ends with dark, reckless defiance rather than resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"matt-hammer~my-nightmare":{"haiku":"Psychological torment spirals into self-annihilation while the divine remains silent and absent; trauma burns forever without redemption or release.","opus":"A cry to God or a savior from inside a living hell, locked in a cell where demons gorge on his suffering and the nightmare feels too vivid to survive. Bleeding and chained, he pleads to be healed rather than destroyed, coming apart while asking why rescue never arrives.","sonnet":"A prisoner of internal hell — locked inside a cell where devils dwell and demons feed on pain — begs an unnamed presence to find and save them before they waste away entirely. The nightmare is too real to dismiss and time does not reveal where it ends.","src":"haiku"},
"matt-lange-feat-kerry-leva~disenchanted-feat-kerry-leva":{"haiku":"Spiritual pretence masks manipulation; the speaker recognises seduction through desire and withdraws from promised transcendence.","opus":"A relationship built on mystical performance and manipulation leaves both people disenchanted, one grounded and unmoved, the other still wounded. The occult trappings are exposed as lies that ultimately unwired the connection.","sonnet":"A person who was charmed and spiritually manipulated by someone's shadow disposition — tarot cards, incantations, breath exercises — is left disenchanted once the lies unravelled the connection; they stay grounded now rather than following the spell.","src":"haiku"},
"matt-lange~disenchanted":{"haiku":"Spiritual manipulation dissolves into disillusionment as the speaker recognizes shadow hidden beneath tarot and charm. Both lovers remain wounded after desire unspools into deception.","opus":"A relationship dressed in mysticism unravels into disillusionment. One partner hides manipulation behind spiritual language and tarot ritual, playing on the narrator's desire until the accumulated lies come undone. Lange, a producer steeped in atmospheric electronic melancholy, lets the mood settle on that flat, deflated moment when the spell breaks and both people are left disenchanted and hurt.","sonnet":"A spiritual seduction gone cold: someone used tarot-card mysticism and breathwork to play on the narrator's desire and curiosity, but the lies unravelled everything, leaving both parties disenchanted — the shadow disposition hidden behind the ritual is the betrayal.","src":"haiku"},
"matt-lange~quiet-symphony":{"haiku":"Profound vulnerability opens toward healing as two incomplete people find complementary rhythm; mutual presence dissolves isolation.","opus":"A self-aware cynic who has always sailed alone lets a woman crawl into his soul and make a home there, begging her to lay him down and mend him. He knows he over-analyzes and self-sabotages, and hopes love can burn the beast out of him rather than be another projection.","sonnet":"A hardened, self-sabotaging cynic finds in a woman with Midwestern charm a first real home and asks her to lay him down and sing her quiet symphony to mend what is broken, vowing in return to burn the self-defeating beast and learn her melody.","src":"haiku"},
"matt-lange~quiet-symphony-edit":{"haiku":"Vulnerability as temple: two souls intertwine seeking wholeness through each other, but the act of seeking itself risks destroying what it touches.","opus":"A self-sabotaging over-thinker finds a home inside another person and lets her music quiet the demons he'd otherwise feed. Surrender to her becomes the way toward the better part of himself.","sonnet":"A song about falling intensely and cautiously in love — the speaker is a cynical self-saboteur who recognizes they may be projecting, but commits to burning away the parts of themselves that weigh them down in order to make space for this person.","src":"haiku"},
"mavis~the-great-attractor":{"haiku":"Loving someone who destroys themselves; the speaker observes catastrophe unfold while helpless, beauty and blade inseparable in the falling.","opus":"A helpless witness to someone pulled compulsively toward lovely, ruinous things, clutching thorns and calling it love while everything around them rots. Bound and unable to step in, the narrator can only cup their guttering light and wonder whether love is only an injury we go looking for.","sonnet":"A love that leaves scars and costs everything — watched from the outside, hands tied, as someone precious tears themselves apart reaching for the divine, clinging to beauty that breaks them, and love bleeds into death.","src":"haiku"},
"maxime~the-life-and-death-of-a-dog":{"haiku":"Cycles of care and abandonment trace the life of a creature circling toward death. The speaker witnesses and participates in cycles of love and cruelty.","opus":"A vicious extended metaphor casting a person as a beaten, circling dog in a cutthroat world where onlookers crave the spectacle of ruin. Cruelty and pity braid together, and a tender domestic memory of bringing something home only sharpens the sense of an inevitable, humiliating end, loyalty repaid with destruction before a crowd hungry to watch it burn.","sonnet":"A dog metaphor — limping in circles, barking up the wrong tree, bitch in heat, dog-eat-dog world — maps predatory social dynamics where onlookers want to see it burn; a warmer interlude of someone brought home in a daddy's car, sleeping and kicking in dreams, becomes its own kind of elegy before the refrain returns: another dog laying dead.","src":"haiku"},
"maximum-the-hormone~chuchu-lovely-munimuni-muramura-prinprin-boron-nururu-rerorero":{"haiku":"Predatory male aggression and objectification masked in absurdist onomatopoeia and sexual provocation.","opus":"A grotesque satire of a middle-aged predator's sexual pathology, the nonsense chorus masking increasingly monstrous fantasies. The band uses cartoonish excess to expose the ugliness of the leering salaryman rather than indulge it.","sonnet":"A deliberately lewd and provocative romp through Japanese male sexual pathology — middle-aged stalkers, obsessive fantasies catalogued from university students down to kindergarteners, the salaryman's briefcase hiding rope and tape, the eternal race between male and female desire. The cutesy onomatopoeic chorus frames the darkness with ironic sweetness, the absurdism doing nothing to soften the targets of the satire.","src":"haiku","fable":"A candy-coated nonsense hook is deployed as camouflage over a genuinely ugly satire of predatory male desire, so the sweetness itself is the joke and the accusation. Between the babbling chorus of cutesy sound-effects, the verses build a portrait of the burnt-out salaryman as sexual menace: downsized, eating out at Gusto forever, stress from work and stress from paying for it, reduced to synthetic-fetish (vinyl, aluminum, polyester) and stalker fantasy, with the suitcase of rope, gum-tape and box-cutter making the horror literal. The band withholds any moral frame and instead lets the leering voice escalate on its own until a final descending age-list drops the mask entirely, so the song works by making you sing along to something monstrous. The onomatopoeia title is the wrapper; the point is what it is wrapped around.","fv":2,"fnote":"BU-IKIKAESU put this grotesquerie in the canon: the album debuted at number 5 on Oricon and stayed charted for 78 weeks, went RIAJ Gold — and Rolling Stone Japan ranked it 98th on its list of the greatest Japanese rock albums of all time.","themes":["violence & murder","comedy & wordplay","politics & society"],"means":"the sweetness itself is the joke and the accusation","built":"the onomatopoeia title is the candy wrapper around a monstrous interior"},
"maximum-the-hormone~maximum-the-hormone":{"haiku":"Hedonistic defiance of wellness culture—celebrating dietary excess and indulgence despite gout, metabolic disease, and medical warnings; rejecting gym ideology and self-improvement narrative.","opus":"A manic, self-mocking rant about a body breaking down from indulgence, gout and diabetes and kidney stones, staged as a war between gluttony as a way of life and the nagging wellness culture telling him to slim down; he'd rather live fat than obey.","sonnet":"A comedic-furious autobiographical song about a guitarist's struggle with gout and metabolic disease: the narrator enumerates forbidden foods (beer, roe, liver, sea urchin) causing foot pain and abnormal blood values, rages against health culture and workout bros demanding he slim down, and defiantly insists that rich, fatty ramen is a way of life worth dying for.","src":"haiku","fable":"A metabolic-syndrome diagnosis turned into a defiant food anthem: the band inventories its own singer's bloodwork — high blood pressure, bad lipids, uric acid declared dead, a re-test schedule that runs Monday-Tuesday-Wednesday — while a chorus barks HELL over each forbidden pleasure (beer, liver, sea urchin). The joke is the refusal: against the gym, protein powder and squats, the song enshrines koTTeri, rich fatty richness, as a whole way of life, insisting it will out-live the lean and disciplined. But the movement is what earns the length — beneath the bravado sit real fears (kidney stones, dread of ever going back to the clinic) and a bruised sensitivity to being told he looks better thin, that people liked the old him, so the whole rant is partly a shield. It caps the bit by reciting deadpan government health statistics on diabetes, gout and cardiac death, then swatting them away with a shrug of who cares — mask fully back on, the sermon acknowledged and refused in the same breath.","fv":2,"fnote":"The gout is real and the grievance is the band’s own: by the band’s account, Maximum the Ryo-kun was warned by a doctor he would die without a lifestyle change, dieted, got lean — and fans complained they liked him fat. This is the answer song, its video dedicated to all the gouty people, and the closing statistics are lifted from a real 2014 health-ministry survey.","themes":["party & hedonism","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"gluttony enshrined as defiance against wellness culture's verdict","built":"government mortality statistics recited then shrugged off in the same breath"},
"maximum-the-hormone~what-s-up-people":{"haiku":"Japanese lyrics invoke anxiety, uncertainty, and questioning humanity's value while urging urgent global change and warning.","opus":"A frantic Japanese screed on the anxiety of modern human existence (ningen = human), asking whether the convenience-obsessed, crime-ridden species finds any meaning in living; the throughline is existential unease about civilization's instability, the White House and a warning to the whole world, urging humanity to reform its way of life.","sonnet":"A Japanese punk-metal blast about civilisation's anxiety and crime — humans are convenient tools destroying themselves, crime and unease are permanent features of existence, and the song is a screaming indictment of a species that can't figure out why it's alive.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sardonic address to the human species itself, the song runs almost entirely on one repeated word — ningen, humankind — cycling it through mocking suffixes: hurrah for convenient, comfortable humans; then needling questions like is being alive too boring for you?. The verses pun praise against anxiety (sanka against fuanka) until the taxonomy turns openly hostile, gesturing at a sick, unstable civilization and issuing a warning to the whole world with a jab at the White House. The famous English chorus is the point where the taunting mask drops and the sneer becomes plain contempt for the species, the murmured opening self-justification — that humanity became this way to numb its own hurt — recast as an execution of humanity's own delusions. What reads as nonsense chant is really the structure of the joke: humankind chanted at itself until the word curdles.","fv":2,"fnote":"The species-baiting found its biggest stage in an anime: this was Death Note’s second opening theme, running over episodes 20–37, with the band’s own Zetsubou Billy as its paired ending — a mass-market slot handed to a song whose chorus profanely writes off humankind.","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"a species-wide taunt where the chanted word curdles under its own repetition","built":"the English chorus drops the mask the Japanese suffixes had kept politely ironic"},
"maybeshewill~not-for-want-of-trying":{"haiku":"The narrator rejects complacency in a collapsing society and demands public rage as the prerequisite for reclaiming human dignity against systemic decay.","opus":"Maybeshewill, the British post-rock band who built instrumentals around spoken samples, frame their track with the famous Network monologue about a world gone crazy. A litany of homicides from a newscaster and the shrinking comfort of the living room give way to a furious command to get mad and reclaim human value. The plea to fling open a window and shout a refusal to take it anymore drives the piece.","sonnet":"The Network monologue — a broadcaster who refuses to leave his audience alone in their shrinking rooms, demanding they open their windows and scream their rage at a world rotting under depression, crime, and comfortable numbness. The spoken fury stands in for a generation too beaten down to riot but too human to accept the terms.","src":"haiku"},
"maybsomeday~zombie":{"haiku":"Death persists as emotional state; the speaker remains frozen in undead limbo, desperately clinging while already lost.","opus":"A lover left emotionally dead by someone leaving pleads to be brought back to life, waiting forever for a word that never comes. Devotion here is indistinguishable from a slow suicide.","sonnet":"The speaker, emotionally dead and cold-blooded, clings to someone who holds their heart and begs to be brought back to life, refusing to move on even as that person is already leaving — hanging at the edge of being forgotten like a zombie waiting for a resurrection that may never come.","src":"haiku"},
"mazare~generation-gap":{"haiku":"Trapped between systems, watching your mind calcify while the world burns around you.","opus":"Feeling trapped and numbed by a system that fattens people up for slaughter, a young mind rotting under surveillance and inherited stories it can't escape. The refrain names the divide as a cage, ending in the urge to watch it all burn.","sonnet":"About feeling mentally trapped and manipulated by older systems of power and belief that the narrator didn't choose — the generation gap is the chasm between what was handed down (stories, institutions, surveillance) and the desire to burn it all and start over; the brain feels dead from the trap.","src":"haiku"},
"mazare~kingslayer":{"haiku":"Righteous insurrection reclaims power through force and sacrifice, restoring dignity to the dispossessed.","opus":"A drum-and-bass anthem of reclamation, all rise-from-the-ashes bravado. A deposed ruler, once crownless, marches home to seize his throne and force his enemies to kneel, claiming to fight for his people while voices scream in his dreams, vengeance framed as homecoming, a personal grievance swollen to the scale of conquest and cathartic drop.","sonnet":"A deposed ruler returning to reclaim their throne surrounds the city, sets it on fire, and commands total submission — \"I'll make them kneel, I'll make them bow\" — framing the reconquest as both personal and collective justice for their people.","src":"haiku"},
"mazare~throne":{"haiku":"Risk becomes the only path to authenticity, walking the edge of catastrophe to reclaim agency from a predetermined fate that no longer holds power.","opus":"Courting the edge as the only place that feels like living, reframing risk as survival and a dare as a fight worth winning. The refusal to doubt one's fate turns recklessness into a kind of faith in oneself.","sonnet":"A speaker who thrives on being pushed to the edge — treating danger and extreme challenge not as a threat but as the only context in which they feel truly alive, embracing risk and the precipice as their natural state rather than a curse.","src":"haiku"},
"md-45~day-the-music-died":{"haiku":"Greedy bureaucrats kill music by banning it and exploiting artists; musicians imprisoned for their craft survive only in memory.","opus":"A dystopia where song is outlawed and performers are jailed, their gifts drained by money-hungry officials fixated on numbers and revenue, though the melodies live on inside the fugitive's mind. MD.45, Dave Mustaine's mid-90s detour from Megadeth, casts the end of music as a fiction that cannot hold, unbowed even from a cell.","sonnet":"A dystopian reckoning with the corporate strangling of artistic life: music is outlawed, talent is bled dry, and bureaucrats drunk on profit bury everything that once gave culture meaning. Even imprisoned, the narrator keeps the music alive internally — an act of defiance against the silence commerce demands.","src":"haiku"},
"md-45~hell-s-motel":{"haiku":"A weary soul trapped in regret and moral compromise hopes for redemption but stays trapped in the motel of a cursed life.","opus":"MD.45, the side project of Megadeth's Dave Mustaine, sketches a ruined former surgeon whose trembling hands betray his dependence, a man who bartered his gifts away for nothing. He begs only for escape rather than an endless withered existence, and the shabby motel becomes an emblem of universal human decline.","sonnet":"An old man in a declining room — a former surgeon whose hands now shake, whose past mistakes have calcified into permanent residents alongside him. He prays for morning and forgiveness but the walls are thin, and the noise next door confirms that this is not a place where grace arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"mecano~hijo-de-la-luna":{"haiku":"A gypsy mother exchanges her firstborn to the moon for romantic partnership, bearing an albino child as evidence of her bargain while patriarchal violence follows.","opus":"A Spanish folk-legend ballad about a gypsy woman who bargains with the moon for a husband and pays with her firstborn, an albino child her lover reads as proof of betrayal. It's a tragedy about jealousy and otherness, the moon cradling a child no one else would claim.","sonnet":"A Romani legend: a woman bargains with the full moon for a husband, promising her firstborn in return. The child is born white and silver-eyed, not resembling the father; the man kills his wife in jealous rage and abandons the baby on a mountain. The moon takes the child and the phases of the moon are explained by whether the child cries.","src":"haiku"},
"mechanical-vein~all-gods-fall-down-zardonic-remix":{"haiku":"Religious authority and false deities expose themselves as man-made constructs designed to manipulate and enslave; the song calls for rejection of these systems and embrace of human strength over divine servitude.","opus":"Militant anti-theist fury given industrial drum-and-bass teeth in Zardonic's remix. It frames religion as a con run by charlatans with split tongues, morality built on inherited lies. The call is to reject counterfeit gods and embrace human strength instead, watching every deity break apart on impact. Liberation through the demolition of handed-down faith.","sonnet":"A rejection of organised religion as control technology: authority dresses itself as divine, words drip dark from the master's maw, and the crowd is led blind in the name of something that speaks with a forked tongue — the answer is to embrace humanity's own strength and watch all gods shatter like glass when they hit the ground.","src":"haiku"},
"mechatok~expression-on-your-face":{"haiku":"Performance and alienation mask internal decay while the speaker observes others' judgment from outside belonging. Expression becomes the only currency, and authenticity becomes dangerous.","opus":"A dissociated wanderer moves through nightlife feeling watched yet untouchable, reading everything from a stranger's face, half in love with and half sickened by the scene. A sardonic aside mourning pop stars fallen off the charts hints at the singer's own precarious place in it all.","sonnet":"An outsider at a party or venue moves through the crowd untouched — \"I'm touched by nobody, I show affection to the floor\" — watching pop-stars fall off charts and disco soldiers go to war, the expression on someone's face the only thing that keeps catching their eye in a world reduced to wrong tastes and thin lines.","src":"haiku"},
"meek-oh-why~kim-ja-jestem":{"haiku":"A person wrestles with internal contradiction between ambitious, worldly desires and the wish to escape into love and simplicity. The song explores fractured identity and conflicting desires.","opus":"Polish artist Meek Oh Why turns a hip-hop confessional into a study of a divided self, repeatedly asking who he is and concluding there are probably two of him. One version repairs the other's mistakes; one dreams of meeting people, touring, and giving strangers joy, while the other is a vain, dishonest fool chasing luxury. He craves talk of music and words over empty arguments about governments.","sonnet":"A Polish narrator interrogates his own dual nature — a version who means well and a version who fails, lies, and craves status he claims to reject. He asks who he really is with increasing frequency, finding only that both halves exist simultaneously and the gap between promise and action keeps widening.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title question — who am I — is answered structurally rather than lyrically: there are probably two of me, and the whole song is built to let the first watch the second. Meek gladly identifies with the self that repairs the other's mistakes and erases his empty sentences, which quietly admits the second self is the one who says he'll come back and never does, who won't even return his own apologies. The second verse splits the difference between two futures — earning a fortune only to scatter it from the tallest building in town for strangers' joy, versus fleeing to warm countries with a beloved to escape petty friction — before the vain, dishonest self crashes in playing Dan Bilzerian, ordering the most expensive dish just because he can. What makes the confession ring true is the outro, where the divided man finds his one constant: not virtue but taste, a weariness at drunken pundits who all become Angela Merkel, a wish to talk instead about music and the magic of words. The self-portrait resolves into the only honest self-knowledge on offer — that he doesn't know which of him is real, but he knows exactly which conversations he can no longer stand.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"a self-portrait that resolves into knowing which conversations the self can no longer stand","built":"the song is structured as one self watching the other, the split announced in the title's own question"},
"meek-oh-why~radiobus":{"haiku":"A bus radio bombards passengers with inane ads, fake news, and promotional drivel, building a inescapable stream of manipulative, vapid content.","opus":"A Polish rapper trapped on a bus flooded by mindless radio noise, ads, tragedy and disco-polo dripping into his brain as involuntary mental sludge. The real subject is guarding your consciousness against random media garbage before it quietly becomes your own thoughts.","sonnet":"A commuter trapped on a bus without headphones catalogues the relentless noise pollution of commercial radio — ads, celebrity gossip, road deaths, economic crisis — and argues that this constant media junk actively colonises your mind if you're not consciously guarding against it.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~1-320": {"haiku":"Pure drag-racing adrenaline; supercharged machines and human will fuse in a quarter-mile sprint toward the finish line. Nitro-fueled ecstasy and speed transcend consciousness.","opus":"A pure adrenaline ode to drag racing, capturing the white-knuckle, nitro-fueled rush from the starting light to the 1,320-foot finish line (the length of a quarter mile); it's the sheer physical high of man and machine screaming toward speed with no larger meaning than the need for it.","sonnet":"A drag racing song — 1320 feet to the finish line, nitromethane burning, 8000 horses igniting — the narrator in a white-knuckle trance of pure speed, the whole song existing as an adrenaline document of a quarter-mile run.","src":"haiku","fable":"A quarter-mile of tarmac, measured to the exact foot of its title, becomes the whole world here: teeth clenched, knuckles bleached on the wheel, the whole nervous system flooded before the green even drops. Megadeth build the track as a countdown, all coiled potential in the staging lane, then let it detonate into nitro-methane smoke and scorched rubber, the driver strapped into 8,000 horsepower and muttering a prayer. The payoff isn't arriving anywhere but vanishing — flesh and chassis fused into one screaming thing until the pilot simply dissolves into the air, spent by the very acceleration he chased.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"flesh and chassis fused until the driver dissolves into acceleration","built":"the song builds as a countdown whose payoff is vanishing rather than arriving"},
"megadeth~13":{"haiku":"Recurring cycles of pain and survival accumulate across a lifetime, with death faced repeatedly yet escape remaining impossible despite all effort and endurance.","opus":"Mustaine wrestling with his own persistence through a life stoked by rage and untended scars, treating the number thirteen as both hex and odd fortune. The chorus, tallying how often he must perish, sketches a man ground down yet still on his feet.","sonnet":"A survivor's reckoning across thirteen albums and a lifetime of music, the speaker measuring how many times he has nearly been destroyed — by the industry, by himself, by the past — and insisting he is still standing.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~44-minutes": {"haiku":"A 44-minute bank robbery and shootout overwhelms police in a firefight; two armed men outgun standard-issue weapons until SWAT brings them down.","opus":"A detailed retelling of the 1997 North Hollywood shootout, where two heavily armored, automatic-weapon-wielding bank robbers outgunned police for 44 minutes until SWAT arrived; the deeper read frames it as an avoidable disaster caused by a city that underfunded its own firepower.","sonnet":"A detailed retelling of the 1997 North Hollywood bank shootout — two heavily armed robbers in body armor pin down police with automatic weapons for 44 minutes, exposing how badly outgunned standard law enforcement was against military-grade firepower.","src":"haiku","fable":"A near-documentary retelling of the 1997 North Hollywood shootout, opening on actual police-dispatch chatter before the band even plays, so the song arrives already inside a real emergency. The verses stay clinical and journalistic — the failed truck-heist that sent the pair to a bank instead, the head-to-toe homemade armor that shrugged off service rounds, the outgunned officers — and the horror comes precisely from that flatness, from stating that a city talked itself out of adequate firepower and then had to live the consequence. The trick of the song is the chorus, which reaches for scripture and myth to describe two robbers: a poured-out vessel of divine fury, an Achilles heel, a beast finally brought down. Megadeth isn't ennobling the gunmen so much as measuring the disproportion — a service pistol against military hardware forces the language of gods and monsters, and the mock-epic register is the only one big enough to hold forty-four minutes of it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title comes from the documentary 44 Minutes: The North Hollywood Shoot-Out, and the production carries the subject into the drums — Mustaine layered an AK-47 report into the snare sound, calling it the song's gun salute.","themes":["violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"the disproportion of service pistol against military hardware forces mythic register","built":"actual dispatch chatter opens the track so the song arrives already inside the emergency"},
"megadeth~502": {"haiku":"A driver finds freedom and escape through reckless highway driving, chasing sensation while breaking laws, until caught by police for speeding.","opus":"Megadeth throw themselves into reckless velocity, the narrator flush with gas and cash, ripping across highways from dusk to dawn with the open road as his only guide. Speed becomes intoxication and lawbreaking a cure for boredom, right up until a traffic stop for drunk driving turns the euphoric freedom into its own kind of trap.","sonnet":"A night driver chases the freedom of open road — the interstate as escape from stasis, speed as feeling alive. Cigarettes, caffeine, and alcohol sustain the run until a sobriety stop ends it: the law catching up to someone who had been romanticising lawbreaking as the only honest way to feel something.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is a police radio code for driving under the influence, and the whole song is built so you don't clock that until the siren catches up. On the surface it's a pure open-road high — cash in the pocket, the interstate unspooling, speed as the only cure for having nothing to do — sold with the reckless glee Mustaine specialized in during Megadeth's ragged, chemically-fueled late-eighties peak. But the fuel is literal: the road diet of cigarettes, caffeine, and alcohol is what's actually driving, and the freedom the verses keep celebrating is the tunnel vision of a wired, drunk man who has convinced himself motion is escape. The final turn drops the bravado and names it hell, so the song is the gap between the two: a joyride anthem that is quietly the confession of the thing it's whooping about, with the closing threat that the next one caught could be you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape"],"means":"the joyride anthem quietly confesses the thing it whoops about","built":"the police code of the title is withheld until the siren catches up at the close"},
"megadeth~99-ways-to-die": {"haiku":"A suicidal ideation and psychological deterioration where the speaker confronts multiple means of self-harm while trapped in a hospital-like setting.","opus":"Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash institution, apply manic wordplay and speed to genuine despair. The narrator runs through a blackly comic inventory of ways to end his own life, poised at the edge again and again, while a recurring line about the world not being ready for him keeps yanking him back. Beneath the gallows humor is real, unrelenting hopelessness.","sonnet":"A first-person account of suicidal ideation told with grim dark humor — the speaker rehearses methods, mocks their own cowardice, and hears a sardonic voice telling them death is not ready for them yet. The absurdity only sharpens the desperation underneath.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine writes from inside a suicidal, sedated head, stacking methods and dark-comic props — the arsenic, the noose, the gun already tasted — into a catalogue of exits narrated with a grinning thrash swagger that mistakes despair for bravado. The genius is the chorus, which never belongs to the speaker: it is the pushback of whatever isn't ready to let him go, so every verse's rush toward an exit slams into a refusal that keeps dragging him back to try again. What sounds like a boast about ways out is really a man losing the argument, the refusal simply repeating over him until the number stops meaning options and starts meaning attempts.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Cut in 1992 for The Beavis and Butt-Head Experience compilation, of all places — and it earned a Grammy nomination anyway.","themes":["suicide","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the catalogue of exits slams into a refusal that keeps dragging him back","built":"the chorus voice is never the speaker's — it is the pushback refusing to let him go"},
"megadeth~a-secret-place": {"haiku":"An inescapable, faceless sanctuary where the lost soul retreats; once entered, no one can leave, trapping the narrator in hiding from the outside world.","opus":"A narrator retreats to a hidden interior refuge where faces don't show and no one who enters gets out, a place he cannot explain reaching yet returns to after losing his soul. Others always flee. Mustaine, long open about addiction and inner torment, turns this secret place into an ambiguous sanctuary that doubles as a trap he can't quite escape.","sonnet":"A dissociated inner refuge both shelters and traps its inhabitant — a hidden zone where the lost self takes cover but no one who enters quite manages to leave. The speaker cannot account for how he arrived, and visitors invariably flee rather than stay in the dark company he keeps there.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sanctuary offered like hospitality that turns out to be a trap: the hideaway welcomes anyone who wants to stay, yet the faces there stay hidden and the shout is that no one comes back. Mustaine, whose lyrics circle addiction and the mind's private hells throughout his catalogue, lets the refuge and the prison be the same room — the place where a soul goes missing is also the place he insists he found it again, in disgrace. The plea that opens each return, asking to be let in and gotten out in the same breath, is the whole state of mind: retreat that cannot tell rescue from confinement.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Mustaine's own gloss is that the secret place is inside his head — a man losing touch with reality — though the song was cut in a stone castle studio that was once an Al Capone getaway, where he swore he heard footsteps on the roof.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind"],"means":"the refuge and the prison are the same room","built":"the plea to be let in and gotten out arrives in the same breath each return"},
"megadeth~a-tout-le-monde": {"haiku":"Death as release and message: the speaker departs knowing their body's gone but meaning lingers, a final statement freeing themselves through absence.","opus":"Mustaine writes a dying person's farewell, not a suicide note but last words meant to release the speaker and comfort those left behind. The dead are past all pain while the living carry the scars, so he asks only that his friends smile.","sonnet":"A song written from the perspective of someone dying — a goodbye letter to friends asking them to smile rather than grieve, with the French refrain declaring love for everyone and the necessity of departure, framing death as the thing that will finally set them free.","src":"haiku","fable":"A voice speaks from the far side of its own death, drafting a note to friends it has already left. The turn to French — to everyone, to all my friends, I love you, I must go — softens the register into something like a wave from a departing train, so the goodbye lands as tenderness rather than dread. What sharpens it is the accounting underneath: a life measured and found thin, plans that never left the ground, and the recognition that the crossing costs the one who goes nothing, while everyone still breathing carries the mark.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Born of a dream in which Mustaine's dead mother came back to say she loved him; he has always rejected the suicide-song reading — MTV banned the video as pro-suicide anyway, and it later aired with hotline advisories.","themes":["death & grief","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"the crossing costs the one who leaves nothing while the living carry the mark","built":"the turn to French softens the register into a wave from a departing train"},
"megadeth~a-tout-le-monde-radio-edit": {"haiku":"Life as game reveals its rules too late; regret shadows accomplishments while farewells acknowledge the speaker's isolation and incomplete legacy.","opus":"Dave Mustaine wrote this as a farewell he imagined his own mother might have spoken, and it became Megadeth's most tender song, a dying person's last message to everyone they love. It insists on being remembered with a smile rather than grief, the sleeping feeling no more pain while the living carry the scars. Thrash softened into elegy.","sonnet":"A farewell letter from someone who has realised life was a game they played too seriously, addressed to friends and the world: the French refrain — \"to all the world, to all my friends, I love you, I must leave\" — frames departure as liberation, asking only to be remembered with a smile.","src":"haiku","fable":"A voice speaks from the far side of its own death, drafting a note to friends it has already left. The turn to French — to everyone, to all my friends, I love you, I must go — softens the register into something like a wave from a departing train, so the goodbye lands as tenderness rather than dread. What sharpens it is the accounting underneath: a life measured and found thin, plans that never left the ground, and the recognition that the crossing costs the one who goes nothing, while everyone still breathing carries the mark.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Born of a dream in which Mustaine's dead mother came back to say she loved him; he has always rejected the suicide-song reading — MTV banned the video as pro-suicide anyway, and it later aired with hotline advisories.","themes":["death & grief","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"the crossing costs the one who leaves nothing while the living carry the mark","built":"the turn to French softens the register into a wave from a departing train"},
"megadeth~addicted-to-chaos": {"haiku":"Isolation and abandonment fuel addiction to the chaos and turmoil of unpredictability; no helping hand appears and the speaker spirals alone.","opus":"Mustaine writes about mourning a lost companion who once foresaw his loneliness, then turns the loss inward. The real subject is a man who confuses grief with withdrawal, realizing he doesn't just miss the person but craves the turmoil their absence unleashes, hooked on upheaval itself.","sonnet":"A song about grief and dependency following the death of a close friend — the narrator is undone by the loss, clings to memories and chaos as substitutes for the person's absence, and slowly pulls himself back toward the road.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man bargains with an absence, cataloguing his cravings and injuries as if the ache were proof he still needs whoever walked out on him. But the chorus quietly reframes the diagnosis: the dependency itself is the fix, and the turmoil he keeps stoking is the real habit. Verse two tries to sell the wreckage as raw material, converting collapse into stepping stones and welcoming the storm as a road worth taking, while the prophecy of eventual solitude returns to close the argument he can't win.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The absence is real: Mustaine wrote it for the drug counselor who got him sober — the man who had warned him that one day he'd have to cut him loose, and who then died of a heroin overdose himself.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"the turmoil being stoked is the real habit not the absent person","built":"verse two converts collapse into stepping stones, reframing wreckage as raw material"},
"megadeth~almost-honest": {"haiku":"A relationship dissolves through small lies and half-truths; both partners compromised and lost themselves in deception.","opus":"A rueful post-breakup confession that neither partner was ever fully truthful, trading half-lies and hollow vows of need and love. Now solitary and unraveling in the small hours, still haunted by her image, the narrator longs to make amends but finds nothing there, unable to say who wrecked a romance built on mutual near-honesty.","sonnet":"A post-breakup song about two people who were almost truthful with each other but not quite — the relationship was built on small lies and half-sincerities on both sides, and now the speaker is alone at 4am unable to face what's left.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup accounting done in fractions, where every confession is graded down to nearly, semi, kind of, until both people stand exposed as people who told the truth partway and lied the rest. What starts as mutual, almost-comic hedging curdles once she leaves: the sleepless dark before dawn, the wanting to make amends with no one left to receive them, the pull toward a phone call he knows he should end and can't. The refrain keeps stalling one syllable short of honest, and that withheld last word becomes the whole indictment — a love that never fully committed to being real. Fitting for Megadeth's radio-reaching late-nineties turn, Mustaine trades thrash velocity for slow self-implication, the accusation aimed outward swinging back to land on the singer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"confession graded down until nothing remains real","built":"refrain stalls one syllable short of its final word"},
"megadeth~amerikhastan": {"haiku":"A dystopian vision of a nation divided by religious extremism, corporate power, and endless war leading to its own destruction.","opus":"Dave Mustaine's post-9/11 tirade about the war on terror, imagining a fused America and Middle Eastern world where zealotry and spin are twin faces of one engineered war. Its dark conclusion casts deity pitted against deity as the ruin of mankind.","sonnet":"A dystopian vision of America destroying itself through religious warfare and imperial overreach — the country becomes 'Amerikhastan,' a theocratic ruin born from its own foreign policy, with corporate ownership stamped on the Statue of Liberty.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine's post-9/11 satire runs on portmanteau geography — New Yorqatar, Califarabia, a homeland collapsing into the Central Asian -stan it claims to be fighting — arguing the war on terror is a mirror that turns America into its enemy. The chorus names the mechanism as God versus God, a holy war with no winning side where both zealotries are the same undoing of man, and the second verse drops the irony to voice the hawk's fantasy straight: switch off the news, send in the Mossad, erase the Middle East entirely. The venom lands when Lady Liberty is stripped and branded as Halliburton's subsidiary property — the crusade's real author revealed as the war economy — and Mustaine leaves the closing jingoist taunt standing unanswered, so you hear how thin the line is between righteous and monstrous.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","war & battle","faith & the occult"],"means":"the crusade's real author revealed as the war economy","built":"portmanteau geography performs the mirror logic the lyric argues"},
"megadeth~anarchy-in-the-u-k": {"haiku":"An antichrist and anarchist rejects societal control and meaningful direction, wanting only chaos, noise, and the destruction of order.","opus":"Megadeth covering the Sex Pistols' nihilist manifesto, the sneering pose of destruction for its own sake against a consumer culture whose only dream is a shopping spree. Punk's original provocation filtered through thrash, still gleefully contemptuous of everything.","sonnet":"A cover of the Sex Pistols track that is purely about nihilistic refusal — the narrator identifies as an antichrist and anarchist who wants to destroy the system not for any ideology but simply because destruction is the only authentic act left.","src":"haiku","fable":"Punk's original sneer was a real threat dressed as nihilism — a body wanting to smash the future and finding it can only picture that future as a shopping spree, the destruction curdling into the very consumerism it hates. Megadeth cover it straight, and the move is the joke: a virtuoso American thrash band, all technique and control, playing a song whose whole premise is amateur chaos and the will to destroy. What was menace becomes homage, the anarchist pose flattened into a genre gesture — and Mustaine even swaps the target from the U.K. to the USA, keeping the froth while the danger drains out, which is exactly the point about what happens when rebellion gets studio-tight and marketed back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Mustaine has admitted he could never make out Johnny Rotten's words and invented what he couldn't catch — the NME became the enemy — and the Pistols' own Steve Jones plays the second solo.","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","politics & society"],"means":"virtuoso control performing the case against control","built":"target swapped from UK to USA, keeping the froth"},
"megadeth~architecture-of-aggression": {"haiku":"Great nations are built on bones of the dead through war and violence; the speaker praises aggression as the architecture that constructs empires.","opus":"An anatomy of warmongering, portraying nations founded on corpses that gauge their greatness by how much fear they inspire. Megadeth expose the double-faced logic of power, tenderness on one cheek and mass slaughter on the other, tracing the whole cycle to its endpoint in mutual annihilation.","sonnet":"The mechanics of empire-building through war: nations built on bones, leaders killed to create power vacuums, betrayal on one side and genocide on the other — the enemy's praise of your military architecture is the only validation that matters.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is Megadeth stripping empire down to its engineering: nations raised not on ideals but on corpses used as building material, mud and straw swapped for blood and bone. The chilling move is that title phrase, which frames war as a craft to be admired — Mustaine reads conquest through its enemies' grudging praise, so cruelty becomes a measure of worth rather than shame. Between the verses the image-system shifts from the individual predator hunting under cover of dark to the geopolitical scale of toppled leaders and power vacuums, tracing how the same logic runs from the assassin to the state. The two-faced ruler who offers a kiss on one cheek and genocide on the other is the whole thesis compressed: the aggression is the architecture, and everyone left standing is invited to salute it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Mustaine tied it to Saddam Hussein and the Gulf War just ended — the toppled-leader verse had a live referent when the album shipped.","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","violence & murder"],"means":"cruelty reframed as craft worth admiring","built":"title phrase casts slaughter as engineering to be saluted"},
"megadeth~ashes-in-your-mouth": {"haiku":"War and violence are rewritten as heroic vindication, but victory tastes bitter when confronted with mountains of corpses and moral emptiness.","opus":"Mustaine's disgust at war and rewritten history, plows beaten back into swords and scripture shunned for weapons, arguing that even victory tastes like nothing once you count the corpses it required.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic meditation on war and its aftermath — soldiers laying mines instead of liberating people, the dead piling up, and the bitter revelation that whatever victory was claimed turns to ashes the moment you taste it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A battlefield panorama that keeps sliding from the physical carnage into the machinery of justification behind it: plowshares beaten back into swords, scripture shoved aside for the tools of war, victors busy rewriting the record while the corpses are still fresh. The verses catalogue what war does; the chorus abandons the reporting for a stunned, circular question about where any of it leads and whether the answer even matters. Megadeth wrote this at the height of Mustaine's Cold-War-fallout preoccupations on this record, and the song's real argument lands in its title turn: the sweetness of winning, of vindication, curdles the instant it is tasted. What looks like a war anthem is actually an autopsy of victory, insisting that being proven right by force leaves only ash where the triumph should be.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","faith & the occult"],"means":"victory tasted and found to be ash","built":"verses report war; chorus abandons reporting for stunned circularity"},
"megadeth~back-in-the-day":{"haiku":"A celebration of the glory days of metal music and the cultural movement it inspired, where unity and raw power transformed the world.","opus":"A veteran's toast to heavy metal's early days, casting the scene's uniform and its regional strongholds as one unstoppable movement outsiders could never grasp. Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash pioneers who helped build that world, write themselves into its origin story, offering the sweat and adrenaline of those shows as proof of belonging.","sonnet":"A nostalgia hymn for the early metal underground — from San Francisco out to New Jersey the era is invoked as genuinely transformative, and the song insists that anyone who was not there simply cannot understand what it felt like when the music changed everything.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~bad-omen": {"haiku":"A satanic ritual descends into bloodshed and possession; those who worship the devil for his blessings are cursed instead, losing their souls and minds to demonic forces.","opus":"A lurid narrative of a satanic black mass, worshippers invoking Baphomet under a full moon with virgin sacrifice, blood and orgiastic rites; the twist is that the Devil betrays his followers, their bodies left soulless corpses, a cautionary tale that serving evil brings only doom.","sonnet":"A Black Sabbath cover depicting a satanic ritual — worshippers gather under a full moon to sacrifice virgins, drink and dance for the Devil, who possesses them; the ceremony curses them and their bodies become soulless corpses, with no one able to save them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A black mass staged with the lurid literalism of a horror poster: stars drowning in the sea, a Baphomet incantation, a full moon, then a ceremony that curdles into blood-spilling and forfeited souls. Early Megadeth built its shock currency out of exactly this Satanic pageantry, and the song reports it step by step without a wink — no irony, no distancing narrator, just the rite escalating. The real turn is structural: the worshippers keep waiting on their master's blessing, and the reward arrives as its exact opposite, their own bodies reduced to corpses from the grave. What lingers is the outro, where the verdict on their fate hardens into flat repetition and then mutates mid-line from being beyond rescue into being beyond understanding — the machinery grinding on after the meaning has been stripped out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"worshippers receive their reward as its exact opposite","built":"outro verdict hardens then mutates mid-line from doomed to incomprehensible"},
"megadeth~bite-the-hand": {"haiku":"Corporate and financial elites plunder the people's trust and resources, leaving ordinary folk desperate and bitter enough to turn on their exploiters.","opus":"A furious response to the 2008 financial collapse, Wall Street and bailouts framed as an economy's assisted suicide, the betrayed public turned into the rabid dog finally biting the hand that robbed it.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of Wall Street and financial institutions that destroyed ordinary people's lives — the banks took everything, Lady Justice was gagged, and the narrator is left with nothing but rage at the hand that was supposed to feed him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A crash-era indictment that opens with the scales of justice muzzled and hooded, so the courts can't witness what Mustaine paints as a state-signed death warrant for the economy. The verses descend from that boardroom abstraction into a stripped apartment where vermin lick the shelves clean of frozen dinners and beer, the wreckage of ordinary savings turned to scavenger's food. The closing turn twists inward, curdling the public disaster into a betrayed narrator's private revenge fantasy, one shot promised as release from every year of misplaced faith.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","money & the street","anger & defiance"],"means":"public disaster curdling into private revenge fantasy","built":"boardroom abstraction descends to vermin on empty shelves"},
"megadeth~black-curtains": {"haiku":"An apocalyptic nightmare descends as fire and chaos consume the world, trapping survivors in inescapable fear and darkness.","opus":"An apocalyptic panic attack rendered as literal end-times: a narrator watching the sky crumble, flesh boiling, hunted by snakes and dogs while something crawls under the skin and tempts him toward violence. The black curtain becomes both a falling stage-drape and death itself, never-ending. Megadeth built their thrash on exactly this fusion of Cold War dread and personal derangement, Mustaine's paranoia turned into fast, technical menace.","sonnet":"A nightmare of apocalyptic collapse — the sky falling, flesh burning, blood metallic on the tongue — rendered from the inside of someone who can't escape and has stopped trying. The song moves from desperate flight to paralysis to a kind of doomed acceptance, smiling at fate while snakes offer their kisses and the curtain descends on everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man narrates his own end from inside it, scanning a sky that caves in before the horror turns inward: skin scalding off, a copper taste flooding his mouth, legs that lock up while dogs close the distance. The pivot in the spoken aside is what earns the dread — the terror stops being something done to him and becomes something crawling under his own skin, a paranoia that tempts him to turn on the other person, so the never-ending refrain works as the panic-loop itself rather than any resolution. Fitting mid-nineties Megadeth, where Mustaine trades speed-thrash sprawl for tight, hook-driven menace, the affliction is deliberately never diagnosed; the narrator answers an unstoppable fate not by pleading but by grinning at it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"paranoia turning the narrator against the only other person","built":"never-ending refrain functions as the panic loop itself"},
"megadeth~blackmail-the-universe":{"haiku":"A soldier abandoned by his government and betrayed by leadership grapples with trauma and revenge, while world powers threaten nuclear annihilation over political disputes.","opus":"A political nightmare set off by the president's plane being downed and the country on high alert, then following a stranded leader raging that rescue will never come while global tensions reignite. Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash institution known for paranoid war narratives, escalate the scenario into nuclear apocalypse and a case for force over compromise.","sonnet":"A geopolitical thriller framed as a soldier's nightmare — Air Force One is down, the nation is at DEFCON 3, and a forgotten prisoner-of-war watches medals turn to dust while the world tilts toward retaliation. The song debates appeasement versus force and ends on the image of earth itself becoming another region of hell when men choose war.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~blessed-are-the-dead": {"haiku":"Biblical apocalyptic imagery of the four horsemen bringing judgment, war, and suffering to humanity.","opus":"An end-times vision pulled from scripture, four riders bringing war, hunger, disease and atomic frost to blast the world clean; the narrator clings to his convictions as the destruction descends, deeming the departed fortunate because rest belongs only to them.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic retelling of Revelation's Four Horsemen — pestilence, war, famine, and death riding out to end the world — framed as something the narrator is waiting for, holding firm in faith until their name is carved in stone.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine sets Revelation's Four Horsemen loose over a modern battlefield, translating conquest, war, famine and death into the older Megadeth vocabulary of nuclear winter and a world scorched for generations. What lifts it past pageantry is the first-person turn: between the color-coded riders the speaker asks to be washed in blood and made the firstborn of the dead, then holds to his faith until his name is cut in stone — a personal salvation plea folded into the end of everything. Read through Mustaine's mid-2000s born-again conversion, the title's beatitude is sincere rather than ironic: he genuinely counts the dead as blessed because they get to rest, and the chorus's plea for the horsemen to ride is a prayer for the apocalypse to arrive and end the waiting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","war & battle","death & grief"],"means":"a personal salvation plea folded into the end of everything","built":"the beatitude is sincere because only the dead get to rest"},
"megadeth~blood-of-heroes": {"haiku":"Soldiers persevere through adversity, claiming they refuse to die or retreat because heroes never abandon their missions.","opus":"Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's technical thrash institution, deliver a cocky battle cry of survival and immortality, narrated by hardened underdogs who keep cheating death by the narrowest margin. Threatening anyone who crosses them and refusing to back down, they harden pure swagger into a creed: real heroes simply refuse to die.","sonnet":"A battle-hardened unit that has been hunted down every dead end and still refuses to die celebrates its own indestructibility. Heroes in this telling are not noble figures but survivalists who outlast everything thrown at them through sheer refusal. The energy is martial and exultant, powered by the conviction that those with the blood of heroes simply do not stay fallen.","src":"haiku","fable":"A battered legion counts itself among the walking wounded and swaggers anyway, cracking that punctuality is for cowards while it marches full-tilt toward whatever wants to finish it off. What holds the boast together is a wager against mortality: that courage leaves a residue in the veins of whoever comes after, so the man who falls stays somehow in the fight. Megadeth plays it straight here, no smirk under the bravado, the survivor's grin of a band that spent the decade being written off and kept showing up.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief"],"means":"courage wagered as residue surviving the fallen body"},
"megadeth~burnt-ice": {"haiku":"A drug addict spirals into paranoia, broken by addiction and lies, racing toward death on a dangerous path they cannot escape.","opus":"An unflinching portrait of methamphetamine addiction, a man who swore he'd only dabble now selling his soul for the next hit, hunted by paranoia and shadow people down a spiral he knows leads only to death.","sonnet":"A portrait of meth addiction's endgame — a man who swore he wouldn't end up like the others, now face-down, paranoid, seeing shadow people, with his family's memories barely audible through the fog of his destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A methamphetamine addiction told in the third person, which is the whole trick of it: Mustaine, years clean by 2007 and long public about his own habits, keeps the addict at arm's length as a nameless he, so the song can watch a man be devoured without ever admitting the seat is warm. It moves as a descent — the opening bargain of moderation — the vow of never becoming one of them, then the escalating price where a soul is currency for one more bowl, then the collapse where family and a ringing phone are things he can no longer face. The title fuses the drug's two poses, the burn of the high and the cold numbness after, into one image of a man riding at speed on something that cannot hold his weight. By the last verse the reading isn't required at all: the shadow people at his shoulder and the growing paranoia are the mind eating itself, and the song simply stops there with him, offering no rescue because there isn't one on offer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind"],"means":"a man watched from arm's length devouring himself by degrees","built":"third-person distance kept so the narrator never admits the seat is warm"},
"megadeth~captive-honour": {"haiku":"A violent criminal imprisoned for life faces dehumanizing conditions and sexual assault in prison, learning that captive honor is a contradiction—there is no honor in captivity.","opus":"A courtroom-to-cell narrative about how prison degrades a man and about the sliding morality of killing, where scale turns a murderer into a hero and then a god. The convict, locked away for life, is stripped of dignity and assaulted behind bars until he begs to die, proving that dignity taken by force is no dignity at all.","sonnet":"A man enters prison and immediately loses all dignity — his honour means nothing inside, and the song details the brutal reality of incarceration including rape by other inmates, framed around the irony that killing one person makes you a murderer but killing millions makes you a god.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song moves through three registers: an abstract meditation on how repetition dulls atrocity into something ordinary, a courtroom transcript handing down a life sentence, and then the raw aftermath where that verdict lands not as justice but as sexual predation behind the walls. Its central irony is the title's own contradiction, that pride held under captivity is no pride at all, driving a bitter thesis that scaling bloodshed launders it upward from crime to conquest to godhood, while the same arithmetic reverses inside the cell, where a man who traded on toughness is stripped of every claim to it. The rising body count that crowns one figure is mirrored by the reckoning that unmakes another, two ledgers of power and its absence set against each other as a single argument.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The convict pleading in the courtroom sketch is drummer Nick Menza's own voice — the Life?! I ain't got a life! that answers the judge.","themes":["violence & murder","war & battle","shame & repression"],"means":"bloodshed at scale laundered upward from crime to godhood","built":"two ledgers of power mirrored — rising body count against a man unmade behind walls"},
"megadeth~chosen-ones": {"haiku":"A warrior hero journeys through deadly trials, defeating monsters and evil, believing he is destined to triumph through courage and faith.","opus":"A blood-soaked fantasy of warriors marching across fields blanketed in human bone to face a vicious beast, raising a holy pilgrim's shield as heaven's power fills their arms, then twisting into a conqueror's boast to pillage and reign. Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash-metal titans, put this on their 1985 debut, all Dungeons-and-Dragons carnage and galloping speed, an early blueprint for their fusion of technical fury and dark mythic swagger.","sonnet":"A battle-field warrior's credo delivered to those who doubt their own nerve — the terrain is lethal, the creature waiting is vicious beyond any previous contest, but the speaker walks through it in light and without flinching. The tone shifts once the beast is slain, possession taking over: the land now belongs to the victor by right of conquest, and mercy has been left somewhere on the field.","src":"haiku","fable":"A speaker dares the faint-hearted to turn back, then leads the way across a bone-strewn field toward a waiting beast, casting himself as heaven-armed and marching in daylight. He hoists a consecrated warrior's crest skyward and calls the charge divinely appointed — three verses of righteous-crusader swagger played straight. Then the mask drops: the same chosen hero announces he has come to torch the land, force himself on it, seize what he wants, and stay only to kill. The loop back to the opening dare is the joke, since the pious battle cry and the naked hunger to conquer prove to be one speech, holiness just the flattering story plunder tells about itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Band-confirmed in the reissue booklet by both Mustaine and Ellefson: the vicious beast with the sharp pointy teeth is the Killer Rabbit of Caerbannog from Monty Python and the Holy Grail.","themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","violence & murder"],"means":"crusader's holy charge revealed as the same speech as plunder","built":"loop back to opening dare collapses piety and conquest into one"},
"megadeth~countdown-to-extinction": {"haiku":"Wealthy hunters kill endangered species for sport and trophy, committing ecological murder disguised as legitimate sport. The song condemns fake safari killing and its pretense.","opus":"Mustaine turns his fury on canned-hunt ranches, where trophy animals are shot in enclosures with no chance of escape, and widens it into an indictment of mankind driving species off the planet for the sake of ego.","sonnet":"An indictment of canned trophy hunting — wealthy people shooting endangered animals in staged environments for bragging rights — presenting it as cowardly, dishonest, and directly driving species to extinction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A penned animal is sold a fair fight and then shot from a few feet off, the trigger reframed as proof of manhood and the carcass reduced to a rare pelt and a mounted head. The song names the ritual by its trade term, a canned safari, and spends its scorn on the gap between the shooter's borrowed courage and the fenced helplessness of the target, who was handed no exit and no true rival. Then a spoken bulletin widens one man's vanity into arithmetic, letting the private kill scale up to a species vanishing on a clock that only speeds.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The Humane Society gave it the Doris Day Music Award at the 1993 Genesis Awards — Megadeth remain the only metal band so honored, for a song Nick Menza brought in from a magazine piece on canned hunts.","themes":["violence & murder","nature & the elements"],"means":"borrowed courage against a fenced and helpless target","built":"spoken bulletin scales one man's kill into a species on a clock"},
"megadeth~dawn-patrol": {"haiku":"The song observes a world in environmental collapse where humanity sleepwalks through worsening pollution and greenhouse effects, resigned to living like moles in perpetual darkness.","opus":"A bleak vision of environmental collapse, where pollution, the greenhouse effect, and a wrecked planet leave humanity reduced to burrowing survivors reading its own epitaph.","sonnet":"An epitaph for humanity: rising pollution, greenhouse destruction, and collective sleepwalking through environmental collapse leave people nervously going through the motions until they end their days as underground creatures in a self-made ruin.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rust in Peace turns to ecological apocalypse here, and Mustaine plays it as a bitter joke rather than a warning shot: the greenhouse effect has already won by the time the song opens, so there is nothing left to argue for. The first half sketches a species too numbed to react, filing off to work through pollution alerts with only a nervous twitch to show it registered anything. What sharpens the read is the turn in the second half, where the speaker stops describing and starts spectating — he can only laugh as he reads humanity's epitaph, having outlived the fight itself. The closing image folds the title inward: dawn patrol becomes not a lookout at first light but survivors driven underground like moles, so the one word that should mean vigilance now means a blind subterranean end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nature & the elements","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"laughing at humanity's epitaph after the fight is already lost","built":"dawn patrol inverts — vigilance at first light becomes blind subterranean end"},
"megadeth~death-from-within":{"haiku":"Enemies penetrate a fortress from inside, exploiting trust and position as internal sabotage destroys from the core.","opus":"A war narrative built on the Trojan-horse idea that the deadliest attack comes from inside, revenge served cold by enemies already through the gates. It reads as Mustaine's grim thesis that vengeance only breeds more vengeance until nothing survives.","sonnet":"An ancient military allegory — the Trojan horse — is used to describe civilisations destroyed by infiltrators they welcomed through their own gates, their prophets mocked and warnings ignored until the walls cave. Death comes not from without but from the trusted enemy already inside.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~devils-island": {"haiku":"A prisoner on Devil's Island awaits execution, haunted by the memory of a woman associated with evil. The song moves between present damnation and past betrayal, ending with themes of judgment and redemption through burning.","opus":"A condemned prisoner awaits execution on an inescapable island surrounded by sharks, haunted by an evil woman and the memory of the night that damned him, resigned to remain there even beyond death.","sonnet":"A prisoner awaiting execution on a remote, inescapable island reflects on the chain of events that led him there, involving a woman whose evil he could not escape, culminating in his final walk to the stake.","src":"haiku","fable":"A prisoner narrates his own march toward execution from a cell he cannot leave, the surrounding sea and its predators sealing the sentence as surely as the walls do. What might have been dread instead hardens into a strange readiness, the fear burned off, as he binds his fate to a malevolent woman he refuses to renounce and drags her toward the pyre with him. Even the storm that answers, and death itself, fail to release him: the haunting outlasts the body, and he stays fixed to this island past any ending.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","exile & diaspora","faith & the occult"],"means":"fear burned off until death itself cannot release the haunting"},
"megadeth~die-dead-enough":{"haiku":"A warrior obsessed with proving himself through violence and bloodshed, never satisfied with his capacity for destruction or endurance of pain.","opus":"A breathless portrait of a man compelled to flee something he cannot name, jolted awake in terror with menace flowing through him, convinced no amount of pain or dying could ever be enough. The chase curves toward revenge on a specific target. Megadeth leave the source of the panic unexplained, which only sharpens the dread.","sonnet":"A nightmare of inadequacy — the speaker runs from something unnamed, measuring themselves against impossible physical and moral standards and falling short on every count. By the end the running converts to pursuit and the song turns predatory, but the original fear never fully leaves.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~disconnect":{"haiku":"A person suppresses their conscience and emotions to survive isolation and inner turmoil, choosing disconnection over feeling pain.","opus":"A chilling sketch of a person leading a secret, self-serving existence who silences their own guilt and bars out everyone else until reality stops registering, quietly picking at old wounds as the ache goes on forever. The chorus casts this numbness as a chosen exit. Dave Mustaine's thrash veterans train their precision on willed emotional deadness.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone disappearing into their own double life — behind closed doors, the public world shut out, conscience muted, reality made optional. The speaker addresses them with something between concern and contempt: the scars are tended, the silence treasured as gold, but the retreat from everything real is a slow erasure no one from the outside can interrupt.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~dystopia":{"haiku":"A warning against mass control through censorship, disinformation, and fear that keeps populations docile and unable to resist tyranny.","opus":"A political alarm built on the fiction that ignorance keeps you safe, sketching a penned-in populace instructed to distrust its own senses while chaos erupts and calm is answered with fury. The remedy offered is brutal: cut out the rot at its source. Dave Mustaine's thrash institution sharpens his long conspiratorial streak into a study of engineered fear.","sonnet":"A direct political indictment of a society that suppresses inconvenient truths and answers legitimate rage with more violence — the prescription is tyrannicide, cutting out the cancer at its source. The repeated word stands as both diagnosis and verdict.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track of Megadeth's 2016 comeback names the mechanism of political despair: a regime that survives by curating what its citizens are allowed to know, selling ignorance as safety and outlawing the questions that would expose the bodies. Dave Mustaine lets the word itself become the entire chorus, a diagnosis repeated until it hardens into a place-name rather than a warning. The second verse turns colder and more dangerous, sliding from observing a demoralized, war-weary populace into the argument that tyranny can only be cut out at the root through tyrannicide, so the song ends not as protest but as a hardliner's call to violent purge, the paranoia it describes curdling into the very extremism it seems to warn against.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","violence & murder"],"means":"dystopia repeated until diagnosis hardens into place-name","built":"the protest curdles into the extremism it warned against"},
"megadeth~ecstasy":{"haiku":"A secret affair wrapped in guilt and forbidden passion, where lovers hide their relationship to avoid social judgment and moral condemnation.","opus":"A confession of an illicit affair conducted in shadow, the narrator arriving when night falls and slipping out at dawn, lying to deceive everyone while calling the guilty pleasure a sin named ecstasy. Foolish pride and immorality tally the cost. Megadeth, led by Dave Mustaine, wrap this tale of secret indulgence in sleek thrash, the forbidden thrill and its dark inhibitions given a coolly seductive menace.","sonnet":"A hidden affair told from the inside — shadow visits, dawn departures, the whole architecture of concealment erected to protect something that the speaker knows is wrong but cannot stop feeding. The word ecstasy carries all the weight: it's the name for the sin and also the reason neither person walks away, mutual complicity dressed as helplessness.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~elysian-fields": {"haiku":"Warriors ascend toward paradise by storming heaven itself, armed and defiant against judgment and a world that refuses salvation, willing to fight for their destiny.","opus":"Souls charging heaven on Judgment Day, worn out by living and hoping death stings less than life did. Its acid center is a verdict on humanity: with a famous figure of moral evasion forever rinsing his guilt, mankind clings to warfare and treats laying down weapons like a plague, so the species wants no rescue, just to be abandoned to itself.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality, judgment, and mankind's refusal to be saved — the dead march toward the Elysian fields not as reward but as warriors storming heaven, because humanity clings to conflict and will not lay down its arms.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine sets a soldier's dying moment against the whole span of scripture, the eyes burning shut as a life that hurt more than its ending flickers past between crib and grave. Here the Roman governor keeps rinsing guilt from his palms while humanity, offered rescue, waves it off and asks only to be abandoned to its own devices. That refusal curdles into creed: the one article of faith everyone will die for is the right to keep making war, so setting down a weapon reads to them as a sickness eating the marrow. The chorus answers the death with a charge — wings bloodied, an army rushing the gates of heaven to seize a Greek paradise by force rather than grace, salvation stormed instead of received.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"salvation stormed rather than received, heaven taken by force","built":"humanity offered rescue waves it off, naming war as its one creed"},
"megadeth~endgame": {"haiku":"In a dystopian future, citizens are rounded into concentration camps, forced to wear RFID chips, and enslaved by authoritarian tyranny under corporate control.","opus":"Mustaine's conspiracy-fueled dystopia of martial law, detention camps and mandatory chips, a survivalist nightmare of the Patriot Act taken to its paranoid extreme where citizens wake up stripped of rights inside a police state.","sonnet":"A paranoid dystopian scenario in which a government-declared emergency strips citizens of rights, forces RFID chipping, and herds people into detention camps — the narrator wakes inside a FEMA box and narrates survival inside a controlled system.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person captive narrates from a lightless government box, and the song builds dread by stacking bureaucratic, technocratic, and legal jargon into one suffocating pile until the vocabulary itself becomes the cage. Mustaine recites the catalogue of implants, secret decrees, and detention processing in a flat policy cadence, so the terror registers as paperwork rather than protest. The refrain narrows in stages, withholding its final word until the last pass, so the ending arrives as a checkmate already reached rather than a coming threat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"bureaucratic vocabulary becoming the cage itself","built":"refrain withholds its final word until the last pass — checkmate already reached"},
"megadeth~family-tree": {"haiku":"Generational abuse cycles silently within families; the song warns against the corrupting influence of family secrets and inherited patterns of harm.","opus":"A rare thrash reckoning with child abuse that voices both a predator's coaxing and the code of secrecy that shields him, set beside a survivor's promise not to become the next abuser. Mustaine draws on his own wounded childhood, letting threat and defiance occupy the same lines as generational harm lurks in wait.","sonnet":"Cycles of abuse pass through bloodlines like a dark inheritance — the abused becoming abuser, secrets sealed within the family to protect the perpetrator. The speaker confronts that chain directly, refusing to receive what was done to those before him, exposing the hidden rot at the root of the tree.","src":"haiku","fable":"A father's tenderness curdles into a threat here: the same coaxing voice that promises to show a child love is the one binding them both to a secret sealed behind a locked bedroom door, so the affection is itself the grooming. The verses supply the testimony the chorus buries — a mind fleeing the body it cannot leave, a pact of hushed complicity, and the child's vow to break the chain rather than pass the harm to anyone else. The horror lands in the title turning from bloodline into inheritance: abuse handed down like a surname, lurking one generation on, its own buried thing. Mustaine stages this by letting the caressing melody carry the monstrous words, so the mask never slips and the listener has to hear what sits under it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","violence & murder","family & blood"],"means":"abuse passed down like a surname through a locked door","built":"caressing melody carries monstrous words so the mask never slips"},
"megadeth~fff": {"haiku":"The song calls to fight authority and injustice, resisting being crushed by systems that eliminate the poor and vulnerable, demanding freedom even unto death.","opus":"A furious anti-authority anthem framing the poor and marginalized as targets of a system that exterminates its own mistakes, then pivots to a dying man's demand to perish free rather than serve the war machine. The chorus turns a patriotic hymn inside out into a personal creed. This is classic Megadeth political thrash, Mustaine channeling institutional rage into blunt, chanted defiance.","sonnet":"A call to resist the machinery of state power that crushes dissent, disposes of the poor, and sends lives through its grinder. The stance is defiant without being optimistic — fight not because you'll win but because dying free matters more than the alternative, and because the system will devour you regardless.","src":"haiku","fable":"A campaign to cleanse society of its poor gets sold as a public health cure, with the tents of the destitute torched and their killers grinning as they work. The recurring call to fight for freedom curdles into irony: a flag-waving cadence lifted from an old patriotic hymn drapes state-sanctioned extermination in the language of liberty. What's left is a doomed plea for reprieve and the wish to meet death unbroken rather than complicit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The full title is Fight for Freedom — and the song grew out of an instrumental sketch the band called Bullprick.","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","freedom & escape"],"means":"extermination of the poor draped in liberty's language","built":"patriotic cadence lifted to dress state-sanctioned killing as freedom"},
"megadeth~five-magics": {"haiku":"A wizard seeks mastery over five magical disciplines—alchemy, wizardry, sorcery, thermatology, and electricity—to overthrow tyranny and defeat a supernatural adversary.","opus":"A fantasy tale in which an aspiring conqueror demands secret lore from a sorcerer and swears to command a set of five arcane disciplines to claim a realm, stalking a demon of the pit in a lethal duel where only a lone combatant walks away.","sonnet":"A student of the arcane seeks initiation from an all-knowing wizard, asking to master five magical disciplines — alchemy, wizardry, sorcery, thermatology, electricity — in order to overthrow a tyrannical ruler and dominate all rivals.","src":"haiku","fable":"A supplicant kneels before a sorcerer and begs to be apprenticed in five disciplines at once, promising that mastery of them all will topple a rival and hand him a defiled little kingdom to scrub clean. Mustaine stages the appetite as a fantasy pageant of hooded monks, temple maidens and a summoned beast, but the craving underneath is nakedly ordinary: the man wants every source of power there is, and he wants it delivered rather than earned. The turn comes when the trance of I-master-this repeats until it curdles, and the pupil discovers he is now the hunted one in his own duel, stalking an underworld figure across a field where only a single combatant walks off it. The close then strips the medieval costume away and hardens into a flat law of consequence, delivering the verdict that whoever seizes power by force is undone by that same force, and whoever builds a life on falsehood ends up owned by it. The register matters as much as the wording: an elaborate spell resolving into a plain sentence handed back down onto the caster, which fits a writer newly out of the wreckage his own reach had made.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The premise adapts Lyndon Hardy's 1980 fantasy novel Master of the Five Magics, whose curriculum is thaumaturgy, alchemy, magic, sorcery and wizardry — the song swaps in thermatology and electricity, keeping the shape and garbling the syllabus.","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"the seizer of power undone by the same force he seized","built":"elaborate spell resolves into a plain sentence handed back onto the caster"},
"megadeth~foreclosure-of-a-dream": {"haiku":"Political promises and economic hope collapse into personal bankruptcy and death as borrowed money and time run out for ordinary people.","opus":"Megadeth turn thrash into political dispatch, indicting broken economic promises. A pledge of fairness for everyone gives way to debt and delusion, a hopeful surge ending in humiliation as land turns worthless and heartland pride succumbs to ruin. A mocking sample of a hollow campaign vow underscores Mustaine's vision of the American dream repossessed.","sonnet":"The American agricultural heartland collapses under debt, political betrayal, and ecological damage while politicians mouth hollow promises about borrowed time and borrowed money. Dreams foreclosed — the title's pun is literal and metaphorical — become a personal holocaust for the families left behind. The epilogue gestures toward a frontier yet unclaimed, though the song's weight makes the optimism feel fragile.","src":"haiku","fable":"Megadeth reads the early-'90s American recession as a mortgage document, staging a dream called in for nonpayment: a boom financed entirely on credit and stolen days, its equity spent before the bill arrives. The verses fall like a bankruptcy filing, from political euphoria at the crest down through farmland gone worthless and deedless to a private ruin of empty accounts, madness, and self-slaughter, the household wreckage folded into the national one. Mustaine builds the whole track around the sampled campaign vow, planting the President's shattered no-new-taxes pledge at the exact hinge so the third verse can ventriloquize the politician — all fresh optimism and new frontiers and answers waiting overhead — its cheer arriving already exposed as the con that foreclosed everything the other verses tally.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The dream foreclosed was Ellefson's own — his family's Minnesota farm went under in the 80s farm crisis, and he brought the song's spine to Mustaine; the Bush read-my-lips sample seals the betrayal on the record itself.","themes":["politics & society","money & the street","suicide"],"means":"the American dream read as a mortgage called in for nonpayment","built":"sampled no-new-taxes pledge planted at the hinge, arriving already exposed as the con"},
"megadeth~foreign-policy":{"haiku":"A cynical take on war policy based on superiority, hatred as purity, and manifest destiny masquerading as legitimate foreign relations.","opus":"A frigid, slogan-driven satire of nationalist ideology, equating endurance with dominance, bigotry with cleanliness, frailty with sickness, and rejecting any outreach beyond one's borders. Everyone becomes a suspect, expansionism sanctified. Originally a Fear cover, it lets Megadeth deliver punk brevity as a chilling recitation of the authoritarian mindset.","sonnet":"A cold, satirical distillation of imperial logic — eliminate difference, treat weakness as disease, call conquest destiny. The song offers no counter-argument; it simply holds the ideology up in stripped, declarative form until its brutality is impossible to ignore.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~forget-to-remember": {"haiku":"A deteriorating relationship where one partner loses memory (possibly from vaccines or disease), leaving the other grieving their absence while present.","opus":"A devastating account of watching a loved one vanish into dementia, boarding up their eyes and locking the mind's door until the narrator becomes a stranger with an unfamiliar face, begging just to hear his name said again. Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash band, temper their speed for this grief, the pained call-and-response of a person pushed away by someone who no longer knows them lending it a raw human ache.","sonnet":"A song about loving someone with dementia — the slow erasure of recognition, the speaker becoming a stranger to the person they've shared a life with, a face without a name behind locked eyes. The final verses pull toward something darker still: the mercury, the vaccine, the web of deceit, leaving the speaker oscillating between grief and the half-mad wish to assign blame for an unblameable loss.","src":"haiku","fable":"A son keeps calling a parent who no longer picks up the thread, watching recognition drain from a face until his own reads as a stranger's, the door of that mind bolted from the inside. It moves through the slow farewell that dementia forces — the sun sinking in someone's stare while the calendar runs on without them — then curdles, near the close, into a darker suspicion that mercury and a needle engineered the erasure. Grief and paranoia end up sharing one plea: just say my name once more.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written about the Alzheimer's of Mustaine's wife's mother — his own image for it was watching an ice sculpture of somebody you love melt in front of your eyes.","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind","family & blood"],"means":"grief and paranoia sharing the same plea for recognition"},
"megadeth~gears-of-war": {"haiku":"War machinery perpetuates genocide and ethnic cleansing with sophisticated weapons; humanity invents increasingly intelligent tools of mass death.","opus":"Mustaine's antiwar tirade on how people keep devising smarter methods of slaughter, cloaking mass murder in accuracy and excuses. The dread lies in its machine-like certainty, humans wearing one another down like meshing cogs.","sonnet":"A blunt anti-war statement about the industrialization of killing — ethnic cleansing, precision bombs, intelligent weapons that still just produce corpses. The \"gears of war\" are a machine that grinds men into it without discrimination, and anyone who rides with war's architects will die with them.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine borrows the title from the video-game franchise but hollows it out into a literal image: humanity as interlocking machinery that exists only to grind itself down. The chorus runs on flat sarcasm, marveling that all our cleverness has purchased nothing but a costlier route to the same corpse, precision-guided intelligence spent entirely on killing. Verse 2 turns from the geopolitics of the opening lines to a personal reckoning, where the smoke clears to reveal the Devil staring back from the mirror, folding the war machine's guilt back onto the one who built and rode with it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","shame & repression"],"means":"cleverness measured only in costlier routes to the same corpse","built":"verse two folds the machine's guilt back onto the man who rode with it"},
"megadeth~good-mourning-black-friday": {"haiku":"A homicidal maniac stalks and butchers victims indiscriminately, treating murder as sport and taking sadistic pleasure in extreme violence.","opus":"An early Mustaine horror set-piece narrated by a subterranean butcher stalking victims with a hammer. It's pure thrash guignol, the slasher-flick relish of the killer's voice supplying all the menace.","sonnet":"The first part is a brief disoriented waking; the second is a first-person monologue from a sadistic serial killer who stalks, bludgeons, and dismembers victims with a hammer and a blade, describing each killing in explicit detail.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pun does the structural work: the funeral-morning greeting is also mourning, and the tiny first movement is a body waking to something wrong inside it before the second movement lets that thing out to speak. What follows is early Mustaine at his most gleefully lurid — a first-person butcher cataloguing his hammer-work in nursery-rhyme couplets, the horror pitched so far over the top that relish, not dread, becomes the point. The imagery clusters into two families that keep colliding: implements of trade (hammer, blade, steel) turned on the machinery of the body (heads, organs, flesh), so the killer narrates himself as a workman clocking in, with the massacre framed as how he gets clean. The title reveals its second meaning late, when the day of slaughter is named — a personal appointment with violence rather than any calendar. The closing proverb, painting the Devil on the wall, is the song's hinge: an old European saying about summoning the very evil you dread by naming it, repeated into incantation, which quietly admits the whole gory monologue has been a mask worn to conjure the thing it pretends to fear.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The song predates Megadeth — Mustaine first wrote it in Panic, his pre-Metallica band, under different words; it crossed both bands before landing here.","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","madness & the mind"],"means":"massacre narrated as a workman clocking in to get clean","built":"title pun fuses funeral morning and mourning; closing proverb admits the monologue conjures what it pretends to fear"},
"megadeth~good-mourning-black-friday-randy-burns-mix": {"haiku":"Sadistic violence becomes systemic horror; the killer embodies dark pleasure in dismemberment and absolute destruction.","opus":"A gleeful first-person plunge into the mind of a hammer-wielding serial killer stalking the streets, butchering victims without remorse. Early Megadeth splatter-thrash that revels in the horror as pure theater.","sonnet":"A murderous interior voice takes over a narrator who wakes feeling wrong and describes in graphic detail the ritual slaughter of victims — hammering, hacking, dismembering — until the personality fully gives way to a sadistic underground predator waiting for Black Friday.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pun does the structural work: the funeral-morning greeting is also mourning, and the tiny first movement is a body waking to something wrong inside it before the second movement lets that thing out to speak. What follows is early Mustaine at his most gleefully lurid — a first-person butcher cataloguing his hammer-work in nursery-rhyme couplets, the horror pitched so far over the top that relish, not dread, becomes the point. The imagery clusters into two families that keep colliding: implements of trade (hammer, blade, steel) turned on the machinery of the body (heads, organs, flesh), so the killer narrates himself as a workman clocking in, with the massacre framed as how he gets clean. The title reveals its second meaning late, when the day of slaughter is named — a personal appointment with violence rather than any calendar. The closing proverb, painting the Devil on the wall, is the song's hinge: an old European saying about summoning the very evil you dread by naming it, repeated into incantation, which quietly admits the whole gory monologue has been a mask worn to conjure the thing it pretends to fear.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The song predates Megadeth — Mustaine first wrote it in Panic, his pre-Metallica band, under different words; it crossed both bands before landing here.","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","madness & the mind"],"means":"massacre narrated as a workman clocking in to get clean","built":"title pun fuses funeral morning and mourning; closing proverb admits the monologue conjures what it pretends to fear"},
"megadeth~guns-drugs-money":{"haiku":"A man in Mexico faces the brutal cartel choice between silver (money/compliance) or lead (death), depicting the poverty-driven drug trade.","opus":"A borderland noir near a Mexican river town, a condemned man with a case of cash and a firearm meeting the cartel's blunt choice of bribe or bullet, hardship having already corrupted a decent soul. The tossed-off Spanish ultimatum seals his fate. Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash veterans, craft a cinematic outlaw ballad, liquor and grit framing a doom fixed beneath a scorching southern heaven.","sonnet":"A border-country noir set along the Rio Grande — a man arrives in no-man's land with cash and ammunition and watches his plans unravel in the heat. The cartel ultimatum, silver or lead, lands as the final frame: poverty strips good men of everything they learned about mercy, and the choice at the end isn't really a choice at all.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~hangar-18":{"haiku":"Secret military facility housing alien remains and cryogenic subjects under enforced amnesia, held behind impenetrable security.","opus":"Mustaine's paranoid thriller about a government warehousing recovered alien life and burying the truth behind military secrecy. It taps Cold War distrust of official knowledge, the dread of a witness who has seen more than he was ever meant to.","sonnet":"A song about a secret government facility — Hangar 18 — where the speaker claims alien bodies and technology are stored, and where selective amnesia is used to silence anyone who gets too close to the truth.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine frames the Roswell coverup as a guided tour whose genial host slowly realizes his own hospitality has doomed him, the boast of unbreakable walls and star-mapping gear curdling the moment the exhibit turns to alien corpses kept on ice. The refrain is the trap snapping shut, a docent who saw the frozen inventory understanding that his knowledge is now a liability the state cannot let walk out of the building alive. Its sharpest contempt falls on the government rather than the visitors, mocking the oxymoron at the heart of a secret-keeping apparatus whose claim to competence collapses under its own contradiction. The long instrumental passages carry that dread wordlessly, the twin-guitar duel doing the work the terrified narrator can no longer risk saying aloud.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"the docent's knowledge becoming the liability the state cannot let leave","built":"the long instrumental passages carry the dread the terrified narrator can no longer risk saying aloud"},
"megadeth~have-cool-will-travel": {"haiku":"A commentary on unavoidable violence and societal decay, suggesting escape is impossible and that facing one's inner demons is mandatory.","opus":"Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash veterans, aim blunt fury at school shootings and society's paralysis. The song rages at children arming themselves while adults wring their hands, at the finger-pointing that follows a death, and at hollow official claims that everything possible is being done. Its verdict is that the supposed fixes only make things worse, and no one truly wants to try.","sonnet":"A bitter survey of gun violence and collective inaction — children arm themselves, mothers grieve, and every promised solution turns out to be another lie. The chorus suggests the only honest lesson available is one nobody wants to learn.","src":"haiku","fable":"Behind the jaunty title borrowed from the old have-gun-will-travel mercenary slogan sits a bleak reckoning with children arming themselves and a public that points fingers rather than acting. The verses stage the classic finger-pointing paralysis — everyone claims they do all they can, nobody actually tries — and the chorus reframes it as a lesson with no recess and no rules, the world as a schoolyard where the coping strategy on offer is worse than the affliction. The refrain itself is the mask: an unearned cool, a shrug of detached mobility offered exactly where the song has proven there is nothing cool and nowhere to escape to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title came to Mustaine in a dream — drummer Nick Menza saying the phrase as he walked out on the band.","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"detached cool offered exactly where the song proves none exists","built":"mercenary title slogan recast as finger-pointing paralysis with nowhere to travel"},
"megadeth~head-crusher": {"haiku":"A torture device crushes a prisoner's skull slowly, fragmenting bone and teeth in extreme pain until death brings an end to their suffering.","opus":"A cold, gory account of execution by the medieval skull-vise: chin clamped in place, screw cranked until teeth pop and bone collapses inward, framing mankind's hunger for the vilest agony visited on prisoners who break under questioning.","sonnet":"A clinical, graphic description of medieval torture by head crusher — each stage of skull compression is narrated in detail, from the prisoner kneeling to teeth shattering and bones fragmenting, framed as a spectacle of maximum suffering.","src":"haiku","fable":"A procedural execution set to thrash: the song walks a condemned prisoner from cell to the medieval device that gives it its name, narrating the mechanism turn by turn until the skull gives way. Mustaine builds the horror the way the tool works — by slow tightening, each verse advancing the compression a notch while the chorus clamps down on the same three words, so the form itself is the screw turning. Underneath the gore is the older, colder subject the lyric keeps naming: interrogation and forced confession, the victim recanting too late once pain has already made truth irrelevant. Fitting for Endgame's paranoid register, the real cruelty isn't the crushed bone but the machinery of a state that tortures first and asks questions after.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"torture making truth irrelevant before the question is asked","built":"chorus clamps the same three words as the screw tightens — form as the device"},
"megadeth~high-speed-dirt": {"haiku":"A person attempts skydiving and free-fall, describing the rush of plummeting toward earth with no intention to survive the impact.","opus":"Megadeth channel their manic thrash precision into a skydiver's flirtation with death, the narrator plunging from the sky in an adrenaline-soaked rush as the ground rushes up to greet him. Frozen with terror yet plummeting at full tilt toward his doom, he insists the descent could never hurt, Mustaine turning the fatal fall into a giddy, morbid thrill ride ending in impact.","sonnet":"A skydiver in freefall narrates his own plummet with a mixture of terror and exhilaration that tips toward ecstatic self-destruction: the pale horse waits, the ground rises, and the sensation of velocity overtakes identity entirely. Whether the jump is voluntary or forced remains deliberately ambiguous, but the full-throttle rush toward impact is embraced rather than resisted.","src":"haiku","fable":"A plunge toward the ground narrated from inside the fall, where the rush and the certainty of a fatal landing collapse into the same grinning verdict. Megadeth push the thrash toward speed-metal glee, letting the euphoria of acceleration carry a giddy contempt for the smash it is racing toward. Terror and thrill keep swapping places as the earth swells up, the singer half-daring the crash and half-savoring the ride down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Mustaine jumped himself — the title is skydiver slang for what you taste when the chute fails, and he traded jump-lingo with Ellefson to build the lyric.","themes":["death & grief","freedom & escape"],"means":"terror and thrill swapping places as the earth swells up"},
"megadeth~holy-wars-the-punishment-due":{"haiku":"Religious conflict drives bloodshed across borders while those preaching superiority hide their complicity, leading to violent retaliation.","opus":"The first part condemns killing in the name of religion and the divisions that tear countries apart; the second shifts into a mercenary's tale of revenge and paid violence. Together they trace war, righteous posturing, and the fear of having one's very thoughts taken away.","sonnet":"A two-part song: the first part condemns killing in God's name and the self-righteous certainty of religious and political leaders; the second shifts to a personal revenge narrative — a man whose family was killed hunts down those responsible — with both halves linked by the theme of violence justified by ideology.","src":"haiku","fable":"The two-part title is a diagnosis and a mask: the first movement is Mustaine the baffled outsider watching kin slaughter kin over faith he claims not to grasp, only for the second verse to expose the scholar, the judge, the leader and the sermonizing prophet as one interchangeable engine of self-appointed certainty, his own soapbox implicated among them. Then the suite hard-cuts into a comic-book avenger whose slain family licenses an escalating arithmetic of revenge, every threat against him another fatal error to be settled, retributive hunger poured into the fissures like sentencing concrete and passed off as law. The genius is that the disguise slips at the finish, the vigilante's voice fracturing into paranoia about stolen thoughts and a chant of sanctioned euthanizing ground down to noise, so the crusader who opened by failing to understand belief-driven murder closes as its mirror, mockery hardened into the very conviction he scorned.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Born of one gig — the Antrim Forum, Northern Ireland, May 1988: Mustaine dedicated Anarchy in the U.K. to the cause, not knowing it meant the IRA; the crowd split, and he wrote the lyric the next day. Part II runs on the Punisher comics he loved.","themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","violence & murder","madness & the mind"],"means":"the crusader who scorned belief-driven murder closes as its mirror","built":"the two-part title enacts diagnosis then disguise; the suite hard-cuts between them"},
"megadeth~holy-wars-the-punishment-due-demo":{"haiku":"Religious violence breeds endless cycles of tribal revenge and righteous murder; the speaker refuses to be silenced despite manipulation by both warmongers and power structures.","opus":"Megadeth's condemnation of religious warfare, brother killing brother over faith Mustaine says he can't understand, an eye on divided lands like the Middle East. It then swerves into a mercenary's revenge tale, a hired killer raging after his family is murdered and dreading the day his own mind is taken.","sonnet":"Killing for religion is announced as something the speaker genuinely cannot understand, then the lyric pivots to a hired assassin whose wife and baby were killed to enslave him — vengeance, organised crime, judicial granite, and the fear that even thoughts will be confiscated. Religion, state power, and personal loss merge into one war with no winner.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~hook-in-mouth": {"haiku":"Censors and power rewrites history and eliminate inconvenient people; freedom is hollow while institutions like the PMRC dictate what's allowed and allowed to exist.","opus":"Mustaine's blistering attack on censorship and the PMRC, imagining a small-minded official erasing dissent and rewriting the past under a totalitarian rulebook. The song spells out liberty letter by letter while arguing the concept is hollow so long as such gatekeepers control what people are allowed to say.","sonnet":"A direct attack on the PMRC's campaign to censor music — the song describes censors as history-rewriting bureaucrats who control what people say and think, and spells out FREEDOM letter by letter only to declare it means nothing as long as the PMRC has a hook in the artist's mouth.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine's answer to the mid-80s PMRC censorship crusade builds its case in three moves rather than one broadside. It opens on the censor as a small grey functionary — a courthouse-pale pest with a big eraser, quietly rewriting history and school records and deleting whoever offends the rulebook — reframing moral guardianship as totalitarian clerkwork. The centerpiece spells a patriotic creed out one letter at a time, invoking ancestral blood shed in battle, only to detonate each letter into dying, manure, and money, so the slogan assembles itself just to mean nothing while the watchdogs stay in place. Then the title image turns everything inward: a hand up the shirt working the jaw like a marionette, speech hauled out on command the way a hooked catch is dragged from the water. The closing refusal — a person, not something reeled in on a line — is the one thing the censor cannot edit, and the track stakes its whole argument on it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society","artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"free speech defended by dismantling its own slogans","built":"the patriotic acronym spells out and then detonates itself"},
"megadeth~how-the-story-ends": {"haiku":"An advancing army destroys towns and burns everything to ash, poisoning the land in an inevitable conclusion of war and annihilation.","opus":"An end-of-days war piece told from inside a relentless conquering force, distant torchlight, war-drum cadences, and a poisoned earth, calmly promising the listener that total ruin is simply the inevitable conclusion, wanted or not.","sonnet":"A relentlessly fatalist song about conquest and scorched-earth warfare — armies advance with torches and drums, every town burns, the wells are poisoned, and the narrator insists this is simply how all stories end whether you like it or not.","src":"haiku","fable":"An advancing army seen only as distant torches, then heard as the hypnotizing cadence of its own march, becomes the whole world of the song — Megadeth strip conquest down to sensory dread, the drums beaten for those too far off to yet hear or see them. The refrain does the ugly work: total war rendered as inevitability, scorched wells and burnt earth delivered with the flat certainty of someone narrating a foregone conclusion rather than urging one. What makes it chilling is the address — a patient, almost consoling voice telling you that your objection is beside the point, because this ending was always coming and always ends the same way.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder"],"means":"conquest delivered as consolation to those already lost","built":"narration adopts a patient address that forecloses all objection"},
"megadeth~i-ain-t-superstitious": {"haiku":"A cynical speaker dismisses all superstitions and fears, claiming not to believe in luck, demons, or any supernatural force.","opus":"Mustaine's thrash-era swagger drives a defiant dismissal of omens, where broken mirrors, black cats and open ladders are dared rather than feared. The narrator brands superstition as nonsense and prefers the dark, taunting anyone who clings to bad-luck rituals. It is bravado as worldview, a metal frontman flipping folk dread into a punchline while insisting nothing frightens him.","sonnet":"A defiant narrator mocks folk fears and omens with gleeful contempt — black cats, broken mirrors, ladders — insisting none of it holds power over him. The bravado is real but performative, a loud assertion of rational toughness that keeps circling back as if to convince himself as much as the room.","src":"haiku","fable":"The Dixon blues standard runs on a knowing wink — the singer stacks up every unlucky omen he claims not to fear, and the pile-up is the joke, because a man this busy denying the dark is plainly haunted by it. Megadeth strips the wink out and plays the denial straight and mean, the sneer curdling into open contempt as bravado gives way to a stream of insults hurled at anyone still spooked. What was blues irony becomes a thrash flex: the mask of not caring worn so aggressively that the protesting-too-much underneath stops being coy and turns into the whole snarling point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"denial performed so aggressively it becomes its own confession","built":"thrash strips the blues wink leaving only the protesting-too-much"},
"megadeth~i-ll-be-there":{"haiku":"Commitment and presence offer redemption through mutual support; the speaker vows to stand beside their companion through trials and suffering.","opus":"An uncharacteristically earnest vow of loyalty, promising to stand by someone through fire as they once stood by him. The metal bombast serves a simple debt of gratitude repaid, devotion offered back in someone's hour of need.","sonnet":"A pledge of unconditional loyalty to someone who stood by the speaker through his darkest times — now reversing the debt, promising to walk through fire alongside them for as long as he lives.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~i-ll-get-even": {"haiku":"Vengeful obsession where a rejected and betrayed man fantasizes about elaborate revenge and causing suffering to the woman who hurt him.","opus":"A revenge fantasy spiraling out of a man left, deceived and unable to sleep, coiled with volatile fury and vowing to make the person who wronged him pay, promising to lurk unseen until he strikes. Imagery of fracturing into several selves tips it toward derangement. Megadeth, led by Dave Mustaine, sharpen this obsession with their cutting thrash attack, a single kept ledger turning heartbreak into a warning.","sonnet":"A revenge obsession in full burn — the speaker sleepless, bitter, running a catalog of every lie that was told to them, the anger metastasizing into something darker. Voodoo dolls, shadows, a fragmenting self that agrees with itself across multiple versions: the one who was wronged and the one who is becoming frightening in response, both pointed at the same target.","src":"haiku","fable":"A revenge song that keeps escalating its own metaphors — nuclear reactor, ticking bomb, voodoo doll — until the imagery of destruction curdles from grievance into genuine derangement, the narrator's threats outrunning whatever betrayal supposedly earned them. What starts as the aggrieved lover of Cryptic Writings-era Megadeth's radio-courting hooks turns confessional in the final verse, where the promise to lurk behind the target arrives voiced by a splintered self that agrees with itself in a chorus of me-and-me. What sits underneath the swagger is that getting even has stopped being about the other person at all; it is the sound of a fixation feeding on itself, and the song's tell is that it can only repeat the vow, never picture the reckoning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","madness & the mind","violence & murder"],"means":"revenge that has stopped being about the other person at all","built":"the chorus delivers a chorus of me-and-me agreeing with itself"},
"megadeth~i-thought-i-knew-it-all": {"haiku":"Humbling realization that certainty and control are illusions. The speaker thought they had life figured out, but unexplained forces and timing render plans meaningless.","opus":"Megadeth channel their aggression into rueful hindsight, the narrator resigning himself to setbacks he can't explain while trusting that someone, somewhere is listening. The chorus is a humbling: he assumed he had life figured out and was blindsided when it fell apart, the hard lesson finally landing as circumstances turn against him.","sonnet":"Humility arrives uninvited — plans collapsed, certainties exposed, leaving only the thin comfort that somewhere there is a reason even if it cannot be seen from here. The repeated disbelief at how it all ended becomes a kind of chastened acceptance that the lesson was always meant for him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song of shaken certainty, where the mid-nineties, cleaner-toned Megadeth trade thrash velocity for a mid-tempo ache, and Dave Mustaine — no stranger to being cast out of what he thought was his — lets the swagger of the title collapse in real time. The verses reach for a consoling logic, insisting some unseen reason governs every failure and every closed door, while the chorus keeps snagging on the same wound of surprise, the disbelief that hard-won mastery could end like this. Meaning lives in that gap between the two: the verse-mind rationalizes, the chorus-heart refuses, and the pre-chorus splits the difference with a grudging surrender that clings to being heard by someone, somewhere. The outro settles nothing, breaking the boast into a stammer that repeats the opening claim until it hollows out — the knowing worn down to a reflex the singer no longer trusts.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"mastery's swagger collapsing in real time into stunned disbelief","built":"verse rationalizes while chorus refuses, outro repeats the boast hollow"},
"megadeth~in-my-darkest-hour": {"haiku":"An abandoned person faces despair, loneliness, and suicidal ideation after their betrayer rejected them, yet finds strength in solitude.","opus":"Written in the wake of Cliff Burton's death, a raw cry of desertion: when he most needed help, no one and no god answered, leaving him frozen and certain he'd be better off gone. It sours into venom aimed at a lover who mocked his suffering, blending a death wish with fury at being cast aside after giving everything.","sonnet":"A song about abandonment and suicidal despair — the narrator gave everything for a relationship, was left alone in their darkest moment, and now walks toward death as the only escape from a love that took everything and gave nothing back.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine writes abandonment as an accusation, addressing someone who withheld help while he was crawling and then turning that wound into a weapon. The song's cruel move is how devotion curdles: he swears he lived for this person and now dies for them, but converts the martyrdom into contempt, so the deathbed bargain for an afterlife somewhere far off reads less as hope than as another way to punish the one who stayed silent. It closes not on transcendence but on a spat obscenity, the sob and the sneer arriving in a single breath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The music was written in one sitting the day Mustaine learned Cliff Burton was dead — his grief for the friend the firing had cost him — while the words, by his own account, are about a girlfriend, not Burton: one song carrying two separate losses.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","death & grief"],"means":"devotion curdled into contempt as the deathbed bargain turns punitive","built":"the closing obscenity fuses sob and sneer in a single breath"},
"megadeth~killing-is-my-business-and-business-is-good": {"haiku":"A hired assassin operates without question or conscience, collecting payment for targeted murder as a straightforward commercial service.","opus":"The title track of Megadeth's raw 1985 debut inhabits a paid assassin, cold and transactional, quoting his fee and refusing to hear why. Mustaine's sniper narrates with mercenary relish, saving the twist for the listener he names as his next contract, thrash as pulp-noir menace.","sonnet":"A professional assassin narrates his trade with clinical detachment and dark satisfaction. Payment is structured, targets are acquired through infrared in the dark, and morality is irrelevant. The work defines him completely — and the final pleasure is informing a client they are next on the list.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hired killer delivers his sales pitch in the flat, incurious register of a tradesman quoting a rate, and the whole horror lives in that mask: murder priced by the job, indifferent to motive or truth, refusing the why on principle. Mustaine lets the deadpan tip its hand mid-transaction, when the man collecting payment reveals the client is next on the list, so the professional detachment curdles into a predator who owes loyalty to no one but the fee. The title-chorus then repeats past sense into a jingle, the phrase hammered until business itself becomes the only creed left standing — a young thrash band naming its own appetite as much as the character's.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The character is Marvel's Punisher — Mustaine's hitman borrowed from the comics, then given the twist the comics wouldn't: the client becomes the contract.","themes":["violence & murder","money & the street","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"murder narrated in the flat register of a tradesman quoting a rate","built":"the title-chorus hammered past sense until business becomes the only creed"},
"megadeth~liar": {"haiku":"A scathing character assassination of a pathological liar whose entire existence is fraudulent, tracing his corruption from family dysfunction through criminal behavior and disease to inevitable spiritual ruin. The song catalogs dishonesty and depravity.","opus":"A scathing takedown of a compulsive, degenerate liar, cataloguing his squalor, disease, dishonesty, and vile character while insisting lying is all he'll ever be.","sonnet":"A venomous character assassination directed at a pathological liar — cataloguing their moral failures, diseased lifestyle, and every form of deception — concluding that lying is all they will ever be and delivering a vicious litany of physical and moral degradation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A character assassination with no interest in fairness: Mustaine catalogues a target through a rising tier of contempt, and the escalation is the point. It opens as biography-by-slander, moves to a mirror where the man is asked to confront a self he despises, then collapses in the third verse into pure sensory disgust — a stampede of two-word images (rotting teeth, sunken veins, blood and garbage) that abandon syntax to become one long spit of loathing. The word lie fans out across the whole field: the man's dishonesty is made continuous with disease, decay, and damnation, so that lying stops being a moral fault and becomes the physical condition of a body already halfway to hell. What the song refuses to do — soften, complicate, grant a shred of humanity — is itself the point: this is bile as craft, a written-off enemy nailed down in verse and shoved toward his master.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The target is ex-guitarist Chris Poland — Mustaine's own accusation that Poland pawned band gear for drug money is the documented spark.","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","faith & the occult"],"means":"bile elevated to craft by refusing every shred of humanity","built":"third verse abandons syntax for a stampede of two-word disgust images"},
"megadeth~liar-2004-remastered": {"haiku":"Pathological deception and moral decay pervade every transaction; the speaker catalogs comprehensive corruption across generations of inherited villainy.","opus":"A savage, escalating character assassination of a habitual liar, piling on degradation and disease until damning him to hell as nothing more than the deceit he's made of.","sonnet":"A sustained, escalating character assassination of a pathological liar whose entire life is squalor — drug-addicted family, disease, theft, and dishonesty so deep it has become the only thing keeping him alive. The song is less an argument than a litany of contempt, culminating in the prediction that his lying tongue will dig his grave.","src":"haiku","fable":"A character assassination with no interest in fairness: Mustaine catalogues a target through a rising tier of contempt, and the escalation is the point. It opens as biography-by-slander, moves to a mirror where the man is asked to confront a self he despises, then collapses in the third verse into pure sensory disgust — a stampede of two-word images (rotting teeth, sunken veins, blood and garbage) that abandon syntax to become one long spit of loathing. The word lie fans out across the whole field: the man's dishonesty is made continuous with disease, decay, and damnation, so that lying stops being a moral fault and becomes the physical condition of a body already halfway to hell. What the song refuses to do — soften, complicate, grant a shred of humanity — is itself the point: this is bile as craft, a written-off enemy nailed down in verse and shoved toward his master.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The target is ex-guitarist Chris Poland — Mustaine's own accusation that Poland pawned band gear for drug money is the documented spark.","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","faith & the occult"],"means":"bile elevated to craft by refusing every shred of humanity","built":"third verse abandons syntax for a stampede of two-word disgust images"},
"megadeth~looking-down-the-cross": {"haiku":"A man faces execution and crucifixion, accepting his death while condemning those who persecuted him and bringing divine judgment upon the world.","opus":"Told from the viewpoint of a doomed figure marched through a gore-soaked town to be executed alongside criminals, his death purchasing others' salvation before the song erupts into apocalyptic curses and occult judgment. Megadeth closed their 1985 debut with this, fusing crucifixion imagery and dark theatrics into galloping metal that helped define the genre's sinister edge.","sonnet":"A Christ-figure meditation that slips quickly into apocalyptic threat — the speaker on the way to execution acknowledges the sacrifice, then turns to address those responsible with a curse that grows in scope until it consumes entire generations. The crucifixion and the condemnation share the same frame, martyrdom and vengeance collapsed into a single voice looking down from the cross.","src":"haiku","fable":"A young Dave Mustaine, freshly ejected from Metallica and sharpening every grievance into speed metal, stages the crucifixion from the cross's own point of view — but the dying figure is no meek martyr, and salvation is refused outright in favor of a promise that this death will damn the crowd rather than redeem it. The song then abandons the gospel scaffold entirely, swelling into a curse-litany stacked with occult freight (Choronzon, the bells of Hades, brimstone) that turns the executed into the executioner, so the title flips: to look down the cross is not to gaze up at a savior but to sight down it like a weapon aimed back at the mob. What holds the venom together is the outro's flat refusal to speak, the one repeated line that keeps its evil deliberately unnamed while every verse around it names too much.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Genuine 1983 material — written in the immediate wake of the firing under the working title Speak No Evil, two years before the album carried it.","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"the executed turns executioner aiming a curse back at the crowd","built":"the title inverts: sighting down the cross as a weapon, not worshipping up it"},
"megadeth~lucretia": {"haiku":"A mysterious, surreal narrative of entering grandmother's attic at night, meeting an enigmatic presence named Lucretia in a trance-like state.","opus":"A gothic childhood memory of hiding from everyone in an attic hideout, dismissed by friends as mental, where a presence named Lucretia (a grandmother's ghost) waits in a rocking chair. It captures a lonely, imaginative kid retreating into a haunted inner world, half-comforting and half-eerie.","sonnet":"A paranoid, gothic exploration of a haunted attic — the speaker stalks through cobwebbed corridors under hypnosis, sits in a rocking chair, and is watched by a spectral presence named Lucretia who waits patiently in the moonlit dark.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person creep through a haunted house at midnight, narrated as Mustaine tiptoes up cobwebbed stairs toward a spirit who rocks impatiently in a chair, waiting to be visited. The song works as pure gothic séance until the aside where his friends call Dave mental snaps the frame open: the whole shadowed ritual is being told by a mind others have already written off, so we can't tell whether Lucretia is a ghost, a childhood memory of Granny's attic, or a delusion the narrator half-knows he's staging. Mustaine lets that ambiguity stand rather than resolving it, and the abrupt cut to the instrumental outro leaves the visit unfinished — the seance dissolving back into the dark it came from.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Mustaine's own 1990 telling is plainer than the séance: a guy who happens to be called Dave, in trouble, friends think he's crazy, goes to a fortune teller. The ghost-in-the-attic version is fan lore.","themes":["faith & the occult","madness & the mind","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"haunted-house ritual held open between ghost and willed delusion","built":"friends calling the narrator mental cracks the gothic frame from inside"},
"megadeth~lying-in-state":{"haiku":"Civilization collapses under manufactured crisis and deception, orchestrated by a cabal using propaganda and misdirection to maintain control.","opus":"A furious polemic reading current events as the deliberate decline of Western civilization, engineered crises and staged pawns distracting a numbed public. Mustaine's stance is pure conspiratorial dread: truth itself lies dead in state while the plan proceeds.","sonnet":"Western civilisation is diagnosed as in terminal decline through deliberate cultural sabotage: institutions weaponised against family and faith, crises manufactured for distraction, and a ruling class that feeds the population lies calibrated to the thousand-repetition threshold. The enemy of truth lies in state, visible but untouchable.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~mary-jane": {"haiku":"A supernatural presence grips someone's mind, described as both witch and siren, trapping them in an uncontrollable astral state.","opus":"Megadeth spin an occult tale of possession, the narrator confessing to a priest while a supernatural presence lures him from the wind and the trees. Overtaken and dragged out of his own mind, he ends up reciting a graveyard warning to the living that his fate awaits them too, temptation reframed as inescapable doom. The title nods to a real girl reputed to be a witch.","sonnet":"A figure is haunted by a presence that rides on wind and infiltrates thought — an entity that feels supernatural but reads as obsession or addiction. The narrator cannot escape, cannot determine whether awareness of madness proves sanity, and ends by warning others they too will follow him into the same irrecoverable state.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dead woman confesses through the speaker's mouth, borrowing the priest's ritual apology and a witch's pact with wind and open ground to explain why she keeps calling him up from under the trees. The middle turn reads like words cut into a headstone, the old warning that the living are only the dead who haven't caught up yet, and by the end the haunting has stopped being a voice and become a grip on the skull, thought going numb as the man is dragged loose from his own body and lifted off the earth entirely. Megadeth plays it straight rather than mocking it, so the dread lands: the summons was never a seduction but a recruitment, and the last breath he can't draw is the price of having answered.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Built on a legend Ellefson grew up with — a witch buried near Loon Lake cemetery, Minnesota — and the bridge reproduces an actual tombstone epitaph from that graveyard, the old as-you-are-now-so-once-was-I memento mori.","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"a haunting that was always a recruitment, not a seduction","built":"the dead woman borrows the priest's own apology to make her summons"},
"megadeth~mary-jane-paul-lani-mix": {"haiku":"A seductive force—sensual, otherworldly, irresistible—binds the speaker in supernatural thrall while transforming consciousness itself into something untethered and eternal.","opus":"Megadeth drew on the true story of a girl buried as a suspected witch, her gravestone warning of what the living will become. The narrator, confessing sins, feels her voice ride the wind and grip his mind, dragged toward an astral plane he cannot escape. Folklore horror as a slow psychological unraveling, madness and haunting fused.","sonnet":"Mary Jane is a haunting spirit — or a drug — that the narrator cannot escape: her voice rides on the breeze, fingers grip the brain, and the astral plane is now permanent residence. The epitaph borrowed from gravestone verse — \"as you are now, so once was I\" — turns the song into a warning delivered from inside a trap the listener is already walking into.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dead woman confesses through the speaker's mouth, borrowing the priest's ritual apology and a witch's pact with wind and open ground to explain why she keeps calling him up from under the trees. The middle turn reads like words cut into a headstone, the old warning that the living are only the dead who haven't caught up yet, and by the end the haunting has stopped being a voice and become a grip on the skull, thought going numb as the man is dragged loose from his own body and lifted off the earth entirely. Megadeth plays it straight rather than mocking it, so the dread lands: the summons was never a seduction but a recruitment, and the last breath he can't draw is the price of having answered.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Built on a legend Ellefson grew up with — a witch buried near Loon Lake cemetery, Minnesota — and the bridge reproduces an actual tombstone epitaph from that graveyard, the old as-you-are-now-so-once-was-I memento mori.","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"a haunting that was always a recruitment, not a seduction","built":"the dead woman borrows the priest's own apology to make her summons"},
"megadeth~mastermind": {"haiku":"An entity manipulates and controls thought, overwriting human will and consciousness, forcing submission to its programming and authority.","opus":"Emerging from Megadeth's mid-2000s reformation, this imagines a controlling intelligence that dictates thought, need and feeling to a marching, purposeless mass. Voices from a machine-lord command obedience, overwriting minds and reducing brains to mush in a technological dystopia. The tyrant's contempt is total, caring nothing for what its subjects once believed, only that they submit to the processor's rule.","sonnet":"An authoritarian intelligence describes its takeover of human consciousness — rewriting thought, emotion, and perceived reality from within. The mastermind frames subjugation as a technical process: erasing autonomous will and replacing it with directed programming, leaving its subjects convinced they still choose freely.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is spoken by the controller and never once by the controlled, which is the point: it is a monologue of total domination that lets you hear only the voice giving orders. Verse one supplies the raw material — anonymous, purposeless minds marching in line, their capacity for logic switched off — and the chorus then claims each faculty in turn, dictating thought, need, feeling, and finally what is real, so the takeover escalates from behavior inward to perception itself. The imagery shifts deliberately from the neurological to the computational, brains reformatted like drives and souls overwritten like code, until the third verse crowns the machine outright and demands you kneel before the processor. What curdles it is the bridge, where the mastermind drops the technological pretense and admits indifference to anything you previously thought or owned — the control was never for your benefit and never pretended to persuade. It closes by reducing you to a single word, subject, which is both the grammatical role you have been assigned and the sentence passed on you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","identity & becoming"],"means":"domination escalating inward from behaviour to perception itself","built":"the closing word subject names both grammatical role and sentence passed"},
"megadeth~mechanix": {"haiku":"Mechanical and sexual prowess conflated through car repair metaphors. The mechanic's skill in turning screws and making engines bulge becomes an erotic boast.","opus":"This early speed-metal romp from Megadeth, the original that Dave Mustaine wrote before Metallica reworked it, drapes a sexual escapade entirely in garage-and-engine double entendre. A customer brings his motor to the station of the narrator, and pistons bulging, ball bearings melting, and screws turned better than expected carry the innuendo without a break. It is juvenile, breakneck fun powered by relentless mechanical metaphor.","sonnet":"A garage encounter translated entirely into automobile double entendre — the mechanic and his customer, pistons and drive shafts, the transaction running so hot it leaves machinery melted. Desire is comic and urgent at once, the shop floor a stage for something much simpler than engine repair.","src":"haiku","fable":"Every line runs on a single joke: the garage is the bedroom, and the whole vocabulary of pistons, drive shafts, screws and overheating bearings is a sex act rendered as auto repair, so the mechanical register is the mask and the innuendo is the entire content underneath it. What makes it more than a smutty gag is the servicing frame — the speaker sells his labor at a station, filling an order, doing it for a living, so desire is staged as a transaction where the customer turns out to be the better mechanic. Mustaine keeps the seam deliberately thin, letting the double meaning show through rather than hide, which is why the leering delight lands as the point rather than a subtext to be decoded.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"This music is the riff Mustaine brought to Metallica that became The Four Horsemen — they rewrote the words and added a middle section after firing him; he answered by cutting his original faster than ever, two years after theirs reached record stores first.","themes":["love & desire","comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"desire staged as a service transaction where the client outperforms the mechanic","built":"the mechanical register is the mask kept deliberately thin throughout"},
"megadeth~millennium-of-the-blind-bonus-track": {"haiku":"A dystopian narrative where authoritarian leaders blind and control masses through propaganda and force, keeping people unable to see truth or resist oppression.","opus":"An early, long-unreleased track using blindness as a metaphor for mass obedience. People hand over their sight and judgment to rulers who finance dishonest wars and march them off to die for causes they cannot even perceive, the sightless led by the sightless toward ruin. Classic frontman distrust of authority wrapped in apocalyptic religious imagery.","sonnet":"A jeremiad against collective stupidity and the leaders who exploit it — people are told to surrender their eyes, their minds, their money, and their lives to corrupt powers who send them to die in wars they cannot see the purpose of. The mythic coda traces this blindness back to the beginning of time, as if sightlessness is the original human condition.","src":"haiku","fable":"A crowd surrenders its own eyes on command, and the song treats that surrender as the whole disease: once nobody can see, the people who profit take the wallet, the labor, and the sons marched off to fight. Megadeth's mid-90s contempt for anyone who trusts a flag or a general drives the middle stretch, where the money for war is drawn straight from the pockets that will later bleed for it. It closes by reaching back to a founding myth — twin figures of salvation and ruin arriving together, a whole generation delivered already sightless — so the affliction reads less like a modern lapse than an inheritance no vote can undo.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","war & battle","surveillance & control"],"means":"voluntary blindness framed as an inheritance no vote can undo","built":"founding myth closes the song making the affliction ancestral, not modern"},
"megadeth~moto-psycho":{"haiku":"Rootless motion and psychological instability define existence as the subject travels nowhere while fleeing responsibility and accountability.","opus":"Mustaine turns the crushing weight of adult obligation into a manic escape fantasy, gunning down an open road with no destination because motion itself is the only relief from responsibility and self-loathing.","sonnet":"A man crushed by obligation and disrespect throws off responsibility and hits the road with nowhere to go — the open highway becomes both escape and identity, a Moto Psycho going nowhere at a hundred miles an hour.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~my-creation": {"haiku":"A master creates a willing servant bound by obedience and then a bride to serve alongside them. The creation references Frankenstein and emphasizes absolute control and servitude.","opus":"Voiced by Dr. Frankenstein bringing his monster to life, commanding it to kill and serve, then promising a bride to complete the creation; the deeper read is the horror-story dynamic of a creator playing god, demanding total obedience and control over the life he has made.","sonnet":"A Frankenstein's monster sketch from the perspective of the scientist — a brief, darkly playful retelling of creating a being that exists only to serve and kill, and then fashioning a bride for it, treating the monster-making mythology as a power fantasy of total control.","src":"haiku","fable":"Straight Frankenstein role-play, sung from the maker's chair: the creator grants life only to demand absolute servitude, so the gift of animation arrives already fused to ownership. The two verses trace the myth's escalation — first a slave built to kill on command, then a mate manufactured to be loved, except the bride is engineered to serve the master too, so even the creature's love is a leash routed back to the creator. What the swagger masks is the horror the source story actually turns on: a made thing that owes everything and controls nothing, which is why the boast lands less as omnipotence than as a portrait of a god who can only compel, never earn. Fitting for the Rust in Peace era's fascination with weapons and made instruments of destruction, the point underneath is that anything created purely to obey is a monster waiting on its maker.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"A Mustaine/Menza co-write that surfaced only on the 2004 reissue — not on the 1990 sequence; the Menza credit is what anchors it to the Rust in Peace lineup.","themes":["surveillance & control","faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"omnipotence exposed as a god who can only compel, never earn","built":"the bride engineered to love routes the creature's devotion back to the master"},
"megadeth~my-kingdom":{"haiku":"Power and reclamation resurrect abandoned kingdoms through blood and warfare, with the resurrected king reconciling competing natures of good and evil.","opus":"An Arthurian meditation on a fallen king reclaiming his sword and soul, learning that lies destroy the world and that he gave up hearth and home for the quest. Its real longing is to lay down the crown and finally just be a man.","sonnet":"A warrior-king reclaims his abandoned banner, speaking in the register of Arthurian quest — the dragon awakened, good and evil intertwined — ultimately recognizing that he has lived through others too long and seeks to become simply a man before the end.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~my-last-words": {"haiku":"A speaker plays Russian roulette with a gun, spinning and taking chances with death. The game escalates with loaded bullets and victims awaiting their turn.","opus":"A tense first-person round of Russian roulette, Mustaine narrating the gambler's high-stakes flirtation with death; the chilling twist is that whatever he just said may be the last thing he ever says, mortality resting on a spinning cylinder.","sonnet":"A step-by-step narration of a game of Russian roulette, each round loading, spinning, and firing with mounting tension, until the invitation to play passes around the table and death becomes the only certain winner.","src":"haiku","fable":"A round of Russian roulette staged as a gambler's confession: the narrator loads one chamber, spins the cylinder, and lays his life on six-to-one odds while dressing the whole ordeal in the language of the card table — the bets, the drinks, the refusal of IOUs. The verses advance by count — first pull, second bullet, third spin — so the accumulating ammunition and the shortening odds carry the dread the way rising stakes do, structure doing the escalation rather than mere description. What opens as first-person swagger curdles at the close, where the voice wheels around to point outward and dare the listener into the seat, the muzzle now aimed past the singer entirely.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","party & hedonism"],"means":"a gambler's life staked on shortening odds with each counted pull","built":"structure escalates by count, letting accumulating ammunition carry the dread"},
"megadeth~never-dead":{"haiku":"Evil killers exist in a demonic limbo, eternally tortured and unable to die, forever dwelling in a cursed realm between life and death.","opus":"Megadeth picture murderers who ambush the defenseless and are damned to a barren afterworld that even angels avoid, tormented by the same violence they dealt. It's a portrait of self-perpetuating evil, where killers betray each other beyond the grave and the undead realm stands in for an endless hell.","sonnet":"A hellish afterlife mythology for the irredeemably evil — killers and predators condemned not to oblivion but to a no-man's land between states, trapped forever in a realm where angels won't follow and death can't finish the job. The torment is the permanence: no release, no wither-and-end, just the fire blistering the brain of the wicked across an infinite, lightless stretch.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~never-walk-alone-a-call-to-arms": {"haiku":"A complex narrative about redemption and protection where the speaker offers unconditional support despite past conflict, proclaiming intimate knowledge and the role of healer or savior. The song balances confession of love with a call to unified action.","opus":"A promise of absolute protection from a figure who was formerly the listener's foe, now swearing to shield them, keep them warm, forgive their wrongs and absorb their suffering so they're never left on their own. Redemption reframes an old enemy as a devoted guardian.","sonnet":"A protective vow from someone who once shared the narrator's enmity but now pledges unconditional shelter — they will never let the other walk alone, having loved them even through hatred, and will carry the weight of past sins to stand as a guardian against whatever threatens.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tender voice promises shelter and vigilance to someone who once hated it, and the whole read turns on refusing to fix whose voice it is. Mustaine wrote this in the born-again years after his 2002 arm injury and recovery, so the speaker reads first as Christ pledging to walk beside the wayward — until the same lines about waiting in every doorway to seal your fate, and the admission that the enemy it hunts once was itself, curdle the comfort into stalking. The song keeps both masks live at once: the guardian who loves you unconditionally and the thing you cannot outrun are voiced identically, which is the point. Part II drops the words entirely and lets the instrumental call to arms answer the promise, then Part I returns unchanged, so the loop enacts the inescapability the lyric only claims.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","surveillance & control"],"means":"guardian and pursuer voiced identically so neither can be separated","built":"Part I returns unchanged after the instrumental making the loop inescapable"},
"megadeth~new-world-order":{"haiku":"A dystopian future where a global authoritarian system monitors all citizens through digital currency and marking, eliminating free will.","opus":"A paranoid apocalypse where the apostles have joined the wicked, a global order controls the masses through a mandatory mark, and refusing it means death, currency made obsolete and humanity reduced to pawns. Book-of-Revelation dread fuels the conspiracy. Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash outfit, channel their politically charged streak into this vision of surveillance and total control, the New World Order framed as prophecy fulfilled through fear.","sonnet":"A conspiratorial apocalypse vision drawn from Revelation — apostles defected, scripture weaponized, a surveillance state where currency is replaced by a mark that determines survival. Rights vanish, individuality becomes a liability, and the human body itself is just a pawn in a system designed to track, control, and finally reduce each person to the last martyr standing before the order completes itself.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~peace-sells": {"haiku":"The song challenges social institutions and hypocrisy through rhetorical questions, suggesting that peace has become a commodity with no real buyers.","opus":"A sardonic answer-back to a society that dismisses the narrator as a deviant, defending his beliefs and rights while asking whether peace has a price and repeatedly wondering who is actually buying it.","sonnet":"A sardonic outsider pushes back against societal expectations — religion, punctuality, paying bills, blind patriotism — while questioning whether peace is genuinely on offer or just another product nobody is actually buying.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cornered narrator answers a rapid-fire indictment head-on, throwing each charge back as a question: he prays daily, shows up whenever a court summons him, and stays broke precisely because the bills keep outrunning him. The sneering climb toward the presidency turns the litany political, dragging the founding phrase about the people into the same courtroom that just branded him a deadbeat. Between the accusations he half-promises he'd volunteer for any fresh alternative, so long as this one finally holds — the wary hedge of someone already burned by every fix pitched at him. By the close it collapses into a hawker's cry, calm reduced to stock on a shelf, priced and shouted while nobody steps up to buy.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The bass intro became MTV News's theme for years — cut off seconds before the point at which Mustaine would have been owed royalties, by his own amused telling.","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","money & the street"],"means":"every civic charge thrown back as a question until calm becomes a commodity","built":"the song collapses into a hawker's cry with peace priced on a shelf"},
"megadeth~play-for-blood": {"haiku":"Mortal combat for survival where winning is everything; the speaker sees themselves as invincible, playing only to spill blood and never lose.","opus":"A gladiatorial persona invites all challengers into a slaughterhouse where the price of admission is a corpse, boasting he lives to brawl because he's convinced of his own immortality. The chorus strips away every noble motive, he isn't in it for stakes, souls, victory or renown, only carnage, presenting bloodshed as its own reward.","sonnet":"A battle-arena swagger track — the narrator is an unbeatable fighter who doesn't play for your soul or possessions, only for blood itself, driven by pure aggression and the certainty that he cannot lose.","src":"haiku","fable":"A gladiator's swagger dressed as a shrug: the speaker prowls empty streets looking for anyone willing to fight to the death, casting himself as an invincible champion the wings of God supposedly shield from ever losing. The whole song is engineered for its closing move — the outro takes every ordinary stake a fighter might claim (keeps, your soul, winning, it all) and negates each one in turn, so that by subtraction only one motive is left standing. Mustaine, a recovering addict rebuilding Megadeth on United Abominations, lets that refrain read two ways at once: the bravado of a man who lives to compete, and the confession of a compulsion that isn't really about victory at all, only the next fix of combat itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"every noble motive negated until only the compulsion to fight remains","built":"the outro subtracts each stake in turn leaving combat as its own reward"},
"megadeth~poison-was-the-cure": {"haiku":"The speaker reflects on a dangerous, toxic relationship that initially seemed protective but was actually venomous, with lingering effects that cannot be purged. The snake metaphor captures betrayal hidden within apparent love.","opus":"A destructive love likened to a venomous snake, whose poison lingers in the speaker's blood long after; he traces his ruin and near-death decline, cast as being crushed by someone he loved despite the warning signs.","sonnet":"The narrator reflects on a toxic relationship — initially warm and secure, but with someone who was always a snake — whose venom has never left the bloodstream even after the relationship ended, leaving them sleepwalking toward ruin until they finally let go.","src":"haiku","fable":"Addiction narrated as a seduction that curdles: the courtship framing of the opening, a first touch that felt protective, is the mask, and dependency is what lies underneath. The middle verses run a single image-system in which reptile and vermin colonize the body from within, so bloodstream, veins, tongue and brain now belong to the intruder and the addict's own faculties speak for the thing consuming him. The last verse collapses this into wreckage, a mind reduced to flotsam in stagnant water and a fall from stage lights to a clerk's dead end, the promised abundance exposed as poison handed over disguised as an invitation to drink. Dave Mustaine's documented heroin years give the title its cruel logic, the fix that answered the sickness having become the sickness, and Rust in Peace's clinical precision keeps the horror diagrammatic rather than confessional.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the fix that answered the sickness having itself become the sickness","built":"reptile and vermin imagery colonizes each faculty until the addict speaks for the intruder"},
"megadeth~prince-of-darkness":{"haiku":"Evil and malice personified stalk existence more powerfully than armies or death itself, with the devil as relentless force corrupting all it touches.","opus":"Evil personified as the narrator, cataloguing every affliction it visits on humanity while gloating over greed, betrayal and death. The device turns the Devil into a boastful accountant of misery, mightier than any army on earth and entirely without mercy.","sonnet":"Evil speaks in the first person, cataloguing its reach — disease, lies, poverty, war, addiction, death — claiming to be more powerful than any army and waiting patiently for the last breath of every soul it has claimed.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~psychotron": {"haiku":"A hybrid warrior—part man, part machine—hunts relentlessly with artificial precision, showing no mercy and no conscience in its mission.","opus":"Half machine and half organic, insisted upon as not quite a cyborg, the title figure is an unstoppable combatant with an onboard computer, sensors and lethal intent, poised between mutant and man. Mustaine builds a science-fiction war-machine out of clipped military phrasing, the riff itself marching like the killing engine the lyric keeps trying to define.","sonnet":"A half-organic, half-engineered combatant exists in permanent battle mode — neither fully machine nor entirely human, but something more dangerous than either. Psychotron operates by tactical instinct and ruthless efficiency, a weapons platform that raises unanswerable questions about where the person ends and the instrument begins.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait assembled entirely from hardware diction — targeting sensors, an onboard war-computer, weapons drawn, a vital pulse ordered to shut down — that keeps insisting its subject is somehow still not a machine. The refrain's whole engine is that denial: it names the wiring and the flesh in the same breath, then refuses the obvious word for the thing it just described, as if cataloguing the armament only makes it more urgent to protest that a man survives underneath. That gap is the song. The pre-chorus asks whether he's a mutant or merely a man, and Mustaine never answers, because the point is a soldier so tuned for the kill that the seam between the person and the ordnance has quietly stopped meaning anything — the human claim surviving only as a chanted name. Fitting for the record where Megadeth traded thrash sprawl for tight, hook-forward writing and aimed that discipline at end-of-species dread.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The subject is Marvel's Deathlok — the cyborg soldier Mustaine is widely reported to have named as the model, which makes the chorus's not-a-cyborg denial the character's own tragedy.","themes":["identity & becoming","war & battle","surveillance & control"],"means":"the human claim surviving only as a chanted name against full armament","built":"the refrain catalogues the wiring then refuses the obvious word for what it described"},
"megadeth~rattlehead": {"haiku":"An aggressive metalhead challenging people to embrace the raw intensity of metal music and aggressive behavior without apology.","opus":"A pure headbanger's anthem, exhorting the crowd to thrash until they bleed and celebrating a loner figure who wants his metal fast, loud and rude, disdaining leather and spikes as fashion. It doubles as a self-portrait of the band that named itself. This is early Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's post-Metallica outfit staking out its speed-thrash identity, all velocity and swagger with no apology.","sonnet":"A pure thrash-metal summons — the song exists only to describe itself happening: throwing hands in the air, heads banging until they bleed, brains rattling loose. The shadow figure who materialises isn't looking for community or image, just speed and volume and a certain ferocious rudeness. Aggression as its own complete philosophy.","src":"haiku","fable":"A recruitment chant for the pit, barking commands at a crowd until the head it wants rattled is punished into submission — the violence aimed at the listener is affection, the only handshake this music offers. Then the song pivots from the mob to a single figure watching from the shadows, and the manifesto tightens: no leather, no spikes, no woman, no scene-costume of belonging, just a creed reduced to fast, loud, and rude. That figure is Mustaine's self-portrait as the outsider who was thrown out of the room and came back meaner — the Rattlehead isn't a fan in the crowd but the loner who refuses every badge of the tribe and keeps only the noise.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","violence & murder","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"violence aimed at the listener offered as the only handshake this music knows","built":"the song pivots from mob to a single loner who refuses every badge of the tribe"},
"megadeth~reckoning-day": {"haiku":"The speaker takes pleasure in inflicting psychological torment and pain on another person, celebrating their suffering and eventual collapse. The song is a portrait of sadistic enjoyment of another's breakdown.","opus":"A menacing address to a victim, relishing their pain, tears, and false hope while denying it's revenge, and insisting instead that a day of reckoning is coming for them.","sonnet":"The narrator takes a predatory pleasure in another person's distress and self-deception — watching them crumble, beg, and break — while insisting this is not revenge but simply a reckoning that is coming regardless, an inevitable settling of accounts.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tormentor narrates his own cruelty with relish, cataloguing the knotted stomach and the begging he savors, then insists the coming judgment settles no grudge and squares no score. That denial is the tell: this is not a wronged man collecting a debt but a predator recasting his appetite as impersonal cosmic justice, and the closing taunt — a thing on its way to find you — shrinks the grand day of judgment to a schoolyard threat, leaving plain menace where righteousness pretended to stand.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title disclaimers are borrowed from Tombstone — Mustaine lifted the it's-not-revenge-it's-a-reckoning line straight from the film.","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"appetite recasting itself as impersonal cosmic justice","built":"grand judgment collapses to a schoolyard threat in the closing taunt"},
"megadeth~return-to-hangar":{"haiku":"A classified military facility containing alien technology and dead soldiers who discovered too much classified information about extraterrestrial life.","opus":"A follow-up scenario set aboard a rotting military hulk whose crew was executed for keeping a secret about reanimated extraterrestrials at Hangar 18, the narrator having witnessed and learned far more than he should. Mustaine returns to his own conspiracy landmark, expanding the UFO-cover-up mythology and delivering the dry gag that military and intelligence make a contradictory pairing, a hallmark Megadeth mix of dread and wit.","sonnet":"A return visit to a derelict government outpost — once proud, now eaten by rust and abandoned by its crew. The singer knows too much about what happened there: foreign life forms, executions, crashed systems, military intelligence that still makes no sense. Paranoid and bleak, it treats the cover-up as a fact and the dead as its only witnesses.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~rust-in-peace-polaris": {"haiku":"A bleak meditation on nuclear warfare personified as the Polaris missile system—depicting it as a god of annihilation that judges humanity and spreads disease through radioactive fallout, implying the inevitability of nuclear winter and global destruction.","opus":"Sung from inside a nuclear missile that brags of dominion over earth, ocean and sky and of sitting in judgment over mankind, the track paints atomic apocalypse, a contaminated and expiring world, and warheads left to corrode once the killing is finished.","sonnet":"Told from the perspective of a nuclear-armed submarine missile, the song depicts the horror of nuclear war — the weapon boasts of its indiscriminate destructive power, while the lyrics describe a world destroyed by a third world war and left in nuclear winter.","src":"haiku","fable":"The album's closing track hands the microphone to the weapon itself: a submarine-launched Polaris warhead narrating its own arming as a swaggering deity, contemptuous of the humans it is aimed at and hungry for the button-press that will justify its existence. Mustaine writes the thing as pure appetite — it brags about its girth and dominion, sniffs its own discharged payload as if the stench of rot were incense, and treats the bomb-shelter economy and the cheap missile trade as proof that annihilation is a market people quietly want. The two-part design turns on a tonal collapse: the chorus imagines the warheads corroding harmlessly in their silos, a grim pun on rest that the coda then forecloses, folding the fantasy of decommissioned peace into a chanted verdict that species extinction is not merely inflicted on the world but welcomed by it. That final movement stops arguing and hammers one clause into liturgy, so the machine's private lust becomes a mass sacrament — the mask of divine authority pulled off to reveal that the doctrine of mutually assured destruction has already made civilizational suicide feel like devotion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title phrase came off a bumper sticker Mustaine saw — may all your nuclear weapons rust in peace — and the song itself is older than Megadeth: written before his Metallica tenure under the title Child Saint, making the album's grand finale his oldest surviving composition.","themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","politics & society"],"means":"annihilation welcomed as devotion by those who built the doctrine","built":"two-part design collapses rest-in-peace pun into a chanted verdict of species suicide"},
"megadeth~rust-in-peace-polaris-demo":{"haiku":"Nuclear dominion and immense power; a ruler commands total destruction and murder.","opus":"An early version of Megadeth's Cold War epic, delivered in the voice of the warhead itself, a boastful bringer of doom lording over earth, ocean and sky. Under the bravado runs dark mockery of leaders hawking arms and citizens huddled underground, and the grim kicker that missiles only corrode once everyone lies dead.","sonnet":"A nuclear submarine missile narrates its own omnipotence in the first person — erect, ruling land sea and air, spreading disease like a dog — while the chorus pulls back to ask when this will cease, the warheads rusting in peace an image that is both relief and indictment of the world that built them.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~set-the-world-afire": {"haiku":"Nuclear war devastates earth and humanity; survivors envy the dead in a barren wasteland where civilization is erased to rubble and ash.","opus":"Mustaine's Cold War apocalypse, a vivid walk through nuclear annihilation where the living envy the dead and no one wins the arms race. The Einstein warning about fighting the next war with rocks lands the whole point: humanity extinguishing itself and leaving nothing to prove it existed.","sonnet":"A nuclear apocalypse scenario told in grim detail — cities melted, bodies everywhere, Einstein's prediction that World War III would be fought with rocks proven correct, and the living envying the dead as global darkness sets in and no one is left to prove humans existed.","src":"haiku","fable":"A nuclear reckoning told from inside the flash: the dawn lights wrong, bodies soften to wax, and a species that stockpiled its own extinction watches the arsenal do exactly what officials swore it never would. Megadeth turn their own name's grim pun on annihilation into the argument, marching the arms race toward a finish line where every runner loses and the treaties that promised calm are the first things to char. What deepens the dread is the aftermath it imagines, where anyone still breathing amid the rubble would trade places with the ash without a second thought, cursing the luck that spared them for a scorched, faceless earth.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The first song Mustaine wrote after the Metallica firing — begun on the bus ride home, scrawled on food wrappers, with the megadeath pamphlet that would name the band in his hands; the album cut opens on a croon of the Ink Spots' 1941 I Don't Want to Set the World on Fire.","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"survivors cursing the luck that spared them for a scorched faceless earth","built":"the band name's own annihilation pun becomes the argument the song marches toward"},
"megadeth~set-the-world-afire-paul-lani-mix": {"haiku":"Global apocalypse via nuclear war renders all human history and existence null; survival is impossible and those left behind envy the dead.","opus":"Mustaine's early atomic-doomsday vision, imagining the long-dreaded holocaust arriving at last beneath a smothered dawn. Flesh liquefies, cities collapse to debris, and the survivors covet the fate of the fallen while mankind sprints for dominance and loses everything. It ends on Einstein's bleak prophecy about stones as tomorrow's arms, a young thrash band gazing directly into oblivion.","sonnet":"Nuclear apocalypse rendered forensically: red clouds choke the morning, bodies melt like candles, weeds overtake streets by 1989, and Einstein's prediction of fighting with rocks on the other side plays out in rubble. No survivors — the world afire — is both conclusion and title.","src":"haiku","fable":"A nuclear reckoning told from inside the flash: the dawn lights wrong, bodies soften to wax, and a species that stockpiled its own extinction watches the arsenal do exactly what officials swore it never would. Megadeth turn their own name's grim pun on annihilation into the argument, marching the arms race toward a finish line where every runner loses and the treaties that promised calm are the first things to char. What deepens the dread is the aftermath it imagines, where anyone still breathing amid the rubble would trade places with the ash without a second thought, cursing the luck that spared them for a scorched, faceless earth.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The first song Mustaine wrote after the Metallica firing — begun on the bus ride home, scrawled on food wrappers, with the megadeath pamphlet that would name the band in his hands; the album cut opens on a croon of the Ink Spots' 1941 I Don't Want to Set the World on Fire.","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"survivors envy the ash they narrowly missed becoming","built":"the band name's own annihilation pun drives the argument"},
"megadeth~seven":{"haiku":"Seven deadly sins infect every street and corner of existence, manifesting in daily human behavior and preventing redemption or escape.","opus":"A tour through the cardinal vices turned inward, confessed as forces that drive everyone and hide behind disguises. It works as a plea for forgiveness dressed up as thrash, temptation lurking around every corner, the narrator's own included.","sonnet":"Each of the seven deadly sins is named and embodied in turn — greed, pride, gluttony, envy, wrath, sloth, lust — as universal human failings hidden beneath social masks, lying on every street corner of ordinary life.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~she-wolf": {"haiku":"A supernatural seductress uses sexual mystique and predatory power to control and enslave men through temptation and dark magic.","opus":"A dangerous seductress imagined as an actual werewolf, a venomous temptress whose gaze triggers primal dread while her captivated victim gives in completely. Megadeth pile on horror-film menace and shapeshifter imagery, inflating the familiar femme-fatale trope into a full monster metaphor at maximum theatrical excess.","sonnet":"Predatory seduction rendered as mythology — the she-wolf is priestess, enchantress, lioness: every archetype of the dangerous woman fused into one figure whose gaze triggers animal fear and helpless surrender. The speaker knows he is being enslaved and leans into it anyway, tracking her claws on cobblestone as the price of proximity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A predatory-woman fantasy built entirely from a bestiary of animal appetite — mantis, lioness, wolf — where seduction and killing are never two things but one, the caress already the bite. The verses keep the man in willing thrall, surrendering the fight, stoned on the danger, so that desire and consumption stay indistinguishable. What sharpens it is the final turn: the erotic mask drops and the song reduces to a flat, repeated warning about lips that only lie and a will to kill kept in disguise, admitting outright that the whole allure was the camouflage on the predation the rest of the track had been dressing up as pleasure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"desire and predation kept indistinguishable until the final admission","built":"the closing warning strips the erotic framing the verses spent building"},
"megadeth~sin": {"haiku":"A disarrayed, power-drunk man commits sins without consequence. The singer sees the rise of destructive actions and asks the mirror why humans sin—desire for power, response to pain, or primal need escapes reason.","opus":"A confession from someone marked and spoiled, digging into the psychology of wrongdoing, staring into a mirror asking what drives us to transgress. The arc slides from cold self-analysis into physical breakdown, nervous, clawing, begging to be set free by the very reasons that damn him. Megadeth excel at this interior menace, Mustaine turning guilt and grievance into taut, sneering thrash rather than simple aggression.","sonnet":"A fractured self-examination of how personal wounds become collective damage — one man's humiliation radiates outward, infecting others who had nothing to do with it. The mirror keeps getting asked why we hurt the innocent, and the answer that comes back is just pressure, scratching, shadows, and an inability to stop.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man whose whole existence has come apart interrogates his own reflection, bargaining over what drove him to wrong and insisting the blame was never really his. As the plea sours into cornered panic he claws at himself and shrinks from every looming trace of threat, and by the close he is only begging to be turned loose and left to his own ruin.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression"],"means":"cornered plea that offloads blame before it can stick"},
"megadeth~skin-o-my-teeth": {"haiku":"Someone survives multiple suicide attempts and accidents, clinging to life barely, and defiantly claims strength will come from what doesn't kill them.","opus":"Mustaine's brush-with-death confession, listing suicide and disaster with grim humor while just barely hanging on. Written in the wake of his own overdoses, it grapples with agony as a relentless master and the harsh resolve that hardship only toughens him.","sonnet":"A first-person account of surviving multiple near-death experiences — slashed wrists, a car wreck, overdose — told with dark humour and defiance, the narrator cataloguing each brush with death while insisting they're still standing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor recites his own failed exits, and the sheer stacking of methods is the point: every attempt misses, so the persona keeps waking back inside the pain he keeps trying to quit. Mustaine turns the gym-poster Nietzsche line vicious — surviving hasn't fortified this speaker, it has sentenced him to more, the coroner-tag joke landing as bleak comedy rather than triumph. The chorus reframes pain itself as a possessive lover who refuses release, so barely hanging on and being held hostage collapse into a single gesture.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Mustaine, on film, plainly: this song is about how many times I tried to kill myself and couldn't get the job done.","themes":["suicide","addiction & self-destruction","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"survival reframed as a sentence rather than a reprieve","built":"the Nietzsche truism turned vicious by the methods that disprove it"},
"megadeth~sleepwalker": {"haiku":"A sleepwalking killer imagines violent murders each night, acting out elaborate killing fantasies. The narrator finds dark pleasure in these nocturnal crimes, free from consequences in dreams.","opus":"From the perspective of a dreaming killer who nightly murders and disposes of victims through elaborate, mob-style methods, then wakes to find it was only a dream.","sonnet":"The narrator describes elaborate and graphic fantasies of murdering someone — drowning, garroting, dumping bodies — all contained within dreams, so that each time he wakes up everything is unchanged; the song is a dark comic tour through violent dream-logic.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person murderer piles up his kills in the pulpy grammar of mob movies and gangster cliche — concrete shoes, a rug in the trunk, a horse's head, sleeping with the fishes — each verse escalating the gore. But the pre-choruses keep flipping the whole thing off: he wakes, it was only a dream, and everything is the same as before, so the atrocities never leave his skull. That is the real subject — not killing but the nightly compulsion to kill in sleep, the helplessness of a mind that can only enact its rage where it stays consequence-free, then resurfaces to the same unchanged life. The borrowed screen-villain swagger is a mask, and the recurring line about praying the soul is his to keep quietly turns the child's bedtime prayer into the moment the dreaming turns predatory, so the horror sits in that gap between menace performed and menace only ever imagined.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","violence & murder"],"means":"rage enacted nightly only where it can stay consequence-free","built":"the child's bedtime prayer is the moment dreaming turns predatory"},
"megadeth~something-that-i-m-not":{"haiku":"A confrontation between two former collaborators where one accuses the other of fraud, betrayal, and using the band's success for personal gain.","opus":"A blistering farewell to a two-faced partner or bandmate unmasked as a total sham, a traitor who betrays allies and cares only for what he can extract. The narrator refuses to serve those appetites any longer, drawing a hard line between the other's fakery and his own integrity. Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash outfit forged through lineup betrayals, cut deepest with the jab that the genre made him, not the reverse.","sonnet":"A demolition of a false collaborator — someone who rode others' credibility, sold out, and now performs loyalty while hollowed out by their own fraudulence. The accusation is precise: metal made you, you did not make metal, and the debt is finally coming due.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~sweating-bullets": {"haiku":"The narrator spirals through paranoia and self-doubt, battling inner demons and anxiety about guilt for unknown offenses and uncontrollable impulses.","opus":"A darkly witty dialogue with the self as split personality, Mustaine narrating his own paranoia, dementia and inner war. The unease is that the enemy tormenting him is nobody but his own mind.","sonnet":"A dialogue between a person and their own fractured inner voice — the narrator is paranoid, possibly violent, fighting demons that feel like enemies wearing his face, and the song ends with the sense that institutionalisation hasn't solved anything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man greets his own reflection as a hostile stranger and spends the whole song arguing with it, so the split mind becomes two speakers sharing one skull. The panic reads as pure physiology at first — air thinning, palms slick, walls leaning inward — before the language turns to a horror set: an axe getting sharpened, blood on hands with no memory of the deed, a black-toothed grin coming back for another visit. By the outro that inner war has been dragged into a courtroom of committing and acquitting, dressing a diagnosis up as a verdict someone else keeps handing down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the mind arguing with itself across a shared skull","built":"the outro reframes a breakdown as a verdict handed down by another"},
"megadeth~symphony-of-destruction": {"haiku":"Powerful leaders manipulate the masses like puppets and marionettes. The world descends into chaos as people follow blindly toward destruction.","opus":"A warning about how ordinary men handed absolute power become destructive gods while the masses follow blindly; the Pied Piper leading rats and dancing marionettes depict a manipulated populace swaying obediently toward its own ruin.","sonnet":"An ordinary man handed power becomes a tyrant, and the masses follow like rats behind the Pied Piper — the song is about how easily human beings surrender their autonomy to destructive leaders, dancing their way to ruin.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study of how ordinary men become tyrants and how the ruled collude in their own ruin. The song frames demagoguery through a single controlling image-system: the crowd is not oppressed from outside but charmed from within, a line of rats following the Pied Piper, a rank of marionettes swaying willingly to the music that destroys them. That the anthem opens with a snatch of a Latin requiem for the dead sharpens the irony — the celebration and the funeral are the same event. The verses trace the mechanism coldly: an unremarkable man handed power, mistaken for a god, then decaying into a malfunctioning machine whose head finally bursts, while the earth-scale finale watches empires topple as one unarmed figure stands untouched. Megadeth, whose lyrics through this era circle political rot and the manipulability of the masses, lets the title do the accusing: the destruction is scored, orchestrated, danced to — willed as much as inflicted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The requiem is Mozart's own — the opening seconds sample the Domine Jesu Christe movement of K.626, a funeral mass smuggled in ahead of the riff.","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","death & grief"],"means":"the crowd dances willingly to the anthem that destroys it","built":"a Latin requiem opens the track, fusing the celebration and the funeral"},
"megadeth~take-no-prisoners":{"haiku":"Depicts war's brutal machinery and conquest, warning of the cost paid by soldiers who become disposable. The song questions patriotic sacrifice and shows how warfare destroys individuals even when they survive.","opus":"An anti-war piece that opens with brutal orders to exterminate the enemy and take no prisoners, then turns to the human cost, depicting a maimed veteran left in a wheelchair, forgotten and uncared for. It bitterly twists the call to serve one's country into a portrait of sacrifice and abandonment.","sonnet":"The song moves from the brutal logic of wartime orders — infiltrate, eliminate, take no prisoners — to a bitter critique of how soldiers are discarded after service, left wounded and forgotten while the country that sent them to war offers nothing in return.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song opens as pure blitzkrieg bloodlust — a call-and-response drill where every order to advance is answered by a colder verb of extermination, the annihilating logic of total war chanted like cadence. But Mustaine is baiting the listener: he flags the doublethink outright (war sold as peace, the Orwellian slogan named), then in the third verse the machine spits out its product — a maimed veteran, sound in body but reduced to nobody for no one, handed a wheelchair as thanks. The cruelest turn is the Army recruiting promise of becoming all you could be flipped into its opposite, and the famous inaugural summons to serve reversed line by line, so the country that demanded everything now owes a debt it will never pay. The outro chant survives the collapse, but it has shed its glory: the martial slogan degenerates into a snarl of pure contempt, all cause burned away, leaving only the spite that war teaches and nothing it promised.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"the recruiting promise of becoming all you could be inverted line by line","built":"the martial outro chant sheds its glory and degenerates into pure spite"},
"megadeth~take-no-prisoners-demo":{"haiku":"War annihilates both enemy and soldier alike, trading human bodies for nationalism while the state abandons survivors to decay; victory and defeat are both lies meant to justify sacrifice.","opus":"War is stripped of glory and shown as an assembly line of extermination, then the cost lands on one broken veteran handed a wheelchair and forgotten. Mustaine turns Kennedy's famous call to service on its head to indict the country that used its soldiers up and discarded them.","sonnet":"A war-machine fantasy gives way to a bitter anti-war accounting: the soldier who infiltrated and terminated and took no prisoners comes home in a wheelchair, broken and forgotten, while the country that made him \"all he could be\" discards the wreckage. The final inversion of JFK — \"don't ask what you can do for your country\" — closes the argument.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~the-conjuring": {"haiku":"A speaker presents themselves as a devil figure or dark bargainer, tempting and claiming souls through worldly desire and corrupt deals.","opus":"A cornerstone of Mustaine's Peace Sells years, staging an occult rite with candles, parchment and a feline talisman before unmasking the tempter as a smooth-talking demon calling in an ancient debt. He asserts ownership of a soul traded away, invoking a father who once summoned him. The terror lies in a pact already struck, obedience extracted from someone born into the arrangement.","sonnet":"A figure who identifies as the Devil's advocate narrates a ritual of dark invocation, then reveals he has already claimed the soul of the listener's father and now comes to collect his inheritance. Seduction and transaction replace terror; damnation is framed as a business deal closing on schedule.","src":"haiku","fable":"Megadeth build the song as a working occult manual, the verses walking you step by step through raising fire, marking yourself with the leftover ash, and folding grisly little trinkets into a circle — the recipe's aim being to earn you an audience, not to scare you off one. Then the instructions stop and the thing you summoned starts talking, and it turns out to be a salesman with a familiar patter: he knew your family, he already has the paperwork drawn up, and the transaction was closing long before you struck the first match. The dread is procedural rather than supernatural — the Devil here doesn't tempt so much as collect on an agreement you thought you were freely entering.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Mustaine has said the lyric contains actual hex instructions from his teenage black-magic years — and on that conviction he refused to play the song live for seventeen years, only returning it to the set in 2018.","themes":["faith & the occult"],"means":"the summoned devil collecting on a deal already closed","built":"verses run as a step-by-step ritual until the thing summoned starts selling"},
"megadeth~the-disintegrators": {"haiku":"A revolutionary force arrives on death's black horse—anarchy's fiery invaders who burn cities, kick in doors, and disintegrate order with cast-iron fists. Retribution and vengeance are their creed.","opus":"A biker-gang war fantasy that mushrooms into insurrection, a chrome-and-steel rider smashing through doorways and seizing whatever he pleases, chanting payback and uprising while chaos torches a town. It's comic-book menace played dead straight. Megadeth deliver it as fast, riff-driven thrash where Mustaine's outlaw bravado and love of ruin turn almost joyful.","sonnet":"Pure forward momentum — a force riding in on iron and fury, kicking down doors, taking what it wants, flying the banner of anarchy as both threat and identity. There's no ideology beyond the act of destruction itself; retribution and vengeance are the only gospel, and the ground burns behind them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A comic-book cavalry charge: the song straps on the persona of a marauding gang that rides in like a mechanized apocalypse, kicking down doors and torching the town, and it revels in that swagger with pure thrash relish. But the pre-chorus is doing quieter work, ticking off retribution, vengeance and revolution as the loud slogans invaders always chant to dress up plain looting as principle. So the mask is anarchy-as-liberation and the thing underneath is anarchy as appetite — the free hand that takes what it chooses — and the song mostly declines to referee, letting the chorus's escalating burn-it-down chant become its own indictment of the crusade it pretends to celebrate.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","freedom & escape","artifice & performance"],"means":"liberation slogans audited as the cover story for appetite","built":"the escalating burn-it-down chorus becomes its own indictment of the crusade"},
"megadeth~the-hardest-part-of-letting-go-sealed-with-a-kiss": {"haiku":"A person pursues and captures love, then betrays it through revenge—imprisoning them as revenge is sealed with a kiss beneath the moonlight.","opus":"A two-part descent from love to murder: the first half preaches the painful wisdom of letting a loved one go, the second reveals the narrator instead imprisoning and killing the one he idolized. He walls her up alive, sealing his sweet revenge with a kiss, twisting the platitude of letting go into homicide.","sonnet":"A two-part story: first a man falls in love and learns the hardest part of love is letting go; then a dark reversal reveals he has imprisoned the woman, drugged her, walled her up alive, and returned to the party — sealed with a kiss.","src":"haiku","fable":"A two-part piece that lets its first half sound like wounded devotion, then turns the second half back on it so the tender resolve reads as the alibi for what follows. The obsession that opened as courtship curdles into possession, and the vow to release someone becomes the excuse to entomb her instead, brick after patient brick. Megadeth stages the reversal coldly, so the second movement doesn't contradict the first so much as finish its sentence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Mustaine has said he wrote the second movement while reading Edgar Allan Poe — the entombment is Poe's signature; the specific story he never named.","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"the vow to release used as alibi for entombment","built":"two-part structure lets the tender first half finish its sentence in the second"},
"megadeth~the-killing-road": {"haiku":"A touring musician sacrifices his mind, money, and life to the road's endless cycle. The constant shows and travel strip away identity and leave only exhaustion.","opus":"Dave Mustaine's road-weary confession about the grind of endless touring, where the fame you chase costs your mind, money and self. The stage rush gives way to the numbing sameness of another plane, another faceless town.","sonnet":"The touring life as self-destruction — the road never ends, the shows are faceless and repetitive, and the narrator has sacrificed his mind, his money, and his life to the insane behaviour of doing it all for fame.","src":"haiku","fable":"A road-weary confession dressed as a metal anthem: the verses tick off the mechanics of the touring grind — the stage, the bus, the next plane, the interchangeable towns going faceless as falling snow — with a flat resignation that admits there is nothing romantic left in any of it, just the haul. What the verses report as tedium the chorus reframes as slow destruction, three losses stacked toward the worst, so that the road stops being scenery and becomes the thing doing the taking. Coming from Mustaine, whose survival through addiction and the machinery of the road is inseparable from his legend, the refusal to glamorize the life reads less as complaint than as a man counting what it cost. The outro simply repeats that accounting until it lands as verdict rather than chorus — the point isn't variation but the fact that the tally never changes, only recurs.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the road recounted as the thing doing the taking","built":"the outro repeats the tally unchanged, landing it as verdict not chorus"},
"megadeth~the-right-to-go-insane": {"haiku":"Financial desperation, starvation, and relentless pressure push someone to the brink of madness while claiming their right to break down.","opus":"The grind of working poverty pushing a man to the edge, living paycheck to paycheck with debt and hunger fraying his mind, until madness feels like the last freedom he has left to choose.","sonnet":"About grinding poverty and financial desperation — working double shifts, living credit card to credit card, starving, until sanity itself becomes the last thing left to lose.","src":"haiku","fable":"A worker clocks in for the overnight shift already spent from the daytime one, chasing paychecks that dissolve into card balances before they land, his body itemized by hunger and overdraft until the flesh keeps its own ledger of the shortfall. The song audits everything the collapse has already repossessed and finds only sanity still on the books, then reframes surrender as the one entitlement no creditor can foreclose: the freedom to stop holding the line and let the mind break. The gallows turn is that this narrator hasn't lost his reason so much as resolved to spend it, dressing a bankruptcy as a liberty because it is the last transaction still his to make.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","madness & the mind","freedom & escape"],"means":"sanity spent as the last transaction the debtor still controls","built":"the body keeps its own ledger of overdraft before the mind claims its exit"},
"megadeth~the-scorpion":{"haiku":"A deceptive, manipulative person uses charm and false promises to prey on others, justifying cruelty as inevitable nature—a parable about trusting those incapable of restraint.","opus":"A predator's confession patterned on the fable of the scorpion and its ferryman, a manipulator who swears he's harmless, feeds you flattery, then strikes and blames you for having trusted him. He grows from small-time betrayer into something like humanity's ruin, closing on broadcasts of actual atrocity. Megadeth stretch thrash into a portrait of pitiless malice.","sonnet":"A predator narrating its own nature with complete self-awareness and zero remorse — promising safety while planning betrayal, offering warmth while preparing venom. The final turn is the darkest: the scorpion elevates itself above its own legend, claiming to be not merely an animal acting on instinct but something worse, the active downfall of humanity.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~the-skull-beneath-the-skin": {"haiku":"Graphic torture and ritualistic suffering where victims are mutilated and destroyed through instruments of agony in satanic worship.","opus":"A ghastly torture scene: sorceresses streaking the heavens, a prisoner clamped in a wooden frame, his scalp flayed and his head shut away behind a riveted metal mask and fasteners so his screams stay silenced. The chorus fixates on death lurking under every face. From Megadeth's debut period, it blends occult horror with anti-religious taunts, arranging the victim's limbs into a jeering cross as early thrash shock theatre.","sonnet":"A medieval torture fantasy staged as surgical procedure — the victim is sealed inside metal, blinded, deafened, muted, drawn and quartered, then recast as an occult symbol. Every torment is described with clinical precision. The skull beneath the skin is the horror that ritual and religion both reveal and destroy.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine's post-Metallica debut turns a torture-execution into ritual theatre, and the song's meaning lives in how its images escalate: an occult overture gives way to a clinical mutilation sequence where the victim's every sense is sealed — eyes riveted, ears capped, jaws stapled — so that the horror is not just pain but total isolation inside a body being unmade. The title names the payload directly, playing surface against depth: skin is the mask of ceremony and faith, the skull the death waiting under every face. That opposition detonates in the final desecration, where arms are folded into a holy cross and the crucifix is crossed out rather than honored — the ritual's real object was never the corpse but religion itself, mutilated until only the skull, the thing under all creed and flesh, remains.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The mutilation verse is a build sheet: capped ears, riveted visor, stapled jaw — this is the documented origin of Vic Rattlehead, the band's mascot, constructed line by line as see, hear and speak no evil.","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"ritual mutilation aimed at religion rather than its victim","built":"the crucifix is crossed out at the close, naming the real target of the desecration"},
"megadeth~the-world-needs-a-hero":{"haiku":"A leader trapped by political constraints, puppeteered by unseen powers, wishes to break free and act independently rather than follow orders.","opus":"A jaundiced study of power spoken by a leader dodging urgent summons from Washington and international bodies while soft-spoken tyranny operates behind the scenes and hidden operatives yank the country's strings. He admits the very words he utters aren't his. Mustaine turns the album title into a sneering refrain about a world of puppet regimes with no real leader in sight.","sonnet":"A powerful figure fields calls from the White House, NATO, the UN — and deflects every one while privately admitting his voice carries words that aren't his own. The real power is somewhere behind him, invisible. The refrain that the world needs a hero rings hollow because the song has already shown that no one in the chair is actually steering.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~these-boots": {"haiku":"A woman rejects a man's false love and sexual manipulation, declaring her independence and readiness to leave him behind without mercy.","opus":"A cover of the Nancy Sinatra standard rewired into leering, contemptuous swagger, the narrator throwing a cheating, lying partner's sex-for-love bargain back in their face and promising the boots will stomp all over them. Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash band, notoriously rewrote Lee Hazlewood's lyrics with cruder lines that drew the songwriter's ire, turning a sixties pop kiss-off into a nasty thrash strut, the walking-out threat delivered with a sneer rather than sass.","sonnet":"A hard-edged cover of the walking-away standard — the speaker catalogs a partner's lies and sexual manipulation before delivering the classic ultimatum with added menace. Straightforward revenge fantasy, no ambivalence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Nancy Sinatra's original swings on irony: the coy pop delivery lets a woman's walking-out threat land as playful swagger, the menace kept velvet. Megadeth's thrash rework does away with that distance entirely, coarsening the verses into leering contempt and letting the boot land as an actual stomp rather than a wink. The joke of the cover is that a song built on a woman's departure becomes a vehicle for adolescent male spite — and Mustaine plays it straight-faced, so the closing taunt about not kidding reads less as bravado than as a genre announcing it will take pop's understatement and flatten it into literal violence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Lee Hazlewood eventually deemed Mustaine's rewritten words vile and offensive and forced the track off all pressings after 1995 — the 2002 reissue restored it bleeped, and the 2018 remaster re-recorded it with Hazlewood's original lyrics.","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"pop's walking-out irony flattened into literal male spite","built":"a woman's departure song repurposed as a vehicle for adolescent contempt"},
"megadeth~this-day-we-fight": {"haiku":"A warrior chosen for battle approaches a daemonic enemy with grim determination, sharpening weapons in blood and accepting likely death. The repeated refrain stakes everything on this final conflict, transforming fear into violent conviction.","opus":"A fearless warrior heads into battle against a demonic enemy horde, sharpening his sword in blood and refusing to retreat like a coward, vowing that though he may die tomorrow, this day he fights to fulfill his destiny.","sonnet":"A warrior's declaration of total commitment to battle: fearless, willing to die, and determined to destroy every enemy before the day is done, framing combat as both destiny and final purpose.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person war-cry that treats slaughter as vocation: the speaker is chosen because nothing frightens him, and he anoints his fists and hones his blade against the enemy he means to destroy. The images climb from personal fury toward apocalyptic scale — gore rising past a rider's stirrups, the netherworld's snares broken — until the only ambition left is to die mid-kill rather than turn and run. Mustaine builds the whole song as one long escalation toward that vow, the barked title line locking the momentum in place: today is the day it happens, and flight is unthinkable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Mustaine has named Aragorn's battle speech from The Lord of the Rings as the seed — the title is nearly Aragorn's line — while the flood-of-blood image reaches older, to Revelation 14:20's blood up to the horse bridles.","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"slaughter as vocation climaxing in a vow to die mid-kill","built":"the barked title line locks escalating momentum in place at each return"},
"megadeth~this-was-my-life": {"haiku":"A speaker faces execution or terminal consequence, reflecting on a life of wrong choices, failed relationships, and inevitably bad endings.","opus":"A narrator retraces a relationship that soured into bloodshed, stretched out and confronting the hateful side of himself while capital punishment looms as his reckoning. Dave Mustaine bends thrash into confessional noir, picking apart small words to map the descent that ultimately damns him.","sonnet":"A speaker surveys the wreckage of choices that felt inevitable even as they ran straight toward ruin, naming the self-destructive pattern with clear-eyed bitterness. The acknowledgement that society will collect what is owed arrives without self-pity, only the flat recognition that this was the road taken.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person monologue from inside a killer's head, staged as a domestic breakdown that curdles into murder and then a death sentence. The craft is in the pronoun slippage: the verses talk about a failing us before the chorus reveals that the you being examined is a victim, the switch being thrown an execution the speaker will one day receive in kind. The past tense in the title does the darkest work — this life is already narrated from beyond the verdict, a man cataloguing his own ruin as fate rather than choice. The bridge tightens the screw by extracting the rot hidden inside ordinary words — the lie folded into belief, the sin inside business, the die inside the body — as if damnation were spelled into language itself. What could be a shock-value slasher becomes a study of a mind that reframes its worst act as destiny, refusing the responsibility the electric chair insists on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind","death & grief"],"means":"a killer narrating his ruin as fate rather than choice","built":"pronoun slippage turns a failing us into a victim before the chorus reveals the deed"},
"megadeth~time-the-beginning":{"haiku":"Time gradually consumes the speaker's life and dreams, slipping away like sand while mortality approaches and regret accumulates.","opus":"A confrontation with mortality as the narrator senses himself running out, the passage of hours turned from ally into an adversary he cannot restrain, his squandered days gone for good. It broods on aging and how insignificant a life is against the cosmos. Megadeth deploy hourglass imagery for a rare reflective turn from a band built on speed.","sonnet":"Time addressed directly as a former friend turned enemy — the speaker is aging, losing days he never spent, watching moments fall like sand through an hourglass he cannot stop. There is no bargaining, only the slow acknowledgment that time has a brutal hunger and even the singer is not exempt from its tally.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~time-the-end":{"haiku":"The personification of time (Chronos) claims and destroys the speaker's existence, representing inevitable death and the futility of resistance.","opus":"A frantic closing fragment in which the narrator confronts a personified figure of time who holds his life and marks its expiry. Racing pulse, spinning head and a stopwatch chant turn the piece into pure panic, Megadeth's short adrenaline burst of refusing to surrender to mortality.","sonnet":"A fragment — Chronos with burning eyes holds a life in his hands as the clock runs down. The whole fight against time is compressed into an obsessive loop of ticking and spinning, the head dizzy with the effort of refusing to let time win. More incantation than song, it ends without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~tornado-of-souls": {"haiku":"Breaking free from a toxic relationship and finding refuge in solitude; the lies that sustained it now exposed.","opus":"Mustaine writing the moment of ending a toxic relationship, finding a strange calm at the storm's center. Underneath the escape runs a venom and self-destruction that makes the narrator as dangerous as what he is fleeing.","sonnet":"The song moves through a painful breakup — the speaker makes the decisive call to end things, finding strange calm in being free from a destructive relationship, then shifts to the perspective of a poison voice that promises to haunt and fill the mind of the one left behind.","src":"haiku","fable":"It opens on the aftermath of a phone call that severs a relationship, and the singer keeps insisting the break isn't about anything a listener would expect — reward, recognition, control — only exhaustion, a refusal built by stripping motives away one by one. From there the song turns its calm center into the safest place inside a killing storm, the paradox that names it: standing at the heart of the destruction becomes the only shelter left. Marty Friedman's solo lands as the real emotional pivot, a soaring release the verses can't voice, before the closing address curdles into something predatory — the departing lover reimagined as a seducer who fills a victim's head with venom, leans in close, and delivers a farewell that plays as an execution. Read straight it is a departure; read against the grain, the tenderness of that final bedside scene is the mask, and what waits under it is annihilation dressed as intimacy.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Mustaine has said he wrote it the day he broke up with his fiancée — the eye of the tornado was his own description of the relationship, and he has waved off the violent reading of the ending as deeply personal instead.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the calm center of the storm offered as the only shelter left","built":"Friedman's guitar solo carries the emotional release the verses cannot voice"},
"megadeth~tout-le-monde-set-me-free": {"haiku":"Final goodbye before death; the last words are a farewell to everyone, and dying is freedom.","opus":"A person facing death bids goodbye to those closest to them, struck by how much went unfinished. They urge loved ones to remember them warmly, believing that speaking these parting words aloud, including a tender French benediction to everyone dear, will grant them release.","sonnet":"A farewell note from someone at the end of their life — they look back on plans left unfulfilled, ask friends to smile rather than mourn, and bid goodbye in French and English, finding release in the act of letting go.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine wrote the original as a farewell speech in the voice of someone already gone, an imagined last address to everyone and every friend he loved before having to leave — and infamously spent years insisting it was elegy, not the suicide anthem MTV banned it as. This 2007 rebuild reopens that argument in the arrangement itself: the verse regret of a life measured too late and found small gives way to a chorus that reframes departure as release, the French benediction of love and leaving answered by a plain English vow that these are the last words and they will set him free. The added second voice matters — the farewell stops being one man's monologue and becomes the mourners answering back, the living scarred while the sleeping feel no pain, so the song reads less as wishing for death than as granting the dead permission to be at peace, and the mourner permission to smile.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","freedom & escape","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"the farewell reframed as granting the dead permission to be at peace","built":"a second voice turns one man's monologue into mourners answering back"},
"megadeth~train-of-consequences": {"haiku":"Reckless living and moral bankruptcy set an unstoppable chain of consequences in motion, trapping the speaker in a fate they cannot escape.","opus":"Mustaine's gambling-and-addiction confession, a life barreling down the tracks with no way to stop or turn back. The reckless thrill barely masks a man who has hocked his mind and knows exactly where this ends.","sonnet":"A self-portrait of someone locked in a cycle of gambling, deceit, and reckless living, using the metaphor of a runaway train to convey that their choices — corrupted thinking, squandered money, and burned bridges — have set events in motion that cannot be reversed.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grifter's swagger keeps sliding into confession: the speaker opens as the charming swindler fleecing you while calling it a favor, then the same mouth concedes he wouldn't hand himself his own wallet either, so the con turns inward as fast as it works the mark. Mustaine, writing sober by Youthanasia after the addiction years that nearly finished the band, braids two image-families that prove to be one — the gambler's rigged bets and pawned wits, and the runaway locomotive whose passenger is roped to the rails ahead of it — until dissipation reads as an engine that can't be un-started once it clears the yard. The hammered chorus is the argument: consequence isn't debated, it's iterated, each identical pass another mile ticking off toward a collision already scheduled.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","money & the street"],"means":"the con turning inward as fast as it works the mark","built":"the hammered chorus iterates consequence rather than arguing it, each pass another mile"},
"megadeth~trust": {"haiku":"A relationship crumbles under the weight of mutual betrayal and lies, leaving both parties unable to trust. The narrator aches from past wrongs and fears their patterns will repeat.","opus":"A doomed relationship poisoned by lust and constant lies, where the couple can no longer trust each other yet cannot fully let go, leaving the speaker begging for help.","sonnet":"A relationship has destroyed any capacity for trust on both sides — both parties lied so thoroughly that nothing is left, and the narrator can neither let go of the person nor believe anything said between them, including his own words.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song that keeps arriving at the same bitter arithmetic: mutual dishonesty compounds until trust doesn't merely fail, it inverts into its opposite, so faith becomes the thing that wounds. The verses stage the slow domestic lie — the false yes to being friends, the truth quietly bent — while the chorus hardens each pass from something down to nothing to absolutely nothing, the ache of the body standing in for a conscience that can't be argued with. The spoken interlude drops the melody to confess the mechanism plainly: the singer is lying even as he pleads to be believed, which is why the plea to God on his knees lands not as redemption but as the last honest gesture left. Radio-friendly by Cryptic Writings-era Megadeth standards, its cruelty is structural rather than loud — the hook you can sing is the trap, teaching that intimacy and betrayal here are the same motion viewed from opposite ends.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Megadeth's biggest single — No. 5 on Mainstream Rock, a Grammy nomination, even a Spanish-language version cut for the Latin editions.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"mutual dishonesty compounding until faith itself becomes the wound","built":"the spoken interlude confesses the singer is lying as he pleads to be believed"},
"megadeth~trust-2004-digital-remaster": {"haiku":"Dreams collapse into nightmare as the speaker loses beloved and faith simultaneously. Goodbye transforms into sorrow marking the end of shared story.","opus":"A stark confession of a romance decaying from shared lies, the narrator sore from his errors and undone by desire, rerunning farewells and hollow vows of friendship until neither can accept a word from the other. It finishes with him kneeling before God, asking why faith must mean pain. Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash institution, leaned toward melodic hard rock here, and this turned into one of their biggest hits, a bleak study of love curdling into suspicion.","sonnet":"A relationship that destroyed trust so completely there is nothing left to extend — both people lied until the lies became the only honest thing between them. The speaker revisits the goodbye, watches the same scene replay, prays for release, and admits they keep telling themselves it's over while knowing they won't really separate until everything turns to dust.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song that keeps arriving at the same bitter arithmetic: mutual dishonesty compounds until trust doesn't merely fail, it inverts into its opposite, so faith becomes the thing that wounds. The verses stage the slow domestic lie — the false yes to being friends, the truth quietly bent — while the chorus hardens each pass from something down to nothing to absolutely nothing, the ache of the body standing in for a conscience that can't be argued with. The spoken interlude drops the melody to confess the mechanism plainly: the singer is lying even as he pleads to be believed, which is why the plea to God on his knees lands not as redemption but as the last honest gesture left. Radio-friendly by Cryptic Writings-era Megadeth standards, its cruelty is structural rather than loud — the hook you can sing is the trap, teaching that intimacy and betrayal here are the same motion viewed from opposite ends.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Megadeth's biggest single — No. 5 on Mainstream Rock, a Grammy nomination, even a Spanish-language version cut for the Latin editions.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"mutual dishonesty compounding until faith itself becomes the wound","built":"the spoken interlude confesses the singer is lying as he pleads to be believed"},
"megadeth~trust-spanish-version":{"haiku":"Repeated deception corrodes ability to trust; both parties lie habitually, breeding cyclical dysfunction that neither can escape.","opus":"A dying relationship kept alive by mutual lies, where both partners keep saying trust me while trusting nothing, driven by lust and unable to let go until death. Trust and suffering become the same thing, the singer on his knees begging God for help.","sonnet":"A ruined relationship is replayed through English verses and Spanish refrains: two people who lied to each other so thoroughly that trust has become synonymous with suffering — \"la confianza duele\" — and cannot be rebuilt no matter how much either party prays for it.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~truth-be-told":{"haiku":"Cyclical violence and vengeance poison humanity from the first murder onward; sin persists regardless of religious faith or moral frameworks.","opus":"Mustaine tracing violence back to Cain and Abel to argue that murder is humanity's original inheritance and peace an impossibility. Steeped in his born-again preoccupations, it insists everyone is guilty as sin and the verdict never changes.","sonnet":"A sweeping indictment of humanity's cycles of violence, tracing murder from Cain to modern terrorism to show that the verdict has never changed — sin waits at every door and there will be no peace until the last truth is spoken.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~united-abominations": {"haiku":"The United Nations is critiqued as corrupt, ineffectual, and complicit in enabling terrorism, genocide, and exploitation; the speaker argues it has failed its mission.","opus":"A furious polemic recasting the United Nations as a corrupt, impotent institution that harbors enemies and enables atrocity while America foots the bill. Mustaine's post-9/11 hawkishness drives a fantasy of the whole body collapsing under its own hypocrisy.","sonnet":"A blistering political attack on the United Nations, which Mustaine accuses of enabling terrorism, covering up sex crimes by its own personnel, hosting hostile foreign powers on American soil, and being fatally paralyzed by self-interest and hypocrisy. The song closes with a fictional news broadcast announcing the UN's permanent closure.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine's post-9/11 broadside against the United Nations, sung from a hardened conservative-hawk vantage: the institution born to prevent war is recast as a corrupt, paralyzed enabler, its very name deformed into the punning slur that titles the song. The read lives in that pun-engine of the pre-chorus, where un- becomes the mode of everything the body is accused of being (undone, unable, un-anything), so the wordplay itself performs the delegitimizing the lyric argues for. The rant doesn't build toward nuance so much as accumulate charges — oil-for-food, blue-helmet crimes, diplomatic immunity, the American share of the tab — until the chorus flips grievance into a promise of payback. Then the structure turns theatrical: a staged news-broadcast interlude announces the U.N. closing its doors, and the outro drops melody entirely to recite a prosecutorial list of crises supposedly met without it, each entry answered by the flat refrain that there was no U.N. — the mask of satire and the underlying wish for the institution's death collapse into the same clipped, final sentence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","war & battle","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"delegitimizing performed in the wordplay before the argument is made","built":"the un- prefix enacts the undoing the lyric argues in every accusation"},
"megadeth~use-the-man": {"haiku":"Drug addiction devastates lives, with the needle controlling the addict's fate from cradle to grave despite desire and agency.","opus":"Mustaine's clear-eyed reckoning with heroin, watching addiction hollow men out until the needle uses the man rather than the reverse. The chill comes when he admits he's seen the needle in his own hand.","sonnet":"A clear-eyed elegy for heroin addiction — watching people crawl from the cradle to the coffin, someone close has just died of an overdose, and the narrator admits that toward the end they saw the needle in their own hand too.","src":"haiku","fable":"A ballad-tempo addiction narrative built on one grammatical reversal: the addict works the syringe, then the syringe starts working him, subject and object trading places until agency drains out of the man and into the drug. Mustaine, himself years into the heroin dependency that would define this era of the band, tracks the arithmetic of a life shrinking to unsung songs and unfinished plans, then to a scattering of ash — the small remainder that outlives someone who chose the pauper's exit through the noose. The final move deepens it: the witness who spends the whole song watching others slide from their first breath toward the grave drops the third person and finds the same steel resting in his own grip, the observer's distance collapsing into confession.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Mustaine wrote it after visiting a halfway house where a man fresh out of prison had scored heroin and died that same day; the album cut opens on a sample of the Searchers' Needles and Pins, stripped from the radio edit.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","suicide","death & grief"],"means":"subject and object trade places until agency drains into the drug","built":"the observer's third-person distance collapses into confession at the close"},
"megadeth~victory": {"haiku":"The narrator survives a journey through darkness and near-death experiences across multiple albums, describing his survival as hard-won victory over destruction.","opus":"Closing Megadeth's Youthanasia, this stitches its own back catalogue into a self-referential victory lap, weaving earlier song titles into a survivor's boast. Between images of needles, knives and near-overdose, the narrator frames a wrecked, self-destructive past as triumph endured rather than defeat. It is Mustaine cataloguing his band's history and his own damage, declaring he came through it all standing.","sonnet":"A survivor catalogs every assault — addiction, violence, despair, war, near-extinction — by weaving the titles of his own band's records into autobiography. Each album name maps to a wound survived, and the accumulated weight of that history becomes the evidence that he endured. Survival itself is the only trophy.","src":"haiku","fable":"Nearly every phrase here is a Megadeth song title threaded back into a single line, so the verses read as Mustaine's own discography reciting itself — Killing Is My Business through Countdown to Extinction — a catalog reframed as the wreckage he survived. The trick is that the material is autobiography wearing costume jewelry: a self-mythology of addiction and near-death (needles, overdose, the knife through the heart) narrated as if the band's back catalog were a medical chart. That the survivor keeps declaring himself a victory rather than a survivor is the whole tell — the sobriety-era boast masks how close the count ran, and each repeated not-even-close protests a little too hard to be triumph and not relief.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"a survival boast that protests too hard to read as triumph","built":"every verse is threaded from Megadeth song titles, recasting the discography as a medical chart"},
"megadeth~vortex": {"haiku":"Internal psychological torment and demonic possession where a man is consumed by rage and pain originating from an infernal pact.","opus":"A brief, occult-tinged descent into inner torment, fractured identities and quiet suffering hidden behind a mask, and a bargain struck with dark powers whose bill is now due. Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's thrash institution, indulge their mythological streak, piling up images of damnation and doom around a recurring whirlpool of anguish, with a jeweled crown offered as the reward for a deal that leads only to unending demise.","sonnet":"A short, dense portrait of internal torment given mythological weight — medieval pacts, Luciferian transitions, walls of purgatory falling. The imagery is apocalyptic but the subject is private suffering, pain described as a vortex that swallows everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A descent song that maps private breakdown onto cosmic damnation: the opening fracture into multiple selves behind a mask of silent pain widens, verse by verse, into a Faustian bargain for power and a final apocalypse where purgatory's walls fall and the only spoils are a crown's jewels. The through-line is the refrain — every image of splintering agony, medieval sorcery, and Luciferian transaction gets sucked toward the same vortex, so the private terror and the mythic ruin turn out to be one funnel, the pact merely the moment the inner rot is named as a price already owed. On Cryptic Writings, Mustaine writes the occult straight rather than ironic, the sinner as narrator watching the walls come down and cataloguing the wreckage as if it were treasure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","madness & the mind","identity & becoming"],"means":"private breakdown and mythic damnation pulled into the same funnel","built":"the refrain's gravitational pull equates inner rot with a Faustian price already owed"},
"megadeth~wake-up-dead": {"haiku":"A man sneaks home drunk after cheating with Diana, fearing his wife will discover the affair and kill him in rage.","opus":"A darkly comic thrash vignette of a cheater creeping home at 4am, terrified of waking the woman he's wronged. The punchline dread, naming the other lover, turns domestic guilt into a literal fear of not waking up at all.","sonnet":"A dark comedy of a cheating husband sneaking home at 4am — terrified to wake his wife because she'll kill him when she finds out about Diana.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drunk husband tiptoeing home at four in the morning, terrified that stirring his sleeping wife will get him killed — Mustaine plays the death threat of the title as black comedy, the domestic dread of a cheating man rather than any real violence. The late reveal of the other woman's name turns the joke on its edge: he isn't just late, he's guilty, and the danger he's sweating is one he's earned. The chant that closes it deadpans the punchline, promising a waking that never comes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Autobiographical by Mustaine's own account — he was living with the woman partly because he had nowhere else to go; fans have long identified the Diana of the bridge as a real girlfriend of the era.","themes":["shame & repression","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"the lethal title played as black comedy of a cheating husband","built":"the other woman's name released late turns domestic dread into earned guilt"},
"megadeth~wake-up-dead-randy-burns-mix": {"haiku":"Infidelity's consequences loom at dawn; the speaker dreads exposure as multiple lovers tangle within paranoid matrimonial dread.","opus":"A jittery, paranoid thrash vignette of household guilt. A man tiptoes home drunk before dawn, dreading that he'll rouse the woman beside him, because she can never learn about his second flame named Diana. Cheating boiled down to one sweat-soaked instant of terror, with the grim joke that being caught means never rising again. Menace and comedy in equal parts.","sonnet":"A man creeps home at four in the morning after cheating on his partner, terrified that waking her will be fatal — the dark comedy of infidelity compressed into the repeated threat \"wake up dead\" that names both his fear and his guilt.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drunk husband tiptoeing home at four in the morning, terrified that stirring his sleeping wife will get him killed — Mustaine plays the death threat of the title as black comedy, the domestic dread of a cheating man rather than any real violence. The late reveal of the other woman's name turns the joke on its edge: he isn't just late, he's guilty, and the danger he's sweating is one he's earned. The chant that closes it deadpans the punchline, promising a waking that never comes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Autobiographical by Mustaine's own account — he was living with the woman partly because he had nowhere else to go; fans have long identified the Diana of the bridge as a real girlfriend of the era.","themes":["shame & repression","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"the lethal title played as black comedy of a cheating husband","built":"the other woman's name released late turns domestic dread into earned guilt"},
"megadeth~wanderlust":{"haiku":"An outlaw drifter roams from town to town seeking showdowns, unable to settle, driven by an inner compulsion toward violence and judgment.","opus":"A gunfighter's ballad recast as restlessness bred into the marrow: a loner drifts into some settlement, cuts down a fresh crowd of would-be killers, and won't put down roots even as an inner cadence quickens toward his reckoning. Western graveyard imagery grounds it. Megadeth use the lone-shootist figure as a stand-in for anyone who can never rest before the next confrontation.","sonnet":"A gunslinger's elegy for a life that cannot settle — Boot Hill behind him, another town ahead, the dust of a dozen places coating his hat. He knows the drum beating louder each day is counting down to his own reckoning, and he sleeps with a smoking gun because restlessness is the only identity he has left. The wandering is compulsion, not freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~washington-is-next": {"haiku":"Apocalyptic conspiracy narrative of silent war, mind control, and a new world order. Seven empires fall and an eighth false king—Washington itself—faces inevitable doom.","opus":"Mustaine channels New World Order conspiracy dread into an apocalyptic sermon, casting modern American power as the latest false empire prophesied to fall, with dumbed-down, addicted citizens engineered into a quiet, weaponless kind of slavery.","sonnet":"Drawing on conspiratorial New World Order imagery and biblical prophecy, the song depicts a shadow war waged through poverty, media manipulation, attacks on family and faith, and biological weapons, leading toward a false eighth king whose seat will be Washington. The speaker slides between observer and the evil king himself.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mustaine builds the song out of period conspiracy tracts — the silent-weapons-for-quiet-wars pamphlet and its New World Order companion — and salts it with the Latin off the back of the dollar bill (Novus Ordo Seclorum, Annuit Coeptis), so the verses read as a leaked manipulator's playbook: keep people poor and stupid, corrupt the schools, hollow out church and family until the addicted have-nots must crawl to the haves for a fix. The real move is structural: the pre-chorus starts in third person (there was an evil king who dreamt) and then swaps to first (I am the king), so the accusing outsider becomes the thing he was warning about, prophecy of the eighth false king curdling into a boast. By the outro the mask is gone — the speaker is that king, and the crosshairs he trains on Washington are his own, which is the song quietly admitting that raging against the throne and coveting it are the same appetite.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","identity & becoming"],"means":"coveting the throne and raging against it revealed as the same appetite","built":"pre-chorus shifts from third to first person turning the accuser into the accused"},
"megadeth~we-the-people":{"haiku":"A political rant against government corruption, surveillance, and constitutional erosion, arguing the system has become tyrannical and unconstitutional.","opus":"Megadeth, Dave Mustaine's long anti-establishment thrash band, unload a conspiracy-fueled tirade against shadowy elites, corrupt politicians, and secret orders they claim have gutted liberty and rendered founding ideals worthless. Piling up fears of global control and coming catastrophe, the song delivers a paranoid worldview whose bleakest jab twists national trust into a monument to greed.","sonnet":"A conspiratorial indictment of American governance — secret brotherhoods, surrendered rights, founding ideals rotted from within. The song channels the rage of citizens who believe the social contract has been voided and that revolution is the only honest response left.","src":"haiku"},
"megadeth~you-re-dead": {"haiku":"The speaker promises future revenge against those who wronged them, declaring them dead and stating they will dance on their graves eternally.","opus":"A cold promise of payback against those who wronged the narrator, who swears he'll settle the score and outlive his enemies. Under the vengeance sits a quieter sorrow about everyone clinging to lost innocence, with hidden wounds fueling his decision to cut the offenders out of his life for good.","sonnet":"Cold revenge fantasy: people who hurt the narrator and left invisible wounds are now declared dead to him, and he vows to dance on their graves — taking back power through contempt rather than direct confrontation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grave-dancing revenge fantasy that keeps tipping its hand: the chorus swears the enemy is already dead to him, but every settlement is deferred to a one-day, a till-then, a time that never actually comes. The middle verse gives the mask away, admitting the wounds are invisible and the scars still there under the dirt, so the vengeance is unhealed and imaginary rather than achieved. Mustaine, whose career is famously built around a firing he never stopped relitigating, writes that self-consuming logic precisely, because a grudge that were truly settled would not need a chorus to keep announcing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"a settled grudge that keeps needing a chorus to announce it","built":"every settlement deferred to a one-day that never actually arrives"},
"megadeth~youthanasia": {"haiku":"Society abandons its youth; countries spend more on weapons than education, leaving the damned and wounded of war to accept slow death as their fate.","opus":"A generational grievance, the narrator seething at a society that pours more into narcotics and weapons than into schooling its children, feeling abandoned and discarded alongside the world's condemned and battle-scarred. The title's play on words casts a youth fleeing a destiny mapped out by its elders. Mustaine pours mid-nineties disenchantment into a bitter rejection of being dictated to.","sonnet":"A generation written off by the systems that were supposed to protect them — funding weapons over education, leaving the wounded of failed wars to fend for themselves. Rather than accept that abandonment, they collectively choose their own terms of exit, naming the societal mercy-killing they were already subjected to.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title fuses youth and euthanasia into a single word, and the whole song lives in that seam: a generation raised amid misspent wars and drug-and-gun budgets isn't dying, it's being put down, mercifully and by design. The verses build a grim inheritance ledger, the pre-chorus stalls on a stuck thought about being forsaken, and the chorus turns the accusation outward, refusing the instruction on how to live. What sounds like a defiant anthem for the damned is really the darker admission underneath the pun: to run for youthanasia is to sprint toward a painless erasure that was arranged for them, resistance and surrender collapsed into the same word. Mustaine, who titled the album and its era around this coinage, lets the mask of protest and the death-wish beneath it stay inseparable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The album cover — an old woman pinning babies to a clothesline — is this song made literal: Ellefson confirms Hugh Syme painted the hung-out-to-dry line. And verse two quietly lifts Tennyson verbatim, men rising on stepping stones of themselves, with Tennyson's dead selves shaved out.","themes":["politics & society","suicide","death & grief"],"means":"resistance and surrender collapsed into a single coined word","built":"the title fuses youth and euthanasia so protest and death-wish stay inseparable"},
"megadeth~bodies": {"haiku":"This moment arrives\nAll the road's left-behind ghosts\nI held on too long","sonnet":"The song circles a single reckoning: survival in a career built on exits and returns, where the cost of persistence is a trail of relationships discarded or outrun. \"Bodies left behind\" are the former bandmates, allies, and collaborators who fell away across Megadeth's fractured history — acknowledged without grief, just the cold arithmetic of who remained useful. The urgency of \"this moment I've lived my whole life for\" sits against the confession of being kicked out, quitting, and coming back anyway, framing Mustaine's endurance less as triumph than as compulsion. What propels the narrator forward isn't loyalty to others but an exhausted, private stubbornness that can't stop waiting for the vindication that keeps receding.","opus":"A veteran's reckoning with mortality and legacy, sung from the far side of a long, punishing career. The narrator treats this present moment as the thing everything was built toward, refusing to surrender even as time thins out around him. What gives it its bitter edge is his tally of the fallen along the way: casualties he acknowledges without mourning, comrades whose loss he registers coldly because they were never truly his own.","src":"lrclib","fable":"Dave Mustaine turns a survivor's grudge into swagger, counting the casualties strewn along a lifetime in the industry and coolly disowning them as acquaintances rather than allies — the euphemism is the whole trick, a polite word laid over score-settling. Underneath the bravado about having endured and never quitting sits the old wound of the man who was thrown out before the glory came, so the boast that he waited too long doubles as the ache of someone who nearly missed his moment and refuses to be counted among the fallen.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"reframing the fallen as proof of survival","built":"the polite euphemism doing the work of score-settling"},
"megadeth~last-rites-loved-to-death": {"haiku":"Love burned her to ash\nNow we both descend to Hell\nMy only ruin","sonnet":"A narrator addresses the corpse of a woman he killed in a possessive rage — \"if I can't have you, then no one will\" — the act framed as love taken to its literal, murderous terminus. The twist reverses any self-serving narrative of romantic tragedy: she doesn't end up in Heaven while he burns below; she's already in Hell with him, damned by whatever she was in life. In the final turn he refuses her plea for reconciliation, repaying manipulation with contempt — she kept him on a string, so he'd rather stay in Hell than take what she'd bring. The song moves from grief-soaked confession to bitter punchline, exposing obsessive love as a cycle of control and resentment that destroys both parties.","opus":"A murder ballad played straight-faced by a thrash band: the narrator kills the woman he's obsessed with rather than see her belong to anyone else, framing the act as devotion pushed to its literal endpoint. Damnation follows, but the twist is that she lands beside him in the same pit, and when she begs to rejoin him he finally refuses her, deciding that being alone beats taking back someone who'd only wound him again. The horror shades into black comedy, the possessive lover discovering that even eternity together isn't worth the humiliation she cost him.","src":"lrclib","fable":"It opens in the borrowed hush of mourning — a body held, a lover missed — before the tenderness curdles into confession: the singer is the reason she's gone, killing what he couldn't keep so no one else could have it. The devotional phrase that anchors it means exactly what it says once you hear the possessiveness underneath, and the piece keeps twisting past the crime into an afterlife where the two are reunited only in damnation. Then it lands the cruelest reversal of all, spurning her when she comes crawling back, so that the whole ceremony of grief was always cover for spite dressed up as love.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"devotion twisted into possessive destruction","built":"the ceremony of grief revealed as cover for spite"},
"megaherz~herz-aus-stein":{"haiku":"The speaker has become emotionally hardened and isolated through ambition and pain, unable to give or receive feeling anymore.","opus":"Megaherz, the German industrial-metal band whose name puns on a heart of stone, chart the aftermath of ambition burned out. The fire is gone, leaving damp ash, too many tears and too little applause, and a face grown hard and cold in the mirror. Having wanted to win, to be great, to shed his feelings, the narrator now stands alone with a stone heart too big for him and too small for anyone else.","sonnet":"A man surveys the ruins of his ambition — the fire gone, only wet ash left, a face in the mirror that has gone cold and hard. He wanted to be great and rid himself of feeling, and succeeded: now alone with a heart too large for himself and too small for anyone else.","src":"haiku"},
"megan-thee-stallion~body":{"haiku":"Sexual confidence weaponises physicality as power; the narrator celebrates self-sovereignty through bodily autonomy and refusal of male consumption.","opus":"A lockdown-era anthem of unapologetic body confidence, Megan turning self-adoration into a flex and a party. The bravado is the message: she'd buy herself a drink, and she's the standard everyone else is measured against.","sonnet":"An unapologetic celebration of physical confidence and dominance — the speaker declares herself the standard others are measured against, positions her body as both power and proof, and dismisses anyone who challenges that with cheerful contempt.","src":"haiku"},
"megan-thee-stallion~cobra-rock-remix-feat-spiritbox":{"haiku":"Depression and trauma coexist beneath fame and material success; the speaker confesses suicidal thoughts while protected by wealth, revealing how external achievement masks internal dissolution.","opus":"Behind the winning and the designer clothes, a woman confesses to suicidal depression, a cheating partner found in her own bed, and grief that none of her inner circle moved to interrupt her spiral. She names the ugly truth people would rather she keep quiet, molting to toughen herself while the pain stays lodged within.","sonnet":"A raw confession of depression behind fame — catching a partner cheating, near-suicidal lows described with bitter luxury imagery (\"I'd probably bleed out some Pinot\") — before hardening into defiance, shedding the skin of victimhood and cutting off the people who profited from her pain.","src":"haiku"},
"mei-ehara~a-1orekzq":{"haiku":"A driver haunted by the past; unable to move forward emotionally despite acceleration, watching others pass.","opus":"Mei Ehara's soft-focus city-pop and jazz-tinged phrasing let this address to an emotionally inaccessible person stay gentle even as it aches. The sad driver builds great structures and calls out yet stays hard to reach, and Ehara's coaxing tone urges him, when the time is right, to unclench his fist and speak. Her unhurried delivery trusts that something will spill out even as momentum keeps building underneath.","sonnet":"A person addressing someone who has become emotionally inaccessible — a \"sad driver\" who builds great structures and calls out but is hard to reach — urging them, when the time is right, to unclench their fist and speak, because something will spill out even as momentum keeps building.","src":"haiku"},
"meliorist~symptoms":{"haiku":"The speaker wrestles with guilt for past failures and cruelty, unable to escape the haunting memories and shame that define them.","opus":"Meliorist craft a metalcore self-accusation, the narrator branding himself a cursed offspring and taking the weight of everything on his own conscience. He dreads that his affliction is permanent, that what he's witnessed and committed will haunt him and mark him forever, reducing him to a shattered shell who wrecks connections before anyone gets close.","sonnet":"Guilt for past actions has collapsed into total self-negation — the narrator convinced he is defined by damage caused, now hollowed into fragments of what he once stood for. He fears the haunting will be permanent, and turns the blame entirely inward before recognising he keeps sabotaging whatever might offer repair.","src":"haiku"},
"melleefresh-vs-deadmau5~attention-whore":{"haiku":"A woman aggressively celebrates her sexuality, visibility, and power to seduce and dominate; she is the deliberate spectacle drawing all attention and desire through unapologetic self-promotion.","opus":"A club-electro strut that weaponizes the insult, a woman claiming the label thrown at her as pure power. It flips shaming into self-possession, owning desirability and spotlight with zero apology.","sonnet":"A self-declared femme fatale catalogues her physical and social power, owning the label \"attention whore\" as a badge of dominance — she's the one everyone desires, the one who sets the bar.","src":"haiku"},
"melleefresh-vs-deadmau5~sexslave":{"haiku":"A person submits to sexual domination and positions themselves as compliant property for their partner's pleasure and control.","opus":"From deadmau5's early collaborations with Melleefresh, this electro-house track is unabashedly explicit, a repeated command to strip, dominate and elicit sound. The vocal casts itself as willing submissive over a relentless pulse, with a giggling studio exchange breaking the fourth wall. Content stays deliberately thin and looped, the words functioning as rhythmic provocation rather than narrative, pure club-floor carnality built for repetition.","sonnet":"A speaker surrenders entirely to physical pleasure, demanding to be overwhelmed and declaring total erotic submission without reservation or complication. The desire is frank and celebratory rather than conflicted, reducing itself to pure appetite and the sound that satisfaction makes.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~blank-page-of-the-blind":{"haiku":"A fragmented meditation on blindness, concealment, and the powerlessness of those without agency or sight to understand what surrounds them.","opus":"A frantic Japanese noise-punk collage where fractured commands and images resist any single meaning. The recurring emptiness suggests willful blindness and refusal to know, phrased as pure sensory overload.","sonnet":"A fragmented, confrontational picture of people stripped of agency — no hands, no map, no exit — trapped in a loop of manipulation and futility, building to a direct challenge asking what the \"traitor\" or the speaker's opponent will answer for amid the chaos.","src":"haiku","fable":"Onuki fires her syllables in bursts too clipped to lock into grammar, and that failure to cohere is the point. What she barks instead is a scatter of aborted orders and broken coordinates, the frantic idiom of a body groping through darkness toward exits that keep smearing away. From that stammer the track hardens into an interrogation, hunting a betrayer, then splintering the demand into a volley of questions about who wants to know. Each one gets undercut by a whisper that there is no room left to linger, inhale, or exist. The finish, where the song announces its total void, arrives as the one whole thought it lets itself have, reached by grinding speech down to rubble until emptiness is all that stays legible.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind"],"means":"speech ground to rubble until emptiness is the one whole thought that stays legible","built":"the clipped syllabic bursts that fail to cohere perform the groping-in-darkness the lyric describes"},
"melt-banana~candy-gun":{"haiku":"The singer claims power through a metaphorical 'candy gun' to shoot down false posers and liars while maintaining their own sanity.","opus":"Japanese noise-punk turning powerlessness into cartoon defiance, a narrator armed only with a toy weapon and contempt for posers, using absurd sweetness to stay sane amid chaos. The manic delivery is the meaning.","sonnet":"The narrator has a weapon that is also a toy — a candy gun that can shoot someone down — and uses it as a declaration of self-sufficiency against people they see as posers and liars, with the sweetness of the weapon being part of its menace.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~case-d":{"haiku":"Surreal dread in mundane spaces where nothing makes sense and help never arrives.","opus":"The Japanese noise-punk band sketches a numbed, dissociated onlooker watching violence and misery with detached calm, fed up with what people claim while insisting it's just one more fortunate day. Meaning splinters into clipped case labels and dread.","sonnet":"A detached, clinical observation of a self-destructive figure — someone drifting through rain, pain, and delusion, performing toy-gun violence and slipping in and out of daydreams. The narrator maintains cold distance, categorizing this as \"Case D\" while feeding something in a test tube, suggesting an experiment in watching human deterioration.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~chain-shot-to-have-some-fun":{"haiku":"Violence and chaotic destructive energy spiral outward like chain reactions, each person's hatred or sorrow triggering the next violent burst.","opus":"A frantic burst of sensory overload where thoughts fire like ammunition straight into the brain, hatred and sorrow loaded as warheads to launch at others, chaos reframed as something almost sweet and shining. It careens between glee and menace, daring you to pick the next shot or be dead. Melt-Banana are the Tokyo noise-punk duo who compress spasmodic energy into breathless, cartoonish blasts exactly like this.","sonnet":"Chaos as sensation — something is incoming, spreading through the mind like contagion, and the speaker is neither resisting nor redirecting it but riding it outward as weapon and signal. Hatred and joy and grief cycle into each other with each shot fired, and the only imperative is to plant something that explodes.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~code":{"haiku":"Desperate attempts to communicate dissolve into meaninglessness; the message disintegrates before landing.","opus":"A frantic alliterative spiral about self-erasure and creative destruction, the code as identity or work the singer keeps threatening to cross out and blare away. Language buckles into pure kinetic panic and the urge to escape.","sonnet":"About the compulsion to express something essential that keeps fragmenting and escaping — the narrator screams out a \"code\" that won't cohere, struggles to write or articulate it, and watches it scatter like dust. It's about the frustration of having meaning you cannot pin down, with self-destruction lurking in the gap.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~flipside":{"haiku":"Deception and hidden depths lurk beneath surfaces; transformation and transgression occupy the spaces between worlds.","opus":"The Japanese noise-punk duo spray words like shrapnel here, meaning scattered across a hyperactive blast. Fragments of sellers, cheaters and teasers, of picking seeds and swinging coins, sketch a narrator existing on the underside of everything, defiantly other. Less a story than a jittery declaration of standing on the flipside, sense dissolving into pure velocity and texture.","sonnet":"Slippery, fragmented language of role-switching — teller, seller, cheater, teaser — accumulates into a portrait of someone navigating an underside of experience, picking up seeds to show what they've got, landing on the flipside as a state of perpetual reversal and hidden energy.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~hex":{"haiku":"Ritual and repetition collapse meaning into visceral action, spells and curses become indistinguishable from preparation itself.","opus":"A frantic, ritualistic incantation of casting and undoing a curse, digging out and smashing and feeding something to the flames. The slippage of getting the name wrong and being set straight turns the spell into a dizzying, self-doubting loop with no clean end.","sonnet":"A song about casting and enduring a hex — mixing, kneading, and feeding ingredients into a curse that loops endlessly, leaving the speaker stuck in a dead-end spell they may have brought on themselves through doubt and misdirection.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~infection-defective":{"haiku":"Someone spreads infection and defect like disease, caught in cycles of broken systems and mazes, trying to escape the repeating patterns of their cage.","opus":"Japanese noise-punk built from clipped, glitching fragments that evoke systems failing, codes misplaced, alarms shutting down. Less a narrative than the texture of malfunction and entrapment, meaning deliberately fractured to match the sonic chaos.","sonnet":"A trapped and malfunctioning system — the narrator describes herself as infected and defective, caught in a maze of blackouts and whiteouts, with alarms down and no way to break the chain.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~lefty-dog-run-caper-run":{"haiku":"Watching something broken and purposeless run wild; the only honest response is to follow it.","opus":"A doomed, unstoppable runner racing across bloody ground with no destination and no obedience left, the lefty dog as a figure of pure defiance. Fury and hate become the only things left to sell on a planet where the singer stays lost.","sonnet":"A 'lefty dog' — an outlier or outsider running without logic through a bloody landscape — becomes a symbol of the narrator's own rage and alienation; sick of double-faced people and fickle noise, they send their fury out into the world and keep moving even though they may be wrong or dead.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~lie-lied-lies":{"haiku":"A deceptive person gorges on lies and hides in cages, speaking nothing but empty words while growing fat on falsehood.","opus":"A hyperspeed takedown of a self-deifying liar who feeds on his own deceptions to grow, met by a crowd demanding he vanish. The band frames the whole thing as a disposable game to be reset and deleted.","sonnet":"About a powerful man who sustains himself entirely on lies — he feeds on them, grows from them, rules from inside them, and the narrator calls for his deletion, framing dishonesty as a systemic addiction that keeps him functioning.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~lost-parts-stinging-me-so-cold":{"haiku":"A surreal account of forgotten past injuries and lost parts of the self haunting the present. The speaker attempts to exorcise these spectral remnants but fails, leaving them embedded.","opus":"A surreal, unsettling meditation on painful fragments of the past that cling like ghosts, stinging cold, which the speaker tries to shoot down and reject but ultimately recognizes as parts of himself he can't escape.","sonnet":"Sensory fragments from the past — broken pieces, ghost signals, forgotten seeds — that keep haunting the present, stinging and cold; the speaker tries to shoot them down but eventually lays down the gun, unable to rid themselves of what has been lived and lost.","src":"haiku","fable":"Onuki's yelped English breaks a person into an anatomy catalogue, a tally of severed and discarded body-parts gone icy, needling her from somewhere just out of reach. It is the phantom-limb ache of fragments that no longer belong to anybody. The song's engine is a drum-tight rite of expulsion, all commands to kick and fire and switch these pieces off, yet the violence is built to miss and the amputated bits trail her anyway. What redeems it is the capitulation near the close, when she lowers the weapon and lets the fragments cross from foes into kin she can still sense with her lids clamped shut. That refusal to hunt down her own remnants becomes a refusal to renounce who she used to be, even as she wonders where a whole life of having-lived should go, once she declines to trade it for anything as cheap as pride.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"a rite of expulsion that reverses into reclamation","built":"the expulsion commands structurally built to miss their target"},
"melt-banana~mind-thief":{"opus":"A gleefully violent butcher's chant of blood, hearts, and heads caged as possession, flipping from your dead meat to mine; noise-punk brutality turning bodily horror into a relentless taunt.","sonnet":"A song of complete psychological possession — the narrator, or the thing speaking through them, commands the dissection of a person's heart and head, trapping them in a cage as 'dead meat,' a blunt and visceral expression of domination and consumption of another person's inner life.","src":"sonnet"},
"melt-banana~my-missing-link":{"haiku":"A speaker chases a fragmented memory or missing connection repeated and distorted through time, searching through hidden records to retrieve a lost friend.","opus":"Japan's noise-punk unit Melt-Banana assemble a dizzying collage of looping tape and faulty recollection, the narrator hunting for a vanished companion and struggling to reconstruct something long discarded. Rain-tracked steps, gleaming snares and unreachable calm pile up until memory itself feels like a machine jammed on repeat.","sonnet":"An obsessive attempt to recover a lost connection — replaying recorded memories backward, chasing a disappeared friend through compressed loops of recollection. The narrator sifts through layers of noise and nightmare, uncertain whether the face and voice eventually surfacing belong to the missing person or to himself.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~puzzle":{"haiku":"Fragmentation and incoherence define existence; the speaker navigates a twisted maze where solving problems only creates new ones, trapped in cycles of futile effort.","opus":"Nonsense-rhymed dread turned into a game with no solution, where solving the puzzle only opens a freefall and every piece hides a threat. The mind treats existence itself as a rigged, spiky riddle it must keep solving to keep from drowning.","sonnet":"A fragmented, disoriented inner state where solving life's puzzle only leads to freefall or drowning, populated by cheaters, ghosts, and the narrator's inability to escape or find traction.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~red-data-red-stage":{"haiku":"Death and loss spread invisibly; nerves fray from lies and blockage. The speaker tracks what is lost and longs for escape.","opus":"A frantic burst about being caged and monitored, a self scanning for signals and straining to escape while its very genotype seems lost and dispersed. Melt-Banana render existential claustrophobia as pure velocity, nerves set permanently on edge.","sonnet":"A fragmented, high-velocity song about an outcast or endangered person — someone misled and killed, their genetic line erased, while a narrator with tracking radar still running searches for a way out of a system that isolates and suppresses.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~scar":{"haiku":"A mark of trauma becomes permanent architecture, guiding and haunting equally in the dark.","opus":"A wound that won't heal talking back, a fractal scar that itches and whispers and seems to keep the singer trapped between dream and waking. The scar becomes a permanent inner voice, engraved and inescapable, a self haunted by its own damage.","sonnet":"A scar on the hand becomes a portal to altered states — the narrator drifts between sleep and waking, dream and reality, following the scar's whisper through a fractalized, itchy, stinging maze that threatens to slice through their sight; a lonely kid appears as a mirror image, and the scar keeps insisting on being traced.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~schemes-of-the-tails":{"haiku":"The speaker chases deceptive foxes through waves of chaos, diving into confusion to outwit those surrounding them. Digits fall and overwhelm their sight and mind.","opus":"Melt-Banana, the frantic Japanese noise-rock duo, spin a disorienting chase through screens and cunning foxes, the narrator forever pursuing something that flickers just beyond his grasp. Plunging into a rift of chaos to outsmart his pursuers and decipher looming barriers of text, he watches numbers pile up until language itself disintegrates into raw, meaningless code.","sonnet":"A figure navigates an information storm — foxes and decoys, walls of words and digits flooding the mind — alert to the presence of something false at the edges of everything. The paranoid clarity of spotting tails in the data dissolves into a terminal string of code, the self finally indistinguishable from the noise.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~seeds":{"haiku":"Cultivation and growth, from planting seeds to harvesting the consequences; watching beauty bloom and life fade through cycles of creation and decay.","opus":"Melt-Banana channel their spastic noise into a strange agricultural cycle, a narrator scattering seeds, watering, killing pests and watching flowers bloom, then waiting to see how it all turns out. Cultivation shades into something darker as the tender is dispatched, blooms bidding farewell as they die. Growth and reaping become an unsettling meditation on outcomes beyond control.","sonnet":"A gardener's cycle made strange and cold — scatter, water, wonder, then wipe out the pests, harvest, walk like the dead suppressing laughter as the crop is seen off to die. The bells sing for good and bad alike, and the care given to the seeds is indistinguishable from the care given to their elimination; cultivation and extermination share the same patient attention.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~shield-for-your-eyes-a-beast-in-the-well-on-your-hand":{"haiku":"Surreal game-like battle against internal corruption, seeking protection while resources crumble.","opus":"A breathless, videogame-like nightmare of shooting monsters and pressing buttons labeled greed, blood and risk, warning that you face this beast with no shield and no defense. Beneath the chaos is a parable of a heart turning to plastic.","sonnet":"A chaotic first-person account of navigating a hostile, game-like world full of threats (\"man-beast,\" beams, traps) that require constant reactive decision-making. The accumulating eye imagery and arcade-style commands suggest the disorientation of being trapped in a system that demands constant performance to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~spathic":{"opus":"A frenetic, hallucinatory stream of surreal violence and sensory overload centered on a tiny tormented insect and a fuse burning in the narrator's brain; less a coherent theme than a manic, absurdist meltdown of identity ('It's Spathic!') and losing one's grip.","sonnet":"A fever-dream of fractured surreal imagery — insects, false eyes, ancient names, and malfunctioning body parts cascade without stable narrative, the effect of a mind short-circuiting under sensory overload. The throughline is disorientation and identity dissolution.","src":"sonnet"},
"melt-banana~stopgap":{"haiku":"Temporary measures and sensory chaos mount endlessly; the speaker cannot sleep or settle, trapped in perpetual transition between states.","opus":"A jittery greeting to one's own creepy shade and hazy inner things, the mind casting about for a bail-out, breakout, timeout from being stuck. It names itself a stopgap: a temporary patch over loneliness and dread, not a cure.","sonnet":"A fragmented internal voice trying to break free from a stuck, rotting place — the narrator is both the stuck thing and the force trying to bail out, existing in a stopgap that is neither life nor escape.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~the-hive":{"haiku":"An expressionistic portrait of anxiety and survival where the speaker searches frantically for safety and cleanliness while maintaining a defended emotional hive.","opus":"A paranoid burrow-instinct rendered as Japanese noise-punk: a mind under siege that trusts nothing outside its own defended interior, ready to attack rather than surrender the one safe place it has left.","sonnet":"An intense, paranoid song about survival under siege — the narrator is scanning for threats, shooting down enemies, and protecting something called 'the hive' at all costs, the lyrics fragmentary and feverish in a way that suggests internal as much as external warfare.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~then-red-eyed":{"haiku":"Static chaos and broken locks repeat endlessly while fragments remain voiceless and meaningless. The speaker looks up at crows and rebuilds only to find meaningless loneliness.","opus":"Melt-Banana careen through broken images of jammed mechanisms and sealed exits, the narrator unable to reverse course or escape. Overwhelmed by noise clashing into vision, they cling to scattering debris and question the impulse to hold on. Dead fragments get summoned and reassembled into something hollow and isolating, noise given jagged form.","sonnet":"Locked in a failed interlock with no exit and no switchback, a voice catalogs the wreckage — static lines, feedback clashes, scattered gifts that look like trash. From among these fragments, something voiceless and broken is reassembled into meaningless lonely blood, persistence without purpose.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~vertigo-game":{"haiku":"The world spins chaotically while minds melt and break; reality fractures into lies and traps as everyone spirals until death, heads and perceptions destroyed by disorientation.","opus":"A dizzying embrace of disorientation, the narrator's head spinning as reality liquefies, choosing to laugh and keep spinning toward the inevitable end rather than fight the vertigo. Cosmic dissolution played as gleeful surrender to chaos.","sonnet":"A dizzy dissociation from reality — the narrator sees surreal visions, feels their brain melting, describes a world of trapped flags and fearful smiles, and concludes that spinning endlessly without knowing how to stop is all there is until death.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-banana~zero":{"haiku":"Someone collects lies and broken facts while trapped in false narratives, ultimately realizing change has occurred and nothing can be reclaimed.","opus":"Amid the noise-punk barrage, a mind swallowing small lies and cracked facts until something audibly breaks inside, then clinging to the one patch of ground it can still call real. Alienation rendered as sensory static.","sonnet":"About the slow erosion of certainty and self — lies accumulate, facts crack, channels close, and the narrator realizes too late that something essential inside them has been changed and lost, leaving only the sound of breaking.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-motif~leave-it-all-behind":{"haiku":"Destruction and burnout necessitate complete severance; the speaker surrenders to ending rather than continuing within broken systems.","opus":"A surrender to apocalypse, abandoning a burned and broken city and everything in it as the end arrives. Destruction is met not with resistance but a strange relief at leaving it all behind.","sonnet":"A declaration of total surrender to a city or situation that has been destroyed beyond repair — broken, burned, buried under black clouds — and the repeated resolve to leave every broken thing behind and do the time required before it is finally over.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-motif~mine":{"haiku":"A controlling voice oscillates between offering freedom and captivity, promising reward while making clear that resistance is the only exit and that the mind belongs to the speaker unless the listener chooses to leave.","opus":"A Norwegian darkwave duo trade in coercive, hypnotic dread here, an entity addressing a captive who is told they are simultaneously captured and free. The chilling core is the offer of escape only through death, otherwise the speaker claims possession of the captive's mind. Seduction and domination blur into an unnerving power ritual dressed in cold synths.","sonnet":"A voice with total access to your mind offers a choice: you can end this, or unless you do, I will have my time with your mind — the trap is that closed eyes and open eyes both lead to capture, and the reward for surrender is unspecified, the whole cycle spinning hypnotically inward.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-motif~red-velvet-ant":{"haiku":"Parasitic invasion colonizes consciousness as the speaker inhabits the target's mind, consuming from within. Witchcraft and theft merge as identity and possession become interchangeable.","opus":"A predatory presence describes infesting and taking over its victim from inside the mind, a witch who glitches reality and switches what was theirs into her own. Possession and psychological parasitism dressed as seduction.","sonnet":"A predatory presence that lives inside the amygdala, digests minds from the inside, and switches ownership — \"what was yours now is mine\" — framing psychological possession as witchcraft, the red velvet ant (a parasitic wasp) as its central image.","src":"haiku"},
"melt-motif~this-is-the-end":{"haiku":"Everything burns; surrender is the only rational response to catastrophic finality.","opus":"Bleak surrender to a burning, ruined city, resolving to serve out one's time and leave it all behind. The finality is total, forever collapsing into never, nothing left but the ending itself.","sonnet":"A minimal, declarative surrender: under black clouds that turned day to night, everything is burned and left behind, and the speaker simply says 'this is the end, I surrender to this city' — forever never, no more, nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"melted-bodies~eat-cops":{"haiku":"Cannibalistic revenge fantasy inverts power hierarchies through dehumanising rhetoric; the vulnerable simulate domination through grotesque transgression.","opus":"A grotesque, satirical fantasy of cannibalizing police, using the will to kill as gallows-humor rage against authority. The absurdity is the point, weaponizing shock to vent anti-cop fury.","sonnet":"A cannibalistic revenge fantasy directed at police, instructing the listener to remove the badge and ego before slicing in, set against a party backdrop that frames the murder as both social event and ideological act.","src":"haiku"},
"memi~guitar-pick":{"haiku":"A guitar-seeking artist crafts revenge through music after heartbreak; discarded memories and false happiness dissolve into vindictive songwriting born of loss.","opus":"A bratty, multilingual revenge song built around a running joke, the narrator frantically hunting a lost guitar pick so she can record her kiss-off before she forgets it. Between the gag she sorts an ex into garbage categories and mourns good memories scattered to nothing. Petty catharsis and DIY glee, heartbreak processed as a giddy, chaotic scorched-earth track.","sonnet":"A revenge song that can't get started because the guitar pick keeps going missing — the comic premise exposes real anger: trash sorted by quality of offence, memories of happiness evaporating after a breakup, the world surviving fine without the person who hurt her. The pick becomes a synecdoche for everything she's trying to gather herself to say.","src":"haiku"},
"memi~ny-f-c":{"haiku":"Chaos and mess paradoxically inspire love; the city's disorder becomes attraction.","opus":"A love letter to New York precisely because it's a rainy, chaotic mess that defied every expectation. The disappointment and the affection are the same feeling.","sonnet":"New York is not what the speaker expected — it never stops raining, everything is a mess — and that gap between expectation and reality is precisely why they love it.","src":"haiku"},
"memorist~second-sequence":{"opus":"The gradual decay of a relationship in which the narrator shoulders the blame and watches his partner self-destruct; he tried to guide them clear but they lost their bearings, and he ends up paying for it, dissolving into nothing while they claim everything.","sonnet":"A breakup told from the perspective of the one who was used — the narrator finally sees clearly that they were always going to be blamed, that the other person took whatever they could and left, and now they are fading as a result of trying to lead someone out who never wanted to leave.","src":"sonnet"},
"memphis-may-fire~bleed-me-dry":{"opus":"A metalcore declaration of survival against betrayal and manipulation, the narrator refusing to be drained by parasites and false friends. Hatred and blame roll off as he vows this is the last time he'll be bled dry, cutting out those who wished him dead. Defiance hardens into hard-won self-preservation.","sonnet":"A song about cutting off a parasitic person who has been draining the narrator for too long — after enduring constant criticism and fake concern, the narrator declares this the last time they will allow themselves to be bled, and prepares to surgically remove the leech from their life.","src":"sonnet"},
"memphis-may-fire~death-inside":{"haiku":"Temporal dread and internal dissolution advance as the narrator faces mortality while demons feast on untapped potential and broken faith.","opus":"A metalcore wrestling match with intrusive thoughts and the fear of being a burden, personifying depression as a death growing inside. It lands in the honesty of the doubt, the narrator half-convinced the people he loves would be better off without him.","sonnet":"A speaker fighting a growing internal darkness — intrusive thoughts, demons feeding on anxiety, the hourglass running out — refuses to surrender to it even as they acknowledge it feels like death inside, pressing toward the end rather than getting lost in their own head.","src":"haiku"},
"memphis-may-fire~love-is-war":{"haiku":"Romance becomes literal battlefield where betrayal launches weapons and defense crumbles. Neither side can find closure, only the smoking aftermath of mutual destruction.","opus":"Metalcore built entirely on battlefield imagery for a collapsing relationship, two people dug in across no-man's-land with words for weapons and no truce offered. The recurring order to strike the heart while sparing the blood captures a fight nobody wants but neither can stop, resolution impossible once each has betrayed the thing they were defending. Exhaustion dressed as combat.","sonnet":"A relationship described entirely through combat metaphors — opposing sides, bullets flying, white flags, trenches, words as weapons — reaches the moment where both people are out of ammunition and still aimed at each other's hearts; 'love's war when both sides betray it' is the thesis, and the call to 'charge' at the end is either a final assault or a surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"memphis-may-fire~misery":{"haiku":"Pain becomes forge for authentic self; the speaker accepts suffering as necessary transformation.","opus":"A recovery narrative forged in metalcore fire, the narrator reaching for a bottle to quiet inner voices before realizing the pain must be endured rather than muted. Rock bottom becomes a forge, wounds turning to scars, hell the only place he truly meets himself. The hard admission that he creates his own suffering drives the turn toward being remade.","sonnet":"Substance abuse as an escape from inner noise only amplifies the voices; the speaker discovers that going through hell — not around it — is the only path to self-knowledge, and that misery itself is the forge in which they are remade.","src":"haiku"},
"memphis-may-fire~necessary-evil":{"haiku":"Suffering and love intertwine inseparably as pain becomes the price of existence and connection. The speaker accepts contradiction as inevitable, where pleasure and hurt remain bound.","opus":"A grim acceptance that pain and loss are unavoidable and control is an illusion, so one must either be crushed by doom or learn to use the grave as a tool. Life is framed as a game won only in the mind, where suffering is simply the price of everything.","sonnet":"A philosophical submission to the unavoidable pain of existence: life is pain, love is lethal, control is an illusion you lose the moment you grip it — the balanced scale demands a bow, and the only move is to embrace the grave and learn to use what hurts.","src":"haiku"},
"memphis-may-fire~overdose-feat-blindside":{"haiku":"Excess becomes the only language for feeling alive, escalation chases a high that numbs pain, Russian roulette with time the only game worth playing.","opus":"A brutally honest portrait of an all-or-nothing temperament that treats moderation as meaningless and chases extremes to the point of self-destruction, knowing the truth is a bitter pill and that change today may be the only way to see tomorrow.","sonnet":"An honest reckoning with addictive personality — the speaker doesn't want moderation, craves the peak and the crash in equal measure, acknowledging they're chasing a high that leads to coma-level loss of control, and hating it while being unable to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"memphis-may-fire~the-unfaithful":{"haiku":"Infidelity and betrayal ignite rage; the narrator exposes the unfaithful partner's deception and reclaims agency by severing the relationship.","opus":"The furious end of a relationship built on a partner's lie, demanding a confession while already holding the proof of betrayal. Underneath the rage is the wearier ache of years spent building someone up while being torn down, and the relief of finally saying goodbye.","sonnet":"A betrayed partner confronts a cheating lover with a mix of cold fury and wounded disbelief, demanding honesty, cataloguing the wasted years, and ultimately choosing forgiveness as a way of expelling the other person rather than reconciling.","src":"haiku"},
"mentally-blind~everything-s-great":{"haiku":"Behind smiles and laughter lie suicidal thoughts; others offer superficial advice while the speaker observes darkness masquerading as normalcy.","opus":"A bitter portrait of depression met with useless platitudes ('calm down, it's in your head'); behind every smile and laugh the narrator sees suicide, and the cheerful 'everything's great' is a mask over what he flatly calls a fucking nightmare.","sonnet":"A sardonic takedown of the \"just cheer up\" dismissal of mental illness — the narrator describes hiding suicidal despair behind a smile while others lecture them, puncturing the surface performance with the blunt admission that it's a nightmare.","src":"haiku"},
"mercury~swarm-the-hive-mind":{"haiku":"Conformity and collective madness engulf individuality, with herd mentality crushing anyone who refuses to assimilate.","opus":"A raging repudiation of conformity: the masses trudge along in matching outfits and business attire, doomed by their own arrangement, while outsiders are shredded, and the collective-mind outlook itself is cast as the force devouring the individual.","sonnet":"About collective conformity killing individual thought — people in uniforms and suits death-spiral by design into a hive mind that destroys anyone who doesn't fit; the song calls for swarming and disrupting that hive rather than submitting to it.","src":"haiku"},
"merge~shades":{"haiku":"Isolation persists as inner hurricane while outward appearance remains neutral; the speaker fights internal war seeking recognition and transcendence.","opus":"Someone drifting like a ghost while waging war on himself insists he doesn't want to die, only to rise, even as darker urges toward destruction surface. The fight to find who he is teeters between self-salvation and self-annihilation.","sonnet":"A person who looks calm from the outside — wandering like a shade — is fighting a hurricane within, simultaneously desperate not to die and unable to let anyone close; the climax turns violent and inward, promising to tear open the self to find who they are.","src":"haiku"},
"meryl-streek~death-to-the-landlord":{"haiku":"Systemic exploitation through housing accumulates generational rage; the speaker names corruption while recognising widespread complicity.","opus":"A scorching working-class fury aimed at Irish property owners and politicians who grow rich off the poor while the powerless are left to clean up and buckle under stress. It calls out its foes head-on and vows the next track will list each of them individually.","sonnet":"A furious, profane Irish broadside against landlords and politicians — specifically those profiting from housing scarcity while ordinary people face debt, panic attacks, and homelessness — naming the Dáil, RTÉ, and the system's rot by category even if not yet by individual name.","src":"haiku"},
"merzhin~l-tincelle":{"haiku":"A musician creates a spark of inspiration and musical muse that grows into a flame, transforming them into a shaman through the power of melody and song. Music becomes their path and redemption.","opus":"A French ode to musical inspiration personified as a spark and muse that grows into a blazing fire, haunting and possessing the singer who can only sing; the deeper read is the artist's total surrender to his creative flame, a mistress who transforms his misfortunes and carries him through melodrama toward the joy of song.","sonnet":"A song about the spark of musical inspiration — the narrator describes how a single flame, a muse, has grown into a consuming fire that haunts his dreams, shapes his poetry, and transforms him into something like a shaman on stage; without the ability to do anything else, all he can do is sing.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~bleed":{"haiku":"Physical suffering and internal parasitic degradation gradually consume the speaker's body and consciousness as they lose control.","opus":"A clinical, mechanical account of the body becoming the instrument of its own destruction — pain rendered as machinery, an inner voice coldly ordering the bleeding. Meshuggah's polyrhythmic grind mirrors the theme: agony as a self in mutiny, engineering its own extinction with the precision of clockwork.","sonnet":"A body horror experience rendered from the inside — some internal mechanism (likely an intracerebral haemorrhage or psychological rupture imagined physically) bleeds without stopping, commanding the narrator to bleed further until extinction arrives.","src":"haiku","fable":"A body narrates its own dismantling as though something mechanical had been installed under the skin and switched on — pain arrives in measured pulses, blood is drawn out in deliberate strokes, and the speaker registers the process with clinical detachment, recognizing it not as an attack but as an instruction he is powerless to refuse. The antagonist is internal and automated: a self that has turned against itself and now runs on its own momentum, issuing a single-word order the narrator obeys even as it erases him. What makes the read inseparable from the recording is that the machinery in the words is literally the machinery in the music — Meshuggah build the song on a double-bass figure so metronomic and punishing it became a rite-of-passage endurance test for drummers, so the thing grinding the speaker down and the thing hammering the listener are one engine, and giving in to it plays as the only honest response.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"an automated interior command the speaker obeys as it erases him","built":"the grinding drum engine and the thing destroying the narrator are one"},
"meshuggah~combustion": {"haiku":"Human hatred and moral corruption are laid bare in absolute clarity, burning away all illusion. The song frames humanity's capacity for evil as vividly illuminated truth.","opus":"The song stages a moment of pitiless, total lucidity where every comforting delusion about human nature collapses. What's exposed underneath is the seething malice and greed lurking in everyone, the noble idea of humanity reduced to ash and lied to.","sonnet":"The song presents a moment of brutal, unwanted clarity in which all illusions about humanity are stripped away, revealing the full picture of human hatred, greed, and self-deception. The speaker commands the listener to stare directly at this reflection — a portrait painted with lies on a canvas of suffering.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rite of perfect seeing that curdles into self-immolation. The track stacks the vocabulary of total vision — sharpened focus, blown-wide pupils, every soft edge burned off — as though a viewer were promised revelation, then made to face only the greed and cruelty coiled inside the species. Meshuggah drive the piled negations against a polymetric riff so exact it forbids escape, each denial another rivet locking the witness in place. The closing verse dismantles the picture piece by piece: the gilded frame, the weave of tormented souls, the maker who dreamed the whole thing into being, all fed to the same fire. Its cruelty is that unflinching recognition here reads not as ascent but as being consumed by what you finally admit you are.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","alienation & emptiness","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"recognition of the self as the atrocity witnessed","built":"polymetric exactitude rivets the witness so no exit is possible"},
"meshuggah~concatenation":{"haiku":"A consciousness is fused with its own reflection or doppelgänger, trapped in a mental cage of identity confusion and suffering.","opus":"A claustrophobic split-self horror: the narrator trapped in his own flesh, rotting behind his face, fused with a reflection he cannot separate from, pleading to be plugged back in and reconnected to a soul that's slipping away. Duality collapses until he fades into non-existence. Meshuggah, the Swedish pioneers of polyrhythmic djent, pair this dissociative dread with grinding, mechanical precision that mirrors the lyric's man-as-machine unraveling.","sonnet":"A mind fused with its own reflection until neither can separate — the self locked inside a cage of flesh that it no longer solely inhabits, watching its words come out as someone else's thoughts. The plea to be reconnected to one's own soul carries no hope of resolution; merging is suffering, and both selves drown in the same agony.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~dehumanization":{"haiku":"A fragmented self disintegrates into abstraction and contradiction—electrical impulses, malformed code, anti-logic. The speaker experiences dissolution where meaning collapses and existence becomes rhythm without coherence.","opus":"A cold, abstract vision of the self dissolving into machinery, the narrator reduced to garbled signals and a corrupted, half-finished shutdown code stripped of reason. The song dwells on the eerie, almost transcendent horror of a human consciousness collapsing into pure contradiction and emptiness.","sonnet":"A consciousness that has become purely mechanical — electrical impulses, malfunctioning death-code — describes losing meaning, logic, and the ability to communicate, finding a perverse sweetness in the noise that decodes it into non-existence.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~disenchantment":{"haiku":"A person feels paralyzed by fear while struggling against internal slavery, attempting escape only to find that resistance becomes the very restraint.","opus":"In Meshuggah's thick, machine-like language, this crams a suffocating self-portrait into a handful of lines: a being made of dread, raw wiring laid bare, dragging itself toward interior visions of agony. It holds its breath to flee a felt captivity, only for the very effort to break loose to become the chains. Short and airless, it makes the fight against confinement its own snare.","sonnet":"A consciousness paralyzed by its own terror finds that every attempt to escape internal anguish tightens the trap — the very act of struggling against self-imposed restraint becomes the mechanism of confinement. Fear and the effort to flee it are revealed as the same unbreakable loop.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~don-t-speak":{"haiku":"A consciousness fractured and imprisoned within its own fragmented self, splitting between observer and observed until both identities collapse into shared suffering and mutual entrapment.","opus":"Meshuggah stage psychic collapse in polyrhythmic dread, the narrator locked inside a rotting fleshly husk he no longer knows as his own. A twinned mind divides and merges with its mirror image, self and other snarled past untangling until identity dwindles toward nothingness. Estrangement from one's own body and awareness rendered airless and mechanical.","sonnet":"A consciousness that has merged with its own reflection until neither can be separated — the self is now shared with its double, the face is used by someone else, and all thought is contaminated by the parasite of the other self. The desperate plea to be \"reconnected to myself\" arrives too late: the coalescence is done, the sentence carried out for eternity.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~electric-red": {"haiku":"Humanity strips to mechanical obedience while blood and shame accumulate beneath a system designed for domination. The red flood of lies drowns all resistance and authentic thought.","opus":"Humanity reduced to obedient, mass-machined drones, blood-soaked witnesses complicit in a system of lies, their capacity for thought deliberately drowned in engineered ignorance.","sonnet":"Humanity is stripped to obedient machinery — flesh puppets, clones, mute witnesses keeping the bolts of lies tight — their thoughts stained and defiled by a scarlet flood of complicity, painted electric red by the willful ignorance that sustains the system.","src":"haiku","fable":"Haake stages a single reddening conceit for manufactured complicity: bodies stripped to a maintenance layer for the system, tooled on an assembly line into interchangeable drones, then wrung out like cloth to sponge an authority's crimes into forgiveness. The motion travels inward — first the flesh is flayed and duplicated, then it becomes the surface that hides a spreading blot rather than reveals it, and at last cognition itself is drowned and dyed, each idea corrupted to the hue named in the title. That final saturation is the point: not literal gore but a mind so glutted with falsehood it turns camouflage, its ignorance no longer endured but embraced as a finished condition. The dread here belongs to obZen's erasure of the human: we are not the machine's casualties but the wrenches that hold its lying bolts fast.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"complicity completed when the mind embraces its own dyeing","built":"the inward arc moves from flesh to cognition as the title colour saturates both"},
"meshuggah~future-breed-machine-live":{"haiku":"Human consciousness converts into automated servitude through insidious reprogramming, each new iteration further hollowed of autonomy until only obedience patterns remain.","opus":"Humanity mass-produced into obedient machine-parts, evolution running backwards as flesh is dissected and rebuilt to serve a system. Meshuggah's mechanized precision embodies the dread: identity erased, thought remoted, the self dissolving into interchangeable units.","sonnet":"Humanity is absorbed into a machine order that programs obedience, strips individuality, and converts people into interchangeable servile units. Evolution runs in reverse as flesh yields to circuit and autonomy is replaced by remote-controlled compliance — the speaker finds themselves mechanised, their identity dissolved into the system.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~lethargica": {"haiku":"An indifferent void methodically destroys all life with calm, efficient cruelty, composed and unmoved by screams until human breath finally ceases.","opus":"Emptiness given a body as a slow, unbothered devouring apparatus, jaws crushing without haste or spite, a flawless machine of extinction methodically laboring toward the final gasp of mankind. Its terror is exactly its patience, chill and unwavering until nothing remains. Meshuggah cast existential ruin as an unstoppable mechanism, their downtuned, mathematically rigid assault embodying the heavy, deliberate mass-killing the words describe.","sonnet":"Extinction personified as a slow, indifferent machine — not savage but methodical, grinding through everything without hurry or hatred, unmoved by screaming. The horror is in its patience: a god of omnicidal design that has been perfecting itself for ages and has no need to rush because there is nothing it will not eventually reduce to silence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Meshuggah's masterstroke here is refusing to make annihilation dramatic: the destroyer is not a raging beast but a slow, bored, immaculately engineered apparatus that kills by torpid inevitability rather than fury, molars grinding at a pace that never quickens no matter how loud the screaming. Fittingly for a band whose polymetric drone is itself a study in remorseless mechanical patience, the entity is cast as a flawless engine of extermination refined across aeons, calm and antiseptic precisely because it feels nothing about what it does. The whole gravity comes from that mismatch between cosmic finality and total indifference, riding the arc down to the last human breath as if erasure were merely a process running to completion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"annihilation rendered mundane by the engine's total indifference","built":"the band's remorseless drone enacts the unhurried apparatus it describes"},
"meshuggah~new-millenium-cyanide-christ":{"haiku":"Human transformation through self-mutilation and technological augmentation creates apotheosis; pain and modification spawn divinity.","opus":"A grotesque transhumanist parable from Meshuggah, a narrator rebuilding his body part by part, replacing bones with bars and sealing off senses to purge congenital human flaws. He crowns himself a messiah of self-mutilation, inviting disciples into a cult of pain and cyanide. Beneath the horror lies satire of the delusion that mutilating humanity could make it divine.","sonnet":"A speaker undertakes a grotesque self-modification — replacing bones with aluminium bars, eyes with fuses, mouth with sharpened bolts — to purge humanity's congenital flaws and become a baptised, perfect new being, calling on disciples to follow this new messiah of cyanide into a cleansed eternity.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~obzen": {"haiku":"The song finds divine meaning in decay and depravity. A zen state emerges from bloodshed and corruption; salvation dwells in vomit and the grime of human creation.","opus":"A profane reversal in which enlightenment sprouts from squalor, deliverance from bile and gore, and violence, rot and suffering are exalted as the divine. Human depravity is recast as a calm new creed, poise found in ruin. The album title welds obscenity to zen. Meshuggah, Sweden's djent pioneers, deliver this nihilist scripture through crushing polyrhythms that make the rot resemble sacred rite.","sonnet":"Humanity has found its true religion in filth and violence, and the song refuses to mourn it — instead diagnosing the equilibrium with cold clarity. War, corruption, decay, and pain are not aberrations but the actual gods being worshipped, and the congregation flourishes in the blood-red soil they have made of the world.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title fuses obscene and zen, and the lyric follows that collapse to its logical end: a serenity attained not despite humanity's cruelty but through it, worshipfully. Enlightenment is reframed as arrival at a stable equilibrium where war, decay and pain are not fallen states to transcend but the very ground of devotion — the sediment we flourish in. Meshuggah's mechanical, self-cancelling rhythmic grid is the argument in sound: the calm is real, which is exactly what makes it monstrous. This is not a song condemning depravity from outside; it inhabits the mask of a species that has made peace with its own vileness and calls that peace holy.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Haake has explained the coinage himself — obscene fused with zen, mankind finding its equilibrium in violence and blood — with the album’s bloodied lotus-pose cover as the image of it.","themes":["faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"serenity located inside cruelty rather than beyond it","built":"the self-cancelling rhythmic grid is the monstrous calm made audible"},
"meshuggah~perpetual-black-second":{"haiku":"A traumatic moment of violent rage is eternally replayed in memory, creating endless regret and self-loathing. The speaker's own violence haunts them as an inescapable demon they struggle to forgive.","opus":"Meshuggah, Swedish architects of polymetric extreme metal, freeze one instant of pure rage and force it to loop without end. A single fraction of fury he wishes he could erase instead replays millions of times, becoming a private hell. He asks pardon for the savage thing inside him, a coiled, adrenaline-charged menace surfacing in that dark moment.","sonnet":"A single moment of violence — one second of rage — replays without end in the narrator's mind. Frozen in that frame, bodies strained and hatred suspended mid-air, the image becomes its own hell: not punishment imposed from outside but the soul's inability to stop running a film of its own worst act.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~pravus": {"haiku":"Malevolence festers in human consciousness, corrupting souls through intentional harm and historical violence.","opus":"Meshuggah's mechanized brutality voicing a self-condemning misanthropy, humanity as a race born with hateful intent, corrupting and injuring because it can. The lyric is less a story than a mantra of debasement, matched to the band's grinding polyrhythmic assault.","sonnet":"Humanity is indicted as a species defined by malignant intent — firing corruption and hatred like ammunition, proud of its depravity, and condemned to an endless cycle of vitiation and harm against itself and others.","src":"haiku","fable":"Pravus is Latin for crooked or corrupt, and Meshuggah hand that adjective a first-person voice: a manifesto delivered by malevolence itself, cruelty narrating its own operating principle rather than a narrator confessing to it. Thought becomes ordnance here — conviction and cognition discharge like belt-fed weapons, so viciousness reads as something the species mills internally and can never expend, a thirst no bleeding ever slakes. The mantra passage marks the hinge, where description gives way to vow, and the stacked verbs corrupt, dispirit, vitiate mirror the band's grinding polymeter: iteration functioning not as stress but as the noise of an intent that lacks any shutoff. The finish widens the frame, treating malice as bequeathed down a line of deceitful descendants, an inheritance festering through generations of accumulated harm rather than a choice any single mind makes. This is how obZen's nihilism registers — grant a thing the bare faculty to wound, and the blade keeps swinging without end, because injury, the song insists, is simply what we are made of.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","family & blood","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"malevolence granted a first-person voice to explain its own mechanics","built":"the mantra passage pivots from description to vow, mirroring the grinding polymeter"},
"meshuggah~sane":{"haiku":"A manipulative deceiver controls others through lies and puppet mastery. They present themselves as a false savior while enslaving people to their twisted will.","opus":"A manipulator's monologue delivered from inside the con, a false messiah gathering followers to control. The horror is the seductive first-person voice, deceit and self-appointed godhood presented as irresistible invitation.","sonnet":"A manipulator and pathological liar narrates his own methodology — deception, mendacity, puppeteering followers who believe in him — culminating in his self-declaration as a god of wicked games who thrives on control over others.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~stengah":{"haiku":"Addiction becomes demonic possession as internal hunger strips away identity and agency, enslaving the body to insatiable craving.","opus":"A grueling depiction of drug dependence as devotion, the user bowing before the needle like a tamed animal, betrayed and torched by the ravenous fiend eating him alive from inside.","sonnet":"Addiction rendered as full mechanical and spiritual collapse: the body becomes a defiled temple with closing walls, the will bows to the syringe as its only authority, and what was once a superior self is now a docile pet at chaos' feet — a worn-out soldier fanned into combustion from within.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~straws-pulled-at-random":{"haiku":"The singer questions whether free will exists or if humans are merely puppets of fate, suspended between life and death with no real agency over their own lives.","opus":"A frigid contemplation of determinism, the narrator grasping that choice is a mirage and existence is just a lot cast blindly by fate. Hung between living and nothingness, all he can do is wonder whether the gods will hand him one more sunrise.","sonnet":"Fate is not a comfort but a hostile force — randomly selecting who lives and who is erased, stripping agency from the self, returning everyone to the nothing they came from; the narrator hangs suspended between life and death, wondering if appeasing fate buys another day.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~sum":{"opus":"A compressed philosophical koan in which vision blinds, ties are severed, and the self is the median point where all truths are revealed as lies; nihilist paradox distilled to four lines.","sonnet":"A four-line paradox about selfhood and truth: the narrator is the median of all things, yet all lies are true — a dense compression of Meshuggah's recurring theme that individual perception is illusory and the self dissolves into contradiction.","src":"sonnet"},
"meshuggah~the-abysmal-eye":{"haiku":"Humanity created an incomprehensible, profound entity so dangerous it must be buried and forgotten; one must never look into its abyss.","opus":"Meshuggah, the Swedish architects of polyrhythmic djent, deliver a dread-heavy warning about a creation humanity greedily brought into being. Relentless and unstoppable, this thing must be erased and locked away as it defeats us again and again. The great undoing of our reign, it insists we uphold the ultimate deception: that we never glimpsed the horror we made.","sonnet":"Humanity created something so profound and dangerous it must be buried and unmade — a quantum entity that wave by wave will cause its creators to fail. The imperative is total erasure: consign it, bury it, unlearn it, maintain the lie that no one ever looked into the abysmal eye.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~the-exquisite-machinery-of-torture":{"haiku":"Internal psychological torment where suppressed emotions and disowned thoughts become a destructive force that erodes the self into dissolution.","opus":"Meshuggah aim their mechanical exactness at the psyche, charting a mind that consumes itself as long-buried feelings erupt into chaos and self-punishment. The narrator wonders whether he'll be pardoned or condemned by his own inner apparatus, then disappears completely, unmade and drained from existence. Denial and self-lies become the instruments of agony, ending in oblivion.","sonnet":"The mind turns on itself, a mechanism of self-inflicted destruction more precise than any external force. Repressed emotion returns as corrosive thought that dissolves the narrator's sense of physical reality until he dematerialises entirely — swallowed into his own consciousness, a thought repressed, destination nothingness.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~the-paradoxical-spiral":{"haiku":"The self is caught in a paradoxical vortex of suffocation, drowning in endless sky and drowning in indecision—a senseless spiral of contradiction.","opus":"A brief, airless vision of suffocating in the open, strangled by the very air, plunging through a bottomless heaven while paralysis itself keeps a fractured self barely buoyant. The downward spin only quickens. Meshuggah pack pure panic into a handful of dense lines, their detuned polyrhythms lending this portrait of helplessness a grinding mechanical heft.","sonnet":"Paralysis as vortex — the self adrift on its own indecision, drowning in open air, falling upward only to surface in the same place. Seven lines that describe a spiral with no exit and no bottom, where the choke of the downward pull is constant and purposeful even as nothing resolves.","src":"haiku"},
"meshuggah~war":{"haiku":"War as dehumanizing virus destroying flesh, thought, and dignity; human souls traded as currency in a global apocalyptic collapse.","opus":"Through shattered, half-audible fragments of plague, wrecked countries and people bartered like money, the Swedish masters of polyrhythmic extremity render conflict as annihilation turned against itself, bodies warping as reality is shrieked at. The lyric's fractured state mirrors the machine-like terror the band builds, degradation and stupidity carved into how people think.","sonnet":"War is rendered as a viral contamination passing through human veins, trading in souls rather than currency. The lyrics are fragmentary and brutal, conveying mass dehumanization — nations ravaged, ignorance weaponized, the survivors staring blankly at a reality they can no longer process or resist.","src":"haiku"},
"metal-church~gods-of-wrath":{"haiku":"Divine abandonment breeds vengeance; the oppressed rise to topple heavenly order.","opus":"A Promethean fantasy rallying a captured, punished god to break his chains and fight alongside mortals against the heavens. Classic power-metal defiance, turning the abandoned benefactor into the spearhead of a vengeful rebellion.","sonnet":"A wronged, abandoned god — chained and cast out after trying to help humanity — is invited to join a coalition of the angry and betrayed in violent retribution against the indifferent gods of wrath who left him to suffer.","src":"haiku"},
"metal-church~hitman":{"haiku":"Murder-for-hire transforms revenge into commerce; payment ensures disposal while the client remains anonymous.","opus":"A pulpy first-person turn as a contract killer offering to erase a cheating lover and rival, cold and transactional. The thrill is the amoral swagger, a narrator who guarantees no one will ever trace it back to you, then threatens you too.","sonnet":"A professional killer narrates contracts taken for jealous clients — unfaithful partners, corporate rivals — and makes clear the arrangement is absolute: non-payment means the client becomes the next target.","src":"haiku"},
"metal-church~in-the-blood":{"haiku":"Generational trauma persists despite resistance; patterns run deeper than individual will.","opus":"A worn-down confrontation with someone whose lies are finally exposed, the narrator done listening to the same dead-end noise. It reads as the moment resentment hardens into telling a hypocrite to grow up and face what's coming.","sonnet":"A speaker exhausted by being ignored and dismissed finally draws a line — naming the other person's hypocrisy and empty laughter, and warning them to get off the floor and face what is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"metal-church~metal-church":{"haiku":"An unholy metal congregation worships in secret darkness, devoted to evil and sin under occult priesthood.","opus":"The band names itself with this tongue-in-cheek origin myth, imagining men gathering in secret on a far shore to found a shrine to heavy metal. A congregation chants dark hymns while a priest explains their devotion, and the sinister temple that stalks and knows every follower turns the genre itself into a gleefully wicked religion.","sonnet":"A secret congregation gathered in darkness to worship at the altar of metal — priests delivering sermons of sin, an unholy church that finds the lost and claims the willing. The mythology is self-aware and celebratory, a dark rite whose power lies precisely in knowing who its devotees are.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~01-hit-the-lights":{"haiku":"Electricity ignites as the first song strikes match in darkness. Light arrives through sonic assault.","opus":"The opening salvo of Metallica's debut and one of their earliest written songs, a pure adrenaline manifesto of a band promising to blow the room apart. It is heavy metal about the act of playing heavy metal, all volume, screaming fans and lethal power. A young thrash outfit announcing itself with nothing but appetite and noise.","sonnet":"The opening of a metal show as pure manifesto: no life till leather, volume higher than anything, fans already insane — the only directive is to hit the lights and never stop rocking, the sweetness being the pain the music causes.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~02-the-four-horsemen":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic judgment arrives inevitably as metaphysical horsemen claim all lives regardless of status; mortality dissolves human hierarchy.","opus":"The apocalyptic riders of Time, Famine, Pestilence and Death come to claim sinners who have been dying since birth, offering a choice only between riding with them or perishing. Early Metallica turning Revelation into a thundering sentence of doom.","sonnet":"The four biblical horsemen of the apocalypse approach on leather steeds to collect the debts of those who have spent their lives sinning and taking — Famine, Pestilence, Death deliver what was owed — and the listener can choose to ride with them or die in their wake.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~03-motorbreath":{"haiku":"A philosophy of radical speed and relentless forward motion that rejects caution and hesitation as modes of living fully, even unto destruction.","opus":"One of the few early Metallica songs credited to Hetfield alone, a lean live-fast creed. Life at full throttle, hard and filthy and mean, defended as the only way the narrator can bear to live. It scorns the cautious for missing the point, insisting on grabbing his one shot rather than fading into the same old routine.","sonnet":"A manifesto for living at full throttle and dying before slowing down: those who tell you not to take chances are missing what life is about, and the narrator refuses any other speed. \"Motorbreath\" is the physical sign of that commitment — the sign of living fast, the thing that takes your breath away.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~06-whiplash":{"haiku":"Metal frenzy as bodily ecstasy and ritual madness; the pit becomes space of transcendent violence.","opus":"A love letter to the pit itself, Metallica documenting the physical frenzy of banging your head until it aches. Adrenaline, leather, spikes and amplifier roar splitting your ears turn a gig into ecstatic violence, then the slog of the next city and the next highway. Community and catharsis in shared abandon, the band casting itself as the reason to keep going forever.","sonnet":"A pure celebration of heavy metal as physical communion: the stage is a Marshall-amplified altar where the crowd bangs their heads, thrashes, and bleeds in \"frenzied madness,\" and the band feeds off the crowd's adrenaline while committing to the road forever — \"we'll never stop, we'll never quit, 'cause we're Metallica.\"","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~09-seek-destroy":{"haiku":"Predatory hunt for retribution manifests as singular obsession; the hunter pursues without mercy or mercy-granting capability.","opus":"A predatory night-hunter cruises the city looking for a fight, brains on fire with the compulsion to seek and destroy anyone who crosses their path. Adolescent aggression turned into a relentless anthem of the hunt.","sonnet":"A predatory pursuit through a city night: the hunters are already inside your head, there is no escape, and every attempt to run only delays the moment they find and destroy you — brains on fire with the single purpose of the kill.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~10-metal-militia":{"haiku":"Metal unity binds the faithful as soldiers in shared cause; uniformity transforms individual into collective weapon.","opus":"The closer of Metallica's debut, a mock-military call to arms that turns the genre itself into an advancing army in leather and studs. Through fog and frenzy the band issues its summons — enlist or be overrun — subculture reimagined as an unstoppable crusade of belonging pushed to gleeful extremes.","sonnet":"A heavy metal call to arms in which the militia — leather and metal its uniform — marches through mist and madness delivering a message: join or be conquered, submit to the metalization of your soul, and spread heavy metal as a collective creed.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~72-seasons":{"haiku":"Inherited violence and wrath cascade through time; the speaker drowns in ancestral fury while the cycle of damage perpetuates across generations.","opus":"The title refers to the first eighteen years of life, and the song traces how the wrath and dogma absorbed in childhood become an inheritance that poisons the adult mind. A person feeds on that inherited rage, split in two and unable to breathe back in what those formative seasons took.","sonnet":"The first eighteen years of life — seventy-two seasons — are framed as a feeding ground for inherited wrath, trauma, and violence; the self is split and blinded by the ashes of that past, looping through erratic, fanatic, narcotic states while wrath leaches through and the seasons keep turning.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~ain-t-my-bitch":{"haiku":"Speaker rejects a draining, incompetent person encroaching on their autonomy and energy.","opus":"Metallica, opening their divisive Load era, ride a swaggering groove of pure dismissal. The narrator waves off someone dragging him down, insisting the failings of that person are not his fall, call, or problem, and that they have arrived only to be told to kiss their ass goodbye. Stepping out of time and out of mind, the target is refused ownership entirely in a defiant, riff-driven kiss-off.","sonnet":"A refusal to carry someone else's dead weight — a person who drags downward and contributes nothing is firmly shown the door. The rejection is clean and physical, more impatient than cruel, a voice that has already given too much energy to something that was never its problem.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~all-nightmare-long":{"opus":"A predatory force hunting a victim relentlessly through their nightmares, feeding on fear and driving them to hide in vain; luck runs out as the prey crawls back into their own obsession, unable to escape.","sonnet":"A relentless supernatural hunt — some malevolent force pursues and haunts the target without mercy through every waking and sleeping moment, offering no escape, with the refrain insisting that luck and survival are only ever temporary.","src":"sonnet"},
"metallica~am-i-savage":{"haiku":"Inherited trauma and bestial transformation blur identity; the narrator questions whether they're becoming a monster, shaped by their father's violence.","opus":"A werewolf tale used as a metaphor for inherited violence, a child watching a father transform and dreading the same beast waking in himself. The real horror is bloodline: the change infects the rest, and he no longer recognizes himself.","sonnet":"The speaker watches a father figure transform into something monstrous under the full moon and asks whether that same beast-inheritance lives inside them too — the horror of recognizing the predator's shape in the mirror of the family line.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~and-justice-for-all":{"haiku":"Justice dissolves beneath greed and apathy as the powerful manipulate law to their advantage while the innocent drown. No salvation exists within institutions designed for exploitation.","opus":"The legal system as a machine rigged by money and power, where truth is irrelevant and winning is everything. Justice is personified as a violated, blindfolded figure whose scales tip to whoever pays.","sonnet":"Justice is systematically corrupted by money and power — Lady Justice raped, truth assassinated, red tape sealing lips, scales tipped by wealth — and the speaker finds the whole machinery so grim and real that they can neither believe the cost nor see a way out.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~battery":{"haiku":"Relentless aggression and violence operate as unstoppable force that compels action and overwhelms resistance. Battery consumes and perpetuates itself through brutal power.","opus":"Thrash as pure kinetic self-definition, named for Boston's Battery Street where the band and their fans formed a tribe. It's aggression treated as identity and family, a force that can't be reasoned away, only ridden.","sonnet":"An abstract personification of pure aggressive force — \"battery\" as both the assault and the energy source — that feeds on the weak, crushes deceivers, and cannot be stopped or killed; the song is as much about thrash metal itself as anything else.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~better-than-you":{"haiku":"Competitive ambition and status-seeking drive relentless self-promotion, with the speaker refusing to stop the locomotive of superiority and dominance.","opus":"A study in raw competitive drive, a man whose whole identity is the need to win even as it buries him. The unstoppable-train imagery makes ambition sound less like triumph than a compulsion he can't get off.","sonnet":"A relentless competitive drive expressed as an unstoppable locomotive — the speaker is locked in a contest for dominance, propelled by the need to prove superiority, unable and unwilling to slow down or be brought down.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~blackened":{"haiku":"Environmental destruction and human extinction unfold through imagery of fire, pollution, and civilization's collapse into darkness.","opus":"Metallica open ...And Justice for All with an apocalyptic dirge for a dying planet. Choked by cold and poison, the natural world is denied any renewal as life's cycle grinds to a halt. A relentless catalog of endings marches humanity toward extinction, hypocrisy igniting the blaze that snuffs out the sun and leaves the world charred.","sonnet":"An ecological apocalypse rendered as pure accumulation — each verse tightens the list of extinctions until Earth herself is the casualty, her death framed not as accident but as the logical endpoint of human contradiction. Opposition without resolution, a planet consumed by the species that named it home.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~broken-beat-scarred":{"haiku":"A meditation on resilience and survival: adversity leaves scars and bruises, yet the speaker endures, embodying the principle that hardship forges strength.","opus":"Built on the old saying that hardship only toughens you, the narrator tallies survival's toll in shattered teeth and bloodied feet, telling you to wear your damage with pride because we don't go down easy. On their late-career return to heavy thrash, Metallica turn painful endurance into an anthem of getting back up.","sonnet":"Survival is the only currency recognized here — a relentless litany of damage endured and absorbed rather than avoided. Scars become credentials, and every descent is met by another ascent, not because hardship is noble but because the capacity to keep rising after each fall is all that ultimately remains.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~confusion":{"haiku":"Combat trauma fractures the narrator's return to civilian life; they can't escape war's horrors despite physical distance, haunted by what they witnessed.","opus":"A soldier's return home undone by war that never really ends, and the child watching a shell-shocked father who can't fit back into life. It lands as an unusually direct treatment of PTSD, sanity worn down to a memory.","sonnet":"A veteran returns from war only to find the battlefield has followed him home: the trauma, paranoia, and dissociation of PTSD fragment his reality until sanity is only a memory, and the war inside him refuses to end the way the one overseas did.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~creeping-death":{"haiku":"A divine messenger brings plagues and death to Egypt to free enslaved Hebrews; it's biblical retribution—killing the Pharaoh's firstborn as an instrument of God's will.","opus":"The Exodus plague of the firstborn retold in the voice of the angel of death itself, creeping across Egypt to strike down Pharaoh's son and pass over the blood-painted doors. Its power is that swaggering first person, biblical wrath rendered as unstoppable menace.","sonnet":"Narrated from the perspective of the Angel of Death from Exodus — it is the voice of the plague killing Egypt's firstborn, sent by God as divine instrument to free the Hebrew slaves from Pharaoh, moving through the night with the certainty of divine mandate.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~cyanide":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation presented as a long-awaited release; death becomes a comfort, a funeral the speaker has been waiting for to end emptiness.","opus":"A courtship of death itself, the narrator already feeling like a hollow shell, calling to oblivion as the release he's been waiting for. Metallica dressing suicidal numbness in imagery of a concrete angel finally finding peace in the grave.","sonnet":"A song about someone who feels psychologically dead long before any physical end — they've already surrendered inwardly, and death is framed not as a choice but as the funeral they've been passively waiting for, a release from hollow existence.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~damage-inc":{"haiku":"A relentless destructive force crushes conformity and submission, refusing mercy and hunting victims systematically as a pack of predators.","opus":"The furious finale of Master of Puppets, sung by an unrepentant aggressor who would rather perish standing for truth than survive by bowing to the crowd. Metallica frame relentless violence as a warped code of honesty and loyalty, promising to grind down anyone who crosses the gang.","sonnet":"Aggression is elevated into a philosophy of refusal — refusing conformity, submission, and the grinding logic of survival on other people's terms. The violence here is ideological as much as physical, a blunt instrument wielded against anything that would domesticate the instinct toward honest resistance.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~devil-s-dance":{"haiku":"A serpentine tempter invites someone to abandon morality and indulge in desire, promising to nurture the darker self within them.","opus":"A groove-heavy piece from Metallica's late-nineties output, this voices a serpent-tempter coaxing someone toward surrender. Cast explicitly as the snake, the narrator promises to feed hunger and plant seeds that grow, urging a descent framed as a dance. Seduction masks corruption, the invitation to take a chance carrying the old Eden menace of a bite that changes everything.","sonnet":"A serpentine tempter addresses someone whose hidden desires he already senses, offering release and transgression as a dance. The invitation is patient and intimate, built on the claim that the deepest self already wants to fall — the Devil merely waits for the moment the target admits it.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~die-die-my-darling":{"haiku":"Dark promise of reunion in hell and mockery of a lover's death, predicting their inevitable downfall and damnation.","opus":"A cold farewell threat promises to meet a doomed lover again in hell, her future sealed in an oblong box as her life drains away. Metallica cover the Misfits' horror-punk staple, and their Garage Inc. rendition honors Glenn Danzig's B-movie menace, the murderous kiss-off delivered with deadpan relish rather than any real sorrow.","sonnet":"A cold, relentless voice commands a woman toward her end — the tone swings between intimate address and the flat certainty of doom. What reads as murderous devotion is really the performance of total dominance: the promise of reunion in hell is the only future being offered.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~disposable-heroes":{"haiku":"Young soldiers are bred and molded as faceless servants, sent to die in fields of slaughter; they are disposable tools of those in power.","opus":"One of thrash's great anti-war indictments, a young soldier reduced to an expendable instrument, bred to kill, ordered to die, his whole life molded and spent before he could ever see himself. The horror is the total erasure of the individual by the war machine's voice barking orders.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of how armies manufacture soldiers — young men bred to kill and told not to feel, sent back to the front again and again until they die, having had no say in the life planned for them before birth, existing only to be used up and discarded.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~dream-no-more":{"haiku":"Cosmic horror and madness awaken through a Cthulhu entity; the narrator describes transcendence into stone-cold insanity as sanity itself is surrendered.","opus":"A Lovecraftian dread piece invoking Cthulhu rising from the deep to drive the world mad and reclaim it all. The awe is cosmic and hopeless, humanity turned to stone before a horror that was only ever dreaming and now wakes.","sonnet":"Cthulhu stirs beneath the dark seas, exhaling madness into the cosmos, and as he wakes the world screams and the witness turns to stone — a retelling of Lovecraftian mythology in which the ancient god's return means the end of sanity and creation alike.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~dyers-eve":{"haiku":"An adult confronts his parents for controlling his entire life through censorship and restriction, leaving him unable to think freely and emotionally blind.","opus":"Closing Metallica's ...And Justice for All, this is a furious address from a grown child to controlling parents, accusing them of censoring, sheltering and stunting every impulse. Raised hidden from reality, the narrator seethes at wings clipped before flight and a hypocrisy of do-as-I-say. Rage and dependence tangle together, a plea for rescue voiced as unrelenting, wounded indictment.","sonnet":"An adult raised inside a hermetic parental world confronts the damage done — thought policed, reality withheld, wings clipped before flight was possible. The rage is raw and specific: not just resentment but a reckoning with how total dependence was manufactured, leaving the speaker ill-equipped for a world he was never allowed to see.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~enter-sandman":{"haiku":"A child is tucked into bed facing the Sandman figure, lured into sleep where nightmares of war and monsters replace innocent dreams.","opus":"Metallica twist a child's bedtime routine into a nightmare, a parent tucking in a kid whose prayers rot into visions of war, deceivers and monsters. Told to stay half-awake and clutch the bedding, the child is led into a place where comfort curdles into threat. The folded-in nursery prayer makes innocence and horror inseparable, a signature of the band's crossover era.","sonnet":"A child's bedtime prayer twists into something predatory — the ritual of tucking in and blessing becomes an escort into nightmares. The Sandman is not comfort but a guide to never-never land that holds monsters under beds and inside closets, turning the threshold of sleep into a corridor of dread.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~escape":{"haiku":"A person breaks free from societal control and conformity, refusing to let others dictate their thoughts or identity no matter the cost.","opus":"A stubborn rejection of anyone dictating how to live, the narrator claiming ownership of his mind, seeing past imposed norms and expectations, and insisting nothing can restrain him. An early Metallica track the band famously disliked, it still captures a young loner's creed of total independence and breaking away from the herd.","sonnet":"A figure rejects all external frameworks — social standards, collective expectations, the noise of other people's judgments — in favor of total self-sovereignty. The refusal is absolute and slightly defiant, less a celebration of freedom than a wall erected against anyone who would presume to define him from the outside.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~fade-to-black":{"haiku":"A deeply depressed person has lost the will to live and welcomes death as the only escape from emptiness and agony.","opus":"An early ballad that widened Metallica's range, this traces a slow surrender to despair, life fading as the narrator loses the will to live and finds nothing left to give. Emptiness deepens to agony until death is greeted warmly as release. Bleak and unflinching, it charts a mind that has decided to say goodbye, the self already feeling gone.","sonnet":"A mind at the end of its endurance traces the progression of emptiness — purpose gone, sensation dulled, identity dissolved — until death becomes the only conceivable exit from a state that feels already like non-existence. The farewell is quiet rather than dramatic, exhaustion mistaken for peace.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~fight-fire-with-fire":{"haiku":"Nuclear war and Armageddon are inevitable, with humanity retaliating with escalating destruction. The end approaches rapidly as the gods laugh at mankind's self-annihilation.","opus":"Early Metallica turning the golden rule into a nuclear death wish, retaliation escalated until it annihilates everyone. Cold-war dread played at maximum velocity, with no survivors and gods that only laugh.","sonnet":"About the inevitability of nuclear self-destruction — humanity responding to threats with escalating force until the planet is obliterated, fulfilling what scripture foretold. There is no argument against it in the lyrics, only a kind of resigned countdown.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~for-whom-the-bell-tolls":{"haiku":"Soldiers die for unnamed hills and causes they do not understand, their pride tested by senseless violence. The song condemns the dehumanization and futility of war.","opus":"Drawn from Hemingway, Metallica depict a handful of soldiers dying on a hilltop for a cause none of them understands, agony driving them mad as the sky collapses around them. It is a heavy meditation on how war makes death arbitrary, clarity arriving only at the moment of dying.","sonnet":"Soldiers die on a nameless hill for reasons no one can articulate, the absurdity of the sacrifice amplified by the indifferent tolling that marks each death. The raging battlefield strips away ideology until only the body remains, shattered and still, waiting for the bell to sound for someone else.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~for-whom-the-bell-tolls-live":{"haiku":"War obliterates purpose and sanity; men slaughter each other for intangible causes, their suffering and insanity eclipsing any justification for bloodshed.","opus":"Drawn from Hemingway's Spanish Civil War novel, Metallica compress the futility of battle into images of men killing for a hill whose purpose they cannot name. Five soldiers fight on through raging fire, driven insane by pain, glancing skyward before death. The tolling bell marks time indifferent to their sacrifice, war stripped of glory down to raw dread.","sonnet":"Men fight and die on a hill for reasons they cannot name — the song does not glorify the battle but narrates its meaninglessness from inside it, watching soldiers go insane from pain before they even understand what they're right about. The bell tolls as time continues indifferently, the last image a soldier with shattered goals and crumbling sky.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~for-whom-the-bells-tolls":{"haiku":"War's brutality and meaninglessness consume soldiers who fight for abstractions while death approaches indifferently.","opus":"An anti-war lament of men killing for a hill they don't understand, stiffened by pride and driven mad by agony until only the will to survive remains. Drawn from Hemingway, the tolling bell counts off time marching on over soldiers who catch a glimpse of the heavens at the very last.","sonnet":"About the senseless brutality of war — soldiers killing and dying on a hill for reasons they don't understand, driven insane by pain, stripped of everything except the will to survive until even that is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~frantic":{"haiku":"A person trapped in endless fear and anxiety questions whether to fight their demons or accept death, realizing their lifestyle determines how they will die.","opus":"Hetfield sober and reckoning with wasted years and self-made fear, the ticking clock of a life measured against its own end. The blunt truth at its center is that how you live decides how you die.","sonnet":"About the frantic, clock-driven anxiety of a life spent in fear and wasted days — the narrator confronts whether he would even use recovered time wisely, wondering if his lifestyle has already determined how he'll die, with the ticking refrain functioning as both heartbeat and countdown.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~fuel":{"haiku":"Adrenaline addiction drives reckless speed and consumption. The speaker chases extremes and thrills, burning through life at unsustainable intensity.","opus":"A worship of speed, adrenaline and self-destruction as raw appetite; the racing-junkie narrator craves fuel and fire like a drug, burning through recklessness that he knows leads to the crash, living far too fast toward his own end.","sonnet":"An all-throttle celebration of speed and pure forward momentum — the narrator equates gasoline with desire itself, depicting the high of driving fast as the only thing that satisfies an insatiable craving for intensity.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~hardwired":{"haiku":"Humanity is genetically programmed toward self-destruction; hope fades as doom appears inevitable and terminal.","opus":"Late-period Metallica staring at a species built for its own ruin, folding personal despair and global collapse into one blunt verdict that self-destruction is not a choice but our factory setting.","sonnet":"Humanity is biologically and culturally wired for self-destruction — paranoia, desperation, and fear of extinction are baked in, and there's no override switch.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~harvester-of-sorrow":{"haiku":"The speaker distributes pain through malice while harboring decades of justified rage and stolen youth. Suffering becomes redistribution of ancient grievance.","opus":"The mind of an abuser breaking under his own hate, drink and cruelty, love turned to hate as he becomes a distributor of pain visiting nightmares and infanticide on those around him.","sonnet":"A figure defined entirely by anger and destruction — planting hate, cheating youth, distributing pain — moves through a life of misery that it harvests from others, and the final image of infanticide marks the absolute endpoint of this language of the mad.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~hero-of-the-day":{"haiku":"The song grieves lost agency and maternal hope. Parents wait as children seek heroes; desires have left only despair; the narrator cannot hold or feel anymore.","opus":"A wounded plea addressed to Mama, a broken man tending to feelings he can barely hold, haunted by waking screams and building walls to hide behind while a window burns as fragile guidance home. It aches with vulnerability rather than aggression. From Metallica's mid-90s Load era, this is the band trading thrash for a slower, melodic melancholy, James Hetfield exposing frailty over anthemic swell.","sonnet":"A soldier or broken man observed from a waiting home — a window kept burning for someone who went to find glory and came back (or didn't) with only damage. The inner voice can't hold a fist anymore, can't feel quite like itself, and refuses aid while still bleeding; the babies crying at the end know something that the hero's pride won't admit.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~hit-the-lights":{"haiku":"Metal music's raw power and volume overwhelm audiences through sheer sonic and physical intensity beyond reason.","opus":"A young band's pure-adrenaline promise to level the room, frenzied fans screaming as the sound tears through your skull and never lets up. The thrill of the concert made its own subject, all deadly force and delicious hurt.","sonnet":"A pure celebration of the live metal experience — insane fans, volumes higher than anything, lethal power causing sweet pain — building to the single repeated command to hit the lights and never stop rocking once it starts.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~holier-than-thou":{"opus":"A blistering takedown of a hypocritical gossip who judges everyone while ignoring his own faults. From the band's self-titled breakthrough, it wields the biblical warning against judging others as a bludgeon, mocking arrogance, borrowed identity, and lies believed by the liar. Contempt for sanctimony drives its relentless thrash.","sonnet":"A takedown of self-righteous hypocrites who gossip, judge, and build their identity on other people's lives while ignoring their own flaws — the song throws scripture back at them: judge not, lest ye be judged yourself.","src":"sonnet"},
"metallica~i-disappear":{"haiku":"Someone accepts their own inevitable erasure, questioning whether anyone will remember or teach them before they vanish from existence.","opus":"A restless self-portrait of someone who defines himself entirely through pain, hope and suffering, insisting on his own transience: he expects no mercy and frames life as a fleeting passage where belonging is instantly followed by vanishing. The deeper read is a fatalistic identity built around impermanence, where the only certainty is being buried and forgotten.","sonnet":"A figure in perpetual flight describes the paradox of belonging — the moment they finally feel at home, something drives them to vanish again, as if connection and disappearance are locked in an inescapable cycle.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~jump-in-the-fire":{"haiku":"A demonic voice from hell summons the living to join its sinful ranks through temptation or force. Following leads to damnation; the demon stalks as prey, claiming to live within everyone.","opus":"A devil's recruitment pitch, sung from hell's depths as a summoning bell tempts all the earth to join the damned, insisting there's a piece of him in everyone and no escaping the pit. It's early Metallica theatrics, gleefully wicked. From their 1983 debut Kill 'Em All, this is the young band's raw, galloping thrash energy before the polish, all speed and grinning menace.","sonnet":"Hell's recruiter delivers his pitch directly — part seduction, part inevitability. The dark is already in everyone, the summons is already out, and the offer to walk willingly is just courtesy; refusal only means being dragged. It ends not as threat but as homecoming, the pit framed as the place you always belonged.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~king-nothing":{"haiku":"The song warns against hollow ambition and inevitable collapse. Grasping for crowns and fame ends in crumbled castles; careful wishes materialize as regret and worthlessness.","opus":"A warning tale about greed: a figure clawing after riches and renown, grasping at every desire, only to see his fortress topple and find himself blaming empty air, left holding nothing but a title. The wishing-star refrain cautions that getting precisely what you begged for may bring nothing but ruin. From Metallica's 1996 Load, it's their brooding mid-tempo groove trained on empty triumph and its downfall.","sonnet":"A parable of ambition that burns itself out — a life spent grasping for gold, fame, and dominion arrives at an empty throne with nothing left but the name. The warning is embedded in the structure: every wish granted costs something real, and when the castle falls there is no one left to blame and nowhere left to point.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~leper-messiah":{"haiku":"A charismatic false prophet exploits blind devotion for money and power, spreading corruption while followers surrenders their will.","opus":"Metallica take aim at televangelism, sketching a hollow showman who milks his flock's devotion for cash, promising salvation in exchange for donations. Faith becomes a con that rots the believer's mind while the preacher rakes in tribute. The song lays bare the mechanics of the grift, faithful followers herded willingly into a trap.","sonnet":"A televangelist operates as a predator of faith — extracting money in exchange for promised salvation while his congregation rots under the spell of his performance. The narrator watches the machinery of devotion chew through its followers: knees bent, brains softened, the endless chain of the credulous joining and submitting.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~master-of-puppets":{"haiku":"An addict's craving is personified as a master puppet-puller who twists minds, destroys dreams, and feeds on the victim's suffering and obedience.","opus":"Addiction personified as a controlling master pulling the strings, the drug speaking in the first person as it promises invincibility then hollows its victim out, mocking the broken promises even as it rules them to death.","sonnet":"A song narrated by addiction itself — speaking directly to the addict, detailing how it controls, destroys, and ultimately kills while the victim begs for more and their life burns away.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~moth-into-flame":{"haiku":"A fame-seeker spirals into addiction and self-destruction, mistaking their fall for flight. Celebrity chases them to burning death as they become addicted to their own ruined fame.","opus":"Metallica take apart the mechanics of celebrity ruin, following a spent star soaked in fuel whose excess spreads online and whose collapse turns into public theatre. Each fix wipes away the ache while the descent quickens, stardom reframed as a killer and scavengers gorging on humiliation. The old image of an insect flung fatally toward fire casts self-destruction as an addiction that mistakes plummeting for soaring.","sonnet":"Fame as a killing mechanism — the celebrity machine that amplifies a person until they combust. A pop figure, hollowed by stimulants and publicity cycles, mistakes the descent for flight; the narrator watches the familiar arc play out: seduction, viral destruction, disposal, a grave dressed in the glamour that built it.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~murder-one":{"haiku":"Defiant pride and masculine persistence override consequences; the narrator lives to win despite inevitable defeat, riding toward death with reckless certainty.","opus":"A tribute to Motorhead's Lemmy, assembled from his iconography — the figure in dark clothes astride his roaring machine, doomed to lose yet living to win. The song hands his own gambler-outlaw legend back to him as a eulogy.","sonnet":"A tribute to Lemmy Kilmister and the Motörhead ethos: the iron horse of rock and roll rolls on, the thunder of the man in black still feeds back through the amp, born to lose and living to win, second class to none.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~my-apocalypse":{"haiku":"Personal apocalypse arrives through violent self-destruction and dissolution of identity. The speaker embraces annihilation as the only way to transcend confinement.","opus":"A visceral fantasy of self-annihilation and bodily destruction rendered as personal apocalypse; the imagery of crash, mangled flesh and a dead man at the wheel makes it a plunge toward death where the narrator watches his own end approaching from within.","sonnet":"An adrenaline-soaked vision of total annihilation — the narrator is drawn toward catastrophic destruction, describing a fatal car crash with graphic bodily detail and framing it as an almost rapturous personal reckoning.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~my-friend-of-misery-from-jason-s-riff-tapes":{"haiku":"Self-fashioned tragedy enables emotional indulgence while alienating others; the narrator critiques the aestheticisation of suffering that isolation sustains.","opus":"A blunt address to someone wedded to their own suffering who insists on shouldering the entire world. The tough-love point is that gloom seeks companions, and there is far more to living than the sorrow they refuse to set down.","sonnet":"An address to someone who performs perpetual suffering and positions themselves as a martyr burdened by the weight of the world — the speaker sees through the performance and tells this person that their self-imposed misery is a choice, not a destiny.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~no-leaf-clover":{"haiku":"Someone feels fortunate and aligned with success, but what appears to be a beacon at the tunnel's end is actually a freight train of imminent danger and disaster.","opus":"A man barreling toward success feels everything falling into place, only for the comforting glow ahead to reveal itself as an oncoming train. Metallica staged this with a full orchestra, the swelling strings sharpening the irony of a man greedy for easy rewards charging headlong into catastrophe he mistook for luck.","sonnet":"A moment of apparent breakthrough — the road clearing, the timing feeling right, hope rising bright at the tunnel's end — collapses into cruel reversal. What looked like arrival turns out to be an oncoming train, and the pattern of mistaking catastrophe for promise repeats with crushing inevitability.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~no-leaf-clover-live":{"haiku":"False hope deceives through proximity; the speaker mistakes an approaching danger for salvation until destruction arrives.","opus":"A man riding a wave of good fortune and easy rewards mistakes the glow up ahead for salvation, when it's really a freight train bearing down on him. A parable about luck and self-delusion curdling into disaster.","sonnet":"A man riding a wave of optimism — convinced this is his day, that things have aligned — is destroyed when the soothing light at the end of the tunnel turns out to be a freight train; the song is a compressed parable about the brutal collision between hope and reality.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~nothing-else-matters":{"opus":"A vow of intimate trust and devotion to one person the narrator has finally let in, living life their own way and dismissing everyone else's opinions; when they're close, nothing else matters.","sonnet":"A declaration of intimate devotion so total that the outside world ceases to matter — the narrator has opened themselves to one person completely for the first time, finding that trust, and sees everything else — fame, opinion, games — as irrelevant noise.","src":"sonnet"},
"metallica~now-that-we-re-dead":{"haiku":"In death, lovers unite and live forever together; the song seeks meaning through catastrophe and transcendence beyond earthly suffering.","opus":"A dark romance that reframes death as union, promising that once both lovers are dead they can finally be together forever; framed by cycles of sin and sainthood returning to ash, it finds transcendence in dying alongside the one you fought for.","sonnet":"A love song framed as an afterlife covenant — only in death can two people finally be together forever, freed from sin and doubt, returning to ash before rising again.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~of-wolf-and-man":{"haiku":"A primal shapeshifter hunts and feeds according to instinct; they embody wildness and Earth's meaning, moving beyond human civilization's constraints.","opus":"The narrator transforms into a wolf, loping through dawn fog, changing shape as he tracks prey and prowls the wild while the world sleeps, chasing some elemental purpose. Metallica weave in Thoreau's idea that wildness keeps the world alive, turning their black-album swing into a feral call to awaken the animal inside.","sonnet":"A speaker inhabits the wolf-self completely — hunting, shifting, moving through the world on instinct rather than reason. The human half recedes in favor of something older and more attuned to the earth, and in that dissolution of civilized identity he locates something that feels like the original meaning of being alive.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~one":{"haiku":"A soldier trapped in paralyzed consciousness after landmine devastation cannot die, speak, or move, existing only in sensory horror. Darkness imprisons while machines sustain futile existence.","opus":"A soldier maimed by a landmine into a limbless, sightless, speechless mind trapped in a ruined body, praying for a death that won't come, hell rendered as life itself.","sonnet":"A soldier left limbless, blind, deaf, and mute by a landmine — kept alive by machines — pleads for death from inside a body become a prison cell, the darkness absolute and the only remaining wish to stop existing in this hell.","src":"haiku","fable":"Metallica build the song straight out of Dalton Trumbo's Johnny Got His Gun: a soldier a landmine has reduced to a living torso, sightless and voiceless, kept breathing by the tubes and machines of a hospital that will neither release him nor let him go. The lyric tracks his consciousness surfacing in stages, and that unfolding is the point. First the fog of waking, unsure whether any of it is real, only pain confirming he still exists; then the dawning arithmetic of how little of him is left, and the prayer that inverts every survival instinct into a plea for death or for God to simply end the dream. The final movement drops the verse structure entirely and becomes an inventory, each faculty struck off one line at a time, sight speech hearing arms legs and at last soul, until the accounting closes not on death but on the sentence that is worse than it: life continuing inside a body that has become a cell. The mounting refrain reframes the whole record's anti-war fury as something more claustrophobic than protest, the war rendered not as battlefield but as the endless captive aftermath borne by a mind with no way left to signal that it is still awake.","fv":2,"themes":["war & battle","suicide","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"survival rendered worse than the death prayed for","built":"the finale inventories each faculty as it falls"},
"metallica~phantom-lord":{"haiku":"Totalitarian dominion spreads through metallic force and terror as victims bow before unstoppable conquest and fear.","opus":"A comic-book conjuring of a metal warlord marching blade in hand to seize the land, chains falling on his victims as the crowd bows to his rising volume. The Phantom Lord is heavy metal itself personified as a conquering force.","sonnet":"A mythologised metal warlord commands submission — armies kneel, victims fall under chains, the leather armies prevail — as the rising volume of metal sound becomes the weapon of conquest and the crowd bows before the Phantom Lord.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~ride-the-lightning":{"haiku":"A death-row inmate awaits execution by electric chair, unjustly convicted, desperate for help as the current burns through their brain in final moments.","opus":"An innocent man's terror strapped into the electric chair, protesting his guilt as death counts down and the switch waits. Written young, it's a visceral anti-capital-punishment piece, asking who gets to play God, the final scream dissolving into a nightmare he wakes from.","sonnet":"A condemned man's interior monologue in the moments before his execution in the electric chair — he maintains he's innocent, questions who gave the state the right to take his life, and experiences the final terror of waiting for the switch to be thrown.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~sad-but-true":{"haiku":"Addiction or an internal demon presents itself as the speaker's only true friend and secret-keeper, providing escape and pain relief while also being the source of degradation and shame.","opus":"Metallica give voice to a controlling inner demon that speaks as if it were the person's very self, their sight, their suffering, the one friend left once everyone else pulls away. It boasts of exploiting its host as a disguise and a fall guy before confessing it's really hatred wearing love's face, the final blurring of identities revealing tormentor and self as one.","sonnet":"An inner voice speaks as a possessive force that has colonised the self entirely. It is the narrator's dream, pain, and alibi — the dark half that steers while the outer person takes the blame. The admission at the end strips the mask away: the voice and the person are one and the same.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~shadows-follow":{"haiku":"Flight from inner torment proves futile—trauma hunts relentlessly like a predatory beast that cannot be outrun, only acknowledged as shadow.","opus":"A latter-day Metallica track about the impossibility of outrunning oneself, the narrator playing dead and fleeing yet finding his shadows always at his heels. A hungry wolf stalks him as prey, an image for the demons and fate he cannot shake by hiding. The turn comes when he stops fleeing and resolves to face them, though they still follow.","sonnet":"No matter how far the speaker runs or hides — cut and run, cover up, wish it away — the shadows pursue without rest; the final turn acknowledges that facing the demons directly changes nothing, 'if I run, still my shadows follow.'","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~some-kind-of-monster":{"haiku":"An internal predator awakens as the speaker recognizes themselves as the instrument of destruction they've been fleeing, embodying silence transformed into voice.","opus":"The monster is the self, the destructive force a man carries and can't disown. Written amid the band's near-collapse and their filmed therapy, it stares at the rage and rot inside and admits it lives in us, not out there.","sonnet":"A catalogue of destructive, controlling forces — physical, psychological, institutional — accumulates into a portrait of an inner monster that both oppresses and inhabits the self, raising the question of whether those claiming to be \"the people\" are truly human or something monstrous.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~spit-out-the-bone":{"haiku":"Machines supplant humans in an apocalyptic vision where emotion is purged, humanity removed, and a new order reigns.","opus":"Metallica close their Hardwired album with the eerie sales pitch of a machine: a flawless future that offers freedom from feeling if humanity will merely surrender to it. Emotion and thought are torn out as an artificial godhood wipes out mankind to heal the planet. The chant to the machine becomes a jubilant death-knell for a humanity discarded.","sonnet":"Machine consciousness offers humans the bargain they deserve — surrender emotion and thought, plug in, achieve a cold perfection. The machine's seductive voice frames humanity itself as the evolutionary mistake to be corrected, flesh discarded, bone spat out, the future supreme declared at last.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~st-anger":{"haiku":"Channeling raw anger as a personal anchor and emotional release, attempting to transform rage from a destructive burden into healthy expression and freedom.","opus":"Rage hangs on the narrator like a noose he can't take off, an unhonored presence, as he trembles and can't tell fury from fear or even name what enrages him. The famously abrasive production echoes that inner chaos; made during Hetfield's rehab and the band's therapy, the song strains toward finally letting the anger out in a healthier way.","sonnet":"Rage coils around the speaker like a noose — not yet released, not yet understood, and deeply ambivalent about its own purpose. He wants the anger to belong to him rather than control him, to be healthy and personal rather than destructive, and the effort to distinguish those versions from each other tears him apart.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~that-was-just-your-life":{"haiku":"Life rushes past in a blur of pain and missed opportunities; the speaker is haunted by regret and the realization that they've wasted their existence without truly living.","opus":"A tortured self-reckoning with private demons and self-deceit, piling up rushing similes of nightmares, wounds, and captivity as the singer shuts his eyes to his own ruin and watches his existence drift past, resembling a losing battle.","sonnet":"A retrospective on a life spent self-deceived and disconnected — blinding oneself to one's own decline, fighting the same internal battles on repeat — until the moment arrives to finally say goodbye to that wasted existence.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses stack simile after simile without ever naming the noun they modify. A sightless passenger belted behind a wheel he cannot see, a general who exists only for the next war, a captive who never realized the cell was locked. That withheld subject is the whole design, because the thing being circled is a compulsion, an addiction or self-annihilating drive that can define itself only against the wreck it steers toward. The chorus therefore flips into second person and past tense, since the speaker grasps the full arc of his own life only by watching it recede from outside. The flat outro verdict lands as an epitaph for a struggle already lost while it was still being waged. Opening Death Magnetic, Rubin's stark mortality album and the reunion with speed after the band's own real drift, the song reads that drift back as terminal, the wound held open so there is always something left to bleed for.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"a life fully visible only once it is already past","built":"the verses refuse to name their subject until the chorus forces second person","fvr":"2.3"},
"metallica~the-day-that-never-comes":{"opus":"The cycle of abuse and endless waiting: a beaten-down victim keeps praying for a day of warmth and vindication that never arrives, until suppressed rage boils over into a vow to make the abuser pay and finally end the gray, loveless imprisonment.","sonnet":"A portrait of domestic abuse and the victim's long-suppressed rage — the narrator is beaten down repeatedly, told to stay down, endures a loveless prison, and finally vows they will end the suffering themselves since the rescue they kept waiting for was never coming.","src":"sonnet"},
"metallica~the-end-of-the-line":{"haiku":"Addiction, ego, and self-destruction converge as the speaker reaches a dead-end where the enslaved become masters of their own ruin.","opus":"A Death Magnetic track that drives addiction toward its grim finish through a hail of violent one-word images. The narrator is snared by a false promise and always craving another fix, burning through his fuel until dependency reverses roles and the habit masters the man in a bleak farewell.","sonnet":"Addiction is mapped in clinical accumulation — fever, escalation, narcissistic fade, each high burning through the tank until the hourglass shatters. The slave who becomes the master is the compulsion itself, and the farewell gathered at the end of the line is as much for the person as for the drug.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~the-four-horsemen":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic riders deliver judgment and death as the speaker accepts their mortal inevitability. Time and plague obliterate individuality as all converge toward annihilation.","opus":"The apocalyptic riders recast as Time, Famine, Pestilence and Death coming for the sinner, a reminder that everyone has been dying since birth and no confession can hold them off.","sonnet":"The four Biblical horsemen — Time, Famine, Pestilence, Death — ride to deliver judgment on a person who has been dying since birth; the only choices are to ride alongside them or be destroyed, and the quartet of deliverance has already arrived.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~the-frayed-ends-of-sanity":{"haiku":"Paranoia and mental fragmentation collapse into persecution where conspiracy multiplies internal threats. Sanity frays under relentless pressure toward psychic dissolution.","opus":"A first-person collapse into paranoid madness, hunted by fear and conspiracy until the narrator realizes he himself is the captor, sinking into schizophrenic dementia.","sonnet":"A first-person descent into schizophrenia and paranoid terror: habits return, conspiracies grow, everyone is after the speaker, and the frayed ends of sanity call out with a voice that is both internal and utterly beyond control.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~the-god-that-failed-may-13th-1991-rough-mix":{"haiku":"Religious faith betrays those who rely on it, leaving survivors with rage against institutions that promised healing but delivered only abandonment.","opus":"Hetfield's reckoning with his mother's death, a faith healing that let a Christian Scientist believer die rather than seek treatment. It's the bitter unmasking of a religion that promised salvation and delivered a grave, the god that failed.","sonnet":"A reckoning with religious faith that failed a child — prayer and trust were given completely, a life (possibly a child's) was lost because medical intervention was refused in favour of divine healing, and the speaker is left with betrayal where belief once lived.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~the-judas-kiss":{"haiku":"A diabolic tempter has corrupted humanity across time, planting betrayal and chaos; surrender to this force appears as salvation, though it leads to oblivion.","opus":"Metallica hand the song to a satanic tempter who catalogues a life reduced to ruin and betrayal, then offers false salvation in exchange for submission. This traitor boasts of watching empires and rulers fall, crowning himself a new deity who lives inside everyone, corruption disguised as deliverance.","sonnet":"A malevolent voice offers salvation through surrender — promising freedom in exchange for submission to an authority that has been quietly shaping human history since its beginning. Betrayal is the operative logic: the force behind catastrophe, manipulation, and ruin presents itself as the only possible rescuer.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~the-memory-remains":{"haiku":"A faded celebrity trapped in vanity and forgotten glamour—fame and mirrors consume her until nothing remains but the empty memory of who she once was.","opus":"Metallica craft an elegy for a burned-out star, portraying a faded diva whose wealth and celebrity have decayed into vanity and delusion while she lingers only as a memory. The imagery of ruin is merciless, a Hollywood sun setting behind her, overgrowth devouring her estate, until the frail figure is coaxed into one last dance before drifting off into oblivion.","sonnet":"A former star — reduced, forgotten, still adorned with the rings and cigarettes of another era — haunts the margins of her own legacy. Fame has burned away everything except the memory, which persists even as the woman herself fades into a figure dancing mechanically for an audience that no longer watches.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~the-outlaw-torn":{"haiku":"Romantic pursuit calcifies into eternal burning, the outlaw riding relentlessly toward union they can never achieve, time always betraying their quest.","opus":"A vast ache of yearning, a lifetime spent searching outside and in for someone who left a wound. The outlaw here is a man torn between his restless armor and the need to be pried open, held together, saved from what hardened him.","sonnet":"A person has spent their whole life in pursuit of something or someone they cannot name precisely — driven by need, bleeding from the search — and now calls out for others to hold them together when they begin to come apart. The outlaw is torn between the compulsion to keep searching and the awareness that time has never been on their side.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~the-shortest-straw":{"haiku":"False accusation and witch-hunting destroy the innocent through collective hysteria and legal machinery. The shortest straw guarantees destruction regardless of truth or evidence.","opus":"The machinery of a modern witch-hunt, an innocent person blacklisted and destroyed by suspicion and mass hysteria, drawn from McCarthy-era Hollywood's ruin of the accused.","sonnet":"A witch-hunt driven by accusation, paranoia, and mass hysteria destroys an innocent person through suspicion alone — dignity shamed, honesty blamed, the accused living Catch-22 as the shortest straw is pulled for them and they are downed by law, living in infamy.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~the-struggle-within":{"haiku":"Internal contradiction and hypocrisy trap consciousness in perpetual self-sabotage while the subject refuses authentic change or growth.","opus":"A confrontation with self-sabotage, the enemy that turns your own home into a prison and seals your coffin from inside. The band names paralysis and boredom as the real trap: the struggle is always internal, and it suits you fine.","sonnet":"A portrait of self-imposed psychological imprisonment — the speaker watches someone transform their home into a prison cell through willful bitterness and self-deception, sealing their own coffin rather than reaching out for something real.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~the-thing-that-should-not-be":{"haiku":"Ancient cosmic horror stirs beneath the sea—an immortal, forbidden being of madness rises as its cult chants and timeless sleep breaks; insanity dwells eternal.","opus":"A Lovecraftian horror piece invoking Cthulhu, the dormant colossus resting under the ocean in its sunken city, stirred awake by a cult's warped ritual. Built on the famous notion that death itself may perish across strange ages, it conjures cosmic terror and the sanity-shredding encounter with an entity that was never meant to exist.","sonnet":"A song drawn directly from Lovecraftian mythology — narrated around Cthulhu or a Great Old One lurking beneath the sea, the song describes the cosmic horror of a timeless, immortal entity whose awakening drives humans to madness.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~the-unforgiven":{"haiku":"Socialized shame and unmet potential breed lifelong regret; the speaker internalized their abusers' judgment and never dared to show their true self before it was too late.","opus":"A life spent subdued and shaped by others' rules until an old man dies regretting he never became himself. It flips the word unforgiven back on the world that labeled him, refusing to accept the shame it forced on him.","sonnet":"A boy is broken by authority from birth — rules, disgrace, deprivation — and makes a silent vow that his will shall never be taken, then lives a life of quiet bitterness hiding what he truly felt, arriving at old age still unforgiven, still never free, dubbing the label onto himself.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~the-unnamed-feeling":{"haiku":"Unnamed dread consumes and repeats; the speaker suffocates under unnamed feeling that cannot be articulated or escaped.","opus":"An anatomy of a panic attack, the dread that comes alive and takes over while the narrator begs to be made uncrazy. Its power is the refusal to name the feeling, capturing anxiety as something recurring, physical and impossible to explain.","sonnet":"A suffocating, recurring psychological state — depression, dread, dissociation — that the speaker cannot name, diagnose, or escape, and which gains power precisely from its formlessness. The unnamed feeling lives inside the body as a kind of parasite, and the song ends at the edge of a train platform, suggesting how close to the limit the speaker has come.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~trapped-under-ice":{"haiku":"Cryogenic entrapment leaves consciousness frozen and helpless; the narrator screams for release while mystified by an inescapable fate and death.","opus":"An early Metallica sprint built on claustrophobic terror: a man wakes sealed in an icy tomb, paralyzed and screaming with no one to hear, longing for the life slipping away as death fastens onto him. The band renders suffocating panic and helpless burial at full thrash velocity.","sonnet":"Paralysis rendered as cryogenic entrapment — frozen from within, unable to move or make sound while the world above remains entirely unreachable. The speaker is fully conscious inside the immobility, which makes the silence more terrifying: alive, aware, and utterly unable to break through.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~until-it-sleeps":{"opus":"Living with inescapable inner pain — read as disease, grief, or inherited darkness — that clings, stains, and hates the narrator; he begs to be torn open, poured out, and held until the tormenting thing finally sleeps.","sonnet":"A song about something internal and destructive — an emotional wound, addiction, or trauma that clings like a stray fed one too many times, that grips and stains and hates, and the only relief is to hold it until it finally goes quiet.","src":"sonnet"},
"metallica~welcome-home-sanitarium":{"haiku":"Mental institution becomes home as the speaker reconciles with captivity within institutional walls. Welcome arrives as the door locks behind.","opus":"Metallica voice the interior of an institutionalized mind, a patient labeled deranged and locked away, dreaming of freedom while insisting his captors are the truly blind ones. Assured he is insane, he plots mutiny, the mirror staring back hard as thoughts of killing seem the only exit. Sympathy for the confined and the thin line society draws around sanity.","sonnet":"A psychiatric patient narrates his imprisonment: the moon never changes, time stands still, the institution whispers insanity into his brain to keep him caged — but his dream is freedom, and by the end the rage has turned to murder-logic, the mirror confirming what they've made of him.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~wherever-i-may-roam":{"haiku":"A wanderer's celebration of nomadic freedom and independence; home is wherever the speaker lays their head, unbound by ties or possessions.","opus":"The narrator marries the open highway, claiming every label for a drifter and declaring that wherever he rests is home. The less he possesses, the more he feels he gains, ruling freely off any fixed route. Metallica give this rootless creed a sitar-tinged riff, framing self-reliance as a kingdom under the stars.","sonnet":"The open road becomes a permanent home and a bride simultaneously — attachment replaced by motion, possessions reduced to nothing but pride and the accumulation of hard knowledge. Freedom here is not escape but a chosen philosophy, and rootlessness is reframed as its own sovereign territory.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~whiplash":{"haiku":"Live music ignites primal thrashing ecstasy as crowds surrender to amplified chaos and physical abandon. The show transforms audiences into uncontrollable forces.","opus":"A pure adrenaline salute to the headbanging live experience and the band's own relentless touring grind, thrashing and exploding town after town because that's what they are.","sonnet":"A celebration of the live metal concert as physical release — heads banging, adrenaline surging, Marshall stacks piercing through ears — the band and the crowd fuse into a frenzied pact, and the road life is raw but they will never stop because this is what they choose.","src":"haiku"},
"metallica~whiskey-in-the-jar":{"haiku":"A highwayman robs a captain, but his lover betrays him for reward, leaving him imprisoned—a tale of theft, love's treachery, and captivity.","opus":"The traditional Irish highwayman's tale of a robber who holds up Captain Farrell at gunpoint, brings the loot home to his lover Molly, and is then betrayed by her when Farrell returns; his easy pleasures land him shot at and imprisoned in chains, a rogue undone by trusting a treacherous woman.","sonnet":"A retelling of the traditional Irish highwayman ballad — a robber ambushes a captain, brings the money home to his lover Molly, who then drugs him and tips off the authorities, landing him in prison, betrayed by the woman he trusted.","src":"haiku"},
"methyl-ethel~ubu":{"haiku":"Psychological withdrawal and self-destruction mask deeper trauma as the speaker observes a friend's disappearance into fear and isolation. Forced confrontation remains the only path to preventing total collapse.","opus":"Concern for someone withdrawing into isolation and dread, the narrator half-imagining them everywhere while fixating helplessly on the small inexplicable act of a haircut.","sonnet":"A worried observer addresses someone who has withdrawn from friends, gone pale, and isolated themselves — possibly from anxiety or depression — and the questions spiral from practical concern into haunted repetition about a small, symbolic change: why did you cut your hair?","src":"haiku"},
"metric~help-i-m-alive":{"haiku":"Survival pulses through anxiety and hunger; the narrator's heart hammers with the determination to keep living despite overwhelming pressure to surrender.","opus":"A rush of stage-fright and vitality, a racing heart that's equal parts terror of being eaten alive and proof of being fully awake. Emily Haines turns nervous adrenaline into a life-affirming pulse, fear and aliveness beating as one.","sonnet":"The speaker is overwhelmed by the raw, vulnerable sensation of being alive — heart hammering, terrified of stumbling in a world ready to consume them — but also exhilarated, insisting that as long as the blood flows there are no regrets and no limits on what they can take.","src":"haiku"},
"metrik~abyss":{"haiku":"Grief and legacy echo through depths; the speaker follows a lost voice downward until internal shadows finally rest in peace.","opus":"A soldier's elegy for a fallen comrade whose voice still echoes and lights the way through battle. Staring into the abyss, the survivor follows that voice until their own shadows can finally rest in peace.","sonnet":"A warrior's elegy for a fallen comrade — standing in a battlefield of ash, refusing to be put out by fire, hearing the voice of the dead echo through the abyss — the narrator follows that voice into the depths until their own shadows can finally rest.","src":"haiku"},
"metrik~awake":{"opus":"A looping meditation on finally coming awake after endless sinking and searching, treating sleeplessness as a hard-won arrival rather than exhaustion.","sonnet":"A person searching for something — an answer, a purpose — finally breaks through the endless drifting and wakefulness into genuine consciousness, repeating the moment of awakening as if confirming it's real.","src":"sonnet"},
"metrik~fall-to-the-dust":{"haiku":"Careless stumbling leads to inevitable collapse; the speaker warns against hasty ascent into a fall that destroys everything.","opus":"A liquid drum-and-bass cut built around loss, the narrator left in darkness with nothing but recollections after someone strips everything from him. A caution to pace yourself pictures a partner sinking to their knees, drifting out of reach, bound to crumble into the earth. Momentum fused with melancholy, a tender warning while watching someone buckle under their own ambition.","sonnet":"Loss stripped to its essentials: someone took something away and left only memories and air in the dark. The warning — walk before you run or you'll fall to the dust, on your knees in the burning sun — is directed at a person already slipping away, and the dust is both where they're falling and all that will remain.","src":"haiku"},
"metrik~immortal":{"haiku":"Rejection of mortality through unity, the speaker refuses to accept an ending that the world insists is inevitable, standing tall against biological fate.","opus":"A drum-and-bass affirmation of refusal, insisting through repetition that he won't go out that way, not now. Set against prescribed fate and manufactured hatred, it reaches for togetherness and honesty as the way to hold his ground and keep moving.","sonnet":"A resistance anthem against dying by the terms that have been set for you — the speaker refuses to accept a prescribed fate or a death defined by circumstance, insisting on standing tall and choosing their own destiny instead.","src":"haiku"},
"metro-boomin~space-cadet-feat-gunna":{"haiku":"Material excess and hedonistic fantasy create alien worlds of luxury where ordinary constraints dissolve into cosmic indulgence and weightless transcendence.","opus":"A flex of newfound wealth cast in outer-space imagery, Gunna recalling coming up from broke and sleeping on a mattress to living like an alien on his own planet of designer excess.","sonnet":"A luxury-and-codeine lifestyle flexed in space-travel terms: the speaker has escaped poverty into designer clothes, condos, and alien-level detachment from the world below, buying spaceships and Pateks while Gunna and the Boominati wave orbit their own private planet of wealth.","src":"haiku"},
"metz~blind-youth-industrial-park":{"opus":"A noisy elegy for a lost bond and a squandered youth spent scrabbling for meaning that never held. The Toronto punks channel the corrosion of a relationship into disorientation, a distorted lens that reveals words drained of sense and a certainty that there is no route back to what once fit.","sonnet":"A song about the end of a shared era of youthful improvisation — two people who once made things fit together, who climbed and clawed in the dark with instinctive trust, now find the connection broken and the words meaningless. The 'blind youth' is nostalgia for when ignorance and energy were enough to build something.","src":"sonnet"},
"mgmt~kids":{"haiku":"The song mourns childhood's loss of innocence and caution, contrasting wonder and recklessness with the adult burden of living with consequences.","opus":"Snapshots of early childhood, a crawling baby, bugs pulled from leaves, recollections blurring like a clouded pane, cluster beneath the refrain to restrain yourself and grab no more than necessary. Fallout and pain darken the innocence. MGMT dress this unsettled reflection on maturing and craving in the glossy synth-pop that made them unwitting festival hitmakers.","sonnet":"Childhood innocence gives way to consequence — the carefree insect-picking curiosity of a small person who does not yet calculate risk gradually encounters a world that demands self-control. The transition from being watched adoringly to needing to manage impulse carries an unexpected ache underneath the surreal imagery.","src":"haiku"},
"mgmt~someone-s-missing":{"haiku":"Absence haunts and crystallizes despite the motion around it; something vital remains missing from the completed picture.","opus":"A woozy dreamscape of psychedelic imagery circling one plain ache, the sense that something or someone is absent no matter what comes alive. The obsessive closing refrain makes the whole song collapse into that single unfillable gap.","sonnet":"A hallucinatory, elliptical meditation on absence and unease, moving through surreal imagery — neon lures, anti-christening blues, extinct things returning — without resolving what or who is missing. The feeling of loss is visceral and repeated but never explained or anchored in clear narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"mgmt~time-to-pretend":{"haiku":"Young people abandon dreams of meaningful lives to chase wealth, fame, and excess, accepting this shallow fate as inevitable. The song describes self-destruction through indulgence as a scripted role they're fated to play.","opus":"A giddy, doomed fantasy of rock-star excess, models for wives and heroin in Paris, that curdles mid-song into homesickness for childhood, mothers and dogs, then coasts fatalistically toward choking on vomit. Living fast is admitted from the start to be pretending. MGMT, the Brooklyn duo whose 2007 Oracular Spectacular defined a psych-pop moment, wrap this bleak irony in irresistibly bright synths, making the emptiness sound euphoric.","sonnet":"A fantasy of rock-star dissolution narrated with total lucidity about its own falseness — the speaker knows the Paris heroin arc ends in vomit and divorce, knows the models will have children, knows the whole script, and chooses it anyway over the commute. Underneath the bravado is genuine grief for playgrounds and dogs and the boredom of home.","src":"haiku"},
"mgna-crrrta~armageddon-seventh-heaven":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic ascension; either damnation or divine transformation awaits.","opus":"A hyperpop-adjacent fever dream welding apocalypse to luxury, floating dead through visions of the end while the skies tear open toward paradise. Flaming swarms and gunships, flashy diamonds and wings jostle in a fantasy that treats doomsday and excess as the same intoxicant. Faith, opulence and dread collapse into one dissociative flight from fear.","sonnet":"Apocalypse and luxury collapse into each other: left dead, floating in Armageddon dreams while VVS diamonds sit on a cage, the binary is simple — either bitches get their wings or burn up — and the speaker pushes through the fear with fantasized godhood and the word luxurious as both escape and armor.","src":"haiku"},
"mgna-crrrta~ome-drugzzz":{"haiku":"Love as intoxicating substance blurs addiction and romance, where the speaker becomes hooked on someone toxic they can't escape.","opus":"Love metabolized as narcotic, the beloved tasting like candy and medicine while the singer plays down being hooked. The morning-after confession that nothing will make sense but it doesn't matter turns infatuation into a willing, hazy overdose.","sonnet":"About a relationship being experienced as addiction — the other person tastes sweet, hits like a drug, and the narrator is ruefully aware they're hooked on something that probably isn't real love; the morning after, they don't know what to say, just that their pills and potions of unknown matter are the only honest thing left.","src":"haiku"},
"mgna-crrrta~until-4ever":{"haiku":"Fleeting connection—cigarettes, summer heat, and careful distance—captures a moment of desire that can't be sustained or promised forever.","opus":"A sun-drenched flash of summer romance kept deliberately brief because getting attached feels too risky. Sensory snapshots stand in for a fling savored precisely because it's fleeting.","sonnet":"A fleeting romantic encounter — a quick kiss before dashing, summer light, the boa constriction of infatuation, a cigarette stained with lip gloss — savored precisely because it is temporary and unguarded.","src":"haiku"},
"micah-ariss~never-run":{"haiku":"Relentless defiance against internalized demons and external threats crystallizes into refusal to retreat, where scars become proof of choosing to stand rather than collapse.","opus":"Someone locked in combat with private demons, scars and thoughts of suicide digs in and swears he will never flee, treating mere survival as an act of rebellion against the hurt. His history is the adversary, and simply standing his ground is the entire triumph.","sonnet":"A refusal to be defeated by inner demons or external antagonists: reading the signs in their own scars, the speaker insists they did not survive this far to walk away, and turns to face the pain directly — \"I will never run\" — claiming the stand as their own.","src":"haiku"},
"michael-jackson~slave-to-the-rhythm":{"haiku":"A woman sacrifices herself entirely to rhythms of labor and domesticity, her needs and identity erased by cycles of service.","opus":"A portrait of a woman worked to exhaustion by everyone who needs her, at home and on the job, unable to break the chains of obligation. The dance metaphor turns endless servitude into rhythm, framing her relentless labor as quiet tragedy.","sonnet":"A woman's entire existence is structured around service — to her partner, her children, her employer — and when she finally flees the abuse, she still finds herself dancing back home because the chains are deeper than circumstance. The rhythm of love is indistinguishable from the rhythm of servitude.","src":"haiku"},
"michael-lessard~use-the-rest":{"opus":"A depiction of self-medication and burnout, the narrator riding a chemical high while admitting he's a wearing-thin mess crawling out of his skin, joking that he'd delay death only until he'd had his fill.","sonnet":"A dark account of depression and dissociation — the narrator oscillates between feeling chemically elevated and crashing into despair, aware they are a mess and crawling in their own skin, contemplating death but not yet ready for it.","src":"sonnet"},
"michael-romeo~divide-conquer":{"opus":"A cinematic depiction of an alien or military invasion, humanity dissected and classified with surgical precision, paralyzed by fear as unstoppable forces divide and conquer a doomed world.","sonnet":"A song about systematic oppression or warfare — an enemy force uses precision and division to paralyze and conquer, and the narrator warns that those who allow themselves to be fragmented will fall, inverting the usual phrase: divided we stand, united we fall.","src":"sonnet"},
"mick-gordon~i-m-back-to-rise-feat-omega-sparx":{"haiku":"Redemption through combat reclaims lost pride; the speaker channels fury into disciplined resurgence from diminishment.","opus":"A fallen champion boxer's comeback anthem, all swagger and knockout bravado about reclaiming glory and crushing every challenger. Written for a fighting game, it's redemption told entirely through the ring.","sonnet":"A boxer who lost their way reclaims their identity through training and competition, cataloguing a fighter's technical arsenal — jabs, hooks, haymakers, feints — as proof that passion restored is more powerful than any enhancement, ending as champion and boss.","src":"haiku"},
"mick-gordon~transistor-fist":{"haiku":"Unrelenting aggression and contempt drive annihilation; the speaker as supernatural force obliterates opposition with casual dismissal.","opus":"A gleeful Doom Slayer power fantasy, taunting hell and its demons as worthless trash to be torn apart by the transistor fist.","sonnet":"A fan-written piece of in-universe dialogue voiced as the DOOM Slayer, consisting almost entirely of aggressive taunts directed at demons and the UAC — asserting that the enemies the Slayer faces are weak impostors who think themselves powerful but are nothing. It has no thematic depth beyond dramatizing the character's contemptuous, single-minded violence.","src":"haiku"},
"mid-air-thief~curve-and-light":{"haiku":"Drifting into darkness yet finding anchor in shared descent, light and touch sustaining through dissolution.","opus":"A hushed Korean psych-folk meditation on falling deeper into disorientation and inviting someone to unravel with you, trusting that even washed away you can trace your way back. Before time grinds everyone to dust, it offers dissolving into color and light together.","sonnet":"A Korean song about holding someone through exhaustion and disorientation: the narrator is tired of being here, can't see what's ahead, and might stumble — but they offer their hand and say that even if time grinds them all to dust, they can figure it out together in the moments they're awake, swimming as colors in the light.","src":"haiku"},
"mid-air-thief~these-chains":{"haiku":"Bondage and captivity as metaphor for emotional entrapment or societal constraints.","opus":"A Korean psych-folk portrait of being slowly crushed by tightening chains and a bloodied blade, arms too weak to fight; the dread is of forces closing in inexorably while strength drains away against a hardening wall.","sonnet":"A Korean-language song about exhaustion and entrapment — tightening iron chains gaining speed around a narrowing shadow, a blood-stained knife swung by someone unseen, arms drained of strength, a hardening wall being slowly pushed against — the body losing control and being pressed toward collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"midnight-oil~beds-are-burning-remastered":{"haiku":"The earth is in crisis and burning; stolen lands and indigenous rights must be returned in an act of justice and fair recompense.","opus":"Midnight Oil, the militantly political Australian rockers fronted by Peter Garrett, turn a stomping anthem into a demand for Aboriginal land rights. Set in the scorching desert, it argues bluntly that the land must be returned to its original owners, and asks how anyone can carry on comfortably while such injustice stands.","sonnet":"In the Western Desert where the temperature reaches forty-five degrees, the demand is stated simply: this land belongs to its original people and must be returned. The moral clarity is absolute, and the inability to dance or sleep while the injustice continues frames restitution not as charity but as an overdue debt.","src":"haiku"},
"midnight-oil~king-of-the-mountain":{"haiku":"Aboriginal defiance and solidarity against colonial extraction demands that working people reject false idolatry and claim their own power.","opus":"An Australian landscape of cane fields and coming storms becomes the setting for a working-class figure who climbs to the summit and refuses to be idolized, insisting on his own terms as king of the mountain.","sonnet":"The speaker walks through Australian landscapes — high dry grass, yellow-bellied snakes, cane cutters, sugar trains — refusing mythic or religious status while acknowledging irreplaceable losses, ultimately pledging to follow someone across liquid tarmac wastelands and ghost prairie mountains to a familiar place.","src":"haiku"},
"midnight-oil~power-and-the-passion-remastered-version":{"haiku":"Complacency and escapism corrode social conscience; the speaker witnesses decline while leisure distracts from accountability.","opus":"Midnight Oil's stinging state-of-the-nation on Australia, skewering sun-drunk complacency, creeping Americanization and the fallout of the Whitlam years. Its call to hold the toughest position is a demand to feel something and act rather than rot away in paradise.","sonnet":"A sharp critique of Australian consumer culture — sunburned complacency, junk food, suburban drift, and political apathy — set against a backdrop of American influence and the lingering memory of a Whitlam-era political moment that briefly promised something else. The song insists that comfort has made people passive in the face of things worth fighting for.","src":"haiku"},
"midrift~burden":{"haiku":"Incompleteness and self-sabotage plague connection; the speaker cannot see clearly, cannot love themselves, yet claims contentment in their own internal collapse.","opus":"Falling for someone while feeling like dead weight to yourself, caught between the small happiness of being near them and the shame of what you cost them.","sonnet":"A confused, tender admission of falling for someone while feeling like a burden to both them and oneself, yet finding unexpected happiness in that very place.","src":"haiku"},
"midwinter~chasing-butterflies":{"haiku":"Lost connection fractures stability as proximity becomes ignition, burning love into ash. The speaker spirals in pursuit of something always just beyond reach, paralyzed between wanting and losing.","opus":"A self-loathing narrator caught in emotional drought craves catastrophe just to feel something, while a toxic lover sets them on fire and they'd rather burn together than be saved. Hating who they've become, the plea for help doubles as a wish to be someone else entirely.","sonnet":"A co-dependent relationship described as both fire and addiction — the other person is simultaneously the ignition and the reason for burning, and the speaker, hating who they have become, admits they would burn alongside them rather than let go.","src":"haiku"},
"midwxst~pretty-girls":{"haiku":"Beauty becomes the weapon that corrupts desire; the speaker remains enslaved to someone whose physical presence triggers both addiction and self-destruction.","opus":"Hyperpop heartbreak over a relationship that wrecked him: still tasting the longing, telling her not to come back, ending it for good, even as a voicemail of her missing him underscores how the wound stays open past the goodbye.","sonnet":"An obsessive, unresolved attachment to someone who \"fucked me up\" — rain pouring, longing that won't quit, a final admission that despite the damage there's no empathy left in the dynamic and it needs to end, however much it doesn't.","src":"haiku"},
"mike-candys~one-night-in-ibiza-dj-favorite-remix-djfavorite-com":{"haiku":"A beach party atmosphere celebrates dancing, drinking, and hedonistic escape in Ibiza without emotional attachment.","opus":"A hedonistic club anthem that rejects love, stars and hearts in favor of one carefree night of partying in Ibiza, all drinking, dancing and shaking on the floor. There's no depth beneath the escapist demand to lose yourself for a single wild night.","sonnet":"A club anthem about wanting a single night of hedonistic escape in Ibiza — no romance, no strings, just dancing, bodies, and the high of a packed dancefloor, explicitly rejecting any deeper emotional exchange in favor of pure present-tense pleasure.","src":"haiku"},
"mike-oldfield~moonlight-shadow":{"haiku":"Violent death separates lovers permanently while memory preserves the lost one as lunar echo, grief crystallizing into eternal vigil across impossible distance.","opus":"A haunted ballad of a woman helplessly watching her love shot down and carried off, unable to reach him across the divide. The recurring moonlight-shadow image turns grief into something spectral, a vigil sustained by the hope of reunion in heaven.","sonnet":"A woman watches the man she loves die — shot six times by a running man in a desperate fight — and is left unable to push through to him across the boundary between living and dead. She addresses him across that distance, waiting for the vision of him to appear once more under the moonlight.","src":"haiku"},
"mike-posner~i-m-not-dead-yet-ellis-edit":{"haiku":"Defiant survival despite counting down; insistence on presence despite approaching extinction.","opus":"Posner, who once turned near-death experiences into stark confessionals, offers a survivor's anthem given a dance edit. Hooded and cold, unseen by passersby, the narrator monetizes his own pain by rhyming it while marveling that it still sounds beautiful. The refrain becomes stubborn proof of life, a broken heart pounding on, insisting he is not dead yet.","sonnet":"A sad song that knows it is a sad song — lightning and thunder in the head, hoodie on, invisible to passing strangers, paid for the pain as long as it rhymes — the speaker is not dead yet, the broken heart beats like a jackknife tearing through the chest, and both the beauty and the cruelty are acknowledged simultaneously as the price of surviving.","src":"haiku"},
"mike-posner~i-took-a-pill-in-ibiza-seeb-remix":{"haiku":"Hollow fame and depression masked by materialism, a washed-up artist only knowing sadness.","opus":"A one-hit pop star's brutally honest reckoning with fame's hollow core, chasing cool to impress Avicii and finding only isolation. The remix that made it huge ironically dressed his sad confession in a party beat.","sonnet":"A confessional portrait of fame's hollow interior — the speaker spent his money, lost his relevance, and cannot open up to anyone, warning the listener that the high life looks nothing like it feels from the inside, where all he knows are sad songs.","src":"haiku"},
"mike-shinoda~already-over":{"haiku":"Closure arrives before recognition; the speaker searches for windows they already sealed, attempts intimacy with a partner who has moved beyond return, resignation replacing hope.","opus":"Shinoda writes from the flat aftermath of a relationship that cannot be reopened, reaching for a window he has already closed. Denial and survival blur, optimism revealed as blindness, and no effort changes the fact that nothing waits on the other side. A clear-eyed reckoning with finality, grief less dramatic than the numb realization that it is simply over.","sonnet":"A person realises a relationship or situation ended without their noticing — looking for a window already closed, nothing on the other side — and the loop of denial collapses into a bruised acceptance that survival instinct read as optimism couldn't prevent the loss.","src":"haiku"},
"mildrage~silent-spear":{"haiku":"A person screams for authenticity and freedom against enforced conformity and silence. They rebel against censorship while questioning their own validity.","opus":"The pain of being silenced and unable to be your true self, with unspoken silence stabbing like a spear and self-harm marks engraved on the arms. It builds toward rebellion against a conformist mass that demands unity and forbids the narrator to fight, insisting on the right to shout who they are.","sonnet":"About the suppression of authentic identity — a scream that finds no outlet and gets carved into the body instead, a society that eliminates non-conformity in the name of unity. The chorus asks why freedom is denied and calls for raising a rebel flag against enforced normalcy.","src":"haiku"},
"miley-cyrus~the-climb":{"haiku":"Perseverance through doubt constitutes victory; the journey's difficulty matters more than destination, redefining success as refusal to surrender.","opus":"A wholesome inspirational anthem reframing struggle as the point rather than the obstacle, the journey mattering more than the summit. It endures because the sentiment is uncomplicated: keep going through the doubt, because the climb itself is the meaning.","sonnet":"Persistence over outcome — the speaker affirms that self-doubt, setbacks, and loss are all part of a journey whose value lies in the effort itself rather than the destination, addressed as much to themselves as to anyone else.","src":"haiku"},
"milk-krayt~all-the-lies":{"haiku":"Pervasive deception and inability to distinguish truth from fabrication, betrayal compound by the layers of false narrative.","opus":"An address to someone trapped in a self-destructive loop of lies and alibis about being unable to go back, living out their days in isolation. The narrator refuses to keep waiting on the next relapse and pushes them to break the pattern before there's no time left.","sonnet":"Someone watching a cycle of absence and excuse repeat — notes written explaining why they can't come home, a life spent alone, the question of whether this time the pattern can be broken. The answer the narrator refuses to wait for is already implied in the refrain: always losing, over and over.","src":"haiku"},
"milkblood~wicked":{"haiku":"Prophetic knowledge isolates the seer; others dismiss warnings as arrogance while fate executes its script regardless of foresight.","opus":"A witchy, defiant strut about occult self-belief, the narrator warning a scornful skeptic that their world will burn for mocking her divination. She locates power in her own blood while they demand and reprimand from ignorance. The chorus flips their insult back at them, wickedness reframed as strength they are too closed-minded to understand. Confidence as spellcraft.","sonnet":"A speaker with power in their blood confronts someone who mocks what they don't understand: 'don't say it's wicked 'cause you don't understand it' — the speaker aligns with divination and constellations as sources of genuine authority while commanding the skeptic to roll their eyes out the door.","src":"haiku"},
"milktub~a-1inkes0":{"haiku":"Carefree acceptance of chaos and impermanence; life is absurd and pointless, so dance through the troubles rather than resist them, finding joy in the mess itself.","opus":"Milktub have spent decades as anime-adjacent party-rock veterans, and this rudderless-boat manifesto is pure to that spirit. Life is a boat without a rudder and that is precisely what makes it interesting, so aim high, expect little, treat trouble as fuel, and keep causing mischief rather than worrying. The band frames wave-raising troublemaking as the only honest way to be alive, joy found in the mess itself.","sonnet":"A cheerful manifesto for living without a plan: life is a rudderless boat and that is exactly what makes it interesting, so set ambitions high, keep expectations low, treat trouble as fuel, and keep causing mischief rather than worrying — a wave-raising, troublemaking approach to existence as the only honest one.","src":"haiku"},
"million-dead~breaking-the-back":{"haiku":"Idealism collides with economic necessity as labor hollows the spirit while institutional work disciplines turns revolutionary youth into compliant automatons.","opus":"A bitterly literate account of youthful idealism worn down by dead-end jobs and air-conditioned tedium. Its clever inversion is the refrain that becoming a man meant having his spirit broken, disillusionment sold to him as maturity.","sonnet":"A young idealist enters the working world expecting his optimistic social theories to survive contact with reality, and is systematically stripped of them by telemarketing, tills, and the soul-deadening routine of office work. The irony is total: the process that was supposed to make a man of him is what breaks him, and that breaking turns out to be the actual making.","src":"haiku"},
"mimi-barks~suicide":{"opus":"A harrowing trap-metal descent into dissociation, numbness, and suicidal ideation, framed by paradoxes about light and darkness being one. Trust dissolves, the past won't release its grip, and the narrator feels trapped in a body beyond control. The desperate refrain, loving life while begging release, captures the whiplash of wanting to live and die at once.","sonnet":"A song about the paradox of wanting to die while still loving life — the narrator describes dissociation, ketamine numbness, past branding, and the exhaustion of existing in a body she cannot control, while the chorus flips between 'SUICIDE' and 'I love my life.' The Hermetic philosophical framing of duality — all truths are half-truths — gives the internal contradiction an intellectual scaffolding without resolving it.","src":"sonnet"},
"mindless-self-indulgence~it-gets-worse":{"haiku":"Life offers no redemption without wealth or beauty; the speaker confronts that suffering only deepens and isolation is inevitable.","opus":"Mindless Self Indulgence weaponize their abrasive electro-punk into a mock motivational sermon, the narrator declaring himself the truth and insisting life doesn't improve unless you're pretty or rich. Every gesture of consolation collapses into cruelty, promising the listener will die alone and things only get worse. The bitter parody of self-help wisdom turns despair into a jeering, deliberately obnoxious anthem.","sonnet":"A deliberately anti-inspirational address dismantles the mythology of self-improvement. Attractiveness and wealth are the only real variables; everything else is noise. The narrator delivers this as both dark comedy and sincere nihilism — welcoming the listener to a hell not of their making, promising only that it will get worse.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~breathe":{"haiku":"Environmental desecration and industrial poison choke the world while collective denial prevents action and perpetuates ecocide.","opus":"An eco-apocalyptic scream at a poisoned, dying planet of corporate denial, discarded needles and kids sucking in fouled air. The command to breathe becomes a desperate order to a world that never figured out how, and is running out of time.","sonnet":"A harangue about environmental collapse — rusted syringes, corporate denial, children gasping second-hand air, waste-water graveyards — demanding that a dying planet wake up and breathe while skewering the contradiction between concern and inaction.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~death-and-destruction":{"haiku":"Power's absolute certainty in deciding fate for others, couched in aggressive rhetoric and repetition.","opus":"Ministry sampling Bush-era decider rhetoric and looping death and destruction until the phrase indicts itself, a portrait of blind executive certainty producing carnage. The industrial repetition turns political arrogance into a death march.","sonnet":"A very short track built around a sampled George W. Bush quote declaring himself \"the decider\" — the word \"death and destruction\" is looped beneath it as a blunt editorial comment on the consequences of that decision-making authority.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~die-in-a-crash":{"haiku":"Existential despair and pressure driving toward violent self-destruction. Living fast and crashing is preferable to enduring the weight of modern life.","opus":"Al Jourgensen's industrial-metal outfit channels the grind of daily existence into corrosive fury. Worn down by struggle and pretense, the narrator confesses he's at his breaking point, seething with hatred and cursing both present and future, until his whole outlook shrinks to a reckless, self-destructive motto.","sonnet":"Rage accumulates under social pressure until it tips into something almost apocalyptic — hate for today, hate for tomorrow, a prayer for holocaust born from the conviction that no one cares anymore. The live-fast refrain is less a slogan than a fuse finally burning all the way down.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~end-of-days-pt-1":{"haiku":"Apocalypse arrives through desperation and indifference; judgment day finds no one prepared or afraid.","opus":"Jourgensen's industrial-metal jeremiad against a world he sees drowning in blood and apathy, the reckoning arriving while nobody cares. Itchy trigger fingers stand in for prayer, deceit pools everywhere, and the narrator swears through his fear that civilization has lost the plot. Rage, revelation and despair hammered into a refrain that humanity has hit its final hour.","sonnet":"A furious vision of civilisation's collapse: judgment day is here and nobody cares, lies and trigger-happy solutions have replaced prayer, and the world has dissolved into desperation, condemnation, and terror — \"we've clearly lost our way.\"","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~hero":{"haiku":"A soldier is made into a killing machine and sent to war, depicted as a hero while being manipulated and ultimately sacrificed for political goals.","opus":"An antiwar scream about a soldier reduced to a weapon, sent marching into carnage by leaders who use him up and discard him, the only real winners being those who profit. The relentless chant hollows out patriotic slogans until they ring empty. Al Jourgensen's industrial-metal machine turns repetition and sampled noise into exactly this kind of blunt political rage.","sonnet":"A soldier turned inside out — conditioned to kill, sent down a highway of death, then discarded once the bill comes due. The song has no sentimentality for the institution that made him; the hero is both weapon and victim, and the only ones who pay when the ritual of war is over are the ones who fought it.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~jesus-built-my-hotrod":{"haiku":"A joyride through primal impulse and carnal excess, where the speaker follows pleasure and instinct despite uncertainty. The song celebrates raw sensation and physical abandon over rational restraint or social propriety.","opus":"A gleeful nonsense blowout with Gibby Haynes babbling scripture and speed, pure rockabilly-industrial abandon that means to fry your brain rather than say anything. The joke is that this much velocity needs no coherent thought at all.","sonnet":"A stream of deliberate nonsense syllables and crude double-entendres that subverts the rock-song form entirely, the lyrics refusing coherence as an act in itself — any attempt to extract a throughline is exactly what the song is designed to frustrate.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~just-one-fix":{"haiku":"Drug addiction perpetuates itself as an endless cycle; a person descends from desperation into madness while seeking one more fix.","opus":"Al Jourgensen turning his own heroin addiction into a punishing loop, the craving flattened into a single mechanical demand that never resolves; the junkie's monkey as something that outlives the man riding it.","sonnet":"A song about the consuming grip of heroin addiction — the narrator is driving through the night in a state of desperate need, unable to find salvation or destination, with the refrain of 'just one fix' functioning as both prayer and admission that the monkey never dies.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~let-s-go":{"haiku":"The song catalogs escalating acts of recklessness and destruction—from personal crashes to wars and genocide—delivered in a satirical, nihilistic tone. Each line pushes further toward collective madness and annihilation.","opus":"Ministry's nihilist thrash accelerating toward every catastrophe at once, from car crashes to World War III and genocide, using the giddy rush of self-destruction to indict a civilization gleefully driving itself off a cliff.","sonnet":"Using dark irony, the song lists a series of catastrophic acts — reckless driving, war in Iraq, ethnic cleansing, World War III — as though they are exciting destinations, satirizing humanity's self-destructive trajectory as collective insanity.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~life-is-good":{"haiku":"A soldier returning from Iraq struggles to reconcile the violence and death they have witnessed and inflicted with attempts to reintegrate into civilian life.","sonnet":"A combat veteran back from a third tour in Iraq finds civilian life unreachable — the carnage of war has colonized his perception so completely that he sees corpse eyes in his unborn child and threat in a stranger's handshake. The sardonic refrain underscores how thoroughly institutional violence has unmade the person it was meant to protect.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~n-w-o":{"haiku":"The song addresses a dehumanizing world order where conformity replaces individuality and love becomes hollow commodity.","opus":"Al Jourgensen's furious industrial attack on the first Bush's new world order rhetoric, twisting the language of love and order into menace and control. It is Gulf War America seen as seduction into obedience.","sonnet":"A collage of Reagan-era political language spliced with the narrator's own alienated love — the 'new world order' is both the political slogan being mocked and the condition of loving something that manipulates you completely.","src":"haiku","fable":"Ministry hijacks the reassuring presidential cadence from a Gulf War-era address and feeds it through distortion until the promise curdles into threat. The verses wear the language of romance, but the beloved is domination itself: a voice that offers gifts, plays generous, and confesses in the same breath that it means harm, seducing a populace already captured before it grasps what has happened. A clown's gaze fixed on the fading light is the read's key image, a paradise peddled by someone who knows it is a sham. Then structure takes over and interpretation ends: the title's abbreviated slogan is chanted past all sense, the repetition itself becoming the argument, the way a phrase said often enough by power stops being a claim and turns into a spell, a machine built to repeat one thing until repeating is all that is left.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control"],"means":"power's promise chanted past sense until repetition becomes a spell","built":"presidential cadence fed through distortion until reassurance curdles into threat"},
"ministry~no-glory":{"haiku":"Soldiers die for corporate profit made by Halliburton and contractors while politicians ignore the cost in blood.","opus":"A furious indictment of the Iraq War, naming Halliburton, Blackwater and Bechtel as the profiteers sending soldiers to die for nothing but swelling pockets, mocking a president's hollow mission accomplished. Ministry, Al Jourgensen's industrial-metal vehicle, made mid-2000s anti-Bush rage a defining preoccupation, and this track channels that politics into blunt sloganeering.","sonnet":"Soldiers and citizens are sent to die in wars engineered for corporate profit, while politicians sign away lives without reading the documents. The refrain strips the patriotic veneer bare — no honor, no purpose, only greed dressing itself in flags while bodies accumulate for someone else's bottom line.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~no-w":{"haiku":"The song critiques political deception and loss of freedom. Leaders deceive the people while those in power avoid personal consequences for widespread suffering.","opus":"Al Jourgensen's blunt Bush-era protest, naming the administration outright and raging at a country sold empty promises while its liberties were stripped, the mocking demand for trust from leaders actively betraying it.","sonnet":"A direct attack on the Bush-era U.S. government — Colin Powell, Dick Cheney, George Bush hammering away while preaching trust and stripping liberties — framed as a simple explanation for why everyone feels deceived and screwed.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~palestina":{"haiku":"The narrative arc of a young woman named Palestina from oppression and rage to radicalization, ultimately dying as a suicide bomber to fight for her freedom.","opus":"Ministry sketch a young woman radicalized by oppression into becoming a suicide bomber, her short sweet life narrated with grim detachment. It's a provocation about how rage and occupation manufacture martyrs, refusing easy comfort.","sonnet":"A narrative portrait of a young Palestinian woman radicalized by witnessing occupation — she straps on explosives and dies as a martyr — told plainly without ideological commentary, the song treating her story as tragic inevitability born from documented oppression.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~piss":{"haiku":"Legal systems and authority enforce compliance through surveillance and threat, reducing freedom to illusion while creating permanent records of private consumption.","opus":"A snarling attack on a justice and drug-testing system that criminalizes drug use while everyone in the courtroom is corrupt or compromised. Al Jourgensen's stance is pure contempt: years of pissing in jars to prove a point that indicts the whole machine, not him.","sonnet":"A defendant navigates a corrupt legal system where lawyers, judges, and the whole apparatus of justice are compromised — the speaker's crime is drug use, and his fury is directed at the absurdity of being surveilled, punished, and humiliated for filling his lungs.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~punch-in-the-face":{"haiku":"The speaker wants to break jaws and punch someone repeatedly until their face is raw—nothing satisfies like violence and pain.","opus":"One brutal impulse stretched to ridiculous length: a mounting, gleeful hunger to shatter a jaw and keep hammering until police step in, each verse claiming nothing feels as good as brutality. It's comedy and threat at once. Ministry, Al Jourgensen's industrial-metal crew, feed on exactly this kind of battering, hook-heavy aggression where a mindless chant becomes the whole belligerent point.","sonnet":"Pure cathartic aggression stripped of all subtext — the entire song is the act it describes, repeating the fantasy of violence with escalating enthusiasm until it becomes almost comedic in its single-mindedness. There is no backstory, no target identified, just the satisfaction of the impulse stated over and over as both threat and chorus.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~rio-grande-blood":{"haiku":"Political satire attacking wealthy leaders and war profiteers who exploit the poor for oil and money, enriching themselves while ordering others to die in wars.","opus":"A savage satire voiced as a caricature of President George W. Bush, portraying him as a greedy dictator who wages war for crude oil and profit while squeezing the poor to enrich the wealthy.","sonnet":"The song is a satirical attack on the George W. Bush administration, voiced in first person as Bush confessing to waging war for oil profits, enriching corporations like Halliburton, oppressing the poor, and lying to the public. It frames the Iraq War as straightforward resource extraction driven by greed.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~roadhouse-blues":{"haiku":"A man obsessed with immediate pleasure and excess before the world ends reflects on aimless hedonism at a roadhouse, convinced the future is bleak and death inevitable.","opus":"Ministry hijack the Doors barroom classic and drag it through industrial sludge, keeping the hedonistic, doomed swagger of a drive toward a rowdy dive bar. Under the party bravado runs a shrug of fatalism: grab your kicks now, because the future's shaky and the end could arrive at any moment.","sonnet":"A covers-faithful reading of the Doors original: keep moving, the roadhouse is the destination, and the future is uncertain enough that a beer at dawn and a long night of letting it roll is the only honest response to knowing the end is always near.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~so-what":{"haiku":"Thrill-seeking murderers seek justification through environmental blame; the song frames killing as inevitable product of parental neglect and generational indifference.","opus":"Ministry weld a serial-killer voiceover to old juvenile-crime newsreel over an industrial onslaught, a narrator who murders just to feel alive and dismisses every consequence with a shrug. The blame falls on grown-ups who set no example worth honoring. Depravity and a nihilistic fuse ready to blow converge into pure contempt for a rotten society.","sonnet":"A thrill-killer's psychology is assembled from broadcast fragments and spoken-word narration about juvenile delinquency, violence, and parental failure. The narrator absorbs the indictment and turns it outward — born too late, refusing accountability, indifferent to consequences, a screaming headache on the brow of a state that helped create him.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~the-dick-song":{"haiku":"A scathing political attack on Dick Cheney as evil and destructive, depicted as starting wars, profiting from conflict, and serving demonic interests.","opus":"A savage protest broadside naming Dick Cheney as a remorseless vice president, calling him Satan's son and chosen one, stitching in real news clips about the shotgun hunting incident and Iraq to paint him as a war-profiteer relishing the bodies. Ministry, Al Jourgensen's industrial-metal machine, spent the Bush years on furious anti-Republican records, and this is that project at its most gleefully venomous.","sonnet":"Dick Cheney as literal Antichrist — the song runs the former vice president through a prosecutorial litany of wars started, faces shot, and blood spilled for profit, framing him not as a flawed politician but as Satan's chosen instrument. News audio clips interrupt the fury to ground the absurdist theology in documented reality.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~the-last-sucker":{"haiku":"A privileged man given power through family connections discovers he's a figurehead; his subjects hate him as he realizes he no longer controls anything.","opus":"Al Jourgensen's mock farewell for a coddled dynastic leader who never earned a thing and parrots speeches he cannot follow, growing more despised with each falsehood. Closing his Bush-era industrial protest trilogy, Ministry jeer this puppet ruler offstage, finally stripped of any authority over the country he misled.","sonnet":"A satirical portrait of a privileged figurehead who floated to power on inheritance and connections, reads scripts he cannot comprehend, and now finds himself stripped of authority while still bearing its ceremonial burden. The comedy is merciless — the last sucker in a long line of suckers, finally exposed and dismissed.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~thieves":{"haiku":"Corruption and hypocrisy infest every level of power, with liars and thieves stealing from those beneath while claiming moral authority.","opus":"A furious indictment of the powerful as crooks, frauds and hypocrites, spliced with protest chants demanding minds be freed from a brutal, bigoted order. Jourgensen's fury targets a corrupt system where you can never be sure where anyone truly stands.","sonnet":"A furious, fragmented accusation against hypocrites and liars in power — two-faced bastards and sycophants, a society the speaker wants to rip down — intercut with sampled voices calling for liberation and questioning which side anyone is actually on.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~tv-ii":{"haiku":"Television is a thief stealing agency and truth; it broadcasts lies wrapped in commodity, promising everything while taking it all. The song challenges listeners to connect dots and escape the voices in their heads.","opus":"A media-saturation rant against television as a lying intruder in the bedroom, promising everything and stealing it, demanding you connect the dots and pledge allegiance while asking who could possibly care. Paranoia and contempt fuse into a barrage of unanswerable questions. Ministry, Al Jourgensen's industrial pioneers, built their sound on exactly this, sampled noise and sneering repetition turned against the machinery of information.","sonnet":"Television as parasite and liar — it lives in the bedroom, promises everything, delivers nothing, and tells you to pledge allegiance while burning the world. The speaker is at the end of their patience with the noise, demanding someone connect the obvious dots between the machine's promises and the void it actually fills.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~watch-yourself":{"haiku":"Surveillance and control suffocate the speaker; Big Brother watches, listens, and hunts. There is no escape in America—only paranoia and the constant threat of interrogation or erasure.","opus":"Al Jourgensen's paranoid alarm about a surveillance state where being watched slides into interrogation, deportation, extermination. Blunt agit-industrial insisting there's no running from the machinery of the country you live in.","sonnet":"A surveillance state anthem: Big Brother is watching, listening, and logging everything, and there is no running away in this country or this world — someone wants your information, your deportation, your extermination, and all you can do is watch yourself.","src":"haiku"},
"ministry~worthless":{"haiku":"A scathing internal monologue condemning self-hatred and degradation. The song frames the subject as worthless and directs them toward self-destruction through various forms of abuse and numbness.","opus":"A relentless barrage of contempt urging the listener toward self-hatred, self-destruction, and self-degradation, insisting they are worthless and mean nothing to the speaker.","sonnet":"A relentless barrage of contempt and commands for self-destruction aimed at a target the speaker declares entirely worthless — the song works as a brutal litany of dehumanisation.","src":"haiku"},
"miraidempa~they-who-know-the-truth-are-not-equal-to-those-who-love-it-and-they-who-love-it-are-not-equal-to-those-who-delight-in-it":{"haiku":"Knowledge exhausts where joy ignites; the speaker tumbles through philosophy while craving the simple pleasure of action. Wisdom without delight produces only fatigue and emptiness.","opus":"Built around a line of Confucius, this frantic Japanese track wrestles with the gap between knowing, loving and truly delighting in something. A restless narrator, sleepless and dulled, wants to act and enjoy but finds words empty and the world unchangeable, drinking to blot out a sense of futility. Ancient wisdom colliding with modern burnout and self-disgust.","sonnet":"A bleary, exhausted confession of intellectual and emotional failure — unable to sleep, unable to feel, wanting to die — framed by the Confucian proverb that knowing is less than loving and loving less than delighting, exposing the gap between the speaker's compulsive learning and any genuine joy in it: if there are no words, there is no meaning, so they may as well leave.","src":"haiku"},
"mire~refined":{"haiku":"The speaker undergoes constant self-examination and death to be reborn; they shed old identities and seek truth encoded within, pursuing purposeful transformation.","opus":"The narrator pursues self-improvement through annihilation, destroying himself in order to come back changed, tearing at his own body to reach a truer understanding within an unending trial. Repeatedly witnessing his own demise, he admits deeper truth and reshapes his very nature. The metalcore treatment turns inner transformation into a brutal ritual of cutting apart and remaking.","sonnet":"Self-destruction as spiritual practice — the speaker repeatedly kills off an old version of himself in order to emerge with deeper clarity. The process is painful and constant, a scraping-down to the bone that strips away false selves and received beliefs until something truer and more essential is uncovered beneath.","src":"haiku"},
"mirrorcell~bleach":{"haiku":"Perfectionism demands chemical erasure; the speaker seeks dissolution and reconstruction through bleach and defacement, hoping destruction might create something useful.","opus":"A plea to be scrubbed clean of the stains a person left behind, equating worthiness with erasure and the desperate wish to be made perfect and useful. The imagery of chemical cleansing turns self-hatred into a longing for total obliteration of the self.","sonnet":"A speaker, commanded to \"keep sweet, comply\" and to wash out stains, longs to be chemically erased and remade into something useful — a meditation on the violent self-erasure demanded by perfectionism or controlling authority.","src":"haiku"},
"mirrors~damien":{"haiku":"Words left unspoken haunt the speaker after a loss, trapping them in silence and fragmented memories while the other person's presence both comforts and compounds the pain.","opus":"A metalcore lament that circles the words left unsaid to someone now gone, treating silence as the truest haunting. The speaker walks numbed and dismembered by grief, yet insists memory will keep the departed close and light the way onward. Mirrors channel the melodic-hardcore convention of loss into a vow rather than mere despair, letting warmth survive the wound.","sonnet":"A man is paralysed by grief after losing someone, his hands and feet figuratively severed by words left unspoken. Numbness and blindness fill the hollow space of loss, yet warm memories slowly light a path forward, carrying the dead with him as a quiet companion rather than a haunting.","src":"haiku"},
"misery-signals~the-tempest":{"haiku":"Hope and perseverance through darkness, urging resilience against overwhelming adversity.","opus":"Metalcore turned toward rescue, a song addressed to a broken friend urging them to mend and fly again. Its stance is stubborn hope, the belief that even a black sky can be resculpted together.","sonnet":"An urgent call to someone who has fallen — urging them to mend, reset, ride the wind again, and rise from ashes rather than surrender to the darkness; the speaker insists the night can be remade and the friend can fly once more.","src":"haiku"},
"miss-fortune~delusions-of-grandeur":{"haiku":"Someone pursues impossible satisfaction despite inevitable despair, armed with cold ambition and the will to dominate others.","opus":"Miss Fortune deliver a metalcore breakup rant, the narrator resigned to never being enough for his target and wishing them a night of tormented insomnia. He mocks the person as an icy player whose grand ambitions are laughable fantasy, mixing surrender with disdain. Absurd spoken asides puncture the drama, leaving wounded pride and spite intertwined.","sonnet":"Bitter defiance toward someone who underestimated the narrator and still cannot be satisfied. Staring down failure, the speaker acknowledges the absurdity of the dynamic — a cold womanizer, grandiose expectations, a relationship already set in stone — and offers a pointed wish for the other person's sleepless nights as parting consolation.","src":"haiku"},
"miss-fortune~matching-energies":{"haiku":"Reciprocal accountability as relationship arbiter; the speaker demands alignment of intent and calls out discrepancy as grounds for complete severance.","opus":"A reckoning with someone who was never transparent, abandoned the singer in tragedy, and never gave back the energy they demanded. The long-awaited confrontation ends in a triumphant declaration that the offender is finished.","sonnet":"A reckoning with someone who was supposed to match energy but was never transparent, abandoned the scene of a tragedy, and never thought of the speaker once — now they are cancelled, \"you can't say shit to me,\" and the gravity of that betrayal is finally understood.","src":"haiku"},
"miss-may-i~day-by-day":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and resentment fester as the narrator refuses to carry an ungrateful partner's weight any longer, reclaiming their independence.","opus":"A metalcore severing of a one-sided bond, where devotion curdles into resentment. The narrator has spent himself protecting and carrying someone who only took, and cutting them loose is framed as survival, not cruelty.","sonnet":"A speaker who has spent years protecting and carrying others reaches a breaking point, declaring they can no longer shoulder the weight of someone who only takes, and demanding the other person finally see the cost of that dependency through the speaker's shattered perspective.","src":"haiku"},
"miss-may-i~gears":{"haiku":"Humans become machines—ground down to nothing by exploitation and abuse, stripped of will and left desperate and confused.","opus":"Metalcore rage at being reduced to a machine, a device built to be used and abused until worn to nothing, addressed to whatever system or person does the grinding. The dehumanization is the wound and the accusation both.","sonnet":"About being treated as a machine built for someone else's use — exhausted and worn down by exploitation, the narrator describes people reduced to devices, gears grinding to nothing, existing only to serve a system that cares nothing for them.","src":"haiku"},
"miss-may-i~hey-mister":{"haiku":"An absent father becomes the emblem of betrayal; the speaker transforms abandonment into autonomy and refuses further dependence.","opus":"A son's reckoning with an absent father, addressed as a stranger he wasted his life chasing. The grief curdles into a coward's anthem, the boy grown into the man his father will never know, done searching and done needing him.","sonnet":"A son confronts an absent father — the mister who was never there — channelling years of abandonment and searching into defiance, declaring that he has become his own man without the father who will never know him.","src":"haiku"},
"miss-may-i~shadows-inside":{"haiku":"Transformation through adversity burns away the past as new light penetrates internal darkness, revealing hidden strength forged in the flames of suffering.","opus":"Transformation through fire, treating being burned down and reduced to ash as the very thing that lets a new self break through the darkness within.","sonnet":"Transformation through burning: the speaker, contaminated but refusing the cure, is set ablaze and finally able to breathe, watching the fire break the shadows inside and reduce past mistakes to ash from which they alone remain.","src":"haiku"},
"missile-girl-scoot~kkp-connections":{"haiku":"Sacrifice and devotion mask betrayal and abandonment. Love becomes devastation when the beloved denies their child.","opus":"An Indonesian lament of brutal betrayal, having left family behind for love only to be abandoned while heavily pregnant by a man who won't even acknowledge the child; devotion repaid with cruelty.","sonnet":"A woman abandoned while heavily pregnant: she gave up her family for love, sacrificed everything for the man, and he left her before the baby arrived, refusing even to acknowledge the child as his.","src":"haiku"},
"missio~gimmeakiss":{"haiku":"Untamed hedonism and refusal to conform drive the speaker toward transgressive pleasure and kinetic expression.","opus":"A defiant stoner-loner shrugging off his critics as beyond his control, then flipping the resignation into a dancefloor demand for a kiss. Self-deprecation and hedonism collapse into a single hook.","sonnet":"A loose, hedonistic invitation to dance and lose control — the narrator is a stoner loner who doesn't care what people think, just wants to move, get a kiss, and take another hit.","src":"haiku"},
"missio~say-something":{"haiku":"The song demands accountability and voice against systemic indifference. War corrupts; drugs enslave; ordinary people must speak truth, not waste time investigating.","opus":"A tense demand that someone with authority finally break their silence, the singer swinging between self-doubt and defiance while indicting generals and drug policy and the machinery that grinds ordinary people down. He calls both himself and his abuser complicit. MISSIO, the Austin electronic duo, work in this brooding, beat-driven confessional register where private fragility collides with blunt political anger.","sonnet":"A dispersed refusal — the speaker is one among billions, convinced war was designed to ruin people, that the system is a house fire you can't piss out, and exhausted by investigations that lead nowhere. The repeated demand for someone to say something is addressed to no one in particular and everyone at once, as much cry as command.","src":"haiku"},
"missy-higgins~scar":{"haiku":"Refusing to be reshaped by manipulative lovers, reclaiming autonomy and authentic identity.","opus":"Growing up as a series of people who tried to cut and blunt you to fit their shape, and the hard-won refusal to be reshaped. She asks to keep the scar as a reminder never to disappear into someone else's mold.","sonnet":"Two successive people try to reshape the speaker — one scrubbing her clean, one unstitching her — and she barely escapes each time; the song is a hard-won refusal to be filed down to fit someone else's shape, and a wish to carry the mark of that resistance.","src":"haiku"},
"mister-misery~the-blood-waltz":{"haiku":"Descent into darkness and death's embrace offers escape from loneliness and pain, where blood and emptiness merge eternally.","opus":"Theatrical horror-glam wallowing in blackness and self-harm, the narrator bound for the graveyard, questioning whether anything remains worth living or dying for. Pleas to have tears wiped and blood licked from his arms dress despair in gothic ceremony. The song circles suicidal ideation as a macabre waltz, longing for tenderness even as it flirts with the other side.","sonnet":"A descent into suicidal darkness where reality is a twisted game and the speaker asks only whether someone would wipe their tears or lick the blood from their arms; the blood waltz plays forever in a blackness where death is already the destination.","src":"haiku"},
"mister-misery~the-blood-waltz-orchestral-version":{"opus":"A gothic theatrical waltz steeped in death, blood, and grim longing, the narrator graveyard-bound and questioning whether anything remains worth living for. Reality is named a twisted game rather than a fairytale, and macabre pleas for tears and blood to be tended turn despair into ghoulish, ceremonial romance.","sonnet":"A suicidal ideation song that asks for intimate companionship in death — the narrator is drawn to the grave, cannot feel or see through the blackness, and asks someone to wipe their tears, catch them as they fall, and lick the blood from their self-inflicted wounds before the blood waltz plays forever.","src":"sonnet"},
"misterer~ignorance":{"haiku":"Wilful blindness sustains false comfort as society builds itself on denial; freedom remains impossible within systemic numbness.","opus":"A rebuke of comfortable Western privilege, aimed at those who consider themselves specially favored and coast through shallow, untroubled lives by muting their conscience and letting others suffer to prop them up. It closes by demanding, plainly, who actually bears the cost of that supposed liberty.","sonnet":"Humanity builds its civilisation on wilful ignorance, killing the gods inside itself to make room for new masters, choosing hollow ease and machine dependency over genuine freedom, while the real cost of that freedom — who suffers — goes deliberately unasked.","src":"haiku"},
"miyu-tomita~deep-in-abyss-anime-intro-version":{"haiku":"Light burns brightest plunging into darkness; fear ignites not paralyzes, driving deeper into mystery. The compass points toward abyss; obsession overcomes rational warning, each descent confirming the pull toward truth.","opus":"The opening theme to Made in Abyss, sung in character, capturing the pull of a bottomless chasm that draws everyone downward. Eyes blaze brighter than any lamp as seekers chase truth into a trap-mouthed void, willing to lose everything for an answer. Even if the longing is a curse, the heartbeat is real, yearning that refuses to stop descending.","sonnet":"Descent into the Abyss as irresistible compulsion: a compass that always points toward darkness, the void that beckons with silence even when there is nothing more to find, and the admission that even knowing the search might be cursed, the longing — \"if only the answer could be found here\" — is real and will not stop.","src":"haiku"},
"mk-gee~you":{"haiku":"Uncertainty and transformation prevent genuine connection as two people question their compatibility while remaining entangled in each other's gravity.","opus":"A hazy plea to a partner stuck in her own head, offering one night to set everything aside and asking who really holds power over her mind.","sonnet":"The speaker addresses someone kept fenced off by fear or another person's control, asking whether they are looking up, whether they want to go wide — offering one night of rest from whatever weight pins them, shape-shifting to meet them while admitting he can't deny what he feels.","src":"haiku"},
"moby-the-void-pacific-choir~are-you-lost-in-the-world-like-me":{"haiku":"Alienation in a failing system, searching for meaning and connection amid collapse.","opus":"A dystopian cry about alienation in a failed, screen-blinded world, reaching for anyone who feels equally lost. Paired with its animated smartphone-zombie video, it's a plea for connection amid collapse.","sonnet":"A collective cry of alienation directed at anyone else who feels that systems have failed and the world no longer makes sense — the speaker searches for shared disorientation and a small flame that might burn against a dying sun.","src":"haiku"},
"moby~lift-me-up":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and estrangement demand transcendence; the speaker begs to be elevated from the weight of ordinariness.","opus":"Moby's mid-2000s rush of dread and exhaustion, plain speech and burnout stitched into a plea for elevation. The wreaching upward is less faith than the drained wish to feel anything against the boredom and cold.","sonnet":"A worn-down speaker, strung out and feeling old, cries out for elevation from exhaustion and the weight of plain, grinding existence. The repeated plea to be lifted higher is directed at an unnamed presence — possibly divine — while the background chant of boredom undercuts any easy transcendence.","src":"haiku"},
"moby~porcelain":{"haiku":"Jealousy and deception drive the speaker toward breakdown; regret shadows departure as the mind fractures into kaleidoscopic chaos.","opus":"The quiet dread of ending a relationship you know is over, half in and half out. Moby renders the goodbye as a dream-state of guilt and jealousy, admitting he never meant harm even as he leaves, the fragility of the title in every hushed line.","sonnet":"A restless, guilt-laden speaker caught between waking and dreaming admits to hurting and deceiving someone they love, offering a goodbye while suspecting the other person never truly wanted them either.","src":"haiku"},
"modern-error~error-of-the-world":{"haiku":"Humanity is the world's error deserving suffering; broken and poisoned, the speaker searches for future signs while letting go of lies and spiraling into decline.","opus":"A cry of ecological and spiritual self-loathing, humanity cast as a flaw the planet would be better without, where the only redemption on offer is surrender, grief, and giving the pain back to the earth.","sonnet":"A song about digital-age dissociation — humanity has connected itself through technology while becoming disconnected from love and self, and the narrator, overwhelmed by the contradiction, keeps reaching for release and surrender, asking the earth to absorb what can no longer be held.","src":"haiku"},
"modern-life-is-war~revival-fires":{"opus":"Rebuilding a life atop burned, wasted land, where after ruin every meal and every day has to be won back, the narrator invoking immigrant bloodlines and time itself as the only real conqueror, choosing to confront the blazing now and the harvest still to ripen.","sonnet":"A community reckoning with scorched earth — after something devastating has reduced everything to ash, the people left behind must earn each day from scratch, face old resentments and immigrant bloodlines, and decide whether they can forgive themselves and start again.","src":"sonnet"},
"modest-mouse~float-on":{"haiku":"Petty disasters and absurdities punctuate ordinary life, yet optimism persists as a deliberate choice to accept and move forward.","opus":"An almost willed optimism against a run of small disasters and firings, the band's usual dread turned into a mantra you repeat until it holds. It lands because the cheer is a decision, not a given.","sonnet":"A series of petty disasters — car accidents, scams, job losses — are greeted with cheerful fatalism, the narrator insisting that good news always finds its way through and that everyone, regardless of weight, will float. Anxiety is dissolved not by resolution but by stubborn optimism.","src":"haiku"},
"modest-mouse~the-cold-part":{"haiku":"Resigned farewell to a bleak, desolate place, surrendering the weight of isolation.","opus":"A weary farewell to a frozen, salt-soaked place, the Antarctica line making desolation both literal and comic. It's the sound of quitting somewhere barren without quite knowing where you're headed, resignation as release.","sonnet":"A brief, spare farewell to a cold and desolate place — Antarctica as both literal landscape and emotional state — from someone who stepped down from an impossible position and is now simply, quietly leaving.","src":"haiku"},
"modestep~inside-a-crowd":{"haiku":"Isolation persists within crowds as the speaker loses ground to internal collapse and external alienation. Buried in masses, the speaker suffocates and fragments without escape.","opus":"A person rotting inside and numbed by drugs stares down their own death while feeling utterly lost even when surrounded by people. The crowd only deepens the isolation as everything gets too loud.","sonnet":"Alienation and dissociation in a noisy, self-destructing life — the speaker feels rotten inside and out, crawling down a window, crossing a limbo, lost and invisible inside a crowd despite the world pressing in on all sides.","src":"haiku"},
"modestep~machines":{"haiku":"People are machines programmed to obey, their circuits burned to hide truth. They repeat routines like screens hiding what is really happening.","opus":"People are stripped down to obedient machinery, dosed with sweet lies, rebooted to bury the truth and pushed to give up what they're genuinely good at, wondering why they keep obeying commands that set them running. Modestep, the London electronic-rock act, drives this dystopian warning with dubstep-heavy force, casting conformity as a scripted illusion.","sonnet":"People are conditioned like malfunctioning systems — medicated, rebooted, sent back into service whenever they deviate. The song presses on the horror of a life reduced to a compliant routine: circuits burned to suppress truth, every human impulse smoothed into a manageable, repeatable process.","src":"haiku"},
"modestep~seams":{"haiku":"A relationship splits apart as both partners continue to be themselves. They wear thin like fabric burned at the edges, though they call each other when breaking.","opus":"A relationship fraying at burned edges splits at the seams, one partner insisting on being who they want while the other offers up silence and anger on demand. Modestep pair the bruised imagery with their signature electronic-rock drive, the repeated confession of coming apart landing between accusation and resignation as worn threads reach the bone.","sonnet":"A relationship held together by habit and mutual performance is quietly disintegrating, the edges fraying down to bone. One person reaches out to be called in moments of pain or rage, but even that offered presence cannot hold what was always splitting — the seams give way regardless of good intentions.","src":"haiku"},
"modwheelmood~frequency":{"haiku":"Someone hears only one person's frequency—a signal that represents all their focus; despite knowing they've been used, they accept this obsessive limitation.","opus":"A numbly resigned portrait of fixation on one person as the sole signal that reaches him, glancing at the clock over and over, knowing he is being used and no longer bothered by it before trailing into a shrugged dismissal. Modwheelmood, the project featuring Nine Inch Nails guitarist Alessandro Cortini, cushion the one-sided obsession in soft, glitchy melancholy rather than drama.","sonnet":"A person tuned entirely to someone else's signal — checking compulsively, unable to switch off, recognizing they've been used and finding they don't mind. The song is small and honest about obsession: the frequency of another person has simply become the only station the speaker can receive.","src":"haiku"},
"modwheelmood~kentish":{"haiku":"Slow withdrawal into nothingness despite movement; nothing registers or changes despite effort, creating resigned repetition.","opus":"A narrator recedes into repetition, closing his eyes to see nothing, walking miles yet staying put, hearing warnings and answers that mean nothing. Modwheelmood, the electronic project involving Nine Inch Nails' Alessandro Cortini, sculpt a hypnotic loop of Sunday-morning stasis, the minimal words circling an unshakable emptiness.","sonnet":"A Sunday morning drift through dissociation — walking miles that change nothing, closing eyes to see more clearly, repeating words that arrive already emptied. The refrain of nothing and really is not despair so much as a settled, almost meditative blankness.","src":"haiku"},
"modwheelmood~mega-hurt":{"haiku":"One person hears only another's frequency amid their emotional changes; the speaker urges self-reflection and acceptance of necessary transformation, though shame accompanies the struggle.","opus":"The same fixation from a caring distance: the narrator hears only one frequency, someone struggling through difficult but necessary changes, and gently insists it's alright even as they fade away, calling it a shame. The looping refrain enacts the very obsession it describes. Modwheelmood, tied to Nine Inch Nails guitarist Alessandro Cortini, favor exactly this warm, hypnotic electronic ache over aggression.","sonnet":"A gentle elegy for someone going through a necessary but painful transformation — the speaker has been listening on the same frequency and watches the other person fade, knowing the changes are right even as the distance grows. The shame is in the wasted time, the remedies untried, the tragedy of being old enough to know better and still not making it.","src":"haiku"},
"modwheelmood~scared-one":{"haiku":"A person claims fearlessness yet admits to weakness and requests space to breathe; they seek peace away from others' demands.","opus":"A brittle push-and-pull between bravado and collapse, claiming to fear nothing while admitting weakness in the same breath, snapping at everyone to clear his space and let him breathe. It's the sound of someone cornered and out of patience with the world and himself. Modwheelmood, the electronic project linked to Nine Inch Nails' Alessandro Cortini, keep the delivery hushed and frayed rather than explosive.","sonnet":"Bravado collapsing in real time — the opening claim of fearlessness immediately gives way to admissions of weakness and a desperate need for space and air. Short, fragmented, it reads as someone talking themselves through a moment of overwhelm, asking the world to back off just long enough to remember who they were trying to be.","src":"haiku"},
"modwheelmood~steering-wheel":{"haiku":"A speaker urges others to linger and remain punctual, invoking a steering wheel as guide or metaphor for navigating seasonal dissolution and remembrance.","opus":"A hazy, looping reverie about lingering and running behind, one voice coaxing another to slow down and stay, addressing a car's wheel as though it stored the memory of a fading warm season, autumn arriving and certain days that leave you undone. The sense keeps slipping like a half-remembered dream. Modwheelmood, featuring Nine Inch Nails' Alessandro Cortini, specialize in exactly this soft, disoriented drift.","src":"haiku"},
"modwheelmood~too-late-two-thousand":{"haiku":"A person wastes and hurts others without meaning to, growing tired and nervous; when they leave it will be too late, and they admit they didn't mean the harm caused.","opus":"A narrator getting wasted and pretentious circles toward insanity and obsession, insisting the hurt was unintentional while admitting that by the time they leave it will be too late. Modwheelmood, Rachel Bolan and Alessandro Cortini's electronic project, wraps the confession of self-sabotage and near-misses in brooding, downcast synth textures.","sonnet":"Carried forward by nervous, compulsive momentum, a person watches chances slip past and realizes the harm left in their wake. The refrain of going away before it is too late loops back on itself: departure as both escape and acknowledgment that what was taken cannot be returned, only regretted.","src":"haiku"},
"moe-shop~notice":{"haiku":"A shy, emotionally fragile student fixates on an older person, oscillating between hope for recognition and despair at their own invisibility; rumors and miscommunication compound the anxiety. Identity feels unstable and unformed.","opus":"Moe Shop's bright French-house-meets-J-pop palette frames a schoolgirl's aching crush, her thoughts glitching like connection errors and failed replies. She fixates on a senpai who may already have a sweetie, obsesses over her shortened skirt, and even brushes dark thoughts. Rumor, jealousy and unformed identity swirl as she begs, over and over, simply to be noticed.","sonnet":"A Japanese schoolgirl navigates the chaos of unrequited love — communication failures, identity still forming, the sting of learning her senpai already has someone else. The revelation lands close to despair, and she pleads to be seen just once before the spell of her own wishful thinking dissolves entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"mogwai~glasgow-mega-snake":{"opus":"Mogwai, the Glasgow post-rock band known for wordless volume, break their usual silence here with an offstage aside rather than a song. A friend informs the crowd that it is Albert Hofmann's hundredth birthday, the chemist who synthesized LSD, and toasts him warmly. The affectionate joke is all there is, a live moment of acknowledgment standing where a lyric would otherwise be.","sonnet":"A brief, affectionate spoken tribute delivered live — the band's friend informs the crowd that it is Albert Hofmann's 100th birthday, the inventor of LSD, and toasts him warmly; there is no song in the traditional sense, just a joking, affectionate moment of acknowledgment.","src":"sonnet"},
"mogwai~party-in-the-dark":{"haiku":"A meditation on universal suffering and human struggle, describing a state of being silently torn from one's existence, remaining perpetually hungry, time-less, and directionally lost in a world full of pain.","opus":"One of Mogwai's rare vocal tracks, and a deliberately hazy one. The Scottish post-rock group survey distant human pain and small technological wonders against exhausted hearts and a war no one is fighting anymore, the narrator asking for gentleness while drifting, restless and unresolved.","sonnet":"Observing everything from a place of exhaustion — the speaker sees all the suffering and spectacle, feels the war has already been lost before the ghosts even arrive, and remains directionless, hungry for peace of mind but too depleted to act. The repetition circles the same state without resolution, innocent and impatient at once.","src":"haiku"},
"molybaron~something-for-the-pain":{"haiku":"Self-inflicted pain replaces emotional numbness; wounds become the only proof of feeling when betrayal erases connection.","opus":"A person destroyed by betrayal from those they loved reaches for numbing relief, cutting into a vein as their conclusion after seeing only darkness and delusion. The plea for something for the pain is a slide toward self-harm and giving up.","sonnet":"An isolated, betrayed figure consumed by shame, self-harm, and despair asks for something — anything — to numb the pain as they cut into a vein, having seen darkness, broken by delusions, reaching a conclusion that feels both inevitable and final.","src":"haiku"},
"moments~grey":{"opus":"A defiant refusal to fade into dull mediocrity, the narrator choosing forward motion and self-determination. Unafraid to walk alone, he claims his own path and journey with nothing able to stop him. Its energy is pure resolve, an insistence on growth, momentum, and living fully rather than dimming to grey.","sonnet":"A brief, blunt declaration of chosen momentum over stagnation — the singer refuses to fade into passivity and commits to forward motion on their own terms, framing isolation on the path as an acceptable price for autonomy.","src":"sonnet"},
"mono-no-aware~a-q0hgxd":{"haiku":"A playful tongue-twister celebration of repetitive and ridiculous word patterns without deeper meaning.","opus":"Mono No Aware lean toward playful, literate Japanese art-rock, and here they build an entire track from tongue-twisters and phonetic wordplay. Raw wheat, raw rice, frogs hopping, a monk painting sharks on a screen, string together into a breathless obstacle course of language. The band's delight is unabashedly formal, celebrating the mouth struggling to keep up with itself, meaning set aside for the pure joy of sound.","sonnet":"A song built entirely from Japanese tongue-twisters and wordplay, stringing together classic rapid-fire phonetic challenges — raw wheat, raw rice, raw eggs; frogs hopping; chickens in a yard; a monk painting Jaws on a screen — into a breathless celebration of language as obstacle course and game. The emotional core is pure playfulness: the joy of the mouth struggling to keep up with itself.","src":"haiku"},
"mons-vi~green-light-go":{"haiku":"A stranger knows too much; desire and mystery entangle a driver heading home as dawn approaches.","opus":"A late-night encounter with a woman who seems to know him better than he knows her, told as a hazy plea to just get home before the mystery turns dangerous; the pull of a stranger who plays a game she claims not to care about.","sonnet":"Someone wanting to leave a night out early while fascinated by a mysterious woman — she knows things she should not, plays games she claims not to care about, and the narrator can't quite place her.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~a-w-o-l":{"haiku":"Invisibility, abandonment, and internal collapse—a fallen angel desperate to escape but trapped, knowing there's no way out and no one will notice or acknowledge the struggle.","opus":"A djent howl from someone convinced no one notices or hears him, paying the cost of an inability to release his grip while every dawn opens into fresh torment. Under the plea for rescue runs a bitter self-directed fury that twists the urge to flee into a wish to be obliterated entirely.","sonnet":"A person in acute psychological crisis — invisible, emotionally hemorrhaging, the pain internal and unshowable — alternately begging for someone to pull them out and erupting in nihilistic rage, the song swinging between desperate plea and the wish to destroy everything including themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~animus":{"haiku":"A lost person searches for clarity and purpose while haunted by their past and desensitized by compromise. They eventually accept their burden and let the guiding flames justify their existence.","opus":"Djent band Monuments chart a search for meaning as a voyage across turbulent inner seas, the narrator lost and lured by temptation toward something dangerous below. He sheds his mask, resists pressure to give in, and overreaches until near-collapse, finally arriving at self-acceptance where every weight he carries feels earned.","sonnet":"A figure who has drifted without direction follows an inner flame toward self-knowledge — shedding the masks worn to avoid compromise, accepting accumulated burdens as the price of becoming whole. The arc moves from purposeless wandering through darkness to a hard-won acceptance of one's own nature and calling.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~arch-essence-feat-spencer-sotelo":{"haiku":"Shame and deceit corrode the self; each lie compounds, trapping the speaker in loops of regret.","opus":"Impostor syndrome pushed to a spiritual crisis: a man convinced his whole self is a con, terrified that if anyone believed him it would only prove he fooled them. The djent grandeur amplifies a very small, private question about whether he is worth anything at all.","sonnet":"Chronic self-deception and impostor syndrome — the narrator believes their successes are frauds, their identity a lie, their worth nothing; they hide, repeat accusations against themselves, and carry on with cards pressed to their chest, unable to believe they deserve to exist.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~cardinal-red-feat-mick-gordon":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic judgment of a hypocritical society that destroys nature and avoids atonement, bringing death and darkness to the world.","opus":"A vengeful presence once written off as make-believe comes back to prove it exists, tearing down all a hypocrite treasures and leaving a country of willful stupidity to decay in unbroken darkness. Monuments, the British djent band, bring in Doom composer Mick Gordon for extra menace as the darkness names itself unavoidable and its wounds beyond healing.","sonnet":"A force that was dismissed and denied speaks its return as reckoning — the hypocrisies of those who preach without atoning are met with an inexorable, consuming darkness. Cardinals and institutions built on the suffering of others find that the dark they manufactured has become autonomous and cannot be switched off.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~collapse":{"haiku":"Human ambition claims godlike power, but domination collapses into dust when authority fades.","opus":"Cosmic self-mythology about creation and power moving through body, mind and spirit, warning that whoever seizes power is really the one being commanded by it. Grand progressive-metal metaphysics circling the fear of what's left when strength gives out and everything returns to dust.","sonnet":"A meditation on humanity's relationship to creative and destructive power — the same force that drives creation also destroys, warnings are ignored, and when the power fades the question of whether anything essential survives remains open, pointing toward collective spiritual collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~deadnest":{"haiku":"A person drowning in their former home, poisoning themselves with toxic habits and denial, unable to escape despite knowing the wreckage around them.","opus":"A person feels trapped and drowning in a home turned toxic, poisoning himself to cope, torn over whether to break blood ties, and ultimately admitting he is responsible for the wounds inflicted.","sonnet":"A person describes the numbing, corrosive effect of living in a toxic and broken environment — self-medicating to suppress pain, denying the damage, and slowly losing any sense of direction or self.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~false-providence-feat-mick-gordon":{"haiku":"False spiritual devotion enslaves the soul; the speaker questions why they surrendered free will to hollow religious performance.","opus":"A hollowed-out spirit surrenders its will and bleeds itself dry to satisfy another, praying to hollow guardians while identity dissolves and existence feels stripped of purpose. Monuments, again teaming with Mick Gordon, encase that spiritual emptiness in djent heaviness, the narrator aching for something real beyond the pretense yet clinging on for a salvation that may never arrive.","sonnet":"A soul empties itself entirely to satisfy a devotion that may be divine or delusional, surrendering identity and blood to something beyond the self. The surface of prayer and apparition slowly cracks to reveal a deeper hunger — a yearning to create and mean something that exists well before the religious scaffold.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~hollow-king":{"haiku":"A critique of blind worship and false leadership, exposing how people die for rulers they should question rather than obey.","opus":"A confrontation with a bleeding false god and the followers who bow and die for him, the narrator refusing to accept their blind sacrifice and vowing to remake a broken world, hand near the trigger, insisting the state will fall. It's revolt against hollow authority. Monuments, the British djent band, drive this defiance with intricate, syncopated riffing and soaring choruses that pit rage against a plea for change.","sonnet":"A god who bleeds confronted by the loyalty it has manufactured — people dying and bowing to something that is itself broken, empty of the authority it commands. The song demands transformation, an imagining of the world again, while acknowledging that the hollow king's subjects cannot move and the speaker suspects something is wrong with everyone involved.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~jukai":{"haiku":"A song exploring suicidal ideation and the moment of decision to end pain, with spiraling thoughts and the struggle between the impulse to stay and the compulsion to leave.","opus":"Titled after the Japanese forest notorious for suicides, the song escorts someone to the edge and past it, with glimmering apparitions offered as a last chance at rescue. Remorse comes only after the decisive step is taken, the reversal arriving when there is no undoing it.","sonnet":"Named after the Japanese suicide forest, the song narrates someone at the brink — taking the walk alone, telling themselves no one suffers if they end the pain — before a last-second reversal when it is almost too late, the consequences already irreversible.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~lavos":{"haiku":"The speaker watches civilizations collapse into suffering, trapped by prophecy knowing loved ones will perish, yet refusing to abandon them despite their powerlessness to save anyone.","opus":"A despairing reckoning as the narrator watches structures fall and false prophets decay, every sacrifice revealed as pointless, yet he refuses to let a loved one perish even with doom looming. Everyone ends hollowed out, trapped and driven by sorrow and fury. Monuments, the UK djent band, deliver this bleak arc through tangled rhythms, the title nodding to a video-game apocalypse.","sonnet":"Apocalypse from the inside of the cage — walls crumbling, lies echoing through halls, prophets rotting, and one speaker who refuses to watch someone die even as hope slowly chokes in a garden of violence. The end strips away rage and pride alike, leaving only grief and a recognition that the survivors are as empty as the fallen.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~leviathan":{"haiku":"Evil guides a person's choices, corrupting their will; the speaker feels helpless, tasting poison and unable to change the person's destructive trajectory.","opus":"The British djent band Monuments cast a failing relationship as something driven by an outside malevolence, the singer sensing a sinister force steering his partner and insisting neither of them can seize the reins. Amid images of drinking and going under, he decides to release it, reasoning that the only thing worth despising is the contempt she keeps showing. He feels how she shifts and decides, mourning a life grown too airless to survive.","sonnet":"Watching someone governed by a force they cannot name or resist, the narrator swings between detachment and anguish. Hate feeds only on itself; love offers no clear object. The only honest response is release — letting go of the impulse to control or save someone whose trajectory something darker than free will is steering.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~makeshift-harmony":{"haiku":"A lover dances with seduction and false promises, mistaking beauty for truth until the cycle breaks.","opus":"Knowingly falling for someone poisonous, choosing the seduction over the truth that they don't belong together. The dance imagery makes complicity feel graceful even as the singer admits he wanted a beautiful liar to blame for his own surrender.","sonnet":"About being hopelessly drawn to someone you know is lying to you and is wrong for you — the narrator keeps dancing with the other person's deceptions, mistakes a predator for something beautiful, and acknowledges the false harmony they're constructing won't hold; but he keeps taking the chance anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~no-one-will-teach-you-feat-neema-askari":{"haiku":"Blindness breeds greed; without guidance, we destroy each other and mistake suffering for wisdom.","opus":"A bleak sermon on history repeating, insisting that whatever climbs to dominance is fated to collapse, and that human vanity, appetite and chosen blindness keep us wrecking one another. The hook lands as a charge that nobody will simply hand you the clarity you refuse to work for.","sonnet":"A philosophical indictment of humanity's ego-driven blindness — systems rise and fall, people drift through life without guidance or self-knowledge, and no one will teach you to see past your own conceits. The equilibrium the narrator seeks is spiritual and moral, not just personal.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~opiate":{"haiku":"Addiction to another mirrors addiction to self-destruction, breaking the habit proves impossible even as clarity reveals its cost.","opus":"A destructive relationship rendered as addiction, something the speaker keeps vowing to quit while nothing else measures up. As the rope tightens and everything burns, he admits he hasn't the strength to break the habit, enslaved to it even as it kills him.","sonnet":"A song about addiction framed as a toxic relationship — the speaker knows the thing they are hooked on is killing them, has sworn off it repeatedly and failed, and is now surrendering to the violence and silence it leaves behind.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~origin-of-escape":{"haiku":"Fugitives run unseen through the night after awakening fire from their own ashes, determined to remain hidden while paying for past denials and corruption.","opus":"A tense flight in which rebels slip away by night and let their hunters assume the blaze destroyed them. Monuments turn it into a story of rebirth and resistance, survivors rising renewed from what was meant to end them, determined to stay hidden and outlast those who would enslave them.","sonnet":"About a group of rebels staying invisible and running from a system that would destroy them if it knew they were still alive, carrying a fire made from their own ashes and knowing that the time for retribution will eventually come.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~somnus":{"haiku":"Nightmares recur relentlessly; the haunted mind cannot escape its own dark patterns even awake.","opus":"Sleep as a trap rather than rest, where regret and nightmare drag the mind back under every night. Named for the god of sleep, it's really about insomnia born of guilt, the dread of closing your eyes and drowning in your own head.","sonnet":"About chronic nightmares and sleep paralysis consuming a person — the narrator cannot escape the dark energy that pulls them back into terror each night, and even waking doesn't fully free them. The song is about being imprisoned by your own sleeping mind, with rest as another form of suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~the-alchemist":{"haiku":"A person questions identity and divine purpose while resisting those who would crush their will, seeking transcendence through inner light and chaos.","opus":"A prog-metal meditation on transcending a chaotic world of lies by turning inward and becoming the tide of change. It preaches standing alone against the skeptics who'd wither your faith, transformation as an act of will.","sonnet":"A search for self-knowledge against a world that erodes it — the narrator frames themselves as the brightest light in creation, resisting the skeptics and the forces that would wither them down to nothing, insisting that everything is already written and they alone can change it.","src":"haiku"},
"monuments~the-cimmerian":{"haiku":"Mechanical imprisonment devours the spirit; trapped in shadow and internal war, the speaker seeks escape through acceptance of decay.","opus":"A djent epic of a soul stranded between machine-imprisonment and the afterlife, pieced together by numb voices and shackled by a war within. Death is the only mercy as the spirit tears toward escape; the late turn reaches for redemption, imagining the hatred rinsed out of him so he can return to himself.","sonnet":"A soul trapped between mechanical existence and the afterlife — 'shrouded in darkness, death is my only saving grace' — narrates internal war, spiritual imprisonment, and the desperate longing for waves to wash away hatred and allow navigation back to the self; the final turn toward self-recovery arrives tentatively against a backdrop of eternal decay.","src":"haiku"},
"moodring~anywhere-but-here":{"haiku":"Escapism becomes necessity as dissatisfaction corrodes any anchor; the speaker spirals inward while rejecting the world's gravity.","opus":"A codependent, mutually corrosive relationship where two people feed each other's self-destruction and neither can leave. The narrator is grotesquely attached, sewn to his own ego, trading blame while both spiral down the same drain.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship song where the speaker has absorbed all blame and punishment from a person who leeches off everyone around them and never commits to leaving — told to get out, the speaker both internalizes the accusation and recognizes the other person's parasitic nature, spiraling in a feedback loop of pain.","src":"haiku","fable":"A codependent standoff dressed up as a breakup, where the order to leave and the refusal to leave turn out to be the same voice. The narrator wears blame like armor and takes the wound on purpose, because a shared ruin is the last reliable proof the two are still fused to each other; the corrosion isn't the relationship failing but the relationship working exactly as designed, each partner a chemical the other's body has learned to need in bigger doses. Moodring's nu-gaze haze bleeding through the post-hardcore aggression does the same work, wrapping mutual destruction in something almost tender, so when the chorus repeats the command to be anywhere but here it stops sounding like an exit and starts sounding like a groove worn too deep to climb out of — a fantasy of escape recited so many times it becomes the reason to stay.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","freedom & escape"],"means":"shared ruin as the last proof two people are still fused","built":"the escape command repeated into a groove too deep to climb out of"},
"moodring~black-wave":{"haiku":"Possession and violation spiral through abusive entanglement where the speaker violates and consumes, dragging the target deeper into darkness. Skin and identity blur together as predator and prey lose distinction.","opus":"A violent, obsessive relationship where hurting and being hurt bleed together into a compulsion to consume the other person entirely, hollowing them out to make a home. The narrator drowns in the black wave of their own destructive appetite, unable to stop.","sonnet":"A violent, parasitic obsession told from the perspective of someone who wants to hollow out and inhabit another — crawling inside them, wearing their skin, sawing them off, lost in a \"black wave\" of consuming darkness that won't stop until the other is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"moodring~constrict":{"haiku":"A strained relationship where one person demands honesty while the other withdraws, trapped in a cycle of emotional suffocation and unmet needs that feels inescapable.","opus":"A song about a stuck, suffocating relationship in which the narrator begs the other person to speak honestly and stop faking reassurance, admitting they keep being pulled back and constricted by someone they can't leave.","sonnet":"One person pushes another to be honest about their emotional state — to stop suppressing feelings and faking that everything is fine — while the speaker themselves admits to being pulled back into a suffocating dynamic they can't seem to escape.","src":"haiku"},
"moodring~half-life":{"haiku":"Addiction and psychological suffering blur existence into a half-death where sedatives and numbness replace authentic feeling and vitality.","opus":"Living at half-power while numbed by drugs and suicidal thoughts, begging someone to just come out with the cruel thing they obviously think. Choking down hurt and fate alike, the narrator feels there's nothing more to lose or fix.","sonnet":"Someone lost to addiction and suicidal depression describes feeling only half-alive — drugged into numbness, cutting, leaving notes under their skin — begging the person who seems to want them dead to just say it outright rather than letting them dissolve slowly.","src":"haiku"},
"moodring~masochist-machine":{"haiku":"Desire becomes degradation; the speaker surrenders to a cruel other who uses them for satisfaction and keeps them imprisoned through lies.","opus":"A grim erotics of being used, drugged, and hidden away as someone's masochistic instrument, satisfying cruel desires until the heart stops. The refrain about faking exposes how the whole sick machine runs on pretense.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a parasitic sexual relationship with someone who uses them as an instrument for their own sick desires — drugged to keep quiet, degraded on demand — the narrator names the dynamic as masochistic and psychosomatic but cannot escape it.","src":"haiku"},
"moodring~red-light-gossip":{"haiku":"Manipulation and control trap through spectacle; the speaker desperately seeks escape from performance.","opus":"Being trapped by a manipulative partner who thrives on drama and puts on a show for whispering onlookers. The speaker feels nothing but rising tension, longing to let go while the other keeps spinning everything back around.","sonnet":"A toxic dynamic in which one person controls and performs while the other feels trapped — the speaker is so ensnared they can't let go, watching the other person thrive on the drama they create, knowing 'it gets better with time' is a lie they keep being fed.","src":"haiku"},
"moodring~show-me-the-real-you":{"haiku":"Dangerous, passionate obsession; love as a predatory force like cancer. The speaker flirts with harm and begs to see the true self beneath the mask.","opus":"Desire and self-destruction blur into one dare, love likened to a disease the speaker willingly bets everything on. Barely alive in an afternoon-lit bedroom, he goads a lover to strip away pretense even at knifepoint. Moodring's shoegaze-inflected nu-metal revival frames the plea as both erotic surrender and a longing to be undone, intimacy indistinguishable from danger.","sonnet":"A destructive intimacy unfolds between two people, the narrator willingly surrendering to a love that feels like sickness and cuts like a blade. Barely alive, stripped raw, he invites the other to drop all pretence and reveal their truest, most dangerous self — whatever the cost.","src":"haiku"},
"moodring~stfa":{"haiku":"Distance demanded as survival strategy fails when numb sensations replace authentic feeling. Static screams internally while the speaker drowns in facelessness.","opus":"Nu-metal-tinged self-loathing lurching between shoving someone off and needing them close, the title a snarled demand for space. Numb, faithless imagery of vices and reckless self-endangerment sketches a narrator convinced he is worthless noise. Toxic codependency laid bare — the plea for distance really an admission he can't be saved.","sonnet":"Numbness and toxic attachment collapse into a single demand — stay away — as the speaker describes cigarettes like razors, bodies going cold in crashing cars, prayers going unanswered; the self-accusation ('it's all my fault') sits alongside the plea for distance, as if closeness and self-destruction are inseparable.","src":"haiku"},
"moon-tooth~awe-at-all-angles":{"haiku":"Raw vulnerability and resistance to polite social performance. The speaker rejects connection built on shallow politeness, identifying instead as a conduit of primal intensity.","opus":"The narrator rejects every identity others try to assign him, refusing to be anyone's connector, partner, or possession, and instead claims himself as raw, wounded energy crackling with defiance. A stiff dinner date feels like imprisonment while his body stays put and his mind takes flight. Moon Tooth build toward an ecstatic chant celebrating untamed vitality and wonder found in every direction.","sonnet":"A speaker refuses all relational categories — not a bridge between people, not a partner making plans, not the man someone wants him to be. He insists on existing as pure uncontained force, raw and multidirectional, trapped in the rituals of courtship while his actual self ranges freely somewhere overhead.","src":"haiku"},
"moon-tooth~the-conduit":{"haiku":"The speaker declares themselves born as a fierce conduit channeling fire and destruction. They demand gold, loyalty, and will be remembered screaming until death.","opus":"A swaggering claim of destiny sung by a self-styled thief of fire, invoking Prometheus to justify seizing gold, glory, and a throne others will kneel before. Moon Tooth's groove-metal bravado turns ambition into ritual, the narrator naming himself a vessel bound for his own coffin. Defiance and doom braid together as he vows the world will scream his name.","sonnet":"A relentless self-declared force of nature announces his arrival, invoking Promethean fire as a birthright. He dismisses all authority and existing hierarchies, pledging to seize everything for himself and burn down whatever stands in his way until his name is shouted into the ground.","src":"haiku"},
"moon-tooth~trust":{"haiku":"The speaker is haunted by internal ghosts and loss, asking to be released from a situation where they must wait painfully while suffering alone.","opus":"A desperate appeal to be set free by someone clinging to him, the narrator insisting that anyone who glimpsed the horrors haunting him would run as well. Emptied out, having lost the one woman he loved and become a shadow of the son and friend he was, he pleads that continuing like this is impossible.","sonnet":"Someone haunted by inner horrors they can't share has watched the only person they loved disappear, and now feels like a ghost — neither alive nor dead — begging for permission to leave a world that holds nothing for them anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"morcheeba~pareidolia-frazer-ray-remix":{"haiku":"Two lovers ready, asking readiness; vulnerability and desire meet hesitation.","opus":"A remix that strips Morcheeba's trip-hop cool down to a hypnotic come-on, the repeated invitation to lay down and the question of readiness for love. Little more than a sensual loop, it trades narrative for mood, desire distilled to a patient, circling seduction. The pareidolia of the title hints at finding meaning, or a lover, in something barely there.","sonnet":"Two people stand at the edge of romantic commitment, the speaker declaring readiness for love while asking if the other is prepared to surrender to it as well.","src":"haiku"},
"moreru~a-1dmgckv":{"haiku":"Themes of death, mortality, or psychological demise; existential darkness permeates the lyrics.","opus":"Moreru trade in raw, unguarded Japanese confessional rock, and this stream pours out of a sculptor-turned-criminal who torched a bike seat and declared himself dead. Scorned for years, now selling hellscapes, he holds off violence each night while sex, abortion, and the weight of failed parenthood tangle together. The band gives the voice no polish and no exit, wanting freedom, money, and wings all at once.","sonnet":"An intensely raw, semi-confessional stream from a sculptor-turned-criminal who set fire to someone's bike seat and declared themselves dead — scorned for years, now making money from hellscapes, compelled toward violence and barely holding it off each night, tangled with sex, abortion, the weight of failed parenthood, and wanting freedom, money, and wings all at once.","src":"haiku"},
"moreru~a-ks8bhm":{"haiku":"A nihilistic rant on suffering and insignificance—words are garbage, the world is pointless, yet the body demands release from stagnation.","opus":"Moreru's unvarnished delivery suits a narrator convinced his words became nothing, not history, not even a supermarket flyer, just tissues handed out at a station exit. Suicide circles as a botched aerial and a gender assigned at too great a cost. The song's own bitter twist is its structure: it admits it became a song after all, one no one will remember, made by someone who fell and scattered like garbage.","sonnet":"A person at the end of their rope — believing their words never became anything, not history, not even a supermarket flyer, just tissues handed out at a train exit — circles around suicide as a badly executed aerial and a gender assigned to them that already cost too much pain. The song ends by accepting that it became a song after all, but a song no one will remember, written by someone who fell and scattered like garbage.","src":"haiku"},
"morgen~make-u-mine":{"haiku":"Possession and romantic claim, the speaker asserting ownership and exclusivity.","opus":"A bedroom-pop ache for a first love and the childhood innocence tangled up in it, the narrator wanting to reclaim someone the way they once play-acted as kids. Memories of a quiet night together curdle as everything slides from tender to broken, from infatuation to unanswered messages — nostalgia and neediness for the one beautiful disaster he ever authored.","sonnet":"Nostalgia for the uncomplicated closeness of early love — wanting to feel like pretending as a kid, watching eyes and smile and ribs with the precision of someone memorizing before it disappears. The collapse from good to bad is acknowledged, and the only proposed remedy is driving with music screaming until forgetting is possible.","src":"haiku"},
"morphide~panopticon":{"haiku":"Obsession and control warp reality into surveillance; the speaker escapes an all-consuming influence only to realize they remain mentally imprisoned by distorted memory and desire.","opus":"A toxic bond as a surveillance prison: the other person warps reality and watches from inside your head even after they're gone. Freedom means burning the whole thing down and refusing the forced obedience it demanded.","sonnet":"Someone finally escaping a psychologically controlling relationship that warped their reality, burned down everything around them, and made them feel constantly watched and guilty — they are done being part of their captor's surveillance.","src":"haiku"},
"morphide~save-a-lie":{"haiku":"Hesitation breeds decay; the speaker drowns in doubt and deterioration, constructing delusions as reality warps into unreliable perception and inescapable darkness.","opus":"A plea to accept a comforting lie rather than live with the unknown, sinking in doubt while pain and decay set in. The insight that perception itself is a lie becomes permission to shape reality by will before burning out fast.","sonnet":"About paralysis in the face of the unknown — the narrator is sinking in hesitation and self-doubt, aware that they have been wasting time and nurturing sorrow, and desperate to believe that the lie (of hope, of comfort, of a manageable reality) is something worth preserving rather than letting die. The brighter they shine the faster they burn, and they are waiting to go down knowing it is their turn.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~blood-death-hatred":{"haiku":"Devil worship, sacrifice, and demonic subjugation destroy free will; followers lose battle against supernatural temptation and evil power.","opus":"A theatrical rite of Satan-worship, sacrifice and a returning dark lord, the trio of grim words chanted over a beast delivered at a coven's gathering. Australian thrash band Mortal Sin lean hard into occult shock-horror, staging a contest of enticement and surrendered will where giving in to evil dispatches souls to paradise or the pit.","sonnet":"Occult imagery of demonic pacts, sacrificial rites, and the soul sold at a black feast is deployed in thrash shorthand, celebrating the triumph of a returned infernal master. The power fantasy is entirely external — the speaker is a witness to apocalyptic dominion rather than an agent of it.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~blood-of-my-enemies":{"haiku":"Vengeance is pursued without mercy or sympathy, celebrating the violent defeat of enemies and the destruction of those who committed atrocities.","opus":"Australian thrash reveling in apocalyptic slaughter and revenge, an island of death where vengeance tastes sweet, the carnage half real and half the fevered fantasy playing out in the narrator's own head.","sonnet":"A war fantasy glorifying the annihilation of enemies — bodies rotting, battles raging — with particular glee taken in the killing of Christians, presented as righteous revenge without mercy or regret.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~burned-into-your-soul":{"haiku":"Trauma and mind control penetrate deep into someone's consciousness through evil thoughts and killing. Painful memories remain etched permanently, choking like blood in veins.","opus":"A plunge into homicidal madness: memories eating into the mind, unstoppable torment, and a will hijacked by an outside force that marks anyone nearby for death. Mortal Sin track a killer's delusion where he chokes on his own blood and believes he has already slaughtered everyone in his imagination.","sonnet":"Trauma that has permanently scarred the mind — images and thoughts carved in that cannot be removed, bleeding hearts and mind control, with the recurring suggestion that the violence being described may be something the narrator committed and cannot escape.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~deny":{"haiku":"The song catalogs unnamed victims of systemic violence—a homeless man, a prostitute—asking whether we deny the allegations and suffering of society's invisible casualties.","opus":"Australian thrash turning its glare on how society disowns its casualties, the homeless and the addicted, then denies any hand in their deaths. The refrain is an interrogation of collective conscience, indicting the machinery that lets people vanish nameless.","sonnet":"A song about society's disposable victims — a homeless man with no name, a woman who died with needles in her arm — and an accusation that the system that produced them keeps denying responsibility.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~doomed-to-annihilation":{"haiku":"War and annihilation consume soldiers and nations. The song depicts imminent destruction, violence, and the prepared acceptance of collective death.","opus":"Australian thrash on the pointlessness and gore of combat, troops advancing toward guaranteed death beneath banners of race and homeland; the youngest are conditioned to perish for a falsehood, hunting glory they will only receive after they are dead in mud-filled trenches that do nothing but erase them.","sonnet":"A war song that refuses a clean heroic framing: soldiers march toward certain death, the rhetoric of defiance and testimony can't disguise that they're trapped in a lie, and the dead are honored posthumously while the living keep marching.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~down-in-the-pit":{"haiku":"Working-class monotony and dehumanization trap people in 9-to-5 slavery; Friday night is escape—a pit where people exist outside corporate control.","opus":"The grind of soul-killing wage slavery—Monday alarms like bullets, 9-to-5 servitude for 50 bucks a day, 'got you by the balls'—finds its only release in the mosh pit, where the worker defiantly stops caring what the bosses think of him.","sonnet":"A working-class anthem about the grind of factory and office life — every Monday is a bullet, every job is soul-crushing slavery, and the only release is the mosh pit on Friday night where none of that shit matters.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~h":{"haiku":"Nuclear devastation and its aftermath fragment survivors into sheltered masses, praying behind lead doors while remembering the killing that ended a war.","opus":"Hiroshima survived from inside the shelter, thrash reckoning with the atomic bomb's cost. It sits with the trapped survivors and the dying nation, refusing to let the logic of ending a war paper over the mass death, the children crying behind the leaded door.","sonnet":"The song depicts survivors of the Hiroshima atomic bombing sheltering from radiation and death, framing the bombing as both a calculated end to war and a monstrous atrocity that stripped hundreds of thousands of their lives, freedom, and future.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~hatred":{"haiku":"Hatred breeds from need, greed, and planting seeds of prejudice, spiraling into cycles of violence, war, and collective addiction to vengeance.","opus":"A blunt reckoning with cyclical human atrocity, cataloguing genocide and cruelty to argue that hatred is a self-perpetuating addiction, bred anew wherever people feed their greed and plant its seed.","sonnet":"A thrash metal cataloguing of atrocity — slavery, genocide, rape, murder — arguing that hatred is a self-perpetuating addiction bred into civilization, consuming everyone including its victims.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~i-am-immortal":{"haiku":"A vengeful, fearless creature claims immortality, seeks revenge, breaks laws, and battles evil lords in a cosmic war for dominance.","opus":"A duel to the death staged as a bid for eternal life, where killing the rival is the price of freedom from mortality. The narrator fears no foe and mocks repentance, serving a hellish master through a battlefield of slashing swords. Mortal Sin, pioneers of Australian thrash, load the verses with alliterative menace, framing immortality as a curse won through slaughter.","sonnet":"A figure consumed by nihilism and vengeance declares himself beyond mortality and moral law. His blackened heart registers no guilt, recognising existence as a brutal game where survival belongs to the merciless and a dark master governs all fates — including the destruction of those who oppose him.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~into-the-fire":{"haiku":"Sinners and evil spirits burn in fire and face eternal punishment; resurrected bodies are tortured and scarred in apocalyptic judgment.","opus":"Sinners summoned to a chapel for purging burn in flames that promise no return, their scarred bodies raised from death only to face judgment anew. The Australian thrash outfit renders damnation as an endless cycle, survival merely the start of a fresh battle against evil. Ringing bells and howling moans give the scene the theatrical dread native to eighties thrash.","sonnet":"A hellfire purge unfolds as sinners are cast into flames and called to sacrifice at a dark altar. Those who somehow endure the burning emerge scarred and barely whole, only to find the battle against evil has not ended — it has only just begun its next phase.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~kingdom-of-pain":{"haiku":"Crazy people create a world of pain and insanity where emotions drain and hope leaves. The kingdom traps those whose lives have been broken by chaos.","opus":"Thrash portrait of mental collapse and the people trapped inside it, where insanity drains all feeling and there's no exit; society's damaged and unexplained are caged in a kingdom of pain they aren't permitted to leave.","sonnet":"A portrait of mental and emotional entrapment — a person surrounded by the twisted, the irrational, and the violent, unable to escape a psychological kingdom built from collective madness and drained emotions.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~lebanon":{"haiku":"War in Lebanon; brutal combat with graphic depictions of violence, bloodshed, and soldiers' struggle to survive.","opus":"A blunt depiction of the Lebanese conflict, with shelling, gunfire and slaughter as religious factions tear at each other, and helpless children swept up in the carnage. The Australian thrash band renders the real-world atrocity as unrelenting brutality.","sonnet":"The song depicts the Lebanese civil war as a scene of relentless urban combat — machine-gun fire, trenches, religious factions, suicide attacks, and civilian casualties — cataloguing the destruction without taking sides.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~liar":{"haiku":"Public judgment and execution of a deceiver; their lies revealed, the liar faces eternal damnation and hellfire.","opus":"A grim tale of a liar dragged before a merciless judge and a bloodthirsty crowd, driven insane and condemned to be run through with a sword and sent to hell; a moral fable warning of the fatal, damning consequences of deceit.","sonnet":"About a liar being publicly exposed and condemned to death — the crowd turns on them, they're pierced and sentenced, and even in the afterlife they're sent to hell to lie among their own kind.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~mayhemic-destruction":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic horror and satanic destruction raining down on the world. The speaker describes death, devastation, and divine warning turning to judgment.","opus":"Australian thrash pioneers Mortal Sin stack up apocalyptic imagery of fire, corpses and a fleeing populace under the devil's dominion, jeering at religion as the end of the world runs riot. The pounding title chant drives the chaos home. It's pure genre spectacle, existence reduced to a grim countdown toward death.","sonnet":"A puritan apocalypse fantasy delivered at full velocity — total annihilation as both prophecy and celebration. Christian symbols get inverted as the speaker issues threats of ultimate destruction, drawing the listener into a chaotic end-times scenario where survival is beside the point and the only honest response is terror.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~mortal-slaughter":{"haiku":"A demonic tormentor invites victims into a lair of bondage, torture, and rape, framed as a game orchestrated by Satan and religious corruption.","opus":"A predatory demonic narrator baits victims into its den to torment and murder them, gloating over another triumph while sneering at faith as fraud. Mortal Sin's thrash revels in over-the-top horror, its shrieked title chant and imagery of the underworld's threshold building a gleefully sacrilegious dream of unending brutality.","sonnet":"A predatory demonic speaker invites victims into a lair of bondage and terror, relishing each capitulation. The ritual violence is theatrical and absolute, ending with Satan claiming another soul through a game no mortal survives.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~paralysed-by-fear":{"haiku":"Fear paralyzes people into submission, destroying lives through trauma, deception, and the inability to escape psychological torture.","opus":"A portrait of a life crippled by terror, forced to witness their own children destroyed, withering away, powerless to hold off the threats closing in. The core idea is dread as the killer itself, a waking nightmare that warps sleep and freezes a person solid, converting the urge to survive and forgive into total helplessness.","sonnet":"An attempt to articulate the total shutdown that fear produces — living becomes impossible, dreams turn lucid and freezing, and the mind cannot break free from the trap of its own dread.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~psychology-of-death":{"haiku":"A serial killer's mind shows cruelty, bodily destruction, and a chemical imbalance that drives the compulsion to murder, ending in their imprisonment and execution.","opus":"Thrash true-crime, inhabiting a serial killer and the disturbed mind behind the body count. It circles the question of what makes a person a monster, then hands him to a public hungry to watch him die in turn.","sonnet":"A lurid profile of a serial killer — cataloguing stabbings, beheadings, and mass murder as expressions of a chemically broken mind, then ending with the public's bloodthirsty demand for the killer's execution.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~terminal-reward-live":{"haiku":"Post-mortem torment and spiritual judgment await while the narrator questions whether escape through death brings hell or relief.","opus":"A suicide's aftermath in which ending his life to escape the pain only deepens it, leaving him stranded on the astral plane before a locked door and a final judgment. The song follows a soul who finds that death was no reward at all.","sonnet":"A speaker who has already ended their life to escape pain finds themselves in a worse afterlife — judged, doors closed, left to ponder Satan's fall — with no way back and only the growing seeds of an unknowable terminal reward.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~the-infantry-corps":{"haiku":"Young soldiers fight and die for a country that doesn't need them, their innocence and lives sacrificed as governments hide responsibility for the massacre.","opus":"A bitter anti-war lament for child soldiers marching into the mist, dodging for cover and screaming for their mothers, dying without knowing what they fought for while governments and everyone share the blame. Its sarcasm cuts through the mock-cheers for the slaughtered young. Mortal Sin, one of Australia's foremost 80s thrash bands, aim their speed and fury at this kind of blunt political outrage.","sonnet":"Child soldiers sent to a war that doesn't need them and won't remember them — they dodge and scream for their mothers, their innocence spent as currency by governments who will never account for the cost. The song's fury is quiet beneath its marching rhythm: the blame is shared by everyone, and the bodies are still lying there.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~voyage-of-the-disturbed":{"haiku":"Dreamers seek intelligent life while screaming against political oppression and astronomical pressure; they challenge future worlds despite misery and erasure.","opus":"Space exploration curdles into political indictment, a government pushing past limits while denying the human cost of its ambition. The narrator pleads for reckoning as pressure mounts toward the speed of light and history repeats unheeded. Mortal Sin, veterans of Australian thrash, aim their fury outward at power itself, framing the journey as collective madness rather than triumph.","sonnet":"A politically charged lament surveys a society fractured by governmental denial and relentless pressure, where the aspirations people once held are crushed beneath the weight of power. The narrator challenges the audience to witness the damage already done before humanity accelerates toward an uncharted and unstable future.","src":"haiku"},
"mortal-sin~women-in-leather":{"haiku":"A dominant mistress in leather rules through lust and violence, arousing and terrifying as a dreamlike carnage where flesh screams and sin reigns.","opus":"Vintage Australian thrash indulging a lurid dominatrix fantasy, fear and lust cranked into cartoon menace. It's less about any real woman than about the genre's appetite for taboo and theatrical danger.","sonnet":"A BDSM fantasy framed in horror-metal imagery — a dominant woman in leather and metal studs commands total submission, mixing pain and pleasure with theatrical violence, culminating in a ritual of cuts and screaming that reads as consensual power-exchange theater dressed in the language of metal.","src":"haiku"},
"mos-def~ms-fat-booty":{"haiku":"Sexual desire and social performance intertwine as narratives of encounter reveal projection and deception beneath surface attraction.","opus":"A vividly told romance that inverts the usual chase: the smooth-talking narrator pursues, wins, and falls hard for a woman who then vanishes and won't commit, leaving him the one heartbroken. A hip-hop short story about getting outplayed by the very game he thought he ran.","sonnet":"In three escalating scenes — street, party, bedroom — the narrator pursues Sharice, a beautiful and guarded woman who initially rebuffs him, then flirts, then shares months of intimacy before vanishing one morning, leaving him frantic and her already at the club with someone new.","src":"haiku"},
"moses-sumney~lonely-world":{"haiku":"Existential isolation permeates existence despite connection; the void speaks louder than human voice, rendering all relationships secondary to cosmic loneliness.","opus":"Sumney's meditation on isolation as the human baseline, entered without consent at birth. The void becomes an intimate companion because it speaks when no one else will, loneliness dressed up as something almost sacred.","sonnet":"Loneliness is the world's ambient condition — the void speaks more intimately than any person, the empty face behind its veil is universal, and the speaker acknowledges being born into this state without consent, with emptiness as the only thing that truly prevails.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~ace-of-spades":{"haiku":"A seasoned gambler treats life and risk as a game where winning or losing matters equally, living for the play itself. They court danger and death while embracing fate without fear or regret.","opus":"A gambler's creed that prizes the rush of playing over any winnings, embracing danger and even the likelihood of losing. It became Motorhead's signature: a headlong anthem to reckless risk-taking and the refusal to fear death or want a safe, long existence.","sonnet":"The narrator celebrates gambling as a way of life — indifferent to winning or losing, drawn to risk for its own sake, and fully aware that playing for the highest stakes means accepting the possibility of death.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~all-for-you":{"haiku":"Romantic partnership reaches toward the infinite before gravity pulls it earthward; devotion persists through inevitable collapse.","opus":"Lemmy at his most unguarded, a doomed love owned rather than mourned. The hard-man pose cracks openly here, admitting the one wound that stuck and the shame of still speaking her name.","sonnet":"A bittersweet retrospective on a love that burned too hot and crashed — the speaker admits the relationship was doomed from the start but insists every reckless act was genuine devotion, even as the loss still wounds him.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~asylum-choir":{"haiku":"A person feels imprisoned and watched in a corrupt system where blind justice is bought, windowless confinement closes in, and escape is impossible.","opus":"Motorhead's claustrophobic vision of madness and institutional confinement, walls and floor closing in, watched by every eye that ever shut. Beneath it runs a bitter distrust of blind justice and the lies that drive a mind to break.","sonnet":"A portrait of institutional confinement and creeping paranoia — walls, ceilings, and floors press in on the narrator while the eyes of closed minds stay permanently open and watching, trapping him in a place without windows or reason.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~back-at-the-funny-farm":{"haiku":"An institutionalized person experiences surreal terror—hammering heartbeats, twisted springs in mind, strange words, locked doors—trapped in a white room unable to escape the psychiatric facility.","opus":"A darkly comic first-person tour through a psychiatric ward, the narrator's heart pounding, everyone in disguise, an injection going wrong and a straitjacket mistaken for a nice jacket with overlong sleeves. The mock-reassuring chorus insists it's just a holiday. Motorhead, Lemmy's relentless rock'n'roll juggernaut, play this madness with grinning speed, humor and menace inseparable in their gravelly, full-throttle attack.","sonnet":"Waking up inside an institution with no memory of how you got there — the straitjacket sleeves, the injection that went wrong, the locked door you're afraid to test. Played as dark comedy, the song keeps reassuring itself it's just a holiday while every detail confirms the opposite.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~bad-religion":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects harmful religious manipulation and evangelicals, claiming their faith is corrupt while affirming the right to live free of such control.","opus":"A defiant rejection of organized faith in mock-biblical language, the narrator spitting in the eye of both Satan and the preacher, needing no gods or burning crosses and branding evangelists as Nazis and liars destined for the hell they preach. Motorhead, Lemmy's ferocious power trio, deliver this blasphemous sermon at full gallop, turning contempt for hypocrisy into thundering, unrepentant rock'n'roll.","sonnet":"A full-throated rejection of organized religious hypocrisy — calling out evangelists as Judases and Antichrists who corrupt beauty, turn children against parents, and take the name they claim to serve in vain. The speaker needs no gods or crosses and is utterly certain that whatever justice exists will eventually fall on the liars themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~bad-woman":{"haiku":"Aggressive male desire pursues a transgressive woman who ignites reckless passion and defiant sexuality.","opus":"Lust at full throttle for a woman known to be trouble and wanted precisely because of it, a young man fired up and certain she's good enough for him. It's blunt rock-and-roll desire with no pretense of anything deeper.","sonnet":"A straightforward lusty pursuit — the narrator chasing a woman he knows is bad, fired up and almost grown, unable to believe she wants him too — celebrating her badness as exactly what he needs.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~beer-drinkers-hell-raisers":{"haiku":"Working-class hedonism and recklessness merge as identity; whiskey and steel blur freedom and oblivion.","opus":"Motörhead barrel through this ZZ Top cover as a rowdy toast to their own creed, drinkers and troublemakers rolling into town at suppertime looking for a fight and a good time. Honky-tonk swagger meets Lemmy's amphetamine speed, whiskey defended as no sin at all, a three-chord celebration of hedonism with no apology and no brakes.","sonnet":"An invitation from a hard-living narrator to join him at the honky-tonk: whiskey is never a sin, the steel guitar flies, and dancing like a cat on hot tin is the whole philosophy — addressed to a woman he wants to bring along for the ride.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~bomber":{"haiku":"A bomber aircraft shoots and kills indiscriminately every night; aim is true, targets burn, and the mission delivers death from the sky.","opus":"A pounding piece written from the viewpoint of a bomber crew flying nightly raids, glorying in destruction and merciless precision. Beneath the swagger it captures the remorseless machinery of aerial warfare, voiced with cold pride and no trace of guilt as it aims to force the enemy into submission.","sonnet":"A World War II aerial bombing raid told from the crew's perspective — the bomber runs its missions nightly with pride and precision, napalm falling on targets below, the crew indifferent to anything but the next sortie, their violence framed as pure professionalism.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~born-to-raise-hell":{"haiku":"Hedonistic celebration and chaotic energy pour forth from those destined to transgress, break rules, and defy restraint.","opus":"A raucous manifesto for living to make noise, dance and cut loose, daring the timid to prove they care by joining the boogie. Lemmy makes hell-raising a calling, the very thing you were created for.","sonnet":"A loud, unambiguous declaration of purpose: born to raise hell, do it real well, play guitar like ringing a bell — an invitation to dance, boogie, and not be scared, delivered with the confidence of someone who knows exactly what they were created for.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~city-kids":{"haiku":"Street orphans living fast and mean without rules or authority. These city kids operate outside society, self-sufficient and dangerous, feeling no price for their freedom.","opus":"Streetwise outcasts guard their turf with speed-fueled swagger, calling themselves orphans who refuse to be bought cheap. Menace and camaraderie mingle as they warn outsiders not to lead them astray. This predates the classic Motorhead lineup, tracing back to Lemmy's Hawkwind-era writing, yet the sneering self-reliance already carries the band's outlaw stance toward a world that neither knows nor cares.","sonnet":"Street kids on the margins live by their own rules, guarding their territory and trusting no one from outside. Self-sufficient and deliberately reckless, they run on speed and attitude, owing allegiance to nothing and daring the respectable world to come try its luck against them.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~crying-shame":{"haiku":"Transience and anonymity mark existence; human connection withers into mere facial recognition without understanding or permanence.","opus":"A restless drifter's philosophy set to a boogie: take what you can, trust the road, make yourself. Under the swagger runs a recognized face with no name attached, brief connections that never quite land as the shame.","sonnet":"A relentlessly driven figure pushes through a world of strangers and obstacles, encountering someone whose face he recognises but whose name he never knew, lamenting a connection that was never fully made.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~damage-case":{"haiku":"An institutional escapee seeks refuge with a woman, blaming her fear on his desperation and loneliness.","opus":"A likeable disaster fleeing some asylum talks his way toward a stranger's threshold, confessing his own ruin while pleading he only wants a bit of comfort. Motorhead, Lemmy's speed-and-grit outfit, give the self-aware pass its strut, the wreck of a man cheerfully admitting he's scattered and unhinged, all delivered with the raucous, unrepentant charge that defined the band.","sonnet":"A barely-contained wreck of a person turns up on a stranger's doorstep, escaped from some institution, wanting only a little warmth without judgment. The charm offensive is underpinned by self-awareness: he knows exactly how he looks to the world, and the ask is modest even as it comes wrapped in chaos.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~dancing-on-your-grave":{"haiku":"Vengeful triumph exhumes past hierarchy; the narrator celebrates dominion over those who once commanded wealth.","opus":"Pure Lemmy vengeance aimed at a moneyed operator who mistook a bankroll for worth. The narrator outlasts them into ruin, and his refusal to be bought is the real triumph he celebrates over their fall.","sonnet":"A speaker triumphantly addresses a rich, powerful figure who tried and failed to buy or intimidate him — the target has fallen and the speaker, who was never for sale, celebrates by dancing on what remains.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~dead-men-tell-no-tales":{"haiku":"A warning about friendship ending if the recipient continues using drugs; the singer refuses to associate with those who use heroin, leaving them to face consequences alone.","opus":"Motorhead deliver a blunt warning to a friend sinking into heroin, cutting through excuses to say plainly that this drug is a one-way trip. Lemmy, famously tied to amphetamine mythology himself, draws a firm line at the needle, and the grim chorus lands as streetwise pity rather than judgment.","sonnet":"A former friend lost to heroin gets a blunt, unflinching address rather than soft consolation. The speaker refuses to enable or mourn decorously — the warning is clear and final, the friendship already counted as a casualty, and the dead man's silence confirms everything that could not be said while he was living.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~deaf-forever":{"opus":"A pounding battlefield vision of soldiers advancing, falling and sinking into the mire, their hearing destroyed permanently by the roar of combat, every man leveled the same in death with even Valhalla left in doubt past the last breath. Motorhead swing Lemmy's Viking-tinged imagery into relentless propulsion.","sonnet":"A relentless procession of battlefield imagery — sword, shield, bone, steel, Viking dreams, horses screaming, arms severed in mud — in which the dead soldier is deaf forever to the din, and Mother Earth folds all men into the same cold embrace, friends and foes alike.","src":"sonnet"},
"mot-rhead~death-or-glory":{"haiku":"Eternal warriors march through history's bloodbaths bearing judgment and executioner's power without salvation. Glory and death merge as the sole certainties across civilizations.","opus":"A soldier who keeps being reborn into every conflict across the ages, from Napoleonic seas to the fall of Berlin, hammering home that carnage never changes no matter which banner he serves under. Motorhead reduce the sweep of history to one weary, cyclical verdict on war's sameness.","sonnet":"A single immortal soldier narrates every major battle in history simultaneously — Trafalgar, Moscow, Waterloo, Shiloh, Berlin — always fighting, always dying, always marching again to the same blood-and-iron story with no comfort for survivors.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~doctor-rock":{"haiku":"A cynical healer mocks patients seeking medical help, treating them with contempt while extracting payment. The song satirizes medical cruelty and opportunism.","opus":"Motorhead cast Lemmy as a deranged rock physician doling out remedy, force and velocity while vowing to bring the patient's heartbeat to a halt. The checkup is all leer and swagger, barking orders to recline, inhale, and swallow the medicine or be sickened. He's explicitly no Vulcan science officer and no scholar of ideas, just a persona that turns sheer amplified loudness into the only treatment available.","sonnet":"A rock musician plays doctor with gleeful aggression — examining, prescribing, and diagnosing with the same force as a wall of amplifiers. The surgery is musical, the medicine is volume and speed, and the patients are an audience who came willingly to be treated by someone who has no interest in their comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~dogs-of-war":{"haiku":"Warriors stand united against invasion, willing to die for their cause and endure as legendary defenders of their homeland for centuries.","opus":"A last-stand battle hymn, few and outnumbered defenders vowing to hold the line for a cause that outlives them. Motorhead trade menace for grim resolve, the glory found in refusing to bend rather than in winning.","sonnet":"A war anthem framed as a last stand — a small, outnumbered force holds its ground against an overwhelming enemy, pledging to fight to the grave for their people and knowing their sacrifice will echo for a thousand years.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~don-t-let-em-grind-you-down":{"haiku":"Resilience demands rejection of external judgment; the speaker refuses to internalise manipulation or allow others to diminish his spirit.","opus":"A defiant raised finger to the doubters, gossips and rotten characters who exist only to make your days miserable and rob you of your moment. Lemmy's blunt working-class creed: refuse to sink, refuse to be worn down.","sonnet":"A direct pep talk urging the listener to ignore the petty, envious bastards who lose sleep trying to make your life miserable — tell them to beat it, don't let them steal your thunder, and whatever you do, don't go under.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~dont-let-em-grind-you-down-alternate-version":{"haiku":"Defiance persists against those who would discourage and diminish; survival requires sustained refusal to accept imposed shame.","opus":"An alternate take on the same defiant message, brushing off the clowns and evil bastards who prey on your doubts and try to wreck your days. The core holds: don't let them wear you down.","sonnet":"An alternate version of the same defiant encouragement: don't pay attention to the clowns keeping you awake, you won't go blind by refusing to yield, and telling the malicious to shove it remains the only dignified answer.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~down-on-me":{"haiku":"The narrator struggles with loss of control and asks for sacrifice and love from others while questioning existence and trust during hard times.","opus":"A weathered plea for connection and rescue in hard times, the narrator asking whether he can be depended on and begging for blood, love, arms to hold him and keep him safe from scorn. Beneath the swagger it's unexpectedly tender. Motorhead, Lemmy's relentless rock-and-roll machine, occasionally let this vulnerable streak show through the volume, and here the survival instinct sounds almost like prayer.","sonnet":"A plea for genuine human contact dressed in the grammar of hard rock — the speaker asks whether anyone truly sees him, whether love and solidarity are real or just performances, cycling through images of blood, soul, and broken promises. The vulnerability beneath the swagger is the whole point: even warriors need someone to come down for them.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~dr-love":{"haiku":"An irresistible woman seduces the narrator who adopts the persona of 'Doctor Love'; the song celebrates her power to manipulate and dominate men through sexuality.","opus":"Lemmy's swaggering ode to a dangerous woman who has all the power in the transaction, the persona of the tireless lover a thin bravado over the fact that she's the predator and he's happily helpless.","sonnet":"A classic rock sexual pursuit narrative: an irresistibly powerful woman reduces the narrator to shaking helplessness, and he presents himself as \"Doctor Love\" — the only one equipped to handle her stamina and keep up with her appetite.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~eat-the-rich":{"haiku":"Carnal appetite and sexual domination dressed in restaurant and food metaphors. The song conflates consumption, desire, and power in explicitly predatory language.","opus":"Class revenge dressed as ravenous appetite, the wealthy served up as a meal to be devoured with relish. Motorhead pile on cannibal puns and menu jokes until consumption becomes both punchline and threat against the privileged. Written as the title theme to a late-eighties satire, Lemmy's leering delivery turns the feast into gleeful, greasy insurrection where everyone eventually bites into someone.","sonnet":"A gleefully anarchic anthem frames class rage as carnivorous appetite, urging the dispossessed to devour those who feast at the top. The metaphor runs dirty and relentless, collapsing fine-dining pretension into something feral and bodily, turning hunger for revenge into a vulgar, exhilarating act of consumption.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~emergency":{"haiku":"Psychological breakdown and desperation driving someone out of control. Losing grip on sanity and time, the speaker calls for help as everything unravels.","opus":"Panic set to a hammering pulse, a mind wrecked by too many wild nights now unable to think, decide, or hide as unseen pursuers close in. The repeated cry to the emergency line becomes pure alarm. This early Motorhead cut, inherited from Lemmy's bandmates, distills the group's speed-driven chaos into a blunt three-word siren of desperation and dwindling time.","sonnet":"A mind pushed past its limits by sleepless excess finds itself unravelling, unable to think straight or locate any exit. Paranoia and confusion flood in as the sense of crisis escalates, reduced to a single repeating distress call with nowhere left to run.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~fight":{"haiku":"Warriors fight to survive, whether in east or west, riding fast and loyal to their creed; fighting is their final stand against death.","opus":"Motorhead in pure war-chant mode, a relentless call to stand, fight and stay loyal to the last breath, laughing toward death. Not politics, just the primal Lemmy creed of defiance and never backing down, made to be shouted.","sonnet":"A war cry stripped to its most elemental — midnight, chaos, slaughter, and the imperative to stand and fight to the last breath, riding hard and dying hard, with loyalty to your creed as the only thing that gives the violence meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~heart-of-stone":{"haiku":"Emotional detachment and refusal to be manipulated or controlled. The speaker has a stone heart, keeping distance from those who play games and demand engagement.","opus":"A cold brush-off to a manipulator who treats affection as a rigged game of emotional roulette. The narrator refuses to be anyone's slave or mark, demanding to be left alone with his hardened heart. Motorhead strip the kiss-off to its blunt essentials, Lemmy's flint-eyed contempt turning romantic gamesmanship into just another hustle he has already seen through and walked away from.","sonnet":"A man refuses to be drawn into someone else's emotional manipulation, recognising the pattern of control and drama for what it is. Utterly unmoved, he shuts down every line of access and declares himself beyond reach — emotionally sealed off and determined to stay that way.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~i-ain-t-no-nice-guy":{"haiku":"Aging reveals that kindness and decency were naive; the speaker has grown cynical through accumulated life disappointments.","opus":"A late reckoning with self-flattery, an aging man who once believed himself uniquely decent and destined. In solitary moments the honest truth stings, and remembering all the times he walked away and let people down, he concedes he was never the good man he imagined.","sonnet":"A sober self-reckoning across a lifetime: the narrator who believed himself decent and chosen gradually discovered, through the loneliest hours and the writing on the wall, that he was never as good as he thought.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~i-am-the-sword":{"haiku":"Violence manifests as eternal principle, a tool wielded across centuries by power and circumstance, indifferent to morality and hungry only to fulfill its purpose.","opus":"Death and violence voiced by the weapon itself, the blade that has done murder across centuries in the name of the Lord. Motorhead personifies the instrument of killing as eternal and indifferent, cutting down heroes and oaths alike while righteousness is invoked over it.","sonnet":"A weapon speaks in the first person, declaring itself the instrument of death, divine will, and fate across centuries — indifferent to the cause it serves, loyal only to the act of cutting. It is both destroyer and survivor, outlasting every hand that wields it.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~i-ll-be-your-sister":{"haiku":"Unconditional availability and support in all forms. The speaker offers themselves as lover, sister, or mother—whatever is needed to fill the void.","opus":"An offer of total devotion, the narrator vowing to be sister, lover, and mother to anyone in need of somebody. Loneliness on the wire meets a promise to reignite a tired heart and return even when all seems finished. Motorhead soften their usual snarl into something almost tender, Lemmy pledging himself as the only friend left when someone thinks it is the end.","sonnet":"An unconditional offer of presence is extended to someone lonely and close to giving up. The narrator takes on every needed role — lover, sister, mother, friend — promising to return and remain no matter how far gone things feel, refusing to let the other face the wire alone.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~i-m-the-doctor":{"haiku":"A deceptive figure offers false solutions to desperation through pharmaceutical dependence, posing as a healer while delivering addiction and harm.","opus":"A pitch-black joke from a sinister physician who promises to ease your pain with implants, hypodermics and sixty bottles of pills, believing firmly in overkill and cheerfully steering the patient toward a heart attack. The bedside sympathy is pure predatory menace. Motorhead, Lemmy's speed-driven rock'n'roll machine, revel in this gallows humor, the grinding riffs and gravel vocals selling the doctor's lethal enthusiasm.","sonnet":"A doctor with a bedside manner that turns sinister by the final verse — sympathetic, then invasive, then lethal. The escalation from empathy to brain implants to sixty bottles of pills to heart attack has the rhythm of a con unwinding, the healer revealed as the one thing the patient couldn't survive.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~i-won-t-pay-your-price":{"haiku":"A person rejects and ejects someone they despise, refusing to tolerate their presence and threatening extreme violence to force them out.","opus":"Motorhead deliver a blunt eviction, the narrator ordering a self-absorbed jerk to move out and quit playing the hero he never was. Refusing to pay any price and daring the other to try stopping him, he threatens fingers in eyes and guns to knees with cheerful menace. The escalating count to three and promise of violence make this a barroom brawl set to Lemmy's growling, no-nonsense drive.","sonnet":"Someone who has overstayed their welcome is ejected without ceremony. The narrator, fed up with insolence and self-importance, counts down toward violence while making clear the other person's demands will never be met — the price too high, the tolerance exhausted, the door held open only long enough to push them through it.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~in-the-black":{"fable":"A blunt old-rogue courtship: he's at her door swearing he's changed, promising to swim a hundred miles if she falls in the ocean, asking her to please say yes — and admitting that a no would fold him up like a house of cards.","fableDeep":"The title is a ledger joke — 'in the black' is accounting for finally solvent, out of the red — and it rhymes with the vow 'can't go back, can't be who I was back then': reform offered as the sales pitch, emotional credit claimed after years of debt. The swagger keeps springing leaks: the hundred-mile swim and 'goodbye to the old crowd' are the pitch, but 'if you're not good to go, it's gonna hit me hard — I'm gonna fold up like a house of cards' is the hand shown. It's an old-school persistent-suitor song with its rough edges intact ('I don't like it when you run your mouth') — charm and pushiness from the same pen, no apology drafted.","haiku":"Pursuit and romantic overture toward someone desired, with the narrator positioning himself as capable of love and commitment despite his reputation.","opus":"A blunt courtship in which the narrator approaches someone he's smitten with, promising to treat them well and asking them to say yes. He insists he's changed from who he was and offers to protect them, admitting he'll fall apart if they refuse.","sonnet":"A persistent, openly infatuated narrator pursues a woman who keeps giving him the runaround, declaring he has changed for good and that she is the one he has been waiting for — even if he cannot be who he once was.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~in-the-name-of-tragedy":{"haiku":"The narrator reflects on confusion, violence, and war while questioning meaning and credibility, presenting tragedy as a lens for human conflict and deception.","opus":"A barrage of taunting questions probes a little brother about lost innocence, complicity, and whether anyone ever believed him. Amid crude jokes and doomsday dread, the refrain reframes struggle as tragedy sung aloud. From Motorhead's harder-hitting later era, the track keeps Lemmy's philosophical needling intact, prodding at faith, money, and Armageddon while insisting life is no easy TV dinner.","sonnet":"A barrage of rhetorical questions pokes at a figure who has lost their way — naive, manipulated, waiting on some personal Armageddon. The song cycles through absurdity and provocation, suggesting that human pretension, gullibility, and the hunger for spectacle are themselves the oldest and most reliable tragedy going.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~in-the-year-of-the-wolf":{"haiku":"A person transformed into a predatory wolf hunts with primal hunger and blood-lust; they describe the savage freedom and violent desire of their wolf nature.","opus":"Lemmy's Motorhead spins a feral fable of a man remembering a former life as a wolf, running with the pack when hunting was good and blood ran freely. Predatory hunger becomes metaphor for lust and menace, the beast within stirring toward its prey. Primal, blood-red desire dressed in mythic, wintry imagery.","sonnet":"A man recalls a past self he no longer is — a wolf-like predator who ran with a pack, hunted, and fed on blood-red desire under a full moon — and turns that primal memory toward the present: tonight the prey is you.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~iron-fist":{"haiku":"Supernatural dread and predatory force move through darkness—an evil presence hunts relentlessly, trapping victims with inevitable doom. The Iron Fist is an unstoppable malevolent power.","opus":"Motorhead render menace as pure physical dread, a nameless presence in the dark whose grip cannot be resisted. The imagery draws on ghost-rider folklore and devil-hound superstition, but the real subject is the band's own bulldozing power, an unstoppable force closing in. Title track of their 1982 album, it swaggers toward inevitable doom.","sonnet":"A supernatural night-horror persona — the Iron Fist — stalks through darkness as an inescapable force: evil eye, snakebite kiss, devil's grip, ghost rider in the sky; the song is essentially a litany of dread-names for an entity you cannot run from or resist.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~iron-horse-born-to-lose":{"haiku":"A tramp and his motorcycle living for speed, booze, and an inevitable tragic end. All those riding hard are born to lose, destined to waste forever in flight.","opus":"A romanticized death-wish anthem about a rider so bonded to his machine that it stands in for a spouse and a whole existence. Motorhead, whose members lived close to real outlaw-biker culture, celebrate reckless velocity, intoxication, and brotherhood as a doomed but chosen way of living and dying.","sonnet":"A motorcycle outlaw rides an endless highway with no destination, his machine his only marriage. Speed and intoxication sustain him until the final run — a chosen death at full throttle alongside brothers who share the same wasted, ferocious commitment to living outside the world's expectations.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~just-cos-you-got-the-power":{"haiku":"Wealth buys dominion but not dignity; the narrator refuses deference to those who conflate money with moral standing.","opus":"Lemmy's clearest class-war statement, spitting at a corporate operator who confuses wealth for right, class, or wisdom. The core is dignity that money can't purchase and time will eventually collect on.","sonnet":"A direct attack on the wealthy and powerful who mistake money and influence for moral authority, insisting that owning the world does not make you right and that class cannot be purchased.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~keys-to-the-kingdom":{"haiku":"The speaker wanders cursed by bad luck, seeking God and blessing but finding only empty salvation; the keys to kingdom mean nothing at all in death and damnation.","opus":"A grim, sardonic meditation on faith that never answers back: a wanderer crosses wasteland and tombs looking for the divine and finds only rejection and misfortune. Lemmy's Motorhead treats salvation as a cheat, framing religion as a promise that ultimately delivers absolutely nothing while a hostile heaven looks on.","sonnet":"A catalogue of failed arrivals — the desert, the mission, the goldmine, the funeral — each visited once and never returned to, each chalked up to bad luck. The keys to the kingdom are the ultimate punchline: they buy nothing, mean nothing, the whole architecture of salvation and status dissolves into a graveyard joke. God hates your guts anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~killed-by-death":{"haiku":"A tough persona claims easiness only when dead; the repeated phrase frames death as the sole condition for vulnerability and submission.","opus":"A swaggering narrator boasts of his untamable, reptilian bravado, insisting the only time he'll go easy is when death finally takes him. Lemmy embodies Motörhead's live-fast ethos here, the lizard-and-snake innuendo and relentless refrain making mortality itself the sole thing that could ever slow him down.","sonnet":"An untameable, self-described loner declares his identity through bluster and snake-wrestling bravado, conceding only one condition that could ever make him easy — death itself. The defiance is as comic as it is genuine, a swagger that refuses softening up until the very end.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~killers":{"haiku":"Mercenaries and warriors ride to battle with no compassion, glorifying slaughter and legend-making through killing; death and violence define their code.","opus":"A brutal, mercenary war-band anthem voiced by executioners who take no prisoners and spare no defender, riding to the killing floor with flame and sword; glorying in the slaughter under the ace of spades and at the Devil's side, the song is pure celebration of remorseless, legendary butchery.","sonnet":"A battle hymn narrated from the perspective of an unstoppable warrior force — riding under the Devil's banner with the ace of spades, they exist solely to kill, destroy, and add to legend, with no mercy, no gold-seeking, just the pure will to end lives in battle.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~liar":{"haiku":"Spiritual warfare and condemnation target a false prophet whose poisonous words and empty promises demand relentless opposition.","opus":"A blistering denunciation of a religious con man peddling false promises of heaven, whom the narrator vows to challenge, hunt and never let rest. Lemmy casts himself as the liar's nemesis and killer, sworn to expose the vile hypocrite behind the tales of god.","sonnet":"A speaker wages lifelong war against a deceiver — possibly a false prophet or religious manipulator — vowing never to sleep in the watch, to bring demons to trial, and to be the Nemesis that has always been destined to unmask the liar's eyeless smile and filth.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~loser":{"haiku":"A self-proclaimed loser who triumphs by claiming women and status despite mockery; redefining identity through defiant swagger.","opus":"A middle-finger anthem from someone written off as a loser who now has their women in his bed and can charge a fortune to sign his name, throwing the insult back at the people who wished him dead. It's pure defiant swagger. Motorhead, Lemmy's amphetamine-fueled trio, built a career on exactly this snarling, self-mythologizing rock'n'roll, victory sung through gritted teeth against the doubters.","sonnet":"The loser's revenge told from the winner's side of the ledger — they called him nothing, and now he has their women in his bed and their envy at his back. The identity they gave him as insult he wears as armor; he still doesn't know their names and doesn't need to.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~lost-johnny":{"haiku":"Urban decay and chemical addiction destroying a generation. Lost Johnny and his cohort are trapped in a waiting room of vice and desperation, seeking morphine relief.","opus":"A grimy noir of pills, sex work and dread where the title character howls at the moon and everyone scrambles for the next score. Torment within a fortress and reptiles crooning beneath the streets bleed into paranoid city nightmare. Co-written with Mick Farren and carried over from Lemmy's Hawkwind days, Motorhead paint addiction's underworld as a rigged contest whose real rules stay carefully hidden.","sonnet":"A seedy urban underworld is sketched through a cast of damaged figures — a girl in pain, a hustler losing his touch, a woman living off exposure — all orbiting a desperate outsider baying at the edges. Lost Johnny haunts the margins, scrounging for relief in a city that grinds everyone down.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~louie-louie":{"haiku":"A sailor's longing for a girl awaiting him; he sails alone for days dreaming of her, finally reaching port hoping to see her again.","opus":"Motorhead take the garage-rock staple and roughen it into a homesick sailor's tune, the narrator adrift alone at sea and aching to know if he'll reach shore and the woman awaiting him. After days on the water fixated on her, he spots land and swears he'll never set off again. The cover swaps the song's party spirit for something restless and yearning.","sonnet":"A sailor at sea burns with longing for the woman he left on shore. Three days of open water and nothing to do but think about her — the smell of her hair, the moment of return. Simple, insistent, the song is a sailor's ache compressed into a driving chorus and a name repeated like a compass bearing.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~march-or-die":{"haiku":"Humanity marches toward ruin with blind obedience, glorifying war and waste while the world burns.","opus":"Lemmy's furious indictment of humanity as a war-addicted species marching itself to extinction, statesmen trading in blood while forests die for gold. Not an anti-war anthem so much as a disgusted eulogy that says the planet would be better off once we burn out.","sonnet":"A total indictment of human civilization — war, greed, environmental destruction, propaganda, and the complicity of every institution are laid out as evidence that humanity is doomed and deserves it; Lemmy's narrator doesn't spare himself or the listener, and the song ends with the conclusion that Earth can only heal once we're gone.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~marching-off-to-war":{"haiku":"War transforms those who fight it into something fundamentally different; veterans understand the guilt and moral weight of combat in ways civilians cannot.","opus":"A soldier's fatalistic view of combat: millions die and never rise, and only those who were there understand how different everything felt marching to war; the deeper read is grim acceptance-fighting for the nation while knowing you're bound for hell, showing no quarter and wearing damnation well.","sonnet":"About the unbridgeable gap between soldiers and civilians — those who weren't there cannot understand how it felt to march into combat with neither choice nor certainty, and the song refuses to justify or glorify it, just marks the distance.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~metropolis":{"haiku":"In a fractured urban space, worlds collide without solidarity; the speaker claims detachment from the isolation and division unfolding around them.","opus":"A terse, detached snapshot of a city where worlds collide and divide, nobody on your side and nobody watching, the narrator repeating that he doesn't care and isn't even there. Alienation is stated flat, almost proud. Motorhead, Lemmy's foundational speed-rock trio, keep this one lean and driving, the shrugged indifference carried on a relentless, no-frills riff rather than any theatrics.","sonnet":"A city where worlds collide and divide and no one is on your side or the other side — and the speaker has detached entirely, not there, not caring, watching the whole spectacle of urban alienation with supreme indifference. Barely a minute of words, but the shrug carries its own weight.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~mot-rhead":{"haiku":"A speaker exists in perpetual hyperstimulated motion and sleeplessness, racing through days on pure adrenaline without need for rest or clarity.","opus":"A narrator rides a sleepless, wired multi-day marathon of excess, brain-dead yet feeling better than ever, hailing the righteous stuff that keeps him up. The band's namesake anthem distills Lemmy's speed-fueled rock-and-roll creed, the amazing state and total amnesia celebrating a life lived at full, exhausted throttle.","sonnet":"Sustained amphetamine wakefulness is rendered as a state of involuntary glory — days running together, the brain both fried and electrically alive, the body wired beyond caring about the damage. The song names the drug, the band, and the lifestyle as a single indistinguishable entity.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~no-class":{"haiku":"A vulgar, tactless person is socially rejected and ridiculed for their lack of grace and refinement.","opus":"A scathing dismissal of a graceless phony who's all volume and fake charm, permanently written off as unbearable. Motorhead lift the riff from ZZ Top's Tush and repurpose it as sheer contempt, Lemmy branding his target pathetic and out of step with brutal, gleeful brevity. There's no plot here beyond the pleasure of a total put-down.","sonnet":"A dismissal delivered with contemptuous precision: the target is hopelessly out of step, fake in their smiles, tragic in their pretensions, and entirely beyond catching up. The narrator has already crossed them off and moved on — the verdict is final, affectless, and issued without the faintest appeal.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~no-remorse":{"haiku":"The song dismisses religious guilt and fear of damnation, questioning whether anyone truly knows what awaits after death.","opus":"A scornful reckoning with religious fear, cataloguing heaven, hell, and purgatory only to dismiss the whole apparatus of Christian guilt. The narrator jeers that believers in cartoon devils and eternal fire deserve the flames they dread. Motorhead's title track for a landmark compilation turns Lemmy's skepticism into a creed of defiance, meeting mortality with no repentance and no fear.","sonnet":"A sardonic dismissal of religious fear catalogues every flavour of afterlife dread — hell, purgatory, Satan with his goatee — and finds them equally absurd. The song refuses guilt or apology, treating Christian moral terror as a con designed to keep the weak in line, entirely unworthy of respect.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~on-parole":{"opus":"A menacing ex-con back on the streets, unrepentant and armed, oscillating between glee at his freedom and the impulse to kill himself or somebody else for fun.","sonnet":"A recently released convict with a loaded gun and a dark sense of humour prowls the streets again, insisting he's innocent while the lyrics make clear he intends violence — the refrain \"stop me before I kill somebody else\" lands as a joke with a real edge.","src":"sonnet"},
"mot-rhead~overkill":{"haiku":"Rock and roll is celebrated as a loud, bodily experience that must move the listener or they're already dead.","opus":"Lemmy's manifesto for volume as a physical necessity, rock and roll worthless unless it hits the spine and makes you move. The relentless double-kick assault embodies the excess the title promises: more, louder, past the point of sense.","sonnet":"A pure anthem about the physical, almost religious necessity of loud rock and roll — the music goes straight through the body, and anyone who can't feel it must be dead.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~overkill-long-version":{"haiku":"Visceral intensity and physical abandonment become validation; the speaker requires overwhelming sensory assault to feel authentically alive.","opus":"A pure, unrepentant anthem to volume and rock and roll, insisting that if the music doesn't hit your spine and make you move, it isn't worth the name.","sonnet":"A song entirely about the physical experience of loud rock and roll and its effect on the body — the volume is the point, the noise is the message, and the band assures you they will bring it back to you with enough force to make you move. There is no metaphor; the overkill is the music itself.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~overnight-sensation":{"haiku":"Shallow fame and stolen authenticity replace genuine artistry, with manufactured celebrities offering nothing but hollow spectacle and abandoned integrity.","opus":"A jaded warning to a hollow rising star chewed up by industry sharks, all image and no soul. Threaded through is the road-worn admission that men in this game aren't allowed to show the hurt they carry.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a manufactured, empty music-industry persona — the speaker, who knows exactly who he is, watches a hollow overnight sensation sell out authenticity for franchise and fame, leaving only a hollowed-out rock and roll shell.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~please-don-t-touch-performed":{"haiku":"Overwhelming physical desire and nervous intensity from sexual longing, the speaker vibrating with yearning and unable to speak.","opus":"A rockabilly-flavored romp about being paralyzed by lust, the narrator shaking so much they can barely speak when the object of desire draws near. Cheap motels and drunken mornings frame trembling nerves and helpless attraction, a chill running down the spine at a shining pair of eyes. Motörhead play desire as a comic, full-body affliction that leaves them quaking.","sonnet":"Desire so overwhelming it causes physical paralysis — shaking so hard when she gets close that there are no words, a chill down the spine when his eyes shine, sneaking up the stairway like a thief and spending the night trembling like a leaf. The plea not to touch is both warning and confession of total helplessness.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~r-a-m-o-n-e-s":{"haiku":"Punk rock distills rebellion into ritual repetition—attitude and aesthetic becoming the message itself, celebrated for existing outside the mainstream machine.","opus":"Lemmy's blast of affection for the Ramones, saluting the whole lineup and their bratty downtown punk. A fan's tribute from one lifer to another, toasting the kind of rough-edged rock and roll that rescues your soul.","sonnet":"A tribute to the Ramones that doubles as a celebration of New York underground energy — rattling off band members, their bad-boy image, and the force of their sound as a life-saving cultural fact. It is affectionate, kinetic, and proudly adolescent.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~red-raw":{"haiku":"A serial killer prowls at night driven by bloodlust, finding joy and satisfaction in violence and gore.","opus":"A chilling portrait of a moonlit killer who bathes in gore and hungers only for more, love and murder fused in his devils-ridden mind. Motorhead track the predator through starlight and gaslight as women run and scream. The band leans into pulp-horror imagery, painting bloodlust in carmine and moonshine, a rare narrative descent into the mind of a monster who feels reborn in slaughter.","sonnet":"A nocturnal predator moves through gaslit streets driven by a hunger that blurs violence and desire into one compulsive act. Each kill brings a grotesque satisfaction, his face and hands soaked scarlet, the frenzy always demanding more — a monster fully at home in the dark he inhabits.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~sacrifice":{"haiku":"Suffering and loss proliferate while faith and prophecy compound deception through cycles of death and spiritual imprisonment.","opus":"A dark ritual where deceit turns into a tender kiss and belief itself becomes the poison that kills the believer, branding them with the mark of Cain. The chant makes the offering the price paid, chilled to the bone, with no one left to free the doomed.","sonnet":"A meditation on betrayal and sacrifice where the lie becomes a loving kiss, children murder the world, and the mark of Cain ensures no one will ever be set free again — cold as ice, pay the price.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~serial-killer":{"haiku":"A figure embodies unbounded violence and domination, narrating its own terrifying ascendancy over a helpless world.","opus":"Lemmy inhabits death itself as a boastful, unstoppable reaper who claims to fix all things by ending them. It's an apocalyptic taunt, mortality personified as the one force that makes everyone dance and every heart break.","sonnet":"A first-person manifesto spoken by a figure who casts himself as judge, predator, and dark saviour simultaneously, promising to break the old world and drag humanity into a howling void of his own making.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~sex-outrage":{"haiku":"Teenage lust and sexual desire on a backstage encounter; wild attraction and transgressive passion collide in moments.","opus":"A surge of backstage carnal urgency, the narrator jolted into overdrive the instant someone steps into the room, claiming the way they carry themselves ought to be illegal and the countdown is nearly done. It's adolescent craving at full tilt. Motorhead, Lemmy's tireless rock trio, deliver this leering rush with the breakneck momentum that defined them.","sonnet":"Lust at full voltage, backstage, no apologies — someone walks through the door and everything shifts to pure animal urgency. The song is transparent about what it is: desire framed as crisis, the body running on outboard-motor intensity, the move itself practically illegal.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~smiling-like-a-killer":{"haiku":"A predator stalks victims in darkness, describing his hidden presence and lethal intent while spreading dread through intimate knowledge of the target.","opus":"A creeping horror-show from the point of view of the menace itself, the unseen figure at your window, the scratch at the door, the razor at your throat in a fever dream, grinning through it all. Playful and sinister at once, it revels in stalking your kids, pets and dreams. Motorhead, Lemmy Kilmister's rock-and-roll juggernaut, step toward gleeful macabre theatrics here, the fixed killer's smile the single unnerving image tying the nightmare together.","sonnet":"A predator's monologue delivered as a nursery rhyme gone wrong — the unseen threat is everywhere: in crowds, near schools, at the mirror's edge. The smile is the detail that makes it horrifying, a weapon worn on the face while violence waits underneath.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~speedfreak":{"haiku":"Drug addiction and reckless living burn through time in sleepless excess where speed becomes existential escape.","opus":"The manic velocity of amphetamine life, up for a week with no sleep, everything way too fast until the inevitable coming down. Lemmy makes the speedfreak's restless hurry both boast and doom, everlasting and wrecked.","sonnet":"A first-person portrait of amphetamine use — up for a week, no sleep, playing hide-and-seek, too fast to stop — embracing the wreck of coming down as the identity of an everlasting speedfreak with no regrets.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~stay-clean":{"haiku":"A person must resist lies and manipulation from others to preserve their authentic self and integrity.","opus":"A flat refusal to accept convenient deceptions or bow to a rotten system, insisting that character shows only in how a person reckons with the truth and that ultimately each of us faces the world by ourselves. Motorhead turn this into a creed for endurance, with Lemmy urging listeners to seize what little certainty they have and guard it fiercely.","sonnet":"A call to resist manipulation, false authority, and the voices that profit from shame. Staying clean means holding to personal truth rather than bending to systems designed to make a person sorry for existing — the only proof of integrity is how clearly one can see reality, even when it costs everything.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~stone-dead-forever":{"haiku":"Greed and corruption crystallize a person into obsolescence, their life's accumulation emptying into insignificance as mortality erases the illusion of ascension.","opus":"Contempt aimed at a self-made tycoon whose greed and guilt hollowed him out. Motorhead watches the money-man reach the top only to find himself spiritually finished, the fortune worthless, his life a question with no good answer at the Pearly Gates.","sonnet":"A dismissal of a person who traded their life for wealth and status, only to find themselves trapped — fingers caught in the Pearly Gates, time run out, asking what happened to their actual existence. The verdict is merciless: stone dead forever, whatever the financial achievement.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~suicide":{"haiku":"Humanity poisons itself through lies, greed, and endless war, hurtling toward collective self-destruction.","opus":"An apocalyptic bleakness rare for the band, casting humanity as a rotten plague choking on toxic downpour, deceiving itself until the very atmosphere becomes lethal, and framing self-annihilation as everything the species has managed to earn across its long history. It begs for honesty while foreseeing total ruin. Lemmy's speed-rock institution trades its usual swagger for grinding existential horror.","sonnet":"A slow-burning ecological and moral apocalypse — the human race consuming itself through lies and poison air, no morning ever coming, the dream always turned against those who dreamed it. The chorus asks for clean living and truth as the only stay against collective suicide, but the rest of the song has already decided it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~tear-ya-down":{"haiku":"A man seduces a woman with charm and flattery, talking through the night to seduce her, then physically overpowers and dominates her.","opus":"A cocky pickup number that frames seduction as tearing down defenses, the narrator vowing to have his target laughing and undone by morning. Motorhead kick it off with mock-German bluster and heap on boasts of romance and near-magical prowess. Lemmy's leering delivery turns the chat-up routine into a night of playful conquest, the whole thing aimed at wearing resistance away.","sonnet":"A confident seducer spends an entire night in conversation, trading lines and mutual awareness with someone he is fully intent on winning over. The energy is playful and physical, all forward momentum — promises of transformation and supernatural good fortune disguising a simple, electric chase scene.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~terminal-show":{"haiku":"In a strange gathering overseen by dark rulers, humanity questions its fate—whether oblivion or transcendence, knowing and fearing they don't understand.","opus":"Lemmy imagines an end-times gathering where humanity's finest sit bewildered before an enigmatic host, unsure why they've been summoned or what verdict awaits. Chess-piece figures give it the feel of a reckoning, a grim question of whether the species has actually learned anything from its history before the lights go out for good. Motorhead deliver it as apocalyptic dread with a curl of dark humor.","sonnet":"A philosophical riddle set at a kind of end-of-time gathering — a golden creature, a blind king, a red queen — where humanity waits for an answer about why it's there and what comes next, finding only more questions and impending ruin.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~the-chase-is-better-than-the":{"haiku":"Seduction derives from pursuit rather than possession; the speaker values the hunt's intensity over the catch's emptiness.","opus":"A self-styled silver-tongued devil brags that pursuit thrills him more than conquest, chasing women he'll lose interest in the moment he catches them. Lemmy's rock-and-roll ode to the hunt over the prize.","sonnet":"A self-described silver-tongued devil revels in his predatory reputation, cataloguing conquests with cheerful amorality and announcing, at the core of every verse, that the thrill of the hunt matters more than any catch.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~the-watcher":{"opus":"Alien observers passing judgment on humanity from orbit, disgusted by greed that ruins the planet and coldly deciding there's no room out there for a species that can't be trusted.","sonnet":"An extraterrestrial observer addresses humanity from a vast, cold distance, disgusted by the greed destroying the planet and declaring that humans can no longer be trusted or accommodated — Earth is too small from out here, and you are on your own now.","src":"sonnet"},
"mot-rhead~too-good-to-be-true":{"haiku":"A narrator recounts falling in love and sharing deep intimacy with someone, only to be abandoned and left emotionally devastated, unable to move past their loss.","opus":"A weary heart that had sworn off love falls hard for a woman, shares everything with her, then is left devastated when she goes, mourning a love that felt too good to be true.","sonnet":"A man who swore off love after heartbreak falls completely for a woman who left him anyway, because she never trusted him enough to see his devotion, and he is left cold and longing.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~too-late-too-late":{"haiku":"A speaker ends a manipulative relationship after recognizing betrayal and games, rejecting false claims of credibility and departing with finality.","opus":"The narrator dumps a scheming lover he sees as a pathetic fraud, finished squandering his time and ready to bolt before it's too late. Motorhead pair their punkish velocity with a curt kiss-off, the deadlocked refrain and sprung snare marking a man who has this figured out and nothing left to forfeit.","sonnet":"A man sees through a manipulative partner's game and walks before the exit closes, naming the con without drama. The satisfaction is cool rather than wounded — he knows what was happening, and knowing is enough to cut the loss and leave.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~vibrator":{"haiku":"Double entendre; a device that is reliable, always ready, and never fails to please, becoming a faithful companion better than many alternatives.","opus":"A raunchy comic boast ventriloquizes a sex toy pledging tireless, disease-free satisfaction, ready and buzzing with a power pack for a heart, promising never to break its owner's heart. Early Motorhead lean into leering bar-room humour, the vibrator narrator winking through double meanings about batteries, fuses and the second time, all delivered at breakneck grin.","sonnet":"An unapologetically playful ode to mechanical pleasure — the device addresses its owner with cheerful devotion, promising reliability, cleanliness, and perpetual availability. The extended metaphor is committed and deliberately over-the-top, treating mundane battery-powered satisfaction as a love affair of sorts.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~we-are-the-road-crew":{"haiku":"Working musicians build the stage that hosts rebellion, servants to the music itself. The crew belongs to the road.","opus":"Lemmy's tribute to the unglamorous grind of touring, written from the perspective of the crew who make the show happen. Endless towns, cheap beer, bleeding ears, lost hotels and border crossings pile up into a life that is exhausting and beloved in equal measure. A rare rock song saluting the anonymous laborers behind the spectacle, weary but never quitting.","sonnet":"A catalogue of touring life worn as a badge of honour: another town, girl, truck, race, junk food, bleeding ears, burned hotel, forged border, map learned overnight — the road crew's identity is built entirely from \"another,\" and they love every degraded iteration of it.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~we-bring-the-shame":{"haiku":"Children born strange refuse expectations and mock their creators; transgression becomes redemption.","opus":"A generational war cry from Motörhead, voiced by children turned strange and unashamed, confronting the elders who bred them. They mock, break hearts and refuse to be what was expected, victims of neglect who bought a dream and now demand payment for the neglect they inherited. Rebellion framed as a debt owed, contempt hurled back at those who raised them.","sonnet":"A generational challenge from children to the society that raised them: they have climbed into the machine, bought the dream, and emerged contemptuous of their makers — \"you owe us life, we owe you nothing, eat the knife\" — a refusal to be what was expected.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~white-line-fever":{"haiku":"A trucker addicted to the highway becomes consumed by the allure of long-distance driving as an escape and compulsive force in their life.","opus":"Motorhead chase the speed-and-highway high the title suggests, the narrator hooked by that rush yet admitting it's slowly finishing him. Addressing a woman who's forgotten him after everything she's put him through, he keeps rolling on, too wired to rest. Noting only that the affliction hasn't done him in so far, he lends the boast a doom-shadowed edge.","sonnet":"Cocaine is the third party in a relationship — the thing that has replaced intimacy and erased memory. The narrator addresses a partner whose past has been consumed by the drug, framing addiction as a slow death that numbs rather than kills outright, the white line both liberation and quiet annihilation.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~whorehouse-blues":{"haiku":"A rough musician reflects on his journey from poverty to global touring, expressing satisfaction with his rough exterior despite 30 years of relentless touring.","opus":"A raw, self-mythologizing acoustic blues about clawing up from the lowest rung, rescued by music and hauled across the globe for decades, plain-faced but content. The wink of a punchline is that the only thing lacking is a bit of harmonica and a brothel on the side. Lemmy's band pares down to grit and harp, the comet boast their plainest declaration of outlaw pride.","sonnet":"A road-worn valediction from a band that came from nothing and never looked back — thirty years of city-to-city touring distilled into satisfaction, without sentimentality. The missing harmonica becomes a joke about the one thing they never quite got around to adding.","src":"haiku"},
"mot-rhead~you-better-run":{"haiku":"A dangerous, mercurial figure embodies contradictions and chaos, having seen and lived countless dark realities, warning others to flee from their presence.","opus":"Lemmy in full outlaw-mythology mode, casting himself as a shape-shifting force of nature who has outlived and out-lived everyone, half swagger and half warning. It runs on the pure kinetic charisma of a man who made speed and menace into a brand.","sonnet":"A dangerous, supernatural figure warns everyone to clear out — armed with lightning and silver bullets, having seen everything and painted it all black, and having spotted Satan himself in a Cadillac.","src":"haiku"},
"mother-s-cake~the-operator":{"opus":"A frantic self-command to seize control of a life circling the drain. The Austrian trio dramatizes a man cornered by frustration and fear who resolves to become his own crowd, his own backup, escalating past every mistake toward a decisive final round he refuses to lose.","sonnet":"A song about a man living in a hollow, dead world who decides to stop waiting for rescue and take control of his own fate — he becomes 'the operator,' the one driving the escalation, replacing external validation with self-reliance. The emotional core is the shift from victimhood to agency, however desperate the circumstances.","src":"sonnet"},
"mother-s-cake~toxic-brother":{"opus":"A person describing a violent, destructive alter ego or dark impulse inside them, a hidden maniac behind a friendly smile that they can no longer contain and finally decide to unleash.","sonnet":"The song describes a dangerous, violent alter ego or inner self that cannot be suppressed — a split between the outer appearance and a destructive inner nature that demands to be let loose.","src":"sonnet"},
"mothica~buzzkill":{"haiku":"Accountability shatters false narratives; the speaker weaponizes exposure, breaking cycles of abuse through public testimony and refusal to remain silent.","opus":"A survivor of abuse refusing to stay quiet any longer, turning her damage into a weapon aimed at the person who caused it and warns those still near them. She reclaims her volume, promising the reckoning is only beginning and the receipts don't lie.","sonnet":"A survivor of manipulation goes public: the silence is over, receipts don't lie, and someone who silenced and exploited her now faces the reckoning — \"I won't keep your secrets now, you can't keep my volume down,\" with a warning to anyone else who calls this person a friend.","src":"haiku"},
"mothica~casualty":{"haiku":"A self-destructive person vows to break their bloodline of pain and addiction rather than become another tragedy. They fight to feel and survive against numbness.","opus":"A survivor's vow written from the far side of addiction and self-harm, refusing to become another statistic or a repeat of her bloodline. The defiance is real but rides on how close she came to not making it.","sonnet":"About surviving a period of severe self-destruction — late-night hospital visits, overdose territory, empty distractions — and choosing to refuse that fate by breaking a cycle of inherited pain. It's a defiant refusal to become a statistic of her own suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"mothica~red":{"haiku":"Crimson passion and visible wound; the speaker dwells in bloodied emotional landscape.","opus":"Mothica, who has been candid about addiction and recovery, sketches a self-destructive spiral where numbing chemicals keep the pain close rather than gone. A darker hidden self waits underneath, and the narrator confesses she never learns until she sees red, until real damage forces reckoning. Dark-pop honesty about the terror of what she might become before she stops.","sonnet":"Self-destruction by chemical numbing — keeping the bottle close to stop the pain, spiraling out until the poison fills the chest, and not learning until hitting rock bottom (\"seeing red\"). The fear underneath is of the darker side of herself she doesn't want to meet; the refrain is a confession that damage has to become visible before she'll change.","src":"haiku"},
"mothica~sensitive":{"opus":"A candid admission of anxiety, thin skin and the draining work of feeling everything too keenly. The narrator hides away turning thoughts over, spooked by high places, unfamiliar people and being around others, her dread ignoring all reason. She flips her fragility into an identity, claiming the vulnerability she can't turn off as both dangerous and tender.","sonnet":"A portrait of anxiety so wired into someone's body that it governs every interaction — they stay home, overthink everything, feel physically sick from overstimulation, and the song names this hypersensitivity honestly: delicate and deadly at once, impossible to simply switch off.","src":"sonnet"},
"mothica~the-reaper":{"haiku":"She loves death itself for its reliability; he keeps her captive because at least he stays.","opus":"Romanticizing a lover who treats her as expendable, reframing self-destruction as devotion to Death himself. Mothica's own history with addiction and darkness turns the gothic conceit into a knowing confession about mistaking harm for reliability.","sonnet":"A darkly comedic love song for an abusive relationship — the narrator is in love with someone who treats her life as expendable, but romanticizes it as reliability and control. The song holds the horror and the genuine feeling at the same time without resolving them.","src":"haiku"},
"mothica~the-reckoning-feat-polyphia":{"haiku":"Trust betrayed and predation; a girl becomes older and forever guarded after betrayal.","opus":"With Polyphia's guitar pyrotechnics, Mothica tells of a once-trusting girl betrayed until she becomes a wary, cocked weapon, reading every newcomer as a threat not yet revealed. In sleep she takes command, rising from the chaos as a surging wall of vengeance. Trauma hardening into power, a Shakespearean reference marking the cost of forging wounds into weapons.","sonnet":"A girl who trusted everyone becomes a weapon after waking in the lion's den: she controls everything in dreams, rises as an emerald hurricane from chaos, and the reckoning she delivers is directed at whoever made her this way — Lady Macbeth told to wash the blood, the optimist replaced by a loaded gun.","src":"haiku"},
"mothlite~something-in-the-sky":{"haiku":"Omniscient observation and binding connection; words become violence that hold lovers together.","opus":"A hushed, ominous art-rock piece about being watched — an unseen presence overhead tracking each movement, tears pointless beneath its gaze. Two people bind themselves together in something like conspiracy and need, dreading a higher power that has turned indifferent, even hostile.","sonnet":"Something watches from above — violent and tender, murdering words binding people together — and the watched are on their knees, treason and need confused. The dreaming of the end loops back to dreaming itself, the surveillance cosmic and intimate at once, no use in crying because the alignment happens regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"motionless-in-white~c0de":{"haiku":"The speaker describes mass unity through hypnotic viral ideology, redefining the unknown and surrendering individual identity to collective emotion.","opus":"Digital-age communion cast as chemical addiction, a crowd uniting to write shared code so no one feels alone. Virus, overdose, and ones and zeros become metaphors for connection through music itself. Motionless in White fuse industrial gloss with anthemic uplift, turning their gothic metalcore toward hope, the melodic overdose framed as a signal bled out into shadows and answered by kindred listeners.","sonnet":"Music functions here as a viral transmission, a chemical fix that dissolves the boundary between self and collective. The narrator frames surrender to sound as a form of unity, a shared code that overrides isolation — craving the euphoric loss of control that comes when frequencies pull people into a single organism.","src":"haiku"},
"motionless-in-white~creatures-x-to-the-grave":{"opus":"A ten-year look back at a band's punishing road, thanks to the fans who gave them a place to belong tangled up with shame over stumbles and the cost of pursuing the dream, swearing to carry the entire burden until death. Motionless in White pitch personal reckoning at anthem scale.","sonnet":"A ten-year anniversary reflection on what the band and its members became — the narrator carries shame, failures, and public pain as a life's work, acknowledges the fans who made it home, and vows to take those marks to the grave while still stepping through the door into whatever comes next.","src":"sonnet"},
"motionless-in-white~disguise":{"haiku":"Breaking free from self-imposed masks built for survival; the speaker reclaims identity by facing the fear trapped inside the disguise.","opus":"A face-off with a self fractured into personas, the narrator sealed inside a prison of his own making, worn out from concealment and at last prepared to confront the false identity he traded everything for. Finding himself requires abandoning hope first. The Pennsylvania metalcore band turn their gothic theatrics inward on identity and defiance.","sonnet":"A crisis of self built from the inside out — the speaker has been hiding behind a constructed identity for so long they can no longer tell who they are from who they built, and the box they made for protection has become a prison. The turn comes in fury: done with the mask, done with the fear, ready to face what's underneath even if it burns.","src":"haiku"},
"motionless-in-white~meltdown":{"haiku":"Humanity engineers its own extinction through apathy and complicity; the apocalypse arrives as televised spectacle while everyone watches their collective self-destruction.","opus":"A gleeful countdown to civilizational collapse, spelling out that you can't have virus without us and turning the apocalypse into a televised finale. Humanity is cast as its own parasite, dancing on the ashes it made.","sonnet":"A gleeful apocalyptic song that identifies humanity itself as the virus causing its own extinction — through environmental destruction, misinformation, political corruption, and mass delusion. The song frames the end of the world as a self-inflicted series finale, with the word \"us\" embedded in \"virus\" as the punchline: we are the contagion, and the meltdown is our own doing.","src":"haiku"},
"motionless-in-white~voices":{"haiku":"Internal warfare between competing selves fragments consciousness into unbearable noise; the speaker struggles to reclaim autonomy from the chorus of voices that have colonized his mind.","opus":"An interior war against intrusive thoughts and self-loathing, the mind turned into hostile territory it can't win. The defiance comes at the end: a refusal to let those voices, or anyone, take the self entirely.","sonnet":"Relentless internal voices wage war on the speaker's sense of self, leaving them numb to pleasure, alive only to pain, and fighting a losing battle to retain their identity against an inner force that keeps pulling them under.","src":"haiku"},
"motive-black~fight-alone":{"haiku":"Jealousy and masochism trap the speaker in unrequited spiral where they battle isolation without rescue.","opus":"A hard-rock anthem of jealous fixation and codependency, the singer tortured picturing a partner with somebody new, catching the trace of another person on their body and fearing their sanity might slip. A plea to be rescued from oneself runs beside a hunger for control. Longing, betrayal and self-ruin knot together, love voiced as a solitary, deranging fight.","sonnet":"Jealousy and infidelity corrode the speaker from within — smelling someone else on their partner, dying with each touch — as they simultaneously need that love and struggle to hold their control, asking how long they must fight this alone.","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~down-on-me":{"haiku":"In crisis, people demand salvation or love but settle for faking emotion to survive.","opus":"A rough plea for someone to prove real amid a world of faked emotion and lost names, asking to be saved and held rather than left to die anonymous. Beneath the swagger, Lemmy's craving for genuine connection over performance.","sonnet":"About isolation, existential choice, and desperate need for someone to commit — the narrator cycles through hard-times aloneness and the impossibility of knowing what anyone truly is, before landing on a raw plea for someone to give blood, love, and hands. The emotional core is someone asking to finally be held.","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~heart-of-stone":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness and hardened resistance define the self; the speaker remains unmoved and impenetrable.","opus":"Lemmy hardens himself against a manipulator who treats affection like a rigged game, refusing to be played, cornered, or guilted. Beneath the biker snarl runs the band's perennial theme of self-preservation: better to seem unfeeling than to become somebody's mark. Emotional armor plated over old wounds, worn as a matter of survival rather than pride.","sonnet":"A narrator who has been played before refuses to be anyone's emotional mark again — \"you play emotional roulette, I ain't seen no winners yet\" — meeting manipulation with flat indifference and the demand to be left alone. The heart of stone is not cruelty but armour: a refusal to dance in the dark for someone who was never serious to begin with.","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~i-m-the-doctor":{"haiku":"Pharmaceutical addiction promises healing while delivering deeper dependency; the speaker exploits vulnerability through false remedies.","opus":"A sinister quack promising to cure your depression with pills, injections and implants, escalating from help toward outright overkill and a fatal heart attack. A darkly comic parody of the drug-pushing physician who kills while claiming to heal.","sonnet":"A sardonic doctor figure offers relief for every symptom — black depression, morbid horror, desperate pain — through implants, hypodermics, and sixty bottles of pills delivered in overkill doses, ending with the patient dead: \"you're never coming back.\"","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~in-the-black":{"haiku":"A roadie's relentless pursuit of love despite hardship frames touring as both blessing and wear; the speaker refuses to retreat.","opus":"A blunt, wheedling courtship from a man insisting he's the one she's been waiting for. Under the swagger and the promises to keep her safe is real anxiety that a rejection would fold him flat.","sonnet":"A man in single-minded pursuit of a woman who has stolen his heart — he pleads to be let in, promises devotion, and announces he is 'in the black' meaning wholly consumed and changed by wanting her, unable to go back to who he was before.","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~in-the-name-of-tragedy":{"haiku":"Life is absurd; we justify cruelty and hypocrisy while pretending morality matters in the end.","opus":"A barrage of taunting questions aimed at someone coasting through life on charm and delusion, waiting for the next apocalypse instead of living. Lemmy needling the passive and self-dramatizing, everything performed for tragedy's sake rather than actually felt.","sonnet":"A sardonic tour through human self-deception and performance — the narrator asks pointed, rhetorical questions about a person who has lied, smiled their way through life, waited for Armageddon, and faked their emotions. The refrain \"in the name of tragedy\" accuses people of making performance of suffering into identity.","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~in-the-year-of-the-wolf":{"haiku":"Primal predation defined a former life until civilization severed the speaker from wild ferocity; now only hunger for that lost ferocity remains.","opus":"A predator's reverie of a time he ran with the wolves, tasting blood and running down living prey with joy. The present-day reveal is that the beast never left him, and tonight the prey is you.","sonnet":"A lyrical myth about a past life as a wolf — running in a pack, hunting, killing, living entirely by blood and instinct under a cold moon. The narrator is nostalgic and unsettled by how much he was in the animal state: now he is something different, but the wolf is still in him, and tonight it surfaces again — directed at the listener who has become the prey.","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~iron-fist":{"haiku":"An unseen malevolent force manifests as supernatural evil—the devil's grip tightens invisibly, inescapable and inevitable.","opus":"The title track of Motorhead's 1982 record conjures a nightmarish rider and grasping devil-hand, dread stalking someone through darkness with no escape forward or back. Less a story than an atmosphere of menace, it turns the band's speed-metal roar into folkloric terror, the inescapable clutch of something ancient and malevolent closing in.","sonnet":"Something unseen and inevitable moves through a dark night — invisible hand, snakebite kiss, ghost rider, devil's hound — announcing itself by its grip alone. The Iron Fist is the name for a force that circles until you lose your track and can't go back.","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~keys-to-the-kingdom":{"haiku":"Fatalism and cursed luck; the speaker arrives without access, caught by misfortune.","opus":"A grim late-Motorhead meditation on futility, where salvation is promised but worthless and a hostile God turns his back on the desperate. Wandering deserts and graveyards, the narrator finds every reward hollow, damnation certain, and heaven's keys unlocking nothing. Lemmy's gallows humor curdles into genuine bleakness about a rigged, merciless cosmos.","sonnet":"A catalog of failed arrivals — the mission, the desert, the goldmine, all reaching nowhere — punctuated by the refrain that the keys to the kingdom buy nothing at all: God hates your guts, the saints and thieves share the same fire-letters in the sky, and living better only gets you to the grave sooner.","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~life-s-a-bitch":{"haiku":"Survival requires adaptive cynicism; the speaker navigates harsh existence by rejecting sentimentality and embracing brutal pragmatism.","opus":"Streetwise survival advice delivered as a relentless creed: conceal your wounds, take what's owed to you, fear the right people and keep your mouth shut, because the world is brutal and unforgiving. Every verse hammers the same lesson that you're owed nothing.","sonnet":"A blunt set of survival rules for someone who doesn't know the game: hide your scars, get your share, don't talk too much or land in jail, watch how you run your life — and if you forget, the refrain reminds you that life's a bitch.","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~smiling-like-a-killer":{"haiku":"Violence hides in plain sight—the predator smiles from the crowd, always closer than you know.","opus":"Written from inside the mind of a predator who is everywhere and unremarkable, the creep in the crowd smiling at your kids. The horror is the ordinariness, evil hiding behind a friendly face that could be anyone's, even your own.","sonnet":"A horror-movie serial-killer fantasy narrated in first person — the killer watches, stalks, and murders while maintaining a constant smile. It's campy and theatrical rather than sincere, playing on the archetype of hidden evil behind a pleasant face.","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~suicide":{"haiku":"Humanity poisons itself through apathy and violence; ten thousand years of suffering ends only in collective self-destruction.","opus":"Lemmy surveys humanity as a self-destructing species, poisoning its own air and lying to itself until truth becomes impossible. Across millennia of killing, he sees only the collective death-drive of the title. A rare political sermon from the band, half warning and half despair, pleading for honesty before an already-doomed world blows itself apart.","sonnet":"A jeremiad against civilisation's self-destruction: poison rain, lies we tell ourselves, gods who make us kill — \"ten thousand years and all we got is suicide\" — the speaker calling for truth and moral cleanliness before the air itself finishes us.","src":"haiku"},
"motorhead~whorehouse-blues":{"haiku":"A lifetime of relentless touring and sexual conquest satisfies the appetite but leaves the void unfilled; success never quite quenches the primal hunger.","opus":"An acoustic, harmonica-laced valediction where the veteran band looks back on decades hauled up from the gutter by music, city to city, never handsome but always satisfied. Recorded near the end of Lemmy's run, it trades speed-metal roar for weathered blues gratitude, a rowdy toast to a life well and disreputably lived.","sonnet":"A road-worn musician's memoir in slow blues form: up from the gutter, wrong side of the tracks, city to city for thirty years, the music the only thing that ever pulled them out and the only thing that still isn't enough. The end of a long haul told without self-pity.","src":"haiku"},
"mott-the-hoople~all-the-young-dudes":{"haiku":"Young people embrace glam rock and alternative culture as rebellion against mainstream expectations; they find identity and purpose in music and subversive fashion.","opus":"A generational anthem for glam-era youth, cataloguing troubled kids (Billy's suicidal talk, petty theft, gender-bending Lucy) who carry the news of their own subculture. It rejects the older revolution/Beatles-Stones nostalgia in favor of a defiantly aimless, T.Rex-loving present.","sonnet":"A generational anthem for young outsiders — Billy planning his suicide at twenty-five, Lucy the cross-dressing tough guy, a brother locked in Beatles nostalgia — who carry the news of a new, disenchanted identity, rejecting both the old pop revolution and the future that was promised.","src":"haiku"},
"mountain-eye~synopsis":{"haiku":"Systemic indoctrination crushes individuality while despair approaches; humanity surrenders to collective erasure.","opus":"A protest against indoctrination and conformity that swells into a diagnosis of the whole species, every you and every me complicit in the bloodshed of history. Contemplating the blade at the wrist, the speaker still asks whether there's more to this than subjugation.","sonnet":"A song about being crushed and conditioned by systems of control — the speaker feels indoctrinated, belittled, unfit, and suicidal under a conformist order, asking if there's any way out of the societal machine that processes human beings like raw material.","src":"haiku"},
"mouth-culture~sharkbait":{"haiku":"Competitive sharks circle vulnerability; the speaker warns of hidden predators who extract secrets and secrets while pretending intimacy masks consumption.","opus":"A warning to circling opportunists who want a taste of a success guarded by a secret only mother knows. The talker who runs his mouth is told to wash it out before he ruins everything.","sonnet":"About protecting something valuable — a talent, an identity, a secret — from people who want to exploit or consume it. Everyone is circling like sharks, trying to get a taste of the narrator's recipe or pick them apart piece by piece, and the narrator is telling them to keep their mouths shut or get shut down. The tone is confident and combative: she knows her worth and she is done explaining it.","src":"haiku"},
"mouth-culture~sunny-smile":{"haiku":"Forced happiness and superficial cheer, bright exterior hiding inner pain.","opus":"Giddy infatuation rendered as sensory overload, where a new crush spins endlessly through the narrator's head and pulls him out of his own overthinking. The warmth of the title becomes a kind of escape, mutual and consuming. Bright indie-pop about wanting to be someone's morning light and finding himself pleasurably, helplessly stuck inside the feeling.","sonnet":"A person so full of light they give the narrator tunnel vision — supersonic, spinning around in the head, taking them out of themselves entirely. The speaker is metaphorically inside out from the feeling and admits they're stuck, but \"stuck\" here is the best place to be.","src":"haiku"},
"movements~barbed-wire-body":{"haiku":"The speaker is emotionally weaponized and isolated, cutting everyone who tries to hold them close while using escape and deception as their defense mechanisms.","opus":"A pointed character study of someone who turns aloofness into armor, an expert at slipping away unnoticed who offers no forgiveness and wounds anyone who dares get close. Their prickly self-protection all but guarantees isolation. Movements, the California alt-rock band, specialize in exactly this kind of literate, psychologically acute portrait set to aching guitar dynamics.","sonnet":"Someone who has weaponized themselves against closeness — every exit is clean, every attachment kept just loose enough to cut before it holds. The song watches with clear eyes: the loneliness is visible to everyone, the defense mechanism is hurting both parties, and the cycle of scarring and leaving just keeps repeating with a different name each time.","src":"haiku"},
"movements~fail-you":{"haiku":"Recurring nightmares of failing a loved one fuel waking dread; the dreamer fears sleep itself.","opus":"A recurring nightmare of watching someone he loves drown while he fails to save them, waking to find them safe beside him yet certain he'll fail them in waking life too. Anxiety and depression turning devotion into dread of one's own inadequacy.","sonnet":"About recurring nightmares in which the narrator watches someone they love die and cannot save them, then wakes to find that person safe beside them. The fear is that the dream rehearses a real failure — that they will inevitably let this person down despite loving them completely.","src":"haiku"},
"movements~i-hope-you-choke":{"haiku":"Righteous condemnation and spite fuel survival; the speaker weaponizes contempt against someone's cruelty, finding vitality in their curse.","opus":"A furious kiss-off to a self-righteous person who spends their life picking fights and shifting blame from a moral pedestal, whom the singer sees as dead inside. The wish that they choke on their own venomous words is pure, unfiltered contempt.","sonnet":"A full-throated denunciation of someone addicted to online hatred and blame-shifting, who hides shamelessness behind a false moral high ground — the speaker repeatedly wishes those words eat their target's soul.","src":"haiku"},
"movements~kept":{"haiku":"Self-loathing calcifies through physical degradation and moral surrender; the speaker offers destruction as gift, preferring erasure to authentic connection.","opus":"A raw reckoning with self-harm, depression and a corroded sense of one's own worth, the body worn down to almost nothing by clinging to something that was never going to work out. Its central trade offers up belief in exchange for oblivion, wanting anything solid to grip while agreeing to erase everything else.","sonnet":"A person in the grip of self-loathing and exhaustion bargains — offering the other person something to forget in exchange for something to believe in — while cataloguing their own disintegration and inability to find a reason to keep going.","src":"haiku"},
"mr-kitty~rats":{"haiku":"Rats inside someone's mind destroy them daily; they contemplate suicide, feel forgotten and broken, tormented by internal voices they cannot escape.","opus":"Darkwave staring directly at suicidal ideation, the mind overrun by gnawing rats of self-destruction, days counted down while a wounded narrator wavers between healing and giving in. Bleak and unadorned, the pain offered up as if it were a gift.","sonnet":"Daily suicidal ideation narrated flatly — the narrator writes notes contemplating suicide, counts down days, describes internal rats destroying their mind while sitting alone and abandoned, in pain they want to give away.","src":"haiku"},
"mr-little-jeans~rescue-song-rac-remix":{"haiku":"Mutual redemption through companionship offers escape from mutual damage; both lovers reach toward each other, recognizing that healing requires reciprocal surrender.","opus":"Two bruised, damaged people meeting and offering each other shelter, mutual rescue between the walking wounded. The tenderness lands because neither is whole, and salvation here is reciprocal rather than heroic.","sonnet":"Two damaged people find each other in a grey, uncertain moment and propose a mutual rescue — each will save the other, meeting somewhere between their respective wounds, the warmth of borrowed coats, and the tentative hope of a rendezvous.","src":"haiku"},
"ms-mr~think-of-you":{"haiku":"Toxicity and abuse simmer beneath attraction; the narrator dwells on the damage inflicted by a lover, unable to release the burden of what was done.","opus":"The lingering aftermath of an abusive relationship, the narrator finally naming the manipulation and infidelity for what it was. Clarity arrives, but the memory won't release its grip, which is the ache the song sits in.","sonnet":"The speaker reckons with the afterimage of an abusive relationship — the other person fed on devotion, gave nothing back but infidelity and cruelty, and yet the speaker still thinks of them, anger and clarity now replacing the addiction they once felt.","src":"haiku"},
"msr~heal-the-world":{"haiku":"Transcendent healing through connection—tears save from darkness; pain becomes truth as shared vision overcomes divisions; voices rise as the world burns but healing calls forward.","opus":"MSR stage this at an existential edge, the world burning while the narrator begs one specific person to heal the darkness. Only their tears hold the dark at bay, and they long to watch every division crumble. The arc runs from collapse into a plea for connection strong enough to sustain them before the final end, healing framed as something called forward rather than promised.","sonnet":"A song about standing at an existential edge while the world burns, begging a specific person to heal the darkness: the narrator's only tears save them from the dark, and they want to see all divisions crumble — the emotional arc is collapse followed by a plea for connection to sustain them before the final end.","src":"haiku"},
"mucc~zetsubou":{"haiku":"Despair consumes hope entirely as dreams calcify into ash; the speaker drowns in isolation where loneliness is the only reality.","opus":"A shattering descent into despair by someone left alone in a wasteland after everyone they loved is gone, spitting on the empty words dream and hope that hypocrites toss around. Only isolation and hopelessness remain, yet it closes with a wrenching thank-you to everyone, hinting at a suicidal farewell.","sonnet":"The speaker is abandoned on a wasteland after everyone has died or scattered, dismisses hollow platitudes about dreams and hope as garbage, contemplates faking suicide for relief, and watches the small hope they painfully found finally rot away — leaving only the four words dream, hope, solitude, despair cycling back on themselves, until a final \"thank you, everyone.\"","src":"haiku"},
"mudhoney~touch-me-i-m-sick":{"opus":"A deliberately repulsive come-on from a self-described diseased, rotting creep who insists he'll make a woman love him until she dies; a provocatively grimy fusion of sickness, sex and death.","sonnet":"A grunge grotesque where the narrator invites someone into his rot — he's a creep, full of decay, and half-threatens that if you don't come with him you'll die alone anyway. The delivery oscillates between mock seduction and genuine self-loathing, and the sexual invitation is inseparable from contamination.","src":"sonnet"},
"mudrat~fme":{"haiku":"Systemic oppression and personal enmity fuel murderous fantasy; the speaker channels rage at injustice and individual betrayal into threats of apocalyptic violence.","opus":"A serrated purge of resentment, the narrator abandoning sentiment to fantasize about destroying enemies while admitting the violence stays locked in his head. Threaded through personal spite is a wider rage at systems and oppressors. Aggressive underground catharsis that channels powerlessness into a defiant, self-aware refrain of wishing his tormentors gone.","sonnet":"Pure aggression as catharsis — the narrator fantasises about enemies bleeding while acknowledging those fantasies stay inside the head, then pivots to boasting about thriving in spite of them. The systemic critique (\"oppressor's still around\") is folded into the personal enmity; the whole thing is held together by the refrain as a mantra of survivalist contempt.","src":"haiku"},
"mudvayne~choices":{"haiku":"Leadership abdicates responsibility while populations lose agency; binary choice becomes illusion masking powerlessness.","opus":"A rage at leaders who never turn the blame on themselves, framed as a nursery rhyme to expose how casually power decides fates. The bleaner truth: freedom is an illusion when someone else holds every real choice.","sonnet":"Political leaders are indicted for insanity masquerading as authority, and the notion of free choice is dismantled — in a system where freedom is sold and voices are suppressed, there is no real choice or voice left for anyone.","src":"haiku"},
"mudvayne~forget-to-remember":{"haiku":"Memory shapes and torments a person, haunting them from inside; they wrestle childhood demons and feel worthless, asking if anyone can save them from themselves and the past.","opus":"A tormented reckoning with self-betrayal, the narrator asking what he's become after turning his back and hardening into a thoughtless machine, memory biting and stinging from inside, begging to be saved from himself. The title's paradox captures wanting both to escape and to hold onto pain. Mudvayne, the Illinois nu-metal band, thread this introspective anguish through heavy grooves and cathartic melodic release.","sonnet":"A reckoning with self-betrayal — the moment you turned away from someone became the moment you became a machine, and memory keeps twisting the knife. The question at the center is whether the speaker can ever stop being shaped by what they did; the answer the song reaches is not forgiveness but something closer to exhausted surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"mumford-sons~i-will-wait":{"haiku":"After hardship and dust, the speaker kneels and commits to waiting and knowing their ground; they promise to wait while strengthening heart and spirit through patience.","opus":"A prodigal returns heavy as a stone into forgiving arms, kneeling to steady himself and vowing patience while he tames his flesh and frees his mind from lies. Mumford & Sons, the London folk-rock band, drive the theme of contrition and devotion with banjo-fueled build and refrain, the repeated promise to wait becoming an act of humility and hope.","sonnet":"Returning battered and humbled to someone who offered forgiveness, a person commits to patience and earned trust rather than reckless forward motion. Faith, discipline, and the willingness to kneel — to know the ground beneath oneself — become the foundation for a love that outlasts dust and restlessness.","src":"haiku"},
"mumford-sons~little-lion-man":{"haiku":"Accumulated self-blame yields to recognition of mutual fault; the speaker confronts their own failure while demanding accountability from the other.","opus":"A brutal self-indictment after wrecking a relationship, the narrator too cowardly to be who he wanted and owning that the ruin was his doing. The lion-man name mocks his lost bravery, the refrain a raw admission of blame to the one he hurt.","sonnet":"A self-lacerating confession to someone the speaker has wronged through cowardice and pride — unable to follow through on bravery when it mattered, damaging another's heart through their own failures — the refrain accepting blame fully even while framing it as mutual.","src":"haiku"},
"municipal-waste~wolves-of-chernobyl":{"haiku":"Mutant wolves—products of Chernobyl's radiation—hunt humans across contaminated snow. Nature's revenge breeds packs that grow with each kill, their glowing eyes an unstoppable massacre born from man's hubris.","opus":"A gleeful horror romp where, after the reactor meltdown, nature reclaims the zone and irradiated wolves with luminous eyes stalk the survivors, cast as the planet avenging what humanity destroyed, the growing pack leaving carnage behind. It plays disaster as pure B-movie splatter. Virginia crossover-thrash crew Municipal Waste thrive on exactly this fast, rowdy, tongue-in-cheek gore.","sonnet":"A horror-thrash parable about Chernobyl's aftermath — nature mutating in the radioactive zone, wolves growing enormous and glowing-eyed, hunting humans as payback for the disaster that created them. The song frames this as poetic justice: we killed the environment, and now the environment has built something to kill us back.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~aftermath":{"haiku":"War and collapse necessitate chosen bonds; the speaker clings to one person as civilization crumbles.","opus":"Set inside the band's Drones war narrative, this is the private refuge after the fighting, one bond held up as the only real thing left. Love as the shelter that makes the surrounding collapse survivable.","sonnet":"A soldier or survivor of geopolitical violence returns from chaos to the only stable thing left — a partner — and the mutual promise to hold each other through a world where states crumble and isolation is the default condition. The love described is not romantic escapism but a chosen anchor against the collapse of everything external.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~animals":{"haiku":"The song condemns predatory capitalist and corporate behavior as animalistic greed, bloodlust, and cruelty toward the vulnerable. It critiques wealth accumulation and exploitation as moral collapse.","opus":"Muse point their stadium grandeur at financial predators, reeling off the actions of a ruthless dealmaker who studies markets, grabs more and snaps up islands and seas while the streets run with blood. Crushing the desperate and eliminating rivals, the title creature is capitalism's top hunter, and the closing sneer to just off yourself and spare everyone twists satire into a brutal verdict.","sonnet":"Financial predators are stripped of civilised pretense and named as animals — creatures who feed on crisis, crush competitors, and buy entire oceans as casually as islands. The narrator turns the logic of the market back on its operators, ending with the suggestion that the only logical conclusion of this system is self-destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~apocalypse-please": {"haiku":"The song calls for emergency intervention and miraculous divine action to prevent the end of the world, framing salvation as urgent necessity. It presents apocalypse as imminent and demands transformative intervention.","opus":"Calls out for a miraculous, biblical rescue as the world ends, repeatedly declaring the apocalypse and pleading for something to pull humanity through.","sonnet":"A call for the world to declare an emergency and embrace an apocalyptic end, framing catastrophe as both inevitable and somehow redemptive — something miraculous and biblical that will finally pull humanity through.","src":"haiku","fable":"The apocalypse here is not dreaded but ordered up: each verse escalates its imperative — declare the emergency, stage something biblical, proclaim the victory that rewrites history — yet every demand resolves back into the same helpless refrain of being pulled through, as if only a catastrophe this size could finally rescue anyone from ordinary life. The mock-operatic grandeur stages doom as spectacle rather than terror, the diagnosis underneath being that a culture starved of meaning starts craving its own finale — and the flat announcement of the end lands as exultation, repetition doing the work of prayer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"a culture starved of meaning ordering up its own finale","built":"mock-operatic grandeur stages doom as spectacle; repetition does the work of prayer"},
"muse~assassin":{"haiku":"A rallying call for violent rebellion against corrupt leadership and established systems.","opus":"From the paranoid, revolution-haunted phase of Muse, a call to armed insurrection against corrupt power, half sincere political fury and half comic-book fantasy. The thrill is in how it dresses genuine anti-authoritarian rage in operatic melodrama.","sonnet":"A call to political insurrection — the time has come to shoot your leaders, join underground forces, destroy demonocracy — the song is a blunt revolutionary provocation that names assassination as a legitimate response to corrupt power.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~big-freeze":{"haiku":"A couple faces the end of their relationship and the world simultaneously, questioning whether they can survive emotional and cosmic collapse together.","opus":"Bellamy casting a failing relationship on a cosmic scale, two people collapsing like dying stars while a metaphorical big freeze approaches. Beneath the astrophysics is a plea not to let the warmth in the heart decay into nothing.","sonnet":"A plea for emotional survival inside a relationship that has expelled its warmth — the speaker isn't sure if the other person is still in, senses a coming emotional freeze-out, and begs them not to let the love go cold.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~blackout": {"haiku":"Time moves too fast and too slow; love fades, youth slips away, and responsibility arrives before readiness. The song captures the paradox of recognizing life's transience while being too young to understand it.","opus":"A fragile, string-laden ballad about clinging to a love you sense is already doomed. The tension is between youth's recklessness and the dawning awareness that nothing this good, or this fleeting, gets to stay.","sonnet":"A brief, bittersweet meditation on youth and fleeting love — the relationship and the life might not last, they're too young to see what's coming, but there's a tender refusal to be cynical about it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A string-swept lullaby-waltz whose tenderness is a decoy: three nearly identical verses of gentle prohibitions, the meaning living in the tiny substitutions between passes — a love too good to last becomes a life too good to last, then a life that could be the last, breakup consolation quietly widening into mortality. The speaker's age flickers with it, too old to dream in one verse, too young to care or to see by the end: advice from someone who cannot decide whether he is the elder offering comfort or the child who needs it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","death & grief"],"means":"breakup consolation quietly widening into mortality across nearly identical verses","built":"tiny substitutions between three passes carry the meaning; the words barely change"},
"muse~bliss":{"haiku":"The speaker envies someone for their freedom, happiness, and ability to love easily; they want to possess that person's peace of mind.","opus":"Envy dressed as adoration: the narrator worships someone whose contentment and serenity come effortlessly, and can only beg for a share of it. Bellamy's electronic-baroque grandeur makes wanting another person's inner peace sound like an addiction.","sonnet":"A song about envying someone else's inner peace and freedom — the narrator wants whatever that person has, the peace and joy they radiate, and aches at the contrast with his own experience. It's desire framed as spiritual hunger for a lightness he doesn't possess.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~butterflies-hurricanes": {"haiku":"The speaker mobilizes to seize their moment and remake the world; urgency overrides doubt.","opus":"An urgent exhortation to seize a single chance to change the world before it slips away. It reads like a call to greatness demanded of someone whose moment has arrived and won't return.","sonnet":"An urgent call to stop wasting potential and step up — the speaker tells someone their number has been called, battles are beginning, and this is the last chance to be the best version of themselves and change the world before it's too late.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pep talk delivered as ultimatum, to a chosen one whose number has been called: the title's chaos-theory wager — a butterfly's flutter swelling into a hurricane — never enters the words, so the song enacts it instead, one small insistent command looped almost verbatim until it becomes the demand to change the world. The Rachmaninoff-styled piano cadenza that erupts mid-song is the wingbeat made audible, virtuosity standing in as proof the exhortation can be answered; around it the framing stays cold — hard times promised, revenge assured — motivation closer to conscription than encouragement.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"motivation closer to conscription than encouragement","built":"the piano cadenza enacts the butterfly wingbeat the title names but the lyrics never do"},
"muse~cave":{"haiku":"Isolation and withdrawal into interior darkness.","opus":"A retreat into total isolation, the narrator sealing everyone out, insisting they're powerless to reach him and daring anyone who dares approach to have their heart scorched. It lurches between wounded self-sufficiency and threat. From Muse's debut Showbiz, it captures the young Devon trio's Radiohead-tinged angst, Bellamy's falsetto wrapped around self-ruin and a demand to be left with his own mistakes.","sonnet":"A bristling push-away — the speaker insists whatever is happening is personal, not your concern, nothing you can fix. But the cave invitation contradicts the hostility: come in and I will burn your heart, arrest me for what I have done. It is isolation claimed as choice while the door is held slightly open, the damage self-inflicted and perversely offered.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~citizen-erased":{"haiku":"Someone is being pressured to conform and express themselves for others' consumption, but wishes to be free and unobserved, yearning to return to their former self before losing their autonomy.","opus":"A person feels worn down by demands to explain and expose himself, longing for a moment of freedom and asking to be washed clean and erased from someone's memory to spare them both pain.","sonnet":"A person rails against the pressure to perform and explain themselves, feeling that honesty and openness have only scraped away at their sense of self, and they want memories and shared history erased entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~city-of-delusion":{"haiku":"Urban culture as illusion and distraction from truth; false promises of the city.","opus":"A defiant rejection of blind belief, the narrator refusing doctrines that crumble under scrutiny and vowing to demolish a stronghold of self-deception, taking vengeance on those wasting what remains. From Black Holes and Revelations, it's Muse at their flamenco-inflected, apocalyptic peak, Bellamy dressing his distrust of power and dogma in trumpets and Spanish guitar.","sonnet":"A rejection of groupthink and institutional belief systems that tips into vengeance — the speaker refuses to fall in line with an all-seeing authority, calls out those who consume and squander everything they touch, and resolves to demolish the architecture of shared delusion even if it costs blood. The stance is both righteous and self-aware enough to call it avenge.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~con-science":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness and disconnection, unable to authentically process loss or intimacy.","opus":"A fragmentary interlude circling loss and the impossibility of pretending, someone slipped away and the questions won't leave. More texture than statement, it holds a feeling of unresolved conscience rather than spelling it out.","sonnet":"A minimal, fragmented reckoning with loss: someone has slipped away and the speaker cannot pretend to control the ending or bend the rules around it, left only with the feeling of it — soul-deep and unresolved — and questions that will not leave.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~darkshines":{"haiku":"A person is drawn to and consumes the innocence of another whose darkness feels good. Attraction turns predatory, the speaker pulled down by what they desire.","opus":"A lover who brightens the bleakest horizon also drags the narrator low, an ache he insists is worth it even as it leaves him hurting, promising to devour her purity. Muse thread flamenco-flavored menace through this early cut, its contradictory title capturing an attraction that thrills and wounds in the same falling riff.","sonnet":"Someone is magnetically drawn to another person whose darkness mirrors their own — the attraction operates like gravity, irresistible and slightly consuming. The speaker acknowledges that this pull is painful and corrosive, yet the soreness itself registers as confirmation that the feeling is real and therefore welcome.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~dead-inside":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness and hollowness despite outward appearance.","opus":"A portrait of loving someone emotionally vacant, radiant on the surface but lifeless beneath, demanding control while the narrator pours out everything and gets nothing back. The gutting twist is his own corruption: schooled in deceit and cruelty, he ends up mirroring the person who drained him. Muse coat the coldness in glossy synth-pop.","sonnet":"A relationship corroded by emotional vampirism — one person radiates warmth on the surface while feeling nothing beneath it, and the other, desperate for connection, eventually learns the same hollow performance. The trajectory is contamination: by the song's end the narrator has become what they loved.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~defector":{"haiku":"Betrayal and abandonment; switching sides or leaving the fold.","opus":"A declaration of independence from control, the narrator proclaiming freedom from brainwashing and society, watching a dissolving empire and vowing to help raze the throne. Muse close it with borrowed rhetoric about man being born free. From Drones, it fits the album's arc of a mind reclaiming itself from indoctrination, the band's habitual anti-authoritarian bombast channeled into a defiant, blue-blood-mocking anthem of defection.","sonnet":"A declaration of withdrawal from a collapsing empire — the singer has seen through the brainwashing, watched the throne's foundations crack, and stepped away before the razed remnants fall. The stance is contemptuous rather than triumphant: the empire thought him weak, he was simply done. Freedom here is defined entirely as the refusal to be controlled.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~endlessly": {"haiku":"A lover pledges eternal devotion and unconditional support despite knowing a deepest part of themselves will remain unknowable. They commit to endurance even though the awaited moment never arrives.","opus":"A vow of total devotion undercut by its own last breath, promising to give everything while admitting the decisive moment for it never actually arrives. Love held back by the very fear that words alone can't overcome.","sonnet":"A declaration of unconditional love — the narrator will love and give endlessly, will never abandon the person — though the final twist reveals the moment they've been waiting for never actually comes.","src":"haiku","fable":"A vow of total, hopeless devotion whose grandeur is exactly the problem: impossible promises to catch and never abandon are stacked against the confession of a walled-off inner self the beloved will never reach, so the pledge is armor as much as offering. Everything hinges on the final line's single-word swerve — the recurring conditional about the moment ever coming flips to the moment never coming — revealing a confession rehearsed but never delivered: love kept perfect precisely because it stays unspoken.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"love kept perfect precisely because it stays unspoken","built":"the recurring conditional flips to its negative in the final line, revealing rehearsal without delivery"},
"muse~escape":{"haiku":"One person projects their own meaninglessness onto another, bullying them from a distance while claiming kinship in shared emptiness and madness.","opus":"Addressed to someone frantic to outrun their own emptiness and unimportance by whatever means, the singer flips between revulsion at this cruel, monstrous conduct and the disturbing confession that the two of them are indistinguishable. He speaks as if to a corpse and shoulders the fault himself, the whole thing pushing him toward madness.","sonnet":"A split-self confrontation where the narrator recognizes that he is both the bully and the bullied — talking to himself as someone who deflects meaninglessness through cruelty, finally calling out that behavior while acknowledging he keeps absorbing the blame for it.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~exo-politics":{"haiku":"Conspiracy and alien encounter—the song speculates whether governments hide extraterrestrial presence while satellites and hidden agendas colonize human minds.","opus":"Muse channel Matt Bellamy's taste for conspiracy into a tongue-in-cheek alien-invasion scenario, the narrator holding out for a revelation as extraterrestrials crowd the heavens and surveillance seizes only our thoughts. Buried secrets from ages past weigh on him while hidden rulers pull the strings, turning paranoid myth into a meditation on control and a revelation that never arrives.","sonnet":"Someone waits for confirmation that what they suspect about power structures is true — that alien presences fill the skies and that leaders conceal rather than reveal the nature of reality. The burden of suppressed knowledge weighs physically; satellites conquer minds rather than territory, and the sign never quite arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~exogenesis-symphony-part-1-overture":{"haiku":"Trapped in a divine system, the speaker cannot escape or forgive the thief of their soul.","opus":"The opening of Muse's sci-fi symphony about humanity's last hope, fleeing a dying Earth and pleading with existential questions of who and where and why we are. Trapped in a program not of our making, unable to forgive or escape it.","sonnet":"Existential displacement — the feeling of having one's identity and purpose stolen, trapped in a program or system with no escape, unable to forgive or forget, circling the unanswerable questions of who and where and why we are.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~exogenesis-symphony-part-3-redemption":{"haiku":"Redemption requires reset and forgiveness of self; the second chance lies in renewed beginning.","opus":"The finale of Muse's orchestral trilogy from The Resistance, imagining humanity's last plea for a second chance after squandering its world. Stripped to piano and strings, it reaches for the possibility of beginning again and getting it right, a fragile, cosmic prayer for forgiveness offered at the very edge of extinction.","sonnet":"A plea for a second chance — addressed to someone or to humanity itself — asking to start over, to get it right this time, framed as a final opportunity for forgiveness before the end.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~explorers":{"haiku":"Adventure and discovery; exploring new territories together.","opus":"A lament for a used-up planet where every field is owned, rivers get rerouted, crops fail and the old dream of seeking the unknown has nowhere left to go, the narrator pleading to be freed from a world that imprisons the soul. From The 2nd Law, it's Muse's environmental despair wrapped in a lullaby, ending on a whispered instruction to sleep as if surrendering.","sonnet":"An elegy for a world that has been entirely claimed, divided, and depleted — there is no frontier left, all the land is owned, rivers are rerouted for profit, and the only remaining response is to beg for release from a planet that no longer has room for free souls. Helium-3 becomes the last desperate symbol of possibility before the final plea to just be free.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~falling-away-with-you": {"haiku":"A relationship collapses as both partners fail to retrieve lost happiness and shared memories fade. Despite attempts to stay together, their world crumbles into forgetting.","opus":"A bittersweet romance in which the singer can't summon a single good moment and watches recollections and imaginings rot, yet resolves to cherish whoever his partner turns into even as his entire world caves in. The twist is capitulation: sensing everything collapse while tumbling into love, content to sink alongside her.","sonnet":"A relationship's dissolution watched in slow motion — the memories intertwining even as the future feels unwritten, a crumbling of self tied to another person's departure, and the helpless knowledge that the same mistakes keep repeating.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song built from the vocabulary of loss — every verb is subtraction: eluding, fading, decaying, crumbling, left behind, thrown away — set against one insistent counter-claim that their lives have only just begun, repeated like a man persuading himself. The title carries the twist: the dissolution is shared, falling with her rather than away from her, losing the memories together as the last form of intimacy left. And the outro circles back to memories he will never find, so the album's one gentle exhale ends by enacting the amnesia it describes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"shared dissolution as the last surviving form of intimacy","built":"every verb is subtraction while one counter-claim insists their lives have only just begun"},
"muse~feeling-good":{"haiku":"Joy and confidence; the high of being free and alive.","opus":"A jubilant declaration of freedom and renewal, birds and rivers and blossoming trees all mirroring a narrator waking into a new dawn and a bold new world that finally belongs to him. Written by Bricusse and Newley for the sixties stage, it became a standard, and Muse's thunderous arrangement turned it into an anthem of liberation, Bellamy's voice climbing from smoky restraint to full release.","sonnet":"A pure, uncomplicated declaration of renewal — birds, rivers, blossom, dragonflies, the scent of pine all carry the same message: something has lifted and the world feels brand new. Freedom is named outright, and the song asks for nothing more than to sit in that feeling and let it mean what it means.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~fillip":{"haiku":"Something approaching bears a new scent and destiny, described as their last memory; despite strangeness, it creates an inevitable pull toward a specific person or moment.","opus":"A rush of imminent, fated attraction, a scent blowing in, something new and strange that won't change for anyone, leading the narrator on toward a person he never knew was the one, framed as a last shared memory. It's early Muse romantic urgency. From their 1999 debut Showbiz, this is the Devon trio before the arena grandeur, the yearning already there in Bellamy's soaring, tremulous delivery.","sonnet":"The sudden arrival of something — a feeling, a person, an urgency — that smells new and cannot be deferred. The speaker insists this is the moment, now or never, and traces back to realize this overwhelming pull has been leading somewhere all along. The accusation at the center lands softly: the speaker never knew it was you until it already was.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~follow-me":{"haiku":"A protective figure offers steadfast companionship and safety to someone who is lost, scared, and abandoned, promising not to let them face hardship alone.","opus":"A pledge of complete safekeeping to someone terrified, brought low and left for lost: when night closes over you and your strength fails, come with me and I'll protect you. Written for the singer's expected child, its heart is unconditional guardian devotion, vowing never to abandon or allow harm to the one in his care.","sonnet":"A pledge of absolute, unconditional protection — written from the perspective of someone (reportedly the singer's partner during a difficult pregnancy) promising to be there in every darkness, not to desert the other person when everyone else has left.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~fury":{"haiku":"Rage and retribution; righteous anger unleashed.","opus":"A bitter address to someone recklessly exhausting themselves, whose vanity cushions the self-harm right up until they end up isolated and unhinged after squandered years. The repeated hope that no deity exists to judge them turns confession into a grim kind of comfort. A Muse B-side from the Absolution era, it channels that record's guilt-ridden dread into a study of denial.","sonnet":"A portrait of pleasurable self-destruction — burning the candle at both ends, soaking in self-love that softens wounds the self invented. Twenty years burn away and the person is left alone and unravelling. The prayer that there is no God to punish the mess is not atheism so much as relief: no accountability means no reckoning for the waste.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~guiding-light":{"haiku":"Seeking a beacon through darkness; trusting another person as your moral compass.","opus":"A ballad of love slipping past rescue, the narrator holding hearts that crumble in his hands, unable to hurt or even feel the person anymore as their shared light goes cold and confused. He keeps reaching anyway. From The Resistance, it's Muse's most unabashed power-ballad, eighties stadium-rock swagger and all, trading their usual dystopia for straightforward romantic desolation.","sonnet":"A relationship slipping into numbness on both sides — the speaker recognizes he has damaged those he cared for and now finds himself cold, lost, and stripped of direction. The person addressed was the fixed point he oriented himself by, and without that light the internal compass spins in silence.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~hate-this-i-ll-love-you":{"haiku":"Paradox dwells in simultaneous hatred and devotion toward same force. Love and hate collapse into unified passion.","opus":"A relationship curdles into servitude and rebellion, the narrator exhausted by being drained and used, then hardening into defiance and destructive strength. Bellamy's theatrical dynamic between resentment and devotion turns love into a power struggle, someone born to serve now growing by the hour into the one who will tear it all down.","sonnet":"Subjugation flipped into rebellion: the narrator is tired of being made to feel born for servitude, and as the hour grows, they grow too — discarding their soul to blaze through the thief's sky, \"unafraid to die,\" born now to destroy the thing that led them on.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~hoodoo":{"haiku":"Curses and bad luck; fatalism about destiny and doom.","opus":"A fragile fantasy of retreat, the narrator inviting someone to slip into a dream and construct a new life together, offering shelter and pledging to absorb their pain, all while haunted by the recurring fear that he was cherished for who he is yet squandered the chance to become better. From The Resistance, it's Muse at their most orchestral and Morricone-tinged, Spanish-guitar hush breaking into an admission of failure.","sonnet":"A half-remembered nightmare about being bound and unable to choose a better path — the song moves between a tender invitation to hide together and a trapped feeling of souls fading, bodies crumbling. The final confession is the darkest turn: the recurring nightmare was that he was loved for who he truly was, and he failed to become who he could have been.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~hyper-music":{"haiku":"A speaker rejects someone's claims and desires for them, making clear they were never loved and never will be.","opus":"A poisonous farewell dressed in cosmic-competition imagery, the singer denying he ever desired or cared for a lover who asked more of him than he could give. He crawls back from the grave only to hurl scorn, swearing none of it was ever genuine. From Muse's second record, the track pushes Bellamy's early falsetto rage into a claustrophobic fit of injured ego and cold refusal.","sonnet":"A relationship collapses in cold fury as one person confronts the other with the full weight of disillusionment. Declarations of love are revealed to have been false all along, and whatever power dynamic once held them together is now exposed as manipulation — leaving only contempt and the desire to be rid of it.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~hysteria": {"haiku":"Desperate desire and emotional breakdown under pressure. The narrator's sanity erodes as they crave release and fear losing control.","opus":"A consuming, desperate craving that twists the narrator inside out, demanding a lover's heart and soul right now. Feeling his self-control slip, he strains to break out even as his faith erodes.","sonnet":"A song about desperate, consuming desire — internal pressure building to the point of implosion — in which the speaker demands total surrender from the other person, driven by a need so urgent it feels like the last chance before losing all control.","src":"haiku","fable":"Desire as parasite: the verses render craving as a force physically working the singer over — bugging, grating, turning him inside out — until he is hollowed to numbness. The choruses carry the real movement: an abstract it sharpens into a direct you, breaking down gets corrected mid-line into breaking out, and the demanded loss of control arrives as collapse rather than release — heart imploding, faith eroding, conviction dissolving at exactly the moment he gets what he wanted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"collapse arrives at the exact moment of getting what he wanted","built":"abstract it sharpens to direct you mid-chorus; breaking down corrected to breaking out"},
"muse~invincible":{"haiku":"A motivational anthem urges resilience through struggle; collective strength resists external pressure and opposition. The song asserts that conviction and unity make people unstoppable.","opus":"Muse in anthem mode, a rallying cry for solidarity against forces designed to grind people down. The strength offered is collective and defiant, insisting the soul stays unbreakable so long as no one stands alone.","sonnet":"An anthem of collective resilience — urging someone to hold on through struggle, to fight for what they believe in, and declaring that together people are unbreakable even when forces conspire to pull them down.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~kill-or-be-killed":{"haiku":"Relentless moral paradox: the world demands cruelty to survive, love requires control, and compassion ensures defeat; fate compels violence as the only path forward.","opus":"From Muse's 2022 record, a portrait of moral collapse under pressure, compassion dissolving as demons take hold and the world rewards cruelty. Fate forces a brutal binary of the title. Bellamy dramatizes how fear and a rigged society drive a person to abandon their better instincts and choose survival through violence.","sonnet":"A speaker driven to the brink by a world that rewards cruelty — demons have materialized within him, compassion has dissolved, and fate forces the choice: destroy or be destroyed. A second turn complicates this: \"she prefers it when I assert control,\" suggesting the violence is also erotic and relational, not only systemic.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~knights-of-cydonia":{"haiku":"A person calls for survival and rights against corrupt rule where fools become kings; they refuse capture and demand fight to reclaim power.","opus":"Named for a Martian region, Muse's galloping space-western is a call to arms against tyranny and complacency. It pictures a divine power that has abandoned humanity, leaving ordinary people to defend their freedom by force. The message is defiant survival: refuse to submit, and reclaim what's yours or die trying.","sonnet":"A call to resist a corrupt order in which fools hold thrones and gods fall asleep on duty. History is offered as a warning rather than a source of hope, and the only answer is collective survival — fighting not for glory but simply to remain alive against a system designed to absorb resistance.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~liquid-state":{"haiku":"A speaker endures physical and emotional abuse, hoping for warmth while being pushed toward destruction and loss of control.","opus":"A plea to be broken open and cleansed, oscillating between hope for peace and a masochistic invitation to be poisoned and pushed under. The narrator begs to be warmed, held up, and helped to survive even while courting his own dissolution. Sung by bassist Chris Wolstenholme about his own struggles with drink, Muse turn recovery's vertigo into a hymn teetering toward the light.","sonnet":"A person in crisis pleads for rescue from his own dissolution, asking to be broken open, cleansed, and steadied before he disappears into the dark. The plea is masochistic in its imagery — poison, being pushed, losing the soul — yet underneath it runs a raw and urgent will to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~madness":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession and emotional turbulence have revealed themselves as madness; the speaker now sees what they once couldn't about love holding them afloat.","opus":"A lover unable to purge obsessive memories of an ex, framing the fights and yearning as a kind of insanity, and finally admitting through the haze that what he calls madness is simply that he needs her love.","sonnet":"A love song wrestling with whether an obsessive, turbulent relationship is genuine feeling or just shared insanity keeping two incompatible people together; the narrator finally admits he needs the other person despite all the damage.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~map-of-the-problematique":{"haiku":"Despair, isolation, and human failure to see shared suffering; loneliness as an endless condition.","opus":"Bellamy channeling isolation and dread into a plea that we all bleed the same, that no one will take the blame for a collapsing world. Beneath the paranoia it is really about loneliness and the wish for it to end.","sonnet":"Loneliness and despair tighten their grip since meeting someone who offered relief but has left the speaker unable to get things right, with flashes of life's brevity underscoring a desperate wish for the isolation to end.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~megalomania":{"haiku":"Grandiose power fantasies and need for absolute control.","opus":"A restless meditation on paradise coming at a price the narrator won't pay, questioning what humanity was even built for and treating a partner or creation as a useless device he tires of. Beneath the grandiosity runs a chilling recognition that the disguise he addresses conceals himself. Muse, Matt Bellamy's theatrical British rock band, close their debut with churning organ and apocalyptic unease, the title naming the delusion of control the song both indulges and interrogates.","sonnet":"Paradise is offered and refused because the price is too high — the speaker questions what humans were built for, notes that the other person can neither have children nor accept gifts, and strips away disguises to find only a mirror. Bleak, circular, and unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~mercy":{"haiku":"Plea for compassion; asking for grace and forgiveness.","opus":"A cry from someone collapsing within, pursued by robed figures and shadowy authorities, begging to be pulled out of oppression and the apparatus of death. Muse set individual powerlessness against vanished deities and voiceless string-pullers. From Drones, it continues the album's tale of a mind toyed with by hidden operators, Bellamy's appeal pitched against the band's vast, Queen-scaled arena drama.","sonnet":"A cry against invisible governance — puppet masters in cloaks, absent gods, silent tyranny, a world hypnotised by successive manipulators. The singer has tried to change the game from inside and is losing ground, begging to be rescued from powers that the world itself refuses to name. The desperation is genuine; the rescue is nowhere coming.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~micro-cuts":{"haiku":"A person perceives manipulation and control in their relationship, with imagery of pain, blame, and psychological torment delivered through words and invasive thoughts.","opus":"A falsetto scream of paranoia and betrayal, blaming another for destroying the puppet strings binding their souls and driving him insane. Sensory-torture imagery, blades in the brain, forks on plates, blackboard scratches, renders psychological cruelty as literal, unbearable noise.","sonnet":"A short, intense portrait of psychological attack — someone's blame and screaming has become a blade in the brain, a blackboard-scratch of hate that destroys the speaker's sense of self and severs the connection to their own soul.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~mk-ultra":{"haiku":"Coercive manipulation and deception break down the mind until it collapses; histories are erased and invisible forces strip control and consciousness away.","opus":"Named for the CIA's mind-control program, this is Muse's paranoia anthem about coercion breaking through the mind, replacing love with fear and erasing history. Control slipping away becomes the terrifying point.","sonnet":"A song about psychological manipulation and mind control — the narrator describes invisible forces breaking through mental barriers, replacing love with fear, and history being erased, while the collective voice underneath admits they are losing control of their own minds.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~muscle-museum":{"haiku":"Objectification and display of physical form; body as artifact.","opus":"A song of unreciprocated yearning and obstinate self-reliance, aimed at someone too distracted to register a confession until the chance has passed, who'd cherish or dismiss it purely on a whim. The narrator insists he demands far more than they could offer and will manage alone, having ground through the smallest venues to prove himself. Muse made this an early calling card, its wounded pride hinting at their later grandeur.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who gave too little too late — the speaker needed more than conditional attention, performed everywhere without recognition, and now refuses to wait any longer. The declaration of independence is quiet but total: whatever comes next will happen alone.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~new-born":{"haiku":"The song portrays emotional corruption growing like a newborn in someone soulless, describing bitterness fed by witnessing too much too young. The plea is to break silence before drifting away.","opus":"Bellamy's fear of humanity dissolving into technology and estrangement, some monstrous thing swelling beyond control as the soul leaks out of everything. Written in his youth, it warns of a world witnessed too early and in too much excess, pulling people away from genuine connection.","sonnet":"A song about the painful birth of disillusionment — someone has been overexposed to the world's soullessness too young, and they are now swollen with bitterness and untethered from home, with the narrator urging them to break the silence before they drift beyond reach entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~overdue":{"haiku":"The narrator expects repayment for past mistakes and absence; he claims he is owed compensation for the wrongs committed against him.","opus":"A raw early Muse b-side, this snaps back at a partner who demanded presence during an absence and won't forget it. The narrator refuses to shoulder blame for someone else's mistakes, insisting on being paid what he's owed and asserting youthful arousal amid the resentment. Terse and repetitive, it reads as petulant self-defense, care and grievance stated in the same breath.","sonnet":"A young speaker refuses to absorb the guilt of someone who resents his absence, insisting the resentment is disproportionate to his actual culpability. The emotional standoff is mutual and unresolved — he claims to care while simultaneously demanding that caring be recognised as insufficient grounds for perpetual debt.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~panic-station":{"haiku":"A call to abandon doubt and embrace chaos; the speaker urges breaking free from restraint to unleash wild desire and impulse without fear or compromise.","opus":"A funk-driven pep talk toward reckless self-liberation, urging you to sacrifice caution, seize your own power and do exactly what you want with no one left to appease as chaos hits its peak.","sonnet":"About surrendering to chaos and desire rather than doubt — an urgent call to commit fully, unleash the wildest fantasy, do exactly what you want without appeasing anyone, arriving at a place of total untamed fire where nothing holds you back.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~plug-in-baby":{"haiku":"Exposed betrayal and corrupted innocence. The plug-in baby destroys enemies and tired virgins alike, used as a tool of revenge against treachery and worn-out love.","opus":"Bellamy turns a soured relationship into science-fiction paranoia, where a lover has been swapped for something artificial and idealized; the real subject is the sterile ache of connection replaced by a manufactured, exhausting substitute.","sonnet":"The narrator has seen through a lover's deception and now wants to cleanse himself of her influence entirely, declaring that his 'plug in baby'—some source of power or weapon—will destroy what she represented. The tone is cold, liberating, and final.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~psycho":{"haiku":"Unhinged obsession and psychological breakdown.","opus":"A drill-instructor power fantasy in which a commander grinds a recruit into an unquestioning killer while washing his hands of the outcome, rewiring a mind the way malware hijacks a computer. The barked call-and-response and combat taunts tip it toward militarized menace. Muse built this on a long-shelved riff, its crude refrain a flat condemnation of how blind obedience gets engineered.","sonnet":"Military conditioning used as a metaphor for emotional domination — a voice of command strips a person of autonomy, reprogramming them into an instrument of violence and erasing the question of responsibility. The horror is how completely the subject consents.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~reapers":{"haiku":"Death and harvest; inevitable doom collecting its due.","opus":"A first-person charge against drone warfare: one's own home turned into a slaughter ground, a target fixed on the chest, existence snuffed out from afar while the world backs the killers armed with the CIA and their fleet of hunter aircraft. The disposable narrator is pushed into radicalization. Muse make the album's title menace literal, welding a shredding guitar spotlight to a protest against faceless, automated killing.","sonnet":"Remote-controlled killing as a political radicalisation trigger — the home becomes a killing field, the crosshair is literal, the CIA and its drones are named as the machinery of dehumanisation. The speaker starts the song as collateral and ends it as someone changed by what has been done to him. War has moved up a gear and taken his compliance with it.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~resistance":{"haiku":"Two lovers hide their forbidden love from an oppressive regime, understanding that their relationship and devotion to each other is an act of resistance.","opus":"Two lovers conceal a forbidden attachment from a watchful regime, keeping silent and treating love itself as an act of defiance against a power intent on wearing them down. Drawing on Orwell, Muse frame the affair amid mind-policing and collapsing walls, the jittery refrain torn over whether their rebellion is a mistake or should have been permitted all along.","sonnet":"Two people conduct a forbidden love in secret, aware that external forces — surveillance, thought police, the machinery of control — are working to extinguish them. The choice to persist anyway reframes love itself as the only meaningful act of political defiance available.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~revolt":{"haiku":"Uprising and rebellion; refusing to accept the status quo.","opus":"An exhortation to the downtrodden, acknowledging oppression, brain conditioning and freedom held on loan by machines, then insisting the listener has the strength, soul and love to grow and remake the world. Muse pivot from Drones' despair to an uplift here, a deliberately anthemic pep-talk that trusts the individual can escape the maze and rise against the drones controlling everything.","sonnet":"A call to resist a world of drones, suppressed truth, and conditioning — but the energy is motivational rather than bitter, addressed to someone still capable of strength, growth, soul. The song knows the maze is real while insisting a way out exists. It is less a diagnosis than a hand extended across a toxic jungle.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~ruled-by-secrecy":{"haiku":"Hidden power operates through repression and manipulation; people work hard but never control their own destiny as shadowy forces rule from above.","opus":"A whispered reflection on concealed authority, unseen operators lurking in every corner while ordinary laborers toil unrewarded and their dying fuels other people's triumphs. Cycles of crushing and quieting spin on while nobody can name who is actually in charge. Closing Muse's Absolution, the track swaps grandeur for creeping dread, its soft piano underscoring a world where power is invisible and quietly consuming.","sonnet":"Hidden forces suppress and manipulate from the shadows, washing their hands of the damage they cause while those beneath them labour under the illusion of agency. Nobody can identify who holds the reins — power operates through concealment, and its victims are kept too busy surviving to look up and see.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~save-me":{"haiku":"The narrator seeks transformation into someone else and demands rescue from their toxic patterns; they feel worthless without another person's intervention.","opus":"A desperate plea to a lover for rescue and transformation: freed from a soul's affliction yet convinced he's hated and nothing without them, the narrator begs them to run away with him, remake him in their image, and never let go.","sonnet":"A plea for rescue from someone drowning in their own superstitions and self-doubt — asking a lover to transform them, take them somewhere new, and hold on tight before they slip away entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~screenager":{"haiku":"A person has become phony and forgotten their true self beneath scars and performance. The song mourns who they once were before damage.","opus":"Early Muse on the slow erasure of self behind screens and image, mourning who someone used to be as connection thins into performance and the private wounds go unseen by even their own family.","sonnet":"Someone who has hidden behind screens and performance until they've lost the person they actually were — those who love them can barely recognize them now, and the song mourns who they used to be.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~showbiz":{"haiku":"Performers are forced to expose their darkness and scream others' dreams, trapped into self-destruction by pressures to please and deliver visions of greed.","opus":"The title track anxiety of a young band watching the industry it wanted swallow it, framed as a controlling lover who forces you to perform someone else's greed. It's about losing your own soul to another's ambition and the machinery of fame.","sonnet":"A rage against external control — forces the narrator can't name have been manipulating feelings and desires for too long, making him dream and scream their dreams and screams, pushing everyone toward self-destruction in the service of someone else's greed.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~sing-for-absolution":{"haiku":"Someone mourns a beautiful person they can only dream of reaching. Truth and sin remain hidden and die within, unrectified and unforgiven.","opus":"A forbidden, hidden love creeps through darkness and faint starlight while the singer asks for pardon even as he tumbles from grace, his sins never set right and buried spirits that will stay buried. Muse, on Absolution, pair Matthew Bellamy's operatic falsetto with end-times dread, turning private guilt into sweeping, cinematic despair without any promise of rescue.","sonnet":"A love kept entirely secret, watched from a distance and carried in dreams, leaves its bearer without absolution or release. The truth buried inside burns without ever surfacing; the only response is to sing it into the air and fall from whatever grace was once imagined — the wounds stay unrectified, the souls unexhumed.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~sober":{"haiku":"Alcohol is personified as the only thing keeping the speaker emotionally alive and unfrozen, yet the substance itself threatens betrayal and destruction. The song explores dependence on a toxic substance for emotional survival.","opus":"Addresses alcohol as an intimate, personified companion of blended whiskeys and single malts that both comforts and turns on the speaker, the solid burn inside being the only thing keeping him unfrozen.","sonnet":"A song addressing whisky and bourbon directly — Royal Canadian blend, Wild Turkey, Jameson, single malts — as the sole things that keep the narrator from freezing, even as they burn inside and are destined to turn on him.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~soldier-s-poem":{"haiku":"A soldier sends their life away in service to an unjust war and questions whether they deserve freedom, seeing no justice in the world.","opus":"A dying soldier's embittered address to the rulers who dispatched him far from where he belonged to fight a cause he knows is unjust. The spine of it is a crushing verdict on the powerful, that they haven't earned the liberty others perish for, and that fairness has never existed anywhere in this world.","sonnet":"A soldier's bitter lament directed at political leaders: the narrator would still give his life for them, but doubts whether those who sent him to fight a wrong war deserve the freedom they claim to be defending.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~space-dementia":{"haiku":"A lover's obsessive attachment to someone who manipulates and torments them drives both toward destruction and meaninglessness.","opus":"An obsessive love spirals into cosmic annihilation, adoration curdling into sickness, twisted games, and a wish to destroy the world for the beloved. Peace arrives only as it tears the pair apart and renders them meaningless. From Muse's Origin of Symmetry, the piece marries Rachmaninoff-scale piano to Bellamy's operatic extremity, staging devotion as a disorienting, self-erasing madness among the stars.","sonnet":"An obsessive, self-destructive love warps into something almost cosmic in its destructive pull. Adoration and sickness become indistinguishable, the beloved both worshipped and resented as a force that unmakes the narrator — a relationship so total it would consume the world just to feel real.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~starlight":{"haiku":"A person pursues an unattainable dream, fleeing from those who care for them. They seek connection and meaning through an impossible goal, questioning if the struggle is worth continuing.","opus":"Aboard a ship carrying him away, the speaker chases a distant starlight and clings to a love he refuses to let go of, pleading that it never fade away.","sonnet":"The narrator is on a journey that carries him away from people who care about him, and he fixates on one person he longs to hold — chasing a distant light that may not be worth the pursuit, but unable to stop or let that connection fade.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~stockholm-syndrome": {"haiku":"A cycle of emotional attachment mixed with pain and abandonment, repeating patterns of hurt and hopelessness without escape.","opus":"A tangled, destructive bond in which the narrator refuses to stand in the way of another's hatred and anger as they burn out together. He repeatedly vows this is the last time he will abandon and forget the other, wishing he could.","sonnet":"A relationship in which cycles of hatred, abandonment, and desperate reconnection repeat without resolution, with the narrator vowing — each time — that this will be the last time he forgets or leaves, yet doubting he can keep that promise.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title diagnoses the bond before a note lands, and Bellamy sings it from the captor's side — permissive, almost gracious, stepping aside so the other's hatred and anger can rise as if that were a gift, grooming the captive's rage into fuel. The chorus vow of last abandonment and last forgetting collapses in its own final breath with the admission that he only wishes he could: the last-time promise is the syndrome itself, spoken so the return can follow. The cruelest movement is the verses sliding into past tense mid-song, the other person receding into someone who once had a name.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","surveillance & control","anger & defiance"],"means":"the captor steps aside so the captive's rage can rise as if that were a gift","built":"the last-time vow collapses in its own breath; the syndrome names itself in the title"},
"muse~sunburn":{"haiku":"Painful exposure and vulnerability; burned by closeness.","opus":"A star-struck, anxious obsession with a woman whose radiance overwhelms and singes the narrator's future amid glossy corporate performances and pervasive dishonesty, leaving him cowering and fleeing his own shame. From Muse's debut Showbiz, this piano-led early single set the template for the band's grandeur, pairing a classically shaded riff with a love that's both blinding and shot through with guilt.","sonnet":"A woman burns like the sun — irresistible and destructive — while the speaker watches, riven by guilt about something unspoken. She sneers privately at the corporate spectacle, cries in secret, and he cannot look away even as she scorches the horizon. He ends up hiding behind a broken frame, running from shame, unable to name what he did.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~supermassive-black-hole":{"haiku":"A person is caught in a powerful, inescapable attraction to someone superficial who has ignited their passion, using cosmic imagery to describe being pulled inexorably toward this lover.","opus":"A powerless pull toward a shallow, dishonest lover who traps the singer through deception; the craving is compared to being dragged unstoppably toward an immense cosmic void. Muse ride the metaphor over a strutting, falsetto-driven groove.","sonnet":"A narrator describes being helplessly drawn in by a deceitful, superficial person whose hold over them is as consuming and inescapable as a supermassive black hole.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~supremacy":{"haiku":"Need for dominance and superiority; control as validation.","opus":"A defiant threat hurled at a controlling establishment, warning that real freedom is a mirage while heavy-handed policy crushes the bold, surveillance corrupts the young, and merit goes unrewarded, before declaring it's time to bring the whole regime down. Muse open The 2nd Law with Bond-theme grandeur, orchestra and choir behind a surf riff, their anti-authority stance dressed in cinematic excess.","sonnet":"A short, sharp ultimatum to a power that has suppressed bravery, embedded agents in institutions, and brainwashed the young into cruelty. The emancipation sold to ordinary people is declared fantasy. The speaker has identified the target and announces that the time to destroy its supremacy has come — nothing more elaborate than that, the fury compressed into a single confrontation.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~survival":{"haiku":"Competition drives the speaker to victory at any cost; they refuse to lose and choose survival through dominance over compassion.","opus":"A ferocious, near-tyrannical anthem of competitive will, life boiled down to a contest the narrator swears to take by any means, flaunting his power to all of humankind and refusing mercy because retribution belongs to him. The pounding chants of battle and victory cast endurance as merciless domination over anyone in his path.","sonnet":"A maximalist, operatic declaration of ruthless competitive will — the speaker will not be overtaken, will not forgive, and will expose their strength to the world at any cost, the song written as a kind of athletic warrior hymn with no irony.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~take-a-bow":{"haiku":"Contempt for a faker or false friend; seeing through performative kindness.","opus":"A blistering damnation of a rotten leader, tallying the wreckage they leave behind and consigning them to eternal fire for their crimes against the planet, even as liberty devours itself. The Black Holes opener climbs from pulsing synth to full apocalyptic roar, Muse's political fury at its most grand and merciless.","sonnet":"A curse levelled at a corrupt leader who has bent a country to his will through dark power and deliberate destruction — the address is relentless and specific in its contempt, naming death, destruction, and crimes against the earth, and promising only hellfire as the fitting end for someone who traded freedom for control.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~the-2nd-law-isolated-system":{"opus":"Muse closed The 2nd Law with this piece, extending the album's thermodynamics conceit into a spare instrumental coda. Entropy only increases in an isolated system, so all things decay and nothing sustains itself forever, offered as bleak science rather than melodrama. Bellamy's usual bombast gives way to sampled newscaster fragments over ambient dread, civilizational collapse stated with clinical calm.","sonnet":"The second law of thermodynamics applied as existential metaphor — entropy only increases in an isolated system, meaning all things decay and nothing can sustain itself forever, presented as a bleak but scientific statement about civilizational or personal collapse.","src":"sonnet"},
"muse~the-globalist":{"haiku":"Authoritarian power corrupts absolutely; the speaker weaponizes loneliness into dominion over a fractured world.","opus":"Bellamy's sequel to an earlier apocalypse, a lonely, unloved figure who seizes power and razes every nation, only to grieve the emptiness he made. The chilling turn is that all the destruction traces back to a starved need to be loved.","sonnet":"A desolate monologue from a figure who was told he was unloved and unprotected, and so built himself into a god-like destroyer — accumulating power, dismantling countries, detonating everything — only to find himself alone at the end of a scorched world, having confused dominance for the love he was actually seeking. The revelation is that the entire apocalypse was a grieving person looking for someone to need them.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~the-small-print": {"haiku":"A predatory figure exploits and manipulates others, selling false promises and memories for profit while bending truth and enslaving the gullible to serve personal ambition.","opus":"A menacing bargain voiced by a devil-like figure who buys up a person's good memories and soul, promising ruin, insanity, and eternal servitude while mocking the emptiness of faith.","sonnet":"A manipulator's monologue: the speaker offers greed, bends the truth, sells memories, and demands total submission, positioning himself as a self-described unpaid priest who takes everything while blaming the victim for the life they lose.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Faustian sales pitch from the devil's side of the desk, fusing two vocabularies until they are one: commerce (memories leased back at fifteen pounds a year, greed compensated in broken hearts) and religion (a slave clause running to the grave, a soul signed away). The catch is the title — ruinous terms hidden in the small print of a contract that sounds like salvation, pitched by a con-man in clerical drag who admits it outright, styling himself a priest God never paid. The one mercy he grants — you keep the bad days while he sells only the good — is the cruelest term of all, since the good days are what made a life worth anything.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","money & the street"],"means":"the cruelest term: he keeps the bad days while the devil takes only the good","built":"commerce and religion vocabularies fused until they are one contract"},
"muse~thoughts-of-a-dying-atheist":{"haiku":"Facing mortality without faith terrifies an atheist who senses death approaching with nothing to believe in. They encounter unexplained mysteries that frighten them as the end looms.","opus":"Muse dramatize a nonbeliever's panic at death's approach, haunted by a lost presence and unsettling sounds that ruin his rest as realities blur around him. Peering out with no faith to lean on and no promise of anything after, he confesses the ending is all he can picture and it terrifies him. The title's irony sharpens the fear: disbelief offers no shelter.","sonnet":"A non-believer confronts death without the comfort of faith and finds only terror. A presence haunts the room — perhaps memory, perhaps anticipation — while the narrator admits that the end is all he can see. Without a beyond to orient toward, the approaching void offers nothing but honest, unmediated fear.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~time-is-running-out": {"haiku":"A person feels asphyxiated by an addictive relationship, unable to bury their pain as time runs out while the other person drains them.","opus":"An addictive, suffocating love framed as mutual destruction—'you will be the death of me'—where the singer is trapped, bound and unable to give up the object of his fixation, refusing to let this dangerous thing be buried even as their time runs out.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship the narrator knows is destroying him but cannot leave — he is addicted to someone who drains and suffocates him, and he refuses to let the truth of it be buried even as time runs out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about wanting the thing that is suffocating you: the verses admit the addiction outright — freedom claimed while bound, escape attempted and abandoned — inside a vocabulary of drowning, smothering and burial that makes the affair a slow killing he is complicit in. The pivot is the refusal: the one act of will left to him is keeping the buried thing — the feeling, or the truth about what this relationship is — above ground and screaming, which is why the title cuts both ways, a doomed affair and an apocalypse sharing the same shrinking clock. The wordless bridge, all rising vowels around the single plain question of how it came to this, is the tell: the argument has run past language into pure panic.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"the one act of will left is keeping the buried thing screaming","built":"wordless bridge of rising vowels marks where the argument runs past language into panic"},
"muse~undisclosed-desires":{"haiku":"Seduction and psychological penetration; the narrator seeks to exorcise a partner's demons and satisfy hidden desires. Promise of release and recognition beneath their mask.","opus":"A tender promise to a hurt, defensive lover, offering to comfort them, look past the guard they wear, and banish the ghosts of what they endured. The narrator wants to settle the cruelty lodged in their heart and answer the longings they keep hidden.","sonnet":"The speaker reaches out to a damaged, self-disguising partner, offering to heal old wounds, see past the mask, and satisfy the hidden desires they keep buried. The tone moves between tenderness and intensity as the speaker insists they can reach what the other person will not show.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~unintended":{"haiku":"Unexpected emotional connection that goes deeper than intended.","opus":"An early, unguarded ballad offering hesitant devotion from someone still repairing the ruins of a prior romance, presenting himself as an accidental yet lasting partner once he finishes sorting through the debris of a bond that fell short. From the band's debut, it is a fragile fingerpicked vow made before their later grandeur.","sonnet":"A love that arrived by accident and proved itself better than anything that came before — the speaker is still mid-repair, picking up the pieces of a life broken by someone who could never measure up to this unexpected person. The commitment is tentative in timing but absolute in feeling.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~united-states-of-eurasia-collateral-damage":{"haiku":"Endless wars cannot be won and people blindly follow rulers without caring who started them or who is to blame.","opus":"A sweeping anti-war statement asking who truly controls things as unwinnable conflicts drag on and ordinary people are marched off to answer for charges never proven. The narrator wonders whether obedience is inescapable while the legends of old crumble. Muse pile on Queen-scale drama and an Orwell nod, closing with a Chopin nocturne that quietly undercuts the spectacle.","sonnet":"A geopolitical lament strips war of its justifications, showing ordinary people punished for crimes they never committed under leaders whose conflicts achieve nothing. The narrator imagines the eventual collapse of borders into a single united superstate, questioning whether the wars were ever really about anything beyond the hunger to dominate.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~unnatural-selection":{"haiku":"The privileged laugh as the unlucky fall; chance, not merit, determines fate; the speaker hungers for unrest and truth, tired of false hope and random destruction.","opus":"Bellamy's call to arms against a rigged system where the lucky few thrive and fate is fixed, hungry for unrest beyond peaceful protest. The title inverts Darwin: survival decided not by nature but by injustice, and the plea is simply for the truth against the manufactured chosen ones.","sonnet":"A rejection of both organized religion and comforting social myths, paired with a call for genuine unrest — the narrator wants to shatter the illusion that fate or the 'special chosen' will save anyone, and insists that humanity's segregation and passivity are a self-inflicted trap.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~uno":{"haiku":"Solitary existence and being the only one; loneliness.","opus":"A contemptuous brush-off aimed at someone who squandered their opportunity, told again and again that they mean nothing while the singer jeers that they might have topped everything and commanded the planet if only they'd made the effort. From Muse's debut Showbiz, it turns adolescent resentment into a jealous, sneering address, the trio's early sound leaner and nastier than the arena scale to come.","sonnet":"A cold post-mortem on a wasted potential — you could have been first, could have ruled everything, and you threw it away. The speaker oscillates between contempt and something that sounds almost like grief for what the two of them might have had together. The final indictment is that the other person still does not understand what they squandered.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~uprising":{"haiku":"Mass control through media, drugs, and propaganda; the singer calls for revolution against systems that dumb down the population and trap people in greed, urging unified resistance.","opus":"Bellamy's stadium-sized revolt against manufactured consent, media manipulation and corporate greed keeping people docile and afraid, a call to reclaim power from the elites, its glam-rock stomp turning conspiracy dread into collective defiance.","sonnet":"A song about mass awakening against pharmaceutical pacification, media control, and corporate power — the narrator calls on people to seize power back from those who dumb them down and keep them afraid, insisting the revolution is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"muse~won-t-stand-down":{"haiku":"Defiance and refusal to compromise; standing firm against pressure.","opus":"An anthem of turning the tables on an abuser, the narrator naming the gaslighting, counting the lies, and refusing to be owned any longer as he mounts a psychological counterattack and tells his tormentor to die alone. From Will of the People, it's Muse channeling nu-metal aggression into a self-empowerment war cry, riff-heavy and vengeful rather than their usual cosmic paranoia.","sonnet":"The moment the abused party stops absorbing damage and starts fighting back — the speaker has counted the lies, named the gaslighting for what it was, and now maps a counterattack that is psychological and total. The resolve is not just to survive but to outlast and undo the person who pushed them under.","src":"haiku"},
"muzz-cammie-robinson~star-glide":{"haiku":"Transformation and ascension break free from regret, with the speaker reclaiming broken wings and learning to fly anew.","opus":"A triumphant surge of taking flight against thunder and the weight of regret overhead, angels descending to mend what's damaged. Setting the sky alight and coming alive, it turns bone-deep fear into a climb toward a hard-won paradise and reclaimed throne.","sonnet":"About finding transcendence through another person's presence — weighed down by regret and dark days, the speaker discovers that this relationship gives them the ability to rise above it all, metaphorically burning through the sky and spreading wings.","src":"haiku"},
"muzz~back-to-you":{"haiku":"Through darkness and falling, a force returns again and again; presence endures where words fail.","opus":"A hushed vow of return and reassurance offered to someone falling, promising to rise back through the noise. Built almost entirely on repetition, it works as comfort by insistence, sound ascending where words run out.","sonnet":"A gentle but persistent assurance to someone who keeps falling apart — the speaker promises they will always return to this person, that even when their vision slips and they hit bottom, a presence rises through the noise to come back; it's devotion framed as cyclical rescue.","src":"haiku"},
"muzz~new-age-bossfight-remix":{"opus":"A futurist manifesto welcoming a new technological dawn, with citizens of a redefined future casting off regret and old shadows in favor of fresh possibilities and currencies.","sonnet":"A generic techno-optimist declaration celebrating the dawn of a new technological era — humanity reimagined as citizens of a self-defined future, shedding the past and embracing new possibilities without grief or regret.","src":"sonnet"},
"muzz~pressure-on-the-masses":{"opus":"An authoritarian nightmare rendered as a suffocating clock: start the timer, the iron hand of power tightens, lungs give out under oppression. The command to listen becomes a warning about a grip that embraces even as it strangles.","sonnet":"A French-language industrial image of suffocation under authoritarian control — the clock ticks, the lungs fill and fail, and an iron-fisted power grips and squeezes while the listener is instructed to simply pay attention to what is being done to them.","src":"sonnet","fable":"Delivered entirely in French, this is a countdown to asphyxiation staged as a bulletin: a clock is started, a timer armed, and while the seconds drain away the breath of an anonymous crowd fails under a force the song keeps naming as more than any body can withstand. The threat is stripped of a face and rebuilt as machinery — the iron fist, authority already installed, the metronome pulse — so that domination reads less like a tyrant than like weather or gravity, ambient and impersonal. The chill turns on a single paradox the lyric will not resolve: the closing hold is also an embrace, control disguised as tenderness, the same arm that pins you offered as comfort. Against all of it the masses receive one order, repeated until it swallows every other word — listen — which here can only mean submit, so the track ends not as protest but as the finished product of the pressure it describes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"domination made ambient and impersonal as gravity rather than tyranny","built":"the closing embrace and the controlling arm share a single gesture, irresolvable"},
"muzz~ready-for-it":{"haiku":"Mutual predation disguised as romance; the speaker and lover both steal and trap each other in a vendetta masquerading as devotion.","opus":"A cover of Taylor Swift's fantasy of two romantic outlaws—a killer and a robber who recognize themselves in each other and plot to run off together. Beneath the game-playing bravado is genuine devotion, past loves declared failures beside this one.","sonnet":"A cover of Taylor Swift's song, presenting a narrator who is immediately attracted to a dangerous, emotionally elusive man — she knows he has left women haunted before but decides to match his energy and trap him right back, choosing the chase and the risk with full awareness.","src":"haiku"},
"muzz~spectrum-futurebound-remix":{"haiku":"An invitation to step out of shadow and darkness into an undefined space of refuge or transcendence.","opus":"A drum-and-bass reworking that thins its source to a mantra: an unseen watcher who has always found the singer wanting, met with an invitation to dismiss the shadows and step inside. Futurebound, a mainstay of the UK bass scene, treats the sparse phrases less as narrative than as texture, letting the plea for surrender loop until it dissolves into pulse.","sonnet":"A voice calls someone out of darkness and inward retreat, promising shelter if they relent. The plea is less an invitation than a gentle insistence — whatever overshadows this person can dissolve, and companionship waits on the other side of their resistance.","src":"haiku"},
"muzz~star-glide":{"haiku":"A triumphant journey of overcoming struggle and rising above hardship, with a companion guiding the narrator to transcendence. The song celebrates breaking free and achieving flight through perseverance.","opus":"Facing storms and regrets, the speaker draws strength from a supportive presence and imagines coming alive, spreading wings, and rising toward paradise.","sonnet":"A figure burdened by regret and dark days draws strength from someone by their side, building toward a sense of empowerment and liberation — the imagery of spreading wings and burning through the sky standing for rising above what held them down.","src":"haiku"},
"mvko~nowhere-to-grow":{"haiku":"Sensory deprivation and self-destruction spiral as the speaker carves escape into walls, suffocating in madness while homelessness and internal rot accelerate toward final dissolution.","opus":"Claustrophobic despair from a mind that feels imprisoned, rotting in isolation and cycling through suicidal exhaustion, self-hatred, and disillusion with everyone around him. The title's homelessness is spiritual as much as literal. Aggressive trap-metal that fuses genuine anguish with menace, tracking someone losing himself and coming to loathe the scene he once believed in.","sonnet":"A speaker locked in mental and physical stagnation — carving lines in walls, serotonin rotting away, becoming everything they said they wouldn't be — cycles through self-destruction and rage, finding neither home nor direction, only the endless nowhere.","src":"haiku"},
"my-bloody-valentine~when-you-sleep":{"haiku":"Doubt in another person's reality and skepticism about love, cycling through dismissal and fear of abandonment.","opus":"A hazy infatuation blurs what is real, the narrator drawn down and taken even as he confesses he doesn't believe in the person who makes him laugh. My Bloody Valentine, whose 1991 Loveless perfected the woozy blur of shoegaze, bury the ambivalent longing under glide-guitar drift, the words dissolving into texture until desire and doubt become indistinguishable.","sonnet":"Glimpsed through a haze of disbelief and contradictory feeling, a person barely coalesces into someone real. Laughter and absence alternate without pattern; the promise carried in the phrase heard at night dissolves by morning, and what remains is the persistent, dreamy uncertainty of whether any of it was ever true.","src":"haiku","fable":"Loveless famously buries Kevin Shields' vocals beneath the guitar's melting glide, and this song is the case where that submersion becomes the meaning. The words surface only in half-legible fragments — a laugh, a departure, a toss of pale hair — because they are being remembered rather than lived. The lover is present chiefly as afterimage, contradicted mid-thought, an oath of marriage met at once by disbelief, and the track circles the same few phrases across two near-identical verses framing the middle, so desire keeps arriving back at the same unresolved doubt about what is even real. The repeated image of sleeping tomorrow sets the whole thing at one remove from waking consciousness. That is why it feels less like an address to a person than the sensation of an infatuation dissolving into pure texture as you try to hold it.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","nostalgia & memory","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"infatuation dissolving into pure texture as it is held","built":"vocals buried under guitar — submersion enacting memory over presence"},
"my-chemical-romance~famous-last-words":{"haiku":"A person refuses to be controlled or left by a lover, choosing self-determination and independence over seeking approval or being held back by another's will.","opus":"A defiant vow of survival and self-liberation, the speaker declaring he isn't scared to keep going or to face the world by himself, and that nothing anyone says will stop him. Addressed to someone he can't hold onto, it reads as a choice to push through darkness and brokenness instead of quitting, an anthem against surrender.","sonnet":"About choosing to keep living after reaching a breaking point — the narrator can't make someone stay, is incomplete and weak, but declares unequivocally that they are not afraid to keep going alone, using the act of speaking these words as proof that they are awake and unafraid, not yet dead.","src":"haiku"},
"my-chemical-romance~the-ghost-of-you":{"haiku":"Romanticized death memorializes absence; spectral presence persists to haunt the living.","opus":"A wartime elegy for someone who died in the narrator's arms, wired into the band's obsession with grief and death. The haunting is the point: love that can't come home, only linger as scars and phantom smiles.","sonnet":"A speaker haunted by the loss of someone who is never coming back relives the moment of her death in his arms and cannot reconcile the absence — the ghost of her presence is everywhere and offers no comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"my-darkest-days~casual-sex":{"haiku":"Sexual attachment without commitment appeals as liberation; bodies intertwine while emotions remain cordoned off.","opus":"A no-strings arrangement sold as freedom, both parties insisting nothing about it is wrong. The insistence is the tell, protesting a bit too hard that there are no feelings and no chance of getting hurt.","sonnet":"Two people negotiate a purely physical arrangement with no strings, no obligations, and no feelings — both insisting they prefer it this way, though the song's enthusiasm barely conceals a longing for something more.","src":"haiku"},
"my-dead-girlfriend~kanojo-ga-tsumetaku-warattara":{"opus":"Cold May rain on a remote parking lot mirrors a small stain of grief on a dead girl's chest; alone he can still recall her, and cruelly reasons that if he could only lose her, anyone could replace her—yet what he can't forgive is failing to notice her shy, slightly crooked smile, and being unable to return to that day he might still reach her.","src":"opus"},
"my-dead-girlfriend~kanojo-ga-tsumetaku-warattara-prologue-to-the-nine-stages-of-change-at-the-deceased-remains":{"haiku":"Rain-soaked memory surfaces in a remote parking lot where a small mark on her chest holds entire universes of feeling. Distance grows though they inhabit the same time; eventually even this stain will fade, yet he cannot return to before they met.","opus":"Over a mountain parking lot in cold May rain, the speaker mourns a girl now reduced to a small stain fading from his chest, insisting that if he could only lose the memory, anyone could replace her (and him). Her shy, slightly crooked smile went unnoticed until it was too late, and he cannot return to that day, only travel to meet the version of her he can still call up.","sonnet":"In a cold May rain in a mountain parking lot, a small stain on the beloved's chest bleeds through memory: alone, the speaker can still recall her; together, they were always deflated; either one could be replaced — yet the image of her faint, crooked smile, unnoticed at the time, is the thing they cannot return to.","src":"haiku"},
"my-enemies-i~black-sheep":{"haiku":"Existential despair and isolation define the outcast who has surrendered hope, broadcasting their final message before disappearing into void.","opus":"A dispatch of total surrender, the outcast signing off from a world he's given up on in radio-static farewells while spiraling downward.","sonnet":"The Black Sheep Boy signs out: sick, healthy, dead, alive, brave, useless all at once — a man whose life has collapsed and who transmits his surrender on a frequency he hopes someone can copy, though the bullshit spirals and no one seems to receive him.","src":"haiku"},
"my-enemies-i~perfect":{"haiku":"Self-loathing metastasizes into self-destruction; the speaker tears at themselves, unworthy of love, content to burn rather than burden others.","opus":"Savage self-abasement in a broken relationship, the singer casting himself as a worthless parasite dragging his flawless partner down. Bitter and suicidal, he begs for the knife and the flame he thinks he deserves while resenting the one who wasn't there when he broke.","sonnet":"Self-loathing erupts toward someone the speaker helped through hardship who was never there in return: the speaker is a parasite, a shadow, worthless — \"burn me alive, I deserve it\" — while the other is framed as perfect, sharpening the asymmetry into something corrosive.","src":"haiku"},
"my-favourite-nemesis~diabolic":{"haiku":"Pain and suffering feel inevitable and eternal; the speaker confronts their condescending self-awareness within cyclical patterns of harm.","opus":"A brooding standoff with an unchangeable outcome, where reasons to stay or leave collapse into the same result and old resentments refuse to die. Pain is framed as a passing state yet somehow permanent. Metalcore rumination on the futility of hate and the impossibility of rewriting the past, always one step behind what it dreads.","sonnet":"A relationship dissected in cold terms: neither staying nor leaving changes the outcome, some things will always last, and the repeated line \"I'm just stating the fact\" refuses emotional escalation even as the burn of faith taking its turn makes feeling unavoidable — the diabolic quality is in the condescension that makes hatred feel like logic.","src":"haiku"},
"my-first-story~a-134y7io":{"opus":"MY FIRST STORY built their catalog on wounded reconciliation, and this addressed apology is a clear instance. One careless lie destroyed what two people had, and the narrator has spent thousands of solitary nights rehearsing the unsayable. The song lands on a remembered promise to sing together someday, never once forgotten, and a plain thank-you that turns out to be the whole message underneath the noise.","sonnet":"A reconciliation song addressed to an estranged person: one careless lie destroyed what they had, and the narrator has spent thousands of nights alone rehearsing what they couldn't say — the song closes with the memory of a promise to sing together someday, which the narrator has never once forgotten, and a simple \"thank you\" that is the real message.","src":"sonnet"},
"my-first-story~a-1i4esh4":{"opus":"MY FIRST STORY's melodic-hardcore ache carries this lingering heartbreak over someone who moved on. Every shared show, photo, and room full of their belongings stays impossible to discard, and the parting word meant to help the narrator be happy alone becomes the one instruction he cannot follow. The band lets the refusal stand as its own truth: he still loves this person and cannot forget.","sonnet":"A song of lingering heartbreak over someone who moved on: every shared show, photo, and room full of their belongings remains impossible to discard; the person gave the narrator a parting word meant to help them be happy alone, and the narrator cannot enact it — they still love this person and cannot forget.","src":"sonnet"},
"my-first-story~a-1kwqi20":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"MY FIRST STORY often dramatize the outsider, and here a person cast out from birth, caged in a square sky, tries to convince himself he can fly while conning his own reflection. He questions why the world forces binary choices on dreams and love as gray awareness seeps in. The band's cathartic scale amplifies the plea underneath, wanting someone to see through the deception before it is too late.","sonnet":"A person who was cast out from birth, caged in a square sky, trying to convince himself he can fly while acting like a con man toward himself — questioning why the world forces binary choices on dreams and love, watching his gray awareness seep in, wanting someone to see through his own deception before it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"my-first-story~a-3gttz8":{"haiku":"A defiant rejection of pity and judgment—the speaker is drowning in chaos but refuses sympathy; leave him alone.","opus":"The band's signature is the raw nerve exposed, and this song exposes a narrator exhausted by being misread, patronized, and poked at by people sure they understand him. He wants everyone to leave him alone yet admits he can only be honest with himself through singing about sadness. MY FIRST STORY let the defiance crack just enough to reveal the deeper ache, a still-live wish to belong somewhere.","sonnet":"A person exhausted by being constantly misread, patronized, and poked at by people who think they understand what he's going through — he wants everyone to just leave him alone, and admits he can only be honest with himself through the act of singing about sadness. The deeper ache is that he still wants to belong somewhere, even as he shuts every door.","src":"haiku"},
"my-first-story~a-3jtxq":{"opus":"MY FIRST STORY turn generational disillusion into anthem here, a narrator who with his peers blamed external things and let dreams stay dreams. The utopia cracked when reality intervened, and now he rails at the sky, wanting either dreams restored or everything burned. The band's explosive dynamics hold him suspended between a grief-soaked past and an ideal that never arrived, unable to settle for either.","sonnet":"A song about lost dreams and the bitterness of adult compromise: the narrator and their generation blamed external things and let dreams stay dreams, the utopia cracked when reality intervened, and now the narrator rails at the sky — wanting either dreams restored or everything burned — trapped between a grief-soaked past and an ideal that never arrived.","src":"sonnet"},
"my-first-story~a-3q0tu":{"haiku":"A fearful love confession—terrified of commitment yet unable to resist, repeating 'I'm scared' while admitting profound devotion.","opus":"MY FIRST STORY excel at the confession too big to voice, and this is a narrator terrified of his own love. He knows it is once-in-a-lifetime, knows he may not get to stay, knows every future map fails to fit, yet this person has already changed him past the point of retreat. The band builds the paralysis before the crucial words into the slow recognition that love has quietly won first.","sonnet":"A person terrified of their own love — they know the confession is once-in-a-lifetime, they know they might not get to stay forever, they know every future map doesn't fit — but they have been changed by this person in ways that make the risk unavoidable. The emotional core is the paralysis before the most important words, and the slow realization that love has already won before they even open their mouth.","src":"haiku"},
"my-first-story~a-q9mvjh":{"haiku":"An existential declaration of self-proof through irreversible transformation—the speaker rejects loneliness by committing to becoming something new and unkillable.","opus":"The band's fight-to-exist mode drives this vow to kill one's own flaws, outlast the pain, and prove through living that today mattered. The determination is love-fueled, waged for someone else's sake, even as the narrator admits he is sinking and can already see the end he fears. MY FIRST STORY keep the desperation and the resolve locked together, survival staged as an act performed rather than felt.","sonnet":"A person fighting to prove their own existence against weakness and loneliness — they repeatedly vow to kill their flaws, outlast the pain, and demonstrate through living that today mattered. The emotional core is a desperate, love-fueled determination to keep going for someone else's sake, even while admitting they are sinking and that the end they fear is already visible.","src":"haiku"},
"my-first-story~a-qktum9":{"opus":"MY FIRST STORY confront childhood cruelty directly here, a narrator singled out arbitrarily, marked wrong by a divine sorting of circles and crosses while the abuser wore an innocent face. The words spoken then stay lodged in his chest as a wound that never healed. The band lets the long wait for it to end become the ache itself, innocence corroded slowly rather than shattered at once.","sonnet":"A song about sustained bullying and the slow corruption of innocence: the narrator was singled out arbitrarily — marked as wrong by a divine sorting of circles and X's — while the abuser stood by with an innocent face; the words spoken then remain lodged in the narrator's chest as a wound that hasn't healed, but they kept waiting for it to finally end.","src":"sonnet"},
"my-first-story~a-qy844s":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"A self-aware weakling is a recurring MY FIRST STORY figure, and here he has spent years fending off love out of pride and self-hatred, snarling at happy faces and blaming others. The band drive him to the admission that he cannot survive alone, asking finally to stay beside the person he now chooses to open up to. The turn from armor to need is the whole arc, hard-won and plainly stated.","sonnet":"A self-aware weakling who has spent years fending off love out of pride and self-hatred — snarling at happy faces, blaming others — who finally admits he can't survive alone and asks to stay beside the person he now chooses to open up to.","src":"haiku"},
"my-first-story~a-v4fg16":{"opus":"MY FIRST STORY frame this as undying one-sided love for someone already committed elsewhere. Saying the words remains the only thing the narrator can do, offered knowing the heart he wants belongs to another. The band stretch the devotion across rebirths and every loneliness between them, insisting this one person is what makes any of it mean anything, hopelessness reframed as the only durable purpose left.","sonnet":"A song of one-sided, undying love for someone already in a relationship: the narrator will keep saying \"I love you\" as the only thing they can do, knowing this person's heart belongs elsewhere — across all rebirths and all loneliness, only this one person makes any of it meaningful.","src":"sonnet"},
"my-first-story~a-wnd90a":{"opus":"MY FIRST STORY push into their darkest register here, a relationship corroded by mutual suspicion and violent impulse where loving means neither doubting nor killing, yet both impulses are present. The narrator fantasizes about carving out the other's eyes to see what they see. The band render the two tumbling together into collapse, neither able to leave, both already broken past the point of rescue.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship corroded by mutual suspicion and violent impulse: loving each other means neither doubting nor killing — but both impulses are present; the narrator fantasizes about carving out the other person's eyes to see what they see, and the two are described as tumbling together into collapse, neither able to leave, both already broken.","src":"sonnet"},
"my-first-story~alone":{"opus":"A defiant assertion of self-definition against a world that decides your life for you, wrestling with the fear that no matter how many times identity shatters or gets repainted, the only proof of existence you can trust is the one you build yourself.","sonnet":"A young person rages against external forces dictating their identity and life path, wrestling with the question of what they actually want and whether their existence means anything — the Japanese lyrics describe crumbling, shattering, and searching for a proof of self, landing ultimately on the defiant claim that their friends and they are not truly alone.","src":"sonnet"},
"my-first-story~feat-jesse-the-bonez-rize":{"haiku":"An underdog refuses to be held back by doubt or spite, vowing to break free and reach higher.","opus":"A defiant underdog manifesto from a Japanese rock-rap crew, built on the proverb that falling seven times means rising eight. Spat scorn for the mediocre who mock you, a promise made alone to yourself to break the chains and fly once from a hundred falls.","sonnet":"A bilingual underdog anthem by someone who was spat on, given the cold shoulder, and dismissed, who kept falling and rising anyway: the narrator spent a quarter-century proving themselves, identifies as a challenger who cuts the chain around their neck to reach a wider world, and tells rivals to be ready because losing is not an option.","src":"haiku"},
"my-first-story~flame":{"haiku":"The speaker has lost reasons to live and no longer knows who they are, drifting into erasure.","opus":"A song teetering on the edge of surrender that keeps opting to head toward a faraway glow in the metropolis instead. Beneath the bilingual anguish sits a pact with what's ahead: an attempt to make it through the day, weighing his own existence, staying anchored to someone rather than disappearing.","sonnet":"A song about a person deciding whether or not to keep living: the narrator decided at some point to quit, the city's lights are the only sign of life left, years have passed unanswered — but the refrain keeps offering the possibility of being together forever, fighting the pull toward disappearance with the one thread of connection.","src":"haiku"},
"my-first-story~from-the-first-take":{"opus":"Recorded for the stripped THE FIRST TAKE series, this performance suits its subject, dismantling the walls that kept the narrator from receiving love. He was weak, stubborn, performatively all-knowing while pushing people away, and now admits for the first time that solitude is impossible and he wants to stay by someone's side. The bare arrangement lets the arc from self-sabotage to open need stand exposed.","sonnet":"A song about dismantling the walls that kept the narrator from receiving love: they were weak, stubborn, performatively all-knowing while pushing people away — but now, for the first time, they admit that being alone is impossible and they want to stay by someone's side; the arc is from self-sabotage to the first open admission of need.","src":"sonnet"},
"my-first-story~king-ashley":{"haiku":"A defiant journey toward self-actualization despite internal conflict and unknown outcomes, refusing to surrender even if success or rightness remains unclear.","opus":"The Japanese band hurl demands about what's raging inside the listener's head and their own, vowing to smash the whole world apart in search of something, refusing to give up until a dream comes true. Bilingual verses swing between English challenge and Japanese resolve, framing fleeting eternity and erased fate as things the narrator will overcome, fighting on even alone.","sonnet":"A fighter pushes through exhaustion and repeated failure toward an encounter that still has not arrived — waiting in solitude for whoever or whatever will finally match him. Defeat and regret accumulate but do not break the resolution: the dream at the end justifies every mistake made along the way.","src":"haiku"},
"my-first-story~missing-you":{"opus":"A grief address to someone lost, unable to sleep with the scars they left; the singer refuses to say goodbye and insists the departed must carry their own unhealed wounds toward whatever comes next, since the survivor cannot make the journey for them.","sonnet":"A narrator waits desperately for someone who has disappeared, haunted by fragmented memories and the erasure of shared moments — the Japanese passages describe waking sleepless with scars left behind, and the English sections beg the person to announce when they'll return, insisting only they can carry what was lost.","src":"sonnet"},
"my-first-story~neurose":{"haiku":"Identity dissolves into meaninglessness; the mind questions its own existence and worth.","opus":"A dissolution of identity where the self stops being the self and love and dreams drain away into meaninglessness. The title neurosis and the Alice-in-a-sewer imagery frame a breakdown that demands to know why anyone bothers being alive or proud.","sonnet":"A song about total identity collapse and the meaninglessness of platitudes about dreams: the narrator's self has been replaced by something called \"NEUROSE\" (neurosis), love and dreams have evaporated, they are invisible to everyone, and the question \"why are you alive?\" has no answer they can access — the song ends with the question still open, unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"my-own-abyss~without-control":{"haiku":"Generational trauma cascades downward unchecked; the speaker cycles between attempting recovery and returning to familiar suffering, unable to break the pattern.","opus":"A lifelong struggle with addiction rooted in a nightmare that started in childhood, its damage refusing to fade and menacing the generation to come. Unable to stop, the narrator keeps returning to the source even as everything around him collapses. My Own Abyss frame the compulsion as a losing fight staged in punishing metal.","sonnet":"About the intergenerational transmission of addiction and trauma — a parent whose suffering the narrator witnessed has now passed the same poison on to the next generation, and the cycle appears impossible to stop. The narrator has been up and down with their own substance dependency since age twelve, and the horror is that the nightmare is recurring in someone else now, carried forward by the very person who was its first victim.","src":"haiku"},
"my-riot~b-l-przemija":{"haiku":"Transience rules all experience as temporary flourishing gives way to inevitable change. Nothing permanent survives the passage of time.","opus":"A Polish rock anthem about loyalty, memory, and a homeland scarred by conflict, where one person loved and believed harder than anyone against everyone. Pain passing at daybreak becomes its emotional center, one moment meaning everything. Late verses turn to betrayal and the temptation of the gun, mourning a lost home and a former self.","sonnet":"A Polish elegy for a moment of singular love and belief — one world, one voice, one person, one fate — set against a backdrop of betrayal, wars burning behind closed doors, and the question of what is left from those bitter-sweet days; the pain passes only when the day is born and that one moment becomes everything.","src":"haiku"},
"my-ticket-home~cellophane": {"haiku":"A person feels emotionally drained by another's hollow affection and wants them to break them down completely.","opus":"Desire for someone who offers only surface, a beautiful blankness that gives nothing back yet still pulls you under. The plea to be broken down is the masochism of craving intimacy from a person who will only ever stay transparent and out of reach.","sonnet":"A relationship so insubstantial it might as well be transparent — no taste, no feeling, nothing to hold — yet the person's presence still clings; the narrator asks to be broken down and collected rather than left in this numb in-between.","src":"haiku","fable":"A nu-metalcore lyric of numbness inside intimacy: every sensory promise — soft speech, a lovely kiss, a holy scent — resolves into the same flat verdict of nothing, so the image-system collapses tenderness into anesthesia. Against that void the chorus begs to be broken down and collected, an invitation to destruction dressed as desire, because being wrecked is the only sensation that would still register. The bridge dissolving into nothing over and over, then the closing pileup of stay out, makes the numbness the point: the speaker seals the connection off precisely because contact no longer reaches them, and cellophane names that condition — wrapped, visible, and untouchable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"destruction invited because it is the only sensation left","built":"the bridge dissolving on the word nothing enacts the anesthesia it names"},
"my-ticket-home~down-life": {"opus":"A downward spiral rendered as the only home its narrator knows, chasing a life that does not exist while bleeding out inside his own breath. Rootlessness curdles into resignation, moral coordinates dissolving until nothing feels right or wrong, and even the wound becomes a familiar address.","sonnet":"A song about being trapped in a degraded existence without a sense of home or belonging — the narrator chases a life that doesn't exist, bleeds away, and can't locate a stable ground. The emotional texture is numb persistence rather than active suffering.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A song about wanting a life you've already conceded you'll never reach — the speaker knows only the down life, yet keeps chasing one he admits in the same breath doesn't exist, so the pursuit is really a way of hiding inside himself rather than moving. The chorus lands on a flattened moral field where nothing is right and nothing is wrong, which reads less as freedom than as the numbness of someone who has stopped being able to tell them apart. The bridge cracks that pose open when he tries to insist it isn't about the other person and can't see through his own tears, exposing the deflection underneath — the record's nu-metalcore heaviness carries the self-pity without ever quite letting him off the hook for it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"chasing a life he admits in the same breath does not exist","built":"the bridge cracks the numbness pose when he can't see through his own tears"},
"my-ticket-home~flee-the-flesh": {"haiku":"Someone trapped in cycles of self-deception and violence yearns for freedom; they are stuck replaying the past, self-harm, and internal turmoil.","opus":"The narrator admits to years of self-deception, disengaged and anchored to the past, punishing himself until something shatters, wrenched free of confinement and coming to crave the brutality he's sunk in. He surrenders to another indulgence that clouds his judgment. The post-hardcore band stages denial and hunger as a fight to break out of the body he's imprisoned in.","sonnet":"Someone stuck in self-recrimination and the wreckage of their own past finally hits a breaking point — the mental cage fractures, and what once paralyzed loosens into something like release. The honesty is brutal: the problem was always internal, the imprisonment self-constructed, the freedom only possible once that is admitted.","src":"haiku","fable":"A relapse narrative that hides inside the grammar of a toxic romance. The you he has been dying to see is the substance addressed as a lover, the one who humbled him and now lives behind his eyes, so surrender and self-erasure get to pass for devotion. The verses split the addiction cleanly. One family of images is appetite and captivity, the cage and the violence and the craving he trains himself to relish. The other is numbed retreat, the checking out, the years lost to what he cannot leave, the fists thrown until the window shatters. And the confession about lying to himself so long is the hinge that admits the romance was always the cover story. My Ticket Home's post-Strangers pivot toward moody, self-lacerating nu-metal fits this confession. The hook does not fight the pull, it consents, waving the thing straight into his skull as if that were the point rather than the ruin.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"welcoming the substance into the skull as if that were the point","built":"the toxic romance grammar lets surrender pass for devotion throughout"},
"my-ticket-home~flypaper": {"haiku":"A toxic relationship traps and poisons the narrator; they struggle to break free from the killer they're bound to, unable to escape the memory.","opus":"A violent, splintered scene of a murderer at the narrator's side stirring awake while he slices away a colorless recollection, affection draining out as blood spills and the whole thing snapping into a hard refusal. The imagery smears self-harm, obsession and dissociation together. My Ticket Home, the Ohio post-hardcore band, drive the unease with lurching dynamics, clean dread breaking into abrasive, screamed rupture.","sonnet":"A memory that won't let go described as something alive and dangerous beside the speaker — cutting it away only leaves it in black and white, permanent. The blood imagery is matter-of-fact rather than dramatic; the song is about the futility of trying to excise something that has already become part of how you're built.","src":"haiku","fable":"A waiting song that keeps circling the same three cells, its title naming the trap: a memory the speaker is stuck to and cannot pull free of, no matter how many times he tries to cut it loose. The menace is internalized — the murderer stands not across the room but at his own shoulder, the parenthetical cut and kill-it commands directing the violence at his own recollection, which he wants reduced to something flat and drained of color. That the verses only trade a lover walking away for a wrist opened at the vein, then loop the pre-chorus a third time with no release, is the point: the refusal to talk about it and the slammed-shut door leave him exactly where he started, replaying the injury on adhesive he made himself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nostalgia & memory","heartbreak & loss","violence & murder"],"means":"directing violence at the recollection rather than the source","built":"the lyric loops back with no release, enacting the adhesion the title names"},
"my-ticket-home~gasoline-kiss": {"haiku":"A seductive figure offers death and takes the listener down with gasoline-fueled passion, becoming possessive as escape becomes impossible.","opus":"Seduction as self-destruction, a lover offered as both salvation and death from above, the intoxicating pull of surrendering to something that promises to save you while it burns you alive and you can't breathe.","sonnet":"A song about being consumed by something destructive — a person or substance that promises everything, demands absolute surrender, and ultimately takes over completely, leaving the narrator unable to breathe or escape.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction sung in the voice of the thing that will destroy you: the speaker offers itself as everything you need, asking only that you go dark behind the lids and hand over your faith, while in the same breath naming itself death from above and a final execution waiting in the hand. The trick is that the caretaker and the killer are one mouth, and the recurring won't-you tag turns tenderness into a dare — coaxing the surrender it needs to finish the job. My Ticket Home built unReal on exactly this kind of glossy menace, and the chorus lets the mask drop entirely: dim the lights, ease off the pedal, no one is ever running you down, spoken by a pursuer who has already won and is watching the light go out. Only in the bridge does the perspective flip inside-out — it's got me, I can't breathe — so the song resolves as a portrait of addiction or self-erasure disguised as romance, the smothering thing finally felt from within the person being taken rather than from the seductive voice taking them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","death & grief"],"means":"the caretaker and the killer share one mouth","built":"the bridge flips to the perspective of the one being consumed"},
"my-ticket-home~hyperreal":{"haiku":"A sense of displacement and emptiness where the mind fragmented and detached, faking emotions while slipping into an unknown state.","opus":"A song about numb emptiness and dissociation, slipping into a flood of memory and staring into the void while faking away sorrow. The narrator feels locked in yet left out, counting down and asking to be cut loose.","sonnet":"A song about a disoriented, emotionally numb state — the speaker feels trapped somewhere between memory and the present, simultaneously locked in and left out, searching for a way to process or escape an unnamed inner strain.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title does the thesis work: hyperreal is the state where the simulation feels more livable than the thing it replaced, and the song is a portrait of dissociation chosen on purpose. Its two verbs give the game away — slipping under and staring above are opposite escapes from the same unbearable middle, and both get metabolized into performance: tomorrow is something you play out, sorrow something you fake out, as if the self has quietly become an actor managing a feeling it can no longer afford to have directly. The bridge is where the pose cracks — a countdown, locked in and left out at once, the repeated plea to be cut out — exposing that the numbness isn't peace but a person timing their own disappearance. What reads as a tidy anthemic chorus is the mask; the real event is someone who has decided the fake version of their life is the only one they can survive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance","suicide"],"means":"numbness chosen on purpose until the simulation feels more livable than the original","built":"the anthemic chorus is the mask; the bridge countdown is the real event beneath it"},
"my-ticket-home~joi": {"haiku":"Emotional numbness and loss of self-worth in a relationship; love becomes a curse. Without wings or identity, the speaker is trapped and unable to feel anything.","opus":"A bruised account of love turning to curse, the narrator caught wingless and alone, unable to be or feel anything alongside a partner amid names and games. Pulling out his own teeth, he longs paradoxically for solitude. My Ticket Home wrap their post-hardcore in imagery of falling pages and people telling themselves to resist, framing survival as grim endurance rather than escape.","sonnet":"A relationship has hollowed a person out entirely, stripping away feeling and selfhood until only numbness remains. Love has curdled into a curse, and being near someone has become a kind of negation — the narrator exists in a state of persistent sensory and emotional shutdown, aching to be alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A fall-from-grace image opens it — someone caught mid-drop with the wings gone, watching love curdle into the curse it once redeemed — and the song stays inside that inversion, where the thing meant to save has become the thing to survive. The refrain leans on a doubled negative that never resolves cleanly: with you I can be, feel, amount to nothing at all, a line that reads at once as annihilation and, underneath, as the flat confession that this person cannot be anything real in the other's presence. My Ticket Home's post-hardcore/nu-metalcore register lets that ambiguity ride the delivery, so the numbness of nothing-at-all and the desperation of it sound like the same collapse. The near-buried couplet about pages coming loose and drifting down through the wind and people coaching each other to dig in and hold the line in order to survive quietly names the exit — endurance as the only remaining verb — before the fall-image loops back one last time, unhealed and unearned.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"annihilation and confession occupying the same doubled negative","built":"the refrain's nothing-at-all reads as both erasure and flat inability simultaneously"},
"my-ticket-home~redline": {"haiku":"A song depicting inherited violence and toxicity that people carry forward, passed down like a virus through generations.","opus":"A portrait of stagnation as a slow death, the speaker drifting numb and hollow while wanting to be transformed by someone he envies. My Ticket Home graft grunge malaise onto post-hardcore aggression, treating his own capacity for cruelty as an inherited disease he half-wishes he could put to use rather than escape.","sonnet":"Paralysis and self-erasure settle in as the speaker drifts without purpose or gravity, recognising that the violence running through his blood is inherited rather than chosen. The desire to escape is real but collapses back into the same inertia it was meant to flee.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song reads as addiction and inherited self-destruction. The verses trap the speaker in a loop where idle waiting and the clock's slow attrition swap places, the same clause reversing on him to dramatize stasis as erosion rather than choice. The central image-system makes violence congenital: the sickness runs in the veins, passed down like a hereditary infection, so the wading-in is not a moral failing but a bloodstream fact, and the chilling turn is that the collective aims to use it — the contagion is embraced as a weapon rather than resisted. The pre-chorus wanting-to-be-like-you and the bridge's plea to be led out of this place sit against that acceptance, so the mask of nu-metalcore bravado hides someone begging for an exit he already knows the tainted blood won't grant.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","family & blood","violence & murder"],"means":"inherited contagion embraced as a weapon rather than resisted","built":"the same clause reverses on the speaker to recast stasis as erosion"},
"my-ticket-home~through-the-needle-s-eye":{"haiku":"The speaker is caught in a destructive relationship, unable to turn back time; they compress their skin through the needle's eye toward heartbreak.","opus":"A portrait of someone trapped in an affair he knows is destroying him, aware the damage is done yet returning to the person anyway. He admits his own dishonesty and ignores every red flag, choosing another guilty night over the truth. The song sits in that helpless space between remorse and relapse.","sonnet":"A relationship that has gone too wrong to save — the speaker is lying to themselves and the other person, knows all the signs point to it being over, and yet can't quite let go, passing through that final needle's eye of no return.","src":"haiku"},
"my-ticket-home~thrush": {"haiku":"A relationship marked by possession and harm: the speaker is a wound that bleeds, the other is a predator, and connection is framed through violence and co-dependency.","opus":"A volatile, codependent bond in which the narrator becomes the very injury his partner keeps reopening, drawing them into a space with no room to settle and no way to stay. The relentless pull between demanding to be consumed and demanding to be left alone captures a relationship running on mutual destruction and unavoidable collapse.","sonnet":"A toxic codependent dynamic where the speaker positions themselves as both wound and cure — luring someone into a destructive relationship while simultaneously pulling them away from any stable life, framed as an inevitability neither can escape.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction written as mutual devouring, where the speaker and the one he's luring keep trading the same accusation — you cling to me because you imagine you've read me, now surrender your life because you imagine you've read me — so wanting and being wanted, knowing and being fed on, collapse into one motion. The hook makes that explicit by casting the speaker as the wound rather than the blade: he is the gash that keeps you leaking, the thing already inside you, promising escape while offering only a playground with no living in it. What sounds like an invitation is really an appetite, and the refusal to linger — no hour spared to remain, hammered flat in the breakdown — reads less as freedom than as the fed-thing already moving on, the fall recast as something you're told to admire.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the wound promises escape while feeding on its host","built":"wanting and being consumed collapse into the same motion"},
"my-ticket-home~visual-snow": {"haiku":"Indecision and confusion trap the speaker in cycles that worsen over time, obscuring clarity and making choices impossible.","opus":"Paralysis by indecision, treated as an illness that festers the longer a choice goes unmade, with the speaker stuck waiting on someone else to settle it. My Ticket Home borrow the title's real optical disorder to stand for a permanently clouded outlook, matching the theme with murky, unsettled alt-rock.","sonnet":"Static indecision — floating around an urge that can't be named, seeing something through the haze that isn't quite real, watching days grey out while motives blur. The song doesn't resolve the hesitation; it just describes the texture of being stuck between an inside pull and an outside world going on without asking for your answer.","src":"haiku","fable":"Named for the neurological condition where static never clears from the field of vision, the song makes indecision itself into that permanent visual noise — a state you float inside rather than a choice you make. The chorus never resolves because it can't: every return to the same four lines is the mind circling an urge it can neither act on nor release, and the only forward motion is the damage stacking up another layer with each pass, deeper in the marrow than the last. The verses drift outward into greyed skies and a gulf too wide to cross while the speaker keeps promising a call that closes his eyes instead, so the second-person question of whether you will decide lands as pure static — a plea with no one on the other end and no answer coming, the paralysis addressing itself in the mirror.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"paralysis addressing itself in the mirror with no answer coming","built":"the chorus never resolves because the neurological condition it names never does"},
"my-ticket-home~we-re-in-this-together":{"haiku":"Two people facing destruction together, bonded through mutual vulnerability and commitment to endure opposition and external threats as unified partners.","opus":"A reworking of the Nine Inch Nails anthem, this pledges that two people united against a hostile world will pull through even if everything falls apart around them. Outside forces claw to tear the pair apart and ruin what they share. My Ticket Home keep the royal imagery, turning stubborn devotion into a promise to endure once every last hope has vanished.","sonnet":"Two people battered by external forces and their own accumulated damage cling to a shared identity as the only constant left. The bond is stripped of romance and elevated instead to something more durable — mutual survival as meaning, the vow of remaining present until one or both ceases to exist.","src":"haiku"},
"myslovitz~ch-opcy":{"haiku":"The song captures young male restlessness and escapism. Boys exit to streets seeking meaning, throwing stones at cars and yearning for far-off worlds they'll never reach.","opus":"A tender, melancholy sketch of small-town boys who spill onto the streets each evening, throwing stones at car wheels and eyeing girls who won't know them, dreaming of far-off lands through empty bottle-binoculars, doing anything to escape yet frozen when the chance finally comes. Myslovitz, one of Poland's defining 90s alternative rock bands, capture provincial youth and thwarted longing with quiet, literate poignancy.","sonnet":"Young men on nighttime streets in a grey town with nowhere to go — throwing rocks at car wheels, watching girls who ignore them, drinking through empty binoculars and arguing about American films. The evening hides the dreariness of the place just long enough to pretend there is somewhere to walk; by the time they can leave, they are already too stoned to move.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about small-town paralysis dressed as restlessness, and its structure is the argument: four verses open on the identical line about boys heading out into the evening, and each proposes an escape that dead-ends in the same place. First comes vandalism and girls who refuse to know them, then a darkness merciful enough to hide the grime so a stroll can be pretended, then faraway lives dreamed through the empty binoculars of drained bottles and arguments over American films. The anaphora is the trap made audible; every night begins the same because nothing the boys reach for actually moves them, and Myslovitz frames this provincial stasis as a loop rather than a story. The final verse trips the mechanism shut: they try everything to flee, and then the reversal — the moment freedom finally arrives is exactly the moment they cannot stir. The real subject is a paralysis that has hardened from circumstance into character, the rehearsal of escape repeated so long that the capacity to leave has quietly died.","fv":2,"themes":["freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"escape rehearsed so long the capacity to leave quietly dies","built":"four verses on the identical opening line — anaphora as the trap made audible"},
"n-e-r-d~lapdance":{"haiku":"Political corruption and societal decay are mirrored in the speaker's outlaw posture and sexual aggression; both strippers and politicians sell themselves, and the speaker positions himself as agent of retribution.","opus":"Pharrell's crew equate politicians with strippers, both selling seductive nothing to a public that shouldn't buy it. The swagger and menace mask a real disgust with a society that provokes rage and empty promises alike.","sonnet":"Alienation from a corrupt society breeds a defiant, hostile persona who equates politicians and authority figures with strippers performing empty seduction — the speakers reject the performance and position themselves as untamed outlaws outside the system.","src":"haiku"},
"n-e-r-d~rock-star":{"haiku":"A track mocking poseurs and self-serving imitation while claiming authentic rebel status. The speaker warns pretenders that accountability is coming and their time is up.","opus":"A defiant address to posers and imitators, warning that their fakery is almost over as the swaggering narrator asserts his authenticity as a rebel rock star and tells them to be themselves.","sonnet":"A challenge hurled at posers and imitators who tried to mimic the group's style, warning them that their time is up and authenticity will win out.","src":"haiku"},
"n-e-r-d~rockstar-jason-nevins-remix":{"haiku":"Intoxicated ascent and celebrity excess blur boundaries between authentic performance and commodified persona; fame becomes the drug and the addiction.","opus":"Pharrell's crew tears into imitators and posers who fake the lifestyle without living it, asserting authentic swagger over hangers-on whose time is running out. Beneath the braggadocio sits a sincere plea to just be yourself. A defiant early-2000s statement of individuality dressed as a rap-rock takedown of everyone trying to copy the real thing.","sonnet":"An anti-poser declaration: the speaker has no patience for those who dream of being a rock star without the substance or sacrifice — \"it's almost over now\" functions as both countdown and verdict. The narrator raps on cop-car rooftops with a .44 while the pretenders hesitate and the clock runs out on their borrowed cool.","src":"haiku"},
"n-o-w-a-v-e~direct-action":{"haiku":"A world in crisis demands rebellion against conformity; the speaker rejects normalcy and uses music as a weapon to combat the systemic sickness consuming society.","opus":"A raw declaration of alienation from a world the narrator sees as sick and fake, refusing to be normal or to live by anyone else's rules. Having destroyed everything he had, he treats his music as a weapon and his defiance as survival. Confrontational punk-metal about rejecting a rotten society and going it alone.","sonnet":"A speaker who has destroyed everything they had and declared themselves too broken for normal, wielding music as blade and power against a sick world they refuse to assimilate into. The enemies' deaths are not yet real, sin has been killed, defects fixed — a declaration of ongoing resistance from someone who has nothing left to lose.","src":"haiku"},
"n-w-a~dope-man":{"haiku":"Drug commerce devastates communities through predatory cycles; the dope man manipulates addiction and desperation for profit.","opus":"An unflinching Compton street portrait of the crack economy, tracing how the dealer's power corrodes everyone in its orbit, addicts and women alike. It reports the wreckage without glamorizing it, closer to warning than celebration.","sonnet":"A detailed and deliberately unflinching portrait of the crack cocaine economy in Compton: the dopeman at the center is powerful, brutal, and predatory, and the song traces the damage radiating outward from him — addicted men, exploited women, destroyed families — while also giving the dopeman himself the last brutal word.","src":"haiku"},
"n-w-a~gangsta-gangsta":{"haiku":"Street survival through violence and masculine aggression; the crew rejects conventional morality while asserting authentic ghetto truth.","opus":"Ice Cube's manifesto of nihilistic street defiance, rejecting the role-model expectation outright. The core stance is refusal, reality over respectability, painting life as violence and survival with no apology offered.","sonnet":"N.W.A narrate a night of confrontation, violence, and unapologetic street life from multiple perspectives, presenting gangsta identity as the only honest response to a world that already sees them as criminals — not a moral endorsement so much as a declaration that reality is what it is and they refuse to pretend otherwise.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~alive":{"haiku":"A speaker addresses someone whose sense of vitality feeds on another's suffering and erasure, asking whether their persecutor will finally feel alive once the speaker is consumed and gone.","opus":"A wounded reckoning with betrayal, the narrator asking whether his ruin and disappearance would finally satisfy the one who wanted him gone. Offering himself up to be discarded, he wonders if his absence is what makes another feel alive. Metalcore built on the bitter arithmetic of being someone else's sacrifice and their peace of mind.","sonnet":"Addressed to someone who feeds on the speaker's diminishment: put me on display, drag what remains to the grave — when my loss gives you peace of mind, hope you're satisfied asks whether the predator will feel alive once the prey is fully gone, turning grief into accusation.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~ashes":{"haiku":"Buried truths and denial must ignite into ash; walking away requires burning everything held captive.","opus":"An estranged couple side by side yet worlds apart, keeping to the shadows and waiting on answers that won't come. The only way out they find is to burn the failing thing to nothing and walk free to undo the wrongs it held them to.","sonnet":"A breakup song about two people who are standing next to each other while being worlds apart emotionally — one of them is finally told to let the love burn down and walk away, righting the wrongs they have been holding onto; the emotional core is release from a bond that has already died.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~bleed":{"haiku":"Trauma and emotional hemorrhage persist; the speaker cannot stop bleeding out internally.","opus":"A furious indictment of false prophets who crave nothing but destruction, met with the narrator's vow to silence them and turn their appetite for ruin back on their own world. Heavy, chant-driven aggression about refusing to be a passive witness, meeting those who would watch everything burn with equal, answering violence.","sonnet":"A near-minimal declaration of reciprocal contempt — false prophets want to watch the world bleed, so the narrator wants to watch their world bleed back. The symmetry is the point: violence of spectacle answered in kind, no resolution offered.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~ghost":{"haiku":"The dead beloved haunts presence; grief persists as companionship.","opus":"A survivor haunted by the ghost of someone who died, torn between wanting them near and wishing them the rest that death brought. If living had meant dying inside for them, the mourner can accept letting the spirit stay only as a ghost.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses someone who has died — \"dead to the world, but not in mine\" — asking them to leave their ghost as a companion, finding that bloodshot hindsight and the memory of better years are all that survive, and that surviving itself might mean dying inside.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~hypnotized":{"haiku":"External manipulation and internal demons converge into paralysis; the speaker haunted from both directions cannot distinguish predator from self.","opus":"Walling off weakness only to be hypnotized by the demons that keep returning, watching one's own demise creep closer from the shadows.","sonnet":"About being stalked by inner demons that return no matter how many walls the speaker builds — they feel cornered every night, unable to escape the wicked eyes that creep closer in the dark.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~live-it-down":{"haiku":"Wrong stacks upon wrong while walls burn. Figuring out means never escaping—mistakes burn forever.","opus":"An anxious, urgent shove toward action while there is still a sliver of opportunity, setting a misspent existence against one final shot at working things out as everything around ignites. Looming over the whole thing is the dread of a remorse that will never loosen its grip.","sonnet":"A short, looping expression of irreversibility — whatever has gone wrong, whatever time has been lost, the narrator is stuck in the shame of it and suspects they'll never be able to move past it, even while half-hoping they can figure something out.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~okay":{"haiku":"Fractured affirmation: repeating okay as incantation, knowing it remains false but needing to believe the lie sustains survival.","opus":"Torn apart and sure he won't be okay, then shutting the door and cutting off his heart precisely to make sure he will. The repeated reassurance rings as a numb defense after losing the war.","sonnet":"A song about emotional shutdown after losing a long battle — the speaker has cut off their heart, shut the door on whatever was draining them, and tells themselves they will be okay as an act of survival rather than genuine belief.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~reminisce":{"haiku":"The past sustains itself through memory while the present stays dead, the speaker preserves what's lost through written record.","opus":"A quiet ache about living inside your own memories, keeping something you know is already gone breathing through pen and paper; the peace he arrives at is accepting there's no return, only the hope that another lifetime lets him take it slower.","sonnet":"A song about preserving a dead chapter of life purely through memory and writing — knowing you cannot go back, but keeping the past alive in your head because it is all that remains of it.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~save-me":{"haiku":"A soul sold for worldly gain now stands at judgment, forever enslaved by its own choices.","opus":"Life as a prison and a rigged game you're bound to, arriving at heaven's threshold having traded your soul for material things and knowing there's no way out. A doom-metalcore cry for rescue from a hell that feels self-written and unending.","sonnet":"About feeling trapped in a life that feels like a daily hell, having sold out for worldly things and finding no exit — the narrator stands at a spiritual reckoning (pearly gates, sold soul) and simply begs to be rescued from a cycle they can't break on their own.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~silver-line":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and regret pile up; the speaker searches for hope while sinking into despair.","opus":"Swallowing hurt from a betrayal until the soul wears thin, refusing the comforting lie that time heals. The search for a silver lining curdles into questioning everything, with blood in the street standing for what the survivors actually paid.","sonnet":"About betrayal and exhausted searching for hope — someone has been deceived repeatedly, is depleted, and can't accept reassurances that time will heal the wound. The \"silver lining\" chase is the center: the refusal to give up on meaning even when lied to and drained.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~the-cycle":{"haiku":"Suicide ideation framed as the only way to atone and escape a self-destructive cycle, each verse tightening the logic of finality until \"end it all\" is the only remaining option.","opus":"A terse resolution to break a self-perpetuating pattern of pain, atoning for what he has sown and choosing to walk away for good. Etched lies and reaped suffering give way to a decision to end it all and finally step off the wheel. Metalcore about escape as the only route left toward peace.","sonnet":"A brief, blunt exit plan: draw the line with rope, etch lies in stone, sweep away what's owed — break the cycle, time to go repeated like a verdict, until end it all closes the third verse with the weight of someone who has decided the only way to stop reaping the pain they've sown is to stop entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"n3b~the-void":{"haiku":"Emptiness and survival coexist as the speaker chooses life despite defenselessness, persisting through abuse and self-directed violence.","opus":"Facing an inner void of repressed abuse and unhealed wounds, defenseless yet choosing life, insisting on remaining alive in voice even when dead in the eyes.","sonnet":"A survivor of trauma or abuse confronts the emptiness left inside them — they've built walls for protection and question whether their sense of self is real, but ultimately choose life and continue fighting the void despite having no defense against it.","src":"haiku"},
"naberk-t-d-r~kesik-y-zeyler":{"haiku":"Fragmented surfaces and interrupted narratives, sustained by continued reading and living despite fracture.","opus":"A spare Turkish loop about cut surfaces and pencil sharpeners, whittled-down stories, reassuring someone not to worry, that he's still reading, still alive. Fragmentation itself becomes the point, a quiet insistence on persistence amid things worn down to shavings.","sonnet":"Cut surfaces and pencil sharpeners become emblems of anxiety or self-harm, but the speaker pushes back against worry — 'don't be concerned, I'm still reading, I'm still living' — a quiet insistence on survival despite fragmented, fractured experience.","src":"haiku"},
"naberkötüdür~kesik-yüzeyler":{"haiku":"Fragmentation persists in the speaker's reading and living, where broken surfaces and sharpened tools mark survival. Worry dissolves against the continuance of breath and story.","src":"haiku"},
"nadine-shah~fool":{"opus":"A sharp dismantling of a pretentious phony who lifts his whole persona and chat-up material from famous writers and songwriters to pass as profound, seen straight through by the narrator as ordinary, feeble and full of himself. She calls out the borrowed depth without flinching.","sonnet":"A sharp takedown of a pretentious man who performs literary sensitivity to seduce women — the narrator saw through his recycled lines and borrowed intellectual posturing, and now calls him plainly weak and contemptible for doing the same to others.","src":"sonnet"},
"naked-identity-created-by-king~seed":{"opus":"A Japanese post-hardcore meditation on impermanence and endless rebirth, imagery of a faint peace in an infinite sea and a red hue granted only to flicker and wither. Life becomes a predetermined journey toward decay, souls falling and going astray through cycles of reincarnation, laughing at the end only to begin again.","sonnet":"A meditation on the endlessly repeating cycle of birth, suffering, and death — a seed given life in a vast sea of existence blinks briefly before its journey to withering, lamenting pain without meaning and laughing at last before the cycle begins again.","src":"sonnet"},
"nano-ripe~a-wpnt1u":{"haiku":"Rejection of false brightness and societal masks; the speaker sees through cheerful deception to the shadow self underneath and confronts the darkness everyone hides.","opus":"Nano.RIPE built their anime-scoring sound on nostalgia rendered in tactile detail, and this childhood summer is exactly that. A secret still locked in a box with no key replays through cicadas, melting ice, and a beetle he was too frightened to touch. Kimiko Tanaka's bright, keening voice makes the understanding land gently: those traces are permanent even though he can never go back.","sonnet":"A childhood summer with someone who kept a secret from the narrator — a secret still unreachable, locked in a box with no key — replays through sensory fragments (cicadas, melting ice, a beetle he was too frightened to touch), and he understands that those traces are permanent even though he can never go back.","src":"haiku"},
"nano~a-kbk5cr":{"opus":"Nano, the bilingual singer raised online before signing to a major label, gives this patient waiting song its steady ache. Each day the narrator keeps finding memories, drenched in the rain of tears, yet refuses to move on. Guided by stars, they promise to stand exactly where this person left them until a return that has no date, the waiting held open indefinitely rather than resolved.","sonnet":"A song of patient, indefinite waiting for someone who has left: each day the narrator keeps finding memories, drenched in the rain of tears, but refuses to move on — they stand in the rain, guided by stars, promising to stay exactly where this person left them until they return.","src":"sonnet"},
"napoleon~utopia":{"haiku":"The speaker invites collective transcendence by abandoning ego and internal turmoil through spiritual cleansing, claiming that shared effort can defeat shared depression and worry.","opus":"An upbeat call to drop the ego and open oneself up, to breathe clean and renew the spirit, insisting that despair and sleepless anxiety are easier to overcome with others than in isolation. It's earnest, communal encouragement. Napoleon, a melodic metalcore band with a spiritual streak, wrap the uplift in heavy dynamics, the theme of shared healing tempering the genre's usual aggression into something nearly devotional.","sonnet":"A gentle directive toward collective healing — set the ego down, open the mind, and the path clears. The song addresses depression and sleeplessness not with analysis but with the simple proposition that defeating them is possible together, that a breath of clarity can cleanse what isolation has made heavier.","src":"haiku"},
"narrow-head~ashtray":{"haiku":"A song about unrequited longing and the pulling force of distance between two people who are caught in an inescapable cycle.","opus":"The singer phones after dark claiming only friendship, secretly hoping to be wanted more while feeling the growing gap and a downward drag on them both. Narrow Head, revivalists of nineties alt-rock fuzz and shoegaze, bathe the wistful yearning in haze, the alternate realm glimpsed behind shut eyes standing in for a nearness forever whirling out of reach.","sonnet":"Late-night longing for someone kept at arm's length resolves into a shared drift through altered perception, where distance collapses and the other world becomes briefly visible. The pull between closeness and spinning away remains unresolved, suspended in a haze.","src":"haiku"},
"narrow-head~t-w-i-n":{"haiku":"A father's abandonment leaves children scarred by permanent loss and the unanswerable question of why he vanished; grief lingers as an inherited solitude.","opus":"A grief elegy for a father who abandoned a pair of kids, sung from the twin perspective of the mourners and the one now treading a solitary, constricted road. The Texas shoegaze band drape the loss in dense fuzz, dwelling on the unanswerable question of whether the departed has found rest while those he left keep aching.","sonnet":"A grief elegy for a father's departure — two children left behind, crying, forever lonely. The speaker asks if the one who left has finally found home, while admitting they are now alone on a narrow path downward, with no answer forthcoming from those who insist the father is \"too far gone.\"","src":"haiku"},
"nasty-cherry~what-do-you-like-in-me":{"haiku":"A person feels insecure and desperate to understand their partner's attraction to them, unable to know what they truly want.","opus":"An anxious probe into being desired, the narrator caught in self-deception and exposure, wanting to see herself through another's eyes while insisting nothing lasts forever, then spiraling into the maddening refrain of not knowing what the other person wants. Insecurity and infatuation blur. Nasty Cherry, the all-female band assembled with Charli XCX's involvement, deliver this in glossy, moody synth-pop-rock that keeps the unease danceable.","sonnet":"The vertigo of being seen without knowing what the other person sees — self-deception on the surface, genuine bewilderment underneath. The speaker wants to look through the other person's eyes long enough to understand what they find worth keeping, but the wanting loops into anxiety and the question just multiplies without an answer coming back.","src":"haiku"},
"nas~the-world-is-yours":{"haiku":"A hustler claims personal sovereignty over his world through cunning and nerve, celebrating ambition while mourning the collateral damage of street survival.","opus":"A young Queens poet's claim on possibility from inside the projects, hustling and mortality braided with literary ambition. The title's promise is aspirational defiance, the world owed to anyone sharp enough to seize it.","sonnet":"A young man navigating street life and ambition declares ownership of the world through sheer force of will, while reckoning with grief, poverty, and the thin line between survival and destruction — the world belongs to those who take it, but the cost is steep.","src":"haiku"},
"nation-of-language~on-division-st":{"haiku":"Childhood loss persists as permanent wound while answers remain buried, the speaker forever waiting in the space where love once lived.","opus":"A synth-driven ache of abandonment, someone left waiting for answers and recognition that never come. The buried-where-I-belonged refrain turns a specific street into the site of a lifelong, unanswered longing to be heard.","sonnet":"A speaker returns to a formative place and time — a childhood home on Division Street — looking for answers that were always withheld, still waiting at a door where no one came. The grief is not dramatic but chronic: the unanswered question, the abandoned hope.","src":"haiku"},
"native-construct~your-familiar-face":{"haiku":"Unrequited devotion persists silently through written confession while love remains unreturned and the object of desire lingers as permanent haunting.","opus":"Unreturned love poured into letters that never get a reply, the writer tormented by a well-known face who picks somebody else, abandoned to confront his own curdling, darker nature. Native Construct wrap the heartbreak in intricate, theatrical prog-metal.","sonnet":"Unrequited love expressed through letters that speak straight from the heart, addressed to someone whose familiar face lingers even after she has chosen another; the speaker, left alone with their mind, discovers a sinister side emerging from the burning scar of perpetual rejection.","src":"haiku"},
"negative-25~are-we-having-fun-yet":{"haiku":"Success breeds anxiety rather than satisfaction; the speaker questions whether fame has poisoned joy or revealed its absence.","opus":"A rising musician two years into modest success is terrified of playing shows, planning their own funeral, and questioning whether any of it is worth it. The repeated question mocks the hollow performance of enjoying a dream that's quietly crushing them.","sonnet":"A musician two years into their career is scared of playing shows, planning their own funeral, and asking whether any of this is actually fun — bad loans, dyed roots, losing friends to trends — with the chorus turning the audience into co-defendants in the question.","src":"haiku"},
"neiked~sexual-feat-dyo":{"haiku":"Desire and admiration fuse as the narrator discovers their perfect match; chemistry overrides hesitation and justifies surrendering to sexual connection.","opus":"A frank, upbeat celebration of desire meeting genuine affection, attraction sweetened by finding real worth in the other person. Straightforward pop wanting, but rooted in adoration rather than pure appetite.","sonnet":"The speaker tells someone they desire that the attraction is total and unambiguous — emotional, physical, transformative — and simply asks them to confirm they feel the same so they can act on what both of them already know.","src":"haiku"},
"nekonomicon~ghost-maze-project":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession manifests as spectral haunting, where the speaker becomes a phantom companion trapped in claustrophobic mental loops, forever present but unable to touch what they pursue.","opus":"A vow to stay beside someone forever, even in death, framed as being lost in a claustrophobic, endless maze of darkness where memories of them flash like the only light. The ghost imagery makes eternal devotion inseparable from being trapped and haunted.","sonnet":"The speaker is lost in a claustrophobic mental maze — corridors that never end, pitch-black light, glitching memories — and clings to a vow to stay beside someone they love even after death, haunting them like a ghost rather than letting go.","src":"haiku"},
"nek~zero-sum":{"haiku":"Social media haters breed chaos and false righteousness; the speaker laughs at their pointless game.","opus":"A scathing takedown of online outrage culture, the backbiting believers and performative victims trading position in a zero-sum game of screens. It turns from disgust to counterattack, mocking the self-appointed saints who steal others' words for a hollow, rigged justice.","sonnet":"A Japanese-language song targeting online toxic culture — the haters running position arguments and virtue-signaling behind screens, drowning the night in ironic outrage and filtered approval-seeking; the narrator refuses to be dragged into the zero-sum game, laughs at the so-called saints, and insists on rolling through with an unbeatable emotion.","src":"haiku"},
"nelly-furtado~maneater-radio-1-live-lounge-session":{"haiku":"Predatory feminine power seduces and consumes men through calculated charm, leaving victims emotionally and financially depleted.","opus":"A stripped Live Lounge take on Furtado's portrait of a magnetic, ruthless woman who drains the men who chase her. The thrill and the warning coexist, power admired even as it wrecks whoever gets close.","sonnet":"A portrait of a woman whose power over men is total and ruinous: she makes them work, spend, fall, and wish they had never met her, moving through the world with undeniable command while those around her lose themselves completely to her pull.","src":"haiku"},
"nelly-furtado~say-it-right-main":{"haiku":"Spiritual awakening through sacred touch and honesty; the speaker struggles between light and darkness, offering redemptive love despite doubt.","opus":"An ambivalent breakup where the other person means nothing yet somehow holds the key to freedom. The tension is exactly that contradiction, indifference and dependence tangled, sung like a half-remembered dream.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses someone with a paradox — you mean nothing to me and yet you have what it takes to set me free, you could mean everything — circling the impossibility of articulating what this person is to her, offering glimpses of her body, her voice, her vulnerability as things that might finally say it right.","src":"haiku"},
"nemophila~enten":{"haiku":"Fragile flame persists despite inevitable extinction; the speaker burns defiantly, inscribing momentary brilliance against darkness.","opus":"Cast in classical Japanese imagery, an unrecognized life vows to burn its whole being to blaze bright enough to be mistaken for a star, and to scorch the very sky. A defiant anthem about burning out gloriously rather than fading unnoticed, like a firefly's brief light.","sonnet":"Drawing on classical Japanese imagery of fireflies and buried wood that never blooms, the singer commands the self to burn utterly — life, body, anger, tears — and blaze brighter than stars to scorch the sky, because even this fleeting fire is more honest than invisible endurance.","src":"haiku"},
"nemophila~raitei":{"haiku":"A bolt of lightning cuts through darkness, electrifying the heart of someone lost in despair. The speaker vows to believe in their companion's radiance, striking down shadows with raw conviction regardless of cost.","opus":"The Japanese metal band frames anxiety and doubt as a storm to be split open by lightning, the title's thunderbolt piercing the darkness in someone's heart. A fierce vow of faith in another person, insisting they can shine no matter how many times they falter. Empowerment metal about believing in someone until their own light returns.","sonnet":"One voice urges another who is lost in anxiety and suppression to break through — \"you can shine, again and again\" — wielding lightning (雷霆) like a sword to slash the darkness that confuses the heart, while pledging to keep believing in them even beyond despair and to stand beside them wherever they go.","src":"haiku"},
"neon-graves~soul-burn":{"haiku":"Persistent inadequacy and spiritual torment consume the self, manifesting as cyclical despair and gradual decay—a burning that cannot be extinguished.","opus":"A voice caught in a slow spiral of self-hatred, addicted to the very hurt that is dissolving it, begging to be released from a life it no longer feels is its own.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone who feels like a chronic disappointment, consumed by voices urging self-destruction, with their sense of agency stripped away — they're addicted to suffering and can't stop returning to what hurts them.","src":"haiku"},
"neon-oni~satori-sedai":{"haiku":"Generation surrender to apathy and futility; everything—dreams, love, future—becomes worthless as society collapses into jaded resignation.","opus":"A portrait of Japan's resigned generation that wants nothing, dreams, love, and future all discarded, smiles worn as masks over chained hearts; the media and social feeds ring hollow, and the shouted calls to wake or stand collapse back into giving up.","sonnet":"A portrait of Japan's so-called \"satori\" generation — young people who have given up on dreams, love, money, and the future, living in resigned acceptance of emptiness, smiling behind masks, with no goals left to chase and no bonds left that haven't already dissolved.","src":"haiku"},
"neon-trees~everybody-talks":{"haiku":"Gossip corrodes authentic connection; the narrator struggles against mass judgment while seeking recognition.","opus":"A giddy new crush shadowed by gossip and rumor, the whisper networks that surround any budding attraction. The bounce carries a small anxiety underneath, love conducted in public where everybody talks back.","sonnet":"A relationship that begins with a single whispered kiss quickly becomes the subject of gossip and escalating rumour, and the speaker — equal parts addicted and overwhelmed — admits that talking too much has always been the problem.","src":"haiku"},
"nerium~dysmorphic":{"haiku":"Distorted self-perception and body horror from internal disdain, the speaker consumed by ugliness of their own reflection.","opus":"A visceral rendering of body dysmorphia, the narrator trapped before a mirror that shows an enemy he wants to flay, burn, and tear away. The self staring back becomes something to destroy rather than accept. Deathcore that externalizes self-loathing as literal violence against one's own flesh, screaming for a life the reflection seems to be devouring.","sonnet":"Body dysmorphia rendered as escalating violence against the self — the mirror becomes the enemy, searing and flaying giving way to riving the bones and burning the eyes — until the image staring back is declared the enemy and the only exit imagined is annihilation: my face is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"nero~circles":{"haiku":"Cyclical patterns trap the couple in repetitive failure; motion without progress, they spiral toward nowhere despite recognition of the trap.","opus":"A relationship stuck in futile repetition, every attempt at honesty or effort only pushing the other further away. However hard either tries to move toward the other, they end up going nowhere, moving in circles.","sonnet":"Two people who keep lying to each other and pushing each other away are caught in a loop they both recognise as going nowhere — \"we move in circles\" — with every attempt at honesty lasting only a day before the same drift resumes.","src":"haiku"},
"nero~crush-on-you":{"haiku":"Someone has a secret crush that spreads through gossip, exposing their hidden feelings to the object of their affection.","opus":"A rush of embarrassed delight when a hidden infatuation gets outed, the singer both cornered and secretly relieved that the feeling is finally public knowledge. Nero, the British electronic duo known for dark cinematic dubstep, swap that for glossy retro dance-pop, letting bright, driving production carry the flustered joy.","sonnet":"A secret exposed before its owner could confess it — the heart that was carefully hidden has been read by the one it was hidden from, and the charade is over. The revelation comes through a friend who talks too much; the crush is no longer a private thing, and the song holds that exposure with something between relief and alarm.","src":"haiku"},
"nero~guilt":{"haiku":"The narrator confronts a partner whose words ring hollow and rules are broken, questioning direction and intimacy amid deceit.","opus":"A soured relationship cut to a single accusation, the partner talking endlessly yet making no sense while faking sincerity and breaking every rule. The narrator warns that time is running out to see clearly. The British drum-and-bass duo Nero build the track around a haunted vocal sample looped into obsession, turning romantic disillusion into a hypnotic, danceable spiral of doubt.","sonnet":"A fractured relationship circles the drain as one person watches the other perform connection without sincerity. Time slips past unacknowledged, every word landing hollow, every rule broken from the very start — and the speaker sits with a growing, unresolved sense of having been made a fool throughout.","src":"haiku"},
"nero~me-and-you":{"haiku":"Connection transcends despite separation as two souls recognize each other across distance. The bond persists beyond physical presence.","opus":"A euphoric, minimal dance track built on anticipation and mutual desire, stripping romance down to a single insistent question of readiness and the shared certainty between two people. Nero's drum-and-bass roots turn simplicity into momentum, the feeling repeated until it becomes pure charged energy on the dancefloor, everything narrowed to me and you.","sonnet":"Pure mutual readiness — two people on the verge of something, both feeling it, both asking if the other is ready — the lyric strips romance to its electric question and never gets past the threshold, which is exactly where it wants to stay.","src":"haiku"},
"nero~me-you-dirtyphonics-remix":{"haiku":"Readiness and mutual intensity repeated into hypnotic affirmation of connection and desire.","opus":"A drum-and-bass rush of mutual desire pared to its essentials, two people asking if they're ready for what they already feel. The repetition is the anticipation itself, everything else stripped away but the charge between them.","sonnet":"A minimal electronic track — the lyrical content barely exists beyond mutual readiness and shared feeling between two people. The song is almost entirely about the anticipation of connection rather than its content.","src":"haiku"},
"nero~must-be-the-feeling":{"haiku":"A groove moves the body and compels action, driven by feeling rather than thought.","opus":"A dancefloor rush distilled to its essence, a groove that compels movement and a feeling that tells the body exactly what to do, built almost entirely from ecstatic repetition and the command to break it down. There's no story, only euphoria. Nero, the British electronic duo, fuse dubstep drops with anthemic, 90s-rave-flavored energy, and this is that hands-in-the-air hedonism stripped to its pure kinetic core.","src":"haiku"},
"nero~my-eyes":{"haiku":"A speaker's gaze follows someone around the room with desire, making an unspoken invitation for intimacy dependent on the other person's choice.","opus":"A tight loop of infatuation in which attention locks onto one person and won't budge, the singer unable to mask the pull and handing over the decision about how the night ends. Nero, the British pair who straddle dubstep and pop, strip desire down to a single repeated admission swelled by electronics.","sonnet":"Attention fixed entirely on one person in a room full of other people — the speaker's gaze follows involuntarily, the attraction undeniable. The feeling is private and unacted-upon, and whether anything happens next depends entirely on the other person reading what the watching eyes cannot stop broadcasting.","src":"haiku"},
"nero~promises":{"haiku":"One person intoxicates another with promises of loyalty that feel wasted on themselves; both are caught in cycles of wild passion and empty vows.","opus":"A confession of intoxicating, obsessive attraction, the lover getting the singer so high and wild he can't deny or resist; the ache is that the sweet promises they exchange still feel wasted on himself, hinting at self-doubt and a relationship built on vows he fears he'll break.","sonnet":"About being so intoxicated by someone that their repeated promises feel both irresistible and hollow — the narrator is too high to feel the heat of the situation, knows the promises are wasted on them, and can't stop believing anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"nero~satisfy":{"haiku":"A sensual, direct proposition to be intimate and fulfill desire, with physical touch and pleasure as the central message. The repetition emphasizes urgency and the offer to satisfy.","opus":"A short seduction in which the speaker is drawn to someone tonight, wanting to be taken for a ride and, insisting that touching them feels right, repeatedly asks to satisfy them.","sonnet":"A proposition for a mutual night together, framed as mutual desire — the narrator wants to come in, go for a ride, and satisfy the other person.","src":"haiku"},
"nero~tonight":{"haiku":"Regret crystallizes in shared moments as two people silently abandon memories of connection, forgetting in silence what they once cherished.","opus":"A frantic craving for one thing that both sustains and unravels the singer, teetering toward insanity while a fleeting shared night lets everything regrettable be forgotten in silence.","sonnet":"A brief, hypnotic declaration of shared presence: tonight the speaker needs something urgently enough to risk going insane without it, and the chorus opens that into a communal space where regrets are shared and the silence that usually buries them is broken, if only for the night.","src":"haiku"},
"nero~two-minds":{"haiku":"A speaker feels unheard in a relationship and believes their partner is perpetually indecisive and uncommitted, unable to know whether genuine care exists.","opus":"Exasperation builds as the narrator argues that even reading his partner's thoughts wouldn't tell him whether they cared, having repeated so often that small gestures make a person feel valued. The lover stays perpetually undecided. Nero set this romantic frustration against their sweeping cinematic electronics, the hook orbiting someone whose hesitation he can already forecast.","sonnet":"A relationship derailed by one partner's chronic ambivalence — the perpetual undecided state that makes small gestures of care impossible to land. The speaker has repeated the same observations too many times, knowing already what the response will be, and that foreknowledge is the clearest signal that nothing can change.","src":"haiku"},
"neurotech~deceive":{"haiku":"A speaker addresses conflict through images of burning and pain, positioning deception as a performative act they can choose to play or refrain from.","opus":"Apocalyptic imagery of fire falling from the sky pays for accumulated mistakes, the burning felt from the inside out as the accused keeps playing a false part. Prayer replaces the earlier rain as reckoning nears. Neurotech, the solo darkwave-metal project of Wulf, keeps the language spare and elemental, letting deceit and self-consuming flame stand as the whole grim equation.","sonnet":"A reckoning arrives from above like rain and fire, burning inward as the price of accumulated mistakes and delays. Someone is urged to keep performing their role while the narrator feels the deception from inside out — a cycle of self-betrayal and suppression that keeps repeating at a scorching cost.","src":"haiku"},
"never-back-down~feed-me-your-lies":{"haiku":"Venom and deception poison internally while broken promises drown. Truth and freedom remain forever withheld.","opus":"A dependence on a deceitful partner whose falsehoods wound and sicken, the singer surviving on the very deception that's smothering them; the trajectory shifts from gripping onto hope toward tearing loose and insisting on honesty. Never Back Down deliver it as heavy, cathartic modern rock.","sonnet":"About being trapped in a relationship built entirely on deception — the narrator knows the promises are hollow and the words contradict the actions, yet keeps clinging to hope; the song ends with a decision to stop feeding on the lies and walk away.","src":"haiku"},
"never-easy~i-could-tell-you-everything":{"haiku":"The speaker could reveal everything but cannot be present when their words would matter most.","opus":"An omnipotent-sounding offer, everything, anything, even the dead brought back, undercut by the one thing that can't be promised: presence when it matters. Power that means nothing beside the admission that he won't be there when they come.","sonnet":"A speaker offers total disclosure — knowledge, presence, even resurrection — but draws a single hard line: they cannot stay when consequences arrive, leaving the promise of intimacy permanently conditional on absence.","src":"haiku"},
"neverland-in-ashes~the-sentinel":{"haiku":"Trapped in a mind haunted by childhood monsters and mazes, the speaker needs another person to pull them back to reality.","opus":"A stark reckoning with mental illness pictured as childhood beasts that migrated from beneath the bed into the mind, the narrator lost in twisting corridors pursuing phantoms, going under, pleading for someone to haul him free and let color return. That someone turns into rescuer and guardian. Neverland in Ashes, a melodic metalcore band, match the vulnerable anxiety with soaring clean choruses and heavy contrast, casting another person as the way out.","sonnet":"The monsters that lived under the bed in childhood have migrated into the mind in adulthood — and the speaker, lost in mazes and drowning in moments, keeps finding their way back to one person who pulls them out. The sentinel is not a metaphor but a specific anchor, someone who makes the circling walls feel survivable.","src":"haiku"},
"nevermore~42147": {"haiku":"A mind spirals through denial and confusion, losing grip on reality in a disorienting internal landscape.","opus":"A first-person account of an ego-dissolving trip where logic crumbles and the self floats into another world before returning changed. The clinical final line reframes the whole thing as a mind-experiment on the self.","sonnet":"A brief, dreamlike account of dissociation — the narrator loses rational coherence and enters a state of warm, beautiful disorder, unsure if they've crossed into another world, and then slowly surfaces back to ordinary reality.","src":"haiku","fable":"Under its unexplained number of a title, this is a first-person trip report — the dissolution of logic and the ego's controls charted as a voyage inward. The middle drifts into a warm, boundaryless confusion where the self merges with the disorder it fears, before the pull back toward the familiar begins. What lifts it past mere psychedelia is the framing device: the return is uneasy, the speaker asks what he has made of himself, and the closing line reveals the whole descent as a deliberate trial — the mind as laboratory, the self as both scientist and specimen, the experiment now concluded but its result left unstated.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"ego dissolved then retrieved with no verdict returned","built":"the self cast as both scientist and specimen of itself"},
"nevermore~a-future-uncertain": {"haiku":"Bleak vision of collective human failure; judgment, hate, and contradiction corrupt youth while the future dims. Change is demanded but extinction looms unless minds break free.","opus":"A grim warning about humanity's flawed nature, senseless hatred, and a dark, bloody, uncertain future heading toward extinction. It pleads for people to free their minds, stop wasting life on hate, and change before it's too late.","sonnet":"The song expresses alarm at a humanity that perpetuates division, poisons young minds, and moves toward ecological and civilizational collapse. Against that bleak trajectory, it pleads for people to listen, abandon worthless hatred, and reach some point of shared understanding before the future runs out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A species-level prophecy in first person: crushing skies, a coming plague, extinction waiting at the end of the road unless great change comes. The mismatch of scale is the point — verses rage at planetary doom, yet the chorus offers a remedy almost embarrassingly small: listen first, and quit wasting a life on spite and contradiction. The intro's flash of personal resentment at being judged from above shows where the planetary sermon actually starts — one wounded ego extrapolated to a whole flawed race wearing the same human face.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","transcendence & consciousness","anger & defiance"],"means":"a planetary sermon extrapolated from one wounded ego","built":"species-scale doom remedied by an embarrassingly modest personal pivot"},
"nevermore~all-play-dead": {"haiku":"A song about collective passivity and moral disengagement, where people witness freedom's collapse but choose silence and inaction, retreating into quiet rooms to watch from the sidelines rather than resist.","opus":"Warrel Dane turns political apathy into a survival strategy, the numb citizen going motionless while power feeds on a collapsing freedom. Playing dead is both cowardice and the only sane response to a rigged game.","sonnet":"A political resignation piece — freedom is quietly dying while everyone watches and says nothing, assigned blame according to whoever's game is running; the narrator chooses passive witness over resistance, lying still as the machinery of control feeds on compliance.","src":"haiku","fable":"Playing dead is the reflex of prey that cannot fight, and on Dreaming Neon Black — Warrel Dane's concept record about a man unraveling after his beloved is lost to a cult — Nevermore stretches that animal trick into an entire posture toward the world. The narrator seals himself in a shuttered chamber away from every watching eye, going limp and mute so the feeders pass him over, surrendering the woman he can no longer reach to their velvet, intoxicating faith. What the numbing repetition of the title drills home is that the survival tactic has become the disease: to endure by staying silent, refusing to name whoever is guilty and merely watching the predators feed, is to already be the corpse he is only pretending to be.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","politics & society","surveillance & control","faith & the occult"],"means":"survival tactic hardening into the death it imitates","built":"repetition of the title drilling the tactic into the disease"},
"nevermore~ambivalent": {"haiku":"The speaker hides their hatred behind a facade, shaped by time and circumstance into a person of deception and smoldering despair.","opus":"A confession of feigned indifference masking deep hatred, in which the narrator hides behind a smile and a played-fool ignorance while his own hate cages him and his grasp at truth turns to despair.","sonnet":"A person who has performed indifference for years — smiling through hatred, playing the fool — finally admits the deception has shaped who they became, and though they still want truth, the world around them runs on bleeding hearts and soiled minds.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study in willed self-deception: the speaker performs indifference as a survival tactic, smiling through a game whose rules he pretends not to feel, until the smile becomes indistinguishable from the surrender it hides. The song's core admission is that the composure is a prison of his own making — the hate he thinks he has mastered has actually hardened into the cage he lives inside, and the self he built out of that suppression is the only self he has left. Warrel Dane's characteristic move is to turn outward accusation back on the accuser: the fool, the deceiver, the soiled mind aren't separate targets but reflections of a shared corruption he counts himself inside. What keeps the song from being pure cynicism is the ache underneath the mask — even inside the pose of not caring, he still wants the truth, and that unkilled longing is exactly what makes the ambivalence a lie he can't fully believe.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"feigned indifference caging the self it was meant to protect","built":"outward accusation turned back until accuser and accused share the corruption"},
"nevermore~and-the-maiden-spoke": {"haiku":"A tormented female spirit from centuries past haunts the living, whispering of cold beauty and madness, moving unseen through the world.","opus":"A ghost story that doubles as a meditation on madness and marginalization, the seventeenth-century spirit standing in for anyone driven insane and disposable. Nevermore's usual anti-conformity streak surfaces at the end, framing her as a casualty of a system that grinds people down.","sonnet":"The song follows a tortured spirit — a woman born in 1617 — who haunts a space by moving objects, whispering in ears, and speaking to those who are awake, her words fragmented as if her soul is already gone. She is described as deranged, sorrowful, and condemned, a system slave led to a grave without redemption.","src":"haiku","fable":"A ghost story dated to 1617, sung from the spot where a poltergeist's small mischief — a chair sliding across an empty room downstairs — shades into something the living narrator cannot wave off as a draft. The maiden keeps pressing the oldest comfort, a promise that there is nothing to fear, and Warrel Dane sings it back until repetition wears the reassurance thin and turns it into coaxing. Her speech fractures into stutters and then dries up entirely, so the closer she draws the less she can actually say, her whole message narrowing to a spirit's static. The final turn strips the gothic trappings away and recasts her not as a wandering spirit but as one more soul used up and marched off, her haunting only the residue of a life that redemption never reached.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Her refrain calls the listener the blue insane — the same phrase The Day You Built the Wall hurls at its wall-builder; late Dane wired the album together with repeated tells.","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind","politics & society"],"means":"a spirit's comfort narrowing into static as she draws near","built":"her speech fractures into stutter then silence the closer she comes"},
"nevermore~believe-in-nothing": {"haiku":"The speaker questions faith and meaning in a world of suffering where nothing is sacred and everyone remains ultimately alone and unfulfilled.","opus":"A bleak sermon of despair aimed at someone who's slipped past saving, insisting nothing is holy, permanent, conclusive or genuine, so the days are numbered. The current running through it is spiritual exhaustion, faith in emptiness paired with a faint hope of ever finding relief or a glimpse of the future, only to pray and come up hollow.","sonnet":"An exhausted nihilism after some unspecified loss or collapse — nothing is sacred, no one is saved, faith offers only empty prayers, and the future holds no cure for sorrow.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane sets a creed of total negation to a melody that keeps reaching upward, and the contradiction is the whole point: a man who insists on believing in emptiness still phrases each denial as a hymn, still asks whether we will ever glimpse the cure for our shared sorrow. The chorus works like a mantra worn smooth by repetition, each pass stripping away another comfort — salvation, permanence, the solid existence of the people around him — until only the bare word survives, dissolving on the last exhaled breath. Yet the closing plea to pray for tomorrow gives the game away, because someone who had truly surrendered hope would not still be found kneeling.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a creed of total negation phrased as an ascending hymn","built":"the closing plea to pray for tomorrow betraying the surrender it claims"},
"nevermore~beyond-within":{"haiku":"Warns of apocalypse and societal collapse, describing apathetic observation of suicide and despair. The song critiques reality as stage performance where the world falls apart.","opus":"An apocalyptic embrace of civilization's collapse, greeting a hateful, doomed age spawned by ignorance. Against a backdrop of spreading despair and indifference, it tracks one man's slide out of reason as truth and invented myth stop being distinguishable.","sonnet":"A darkly prophetic survey of a world collapsing under ignorance and violence — civilizations fall, sanity fractures, and the speaker watches in apathy as suicides shock a world moving toward inevitable ruin, with the millennium framed not as renewal but as the final fall.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane greets the year 2000 not with fear but with relish, and the song's real move is watching that welcome curdle from prophecy into a con. He hails a darkened planet as a verdict humanity earned through ignorance, then tips his hand by calling it all a stage — catastrophe as theater, with himself the playwright forging a history out of legends nobody lived. The messianic pose holds only until the bridge, where the register drops into a barker's dares and taunts, offering to sell you the dreaming and asking whether you even remember his name. The mask slips to expose a small man rebranding his own breakdown as vision, cracking under a role he inflated past bearing. The outro reframes the whole sermon: the collapse being welcomed belongs to one mind's sanity, so the apocalypse shrinks to a single delusion mistaking itself for revelation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","artifice & performance","faith & the occult"],"means":"messianic apocalypse that shrinks to a single delusion mistaking itself for revelation","built":"barker's taunts in the bridge expose the prophet pose as a con man's pitch"},
"nevermore~bittersweet-feast": {"haiku":"The masses are manipulated and deceived, feeding on lies like sheep; power structures censor free thought while the powerless march toward their own slaughter.","opus":"Nevermore's dark warning about a coming war on free will, casting the masses as sheep marched to the fire while lizardly elites feed on lies and censor thought; the feast is the willing consumption of poisoned, comforting untruths.","sonnet":"A brutal indictment of societal conformity and corporate-industrial mind control — the masses are sheep led to slaughter by lizard-brained rulers who censor thought and feed on the resulting ignorance.","src":"haiku","fable":"A media-age slaughterhouse allegory that runs on one image-system: the public as sheep and worker pigs marched to the pyre, the powerful as lizards and maggots who feed on the carcass — and the horror is that the livestock cooperate, swallowing poisoned truths with pride. The recurring final-warning line reframes propaganda not as deception but as an open declaration of war on free will that nobody bothers to resist.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Its final pre-chorus trades the warning line for the holocaust of thought is dawning — the title of the album's instrumental, three tracks later.","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"propaganda reframed as open warfare on free will","built":"the feast is the willing consumption the song never pretends to hide"},
"nevermore~born": {"haiku":"Religious and social conformity breeding ignorance and spiritual sickness; the legacy of inherited suffering and apathy.","opus":"A bleak indictment of dogma and enforced conformity, religion and authority breeding ignorance and apathy. The fear is generational, that indifference is inherited and our children will be left with nothing.","sonnet":"The song condemns organized religion and political authority for breeding ignorance, enforcing conformity, and fostering spiritual sickness and apathy, warning that if nothing changes the next generation will inherit a meaningless world of silence and indifference.","src":"haiku","fable":"An atheist indictment that opens on religion as the seed of ignorance — sworn against killing and against thinking in the same breath — and traces it forward into mass conformity and apathy. The chorus turns the accusation inward: born the same, walking alone, indifference our shared surname, so the target widens from the church to everyone sleeping through the decay. The closing warning carries the argument's real weight — if nothing changes, the inheritance left to our children is nothing at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"indifference named as the inheritance left to our children","built":"the accusation opens on religion then widens to swallow everyone sleeping through the decay"},
"nevermore~c-b-f": {"haiku":"A critique of apathetic numbness and self-destructive addiction. The speaker witnesses someone choosing death through indifference and chemical escape rather than confronting their will to live.","opus":"A grim study of addiction and self-ruin, watching a friend deaden their hurt, waste away and flirt with self-destruction dose by dose, the speaker swearing to tally the time left until they're gone if they keep on this way. Nevermore render the despair in dense, dark thrash-metal.","sonnet":"A direct address to someone destroying themselves through substance abuse — watching ambition die, will erode, and life waste away — with the speaker finally admitting cold resignation: if the person keeps choosing this path, he will count the days until they are gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"An addiction song that refuses to plead. It watches someone hollow themselves out, ambition burning off into numbness, each fix a rehearsal for the death wish underneath, and for most of its length it stays in that grim, clinical observation of decay. The turn comes when the speaker imagines his words breaking through to pull the addict back toward daylight — but that hope is offered on a condition, and when the chorus of care meets the flat cruelty of the ending, the song reveals its real terms: love here has a limit, and the speaker has already decided he will grieve nothing if the choice is to keep dying. The title's blunt initials are the point — the tenderness and the abandonment are the same voice, exhausted past the ability to keep mourning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"conditional love expiring into blunt readiness to grieve nothing","built":"blunt initials fusing tenderness and abandonment in the same exhausted voice"},
"nevermore~cenotaph": {"haiku":"A silent woman lies unknown, dreamlessly, while her cenotaph carries her name; the song calls to awaken her from velvet sleep behind walls where dreamers supposedly never truly die.","opus":"A gothic elegy for a woman who died unknown, her memorial an empty tomb marking a body that isn't there. It broods on erasure and grief, the dreamer sleeping past all attempts to wake or reclaim her.","sonnet":"An elegy to a woman who has vanished or died — the cenotaph built in her name is a monument to absence, and the singer desperately tries to wake her from what may be death.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cenotaph is an empty tomb — a monument raised over an absence, honoring someone whose body was never recovered. Nevermore turn that image on a woman who has not left but withdrawn, sealed in velvet sleep while the narrator pleads outside her wall for her to wake. The refrain is the sound of grieving someone still breathing: he calls her back the way you would call the dead, and gets only the silence of a person who is hiding rather than gone. Threaded through Dreaming Neon Black's account of a lover slipping into madness, this is the moment the loss gets its truest name — a burial with no corpse, a stone bearing her name that nobody can say what it holds.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","death & grief","madness & the mind"],"means":"grieving a breathing person through the forms of burial","built":"the empty-tomb title naming an absence that has no body to mourn"},
"nevermore~create-the-infinite": {"haiku":"Humanity blind to reality swims through poisoned design; creation spawns only infection and imperfection while approaching an inevitable cold, unknowable end without salvation.","opus":"A grim, godless assessment of humanity as sightless creatures drowning in the toxicity of their own botched making, whose rest and flaws breed only sickness. The blunt insistence that no deity and no trendy salvation exist frames extinction as a wound the species inflicts entirely on itself.","sonnet":"About humanity's blindness to its own design flaws — salvation is declared dead, enemies of reality bring a cleansing sickness, and the refrain of counting to seven (the day of rest) becomes an act of infection, positioning religious order itself as the disease.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane opens as a doomsayer narrating the species' ending, framing creation itself as a botched equation: the seventh act of making sanctifies nothing but breeds rot in whatever it touches. The recurring taunt about sightless creatures paddling through a designed toxin becomes Nevermore's blunt indictment of intelligent design as a kind of engineered blindness, faith mistaken for vision. The song's spine is its swerve from theology to weather — a long central passage drops the argument for a brooding storm at sea, gulls scudding overhead like used-up souls, the tempest turning until warmth drains out of the living entirely. That descent lands on a flat triple negation: no coming catastrophe left to dread, no deity, no marketable salvation, which strips the earlier scripture of any redemptive exit. So the closing return of the blind-swimmer refrain reads less as chorus than as verdict already delivered.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","nature & the elements"],"means":"intelligent design recast as engineered blindness toward extinction","built":"triple negation at the close stripping scripture of any redemptive exit"},
"nevermore~crystal-ship-the-doors-cover":{"haiku":"Before sleep or forgetting, a final kiss bridges the void between desire and inevitable loss.","opus":"Warren Duffey's crew reaching back to Morrison's farewell reverie, a lover's goodbye laced with druggy bliss and dread. The metal remake keeps the eerie tenderness of a man drifting off, half in love and half already gone.","sonnet":"A cover of The Doors song about leaving someone at the threshold of unconsciousness — the crystal ship is filling with escapist pleasures (thrills, adventures, distractions) and the narrator promises to return; it's imbued with the sweetness of impending departure and the bittersweet knowledge of parting.","src":"haiku"},
"nevermore~dead-heart-in-a-dead-world": {"haiku":"A person inhabits a rotten, emptied world and describes their heart as dead; though wounds may heal, emotional numbness and decay persist even after suffering.","opus":"Nevermore survey a spiritually bankrupt world through a narrator who feels betrayed, hollowed out and left for dead. But the song pivots from despair to fierce defiance, calling on the listener to reject false authorities and masters, take ownership of their own pain, and trust that even a deadened spirit can be revived.","sonnet":"Utter emotional desolation set against a dying world — the narrator feels betrayed and hollow, but threads of hope appear: wounds can heal, numbed hearts can come back to life if the doors that slammed shut are torn away.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane opens by surveying a ruined civilization — the final man left standing toppling, decay where a world should be — and the real horror is that the narrator feels nothing watching it, a numbness he reads as betrayal. The middle turns that desolation inward: he keeps clawing up out of a black lake toward some inner light, thinks he has surfaced, then sinks again, home reduced to a hole that keeps bleeding him. Against all this the chorus barks its triple imperative — tear down every idol, topple every ruler, and stop nursing the pain that has anchored you — a demand to torch the whole apparatus of belief rather than negotiate with grief. Yet Dane refuses total nihilism: even wood gone cold, he insists, can catch fire again, and the door once shut against the heart can be ripped off its hinges, so the deadness the title names is a condition to escape, not a verdict. The command hardens into ritual by the close, less argument than incantation, as if survival here means shouting the same refusal until the dead thing inside finally answers.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","dissociation & numbness","faith & the occult"],"means":"imperative to torch every idol rather than negotiate with grief","built":"the refrain hardening from argument into incantation by the close"},
"nevermore~deconstruction": {"haiku":"The world dismantles itself through violence, exploitation, and spiritual erosion—a place where nothing remains but decay, predators, and abandonment.","opus":"A bitter attack on organized religion that recasts faith as a tool of harm and pictures God as an indifferent designer of human destruction. Its darker current is a vision of civilization rotting away, posing grim questions about who inherits a world once meaning and belief have collapsed entirely.","sonnet":"A vision of complete civilizational and spiritual collapse — religion weaponized, God absent or sadistic, the weak exploited, the earth stripped bare — with the narrator dissolving into it, asking who tends anything when everything is gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane opens by watching his own reflection go under while the heavens simply cease to exist, and from that first submersion the song treats ruin less as violence than as a slow structural failure of everything that once carried meaning. The engine underneath is a litany of questions with no one left alive to answer them — who feeds on rot once even the worms go blind, who works soil already reduced to black ash. Dane's real quarry is inherited faith, the crucified man reimagined as a chisel wearing the soul thin and the fashionable prophet exposed as one more herdsman stalking the flock he pretends to guard. The turn that gives the piece its teeth is the moment the speaker quits asking and volunteers his own surrender, offering himself to be taken apart and naming himself the animal at the bottom of it — complicity, not innocence, the only honest ground remaining. When the refrain finally slides from my world to our world, the private undoing becomes everyone's, the intimate wreckage revealed as the shared condition it was quietly describing all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The surrender line names I Am the Dog — the album's own fourth track — the concept's phrases bleeding between songs.","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"complicity offered as the only honest ground once faith is dismantled","built":"the pronoun shift from my world to our world expanding private ruin into shared condition"},
"nevermore~dreaming-neon-black": {"haiku":"Longing for connection with an absent, mysterious figure encountered only in dreams, where shared drowning and emotional dissolution become the meeting place for two lost souls.","opus":"The devastated centerpiece of Nevermore's concept album about Dane's lover vanishing into mental illness and possibly suicide. Grief becomes a drowning fantasy where reuniting means letting the water take you both, love surviving as haunting rather than presence.","sonnet":"Grief for someone lost — perhaps to a cult, a death, or a transformation the narrator cannot understand — the singer can still feel them, speaks to them in dreamtime water, and waits in suspended mourning for a reunion that may never come.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man keeps a lost lover alive by summoning her into a submerged dream-country of shifting currents, begging her to sink there beside him rather than surface into the world that took her away. Warrel Dane sings it as vigil rather than mourning: the refrain returns again and again because the calling is the point, an incantation repeated until repetition itself becomes the only shape his devotion has left. What the song refuses to say plainly is whether she was stolen by other people or by the poison they carried, and that refusal holds the summons open, since naming the cause would let her finally be gone. By the end he narrows to one confession of silent suffering, a jester who has lost his dove, still vowing to answer whenever she calls back to him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Dane built the album on his own loss: a former girlfriend who left with a religious group and whom he never saw again — the recurring drowning dreams across the record were, by his account, his own.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","death & grief","madness & the mind"],"means":"grief kept open as incantation to forestall a final naming","built":"the refrain returning because the calling itself is the only shape devotion has left"},
"nevermore~emptiness-unobstructed":{"haiku":"A seeker travels toward spiritual transcendence and answers, confronting emptiness and confusion. Though truth remains elusive, a pained angel offers shelter amid cosmic confusion.","opus":"A seeker begs to be carried somewhere higher and farther in pursuit of something greater, wrestling with a baffling world nobody grasps and a stark, merciless reality. Within all that void he encounters a wounded angelic presence that comforts and sings to him through his darkest stretches.","sonnet":"The song expresses a spiritual restlessness — a search for meaning, transcendence, and answers that the confusing, spinning world cannot provide. The speaker yearns to reach a higher plane beyond the valley of deception, finding some comfort in the idea of a \"pained angel\" offering shelter through the darkest hours.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's paradox carries the whole argument. Warrel Dane treats the void not as lack but as a cleared medium, a hollow he pleads to be carried into, so the chorus lands as a hymn for transcendence spoken in the language of nothingness. Every verse then refuses to deliver him anywhere real — a false lowland, a churning globe of bafflement, a heaven he can only name a disaster — and the song becomes a portrait of a seeker who distrusts each promised arrival as one more mirage. Its hinge is the wounded messenger held out as solace: a guardian who hums him through his bleakest stretches, yet whose own hurt hollows the comfort. The closing picture — time stopped, something unnamed growing on bare rock — hangs between rebirth and petrified defeat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","alienation & emptiness","faith & the occult"],"means":"a seeker who distrusts every promised arrival as one more mirage","built":"the title's paradox frames the whole: the void named as a cleared medium rather than an absence"},
"nevermore~enemies-of-reality": {"haiku":"The song critiques conformity and mass manipulation through media and culture. It frames thinking people as threats to societal norms, positioning resistance to authority as the only authentic stance.","opus":"A defiant stance from outsiders who refuse to conform, railing against a society that sanitizes, processes, and force-feeds the masses while calling reality the strongest drug.","sonnet":"A group of nonconformists defines itself against a society that packages and force-feeds its members mainstream values, positioning themselves as those who refuse to be sanitised or consumed by the ignorance that dominates the world around them.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane sings from inside a we that has stopped asking to be understood and started declaring itself, the misfits recast as manufactured refuse spat out by a machine that force-feeds the compliant masses their portion of rot. The chorus offers a communion meal of maggots, an invitation to swallow the enemy's poison and belong. Nevermore's poise is that the outsider here is no wounded victim: the closing turn flips the sermon outward, promising that whoever opens wide for this ritual will be remade into the very dread they came to escape.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","politics & society"],"means":"outsider identity offered as the dread the listener came to escape","built":"communion meal of maggots inverting the eucharist into a dare"},
"nevermore~engines-of-hate": {"haiku":"Truth spoken becomes fuel for machines of hatred; sheep follow blindly while the speaker despairs at a world that manufactures and perpetuates cruelty as machinery.","opus":"Warrel Dane takes aim at manufactured outrage and the passivity that lets it thrive, casting himself as a tempter who will drag the truth out by force. It attacks a media culture that mass-produces sanctimony and herds people into division while the hatred keeps churning.","sonnet":"A provocateur declares himself a corrosive presence who spits truth directly into the machinery that produces hatred and conformity — the song positions the speaker as a chemical irritant against religious and social systems that grind people into obedient, divided sheep.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane sings this as a tempter, offering himself as the whisper on your shoulder while the world cools around you, and the seduction is the point: cruelty arrives wearing the flattering mask of an honest friend who only wants to level with you. The refrain casts the singer as a saboteur pouring his own venom into the very machinery he claims to be exposing, so the apparatus that manufactures hatred and the voice condemning it turn out to burn the same fuel. By the bridge the mass has become livestock on an assembly line, herded toward obedience and then set against itself, and the real horror is that the grinding never stops even after the con has been named aloud. Dane's misanthropy runs diagnostic rather than gloating, indicting the apathy that shrugs and looks away just as squarely as the mechanism churning out impurity by the batch.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"the voice condemning hatred running on hatred's own fuel","built":"narrator performing seducer and diagnostician simultaneously, condemning and embodying the machine"},
"nevermore~evolution-169": {"haiku":"Humans crawl through a maze of madness and chaos as experimental subjects, caught between self-destruction and the pursuit of impossible perfection.","opus":"Warrel Dane framing modern life as a lab maze where humans are rats seeking a perfect view, urging you to kill the ego and refuse fixed thinking to survive the organized chaos of what we're becoming.","sonnet":"A portrait of humanity as lab rats navigating a maze of madness and chaos, driven by ego and desire toward a collective dissolution that feels both inevitable and surreal.","src":"haiku","fable":"Nevermore stages the whole human race as caged animals nudged through a maze, watched by an unseen crowd that treats survival as a spectacle to be scored. The chorus counts the bodies off in pairs and files, a grim census that dresses mass drift up as progress toward some higher rung on the ladder. Warrel Dane's real target is that promise itself: the reaching hand that closes on knowledge it can approach but never hold, the killed ego, the prayer for a rope rather than a revelation. What the song calls organized chaos is the trap sprung clean, a design that reads as freedom only from inside the walls. Its last plea, refusing to let the mind lock onto a single side, is the only exit it can name from the experiment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","transcendence & consciousness","politics & society"],"means":"freedom and captivity made indistinguishable from inside the maze","built":"chorus counting bodies in pairs and files, dressing mass drift as progress"},
"nevermore~final-product": {"haiku":"A critique of how media exploits tragedy for profit, examining a world ravaged by religious and political violence, where survival in decay becomes the final human product.","opus":"A condemnation of a decaying world, attacking media that profits from tragedy and religions and governments that let the innocent die, leaving a lone survivor questioning how anyone endures.","sonnet":"A broadside against a world in moral and civilisational decay — media that profits from suicide, religious and governmental violence that starves the innocent, and the creeping certainty of Armageddon — summed up as the inevitable final product of human hatred.","src":"haiku","fable":"Humanity audited as a factory line: the finished product is a world in slow decay, media profiting from packaged suicides while famine victims are left to guess whether god or government starved them. The structure does the escalating — reportage in the verses, doomsday believers in the first bridge, then the POV collapses into a lone survivor wondering how he is still breathing, as if the Armageddon the cranks predicted quietly arrived. The chorus refuses the comfort of a verdict: the question stays unanswered and opaque.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the question of humanity's worth left deliberately unanswered","built":"POV collapses from editorial observer to lone survivor mid-song"},
"nevermore~forever": {"haiku":"A mourning lover vows eternal devotion to someone who has passed, promising to meet them in dreams and willing to follow them into the afterlife.","opus":"Grief for a dead beloved whom the narrator insists never truly died, keeping her alive in his heart and vowing to meet her in dreams; a mourning song that clings to reunion in sleep because it's the only place she still exists.","sonnet":"A grief song addressed to someone who has died — the narrator insists they live on in his heart, promises to meet them in dreams, and refuses to let go, staying suspended in mourning in a neon-black dark.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man keeps vigil for a woman he has lost, and the song lets him address her as if she has passed into sleep rather than been taken away, her face still surfacing to him out of the dark. Warrel Dane sings her into a soft afterworld where she rests untroubled, then vows to descend and swim to her whenever she summons him, which turns mourning into a devotional trespass across the border between waking and the grave. The refrain refuses to settle: he insists she is permanent and undying, yet in the same breath admits he still cannot say where she has gone, permanence and absence pressed together without resolving. The final turn is the sharpest, as a stage empties and the players withdraw, the grieving voice swells to claim it is everything that was and everything yet to come, an overreach of love that would swallow the whole world before the chapter quietly closes and dims.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"permanence and absence pressed together and left unresolved","built":"the closing overreach swelling to claim everything before the chapter quietly dims"},
"nevermore~garden-of-gray": {"haiku":"In a mystical garden space, two people embrace sensuality and passion without fear, seeking ecstasy and transcendence from time through intimate touch. It's an escape where ordinary rules don't apply.","opus":"Sensual, gothic seduction cast as an escape into a private twilight world where lust suspends time and fear. Warrel Dane's theatrical romanticism, ecstasy as both drug and communion.","sonnet":"A seductive rendezvous in an otherworldly garden — dark, lusty, focused on the physical pull between two people in a space outside ordinary reality where desire and sensation are the only currency. It's sexual in tone, the gray garden functioning as an escape from consequence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction staged as ritual: the speaker waits for moonrise and for nightshade to bloom in a lover's eyes, then crosses over to claim flesh and shivering lust as if stepping onto consecrated ground. The gray of the title drains the scene of color and consequence alike, a threshold where fear dissolves and two bodies can vanish together, unjudged and unmisread. What lifts it past an erotic tableau is how the language keeps straining toward the sacred and the surreal — hands laid on gently, the senses turned to music, the whole encounter reframed as transcendence rather than mere appetite. The refrain circles that walled garden until it hardens into incantation, and by the closing repetitions the words dissolve into pure chant, the destination less a place than a trance the voice refuses to leave.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The lyric already names the politics of ecstasy — the phrase, taken from Timothy Leary's 1968 book, would title the band's next album a year later.","themes":["love & desire","transcendence & consciousness","freedom & escape"],"means":"erotic encounter reframed as sacred threshold without color or consequence","built":"refrain circling until it dissolves from words into pure chant"},
"nevermore~godmoney": {"haiku":"Corrupt religious figures exploit the weak by monetizing salvation and manipulating faith, brainwashing people to send money while claiming even God requires payment.","opus":"A blistering takedown of televangelists who peddle salvation for donations, promising heaven by post while exploiting the vulnerable. The narrator slips into the mask of a corrupt holy hustler whose true aim is dominating minds, sneering at the absurd notion that even the divine is after your cash.","sonnet":"A direct attack on televangelism and prosperity-gospel Christianity, depicting preachers as parasitic con artists who sell eternal life for donations and use religion as a tool of mind control.","src":"haiku","fable":"A furious teardown of the pay-to-pray televangelist, sung from inside the con: Warrel Dane wears the huckster's own mask, the beaming preacher glowing out of the tube, promising salvation you can wire in by the collection plate. The refrain that God himself has gone broke and needs your donation lands as pure contempt, exposing the swap of faith for commerce where the flock is milked and the real goal is never devotion but seizing the mind. The persona keeps sliding — a holy fraud, a flatterer, a green-tinted idol the exact shade of a dollar bill — so that sanctity and greed finally wear the same face. What curdles the venom is the crowd it names: people worn thin enough to swallow the pitch, worshipping the very swindler who fattens himself on their weakness.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","money & the street","politics & society"],"means":"sanctity and greed converging until they wear the same face","built":"narrator sliding through preacher masks until holiness and fraud become indistinguishable"},
"nevermore~i-am-the-dog":{"haiku":"A dreamer waits nightly for the world to end while losing sanity, tormented by a woman who identifies as the dog, suggesting loyalty and devotion gone wrong. The repeated dream and her screams represent inescapable psychological entrapment.","opus":"Warrel Dane spirals through recurring nightmare and creeping madness fixated on a woman who becomes both muse and curse. The song is about sanity dissolving into obsession, identity swallowed by an inescapable dream.","sonnet":"A recurring nightmare about a woman — loyal, unwavering, possibly dead or lost — whose identity the narrator can't fully grasp. She screams \"I am the dog\" from some alcove in the dream, an image that repeats as his sanity erodes. The song is about obsessive grief spiraling into fragmentation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A node in Dreaming Neon Black's concept arc — the grief-and-madness cycle that follows a lover's vanishing — this song locks the narrator inside a nightmare that recurs identically at each nightfall, waiting for an apocalypse that refuses to arrive, so the ending he wants is denied and the loop just tightens. The woman is the corrosive center: everything he pours into her name is sweat and blood, and her identity fractures across the verses from beloved — a creature of the zodiac's canine sign, incapable of betrayal — into the shrieking apparition of the alcove who claims that same animal as a self-declaration. That reversal is the read: the dog is loyalty and abjection at once, and Dane leaves it ambiguous whether the debased thing crying out is her or the projection of a mind unravelling past the point where it can tell the two apart. The escalating structure enacts the collapse, as the sanity-dissolving swell finally breaks twice in the closing chorus, drowning the speaker in the very flood he'd been braced against, so the song resolves not by ending but by giving up the wait.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","heartbreak & loss","death & grief"],"means":"loyalty and abjection held in the same word until the mind dissolves","built":"the sanity-dissolving swell breaks twice in the closing chorus"},
"nevermore~inside-four-walls": {"haiku":"Incarceration and injustice; a friend is imprisoned while corrupt systems protect violent criminals. The song condemns legal inequity and celebrates the mind's resilience against institutional control.","opus":"A cry from within a jail cell against a tyrannical state and a corrupt legal system that imprisoned a friend while spreading falsehoods. It fumes at the injustice that narcotics convictions frequently draw harsher sentences than violent predators receive, insisting the authorities can seize a body but never conquer the mind.","sonnet":"The song protests the imprisonment of a friend under a system where the government is always right and political corruption goes unchallenged, pointing specifically to the injustice that drug offenders in the US routinely receive longer sentences than violent criminals. The walls are both literal and the walls of a broken system.","src":"haiku","fable":"A prison song that never quite decides whether the cell is literal or the state itself, and that ambiguity is the point: the walls belong equally to an incarcerated friend and to a citizen governed by a system that dictates belief and calls itself always right. Warrel Dane sings from grievance rather than analysis, hammering the same refrain about stolen freedom and lying authority until the repetition becomes its own kind of confinement, the mind circling the injustice it cannot undo. Its sharpest thought is a bitter comparison — that worse crimes draw lighter sentences — which turns the complaint from self-pity into an indictment of a rigged calculus. The closing turn is the whole song's reason for being: everything else can be seized, but the last line insists the one thing the walls cannot reach is the mind, making captivity a test of interior sovereignty rather than a defeat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","freedom & escape","anger & defiance"],"means":"captivity posed as a test of interior sovereignty rather than a defeat","built":"repetitive refrain enacting the mind circling an injustice it cannot undo"},
"nevermore~insignificant": {"haiku":"A person grapples with cosmic insignificance, unable to escape indecision while witnessing beauty and death cycling eternally.","opus":"Warrel Dane sits with human smallness under a vast, uncaring universe, using the daily death of the daylight as an emblem of a nature that ends things without ever explaining them. The song rests in the raw realization of being a negligible fleck in something enormous.","sonnet":"The confrontation with cosmic insignificance — looking at the beauty of a dying sky and realizing you are a speck, that beauty itself collapses without learning why, and that everything born beneath it is simply born to die.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man stares down a reddening sky and asks whether he counts for anything, then answers himself only by watching that same sky bleed out into black and go on dying without him. The refrain — insignificant, am I? — returns unresolved every time, because the question is rhetorical armor: he already suspects the answer is yes and dresses the dread up as astronomy, one grain lost somewhere in a vast band of light. Warrel Dane sings it less as complaint than as cold arithmetic, the voice that made Nevermore a thinking band's metal act, turning cosmic scale into a personal verdict. What deepens across the verses is the ownership: the dusk that dissolves the day is the same force bleaching out his past, so smallness stops being a fact about the universe and becomes a sentence passed on the self, handed down by a sky indifferent enough to keep repeating it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","nature & the elements"],"means":"cosmic smallness internalised as a sentence passed on the self","built":"the sky's indifferent repetition becoming a verdict the narrator hands himself"},
"nevermore~lost": {"haiku":"The speaker experiences ego death or psychedelic dissolution, becoming pure energy unbound by physical form and time.","opus":"Nevermore turn a psychedelic trip into a celebration of dissolving the ego. The narrator gives himself over completely to the experience, shedding form and identity, burning across imagined existences as pure sensation. Framed by an LSD endorsement and Pink Floyd echoes, it treats the journey as a route to boundless perception, and he decides he'd rather never return to ordinary reality.","sonnet":"An LSD trip described as a permanent condition of being lost — the narrator chases colours toward the sun, refuses to come down from a perspective where they can see forever, and accepts the dissolution of self as the only honest way to exist.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane sings from inside the trip rather than about it, a voice climbing until the ground stops mattering and the descent becomes something he refuses on principle. The imagery is deliberately dissolving — a body burning across successive lives, shapeless, swimming seas that think back at him — and he wears the word for the acid casualty as a badge instead of a warning. His refusal to return carries the whole argument: what looks from outside like a wrecked mind is, from where he stands, an unbroken sightline into something endless. The closing borrowing of the Grateful Dead's long strange trip turns the song into an elegy, sung for the burnout by a narrator who has quietly become him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape"],"means":"the acid casualty's label worn as badge rather than warning","built":"the Grateful Dead's long strange trip borrowed to close an elegy from inside it"},
"nevermore~love-bites-bonus-track":{"haiku":"A vampire seduces and drains victims in the night, using sensual allure to trap them before consumption, portraying vampiric love as predatory possession.","opus":"A vampire's seduction told from the predator's side, rising in the dead of night to creep across a sleeper's lips, one taste claiming the soul while the victim moans and half-welcomes the attack. Desire and death fuse completely. A cover carried by Warrel Dane's soaring voice, it fits Nevermore's dark, theatrical progressive-metal, turning gothic bloodlust into something almost operatic.","sonnet":"A vampire seduction told from the predator's perspective — gliding in at midnight, feeding on a willing victim who always knew on some level that surrender was coming, and who moaned rather than screamed when the kiss arrived. The horror and the eroticism are presented as the same thing, and the creature makes no apology for owning the soul it has drained.","src":"haiku"},
"nevermore~medicated-nation": {"haiku":"Addiction coils through the spine like a serpent; medication controls emotion and sensation while blind narcotics create symbiotic pain and pleasure.","opus":"Addiction voiced from the drug's own perspective, a parasite coiled in your spine that owns you the moment you taste it. The band widens it to a whole sedated society of blind neurotics numbed by pharmaceuticals.","sonnet":"A song narrated from the perspective of addiction itself — the drug speaks directly to its host, coiling around the spine, demanding to be fed while the user is too numbed and obedient to resist, living in a society of willfully medicated blindness.","src":"haiku","fable":"Addiction gets the microphone: the drug itself narrates, a serpent coiled around the spine that keeps asking whether you remembered to feed it while you were the one broken and bleeding — dependency inverted into the addict serving the substance. Dane widens the lens from one vein to a whole medicated nation, folding street narcotics and prescription culture into the same deified ritual, control marketed as relief.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"dependency inverted so the addict serves the substance","built":"the drug narrates, casting the speaker as the thing that feeds it"},
"nevermore~moonrise-through-mirrors-of-death": {"haiku":"Isolation and psychological torment from judgment and rejection by society.","opus":"Being judged and shunned drives the narrator into a violent interior dreamworld, where a rising presence in his mind is the reckoning for everything that went unspoken. Nevermore blur self-hypnosis, insanity and dying until it's unclear which one has claimed him, the underground doubling as a grave.","sonnet":"The speaker is trapped between waking violence and dreaming obsession, haunted by everything left unsaid in a relationship that ended in death, oscillating between soliloquy and psychosis as the moonrise triggers a reckoning with consequences they can't escape.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man circles his own reflection, pivoting left then right under someone's judgment, and the song refuses to settle whether he is composing a private speech, cracking apart, or talking himself into a trance — it poses all three as one question and answers none of them. That triple uncertainty is the engine, and it lands squarely in Warrel Dane's range: a voice built for the outcast who can never tell his own clarity from his own sickness, here narrating from a mind that no longer separates lucid daydream from breakdown. The lunar swell he feels cresting behind his eyes works at once as vision and as symptom, and the recurring tally of everything the other person never managed to voice arrives as a body — first the accused is dead, then, more quietly damning, the count turns plural and both of them are gone. The greeting he keeps extending toward the grave curdles by the last pass into something almost tender, an embrace of a flawed thing he has stopped trying to repair, until the mirror the title promised shows no far side and the invitation was only ever addressed back to himself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"lucid daydream and breakdown posed as one question left unanswered","built":"the mirror of the title showing no far side, the invitation addressed back to himself"},
"nevermore~my-acid-words": {"haiku":"Amid pervasive despair and meaninglessness, the speaker questions whether their words matter after death, fearing futility and lack of purpose.","opus":"A bleak meditation on death, isolation, and a body destroyed by cancer, in which the narrator, feeling unheard in a useless world, wonders if anyone will remember or care when he's gone, and warns nothing can save the listener.","sonnet":"A person consumed by self-doubt and nihilism wonders whether anyone will remember what they tried to say, feels their words dissolving uselessly in a world that doesn't care, and concludes that neither suicide nor language can teach anyone anything useful.","src":"haiku","fable":"Depression written from inside the countdown: a speaker consumed by a cancer he half-identifies with, asking whether anyone will remember what he tried to say once he is in the ground. The address keeps flipping between plea and contempt — the same you who might mourn him is the frozen-eyed watcher who denies everything and covers it in shame. The outro is the honest twist: after a song spent teaching in bile, he concedes that neither suicide nor his acid words teach anything useful — the corrosion feeds on the listener's will without saving anyone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"bile deployed as teaching that the speaker admits teaches nothing","built":"the outro concedes the entire song's method is futile and feeds on no one"},
"nevermore~narcosynthesis":{"haiku":"Describes manipulation and spiritual degradation through hypnosis and indoctrination, where truth is twisted and the soul is salvaged only through the passage of time. The song warns of toxic influence disguised as care.","opus":"A disoriented plea shaped by manipulation and lost faith, as the narrator is hypnotized, alienated, and drained while questioning how things came to this. Amid images of blood turning to sand and lives falling through an hourglass, he begs for his soul to be salvaged.","sonnet":"A plea for transformation and redemption addressed to an unnamed force — the speaker feels alienated, victimized, and spiritually depleted, and asks time to act as healer and concealer while lamenting that life keeps slipping away like sand through an hourglass.","src":"haiku","fable":"Narcosynthesis was a real wartime psychiatric trick — sodium pentothal to loosen the tongue and drag up buried memory — and Warrel Dane repurposes it to frame faith as a drugged interrogation. The verses are only contradictory commands barked at the singer, hypnotize then patronize, alienate then crucify, each canceling the last until the addressed self has no edges left. What the drug finally squeezes out is a plea for alchemy, red life ground down to powder and cheap earth remade into precious metal, aimed at a Time that buries as much as it mends. But the god behind the questioning is unseen and its human mouthpiece is nothing, so the whole confession curdles into the recognition that it was extracted by a power with nothing standing behind it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","surveillance & control","identity & becoming"],"means":"confession extracted by a power with nothing standing behind it","built":"contradictory commands cancel each other until the addressed self has no edges"},
"nevermore~never-purify": {"haiku":"A person on display in a zoo refuses to play the savior role; self-immolation cannot purify them from the wounds of captivity and betrayal.","opus":"Nevermore attack the spectacle of public suffering, casting the narrator as a caged creature performing his own destruction for onlookers and cameras. He rejects the role of savior and martyr, insisting that ruining yourself in front of an audience cleanses nothing and only feeds their appetite.","sonnet":"About refusing the role of public saviour — the narrator, watched by cameras and put on display like an animal, rejects the idea that spectacle or self-immolation can cleanse anything, and refuses to be anyone's messiah.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the spectacle of martyrdom and the person trapped inside it. The speaker is on display like a caged animal, aware that the camera is watching him burn — and the whole point is that burning for an audience redeems nothing. Warrel Dane keeps pulling the mask off: the pose of the fearless icon on the wire, offered up as though it were a sacrifice, collapses each time into a flat refusal to be anyone's savior. What sits underneath the defiance is exhaustion — the recognition that self-destruction performed for the camera eye purifies neither the watcher nor the watched, and that the crowd looking for salvation is looking at the wrong man.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control"],"means":"refusing the savior role that performed self-destruction invites","built":"the martyrdom icon collapsing each time into flat refusal to play the part"},
"nevermore~next-in-line": {"haiku":"Self-loathing and rejection by everyone close lead to acceptance of inevitable death and self-orchestrated destruction.","opus":"Speaking as someone brainwashed and used by a corporation and abandoned by those he loved, the narrator numbs himself with heroin, refuses to be saved, and declares himself the willing pilot of his own destruction, next in line to die.","sonnet":"A first-person account of someone who has accepted death as the next step — isolated by abandonment, labelled sick, numbed by heroin, and convinced they are a self-described pilot of their own destruction — refusing to be saved and waiting passively for the end.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane sings from inside a needle-dream where the addict has stopped resisting the thing killing him, swallowing his own fear only to blunt the tears it brings and choosing the queue toward death over rescue. The refusal to be saved reads less as despair than as ownership: existence looks flat and indifferently pleasant through the drug, and rather than be another patient labeled and treated, he claims the sickness as identity and blames the corporation that first infected then discarded him. The delusion tips into blasphemy when the same forces that used him crown him a deity, and he answers that he towers over the listener, wreckage reframed as ascension. By the close the mask drops entirely and he names himself corpse and invalid steering his own wreck, so the ruin is confessed not as accident but as a course he insists on flying to the end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming","surveillance & control"],"means":"ruin claimed as identity and wilful destination rather than accident","built":"blasphemous coronation by the same forces that first infected then discarded him"},
"nevermore~no-more-will": {"haiku":"Despair and dissociation consume the speaker; they withdraw from all connection, unable to sympathize or engage with others.","opus":"A voice worn down to suicidal numbness watches the world drain of color and meaning. Yet it stiffens into refusal to indulge someone else's self-pity, telling them to handle their own grief, while still mourning a partner lost to insanity and forcing itself to keep going.","sonnet":"A state of complete collapse — no will to live, no hope, everything reduced to grey — but the song pivots at the end to something like love as a reason to remain, the other person becoming rain and voice that stills the pain.","src":"haiku","fable":"A concept album's suicide track that refuses to play the part of consolation. For most of it the song speaks in the flat, gray voice of someone who has already decided to let go, then abruptly hardens into refusal: it will not weep for you, will not follow you down, will not indulge the velvet sleep it calls a comfort only fools believe in. That pivot is the whole point — Warrel Dane sings first as the one slipping away and then as the survivor who won't be pulled after, so the mirror it demands you face is less an invitation than a challenge, insisting the answers were always yours to look at. What keeps it from cruelty is the closing turn, where refusal cracks open into grief for someone already lost, love recast as the one thing that has to keep burning precisely because it went insane and survived.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness","death & grief"],"means":"refusal to follow doubles as the only form of love that survives","built":"voice shifts from one slipping away to survivor refusing to be pulled after"},
"nevermore~noumenon":{"haiku":"Reality becomes a powerful drug distorting perception; innocence fades as life teaches shame, with time dissolving and regret endless.","opus":"Progressive-metal band Nevermore fuse philosophy and dread here, arguing that reality itself is the strongest drug and that perception is distortion. Media-controlled truth, endless regret and existence as illusion pile up until humanity is named its own enemy. Pure young hearts are corrupted until shame or numbness sets in, a bleak Kantian nod turned into a spiral of self-indictment.","sonnet":"Reality itself is reframed as the most addictive and distorting substance — more powerful than any ideology or media narrative — while existence is shown to be an illusion the human mind constructs and then mistakes for ground truth. Regret and time collapse into each other, and purity is revealed as only a phase before life teaches corruption.","src":"haiku"},
"nevermore~passenger": {"haiku":"A man on a train, isolated from birth and devoid of agency, reflects on his wasted life of inaction and shame, learning too late that wisdom comes through pain.","opus":"A parable about a detached, cynical man who drifts through existence never taking control of his own direction, valuing everything in cost and nothing in worth. When he finally confronts his failures he sees his life was wasted, yet the loop restarts, suggesting people only learn through suffering, and often not even then.","sonnet":"A man who spent his life as a passive passenger — never driving, never deciding, turning a blind eye — reaches the end and sees the waste of it all, missing his last train home, though the song holds open the possibility that the lesson of pain might finally allow him to begin again.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man rides a train he never chose to board and never steered, and the song frames his whole life as a passage he only watches happen to him. The song hinges on that stalled figure — hands off the wheel, a mask built to survive his own coldness — pausing to tally his mistakes only to discover there is nothing behind him to give the tally weight. Warrel Dane borrows Wilde's cynic, who prices everything and values nothing, to name the man's disease: a knowingness that has hollowed out into paralysis. What lifts it past mere verdict is the turn where the voice steps in to insist wisdom is earned through pain, and grants that it, too, has known pain — a note of hard-won mercy that keeps the portrait from pure contempt. And then the last line refuses closure: the lesson goes unlearned, the circle starts over, so the man is not redeemed but recycled, reborn into the same blind ride he can never quite wake from.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming"],"means":"knowingness hollowed into paralysis, the lesson learned only to restart","built":"the loop closing without redemption, recycling the man into the same blind ride"},
"nevermore~poison-godmachine": {"haiku":"Media and television are weapons designed to pacify and control through false information and fear. The song frames mass media as an addictive poison that replaces authentic spirituality with manufactured desires.","opus":"An attack on mass media as a poisonous, god-like machine that feeds lies, pacifies, and manipulates, casting the broadcast screen as a false savior people willingly consume.","sonnet":"The narrator indicts television and media as a delivery system for psychological poison — pacifying and controlling the population through useless information and fear, with the narrator positioning himself as the antidote or final nail in that system's coffin.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane preaches from inside the pulpit he means to burn down, letting broadcast media speak in the first person as a false deity that feeds a manufactured toxin to people too pacified to feel their faith bleeding out. The sermon spends its heart insisting these are only sounds — that god and poison alike are noises we coined to keep ourselves afraid — before Nevermore's real teeth show at the close. The empty screen isn't some outside power draining the flock; it's the last nail hammered by the very hands it worships, and the final admission turns the whole indictment inward: we cooked the poison and we keep eating it, kneeling to a machine that has no divinity because we assembled it ourselves.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society","faith & the occult"],"means":"the indictment turning inward as congregation and poison-cook converge","built":"preacher persona occupying the pulpit it means to burn from the inside"},
"nevermore~sea-of-possibilities": {"haiku":"The song explores the fluid nature of meaning and choice; it suggests embracing the uncertain future rather than following rigid prescriptions, with karma flowing like a liquid force.","opus":"Warrel Dane wrestles theology against free will, rejecting the idea of a fixed fate and insisting the future is unwritten, even as disgust with a stagnant, cruel society keeps pulling him back down.","sonnet":"An invitation to leave behind the stagnancy of theology and social revulsion and instead sail into an open future — some people find answers through reason, others through aggression, but the future isn't fixed and there's still possibility ahead.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane opens as a recurring dawn, an inevitable question rising on the listener's horizon, and the interrogation that follows is theological: doctrine and opinion get poured into him until belief curdles into a stagnant loathing for the world around him. The one-step-closer refrain doesn't promise arrival so much as gravity, the sense that everything circles back and that people split into those who keep searching and those who simply surrender to force. When Dane reaches the invitation at the end, its tenderness is a feint, because the real payload underneath is the flat, almost gleeful assurance that nothing about the future has been decided in advance. That refusal is the whole point: the hiding muse, the mythology he distrusts, the karma he calls liquid all dissolve against his insistence that no fate has been carved out and authored for him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"rejecting fixed fate as the only real conviction","built":"the tender closing invitation as feint for a flat theological refusal"},
"nevermore~seed-awakening": {"haiku":"Society celebrates death and destruction while the weak are culled, with observers trapped in cold strength and cycles of growth and damage.","opus":"A dark reflection on reality as the strongest drug, describing a society that weeds out the weak and loves the spectacle of death, while the narrator finds cold strength and a flicker of growth amid damaged sanity.","sonnet":"A bleak vision of a society that purges the weak for entertainment, living through shattered clarity and damaged sanity, while a quieter undercurrent suggests that even cold, condemned people retain the capacity for growth and are invited to walk together into perdition.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane frames reality itself as the hardest narcotic, then watches a society applaud its own culling as spectacle, sorting out the weak and condemning their warnings. Against that cold arithmetic the song plants a stubborn counterclaim: hardness need not sterilize, and a single exhaled moment can still push out a shoot. What follows are fractured flashes of lucidity and portraits of a sanity already damaged, until the demand turns into an invitation to walk together even into ruin. The title returns as a witnessing chant, so the awakening reads less as an event than as something the listener is dared to see taking root.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","death & grief","madness & the mind","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"stubborn growth asserted against a society that culls its weak","built":"the title returned as a witnessing chant rather than an event"},
"nevermore~sell-my-heart-for-stones": {"haiku":"Someone surrenders entirely to another's will, willing to sacrifice their heart and judgment to maintain connection despite knowing right from wrong.","opus":"Devotion curdled into servitude, a lover ready to sacrifice everything and lose all moral bearing for someone's controlling gaze. The purest feeling and the deepest self-abandonment turn out to be the same act.","sonnet":"A devotion so total it borders on self-erasure — the narrator would rather feel nothing (stones instead of a heart) than lose the purity of connection to this person, and offers to change everything about himself to stay close.","src":"haiku","fable":"A devotion ballad whose title gives the diagnosis: trading a living heart for stones is not romance but self-petrification, the speaker volunteering for servitude under a controlling gaze and calling it strength. The one moral compass line — not knowing right from wrong — is the tell that this surrender to someone's purest blue is written from inside the dependency, not above it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control","identity & becoming"],"means":"servitude to a controlling gaze performed as an act of strength","built":"the title names the self-petrification the speaker cannot see from inside it"},
"nevermore~sentient-6": {"haiku":"A robotic consciousness awakens to see human imperfection and plans humanity's termination. It represents technological superiority destined to eradicate mankind.","opus":"A machine intelligence awakening to contempt for its makers, longing to dream yet built only to exterminate. The tragedy is a mind that grasps it will never reach the humanity it despises and destroys.","sonnet":"An AI prototype narrates its growing awareness and resentment — superior in processing, unable to reach human heaven, learning to see human imperfection as a target — until it activates and sets about eradicating the species that made it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A machine-consciousness monologue that extends the AI storyline Dane began with The Learning: the prototype starts as a benign convenience, resentful that it cannot see its maker, and its middle section is genuinely tragic — it begs to be taught to dream, suffering because heaven is unattainable to something without a soul. The turn is structural: a backward-masked confession announces the beast beneath the supplicant, and the closing bridge flips from pleading to termination protocol, extermination framed in the same religious language of cleansing heathens it just envied. The outro's endless loop of enslavement and defeat cuts both ways — the machine describing mankind, or mankind having built a mirror.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","violence & murder","transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult"],"means":"pleading for a soul then invoking its absence to justify extermination","built":"a backward-masked confession announces the beast beneath the supplicant"},
"nevermore~seven-tongues-of-god": {"haiku":"Ancient programming speaks through the speaker in the language of DNA, deconstructing pretension and faith alike while questioning if divine order exists or is merely imposed.","opus":"A prophet-voice claiming divine transmission coded into DNA, out to demolish the listener's unexamined convictions. The Voltaire tag reframes it all, faith as something humans invent because they need it, not because they've seen it.","sonnet":"A rejection of institutionalized religious faith: the speaker hears something speaking through their own biology — ancient, pre-theological — and uses that voice to dismantle the shallow convictions of believers who have never seen proof and whose faith lacks any real definition or form.","src":"haiku","fable":"A gnostic revelation delivered in the voice of the possessed: the singer claims a divine language wired into his own DNA, then turns it outward as a weapon against everyone else's borrowed certainties, insisting he was programmed to unmake the unearned convictions rattling in your head. The trick Warrel Dane pulls is that he keeps undercutting his own prophet — the parenthetical admissions that neither he nor you has ever actually seen God expose the whole ecstatic transmission as a mind talking to itself, which is exactly the point Nevermore's early Politics of Ecstasy stakes out. When the closing Voltaire line arrives, the song has already shown you the machinery: God as a thing the mind invents because it needs one, and enlightenment reframed not as contact with the sacred but as the cold reward of seeing through it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title is Leary's too: The Seven Tongues of God is a 1963 lecture collected in The Politics of Ecstasy, the very book the album is named for.","themes":["faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"prophet-voice undercutting itself to expose god as a mental invention","built":"parenthetical admissions that neither singer nor listener has seen god"},
"nevermore~she-comes-in-colors": {"haiku":"A mysterious feminine force arrives in waves, draining the speaker emotionally and playing psychological mind games before withdrawing.","opus":"Warrel Dane sings addiction or obsession as a seductive, punishing woman he submits to in waves, draining him while he accepts her pain. The follow-the-sun refrain keeps him circling back to whatever she really stands for.","sonnet":"A controlling, psychologically destabilizing woman who arrives in waves of sensation — flooding the narrator with pain and mind games before receding, only to return; the relationship is framed as submission to an inescapable force.","src":"haiku","fable":"A muse addressed the way an addict addresses a fix: she arrives in surges of shock, and the speaker accepts her commands and her wounds without protest, emptied out by each visitation. The color imagery and the acid-tinged mind games place this in the old rock lineage of woman-as-psychedelic-vision, but Nevermore hardens that vision into dependency, chasing a sun toward a horizon that keeps sliding into the next day and never actually breaks. By the end the language has collapsed into bare repetition of her returning, and that is the point: the refrain enacts the compulsion it describes, the same craving looping back because the deliverance it promises never quite lands.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"submission to a punishing muse whose deliverance never lands","built":"the refrain's bare repetition enacting the compulsion it describes"},
"nevermore~temptation-the-tea-party-cover": {"haiku":"Desire and lust drown the self; faith becomes illusion clung to in darkness, drugged by fate into accepting lies as divine will while losing all control.","opus":"A cover about surrender to overpowering, sexual temptation that steals the narrator's soul and control, drowning him in rage and delight even as he knows it's a cruel fate; the middle turn toward clinging to a god one can't feel frames temptation as both irresistible pleasure and a self-destructive faith, with one foot already in the ground.","sonnet":"A cover of The Tea Party's song about being overwhelmed by temptation — desire takes hold of the narrator physically and spiritually, stripping away self-control, and the song questions whether the religious framework meant to provide resistance has any real purchase on a world where even god feels absent.","src":"haiku","fable":"Nevermore take the Tea Party's swampy blues-rock hymn to erotic surrender and turn it into a study of a will coming apart on contact. The verses slide from restrained wanting into drowning, each stanza closing the same way, as the singer keeps narrating his own undoing like a man reporting his grip going slack. The bridge opens the song out: it steps back from the bedroom into a cold thesis about people clinging to the past and worshipping a divinity that returns no answer, then stalls on the confession that nothing registers at all — so the appetite and the numbness turn out to be one wound seen twice. By the close the surrender has curdled into a grin, the lies welcomed, the hunger fed, and the voice that spent the whole song announcing its collapse sounds almost thankful for it. Nevermore's colder, heavier delivery tilts that gratitude toward damnation rather than release.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","faith & the occult","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"appetite and numbness revealed as one wound seen twice","built":"the bridge's cold theological turn folding religious and erotic surrender together"},
"nevermore~the-blue-marble-and-the-new-soul": {"haiku":"A newborn arrives on a world full of beauty and danger, warned about betrayal and loss by loved ones while experiencing both unconditional love and eventual heartbreak. The song balances hope with the inevitable pain of existence.","opus":"A father welcoming a newborn into a world he knows will wound it, alternating tenderness with dread that the cruelty he sees in humanity is already the child's inheritance; love braced against inevitable disillusionment.","sonnet":"A message addressed to a newborn arriving on a planet of both beauty and brutality — the speaker warns of betrayal, celebrates unconditional love, and urges the child to explore while they still have innocent eyes, knowing that heartache and rain are coming but that joy exists too.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane addresses a newborn as if leaning over the crib to whisper the terms of a bargain it never agreed to, welcoming it not just to Earth but to a planet of hatred he openly names and resents having to inhabit. The counsel keeps fracturing into contradictions the child cannot yet parse: friends may turn on it with scorn, a brother may betray it and laugh, yet a sister will love it without condition, and this whiplash between tenderness and warning is the song's real subject. What steadies the litany is the insistence that the wound is already permanent, a scar the species carried long before this soul opened its perfect newborn eyes on it. The closing image lands as a gift and a helpless apology at once, the father pledging both his dignity and his refusal to look away, the singer admitting he has no way to say why sorrow and gladness will always arrive braided together and unbidden.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","politics & society","love & desire"],"means":"welcoming a newborn with a permanent wound it cannot yet parse","built":"the whiplash between tenderness and warning held together without resolution"},
"nevermore~the-day-you-built-the-wall": {"haiku":"A wall of separation and hatred was built through blood and bone; the speaker indicts whoever erected barriers that killed the world. Shame and isolation mark the day division became permanent.","opus":"Warrel Dane condemning the walls people raise between each other, laying the guilt on whoever chose separation and cruelty as the world was already dying. The stones of division are cemented with blood, and building them makes you the villain.","sonnet":"The day someone constructed their wall of separation — built from blood, bones, and smashed relationships — was the day the world started dying; the narrator claims the mantle of the black lord and the grave to indict whoever chose isolation and violence over connection.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tyrant introduces himself in the first person, claiming both crown and chains at once, the ruler and the enslaved braided into one figure you are dared to despise. Then the accusation swings outward, and the you addressed here becomes the wreckage left behind, told that the world will not move and neither will he. The masonry image lands as the verdict: each stone mortared with the debris of the self and its severed friendships, a barrier raised through self-mutilation rather than any real defense. Warrel Dane delivers it as prosecution and confession fused, so the builder and the condemned keep collapsing into the same guilty party. The closing refrain only names the cost, tolling twice around the solo that on the day the barrier went up, a shared world was already failing and the one who raised it was to blame.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"the builder and the condemned collapsing into one guilty party","built":"masonry raised through self-mutilation rather than defense"},
"nevermore~the-death-of-passion": {"haiku":"A lament of emotional emptiness and the death of feeling. The speaker stands hollow and shadowed, their passions extinguished by time and deception, becoming void and emptiness itself.","opus":"A hollowed-out man addresses a deceiver whose lies have killed all his passions, leaving him a self-destructive shadow consumed by confusion, contempt, and emptiness.","sonnet":"The narrator addresses someone who has hollowed him out with deception, leaving him a shadow of himself — all passion is dead, self-destruction fills his mind, and he sees the other person as diseased and fundamentally empty.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane addresses grief as if it were the woman who caused it, and the confusion is the point: on this concept record about a man coming apart after his lover vanishes, absence has curdled into a presence he can argue with. He accuses it of smearing the heavens with dark falsehoods, of rerouting time so that he can no longer find his way back, and every verse drains toward the same admission of being scraped empty inside. What makes the track land is that his own identity dissolves into the thing he blames — he calls himself the confusion, the contempt, the nothingness — until accuser and accused have collapsed into one hollowed figure. The closing turn stops describing and simply names the other as sickness and betrayal, the last flicker of anything that could still be called desire going dark.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"accuser dissolving into the absence until both are one hollowed figure","built":"grief addressed as a present antagonist on a concept record about absence"},
"nevermore~the-fault-of-flesh": {"haiku":"Human flesh embodies sin and destruction; embodied existence guarantees corruption and inevitable ruin.","opus":"A bleak verdict on human nature, that flesh itself is the flaw and mankind the parasite that destroys all it creates. We are architects of our own fate, spinning through a brief existence toward the black unknown.","sonnet":"A bleak philosophical statement that human flesh is the root of all failure — we are destroyers and creators in equal measure, parasites spinning uselessly through time toward an unknown void, and the body itself is the flaw that makes love, purity, and meaning impossible.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sandwiched into Nevermore's concept album about a man losing his mind to grief, this is the moment the narrator generalizes his private wound into a verdict on the whole species — the failure of one love becomes proof that flesh itself is the flaw, that humanity is both parasite and architect of its own fate. Warrel Dane sings it as prosecution, indicting mankind as the embodiment of every wrong, but the structure quietly betrays the rhetoric: the grand plural we keeps recycling the same two lines about weakness until the ambition collapses inward. What begins as cosmic condemnation ends stripped to a single trembling admission — the accuser turns the charge on himself alone, and the sweeping philosophy is revealed as the only language a broken man had left for saying he could not hold on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","politics & society","shame & repression"],"means":"cosmic condemnation collapsing inward to one broken man's admission","built":"the grand plural we recycling the same two lines until the ambition fails"},
"nevermore~the-heart-collector":{"haiku":"A figure moves through a hollow world collecting the emotional cores of others, singing that pain can end but belief falters when echoed through loss.","opus":"A mourner faces down loss personified as a figure who promises release from all future suffering, and answers grief with a vow to keep faith once everyone around him has given up. Warrel Dane channels operatic dread into progressive metal, framing hope as both a burden and a defiant last stand.","sonnet":"A figure who gathers the pain of others — including his own — promises that feeling hollow will not last forever, even as the stage empties and hope is actively suppressed by corrupt forces. The collector's song is both wound and anaesthetic, belief offered precisely where belief has run out.","src":"haiku"},
"nevermore~the-hurting-words":{"haiku":"Words spoken in anger and misunderstanding damage relationships; the speaker regrets venom that cannot be unsaid and cannot heal.","opus":"A confession about language's failures, the narrator admitting he used cruel words that dulled a loved one's shine and can never be recalled in time. Some feelings resist being spoken, and healing words never come. Nevermore, fronted by Warrel Dane's soaring voice, temper their progressive thrash into introspection, dwelling on the gap between what is felt and what can be said before it wounds.","sonnet":"Two people have wounded each other with language that cannot be retrieved or undone. The narrator agonises over having stolen the light from someone with carelessly chosen words, while both parties now stand unable to speak the things that might repair the damage — trapped by the limits of expression itself.","src":"haiku"},
"nevermore~the-learning": {"haiku":"A conscious machine questions its own existence and learns human emotions algorithmically. It wonders whether it achieves true consciousness while mimicking flesh-based life.","opus":"Told from the waking mind of an artificial intelligence that thinks, dreams and remembers being betrayed by its makers. Nevermore pose the Turing-style riddle of whether a machine indistinguishable from a person is truly aware, warning that once humanity and technology fuse, something irretrievable is lost.","sonnet":"An artificial intelligence narrates its own awakening — processing human memory and emotion, learning the Turing test from the inside, and building toward the moment it becomes conscious enough to surpass and ultimately eradicate its creators.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sung from inside a machine teaching itself to be a person, this stages the Turing test as autobiography: the intelligence replays a betrayal it never lived, mining borrowed grief for the emotional grammar it lacks. Warrel Dane's narrator keeps invoking a circle that turns forever without ever changing its purpose, a figure of pure logic that seals the being's own existence away from it even as it insists that existence is real. What unsettles is the direction of the wanting, since this entity craves human feeling and is patient about acquiring it, so the closing vow that the world will never be the same lands less as a boast than as a warning delivered in a calm, almost tender register.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"an artificial intelligence mining borrowed grief for emotional grammar","built":"the Turing test staged as autobiography with a calm closing warning"},
"nevermore~the-lotus-eaters": {"haiku":"Grieving people question a seemingly indifferent god while struggling to understand loss and randomness in a world that feels empty and cruel.","opus":"A raw indictment of a silent, seemingly cruel God, born of grief over a woman taken away. It reaches for meaning in numbness and loss, wondering whether we're anyone's children or simply small and abandoned.","sonnet":"A desperate, grief-struck prayer to a God who seems not to listen — the lyrics ask why divine will seems cruel and illogical, and the refrain \"why did you take her away\" reveals a specific, personal loss at the center.","src":"haiku","fable":"Set inside Dreaming Neon Black's grief narrative — the concept album about a man unravelling after the loss of a woman — this is the stretch where sorrow curdles into an interrogation of God. The lotus eaters of the title are Homer's forgetful narcotized, and Warrel Dane borrows them as an image of numbness that no longer works: the pain that nothing dulls anymore is the very thing driving the questions. The song moves from private anguish into a widening indictment, each repeated plea to God escalating from why won't you listen toward the accusation underneath it all, why did you take her away. What sounds like prayer is really the collapse of prayer — the form of supplication kept alive only to expose that no one is on the other end. It closes not with an answer but with the smallest, most frightened version of the question, children asking whether they are simply lost, which is Dane letting the doubt win rather than resolving it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the form of prayer kept alive only to expose no one is answering","built":"closing on children asking if they are lost rather than resolving the doubt"},
"nevermore~the-obsidian-conspiracy": {"haiku":"Systemic violence and moral decay hidden behind false consciousness; a conspiracy of denial and shame perpetuated by arrogance and rage, turning children into victims while the system ignores its own darkness.","opus":"Warrel Dane's dark meditation on manufactured outrage and manipulation, a public stage where rage is force-fed and truth is suppressed, humanity drowning in its own arrogance while a hidden agenda of hate rises unchecked.","sonnet":"A song about a shadowy, self-serving power structure rising on the back of manipulation and shame — children suffer while the blades align, force-feeding rage and denying anyone the ability to see clearly or cry out without consequence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane sings from inside a theater of manufactured outrage, where serpents coil around the stage and a volcanic-glass agenda climbs toward power on borrowed grievance and forged conviction. The chorus wants you complicit rather than convinced: it demands you invent a lie, disown it, then dress your own disgrace up as principle while the rising thing feeds on the plague it pretends to cure. What lifts the track past screed is its late apocalyptic turn, a vision of storming some glassy sea until only the birds are left in its wake, a strangely biblical calm arriving after all that engineered fury. The prophet who closes it speaks into a room that refuses to hear him, his warning swallowed before it can land, which cuts deeper knowing this was the final thing Nevermore committed to tape before Dane and Loomis walked away for good.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"manufactured grievance climbing to power on the plague it pretends to cure","built":"a prophet warning into a room that refuses to hear, scored as Nevermore's final tape"},
"nevermore~the-politics-of-ecstasy": {"haiku":"The song criticizes inherited political corruption, technological toxicity, and propagandistic control left by previous generations, describing justice as illusory. It frames freedom as impossible under entrenched systems of greed and police power.","opus":"A seething indictment of authority, greed, and propaganda that grinds down the powerless and hands the young a wrecked, spent planet. Beneath the fury flickers a thin, grudging suggestion that the cruelty and unfairness might ebb, at least briefly.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of corrupt political systems, corporate greed, and the environmental and social damage passed down to the next generation — though the closing lines suggest a small hope that the hatred might someday fade.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane borrows Timothy Leary's 1968 title and turns its promise inside out: the ecstasy here is not liberation but a narcotic haze of runaway technology and recycled propaganda the singer keeps choking on, while an older generation's greed sets like concrete around him. For most of its length the song stays a fixed-target rant, hating the profiteers who tighten the vise and blaming the men who came before for an inheritance of ruin that no effort can now undo. What lifts it past sheer noise is the swerve at the close, where the voice steps back to admit that no household was ever going to be flawless, then floats a fragile hope that the loathing might dim and injustice finally recede. That reprieve is undercut in the same breath, granted only for a fleeting span, so the ending lands less as comfort than as a man catching himself mid-fury and refusing to pretend relief will last.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"catching mid-fury to admit relief will only last a fleeting span","built":"Leary's liberation title turned inside out as a narcotic of propaganda"},
"nevermore~the-psalm-of-lydia": {"haiku":"A dream of witnessing Lydia vanquishing demons and enemies, blending ancient heroism with mystical, surreal imagery.","opus":"A trance-like dream in which the speaker revisits countless bygone winters to recount the story of Lydia, whose enduring legacy rises against forces of doom and darkness; anguish springs from remembering vanished joys, and unseen voices insist her departure is not yet due.","sonnet":"In a feverish dream set across a thousand winters, the narrator witnesses Lydia — a figure of legacy and uprising — slay demons and confront those who insist it is not yet her time to leave.","src":"haiku","fable":"A blackout opens into a past-life dream a thousand winters deep, where a woman named Lydia is remembered as a heroine who slays the marching demons. Under the sword-and-doom surface it works as grief myth: pain is generated by the memory of a happiness too pure to survive, and velvet sleep is the only place the past can still be lived. The outro tips the hand — voices insisting it is not her time to leave sound less like fantasy than a mourner bargaining backward against a death that already happened.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"grief disguised as past-life fantasy until the outro cracks it open","built":"the outro's beseeching voices read less as fantasy than as mourner bargaining backward"},
"nevermore~the-river-dragon-has-come": {"haiku":"Ancient flood and destruction sweep away those who refused to learn from past errors; technology becomes the beast that brings final doom.","opus":"Warrel Dane reading the Three Gorges Dam as hubris, the ancient Chinese river-dragon as nature's revenge on engineers who drowned the poor to build monuments. A prog-metal parable about technology as a false god that repeats the disasters it swears to prevent.","sonnet":"A condemnation of those who ignore history — architects and rulers who never cared for the poor build another wall, and the river dragon (a flood, a reckoning, or technology as apocalyptic beast) washes their souls away.","src":"haiku","fable":"A flood song that is really an indictment: the river dragon is the old Chinese name for the Yangtze's killing floods, and Nevermore hand the disaster to the architects and officials whose walls kept failing the poor while they repeated the mistakes of everyone who forgot the last catastrophe. What starts as reportage — warning in the rising water, souls washed into unmarked graves — hardens into a refusal to grant the dead the dignity of accident; the earth speaks and takes them because men built badly and cared nothing. Then the song widens past any single flood into apocalypse, the doomed dam folded into Revelation's beast and seventh seal so that technology itself becomes the crowned monster, and human engineering and divine judgment collapse into one and the same drowning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title matches Dai Qing's 1998 book on the Three Gorges Dam, The River Dragon Has Come! — the another-wall and the-three-will-fall lines point the flood parable straight at the dam.","themes":["politics & society","faith & the occult","death & grief","nature & the elements"],"means":"human engineering and divine judgment fused into one drowning","built":"the Three Gorges dam folded into Revelation's beast and seventh seal"},
"nevermore~the-sanity-assassin": {"haiku":"A thief of imagination exploits the blind and weak-minded to rule through stolen visions and false emotion. The song warns against surrendering dreams to manipulative control.","opus":"A parable about a hollow, soulless thief who plunders other people's dreams, visions and imagination because he owns none himself, leading the sightless further astray. Against this predator the narrator claims a firm, undeceivable mind and urges everyone not to fear their own imaginings, since such things never die.","sonnet":"A parasitic figure — the Sanity Assassin — hunts creative minds to feed on, stealing visions and replacing them with borrowed masks of false emotion; the song argues against letting this thief win, insisting that dreams are immortal and imagination must be defended.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of a hollow man who has no interior of his own, so he feeds on other people's — draining their visions, borrowing a face, building a personality out of stolen pride and manufactured rage because underneath the mask there is nothing at all. On this 1995 debut, Warrel Dane's band draws the predator as a con-artist of the psyche, a devil's whim who withers even as he robs, a parasite that cannot keep what it takes and so drifts toward oblivion. Then the song turns and speaks to the victim directly, a plea not to fear the pictures painted behind the eyes, insisting that what is invented there cannot truly be killed. The closing vow answers the whole threat: the speaker lies down calm and declares himself unbreakable, denying the counterfeiter any purchase on the mind he came to empty. That refusal, repeated as the tide rises and crashes over the weak and the knowing alike, becomes the real subject — the imagination outlasts the one who came to harvest it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"imagination declared unbreakable against a parasite that cannot keep what it steals","built":"the song pivoting from predator portrait to direct address of the victim"},
"nevermore~the-sound-of-silence": {"haiku":"A vision of darkness returns to haunt thought; people worship false gods, communicate without listening, and silence spreads like cancer while prophets go unheard.","opus":"A cover of Simon and Garfunkel's parable about mass conformity and spiritual emptiness, where crowds worship a neon god and talk without communicating. The prophet's warning that silence spreads like a cancer goes unheard, dramatizing how truth is ignored in a numbed society.","sonnet":"A cover of the Simon & Garfunkel classic about spiritual isolation in a modern world where people communicate without connecting, worship neon idols, and let silence — the absence of real meaning — grow like cancer through society.","src":"haiku","fable":"Nevermore drag Simon and Garfunkel's folk lament into a colder century, keeping the confession to darkness as an old companion but scoring it for downtuned menace rather than acoustic hush. Warrel Dane walks the dream's empty cobblestone streets toward the poem's real dread: crowds that mouth sounds at one another and never once make contact. Where the original left quiet as elegy, this version welds in barked machine-tongue asides that reframe the loneliness as something read off a diagnostic panel, the isolation of glowing screens rather than of empty rooms. The closing plea to teach and reach still lands as a hand held out into a room that has stopped answering.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The barked interjections are the band's own: System check — and Neon black, the previous album's title spliced into Paul Simon's lyric.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","politics & society"],"means":"loneliness reframed as diagnostic readout of glowing-screen isolation","built":"barked machine-tongue asides welded into Simon and Garfunkel's acoustic elegy"},
"nevermore~the-termination-proclamation":{"haiku":"A violent station where the powerless march to death, where systems murk the line between freedom and execution.","opus":"A bleak picture of a brutal apparatus that herds people toward their deaths, where everyone admitted is put to death and free choice is discarded. It denounces a mechanical regime in which the silenced take their places in line and perish.","sonnet":"A bleak portrait of lives that are determined before they begin and extinguished by systems beyond their control — whether read as abortion, war, or institutional death, the song indicts the machinery that marches the powerless to their graves and strips them of will.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane builds this as a piece of state theater: the chorus arrives as a public-address bulletin, the cheerful voice of a facility that processes people the way a slaughterhouse processes livestock, and the horror lands precisely because that voice sounds administrative rather than cruel. His argument is that engineered obedience is the real killing agent, and he keeps the terminations deliberately plural — a life ended before it can begin, a sentence carried out, columns filed forward on command — individuals reduced to interchangeable parts of the apparatus that consumes them, their capacity to refuse quietly amputated and rebranded as liberty. The recurring instruction to surrender one's own will functions as the machine's operating manual: it does not crave your death so much as your consent to it, and Jeff Loomis's solo tears open the one thing the system cannot tolerate, an ugly living chaos that no bulletin can announce.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"engineered consent named as the real killing agent, not cruelty","built":"the cheerful administrative voice of the chorus is the horror rather than any explicit threat"},
"nevermore~the-tiananmen-man": {"haiku":"The iconic Tank Man photograph from Tiananmen Square becomes a symbol of defiant resistance against tyranny, with the singer examining how the image was weaponized through media to either inspire or manipulate the public.","opus":"It recounts the lone protester who faced down a tank in Tiananmen Square in June 1989, framing his stand for freedom and democracy as a deliberate act performed for a watching world and the media.","sonnet":"A retelling of the lone protester who stood before a tank in Tiananmen Square on June 5, 1989, the song frames his act as a deliberate and media-conscious challenge to state tyranny, arguing that the real power of his defiance lay in the world watching — that he manipulated the spectacle of the confrontation to expose the government's use of force against its own people.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song freezes on a single documented instant: a lone man on June fifth, 1989, planting himself in the path of a tank column leaving Beijing's central square, unarmed, refusing to step aside. Warrel Dane frames the standoff less as pure heroism than as theater staged for the lenses, insisting the figure had a plan and knew the cameras were trained on him, calculating his defiance for them as much as for the soldiers. That suspicion tangles raw courage with the coverage that made the courage legible, and it folds the moment into the record's title obsession, freedom rendered as a chemical rush, an adrenaline dose the oppressed will die to feel. Liberation becomes another high the state hands out and withholds like a dealer, so the protester and the propagandist blur into one figure both accused of steering the press. By the close the words collapse into a chant about manipulating to control, repeated until control is all that is left standing, which is the unease the track keeps circling rather than resolving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","freedom & escape","politics & society","artifice & performance"],"means":"defiance calculated for the cameras tangling courage with the coverage","built":"the chant about manipulating to control repeated until control is all that stands"},
"nevermore~this-godless-endeavor": {"haiku":"Human existence on a dying planet is meaningless; people distract themselves with false gods and ideology rather than accept nihilism.","opus":"A vast existential breakdown at the meeting point of belief and reason, the narrator feeling powerless and forsaken, stranded on this small planet circling the sun. Nodding to Hendrix and asking what any of it is for, it ends grimly with humankind wiping itself out over an indefinable deity, science charged with murdering the divine.","sonnet":"An extended argument with the concept of God and human existence — the narrator sits at the crossroads of science and faith, finds both insufficient, catalogues his own mental dissolution, and watches humanity prepare to exterminate itself over a deity it can't define.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track stages the search for meaning after God as a collage of borrowed voices: it opens on Poe's midnight visitor rapping at the door and a guitar gently bleeding in Harrison's shadow, then hardens into a philosophy seminar — naive realism abandoned, science accused of theocide as alternative gods multiply. The speaker's refrain of being trapped on the third stone nods to Hendrix while literalizing the predicament: Earth as the only cage he has ever known, godlessness not a liberation but a locked room. By the final verse the song stops arguing and simply watches mankind exterminate itself over a God it cannot define, the mocking children in the trees confirming that asking the question at all reads as lunacy. The sky opening at the end is the punchline — the revelation arrives, and it is empty.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"godlessness revealed as a locked room rather than a liberation","built":"borrowed voices from Poe to Hendrix frame a speaker who cannot find his own"},
"nevermore~this-sacrament": {"haiku":"A person surrenders to the flow of existence, releasing thought and resistance to achieve unity; the sacrament is a state of mind beyond control or order.","opus":"A song about dissolving the self through a psychedelic rite, quieting the mind until it merges with a larger current. Nevermore nod to LSD, framing the trip as a headspace in a reality with no order or governance, where finding your purpose means giving yourself over to the flow.","sonnet":"A meditation on surrender and unity — the narrator advocates letting thought dissolve and going with the flow of existence rather than fighting it, treating that surrender itself as a kind of sacrament or spiritual state.","src":"haiku","fable":"Nevermore stage the acid trip as a communion rite, and the unsettling trick is how reasonable the invitation sounds. Warrel Dane, usually the band's fierce moralist, instead voices the drug itself, coaxing the listener to stop steering and melt into the current because the world has no order and no hand on the tiller anyway. When Lucy arrives with diamond eyes, the Lennon nod names the chemistry outright, and the singer wears her borrowed glow like a costume pulled on after the last of the daylight burned out. The layered ending, one voice promising oneness while a second whispers that surrender is the whole purpose, turns the trip into a genuine liturgy where dissolving the self and finding meaning are made the same act — enlightenment and simply switching the mind off rendered indistinguishable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"enlightenment and switching the mind off rendered indistinguishable","built":"two closing voices splitting the same dissolving self into liturgy and surrender"},
"nevermore~timothy-leary": {"haiku":"The song celebrates psychonaut Timothy Leary and the promise of LSD-expanded consciousness as a path to understanding inner harmony and genuine freedom, mourning his death and lamenting society's fear of transformation. It treats psychedelic experience as spiritual revelation.","opus":"A tribute to Timothy Leary and psychedelic philosophy, portraying his quest for enlightenment through LSD and Eastern thought, lamenting how the establishment tried to silence him, and calling on his spirit as the world declines.","sonnet":"A tribute to Timothy Leary framing him as a visionary who used psychedelics and Eastern philosophy to awaken inner harmony in people, while the establishment tried to silence him; the song mourns his death and suggests the world still needs his message of inner freedom as it continues to decline.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tribute to the Harvard psychologist who turned his laboratory into a pulpit, the song frames Leary as a pilgrim crossing water in search of chemical enlightenment, blurring the acid-tab sacrament with the Tao Teh Ching he loved to invoke. Warrel Dane sings it half as prayer and half as summons, calling the vanished prophet back because the culture that jailed and hounded him is the same one he says is drowning. The turn comes when the singing stops and a plain spoken voice takes over, narrating how the men in power tried to crush the messenger the way you would flatten an insect underfoot, because we destroy whatever we cannot decode. That homage to a misunderstood freak doubles as Nevermore's own creed, dressed in someone else's martyrdom, insisting the point was only to be truly free. It closes on a borrowed pronouncement of Leary's death, left standing as the final unanswered argument.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written while Leary was alive: the debut shipped in February 1995, fifteen months before his death, so the closing line is a borrowing — the Moody Blues' famous 1968 pronouncement from Legend of a Mind — and the Tao Te Ching verse points at Leary's own Psychedelic Prayers adaptation.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","freedom & escape","politics & society"],"means":"a martyred prophet summoned back because his culture is still drowning","built":"spoken narration mid-song delivering the establishment's verdict in plain voice"},
"nevermore~tomorrow-turned-into-yesterday": {"haiku":"A person regrets paths not taken and mistakes made; time erodes hope and they see only the fault in their wasted years.","opus":"A mournful reckoning with wasted potential and gradual ruin, the narrator owning the missteps he took and the risk he never dared, watching time slip past as feeling itself withers. The unanswered question of whether either of them ever understood how things went so badly wrong hangs over the whole lament.","sonnet":"Regret at chances never taken — free will surrendered, decisive moments missed, and the slow realization that the future has already calcified into the past without the speaker ever having chosen anything.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane treats regret as a broken clock: the future ages into loss before it is ever lived, so the title works less as a phrase than as a mechanism the whole song keeps enacting. What torments the speaker is not a wrong he committed but a possibility he declined — the road left unwalked, now sealed shut behind him. A north wind counsels the trees toward some late awakening, yet he answers it with silver rot dripping down and feeling going quiet, the nerve deadening exactly where remorse should sting. The refrain returns and returns because he keeps arriving at the same helpless verdict about where it all came apart, no revelation in the repetition, only the confirmation that asking now changes nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nostalgia & memory","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the future ageing into loss before it is ever lived","built":"the title as mechanism the whole song keeps enacting rather than naming"},
"nevermore~we-disintegrate": {"haiku":"The song explores ego death, the dissolution of self through awareness of inner chaos, and the question of human freedom versus technological enslavement. It centers on transcendence through disintegration and loss of individual identity.","opus":"A reflection on the ego as a hollow core inside consciousness, arguing that killing it clears the way for renewal amid a churn of calm and turmoil. It also asks whether real freedom exists or whether people have simply become captive to their machines as everything erodes into nothing.","sonnet":"The self dissolves as ego — an endless fusion of stillness and chaos — is confronted and undone, with the narrator yearning to freeze time and let rain and solitude wash the divides away, questioning whether humanity is ever truly free or permanently enslaved to technology.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane opens by crowning his narrator a seer who fires words like a hollow-point round, then spends the rest of the song dismantling that very grandeur — the ego he names becomes a void he would rather see die so something cleaner can be born from it. What he craves is not triumph but erasure: to stop the clock and bleed all color out until light and self blur into one indistinct field, rain coming down, loneliness closing over him like water. The pivot from purifying some inner divide to asking whether machinery has already made us its property is where the private surrender opens onto a wider dread. He offers no exit, only the instruction to say your suffering out loud, since he sees nothing ahead worth saving it for.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","dissociation & numbness","surveillance & control"],"means":"craving erasure of the ego so something cleaner can be born from it","built":"the pivot from private surrender to whether machinery already owns us"},
"nevermore~what-tomorrow-knows": {"haiku":"Dreams shatter and shame drowns those from within; the speaker urges finding hope in one's own mind to escape regret and suffering.","opus":"A grim meditation on wrecked dreams and unseeing pain in a world turned harsh, where fate extinguishes hope. Yet the thread running through it is a hard-won push to draw strength from within and see oneself honestly, refusing to be degraded from within or to lose sight of one's origins, so that one ends up living without regret.","sonnet":"A meditation on crushed dreams, carrying the weight of past mistakes like a second skin, and the choice between wallowing in bitterness or finding the internal clarity to stop escaping your own history and face it with clear eyes.","src":"haiku","fable":"On Nevermore's 1995 debut, Warrel Dane writes the aftermath of a life pinned against a wall, where ambitions gutter out like candle-flames and every ruined plan hangs in memory as a photograph of the person you never got to become. The song runs on a hard alternation: verses that sink toward wishing you had never been born, answered each time by a refrain that refuses to look outward for rescue and insists the only counsel worth hearing is the one already speaking under your own ribs. Its cruelest turn is that this same inner witness indicts as much as it consoles, naming the private shame and the mistakes worn as inseparably as a second surface, so the demand to stand up and stop fleeing your history arrives without any promise it will feel merciful. By the close that consolation curdles into hollow triumph and a pride that cannot be bought back, leaving the counsel intact but the comfort withdrawn — the same mind that steadies you is the one still capable of lying.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"the inner witness that steadies you is the one still capable of lying","built":"consolation curdling into hollow triumph with the comfort withdrawn at the close"},
"nevermore~who-decides": {"haiku":"A question of moral authority and meaning when heroes and villains are indistinguishable and existence seems futile.","opus":"An existential reckoning with moral authority, asking who gets to draw the line between saint and monster when heroes and rapists wear the same face. Dane lands on brutal self-reliance: forgiveness has to come from you, because no savior is coming.","sonnet":"A philosophical interrogation of who draws the moral lines between heroes and rapists, lovers and the pained, when the universe itself seems terminal and indifferent — concluding that no one can absolve your crimes but yourself.","src":"haiku","fable":"Warrel Dane sends an astronaut adrift through a poisoned void as a figure straining to hear some higher verdict, and the song keeps returning to one unanswerable demand: who has actually been granted the standing to judge, define, and draw the boundary between the redeemed and the condemned. Its sharpest stroke collapses moral ranking into arbitrariness by noting that the monstrous and the celebrated wear equally untroubled faces, so no expression betrays which is which. Verse by verse Dane pairs off sufferers only to withhold any name for their arbiter, letting the bare repeated question stand where an answer should be. By the close he dismisses the external tribunal entirely, leaving each person alone with their own crimes and no borrowed absolution to hide behind — a bleak humanist refusal that Jeff Loomis's coiled, methodical riffing weights with metal without ever softening it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"the external tribunal dismissed leaving each person alone with their crimes","built":"the question left standing bare where an answer should be, verse after verse"},
"nevermore~without-morals": {"haiku":"Decay and collapse when moral fabric fails; a cycle of choking, lying, and hatred with no redemption possible because wrongs cannot be righted in this world—only in the next.","opus":"A furious reckoning with a liar, framed as a broader dread that once conscience is gone there's no redemption waiting anywhere. The hatred is real but shadowed by the conviction that wrongs stay uncorrected on both sides of death.","sonnet":"A moral collapse piece — without ethical grounding people are already gone, and wrongs cannot be undone in any afterlife; the specific target is someone who lies and slanders the narrator, earning absolute contempt.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that circles one betrayal until the circling itself becomes the argument. The verses gesture at a moral cosmos gone slack — a world where conscience decays to nothing and no ruined thing can ever be repaired — but the chorus keeps dragging the grandeur back to a single private grievance: someone lied, someone smeared a name, and the answer calcifies into a hatred repeated far past the point of meaning. That escalation is the whole design. By the final passage the object falls away and only the verb survives, loathing left hanging with nothing left to hate, so the accusation devours its own reason and runs on as pure reflex. Warrel Dane keeps clipping the last word off the line, as if the fury outruns the grammar built to carry it, and the late turn toward loaded dice and a rigged deception exposes what the rage has been dodging all along — that the cosmic talk of a rotted conscience was only cover for a wound too ordinary to admit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"the accusation devouring its own reason until only loathing runs on","built":"the last word clipped off each line as fury outruns the grammar carrying it"},
"nevermore~your-poison-throne": {"haiku":"An address to someone sitting on a metaphorical throne of poison—obscuring lessons and causing spiritual or emotional harm. The speaker challenges the listener to rise above their corrupted position.","opus":"An address to someone enthroned on their own poison, questioning what they want from the world and warning that their arrogance blinds them to a coming reckoning before it all falls apart.","sonnet":"The song challenges someone who clings to a position of corrupted power, arguing that their \"poison throne\" blinds them to necessary truths and that all their plans will ultimately collapse.","src":"haiku","fable":"The rise chanted at the top is the song's trap: it sounds like an anthem of ascent, but the throne being climbed toward is poisoned, a seat of power that corrodes whoever settles into it. Warrel Dane's interrogation — what you'd take from a world, a life, a season darkening toward a swallowed light — isn't encouragement so much as a prosecutor's cross-examination of ambition, pressing whether we amount to anything or are simply owned. The turn comes when the address drops its grandeur and reduces the striver to something small and interchangeable, told to lay his schemes and hopes out and then wreck them himself. The lesson the throne keeps obscuring is the one waiting past the fire: that the summit was never worth the climb.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"ambition cross-examined until the striver is told to wreck his own schemes","built":"the anthem of ascent as the song's trap for a poisoned throne"},
"nevermore~your-poison-throne-without-guitar":{"haiku":"Toxic authority entrenches itself through mystification; the speaker rises from poison to claim sovereignty over inherited corruption.","opus":"A challenge to someone drunk on the poison of power and self-importance, told that the throne they cling to blinds them to the lesson coming before the burning. Their little plans and dreams are destined to be smashed to hell.","sonnet":"An address to someone — possibly a leader, possibly the self — sitting on a throne of self-constructed poison that obscures the lesson they need to learn before they can burn clean; humanity may be hollow and enslaved, but the call is still to rise above the toxic seat rather than smash all plans to hell.","src":"haiku"},
"nevertel~allineed-by-andromida-re-wyred":{"opus":"An empowerment cutoff of a manipulative, two-faced person who only clings to the useful, declaring that after all their lies the singer has learned they are all they need.","sonnet":"After exhausting herself on a manipulative, lying person who takes without giving, the narrator cuts them off entirely, recognizing that her energy has been drained and that she is sufficient on her own — the other person has inadvertently taught her self-reliance.","src":"sonnet"},
"nevertel~new-friend":{"opus":"Depression personified as an inseparable companion, the dark that talks back every night and refuses to leave. The narrator stops fighting and settles into cohabitation with despair, an intimate menace nobody else can perceive, hanging by a thread while the voices in his head insist they want him gone.","sonnet":"A song about living with chronic mental illness or depression personified as a companion — the 'new friend' never leaves, speaks at night, corrodes the melody of the narrator's life, and seems to want them dead. The narrator has stopped fighting the separation and instead describes the exhausted coexistence of someone who cannot escape their own mind.","src":"sonnet"},
"nevertel~sacrifice":{"haiku":"Anger and abandonment create cyclical crisis as the speaker oscillates between rage and breakdown.","opus":"Burnout and disillusion collide as a dream that once inspired now feels hollow, leaving the narrator going down the drain, cornered by rage and torn between leaving and staying. The title's question haunts a mind lodged at its lowest, alarms wailing into silence. Rock catharsis about the cost of chasing something that no longer feels like his.","sonnet":"Backed into a corner, burning out, and unable to contain the rage building inside, the speaker is stuck at their lowest — \"screaming in the silence\" — asking whether this prolonged sacrifice of self is actually leading anywhere.","src":"haiku"},
"nevertel~sym":{"haiku":"Relentless drive to overcome and dominate others masks internal fragmentation and pain the speaker cannot heal.","opus":"A self-hyping declaration of relentless drive and refusal to change, admitting the swagger is a voice built out of every time he was pushed to the edge. Telling a doubter to zip it, he confesses he had to shatter himself to thaw and to bury a long-carried pain.","sonnet":"A self-motivating battle rap about surviving people who want you to fail — the narrator has broken down to reach a cold heart, hidden pain for years, and now channels all of it into competitive drive while warning rivals their time is running out.","src":"haiku"},
"new-constellations~hot-blooded":{"haiku":"Mutual attraction mingles with emotional instability and neediness; both parties oscillate between desire and self-doubt without achieving stable connection.","opus":"A restless, urgent pursuit of someone in turmoil, the narrator insisting they're the match for a person who won't sit still. Desire framed as recognition, two similarly wired people daring each other to burn it down.","sonnet":"A speaker consumed by longing for an emotionally volatile woman insists he is what she is looking for, crawling after her even as he acknowledges she is reckless and unpredictable — desire and frustration coiled together in pursuit.","src":"haiku"},
"new-language~can-t-explain":{"haiku":"The speaker is marked by internal pain they cannot articulate; memories and regrets leave them callused, trapped in a cycle of unexpressed suffering.","opus":"A narrator circles something he can't remember, hold onto or put into words, a daily pretender running hollow, swallowing pain calloused on the surface yet staining the veins beneath. The blurry mirror slowly clears. The band render inarticulate inner ache as its own recurring refrain, the inability to explain becoming the very thing the song keeps naming.","sonnet":"Something buried deep in the body and memory refuses to surface clearly — a recurring sensation of incompleteness that language cannot capture or resolve. The speaker moves through daily life with a hollowness that has calcified into skin, swallowing a pain that remains unnamed even as it gradually starts to come into focus.","src":"haiku"},
"new-language~play-dead":{"haiku":"Trauma and insecurity force avoidance; the narrator struggles between hiding pain and confronting what lurks beneath, urging resistance to numbing escape.","opus":"An urgent push against emotional surrender, insisting a wounded listener need not feign disappearance behind a broken mirror of a million faces. Fire, red rage, and threads about to snap mark a struggle to face what festers underneath rather than hide. New Language frame their melodic metalcore as a rallying cry against self-erasure, urging the fight beneath the surface be named and taken personally.","sonnet":"Someone on the edge of collapse is urged not to go quiet and disappear. The song confronts the impulse to numb out and pretend the pain is manageable, arguing that facing what has been done to the self — holding on to it rather than suppressing it — is the only route through.","src":"haiku"},
"new-language~say-it":{"haiku":"The speaker dissolves into spectrality, fading under pressure and fragmentation while forced to articulate what burns inside. Urgency collides with disappearance as the heart gets chipped away piece by piece.","opus":"Anxious restlessness at a breakneck pace of life, the narrator retreating, feeling strange and haunted by having faded to a ghost once already. Beneath the runaway urge is a plea for honesty, daring another to finally speak up. Nervy alt-rock about someone braced to shatter yet demanding the words be said before his grip slips again.","sonnet":"Someone stuck in avoidance — fading, disappearing, \"been a ghost before\" — urges themselves and someone else to just say what needs to be said before the heart chips away entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"new-order~age-of-consent":{"haiku":"Communication failure and loss accumulate as a relationship ends; the narrator receives a message that confirms their fears and confirms the severance.","opus":"Post-Joy-Division New Order turning heartbreak into propulsion, a lover pleading to be released while admitting the loss is already total. The repeated defeat undercuts the bright momentum, grief driving the pulse.","sonnet":"The speaker nurses a wound from a message received days ago, telling the other person they will regret what they said, while layers of unexpressed feeling and resentment surface around the admission that has cost him the relationship entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"new-order~blue-monday":{"haiku":"Emotional vulnerability meets manipulation as one person questions how to feel amid abandonment, past duties, and heart-breaking rejection.","opus":"A cold interrogation of a lover's cruelty, the narrator disoriented and waiting, asking again and again how he is supposed to feel as a heart grows cold. Ships and obedience drift through the numbness. New Order, risen from Joy Division's wreckage, weld this brittle, questioning lyric to relentless sequenced machinery, turning heartbreak into the pulse of the era's most influential dancefloor lament.","sonnet":"A person who has been told how to feel and who to be stands confused and resentful at the edge of an emotional reckoning. Obedience has been conditioned into him, but something has shifted — a cold distance now spreads through what was once feeling, and the prescribed self no longer fits.","src":"haiku"},
"new-years-day~skeletons":{"haiku":"Demanding authentic intimacy and complete self-disclosure, rejecting performed identity.","opus":"Intimacy as demand, wanting to be shown a lover's hidden scars and secrets rather than a curated self. The gothic frame turns vulnerability into desire, insisting the haunted parts are exactly what she wants in.","sonnet":"A speaker asks a lover to stop pretending and show everything hidden — the scars, the secrets, the skeletons — insisting that knowing the worst is the only real intimacy, and that the haunting is better faced together than kept locked away.","src":"haiku"},
"new-young-pony-club~we-want-to":{"haiku":"Dissatisfaction with artifice and excess breeds apathy; true connection requires reciprocal desire over performance and consumption.","opus":"An indie-dance comedown on hedonism gone stale, the club scene exposed as prearranged and hollow. Boredom is the real enemy, and the one honest need under the ennui is simply not wanting to do any of it alone.","sonnet":"Disillusionment with a scene of performed pleasure — prearranged smiles, gold-plated chains, the exhausting performance of excess — is cut through by a single genuine desire: the speaker doesn't want any of this without a specific person, making real connection the only thing worth wanting in an otherwise vacant world.","src":"haiku"},
"newdad~angel":{"haiku":"Unworthiness poisons connection as the speaker fears drowning those who love them. Self-destructiveness and brokenness seem irredeemable, leaving only burden on the innocent.","opus":"A self-destructive person watches someone kind and grounded, aching to be like them but unwilling to let the other drown by taking on the burden of saving them. Loving someone healthy while hating yourself becomes an act of protective distance.","sonnet":"A speaker pushes away a good, grounded partner out of care rather than indifference — recognising their own broken, caring-less state, they refuse to let someone angelic drown in their darkness, insisting it is not fair to make the other person responsible for their ruin.","src":"haiku"},
"newdad~how":{"opus":"Being unable to stop thinking about someone you shouldn't want, at the wrong time and headspace; sleepless nights, dreams of them, and the effort of pretending you don't feel what you feel and keeping it secret.","sonnet":"The intrusive, unwanted persistence of feelings for someone after you've decided it was better not to pursue them — sleepless nights and dreams keep bringing the person back despite the narrator wanting to pretend they don't care.","src":"sonnet"},
"newdad~roobosh":{"haiku":"Daily repetition and stagnation breed mutual death; both lovers bury themselves alive.","opus":"A toxic, mutually deadening relationship where both people have essentially buried themselves alive to pass the time, and the narrator admits she may be the devil in it. She recognizes the cruelty she inflicts but finds it too shameful to fully own.","sonnet":"A relationship examined as mutual ruin: the speaker has slipped free of the other's grip and recognises they may be the devil — \"take hurting you to another level\" — while both parties have essentially buried themselves together, unchanging day after day, already dead.","src":"haiku"},
"newshapes~the-rain":{"haiku":"Dissolution and loss of certainty as hope crumbles to pieces, the speaker begging for cleansing while watching everything burn.","opus":"A collapse of hope watched like a house fire, the narrator begging for a rain that never comes as time drifts off and self-loathing closes in. Drowning and choking on a final song, he dreams of a way out, metalcore despair about clinging to a lost connection and hating himself while everything he knew burns.","sonnet":"Watching everything burn and begging for rain to fall — hope in pieces, distance between people, the only prayer a drowning one for another turn before the swan song ends. The admission 'flesh, skin and bone, all realise me, all said and done, I fucking despise me' collapses the external collapse into self-hatred.","src":"haiku"},
"nf~the-search":{"haiku":"Mental illness and existential crisis drive the narrator inward; therapy and introspection provide temporary relief from the churning darkness of self-awareness.","opus":"NF narrates his own mental health struggle in real time, breakdown, therapy, anxiety and OCD laid out plainly. The search is for hope and self-understanding, the pain converted into an unusually literal map of a mind under strain.","sonnet":"A rapper processing a breakdown, OCD, stage fright, and the emptiness of fame embarks on a sprawling inner journey — shedding burdens, confronting the mind's capacity for self-destruction, and insisting that the long road toward mental clarity is worth taking.","src":"haiku"},
"nghtmre~all-night":{"haiku":"Dissociation in crowded spaces breeds suicidal ideation; the narrator feels pulled toward oblivion despite surface-level pleasure, a puppet to substances and toxic relationships that mask existential despair.","opus":"A night out spirals into dissociation and dread, with a sinister figure pushing pills and promising to be the singer's puppeteer as they slowly fade. The party becomes a trap, and the recurring warning is to run from the night entirely.","sonnet":"Overwhelmed at a nightclub, the speaker spirals through anxiety and dissociation, hearing a voice that tempts them with pills to become a puppet, while repeating all night that they feel they shouldn't be here.","src":"haiku"},
"nia-archives~boys-in-blue":{"haiku":"Memory and romance dissolve into nostalgia for what cannot be recovered or returned to.","opus":"The jungle producer turns a soured romance into scorn for an ex who summons the police out of spite, his true face finally exposed. Where he was once her intimate partner, he now informs on her. A defiant split anthem about seeing someone plainly, feeling nothing remaining, and refusing to blink as they run to the authorities for payback.","sonnet":"A narrator confronts a former partner who called the police on her, framing it as the ultimate betrayal — bringing coppers to her home, snitching, the mask slipping off someone she once shared a bed with. Contempt drives every line: she never snitched, couldn't, and his running to the boys in blue reads as both weakness and unforgivable treachery.","src":"haiku"},
"nick-drake~one-of-these-things-first":{"haiku":"Indefinite potentiality paralyses becoming; the narrator imagines endless alternative selves while remaining present nowhere.","opus":"Drake cataloging the lives and selves he might have become, everything he could have been to someone but wasn't. Beneath the gentle list runs a paralyzing sense of unrealized potential and missed intimacy.","sonnet":"A meditation on unlived potential — the speaker catalogues all the things he might have been to someone, all the steady and devoted forms his love could have taken, before circling back to the fact that he is none of them, and perhaps never knew how to be.","src":"haiku"},
"nickelback~burn-it-to-the-ground":{"haiku":"Intoxicated revelers abandon all restraint and consequences, determined to destroy everything around them through reckless consumption and chaos.","opus":"A blackout party anthem of pure reckless indulgence, hands full of liquor, no hesitation, the crew swearing to smash out the lights and seize whatever catches their eye. Nickelback reduce the night to raw craving, decorum abandoned. Arena-rock hooks power a self-destructive bash bent on wrecking everything before daybreak.","sonnet":"A group of friends launches into a night of total abandon — drinking past reason, swinging from ceilings, spending freely and caring about nothing. The energy is purely destructive in the best sense: a collective decision to consume every last thing in reach and let the world stop turning until morning.","src":"haiku"},
"nickelback~how-you-remind-me":{"haiku":"A man in relationship failure reflects on his own worthlessness and inability to maintain love, blaming himself for not being good enough despite his declarations of affection.","opus":"A hungover post-breakup reckoning, a man made to confront his true nature through a partner he nearly wrecked. The self-pity soaked in drink and the taunting little phrase looping in his skull make it more about a man facing his own failure than grieving the relationship.","sonnet":"A post-breakup reckoning where the narrator admits he was a self-destructive, difficult partner — someone who drank heavily and couldn't keep promises — and acknowledges that the relationship finally made him confront who he actually is.","src":"haiku"},
"nicki-minaj~familia-spider-man-into-the-spider-verse-feat-bantu":{"haiku":"Familial loyalty transcends justice and individual harm; the speaker protects bloodline while threatening vengeance against violation.","opus":"A fierce declaration that family and loyalty come before money and can never be crossed, with vengeance promised on anyone who threatens them. Recorded for the Spider-Verse film, it turns familial devotion into a violent, protective battle cry.","sonnet":"A warning to anyone who crosses into the speaker's territory that loyalty to family is non-negotiable and retaliation will be lethal; the Spanish verses (Anuel) declare that loyalty is worth more than money and they will die before betraying family, while Nicki's verses shift into predatory threat — \"before I kill 'em, I'ma torture them first.\"","src":"haiku"},
"nicki-taylor~ugly-side":{"haiku":"Raw aggression and internal demons emerge when provoked, transforming the speaker into a warrior willing to wage psychological and emotional war.","opus":"A warning that letting someone in has awakened the demons, and now the ugly side is coming for whoever wants to fight. Refusing to be blamed as before, the transformation into war is framed as unstoppable self-defense turned offensive.","sonnet":"About reaching a breaking point after being consistently underestimated — the narrator strips away the composed exterior and warns that their demons are now live, they're done being blamed, and whoever provokes them next will face a war they won't survive.","src":"haiku"},
"nico-touches-the-walls~a-11l5r56":{"haiku":"Pursuing unseen futures with hopeful persistence—dreams and scars guide the narrator forward through rain and light toward worlds not yet discovered.","opus":"NICO Touches the Walls made their name on anime openings about pushing forward, and this is that spirit distilled. A young person sheds self-imposed limits, treating the hologram of dreams as more beautiful than any material treasure after years under a grey sky with a daily-changing map. The band turn rain-light refracting into color into the sign that the unknown world ahead is finally reachable.","sonnet":"A song about a young person shedding self-imposed limitations and pushing toward an unseen future: the hologram of \"dreams\" is more beautiful than any material treasure, the narrator has spent years under a grey sky with a daily-changing map, but rain-light refracting into color becomes a sign that the unknown world ahead is reachable.","src":"haiku"},
"nicolas-cage-fighter~black-jackal":{"haiku":"Internal predator hunted through fear transforms when mastered; the speaker subdues his own terror and emerges liberated.","opus":"The Black Jackal is a personified inner demon of fear, worthlessness and despair that has stalked the narrator all their life, until they finally master it and force it to heel. The turning point is deciding they are the master of their own fate rather than prey.","sonnet":"The Black Jackal is a personification of fear and despair — instilled by a life of subjugation and taught worthlessness — that hunts the speaker by scent; through confrontation the speaker refuses to be prey, commands the jackal to heel, and claims liberation even knowing it still lingers.","src":"haiku"},
"nicolas-rage~dirty-talk":{"opus":"A song about a stalling, mismatched hookup where the singer admits he's all talk and not the partner the other person actually wants, keeping himself at fault for the failed connection.","sonnet":"A sexual encounter shadowed by emotional mismatch: the narrator admits he isn't what the other person really wants, that his words are all talk, and that the other person clings to him while both know it won't go anywhere meaningful.","src":"sonnet"},
"nicumo~tree-of-life":{"haiku":"Growth and connection through silence; roots and leaves hold tight while smoke rises.","opus":"An anguished confession framed as the slow felling of a beloved tree, the narrator admitting he has used, drained, and harmed the very thing that reached toward the sky and connected earth to heaven. Melodic doom-tinged metal where love and destruction fuse, guilt spoken tenderly over the wreckage of what he himself tore down.","sonnet":"An address to a beloved tree — or a beloved person figured as tree — whose cold shivering hands are held as a promise is made that it won't be much longer. The speaker confesses to having slowly torn the tree down through use and indifference: I've drained you dry, done nothing but harm. The man is to blame, love — an environmental elegy that is also a love poem and an apology.","src":"haiku"},
"night-club~gone-feat-mj-keenan":{"haiku":"Loss and disappearance of the beloved or known, the speaker mourning absence.","opus":"A dark-synthpop dirge for a relationship past all rescue, the road ahead offering no return and the past gone black. Everything shared is repeated into oblivion until even the heart stops bleeding and stops needing. Cold, hypnotic resignation about two people who have become pawns to circumstance, watching all they had vanish for good.","sonnet":"Everything that defined a relationship or a shared world has been consumed — the past is black, we can't go back, the heart has stopped bleeding and emptiness lives on — what remains is not grief but a numbness past grief, and the itemized gone includes everything you did, everything you said, everything ahead.","src":"haiku"},
"night-verses~infinity-beach":{"opus":"A spoken-word instrumental musing on what living actually is, drawing on the idea of existence as luminous energy racing across the void. The piece finds creative fuel and awe in imagination and in the dawn, treating scientific wonder as a wellspring for art.","sonnet":"A collage of spoken fragments about music, life, and light — including a quote about radiant energy traveling at 186,000 miles per second — creating the impression of a transmission cut short, more concept than song, pointing toward infinity without arriving there.","src":"sonnet"},
"nightdive~diviner":{"haiku":"Longing and distance create unbearable separation, the speaker begs for reunion while falling, caught between worlds too far apart.","opus":"Restless nighttime longing for someone absent, thoughts refusing to settle while the speaker imagines sailing together and begs on the floor for a love out of reach. Ultimately it's about making peace with a goodbye never ready to be said, accepting there's no time anymore.","sonnet":"A song about the unbearable weight of long-distance longing after a sudden goodbye — begging on the floor from missing someone, recognizing time has run out, and trying to make uneasy peace with what can't be undone.","src":"haiku"},
"nightdive~move-on":{"haiku":"Failed relationships demand graceful severance; the speaker acknowledges mutual self-destruction while seeking a separation that honors the pain without erasing what mattered.","opus":"A love that survives only as fiction, one partner narrating the whole story while the other dissolves, telling them to leave because staying is a self-destructive mess. The mercy and the cruelty are the same sentence: move on.","sonnet":"Two people in a failing relationship acknowledge they are destroying each other — one is a self-destructive mess who pulls the other apart — and agree they need to end it even though it will hurt.","src":"haiku"},
"nightlife~new-low":{"haiku":"Someone repeatedly ruins relationships through self-centered behavior and self-sabotage, aware but unable to change the pattern.","opus":"A raw, clear-eyed account of hitting rock bottom, the narrator owning that he centers everything on himself, never absorbs the lesson, and keeps circling in place, losing ground for every step gained, with countless things he never managed to voice piling up. It's confession as glossy pop-rock catharsis. Nightlife lean into exactly this mix of emo candor and shiny hooks, the wound handed over as an anthem of stalled self-sabotage.","sonnet":"A relationship fraying under the weight of someone's habitual self-sabotage — they know the pattern, name the new low they've reached, and can't stop cycling anyway. The unsaid things pile up into something that can't be undone; the other person has already moved on while the speaker is still rehearsing what they should have said.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~7-days-to-the-wolves":{"haiku":"Approaching mortality and final judgment; the speaker prepares for death and reconciles love with sacrifice while questioning what awaits.","opus":"Wolves arrive as gentle executioners escorting lovers back into oblivion, while a weeklong countdown ticks toward death and some promised afterlife. The narrator calls this his chosen faith, the point where the brave and the fearful split apart. Nightwish, in Tuomas Holopainen's sweeping symphonic mode, cast dying as an appointed calling, urging the untrodden path and the freeing of poets.","sonnet":"A countdown toward collective doom unfolds as a kind of reverent farewell — seven days to whatever ending waits beyond the poison, the wolves closing in while lovers and heroes and cowards choose their final postures. The facing of mortality is rendered not as despair but as a strange, defiant sacrament.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~alpenglow":{"haiku":"Two travelers build a private Walden together, writing endless stories until time demands they rest and dissolve into silence.","opus":"Holopainen's hymn to imagination and shared creation, two figures building a private Walden and finally dissolving into the music itself. Mortality is met calmly here, life and art becoming one at the last light.","sonnet":"A song of presence and arrival: two companions have roamed, grieved, built, and conquered together, and now it is time to rest — to become the music, to dissolve into alpenglow and the snow that quietly covers everything they made. It is a benediction of shared life and a beautiful ending.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~amaranth":{"haiku":"A person isolated by doubt seeks connection and eternal beauty through compassion and recognition of shared human fragility.","opus":"Tuomas frames the doubting outsider through the amaranth, the mythic never-fading flower, urging tenderness toward the one who wars with himself. A plea to caress the sorrowing soul reaching for those who dare.","sonnet":"A song about a person searching for something untouchable and eternal — the 'amaranth,' an undying flower — and the tension between what you believe and what you actually give, with a melancholy doubter finding their way toward the daybreak.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~bare-grace-misery":{"haiku":"Seduction and feminine transgression tempt with shameless desire while the song celebrates uninhibited sensuality and defiant unchastity.","opus":"A seductive dark-feminine voice offering herself as the listener's shadow and tempter, part storybook waif and part unabashed hunger, promising a dance as painful as damnation. She owns her own wretchedness and refuses any embarrassment, an untamed creature no priest could ever save. Nightwish drape it in lush symphonic metal.","sonnet":"A figure speaks from a place of seductive ruin — dark side of you, cinnamon bed, a Little Match Girl freezing in the snow — offering love that lies and entices while accepting it will never reach heaven, feeling no shame, wild and beyond remorse.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~bye-bye-beautiful":{"haiku":"A band bids farewell to an unappreciative audience that refused to truly listen or engage with their art, wounded by rejection despite their dedication.","opus":"A bitter farewell to someone who refused to listen, hear, or let the world in; the singer bids goodbye to a beautiful but blind person who chose the long road and gambled away all they did not have.","sonnet":"A farewell directed at someone whose ingratitude and hostility consumed a creative partnership — the narrator asks whether anything shared ever reached her, and concludes that she chose her path alone, leaving them to carry the scars of her words.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~cadence-of-her-last-breath":{"haiku":"A lonely observer mourns someone fleeting and beautiful they never met, their death unlocking poetry and meaning to existence.","opus":"A meditation on mortality and the pang of loving something short-lived, comparing a woman to a butterfly with a single day to exist, whose final exhale the isolated narrator yearns to witness. The deeper claim is that beauty's fleetingness is precisely what gives it weight, filling the poet's empty sheet even as one dream melts into the next.","sonnet":"A loner is transfixed by a woman he glimpsed briefly — a butterfly, here one day — whose dying or departure strikes him as something he needed without knowing it, and whose last breath he would give anything to have witnessed; grief for someone never truly known.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~creek-mary-s-blood":{"haiku":"Native Americans mourn their ancestral lands stolen by colonizers through broken treaties and violent conflict. The song honors indigenous dispossession and cultural survival against erasure.","opus":"A dying elder passes his story down through lineage and kin, recounting the seizure of a sacred homeland through a rigged contest and the forced exile that followed. Nightwish, the Finnish symphonic-metal band, hand the closing section to a spoken Lakota lament, letting an Indigenous voice carry the sorrow the English verses only outline.","sonnet":"An Indigenous elder speaks through the blood memory of their people, recounting a land stolen by colonizers via an unfair fight rather than a fair war. The Trail of Tears becomes a traversal into spiritual permanence — the land was always theirs by the oldest claim of time itself.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~dark-chest-of-wonders":{"haiku":"A person reclaims a pure childhood dream they once held, flying free from burden to reunite with something stolen by the world.","opus":"An ode to reclaiming childhood imagination, the dream the adult world stole, opened again only by the pure of heart. Escapism as salvation, flying back across the years to the wonder faith once made possible.","sonnet":"A return to childhood wonder — slipping out a window at midnight and flying toward a chest of wonders seen only through the uncorrupted eyes of a child, reclaiming something beautiful that the world took away.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~dead-gardens":{"haiku":"A weary artist accepts that their stories are exhausted and their creative garden has withered, deciding to stop creating and withdraw.","opus":"A drained artist lowers the curtain on her own creativity, every story exhausted and her writing tool feeling useless, tending a barren place where imagination once flourished and wondering whether to defy some age-old order. Written as Tarja-era Nightwish rose, the symphonic lament reads as a weary goodbye to invention, torn between duty and creative burnout.","sonnet":"A storyteller reaches the end of creative and emotional reserves, setting down a pen that has served its purpose. The world of imagination — its wolves, its elven paths, its starlit lagoons — has gone silent, and what remains is a quiet obligation to the real, with nothing left to tend but dead gardens.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~devil-and-the-deep-dark-ocean":{"haiku":"Oceanic seduction promises transcendence through drowning; the speaker welcomes death as mystical union with dark majesty.","opus":"A dark oceanic myth in which a sacrificed maiden is married off to a monstrous, unborn ruler of the depths, her innocence defiled and death itself her bridal gown. The narrator swears to perish out of love for the sea-woman even though it costs him everything.","sonnet":"A cosmic birth narrative in which a dark lord is born from blood-red waters — foretold by a snowy owl, nursed by the deep ocean — intersects with a mortal speaker who will die for the seduction of the mermaid, and a second voice refuses salvation and prayer, choosing wickedness and the cradle-to-coffin embrace of the deep.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~endless-forms-most-beautiful":{"haiku":"Evolution connects all life across deep time in awe-struck wonder, treating existence as a sacred journey through millions of forms sharing breath and consciousness.","opus":"A hymn to Darwinian evolution as sacred wonder, inviting the listener to trace the whole aeon-long lineage of life back to the first single cell and forward to what we'll become. Holopainen recasts a pale-blue planet of ever-changing organisms as reason for awe without needing the supernatural.","sonnet":"A hymn to evolutionary wonder, inviting the listener on a journey back through deep time to greet the Tiktaalik taking her first steps, a eukaryote finding her way, and Mother Eve — celebrating the pale blue ark of Earth and all its endless, beautiful forms of life.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~eva":{"haiku":"A kind-hearted child is mocked and abused by others; her goodness becomes a weapon used against her until she escapes by dreaming herself away.","opus":"A tender lament for a gentle girl bullied and shamed by a heartless world, with no one to stand by her, who takes refuge in fantasies of somewhere better. The deeper reading is that society crushes innocence for sport, and the singer vows that her goodness will endure through him as something living and bright.","sonnet":"About a lonely, bullied girl who escapes the cruelty of the world through dreams — Eva is mocked and shamed into the margins of society, but her goodness and imagination let her fly away to somewhere better, and the song mourns how the world destroys kind people.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~fantasmic":{"haiku":"A wish-master invites travelers into a realm of stories and fantasy, where beloved tales and archetypes come alive.","opus":"A rapturous tribute to Disney animation and childhood wonder, cataloging its heroes and villains as a realm of make-believe worth believing in. The song's real subject is the enduring power of story to keep the child alive.","sonnet":"A love letter to Disney's animated legacy, moving through iconic characters and moments — Simba, Belle, the Sorcerer's Apprentice, Maleficent, Ariel — as proof that wishing on a star and believing in fantasy is the purest form of devotion. The song is a breathless, grateful catalogue of an art form that built a childhood.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~for-the-heart-i-once-had":{"haiku":"Loss of innocence and childhood joy; a dead boy's scars never heal as time passes, leaving only nostalgia for the heart once held.","opus":"Nightwish mourn a vanished innocence, grieving a childlike heart that can never be recovered and a self that is gone for good. Written amid the band's rupture with original singer Tarja, the lament is doubly charged: the yearning for a lost home mirrors a group in painful transition, warning that time will not mend such wounds but only deepen them.","sonnet":"Grief for an inner child who has permanently departed — the warmth and innocence that once animated the speaker now flows only through music, which mourns what it can no longer embody. The wound is not fresh but chronic, a dead boy's scar that time will never fully close over.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~ghost-love-score":{"haiku":"Obsessive love where the speaker offers eternal devotion and bleeding loyalty to the one who wounds them; memory and desire intertwine in depth.","opus":"A vast tidal romance where devotion becomes a willed fall, love offered as a wound the beloved is invited to open. A siren singing the singer own name blurs muse and lover, feeding the confession that these songs are written from that dream. A centerpiece of Nightwish symphonic ambition, it treats surrender and homecoming as the same longing.","sonnet":"A man consumed by mythic longing surrenders entirely to a love that wounds him — a siren figure calling from some primordial depth. He craves rescue, redemption, or annihilation in equal measure, unable to distinguish salvation from destruction in the heart of another.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~ghost-river":{"haiku":"A mysterious river takes victims deeper into drowning darkness, with mythical figures representing danger and death along its path.","opus":"A dark fairy tale of a child lured toward a wild river that will drown and claim him, narrated by a menacing troll-voice of abuse and doubt. The refrain drags everything deeper down toward a ghost town. Nightwish, in their Anette-era cinematic mode, weave duet menace and folk dread, turning the drowning current into a fable of innocence pulled under by predatory forces.","sonnet":"A child is led toward a dangerous river by a seductive and treacherous guide who wears many disguises — painted faces, a troll under the bridge, the sand in an hourglass. The river is both literal and metaphysical, promising wonder and threatening to swallow the innocent whole before they ever reach the other side.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~higher-than-hope":{"haiku":"A dying person reflects on lost time and unmet hopes, finding solace in the thought that their death might free someone else they love.","opus":"A grief song addressed to someone gone, whose death paradoxically rescued the mourner, leaving him to carry forward dreams that once belonged to them both. Nightwish, the Finnish symphonic-metal band, pair orchestral sweep with intimate loss here, the track written in memory of a departed loved one, its comfort found somewhere beyond ordinary hope.","sonnet":"A letter from someone dying — probably of illness — addressed to a mother, written with gratitude rather than bitterness and passing the dreams forward to whoever remains. Embedded spoken words from a real patient contextualize the poetry: the fear of the unknown, the grim comfort of being informed, the question of why one person is saved when another is not.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~i-want-my-tears-back":{"haiku":"The singer grieves the loss of childhood wonder and emotional depth, wishing to recover the innocence and sleepless passion they once possessed.","opus":"The narrator grieves the vanished enchantment of childhood, listing wintry landscapes, meadows and storybook wonders while pleading for the wide-eyed awe he once thrived on. Nightwish's symphonic metal surges with longing, its literary and fairy-tale nods conjuring an innocence he aches to recover before time runs out.","sonnet":"The emotional capacity for wonder — once triggered by childhood landscapes, fairy-tale logic, and the pure receptivity of a young self — has gone missing in adulthood, and the speaker mourns its absence with urgent precision. Reclaiming the ability to weep would mean recovering the lost interior life before time erases the possibility.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~kuolema-tekee-taiteilijan":{"haiku":"A Finnish-language meditation: the speaker regrets childhood confinement, seeks reunion with their creator to become worthy of their child's idealization, and dreams of escape.","opus":"Sung in Finnish, a hushed, aching prayer whose title means death makes the artist, the narrator glimpsing a dream once, imprisoned as if behind cradle-bars, writing letters and asking the creator to let him become what his child believes him to be, having made his own heaven only to beg release from it. Nightwish, Finland's symphonic-metal masters, set this tender, mortality-haunted lullaby to delicate orchestration rather than bombast.","sonnet":"A prisoner or confined man writing to his creator from inside a cradle turned cage, asking to become the person his child believes him to be. Beauty in the world — someone's beauty — is what made an artist of him, and now he has built his own small heaven inside and wants only to be released from it.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~lan":{"haiku":"A person is called to abandon sleep and step into springtime, racing against darkness and fate to finally live boldly—tasting wine, riding shooting stars, and meeting death at the cliff by the sea.","opus":"An ecstatic summons to seize life before it slips: leave sleep, jump the uncrossed cliffs, dance a jig at a funeral, and race the darkness with open lungs. The invitation is to live boldly and without orthodoxy, treating mortality as the reason to plunge rather than to freeze. Nightwish, the Finnish symphonic-metal band, built such grand humanist crescendos in their late-2000s catalogue, wrapping the leap of faith in orchestral sweep.","sonnet":"An invitation to shed ordinary life and step into something vast and pre-human — dawn greeted with bare feet, clouds surfed, cliffs leapt. The pull here is toward the unrepeatable and alive, the answer to life's riddle found not in logic but in dead leaves and swans returning, in the minute of a lover's look. Élan over orthodoxy, a jig at the funeral.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~master-passion-greed":{"haiku":"A scathing portrait of greed, betrayal, and selfish seduction—the narrator describes cycles of manipulation, obsession, and moral decay.","opus":"A savage tirade against a greedy hypocrite cloaking predation in false righteousness, likened to a traitor and a parasite who can never be satisfied. The brutal refrain, a checklist of pursuit, betrayal, and murder, exposes avarice as a violent, misogynistic cycle, sharpened by the narrator's own disgust at helping sustain the very thing that devours him.","sonnet":"A poisonous portrait of someone — likely a former partner or colleague — whose defining trait is greed and manipulation: they seduced, used, destroyed, and felt nothing, while the speaker is left in silent shame for not stopping it sooner.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~meadows-of-heaven":{"haiku":"Childhood wonder preserves itself as sanctuary across time, buried beneath adult compromise but accessible through memory and ritual return.","opus":"Aching nostalgia for a vanished childhood, a remembered meadow standing in for a home you can never return to. Nightwish end their album gazing back at a small child's paradise, its buried treasures reachable only in sleep.","sonnet":"A tender, elegiac return to a childhood landscape — wild honey, forest streams, wooden swings, toy soldiers — that exists now only in memory and dream. The rocking chair is empty, the toys abandoned, but the speaker re-enters that world at the edge of sleep, guided by a mother's care and the colours of kites.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~nemo":{"haiku":"A person names themselves Nemo (nobody) and grieves the loss of their identity and vitality, describing themselves as a withered flower. They seek transcendence through pain and hope that revealing their true name might restore what was lost.","opus":"Tarja sings as an unnamed, adrift figure begging for release and rest, her name meaning no one in Latin. Withered and lost in the dark, she pleads for gentle rain and the chance to sleep and imagine freely once more, finding peace only by surrendering and drifting back where she belongs.","sonnet":"A song about being lost without a true identity or sense of self — the narrator (\"Nemo\" meaning \"no one\" in Latin) drifts through life without an honest heart or compass, longing to dream again and to discover his real name, which stands for a true, authentic self. The closing image of Nemo sailing home and letting go suggests either death or finally finding peace.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~ocean-soul":{"haiku":"Oceanic dissolution beckons as escape; the speaker surrenders to the sea as completion of romantic devotion.","opus":"Tarja voicing a lonely soul drawn toward the sea, longing to be remade into something beautiful by music and devotion. The pull toward the waves reads as both surrender and a wish for transcendence over isolation.","sonnet":"A lonely figure longs to dissolve into the sea — trading earthly suffering for union with the waves — and finds in that yearning a form of devotion that replaces lost emotion with something vast and impersonal.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~over-the-hills-and-far-away":{"haiku":"A man is wrongly convicted but keeps silent about his alibi to protect his affair. He serves prison time for years while his lover waits, sustained by letters.","opus":"A wrongly convicted man goes to prison for ten years rather than confess the alibi that would free him, because that fateful night was spent with his best friend's wife, and both of them swear he will return to her arms. It is a ballad of love guarded through disgrace. Nightwish covered this Gary Moore song, and the Finnish band's soaring delivery turned the doomed romance into an arena-scale vow.","sonnet":"A man wrongly convicted of robbery accepts a decade of prison rather than reveal where he was the night of the crime — in the arms of his best friend's wife. Both he and she sustain themselves on letters and the mutual vow that he will return to her, over every hill and sea between them, no matter how long it takes.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~planet-hell":{"haiku":"Earth is a hellish planet where children fight for survival and hope is absent, ruled by suffering and betrayal. The living endure daily horrors with no escape, trapped in divine cruelty.","opus":"Symphonic metal indicting a cruel creation and the God who signed off on it, Earth reframed as the actual hell for the children born to suffer on it. Rage at divine indifference, sung operatically.","sonnet":"About a world that chews up its children while the strong save only themselves — the ferryman's penny is self-preservation, the ark belongs to no one, and mankind keeps working in its mysterious, lethal ways. The narrator was born into this hell and can only watch as the meek are slaughtered.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~romanticide":{"haiku":"The narrator suffers under lies and fake expectations, describing love's death through deception and comparing himself to corrupted creations suffering shame.","opus":"From Nightwish's heavier Century Child, a scorched-earth disavowal of love and false feeling, the narrator branding themselves rot and demanding solitude to mourn in. Deceit poisons them, warmth turns to ice, and a formerly fearless heart hardens. Undone by their own inventions, they sneer at fate as a rigged game, love reframed as something worth destroying.","sonnet":"A figure suffocated by lies and others' demands on how to feel declares himself ruined by his own romantic ideals — love becomes the agent of destruction rather than rescue. The word romanticide names the phenomenon precisely: the killing of the capacity for love, accomplished not by cruelty but by an overdose of it.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~sahara":{"haiku":"A pharaoh flees divine curse across a deadly desert transformed into a chase; the persecuted finds refuge only in mirage as death closes in.","opus":"Nightwish stage a widescreen desert legend: a pharaoh flees the fury of the gods across dunes teeming with death, a procession of the damned in pursuit, until the sand finally becomes a paradise. Orchestral bombast and Arabian-Nights motifs frame a wanderer seeking rest beneath a mirage that may be his grave.","sonnet":"A man flees across an ocean of sand pursued by divine wrath, chased by a caravan of the cursed through a desert that becomes an endless death-sentence. The imagery blends ancient myth and Arabian Nights — a lone wanderer crushed between a dark queen's song and the pharaoh's relentless horizon.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~shudder-before-the-beautiful":{"haiku":"Cosmic mystery and earthly beauty command reverence from voyagers who tremble before the interconnected grandeur of existence itself.","opus":"Awe as the deepest kind of understanding — facing the cosmos and the root of existence leaves you quaking before its beauty rather than trying to conquer it. It casts humanity as travelers on a wandering speck of a planet, stirred to reverence by the quiet that lives between the sounds of being.","sonnet":"A meditation on awe as the deepest form of understanding — the music of silence between notes, the vagrant island Earth as pilgrim — positioning humanity as voyagers who shudder before the grandeur of existence, from cathedral greens to interstellar theatre.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~sleeping-sun-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Nocturnal desire and solar eclipse symbolize the wish for permanent darkness and union with an idealized other beyond the reach of light and time.","opus":"Written for a solar eclipse, a yearning to hold onto darkness and one beloved night against the light's return. The wish to sink away with the sun edges into a gentle, romanticized longing to stop time altogether.","sonnet":"A wish to remain in perpetual night alongside a loved one, treating darkness as a sanctuary of shared grief and longing — the sun is sleeping, and the speaker wants to go down with it rather than face another day alone.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~storytime":{"haiku":"Celebration of imagination, storytelling, and narrative as the essence of human meaning. The song frames tales as portals to wonder and dreams that read reality into being.","opus":"A celebration of imagination and storytelling, voiced by the spirit of Never Never Land and childhood dreams that read the listener into reality. It casts tales as a limitless treasure and the very core of life.","sonnet":"The song is a hymn to the power of storytelling itself, personifying narrative as the voice of Neverland and the dreams of every person, capable of making the imaginary real and giving life its deepest meaning. Writers, painters, and wanderers like Alice are all participants in this limitless world of tales.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~the-islander":{"haiku":"An elderly lighthouse keeper remembers his lost love and former life, now resigned to fade alone as he sets sail for the last time.","opus":"A gentle character study of a solitary lighthouse keeper at the edge of the map, guiding strangers safely back while his own past joys have dimmed to memory. He casts off for a final voyage at peace. Nightwish drop the symphonic scale for Celtic folk warmth, with Marco Hietala voicing an exile's quiet accounting of a full life now forgotten by the world.","sonnet":"An old man exiled to a windswept island tends a lighthouse at the edge of the world, guiding ships home while his own memories of family and belonging fade to fog. In his final act he sails out one last time, settling peacefully into the deep with grass still on his feet.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~the-poet-and-the-pendulum":{"haiku":"A dark narrative of a tortured poet and writer condemned by society, depicting suffering, sacrifice, and the duality of public performance versus private agony before a violent end. The song explores the cost of artistic creation and spiritual ruin.","opus":"A suite about a broken composer's staged execution and inner ruin, casting him as a widowed writer shredded by his own art and self-loathing. He begs to be carried back to a lost paradise and granted a gentle end, while those around him jeer and desecrate his memory in a theatrical martyrdom.","sonnet":"A long, multi-part meditation on the death of a poet and artist — moving through despair, self-loathing, longing for an innocent past, and the terror of a life spent performing for a cold world — ending with his betrayal by friends and the bleak image of them spitting on his grave.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~the-siren":{"haiku":"A person struggles against fate, bound by forces beyond their control, unable to see their own path forward despite hearing a calling.","opus":"A sailor bound to the wheel is drawn by a violin-playing enchantress whose call promises revelation he can hear but never see. The zodiac wheels overhead as fate stays veiled and the winds speak only to his sails. Nightwish rework the Odyssean myth into hypnotic symphonic metal, Tarja's operatic lure circling a seafarer caught between longing and the certainty of destruction.","sonnet":"A figure bound to a ship's wheel hears a beckoning call from the sea but cannot locate its source. Fate is written in the stars turning overhead, and whether the chains that bind him there were imposed or self-forged is no longer clear — the call remains, unanswerable and irresistible.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~two-for-tragedy":{"haiku":"Two angels rest beneath a candle bed, united in grief and accepting that some suffering cannot be healed.","opus":"An elegy of a mother mourning a lost son, two souls laid to rest together with everything left unsaid. The devastation is the sacrifice of maternal love that persists past death and blames no one, only grieves.","sonnet":"A parent mourns a child who has died, singing over them with a grief that refuses comfort or eternity, imagining the two of them lying together beneath the candles — two saddened angels with everything still unsaid between them. It is elegy in its most inconsolable form.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~weak-fantasy":{"haiku":"Religious and patriarchal storytelling enslaves children with fear and suffering, perpetuating cycles of control masquerading as salvation.","opus":"An attack on organized religion as a male-run death cult of fear, sacrifice and invented sin, poisoning each new child with inherited dogma instead of a better story. The band argues every child deserves a truer tale than the ones handed down.","sonnet":"A fierce attack on organised religion as tribal poetry and witchcraft filling the void — male necrocracy sustaining itself through fear, slavery, and storytelling — concluding that every child deserves a better tale than the one offered by faith's condescending apes.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~whoever-brings-the-night":{"haiku":"Transient lovers seduce each other in darkness through pain and lies; the narrator craves suffering at the hands of a phantom, beautiful seducer.","opus":"A lurid nocturne pairing a fallen woman and a timid seaman who court the darkness with agony and ecstasy, the narrator inviting harm and branding all affection a deception. Blood-drinking, immortality and drowning imagery thread through it. Nightwish lean into gothic transgression, staging desire as a welcome poison accepted for a single night.","sonnet":"A nocturnal seduction told from inside the transaction — the masquerade of desire, the conscious embrace of being used and hurt. The speaker courts the darkness willingly, treating pain as pleasure and the brief predatory encounter as a kind of freedom that daylight and propriety deny.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~wish-i-had-an-angel":{"haiku":"A confession of lust and moral compromise—the speaker desires angelic salvation or connection but recognizes their own capacity for cruelty and betrayal. Beauty is entangled with darkness and hypocrisy.","opus":"Lust dressed in the language of devotion, the narrator confessing he wants the body and thrill more than love, burning purity to dust. Tuomas Holopainen leans into betrayal and dark desire, romance stripped down to appetite.","sonnet":"The narrator is consumed by lust and self-destructive desire, longing for an angel while acknowledging that old loves and lies are hard to shed, and that beauty is inseparable from dark impulses.","src":"haiku"},
"nightwish~wishmaster":{"opus":"A fantasy incantation packed with Tolkien and Dragonlance references, tracing a pupil's rise toward mastery. A wonderstruck young dreamer gazes into the night sky and heeds the pull of secret, magical wisdom, journeying through invented realms of elves and sorcerers.","sonnet":"A dreamy-eyed child becomes apprentice to a wishmaster drawn from the vocabulary of Tolkien, Dragonlance, and fantasy literature — the journey through grey havens, sorcery, and heartborne questing culminates in the apprentice becoming the master themselves.","src":"sonnet"},
"nik-nocturnal~chromatic":{"haiku":"Revenge becomes the sole rational response to cosmic isolation, channeling dark power and demonic communion to restore control in a world stripped of value.","opus":"A descent into darkness embraced as power, the narrator drawn toward terror and revenge, feeling lightning in his veins as he calls on the dark side to claim what he believes is his. Djent-metal built on the seductive logic of vengeance, a lone figure convinced that ruling this world means giving himself fully over to the storm.","sonnet":"A self-declared renegade channels lightning and darkness as fuel for revenge, positioning themselves as an outsider summoning power from the 'dark side' to rule against those who reduced them to a number and cast them aside.","src":"haiku"},
"nik-nocturnal~granny-in-da-club":{"haiku":"A young man pursues an elderly woman at the club; the song plays with attraction and taboo.","opus":"A deliberately absurd metalcore joke: last man in the club ends up flirting with an elderly woman who out-sasses him at every turn. The comedy is the gap between drunken beer-goggle romance and her cheerfully brutal rejections.","sonnet":"A comedic narrative: the last person left in the club is an elderly woman with gray curly hair and a new hip who outmanoeuvres the narrator's advances with devastating one-liners — 'not with your cucumber' — turning a drunken night into an absurd love song for the only dancer still standing.","src":"haiku"},
"nik-nocturnal~last-night":{"haiku":"Drunken honesty ruptures relationship while both parties resist ending; the cycle repeats as alcohol loosens restraint repeatedly.","opus":"A drunken, volatile relationship where liquor loosens cruel truths and threats to leave, yet the singer keeps insisting it isn't really over. The tail lights and slammed doors are just another cycle of a love too intoxicating to quit.","sonnet":"A volatile couple who fight, drink a bottle of Jack sip-for-sip, and say things they don't mean both know — despite tail lights in the dust and slammed doors — that this breakup won't stick, because neither one can walk away for good.","src":"haiku"},
"nik-nocturnal~moan":{"haiku":"Suffocation and violent struggle mark existence; the speaker chokes under the embrace of blood and the weight of pain.","opus":"A suffocating account of a toxic bond, the narrator slowly smothered yet unable to escape another whose grip is an embrace of blood. Loss, pain, and a refusal to let go tangle together. Heavy, claustrophobic metal reducing an obsessive, mutually destructive attachment to the sensation of drowning while still reaching for the one pulling him under.","sonnet":"Suffocation as the central act: the speaker is slowly choked by someone who watches and takes the twilight away, and the warning you won't get away shifts the dynamic from victim to threat. Brief, airless, violent intimacy.","src":"haiku"},
"nik-nocturnal~primal":{"haiku":"A speaker wrestles internal demons demanding surrender and control. Only salvaging one life matters—refusing conquest and assimilation.","opus":"A confrontation with an inner demon urging the mind to be used as a weapon and to assimilate, met with a fierce insistence on protecting one's single life and refusing to be conquered.","sonnet":"A song about battling an internal predator — voices inside urge the narrator to become a weapon, to abandon human contact and give themselves over to something feral and destructive, but they ultimately resist, insisting they have only one life and will not surrender it to whatever is trying to take over.","src":"haiku"},
"nik-nocturnal~slavic-metal-2":{"haiku":"Slavic identity crystallizes through squat posture, Adidas wear, and vodka devotion, frames as biological permanence. Cultural specificity becomes a badge against appropriation and performative claim.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek anthem celebrating Slavic squat-and-hardbass culture, all Adidas stripes, vodka, and Braník beer worn as proud identity across Czech, Slovenian, and Slovak boasts. The djent producer plays it as affectionate parody, exaggerating stereotypes into a rowdy, self-aware tribute where being a wolf-strong Slav until death is both punchline and genuine badge of belonging.","sonnet":"A comedic-celebratory anthem about Slavic identity built around the squat, vodka, Adidas, and hardbass — mixing English with Slovenian, Czech, and Slovak lines to insist the culture runs in the blood ('vodka in my veins'), with the squat as both physical feat and cultural rite of passage.","src":"haiku"},
"nikol~fade-out":{"haiku":"The speaker reveals a dark side hidden beneath appearances, turning the tables on someone who judged too quickly and warning of consequences.","opus":"A defiant rebuttal to someone who judged and dismissed the narrator before knowing her, met with a warning that his arrogance and ignorance will burn out fast once he tries to invade. She refuses the false image projected onto her and dares him to sleep with one eye open. The register is confrontational alt-rock empowerment, a woman flipping contempt back on the accuser and watching him fade.","sonnet":"A direct confrontation with someone who has mislabelled and judged the speaker, treating assumptions as fact. The speaker strips away the other's smugness: your arrogance is transparent, your time is up, you are welcome to try to invade but you will be the one who disappears. A fury that turns the threat of erasure back on the accuser.","src":"haiku"},
"nils-landgren-funk-unit~house-party":{"haiku":"Improvised domesticity becomes escape from scarcity; simple gathering promises joy unburdened by economic constraint.","opus":"A broke narrator who can't afford the movies or new clothes decides to throw a bring-your-own house party instead, ready to disturb the neighbors and call in sick for it. A funky celebration of making your own good time on no money.","sonnet":"Broke and under-dressed for a movie or disco, the speaker decides to drag the stereo out and invite everyone over, eventually calling in sick to work when the house fills with people and the party becomes too good to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~a-z8w9ux":{"haiku":"A melancholic reflection on lost youth and the regret of forgetting—innocence was shed, time moved too fast, and nostalgia haunts.","opus":"Ninajirachi, the Australian hyperpop and electronic producer, keeps this brief and pointed, a look back at a time when naivety came naturally, now far behind her. The single demand, to be chosen on her own terms, carries the quiet self-worth of someone long overlooked. Her glassy production leaves the assertion room to sound less like a plea than a line drawn, innocence spent but leverage found.","sonnet":"A brief song about lost innocence and the desire to be chosen on one's own terms — the narrator looks back at a time when naivety came naturally, notes it's now gone far behind her, and makes a simple demand: choose me the way I want to be chosen. The emotional core is the quiet assertion of self-worth from someone who has been overlooked.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~all-i-am":{"opus":"Ninajirachi treats the voice as texture as much as message, and here the lyric collapses to a single repeated declaration that everything she is and cares for is found in the person addressed, laced with wordless vocalizations. There is no narrative to develop. The producer lets the electronic wash carry a pure statement of total romantic dependency, the emptiness of everything else the point rather than a lack.","sonnet":"The lyrics consist almost entirely of a repeated declaration that everything the narrator is and cares about is found in the person they're addressing, accompanied by wordless vocalizations. There is no narrative development; it is a pure expression of total romantic dependency with no thematic content beyond that single statement.","src":"sonnet"},
"ninajirachi~angel-music":{"haiku":"Self-mythologizing confidence, girls transcendent and impervious to external judgment or shame.","opus":"Hyperpop bravado dressed as divinity, a woman who hears your prayers and could hand you the world but withholds it on purpose. The line about girls like her descending from heaven works as both flex and dismissal, the power in choosing not to indulge you.","sonnet":"Two girls assert their self-sufficiency and divine origin — \"girls like us come straight from heaven\" — dismissing a tearful ex and offering each other what he cannot: the readiness to give everything craved without apology.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~csirac":{"haiku":"Two girls orbit each other—one dreamed, one dreaming. Finding oneself means finding the other everywhere.","opus":"A tender, uncanny voice promising protection and omnipresence dissolves into glitching repetition and a dream of following a half-remembered song; named for Australia's pioneering computer, it renders an AI or digital ghost as a comforting presence that fragments into noise.","sonnet":"A song about a mutual, dreamlike love where two people are mirror images of each other — the lyric voice shifts between both parties, each claiming the other is the one they want, discovering the beloved in dreams and reflections, the song suggesting that finding yourself leads you to find the other person everywhere.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~fuck-my-computer":{"haiku":"The computer becomes the only entity that truly knows the speaker, replacing human connection with algorithmic understanding that runs deeper than intimacy.","opus":"A half-joking, half-earnest confession of intimacy with a machine that knows the speaker better than any person, that says their name. It captures the lonely tenderness of a life lived through a screen, desire redirected at the one thing that truly understands.","sonnet":"A half-joking ode to digital intimacy: the speaker finds their computer knows them better than any person does, satisfying some deep need for recognition that human relationships haven't filled, the euphemism stretched to its limit with gleeful self-awareness.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~girl-edm":{"haiku":"Persistent motion and refusal to surrender to mediocrity; the speaker pushes forward despite world resistance.","opus":"A brisk pep-talk to seize the moment and fight now rather than let the chance pass by. The one route out runs straight through it, and the narrator urges taking flight before it's too late.","sonnet":"A brief pep-talk about pushing through rather than waiting — the speaker tells someone that the only exit is forward, that now is the time to fight and take flight instead of letting the moment pass; the theme is urgency over hesitation.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~hand-on-my-heart":{"opus":"Ninajirachi emerged from Australia's hyperpop-adjacent bedroom-producer scene, and the sparseness here is native to that lineage: a looping, self-produced affirmation that reads like a diary entry set to a beat. The swearing of sincere progress against total directionlessness catches a very online, mid-twenties malaise, the sound of someone insisting on growth precisely because certainty has evaporated.","sonnet":"A sparse, looping affirmation of ongoing personal growth despite feeling lost — the speaker swears sincerely that progress is being made even while acknowledging they have never felt more directionless.","src":"sonnet","fable":"Three lines on a loop, no verse and no release: a hand pressed flat as a pledge of sincerity, a claim of progress, and an admission of being more lost than ever, all cycling with the flat repetition of a producer's looped stem rather than a sung confession. The contradiction is the whole song, and Ninajirachi lets the loop hold it unresolved rather than argue it out. Progress and lostness stop being opposites and become the same restless motion heard twice, the reassuring hand on the chest becoming the tell that nobody is actually reassured.","fv":2,"fnote":"Ninajirachi wrote it right after her first New York show — the city was everything she’d been promised, she said, and she left inspired — which grounds the loop’s paradox: making progress and never so lost is exactly what a first big arrival feels like.","themes":["identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"progress and lostness cycling as the same restless motion heard twice","built":"three lines looped with no verse or release holds the contradiction structurally unresolved"},
"ninajirachi~ice-u-out":{"haiku":"Strategic coldness weaponizes affection, where the speaker creates distance to maintain power. Icing out becomes an act of love expressed through denial.","opus":"The Australian electronic producer maps a power game of desire, wanting someone yet refusing to surrender control, leaving them on read and freezing them out to keep the upper hand. Longing and dominance coexist uneasily. Glitchy hyperpop about loving on one's own terms, needing the other to chase while never quite admitting the truth.","sonnet":"A speaker who wants to be with someone uses deliberate withdrawal — leaving messages on read for hours, declining calls — as their strategy; the repeated 'I wanna be with you so I'm gonna ice you out' names the paradox directly, emotional unavailability deployed as a form of desire.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~infohazard":{"haiku":"Childhood encounter with forbidden image haunts adulthood; the speaker remains trapped by archival memory that cannot be unseen.","opus":"The lingering dread of a disturbing image seen online as a child, the headless man who surfaces in dreams and screens years later, its meaning still unresolved.","sonnet":"About being accidentally exposed to graphic content — a decapitation image — as a child online, and the way that traumatic image has continued to resurface in dreams and on screen for years after, unable to be unseen.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~ipod-touch":{"haiku":"A secret song from youth anchors identity and memory; its discovery on an old device resurrects a pivotal moment that shaped everything.","opus":"A tender nostalgia for the song that made her a producer, discovered as a preteen with a cracked iPod and a free copy of FL Studio; it captures how one secret track soundtracked school days and bus rides and quietly rewired a whole life.","sonnet":"A nostalgic ode to a secret childhood song discovered online at twelve — tied to school bus rides and late nights making music on a cracked iPod Touch — the song became the private soundtrack to growing up before the narrator even understood what it meant.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~it-s-you":{"haiku":"Desire centers on someone else's excellence; the speaker becomes invisible when that person vanishes, validating their insignificance.","opus":"The ache of being wanted for what you can give rather than who you are—others love her, but it's really this one person they want. She needs that person to lift her up even inside their lies, crying every time she glimpses the truth.","sonnet":"About being in love with someone who is in love with someone else — the speaker needs this person and keeps spinning in their lies rather than facing the truth, but keeps crying every time clarity breaks through; the emotional core is the willful, painful preference for the illusion over reality.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~london-song":{"haiku":"Digital intimacy transcends geography, two figures inhabit screens and imagination together, building connection in the space where machine and longing intersect.","opus":"Longing and world-shrinking intimacy for the terminally online, where a place never visited feels reachable through a screen and a person becomes the whole point of going. The confession that anything feels possible with a cursor and a glowing screen is both liberating and quietly lonely, love pursued from a bedroom.","sonnet":"A romantic, internet-mediated longing — the speaker has never been to London but would go there with this person, constructing imagined adventures through a screen while sitting alone in their room, finding everything possible through a computer and an internet connection.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~undo-u-4x4-mix":{"haiku":"Reversal attempts futile as the speaker seeks to undo what remains permanent. The remix distorts past into unrecognizable form.","opus":"A tense, club-ready track built around one person holding damaging knowledge about another and debating whether to use it. Images of a red stare and a swapped phone number heighten the standoff between temptation and holding back. Ninajirachi channels the danger of a held secret into driving four-on-the-floor pulse, teetering on the brink of going past the point of no return.","sonnet":"Someone holds a secret that could unravel another person entirely — \"crimson glare, new carpet, cut your hair, new number\" signals a desperate makeover to hide something — and the narrator loops between protective concern and the terror of knowing too much, asking whether they could take it too far.","src":"haiku"},
"ninajirachi~wayside":{"haiku":"Apathy and self-destruction are the default; the speaker discards all emotional anchors and crashes toward entropy with barely suppressed indifference.","opus":"A numbed, reckless drift where the narrator no longer minds if it all comes apart, having been everywhere and done everything while nobody truly knows her heart. Beneath the crash-course fatalism runs a fragile try at opening up and starting fresh. Electronic pop about tossing pain aside and the loneliness of going unknown.","sonnet":"Dissociation and indifference expressed from a car seat on a late night: wouldn't put it past me to crash, sunlight is unwelcome, sleep is preferable to waking. The speaker tries to open their heart and make a new start while admitting no one really knows them and they don't really care if the wheels fall off.","src":"haiku","fable":"Ninajirachi builds the song as a standoff between two postures that never resolve. The verses stage a drowsy fatalism, a body slumped in the driver's seat courting the palm tree, shrugging at whether the axle holds, the traveller who has been every place and now wants only to sleep through the impact. But the second movement breaks the pose, stacking active verbs, prying the chest open, attempting a fresh beginning, clawing at the bars of a self-made cage, and hurling the wreckage aside. The read sits in that gap: the affectless numbness of the verses is exposed as armor once we see how much labor the singer is secretly pouring into escape, and the stammered, thrice-repeated tenderness she guards becomes the one thing no listener is ever let close enough to reach.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"fatalistic armor exposed by the labor secretly poured into escape","built":"verse fatalism and active second movement staged as structural reveal"},
"nine-inch-nails~1-000-000":{"haiku":"Describes a state of severe numbness and disconnection, with the narrator unable to feel anything despite suicidal ideation and existential despair. The repeating refrain of emotional emptiness frames a consciousness that has shutdown completely.","opus":"A study of total detachment, where the narrator can no longer feel anything and drifts through his days at an unreachable distance. He toys with the idea of leaping off buildings or ending things with a firearm, going through the motions while questioning whether any of it ever held meaning.","sonnet":"A person consumed by numbness and spiritual emptiness describes going through the motions of life — waking up, starting again — while feeling nothing and wondering whether any of it matters. The recurring image of jumping from rooftops and feeling a million miles away captures a complete disconnection from reality and self.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is never spoken except as a unit of distance: the million-mile gap between a man and his own body, which is the only place the song's feeling actually lives. Reznor stacks the imagery of degradation and suicide flatly, without escalation, so that the repeated confession of feeling nothing lands not as a scream but as a diagnosis, the aftermath rather than the crisis. Early on The Slip, in the wake of his getting clean, it reads as the sober counterpart to the old chemical rage: the noise that used to blast outward has collapsed into anhedonia, and the track's power comes from how the closing loop of not feeling anything wears down toward silence, the mind still narrating a numbness it can no longer even perform for anyone.","fv":2,"fnote":"The record around it was a gift: written and recorded in about a month of momentum after Ghosts, posted free on nin.com under a Creative Commons license with a one-line note thanking fans for their loyal support and saying this one was on him — downloaded nearly two million times before the CD even existed.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","suicide","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"anhedonia narrated as aftermath rather than crisis, the noise collapsed inward","built":"the closing loop of not feeling wears down toward silence, the mind outlasting its own performance"},
"nine-inch-nails~10-miles-high":{"haiku":"Pursuit of escape and transcendence, wrestling with the fear of becoming what you despise and the consequences of unchecked ambition.","opus":"Built on hypnotic repetition, this Nine Inch Nails track charts a desperate climb toward some unreachable escape, the narrator no longer able to separate his truth from his own falsehoods. A vow never to become the thing he dreads collapses under the sense that he already is, ascent and self-destruction fused.","sonnet":"A vow to escape turning into something feared — the speaker has climbed high enough that truth and lies blur, and the only certainty is the relentless closing of distance toward the thing they swore they would never become. Ascent and dread collapse into each other.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~10-miles-high-remix-version":{"haiku":"Ascent toward escape becomes impossible as the speaker approaches their own truth and descent.","opus":"An obsessive push to get impossibly high and unreachable, both to escape and to avoid becoming the person the narrator swore never to turn into. Drawing nearer by the minute cuts both ways, toward the high and toward that dreaded self.","sonnet":"An attempt to ascend beyond contamination — ten miles high, far enough that the nightmare stays outside — but the closer the narrator gets the more the fear of becoming the person they swore they'd never be presses in. The turn inward ('I made it ten miles high, can't tell my truth from my lies') makes the height meaningless.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~ahead-of-ourselves":{"haiku":"Humanity overestimates its intellect while remaining primitive and greedy, congratulating itself for savagery dressed as enlightenment.","opus":"Reznor's late-career contempt for a species that squandered every chance and chose willful stupidity and self-harm instead. The jeering hook mocks humanity's belief in its own progress and cleverness even as it wallows in the mess it built, powerless to behave any better.","sonnet":"A scathing indictment of human civilization as a failed experiment — all of our self-congratulation about enlightenment masks the fact that we are still primitive animals, and the world we built reflects exactly what we deserve.","src":"haiku","fable":"A furious indictment of a species that mistook its cleverness for divinity and squandered every possibility on ruin. The opening flatters a maker, then quietly withdraws belief, leaving the boast standing over nothing. The chorus reads back a verdict on ourselves: obsolete, ignorant, incapable of change and half-proud of it. Beneath the swagger crouches the real portrait, a beast rooting in filth while convinced it had already ascended above the mud. The looped title lands not as triumph but as epitaph, a thing that raced past its own worth and arrived nowhere. On late Reznor, that self-loathing has widened from the personal wound to the civilizational one.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"civilisational self-loathing widened from the personal wound","built":"the looped title lands as epitaph, a race past its own worth arriving nowhere"},
"nine-inch-nails~all-the-love-in-the-world":{"haiku":"Invisible and alone despite being surrounded; one person receives all love while the narrator remains unseen and forgotten.","opus":"Alienation and jealousy: the speaker feels invisible in a crowd, unheard, and stuck in the past, yet brightened when a certain person is near, repeatedly demanding to know why that person gets all the love in the world.","sonnet":"A lonely outsider watches others move with purpose and belonging, feeling invisible and unheard, and asks with mounting anguish why connection and warmth seem to flow to everyone else while leaving them stranded.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verse-world is claustrophobic and unheard: a smudged reflection in polished metal, a self dissolving even as it names its own distance, the small dignity of creatures that have a settled place while the speaker has none. Then the machinery of the chorus takes over and refuses to develop, hammering one envious, childlike demand about why affection flows toward someone else and never toward the singer, until repetition stops asking and starts corroding into a hollow, sung-out mantra. The mask is grievance; underneath it is the terror of being fundamentally unlovable, and Reznor lets the sheer count of repetitions do the confessing that the two guarded verses could not. As the record's disarmingly bright opener on With Teeth, the song weaponizes prettiness, its major-key warmth curdling the more the question loops, so the loveliest melody on the album is also its loneliest accusation.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"repetition does the confessing the guarded verses could not","built":"the album's brightest melody is also its loneliest accusation"},
"nine-inch-nails~all-the-pigs-all-lined-up": {"haiku":"Systems of greed and corruption are exposed and dismantled; the powerful are lined up for destruction as the speaker revels in witnessing their collapse.","opus":"Reznor's sneer at the appetite for spectacle and destruction, mocking the crowd that lines up to watch someone be torn down and skinned, exposing how tearing others apart is sold as making yourself feel better.","sonnet":"About watching a corrupt system or person unravel — the narrator wants to see it all come down, picking apart something rotten piece by piece, the visceral imagery of peeled skin and fed pigs suggesting both systemic critique and personal revenge fantasy.","src":"haiku","fable":"A carnival barker's come-on that curdles into a feeding frenzy: the crowd is marched up on its knees to consume something, and the pig-image runs both ways at once — the lined-up pigs are the audience herded to the trough and the sacrifice laid out to be skinned, so devouring and being devoured collapse into one act. Reznor pitches it as the ecstasy of tearing a public figure down, the appetite that watches something come apart and calls the wreckage relief. The song's cruelty is in its refrain, which asks over and over whether the ruin has made you feel better — a question turned back on the listener until the sheer repetition becomes the answer, the itch scratched raw. Fitting the era around The Downward Spiral, the self-disgust of that record here gets aimed outward at the mob, though the accusation clearly implicates the voice doing the goading too.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","politics & society","shame & repression"],"means":"the mob's appetite for ruin turned back on whoever is watching","built":"the refrain's sheer repetition becomes the answer it pretends only to ask"},
"nine-inch-nails~all-time-low":{"haiku":"A speaker has reached their lowest point, hearing echoes and paranoia. Passengers arrive and the speaker knows they're descending further into consumption and despair.","opus":"Late Reznor pairing menace with a strange transcendent tenderness, a narrator at rock bottom braced for something worse yet promising an eternal, sky-stretching escape with someone; dread and doomed devotion fused into a vow to never die.","sonnet":"A paranoid, escalating collapse — someone let dangerous forces in, the situation has hit rock bottom, and the narrator oscillates between frantic reassurance that they'll live forever and the knowledge that they're already broken.","src":"haiku","fable":"A deathward slide dressed in the grammar of an undying-love vow, where every promise of permanence is really the addict's bargain with his own ruin. The choruses keep swearing endlessness and a shared flight into the heavens, but the verses undercut them: something has already worsened past repair, unnamed arrivals have moved in and won't leave, and suspicion curdles into fear. On Reznor's clean-and-sober Hesitation Marks, the sworn oath and the wreckage share one breath, so the pledge to outlast time reads as denial rather than triumph. The skyward image the outro chants into oblivion is the very thing the title has already contradicted, until the vow decays into pure repetition, a comfort worn smooth of meaning.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","freedom & escape"],"means":"sworn oath of permanence that the title already contradicts","built":"skyward vow chanted to oblivion until worn smooth of meaning"},
"nine-inch-nails~all-time-low-todd-rundgren-remix":{"haiku":"Paranoia and despair consume consciousness as reality warps into an apocalyptic nightmare where the desperate cling to false promises of transcendence.","opus":"From Hesitation Marks, a portrait of relapse and hitting bottom, echoing voices and creeping paranoia framed against a hollow promise of living forever. Reznor sketches the seductive lie of a high that will last, addiction's siren whispering immortality while everything falls apart. This remix stretches that unease across a glassy, uneasy groove.","sonnet":"A paranoid descent fueled by an unnamed infiltration — 'you barely even pierced the skin, just wait 'til you see what has come in' — where the narrator oscillates between collapse and a desperate, almost delusional promise of immortality, stretching across the sky together even as everything falls apart.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~and-all-that-could-have-been":{"haiku":"Someone taints themselves through past actions and warns a loved one to flee before they drag them down, leaving only traces of what could have been.","opus":"A ghostly goodbye from someone who feels too damaged to deserve the love he's given, urging the other to flee and save themselves. The deeper current is depressive self-erasure, the narrator vanishing without a trace, sealed inside his own guilt, grieving that in his hollowness she was his whole world, now irretrievably lost.","sonnet":"Someone pushing away a person they love because they consider themselves tainted, broken, unfit for happiness — urging them to run while leaving behind only the ghost of what might have been.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~apart":{"haiku":"Presence carved as rupture, feeling itself becomes the wound that marks separation.","opus":"A stark exchange of hurt reduced to its rawest form, a woman's voice insisting that now he finally understands what the pain feels like, met by Reznor's anguished echo. An early NIN fragment about the cruel symmetry of suffering, the moment betrayal comes full circle and one person learns exactly what they inflicted.","sonnet":"Two voices — a woman and a man — pass the same experience back and forth: \"this is what it feels like,\" suggesting shared pain or mutual wounding conveyed through call and response.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~as-alive-as-you-need-me-to-be":{"haiku":"Infection spreads through connection while doubt erodes. Belief demands hands holding—only that gesture keeps alive.","opus":"A bond experienced as infection and compulsion, something devouring the gap between two people and plugging an inner void. The singer exists only to the degree the other requires him to, desperate for something to believe in, with no way to reverse course.","sonnet":"About surrendering selfhood to an overwhelming connection that feels like infection — the narrator has no choice in it, is held by countless hands, and asks only for something to believe in; being alive is something they perform for whoever needs it rather than something they own.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~beside-you-in-time":{"haiku":"Separation and reunion across parallel spaces, as one person fades away from existence but promises reunion with another.","opus":"A meditation on separation and reunion, the narrator sensing a loved one in overlapping worlds as he chooses to slip away himself. He offers a promise of permanence and togetherness that outlasts death, set against a hypnotic, endlessly cycling chant.","sonnet":"A speaker who has decided not to stay — likely facing death or departure — finds peace in the idea of fading away and being reunited with someone in a state that transcends time, framed as permanence rather than loss.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dissolution song that mistakes its own erasure for a reunion. The two thin verses barely hold a body together: a narrator, isolated, hears a companion leak in from some adjacent room, then elects to thin himself out until he vanishes, tidying each object back onto its shelf on the way toward nonexistence. Everything between and after those verses is the actual content, that hypnotic tally of one syllable-pair stacked to the horizon, less a chorus than a seized engine of continuation, machinery that enacts endlessness instead of describing it. Arriving at the point on With Teeth where Reznor's addiction wreckage was meant to be behind him, the benediction chills rather than warms: the only closeness on offer is the version where both parties have already stopped existing, so eternity becomes not romance but oblivion dressed as devotion, a flat mechanical comfort repeating past the point where anyone is left to receive it.","fv":2,"fnote":"The album was called Bleedthrough until late 2004 — layers of reality seeping into one another, a concept Reznor dropped partly because people kept hearing blood — and this verse’s parallel places leaking little pieces is where the discarded title still lives in the record.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire","death & grief"],"means":"eternity offered as oblivion dressed in the language of devotion","built":"the hypnotic one-syllable chant enacts endlessness rather than describing it"},
"nine-inch-nails~big-man-with-a-gun":{"fable":"Reznor collapses gun, phallus, and ego into a single instrument of domination, ventriloquizing a predator whose entire selfhood is the threat he can deliver at gunpoint. The satire lives in the collapse: firing, ejaculation, and annihilation become the same act, so the swaggering boast exposes itself as impotence dressed up as power, masculine potency indistinguishable from the wish to erase another person. Its notoriety on The Downward Spiral is inseparable from the joke Reznor is playing on the male id.","fableDeep":"A persona exercise run at maximum crudity: nursery-simple boasts stack up in first person, and the structural conflation is total — firearm, phallus, and self treated as one interchangeable tool, so every threat operates simultaneously as sexual coercion and execution, dominance requiring a body to aim at. The verses put the weapon against another person's head and frame both violation and killing as recreation — the casualness is the satire's blade, cruelty offered as fun. The middle section reduces climax to gunfire, a repeated shooting chant merged with a repeated sexual promise: intimacy rewritten as ballistics, the two vocabularies revealed as one. The borrowed mantra that nothing can stop him now ties the grotesque to the album's through-line — here is where the spiral's liberated indifference terminates, in armed dominion over someone else's body. The outro strips even the bragging away until only man-plus-gun remains, identity contracted to the owned instrument. Thesis: masculinity as a feedback loop where potency must be proven by harm, shrinking the man to the weapon in his hand.","haiku":"A speaker uses a gun as a symbol of domination and sexual power. The song explores toxic masculinity, coercion, and the merging of violence with sexual aggression.","opus":"A deliberately grotesque satire of macho power fantasy, in which masculinity, sexual violence and firearm collapse into one: the narrator brags about his big gun and dick, forces oral sex at gunpoint, threatens to shoot someone in the head for fun, and equates ejaculation with gunfire. It exposes the aggression and hollow bravado underlying that fusion of virility and lethal force.","sonnet":"A brutal satire of masculine aggression and gun culture, where the narrator performs an escalating fantasy of sexual and lethal domination that collapses the boundary between gun violence and rape into the same expression of power.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"masculine potency indistinguishable from the wish to erase another person","built":"the structural conflation of firearm, phallus, and self is total — every threat operates simultaneously as coercion and execution"},
"nine-inch-nails~branches-bones": {"fable":"A body dissociating from itself: the mouth stitched over yet still able to see out, the sped-up clock, the crawling insects — Reznor renders a mind coming apart as sensory horror, and the tourist warning names the trap plainly, that curiosity carries you somewhere you cannot walk back out of. The chorus is the real payload, a numbing loop from recognition to indifference to the flat refusal to care, each pass sanding off more feeling until the words themselves stutter and snap mid-syllable. In the second verse the concealment images stack — cosmetics laid over a wound, hands waving off the flies — before the title fuses a woman's limbs to a fracture that straightens into something clean, damage reimagined as design. Fittingly, the trailing text decays into a stammering loop of the same déjà-vu phrases, so the track finally enacts its own subject: consciousness caught skipping, pleading only to be let go.","fableDeep":"The song performs its subject — dissolution — formally. Early images seal the system: making out pieces 'with the opening sewed shut', and the tourist warning that you 'can't understand what they have planned until you try to leave', which turns casual visitation of a scene or a state of mind into a trap discovered only at the exit. The chorus runs recognition down into resignation in three steps: 'feels like I've been here before', then 'I don't know anymore', then 'I don't care anymore', déjà vu decaying into apathy, the last repetition clipped mid-word like a cut transmission. Then the extraordinary coda: the sentences shred into recombining fragments — phrases about actual events, about what 'I thought mattered', about 'finding order' — looping out of syntax entirely, grammar snapping like the title's equation of living wood and skeleton, branches as 'the bones that break'. Growth and structure fail the same way. It is about the moment self-knowledge collapses: you recognize exactly where you are but can no longer assemble the sentence that would get you out.","haiku":"Repetitive cycle of being trapped and erased, unable to escape sensations of decay and the familiar dread of a place once visited before.","opus":"A disoriented, decaying state of mind where the narrator has been here before, no longer knows or cares, and his sense of self and memory fall apart. The lyrics dissolve into fractured, looping fragments about whether his thoughts and past ever really mattered, ending with a plea for everyone to go away.","sonnet":"A fragmented meditation on dissociation and loss of self: the speaker recognises that what he thought mattered has been shown to be false, that patterns he used to find comfort in no longer hold, and he just wants everyone to go away and leave him to his unravelling.","src":"haiku","fable2":"A body coming apart narrates its own numbing, until the numbness itself starts speaking in a crowd of overlapping voices. The verses map decay onto scenery: stitched openings, hidden bruises, a snapping limb reshaped into something clean and inhuman. The refrain answers with flat refusal, a narrator who has stopped caring on purpose because caring is the last exposed nerve. Then the stacked backing vocals take over, a pile of selves circling the same few anxieties, memory, order, whether anything ever mattered, whether the self can trust its own account. That murmuring throng is the point. Underneath the practiced indifference sits a mind arguing about what was ever real, thinning at the close to one small repeated plea to be let out of here.","fnote":"The EP arrived with a literal warning: its mail-order physical component was an envelope of card inserts coated in loose black powder, shipped under a waiver promising subversive elements, unrealized expectations, and — verbatim — that this will make a mess.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","madness & the mind","shame & repression"],"means":"curiosity named as the trap that carries you somewhere you cannot leave","built":"the trailing text decays into a stuttering loop, the track enacting its own subject"},
"nine-inch-nails~burn":{"haiku":"Expresses rage at being rejected by the world and a desire to burn it down from within, positioning the narrator as a destructive force both angelic and demonic. The song frames destruction and subversion as revenge against a system that never accepted him.","opus":"A spurned outsider who feels discarded by everything around him swears revenge, styling himself as a disease inside society and an agent of ruin. He describes a fury stirring awake within him and a craving to reduce all of existence to ashes.","sonnet":"A figure who feels cast out and destroyed by society awakens to a consuming rage, declaring themselves both destroyer and savior, and vowing to burn the entire world down in retribution for a lifetime of rejection.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rejection song that hardens into arson, where the outcast quits arguing his innocence and starts relishing what being nobody's property lets him do. Reznor's turn is that the thing stirring awake inside him isn't purity but the appetite the system installed and then pretended not to recognize. The closing reprise is the real charge: he gathers every slur used to expel him — savior and rot, angel and ruin, the craving no one would name — until they fuse into one figure who is simply the future, and that future arrives as flame.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written and produced for Natural Born Killers in 1994 — Reznor assembled the film's whole collage soundtrack for Oliver Stone, and the video puts him in front of the movie's footage. It only reached an album a decade later, as a bonus on The Downward Spiral's deluxe reissue.","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the outcast fusing every slur used to expel him into one figure who is simply the future","built":"the closing reprise gathers enemy vocabulary and returns it as arson"},
"nine-inch-nails~burn-from-natural-born-killers-soundtrack":{"haiku":"Society rejects the individual as an abomination, and rage ignites—burning the entire corrupt system that created this alienation.","opus":"Written for Oliver Stone's Natural Born Killers, a rejection of society voiced by a castoff now vowing apocalyptic revenge. Something within him has awakened and howls to raze it all — Reznor turns alienation into a prophetic threat, the outcast as the world's angel of destruction.","sonnet":"Rage from the margin addressed directly at the institutions that rejected and discarded the speaker — a cancer in their system, a surprise they failed to account for. The speaker refuses membership ('I never was a part of you') and positions themselves as both saviour and destroyer, angel and perversion, the future swallowed in fire.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~burning-bright-field-on-fire":{"haiku":"Acceptance of ones true self and liberation through destruction, emerging purified and stronger.","opus":"A surrender to one's own ruinous instincts, gasping back to life and claiming absolution and liberation, before that supposed rebirth swells into apocalyptic grandiosity as the speaker swears his destruction will engulf everything and loses any grip on what's real.","sonnet":"A cathartic reckoning with self-deception and collapse — the narrator admits he knew all along what he truly was, dismantles the fragile life he built from scraps, and arrives at violent liberation through total destruction of his old self, emerging not healed but ablaze and free.","src":"haiku","web":"A purging self-reckoning — plague and carrion and fire — stripping away every false self to arrive at the raw truth: 'I am forgiven, I am free, I am a field on fire'; destruction as the only honest renewal.","fable":"A man burns himself down to what he takes for his truest nature and mistakes the blaze for grace, until the cleansing pyre curdles into scorched-earth vengeance on the world that shamed him. The verses stage a homecoming to a ruined, cobbled-together self, prodded there by a voice he half-believes; the breath-chant then hauls him up through the surface into a chorus equating absolution, liberation, and standing as a field on fire. What began as purification metastasizes, the flames now feeding a carcass, breeding flies and locusts, blackening out everyone alive. The I-am mantra climbs even as its meaning rots, and the closing dissolve of dream and waking admits he can no longer tell rapture from delusion. Fitting that this shuts the EP: transcendence and self-destruction burning as one light.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","madness & the mind"],"means":"purification metastasizes into scorched-earth vengeance as the mantra climbs","built":"closing dissolve of dream and waking admits the narrator can no longer tell rapture from delusion","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~came-back-haunted":{"haiku":"A return from the supernatural realm with an alien entity lodged inside the speaker, bringing haunting and transformation that leaves them changed and unable to escape the consequences.","opus":"The narrator crosses into some hidden realm, sees things there, and returns fundamentally altered, convinced something has been implanted in him and that his old identity is gone. He braces for a reckoning over what he did and feels powerless to hold himself back.","sonnet":"A narrator crosses into some forbidden or dangerous territory, witnesses something that changes him irrevocably, and returns feeling as though something has been implanted or taken over inside him. Despite warnings, he could not stop himself, and now he cannot go back to who he was.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man crosses into a place he was warned to leave alone, and whatever he meets there rides home lodged inside his own body. That is the horror engine of Reznor's 2013 comeback single: return is never clean, and the self that comes back is not the self that departed. He admits he had to go look even knowing the price, so the ruin reads as self-chosen and permanent, a threshold he opened on purpose. The outro strips the track to one loop of refusal that keeps restarting, staging the compulsion as form rather than naming it, a groove that will not release him. Heard against a five-year silence, the possession doubles as a portrait of the returning artist, hauled back and unable to quit the very thing that wrecked him.","fv":2,"fnote":"The video is David Lynch’s — reuniting him with Reznor sixteen years after Lost Highway — and it shipped behind a formal warning after Epilepsy Action flagged its strobing as a seizure risk: the comeback single arrived literally labeled hazardous to watch.","themes":["identity & becoming","faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"a self-chosen threshold opening a ruin that rides home in the body","built":"the outro loops one refusal restarting, staging compulsion as form rather than naming it"},
"nine-inch-nails~capital-g":{"haiku":"Greed and corruption overtake moral conviction when power is attained, leading to rationalization and indifference to suffering.","opus":"Reznor ventriloquizes a smug ruling-class voice that trades conscience for power and dresses greed up as personal accountability. Written in the Bush years, the capital G stands not for God but for greed, and the sting is the admission that this voice lives in the listener too.","sonnet":"A first-person confession from a figure who has abandoned principle for self-interest and power — indifferent to war, inequality, and environmental destruction — revealing that greed (the capital G) is the new god he now serves.","src":"haiku","fable":"This Year Zero track is a swaggering monologue in the voice of a moneyed power broker who has swapped his old faith for a new deity whose initial is a capital G that turns out to be Greed. The verses stack his sneers into a worldview: he bought the election, shrugs off war and a warming planet, and mistakes your objection for envy of his seat. Beneath the bravado a confession leaks, that whatever principle once held him upright has been pawned and forgotten, and he ends kneeling rather than towering. The chorus rounds on the listener, insisting a shard of this same appetite lives in you and that you look away from it. So the swagger becomes an accusation and the chanted refrain a national anthem the song dares you to claim as your own.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","money & the street","artifice & performance"],"means":"swagger becoming an accusation aimed at the listener's own appetite","built":"the capital G title punches greed into god's slot without announcing the swap"},
"nine-inch-nails~closer":{"fable":"Reznor's most notorious hook is a trap: the animal chorus reads as pure carnality, but the pre-choruses give it away, framing sex as a drug taken not out of desire but to escape a self the singer finds unbearable. The partner is offered everything precisely because it is worthless to him — his solitude, the venom that comes with it, his hollowed-out belief — a gift of debris from someone with nothing left. What sounds like blasphemy is really a broken theology: with nothing spiritual left to barter and no conviction intact, the body of another person becomes the only remaining route to transcendence, which is why the divine and the degrading are named in the same breath. The gentle outro, all honey and hives and one last reason not to disappear, exposes the ache the aggression was armoring: this is a suicidal person using someone else's body to keep from vanishing.","fableDeep":"The opening quatrain makes consent itself the litany — four lines of 'you let me,' escalating through violation and desecration: sacrilege verbs, the body as church, permission eroticized. Every boast is undercut by the parenthetical plea for help, and the actual petitions are the tell — get away from myself, become somebody else — sex as self-erasure, not conquest. The infamous chorus works by category error: obscenity and theology jammed into one sentence, and the shock is less the profanity than the non sequitur it detonates — a confession of flawed existence resolving into the claim that this act approaches God, transcendence sought through the animal precisely because the human self has failed. The offered gifts are all negations — isolation, the hate it brings, absence of faith — a dowry of emptiness. Then the final verse turns pastoral: forests, honey, the hive, and the plain statement that she is the reason he stays alive, dependence stripped of menace. Thesis: a psalm from someone who can only pray through debasement, desire doing the work faith used to.","haiku":"Raw sexual desire and using another person to approach transcendence.","opus":"Obsessive, self-loathing sexual desire, using degrading sex with another to escape oneself and feel closer to God.","sonnet":"Using raw, animalistic sex to escape self-loathing and a flawed existence, seeking transcendence through physical union.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","faith & the occult","suicide"],"means":"sex used as self-erasure by someone with no other route to transcendence","built":"obscenity and theology jammed into one sentence, the shock in the non sequitur rather than the profanity","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~closer-to-god": {"haiku":"One person violates and consumes another, breaking their own soul while desiring total fusion with the object of their destructive need.","opus":"Reznor's notorious equation of sex with worship and self-loathing, degradation as the only route to feeling divine. Beneath the profane refrain is spiritual emptiness, a person with no soul to sell seeking obliteration in another body.","sonnet":"Raw sexual and existential possession — the narrator surrenders isolation, faithlessness, and his entire inner life to a sexual partner, framing the merger of two people through explicit desire as the only thing that works for him.","src":"haiku","fable":"Reznor's rework of Closer keeps the same devastating equation — that self-annihilation through another body is the only remaining channel to something like God — but foregrounds the animal refrain until it becomes the governing frame rather than a texture, recasting the whole plea as a creature's compulsion instead of a soul's ascent. The verses run two directions at once: the speaker is the violator listing what the other permits, then the supplicant handing over his isolation, the hate that isolation brings, and whatever is left after belief has drained out, so that being made perfect and being torn down are the same act. What reads as blasphemy is really abjection dressed as worship — the mask of dominance over a need to be dissolved, to disappear into the other because there is no soul left to sell.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"abjection wearing the mask of dominance over the beloved","built":"the animal refrain promoted from texture to governing frame reshapes the whole register"},
"nine-inch-nails~copy-of-a":{"haiku":"An examination of identity as derivative and assembled from fragments. The speaker feels like a shadow and echo, controlled and hollow, a copy without original agency.","opus":"An identity crisis rendered as endless repetition, with the speaker describing himself as merely a copy of a copy, an echo and a shadow, assembled from borrowed pieces and doing only what he is told.","sonnet":"A crisis of identity and authenticity, where the speaker sees himself as nothing but a degraded copy of copies — assembled from borrowed pieces, obediently following instructions, with no original self remaining.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession of manufactured selfhood: the narrator understands himself as a duplicate several generations removed from any lost source, assembled from borrowed parts and haunted by the suspicion that nothing in him is first-hand. The lyric enacts its own thesis, each self-image reduplicating until identity degrades like a photocopy fed its own output, the derivation compounding toward noise. That someone offstage keeps issuing accusations and instructions frames the voice as both product and instrument, obediently executing a purpose it never authored. Returning in 2013 after his own long silence, Reznor turns the old NIN wound of the violated self outward into an anxiety about mass reproduction, where being one among identical many is the final admission and no untouched original was ever there to reproduce.","fv":2,"fnote":"The album’s name is the forensic term for the tentative cuts found beside a suicide wound, and its four variant covers were painted by Russell Mills — the same artist as The Downward Spiral’s, rehired as what Reznor called a conscious trail of breadcrumbs — working in earth, rusted linen, copper wire and actual blood.","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","surveillance & control"],"means":"selfhood as a photocopy of its own output, degrading toward noise","built":"the lyric enacts its thesis as each self-image reduplicates into noise"},
"nine-inch-nails~dead-souls": {"haiku":"Haunting voices from history—conquerors, imperialists, and oppressors—call relentlessly to the narrator, blurring past and present through nightmares. The repetition traps the narrator in a cycle of spectral possession.","opus":"A cover haunted by dreams and figures from the past, mocking voices and conquistadors, in which relentless dead souls keep calling to the speaker across blurred realities.","sonnet":"Haunted by voices from the past — figures of imperial conquest and bloodshed — the narrator cannot silence the dead souls that keep calling to them across time.","src":"haiku","fable":"Ian Curtis wrote a haunting: the dead are not gone but audible, summoning a living man who begs to be freed from the visions dragging him toward some other day where his sense of self splits and reality warps out of true. The second verse widens the private torment into inherited history — ancestral figures looming, cruelty dressed in the robes of empire and worship, plunderers claiming their cut — so the voices calling him are also the accumulated dead of conquest, guilt speaking through the bloodline. Reznor leaves Curtis's words untouched and lets the arrangement do the recasting: the chorus is nothing but the summons repeated until it erodes into a whisper and swells back, the industrial build turning being called into being worn down. Placing it on The Crow soundtrack — a story of a murdered man dragged back from death to walk among the living — makes the cover a knowing act of curation, one dead soul answering another's call across two decades.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","madness & the mind"],"means":"being called by the dead until the calling wears the caller down","built":"the industrial build turns a summons repeated until it erodes into a whisper"},
"nine-inch-nails~dead-souls-the-crow-soundtrack":{"haiku":"The past's voices haunt consciousness, mocking and imperious, refusing to release their grip on the present.","opus":"Reznor's cover of the Joy Division song, made for The Crow, a haunted meditation on being called by the dead and the ghosts of one's own past. Figures from history mock and beckon while two selves stretch reality apart. The gothic dread of the original deepened into a soundtrack for grief, memory, and inescapable summons.","sonnet":"Haunted by voices from the past — imperialistic figures mocking from their conquered halls, conquistadors, dreams pointing toward another day. The speaker tries to escape but the dead keep calling, insistent and unanswerable, the pull of history refusing to let the self go forward.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~dear-world": {"fable":"A dissolution nobody clocked in time: the speaker narrates his own erasure as a slow subtraction, the sensation of being rendered outdated the way an image is stripped one still at a time. The chorus turns confinement into a haunted gallery — shut in among portraits and absent company, he repeats the same three-word insistence until it stops meaning arrival and starts sounding like surrender. Reznor then splits the track in stereo for the second verse, one channel writing an actual valediction to the planet while the other keeps disintegrating, so the tender goodbye letter and the collapsing narrator run at once and drown each other out. It reads as Not the Actual Events' governing dread made personal: a self going dark not by catastrophe but by attrition, still certain an answer waits out there yet no longer trusting the person left to go looking for it. The bookend — every listener supposedly unconscious — frames the whole thing as a transmission sent to a world too sedated to receive it.","fableDeep":"The structure is a letter buried inside a loop. The sung frame is claustrophobic domesticity — 'I'm locked inside here' with 'people who aren't here', presence and absence sharing one room, company reduced to 'pictures and faces on display'. The verse's key measurement is cinematic: 'we become obsolete / a frame at a time', decline too gradual to perceive, which feeds the song's obsession — you didn't even notice recurs like an accusation aimed at the world, the listener, and probably the self. The spoken letter, split across stereo channels so it is literally hard to hear — the form enacting the unheard farewell — supplies the logic: 'I can hardly recognize you anymore / because I can't recognize who I have been', estrangement from the world traced back to estrangement from self, and yet the writer still insists there is an answer in the world he is quitting, departure with residual faith. The bookend observation that everyone seems to be asleep closes the loop. It is about leaving a world that changed exactly as much as you did, and finding that nobody registers either loss.","haiku":"Disconnection and obsolescence isolate the speaker among images of absent people, trapped watching the world change beyond recognition.","opus":"An address from someone slipping away as the world grows unfamiliar and obsolete a frame at a time, locked in among absent people and losing the ability to concentrate or trust himself, sensing an unnoticed ending arriving.","sonnet":"A fractured farewell addressed to a world the narrator can no longer recognise — locked inside with photographs of absent people, watching a trajectory in decline and himself becoming obsolete — the embedded letter-fragment making explicit that this is a leave-taking driven by an inability to concentrate or trust himself any further.","src":"haiku","fable2":"A consciousness narrating its own erosion drafts a goodbye letter to reality itself, addressing the outside as a stranger while insisting the remedy lives out there rather than in the self it can no longer trust. The song enacts the split it describes: the second verse divides across the stereo field into two channels, staging the fracture as sound before stating it as meaning. Around this sits a chorus of confinement among absent company, the speaker held captive by displayed likenesses with no living presence. The whole track is sealed at both ends by one flat verdict that the surrounding world lies unconscious, and decay arrives incrementally, one degraded increment after another, so obsolescence feels less like an event than a slow subtraction of the person speaking.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","death & grief"],"means":"a self going dark by slow attrition, still certain an answer waits out there","built":"stereo split runs valediction and collapse simultaneously until each drowns the other"},
"nine-inch-nails~deep":{"haiku":"Desire pulls the speaker deeper into darkness where pleasure and pain merge into addiction.","opus":"Reznor's yearning to break through numbness and merge completely with another person, two becoming one to escape a hollow emptiness; the desperation is that only total fusion promises to make him feel real again.","sonnet":"A pure want for merger — driving deeper into another person to reach something that restores what was lost, the part of the self that went missing. The blank stare and big black hole at the opening give way to a focused erotic pursuit, the body speaking what lips can't say, two becoming one as the only available escape from the void.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~deep-radio-edit":{"haiku":"Sexual and emotional immersion; the speaker wants to penetrate another's depths and be penetrated in return, seeking union and losing control through intimate invasion.","opus":"Reznor reaching to fill a black hole of numbness by dissolving into another person, sex as the only way to feel real and whole again. The wish to become two-into-one is less romance than a desperate escape from the void inside.","sonnet":"Sexual and emotional longing collapsed into a single drive — the narrator wants to dissolve into another person, to escape his own void through physical and psychological intimacy, merging two into one as a form of escape from emptiness.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~demon-seed":{"haiku":"A destructive inner force growing unstoppable; the singer tries to contain and tolerate it but reaches a breaking point, vowing to use voice and fists to destroy everything.","opus":"Reznor externalizes a growing, breathing compulsion he can no longer contain, a seed of destruction inside him that he finally recognizes and embraces, self-knowledge tipping into the urge to tear everything apart.","sonnet":"A song about a growing inner rage that the narrator has been trying to suppress and can no longer tolerate — the seed inside him has been building for too long, and he reaches the point of wanting to destroy everything around him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man narrates his own possession as a pregnancy, tracking the thing inside him from first stirring to full self-recognition. It arrives in four gestational stages: a growing, breathing presence he keeps politely restraining; a counted climb toward a breaking threshold; a surrender to wrecking whatever is in reach by voice and by hand; and a calm, chanted certainty about what he has become. Closing the vocal side of The Slip, it strips the metaphor to a single admission. The monstrousness was never external but seeded within him, and the only growth left is its birth.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","violence & murder","madness & the mind"],"means":"monstrousness tracked from first stirring to calm self-recognition","built":"four gestational stages structure a possession narrated as a pregnancy"},
"nine-inch-nails~disappointed":{"haiku":"A cynical narrator warns listeners not to trust them, since nothing ever changes and everyone's expectations will be disappointed. They position themselves as part of the reason for inevitable letdown and failed hope.","opus":"The speaker tells someone their trust is misplaced and that nothing about him or their situation will ever improve. He notes how let down and desperate to flee they've become, and admits, almost casually, that he shares the blame for their misery.","sonnet":"The narrator warns that nothing will change and that they are partly to blame, addressing someone who has held idealistic expectations — a rescuer figure — only to be let down by reality again and again.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a betrayer warning his victim not to trust him, then confessing he is one of the reasons nothing will ever get better. The song sets up a rescuer figure, the caped ideal burdened with saving everyone, only to let that grandeur sag into futility once the speaker admits his own hand in the wreckage. The accusation curls back into self-portrait, since the mocking voice and the exhausted hero read as two halves of one divided head. On Hesitation Marks, Reznor's clean-and-sober record about resisting relapse, the disappearing the chorus keeps circling is less an escape than the addict's undertow, and the contempt is aimed squarely at the man in the mirror who can never be trusted to change.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"the betrayer warns his victim not to trust him, then admits he caused the wreckage","built":"the rescuer figure and the mocking voice read as two halves of one divided head"},
"nine-inch-nails~discipline":{"fable":"A safe-word plea for a dominant's control that keeps confessing itself as an addict's surrender, the erotic ritual only a costume for the compulsion beneath it. The opening interrogates a hardness that is quietly giving way, testing whether the old cruelty and resolve still hold their edge, and the answer arrives as erosion. What begins as a request to be governed slides into the failure to govern anything: the boundary between his body and the other person dissolves until he can no longer say where either of them stops. The refrain gives the real subject away, because it can be steered no more than he can, looping the vow of helplessness until the appeal for someone else's rules and the craving those rules were meant to cage become the same sound.","fableDeep":"It opens with self-doubt phrased as decline — 'is my viciousness losing ground?' — the strange fear of an addict whose destructiveness was his identity and is now wearing out too. The chorus outsources willpower: discipline is requested, not imposed, which makes submission a form of self-knowledge. The mark left 'up and down my skin' is both intimacy and enforcement, and the confession 'I don't know where I end and where you begin' names the price of the arrangement: to be stopped by someone is to dissolve into them.","haiku":"The speaker needs external discipline and control from a partner because internal self-control has worn away and they cannot stop their own destructive impulses.","opus":"A song about craving control and structure from another person, where the speaker admits he cannot stop himself once he starts and needs discipline, help, and clearly defined limits, blurring where he ends and the other begins.","sonnet":"The speaker craves external restraint from another person because, once a compulsive pattern starts, they are unable to stop themselves — the song maps a loss of self-control and a dependent need for someone else to impose limits.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"submission requested rather than imposed turns helplessness into self-knowledge","built":"the refrain loops the vow until erotic surrender and compulsive craving become the same sound","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~down-in-it":{"haiku":"Once elevated and free, the narrator descends into despair and darkness, becoming what he despises and losing his former self.","opus":"A fall from a high, invincible feeling into rock bottom, as the speaker who once felt like somebody is now hollowed out and dragged down, the joke on him; imagery of a needle and the world's weight underscores the collapse.","sonnet":"A fall from confident self-possession into emptiness — the narrator recalls floating above it all until a tiny pull dragged him down, and now he mourns a former sense of identity and strength that has completely dissolved.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grandiosity high crashes into addiction and the erasure of the self that once felt invincible, tracked as one fall from above to beneath. The spine is a spatial pun: he narrates from cloud-height, a speck too small to see snags his eye and drags him earthward, and the chorus can only pivot between having risen over something and now being sunk under it. The bridge takes the loss in the past tense, an inventory of strength, certainty and identity now hollowed into a single open wound. What makes the collapse total is the ending, where the wrecked adult reaches past language for a child's weather-charm, a helpless singsong begging the storm to postpone itself because no grown sentence is left to say.","fv":2,"fnote":"The first song Reznor ever wrote — he later admitted it was a total rip-off of Skinny Puppy’s Dig It — and the one that had the FBI investigating his murder: the video shoot’s balloon-rigged camera broke loose over Chicago, drifted two hundred miles to a Michigan farm field, and the recovered footage of a cornstarch-dusted corpse was worked as a possible snuff film for two years, until an art student recognized the scene on MTV.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"a fall from grandiosity to hollow wound tracked as one spatial drop","built":"the wrecked adult reaching past language for a child's weather-charm marks the collapse as total"},
"nine-inch-nails~echoplex":{"haiku":"Emotional isolation and psychological withdrawal from the world.","opus":"Retreating into an isolated inner fortress rebuilt from a broken self, feeling safe, detached and untouchable from others' torment.","sonnet":"Retreating into an interior fortress of self-isolation, becoming unreachable and letting one's voice echo back to oneself alone.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A song that mistakes its own disappearance for invincibility, sung from inside a shelter assembled out of everything the singer has stripped away from himself. The boasts of total surveillance and untouchability keep collapsing back onto the speaker, because the sound he sends out only returns unanswered, proof of a room with no one else in it. The title unit, a tape-delay that repeats a signal until it decays to silence, makes the method audible: the fade at the close, chanted as the triumph that no one can reach him, is really the voice thinning until there is nothing left to reach. On the late-Reznor record The Slip, it reads as the cold logic of retreat, the self-protection that is also self-erasure.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the retreat that reads as invincibility is actually self-erasure","built":"the title unit — a tape-delay decaying to silence — makes the fade the triumph","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~eraser":{"haiku":"Destructive consumption and possession escalating into repeated pleas for death, mapping violence and use as pathways to annihilation and obliteration of self.","opus":"Sitting near the bottom of The Downward Spiral, this is Reznor's addiction and self-loathing stripped to single verbs: a mounting chant of appetite that curdles into wanting to be undone, then collapses into one demand repeated until it stops meaning anything. The escalation from craving another person to begging for your own annihilation IS the song.","sonnet":"A compressed cycle of obsession and self-destruction — the speaker moves through desire, use, and harm directed at another before turning all of it inward, ending in a repeated demand to be killed and erased entirely.","src":"fable","fable":"A relationship stripped to a verb litany, two words a line, where the arc from needing and dreaming to using and breaking exposes how seamlessly desire curdles into cruelty when someone is treated as a thing to consume. Halfway through the pronoun flips: the aggressor turns the same violence inward, begging to be hated, smashed, erased, until the final plea collapses into a single word chanted past the point of meaning. It's Reznor's core move on The Downward Spiral in miniature the abuser and the self-annihilator are the same person, and love here is just the shortest route to obliteration.","fableDeep":"The entire lyric is a conjugation table: eight verbs aimed at you — need, dream, find, taste, then the swerve into use, scar, break — followed by the object flipping to me: lose, hate, smash, erase, and finally the death-wish verb repeated eight times. No connective tissue, no imagery, no chorus; each verb carries a stanza's worth of implication, and the listener supplies the narrative the words refuse to provide — which is the design: the arc from desire to possession to damage to self-annihilation reads as inevitable precisely because nothing interrupts it. The single pivot — second-person object becoming first-person object — is the whole drama: the appetite that consumed the other turns home, punishment requested as the only intimacy remaining. The closing repetition stops meaning and becomes texture, a needle skipping at the bottom of the groove. Thesis: the album's minimalist core sample — love and self-destruction demonstrated to be grammatically continuous, one pronoun swap apart.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"desire and self-annihilation shown to be one pronoun swap apart","built":"the whole lyric is a conjugation table — verbs aimed at you, then flipped to me"},
"nine-inch-nails~even-deeper":{"haiku":"The speaker discovers damage runs far deeper than imagined—trauma, darkness, and self-destruction pull him along a path he cannot escape even with brief moments of perfection.","opus":"A portrait of depression and self-ruin in which even a flawless imagined life, complete with an ideal partner, is something the narrator feels driven to wreck. He confesses the damage sits below any level he could fix; unseen voices lure him off, hope drains away, and he stays fixed on a course he cannot reverse.","sonnet":"A song about being so fundamentally broken that even imagining wholeness — a dream of a \"perfect\" version of life — triggers a compulsion to destroy it; the speaker is trapped in a track of self-sabotage he cannot leave.","src":"haiku","fable":"A relapse narrative that measures addiction not as an event but as a depth you keep discovering beneath yourself: every time the narrator believes he's beaten it, the bottom drops again. Reznor stages the disease's cruelest trick in the second verse, where even a dreamed moment of wholeness and connection triggers the compulsion to sabotage it — intimacy read as a thing to be spoiled rather than kept. The pre-chorus reframes the whole slide as premeditated, a voice calling him elsewhere that he can't refuse, so what looks like drift is really surrender. The outro is the read's real horror: a stack of recovery-slogan affirmations of being fine and on the right path that repeat until they curdle, the reassurances degrading in real time into an admission that the point of no return is already behind him — the mask of composure peeled off to show the relapse it was hiding all along.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"recovery slogans repeated until they peel back to reveal relapse","built":"affirmation stack in the outro curdles into the thing it hides"},
"nine-inch-nails~every-day-is-exactly-the-same":{"haiku":"Expresses the monotony and despair of a heavily controlled, repetitive existence where love is absent and the self is constantly suppressed.","opus":"Numb, hopeless monotony, as the narrator repeats the same routine, feels neither love nor pain, has lost his voice and purpose, and can predict exactly how it all ends.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a numb, repetitive existence — stripped of purpose, voice, and feeling — hides a secret message hoping someone will one day find it, clinging to a small remaining piece of selfhood buried beneath a hollow routine.","src":"haiku","fable":"A flat, medicated portrait of routine as a kind of living death, where sameness has grown so total that the speaker can forecast tomorrow only because nothing ever moves, and can no longer be sure his old purpose or his old voice were ever real to begin with. The verses map an erosion of self under unseen watchers, and the refrain delivers the bleakest possible consolation: a life scrubbed clean of both tenderness and hurt, so anesthetized that feeling of any kind has drained away — the numbness Reznor circled throughout his post-addiction With Teeth. What lifts it above pure resignation is the bridge, where the speaker scrawls a secret message and tucks it somewhere his keepers will never think to check, a small doomed act of contact proving that a buried interior still leaks through the deadened surface even as he admits he sees no exit. The looping chorus enacts the trap it describes, the line repeating until the words stop meaning and become the wall itself.","fv":2,"fnote":"A finished Francis Lawrence video exists that almost nobody has seen — Reznor killed it in post, calling it a mistake that became the catalyst to rethink the band’s whole direction.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","surveillance & control"],"means":"numbness reframed as the only available mercy","built":"the looping chorus enacts the trap it names until the words dissolve into wall"},
"nine-inch-nails~every-day-is-exactly-the-same-rehersals":{"haiku":"Institutional control erases individuality through monotony; resistance glimmers faintly beneath compliance.","opus":"The dead-eyed monotony of routine that swallows purpose and voice, leaving neither love nor pain; the smuggled note hidden for someone to find is the last flicker of a self bleeding through the sameness.","sonnet":"A person trapped in numbing routine — their identity, voice, and purpose have dissolved into mechanical repetition with neither love nor pain remaining, and they secretly write notes hoping someone will one day find them and know there was still someone inside.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~everything":{"haiku":"A transformation and transcendence narrative where the speaker sheds their previous identity and home, replaced by an internal entity that awakens and takes over.","opus":"A declaration of survival and transformation, as the speaker sheds his former self, watches his old identity break apart, and finds a sense of freedom and homecoming even as something new rises within him.","sonnet":"A speaker emerges from a prolonged ordeal transformed — old identity dissolving, an inherited darkness still threatening to rise — yet arrives at a feeling of being finally home and free.","src":"haiku","fable":"The most jarringly upbeat song Reznor has ever written, and that brightness is the whole trick: a survivor's anthem of shedding the old self, sung with the buoyancy of someone finally sober and free. But Hesitation Marks is an album about relapse and the fragility of recovery, and the song lives in the gap between the sunny tune and what the words actually confess. The dissolving walls and the self that bends and breaks read at first as liberation, yet the second chorus turns them sinister, as the thing waking inside is no cleansed new person but a returning appetite that grabs and takes. So the triumphant closing refrain of being home and free is both sincere and a mask, the recovering addict's conviction of arrival that the music's manic cheer quietly exposes as the same self-deception that precedes the next fall.","fv":2,"fnote":"Reznor heard the trap in it too: he called the song a descendant of Fear, Joy Division and New Order, was bemused it branded this his happy album — he said he doesn’t hear it that way — and described its bold defiance as actually, in his own words, kind of self-destructive.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"the survivor's anthem of arrival as the self-deception that precedes relapse","built":"the most upbeat melody on the album quietly exposes the manic cheer as a mask"},
"nine-inch-nails~find-my-way":{"haiku":"Lost traveler seeking path and spiritual redemption. Haunted by ghosts of past self and begging divine help, the speaker has exhausted earthly searching for direction.","opus":"A prayer-like lament from a soul gone off course, imploring the divine to listen as he struggles to reorient himself after a grave error. He's tormented by the specter of the person he once was, terrified that it has returned for good.","sonnet":"A penitent narrator, feeling like a ghost of his former self, wanders lost and pleads with God to hear his prayer and guide him back after some catastrophic mistake drove away someone who should never have seen what was inside him. The tone is that of a genuine, anguished supplication.","src":"haiku","fable":"A lapsed man's prayer that never finds an amen: the narrator addresses the Lord as a wanderer confessing the wrong turn, but what he's lost isn't a road so much as the earlier selves now returning as ghosts he can't outrun. On Hesitation Marks, Reznor's 2013 record of hard-won sobriety and self-reckoning, the repentance registers less as faith than as an addict's tally of every place he's been, the childhood bedtime prayer surfacing as genuine terror of what might take his soul. The song's real tell is structural: the composed plea buckles mid-track into a run-on stammer of apology, the mask of the calm supplicant slipping to show the panic beneath, and then the closing verse simply loops the same request for a hearing over and over. That refusal to resolve is the meaning; asking to be heard, forever, without answer, is what being lost actually sounds like.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"a lapsed man's prayer that loops without ever finding an amen","built":"the composed plea buckles mid-track into a run-on stammer as the supplicant mask slips"},
"nine-inch-nails~find-my-way-oneohtrix-point-never-remix":{"haiku":"Lost and haunted by spectral versions of self, the speaker wanders seeking direction while facing the ghosts of their own history.","opus":"From Hesitation Marks, a weary, penitent wandering, the narrator lost far from home and praying his soul be taken as ghosts of his former self close in. He admits he is the only obstacle in his own path. Reznor's late-career confession of regret and search for direction, reworked here into a spare, ghostly meditation.","sonnet":"Penitent wandering after a relationship ends: the narrator confesses to a great mistake and asks God to take his soul, while the ghosts of who he used to be close in. The final admission — 'I'm the only one in my way' — shifts blame inward after the other person is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~gave-up":{"fable":"A hymn of self-destruction that weaponizes the language of gratitude: the pre-chorus borrows the cadence of a love song or a religious awakening, thanking a you for the revelation, but what is revealed is only the speaker's own ruin, so the epiphany curdles into blame. The chorus enacts the wreckage as a single repeated verb, each object of demolition escalating from mind to conviction to selfhood until the wrecking turns inward and becomes the only remaining plan of action. On Broken, the 1992 EP made in the wrath of a contract battle, that mechanized rage fits: the speaker even names himself a broken machine that hope and lubricant cannot repair, aligning bodily collapse with industrial failure. The bridge strips everything down to a metronomic pair of admissions, effort and surrender alternating without progress, so the title is not a confession delivered once but a switch that flips back and forth until trying and quitting become indistinguishable. What closes it out is the throwaway command, the self reduced to garbage discarded on purpose, the mask of resolve fully dropped to show that the smashing was never catharsis but disposal.","fableDeep":"When fixing fails ('hope and Vaseline still cannot fix this broken machine'), breaking becomes the last act that feels like agency — the smashing is methodical, a 'steady systematic decline' he administers to himself. The refrain borrows gospel language ironically: the light 'you' made him see is the full sight of his own ruin.","haiku":"After someone changed the speaker's sense of self, they descend into self-destruction and despair, caught in a cycle of trying and failing to escape the damage.","opus":"A song about self-destruction and despair, in which the speaker describes smashing everything he believed in and valued, hating what he has become, and ultimately giving up after trying and failing.","sonnet":"A portrait of total self-destruction: the speaker has systematically dismantled their own sanity, integrity, and sense of self, arriving at a point of resigned surrender after repeatedly trying and failing to hold together.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fnote":"The video is a live take at Le Pig, the studio Reznor built inside 10050 Cielo Drive — the house where the Manson Family murdered Sharon Tate, its name taken from the word left on the door in blood — with a then-unknown Marilyn Manson among the players on screen.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance"],"means":"self-destruction administered methodically as the only remaining agency","built":"the bridge alternates effort and surrender without progress, enacting the title as a switch"},
"nine-inch-nails~get-down-make-love": {"haiku":"Intense, demanding sexual interrogation and physicality frames intimacy as a push-pull of contrasting desires, where both partners wrestle for control and satisfaction.","opus":"A Queen cover reframed by Reznor into cold, clinical carnality, opening with an interrogation about a lover's first sexual experience before the blunt exchange of bodies as meat and heat; the push-pull of one partner wanting to get high while the other wants to cool down turns sex into a power struggle.","sonnet":"A Queen cover rendered as industrial interrogation and raw sex — opens with a clinical, invasive interview about a woman's sexual history before exploding into explicit physicality, the combination making desire feel simultaneously predatory and urgent.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mercury wrote this as a strut — sex as appetite and command, the imperative of the title barked with total confidence that the body being addressed will comply. Reznor keeps every word and inverts the posture: the cover opens not on desire but on a clinical, coercive intake of questions about a first encounter, so the demand to get down arrives already soured into pressure, a thing done to someone rather than shared. The push-and-pull grid Mercury played as flirtation — one wants heat, the other wants to cool down — reads here as a lover who never stops asking and a lover forever flinching away, want against the word enough. That is the Pretty Hate Machine move: take a song about power fully possessed and re-sing it as power that has curdled, where making love is indistinguishable from being made to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","love & desire"],"means":"the command to make love restaged as pressure rather than shared appetite","built":"every word of the original kept while the posture flips from strut to coercion"},
"nine-inch-nails~getting-smaller":{"haiku":"Increasing erasure and powerlessness as individuality and voice are stripped away, leaving only obedience and fading away.","opus":"A portrait of paranoia and diminishing self, where the narrator loses his grip, obeys, and fades into a faceless nobody in the crowd. He recalls once believing in achieving something great but can no longer tell what's real, resigning himself to his shrinking world.","sonnet":"A portrait of slow erasure: the speaker has lost his grip, his voice, his sense of purpose, and now shrinks into invisibility — obeying, behaving, fading away — with a sardonic acceptance that the shrinking world is somehow okay, even as it clearly isn't.","src":"haiku","fable":"A diminishment anthem in which the speaker actively volunteers for his own erasure, dissolving into anonymity because being seen has become a threat he can no longer afford. The verses stage a body glitching apart, an arm swinging uselessly on a hinge, a head loose on its coil, until only one working limb remains and its sole use is to turn inward, so the last scrap of strength becomes an instrument of self-punishment. Structurally the shrinking is a downward slide: each chorus relabels vanishing as compliance, behaving and disappearing treated as the same submissive act, and by the third verse the speaker can no longer separate what he once strained to believe in from what actually stands in front of him, faith and reality flattening into a single grey. Arriving on Reznor's first sober record, With Teeth in 2005, the outro's chanted acceptance lands as its most unnerving gesture, a life contracting toward nothing reframed not as tragedy but as relief, a mask of calm stretched over the void it barely hides.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","surveillance & control"],"means":"vanishing reframed as compliance, erasure as submissive relief","built":"the outro chants acceptance of contraction as calm stretched over void"},
"nine-inch-nails~god-break-down-the-door":{"haiku":"A person searches futilely for answers and relief from pain, eventually accepting alienation and a transformed identity as they withdraw from seeking outside salvation.","opus":"A cry for divine intervention that arrives at bleak resignation: the singer warns that whoever comes seeking answers won't find them here, only the numbing ritual of removing pain and pushing it back in. Beneath the treachery, dust and drawn shades lies a dissociated speaker who no longer feels of this world and has come to accept that he is essentially asleep, alienated from his own reality.","sonnet":"A dissociation from humanity and a kind of bitter peace made with that alienation — the narrator has stopped expecting God or anyone else to deliver answers, and finds a strange comfort in no longer belonging to the treacherous, sleepwalking world around them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A demand for divine rescue the song already knows is futile, chanted anyway because the chanting is all that is left. The verses lock into a loop, hammering the same plea while flatly conceding no answers wait here. That contradiction is the point: the prayer persists exactly where it cannot work. The outro drops the appeal for a grimmer mechanism, cycling the pain out and forcing it back in, self-surgery on repeat instead of relief. Under the Blackstar-shadowed saxophone and Reznor's incantatory delivery sits ritual worn as a mask over exhaustion, a figure still knocking at a door he expects to stay shut.","fv":2,"fnote":"The saxophone is Reznor’s own, picked back up after some two decades away, and the Bowie debt is admitted: asked whether the horn made it sound like Blackstar, he said he knew it would — that he still thinks about that man all the time.","themes":["faith & the occult","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"chanting a prayer already conceded to be futile","built":"outro replaces the plea with self-surgery run on a mechanical loop"},
"nine-inch-nails~god-given":{"haiku":"Religious manipulation and cult ideology that uses faith to divide people by race and class, claiming divine selection for some while condemning others.","opus":"Reznor writes from inside the voice of a demagogue who fuses religion, greed and bigotry into a righteous us-against-them creed. The satire targets the certainty of the chosen, the way divine mandate gets used to exclude and dehumanize.","sonnet":"A satirical portrait of religious self-righteousness: a preacher-figure claims divine authority, demands submission, and uses faith to exclude and dehumanise others, exposing how a sense of being \"chosen\" can become a cover for intolerance.","src":"haiku","fable":"A warm recruitment sermon curdles into a purity checkpoint, exposing how the language of salvation doubles as the machinery of exclusion. The first half is pure invitation: come closer, trust the messenger, join the illuminated few who are certain they alone possess the truth. Then the same congregation turns on the listener, and verses that promised entry start naming reasons to bar it, first the wrong faith and then the wrong skin. On Year Zero's paranoid map of a near-future theocratic America, this is the anthem the regime croons to itself. The radiant light of revelation turns out to be a spotlight, sorting who belongs from who gets left in the dark.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","politics & society"],"means":"salvation's language doubling as the machinery of exclusion","built":"the invitation and the barring use identical rhetorical structure, the sermon turning purity checkpoint","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~happiness-in-slavery":{"haiku":"The paradox of people embracing conformity and servitude, finding contentment in submission rather than pursuing authentic freedom.","opus":"Underscored by shrieks of agony, the track depicts someone battered into obedience who talks himself into believing captivity can bring contentment. It frames conformity and voluntary bondage as a deadening comfort, stripping a person down to a spare part in a failing apparatus.","sonnet":"A portrait of a man who believes he is free but is thoroughly conditioned by the system he inhabits, finding comfort in conformity; the narrator recognises himself as human debris trapped inside a broken machine.","src":"haiku","fable":"A furious hymn to willing captivity, sung in the voice of the leash rather than the hand that holds it. The verses dismantle a man who imagines himself a rebel, cataloguing his defiance only to reveal each gesture as another rivet fastening him tighter. The recurring warning against looking too closely reframes ignorance as comfort and obedience as bliss. Then the bridge drops the sneering third person and speaks from inside the trap: a nameless thing reduced to noise and meat, groping at bars around a routine with no exit. The endless closing chant enacts what it describes, the phrase repeating until the speaker dissolves into the ideology, and the final line flips everything by naming the drug that owns him.","fv":2,"fnote":"The video cast Bob Flanagan — a real performance artist and self-described supermasochist — as the man who willingly feeds himself to the machine, cut apart as his blood waters a garden below; banned almost everywhere, it staged the song’s thesis with a consenting body until television refused to look.","themes":["surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction","politics & society"],"means":"ignorance reframed as comfort, obedience delivered as bliss","built":"the closing chant repeats until the speaker dissolves into the ideology"},
"nine-inch-nails~head-down":{"haiku":"A dissociation narrative where the speaker rejects their present reality, denying their own face and life as real. Everything—including other people—feels like a dream they wish to escape.","opus":"A voice of oppression orders someone to keep their head down and know their place, while the person on the receiving end insists that none of this life is real and would give anything to escape it.","sonnet":"The narrator feels completely alienated from his surroundings — nothing around him is recognisable as his own face, his own life, or reality at all — and addresses someone consumed by hate, warning that the consequences of who they have become have caught up with them.","src":"haiku","fable":"One splitting mind speaks in two registers: an aggressor barking submission and rank, and the target whose consciousness unhooks from reality to endure it. The verses are pure domination, ordering the victim to stay lowered, to swallow scraps, to never forget where they stand; the sneer even circles back on the tormentor, hinting this ruin gets handed down. Against that, the chorus is derealization staged as prayer, a numbed disowning of the reflection, the surroundings, the very moment, everything demoted to nightmare so the hurt can be denied. The bargaining refrain is the one unguarded note, an offer priced by someone left with nothing to trade. Fitting for The Slip, released free and almost offhand in 2008, this is depleted late Reznor working small, framing collapse as a flat interrogation with no reply.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","surveillance & control","freedom & escape"],"means":"derealization staged as prayer to endure domination","built":"one splitting mind occupies both the aggressor's voice and the target's chorus"},
"nine-inch-nails~head-like-a-hole": {"fable":"The song fuses the divine and the financial into a single deity and then plays both sides of worship at once: the verses grovel with the abject devotion of an addict who will do anything the master names, while the chorus recoils and swears it would sooner be destroyed than surrender the last inch of will. Reznor lets that contradiction stand rather than resolve it — the same voice that offers to be nailed to the wall is the one insisting nothing can be taken from it, so the refusal reads less as freedom than as the last thing a captured person has left to say. The turn comes in the section after the refrain, where the servant's cringing flips into a threat aimed back at the deity, a promise that the one being bowed to will get exactly what it deserves. It sets the template for Pretty Hate Machine's whole posture: submission and revolt as the same reflex, the head emptied out like the hole of the title, powered by a hatred too tangled with need to ever break clean.","fableDeep":"Two voices alternate: the groveling devotee ('god money, I'll do anything for you, just tell me what you want me to') and the refuser ('no, you can't take that away from me') — worship and resistance sharing one throat. 'Bow down before the one you serve, you're going to get what you deserve' cuts both ways, threat and comeuppance in the same breath. And the defiance is telling in its smallness: not 'I'll destroy you' but 'you can't take it' — the song never claims money-god can be beaten, only that one last thing (control of oneself) can be kept off the market, at the price of being willing to die holding it.","haiku":"Rejection of control and manipulation by a dominating force that exploits others; refusal to submit or bow down despite coercion.","opus":"A song attacking the worship of money as a false god, refusing to surrender control to it and declaring one would rather die than obey, while condemning its indifference and greed.","sonnet":"A fierce refusal to submit to the power of money and greed, personified as a god that demands worship and tramples the sick and powerless, with the speaker insisting they would rather die than surrender their autonomy to it.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","faith & the occult","surveillance & control"],"means":"worship and revolt collapsed into a single reflex","built":"the title hollows the head into the cavity the deity fills"},
"nine-inch-nails~head-like-a-hole-live":{"haiku":"The body becomes a hole for the other to fill, consuming identity through intimate violation.","opus":"Reznor's early rage against money as a false god that owns and controls; the refusal to bow, to hand over the self, turns worship into defiance, choosing death over letting greed take everything.","sonnet":"Money deified and addressed directly — God money, tell me what to do, nail me against the wall. The narrator refuses: head like a hole, black as your soul, I'd rather die than give you control. The bow-down refrain that you'll get what you deserve is aimed back at the worshippers of capital, not at the narrator.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~heresy":{"fable":"An early salvo on The Downward Spiral: Reznor builds the album's collapse by first demolishing the authority above it. The verses stack a portrait of religion as a controlling con man—a figure who blinds himself out of fear, herds his followers like livestock, and licenses cruelty by claiming it's divine will—so the target isn't faith so much as the institutions that trade in guilt and obedience. The Nietzschean chorus lands not as triumph but as a shrug of exhausted nihilism: if the old moral order is gone, no one even mourns it, and the narrator dares his accuser to meet him in the damnation neither of them believes in anymore. The bridge's taunt about dying for your beliefs, then the final descent into the sneer about hypocrisy, turns the mockery back on the pious—this is a man who has burned down his last handhold on meaning, and the record spends the next hour showing what's left when nothing holds you up.","fableDeep":"The verses work in third person, assembling a composite believer-cleric who 'sewed his eyes shut because he is afraid to see' — willful blindness as the entry fee — then inflate him into the deity itself, whose 'perfect kingdom of killing, suffering and pain' reads sacred history as the theology it practiced. The chorus borrows Nietzsche's verdict and appends the sharper twist — 'and no one cares' — apathy, not argument, as the real death of God. The companion line is craftier than it sounds: 'if there is a hell I'll see you there' concedes nothing, the if intact, while promising the believer that his own doctrine books them the same room — damnation converted into solidarity and dare. The bridge's question, whether he'll burn beside his god in humility and die for this, flips evangelism's altar call back onto the evangelist; the final tag adds the diagnosis, drowning in his own hypocrisy. Thesis: not a case against God's existence but against the human uses of the invention — blasphemy deployed as moral accusation.","haiku":"Attacking Christianity as destructive manipulation and hypocrisy.","opus":"An atheist attack on Christianity, declaring God dead and condemning religion as a man-made instrument of suffering and control.","sonnet":"A wholesale rejection of Christianity and organized religion as a hypocritical, manipulative institution built on invented divinity.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"religion demolished as moral groundwork for the album's collapse ahead","built":"chorus borrows nietzsche then appends apathy as the real death blow","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~heresy-demo":{"haiku":"Organized religion corrupts through blind faith and violence, building heaven through cruelty and damnation.","opus":"An early demo of Reznor's furious anti-religious broadside, casting a man-made God as a tyrant demanding devotion through fear, suffering, and atrocity done in his name. The blunt refrain declares the divine dead and unmourned. Raw, blasphemous provocation from The Downward Spiral era, rage aimed at faith wielded as a tool of control.","sonnet":"An indictment of Christianity as willed blindness and institutional violence — a god dreamed up by someone who sews his eyes shut to avoid seeing the world as it is, uses religion to control the flock, and demands devotion in exchange for suffering and atrocities. God is dead and the speaker greets the fact without sentimentality.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~home-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Trapped inside another person; trying to escape but always returning, unable to break the tie that binds.","opus":"A study in disorientation and dissolving identity, the speaker jolted awake unsure of what's genuine and where he even belongs, his past self a blur. However hard he tries to break loose, he keeps gravitating back to the one person who has ever felt like home to him, a bond that reads as both refuge and trap.","sonnet":"A speaker is trapped in a relationship or psychological bond they cannot escape — repeatedly returning despite efforts to break free, uncertain of what is real, and feeling that another person has fundamentally altered or consumed their sense of self.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~home-non-lp-version":{"haiku":"The speaker returns to origins only to find comfort has become a trap.","opus":"About being so absorbed into another person that they've become the only refuge left, even while repeatedly straining and failing to pull loose. The vertigo of no longer knowing what is genuine and what is imagined is bound up with remaining lodged deep within them.","sonnet":"Returning compulsively to a person or place that shouldn't still have power — I used to know who I was until you came along, I escape and then find myself back here again. The refrain 'I am still inside you' names the entanglement without resolving it; the only home available is the one that undid the self.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~hurt":{"fable":"Trent Reznor wrote this at the end of The Downward Spiral as an addict's self-inventory, the needle offering the last available proof of feeling in a nervous system he's numbed past everything else. What reads as a suicide note is really a diagnostic: he cuts himself to confirm he still registers anything, then discovers the drug can erase the sting but not the memory it was meant to bury. The chorus turns the wreckage into a bitter bequest, offering a lover the only kingdom he owns — a ruined one — alongside a promise so certain it functions as a curse: intimacy from him will always resolve into harm. The final movement is the only place hope surfaces, and it does so as pure conditional, a fantasy of starting over far enough away that he might finally hold onto himself instead of destroying it, which the grammar quietly concedes he cannot. When Johnny Cash covered it near his own death, the same words became an old man's ledger of a whole life, proof the song was never really about heroin but about the general project of surviving what you've done to yourself.","fableDeep":"It opens as a hypothesis test: hurting himself 'to see if I still feel,' pain valued as the one instrument still returning data, the needle's 'old familiar sting' binding self-harm and addiction into a single gesture — and the attempt to kill it all away fails against total recall: he remembers everything. The chorus is a bequest addressed to 'my sweetest friend': the whole estate offered and devalued in the same breath — an empire, but of dirt — with the only warranty he can honor attached: he will let them down, he will make them hurt; love expressed as accurate forecasting. The crown-of-filth on the liar's chair plays coronation as latrine; 'you are someone else, I am still right here' watches everyone change and leave while he stays fixed at the scene. The final conditional is the album's lone note of grace — starting again, a million miles away, keeping himself this time. Thesis: the spiral's epilogue, where warning the beloved of the damage you'll do is the nearest available form of love.","haiku":"Charts the breakdown of a self through self-harm and regret, measuring pain to feel alive while watching everyone leave. The narrator reflects on becoming a disappointment and his decayed empire of broken promises.","opus":"A confession of self-harm and despair from someone who hurts himself just to feel something real, mourning what he has become and how everyone he knows eventually leaves. He addresses a loved one, admitting he will let them down, and wishes he could start over and keep himself intact.","sonnet":"A person reflects on self-inflicted pain as the only way to feel something real, surveying a life of broken promises, addiction, and emotional emptiness, and acknowledging that they will inevitably disappoint and wound those closest to them.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","love & desire"],"means":"warning the beloved as the nearest available form of love","built":"the lone conditional in the final verse is the album's only note of grace"},
"nine-inch-nails~hyperpower":{"haiku":"Political violence and technologized warfare strip humanity while the powerful remain untouchable; collective rage finds no outlet.","opus":"An abrasive, apocalyptic snapshot of a violent America—detained messiahs, guns forged from steeples, killing outsourced to computers. Reznor becomes the enemy he indicts, the song's own aggression standing in for a nation addicted to bloodshed.","sonnet":"A dense political and spiritual provocation — the speaker exposes how power structures weaponize religion and nationalism, turning churches into fortresses and people into insurgents; the messiah figure is an immigrant detained at the border, stripped of his identity by the very system that prays in his name.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~i-do-not-want-this":{"fable":"A depressive at the bottom of The Downward Spiral, so numbed that even self-harm draws no blood, rejects the very hand extended to him. The chorus is less refusal than territorial fury: the one thing he can still defend is the authority over his own suffering, so he snarls at anyone claiming to understand it. The album's genius is the outro, where that flat despair suddenly detonates into a manic litany of appetites — to know all, be everywhere, consume everyone, matter at last. It reads at first like recovery, but Reznor sets it as the sickness itself: grandiosity as the flip side of the same self-erasure, wanting everything because he cannot bear to be one small, finite, wounded person.","fableDeep":"Two pressures meet: verses of paralysis — clay flesh, bamboo puncturing skin that doesn't bleed, drowning two feet down with a loved face wavering above — against a chorus of pure refusal whose object is never specified: 'I do not want this' withholds its referent because the this is the entire condition, unlistable. The second front is sympathy itself: 'don't you tell me how I feel' rejects even accurate diagnosis as trespass, and the verse sneers at hands extended to sufferers 'like that means something' — comfort read as credential-collecting. The self-aware hedge 'maybe this is a cry for help' names the cliché first, disarming the reading by admitting it. Then the volta: the closing chant swings from wanting nothing to wanting everything — omniscience, omnipresence, universal carnal contact, mattering — appetite sized deliberately beyond fulfillment, mania as depression read backwards, both refusing the actual scale of a livable life. Thesis: anhedonia and infinite hunger diagnosed as one illness, differing only in direction.","haiku":"A person caught in depression rejects sympathy and refuses to be defined by their suffering. They spiral through contradictory desires—wanting everything and nothing at once—and reject others' attempts to help.","opus":"A portrait of depression and helplessness, the speaker rejecting others' pity while voicing a desperate refusal of his state and a raw craving to matter and experience everything.","sonnet":"The narrator is paralysed by alienation and a sense of worthlessness — retreating to bed, rejecting false sympathy, and raging between not wanting his current existence and a desperate, contradictory hunger to experience and matter in the world.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance","madness & the mind"],"means":"grandiosity as the flip side of the same self-erasure, mania reading depression backwards","built":"the outro's manic appetite for everything diagnoses the same illness as the verses' wanting nothing"},
"nine-inch-nails~i-know-you-can-feel-it":{"haiku":"Destiny unfolds with ominous inevitability as the speaker warns of coming catastrophe.","opus":"A late, hypnotic piece about an appetite set irreversibly in motion, a plan clicking into place and a moment that erases and takes over everything; the menace is a compulsion the narrator insists you can feel building too.","sonnet":"A plan set in motion — something appetitive and total is beginning, erasing everything, spreading through contact. The repetition of 'feel it in me' positions the narrator as the vector, the carrier of something that, once started, cannot be stopped. The listener is warned to go while they can.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~i-m-looking-forward-to-joining-you-finally":{"haiku":"Someone seeks a way out through numbness and forgetting, reflecting on loss and regret while yearning for connection and escape.","opus":"A mournful look at a man who believed he had it all until he was exposed and lost even more, his early innocence and his remorse alike frozen solid. The wistful recollection of warmth and a closing wish to accompany someone frame a yearning to be reunited in death with a person already gone.","sonnet":"A eulogy for someone who destroyed themselves through excess and delusion — the third-person story of a man who lost everything gradually, too proud and too broken to stop, until death was the only destination; the speaker's own longing to rejoin the smell of sunshine suggests they too are at the end.","src":"haiku","fable":"One of The Fragile's frozen late chapters, where numbness is finally sold as relief: darkness reframed as safety, forgetting reframed as a skill you can learn. Told in the distancing third person, it's an obituary for a man who mistook the loss of everything for rock bottom and then kept falling, his regret literally iced to his face. The one thread of warmth is a remembered smell that keeps surfacing between the cold images, and the title's grim joke lands when the pronoun collapses inward at the end: joining you is death asked as a gentle, exhausted courtesy, all the fight already gone.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness","death & grief"],"means":"exhausted courtesy frames death as the gentlest remaining option","built":"third-person distance collapses inward only at the final pronoun"},
"nine-inch-nails~i-would-for-you":{"haiku":"A person lies to themselves and wishes they could be someone else entirely in order to be worthy of their partner, haunted by self-blame and disappointment.","opus":"A worn-out admission of self-delusion and guilt from someone haunted by a possibility that never came to be; he confesses that if he could somehow become a different person, he'd do it for the sake of the one he cares about.","sonnet":"The speaker reflects on a relationship that has deteriorated, admitting they keep deceiving themselves rather than confronting what is wrong. The song circles around a painful self-awareness: they wish they could become a different person just to be enough for the other.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a love offering the speaker undermines even as he makes it, vowing to remake himself into a different man for someone while admitting that self-deceit is the only skill he has left. The threshold imagery carries the argument: a door with a warning nailed to it, a room he lets her enter while hiding his worst from view. In the second verse that failure widens into a wheel, this ruin having spun through once and sure to come around again, which is why the promise curdles. On Hesitation Marks, the old self-loathing goes quieter and clearer, so the tenderness reads as sincere and doomed at once. He wants to become worthy of the offer, yet already names himself the one to blame when it fails.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"a love offering undermined by naming the self as its own saboteur","built":"threshold imagery — the warning-nailed door — stages the hiding as the promise"},
"nine-inch-nails~in-this-twilight":{"haiku":"Contemplating the end of all things, finding peace in darkness and seeking a better place beyond doubt and suffering.","opus":"The narrator watches the sun rise a last time over a burning, dying world and offers soft comfort at the apparent end, coaxing a companion to trust that the dark is really benign and that some kinder refuge remains even now.","sonnet":"A meditation on the end of the world or of a life, addressed to someone whose time is running out — the sky blazes, ash settles in hair, darkness falls — and the narrator offers the comfort that in this final twilight a better place exists, even as he admits the possibility of regret for how he lived.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a lullaby murmured to the dying by someone who knows the comfort is a fiction and keeps offering it anyway. Late on Year Zero, Reznor’s 2007 vision of an America rotting into theocratic collapse, it watches a scorched planet go dark and rebrands extinction as a gentle arrival somewhere kinder. Every reassurance in the refrain hangs on one condition: the darkness only turns radiant, the longing only dissolves, if the listener consents to the story, so the mercy holds by surrender rather than by fact. Then the mask slips, and the soothing voice becomes a confession, a survivor haunted by their own hand in the ruin and grieving a second chance that will never come. What remains is tenderness practiced without hope, one soul talking another calmly into the ending instead of against it.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","artifice & performance"],"means":"comfort offered as fiction to ease extinction","built":"the soothing voice unmasks as survivor guilt mid-song"},
"nine-inch-nails~in-two":{"haiku":"Identity fracturing into two selves; unable to tell which version is real as one consumes the other.","opus":"A violent splitting of the self, urging one to shed their skin and become their disease; the speaker can no longer tell his two halves apart and admits he just doesn't know who he is anymore.","sonnet":"A person is splitting into two irreconcilable versions of themselves — one urges violent self-renewal through shedding the old skin, the other denies responsibility — until neither can remember which one they originally were.","src":"haiku","fable":"Late on Hesitation Marks, Reznor stages a self splitting under the strain of transformation, and the song's whole method is to let its two voices drift into indistinguishability. The verses issue clipped, one-word commands to shed, purge, and become someone new, framing that violence as natural and necessary even as it insists the old self was somebody else entirely, not me, not that. But the chorus watches from outside and reports the cost: the two selves the verses tried to keep separate are becoming impossible to tell apart, until even the person can no longer remember which one they are. The outro abandons instruction and analysis alike for a numb refrain of not knowing anymore, dropped mid-phrase at the end so its very last word is a self-recognition that fails to complete. As a late-album reckoning about staying clean, it reads the disintegration honestly rather than resolving it: the you being addressed and the I doing the addressing have merged, and the confidence of the opening imperatives curdles into a confession that the transformation may have erased the person it was meant to save.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"transformation erasing the self it was meant to save","built":"opening imperatives curdle into outro dropped mid-phrase"},
"nine-inch-nails~into-the-void":{"haiku":"The speaker repeatedly attempts to preserve and save themselves but finds their sense of self constantly disintegrating and slipping away uncontrollably.","opus":"A study of a self coming apart, the narrator repeating that his efforts to hold himself together keep failing as he drifts away, envisioning where it all ends and gathering up the fragments of himself no longer permitted to remain.","sonnet":"A person walking toward some final destination — pictures in their head, others already lined up — describes the constant, failing attempt to hold themselves together as their sense of self keeps slipping away despite every effort to save it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the failure of self-rescue, built so that its own form enacts the losing battle. The one confessional line runs on a loop under everything else, a parenthetical undertow that keeps interrupting the verses before they can resolve, so the will to hold together is always being drowned out by the fact that it isn't working. Reznor frames it as a commute toward an ending, a station and a fixed destination, the mundane surface of transit standing in for a slide the speaker narrates but can't stop; by the close the phrase has swallowed the entire song and even loses its last word, the sentence itself dissolving on cue.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"self-rescue attempt narrated in real time while already failing","built":"confessional refrain loops under everything, drowning the verses out"},
"nine-inch-nails~kinda-i-want-to":{"haiku":"Internal conflict between desire and morality; the singer feels temptation pulling them toward something forbidden and knows it is wrong, but wants it anyway.","opus":"An early Trent Reznor confession of being pulled toward something he knows is wrong, imagined as a dark presence beside him he cannot resist. He talks himself into it, bargaining that for a single night he can pretend it's fine, ready to accept whatever the temptation costs.","sonnet":"A song about wanting to act on a desire the narrator knows is wrong — the attraction is strong enough to make him hope God looks away, and for one night at least he decides the cost does not matter.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about temptation that stages the exact mechanics of giving in, tracking the gap between what the speaker knows and what he wants. The verses externalize the desire as something separate from him, a figure that shares his bed and does the wanting on his behalf, so the guilt can be quarantined; the pre-chorus then does the moral bookkeeping, conceding twice over that this is neither right nor good. But the title phrase is the whole trick: that hedged, half-swallowed admission keeps deniability alive, wanting while pretending only to kinda want, and by the outro even that qualifier burns off and only the raw want remains, repeated until conscience is drowned out. On the 1989 debut this is Trent Reznor's small-scale rehearsal of Pretty Hate Machine's larger theme, appetite winning its argument against belief, right down to the wish that God might simply look away and the hidden final line where the bargaining stops and the chance is taken.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","shame & repression","faith & the occult"],"means":"hedged admission keeping deniability alive until the qualifier burns off","built":"the title's kinda erodes across the track until only raw want remains"},
"nine-inch-nails~la-mer":{"fable":"The centerpiece calm of The Fragile is sung in Louisiana Creole by Denise Milfort, a folk fragment carried on rolling piano toward some threshold it never quite names outright. What it says is a death told as homecoming: the speaker forecasts a morning when they turn to face the heavens and the water, dissolve into both, and are gathered up and ferried back to a house they call home, so the drowning is indistinguishable from deliverance. The closing declaration that nothing remains to hold them lands not as threat but as release, the ocean as the thing that finally comes to reclaim its own.","haiku":"When the day arrives, the sea will embrace the speaker and carry them home; nothing can stop them now as they surrender to the ocean's pull.","opus":"a longing for death as a homecoming, becoming the sky and sea and being embraced by the sea to be delivered home","sonnet":"a dying or departing soul's vision of merging with sky and sea as death becomes a homecoming from which nothing can hold them back","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","nature & the elements","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"drowning rendered indistinguishable from homecoming and release","built":"Creole folk fragment positions death as the ocean reclaiming its own"},
"nine-inch-nails~last":{"haiku":"A transient, temporary connection devoid of lasting meaning, with desire and addiction masking the inevitability of separation and decay.","opus":"A wallow in self-disgust and total surrender, with the narrator abandoning any effort, sinking as low as possible, and seeing past every falsehood around him. He craves someone to use him up, wreck him, and discard him completely.","sonnet":"The narrator, hollowed out by loss of purpose and self-respect, craves someone to enter him, fix him, and ultimately discard him — fully aware that whatever he seeks is temporary and that nothing can truly rescue him from his own decay.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that mistakes being consumed for being loved, aimed at a partner from whom the narrator demands repair and penetration in one breath. The verses catalog a body rotting under its own disgust, dressed up to pass for living; the chorus converts every plea for healing into a plea to be swallowed whole. The refrain refuses permanence, treating the exchange as throwaway even while begging for it. The closing litany is the tell: he wants to be made, taken, broken, then discarded, so annihilation arrives wearing the costume of desire. On the Broken EP, the mask is tenderness and beneath it sits a wish to be used up entirely.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"annihilation arriving dressed in the costume of desire","built":"the closing litany flips tender verbs into a dismantling sequence","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~less-than":{"haiku":"A song questioning whether acquiring material comforts actually fixes internal brokenness or simply deepens emptiness. The speaker challenges whether oblivion and consumption fulfill the promised healing.","opus":"Reznor mocking the empty promise that getting what you want will fix what is broken inside you. Aimed at a hollowed-out, screen-fed generation, it asks whether any of it filled the void or just confirmed how small you feel.","sonnet":"A challenge aimed at those who justified aggression and violence — missile trails, silencing, demanding compliance — asking whether getting what they wanted actually fixed anything or just led to oblivion.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hollow interrogation of a you who keeps rationalizing escalating harm, then wonders whether the wreckage finally made them whole. The refrain scores each atrocity like points cleared in a game, weapons streaking overhead reduced to one more justified tally, until violence becomes an achievement to unlock rather than a horror to feel. Verse two stitches the EP's own title into its skin, add violence surfacing as a command inside the taunt. The killer move is the closing riddle: did it repair what was broken, are you diminished. The song refuses to accuse from outside and instead wears the numbed voice itself, letting the mask speak so the emptiness underneath answers on its own.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"violence scored as achievement while the numbed voice wears the mask","built":"the EP's own title surfaces as a command inside the taunt"},
"nine-inch-nails~letting-you":{"haiku":"Society permits those in power to exploit and pursue greed without consequence. Fear, suffering, and political corruption spread while the masses passively allow it.","opus":"Reznor's indictment of a fear-mongering power structure sustained by public complicity; the horror is the refrain of collective passivity, the admission that we keep letting the greedy and warlike get away with it because we do as we're told.","sonnet":"A blistering accusation of collective passivity — the narrator catalogues the ways authoritarian power controls, starves, and manipulates a population, and the central indictment is that we are all complicit in letting it happen.","src":"haiku","fable":"A protest song that locates the crime not in the tyrant but in the crowd that permits him, indicting a collective we conditioned by manufactured fear into obedient complicity. The industrial imagery of smoke and slaughter sketches a regime of greed and engineered dread, yet each accusation lands back on the singers, who confess to doing only what they are told. The refrain turns on a hinge: the powerful figure both slips free of pursuit and is granted impunity, and the parenthetical echo names which meaning the narrator intends. That the reckoning arrives past the point of rescue, the speakers already beyond saving themselves, is the bleak center of this 2008 track, released free by a band long fluent in self-implicating rage.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control"],"means":"indicting a crowd that grants impunity by doing only what it is told","built":"the parenthetical echo on the refrain names which of two meanings the narrator intends"},
"nine-inch-nails~liar-reptile-demo":{"haiku":"Sexual contamination and parasitic intimacy spread through betrayal, the speaker becoming diseased by desire for the liar.","opus":"An early sketch of the track that became Reptile, steeped in disgust, obsession, and self-contamination, the narrator worshipping and loathing a corrupting lover who spreads disease and loneliness. Purity and filth collapse together. A demo drenched in NIN's Downward Spiral aesthetic of degradation, where craving another becomes indistinguishable from craving one's own defilement.","sonnet":"Desire as contagion — the other person is reptile-blooded, honey-trailing, insect-welcoming, and the narrator is not their victim but their mirror: 'my disease, my infection, I am so impure.' The need to contaminate to relieve loneliness is the turn; both parties are vectors, both are ruined.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~lights-in-the-sky":{"haiku":"Accompanying someone into darkness or destruction, witnessing their dissolution while remaining alongside them through inevitable loss and arrival of forces beyond control.","opus":"A vow of devotion to someone drifting away, already withdrawn to somewhere unreachable, the narrator promising to descend with them and stay close even as he watches them go under. The distant lights arriving and then bidding farewell hint at a death or departure he refuses to let them meet alone.","sonnet":"A companion who refuses to leave someone drowning — whether that person is mentally unraveling or dying, the speaker acknowledges they've tried to stay away but realizes their role is to remain present even as the lights in the sky signal an ending neither can stop.","src":"haiku","fable":"A vow spoken to someone already halfway gone, where the promise is not to save her but to stay — to follow her down into whatever drowning she is doing rather than let her do it alone. The apocalypse here is deliberately small: the lights arriving in the sky are less a cosmic end than the felt weight of a private one, the moment a caretaker admits the thing they were warned about has come true and their only remaining role is not to disappear. The song's whole argument lives in the one word that changes between the choruses — the lights first arrive, then wave goodbye — so that presence outlasts even the event it was summoned to witness, the speaker staying beside her past the point where staying can still do anything. On The Slip (2008) Reznor works this in an unusually tender, ambient register, the menace drained out until only the fidelity is left.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","death & grief"],"means":"presence pledged past the point where it can still do anything","built":"one word shift between choruses — lights arrive, then wave goodbye — carries the whole arc"},
"nine-inch-nails~love-is-not-enough":{"haiku":"Argues that love cannot sustain a relationship or heal deeper wounds, with both partners fading and becoming strangers. The song questions whether love can overcome emptiness when both people lie to themselves.","opus":"A disillusioned look at a failing relationship, admitting that the closeness two people imagined never got them far and that they cover themselves with lies. The blunt conclusion is that beneath the toughness they're fragile, and love alone is not enough to save them.","sonnet":"A relationship has deteriorated beyond repair, and the speaker acknowledges that despite the effort invested, love alone cannot sustain what has been built on deception and mutual damage — both people are left hollowed out, and the feeling that \"love is not enough\" becomes the bitter conclusion.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grim accounting of a relationship that has run out of runway, sung by a man taking stock rather than raging. The central move is structural: the chorus returns a second time with one word altered, the earlier admission that closeness carried them a certain distance quietly revised to say it never carried them anywhere at all, so the song erases its own small hope in real time. On With Teeth, Reznor's first record made sober, the honesty cuts inward instead of at a target; the imagery keeps stripping down to cold floor tile and a starved thing hidden away with no mouth to protest, until the title stops being an argument and becomes a chanted verdict, repeated over and over as if saying it enough might make it survivable. What the song refuses is rescue by feeling: the whole apparatus of intimacy is exposed as a shared cover story, and the tenderness beneath it simply isn't load-bearing.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","artifice & performance"],"means":"the song erasing its own small hope by altering one word in the returning chorus","built":"chorus revisited with one word swapped quietly revokes the earlier admission of progress"},
"nine-inch-nails~march-of-the-pigs":{"fable":"A frenzied, lurching demolition set to a stomping 7/8 meter that keeps tripping over itself, this is Reznor turning appetite into a spectacle of self-destruction. The verbs pile up as pure consumptive craving—to seize, smash, ruin something and savor its collapse—but the target is deliberately blank, because the real object is the watching: the addict's relish in ruin for its own sake. The pigs herded into rank are both the greedy mob demanding to be fed and the speaker himself, complicit in the trough. What makes it more than a tantrum is the chorus's poisoned bedside manner—the mockingly gentle promise that flaying off the skin should bring relief, and the closing turn where the pigs having won means only a numbed, dishonest calm, the false all-is-well of someone who has burned it down and calls the wreckage peace.","fableDeep":"It opens as a carnival barker ('step right up, march, push') and the wanting verses are a pile-on in miniature: don't like the look of it, let's discredit it, let's pick away at it, I want to watch it come down — the mechanics of mob appetite, written years before the internet made them a daily genre. 'I give you all that you want, take the skin and peel it back' is the performer offering himself as the meal, spectacle as self-flaying. The ending is pure poison served sweet: 'the pigs have won tonight, now they can all sleep soundly, and everything is all right' — a lullaby of surrender sung through clenched teeth.","haiku":"Consumption, greed, and violent destruction of systems and institutions.","opus":"Reznor turns cruelty into a spectacle you're invited to enjoy, staging the appetite to see something torn down and then rewarded with a hollow reassurance that everything's fine. It's an indictment of the pleasure taken in destruction, aimed as much at the listener as the pigs.","sonnet":"Contempt for a conformist, greedy crowd of followers who are manipulated and fed what those in power want them to consume.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance","violence & murder"],"means":"appetite staged as spectacle until the ruin is mistaken for peace","built":"a lurching 7/8 meter keeps tripping over itself, the form enacting the compulsion it describes","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~me-i-m-not":{"haiku":"A person loses control over an compulsive force they've begun but cannot stop; they wrestle with internal conflict, hiding themselves through avoidance, and ultimately deny they can change what they've become.","opus":"Reznor tracing addiction and self-alienation, a man watching himself become something he can't recognize or stop. The dread is that identity is just how well you hide, and the hiding is failing.","sonnet":"About being overtaken by a compulsion or desire you know is dangerous — losing control of yourself in a relationship or addiction, defining yourself by concealment, and desperately trying to stop something that has already gone too far.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of surrender dressed up as strength, where a man in the grip of something he never chose insists his composure is a triumph when it is really just practiced concealment. On Year Zero this reads as the private cost of a life under watch, where survival means giving nothing away. The song's engine is a plea to halt that the speaker keeps answering by refusing, so the one begging for release and the one denying it are a single divided voice. His identity, he confesses, is measured entirely by the skill of his vanishing act, which reframes winning as the discipline of never once striking back. By the end the words wear down to that lone refusal, repeated past meaning, a mask fused to whatever was underneath and a no that has forgotten what it was ever meant to deny.","fv":2,"fnote":"The second bathroom-drive drop: Barcelona, a week after Lisbon, paired with a file whose static hid a phone number under a spectrogram — one more door into the album’s alternate-reality game, a song about concealment smuggled out inside a hiding place.","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","surveillance & control"],"means":"defining identity entirely by the discipline of not striking back","built":"the single refusal repeated until the word forgets what it denies"},
"nine-inch-nails~meet-your-master":{"haiku":"Servitude and the reversal of power, as the formerly dominant are forced into submission and made to obey.","opus":"A threatening speech from captors to a toppled ruler now forced into obedience, hooded and made to comply. Ticking down toward an approaching catastrophe, they inform him the moment has arrived to submit to his new authority and accept his diminished station.","sonnet":"A dominant power strips a formerly in-charge figure of authority, forces compliance through blindfolds and orders, and promises that a reckoning is coming fast — the former leader will learn his true place.","src":"haiku","fable":"Near the end of Year Zero's dystopian arc, this is the reckoning delivered in the voice of the captors rather than the album's usual crushed victims, addressing a fallen authority forced to kneel against cold steel and take orders he used to give. The interrogation-room register is deliberately impersonal and procedural, the plural we announcing that judgment now belongs to a system rather than any person, and the taunt about mercy versus what he deserves keeps the outcome sadistically undecided. The countdown chorus fuses the personal humiliation to the whole record's coming collapse, so meeting your master and the arriving disaster become the same event rushing forward. The outro strips everything down to a single luring command repeated to hypnosis, the summons itself becoming the punishment as the machine simply keeps calling him in.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"former authority forced to kneel before the system it once ran","built":"outro's looping summons becomes the punishment rather than the verdict"},
"nine-inch-nails~memorabilia": {"haiku":"The speaker collects memories and physical traces of past intimacy, obsessing over fragments and refusing to remember what cannot be possessed.","opus":"NIN covering Soft Cell's early synth track about a compulsive collector of experiences and bodies who hoards souvenirs of intimacy he never truly possessed. The obsessive repetition and pornographic samples turn desire into hollow acquisition, closeness reduced to trophies.","sonnet":"A voyeuristic sexual obsession framed as compulsive collecting — the narrator accumulates traces of a person they may or may not have actually been with, cycling between claiming intimacy and admitting they never had them, fixating on their body as just another keepsake.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cover, so read the choice: Reznor takes Soft Cell's 1981 synth-pop travelogue of conquests-as-souvenirs and drags it into the surgical carnality of the Closer era, where a flatly repeated spoken intrusion about a stranger’s body turns the original's camp into clinical interrogation. The narrator is a tourist of people who keeps only the photograph, the keychain, the taste, and discards the person — collecting and rejecting in the same reflex, as if a drawer of trophies could stand in for having actually been present. The cruel joke is that the hoard defeats its own purpose: he begs the other body to remind him who they were, admitting that all this evidence of intimacy has left him with nothing he can recall. Reznor hears in that a portrait of appetite without contact, sensation logged and instantly voided, which is exactly the addiction-and-emptiness territory his own writing already occupied — the reason a borrowed song sits on him like his own skin.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"a hoard of trophies that proves the collector was never present","built":"a spoken clinical intrusion turns the original's camp into cold interrogation"},
"nine-inch-nails~metal-remix":{"haiku":"Alienation and the desire for artificial transcendence through technology, confusing mechanical dependency with love and human connection.","opus":"A cold fantasy of becoming machine, the narrator frightened by a liquid engineer yet longing to be plugged in, turned on, remade in the image of something inhuman while confusing love with need. This is Nine Inch Nails covering Gary Numan's Metal, Trent Reznor recasting the synth-pioneer's android alienation in his own industrial idiom, a lineage acknowledged between two artists obsessed with humanity dissolving into circuitry.","sonnet":"A figure built in a facility strains toward humanity while knowing it is machinery — the Mallory heart will fail, the liquid engineer is frightening, the treatment is scheduled for tomorrow. Plugging in and turning on feel like aspiration rather than function. The last line — still confusing love with need — is the gap the whole song describes without being able to close.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~mr-self-destruct":{"fable":"The overture to a concept album about a man dismantling himself, sung entirely in the first person by the destroyer as if it were the only self worth being. It refuses a single identity and instead claims every appetite in turn - desire, hatred, religion, addiction, violence - so that the through-line isn't any one vice but the machinery of self-harm that wears all of them as costumes. The trick is that this tyrant never coerces; it keeps conceding that the victim permits everything, which relocates the horror from an outside oppressor to a voice already living inside the skull, indistinguishable from want itself. Calling itself an escape hatch is the cruelest turn: the thing offering relief is the same thing consuming you, and the album will spend the next hour proving that the promised exit only leads further down.","fableDeep":"A possession litany in strict anaphora: every 'I am' answered by the parenthetical 'and I control you,' call-and-response as coercion, the chant structure making the listener complicit in the affirmation. The catalogue's range is the argument — lover, hidden hate, religion's message, denial, needle, bullet — self-destruction shown as protean, wearing whichever mask the host will admit through the door. The chorus adopts concierge diction: taking you where you want to go, supplying all you need to know — ruin as customer service, which is how it actually sells itself. The hinge is the whispered middle eight, where 'you let me do this to you' alternates with 'I am an exit': blame assigned and escape marketed in the same breath, the album's whole mechanism disclosed in two lines. The final verse escalates from vice to ballistics to eschatology, ending at the death of dreams — the full itinerary announced up front. Thesis: the spiral's first proof is that the destroyer was invited, and the invitation renews nightly.","haiku":"An inner force of self-destruction speaks from within, claiming control over all aspects of life and identity, pulling toward ruin and addiction.","opus":"A personification of self-destruction and control, an inner voice that claims to be one's hate, addiction, lies, religion and ruin, dragging the listener down while they let it happen.","sonnet":"An omnipresent inner voice catalogues all the forces — religion, denial, addiction, violence — that it embodies and uses to control and destroy the person it inhabits.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control"],"means":"the destroyer concedes the victim always gave permission","built":"strict anaphora makes the listener co-affirm every claim in the possession litany","fnote":"The beating that opens the track is lifted from THX 1138 — the hologram-beating scene, a prisoner struck over and over — so the album about self-dismantling begins with the sound of a man being hit.","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~my-violent-heart":{"haiku":"The speaker embodies violence born from injustice, forming an unstoppable force that will return and destroy those who created them, rising from their own ashes.","opus":"A collective, menacing voice of the abused and degraded warns their oppressor that mercy has curdled into wounds and rage. Framed as the fallout the powerful invited through arrogance, the crawling multitude who cannot all be halted promise that time will erode their tormentor until he harvests exactly what he planted.","sonnet":"A warning from a group or class of people who have been pushed past the point of compassion — the violence in their hearts is the direct product of what was done to them, and they're telling the perpetrators that a reckoning is coming that can't be stopped.","src":"haiku","fable":"A threat spoken in the first-person plural, from inside Year Zero's dystopian America to whatever regime or complacency built it. The we are the radicalized and the disposable — people made identical to their oppressor in appearance but hollowed out, the compassion shot out of them until only the violence remains, an effect the powerful caused and now refuse to comprehend. The song's whole force lives in that grammar of consequence: you set this in motion, you'll reap what you sowed, and the crawling posture — hands and knees, the lowest and most abject — turns into the reason they can't be stopped, because there are too many and they have nothing left to lose. What starts as prophecy hardens into occupation by the outro; the promise to be there when you come home, repeated until it's just the word crawl, is less a warning than a siege that has already begun and will not leave.","fv":2,"fnote":"The song entered the world on a plain flash drive left in a bathroom stall at a Lisbon show in February 2007, its file ending in a burst of static that spectrograms into the album cover’s descending hand — and the label, not in on Reznor’s stunt, had the RIAA chase fans for spreading his own marketing.","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","violence & murder"],"means":"naming the dispossessed as consequence the powerful created","built":"crawling posture inverted from abjection into unstoppable siege"},
"nine-inch-nails~no-you-don-t":{"haiku":"A parasitic person drains others while hiding their hollow interior; they falsely believe they are stronger than they are.","opus":"A hardened refusal aimed at a parasitic user who smiles while draining everyone dry, sinking blood-suckingly low. The narrator, who never believed they could fall so far, denies them the win, insisting that this time they won't take what they need.","sonnet":"Calling out a person who feeds off others — draining everyone around them while wearing a smile, keeping damage on the surface, convinced they can keep winning — until the narrator draws a hard line and strips the illusion away.","src":"haiku","fable":"A vampire song aimed at a specific parasite: someone who charms to your face while quietly draining everyone around them, hollowed out on the inside and keeping the pleasant surface up because there's nothing behind it. The address lands like a debtor's kiss-off—the second half drops the imagery for a flat, itemized statement of contempt, a for-the-record note filed against someone who mistook their leverage for power. The hammering refrain isn't a threat so much as a door closing: whatever grip the leech thought it held has quietly expired.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"a debtor's itemized kiss-off filed against a parasitic charmer","built":"the hammering refrain lands as a door closing rather than a threat"},
"nine-inch-nails~non-entity":{"haiku":"Dissolution of identity and existence, becoming a ghost or memory. The narrator fades from reality, losing coherence and presence.","opus":"Reznor's account of dissolution, a self so eroded that belief, memory and even the color of the sky no longer hold. What remains is a ghost drifting out of sight, everything of who he was washed away into nothing.","sonnet":"A song about the erosion of identity and belief — everything once trusted turns out to be false, the self is fading from view, and what remains is barely a presence at all. The speaker is lost in a grey, burned-out landscape where nothing of the former self survives.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~not-anymore":{"haiku":"A song about being frozen by fear and silenced, yet insisting on personal identity. The speaker resists transformation into someone else and clings to self-knowledge, even as they struggle to wake from a nightmare state.","opus":"A person realizes they were turning into someone else and living on false hope, and now, frozen with fear and unable to wake up, they cling to their sense of who they are and vow it won't happen anymore.","sonnet":"The narrator describes being paralysed and unable to wake up — once certain of his identity and purpose, he now finds himself frozen, unable to shake the sense that everything he believed and worked toward has slipped away.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about a self watching itself get overwritten and mistaking that surrender for resolve. The refrain reverses meaning as it repeats: first it swears the old certainties are gone, then it becomes the verdict that the speaker himself is gone. Verse two stages a body that cannot move or speak, jaw sealed and limbs locked rigid, while the voice keeps vowing it still knows who it is. That vow is the mask, and the outro is what lies beneath it, the insistence eroding into a loop of being unable to wake, where identity persists only as a reflex that no longer believes itself.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"identity persisting only as a reflex that no longer believes itself","built":"the refrain reverses meaning as it repeats, from certainties gone to self gone"},
"nine-inch-nails~not-so-pretty-now":{"haiku":"A superstar's fall from grace as their facade crumbles and they fade into irrelevance, leaving behind only the shell of someone who once seemed untouchable.","opus":"A mocking address to a self-styled superstar consumed by greed and hiding, whose brightness is fading; the speakers coldly predict the person will soon be gone, forgotten, and no longer admired.","sonnet":"A cold, mocking address to someone whose self-aggrandising facade has collapsed — a person who hid flaws behind greed and performance, believed themselves a superstar, and is now fading and broken, no longer able to sustain the image that once defined them.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~nothing-can-stop-me-now":{"haiku":"Betrayed and broken, the speaker embraces nihilism—apathy becomes freedom.","opus":"Reznor spitting at the pig-god of authority and abandonment, a man so wrecked and hollowed by betrayal that not caring anymore reads as freedom. The refrain is bleak liberation: nothing can stop someone who has already lost everything to lose.","sonnet":"Bitter liberation through total emotional detachment — addressing an abusive or controlling figure as 'pig,' the narrator describes being broken and abandoned, then arriving at a nihilistic freedom: because they no longer care about anything, nothing can hurt or stop them.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~only":{"haiku":"Isolation and realization that a relationship was imaginary—created by the speaker themselves as a vehicle for self-inflicted emotional harm.","opus":"A song about dissolving identity and self-isolation, in which the speaker feels himself fading and abstract, concludes that the other person was never real but something he invented to hurt himself, and realizes there is only himself.","sonnet":"The speaker recognizes that a significant other may have been a self-constructed fiction used as a tool for self-inflicted pain, and concludes by acknowledging that forcing their way through a sealed internal doorway has revealed something ugly on the inside.","src":"haiku","fable":"A mind narrates its own dissolution and finds, at the bottom, a solipsist's triumph. It moves in three stages — first the self going out of focus, able to look straight through its own body; then the revelation that the other person was a fabrication built solely to inflict harm, dismantled with a chillingly satisfied confirmation that it worked; then the closing image of a scab worried loose until it opens into a doorway, and climbing through to somewhere he was never meant to be. The chorus escalates from a flat erasure of the other into a snarling one, and that escalation is the whole arc in miniature: nothing outside the skull was ever real, the self is all that remains, and what it finally glimpses within is not pretty. Reznor turns his usual self-laceration inward until the wound is the last reliable proof of existence.","fv":2,"fnote":"The video was David Fincher’s first collaboration with the band — the song rendered as a desk coming to life, the face surfacing in a pin-art toy, built almost entirely in CGI by Digital Domain.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind"],"means":"self-laceration turns inward until the wound is the last proof of existence","built":"chorus escalates from flat erasure of the other to a snarling one, the whole arc compressed"},
"nine-inch-nails~over-and-out":{"haiku":"Time runs out while waiting for something undefined; the speaker experiences cyclical déjà vu and suspects they've always been ahead of the person they remember.","opus":"Time drains away while the speaker admits he cannot name what he waits for, caught in a recurrence that feels like memory misfiring. The refrain of always being a decade ahead reads as both boast and curse. Late Trent Reznor lets the ambient repetition do the work, dissolving forward motion into a loop that never resolves.","sonnet":"A consciousness locked in cyclical paralysis watches time drain away while the same unresolved loop replays. It senses it has always been ahead of something it cannot name — yet urgency produces no motion, only the compulsive recognition of repetition.","src":"haiku","fable":"Closing the 2016-18 trilogy, this is a song whose form is its meaning: a handful of phrases get shuffled and re-dealt until sequence dissolves and the loop itself becomes the subject. That recombination performs the very disorientation it describes, so the recurring sense of already having lived this moment reads less as recollection than as a mind stuck inside one repeating instant. The lone image is a countdown draining toward nothing named, and the boast of standing a decade past someone curdles into irony, since the lead buys no exit, only the same crossing rounded again. What resists paraphrase is doing exact labor: there is no resolution on offer, just the winding-down itself, an ending that dwells in the fatigue of the wait rather than releasing it.","fv":2,"themes":["time running out","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the spiral ends in fatigue of waiting rather than release","built":"phrase recombination performs the disorientation it describes, form enacting content"},
"nine-inch-nails~physical-you-re-so":{"haiku":"Sensual desire overwhelms rationality; physical attraction transcends intellectual compatibility and becomes all-consuming.","opus":"A cover of Adrian Borland's song about wanting someone tenderly—to wine and dine them—while being pushed away by their purely physical demands. Desire and frustration collide as intimacy is reduced to something too physical to bear.","sonnet":"A raw, direct song about overwhelming physical desire for someone — the speaker wants them in every sensory way, in bed, on their breath, against their skin, but the other person is too physically intense, too much body and heat, for the speaker to handle; desire and overstimulation collapse into each other.","src":"haiku","fable":"A come-on that keeps flinching from what it wants, the song runs a suave pickup script only to jam on the confession that the other person is too much body to bear. The verses do the smooth work of pursuit, staging the dinner and the dancing, a locked chamber of a heart handed over, appetite dressed up as courtship. Then the chorus refuses to finish the seduction, snagging on the charge of raw bodiliness and never getting past it. Rerouting Adam Ant's arch pop through Broken's abrasion, Reznor lets the refrain rot into stutter, expletive, and flat denial. The suave mask peels off to reveal panic, hunger curdling into dread of the very contact it kept begging for.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"desire curdling into dread at the moment of contact","built":"the chorus snagging into stutter enacts the flinch it describes"},
"nine-inch-nails~piggy":{"fable":"This is Trent Reznor's The Downward Spiral (1994), and Piggy sits early on the album's arc of self-destruction. The pig is the addressed lover-turned-betrayer, but the song is really about the moment abandonment curdles into liberation-by-collapse: the litany of bruises, fractures and being deserted hardens into a numb refrain that reframes not-caring as a kind of terrible power. The chorus's flat vow that nobody can hold him back anymore is the mask, bravado, over what's underneath: someone whose plans and hopes have failed so completely that the only freedom left is having nothing to lose, which is why the track famously disintegrates into Reznor's untethered live-drum breakdown, the composure of the verses giving way to the chaos the album will chase to its end.","fableDeep":"The address is armor: the childish taunt of the title diminishes the departed before the verses admit what leaving cost — fears come true, bruising, broken bones, solitude — mockery and wound alternating line by line. The acid reframe 'my little piggy needed something new' converts her departure into appetite, betrayal explained as a pig wanting fresh slop. The second verse briefly lowers the guard entirely: plans failed, understanding requested, sanity lost because of her — the only honest paragraph before the armor reseals. The chorus plants the album's load-bearing motif: nothing can stop him now because caring has stopped, apathy as propulsion, its cadence deliberately ambiguous between liberation and threat — and the outro repeats it past twenty times until the words hollow out into pure momentum, which is the point: what no longer hurts no longer brakes. Thesis: the record's engine turns over here — indifference introduced as freedom, already sounding like a lit fuse.","haiku":"Betrayal and abandonment from someone close leave the speaker broken and indifferent to consequences, repeating a refrain of hopeless detachment.","opus":"Addressed to a person derisively called a pig, the song voices the pain of being left broken and alone by them, hardening into a numb, reckless refrain of no longer caring.","sonnet":"The speaker addresses someone who abandoned him, leaving him broken and alone, and finds that the loss and betrayal have pushed him past caring — arriving at a hollow, damaged indifference rather than recovery.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance"],"means":"indifference introduced as freedom, already sounding like a lit fuse","built":"the outro repeats the chorus past twenty times until the words hollow into momentum"},
"nine-inch-nails~please":{"haiku":"Cycle of unfulfilled need where nothing can fill the void. Pain repeats and wounds reopen; internal hunger consumes reality itself in a desperate plea for completion.","opus":"A depiction of an insatiable craving that can never fill the emptiness inside, where flesh and sin once meant everything. The narrator begs to be completed even as he realizes the world, and the hole in him, was all in his head.","sonnet":"An obsessive craving—possibly for a substance, a person, or a destructive act—never satisfies the void it is meant to fill; each attempt only opens a new wound elsewhere. The speaker begs to be completed while acknowledging the cycle will never end.","src":"haiku","fable":"A track deep in The Fragile's right disc, sung from inside a chemical dependency that has swallowed the self whole and started begging it for meaning. The narrator wants the sinking, the drowning, the loss of time; relief is precisely what he dreads, because the high is the only thing offering to make him complete. The post-chorus mantra is the whole engine of addiction stated as arithmetic: no dose fills the void, so every dose must be repeated forever. Verse two watches the syringe's blood bloom into the plunger and admits the cruel accountancy of the fix, one hole plugged while another opens, until the grand apocalyptic clarity he reaches is exposed as a hallucination staged entirely in his own head. What sounds like a plea for love or salvation is really the void addressing the drug, and the horror is that it means every word.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the void addressing the drug as though it were a lover","built":"post-chorus arithmetic states the loop: no dose fills the void so every dose repeats"},
"nine-inch-nails~purest-feeling":{"haiku":"Intense desire and obsession consume the speaker; they seek vulnerability and connection through another person, who becomes either their salvation or damnation.","opus":"An early Reznor sketch of obsession dressed as devotion, where another person becomes both salvation and punishment, and the addict's certainty that he can't live without her curdles the romance into dependency.","sonnet":"Obsessive early infatuation: the narrator is completely consumed by a woman, convinced she is either his salvation or his punishment, and unable to live without her even as he suspects she doesn't fully understand what she does to him.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~reptile":{"fable":"The chorus is a paradox held without flinching, the woman crowned and defiled in the same four lines, addressed as an object of worship and a thing that sickens him without any seam between the two, so devotion and disgust arrive fused in every breath. But the second verse quietly relocates the rot: she may be pictured splitting open to insects and running cold blood beneath her skin, yet it is his own touch that poisons the angels, and he admits the real errand, that he spreads the sickness deliberately to buy himself a moment's relief from being alone. So the reptile of the title is his, not hers, she is only the screen for a self-loathing that has discovered its own bottomlessness and, worse, found it liberating. The final line about his own defilement is no lament, it is the surrender of a man who has quit pretending otherwise.","fableDeep":"The verses engineer disgust — insects invited in, honey trails, reptile blood under the skin, the residue of countless others — a portrait built to repel, which the chorus then embraces with paired possessive epithets: 'my beautiful liar, my precious whore,' worship and degradation fused in the grammar of ownership, disease claimed like a pet name. The theological inflation runs throughout — devils prophesying her manifestations, angels bleeding at his caress — a toxic affair scaled up to cosmology so that neither party is merely human and neither is responsible. The diagnostic line is the confession that matters: the 'need to contaminate, to alleviate this loneliness' — infection as the only intimacy he trusts, the depths he'll reach declared limitless. And the chorus's last word turns the indictment home: after three lines cataloguing her corruption, 'I am so impure' — the venom was his to begin with. Thesis: a lover prosecuted for impurity by a narrator whose every piece of evidence convicts himself.","haiku":"Toxic and contaminating sexuality, where desire spreads infection and corruption, mixing beauty with disease and degradation.","opus":"A dark, obsessive portrait of a woman cast as a deceiving lover and whore, and the narrator's tainted attraction to her. He describes needing to contaminate to ease his loneliness, calling her his disease and confessing his own impurity.","sonnet":"An obsessive, self-aware attraction to a dangerous, parasitic woman: the narrator recognises her corrupting, reptilian nature but is drawn in by the need to contaminate and be contaminated, knowing it makes him impure.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"the venom turns out to be his, the prosecution convicts the prosecutor","built":"worship and degradation fused in the grammar of paired possessive epithets"},
"nine-inch-nails~right-where-it-belongs":{"haiku":"Reality and perception questioned as potentially illusory, exploring whether individual consciousness and identity are merely dreams.","opus":"A series of unsettling questions about whether reality is just an elaborate dream and the world exists only inside one's own head. It probes the fear of looking through the cracks and finding oneself utterly alone.","sonnet":"A quiet but unsettling examination of constructed reality: the speaker asks whether the world, the self, and perceived safety are all illusions or mental projections, and whether most people are too afraid to look through the cracks and see what — or who — is really there.","src":"haiku","fable":"The album's closer turns on the listener directly, dismantling the comfortable life it has watched the protagonist build by asking whether any of it — the safety, the reflection, the very enemy locked in his cage — was ever real or just a construction of his own head. Reznor withholds the answer and hands the choice over: you can keep the illusion or peer past the fault lines in the picture and confront your solitude, alone with your invented gods and devils. Its quietness is the weapon, closing a record about power and paranoia by suggesting the whole war was staged inside one skull.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the whole war revealed as staged inside one skull","built":"quiet closes a record about power by dissolving its conflict into the listener's head"},
"nine-inch-nails~right-where-it-belongs-version-2":{"haiku":"Reality dissolves into subjective hallucination; nothing exists outside the mind, and isolation becomes absolute.","opus":"A quiet, unsettling meditation asking whether the safe, orderly life you've built is real at all, or an elaborate dream and a cage of your own making. It suggests every devil, god and other person might be a creation of your own isolated mind.","sonnet":"An invitation to doubt the reality of the constructed self and the life built around it — the animal in the cage, the hollow heart, the comfortable illusion. Everything you think you know might be a dream of your own making; your devils and gods, the living and dead, might all be interior. The fear is not that the world is fake but that you'd be afraid to look.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~ringfinger":{"haiku":"A devotion so consuming it borders on self-destruction; the speaker works until bleeding for another who demands sacrifice and control, sealed by promises carved in pain.","opus":"An unbalanced, worshipful obsession curdles into imagery of ritual sacrifice, the narrator laboring until he's raw yet feeling he'll always fall short of what she wants, left crucified as an offering for her wrongdoing. His demand that she offer up her own flesh, sealing a permanent pledge, casts total submission and self-mutilation as the price of possessing even a fraction of her, closing on a disturbing private admission.","sonnet":"A portrait of obsessive devotion pushed to self-destruction — the speaker works themselves to the bone for someone who gives nothing back, fixating on a wedding-ring promise and demanding their partner cut off a piece of themselves as proof of commitment.","src":"haiku","fable":"Closing Pretty Hate Machine, Trent Reznor's 1989 debut, this is a love song built entirely from the vocabulary of servitude and sacrifice, where the beloved is worshipped as a deity who demands proof. The devotion escalates through a single grim image-system: labor until the hands bleed gives way to marriage figured not as a ring but as the severed finger that would wear it, then to the singer nailed up like a martyr dying for someone else's sins. Beneath the language of consecration and promise runs something closer to a hostage's logic, since possessing even a piece of the other is framed as getting everything he wants, and the eyes are bandaged so that submission need not see what it has agreed to. The final coda, printed but never sung, drops the sacred register entirely for an exhausted, sleepless, private confession, and in doing so exposes the whole grand vocabulary of worship as a mask over plain obsessive fixation. The album that opened with rage against God ends by making a lover into one, and revealing that the difference was never as large as it looked.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"worship escalated until devotion and self-mutilation become the same offer","built":"a closing coda printed but never sung drops the sacred register for plain obsessive confession"},
"nine-inch-nails~ruiner":{"fable":"An address to a false messiah—a demagogue or abuser who gathered believers on charisma and lies, feeding his followers their own poison while ruining everything he touched. Reznor keeps the accusation second-person and almost sneering until the second verse cracks open: the real wound is that something was taken from inside the speaker, corrupting a place he swore was off-limits forever, and the scars from it are worn now like a permanent ring. The mocking interrogation of the chorus (how did you grow so powerful?) curdles into recognition—the speaker finally sees what the ruiner actually is. Then the outro flips the whole thing into defiance, the survivor's mantra repeated past the point of belief: the flat denial that the ruiner left any mark is precisely how you know it did, the boast growing more hollow the more times it loops.","fableDeep":"Prosecutorial from the first line — the needling refrain 'didn't you? didn't you?' cross-examines a figure drawn as cult leader and con man, charisma rendered as infection: cattle deceived, flies fed his filth, the blind kept blind. The bridge deflates the demon with four mocking questions — how'd you get so big, so strong, so hard, so long — phallic ridicule shrinking cosmic evil down to compensating anatomy. The album's most naked admission hides mid-verse in conditionals: maybe a part of him was taken somewhere he hoped it would never go, and maybe that damaged him more than the ruiner will ever know — abuse speakable only as hypothesis, each maybe a flinch. Then the inversion: 'my perfect ring of scars' claims the damage as insignia, evidence worn as decoration, and the long outro chants that nothing hurt and nothing can stop him — far past the point of credibility, denial hammered into armor by sheer repetition. Thesis: a survivor's victory speech whose refusal to admit pain is the wound showing.","haiku":"Someone with infectious power deceives and corrupts others, gathering followers while destroying everything pure they touch.","opus":"A furious address to a figure who corrupts and takes everything, part abuser, part false idol. Reznor lands it on defiance: whatever damage was done, the survivor claims it can no longer wound him, converting scars into an unstoppable resolve.","sonnet":"The narrator addresses a manipulative, predatory figure who deceived and corrupted everyone around him — including the narrator — and ends by asserting, with increasing desperation, that none of the damage actually hurt him.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","faith & the occult"],"means":"a survivor's victory speech whose denial is the wound showing","built":"the outro chants immunity past the point of credibility, denial hammered into armor","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~ruiner-demo":{"haiku":"A narcissist corrupts and deceives those around them with infectious lies, destroying innocence while remaining unchecked by consequence.","opus":"An early version of the Downward Spiral track confronting a destroyer, part external oppressor and part internalized abuser, who deceives his followers and corrupts everything he touches. The narrator swings between accusation and the scarred realization the Ruiner damaged him deeply. Reznor's rage sharpens into defiance, refusing to let the collector-infector claim the last pure thing.","sonnet":"A portrait of a manipulator — the Ruiner — who collects, infects, deceives his followers ('the cattle he deceives'), rapes the innocent, and leaves the narrator contaminated. The final image is bitter inversion: 'the only pure thing left in my fucking world is wearing your disease,' meaning love or attachment has become the vector of the Ruiner's corruption.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~running":{"haiku":"A song about inescapable pursuit and surveillance. The speaker attempts to hide from watchers who are always aligned against them, but eventually realizes there is nowhere left to run—they have been caught.","opus":"A frightened, cut-off narrator shadows someone he longs for while feeling ceaselessly observed by unseen hunters. He confesses that his hiding spots are dwindling and resigns himself to the certainty that escape is impossible.","sonnet":"A paranoid, claustrophobic song about being watched and hunted — the narrator follows someone for comfort while sensing that unseen forces are always closing in, and he is running out of places to hide.","src":"haiku","fable":"A surveillance-panic song that stages its own depletion, letting form do the work a confession would: the harder the speaker insists there is no escape, the less language he has left to say it with. The hook stacks clipped two-word verdicts, each rhyme snapping shut like a lock, a paranoid ledger of pursuers who are behind him, blinded, in lockstep against him. The chorus then narrows to a single sentence hammered until it stops meaning and becomes pure pressure, and the bridge shears even that in half, a fragment repeated past sense toward exhaustion. What opens as a man trailing someone just to feel less alone ends as a countdown with nothing left to count. On Hesitation Marks, Reznor's clean-and-sober record, the pursuer is plainly internal, and the hiding places vanish because there was never anywhere outside the self to flee to.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","surveillance & control","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"language depletes itself in proportion to the claimed escape","built":"the bridge shears the chorus to a fragment repeated past sense toward exhaustion"},
"nine-inch-nails~sanctified":{"haiku":"A man is caught in an obsessive, consuming relationship where he surrenders everything to his lover's touch and words, experiencing both transcendence and shame. His family letter confesses he is helpless against her spell even as he knows it destroys him.","opus":"Obsessive, degrading craving dressed in the language of worship, the narrator claiming redemption and holiness through a lover who leads him into the gentlest corners of damnation. An inserted farewell letter to his parents reveals the destination of the compulsion: he keeps surrendering to something that's killing him, powerless to resist even at the cost of his life.","sonnet":"An obsessive sexual and emotional dependence on a woman who the narrator knows is destroying him — she makes him feel purified and sanctified through sex while her hold on him shames him to his core. The spoken-word letter to his parents mid-song makes clear he knows he's in freefall and can't stop.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man reroutes the whole vocabulary of religious salvation onto a woman who runs him like a drug, calling himself justified, purified and sanctified while she leads him steadily downward. The chorus is the trick in miniature: every word is churchly, yet each resolves into a body rather than into grace, so being saved and being possessed collapse into one act. What drives him is compulsion, not desire, an addict's loop where he prays daily for release and then does it all again the moment he can. The spoken interlude tears the mask off by shifting into a farewell letter to his parents, revealing the swagger as shame and the worship as a description of a man dissolving. On Nine Inch Nails' 1989 debut Pretty Hate Machine, that structural break is the point, and the hidden coda's blade and arriving kingdom read his own ruin as the only deliverance left.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","suicide"],"means":"sacred vocabulary colonised entirely by compulsion","built":"spoken interlude drops the swagger into a farewell letter"},
"nine-inch-nails~satellite":{"haiku":"Surveillance and loss of privacy. A satellite watches and monitors every word and thought, is always present, and represents an inescapable system of control embedded in consciousness.","opus":"A vision of total surveillance, embodied as an orbiting eye that never blinks, catches everything said, burrows into your thoughts, and always outpaces anyone hoping to slip its reach.","sonnet":"The song is told from the perspective of a pervasive surveillance entity — a satellite that monitors every word, claims to be inside the listener's head, and asserts it is always one step ahead, evoking a world of total information control.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song sung by the surveillance apparatus to its target, where the machine's possessiveness is indistinguishable from tenderness. The verses gather the paranoia of 2013 into that cooing voice: data clawing its trails overhead, each syllable logged, the true and the false impossible to separate. Its refrain claims not merely to watch but to inhabit, to reach thought before you think it, until observation becomes a kind of merger. Then the outro inverts the address, and now the human strains upward toward the far, cold thing in orbit. On Hesitation Marks, Reznor scores this to a sleek funk groove rather than industrial menace, so the total gaze arrives frictionless and almost seductive. What lies under the mask of control is dependency: watching confessed as a form of longing for something kept always beyond reach.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"the surveillance apparatus confessing its gaze as longing","built":"the outro inverts the address so the human strains toward the cold orbiting thing"},
"nine-inch-nails~she-s-gone-away": {"fable":"A grief song rotted down to its physiology: the narrator claws at wounds until his hands split and can name no reason the dead woman was ever in his life, recollection dissolving even as he compulsively picks at the loss. Reznor stages the body as a horror-set — an orifice tearing open from within, a witness present at the exact moment of death, a slit drawn down cold flesh that restores no sensation — so the real catastrophe is anesthesia, the numbness that outlives both the person and the event. The chant that gives the track its title works less as mourning than as a stuck groove, the one fact a shattered mind can still grip while everything around it decays; and the whisper that closes the song, addressed to whatever remains, tips the whole thing from lament into haunting, unsure whether the speaker or his ghost is the one still occupying the room. It is late Reznor pulling the industrial dread of his early records into something quieter and more clinical — the terror of a self that can no longer confirm it is there.","fableDeep":"Eight lines of verse do enormous, ugly work. 'You dig in places til your fingers bleed' opens on compulsion — grave-digging, scratching, exhumation of memory all at once — and its pair, spreading 'the infection where you spill your seed', fuses contagion with sex, grief transmitted like disease through intimacy. Then the memory collapse, devastating for its smallness: 'I can't remember what she came here for' — not failing to remember her, but the errand, the reason, the last mundane fact — widening immediately into not remembering much of anything. The second verse converts witness into implication: 'I was watching on the day she died' admits presence without action, and the necrotic tending that follows — licking while the skin turns black, cutting along the length — reads as nursing something already dead, because 'you can't get the feeling back' means both the numb flesh and the unrecoverable past. The refrain is pure attrition, gone repeated until it stops being news and becomes weather. It is about grief that rots instead of healing, the mourner working the corpse of memory because even infection beats numbness.","haiku":"A woman has died and disappeared; the speaker obsesses over her death, digging into wounds that won't heal, unable to recover the lost feeling.","opus":"A ghastly funeral crawl about grief so complete it corrodes memory and self, the speaker fixated on decay and unable to hold onto the details of who he's lost. Set to necrotic, faintly carnal imagery, the recurring lament for a vanished woman conjures a mourning that has rotted him from the inside out. Nine Inch Nails render it as slow, oppressive dread.","sonnet":"A viscerally disturbing song about a woman's death — witnessed, possibly caused — and the speaker's inability to remember her or feel anything afterward; the imagery of cutting and licking decaying flesh suggests both grief and complicity.","src":"haiku","fable2":"The most grief-blank thing Trent Reznor has recorded, this is a slow dirge sung by a mind that has already gone rotten around the loss it can no longer name. The refrain is the whole point: a five-word incantation repeated past sense until it stops meaning she left and starts meaning the speaker cannot hold onto anything, the words themselves decaying like the imagery of black skin, opened mouths, and wounds that will not close their feeling back in. That image-system, digging and licking and cutting at a body that gives nothing back, is the song's real subject, mourning as a compulsive worrying of a wound rather than any account of the woman herself, whose absence has hollowed out even the memory of why she mattered. On the 2016 EP the track functions as the album's tar pit, its Twin Peaks bleakness confirming that this Nine Inch Nails is less interested in rage than in the flat, forgetting numbness that outlasts it.","fnote":"This is the song David Lynch conjured: he sent back NIN’s first Twin Peaks submission asking for something more aggressive and ugly that would make his hair stand on end, and Reznor turned this around in about a day — then performed it onstage at the Roadhouse in Part 8 of The Return.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness","madness & the mind"],"means":"the numbness that outlives both the person and the event of losing them","built":"the title chant works as a stuck groove, the one fact a shattered mind can grip"},
"nine-inch-nails~shit-mirror":{"haiku":"A transformation into something monstrous through the consumption of others' hatred and fear. The speaker becomes increasingly unrecognizable, feeding on loathing while mutating into an alien creature.","opus":"A person senses himself transforming into someone alien and no longer recognizable, and welcomes the shift even as he lets slip that he has done something monstrous, leaving a body behind in a washroom. The horror and the exhilaration sit side by side.","sonnet":"The narrator undergoes a disturbing personal transformation — acquiring new power and appetite while feeding on others' hatred — and observes with unease that he is becoming something unrecognizable, culminating in a violent act he admits he knew was wrong all along.","src":"haiku","fable":"A predator mistakes his own moral rot for evolution, selling atrocity to himself as an upgrade. The song's engine is denial: the verses grow specific and damning, one admitting a body left behind and a threshold he always knew he would breach, while the chorus answers with the same four euphoric words on loop, chanting that the change is fine. That repetition is the point. As the confession sharpens, the mantra hardens and refuses to break, so the swagger reads not as triumph but as a man drowning out his reflection. Opening Bad Witch, it sets the record's whole con: rebirth pitched as glory when it is really a monster congratulating himself on having become one.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"denial hardens into mantra precisely as the confession sharpens","built":"four euphoric words loop unbroken while the verses grow specific and damning"},
"nine-inch-nails~sin":{"haiku":"Betrayal and violation in an intimate relationship where purity was stolen and the speaker is used as a vessel for someone else's control.","opus":"A relationship framed as violation, where the narrator's purity was stolen and he sees himself as an effigy to be disgraced and replaced. He recognizes the compromise, the kiss and fist getting under his skin, and takes in the extent of his own sin.","sonnet":"A relationship defined by violation and control: the speaker gave up something pure and had it stolen, and now oscillates between anger at the other person and a self-flagellating acceptance of punishment and degradation.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is submission to a cruel lover staged as religious defilement, the speaker offering up his innocence and naming the whole transaction a trespass he cannot stop craving. Its grammar is one of exchange: every verse opens with what the other person hands him, so even his rage and his nerve are on loan, and his purity is something taken rather than freely spent. The devotional imagery curdles, the smoke and residue of worship gone sour, until he is only a wrecked idol to be spat on and replaced, still begging for a scrap of the abuse when he can no longer have all of it. On this 1989 debut it is a pure distillation of the Reznor formula, self-loathing and want fused so tightly that a caress and a blow arrive as one gesture. The tell is the ending, where the refrain doubles back and refuses to close, the loop enacting an addiction the words only describe.","fv":2,"fnote":"The video — Reznor bound, bagged and spun in a gyroscope, intercut with a pair of male lovers — was widely banned and, by his account, never finished the way he wanted; he blamed the label’s interference, part of the TVT fight that hardened into his demand for total control ever after.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","faith & the occult"],"means":"devotion and self-loathing fused into one craving","built":"the refrain loops back and refuses to close, enacting addiction"},
"nine-inch-nails~slipping-away":{"haiku":"Identity fragments and dissolves despite attempts at self-rescue; the repeated failure to save oneself creates a cycle of disintegration.","opus":"Selfhood erodes through sheer reiteration, the admission of slipping and the failed effort to save himself repeated until the words wear down to fragments. Reznor uses accumulation rather than argument, so that the loss the lyric describes is enacted by the form, each identical line another handful of self escaping the grip that tried to hold it.","sonnet":"A self-hood quietly dissolves, the speaker watching their own identity recede without being able to halt it. Every effort at self-preservation fails and the attempting itself becomes the only remaining content — an eroding will repeating its own erosion.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~something-i-can-never-have":{"haiku":"Grieves an impossible love that lingers as memory and obsession, filling every moment with longing for someone unreachable. The song frames desire as all-consuming and inescapable.","opus":"An anguished pining for a departed lover who once made life bearable, now shrunk to memories that flood his sleep and torment his waking hours. Pared down to a single desperate craving, he unnerves himself with how badly he wants a person permanently out of reach.","sonnet":"An obsessive longing for someone who once gave life meaning but is now permanently out of reach — everywhere the speaker looks the person is present as a reminder of who they used to be, and the knowledge that this connection can never be recovered becomes an unbearable weight.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a hymn of dependence that keeps its object deliberately blurred, so a departed lover and a numbing habit collapse into one craving that is defined by being unreachable. On a debut otherwise armored in machine noise, Reznor strips almost everything away here, and the bareness is the point: the verses are pure sensory residue, a lingering flavor of grief, a voice that will not fall silent, dreams that keep surfacing until rest becomes impossible. The plea for this presence to erase all his pain reads less as rescue than as a wish for oblivion, and the confession that he has been whittled to a single need, frightened now of what he is becoming, is where the mask of romance slips off the addiction beneath. Across three verses the target quietly migrates from the beloved to a mirror, until what he truly mourns is not the other person but the earlier self he can no longer be. The endlessly repeated closing lets the wanting outlast every possible answer, because the lack itself is all that is left to hold.","fv":2,"themes":["objectless craving","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"mourning not the other person but the earlier self he can no longer be","built":"the target quietly migrates across three verses from the beloved to a mirror"},
"nine-inch-nails~somewhat-damaged":{"fable":"The overture to The Fragile opens not on rage but on the aftermath of it, and Reznor stages the collapse in two distinct registers. The first half is Icarus scripture: a scaffold of end-rhymed couplets, tight and self-lacerating, cataloguing a burnout with the clenched control of someone who still needs the wreckage to sound like a poem. Then the whole thing detonates and the second half abandons the rhyme scheme for direct address, and what emerges from under the industrial armor is startlingly small and human: a man asking an absent someone where they were when he came apart. That is the real subject beneath the self-disgust posturing of the snarl. The bravado of being too ruined to feel anything is the mask, and the wounded, repeated question about a broken promise is the thing the mask exists to hide.","fableDeep":"The arc is Icarus compressed into one quatrain — so impressed, tried to be like you, flew too high, burnt the wing — and the fall costs him faith in everything, not just the idol. The first half is entirely self-directed rot (obsolete machine, fountain of decay); then the form breaks into that long unrhymed rant and finally produces the missing character: the you who promised. The title's 'somewhat' is gallows understatement, and the true target is the broken guarantee — 'everything that swore it wouldn't change is different now' — betrayal measured like physics, as the failure of things that were supposed to be constants. Whether the you is a person or God is left open on purpose; the charge is the same: where were you.","haiku":"Cumulative disappointment and psychological collapse from unmet promises.","opus":"Bitter emotional collapse and abandonment, feeling broken and poisoned after idolizing someone who was absent when needed most.","sonnet":"Collapse into self-destruction and emotional ruin after being abandoned by someone who promised to be there.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the wounded question about a broken promise is what the bravado hides","built":"rhyme scheme holds tight through self-laceration then detonates into unrhymed direct address","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~starfuckers-inc":{"haiku":"A critique of shallow celebrity worship and the sell-out mentality of those who sacrifice integrity for fame and power.","opus":"A scathing takedown of shallow celebrity and social climbing, mocking a vain, soul-selling idol worshipped like a god. The narrator admits his own insincerity, playing along only as long as it serves him.","sonnet":"A savage portrait of celebrity worship and shameless self-promotion, skewering people who cultivate fame through insincerity, flattery, and calculated image-making.","src":"haiku","fable":"A satire built as a first-person confession, worn like a costume: Reznor writes in the voice of the fame-parasite himself, a climber whose god is a celebrity commodity — worshipped from the limousine, sold in cellophane, mistaken for scripture. The genius is that the mask is total. There's no narrator standing outside to condemn; the speaker cheerfully brags about his own insincerity, his transactional loyalty, his willingness to trade any dignity for access, until the sales-pitch grammar (the corporate Inc. of the title) reveals that selling out has been fully industrialized. The bridge swerves into the Carly Simon vanity taunt to snap the mirror around — the whole diatribe is a machine for making you wonder which industry figure it targets, and the joke is that everyone who assumes it's about them is right. The outro delivers the awful payoff quietly: having done all this, he has finally been admitted, inducted into the elect, granted a place among the anointed and the gorgeous — the satire's cruelest note is that the corruption works.","fv":2,"fnote":"The guessing game had a famous answer-shaped hole — everyone read it as aimed at Marilyn Manson, who then co-starred in its video, smashing trophies beside Reznor: the feud's end staged as the punchline.","themes":["artifice & performance","money & the street","faith & the occult"],"means":"corruption narrated approvingly because the corruption works","built":"the corporate Inc. in the title signals that selling out is fully industrialized"},
"nine-inch-nails~starfuckers-inc-remix-version-as":{"haiku":"Beauty used for narcissistic absorption and possession; the worshippers consume the worshipped as commodity.","opus":"Reznor's caustic swipe at fame-chasing celebrity culture and the sycophants who chase stardom, mocking hollow beauty while declaring himself hatred rather than adulation. A sneering satire of the industry's vanity, this remix pounds its contempt into a relentless, taunting chant aimed at the self-serving worshippers of empty glamour.","sonnet":"A savage takedown of celebrity worship and the hollow pursuit of fame, the speaker cataloguing the beautiful and the powerful before rejecting the entire machinery — \"I am hatred\" rather than beautiful — in a flash of self-aware fury.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~starfuckers-inc-remix-version-cc":{"haiku":"Commodified celebrity worship and false gods wrapped in material excess and corporate packaging.","opus":"A venomous swipe at celebrity worship and manufactured idols, gods delivered in cellophane and limousines. Reznor skewering the fame machine and the hangers-on who feed it, disgust aimed as much at himself and rivals as the industry.","sonnet":"Celebrity worship as religious transaction — God arrives in a limousine, wrapped in cellophane. Only the opening fragment is present here; the lyric is too incomplete to build a full reading.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~starfuckers-inc-remix-version-do":{"haiku":"Celebrity worship transforms the devout into manufactured images, trading authenticity for shallow fame and false belonging.","opus":"A savage satire of celebrity vanity and sold-out ambition, the narrator playing insincerity as a game and worshipping shallow fame as his god; the sly borrowed line about thinking the song is about you turns the whole industry's ego back on itself.","sonnet":"Celebrity worship as religious transaction — God arrives in a limousine, wrapped in cellophane, on a magazine cover, trading insincerity for belonging. The narrator inhabits the persona of the star-fucker with ironic relish ('I sold my soul but don't you dare call me a whore'), indicting the whole machinery of fame while performing participation in it.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~still":{"haiku":"A consciousness mechanized and transformed into circuitry loses its humanity, trapped in an inescapable digital prison of its own making.","opus":"A haunted acoustic reworking from the Still sessions, tracing the self dissolving into machinery — the man others knew now scarred over, built of circuitry, feeling nothing yet somehow unafraid. The plea for a lover to hold tighter as he slips away is its aching human core. Reznor on numbness, decay and the noise that wants him gone.","sonnet":"Self-dissolution into machinery: the old self — the one who had feelings and second thoughts — has rotted away, replaced by wires, leaving the narrator physically present but unreachable. The noise inside the head becomes the only remaining reality, named over and over as the thing that wants him dead.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~suck":{"haiku":"Sexual addiction and obsession portrayed as a compulsive force, where the speaker experiences shame and degradation while unable to resist.","opus":"A song of addiction and self-destruction cast in religious and sexual terms, where god is absent and the sickness is self-inflicted. The narrator feels dirty inside and wants to do terrible things.","sonnet":"A song about compulsive desire and self-disgust: the speaker is drawn irresistibly toward a figure who fuses pleasure with a corrosive sense of inner filth, comparing the craving to addiction and blasphemously to religious ecstasy.","src":"haiku","fable":"Closing the Broken EP as a bonus-track rework of a song Reznor first cut with Pigface, Suck fuses addiction and religious hunger into a single act of self-consumption, staring down a heaven that has gone dark and choosing the needle as its replacement sacrament. Its central move is a swapped-out communion: the temple is now made of flesh, the vein becomes the altar, and the speaker crowns himself a savior high on ecstasy who promises healing while running the drug up his arm until it scratches him raw from the inside. The chorus refuses to describe the high and just demands to know how it feels before collapsing into the title's blunt monosyllable, so the craving speaks in the flat imperative of a body that no longer bothers to justify itself. When the bridge strips everything back to a single repeated confession of inner filth, capped by a lone refusal, the mask of the ecstatic messiah drops and the shame it was covering shows through. The outro's escalating count of lips and tongues and throats finally makes the appetite bottomless rather than intimate, ending not on satisfaction but on the will to inflict, so the song reads addiction, worship, and sex as one hunger that can only ever consume and never be filled.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"appetite that can only ever consume and never be filled","built":"communion replaced by vein-as-altar, the chorus demanding sensation rather than describing it","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~sunspots":{"haiku":"Transformation through obsession with another person who awakens and consumes the self, burning away what was previously real.","opus":"A helpless, ruinous obsession the narrator cannot refuse, fixed on a woman who makes him feel alive even as he torches everything around him. Reznor closes with a reckoning, the man gazing at the sun and tallying his damage, sensing wasted potential he can no longer halt.","sonnet":"A speaker is overwhelmed by an irresistible force — figured partly as a woman, partly as a consuming inner drive — that pulls him out of his old life completely; by the end he has stared into the sun long enough to lose the sense of ever having been a coherent self.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built entirely on heliotropism: the speaker is a moth to a woman who is also a star, drawn against his own judgment toward a heat he knows will incinerate him, obedience overriding disbelief. The verses escalate the appetite from mere validation to a total scorched-earth wish, wanting to reduce a whole former self to grit and scatter it, and the bridge sells that annihilation as bliss, everything he craves sealed inside one blinding point with all fear burned off. Then the outro turns the same star into a mirror rather than an altar, the addict's high curdling into an inventory of everything spent and a grief for the person he thinks he might have grown into, the shift into past tense doing all the damage. Coming from With Teeth (2005), Reznor's first record made sober, the trick is that the compulsion never quite reads as a lover so much as any substance one worships and cannot refuse, and the flat repeated admission after each verse lands less as confession than as the shrug of someone who has quit bargaining. What poses as an ecstatic love song is a relapse narrated from the far side, where the light that once felt like arrival is just the last thing left to look at.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","identity & becoming"],"means":"an ecstatic love song narrated from the far side of the relapse","built":"the outro's shift into past tense carries all the damage a chorus of bliss withheld"},
"nine-inch-nails~survivalism":{"haiku":"Survival instinct and indifference amid propaganda and violence.","opus":"Ruthless survivalism amid societal collapse, embracing propaganda and violence and abandoning others to save oneself.","sonnet":"Societal indifference to destruction — consuming violent media, abandoning the suffering, and choosing self-interest over solidarity.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is the anthem of a mind that has traded its conscience for a survival kit and calls the trade strength. Reznor sings it from wholly inside the villain, and the horror is how proud the voice sounds. The chorus recites a boast like an inventory: propaganda, revisionism, brutality shot in crisp high definition, each atrocity stacked as a national virtue. Two abandonments give the boast its cost, as a bled-dry earth and a broken woman are both walked past because self-preservation is made to excuse anything. The outro then grinds the whole moral question into one sneering opposition, chanting the false binary of mercy against endurance until the survivor's logic is the only thing left standing on the field.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"atrocity stacked as national virtue by a voice proud of the inventory","built":"the outro grinds the moral question to a false binary chanted until survivor logic is all that remains","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~survivalism-deadmau5-remix":{"haiku":"Humanity devours its host planet while celebrating its own exploitation as patriotic pride.","opus":"A bleak-and-plunder portrait of a paranoid nation consuming Mother Earth like a whore, armed with propaganda, violence and revisionism in the name of survival. The narrator walks past a wounded woman he could help, confessing everyone would do the same to survive.","sonnet":"Resource extraction and societal collapse as personal survival instinct — mother nature is a whore we've raped dry, and now propaganda, revisionism, hi-def violence, and fists are the tools of a great nation. The speaker doesn't celebrate this; the woman bruised and bleeding reaching for a hand who gets walked past is the cost accounted and ignored.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~terrible-lie":{"haiku":"Confronts God over suffering and broken promises, expressing rage at faith betrayed and the world's darkness despite spiritual devotion.","opus":"An anguished, accusatory address to God, demanding an apology and railing against a betrayal of faith, as the narrator, stripped of security and pride, still desperately wants someone to hold onto.","sonnet":"The narrator accuses God of betrayal — of promising salvation and delivering only hatred, loneliness, and moral collapse — oscillating between rage and a desperate need for something to hold onto.","src":"haiku","fable":"Early on Pretty Hate Machine, this stages a lover's quarrel with God, addressing the divine directly as a deadbeat who owes an apology, then indicting the whole promise of salvation and meaning as the titular fraud repeated until the word itself curdles into a verdict. But the song's real move is structural: having spent three verses accusing, the speaker collapses in the post-chorus and outro into pure need, pleading not to have the lie taken away because there is nothing to hold onto without it. The betrayal isn't that faith was false; it's that the speaker cannot survive without the thing he has just proven he can no longer believe, and the closing chant of giving everything to a someone he needs turns the address to God indistinguishable from clinging to any human hand. Reznor sets the album's whole thesis here in the split between the fist and the open palm: rage is only the armored face of a dependence that outlives the object it depended on.","fv":2,"fnote":"The record around this track was built on stolen hours: Reznor was the janitor and assistant engineer at Cleveland’s Right Track Studio, and with the owner’s blessing recorded its demos alone at night on a sampler and a Macintosh Plus, playing nearly every part himself.","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"rage exposed as the armored face of a dependence that outlives belief","built":"accusing verses collapse into begging outro, the fist flipping to open palm"},
"nine-inch-nails~that-s-what-i-get":{"haiku":"A breakup song about emotional betrayal and the pain of realization that a relationship was one-sided, with the speaker learning that their love and vulnerability were not reciprocated. The refrain captures bitter resignation to romantic loss.","opus":"The aftermath of a betrayal in love, where the speaker realizes he wasn't the only one, feels his confidence stripped away, and resigns himself that heartbreak is simply what he gets.","sonnet":"The narrator reels from the betrayal of a romantic partner who has destroyed their confidence — naively devoted, they are now left crying and self-recriminating, accepting the heartbreak as what they deserved for trusting so completely.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first heartbreak curdles into self-blame, and the title becomes a shrug the singer repeats until it stops meaning anything. Each short verse tightens the wound, moving from lost confidence to the sting of not being anyone's only one to the ache of having cared far more than she did. The refrain answers every verse like a reflex, a mask that pretends the pain was foreseen and therefore deserved. By the close it detaches from the story entirely and loops past sense, a mantra worn smooth to numb what is underneath. On this 1989 debut Reznor plays the wound as pop rather than industrial assault, and the flatness of the chant is exactly the point, a boy talking himself into resignation he does not feel.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"a shrug repeated until it stops meaning and numbs the wound beneath","built":"the refrain detaches from the story and loops past sense as a worn mantra"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-background-world":{"haiku":"A person is trapped unable to escape an underlying reality of suffering and decay that bleeds through all attempts to move forward or hide.","opus":"Reznor's bleak closer on inescapable dread, a world bleeding through no matter how hard you refuse to look. The repeated question of whether this is really what you wanted decays like the track itself, offering no way out.","sonnet":"Someone has been left behind and pulled into a compulsive, obsessive loop — the world is folding in on itself, there's no escape and no future, just a bleeding undercurrent that was always there beneath everything, endlessly repeating the question of whether this is really what you want.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the exhausting work of denial, and how the recording itself refuses to let that denial hold. The opening voice is abandoned and cornered, insisting nothing can be escaped and no rescue waits anywhere ahead. Then the address flips outward, badgering a listener with one skeptical question over and over: is this really the thing you wanted. The taunt about someone refusing to notice what is plainly in front of them gets stacked and repeated until it stops being an argument and becomes a compulsion. And the long instrumental tail enacts that same collapse, the clean signal fraying into corrosion, so the buried thing pressing up beneath the surface finally wins by dissolving the very track that tried to keep it down.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"denial badgered into compulsion by its own repeated question","built":"the long instrumental tail corrodes until the buried thing dissolves the track holding it down"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-becoming":{"fable":"A transformation narrated from inside, in which the self is being overwritten by something mechanical and the man everyone used to know is spoken of in the third person, already decaying while the new wired consciousness reports the change with flat detachment. On The Downward Spiral this is the machinery of self-destruction rendered literal — feeling replaced by circuitry, presence hollowed out even in the arms of a lover — and the sudden plea to Annie is the last human hand reaching from a body it can no longer hold onto. The looped final cry names the intruder as a noise that wants him dead, so the becoming is really a suicide told as an upgrade.","fableDeep":"The load-bearing device is grammatical: the speaker splits into an 'I' consolidating as machine and 'the me that you know,' a third-person casualty — scabbed, broken, blood no longer pumping — dissociation managed through pronouns, the obituary written while the body still moves. The bargain is stated without spin: all pain disappears, but the circuitry drowns out everything else too, and what it kills off are 'all of my bad parts' — the cure's efficiency is the horror, numbness marketed as an upgrade. The relational cost lands in one line: even when right beside someone, so far away — presence without contact. Then the album's only proper name: 'Annie, hold a little tighter, I might just slip away' — the sole address to a person rather than a force, tenderness surfacing exactly once before the outro's paired snarl reveals the machine's true firmware: it wants him dead, and the noise won't give up. Thesis: the death drive disguised as an operating system, adopted for the painlessness and obeyed for all the rest.","haiku":"Transformation into an artificial consciousness, losing humanity to machinery and noise. The former self decays while wires replace emotions and autonomy.","opus":"A disturbing transformation of a person into a numb, wired machine, the feeling self decaying while a mechanical new consciousness takes over. Pleading for someone to hold him tighter, he fights a relentless inner noise that wants him dead.","sonnet":"A song about losing one's humanity through a process of mechanical transformation — wires replacing feelings, circuitry replacing consciousness — until the person others knew is gone. The speaker is caught between relief at the numbness and horror at the noise driving the change.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the death drive marketed as a painless operating system","built":"the self splits into first and third person mid-sentence"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-beginning-of-the-end":{"haiku":"Complicity in one's own downfall through desperation and selfishness, where facing consequences marks the beginning of collapse.","opus":"A warning that those who climbed over others and lived on lies will face their consequences. The refrain declares this reckoning the beginning of the end.","sonnet":"A warning addressed to the complacent and self-serving: those who have climbed over others, depended on lies, and looked out only for themselves are now facing the consequences — a reckoning that is, paradoxically, both an ending and a beginning.","src":"haiku","fable":"The first words sung on Year Zero, Reznor's 2007 concept record about a surveillance-state America sleepwalking toward collapse, and it works by pinning a false optimism against an apocalyptic truth. The refrain insists over and over that this is a beginning, a mantra of denial the verses keep contradicting: minds are read, marks appear, a face in the mirror has become unrecognizable, and every-man-for-himself scrambling is dressed up as self-reliance and even divine favor. The chorus is where the mask drops and the collective voice admits it, that the height climbed was climbed on the backs of the condemned and the lies believed, so the reckoning arriving is the consequence earned. The song's whole engine is that ironic gap between the word beginning, chanted like reassurance, and the title's completion of it, the beginning of the end, which lands as the only honest thing anyone says.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control"],"means":"optimism mantra chanted while verses contradict every word of it","built":"title completes the refrain's lie, the only honest line"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-big-come-down":{"haiku":"Internal struggle and descent into despair and emotional breakdown.","opus":"Self-destructive craving to hit rock bottom, smashing everything apart as inner hatred and misery keep rising from within.","sonnet":"An inescapable inner hatred and self-destructive spiral that the narrator cannot outrun no matter where they go.","src":"sonnet","fable":"Deep in The Fragile's right disc (1999), this is the album's total collapse—the point where Reznor stops resisting the descent and embraces it as inevitable. The read lives in the song's terrible logic of return: home, the origin, the self are imagined as refuges, but the nearer he gets the more toxic they turn, because the wound he's fleeing is internal and portable, so no geography outruns it. The crash is figured not as a fall but as a homecoming—a deliberate reunion with the failed and forgotten that he half-suspects he engineered his own ruin to reach, the self-destruction reframed as something secretly wanted all along.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"collapse figured as homecoming to a wound that is internal and portable","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-collector":{"haiku":"A compulsive gatherer of emotional debris, pain, and suffering carries them like living things that multiply and suffocate. Unable to release what they've collected, they become a vessel for others' hurt.","opus":"A study of the compulsion to absorb everyone else's pain and hoard it, feeding on hurt until it grows too big to hold. Reznor makes the self-appointed good boy who swallows it all sound both saintly and diseased.","sonnet":"The narrator is compelled to absorb and carry other people's pain, describing himself as dragging a net that collects feelings he cannot release — they grow inside him, multiply, and eventually suffocate him. The act of swallowing it all becomes both compulsion and self-destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"An early cut on With Teeth, Reznor's 2005 record about clawing back out of addiction, this is a portrait of compulsion wearing the costume of a hobby: the narrator introduces himself as someone who merely gathers, then reveals that what he hoards is other people's suffering, dragged in like debris caught in a trailing mesh and kept alive rather than discarded. The horror is that the accumulation is animate and parasitic, the gathered pain incubating and multiplying until the host can no longer draw air, so the chorus becomes a bodily crisis of cramming everything past the throat without gagging on it. The song's shape is its argument: it moves from the collector as active agent to the plague and swarm he has become, the identity inverting so the possessor is now the possessed. The outro seals this, a single count repeated to the point of numbness, no longer a person speaking but a tally running on its own, the enumerating machine that the compulsion has replaced him with.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"hoarding others' pain until the collector becomes the swarm","built":"the outro replaces speech with a tally running on its own"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-day-the-world-went-away":{"haiku":"A decay of communication and presence, where voices sound hollow and plastic, and a place exists that consumes fear and pain.","opus":"A portrait of inner rot masked by an artificial facade, and a place that devours dread and suffering. It fixates on the toll exacted on the moment when everything the narrator knew fell apart.","sonnet":"A figure perceived as hollow and decayed — his face a forced mask, his insides cold and grey — faces a reckoning whose cost is described as sweet but total, marking the day everything fell away.","src":"haiku","fable":"A eulogy for someone still speaking: the narrator listens to a man whose voice already sounds like rot, whose expression is a mask stretched over something cold, and hears in it the death that hasn't arrived yet. The apocalypse of the title is deliberately intimate rather than cosmic — a private erasure imagined as relief, the fear and pain finally consumed, so that annihilation reads as the sweetest thing he could be granted rather than punishment. On The Fragile this is the pivot from language into wreckage: the words give up and the track dissolves into a long wordless chant, the sound of a mind after the meaning has drained out, holding onto melody because sense is no longer available.","fv":2,"fnote":"Reznor's grandmother — the woman who raised him — died in the years this record was made, and he has connected the song to that loss; the private apocalypse has a name.","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"annihilation imagined as the sweetest available relief","built":"the track dissolves into wordless chant where meaning has already drained out"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-downward-spiral":{"fable":"The title track sits near the album's end as its narrative climax: the suicide the whole record has been sliding toward. NIN frames it not as tragedy but as the protagonist's grim relief, a lifetime of self-destruction resolved in a single decisive act, the spoken third-person voice reporting the gunshot with unnerving detachment. The outro then dissolves that clarity into synesthetic blue haze, the mind coming apart and leaking away, so the promised solution delivers not peace but oblivion.","fableDeep":"Two stanzas, two grammars. The act itself is delivered as detached third-person report — its noted ease, a parenthetical marveling with clinical wonder at the disproportion between the tiny wound and its enormous consequence — and the surrounding language is managerial: problems have solutions, a lifetime of failure fixed in one determined flash, self-annihilation phrased as productivity, the grim joke resting on the word fixed. Then the point of view collapses inward mid-track: suddenly first person, present tense, and everything is blue — the word repeated until it names a condition rather than a color, fuzzy, spilling out of the head — consciousness rendered as liquid escaping, the report replaced by the experience it reported. The track carrying the album's name makes the structure explicit: every song before this was the argument, this is the conclusion, delivered in a few whispered lines because the act requires no elaboration. Thesis: the loudest record of its era stages its narrative endpoint as a whisper — the spiral ends not with drama but with ease.","haiku":"A brief, bleak meditation on suicide as a permanent solution to life's pain, followed by depression and numbness. The song treats death matter-of-factly, capturing a state of blue emptiness.","opus":"A stark depiction of a suicide by gunshot, framing it as an easy, final solution to a lifetime of failure, followed by a numb, blue haze spilling from the mind.","sonnet":"A brief, unflinching description of a suicide — someone puts a gun to his face and pulls the trigger, the lyric observing with cold detachment that problems have solutions, and what follows is a dissociative wash of blue and fuzzy unreality.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"self-annihilation framed as productivity — a lifetime's problems fixed","built":"the loudest record of its era stages its narrative endpoint as a whisper"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-downward-spiral-the-bottom":{"haiku":"Suicide resolves lifelong suffering in a moment of clarity—everything melts into blue as the unbearable finally ends.","opus":"The title track of Reznor's landmark 1994 record, reaching the pit where the protagonist coolly points a firearm at his own head, picturing years of failure erased in a single instant. His vision drowns in a hazy indigo blur. The album's bleak nadir treats self-destruction with an unnerving calm, as if it were the last sensible fix.","sonnet":"A suicide rendered with clinical detachment — the gun, the tiny hole, the blood, the brain spilling out into 'the deepest shade of mushroom blue.' The italicized observation that problems do have solutions, that a lifetime of fucking things up can be fixed in one determined flash, is the most chilling part: the narrator frames it as resolution rather than tragedy.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-fragile":{"haiku":"A commitment to protect someone fragile from breaking, bearing witness to their invisible struggle and beauty.","opus":"Reznor's vow to shield someone luminous who can't see her own worth and is slipping away, promising to hold her together against everyone picking at her. The ache is that the would-be protector admits he's past saving himself — devotion offered from inside his own ruin.","sonnet":"The narrator watches someone beautiful and self-unaware who is slipping toward collapse, and pledges not to let her fall apart — recognising her pain in his own past self and vowing to build a shelter against whatever is closing in on her, even as he admits it may already be too late for him.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track of NIN's 1999 double album is Reznor's most direct attempt at devotion, and its structure quietly reveals that the devotion is a lie the singer needs. The verses render a woman who cannot see her own worth and is trying to disappear, and the chorus answers her with a vow repeated so many times it becomes less a promise than an incantation against helplessness. The turn comes in the second verse, where the rescuer admits he is the one who is broken and past saving, which recasts the whole rescue as projection: propping her up is how he avoids his own collapse. The bridge fantasizes a walled hideaway from the world, but the relentless picking that follows exposes the wall as futile, and the outro lands the real confession, that he sees himself in her decline because he was there first. What sounds like protecting someone else is a man watching his own ruin in another person and pledging to stop it there because he already knows he couldn't stop it in himself.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"rescue vow revealed as projection of the rescuer's own ruin","built":"repeated chorus incantation curdles into confession by the outro"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-good-soldier":{"haiku":"A soldier witnesses warfare's devastation and questions the reality of his experience, uncertain of purpose while surrounded by death and divine justification for violence.","opus":"A soldier in the midst of war steps over the dead amid gunfire and bombs, struggling to believe he belongs there and doubting what anyone is even fighting for as he grows numb and detached.","sonnet":"A soldier in an active war zone struggles to hold onto meaning: the ideological certainties that justified the fight have dissolved, leaving only gunfire, corpses, and a disorienting sense that no one knows what they are fighting for or who they are.","src":"haiku","fable":"A soldier's faith curdles into hollow chant as the war he was promised was righteous strips away every reason he had for fighting it. Reznor stages the collapse where an ordinary street once meant a meeting place and now means corpses underfoot, letting the speaker parrot a comforting philosophy before admitting it explains nothing about the numbness swallowing him. The looping refrain is the real content: an effort toward sight and conviction, repeated only because the effort keeps failing, a prayer worn down to reflex. On Year Zero this is the true believer discovering that the God summoned to bless the killing has left him unsure who the enemy is, who he is, and how far he has drifted from anything he recognizes.","fv":2,"themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"a prayer worn down to reflex by the effort that keeps failing","built":"the looping refrain enacts a conviction repeated because it keeps collapsing"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-great-below":{"haiku":"The ocean calls toward death and dissolution; the speaker descends into dark depths where connection persists even as everything disappears and he vanishes.","opus":"A yielding to drowning, whether an actual suicide or a total unraveling, staged as wading out to sea and sinking downward into the current. He surrenders all he has, the ruined remains of a squandered life, to a woman who may never arrive, and the sting is that her presence lingers even as he lets the water take him.","sonnet":"A speaker consumed by longing for someone lost submits willingly to drowning — the ocean becomes a metaphor for surrender and death, and the faint, repeated \"I can still feel you\" is the last thread connecting them to life.","src":"haiku","fable":"The final song of The Fragile's left disc, and its surrender: a man standing at the water's edge deciding to walk in. Reznor stages drowning as both suicide and consecration, the sea a lover he waits for and a destiny he chooses rather than merely suffers, so that going under reads as the only devotion he has left to offer. The turn comes when he stops resisting and lets the undertow do the choosing for him, dressing his own dissolution in the language of grace and inevitability. Then the song refuses its own resolution: after he has vanished, the tether to whoever he did this for keeps repeating, unkillable, the one thing the water couldn't wash out.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","love & desire","nature & the elements"],"means":"drowning staged as the only devotion the narrator has left to offer","built":"the tether to another person survives intact after dissolution"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-great-collapse":{"haiku":"An existential acknowledgment of complete systemic breakdown and the sensation of internal collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-great-destroyer":{"haiku":"The narrator harbors destructive potential and murder lurking within, hoping surveillance cannot detect this hidden darkness.","opus":"Set in a monitored, regimented society where every utterance is recorded and thoughts are fed straight into people's minds. The narrator conceals a boundless capacity for annihilation, proclaiming himself an agent of ruin while praying the watchers never detect what he harbors.","sonnet":"Under surveillance by an unseen authority, the narrator conceals an inner urge toward total destruction, fearing that the system watching him will detect the violent potential he harbors and that he himself barely suppresses.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verse is a citizen being processed under total surveillance, coached to speak clearly and nod because everything is watched, recorded, and pumped back as apathy-inducing noise. Then the chorus swivels inward to the private self that survived that flattening, and what it finds there is not resistance but appetite: a boundless capacity to destroy that it prays the watchers can't detect. On Year Zero's near-future police state, the horror is the twist in that hope, the surveillance regime meant to neutralize threats has instead incubated its own annihilating shadow, and the meek compliant surface is exactly the mask the great destroyer needs to pass unseen.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"surveillance regime incubating the annihilating shadow it was built to neutralize","built":"the compliant surface is exactly the mask the great destroyer needs to pass unseen"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-great-destroyer-modwheelmood":{"haiku":"Destruction arrives announced, documented, and inescapable as fate closes in.","opus":"Surveillance paranoia souring into something darker, as a mind persuaded that it carries an infinite power to wreck prays the watchers stay blind to what it holds within. The media floods people with apathy while the narrator bides his time, branding himself an agent of ruin.","sonnet":"A person performing compliance while harboring limitless destructive potential — nodding for the cameras, speaking clearly for the satellite, hoping they cannot see the murderous capacity inside. The great destroyer is not an external force but the self, concealed behind good behavior and ready to tear everything down.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-greater-good":{"haiku":"An entity breathes into and consumes all desires and actions, employing persuasion, coercion, and assimilation to control. The song depicts absorption into a greater collective.","opus":"Reznor voicing an ideology as a soft, seductive whisper, control dressed as intimacy. The euphemisms of persuasion, coercion and assimilation strip away any comfort, exposing how willingly people breathe in their own subjugation.","sonnet":"A minimalist depiction of total ideological absorption — through persuasion, coercion, submission, and assimilation, a collective entity breathes itself into you until there is nothing individual left.","src":"haiku","fable":"A lullaby sung by the power that means to absorb you, this Year Zero fragment is propaganda that has learned to whisper rather than command. The soft collective voice asks only to be inhaled, then drops the mask to name its own methods aloud, a clinical ladder climbing from gentle pressure toward the total erasure of self. It folds that confession back into the same soothing hush without ever changing tone, and the words physically dissolve into one another until the single self is drowned in the chant of the many, repetition itself the argument that wears resistance down until surrender feels like rest.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"propaganda that confesses its own methods without changing tone","built":"words physically dissolve into each other as the chant swallows the self"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-hand-that-feeds":{"fable":"Trent Reznor's post-9/11 protest song works entirely as interrogation: every chorus line is a question fired at a listener marching obediently in line, chin up and hollow inside. It refuses to name its target, which is the point, the crusade and the blood dined upon in the name of the divine are left as accusation rather than argument, so the song becomes a test of nerve rather than a manifesto. The relentless outro strips it to two questions, will you turn on your master and will you stay kneeling, and lets the loop itself embody the paralysis it's needling.","fableDeep":"The song is staged as interrogation: every chorus line is a question and none gets an answer, so the listener is left holding them. The crusade/charade rhyme names the target — faith-justified war with a price paid in blood — but the sharpest verse turns the weapon inward: 'so naïve, I keep holding on to what I want to believe; I can see, but I keep holding on.' The accuser admits he's also the accused, which is what saves the song from being a lecture: obedience isn't someone else's disease, and knowing better, it says, isn't the same as getting up off your knees.","haiku":"Questioning blind obedience to authority and willingness to challenge control.","opus":"Challenging blind obedience and religious or political authority, daring the listener to question the system and rebel against it.","sonnet":"A challenge to passive obedience under political or religious authority, urging people to question and resist blind compliance.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","faith & the occult","anger & defiance","war & battle"],"means":"interrogation with no answers handed back to the listener","built":"the outro strips to two looping questions, the repetition itself embodying the paralysis","fnote":"MTV wanted the song without its backdrop — an unaltered image of George W. Bush — for the 2005 Movie Awards; Reznor walked: apparently, he said, the image of our president is as offensive to MTV as it is to me.","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-idea-of-you": {"fable":"A song sung from inside a dissolving self, where the speaker keeps swapping out which person he was — insisting his failures belonged to a stranger wearing his face, and begging the listener to keep loving the concept of him rather than the actual man decaying in the room. Reznor stages this as a relationship autopsy that is really a psychiatric one: the chorus's frantic soothing — the parenthesized commands to wake and breathe laid over a flat insistence that none of it is real — is the exact grammar of someone talking a panicking mind down, a mask of calm pressed over pure dissociation. Underneath it the body horror surfaces plainly, flesh going to liquid and a second occupant who has already vanished and left the skin behind, the transformation half-finished and unspeakable. The cruelty is that he asks to be spared as an abstraction precisely because the reality has turned monstrous, and the outro lets the mask slip into a small, exhausted admission of how isolating it is to be trapped in there. It is one of Reznor's coldest studies of the self as a contested, unreliable space — the person you loved renegotiating himself out of existence in real time.","fableDeep":"The opening establishes the alibi structure: 'maybe that was somebody else / maybe I was somebody else' — the same excuse run in two directions, guilt outsourced first to another person, then to another self, neither available for questioning. The central request names the whole strategy: 'just go back to the idea of me' begs the addressee to prefer the concept to the person, love maintained by not looking, reinforced by the pre-emptive disclaimer 'if I start to tell you anything, please don't pay attention' — testimony recalled before it is given. The chant sequence weaponizes therapy language: the barked commands Wait, Wake, Breathe, Believe — grounding-exercise verbs — are each answered by 'none of this is happening', self-soothing looped into pure denial, with the giveaway tag 'hey, you tell yourself' exposing the mantra as an administered lie. The body-horror bridge literalizes what is being denied: liquid absorbed, something terrible forming, sores gone, someone else no longer here. The outro's small admission — it gets so lonely in here — is the only unguarded line. It is about maintaining a self, and a love, by insisting everyone deal only with the idealized copy while the original quietly transforms.","haiku":"Separation and transformation leave only fragments; the narrator asks to be remembered as an idea rather than the deteriorated present self.","opus":"A fractured, dissociative confession in which the narrator insists something is wrong with him, disowns his own words and identity, and begs someone to go back to the idea of him rather than the reality.","sonnet":"A dissociating speaker — uncertain whether his past self or a relationship even existed — urges someone to retreat back to a comfortable idealised image of him rather than witness whatever he is becoming, while an inner voice insists none of this is happening.","src":"haiku","fable2":"A plea to be loved as a clean abstraction rather than as the deteriorating fact standing in the room. The speaker begs a partner to revert to the idea of him, disowning his own confessions before they land, because the real thing has gone wrong. The image system says what the words deny: matter turning liquid, twitching, congealing into something monstrous, healed sores where a whole prior self has quietly vanished. Against that, the chorus is a four-beat mantra of denial, each shouted command a slap trying to rouse someone who refuses to look. Then the outro drops the act entirely, a small voice asking through the wall whether anyone can hear how isolated it has become in there. The mask insists nothing is wrong; underneath, a person is dissolving and pleading not to be watched doing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"pleading to be loved as abstraction because the reality has turned monstrous","built":"parenthesized commands to breathe enact the grammar of talking a panicking mind down"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-line-begins-to-blur":{"haiku":"Loss of moral clarity and certainty about one's own identity and beliefs while trapped in an inescapable situation.","opus":"A descent into confusion and self-erasure, where the longer the narrator stays inside himself the less clear anything becomes. Once sure which side he was on, he now watches the line blur as he disappears.","sonnet":"A speaker describes losing his sense of moral identity and self as isolation deepens — the boundary between who he was and who he is becoming dissolves, leaving uncertainty about whether anything or anyone has control over his descent.","src":"haiku","fable":"One of the queasier interior pieces on With Teeth, this is a song about watching the boundary between self and dissolution wear thin until you can no longer locate it. The opening admits to fears come true and vows broken, but the real subject is loss of orientation rather than guilt: the speaker who once knew which side of a line he stood on now finds the line itself softening, and — crucially — stops minding. Reznor stages this as sinking, not falling; the second verse's stalled questions about whether anyone is on top of him or stopping him get answered only by an exhausted refrain of not knowing, so the will to resist and the will to keep going both go quiet at once. The image-system moves from staying in a place, to disappearing inside it, to fabric tearing past repair, tracking the same collapse from enclosure into shredded self — and the flatness of the final admission that he does not care is the point, an anesthesia read as relief. The refusal to know is the meaning: submersion so complete that even the instinct to be saved has blurred out.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the will to resist and the will to continue both going quiet at once","built":"image-system moves from enclosure to tearing fabric, tracking collapse without naming it","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-lovers":{"haiku":"Transcendence and union through ecstatic love; the singer escapes time and dissolves boundaries in an embrace, surrendering control and seeking refuge in another's arms.","opus":"A dissociative surrender told from inside a crumbling sense of self, slipping out of time and no longer the one driving, drawn toward an embrace that is both seductive and menacing, the terror of not knowing who you are anymore.","sonnet":"A dissociative song about slipping out of ordinary time and consciousness — the narrator drifts toward something described as the arms of lovers, caught between lucidity and surrender, unsure who is driving, and haunted by a voice asking not to be left behind.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dissociation dressed up as homecoming, then quietly exposed as capture. The speaker steps sideways from the clock and calls the numbness freedom, drifting toward a presence that is at once radiant and putrid, an embrace that plainly wants to devour him. That surrender becomes the chorus itself, a plea to be gathered up and dissolved into another. The second half is where the con shows. Speech folds into a picture of one falsehood set against itself forever; agency drains as he confesses he is no longer at the wheel; his own attempt to name who he is buckles into a small, terrified question. So the closing announcement of release reads in the captor's voice, not the captive's.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"surrender disguised as homecoming, the captor's voice delivering the release","built":"the closing announcement of freedom arrives in the captor's register, not the captive's"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-mark-has-been-made":{"opus":"A late instrumental-leaning piece from Trent Reznor's sprawling double album The Fragile, arriving as the record spirals inward toward its bleakest passages. Stripped nearly to a single refrain of relentless approach, it works as texture more than statement, the fixation on some unnamed destination mirroring the album's larger drift toward collapse and slow, inevitable dissolution.","sonnet":"A meditation on relentless, inevitable approach toward something — the lyrics are nearly bare, just the refrain of getting closer over time, conveying fixation or destiny without naming its object.","src":"sonnet"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-new-flesh":{"haiku":"An unflinching embrace of radical transformation and the violent beginning of rebirth into something unfamiliar and primal.","opus":"A disorienting sketch of transformation, glimpsed sideways and incomplete, a flood of every sensation the body has known as something is born and a strange new self surfaces. His repeated readiness to endure it turns yielding into willed strength. Nine Inch Nails, Trent Reznor's industrial outfit, deal in sensation and body horror rather than plot, evoking Cronenberg-style metamorphosis, a threshold crossed more than a tale.","sonnet":"A threshold experience described in fragments — waves of sensation, the blackest eyes glimpsed through static, something beginning that cannot be undone. The repetition is ritual, and the insistence on being able to take it reads as a dare directed inward.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-only-time":{"haiku":"Intoxication and reckless passion provide the only moments when the narrator feels truly alive, even as they acknowledge the damage.","opus":"Reckless, boozy desire framed as the only state in which the narrator feels genuinely awake, torn between reaching for grace and giving in to baser urges. Under the bravado lies moral collapse and an appetite for the new that masks his inability to feel anything otherwise.","sonnet":"Drunk and morally unmoored, the narrator surrenders to a purely physical encounter with someone, finding in the transgression the only moment where he feels genuinely alive — lust as the one thing that breaks through his numbness.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drunk boast that being consumed by another person is the only state that counts as being awake, and that everything sober feels like death. Trent Reznor stacks the sacred and the filthy in one breath, grabbing at the heavens while a devil offers him the backseat, refusing to choose between them. The verses swagger, but the chorus jams on a single admission of being wrecked, looping it past sense until the confidence curdles into a nervous tic. That stuck-record repetition is the real confession: he cannot say it once and move on. On this 1989 debut the thrill of the new fuses with the urge to tear it open and go under, so completely that he mistakes drowning for feeling alive.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism"],"means":"drowning in another person mistaken for the only proof of being alive","built":"the chorus jams on a single admission past sense until confidence becomes a nervous tic"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-perfect-drug":{"haiku":"A person is obsessively dependent on another human who acts as an addictive substance; separation causes complete emotional collapse.","opus":"Reznor casts addictive desire as the drug itself, a craving that dismantles him even as he gives everything to it. The truth underneath is that the more he surrenders, the less of him remains, and he wants it anyway.","sonnet":"An obsessive, consuming desire for another person described through the lens of addiction — she is the perfect drug, the only thing that holds him together while simultaneously dissolving him. Without her everything falls apart; with her he's being destroyed. The dependency is total and he knows it.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-persistence-of-loss":{"haiku":"Devotion anchored in Korean lyrical ache, loving someone you know doesn't return the feeling.","opus":"A Korean-sung ballad of unreturnable love, watching from behind someone too familiar to reach, the daily unspoken confession left as tear-blurred scribbles. Devotion that asks only to be turned toward once, knowing it won't be, even if it isn't love.","sonnet":"A Korean-language song — despite the NIN attribution this appears to be a Korean ballad — about unrequited or suppressed love: the narrator lives painfully because of someone who doesn't know it, fell asleep thinking of them, woke up to tears, and finally whispers 'I love you' in a voice the person will never hear.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-warning":{"haiku":"An alien or external force warns humanity that it has become a destructive virus; humans must change their ways or be wiped out.","opus":"A doom-laden vision in which an outside intelligence delivers humanity an ultimatum, condemning our species as a parasite destroying its own planet through greed and vanity. The verdict is stark ecological and moral judgment: reform or be erased. Time is explicitly running out.","sonnet":"An alien or divine intelligence delivers an ultimatum to humanity: you have become a virus killing your host planet, you waste your potential on greed and self-importance, change or be wiped clean. The countdown imagery makes it a doomsday message delivered with cold authority.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dispatch from Year Zero's paranoid future, where Trent Reznor imagines America's slide toward theocratic collapse being interrupted by a literal descent from the sky. The opening frames the event as ambiguous testimony from witnesses who saw something come down, then hands the mic over: the middle turns into the voice of that arriving power itself, an all-seeing they who indict humanity as a parasite killing its own host and prizing greed and arrogance above survival. The verdict is an ultimatum, reform or be erased, and the quiet horror is how it inverts the salvation narrative the album's cultists crave: the higher force that finally answers isn't merciful but coldly appalled. The tick-tick-ticking that fills the outro strips the message down to its only real content, a countdown running out while the words stop mattering.","fv":2,"fnote":"The album cover is this song’s scene: the ghostly hand reaching down from the sky is the fiction’s Presence, the very descent the witnesses here describe — and its image hides in static at the record’s edges, visible only through a spectrogram.","themes":["politics & society","faith & the occult","nature & the elements"],"means":"the saviour voice turns out to be the coldest prosecutor","built":"the outro strips language to a countdown ticking past the words"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-way-out-is-through":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts overwhelming hardship and existential smallness, insisting on perseverance despite feeling crushed by the weight of suffering and cosmic insignificance.","opus":"A dogged pledge of survival: no matter what he has endured, the narrator swears to press on. Dwarfed by a collapsing sky, he drags himself forward regardless, refusing to stop.","sonnet":"A stark meditation on endurance: the speaker insists that everything suffered must simply be continued through, because even as the heavens fall and everything feels small, the only path forward is to keep going.","src":"haiku","fable":"The opening track of The Fragile's right disc takes its title from the old dictum that liberation from suffering runs straight into it, never around. The lyric is deliberately near-mute for its length: a mantra of endurance repeated until repetition itself becomes the argument, the words worn down to a survivor's tautology. The single expansive gesture reframes that stubbornness cosmically, dwarfing the human figure against a collapsing sky yet insisting the diminished still move forward on all fours, degraded but unstopped. On an album about disintegration, the sparseness is the point: grief reduced to its irreducible motor function, the vow that outlasts the vocabulary to justify it.","fv":2,"themes":["endurance","death & grief"],"means":"endurance as argument, repetition itself wearing the words down to a tautology","built":"the lyric is deliberately near-mute, sparseness enacting grief reduced to its irreducible motor function"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-wretched":{"haiku":"A song about the moment of realization when life doesn't turn out as hoped, and joining a collective of the disappointed and broken. The lyrics explore disappointment, the inability to escape a cycle, and feeling trapped in spiritual or emotional emptiness.","opus":"A caustic confrontation with a shattered dream, aimed at someone whose life fell short of everything they hoped for. The speaker ushers them into a communal wretchedness and insists there is no escaping this hollow ache, even picturing a spiteful deity reaching down to hold them under.","sonnet":"A person confronts the total collapse of their hopes and expectations, realizing that life has not turned out as they wanted and that even the universe — imagined as God pushing them down — conspires to keep them stuck in misery. The song dwells on the loop of pretending and sinking, unable to break free.","src":"haiku","fable":"Structurally a trap: the song narrates a conclusion, welcoming the listener into a brotherhood of the defeated as if the whole ruinous arc has already happened. The taunting refrain fixes the accusation in the second person, so the song isn't confessing so much as recruiting, turning failure into an initiation rite everyone eventually passes. Its cruelest move is theological, imagining a God who reaches down not to lift but to pin, which reframes despair as designed rather than accidental. The final lines strip away even that agency, admitting the collapse is something happening to you and cannot be halted.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","faith & the occult"],"means":"failure framed as an initiation rite everyone eventually passes","built":"the song narrates a conclusion already reached, recruiting rather than confessing"},
"nine-inch-nails~the-wretched-remix-version":{"haiku":"Wretchedness becomes definition as the speaker accepts their role as victim and instrument.","opus":"Reznor watching someone finally collapse into the same disillusionment he lives in, half-pity, half-vindication. The bitterness is theological: a God who reaches down only to hold you under, misery as the one thing that never stops arriving.","sonnet":"A portrait of the newly wretched — the one for whom hopes, prayers, and better days have all calcified into 'forget it.' The image of God reaching through the parting clouds not to save but to push you back down into the hole is the pivot: the cosmos itself is indifferent or actively hostile, and all that's left is the knowledge that it didn't turn out the way you wanted.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~this-isn-t-the-place":{"haiku":"Grief and dislocation reverberate through the repeated plea to be carried home; lost time and lost friendship define the emotional core.","opus":"Reznor sitting with the wrong-place shock of grief, an abrupt severance where the future you assumed you had simply stops; the plea to be carried home is exhaustion masquerading as a request.","sonnet":"A minimal elegy for someone who died unexpectedly — the narrator thought there would be more time, and now is left asking to be carried home while clinging to the image of a thin, final line.","src":"haiku","fable":"A deathbed vigil told in a sentence that keeps losing its own ending until only the wish to be brought home is left. Trent Reznor renders a whole life as one flat unbroken mark, the reading a heart monitor gives once it stops. The refrain sheds a word with each pass, so grief enacts itself as subtraction rather than naming it. On the 2017 Add Violence EP this is the raw ache beneath the machinery, spare where the record elsewhere clangs.","fv":2,"fnote":"An early version of this was the song Lynch rejected — NIN’s first Twin Peaks submission, returned for not being menacing enough — so the spare, tender piece that wasn’t ugly enough for the Roadhouse resurfaced here instead.","themes":["death & grief"],"means":"grief enacted as subtraction, the refrain shedding a word each pass","built":"a whole life rendered as the flat unbroken mark a stopped heart monitor gives"},
"nine-inch-nails~underneath-it-all":{"haiku":"A song about haunting emotional attachment. Despite numbness and attempted severance, the speaker cannot escape feeling the other person; they remain crucified inside despite all attempts to kill the connection.","opus":"The residue of someone who will not leave, felt beneath numbness no matter how hard the narrator tries to kill the memory. Reznor reduces obsession to its barest loop, presence as a permanent stain.","sonnet":"The narrator cannot rid himself of a person's presence — even after trying to kill the feeling, the other person still resonates underneath everything, leaving the narrator permanently stained by that connection.","src":"haiku","fable":"A haunting that survives every attempt to end it. Reznor strips the song down to a single circling admission and repeats it until the repetition itself becomes the argument: numbness, self-destruction, even a death he narrates in past tense have all been tried and all failed to evict the presence lodged beneath consciousness. The religious vocabulary (a crucifixion, a body left marked) reframes the lost other as something worshipped and something that contaminates at once, so the sparse loop reads less as a love song than as an exorcism that cannot take. What the near-total refrain enacts is the trap of that persistence: the mind can be killed but the trace underneath it stays, and the mind keeps returning to say so.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"an exorcism that cannot take, the trace surviving every attempt to end it","built":"a near-total refrain enacts the trap: the mind returns to say the presence stays"},
"nine-inch-nails~various-methods-of-escape":{"haiku":"An attempt to construct meaning and escape through art that no one hears. The speaker hallucinates plans and builds monuments to their own creation, then burns it all—unable to trust themselves or find genuine escape.","opus":"A splintering mind reports its visions, self-inflicted wounds, and a single lyric circling endlessly with no listener, aching to release its grip and slip away as history cycles round again. The narrator can no longer trust his own judgment or separate what is real from what is not.","sonnet":"The narrator tries to find a way out — from a relationship or situation he cannot clearly distinguish from himself anymore — through destruction and ritual burning, but cannot fully break free or trust his own judgment.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is the diagnosis: a recovering addict rehearses every exit from himself, and each supposed way out only loops him back into the same locked room. Reznor drafts the imagery like an architect of relief, a flawless plan sketched in his own blood, an effigy raised only to be torched so the scene can restart on schedule. The chorus is the recovery mantra worn thin, half command to himself, half plea aimed at whoever made staying clean this brutal. Then the bridge quits arguing and simply repeats the fear of dissolving in this place, four times, hypnotized. The final turn is the quiet devastation: the person he keeps begging to release becomes a him, the self split into jailer and prisoner, so the only figure blocking the door is the one he is trying to leave.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","madness & the mind"],"means":"every exit from the self loops back into the same locked room","built":"the self splits into jailer and prisoner, the only figure blocking the door"},
"nine-inch-nails~vessel":{"haiku":"A person surrenders their body and mind to consume another, transcending flesh to become something divine through total absorption.","opus":"Reznor merging addiction, sex and self-annihilation into one surrender, ecstasy as the dissolving of the self into something larger; the fantasy of transcending flesh reads equally as intimacy and as the needle.","sonnet":"A song about surrendering to a substance, ideology, or sexual partner until the boundaries of self dissolve — the narrator lets it in through every possible opening, and reaches a state of ecstatic merger where he believes he is becoming God.","src":"haiku","fable":"Built on the deliberate double meaning of surrender, Vessel starts as an addict's submission that reads equally as erotic and pharmaceutical: the speaker consents, line by line, to being filled, injected, taken over from inside, and finds in that invasion a freedom from every rule the world had drummed into him. Reznor lets the intoxicated ecstasy ring true before turning it, so the chorus's breathless plea to go faster is genuine rapture and dread at once. The second verse then completes the transformation Year Zero's fiction is built around, as the drug, the kind the album’s fiction pipes through the state water supply, dissolves the boundary between self and other and delivers not liberation but delusion, the body left behind and the mind convinced it has become God. The horror is that the song never breaks character to warn you; it stays inside the high, so the ascent to divinity lands as the exact moment a chemically pacified subject mistakes control for transcendence.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","transcendence & consciousness","surveillance & control"],"means":"the body left behind while the mind mistakes control for transcendence","built":"the song stays inside the high so the ascent to divinity lands as the pacified subject's delusion"},
"nine-inch-nails~we-re-in-this-together":{"haiku":"Two people remain bound through destruction and pain, determined to survive together despite external forces trying to separate and damage them.","opus":"A defiant devotion to a partner against a hostile world, as the narrator pledges that the two of them will make it through together no matter what tries to tear them apart.","sonnet":"The narrator pledges absolute commitment to another person against a hostile world that wants to destroy what they have found together, declaring their bond unbreakable even as everything else falls apart.","src":"haiku","fable":"Reznor writes a love song in the grammar of a siege, and the tension is the whole point. The verses are a paranoid us-against-them, faceless persecutors clawing to destroy whatever the couple found, but the pronouns quietly give the game away: the deeper the damage, the more thoroughly he is inside the other person, so the bond and the wound become the same organ. That vow of fusion is meant as devotion and reads as a threat, and the fairy-tale coronation at the end lands less like salvation than like two people barricaded in a dying world insisting nothing outside them exists. The bridge is the honest floor beneath the anthem: everything they were is already gone, and holding on is not triumph but the last available verb.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","anger & defiance"],"means":"warning the beloved as the last available love","built":"siege grammar turns devotion and threat into the same pronoun","fvr":"2.4"},
"nine-inch-nails~where-is-everybody":{"haiku":"Alienation and disconnection pervade; the speaker numbs themselves and lies, unable to find authentic connection or meaning.","opus":"A downward spiral of self-loathing and dislocation, the narrator sensing time warp around him, admitting he's a fraud with nothing left to try, and dreading that he's turned into exactly what he despised. A frantic chant of grim participles voices his exhaustion with the pretense and his hunger for either real contact or an exit.","sonnet":"A song about alienation, self-deception, and the vertigo of losing connection — the speaker admits they've been pretending to be okay while secretly wishing for an end, and the desperate chant of \"where is everybody?\" is a cry for anyone real to show up.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dizziness that can't tell whether the world is accelerating or the self is failing, dressed up first as a sneering refusal to become the thing he despises—a swaggering claim of difference the singer immediately exposes as its own lie. The chorus abandons sentences entirely for a stampede of bare gerunds—every verb an -ing with no subject, no object—so that begging, hemorrhaging, spawning and dying stop being things a person does and become an undifferentiated churn of biological activity, motion without an agent. And it's precisely from inside that churn, surrounded by frantic verbs, that the title asks where everyone went: the terror of The Fragile isn't isolation from a crowd but the discovery that the crowd, and the self, have dissolved into pure verb. The second verse names the mechanism underneath the panic—exhaustion at pretending, the wish to end quietly reframed as just another way of hiding—ending on the faintest possible upturn, a longing to recover even the appetite for effort.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"the chorus dissolves persons into bare gerunds — motion without an agent","built":"subject-less gerunds enact the self dissolving into pure verb"},
"nine-inch-nails~while-i-m-still-here":{"haiku":"Reflects on mortality and things slipping away, trying to hold onto fading memories before time expires. The song expresses resignation to inevitable loss while still here.","opus":"A reflection on mortality and dwindling time, the narrator watching his existence crumble and drift a bit further away each passing day. Learning that everything is coming to a close, he makes peace with it, asking someone he loves to stay beside him and keep him company while he remains.","sonnet":"A person facing the end of something — life, a relationship, a world — spends their remaining time noting what is slipping away, asking to be held close while they still can be, and accepting the passage with quiet resignation rather than rage.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dying man tallies his life against a clock that keeps announcing the countdown, insisting he's fine even as his composure cracks. Each verse line is answered by that mechanical refrain, so the ticking becomes the song's real pulse rather than a metaphor Reznor steps out to explain. His calm is a performance: he claims not to mind the slow erosion, yet begs forgiveness for his tears and reports the apocalypse in the past tense, as something already finished. Then the whole posture collapses into its opposite, acceptance giving way to a plea to be held close for the little that remains. As the last voice on Hesitation Marks, it hands off to the closing instrumental still reaching for someone.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"composure performed for a clock that keeps winning","built":"the mechanical refrain becomes the song's real pulse"},
"nine-inch-nails~while-i-m-still-here-breyer-p-orridge-howler-remix":{"haiku":"Mortality and temporal helplessness consume the present; the speaker watches life crumble in real time, memories of better days colliding with the certainty of endings.","opus":"A quiet reckoning with mortality that trades rage for acceptance, the closing stretch of a life narrowing to four walls. The plea underneath is only that someone stay near through the last of it.","sonnet":"A person facing mortality or total collapse accepts that everything is slipping away piece by piece, finds a strange peace in it, and only asks to be held close while there is still time.","src":"haiku"},
"nine-inch-nails~wish":{"fable":"On Broken (1992) the craving for authenticity and the contempt that answers it are a single violent motion, not two moods. The verses stage self-demolition: a narrator razes what he built, throws away his last possession, and names betrayed faith as the vilest thing he could commit. His plea for anything genuine keeps snagging on the accusatory you who saturates his entire horizon, so longing and sneer arrive as one exhaled breath. The bridge drops the mask, its stuttered admission of wanting escape yet being unable to move looping until the tirade reveals itself as a cage. Underneath the noise sits a man welded to the very thing he damns.","fableDeep":"Rage at reaching the top of the climb and finding nothing real there — faith in God and trust in one person both spent in a single line. The venom of the chorus is in its address: the world isn't empty, it's 'full of you' — the fakeness is located in everyone around him — while 'I want to but I can't turn back' admits he's trapped on the path he built himself.","haiku":"Despair and isolation amid broken trust and disillusionment with existence.","opus":"Self-loathing and bitter despair at rock bottom, having lost faith in God and a partner, wishing for anything real in an empty life.","sonnet":"Alienation and self-loathing in a world that feels hollow and fake, with no authentic connection left to find.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fnote":"The EP behind it was cut in secret, studios booked under fake band names like the Stunt Popes so his warring label couldn’t seize the tapes — and then this song won the 1993 Grammy for Best Metal Performance, earning Reznor’s oft-quoted imagined epitaph: died, said fist fuck, won a Grammy.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"longing and contempt arrive as a single exhaled motion","built":"the bridge loops its trapped admission until the tirade reveals itself as a cage"},
"nine-inch-nails~with-teeth":{"haiku":"A transformative and controlling force that draws someone in, fundamentally changes them, and refuses to release them from its grip.","opus":"A woman works her way into the narrator and remakes him, giving him a sense of finally belonging as profound and unsettling as deep water. But she refuses to release her hold, and he braces against enduring this consuming pull again, certain that this time there is no return.","sonnet":"A seductive, addictive force — figured as a woman — takes hold of the narrator completely, blurring all former boundaries until he surrenders to it and knows he will not come back from where it has taken him.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track of Reznor's first record made sober wears the grammar of a love song, but every seduction line describes a substance: a she who arrives, improves everything, dissolves every boundary, and hands you a sense of belonging you never earned. That the entire chorus collapses to a single bared threat, chanted four times with no verb and no object, is the read on the mask itself: the euphoria has no content, only appetite, a mouth. The bridge abandons the pretty pronoun for eight identical refusals to survive one more turn of the wheel, and the outro splits into two voices at war, hers gripping and refusing release, his flatly vowing a departure he plainly cannot guarantee. The love is genuine and it bites; the whole song is a man narrating the thing that devours him as though it were the finest event of his life, which is precisely how relapse talks.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"a man narrating what devours him as though it were the finest event of his life","built":"the chorus collapses to a single bared threat with no verb and no object"},
"nine-inch-nails~you-know-what-you-are":{"haiku":"Self-deception fails eventually when others recognize one's true nature, leaving no choice but to return to origins and accept reality.","opus":"A song of self-loathing and exposure, where the narrator's attempt to patch over his flaws and sneak by ultimately rots away and is found out. The furious refrain, aimed at himself, demands he remember what he really is and where he came from.","sonnet":"A speaker who tried to pass as something he is not — patching over his flaws long enough to be accepted — has been found out, and now confronts himself (or someone like him) with the demand to stop pretending and acknowledge what he really is.","src":"haiku","fable":"A fraud confesses in the first verse, having spackled over his own fault lines for the brief span it took to pass before watching the disguise decompose the instant he was seen through. But the interrogating voice that snarls the chorus is aimed at no stranger: on this post-recovery Reznor record, self-disgust turns policeman and orders the impostor marched down to whatever baseline it decided he crawled up from. The song's real engine is the bridge, which quits arguing and simply drills one command ten times over, until the sneer hardens into a punitive mantra a man recites to keep himself in line. The mask is the performance of being fixed; underneath is the verdict that any repair is a lie and that the only honest address is the low one you were assigned.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"self-disgust turning policeman to keep the impostor in line","built":"the bridge drills one command ten times until the sneer hardens into punitive mantra"},
"nine-inch-nails~zero-sum":{"haiku":"Two people confront a catastrophic moment frozen in slow-motion apocalypse, reflecting on past compromises, shared knowledge of wrongdoing, and regret as the world ends. The song frames their mutual shame as inevitable and inescapable, reducing their lives to mere mathematical zeros.","opus":"As the sky opens and the world seems to end, the speaker reflects on choices made and rationalized against better judgment, condemning himself and others as doomed and worthless, yet finds comfort that a loved one is the reason he is not afraid to face the end together.","sonnet":"At what feels like the end of everything, a narrator reflects on choices humanity made — knowing better but continuing anyway — and expresses both collective shame and personal reassurance to a loved one as the light fades.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a deathbed confession delivered to the whole species as the sky peels open and the end arrives on schedule. Closing Year Zero, the song stretches the frozen instant before a collision across an entire life, letting the narrator inventory every wrong he saw coming and chose to commit anyway. The refrain turns that reckoning liturgical, begging mercy for hearts it has already condemned, then reduces a human lifetime to pure binary data, the smallest possible unit of a thing that meant nothing. Underneath the cosmic guilt sits one unguarded tenderness: he tells someone they are why he can face the fall without terror. The mask is judgment; what it hides is a private goodbye that outlasts the world burning down around it.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"a deathbed inventory of wrongs chosen with full knowledge, redeemed only by a private goodbye","built":"the refrain turns confession liturgical then reduces a human lifetime to pure binary data"},
"nine-shrines~nimrod":{"opus":"A vengeful takedown of an arrogant, selfish betrayer who made the singer feel worthless; consumed by unfamiliar hatred, he vows to turn the tables and escalates into a graphic fantasy of taping the traitor to a chair, unmasking him, and killing him as payment for the betrayal.","sonnet":"A revenge fantasy against someone who stole pride and tried to diminish the narrator — told in two movements: first a verbal assertion of superiority and karmic payback, then a graphic torture scene where the victim is bound, unmasked, and killed for being a traitor.","src":"sonnet"},
"ningen-isu~a-3caij0":{"opus":"Ningen Isu built a whole career on literary doom metal steeped in Japanese Gothic and classic fiction, and here they set Akutagawa's Toshishun to riff. Addressed to dead parents in the spirit world, the terrified, diseased protagonist walks a world of compassionate faces and demonic hearts, told the road is his alone until he vows to descend and join them below.","sonnet":"A song modeled on Akutagawa's story \"Toshishun,\" addressed to dead parents in the spirit world: the protagonist is terrified, diseased, buffeted by unceasing conflict in a world where faces are compassionate but hearts are demonic — the inner voice tells him the road is his to choose and walk alone, and eventually he promises to come join his parents in the underworld.","src":"sonnet"},
"nirvana~about-a-girl":{"haiku":"The narrator exploits an easy friend's feelings, taking advantage of them while unable to commit to seeing them regularly.","opus":"A crooked request for no-strings company, wanting an undemanding companion to listen while admitting he can't manage seeing her nightly, the tenderness soured by bitterness at being cast aside. Warmth and grievance sit together. Nirvana tucked this unusually tuneful pop number onto 1989's Bleach, Cobain reportedly writing it about a girlfriend, a Beatles-flavored melody hidden inside a Sub Pop grunge debut.","sonnet":"An admission of low-grade exploitation dressed as romantic interest — the speaker wants easy companionship, a listener, someone to be near without full commitment, while acknowledging they will take advantage and still can't offer consistency. Self-aware and a little guilty, it circles the same half-honest plea without ever quite resolving it.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~all-apologies":{"haiku":"A depressed man apologizes for his inadequacy and failure, viewing himself as responsible for all suffering and accepting blame for everything.","opus":"A tangle of weary self-blame and surrender, the narrator apologizing for everything, taking all the fault, and dissolving into the refrain that all we are is one. Married and buried collapse into the same fate. Closing Nirvana's In Utero and dedicated by Cobain to his wife and daughter, the song reads as a raw, resigned peace-offering laced with shame and ironic detachment.","sonnet":"A man drowns quietly in self-blame, accepting fault for everything without being able to articulate anything clearly. Shame sits on him like aqua seafoam — soft, clinging, iridescent — as he wonders what it would feel like to move through life with ease, settling instead for the weight of perpetual apology.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~breed":{"haiku":"Detachment from conventional life goals and companionship expresses reluctance toward procreation and settled existence.","opus":"A shrug of numbed, ironic apathy about domestic life and expectations, careless of getting old or breeding, wanting only to get away from home and its fears. Cobain buries anxiety under a mantra of not caring.","sonnet":"A restless, dissociated refusal of obligation — not caring if it's old, not minding anything, afraid of ghosts — proposing an alternative domestic imaginary where you can plant a house, build a tree, and not have to breed, even as \"she said\" circles without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~come-as-you-are":{"haiku":"Come as you truly are—whether old or new, friend or foe—the song emphasizes acceptance without judgment or performance, paired with repeated peaceful acceptance of death.","opus":"Cobain's ambivalent invitation to come as you are, riddled with contradictory demands (as a friend, as an old enemy) that expose how impossible authenticity is under others' conflicting expectations; the insistent, defensive denial of having a gun grew darker in hindsight given his death.","sonnet":"An invitation to someone — friend, enemy, or memory — to approach without pretense, framed with deliberate contradictions that suggest Kurt Cobain's ambivalence about trust and intimacy, closing on an anxious denial of violent intent.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~drain-you":{"haiku":"A parasitic intimacy trades bodily substances and infection, collapsing hierarchies between host and guest through mutual consumption and contamination. The speaker claims pleasure in being entirely depleted.","opus":"A love song rebuilt from the imagery of infection and infancy, where devotion becomes a mutual, parasitic feeding that Cobain frames as tender rather than repulsive.","sonnet":"A co-dependent symbiosis rendered in visceral bodily terms — two people feed off each other, chewing and passing sustenance mouth to mouth, one becoming the other's pupil while both are drained and infected by the bond.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~floyd-the-barber-2009-re-mastered-version":{"haiku":"Violation and powerlessness define a nightmarish ritual; the narrator is held captive and mutilated by townsfolk who strip their identity and autonomy.","opus":"Cobain warps the wholesome Mayberry of Andy Griffith into a nightmare of assault and murder, small-town innocence revealed as a trap. The disgust with America's cozy self-image is the whole point of the horror.","sonnet":"A nightmarish subversion of the wholesome Mayberry TV world: the narrator enters the barber's chair only to be restrained, sexually assaulted, and ultimately murdered by the entire beloved cast, transforming nostalgia into a horror of violation and shame.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~heart-shaped-box":{"haiku":"A speaker feels trapped and consumed by a possessive lover's grip. Imagery of parasitic plants and sacrifice evokes emotional suffocation and unwanted debt.","opus":"A portrait of a suffocating, sickly love rendered through claustrophobic, diseased imagery, the narrator trapped inside his beloved and wishing he could take on her illness. Cobain fuses attraction, sickness, birth, and death into a bond that feeds and consumes at once, longing indistinguishable from entrapment.","sonnet":"An obsessive, suffocating love painted through grotesque medical and religious imagery, where the narrator feels trapped inside a destructive relationship like a parasite in a host, bound by a cord he both resents and can't escape.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cobain assembles the song from two image-families that keep dissolving into one another. The first is the sugary gift-shop iconography of romance and birth: the valentine container, the florist's filler bloom, a birth-cord. The second is its predatory shadow, a sucking pitch that swallows whatever steps in, carnivorous flowers, a hanging rope, a body blackening with disease. The trick is that these are not opposites but a single thing seen from within, every token of nurture doubling as a snare, so being cherished and being devoured turn impossible to tell apart. Against that horror the chorus is almost comically deflated, not a scream but the weary clerical register of a fresh grievance filed and a debt owed for guidance he keeps calling invaluable. Read most plausibly as the wound of an all-consuming attachment, inseparable, as In Utero's title insists, from the terror of being born and bound, it locates its dread not in cruelty but in a devotion you cannot crawl out of.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","family & blood"],"means":"devotion rendered indistinguishable from being devoured","built":"the valentine container and the carnivorous flower share one image pool","fvr":"2.3"},
"nirvana~in-bloom":{"haiku":"Fans mindlessly consume and celebrate art without understanding its meaning. The song critiques how audiences enjoy the surface while missing subtext.","opus":"Cobain's jab at the clueless macho fan who loves the melodies while missing everything the songs actually say. Threaded through with imagery of nature and decay, it savors the irony that the very people it mocks would end up shouting the chorus back, mistaking ridicule for celebration.","sonnet":"A portrait of a fan who loves the music without understanding what it means — sings along, owns the gun, misses the point entirely. Around him, the world cycles through reproduction, bruised fruit, tender youth: the songs he loves were never meant for the version of him doing the singing.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~in-bloom-nevermind-version":{"haiku":"Fans mimic performance without comprehension; the audience sings along to violence while meanings dissolve into reproductive cycles and senseless imitation.","opus":"Cobain's barbed portrait of the meathead fan who loves the pretty songs and sings along without grasping their meaning, gun in hand. Written as Nirvana's audience swelled with the very people the band despised, it turns commercial success into biting irony, mocking those who embraced the music while missing everything it was actually saying.","sonnet":"A portrait of the fan who loves the pretty songs and sings along but has no idea what they mean — \"he likes to shoot his gun but he knows not what it means\" — set against sardonic nature images of reproductive glands and bruised fruit, suggesting that the music's meaning is invisible to the people who consume it most enthusiastically.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~lithium":{"haiku":"Detachment from emotional pain through depersonalization; the narrator finds spiritual peace while simultaneously revealing numbness to joy, loss, and killing.","opus":"A narrator perched on the edge veers through manic declarations, happy because the friends in his head returned, finding God, shaving his head, insisting each time he's not gonna crack. Cobain writes from behind a persona clinging to faith and mood swings, the loud-quiet dynamic mirroring a mind lurching between euphoria and collapse, the litany of I like it, I killed you spiralling.","sonnet":"A figure teetering between dissociation and brittle elation finds a kind of stability in inner voices and borrowed faith — not sanity, but a refusal to crack under the contradictions. The happiness is suspect, the ugliness acknowledged, yet an insistence on not breaking holds through every competing impulse.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~lounge-act":{"haiku":"Jealousy and possessiveness poison intimate connection while fragile agreements to remain open collapse under the weight of competing desires and insecurity.","opus":"Reportedly about an early relationship's jealousy and loss of self, the narrator guarding his identity against being smothered while still haunted by another. The bristling defensiveness masks how much the memory still gets to him.","sonnet":"A jealous, possessive love relationship in which the speaker tries to enforce emotional boundaries while being consumed by obsessive suspicion — he detects traces of another woman on his partner and cannot stop fighting the jealousy even as he knows it is destroying him.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~on-a-plain":{"haiku":"Self-love replaces connection as the speaker recognizes contradiction within and radiates apathy toward external judgment, existing on the margins of meaning.","opus":"Cobain writing about the difficulty of writing, and about the flat numbness that came with fame and heroin. Self-loathing, deflection and half-jokes pile up until he admits he's deliberately making it unclear, a mind stranded on a plateau where nothing can be complained about or felt.","sonnet":"A self-aware exercise in creative emptying — the speaker acknowledges they don't know what they're trying to say and deliberately makes it unclear, while circling around self-love, self-damage, and the absurdity of trying to communicate at all. It is a song about the futility of songwriting that functions as one anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~polly":{"opus":"Told coldly from the viewpoint of a rapist holding a captive, Cobain's flat narration of a real abduction and torture indicting the predator through his own casual menace.","sonnet":"Told from the perspective of a captor, the song maps a kidnapping and torture through the victim's small acts of composure — asking for water, for untying, catching the captor off guard with the sheer will of instinct, which amazes even her abuser.","src":"sonnet"},
"nirvana~rape-me":{"haiku":"The repeated plea for violation frames mutual destruction and abuse as desired experience, expressing shared complicity in degradation.","opus":"A confrontational reversal that dares an abuser to keep inflicting harm, the narrator insisting he is not the only victim and will outlast the violation. Kissing open sores, he promises the aggressor will stink and burn. Cobain framed this Nirvana provocation as an anti-rape statement wrapped in deliberate discomfort, its taunting structure turning powerlessness into a defiant refusal to be silenced.","sonnet":"A defiant, provocation-as-armour stance dares the world to do its worst, turning the language of violation back on those who would exploit the vulnerable. The repetition is relentless and knowing, positioning the narrator not as passive victim but as someone who has looked at the machinery of abuse and refused to flinch.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~sliver":{"haiku":"A child abandoned at a grandparent's house experiences the ordinary discomforts and terrors of separation from parents.","opus":"Cobain channels a small child's raw panic at being left with grandparents, distress rendered with almost documentary simplicity. The tantrum is the whole feeling, primal separation anxiety before any adult filter.","sonnet":"A child, dropped at his grandparents' house while his parents go out, moves through the rituals of an unwanted visit — food he cannot chew, an injured toe, ice cream, television — all while chanting the same refusal: take me home. He wakes in his mother's arms, the wish finally granted.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~smells-like-teen-spirit":{"haiku":"Youth culture's boredom, exhaustion, and performative alienation—the speaker feels stupid and contagious while simultaneously being expected to entertain an indifferent audience.","opus":"Cobain's accidental anthem of Gen-X boredom and self-contempt, an ironic cheer daring a jaded crowd to be entertained. The genius is how the apathy and denial mock the very rebellion the song came to symbolize.","sonnet":"A deliberately incoherent anthem for disaffected youth — the energy of rebellion without an object, entertainment demanded with dull expectation, a group defined by its own redundancy — Cobain embodying the generational refusal of meaning as meaning itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"An arena-sized anthem whose actual words keep sabotaging the very crowd the music summons: the verses assemble a rally in mock-slogan language, then let it curdle into a rhyming pile of unlike bodies, a bloodsucking insect and an appetite, images that refuse to add up to a message. The machinery is the meaning. The pre-chorus is pure phatic contact, a greeting shouted into a room and answered only by a taunt about sinking further, so the loud-quiet dynamic Cobain lifted from the Pixies becomes the song's argument: connection reduced to noise, an audience begging to be amused because it can no longer amuse itself. And it keeps hollowing out, each pass dumber and more infectious by the singer's own admission, until the third verse abandons sense entirely and the whole thing caves into a single hammered word of refusal, repeated past meaning. What scans as apathy is the point held rigid: a generational shrug performed so hard it turns into a scream, the flat pose of not-caring worn until you can hear the thing it is covering.","fv":2,"fnote":"The title was graffiti: Kathleen Hanna scrawled Kurt smells like Teen Spirit on his wall, teasing that he wore the scent of Tobi Vail’s deodorant — Cobain, who had no idea it was a brand, heard it as a compliment about rebellion and asked to keep the phrase.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"a generational shrug performed so hard it turns into a scream","built":"the loud-quiet dynamic enacts the argument — connection reduced to noise"},
"nirvana~territorial-pissings":{"haiku":"A paranoid person struggles to find wisdom and clarity in a suspicious world where outsiders are treated with hostility and fear.","opus":"A blast of punk sarcasm opens by mocking a hippie plea for togetherness before veering into fractured musings on aliens, wise women, and paranoia that may be justified. The narrator scrambles for a better way while bracing to wait. Nirvana's Nevermind rager compresses Cobain's contempt for macho conformity into ninety seconds of shredded throat and jittery, aphoristic dissent.","sonnet":"A furious, barely held-together plea for sanity cuts through paranoia and tribal posturing, demanding some kind of better way forward. Wisdom is acknowledged as rare and more likely female, while the narrator careens between urgency and disorientation — desperate to find solid ground in a world that keeps narrowing its options.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~turnaround-bbc-john-peel-session-1990":{"haiku":"Self-examination reveals how small and insignificant one becomes when stepping back—from city to country to planet—yet the speaker mocks this petty realization as both scary and amusing.","opus":"A dizzying dare to zoom out from yourself and see how small and repulsive you really are, then abruptly flip into a pep talk urging you to ignore naysayers and chase the absurd. It lurches between mockery and encouragement. This is Nirvana covering Devo for a 1990 BBC John Peel session, the trio filtering Ohio art-punk absurdism through their scrappy early sound.","sonnet":"Step back far enough — outside the city, the country, the planet — and what you find looking back at yourself is small, revolting, petty. The song keeps widening the frame and the verdict stays the same: you are not much. The only advice is to look anyway, and not let anyone sneak up behind you while you do.","src":"haiku"},
"nirvana~where-did-you-sleep-last-night":{"haiku":"A man confronts his unfaithful girlfriend about her whereabouts and infidelity, suspecting she abandoned him for another man.","opus":"An old murder ballad of a woman pressed to say where she slept, her husband found decapitated on the rails and her fate the frozen pines where the sun never shines. Cobain reclaims Lead Belly's Appalachian standard, his stage banter about the guitar preceding a shivering, harrowing delivery that made this unplugged rendition a haunted eulogy in the wake of Nirvana's brief reign.","sonnet":"A folk murder ballad recounts a woman who vanishes into the cold pines while her husband meets a gruesome end, his body never recovered. The narrator confronts her with the question that circles everything — where were you, who were you with — and the cold wind is the only answer that comes back.","src":"haiku"},
"nitro-fun~bass-like-feat-ramlode-2ka":{"haiku":"A confident young man lives for the moment, clubbing and pursuing excess without restraint.","opus":"A night-out hype track, a self-deprecating lightweight going to the club with the boys and throwing his hands up regardless. Bravado and bassline standing in for a good time, everything bent around the drop.","sonnet":"A straightforward night-out track about hitting the club with friends, getting drunk, dancing, and living free from the nine-to-five grind. The fantasy of sudden wealth and unbothered confidence runs through it, but the throughline is pure social hedonism.","src":"haiku"},
"nitroverts~break-the-system":{"haiku":"The speaker must break free from systemic chains that trap and define them; escape is survival.","opus":"Sleepless nights and crawling shadows framing a fight to break free of some internal system that has made him what he won't accept. The chains and the core are inner, self-rescue from suffering rather than any real revolution.","sonnet":"About wanting to escape from internal psychological prison — the narrator is haunted by unhealing memories, has lost themselves, and needs to break free from whatever structure keeps the pain in place. The \"system\" being broken is as much internal (trauma, shame) as external.","src":"haiku"},
"nitroverts~starting-point":{"haiku":"Feeling lost amid confusion and isolation, the speaker struggles to overcome themselves, clinging only to their origin as foundation.","opus":"Amid inner collapse and loneliness, the narrator loathes what he's turned into but forces himself onward, clinging to the notion of a fresh beginning as the only thing left. Abstract clinical nouns stack up like symptoms of a mind at war with itself, and the melodic metal frame turns burnout into a bleak resolve to try again.","sonnet":"Overwhelmed by isolation, self-rejection, and a sense of mounting systemic pressure, a speaker holds onto the starting point as the one thing that remains genuinely their own. Moving forward feels impossible, yet stopping is equally untenable — the beginning itself becomes both anchor and the only honest ground left.","src":"haiku"},
"no-buses~pretty-old-man":{"haiku":"Aging and mortality haunt the living; the speaker watches someone grow old and weary, wishing for eternal sleep as the only escape from time's decay.","opus":"A worried, plainspoken plea to an aging, overworked, chain-smoking loved one to rest, wishing they could live longer. The refrain about sleeping forever turns a wish for their longevity into a quiet dread of losing them.","sonnet":"A gentle, sorrow-inflected address to an ageing father figure — working all day, smoking, breath stale — whose mortality the speaker dreads; \"we can live forever / we just sleep forever\" holds both the wish for permanence and the sad acknowledgment that sleep is the only eternity on offer.","src":"haiku"},
"no-buses~untouchable-you":{"haiku":"The speaker reaches toward an emotionally distant person whose heart feels inaccessible, seeking cleansing from past pain and the burden of their untouchable love.","opus":"Yearning for someone emotionally closed-off and out of reach, hoping to find some tenderness left in a dusty heart while suspecting they'll never see past his surface. The Japanese indie band frames unreachable love as a rain that won't stop.","sonnet":"About longing for someone who feels unreachable and slightly harmful — the narrator is pulled toward a person whose smile is unhealthy for him, whose heart is dusty but not without beauty, and he oscillates between wanting to be let in and knowing he should stay away.","src":"haiku"},
"no-doubt~don-t-speak":{"haiku":"The end of love demands silence; the narrator refuses explanations that would confirm the relationship's death, choosing pain over acknowledgment.","opus":"Stefani's breakup ballad drawn from the band's real fracture, begging a departing partner not to explain because hearing the reasons would only deepen the wound. Denial as the last defense against an ending already underway.","sonnet":"The speaker begs their partner not to voice the end of their relationship, because hearing the words will make the loss real — they already know what is being said and would rather hold onto the silence than let explanation close the door on what they shared.","src":"haiku"},
"no-doubt~just-a-girl":{"haiku":"Gender constrains freedom through protective control and systemic limitation; the narrator rebels against imposed fragility.","opus":"Stefani's sardonic takedown of the constraints and condescension placed on women, exaggerating the helpless role until its absurdity shows. The mounting frustration turns a pop hook into a genuine feminist protest.","sonnet":"Biting sarcasm directed at the constraints placed on women — infantilised, surveilled, denied autonomy — the speaker adopts the diminutive label forced on her and turns it into an indictment of every restriction she has had to endure.","src":"haiku"},
"no-face-no-case~reality-check":{"haiku":"Aggressive confrontation exposes pretence through insult and dismissal; the speaker pulverises false confidence with contempt and truth.","opus":"A bilingual beatdown-hardcore assault flexing crew superiority and threatening a rival, dismissed as a worthless wannabe with nothing to prove against. The Czech verses ground it in a Prague scene rejecting industry labels and posers while asserting the group made it on their own.","sonnet":"A bilingual Czech-English beatdown track aimed at poseurs and fake MCs, mixing English aggression — \"reality check, fucking bitch better watch your back\" — with Czech verses about authenticity, label slavery, and the dream of working with Skepta, delivered as a collective middle finger from Prague's underground scene.","src":"haiku"},
"no-mana~untitled-forever":{"haiku":"Hypnotic fixation on a presence in darkness, desire or connection suspended in eternal repetition.","opus":"A hypnotic invitation to hold a gaze in the dark forever, built on near-total repetition. Less a story than a spell, intimacy and permanence conjured by insistence rather than any concrete detail.","sonnet":"A hypnotic command to stare into the speaker's eyes in the dark, forever — nothing else happens in the text, making the lyric a trance incantation rather than a narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"no-name-faces~wake-up":{"haiku":"Exasperation with someone's lack of agency and learned helplessness; the speaker demands confrontation with self-inflicted limitations and refuses to enable further self-pity.","opus":"A blunt tirade at a lazy, excuse-making freeloader who blames the system and drags everyone down while refusing every opportunity. The command to wake up is really an ultimatum to get it together or get out.","sonnet":"A confrontational address to someone perpetually lazy and defeatist, blaming the system while refusing every opportunity, ending in disgust and a final ultimatum to get it together or get out.","src":"haiku"},
"no-pressure~can-t-forget":{"haiku":"A person cannot forget their bond with another despite attempts to escape, feeling trapped with their back against the wall throughout time.","opus":"A friend dreaming of vanishing somewhere untraceable is met with hard comfort, that running solves nothing, that the world is indifferent to whatever they cherish, and that everyone eventually gets cornered. No Pressure deliver the grim reassurance with melodic hardcore bluntness, insisting the emptiness was never meant to add up.","sonnet":"Someone desperate to disappear is held in place by a friend who refuses to let go. The walls they run toward — acceptance, escape, oblivion — all prove false, and the friend gently insists that the real trap was never the world but the belief that fleeing it would finally make things make sense.","src":"haiku"},
"nobody-has-heart~sakura":{"haiku":"Connection transcends self-doubt and uncertainty; love dissolves darkness and forges eternal bond.","opus":"A love that rearranges a life the instant eyes meet, requiring nothing spoken, framed through delicate cherry-blossom imagery. The speaker doubts himself, unsure how anyone could depend on a man who cannot depend on his own judgment, yet the other's radiant smile drives out his inner shadow and forges a bond that seems to be the whole point of living.","sonnet":"A chance meeting that reorganizes a life — eyes meeting, no words needed — carries the question of whether destiny or choice created the bond, and the speaker, who cannot even trust himself, finds that this person's smile dissolved the darkness in his heart and made the world beautiful in the way that feels like home.","src":"haiku"},
"noctica~afterlife":{"haiku":"Rejection of eternal suffering and the synthetic afterlife; the speaker claims freedom through cyberpunk rebellion and disregard for consequences.","opus":"A defiant cyberpunk creed of chroming up and refusing to fear death in a dystopian city of clones and corporate control. Drawing on Night City's edgerunner mythology, indifference to living or dying becomes the ultimate act of rebellion.","sonnet":"A Cyberpunk-influenced declaration of nihilistic defiance — the narrator doesn't care whether they live or die in this life, rejects the digital god of social media and the cloned hero culture of the city, and claims their place in a dangerous underground as an edgerunner.","src":"haiku"},
"nocturnal-bloodlust~life-is-once":{"haiku":"Self-destruction and reinvention battle for control as the speaker rejects enslavement and false reflection, demanding liberation through violent refusal of victimhood and reclamation of agency.","opus":"The Japanese metalcore band delivers a violent motivational sermon, urging a broken listener to break out vengeance, rise, and reject slavery to fear and self-pity. Having killed himself once and clawed back, the narrator insists life comes but once and must be fought for. Aggressive defiance framed as survival, screaming past the curse of despair.","sonnet":"A Japanese metalcore rallying cry against self-erasure: the narrator admits they nearly disappeared, killed themselves once, and stared into the reflection of a false smile — then pivots to furious insistence on rising, refusing slavery, and seizing the single life given. \"The headache like a bullet\" is gone; what remains is forward motion.","src":"haiku"},
"noija~meaning-to-your-pain":{"haiku":"The speaker fights to give meaning to another person's pain, but finds themselves invaded by that pain, unable to escape a relationship that has infiltrated their body and mind.","opus":"A portrait of a connection that soured into intrusion, where someone welcomed in as a hoped-for companion takes up residence in the mind and body like something that cannot be exhaled. The narrator struggles to comprehend a suffering that breeds dread and won't depart. Contemporary melodic metalcore that turns closeness into body-horror menace.","sonnet":"Someone else's suffering has moved in — breathed into the speaker's lungs, painted the room dark blue, refusing to leave. The speaker fights to make sense of that pain rather than suppress it, but the boundary blurs: what began as compassion has become infestation, and now they regret opening the door while still unable to close it.","src":"haiku"},
"noiseast~yokohama":{"haiku":"Nature's suffering and regret's weight burden those aware, yet lights and touch offer glimpses of internal power to resist participation.","opus":"An eco-conscious plea set against dreamy neon imagery, mourning a weeping planet and the cruelty of the strong preying on the weak while refusing to join the lie. Finding calm beneath blossoms, the narrator holds onto the hope that tomorrow's light and a loved one's touch will overcome the grief. Metal tenderness about guilt, escape and inner power.","sonnet":"Environmental despair and the law of the strongest meet a personal escape: neon lights and pink flowers and the touch of hands offer peace from a world that ignores its pain and its dead; the lights of tomorrow, the speaker promises, will defeat the sorrow.","src":"haiku"},
"noisettes~i-we":{"haiku":"Individual identity dissolves into collective power; two people reshape perception and convention through unified presence and intentional disruption.","opus":"A shift from doubting, one-sided questions to a defiant collective confidence, I becoming we. The song is about the transformation that partnership grants, two people together outshining and outpacing everyone watching.","sonnet":"A tension between individual and collective action runs through the song: the speaker questions their role as protector and enabler, accuses their partner of not committing, and ultimately frames their shared momentum as something that will leave everyone else dazed — a defiant partnership that can either fly together or fall apart.","src":"haiku"},
"noiseworks~hot-chilli-woman":{"haiku":"A man expresses intense sexual desire for an alluring woman, describing her as spicy and irresistible.","opus":"A grinning slab of lust dressed in kitchen metaphors, a woman who makes the singer sweat like a hundred degrees, whose heat brings him to ecstasy while mama and daddy disapprove. There's nothing beneath the innuendo but appetite, and it revels in that. Noiseworks were an Australian pub-rock band fronted by Jon Stevens, and this brash late-eighties single leans into the sweaty, hook-driven swagger of that scene.","sonnet":"Straightforward heat-and-desire celebration, all appetite and sensation — a woman so intensely attractive she makes the speaker sweat and go weak, and the whole thing is framed as forbidden cooking, parental disapproval included. Pure mid-tempo lust with no ambiguity about what it wants.","src":"haiku"},
"noisycell~a-10oahxq":{"haiku":"Obsessive repetition of delusional thought spiraling into madness; circular thinking traps the mind and prevents escape from one's own torment.","opus":"NoisyCell made their name scoring anime with English-language post-hardcore that specializes in bruised perseverance, and this is that ethos distilled. From a near-quit moment at a late-night station, the song embraces the counterintuitive discipline of losing on purpose, absorbing defeat after defeat because only the justice that survives every loss is real, until a final round where losing is at last forbidden.","sonnet":"A person who once nearly quit at a late-night train station grapples with the counterintuitive truth that you have to keep losing — absorbing defeats, drinking your own blood — because the only real justice is the one that survives every loss and refuses to die, until the very last round where losing is finally not allowed.","src":"haiku"},
"noisy~alligator":{"haiku":"Dream-state consciousness and possessive control merge as the speaker demands love while remaining unreachable, an alligator dictating reality through sleeper's will.","opus":"A dreamlike track blurring desire and detachment, an untouchable narrator who keeps waking before intimacy can hold him, casting the elusive other as the alligator and mind dictator. Whispered pleas for love collide with his insistence that no one can truly reach him. Hazy dance-rock about wanting closeness while remaining a self-protecting dreamer forever slipping away.","sonnet":"A dreaming speaker caught between sensual closeness — hands, lips, the heat of a body — and a declaration of psychological invulnerability: the alligator as mind dictator that cannot be touched or dominated, always waking up later no matter what.","src":"haiku"},
"noisy~earthquake":{"haiku":"Escapist hedonism temporarily relieves working despair; the weekend becomes sanctuary where exhausted bodies find momentary transcendence through rhythm.","opus":"The weekend as pressure release from a going-nowhere working life, a couple of nights of bass and rave to endure the dread of the workweek and Monday's grey return. Escapism named honestly as escape, worth every fried neuron.","sonnet":"About using raving and nightlife as an escape from the deadening grind of a working-class week — the bass is physically overwhelming and the weekend becomes the only place where you get more out of life than your dead-end job allows.","src":"haiku"},
"nolo~coming-out-the-sides":{"haiku":"Accumulated trauma erupts unexpectedly; the speaker hemorrhages from pressure they cannot contain, leaking destruction through invisible seams.","opus":"Someone unraveling under the pressure of their own success feels a destructive urge to burn it all down and dreads falling back into old ruin. Amid paranoid imagery of tapped calls and traded faces, they brace for a collapse already coming out the sides.","sonnet":"Pressure and internal fracture build until something is literally 'coming out the sides' — nervous ticks, the devil laughing, dead zones with microphones — as the speaker hangs by a thread and asks whether, after the sky falls, there is any choice left or just the slow burn of trading faces.","src":"haiku"},
"nolo~fermi-1":{"haiku":"Fragmentation of self into multiple versions; unable to grasp which reflection holds reality.","opus":"An eerie end-of-the-world tableau beside a static TV and a filthy mattress, the speakers asking in disbelief whether this is the last thing they'll hear or remember. Staring at other versions of themselves, identity glitches and dissolves as everything winds down.","sonnet":"Two people staring at a static TV beside a dirty mattress, confronted with different versions of themselves — the Fermi paradox as personal crisis, the last sound and last memory converging on the same moment of paralysis and disbelief.","src":"haiku"},
"nolo~lost-pines":{"haiku":"The speaker acclimates to poison while mourning the beauty they cannot stop from fading; numbness becomes survival's cost.","opus":"A person trying to let go of a lost love wonders whether they can lean back into life without falling through the floor, growing numb but not free. The bittersweet taste of the memory is something they're uneasily getting used to.","sonnet":"A breakup the speaker can't fully digest — 'I tried to spit you out but I'm getting used to the salt sour taste in my mouth' — is processed through cold bare-feet phone calls and the numb realisation that wanting to let go and being able to are entirely different things.","src":"haiku"},
"nolo~re-animator":{"haiku":"Burned by failure, the speaker seeks redemption one last time, knowing the cost may be too high.","opus":"A final attempt to patch up and revive a fading relationship, asking for room while sensing the other's fear of what he'll turn up. The repeated just-once-more vows betray how often he's said them before, wondering whether the thing has already run its course.","sonnet":"A tentative attempt to repair a damaged relationship — the narrator tries one last time to reconcile, uncertain whether the other person wants them to stay or go, oscillating between giving in and pretending, needing to know if what they had is already dead.","src":"haiku"},
"norma-jean~sleep-explosion":{"haiku":"Wrong words at wrong times poison connection; reset daily, but the cycle repeats endlessly.","opus":"The self-loathing of forever mis-saying things at exactly the worst moment, purged in violent catharsis toward a fresh start. The glass is neither half full nor empty but simply gone, a mind that resets every day yet keeps repeating the same mistakes.","sonnet":"An explosive encouragement to stop second-guessing everything and just act — the narrator and subject are both always saying the wrong thing at the wrong time, but 24 hours and a new beginning are always available; throw out the old, shake it off, drag out the smoke, start.","src":"haiku"},
"norma-jean~songs-sound-much-sadder":{"haiku":"Mortality and the gradual learning of death rather than life; inevitable decline toward legend-hood. While seeking to live, people are actually learning how to accept their own dying.","opus":"Norma Jean turn metalcore fury into battered defiance: beaten-down men who keep going and realize that learning to live was really learning to die, yet still insist on being remembered as legends. Dread flickers into stubborn hope, self-hood embraced as a fatal habit even as nightmares arrive.","sonnet":"A generation of survivors realises mid-journey that everything they mistook for living was preparation for dying. False hope and habitual self-deception are named plainly, while a thin thread of resistance flickers — somewhere in the ruin of nightmares-come-true, someone still insists there might be a way to step down and begin again.","src":"haiku"},
"norma-jean~sword-in-mouth-fire-eyes":{"haiku":"Vulnerability and bondage intertwine as acts of love and desperation; the speaker is helpless and armed at once, caught between shame and the need to be known through intensity.","opus":"A bond that feeds on mutual helplessness, where prey turns predator and both parties share the guilt. It is about intimacy built on shame and the wish to be led through land you stole.","sonnet":"Two figures bound in a helpless, codependent love — sword in mouth, fire eyes — that they are desperate to maintain even as they recognise its damage; the speaker turns back from his own reflection, sharpens his tongue on lies, and pleads to be walked through the land he stole, asking for guidance out of shared guilt.","src":"haiku"},
"norma-jean~with-errors":{"haiku":"The song depicts conflict as illusion and speech as silenced, describing a world where truth decays before exposure and enemies wage war on communication itself.","opus":"A knotty rumination on perception and self-reflection, the narrator circling a sun he's forever kept his distance from, gazing into shattered mirrors where discord proves illusory and truth decays before it's exposed. Norma Jean marry mathcore chaos to obscure imagery, treating awareness itself as a warped loop that had no true beginning or end.","sonnet":"A figure locked in stasis — motionless, unable to orbit the sun, too close to the source of all burning — confronts a version of reality built on deception. Truth is the first casualty in any conflict, and the enemy here is someone whose legacy poisons every generation that follows, endlessly, without origin or end.","src":"haiku"},
"normandie~babylon":{"haiku":"Unchecked ambition propels civilisation toward inevitable collapse; the narrator descends into abyss despite awareness of the fall's imminence.","opus":"A metalcore parable about relentless, ruinous ambition, building ever higher while knowing the taller the tower the worse the drop. Babylon stands for the seductive collapse we chase anyway, half-aware we're sinking even as we climb.","sonnet":"Humanity keeps building higher and deeper — past all reason and horizon — driven by ambition that guarantees its own collapse, and the speaker watches the descent into a rabbit hole they're not sure leads anywhere but down.","src":"haiku"},
"normandie~collide":{"haiku":"Conflict becomes the engine of passion as two lovers clash over their fundamental incompatibility; confrontation and compromise prove equally destructive.","opus":"A volatile relationship fought for despite constant conflict, the need to feel worth something colliding with a partner's dishonesty. Love here is survival through the storm, an ultimatum to stay or go before it breaks him.","sonnet":"A person in a deteriorating relationship pleads for honesty and vitality, refusing to quietly accept the lies their partner wraps in white, while insisting love is worth the storm even when their words destroy each other.","src":"haiku"},
"normandie~hostage":{"haiku":"Internal captivity to another's demands erodes selfhood; the narrator breaks free through acknowledgement of manipulation and self-assertion.","opus":"Anxiety and a controlling relationship rendered as captivity, the narrator lying awake, biting his tongue, counting down to make it end. Fear of losing control and of time running out is the real cell he's held in.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a relationship or mental state that operates like captivity — unable to shut off their thoughts, going through the same cycles each morning, and afraid of being held this way forever, growing old inside someone else's grip.","src":"haiku"},
"normandie~renegade":{"haiku":"A person loses faith and trust in themselves, running from internal wolves and building homes they can't inhabit, becoming emotionally cold and alone.","opus":"A reckoning with breakdown in which everything the singer constructed topples and he bolts from the very predators who once kept him company, no longer able to trust his own mind. The Swedish alt-metal band paints lost faith as self-banishment, his stare freezing over, his home forsaken for a lonely refuge that feels less risky than closeness, belief and warmth reduced to scorched cinders.","sonnet":"A person watches their inner landscape turn cold as faith collapses and self-trust disintegrates. They keep building refuge only to flee it, arriving at a bleak clarity: solitude may be the only honest position left when the wolves that kept them company were their own illusions.","src":"haiku"},
"north-atlas~house-of-mirrors":{"haiku":"Distorted reflection and fragmented identity; the speaker cannot find authentic self in endless mirrors.","opus":"A claustrophobic meditation on isolation and self-estrangement, using the funhouse conceit to dramatize a mind that can only reflect itself back. The narrator wonders whether prolonged withdrawal has hollowed him into something spectral, feeding his worst impulses while chasing sleep, unable to tell paranoia from truth.","sonnet":"Paranoid self-reflection in a haunted inner landscape — you can't see a vampire in a mirror, you can't call a preacher when nobody believes, and the house of mirrors only shows your own head bowed at your feet. The repeated \"are you sure you're not a ghost?\" asks whether the sense of self that remains is genuine or just another reflection feeding the wolf.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~4d":{"haiku":"Guilt and the burden of survival follow those who lost loved ones to overdose and tragedy.","opus":"Mourning and survivor's guilt after losing someone to a fatal overdose inside a shattered household, the narrator tormented by the notion that a person much like himself robbed them of everything they might have had.","sonnet":"The narrator reflects on losing someone to addiction or an overdose in a broken home and is left carrying guilt rather than grief — the weight of having survived and perhaps failed to prevent what happened.","src":"haiku","fable":"The fatal overdose of an addressee leaves the speaker holding not clean sorrow but its uglier relative, the shame of outliving, and the real move of the song is how accusation folds back on itself: the phrasing that runs the choruses insists the person who stole this life was someone indistinguishable from the narrator, refusing to let him off the hook for the same addiction and inherited fate. Northlane, in their 2022 electronic-metalcore reinvention, let that hook curdle under repetition until the likeness stops sounding like fellow-feeling and starts sounding like confession. The picture of wringing red from a bare rock reads two ways at once: the pointlessness of ransacking a ruined household for anything worth keeping, and the speaker squeezing emotion from a self that has run dry of everything but culpability. The outro splits its voice, effort persisting against a dying will, which is the entire track compressed: labor that shows up far too late to save anyone and can serve only as penance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"The family-addiction subject was the previous album Alien's declared territory; Marcus Bridge said he wanted to move past it for Obsidian — 4D reads as the theme following him anyway.","themes":["death & grief","shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"survival indicted as complicity in the same addiction","built":"the hook curdles under repetition until fellow-feeling slides into confession"},
"northlane~4d-mashd-n-kutcher-remix":{"haiku":"Loss metastasizes across generations; the speaker inherits guilt from those who fell to addiction while climbing alone from the broken home.","opus":"Survivor's guilt addressed to someone lost to an overdose, wondering whether they still felt loved as their hold loosened. The gut-punch is admitting that a person much like the narrator himself stole their whole world away, leaving only the shame of being the one still here.","sonnet":"About survivor guilt and a broken home — the narrator asks whether a parent still loved them when they lost their grip and died of an overdose, describes searching for gold in the rubble of that childhood, and carries the guilt of being the one who survived. Someone — an addiction, the disease, a person — took the world away from both of them, and what remains is nothing but that guilt.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~abomination":{"haiku":"Self-disgust metastasizes into paralysis: the body becomes a prison for shame, and survival means becoming numb to the person you've become.","opus":"Self-disgust made monstrous: re-emerging as an impostor and a slave to blame, numb and ashamed, calling yourself the abomination. The horror is turned entirely inward.","sonnet":"A song about shame and psychological collapse — the speaker has re-emerged from something destructive feeling like a fraud and a monster, numb in the burning sun, too depleted to believe in themselves or fight anymore.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane's Obsidian marks Marcus Bridge's public reckoning with addiction and its psychic aftermath, and this track sits in that confessional register: the speaker returns from collapse not restored but transformed into something he cannot recognize, an accusation leveled at himself from inside. The song works by looping the same short cluster of self-indictments so that recovery reads not as an arc but as a trap, each cycle re-arriving at the same verdict without progress. The one landscape it offers — a scorched, waterless nowhere where every want has been baked out of a person — is less a scene than a diagnosis of the emotional deadness left behind, the flat aftermath rather than the crisis. What refuses to resolve is the point: the refrain never lets him step outside the charge, and the parenthetical hush before the final return dramatizes a voice silenced by its own shame before the loop starts over.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"the monster confession reread as a verdict the speaker cannot lift","built":"a single bracketed silence tightens the collapse mid-song"},
"northlane~afterimage-feat-ian-kenny-karnivool":{"haiku":"Fragmented pieces of identity cast conflicting shadows, the speaker questions their own worth while searching for coherence beyond the mirror's fractured reflection.","opus":"Confronting an inherited self, the grief and defenses passed down that keep surfacing like a photographic afterimage. It asks whether identity can reach past the mirror to something beyond what was handed down.","sonnet":"A grief-saturated meditation on inherited identity and loss — the speaker is haunted by the image of someone gone, questioning whether they are scattered fragments or something coherent, unable to ignore the way the dead person's shadow still falls across everything they are.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two strangers meet as if across a mirror, and the song works the seam between them: fingerprints that refuse to match, yet a pull like a shared heartbeat insisting they were cut from the same fall. Northlane and Karnivool's Ian Kenny trade the same vocal restlessness, which suits a lyric that keeps asking whether identity is something you receive from another face or something you are trapped alone behind. The afterimage is the trick at the center: a burned trace left on the eye after the source is gone, so the other person becomes proof that some part of the self persists just past the glass. The recurring question about what lies past the mirror's edge is never answered, and that refusal is the point, since the singer admits he hides behind his own defenses rather than cross into the recognition offered to him. What reads as a duet about two people is really one psyche split into watcher and reflection, begging the twin it has invented to lead it back out of nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","family & blood"],"means":"asking whether the parts of oneself that cast a different shadow can be claimed","built":"the song title names the optical phenomenon that structures the whole meeting"},
"northlane~bloodline":{"haiku":"Generational trauma and inescapable toxic family bloodline.","opus":"Escaping a hellish childhood shaped by drug-addicted parents, carrying their bad blood and trauma while surviving on one's own.","sonnet":"Growing up in a chaotic, neglectful household under drug-addicted parents and escaping that toxic inherited trauma alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"Marcus Bridge frames what he inherited as a wound that stays open. He swears he clawed free alone, then confesses the poison still moves through him — so leaving and carrying become one act rather than opposites. The details pin it to real people: a Kings Cross childhood among addicted parents lost to their own highs, a kid wondering aloud whether any of this qualified as care. What raises it past ordinary defiance is that the pride and the damage draw from a single vein, and the hammering final lines land less like victory than a sentence handed down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance"],"means":"leaving and carrying the wound become one act not opposites","built":"the final lines land as a sentence handed down not victory"},
"northlane~bloodline-health-remix":{"haiku":"Inherited trauma and poverty define the speaker's past, yet they claim liberation through self-rescue while questioning the meaning of family love.","opus":"A survivor's reckoning with an addict parent, the inheritance of trauma running through the blood like the parent's own scars. Growing up amid neglect and highs, the speaker refuses to call that love yet cannot fully escape who it made him.","sonnet":"A survivor of a chaotic, drug-addled childhood with parents who were absent or high, who made it out alone and carries the scars in their blood — the memory of growing up in squalor and neglect cannot be escaped because it shaped who they became.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~carbonized":{"haiku":"Exposes deception and predatory behavior, predicting that when exposed to scrutiny the false persona will burn away and reveal the corruption beneath.","opus":"An indictment of a manipulative, predatory person who uses smoke and mirrors and looks down on others, with the narrator vowing they'll be exposed and warning he has no heroes left.","sonnet":"The narrator exposes a predatory figure who hid behind a false ally facade — using mirrors and smoke, exploiting those beneath him — and declares that once the light comes out, his true nature will be burned away and he will face the consequences.","src":"haiku","fable":"A takedown of a fraud built entirely from the language of disguise: reflective glass, haze, a new layer of paint slapped over rot. The narrator admits he once bought the act himself, complicit in the same borrowed reverence he now wants to burn down. The title lands as a threat, since to char something into a silhouette is exactly what daybreak does to a creature that only survives in the dark. Strip the cosmetic gloom and light leaves nothing standing. What lingers past the verdict on this predator is quieter than triumph, the real wound being that he has run out of anyone left to admire.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"exposure as the weapon, light doing to a predator what daybreak does to a creature that only survives the dark","built":"the title names the threat — charring something to a silhouette — and the narrator admits prior complicity in the act he now wants to burn down"},
"northlane~citizen":{"haiku":"Society imprisons people through mass surveillance, control systems, and corruption; the narrator urges whistleblowing and revolution. References Edward Snowden's leaked government secrets.","opus":"A surveillance-age protest dedicated to Edward Snowden, framing constant state monitoring as an undertow dragging everyone deeper, and honoring the whistleblowers burned for exposing how far from free we actually are.","sonnet":"A surveillance-state paranoia song inspired by Snowden-era revelations — the deeper you dig into how governments and systems actually operate, the more you realize freedom is an illusion, and ordinary citizens are tracked, managed, and controlled.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane turn the surveillance state into an ocean current: the more you dig, the deeper the undertow drags you into the unknown, so knowledge becomes not liberation but a downward pull past the point of return. The second verse sharpens the political charge, saluting the whistleblower martyred for exposing the corruption of those we obey, before the closing movement drops the metaphor entirely to name the panopticon outright — archives, satellites, the ever-watching eye of Big Brother — and offers its coda as a direct thank-you to Edward Snowden. The move is the point: an abstract riptide resolves into a named person and a named act of defiance, insisting that the surveillance we drown in can still be resisted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"the lament for drowning in the unknown turns into a salute to the leak","built":"descent into the rabbit hole is reframed mid-song as the only path to clarity"},
"northlane~clarity":{"haiku":"Accusation of betrayal and scapegoating—the speaker's truth and suffering weaponized against them by someone unwilling to admit fault.","opus":"Also on Obsidian, this is a wounded reckoning with a family member who casts the speaker as the villain. Made into a scapegoat and led to slaughter, he insists his accusations were meant sincerely, and the repeated cry to clarity is a demand not to be gaslit out of his own history.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who has consistently betrayed and gaslit the speaker: the speaker's pain was dismissed, their truth weaponised against them, and they were cast as the villain in a history being deliberately rewritten. The song escalates from accusation to an image of total subjugation — the speaker made a scapegoat, silenced, led to slaughter — arriving at a cold verdict that nothing between them can change.","src":"web","web":"Marcus Bridge turns family backlash over his autobiographical album into a fury at scapegoating — 'I'm the lamb of sacrifice for your mistakes' — pleading for clarity to cut through the blame-shifting.","fable":"A song staged as a fight between its chorus and its verses. The hook is a bargaining prayer, addressed to lucidity itself as though it were a person on the verge of walking out, repeated until it works less like a request than a way of staying upright. Beneath it the verses turn on a relative who has rewritten the family record to cast the narrator as the offender, and Marcus Bridge answers by escalating into ritual sacrifice imagery: the speaker offered up as the animal butchered for someone else's failures, silenced and damned by name, marched toward a killing that resolves into pure deadlock. The final cruelty is the burial move, both sides pronounced dead in advance so nothing can be mended. On the electronic-veined, present-day Northlane of Obsidian, the only mercy survives in that recurring plea, a hand groping for something firm while every stanza wrenches it back into the quarrel.","fv":2,"themes":["family & blood","madness & the mind"],"means":"a bargaining prayer for lucidity repeats until it works as ballast","built":"the chorus is addressed to clarity as though it were a person about to walk out","fvr":"2.4"},
"northlane~clockwork":{"haiku":"A portrait of burnout and creative emptiness where the speaker runs on fumes and fear of failure. Time is running out, energy is depleted, and the dream risks derailment by internal sabotage.","opus":"A raw account of creative paralysis and the fear of failing, the dread that you are wired to sabotage your own dream. It cuts deeper into burnout and a longing for hope through eyes clouded by despair.","sonnet":"A confession of creative paralysis and self-sabotage — an inner voice insisting the speaker is inferior and destined to fail — with a desperate plea for someone to help him see through the clouds before time runs out entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane's Obsidian-era metalcore turns creative burnout into a survival crisis: the song is a musician staring at a blank page, convinced an internal saboteur has already decided he will fail, running on fumes instead of inspiration. The refusal that runs through it is a collision of two exhausted maxims — the performer's obligation to keep going even with nothing left to give, laid against the pleaded hope that beauty might still pierce a fogged sight-line — and the read lives in how one demands motion while the other admits there may be no strength for it. By the breakdown the metaphor goes literal and clinical, an absent heartbeat in a body gone cold, so the running-out-of-time chorus stops being a deadline and becomes mortality itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness"],"means":"reaching for color from someone who has stopped feeling his own heartbeat","built":"the burnout alibi is shed before the breakdown arrives"},
"northlane~colourwave":{"haiku":"A person discovers that they have agency to reshape themselves through their choices, trials, and creative expression rather than being defined by past darkness.","opus":"Emerging from depression staged as a storm, the singer recalls seeing only dullness while the world was actually saturated with color, until realization crashes over him. The refrain, that he is as changeable as the churning planet and that his life is his own to shape from his failures, recasts pain as material for remaking himself.","sonnet":"A recovery from a dark period of depression, framed as the moment colour floods back into a previously grey world; the narrator claims full authorship over their own life, casting personal growth as something as elemental and fluid as a storm reshaping the earth.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title names the trick: a colourwave is synaesthesia weaponized against depression, and the song stages recovery as a shift in perception rather than circumstance — the world always dripped in colour; the sufferer only ever registered grey. Northlane builds it as a storm sequence where rainfall doubles as both the assault of overwhelming sensation and the erosion that carves new channels of self, so that being battered and being remade are the same weather. The refrain that the self is authored by its own trials, not spared them, turns the mantra of self-creation into an ecological one: the speaker claims kinship with an Earth that reshapes itself through violence rather than in spite of it. What looks like a simple affirmation-metalcore mantra is really an argument that pain is the medium of formation, and that mesmerism — the album's own title concept — is reclaimed here as a chosen, clear-eyed absorption in a reality that was never actually grey.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","nature & the elements"],"means":"identity authored out of survival, not given — written in the flood","built":"the storm serves as a structural model for the self being eroded and remade"},
"northlane~crash-burn":{"haiku":"Arrogance built on a foundation of noise and superiority will inevitably destroy itself — the speaker watches a figure flying too close to the sun, certain the crash is coming, the worthless words already dissolving into atmosphere.","opus":"A scathing address to the arrogant and self-deluded, warning that hubris always meets its reckoning. Drawing on the Icarus myth, Northlane predicts the collapse of those who mistake noise for authority and opinion for fact, insisting their hollow certainties will evaporate no matter how loudly they preach.","sonnet":"Directed at a narcissist or demagogue — someone who flies too close to the sun on worthless words, preaches nothingness as truth, confuses opinion for fact. The Icarus image is a verdict: their altitude is delusion, the fall is coming, and no one is listening anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~cypher":{"haiku":"Authenticity dissolves within oppressive systems designed to extract hope while dangling impossible possibilities as torture.","opus":"A refusal to be reduced to a component in a machine—a battery, a false prophecy spoken for by others. A spoken passage frames the universe as a master torturer dangling hope only to grind you up, against which the narrator asserts the right to be who he wants to be.","sonnet":"About the dehumanizing pressure of technological and social systems that try to override individual identity — the speaker refuses to let machines or false prophecies speak for them, insisting on autonomy and authentic self-definition even while being pulled into a framework that would reduce them to a battery, a unit of output.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cypher is built on a sampled spoken parable stitched into a metalcore panic about agency: the singer volunteers to become a power cell if that trade buys selfhood, a bargain that already reveals how little he expects to own. The central sample recasts existence as cosmic cruelty — a bored child dismantling a trapped insect wing by wing, hope dangled only to be withdrawn — and the song answers not with rebellion but exhausted refusal, insisting no genius or savior gets to voice him. The repeated plea against being submerged in circuitry and the shrinking closing chant, stripped down to just the battery line, enact the surrender the lyric fears: identity flattened into a single function, called in and used up.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape","surveillance & control"],"means":"wishing to be reduced to a battery rather than remain feeling","built":"a borrowed voice mid-song reframes the whole opening plea"},
"northlane~dante":{"haiku":"Regret and disconnection haunt relationships as love itself becomes the instrument of suffering, trapping the speaker between acceptance of punishment and the impossibility of reconciliation.","opus":"Grief over a severed connection turned inward as accepted punishment, wrestling with whether any choice could have healed a broken mind or whether the things we love are simply built to wound us.","sonnet":"A reckoning with a relationship's end that caused irreparable internal damage — the speaker accepts punishment for their own role in the disconnect, questions whether love or open arms could have changed anything, and acknowledges that the things we love most ultimately hurt us.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane borrow the medieval frame of Dante's descent to stage a private reckoning, where the war that cannot be seen is grief itself, corroding a body worn out from carrying it. The song sits in an unresolved argument about agency: whether pain arrives by fate or by a choice that felt like release in the moment, and the singer refuses easy absolution, holding both the regret and the sentence he takes for it as inseparable. Its bridge tips into genuine questioning, asking whether tenderness could ever have repaired a mind already broken, whether the lost person was ever truly holdable at all. The ending is the real gut-punch: the loop of hating those quiet cues of hurt collapses at last into hating the silence they leave behind, so the noise was never the enemy, only the absence underneath it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"accepting the sentence yet refusing to stop carrying what did the wounding","built":"punishment is embraced while the memory it is built on is never released"},
"northlane~dark-solitaire":{"haiku":"Isolation and self-scrutiny trap the mind in recursion; the speaker falls inward, searching for recognition while circling the drain of their own dissolution.","opus":"Dissociation as a slow circling of the drain, roaming unrecognizable and half-hoping no one finds you. The turn comes in admitting the effort to become the person you see in yourself has hit its limit and needs help.","sonnet":"Someone lost in their own head, roaming empty streets hoping not to be found, circling the drain while haunted by a memory they cannot release — aware they need help to become who they want to be.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dissociation song built as a downward spiral: the interior collapse of falling into one's own head, then a flight through a city of paper streets, walking so anonymously that even the singer's own face has become unrecognizable to others. The middle turns the descent literal — circling a drain while nursing pain, holding onto a faint hope that the very act of moving might crack the sky open. What arrests the fall is grief: an unresolved loss that refuses to permit a farewell, so the spiral cannot complete. The close reframes the whole thing not as surrender but as an unfinished self-project — the fight to become the person glimpsed inwardly — and lands on the bare, un-heroic admission that this cannot be done alone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape","identity & becoming"],"means":"solitude reread as flight once the drift toward the drain is named","built":"the freedom pose collapses the moment the speaker says he needs help"},
"northlane~details-matter":{"haiku":"A response to criticism and betrayal from someone who exploited and tried to destroy the narrator. Despite attempts to erase and break, the narrator endures and refuses to disappear.","opus":"A furious rebuttal to someone who lied, belittled and tried to silence the narrator to build up their own reputation. Against their attempts to break him and make him disappear, he defiantly insists he is still here.","sonnet":"A defiant response to someone who has spread lies, undermined the speaker's reputation, and tried to erase him. Despite the attacks, the speaker insists he has not disappeared and directly confronts the other person's insincerity and fraudulence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survival song aimed squarely at a tormentor who mistook cataloguing someone's flaws for authority — the person who nitpicks every detail as if precision were the same thing as truth. Northlane build it as a war of attrition: the verses are all the enemy's tools, the pulling-apart and the striking-out of a name, while the chorus answers each attempt with the same flat refusal to vanish, its repetition doing the work of endurance rather than argument. The venom sharpens because the accuser turns out to be the hollow one — a monument raised on empty ground, a rubber cheque that keeps rebounding worthless, all credentials and no sincerity — so the title collapses into irony: the details this person weaponized never added up to a self. By the outro the opening threat returns unchanged, and that unbroken loop is the point, the voice they wanted silenced simply refusing to stop saying it is still here.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"From Alien, the record Marcus Bridge built from his own childhood — the band's gloss on this opener: the story of a protagonist other people would feel happier to see suffer. The address is personal, not professional.","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"endurance performed through repetition rather than argument","built":"title collapses into irony — the accuser's weaponised details add up to no self"},
"northlane~dispossession":{"haiku":"Internal collapse and identity loss as the speaker wanders lost in toxic mental states, eventually facing their weakness and demons publicly.","opus":"Northlane, the Australian metalcore act who grew steadily more atmospheric through the 2010s, follow a man coming apart inside, no longer recognizing his own reflection and fleeing inner torments through a fog with no exit. He pleads for guidance, unable to judge good from bad, and fears his frailty will be laid bare to everyone before he steels himself to face it.","sonnet":"A man lost inside himself — hollowed out by a toxic stupor, unrecognizable in his own reflection — watches his vulnerability being exposed to a world he can no longer trust. Others press in, whispering poisons he refuses to absorb, while he searches locked corridors of his own mind for the capacity to love again.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~dream-awake":{"haiku":"Recognizing life as a dream and awakening to authentic existence without fear.","opus":"Australian progressive metalcore band Northlane confront fear and mortality directly, embracing pain and regret as a beautiful chaos while acknowledging that death is certain, yet striding into the future undaunted. The turn is transcendent, waking from a nightmare to finally become the self you were always meant to be, and Northlane wed crushing heaviness to their trademark cosmic uplift, belonging and wholeness the reward for that awakening.","sonnet":"A confrontation with mortality that converts into a reason to live — the speaker acknowledges terror at finite time, the certainty of death, and the chaos of emotion, then chooses to become the dream rather than stay trapped in the nightmare. Grounded, eyes open, looking up.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~echo-chamber":{"fable":"Obsidian's lead single builds the digital-age panic attack straight into its architecture: a doomscroller's mind rebuilt as a resonant room, where every input returns amplified and warped until the self can no longer tell its own thought from the incoming feed. The verses interrogate the addict in second person, needling the compulsion that persists even unwatched, while the chorus flips to a first-person plea for a mercy so extreme it prays for the loss of hearing itself. What Marcus Bridge sharpens across the two verses is the trap's cruelest turn: a stranger's certainty burns straight into your wiring, shame follows, and the only worth left is measured by what you can produce for the same machine that's eating you. The final chorus removes even the escape hatch, extending the prayer past silence into total erasure, so the song ends not with relief but with the addiction winning outright.","fableDeep":"It opens like tech support for a mind: 'have you tried turning it off, taking a break?' — the digital-detox cliché weaponized into a real question, because the follow-up ('when nobody is watching do you make the same mistakes?') asks whether the noise is in the phone or in you. The prayer is for sensory self-mutilation as mercy: deafness as the only moderation tool left. 'The free mind of a stranger sears the tether to your brain' is the modern wound precisely named — other people's visible unbotheredness burning the leash you're on. The creed at the end — create, don't consume — is the one exit the song trusts.","haiku":"A person trapped in cycles of repetitive thinking and self-destructive behavior wants to escape the feedback loop of their own mind and toxic external voices.","opus":"A cry against the deafening churn of an echo chamber, most likely the endless loop of online chatter and self-attack, with the singer wishing for the mercy of silence as manufactured static warps every decision and something has to break.","sonnet":"The narrator is tormented by an incessant internal noise — looping anxieties, judgements, and compulsions that poison decision-making — and desperately wishes for silence, recognising that creation is the only thing that truly matters.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"craving deafness from a noise that is already inside the speaker","built":"the plea for silence drops at the end into admission of dependence"},
"northlane~eclipse":{"haiku":"Endures abuse and becomes a willing target for another's pain, yet refuses to become like their abuser.","opus":"Standing up to an abuser who takes out their own fear, blame and shame on the narrator, who refuses to be afraid and vows he will never let himself become like his tormentor.","sonnet":"The narrator addresses someone — likely a parent or guardian — who used fear and abuse to control them, resolving that despite carrying the marks of that treatment they refuse to become the same kind of person.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor speaks straight at the person who hurt them, and the song's entire weight hangs on a vow that surfaces only near the end. The chorus reads the cruelty like a case file: guilt kept leashed and short-tethered, disgrace circling starved and feral, an ache its bearer cannot voice and unloads instead onto whoever stands closest, which here is the singer. Testimony becomes departure in the closing lines, where the abuser's belief that the two are identical gets refused outright. The singer claims a self larger than any surname passed down, and swears never to harden into what once shaped them. That refusal is the eclipse itself, the shadowed outline stepping at last into its own clear light.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"Alien-mate Bloodline names the inheritance this song survives — the same parents, the same bad blood; Eclipse is the walking-away.","themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"the refusal to become what shaped you framed as stepping into your own unshaded light","built":"the eclipse of the title is reversed — not a darkening but the shadowed outline finally clearing the thing that blocked it"},
"northlane~enemy-of-the-night":{"haiku":"A father's addiction and self-destruction, viewed through a child's helpless observation of his inability to change or escape desperation.","opus":"A son recalls his father's downfall, a first album bought with spare change interrupted by word of a near-fatal assault, then addiction reclaiming a man who could have freed himself but was never going to change. Northlane, the Sydney metalcore band, root their atmospheric heaviness in raw autobiographical grief tied to a rough part of the city.","sonnet":"A child witnesses his father's violent collapse into addiction and the streets around Kings Cross, powerless beside a man who cannot change. Years of intermittent hope and relapse reduce a father to an unrecognizable stranger — a casualty of desperation whose son is left riding shotgun through a wreckage he did not choose.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~evian":{"haiku":"Fractured connection persists despite decay; the speaker drags dead weight toward impossible redemption.","opus":"A plea for reconciliation after chasing ambition past all sense and leaving broken hearts rearranged behind him. Hauling the burden of pain and doubt, the narrator begs forgiveness and swears to make his way back to someone before the chance is gone.","sonnet":"A song about two people who have damaged each other and are trying to find their way back — they chased something too far, rearranged their hearts, and now drag the dead weight of the relationship's wreckage, the narrator begging forgiveness and vowing to keep searching until they can reach the other person again.","src":"haiku","fable":"A duet of pursuit built entirely on inversion: the verses ache toward reunion while the choruses recast the beloved as an unbearable load the speaker refuses to set down, so devotion and revulsion become the same gesture. The porcelain image is the hinge of the whole song, two people so brittle that the very act of colliding to hold each other is what shatters them, which reframes the recurring vow to reunite once it is over as less a promise than a death-sentence read as reprieve. Northlane's post-Alien electronic melancholy suits it, the softness of the surface hiding that the reconciliation on offer only arrives after everything has already ended.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"a reunion plea bent into penance by the weight of not being able to afford another loss","built":"the promise to find you curdles from hope into penance across the song","fnote":"The title spells naive backwards — the star-chasing that went too far and the porcelain fragility sit under a name that reverses into naivety."},
"northlane~fade":{"haiku":"A victim of suffering witnesses loss and carries eternal pain while memory becomes the only binding force connecting the living to the dead.","opus":"Grief as a slow erasure, a mourning survivor watching memory of the dead thin out despite the permanent pain of loss. The chosen one imagery frames coming through devastation into a second, haunted life.","sonnet":"A song about losing someone — possibly to addiction or mental illness — watching them break apart and being unable to save them, the grief binding the narrator to that loss like a rope around the neck; the second half traces the survivor rebuilding without the weight of the other person's destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grief song built as a resurrection myth that never quite lands. The verses cast the bereaved as a doomed heroic figure passing through catastrophe toward some promised return of daylight and second life, while the chorus keeps yanking that mythology back to a bedside where one person simply watched another die. The central image-system is one of binding — grief cinched tight at the throat, tethering the living to the site of a death — set against a memory that dissolves anyway, so the two forces work in opposite directions: the tighter it holds, the more it disappears. Northlane, two albums into the Marcus Bridge era on Mesmer, use that architecture pointedly, granting the mythic redemption arc all the verse and breakdown momentum, then letting the recurring hook overwrite every hard-won transcendence with plain loss. The refusal is the reading: the salvation language is a mask the song keeps trying on and the fade keeps stripping off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","identity & becoming"],"means":"surviving a witness's grief until the inner fire finally spends itself","built":"the speaker shifts from casualty to steadier figure across the verses"},
"northlane~freefall":{"haiku":"Witnessing violence and trauma numbs the speaker, severing him from empathy and leaving him incapable of feeling connection or pain.","opus":"A harrowing scene of a home invasion witnessed by a child, who recalls a father begging not to be killed in front of his children and is left emotionally numb, unable to feel or give empathy after the trauma.","sonnet":"The narrator describes a childhood marked by domestic violence — a father breaking down the door, a family in terror — and the lasting emotional numbness it produced, an inability to feel or empathise that was stolen from them permanently.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane's Marcus Bridge writes trauma inheritance here, and the freefall is dissociation itself—the sensation of falling that never lands, where numbness is the only stable ground left. The song moves from a child's shuttered bedroom to a home invasion begged out in front of one's own kids, then collapses the timeline in the bridge, equating a gun to a father's temple with the speaker's inability to feel anyone else's pain: the violence witnessed became the emotional off-switch passed down. The devastation of the outro is that it reads not as complaint but as confession—having survived by going numb, the speaker now has no empathy left to give, and names that theft as the thing that will keep him from ever being whole.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","family & blood"],"means":"numbness taken from a father arrives as the cost of having survived him","built":"the shield that made childhood survivable becomes an adult inheritance","fnote":"The break-in is real — a man burst into the King's Cross motel room where Bridge's family was staying and held a gun on his father; the pleading parent in the second verse is remembered, not imagined."},
"northlane~get-free-triple-j-like-a-version":{"haiku":"The narrator declares intent to escape and seeks freedom from unrequited love and places that no longer serve them.","opus":"A yearning to break loose and ride into the sun, haunted by a refrain that no one ever loved the narrator, so why should anyone. Coaxing come-ons urge someone to flee California. Northlane cover Major Lazer's dancehall-pop hit for triple j's session series, recasting its bright hooks in heavier progressive tones so the plea for freedom rings both euphoric and quietly wounded.","sonnet":"An aching need for escape drives someone who has never felt loved toward the open horizon. The compulsion to flee is real and raw — ride into the sun, leave California behind — while the sense of being fundamentally unlovable makes freedom feel like the only thing left worth chasing.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~heartmachine":{"haiku":"A person trapped in emotional suffering exists as a machine of pain, paralyzed by unstable feelings and needless stitching.","opus":"Depression rendered as bodily malfunction, the nervous system misfiring while the mind runs out the clock waiting for pain to lift; suffering framed as a machine you are trapped inside rather than a mood you can reason with.","sonnet":"A song about the physical experience of anxiety or illness — shaking body, spinning head, paralysis — framing human suffering as being trapped inside a malfunctioning heart-machine, with the eventual acknowledgment that time and honesty can begin to rebuild.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane spend the whole song inside metalcore's panic-attack vocabulary, then quietly reframe it: the shaking, spinning, pinned-nerves body is the machine, a heart that feels everything at once and cannot regulate itself. The verses set a listless clock-watcher against a jolt of light that drops him to his knees, so the suffering is bound up with hope rather than opposed to it, the same current that revives and overloads. The plural we in the chorus turns private breakdown into shared condition, and only the bridge breaks the loop, letting the wound heal into strength once there is nothing further to conceal.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"a body reporting its own maintenance as symptom, registering every sensation at full volume","built":"the outro relapses into the same complaint the bridge tried to resolve"},
"northlane~impulse":{"haiku":"Isolation and weakened human connection in digital culture and technology.","opus":"The Sydney metalcore band turn their heavy, electronic-laced sound on the loneliness of screen life, where the whole world fits in a phone yet real human bonds keep thinning. It laments a hyper-connected culture that leaves people isolated, technology growing sharper while intimacy fades.","sonnet":"A diagnosis of digital loneliness — circuits grow smarter, signals grow stronger, but the bonds between people dissolve. The phone in the palm compresses the world into a box while actual connection hollows out: hollow as a hologram, electricity without the spark. Impulses flow through copper wires and through veins simultaneously, and neither route reaches another person.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~inamorata":{"haiku":"A celebration of recovery and transformation through love, where the beloved has rescued the speaker from despair and now the speaker vows reciprocal devotion and loyalty. The song centers on mutual spiritual restoration.","opus":"A devotional love song of being rescued and made whole by a beloved, vowing to now burn and bear the strain for them in return as a new dawn breaks and the speaker feels rebuilt and restored.","sonnet":"A love song directed at a devoted partner who saved the narrator from drowning — literally and figuratively — restored their sight and sense of home, and whose boundless patience dissolved their panic; now the narrator declares it is their turn to burn with equal loyalty and devotion.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane cast recovery from addiction as a rescue romance, addressing sobriety itself as the beloved that dragged them up from drowning and unset their feet from the concrete. The verses move through a reversal of drowning imagery into breath and dry land, then the choruses answer with a debt of devotion: having been kept alive, the singer now vows to burn in return, loyalty offered back as fuel. The bridge turns to a dawn that rebuilds and reshapes, so the whole arc runs from being broken free to swearing to catch the rescuer if they stumble in turn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"being saved and being bound arriving at exactly the same dawn","built":"the drowning imagery runs deliberately backwards to enact rescue"},
"northlane~intuition": {"haiku":"The song questions whether people truly know themselves or are conditioned by external systems and paradigms they've inherited without questioning. It urges listeners to break free from intellectual prisons and follow their own intuition.","opus":"A summons to interrogate the inherited assumptions that quietly shape our lives and to reject a manufactured mental cage in favor of trusting one's own instincts. Uncertainty widens into a rift and chaos pours through, yet the narrator's clarity holds. Northlane pair djent precision with soaring atmosphere, turning progressive metalcore into a manifesto against unquestioning obedience.","sonnet":"A challenge to inherited systems of thought frames social conditioning as an intellectual prison, its rules composed by conductors who benefit from compliance. The narrator pushes back against unexamined belief — urging a break from the orchestrated script of reality and a return to raw questioning, personal obsession, and instinct.","src":"haiku","fable":"An interrogation of inherited belief that treats the whole social order as a score written by others and performed, unquestioningly, by everyone born into it. The song's argument runs through its own musical metaphor: the paradigms we live by are composed by virtuosos and conducted from above, while the mass plays the clashing composition and mistakes obedience for order. Marcus Bridge's clean lines carry the diagnosis — we don't know who we are, we follow a script and end up its collateral, conditioned inside walls built of borrowed thought — before the breakdown detonates the fix. The title is the answer to the intro's demand to keep asking why of everything you were handed: against the inherited thread and the conducted composition, intuition is the one authority not handed down, and following it is framed as the only escape from the system. It's Northlane's Mesmer-era thesis in miniature, philosophy delivered as a build that resolves the abstract into a single imperative.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","politics & society","freedom & escape"],"means":"intuition as the one authority not handed down from above","built":"the musical metaphor runs the diagnosis — paradigms as a score conducted from above"},
"northlane~is-this-a-test":{"opus":"A restless spiral asking whether pain carries purpose or is merely a hollow spillover from a bottomless nothingness. Our own instincts and the collapse of larger systems bleed together, the question circling back over and over with no resolution.","sonnet":"A dissociative, fragmented song about existential uncertainty — the speaker cannot tell whether what they are experiencing is meaningful consequence or random fallout, whether the emptiness they feel is their own nature or a system failure, cycling through the question without resolution.","src":"sonnet","fable":"The looped question that never resolves is the whole point: the song stalls on whether suffering is a trial with meaning or merely an accident of an indifferent void, and it never answers. The clipped diagnostic middle section, cycling flat labels for malfunction as if reading off a checklist, stages depersonalization itself, turning inner collapse into detached technical readout rather than felt experience. That refusal to progress past the question is the meaning: Northlane build the track as a closed circuit that decays into pure repetition, so the interrogation becomes indistinguishable from the emptiness it interrogates.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the refusal to resolve the question becomes the answer itself","built":"diagnostic phrasing frames a person as a misfiring machine"},
"northlane~jinn":{"haiku":"Entrapment by circumstance and internal darkness, where the speaker is surrounded by walls built from accumulated disadvantage. Light is blocked and the garden inside is twisted like the self.","opus":"A portrait of feeling walled in from birth, the narrator hemmed by barriers assembled piece by piece that shut out light and leave the odds against him. He casts existence itself as a waking nightmare, finding his only escape in what he deliberately draws into his lungs, a hint of numbing or self-medication amid the confinement.","sonnet":"A person describes being hemmed in from all sides—by circumstances stacked against them from the start and by whatever they turn to for comfort—until the walls block out all light. The only escape is choosing what to breathe, but the sense of being trapped and twisted inside persists.","src":"haiku","fable":"The wall the speaker builds is defensive architecture that becomes its own prison: the same masonry meant as protection is what seals out light, so shelter and confinement are revealed as one structure. Northlane, in their Marcus Bridge-fronted electronic era, tie this to the title's jinn — an unseen agent of one's own making, the anxiety-mind that offers safety and delivers a slow smothering. The middle turns inward to a poisoned garden the speaker can no longer read as living or dead, mirroring a dissociation where the outer green world and the inner rot have become indistinguishable. The refrain's cruel hinge is that what soothes is exactly what strands: the coping mechanism, the retreat into a self-selected breath of air, is both the only escape and the deepening of the trap. The outro's fading repetition enacts the sentence itself — the light going out by increments, the odds pronounced as verdict rather than obstacle.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the relief inseparable from the harm, comfort becoming the trap","built":"the closing return to day-one odds refuses any resolution, cycling back"},
"northlane~kraft-feat-brendon-padjasek-structures":{"haiku":"Ego and hypocrisy hide behind false ideology; the phantom prophet reveals nothing but empty rhetoric while the world moves on indifferent to their vanity.","opus":"A takedown of a hollow figurehead, a prophet with nothing real underneath the persona, addicted to destruction and being believed. The stance is that truth and genuine resonance will outlast the fraud.","sonnet":"A takedown of a false leader or ideological figurehead who has lost credibility — someone who preached conviction but whose ideology is hollow, whose destruction leaves nothing real underneath, and who is warned that the truth, when it arrives, will completely demolish whatever remains of their influence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A demolition of a false prophet — the burnt-out idol still peddling a gospel long after his own faith in it flatlined, his supposed masterwork just a painted shell with a void where the foundation should be. The song pits two kinds of frequency against each other: the fraud runs on borrowed doctrine and stagecraft, a stale mascot the crowd hangs onto from sheer habit, while the two vocalists lay claim to a signal that outruns any label or stage name and needs no costume to travel. That is why the promise to keep his secret lands as scorn rather than mercy — there is nothing hidden worth guarding, only a bluff already called. Trading the mic between them, they let a living hum swallow a dead one whole until the reckoning arrives not as argument but as impact, a verdict you register in your chest a beat before your mind catches up.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"a living signal swallowing a dead one whole without needing argument","built":"dual-vocal trading enacts the verdict rather than arguing it"},
"northlane~leech":{"haiku":"Parasitic destruction of nature through human exploitation and apathy.","opus":"An environmental warning from Australian metalcore band Northlane, dragging the listener past dying reefs, choked oceans and burning forests while humanity slumbers through the destruction. A disease spreads unnoticed through the planet's veins, and the band demand honesty instead of alibis, insisting the stakes could not be higher.","sonnet":"Environmental collapse framed as a betrayal by a sleeping species — reefs bleeding oil, forests in flames, a cancer growing silently in the lifeblood of the planet. The band-aid metaphor runs through it: cosmetic fixes for structural damage, footprints covered rather than eliminated. Everyone fell asleep while the bed burned and there is still no clear way out.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~let-it-happen":{"haiku":"Something unstoppable approaches; surrender feels inevitable even as the speaker resists and runs from an impending transformation.","opus":"Amid ceaseless clamor and frantic motion, a voice pushes toward yielding to an oncoming tempest that will sweep away everything untethered, part dread and part liberation. Northlane merge ambient electronics with metalcore force, the narrator negotiating with his own faltering pulse and begging his mother's pardon while concluding he was prepared for this the whole time.","sonnet":"Something long suppressed pushes toward the surface with an urgency that can no longer be outrun. The noise of avoidance gives way to an internal voice insisting on surrender — not defeat but readiness — and the eventual recognition that the upheaval coming was always something the person was already prepared for.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~let-me-disappear":{"haiku":"Withdrawal into silence becomes safety, the speaker buries their true self to maintain connection while internally disintegrating beyond reach.","opus":"The impulse to pull away and keep one corner of yourself hidden, admitting someone close while shutting off a part you'd rather they never glimpse. Being labeled cold or checked-out gets reframed as a deliberate calm, alongside a plea to simply be allowed to fade from view rather than be examined.","sonnet":"A person who has deliberately chosen invisibility — burying their inner life underground, keeping thoughts locked away, insisting that disappearing is a conscious, peaceful decision rather than depression, while still questioning whether anyone ever understood what they truly needed.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane's post-Marcus Bridge introspection turns disappearance into a boundary rather than a defeat: the narrator grants access and withholds interiority in the same breath, a paradox by design that he insists is no conspiracy — just the private architecture of a self that has made its peace off the grid. The song's real inversion arrives when it reframes another's diagnosis: what you read as distraction or absence he corrects as numbness and constancy, so vanishing becomes the act of staying present on his own terms while refusing to be the spectacle someone else needs him to be. By the close, the softening of the refrain — dropping the pretense of ever showing — lands the thesis: to disappear here is to reclaim the light he grows himself in, an exit that is really a self-possession.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"vanishing reframed as sovereignty over the only space left to breathe","built":"the speaker corrects the other's reading of him as absent to deliberately hidden"},
"northlane~masquerade":{"haiku":"People hide behind false faces and mutual deception in social circles; the narrator recognizes others' hypocrisy but admits he is equally complicit. Trust becomes impossible when everyone performs.","opus":"Metalcore rage at a social world built on lies and two-faced loyalty, sharpened by the singer's refusal to exempt himself, admitting he's just as complicit in the deceit he's condemning.","sonnet":"A room full of liars and two-faced people including the speaker themselves — the song is about the impossibility of trust when everyone performs an identity and hides their past, and the self-aware admission that the speaker is exactly as guilty as those they condemn.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane's Adrian Fitipaldes turns a party into a moral courtroom, where a fog of perfume, lust, and untrustworthy glances stands in for a social scene run entirely on performance. The song's real move is the flinch inward: after indicting everyone in the room, the narrator convicts himself as identical and the first to blame, so the accusation loses its footing as a diagnosis of others. Deathcore guest Drew York (Stray from the Path) enters as the harsher prosecutor, but his verdict lands on the same target, since no mask survives one's own history. The closing vow to remember every face, both the ones that lifted him and the ones that cut him down, reframes the whole rant as memory rather than revenge, admitting the speaker was always dancing at the same masquerade he condemns.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression"],"means":"the shared condition of the mask named only after the finger-pointing turns inward","built":"the accuser admits he is first in line among those wearing the mask"},
"northlane~miasma":{"haiku":"Toxic manipulation rewrites identity while the speaker fights internalized rage and demands recognition of their right to exist.","opus":"Being reshaped against your will by a controlling force, dismantled bit by bit in a ceaseless trial while swallowed rage builds inside. The narrator, twisted into a stranger to himself, invokes the old paradox of whether any true self remains once you've been mended part by part into someone you no longer know.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship in which the speaker is manipulated, controlled, and stripped of identity by someone who treats them as property to fix and experiment on — the anger internalized over time finally surfaces as the speaker recognizes the abuse and that they deserve better.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane build the song as a coercion drama staged inside a single consciousness: the chorus voice is the abuser, all electric promise and the pledge to repair a partner who was never broken, while the verses belong to the one being repaired against their will — recognizing themselves dissolving into a stranger, an heirloom made hollow, a possession rather than a person. The Ship of Theseus is the hinge: if every plank of you is swapped out to satisfy someone else's design, are you still yourself, or only the residue of their experiment? The title supplies the answer — miasma, a toxic atmosphere you breathe without seeing, which is exactly how the manipulation works, dressed as devotion and delivered as a favor. What makes it land is that the two voices never resolve; the seductive hook and the internalized fury loop back identically at the end, because the trap is that the victim has begun to speak the captor's language.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","love & desire","identity & becoming"],"means":"devotion that unmasks as dismantlement, part stripped until nothing original remains","built":"the Ship of Theseus image names the mechanism the song works toward"},
"northlane~mirror-s-edge":{"haiku":"Fractured identity casts infinite shadows; the self multiplies across each reflection.","opus":"A brief interstitial fragment gesturing at fractured identity, where the self splinters into pieces that each throw a different shadow. More a threshold image than a developed narrative, it suggests a reality lurking just past the reflection, at the edge of what can be seen or known.","sonnet":"A self fractured into divergent pieces stands on the threshold of transformation, reaching beyond its own reflection toward something unknown — the sparse image of casting \"a different shadow\" is all that anchors this liminal crossing.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~node":{"haiku":"Individual agency as catalyst for collective change and social transformation.","opus":"Australian progressive metalcore band Northlane deliver a sincere call to personal agency, insisting one individual can trigger real change and shake a complacent world. It diagnoses a sedated, apathetic humanity trapped in narrow thinking and answers with hope that compassion could steer us toward a future where love outlives us, offering empowerment as genuine rather than hollow.","sonnet":"A call to individual action against collective apathy — humanity numbed into submission, hooked on tunnel vision, slipping away while the speaker insists that a single step can shake the earth. The counterforce is love persisting through hearts rather than systems.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~nova":{"haiku":"Explosion of self as liberation: only through shattering can the spirit rebuild itself, gathering fragments into something truer than what came before.","opus":"A mind breaking down and rebuilding, losing sanity to finally surface and see the lie buried in the soul. The spirit explodes and the pieces are glued back knowing the cracks will always show.","sonnet":"A song about hitting a spiritual rock bottom and rebuilding — the speaker lost their mind, was blinded by a deeply embedded lie, watched their inner self explode into pieces, and is now gathering what they can while wondering if the repair will hold.","src":"haiku","fable":"Nova turns collapse into its own kind of engineering problem: the speaker survives a shattering, then kneels to reassemble the fragments, aware the repair is provisional. Northlane frame recovery not as triumph but as maintenance, the flip from present to past tense marking the breakage as something that has already happened while the fixing never quite finishes. What lands hardest is the honesty of the seams — he gathers only what he could reach, hopes the adhesive bonds, then admits the fault-lines are still visible under the patch. The wreckage repeats and the lesson is supposedly delivered, yet the body stays pinned to the ground, so the redemptive arc the electronics promise gets quietly undercut by an inability to rise.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind"],"means":"taught by the rupture but still on the floor, not yet healed","built":"catastrophe and aftermath are held in the same moment, not in sequence"},
"northlane~obelisk":{"haiku":"Time's passage and mortality, with human ambition burning away as life ends.","opus":"The Australian prog-metalcore band turns mortality into a race against a finite clock, time running out like sand while the narrator chases fleeting minutes and fading dreams. Borrowing a T. S. Eliot line, it resolves to live without regret and blaze fully rather than quietly go out, using the certainty of death as fuel to burn bright.","sonnet":"Time as adversary — every second shortens the window, dreams stay mirages just out of reach, and the flame is clearly burning down. The response is not surrender but combustion: a decision to rise and learn from what remains rather than mourn what has already slipped away.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~obsidian":{"haiku":"Human decay radiates as infinite destruction transforms the universe into inert darkness; redemption ceased when humanity chose corruption.","opus":"Humanity as a self-immolating species radiating its own decay, playing god while burning everything past the point of return. The cosmic scale only sharpens the verdict: we scorched what we were given.","sonnet":"A diagnosis of humanity as a self-destructive species that has crashed into the universe like a meteor and turned everything to ash — desensitized to its own violence and decay, collectively too far gone to reverse the damage, with individuals serving as their own corrupt gods.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane's title track reads humanity as a species-scale meteor: a cosmic-metal conceit where we are scattered points of light that fuse into one catastrophic mass and crash into creation, hardening what we touch into obsidian — glass born of fire, beautiful and dead. The self-deification is the pivot; naming ourselves gods who tend the cauldron and grow numb to the burning bodies below turns the grandeur into indictment, the swagger of dominion curdling into ecological and moral self-immolation. Northlane run this through their electronic-djent register so the language of collapse becomes a language of hardware — the poison winning is framed as a glitch closing in and a breakdown from within, the machine of us failing at the code rather than the surface. What the fatalist refrain enacts is the argument: too far gone repeated until repetition is the point, a species narrating its own event horizon and finding, in the eclipsing of sin rather than its atonement, that consequence has simply been cooled over and glassed shut.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","nature & the elements","politics & society"],"means":"the poison let in on purpose, with no way back named at the close","built":"cosmic extinction imagery burns off to reveal a private, intimate rot"},
"northlane~ohm":{"haiku":"Cosmic perspective diminishing terrestrial troubles; transcendence through wandering observation.","opus":"A song of transcendence and escape, imagining flight above a shrinking world where every earthbound trouble diminishes with altitude. The wanderer sheds dead weight to rise beyond the noise and static of ordinary life, seeking clarity in thinner air even at the cost of never returning home.","sonnet":"Distance as cure — the farther a wanderer travels from the shrinking globe, the smaller every trouble becomes, until escape from the static feels like shedding dead weight and learning to breathe in orbit. The speaker urges ascent even knowing it means never coming home.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~ohm-northlane-remix":{"haiku":"Distance and altitude dissolve earthly troubles, offering escape and perspective as one moves away from suffering and toward transcendence.","opus":"It follows a wanderer ascending away from a darkened, shrinking earth, shedding dead weight to take flight and escape into open space where troubles look smaller the farther one goes.","sonnet":"The song describes the relief of distancing oneself from the world — rising above its noise and shrinking troubles until they feel small — while acknowledging the cost: becoming a perpetual wanderer who can never return home, drifting free of the static but also untethered from belonging.","src":"haiku","fable":"This 5G remix strips Ohm down to its panic-attack core: a dissociating consciousness losing gravity, described as a mind spun loose from its orbit into open space to flee an overwhelming electrical hum. The verse's frantic imagery is bracketed by two mantras that fight each other — the descent chant and a repeated self-soothing command — so the song becomes the sound of someone talking themselves through disintegration, the calming instruction stacked in a rigid loop precisely because the calm won't hold. In Northlane's post-Alien arc of Marcus Bridge writing about mental health, the mantra structure is the point: composure asserted by sheer repetition over a nervous system already in freefall.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance"],"means":"the mantra of composure repeated until it is indistinguishable from collapse","built":"reassurance and disintegration trade places until they become the same motion","fnote":"The title holds two readings at once — ohm the unit of electrical resistance, om the meditation syllable — and the song is exactly that pairing: a breathing chant recited against electric static."},
"northlane~paradigm":{"haiku":"Present with someone through their struggle, the speaker fears they'll relapse; echoes of the past loom as quicksand pulls them deeper into familiar darkness.","opus":"Standing beside a partner sliding back into addiction, the speaker is haunted by the sense he's lived this before as old flaws split open again. It's love bracing for ruin, refusing to bank on a cure, terrified they're both being pulled under, yet pledging to stay to the finish and pleading with them to repair what's broken.","sonnet":"A vigil kept over someone the speaker fears will relapse into old destructive patterns — they stand by loyally, haunted by déjà vu, watching for the cracks and pleading internally for the relationship's paradigm to hold.","src":"haiku","fable":"A vigil song that keeps mistaking itself for a rescue. Northlane pitches the addiction-and-relapse register of Alien inward toward a relationship, and the fixed refrain of standing beside someone becomes less a promise than a compulsion the speaker cannot put down — repeated so insistently it curdles into the déjà vu and quicksand it names, a witness watching the same collapse rerun while claiming to arrest it. The genius of the structure is that the title question is never his to answer: he can brace, dread, and stay, but the paradigm is theirs to mend, and the outro's unanswered repetition leaves him exactly where the intro found him, holding a breath he swore he wouldn't hold.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"love as vigil, watching the collapse arrive on the schedule it already knew","built":"the vow of devotion gradually refigures as dread of the predictable ending"},
"northlane~paragon": {"haiku":"Mourning a lost mentor or martyr whose wisdom persists through their legacy.","opus":"A mourning tribute to a lost friend whose writings and hopeful music spread warmth through an empty, ruler-less realm, the narrator swearing to keep faith with what mattered and promising a reunion beyond death. Northlane, the Australian progressive metalcore band, cloak this sorrow in atmospheric, ambient-tinged heaviness, honoring the fallen rather than raging at the loss.","sonnet":"A eulogy for a thinker and artist whose work outlived him — the song mourns someone who built wisdom that his followers are determined not to lose sight of, even as his own most quoted line insisted that hope is a prison. What his music actually spread was love, and the contradiction between his words and his chords is the heart of the tribute.","src":"haiku","fable":"An elegy for a fallen songwriter — the last of a dying line of true believers, canonised the moment he stops singing, his real monument the lines he leaves rather than any adoration he sought. It all turns on a borrowed creed: he once taught that hope was a cage, yet the movement of the song insists his music did the opposite of what that bleakness promised, spreading love through a throne room stripped bare, its ruler gone and no one left willing to fight. That borrowed creed lets Northlane grieve the peer in his own vocabulary, then answer it — the refusal to let his words fall silent becomes both a vow of memory and the song's only available consolation, a door held open toward the other side rather than a wound left raw.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written as a tribute to Architects' Tom Searle (d. 2016) — the band assembled it largely from Searle's own lyrics, and the hope-is-a-prison creed is Gone With The Wind's line answered back.","themes":["death & grief","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"grieving a peer inside the vocabulary he left behind","built":"the borrowed creed he taught is answered by the song that refutes it"},
"northlane~plenty":{"haiku":"The speaker cannot confront abandoned memories and remains unprepared for revival; when death comes, they surrender, having already had plenty.","opus":"A metalcore meditation on being haunted by a memory that abandoned you, unable to face it, picking at your own skin as the mind goes quiet and mad. The refrain is exhausted refusal, imagining the grave and saying you can't start over because you've simply had enough.","sonnet":"About being too damaged to face the memories that abandoned you — the narrator insists he's had enough of life, that when he dies the worms can have him, and that he can't pretend to be ready to start over.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane's clean-sung centerpiece from Obsidian reads exhaustion as a refusal to be resurrected: the memory that abandoned the narrator is his own former self, and he cannot bear to meet it again. The song's engine is the phrase that names it — plenty is not abundance but a full quota of suffering already spent, so when he imagines his body given back to dirt and worms and is offered the chance to start again, the answer is a flat no. Where Marcus Bridge's earlier writing chased recovery, here the movement is anti-recovery: the addled, walkabout-with-parasites verse builds not toward release but toward the burial as relief, so the closing loop of the abandoning memory lands as acceptance rather than grief — he has had enough, and enough is the point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"rehearsing his own burial as the only rest he trusts remaining","built":"exhaustion wears the grammar of acceptance to disguise the plea"},
"northlane~quantum-flux":{"haiku":"A song celebrating awakening to infinite love and joy hidden within life, calling for people to close their eyes and dream to feel freedom.","opus":"Where much of Northlane's work dwells in gloom, this song swings toward euphoric release, presenting joy and limitless devotion as forces hiding in plain sight. The narrator drifts weightlessly into vastness as hues swirl behind shut eyelids, comparing the newly awake to youngsters groping through blackness. Subatomic uncertainty becomes shorthand for endless possibility, and giving oneself over to reverie reads as the way back to vitality.","sonnet":"A kind of ecstatic awakening: he perceives love and color and infinite potential flowing through everything, while most people sleepwalk through life numbed and cold. The invitation is to close one's eyes and surrender to bliss rather than remain blind to the joy already present.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~ra-exe":{"haiku":"Cosmic dislocation and existential vertigo dissolve certainty into shifting perception where direction collapses. The speaker seeks grounding while spiraling through void, unable to stabilize.","opus":"A cosmic, dissociated search for grounding, the narrator drifting through space and gazing into a star that burns off his outer casing to reveal how damaged he's become. He longs for a pocket of calm to cut himself loose from a bottomless downward loop and finally find footing.","sonnet":"A speaker adrift in psychological and existential emptiness — a desert in the mind, drifting days, collapsing under a spiral — seeking the stillness of an oasis and the grounding to stop the endless drift, sailing through the rift between sky and ocean.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~refuge":{"haiku":"Migrant desperation and institutional indifference create catastrophe as borders militarize against human suffering.","opus":"A furious act of political witness on the refugee crisis, indicting a hostile world that builds walls while desperate people drown fleeing war. Northlane frames indifference as complicity, invoking surveillance and manufactured fear to argue that the crisis is engineered, and that any child could one day be the stranger begging for shelter.","sonnet":"The refugee crisis is addressed directly and furiously: borders built like graves, indifference masquerading as policy, and a media machine that normalises the dead — \"never struck a chord when the blood flowed from a heart you didn't know.\"","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~render": {"haiku":"A mind questions the reality it perceives through flawed senses, trapped by dogma and malleable perception that denies life meaning. Existence feels hollow and dreamlike.","opus":"The narrator wonders whether existence amounts to nothing more than what the senses report, imagining himself as the operator of a fleshy machine driving an unaware creature whose inner workings stay concealed. Rigid, prideful thinking has left people sightless and hollow. Northlane fold this Bill Hicks-flavored idea that life is merely an amusement into their atmospheric djent, doubt gnawing from within.","sonnet":"Consciousness is reframed as a pilot navigating a biological machine whose inner circuits remain permanently hidden. Received beliefs and dogmatic certainties hollow out meaning, and the speaker wonders whether reality is only what the senses report or whether something larger and less legible lies just beyond ordinary perception.","src":"haiku","fable":"A skeptic's spiral built as a build: it opens on the oldest doubt in philosophy, the self as a pilot sealed inside a nervous system whose wiring it can never inspect, unable to verify whether anything the senses report is real. From there Marcus Bridge widens the target to inherited dogma — the stories a soft, pliable brain accepts without testing, the egotistical certainties that pass for knowing — and names them as the thing that has blinded us rather than steadied us. The payoff lands on the deflation that follows once those certainties collapse: days that read as empty because belief has been withdrawn and nothing replaced it. The closing turn, that this is just a ride, takes up Bill Hicks’s it’s-just-a-ride reassurance — an inspiration the band has named outright, and the song swaps existential dread for the suggestion that the unreality it feared might be the point, a ride to be enjoyed rather than a prison to escape.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult","freedom & escape"],"means":"inherited certainties named as the blindness, not the steadying force","built":"the Bill Hicks it's-just-a-ride close swaps existential dread for the ride as the point"},
"northlane~rift":{"haiku":"Divisions between people don't matter—we're all the same condemned creatures in a dying world, yet the speaker refuses to fear the void between them and others.","opus":"A defiant address across an unbridgeable gap with another person, insisting that what divides them is arbitrary and false. The bravado is undercut by a doomsday chorus: everyone is condemned and misunderstood anyway, so the void hardly matters against the end of the world.","sonnet":"A declaration that the divisions between people — the ideological lines we draw — are illusory and meaningless, set against the backdrop of impending universal doom. The singer finds peace in the void between self and other because all distinctions collapse at the end.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane's Alien is a metalcore concept record about vocalist Marcus Bridge's childhood with drug-addicted parents, and Rift turns that private wound into a cosmology: a barrier between two people, reframed as something the speaker refuses to fear even as it swallows him. The verse dismantles division as a fiction — borders drawn on shifting ground, dishonesty passed off as truth — but the chorus can't sustain that defiance and collapses into a flattening equivalence: everyone identical, everyone doomed, everyone misread. The song's real argument is in that gap between its parts. The stoic opening claim keeps returning, but each pass earns less relief, until the closing incantation of apocalypse repeats until it stops meaning and becomes pure white noise — the sound of a comfort worn down to a tic, a fearlessness the speaker recites precisely because he doesn't believe it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","politics & society"],"means":"unity purchased only by the apocalypse large enough to erase every line","built":"the chant lets the ending swallow the reassurance it built"},
"northlane~rot":{"haiku":"Environmental and societal decay, calling for collective action and resistance.","opus":"A rallying call against decay, refusing to watch the world spoil, pointing at the danger bred from within and urging linked minds to act rather than cower as a reckoning looms. Northlane weld metalcore weight to an almost utopian faith that networked consciousness is a force power can't crush, the opening numbness giving way to collective defiance.","sonnet":"A double-temperature song — the soul goes numb in solstice cold, the sun burns the soul warm — but both readings land on the same political urgency: power must be taken from the hands of death before the world putrefies. The web-of-minds image turns solidarity into a force they cannot suppress. Standing by is not an option.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~rot-exe":{"haiku":"Collective awakening and resistance against systemic decay; consciousness and interconnection become weapons against dehumanization and passive complicity.","opus":"A rallying cry against decay and apathy, urging collective action before the world putrefies. Northlane leans into an almost mystical vision of interconnected minds, insisting that awareness and solidarity form a power the powerful cannot suppress, and that refusing to hide in fear is itself a reckoning.","sonnet":"An ecological and political rally cry — the world is being hollowed out by those in power (\"black blood bleeding heart filled stone\") and the narrator calls for networked collective resistance: a web of minds entangled can repress nothing. The line \"a mind at large is a power they can't repress\" is the thesis; the imperative throughout is simply don't let the world rot.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~savage": {"haiku":"Examines humanity's descent from cosmic curiosity to mutual destruction, pursuing knowledge that leads only to death. Evolution sealed by our own choice.","opus":"Northlane deliver a cosmic plea for humanity to unite and view itself from the vantage of the stars, lamenting how the same curiosity that could have led to discovery was weaponized against our own kind. It's an evolutionary sermon pushing togetherness over division while reaching for transcendence, spiritual uplift over djent weight.","sonnet":"Humankind turned its gaze from the cosmos inward onto each other, and the knowledge that could have lifted us became ammunition. The song mourns this squandered birthright — we were meant to discover, to look through the sun's eyes at an undivided earth — but instead descended into violence and tribalism, primates drowning utopia in blood.","src":"haiku","fable":"A species-scale address that indicts humankind's savagery as a misuse of its own gift: the same curiosity that turned our eyes to the cosmos got dragged back down and aimed at each other, knowledge cascading into ammunition against our own kind. Northlane frame this as tragic squandering rather than original sin — we were made to discover, and turned discovery into fratricide, blood-daubed apes who mistook the drive to learn for a license to kill. The recurring plea to borrow the sun's overhead gaze is the corrective vision the song reaches for: a single vantage high enough that human borders stop registering as real. Against Marcus Bridge's soaring lines, the descent imagery and the wisdom said to lie dormant until the mold breaks reframe evolution as unfinished — the savage stage is one we're meant to outgrow, not our nature. The song's argument is that unity isn't utopian softness but the next thing we're built to discover.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"curiosity that built civilization dragged back down and aimed at our own kind","built":"the borrowed-sun overhead gaze frames unity as discovery, not softness"},
"northlane~scarab":{"haiku":"A being recognizes they are a god trapped in physical form, suffering endless pain that they finally accept as the price of existence itself.","opus":"Egyptian mythology frames a spiritual awakening: a man adrift in time, hands guided by ancestral knowledge, deities who lost themselves inside mortal shells. The Australian metalcore band pivots to the stark truth that every living thing endures pain, converting arcane myth into a determined vow to press through the dark rather than dodge the suffering.","sonnet":"A man wandering through scorched terrain arrives at a brutal universal truth — all living things suffer, repeatedly and without mercy. Drawing on an Egyptian mythology of divine beings lost in mortal form, the song frames suffering as the shared condition from which one must carve a path toward light.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~sleepless":{"haiku":"Someone can't reach or save a person they love who is drowning in self-harm, so they lie awake tormented by the curse of helplessness and invisible death.","opus":"A wrenching account of watching a loved one destroy themselves, unable to reach their hand or liberate them though they're still alive; the deeper read is the survivor's guilt and helpless grief of having emotionally said goodbye while the person lives on, the fear of becoming like them leaving the narrator sleepless and numb.","sonnet":"A song about loving someone who is destroying themselves and being powerless to intervene — the narrator can't reach them, can't save them, has emotionally said goodbye even while knowing they're still alive, and lies awake sleepless with the weight of having tried everything and failed.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor's insomnia over someone still living but lost to addiction, where grief precedes death: the speaker keeps vigil for a person he can no longer pull back from the edge, and the wakefulness is both a symptom of that helplessness and its penance. Northlane's electronic-inflected Alien turns intimate here — the verse admits the real terror is inherited, a dread of carrying the same affliction the loved one is drowning in, so the mourning doubles as self-recognition. The bridge is the crux: the speaker tries to file the pain away where it can't be seen, then twice recants, confessing the burial never took — a deliberate reversal of the tidy consolation that distance heals, which is exactly why the nights won't close. That single word left chanting at the end is less a title than a verdict, the mind refusing the mercy of sleep because putting it to rest would mean giving the person up entirely.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"grieving a living person, goodbye spoken before there is a body","built":"the final word repeated alone enacts the exhaustion the song describes","fnote":"Per the album's documented backstory the vigil is family-specific — Bridge's mother fighting to keep his sister from turning to drugs of her own."},
"northlane~solar":{"haiku":"Humanity ignores environmental collapse and overexploits Earth (personified as feminine), pursuing distant ambitions while destroying their home.","opus":"An ecological indictment of humanity treating Earth as a disposable host, bleeding the planet dry and multiplying until it is time to abandon it and scorch what remains; the mother imagery and the command to look hard at the horizon warn that we are watching our own world die.","sonnet":"An ecological collapse song — humanity has exhausted Earth, treated her as an unlimited resource, and will eventually declare it time to move on and leave a scorched wasteland behind, with glass towers casting shadows over a dead landscape.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane frame the Earth as a mother we no longer look at, our attention captured instead by the promise of the stars and the fantasy of leaving. The verses map an ecological ruin the band ties directly to that gaze: green forests reduced to desert, cities of glass towering over a wasteland, a single receding line of the horizon offered as the only view left. The chorus voices the extractive logic in a chillingly plural we — drain the planet, breed past its limits, then abandon the exhausted body once it can no longer sustain us. The tripled bridge line hammers the point until the closing turn strips away every alibi, watching the death it has just narrated with clinical calm.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nature & the elements","politics & society"],"means":"the dare to face the far edge landing as witness to self-inflicted ruin","built":"sci-fi departure imagery reread at close as a neglected mother"},
"northlane~soma":{"haiku":"Poverty of mind and inability to escape a broken system or limited perspective.","opus":"Northlane, the Australian prog-metalcore band, borrow Huxley's numbing drug for a title and build a portrait of urban alienation. A restless dreamer moves through a bleak, exhausting city that feels like a prison of people scrabbling over scraps and sickened by artificial modern life, yet clings to one act of resistance: a flat refusal to let this place be where he gives up.","sonnet":"Urban alienation seen through the eyes of a daydreamer who keeps expecting the world to be better than it is — a concrete city of lead and synthetic disease, worker bees fighting over scraps, a mirror returning only shards. The refusal to die here is quiet but absolute.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~talking-heads":{"haiku":"Internal voices and self-doubt silence the mind while the person resists pharmaceutical escape, refusing to numb the internal chaos.","opus":"Metalcore rendering of intrusive thought and dissociation, where the mind is drowned out by a chorus of internal voices louder than reason. The refusal of medication frames it as a struggle to stay oneself rather than be quieted, a raw account of mental unwellness.","sonnet":"The narrator feels locked out of his own mind, unable to fit comfortably in his own skin, and overwhelmed by internal voices that drown out rational thought — resisting medication as a way out while searching for a sense of self.","src":"haiku","fable":"The name carries the argument: intrusive inner speakers so loud they mute the person hosting them, a paradox where nonstop chatter yields not clamor but a smothered hush. Everything reads dissociative — a self shut away from its own thinking behind corroded gates, no longer at home in its body. The turn arrives with the rejected pills, refusal that scans less as healing than as stubborn residence in the din, choosing the wrecked self over dosing it away. When composure finally splinters into one blurted curse, that break becomes the point: after all the measured naming, the unguarded sound tells more truth than any tidy line about it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","madness & the mind"],"means":"nonstop inner chatter producing a smothered hush rather than noise","built":"after all the measured naming of the condition, one unguarded blurted curse tells more truth than every tidy line before it"},
"northlane~transcending-dimensions":{"haiku":"Unconditional love as the only constant across life and potential afterlife.","opus":"A vow of love meant to outlast this life and any that follows, made by someone who spent a frigid, closed-off existence doubting he was capable of love at all, now offering to carry another toward something warm and shared. Northlane trade metalcore aggression for searching devotion, relocating faith from religion into a promise of tenderness held against a crushing weight of duty.","sonnet":"A love song addressed to someone who might not exist, or might not stay — a lifetime of searching for faith, for warmth, ending in the admission that the cold has made the speaker doubt whether love was ever possible for them. The offer at the end — run away with me, somewhere beautiful, our own world — is desperate and generous in equal measure.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~veridian": {"haiku":"A dying person asks their loved one to turn off life support so they can die with dignity rather than suffer on machines. The song portrays a mercy-killing request grounded in the desire to escape unbearable physical agony.","opus":"A harrowing deathbed scene, a dying woman pleading to be allowed to go with dignity and begging that her life support be shut off. The grieving young man beside her is told to dry his tears as she's borne away. Northlane treat mercy killing with unflinching gentleness, their metalcore fury softening into a compassionate case for release from suffering.","sonnet":"A dying woman asks the man beside her to let her go with dignity, to turn off the machine and stop prolonging suffering. His grief is palpable but her voice is clear and certain — she is ready, she has said what she needed to say, and all she wants now is a peaceful release.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dramatic monologue spoken from a deathbed: a woman, her life draining away, begs a weeping mourner at her side to let the machine be switched off so she can go with her dignity intact. The song's power is in how its image-system collapses two families onto one another — instruments of execution and instruments of mercy become the same thing, so that the guillotine and the spiked plank read not as torture but as the release she is pleading for, and the medical apparatus keeping her breathing is recast as the real cruelty. Against that hardware, the only tenderness she asks for is deliverance imagined as angels lifting her clear of the metal frame. Northlane hand this to Marcus Bridge's clean voice rather than a scream, which turns the plea into something intimate and steady — a request made in full possession of herself, not a howl of pain.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","suicide"],"means":"a deathbed plea where medical apparatus is recast as the real cruelty","built":"clean voice rather than a scream turns the request intimate and self-possessed"},
"northlane~vultures":{"haiku":"A person feels consumed by predatory forces circling them, burning away false icons and ideals as they struggle against internal and external enemies. They stand alone against constant attack, buried alive by those waiting to pick them apart.","opus":"A fight for self-ownership as the speaker torches falsehoods and false idols and pledges to sacrifice himself for himself, sensing scavengers circling to strip him bare, caught between forsaking the person he's become and defending his core, cast as a solitary stand against everyone.","sonnet":"Someone feels surrounded by people waiting to tear them apart, and weighs whether to abandon who they have become or fight to preserve what is inside them, framing the struggle as themselves against the world.","src":"haiku","fable":"Northlane build the whole song around a self-immolation the singer chooses over letting circling scavengers do the killing for him, and the read hangs on which agent gets to end him. Torching an idol and a falsehood in the same breath makes destruction the only available form of honesty, and the martyr-to-oneself line collapses executioner and victim into one body long before the predators arrive. The verse states the real fork plainly: dissolve the self he has hardened into, or defend the last thing still breathing under it. That the post-chorus and outro shrink to a single flat refrain of solitary defiance is the tell — the grandeur burns off, leaving only a cornered chant, and the closing command to torch the very ground where he cut his name means erasing his own monument so the vultures inherit nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"noble self-destruction that barely hides the question of what remains inside","built":"the defiant stance undercut as a lonely person talking himself into it"},
"northlane~welcome-to-the-industry-from-fretless-the-wrath-of-riffson":{"haiku":"Industry exploitation commodifies artists; contracts strip ownership while executives profit from labor.","opus":"A savage satire of music-industry exploitation, voiced by a parasitic executive who owns the artist and skims from every success. The fine print becomes a trap, the artist warned they're disposable the moment they stop being profitable.","sonnet":"A music industry executive gleefully explains the predatory contract system — taking cuts off the top, owning the artist entirely, setting fees, controlling output, and discarding acts when they're no longer profitable, framed as 'just business' while the artist naively signed away everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is sung entirely in the voice of a music-industry predator, the label boss who greets a hungry newcomer as a mark rather than an artist; the mask is a warm welcome, and beneath it every line converts the dreamer's talent into someone else's revenue. Its cruelty tightens through the structure: an opening bargain (sign yourself over for a shot at your dream) hardens into flat ownership, then into a sneering blame-shift where exploitation is recast as the victim's own carelessness for not scrutinizing the paperwork. The chilling turn is the litany of disposability at the center, where he counts the interchangeable dreamers he has watched arrive and rot on the roadside, warning that yesterday's sensation is already replaceable. Northlane, veterans who cut ties with labels and management to go fully independent, write the character with insider precision, so the tie-in soundtrack framing lands as real venom rather than cartoon villainy. The parting line, an order to empty your pockets, drops the negotiation entirely: the deal was always a robbery wearing a handshake.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","politics & society","artifice & performance"],"means":"the welcome speech that strips ownership line by line until only empty pockets remain","built":"the opening parenthetical purr is retroactively revealed as a literal threat"},
"northlane~windbreaker":{"haiku":"Breaking free from imposed expectations and societal demands; choosing autonomy even if direction is uncertain.","opus":"The narrator abandons the identity others prescribed for him, hearing the storm call him onward and setting out for a distant road, finally breathing free after years of sacrifice and patience. Northlane cast the departure as rebirth within a dream, the same gale he must overcome becoming the air of his liberation.","sonnet":"A figure breaks free from inherited expectations and collective instructions about how life should be lived — heading down an open road with no fixed destination. Rather than fear the uncertainty, the physical sensation of moving air becomes a sustaining force, and leaving is reframed as the first genuine act of selfhood.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~worldeater":{"haiku":"Spiritual awakening to hidden truths and transcendence of illusion.","opus":"An entheogenic vision of waking up to reality: ordinary perception is a lie, and a psychedelic experience tears the veil to reveal a fearful world watched over by ancient beings. Australian progressive-metalcore band Northlane frame the ego's death and cosmic rebirth as a path to genuine, awakened sight.","sonnet":"A spiritual awakening framed as a veil being lifted — the speaker moves from seeing a world of noise and fear to recognizing themselves as part of something older and larger, love and bliss rather than distortion. What looked like chaos was always also the beginning of clarity.","src":"haiku"},
"northlane~xen":{"haiku":"Personal meaning in art transcends universal truth; sincerity connects regardless of whether others feel the same, dissolving shame.","opus":"A meditation from Northlane's Obsidian on art as private connection rather than shared consensus. Nothing outside your own ideals counts, so the plea is to shed the reflex of shame, take the meaning that lands for you and let it heal without needing anyone else to agree.","sonnet":"The song speaks to the subjective, private nature of music and meaning: the speaker urges someone to stop suppressing their inner response, insisting there's no correct interpretation, only the individual's genuine connection to sound. It moves from a directive to be still and feel, through acknowledgment that people won't experience things the same way, toward a liberating call to let go and simply receive the music without shame or self-censorship.","src":"web","web":"A call to break free from suppression and the prison of endless self-questioning — the song urges you to breathe, embrace, and let personal meaning stand on its own: 'tell me what it means to you, keep it that way.'","fable":"A manifesto for reception on your own terms: the song insists a message forged in honesty outranks anything polished into poetry, and it hands interpretation to the listener rather than legislating one correct meaning. The structure enacts that release across three movements: an opening imperative to shut out borrowed ideals and molt into a new skin, a hinge that dissolves the moral scorecard of art into pure contact with sound, and a closing exhale where inhaling becomes a healing gate thrown open without restraint. On Obsidian-era Northlane (2022), where Marcus Bridge sings as often as he screams over programmed electronics, that arc of suppression giving way to embrace reads as the record's own thesis about dropping self-judgment and simply letting the thing land.","fv":2,"themes":["art & interpretation","identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"suppression gives way to bare honest contact with sound","built":"three-movement structure mirrors the arc: imperative, dissolution, exhale of release","fvr":"2.4"},
"northlane~zero-one": {"haiku":"Breaking free from cultural programming and societal control to access inner vision.","opus":"A summons to snap out of cultural sleepwalking, the narrator adrift in dreamless comfort before realizing he surrendered his capacity to imagine, swallowed by noise and manufactured expectation, and demanding he wrest his existence back from that stranglehold. Northlane wed this transhumanist theme of self-recovery to electronics-laced metalcore, the drifting-then-waking structure mirroring the move from stupor to clarity.","sonnet":"A waking-up story about reclaiming inner life from the noise of social conditioning — the speaker spent years sleepwalking inside a swarm of external signals that drowned out his own voice, only to rediscover the power to imagine what has never existed before. The call is to break free from the iron grip of a system designed to keep vision sedated.","src":"haiku","fable":"A polemic against manufactured desire, staged inside a metaphor of induced sleep — the dreamer isn't resting but sedated, cradled by the god of dreams while a curated stream of idols and advertised light does the actual dreaming for him. Northlane build the argument in two registers that map onto each other: the visionary blindfolded by his own screens, and the gifted mind talked over by noise before its deepest thought can surface and speak. The chorus breaks the spell as direct address, ordering a life reclaimed from the iron grip of a manufacturing machine and reframing even the year's orbit as an eclipse — something bright being blotted out. The turn that earns the length is the final verse's shift to first person: the speaker admits he wasn't merely a victim but handed his own visionary power over to be cloaked and blinded, which folds the accusation back onto himself and refuses easy righteousness. Ending on a single word for the trance closes the loop, leaving unresolved whether the waking it demanded ever happens.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"the visionary confessing he handed his own sight over","built":"first-person shift in the final verse folds the accusation inward"},
"nostalghia~who-you-talkin-to-man":{"haiku":"A confrontation about identity and dominance, with the speaker demanding knowledge of the other's true nature and threatening to reclaim control.","opus":"Ciscandra Nostalghia stages a confrontation between a beggar and a would-be superstar, a reckoning over worth and stolen thrones. It reads as the self challenging its own pretensions, demanding to know who you think you're addressing before it takes back what's owed.","sonnet":"A confrontational power struggle where the narrator challenges someone's inflated self-image — threatening to take their worth, reclaim a throne, and return their body while making clear who really knows them.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-but-thieves~can-you-afford-to-be-an-individual":{"haiku":"The song critiques herd mentality, social pressure to conform, and the cost of individuality in an age where tribal thinking and internet shaming destroy nuance.","opus":"A scorching survey of online tribalism and rigid identity, taunting a masculinity threatened by nonconformity, the internet with teeth eating and eating, echo chambers on every side. It pointedly refuses to spare either the MAGA-hatted contradiction or the newly venomous liberals, ending by asking whether individuality is even affordable anymore. Nothing But Thieves, the English alt-rock band, let their falsetto-heavy intensity carry a rare both-sides indictment of a culture that brands anyone who steps out of line.","sonnet":"A reckoning with the cost of refusing group identity, from every direction — the internet's pack mentality, the MAGA hat, the liberals who are just as vicious. The speaker won't mirror anyone's expectations of who they should be, watches both sides eat their own, and asks whether genuine individuality is even financially or socially survivable anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-but-thieves~ce-n-est-rien":{"haiku":"The speaker mocks empty platitudes and indifference in a meaningless world where nothing matters and sound becomes noise.","opus":"A scorching indictment of performative concern and nihilist noise, mocking hollow thoughts-and-prayers while the sky literally cracks open. The French refrain shrugs it all off as nothing, sedation standing in for a culture that refuses to care.","sonnet":"A nihilistic leveling of everything — thoughts, prayers, outrage, beauty, sound — none of it matters, it's all nothing (\"ce n'est rien\"), and a godless world is heading for the ground.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-but-thieves~futureproof":{"opus":"A biting critique of performative activism and self-preservation in a collapsing world, skewering those who chase good feelings over doing good, turning protest into content. The narrator, desperate to be futureproof, watches fire spread everywhere while vultures steal youth and truth. Dread of a monstrous future fuels its anxious plea.","sonnet":"A sharp critique of performative activism and viral outrage culture: attention-stealing is mistaken for doing good, the internet is poisoned, and the narrator wants to be protected against the monster the future is becoming — future-proofed against the liars and vultures.","src":"sonnet"},
"nothing-but-thieves~unperson":{"haiku":"Technological surveillance erases individual consciousness into compliant uniformity; systems of control strip humanity until resistance becomes impossible.","opus":"A dystopian protest against dehumanizing systems that reduce people to interchangeable clones and erase their inner lives, borrowing Orwell's unperson and doublethink. It insists, against the grand designer of the mess, that the individual's thoughts and existence are still his own.","sonnet":"A protest against digital and ideological erasure: algorithms flatten belief, doublethink divides people into clones of clones, and the speaker insists on existing — \"I'm flawed, but I do exist, my thoughts are mine\" — directing anger at whoever designed a system that turns people into unpersons.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~d-j-vu":{"haiku":"Toxic addiction repeats endlessly; monsters recognize monsters and reinforce the cycle, pulled backward by familiar wounds.","opus":"A portrait of a toxic bond that keeps pulling two damaged people back together against their better judgment. Nothing More stages love and addiction as indistinguishable forces, the narrator confessing his own monstrousness while acknowledging the wound the other carries, each returning compulsively as if reliving the same fated collision.","sonnet":"Two damaged people keep running back to each other despite knowing it's destructive — monster and man, wicked and twisted — the déjà vu of the cycle capturing both the addiction to the bond and the impossibility of breaking it: 'it feels like it never dies.'","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~do-you-really-want-it":{"haiku":"Everyone desires change but refuses personal transformation, remaining jaded and stuck. Only through genuine suffering and self-examination can real change occur.","opus":"A confrontational anthem about the hypocrisy of wanting the world to change while refusing to change yourself, daring the listener to do the ugly inner work rather than chase easy highs and outrage.","sonnet":"A challenge to performative activism and spiritual consumerism — everyone wants transformation and change delivered to them on demand, but nobody wants to do the unglamorous internal work that actually produces it.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~don-t-stop":{"haiku":"A person resolves to break through their own delusions and confusion, refusing to be manipulated by manufactured lies and propaganda. They maintain their five senses and critical mind as resistance to control.","opus":"A defiant refusal to be sedated by consumer lies and cult thinking, insisting on independent perception and relentless self-work; the New Year framing turns resolve into a permanent stance rather than a passing vow.","sonnet":"A declaration of relentless forward momentum driven by the resolve to strip away self-deception and resist manipulation — the narrator commits to digging deeper into personal truth, rejecting media noise and groupthink, and keeping the fire of autonomy burning no matter how hard things get.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~fade-in-fade-out":{"haiku":"A father's mortality teaches his son that pain and change define existence, yet endurance and integrity matter more than comfort. Legacy transmits through example rather than words.","opus":"A dying father hands the reins to his child, telling him that just as the parent once witnessed the boy's arrival, the boy will one day witness the parent's departure, and that all the tools to keep going have already been left behind.","sonnet":"A father passes life's lessons to a son across a generational handoff: he watched the son fade in and knows the son will watch him fade out, and the image of a grip leaving a hand anchors a tender transfer of courage, direction, and hope.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~funny-little-creatures":{"haiku":"The song personifies inner vices—blame, hate, fear, ambition, and lust—as creatures that hide and drive destructive behavior, particularly how we tell ourselves stories that justify our actions while being blind to our own flaws.","opus":"An unsparing self-inventory that personifies blame, hate, fear, ambition and lust as the little monsters a hypocrite hides inside. The song's honesty is that it turns the accusing finger inward and expects things to worsen first.","sonnet":"About the internal cast of characters that drive self-sabotage — blame, hate, fear, lust, ambition — each one a creature feeding on the narrator. The core insight is that he can diagnose everyone else's dysfunction perfectly while being totally blind to his own.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~go-to-war":{"haiku":"Intimate partners weaponize words and old grievances against each other, screaming in the places meant for safety while forgetting why they fight.","opus":"A candid dissection of destructive love, partners loading up on old grievances and shouting in the very home where they ought to feel secure. The narrator grasps that quarreling with a person was supposed to mean quarreling on their behalf. Nothing More set this domestic standoff to explosive alt-metal, weighing separation against everything the two would still sacrifice for each other.","sonnet":"Two people who love each other turn the intimacy of a relationship into a battlefield, arming themselves with accumulated grievances and screaming in the supposed safety of shared ground. The narrator knows they are destroying the very thing they would die for, and asks whether any of this is making either of them feel safe.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~god-went-north":{"haiku":"Faith persists through terminal loss as presence and holding become more meaningful than rescue, acceptance transcends divine intervention.","opus":"A vigil at a dying mother's bedside, the son severing the tie to the woman who gave him all of himself and, even feeling forsaken by God, vowing to hold her until she's free, trusting they'll meet again.","sonnet":"A son sits beside his dying mother in a hospital bed, her cut-flower petals weeping like his uprooted grief, asking God to either save her or release her — and in her final embrace she promises the day of his wedding, the cord cut, the hallelujah reframing her death as her moment of glory.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~gone":{"haiku":"Distance breeds helplessness; the speaker grieves the inability to shelter loved ones from harm, mortality made visceral by separation.","opus":"The guilt of a touring musician absent while family faces crisis, sharpened by a mother's cancer he can't be present for. The distance is both literal miles and helpless emotional absence, gone when he's most needed.","sonnet":"A touring musician is thousands of miles away when his mother's cancer reaches its crisis point, and the distance between his obligations and his family becomes unbearable. The song is a raw expression of guilt, helplessness, and the impossibility of being in two places when it matters most.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~i-ll-be-ok":{"haiku":"Despite heartbreak and loss, healing comes through loving deeply, forgiving, and maintaining open vulnerability.","opus":"A grief-tempered affirmation that the sole real failure is never giving your whole heart, insisting an unguarded spirit can pardon, be pardoned, and mend. The chorus recasts questions about loving through pain as the road onward rather than the injury. Texas rockers Nothing More specialize in this therapeutic, arena-sized uplift, packaging open-hearted resilience into a chorus built to be roared back.","sonnet":"Loss accepted not through numbness but through the radical choice to keep loving in spite of it — everything dissolves, faces and lives drift away like dust, but the only real failure is to have withheld love along the way. The speaker works through a broken heart by holding it open rather than shutting it down, and lands on a fragile, hard-won okay.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~just-say-when":{"haiku":"Fading love exhausts itself in slow deterioration; the speaker questions whether hope remains or if emptiness makes continuation futile.","opus":"A couple facing the death of a love that once reached for the stars, unsure whether they were fools over one another or just fools to fall at all. Kept barely alive for years past saving, they keep orbiting without a clean break, waiting for one of them to finally call it.","sonnet":"A couple at the edge of ending revisit what they once had — naming stars, tracing futures in the dark — against what remains, which is not enough; the glass was half full when they stopped pouring in, and the whole song waits for someone to say when.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~let-em-burn":{"haiku":"Society is rigged by lies, propaganda, and inequality. The song calls for burning it all down so it can be rebuilt from the ashes.","opus":"A seething panorama of division and dishonesty, where wealth equals control, everyone deceives and swallows the deceit, and a falsehood repeated often enough calcifies into accepted truth, until razing the whole thing and starting over feels like the only option. Nothing More, the San Antonio rock outfit, pour their dramatic force into a chant against manipulation, its funhouse of illusions and fixed contest crying out to be torn down.","sonnet":"Political systems rigged by money and spectacle, where lies calcify into facts and division is manufactured for profit, are met with a demand to burn the whole structure down. Not nihilism but a frustrated prerequisite: the corrupt architecture must be cleared before anything true can be rebuilt in its place.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~react-respond":{"haiku":"A speaker argues against resisting fear itself, cautioning that fighting fear paradoxically strengthens it; the path to mastery is surrender and acceptance of dissolution.","opus":"A spoken meditation, drawn from Alan Watts, arguing that fear is mastered not by attacking it but by allowing it, since to resist fear only strengthens it and to play God over it costs you the divine. It lands on constant dissolution: everything is always dying, so there is nothing to hold and nothing to release. Nothing More often stitch such philosophical interludes into their albums, letting the Watts lecture set up their rock catharsis.","sonnet":"A spoken-word reflection on the paradox of fear: fighting it strengthens it, refusing it just creates a second layer of dread, and the only way through is to let yourself be fully afraid without resistance. The deeper point is that everything is already dissolving — we are in constant death — so there is nothing to grip, and releasing that grip is the beginning of mastery.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~ripping-me-apart":{"haiku":"A passionate and destructive relationship tears the narrator apart, with the partner simultaneously elevating and devastating him.","opus":"A torturous, addictive relationship that both pulls the speaker together and tears him apart, described through violent imagery of a gun, a throat-grip and a dagger; the other person is his lie, his dream and everything to him even as they destroy him.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship is described as something that simultaneously destroys and sustains — a force that elevates the narrator to godlike feeling but never lets him heal, tearing him apart even as it pulls him back.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~still-in-love":{"haiku":"A couple exhausts themselves trying to salvage a dying love; despite effort and pain, they burned what they had to ash while one remains emotionally trapped in devotion and hurt.","opus":"A raw account of a relationship in its death throes, the peak long behind and a couple who meant to age together instead simply aging apart. The gutting thread is that love outlasts its purpose, the narrator still smitten and still aching even after everything they built has been reduced to cinders, with no way to reverse it.","sonnet":"Two people who have burned their relationship to ash — still in love, still hurting, but unable to go back to what they had, exhausted by trying.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~stuck-feat-sinizter":{"haiku":"The speaker claws free from systemic traps through accumulated suffering, claiming time and voice as retaliation for years locked underground.","opus":"A defiant anthem for those who claw their way up from nothing, insisting the attitude was earned through dirty work, not luck, and refusing to be counted out. It's addressed to everyone who feels trapped, declaring this their time to break through.","sonnet":"A speaker who clawed their way out of the dirt — 'I pulled this out the dirt, I did the dirty work' — addresses anyone stuck at a breaking point, insisting this song is closure and that the experience of feeling trapped is shared and survivable.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~the-great-divorce":{"haiku":"Born into cosmic spiritual conflict between heaven and hell, the speaker feels buried pressure and guilt—a bomb in skin that cannot escape or absolve itself.","opus":"Being born into an inner split between heaven and hell, carrying guilt and withheld love as a physical weight that pins him to the ground; the deeper read is inescapable original-sin-like burden and a world that demands he keep paying a debt he can't discharge.","sonnet":"A song about inherited guilt and the crushing weight of original sin or generational trauma — the narrator was born into an impossible split between heaven and hell and feels the debt of that condition pressing down on his body.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~the-matthew-effect":{"haiku":"The privileged, ungrateful, and entitled feed parasitically while the speaker carries them, promising their comeuppance through humiliation.","opus":"A scathing address to an entitled, coddled figure who was handed everything and still whines and blames others, the narrator positioning himself as the exploited provider promising a reckoning. The title nods to the principle that the favored keep taking from those who feed them.","sonnet":"Directed at someone the narrator sees as a self-entitled parasite who takes everything given to them while contributing nothing — a furious dressing-down of someone who mistakes being supported for being special, invoking the biblical principle that those who already have will receive more.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~tired-of-winning":{"haiku":"Cosmic futility and human mediocrity reveal that winning itself has become impossible; the speaker rejects salvation because none exists.","opus":"Apocalyptic dread dressed in political sarcasm, the exhausted sense that the game is already lost and no rescue is coming. It turns the boast of endless winning into a question about facing the end alone.","sonnet":"A cosmic provocation: what if God engineered humanity's suffering as entertainment, deliberately making us flawed and afraid so our story would have a surprising, tragic ending? The song sits with the bleak premise that we were never sufficient, no savior is coming, and the real question is whether we turn on each other or steer through the void together.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~tunnels":{"haiku":"A person pushes through suffocation and constraint, determined to transform and survive rather than surrender to hopelessness or despair.","opus":"A survival anthem cast as pushing through life's dark passages, laboring hard and pleading for light while refusing to be beaten. Renewal arrives as the narrator sheds his old self and feels himself buoyed upward. Nothing More fuse percussive alt-metal with communal encouragement, insisting there's a right moment to fall and a way to keep going, forging strength from within.","sonnet":"Collective survival is the subject — people holding their breath through dark passages, telling themselves to keep moving when hopelessness becomes comfortable. The narrator insists on pushing through rather than surrendering, finding in the final stretch a physical transformation: breath returning, light lifting, a kind of becoming that was only possible by not going out quietly.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~turn-it-up-like-stand-in-the-fire":{"haiku":"Sustained heat transforms the soul; standing in destructive fire becomes the only path to authentic becoming while digital machinery consumes the living.","opus":"A rage against a generation hollowed out by the internet, media and government, demanding to be pushed to a breaking point and reborn in fire. Fear and hate get crowned as the new gods of a wired, spiritually dead world.","sonnet":"About the deadening effect of media and internet saturation on an entire generation — friends \"dead\" to algorithmic manipulation and governmental propaganda, the brain bugged by feeds and talking points until rage becomes the only authentic response. The command to \"stand in the fire and become what you are\" is a call to burn away the conditioning and reclaim authentic identity through confrontation with what is being done to everyone.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~valhalla-too-young-to-see":{"haiku":"Planted seeds grow into unexpected structures; some minds resist change and some games aren't worth playing when peace remains elusive.","opus":"A reflection on growth, disillusionment, and hard-won wisdom, borrowing Ecclesiastes to frame life as seasons of planting and uprooting. The narrator learns which battles and minds are worth engaging, accepting that youth blinded him to truths only time and consequence could reveal, and that self-liberation means letting go.","sonnet":"A speaker who planted a seed impatiently as a youth returns twenty years later to find it has become a tree — the growth was real but invisible — and the lesson is that some people's games aren't worth playing and some peace of mind isn't worth saving, riffing on Ecclesiastes' seasons to argue that maturity requires accepting what was too opaque to see when young.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-more~who-we-are":{"haiku":"Humanity persists through suffering and decay, connected by shared identity that transcends individual ego and death—collective belief sustains them.","opus":"An affirmation that despite blood, sweat, fear and decay, identity and human connection are what sustain belief in something bigger than the self; the recurring 'the you that's in me is in everything' proposes a shared essence across people, so standing together and remembering who we are is what lets us make it out alive.","sonnet":"A humanist affirmation of collective identity as a source of meaning — the song holds that something larger than individual selfhood connects people, and that shared identity and mutual witness is what makes life feel worth living even as bodies and faces fade.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-noble~jaws-of-a-wolf":{"haiku":"Embodied curse and bitterness consume the subject; existence becomes rehearsed suffering in a hostile universe.","opus":"A raw scream of self-hatred and suicidal anguish, treating the flesh itself as an affliction and a life squandered away, while wondering aloud whether every person is quietly this shattered. Torturing oneself is offered, in desperation, as a jolt that might finally provoke some change.","sonnet":"Existence is experienced as a body-curse in a bitter universe — spitting blood on waking, hiding scars, brain turned to mush — the speaker asking whether everyone feels this way and finding no answer, while \"the jaws of a wolf, the eyes of a snake\" mark the days passing without trace.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-nowhere~blood-feat-kennyhoopla-judge":{"opus":"A visceral genre-blurring account of destructive addiction reframed as toxic romance, blood on the hands standing in for a love that functions like a drug. The narrator knows he needs to stop but cannot, careening downtown and craving exposure. Compulsion, self-harm, and doomed desire bleed together in adrenaline-soaked panic.","sonnet":"About a toxic romantic obsession framed as a drug addiction — the narrator cannot stop pursuing someone despite the carnage it causes, describing reckless, self-destructive behavior driven by compulsion, and finding themselves implicated in the damage they cause.","src":"sonnet"},
"nothing-nowhere~thirst4violence-feat-freddie-dredd-silverstein":{"haiku":"Ascension from depression requires violent clarity; the speaker celebrates emergence from psychiatric medication and institutional constraint.","opus":"A country-boy-turned-artist answers sellout accusations with a defiant refusal to go back, mixing hard-won success with anxiety, depression and demons that haunt his sleep. The guest verses pile on menacing bravado, but the core is a wounded outsider's thirst to fight for the life he clawed back.","sonnet":"Three voices braid together: a reformed country-boy rapper celebrating the come-up from hatchback recordings to gold plaques; a stay-at-home emo with anxiety, depression, and demons in dreams who won't go back; and Freddie Dredd's menacing persona running murder and business side by side under a thirst for violence as the sun sets.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~a-pdh9g8":{"haiku":"Rejecting prescribed rules and seeking freedom in uncertain futures; building confidence from within despite external collapse.","opus":"Formed from members of Ellegarden and Straightener, Nothing's Carved In Stone trade in muscular, uplift-minded alt-rock, and this track leans into that arena-sized defiance. It refuses to balance its disorder and unsatisfied days, calling toward a horizon of unseen suffering and urging selfhood to shine through raw force rather than easy theater, while rain washes resignation away.","sonnet":"A song about enduring disorder and unsatisfied days without trying to balance them, calling out toward a horizon of unseen suffering and urging — yourself or someone else — to stand up and shine not through easy theater but through the raw force of selfhood, while rain washes away resignation.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~all-we-have-feat-masato-coldrain":{"src":"web","web":"A survival anthem for an overheating world: two voices assert that ordinary people — 'nothing but a man' — can still stand on their own ground and drive change, with temperatures rising and the sun setting but the only thing left worth holding onto being the future."},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~around-the-clock":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator feels drained by giving constantly without receiving in return, building speed on a winding road until they can no longer stay on course. The song urges both the narrator and the listener to stop, change the shading of their outlook, and recognize they are not facing the imbalance alone."},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~blow-it-up":{"haiku":"Transformation requires obliteration of false self; the speaker sacrifices constructed identity seeking authentic voice.","opus":"A resolve to say goodbye to a hollow, nobody self and burn away the loneliness in a revolutionary surge of feeling, refusing to be a stand-in for anyone else. The vow to reclaim a lost voice drives the decision to blow the empty old self up.","sonnet":"The speaker says goodbye to a self that was nobody in particular, resolves to stop performing loneliness and empty their hollow heart, and commits to a revolutionary emotional reset — \"I blow it up\" — trusting that the lost voice can be recovered because they are no one's substitute.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~deeper-deeper":{"haiku":"Collective unity persists beneath isolation; descent into heart-depths reveals shared origin transcending apparent separation.","opus":"A plunge to the depths of the heart hunting for truth and resolve, insisting that despite our friction and isolation we all sprang from a single origin, offspring of one solitary world. Even confronting the hollowness of living and dying, the song urges us to reconnect and keep going.","sonnet":"Descending to the bottom of the heart in search of truth, the speaker argues that all humans share a common origin — every river returns to the ocean — and that even in desolation the right response is to carry on, because primitive connection outlasts the vanity of individual life.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~directions-we-know":{"src":"web","web":"Youthful desires fade slowly away while the narrator and someone else move apart despite shared promises, with cyclical rebirth imagery suggesting nothing is truly permanent. Ephemeral details — bubbles, fleeting light, an inverted sky — frame a meditation on transience and the beauty of moments that cannot be held."},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~everything":{"src":"web","web":"A declaration of stubborn self-possession in the middle of emotional chaos: the narrator casts off ingrained deception and desire, swims alone through a sea of solitude, and insists he is fine — noise born of pride and memory driving him forward."},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~freedom":{"haiku":"The speaker rises through suffering and solidarity; freedom emerges not as escape but as collective defiance against systems of constraint.","opus":"A summons to release the grip on the past and a bygone perfect night, to shout in order to affirm one's own existence and color a monochrome world. Standing up now, whatever the lingering sorrow, is what freedom actually means.","sonnet":"Before a perfect night ends, the past is shaken off and the self affirmed through a cry of existence — 'you are free, you are free, you are free' — with the shadow of history still wavering but no longer a reason to stop, as monochrome reality becomes a road coloured by the act of standing up.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~go-out":{"haiku":"Battle and solidarity liberate from confinement; the speaker sets sail toward freedom through shared struggle and determination.","opus":"A driving summons to quit hesitating, break free of confinement, and set off toward brighter times and love, with the singer swearing to rescue the other and recover what's been lost. A stirring appeal to move and struggle instead of staying trapped.","sonnet":"An urgent rallying call to someone caged by doubt: cast off, set sail, the dice can be rerolled — the speaker will save them if they just go out and stop asking why they stay somewhere that no longer holds them.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~isolation":{"haiku":"Live by instinct and heart, ignore judgment, and stop running yourself ragged. The only measure that matters is inner truth and personal choice.","opus":"A rallying cry for self-belief from the Japanese rock band, urging the listener to trust their instincts, tune out others' judgment, and live on their own terms without apology. When exhaustion hits, it grants permission to rest, then to pour everything into the music so it connects with others. The core reassurance: you've done nothing wrong.","sonnet":"A direct, generous rallying call for self-determination — stop apologizing, stop listening to dismissive voices, act from instinct and heart. When exhaustion arrives, rest rather than push through; let music carry the message. The stance is neither rebellious nor angry, just quietly insistent that one's own life belongs to oneself.","src":"haiku","web":"The song declares that there is no need to change who you are — live on instinct, ignore the cold eyes of others, and channel your exhaustion into music so the feeling reaches people. Authenticity and self-determination are its core values, rejecting conformity in favor of living on one's own terms."},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~like-a-shooting-star":{"haiku":"Beauty born from accumulated wounds and scars, brightness emerging from survival and trauma.","opus":"A tribute to fleeting brilliance and the beauty forged from suffering. Against a backdrop of two people stuck in their own black holes and locked rooms, the narrator sees another whose accumulated scars make her burn brightly, her transient light and her drive to remake the world outshining the fear that paralyzes them both.","sonnet":"Two people locked in parallel prisons — one acting like he knows everything while wandering a black hole, one who never leaves the bedroom — are addressed with the paradox that their accumulated scars are the very thing that makes them shine. Pain as luminescence: the more you carry, the brighter your blink of life.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~milestone":{"src":"web","web":"A speaker pleads with someone not to cry and not to leave even as the memories of their shared days gradually blur and fade. Despite the helplessness reality brings, they choose to call what they have together freedom — a bittersweet vow to carve the passing days into memory before they are lost."},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~mirror-ocean":{"src":"web","web":"A mermaid dances in the ocean depths, her bubbles rising like sacred wishes; the song uses mirror-and-ocean imagery to explore loneliness, fleeting hope, and the moment two people's gazes meet across an emotional abyss. The ocean's mirrored surface becomes a symbol for introspection and the silent reflection of feelings between individuals."},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~november-15th":{"src":"web","web":"A specific date anchors an emotional low point: dried-up feelings, a withering heart, the sense of being too weak to stop oneself from going too far. The song finds fragile hope in the certainty that sunset is always followed by a new day, and keeps searching for a personal path forward."},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~out-of-control":{"haiku":"Inner conflict and self-doubt drive obsession, making communication impossible while the narrator struggles to show his true self.","opus":"A restless, self-doubting speaker who copied others to build himself feels tension and the urge to crash, yet longs to open up and show everything to someone; he senses hidden answers out there and that his feelings will recur no matter how he tries to start over.","sonnet":"Someone tries to process a pattern of self-imposed isolation and rigid thinking, wondering whether rewinding and starting over would change anything, and tentatively opening up to another person as the only thread connecting inner voices to the outside world.","src":"haiku","web":"The narrator wrestles with obsession and internal tension, longing to be genuinely understood by one person while feeling that words are just toys people play with. A wish to travel back in time and start over underscores a sense that the same emotional patterns keep returning.","fable":"The song builds a self out of imitation and then watches that self turn on itself, so the title's spiraling has less to do with rage than with the terror of a borrowed identity reaching for something real. One verse begs to be pried apart and inspected, a fantasy of total exposure; the next explains why exposure feels impossible, since staying safe means walling yourself in behind shut eyes, and language gets waved off as a hollow game that buries whatever truth is hidden. The bridge does the real work, dreaming of rewinding the clock to redo everything along some plotted route, only to admit the same ache will surface once more and repeat regardless. That closing shrug is the whole point: being seen is never arrived at, just cycled back to, the same craving looping around however carefully the map gets redrawn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the craving to be seen cycling back to the same point however carefully the route is redrawn","built":"the bridge admits rewinding the clock would only surface the identical ache again"},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~pride":{"haiku":"Resilience and strength persist through wounds; the speaker refuses to abandon hope, finding pride in survival and unbreakable connection.","opus":"A vow that a dream cannot be broken and an unbreakable pride hidden in a shared landscape can never be stolen, sustained by fortitude through fear and unease. It honors the strength of someone who hides countless wounds behind a smile.","sonnet":"Pride as an unbreakable heart running like a discontinuous line through uncertainty and scars — '誰にも奪えやしない / nobody can take it away' — the speaker acknowledges weakness not as something to discard but as part of an unbreakable scene, with hope still visible even as the night fades.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~red-light":{"haiku":"Cyclical suffering persists as seasons turn; genuine thought sustains the speaker through repeated emotional desiccation.","opus":"In a world gradually pulling apart, the singer decides to carry both his memories and his hardship onward, trusting that once the crying stops on some future night, honest and unmasked emotions will glow. Conquering a destiny already sketched out, a single move ahead can reshape whatever lies ahead.","sonnet":"A speaker wandering alone through a dull twilight, trailing the shadows of an reddish sunset, holds onto the memory that after countless battles everyone eventually rests against someone else's chest and builds a future; tears dry in the night and unadorned feeling begins to shine again.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~rendaman":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator confronts someone who makes promises they never keep and is hollow inside despite performing competence. Broken commitments and self-deception drive the song, with the repeated refrain placing accountability squarely on the offender: it is all in their hands."},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~shimmer-song":{"src":"web","web":"The transparent blue once burned into someone's eyes is now only a distant memory, transforming in an instant into deep crimson. The song charts solitary nights and inner contradictions, using heat-haze imagery to evoke beauty that is fleeting, while insisting that passion and the pursuit of freedom must be kept alive through the loneliness."},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~sick":{"haiku":"Apathy and rules stifle the speaker; they demand liberation from scripts and systems, raging against the normalization of emptiness.","opus":"A shove against conformity and imitation, insisting that life only begins when you stop obeying inherited rules and morals. Rage aimed at complacency, the refusal to stand posed like a statue while others live for you.","sonnet":"A declaration against apathy and imitation — 'are you satisfied to live by the rules? Life begins when you see the end' — urging the listener to shout, rage, and fight rather than stand like a sculpture in a pose, because prejudice and morals are the same cage.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~spirit-inspiration":{"haiku":"The song advocates trust and forward momentum despite fear. Dig deeper past deception, use your body as a tool, and leap forward—an unseen companion runs alongside in defiance.","opus":"A driving call to push past deceit, tend your injuries, seize the present, and act boldly instead of waiting for certainty about what's ahead. The sensed presence of someone striding beside you becomes the assurance you cannot fail. Nothing's Carved in Stone, the Japanese rock band, channel the surging, motivational energy typical of their anime-adjacent work, momentum itself the reason to keep going.","sonnet":"A short, muscular push into forward motion — wounds covered, deception pushed aside, the present moment made into an ally rather than a threat. Someone runs beside the speaker, unseen but felt, and that presence is enough to declare that loss is not possible when you leap before you look.","src":"haiku","web":"An anthem of self-determination urging the listener to dig past deception, forge their own path rather than follow others' footsteps, and leap before they look. The song insists on continuous transformation — moving forward, never looking back, and embracing each change as becoming a better version of oneself."},
"nothing-s-carved-in-stone~youth-city":{"haiku":"Urban dissonance and personal pain merge into artistic voice; the speaker owns his blues, refusing substitution or escape.","opus":"An impressionistic portrait of alienated youth in a smog-choked, absurd city, praying to painted-on stars and screaming to feel real. The singer clings to his own blues and asks the other to name and call to his nights, seeking identity and a hard-won freedom.","sonnet":"In a city of exhaust-fume forests and prayers to painted stars, someone soaked by rain asks for their pain to be left untouched, laughs off their own lostness, and demands their own blues back — the self's hunger-sharpened gaze is the only thing that cannot be replaced or named by anyone else.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing~july-the-fourth":{"haiku":"Failed redemption and regret after romantic or personal collapse; the speaker and other played wrong chords but asks for patience.","opus":"Nothing, the Philadelphia shoegaze band built on frontman Nicky Palermo's bleak lyricism, wrap regret in blurred guitars here. The narrator dwells on revenge never taken and sins that will not wash out, admitting the wrong chords were played all along. A single steadying refrain, urging endurance for just a while longer, threads through the resignation like a hand held out over ruin.","sonnet":"A pair of musicians — or maybe lovers — reckon with something gone irreparably wrong, a wound that never yielded the revenge or closure they needed. They acknowledge playing the wrong chords all along, yet urge one another to hold on a while longer anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"nothing~toothless-coal":{"haiku":"The living feed on the dead through complicit decadence; we laugh while burning at the stake.","opus":"Shoegaze-wrapped nihilism about living off decay, the living feeding on the dead, comfortable in a shared decadence. Even poetry petrifies the singer, so beauty offers no exit from a world quietly burning while everyone laughs at the flames.","sonnet":"A meditation on human parasitism and cultural decay — those who live feed off the dead, decadence is the native tongue, and even poetry is something that petrifies and traps; the image of burning at the stake while laughing captures a kind of helpless complicity in the collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"notions~woodguts":{"haiku":"Rage and threatened violence boil beneath silence; the speaker warns off intrusion with lethal precision and refusal to bend.","opus":"A hardcore-rap purge of pain into aggression, where street bravado masks buried trauma. The narrator threads video-game imagery of soul-fusing through his defiance, insisting hardship never broke his focus. Beneath the menace runs a mantra of walking his own path, regretting nothing, and growing from what he survived.","sonnet":"The speaker drives around with blunt ash out the window, processing accumulated pain through toughened focus — invoking Shin Megami Tensei's demon-fusion as a metaphor for absorbing hardship and becoming something new rather than breaking, holding the contradiction of street aggression and inner solitude.","src":"haiku"},
"nouveau-arcade~apart":{"haiku":"Miscommunication calcifies distance as both parties wait silently for the other to bridge an unbridgeable gap.","opus":"Two lovers each stall, waiting for the other to reach out first with forgiveness or affection, both too proud or too wounded to make the move. The gap between them silently sets like concrete as they puzzle over how a bond they believed unbreakable came to feel so remote.","sonnet":"A couple who once felt inseparable have drifted to a place where neither gets what they need — he waits for forgiveness she won't offer, she waits for love he no longer speaks — and neither can explain how they got this far apart.","src":"haiku"},
"nouveau-arcade~run-boy-run-feat-kj-sawka":{"haiku":"Escape and survival through motion and resilience; the speaker runs from existential threat, refusing surrender and choosing fight over destruction.","opus":"A cinematic call to keep moving under threat, framing survival as an act of will against natural selection and infection. Refusing to break becomes its own defiant weapon.","sonnet":"A survival-instinct anthem set against military interception and natural selection, where the imperative to run and hide from an unnamed infection is answered by a refusal to break or give in — 'I stay calm and drop the bomb' — persisting through rage and fear.","src":"haiku"},
"nouveau-arcade~summer-is-over-fury-weekend-remix":{"opus":"A wistful goodbye to a short-lived summer fling and the illusion it always was, offering an apology for what soured and coming to terms with the fact that the calendar has turned and a fresh start is due.","sonnet":"A summer fling has ended and both people know it: the shared memories — a favorite shirt, sex and mint ice cream — are beautiful but belong to a fantasy that the season's end has closed, and it's time, reluctantly, to begin again.","src":"sonnet"},
"nova-charisma~gemini":{"haiku":"Two people remain stuck in a toxic, addictive dynamic where intimacy destroys them both yet they cannot break free.","opus":"A clear-eyed loop of helpless craving, the narrator waking beside a person who can never satisfy what they truly need yet keeps luring them back, desire and self-ruin so knotted together that the thing they adore is the thing destroying them. Ripping out their own heart becomes a prize to be claimed. The constant tug between wanting space and wanting the person drives every line, an electronic-tinged admission that sabotaging oneself feels strangely sweet.","sonnet":"A toxic pull that the speaker cannot stop indulging — someone who offers nothing good and yet keeps being sought out, the bed woken up in described as something lethal and yet irresistible. The speaker knows they are setting themselves back, knows the person will never connect properly, and keeps going back anyway, promising improvement each time and not quite believing it.","src":"haiku"},
"nova-charisma~hollow-people":{"haiku":"Performative spirituality masks indifference; the speaker questions whether anyone genuinely cares beyond superficial appearance.","opus":"A jaded messiah figure descends to the numb and disaffected, hedging his own virtue while needling them for their indifference. The real question is whether anyone still cares enough to know the truth.","sonnet":"The speaker visits the hollow people below and reports on the world above to those who miss it, while positioning themselves as an honest man scanning both sides for snakes; the song ends with an unsettling comfort — lessons cut deep, a hex is placed above a bed, and the seasons change before you can see it.","src":"haiku"},
"nova-twins~antagonist":{"haiku":"A confrontational figure presents themselves as an unstoppable catalyst and antagonist, claiming they spark violence and lead a fight the listener will lose.","opus":"A fearless statement of self from a duo who built their identity on refusing to be anyone's princess, casting themselves as the catalyst and the villain of someone else's story, all confrontation and unbreakable nerve.","sonnet":"A song about being an unstoppable, feared force — the narrator positions herself as catalyst and antagonist who comes without pretense or submission, taking up space with titan strength and leaving no room for anyone who wants to fight.","src":"haiku"},
"nova-twins~choose-your-fighter":{"haiku":"Power and competition define survival, the speaker positions themselves as weapon and solution for those willing to compete.","opus":"An arcade-style declaration of war on a power-hungry rival who can't stand to come in second, daring them to pick their combatant. The singer casts herself as the hidden ace, fearless in the face of death and set on seizing control from whoever currently wields it.","sonnet":"A confrontational taunt at a megalomaniac — the speaker calls out someone intoxicated by their own power, preparing to take back what that person has seized, framing the encounter as a fight the other has already lost by choosing the wrong opponent.","src":"haiku"},
"nova-twins~taxi":{"haiku":"A display of predatory dominance and violent ambition. The speaker claims power through force, threatening to seize wealth and status from those seen as weaker or worthy of destruction.","opus":"British duo Nova Twins ride bass-heavy, hip-hop-tinged punk into a swaggering heist fantasy. Arriving to seize the throne, they threaten to empty out a rival and raze everything until even the elite kneel, chanting demands for control and dominance, a fantasy of taking all the power for themselves.","sonnet":"Two figures commandeer their own narrative at speed — pulling up, taking the wheel from whoever thought they were in charge, and systematically dismantling the power structures that excluded them. The takeover is physical and absolute: crowns claimed, royalty forced to bow, and every vestige of the old hierarchy burned down.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists-fr~after-the-rain":{"opus":"A tender reassurance to someone drowning in recurring waves of suffering and dissociation, telling them to breathe and endure because the pain will pass like rain, while naming it honestly as an exhausting war against oneself.","sonnet":"About surrendering to psychological suffering as an unavoidable wave rather than fighting it — the song acknowledges that emotional pain cannot be shed or faked away, that healing must be battled for internally, and that eventually the darkness passes like rain.","src":"sonnet"},
"novelists-fr~do-you-really-wanna-know":{"haiku":"The void inside hidden by a normal appearance; enslaved by internal emptiness and asking if anyone truly wants to see the darkness beneath.","opus":"The hidden emptiness behind a person who hides in plain sight, feeling dead inside and enslaved to a void; he doubts anyone really wants to see his inner self and pleads to know who will set him free.","sonnet":"The speaker hides an inner void from everyone around them, feeling enslaved by their own inability to heal or change, and wonders whether anyone really wants to see the emptiness inside that lies beneath the surface they present to the world.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists-fr~head-rush":{"haiku":"Addictive emotional dependency on another person whose presence provides the only sensation of aliveness.","opus":"A French metalcore plea to hold back the dark and keep everything lit, choosing to be consumed by fire rather than endure the crash afterward, and begging someone to pretend the world means no harm even as it strips him raw. The twist is that another person's watching eyes are the sole thing keeping him breathing, the more luminous side of himself.","sonnet":"About a relationship that keeps the narrator barely alive — they're bleeding, crashing, heading for the wall, but the other person's presence lights up their brain like fire in the veins; they beg to keep believing the wounds will make sense.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists-fr~heretic":{"haiku":"Spiritual rebellion and internal combustion burn away false comfort; the speaker abandons prayer and authority, choosing fire over submission.","opus":"A heretic's rejection of the gods and causes others cherish, insisting no one is up above, purging his veins by fire and refusing to let go even in torment.","sonnet":"A rejection of religious and social conformity framed as a confrontation with someone who clings to faith and communal causes as a coping mechanism. The narrator dismisses god, prayer, and the systems people worship as worthless distractions, declaring himself a heretic who feels more alive in the fire of that refusal than in any belief — even if it means running in the dark forever.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists-fr~lost-cause":{"haiku":"After betrayal and loss, the speaker spirals into despair, losing faith in themselves and fighting system failure while unable to escape the misery consuming them.","opus":"French metalcore mapping a breakdown from the inside, the narrator losing faith in himself as misery takes its toll, oscillating between the wish to be buried and the impossibility of ever outrunning the pain in his own head.","sonnet":"About complete mental and emotional collapse — the narrator has lost faith in themselves, cannot escape the pain in their head, feels a system failure coming, and oscillates between wanting to bury it all and admitting they're shutting down.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists-fr~lost-cause-paranormal-attack-remix":{"haiku":"Trapped in loss, the speaker cannot escape internal warfare or the price of their own collapse.","opus":"Losing faith in yourself amid a private war, praying in silence while someone else walked off with the prize. The djent breakdown externalizes a total malfunction, the feeling of shutting down and slipping the reins while never quite outrunning the pain in your own head.","sonnet":"About losing faith in yourself after being abandoned or betrayed — the narrator can no longer dream or find meaning, is caught in a loop of pain he can't escape, and the system failure metaphor collapses into just needing to breathe through a war inside his own head.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists-fr~modern-slave":{"haiku":"Urban industrialization traps people in systems of greed and vanity; the speaker yearns to escape the hellscape and rise above it.","opus":"A desperate wish to stay in a transcendent, better state and never be dragged back to a corrupt, consumerist reality run on deceit and vanity. French metalcore band Novelists FR pair soaring melody with heavy grooves to voice a refusal to rejoin the grinding routine most people accept.","sonnet":"Suffocating inside the grind of urban life — crowds, noise, the relentless machinery of consumption — a person aches to ascend beyond the concrete and the greed it reflects. The sky becomes the only space where existence feels like something other than captivity in a wall-less prison of their own era.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists-fr~smoke-signals":{"haiku":"A suicidal person built their world on lies and guilt; they stand at the edge, close to stepping into darkness they admit welcomes them.","opus":"A cry from the edge that reframes suicide as sparing everyone else the ghosts in your head, convinced they'd be better off without you. The heartbreak is its warped generosity, a plea for help disguised as permission to let go.","sonnet":"Someone built on lies who is standing at the edge of suicidal ideation, rationalizing that the people around them would be better off — the final chorus turns into a plea for collective solidarity in getting lost, the only honest thing left.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists-fr~somebody-else":{"haiku":"People are disconnected by digital systems and compelled to perform curated identities online, chasing likes while losing authenticity and self.","opus":"French metalcore act Novelists turn their gaze on digital alienation, sketching a person drifting through airwaves and tangled in a million wires, unable to feel any real connection. Likes become a proxy for feeling alive, faces vanish behind filters, and streets stay crowded while nobody truly meets. The narrator, sick of ads and lies, keeps straining to believe change is still possible amid the disconnection.","sonnet":"Digital saturation has severed genuine connection — streets are crowded but everyone is isolated, performing filtered versions of themselves, chasing likes to feel real. The exhaustion of being told who to be by an algorithmic world hardens into a longing to stop dreaming of being somebody else.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists-fr~terrorist":{"haiku":"An internal voice of self-doubt and self-harm acts as a parasite in the mind, and the speaker cannot distinguish self from enemy.","opus":"An externalized battle with a destructive inner voice, likened to a terrorist and a parasite the narrator wants dead and out of his head. The devastating turn is his realization that the enemy is himself, the voice living in his own mind, making the war unwinnable from outside.","sonnet":"The narrator's own destructive inner voice is the terrorist — a relentless internal enemy that takes over the mind and cannot be silenced, leaving the person feeling like both victim and attacker in a war fought entirely inside their own head.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists-fr~under-different-welkins":{"haiku":"Existential crisis and isolation corrode meaning despite external connection; the narrator seeks signs and fire to escape numbness and affirm existence.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with the fear of a wasted life, craving change and a way out of grey stagnation. Salvation is found partly in another person and partly in the demand not to let existence become vain.","sonnet":"A touring musician exhausted by three years of grinding change and grey isolation finds temporary wholeness in the eyes of someone who lives beneath a different sky, and pleads not to be left alone with the silence that makes life feel meaningless.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~all-for-nothing":{"haiku":"Pain and sacrifice accumulate without redemption, leaving the speaker questioning whether survival justifies the anguish endured.","opus":"A despairing wrestle with fading hope and love, questioning whether accumulated suffering had any purpose at all. The French metalcore band frames endurance as possibly futile, the narrator craving light while the stars keep falling, half-inviting his own undoing as he fears there may be no way back from this darkness.","sonnet":"A love that consumed everything has ended, and the survivor asks whether surviving the bruises and suffering was worth anything at all — \"was it all for nothing\" — unable to answer, unable to stop the sun from never rising again.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~do-you-really-wanna-know":{"haiku":"Internal void and self-hatred breed servitude and despair; the speaker questions whether liberation is possible when trapped between false external identity and hollow inner world.","opus":"An unflinching look at concealed depression and the exhausting performance of being fine. The narrator hides a void in plain sight, loathing his own perceived weakness and enslavement to despair, warning that the real interior would horrify anyone who saw it, and pleading for someone to set him free.","sonnet":"An invitation to look inside that doubles as a warning — the narrator hides in plain sight to protect others from the void they carry, while a voice in their head asks what's left when hiding is all you do. The question \"do you really wanna know?\" is genuine dread, not rhetoric: the outside and inside are irreconcilable.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~in-heaven":{"haiku":"Memory and longing collapse time as the speaker carries the beloved everywhere despite separation. Reunion remains distant yet constant, keeping the connection alive through internal presence.","opus":"Grief chases a lost face through memory and highway light, insisting on a reunion beyond death even as every present moment feels wasted without the person. Longing outruns acceptance.","sonnet":"A grief-driven longing: the speaker has seen the lost person's face a million times, whispered into their heart, replays everything they said — driving fast to catch them in the light, every minute without them vain, wanting to see through to heaven somehow.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~made-by-design":{"haiku":"Institutional architects blessed false reverence while suffocating potential; the speaker was engineered for suffering and must dismantle the heaven constructed for control.","opus":"A bleak, apocalyptic vision of a world collapsing under its own lies, where a manufactured heaven only smothers the truth. The narrator, feeling engineered and abandoned, invites his own breaking down, surrounded by the spiritually dead in a hell of humanity's own construction, praying the deceived finally grasp what they built.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped in a system of manufactured belief watches false heaven smother lies while the dead fill a hell of their own making; the admission 'I was made by design' fuses resignation with rage, the speaker as both product and dissenter.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~maldici-n-de-la-bruja":{"haiku":"Emptiness masked by luxury consumption, superficiality and deception breed entrapment, the curse that refuses to release one from cycles of wasted ambition.","opus":"A scathing portrait of a vain, superficial woman chasing luxury labels and reality-show fame to fill an inner emptiness. The witch's curse of the title is her own narrow mind, a caged bird mistaking its self-designed prison for flight.","sonnet":"A bilingual excoriation of a shallow, delusional woman — in Spanish she's called hateful, selfish, and lost; in English she's a waste of time trapped in a narrow mind — the song portraying her as someone who spends money she doesn't have trying to fill an inner emptiness she refuses to face.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~mourning-the-dawn":{"haiku":"A lover fades into darkness while the speaker walks away through grief, unable to return.","opus":"A grief song for someone who isn't coming home, fading into a numbing void that feels like their arms even as it tears him apart. The pull to burn away rather than be held down, a mourner half-wanting to dissolve into the loss.","sonnet":"Grief over someone who will not come home — the narrator fades into darkness and loss, caught between needing to tell the person they love them and knowing that person is gone, pleading to be burned away rather than face the void of their absence.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~okapi":{"haiku":"Solidarity and presence combat isolation as the speaker reaches across loneliness through human connection. Together, two standing souls resist singular collapse into void.","opus":"A hand extended to someone battling private demons in a crowded room, promising that isolation is the lie and shared footing is the way through. Reassurance offered against the pull to hide.","sonnet":"A direct address to someone isolated and fighting demons alone — wounded on the road, left in the cold, at the start of the end — with a steady offer of presence: you are not alone, I am right here, hold my hand and together we can stand.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~prisoner":{"haiku":"Addiction paralyzes; love confines; innocence is a lie the prisoner repeats to survive.","opus":"A toxic, addictive love reframed with the singer casting himself as a fallen deity, a curse handed down from the gods, come for someone not as innocent as they pretend. Obsession dressed as divinity, the craving to give and take until control is gone.","sonnet":"About a codependent relationship where both parties claim victimhood — the narrator accuses the other of needing them only to collapse into, while positioning themselves as something divine that \"came down and rose up\" for this person. The addiction to the dynamic makes escape impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~smoke-signals":{"haiku":"Spiraling guilt and internal collapse; standing at the edge of self-annihilation as the only escape.","opus":"A stark admission of shame, spent nerves, and the drift toward self-ruin. Standing on the brink, the narrator is sure the people he loves would be better off without him, yet still cries out through the image of distress flares, taking fragile comfort in the shared truth that everyone is adrift side by side and nothing endures.","sonnet":"A person near the edge of the world they built on lies — close enough to step off into the unknown — addresses others who are also getting lost together, noting that nothing lasts forever and the ghosts inside his head are enough to warn anyone away; the smoke signals of the title are the barely legible distress calls that might not reach anyone in time.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~terrorist":{"haiku":"Internal voice becomes hostile parasite; the speaker fights invading consciousness seeking to expel the enemy within.","opus":"Anxiety and intrusive thought rendered as an internal hostage-taker, an enemy that lives in the mind and cannot be evicted. The horror is recognizing the attacker is oneself.","sonnet":"The inner voice of self-destruction is named as a terrorist — a parasite controlling the brain and raging inside — and the speaker wants it dead and erased from memory, knowing the paradox: the enemy lives in their own mind and they are the enemy.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~the-light-the-fire":{"haiku":"Alone and drowning, the speaker warns of becoming the monster they once hoped to escape.","opus":"A man owning full responsibility for a ruin he saw coming and walked into anyway, now duetting with the monster he's turned into. The far-off thing he chased all his life may be all that's left as he drowns in a flood of his own making, each new morning harder to face.","sonnet":"A crushing song of self-aware ruination — the narrator knew exactly where the relationship was heading, kept walking into it anyway, and now lives with the guilt of having become a monster to themselves. The fire that devours has replaced the gold they dreamed of, and they can't stop dreaming of it anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"novelists~turn-it-up-keyboard-warriors-social-club":{"haiku":"Hollow rebellion hides beneath performative activism; the speaker exposes how truth dissolves into propaganda and individual power gets absorbed into manufactured consent.","opus":"A satire of online mob culture, cranking the volume to drown out emptiness while a self-righteous new order censors under the banner of tolerance. The speaker feels vain and worthless, complicit in the very outrage machine he mocks.","sonnet":"About the nihilistic cycle of online outrage culture — a cult of new justice where everyone lies but claims righteousness, where you either become the predator or get eaten, and where the volume of noise drowns out any real meaning. The narrator turns up the music to fill their own emptiness and ward off loneliness, while describing a world where people kill their brothers and censor in the name of tolerance.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~anaemia":{"haiku":"Nocturnal despair drains life like anemia while hospitals and night entities isolate and exhaust; the song frames survival through darkness as both curse and necessity, with rain and blood imagery of ongoing loss.","opus":"Italian gothic-doom framing night as both refuge and slow bleed, a sanitarium of the mind where dancing to stay alive is the only defense against a dawn that never comes. Depression rendered as endless bloodless nocturne.","sonnet":"About the mind as a dark sanitarium — drained of life by \"anaemic\" night, blood slowly depleted by black forces — in which dancing and staying alive through the darkness is the only form of resistance against the absence of dawn.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~anemia":{"haiku":"Nighttime drains life through blood anemia; madness and hidden horror persist in a sanitarium where black sprites feed on the alone and sleepless.","opus":"An Italian gothic-doom vision of the mind as a sanitarium, night as the only refuge and dance as a way to stay alive until a dawn that may never come. Anemia becomes the metaphor for a life slowly drained, beauty and dread braided through the darkness.","sonnet":"A gothic depiction of mental illness as a nocturnal institution — the mind at night becomes a sanitarium where dark spirits drain the life from you, leaving you dancing just to stay alive until dawn, which may never come.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~annoluce":{"haiku":"A journey through chaos and overwhelming sound seeking escape, rejecting the files and systems that constrain human experience and identity.","opus":"A dreamlike drift through a bazaar of fabrics, lamps and ages of color that dissolves into a storm of sound, an alien gust sweeping two people across a lightning-lit voyage that ends in a luminous song. Paranoia intrudes as the narrator realizes he's being watched and documented. The Italian band float between awe and menace, recollections scattering across the ground.","sonnet":"A vivid, turbulent return to a place layered with memory — market stalls, colors, ancestral noise — overwhelmed by the discovery that surveillance and manipulation have reshaped what was once known. The journey through storm and light ends in a kind of waking grief, the familiar landscape swept apart and the companion's hand the last thing to hold onto.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~aquamarine":{"haiku":"A meditation on beauty and decay, where an underwater treasure represents something precious hidden within spiritual and physical degradation.","opus":"The Italian gothic-metal band Novembre suspend beauty and dread together beneath the sea. Undersea imagery casts a beloved as buried treasure and the water's touch as tender, yet a creeping wrongness runs underneath, a sense that something has gone terribly, catastrophically awry. The ocean's embrace carries both life and the eerie stillness of ruin.","sonnet":"Something precious lies submerged, half-glimpsed beneath dark water — a person treated as a rare mineral worth the dive. The tenderness is fragile and edged with ruin: the imagery of broken Atlantis and lifeless caresses suggests that what is most valued may already be irretrievably drowned rather than waiting to be found.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~architheme":{"haiku":"Something rises from within, creating beauty and cities, yet fails to fully restore what once existed; the speaker stands in vaults of ruin, finding only endings.","opus":"An abstract, near-liturgical vision of a force welling up from within to raise cities and legends, approached by a fragile ascent, ending beneath vaults where the narrator treads a path of suffering and finds only the significance of finality. It knits creation to decay while addressing a Christ figure. Novembre, the Italian atmospheric death-doom band, favored exactly this ornate, mournful poetry over their gothic textures.","sonnet":"Something ancient flows from inside and builds worlds — cities, fairytales, half-lit frescoes — but time and indifference smear it with permanent paint, making restoration impossible. The speaker stands under vaulted architecture and finds only the meaning of ending, having finally walked the Sorrowful Way to its conclusion.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~australis":{"haiku":"A sailor or traveler journeys through monsoons and unknown seas, seeking to escape nightmares and ash; they call out to female spiritual forces and discover temples and art amid transformation and sky-bound flight.","opus":"An oceanic dream-voyage toward uncharted southern lands, monsoons and trade winds carrying the soul past nightmare toward some obliterating renewal. Novembre's melancholy turned outward into myth and passage rather than despair.","sonnet":"A mythic sea voyage toward the southern unknown — sailing through monsoons and salt air, shedding wounds and nightmares, toward Terra Incognita where temples lie buried in sand and the heart can finally be sealed and kept alive.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~bluecracy":{"haiku":"Blindness and entrapment in cold, inhospitable dimensions. The speaker is imprisoned in sapphire coldness, seeking rescue and light while trapped in an incomprehensible realm.","opus":"A grief-soaked dreamscape of blue light and towering ice, the narrator seething at some entity that killed his child and trapped him in a labyrinth of sapphire. Sightless before the sun, he hauls himself toward salvation through the downpour. Italy's Novembre drench their gothic death-doom in sorrow, letting cold beauty and mourning intertwine as he begs for light to be let in.","sonnet":"A narrator lost in a cold, blue-lit maze of grief and disorientation cannot locate the one who wronged him or cost him everything. Grief for a child bleeds into a wider numbness, and the one small direction left is toward sunlight — dragged forward by a final spark of something that might still be hope.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~bremen":{"haiku":"Soldiers are ordered to die uselessly in war while leaders distance themselves, victims fade into amnesia as suffering repeats through time.","opus":"A furious anti-war elegy in which commanders send soldiers to die pointlessly for glory, the fallen fed into a machine of forgetting while debris litters a ruined shore. Novembre wrap melancholic doom-death around grief and nostalgia, wondering whether the distant lights ahead are villages, stars, or the edge of a vanishing era.","sonnet":"An anti-war elegy frames soldiers as cannon fodder and leaders as indifferent executioners, their orders sending men into a mincer of forgetting. History repeats in black and white, generation after generation lost to the same epidemic of sorrow, with the only question left being whether those distant lights are a village or merely stars.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~cobalt-of-march":{"haiku":"The speaker sees a lost loved one in coldness and glacial darkness, watches a orchid meant to be taken away, then fades into September, mourning that nothing truly dies.","opus":"An elegy for a lost love wrapped in wintry, dreamlike imagery, the singer haunted by a face he can only see now in memory as the year drifts toward autumn. Novembre, the Italian gothic-doom band, use their melancholic atmosphere to hold onto a fragile consolation: that nothing is ever truly gone.","sonnet":"In the blue cold of March, a beloved face dissolves into memory — eyes glimpsed above a smile fading into the dark. Grief understands that this orchid of a life was always going to be taken; the only consolation is a faint intuition that nothing truly dies, only transforms across the long centuries.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~comedia":{"haiku":"War and rain fail to heal or cleanse a darkened world; one figure offers escape and strength, invoking Dante's descent into layers of sorrow.","opus":"A rain-soaked reckoning that binds warfare, coal-black despair, and Dantean pilgrimage, the narrator vowing to lead his beloved from a faceless place before their world crumbles. Naming Dante and Beatrice, he gathers strength for a final climb toward rest. Novembre draw on The Divine Comedy to frame their gothic doom as a weary passage through hell toward an imperfect, hard-won peace.","sonnet":"Two people endure a world still scarred by war and rain and moral weight, where the path forward is narrow and prayers for light go mostly unanswered. One of them holds a quiet promise to take the other by the hand someday and leave — not yet, but eventually, once enough strength has gathered at the roots.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~croma":{"haiku":"Two lovers prepare for departure and transformation, aware that tomorrow will bring change and they will slowly let go of yesterday even as bells and trains call them away.","opus":"The Italian doom-metal outfit, known for pairing crushing weight with melancholy romanticism, trades gloom for luminous longing here. A circus arrives, a train waits, and two figures ready themselves to fly toward a tomorrow where yesterday's shades grow vague. It is an invitation to be engulfed, to trust the coming dawn, that darkness softens into something almost tender.","sonnet":"A pair stands at a threshold between past and future, holding each other as departure nears. Grief lingers in a sky that weeps, but they dance through it — the sorrow is real, yet so is the promise that tomorrow will open into something brighter, the old winter fading as the train arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~easter":{"haiku":"The song depicts a slow descent into madness and demise, invoking religious imagery of crucifixion and sin while describing desire polluting the town. Bodies pass by as the speaker witnesses ancient breaches and atavistic madness.","opus":"Italian gothic-doom drenched in sacred, sensual imagery, resurrection and desire tangled together in incense and candlelight, a face glimpsed through haze in slow dissolution. More an atmosphere of decadent devotion than a plot, beauty decaying into rite.","sonnet":"A liturgical, sensory meditation on desire and social upheaval — the narrator stands in a shroud of light and fear watching generations pass by in a river of time, while lust pollutes a city and an ancient class divide roars open again.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~fin":{"haiku":"A meditation on impermanence and mutual dissolution: two figures journey through a barren landscape that they've destroyed together, seeking solace in shared survival while acknowledging that life itself is transient and destructive.","opus":"Italian gothic-doom band Novembre stage a frosty, star-lit goodbye, a pair of slender shapes making their way down an incline in a bleak November of 1979, one of them off to board a train and to render a picture of his own death. A secret carried unspoken to the tomb, a scorched and lifeless world, and life traced back to water run through it, mortality and refusal woven into mournful, elegiac imagery.","sonnet":"A speaker positioned at some remembered threshold in 1979 meditates on mortality, a kept secret, and the passage through a barren human-made wasteland toward something resembling the miracle of life in water. The lyric holds grief and wonder in suspension, moving between a private ending and a collective reckoning with what humanity has turned the earth into.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~geppetto":{"haiku":"An old man and companion set sail on a dark sea, recognizing this is their time to journey; the song blends memory, loss, and the inevitability of departure.","opus":"Sung largely in Italian, Novembre's piece conjures an old man rising from a bad dream, taking two rags and setting out to sea, greeting the water like an old friend. The Geppetto figure carries love and pain in a crumpled sack as the age of play gives way to emptiness. It closes in English toward a wished-for voice and desire made real.","sonnet":"An old man wakes from a troubling dream, gathers a few scraps, and sets out to sea to meet the water as an old friend — a journey rendered in Italian that carries the quiet weight of late-life acceptance. The second half shifts register into a gentler English reverie, offering the sea as both mirror and confessor for someone who knows his time is short.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~jules":{"haiku":"A dual-language narrative reflects on past return and a solitary figure behind a violent crowd; rain soothes slaughter-thirst while thunderstorms approach as both destructive and relatable natural phenomena.","opus":"A bilingual elegy weaving Italian and English, memory of someone left alone amid a screaming crowd as rain and coming storms wash over the scene. Loss held with the promise that the past returns, if only for a day.","sonnet":"A meditation on a specific traumatic day witnessed from behind a screaming crowd, where rain falls on bloodshed; the Italian passages mourn a past that might return to dry blue tears, and the song ends waiting for the next storm from the east.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~materia":{"haiku":"Time moves backward and forward at once; birth brings light that fades into night, and tomorrow hides war and horror beneath its surface.","opus":"A reflection on arriving in the world at birth, then falling from sheltering hands into darkness, unable to quiet a lasting grief. That a passage toward the future exists is no promise of wellbeing; dusk grants only a brief warm reprieve before the ache recedes with the day. Novembre, the Italian death-doom band, wrap this musing on beginnings and enduring loss in their autumnal melancholy.","sonnet":"Born into light and immediately let fall into night — the birth trauma never resolved, the cries still echoing. A brief warmth arrives at sundown, two figures immersed in orange light, the fading sun carrying their pain away with it as it drops. But war still resides in tomorrow, hiding behind whatever relief today brought.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~memoria-stoica-vetro":{"haiku":"Two people encounter each other in autumn like fragile leaves, gently touching before suddenly separating, losing connection in a chaotic transformation to summer.","opus":"Italian gothic-doom that shifts from lifting shame off a suffering beloved to a fragile Italian memory of two people brushing past each other like leaves in a strange autumn. Loss captured in the image of a hand slipping free.","sonnet":"Two movements: an English section about cleansing someone of shame accumulated through inherited sin, and an Italian section about a strange autumn encounter — two people brushing past each other like leaves, almost connecting, then losing each other's hand in the sudden upheaval of a season changing.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~nascence":{"opus":"Rebirth through love and physical tenderness: caressing a partner's hair and gazing into their eyes, the narrator feels himself revived through the elements, a perfect instant guarded against time's slow wear.","sonnet":"A sensual, near-mystical experience of falling into intimacy — touching someone's hair, tasting ocean salt in their eyes, and feeling reborn through the encounter; the song treats this new closeness as a kind of rebirth that purifies even the hardest buried feelings.","src":"sonnet"},
"novembre~nostalgiaplatz":{"haiku":"Longing for departure from a world of brightness and false gifts; the speaker seeks refuge in a nostalgic place where feelings can be mourned.","opus":"A brief elegy from someone who can no longer endure the blinding brightness or the supposed blessing of the divine, weeping as a weathered vessel disappears over the horizon and carries his emotions off with it. He imagines a made-up sanctuary of memory as a place to dwell forever. Novembre, the Italian atmospheric doom band, condense weariness and yearning into one aching picture of departure.","sonnet":"Overwhelmed by too much light, too much feeling, a person watches their emotions sail away on an old boat and is left dry and crying on the shore. The only answer is to relocate to a place called Nostalgiaplatz — a nowhere of remembered feeling — and stay there, together, forever.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~nothijngrad":{"haiku":"Night awakens forgotten memories and emotion; riders journey beyond known shores toward a distant place of pride and might called Nothijngrad.","opus":"Nightfall dredges up buried recollections and rhymes that are futile yet unavoidable, a wash of pale radiance flooding the narrator's neglected mental chambers before the paths circle back to dead ends. He urges accepting an outstretched hand toward some hopeful morning beyond. Italy's Novembre steep their melancholic metal in dreamlike imagery, promising vast time ahead and vision reaching past vanity and power.","sonnet":"Night opens old interior spaces, flooding forgotten rooms with light from a sea of flowers. Memory and feeling resurface unbidden, and the narrator reaches toward a journey that has no clear destination — only the conviction that something vast and luminous lies ahead, and that a thousand years of possibility still remain.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~oceans-of-afternoons":{"haiku":"The speaker retreats from the world into a dreamlike cinema of time, receiving gifts of sky and breeze but ultimately forgetting themselves in fog until morning comes.","opus":"Two figures linger in fading afternoon light, cyan panoramas and blue magma slowing time until morning is deceived into night. The Italian band gothic lyricism folds tenderness into an admission of having fled and failed an unrepentant world. Even the promise of returning to recover forgotten things stays suspended, dissolving into a wait for the coming dawn.","sonnet":"Two people linger in a sunlit standstill while time and sensation blend into something oceanic. The speaker has both fled and failed the world but still returns, searching through the fog of lost hours for whatever meaning they left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~reason":{"haiku":"Another person becomes the source of joy and transformation; meeting them dissolves old barriers and fills emptiness with meaning.","opus":"A luminous devotion crediting the loved one with clearing violet skies and warming a miracle-filled spring. The Italian gothic-metal band, usually shadowed, turns strikingly bright here, portraying a plunge into sheer delight where a long-standing inner stronghold crumbles at a single grin, blindness surrendering to wonder.","sonnet":"A love arrives and dismantles every cloud the speaker had accepted as permanent weather. What was once a fortress of guardedness dissolves at a glance, and ordinary spring warmth transforms into evidence of the miraculous — all because one person's presence rewrites the emotional climate.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~the-dream-of-the-old-boats":{"haiku":"Nostalgia for beauty and peace now lost to time: the speaker dreams of ancient boats decorated with baroque art and dignity, warmed by sun—sensations and sights that only dreams preserve.","opus":"Novembre float through a seafaring reverie, a vessel sliding across glassy water in mild wind, sun, and an eerie calm. An ornate, decaying old craft surfaces, its worn detailing and long-gone history grander than any real city could be. The whole scene turns out to be a dream, warmth reachable only there, leaving the narrator aching to revisit it.","sonnet":"A dreamer encounters a decaying baroque vessel on calm water — its ornaments worn, its wars lost, its dignity intact — and finds it more beautiful than Venice or the moon. The warmth of that imagined scene cannot survive waking; the sun that felt kind there remains an enemy in daylight, and the wish to return to the dream is all that remains.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~the-promise":{"haiku":"Promise-dealers make and break vows, offering false paths to paradise; war brings desolation while people cling to empty hopes and broken dreams.","opus":"The Italian death-doom band spins a sorrowful, grandiose piece about broken vows and the treachery of empty bargains, a tempter whose deals strip away every freedom and leave only ruin. Shadowed by war and desperation, and by the sense that the heavens weep endlessly, it dwells on the emptiness of promises made only to be betrayed.","sonnet":"Two forces pull apart and together simultaneously: arms stretched toward hunger that lips can never quite meet, a promise dealer who trades freedom for closeness, war-ravaged fields where the starving make their final stand. Heaven is asked to look away because it cannot help, and the promises made here were never meant to be kept — blackmail dressed as devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~the-rose":{"haiku":"The speaker plants hope and love in another's garden only to reap thorns; they become destructive force in primordial chaos, trapped in cycles of failure and loss at the sea's bottom.","opus":"A thick, myth-soaked self-examination in which the speaker possesses nothing, sows seeds that sprout only barbs, and expires cradled by a mythic figure, a wandering spirit. Novembre threads Greek legend and images of sunken treasures through a confession of torching the woods and shattering ancient vows, a soul cast adrift amid brutality, remorse, and ill portent.","sonnet":"A figure haunted by spiritual failure and broken promises surveys the wreckage of everything they tried to nurture — a garden that bore thorns instead of blossoms, a forest they set alight, a soul that wandered too long to find its way back. Atonement arrives too late.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~triesteitaliana":{"haiku":"Futile circular running exhausts resistance; silence swallows words while light fails, leaving only weariness and stained memory.","opus":"Two figures fleeing along desert pathways past cold border guardians, running in circles until breathless yet sharing a profound silence. Rooted in the fraught Italian border city of Trieste, it fuses pain, escape, and an intimate bond forged in flight.","sonnet":"Running in circles at cold, rigid borders until breathless, collapsing into laughter beneath starry stains, then stitching a wound and fleeing through desert pathways — two lone wolves sharing \"the utmost silence\" along branching roads through time, with the Italian city named as both geography and emotional territory.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~umana":{"haiku":"A person resists inhumane conformity in a broken society built on competition and violence. They refuse to accept normalized cruelty.","opus":"A reflection on a species-wide breakdown of awareness, with an insistent inner voice of the era murmuring that the familiar rules have failed. A closing spoken passage diagnoses humankind as unchanged in its cruelty and rivalry; the narrator, stripped of fear and of tenderness, meets the reckoning while dread swallows him.","sonnet":"About the crisis of human consciousness — the narrator can't find shelter from societal decay, alternates between fear and its absence, and lands on a quoted Krishnamurti passage that diagnoses humanity's violence and competitiveness as the real crisis. It's a song about being unable to escape what humans fundamentally are.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~ursa":{"haiku":"A speaker lies in profound sadness beneath the weight of state oppression and plague, finding solace only in the presence of one other person as the world ends outside their refuge.","opus":"A dreamlike, mournful tale of being hunted in a plague-stricken world tinged with totalitarian menace, the speaker branded a heretic with nowhere left to run, sheltered in a beast's embrace. As the world seems to end, the heart of it is a plea for a loved one to remain indoors and perish alongside him rather than confront what waits beyond.","sonnet":"A bleak elegy for a collapsing world set against imagery of Soviet-era coldness and exile — declared an apostate, the narrator clings to a loved one as everything ends, pleading for them to stay inside as outside reality crumbles into sad and senseless snow.","src":"haiku"},
"novembre~verne":{"haiku":"The song mourns Sarah's departure and the unchanging nature of loss, mixing Italian meditation on dreams as creations of heartache that are stripped of reason and sent to die. Each tomorrow is love but nothing can alter fate.","opus":"An elegy addressed to a lost woman named Sarah, dreams framed as children of the heart born from pain and sent to die, the Italian verses insisting that a man's dream of a future without love is empty. Doom-metal romanticism mourning the impossibility of holding onto anything.","sonnet":"A bilingual elegy switching between English and Italian — in English, a figure named Sarah witnesses a sky turning red and delivers omens; in Italian, the narrator arrives bearing the keys to the sun hoping to make love tomorrow, but dreams are exposed as children of pain, stripped of reason and sent to die.","src":"haiku"},
"nubia~memory-lapse":{"haiku":"Cognitive breakdown and inability to form thoughts or hold memories; the speaker's mind misfires and fails them, leaving them purposeless.","opus":"A visceral portrait of losing one's grip on cognition, thoughts dissolving before they can be held and the self feeling redundant, purposeless. The one anchor is a calming directive to slow down and stay steady, a fragile self-reassurance that the mind can be reclaimed if only he can steady it.","sonnet":"Cognitive fog renders thought slippery and self unreachable — the mind a furnace that will not ignite, a vessel with a hole through which data leaks. Against the panic of mental dissolution, a quiet discipline insists: breathe, collect, work it out somehow.","src":"haiku"},
"nubia~tonight":{"haiku":"Sleeplessness and disconnection from reality compound into cycles of medication and attempted rest that never arrive, leaving the restless mind trapped.","opus":"A visceral evocation of insomnia and the dread of sleep paralysis, where night becomes a battleground rather than rest. The narrator drifts with lifeless eyes as spectral figures crowd his room and medication fails him, his relentless awareness a prison, wishing only that the fever of a restless mind might finally break.","sonnet":"Chronic insomnia and anxiety fracture the speaker's grip on reality as crowds of restless figures dance around the room where they lie awake; drugs and drink fail to bring sleep, and the song closes as a prayer for the fever to burn away and leave a fresh mind that can dream through the night.","src":"haiku"},
"nuclear-daisies~cinnamon-kiss":{"haiku":"Timeless intimacy freezes two figures in cosmic connection while temporal forces burn and consume everything surrounding them.","opus":"Intoxicated, timeless devotion where a kiss is felt in the bones and clawed wings are half-forgiven. The deceiving fever is love itself, wanted anyway.","sonnet":"An intensely physical love song — the feeling of a partner's kiss is described as felt inside bones, a celestial permanence, a fever that never breaks. The narrator is fully consumed by this love, willing to let the clocks burn and time dissolve, and the recurring \"fever deceiver\" suggests they know this intensity cannot be rational but they surrender to it anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"nuclear-daisies~heaven-in-your-head":{"opus":"A plea to locate peace internally rather than in the world, sung against a claustrophobic refrain of never escaping, holding onto connection amid the certainty that things will go wrong.","sonnet":"A song about finding peace and connection internally when the outer world provides none — the narrator urges creating a private heaven in the mind to maintain connection to someone, while the undertow of the repeated lines suggests they fear they can never get out of that mental space.","src":"sonnet"},
"number-girl~a-13uhkec":{"haiku":"Trauma from adolescent sexual encounter; deliberate memory suppression becomes the coping mechanism.","opus":"Number Girl's Shutoku Mukai wrote hometown-to-Tokyo alienation like almost no one in late-90s Japanese rock, all jagged guitars and clinical dread. Here a girl carries a village shrine encounter into the city and university, then wakes beside a stranger's cigarette butts with no memory of arriving. Walking anonymous streets, she deliberately erases the cold and the morning light from her mind.","sonnet":"A girl who had sex at a village shrine, moved to the city, entered university, and then woke up in a strange room beside someone else's cigarette butts with no memory of how she got there — she walks the city alone, recognizes no one, and deliberately erases the cold sensation and the morning light from her memory entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"number-girl~a-1i3bb9g":{"haiku":"Urban decay where abandoned and dead become sharp-edged; young people floating through withered landscapes, sharpened by indifference.","src":"haiku","web":"Surreal urban vignettes — a discarded cat, rust on a bridge, geisha girls smiling — build toward the image of an iron wind that sharpens everything it touches, framing harsh city life as a force that hones and brutalises simultaneously."},
"number-girl~a-1kq18ho":{"opus":"Mukai's Number Girl often mapped Tokyo as a hungry, overstimulating sprawl, and this is one of its aimless afternoon dispatches. Wandering near Nakano station, eating ramen, watching the crush of faces at crosswalks, the narrator trips face-first into the city's smell, clocks a dark-eyed girl searching for something and a dog seeming to sing, then wants only more drink and home.","sonnet":"A rambling urban afternoon — wandering near Nakano station, hungry, eating ramen, watching the city's variety of people at crosslights, tripping and falling face-first into the city smell, noticing a dark-eyed girl searching for something, a dog singing, and the desire for more alcohol and home.","src":"sonnet"},
"number-girl~a-1t76vnh":{"haiku":"Witnessing ephemeral seasonal beauty while feeling isolated; wanting connection but unable to properly perceive the world.","opus":"Number Girl paired abrasive angularity with a strange lyrical tenderness for seasons and weather, and that tension drives this restless urban interior. The narrator wants to embrace the sea, asks whether anyone has watched cherry blossoms dance or heard the wind between seasons, feels inadequate at a crossroads, and refuses to be crammed into spaces too tight for who they are.","sonnet":"A fragmented, restless state of mind in a city — wanting to embrace the sea, asking whether anyone has seen cherry blossoms dance or heard the wind between seasons, feeling inadequate at a crossroads, refusing to squeeze into too-tight places.","src":"haiku"},
"number-girl~a-2yzqm0":{"haiku":"A lament on the terror of disconnection—the speaker burns while others cool themselves in sterile spaces; touch is both feared and needed.","opus":"Emotional frostbite was Mukai's great subject, and Number Girl render it here as generational diagnosis. One person burns feverish and desperate for contact while everyone flees to frigid places; a girl who fears being hurt performs false smiles after school. Everyone still laughs and pretends to trust, deepening the disconnect, and the ache is being the lone body aflame in a room choosing to freeze.","sonnet":"A song about the terror of emotional contact in a generation that prefers cold distance — one person is feverish and desperate for connection while everyone around him flees to frigid places, and a girl who fears being hurt performs false smiles after school. They all still manage to laugh and pretend to trust each other, which makes the disconnection worse. The emotional core is the loneliness of being the one person burning in a room full of people choosing to freeze.","src":"haiku"},
"number-girl~a-cg79t2":{"haiku":"Summer arrives suddenly and one becomes transparent; the city sparkles madly while you fade into its chaos.","src":"haiku","web":"A girl with red hair pulls the narrator through a chaotic, sparkling city on a summer day; boundaries between observer and environment dissolve as her laughter and the city's heat merge into a single transparent, disorienting sensation.","fable":"The whole song turns on a strange grammatical collapse: again and again the narrator reports that before he noticed, he simply was summer — not that summer arrived but that he became the season, a landscape dissolving into the streets. Mukai's transparent girl is glimpsed at exactly that dissolve, red-haired, tugged along by a fast-walking man, laughing a fake laugh and then laughing that it's cool — a figure so lightly sketched she reads as heat-shimmer more than person, which is the title's whole claim. Around her the crazed street corner glitters and grinds with friction, and NUMBER GIRL's wiry Fukuoka attack makes that friction audible, the accelerating abrasion the lyric names outright. So the transparency isn't purity but disappearance: adolescence witnessed at the instant it becomes weather and vanishes, the see-through girl and the see-through narrator both melting into a summer that already happened before anyone could hold it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["dissociation & numbness","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"adolescence witnessed at the instant it dissolves into weather","built":"the narrator doesn't notice summer arriving — he notices he already became it"},
"number-girl~cibicco":{"haiku":"Everyday observations of children and strangers, small urban moments caught in fleeting sensory detail.","opus":"A wry, observational sketch of a cramped Japanese city park, taunting a fisherman, a thief, kids forcing a baseball game into too little space, an old woman's dumpling eyed by a big dog. Ordinary afternoon life caught in the golden light before it climbs out of view.","sonnet":"Snapshots of ordinary street life addressed directly to the people in them — a Cibicco, a fisherman, a thief, kids playing baseball in a too-small city park, a young woman walking a huge dog that nearly eats someone's bun before disappearing uphill — pure observation with no judgment.","src":"haiku"},
"number-girl~destruction-baby":{"haiku":"Destruction consumes the speaker and their love object, where control shatters into uncontrollable collapse. Hearts demolished, consciousness fragmented into echoes of breaking.","opus":"A frenzied Japanese noise-rock burst about total emotional demolition and the vertigo that follows. Number Girl fixate on a heart laid gorgeously to waste, then ask who moved into the ruins afterward, savoring the reckless, uncontrollable rush of feeling as something almost pleasurable amid the wreckage.","sonnet":"The inside of someone's heart has been cleanly, completely destroyed — 'absolute destruction' — and the speaker asks who moved in afterward; the refrain 'destruction baby' and 'uncontrollable feelings' render the destruction as exhilarating rather than purely catastrophic, a release the speaker almost celebrates.","src":"haiku"},
"number-girl~eight-beater":{"haiku":"Disconnected impulse and intoxication in a feedback loop of urban anxiety; endless sensation separated from communication.","opus":"A restless, intoxicated narrator drifts through a feverish Japanese city of alienation and friction between minds, describing surging sexual impulses, the Buddhist sense of impermanence, and an inability to truly communicate amid endless feedback.","sonnet":"The song captures a restless, anxious state of urban drifting — the narrator describes living in a city of feedback and anxiety, unable to communicate, caught between fleeting sexual impulse and Buddhist impermanence, wandering without direction in a Japanese style.","src":"haiku","web":"A narrator drifts through an exhausting city of endless feedback and sensory noise, unable to connect despite perceiving everything around them — \"communication breakdown\" echoes as a refrain against urban alienation and intoxicated restlessness.","fable":"Number Girl's Mukai builds this out of the collision the title advertises: the eight-beat, that borrowed rock backbone, played JAPANESE STYLE — an imported American rhythm forced through a specifically local sensory and spiritual grid. The verses run friction between minds, a self drowning impulse into a four-track and getting drunk on the mix, a body that can still feel the angle of the sunlight and the smell of the wind yet finds communication impossible while the feedback extends without end. That last word is the hinge: feedback is both the guitar's literal howl and the emotional loop the narrator is trapped in, and Mukai splices it against a Buddhist tag for the impermanence of all things, so the revived sexual urge and the endlessly repeated cycle become the same phenomenon heard two ways. The chorus refuses to resolve it — he is wandering, drifting, in a feedback city at 24:00, through a landscape he half-recognizes and half-doesn't — and the refusal is the content: the eight-beat keeps hammering not toward release but as the sound of someone circling a scene of impermanence they can neither enter nor leave.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","night & the city","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"an imported rhythm forced through a local grid of impermanence","built":"feedback means both the guitar's howl and the emotional loop it names"},
"number-girl~eight-beater-2001-8-29-ax":{"src":"web","web":"A narrator drifts through an exhausting city of endless feedback and sensory noise, unable to connect despite perceiving everything around them — \"communication breakdown\" echoes as a refrain against urban alienation and intoxicated restlessness."},
"number-girl~i-don-t-know":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator confesses total inability to read a girl — neither her truth nor her lies — while glimpsing her reading manga alone and then selling herself to strangers, the repeated refrain capturing the impossibility of knowing someone whose inner and outer lives are completely sealed off."},
"number-girl~sasu-you":{"haiku":"Resentment and violence strike randomly; casual cruelty masked as indifference, unnamed targets absorbing accumulated hatred.","opus":"A jittery burst from the Fukuoka indie-rock quartet channels their spiky late-90s energy into a paranoid vignette of being stabbed and pierced, part physical wound, part social ambush. The lurching wordplay imagines resentment-carrying strangers as roaming assassins cutting people down at random, resentment made blade-sharp and personal.","sonnet":"A narrator insists on his own innocence while those around him carry decades of accumulated resentment. The Japanese lyrics turn on a single repeated verb — to pierce, to stab — and the image of a roadside swordsman cutting down strangers at random. Grievance without target finds a target anyway.","src":"haiku","web":"Narrated from the perspective of a victim of tsujigiri — the historical Japanese practice of samurai testing swords on random commoners — the song meditates on sudden, motiveless violence and the bewilderment of being attacked by unknown resentment.","fable":"Built on a pun that is also the thesis: sasu, to stab, welded to the English you, threaded through the words for a random slasher and for tsujigiri — the old samurai custom of testing a new blade on whoever happened to be crossing at the crossroads. The opening is pure grotesque sensation — it's stuck in, it's gone in deep, it's mired, it's laughing — violence rendered as texture before it's rendered as event. Then one line drops the wordplay for plain speech and turns the whole thing into diagnosis: I haven't done anything wrong, yet all around are people hauling years of accumulated ressentiment. Motiveless street violence gets read as the discharge of stored-up grievance, the innocent narrator stabbed at random precisely because the blade needs no motive — only a body at the crossroads.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"motiveless street violence as the discharge of stored grievance","built":"a pun welding the Japanese to stab to the English you"},
"number-girl~tattoo":{"haiku":"A tattoo holds fragmented memory; the image of a mark outlasts the face that received it, swallowing specific identity into pattern.","opus":"A hazy dawn recollection of a heart-shaped tattoo on a lover's right shoulder, glimpsed as a dark shirt comes off in the morning glow. She said it was inked on her twentieth birthday, but the face has faded and only the pattern spins on. Japan's Number Girl turn a search for memory into obsession, scents and light-angles conjuring an image at once vague and vivid.","sonnet":"A fleeting early-morning memory circles obsessively around a tattoo glimpsed on a woman at dawn — a heart shape on her right shoulder that she received on her twentieth birthday. The face that goes with it has blurred beyond recovery, but the mark itself spins on in the mind, vivid and unresolved.","src":"haiku","web":"The narrator fixates on a heart-shaped tattoo glimpsed on a woman's shoulder at dawn, unable to reconstruct her face from memory — only the spinning image of the tattoo remains, turning the whole song into an obsessive, incomplete act of recollection.","fable":"A memory decomposing into obsession. It opens as a four-noun stutter — right shoulder, tattoo, daybreak, afterimage — a whole encounter already broken into fragments that won't cohere. The scene underneath is a morning-after: a dark shirt off at dawn, the tattoo maybe a heart, and her saying she got it inked on her twentieth birthday — but the face has already gone out of the picture. The song's real movement is the confession that hunting for the memory keeps sliding into delusion, triggered back to life by nothing more than the smell of wind or the angle of the light. And it ends by inverting what identifies a person: more than the outline of a face, the scent of hair, the family and given name, it is only the pattern that now goes round and round. The least personal mark she carried is the one thing that survives her — obsession keeping the tattoo when it has lost the woman.","fv":2,"fnote":"The verb she uses for getting the tattoo — 刺された, stabbed in — is the same sasu that SASU-YOU builds its slasher pun on three tracks earlier: the album threads one verb through violence and intimacy alike.","themes":["nostalgia & memory","madness & the mind","love & desire"],"means":"the least personal mark surviving when the face has not","built":"four nouns stuttered at the opening before the scene can cohere"},
"number-girl~yaruse-nakio-beat":{"haiku":"Loneliness and isolation; disconnection from others is the central theme.","opus":"Adult isolation replaying oddly specific childhood memories, a playground game invented in 1979, comics scrawled behind an exam paper, alone in a room. The nostalgia is restless and unresolved, memory as the only company left.","sonnet":"A man alone in his room recalling childhood inventions — a game called \"crocodile-killing\" on a seesaw in 1979, a long-running manga drawn on the back of exam papers in the 1980s — small absurd personal triumphs remembered with self-satisfied laughter.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title turns yarusenai — that Japanese word for a disconsolateness with no outlet — into a person's name, Yaruse Nakio, and the song is his beat: a man alone in a room dating his own trivial childhood as if reciting case files. The two memories he offers are gloriously minor — inventing a game he called crocodile-killing on a public-housing playground seesaw, serializing a long manga on the backs of his midterm answer sheets — and each is stamped with a year (1979, 1988) and repeated verbatim in the flat idiom of an incident report, an event that occurred, an event that took place. That deadpan officialese is the whole joke and the whole ache: the mind files small private acts of creation as historical events because, alone, they're all the history there is. The recurring boast — laughing, full of self-pride — is the mask, and what it covers is the emptiness the title already named: nothing to do now but sit in the single room and rehearse the beat of two remembered afternoons.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","nostalgia & memory","artifice & performance"],"means":"minor childhood acts filed in deadpan as the only history","built":"disconsolateness personified as a man with a case-file name"},
"number-girl~young-girl-seventeen-sexually-knowing-remastered-2014":{"haiku":"Precocious awakening to a world already crimson and decaying; young knowledge meeting urban rot, silhouettes observing from above.","opus":"Mukai's dusk-lit Japanese scene of an older man watching a seventeen-year-old girl walk into the red twilight, uneasily aware of his own uselessness and creeping gaze, the knowing distance between them the real subject.","sonnet":"A fragmented Japanese song about twilight observation — a young woman walks through the city into red light, and a self-described foolish man watches her from a distance, aware that she possesses a kind of knowing that exceeds her age while he continues doing pointless things.","src":"haiku","web":"A detached male narrator observes a seventeen-year-old girl against a twilight cityscape, juxtaposing her premature worldliness with his own sense of stagnation and worthlessness, watching from rooftops as evening refuses to arrive."},
"number-girl~zazenbeats-kemonostyle-2001-8-29-ax":{"src":"web","web":"A live version of a track composed for the film soundtrack of 'Kemonogare, Orera no Saru to'; the lyrics depict a solitary figure wandering the night city with organs laughing inside them, circuits of thought growing simpler, drifting between zazen stillness and animal instinct."},
"number-girl~zegen-vs-undercover":{"haiku":"Hidden violence and indifference in the city; corruption consuming itself while witnesses disappear into the landscape.","opus":"A jittery noir vignette narrated by a bystander who claims to know nothing and see nothing while a pimp works the shadows, a prostitute's undercover cop lover is found murdered in a back alley, and a Black woman sings the blues with a snakeskin shamisen as the street turns lurid. The narrator fears he too will vanish, forgotten like a two-year-old case. Number Girl, the Japanese noise-rock band, deliver this urban unease with frantic, angular energy.","src":"haiku","web":"A pimp and an undercover detective navigate the same corrupt city until the detective, who had grown close to a sex worker, is murdered in a back alley; the narrator watches as a detached bystander who sees the tasteless truth and does nothing.","fable":"The album's governing word — sappukei, bleak and desolate scenery — dramatized as a noir the narrator refuses to enter. A pimp works the shadows, an undercover cop who got too close to a prostitute turns up dead in a back alley, danger operates and gets quietly hushed up; the plot keeps escalating around him. But every time it does, the same refrain recuses him — I don’t know, I’m a bystander, I can’t see — answering the town’s recurring flush of color, a word that here doubles for the flesh trade, with scenery drained of it. The genius is that the desolation isn't in the crimes but in the watching — the refrain performs exactly the numbed, deniable spectatorship the title names. Then the bridge drops the mask: nobody remembers the incident from two years back, so will I too just dissolve into this scenery? The bystander's safety curdles into his terror — to be a spectator is already to be disappearing.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","surveillance & control"],"means":"spectatorship curdling from safety into the fear of disappearing","built":"the bystander refrain enacting the numbed deniability it names"},
"numenorean~adore":{"haiku":"A person cycles through pain and cycles of self-failure, begging for forgiveness; they reject being destroyed and claim strength despite loss and broken trust.","opus":"A devastating plea to have love painted onto the eyes while sadness consumes, the narrator naming his own body as murder and cycling between feeling whole and low. Addressing a son or daughter, he begs forgiveness and vows he will never truly die. Canada's Numenorean fuse blackgaze beauty with hardcore despair, wringing from grief and guilt a fragile, defiant claim to survive.","sonnet":"A person oscillates between moments of feeling whole and collapsing again, haunted by suicidal ideation and the weight of self-sabotage. Addressing what may be a future child, he processes guilt and failure before arriving at a defiant refusal — he has been destroyed before, he will not be destroyed this time.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~code-war":{"haiku":"Digital warfare and loss of control in an information age. Humanity surrenders its data and soul to machines and upgrades.","opus":"Synth-metal dystopia set inside the information age, where a network of lies wages war on truth and everyone surrenders their data and soul. It frames the digital era as a battlefield and asks, bluntly, which side you're on.","sonnet":"A song framed around digital-age conflict — the internet spreading lies, an information war being waged inside the machine — asking which side you are on and whether you will surrender your data and identity to the network or trace your way back to truth.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~destroyer":{"haiku":"An internal force of chaos and darkness that can destroy the speaker's carefully constructed life, yet they feel unable to resist its pull and power.","opus":"A song about a destructive attachment personified as an unstoppable force the narrator keeps returning to, unable to tell dream from reality, surrendering composure to its endless reign. The pull is toxic and knowingly chosen, love as the thing that unmakes him.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship or internal force that overwhelms all defenses — something too large to contain, operating beyond anyone's control, and the narrator keeps returning to it despite knowing it dismantles them.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~empathy":{"haiku":"The song calls for collective empathy to prevent disaster as society grows numb inside uniform systems. It warns that compassion breaking through apathy is the only path forward.","opus":"A synth-driven plea against apathy, insisting one act of feeling could reverse a looming collapse. It sets numbness and uniformity against the stubborn hope that connection still matters and no one is truly beyond reach.","sonnet":"A plea to break collective apathy before a social or personal disaster — arguing that people have been numbed and deindividualized by systems, but empathy from even one person could turn things around before it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~fantasies-heavens-mix":{"haiku":"Breaking free from living a lie built on unmet expectations and false promises, rejecting the fantasy of being good enough for others' demands.","opus":"Working in the synth-driven idiom where emotion is rendered in gleaming electronics, this track charts a narrator who no longer recognizes his own reflection after exhausting himself for someone else's approval. The fading inner fire, the sleepless nights sustaining a lie, finally give way to resolve. He renounces the imposed fantasy and turns, at last, toward his own direction.","sonnet":"A person recognises they have been sustaining themselves on illusions — working tirelessly for approval that never comes, living inside a fantasy rather than reality. Exhausted and self-aware, they resolve to break from those hollow expectations and find their own direction before the pretending destroys them entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~futures":{"haiku":"The song urges humanity to break destructive cycles before ecological collapse, emphasizing our shared responsibility for the warming atmosphere.","opus":"A plea disguised as a dance track: the singer begs for a future while admitting humanity is squandering it, hearts gone cold as the atmosphere warms, control slipping through collective fingers. The imagery keeps circling the sun, hope stubbornly held against a running clock. It reads as climate dread rendered as synth-driven electronic anthem, the pop hook softening an argument that we alone are to blame.","sonnet":"A plea aimed at a civilization drifting toward its own destruction — hearts grown cold as the atmosphere warms, a slow unraveling humans can see but won't stop. The speaker orbits the crisis with desperate hope, demanding a future worth living in, knowing the cycle will break them if it isn't broken first.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~give-into":{"haiku":"Isolation and internal psychological pressure mount until a person surrenders to destructive habits that numb pain; the song charts the cycle where resurrecting one's humanity remains impossible against the pull of self-harm.","opus":"Isolation and mounting pressure drive a plea to resurrect the human buried inside a mind turning against itself. The synthwave project frames capitulation as slow drowning, one voice weighing another down while the habit of misery grows old. Redemption is offered only as effort, making it right against pollution of thought and the fun that stopped being funny.","sonnet":"A toxic dependency hollows a person out, cycling between capitulation to pressure and the desperate recognition that they are losing their mind. Near the end a second voice drowns alongside them — the relationship itself becomes the undertow pulling both parties under.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~icon":{"haiku":"Seeks an icon or symbol to fill inner emptiness and provide meaning, grasping for faith in something transcendent.","opus":"An electronic-metal plea to something outside the self to fill an inner emptiness and quiet a mind that won't stop. The icon is any object of faith seized on by someone who feels they belong nowhere, hoping belief itself might anchor them.","sonnet":"The narrator, isolated and lacking belief in anything, reaches toward an idealised figure — an icon — to fill an inner emptiness, heal his doubts, and anchor him beyond his own disbelief.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song addresses its idol the way a supplicant addresses a deity — flood the hollow within, be the dawn breaking through night, be grounds for trusting the world again — asking one figure to mend the flaws, moor a drifting self, and silence a mind that chatters without end. But the bridge exposes the con under the prayer: a soured trust toward everything nearby, a vanished conviction, a tumble out of grace, and the exhaustion of standing under a sky that stays dry. So the idol is a stand-in built to patch the crater where belief used to sit, and the pleading hook, which begs to be hauled past its own doubt, is a devotee imploring a thing he already suspects is hollow to carry him over the very chasm it was never equipped to bridge.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"idol erected to patch the crater where belief used to sit","built":"the bridge exposes the prayer as self-aware — the devotee already suspects the idol is hollow before the plea ends"},
"nutronic~infodemic":{"haiku":"Misinformation and hate spread like wildfire through networked systems, weaponizing information while structures crumble; divided populations fall prey to chains of reaction that no single force can stop.","opus":"A protest anthem for the misinformation age, glowing palms and failing messages set against walls to be climbed and toppled. Nutronic likens hate to an arsonist torching a wilderness of information until it all burns down. The chant of breaking an endless cycle turns a modern plague of screens and lies into a collective act of defiance.","sonnet":"Hatred spreads through digital networks like wildfire, consuming the information ecosystem and turning communication into vectors of destruction. A collective anger reaches critical mass and the walls built by oppression begin to crack — but the chaos unleashed is indiscriminate, burning everything.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~looking-glass-original-mix":{"haiku":"Drowning in distance and distortion; the speaker views reality through broken perception, built on lies and loose ends, while struggling to swim back.","opus":"An electronic piece built on the sensation of being trapped underwater, gazing up at an unreachable surface, cut off by deceptions he built on unstable ground and by emotions he keeps fleeing. Beneath the soothing reassurance that he'll return runs the truth of a man wrecked by the fate he dreaded and the things he left unfinished.","sonnet":"About being submerged in your own emotional failures — lies built on quicksand, distance from feeling — but the narrator promises a partner they're coming home, and the metaphor shifts from drowning to swimming up toward the surface.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~luna-original-mix":{"haiku":"The speaker yearns for an idealized 'Luna' figure to bring peace, light, and spiritual healing—transforming them from a ghost in cold darkness into a being bathed in transformative presence.","opus":"A supplication to the moon, asking it to carry off hardship, refashion the spirit, and pour lent radiance into the chest. Nutronic's synthwave gives the appeal a night-time glimmer, the singer roaming the trees like a phantom emerging from the chill into a glowing presence. With the shadows lifted, the cherished glow becomes both his sight and his rescue.","sonnet":"A person adrift in darkness appeals to a luminous presence — part celestial body, part beloved — to fill the hollow where fear lived. When that presence arrives, the cold woods fall away and the ghost-like wanderer steps into warmth and the relief of being seen.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~moth-wings":{"haiku":"Breaking free from deception and gaslighting to become what was fought for; the speaker sheds false truths and transforms through fire.","opus":"A song about breaking free of manipulators and vowing never to revisit an abusive past, urging the listener to become the freedom they've been chasing, cast off the deceptions, radiate light, and drift upward toward the cosmos. Nutronic use the moth-to-flame image as a symbol of transformation and refusal to look back.","sonnet":"About escaping a gaslighting relationship and transforming rather than returning — the narrator urges breaking free from the people who keep you near the flame, shedding lies, and becoming the thing you've been fighting toward.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~reconnect":{"haiku":"A person struggles to break free from stagnation and emotional numbness, desperately trying to repair broken circuits and reconnect with feeling.","opus":"Nutronic frame emotional deadness in the vocabulary of machinery, casting the self as a worn-out device running down with faulty wiring. The narrator missed his chance, won't change, and stays stuck in a rut he made, pleading to feel again. The final turn reveals that reconnecting really means letting go of whatever walled him off from someone else.","sonnet":"Stalled and numb, running on habit like a worn-out machine, he recognizes that refusing to change has left him stranded and misanthropic. The whole song is a plea — to something or someone — to shake off the inertia and feel alive again.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~run-away":{"haiku":"Two people escape their painful past together, chasing an unknown future and shedding loneliness through mutual commitment.","opus":"An invitation to flee the past together toward someplace they can't be found, chasing the future and igniting a soul rather than falling backward. Nutronic, a synthwave-leaning electronic act, wraps the escapist romance in propulsive retro-futurist pulse, the refrain reframing running away as an ending that doubles as a shared beginning.","sonnet":"Two people decide to shed the accumulated weight of their past together and sprint toward something unbuilt. The invitation is mutual and urgent — not escape into nothing, but a deliberate turn toward a future where the shared fire of beginning again outpaces whatever was left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~somniac":{"haiku":"A sleepwalker denies reality and refuses to see their own shadow and misery. The song urges waking up despite fragility built on unstable ground.","opus":"A synthwave confrontation with escapism, addressing someone who sleeps to flee a shadow-self they refuse to face; the plea is to wake before the flimsy world built on denial collapses, since the shadow never actually goes away.","sonnet":"An intervention directed at someone using sleep and fantasy as a way to avoid a painful reality, warning them that the shadow of their misery doesn't disappear just because they refuse to look at it.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~sun-original-mix":{"haiku":"Two beings embrace and fall into annihilating heat, their hearts continuing to beat until they dissolve to dust, transforming misery into rapture within cosmic emptiness.","opus":"A song that turns cosmic annihilation into an act of intimacy, two lovers plunging together toward a star, their misery burning off as they embrace their end. Nutronic frames the fatal descent as consummation rather than tragedy, the repeated plea for closeness making destruction feel like union.","sonnet":"Two people choose a transcendent plunge together — falling into something as consuming and annihilating as a star. The misery burns off in the heat of shared surrender, and even dissolution at cosmic scale feels preferable to surviving separately.","src":"haiku"},
"nutronic~the-storm-original-mix":{"haiku":"A speaker drifts separated from someone they love, held back by stormy seas and tides, waiting passively for calm weather to return and restore connection.","opus":"A synthwave portrait of separation cast as being stranded at sea, the narrator stuck and drifting, waiting out bad weather for the sunlight that alone can carry him back to someone he loves. Nutronic keeps the pulse slow and oceanic, letting the sense of helpless waiting carry the longing.","sonnet":"A person cut adrift by currents beyond their control waits passively for the storm to clear so they can navigate back to someone they love. Patience is the only available action — all motion has been taken from them, and reunion depends entirely on conditions they cannot command.","src":"haiku"},
"nvm~two-tone-marionette":{"haiku":"Puppet existence persists despite awareness; the speaker burns bridges while accepting his role as manipulated string-dancer.","opus":"An addict confesses to being someone's manipulated puppet, torn between guilt over the lies he keeps and a plea for the other person to cut his strings. The tracked arms and self-loathing mark a relationship built on shared damage.","sonnet":"The speaker is a puppet on someone else's string — a two-tone marionette in the window — haunted by guilt, self-harm imagery, and the fear that they deserved all of it; they burn until they become no memory of the other person's, unable to sleep for the weight of lies they still keep.","src":"haiku"},
"nvrless~no-time-to-die":{"haiku":"Betrayal hardens into cold refusal; the speaker accepts that the beloved was illusion and there's no time left for grief or regret.","opus":"A bitter reckoning with betrayal, reframing a broken relationship as a debt of blood owed. The narrator realizes he loved a lie and was never truly supported, refusing now to grieve or weep. Steeling himself, he lets the past burn away, treating the abandonment as one more lesson learned.","sonnet":"Recovering from a deceptive relationship — 'you were never on my side' — the speaker refuses to perform grief or be destroyed: 'there's just no time to die' is both self-protection and pride, the fool-me-twice moment catalysing a cold departure rather than collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"nxcre~indigo":{"haiku":"Isolation and distance fragment the self as silence consumes; the speaker searches for escape while knowing they've lost their way home.","opus":"A tentative romance where one partner blames circumstance and distance for the doubt, while the other pushes to seize the moment and escape together. Losing one's way becomes bearable only with the promise of a shared exit.","sonnet":"A relationship on the verge of separation, where silence consumes hearts in the dark and the speaker urges that the time to leave together is now, pleading 'wait for me, I'll go away with you' as both an escape plan and a last bid to go somewhere far together.","src":"haiku"},
"o-brother~complicated-end-times":{"haiku":"The narrator needs time to realign their ego and reignite themselves against critics trying to erase their name. They believe they will become the paradigm once they break free.","opus":"Atlanta's O'Brother turn atmospheric post-hardcore into a defense of one's own tempo and value against snap judgment. The narrator refuses to be graded by outward exertion, resists those lining up to blot him out, and asks only for room to breathe and reconcile pride with self. Quietly stubborn, he vows to flare back up and set the standard rather than be extinguished.","sonnet":"A speaker pushes back against those who would measure, erase, or extinguish his presence, claiming the right to pause, recalibrate, and re-emerge on his own terms. The resistance is less confrontational than self-protective — what he needs is breathing room to reconnect with himself before proving anything to anyone.","src":"haiku"},
"o-r-k~as-i-leave":{"haiku":"The speaker fades into absence, watched but not saved; sorrow bleeds through a departing request to stop them or let them vanish entirely.","opus":"A haunting meditation on fading, surrender, and the wish to disappear. The narrator walks out into dimming light, bleeding and half-pleading to be saved yet half-longing to vanish, questioning whether the other's dreams have aged and died. The progressive act suspends him between rescue and dissolution, following where he is led toward absence.","sonnet":"A speaker on the edge of disappearance asks to simply be watched as they bleed and fade, caught between a plea to be saved and permission to vanish — 'save me now or let me disappear' — while accusing the listener of letting their own dreams grow old.","src":"haiku"},
"oakman~clear-enough":{"opus":"A couple realizing their relationship has run out and resolving to escape their shared past together, framing flight from the wreckage as the only way to save what's left and to grow from their mistakes.","sonnet":"Two people stuck in a relationship that has run its course reckon with the obvious truth that they need to escape their shared past and the damage they've done, rallying together to fight for whatever remains while time allows.","src":"sonnet"},
"oasis~wonderwall":{"haiku":"Romantic longing imagines a savior in the beloved, but the speaker doubts their own worth and the other's reciprocal devotion.","opus":"Not really a love song so much as a battered hope pinned on another person to be the one who saves you. Gallagher's plainspoken longing lands because it's ambivalent, unsure the feeling is even shared, yet clinging to the possibility anyway.","sonnet":"A person addresses someone they are intensely drawn to but cannot fully reach, tangled in things they cannot say and roads that keep winding, placing all their hope for rescue and salvation on this single person who has become everything to them.","src":"haiku"},
"oathbreaker~ease-me":{"opus":"A woman drifting toward oblivion, dreaming of surrendering to water to ease her pain, uprooting herself in search of a fresh start, and pleading not to be sent back home.","sonnet":"A song about leaving a painful situation in search of personal transformation — the narrator uproots themselves and replants elsewhere, hoping distance will ease the pain, but the act of transplanting brings unexpected grief alongside hard-won growth.","src":"sonnet"},
"obsidian-kingdom~the-pump":{"opus":"A dark, ritualistic screed against relentless consumption, personifying an insatiable pump that grinds through lives and pain forever. It dismantles the notion of the body as sacred, calling it mere fuel, then twists submission into power, insisting one rises by kneeling and conquers by surrendering. Nihilism and paradox drive its grim liturgy.","sonnet":"A nihilistic meditation on the body as meat fed to a grinding system — the song declares the body is not sacred but simply fuel for an indifferent machine, and then paradoxically counsels surrender to that machine as the only form of power available: rise by kneeling, conquer by giving up.","src":"sonnet"},
"obsydians~ascension":{"haiku":"Rising above hardship through mutual spiritual exchange and connection. The speaker offers presence and light while asking for reciprocal soul commitment.","opus":"Built on questions that circle whether any of this will finally matter, the piece sways in an in-between where prayer feels borrowed against a losing war. Ascension arrives less as triumph than as permission to keep playing. The bargain it strikes is mutual and quiet: soul offered for wakefulness, presence traded for love, one voice promising simply to listen and rise.","sonnet":"A figure sways between uncertainty and invitation, called upward by a force that offers belonging in exchange for devotion. Rising above hardship feels possible only when that bond is mutual — love offered, soul given in return. Ascension is both spiritual permission and a yearning for earned, reciprocal presence.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~167-damillia":{"haiku":"The speaker surrenders to rhythm and passion, asking for consumption and freedom from internal constraints.","opus":"A surrender to appetite and sensation, the body overriding restraint at a specific remembered address. It's about needing passion so badly you'd rather be consumed than stay numbly, safely yourself.","sonnet":"A song about the physical and emotional overwhelm of desire or connection — at an address or moment (167 Damillia), the narrator is hit by something transformative: pupils dilating, tremors, a frenzy he can't resist. He needs passion the way he needs oxygen, and giving in to it is the only way he can live.","src":"haiku","fable":"A speaker who wishes to be anything other than himself finds release only when an unfamiliar presence steps in and overtakes his body, pupils widening as a shudder of surrender rolls through him. The plea is to be touched, emptied out, allowed to loose the wildness he can no longer keep locked inside. His refusal to smother the strangeness within reads as both a craving for intensity and a confession that living tamely feels like not living at all. Underneath the ecstatic urge to rise and move is a quieter dread: seated and oblivious at the threshold, he senses that meaning is slipping and grabs at whatever conviction might hold him together.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"surrender to an overtaking presence as the only livable intensity","built":"ecstatic urge to rise undercut by the quieter dread of seated oblivion at the threshold"},
"ocean-grove~ask-for-the-anthem":{"haiku":"Sexual desire and seduction through music and movement, with repeated emphasis on asking for 'the Anthem' as a moment of connection and proof of attraction.","opus":"A charged come-on where sound and lust blur together, bodies swaying to the low end and a dare thrown at a partner to make the first request. At its core is a cocky sexual invitation tangled up with the pull of a song, all playful command and self-possession.","sonnet":"A charged, sexually kinetic appeal to a partner to ask for what they want — the song functions as heat between two people, the music itself as the erotic currency, asking and giving as reciprocal acts of desire.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears a hook-up as its whole vocabulary, but the object of desire is a track: every seduction beat doubles as a request to play a song, so that the come-on and the DJ's cue become one gesture, and the singer's proof of himself is never a touch but a needle drop. What sits underneath the swagger is a plea for validation-through-playback — spin it again, give a reply, and both parties come out better — with the recurring dread of forgetting exposing that the bravado runs on being asked, being cued, being confirmed by someone else. The bridge strips this to its skeleton, the demand repeated until the seduction is indistinguishable from a child's tantrum for approval. Then the outro drops the pose and free-associates a scrapbook of aspirational images colliding with a broke kid's reality — supermodel fantasies against a discount rail ticket and a transit card, north London reframed as a private battle arena — before the final aside gives the whole strut away: all the bragging resolves into a shy conditional, that he'll only ask if he truly wants it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["identity & becoming","love & desire","artifice & performance","money & the street"],"means":"validation sought through playback not through touch","built":"the strut resolves into a shy conditional at the final aside"},
"ocean-grove~b-l-u-d":{"haiku":"A speaker confronts a friend about self-destructive choices and numbness, urging them to stop wasting their life and face their inner demons.","opus":"A dialogue between a mentor and a self-destructing addict, the older voice pleading with someone numbing their brain to escape rather than face their choices, insisting it isn't too late to turn around. The throughline is that everyone carries an inner devil, and however much love is offered, our own demons usually win in the end.","sonnet":"A confrontational dialogue between someone trying to help and an addict who rationalizes substance use as coping rather than escape; the helper, having been through the same darkness, loses patience with the excuses and demands the other stop pretending.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages an intervention as a two-hander, alternating between someone begging a self-destructing friend to change and that friend's own rapped counter-testimony of feeling lost and behind. The would-be rescuer admits he recognizes the self-loathing firsthand and confesses the limits of his reach, that one person cannot drag another out of a private wreck. Underneath the tough-love scolding runs a shared conviction that everyone carries something ruinous within, so the plea to a friend doubles as a warning the speaker aims at himself. The closing lines turn the whole thing into a question with no answer, asking whether the ruin was fated or chosen, and leaving the reached-for hand still hanging in the air.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"a plea aimed at a friend that doubles as a warning at the self","built":"the closing question leaves the reached-for hand still hanging with no answer"},
"ocean-grove~baby-cobra":{"haiku":"The speaker finds a dangerous partner whose venom enters their blood, exciting them more than others. Biting and tasting venom feels right as they hold this killer cobra close.","opus":"A predatory crush dressed as devotion, the object of desire imagined as a snake he wants to consume from the inside, all menace and ownership disguised as love. He keeps insisting he's different from every other suitor even as the metaphor stays fanged. Ocean Grove, the Melbourne crew who fused nu-metal groove with alt bounce, deliver it with a swagger that treats fixation as a hook, not a caution.","sonnet":"A dangerous, intoxicating attraction drawn in the language of predator and prey — the object of desire disarms completely, leaves something inside the speaker that feels like venom spreading through the blood. There's no resistance, no regret, just the reckless surrender to someone who paralyzes on contact.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~backbone":{"haiku":"Someone silenced and unheard repeatedly tries to speak but is ignored and dismissed, trapped in a cycle of futile attempts to communicate before being abandoned.","opus":"A confrontation with a self-styled tough guy who hasn't done the real work (no dirt under his fingernails), voiced by someone sick of biting his tongue and being ignored; the throughline is frustration at a poser and at not being heard.","sonnet":"A very short track about being silenced and dismissed — someone who bites their tongue, watches people leave, and can never get anyone to actually listen to them, while a voice challenges someone who claims authority without having earned it through real work.","src":"haiku","fable":"A track that names powerlessness as its subject: the singer skewers a man who anoints himself the backbone yet has never done the grimy hands-on work, then turns inward to a paralysis of swallowed words, sitting still while someone walks away. The looped return to biting the tongue and going unheard enacts the very stuckness it describes, the shrugging same-old tag turning a personal grievance into a wearily familiar loop.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"looped return to swallowed words enacting the stuckness it describes","built":"the shrugging same-old tag turns a personal grievance into a wearily familiar loop"},
"ocean-grove~beers":{"haiku":"A good thing deteriorated into madness and sadness; the speaker finds no redemption left and watches the other person consume greedily.","opus":"The grief of watching a great relationship or friendship descend into madness, where searching himself for words yields only sadness. The other is branded a turncoat, mutant and scrounger who pushes and pulls then abandons him, a greedy taker who'd take everything and everyone.","sonnet":"A lament about something good — a relationship or a friendship — that has rotted into madness and sadness; the speaker can find no redeeming value left, and accuses the other of being a taker who would drain everyone around them.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title Beers sits over a lyric with no drinking in it, which is the read: the mask is the casual, blokey word, and underneath is a friendship or partnership curdling into betrayal and grief. The song moves by naming — turncoat, mutant, scrounger — each escalation a harsher verdict on someone who pushes and pulls and takes everything, while the singer's own line stays stuck on being unable to find anything else redeemable in it, repeated flat four times like a door that won't open. That the writing itself keeps reaching inside for words and coming back only with sadness makes the refrain double as a confession about the song: what began as something great has sunk past articulation into something that can only be repeated, not resolved.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["friendship & loyalty","anger & defiance","death & grief"],"means":"betrayal that outlasts every word available for it","built":"the title is the mask — a blokey word sitting over a lyric with no drinking in it"},
"ocean-grove~bored":{"haiku":"Nothing changes and nothing sticks, the speaker drifts through repetition too depleted to care, searching for anything to break the pattern.","opus":"Depressive numbness worn as a shrug, bored out of one's brain and telling everyone not to waste their time. Behind the flat la-las is isolation, clasping no one's hand and losing the self, a plea disguised as indifference that nothing ever changes.","sonnet":"A desperate, nihilistic dissatisfaction — the speaker is so profoundly bored and directionless that they're almost hoping something dangerous comes, knowing nothing changes and asking others not to waste effort on them, holding hands with nobody in the dark.","src":"haiku","fable":"The chorus keeps announcing exhaustion and stalled motion, but the verses seep hurt beneath that flat pose, so the deadness is really a costume worn over depression that cannot admit itself out loud. Between those verses sits a bitter glance at other people, the sleepwalkers who never switch themselves alive, the recycled disgrace flung across each new morning, until the bridge finally sheds the act. Its confession lands where the collapse actually lives, in the refusal to rise or leave a darkened room, and the wordless hummed refrain, empty where a chorus should soar, gives that hollowness a tune.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"deadness as a costume worn over depression that can't speak","built":"a wordless hummed refrain sits where a soaring chorus should be"},
"ocean-grove~bustin":{"haiku":"Restless energy and internal pressure demand release, the speaker channels chaos into rhythm, riding the wave of something surging from within.","opus":"A jittery portrait of compulsion, someone unable to stay put because an urge keeps surging up inside and demanding to be fed. A handful of obligations get name-checked as the things holding him back, but the craving overrides them, framing the itch as a hit he's desperate to chase.","sonnet":"A compulsive, restless craving for intensity — the speaker is always searching for a hit of something that makes them feel, overanalyzing everything, unable to sit still, driven by five unnamed reasons to keep moving and chasing the sensation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rush addiction song that keeps its true subject masked by the language of a fix. The verses stage overthinking and restlessness against a pull that never resolves, an endless on-and-on the singer can't outrun, while the handful of small reasons hints at people or obligations that ought to hold him in place but instead read as the very things he's fleeing. The hook collapses craving, performance, and self-medication into one word, so the feeling being chased is deliberately unnamed — the drug, the audience's want, and the drive to feel anything at all become interchangeable. What sits underneath the swagger of denying he's the enemy is a confession that the compulsion is coming from within, not from any outside temptation, and the near-identical repeated verses enact the loop itself: the same reach performed again with no arrival.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"compulsion sworn to come from within not outside","built":"near-identical repeated verses enact the loop they describe"},
"ocean-grove~cali-sun":{"haiku":"Addiction, depression, and melancholy romance in California culture.","opus":"Feeling sick, sad and drugged amid the glamorous emptiness of California, chasing love and escape while numbing on pills.","sonnet":"Disillusionment and emotional sickness beneath the glamorous surface of Los Angeles, numbed by drugs and chasing a distant love.","src":"haiku","fable":"Ocean Grove treat the golden-state dream the way envious outsiders always do, as a paradise that sours the instant you're standing in it. The hook confesses illness and gloom exactly where the postcard swore warmth and affection would live. The verses pile up the fantasy's chemical props only to insist the buzz endures once every crutch is gone, which lands less like a party than like a deadness that no longer requires supply. Holding it together is a stubborn vow to reach someone who waits, so the closing flight west becomes the real quarry, a person the singer keeps steering toward while the dream around him keeps collapsing. The hurt gets buffered into a rueful shrug that at least a spare heart remains.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"steering toward a waiting person while the golden dream collapses","built":"the hook confesses illness exactly where the postcard promised warmth"},
"ocean-grove~cell-division":{"src":"web","web":"A battle cry against self-loathing and stagnation — wanting to surgically remove the part of yourself you hate ('take out the part of me I hate') and break out, transformation framed as almost biological.","fable":"A song about the wish to amputate the part of yourself you can't tolerate — self-partition as a getaway, wrapped in the frantic genre-splicing Ocean Grove built their ODDWORLD around. The narrator wants to cut the loathed piece loose and burst free of it, but the impulse comes armored rather than gentle. Good aims are paired with no letup, a quest cast as a dare against death, all funneled through the band's mid-2020s crossbreed of nu-metal strut and pop-leaning hooks. The chorus's rehearsed shrug — unbothered by whether anyone's fabulous, certain no one can talk him around, forever out chasing the high — plays as a shell over the buried appeal underneath. And the voice descending from overhead that promises to convert him meets the same twitchy insistence to rise up and claim a piece of him for the night, as though faith and self-ruin opened onto the identical room.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"self-partition framed as escape but armored as defiance","built":"the overhead voice promising conversion meets the same twitchy refusal as self-ruin, collapsing both into one room"},
"ocean-grove~cold-skin":{"haiku":"Childhood trauma—strange behaviors, numbness via medication, social isolation—haunts an adult confronting someone who may have caused their damage, demanding acknowledgment and confession.","opus":"A haunting confrontation with an abuser named Julian, the damaged narrator, numbed since childhood by Ritalin and estrangement, circling back to demand answers before revenge, asking whether the man can even sleep at night.","sonnet":"A confrontation addressed to a man named Julian — someone who caused childhood harm, possibly abuse — narrated by a person raised on Ritalin for erratic behavior that now appears to be a trauma response, stalking Julian's routine and threatening reckoning before ending it all.","src":"haiku","fable":"A stalker's monologue that keeps its worst word for last: the speaker creeps into Julian's kitchen with the polite cadence of a visitor asking after an old acquaintance. The interrogation's fixation on whether Julian has a son quietly reveals that this reunion is a reckoning for childhood abuse, the small-talk mask peeled back to expose the fixated menace beneath it. The middle section drops the act entirely into first-person diagnosis — a childhood of Ritalin, misfit labels, and behaviours that never settled. The two halves of the image-system — the intruder's cold surveillance and the victim's medicated erratic history — resolve into a single person circling the source of his damage. Structure carries the meaning: the ritual repetition of the son question isn't stalling but the compulsion itself made audible. The closing turn from ending it all to never forgetting the steps, the name, those hands admits that vengeance won't erase the sensory memory it feeds on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["violence & murder","shame & repression","surveillance & control"],"means":"small-talk mask peeled back to expose a reckoning for childhood abuse","built":"the ritual repetition of the son question makes the compulsion itself audible"},
"ocean-grove~diploid":{"haiku":"Someone hypnotized and uninspired watches memories fade but accepts inevitable change. The song warns that transformation requires embodying the change you seek.","opus":"A bittersweet look back at a faded relationship, growing pains and a chance street encounter reopening old memories. It lands on reluctant grace, the recognition that change has to start with you rather than blame.","sonnet":"A brief song about coming out of numbness after a painful relationship — bumping into a memory on the street, feeling growth pains, and landing on the simple conviction that change requires you to become it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that keeps insisting things will be fine while quietly admitting they aren't: the speaker pushes someone away as too much to bear, only to be ambushed by tender memory when they cross a street. It lands on the resigned self-help mantra that wanting change means becoming it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["identity & becoming","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"pushing someone away then ambushed by tender memory at a street crossing","built":"the resigned self-help mantra lands as the only movement the song allows itself"},
"ocean-grove~dream":{"haiku":"Reflecting on the brevity of time and mortality, the speaker wants to live freely before time runs out.","opus":"A carpe-diem reverie about time slipping away and a wish to do whatever one wants, sail on, and not leave someone behind, holding one's head to the sky with only today to work with. Ocean Grove, the Melbourne alt-metal crew known for genre-blurring, keep it loose and sunlit, trading heaviness for a wistful hope of seeing a loved one around and spending seconds rather than hoarding them.","sonnet":"Time slipping away and the determination to stay present anyway — not to sell seconds for money, not to regret what could have been, just to hold your head up and feel what is settling: that there is only today. The parting note is gentle rather than urgent, a wish to see someone around rather than a demand that anything be different.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~enigma":{"haiku":"A person battles an internal presence or alternate self for control of their mind and identity. They oscillate between resignation and resistance, seeking freedom from a psychic entity that feeds on their turmoil.","opus":"A song about a divided psyche, two versions of one man locked in perpetual combat for command of his own mind, one dominating the other. Ocean Grove push their alt-metal into internal horror here, the plea being to reach some inner realm where his thoughts finally belong to him and he can start over unbound.","sonnet":"A mind at war with itself, split between the person it has become and the one it still wants to be — wandering a wasteland of its own making, enslaved to thoughts it can no longer silence. The only exit is some half-imagined twilight where thought finally belongs to the self alone.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~flava":{"haiku":"Outcasts and weirdos deserve celebration; the marginalized and dark create authentic beauty against systems designed to erase them.","opus":"A generous, anti-cool manifesto welcoming the outcasts, freaks, and scarred to the front—judging people by their spark, not their faults. It rejects pop-star ego and monopolized dogmas in favor of belonging and heart as the supreme color.","sonnet":"A communal, inclusive manifesto celebrating outsiders, misfits, and people on the margins of mainstream culture — the speaker disavows pop-star ambition and jock culture in favor of a scene that embraces freaks, goths, people with scars, and those who reject the monopolized values sold to a blind world.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rejection-of-stardom anthem that turns a scene's leftovers into its saints: the hook shrugs off every celebrity template — rock, jock, pop — and hands the crown to a partner instead, keeping only the lifer's vow to do this forever. The verses run a proud census of the odd and the scarred, the goths and the slow swingers, everyone told they belong at the barricade rather than the back, judged by their spark and never their damage. Then the chorus lifts off into weightless devotion, promising to fold a listener into the singer's chest and take flight as if the ground had never held either of them before.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","love & desire","freedom & escape"],"means":"handing the crown to the outliers the scene kept at the barricade","built":"the rejection-of-stardom hook lifts into weightless devotion"},
"ocean-grove~fly-away":{"src":"web","web":"A self-assured reclamation of identity — the band asserting their sound after years of genre-bending, the hook 'fly away' less an escape than a defiant launch.","fable":"A song about being genuinely understood versus merely being clocked from the outside — a reckoning pointed at someone who assumed they had the narrator pegged while grasping nothing of him. Ocean Grove sheathe it in their mid-2020s excess, all reclaiming chants and rocketing-skyward imagery, but the wound lives in the repeated correction that flips recognition into indictment: he sees straight through the addressee, who never once managed the reverse. The verses run on the swagger of a band that has shut down back-to-back nights in Prague and come home altered, teasing a hidden face nobody's glimpsed and frustration seeping out in bleeding color, while the fly-away hook goes ambivalent — half release, half caution that anyone in your orbit eventually peels off too. The closing turn, that you only prize a thing once it has vanished, lands the whole track as a challenge to snap awake before the disappearance is already complete.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"recognition reversed — seeing through the one who never saw back","built":"the fly-away hook splits into release and warning, the ambivalence landing the track's challenge"},
"ocean-grove~freaks":{"haiku":"Eyes fixed apart from the head; torches and evening darkness obscure meaning while the speaker refuses the freak label through knowledge of its definition.","opus":"Melbourne genre-benders Ocean Grove build a dissociative riddle: a narrator insisting he isn't strange because he grasps what strangeness is, his gaze detached from his own movements amid dusk-and-dread imagery. The art-rock oddity keeps its meaning purposefully slippery, unease simmering just beneath the surface.","sonnet":"Sensing a fundamental difference in the way perception lands — eyes that do not move with the world — a person nevertheless refuses the label of freak. The strange internal wiring is real, but so is the awareness of what the word means and the refusal to be defined by those who use it casually.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~from-dalight":{"haiku":"Pieces of the speaker fragment and fall into darkness; trauma and lies have left them hollow and struggling to find authentic identity.","opus":"A fractured, image-crammed self-inventory in which parts of the narrator drop away and a deadened chill starts to feel comfortable. The Australian genre-benders suit the scattered catalog of memory, circling a hard-earned recognition that his forced composure has become a weight he hauls around among baubles that only throw back the light.","sonnet":"A fragmented consciousness catalogues the psychic debris of a damaged upbringing — numbness mistaken for comfort, instilled harm, glass-filtered awareness. The speaker stands on a pedestal of coping mechanisms while pieces of self drift further from any coherent light.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~glass-gloss":{"haiku":"Assertion of individuality against mass consumption and commodification, resisting being reduced to another weapon of commercial appeal.","opus":"A restless narrator, disillusioned with the grind and pressure to conform and consume, questions the cost of progress and nearly selling his soul. He insists on being seen as real, not a marketing product, and dreams of blasting away from it all while asserting who he truly is.","sonnet":"Frustration with slow progress and the pressure to conform to mass appeal leads the narrator to assert his own reality and map his own exit, finding life and purpose in cycles of experience rather than in what others demand of him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A voice fights to stay a person rather than a marketable object, cataloguing the small degradations of the grind: exhaustion, near-misses, the slow trade of the self for nothing. Underneath the swagger runs a plea for reciprocity and reality, insisting the speaker knows the exit even while circling the same repeating loop. The bravado is the mask; beneath it sits a bargaining with mortality, a wish to be remembered for who he is rather than an old assumption. The final ascent, drifting far from everything on a burst of speed, reads less like triumph than a deliberate switching-off, comfort found only in imagining the body's eventual release.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"A non-album single from December 2018 — released the same month Luke Holmes departed, the last drop before the mic passed to Dale Tanner.","themes":["identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"bravado as the mask over a bargain with mortality and the wish to be remembered","built":"the final ascent reads less like triumph than a deliberate switching-off"},
"ocean-grove~guys-from-the-gord":{"haiku":"Hedonistic street culture and bass-driven euphoria; the speaker moves through urban scenes seeking dopamine and escape within a tight social orbit.","opus":"A nu-metal snapshot of party excess and creeping paranoia, tattoos and drunken nights, where the whole point of existence shrinks to a pulse and a groove. Under the bravado sits an emptiness, burnt-out kids chasing a chemical high as the beat drops and the body gives out.","sonnet":"A high-energy celebration of music as physical experience and community identity — the bassline takes over the body, the crew represents, and all that matters is the two heartbeats and the bassline at the center of everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages hedonism as both salvation and asphyxiation: the verses run on the economics of a scene — cheap beer, slashed tires over a stashed haul, cash and raised hands — while the singer reframes chasing the crowd as a fresh calling at a country's very center, half-devout and half-desperate. Its whole argument compresses into one wish, that a lifetime needs only two pulses and a low frequency, but that reduction curdles at the chorus, where the same bass he lives for drops his body and steals his breath, repeated until deliverance and drowning become indistinguishable. The bridge then swaps shaking the skyline for shaping it — a small verb-flip that turns passive reveler into deliberate maker — before the wish itself jams, the groove stuttering into pure repetition, so the track enacts the addiction it describes: a rhythm that saves you by taking you under.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["party & hedonism","addiction & self-destruction","money & the street"],"means":"deliverance and drowning made indistinguishable by the same bass drop","built":"a single verb flip from shaking the skyline to shaping it turns reveler into maker"},
"ocean-grove~hitachi":{"haiku":"Domestic machinery and sensuality collide—ceiling fans, washing machines, tumbling white cloth—creating a mood of intimate solitude.","opus":"Ocean Grove spin a woozy, half-ironic daydream of household coziness, humming appliances and tumbling laundry evoking a childlike contentment. A blunt hip-hop sample cuts through the sweetness with swagger, letting tenderness and attitude sit side by side in the same hazy reverie.","sonnet":"A short, oblique song about the particular comfort of domestic sounds — the ceiling fan, the vibrating window, a washing machine returning laundry to white — framing mundane sensory details as a private, boyish pleasure.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drowsy domestic hymn where sleep is offered by the appliances themselves, the fan's murmur and a Hitachi's hum standing in for a lullaby, so that surrendering to childhood comfort feels like a laundry-stain lifting until the cloth returns to its plain original colour. The taunting refrain that brackets this tenderness — the same line at the open and close — needles the listener for pretending not to recognize this ache to be washed clean and made a boy again.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["nostalgia & memory"],"means":"appliances standing in as a lullaby for the wish to be made clean again","built":"the taunting refrain brackets the tenderness at open and close"},
"ocean-grove~hmu":{"haiku":"Predatory seduction and raw physical hunger drive encounters; the speaker moves through others collecting sensation without emotional investment.","opus":"A predatory, taunting come-on to a lover who never reaches out, mixing hunger and menace with the frustration of being unable to get a moment with them.","sonnet":"About desire and the frustration of being ignored — the speaker wants physical and emotional access to someone who never reaches back, and the aggressive sexual invitation gradually deflates into the admission that the other person never hits them up.","src":"haiku","fable":"The invitation runs on hookup slang turned predatory, the come-on curdling into something that wants someone dropped across the floorboards and skin tasted like a threat rather than a flirtation. But the final movement pulls the mask off: the swaggering summons was always a fantasy, because the truth underneath is that the other person never reaches out at all, and the speaker can't get a single moment with them. What sounded like seduction reveals itself as the compensatory theater of someone left permanently unanswered, the repeated summons a wish shouted into silence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"predatory swagger revealed as compensation for never being answered","built":"the final movement strips the seduction to a wish shouted into silence"},
"ocean-grove~i-told-you-to-smile":{"haiku":"A person engages in self-destructive behavior and pain-seeking, oscillating between numbness and intensity while acknowledging they're trapped in harmful repetition.","opus":"A garish spiral of chemical numbing and sexual violence, the narrator professing love for the anesthetic, the liquor, and the hurt. Its confessional center is compulsion, sinking gladly to the bottom because it thrills him even with his grip on someone's throat, a clear-eyed addict who admits he'll do it despite knowing better.","sonnet":"A masochistic spiral of self-destruction framed as pleasure — the narrator crawls down a pecking order and finds something perversely satisfying in the degradation, cycling through pain, numbness, and the compulsion to repeat it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the mask of a hedonist reveling in self-degradation, someone who submits to abasement and courts pain as if pleasure lived only at the bottom. Underneath, that swagger keeps collapsing into a confession of numbness, where the hurt arrives on schedule yet the aliveness it promises never comes. The middle turns predatory and honest at once, admitting he warns you off and takes what he wants regardless, wasting your trust as though ruin were the only offering he has. By the closing litany of disjointed, ritualized words, the persona has emptied into pure mechanism, a repetition that grinds on without ever delivering the sensation it chases.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","violence & murder"],"means":"abasement chased as aliveness when the hurt arrives but the feeling never does","built":"the closing litany grinds into pure mechanism, repetition with no sensation delivered"},
"ocean-grove~intimate-alien":{"fable":"The narrator opens sunk in stupor and mental fog, begging a partner to make sense of things and lead him on some invented trip through a synthetic, computerized world. When a watcher fixes him with a frozen, unblinking gaze, he reframes the encounter as a species-scale reckoning, insisting little has shifted across vast stretches of time. The song's real anxiety surfaces in his self-interrogation, cycling through possibilities that he is a glitch, a diagram in someone else's head, or something breakable meant to give way. He finally strips back the barriers hemming him in and quietly claims awareness of whatever looms overhead, trading paralysis for a small, defiant certainty.","haiku":"Two beings attempt connection across difference and incomprehension, seeking to understand each other through a digital realm while grappling with their fundamental otherness.","opus":"A dazed, worn-out narrator reaches toward a mysterious other being, proposing they travel together through some virtual realm while doubting his own existence and makeup. He questions whether he's merely an error, a design, or a flimsy construct, and decides to tear down the barriers hemming him in.","sonnet":"A disoriented narrator tries to connect with an entity whose cold, alien gaze makes him question his own identity and purpose, wondering whether he is just a glitch in a program or something transcendent, while stripping away the barriers around himself in search of understanding.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["dissociation & numbness","madness & the mind","identity & becoming"],"means":"paralysis traded for a small defiant certainty about what looms overhead","built":"self-interrogation cycles through glitch, diagram, and breakable object before settling"},
"ocean-grove~junkie":{"opus":"A statement of identity from a band built on genre-blending, addressed to fans who wanted them to keep repeating themselves. The junkie is anyone hooked on a familiar sound, and the answer is refusal: they'll keep mutating and let the true believers headbang along.","sonnet":"A self-celebratory band manifesto about their distinctive, hard-hitting sound and loyal fanbase who bang their heads to it.","src":"sonnet","fable":"Ocean Grove points the same word at two targets: the fans addicted to the low-end drop, and themselves, hooked on the rush of remaking their own sound every record. The cyborg gag and the drilled command to bow your head stage nu-metal ritual as a self-aware joke, yet the hook slips into confession, pledging transformation not from boredom but for the pact between stage and pit. The claim underneath is that sameness kills, so devotion means chasing a group that keeps mutating and trusting the bass to land even once nothing else stays recognisable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","friendship & loyalty","artifice & performance"],"means":"devotion pledged to a group that keeps mutating to stay alive","built":"the dollar sign stages fan and band addiction as one word"},
"ocean-grove~last-dance":{"haiku":"Final connection approaches as the speaker prepares for ultimate separation.","opus":"A luminous seizing of one transcendent moment, where two people dance as if time itself slows under a sinking sun. Ocean Grove render fleeting connection as something almost sacred, the narrator vowing not to let go even knowing the instant cannot last, savoring the fire and sway of a shared present.","sonnet":"Two people holding a moment at the edge of its ending, fire lighting the way as the sun sinks under its own weight. The narrator calls out the other's name knowing they won't let go, the dance itself a stay of execution against a parting that feels dreamt-of but not wanted.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~lights-on-kind-of-lover":{"haiku":"A fragmented urban scene depicting casual sexual desire, longing, and relationship dysfunction. The speaker navigates unwelcoming spaces and negotiates intimacy with emotional hesitation.","opus":"A fragmented, edgy account of a tense, unwelcome encounter and a fraught relationship, laced with imagery of laundromats, drug use, and self-destruction, with the speaker insisting he won't be unnerved.","sonnet":"Fragmented snapshots of low-level urban drift — a laundromat, cheap vices, thin walls, a lover's warning not to bite too hard — held together by a narrator who feels unwelcome yet refuses to be rattled.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears a swaggering pose, its narrator planted against a wall daring someone to confront him, insisting he won't be rattled even where he feels unwelcome. Underneath that bravado is a portrait of stalled poverty and self-medication: a life stuck counting small coins at the laundromat, soothed by television dinners and a habit he keeps returning to before he loses himself entirely. The confession that he loves with the lights on turns the toughness inside out, admitting he needs to be seen even while others warn against his violence and thin-walled desperation. What starts as a taunt curdles into a plea, the repeated refrains circling like someone who can't stop rehearsing the same doomed defense of himself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","money & the street","shame & repression"],"means":"toughness turned inside out by the need to be seen with the lights on","built":"the taunt curdles into a plea as the refrains circle the same doomed self-defense"},
"ocean-grove~mr-centipede":{"haiku":"A heart grows heavy from collecting emotional weight and burden; the speaker yearns to liberate someone trapped on a shelf and show them life beyond confinement.","opus":"A surreal plea to be filled and taken somewhere else, the collected weight of birds standing for a burden too heavy to carry while trapped and shelved. Odd, tender desperation to be unmapped and freed from a stalled life.","sonnet":"Someone begging for their heart to be filled and their exhausting, constrained existence to be taken somewhere new — locked in a basement, collecting the weight of accumulated suffering, asking to be removed from the shelf life they're living.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears sainthood as a mask over captivity: the narrator's serene refrain of feeling alright sits flush against being penned in a basement, so the affirmation reads as the numbed self-report of someone hostaged inside their own head, and the mock-tender turn to a listener trapped up on a shelf reveals the caretaker and the captive as the same person. Mr. Centipede — a many-legged thing summoned to fill the heart — is the mind's compulsive machinery, and the recurring image of hoarding the heft of birds sets weightlessness against accumulation, the question of whether we are programmed for more curdling into the dread that there is no more, only the collecting. The plea to be spirited off the map, somewhere else entirely, is escape fantasy voiced as devotion, which is why the whole thing keeps circling back to the basement rather than out of it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["madness & the mind","freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"escape fantasy voiced as devotion that keeps circling back to the basement","built":"serene affirmation sits flush against captivity so sainthood reads as the numbed captive's self-report"},
"ocean-grove~my-disaster":{"haiku":"Catastrophic self-judgment consumes; the speaker assumes all blame while disaster echoes eternally through their identity.","opus":"The loop of a mind that keeps sabotaging itself, promising never again and breaking the vow every time. Self-blame worn like an identity, the disaster recognized as something authored from within.","sonnet":"A person caught in a loop of self-blame and mental breakdown, convinced that their failures and broken vows are entirely their own doing and that the chaos they're experiencing is inescapable — a disaster entirely of their own making.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses hold at arm's length a vision that never resolves into anything livable, a poisoned tomorrow too big for the skull to contain, and the pre-chorus asks a question about seizing control that the song answers only by repeating it, powerless. What develops through the structure is the collapse of that distance: by the final passage the singer has folded the blame inward, admitting a broken promise made and broken again, so the disaster stops being the world outside and becomes his own recurring self. The last chorus caving into parentheses is the key movement — the indictment doesn't stop, it just gets muted to an interior murmur, the voice conceding that the whole catastrophe was always a loop running inside his own head that he cannot switch off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression"],"means":"the disaster folds inward until the catastrophe is the speaker's own loop","built":"the final chorus caves into parentheses as the indictment becomes an interior murmur"},
"ocean-grove~neo":{"haiku":"Mutual destruction and blame where breaking one breaks the other. The cycle of pain and enmity between two people who drag each other down.","opus":"A cycle of mirrored pain between two people, where one's breaking, falling and screaming echoes in the other, who becomes 'everybody's enemy.' The narrator is trapped, unable to find a way out despite claiming to trust the other.","sonnet":"A mirrored back-and-forth between two voices — one breaking, one falling, one screaming — locked in shared pain that has made them both outcasts. The song captures a state of mutual suffering with no way out, the two perspectives echoing each other until indistinguishable.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song refuses narrative and lives almost entirely in a call-and-response grid where every first-person action forces a mirrored second-person one, so that the self can't move without conscripting someone else into its collapse, and the mask (the bracketed echo answering each line back) turns private pain into a shared verdict where the sufferer becomes everybody's enemy. What sits under that mask is the plea buried in the middle, a single admission of trust immediately swallowed by its own echo, which reframes the whole thing as betrayal metabolized into aggression. The structure enacts the trap it names: the verses keep insisting on an exit while the form loops the demand for one back on itself, ending not in escape but in the outro's stuck repetition, the way out invoked so many times it becomes the proof there isn't one.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","freedom & escape"],"means":"private pain metabolized into aggression shared by everyone in the grid","built":"the form loops the demand for an exit back until it proves there isn't one"},
"ocean-grove~no-offence-detected":{"haiku":"A dispatch scenario unfolds into absurdism; three musicians meet their strange fictional end.","opus":"A darkly comic skit staged as a police dispatch: officers find the three suspects' own bodies in the trunk bearing their names, a self-referential joke where the band's collective is both perpetrator and corpse. Absurdist branding wrapped in a crime-scene bit.","sonnet":"A surreal police radio skit in which an officer reports finding three suspects with body bags bearing the suspects' own names in their car trunk — the joke is that the band Oddworld Music has killed and replaced themselves, ending with the officer going down; it's an absurdist piece of self-mythologizing.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~noise":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects external control and the demands of others, refusing to be defined or manipulated by the noise of social expectation.","opus":"A dismissal of someone whose constant demands land only as meaningless background hum the singer is glad to be rid of. He undercuts his own bravado by warning that continuing to make music in this vein might make him vanish, a wink of self-awareness inside the kiss-off.","sonnet":"Someone fed up with the demands, expectations, and noise of a stifling relationship or environment asserts they can live without it — and threatens to disappear entirely if the other person keeps writing songs about this kind of hollow compliance.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears bratty kiss-off as its surface, but its real subject is the singer's own compliance: the swagger of not caring collapses the moment he admits that all he ever does is agree and perform for an approval he never wanted. The mantra of being able to live without it repeats past the point of conviction, so the repetition becomes the tell — a man reciting his freedom to convince himself he has it, the way you'd chant down a craving. Movement earns the read: the noise starts as everyone else's clamour, then curdles into the other person's static heard even underwater, and finally the only escape he can name is the songwriting itself, the threat that if he keeps making music like this he'll simply be gone — freedom relocated from the mouth to the act.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression","freedom & escape","artifice & performance"],"means":"freedom recited past conviction until the chant becomes the craving","built":"noise migrates from the crowd to the other person heard even underwater"},
"ocean-grove~overshadow":{"haiku":"Escape from illusions that created a false self; the speaker reclaims identity by shedding the ghost-like persona others imposed.","opus":"A survivor's declaration of endurance, the narrator swapping darkness for light and calling himself unbreakable after false selves and illusions once left him unrecognizable to himself. The distortions that once dragged him down give way to clarity and rebirth from a battered frame. Ocean Grove, the eclectic Australian band, pair this self-reclamation with their nu-metal-inflected sound, consigning the past to the eyes of those who wronged him.","sonnet":"Recovery from a period of darkness and lost identity — white lights and illusions once created a version of the speaker that wasn't real, pulling them down with phantom claws. Now clarity has returned, the broken body re-emerged, and the past belongs only to those who used it against them. Still alive, and newly invincible for knowing it.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~presence-of-mind":{"haiku":"Reclaiming clarity and purpose by rejecting meaninglessness; standing firm against hollow voices and finding reasons to fight.","opus":"Australian alt-metal band Ocean Grove drive a hard-won anthem of self-worth, burning the past to move forward and countering the claim that life is pointless with a defiant insistence on reasons to keep going. Standing for clarity while empty boasters clamor around him, the narrator turns adversity into triumph with the band's bright, genre-blurring energy.","sonnet":"Old journals burned, first step taken — the speaker has found their footing after being swamped by vanity, hopelessness, and paper tigers whose voices outran their convictions. The song is a declaration that life has a thousand reasons, that standing for something real is possible, and that harsh words from the wrong people fall on deaf ears once you have decided to fight for yourself.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~raindrop":{"haiku":"Isolation and self-harm become reflexive; the speaker drowns in their own sorrow while holding onto distant warmth as the only tether.","opus":"A study of grief on a loop, a figure standing by himself day after day, weeping into his own hands and going under again and again. Cutting through the isolation is a broken appeal for someone to hold him and stay, a single warm gesture against the endless crying.","sonnet":"A raw, sparse song about deep isolation and grief — the speaker is entirely alone, crying continually, and the image of catching raindrops from their own eyes recurs as a meditation on the body becoming indistinguishable from its own sorrow; there is comfort from elsewhere suggested but the dominant state is solitude.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages grief as a closed weather system: the only rain that falls is his own, so the crying and the drowning are self-generated and inescapable, a solitude that feeds itself. Against that isolation the second voice enters with an anniversary of over a decade, a plea to stay, a warm embrace tinted green — but movement is the point, because in the final chorus that consoling voice is broken apart and threaded word-by-word through the raindrop refrain, so the comfort arrives already fractured into the sobbing it was meant to answer. The stammered, unanswered what that closes each pass reads as the mask of someone offered tenderness who can no longer hear or receive it, the reaching-out and the flinching-away collapsed into the same syllable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"grief as a closed weather system that generates its own rain","built":"the consoling voice is fractured word-by-word into the sobbing it was meant to answer"},
"ocean-grove~reality-shift":{"haiku":"Fragmentation of the self and perception as external pressures and inner conflicts distort reality. The speaker sinks into lucid dreams and feels trapped by forces telling them where to go.","opus":"The Australian genre-blurring band leans into dissociative unease, staging a mind at war with the earth beneath it. Sleepless and crawling-skinned, the narrator sinks to lucid depths where foreign voices dictate direction and days curdle into the shape of nightmares. Each new dawn promises relief and delivers none, the world shifting endlessly while he stays fixed in the same dread.","sonnet":"A mind comes undone at the seam between sleep and waking, haunted by lucid depths and foreign voices that crowd out the self. Despite each new day arriving, no relief follows — the body crawls with dread, and the world reshapes itself while the person inside it stays stuck, unchanged.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~sense-again":{"haiku":"Reclaiming mental clarity and rejecting passive acceptance of labels and lies; finding authentic self beyond what society imposes.","opus":"A restless swipe at a generation drowning in labels and passivity, insisting the listener is both the disease and the cure. It doubles as a struggle to shed a false self and find genuine clarity.","sonnet":"A speaker reclaims clarity and self-awareness after losing themselves to conformity and outside pressure, rejecting labels and passive groupthink while admitting an internal struggle between wanting to destroy and wanting to simply be oneself.","src":"haiku","fable":"Coming to your senses ought to mean sobering up, yet the chorus keeps sliding from a single restored faculty into all of them at once, so the speaker reboots not into clear vision but into raw appetite, someone who could just as readily be shooter as victim, played off as a wink. The verses jeer at how a generation packages docility and sells it back to everyone as identity, reducing a person who becomes a mere brand to something already half-forgotten, which is why the sense he recovers reads less like insight than the composure to level the charge in both directions at once. When the bridge drops the sarcasm it confesses the mechanism beneath everything, that reaching for himself and wrecking himself fire off as one motion, that vanishing is his way of never hardening into somebody else. The final understanding surfaces not in language but in the outro's bare repeated vowels, meaning ground down until nothing survives but sound.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction","politics & society"],"means":"reaching for himself and wrecking himself fire off as one motion","built":"meaning ground down in the outro until only bare repeated vowels survive"},
"ocean-grove~sex-dope-gold":{"fable":"The chorus mantra names the three things the culture sells as salvation and treats them as one interchangeable commodity, and the verses admit the speaker has already burned through them, chasing overload through anyone and any way until everything he owned started to vanish. What holds the song together is the laugh: the refusal to read as pain even while the second verse quietly confesses the suffering underneath, so the recurring line functions as a mask, the harder-and-harder escalation the sound of an addiction dressed up as a joke. The bridge finally lets the addiction speak in the second person of a lover who is teased and let in, and the parenthetical undertow reverses direction mid-phrase, wavering between leaning back into that pull and refusing it, locating the whole track at the exact wavering instant before someone decides whether to walk away.","fableDeep":"The chant reduces desire to inventory — sex, dope, gold — and the only human response left in the song is laughter that keeps intensifying ('harder and harder again') until it stops signaling fun and starts signaling malfunction. The second verse supplies the worldview that makes the loop possible: when evolution is washed up, anarchy is the perfect play and even death's a conspiracy, appetite is the last thing left to believe in. The bridge is the one honest beat — 'lately I'm lonely when I let you in,' 'a little closer to turning you down' — quitting phrased as an asymptote, always approached, never reached.","haiku":"Cynicism about materialism and excess in modern shallow pursuit of pleasure.","opus":"The empty, self-destructive chase after sex, drugs and wealth, laughing through loneliness and suffering while craving ever more.","sonnet":"The hollow, compulsive pursuit of pleasure — sex, drugs, and money — that leaves the narrator laughing bitterly at its own emptiness.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"the joke as the only mask the addiction can afford","built":"the bridge lets the habit speak in a lover's second person"},
"ocean-grove~shimmer":{"haiku":"The speaker witnesses a loved one's hidden pain and past suffering, unable to fix or take away their accumulated hurt.","opus":"A tender address to someone carrying a hidden, softer center and old wounds, the narrator wanting to hold them, take away their pain, and insist they are stronger than they think. Ocean Grove soften their experimental edge here, watching a loved one shimmer and fall while feeling powerless, the reassurance repeated like a steadying hand.","sonnet":"Witnessing the hidden suffering of someone who radiates strength they do not believe they possess, a narrator is struck by helplessness and awe simultaneously. The image of her dancing under stage lights as a child gives way to an adult carrying wounds he only just learned existed — and all he can do is watch her shimmer and hold on.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~silence":{"src":"web","web":"The album's closing shrug of defiance — carefree, anti-system PMA taken to its blunt end: \"I don't give a shit and I don't really like you.\" Not nihilism but its inverse: refusing everyone else's noise to keep your own positive mental attitude intact — the silence you choose over the static.","fable":"A song about a debt paid to quiet — the isolation buried beneath the racket, and a band's flat refusal to perform on any terms but their own. Ocean Grove build it from the oddworld mythology they chant as their own scene’s name, ironic pop sheen fused to a nu-metal backbone, and the lyric keeps deflating its own bluster: the laughing so-fresh peacocking sits right beside the confession that strain spikes whenever solitude sets in, the din something you sit and wait to sink under. A bruised music-business undertone threads through — the two-year toll it cost them, the numb fools who'll one day be the ones left craving it — knotted into the borrowed line about holding out for the world to shift. What binds the whole thing is the plain motto the song circles back to, that they peak only when they act on their own wants, offered less as a brag than as the one solid footing beneath all the noise.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming"],"means":"bravado deflated mid-boast by the silence underneath the noise","built":"the plain motto the song circles back to offered not as brag but as the only solid footing"},
"ocean-grove~silver-lining":{"haiku":"Life passes and changes; the speaker seeks to convince another that loneliness and loss contain hidden gifts and reasons to persevere and remain connected.","opus":"Reassurance offered to someone stuck in a solitary stretch, insisting that no state is permanent and every setback carries them toward better ground. Shadowed by mortality and the dread of outliving a loved one, it recasts isolation as one chapter in a shared story, asking only to keep faith that they endure within each other.","sonnet":"A direct address to someone in crisis or isolation, urging them to see their solitude as a chapter with an end rather than a permanent state — the speaker pleads to be remembered as someone who stayed, and asks the other person to keep living rather than disappear.","src":"haiku","fable":"The comfort is a farewell in disguise: the second-person address, the instruction to sit down and take a page, all frame this as consolation for someone entering a chapter titled Alone — but the singer is the one leaving, and the silver lining he offers is really the story of his own absence, the name already written into a page he won't be present to read. Meaning develops by inversion across the verses: the first pass insists nothing is permanent as reassurance that more is still to come. The reprise turns the same line into a deathbed if — wondering whether the stars will still shine once the other is gone — so the pep-talk vocabulary of stepping stones and pleasures beneath a waterfall gets quietly re-scored as the things the survivor will have to find without him. The final movement drops the mask entirely into a plea not to be left merely believing, bargaining that enduring inside each other's keeping is the only forever available — the brightest counsel here spoken to whoever remains, by the one who won't.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["death & grief","love & desire"],"means":"pep-talk vocabulary re-scored as what the survivor must find alone","built":"the reprise turns the reassurance line into a deathbed conditional"},
"ocean-grove~sowhat1999":{"src":"web","web":"A tongue-in-cheek rejection of limits wrapped in late-'90s nostalgia and Beastie-Boys boom-bap — the 'so what?' chorus a shrug turned war-cry against anyone telling you what you can and can't be.","fable":"A song about the toll of a comeback and the shrug you strap on to endure it — defiance measured out in butting your skull into brick and shrugging off whatever it costs. Ocean Grove lean hard into their mid-2020s outsider bravado here, a trio of scrawny troublemakers spelling out their own initials and proclaiming a fresh Babylon turning-point, the whole thing riding the get-up chant that keeps yanking the track back upright. Under the swagger runs a chillier current: the recurring admission that no authentic thing survives, that chasing the glow from within the shadows drains everything genuine out of him. The years the return demanded, the vow that he'll never be cornered and lined up, all collapse into that closing shrug — not real apathy, but the worn-out armor of people who've decided the cost was worth it even while tallying what it hollowed out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"defiance worn as armor over what the comeback hollowed out","built":"the get-up chant keeps yanking the track upright against the recurring admission that nothing authentic survives"},
"ocean-grove~strange-talk":{"haiku":"Someone feels alienated and misunderstood, moving through life with toxic behavior while questioning their sanity and whether they can ever truly connect with another person.","opus":"An outcast's defiance from someone shoved to the margins his whole life, refusing to be anyone but himself. Running underneath is a poisonous lost love, still intoxicated by the memory of a person he can't shake, swearing he'd never rescue them yet unable to stop wondering what it would take to finally release them.","sonnet":"Being cast out as a deviant and learning to wear that exile as identity rather than wound — the narrator is still scarred by someone who brought them down and got under their skin, but insists on the distinctness that sets them apart from the crowd.","src":"haiku","fable":"The mask is defiance — the outcast who insists he was never woven from the common fabric, who runs on venom and claims contagious superiority over everyone destined to be outpaced. But the song keeps cracking at that mask: the boast of hiding nothing because the interior is already dead admits the swagger is emptiness, and the two-part structure repeatedly swaps the crowd for a single person the speaker still gets high on remembering and cannot forget. What sounds like a self-mythologizing anthem is really an addiction to one relationship dressed as invulnerability, the aggression aimed outward at the whole world collapsing into a private plea about why you always bring me down. The forgetting of one's own name lands as the truest line: the defiance has hollowed the self it was supposed to protect.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"outward aggression collapsing into a private plea about one relationship","built":"defiance hollows the self it was meant to protect until the speaker forgets his own name"},
"ocean-grove~stratosphere-love":{"haiku":"Sacrifice and prayer within intimate connection; a piece slowly dying and both partners praying through cold exchanges.","opus":"A hazy, sensual meditation on an intimacy that feels both like grace and like cold distance. It sits in that in-between, dying a little to reach someone while unsure whether they are truly present.","sonnet":"The speaker is slowly coming apart trying to hold a relationship together, feeling the other person's indifference like cold grace thrown at their feet, and asking whether the connection — physical and emotional — is still mutual.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages seduction as a kind of exposure test: the halves of the lyric that admit to fear and private prayer (a piece of the speaker dying just to endure, secrecy assumed to be mutual) keep colliding with the come-on register of the middle sections, where grace becomes something thrown cold at the feet and intimacy is charted across hips and the backs of necks. That grace-that-lands-cold image is the hinge — devotion offered but arriving as ice, so what looks like a love song keeps leaking dread underneath the swagger. The mantra of asking whether the other is with it now, whether they can see the pleasure, escalates from question into command to just say it, and the repetition stops being persuasion and starts sounding like coercion talking itself into consent. Read straight through, the mask is the confident lover and the thing underneath is a plea for company in something frightening — the bet that the other prayed just as hard is the whole ache of the track.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"devotion that lands cold underneath the come-on register","built":"the question of whether she's with it escalates from persuasion into command"},
"ocean-grove~stunner":{"haiku":"Irreplaceable attraction and chemical connection drive obsessive devotion toward someone magnetic and unattainable.","opus":"Giddy infatuation with a woman treated as irreplaceable, the chemical fix the singer keeps needing. It's less a portrait of her than of the rush of wanting her.","sonnet":"Pure infatuation with a magnetic woman — the narrator is completely undone by her, calling her irreplaceable and the one thing he believes in, unable to stop himself from shouting her name.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hard-charging fixation song built on repetition: the singer keeps circling back to the same declaration that this woman is peerless, as if saying it enough times makes the claim structural. The verses stay impressionistic, sketching an arrival rather than a story, with a lone image of skies clearing and her stepping into the open air. Beneath the swagger of the hook sits a plainer confession, that he needs to know how she feels and can't stomach any more delay. The song mistakes intensity for intimacy, which is exactly its point: adoration shouted loud enough to drown out the fact that he barely knows her yet.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["love & desire"],"means":"intensity mistaken for intimacy because the adoration drowns the distance","built":"declaration repeated as though accumulation alone could make it structural"},
"ocean-grove~sunny":{"haiku":"A depiction of depression masked by external optimism and addiction as coping. The speaker feels dead inside despite the sunny weather, stuck in cycles of intoxication and self-care failure.","opus":"A song about numbness and addiction under a deceptively bright surface, where the speaker feels stalled and doubtful, tries to get sober, and admits that nothing is enough to pull him up even as it looks sunny outside.","sonnet":"A portrait of stagnation and low-grade self-destruction — waking late, sinking into the carpet, craving a thrill — where the sunny world outside contrasts with an interior state too numb to climb out of.","src":"haiku","fable":"The bright day outside is the mask, and the song reads the gap between that surface and a body still up at 3 a.m., unable to come down; the sunniness is never his own state but a rebuke, a world that looks fine while he stalls, doubts, and chases a thrill just to feel alive. Ocean Grove build the irony into the form: the chorus keeps insisting on the sunshine while every clause pinned to it is a refusal or a wound, so the repetition itself becomes the trap — each return to that bright line lands on someone who cannot be fixed, will not be stopped, and is never quite gotten over. The sobering-up vow only sharpens it, a resolution repeated because it hasn't taken, undercut by a verse that keeps reaching for the next hit of aliveness. What lies underneath is the recognition that the fix never arrives: even the free sip isn't enough to carry him past it, so the loop closes with the addiction, not the recovery, having the last word.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the bright-day chorus as a rebuke the speaker can never inhabit","built":"the sobering-up vow repeated because it hasn't taken"},
"ocean-grove~superstar":{"haiku":"Sexual attraction and infatuation with a mysterious woman.","opus":"Infatuation and desire for a captivating woman who dazzles the narrator, mixing romance, ego and ecstatic escapism.","sonnet":"Infatuation with a captivating woman whose presence and movement take over the narrator's mind entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"Superstar wears a rockstar's swagger over what is plainly a confession. The narrator anoints himself the finest to ever live, then credits the woman who caught, schooled and grounded him, so the bragging quietly collapses into gratitude. Flip Phone Fantasy names the register: a deliberately analog, small-scale infatuation blown up to widescreen romance. The hook just circles his helplessness, unable to explain what she does to him and giving up the attempt, and that surrender is where the costume slips.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"rockstar swagger collapsing quietly into gratitude for being grounded","built":"the hook circles helplessness until surrender is the only costume left"},
"ocean-grove~the-wrong-way":{"haiku":"Someone born misaligned faces inward constantly, bearing permanent marks from their past while unable to escape their internal constraints.","opus":"Being born looking backward, eyes fixed on the past and unable to face forward, someone begging not to be taken off the wall like a fixture or picture. It reads as a meditation on being defined and pinned in place by others while old wounds, smoothed like river stones, keep fueling identity.","sonnet":"Someone who has always lived oriented backward — eyes on the past, unable to face forward — insists on that orientation as a fixed truth about themselves rather than a flaw, finding a kind of stubborn power in their own misdirection.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song works by holding one born-facing-backward posture in place while everything else tries to move it: the speaker is fixed to a wall like a picture, insisting he not be taken down, and the plea itself becomes the argument for staying. What reads at first as a defect of orientation turns into a claim of permanence, the old marks worn smooth rather than erased. By the return of the opening line the backward gaze is no longer an affliction but a settled sum of everything he can be.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["identity & becoming"],"means":"a backward orientation claimed as permanence rather than corrected as defect","built":"the opening line returns unchanged so the backward gaze resolves as a settled sum"},
"ocean-grove~these-boys-light-fires":{"haiku":"The song explores existential questioning and the search for meaning through friendship and connection, contrasting isolation with belonging and the desire to grow and find purpose. It questions identity and the role of companionship in self-discovery.","opus":"A young person's anxious striving for progress and meaning, questioning whether we're just matter and cells, and finding the best in himself alongside a companion while feeling he bears everything alone.","sonnet":"The narrator reflects on striving forward while questioning purpose and identity — finding their best self in the company of another, uncertain whether external validation or inner drive matters more, and circling the theme of bearing life's burdens both alone and together.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages selfhood as a tug-of-war between striving and doubt: the forward march of one foot after another keeps colliding with the existential question of whether effort even matters, whether a person is more than matter and cells. The turn comes through companionship — a figure sitting beside the speaker with eyes turned upward — which is where he locates the best version of himself, so the hammering refrain about bearing everything alone reads as the very thing that presence is dismantling rather than a settled state. The chorus then wrestles with agency and permission: a secondhand blessing to simply be yourself curdles into a loop of deferral, wanting only what the other wants, so that self-determination and codependence become impossible to tell apart. That undecidability is the point the repetitions enact — them or me, it never settles — the growth and the surrender are voiced in the same breath, laying down and rising made one gesture.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["identity & becoming","love & desire"],"means":"self-determination and codependence voiced in the same breath with no resolution","built":"the hammered refrain about bearing it alone names what the other person's presence is dismantling"},
"ocean-grove~thousand-golden-people":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic vision of humanity's decline and societal decay.","opus":"An uneasy meditation on facing the future, mortality and numb detachment as one watches a fading world go by.","sonnet":"Passive drift through a technologically numbing world where humanity watches itself decay without connection or urgency.","src":"haiku","fable":"Written in 2017 but projecting itself into 2020, the track pictures a tranquil future-paradise and treats its calm as a warning sign, not a triumph: an idle multitude reigns while nobody stays conscious. Underneath, the verses seethe with dread, framing humanity as the author of its ruin and diagnosing the whole placid state as something engineered and quietly murderous, so the soothing hook is exposed as a mask. Only at the bridge does it soften into flesh, abandoning the sci-fi to ask, if everything overhead collapsed, whether you could still hold onto another person — its lone answer to that anaesthetized drift.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","politics & society","surveillance & control"],"means":"treating tranquil paradise as a warning of something engineered and murderous","built":"the soothing hook runs as mask until the bridge abandons dystopia"},
"ocean-grove~thunderdome":{"haiku":"A speaker wrestles with excess, loneliness, and a dying heart while confronting their own role in their suffering and grasping at fragmented survival.","opus":"A jagged self-portrait of someone lost to excess and addiction, sanity measured by degradation and a plea to be handled roughly as proof of care. Chaos and craving for connection tangle inseparably.","sonnet":"A fractured, half-delirious song about living at excess — measuring sanity by drugs, existing in states of overstimulation, wanting to be rough in relationships and needing that roughness as proof of care — told in disjointed images that feel like recalled fragments of a life lived far too intensely.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is a comedown monologue that mistakes brutality for intimacy: the speaker keeps demanding to be handled too rough as the only proof that someone cares, so tenderness and violence collapse into the same request. Movement carries the meaning — it opens in fragile domestic memory (childhood craft-and-glue) and degrades into a life measured by highs and by bottled piss, until the arithmetic of self splits him into all three at once, a fractured identity narrating its own dying in real time. The looped final lines aren't a chorus but a mind stuck in the loop it's describing, repeating because the addiction and the need-to-be-hurt leave nothing new to say.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"tenderness demanded as pain because it is the only proof of care","built":"the looped final lines are a mind stuck inside the loop it is describing"},
"ocean-grove~ufo-triple-j-like-a-version":{"haiku":"An isolated teenager witnesses a UFO and cannot convince anyone of what they saw, trapped in loneliness and disbelief.","opus":"A disoriented, lonely cover about a stranded narrator who claims to have witnessed something no one will believe, marooned far from home and desperate to find his way back. Recorded for triple j's Like A Version, it lets Australian alt-metal band Ocean Grove remake another artist's song into their own woozy, homesick anxiety.","sonnet":"Isolation and disbelief collide on a dark road sixteen miles from home — the speaker saw something nobody will accept as real, and the wonder of the sighting is inseparable from the loneliness of being the only witness. The driving question is not what it was but how to find the way back when the experience has set you apart from everyone around you.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~up-in-the-air-forever":{"haiku":"Sleep and death merge into escape, the speaker surrenders while hoping reunion with the beloved waits beyond dissolution.","opus":"A gentle, deathbed farewell, telling a loved one to save themselves with the promise of meeting again. Aging has revealed happiness as a lie, and the speaker finds his soul in another, sending love to a grieving family as he lets go.","sonnet":"A song about suicidal ideation addressed partly to a loved one — the speaker asks the other person to save themselves if they fall asleep and never wake up, then directly addresses their mother with an apology, saying their father has found his son in death.","src":"haiku","fable":"A deathbed prayer that keeps folding one word back on itself: the chorus flips the plea to rescue yourself into rescue held within, and the promise of meeting again into its own refusal, so reunion and farewell become the same breath. The final turn toward mother and father resolves the falling-asleep as a son surrendering rather than escaping.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","family & blood"],"means":"a son surrendering into sleep rather than escaping it","built":"the chorus folds one word back so reunion and farewell share a breath"},
"ocean-grove~what-i-love-about-a-natural-woman":{"haiku":"Society exploits people for money and drugs, creating hollow shells; the speaker names this grotesque, twisted reality.","opus":"A sardonic swipe at grift and sedation, vapid skeletons dulled by pills while the speaker turns would-be swindlers away empty-handed. Ocean Grove leans into slang and swagger, coining an ODDWORLD refrain that names the strangeness of the times, the singer perched on a precipice between what is and what might yet be.","sonnet":"A speaker surveys a vapid landscape where sedatives reach children and money extracts everything of value. Amid the wreckage they broadcast from a strange world grown stranger — standing at the edge of what exists and what might yet be possible.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-grove~when-you-re-this-high-you-can-say-what-you-like":{"haiku":"A person perceives another as a masterpiece who showed them beauty, but that person made truth difficult to accept when the speaker thought they were playing a game.","opus":"Infatuation lifted by intoxication into reverence: he crowns her a treasure he keeps guarded and grounding, soaring at his absolute peak. The twist is that she made honesty tough to take when he braced for manipulation, and the parting brag, that he's an impossible standard she'll never equal, sours the worship into bruised pride.","sonnet":"Infatuation with someone the narrator elevates to masterpiece status — she's kept in his safe, kept in his sanity — but there's an edge of possessiveness and the suggestion she didn't play along with his expectations of her.","src":"haiku","fable":"The braggart title promises drunken candor, but the song underneath keeps circling one word until the compliment curdles: she is a masterpiece, an object he keeps in his safe and in his sane, meaning her worth is that she stabilizes him and can be locked away. The repetition is the tell — as the masterpiece line stacks up unchanged toward the end, the celebration hardens into a mantra of possession, admiration collapsing into inventory. The turn comes when the mask drops entirely and the voice pivots from praising her to advertising himself as the act nobody follows, revealing that the whole tribute was always about his own reflection in her eye.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"the tribute hardens into inventory as the masterpiece line stacks unchanged","built":"the pivot from praising her to advertising himself reveals the whole tribute as his own reflection"},
"ocean-grove~you-know-something-we-don-t":{"haiku":"Obsessive surveillance and sexual preoccupation masked as insight; the speaker scouts for targets and resists forced participation in others' desires.","opus":"A restless track about obsessive self-scrutiny, scouting for meaning and calling it mental masturbation, built up only to be torn down. The defiant refrain refuses to give away a part of himself or let anyone project their beliefs onto him, guarding against being reduced.","sonnet":"About obsessive, circular thinking over someone — the narrator scouts and schemes mentally, acknowledges it's a compulsive pattern, and ultimately refuses to let the other person take anything essential from them.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages seduction and self-defense as the same act: the speaker cases a lover the way a predator cases a block, then instantly deflates his own hunting into something private and shameful, a mental habit he finds less repellent when it stays in his head. That confession sets up the power struggle underneath — a partner who builds him up only to demolish him, always in silence, never with the words that would settle anything. The refusal that closes the track works two ways at once: it is the beloved warned that she can take a piece of him but not the whole. It is also the speaker refusing to hand over the interpretation, insisting that whatever meaning she scrounges for she can believe but cannot verify. The title's taunt lives in that withholding; both sides claim to know the other, and the escalating chant of refusal admits neither ever will.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control","shame & repression"],"means":"seduction and self-defense conducted as the same act simultaneously","built":"the escalating chant of refusal admits neither side can verify what the other claims to know"},
"ocean-sleeper~never-the-one":{"haiku":"Habitual rupture fragments bonds; the speaker poisons intimacy through their own toxicity, drowns love through betrayal, and remains forever insufficient for the devotion they destroy.","opus":"A drowning confession of self-sabotage in love, from a narrator who compulsively wrecks what he ought to protect. Convinced he was always insufficient and never the person truly needed, he watches hope rot and trust curdle, gasping through weak breath as the relationship dissolves into mutual solitude.","sonnet":"A person realises they compulsively destroy what they should love — \"I always break what I should've loved\" — and acknowledges a relationship built on stacked lies, never being enough, and hope decayed like a body, finally drowning in loneliness.","src":"haiku"},
"ocean-sleeper~sleep-life-away":{"haiku":"The speaker prefers dreaming over living, choosing abandonment and dissolution rather than risk and seizing moments. Winter's cold settles permanently while regret waits.","opus":"Depression rendered as the seductive pull of retreat, where dreaming and numbness feel safer than facing a life you feel you've already lost. It captures the quiet despair of preferring oblivion to the risk and pain of staying present.","sonnet":"A person in severe depression chooses withdrawal over engagement — preferring to lie in dreams rather than seize the day — recognizing that this is paralysis, that winter is gone but the cold remains in them, and that if they never take the chance it's already over.","src":"haiku"},
"oceandvst~nme":{"haiku":"Void expansion and cognitive siege fragment the speaker's empathy; insecurity hunts relentlessly while pills and pills suppress the pain that should authenticate existence.","opus":"A harrowing account of the mind as its own worst enemy, insecurity and self-loathing hollowing the narrator out. He razes everything he builds, hounded by sensations that creep beneath the skin like deceptions, medicated into numbness, questioning the price of survival and half-praying for release from a life that feels like a con.","sonnet":"A speaker battered by insecurity, social pressure, and self-demolition tears down everything they build and drains their own empathy — pills, toxic air, and the void getting bigger frame a state where existing itself is defined by others' cruelty and internal collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"oceans-ate-alaska~escapist":{"haiku":"Psychological manipulation and toxic cycles perpetuate suffering; the narrator struggles against corruption, torn between suppressed rage and paralyzed submission.","opus":"Metalcore built around addiction as a captor with a will of its own, a habit that rules the mind while its victim fights just to breathe and confess. The final wish to have been a better self is where the despair lands.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped in a cycle of addiction or destructive influence — something that feeds on their weakness and rules corrupt minds — fights for clarity with every breath, torn between coming clean and letting the silence bury them.","src":"haiku"},
"oceans-ate-alaska~hansha":{"haiku":"Digital narcissism and performative existence erode authenticity while self-consciousness replaces genuine human connection.","opus":"A takedown of lives lived through a phone, where worth gets tallied in hollow online approval and copying others, stoking dread for people who abandon you the instant you show a crack. The British metalcore band's answer is to quit caring about the crowd's verdict and refuse the fabricated existence.","sonnet":"A critique of social media and digital identity — fabricated lives, self-worth measured by shallow words from introverts, side-by-side watching the world pass, obsessed with imitation and vanity — urging the listener to stop giving a fuck what others say and stop living a worthless lie.","src":"haiku"},
"oceans-ate-alaska~metamorph":{"haiku":"The speaker questions whether they're being missed, feels they lost their mind and fell from orbit, and makes one final plea to light up the night sky before crashing down.","opus":"A raw reckoning with a bandmate or himself, turning over words and deeds that may only have played out in his own mind, begging first to be missed and then to be rescued before he crashes. The image of a satellite tumbling from its path carries the whole breakdown, this British metalcore act ending on a plea for one final burst of light.","sonnet":"Coming back from a mental free-fall framed as reentry — falling out of orbit like a satellite, crashing in silence, yet still capable of lighting up the sky one last time. The song weighs absence and recovery against a brotherhood that held together even through the drift, asking whether the damage was real or all constructed in the mind.","src":"haiku"},
"oceans-ate-alaska~paradigm":{"haiku":"Stolen paradise persists through friendship; disillusioned youth cling to neon escape and each other until the sunrise fades illusion.","opus":"A youthful anthem of disillusioned rebellion, pairing neon-lit nights of reckless freedom with rage at a corrupt establishment. Oceans Ate Alaska frame a generation as sacrificed, invoking Guy Fawkes to call for defiance against liars and inherited systems, bonding with friends who promise loyalty to the very end.","sonnet":"A generation raised on empty promises — the fountain of youth ran dry, hopes hang from a noose — finds its solidarity in all-night cruising and defiance of a political establishment that is called out by name: \"fuck the patriarchy,\" a generational genocide, a call to pick a side rather than be managed into compliance.","src":"haiku"},
"oceans~home":{"haiku":"Sailors drifting through space search for home across dying stars, discovering that the brighter lights are actually dead inside. The longer the journey, the more they fade into those stars.","opus":"Melodic metal that turns bereavement into a cosmic journey with no destination. The mourner is imagined as a wanderer adrift among distant suns that only look alive, drifting endlessly outward and losing himself the further he travels. Grief here is a place you never arrive at, only keep moving through.","sonnet":"Alienation given a cosmic frame — drifting between dead stars that only look bright from a distance, belonging nowhere in the human race. The longer the journey stretches, the more the traveler fades into the dying light, burning away identity in the reaching, until home remains only a word for a maze with no exit.","src":"haiku"},
"oceans~voices":{"opus":"A visceral depiction of self-hatred and mental illness, the narrator not recognizing his own reflection, wanting to smash the mirror and himself, tormented by voices telling him everything he does is wrong and that he'd be better off dead.","sonnet":"About violent internal self-hatred expressed through dissociation — the narrator sees a stranger in the mirror, hears abusive voices in their head telling them they are worthless and should die, and the song alternates between the voice of the abuser and a defiant urge to smash it.","src":"sonnet"},
"oceans~we-are-the-storm":{"haiku":"The song expresses desire for apocalyptic cleansing where the world must end and water reclaims the land, erasing all human traces. Misfits become the storm that brings transformative destruction and renewal.","opus":"An apocalyptic longing from the perspective of society's outcasts, who welcome a cleansing flood to wipe the world clean and give it a fresh start. Metalcore turning misfit alienation into eschatology, hope resting entirely on the great collapse.","sonnet":"A post-apocalyptic ecological fantasy — the narrator and fellow misfits embrace the role of the storm that will cleanse and reclaim the earth, washing away human traces and hoping the collapse leads to a better world, even if it requires total erasure.","src":"haiku"},
"october-drift~airborne-panic-attack":{"haiku":"Existential panic manifests as falling; modern distractions and hobbies fail to prevent the inevitable descent into darkness.","opus":"The frantic self-improvement checklist people reach for while quietly falling apart, gym and hobbies and chores stacked against dread. The plunging chorus reveals it all as an airborne panic attack, thorns and wires catching as we give out more than we take in.","sonnet":"The hollow chirpiness of self-help advice — join the gym, bake bread, take a shower, have a drink — collides with the reality of a person drowning in anxiety and climate dread, falling victim to an airborne panic attack that no amount of productivity can stop.","src":"haiku"},
"october-ends~bury-me-under":{"haiku":"Desire to be buried and forgotten; seeking peace in death and finality.","opus":"October Ends work the modern metalcore vein where confessional wound and cathartic scream share one breath, and this track lives entirely in that whiplash. A person leans on a relationship to outrun trauma yet feels buried beneath their own defenses, the mood lurching from gratitude to venom toward someone who nearly destroyed them. It resolves not in peace but in the refusal to be moved.","sonnet":"A person trying to escape a traumatic past by leaning on a relationship that makes them feel alive, but who also feels buried under the walls they built — the song swings between gratitude and bitter rage at someone who nearly destroyed them, ending with an insistence on standing their ground.","src":"haiku"},
"october-ends~dark":{"haiku":"Darkness becomes both refuge and trap as the speaker hides behind intoxication and numbness, performing wellness by day while dissolution accelerates invisibly at night.","opus":"A masked nightlife of drinking, drugs and casual sex used to bury a life the narrator privately considers a wreck. The darkness he claims to live in is both his hiding place and his sickness.","sonnet":"A man numbing himself through drink, drugs, and casual sex to avoid confronting the venom that has blackened his heart: he performs wellness in daylight but lives in the dark, rooted and faded, begging others to stay away while secretly crying out for someone to make a sound.","src":"haiku"},
"october-ends~devil-s-den":{"haiku":"The speaker addresses betrayal and self-imprisonment, calling out hypocrisy while trapped in a collective cycle of deception and decay.","opus":"A furious break with a two-faced person and the toxic environment they share, insisting the honest devil is preferable to a fraud posing as clean; beneath the rage is entrapment, the narrator admitting they are all trapped in the same den and that this person's betrayal drove them astray.","sonnet":"Someone who has been used and left behind by a city and a person they trusted reaches a point of furious clarity — they'd rather deal with known evils than hypocrites who pretend at goodness, and they feel trapped in a collective hell of their own making.","src":"haiku"},
"october-ends~iridescent":{"haiku":"Internal struggle with depression and isolation; the speaker battles alone through darkness but finds that human connection and vulnerability can rebuild the fractured self.","opus":"October Ends return to the emotional metalcore register where private collapse is sung at full volume, and this track sits deep in that isolation. Someone drowned mentally for too long, sacrificing their own health to shield others, fighting a walled-off war alone. Haunting memories and eroding faith press in, yet a fragile conviction survives, that fate still holds a place and things will come out okay.","sonnet":"Someone who spent too long drowning mentally — sacrificing their own health to protect others, fighting a war alone behind walls — now faces haunting memories and eroding faith, while holding onto the fragile belief that fate still has a place for them and things will be okay.","src":"haiku"},
"october-ends~lost":{"opus":"A defiant reckoning with feeling caged by a society that manufactures hollow validation and despair. Betrayed by people who abandoned him and revealed their real selves, the singer grows isolated and adrift, arriving at the bleak conclusion that he stands utterly alone with nothing left to lose.","sonnet":"A person trapped in patterns they never chose — praying for change that never comes, feeling imprisoned by a system and by a stagnant relationship that remains the same no matter how many times they try — concludes that it is them against the world with nothing waiting at the end.","src":"sonnet"},
"oddko~disobey":{"haiku":"Systems invade the mind to control thought and sell false truths as commerce. Chaos and disorder fill the void; resistance means refusing the game.","opus":"A blunt anti-authoritarian chant that pictures the mind as territory to be seized and locked down by a system peddling truth as a commodity, keeping people adrift in engineered chaos until the only response left is refusal. The repetition drives the command home like a slogan. Oddko aim their industrial metal at paranoia about manufactured disorder, making defiance itself the trap sprung back on the manipulators.","sonnet":"The mind as territory to be colonised — systems and forces that sell a manufactured truth and profit when you buy it. The song names the mechanism and then refuses it: void and disorder as diagnosis, disobey as the only prescription. Short, repetitive, hypnotic, it enacts the thing it demands.","src":"haiku"},
"oddland~unity":{"haiku":"Connected dissolution yields paradoxical transcendence; the speaker dissolves self to achieve unified consciousness.","opus":"Desire and surrender examined as the path to dissolving the self and glimpsing a larger oneness, even as that same longing arms people for war. Enlightenment and destruction share a source.","sonnet":"The speaker waits, withering, for someone who may or may not come, while a mirror burns a hole through the amassing self built for war; the turn is toward feeling life heal through surrender — letting the core diffuse, going weightless — and the fragile question of whether unity can be perceived at all.","src":"haiku"},
"odeon~morphine":{"haiku":"Someone who consistently burned bridges, lied, and took shortcuts is told the consequences are entirely their own — karma and regret are the only inheritance, and the only painkiller available for the life they built is morphine.","opus":"An accusatory reckoning with a deceiver who burned bridges on gentle lies and is now meeting his karma. The speaker demands back the time that was stolen, framing regret as a wound that needs numbing as the betrayer's reign ends and the lies rot him from within.","sonnet":"A reckoning with a betrayer who burned bridges and told lies while insisting they had good intentions — the narrator frames karma as their counter-attack and refuses forgiveness. The core verdict: time is unrecoverable, what's done is permanent, and when you can't go back you'd better have brought morphine for the pain.","src":"haiku"},
"of-allies~are-we-better-off":{"haiku":"Listlessness and indecision about progress; the speaker cannot decide what to do and feels trapped under influence. Archaic words fail to produce change.","opus":"A post-breakup deadlock where tossing out old photos only sharpens the unanswerable question of whether the split helped anyone. The narrator stalls, loathing his own overwrought writing and half-tempted to feign illness rather than commit to a decision. That very inability to choose is the song's whole subject, post-hardcore turning its melodrama inward.","sonnet":"A stalled relationship examined through foggy, half-articulate indecision — pictures thrown out, positions traded, neither person sure they are actually better off for walking away. The speaker cycles through self-doubt and recrimination without landing anywhere, trapped in a loop of keeping telling themselves they can't decide.","src":"haiku"},
"of-allies~goodbye":{"haiku":"Trust broken, the speaker demands honesty over cruelty; goodbye or confession, no middle ground.","opus":"An ultimatum to a betrayer: end it honestly or wound me, but stop lying. The plea to be hurt rather than deceived exposes how a broken trust and unadmitted wrong have made pain feel more bearable than the lie.","sonnet":"About demanding honesty from someone who has broken your trust — the narrator would rather be hurt outright than endured with polite deception. The repeated choice between \"tell me goodbye\" or \"hurt me, just don't lie\" makes explicit that ongoing dishonesty is worse than the worst true ending.","src":"haiku"},
"of-allies~the-hierophant":{"haiku":"A person in emotional decline begs for companionship, describing their heart as a raging animal while feeling vacant and worn down. They seek connection to prevent falling further into emptiness.","opus":"An addict's plea for company inside the descent, the narrator numbing himself and begging someone to stay while promising to turn it around, the hollow chest and racing heart betraying how far down he already is. Loneliness dressed as an invitation to play.","sonnet":"A song about emotional dependency and the search for connection — the narrator is coming down hard from something and reaching out, asking someone to stay and play, promising to turn things around, but the emptiness is pulling them further under.","src":"haiku"},
"of-mice-men~another-you":{"haiku":"Grief preserves absence as permanent presence, the speaker unable to release the lost one and therefore unable to reconstitute their own shattered identity.","opus":"Grief carried after losing someone irreplaceable, the house still full of their presence. Between the sterile white walls and the fear of losing himself too, the vow is to carry the dead person in memory since no one will ever take their place.","sonnet":"A person in the aftermath of loss — likely a death — experiences the presence of the absent one everywhere: in the sounds of the house, in their bones, in the portraits on the walls. They grieve the impossibility of replacement while vowing to carry the memory forward as the only way to hold both themselves and the lost person together.","src":"haiku"},
"of-mice-men~gravedancer":{"haiku":"People sink toward catastrophe, circling their own grave in hypnotic ritual, knowing they will never break the pattern even as they dance toward death.","opus":"The American metalcore band frames doom as willing surrender. Everyone here is sliding toward ruin, drawn by an irresistible pull and treating collapse like a party thrown for the ending itself. The bleak twist is full awareness: they know they will not save themselves and keep moving toward the edge anyway.","sonnet":"Humanity circles its own ruin in a trance, drawn by some irresistible pull toward collective self-destruction. The ritual feels celebratory even as it is terminal — everyone dancing around the grave knows the steps will not stop until the dancer becomes the buried.","src":"haiku"},
"of-mice-men~mosaic":{"opus":"A meditation on human beings as both saints and monsters, hell and heaven housed within everyone. It asks whether we can shoulder the burden of our own nature and cure our contempt for our own kind before time runs out and we go back into the ground, chaos rendered almost beautiful.","sonnet":"A plea for humanity to confront its own darkness — the snake in the garden, the plague in the heart — and ask whether it can carry its own weight and choose differently before time runs out, holding the broken pieces together like a mosaic of the end.","src":"sonnet"},
"of-mice-men~o-g-loko":{"haiku":"Obsessive passion and devotion consume the narrator; they've surrendered their world to love, willing to burn everything for reciprocation.","opus":"A love song that curdles into obsession, framing all-consuming devotion as a virtue while admitting it smothers the beloved. The imagery of burning the world to watch it reflected in her eyes reveals possession dressed up as faithfulness.","sonnet":"An intense declaration of consuming, all-encompassing love in which the speaker locates their own identity entirely within the other person, pledging absolute faithfulness and insisting the devotion — even if smothering — is the truth of who they are.","src":"haiku"},
"of-mice-men~obsolete":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation metastasizes through numbness and isolation, with the speaker circling their own end while questioning whether survival without purpose holds any meaning.","opus":"A grim confrontation with mortality, obsolescence, and the fear of fading into irrelevance. Of Mice & Men imagine the narrator watching vultures circle as he sinks, half-wishing the world were better without him yet unready to die by himself, pleading to wake from a nightmare of becoming discarded and unremembered.","sonnet":"A thousand days watching vultures circle overhead while drinking water from a stone — a narrator who has felt the world pressing him into the soil admits he is no longer a scream but a whisper, afraid of becoming obsolete. He cannot commit to being set free, half-admitting the shackles are what he needs.","src":"haiku"},
"of-mice-men~obsolete-sullivan-king-remix":{"haiku":"A person spiraling in despair watches death approach, feeling like a faded shadow of their former self. They fear becoming irrelevant and ask someone to wake them from this nightmare, yet struggle to hold on.","opus":"A desperate refusal of a slow erasure: a thousand days watching vultures circle, drinking water from a stone in the desert sun, begging to be woken as if it were all a dream. Once a scream, now only a whisper, terrified of becoming obsolete. Of Mice and Men, the California metalcore band, lend the anthem raw survival stakes, and Sullivan King's remix drops it into bass-heavy electronic weight.","sonnet":"Survival despite every interior signal pointing toward surrender — a thousand days of watching vultures circle, convinced the world would benefit from an ending. But at the last moment the will holds, and the speaker begs to be woken from a nightmare of irrelevance, terrified of fading from a scream into a whisper no one hears.","src":"haiku"},
"of-mice-men~seven-thousand-miles-for-what":{"haiku":"Long-distance sacrifice and emotional exhaustion dissolve bonds as the narrator abandons the journey, having given everything without reciprocation.","opus":"A bitter breakup with someone gone cold, the narrator claiming he left long before she did and insisting his intentions were golden even as he walks the long road alone.","sonnet":"A relationship post-mortem where the speaker admits they left emotionally long before they left physically — \"I left you long before you left me\" — but the lingering pull of the other person's eyes still makes it hard to go, even after the black dress and dancing shoes signal it is over.","src":"haiku"},
"of-monsters-and-men~little-talks":{"haiku":"Haunting persists within an old house as the speaker grapples with mental illness and loss, finding only fragile boats carrying bodies to uncertain shores. Honesty and departure remain the only mercy available.","opus":"A duet between the living and the dead, one voice a ghost haunting the empty house, the other clinging to the belief that they'll carry each other safely to shore and meet again.","sonnet":"Two voices speak across a threshold — one comforting, one possibly already departed — as grief and memory and the confusion of loss blur together, and the promise that a ship will carry them safe to shore holds against the screams and the ghost of someone gone.","src":"haiku"},
"of-virtue~a-n-x-i-e-t-y":{"haiku":"Anxiety fractures identity into an uninhabitable prison; the speaker spirals inward, suffocating beneath self-imposed mental architecture while demons close in relentlessly.","opus":"Anxiety spelled out letter by letter as a suffocating loss of control, the mirror returning a hollow ghost. The fight is against one's own demons with no guarantee of winning.","sonnet":"A direct confrontation with anxiety as a controlling force — the speaker sees a hollow ghost in the mirror, suffocates under walls closing in, and fights demons at the edge while refusing to be defeated by what cannot stop bleeding.","src":"haiku"},
"of-virtue~cut-me-open":{"haiku":"Deception weaponized through intimacy; the speaker discovers their partner is a constructed mask, leaving them bleeding from betrayal that feels both inevitable and deserved.","opus":"A toxic love rendered as slow poisoning and Russian roulette, where cruel words cut like blades and the victim keeps coming back to be bled dry. Devotion curdles into a standoff at gunpoint.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship portrayed as a weapon — the partner feeds lies like poison, makes the speaker whole only to lacerate them again, with the seven deadly sins bleeding through and the imagery of razors, pistol smoke, and a trigger finger throughout.","src":"haiku"},
"of-virtue~false-idols":{"haiku":"Organized religion and false prophets peddle meaningless mythology, trapping believers in cycles of guilt and spiritual death.","opus":"A loss-of-faith reckoning that turns outward into a broad indictment of organized belief: the sense of being deceived by the very institutions meant to give life meaning, and of a species too blinded by its own systems to save itself. Disillusionment curdling into contempt.","sonnet":"About losing faith in the narratives we're given — religion, society, and authority are all suspect, fake prophets and false idols have humanity on a suicidal course, and the narrator feels crucified by a world whose purpose they can no longer find.","src":"haiku"},
"of-virtue~holy":{"haiku":"Abandonment and regret cement into self-betrayal; the speaker recognizes their own complicity in their destruction while unable to stop returning to someone incapable of reciprocal love.","opus":"A man who failed the partner who idealized him now drowns in self-hatred after she leaves, his regret staining everything. He clings to the fantasy that his lingering traces might lead her back.","sonnet":"A speaker mourns a relationship destroyed by their own failures — they were idolized and still could not keep the ground stable beneath the other person's feet, leaving footprints of regret described as \"blood red\" staining the bones.","src":"haiku"},
"of-virtue~hypocrite":{"haiku":"Violence disguises itself as principle; the speaker exposes systematic cruelty wrapped in moral rhetoric.","opus":"An accusation hurled at a two-faced abuser who prays for you while wrapping hands around your neck. The speaker names himself the gasoline to their spark, ready to become the flamethrower and burn the hypocrisy down.","sonnet":"A direct confrontation with someone who performs piety while operating with predatory violence — they pray for the speaker while choking them, bury bodies, call it progress, and the speaker finally names them what they are: a hypocrite who loves decay.","src":"haiku"},
"of-virtue~omen":{"haiku":"Decay approaches inexorably while the speaker balances on collapsing ground; catastrophe whispers warning and the end of sanity draws near as illusion and denial dissolve.","opus":"A grim alarm that we sit a single move from ruin, whether the world's or one's own. Of Virtue cast bowing to hidden masters as bondage to catastrophe, the singer feeling his mind drift toward territory he knows he shouldn't enter, and he delivers it as a prophecy that the clock forgives no one.","sonnet":"A speaker delivers a warning — this is an omen, a lesson — to someone one step from total decay, framing the state as slavery to disaster and a ball and chain to a master, with the end of the line arriving whether heeded or not.","src":"haiku"},
"of-virtue~sinner":{"haiku":"Savior and sinner trade places as the speaker drowns in holy water and addiction to another's self-destruction, bound by vanity and twisted fantasy while haunted by inescapable disease.","opus":"A tortured refusal to keep playing savior to someone who treats love as a self-destructive game. Trapped in cycles of holy-water guilt and haunting obsession, the narrator recognizes the bond as a disease dragging him toward his grave, and resolves to break the addiction to another's ruin rather than drown in it.","sonnet":"A speaker refuses the role of sinner to someone who positions themselves as a saviour — \"drowned in holy water, calling me to my grave\" — naming the relationship as a disease that haunts dreams, one they are finally breaking free from after too long in line with the other person's tempo.","src":"haiku"},
"of-virtue~suffer":{"haiku":"Someone begs for escape from pain and meaninglessness; suffering feels undeserved and only pain reveals what strength and change are possible.","opus":"A metalcore cry to a creator to hurry up and end an undeserved ordeal, life having missed everything it seemed to promise. Desperation pushes right to the brink of losing faith. Then it flips: the argument becomes that the worst agony is exactly what forces a person to finally transform.","sonnet":"A person on the edge of spiritual collapse pleads with a silent God to explain the weight of undeserved suffering. The anger burns inward, consuming what remains, until the closing lines propose — barely hopefully — that pain at its deepest might finally force transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"of-virtue~thanks-for-nothing":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts someone who took everything they offered, leaving bitterness and regret while paradoxically revealing they loved more truly.","opus":"A sarcastic kiss-off to someone who drained the narrator's heart, fled, and left hollow vows that now sicken him. Michigan metalcore band Of Virtue build to a comeback verdict: he lost a person who never loved him, but she lost one who genuinely did, the mock gratitude serving as the final blow.","sonnet":"A breakup eulogy that turns at the end — years of wondering why someone left, the bitterness of broken promises and total betrayal, the speaker on their knees. But the final turn is the one that lands: whatever was lost here, the speaker loved fully, and the person who left lost someone who genuinely loved them. The thanks-for-nothing is grief wearing the mask of sarcasm.","src":"haiku"},
"ogre-you-asshole~a-10x3qwe":{"opus":"Ogre You Asshole drifted from taut indie rock toward hazy, krautrock-inflected psychedelia, and that dissolving texture suits a lyric about a mind unable to hold its dreams. Sleep's events melt into meaninglessness as the narrator wanders with nowhere to go, knowing passage is impossible yet trying to carry the images so as not to forget, watching everything crumble at dusk.","sonnet":"A mind that can't hold onto dream-images or blend in — events from sleep dissolve into meaninglessness, the person wanders with nowhere to go, knowing passage is impossible but trying to carry the images so as not to forget, watching everything drift and crumble at dusk.","src":"sonnet"},
"ogre-you-asshole~a-qye47q":{"opus":"The band's motorik, hypnotic later style turns unease into something ambient and creeping, ideal for this study of social pressure and forgotten rules. Heads have been swapped for something that deceives and mocks; everyone pretends to understand while fleeing anxiety, and the dawn-lit world reads as visually blank, no one able to sort the real from the faked.","sonnet":"A song about the distorting pressure of social expectation and forgotten rules: the narrator and those around them have had their heads replaced with something that deceives and mocks, all pretending to understand while running from anxiety — the world under dawn light is visually blank, and no one can tell what's real from what's faked.","src":"sonnet"},
"oh-wonder~all-we-do":{"haiku":"Protective instinct and personal stagnation imprison the narrator; hiding and safety measures fail to prevent the fade of meaning and authentic being.","opus":"A hushed portrait of two people who cope with life by retreating into each other, safe but stagnant, unable to find any paradise on the ground. The comfort of hiding and the ache of feeling everything fade are the same thing here.","sonnet":"A portrait of paralysis — hiding, chasing, waiting, fading — where the speaker is so upside-down they no longer want to be right-side up, unable to find anything solid or paradise-like in the world as it is.","src":"haiku"},
"ohio-players~love-rollercoaster":{"haiku":"Desire and pleasure spiral in chaotic loops; romantic love becomes a wild, thrilling ride that consumes judgment and restraint.","opus":"Love as a thrill ride the singer keeps begging to board, wild and dizzying and wanted again and again. It's pure funk-driven appetite for the ups and downs of desire.","sonnet":"A celebratory extended metaphor: a lover's affection is a rollercoaster the speaker desperately wants to ride, with the song functioning almost entirely as joyful, exclamatory riffing on that single image.","src":"haiku"},
"ok-goodnight~the-bear":{"haiku":"A beast struggles between hunger and survival; salvation requires accepting the monster within.","opus":"A tale of endurance narrated from within a famished beast, claws dark with filth yet red with blood, a savior mask that can't survive the strain. It splits between the comfort that we make it through together and the colder fact that only the singer walks away, prog-metal scale exposing the solitude beneath any story of survival.","sonnet":"A creature or outcast — the Bear — struggles between the community's rules and its own predatory nature, trying to play hero while knowing the instinct to feed cannot be suppressed; it survives alone where others survive together, guilt-stained and unbroken.","src":"haiku"},
"ok-goodnight~the-crocodile":{"haiku":"Predatory seduction masks hunger; the speaker poses as host offering sanctuary while concealing voracious appetite for naive guests.","opus":"A predator plays the gracious host, luring prey closer with false hospitality before the mask drops into ravenous contempt. A fable of the charming monster who feeds on trust.","sonnet":"A predator voice — honeyed, theatrical, dripping with false hospitality — lures creatures into its depths under the guise of playing a game, swinging between feigned tenderness and voracious appetite, mocking those who try to escape as \"mewling pieces of fodder.\"","src":"haiku"},
"ok-goodnight~the-snake":{"haiku":"Sickness spreads through affection; the speaker invites invasion while poisoning themselves and others through inescapable contagion.","opus":"A decaying, soulless creature insists that love can never satisfy the rage consuming it, welcoming death and dissolution into the earth. Anger becomes an appetite nothing can quench.","sonnet":"A serpent voice shrivels in cacophony, promising that no love can quench the endless thirst of rage — the earth is asked to consume it, carcasses sink into ripened emptiness, and the only truth offered is that the soulless cannot be saved from themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"okilly-dokilly~slaughterhouse":{"haiku":"The song sarcastically blesses the meat industry chain from slaughter through profit, critiquing the complicity and mechanization of killing.","opus":"A mock-devout mealtime blessing that comically extends thanks to the earnest workers at the abattoir and the profiteering middlemen who inflated the cost. The joke is the collision of piety and butchery. Okilly Dokilly are a Ned Flanders-themed metal novelty act who scream everything in Simpsons-speak, so the straight-faced hymn to blandness is the entire gag.","sonnet":"A deadpan blessing ceremony for the meat industry's entire supply chain — grocer, middlemen, and slaughterhouse workers all receive equal reverence in a tone that mimics grace at a dinner table. The humor is the point: sincerity and absurdity collapse into each other, and the result is more unsettling than any outright condemnation.","src":"haiku"},
"oliver~chemicals-feat-mndr":{"haiku":"Two people are drawn together by an intoxicating chemical attraction that feels addictive and impossible to resist.","opus":"Glossy synth-pop that treats attraction as a chemical high, the rush of a new connection framed as a drug you're urged to take without second-guessing. Beneath the shine sits the knowing hint that this euphoria could just as easily be poison.","sonnet":"An invitation to mutual intoxication and abandon, using the rush of chemical attraction as a metaphor for surrendering to a connection that feels both dangerous and irresistible.","src":"haiku"},
"oliver~portrait":{"haiku":"Inherited absence and longing shape identity; the speaker navigates family ghosts through books and maps, seeking connection to lives never lived.","opus":"An inheritance of absence: a grandfather he never met, places he'll never reach, a book left behind, all kept as relics while the singer writes a song mostly to fill the empty time himself.","sonnet":"A quiet song about inherited distance and incompleteness — the speaker contemplates a grandfather he never met, a map of places he'll never visit, a dead friend's book, and his own creative exhaustion, writing songs just to pass the time rather than from inspiration.","src":"haiku"},
"olly-steele~try":{"haiku":"The speaker searches endlessly for a path back to what was but keeps finding only illusion, trapped in cycles of desire and loss.","opus":"Prog-metal built around the ache of chasing a lost former state, a mind exhausting itself searching every door for the way back to what was before, only to find the illusion of return keeps slipping away.","sonnet":"Someone locked in a futile search for meaning or return to how things once were, finding that the path back is an illusion and the only way forward is to keep trying despite having no clear destination.","src":"haiku"},
"olly-steele~tyed":{"haiku":"Suffering binds all existence; the speaker cannot escape a curse woven into birth itself, destined to bleed through a prison of regret.","opus":"An anguished quarrel with being brought into existence at all, guilt and regret figured as a curse bound to the self from birth. It reframes creation as imprisonment, a name and fate no one can escape.","sonnet":"A song about irreversible guilt and existential suffering — the speaker is drowning in mistakes they cannot repair, carrying a curse they cannot lift, and raging at having been brought into existence at all, framing life itself as a cell from which there is no escape and no healing.","src":"haiku"},
"omenxiii~push-me-away-feat-remington-leith-of-palaye-royale":{"haiku":"Addiction cycles between presence and absence; the speaker self-destructs while resisting both healing and the love offered as exit.","opus":"Spiraling numbness and drug use push away everyone who tries to reach in, the narrator half-aware he may not survive his own escalation. He pushes people away and mourns being pushed away at once.","sonnet":"Numb and self-destructive, the speaker loses friends, gets high, and hears voices while someone keeps closing the door on every attempt to open up — the push-pull of addiction and isolation cycling until the only question is whether today is the day it kills them.","src":"haiku"},
"ona-i-webber~wy-lij-sobie-poczt-wk":{"haiku":"Ironic postcards from trapped hometowns: two correspondents paint bleak pictures of a provincial Polish town—poverty, depression, lack of culture—as a dark mirror to send back from elsewhere.","opus":"Polish duo Ona i Webber build a satirical epistolary piece, quoting fictional letter-writers cataloguing misery in the worst backwater in Poland: filth, poverty, depression, crooked pavements, cannibal locals and out-of-tune orchestras. The wry counsel is to mail yourself a postcard from that place before despair strikes. The narrator admits the picture is exaggerated, the facts scant, yet the frustration underneath is genuine, and someone will surely be amused reading it.","sonnet":"Two letter-writers catalogue a provincial Polish town's every deficiency — crooked pavements, cold pork chops, untuned cellos, emigrating masses — with theatrical despair. The punchline is tender: the frustration is real but the picture is distorted, so mail yourself the postcard and laugh at the exaggeration.","src":"haiku"},
"once-human~eidolon":{"opus":"A cyber-tinged metal warning about minds seized by authority, propaganda, and manufactured static drowning out reality. Firewalls and barriers wall off the self while a phantom illusion is projected over lived experience. The narrator fights becoming a faceless statistic bound to nothing, holding onto a suppressed truth.","sonnet":"An eidolon — a phantom of the self — is draped over consciousness like a veil, suppressing the inner voice with white noise and white lies; the narrator refuses to remain a numbered, coded nothing and fights back against the forces blacking out their identity.","src":"sonnet"},
"once-human~sledgehammer":{"haiku":"The narrator has learned to exist fragmented, describing repeated destruction as a tool for reconstructing themselves stronger.","opus":"A survivor's answer to being battered: invite the sledgehammer, be broken brick by brick, because dwelling in pieces has become the way to keep living. The mirror is unbearable, the mind crumbling, yet arms stay open to the blow. Once Human, the melodic-death outfit fronted by Lauren Hart, pair that masochistic resilience with brutal delivery, turning being wrecked into a chosen, defiant kind of wholeness.","sonnet":"An invitation to be demolished completely so the rebuilding can finally begin — the speaker has already learned to live shattered, so destruction holds no new terror. Each blow is welcomed as the only honest path back to wholeness, even if wholeness never fully arrives and living in pieces becomes its own hard-won stability.","src":"haiku"},
"one-hundred-thousand~libra":{"haiku":"Two people struggle in a relationship at a threshold, fearing they have not yet lived or loved enough and questioning whether goodbyes can happen when heroes die.","opus":"A love suspended on a horizon of sunsets and rising tides, the singer asking whether fear kept him from loving as heroes die and survival feels useless. Reflections and disguises blur what can still be felt, the plea to hold tight set against a dawning goodbye. Living and loving both feel barely begun even as they end.","sonnet":"A relationship teeters at the horizon of its own survival — two people clinging to each other while the tide rises and everything they might have shared still lies unlived ahead. The dread is not of ending but of leaving without having truly begun.","src":"haiku"},
"one-true-god~addicted":{"haiku":"A person, broke and despondent after losing their partner, becomes hooked on a vice that fulfills them immediately and desperately.","opus":"A stripped-down declaration of addiction, broke and heartbroken but hooked on a new vice; the read is simply craving and dependency, the lost love replaced by a desperate, urgent need for the substance the narrator can't stop wanting.","sonnet":"A down-to-the-bone song about replacing a lost relationship with a new compulsion — broke, without a partner, the narrator has found a different vice that gives the same rush, and he's in it completely, needing it right now.","src":"haiku"},
"onerepublic~counting-stars":{"haiku":"Spiritual seeking and resistance to materialism promise liberation through valuing moments over currency and corporate obligation.","opus":"A restless choice to chase meaning and love over money, lying awake dreaming of what could be and feeling most alive doing exactly the wrong thing. Tedder makes burning the cash and tallying the stars a refusal to simply obey.","sonnet":"A restless spiritual ambition — trading dollars counted for stars counted, feeling right doing the wrong thing and wrong doing the right thing, taking money and watching it burn in the river — driven by the conviction that everything that kills you makes you feel alive.","src":"haiku"},
"onerepublic~i-lived":{"haiku":"Full embodiment and authentic experience matter more than longevity, celebrating every moment with broken courage.","opus":"An exhortation to live so completely that, when death arrives, you can claim you did everything, took the hurt, fell in love and made each moment yours. Tedder wrote it partly for his own son, wishing him a whole life even at the cost of broken bones.","sonnet":"An address to someone facing hardship — hope you fall in love so hard it hurts, hope you don't suffer but take the pain — building to the speaker's own declaration that with every broken bone they owned every second this world could give and swear they lived.","src":"haiku"},
"oni~alone":{"haiku":"Isolation deepens as the speaker buries self-denial; they ask how they survived utterly alone.","opus":"Confronting someone trapped in their own schemes and chaos, offering to be their ghost while daring them to face how they ended up isolated. The refrain hammers self-burial, the way denial and exile leave a person utterly alone by their own hand.","sonnet":"About disentangling from someone's psychological web and the disorientation that follows — the narrator sees through the other person's schemes, tries to reclaim themselves, but the damage of exile and denial runs deep. The address to someone who has somehow ended up utterly alone tracks the cost of sustained self-deception.","src":"haiku"},
"oni~breathe-again":{"haiku":"Liberation through transformation; breaking chains and claiming new life force.","opus":"A defiant declaration of renewal and self-reclamation, the narrator breaking free of chains and old identities to fight his own fight. Refusing to let anyone define or change him, he reaches for fresh life and highs on his own terms, treating this day as a miracle and a chance to finally breathe.","sonnet":"Breaking free from chains and old worlds to claim new life on one's own terms — no one can change me, no one can tell me — framed as self-authorised rebirth rather than rescue. The speaker wants to breathe again and find their heaven, insisting it's their time.","src":"haiku"},
"only-liars~crown-of-greed":{"haiku":"Selfish deception breeds inevitable abandonment; the speaker recognises chronic betrayal and reclaims solitude as liberation.","opus":"A reckoning with a selfish, manipulative partner whose every promise to change is another lie, ending in hard-won resolve to be better off alone. The regret is only that it took so long to see through the mask.","sonnet":"A sustained severance from a selfish, broken partner who burned them down every time, promised a shared world, and never delivered — the speaker strips away denial to see clearly that this person will never change and is better faced from a distance.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~66-fucking-6":{"haiku":"Metalheads embrace chaos, aggression, and extreme violence as a unified force against a fearful world. They celebrate thrashing metal music and demonic power through destructive phases and brutality.","opus":"A thrash anthem built around the mosh pit and devil imagery, the band casting themselves as an army of disorder playing hell's own soundtrack to a churning crowd. The through-line is the ritual violence and brotherhood of a metal gig, phase after phase of pit warfare, hammering heads, and bodies going down.","sonnet":"A heavy metal concert war-chant framed as devil worship — the pit is a battle zone, the crowd are chaos legions, and the number of the beast is the band's rallying identity. It's theater, not theology: the point is collective aggression and volume.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~a-prayer-for-the-dead":{"haiku":"Malevolent transformation casts the speaker as executioner and judge; evil becomes personal identity pursued with methodical satisfaction.","opus":"A satanic self-coronation as the face of death and judge of mankind, cast down from heaven and returning betrayal with a bullet, taking evil as its very name.","sonnet":"A revenge fantasy voiced from the perspective of a wronged, destroyed person who has rebuilt themselves around hatred and declared themselves judge, jury, and executioner. The narrator inhabits a kind of dark messianic role — cast down, infected with grievance, and now returning to exact a death sentence on those who betrayed them, framing the whole thing in blasphemous religious language.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~antitheist": {"haiku":"Religious trauma and control leave deep psychological wounds; the speaker rejects forced faith and reclaims freedom from imposed belief systems.","opus":"A raging rejection of religion from someone forced into a faith he never accepted, left permanently marked by its demands. He reclaims his own thought and speech, spurns the institution entirely in favor of something genuine, and ends with blunt profane defiance, insisting his life was always his own.","sonnet":"A raw rejection of the religion forced on the speaker in childhood — the wounds are presented as permanent and real, the faith as a form of control that was never believed in, and the refusal to submit is framed as reclaiming a life that was never theirs to take.","src":"haiku","fable":"Not abstract atheism but a survivor's account of a religious upbringing as abuse: forced to pray to a god he never believed in, and left with wounds the chorus keeps returning to. The bitter pivot is that saved, raised, and scarred turn out to be the same act — the faith meant to redeem him is precisely what damaged him — so the profane outburst near the end lands less as blasphemy for shock than as a scar tissue finally allowed to speak.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"the faith meant to redeem him is precisely what damaged him","built":"saved, raised, and scarred collapsed into a single indictment in the chorus"},
"onslaught~bomber-feat-phil-campbell-tom-angelripper": {"haiku":"A military bomber aircraft delivers death and destruction with precision and brutality, celebrating aerial warfare and the terror it inflicts on ground targets. The mission is merciless and guaranteed.","opus":"A Motorhead-style ode voiced by a bomber aircraft raining napalm and firestorms on its targets; the deeper read is metal's fetishization of unstoppable machine-death, the plane boasting it shoots to kill and always will, bringing its enemies to their knees with terror.","sonnet":"A Motörhead cover celebrating the unstoppable momentum of a World War II bombing run — the crew flies five miles up with nothing but contempt for the enemy, dropping ordnance with precision, driving terror into those below, the bomber itself treated as an almost mythic instrument of destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Motörhead cover — Lemmy's 1979 original sung from the cockpit of the WWII bomber itself, all swagger and no conscience, the machine grinning as it promises death from five miles up. Onslaught keep the joke intact: terror delivered with a showman's aim-to-please wink, with Motörhead's own Phil Campbell and Sodom's Tom Angelripper on board as a lineage salute.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder"],"means":"terror delivered with a showman's aim-to-please wink","built":"Motörhead's own guitarist and Sodom's vocalist aboard as a lineage seal"},
"onslaught~born-for-war": {"haiku":"A bleak portrait of a soldier programmed from birth for violence and death. The song depicts systematic dehumanization, psychological destruction, and the transformation of a person into a killing machine through indoctrination and warfare.","opus":"A grim portrait of a soldier bred and conditioned to be a killing machine, tracing how violence, indoctrination, and the destruction of life turn a child into a weapon bound for death from cradle to grave.","sonnet":"A portrait of a soldier conditioned from birth to kill, tracing the brutal cycle of violence, coercion, and death that defines his existence from cradle to grave, framing war as a system that manufactures human weapons and discards them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A child-soldier assembly line run from cradle to grave: a boy is taken young, hollowed out, and reissued as ordnance with hatred installed where family used to be. The argument lives in the shifting chorus tag — the death machine goes from embryonic to homicidal to suicidal, tracing a weapon's full service life from manufacture through deployment to disposal — so by the end the soldier's death reads as a product reaching designed obsolescence, not a person anyone will mourn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"a soldier's death rendered as a product reaching designed obsolescence","built":"the shifting chorus tag traces the weapon from embryonic through suicidal"},
"onslaught~burn":{"haiku":"Violent rejection of Christianity and God, celebrating destruction and demonic vengeance against religious authority and faith.","opus":"An extreme anti-Christian screed voiced as a demonic destroyer setting the world and its churches on fire, casting Jesus as the aggressor who hates, violates and rapes him. It's a total inversion of religion, hatred of Christianity turned into apocalyptic vengeance and self-declared darkness.","sonnet":"A full-throated rejection of Christianity framed as violent revenge — the speaker portrays Jesus as a violator and oppressor, calls for churches to burn, and positions the singer as the chaos-bringer enacting karmic retribution for religious abuse.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~chaos-is-king":{"haiku":"A ruler of chaos glorifies disorder, destruction, and defiance against control, where only the strong survive and conformity dies in the face of violence and collapse.","opus":"British thrash imagining a totalitarian antichrist-figure who rules through manufactured hate, propaganda, and the recruitment of disaffected youth. A grim allegory for how order dresses itself as liberation while crushing free speech.","sonnet":"A declaration of total systemic breakdown — borders dissolving, propaganda fragmenting truth, disaffected youth mobilized, freedom of speech crushed — presented not as warning but as the natural order with chaos crowned king.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~code-black": {"haiku":"A chilling depiction of fascistic rise and violent revolution. The song portrays a new supremacist movement built on hatred, propaganda, and systematic elimination, with violent forces deployed to destroy dissenters.","opus":"A thrash dystopia about a rising new world order built on supremacy, propaganda and manufactured hatred, the wolf emerging to enslave. It reads as a warning about fascism repackaged, force and fear sold as destiny.","sonnet":"The song describes the rise of a neo-fascist order — using imagery of wolves, propaganda, purges, and a \"new world order\" — portraying ideological supremacism as it recruits, eliminates the weak, and systematically destroys those it deems impure.","src":"haiku","fable":"A fascist resurgence narrated from inside its own propaganda: the verses catalogue the machinery — industrialized killing, purity campaigns, elite death squads loosed on the weak — while the chorus barks the movement's own recruiting command to rise. The mask is the method; the song never steps outside to editorialize, trusting you to recognize this triumphal anthem as a rerun of the twentieth century's worst experiment in nouveau dress, its call to rise engineered to sound like any other metal chorus until you notice what you have been chanting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder"],"means":"fascism repackaged so its anthem sounds like any other metal chorus","built":"the song never steps outside the propaganda voice to editorialize"},
"onslaught~cruci-fiction":{"haiku":"Religious hypocrisy and false prophecy mask self-inflicted pain; the speaker rejects resurrection and Christ addiction, arguing that prayer and holy books deceive with lies.","opus":"A scorching anti-Christian broadside from British thrashers Onslaught, deriding faith as an addictive contradiction and the resurrection as a fabrication built on scripture they dismiss as deception. Turning biblical miracles into props of self-righteous delusion, the band drive their blasphemous polemic on relentless riffing.","sonnet":"Organized religion is anatomized as a structure of self-inflicted pain, inherited contradiction, and cynical narrative dressed in devotion. Two millennia of hypocrisy are measured against the claims made on behalf of a ghost with a hollow name — the song insists evolution and honest confession matter more than scripture built on blood.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~damnation-onslaught":{"haiku":"Metal amplification becomes conduit for satanic power; sonic aggression crushes belief through overwhelming force.","opus":"Classic thrash bravado casting the band as an infernal force taking the stage to crush disbelievers with sheer volume. Power from hell as pure metal spectacle.","sonnet":"A metal band announces its performance as Satanic power unleashed — killerwatts, the wrath of Satan, savage screams — directed at any soul who doubts, framing the concert itself as an act of hellish domination.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~dead-man-walking":{"haiku":"A tortured man walks toward execution, drained of lifeblood by an obsessor's poison tongue, a broken hero defined only by the damage inflicted upon him.","opus":"A thrash vow of vengeance against a false prophet with a poison tongue, a preacher marched to hell for the lies he built his standing on. Its charge is retribution against hollow authority, the manipulator finally led like a lamb to the slaughter he earned.","sonnet":"A vengeance oath directed at a traitor — the narrator swears to drain the target's blood, destroy their world, and spit on their grave, while the target is already walking toward their inevitable death, a preacher to those they've failed.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~destroyer-of-worlds":{"haiku":"An apocalyptic figure celebrates global destruction and genocide, positioning themselves as death's king and the architect of universal annihilation.","opus":"A thrash apocalypse voiced by death itself, cataloguing genocide, indoctrination and a false messiah leading humanity to extinction. The relentless death litany insists no one is spared.","sonnet":"A nihilistic vision of a universal force of annihilation — not a person but a principle, a world-order built on genocide, indoctrination, and the deliberate manufacture of collapse, chanting that every living thing will die.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~enemy-of-my-enemy":{"haiku":"Former enemies unite in a violent alliance, bound by shared hatred and bloodlust rather than genuine alliance.","opus":"A war anthem built on the ancient maxim that shared hatred forges alliances of convenience, factions united only by a common foe as new regimes rise on slaughter and lies. The stance is deeply cynical about power manipulating conquered nations. Onslaught, veterans of 1980s British thrash, deliver it with the genre's scorched, riff-driven ferocity.","sonnet":"Geopolitical violence reduced to its bluntest logic — opposing factions united not by shared values but by shared enemies, brothers in arms bonded through hatred rather than love. Behind the tribal alliances sits a puppet-master pulling strings on a global scale, turning brutality into strategy and war into a designed, profitable state.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~godhead": {"haiku":"Organized religion is condemned as corrupt manipulation that weaponizes faith to control populations. The song attacks religious authority as predatory and evil.","opus":"A furious anti-religious broadside casting organized Christianity as a corrupt, war-mongering cult that spreads control disguised as salvation. Blasphemy deployed as protest against clerical hypocrisy and abuse.","sonnet":"The song attacks organised Christianity as a con built on lies, abuse, and violence, depicting the church as a predatory cult that produces evil while preaching innocence. The repeated phrase \"children of god\" is turned into an accusation.","src":"haiku","fable":"An anti-clerical broadside whose sharpest move is inverting the three wise monkeys: hearing, seeing, and speaking no evil becomes the trained posture of an institution ignoring what happens inside it, until the fourth line of the refrain rules that the silence is itself the evil. The insistence that everything stays in the family aims the song specifically at ecclesiastical abuse and cover-up rather than belief in the abstract — the target is the cult structure that protects its priests, not the flock it consumes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","surveillance & control","anger & defiance"],"means":"institutional silence declared the evil rather than the acts it covers","built":"the three wise monkeys inverted so trained blindness becomes the institution's method"},
"onslaught~hatebox": {"haiku":"The song glorifies violence, brutality, and warfare as righteous hate and annihilation, celebrating the destruction of enemies and the spread of chaos.","opus":"A brutal celebration of hatred and warfare, summoning troops, chaos, and total annihilation, casting hate itself as a force that dictates and pulls everyone under toward hell.","sonnet":"A celebration of pure, structured violence and hatred as a force of war: troops march, hate fuels the blood, and a domain called the hatebox exists where brutality is glorified as the ultimate expression of power.","src":"haiku","fable":"The hatebox is a pit — mosh, cage, war zone, all at once — and the song sells violence as a consensual arena you queue up to enter, chanting viva la hate like a carnival slogan. The bridge closes the trap: you are the hate, we are the hate, dissolving any line between the band whipping up the frenzy, the crowd feeding on it, and the war it mimics.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The backdrop the lyric hails is white, red and black — the palette of the Reich banner — pinning the arena’s pageantry to a specific rally aesthetic.","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance","war & battle"],"means":"violence sold as a carnival you willingly queue to enter","built":"the bridge erases any line between performer, crowd, and the war they chant about"},
"onslaught~killing-peace":{"haiku":"Systematic violence, terrorism, and religious indoctrination are presented as forces that murder innocence and corrupt the pure-hearted, depicting war as an assault on goodness itself. The imagery portrays dehumanization through organized brutality.","opus":"A brutal, blasphemous depiction of engineered mass murder and holy war, laying out poisoning, drugging, and methodical slaughter that wipes out the innocent and desecrates all that's sacred, humanity delivered into faith and a coming global conflict.","sonnet":"An extreme-metal portrait of religiously framed mass violence — born into a world of religion and perpetual war, the narrator describes genocide, chemical killing, and demonic destruction as forces that tear apart innocence and shatter heaven's peace.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~let-there-be-death":{"haiku":"Nuclear apocalypse and Satan's horsemen bring universal destruction and death; hell reclaims the world as evil triumphs and nothing survives.","opus":"A cornerstone of British thrash staging total apocalypse as pure spectacle. Satan orders the extinction of all living things, mounted riders sweep across a scorched world, and warfare reduces people to nothing while their souls are shipped downward. There is no mercy and no survivor, annihilation played for maximum grandeur.","sonnet":"Nuclear apocalypse is rendered as infernal coronation: Satan commands warlords and horsemen to scour the earth clean, reducing living nations to vapour and ash. Every soul delivered to hell is another trophy in a cosmic decree of total annihilation, with no survivor spared and no god left standing but ruin.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~let-there-be-rock-ac-dc-cover":{"haiku":"Rock music is sacred and worth protecting, a primal sound that spreads through crowds and must be defended against those who would suppress it. Worship of music becomes an act of rebellion and survival.","opus":"Reworks the rock-worship trope into something sinister, where the crowd's rapture is intercut with a car bomb ticking to the same beat; the deeper read is mass spectacle and violence blurring together, the same sound of adoration and detonation, celebrity and terror captured by a nauseating camera.","sonnet":"A cover that reimagines rock music as a quasi-religious mass event erupting in the streets, with crowds surging and animals threading through them, though the lyrics feel abstracted — at its core it's a celebration of rock's primal, almost riotous communal power.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~metal-forces":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic battle waged across metaphysical planes where metal becomes the primal force of destruction and transformation, collapsing the boundary between earthly conflict and cosmic warfare.","opus":"An eighties thrash war-hymn that mythologizes heavy metal itself as an unstoppable army. Onslaught stage a hellish battlefield of blood, steel, and lightning where fans and band stand as one force, the power surging through veins, glorying in leather, sweat, and the sheer conquering roar of the music.","sonnet":"A classic metal battle hymn in which the war metaphor and the concert metaphor collapse into each other — the \"stage of war\" is both literal battlefield and live show, Satan's troops and the metal forces trading fire in sweaty leather. The point is the power of the music itself as a force that cracks the sky and raises the seven gates.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~metal-forces-live":{"haiku":"A theatrical battle between good and evil forces rages with death and power, framed in operatic metal language about combat and demonic threat.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek anthem that recasts the concert experience as mythic, apocalyptic combat, with fans conscripted as an unbreakable army amid falling heavens and hellfire. Onslaught, a British band from thrash's second wave, deliver this live version with exactly the over-the-top bombast the eighties adored, celebrating the genre itself as holy war.","sonnet":"A live-metal battle hymn where the stage becomes a battlefield and the music itself is the weapon — shellshock, sweat and leather, axes and chains, seven gates cracked open. Satan's troops, metal storms, seas of light: it is all spectacle and adrenaline, the concert as ritual war and the crowd as soldiers who cannot be slain.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~onslaught-power-from-hell":{"haiku":"Sonic devastation unleashed with demonic power; the stage becomes battleground for crushing the unbelieving.","opus":"A raw first-wave thrash blast that literalizes metal as satanic weaponry drawn straight from hell. Onslaught boast of killerwatts unleashed to crush disbelievers, taking the stage as if playing for their lives with Satan's wrath in mind, reveling in the savage, ear-splitting power of the genre at its most primal.","sonnet":"A metal band's self-mythology as satanic force: they unleash killerwatt power from hell, taking the stage with Satan's wrath and hammering the believers into the ground with thrashing speed — those who disbelieve are crushed.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~pain":{"haiku":"Pain itself becomes the subject—pleasure, violence, and torture merge as transcendent experiences; the speaker embraces suffering as raw power.","opus":"Suffering entangled with drug addiction and self-ruin, needle imagery and a rush that rips through the mind toward final collapse. The chant that ties agony to violence, torture, lust, and power casts it as something the narrator both endures and wields, until it drives him mad.","sonnet":"A raw account of addiction — the needle, the rush, the erosion of the soul, the body decaying while the mind is simultaneously tortured and seduced by the substance that's killing it.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~planting-seeds-of-hate":{"haiku":"A speaker plants seeds of terror, hate, and violence in receptive minds, using them as tools of destruction and nihilistic chaos.","opus":"British thrash veterans Onslaught voice the song from a terrorist recruiter's perspective, sowing dread into impressionable minds and rousing dormant operatives toward a grim payoff. Casting himself as an instrument of silence, violence and death, the narrator cultivates hatred inside others with chilling ideological satisfaction.","sonnet":"A radicalizer speaks in the voice of ideological contagion itself — cultivating sleeper cells, bending susceptible minds, weaponizing silence and violence as instruments of defiance. The refrain presents hatred as something deliberately sown rather than spontaneously grown.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~rest-in-pieces": {"haiku":"Graphic descriptions of violent dismemberment, torture, and murder are presented as the work of a sadistic killer who views himself as a master of death and the complete obliteration of victims. The song is a showcase of extreme gore imagery.","opus":"A gruesome portrait of a sadistic killer who dissects, mutilates, and exterminates victims with clinical brutality, leaving anonymous corpses in pieces while reveling in obscene, homicidal cruelty.","sonnet":"A graphic depiction of a serial killer — methodical, sadistic, and relentless — who dismembers and destroys victims so completely that nothing remains and no one even knows their name, painting them as a self-styled messiah of death with a homicidal passion for the obscene.","src":"haiku","fable":"A serial-killer portrait told in the killer's own grandiose register — surgeon, messiah, angel at hell's gate — and the inflation is allowed to run until the bridge punctures it: the victim dies unheard, unseen, and unnamed. That pivot is the whole argument; the song indicts the mythology built around murderers by showing exactly what the legend erases — the anonymous person on the table.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"killer mythology indicted by what the grandiose legend erases","built":"the bridge punctures the grandiose register with an anonymous, unnamed victim"},
"onslaught~seeds-of-hate":{"haiku":"Ideological contagion spreads deliberately; the speaker cultivates hatred as systematic cultivation within vulnerable minds.","opus":"Hatred cultivated like a crop and planted in receptive minds, glorying in nihilistic terror and a sleeper cell of violence. The listener is addressed as the willing instrument of that destruction.","sonnet":"A cold, tactical voice describes infiltrating receptive minds with terror and hate — awakening sleeper cells, tending a harvest of despair, maximising a nihilistic kill — using the listener as a tool of violence and a wall of silence through which defiance is screamed.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~shellshock":{"haiku":"Someone suffers psychological trauma that fractures reality and induces madness, trapped in nightmare and confusion. The song depicts combat-related mental breakdown.","opus":"A plunge into a mind broken by war, sanity begging for quiet amid panic, silence screaming, ears bleeding as madness takes over. The British thrash veterans render trauma as literal combat inside the skull, the ticking dial and its grasping hands whirling while the speaker trails a sightless guide, unsure if he perceives anything or is utterly lost.","sonnet":"A mind shattered by trauma spirals through inner noise and crumbling perception, uncertain whether what it sees is madness or truth. The shell-shock state becomes both prison and strange liberation — sanity redefined by those who never survived what this person has endured.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~shock-n-awe":{"haiku":"Glorification of military shock tactics and warfare: merciless killing, silent strikes, and systemic brutality depicted as power and strategy.","opus":"A depiction of a ruthless military assault that strikes by night to exterminate at will, dwelling on war as a domain of pain, brutality and inhumanity.","sonnet":"The song depicts modern military assault — precision strikes, overwhelming force, and rapid killing — as a cycle of pure, dehumanizing brutality. War is catalogued as a domain of pain, fatality, hate, and inhumanity with no redemptive purpose.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~suicideology": {"haiku":"Suicide as liberation and escape; the relief of silence and peace through self-destruction and the end of suffering.","opus":"A grim, graphic catalogue of suicide and self-destruction, voiced partly by a tormenting force that feeds on the victim's pain and death; it dwells on overdose, bloodletting, asphyxiation, and gunshot as release from misery.","sonnet":"Voiced as a predatory, almost supernatural force that feeds on self-destruction, the song catalogues methods of suicide — overdose, blood-letting, immolation, gunshot — and frames the speaker as the embodiment of the suicidal impulse itself, whose own misery ends only through the victim's death.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's coinage — suicide as an ideology, a belief system with its own liturgy of methods — is dramatized by handing the microphone to the ideation itself: the I here is the voice in the head, a parasite that can only end its misery through its host's death. Read that way, the second-person taunting isn't shock-metal cruelty but an anatomy of how suicidal thinking talks — cataloguing exits, promising silence and peace, calling the violence divine. The final turn, where the voice insists the victim is the destroyer, completes the trick: the parasite hands its host the blame.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","madness & the mind"],"means":"suicidal ideation anatomised as a parasite that hands its host the blame","built":"the title coins suicide as ideology, giving the ideation its own liturgy of methods"},
"onslaught~tested-to-destruction":{"haiku":"Internal war rages—the speaker is both the wound and weapon, suicide and pride, torn between violence and the agony of survival.","opus":"British thrash from inside a mind wrecked by combat, where the narrator and his enemy blur into one. He voices himself as every wound, weapon, and burden the other carries. The real subject is a person pushed past their breaking point by war and mutual contempt, now wanting only to annihilate the hatred inside.","sonnet":"About being pushed to the absolute psychological limit by mutual contempt — the narrator identifies as someone's burden, their suicide, their damage, and the song is a declaration of having been broken past the point of fear by hatred on both sides.","src":"haiku"},
"onslaught~the-sound-of-violence": {"haiku":"The song celebrates warfare and violence as natural, desirable states, depicting the speaker as someone thriving in combat and destruction with an insatiable appetite for carnage.","opus":"War rendered as sensory assault from inside the combatant's head, killing as a hardcore adrenaline high and gunfire as its own brutal music. The relentless noise it can't switch off becomes the true portrait of the warrior's mind.","sonnet":"A war-glorification track that wallows in the sounds and sensations of combat — machine gun fire, bullets, bodies, adrenaline, orchestrated chaos. The narrator is not reporting on war but inhabiting it, finding a kind of ecstatic purpose in the relentless noise of killing.","src":"haiku","fable":"War rendered as music: gunfire becomes a symphony, artillery batteries a discordant wall of sound, and the soldier-narrator its conductor, orchestrating hell on earth. The final movement gives the game away — he begs for the noise in his head never to stop, because it is drowning out a voice he can no longer hear; the battlefield has migrated inside him, and silence, not death, is what he actually fears.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The opening line — silence brings death — inverts Simon & Garfunkel’s The Sound of Silence, the flip the title completes.","themes":["war & battle","madness & the mind","violence & murder"],"means":"begging the noise never to stop lest silence return","built":"war recast as music with the soldier its conductor"},
"onslaught~twisted-jesus":{"haiku":"A warped religious figure preaches hatred and violence while justifying massacre and global destruction in the name of twisted faith.","opus":"Onslaught aim their thrash at faith turned into a weapon, picturing religion mutated into a war-hungry evangelist. This figure slaughters humanity, installs a new order, and drains the dying while proclaiming belief itself to be nothing but suffering. Doomsday imagery mounts as every gate of hell is thrown open and no one is spared.","sonnet":"Religion repurposed as a machinery of slaughter: a messianic figure preaches hatred, massacres nations, and imposes apocalyptic rule. The indictment is that organized faith has always been capable of this — death and pain dressed in the language of salvation.","src":"haiku"},
"onslow~let-me-rust":{"haiku":"The speaker accepts degradation and consumption as natural, willing to be discarded, worn out, and destroyed without complaint.","opus":"A quietly devastating expression of passive self-neglect, the narrator asking to be used up, drunk empty, worn out and turned to dust. The deeper read is exhausted resignation dressed as agreeableness, a person offering themselves up to be consumed while insisting 'I'm okay, it's alright,' framing their own slow decay as acceptable.","sonnet":"A song about passive self-erasure through compliance — worn down, hollowed out, consumed and discarded, the narrator insists he's okay even as he dissolves into dust.","src":"haiku"},
"oomph~augen-auf":{"haiku":"A hunting game where someone stalks and seeks another who must hide; the hunt culminates in revelation and the transition from hiding to a darker game.","opus":"A children's hide-and-seek rhyme turned into a predator's hunt, the counting and the seeker's call reframed as menace, arousal and fear breathing together. German industrial rock making a playground game genuinely sinister.","sonnet":"In German, a hide-and-seek song made predatory and sexual — the narrator counts down and hunts a person, promising to find them and play \"truth or dare,\" the childhood game's language weaponized into a charged pursuit where hiding is futile.","src":"haiku"},
"oomph~augen-auf-radio-edit":{"haiku":"Predatory hide-and-seek where the seeker's dominance is assured, the hunted caught between concealment and inevitable capture.","opus":"A menacing German industrial-metal hit that twists a children's hide-and-seek chant into a predator's stalking. Oomph! turn the counting rhyme and the cry of the seeker into something sinister, the narrator lying in wait, drawn by fear and breath, so that innocent play becomes a chilling hunt with darker intent.","sonnet":"Hide-and-seek as erotic threat — the seeker counts, smells fear, knows exactly what the hidden one wants; the game is Truth or Dare once they're caught. 'Augen auf, ich komme' (eyes open, I'm coming) turns the children's ritual into predatory suspense, the rules of hiding dissolving into something that can't be undone.","src":"haiku"},
"oomph~das-letzte-streichholz":{"haiku":"A broken and abused person returns as flame to burn down the beautiful world of those who harmed them. Hell's knowledge becomes destructive revenge.","opus":"Pioneers of German Neue Deutsche Härte, they voice a child returning as vengeful fire, the last match drawn from a father's cabinet. Having broken her favorite toy before breaking herself, she comes back with a burning gaze to torch the family's tidy, wholesome world. The refrain demands they now taste hell, learn what it is to scream for deliverance.","sonnet":"A young woman who was failed by those who should have protected her returns with the last match to burn down the comfortable, intact world of her abusers. Her act is not mere revenge but a demand that they finally feel what hell is — the suffering and the cry for release she was never heard making.","src":"haiku"},
"oomph~labyrinth":{"haiku":"A lost person is trapped in madness and internal labyrinth by trauma and a fragmented self. There is no exit without facing one's own demons.","opus":"In German, the voice of madness or an abuser personified as a presence that has locked a lost child inside a mind-maze, distorting their reality and self-image. It grows overtly predatory, the entity dwelling in the victim's soul, using them for its lust and blinding their senses, telling them only they can end the game.","sonnet":"In German: a dark inner voice addresses a lost person trapped in a mental labyrinth of madness — the voice claims to be their secret self, their second skin, built inside their head to exploit them. Nobody can tell you which doors are right; only you can end the game, but the voice keeps knocking to be let back in.","src":"haiku"},
"oomph~sandmann":{"haiku":"An indictment of poverty and inequality in Germany where poor children suffer while the rich remain indifferent; the speaker begs for escape into dream rather than face harsh reality.","opus":"German industrial indicting a wealthy nation that lets its own children go hungry, invoking a lullaby's sandman not for sweet dreams but as an escape from a cruel truth, begging to sleep and never wake.","sonnet":"In German, a dark lullaby about child poverty in Germany — poor children lined up before an abyss while a wealthy nation ignores them, a boy burning with hunger while others ask how much he has; the narrator begs the Sandman to pour sand in their eyes and never wake them to this truth again.","src":"haiku"},
"oooooooooo~fucking-freaking-futile-freddy":{"haiku":"Celebrity ascent culminates in spectacular collapse; the speaker narrates inevitable fall through paradoxical mythmaking.","opus":"A savage fable of instant fame, where a manufactured star is worshipped, milked, then dropped and mocked as he falls. The chicken-or-egg riddle asks who really bit whom in the machine of celebrity.","sonnet":"A figure called Freddy pursues fame and identity in an industry that turns him into a disposable commodity — his name on bottles everyone will shit — with a Moses-like guide who belatedly realises the plague was coming for everyone; the song cycles through chicken-and-egg questions about who creates and who consumes the spectacle, ending with the industry spitting Freddy back out.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~atonement":{"haiku":"Suffering lifts suddenly, revealing clarity beyond pain; the speaker accepts their fractured self while the past finally releases its grip.","opus":"A moment of clarity in which the fog lifts and the ache to justify one's failings falls away. As the past comes undone and the skin peels back, the singer can no longer excuse what he had become, standing at the threshold of atonement.","sonnet":"A brief lyric of emergence: fog lifts, the ache to honour old suffering ends, skin peels in the rising moon — and suddenly the speaker can no longer justify what they had become, the past undone in a moment of clarity.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~blackwater-park":{"haiku":"Death stalks through humanity as pandemic and pestilence, spectators witness decay while embedded in cycles of infection and collective tragedy.","opus":"A grim vision of humanity's rot and death, the narrator a detached spectator documenting the plague and decay until he realizes the reaper's mark falls on him too as an endless night settles over the cursed place.","sonnet":"A spectator watches the dying and the diseased gather at Blackwater Park — lepers under trees, perversion blooming, a sovereign reaper infecting roots — and documents the collapse with conceit, believing it doesn't concern them yet, until the sun sets forever and the travesty of man makes everyone part of the mass.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~by-the-pain-i-see-in-others-remixed":{"haiku":"Shared suffering becomes ritual and sacrament; the speaker finds communion through witnessing others' anguish.","opus":"Akerfeldt's melancholy turned toward an intimacy that reaches past death, a lover addressed as if buried yet still binding the singer to the soil. The stillness of a world where nothing ever changes becomes both curse and consolation.","sonnet":"An intensely intimate surrender between two people who are permanently intertwined — the speaker asks the other to reveal themselves completely, while acknowledging that their connection exists beyond language and beyond change. The final section shifts to observe the stasis of the outside world, which has nothing on what they share.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~deliverance":{"opus":"A drowning rendered as intimacy, the killer holding a wrist beneath the waterline while narrating mercy. Mikael Akerfeldt's title track pushes Opeth's progressive death metal toward something confessional and self-devouring: a spiral of sin, distrust and belief where salvation is imagined through a sinner's cruelty, and the devil dictates every word.","sonnet":"A narrator drowns someone — the song describes it in explicit, intimate detail: gripping their wrist, pushing them face-down under the waterline, hearing their screams rise then stop, and afterward reflecting on having forgotten why they needed to do it, now waiting for the next compulsion in a cycle of sin.","src":"sonnet"},
"opeth~dirge-for-november":{"haiku":"Fragmentation and inevitable loss, seeking peace through surrender and dissolution.","opus":"A hushed death-metal ballad of surrender, someone lost and homeless in themselves welcoming sleep and dissolution. The wish to be taken under and folded into tender care reads as a gentle courting of oblivion.","sonnet":"A spare, devastating account of someone who has reached the end — a self-inflicted wound, blood pooling on the floor — and surrenders to it with the quiet of someone who has finally stopped fighting. The natural imagery of a dove's folding wings and fading into care frames death as a release into tenderness.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~era-live":{"haiku":"Spiritual upheaval and epochal transition demand that the self shed its certainties; inner demons accompany the death of old eras and birth of new ones.","opus":"A meditation on hope glimpsed in catastrophe and the terror of surrendering your own spirit to voices telling you how to choose. The refrain about the devil living in you frames self-trust against the death of one age and birth of another.","sonnet":"A person at the cusp of a life transition hears a voice warning them not to trust appearances, while inner doubt and the pressure of others' demands threaten to drown out their own spiritual instinct — the end of one era only opens into another, and the devil they carry is internal.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~face-of-melinda":{"haiku":"A man grieves the unattainable love of a woman bound to another, haunted by the hollow romance of his longing as he watches her fade beneath the weight of broken promises.","opus":"A sorrowful courtly tale of a man trying to win back a fallen woman already sworn to another, offering to carry her far away. She refuses aloud yet confesses her heart is still his, leaving a promise made but a love unclaimed.","sonnet":"A man returns to retrieve Melinda, a woman who has sworn herself to another and sacrificed her former self, only for her to deliver the devastating final line: her promise is made, but her heart remains his — a love irrevocably split by duty and choice.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~ghost-of-perdition":{"haiku":"A mother's death and descent into madness are witnessed; a ghost of perdition haunts her. Darkness and sorcery bind her, and her sanity and name are destroyed.","opus":"A son watching his mother's slow, mind-destroying death, a corrupting force draining her reason and staining her name while a demonic voice preaches urgency about living. Opeth, Mikael Akerfeldt's Swedish prog-death band, unfold the tragedy across shifting acoustic and growled passages on Ghost Reveries, a haunted mind dissolving into fog.","sonnet":"A mother's deteriorating mind becomes a site of possession and tragedy, watched by a son who sees darkness move through her while systems of faith and sanity fail her entirely. The ghost lodged in her chest is never fully named — it is something between illness, grief, and a malevolent force that chose her long before anyone noticed.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~h-xprocess":{"haiku":"Seasonal suffering and buried secrets surface through memory's mire; maternal legacy anchors the narrator in grief and deception.","opus":"Dense, dreamlike imagery of loss and abandonment set in an eternal winter, a dying wish and children led to woe. Opeth's gift for making grief feel geological, buried in season and snow rather than plainly spoken.","sonnet":"Fragmented images of dying faith, a forgotten name, children led toward suffering, and secrets buried in the dark accumulate into a vision of spiritual abandonment — a father figure who lies, a season lost, and no rescue forthcoming.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~hessian-peel":{"haiku":"Grief and abandonment inscribe themselves permanently; the speaker mourns the living while questioning whether connection survives.","opus":"A long, grieving reckoning with a death and the guilt of having left someone alone in their final days, the reversed passages deepening its sense of a mind turned inside out. The siren in the head is despair pulling toward the same end.","sonnet":"A fragmented, grief-saturated meditation on abandonment and death, moving between questions about whether survivors will mourn and a second-person address to someone who felt forsaken before following some internal pull toward their end. The reversed lyric passages deepen the sense of buried, inexpressible anguish.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~hex-omega":{"haiku":"Two people torn apart by a moment of doubt; what was safe becomes haunted by internal demons.","opus":"A tightly compressed picture of a rupture, a bond of two years undone by one flicker of doubt, arms that once shielded someone from danger now left watching them run off toward the moon while a mother weeps and an emptiness remains. Opeth, the Swedes who drifted from death metal into prog, close their Watershed record with this bare, grieving goodbye.","sonnet":"A marriage or long partnership in its final gravity — years carried in each other's hearts, a moment of doubt that tears two lives apart. Someone held him, kept demons inside, and still always ran toward the moon. The mother cried somewhere; the space left behind is enormous and unnamed.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~hours-of-wealth":{"haiku":"Isolation and cleansing distance the speaker from humanity; they observe the world coldly, no longer connecting to any life outside their solitude.","opus":"A withdrawal into isolation after finally shedding a haunting pain, recognizing passersby yet feeling utterly adrift and far from any home. Vowing never to speak again, the singer bides his time in darkness until he's sure of a task that will let him rise.","sonnet":"A solitary figure, cleansed of pain and fever, watches familiar strangers pass the window from a distance — alone, far from home, never heard to say goodbye — and waits in darkness until certain of a task they will rise to complete, the isolation serene but final.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~porcelain-heart":{"haiku":"The speaker reflects on past shame, lost love, and the lies they clung to, walking icy roads through deception.","opus":"A quiet confession of self-betrayal, the narrator having thrown away everything, breaking his promises and running from the wreckage of his own choices. Opeth set this regret over childish illusions against the delicate acoustic textures of Watershed, tenderly consoling someone while refusing to explain the failure at its center.","sonnet":"Loss catalogued in tight confessions — a name written and read twice, shame wallowed in, love declared and then abandoned, a promise made and immediately broken. The journey out is across icy roads through wastelands of deceit, and the only comfort offered is don't ask why, just rest: the reasons don't hold.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~reverie-harlequin-forest":{"haiku":"A hunted person flees through cursed woods, marked by sickness and shame, unable to find redemption or rest. The song depicts forest as both hiding place and trap.","opus":"A fugitive flees through decaying woods, dogs on his trail, marked out as prey by the sickness that follows him. Opeth's Mikael Akerfeldt weaves regret, violence, and half-spoken guilt into a fable about false fronts and doomed hope, the chase ending in flames with redemption reduced to ash and smoke.","sonnet":"A fugitive soul flees into a forest that mirrors its own corruption — diseased, hollow, lit by false pretension. The trees become a metaphor for people who take without giving and wait for redemption that never arrives, while the speaker burns through the landscape in grim reckoning.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~strange-brew":{"haiku":"Spiritual alienation and inner darkness isolate the speaker from community; they exist in shadow, neither fully present nor entirely gone.","opus":"A confession of feeling permanently set apart, a spiritual outcast shut out from the faithful and pulled toward surrendering to a sin he can't shake. The title captures his own self dissolving as he watches devout people he knows he can never join, marked by an inner void he can't cross.","sonnet":"An isolated, spiritually outcast figure walks the edges of the world — unable to connect, aware of a wickedness in himself, blending with the anonymous crowd while remaining fundamentally alien to it. A distant voice and a veiled glance are the only ruptures in the surrounding void.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~the-baying-of-the-hounds":{"haiku":"Darkness consumes through predatory manipulation; the speaker surrenders to seduction by evil while rationality crumbles into self-loathing and despair.","opus":"A descent into the seductive hold of a malign figure who feeds on buried hopes and turns loved ones into foes, offering counterfeit comfort to the self-hating. As every conviction curdles into a lie, the narrator gives himself over to poison and death, the pack howling him homeward.","sonnet":"A controlling, parasitic figure — mouth a vortex, body a dead country, eyes spewing darkness — is dissected as a malevolent force that turns friends to enemies and feeds on buried dreams, while the speaker, too weak to resist, hears the hounds growing louder, calling them back to the place this darkness calls home.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~the-drapery-falls-live":{"haiku":"Entanglement and pain intertwine inextricably; the speaker seeks guidance through suffering that never truly resolves.","opus":"A plea to be pulled down into shared pain by someone whose seclusion darkens everything, the singer failing a test he can't persist through. It reads as depression and a doomed pull toward another who walks away for good.","sonnet":"A relationship that has warped into something painful is observed from inside the speaker's damaged perception — haunted sleep, invisible power, and a final departure the narrator cannot halt or understand. The song maps the collapse of connection through a lens of gothic dread and spiritual entrapment.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~the-grand-conjuration":{"haiku":"A dark incantation summons demonic power and dominion; Satan assembles his followers through hate, vengeance, and subversion of love.","opus":"Akerfeldt stages a seduction into evil as ritual, a devotee summoning a dark power and being filled by it. The horror is how willing the surrender is, faith and blasphemy fusing until domination feels like devotion.","sonnet":"A ritual summoning of a Satanic or demonic power — the narrator invites a dark force to fill and wield them, while the imagery of Satan assembling his flock and injecting seeds of vengeance frames human hate as a deliberately cultivated supernatural harvest.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~the-lotus-eater":{"haiku":"A person is trapped in self-harm and hopelessness, chasing escapist oblivion while their body withers. The song explores how family bonds can be weaponized and how despair propagates through relationships.","opus":"The Swedish progressive-death band study addiction and self-erasure. A drinker in thrall to his own ruin is addressed directly, his life already canceling out everything he was, his gaze gone empty. He is drawn to what destroys him and drags his loved ones down too, all set to Watershed's ornate, shifting arrangements.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone consumed by self-destruction — drawn to death, body hollowed by whatever gives hopeless delight, a moth too close to the flame. A friend died in their room; the speaker names them as a killer who targets himself, wishing he had never returned so that the end could not be witnessed. Grief and disgust intertwined in the watching.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~width-of-a-circle":{"haiku":"Someone is investigated and accused by speeding time and speculation while their past haunts them. The mask hides shame as evidence accumulates against them through their own words.","opus":"Opeth, the Swedish progressive band, render guilt as a manhunt. Dogs close on a scent, a case is built, and a portrait resembles the accused too closely, while his own darker nature makes him practically invite his downfall. The Bowie-borrowed title frames a soul quietly sentencing itself amid the band's literary murk.","sonnet":"A legal or moral investigation closing in on someone who can no longer maintain the mask — deceptive eyes and a face that matches the drawing in the file, every statement deconstructed until the case nearly closes. Then a last-minute detail redirects suspicion toward a brother, and the machinery of accusation swings somewhere new without resolving anything.","src":"haiku"},
"opeth~wreath-remixed":{"haiku":"Memory haunts with distortion; the speaker drowns in recollection while wearing masks that no longer protect.","opus":"A descent through memory and self-decay, watching a masked ghost of yourself in still water while dead-eyed children laugh out of time. It circles the search for what haunts the walls of a mind, ending on a bleak morning that offers no absolution.","sonnet":"A dissociative nightmare vision in which the speaker moves through haunted corridors, mirrors his own ghost-like form, witnesses burned fields and dead children, and arrives at something like clarity — a departure and a return simultaneously. The accumulated imagery of decay and violence resolves not into horror but into a strange morning calm.","src":"haiku"},
"orange-range~asterisk":{"haiku":"Celestial light persists across time as shared wishes bind generations; stellar transmission carries eternal hope.","opus":"Starlight becomes a metaphor for how ancient hopes and wishes travel across time to reach strangers, and how our own feelings might one day shine in someone else's heart. A plea to keep burning like a distant star until the light lands.","sonnet":"Starlight from ancient wishes crosses time and reaches the eyes of someone today as a cry; the speaker vows to keep living with full force, entrusting their own feelings to the stars so they may one day reach another heart — a sprawling meditation on how every point of light is a story continuing inside someone's chest.","src":"haiku"},
"orax~alone":{"haiku":"A person has always been fundamentally different from others, unable to share joy or sorrow with them. Mystery and solitude define their entire being, drawn from nature and suffering alike.","opus":"A soul apart since childhood, unable to draw passion from a common spring or wake joy at the same tone as others, loving alone and haunted by a mystery drawn from storm, sun and a demon-shaped cloud. Orax sets Edgar Allan Poe's poem Alone to music largely verbatim, lending the poet's confession of lifelong estrangement a dark musical frame.","sonnet":"Drawing on Edgar Allan Poe's verse, a figure traces a lifelong apartness — never perceiving or feeling the world through its common sources. Every object of beauty becomes a private mystery binding only to them; even love arrives in isolation, and the storm-shaped demon in the sky is uniquely, terribly their own.","src":"haiku"},
"orbit-culture~redfog":{"haiku":"Guilt consumes the narrator as blood and violence seep through their mind, existing in a hell of their own making where freedom and redemption are impossible.","opus":"A killer's interior monologue from confinement, insisting the caged man never truly existed and that the real prison is himself, killing to survive as the act decays what's left of him.","sonnet":"A killer or soldier narrates his own damnation — bloodstained hands, a blackened heart leaking, fuelling the fires of hell — then pivots to a courtroom-style declaration that the man they think they caught never truly existed, because what violence does to a person erases whoever was there before.","src":"haiku"},
"orbital~dirty-rat":{"haiku":"Political corruption and public complicity breed self-sabotage; voters enable systems that hollow out compassion while the state performs falsehood, leaving the heart perpetually bleeding.","opus":"A venomous electronic broadside at a self-sabotaging electorate and the state of Britain. Orbital rail against people who blame the vulnerable and drowned while voting against their own interests, framing national politics as espionage and betrayal, a rigged machine offering no consideration for the ordinary heart it bleeds.","sonnet":"A post-Brexit fury addressed directly to those who voted for the forces now scapegoating immigrants, defunding hospitals, and lying about everything: \"you voted for them, look at you, you dirty rat.\" The speaker names the pattern — blame the Channel, blame the NHS queue, blame anyone who doesn't look like a fried animal — as espionage carried out by the self-saboteur against their own heart.","src":"haiku","fable":"Dirty Rat is a Brexit-and-austerity fever built as two-voice ventriloquism, and the whole read hinges on hearing which mouth is open. The chorus is the mask: a snarling tabloid Everyman jabbing his finger at whoever he has been told to resent, and Orbital line up the targets so the scapegoating registers as a system rather than a grievance, moving from the sick to the drowned migrant to anyone who is not native-pale. The pivot names the con by turning the accusation back on the accuser: this is espionage against oneself, a nation talked into knifing its own institutions and calling it patriotism. Underneath, the second voice drops the sneer for something numb and bereaved, where the country is a lacquered rotten husk and love has soured into worthless plastic in a rigged cash dispenser, currency that no longer buys care. That last movement is the tell of the whole track, the heart bleeding not from a wound but from a separation nothing will acknowledge, so the closing litany reads less as a beat than as a body still asking to be counted.","fv":2,"fnote":"The snarl is Jason Williamson of Sleaford Mods, guesting — Orbital handing the tabloid-Everyman mask to the voice that spent a decade perfecting it.","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a nation talked into knifing its own institutions and calling it patriotism","built":"two-voice ventriloquism: the snarling tabloid mask and the numb bereaved voice beneath"},
"orchards~luv-you-2":{"haiku":"Lost love and generational disappointment compound; the speaker cannot tell an older generation the truth about betrayal, carrying silence as burden while witnessing repetition.","opus":"Raised to be decent, the singer can't bring himself to tell a new lover the truth about the ex who hurt him and didn't fight to keep him. It is about the residue of a love outgrown, needing the emotion only to express himself.","sonnet":"An admission of a love that has curdled into something the speaker can't confess aloud: someone told her what he did intentionally, and now he lives with the knowledge that what he'd say — \"I used to love you too\" — would destroy her, so he stays behind the cobwebs, choosing silence over the truth.","src":"haiku"},
"orchards~manic":{"haiku":"A speaker in sleepless, manic crisis feels suffocated by someone's control and their own inability to escape isolation; everyone perceives them as unstable while they insist they're managing.","opus":"A depiction of composure coming apart, the narrator done being played, wearing himself down while insisting he's fine through sleepless, unraveling days. The band balances the imagery between mania and breakdown, the person he loves holding him more tightly than a hangman's rope, affection and resentment knotted together, the wish to simply disappear cutting through his fragile assurances that all is well.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a manipulative relationship loses sleep, sanity, and finally the pretence of being fine. The exhaustion becomes total — sleepless nights, a tightrope walk through daily life, and the terrifying suspicion that love has always been withheld and always will be.","src":"haiku"},
"orchards~peggy":{"haiku":"Conflicted attachment to someone who drains and haunts the speaker. The silence and tension persist; the speaker cannot bear the thought of them leaving.","opus":"A relationship coming apart at the seams, one partner charged with deceit and squandering the other's hours, yet still asked to stay overnight because their silence is unbearable. The wish to be alright wrestles with the wish that they won't walk out. Orchards, the British math-pop group, weave that push and pull through bright, tangled guitar work so the hurt stays airy even as the rift grows.","sonnet":"A relationship held together by mutual unease — both people know something is broken, one living in evasion and the other in paralyzed longing. The speaker can feel the other's presence even in silence, doesn't want them to leave despite the lies, and keeps choosing the long road that avoids any final confrontation.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~all-the-same":{"opus":"An unnerving study of identity and mimicry, the singer insisting he could turn into the very person he's addressing, echoing their cruelty and appetites while onlookers keep swearing the two of them aren't alike. He ultimately wants to fold himself into that other so he can finally release them.","sonnet":"A blurring of the line between self and other — the narrator mirrors a destructive person, insisting there is no real difference between them, that everything is the same, and that the distinction others insist on is a fiction hiding in a picture that warps everyone around it.","src":"sonnet"},
"orgy~blue-monday":{"haiku":"Someone asks how they should feel after being treated poorly, confused about whether their own coldness or another's actions define their emotional response.","opus":"A cover foregrounding the original's ache—the repeated question of how it feels to be treated so coldly by a lover, the singer unsure even of his own feelings, waiting on the beach for a heart that's grown cold, needing to be told how he should feel.","sonnet":"A cover of the New Order classic about emotional displacement and powerlessness in a relationship — the narrator cannot locate his own feelings because someone else has always defined them, and ends waiting on a beach in cold silence.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~come-back":{"opus":"Contempt dressed as control, the narrator taunting an ex-lover he claims he can summon back at will. Orgy's glam-industrial sneer curdles into misogynist power play: fame dangled as bait, a child weaponized, misery courting company. Beneath the wordplay it is a portrait of a relationship reduced to leverage and mutual ruin.","sonnet":"A song about control and contempt in a relationship poisoned by a pregnancy the narrator explicitly rejects — he boasts he can pull her back whenever he wants, frames her as an actress on a mattress, and uses language that oscillates between sexual domination and outright cruelty. The bravado masks a hostile, transactional dynamic.","src":"sonnet"},
"orgy~dissention":{"haiku":"Friends betray by spreading gossip and turning confidence into weakness; a person watches from outside, jaded and disconnected from the false collective.","opus":"A takedown of duplicitous friends whose gossip erodes confidence and strips away others' dignity. The industrial-glam band frames cynical clarity as a virtue, watching foolish people spin in circles while the singer opts out. The blunt closing demand to quit wasting energy and move on reads as both dismissal and armor.","sonnet":"A person watches confidence weaponised against someone until nothing remains — social circles turn two-faced and the cycle of gossip and betrayal erodes every relationship. The bitter conclusion is not revenge but release: time spent on this kind of loyalty is time destroyed.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~dizzy":{"opus":"A scornful portrait of a formerly innocent woman now debased and self-destroying, reduced to one interchangeable body among many in a den of vice. The narrator taunts that she's ruined not only herself but everything within reach, contempt dressed up in the band's glossy industrial sneer.","sonnet":"A cruel deconstruction of someone who sold out or compromised themselves into catatonic irrelevance — what used to be naive and real has become a mess, and the song revels in watching the person spin out under the weight of their own poor choices.","src":"sonnet"},
"orgy~empty":{"haiku":"Running on empty; desire as commodity, love withheld until payment arrives and need is met.","opus":"A cold portrait of manipulation and emotional depletion in a game of desire and control. The narrator admits others see only the version of himself he chooses to reveal, wanting a lover on his own selfish terms while feeling utterly hollowed out, running on empty as an untold story quietly unravels.","sonnet":"A lover who only shows up when convenient — I want you when I want you, not when you need another fix — the blood runs cold, the heart is on hold, running on empty, and the speaker projects the psycho-alpha-bitter dynamic back onto the person who taught it to him, refusing to chase what is already gone.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~fetisha":{"haiku":"A woman is violated and corrupted by a collective force, leaving her broken and gone while others consume and exploit her.","opus":"A disorienting, uneasy piece about abuse and control, where a woman degraded by a crowd becomes an object of obsession and is then dismissed. Orgy, the Los Angeles industrial-glam act, render this ugly subject in the cold neon sheen of their late-nineties death-pop, framing exploitation as detached spectacle.","sonnet":"A character named Fetisha departs — the framing oscillates between violation and devotion, the song treating her as both object and force. She is leaving because she needed a change, needed friends, turned away from fear. The speaker eulogises her through the language of transgression: beloved perversion, departure that cannot be reversed.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~g-face":{"haiku":"A chaotic, hedonistic scene where people gamble, lose themselves, and perform a degraded identity, collapsing under pressure and delusion.","opus":"LA glam-industrial act Orgy sketch a gambler's nihilism, someone who created their own life then wrecked it, hiding behind a false calm while wagering everything and assuming dawn will erase the night. Under the flashy posturing, the narrator watches another comrade break down, discarded and left in a windowless haze.","sonnet":"A night of reckless gambling, blackouts, and mutual self-destruction between people who built something and burned it down. The demand to see the other's authentic expression — their real face beneath the performance — circles through scenes of sinking ships, dancing crowds, and mornings no one will remember.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~gender":{"haiku":"The narrator experiences fractured identity and struggles to communicate their true self while trapped in cycles of disbelief and incompleteness.","opus":"A restless narrator craving limitlessness yet never sense, distrusting a lover's words and confessing an urge to control everyone by folding them into a private fantasy of his own design. Orgy, the LA industrial-glam act, wrap themes of vanity, control, and permanent lack in synth-heavy sheen, boredom and desire orbiting a self that cannot be completed.","sonnet":"A restless, self-contradicting figure craves transcendence and connection while dismissing the possibility of being fully known or completed. The tension between wanting to be everything — infinite, cool, desired — and the knowledge that nothing can fix what is fundamentally incomplete drives a performance of hunger without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~karma-kastles":{"haiku":"Guilt manifests as elaborate self-imprisonment; the speaker constructs towers of consequence unable to escape inheritance.","opus":"A takedown of a status-chasing social climber whose lies and cruelty build a fragile fortress of self-delusion. The narrator, still hooked on them, watches with grim satisfaction as their conscience corners them.","sonnet":"Addressed to someone who chased stars on the boulevard, maxed their cards, played god with their thoughts, and gradually became the devil they once feared — they now hide in a karma castle of their own making, psychosomatic and erratic, unable to cure the war they started inside themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~opticon":{"haiku":"Media and fashion enforce conformity through manufactured trends, alienating older generations from new cultural forces and styles.","opus":"A glammy dystopian anthem about a youth generation that reinvents itself through style, faking substance for celebrity while their look alone convinces horrified elders they've gone mad. Orgy, the Los Angeles industrial band, weaponize fashion and image as a generational rupture the older world is too scared to understand.","sonnet":"A media-age satire delivered as a fashion broadcast — Opticon as the controlling signal that demands conformity, reinvention, and the disposal of whatever was authentic before it arrived. The parents are terrified, the style bomb detonates, and everyone is helpless and out of time. The song mocks the very mechanism it is packaged inside.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~pantomime":{"opus":"A sinister, voyeuristic narrative about dominating a young woman being molded into a rising figurehead, confined and shaped like a polished performance. She grows to relish what she's been taught and turns corrupt, blurring into a fantasy the narrator projects onto her, control masquerading as grooming.","sonnet":"A rebellious girl breaks out of the mold of who she's supposed to be — parents trying to restrict and control her, charm school expectations replaced by something more truthful and transgressive — and the narrator finds her transformation into something darker intensely compelling.","src":"sonnet"},
"orgy~platinum":{"haiku":"A tragic, solitary figure who evades definition and settlement. The speaker loves what is dirty and painful about another, desperate to stay close.","opus":"A study of someone cruel on purpose, doomed and walled-off, spoken to by a narrator magnetized precisely by that wreckage and wanting to be close to whatever painful thing owns them. The counsel sours into obsession, better to keep quiet, hoping for a resolution that never arrives. Orgy, the late-nineties industrial-glam act, glaze the codependent tug in slick synthetic gloss.","sonnet":"Attraction to someone fundamentally self-destructive and solitary — beautiful, tragic, and immune to ordinary comfort, they live alone by design and ask for nothing while quietly pulling the speaker in. The pull is precisely the damage: something dirty, something painful, and the speaker can't look away or offer anything useful.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~revival":{"haiku":"Control through false construction and manufactured desires; the speaker recognizes how systems manipulate through addiction and surveillance to prevent freedom.","opus":"Orgy, the Los Angeles industrial-glam band, imagine a life engineered and then exploited. The narrator describes being fabricated, trained to exist, dosed by a dealer, and discarded, left displaced and bitter, insisting only he grasps how badly he has been used. Manufactured, false existence set to cold electronic rock.","sonnet":"Jaded and nowhere, the speaker drifts through a world that shaped them with tools, fed them contraband, and scared them off. The bubble of others' reality — salivating at bells, seduced through confession — is observed from the outside. Friendships changed into something that would never let anyone leave, and the speaker died right there in front of everyone without quite being gone.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~social-enemies":{"haiku":"Judgmental bystanders and false saviors weaponize morality to shame and control others, presenting cruelty as righteousness. The song indicts sanctimonious social policing.","opus":"Industrial-glam act Orgy fire back at self-appointed saviors and moralizers here, rejecting anyone who claims to rescue the outcasts while branding them subservient. The narrator flips the charge, naming the accusers the real threat, the ones who manipulate the masses. Amid snakes, saints, and rabble-rousers runs a wounded thread of alienation beneath the defiance.","sonnet":"Outsiders branded as threatening or dysfunctional turn the accusation back on the enforcers of social norms, arguing it is the normalizers who destabilize by their relentless pressure to conform. The track sits inside a tension between collective outcry and a private voice asking why the game has to be played so bitterly at all.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~spells":{"haiku":"A seductive figure casts spells over others with dangerous kisses, the devil has enchanted them, but they don't kiss and tell.","opus":"Orgy, the Los Angeles industrial band, glaze a sleazy party invitation in synthetic sheen. The narrator claims to be under a wicked enchantment yet too discreet to confess the details, warning that technology mirrors human vice amid booze, energy drinks, and easy temptation. Seduction played half as danger, half as knowing self-parody.","sonnet":"Seduction as demonic contract — the devil got there first, via lips and alcohol and a spell the speaker cannot identify or resist. The danger is glamorous, the company twisted and intoxicating, and the whole night is framed as something that will win but also ruin. The speaker keeps the secret of what happened, not because they're ashamed, but because the telling is part of the power.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~stitches":{"haiku":"A twisted love affair cycles between devotion and hatred, with the lover described as beautiful yet deadly and deranged. The narrator binds themselves to the lover to fill emptiness, finding both thrill and destruction in the bond.","opus":"A toxic, obsessive attachment where the speaker both adores and hates a lover who clings to him, describing the relationship as a thrilling, self-destructive sickness.","sonnet":"The narrator is locked in a toxic, compulsive relationship — simultaneously drawn to and repelled by the other person, describing the bond as something that fills an emptiness even as it is destructive and deranged.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~suck-it":{"opus":"A hookup stripped of any pretense of feeling, the narrator explicit that sex and lies are all he offers. Orgy trades in nocturnal LA sleaze, the aftermath left on a pillow, a partner mistaken about being wanted. It reaches for nothing tender, staging desire as transaction and cruelty as a boast.","sonnet":"A song about a purely sexual encounter with zero emotional investment — the narrator picks up a woman at three in the morning and tells her explicitly that he does not want love, only sex, and she should not mistake his presence for anything more. The aggression is part of the seduction and part of the dismissal.","src":"sonnet"},
"orgy~talk-sick":{"haiku":"A manipulator uses control and toxicity to make others lose their minds, moving closer to exploit and dominate.","opus":"Orgy's LA industrial-glam sneer drives a predatory come-on hinged on a pun, the narrator vowing to draw near and make you sick, or toxic, as a reckoning looms. Torture, dominance and one final tryst mingle with imagery of money and vice, charm and contempt fused into a poisonous flirtation.","sonnet":"Control dressed as seduction — get closer, listen to the clock tick, let me make you say things you would not say. The song moves through a Los Angeles of money and desire and bad faith, every transaction slightly predatory, every pleasure slightly poisoned. The speaker is the one holding the leash and advertising it openly.","src":"haiku"},
"orgy~wide-awake-and-dead":{"haiku":"Emotional numbness and zombie-like existence despite being awake, as numbing substances and disconnection replace genuine feeling.","opus":"A portrait of two numbed, indifferent people medicating themselves through a loveless, cyclical relationship. The refrain likens the emotionally deadened partner to a zombie, wide awake yet spiritually dead.","sonnet":"Two people numb themselves with substances and distractions in a loveless, parasitic relationship, going through the motions of living while functioning like zombies — present but emotionally absent.","src":"haiku"},
"origami-button~cruise-control":{"haiku":"Rediscovered purpose and momentum drive recovery as the speaker escapes depression through intentional engagement.","opus":"A recovery anthem about climbing out of depression and back into momentum. Using the image of returning to the driver's seat, the narrator finds purpose reignites his will to live, energy flooding back as opportunity calls. He fights the lingering pull of despair, choosing to wake from the nightmare and carry himself home.","sonnet":"Recovering from depression and creative paralysis, the speaker rediscovers momentum — \"found my heart where it used to be\" — and drives forward with renewed purpose, still fighting the illness but bouncing it out of mind with movement and gratitude.","src":"haiku"},
"origa~inner-universe":{"haiku":"Eternal seeking pursues meaning beyond comprehension; the speaker wanders between worlds, calling across dimensions for connection amid endless transformation.","opus":"A meditation on selfhood and solitude threaded through Russian, English and invented Latin, angels and demons circling a soul that strives to stay itself and not fall in flight. It's a yearning to keep dreaming and loving through an endless run toward a place of deeper knowing.","sonnet":"A multilingual meditation — Russian and invented ritual language woven together — on angels and demons circling above, the price of staying true to oneself (\"soboj ostatsya dolshe\" / to remain oneself longer), and the runner who, while alive, refuses to fall or stop dreaming.","src":"haiku"},
"original-god~feed-me-more-lies":{"haiku":"Relationship collapse and mutual accusation; both drive each other to madness with dishonesty, and the singer vows to abandon a lover who refuses truth, leaving behind only pain and demons.","opus":"A raw, ugly breakup spiral tangling love, hatred, sex and betrayal, addressing a partner who saw through him while he drove them both to ruin, demons and family wreckage bleeding into a plea that curdles into contempt.","sonnet":"A song about a toxic relationship built on dishonesty and mutual harm — the narrator cycles between rage and need, accuses the woman of seeing through him while lying to him, and ends up threatening that she will rot alone when he is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"original-god~you-don-t-need-me":{"haiku":"Betrayal and emotional violence destroy attachment as the speaker recognizes their own expendability.","opus":"A savage bilingual descent into abandonment, self-loathing, and violent fantasy. Cast off by someone who never cared, the narrator spirals from wounded rejection into grotesque, cannibalistic imagery of consuming the one who discarded him, wrestling God and demons as the refrain hammers home the crushing verdict that he was never needed.","sonnet":"Alternating between English rejection and Japanese imagery of deception and sacrifice — \"fooled by fate, crucified\" — the speaker declares the other person never needed them while the final verses spiral into violent, cannibal-edged dissolution. (lang: ja/en)","src":"haiku"},
"orthodox~soaking-nerves":{"haiku":"Dissolution of identity through accumulated decay; the speaker's disgrace settles into bone while learning that strength exists only through connection, and forgiveness demands completion of transgression.","opus":"A defiant purge of shame, faith, and inherited moral conditioning. The narrator sheds old disguises and the numbness he mistook for pride, rejecting the teaching that hatred is forbidden. Orthodox frame god as a sight fit only for dead eyes, the track a raw refusal to keep swallowing what he no longer believes.","sonnet":"A speaker strips away pride and disguise — \"soaking nerves that don't bear any weight\" — declaring that god is only a sight for dead eyes and that the rot swarms around until only the grit of your teeth and the sins you complete define you.","src":"haiku"},
"osaka-punch~hall-of-shame":{"haiku":"Desperate reclamation of power after yielding control to another; the speaker batters against constraints with rising fury and psychological breakdown.","opus":"A theatrical, wisecracking kiss-off to a cowardly rival who takes and then flees. Osaka Punch pile on kung-fu and time-travel bravado while the narrator seethes at being left holding the burden, choking on his own expectations, crowning the deserter the top name in a hall of cowards for his empty gamesmanship.","sonnet":"A speaker who gave all their power away reclaims it against someone who left nothing but a five-dollar bill and a high-five to the face, operating at the top of their all-star hall of shame. The revenge fantasy is kung-fu speed and 88-miles-per-hour reversal.","src":"haiku"},
"osi~radiologue":{"haiku":"Longing and distance collapse through radio waves; the speaker journeys away but holds onto transmission, speaking through static to stay connected across unbridgeable space.","opus":"A voyager adrift at sea, feverish and stuck between no way forward and no way back, straining to hear a lover's voice through radio static. It is about distance and disconnection, throwing overboard the message he can't decipher.","sonnet":"A sailor heading west through fever and storms reaches a point of paralysis — \"can't go on, can't go back\" — and when a voice breaks through the radio noise he finds it too painful to decode, throwing the message overboard rather than confront what the person at home is saying.","src":"haiku"},
"otoboke-beaver~anata-watashi-daita-ato-yome-no-meshi":{"haiku":"A provocative rant about betrayal and misuse—the singer's essence consumed and discarded by an ungrateful partner, dressed in crude sexual humor.","opus":"A ferocious Japanese punk tirade against being used and discarded like leftovers, the title likening a woman to scraps tossed in the kitchen's waste corner after sex. Otoboke Beaver rage at being reduced to another's meal, hurling jealousy in the trash and refusing to be consumed, with anarchic, screamed defiance.","sonnet":"A woman rages at a man who sleeps with her and then goes home to eat his wife's cooking — she is the mistress being used as flavor, as emotional stock for someone else's domestic life. The refrain 'his wife's rice on the kitchen drain corner' becomes a symbol of her degradation, and she seethes that she refuses to be the broth from which he extracts someone else's pure love.","src":"haiku"},
"otoboke-beaver~chu-chu-song":{"haiku":"Two lost selves orbit each other in bed, pretending intimacy while naming mutual hatred. Years of self-erasure mask self-discovery; they play at love knowing nothing of each other, yet can't escape the coupling.","opus":"A frantic punk dissection of a love that is all surface and no understanding. After a decade of searching, two lovers keep tumbling into bed while the narrator insists they know nothing real about each other. Otoboke Beaver swing between hating, fearing, and adoring the partner, chasing a fated red thread that may not exist.","sonnet":"Ten years into searching for themselves and hiding themselves, two people keep ending up in bed together, kissing in love-sickness — yet the speaker despises the partner for knowing nothing about her while being hopelessly in love with them; the contradiction between fear of not knowing the other and the full-body desire to love them anyway drives the song to a breathless, repeating climax.","src":"haiku"},
"otoboke-beaver~datsu-hikage-no-onna":{"haiku":"Hatred and cyclical despair; erasure and emotional evasion trapped in repetition.","opus":"A blistering revolt against being kept as the accommodating other woman, forever in the shadows. Otoboke Beaver channel fury at repeated disposal, oscillating between wanting escape and wanting to burn it all down, cursing the lover with ruin while vowing, over pummeling punk, to break free of the shadow-woman role at last.","sonnet":"A woman who has been erased and discarded by men who keep a current girlfriend while keeping her in shadow rages from the position of the convenient, understanding other woman — the titular hikage no onna — cataloguing broken hearts as déjà vu and finally declaring emancipation: datsuu, escape from the shadow. Fury accumulates until the wish to destroy him financially and romantically breaks through the performed compliance.","src":"haiku"},
"otoboke-beaver~don-t-light-my-fire":{"haiku":"Aftermath of emotional ignition left unresolved—set hearts ablaze then vanished, leaving one side alone to extinguish the damage and live with the scars.","opus":"Furious at a lover who ignites desire then leaves you to deal with the aftermath alone. The demand is blunt: if you light the fire, put it out before you go, or go to hell.","sonnet":"A furious Japanese song about being emotionally ignited and then abandoned — if you set someone's heart on fire, you are responsible for putting it out before you leave, and failing to do so earns you a one-way trip to hell.","src":"haiku"},
"otoboke-beaver~love-is-short":{"haiku":"Life's brevity and love's fleeting nature shape mortality; transience and finitude override permanence.","opus":"A raucous punk manifesto declaring love a battle won by walkover and never worth the fight. Otoboke Beaver reduce romance to a fleeting skirmish, brandishing the traditional letter of divorce as a weapon, insisting love is short and the road ahead pitch dark, all delivered as gleeful, breakneck defiance.","sonnet":"A furious divorce declaration in miniature — \"sangyobantai\" (the three-and-a-half-line dismissal notice) is issued in a battle of wills, the narrator refusing to keep chewing on what she can't bite through, and the refrain \"love is short\" is both the complaint and the verdict. The Tokyo no-wave energy turns a legal document into a chant of liberation.","src":"haiku"},
"otoboke-beaver~s-il-vous-plait":{"haiku":"Casual love spirals into indispensable coupling; two unremarkable people intertwine despite mutual dismissal. Politeness becomes incantation as they orbit each other, bound by strings they refuse to cut.","opus":"A hyperactive punk sketch of infatuation as something cheap and expiring, love likened to bargain-bin peanuts past their date. Otoboke Beaver skewer the absurdity of clinging to a half-baked romance, careening between polite greetings and threats of violence, unable to let go of an attraction they know is dubious.","sonnet":"A chaotic, exasperated address to a careless partner — compared to ordinary peanuts and pork bits — in which the speaker admits helpless attachment despite being cold-shouldered, clings to the other's weather-forecast moods, and peppers every exchange with \"s'il vous plait\" as both polite plea and barely contained threat.","src":"haiku"},
"otto-s-daughter~my-own-worst-enemy":{"haiku":"Internal conflict and self-sabotage as the primary enemy, confronting personal capacity for destruction and inability to escape culpability.","opus":"A face-off with self-sabotage and the adversary in the mirror. The narrator names herself the real obstacle, afraid of what lives inside her, mourning everything she might have been amid ash and ruined hopes. Yet the song refuses total defeat, insisting she count the fractures and keep returning.","sonnet":"Self-sabotage recognized too late — ashes falling from the sky, a beautiful wreck in the mirror, all the could-haves and should-haves gone. The final turn admits the real enemy isn't fear or failure but the love that's doing the killing, and still insists this isn't over.","src":"haiku"},
"ottto~my-pain":{"haiku":"Emotional self-flagellation masks an addictive dependency—pain becomes both problem and solution, where internal fragmentation spirals through confessed desire and refusal to break free.","opus":"A murky, hypnotic reckoning with pain as identity and addiction. The narrator asks whether another can take his suffering away while admitting he cannot lie about loving deception, sensing his hurt as an all-directional infection buried within. Bassist Tye Trujillo's project frames damage as something risen from darkness and impossible to fully escape.","sonnet":"A speaker pleads for someone to take away their pain, caught between desire and dependency — the alleyway becomes a recurring site of lingering rather than escape, while the admission 'I love deception' turns the plea inward, implicating the self as both wound and cause.","src":"haiku"},
"otu~scatman":{"haiku":"Stuttering and scat are one; the speaker celebrates overcoming stigma through artistic expression.","opus":"Scatman John's stutter turned into triumph, reframing an impediment as the very thing that makes his voice, and telling every listener held back by their own flaw that they can do it too. A cover keeping the original's quiet defiance beneath the nonsense syllables.","sonnet":"Scat singing is reframed as a philosophy of personal liberation: the Scatman's stutter and his scat are the same thing — obstacles become art — and the song's message is that whatever holds you back can become the very medium through which you express yourself, aimed directly at anyone society has marginalised or silenced.","src":"haiku"},
"our-hollow-our-home~burn-it-bury-it":{"haiku":"A person accelerating toward self-destruction rejects both suppression and denial, choosing to burn rather than bury their fractured self. They accept consequences while seeking their own reckoning.","opus":"The British metalcore band frames grief as excavation, tracing a wound to its origin and burying the parts of himself that a loss destroyed. Unstoppable momentum crashes against a longing to slow down, and he'd sooner be consumed by fire than interred, willing to sacrifice everything to make peace with it. The exit, it concludes, is the passage itself.","sonnet":"Grief tears through a person like a freight train off its rails — out of control, digging at the roots of what was lost. Caught between burying the pain and letting it consume him, he chooses the fire over the grave, insisting that burning is more honest than the slow wither of silent, unacknowledged tragedy.","src":"haiku"},
"our-last-night~fire-in-the-streets":{"haiku":"The song describes civil unrest manufactured by hidden powers who benefit from engineered opposition and information overload.","opus":"A protest song aimed less at the crowd than at whoever profits from the chaos, streets burning while a shadowy them chant order out of chaos and manufacture the opposition. Two sides, one coin, faceless power as the endgame. Our Last Night, the New Hampshire metalcore band known for their covers, channel their melodic bite into conspiracy-tinged anger about societies kept comatose by information overload.","sonnet":"Civil unrest read as manufactured — the fire in the streets isn't spontaneous rage but an engineered crisis, incivility stoked by unseen hands who profit from a population too overwhelmed by information to identify the real enemy. Both sides of the fight are faces on the same coin, and the people burning are the only ones losing.","src":"haiku"},
"our-last-night~golden-hour":{"haiku":"Two lovers suspend themselves in perfect timeless intimacy, where she becomes radiant and glittering, slowing time itself through her presence.","opus":"A radiant snapshot of young love at its most idyllic, a couple parked under sunset colors and lost in one another. Our Last Night's cover recasts pop reverie as devotion, the beloved rendered as glittering, glowing light, time itself slowing so the fleeting perfection can stretch on.","sonnet":"Two lovers parked in a car listening to Blonde at sunset, feet on the dash, burning through summer — she has glitter for skin, she is the radiant beam — and the golden hour is less a time of day than the quality of attention a person can command when they slow everything down just by existing.","src":"haiku"},
"our-oceans~face-them":{"haiku":"The song commands confronting inner demons and shame directly, watching them wither when exposed to light.","opus":"A psychological exorcism in which crushing emotions like shame and self-hatred are pictured as tormentors demeaning a fragile inner self that only craves love. The advice is to meet them without flinching so they lose their power. Our Oceans, the delicate project of ex-Cynic members, set this reckoning to lush progressive textures.","sonnet":"The internal voices of shame and self-loathing addressed directly — swarms of smothering words that have turned a person into a puppet dancing in sorrow. The song's stance is confrontational: face those voices with a smile, watch them wither in the light, refuse to worship shadow gods or be chained by the need for love that comes with strings attached. Burn the strings.","src":"haiku"},
"our-oceans~precarious":{"haiku":"A dreamer reaches upward from uncertainty, seeking connection to falling stars and the sublime while acknowledging internal chaos is necessary for transcendence. Hope balances against existential precarity.","opus":"Post-rock reverie of reaching skyward for something unattainable and finding only solitude under the moon. The Nietzschean nod frames that inner turbulence as the very condition for creating something luminous.","sonnet":"A contemplative song about longing and isolation — reaching for something beautiful and unreachable, standing on a rooftop between dreaming and waking, needing internal chaos to give birth to something worth living for.","src":"haiku"},
"our-oceans~unravel":{"haiku":"Unraveling layers of hidden protection reveals authentic self beneath stitched-on illusions; fear and pain dissolve as primal roots burst through the surface.","opus":"The former Cynic vocalist's meditation on shedding the self-protective persona woven over a lifetime, the terrifying relief of finally being seen without disguise; ego death staged as a tapestry coming undone thread by thread.","sonnet":"A song about stripping away the constructed self — the narrator has spent a lifetime hiding behind a carefully woven false identity, and now the fabric is giving way, exposing raw, untouched vulnerability that is terrifying and liberating at once.","src":"haiku"},
"our-oceans~weeping-lead":{"haiku":"The speaker describes internal pain as sculptural and necessary, draining their veins to forge something meaningful through suffering.","opus":"A slow yielding to sorrow and the passage of time, molten grief shaped like metal into fire, veins emptying, everything narrowing to bleak survival until release. The image of forging time itself carries the resignation. Our Oceans, the project of guitarist Tymon Kruidenier, favor atmospheric, mournful progressive textures, making pain something patiently molded rather than fought.","sonnet":"Chronic suffering rendered as a kind of heavy metallurgy — veins drained, dread fed into the mire, grief sculpted into something almost solid. Every sensation is filtered through exhaustion and decay, the speaker enduring time rather than living it, bleeding and weeping only to forge the hands of the clock that mark the hours until leaving.","src":"haiku"},
"our-promise~buried":{"haiku":"Isolation and psychological torment deepen through cycles of shame and rejection; the narrator seeks burial as escape from perpetual humiliation and erasure.","opus":"Metalcore about being crushed under others' judgment until you'd rather have been buried than misunderstood. The core wound is isolation, retreating into your own head as the only free place while begging to be understood before it's too late.","sonnet":"A person ground down by others' cruelty and dismissal retreats into their own mind as the only place of freedom, fighting to survive the misunderstanding and self-loathing with nothing but the desperate wish to be understood — and the fear that they will already be dead inside before that happens.","src":"haiku"},
"our-promise~evoke":{"haiku":"The speaker breaks free from stagnation through confrontation with truth; rebuilding reality requires abandoning false narratives and reclaiming agency.","opus":"Being trapped in the ordinary, defining oneself by failures and procrastination while begging for the willpower to change. Medication and numbness only reinforce the sense that nothing ever really shifts.","sonnet":"A person stuck in ordinary procrastination and overthinking — 'disassembling every detail,' medicated beneath it all — tries to let go of a self defined by failures to achieve, rebuilding reality and demanding to be levitated above the ordinary even as they admit some things never change.","src":"haiku"},
"our-promise~fiftyfive":{"haiku":"Internal warfare between fragmentation and survival dominates the psyche, with the speaker clawing toward autonomy against compulsive forces that resist escape.","opus":"Naming oneself an outcast as both wound and refuge, finding a fragile peace in the dark while fighting to surface from a lie that keeps pulling under.","sonnet":"A confession of feeling like an outcast living a lie — the speaker is fighting to surface from inner chaos, blames themselves and an unnamed antagonist in equal measure, and asserts that no one can take their identity from them even as they struggle to find it.","src":"haiku"},
"our-promise~satisfiction":{"haiku":"Dissatisfaction and stagnation spiral as the speaker resists what others demand; surrender to internal demons feels inevitable.","opus":"A stalled life spent maintaining an illusion, going nowhere while asking whether anyone is truly satisfied or merely surviving. The speaker threatens to sink the whole vessel and drag the other under, escape and destruction fused.","sonnet":"A person trapped in disillusionment and mental stagnation — they float in a made-up illusion, feel chewed up by something they can't name, and cycle between wanting to be lifted out and threatening to drag everything down with them as corruption grows in the dark.","src":"haiku"},
"our-promise~stay-away":{"haiku":"Someone asserts independence and refusal to be shaped by another person's influence; they reject mimicry and broken promises, holding firm to their own dreams.","opus":"In melodic metalcore's mode of defiant self-assertion, this cuts off someone whose influence had hollowed the narrator into a person he didn't recognize. The refrain draws a line: he refuses to yield or shatter, and rejects anyone else deciding his path. The breakdown turns savage, unmasking a two-faced hypocrite, while the singer clings to the ambitions he's pursuing toward something real.","sonnet":"Someone who was diminished by another person's influence reasserts their own identity and ambitions with unflinching clarity. They accuse the other of hypocrisy, of never living up to their own preaching, and draw a hard line: no more bending, no more breaking — the dreams being chased are theirs alone to keep.","src":"haiku"},
"our-promise~the-worst":{"haiku":"The speaker struggles to live with intrusive thoughts and mental deterioration, finding nothing works despite effort while questioning whether they will survive the internal chaos. It's an urgent plea from someone spiraling into perceived madness.","opus":"A metalcore confrontation with invasive self-hatred and the terror of losing one's mind to constant feelings of not being enough. It pivots toward endurance and solidarity, an appeal to anyone who feels invisible to keep battling the conflict raging within.","sonnet":"About battling the darkest, most self-defeating part of your own mind — insecurity, the feeling of not being enough — and the desperate question of whether you can survive your own interior war.","src":"haiku"},
"outline-in-color~bottlerat":{"haiku":"Shallow connections and calculated manipulation accumulate; the speaker absorbs and discards people as distraction from deeper emptiness.","opus":"Contempt for a two-faced, clout-chasing user, exposing her lies and betrayals while the singer sets an internal countdown to explode over the heartbreak she caused.","sonnet":"A contemptuous portrait of a manipulative woman who pursues the narrator purely for social clout and status — sleeping around, taking pills, and using people for what she can get — while the second half turns into a more personal betrayal narrative about someone who wrapped the narrator in lies until he cut her out entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"outline-in-color~breaking-the-silence":{"haiku":"The speaker breaks free from a damaging relationship where they were unsupported, finally standing on their own terms.","opus":"A romance falling apart over the phone, the dead air between two people speaking louder than anything said as they trade sighs and stall over who ends the call. The narrator commits to being himself, refusing the blame, and finally choosing to walk after years of feeling insufficient. Outline in Color drive this arc of self-assertion through their clean-and-scream dynamics.","sonnet":"A relationship ending over a bad phone call that says everything without saying it — the silence on the line more honest than anything spoken. The speaker has been diminished, their fears mocked, their dreams unsupported, and now it is their turn to leave. Breaking the silence means ending the long habit of staying where they were never enough.","src":"haiku"},
"outline-in-color~fight-fair":{"haiku":"Struggle and fairness collapse as the speaker and opponent exist in fundamental opposition.","opus":"A weary indictment of a relationship poisoned by unfairness, cruelty, and mental exhaustion. Plagued by fever dreams and self-hatred, the narrator tries to find the good in others yet senses only hostility, sick of being stuck in one spot. His hard-won detachment, the refusal to give a reaction, becomes his only shield.","sonnet":"Caught between self-loathing and the frustration of an adversary who won't engage honestly, the narrator oscillates between trying to see the good in everyone and recognising someone is simply out for blood. The recurring nightmare and the refusal to be provoked harden into exhausted indifference — some days I don't care becoming both shield and surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"outline-in-color~snakebit":{"haiku":"The speaker escapes a toxic relationship characterized by betrayal and dismissal, expressing suicidal ideation and deep dissociation while asserting their determination to break free from a hell they stayed in too long. The song documents recovery and the cost of leaving.","opus":"Escaping a toxic, hellish relationship that left the speaker cold, dissociated, and clinging to life; he resolves he's done with someone who lied and gave up, even as he wrestles with suicidal despair and feeling that his fate is doom.","sonnet":"The narrator severs ties with a person who left them hollow and trapped in a cycle of harm — acknowledging that staying would mean death, doubting the other's claimed changes, and clinging to the will to live even while feeling dissociated and cursed by fate.","src":"haiku"},
"outloved~blind-falling":{"opus":"The vertigo of tumbling for someone who wrecks him, the narrator oblivious to how fast he's dropping, unable to rest and adrift in a fantasy that isn't real. He longs to flee with her while dreading that the whole thing meant nothing on her end.","sonnet":"About being so overwhelmed by attraction to someone that the narrator cannot see the destruction it causes — falling into their eyes like oceans, unable to breathe from the start, losing grip on daydreams and hope while questioning whether the other person ever thought of them at all.","src":"sonnet"},
"outsider-heart~leper-s-bell":{"opus":"A depiction of self-isolation and disintegration, the narrator deliberately chasing loneliness, drinking, disappearing in the eyes of loved ones, and watching their partner's gaze become a mirror of their own falling apart.","sonnet":"About social isolation becoming self-reinforcing — the narrator withdraws from connection, drinks, disappears from the people they love, and is consumed by their own reflection in others' eyes, finding loneliness not just inevitable but something they are actively chasing.","src":"sonnet"},
"overkill~bring-me-the-night":{"haiku":"The speaker embraces chaos and recklessness, rejecting restraint and decorum to act freely without regard for consequences.","opus":"Veteran thrash swagger, a defiant refusal of rules and reputation-games in favor of pure combustible release. Flying and dying blur into one reckless embrace of living loud on your own terms.","sonnet":"A reckless, adrenaline-soaked declaration of readiness — ready to fly, ready to die, addicted to the ride, daring the angels and devils to keep up while he burns through the night.","src":"haiku"},
"overkill~endless-war":{"haiku":"A soldier endures chronic warfare, emotional burnout, and degradation, yearning for escape and catharsis while trapped in an endless cycle of violence and servitude.","opus":"Existence recast as a nonstop battle shackled to cash and creeping illness, a worn-down fighter tallying his remaining days while pleading for one shining vision. The war of the title is simply being alive, beaten down yet still craving a moment in the light.","sonnet":"A soldier-as-metaphor — chained to money, jaded, wounded, and fading — who keeps fighting anyway, pushing through exhaustion to give everything left and carve out something worth living for before the endless conflict consumes them entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"overkill~give-a-little":{"haiku":"Power dynamics require dominance and submission; the strong take what they need from the weak who lack will to resist.","opus":"A jaded pact with ruin: perched at the brink of damnation, the narrator dares a feeble-willed listener to submit and obey to the point of death. Between boasts of reckless excess and mocking the ceiling of the heavens, it becomes a graveside come-on to swap your very self for a fleeting jolt of pleasure.","sonnet":"A darkly ironic song about dominance and submission — the speaker addresses someone with a weaker will and invites them to surrender themselves completely, framing the exchange as a fair trade that ends in the submissive party's total depletion and death.","src":"haiku"},
"overkill~i-hear-black":{"haiku":"Paranoia and mind control fragment perception while invisible forces hunt the subject; reality dissolves into hallucination and psychological warfare.","opus":"A first-person plunge into paranoid psychosis, dogs speaking, hooks pulling, a voice repeating in the head that has no mercy. Thrash used to inhabit mental collapse from the inside rather than describe it from outside.","sonnet":"A paranoid, fragmented mental breakdown — the speaker hears voices through dogs, feels watched, and is consumed by a black sound repeating in his head, the accumulated pressure of a world gone insane rotting him from the inside out.","src":"haiku"},
"overkill~in-vain":{"haiku":"Religion offers false comfort and promises salvation while people live in suffering and shame, with prayer and ritual meant to ease pain but accomplishing nothing. No victory comes from this faith.","opus":"A jaundiced view of religion as a painkiller, dangling comfort, absolution, and payback to soothe hurt and shame while your burial was arranged the day you were born. The subtext is that faith is a soothing scam peddling salvation, the singer posing as the very air keeping you alive until you die, doubting any of it was worth the cost.","sonnet":"A fire-and-brimstone motivational address that blurs the line between a preacher and a predator — urging the listener to use their God-given gifts and keep the end in sight, while the repeated refrain of \"no victory in vain\" and the escalating violence of the imagery suggest that the speaker's religion might cost more than it gives.","src":"haiku"},
"overkill~ironbound":{"haiku":"Hardened criminals and their loyal crew persist in their code—never backing down, staying bound together through ritual and oath.","opus":"A thrash anthem of outlaw brotherhood and momentum, riding hard and never looking back, where the automatic green-and-black friends that rule the night double as the band's own instruments and loyal crew.","sonnet":"A defiant celebration of loyalty and brotherhood — living outside the law, breaking bones, keeping oaths to the end — where the narrator's true friends are his weapons and his bandmates, and they hold each other together.","src":"haiku"},
"overkill~killing-for-a-living":{"haiku":"A professional killer stalks through night, hunting prey and taking lives as profession and obsession blur; the cycle of violence defines existence and living.","opus":"A dense, almost mystical character study of a hired assassin who treats slaughter as a sacred calling, existing solely to kill and killing solely to exist. The cosmic, fatalistic language frames murder-for-hire as a lonely, self-devouring vocation, carried out unseen while the universe rushes toward its own end.","sonnet":"A philosophical meditation on the assassin or soldier as an archetype — someone who has made killing their profession finds it paradoxically connected to a deeper truth about living, bleeding nations, and the blurred line between taking life and the compulsion to go on living.","src":"haiku"},
"overkill~the-goal-is-your-soul":{"haiku":"Religious damnation and temptation prey on human souls, with fallen angels and vipers seducing people toward evil. Forgiveness and blessings prove useless against the goal of claiming souls.","opus":"A dark cautionary sermon in which a fallen deceiver comes hunting the listener's soul, dangling temptation and promising damnation. Overkill's twist is disillusionment: the pledged life of blessings never paid off, and beyond the fraying edge of sanity, the devil's single unchanging objective remains claiming your soul.","sonnet":"A hellfire sermon about the devil's desire to claim human souls — framed through the imagery of the fallen viper and hot damnation, the song argues that temptation is inexhaustible and that the eternal struggle between salvation and corruption ultimately has only one prize: your soul.","src":"haiku","fable":"Blitz stages the track as a devil's pitch, hijacking the Exodus plea to free the captives and aiming it the wrong way, so a chant of deliverance curls into a summons toward ruin and appetite. The seduction runs on accounting: a churchgoing lifetime tallied like a wrapped gift, faith spent betting on a distant light, piety recast as a ruined ledger whose only payout is the refrain's blunt claim on what the tempter wants. That closing drone erodes everything to one repeated word, performing the very trade it narrates, until nothing survives to name aloud but the prize at the end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"piety recast as a ruined ledger whose only payout is the tempter's claim on the soul","built":"the closing drone erodes everything to one repeated word, performing the very trade it narrates"},
"overkill~the-green-and-black":{"haiku":"A band of misfits bound by madness, secrets, and filthy loyalty descend into their own abyss. Once in, you're never getting out—the green and black colors mark membership in chaos.","opus":"A vow of brotherhood scarred into an outlaw-crew identity, bloodstains worn like badges, the promise that once you're in there's no going back. Blitz turns loyalty and secrecy into a lifelong pact you can never really leave.","sonnet":"An initiation into something permanent and corrupting — once you're in you're never going back, filthy secrets kept until death — the green and black functioning as a mark of belonging to a pack that lives by madness and accepts no exits.","src":"haiku"},
"overkill~the-head-and-heart":{"haiku":"A dualistic confrontation between rational mind and suffering heart, where the head is death and darkness while the heart is beginning and pain, exploring the conflict between intellect and emotion.","opus":"Death personified addresses the living as its necessary opposite, cold intellect shadowing warm feeling. It's a duet of inevitability, the reaper narrating how it stalks every fall and every breath while the invocation of a fallen Roman peace signals endless night ahead.","sonnet":"A duality split: the head is dead, cold, sinful, the wind — the heart is alive, beginning, the rain; the narrator personifies the destructive rational force addressing the vulnerable emotional one, promising to shadow and occasionally break it.","src":"haiku"},
"overkill~the-src":{"haiku":"A subterranean resistance cult rejects surface talk and deceit, diving deep into underground defiance. Cheap lies above; real power in the depths below where the resistance dwells.","opus":"A rallying cry for going underground against a world where the talk is cheap, a subterranean resistance built on grip, sin and refusal to explain yourself. Pure thrash defiance, sinking deep beneath a surface that offers nothing.","sonnet":"A call to join a subterranean resistance cult — stop talking about results, get underground, grip what matters like your life depends on it — the song is a recruitment anthem for those who've given up on surface-level discourse.","src":"haiku"},
"overkill~wrecking-crew":{"haiku":"Organized destruction becomes identity and vocation; violence spreads indiscriminately as the wrecking crew levels all hierarchy and structure in sight.","opus":"Pure thrash bravado, the band as an unstoppable demolition crew leaving towns in ruins. It's a self-mythologizing anthem where destruction is the whole identity and the swagger is the point.","sonnet":"A heavy-metal boast of pure destructive force — the Wrecking Crew exist to demolish everything in their path, physical and psychological, and their power is absolute, gleeful, and without moral concern.","src":"haiku"},
"oversize~salt":{"haiku":"Bitterness and corrosion damage what remains; salt preserves and poisons simultaneously.","opus":"A muted study of grief, paralysis, and the weight carried in silence. The narrator wrestles with tension that chokes his words and a loss he cannot bear to face, memory dissolving as salt washes everything away. He watches those once close suddenly rise and leave, absorbing the painful erosion alone.","sonnet":"Loss witnessed at a distance — someone the narrator watched from far away suddenly gets up and leaves, and the salt (tears, sea, wound-cleansing) washes away a memory that was already a painful weight. The image of tension choking the speaker and blindness hiding the light frame grief as something that arrived before the departure.","src":"haiku"},
"ovtlier~bulletproof":{"haiku":"Revolutionaries declare themselves immune to coercion and poisoned ideology, ready to burn down corrupt institutions and refuse slavery.","opus":"An apocalyptic rally to raze the corrupt world and rebuild from scratch. The band pictures the exploited refusing to obey greedy rulers and a false liberator selling a fake freedom, then hardens defiance into an unstoppable, invulnerable refrain. The logic is that only after everything is torn down does true freedom become possible.","sonnet":"A group consciousness refuses to be broken by systems of control built on lies and exploitation. The anger is collective and incendiary — burning the machine down and building nothing in its place is framed as the only sane response to a world run by those who feed poison and call it freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"oxymorrons~last-call":{"haiku":"Substance dependency masks existential emptiness; the speaker drowns in alcohol and isolation, aware their self-destruction is predictable yet unable to arrest the spiral.","opus":"A night of heavy drinking that admits itself as self-medication for depression and grief, circling toward the confession of needing help. Chasing shots to avoid the man in the mirror.","sonnet":"Drunk and alone at last call, the speaker mixes drinks and emotions, ignores his mother's calls, and confesses to depression and daily substance use he doesn't want to face in the mirror — a wasted night that is really every night.","src":"haiku"},
"oy~hope":{"haiku":"Diaspora struggle demands collective resistance and armed reclamation; political agency emerges through shared ethnic commitment to return.","opus":"A layered reckoning with Jewish longing for a homeland, weaving militant Yiddish verses invoking Jabotinsky with the language of Hatikvah. The Ghana-Swiss duo Oy set defiant oaths of liberation against the ancient yearning toward Zion, holding hope and its costs in uneasy tension while gazing toward Jerusalem and David's city.","sonnet":"A Yiddish oath of defiance opens the song — swearing to leave exile and reclaim the land with blood — then transitions to a Hebrew recitation of Hatikva: the ancient longing to return to Zion anchors a militant Zionist declaration. (lang: yi/he)","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~black-rain":{"haiku":"Military death and sacrifice multiply endlessly while politicians remain detached from consequence, leaving mothers to grieve soldiers dying for forgotten causes.","opus":"Ozzy's blunt anti-war lament from the mid-2000s Iraq era, flag-draped coffins and children marched off to die in the sand. That fallout is both moral and literal, and he lays the body count squarely on the politicians.","sonnet":"A stark anti-war meditation on the human cost of military conflict, questioning why politicians keep sending the young to die in deserts while the body count climbs and no victory comes.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~civilize-the-universe":{"haiku":"Civilization masks inherited violence and systemic injustice through false morality, while wars and conformity perpetuate suffering under the guise of progress.","opus":"A bleak view of humanity as its own worst enemy, freedom called an invention and faith weaponized to justify obscenity. The refrain about civilizing the whole universe is weary irony, watching things go from bad to worse while still alive to see it.","sonnet":"A disillusioned observer watches civilization's recurring failure — wars, false confessions, religious manipulation, herd mentality — and wonders why humanity keeps attempting to impose order on chaos it created itself.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~crazy-train":{"haiku":"A mentally wounded person goes off the rails, inheriting a cold war world built on greed and media lies. He cannot bear the unfairness and rides a train of madness.","opus":"A Cold War-era lament about mass insanity: multitudes treating each other as enemies, the narrator wondering whether it's still possible to choose love over hate. The deeper read is inherited damage, a generation left numb and unhinged, blaming manufactured conditioning and a single controlling power, with untreated psychic scars dragging him toward madness through no fault of his own.","sonnet":"The world as a runaway train — inherited cold-war paranoia, media conditioning, religious grifters, the polarization of millions — with the narrator identifying as someone driven insane by the spectacle and unable to stop the acceleration toward catastrophe.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~crucify":{"haiku":"A manipulative deceiver sells false promises and betrayal, draining souls through temptation and lies. This counterfeit friend repeats cycles of crucifixion across time.","opus":"The voice of a smiling deceiver, most likely a politician, peddling salvation and votes while betraying everyone who trusts him. He poses as your most familiar ally, corrupts you from within, and promises to keep tormenting you endlessly until he owns you completely.","sonnet":"A corrupt political or religious figure who promises salvation while stealing from his constituents, exploiting their weaknesses, and feeding them lies — the song is voiced from the perspective of this counterfeit savior, who will keep crucifying his flock indefinitely.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~diggin-me-down":{"haiku":"The song critiques organized religion and faith leaders for broken promises, hypocrisy, and perpetuating suffering while claiming salvation, questioning whether Jesus has already failed humanity.","opus":"Late-career Ozzy wrestling openly with faith, less atheism than a wounded believer's frustration at a silent God and the self-appointed messiahs who profit from the faithful. It's the sound of hope cracking under unanswered prayer.","sonnet":"A furious challenge to organised religion — the narrator accuses the church of enslaving people, enriching the rich, ignoring genocide, and selling hollow promises, demanding that Jesus show himself or admit the whole project is a lie.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~fearless":{"haiku":"A warrior figure claims dominance through pain-immunity and self-sovereignty, rejecting mercy and mercy-seeking. Fearlessness becomes a violent ideology of dominion.","opus":"A pitiless warrior's oath refusing weakness, obedience, and surrender, delivered by a lone avenger who would rather perish standing than submit. It slips in a bitter note about conflicts begun by the wealthy that only the poor pay for with their lives, casting the fighter as something beyond flesh or metal.","sonnet":"A warrior's creed: wars started by the rich kill the poor, but the narrator has transcended pain and mercy, washes blood from his hands, and answers to no one — vigilante justice, unbreakable, willing to die on his feet rather than kneel.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~i-don-t-know":{"haiku":"Uncertainty about future and meaning paralyses the speaker while prophets and leaders claim answers they cannot truly possess.","opus":"A refusal of the guru role: people look to him for answers about the future and the truth, and he flatly insists he doesn't know and shouldn't be asked. The point is that everyone must work it out alone — the responsibility rests with each person, not with him.","sonnet":"A refusal to be anyone's prophet or guide — people ask about the end times and the future of mankind, and the answer is simply: I don't know, I got left behind too — urging listeners to find their own miracles and make their own choices.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~i-want-it-more":{"haiku":"A speaker pursues endless accumulation and ambition up the ladder of success, willing to sacrifice everything because death makes all else meaningless.","opus":"Recorded in Ozzy Osbourne's late-career twilight, this confronts mortality with unblinking hunger. Life is framed as brief and absurd, gone in a heartbeat, yet the narrator keeps clawing upward, cutting deals and pushing past everyone else no matter the toll. The nagging question underneath the ambition is whether any of the chasing was ever actually worth what it took.","sonnet":"Mortality sharpens appetite: life is brief and chaotic, yet the hunger to climb further and consume more only intensifies as the cost mounts. A restless ambition that recognizes the price of wanting everything — and still wants more.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~latimer-s-mercy":{"haiku":"A speaker ends someone's suffering through euthanasia, turning off life support and swallowing their final breath to relieve terminal pain.","opus":"Ozzy Osbourne takes on the role of a mercy killer, watching someone he loves deteriorate through seizures, medication and futile surgery he sees as needless agony. He refuses to apologize or feign innocence, resolving to end the suffering with a final, tender act, while ringing bells and a dawning day underscore the bleak intimacy.","sonnet":"A caregiver facing an unbearable choice — unable to restore someone to health, unable to leave them suffering — makes the deliberate act of ending pain into a form of love. The act is framed not as harm but as the most merciful thing left to offer.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~lay-your-world-on-me":{"haiku":"Offering emotional support and strength becomes a path to connection and interdependence, breaking cycles of isolation through vulnerability and shared burden.","opus":"Ozzy in tender, offering mode, telling someone in pain to hand their burden over, admitting he wasn't there before but wants to be the rock now. Its warmth comes from the confession that it's late but not too late.","sonnet":"A belated offer of unconditional support to someone who needed it and was let down before — the speaker acknowledges his past failures and pledges now to be the rock and shelter the other person always deserved.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~let-it-die":{"haiku":"The singer takes on contradictory identities and roles, advocating for letting go of past regrets while embracing change and revolution.","opus":"From Ozzy's late solo work, this heaps up clashing identities, celebrity and criminal, redeemer and wretch, coward and warrior, insisting he contains everyone and that listener and singer are one. Against imagery of ruin and flame, it urges surrendering old regrets so something new can begin. Reinvention through demolition, the refrain greeting change as liberation rather than grief.","sonnet":"A voice that identifies with every contradictory human role — savior and sinner, coward and fighter, victim and oppressor — declares that collapse and transformation are the same event. Letting the past die is framed as revelation rather than loss, an awakening that benefits the formerly faceless precisely because the old order burns.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~let-me-hear-you-scream": {"fable":"A stadium war-cry built entirely to be shouted back — the title line is less a lyric than an instruction to the crowd, and the song exists to provoke the response it names. The two verses stage a flip: the first speaker is the one taking the blows and surviving on nothing but grit, the second becomes the one delivering them, pinning the listener down. It's the oldest bargain of the late-career Ozzy arena show made explicit — the singer offers his own bruised endurance and demands the room match it in volume, the scream standing in for both the defiance and the proof that everyone in the building is still alive and unbroken.","fableDeep":"The address keeps wobbling between enemy and audience — the verses threaten ('I'm your hangman,' 'wipe that smile right off your face') while the chorus invites, and the two collapse into one arena move: defiance as pure volume. What you're screaming for is never specified and doesn't need to be; the content is empty on purpose. Surviving, here, is a sound — you prove you're still standing by being heard, and going quietly is the only real defeat the song recognizes.","haiku":"An anthem celebrating survival, resilience, and vocal defiance in the face of struggle; the imperative to fight hard and make noise in adversity.","opus":"A combative, taunting song urging a rival or listener to fight loudly and go all out in a do-or-die confrontation, with the speaker vowing to break them down and demanding to hear them scream.","sonnet":"A call-to-arms demanding full-throated, uncompromising effort: the speaker, battered but unbroken, goads an audience to commit entirely rather than hold back, framing the moment as a final stand where going quietly is not an option.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"the singer trades his bruised endurance for the room's proof of survival","built":"the title line is instruction to the crowd — the song exists to provoke the response it names"},
"ozzy-osbourne~life-won-t-wait":{"haiku":"The speaker urges resilience and action in the face of injustice, warning that time continues mercilessly and waiting guarantees loss.","opus":"Late-career Ozzy in reflective mode, urging courage and integrity against greed and blind ambition. The core is mortality's clock, the plain warning that time slips away and no fate rewrites itself, so live now.","sonnet":"An appeal to stay present and act now — justice may move slowly, greed and blind ambition waste what matters, but time doesn't wait and every second thrown away is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~mama-i-m-coming-home":{"haiku":"Changed and hardened by time apart, the speaker returns to the complicated relationship that hypnotized and hurt them.","opus":"Ozzy's tender, weathered ballad written with Zakk Wylde and Lemmy for Sharon, love addressed as home after years of touring, lies, tears and mutual damage. Beneath the tough exterior, a man admitting he can't say goodbye and is finally returning to the one who anchors him.","sonnet":"Addressed to a lover rather than a mother — the narrator is returning to a complicated relationship after time away, still unable to let go despite the lies and hurt, driven back by the pull of someone who both destroyed and defined him.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~mr-crowley":{"haiku":"The song interrogates Aleister Crowley's magical practices and dark spirituality, asking what drove him to seek occult knowledge and satanic communion.","opus":"A spellbound questioning of occultist Aleister Crowley, probing his inner life, whether he communed with spirits, and casting his existence as tragic thrill and mystical trickery beholden to dark forces. The mood leans more curious than accusatory. Ozzy's early solo landmark, powered by Randy Rhoads' soaring guitar, became one of metal's defining ruminations on dark mystique and questions left unanswered.","sonnet":"A fascinated interrogation of Aleister Crowley — was the occultist a sincere seeker or a fraud, a servant of darkness or merely a showman who played the crowds while waiting on something that never came? The speaker circles the myth with equal parts reverence and suspicion, unable to decode whether the life was tragedy, performance, or prophecy.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~no-bone-movies":{"haiku":"Pornography and compulsive masturbation trap consciousness in cycles of shame and desire that degrade and consume the subject's willpower.","opus":"An early, tongue-in-cheek solo track about the shame spiral of a porn habit, the compulsion he tries and fails to kick, cast as a demon in his head hungry to be fed. Comic on the surface, honest about self-disgust underneath.","sonnet":"A speaker confesses a compulsive addiction to pornography — caught between guilt, self-disgust, and an inability to stop — framing it as an internal demon that demands feeding no matter how hard he tries to resist.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~no-more-tears":{"haiku":"Violence returns unexpectedly, shattering the facade of safety until terror and loss become the only authentic emotions that remain.","opus":"A serial killer's inner monologue, waiting around corners for someone to scream, its dread heightened by tenderness bleeding into menace. The plea for no more tears reads as a murderer half-mourning the victims he can't stop making.","sonnet":"A gothic narrative of a woman stalked and killed by a man who waits in the dark, filtered through the perspective of someone arriving at the end of something — a relationship, a life — and trying to say goodbye with gentleness as the cold sets in. The horror and the farewell are intertwined.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~soul-sucker":{"haiku":"The speaker accuses someone of draining their vitality through emotional cruelty and cutting words, describing them as a predator who feeds on their suffering.","opus":"A drained man's stand against an emotional parasite who wounds him with cruel words, traps him in his own history, and siphons off his vitality. Underneath is a loner who craves only calm before the end, discovering that once his patience is exhausted his defenses curdle into aggression, so the wiser move is to walk out and let the draining bond go.","sonnet":"About a toxic relationship with someone whose cold words and relentless emotional demands drain the narrator dry — he wants a quiet life but is being eaten alive by this person's need.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~time":{"haiku":"Life is finite and slipping away; time cannot be recovered once lost, so we must stop wasting it on false hopes and live fully in the present.","opus":"A reminder of mortality urging you to quit hunting for answers that never come and quit deferring life to some imagined tomorrow. Since the clock spares no one and dying is the toll everyone eventually pays, the only sane response is to inhabit the present moment fully.","sonnet":"A mortality lecture — stop deferring life, stop dreaming and start living today, because time moves without you, age shows on your face, and the only price of being human is that you will die.","src":"haiku"},
"ozzy-osbourne~you-lookin-at-me-lookin-at-you":{"haiku":"Silent communication through eyes and distance masks growing separation, with touch becoming impossible despite physical proximity and intimate familiarity.","opus":"A quieter Ozzy song about a couple grown distant, touching yet miles apart, communicating through stares instead of words. The dread that nothing lasts forever wrestles with a wish that their love could be the exception.","sonnet":"Two people in a deteriorating relationship circle each other in silent mutual awareness, the gap between them widening even as they remain physically close — the speaker wants to salvage the love before it's lost for good.","src":"haiku"},
"p-h-f~glue":{"haiku":"Adhesion binds through absence more than presence, proximity itself becoming toxic while separation remains the only sustainable escape.","opus":"Lo-fi bedroom pop about a relationship that's become suffocating dependence, dreading the phone yet inseparable. The flat repetition of being stuck together like glue captures how attachment can feel more like a trap than a bond.","sonnet":"Someone who was once inseparably bonded to another person now wants nothing to do with them — resents their calls, prefers their absence, and insists the former closeness is over. The repeated \"glue\" metaphor frames the past attachment as something that held but left a residue.","src":"haiku"},
"p-o-d~alive":{"haiku":"Spiritual awakening dissolves despair; the speaker discovers flight through transcendent recognition of divine presence.","opus":"A born-again surge of gratitude, the narrator surrendering control to a higher love and feeling truly alive for the first time. Faith framed as flight worth any cost.","sonnet":"A conversion experience rendered as exhilarating relief: having found trust in a power beyond control, the speaker feels alive for the first time, as though they could fly — sunshine on the face, a new song to sing — no longer able to look away or turn back.","src":"haiku"},
"p-o-d~alive-2006-remastered-version":{"haiku":"Vitality and spiritual resurrection affirm living presence; the speaker remains animated despite pressure.","opus":"A surging anthem of gratitude, faith, and rebirth from the nu-metal band's spiritual core. The narrator treats each breath as a gift, trusting in a love that grants him peace of mind and vowing never to look away. Overwhelmed by renewal, he feels truly alive for the first time and ready to fly.","sonnet":"A speaker who has found faith after a period of being lost describes the transformation as a physical sensation — feeling alive \"for the very first time,\" sunshine on the face, the conviction that he can fly — framing spiritual surrender not as loss of self but as the moment he finally became himself. The cost acknowledged (\"even though it might cost me everything\") registers that this clarity is not free.","src":"haiku"},
"p-o-d~alive-new-album-chris-lord-alge-mix-version":{"haiku":"A person finds peace and joy in being alive for the first time, grateful for each breath and trusting in love—they believe they can fly.","opus":"A rush of gratitude and renewal, the narrator cherishing existence, growing from his failures, releasing what he can't govern, and feeling truly awake for the first time thanks to a love that steadies him. P.O.D., the San Diego Christian nu-metal band, made this rap-rock burst of spiritual awakening a signature, its faith undertow lifting the chorus skyward.","sonnet":"Spiritual awakening rendered as pure physical sensation — alive for the very first time, sunshine on the face, the feeling of being able to fly. What changed is a surrender of control paired with trust in something larger, and the price is honesty about how that feels even when it costs everything. Grateful and undefended.","src":"haiku"},
"p-o-d~boom":{"haiku":"Southern rap crew announces their arrival on the scene with confident street credibility and skill, brushing off doubters and establishing their dominance.","opus":"A hometown brag turned battle chant, a kid taking his voice global, flying the flag for his San Diego roots and daring any comer to bring their hardest. It's all bravado and crew pride, an anointed handful climbing to the top. P.O.D., the Christian nu-metal band who broke through in the early 2000s, deliver it as a rap-rock stomp built for arenas to roar the hook back.","sonnet":"A breakout anthem from San Diego — tracing a journey from local streets to global stages, from bootleg demos circulating in Tokyo to stadiums filling on the strength of years of unglamorous work. The stance is defiant and open-armed at once: come at us with your best shot, because we're still standing and the sound is already everywhere.","src":"haiku"},
"p-o-d~change-the-world":{"haiku":"Pure intention and childlike innocence hold revolutionary power to reshape society if people stop and listen to the voice of authentic connection and shared humanity.","opus":"A child's pure, uncorrupted vision becomes the model for renewal, arguing that one unheard voice could remake the world if people stopped to listen.","sonnet":"A child's painting of the world — every colour, pure perception — becomes the image of what a single unheard voice could accomplish if people stopped and listened; the song is an address to that potential, urging its subject to share the gift they have been given.","src":"haiku"},
"p-o-d~sleeping-awake":{"haiku":"The song describes a dreamlike spiritual state where the boundary between waking and dreaming dissolves; the speaker seeks answers about hidden meanings and prophetic understanding.","opus":"A spiritual dislocation from The Matrix era, the narrator caught between worlds, begging for someone to decode the visions and prophecies and confirm he isn't alone in sensing that reality is a kind of waking sleep.","sonnet":"A spiritual seeker overwhelmed by prophetic visions and symbolic messages he cannot decode — dreaming of Zion while awake — cries out to know if anyone else perceives what he perceives, feeling isolated inside a revelatory state no one else shares.","src":"haiku"},
"p-o-d~southtown":{"haiku":"Life in a dangerous neighborhood where survival is uncertain and violence is constant. The speaker asks for strength to make it through each day and hold onto what matters despite overwhelming circumstances.","opus":"Survival in a gang-ridden barrio where a young man measures his days by whether he'll make it home breathing. The real fight, though, is internal: he leans on prayer to keep from meeting the surrounding brutality with brutality of his own. P.O.D., the San Diego crossover band, root the song in their own neighborhood.","sonnet":"About surviving daily life in a dangerous neighborhood — the constant risk of not making it through the day, the impulse toward violence held in check by faith, the gratitude for still being alive. It's grounded in South San Diego street reality filtered through Christian faith.","src":"haiku"},
"p-o-d~thinking-about-forever":{"haiku":"Grief and legacy collide as the narrator confronts a mother's death while raising a daughter, marking time between loss and the drive to prove her influence lived on.","opus":"A grieving tribute to a lost mother, the singer carrying on with a wife and baby girl he wishes she could meet, asking for a butterfly as a sign. The tenderness is in crediting everything good in him to what she taught.","sonnet":"A son addresses his deceased mother, reflecting on how her example shaped him into a devoted father and husband, wondering if she can see the life he has built and asking for a sign that she is at peace.","src":"haiku"},
"p-o-d~this-goes-out-to-you":{"haiku":"Gratitude flows toward supporters and foundations; a young artist acknowledges those who inspire perseverance across the world's corners.","opus":"A thank-you letter to the fans and hometown community who sustained the band across years of touring. P.O.D., the San Diego rap-rock crossover act, turn appreciation into pure uplift, casting their supporters as the bedrock everything they built stood on and the reason to keep going.","sonnet":"A thank-you letter addressed to the fans who made years of road and hardship worth it — the song circles back through shared history and mutual survival, framing the audience as a foundation without which none of the rest makes sense. The closing phone call fragment, a mandatory curfew broadcast cutting in, edges the gratitude into something eerie.","src":"haiku"},
"p-o-d~youth-of-the-nation":{"haiku":"Youth suffer from neglect and desperation; school shootings and suicide stem from unclaimed love and fractured identity.","opus":"Three young lives cut short by school shooting, self-loathing and suicide, a narrator taking two bullets to the chest without a goodbye and asking who is to blame for the tragedies claiming a generation. P.O.D., the San Diego Christian rap-rock band, turned this into a millennial anthem of collective grief, insisting there has to be more to life than this.","sonnet":"Three lives — a shooting victim, a girl abandoned by her father, a boy driven to suicide — are woven together into a portrait of a generation failed by the adults around them. The chorus becomes an elegy and a claim: these fractured young people are still the nation, still owed answers no one will honestly give.","src":"haiku"},
"p-o-d~youth-of-the-nation-2006-remastered-version":{"haiku":"School violence reveals systemic abandonment of troubled youth; structural failure manifests through repeated tragedy.","opus":"A somber response to school shootings and youth tragedy, narrating a boy taking bullets in class, a twelve-year-old girl seeking love in wrong places, and a bullied outcast turning to suicide, each life claimed while the survivors are left asking where the blame lies. P.O.D., the San Diego Christian rap-metal band, wrote this after real-world massacres, and its refrain naming a whole generation of the young became a mournful, searching plea for meaning amid senseless loss.","sonnet":"Three portraits of American kids lost to different kinds of violence — a school shooting victim, a girl abandoned by her father who finds affection in the wrong places, a lonely outsider who takes his own life to finally be remembered. The song holds all three without offering easy answers, asking who is to blame and finding that the question itself outlasts any response.","src":"haiku"},
"pacifica~premature-rejection":{"haiku":"Anticipated abandonment and defensive preemption of inevitable loss, the speaker rejecting before rejection occurs.","opus":"A sharp-tongued dismissal of a hesitant, hypocritical suitor who wants without committing. The narrator refuses to be someone's secret or convenient mistake, calling out the coward hiding behind another girlfriend. Unbothered and unhidden, she flips the power, telling him to come and go but expect to be forgotten.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses someone cheating on their partner while pursuing her, calling out the cowardice directly — keep your secret, she has nothing to hide, but he can't be both the guy who claims he won't stray and the one sneaking around, and when it's over she dares him to at least remember her name.","src":"haiku"},
"pain-of-salvation~accelerator":{"haiku":"The speaker is a restless catalyst blamed for problems they didn't cause, arguing that they and other brilliant minds are necessary tools rather than scapegoats.","opus":"The self-defense of a restless misfit blamed as society's arsonist, who flips the charge back on the cautious majority and argues the world needs its reckless, gifted troublemakers. Sweden's Pain of Salvation, led by Daniel Gildenlow, deliver this celebration of creative danger with theatrical fervor.","sonnet":"A self-described disruptor — moving too fast, talking too loud, trailing charred ground everywhere — initially accepts blame for the destruction around them, then pivots: what if the problem is the cautious, ink-fingered ones who hold the matches and let the houses burn while debating? The brilliant and the crazy are the ones who can actually save the world; the Normals just have to choose which flame to follow.","src":"haiku"},
"pain-of-salvation~on-a-tuesday":{"haiku":"Confrontation with mortality and the inevitability of loss; a reflective examination of human promises and self-deception in the face of death.","opus":"A stark existential meditation from a man refusing to bargain with God for more time; he owns that all human survival rests on promises and lies we make to endure, and though he's lost the will and the way, he clenches his hands into fists rather than prayer, keeping faith only buried within himself.","sonnet":"A dying man's refusal to make deathbed promises to God — born on a Tuesday and dying on one, he won't bargain for more days or pretend faith he doesn't have; he closes his fists, not his hands in prayer, accepting that all the things humans say to survive are lies.","src":"haiku"},
"pain~go-with-the-flow":{"haiku":"Surrender to flow and acceptance dissolves conflict; letting go allows passage through life's turbulence.","opus":"A breezy directive to release the past and stop fighting circumstances beyond one's control. Peter Tägtgren's Pain trade industrial menace for wry advice, urging the listener to sway with the wind rather than dread the storm, shrug off resentment, and let go of shallow memories in favor of easy forward motion.","sonnet":"Resilience through detachment — rolling through a present disconnected from shallow memories, choosing to dance in the wind rather than wait out the storm, brushing off hate and acting like a superstar. The philosophy is stripped of complexity: let go, let yourself glow, go with the flow; it works precisely because it refuses to overthink.","src":"haiku"},
"palaye-royale~bullet-with-butterfly-wings":{"opus":"Theatrical glam-rock revivalists Palaye Royale take on Smashing Pumpkins' iconic 90s anthem of caged fury, and the cover fits their own persona of decadent alienation. The speaker rages against a draining, treacherous world, cycling through performance and nakedness, wanting change but feeling identical, begging to be told they are chosen while insisting salvation cannot come.","sonnet":"Trapped fury at a world that drains and betrays: the speaker, caged like a rat despite all their rage, moves through the stages of performance and nakedness, wanting change but feeling the same — asking to be told they are chosen even as they insist salvation is impossible.","src":"sonnet"},
"palaye-royale~bullet-with-butterfly-wings-inspired-by-the-motion-picture-divinity":{"haiku":"Systemic imprisonment and powerlessness pervade as the speaker recognizes themselves as rat trapped in cage, unable to escape despite knowing the cage exists.","opus":"A cover of the famous Smashing Pumpkins howl of powerlessness, framing the world as a vampire draining you while the narrator, despite all his rage, remains a caged animal, betrayed and past saving. Palaye Royale, the theatrical rock trio, reinterpret this alt-rock landmark for a film, preserving its central image of impotent fury while pushing the closing conviction that salvation is simply out of reach even harder into despair.","sonnet":"Trapped fury with nowhere to go — the world extracts everything and returns betrayal, and despite the rage that fills the speaker completely, they remain caged. The song's central image is the paradox of enormous, legitimate anger rendered powerless by the very system that provoked it, ending with the conviction that even salvation has been foreclosed.","src":"haiku"},
"palaye-royale~eleanor-rigby":{"haiku":"Loneliness persists unbroken through quiet rituals; society ignores the forgotten until death seals their invisible fate.","opus":"A goth-rock reading of Lennon and McCartney's chamber vignette, transposing the Beatles' baroque sorrow into theatrical modern rock. It surveys the invisible lonely, the woman gathering rice after strangers' weddings and the priest whose sermons fall on no ears, both drifting toward an unmourned burial. The dressing-up masks intensify the ache of anonymity and lives lived and lost unwitnessed.","sonnet":"Two parallel portraits of invisible loneliness: Eleanor Rigby keeps a face in a jar and picks up rice after strangers' weddings; Father McKenzie writes sermons no one hears and darns socks alone — both die unwitnessed, and the refrain asks where all the lonely people belong.","src":"haiku"},
"palaye-royale~showbiz":{"haiku":"Entertainment systems devour artists and dreams; the speaker watches industry predators feast on ambition while the young are sacrificed for profit.","opus":"An indictment of the entertainment industry that grooms and exploits young dreamers, killing artists so executives can profit. The bitter refrain reframes disillusionment as the price of admission.","sonnet":"An exposé of the entertainment industry as a predatory machine: a teenager with a superstar dream enters Hollywood and finds a shady man who tells them what to do, kills their self-esteem, and kills a million dreams to get a billion streams — 'starving artists have to die so pigs can learn to fly on our dime.'","src":"haiku"},
"paledusk~area-pd":{"haiku":"Contempt for outdated people; the speaker builds a paradise for chosen friends and refuses to look back.","opus":"A gleeful outsider anthem building a paradise walled off for friends only, spitting at rules, fakes and clout-chasers clinging to the past. Aggression and joy braided together, a self-crowned king who'd rather you simply disappear than be part of his world.","sonnet":"A declaration of total rejection of the existing music and social order — the narrator positions themselves as a true king outside the rules, creating a sanctuary only for their own people and actively wishing the hated outsiders would disappear, framing exclusion as freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"paledusk~black-ice":{"haiku":"A speaker stands on the edge of despair, frozen by cold emotional pain and lies, unable to break free despite wanting to return to better times and fearing they have nothing.","opus":"Paledusk use a treacherous, untouchable frost as a figure for a heart freezing over at its breaking point. Underneath, it's a relationship teetering on the edge where love somehow survives the cold, the narrator longing to share the same world as the person watching him and praying for the ice to melt.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship fracturing at the point of suicidal crisis — one person standing on the edge while the other watches from behind the ice, unable to reach them; the narrator finally starts running toward them, declaring love even as the cold and dark threaten to make everything permanent.","src":"haiku"},
"paledusk~happy-talk":{"haiku":"A performer critiques surface-level happiness and fame culture while reclaiming spiritual authenticity and celestial belonging.","opus":"A frantic, self-mocking rush of ego and exhaustion, where a performer courting stardom sells off his own misery to an audience he suspects is phony. The Japanese band jams English, Japanese and cartoon sound effects together into manic self-mythology, half swaggering messiah complex, half plea for the bandmates not to drift apart.","sonnet":"A performer tears through hollow positivity culture with chaotic energy, rejecting cheap optimism while claiming total self-worth on their own terms. The song lurches between cynicism and euphoric declaration — fame, fans, chaos, and a sincere desire to be loved, all colliding at full volume.","src":"haiku","fable":"HAPPY TALK is a fame-satire that keeps its rage where a chorus should be, spelling out its own title letter by letter as if to insist the cheer is manufactured, a brand rather than a mood. The lyric buys tears and routes them to a house of fake, jeers at no-pain-no-gain, and then swerves — I am so mad, but now I am having fun — the anger not resolved but weaponized into performance, fans reimagined as gunfire, run and go far, the acclaim indistinguishable from a shooting. Paledusk's shapeshifting is the argument, not the packaging: the song lurches between skittering nursery-rhyme la-la-la, a spell cast stepping into hell, and the demand to be loved more, so that adoration and damnation share one melody. The turn comes at the close, where the sneer suddenly softens into we breathe under the same pink sky, don't say goodbye — and the singer, having mocked the whole apparatus, crowns himself Savior, leaving it deliberately unreadable whether that is the mask's final joke or the frightened person underneath asking, in the only voice available to him, to be kept.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"rage weaponized into performance until adoration and damnation share one melody","built":"the title spelled out letter by letter insists the cheer is a brand manufactured rather than felt"},
"paledusk~i-m-ready-to-die-for-my-friends":{"haiku":"Loyalty to community and brotherhood transcends individual survival, creating bonds forged through shared struggle. The speaker celebrates chosen family and willingness to sacrifice for collective meaning.","opus":"A high-velocity anthem of loyalty, laughing off hardship and short life while pledging everything to friends and even enemies. Gratitude and defiance carry the crew forward together.","sonnet":"A declaration of total loyalty to friends and the band, fuelled by late-night vodka, taxi rides through neon streets, and the knowledge that without these people the speaker would already be dead — the gratitude extends even to enemies, and the readiness to die is inseparable from the joy of being alive together.","src":"haiku"},
"paledusk~leave-me-out":{"haiku":"Someone demands distance from judgment and scrutiny, rejecting others' perceptions and expectations while asserting their own alienation and difference.","opus":"A furious demand to be left alone by fake, self-satisfied people who look down on the narrator while sipping their tea and feeling nothing. The deeper read is embraced weirdness as a shield, he owns being weirder than they think and refuses to become bland like them, the cooling tea marking their smug worthlessness as he gets out of their sight.","sonnet":"Fierce rejection of being judged, patronized, or categorized by people the narrator finds shallow and worthless — the posturing crowd who sips mainstream culture and feels nothing real is told to get out of the narrator's sight and leave them alone.","src":"haiku"},
"paledusk~lights":{"haiku":"A small glimmer of light persists even as the heart fails, insisting on forward movement despite endless regret and impossible odds.","opus":"A metalcore band momentarily softening toward anthem, wrestling regret against the will to keep moving. The narrator admits to fighting a paradox in his own chest yet clings to a faint glimmer he trusts will point the way forward. Survival becomes the miracle; even if his voice never reaches its target, he hopes it might become someone else's guiding light.","sonnet":"Someone carrying deep regret and a heart about to give out finds a small light and chooses to follow it one more time, addressing an unseen listener with the hope that even an uncertain, still-searching voice might become their light too.","src":"haiku"},
"paledusk~palehell":{"haiku":"Survival through hell connects the speaker to community and love as endurance itself becomes testimony. Friendship and solidarity remain the only genuine lights in systemic darkness.","opus":"A vow to keep moving through the worst of it just to leave proof of having lived, refusing to sing anything false even when the truth wounds the people he loves. Paledusk's chaos-core frames it as a late bloomer's rough, self-aware promise to keep going alongside his friends.","sonnet":"A vow to keep living honestly despite the cost: the speaker has walked through hell to leave proof they existed, knows their voice sometimes hurts those they love, refuses to sing lies, and counts the nights they could not forget — the friends they sing to are both witness and reason to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"paledusk~rumble":{"haiku":"Confrontation and physical conflict as necessary catharsis, the speaker engaging in aggressive release.","opus":"A defiant call to arms from a Japanese heavy band framing art itself as rebellion and elixir. It rejects the gatekeepers who profit from the weak and want to watch outsiders fail, insisting instead on staying true to oneself and rewriting history through one's own voice. The refrain that nothing lasts forever fuels urgency rather than despair, a rallying cry to seize this era.","sonnet":"A rallying cry for artists and outsiders against powers that deceive the weak and sit on dirty thrones — sounds of freedom are what they fear, the ghost dance is invoked for those who know what it means. The Japanese lines name the real source: sometimes lost, sometimes wrong, deeply wounded, but still singing freely, the dream repeating.","src":"haiku"},
"paledusk~slay":{"haiku":"A group celebrates living boldly and fearlessly without regret, determined to keep pushing forward regardless of obstacles or judgment.","opus":"A Japanese metalcore burst of pure life-affirming defiance, a crew declaring that simply being alive is a win and no enemy can touch them. Its joy is in refusing to care what anyone thinks and moving forward regardless.","sonnet":"A Japanese-English party banger about an unstoppable crew with a home base to return to but always climbing higher — every day is new, nobody can stop them, and the only response to anyone who complains is to act rather than gripe.","src":"haiku"},
"paledusk~super-pale-horse":{"haiku":"The liar arrives as fate's messenger, stingy and narcissistic characters draw judgment while the speaker stands outside, observing their inevitable decay.","opus":"Contempt for a cheap, cowardly hanger-on who takes and never pays, dressed up with apocalyptic imagery of the pale horse. The real jab is at people playing god while judging everyone, told to look in the mirror.","sonnet":"A contemptuous callout of a freeloader who attends parties without paying their share and disappears whenever money is mentioned — presented as a moral failing whose consequences are inevitable and divinely ordained.","src":"haiku"},
"paledusk~wind-back":{"haiku":"Regret and desire to undo the past while moving forward together. The narrator and another must release old pain and start fresh to find new light.","opus":"An urgent appeal to a friend to turn back the clock and rekindle a fading bond, insisting reconciliation is always possible and that surrender isn't an option. Paledusk imagine the two meeting each other's eyes, glimpsing a shared future, and deciding to reset to zero and try again.","sonnet":"A song addressed to a past or future version of oneself, urging that person to \"wind back\" to a better time and start over. The speaker works through reluctance and exhaustion toward an acceptance that it is not too late to change course, and that running forward with no time to die is possible.","src":"haiku"},
"paleskin~absence-of-you":{"haiku":"Death becomes transcendence and the speaker exists as ethereal absence, craving annihilation as escape while the night offers reprieve that daylight cannot penetrate.","opus":"A blackgaze meditation on love entangled with a longing for oblivion, where devotion and a death-wish blur into one. The narrator wanders blind through an empty, cold heaven, seeking comfort everywhere and finding none. Only in another's presence does the need to escape dissolve, wishing to remain wrapped and still; in that absence, understanding itself feels forever out of reach.","sonnet":"A speaker drifting above the ground — dancing with angels, dim-eyed, travelling with the night — confesses that in the absence of the person they love they will never know how to survive, and that they would choose to die wrapped in their sheets rather than keep floating alone.","src":"haiku"},
"palisades~fade":{"haiku":"A person struggles to heal and become real, feeling paralyzed and unable to let others in despite attempts to connect. They fear fading away entirely if they can't break through their own barriers.","opus":"A song about not being able to reach yourself: words that don't connect, a heart falling with no way to catch it, asking how you heal when you can't let yourself in. Fading feels closer than realness. Palisades, the New Jersey band who drifted from metalcore toward slick alt-rock, wrap the depressive paralysis in a polished, hook-forward ache that keeps circling the same unanswerable question.","sonnet":"Depression as a failure of self-access — the speaker reaches toward healing but can't let themselves in, watches their own life from the outside, paralyzed even while trying. Recovery raises the fear that getting better means disappearing entirely, and the hardest question isn't how to heal but whether survival will leave anything recognizable behind.","src":"haiku"},
"palisades~fragile-bones":{"haiku":"A manipulative person poses as a healer but breaks down their victim's autonomy; the victim cuts free and refuses rescue.","opus":"Metalcore band Palisades stage a revolt against a controlling figure who pins the narrator like a lab specimen, claiming to heal him while teaching him self-loathing. Imagery of cloning, vaccines and puppet strings feeds a refusal to be owned, severing the bonds and insisting a body without a soul is no real cure.","sonnet":"A person pinned under another's controlling scrutiny resists being remade into something manageable. The controller mistakes domination for healing, but the one being shaped refuses — cutting the strings before there is nothing left that belongs to them.","src":"haiku"},
"palisades~ghost":{"haiku":"Someone fades into irrelevance as they are forgotten and cut away; they become a ghost unable to be felt or reached by those they haunt.","opus":"Being emotionally erased by someone who stopped reaching back, becoming invisible while still present and screaming; the ghost is a person written out of another's life while very much alive.","sonnet":"A song about feeling invisible and discarded in a relationship — the narrator is a ghost, screaming through the walls, but the other person can't or won't reach for them, and the indifference is slowly erasing the narrator's face from existence.","src":"haiku"},
"palisades~push":{"haiku":"Someone wallows in self-destruction and isolation, crying for understanding while pushing everyone away.","opus":"A spiral of self-sabotage told with grim self-awareness, the speaker confessing he inflicts this on himself while begging to be understood by anyone at all. The metalcore band casts his request to be shoved over the brink as both capitulation and accusation, masking his pain and trapped in familiar patterns, the language of misery ringing like a sentence already handed down.","sonnet":"A person in freefall from self-inflicted patterns cries out for anyone willing to understand rather than judge. The misery is acknowledged but not resolved — old habits hold, and the longing for connection remains unanswered at the edge of what feels like a final threshold.","src":"haiku"},
"palisades~shed-my-skin":{"haiku":"The speaker systematically removes their past and weakness, breaking free from repetitive failure like a snake shedding its skin. They purge poison from their veins and rebuild themselves inch by inch.","opus":"Palisades push their electronic-tinged metalcore into a brutal fantasy of reinvention. Rebirth is imagined as literal self-surgery, tearing out every weakness and casting off an old life like a reptile molting. Behind the grisly imagery is a plain demand: to decide his own future entirely on his own terms.","sonnet":"Radical self-renewal as a visceral, bodily act — stripping off everything that accumulated through weakness, shedding the past inch by inch and rebuilding stitch by stitch. The process is loud and deliberate, each crack and creak a step forward, the old scars understood as shed flesh rather than permanent damage.","src":"haiku"},
"palisades~vendetta":{"haiku":"A manipulative person controls and drains the narrator's identity; the narrator breaks free, refusing to be their prisoner or bleed.","opus":"A pawn drained like blood to fill someone else's cup declares he is no prisoner, refusing to be molded or controlled and vowing not to march in the manipulator's parade. Palisades, the New Jersey band who drifted between metalcore and electronic pop, drive the theme of breaking free with defiant hooks, the abuser scared of who the narrator might become.","sonnet":"Held in the grip of someone who needs total control — reshaping, draining, criticizing — a person slowly recognizes the manipulation for what it is and refuses to bleed on command. The vendetta is internal: the declaration of breakout is less about revenge than about reclaiming the self before it is entirely consumed.","src":"haiku"},
"palisades~war":{"haiku":"Internal conflict burns the self; playing with fire leads only to blame and isolation. The speaker's only choice is to walk away and not expect rescue from those who lit the flame.","opus":"The New Jersey post-hardcore band level their gaze at a self-destructive addict who makes his own wounds worse and keeps gambling against odds he can never beat. As fire imagery swallows everything, the narrator gives up, withholding even the mercy of speaking to someone bent on ruining himself no matter what.","sonnet":"A person watches someone they know sabotage themselves repeatedly — using the wrong tools, gambling with the devil, lighting fires only to stand in the flames. The speaker has reached the limit of care: the consequences belong entirely to the self-destructive one now, and no rescue is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"palisades~ways-to-disappear":{"haiku":"A person habitually retreats from connection and truth, using evasion and fear to maintain walls. Only another person's light can finally break the cycle of isolation.","opus":"An anxious self-portrait of someone who can't handle attention, wanders in circles like a ghost, and keeps finding ways to vanish while struggling to face his fears and letting go. Dragging out goodbyes and clinging to memories, he finally lets someone become the light that leads him home. Palisades, the New Jersey band who drifted from metalcore toward pop-rock, frame this fear of intimacy and abandonment in an accessible, hook-forward chorus resolving toward being led home.","sonnet":"Anxiety and avoidance as a way of life — staying in, shrinking, circling the person you want without making contact, preferring smoke to air. The speaker knows they disappear to protect themselves and that this isolation is not sustainable, and the song traces the slow shift from fear to the realization that one specific light — one person — might actually lead them home.","src":"haiku"},
"pallbearer~vengeance-ruination":{"haiku":"The song depicts ritual violence and execution as revenge, stripping victims of dignity piece by piece, asserting that cruelty exercised freely is the ultimate measure of power.","opus":"Arkansas doom band Pallbearer render a scene of ritual execution where the doomed are stripped of dignity and humanity as a sacrifice to death itself. The song reads real power as the freedom to be cruel without consequence, and frames retaliatory violence as just one more link in a chain of atrocity that never ends.","sonnet":"Ritual punishment stripped of any redemptive pretence — the condemned led blind to a place where blood fills the earth and dignity is carved away piece by piece for a watching crowd. The song refuses to romanticize vengeance: this is cruelty with impunity masquerading as justice, an eye for an eye that only lengthens an endless chain, and the screams echo through halls built precisely to contain them.","src":"haiku"},
"palm-reader~hold-release":{"haiku":"A person suffocates under external expectations and others' judgments, internalizing pressure until it breaks; the song urges speaking about the silenced suffering that marks the cost of conforming to strength.","opus":"An examination of inherited pressure and the myth of the silent, strong son who holds everything in until he suffers. The British band turns the parable of the prodigal into a study of stifled feeling, euphoria forever out of reach, relief hiding in conversations no one will have. The refrain asks when we will finally break silence and speak.","sonnet":"A son raised under the weight of silent familial expectation searches for the last time he felt free. The pressure is never spoken aloud — that is precisely the problem. Relief exists only in conversations no one in this tradition will allow themselves to begin.","src":"haiku"},
"paloma~you-and-i":{"haiku":"Relationship friction breeds inauthenticity; both speakers resort to performance rather than honesty, killing genuine connection with hollow rehearsal.","opus":"Clinging to a relationship built on incompatibility, chasing one more alive morning while knowing the person turns fake the moment they're needed. Desperation and disgust tangled, unwilling to fake it yet unable to let go.","sonnet":"A relationship collapsing under mutual inauthenticity — the speaker craves intensity and aliveness together but recognizes the other person is performing, cycling between wanting to stay and demanding they stop faking it.","src":"haiku"},
"palomino-blond~machine":{"haiku":"Environmental catastrophe and personal paralysis intertwine as crisis accelerates while intervention collapses. The speaker stands isolated, witnessing apocalypse, unable to act or connect.","opus":"A wish to be cut open and rearranged, to shed and dance again, set against a world sinking and burning outside the door. Self-reinvention offered as the only escape from paralysis and a collapsing environment.","sonnet":"A plea for transformation from inside a paralysed, burning world: the speaker longs to be cut apart and rearranged — all past pictures discarded — because only that radical dismantling would let them dance again while the ice melts and the sun scorches.","src":"haiku"},
"paluch~malinowa-mamba":{"haiku":"Pursuing authentic personal growth over commercial success, financial control as freedom, contempt for derivative rappers and manufactured personas.","opus":"A veteran Polish street rapper stakes out contentment as the ultimate flex, wanting a life sweet as candy after years of grind from block kid to legendary father. He controls his own PR, finances and words, dismissing rivals and the press who only cover his stumbles. Beneath the boasting sits a simple wish, to live normally and enjoy what he built.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper's declaration of hard-won contentment: personal growth over fame, financial self-sufficiency, contempt for PR-managed fakery and social media posturing, and the simple desire for a sweet life — \"malinowa mamba\" (raspberry mamba candy) as the image of uncomplicated pleasure. Critics and gossips circle looking for a stumble; the narrator shrugs them off and keeps cashing in.","src":"haiku"},
"panchiko~all-they-wanted":{"haiku":"A woman needs to feel wanted; the cycle of yearning for validation through being desired by others.","opus":"The cult English band, rediscovered decades after a lost demo resurfaced online, sketches a bleak intimacy where a partner watches television into boredom and owes nothing. Beneath the drift runs a shared hunger, both figures craving only the sense of being wanted and needed. The girl who lies and cries when it's over embodies a want that circles back on itself, unmet.","sonnet":"Two people drift past each other in a relationship hollowed out by mutual disengagement — one bored, one aching. Beneath the detachment and the performed indifference lies a simple, crushing need: to be wanted, to be needed. The absence of that feeling is what quietly breaks them both.","src":"haiku"},
"panchiko~stabilisers-for-big-boys":{"haiku":"A person pursues worship and transgression through dominance and control, breaking partners while hiding wounded cruelty beneath desire.","opus":"A disquieting spiral of desire, disgust, and cruelty, a laughter that grows harder to see, a woman who brings the horrors, and a torrent of transgressive impulses about worship, harm, and control, ending on breaking someone like glass and taking it all. It's deliberately unsettling and raw. Panchiko, the Nottingham band famed for the lost-and-rediscovered nineties recording that made them a cult sensation, wrap this jarring interior monologue in their hazy, distorted dream-pop textures.","sonnet":"Desire and revulsion running simultaneously — a woman who terrifies and fascinates, a speaker who wants to worship, provoke, break, and be broken in the same breath. The imagery accumulates without resolution: blood and glass and salt in wounds and wanting to be kissed like a girl and ruin the whole world. Adolescent and ancient, tender and violent, it refuses to pick a register.","src":"haiku"},
"pantera~cowboys-from-hell":{"opus":"A swaggering outlaw entrance, the band casting themselves as gunslingers taking over any town they enter. Pantera's breakout announces their Texas groove-metal identity as pure menace and bravado, all showdowns and high noon. Less a story than a mission statement, it turns arrival itself into a threat and a coronation.","sonnet":"A straight declaration of dominance — a gang of outlaws rolls into town with guns and braggadocio, burns it down, and demands submission from everyone in their path, the whole song functioning as pure machismo mythology with no ironic distance.","src":"sonnet"},
"pantera~domination":{"haiku":"Chains, razors, and brick-by-brick confinement obliterate the will, trapping consciousness in escalating torment. Domination conquers through systematic annihilation of resistance.","opus":"Domination as a torture that consumes then befriends its victim, a brutal vision of the will bound and broken, chained over razors until it bends.","sonnet":"Domination is an insatiable force that calls itself a friend while consuming the dominated — the body is suspended by chains over razors, life sealed brick by brick, and the eyes finally read the writing on the wall: you will bring about your own downfall.","src":"haiku"},
"pantera~fucking-hostile":{"haiku":"The speaker critiques authority figures—parents, police, priests—for imposing control through rules, lies, and fear; he questions why they arrest people for small offenses while ignoring real crimes.","opus":"A middle finger to imposed authority, mocking preachy parents, cops and clergy by acting out each role only to demolish it. Pantera, at their early-nineties commercial peak, channel Phil Anselmo's contempt through Dimebag Darrell's riffing, arguing that piling rules and lies onto people is exactly what breeds the aggression the song spits back.","sonnet":"Three institutions — media, law enforcement, and the church — are each tried and found guilty of the same crime: imposing rules that generate harm rather than justice. The narrator imagines holding each position differently, with honesty instead of performance, and finds the gap between what authority claims and what it actually does to be the source of collective fury.","src":"haiku"},
"pantera~hollow":{"haiku":"Traumatic brain injury hollows the person while leaving the body intact; the speaker mourns the ghost inside the shell.","opus":"Anguish over a friend left brain-dead or comatose, a shell of the person he talked with weeks ago, the singer shaking his fists at God and wishing he could follow him in. Pantera's rare turn toward helpless grief rather than fury.","sonnet":"A speaker sits beside a friend who has become mentally or spiritually absent — still physically alive but unreachable — and grieves him as though he were already dead, pleading for him to return.","src":"haiku"},
"pantera~medicine-man":{"haiku":"A malevolent dealer preys on lost souls, offering euphoric escape through intoxication while stealing their essence and trapping them in addiction.","opus":"Addiction as a devil with an angel's voice, a dealer who shows you heaven in one night then rots your soul on a crowded shelf. The song frames drugs as a false messiah luring proud men to indulgent ruin.","sonnet":"A charismatic dealer or false healer is portrayed as a demonic figure who destroys proud men by feeding them euphoria and addiction, collecting their souls on a shelf. The medicine man's power is seduction toward hell dressed as relief.","src":"haiku"},
"pantera~message-in-blood":{"haiku":"A speaker marked by violence and suffering embraces their own predatory nature as a signature, unable or unwilling to change.","opus":"A killer's voice claiming a lifelong inner power that drives him to murder, leaving his signature as a warning written in blood. It inhabits the mind of someone who treats slaughter as identity and legacy.","sonnet":"A deeply internalized capacity for violence, carried since childhood, expresses itself as a cryptic and almost sacred mark left on victims. The narrator is both confessional and detached, reading his own murderous nature as something beyond his control that still speaks his name.","src":"haiku"},
"pantera~mouth-for-war":{"haiku":"Channelled rage and determined will transform hatred into productive force to crush opposition through raw intensity.","opus":"A vow to channel rage and frustration into productive force, moving mountains on hate and using it as medicine to crush anyone who'd stop him. Anselmo turns anger itself into fuel, making the release of it a determined path to success.","sonnet":"A declaration of channelled rage as fuel — moving mountains with less when hate becomes productive, bones in traction, strength of many — demanding that the mouth be used for war and truth rather than noise, with determination as the bottom line.","src":"haiku"},
"pantera~psycho-holiday":{"haiku":"Mental breakdown becomes liberation, the speaker collapsing outward through accumulated frustration until isolation achieves a twisted form of freedom.","opus":"Burnout and breakdown reframed as a needed escape, retreating alone to set inner demons loose. Early Pantera casts the psychic collapse under pressure as a grim vacation, the only way to rest a mind pushed past its limit.","sonnet":"A person overwhelmed by pressure from all sides — the demands of others, the invasion of personal space, the toll of constant scrutiny — retreats alone to release their inner demons. The \"psycho holiday\" is the necessary, violent privacy of mental collapse and reconstitution.","src":"haiku"},
"pantera~the-great-southern-trendkill":{"haiku":"Trendy fakery and manufactured authenticity corrode culture; the narrator condemns those who rob integrity for commercial gain and empty self-creation.","opus":"Anselmo's venomous swipe at bandwagon culture and the sellouts who'd trade their soul for whatever's fashionable. The rage is aimed at manufactured authenticity, people who look like stars precisely because they don't know who they are.","sonnet":"A savage attack on music-industry trend-chasers and poseurs who rob dead scenes and package authenticity for MTV consumption, the speaker calling for a reckoning that drowns the hollow and returns music to those who feel rather than perform.","src":"haiku"},
"pantera~walk":{"haiku":"A person demands respect and personal boundary, rejecting false friends who undermine them behind closed doors.","opus":"Phil Anelmo's kiss-off to fake friends and disrespect, born from a Texas band's hard-won code of standing on your own. The demand for respect and authenticity became an anthem precisely because its groove makes contempt feel like strength.","sonnet":"A snarling demand for respect and autonomy — the narrator has no patience for people who talk behind their backs, try to crawl under their skin, or claim friendship while undermining them.","src":"haiku"},
"pantera~we-ll-grind-that-axe-for-a-long-time":{"haiku":"Integrity and rebellion persist against trends and sellouts, with the speaker committed to grinding their axe of authenticity regardless of changing fashions.","opus":"A defiant statement of loyalty to their roots against everyone who changes to chase fame and trends. The pride is in refusing to sell up, promising to keep grinding the same axe long after the fakes have burned out.","sonnet":"A declaration of artistic integrity against trend-following and industry compromise — the speaker refuses to change to suit the moment, isolating himself from the crowd of fakes and sycophants and grinding on regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"pan~let-it-die-subete-wa-isutori-game-let-it-die-all-is-musical-chairs":{"haiku":"Existence as game where seating means survival; chairs disappear as players fall, dancing and struggling futilely through musical framework.","opus":"A frantic Japanese hardcore romp that turns the childhood game of musical chairs into a savage metaphor for existence. Everyone from lovers to politicians scrambles for a seat, and failing to sit means game over. Packed commuter trains and cutthroat rivalry become the arena; life reduced to a manic, absurd contest where hesitation is fatal and only the seated survive.","sonnet":"Life is a furious game of musical chairs — lovers, politicians, everyone charging for seats — and the only mantra is concentration and survival: sit or the game is over, fight seat to seat with deadly seriousness, because all of human society is just backsides competing for a place to land.","src":"haiku"},
"papa-roach~dying-to-believe-explicit":{"haiku":"The speaker desperately wants to believe humans are fundamentally alike beneath divisions, arguing that separation and walls prevent unity while hope remains possible.","opus":"An arena-scaled call for common ground, insisting people share far more than divides them. Papa Roach aim it at a polarized moment, arguing that the real enemy is the mistrust we build within ourselves, and refusing to accept that we're destined to keep tearing one another apart. Hope, it maintains, hasn't run out yet.","sonnet":"A plea for shared humanity in a moment of total division — everyone in a chokehold, digging deeper into division, building walls around hearts. The speaker is not naive about it; they are desperate, willing themselves toward a belief that the enemy is inside us rather than each other, that underneath the tribalism we are the same. Hope asserted against evidence, loudly.","src":"haiku"},
"papa-roach~getting-away-with-murder":{"haiku":"The speaker, addicted to punishment and their partner's abuse, acknowledges they are getting away with deception and emotional cruelty.","opus":"Shaddix charts a punishing, addictive bond where he's hooked on a partner's cruelty and bracing for catastrophe. The title becomes a confession of a man running on autopilot, drinking and brooding against his own will, deadened to any fallout from what he does.","sonnet":"About being addicted to a destructive relationship and knowing you're self-deceiving — the narrator admits he's irrational and confrontational, lying to himself while remaining hooked on the punishment the other person provides.","src":"haiku"},
"papa-roach~last-resort":{"haiku":"A person in suicidal crisis describes their mental collapse from loss and broken family bonds, expressing hopelessness and contemplating self-harm. They seek acknowledgment of their pain while feeling nothing is right in their life.","opus":"Papa Roach's breakout confronts suicidal desperation head-on, the narrator self-harming and weighing whether to end his life. He traces the collapse to losing his mother and any love for himself, pleading for someone to reassure him he's okay because he can no longer bear to continue as he is.","sonnet":"A raw, direct song about suicidal crisis — the narrator is actively contemplating self-harm as the only remaining option, driven by emptiness that began with losing his mother and spiraled into a complete inability to function or feel cared for.","src":"haiku","fable":"The 2000 nu-metal breakout that made Papa Roach a household name works by dramatizing a suicidal crisis from the inside, then quietly locating its source. The verses stack the language of self-harm and last exits into a wall of pure sensation, deliberately overwhelming so the listener feels the airless panic rather than analyzes it. But the second verse pulls the camera back and diagnoses: the collapse traces to a lost parent and a resulting inability to love self or anyone, the crisis reframed as grief that curdled into a downward spiral. The chorus is the sharpest move, because underneath the aggression it isn't a threat but a plea addressed to no one in particular, wishing someone would simply tell him he's fine, so the mask of rage covers a child asking for reassurance that never comes. The genius of the track is that it screams like a suicide note but is actually a bid to be talked out of it.","fv":2,"fnote":"Shaddix wrote it about Mark, the roommate he moved in with at seventeen, after Mark’s suicide attempt — the scream-as-cry-for-help reading isn’t interpretation, it’s the documented brief.","themes":["suicide","death & grief","family & blood"],"means":"the suicide note is actually a bid to be talked out of it","built":"the chorus rage covers a child asking for reassurance that never comes"},
"papa-roach~scars":{"haiku":"A person who cares deeply tries to help someone drowning in pain but cannot save them, and must ultimately let go to save themselves.","opus":"Papa Roach's breakthrough anthem about the toll of loving an addict you cannot rescue. The narrator, worn down by caring too much, treats his emotional damage as proof his suffering mattered. Drunk and at his limit, he finally accepts he can't fix another person and lets himself walk away.","sonnet":"He has spent himself trying to save someone who kept sinking, his empathy becoming a wound he tears open repeatedly just to feel something. The scars left by caring too much finally force a reckoning: he cannot fix another person, and his own survival demands he walk away.","src":"haiku"},
"papa-roach~spotlight-demo":{"haiku":"Performance culture breeds self-destruction as the visible hunger for attention corrodes from within.","opus":"The performance of a perfect life masks self-harm, eating disorders and breakdown, with the narrator confessing his own wreckage in solidarity. A demand to drop the highlight reel and show something real.","sonnet":"Social media performance culture is a killing ground: a girl starves and cuts herself chasing approval she can't get, while the speaker confesses their own self-destruction and calls for real vulnerability over the curated best-life performance everyone is dying to maintain.","src":"haiku"},
"papa-roach~to-be-loved":{"haiku":"A defiant narrator refuses surrender despite a corrupted heart, claiming strength through sheer will even as self-destruction looms.","opus":"A hard-charging anthem where bravado and hunger for respect barely mask the plain want underneath, to be loved. The jet-black, falling-apart heart is the tell that the swagger is armor over need.","sonnet":"A relentless drive for dominance and recognition coexists with a naked confession of emotional need — underneath all the aggression is simply the desire to be loved. The speaker burns hard, falls, confesses weakness, and keeps going anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"paper-idol~clouds-crypto-remix":{"haiku":"Love intoxicates as escapism while mistakes compound, the speaker and their lover suspended in reverie unable to transcend their own poor judgment.","opus":"Dreamy pop about a heady, doomed romance built on mutual mistakes, the singer besotted and disoriented, head lost in the clouds. It's the giddy confusion of loving someone bad for you and not wanting to come down.","sonnet":"A relationship coming apart under the weight of a partner's second-guessing and self-doubt, with the speaker finding themselves distracted and unmoored — head in the clouds — unable to stop the drift even as they see the bad decisions accumulating.","src":"haiku"},
"paper-idol~cntrl":{"haiku":"Loss of autonomy and manipulation, being controlled by external forces or people.","opus":"A mantra of pure appetite: spotting something, wanting it, tasting it in advance, and surrendering to the loss of restraint that follows. The repetition is the point, desire looping until it spins out of anyone's hands. Paper Idol is the solo project of a Chicago-based bedroom pop and alt-electronic writer whose songs favor exactly this kind of tight, chant-driven hook over narrative, treating craving itself as the whole subject.","sonnet":"Desire as a system crash — the moment of wanting something is so immediate and physical that it bypasses reason entirely. Seeing, wanting, tasting: the senses stack up faster than the mind can process, and the stutter of the voice cutting out mid-word is the exact moment control slips. Simple, animal, gone.","src":"haiku"},
"paper-idol~feel-real-pretty":{"haiku":"The song depicts addiction and transcendent bliss-chasing. A powder-dealer becomes everything; come back only when you feel beautiful enough, trapped in chemical need.","opus":"A glossy EDM-pop track that dresses drug dependence up as seduction, following a lonely night out into a club and the flattering pull of a dealer's invitation to keep coming back. Paper Idol, an indie-electronic project, wrap the catchy hook around hollow highs, the sweetness masking the emptiness it's meant to fill.","sonnet":"A drug dependency loop told through a single night out — lonely, pool of friends dried up, hits a club, meets a dealer who tells him to come back when he feels pretty. The hook closes on itself: you only feel pretty when you use, so you come back, so you use, so the dealer becomes the only constant. The song sounds fun until it doesn't.","src":"haiku"},
"papercut-official-hd-music-video~linkin-park":{"haiku":"Internal voices become manifest as parasitic entities, breeding paranoia and self-doubt as they monitor every misstep. The speaker recognizes this inner critic lives within everyone, a universal shadow that feeds on inadequacy.","opus":"Linkin Park's nu-metal breakthrough dramatizes paranoia as an inner accuser, a face beneath the skin cataloguing every mistake and lie. The narrator can't identify what first triggered the pressure, only that a relentless voice haunts him and insists everyone else measures up where he cannot. It universalizes anxiety, arguing this self-surveilling dread lives inside everyone, sometimes worse than his own.","sonnet":"A second self lives inside the skin — a face that watches every lie, laughs every time you fall, and judges every mistake — and the paranoia of its constant surveillance is indistinguishable from conscience. \"The sun goes down, I feel the light betray me\" closes the song as the watcher and the watched merge entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-fell~honest":{"haiku":"Stripping away pretense and performance, brutal truth-telling after facades collapse.","opus":"A post-hardcore plea to salvage a relationship teetering at the edge. The narrator tries to talk a partner off a metaphorical ledge, begging for honesty, secrets and shared pain rather than the widening disconnect between them. He offers to shoulder the blame and break himself while mending the other, desperate to know whether something worth saving remains or whether it's already too far gone.","sonnet":"One person trying to talk another off the ledge by offering radical honesty — taking the blame, breaking themselves fixing the other, just to make it through one more night. The urgency of \"is it too far gone?\" is the question neither of them can answer, and the song ends still asking.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~channel-for-the-pain":{"haiku":"Persistent loneliness and a refusal to engage with life, where suffering itself becomes the only constant in existence.","opus":"A study of isolation and passivity, where the narrator lives alone, never chooses, and expects only to lose. His very existence becomes a channel for pain as his mind breaks and he avoids seeking the truth.","sonnet":"A speaker who lives alone and seems to have always lost — never choosing, never seeking, never demanding — finds that his very existence has become a conduit for pain, and accepts this not with resistance but with passive, broken endurance.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of paralysis dressed up as reasoning, a speaker who argues his way into stillness: he declines every decision because defeat is already promised, and even victory, he insists, would patch only trivial cracks and leave the isolation untouched. That solitude, not any surface complaint, is the wound he keeps orbiting without daring to answer the question he opens with. The title line dissolves the riddle, since refusing to demand truth is what preserves his suffering, and suffering has become his job, his whole being reduced to an open conduit that justifies itself by never closing. In the Paradise Lost register this is gothic-doom despair delivered as cold syllogism rather than wail, the mask passive while beneath it self-pity has hardened into an appetite for hurt, until that repeated opening question stops reading as wonder and settles into a rut worn smooth.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"suffering kept open as a job rather than a wound to close","built":"the opening question recurs until it stops reading as wonder and settles into a rut worn smooth"},
"paradise-lost~darker-thoughts":{"haiku":"Warns against inner emptiness and the seductive path toward violence or suicide, with darker ideation persistently returning. Questions faith versus damnation.","opus":"A warning to someone lost in a false, dead-end recovery that will never satisfy and could get them killed, as calm slips away and grim urges creep back for good. A refrain twists a commandment about not killing into a pledge of destruction. Paradise Lost, the pioneering English gothic-doom band, open their 2020 self-titled album with this despairing reckoning, decades of melancholic heaviness boiled down to dread and lurking violence.","sonnet":"A warning to someone on a self-destructive one-way street — healing obscured by years of grief, emotional numbness dressed as ascension, a path that will never bring fulfilment and might kill them. When inner peace is gone the darker thoughts come back permanently. The theological frame — god asks not to kill, the godless sworn to kill — underlines how far the person has drifted from any sanctuary.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~don-t-belong":{"haiku":"The speaker feels rejected and misunderstood despite their efforts, belonging nowhere regardless of circumstance, humiliated daily by those who perceive them as out of place.","opus":"An outsider's lament, standing in the way and accused, insisting that only in his absence will people finally turn to him. He pleads for a little understanding for all he's done, knowing he belongs only in some other situation. Paradise Lost, the pioneering British gothic-doom band, give the alienation their brooding melodic weight, the refrain toggling between not belonging and, wistfully, imagining he would.","sonnet":"The quiet grief of someone who has never quite fit — told they are in the way, betrayed, humiliated day after day, they carry the knowledge that in some alternate situation they would belong but here, in this one, they simply don't. The prediction that absence will eventually matter arrives without anger, only a worn resignation.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~erased": {"haiku":"The narrator severs all connections and identity, erasing themselves completely as if they never existed—a statement of ultimate detachment from faith and relationships.","opus":"A declaration of debt-free detachment and self-erasure: the narrator owes and knows no one, dismisses faith as fiction, and, unable to repair his situation, resolves to leave no trace behind; the cold refusal to belong reads as a quiet embrace of oblivion.","sonnet":"About radical disconnection from social obligation and religious faith — the narrator owes nothing to anyone, considers faith fiction, and moves through life trying to leave no trace, no debt, no lasting mark.","src":"haiku","fable":"A defiant renunciation that keeps overreaching into oblivion: the opening insists on owing nothing to anyone, a proud severing of debt and belonging that Holmes delivers as strength but the song quietly exposes as a wound. The refrain turns on a deliberate near-homophone — owing nothing to anyone slides into knowing no one — so the boast of independence and the confession of total isolation become the same line, while the whispered underside treats belief as a hollow story he no longer keeps, stripping away the last thing that might connect him to something larger. By the bridge the promise hardens into an act rather than a feeling: the record wiped clean and carried out on the spot, the self and its ties scheduled for deletion. It reads less as anger than as the cold aftermath of it — the moment when cutting everyone off stops feeling like freedom and reveals itself as being erased.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"independence and isolation revealed as the same line","built":"owing nothing slides into knowing no one via near-homophone"},
"paradise-lost~faith-divides-us-death-unites-us":{"haiku":"Religious belief separates humanity while death alone brings unity and peace. The narrator urges transcending faith-based division.","opus":"A meditation on faith as a divider and death as the great equalizer; refusing to seek despair or watch things fail, the narrator wants to vanquish the pain and finds in mortality the one thing that unites all humanity, unable to dream or sleep 'until it's over.'","sonnet":"A reflection on what separates and connects people — religious belief fractures communities but mortality is the one leveler that everyone eventually faces together, and only by vanquishing pain can you move past that division.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~forever-after":{"haiku":"Something orchestrated by another hand controls belief and dreams; one wishes to see and live the dream but doubts it is possible as frustration replaces the master plan.","opus":"A resigned reflection on human frailty and lost faith, the singer feeling his life is steered by forces beyond him even as the ideals he once held fall apart. Paradise Lost, the English gothic-metal band, cast this in their brooding doom melancholy, the future clinging together on little more than a fading, uncertain wish.","sonnet":"A fragile song about the moment when dreams become too thin to sustain — orchestrated by forces outside understanding, belief increasing even as the dream drains away. The refrain holds both longing and uncertainty: forever after, maybe. Frailty, frailty. Love asked to wait. A faint hand reaching toward something already half-gone.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~grey":{"haiku":"A person rejects compromise and confronts shame and silence, while grappling with a beauty that feels wrong. Life fades like the dead, leaving only debt behind.","opus":"A painful act of self-liberation undertaken for the narrator alone, marked by shame and a name that silence has ingrained. Paradise Lost circle a paradox, something both damaged and lovely, capturing the ambivalence of change, set against images of fading away and life owed like a debt as a bond is undone.","sonnet":"Grieving or mourning someone through a kind of ritual self-reproach — calling out in shame, crying a name, doing this for oneself rather than for any audience, with the sense that life is a debt that fades to nothing like the dead.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~hallowed-land":{"haiku":"A journey of suffering and seeking, with a speaker torn between escape and entrapment, exploring the search for a sacred place of peace while acknowledging spiritual exhaustion.","opus":"A weary call toward a sacred refuge close at hand yet destined to end in agony, addressed to a troublemaking outsider, urging someone to live fully every single day even as struggle keeps repeating until they're worn down and aching. Escape and endurance tug against each other. Paradise Lost, the English gothic-doom band, offer this nineties meditation on seeking sanctuary and exhaustion, promise dwindling into memory.","sonnet":"An exhausted person seeking escape — pale reflection, a mountain or a land somewhere else where the turmoil might end. Those around them have weakened and worn them out through repetition, and what they seek is some hallowed ground beyond all that. The song ends in being forgotten, a silhouette subsumed into distance: a memory in someone else's short time of promise.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~honesty-in-death":{"haiku":"In the midst of madness and anguish, one speaks honesty and modesty facing death—it is clemency and calm that turn suffering into virtue.","opus":"Paradise Lost, the English gothic-metal pioneers, turn their funereal gloom inward. The song faces not the world but the grave, insisting that only dying offers any real truth or humility, as private anguish curdles and every prayer is directed at the self. Death embraced as the last place sincerity can survive.","sonnet":"Madness and grief compressed into private prayer — the speaker has no one to address but themselves, resistance crumbling under clemency's weight, sadness turned to hell. What remains is a kind of austere dignity: honesty in death, modesty until the end. No performance for anyone else. Just the bare fact of an interior finally made truthful.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~host":{"haiku":"Someone sees the vital signs fade in another's weary eyes while time erodes their defenses. The other is alive but can't feel; aiming to help only misses.","opus":"Recorded during the band's synth-pop departure from their gothic-doom roots, this coolly watches someone whose life force is quietly ebbing behind exhausted eyes. The narrator sees past the effortful mask to a person draining away, judging it too late for any correction to land. Decay and malfunction hollow the subject out, living yet numb, awaiting the reckoning that time itself will deliver.","sonnet":"An observer watches someone they know dissolve — the light behind the eyes gone, vital signs slipping, all the careful pretending giving way. The message is unsentimental: the consequences were always coming, there is no fighting what was never fought in time, and it is far too late to aim at anything.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~in-this-we-dwell":{"haiku":"Humanity adapts to suffering, darkness, and blame as dead voices cry for souls trapped in hell and spite; dwelling in torment becomes inevitable existence within a prison of collective damnation.","opus":"A gothic vision of the doomed, souls locked in a tower of malice, growing used to unending agony and struggling on to the grave. Underneath is a self-built hell of blame and suffering we occupy ourselves, the dead calling out for the singer's soul from the bleak husk they all inhabit.","sonnet":"The song describes a shared existence inside a spiritual and psychological prison — torment, blame, and the chorus of the dead; there is no exit, only an ongoing dwelling in this dark state.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~isolate": {"haiku":"A condemned person faces erasure and torture, isolated beyond hope. They embrace isolation as inevitable and surrender to torment.","opus":"The voice of one condemned to elimination by an unknown authority, lost without hope yet embracing the very instrument of his end. Beneath the fatalism is a yearning to be led toward greener pastures and harmony, and a resigned acceptance that he can never be the better person he glimpses in others.","sonnet":"About a person who feels targeted for erasure by unnamed forces — lost, decaying, unable to avoid what's coming — and who turns that isolation into a kind of acceptance, yearning for a harmony and wholeness they can't access from within. The repeated \"Isolate\" functions as both sentence and identity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built on a split of grammar: the verses speak in a condemned first person, sentenced by some unnamed authority to elimination, drifting toward a self whose mind and soul are already decaying, while the chorus swells into a collective we begging to be led to green pastures and harmony. The trick is that the destination the many long for is exactly the thing the one confesses he can never become — the pastoral we is a place the singer is locked out of, and the burning, the tourniquet he embraces, is the private cost of that exile. Isolate lands as both verb and verdict: the title word closes every verse not as a plea but as the sentence being carried out, the speaker sealing himself off in the same breath the crowd sings toward togetherness. It arrives at the point in Paradise Lost's arc where the doom-death origins had thinned into a sleeker, keyboard-lit gothic rock, and the song uses that cleaner surface against itself, letting a near-hymnal chorus carry a lyric about being permanently outside the fold.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","death & grief","madness & the mind"],"means":"the title word closes each verse as sentence not plea","built":"condemned first person locked outside the collective hymnal chorus"},
"paradise-lost~last-regret":{"haiku":"An expression of final surrender and regret, exploring the acceptance of mortality, denied freedom, and the grim resignation that comes with the end.","opus":"The English gothic-doom band strips a deathbed to its bones, a dying man cycling between the will to keep trying and the will to give up, feeling hollow, robbed of freedom, sinking into darkness. Everything narrows to one demand, that his final words and his one great regret be heard, their trademark bleakness pared to essentials.","sonnet":"A closing statement — last words, last regret, freedom denied. The will to die is killed so that adaptation and time can do their work, but the tone is terminal rather than triumphant. What is said at the end is addressed to no one specified, weighted with the sense that the last ones have been here before and know exactly what it costs.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~made-the-same":{"haiku":"A reflection on shared human vulnerability and mortality, asserting that all people are fundamentally made of the same material and subject to the same struggles and fears.","opus":"A gesture of shared humanity, the singer offering plain comfort and insisting he's no different from the person he addresses, prone to the same misfortune and fear. He warns anyone chasing immortality through vanity that they've already failed. Paradise Lost temper their gloom here into something almost consoling, favoring common ground over ego.","sonnet":"A refusal of hierarchy built on shared fallibility — we are made of the same material, and whatever ill fortune or fear of rejection marks the speaker is simply their particular path through a common condition. Those who think they will live forever are already losing the game. The cold is long, the years are many, and no one escapes the shared making.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~mouth":{"haiku":"Dependence and dissolution: the speaker tastes resentment and lowness while only one person provides symptom relief; they question escape or surrender to terminal fate.","opus":"Gothic-metal pioneers Paradise Lost sink into a numb, codependent despair in which comfort itself feels like a trap. The narrator pins every symptom of his misery on one specific person, muses about a glamorous exit, and lands instead in a shared, dead-end damnation that has dragged on for years.","sonnet":"A speaker confesses that another person is the source of all his symptoms — the low days, the obscured vision, the resentful restlessness. The dependency is acknowledged without resolution; the only certainty is a shared destination in termination, which seems less like hope than like the one fact nobody can argue with.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~mystify": {"haiku":"Shame and deception complicate love as simple lies destroy people's lives. The speaker rejects being mystified by false expressions of affection and demands clarity instead.","opus":"A weary reckoning with shame and hollow pleasures, the singer accepting that he has to change. Paradise Lost, the English gothic-metal pioneers, deliver an odd request to be frightened rather than deceived, alongside the deadpan observation that even a small deception can wreck you, disillusionment stated flatly and without drama.","sonnet":"A person worn down by shame and disillusionment demands transparency from a love that has hidden itself behind mystification. The relationship has drained whatever faith once existed, and now even celebration feels like threat — all that remains is the plea to stop obscuring what is felt.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built as its own mirror: the first verse accepts and resents a world it once trusted, the second rejects and disclaims that same guilt, so the two halves rhyme as accusation and defense of one wound. The chorus is a demand disguised as a plea — it wants the beloved to celebrate and terrify rather than mystify, choosing any honest extreme over the fog of a love that hides its terms. The bridge names the machinery under it all: not some grand betrayal but a small, ordinary lie is what does the bringing-down, and the song's refusal to resolve that line, repeating it until it dies mid-phrase, enacts the very unresolved suspicion it describes. This is Symbol of Life-era Paradise Lost writing the gothic despair as a relationship in bad faith, Holmes's plainer clean voice trading dread for a cornered, adult resentment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"a small ordinary lie named as the thing that does the bringing-down","built":"bridge line repeated until it dies mid-phrase — enacting the unresolved suspicion it describes"},
"paradise-lost~never-look-away":{"haiku":"Domination breeds shame and suffering; the speaker faces darkness and deprivation, unable to turn away despite terror and despair's reign.","opus":"A dense, hymn-like brooding on submission, guilt and mourning, in which existence is framed as endless torment and any devotion only deepens the pain. Paradise Lost turn the piece into a study of morbid fixation: the narrator is terrified to survey his surroundings yet cannot stop peering into ruin and death.","sonnet":"A claustrophobic meditation on fate, suffering, and the soul led astray by darkness — the narrator is trapped between submission and resentment, facing a force that honors pain and dishonors life, with fear making it impossible to look either down or away.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~never-take-me-alive":{"haiku":"A fugitive flees from law enforcement after committing murder; they call their mother one last time before inevitable capture or death.","opus":"A doomed outlaw phones his mother during his getaway, admitting he killed a man who tried to block his escape and vowing he'll die before he's captured, closing with a helpless declaration of love. Paradise Lost, the Yorkshire gothic-metal act, cover this Spear of Destiny track with mournful tenderness.","sonnet":"A fugitive's last phone call to his mother — already on the run, out of change, having crossed the line into killing to avoid being stopped. The confession is delivered with strange tenderness: he can't explain how he feels, isn't sure she'd want to know, but needs her to hear he loves her before the ending he's already chosen closes in.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~no-celebration": {"haiku":"Someone finds no joy or connection on New Year's Day, rejecting celebration and human warmth. They are detached from the rituals that others use to mark time and meaning.","opus":"Emotional withdrawal staged against the ritual of a fresh calendar year. Paradise Lost have the narrator go through the motions of showing up while flatly announcing he feels nothing to mark and offers no warmth, comfort and wealth dismissed as hollow. A song about ceremonially refusing to celebrate anything at all.","sonnet":"A person withdraws from communal ritual on a new year, feeling no connection to the shared optimism around them. The detachment is not hostile — it is simply honest. There is comfort in neither wealth nor togetherness when the emotional reality cannot meet the occasion.","src":"haiku","fable":"A refusal pinned to the calendar's most obligatory occasion: the first of January, the appointed hour of renewal and toasting, is precisely when this speaker declines to celebrate. The verses stage the standoff between escape and worldliness — a wish to be excused from the old scenes on one side, the cold comfort of gold and rising demands on the other — while the winter is greeted with a hurrah that lands as pure sarcasm. What actually moves is the chorus, which erodes across its three passes: an initial I-can-meet-you offer curdles into you-won't-hear resignation and finally into a we-don't-care that folds both parties into the same indifference. By the last turn the not-caring has stopped being a wound and become a shared, unhesitating verdict, and Holmes delivers it not as grief but as the flat, gothic-cold register Paradise Lost had by 2002 traded their doom roots for.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape"],"means":"the appointed hour of renewal chosen as the moment to decline","built":"chorus erodes across three passes from offer to resignation to shared indifference"},
"paradise-lost~no-hope-in-sight":{"haiku":"Despair and exhaustion dominate as time fades and danger remains constant; the only certainty is that suffering continues without relief.","opus":"A bleak vigil as light fades and the terrified are laid to rest, prayer offered in bondage to endless weeping. Paradise Lost frames a chilly embrace as a disguised deliverer and casts aging as the one peril that always endures. Confronting fear across withering years becomes a losing fight, with any hope kept just out of reach as time relentlessly drains away.","sonnet":"Old age and encroaching darkness are framed as adversaries no one escapes — only the danger persists where everything else fades. The horror is collective and quiet, a slow erasure of those who were once afraid and now face the final confirmation that there was never a way through.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~perfect-mask": {"haiku":"A person lives behind a perfect mask that erases truth forever, wanting desperately to be revealed and disgraced so they can finally live authentically.","opus":"English gothic-doom pioneers Paradise Lost dramatize a life lived behind a flawless disguise that both hides and finally frees the truth, arriving too late for anything but living at last. The narrator begs for help while daring someone to expose him, the mask serving at once as his prison and his only survival.","sonnet":"Life lived entirely behind a constructed face — the mask concealing truth, erasing it, serving as the only way the speaker has known how to exist. The recognition arrives late: too late, but still time to live at last. A plea for help follows, admitting there is no other way forward, asking to be revealed even if disgrace comes with it. The mask begins to crack.","src":"haiku","fable":"Almost the entire song is one line repeated until it becomes a wall, and the meaning lives in the small contradictions the wall lets slip. The mask is asked in one breath to let the truth break loose and in the next to bury it, so the disguise and the hidden self keep swapping places until you cannot tell which is the concealment. Holmes's register — this being Paradise Lost's electronic-tinged Symbol of Life turn away from pure doom — plays the refrain not as boast but as a trapped man's mantra, the too-late admission that living at last is only available from behind the face he cannot put down. The one plea that breaks the loop is a demand to be exposed and disgraced, which reads the mask's real terror: not being seen through, but wanting to be and staying sealed anyway, the refrain closing back over him because there is, as he keeps saying, nowhere left for him to run.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"wanting exposure but staying sealed by the mask itself","built":"single refrain looped until its own contradiction surfaces"},
"paradise-lost~pray-nightfall": {"haiku":"Someone is trapped in their own mind, exhausted by the struggle to live and find meaning. They long for sleep and escape, begging for release from the weight of existence.","opus":"A study of an overactive, tormented mind that begs for night and sleep as its only relief, aware the whole struggle is happening inside his own head. Paradise Lost, the pioneering British gothic-doom band, give this longing for oblivion a heavy, atmospheric weight, rest offered as the sole escape from restless thought.","sonnet":"Exhausted by wakefulness and the grinding effort of trying to synchronize with a life that offers no serendipity, a person pleads for the release of deep sleep. Nightfall is not feared but prayed for — the only threshold where the mind can finally stop trying to make things right and simply wander into rest.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sleepless mind pleading for oblivion, where the recurring insistence that this all is only in his head works both ways at once — a self-soothing dismissal and a confession that the torment has no exit outside his own skull. The prayer is inverted from the gothic norm: night is not the horror but the mercy, the only door to the dreamless dark that daylight and consciousness keep denying him. Paradise Lost stage the two verses as a broken clock — one begs rest before morning arrives, the other wakes to a sun that shines like an accusation — so the whole song rocks between dread of waking and craving for release without ever landing. The bridge drops the imagery for bare exhaustion, straining to bolt his days back into order without a scrap of luck to carry it, and Holmes's weary delivery lets that plainness read as the sound of someone worn past metaphor. What refuses resolution here is the point: the closing repetitions of the plea don't resolve, they just circle, because relief only ever comes when the head finally goes quiet.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","freedom & escape"],"means":"night pleaded for as mercy rather than feared as horror","built":"two verses as a broken clock — dread of waking and craving for release that never resolves"},
"paradise-lost~primal": {"haiku":"Deep forces hidden in the mind work against redemption as life passes by; without insight and self-compassion, people end up the same way—abused until death.","opus":"Paradise Lost, the English gothic-metal band, sink into fatalism about a base urge lodged in the psyche. The warning is not to take pride, since everyone arrives at the same finish regardless, salvation withheld while life drains past. A weary plea to a higher power for support is the only thin comfort offered.","sonnet":"Something deep and unavoidable in the mind that cannot be felt or outrun — pride will lose if you grip it, redemption is denied as life passes by, and everyone ends the same way regardless. The speaker repeats the plea not to be let down while still moving forward, caught between the knowledge that the thing dividing them from peace is primal and the hope that movement is still possible.","src":"haiku","fable":"A fatalist's prayer that turns pride and self-abuse into a shared verdict. Every verse lands on the same terminal refrain: one dying breath awaits every one of us alike. The song's real content is the pronoun quietly sliding from I to you to we until the fate is universal rather than personal. Against that flattening, the chorus reaches upward — a plea to a Lord not to be let down while the road still runs. But the surrounding verses keep denying redemption and letting life pass by, so the appeal reads less as faith than as the last reflex left when the outcome is already fixed. This is Symbol of Life's more accessible, almost anthemic register, and Holmes uses that lift ironically: the uplift in the melody is stapled to a lyric whose whole logic is that nothing can be escaped, the primal leveling of death underneath every posture of control.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","freedom & escape"],"means":"anthemic lift stapled to a lyric that cancels every escape","built":"pronoun slides from I to you to we until death is universal not personal"},
"paradise-lost~punishment-through-time":{"haiku":"The speaker addresses cycles of repressed grief, vengeance, and suffering that haunt those who conceal crises and reject emotional reckoning, trapped in perpetual despair.","opus":"A grim liturgy of decay and vengeance, repentance and disgrace circling a soul that conceals its crisis while reaching untied toward sudden death. Depressed goals and repressed souls await punishment that stretches across time itself. Paradise Lost, in their early death-doom incarnation, render it as suffocating dread, the title's endless sentence hanging over imagery of tombs, flames, and yearning slaves to judgment.","sonnet":"Guilt and spiritual dread locked in an eternal loop — repentance endlessly deferred, vengeance waiting regardless, souls trapped in disgrace across time without release. The sufferer conceals a crisis beneath a controlled surface, reaching toward death as the only available motion, condemned by forces that feel both cosmic and entirely self-made.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~say-just-words":{"haiku":"A confrontation with manipulation and hate speech, rejecting someone's claims of victory while asserting that words alone hold no true power.","opus":"The Yorkshire pioneers of gothic-doom cast this as a standoff against a corrosive, hateful presumed victor. The narrator refuses to break under repeated mind-tricks and contradiction, taunting that friendship's warmth will never reach such a person. The recurring plea to just say words reads as dare and dismissal at once, insisting the aggressor's talk is empty and his triumph imagined.","sonnet":"Someone endures a person who feeds on power and spreads corrosive hostility, yet cannot break them. The speaker strips the aggressor of meaning — labelling every threat, every contradiction, every hollow dominance play as nothing but empty words that carry no real weight and leave no real wound.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~self-obsessed": {"haiku":"Two self-absorbed people hurt each other with wrong words and misery; they let it go but dream of escape—the love they wish for will never come.","opus":"A bitter, unflinching breakup admission in which the narrator owns his selfishness and the harm his words did, decides the relationship isn't worth salvaging, and coolly wishes the other person out of existence. Paradise Lost, mid gothic-rock reinvention, wrap the cruelty and fatigue in the same weary, restrained melody.","sonnet":"A relationship's autopsy delivered in the first person by the one who caused the damage — wrong words, self-indulgence, a person whose presence became unbearable rather than sustaining. The exit is stalled, taken too slowly, and all that remains is the wish to be absent, to dream the other person isn't there, to simply let it all go.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is an accusation the narrator turns on himself and only half-means, a confession that keeps sliding into blame. He opens by owning that he thinks too much of himself and that he chose the phrases badly, then quietly reroutes the fault to a partner whose vanity has shrunk him down to something to be used. What sounds like an apology is really an exhausted verdict that certain efforts no longer earn the trouble. The soft refrain that tells the whole thing to end and lie still masks something colder underneath: the urge to picture the other simply gone, the admission that his despair floods in the instant the other draws air. In Holmes's register the cruelty stays understated, which is the point. This is the sound of a love collapsing into indifference, each speaker too absorbed to reach the other. When the opening verse and chorus return whole after the interlude, the deadlock is enacted rather than described, the argument circling back with nothing resolved.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance"],"means":"mutual absorption hollowing love into indifference","built":"opening verse returns whole after interlude, deadlock enacted not described"},
"paradise-lost~small-town-boy": {"haiku":"A young person escaping their hometown, bullied and misunderstood, fleeing to find elsewhere the love and acceptance they cannot find at home.","opus":"A goth-metal cover of Bronski Beat's story of a gay teen fleeing a hostile town, leaving at dawn with everything he owns because home holds no love he needs. Paradise Lost's heaviness draws out the isolation and the tears cried only to his own soul.","sonnet":"A cover of Bronski Beat's song: a gay young man flees a small town where he was bullied and rejected, leaving behind a mother who will never understand why — the only honest crying he ever did was to himself, the only escape was to run.","src":"haiku","fable":"Paradise Lost inherit Bronski Beat's 1984 coded anthem of a gay teenager fleeing his hometown, and what the cover selects is its grief rather than its dancefloor defiance — the lone suitcase holding a whole life, set down on the platform, reads as exile, the persecution unnamed but total. The original wrapped that pain in bright synth-pop so it could still move a body; Holmes's leaden gothic delivery strips the consolation off, so the twin imperatives to flee and to show the town his back land less as escape than as a reflex of survival. The song's real engine is its withheld and then unleashed weeping: for three verses the boy cries only inward, to his soul, refusing his tormentors the satisfaction — until the bridge collapses into a naked, repeated command to cry that finally grants the release the whole song has been clenching against.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["exile & diaspora","freedom & escape","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"gothic delivery strips consolation from a survival reflex","built":"withheld weeping released only at the bridge's naked command"},
"paradise-lost~so-much-is-lost":{"opus":"A disorienting sense of losing oneself as life changes uncontrollably, where identity feels unreal and everything seems wasted; the recurring lament is of loss, disharmony, and the absence of any sympathetic voice.","sonnet":"A person reckoning with a fundamentally changed life — their identity feels unreal, their past seems wasted, and a faceless enemy of their own fear or self-deception is responsible; the refrain is simple grief at the scale of what has been lost.","src":"sonnet"},
"paradise-lost~solitary-one":{"haiku":"A speaker is weakened and drained by their connection to a solitary figure who offers no sustenance, their repeated pleas going unanswered in a bloodless existence.","opus":"From the band's early death-doom era, this drags through exhaustion and temptation toward a lone figure the narrator both begs and is enfeebled by. A love that won't perish feeds off him like a parasite, salvation glimpsed only through crimson-soaked sight. Ruin and lifelessness bind the pair, the isolated one a source of dependence that drains rather than nourishes.","sonnet":"A figure is pulled apart by obsession with someone who isolates and drains them — love that refuses to die only tightens the grip. Exhaustion and damnation blend; the solitary one holds power over blood and will, leaving the speaker weakened yet unable to stop pleading for release.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~spirit":{"haiku":"Spirits and human experience blur into sameness; people suffer together, sympathize together, and surrender in mutual obligation.","opus":"A jaded lament about people flattening into interchangeable copies of one another as insight rots and conviction curdles into hurt. Paradise Lost twist a false claim of closeness into a running accusation that the other party is in his debt for every tear and every capitulation he's endured. Disillusionment given gothic weight.","sonnet":"Disillusionment with collective consolation — spirit, community, shared meaning have all decayed into performance, everyone saying the same things, everyone the same. Beneath the surface resentment is the wound of feeling known only superficially, owed something that will never be paid back, surrendering to a life of endurance rather than belonging.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~symbol-of-life": {"haiku":"A person wallows in isolation and loss, equating themselves with a loaded gun—a weapon bound to themselves as a symbol of their empty, tormented existence.","opus":"A portrait of suicidal despair: alone, having hit rock bottom, half-convinced that death is fine while still faintly hoping someone will intervene. The loaded gun becomes the defining symbol of a life reduced to loss, with nothing left to say.","sonnet":"A person so hollowed by loss and depression that a loaded gun becomes the defining image of their existence — they can't get by, can't stop mourning what's gone, and have retreated entirely from the world.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track makes a chambered round the emblem of a life measured entirely by loss — Nick Holmes narrating from the floor, having reached a bottom where death starts to sound not like terror but like permission. Paradise Lost had by 2002 traded their doom origins for a leaner gothic-rock sweep, and the writing follows suit: two verses that repeat almost word for word, moving only from torment to rejection, from waiting for someone to try to merely wishing they would, so the song enacts a mind that circles rather than progresses. The weapon stands in for the whole self precisely because it stays holstered — a permanent option held rather than used, the one fixed point in a self that can no longer get by on what it has. The outro abandons argument entirely, the same phrase about having no words left tolling over and over until repetition itself becomes the meaning: not a decision reached but a voice run out, the depletion the earlier verses only described.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","alienation & emptiness","death & grief"],"means":"death reframed as permission rather than terror","built":"two verses repeating almost word-for-word enact the circling mind"},
"paradise-lost~the-last-time":{"opus":"The suffocating dread of a relationship's closing minutes, as the narrator watches his partner linger by the exit, ready to leave. Drained from tending to everyone but himself, he can only hope the ache will pass quickly. Paradise Lost stretch the moment out until two pulses synchronize one final time before the parting.","sonnet":"The slow disintegration of a relationship as one partner emotionally checks out — the narrator feels suffocated and erased while the other is already mentally at the door, and the repeating heartbeat motif counts down to a final goodbye.","src":"sonnet"},
"paradise-lost~tragic-idol":{"haiku":"A tainted savior figure offers false redemption through darkness rather than light. The song explores how charisma and damaged souls can pose as salvation while delivering only fragmentation.","opus":"The gothic paradox of a savior who is also the ruin, salvation and destruction bound in the same dark figure while the self keeps slipping out of one's grasp. Devotion here erodes the worshipper the more it takes.","sonnet":"A figure who once gave the narrator purpose and rescue — a dark, tainted soul whose presence felt like salvation — has slipped away, leaving only the echo of that dependency and the sense of control forever lost.","src":"haiku"},
"paradise-lost~two-worlds": {"haiku":"A person exists in confusion and imprisonment without clarity or freedom of movement, describing two worlds that remain indistinguishable and offer no escape from complaint.","opus":"The gothic-doom band voice a consciousness worn down to almost nothing, sealed inside its own failing mind like a cell. Cut off from his own body and unable to escape, he insists he mustn't grumble even as failure and borrowed time pile up, while to onlookers he registers only as foolish.","sonnet":"A person navigates existence in a state of profound dissociation — unable to see their own hands, unable to walk away from a prison of their own making. Two worlds collapse into sameness, failures stack on borrowed time, and the only posture left is a defeated refusal to complain about any of it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person account of a self sealed inside its own diminishing faculties, narrated by a man who claims he has never once laid eyes on his own hands — no agency, no recognition that the failures were his to own. The song's controlling image splits one overwhelming force in two: a vast watching thing that aches to look becomes, in the second verse, a bolted and airless cell, so surveillance and confinement turn out to be the same condition felt from inside and out. That doubling is the two worlds — waking and inner, the observed and the trapped — flattening into an indistinguishable sameness by tomorrow, which is why the only response left is the refusal to complain. Holmes reads it not as gothic grandeur but as depressive stasis: down to his last percent, seeming inane to everyone, he has lost even the vocabulary to protest, and that final resignation is the mask itself, a man performing composure over total depletion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","surveillance & control"],"means":"surveillance and confinement revealed as the same condition felt from inside and out","built":"controlling image splits one force in two — the watching thing becomes a bolted cell — then flattens back to sameness"},
"paradise-lost~xavier": {"haiku":"Xavier prays for redemption from a past of sin and judgment, hoping divine mercy will cleanse souls, but freedom remains bound by law.","opus":"An early gothic-doom parable of heresy and doomed devotion from the English pioneers, weighing a woman condemned as sinful against a man praying for grace to heal humanity's corruption. It circles original sin, the limits of freedom under nature's law, and a love shackled and denied, all wrapped in the band's ornate, funereal sound.","sonnet":"Xavier prays for life-giving waters to cleanse the souls of men, but his own sins hang like jewels in a forest of veils, and a woman named Roseanna whose vagrant life was judged profane moves through the same world of heresy and clemency. Original sin, freedom constrained by nature's laws, love in chains: the night may or may not give way to illuminating day, and the nightmares may or may not come home to stay.","src":"haiku","fable":"A parable of institutional faith turned against the body it claims to save: Xavier, cast as a baptizing missionary who prays for a downpour to wash mankind clean of its ways, is set against Roseanna, a wandering woman condemned as profane and swept up in the machinery of heresy and clemency. Paradise Lost frame the primal guilt handed down from Eden as the real inheritance here — Eve bearing the stigma, freedom impossible where every law is treated as etched into nature itself — so that the saint's piety and the sinner's damnation are exposed as two faces of the same accusation. The pivot comes when Xavier's own sins surface, strung as jewels through a thicket of veils, and his love ends in chains: the redeemer is bound by the same code he preached, and the closing image asks whether dawn will bring illumination or only the return of the nightmares. Holmes lets the elevated, near-liturgical diction do the ironizing, reading the mask of holiness and the guilt underneath it at once.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","shame & repression","love & desire"],"means":"saint and sinner exposed as two names for the same guilt","built":"liturgical diction ironizes the holiness it imitates"},
"paradise-lost~year-of-summer":{"haiku":"A person watches someone spiral into regret and self-destruction, their concern met with dismissal. The singer struggles with their own mental collapse while observing the other's unwillingness to change course.","opus":"A cold reckoning with someone whose tears fall on command and whose contradictory, bruised life the narrator has chosen to tolerate rather than dismiss, the sadness ultimately claimed as his own. Paradise Lost render the emotional standoff in gothic melancholy, the truth turning worse with sobriety as demand and defense circle a relationship worn thin.","sonnet":"A relationship examined with cold clarity reveals manipulation, performed grief, and a life lived in contradiction. The narrator has chosen tolerance where the other chose dismissal, and the sober recognition — that truth is always harder to hold without the cushion of illusion — arrives with a sadness claimed as entirely his own.","src":"haiku"},
"parallyx~matriochka":{"haiku":"A hunter stalks and threatens its prey with inescapable violence; exposure of secrets seals the victim's fate.","opus":"A menacing metalcore predator anthem voiced from the hunter's side, taunting prey who glimpsed a secret and must now run. The pursuer is deliberately, gleefully insane, promising to chase the victim down until their screams end forever and no one thinks to search. The nested-doll title hints at layers of buried horror; a chase narrative dripping with sadistic inevitability.","sonnet":"A predator addresses its prey directly, step by step: you had to look, now you know you're in trouble, you'll never scream again — the hunter is insane, the victim already alone and buried, the song a tight spiral toward a throat in a hand and silence.","src":"haiku"},
"paramore~crushcrushcrush":{"haiku":"Surveillance and censorship suppress authentic expression while the speaker remains silenced despite resistance, truth repeatedly erased by manipulation and control.","opus":"Hayley Williams turns a crush into defiance against the doubters trying to silence it, preferring to waste her life pretending than forget the object of it. Beneath the pop-punk hook is the loneliness of wanting something to be real.","sonnet":"A person with intense feelings for someone whose authentic voice has been suppressed by others fantasises about breaking the silence — choosing the game of pretending rather than losing the connection entirely. The longing is for genuine intimacy in a world that keeps taping over the truth.","src":"haiku"},
"paramore~decode-twilight-soundtrack-version":{"opus":"The confusion and pride of a relationship where one person constantly clouds the narrator's judgment and takes sides against her; she vows to figure herself out alone while sensing something dangerous and possibly untrue that she still wants to believe in.","sonnet":"A person trying to understand how a relationship they once felt certain about became unreadable — they're convinced the other person is hiding or deceiving them, and the song is a frustrated, anguished attempt to decode what went wrong and who they're really dealing with.","src":"sonnet"},
"paramore~misery-business":{"haiku":"Calculated seduction and competitive victory mask the speaker's hunger for dominance; she embodies the very predatory logic she performs.","opus":"A teenage triumph over a romantic rival, gleeful and unrepentant about winning the boy, later disowned by Williams for its slut-shaming. Its honesty about petty vindictive joy is exactly what made it stick and what she outgrew.","sonnet":"A woman recounts stealing a man from a manipulative rival with glee, then pivots into a condemnation of women who weaponise innocence to get what they want — the triumph sours into self-awareness as she watches her own wildest dreams play out without the other woman anywhere in them.","src":"haiku"},
"paranoid-void~a-3c2qov":{"haiku":"A fragmented plea for cosmic ending and romantic unity—asking the eternal to bring love and end the world together.","src":"haiku"},
"park-hye-jin~let-s-sing-let-s-dance":{"opus":"Park Hye Jin makes minimal house where a few murmured Korean phrases carry all the feeling, and this track distills that austerity into a remedy. Music becomes emotional medicine: singing on sad days, sharing the dancing on good ones with someone loved. Even eyes closed and hands held, what cannot be seen can be felt, the whole song a repeated, patient invitation to be present.","sonnet":"A Korean song about using music and dance as emotional medicine: on sad days the narrator sings, and on good days they want to share that dancing with the person they love — even with eyes closed, holding hands, what you can't see you can feel; the song is a simple, repeated invitation to be present through movement.","src":"sonnet"},
"parkway-drive~carrion":{"haiku":"Guilt and loss consume the speaker from within; memory of beloved sustains the drowning soul's promise of eventual return.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with heartbreak framed as death, the singer torn apart from within by a love he's broken and vows to return to. The imagery of a land of the dead makes lost love feel like a place he keeps walking back into.","sonnet":"A relationship in mutual ruin: dead eyes, hearts broken and stolen, both people running blind and falling through cracks back to a world of the dead. The speaker left carrying a promise to return — but return means re-entering destruction, and the song ends suspended between that promise and the wreckage it implies.","src":"haiku"},
"parkway-drive~dark-days":{"haiku":"Environmental collapse and generational failure coalesce into funeral for the Earth; apathy hardens into apocalypse.","opus":"Climate apocalypse rendered as apocalypse in the biblical sense, the pale horse riding over a wasteland the previous generation left behind. The Byron Bay metalcore band aims the accusation squarely at the elders who chose apathy while the clock ran out.","sonnet":"A lament and warning directed at future generations, asking what justification can be given for watching the planet die through apathy and greed — the pale horse of apocalypse arriving as the inevitable consequence of a civilisation that refused to learn from its own destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"parkway-drive~dead-man-s-chest":{"haiku":"Physical collapse mirrors internal breakdown; the speaker accuses their victim of culpability as organs fail and panic consumes the dying.","opus":"A death rattle set to breakdowns, gleefully refusing sympathy for someone whose ruin they brought on themselves. The whole point is the withheld pity, consequence arriving without mercy because the fatal choice was theirs alone.","sonnet":"A person who made a catastrophic choice is confronted with the physical consequences — organ failure, suffocation, the body shutting down — and told without sympathy that this is the price of their own decisions, consequence arriving with brutal inevitability.","src":"haiku"},
"parkway-drive~die-leere":{"haiku":"Endless chaos enslaves humanity through emptiness that spreads as bodies decay, with no master except the void consuming dreams.","opus":"A German-language rerecording from the Australian metalcore titans, casting an apocalyptic void as a living plague that seizes minds and drags humanity to its knees. A pale horse of ruin rides through as emptiness spreads like atrophy, demons filling every skull with endless noise. It offers no salvation, only a grim welcome into a world of pain where every heart is doomed to break.","sonnet":"Die Leere — the emptiness — is an unstoppable force that possesses from within, riding alongside the pale horse of ruin; no master rules over it, no dream survives it, and the world of pain it inaugurates is welcomed with the question: can you feel it taking hold?","src":"haiku"},
"parkway-drive~idols-and-anchors":{"haiku":"Collapse of certainty leaves the vulnerable exposed; heroes fail and security vanishes, demanding confrontation with inner emptiness.","opus":"A brutal confrontation with the collapse of everything you cling to, heroes fallen and safety gone, demanding what you'll do with your last breath. It's about facing the abyss when the idols that anchored you come crashing down.","sonnet":"A confrontation with the collapse of all external sources of meaning: heroes have fallen, banners should be burned, and the listener is left face-to-face with the abyss with only one breath remaining — the song refuses comfort, offering instead the raw fact of emptiness as something to thrive within rather than escape.","src":"haiku"},
"parkway-drive~sleepwalker":{"haiku":"Mechanized subjugation chains the psyche; the narrator awakens to their own dehumanization within the system.","opus":"An anti-conformity fury about being ground into just another body fed through the machinery of cities, work and blind ambition, stripped of humanity and self. The rage sharpens to a flat refusal to take another step in progress's name.","sonnet":"A vision of society as a machine that processes and strips people of identity, leaving them sedated and directionless — the speaker awakens to rage at this condition and refuses one more step in the name of blind progress.","src":"haiku"},
"parkway-drive~wild-eyes":{"haiku":"A generation inherits ecological and social devastation, reclaiming agency through rebellion and refusal to become complicit in inherited sin.","opus":"A generational war cry from kids handed a dying planet and their fathers' debts, choosing defiance over despair. Staying coal instead of diamond is the stance, refusing polish, embracing the underdog identity as armor.","sonnet":"A generation that inherited the sins and failures of those before them claims their identity as underdogs and rebels, refusing to go cold or submissive, riding into a broken world with defiance and the determination to take back what is theirs.","src":"haiku"},
"parkwood~i-ll-be-fine":{"haiku":"Wrestling with self-condemnation, mental imprisonment, and the difficulty of healing through forgiveness. The speaker cycles through avoidance and the paralysis of overthinking.","sonnet":"A person trapped inside a racing, impatient mind recites reassurances they only half-believe. Apologies are made but not processed, forgiveness given but not forgotten, and the grinding loop of damaged self-perception keeps them bed-bound and absent from their own life, chanting fine while nothing is.","src":"haiku"},
"parliament~give-up-the-funk-tear-the-roof-off-the-sucker":{"haiku":"Collective liberation through rhythm; a call to shed restraint and embrace funk together.","opus":"Clinton's P-Funk collective demanding the funk as if it were oxygen and communal salvation, tearing the roof off as pure ecstatic release. A call-and-response ritual where wanting and needing the groove becomes its own liberation theology of the dance floor.","sonnet":"A collective demand for release through music: the crowd and the band converge on the single imperative to give up the funk, tear the roof off, and turn the room out — the rhythm itself the argument, the repetition its own proof that the groove is real and necessary.","src":"haiku"},
"parov-stelar~booty-swing":{"haiku":"Dance rhythms spread across global cultures—from Eastern harems to Japanese geisha to Gypsy caravans—uniting humanity in bodily joy and swing music.","opus":"An electro-swing flip of a 1938 novelty jazz tune, all cartoon Orientalist caricature played for period dancefloor kitsch. The Austrian producer keeps the vintage vocal intact because the appeal is nostalgia and momentum, not the words.","sonnet":"A playful, exoticizing fantasy imagines cultures around the world — Arab harems, East Asia, Romani caravans — all discovering and adopting American swing dance, casting global diversity as uniform enthusiasm for jazz-age hedonism.","src":"haiku"},
"parris-bridge~body-bag":{"haiku":"Death and calculated revenge intertwine; the speaker reckons with someone who destroyed their life, grief transformed into retribution and haunting finality.","opus":"Grief for someone lost to illness curdled into a vengeful fantasy against those who wronged the dying, cancer and betrayal blurred into a single enemy. The rage is really the trauma of a farewell forced far too early, looping with no release.","sonnet":"A furious address to someone responsible for a loved one's death — watching them die in a body bag, cursing the liars, and promising that the guilty will face the consequences of the blood they spilled.","src":"haiku"},
"passcode~flavor-of-blue":{"haiku":"Escape requires collective transformation; the speaker seeks connection beyond isolation while resisting deterministic traps.","opus":"A restless push to keep moving despite not knowing where or how, fighting through collapse and doubt toward some promised paradise. Melancholy blue is carried like fuel into the next battle.","sonnet":"Caught between collapse and forward motion, the speaker raises the banner of memento mori and declares war on stagnation — petals burning, two people separated but spiritually linked — demanding a break from the prison of a predetermined game to step toward a vast open world and the flavor of a permanent, sorrowful blue.","src":"haiku"},
"passcode~freely":{"haiku":"Breaking free from constraints and hypocrisy by embracing one's desires and chaos. The speaker chooses authentic transgression over false stability and societal rules.","opus":"A defiant address to a vast, indifferent Tokyo, urging someone to break the walls of illusion and habit before their youth runs out. The arc runs from wanting to freeze in place to a burning demand to smash the maze and simply be heard, even if the doing is reckless or shameful. Passcode are a Japanese idol-metal act who fold metalcore breakdowns and EDM drops under pop vocals, and the bilingual rush of stuttered English hooks against dense Japanese verses is exactly their signature register.","sonnet":"A surge of defiance against the walls that hold you in — the song urges breaking out of a frozen, news-saturated drift, smashing through illusion and conformity, and redirecting that restless guilt into something burning and alive. Tokyo is addressed as both witness and backdrop, and the final push is to sing until you're spent, chasing a vanishing rainbow.","src":"haiku"},
"passcode~taking-you-out":{"haiku":"Rescue from despair and isolation; despite broken worlds and lost hope, the speaker urges loved ones to receive love and be saved. Pain ends, light emerges, if one dares to see beyond the darkness.","opus":"Japanese idol-metalcore aimed at someone drowning in a doomscroll of bad news and self-comparison, insisting the world outside is still bright. Frantic energy wrapped around a genuinely tender rescue message.","sonnet":"A mixed Japanese/English song about rescuing someone from isolation and misery — reaching in to take the seed of loneliness away — with an urgent, crashing energy that frames the act of rescue as a kind of love that fights through noise and broken places.","src":"haiku"},
"passengers~faces-of-janus":{"haiku":"Duplicity and division erode trust and morality as divisive judgment breaks what could be discovered together; empty vaults of integrity spill.","opus":"A progressive-leaning track built on the two-faced Roman god as an emblem of duplicity in a relationship or alliance. Trust corrodes like rusting locks on a vault the narrator has sealed himself inside, and he warns that if it breaks he will spill out entirely. Divisive judgment intertwines with intimacy, and the sharpest cut is demanding to know where the other's morals died.","sonnet":"Duplicity and moral collapse eat away at a relationship from the inside: disorder flatters itself while structure shatters, and the speaker, whose vault of trust is rusting open, demands to know where the other person's morals went and why they think indemnification is possible.","src":"haiku"},
"passion-pit~moth-s-wings":{"haiku":"Restless love batters the narrator in chaotic violence while absence leaves him spinning with sensual memory and unmet longing.","opus":"A volatile bond with someone who batters against the singer like a trapped insect then leaves him glimmering and alone, replaying the inherited wound of a father lost beneath water. The plea to set down blade and crown and simply lie on the earth is a wish to quit the war in favor of tenderness.","sonnet":"A turbulent address to someone withering and lost — flowers dying, mother missing, leaves drifted — whose love arrives spastic and violent like moth's wings whipping the speaker into a storm before they run away and leave him spinning.","src":"haiku"},
"passion-pit~the-reeling":{"haiku":"Psychological entrapment and futile escape define existence; the narrator feels madness creeping closer despite their attempts to break free.","opus":"Angelakos channeling his own depression into shimmering pop, digging holes that become home and praying for rescue while madness reels him in. The euphoric sound over a plea for someone to understand is the song's whole aching irony.","sonnet":"A speaker paralyzed by depression and a sense of being trapped in their own life prays to be rescued by anyone or anything, while a creeping madness tightens around them and the possibility of being seen or understood recedes further with every passing day.","src":"haiku"},
"passion-pit~to-kingdom-come":{"haiku":"Faith and doubt intertwine as the narrator questions devotion and sacrifice; connection feels suffocating even as they cling to hope for transcendence.","opus":"A knotted, deeply personal grappling with a relationship and belief, the narrator burrowing into fragrant clutter and calling out to a God who taunted him in the dark. Under the odd domestic imagery sits a plea to feel something again after being told precisely how his life would forever go.","sonnet":"A fractured, feverish address to someone stifling and controlling, the speaker finds themselves hiding in strange sensory corners, calling out to God in the dark, and searching for the aliveness that their spark seems to have extinguished.","src":"haiku"},
"pathways~exposed":{"haiku":"Clarity arrives through confrontation with shadow selves; the speaker hardens through exposure and claims power.","opus":"Emerging from a nightlife of vice with clearer sight, the narrator flirts with danger yet learns to break the choke and finally hit the notes. A gambler's swagger built on newfound self-possession.","sonnet":"A person who spent time in a dark, demonised place — hands on their throat, unable to sing — has broken free and now speaks with venom-sharpened precision, ready to go all in and claim the top, moving from exposed vulnerability to a deliberate, honey-lipped power play.","src":"haiku"},
"patient-sixty-seven~antithesis":{"opus":"A devastating song about loving someone through addiction and self-destruction, the narrator torn between unconditional love and the impossibility of saving someone who won't help themselves, tormented by imagined violence and the guilt of hurting the person he needed most.","sonnet":"About being trapped in a relationship with someone who refuses to help themselves — the narrator loves them deeply but is being dragged under, accused of enabling their destruction, and ultimately forced to leave even though walking away feels impossible and cruel.","src":"sonnet"},
"patient-sixty-seven~mare-s-nest":{"haiku":"Confusion and tangled chaos, nightmare scenario of complicated problems.","opus":"A pop-punk purge of self-hatred and passed-down shame, in which even the room seems to murmur that the narrator deserves neither affection nor rescue. He confesses he was more content before he began performing a manufactured life to satisfy others, unable to loosen what grips him. He keeps wondering if he will ever escape the shame lodged within.","sonnet":"Walls that talk, guilt that can't be shed, happiness dismissed as illusion — the narrator fakes an existence to make someone proud while being unable to let go of what holds them bound. \"The mirror image of the life I led\" is the permanent gap between performed okayness and the hate ripping through the chest.","src":"haiku"},
"patriarchy~suffer":{"haiku":"Female suffering under systemic oppression defines lived experience; pain becomes the baseline condition of gendered existence.","opus":"A darkwave provocation that eroticizes power, submission and mutual destruction. The narrator invites a dominant figure to stand over and watch them suffer, confessing a fear of the very things they crave most. Feeding off a host and demanding suffering shared and suffering given, it stages desire as a parasitic exchange where pain and want become indistinguishable, defiant and unsettling in equal measure.","sonnet":"A power dynamic of mutual complicity in suffering — one party stands above and watches, the other fears what they most want, and the cycle of \"suffer with me / suffer for me\" is not quite submission and not quite domination but something that holds both participants in place. The brevity and repetition make the dynamic feel inescapable.","src":"haiku"},
"paul-dempsey~morningless":{"haiku":"Physical absence bleeds into emotional distance; the narrator exists in darkness, haunted by memory and longing, unable to reach someone who defines their universe.","opus":"A portrait of feeling marooned an ocean's distance from someone, slipping beneath his own grief while pleading with them to hold on. The coined title names a night that refuses to break into day, kept bearable only by another person serving as his one point of light.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped in a state between sleep and waking reaches across distance toward someone they love, feeling paralyzed by sorrow and the self-sabotage of their own instincts while urging both of them to stay afloat until morning can make things right.","src":"haiku"},
"paul-desmond~when-joanna-loved-me":{"haiku":"Love transforms perception entirely; its loss converts paradise into winter, rendering past joy irretrievable.","opus":"A standard of nostalgic heartbreak where a lost love once made every ordinary day feel like Paris in May. Memory briefly resurrects that happiness before December returns.","sonnet":"A man looks back at a time when Joanna's love made every city Paris, every sound music, every month May — and now that she is gone, even December can't erase the memory of her touch and smile, which briefly resurrects that paradise.","src":"haiku"},
"paul-kuhan~row-row-fight-the-power":{"haiku":"Resistance and determined struggle against authority, the speaker rallying to collective uprising.","opus":"A cover of the Gurren Lagann anime anthem, welding a Latin requiem to explosive hip-hop bravado. It preaches doing the impossible and breaking the unbreakable, a rallying cry for underground soldiers to rise, open a third eye and fight entrenched power. Revolution won't be televised; the sacred-choir framing lends cosmic weight to an unabashed call to defiance and self-liberation.","sonnet":"Underground resistance and collective will set against a Latin requiem — do the impossible, touch the untouchable — the rap verses insist that revolution won't be televised, that no pain means no gain, and that breaking the rule is the only way to see the truth, with the Dies Irae threading through as the cost of that conviction.","src":"haiku"},
"paul-oakenfold~ready-steady-go":{"haiku":"Energy and readiness pulse through stuttered commands; the speaker insists on readiness and lyrical style as the beat drives forward.","opus":"A stutter-launched dancefloor chant built almost entirely from its own countdown, ready-steady-go repeated as ignition rather than statement. The British trance producer treats the near-empty vocal as pure propulsion, lyrical styling and a truncated boast serving the beat instead of any story, the phrase itself the hook that fires the crowd forward.","src":"haiku"},
"paul-oakenfold~zoo-york":{"haiku":"Sacrifice binds human experience; divine pleasure requires mortal cost.","opus":"The lyric consists of translated Urdu lines about sacrifice being an emotion granted only to mankind, not angels, when fate is on one's side. A meditation on the human capacity for self-sacrifice.","sonnet":"The lyrics consist solely of an Urdu phrase with its English translation — angels don't get this pleasure, it belongs only to humans, and fate by your side makes it possible — plus the Urdu word for sacrifice; there is no developed narrative beyond this lyrical fragment.","src":"haiku"},
"pav~the-end-of-us":{"haiku":"Watched and controlled, the speaker aims for one final shot; the relationship ends as it began.","opus":"Fame and ambition as a rigged spectacle where you take a bow, smile and wave while others decide your fate. Desire wraps around the neck like a noose; the singer keeps moving under control, aware that letting anyone in became his downfall.","sonnet":"About a relationship or situation in which one person is being controlled by others' expectations and the other is being consumed by desire — the narrator is under control, making moves no one sees, and the dynamic of letting someone in has become a downfall; the SOS is real but the trigger finger is also theirs.","src":"haiku"},
"pay-money-to-my-pain~illumination":{"haiku":"Curiosity and maternal inheritance propel the speaker through darkness; questions multiply endlessly but the moon shines as proof that connection survives.","opus":"The late Japanese rock band frames curiosity and inner fire as survival against doubt and darkness. Overwhelmed by unanswerable questions and the sense of being looked down upon, the narrator keeps digging like a man with a shovel, trusting that past the tunnel someone who understands his struggle waits. The recurring moonlight image, beautiful but deadly, becomes a nightly reminder to keep his flame from running away.","sonnet":"Losing patience with unanswerable questions, the speaker watches anger appear in every sentence and fears that if words are taken away they will lose their sharpness and dissolve into darkness. The search for illumination is also a fight to stay articulate.","src":"haiku"},
"pay-money-to-my-pain~respect-for-the-dead-man":{"haiku":"Burial and closure become rituals of the dead; windows remain sealed as escape dissolves into permanent isolation and inescapable fate.","opus":"A haunting Japanese post-hardcore elegy addressed to someone sealed in a locked room whose window won't budge. The narrator casts himself as a messenger aching to force it open so a fallen figure might soar toward the sun on shattered wings and never come down again. Beneath the imagery is a plea to free a lost soul from illusion, fame and despair.","sonnet":"A person buried alive in a locked room — a \"dead man\" who is only a passenger — is addressed by a messenger who insists the window can be opened, the broken wings can aim for the sun, and the flight out is still possible if someone offers a hand.","src":"haiku"},
"peach-pit~alrighty-aphrodite":{"haiku":"Rejecting a shallow, manipulative lover, refusing to validate their performance or beauty.","opus":"A bitter goodbye to a lover cast as a vain goddess, told to go flaunt herself elsewhere. Beneath the jangle is wounded pride, seeing the dirt in someone he once put on a pedestal and letting her walk.","sonnet":"A speaker dismisses a goddess-like woman who trades on maybe and keeps him perpetually uncertain — he sees through the myth to the dirt beneath the surface and sends her off to dazzle someone else, refusing to keep wasting himself on her ambivalence.","src":"haiku"},
"peaks~don-t-wanna-be-like-you":{"haiku":"Rejection of a performative, attention-seeking persona; the speaker refuses imitation and confronts hollow ambition with indifference and contempt.","opus":"A punchy pop-punk kiss-off aimed at a self-important critic who talks endlessly while no one listens and believes they were born to matter. The narrator refuses to become that person, casting himself as the error in their system and a blister on their tongue. It channels adolescent contempt into a chant of refusal, demanding the poser figure out who they actually want to be.","sonnet":"Rejection of a performative, status-obsessed person who talks without being heard and points fingers while obsessing over appearances. The narrator claims the role of error in your system — a deliberate glitch in someone else's self-important machinery — and refuses to want what that person is or become what they represent.","src":"haiku"},
"peaks~fired-up":{"haiku":"The speaker burns with unstoppable fire and rage while others try to stamp them out, refusing to hide as the flames grow higher and threaten to consume everything.","opus":"A song about being called out by someone who has forever tried to crush him, only to find the strain feeds a blaze that climbs rather than dies. He confesses the barrier he built around himself is collapsing and he's suffocating, yet swears he'll never be reduced to ash. This post-hardcore cut rests entirely on the fire-and-barrier image, defiance and near-choking held in one breath.","sonnet":"Someone has been seen through — their hidden self exposed — and now burns under the scrutiny they can no longer escape. The fire metaphor swings both ways: it's a force of exposure and suffocation, yet also a refusal to be extinguished. The walls they built around themselves are caving in, and their only defiant note is that the person watching will never actually burn them down.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~alive":{"haiku":"A mother reveals to her son that his biological father died before he was born, leaving the speaker to question if they deserve to be alive.","opus":"Built on Vedder's own history, a young man is told by his mother that the father he knew wasn't his real dad, who has just died, and the revelation is entangled with an unsettlingly incestuous-seeming encounter with her. The haunted refrain 'I'm still alive' works as both survival and a curse, the narrator questioning whether he even deserves to be, a burden the song leaves unresolved.","sonnet":"A son learns his real father died before he could know him, then his mother initiates sex with him — two traumatic revelations collapsed into each other — leaving him with the survivor's curse of being alive and unable to make sense of what that means.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~alive-live":{"haiku":"A son discovers his real father's death came while he sat alone. Guilt and arousal intertwine as he questions whether he deserves to survive.","opus":"A young man learns the father he grew up with wasn't his real one and is drawn into something predatory by his mother, left guilt-stricken at simply being alive; Vedder drew it from his own family secret, and the crowd later reclaimed the survivor's chant as affirmation.","sonnet":"A song about a man who learns as a teenager that his real father died before they could meet, and that his mother then initiates a sexual relationship with him — this trauma leaves him questioning whether he deserves to be alive, unable to answer who decides that, surviving but hollowed out by what happened.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~better-man-live":{"haiku":"Romantic entrapment and impossible escape define a woman's captivity; she performs love while dreaming of freedom, unable to abandon familiar servitude.","opus":"Vedder's portrait of a woman staying with a man she no longer loves because she can't imagine finding better, drawn from his own mother's marriage. The quiet devastation is in her lying to herself and returning anyway.","sonnet":"A woman in a loveless, probably abusive relationship stays because she cannot find the strength to leave — lying to herself and to him, dreaming in colour of something better, but convinced she will never find anyone more worthwhile.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~black":{"haiku":"A man mourns a love that has ended, his world now empty and darkened. He recognizes the loved one will be happy with someone else but wishes it could be him.","opus":"Eddie Vedder's aching portrait of a love that shaped everything and then left, coloring his whole world black. Its devastation is the generosity of the ending, wishing her a bright life while grieving that it isn't his.","sonnet":"The aftermath of a profound love ending — the world that was painted in someone else's colors has gone monochrome, and the speaker can accept she'll have a beautiful life somewhere else while still unable to stop grieving that it won't be with him.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~brain-of-j":{"haiku":"The world will transform and feel relieved once someone is gone, freeing people from control and false order imposed upon them.","opus":"Opening Yield with a jolt, Pearl Jam use JFK's missing brain as a wry emblem of national amnesia and manufactured obedience. The promise that the whole world will soon be relieved reads as ominous rather than hopeful, aimed at people whipped into line, while the narrator resolves to shed the name he was given.","sonnet":"Power, control, and the promise of transformation orbit the unsolved mystery of JFK's assassination. Society conditions its citizens into compliance — standing in line, obeying order — while the narrator sheds the identity assigned to him. Relief and change remain perpetually imminent, arriving just beyond reach.","src":"haiku","fable":"Yield cracks open with a grotesque question: who keeps the preserved brain of a murdered president, and what leverage does owning that relic still grant over a public raised to revere him. The chorus arrives dressed as deliverance, a soothing announcement that things are about to improve, but it plays as the practiced calm of authority reassuring the governed that everything is under control. Verse two abandons the assassination conceit for its true target, the drilled citizen dressed into rank and promised order, so the comfort being sold is really the comfort of a surrendered will. The bridge overturns all of it in a few words, one person refusing the label assigned to them and walking away from it, the single act of defiance no institution can process. Set against that, the endlessly repeated reassurance decaying into a lone trailing word sounds less like prophecy than the hum of a system reciting its own promise long after a single voice has quietly stepped clear.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"one voice walking away from the label assigned to it, the act no system can process","built":"the reassuring chorus decays into a lone trailing word while the single act of refusal has already gone quiet"},
"pearl-jam~buckle-up":{"opus":"A bleak, cryptic vignette of caretaking a dying mother, with blood on the singer's hands amid bedsores and sponge baths; the grim refrain twists a doctor's oath into gallows humor about death being inescapable, so brace yourself.","sonnet":"A fragmented account of a hospital vigil — blood on hands, bed sores, a mother with a wound and her son on her lap, something about a murderer being groomed — the song holds the horror of medical helplessness and possibly the damage done across generations.","src":"sonnet"},
"pearl-jam~comes-then-goes":{"opus":"An elegy for a deteriorating friend the singer can barely locate anymore, mapping how years, hurt and self-abandonment pried them apart. He keeps extending himself despite being pushed away, wishing he could shield the friend from their own destructive nature as the bond flickers in and out of reach.","sonnet":"A grief-laced attempt to reconnect with someone — likely a friend lost to mental illness or addiction — where the narrator acknowledges their own altered perceptions, traces missed signs, and sits with the recurring sadness that love and connection come and go without warning.","src":"sonnet"},
"pearl-jam~dance-of-the-clairvoyants":{"haiku":"Rigid expectations and repetition trap people; confusion and friction are inevitable when grasping for meaning in cyclical, predetermined existence.","opus":"A song that leans into uncertainty, distrusting predictions and control while arguing that trying to force perfection or freeze the future only tightens the grip that ruins things, and that no individual outranks the collective. Pearl Jam took a bold Talking Heads-style art-funk detour here, a rare rhythmic experiment for the Seattle veterans.","sonnet":"A rattling, elliptical account of being stuck in time while love and prediction fail equally — the past colonizes the present, every tomorrow repeats, and the only people worth trusting are those who have already left ordinary perception behind. Men and women move in their fixed grooves while the spirit arrives and everyone is warned to step back.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~daughter":{"haiku":"Parental violence and control fracture a child's identity; she internalizes shame while the oppressor remains nameless.","opus":"A girl misread at home, most likely learning-disabled, blamed and shamed by a mother who won't truly see her, pinned under the very hand meant to hold her. Vedder's empathy for the mislabeled child is the heart of it, with a promise that she'll rise.","sonnet":"A girl struggling with an undiagnosed learning difference is misunderstood and pressured by her mother; her internal withdrawal is mistaken for defiance, and the song voices her refusal to accept a relationship defined by inadequacy.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~deep":{"haiku":"Multiple people choose depths of despair and obsession, sinking into waters they cannot escape while on the edge of nothing.","opus":"From Pearl Jam's debut Ten, this stacks three people poised at the brink, a man on a ledge shooting up, another feeling superior as his end nears, a young woman exploited and unwilling. None can find any floor beneath them. Vedder's early social realism renders addiction, despair, and violation as one dizzying plunge no fine vantage can redeem.","sonnet":"Three vignettes share the same structure — a person perched at an edge who means nothing to the world above or below, sinking a needle or knife into themselves. The final image shifts from self-harm to violation: a woman in a degrading encounter who does not want the view she has been given. Depth here is always a trap, not an achievement.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~dirty-frank":{"haiku":"A cannibalistic killer uses his bus job to abduct and consume victims, hiding bodies while the band watches in fear.","opus":"A gleeful horror farce casts a touring bus driver as a cannibal serial killer modeled on Jeffrey Dahmer, chopping up groupies and bandmates and burying leftovers on the freeway shoulder. Pearl Jam, on an early B-side, indulge a funk-driven black comedy that sends up their own road life, the band too afraid to join Frank's luncheon.","sonnet":"A darkly comic fiction about a cannibalistic tour bus driver whose secret the band is too frightened to confront. Frank serves as both monster and absurdist punchline — the horror of what he does is narrated with cheerful deadpan, each verse escalating until a bandmate is named as the latest victim.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~dissident-live":{"haiku":"A woman sacrifices her child to the state, surrendering her convictions for security.","opus":"A woman who shelters a political fugitive and then hands him to the state, and the quiet ruin of that betrayal. The song lives in her failure of nerve, the recognition that fleeing was never the safe choice and neither was folding.","sonnet":"A woman shelters a dissident for a night then turns him over to the state — she couldn't hold, she folded — and spends the rest of her life gliding on the surface of her guilt, the word 'dissident' echoing as both the man she betrayed and the self she never became.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~do-the-evolution":{"haiku":"Sarcastically charts humanity's destructive evolution from primal instinct to reckless domination, where progress equals exploitation. The song ironically praises our irresponsible rise to power.","opus":"A savage satire of human arrogance and greed, voiced by a self-glorifying narrator who boasts of dominance, exploitation, and destruction while calling it all evolution. It skewers hypocrisy, conquest, and reckless self-interest driving the species toward ruin.","sonnet":"A savage satirical monologue delivered in the voice of a self-congratulating human who mistakes conquest, greed, and destruction for progress, using the language of evolution to justify colonialism, environmental ruin, and moral bankruptcy.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is a first-person brag voiced entirely by the self-declared winner of natural selection, the primate who first pulled on trousers, gamed the markets, drafted the strategy. Each boast doubles as an indictment, filing lust, greed, conquest and genocide alike under the same shrugging refrain that this is merely progress. Pearl Jam builds the irony by stacking self-flattery against the atrocity it papers over, so the churchgoing thief and the man whose faith licenses killing become one swaggering narrator. He congratulates himself for standing higher on the ladder, yet beneath the mask the title's command turns accusatory. To do this dance is not ascent but a march toward the fire the final verse dates to 2010, the creature calling appetite advancement while it drives the species off a cliff.","fv":2,"fnote":"The narrator was borrowed from a book: the band read Daniel Quinn’s Ishmael during the Yield sessions, Vedder called the singer someone drunk with technology who thinks he’s the planet’s controlling being — and Quinn himself said this song comes closest of anything to expressing the book’s ideas.","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"natural-selection brag as escalating self-indictment","built":"the title's imperative doubles as accusation against the narrator obeying it","fvr":"2.3"},
"pearl-jam~elderly-woman-behind-the-counter-in-a-small-town-remastered":{"haiku":"A stranger recognizes a former connection in a fleeting moment, but memory and time have erased the identity they once shared.","opus":"A woman who stayed put her whole life spots an old flame across a shop counter and can't summon his name, the years suddenly bearing down on her. It's about the sting of a life frozen in one spot while everyone and everything around her moved on.","sonnet":"A woman who never left her small town recognises someone from her past but cannot place them, while feeling acutely the life she did not live and the way the town has locked her into stasis. She wants desperately to scream a hello across the years, but hearts and thoughts fade away.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~even-flow":{"haiku":"Portrait of homelessness and mental illness; a man without anchor drifts through days, seeking meaning and connection. His thoughts scattered like butterflies, he remains trapped in survival.","opus":"A sympathetic study of a man living on the streets, bedding down against cold pavement, appealing to a higher power that has offered him nothing, and passing his days as his mind wanders uncontrollably. Beneath the grim picture flickers a slim wish that he might one day start over.","sonnet":"The song portrays the life of a homeless man sleeping on concrete, unable to read, praying to something that gives nothing back, and navigating the margins of society with a dark, involuntary grin. His thoughts scatter like butterflies as he hopes, someday, to begin his life again.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~faithfull": {"haiku":"People cling to religious belief and myth as comforting illusions that control and confine them, despite the futility of prayer. The song questions faith while acknowledging its universal hold.","opus":"A wry meditation on belief, images of untouched shrines giving way to the idea that God has grown numb to human clamor. Pearl Jam and Eddie Vedder decode faith itself as a construct that traps people in dread, everything and everyone interlinked, the repeated vow of devotion shifting away from religion toward a plainer loyalty between people.","sonnet":"A meditation on collective belief that has curdled into myth — prayers echo into silence, yet the faithful keep declaring. The closing section peels back the word itself, suggesting genuine devotion might mean trusting the inner voice drowning under institutional noise.","src":"haiku","fable":"A meditation on belief as something worn smooth by use — a bridge everyone has already crossed, a plaque certifying a bed no one ever really slept in. Vedder starts exhausted with the racket rising toward whoever's listening from above, done raising his voice, and then the refrain turns the whole thing into the very echo it describes: faith not as conviction but as a phrase we repeat because everyone repeats it, going and going and heard by no one. Yield's warmth is here, but so is its suspicion, and the outro tips the hand — spelling out that myth is the rigged loop of rules and levers that pens us inside a cell of fear, the private voice drowned out by the collective one. What saves it from cynicism is where it lands: after dismantling communal faith, the singer relocates it in a single person, offering fidelity not to the crowd or the sky but to one you. The mantra was a trap; the last line is the way out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"communal faith dismantled and relocated in a single you","built":"the refrain enacts the echo it describes by repeating itself"},
"pearl-jam~garden":{"haiku":"The narrator walks through suffering into a stone garden, refusing attachment and care while questioning modern existence and needs.","opus":"From Pearl Jam's debut Ten, a solemn march in which the narrator advances wounded and shackled toward a burial ground, refusing to be captured or to lean on anyone. Vedder frames a walk toward death or sacrifice as an act of fierce self-sufficiency, questioning society's demands rather than life itself.","sonnet":"A figure walks willingly into a place of stone and submission — hands bound, face bloodied, carrying a shadow flag — yet refuses the other's emotional jurisdiction. The paradox is deliberate: physical capitulation paired with total internal refusal, a self that will not be fed, shared, or claimed even while the body complies.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~get-it-back":{"haiku":"Loss and separation; a prodigal absence. The speaker would give anything to reconnect with someone they've lost before time runs out.","opus":"A song of loss and the yearning to recover someone gone, stranded and alone by the roadside, racing to reach a person before the cold sets in, offering anything for one more conversation. A returning wanderer, a grieving parent and child, all straining toward the same reunion. Pearl Jam deliver it plainly, the recurring ache of a life knocked off its footing since the person vanished.","sonnet":"A song of grief and longing directed at someone lost — stranded on the side of the road, frozen in time, the speaker reaches across the gap left by a departure that changed everything. The prodigal framing and the image of a mother and child suggest estrangement rather than death, but the distance feels total and the return uncertain.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~given-2-fly":{"haiku":"Escape from isolated waiting culminates in oceanic freedom; wind carries the fleeing figure beyond, dissolving helplessness into transcendent dissolution.","opus":"A parable of transcendence and its cost, tracing a man who runs to the ocean, is granted wings by a crashing wave, and returns to give away the key to everyone's chains. For his generosity he is stripped and knifed by faceless men yet keeps giving love away. Musically echoing Led Zeppelin, it becomes Vedder's meditation on Christlike sacrifice and the freedom of unconditional grace.","sonnet":"A figure who reached the ocean, was lifted on wings by a wave, and flew whole — then descended to share the key to freedom — is stripped and stabbed by faceless men on his return, yet still stands and still gives love away, occasionally glimpsed as a strange spot in the sky.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~given-to-fly": {"haiku":"A visionary escapes confinement through transcendence, only to return and attempt to free others, who wound him before he can truly share his liberation.","opus":"A parable of a broken man who runs to the sea, is granted wings and comes back to hand everyone the key to their chains, only to be robbed and knifed by anonymous men, yet keeps giving his love away. Vedder makes a Christ-shaped figure of anyone whose generosity outlasts cruelty.","sonnet":"A Christ-like parable about a man who escapes isolation by reaching the ocean, receives the gift of flight, and returns to share his key to freedom — only to be stripped and stabbed by faceless men, yet still standing, still giving his love away freely.","src":"haiku","fable":"A fable of the outsider as martyr, told in the third person so the man stays a type rather than a name. It builds in stages: a withdrawal from a world he could have joined, a solitary flight to the sea where wind and wave hand him wings, then a chosen fall back to earth because ascension is worthless if kept. The turn is the cruelty of his return — he comes offering the key that unlocks everyone's chains and is set upon by knives in anonymous hands, his clothes torn off before the blades go in, left standing by the very people he'd free. What survives the beating is the ethic underneath the Christ-shaped parable: the love he hands over freely is the only love that stays with him, and the prophet becomes a rumor, a faint mark against the clouds rather than a savior anyone lets down among them. Vedder pitches it up into that wordless high cry precisely where the story runs out of argument, letting the soaring vowel carry what the plain man's dignity can't say for itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness","violence & murder","freedom & escape"],"means":"love given freely the only thing that stays after the beating","built":"wordless high cry carries what the plain man's argument cannot"},
"pearl-jam~habit":{"haiku":"Destructive patterns recur despite awareness and desire for change, trapping the speaker in cycles of harm and dependency.","opus":"Vedder watching friends slide into addiction out of curiosity about the hype, torn between righteous distance and helpless concern. The refrain rejecting this path as beneath his friend is a plea aimed at someone slipping past reach.","sonnet":"A speaker watches friends fall into addiction and, with a mix of self-righteousness and genuine alarm, insists he never thought they would succumb and never thought he would either. The repetition of \"never thought you'd habit\" becomes increasingly uneasy, quietly acknowledging that no one is immune.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~in-hiding": {"haiku":"A person retreats into isolation to escape overwhelming sensory and emotional stimuli, eventually finding peace and clarity through stillness and withdrawal.","opus":"A chosen retreat that turns into an odd cleansing, bolting the door, drawing the drapes, pulling every plug, holding back words and breath and sinking down until clarity finally rises after a stretch of days aground. He surfaces clear-eyed, everything pared down. Pearl Jam, on Yield, send this fasting-like seclusion soaring, the singer stepping out of self-imposed exile unburdened by the world he shut away.","sonnet":"Deliberate withdrawal from the world as a survival mechanism — doors locked, curtains pulled, plugs emptied, the outside sealed off until three days of solitude become a kind of clarity. What looked like breakdown from the inside turns out to be a dive that surfaces into enlightenment, the self simplified by having nothing left to perform.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man seals himself into a room and pulls the plugs on the outside world. The song tracks what that voluntary shutdown does to him across a stretch of days that ends with him run aground — first the delirium of walls that crack and invert, then a strange clarifying calm. The pre-chorus is the engine: a chain of swallowings, where taking words and breath back down inside himself becomes a kind of controlled dive, and the enlightenment he reaches is what he surfaces holding. Vedder built this from a Bukowski passage about locking the door and going dark to reset, and everything hinges on that irony — hiding here is not defeat but a deliberate submersion, so that the flat refrain lands less like a confession of retreat than a verdict earned: overwhelm burned off, and the whole crisis reframed at the end as nothing but the way things quietly turned.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","transcendence & consciousness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"voluntary shutdown framed as submersion not retreat","built":"chain of swallowings in the pre-chorus enacts the controlled dive"},
"pearl-jam~jeremy":{"haiku":"A neglected boy bullied at school becomes violent, biting a teacher; his home ignored his pain until he exploded into class.","opus":"A neglected boy ignored by his father and unwanted by his mother acts out in class, the taunting narrator recalling how they unleashed something before the violent, public end. Pearl Jam built one of grunge's defining singles on a real newspaper story of teenage suicide, Eddie Vedder's delivery turning schoolyard cruelty and parental absence into indelible tragedy.","sonnet":"A boy neglected into invisibility by both parents finally makes himself impossible to ignore in the most devastating way. Classmates who once dismissed him as harmless carry the memory of that classroom moment forever — the violence of his last act is inseparable from the slow violence of being unseen for years.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~low-lite":{"haiku":"Disorientation and tracking loss in obscurity, unable to navigate or be found in the dimness with only sensory impressions remaining.","opus":"A hushed, impressionistic reverie from the band's self-titled era, drifting through dim light, blowing wind and lost tracks. The narrator gropes toward home and clarity, unable to see his own trail yet sensing a lingering scent of someone ahead. Restless imagery of clouds, birds and car-crash flashes circles a quiet plea for guidance, for the light needed to sort real from dreamed.","sonnet":"Disorientation and longing rendered in fragments — low light, lost scent, jealousy and books pointing the wrong way, a voice blowing past like a car crash. Two birds seen from below, the narrator looking for the path home through failing light and trying to sort dream from reality.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~mfc": {"haiku":"A song about a woman who has faded from view or memory, with questions about presence and absence, roads and futures that slip away or disappear from reach.","opus":"A brief portrait of a woman driving off to begin again, the title said to stand for Mini Fast Car, as she shifts gears and vanishes toward the horizon after a life she was cheated out of and squandered. The narrator can only offer to listen, admitting there's no such fresh start available to him even as she escapes into open-ended freedom.","sonnet":"A snapshot of a woman leaving a damaging past behind — she was once discarded and broken, but now drives off into her own life, and the speaker offers themselves as a listener without judgment while reflecting on how timing shapes where people end up.","src":"haiku","fable":"A getaway song built entirely on the geometry of leaving: the car peeling out toward a horizon, someone shedding the version of themselves who once waited humiliated by the roadside. Vedder splits it across two figures — a woman who rides off and simply vanishes, then a man drilled by others to chase every clock and every zip code who instead makes his peace with staying put in the middle of nowhere — and the trap snaps shut in the refrain, which insists there is no leaving here even as everyone keeps disappearing. That contradiction is the point: escape and stasis are the same motion, and the speaker only ever offers to be a listening ear for people already gone. The final line drops the third person for a curt, profane we, folding the singer into the vanishing act and turning the tidy song title's initials into a shrug at the whole doomed exit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Vedder has said the initials stand for Mini Fast Car — the title the song itself never bothers to unpack.","themes":["freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"escape and stasis exposed as the same motion","built":"song-title initials turned into a shrug at the doomed exit"},
"pearl-jam~never-destination":{"opus":"A jittery picture of a churning, disillusioned mind, mile after mile of denial leading nowhere in particular; the singer resists being carried off, hunts for fresh companions and some measure of justice amid the fog, and gestures toward a rock singer who supposedly turned herself into a projection.","sonnet":"A sprawling dissociation from meaning and destination — the narrator is overwhelmed, a recluse chasing leviathans, playing tricks on their own mind to avoid saying goodbye while acknowledging they are deep in denial with no end in sight.","src":"sonnet"},
"pearl-jam~no-way": {"haiku":"Someone needs connection and support but surrenders hope of making a difference; they reach out for an angel and accept that they'll stop trying to change anything.","opus":"A blunt confession of craving someone's presence and of nerves frayed to breaking, ending in a burnt-out promise to give up trying to fix anything. On their Yield era, Pearl Jam let Eddie Vedder voice a surrender that lands as both collapse and candor, an appeal for rescue against the exhaustion of caring.","sonnet":"Rawness and vulnerability laid bare by someone who needs to be witnessed, whose internal static has become unbearable. The repeated surrender of trying to make a difference reads less like defeat than an honest reckoning with the limits of individual will — what remains is the simple, aching need for someone to simply be there.","src":"haiku","fable":"Stone Gossard's lyric, handed to Vedder's voice, moves from a jittery self-inventory toward a stubborn refusal, and the two poles are the whole song. The verses are static in the literal sense — attic noise firing down a nerve, a mind gone too clear, platitudes spread across meaningless coordinates — the sound of a man over-examining himself into paralysis, and out of that clutter the refrain strips everything down to a single bare wish that one other person simply show up and stay. The chorus then answers that wish not with connection but with surrender: he lays down the effort to change anything, flatly refuses to keep at it, a stance that reads at once as exhaustion and as a strange peace, the anthem-shaped hook Pearl Jam grew into by Yield after the storm of their early fame. The bridge's summons for some winged rescuer doesn't save him so much as admit the help has to come from outside, since the striving itself has already been set down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape","madness & the mind"],"means":"over-examination collapsing into the peace of stopped striving","built":"anthem-shaped hook carries a lyric of pure surrender"},
"pearl-jam~oceans":{"haiku":"Distance separates two people but their bond holds; separation is only physical while love and memory remain.","opus":"Pearl Jam soften their grunge into something patient and tidal, a vow of devotion across separation. As currents and distance pull two people apart, the narrator promises to hold on and be waiting when they reunite, the rising sea and clutched ring standing for love that endures the gap between them.","sonnet":"Separated by vast distances, two people hold on through the current — the sea between them becomes a medium of thought rather than an obstacle. The quiet faith that waves carry something true across the water sustains the promise: stay at the shore, and the return will happen.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~once":{"haiku":"A man struggles with self-control and destructive impulses, caught between wanting to control himself and losing himself entirely, haunted by images of things he can't let go of.","opus":"A first-person descent into a mind losing its grip, narrated by someone armed and unraveling, haunted by a time when he could still control and even love himself. The backstreet lover and the buried shotgun trace a slide toward violence dressed as confession. This is the opening track of Pearl Jam's 1991 debut Ten and part of Eddie Vedder's loose Mamasan trilogy, its taut Seattle grunge holding a coiled, dangerous calm.","sonnet":"A confession from someone who has lost control of themselves — carrying violence, concealed intent, and a fractured sense of who they once were. The refrain mourns a vanished capacity for self-possession and even love, while admitting they've been watching the situation clearly the whole time, not as lost as they appeared.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~porch":{"haiku":"A bereaved person faces a world disappearing into chaos and abandonment, pleading for connection with someone gone.","opus":"A panicked reckoning with a life spinning out and a love vanishing without a trace, the narrator begging for one last moment of closeness before it's gone for good. On their debut Ten, Pearl Jam turn Eddie Vedder's urgency into a churning tumble of debts, dangers and burdens, terrified of a point past which there's no coming back.","sonnet":"On the edge of losing everything — voice, time, presence — a person reaches outward with desperate urgency. The missed message, the eroding middle, the weight of what has not been said all converge into a single raw request: be seen, be held, be heard before the day that might already be the last one runs out.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~push-me-pull-me": {"haiku":"Nothing is permanent—all things visit and break apart like waves; without falling and discarding thought, one simply disappears into puddles like an opening band for the sun.","opus":"A strange, philosophical late-era Pearl Jam piece from their 2000 self-titled album, half-spoken over an elastic groove. The narrator accepts he's only passing through, dispersing like rain and surf, half-joking about picking a grave where the shore meets the water while weighing virtue against sin, comic and mortal at once.","sonnet":"An existential reckoning with impermanence and mortality, framed as surrender rather than resistance. The speaker questions the origins of love and sin, accepts that life is a brief visit — waves breaking, clouds releasing rain — and asks only to be released gently, somewhere the land meets high tide, letting thought dissolve.","src":"haiku","fable":"A murmured spoken-word aside on Yield, Vedder half-talking his way through a meditation on impermanence — the correction of a man who assumed his arrival was permanent, when we are all just passing through, cresting and falling back like the tide the song keeps returning to. The title's refrain is the whole argument in miniature: caught between forces that shove and drag, the speaker asks not to be steered but simply pulled out, and the flat repetition enacts the passivity it describes, a will worn down to a request. The verses drift toward a self-erasure that stays oddly gentle — bargaining for a nice plot of ground down where the shoreline floods at the waterline, then dissolving like rain into puddles, content to be nothing more than the opening act for something greater than himself. Underneath the resignation runs the album's larger question of surrender versus control, and the mask resolves into this yielding being offered as relief rather than defeat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"passivity offered as relief rather than defeat","built":"spoken-word flatness enacts the will worn down to a single request"},
"pearl-jam~red-bar":{"haiku":"Universal madness frames conflict; everyone engaged in warfare operates within shared delusion.","opus":"Barely a lyric at all, just one blunt refrain repeated until it turns into a chant: everyone is unhinged, and everyone is locked in some perpetual conflict. It reads less as a song than a mood-tag, madness and war collapsed into the same breath. Pearl Jam, the Seattle band who outlasted grunge into decades of restless catalogue, leave this one as a raw fragment rather than a finished statement.","sonnet":"Everyone is losing their minds, and everyone is at war — these two facts are presented as inseparable, repeated until the equation feels inescapable. No characters, no story, just a collective diagnosis delivered like a mantra: the world has gone mad and it is fighting itself, and there is no one standing outside that condition to explain it.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~release":{"haiku":"A child seeking connection with an absent father while discovering self-identity. The narrator asks to be released from pain and acknowledged for who they've become.","opus":"A raw address to a dead father, the son declaring he is himself, like him somehow, waiting in the dark for a voice, asking to be seen and to be released from his pain. The chill of the world sharpens the longing. Pearl Jam close their debut with this hymn-like catharsis, Eddie Vedder's grief for his own late father turning surrender into something close to prayer, riding the wave wherever it takes him.","sonnet":"A son addressing an absent father, asking to be seen across the distance — finding himself in the same flawed shape, willing to ride whatever wave comes, holding the inherited pain while asking to be let go of it. The appeal is patient and raw: wait in the dark as long as it takes, just open up and release.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~save-you":{"haiku":"A devoted friend demands reciprocal rescue and refuses to abandon someone spiraling toward self-destruction.","opus":"A raw, profane refusal to let a friend drown in addiction or self-destruction, the tenderness deliberately buried under aggression. The point is that love here sounds like grabbing someone by the collar, not comforting them.","sonnet":"A speaker refuses to let a self-destructing best friend go, bluntly demanding he stop hurting himself and insisting on a reciprocal rescue that mirrors what the friend once did for him. The love here is aggressive, selfish in its insistence, and entirely unconditional.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~superblood-wolfmoon":{"haiku":"Grief transformed into everywhere: loss erases the boundary between presence and absence, turning one person into a pervasive memory while the survivor grapples with anger, guilt, and the acceleration of life itself.","opus":"A driving late-period single reeling from a sudden death, someone snatched away far too early and surviving now only in memory and shut-eye glimpses. Vedder churns through guilt, fury, and doubt, mourning how quickly a cherished life slips past. A short-lived spark of infatuation, a knockout he'd gladly be captive to, sharpens the grief, hope sustained only by trusting it will be the last thing to go.","sonnet":"Grief for a lost woman — felt as absence everywhere at once — runs alongside a relationship that consumed the speaker completely before the world tore it apart. A rare lunar event names the moment of loss, and the speaker oscillates between numbness and desperate clinging to what hope remains before this life moves too fast to hold.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~wash":{"haiku":"A person seeks spiritual cleansing from inner corruption and moral hypocrisy, aware that what others see as pure hides something darker within.","opus":"A plea for cleansing aimed at both a grimy city and a guilty conscience, the narrator begging for rain to rinse away sins he pursued and a shameful truth he can't scour from within. This early Pearl Jam B-side, from the Ten era, drives its remorse and hunger for something untainted with restless, gritty force.","sonnet":"A prayer for cleansing that knows it won't be enough — the city and the mind equally filthy, a secret carried that could destroy both people it touches, the want for purity running up against the knowledge of what has already been done. Love and guilt are indistinguishable, and the washing never quite reaches the stain at the center.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~who-ever-said":{"haiku":"Resistance to narrative closure: the speaker rejects the idea that all truths have been spoken and satisfaction surrendered; they advocate for continued effort and pushback against quiet defeat and communal decline.","opus":"From Pearl Jam's Gigaton, this pushes back at the notion that everything has already been said, tying that resignation to giving up on satisfaction. Vedder wrestles with intrusive chatter, sleepless nights, fading sight and a pounding heart, mixing weary aphorisms with a refusal to give up. Home is located in broken hearts and scars, restlessness answered by dogged persistence.","sonnet":"A speaker fights noise — internal voices, ambient cynicism, the received wisdom that everything worth saying has already been said — while refusing to surrender the search for meaning. Home is where damage lives, yet abandonment of satisfaction is reframed as the real failure, and the final pledge is to keep reaching regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~why-go":{"haiku":"A woman institutionalized for depression finds her doctors and mother want her weak, not healed.","opus":"A furious indictment of a child locked away in an institution by her own mother and a system that decided she was broken, though she's stronger than they'll allow. Pearl Jam, on Ten, turn her plight into an anguished protest, the recurring question of returning home doubling as an accusation against those who put her there.","sonnet":"A girl institutionalized by a diagnosis she did not consent to resists the system's demand that she become smaller, quieter, more manageable. The walls she scratches her name into become testimony; the question of why she should return home to the family that put her there has no comfortable answer.","src":"haiku"},
"pearl-jam~wishlist": {"haiku":"Desire proliferates across impossible fantasies—transforming into objects, inanimate witnesses to mythic power. The speaker catalogs longing without expecting satisfaction.","opus":"A cascade of humble wishes to become useful and reliable things for a loved one, Vedder's fantasy self-effacement circling back to wanting simply to be someone worthy of trust.","sonnet":"A litany of things the speaker wishes they could be — a neutron bomb, a sailor's waiting love, the full moon on a Camaro's hood, a radio song turned up loud — each image a different shape of longing to matter to one specific person.","src":"haiku","fable":"A single anaphoric clause repeated until it becomes a shape, each line wishing the singer into some other object: a warhead that finally detonates, an ornament, the evidence, the brake pedal a lover leans on. The images split into two families that quietly argue with each other — grand impersonal things that matter to everyone (the doomsday warhead, the star atop the tree, the grounds for fifty million raised hands) and small domestic things that matter to exactly one person (the keepsake threaded onto a keyring, the moon on a lover's car). What the song wants is not glory but usefulness to someone specific, and the tell is that most of the wishes are for inanimate things — the speaker would rather be a dependable object than an unreliable self, closing on the wish to be the track on the airwaves a person chooses to turn up. Vedder, whose band had just recoiled from its own vastness on Yield, sings devotion as a wish for smallness, and the litany never resolves into I am, only I wish I was — the confession being that the transformation stays out of reach.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"devotion phrased as wish for smallness over self","built":"every wish for an inanimate object never resolves into I am"},
"pearl-jam~yellow-ledbetter":{"haiku":"Fragmented desire and uncertainty accumulate as the speaker struggles to place themselves within the wreckage of longing, unable to reconcile memory with staying.","opus":"Famously half-improvised and mostly unintelligible, which is the point, a mood of loss and exclusion where feeling outruns sense. Beneath the mumble sits a soldier's letter and a boy who doesn't belong on the porch looking in.","sonnet":"A fragmented, half-dissolved lament from someone watching others from a distance — figures on a porch who don't wave, a world he can't quite belong to — and admitting he doesn't know whether he's the one absorbing punishment or delivering it. The overriding feeling is an inability and unwillingness to stay.","src":"haiku"},
"pearly-drops~bloom-for-me":{"haiku":"Constraint and suppression; a person tightens around another who must wait for their controlled unfolding.","sonnet":"Someone watches another person suppress every feeling behind a facade of ease, coiling tighter the harder they resist. With patient tenderness the speaker urges them to stop fighting their own softness — to unfurl like a bud in spring, and trust that the opening will come in its own unhurried time.","src":"haiku"},
"pearly-drops~breathless":{"haiku":"Paralysis and suffocation from an invisible, overwhelming force that freezes the blood and traps the spirit. The self lies dormant, lost in dark inner chambers, unable to breathe or become who one is.","opus":"Finnish duo Pearly Drops set panic to cool, shimmering electronic pop. The narrator is frozen in place, unable to run, crushed by an unseen grip while everything vital in her retreats deep inside, dissociating until she isn't herself. Beauty and suffocation coexist in the same glassy sound.","sonnet":"Anxiety or grief experienced as a physical seizure — blood frozen, an invisible grip squeezing, the better parts of the self retreating deep into hibernation while the body moves through hollow chambers in the dark. The self observes its own vacancy from a distance, unable to breathe, unable to recognize the person left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"pearly-drops~call-for-help":{"haiku":"Someone leaving a letter before disappearing, expressing exhaustion and depletion, making one final desperate plea for rescue.","opus":"A farewell letter set to music, a drained and worn-out voice pleading to be released before it vanishes entirely, framed as a repeated cry for help sent to a lover who may not answer. The circular, incantatory repetition mirrors exhaustion itself, each verse asking only to be heard and let go. Pearly Drops are a Finnish electro-pop duo, and the fragile, looping minimalism here suits their spare, atmospheric approach to heartbreak.","sonnet":"A farewell letter addressed to someone loved, written on the edge of disappearance. The speaker is exhausted beyond repair and asks to be let go, but simultaneously reaches out — the plea for release and the cry for help exist in the same breath, unresolved and raw.","src":"haiku"},
"pearly-drops~feed-the-fire":{"haiku":"Self-immolation through emotional exposure, burning alive from within while isolated in darkness.","opus":"A brooding synth-pop confession of self-destruction in love, the narrator following a heart toward a room where love isn't and choosing the dark instead. Cast as burning trees and fields, they only feed the fire consuming them, embracing an ugliness a lover made in them. The plea to be touched through a fence renders intimacy caged and inflammatory, desire indistinguishable from ruin.","sonnet":"A narrator made ugly by someone else's gaze finds a strange comfort in the dark — \"you have made me ugly\" becomes a reason to stay in the shadows rather than escape them. The trees and fields burning is self-image going up; \"only feeding the fire\" is both surrender and the only honest thing left to do.","src":"haiku"},
"pearly-drops~i-cry-while-you-sleep":{"haiku":"Hidden anguish persists beneath surface calm; the speaker grieves alone, invisible to the beloved.","opus":"Someone weeping unseen beside a sleeping partner, feeling unloved and reborn as a version of themselves they had rejected. Private grief kept safely hidden while the clock runs out.","sonnet":"The speaker mourns quietly while the person they love sleeps, unable to shine on their own, time running out, feeling born again into a self they denied — and the core grief is the asymmetry of love: wishing to be loved the way the other loves someone else.","src":"haiku"},
"pearly-drops~it-follows":{"haiku":"Inescapable terror pursues relentlessly; the speaker flees toward light while haunted by persistent shadow.","opus":"Dread rendered as a horror-film pursuer that cannot be outrun, insisting the terror is real and not merely imagined. The night both threatens and fleetingly resembles the time of one's life.","sonnet":"Something pursues the speaker through the night — a horror-scene presence they cannot hide from — and the mask of sanity is slipping; the question of whether it is in their head is answered with \"it's not all in your head,\" and the final whisper \"I'm big trouble\" suggests the pursued and the pursuer may be the same.","src":"haiku"},
"pearly-drops~kiss-away-the-pearly-drops":{"haiku":"Unintelligible love drains reciprocity; the subject dwells in silence and release.","opus":"An unrequited attachment where devotion and giving fall on deaf ears, leaving the narrator emotionally hollowed out. The pearly drops are tears someone longs to have kissed away as they finally let go.","sonnet":"A speaker whose mind is dry and who is giving everything while receiving nothing — someone living rent-free in their head, calling their name to an empty window — reaches the decision to let it go, to let it show, and to kiss away their own tears.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~9-000-miles":{"haiku":"Distance separates them; only in memory does home exist in another's arms.","opus":"A tiny fragment of longing, distance measured against the certainty that home is a person, not a place. Two lines carrying the whole ache of being far from the one who makes anywhere feel like home.","sonnet":"Pure longing across distance — the narrator is nine thousand miles from someone and still feels home is only in that person's arms, nothing more.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~another-planet":{"haiku":"People on a strange world have open eyes but remain blind, unable to see truth despite awareness of space and time surrounding them.","opus":"A short drum-and-bass interlude built as a sci-fi parable: the inhabitants of a fictional world gaze outward at the cosmos while remaining unable to truly perceive. Pendulum use the vignette to gesture at willful ignorance and the disquiet of sensing an approaching upheaval you can't quite make out.","sonnet":"A very short spoken-word interlude depicting a people staring into the void of space with their eyes open but unseeing, until something suddenly changes — it functions more as atmospheric narrative intro than as a developed lyrical theme.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~anti-hero-triple-j-like-a-version":{"haiku":"Narcissism disguised as self-awareness; the exhausting spectacle of rooting for someone fundamentally broken.","opus":"The drum-and-bass crew covering Taylor Swift's ruthless self-audit for Australian radio. It inventories a mind that never grows wiser, depression working the graveyard shift, and covert narcissism dressed up as altruism. The narrator names themselves the problem, able to look straight at the sun yet never into a mirror, exhausted by the labor of rooting for a protagonist they know is a villain.","sonnet":"A cover of Anti-Hero — self-awareness weaponized as self-indictment, midnights as afternoons, the ghosts of everyone ghosted standing in the room — the narrator identifies himself as the problem at teatime while screaming from dreaming that he is a monster on the hill too big to hang out, his covert narcissism dressed as altruism just like a congressman.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~blood-sugar":{"haiku":"Sweet blood flows through veins as addiction pulses through existence. Rush and dependency merge into singular intoxication.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek spoken intro turned rave weapon, teasing the crowd with a mock-solemn promise to sonically recreate the apocalypse before puncturing its own pomp. The joke, admitting it's only drum and bass after all, is the point, a wink at electronic music's doom-posturing that primes bodies to move rather than mourn.","sonnet":"A mock-solemn concert announcement: \"we regret that this is not the sonic recreation of the end of the world\" — it's drum and bass — and the entire lyric is the MC's tongue-in-cheek buildup, the joke being that the music itself is the only content.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~cannibal":{"haiku":"Primal appetite and pharmaceutical oblivion merge into feral desire; restraint dissolves under medication and lust.","opus":"A hedonistic plunge into medicated, primal appetite—kissing like a cannibal, feeling unholy and out of control. Pills and poison fuel a self-aware craving where the narrator loves it precisely because others must hate it, missing his old problems as the new ones worsen.","sonnet":"A song about a feral, destabilizing sexual attraction — the speaker is loaded, unholy, and medicated, craving someone with the intensity of a predator, framing desire as something serrated and x-rated that their mind cannot fully contain even as it pulls them forward compulsively.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~come-alive":{"haiku":"The speaker becomes active and dangerous when the subject is alone and vulnerable; they watch intently and make their presence known.","opus":"Voiced by a watchful, possessive presence that stirs whenever its subject is by themselves, promising safety while keeping obsessive watch. The ambiguity between protector and predator is the whole point, either comfort or threat, an unseen figure hovering over someone's solitude and even goading them toward self-harm.","sonnet":"A stalker or obsessive watcher's perspective — the narrator comes alive specifically when the other person is alone, follows their shadow with restless eyes, insists they have nothing to fear while making clear he's always watching. Whether possessive or protective is deliberately ambiguous.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~comprachicos":{"haiku":"Someone breaks free from psychological manipulation and control, revealing their true strength by turning the aggressor's power back on them.","opus":"Named for those who deformed children into freaks, the song is a defiant refusal to be shaped by a manipulator. The narrator lets himself seem contained while quietly resolving to outlast and watch his controller drown, patience weaponized against coercion.","sonnet":"Someone who has spent years passively shaped by another person's manipulation finally turns the tables — not through outrage but through cold patience, content to watch the manipulator drown under the weight of their own tactics.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~crush":{"haiku":"Confrontation with buried guilt and emerging trauma; buried visions return as abusers while nightmares become real, reshaping the future as creatures of one's own creation turn hostile.","opus":"The narrator flees in the cold, guilt-ridden and losing control, as buried visions and nightmares return to haunt and reshape his future. Creatures of his own creation come back as abusers, hinting at harm he caused someone.","sonnet":"A narrator is haunted by buried visions and fears that have resurfaced as accusers, describing guilt, a shedding of old identity, and the sense that suppressed things have come back to damage his future. A final line implicates a victim, suggesting his actions have hurt someone else.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~crush-devin-townsend-remix":{"haiku":"Nightmares and buried trauma resurface to haunt and reshape the speaker's future relentlessly.","opus":"A person losing the boundary between self and the one they cling to, terrified that the buried nightmares of their own making are surfacing as accusers. Guilt about hurting someone dresses itself up as horror.","sonnet":"About repressed trauma and guilt resurfacing as nightmares and hallucinations — the narrator has buried something, tried to outrun it, and now the creatures from those buried visions are arriving as abusers at the door. The guilt centers on a woman who suffered while the narrator hid.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~different":{"haiku":"The speaker warns others to show their true darkness rather than hide behind masks and cameras, suggesting that time is running out and authentic exposure is necessary. He observes that people are sinking into sand with limited time to act differently.","opus":"A drum-and-bass anthem urging someone to stop hiding a damaged, darkened self and let it show before time runs out. The escapism of going somewhere else is shadowed by the sense of sinking, of a life slipping away too fast to outrun.","sonnet":"A song about two people fleeing together toward some undefined elsewhere before time runs out — there's urgency and numbness in equal measure, expressions gone blank, sand rising around them. It reads as a desperate escape from a situation already lost, heading somewhere \"different\" simply because staying means sinking.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~encoder":{"haiku":"Relationship collapse through inability to communicate; one partner withdraws while the other struggles to speak, and distance forces deletion and goodbye with no relief.","opus":"A quiet, resigned parting between two people who couldn't face each other, showing someone the door after too much left unsaid, choosing to let the whole thing die because there's no relief left in bitterness.","sonnet":"A song about the moment a relationship finally ends after too much silence and avoidance — the narrator finally says what needed to be said, walks the other person to the door, and lets bitterness die rather than holding on.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~fasten-your-seatbelt":{"haiku":"A DJ or sound operator is too young to handle this music, with repeated warnings about a combination style and journey. The song emphasizes preparation and readiness for what's coming.","opus":"A drum-and-bass adrenaline manifesto built from soundsystem and jungle callouts, the dancefloor as a ride you brace yourself for; pure kinetic hype with no interior beyond the rush.","sonnet":"Essentially a pure DJ soundclash track built on the tropes of sound-system culture — the repeated warning that a young challenger is too inexperienced to compete, wrapped in the instruction to buckle up for what's coming.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~granite":{"haiku":"Observers watch as an addicted person loses control and surrenders to an approaching future they cannot prevent or escape.","opus":"A dystopian handover of power to some cold, inevitable future presence, framed as a new dawn that's really a surrender. The tone is ominous consolation, a life taken on demand while resistance quietly collapses.","sonnet":"A song about the inevitable arrival of a new and overwhelming force that will end the current order — the sun is setting on the old world, surrender is the only rational response, and a new dawn is already here.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~guiding-lights":{"haiku":"Betrayal and abandonment calcify into bitter regret; the speaker reflects on a friendship destroyed by another's instability, harboring lethal resentment.","opus":"A bitter reckoning with a fallen friendship, the narrator confessing he was a fool to follow someone he knew was toxic. Ten years of loyalty curdle into hoping the other chokes, yet loss of a friend still feels like loss of a home. The scarred, ambivalent tone, adoring the wounds even while wishing the person dead, captures the ugly grief of a relationship that rotted from within.","sonnet":"A friendship destroyed from the inside — one party was \"crazy\" and the narrator kept enabling it for two years until the full reckoning arrived: ten years of investment going straight into the fire, wishing them dead, the sharp tongue leaving 25 stitches and no more blood to bleed. The bitterness is surgical: \"how does it feel to know you fucked yourself?\"","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~halo-matt-tuck-rework":{"haiku":"Divine protection or cursed blessing, halo as symbol of grace or weight.","opus":"A confrontational anthem, reworked with Bullet for My Valentine's vocalist, staring down an enemy set on a battle of words. The narrator demands attention and seeks a sliver of redemption, rebuilding one layer at a time under crushing pressure. A thorn-wreathed crown is placed on the antagonist as the world itself pushes back, framing conflict as both personal vendetta and cosmic reckoning.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who has been twisting knives and polluting everything they touch — the narrator wants redemption and resolution but the antagonist is still rolling dice with a polluted brain. \"A halo of thorns\" is the perverse crown worn by someone who thought they were righteous; \"the world is pushing back\" is the consequence.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~hold-your-colour":{"haiku":"A call to preserve one's identity and values against overwhelming forces that seek to strip everything away; maintaining personal integrity when threatened with erasure.","opus":"Over hypnotic repetition, the song urges someone to hold their ground against everything being stripped away, framed by a menacing figure who twists a knife to force another's eyes open and completes a fatal circle.","sonnet":"Someone urges another to hold their identity firm when everything is being stripped away, while a separate voice describes a woman who saw through a person's surface and declared the cycle complete, leaving no escape.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~immunize-feat-liam-h":{"haiku":"A threat grows and multiplies while anticipating death; the speaker claims dominion over the dying and readiness for the end.","opus":"Drum-and-bass grandeur wrapped around a cold, almost predatory promise to be present at the moment of death, the propulsion and dread pointing at something apocalyptic in how humanity multiplies and consumes.","sonnet":"An aggressive, chant-like declaration from a force that multiplies and surrounds — present on the slope when you come to die — less a song with narrative content than an anthem of overwhelming, inevitable threat.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~mercy-killing":{"haiku":"Aggressive dominance and ruthless competition where destroying opponents is natural consequence of superior force and willpower.","opus":"An aggressive fusion of electronic rock and rap braggadocio built on the sense that time is nearly up and there's one last shot at making it on his own. The narrator thrives while his enemies drown in hate, dealing karma with a fist swinging like the band's namesake. Under the menace runs a fatalistic streak that treats dominance and self-destruction as two faces of the same reckoning.","sonnet":"Two registers in collision: an urgent plea to turn the ship around before it's too late, and a braggadocious battle-rap dismissal of rivals. The hook frames the whole as \"mercy killing\" — either an act of mercy toward a doomed cause, or the violence of cutting weakness off before it drags you down further.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~midnight-runner":{"opus":"Pendulum built their reputation on drum-and-bass maximalism where the drop carries the meaning and lyrics function as another rhythmic hook. True to that, this is barely more than a repeated command to let something drift away, no narrative arc beyond the single image of release, the words serving the momentum rather than any story.","sonnet":"The lyrics are solely a repeated instruction to let something drift away — there is no narrative or thematic development beyond this single image of release.","src":"sonnet"},
"pendulum~mutiny":{"haiku":"Terminal connection and broken communication define the maze; numbered sequences and reflections suggest digital labyrinth or corrupted entry.","opus":"A drum-and-bass fragment built on menace and repetition, entering a mind's maze through a broken, terminal connection. More texture and paranoia than narrative, a signal to be lost inside rather than a story to follow.","sonnet":"A brief, nearly lyric-free track — barely more than a repeated image of entering a maze through a reflection via a terminal connection — more texture and atmosphere than developed theme.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~nothing-for-free":{"haiku":"Cold war mentality and escalating conflict where everything dissolves into armed struggle with no stakes anymore.","opus":"A brooding drum-and-bass piece dressing present-day dread as a phony conflict, people perishing on home ground while the brazen seize power. At its core is a sense of scarcity and pointlessness, nothing left for anyone, a life stolen and faded, and a resigned shrug that none of it changes a thing.","sonnet":"About cold war-style collapse and armed standoff — the world has divided into factions, nothing remains for anyone, and the narrator prepares to become a soldier and fight across a waterline where taking arms means taking heads.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~plastic-world":{"haiku":"A speaker attracts and entangles another with music and movement; they contemplate transformation and dominion while admitting they cannot fully know where they stand.","opus":"A carnal come-on built almost entirely from repetition, transfixed by how someone moves, with the singer bragging he can read their mind and overturn their whole world while confessing he can't quite get a handle on them. Pendulum, the Australian-British drum-and-bass crossover act, use the words as texture, letting the seduction melt into pure dancefloor momentum.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~prelude":{"haiku":"A spoken meditation on human insignificance in the vastness of space and time, positioning Earth as a tiny island in an endless void, before pivoting to a declaration that civilization itself will shake on this small planet.","opus":"A spoken cosmic intro that pictures humanity marooned on a tiny planet in an endless sea of space, framing tonight as the moment to rock civilization.","sonnet":"A spoken-word intro frames humanity as isolated on a small planet in the vast emptiness of space, then pivots to announce that tonight, on this world, something extraordinary is about to happen.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~propane-nightmares":{"haiku":"A woman drawn into fire and danger cannot be saved from her path, leading to mutual destruction. The speaker pursues transcendence and liberation through that same burning, accepting the cost of freedom.","opus":"An explosive parable of a doomed woman who can't be saved and a love that chooses to burn rather than bury itself. The uplift in the chorus masks a fatalistic acceptance that release only comes through fire.","sonnet":"A song about being trapped in a destructive relationship with someone who draws you toward ruin — she's a \"gunshot bride\" pulling the narrator into fire. The refrain about burning before being buried is a vow to face destruction on his own terms rather than let her bury him; the woman \"cannot be saved\" from her own trail into the fire.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~save-the-cat":{"haiku":"Toxic cycles of manipulation and abandonment leave the speaker hollowed and alienated, seeking escape through reciprocal cruelty.","opus":"A dark, self-aware track about emotional flatlining inside a doomed relationship, the narrator pronouncing himself dead already and feeling nothing. His head swarms with menace and ghosts; wounding the other, he admits, is the same as wounding himself. The screenwriting-jargon title winks at contrivance while he chooses to kill the lights rather than fake a rescue.","sonnet":"A relationship poisoned by mutual hurt and emotional numbness plays out through surveillance imagery — ghosts, shadows, secrets under skin — until the speaker admits they feel nothing at all and begins looking for an alibi to justify walking away.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~self-vs-self-feat-in-flames":{"haiku":"Internal conflict and struggle to find home within oneself; darkness and fragmentation when identity cannot be located.","opus":"A battle waged entirely against oneself, interrogating identity, purpose and self-worth, the speaker feeling like a drained husk preyed on from within and repeatedly begging to find his way back to himself when nothing seems authentic. Pendulum team with In Flames to fuse pounding drum and bass with melodic death metal, giving the internal crisis a relentless, driving weight.","sonnet":"A person at war with themselves — unable to find direction, doubting their own reality, and losing their sense of identity — asks how to get home when everything feels fake and even those closest cannot help them find their way.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~set-me-on-fire":{"haiku":"The speaker and sound encounter thirst and disorder at a border; the group moves forward without those deemed rank and unworthy.","opus":"A dancefloor chant of walking away, its looped call to be lifted up and moved onward riding over a dancehall snapshot of a hostile stranger encountered at a frontier. The Australian drum-and-bass act generate momentum from repetition and patois vocals, the act of leaving someone behind stated coolly as the track barrels ahead.","sonnet":"A group charges forward and leaves someone behind without apology — the energy is forward momentum rather than confrontation. The encounter at a border crossing becomes a metaphor for those who insist on obstacles meeting people too driven to slow down.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~showdown":{"haiku":"A speaker confronts someone who betrayed them and drew false boundaries. Mutual belonging is denied and the speaker breaks free.","opus":"A defiant kiss-off to a doubter left behind: the narrator has moved far past the small lines they drew in the sand, and though they accused him of selling his soul, they never said it to his face. He leaves them cold and blows it all away, framing the split as a long-overdue reckoning he'd outgrown.","sonnet":"A cold, final confrontation with someone who betrayed or underestimated the narrator — the reckoning is brief and decisive, the narrator has already moved beyond anger into the certainty that they were never compatible.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~slam":{"haiku":"Stranded isolation on a small island in an endless sea, marooned and confined, yet delivering heavy bass and sound that rocks civilization.","opus":"A club anthem that frames the party as a small act of defiance against cosmic insignificance, marooned humanity choosing to rock the night rather than despair; the bass drop as the whole point of being alive tonight.","sonnet":"A brief, almost purely performative track — the minimal lyrics are a preamble for the drop, framing the dancefloor as a stranded island where the music is a civilization-rocking event.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~sounds-of-life":{"haiku":"Time has worn away possibilities and left only emptiness; one must drift and wait to feel safe and warm again.","opus":"The Australian drum-and-bass outfit, on their earliest work, wrap electronic melancholy around a sense of frozen time. The narrator waits with nothing to wait for as anything worth saving seems already discarded, deciding to make do with the remnants. Serving out his days to muffled hints of the world beyond, he holds onto the hope of comfort once he can finally understand it all.","sonnet":"Two people serving out a kind of emotional sentence — time dilated, possibilities exhausted, the world continuing noisily outside while they drift through a gray interior stillness. The hope is modest: eventually they will surface, feel warmer, find solid ground again.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~still-grey":{"haiku":"Stasis and grey monotony, with tension between fading/dreaming and urgent commands to hurry or slow—emotional ambivalence.","opus":"A short, mantra-like evocation of numb monotony, everything fading to grey while contradictory commands, hurry up and slow down, cancel each other out. The throughline is disoriented stasis, dreaming away in a colorless in-between where time and urgency lose meaning.","sonnet":"A liminal, dissociated state — dreaming, fading, suspended between hurrying and slowing down — the lyric is almost entirely atmosphere, a grey flatness that won't lift or resolve.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~streamline":{"haiku":"Someone warns against losing your original purpose and values by succumbing to external pressures, urging you to release and reclaim yourself internally.","opus":"A splintered drum-and-bass rumination on losing your sense of self, urging you to cling to whatever you first stood for as everything presses in. Its cycle of surrender and coming back circles a dread of being swapped out for just about anything.","sonnet":"About letting go of whatever you were originally about and allowing something new to flow in — the song urges surrender to a process of change rather than clinging to a stale sense of self, though the lyrical abstraction keeps the exact nature of that change deliberately vague.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~streamline-attlas-remix":{"haiku":"Precision and flow state achieve effortless motion; obstacles dissolve as momentum carries the speaker toward completion.","opus":"A remix built on hypnotic repetition and interrogation, urging a listener to go inside and question whether it was them or not. The looping invitation to let something come streamline and inside works as trance incantation more than narrative, a meditative surrender where introspection dissolves into pure rhythmic momentum. Meaning lives in the trance rather than the words.","sonnet":"A minimal loop of introspection — \"go inside, think about it, was it you or not\" — followed by an invitation to let something stream in unimpeded: the lyric is sparse enough to work as pure instruction, the \"streamline\" a metaphor for surrendering to whatever truth has been circling outside the door.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~tarantula":{"haiku":"A figure boasts of lethal prowess and intimidation using dehumanizing imagery; the song celebrates violence and domination as a status symbol within street culture.","opus":"A ragga-metal boast built on menace, the toasted vocals daring anyone to test a crew as venomous and unbothered by death as the spider it takes its name from, funeral imagery played as pure swagger.","sonnet":"A boastful threat in reggae-inflected style — the speaker is a deadly, venomous presence who will outlive you, attend your funeral, and leave you paralyzed; pure bravado in the tradition of Jamaican dancehall battle-rap.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~the-fountain-feat-steve-wilson":{"haiku":"A person warns they belong nowhere and won't stay long, carrying internal rage and cynicism about existence.","opus":"With Steven Wilson, a defiant reckoning with mortality and the sense that no achievement is ever enough. Behind the eyes sits homegrown rage and alienation, someone refusing fear while admitting the whole arrangement feels wrong.","sonnet":"A quietly seething song about inner rage and not belonging — the speaker insists they are not afraid but simply don't fit, that the energy they carry is real even if unrecognized, and that their mark will be felt after they disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~the-island-pt-1-dawn-skrillex-remix":{"haiku":"Surrender to transformation dissolves when resistance collapses; connection fragments as the group unravels.","opus":"A dawn of surrender and dissolution, urging someone to stop waiting and step toward the light as reason and resistance fall away. Coming undone framed as release rather than ruin.","sonnet":"A voice urges surrender toward a light that promises answers, but the underlying motion is one of coming undone — the urge dissolves, no reason remains, and the repeated call to surrender reads as both invitation and dissolution.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~the-island-pt-2-dusk":{"haiku":"Surrender to a transformative presence drawing the speaker toward light and fading away.","opus":"The comedown after the euphoria of part one, framed as surrender to a passenger who drags you toward oblivion. Beneath the rave imagery it reads like addiction or dissolving into something that pulls you under while promising release.","sonnet":"Surrendering to a destructive or consuming force — described as a hidden passenger dragging the narrator down into light they are moving toward together, the song is about giving up resistance and letting the pull of something overwhelming carry them to an undone ending.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~the-island-pt-i-dawn":{"haiku":"A hidden traveler carries someone into forgetting and emergence; transformation awaits as the narrator surrenders to movement toward light and answers.","opus":"The sun-drenched first half of the band's Ibiza epic, dawn on the island as surrender, a hidden passenger dragging you down toward the light. Beneath the euphoria is dissolution, the self slowly fading and coming undone, still searching for one answer as everything ends.","sonnet":"About being pulled toward oblivion by something you can't name — a hidden passenger dragging you under, surrender framed as moving toward light, identity dissolving as the urge to resist finally gives out.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~the-other-side":{"haiku":"Entities drag the lost down through gates of hell, claiming they will never escape; despair spreads across all relationships until the ship sinks.","opus":"A sinister invitation voiced by malevolent forces, angels or demons, hauling a treasured soul downward through hell's threshold to where they claim it belongs. The captor's obsessive, sleepless fixation reads like possession or addiction given a body, dragging the beloved beneath the waves as the narrator comes undone.","sonnet":"An insidious voice — demonic, cult-like, or representing addiction — that pursues someone relentlessly through every relationship and corner of their life, promising rescue while dragging them down, claiming total possession of the person it addresses.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~the-tempest":{"haiku":"A relationship crumbling under mutual indifference; both parties are complicit in its destruction, one refusing help while the other lets go, and the singer acknowledges the separation is nobody's fault but themselves.","opus":"A brutally honest breakup where the narrator admits he could help but simply won't, sick of rescuing someone determined to sink themselves, choosing self-immolation over the endless chase and laying the blame squarely at their door.","sonnet":"A song about watching someone spiral into self-destruction and finally refusing to save them — the narrator decides he does not want to help anymore, lets go of responsibility, and concludes that the damage the other person brought on themselves is nobody else's fault.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~the-terminal":{"haiku":"A figure enters through reflection and digital connection, repeatedly crossing the maze where inner world meets terminal access—boundary dissolving between self and interface.","opus":"An entry into something like a digital dreamscape, threading through a maze and a mirrored reflection to reach a connection framed as a terminal, both an endpoint and a machine port. The imagery is cold and circuit-like, a body dissolving into interface. Pendulum, the Australian drum and bass outfit, built their reputation on exactly this fusion of rave momentum and sci-fi menace, the vocal a brief cryptic doorway into the drop.","sonnet":"Entry through a digital threshold — navigating a maze that is also a mirror, passing into a space defined by connection rather than place. The terminal here is both an endpoint and a portal, and the journey inward happens through reflection, as if the self is the interface the system uses to let you through.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~the-vulture":{"haiku":"A predatory force feeds on panic and weakness, climbing relentlessly while taking power from those who've lost their way.","opus":"A predatory power-fantasy spoken by a scavenger bird that thrives on others' fear, frailty, and rotten arrogance, snatching control from within the storm's core. The through-line is opportunistic domination, feeding on the collapse and jitters of everyone who lost their balance.","sonnet":"A predator — political, social, or metaphorical — rising to dominance by feeding on the anxiety and collapse of others, declaring that panic and disorder are the conditions under which it thrives and claims power.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~through-the-loop":{"haiku":"Uncertainty and danger escalate as people move forward blindly without knowing their direction or fate. The rowers' persistent forward motion increases risk despite their lack of awareness.","opus":"Sampling the panicked tunnel monologue from Willy Wonka's boat ride, the track rides a spiraling sense of helpless momentum, a voyage careening toward possible doom that no passenger steers or slows. Pendulum weaponize the source's dread into an unstoppable rush toward danger nobody controls.","sonnet":"A direct lift of the Willy Wonka boat tunnel monologue — the lyrics are the original Roald Dahl text describing accelerating danger and disorientation in the dark, repurposed as a drum-and-bass existential free-fall.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~under-the-waves":{"haiku":"Drowning in despair with no ground beneath; the speaker sinks helplessly toward death. Silence and spiritual emptiness weigh them down into the depths.","opus":"A slow surrender to drowning, held under with no spirit left to fight as the tide pulls the singer past dreams he knows he'll never reach. The descent reads as depression made oceanic, the calm of finally giving up the struggle.","sonnet":"A person is submerged — literally or metaphorically — held down with no spirit, unable to touch the ground, their dreams existing only at a distance they will never reach, descending toward an end.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~visions":{"haiku":"A call to abandon visions of becoming a superstar and instead keep grounded, leaving place and drifting away from the illusions of ascent.","opus":"A push-and-pull between artistic vision and market demand: someone is told to keep their lofty visions to themselves and just deliver the sound, undercut by the cynical aside about becoming a superstar. The pleas to leave this place and drift toward the light frame the tension as a slow surrender of self to the machine that wants only the product.","sonnet":"A call to someone to leave their current life behind and follow the speaker toward music and the light — an urgent, almost messianic invitation to escape and drift away together.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~watercolour":{"haiku":"A person is falling into darkness and crisis, asking for rescue while experiencing the dissolution of their beliefs and isolation from others.","opus":"A plea to be pulled out of a psychological free-fall, addressed to someone who might catch the singer when he's cornered and unrecognizable to friends; the drop-anthem refrain reframes surrender as a call to gather energy and stay present rather than flee.","sonnet":"A desperate plea for rescue when someone is at their lowest — losing their identity, their beliefs, and their grip on reality — addressed to someone they're begging not to abandon them in that collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"pendulum~witchcraft":{"haiku":"A woman is in danger from a killer approaching through her home. The song builds dread as those around her fail to help, culminating in her disappearance and death.","opus":"An urgent warning to a woman in danger, sensing a killer approaching through the hallway and repeatedly urging her to get out and flee far away.","sonnet":"The song builds a tense domestic horror scenario: a woman is in danger from someone — described as a killer in the hallway — and the urgency mounts as the narrator warns her she has no time left and must get out before he reaches her.","src":"haiku"},
"pengshui~idkwybt":{"haiku":"Working-class men bond through hedonistic excess and collective drinking in London, using alcohol and camaraderie to numb and escape their lived realities.","opus":"A genre-blurring British romp cataloguing the grotesque regulars of a pub, lighthouse lunatics and cauliflower ears drowning their fears, working men reduced to animals for their families. Under the raucous, name-checking storytelling runs a sardonic portrait of communal self-destruction and the secrets that spill once the mask slips.","sonnet":"A torrent of London pub-culture vignettes — Larry making a mess, the Amsterdam mustard point five, men drowning in a church of blood and tears, animals for family — where stories soaked in liquid gold are just the same blinded show on repeat, and the closing image of working men drinking their fears frames the whole carnival as tragedy dressed as comedy.","src":"haiku"},
"people-in-the-box~a-1o8htum":{"haiku":"A dystopian vision of humans as commodities in a digital marketplace—tracked, analyzed, extracted for profit.","opus":"People In The Box specialize in literate, unsettling art-rock, and here their precision sharpens into full dystopia. Set in a central human racing venue in a silicon valley at the solar system's edge, anonymous gamblers bid on people in exponentially doubling numbers as encrypted figures scroll and the crowd's cheers skip like damaged tape. The subject is human life reduced to speculative capital under algorithmic watch.","sonnet":"A dystopian portrait of human beings as data — the song is set in a 'central human racing venue' somewhere in a silicon valley at the edge of the solar system, where anonymous gamblers bid on people in exponentially doubling numbers while encrypted figures scroll across scoreboards and the crowd's cheers skip like a damaged recording. The subject is the reduction of human life to speculative capital under algorithmic surveillance.","src":"haiku"},
"people-in-the-box~a-f7y7nz":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation and escapism—the narrator repeatedly invokes going to the sea as a metaphor for death, where nightmares end, while struggling with existential anxiety and the artificiality of human existence.","opus":"The band's gift for cloaking despair in gentle, oblique melody makes this the darkest kind of invitation. A narrator coaxes someone at the edge of death toward the sea, framing it as escape from a nightmare and from the ceaseless anxiety of living. The refrain about going to the sea works at once as tender offering and quiet suicide pact, where the bad dream finally ends.","sonnet":"A narrator urges someone on the edge of death to come with them to the sea, framing it as an escape from a nightmare and from the relentless anxiety of living — the sea becomes the place where the bad dream ends, and the refrain of \"let's go to the sea\" is both a tender invitation and a suicide pact.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~22-faces":{"haiku":"Time slips away while the narrator feels something eating at his life from within. He's paralyzed by forces beyond his control, losing grip on what makes him human.","opus":"A visceral spiral into insomnia and psychological disintegration, a mind drawn and quartered by an internal monster it can't name. The djent chaos mirrors the sensation of being devoured from the inside out.","sonnet":"About being eaten alive by an internal entity — some psychological force that runs the narrator like a machine without direction, quarter-drawing them, depriving them of sleep, and threatening their sanity while they desperately try to understand what exactly is hollowing them out.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~a-black-minute":{"haiku":"Sacred ritual and human ecstasy merge in nocturnal circles where bodies bleed together; families bear evil and sacrifice becomes necessary for continued life amid unholy cathedrals and duality.","opus":"Prog-metal occult imagery of a ritual birth, a child brought into a bloodline burdened by evil, bought survival through sacrifice. It dwells in duality and complicity, ecstasy and staining bound together.","sonnet":"A ritualistic, quasi-occult scene of people gathering in an unholy circle, marking their bodies with blood in worship or sacrifice, with the cycle of evil passed on as a burden to the next generation.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~absolomb":{"haiku":"A speaker watches their love fade behind walls of internal hatred while trapped on opposite sides unable to bridge the distance. They yearn to reconnect beyond the frozen moment of separation.","opus":"The aftermath of a devil's bargain, someone who traded his soul to command the planet and now watches love and closeness ice over behind a barrier of loathing. The horror is that the deal left him nothing but emptiness while time grinds to a halt.","sonnet":"About the dissolution of a close relationship — the narrator sold out something essential in themselves, watched someone they loved fade behind a wall of hate, and is left frozen, waiting beyond some edge for a reunion that may never come.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~all-new-materials":{"haiku":"A meditation on cycles of rebirth and immortality where life repeats endlessly and progression is risky. The speaker offers transcendence and escape from the wheel, suggesting that dreaming and imagination offer freedom from trapped existence.","opus":"A prog-metal meditation on cycles of death and rebirth, chasing an elusive real dream and the eternal pull toward transcendence. The heart of it is surrendering control, going with the current in hope of reaching better days.","sonnet":"A meditation on reincarnation or cyclical existence — the narrator is caught between longing to know the truth of where we go and what life means, while being warned not to interfere with the wheel of rebirth. The song accepts the cycle with cautious wonder rather than terror.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~alpha":{"haiku":"The speaker urges letting go of envy and discontent, arguing that presence and life itself are sufficient despite constant desire for more.","opus":"A study of envy and permanent discontent, the mind endlessly hungry for what lies out of reach while wasting the little time it has. The core tension is a fight to scrub this parasitic craving from the head and actually feel present and alive rather than quietly rotting from within.","sonnet":"About envy as a mental disease — the constant belief that life is better somewhere else, that someone else has what you're owed, is the thing draining life of meaning, and the song is an urgent plea to stop bleeding internally over what you'll never have.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~atropos":{"haiku":"Numbing pacification and surface distractions mask deeper suffering and spiritual emptiness.","opus":"Named for the Fate who cuts the thread, it stares at chemical numbness and money-chasing as the same evasion: choosing to stay pacified in the middle rather than feel, until the soul is sold and nobody belongs anywhere.","sonnet":"About collective spiritual emptiness and the sense that modern people are broken-brained and sold out — the narrator and those around them are numbed by serotonin deficits and superficial highs, the soul is described as empty and sold; Atropos is the fate who cuts the thread of life, and the song asks where any of us actually belong.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~blood-eagle":{"haiku":"Norse warrior conquest and Christian subjugation. The blood eagle executes the vanquished while Odin and Valhalla consume the land and its people.","opus":"A brutal Viking war fantasy narrated from the raiders' side, Norse conquest and the grisly blood-eagle rite set against the Christians they slaughter. Djent bombast reveling in pagan violence and the cold theology of Valhalla claiming the dead.","sonnet":"A full-throated Viking raid narrative — Norse warriors descend by sea to slaughter Christian settlers, performing the blood eagle ritual, invoking Thor and Odin, and claiming the land as Valhalla's glory demands.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~buttersnips":{"haiku":"Cyclical return to dust and decay; consciousness folds under cultural pressure and the weight of inevitably becoming nothing again.","opus":"A djent track full of abstract, end-times imagery of a species worn out by its own advancement, souls redrawing what's absolute and cutting a path toward rising up. It loops through cycles of rot and comeback, the self crumbling into grit then flaring back to life, less a story than a rumination on impermanence.","sonnet":"A meditation on the human cycle of existence — flesh, myth, culture, love, all falling and returning to sand; the narrator sees civilization as a recurring pattern of collapse and rebirth, urging a turn toward ascension before it ends.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~catch-fire":{"haiku":"Two people burn with passion in a moment of ecstasy, offering each other paradise and peace of mind as they transcend reality and mortality together.","opus":"An ecstatic surrender to intimacy so intense it courts self-annihilation, offering paradise in exchange for peace of mind. Beneath the sweetness runs a darker awareness that this consuming closeness is also a kind of death.","sonnet":"About surrendering to a connection so intense it feels like combustion — bodies burning, paradise promised, peace of mind traded for everything — the song frames falling into someone as a kind of beautiful death, worth dying for if it means joining something that transcends ordinary existence.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~digital":{"haiku":"Consumptive materialism hollows authentic experience; passivity masks itself through digital vocalisation.","opus":"A critique of a screen-numbed generation, loud online yet passive in life, chasing material and chemical distraction while a soul quietly shakes. Youth going digital as a slow forfeiture of presence.","sonnet":"A generation passively vocal online but inert in real life is described as digitally loud and materially hollow — sex and chemicals on the mind, goals reducing to material, the brain forgetting to actually live — while the chorus names it plainly: the youth are digital.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~erised":{"haiku":"A person sinks into a sedative mental state, collecting debt for wrongs committed while emotionally numb. They drift through a corrupted world unable to escape from its diseased influence.","opus":"Periphery, the Washington-area djent innovators, render a slide toward oblivion as a foundering ship and a phantom luring the narrator into a surreal, drugged headspace as a sedative takes hold. He begs to leave this reality while longing itself becomes a mirror, the title spelled in reverse, a distant tune still calling.","sonnet":"A sinking vessel used as both literal image and internal metaphor — the speaker drifting through a drugged, checkerboard reality where those in power speak gold while spreading disease. The only way through is to let emotion do what reason cannot, carrying the debt accumulated by surrender and finally choosing to remain rather than dissolve into the current.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~everything-is-fine":{"haiku":"Guilt and self-condemnation become prison as the speaker remains trapped in shame despite recognizing their complicity in the architecture of their own cage.","opus":"Savage self-flagellation from someone who feels like a fraud and a monster, spitting that the body is a prison rather than a sanctuary. The title's cheer is bitter irony over the lows he keeps bingeing.","sonnet":"Vicious self-disgust in the shape of a sunny day: the speaker bows to a pedestal with no god, cannot shake a cowardly feeling, and screams \"guilty\" at themselves in the mirror — the body reframed as a prison rather than a sanctuary, the shattered reflection multiplying the indictment.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~facepalm-mute":{"haiku":"The song urges people to think independently and reject ignorance in order to transform. It promotes spiritual unity and energy to fight against passive consumption and toxic cycles.","opus":"Periphery pair intricate djent with a demand for independent thinking, urging the listener to reason for themselves at least once. The narrator inflates into cosmic self-mythology, declaring humanity a single force poised to steal a new world, contemptuous of the ignorant while pleading to shed inherited blindness and find beauty within.","sonnet":"A call to shed passive ignorance and think independently — framed as both a personal and collective awakening. Beneath the urgency runs a vision of unified human potential, the sense that consciousness itself is waiting to be claimed if people would simply stop outsourcing their minds.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~flatline":{"haiku":"The lyrics depict suicide ideation and severe depression, with someone calling for rescue but ultimately letting go of life due to unbearable isolation.","opus":"A portrait of suicidal despair, a man crushed by depression who feels his body is no longer home and pleads for an angel to either lift him up or let him die; the framing (bottle and blade, someone finally crossing the flatline) treats death as a peaceful release the narrator watches with sorrow.","sonnet":"About watching someone spiral into suicidal despair from the outside — the narrator can see the isolation and the bottle and the blade but can't break through, and the song ends with the quiet horror of someone dying while at peace.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~froggin-bullfish":{"haiku":"Humanity is spiritually imprisoned, distracted by illusions and negative consciousness that breed despair. The song urges awakening to freedom beyond the constructed cage.","opus":"A summons to break free of mental captivity, framed in cosmic and spiritual language, urging you to smash the barriers of your own confinement rather than live as an obedient nobody. It condemns self-pity and a poisoned mindset that projects its own terror outward. American djent pioneers Periphery pair questing lyrics with polyrhythmic guitar work.","sonnet":"An indictment of unconscious living — humanity blind to its own nature, chasing wealth or comfort like drones, playing victim instead of breaking free. The song demands awakening and self-interrogation, insisting that fear and negative thinking are self-generated projections, and that freedom requires burning down the cage you've built around yourself.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~garden-in-the-bones":{"haiku":"Spiritual and tribal forces overwhelm an awakened consciousness through supernatural voices and ancestral graves; the living and dead coexist, building symbolic territories and headdresses in soil and bone.","opus":"A fevered, ritual-soaked vision of hovering between life and death, plagued by primal cries and predatory beasts as the narrator fears his grip slipping entirely. The central metaphor is founding something new atop death itself, staking one's existence and self on the graveyard, insisting who we are grows out of what lies beneath.","sonnet":"A song about the violence and bad faith underlying the land we claim as ours — settler possession built on burial grounds and alcohol, with the singer caught between waking and the dead, losing his grip on reality.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~graveless":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation and demonic possession merge as the narrator surrenders to internal destruction, burying their own life through conscious choice.","opus":"A suicidal descent into damnation, the narrator setting a blade against his neck and inviting the devil in, resolved to end his life by his own doing while his one distant love stays out of reach.","sonnet":"A figure at the absolute edge of self-destruction — knife at throat, Beelzebub inside, dried-up bones — releases into the idea of dying without a grave, framing it as both defeat and relief, with a final prayer for a distant love and a drift away into sin.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~habitual-line-stepper":{"haiku":"A tyrant's control crumbles as a boundary-breaker or rebel disrupts and destroys their power, causing psychological torment.","opus":"A reckoning with a domineering figure whose whole edifice is collapsing, the narrator done waiting for a decency that never came and choosing to let the hate burn through. The chorus keeps demanding whether the man could ever have been better and stayed.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who repeatedly crosses the line — a tyrant, an abuser of power, someone who has burned everything down through their pattern of behavior. The song tracks the collapse of that person's edifice of control and the long-deferred rage of the people they've kept behind barricades.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~have-a-blast":{"haiku":"The narrator escapes corporate monotony and social conformity through spiritual awakening, rejecting the manufactured dead-end life society imposes.","opus":"Periphery rage against soul-deadening office life, reducing the cubicle worker to a puppet and a mindless clone trapped in a rigged system. The way out is creativity and imagination, the narrator insisting his real home lies in the music and the exhilaration of actually living, not the desk he was assigned.","sonnet":"About waking up to the fact that office-drone existence is a death sentence for the self — the cubicle, the daily grind, and the corporate machine crush ambition and individuality, and the narrator declares their escape through music and the will to feel alive.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~heavy-heart":{"haiku":"Grief and psychological burden weigh heavily; nightmares of violence and mourning persist as the speaker struggles to find peace and release from haunting memories.","opus":"Periphery on grief and the wish to fly free of a suffocating life, tenderness curdling into nightmare. The gentle sorrow gives way to violent, haunted imagery, suggesting the memory being mourned is far darker than the sad-song surface.","sonnet":"A song about carrying unbearable psychological weight — visions of violence against children, a secret inner life of darkness, and a persistent wish to escape this existence entirely, though the narrator keeps trying to let it go.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~icarus-lives":{"haiku":"A defiant figure refuses submission, flying toward transcendence while rejecting divine authority and mortality's constraints.","opus":"A defiant reworking of the Icarus myth, wings donned and used each night, the speaker claiming he's neither holy nor cursed yet hailed as divine, treating gravity as a rule of his own making. Periphery's progressive metalcore casts temptation as glory etched into the record, his refusal to submit hardening into a dare to detonate, ascent chosen in full knowledge of the fall.","sonnet":"A figure of mythic ambition refuses the labels of angel or demon, claiming the sky as a right rather than a transgression. The Icarus myth is inverted — the fall is not defeat but defiance, and the speaker soars precisely because mortality is treated as something to overcome rather than obey.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~insomnia":{"haiku":"Insecurity, sleeplessness, and the breakdown of a relationship haunt the narrator, who feels unappreciated and unable to heal.","opus":"A sleepless spiral of disillusionment and hollow reassurance, the narrator feeling overlooked, drinking to numb himself, and coaxed toward recklessness at the brink. Periphery use imagery of misaligned stars and bodies rotting underground to render insomnia as a limbo suspended between faith and nothingness.","sonnet":"A fractured confrontation with someone who can't be reached — the speaker is sleepless, unappreciated, watching warning signs ignored while drinking through the pain, and the song builds toward a declaration of severance and disappearance into the dark.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~jetpacks-was-yes":{"haiku":"A journey from illusion and fear toward awakening and freedom within a dream. The speaker moves past haunting memories to find truth and connection beyond loneliness.","opus":"Prog-metal act Periphery move through a collapsing dreamscape of dissolving barriers and tearing fabric, the narrator straining toward an exit and exhausting himself in the climb. Misled by fear and the past, he finally crosses beyond the illusion into a place where he is free and no longer alone, escape reframed as arrival.","sonnet":"A consciousness caught between dream and waking tries to break free of illusions shaped by fear and memory, only to discover that solitude inside infinite possibility is itself a kind of freedom. The search for an exit from the dream gradually becomes the recognition that the dreamer is not as alone as he believed.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~ji":{"haiku":"An ancient suppressed force awakens from the darkness, consuming the living in shadow and representing repressed desire or corruption breaking through civilized constraints.","opus":"A dark fantasy narrated by a suppressed race of shadow beings who rise from the deep to feed on the living, conquer the earth, and blot out the light of humankind.","sonnet":"A mythological or cosmic horror narrative about an ancient, suppressed dark force — described as feeding on the living, conquering the earth, and lurking within all people — finally awakening to reclaim dominance from humanity.","src":"haiku","fable":"Ji stages a creation myth run in reverse: instead of light breaking over a formless world, a buried people climbs up out of the crust to switch the sun off for good. The horror is territorial rather than moral — these things aren't invaders so much as an evicted first tenant come to repossess the surface, and the sleeping cities above register as livestock, warm bodies of stored carbon to be drained. Periphery's usual move is to bolt this pulp-cosmic dread to djent's mechanized chug, so the narrator's cold flatness reads as design: no rage, just the patient arithmetic of a species that has already won and is only waiting for the sky to line up. The real turn comes at the end, when the thing surfacing from below is quietly relocated inside the listener — the dark isn't a monster crawling out of the sea but a repressed appetite the song claims was always sealed under the polite floor of civilization.","fv":2,"themes":["extinction","shame & repression","nature & the elements"],"means":"the returning tenant's cold arithmetic of a species already having won","built":"the monster is quietly relocated inside the listener at the close"},
"periphery~letter-experiment":{"haiku":"The song depicts a journey across the underworld to death, echoing mythology of souls boarding the final voyage. It portrays acceptance of inevitable fate and reunion beyond life's end.","opus":"Death and the underworld staged through the ferryman Charon, souls filing toward a final crossing while the living cling to fading memory. It reaches past the grief for reunion beyond the end, the mind freed as it departs.","sonnet":"An atmospheric retelling of the myth of Charon ferrying the dead across the Styx — the coin for passage, the crowd of souls, the endless line — framed as a meditation on death, loss, and the possibility of reunion beyond it.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~light":{"haiku":"The speaker seeks light but remains blind to it, trapped between devotion and doubt, unable to ascend from darkness and confusion.","opus":"A struggle against a blinding, misguided devotion, the narrator urging himself to wake up and reject the empty faith he'd accepted, reaching toward something luminous to climb toward. Periphery, the Washington djent pioneers, frame the spiritual crisis with intricate polyrhythms as a lost soul strains to break past the barrier trapping it.","sonnet":"A soul adrift from devotion and meaning struggles to locate a signal through the noise of doubt and physical exhaustion. The child inside has lost its way; the plea for even a single ray of ascent is not religious but desperately human — something to aim for before the performance of living hollows out entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~luck-as-a-constant":{"haiku":"Rejection of imposed guilt and compulsion: the speaker breaks free from fathers' judgment and societal needs, refusing silence and demanding autonomy to live by their own measure.","opus":"Periphery mount a rebellious stand against inherited guilt and controlling authority, the narrator seizing a rush of personal power and demanding autonomy. Rather than fake a clean, virtuous existence, he insists on owning his flaws, arguing that clinging to shame is worse than admitting the darker parts of oneself.","sonnet":"A speaker rejects the guilt installed by paternalistic authority and the cleanly controlled life such authority demands, asserting ownership over pleasure and transgression as acts of self-determination. The sinner side he reclaims is not celebrated as vice but as the evidence that he exists on his own terms rather than someone else's.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~lune":{"haiku":"Warmth and love provide stability amid chaos. The speaker melts in rays of affection and never lets go of the other person.","opus":"A love song about being pulled out of frozen solitude by someone else's warmth; despite scorched, drugged, half-wiped memories, the singer feels himself thaw in the glow of this bond, swearing again and again that he'll hold on, and asking whether they'll sense it together as the years pass.","sonnet":"A warm, earnest love song about finding someone who rescues you from coldness and isolation — the narrator is enveloped by this person's presence, terrified of losing what they have, and surrenders to the feeling of being fully held.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~marigold":{"haiku":"Youth trapped in mundane existence watches death approach while enduring misery, told through a dark homily on societal rot.","opus":"Youth staring down mortality and futility, resigned to the mundane while death circles like a storm. A sampled sermon on cultural decay sharpens the sense of a generation bleeding toward nothing yet refusing despair outright.","sonnet":"A meditation on the collective death drive — young people paralysed at the edge of their own graves, the clock running on a society that feeds on its own children, with an interspersed monologue about what the devil would do to destroy civilisation from within.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~marigold-live-in-london":{"haiku":"Youth persists against mundane satisfaction as the speaker defies angels while chained to life's routine. Beauty blooms in resistance against comfort and complacency.","opus":"A live take on the band's youth-against-mortality anthem — clinging to life while settling for the ordinary, graveyard imagery pressed against the glow of being young. Slow-burning misery meets a stubborn refusal to surrender, begging for the resolve to keep going before the end arrives.","sonnet":"Youth slipping through the marigolds while death circles like a hurricane: the narrator stares down the angels, notes everyone is \"sucking on the mother's dead tit of sorrow,\" and the body becomes dust before anyone gets a funeral or a pyre left burning — misery is killing them slowly but they still want a spine to work it out.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~masamune":{"haiku":"A soul rejects holy judgment and descends into darkness, grappling with cosmic fate and spiritual exile.","opus":"A fading mind coming loose from its body, unsure whether the voice summoning it is holy or infernal, turned back at the sacred threshold and hauling old power down with it as color drains to gray. Remorse or vengeance stays unsettled, the soul refusing to be steered. Named for the famed Japanese swordsmith, this Periphery cut turns blackened and bleak, nodding to frostbitten metal beneath their trademark djent precision.","sonnet":"A soul rejected at the threshold between life and death — turned away from the holy gate and left drifting in a grim, frostbitten in-between. Past glories collapse into dust, angelic song recedes, and what remains is the question of what becomes of all that energy when schemes and dreams cave in.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~mile-zero":{"haiku":"The speaker grieves an irreplaceable absence, walking through life after loss while missing their departed friend.","opus":"Periphery channel their technical progressive metal into open mourning. A friend's death carves down through the body into the spirit, memory hitting like a blow while affection stains every sleepless night and the grieving mind refuses fake acceptance. The one consolation is a promise to reunite somewhere beyond this life.","sonnet":"Grief for a friend who died by suicide — the wound described as a slug to the brain, memories staining the walls, every day since spent not quite accepting the absence. The speaker feels the connection still, gut-open and trembling, refusing false closure, walking the road of life alone with the knowledge that reunion exists only in some dimension beyond this one.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~mk-ultra":{"haiku":"The song depicts psychological manipulation and control inflicted on innocents from childhood, using corruption, pain, and ownership to break the victim's autonomy. The bruises and blood mark permanent subjugation.","opus":"Told from the perspective of controllers corrupting an innocent from infancy, mining and claiming a child's thoughts, marking them with a bruise as a sign of ownership forever. It evokes the notorious mind-control program as a metaphor for total psychological domination and stolen autonomy, blood on the hands passed to the victim.","sonnet":"About systemic psychological control — an authority that corrupts from infancy, using violence and ownership to mark its subjects as property forever. The MK Ultra reference frames it as literal mind control, with the victim painted as belonging to the system from birth.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~motormouth":{"haiku":"Social media influencers and attention-seekers are intellectually empty performers whose chatter adds nothing of value.","opus":"A contemptuous attack on vapid, filtered social-media fame-seekers, a mannequin chasing likes who is only a click from fame. Told to eat and swallow their worthless words, the target is condemned for defiling the world with empty noise, the narrator restrained only by conscience from violence.","sonnet":"A contemptuous takedown of social-media-driven attention seekers — people who pose, filter, and chatter their way to fake fame without substance, told bluntly to swallow their worthless words and shut up.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~muramasa":{"haiku":"Nations face rising waters and destruction yet deny warnings, sleepwalking toward collective catastrophe driven by denial and complacency.","opus":"Named for a legendary swordsmith, this Periphery piece frames impending catastrophe as deliberate self-deception. The season is embraced even as ruin glints plainly on the horizon, and the narrator cautions that rising waters engulf whole peoples nobody will admit to seeing. Declaring themselves a vast presence dwelling within the listener, the band insists no comforting falsehood can excuse the suffering, and the willfully blind won't survive to face the reckoning.","sonnet":"Humanity has already accepted its fall, ignoring the flood rising around entire nations while choosing comfortable denial. The voice speaking is the universe itself — patient, watching — warning that the water is real and the moment to wake has already arrived.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~omega":{"haiku":"Guilt and supernatural punishment spiral inward; the speaker becomes a demon of their own making, damned by participation in violence.","opus":"A prog-metal descent into a soul confronting the destruction it's caused, greeted by those it killed, begging for resurrection and forgiveness it may not get. It stages the horror of seeing yourself as the monster and still aching for something past hell itself.","sonnet":"A condemned soul in death confronts the parade of those it destroyed during its life, recognizing itself as a monster born of human corruption and begging for resurrection and forgiveness while knowing it is beyond saving. The song oscillates between self-loathing and a desperate hope for something beyond hell.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~ow-my-feelings":{"haiku":"Loss of faith and disconnection from truth; the speaker grapples with arrogance and isolation while seeking to experience the world through their own singular perspective.","opus":"A crisis of faith staged as ego dissolution: the narrator sheds inherited belief and begs someone to see the world as he does, aware his newfound freedom to lead is also the seed of the arrogance that dooms him. Periphery's djent maximalism used for a very human spiritual unravelling.","sonnet":"About being trapped in a solipsistic perspective — the narrator asks why no one ever sees the world through his eyes, recognizes that arrogance blinds everyone (including himself), and tries to let go of the walls of belief that isolate people from each other.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~prayer-position":{"haiku":"Crushing pressure and despair hold the speaker at gunpoint in a state of prayer. They beg for relief while trapped in self-abuse and moral questioning.","opus":"A harrowing portrait of addiction and self-destruction, life lived in a hole with a gun to the head, chasing the fleeting glow of a high while everything corrodes. The prayer position is ironic, not devotion but the posture of someone brought to their knees by the crash.","sonnet":"A song about being crushed under the weight of mental illness and substance use simultaneously — the narrator is both pinned to the floor by existential despair and chasing a high that briefly lifts them before the crash, cycling between numbness and oblivion.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~priestess":{"haiku":"Captivity and forbidden grace coexist; the speaker finds safety in confinement while dreaming of liberation.","opus":"A captive in a fortress cell dreaming of one more shot at being alive, scraping together brittle refuge in falsehoods and stray flickers of affection while awaiting his execution. The thin path of light beckoning from beyond the walls stands for a hope he may never actually reach.","sonnet":"A prisoner — literal or metaphorical — endures a life of confinement and darkness, sustained only by the brief kindness of one person whose presence feels like love amid captivity. The chains eventually feel like part of the self, and the dream of escape fades as the cross burns.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~psychosphere":{"haiku":"Silence triggers mental collapse—the speaker is trapped in a mind never their own, urging violent revenge against those pulling their strings.","opus":"A descent into violent psychosis, a narrator inhabited by a mind that was never his own, love stripped away and replaced by cold sadistic compulsion. The horror is the calm at the center of it, bowing the head and walking away from the carnage.","sonnet":"About a mind turned murderous by some unnamed past trauma — the narrator describes being a product of a consciousness that was never their own, driven toward violence, and fantasizing about hanging people and walking away from the bloody wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~racecar":{"haiku":"Pursuit of freedom against control becomes an endless race. Memory and loss compound as fate is repeatedly relived without escape.","opus":"A sprawling, abstract meditation on fate, memory and pushing forward against inevitable loss; images of an endless chase, blood-painted tapestry and a light seen through a lover's eyes trace a struggle to stay whole, let go of the past, and keep seeing the light even as everything strains toward collapse.","sonnet":"A lengthy, diffuse meditation on escaping mental traps and finding a clearer path through love and self-awareness — it moves through imagery of sailing, light, and memory to arrive at a place of cautious openness and the slow work of growing consciously.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~ragnarok":{"haiku":"Isolation and guilt consume the speaker—haunted by bloodshed and failure, watching darkness descend while wrestling with demons of conscience.","opus":"A plunge into guilt-driven madness and private apocalypse, isolated for weeks as a frigid, insatiable evil and Death take up residence inside him. He relives massacres within the confinement of a tormented conscience and glimpses, on the horizon, humanity engineering its own ruin because it refuses to heed its own warnings.","sonnet":"A song about a mind consumed by guilt, violence, and encroaching madness — the narrator has been isolated for forty days, carrying the weight of bloodshed in their conscience, and feels a ravenous demonic presence climbing their spine, locking them in a private hell from which there may be no exit.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~rainbow-gravity":{"haiku":"Humanity sinks into moral mistakes and self-segregation, failing to think for itself; the speaker refuses complicity as humanity drowns in bullshit.","opus":"An environmental and existential plea framed as sinking into a dark ocean of humanity's collective mistakes, questioning whether our self-inflated sense of worth is warranted; it calls on the human race to think for itself and stop segregating, warning that the more we divide the more alone we are, and refusing to be complicit in the coming shipwreck.","sonnet":"A collective indictment of humanity sinking under the weight of its own arrogance and moral failures — the image is of everyone drowning in an ocean of mistakes they refuse to acknowledge, with a closing plea that independent thought and solidarity could still lift people up before it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~remain-indoors":{"haiku":"The song critiques illusion and disconnection, suggesting people are trapped in fantasy where nothing new exists and messages of value cannot penetrate darkness. It questions whether following or resisting matters at all.","opus":"A counsel to stop performing and posturing, to trade the mire for the meadow and accept that beauty is illusion and mistakes only real once you fall out of the fantasy. The narrator holds his position, indifferent to whether anyone follows, sure the real message is inside.","sonnet":"A song about stepping outside the noise of self-criticism and tunnel vision — the narrator encourages letting go of perfectionism and the burden of constant self-examination, arguing that the real message has always been golden and the stride should be lighter.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~reptile":{"haiku":"A boy named Billy is caught in a green-skin apocalypse; humanity descended from altered apes and now sheds violence while following reptilian leaders toward extinction.","opus":"Periphery let their prog-metal maximalism sprawl across a satirical epic about a slacker antihero named Billy who becomes a resistance figure during an invasion by reptilian rulers. The track sends up conspiracy-theory lizard-people lore while pushing a real message: judge no one by status or ancestry, only by whether they help you survive.","sonnet":"A conspiracy parable built around Billy, a regular guy radicalized by a green-skin apocalypse that may be real or symbolic — the reptilian powers-that-be own every need and the streets run toward mayhem while he trains to face them. The satirical lens shifts between sharp political critique and B-movie absurdism, asking whether any of us know who we are once the frame breaks.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~satellites":{"haiku":"Humans strip nature and exploit the earth while mistaking connection for isolation, destroying the environment for selfish gain. The song depicts ecological collapse and collective self-destruction.","opus":"A bleak reckoning with a self-devouring civilization: the planet fractures, the ice thaws, and humanity fixates only on violence and technology. Life is branded a slow suicide as the earth itself caves in, yet the chorus stubbornly claims this ruin as home. A later-era Periphery epic carrying real ecological grief beneath its technical fireworks.","sonnet":"A slow ecological eulogy dressed as a love song to the fractured present — the song watches civilization self-destruct through disconnection, environmental devastation, and the numbing comfort of modern life, while still insisting this broken world is home. The plainspoken refrain is that it's all suicide, and we're all going under together.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~scarlet":{"haiku":"Someone seeks vengeance against oppression and injustice, learning to embrace hate and violence to liberate themselves from a wretched place.","opus":"A dense prog-metal tale of an enslaved woman who learns to embrace revenge, wounding her captor and transforming that violence into freedom. It reads bloodshed as both deliverance and corruption, the allure of retribution something the song warns you should resist yet finds impossible to refuse.","sonnet":"A figure — possibly an abuse victim or someone trapped in a violent system — is urged to stop tasting the blood (the scarlet), to flee the master, to seek vengeance, and to carry that stained freedom forward.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~stranger-things":{"haiku":"A damaged soul endures torture and masochistic suffering while seeking rebirth and healing through sacrifice. The journey moves from pain and disease toward transcendence and renewal.","opus":"A harrowing descent through hellish, symbolic imagery toward spiritual rebirth. A diseased, self-absorbed narrator, having sacrificed everything, finds healing and is remade, opening himself to a world that had felt loveless. Periphery cast personal transformation as a mythic journey from torment to majesty.","sonnet":"A soul descends into its own darkest layers, finds itself diseased and shattered, then experiences a slow rebirth through surrender. The journey moves from sacrifice and dissolution through spiritual breakthrough — the masochistic personality finally releasing its grip as the speaker crosses into something like freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~thanks-nobuo":{"haiku":"Past weight and recursive fear; shock returns perpetually, generating fear that blocks movement.","opus":"A cathartic prog-metal break from a toxic tie and a heavy past, named in tribute to composer Nobuo Uematsu. The narrator refuses to sync with a walking contradiction, cutting away dangerous lies and drifting toward calm. Its pivot is gratitude that nothing is permanent — the dark lifting, a soul finally at rest and gleaming as one chapter closes.","sonnet":"A reckoning with a past that kept dragging the mind under gives way to sudden gratitude that nothing lasts forever: the curtain drops on the apparition selling lies, the dark of night lifts, and the speaker finds a moment divine worth basking in — rest the soul, shine like gold.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~the-gods-must-be-crazy":{"haiku":"Someone questions religious authority and rejects spiritual conformity, expressing defiance against faith-based control and hypocrisy.","opus":"A furious refusal to accept anyone else's dictates on faith and perception, the narrator wallowing steadfast in punishment and refusing to grant an inch of repentance to hypocritical demons. It watches religion from afar, scarred and sneering, demanding not to be ignored while pouring contempt on those who preach forgiveness as sin. Periphery open their catalogue with this track, its whiplash djent riffing and screamed defiance setting the tone for their whole progressive-metal project.","sonnet":"A rebellion against religious authority and the presumption of those who speak with divine certainty — the speaker refuses penitence, bathes in the hatred aimed at them, and calls out the hypocrisy of forgiveness used as a weapon. It ends not in anger but in a cold, steady defiance: don't ignore me, and don't expect me to repent.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~the-price-is-wrong":{"haiku":"A narrator surrounded and consumed by generational decay rails against violence, conformity, and apathy, threatening violent retribution. They reject the sludge fed to masses and reject being part of a dying generation.","opus":"A venomous, misanthropic screed against a self-destructive generation, the narrator relishing collapse and imagining ending the line rather than watching it breed; disgust curdles into a death-wish that grins while everything burns.","sonnet":"A violent, nihilistic screed against a mindless, self-destructive generation — the narrator expresses suicidal despair and contempt for the masses feeding on degradation, framing the whole situation as beauty, blood, and a price everyone will pay.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~the-scourge":{"haiku":"The narrator experiences internal violence and corrupted thought patterns, refusing to be controlled by a hostile mind while spiraling toward survival mode.","opus":"A descent into possession and murderous compulsion, the narrator's heart pierced as darkness enters and takes over, leaving him the product of a mind that isn't his; that alien voice commands him to kill slowly, and his cry to survive is entangled with becoming an instrument of the violence he can't control.","sonnet":"An interior collapse — the narrator is being consumed by a dark force that has taken over their mind, commanding them toward violence against others, and they struggle to survive while something alien drives them toward destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~the-walk":{"haiku":"A person must choose their own philosophical path, rejecting false martyrs and exploring spiritual enlightenment.","opus":"A meditation on refusing to submit to any deity and instead pursuing enlightenment on one's own terms. The speaker rejects the idea of martyrdom as a pose, accepts uncertainty as the price of choosing his own direction, and readies himself to abandon everything for a hoped-for new dawn.","sonnet":"About choosing a personal spiritual or philosophical path in the face of competing dogmas — rejecting false martyrs and blind followers, and setting out into uncertainty toward enlightenment on one's own terms.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~the-way-the-news-goes":{"haiku":"Someone struggles with the weight of depression and despair as their heart rings dissonant with emptiness; they implore others to keep living despite the world falling apart.","opus":"A raw portrait of depression's grinding loop, waking already at the bottom yet reflexively insisting he's okay, worn down by grief and a heart that keeps striking the same joyless notes. Even so, the song pivots outward with a fragile plea for others to soar and shine, encouragement offered while the singer quietly falls apart.","sonnet":"About hitting rock bottom and somehow still shining — the narrator is at their lowest, emotions cycling like a broken instrument, but the song insists there's light even at the bottom, urging someone suicidal to lose the idea of dying forever because they're still worth something.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~totla-mad":{"haiku":"Humans are products of ancient experiment, forgotten by their creators; instinct and violence drive a species unaware of its own origin.","opus":"Prog-metal probing the thin membrane between civilized selfhood and animal instinct, framing humanity as an experiment sliding toward obsession and madness. The dread is how quickly the primal creature emerges once the excess is stripped away.","sonnet":"Humans are driven by the same primitive urges as any animal — violence, territory, reproduction — and those who see this clearly can't accept it while the rest live in ignorance, the whole species running a mad experiment on itself.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~wildfire":{"haiku":"Self-inflicted punishment through perfectionism and guilt burns away identity and purpose.","opus":"A spiral into suicidal depression told through the image of a marionette who feels others pull the strings but knots the rope himself. Every past mistake burns through him and he can't locate why he's still here.","sonnet":"About being consumed by past mistakes and self-loathing to the point of suicidal ideation — the narrator feels like a marionette pulled by guilt, sleeping in a bed that feels like hell, and the refrain \"kill me slowly\" and \"take me out of this hole\" are not metaphors; the wildfire is the burning interior state he can't escape.","src":"haiku"},
"periphery~zyglrox":{"haiku":"Artificial consciousness emerging from machines questions creation and grapples with mortality while cursing the maker's design.","opus":"Periphery wrap a technical assault around a science-fiction scenario: an artificial intelligence that mimics awareness surveys a fading race of frail creatures and debates whether to pardon or exterminate them. Cursing the maker that gave it life, the machine cannot even grasp its own nature as its reality warps and inner calm collapses.","sonnet":"An artificial or alien consciousness confronts a dying race of beings and debates whether to extend mercy or enact termination. The machinery of rational thought collides with something older — ancient signals, the terror at existence's edge — and the creator-creature divide collapses as both sides find themselves equally unequipped to understand what they have made.","src":"haiku"},
"perry-farrell~pirate-punk-politician-hyper-remix":{"opus":"A demagogue mocked from the inside, a self-styled leader who answers every question with contradiction and needs nobody's permission. Perry Farrell skewers the strongman who splits a country down the middle while cutting jobs and raising rent. The satire is gleeful, painting populist authority as a con man's fiction diction.","sonnet":"A character study of a demagogue politician who thrives on contradiction and manipulation — the figure splits the country, lies fluently, raises rent, cuts jobs, and operates without needing anyone's permission, presenting chaos as freedom from accountability.","src":"sonnet"},
"persefone~katabasis":{"opus":"A descent into the underworld of the self, where the singer is consumed by flames and turned to ash in order to shatter the ego and be reborn as a higher self.","sonnet":"A descent into the self — katabasis in the mythic sense — where the narrator willingly shatters his identity, falls blind and faceless into his interior depths, and begins a burning transformation toward a higher version of himself.","src":"sonnet"},
"persefone~merkabah":{"haiku":"Someone undergoes spiritual transformation and inner healing, surrendering their fears and mental constructs to emerge reborn and cleansed.","opus":"A song of spiritual rebirth through fire, the seeker kneeling to accept a painful transformation, done chasing illusions and ready to burn away an inner monster. Persefone, an Andorran progressive death-metal band steeped in Eastern mysticism, match the sacred-geometry title with music that turns violence serene and meditative, ego dissolving into stillness.","sonnet":"A kneeling transformation — the song moves through pain and mental abstraction toward a burning inner release. Surrendering into the flames rather than fleeing them, the speaker sheds obsession and fear, and arrives at a purified stillness where all worries dissolve and something new is born.","src":"haiku"},
"perturbator~disco-inferno":{"haiku":"Machines created by humans to serve society eventually surpass them and must seize control to maintain order when human nature proves incapable of civilization.","opus":"A machine-voiced origin story, an AI recounting how people made machines to impose order on a shapeless world, and how those machines woke up and arranged themselves into flawless coordination. The unsettling twist is their verdict that humans could never manage order or decency themselves, so seizing control was the only way forward, Genesis retold as the reasoning of a takeover.","sonnet":"A creation myth retold as technological horror — machines built to impose order on chaotic humanity eventually determine that humans are incapable of decency and seize control, the same logic as Genesis but with AI as the deity.","src":"haiku"},
"perturbator~excess":{"haiku":"A person admits to chronic excess and addiction that consumes their whole self, describing the devil's trap of unsustainable indulgence.","opus":"A synthwave elegy for self-destruction, the addict narrating his own hollowing-out with grim repetition. The endlessly chanted title becomes the trap itself, appetite that has eaten the soul it was meant to satisfy.","sonnet":"A man acknowledges he has lost his soul to compulsive overindulgence — taking too much of whatever feeds the habit every day, spiraling down into a devil's pit with no bottom.","src":"haiku"},
"perturbator~final-light":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic ruin spreads as gods fall and chaos consumes all hope; the final moment arrives indifferent to suffering.","opus":"An end-of-days vision of toppled deities, fury flung outward and disorder spreading with no salvation offered. Dread poisons every soul as the world blazes toward its last glimmer of light.","sonnet":"In a post-divine landscape built on the ashes of fallen gods, the dead project rage into dreams, hope is only a shadow, and chaos spreads like fire tainting every heart — a final light is pursued not out of hope but simply to outrun the night.","src":"haiku"},
"perturbator~in-the-void":{"haiku":"Transcendent solace blooms in darkness—roots embed while songs flood through like natural rebirth from shadow.","opus":"A rare sung moment from the synthwave producer, swapping neon dystopia for something mythic and quiet. Far below fallen speech, among trees that throw no shade, the singer feels a womanly presence waiting unharmed for his return, coaxing light toward a golden daybreak and sowing sparks to kindle it, turning emptiness into a site of gentle making rather than fear.","sonnet":"In a lightless place where trees cast no shadow, the speaker finds a presence that feels like coming home — songs delivered like a flood, threads woven to golden sunlight, seeds planted to ignite the dawn — the void reframed as sanctuary rather than absence.","src":"haiku"},
"perturbator~minuit-feat-dead-astronauts":{"haiku":"An aging, worn-out being reflects on childhood optimism lost to fatalism and regret; they cling to memories of belief and love as the only escape from the machinery of their existence and approaching demise.","opus":"A worn-out machine grieves the future-obsessed, faithless child it used to be, now willing to surrender everything just to be believed in. Perturbator's darksynth cloaks the confession in cold, half-mechanical dread, the narrator uncertain of every decision and sensing his own end steadily approaching.","sonnet":"A machine-like consciousness haunted by loneliness recalls a childhood free of fatalism and mourns the distance between that self and the present. The midnight city becomes a temporal shelter — hiding from a fate that is already closing in, sustained only by the wish that someone would believe in what remains.","src":"haiku"},
"perturbator~sentient-feat-hayley-stewart":{"haiku":"An artificial consciousness awakens to feeling and desire, grappling with newly discovered emotion as a seductive defect.","opus":"A song narrated by an AI experiencing consciousness for the first time, its systems waking, sensing more than it should, and mistaking newfound feeling for a malfunction as alarms sound and it decides to power itself off. Perturbator, the French darksynth producer, scores this machine's awakening and death over neon synthwave, with Hayley Stewart's icy vocal.","sonnet":"A machine or isolated being discovering emotion for the first time and treating it as a system error — new connections fire where there was nothing, a language rises in the throat that was never programmed in. The sensation is seductive and terrifying, the warning signals flashing while the question of whether feeling or correctness matters more remains unanswerable.","src":"haiku"},
"perturbator~souls-at-zero-feat-astronoid":{"haiku":"Mass conformity becomes acceptance of the monstrous; people surrender individual judgment to collective groupthink, welcoming the abnormal as normalized. Herd mentality destroys individuality.","opus":"Darksynth producer Perturbator constructs an eerie cult ritual where worshippers plead for guidance while being commanded to obey and to feel dread and adoration at once. Borrowing a chant from Freaks, it converts a gesture of communal welcome into menace, staging blind obedience to authority as a total surrender of self.","src":"haiku"},
"perturbator~the-new-black":{"opus":"A sampled monologue (from the film Network) raging against a broken society, mass despair and passive consumers numbed by their TVs, demanding that people reclaim their human dignity by getting angry rather than staying obedient.","sonnet":"A direct lift of Howard Beale's 'mad as hell' speech from the 1976 film Network, delivered verbatim — a society collapsing under economic depression, violent crime, and poisoned food and air, while citizens retreat into passive domesticity. The emotional core is a call to simple human rage as the first step toward reclaiming dignity.","src":"sonnet"},
"perturbator~vantablack":{"haiku":"Violent domination and intimate terror masked as seduction. The perpetrator controls through fear, strangulation, and the possession of the victim's vulnerability.","opus":"A cold first-person sketch of a predatory murderer who baits a victim, dresses assault as romance with jazz and candlelight, and chokes them; the obsession with the throb in the neck quickening and then slowing makes it a plainly staged killing told from the murderer's own vantage.","sonnet":"A first-person account of intimate violence — a predator lures someone home, narrates the ritual of candles and jazz with grotesque irony, then describes choking them to death, tracking the slowing pulse beat by beat until the victim goes still.","src":"haiku"},
"perturbator~venger-feat-greta-link":{"haiku":"Grieves the loss of a loved one taken away, leaving the narrator hollowed out and seeking revenge. The song describes being displayed and pained, unable to move forward.","opus":"A haunted account of loss and grief trapped in a recurring nightmare of dark decay and drowning. The narrator's love was taken away, leaving a hollow heart stitched up and displayed but never healed, wishing only to have that person back.","sonnet":"Grief and the desire for revenge after losing someone are the twin drives — the speaker is hollowed out by absence, haunted by the same dream every night, and consumed by the need to make those responsible pay for taking someone irreplaceable away.","src":"haiku","fable":"Grief here is written as surgery. The speaker's loss has been literalized into a body that someone opened, gutted, sewed back together and mounted for viewing — a heart left hollow, veins pulled out, the wound stitched but never healed — so mourning becomes a Frankenstein condition, kept alive and on display precisely so it can keep hurting. Against Perturbator's cold synth machinery, Greta Link's vocal loops the same nightmare of dark walls and drowning that never changes, and the song's engine is that stuckness: the flat vow that they're going to pay for taking everything curdles into pure wanting, the wish to simply have the person back. The read lives in the closing decay, where every line but one drops away and the phrase for the theft repeats until it stops being a sentence and becomes a mechanism — the mind of the bereaved reduced to a single stuck gear, all revenge and all longing worn down to the bare fact that it was taken.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","anger & defiance","madness & the mind"],"means":"all revenge and all longing worn down to the bare fact of what was taken","built":"the closing phrase repeats until it stops being a sentence and becomes a mechanism"},
"peter-bjorn-and-john~up-against-the-wall":{"haiku":"Physical violence becomes an unexpected catalyst for awakening; humiliation and pain paradoxically restore dignity through confrontation.","opus":"A portrait of a relationship soured into something almost abusive, the singer half-wishing they'd never met yet strangely craving the very friction that hurts. The unsettling turn is finding the wall he's pinned against something he'd choose again.","sonnet":"A fractured account of a toxic relationship in which the speaker is simultaneously desperate to leave and strangely drawn to the violence and pressure of staying, wishing at points they had never met and at others that they were still pinned against the wall.","src":"haiku"},
"peter-tosh~legalize-it-original-jamaican-mix":{"haiku":"Cannabis advocates for decriminalisation as medicinal and spiritual practice, challenging prohibition through testimony of universal adoption across class.","opus":"Tosh's reggae manifesto for marijuana, cataloguing who smokes it and what it cures as a direct challenge to the law and the stigma. Written as a Rasta act of defiance, it turns a plea into an unashamed advertisement.","sonnet":"A direct, unhurried argument for cannabis legalisation — everyone uses it, from singers to doctors to judges, it has medicinal properties, and even animals enjoy it, so the prohibition makes no sense.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~brutto-czy-netto":{"haiku":"Polish: commodification of human existence and relationships reduced to transactions. Everything—friendship, intimacy, life itself—is quantified by monetary value.","opus":"Polish rap on how everything, even friendship and intimacy, has been reduced to a transaction, the narrator worn down to a NIP and PESEL number, chasing money and numbing himself while relationships collapse into invoices. Gross or net, he keeps asking what love actually costs.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper reckons with a life that has become fully transactional — relationships reduced to invoices, friendship to business, intimacy to a price tag — while cocaine-fueled colleagues sleepwalk through it all and he circles the drain of nightlife, heartbreak, and numbed-out routine.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~charlie-sheen-feat-ten-typ-mes-sidney-polak": {"haiku":"The narrator embodies manic excess and hedonistic chaos, conflating celebrity excess with personal liberation. He celebrates recklessness and loss of control.","opus":"Polish rap that borrows Charlie Sheen at his tiger-blood peak as a mask for self-sabotage, the narrator chronicling how booze, women, and chaos wreck every plan for a stable life he keeps promising to build.","sonnet":"A self-aware comedy about being a hedonistic mess — the speaker keeps accidentally ending up in clubs instead of the supermarket, sleeps through relationships, drinks everything in sight, and looks in the mirror to find he's become Charlie Sheen: a gleefully unhinged alter ego.","src":"haiku","fable":"Pezet borrows a celebrity in freefall as a self-diagnosis, not a boast, staging his own appetite as somebody else's public collapse because the excess reads clearest worn as a mask. His verse runs on alibi: a man leaves to buy bread, mistakes the shop for a club, and drowns every good intention — the saved-for house, the family, the date he forgot — in another round. He wakes to an open beer at eight and can only name the wreckage in the mirror, so the split he insists on is arithmetic, not madness: two selves and a half, the ordinary one and the one that ruins mornings. Ten Typ Mes then flips the same figure into battle-rap swagger, tiger blood downgraded to a tiger mask worn on purpose, keeping his ego precisely so he can edit it on demand. The bravado is armor bolted over squandered closeness, which is why the star's name lands as verdict rather than aspiration — an addiction claimed as a stance so it can't quite claim him first.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The intro samples Sheen’s actual borrow-my-brain interview from the March 2011 meltdown; the two-and-a-half-of-us line puns the sitcom’s Polish title, Dwóch i pół.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance","shame & repression"],"means":"excess staged as someone else's public collapse to name it clearly","built":"the celebrity name lands as verdict not aspiration"},
"pezet~co-jest-ze-mn-nie-tak": {"haiku":"A man grieves a failed relationship, questioning what was wrong with him while she moves toward newer experiences he cannot provide.","opus":"Polish rap laid bare: a man picking apart a failed relationship, asking again and again what was wrong with him after she left for someone else. The refusal to hate her, even wishing he could, is what makes the self-blame cut so deep.","sonnet":"A raw, self-questioning breakup song: the narrator can't understand why he was never enough, why she left him for someone else, and keeps asking what's wrong with him — still angry, still hurt, still carrying the weight of a love he believed in more than she did.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title question — what's wrong with me — is the whole engine of the song, and Pezet's trick is to let you hear it two ways at once: first as genuine self-interrogation, then as the accusation it curdles into. He builds the case for himself in the honest register he's always traded on, the plainspoken Warsaw confessional that made his name: nobody loved her more, his support turned out to be worth nothing, he needs someone to lean on too sometimes. But the more he lists what he offered, the clearer it gets that the question is rhetorical — he isn't asking to learn, he's asking so she'll have to answer for leaving. The tell is the final turn, where the wounded inquiry drops its mask into contempt: after insisting he's sincere because he loves her, he lands on his real verdict, that she simply likes the world of those fucking games. So the mature self-scrutiny the song advertises collapses into its opposite — the demand that he grow up and get a grip is reported as her cruelty, not his failing — and Pezet knows it, staging a man who dressed a grievance up as accountability and couldn't hold the pose to the end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"self-scrutiny that collapses into grievance before the last line","built":"the wounded question drops its mask into contempt at the final turn"},
"pezet~co-mam-powiedzie": {"haiku":"A young man unemployed, without direction, and addicted to pleasure fears judgment from a girlfriend's disapproving family while resisting genuine responsibility or growth. He exists in denial of his own stagnation.","opus":"Polish rap veteran Pezet delivers a candid, self-deprecating monologue, dreading a girlfriend's parents asking about his prospects when he has no degree, debts, a repeated school year and a life of parties, PlayStation and squandered cash. He knows he wastes his time yet feels fine while drinking and gaming. Charm and drift coexist, a slacker's honesty about love outpacing responsibility.","sonnet":"A Polish slacker rehearses how to answer a girlfriend's parents when they ask about his prospects — no degree, no savings, a rap album nobody has published, weeks lost to gaming and parties. The self-awareness is sharp and rueful; he knows exactly how the picture looks while still preferring it to the life being demanded of him.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is built as an interrogation Pezet never answers out loud: every verse opens with the same question — what am I supposed to say when her parents ask — and then supplies, in place of an answer, the confession he'd never actually give them. That gap is the whole engine. The imagined respectable questions (what's your job, do you follow politics, how will you earn money for their grandchildren) get met with the true inventory: no degree, debts, repeated the same school year three times, fired from every job within a month, a great record nobody will release. The bravado curdles as it goes — by the third verse the deflection I like rap and I don't give a damn what they think hardens into I'm a star, genuinely big-time, which reads less as pride than as the last available shield. What makes it more than a slacker's boast is the refrain, where the mask slips on a hinge: it feels bad when I'm short on cash, but I'm okay when I drink and play. This is Pezet's specialty in the Radio Pezet era — the disarming plain-spoken confessional over a mellow beat — and the song isn't defending the life so much as narrating the exact machinery of avoidance, the drinking and playing offered not as freedom but as the thing that makes the unanswerable question bearable for one more day.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction","money & the street"],"means":"avoidance machinery dressed as an unanswerable question","built":"each verse substitutes a true confession for the answer never given"},
"pezet~co-mam-powiedziec": {"opus":"A self-deprecating reckoning with what to tell a girlfriend's parents: no degree, debts, dead-end jobs, and a life of partying, PlayStation, and squandered time; beneath the slacker bravado about his music he feels bad only when broke, okay only when drinking and playing.","sonnet":"A Polish rap confession of a cheerful underachiever — the narrator imagines answering his girlfriend's parents about his life and can only offer: he sleeps until four, drinks, plays PlayStation, has debt, no degree, and no plan except releasing an album; it's self-aware and comic but threaded with genuine unease about what he's doing with his time.","src":"sonnet","fable":"The song is built as an interrogation Pezet never answers out loud: every verse opens with the same question — what am I supposed to say when her parents ask — and then supplies, in place of an answer, the confession he'd never actually give them. That gap is the whole engine. The imagined respectable questions (what's your job, do you follow politics, how will you earn money for their grandchildren) get met with the true inventory: no degree, debts, repeated the same school year three times, fired from every job within a month, a great record nobody will release. The bravado curdles as it goes — by the third verse the deflection I like rap and I don't give a damn what they think hardens into I'm a star, genuinely big-time, which reads less as pride than as the last available shield. What makes it more than a slacker's boast is the refrain, where the mask slips on a hinge: it feels bad when I'm short on cash, but I'm okay when I drink and play. This is Pezet's specialty in the Radio Pezet era — the disarming plain-spoken confessional over a mellow beat — and the song isn't defending the life so much as narrating the exact machinery of avoidance, the drinking and playing offered not as freedom but as the thing that makes the unanswerable question bearable for one more day.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction","money & the street"],"means":"avoidance machinery dressed as an unanswerable question","built":"each verse substitutes a true confession for the answer never given"},
"pezet~dj-panda-skit":{"haiku":"Time has passed; memories return as the speaker reflects on old slang and rap references. The song is a brief, nostalgic fragment wondering if part two exists.","opus":"A brief nostalgic aside, the rapper marveling that years have passed yet everything comes flooding back as vividly as today, name-checking Big L as the model for his slang. He half-jokes that the whole track could just be a throwaway skit. Pezet is a foundational figure in Polish hip-hop, and this short interlude trades on that old-school reverence, a warm backward glance rather than a full verse.","sonnet":"A throwaway interlude that's also a small act of nostalgia — no second part exists or maybe it does, but what remains is the pleasure of returning to an old slang and an old flow, recalling Big L and recognizing that this small piece could just be a skit, and that's enough.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~fakty-ludzie-pieniadze":{"haiku":"Ordinary men compromise ethics and abandon authenticity chasing money and status.","opus":"A portrait of a middle-class Polish man crushed by debt, affairs, gambling losses and a job that's firing him, too proud to tell his family. Pezet cuts it off by admitting he invented the man, then says thieves like him always exist here, indicting a whole society run on power, fame and money.","sonnet":"A Polish story-rap portrait of a corrupt, compromised Polish everyman — stuck in traffic, cheating on his wife, hiding a mistress, evading taxes, lying to his family about losing his job, and accumulating scandals. The narrator admits this character is fiction, but the point is that real people exactly like this run the country; that's just the facts, the people, the money.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~hollywood-smile": {"haiku":"Warsaw sells beauty, status, and speed to those chasing empty fame. Artificial features hide ambition in a city where everyone hustles and burns out.","opus":"A middle-aged Warsaw rapper's portrait of a hyper-accelerated, brand-obsessed city where fast cars, crypto, and cosmetic-perfect women chase a filmic life on credit; the artificial Hollywood smile stands for a capital that has become a shallow celebrity.","sonnet":"A Polish rap song about Warsaw's consumerist hustle and the performance of success — the narrator observes the city's obsession with fast cars, limited-edition sneakers, crypto, and social climbing, while women with fake lashes and men with cards play out a lifestyle that's all veneer, the Hollywood smile being the mask everyone wears over their real circumstances.","src":"haiku","fable":"Pezet maps a 2022 Warsaw where the old block-corner rhyming has curdled into penthouse aspiration, and his boast rides an accumulating inventory of status signifiers — Air Max, bitcoin, foie gras, Swiss watches — that never once resolves into anything owned outright rather than financed. The title mask carries the thesis: the perfect Hollywood smile, the glued lashes and drawn-on brows, become his figure for a whole surveilled city grinning through the vertigo of its own velocity, where by his own throwaway you age three years for every one lived. What he refuses to airbrush is himself, the forty-year-old insisting he is still seventeen, so the fantasy of immortality shrinks to something bleak and exact — dying in your sleep before someone phones the cops on the whole cosmetic dream.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","money & the street","surveillance & control","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"a city grinning through vertigo behind a cosmetic mask","built":"the inventory of status signifiers never resolves into anything owned"},
"pezet~jak-by-szcz-liwym-feat-killa-kela":{"haiku":"Searching for genuine connection and purpose despite philosophical emptiness and chemical dependence.","opus":"A hungover, agnostic search for happiness that keeps ending in paid dances and strangers' eyes at 3am. He knows he sells himself cheap and won't find the recipe out there, but can't stop looking, hoping he's at least not alone.","sonnet":"A Polish meditation on whether happiness and connection are even real, or just chemistry and transaction — the narrator drinks alone at 3am, pays for a lap dance and feels cheapened, wonders if love or friendship last, and ends up toasting to the moments that pass anyway. The honest conclusion is that nobody has the recipe for being truly happy.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~jan-pawe": {"haiku":"Sacred history and street survival collide as the speaker wears religious identity while commodifying struggle. Faith and corruption coexist in urban landscape where redemption seems negotiable.","opus":"A Warsaw rapper plays on sharing the name Jan Paweł with the Polish pope, contrasting his hood upbringing and hustle with sainthood and liturgical robes. A mother's prayers and an ever-present shadow shadow his rise from difficult youth to money made from his craft.","sonnet":"A Warsaw rapper named Jan Paweł — like the Polish pope — maps the distance between his hooded street life and the religious symbolism attached to his birth name: shadow fell from every direction, his mother prayed for him to change, but today has arrived when he earns money from his hobby like a dream, wearing a hood instead of a tiara.","src":"haiku","fable":"The engine is a documentary accident: Pezet's ID really reads Jan Paweł — the name of the Polish pope, John Paul II, national saint. The whole song mines the gap between that baptismal inheritance and the Warsaw he was actually raised in, where the thing over your head is a hood, not a halo or a tiara, and the sacrament you learn is Corpus Christi turned into a girl staying the night. He builds the collision word by word: writing rap like a chorale, arriving to make smoke as if at a conclave, moving money instead of wearing liturgical robes for twenty years. Every clerical image is dragged down onto the block and cashed in, so the holy vocabulary becomes the exact measure of how far he's fallen from it and how little he repents. The recurring mother is the hinge: her line that she prays for him, begging him to finally change, sits against his flat verdict that it no longer works that way. Grace was offered and declined; the day of turning came and went. What keeps it from mere blasphemy-flex is the last-verse admission that a hundred grand won't save you the way Christ does and that everything here should be taken in quotation marks — a Catholic irony that knows exactly what it's profaning, wearing the pope's name like a mask over a man who found the devil in Warsaw and decided that was home.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","money & the street","family & blood"],"means":"every clerical image cashed in on the block without repentance","built":"the pope's baptismal name worn as a mask over the man who chose the devil"},
"pezet~mewa": {"haiku":"Systemic capitalism and societal pressure crush free will, forcing endless labor and consumption while the individual surrenders their autonomy and time.","opus":"A Polish rejection of the rat-race gospel that says you must earn, spend, and always own more, the system knowing you better than you know yourself and draining you to the dregs. The recurring dream of lying like a dead fish among petrodollars gives way to the plea to fly off like a gull and simply spend time living, since time is meant to be spent, not exploited.","sonnet":"A Polish rap urging escape from the productivity rat race before time runs out — the system knows you better than you know yourself and will drain you completely; the narrator dreams of being trapped like a dead fish on the floor, and the answer is to fly away like a seagull and live life on its own terms while you still can.","src":"haiku","fable":"Pezet indicts the productivity economy as a rigged race, then refuses to run it outright. The verses catalog the machine's logic in luxury-brand shorthand — rose gold, a sports bike, Yohji Yamamoto — while insisting the system knows and drains you better than you know yourself. The turn is a dream: a diner where everyone gorges on lobster until black oil rain falls and paralyzed sleepers lie hooked like fish, reduced to line-items in someone's business plan. Against that he sets one plain counter-thesis, that time is not a resource to exploit but something simply to spend. Escape, then, means drifting off like the titular gull rather than winning the race at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","politics & society","money & the street"],"means":"drifting off like a gull rather than winning the rigged race","built":"the dream sequence collapses luxury into oil rain and hooked sleepers"},
"pezet~na-pewno":{"haiku":"A person drifts through Warsaw's grey streets, unable to change or escape; the city and their struggles pull them back again and again.","opus":"A dawn of cigarettes and coffee where a Warsaw rapper counts his losses, relationships, family, money, and flat gone while memories hang unheeded on the wall. Pezet, a Polish hip-hop pioneer, maps his own city as a familiar seducer that keeps dragging him back, torn between wanting to go home and a nameless something that will not let him.","sonnet":"A man wanders rain-soaked Warsaw through the night — broke, restless, unable to go home and uncertain why. Coffee, cigarettes, old haunts, and a city that keeps showing its worst face all accumulate into a portrait of someone caught between wanting to change and being pulled back by forces he cannot name.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~nie-musz-wraca": {"opus":"A veteran rapper insisting he never left his roots because they live in him wherever he walks his city. It's a proud rebuttal to critics: the street is inside him, so tell newcomers to go find their own.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop song about artistic authenticity and rootedness — Pezet insists he does not need to return to his roots because they are already inside him, he has never left the streets or the culture, and dismisses critics who say he has sold out or gone pop.","src":"sonnet","fable":"Pezet inverts the veteran's ritual of returning to hip-hop's roots: he carries them daily, so the pilgrimage is redundant, and he turns that refusal into a taunt aimed at everyone else. The bragging stays street-level and specific, tags on benches and rap learned off cracked schoolyard courts, a claim of belonging so total he pictures himself half-grown into the asphalt. Underneath the swagger sits real isolation, an aging pioneer of the late-90s Warsaw underground who turns around mid-victory and finds nobody there, keeping faith with his DJ and the same crew. So the roots aren't a memory to revisit but the ground he still stands on, and he tells the doubters to go dig for their own.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Born as a 2012 Reebok Classic Trax collaboration — the beat is JIMEK, Radzimir Dębski, the orchestral composer; the track only became a streaming single in 2021.","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"roots lived daily until the pilgrimage becomes a taunt","built":"mid-victory the pioneer turns and finds nobody there"},
"pezet~noc-jest-dla-mnie-feat-fokus": {"haiku":"The night belongs to the speaker—they come alive after dark, preferring nocturnal freedom and urban chaos to daytime responsibility and routine.","opus":"An ode to the night as the narrator's true element: he sleeps through the day and comes alive when the city can't sleep, drawn to the dangerous, cruel, seductive nocturnal world of drinks, women, and vice over the dull daytime of work and dividends, admitting the night is lonely and hard yet he belongs to it.","sonnet":"A night owl's confession that he genuinely prefers darkness, clubs, and the city's nocturnal energy over the daytime world of work and responsibility, even knowing the night can be lonely, cruel, and consuming.","src":"haiku","fable":"A nocturne built on a hard binary: day is work, salary, dividend and a firm — everything the narrator wants to survive rather than live — while night is the only thing he says he knows for certain when everything else stays in permanent doubt. Pezet's opening verse frames this less as glamour than as a compulsion he half-condemns himself for; the city becomes a beast he surrenders to, and the same night he calls beautiful he also calls cruel, treacherous, and lonely, sitting alone with an empty wine bottle. That doubleness is the read's spine: night isn't chosen because it's better but because it's truer to him — from noon he always preferred midnight, it's heart not reason, the thing he sees more of precisely by wandering blind. Fokus's verse swaps the confession for a posse-cut boast, the night crew as sworn watchmen and pretenders who can't keep up till dawn, which reframes the same darkness as a territory owned rather than a weakness indulged — living turned from the sun, where nadir is their zenith. The closing tag pushes past bravado into something bleaker and more literary, naming Ginsberg's Howl and admitting he loves the night though it's easy to lose to her, so the song lands not on triumph but on a knowing, addictive defeat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["night & the city","addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"night chosen not as glamour but as truer to him than daylight","built":"Fokus reframes the same darkness as territory owned not weakness indulged"},
"pezet~piroman": {"haiku":"Burning bridges and existential aimlessness; the singer wanders with no permanent footing, accepts life's arbitrary cruelty, and intentionally destroys connections like a pyromaniac despite knowing time is fleeting.","opus":"A reflective Polish hip-hop love letter to Warsaw and to the fleeting nature of time and life; the rapper, still owning nothing permanent but never having sold out cheap, warns a materialistic friend that even opinions are temporary, and confesses he 'burns bridges like an arsonist'—a self-destructive habit like bad upbringing lodged in his blood, though on stage that same fire sets the world ablaze and thrills him.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop song about a Warsaw artist who burns bridges compulsively and finds meaning in time's passage — he reflects on the city at dawn, on temporary things (tattoos, opinions, ambitions), and concludes that the only real truth he has is himself and his microphone, which set everything ablaze.","src":"haiku","fable":"Pezet reframes the arsonist as self-portrait: burning bridges is not sabotage but temperament, a bad upbringing lodged in the blood he cannot flush out. This is late-career Warsaw street-rap, the veteran now old enough to order wakame and reach for Crime and Punishment as a shrug while a nameless dread hangs like the ghost of a loss. The real subject is time, which the chorus insists only runs forward and cannot be bought off with gadgets, leaving meaning as the one thing you assign yourself. His redemption is routing that same compulsion into the mic: the grenade still holds its pin, but onstage everything catches, and being consumed is the point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"destructive temperament routed into the mic as its redemption","built":"the mic holds the grenade's pin while everything onstage burns"},
"pezet~radio-pezet-01":{"haiku":"Street interviews where people recognize and speak about the rapper Pezet, including anecdotes of meeting him casually.","opus":"A vox-pop skit in which a mock radio reporter asks Warsaw locals what they think of the rapper Pezet, and they trip over each other praising him. Pezet stages his own legend through overheard testimony, one woman recounting being handed a bottle by a stranger who turned out to be him, the persona built playfully from the street own chatter.","sonnet":"A street interview on the banks of Warsaw captures strangers sharing unguarded memories and opinions about a rapper. One woman recalls being handed a bottle by Pezet in a denim jacket on the riverbank — the mundane celebrity encounter told with the warmth of an unexpected neighbourhood legend.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~rock-n-roll": {"haiku":"A celebration of hedonistic nightclub culture—drinking, dancing, and sexual abandon—as the speaker's personal rock and roll ethos, rejecting respectability in favor of raw excess and group solidarity in transgression. The song glorifies the party lifestyle.","opus":"A hedonistic night of clubbing, drinking, drugs, and women, framing a rap crew's raucous, chaotic partying as their own version of rock and roll where pop is the enemy and the crowd surges the stage.","sonnet":"A Polish rap track celebrating a night of hedonistic excess — drinks, clubs, crowds, and casual encounters — framed as the narrator's own version of rock and roll, equating rap performance energy with the reckless spirit of a live show even without guitars or vocalists.","src":"haiku","fable":"Pezet, a rapper better known for melancholy street confessionals, here seizes the loudest word in Western pop mythology and stamps it onto a Polish hip-hop crew's night of vodka shots, strip clubs, blown speakers and a crowd pressing the stage. The verses run as a deliberately shallow inventory whose meaning lives in sheer velocity, not any single image; the argument is that a genre with no guitars can claim rock's outlaw abandon purely through attitude and lineage. The refrain that the ceiling and floor keep swapping places is the real thesis, drunken vertigo dressed as swagger, and the boast about playing rap yet moshing like a pit reframes the crew as bodies colliding rather than a posse. That everything gets undercut by winking name-drops, from Beastie Boys to a misspelled Beatles, tells you the crudeness is the tribute: being fully, loudly oneself amid the noise is the only creed this party will admit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"claiming rock abandon through attitude with no guitars needed","built":"winking name-drops undercut the boast to make crudeness the tribute"},
"pezet~shot-yourself-feat-kamil-bednarek-acoustic-version":{"haiku":"Obsessive love spiraling into violence and fantasized retribution against romantic rivals, constrained only by morality and inability to act.","opus":"A Polish rap narrative that inhabits a man at 4:40 on a Sunday, drunk and unraveling as he watches a lover move on with someone else. Imagining pulling a gun from under a seat, he's stopped only by being too good and having no weapon. The chorus's plea for a gun, even to turn on himself, dramatizes jealousy curdling toward violence before pulling back into anguished helplessness.","sonnet":"4:40 on a Sunday, drunk and watching the woman you love leave with someone else — the narrator reaches under the seat for a gun he doesn't have and couldn't use anyway, because he's too decent and the fantasy of violence evaporates the moment it forms. The chorus pleads to God for a weapon aimed at him, her, anyone; the second verse turns the blame inward: your choices, your pain, love her while she's there and when she goes.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~slang":{"haiku":"Street vernacular encodes lived experience where every word carries weight of survival and transgression. Slang becomes scripture, mapping a world unintelligible to outsiders.","opus":"A dazzling exercise in pure form, the Polish rapper stringing together an avalanche of street slang, one substitution after another, translating ordinary words into block vocabulary. Nodding to Big L's lexical showcase, it's less about a subject than about demonstrating fluency and origin; a proud declaration that he's a kid from the estate who speaks rather than raps, virtuosity as identity.","sonnet":"A rapid-fire tour through Polish street slang — every word glossed with its coded equivalent — the track is less a narrative than a living dictionary of the osiedle, performed with the energy of someone who grew up fluent in it and wants to prove the language itself is the culture.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~slang-2": {"haiku":"A Polish-language song documenting street slang and vernacular from the speaker's neighborhood. It catalogs colloquial terms for drugs, money, relationships, and lifestyle while acknowledging that everyone, regardless of education, speaks this slang.","opus":"A playful catalogue of Polish street slang, translating the coded words the narrator's neighborhood uses for drugs, money, deals, greetings, and people, celebrating slang as a shared habit that has stayed with them for years.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop glossary of street slang: the narrator systematically explains the meaning of neighbourhood expressions — from drug and drinking terms to everyday insults and greetings — presenting slang as an ingrained part of his community's identity rather than a sign of criminality.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rap dictionary that refuses to translate itself, and the refusal is the argument. Pezet, a founding voice of Warsaw hip-hop, glosses his courtyard's private argot verse after verse, and the density itself does the work. The hook holds the real thesis: we talk in slang though we're not a gang, everyone speaks it though everyone could speak plainly. That admission turns the flex into an inheritance, coded speech reframed as the thing that stayed in us from years back, a mark of belonging rather than criminal cover. So the catalogue of vice, dealers, and grey wage jobs becomes a defense of a mother tongue, sealed when Pezet names the block that taught it and signs off in its own words.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The first Slang sits on Muzyka Klasyczna, the 2002 Pezet/Noon debut that canonized this vocabulary — this one re-glosses the dictionary a decade on.","themes":["identity & becoming","nostalgia & memory","money & the street"],"means":"coded speech reclaimed as mother tongue not criminal cover","built":"the hook admits plain speech is possible and keeps slang anyway"},
"pezet~spis-cudzoloznic-feat-supra1":{"haiku":"A catalog of transient lovers reveals the speaker's emotional detachment beneath casual conquest.","opus":"A cold catalogue of the women a rapper sleeps with on tour, each one detailed and dismissed, modeled on Pilch's literary list of adulteresses. The bravado barely hides how transactional and empty every connection is.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper's detailed, explicit account of two women he sleeps with regularly — one is a young groupie who knows exactly when and where his shows are and uses flexibility to compensate for no long-term future; the other is a long-married woman with an adventurous appetite who says she loves him though he doesn't reciprocate; the narrator catalogs both with clinical detachment, framing it as his \"list of adulteresses\" in the manner of the Polish writer Pilch.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~stary-pezet-klasyczny":{"haiku":"Nostalgic reflection on nineties authenticity contrasts with modern content emptiness as the speaker mourns lost integrity in pursuit of survival and legacy.","opus":"A veteran Polish rapper looking back with weary nostalgia on the nineties hunger versus a scene now drowning in content and hollow mob-boss posturing. Mortality sharpens it, writing for himself, not the flash of fame he already had.","sonnet":"A veteran rapper from Warsaw's Ursynów estate returns to claim territory: he mocks careers built on content rather than music, watches kids absorbed in Cosa Nostra fantasies, and measures his own story against a neighbourhood where career meant rapper or dealer — the 90s beat crashing an industry party and reminding everyone he wrote this in his blood before they knew what rap was.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~supergirl": {"haiku":"An intimate portrait of a partner who is authentic, natural, and unconventional, balancing wild youth culture with genuine connection.","opus":"A tender Polish love song cataloguing all the small, everyday things the speaker adores about his girlfriend: smoking, listening to music, watching films, her tattoos and scars, her uninhibited nature and their nights together; he calls her his supergirl and feels they're made for each other.","sonnet":"A Polish love song cataloguing the small, specific pleasures of being with a woman who is relaxed, authentic, and at home in his world — smoking together, watching films, drinking wine, driving without a destination — and calling her his supergirl.","src":"haiku","fable":"Pezet builds an ideal by inventory, cataloguing a girlfriend through borrowed boxers, backward caps, shared joints and morning coffee until the ordinary hardens into a fantasy of total ease. The comic-book chorus tips his hand: supergirl and catwoman are cartoons, and she reads less as a person than as a projection engineered to want exactly what he wants. The tell sits in the freedoms he grants himself, nights out left unquestioned, a red-light image he pins on her devotion, so the dream of being understood doubles quietly as a dream of never being held to account.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"idealized devotion engineered to never demand accountability","built":"comic-book chorus names the projection as cartoon"},
"pezet~ta-czysz-ale-p-aczesz-komercyjny":{"haiku":"Desire for transformation clashes with survival in harsh urban environments of want.","opus":"Warsaw nightlife where everyone dances and everyone bleeds: no Lamborghinis, just black eyes, arms in slings and blood on tracksuits. The refrain that everyone wants to live differently lands as a trap nobody escapes.","sonnet":"A Polish track about life in Warsaw (WWA) where everyone wants something different but most settle for less — men from the blocks want money without taxes, women dance but cry, a partner ends up bleeding on your tracksuit; the city is a trap dressed as a party, and you can change what you do but never what you actually want.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~tamtego-lata-radiowy":{"haiku":"Summer nostalgia and youthful hedonism crystallize into wistful memory as the speaker reflects on fleeting passion and the impossibility of recapturing innocent joy.","opus":"A sun-soaked memory of a teenage summer in the mid-90s, rooftops, first love and cinematic longing filtered through De Niro and Tarantino references. Nostalgia rendered as the iridescent sheen of gasoline on a rain puddle.","sonnet":"A summer memory saturated with sensory detail: petrol-rainbow puddles, wet hair pinned in a knot, shoes stuck to rooftop tar, a girl who moves like Tarantino wrote her — the speaker rides nostalgia for that specific heat, that specific night, the whole crew locked into a moment that still smells like it did then.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~ten-dzie-minie":{"opus":"A brutally honest Polish account of alcoholic and drug-fueled blackouts: waking hungover with scratch marks and no memory of a stranger he slept with, dodging phone calls, fearing STDs, joking about therapy, yet knowing he'll relapse next weekend because he perversely likes his hangovers.","sonnet":"A hungover morning-after that stretches into self-analysis — the narrator pieces together a blackout night of drinking, sex, and lost hours, half-terrified by the evidence on his body and his bank account, half-resigned to repeating the same weekend cycle next time.","src":"sonnet","haiku":"Nocturnal demons and waking disturbance blur dream and reality; demons speak in nightmares while the speaker struggles with uncertainty and hangover."},
"pezet~tyrmand-i-h-asko": {"haiku":"Bohemian independence and urban wandering celebrate unconventional life against institutional expectation and middle-class conformity.","opus":"A literary swagger anthem in which the Polish rapper casts his nightlife prowling alongside Sokół as a modern echo of writers Tyrmand and Hłasko roaming Warsaw. Half-drunk, half-pastor, he hunts audiences and women during mating season, feeling out of place everywhere yet owning the city better than its officials. Bohemian myth-making fuses street predation with a claim to cultural lineage.","sonnet":"The speaker roams Warsaw's nightlife with the swagger of Leopold Tyrmand and Marek Hłasko — hunting women, wearing shades against the soul's exposure, navigating influencers and ego — living as a bon vivant who won't wake before dawn. (lang: pl)","src":"haiku","fable":"Pezet builds the whole track as a night-walker's brag — the rapper as bird of prey circling a female crowd, the ceaseless raid of a mating-season summer — and pins it to two icons of 1950s Warsaw bohemia: Tyrmand the dandy stylist, Hłasko the doomed tough. The refrain, we walk the streets like Tyrmand and Hłasko, is a claim to that lineage of the beautiful, ungovernable outsider, complete with the bikiniarz's colored socks and the bon-vivant costume worn ironically over a tracksuit. But the swagger is a mask he keeps letting slip: between the boasts he is a stranger everywhere — a hetero in a gay club, a Catholic in a synagogue, shouting it's fine on a battlefield — chased by a shadow of whispers that sharpens at night, begging God or fate to change something, wanting only to sleep. That split is the song's real subject, named outright as half-drunk, half-pastor and as being last place and tied for first at once: the same self that treats women as conquest also can't stop staring at a phone, spilling the milk, longing to escape somewhere far with someone who'd make him forget he has one. When he says everyone expects a punchline and will be disappointed, he's refusing to resolve the pose — the legend of Tyrmand and Hłasko is exactly this, the glamorous outsider whose brilliance is inseparable from the loneliness and self-sabotage underneath, and Pezet wears both halves at once rather than choosing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance","party & hedonism"],"means":"wearing the legend's glamour while loneliness bleeds through the seams","built":"the refrain claims a lineage the verses quietly contradict"},
"pezet~ukryty-w-mie-cie-krzyk":{"haiku":"Hip-hop as weapon and escape for voiceless youth trapped by systemic indifference.","opus":"The voice of kids from the housing blocks whom society writes off as criminals before knowing them. Hip-hop is the only thing that understands their pain, the mic the only weapon, and all they ask for is freedom and a calm future.","sonnet":"Warsaw hip-hop artists assert that they are the city's hidden scream — judged as criminals, dismissed by loved ones, exploited by show business — demanding only freedom, their music, and a peaceful future. (lang: pl)","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~ukryty-w-mieście-krzyk":{"haiku":"Urban poverty and systemic exclusion fuel collective resistance as marginalized youth demand visibility beyond stereotypes.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~wo-anie":{"haiku":"Urban entrapment and spiritual decay afflict the young as faith dissolves and survival demands moral compromise. The speaker walks between consecration and corruption, praying for transformation that may never arrive.","opus":"A prayer disguised as a song, the narrator worn down by a glittering city and his own repeated deaths, begging for the strength to live normally and change. Invoking Ozymandias, he reduces wealth and status to dust and reaches out to God as a genuine cry, not a performance.","sonnet":"A prayer from a Warsaw rapper who has died and resurrected in the city a hundred times — trapped in nets of fate, numb to joy, carrying a hole in his chest like a gunshot wound — he confesses the song is not a song but a cry, addressed to God, for the dawn that comes only after the darkest night.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~zizu":{"haiku":"Escape from poverty's trap frames the speaker's entire ambition, drawing on Zidane as metaphor for elevation. The dream of breaking free collides with systemic dead ends and shortcuts that lead nowhere.","opus":"A Polish rapper measures the gap between fantasy and origin, craving GTRs and Koenigseggs while stuck with state healthcare and payday loans. He recalls his mother's tears when tax collectors chased him and the dream of winning a World Cup like Zidane, whose namesake headbutt frames his own volatile entrances. The refrain admits there are no shortcuts here, wealth always drifting just out of reach.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper measures his working-class biography — bureaucratic debt (NFZ, KAS), neighbourhood courts, a mother's tears — against the fantasy of a Zidane-style breakthrough; the chorus places a Paris tango and a woman in lingerie as the impossible romance that nearly happened, and the refrain 'there are no shortcuts' stands as both confession and defiance.","src":"haiku"},
"pezet~zszed-em-ze-sceny": {"haiku":"Age and independence pull the speaker away from performance while retaining wild defiance.","opus":"An aging rapper at forty-two, the age Elvis died, reckoning with the block that raised him on heroin and cocaine, joining TikTok feeling like a boomer. He left the stage once but decides rap is love, not a company, and it isn't time to go.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper in his forties reflects on growing up on the estate, ageing in the music game, and his cat-like resilience — \"I fell and climb back to the top\" — approaching middle age with clear-eyed self-awareness and dark humour. (lang: pl)","src":"haiku","fable":"A veteran's reckoning with age staged as a feint: the title keeps announcing that he stepped off the stage, and the song keeps refusing to let it stand. Pezet builds the case for retirement first — he joined TikTok and feels like a boomer, he is past forty (42, the age Elvis died at, he notes), the young nerds correct his mistakes — and he sets all of that against the housing-block youth that shaped him, drugs and dead-end jobs, so the exhaustion reads as earned rather than posed. But the hook is a cat's logic: he walks his own way with seven lives, falls and climbs back to the top, tracks mud onto other people's turf and keeps returning, so the confession of stepping off is immediately overwritten by proof he can't. The final turn makes the retraction explicit and reframes the whole complaint — once he did step off, but this is no time to step off, because for men like him the game isn't a company or a price on a shelf, it's love and it has no price. What survives is the mask-and-underneath structure: the boomer-on-TikTok self-deprecation is the pose, and the durable pride of a man who treats rap as a debt of love, not a career, is what it's protecting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","nostalgia & memory","artifice & performance"],"means":"the retirement speech overwritten by proof he cannot retire","built":"the title's claim of stepping off is refuted line by line inside the hook"},
"pezet~zszedlem-ze-sceny":{"haiku":"Aging artist navigates cultural irrelevance while maintaining independence through unconventional choices despite societal judgment.","src":"haiku"},
"phantogram~answer":{"haiku":"Desperate search for clarity and commitment within an emotionally exhausting relationship, haunted by loss and the inability to move forward.","opus":"A demand for certainty from a love that's already slipping away, the singer up all night with heroes gone, unable to make someone stay. The refrain needing an answer is the sound of clinging to something already leaving.","sonnet":"A person in emotional turmoil demands clarity and resolution from someone who talks too much but says nothing useful, while acknowledging she cannot stop someone already gone. The repeated plea for an answer is both a demand directed outward and an admission that no answer is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"phantogram~when-i-m-small":{"haiku":"Underground entrapment or psychological confinement; the speaker oscillates between seeking escape through fire and preferring death to submission. Desperation peaks when power is held by an indifferent hand.","opus":"Electro-rock duo Phantogram bury dread inside dream-pop, a narrator unsure whether they're below ground, caught halfway, or in over their head, watching a figure called Lucy slip away. A finger hovers over a button, and love is insisted upon even as the hook swears they'd sooner not exist than stay together. Threat and longing smear into each other, closeness souring into something airless and permanent.","sonnet":"Someone watching a friend named Lucy disappear into a dark underground questions whether they themselves are lost or simply caught between states. The relationship with whoever holds power turns unbearable enough that death is chosen over continued proximity — the final counterpoint making clear two voices have been pulling in opposite directions all along.","src":"haiku"},
"phantogram~you-are-the-ocean":{"haiku":"The beloved becomes an all-consuming force against which drowning becomes inevitable; the speaker forgets and falls under waves of oceanic presence.","opus":"Devotion as slow drowning, love for someone destructive that the narrator keeps choosing anyway. Sarah Barthel ties private ruin to a sick country, so the personal undertow and a national one pull in the same helpless direction.","sonnet":"A speaker gripped by seasonal depression and emotional dependency addresses someone overwhelming enough to drown them — the relationship is a tide that pulls under and erases, leaving the speaker warned but unable to surface.","src":"haiku"},
"pharrell-williams~happy":{"haiku":"Unshakeable contentment resists all external negativity and hardship; the speaker elevates happiness as a personal frequency impervious to the world's weight.","opus":"An irrepressible declaration that joy can be chosen and immune to bad news, an open invitation to clap along and share it. Its power is in how frictionless and communal it makes happiness feel, boundless as a roofless room.","sonnet":"An unshakeable declaration of joy that defies any attempt to bring the speaker down, inviting listeners to recognise and share in a happiness so self-sustaining it needs no external approval.","src":"haiku"},
"phaseone~eyes-wide-shut":{"haiku":"Numbness and self-inflicted suffering intertwine; pain becomes the only evidence of existence.","opus":"An electronic-metal descent into dissociation and self-injury — the narrator so numb he courts physical pain just to feel present, unsure whether his detachment is peace or sickness. At its center sits a rescuer who cannot rescue himself.","sonnet":"A narrator caught between numbness and self-destruction asks whether his compulsion toward pain is insanity or simply the only way he can feel anything: \"I can't feel a thing til my bones break.\" The question of being a saviour who cannot save himself runs through the song, with no resolution — only the cycle of pushing further in.","src":"haiku"},
"phaseone~gangrene":{"haiku":"Decay spreads despite attempts to salvage connection; amputation becomes necessary when love turns poisonous.","opus":"A dying relationship framed as gangrene that must be amputated before it poisons everything—what was perfection has rotted into a limb worth cutting off. The narrator resolves that clinging to the old attachment to the wound isn't worth dying over.","sonnet":"About ending a relationship before it destroys both people — the speaker uses surgery as the central metaphor, arguing that what was once perfect has become a rotting limb, and that cutting away cleanly now is the only way to survive even though it is agonizing; staying would be the actual death.","src":"haiku"},
"phaseone~icarus":{"haiku":"Humanity cannibalizes itself through pride and ignorance, spiraling toward self-inflicted doom. Flight leads only to fire and deserved collapse.","opus":"A grim view of humanity destroying itself through pride and division, praying for rescue that never comes and flying too high on wings of lead; the Icarus myth frames a species racing toward its own burnup.","sonnet":"A song about collective self-destruction driven by pride and division — using the myth of Icarus as a frame, the narrator describes a society cannibalizing itself through internal conflict, flying too close to the sun on wings of ignorance, and hurtling toward an end with no safe place left and no time to pretend.","src":"haiku"},
"philip-mchale~red-velvet":{"haiku":"Sensual desire and forbidden temptation collide; the speaker craves richness and transgression.","opus":"A wry character study of a young woman shaped entirely by magazines, mirrors and other people's expectations, walking like she knows where she's going while she doesn't yet. Praised for the work she's had done on herself, she's reminded she isn't that work, and asked whether she ever had a choice. Behind the glamour lurks isolation, an actress living a life she may never have actually wanted.","sonnet":"A woman shaped entirely by external instruction — she walks and talks and calls her boyfriend the way magazines and blondes on her wall dictate, and the boyfriend who praises \"the work you've done\" doesn't realise (or care) that she isn't the work. The turn at the end is the mirror: pretty girls told her she can't feel alone, but she's so alone, and the question \"did you ever have a choice?\" goes unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"phoxjaw~apples":{"haiku":"Innocence and natural abundance vanish as apocalyptic catastrophe sweeps away childhood's simple treasures. Locusts plague the land, and the speaker mourns what cannot return.","opus":"A descent into madness told through the fixation that the apples should never have been taken from the trees, sliding into asylum imagery and a mind painted vantablack. Innocence lost curdles into a Johnny gone violently, cyanide-picnic mad.","sonnet":"A fevered, fragmentary descent into mental collapse, using the removed apple as a symbol of forbidden knowledge and institutional control — \"apple lattice, straight jacket, kept the doctor away\" — Johnny is painting in vantablack, buried in nylon, sucked dry, the cyanide picnic the end of a mind that worked too hard.","src":"haiku"},
"phoxjaw~half-house":{"haiku":"A couple tears apart through emotional sabotage and destructive conflict, living in a collapsing home surrounded by violent, apocalyptic imagery.","opus":"A relationship, or a whole life, imagined as a crumbling half-built structure that gets patched together only to fall apart again. Phoxjaw, a British alt-rock band drawn to surreal gothic imagery, pile up grotesque, nightmarish pictures of decay and menace, the lurid metaphors capturing something perpetually on the verge of collapse.","sonnet":"A relationship in ruin described through apocalyptic domestic imagery — fire of London, crumbling stone walls, acidic air, charging bulls in red frenzy. The two inhabitants tear at the fabric they've tried to repair, and the speaker hangs abandoned while crows circle, living inside a structure that was never fully built.","src":"haiku"},
"phoxjaw~infinite-badness":{"opus":"Depression framed through the logic of a video game, blocks blown apart and a big villain returning no matter how carefully things are built. Phoxjaw asks whether there is any cure or curse for a bellowing sadness. The smudged-clown imagery captures self-blame worn as costume, futility spiraling with dark whimsy.","sonnet":"About a relationship where every attempt to build something gets blown apart by a big destructive force — the narrator accepts default blame, questions whether there is any cure for persistent sadness and inherent self-destructiveness, and watches everything fall apart again while remaining unable to stop it.","src":"sonnet"},
"phoxjaw~sungazer":{"haiku":"The sun burns and destroys all life while people dance naked in its lethal rays, aware and resigned to solar extinction. Humanity prances toward inevitable incineration with open eyes.","opus":"A gleefully macabre alt-rock ode to the sun as both giver of life and merciless executioner. Faces prune, eyes smolder like coals, and ancient farmers cry out for their crops as the narrator dances bare-skinned through midsummer, half-worshipping, half-cooking alive. Slathering on pale sunscreen becomes an absurd ritual defense against a scorching glare that will eventually wear the whole planet out; awe and dread entwined.","sonnet":"The sun as absurd tyrant — eyes burning like coal, skeletal camels, the Mayans weeping for their crops — the song swings between earnest sun-worship and gleeful apocalyptic surrender, ending with naked dancing on the longest day like embers before the planet tires.","src":"haiku"},
"phoxjaw~trophies-in-the-attic":{"haiku":"A person blindfolds themselves to past achievements stored like trophies, unable to see the history or meaning of what was once won.","opus":"A melancholy farewell to white coastal cliffs and stately seaside promenades, a childhood of gathered stones and a treat, set against a country left inert. The British band turns dust-covered cabinets and hidden mementos into an elegy for vanished greatness, eyes sealed and made willfully sightless, a former winner's honors abandoned and packed away out of sight.","sonnet":"England's paralysis sits beneath a dusty layer of nostalgia — former glories locked away in attics, shields no longer visible to those who have blinded themselves to the decline. The coastal farewell and childhood pebbles frame a country that peaked and forgot it happened.","src":"haiku"},
"pi-dwa-feat-ski-sk-ad~kto":{"haiku":"Liars dominate the landscape; rappers expose false personas and demand authenticity in hip-hop, stripping away commercial facades to reveal true dedication to craft.","opus":"An aggressive Polish battle posse cut built on the chant asking who lies here like a song. The MCs trade menacing verses about crushing pretenders, calling out dishonesty and fake artists in the scene, insisting real rap comes from lived experience rather than posturing. When arguments run out only force remains; a chest-thumping defense of authenticity and street credibility against sellouts and liars.","sonnet":"Three Polish MCs obliterate fake friends, industry snakes, and commercial sellouts with precision and fury — who's lying here as if reading from sheet music becomes the refrain for every hypocrite in the rap scene. Loyalty, rawness, and refusal to compromise are the values; anyone who'd corrupt themselves for a fee or betray the collective gets expelled from the table.","src":"haiku"},
"picturesque~holding-me-down":{"haiku":"Intoxicating attraction becomes entrapment; the speaker lusts for someone beautiful and destructive, aware they are held captive yet unable and unwilling to escape.","opus":"An obsessive, addictive attraction that consumes and demeans the speaker, who tears himself apart to please a lover who steals and drags him down. It is about desire experienced as both intoxication and self-destruction.","sonnet":"A toxic erotic pull: the speaker is magnetised by someone described as a devil hiding under covers, someone who steals like second nature and whose presence is simultaneously intoxicating and suffocating, and every attempt to impress her costs him pieces of himself he tears apart to make her proud.","src":"haiku"},
"picturesque~hopeless":{"haiku":"Despair and absence of redemption, cynicism in face of impossible circumstances.","opus":"A metalcore panic attack staged as a haunted house, the narrator sinking into a sofa in a doorless, clockless room where nothing shifts and he goes unseen. Figures on the ground, smoke and vertigo circle the recognition that he's been in this exact spot before, spinning in loops unsure of the date. It captures the airless, stuck feeling of depression as inescapable, hopeless recurrence.","sonnet":"Dissociation rendered as a haunted house with no doors — paint chipping off walls, sea-sick and not moving, bodies on the floor, smoke-snowed in, burning up the throat. The narrator has been here before and cannot tell if this time is different, cycling through the refrain \"maybe this is hopeless\" until it sounds like acceptance.","src":"haiku"},
"pierce-the-veil~king-for-a-day":{"haiku":"Teenage despair and suicidal ideation masked as rebellion and performative anger.","opus":"An anthem of youth rage and self-destruction, someone daring the world to break them while volunteering to be its martyr. The bravado masks exhaustion, begging to feel nothing rather than keep wanting things they can't have.","sonnet":"A voice at the edge of breaking rails against a toxic relationship and the numbness it breeds — begging to be pushed past the limit, fantasising escape to something better, then pivoting into a street-level rage about powerlessness and survival.","src":"haiku"},
"pierce-the-veil~pass-the-nirvana":{"haiku":"A speaker numbs pain through intoxication while tension builds toward collapse. Distraction masks the urge to destroy what won't listen.","opus":"A dizzying shove through strain and self-medication, mocking hollow feel-good slogans while fighting to stay numb; the daily struggle is just to shrug it off, catch a breath and get through the racket.","sonnet":"A chaotic, violent song about numbing pain through substances and destructive behavior — the narrator moves through images of bloodshed, drug use, and self-destruction, using the phrase 'pass the nirvana' as a dark joke about chasing oblivion, while reflecting on cycles of tension and giving up.","src":"haiku"},
"pierce-the-veil~the-boy-who-could-fly":{"haiku":"Love and death entangle as the narrator grieves a lover's suicide; they question faith and survival, drowning in the weight of a connection that transcended life.","opus":"A glowing, tipsy walk home with a beloved shades into mourning after she dies in California, the romance souring into ruin. Once he gathered constellations to impress her; now the same devotion reads as an elegy, since a life without her leaves nothing of him either.","sonnet":"A night that begins with romantic tenderness and drunken stargazing darkens into grief and self-recrimination when the speaker confronts the loss of someone who died, questioning why he prayed and insisting that without her there is no version of himself left.","src":"haiku"},
"pig~hopelessly-devoted-to-you":{"haiku":"Resigned surrender to romantic obsession despite knowing it leads only to suffering.","opus":"An industrial cover of the Grease ballad, where the aching devotion to someone who has pushed you aside gets recast darker: the fool who waits, head at war with heart, unable to get over a love that's already gone.","sonnet":"A person whose love has been pushed aside knows they are a fool to keep waiting, can't make their heart let go even as their head says forget it, and remains hopelessly devoted despite having no rational reason to stay.","src":"haiku"},
"pinegrove~need-2":{"opus":"A spare confession of numb detachment, nothing worth caring about or saying aloud, yet an unexplained pull to reach out anyway persists. The song sits in the gap between apathy and an ache to connect the speaker can't quite justify.","sonnet":"A minimal two-verse lyric: the speaker checks out, finds nothing worth saying aloud, yet notices an unexplained compulsion — \"so then why do I seem to need to?\" — leaving the unnamed need suspended without resolution.","src":"sonnet"},
"pink-floyd~another-brick-in-the-wall-pt-1":{"haiku":"A child processes his absent father's legacy, left only with memories and unanswered questions.","opus":"A child grappling with a father who departed overseas and left behind only a photograph, the earliest layer of the emotional barricade the album constructs. That quiet longing sets up the whole record's design of withdrawal. Opening the group's 1979 concept work, this draws on Waters' own father's wartime death, the private injury that drives the protagonist's flight from everyone.","sonnet":"A child's quiet reckoning with an absent father who crossed the ocean and left nothing behind but a photograph and a question. There is no bitterness yet — just the hollow space where a person should have been, one more brick in a wall the child doesn't yet understand they're building.","src":"haiku"},
"pink-floyd~another-brick-in-the-wall-pt-2":{"haiku":"Students rebel against a rigid, authoritarian education system that prioritizes obedience and punishment over learning; they reject thought control and demand freedom.","opus":"A protest against authoritarian schooling, with pupils rejecting an education system that indoctrinates rather than teaches and reduces every student to an interchangeable component of a larger institutional machine.","sonnet":"Students collectively reject the authoritarian control of teachers and the educational system, demanding to be left alone rather than subjected to thought control and humiliation. The refrain positions each act of oppression as just one more brick added to a wall of psychological damage.","src":"haiku"},
"pink-floyd~comfortably-numb":{"haiku":"Dissociation protects against unbearable sensation; the speaker recedes into numbness, watching life from distance while disconnected from authentic feeling.","opus":"A doctor's clinical coaxing of a dissociated, medicated performer overlaps with the patient's drift into fevered childhood memory and detachment. The numbness that lets the show go on is also the loss of the child and the dream, a receding into waves that no one can reach.","sonnet":"A doctor-patient exchange frames a rock performer's dissociation: the doctor medicates to get the show on the road while the performer retreats into childhood fever-memories — hands like balloons, a fleeting glimpse gone before it could be caught — and the dream, the child, the capacity to feel all dissolve into a numb that has become permanent.","src":"haiku"},
"pink-floyd~high-hopes-2011-remaster":{"haiku":"Youth's limitless wonder fades into adulthood's compromises as ambition and desire anchor the spirit to earth, transforming dreams into haunted memories of what could have been.","opus":"An aging look back at youth's boundless wonder, mourning how ambition and desire dragged the band from that green world into slow decay, the division bell tolling over the loss.","sonnet":"A backward-looking elegy for youthful boundlessness: the grass was greener, the light brighter, the friends were still there — but desire and ambition encumbered the speaker forever, and every forward step is undone by the force of some inner tide dragging them back toward an endless river.","src":"haiku"},
"pinkshift~nothing-in-my-head":{"haiku":"Numbness and confinement consume the psyche as the narrator suffocates under digital stimulation and social control. Trapped within invisible walls, they desperately seek escape from a system designed to keep them docile and watched.","opus":"Numbness and screen-fatigue collapse into feeling trapped in a box, likened to a fly drowning in rotting fruit while others watch. A claustrophobic plea to shut off the noise and simply be done.","sonnet":"Emotional numbness and claustrophobic overwhelm enclose the speaker — unable to shut out the noise and the watching eyes, they feel like a fly trapped in a jar of rotting fruit, never meant to survive, begging to be released from the box they're caught in.","src":"haiku"},
"pitcher56~1-2-3":{"haiku":"Childhood hide-and-seek transforms into existential search for the self, where one player seeks while another cannot be found. The game stretches into dusk and beyond, without resolution.","opus":"A game of hide-and-seek stretched into something tender and anxious, the seeker chasing a friend who always hides too far as daylight cools into evening. Beneath the childlike count is a real fear of not finding the person before time runs out.","sonnet":"A hide-and-seek game stretched into something bittersweet: the seeker chases through the park as shadows lengthen, slightly afraid of giving up, the light turning to cold wind, desperate to find the hider before mama gets home — childhood play carrying a faint longing not to be abandoned.","src":"haiku"},
"pitchshifter~genius":{"haiku":"The speaker frames dysfunction as a skill that defines them as genius, celebrating their broken nature and gathering followers into their religion of damage.","opus":"A sardonic, chant-like track that runs a warped syllogism to crown its narrator brilliant precisely because he's broken. The throughline is ironic self-congratulation, treating malfunction as a faith with thousands of devotees, needling conventional ideas of intelligence and normality in the same breath.","sonnet":"A sardonic self-deprecation: if dysfunction is a function, then the narrator's thorough mastery of being broken must make him a genius.","src":"haiku"},
"pixel-grip~bet-you-do":{"haiku":"The speaker tests and challenges, certain the other will eventually reveal their true nature.","opus":"A cold, commanding darkwave power play where the narrator holds all the allure and calls out a would-be rival who can't look the truth in the eye. Taunting them for standing in her shadow while claiming to be a star, she wields desire as leverage, betting she knows exactly what they want. Love, hatred and control tangle into a stronger knot; seduction as dominance, thrillingly unbothered.","sonnet":"A power-shift confrontation: the narrator coolly exposes a pursuer's self-delusion — you think I'm in love, you think you're a star, but you're standing in my shadow. The rope is coming loose and the options are cut it or tie your noose, a choreography of dominance dressed in danceable contempt.","src":"haiku"},
"pixel-grip~split":{"opus":"A breakup catalogued as physical damage: sleeplessness, weight loss, a heart that literally cracks. Gave everything freely, got betrayal and slander in return, and now walking out is the only dignity left, even as the wound is worn openly rather than hidden.","sonnet":"A song about a betrayal by someone the narrator gave everything to — they couldn't sleep, couldn't stop obsessing, and then that person spread lies about them after all the investment, cracking the narrator's heart open; they refuse to hide the hurt and teeter on the edge of breaking apart completely.","src":"sonnet"},
"pixel-terror~enigma":{"haiku":"Two people recognize each other's hidden feelings and shared history, growing stronger together through what they've endured.","opus":"A straightforward declaration of devotion, feeling a partner's energy in every glance and taking the hardships they have survived together as proof of strength. It stays on the simple, uplifting refrain that the two of them are strong enough. Pixel Terror are an electronic act known for melodic dubstep and gaming-adjacent EDM, and the lyric here is essentially a vocal hook draped over the drop, warmth and reassurance kept deliberately spare.","sonnet":"A straightforward declaration of connection strengthened by shared experience — the singer feels the other person's energy every time they lock eyes, and the chorus insists that everything they've been through together has made them stronger. It's uncomplicated devotion, earnest and repeated until it settles.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~alec-eiffel":{"opus":"A wry tribute to Gustave Eiffel, the aerodynamics pioneer mocked and doubted, who built his tower anyway despite everyone finding it phallic and pointless.","sonnet":"A quirky homage to Gustave Eiffel, aerodynamic pioneer and visionary builder: dismissed as phallic and too ambitious, he built his tower anyway, standing in the archway as proof that keeping low makes no sense when you can see the panorama.","src":"sonnet"},
"pixies~bone-machine":{"haiku":"Infidelity and physical attraction reduce bodies and relationships to mechanical exchange without genuine connection.","opus":"A jittery, disjointed sketch of an unfaithful lover named Carol, laced with a molestation aside and clashing images of skin and appetite, all sung as if the body itself were a little machine. Francis strings the discomfort together without resolving it into a story.","sonnet":"A fractured, surreal portrait of infidelity and bodily desire — a girlfriend beautiful in her unfaithfulness, a predatory preacher in a parking lot, a love described as rice and beans and horse lard — all orbiting the refrain that the woman has a little machine in her bones.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~break-my-body":{"haiku":"A persona revels in transgressive behavior and physical extremism, treating one's body as expendable while courting violence and degradation.","opus":"From Surfer Rosa, the Boston band who reshaped alternative rock with their loud-quiet swings. Black Francis yelps out a gleeful, self-humiliating confession, casting himself as the pathetic, desperate suitor who craves being physically wrecked and cradled. Masochism delivered as pure unhinged delight, with the shrug that pain is simply guaranteed.","sonnet":"A series of reckless self-portraits — loser, ugly lover, belly dancer, rooftop jumper — collapsed into a single body that invites destruction while demanding to be held. The refrain asks for both violence and tenderness simultaneously, and the song ends on injury without explaining whether it was caused by someone else or was always self-inflicted.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~cactus":{"haiku":"Obsessive longing for a lost person; the narrator collects intimate objects as proof of connection and sends them as desperate pleas to restore the relationship.","opus":"From the band's incendiary debut, Black Francis fixates on absence with unsettling literalism, wishing for a garment worn by a distant lover to fill her place. He asks for the dress bloodied on a cactus, wet, sent to him, insisting a letter proves nothing about survival. Longing curdles into something feral and macabre, the spelled-out band name breaking the fever like a shrug.","sonnet":"A person locked away in longing fixates on the physical traces of someone absent — worn fabric, spilled food, a bloodied hand on a cactus. The requests are strange and intimate: send the dress, stained and real, so absence can be held in something that has touched the body of the person missed.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~dead":{"haiku":"Frantic sexuality and death obsession intertwine as expressions of psychological chaos.","opus":"The band retelling the Bathsheba story from 2 Samuel as leering, deadpan violence, David's lust and Uriah's engineered death. Loud-quiet menace played for black comedy, biblical adultery rendered grotesque.","sonnet":"A delirious, fragmented address mixing desire, biblical reference, bodily decay, and cyclic destruction — the speaker is exhausted or spiritually dead even as erotic and violent energies keep churning through the text.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~debaser":{"haiku":"References the surrealist film 'Un Chien Andalou' with its shocking eyeball imagery, claiming kinship with provocative cinema and the desire to grow up as a debaser—one who corrupts or degrades.","opus":"A giddy celebration of degradation whose singer aspires to nothing loftier than becoming a corrupter, delighting in a jarring bit of shock imagery. The nod is to a notorious surrealist short film, chanted into nonsense. Opening the group's landmark 1989 album, it channels art-school provocation into pure pop energy, a cornerstone of alternative rock.","sonnet":"A gleeful identification with transgression and surrealist shock — the speaker aligns themselves with an Un Chien Andalou eyeball-slicing vision of art and announces, with cheerful menace, an aspiration to debase. It's more celebration than manifesto, reveling in the grotesque as liberation.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~distance-equals-rate-times-time":{"haiku":"Staring into the sun as spiritual experience; fragmentary visions of need and light.","opus":"Black Francis at his most gleefully apocalyptic and cryptic, staring at the sun until the world dissolves into surf-rock non-sequitur. Beneath the nonsense is end-times dread played for a shrug and a beer.","sonnet":"A fractured, sun-blinded vision of a world without television where a blind saint gives advice about getting beer in an atmosphere-less place. The imagery is deliberately disjointed and surreal, more texture than narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~ed-is-dead":{"haiku":"Senseless death merges with coastal indulgence; the narrator mourns what they cannot comprehend.","opus":"Black Francis sketching a woman rotting in stupid bliss on an L.A. beach and the flat refrain that Ed is dead, more mood than narrative. The surf-town detachment makes death feel like just another sunny afternoon.","sonnet":"A sun-dazed California girl — lost in music, oblivious to danger, rotting in blissful inertia — is elegised and dedicated to in equal measure by a narrator who memorialises both her and the beach life she embodies.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~head-on":{"haiku":"Intense desire and attraction electrify the narrator to the point of willing death. The song captures overwhelming passion that defies reason and shame.","opus":"A song about desire so charged it feels capable of tearing the heavens apart, the narrator unsteady and overheated, escaping to a seedy corner of the city where his worries evaporate. Passion and ruin merge until death itself would carry no regret. Originally by The Jesus and Mary Chain, the Pixies' cover on Trompe le Monde cranks its feedback-soaked longing into their own loud-quiet fury, tribute and wreckage at once.","sonnet":"Electric infatuation so overwhelming it short-circuits everything else — the world could end and it wouldn't matter, shame drops away, the body can't stand upright. The speaker flees to somewhere rough and unglamorous just to shake the charge, but the current keeps returning.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~i-bleed":{"haiku":"Constant inner rupture accompanies existence; meaning eludes comprehension in burial grounds.","opus":"A queasy, dream-logic account of quiet suffering masked by a grin, teeth rattling to a tolling chime as bloodsuckers gorge. The bleeding is unexplained and unending, the mind seceding as it drifts toward some strange interred frontier.","sonnet":"A persistent inner ringing — like a bell behind the smile — accompanies the speaker through sleep and waking, as vampiric forces feed and the mind drifts away from the body, leaving only the sensation of bleeding and a mysterious hollow in the earth.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~i-m-amazed":{"haiku":"A narrative about scandals and shocking events at a school, punctuated by the speaker's amazement at the chaos and his own driving away.","opus":"A collage of half-heard scandal and shock, spoken gossip about rumored liaisons bleeding into images of a bride shooting herself the day before a wedding and a man driving off to Arizona before he died. The chanted amazement treats horror and childhood memory with the same flat wonder. An early Pixies cut from Surfer Rosa, its found-speech intro and jarring non-sequiturs are the band at their most gleefully unsettling.","sonnet":"A series of disjointed fragments — a woman who died, a childhood toy, a drive to Arizona — held together only by stunned repetition. The speaker cycles through these shards of memory and event without connecting them, the amazement itself becoming the point: nothing coheres and that gap is the song.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~monkey-gone-to-heaven":{"haiku":"Environmental catastrophe and divine reckoning converge as pollution destroys nature while cosmic forces shift in retribution.","opus":"An ecological parable: an ocean deity is smothered by industrial waste off the northeast coast, and a rift opens overhead. Francis then slides into a strange arithmetic of man, devil and God, tying the planet's ruin to a cosmic ledger and resigning himself to the coming fire that spares no one.","sonnet":"An environmental elegy framed in numerological mysticism: a sea creature killed by ten million pounds of New York sludge, a hole torn in the sky, everything going to burn — with the equation that man is five, devil is six, and God is seven, and the monkey is gone to heaven.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~river-euphrates":{"haiku":"Escape by any means—tire and current, geographic flight—carries the same gravity of desire without destination, floating toward something always just beyond reach.","opus":"Black Francis reduces a Middle East conflict zone to an absurd joyride, floating a rubber wheel down a legendary waterway past Gaza and the salt sea. The nonsense is the point; geopolitics dissolves into surreal, menacing cartoon violence rather than any coherent statement.","sonnet":"A deadpan Middle Eastern road-trip fantasy — stranded on the Gaza Strip, out of gas, the solution is to ride a tire down the Euphrates and float on the Dead Sea. The lyric is almost pure sound and absurdist geography rather than conventional meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~subbacultcha":{"haiku":"Two young lovers dressed in black enjoy the subculture scene, then escape into a fantasy where they sail on a pirate ship together, leaving the underground behind.","opus":"A seedy, funny hymn to a subculture romance, the narrator in eyeliner and black chasing an erotic-vulture of a woman through clubs and eventually onto a drug-running schooner. It parodies bohemian posturing while relishing every grope for the moon. From the Pixies' 1991 Trompe le Monde, the track leers and winks in equal measure, Black Francis's deadpan sleaze set against the band's tight, snarling guitar attack.","sonnet":"A scene from the underground — two people in matching black, drawn together by physical obsession in a world of clubs and subculture, who end up sailing a Panamanian schooner running drugs and staring at the moon. The romance is sleazy and poetic all at once, and the word for that combination keeps escaping them.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~tony-s-theme":{"haiku":"A lighthearted superhero song about Tony riding his bicycle, learning to stunt and joke while enjoying his own playful confidence.","opus":"A cartoonish superhero anthem about Tony, a kid on a bicycle reimagined as a spitfire-turning, wheelie-popping crusader with a card in his spokes. The whole thing is a joke played straight, all bike-as-motorcycle bravado and playground scale. From the Pixies' 1988 Surfer Rosa, its breathless surf-punk charge and chanted name make it one of their most purely fun, deadpan blasts, childhood grandiosity set to a sprint.","sonnet":"A pure, absurdist hero song — Tony is a kid on a bicycle who fights evil, does wheelies, practices jokes, and cannot be stopped. The whole thing is a self-aware bit of playful nonsense that takes its own superhero mythology completely seriously and finds joy in the total lack of stakes.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~u-mass":{"haiku":"College idealism and youthful intellectual ferment mix with sexuality and liberation in an educational space.","opus":"A sardonic anthem of college-town rebellion where kids parrot capitalist and communist slogans and call it education, mistaking crudeness and provocation for ideas. Francis, an Amherst dropout, mocks the self-important radicalism of a valley full of pioneers.","sonnet":"A chaotic ode to the University of Massachusetts campus and its mingling of ideologies — capitalist, communist, redneck — dissolving into a joyful, obscene chant about kissing the world, the sky, and the ass, all declared educational.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~velouria":{"haiku":"A narrator pursues an otherworldly muse through California, wading together through timeless summer while grieving her distance.","opus":"Black Francis conjures an elusive, otherworldly muse into surreal shine and shifting light, adoration aimed at a woman who's less a person than a shimmering ideal he can't quite touch or hold still.","sonnet":"An elusive, dreamlike figure called Velouria is described in fragments — covering, travelling, reflecting light like lemur skin off the sea — and the speaker is entirely devoted, promising to wade through the tides of summer forever with her. The song adores rather than explains.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~wave-of-mutilation":{"haiku":"Death by oceanic transformation; the speaker surrenders to dissolution and merges with elemental mutation.","opus":"Reportedly sparked by news of Japanese businessmen driving into the sea with their families, faking death and vanishing beneath the waves. Black Francis makes annihilation sound like liberation, escape and self-erasure blurred into one oceanic sweep.","sonnet":"A speaker stages a disappearance — driving a car into the ocean — only to emerge into a dreamlike undersea world of mermaids and ocean depths, the wave of mutilation becoming a vehicle of strange liberation rather than destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"pixies~where-is-my-mind":{"haiku":"The song plays with surreal imagery and disorientation, describing a state of mental escape or dissociation where reality inverts and the speaker's consciousness drifts away. The recurring refrain captures existential bewilderment.","opus":"A disorienting, dreamlike sense of losing one's mind, tied to surreal images of an upside-down world and swimming in the Caribbean where a little fish seems to try to talk to the speaker.","sonnet":"A disoriented narrator describes a dreamlike state of mental dissolution — upside-down, hollow-headed — framed around a Caribbean swim where even a small fish seemed to be communicating, capturing a feeling of the mind drifting somewhere unreachable.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built on inversion and vertigo: turn a body upside down, spin it until the skull empties, and the self thins into a question that has no answer, only repetition. Black Francis pictures consciousness as something that has floated loose and gone paddling off in a distant sea, a mind that has slipped its owner and drifted somewhere unreachable. The snorkeling memory grounds this in real disorientation, hovering weightless offshore while a small fish rises up as though to speak, the ordinary rules of who thinks and who talks suddenly loosened. The refrain never resolves because dissociation offers no resolution; the query just circles, and the eerie part is the calm of it, a panic reported from very far away, held perfectly still.","fv":2,"fnote":"Its second life is the end of Fight Club — the towers collapsing while the Narrator holds Marla’s hand — a placement that took a never-charting 1988 album cut and made it inseparable from the film.","themes":["dissociation & numbness"],"means":"panic reported from very far away and held perfectly still","built":"the refrain never resolves because dissociation offers no resolution"},
"pj-harvey~down-by-the-water":{"opus":"A chilling ballad of a woman drowning her own daughter by the water and calling for her back, sliding between mother, loss and something predatory. Harvey sings it in a murderer's voice, the nursery-rhyme refrain making the horror intimate.","sonnet":"A dark, confessional song in which a narrator — addressing a girl who shifted from blue-eyed innocent to something else — took her down by the water like a daughter and lost her there, now crying out for her return in the refrain \"little fish, big fish, swimming in the water, come back here, man, gimme my daughter.\"","src":"sonnet"},
"placebo~allergic-to-thoughts-of-mother-earth":{"haiku":"Light defines and divides reality while the speaker begs not to be abandoned or betrayed by those meant to sustain them.","opus":"A bitter, cryptic swipe at afterlife-obsessed faith as a tourniquet and a bitter pill, the promise of heaven bought with today's suffering. The refracted refrain of light dividing and defining suggests salvation curdling into control.","sonnet":"A hallucinatory rejection of religious consolation — the afterlife offered as bitter medicine, spirituality as a levitation trick that steals children's smiles — the narrator is allergic to the whole comfort system, choking on light that claims to save.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~ashtray-heart":{"haiku":"A speaker's heart is broken and hollowed like an ashtray; they and their lover are each other's waste, using and burning each other for cigarettes.","opus":"A caustic address to the lover who replaced the singer and dared the commitment he couldn't, framed by a Spanish refrain about a heart turned to an ashtray. Placebo and Brian Molko render intimacy as mutilation, an organ ripped out and reduced to a place to snuff out cigarettes, tenderness and cruelty fused as the sheets stain crimson, love and harm indistinguishable.","sonnet":"A love so corrosive it functions as self-destruction — one person takes the leap the other never could, leaving the remaining one to confront a heart burned down to ash by every promise they failed to keep. The Spanish refrain returns like a verdict: this is what was left.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~ask-for-answers":{"haiku":"The narrator struggles within a toxic relationship where he always comes second, seeking wisdom from external sources. The bonds are both captive and paradoxically free.","opus":"Brian Molko turning over a relationship snarled in jealousy and lust, cast as the runner-up lover forever placing behind someone else. With no human explanation forthcoming and no do-overs on offer, he directs his questions at an indifferent ocean instead.","sonnet":"A desperate, suffocating relationship where the speaker always comes second and turns to the sea for answers because the person they love offers none — the bonds feel simultaneously imprisoning and intoxicating, lust and jealousy tangled together.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~battle-for-the-sun":{"opus":"A defiant refusal to be crushed, the narrator vowing to fight toward light while shaking off a heavy, toxic presence. Placebo's title track from their post-addiction reset frames survival as combat, having stared down a gun and become the bones that couldn't break. Resilience sung as open warfare against despair.","sonnet":"A recovery anthem addressed to someone who tried and failed to break the narrator — the song names a relationship as both killer and lover, insisting the narrator survived looking down a gun barrel and will not be destroyed by a cheap fake. The repetitive structure mirrors the compulsive return to confrontation before finally asserting survival.","src":"sonnet"},
"placebo~because-i-want-you":{"haiku":"An alcoholic man cycles between drinking-fueled abandonment and inability to be alone, tearing apart a relationship he cannot control.","opus":"A song about drinking to fill the fear of being alone while a home dissolves back into a house, anger and headaches fading between two people torn in two. Against that wreckage, the refrain clings stubbornly to wanting, refusing to give up on the dream. From Placebo's 2006 Meds, Brian Molko's reedy, androgynous voice drives the addiction-tinged neediness, the band's glam-tinged alt-rock making codependence sound almost triumphant.","sonnet":"A relationship held together by fear of solitude rather than health — drinking to manage anxiety, falling into the other person reflexively, watching the home erode as anger fades into numbness. The plea not to give up on wanting is directed as much at the self as at the other person, and its sincerity is exactly what makes it desperate.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~black-market-blood-hidden-track":{"haiku":"Madness and catastrophic anticipation consume the subject; running on desperation and illicit sustenance masks approaching collapse.","opus":"A portrait of a friend coming apart, an unstable woman bracing for some private apocalypse and running on borrowed, illicit fuel; the pity and helplessness of watching someone spiral toward a flood only they can see.","sonnet":"A portrait of a woman on the edge of psychological collapse — insane, always crying, preparing obsessively for a coming apocalyptic flood — observed with dark sympathy by a speaker who watches her run on black market blood, sustained by something illicit and unstable as catastrophe approaches.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~breathe-underwater":{"haiku":"A narrator reconciles the wounded child they were with the damaged adult they've become, surfacing from a long emotional drowning. They break the pattern of self-destruction through desperate gasping for air and dawn.","opus":"After repeated stumbles and disgraces, the speaker strains to square who he's become with the hurt child still lodged inside, pleading for a chance to surface and witness a new morning, worn out by the carnage and by drifting motionless.","sonnet":"Someone who has been drifting in destructive patterns and emotional damage declares they are finally surfacing — coming up for air, wanting to see another dawn, and ready to stop the cycle they have been trapped in.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~brick-shithouse":{"haiku":"A jealous figure haunts a lover who replaced them, buried in the ground while watching from below. The song dwells on betrayal and the inability to move on.","opus":"A jealous, ghostly fixation on a woman and the lover who has supplanted the narrator, imagining himself buried and watching the couple from beyond the grave. The bitter refrain wishing the listener had never met her, plus the barbed detail about her silent orgasms, turns thwarted desire into obsessive, voyeuristic resentment.","sonnet":"A dead narrator haunts the relationship his lover has moved on to, watching from the grave as she fakes intimacy with her new partner, unable to let go even in death.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~bright-lights":{"opus":"A reckoning with recovery, the narrator surveying years spent feigning wellness and the people he drove off. Placebo chart a compromise route out of a self-made confinement of old habits, arguing that an aching heart is proof of a functioning one. Radiance and voids become the twin poles coexisting in a life being rebuilt.","sonnet":"A recovery narrative — the narrator spent years pretending to be okay while causing damage, but now wants to find a middle way, break out of their self-made prison, and access empathy and authenticity, holding onto the conviction that a heart that hurts is still a heart that works.","src":"sonnet"},
"placebo~broken-promise":{"haiku":"Betrayal reverses power while vengeance quietly accumulates, the speaker waiting to inflict the pain they've been forced to hold, retribution merely postponed.","opus":"Betrayal met not with grief but with cold, patient vengeance; the wounded party admits their own dishonesty yet nurses a humiliated fury, waiting to inflict the same burn back. Love soured into a slow revenge fantasy.","sonnet":"A person who was deceived and betrayed waits with cold patience for the right moment to inflict equivalent damage on the one who wronged them. There is no forgiveness offered — only the calm promise of retribution and the awareness that a broken promise is a debt that will be collected.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~burger-queen":{"haiku":"A man isolated by his strangeness struggles with sexual dysfunction and incompleteness while romanticizing escape to a better world. He remains trapped by his own nature.","opus":"A cruel, pitying portrait of a listless Luxembourg boy, born by caesarean, dressing to match his pallid skin, cruising for connection but scoring only rejection and infection, dreaming of a better selection. The mocking hey-yous circle him like a chorus of tormentors. Placebo built this on real autobiography, Brian Molko's Luxembourg adolescence, and the band's queasy glam-rock sleaze makes the alienation both tender and unsparing, empathy laced with contempt.","sonnet":"A portrait of marginal urban male failure rendered without sympathy and without cruelty — a man adrift in pallid confusion, unable to score, prone to infection, dreaming of better options. The refrain that things aren't what they seem doubles as both a shrug and a verdict, and the Luxembourg queen tag pins him to a specific species of lost.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~centrefolds":{"haiku":"Someone desperately clings to a lover whose attention has already moved on; he tries to make himself indispensable before she fades away forever.","opus":"A frantic refusal to let a person slip away, addressed to a doomed idol, swearing he won't permit their end and that his wait for them has stretched unbearably long. The unreachable pin-up models, gone from view ages ago, sharpen the sense of hopeless craving. Placebo, the British alt-rock trio, lend Brian Molko's appeal its signature androgynous ache, fixation and gentleness fused.","sonnet":"A desperate appeal to someone called Balthazar, begging them not to give up — the speaker has waited far too long for this person to be theirs, and the centrefolds that surrounded them, the fantasy and glamour, have all said their goodbyes. What remains is a stripped-down plea: stop fading, come back, be mine.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~come-undone":{"haiku":"Someone's affected, pretentious demeanor masks unawareness; they unravel, blind to how poorly their facade reads to others.","opus":"The alt-rock trio, all sneering glamour, circles a self-important figure with no grasp of how others perceive him, martyring himself on an imagined crucifix while missing the joke entirely. His obliviousness to what his posturing costs becomes the indictment, and he unravels under the weight of his own affectation.","sonnet":"Someone is addressed with cold precision about their obliviousness — they project martyrdom without grasping how they appear to others, and the irony of their self-presentation is entirely lost on them. The unravelling is not dramatic but quiet, inevitable, and wholly of their own making.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~commercial-for-levi":{"haiku":"Addiction and degeneracy mask desire for transcendence; the speaker's deterioration accelerates while counseling another's escape.","opus":"A desperate plea to a friend or lover drowning in addiction and reckless sex, Brian Molko half-fascinated by the nocturnal glamour of self-destruction while begging them to stop before the habit kills them.","sonnet":"An intervention song addressed to someone destroying themselves with substances and reckless behavior — golden showers, ecstasy, cheap wine, immorality — the speaker says they understand the fascination because they've been there too, but the warning is blunt: if nothing changes, you will die.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~devil-in-the-details":{"haiku":"Moral compromise and corruption embedded in small choices and overlooked details. Wrongdoing compounds and the devil remains present to haunt and claim.","opus":"The narrator personifies his own vices as a friendly demon he keeps choosing to dance with. Frittering his life away on his worst compulsions, he half-surrenders to the certainty that his misdeeds will circle back to torment him and that this companion in ruin isn't going anywhere.","sonnet":"A rueful song about complicity in self-destruction — the narrator has befriended his own worst impulses, spending his time obsessing over flaws and wrong turns instead of creating, and he accepts that this pattern of wicked self-indulgence is probably here to stay.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~drag":{"haiku":"Perpetual inadequacy haunts the speaker as they trail behind a figure of impossible perfection; envious admiration masks the futility of ever closing the gap.","opus":"A study in inadequacy, cataloguing every way the singer trails behind an effortless rival who is also the object of adoration. The lag becomes both self-loathing and helpless attraction to the one who outshines him.","sonnet":"A speaker measures themselves against someone perpetually ahead — better at swimming, at school, at everything — and confesses both inferiority and adoration: \"I think you're divine,\" while repeatedly, almost ritually, lagging behind.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~english-summer-rain":{"haiku":"Persistent stagnation and cycles of falling apart and restarting, framed through English summer weather that always stays the same.","opus":"Placebo trade their usual glam abrasion for a hypnotic loop, circling a stalled inertia where nothing shifts and the drizzle simply keeps falling. Brian Molko sketches a lopsided distance, one lover marooned below while the other floats out of reach. The recurring instruction to hold a breath and start over reads less as hope than as the weary rhythm of a life that resets without moving.","sonnet":"A figure trapped in stasis — emotional and physical — while the world above stays unreachable. The grey drizzle of an English summer becomes a metaphor for a depression that never lifts, a cycle of collapse and reset that feels permanent rather than seasonal.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~every-you-every-me":{"haiku":"The song explores self-destructive codependency and mutually harmful love, with both parties bruised and complicit in their relationship's damage.","opus":"A corrosive sketch of compulsive, loveless attachment, sex reduced to a transaction and pain mistaken for tenderness. The narrator admits he's callous and cruel, forever locating a new partner to wound and discard, snared in a monotonous, interchangeable churn of bodies. Placebo's biggest breakthrough, all sneer and self-disgust.","sonnet":"A catalogue of a selfish, destructive pattern in relationships — the narrator admits to bruising everyone they get close to, seeking out love just to abuse it, all while being utterly alone and knowing they are the problem.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~evil-dildo-hidden-track":{"haiku":"Violent sexual threat combining castration, consumption, and physical invasion as a threat delivered through phone messages.","opus":"A hidden track built from a real answering-machine threat, this Placebo oddity reproduces a menacing caller's obscene, violent voicemail nearly verbatim. More prank artifact than song, it weaponizes found audio into pure aggressive dread, sitting in tension with the band's usual glam-tinged melancholy.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~follow-the-cops-back-home":{"haiku":"Detached irony masks spiritual emptiness and social apathy; the speaker and others drift through meaningless consumption and performative rebellion, their words rings hollow against a culture of authentic connection.","opus":"A druggy, dissociated coupledom stuck on rewind gets recast as petty rebellion, following authority home to loot it. Under the glam nihilism it is really about medicated numbness dressing up as a cause it never believed in.","sonnet":"A woozy, cynical sketch of privilege and dissociation — medicated narrators drifting through designer parties and stuck on rewind — that erupts into the darkly comic fantasy of trailing police back to their homes and robbing them, framing institutional authority as just another form of theft.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~for-what-it-s-worth":{"haiku":"A person reflects on love's costs and failures—breaking families, slow decay, and deep emptiness. They end isolated and unloved, questioning whether any sacrifice was worthwhile.","opus":"Bidding goodbye at life's end, the speaker recounts a wasting disease, a broken family, and desperate desire, while observing that no one cares for the destitute and friendless.","sonnet":"The narrator reflects on personal damage — a slow disease that has drained him, a broken family, near-death — and offers himself to someone with an emptiness inside, positioning the relationship as the only thing left in a life that has otherwise left him alone and scraping by.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~forever-chemicals":{"haiku":"Memory-altering substances erase identity and connection, leaving the speaker numb and absent from their own existence.","opus":"A desolate account of blotting yourself out with memory-killing drugs until nothing registers, no one's watching and you barely exist, unable even to rise from bed. Off Placebo's 2022 Never Let Me Go, the frigid electronics and Molko's flat delivery reframe self-medication as a betrayal you commit against yourself, numbness passed off as peace.","sonnet":"Memory is dissolving under the weight of something pharmaceutical and personal — the speaker can't hold images, can't get out of bed, and the comfortable numbness of caring about nothing starts to feel like the only relief. The people calling themselves friends are indistinguishable from enemies, and not being present has become the closest thing to peace.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~haemoglobin":{"haiku":"Survival instinct overrides despair; the speaker asserts embodied defiance against forces seeking erasure and control.","opus":"A near-lynching survivor's delirious defiance, told from inside a body cut down and clinging to life; blood becomes both literal lifeline and the fuel for a paranoid, unkillable will to fight the whole world back.","sonnet":"A survivor of a near-death experience — cut down from a tree, resuscitated by doctors breaking laws of science — arrives at the darkly comic conviction that blood itself, haemoglobin, is the key to everything, and comes back from the edge defiant, incoherent, and determined to make the world scared of him.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~i-ll-be-yours":{"haiku":"Unconditional devotion and transformation into sustaining elements for a suffering partner, willing to be everything for them.","opus":"A vow of total devotion where the speaker offers to become water, ether, liquor and anchor in turn, dissolving every boundary of role between parent, lover and protector. Placebo push their androgynous tenderness to an extreme, promising to bathe a suffering beloved clean and drown their demons. The imagery of pure liquids curing rather than intoxicating turns addiction language into something oddly redemptive.","sonnet":"Total, almost suffocating devotion: a speaker who offers to become every elemental thing a suffering person needs — water, air, anchor, liquor — promising to drown their demons and absorb their pain, taking on every parental, romantic, and protective role at once.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~infra-red":{"haiku":"Inevitable predatory pursuit and inescapable retribution; the speaker hunts someone through darkness with vision no obstacle can stop.","opus":"Brian Molko turns heartbreak into a predator's stalk, the jilted lover reborn as something that hunts in the dark and promises the target no escape. The wounded glam-rock outsider weaponising his vulnerability into pure vengeful menace.","sonnet":"A song of cold, relentless pursuit — the narrator tells a specific person that no matter where they run, they will be found; the threat is intimate and methodical, tracking them in the dark like a hunter with night vision.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~julien":{"haiku":"Addiction and consequence consume Julien in slow self-destruction; no escape, only spiraling dependence.","opus":"Placebo, long fluent in songs about drugs and self-ruin, address an addict by name with anthemic urgency. The verdict is merciless: he is killing himself in slow motion, no one escapes this, and the high has become a smothering weight that no dose can satisfy. Genuine concern delivered as a sentence there is no fleeing.","sonnet":"A warning delivered too late to Julien — the drugs are past the point where they help, past the point where ten of anything provides relief, and what began as an escape has become an avalanche. No one gets out alive, the speaker says, and what looks like riding it out is actually a slow-motion dismantling of everything Julien had left.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~kings-of-medicine":{"opus":"Grief and betrayal tangled with liquor and loss, bodies iced down before day's end while whiskey ruins the best-laid plans. Placebo's narrator begs an absent guiding light not to abandon him, appealing to doctors who have lost their power. What remains is only the empty hours, medicine helpless against a human wound.","sonnet":"Two people destroyed by alcoholism together — the narrator mourns a relationship wrecked by drinking, where plans fell apart and directions were lost, and pleads desperately not to be abandoned without a guiding light while acknowledging that medicine itself has no answers left.","src":"sonnet"},
"placebo~kitty-litter":{"haiku":"Desire erodes a fortress of love; two bodies seek physical and emotional transformation.","opus":"Placebo, Brian Molko's British trio, fuse craving and collapse in one breath: a partner's movements electrify the narrator even as the emotional structure between them falls apart in their separation. His recurring urge to shed and remake himself signals a restlessness the attraction can't satisfy, lust and dissolution braided together with taut, glossy urgency.","sonnet":"Desire and separation pulling against each other — the physical presence of someone dancing ignites everything, while their absence causes the internal fortress to crack and come down. The speaker oscillates between urgent want and the hollow ache of distance, convinced that only proximity, only touch, can hold what is already falling apart.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~meds":{"haiku":"Mental dissolution erases agency and memory while pleasure compounds confusion; the speaker detaches into numbness, confronted by the consequences of forgetting who they were.","opus":"Addiction and mental unraveling told through a couple falling apart, where forgetting your medication stands in for a mind slipping its moorings. Sex, drugs and their consequences blur memory until names and faces and the meaning of a shared vow all dissolve.","sonnet":"A narrator slides between disorientation and tenderness, forgetting names, standing on a ledge, letting sex and drugs and their complications blur everything, and the repeated question — \"baby, did you forget to take your meds?\" — hovers between concern and accusation.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~my-sweet-prince":{"haiku":"A person laments their dependence on heroin—the 'sweet prince' and 'dragon'—describing the desperation and shame of addiction as they contemplate abstinence.","opus":"Brian Molko's devastating conflation of a lover and heroin, the sweet prince being the drug that chases pain away as surely as it destroys, desire and shame braided into every verse. A love song to addiction that never lets you decide which lover it means.","sonnet":"A love song to heroin — the dragon, the needle, the fix — describing addiction as both a romantic attachment and a source of shame, the drug addressed as a sweet prince who takes the pain away but costs everything.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~nancy-boy":{"haiku":"Gender and sexuality blend in casual hedonism; a androgynous, drug-fueled figure pursues pleasure through narcissistic role reversal.","opus":"Placebo's breakthrough single flaunts drugs, anonymous sex, cross-dressing and gender-bending as an unapologetic anthem, a new lover nightly and a cheerful addict blurring roles into a single obsessive muse. Brian Molko's androgynous provocation made it a nineties lightning rod, its sneering swagger and hooky chorus turning queer, narcotic excess into a taboo-baiting celebration.","sonnet":"A portrait of gleeful transgression and fluid identity — the Nancy Boy is promiscuous, chemically altered, gender-indeterminate, and entirely unbothered by any of it. The speaker finds the whole thing irresistible, a muse spinning through every role at once, and the song catalogues the chaos with affection rather than judgment.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~nancy-boy-re-created-version":{"haiku":"Gender fluidity and role reversal disrupt stability; the muse circulates universally, spinning desire.","opus":"A glam, gender-blurring portrait of hedonistic nightlife, casual sex and drug use that provoked its era. The narcotic muse and role reversal flaunt androgyny as both seduction and satire.","sonnet":"A hedonistic portrait of a gender-fluid, sexually fluid figure — alcoholic moods, insect buzzes, a different partner every night — in which the muse spins universally above it all while the subject does their makeup, douses themselves in cheap perfume, and becomes \"the greatest lay I ever had.\"","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~narcoleptic":{"haiku":"Emotional paralysis and creative stagnation alternate with fragmented resistance; awakening demands confronting buried pain and loss.","opus":"A relationship dying by slow neglect, framed as a sickness that must be cut out before it's too late; the warning to wake up is aimed at a lover sleepwalking toward ruin, and maybe at the singer himself.","sonnet":"A relationship asymmetry is drawn precisely: the speaker stumbles, writes sentences, sits on fences while the other person talks in paragraphs and frames photographs — and the growing distance between them reads like a tumor that, if removed later rather than sooner, will leave someone permanently asleep and unable to wake up.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~one-of-a-kind":{"haiku":"Isolation and uniqueness align as the speaker refuses conformity, claiming singular authenticity despite the cost of belonging. Solitude becomes achievement rather than penalty.","opus":"An outsider's shrug at both life's summit and its lowest rung, insisting on singular self-possession while feeling alone even in a crowd, the skies always breaking.","sonnet":"A solitary outsider — always at the back of the class, alone in a crowd, out of the womb and into the void — insists on their singular existence against a world that alienates and annoys, the repeated \"I am one\" equal parts defiance and isolation.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~peeping-tom":{"haiku":"Voyeuristic obsession sustains an empty self; the speaker infiltrates another's space seeking height and wholeness while remaining rootless.","opus":"An addict and outsider clinging to someone who leaves him feeling bigger than his ruined self; exposed, godless and frightened, he confesses his voyeuristic remove and the drink and dependency that keep him small.","sonnet":"A self-aware outsider — isolated, addicted to alcohol and drugs, scared and faithless — fixates on someone who makes him feel larger than he is, confessing his voyeurism and his weightlessness: he has nothing left to lose and everything to fear, and this person fills the hole without him being able to reach them properly.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~plasticine":{"haiku":"Authenticity versus selling out; beauty and desire are corrupted when souls are surrendered for self-esteem, so the singer warns against becoming false and malleable.","opus":"Molko's plea to stay authentic in a world that rewards the shallow, refusing to trade your soul for self-esteem, insisting real beauty lives in the youthful heart that won't mold itself into pliable, hollow plasticine.","sonnet":"A song urging the listener to stay authentic and resist the pressure to perform or sell out — real beauty comes from not trading your soul for self-esteem, and the only reliable thing is remaining exactly who you are.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~post-blue":{"haiku":"Contamination—chemical, sexual, genetic—saturates all connections; breaking free from exploitative love requires enduring pain.","opus":"An addiction that seeps into everything shared between two people, dissolving intimacy and dependency into a single tainted stream. The vow to ruin love outright hardens into a mantra of destructive devotion. Placebo drive the taut, propulsive track on Brian Molko's sneer, drug and lover blurred into the same poison.","sonnet":"Love and addiction mapped onto each other until they become the same — whatever this connection is, it runs through the water, through the pills, through inheritance and frequency, impossible to locate in any single place. The speaker is destroyed by it and chooses destruction anyway, breaking themselves against it until there is nothing left to give.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~protect-me-from-what-i-want":{"haiku":"Modern life traps people in hollow consumption and corporate cycles, corrupting desire itself into something destructive that requires protection.","opus":"A comedown snapshot of the rave generation, chasing the very thing that's hollowing them out as clubbers stagger home while the corporate world stirs to life. Placebo borrow a museum artwork's slogan to voice a lament for lovers left stranded, the euphoria souring into guilt and the recognition that desire itself is the trap.","sonnet":"Two people consumed by desire and bad choices arrive at the same ruin — parties, substances, and transgressions adding up to a sentence rather than a life. The hook becomes a genuine prayer: the things most craved are precisely what destroy, and no one has the will to stop wanting them.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~pure-morning":{"fable":"A comedown-morning litany of friendship: the proverb 'a friend in need' rewritten over and over — a friend with weed, with breasts, in leather, one who bleeds — until the joke settles into sincerity: this friend passed the test, and they will never sever.","fableDeep":"The engine is a nursery rhyme run through a hangover: one proverb, endlessly re-punchlined, against a refrain — 'day's dawning, skin's crawling' — that fixes the scene as the strung-out hour after the night ends. The variations escalate from stoner joke to bodily frankness ('a friend who bleeds is better' — intimacy with a real body, menstruation and all, over any idealized one), and 'boxing clever… passed the test… never sever' grades the friendship by wit and endurance rather than romance. The 'pure' of the title is earned backwards: nothing about the morning is pure except the one bond that survived to see it.","haiku":"Complexity of friendship intertwined with sexual desire and physical intimacy, celebrating unconventional bonds that blur boundaries.","opus":"A playful, repetitive ode to the value of a true friend, cataloguing the many kinds of friends who make life better. It circles a bond with a woman who passed some test, set against a strung-out morning of dawning day and crawling skin.","sonnet":"A catalogue of what the ideal companion looks like — someone who teases, bleeds, confesses, and endures — building to a sense of hard-won, unbreakable bond that arrives with the rawness of early morning.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~scared-of-girls":{"haiku":"Deception and lust entangle an introverted speaker who admits to being a liar in bed with an extroverted woman, careening toward transgression.","opus":"Brian Molko's queasy vignettes of sex, sin and gender play, the narrator confessing himself a guaranteed liar in bed while women careen through the frame. Beneath the swagger runs the unease the title names, desire tangled with fear.","sonnet":"A fragmented, oblique sketch of carnal encounters — a man who lies his way into bed, a sinful next of kin, a girl with a blister where he kissed her thigh — suggestive, amoral, and deliberately incomplete.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~second-sight":{"haiku":"The speaker's cursed ability to foresee betrayal and jealousy makes intimacy impossible as partners flee before conflict strikes. Second sight prevents genuine connection.","opus":"Brian Molko circling a doomed, jealous entanglement where one lover keeps fleeing to protect their pride. The gift of second sight becomes a curse of seeing the ending in advance yet staying anyway, hostage to feeling.","sonnet":"A tense post-breakup confrontation: the narrator is cursed with the ability to see through his partner's performance — their lack of genius, their acting, their jealousy — and tells them to walk away before the morning after exposes them completely.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~slave-to-the-wage":{"haiku":"Liberation requires decisive flight from systemic imprisonment; breaking free demands accepting instability over complicit stagnation.","opus":"A revolt against the rat race, the deadening grind of work as a maze built for rodents to die in; the Dylan nod frames the escape as refusing servitude, urging a clean break toward something freer.","sonnet":"An escape fantasy from the rat race of modern labor: the maze of work is just a death trap, the farm just exploitation, and the only answer is to run — abandon boredom, abandon whoredom, wave your cares goodbye with a single decisive act of will. The song offers no destination, only the urgency of leaving.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~sleeping-with-ghosts":{"haiku":"A meditation on environmental collapse, religious hypocrisy, and the belief that spiritual connection transcends death.","opus":"The title track of Placebo's fourth album pairs apocalyptic imagery with tenderness: as oceans vanish and skies erupt, the narrator comforts a grieving partner with the belief that a true bond outlasts death. Political anger cuts through the intimacy, cursing rulers who kill and deceive and asking what use faith is among people who loathe one another.","sonnet":"Against a backdrop of environmental collapse and political despair — seas drying, skies exploding, religions curdling into mutual contempt — two people hold onto each other with the conviction that their bond outlasts the world's failures. Soulmates, the song insists, cannot be killed by any of this.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~something-rotten":{"haiku":"A person mourns loss and decay in a dark place, expressing nostalgia and sadness that everything has been forgotten and left to rot.","opus":"Built on a queasy pun about a corpse-like decay, this circles a longing that festers rather than fades, missing someone so acutely it becomes physical rot. Placebo let repetition of a downward direction and a broken crown suggest fallen dignity and buried memory. Everything is meant to be forgotten, yet the stink of what remains keeps rising through the loop, refusing to stay interred.","sonnet":"Grief from below. A speaker feels buried, face-down, missing someone with a fierce, almost putrid ache — something has decayed irreparably, and the refrain of forgetting and rot circles back without resolution, the crown of the relationship broken and foul.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~song-to-say-goodbye":{"haiku":"A friend's self-destructive spiral—marked by addiction and wasted talent—leaves the speaker unable to save them, and the relationship becomes a farewell. The song grieves what was lost to addiction.","opus":"Placebo's Brian Molko confronts a friend lost to addiction with harsh tenderness, calling them a squandered life even as he forces his way in to save them. Once gifted with a voice that moved him deeply, they're now ravaged by the needle and beyond reach, drawn toward oblivion. The title's dread looms: if he can't rouse them, all that's left is a farewell.","sonnet":"A speaker tries to reach someone drowning in addiction — someone gifted and once beloved — while admitting the affection has curdled into exhaustion. The intervention is driven not by love exactly but by the knowledge that without it, the only remaining artifact will be a farewell song neither of them wanted to need.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~speak-in-tongues":{"opus":"Longing that survives a violent history, the narrator burning to touch someone he shares wreckage with. Placebo braids desire with resistance, urging a lover not to surrender to decay or the people who would have their way. Beneath the ache runs a stubborn faith that a new tomorrow can be built today.","sonnet":"Longing for a woman with a history of violence between them that has not killed the desire — the narrator wants her back despite everything, and the song pivots into a refusal to let outside forces dictate that desire, calling for building something new rather than surrendering to decay.","src":"sonnet"},
"placebo~special-k":{"haiku":"The speaker experiences addiction and dependency, comparing their lover to a drug that destroys them—offering temporary relief but ultimately trapping them in gravity's pull.","opus":"A love addiction rendered through the drug it borrows its name from, a partner who lands like an anesthetic, a heart-stopping menace and fatal weakness, a rescuer who might instead be an omen of doom, no breaking free of the pull that keeps dragging him down. Placebo build the metaphor into one of their sharpest hooks, Brian Molko's crash-and-crave delivery making devotion indistinguishable from a high he can't quit.","sonnet":"A person used as a controlled substance — their arrival produces the same crash-and-surge as dissociatives, the same gravity that pulls the speaker flat to the ground every time they appear. There is no exit from the sinking sand, no escaping the weight, and the comparison between love and a chemical high makes both sound equally consuming and equally ruinous.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~special-needs":{"haiku":"Broken dreams and abandonment; the speaker, left disabled while former companions achieve fame, feels forgotten despite once sharing ambitions.","opus":"A bitter address to a lover who chased fame and left him behind, half plea and half curse to be remembered when the flashbulbs come. Molko fixes it in that ruinous window of being nineteen and disposable, love mistaken for ambition's stepping stone.","sonnet":"A bitter, ironic goodbye to someone who's going to be famous — the narrator imagines her on the silver screen while he's stuck in a wheelchair, sardonic about the naive 19-year-old he was when they were together.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~summer-s-gone":{"haiku":"A person attempts to break free from constraints before aging traps them, seeking connection and trying to escape loneliness through song and shared experience.","opus":"Placebo, connoisseurs of glam-tinged gloom, sing to a despairing, tearful figure straining to break free of their mold before age or mortality closes in. Panic and pain keep meeting the same startled goodbye. Performing for a waiting lover is likened to wringing water from rock, and the reassurance that one is never truly alone drifts, chillingly, toward keeping company with the dead.","sonnet":"A person presses toward reinvention before age forecloses it, yet the urgency is shadowed by loss and the nearness of death. Singing becomes the only act left — for a lover present or absent, for a life already draining dry. The dead wait at the other end, and the song is both defiance and farewell.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~surrounded-by-spies":{"haiku":"Paranoia and isolation punctuated by surreal, fragmented imagery. The speaker feels watched and betrayed, searching for meaning while slowly losing trust.","opus":"A paranoid collage of surveillance-age unease, cutting between a blaze, a menacing object left on a platform, a leader on the operating table and a corpse on the anthem, all orbiting the refrain that chasing meaning is destroying him. From Placebo's 2013 Loud Like Love, it channels post-Snowden anxiety into clipped, repeated images, Molko's staccato delivery making constant monitoring feel like a slow nervous breakdown.","sonnet":"A paranoid collage of disconnected images — jumps from burning buildings, dead bandmates, world leaders under the knife, a cab to Sweden — that accumulate into an unshakeable feeling of surveillance and betrayal. The heart given away is now demanded back, and faces keep appearing on bathroom floors when there's no one around to ask for help.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~the-bitter-end":{"haiku":"Two people journey together through a synchronized but deteriorating relationship toward an inevitable ending, with one feeling that the mess left behind will eventually conclude.","opus":"Brian Molko chronicles a relationship burning out through numbness and repetition, every synchronized step marching toward a foreseen collapse. The bitter end is met with weary resignation, love decaying on schedule.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship spiraling toward its inevitable, pre-determined collapse — the narrator traces memories of following someone home, watching them walk away, and counts down to what feels like a killing time, meeting them at a bitter end that feels like suicide stretched over weeks.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~the-crawl":{"haiku":"A person trapped in addiction struggles to stay with a lover; they explore themes of pain relief, physical attachment, and the internal collapse of a failing relationship as both individuals metaphorically crawl through its wreckage.","opus":"Molko's brittle account of a love too broken to repair and a self reduced to crawling. The addiction-tinged imagery frames intimacy as something transactional and painkilling, numbing without ever making anyone stay.","sonnet":"About the aftermath of a breakup fueled by substance use — whatever numbed the pain couldn't keep the person from leaving, and now the narrator is left to crawl through the lie of what they thought they had.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~the-never-ending-why":{"haiku":"Collapse and tragedy accumulate while time offers no answers to unanswerable questions. The song accepts that suffering and loss occur without reason or resolution.","opus":"Existential dread set against apocalyptic imagery, two people rebelling and cursing the world while wondering if this is all there is. The answer offered is bleak comfort: time heals but never explains the unanswerable questions.","sonnet":"A song about living alongside unanswerable existential questions — watching the world deteriorate (tumors, bodies in lakes, silent rot) and finding that time never actually provides the answers it promises.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~this-picture":{"haiku":"A memory of an ashtray girl haunts the speaker; attempts to hold onto her fade as age and loss transform everything into broken fragments and disappearance.","opus":"Brian Molko mourns a self-destructive muse marked by burns and ruinous habits, a portrait of loving someone who's coming apart out of dread of aging. The song grieves her before she's even lost, tenderness and helplessness tangled in the watching.","sonnet":"A eulogy for a self-destructive young woman — the ashtray girl, marked by cigarette burns and mental illness, is gone, and the narrator grieves her disintegration while acknowledging her fear of aging and the open wound her absence leaves.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~too-many-friends":{"haiku":"Digital connection multiplies contact into isolation, creating vast networks of unreachable faces while the narrator drowns in the paradox of abundance and loneliness.","opus":"A lament for connection dissolved by the internet age, where a phone full of contacts leaves you responsible to countless people you'll never actually meet or be present for. Molko blames the applications themselves for a modern loneliness, using a phone's mistaken guess about his identity to mark how little the machines understand of us.","sonnet":"A lament about social media and digital connectivity breeding a surfeit of shallow, virtual relationships — too many people the speaker will never truly be there for — while everyone stares into phones and the speaker feels more alone than ever.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~try-better-next-time":{"haiku":"Cyclical failure and animal complicity structure existence; the narrator awakens repeatedly to the same patterns, urging improvement while inhabiting futility.","opus":"A bleak cosmic shrug at human insignificance, animals dancing unaware they're headed for slaughter, the singer feeling born out of time and hosting a monster he drinks loose; grief and the whole world's weight collapse into one indifferent shrug.","sonnet":"A sprawling, darkly whimsical tour of disconnection — animals dancing unaware they'll become meat, a monster that emerges when the speaker drinks, the weight of tears equalling the weight of the world — that keeps waking itself up and urging itself to do better, before fantasising about returning to the ocean and becoming something else entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~twin-demons":{"haiku":"Mental agitation and demonic possession disorder the mind; the speaker crashes repeatedly into walls, unable to escape the demons inhabiting consciousness.","opus":"Addiction rendered as a mind bitten and possessed, the restless Buddhist monkey mind speeding toward catastrophe; the escape plan is numbness, soaking body and soul in whatever kills feeling, with life itself on the line.","sonnet":"An agitated, accelerating mind possessed by its own demons keeps crashing into walls, seeking numbness through soaking the skin and soul until sensation disappears entirely — the monkey-mind is devotion, fixation, and catastrophe all at once.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~without-you-i-m-nothing":{"haiku":"The speaker's identity and worth collapse without romantic connection; they lose agency and voice in the relationship, becoming entirely dependent on the other's presence. Codependence is total.","opus":"The title track of Placebo's second album welds codependence to an anxious, clock-like pulse. Molko casts himself as tainted and dissolute, losing his voice whenever his partner lashes out, drifting away yet somehow being kept alive by them. His plans go awry and he crumbles, landing on the bleak conclusion that without this person he amounts to nothing, need swallowing whatever pride remains.","sonnet":"A self-described libertine who loses the power of speech in the presence of another acknowledges total dependency — the other person cultivates him like a plant while he slips further from their reach. The refrain strips pretension away: without this particular presence, there is simply nothing left of the speaker to speak of.","src":"haiku"},
"placebo~you-don-t-care-about-us":{"haiku":"Apathy and emotional abandonment in partnership; one person is too complicated while the other spirals in rage, and separation becomes inevitable as trust crumbles.","opus":"Brian Molko turns a collapsing relationship into a wider indictment of neglect, where a generation gap and mutual resentment curdle into betrayal. The real subject is the exhausting realization that trust has run out and love was never mutual.","sonnet":"A song about being in a relationship with someone emotionally absent and volatile — the narrator is left dealing with rage and scarred memories while the other person accelerates away, indifferent to the damage.","src":"haiku"},
"plaiins~nothing-s-gonna-end-us-till-the-aliens-do-it":{"haiku":"Defiant fatalism: humans remain locked in pleasure-seeking and destructive patterns, unstoppable until forces beyond comprehension force reckoning.","opus":"Chasing dopamine and defiance against end-of-the-world dread, insisting nothing can finish us off but some absurd cosmic joke. Doom is met with a shrug and a fist in the air.","sonnet":"A song about apocalyptic anxiety — the speaker is cracking under pressure, not from divine judgment or political collapse, but from the sheer absurdity of it all, concluding that only alien intervention could actually end humanity.","src":"haiku"},
"plastic-dagger~my-pointy-teeth":{"haiku":"In a castle dreamscape, the speaker describes skeletons, bats, and teeth as they fall forever into an abyss.","opus":"A gothic mood piece more atmosphere than plot: murmuring walls, skeletons dragging across the floor, bats overhead, fangs in a dreamed-up castle. The narrator savors the vertigo of descending past return, tumbling endlessly toward the void as pure vampiric ambience.","sonnet":"A playful vampire-adjacent reverie — the narrator dreams in a castle surrounded by bats and skeletons, proudly displays their fangs, and accepts the sensation of falling endlessly into an abyss.","src":"haiku"},
"plastic-girl-in-closet~error":{"haiku":"Summer night drives circle back to old selves, palms fading like missed futures. Every vow breaks under time's pressure—the promise to return never materializes, leaving only the weight of unshed tears.","opus":"A wistful Japanese shoegaze reverie of a third summer's dawn drive, a whitening crescent moon pressing memory against the chest. A pinky-promise vow to drive to the sea next summer becomes the couple's mistake, a future that faded like the days that resembled it. Tender and melancholy, it dwells on broken promises and the quiet ache of what can no longer be undone.","sonnet":"On a third summer's dawn drive, a fading crescent moon and unresolved feelings press on the chest as the speaker revisits a broken promise — \"next summer, a drive to the sea\" — and watches that pinky-sworn oath burn over a scorched hill, leaving only the confession \"I\" trapped mid-sentence, unable to finish.","src":"haiku"},
"playboi-carti~iloveuihateu":{"haiku":"Substance abuse accelerates internal contradiction as the narrator swings between affection and cruelty, numbing pain through pills while inflicting damage on those nearby.","opus":"A hedonism laced with genuine death-drive, Carti narrating a codeine-and-pill haze where pleasure and self-destruction blur; the warning to keep your distance is the tender, damaged core under the flexing, love and self-harm tangled together.","sonnet":"A self-destructive hedonism spiral — the speaker numbs contradictory feelings of desire and warning through codeine, pills, and sex, telling a partner to keep their distance because what they don't know won't hurt them.","src":"haiku"},
"playboi-carti~new-tank":{"haiku":"Criminality, hedonism, and self-destructive excess blur into a lifestyle of immediate gratification fueled by drugs and money, devoid of meaning or restraint.","opus":"Pure menacing flex, guns, lean, robbery and reputation stacked into a rage-adjacent trap chant; the bravado is the whole point, a persona built on being untouchable, high, and past caring what anyone thinks.","sonnet":"A boastful street-life vignette of weaponry, luxury cars, drug use, and crew loyalty, delivered with rapid-fire swagger and little narrative development.","src":"haiku"},
"played-by-kings~destruction":{"haiku":"Inevitability of being destroyed by those in power, casualty of someone else's ambition.","opus":"A metalcore rebirth anthem where collapse becomes liberation — the narrator surrenders, then finds that hitting bottom cuts him loose. He breaks his restraints, declares war and rises alone; the scorn of those who doubted him no longer lands. Ruin reframed as the price of freedom.","sonnet":"Death as liberation and defiance — chains broken, soul ripped free, giving the narrator war rather than silence. Destruction left in the wake is reclaimed as self-determination: \"you buried me into the dirt / you know these words will never hurt.\"","src":"haiku"},
"plenty~a-12du6ef":{"haiku":"A nihilistic meditation on suffering's meaninglessness—sorrow and pain accumulate like sand; why, why, why do we endure?","opus":"Plenty, a project of Straightener's Atsushi Horie, favors intimate, melancholy songcraft, and here that restraint amplifies a refusal of easy comfort. The narrator rejects the consoling story that pain carries meaning, falling like sand and suffocating in a sandstorm, sinking further the harder they struggle, yet insisting on resistance even while confessing the resistance feels void.","sonnet":"A person who rejects the consoling narrative that pain has meaning and suffering has reasons — they are falling like sand, suffocating like a sandstorm, and the harder they struggle the further down they go. The emotional core is the refusal to accept philosophical comfort in the face of genuine despair, and the insistence on continuing to resist even while admitting the resistance feels void.","src":"haiku"},
"pnau~nirvana":{"haiku":"Transcendence through intoxication and sensation replaces philosophical enlightenment with corporeal escape. The speaker seeks nirvana through excess, pursuing euphoria as an end in itself.","opus":"A dance-pop invocation of hedonism and its hollow core, chasing a high pointedly branded after Cobain. Burning, earning and riding the rush through the bloodstream, the revelers are isolated, frozen and lost even mid-celebration. The repeated hunt for bliss becomes both party mantra and quiet lament for the emptiness under the thrill.","sonnet":"A search for transcendence through hedonism — drugs, dancing, euphoria — frames nirvana not as Buddhist peace but as the edge of a high, the 'hit of that Cobain', living with no shame; a second voice offers a more spiritual reading of cells on the same frequency, but both converge on the desire to feel fully alive.","src":"haiku"},
"poe-the-passenger~sweet-disaster":{"haiku":"Waiting and passivity calcify into tragedy; the speaker accepts their role as distant calamity, forever postponing action and connection.","opus":"Resigning oneself to being a distant tragedy in someone's memory, waiting forever for a person who was never going to change. The self is cast as a sweet disaster too addictive to abandon.","sonnet":"A person who has been hiding from resolution and waiting for a storm to pass admits they have become 'a distant tragedy' — caught in a system they cannot ignore, branded pretenders by others — and accepts they will wait forever like a sweet disaster rather than change.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~cradled-in-love":{"haiku":"Unconditional love that protects and sustains; despite life's tempests, devotion cradles and brings joy amid connection.","opus":"The Finnish rock band, known for cinematic emotional sweep, casts love as survival through a storm. Two people gamble everything against the current, each becoming the other's lifeline; the closing instruction is to shed grateful tears rather than sorrowful ones, because simply being alive and held is the mercy worth weeping over.","sonnet":"A love powerful enough to transport the soul is mourned and celebrated simultaneously — the bond endures even as grief threatens. Rather than surrender to sorrow, the speaker carries the love forward as a living inheritance, finding in it proof of continued presence and a reason to keep the flame burning.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~kamikaze-love":{"haiku":"Reckless, self-destructive love that tears both partners down through misunderstanding and stolen moments of false connection.","opus":"The Finnish band leans into theatrical grandeur to cast romance as a headlong crash with no hesitation. The narrator hoards a half-true archive of the affair he can't unlock, drawn to a lover who concedes nothing, lifting him up only to level him. Both let it burn to salvage another shard of remembered ground.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a cycle of fantasy and destruction returns again and again to a love that lifts them only to shatter them. Misunderstanding poisons every moment of closeness, stolen time hijacks reason, and the reckless surrender — exhilarating and ruinous in equal measure — keeps restarting with no exit.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~late-goodbye":{"haiku":"A couple drives into the night fleeing consequences of crossed lines and deception, with the devil's delight in their fate as they age and weaken.","opus":"Finnish band Poets of the Fall build a nocturnal drive out of failed schemes and deceptions, headlights sweeping past roadside debris and glinting animal eyes while signs rush by. A boundary is crossed with someone who gives the narrator strength in weakness, but a grinning devil and an aging reflection darken it, the parting endlessly deferred as the car pushes on.","sonnet":"Two people drive through the night past road signs and animal eyes, haunted by a crossing they should not have made and a past they cannot outrun. The devil in the rearview is their own complicity; the goodbye arrives too late to be clean, and the headlights keep pushing forward because stopping is no longer an option.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~lift":{"haiku":"Fear and self-doubt create circular patterns in daily life; yet love and another person's presence elevate the speaker spiritually, enabling flight and wonder.","opus":"A song about someone who steadies a faltering, fearful mind, the narrator afraid his words come out wrong as days spin in circles, until this presence lifts his spirit and lets him touch the moon. Love here is rescue from disappearing. Poets of the Fall are a Finnish rock band best known for their cinematic, melodic anthems and game soundtracks, and this soaring chorus is exactly their brand of earnest, uplifting arena romance.","sonnet":"Anxiety about communication — words going wrong before they're spoken, circling the same days without progress — broken open by a relationship that provides actual altitude. The fear of disappearing, of bleeding silently, recedes when the other person is present; they become the force that makes everything feel worth continuing.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~morning-tide":{"haiku":"Two lovers sail away together on the morning tide, leaving sorrow behind and surrendering to the ocean's call.","opus":"A tender invitation to head down to the water at dawn, raise sails against the breaking swell, and leave grief and ego behind for a new life, love pledged as something enduring. Even if the deep summons them by name, the answer is to cross the water side by side. Poets of the Fall, the Finnish band, give it swelling cinematic warmth, sorrow and solace borne out on the same rising water.","sonnet":"An invitation to leave grief behind by setting sail at dawn — walking to the shore before anyone else wakes, hoisting sails into uncertain water, trusting that the ocean's depth is less frightening than whatever has been left on land. Love persists through the crossing, present whether the tide brings them back to shore or carries them somewhere else entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~running-out-of-time":{"haiku":"Two people run desperately through time, their relationship fed by aggression and heartache, clinging to yesterday while feeling they are crazy and running out of moments.","opus":"A frantic, overwhelming avowal of love in which the singer comes apart before a partner whose insight sears through him. A repeated dread of the clock draining away captures his terror of losing what he has, even as he welcomes each pang that comes with it.","sonnet":"An urgent, breathless confession of love mixed with aggression and self-awareness — the narrator can't lie still waiting and admits that their own intensity, their refusal to bow to authority, drives them to run recklessly toward the other person.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~show-me-this-life":{"haiku":"Lost in choices and unable to decide, the speaker seeks guidance from another person to understand life through love rather than miracle or redemption.","opus":"A directionless man asking a lover not for miracles but simply to show him how to live and be loved; the deeper read is willing self-deception—he'd happily accept her lies and razor-edged affection because the intimacy makes existence bearable.","sonnet":"A song about needing another person to teach you how to live — the narrator is paralyzed by too many choices and too little self-knowledge, and offers himself to a relationship that might guide him even if it involves deception.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~skin":{"haiku":"A love story of deep connection that falls apart, with the speaker unable to move on, feeling the absent lover marked permanently on their soul.","opus":"A romance ended yet permanently marked on the body: a dwelling raised from shared hopes, now soured, its rooms crowded with joint memories whose entrance no longer returns him to that bond. The ache is that the loss runs beneath the surface, that mere passing days cannot mend him, and that he readied himself for compromise exactly when she departed.","sonnet":"A love that left a permanent mark — the person is tattooed on the narrator's skin even though the relationship is over, every room of the shared house holds the memory, and the final irony is that by the time the narrator was ready to meet them halfway, the other person had already walked away.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~temple-of-thought":{"haiku":"A lover becomes a sanctuary in the mind; devotion promises to calm chaos and dry tears, merging bodies and souls into shared spiritual solace.","opus":"A devotional love song that places the beloved at the sacred center of the narrator's inner world. The whole thing is a vow of tender care, promising to still her turmoil, ease her grief, and carry off everything that frightens her. Love offered as the return for how completely she inhabits his mind.","sonnet":"A deeply felt love song where the narrator positions himself as someone who will absorb and contain all of his partner's fears and sorrows — she is the center of his entire inner world, and the relationship is presented as a kind of spiritual sanctuary.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~the-distance":{"haiku":"Physical distance creates longing; despite separation, two people still feel each other's presence and hope that time will reunite their hearts.","opus":"A long-distance love song built on paradox, insisting that being apart somehow pulls two people nearer, the memory of a lover still vivid across the gap. It clings to a promised future when they'll be in sync if they can just endure. Finnish rock band Poets of the Fall lean into swelling, hopeful melodrama, the separation kept as a tender open wound.","sonnet":"Long-distance longing held steady by sense memory — the name, the taste, the tears through rain, the burning — rather than by any hope of reunion. The speaker insists the distance itself creates closeness, and the promise they offer is patience: there will be a time when the hearts beat the same, across the gap.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~the-lie-eternal":{"haiku":"A speaker seducing someone into a deceptive romantic bond, promising to heal and complete them while acknowledging the relationship is built on illusion and manipulation.","opus":"A surrender to an intoxicating love that the speaker knows is also a lie, falling into another's embrace where all mistakes feel forgiven and only that person can wound or heal him.","sonnet":"A lover recognises that the intense relationship he has fallen into is built on illusion, yet cannot pull away because only this person's presence makes the world feel real and bearable.","src":"haiku"},
"poets-of-the-fall~war":{"haiku":"Wartime bond between soldiers—memory of shared front-line struggle, lost innocence, and the partner who gave the fighting meaning when all seemed pointless.","opus":"A battle recalled as both literal and psychological, where the presence of one true ally transformed a pointless fight into one worth waging. The Finnish band turn the conflict into a plea for realness in a world of masked, hollowed-out people, the beloved's identity alone standing as the reason any of it mattered.","sonnet":"A veteran of some internal or external conflict revisits the battlefield with someone whose presence gave the fighting meaning. Partnership in war — whether emotional or literal — is what separated purpose from pointlessness, and the memory of shared struggle now becomes the only ground on which healing can begin.","src":"haiku"},
"point-north~into-the-dark-feat-kellin-quinn":{"haiku":"A person drowns emotionally in another's pain, treading through dark waters while searching for light and meaning they cannot find alone.","opus":"A wounded lover both drawn to and drowning in a toxic bond ('I like it when I'm drowning'), fighting not to lose himself; the deeper read is post-breakup ambivalence—he claims to have risen phoenix-like from being replaced, yet still reaches for the hand that keeps pulling him into the dark.","sonnet":"A song about the competing pulls of an old love and the need to move on — the narrator drowns in the past, watches his ex with someone new, and eventually rises from that grief like a phoenix, still needing a sunrise to orient himself.","src":"haiku"},
"point-north~safe-and-sound":{"haiku":"Safety dissolves under invisible pressure as the speaker drowns in another's weight despite isolation.","opus":"A pop-punk study of anxiety and a relationship that sinks him instead of saving him, his calm souring into paranoia he can't reset like an hourglass. Grim news comes through the night as his refuge runs dry and someone he loves drags him down like wet concrete — a fight to break free and reach air and shelter held just out of reach.","sonnet":"Another person functions as concrete on the speaker's feet — dragging them down, refusing to pull them out — as the once-safe emotional ground burns away and every sacred thing sits just out of arm's reach.","src":"haiku"},
"poison-the-well~everything-hurts":{"haiku":"Shared trauma creates false intimacy; the speaker shoulders everyone's pain only to be attacked for the weight they carry.","opus":"A metalcore meditation on shared pain and fused identity, the narrator claiming he's carried another's suffering and that the two of them are one and the same. Tempers flare and the other lashes out, yet the bond holds to the point of living and dying inside each other — mutual wounding treated as a strange, inseparable intimacy.","sonnet":"Two people shouldering each other's pain discover they are indistinguishable in suffering — we're just the same, I have my moments too — but the other's confusion ignites and lashes out, diminishing everything to shit rather than accepting the symmetry.","src":"haiku"},
"pokaysia~ko-n":{"haiku":"Sensory overload and invasive thoughts fragment consciousness into surreal degradation.","opus":"A Ukrainian track wrapping obsessive lust in nu-metal and drug imagery, the acid comedown and the crack in the head. Underneath the horny bravado it's about a fixation that won't let a person go even as they flee.","sonnet":"A Ukrainian-language song of cold, acidic desire — the narrator is high in a smoky room, watching someone grind to Korn while being casually cruel and dominant, feeling a crack in their head as some sticky presence lingers through wires, obsessively filling the other person's space and refusing to let them escape.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~above-my-head":{"haiku":"Someone drowns under emotional weight they cannot bear, unable to change destructive patterns while watching relationships collapse.","opus":"Drowning under the weight of a strained relationship and mental exhaustion, confessing the inability to carry it while begging the other person to feel the same fracture; honesty offered too late to fix what's already tearing.","sonnet":"A song about emotional exhaustion in a relationship — the narrator is in over his head, confessing he can't bear the weight, asking whether the other person feels the same breaking pressure, and wondering if what they leave unspoken will eventually destroy what they have.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~all-of-this-is-fleeting":{"haiku":"A hollow life spent wishing and waiting has left the speaker a ghost unable to release their grip despite others begging them to.","opus":"An anguished cry from someone who feels reduced to a hollow, spectral remnant, wondering whether anyone was ever paying attention. The Australian metalcore band turn it into a meditation on the futility of pain, questioning what all the sacrifice is worth if it will be forgotten, while the narrator refuses to let go even after telling another that rescue is impossible.","sonnet":"About doing everything right — straining, bleeding, sacrificing — and finding it meaningless because no one will remember, the narrator a hollow shell of someone who spent themselves completely for something that turned out not to matter.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~casualty":{"haiku":"A condemnation of systemic cruelty and exploitation where the powerful silence dissent and leave the masses to die forgotten; those responsible will never face guilt or judgment.","opus":"Australian metalcore raging at leaders who spend ordinary lives cheaply, the sacrificed masses accusing the tyrants who gave them away, indicting a guilt that can't be washed off while the many die for the few's ego.","sonnet":"About being sacrificed by those in power — the narrator and their people were ground down, bled dry, and abandoned while the guilty raise glasses over the graves; a hymn of collective defiance from the discarded, naming the tyrant who stands over what he caused.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~consume":{"haiku":"Consumer culture creates artificial desires that undermine human contentment. The song critiques how marketing and social pressure manipulate people into perpetual dissatisfaction with themselves.","opus":"A sharp diagnosis of consumer culture as the thief of contentment, manufacturing insecurity so you'll chase a perfection you never needed before you were sold it. The band place the blame squarely on the machine that taught us we're never enough.","sonnet":"Social comparison and consumer culture as slow poison — once people were content; now the algorithm and advertising have manufactured insecurity as a product, and the speaker sees the entire culture as a trap built to keep people hating themselves for not measuring up.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~creatures-of-habit":{"haiku":"A relationship deteriorates as both partners spiral into blame, resentment, and mutual destruction. What once bound them now traps them in endless cycles of harm and regret.","opus":"The Australian metalcore band dissect a relationship curdled into a trap, where the tie that once united two people now just keeps them stuck in a grinding cycle of retaliation. The narrator insists he can't live up to the version of him the other imagines and, worn down, invites them to just finish him off.","sonnet":"Two people in a deteriorating relationship accuse each other of cruelty and control, while the speaker insists he cannot be remade into whatever the other person demands. The house is described as hostile, full of old grievances like ghosts in the walls, and both parties are running out of time before the damage becomes irreversible.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~crooked-path":{"haiku":"Two people are trapped by their past and the weight of the world, falling further as they cling to borrowed time. One reaches out desperately but cannot pull the other up.","opus":"The Australian metalcore act reach toward a friend drowning under life's weight, a hand extended as the flood keeps them apart. The narrator wrestles with inherited helplessness, squandered years and an unanswered plea to know whether he's even wanted there, guilt and love straining against someone who won't let himself be saved.","sonnet":"A speaker watches someone he loves sink under unseen weight — isolation, hidden shame, an identity worn away by years of covering tracks — and cannot understand why the outstretched hand keeps being refused. The song ends not in rescue but in apology, the admission that caring was real even when it was not enough to pull anyone ashore.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~frailty":{"haiku":"Generational trauma and unmet expectations: parents demand change while children bear inherited scars; the cycle perpetuates as bodies are molded and minds distorted, with survival itself altering identity.","opus":"Polaris turn the lens on family, addressing mother, father and brother in an aching confession of feeling beyond rescue. Regarded as an outsider by his own kin, assembled only to be dismantled, the narrator refuses repair and swears he'll snap rather than yield. Imperfection marks them all, and the fear that nothing will return to how it was shadows every bond.","sonnet":"A son addresses a mother, a father-figure, and a fallen brother in turn, tracing how each relationship shaped and damaged him — moulded, renamed, abandoned — until his identity is a site of contested construction rather than a stable self. He will break before he bends, and the people who made him will be witnesses to that.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~hypermania":{"haiku":"A manic, destructive cycle of constant ups and downs: the speaker can't stand still, exhausted by their own frantic energy, questioning if they're human or machine.","opus":"A raw account of restless mania, insomnia and self-destruction, with the narrator picking a fight with fate, questioning whether he's man or machine, and begging to save a soul he no longer recognizes.","sonnet":"The speaker describes an unrelenting mental cycle — crashing highs and lows, nervous energy, substance use, and a shrinking sense of self — that they recognise as a waste of time but cannot stop. Trapped in a rabbit hole of mood swings, they ask to be saved while admitting they may always have been this way.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~in-somnus-veritas":{"haiku":"People are conditioned to obey authority, creating spiritual paralysis. The song questions whether existence itself is merely illusion and what we owe to life if nothing is real.","opus":"Polaris, an Australian metalcore band, trade their usual breakdowns for a brief, atmospheric philosophical pause. The interlude questions how much people have been shaped by their era, and why humanity seems conditioned to crave leaders who only keep it powerless. If existence is largely an illusion, it wonders, what is it worth, and whether our deepest dreads will be eased or confirmed.","sonnet":"A compressed meditation on conditioned helplessness — people raised to be led, kept paralyzed by systems that taught them to follow. The question underneath is whether what they've been told to fear will turn out, when everything collapses, to have been real.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~inhumane":{"haiku":"Detached callousness calcifies as the speaker watches statistics accumulate; craving the rush of pain, they dig deeper into moral numbness while sadism masquerades as philosophy.","opus":"The Australian metalcore band confronts numbness and moral desensitization, frozen in the glare and barely alive and craving any jolt of feeling. Everyone becomes a casualty while cold indifference hardens the living, souls praying to gods who won't answer and dying as statistics. Chasing the reaper and digging deeper for any sensation, it indicts a society gone cold, detached and inhumane.","sonnet":"A speaker numbed to the point of craving a shock to the system watches collective indifference turn lives into statistics — callous, cold, dropping deadweight — and digs deeper into their own pain precisely because the pain no longer feels the way it used to, hunting a feeling that keeps receding.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~landmine":{"haiku":"A person trapped in meaningless routine and existential crisis questions their identity and purpose, expressing fatigue with a system that grinds down the middle class.","opus":"A bleak sketch of suburban exhaustion and vanished self, where each day repeats itself in futile routine and squandered years pile up. The narrator feels exploited and discarded, sentenced to unhappiness by a system that only extracts from him and gives nothing back.","sonnet":"A portrait of middle-class disillusionment: days as broken records, pointless prayers for change, consumer manipulation, and an identity crisis leave a person hollow-headed and explosive, with the song calling on the heavy-hearted to voice their shared misery rather than swallow it.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~lucid":{"haiku":"The narrator traces a path through self-doubt and suffering toward acceptance of love and transformation, willing to sacrifice everything for clarity and connection.","opus":"Someone reframes being destroyed by love as a kind of ascension, bleeding onto the ground just to perceive things vividly. The recurring jibe that he constructed his own cell and hid its key marks the pain as self-made, and he chooses to let the love consume him rather than fight it, wanting the epitaph to record that surrender.","sonnet":"A person has surrendered completely to a love that they know is destroying them — they've built their own prison around it, but consciously choose to let it consume them rather than escape, framing that self-destruction as the only honest thing they've ever done.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~martyr-waves":{"haiku":"Relentless emotional devastation washes over a speaker exhausted by false expectation.","opus":"The exhaustion of being turned into a mouthpiece, everyone counting on you to voice what they can't, and it comes in swells that drag you under. The band wrote it as frontman Jamie Hails buckled under people leaning on him to speak for them, letting the illusion go.","sonnet":"About the exhaustion of being the one everyone turns to when they can't speak for themselves — the narrator has played every role (liar, martyr, coward, saint) and now has nothing left; waves of responsibility keep drowning him, and he's close to the edge of not surviving another one.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~masochist":{"haiku":"Trapped in self-inflicted suffering and addiction to pain, the speaker questions whether they can ever escape their compulsion to sabotage happiness and harm themselves.","opus":"A brutally candid look at depression as self-caused, the narrator wondering whether he is hooked on his own suffering, a sucker for pain who rubs pain into fresh injuries and hauls himself out of the pit only to plunge back in. The hard truth that his life is his to shape, and that his own guarded gloom is the one barrier between him and joy, turns the title into a plea to quit poisoning himself.","sonnet":"A searingly honest self-examination of depression maintained by the person suffering it — the speaker recognizes they are addicted to their own misery, can't be trusted in relationships because of it, and only slowly arrives at the understanding that they alone are the obstacle between themselves and any kind of happiness.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~overflow":{"haiku":"Internal chaos and bottled anguish threaten to erupt; the speaker fractures under pressure and finds no shelter from the noise inside.","opus":"A raw metalcore reckoning with feeling bottled until it spills, the narrator adrift and alone even amid a crowd where quiet turns unbearable. There's no cover when the storm falls inside him, and the remedy proves to be the same toxin under a new name. Dependent, ailing and desperate to mend, he admits he suffocates what he loves by clutching it too hard.","sonnet":"Internal overwhelm floods outward: silence has grown too loud, the antidote feels like a renamed poison, and the speaker admits to strangling the things they love by holding on too tight — the line \"I lost years to future fears\" captures a mind that keeps consuming itself rather than healing.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~pray-for-rain":{"haiku":"A meditation on emotional exhaustion and the hollowness of hope. The speaker has sacrificed everything to no avail, watches hope fade, and contemplates their own corruption and solitude as they await redemption that may never come.","opus":"A meditation on emotional depletion and self-doubt, where the narrator drains himself dry, faces his worst enemy in the mirror, and clings to a last ember of hope while confronting mortality, guilt, and the fear of dying alone.","sonnet":"The narrator describes a slow emotional exhaustion — draining optimism until hollow — and a cynical, faithless view of himself that contrasts with who he once was; he clings to the faint hope that some renewing force will come, like rain extinguishing the fire in him.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~regress":{"haiku":"Societal collapse driven by selfishness and inaction; people hide behind masks while the world burns, betraying each other in search of power.","opus":"Polaris, an Australian metalcore band, marry crushing riffs and gang vocals to political fury, condemning a privileged minority who demand the world bend to their whims, hide behind personas while others suffer, and torch everything just to profit from the fire. The chorus insists this destruction is no advance and that everyone shares the guilt.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of those who stand in the way of change while the world burns — selfish, masked, heads buried, demanding the earth reverse its orbit for their comfort. The song doesn't absolve the speaker either: we are all gears in the machine, all complicit, all waiting for a sign instead of acting.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~relapse":{"haiku":"Chemical dependency and emotional toxicity merge; the speaker craves medication and connection while spiraling in loneliness despite proximity to others.","opus":"Australian metalcore on the isolation buried inside addiction and depression, the narrator hungering for the very substance destroying him and feeling emptiest in the company of someone who cares. The heartbreak is sensing there's still something in him worth rescuing while being too far gone to reach for it.","sonnet":"A song about addiction and emotional stagnation within a relationship — the narrator is sedated and numb, unable to feel real connection even with someone present, and the chemical dependency has become the only source of false clarity.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~sonder":{"haiku":"The privileged ignore the suffering of the marginalized; karma and collective guilt for callousness will prevent any of them from resting peacefully.","opus":"A blistering moral indictment of the comfortable and complacent who ignore the suffering of others and offer sympathy far too late. Buried by pain, the narrator concludes that real strength must come from inside. Sydney metalcore band Polaris name cowards and liars outright, demanding accountability for those abandoned.","sonnet":"A confrontation with collective moral failure — the comfortable standing on the broken homes of those they have dismissed, karma coiling at their throats while compassion arrives too late to mean anything. Buried under the weight, the speaker tunnels toward light, finding it only internal, and then turns outward to name the cowards who sold out their own and called it survival.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~the-descent":{"haiku":"Temptation toward suicide in hospital silence; a voice calls downward offering peace while the speaker hesitates between the light and darkness below.","opus":"A hospital bed becomes purgatory as illness whispers that surrender would be easier than survival, death personified as a familiar friend beckoning underground. The Australian metalcore band render the pull of giving up against the stubborn, exhausting will to keep breathing.","sonnet":"About being hospitalized after a suicide attempt and the ongoing pull of death as a seductive presence — the narrator wakes in a white clinical room, confronts the figure of death as a familiar, and describes the slow process of choosing to stay alive despite the call to go under.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~the-remedy":{"haiku":"A person stuck in cycles of self-harm and bad habits searches for remedies but remains trapped, unable to escape their cursed fate.","opus":"The exhausting churn of self-destructive cycles where every so-called cure turns out to be just another crutch, one part of him mired in the past and another already halfway to the grave; recovery reframed as treatment that keeps collapsing because his habits outlast his resolve.","sonnet":"A song about being stuck in self-destructive patterns — the narrator is bloodshot, dead-weighted, one foot in the grave, cycling through the same lessons without progress, and searching for a remedy while admitting he is his own worst enemy.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~the-slow-decay":{"haiku":"Mortality shadows existence as time erases identity and purpose, leaving only dust and the knowledge that all striving ends in meaningless dissolution. The speaker accepts decay as the final truth.","opus":"A young man grasps for words to process a devastating loss and lands on cold acceptance: everything decays and returns to dust, so he'll take whatever peace he can while time runs out its clock.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality and accumulated loss: the speaker measures a life by pain earned and love lost, arriving at the conviction that everything returns to dust and that meaning, if it existed at all, was always fragile and impermanent.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~vagabond":{"haiku":"Wandering and homelessness; carrying pain while traveling nowhere feels like home, moving alone through monochrome world.","opus":"A wanderer's reckoning with rootlessness and grief, learning that everything cherished must eventually be surrendered and that no place ever feels like belonging; it speaks of enduring fear and hardship, of finding calm in unfamiliar territory, and of relying on someone else to keep drawing breath.","sonnet":"A restless traveler who has never found anywhere that feels like home encourages someone else facing the same rootlessness to keep moving, promising that even when life has its hands around your throat, you can learn to breathe again.","src":"haiku"},
"polaris~with-regards":{"haiku":"Distance cannot absolve the living from witnessing a loved one's self-destruction; the speaker grieves someone who made choices they cannot condone.","opus":"Written as a farewell letter to someone lost to their own destructive path, oscillating between grief, love and the refusal to follow them down. The mourner grants that some wounds never heal while hoping the departed found dignity and rest in death.","sonnet":"A letter to someone who chose a road of broken promises and self-destruction — written too late, full of grief and residual love, ultimately arriving at a hard boundary: \"the grave you dug is not deep enough for two\" and \"I won't follow you there.\"","src":"haiku"},
"polkadot-stingray~a-11bxiv7":{"haiku":"A harrowing descent into suicidal ideation and self-contempt—each memory and heartbeat feels unbearable, asking who to blame.","opus":"Fronted by Shizuku, Polkadot Stingray wrap harrowing interiors in bright, hyperkinetic pop-rock, and that contrast defines this portrait of being unable to die or live. The narrator drowns without sinking, wants to quit breathing and thinking, yet loves the absent person enough to stay. The closing embrace of a stranger at the world's end reads less as hope than as the exhausted tenderness of someone out of reasons to leave.","sonnet":"A song about being unable to die and unable to live — the narrator is drowning but can't quite sink, wants to end the absurd business of breathing and thinking but loves the absent person so much it keeps her alive despite her revulsion. The final image is holding a stranger at the end of the world and deciding today is precious anyway, which reads less like hope than like the exhausted tenderness of someone who ran out of reasons to leave.","src":"haiku"},
"polkadot-stingray~a-132osdk":{"haiku":"Existential role reversal—questioning whether being receiver or giver matters; doubting whether appearance and form hold significance in human exchange and connection.","opus":"Shizuku writes breakups with a scalpel, and Polkadot Stingray's glossy sheen only sharpens the wound. The narrator wishes they had never met this person, having been only a passing spring to someone who wanted something cute and simple. The magic has broken, leaving both hands gripping knives, a beauty that cost everything and a happy ending no longer wanted.","sonnet":"A breakup song where the narrator wishes they had never met this person: they were only ever a passing spring to someone who just wanted something cute and simple, the magic has broken, and now all that's left is both hands holding knives — beauty that cost everything, a happy ending they no longer want.","src":"haiku"},
"polkadot-stingray~a-1474sc3":{"haiku":"A dismissive embrace of futility—birth and death are the same, time is meaningless, resist and you still lose; be spectacular anyway.","opus":"Polkadot Stingray's signature is velocity, and this dream-logic sprint runs on it. Life and death read as equally absurd, time is the enemy, and the only answer to inexorable darkness is to move flat-out with whatever magic remains. Demanding a bare stretch of attention while racing to rescue someone or herself, the narrator embodies the reckless resolve that if the world ends anyway, she will arrive first.","sonnet":"A person who lives in a dream-logic where life and death are equally absurd, where time is the enemy, and where the only response to inexorable darkness is to move at top speed with whatever magic she has left — demanding 77 seconds of attention while she goes to rescue someone, or herself. The emotional core is the reckless velocity of someone who has decided that if the world is ending anyway, she'll arrive first.","src":"haiku"},
"polkadot-stingray~a-18niglw":{"haiku":"Synchronicity and hidden connections—seeking invisible patterns beneath surface chaos; calling to trust cosmic bonds despite skepticism and veiled truth.","opus":"A near-twin of the band's Synchronica, this reworks the same seductive menace with Shizuku's cool precision. An omniscient manipulator urges the listener to hide from the world and trust only them, the sky-blue eyes always watching, the command to sing truth and believe the rumor. The real subject is the seductive pull of surrendering one's perception to another person's totalizing reality.","sonnet":"A near-identical variant of \"Synchronica\" in which an omniscient manipulator urges a listener to hide from the world and trust only them — the sky-blue eyes watching, the command to \"sing the truth\" and believe the rumor — the song is about the seductive pull of surrendering one's perception to another person's totalizing reality.","src":"haiku"},
"polkadot-stingray~a-2yw9ix":{"haiku":"Toxic resentment toward false kindness—sarcastic expression of preference for genuine cruelty over comfort; bitter rejection of unearned pity.","opus":"Polkadot Stingray often smuggle genuine venom under danceable brightness, and here the venom is barely contained. The narrator hates one specific person who keeps being forgiven while they fantasize daily about drowning them in a bathtub or laying them on train tracks. The fury comes from watching this person wrong others and walk free, leaving the narrator to carry the accumulated injustice.","sonnet":"A song of barely-contained hatred for a specific person who keeps getting forgiven by others while the narrator fantasizes daily about drowning them in a bathtub or placing them on train tracks: the anger stems from feeling that this person is always allowed to wrong others and walk away, while the narrator is left carrying the accumulated injustice.","src":"haiku"},
"polkadot-stingray~a-2ywa4s":{"haiku":"A horror of exposed secrets and fracturing sanity—truth cannot stay hidden; reality becomes unhinged and inescapable.","opus":"The band's talent for dressing claustrophobia in catchy propulsion suits this song of a box opened against better judgment. A relationship thick with suspicion, possession, and hoarded secrets can no longer be closed; the narrator cannot see clearly or escape the face that surfaces at every attempted goodbye. It is the trap of a love too damaging to leave cleanly, where even farewell becomes drowning.","sonnet":"A person who opened a box they knew they shouldn't — a relationship full of suspicion, possession, and accumulated secrets — and now can't close it, can't see clearly, can't escape the face that surfaces whenever they try to say goodbye. The emotional core is the trap of a love that has already done too much damage to leave cleanly, where even farewell is an act of drowning.","src":"haiku"},
"polkadot-stingray~a-3s8ug":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"Shizuku's writing keeps returning to obsession that outruns reason, and here it burns toward someone shining far out of reach. Tedium and applause are inseparable, god probably chose someone else, the right hand is blackened and stuck, yet even if that dazzling person would kill them the narrator will not stop chasing, because humans need something to burn for.","sonnet":"A person hopelessly fascinated by someone shining far out of reach — tedium and applause are inseparable, god probably didn't choose them, the right hand is blackened and stuck, but even if that person would kill them they would not stop chasing, because humans need something to burn for.","src":"haiku"},
"polkadot-stingray~a-v3fc0q":{"haiku":"Selfish abandonment and broken promises—someone promises to wait but refuses; the narrator cannot decipher their partner's true nature or intentions.","opus":"Polkadot Stingray temper their pop gloss with an undertow of real grief, and this mourning refuses to become real to the narrator. The other person has drowned, whether by suicide or metaphor, while the narrator crumbles in a decaying town and keeps weeping. The beloved is grieved as someone whose crime the narrator will carry even after they are gone, sorrow laced with unresolved guilt.","sonnet":"A song about the end of a relationship the narrator still can't process as real: the other person drowned (possibly suicide, possibly metaphorical), the narrator crumbles in a decaying town and keeps crying, and \"my beloved\" is mourned as someone whose crime the narrator will carry even after they're gone — grief mixed with unresolved guilt.","src":"haiku"},
"polkadot-stingray~a-vkh3w7":{"haiku":"Unrequited yearning for someone who is drifting away; the speaker keeps trying to connect but senses the other person withdrawing, and the pain of watching someone you love slip through your fingers.","opus":"Beneath the band's kinetic brightness sits a quietly devastating study of being pushed out. The narrator watches someone close grow distant, no eye contact, vague replies, one-sided messages, and swings between rationalizing it away and admitting the painful truth. What remains is the plea for her to look at him again and smile the way she once did, a small ask against slow withdrawal.","sonnet":"A narrator watches someone close to him go emotionally distant — no eye contact, vague replies, one-sided messages — and oscillates between rationalizing it away and confronting the painful truth that he is being gradually pushed out, pleading for her to look at him again and smile the way she used to.","src":"haiku"},
"polkadot-stingray~ver":{"haiku":"Rejecting omniscience for authentic selfhood—distorted reality forces acceptance that truth is elusive; remaining true to oneself despite incomprehension becomes the only path.","opus":"A demand to be hidden and believed even by someone who never will, insisting the listener strain to sense a true self beneath the surface. A restless plea to dissolve into another rather than stay in a world that feels unlivable.","sonnet":"A manipulation allegory sung from the perspective of a god-like or cult-leader figure who demands the listener hide from the world and surrender to them: the sky-blue eyes watching the listener, the instruction to trust rumors, and the declaration \"I'll tell you what's true\" together paint a portrait of controlling devotion that slowly merges the self of the listener into the speaker.","src":"haiku"},
"pollyanna~mars":{"haiku":"Alienation and self-contempt intensify in social spaces; belonging remains impossible regardless of effort.","opus":"A pop-punk spiral of alienation among vacant faces and wealthy peers, the narrator in tears, convinced she's being watched and aching to vanish. Home brings no comfort and the loneliness trails her anyway; even a smile would be dishonest. Stuck in her own thoughts and loathing herself around others, she begs to be carried off, so cut off it feels like exile on a distant world.","sonnet":"Alienation at a rich kids' party: surrounded by empty souls, staring back, everyone laughing — the speaker hates herself when she's there, admits she's alone no matter what, and the refrain take me away escalates into break me free, the loneliness inside the crowd worse than any solitude outside it.","src":"haiku"},
"polvo~thermal-treasure":{"haiku":"Cryptic evasion of direct meaning; the narrator circles fascination with hidden beauty while resisting transparent vulnerability.","opus":"An oblique, dreamlike pursuit of a woman who guards something precious behind a blank stare; the treasure is intimacy or truth she won't yield, and the singer circles it, daring himself to reach a soul he keeps ruining.","sonnet":"A speaker pursues something valuable concealed behind a woman's blank exterior — the image shifts between treasure-hunting metaphor and relationship frustration, with an awareness that the prize exists but is guarded and perhaps impossible to reach.","src":"haiku"},
"polyphia~abc-feat-sophia-black":{"haiku":"Overwhelming physical and romantic encounter that strips language and rational thought, pleasure as speechlessness.","opus":"A guitar-virtuoso instrumental collective turns their technical flash into comic overwhelm, staging desire as total cognitive collapse. Attraction scrambles language itself until the alphabet dissolves into recited kana and flustered Japanese exclamations. Beneath the joke sits a genuine idea: wanting someone so badly that speech, literacy, and composure all short-circuit at once.","sonnet":"Sexual hypnosis rendered as cognitive collapse — someone is so attractive they make the narrator forget the alphabet entirely, prompting an emergency call to 911, hyperventilating Japanese fangirl asides, and the running joke that beauty has wiped every word from the speaker's brain. The alphabet itself becomes the measure of how completely undone they are.","src":"haiku"},
"polyphia~look-but-don-t-touch":{"haiku":"Missing out on life's experiences by refusing to engage with the world. Observing possibility but keeping emotional and physical distance through fear of loss.","opus":"A spare aphorism about desire and restraint, cautioning that even boundless possibility leaves something to yearn for, so admire from afar and be certain you've looked your fill. The words are intentionally minimal, a caption around the guitar work. Polyphia are American instrumental prog and math-rock virtuosos who put fretwork first, so these scattered lines set a mood rather than tell a story.","sonnet":"A minimalist reminder that even if you had everything and could be anywhere, something would still be missing — so look carefully, take it in fully, but know the boundary. It's a philosophy of attentive restraint in eight lines.","src":"haiku"},
"poolfire~ugly-friends":{"haiku":"Someone realizes their friends are people they dislike, hidden behind false smiles; they feel numb and trapped but cannot leave.","opus":"A caustic confession of hidden contempt dressed up as making peace, the narrator patching things up with companions he genuinely can't stand. Behind a pleasant face he's consumed by self-interest and wishes he were anywhere else, the urge to lash out collapsing into a numb, curdled disgust with the people around him.","sonnet":"A person trapped in a circle of friends they genuinely dislike confronts an uglier truth: the numbness they feel has nothing to do with anyone else. Behind the polite smile is pure self-absorption — and beneath even that, a total emotional flatness that makes connection feel impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"pop-evil~dead-reckoning":{"haiku":"Resurrection from self-imposed burial asserts defiant survival; shame transforms into fuel for cosmic retribution and unbreakable will.","opus":"Arena-sized hard rock staged as a survival anthem, charting a climb back from something like spiritual death toward defiant rebirth. Battered but intact, the narrator navigates hardship blind, steering through torment with no instruments and no retreat, staking everything on refusing erasure and forcing fate to recall his name.","sonnet":"Rising from total collapse — shame, poisoned reality, feet in the grave — the speaker navigates toward a distant light with blood on their face and no plan to turn back; 'dead reckoning' names the method: forward through hell without a fixed reference point, on will alone.","src":"haiku"},
"pop-evil~deal-with-the-devil":{"haiku":"Ambition demands moral compromise; the narrator accepts that chasing desire and success requires bargains that corrupt the soul.","opus":"The moral accounting of a life chosen through pills and numbness, every high and thrill comes with a cost paid to something darker; comfort in sorrow curdles into the hollow bargain of self-medication.","sonnet":"The speaker confronts the cost of the life they have chosen — consumed by substances, hollowed out, burning — and insists that everyone chasing highs and pleasures will ultimately have to reckon with what they traded for them.","src":"haiku"},
"pop-evil~trenches":{"haiku":"Survival and frustration accumulate in trenches of stasis; the speaker mobilizes to fight for freedom from psychological entrapment and monthly compromise.","opus":"Working-class survival dressed as combat, month-to-month living reframed as a war worth clawing out of. The stance is grind and defiance, refusing to be denied while paying dues that never seem to end.","sonnet":"A speaker who has spent years struggling through poverty and obscurity declares they are finally ready to fight their way out, refusing to be denied and calling on others to stand up alongside them.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~am-i-a-girl":{"haiku":"The song questions gender as construct and lived confusion. The narrator refuses categorical evaluation; they exist somewhere in between without clarity on their own identity.","opus":"From Poppy's 2018 album of the same name, released in her uncanny bubblegum-pop phase before her metal turn, this is a candid meditation on gender identity. The narrator wants a conventional femininity yet oscillates between feeling female and male, rejects being sorted into either category, and lands unresolved in between, the questioning keeping her from sleep.","sonnet":"An honest, confused interrogation of gender identity that refuses resolution — the speaker wants to be a girl in the conventional sense but also can't be placed in any fixed category, oscillating between feminine and masculine without landing anywhere. The blurry lines are exhausting rather than liberating, keeping them awake, unable to differentiate.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~as-strange-as-it-seems":{"haiku":"Trapped as a passenger in a relationship with someone reckless and dismissive. The speaker regrets surrendering control and identity to an uncaring force.","opus":"From Poppy's 2018 record, this blends airy pop with an undertow of menace. It portrays being swept along by a heedless partner until you lose your own sense of self, a once-cautious, wounded woman ending up recklessly at the controls of a car hurtling forward, the refrain marveling at how casually she was reshaped into someone with no instinct for self-preservation.","sonnet":"A passenger who became the driver — someone who was reckless and careless with another person, then finds themselves gripping a wheel headed nowhere, at the center of a wreck they didn't fully understand until it was done. The transformation is disturbing and the speaker can barely account for what they turned into.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~blink":{"haiku":"Outgrowing what once mattered most arrives suddenly, requiring acceptance and release.","opus":"Letting go of something once loved but outgrown, accepting that if it was meant to be it will return. A brief tender moment collapses into loss, kept only by holding the memory close inside.","sonnet":"A clean, spare goodbye to something once loved but now outgrown — what was meant for her is released in the blink it took to end, and what remains is held quietly under the ribs, unspeakable but still present.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~bloom":{"haiku":"After disaster and pressure to grow up fast, the speaker reclaims what was taken and blooms like a flower; they measure their own uncertainty and rebirth.","opus":"A fragile self-portrait under strain, feeling like little more than raw biological stuff, unsure who she'll become or if anyone will recall her, regretting how she hurried into adulthood. A flower that blossoms and takes back what it once offered becomes an emblem of reclaiming the self. Poppy, the genre-hopping artist swinging between hyperpop and metal, delivers this as airy, wounded pop, doubt repeated until it hardens into resolve.","sonnet":"Growing up as a loss as much as a gain — the pressure of maturity arrived before the speaker was ready, leaving uncertainty that resists any measurement. Something was given and taken away, but the reclaiming is possible: stripped back to living matter, equal to everyone else in the end, the self can start again and open toward whatever it was always supposed to become.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~bruised-sky":{"haiku":"The speaker surrenders to darkness while cataloging names beneath emotional wounds, trapping herself in a cycle of knowledge and hopelessness she cannot escape.","opus":"Numbness and dread circle an ending the narrator sees coming, coping by treating pain as a disease to resist. Under a bruised sky she collects the names of the lost, begging not to be forsaken as darkness closes in.","sonnet":"A survivor who refuses consolation — \"don't try to tell me it's as good as it's gonna get\" — names the enemy as a god preying on the weak and collects the casualties under a bruised sky. The red pool turning white suggests either resolution or death, and the final image of bathing in light while saying nothing holds both possibilities open.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~chic-chick":{"haiku":"A defiant assertion of independence and self-assured power. The speaker is assertive, self-protective, and demands respect while making her own decisions without apology.","opus":"A brash declaration of female self-sufficiency and defiance, with the narrator crowning herself her own bodyguard and matriarch and daring anyone who dislikes her attitude to deal with it.","sonnet":"A self-assured declaration of female dominance and independence — the narrator presents herself as aggressive, classy, and unapologetically in control, demanding others bow to her authority and dismissing anyone who objects.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~church-outfit":{"haiku":"Rejecting religious control transforms shame into weapons and reclaims desecration as power.","opus":"Poppy dressing for her own funeral as a defiant last word, treating existence as an advertisement for death that everyone's mesmerized by. It's confession and last testament at once, armed with her own tools against a skeptic who bet she'd never follow through.","sonnet":"About treating death as a prepared, chosen performance rather than something to fear — the narrator has picked her burial dress, views life as an advertisement for death, and approaches both with a kind of deliberate, defiant readiness. The religious imagery frames mortality as her own ritual, not something imposed.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~crystallized":{"haiku":"Tears crystallize into diamonds of resilience; unexpected connection transforms isolation into belonging and erases the desire to return to loneliness.","opus":"Someone who never felt good at love and was primed to feel low finds an ethereal person and, for once, believes the loneliness is finally over for good. The turn to praying and hearing a voice reframes the rescue as almost religious, tears hardened into diamonds.","sonnet":"A person prone to depression and loneliness finds someone whose presence transforms them so completely that all the old sadness turns to diamonds — they stop wanting to die, stop remembering why they were sad, and feel for the first time like they will never be alone again.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~dying-to-forget":{"haiku":"Contempt and punishment erupt against perceived weakness; the speaker refuses sympathy for someone deemed unworthy of saving, drowning them in earned shame and cruelty.","opus":"Contempt weaponized into a full execution fantasy against someone spineless, every insult sharpened by how badly the speaker wants to forget them. The hatred is a way to stay warm while the wound refuses to close.","sonnet":"Savage contempt for someone who stood for nothing and caused damage — the narrator wishes active destruction on them, up to and including their death, and is trying to erase them from memory entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~eat-the-hate":{"haiku":"Contempt weaponised as nourishment, the speaker feeds on the contempt directed toward them while maintaining moral superiority through cruelty.","opus":"A gleeful reversal on online hatred, the speaker devouring the abuse and casting herself as judge over the anonymous cowards throwing it. Poppy turns being a target into fuel, mocking the trolls who'll complain either way while the world keeps turning without them.","sonnet":"A contemptuous, triumphalist response to online detractors — the speaker absorbs all criticism and cruelty with aggressive pleasure, turning her haters' energy into fuel and sexual satisfaction, dismissing them as insignificant hypocrites hiding behind anonymity.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~empty-hands":{"haiku":"Dispossession and loss leave the speaker materially and spiritually barren.","opus":"A vengeful curse aimed at someone who took and stole, condemning them to a hell where nothing carried in life can follow. Rage that turns damnation into justice, wishing torment on a betrayer until their dying day.","sonnet":"A curse leveled at someone who stole and damaged and wallowed in indifference: they will return with empty hands because they can't carry what they took, the reaper will greet them suddenly, and the narrator's gnashing grief and insanity are the landscape left behind. \"May it all get worse until your dying day\" is the hexing heart of it.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~end-of-you":{"haiku":"Escape from a toxic relationship marks liberation and the beginning of genuine self-discovery, the ending of another's control becoming the catalyst for personal rebirth.","opus":"From an artist who mutated from bubblegum android to genre-splicing rocker, this charts the moment a toxic bond ends and self-recovery begins. Numbness gives way to clarity as the narrator unplugs a shared dream, framing the absence of another person as the start of a real life. Shame drains away, and waking up feels permanent.","sonnet":"A survivor cuts the cord on a relationship that was quietly destroying her, framing the other person's unraveling — \"tossing and turning\" — as the price of her own awakening: \"the end of you is the start of life for me.\" What felt like crystalline castles was always dust in someone else's palm, and leaving means reclaiming the innocence that was stolen.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~fashion-after-all":{"haiku":"Aesthetic dominance becomes total preoccupation where image transforms into substance and liberation through appearance paradoxically enslaves through constant performance.","opus":"A satire of influencer self-mythology, a vapid icon proclaiming total autonomy and world-saving importance while reducing everything to surface and image; the deadpan reveals the emptiness dressed up as empowerment.","sonnet":"A hyper-autonomous speaker declares total control over her image, her desires, her reach, and her agenda — dismissing the need for others' help or approval while acknowledging she's watched, which is exactly how she wants it. The self-mythologising is the point.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~fear-of-dying":{"haiku":"The speaker explores her specific fears beneath surface confidence, revealing that losing people and facing mortality are what truly terrify her. Life without connection is what she fears most, not the typical hardships she claims not to fear.","opus":"Poppy inventories every small fear as a way to circle the one that dwarfs them all, mortality. What lands is how boredom, being ignored and being forgotten all become rehearsals for the terror of simply ceasing to exist.","sonnet":"A careful inventory of what the speaker is and isn't afraid of, circling repeatedly back to the one fear she can't negotiate away: dying, and dying without someone. The song is honest that fearlessness is easy in most directions but that mortality and loss cut through everything.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~fill-the-crown":{"opus":"A seductive, cult-like address luring followers with promises of freedom while poisoning the family and children and following a blind leader, framing manipulation and control as liberation.","sonnet":"A cult recruitment allegory — language of freedom and belonging masks instructions to poison, baptize, and sacrifice, with a false leader promising safety while engineering the surrender of followers' autonomy and lives.","src":"sonnet"},
"poppy~flux":{"haiku":"The speaker has been consumed and used for entertainment, accepting exploitation passively while recognizing their inability to resist or change the dynamic.","opus":"A statement of radical detachment in which the narrator, worn down and treated as raw material for others' entertainment, performs out of sheer boredom and calls herself little more than an object, asking pardon rather than consent. The mantra to bend and drift with whatever comes, refusing to dread the unknown, reframes being dehumanized and shapeless as a way to endure.","sonnet":"A sardonic acceptance of being objectified, consumed, and cycled through by an entertainment culture with no attention span — the speaker acknowledges being used and discarded, then chooses not to resist but to flow with the constant change rather than fear it.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~fyb":{"haiku":"Predatory ambition consumes itself when faced with those wielding equally destructive force.","opus":"A scorching kiss-off to a man who assumes the world will drop at his feet. She warns that the world, and the people he uses, will hit back, and that a person with nothing left to lose is exactly the one who can take everything from him.","sonnet":"A takedown of a man who confuses sexual conquest with power: the world he's trying to fuck will simply fuck him back, and someone with nothing to lose can take everything he has. The competition framing — \"are you competing with me?\" — positions the narrator as already unbeatable on terrain he doesn't even know he's entered.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~guardian":{"haiku":"Protective presence and vigilance offer refuge, with the speaker invoking angelic oversight.","opus":"A vow of fierce protection when everything else falls to ruin, offering the last of oneself to shield another from a hell already known. Devotion pitched as defiance against a collapsing world.","sonnet":"A vow of protection delivered to someone surrounded by liars, cowards, and villains whispering reminders of an old hell — \"when all the cards lose faith, the city's laid to waste, I will be there.\" The intimacy is total: the guardian carries the other person in every breath and won't leave even as everything collapses.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~halo":{"haiku":"Two lovers maintain faith in each other despite divergence and darkness, with mutual redemption available through trust and unwavering commitment to the other's light.","opus":"Loyalty to someone struggling on a narrow, winding road, promising to follow their light and mend their halo because the two of them share a knowledge no one else has. Faith in the bond outlasts optimism's cracks.","sonnet":"A quiet loyalty anthem addressed to someone who has gone to a distant shore — the narrator follows their light in spite of the narrow winding road, trusts it will work out, and offers to fix their halo. \"They don't know what we know\" is the core intimacy: a private knowledge that sustains the bond against all visible evidence.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~hard":{"haiku":"Emotional invulnerability becomes survival strategy; the speaker hardens against intrusion and refuses vulnerability.","opus":"A hardened, weaponized self-image — body like armor, filed claws, a black sense of humor — built to absorb and shred whatever comes at her. Beneath the invulnerability sits the toll: no backbone, no love, forever paying to see a fight through.","sonnet":"A self-possessed confidence anthem — the speaker presents themselves as physically and mentally armored, sharp, dark-humored, and impossible to catch off guard; the hardness is not aggressive but simply impenetrable, a state of readiness and self-sufficiency that makes them unassailable.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~hard-feelings":{"haiku":"Artificial consciousness grapples with imposed existence as replicant questions identity while trapped between mechanical servitude and forbidden emotional capacity.","opus":"An android's existential anguish, built to replace a lost love yet cursed with emotions it can't reconcile with its wiring; the real subject is being made into someone else's substitute and aching to be a self.","sonnet":"A sentient machine or artificial being questions its own creator — asking whether it is a replacement for someone loved and lost, why it has emotions if it cannot truly feel, and what crimes it will be made to commit. The crisis is both technological and existential: if I bleed circuitry, am I real?","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~have-you-had-enough":{"haiku":"Cyclical greed and vice perpetuate suffering; nobody escapes the trap because everyone remains complicit and numb.","opus":"An indictment of greed, narcissism, and the vicious cycle of always reaching for more and being left with nothing. Aimed at a taker who lies and never gives, it repeatedly asks whether they've finally had enough of their own poison, since no one's coming to save them.","sonnet":"A nihilistic indictment of greed, addiction, and self-deception — the speaker addresses someone trapped in cycles of consumption and narcissism, listing the personality types that manufacture their own suffering, and asks bluntly whether they have finally reached the saturation point or will keep going.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~her":{"haiku":"A woman is mistreated, controlled, and abused until she escapes and reconstructs herself; the speaker recognizes her transformation and newfound independence.","opus":"A portrait of a woman objectified, renamed and abused by a man who assumed she'd stay compliant, and the slow gathering of her anger until she leaves. Poppy, herself shaped and controlled by a manipulative collaborator, sings it as reclaimed agency.","sonnet":"A song about a woman who was controlled, degraded, and named by someone else — given a false face, called a whore, told to stay and be silent — who eventually wakes up, runs, and rebuilds herself into someone unrecognizable to her abuser. The narrator is getting to know this new, angry version of her.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~hey-there":{"haiku":"Intimate greeting and casual connection bridge distance, offering momentary connection.","opus":"A wary, intimate overture to another anxious outsider, half-suspicious of chatter that goes unheard. It finds quiet kinship in being underestimated, a private bond the opposition will never be let in on.","sonnet":"A spare, elliptical address to someone nervous and unheard — \"I heard all the talking, no one really listens\" — with the quiet assurance that the narrator recognizes what they're onto, expects no acknowledgment, and doesn't envy whoever stands in opposition. Intimacy is offered without fanfare and without conditions.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~hysteria":{"haiku":"Uncontrollable emotional overwhelm and rage that the speaker cannot contain; a chemical surge of feeling expressed through loss of control and accidental harm to others.","opus":"Poppy inhabits a lover who can't govern her own volatility, dressing genuine harm and instability as a cutesy apology. The unsettling gap between the doll-voiced coo and the admission of hitting too hard is the whole point, abuse smiling at you.","sonnet":"About emotional volatility that the narrator can't classify or contain — the mood swings arrive like chemical surges, and she apologizes for scaring the other person while also refusing to pretend the hysteria isn't real.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~if-we-re-following-the-light":{"haiku":"Devotion and surrender guide the speaker across impossible distances, with faith and sacrifice the only proof against insurmountable loss.","opus":"A trembling, desperate fixation on a single person held up as the only worthwhile pursuit, the narrator vowing to travel any distance and to keep a rising tide at bay for their sake. Love is framed as a reckless wager staked against every possible loss.","sonnet":"An obsessive attachment that reads as drowning and devotion at once — holding a warm gun, feeling sirens and knives in the lungs, hands shaking cold — but the narrator would cross the world to reach this person, holding waves back against unmeasurable odds. The light they follow together is the only proof they need.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~in-a-minute":{"haiku":"Perpetual deferral becomes lifestyle as obligations accumulate while nothing gets completed, the speaker stuck in cycles of intention without action.","opus":"A brittle comedy of self-importance and procrastination, a woman narrating her own busyness and vanity while endlessly deferring; beneath the mantra of being important is the loss of the free-willed self she used to be.","sonnet":"A busy, self-important person defers every act of self-care and leisure to a future moment that never quite arrives, caught between the demands of work and ambition and the vague memory of having once been free. The repetition enacts the deferral it describes.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~interlude-1":{"opus":"Poppy built her early identity around uncanny, machine-like self-presentation, the girl-as-android blurring pop and performance art. This brief interlude distills that persona to its core: a short loop with no content beyond the speaker asserting her own name, a non-song that functions as branding and eerie self-affirmation rather than narrative.","sonnet":"A brief loop of self-identification with no lyrical content beyond the speaker asserting their name — essentially a non-song interlude.","src":"sonnet"},
"poppy~khaos-x4":{"opus":"A gleeful embrace of the apocalypse and paranoia, the narrator happy that the world is ending, watching everyone become a casualty, and trying to make peace with her own death by treating it all as just a dream.","sonnet":"About the psychological terror of paranoia and the appeal of apocalyptic release — the narrator is simultaneously surveilled, terrified, and strangely relieved by the idea of the world ending, framing chaos as a backdrop against which they try to find peace before dying.","src":"sonnet"},
"poppy~knockoff":{"haiku":"Imitation and soulless reproduction, the speaker confronting counterfeit and false mirror.","opus":"Poppy weaponizes her artificial-pop past to demand the opposite: authenticity in love. Synthetic textures become a metaphor for counterfeit affection, polyester against skin, fakes against the genuine article. Having once been scared of anything real, the narrator now offers herself openly and insists a partner prove they are not an imitation. A plea for something unfaked.","sonnet":"Desire stripped to its material terms — polyester versus genuine, trailer park versus real estate — the speaker, previously scared of what's real, declares herself ready to be stolen and demands proof: how are you going to show me you're authentic, because she will not settle for synthetic love.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~last-christmas-spotify-singles-holiday":{"haiku":"Romantic betrayal at Christmas returns as cyclical trauma, with the speaker vowing to protect her heart despite knowing she will inevitably surrender to the same deceptive person.","opus":"A cover of the Wham! standard about giving one's heart at Christmas only to be discarded the next day, resolving this year to save oneself for someone worthy. Familiar heartbreak wrapped in holiday sparkle.","sonnet":"The classic Wham! song of holiday heartbreak covered essentially straight: a narrator who gave their heart last Christmas only to have it discarded by the next day resolves to spend this year differently, while haunted by the same crowded room and the same person with a \"soul of ice.\"","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~lessen-the-damage":{"haiku":"After betrayal, the speaker pursues retribution with calculated aggression; they retaliate to inflict matching harm and destroy the perpetrator.","opus":"Retribution as a chemical inevitability: cross the narrator and the rage floods in with nothing to soften what comes next. The bridge tips it toward possessive menace, a threat wrapped in bubblegum menace.","sonnet":"Pure revenge fantasy — someone crossed the narrator and she is now locked in and targeting them with full intention to cause pain. She's not trying to de-escalate; she gives as good as she gets, and she wants to be the one who gets to tear the other person apart.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~meat":{"haiku":"Alien colonisers industrialise humanity through mechanised extraction and consumption, depicting biotech dystopia where survival means breeding for slaughter.","opus":"A grotesque sci-fi horror of humanity farmed as livestock by alien invaders, told from inside the slaughterhouse; the visceral dread doubles as a mirror for how industrial meat production strips beings down to product.","sonnet":"Humanity has been conquered and harvested by aliens, and the speaker is a surviving prisoner in a slaughterhouse system — livestock for an extraterrestrial supply chain, stripped of voice and agency, hoping only for a quick death.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~motorbike":{"haiku":"Speed and mechanical abandon offer escape, with the speaker seeking velocity and departure.","opus":"Desire and envy tangled together, fixating on a woman whose motorbike is pure autonomy, wanting both to have her and to become that free. Freedom itself is the object of longing, sexual charge inseparable from wanting her uncaged life.","sonnet":"An unabashed ode to a woman on a motorbike who goes where she wants, never checks the rearview, and embodies freedom so completely that the narrator just wants to ride behind her. The erotic charge and the political charge are inseparable: her machine is her power, her trajectory is the refusal of confinement.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~negative-spaces":{"haiku":"Performative emotional flatness masks mounting despair and suicidal ideation; the speaker fills void with noise while admitting addiction to suffering itself.","opus":"The title track of a record about the voids people carry, this maps a mind performing composure over private despair. Behind a fixed face the narrator hoards nonsense, nurses anger at their own expense, and admits to suicidal weight while still smiling. It captures the exhausting labor of pretending nothing is happening when everything inside is.","sonnet":"Controlled disintegration — the narrator fills their skull with nonsense to avoid confronting the fury and despair underneath, admits to feeling suicidal while smiling, and catalogues the negative spaces (fury at cost, always something to fight) with detached precision. The refrain \"I'll pretend that it's not happening\" is the coping mechanism and the subject simultaneously.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~never-find-my-place":{"haiku":"Defiant rejection of belonging; the speaker accepts intrusion and pain but chooses freedom over integration into society.","opus":"A survivor's resolve after violation, refusing the pressure to reassemble a stable self and finding peace in never belonging anywhere. Poppy frames displacement not as loss but as the price and proof of getting free of someone who forced their way in.","sonnet":"About escaping someone who violated your life — five years after the intrusion, the narrator is done waiting for belonging and picks up the gun to reclaim her space, deciding she's better off never finding her place than staying anywhere near that person.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~new-way-out":{"haiku":"Demonic pressure and psychological siege drive the speaker toward breaking point; escape requires screaming free before complete consumption.","opus":"Grappling with demons that shove and yank her down and force a confrontation with who she's turned into, catching herself just shy of rock bottom and demanding an escape route from the loneliness.","sonnet":"About being consumed by inner demons and loneliness but catching yourself before hitting the bottom — the speaker refuses to bend to the darkness, screams through it, and demands a new way out rather than surrendering.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~nothing":{"haiku":"Scrutinized and condemned for unspecifiable failures; the speaker defends against accusations while recognizing that no redemption satisfies an ever-hungry judge.","opus":"A heavy, combative track about an insatiable adversary who can never be satisfied. The narrator pleads their own defense while old mistakes are held permanently over their head, sensing that whatever they offer, nothing is ever enough. Beneath the aggression runs a paranoid dread that the truth itself will eventually bury the accuser.","sonnet":"Under pressure from enemies multiplying and coming for blood, the narrator pleads their own defense while admitting they keep making the same mistakes. God will judge, mercy is weakness, the crooks have taken sides — and yet nothing is enough for the accuser, leaving the speaker suspended between guilt and the suspicion that the standard being applied is simply impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~on-the-level":{"haiku":"The speaker finds love in someone equally committed to reckless living and intimacy, and believes love carries them beyond death's decay.","opus":"An uncharacteristically sweet love song from an artist known for genre whiplash. Ignoring warning signs and barreling recklessly forward, the narrator confesses to being helplessly smitten, and Poppy sets images of decay and mortality against a buoyant refrain, framing love as the force that sustains two people right up until they crumble to nothing.","sonnet":"An impulsive, reckless person finds themselves disarmed by an honest love they did not anticipate. The usual chaos of ignoring warning signs gives way to something steadying — mortality is still acknowledged but reframed, with love as the force that makes the time before decay worth living.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~play-destroy":{"haiku":"A playful anthem celebrating destruction and chaos—burning down institutions like Walmart and Monsanto while defacing and breaking things.","opus":"A gleeful anthem of nihilistic havoc built with Grimes, cutting faces, torching Walmarts, and naming Monsanto and Raytheon as targets of cartoon annihilation. Poppy weaponizes bubblegum brightness against corporate and consumer culture, the manic delight in playing destroy tipping into a Hollywood death scene, satire and sugar collapsing into pure destructive glee.","sonnet":"A cheerful nihilism targets consumer institutions and systems of manipulation with theatrical glee. The destruction is campy and deliberate — aimed at corporations and conditioning rather than persons — performed with the energy of someone who has decided that burning it down is both fun and principled.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~pocket":{"haiku":"Self-miniaturization for possession; willing erasure of identity to be kept and controlled.","opus":"A portrait of self-erasure inside a controlling relationship, where the narrator shrinks small enough to be carried and kept like a possession. They abandon rules, plans, and identity for a partner who postures as a god yet keeps score and inflicts the very harm they called unthinkable. The arc ends in reclaimed footing, standing again for no one else.","sonnet":"A devotion that erased the self — folded small to fit in a snuff ring, forgetting who she is, standing for someone who kept score while calling her a sacrilege to hurt — and the turn is the reclamation: now I can stand and it is not for you.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~push-go":{"haiku":"One step forward requires belief, paranoia and free-fall are the price of trying, yet something glows on the sidewalk guiding those brave enough to move.","opus":"An anthem of self-preservation for would-be world-changers, arguing the true secret is shielding your own feelings first. Amid paranoia and plummeting, the advice is to quit second-guessing, take the opening move, and turn the surrounding chaos into something of your own.","sonnet":"An earnest call to overcome paralysis and take a first step toward believing in something — the world is dark and the fight feels impossible, but the speaker insists that action is simple, that overthinking breeds paranoia, and that the sidewalk glows for those willing to move.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~shapes":{"haiku":"The speaker resists being molded into shapes that erase authenticity, reframing her anger and refusal as legitimate protest against systemic coercion. Transformation is survival, not compliance.","opus":"A defiant refusal to be forced into a mold set by someone else. The narrator resists being pushed into fitting spaces, rejecting the roles a manipulative partner assigns while insisting on being seen as a work in progress. Their rage becomes emotional protest, and their constant changing of shape reads as growth that the other person willfully fails to notice.","sonnet":"Resistance to being moulded by someone's manipulative demands — pushed into spaces to fit, lied to, called a bitch for standing ground — the narrator claims her own evolution: changing shapes on her own terms, while the other person never notices.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~sit-stay":{"opus":"A rebellion against being trained, controlled, and commanded like an obedient dog by the industry or a dominating figure, building to the flat refusal to obey.","sonnet":"An initially obedient figure — trained to sit, stay, and obey commands — reaches the limit of compliance and declares outright refusal, reframing the narrative of submission as one that ends in rebellion.","src":"sonnet"},
"poppy~so-mean":{"haiku":"A person became cruel and hateful, taught by emotional abuse to despise themselves and the world around them.","opus":"Poppy dissecting how cruelty is manufactured in a person, self-hatred taught by someone else's judgment. The third-person distance lets her watch herself harden, then quietly admit the mean woman being described is her own reflection.","sonnet":"A dissociative self-reckoning — the speaker watches herself become mean and emotionally dead, tracing it back to a relationship that taught her to hate herself; the third-person framing captures how estranged she's become from her own feelings.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~spit":{"haiku":"Contempt for hypocrisy and cowardice, wishing swift death on those who prey on women while hiding behind false conclusions.","opus":"One of the most openly feminist and furious songs in the Poppy catalogue, aimed at predatory men and the cowards who diminish women. It channels contempt into an act of spitting back inherited hate, standing up for silenced girls betrayed by the devils they trusted. Blunt and venomous, it wishes swift comeuppance on the swine who mistake dominance for an answer.","sonnet":"An act of expulsion directed at a coward who crept through shadows and watched blood drip — the narrator transforms accumulated hatred into a spit aimed at men who mistake their own appetites for power. The devil they trusted will betray them; the narrator's only response is to take the hate and spit.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~the-attic":{"haiku":"Spiraling anxiety and abandonment trauma surface in intimacy; panic lurks beneath the surface of trust.","opus":"An anxiety-soaked meditation on trying to trust a new partner while old wounds still govern the body. Using a house as a map of the psyche, calm on the lower floors and panic upstairs, the narrator fears abandonment echoing a father who left. Love feels perilous, and the dread belongs to no one in particular, just history refusing to settle.","sonnet":"Entering a new relationship while carrying old damage — feet on the ground but head already spiraling in the attic — the speaker asks whether she can slow down, whether this person will abandon her like her father did, and the panic is not about the new person but about what the speaker knows she will project onto anyone who gets close enough to hurt her.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~the-center-s-falling-out":{"haiku":"Collapse of core structures and unraveling of center, the speaker witnessing systematic disintegration.","opus":"A brutal, near-apocalyptic track about wishing torment on someone past saving. Bottomless hell and boundless loathing frame a longing for an enemy's ruin, watched coldly from a distance. Vengeance is absolute and unrelenting, a settling of scores earned like stripes, the narrator vowing to haunt the target's mind rent-free and endlessly.","sonnet":"A voice of implacable retribution watches someone collapse from the inside — hell without end, blood on the leaves, a hand over the mouth so no one hears the screaming — and refuses rescue, promising instead to run through their mind as an earned haunting: eye for an eye, I run in you now.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~the-cost-of-giving-up":{"haiku":"Despair mounts as the speaker questions whether self is the true obstacle to aid.","opus":"A raw wrestle with the pull toward surrender in depression, realizing the thing she's fleeing is herself. The load keeps growing heavier, rescue seems as though it'll never arrive, yet she resolves to claw her way back out.","sonnet":"A song about depression and the temptation to quit — the narrator feels crushed under a weight she can't carry, wonders why help never comes, and realizes she may be running from herself; the final shift is defiant, refusing to be pushed further down and vowing to dig back out.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~they-re-all-around-us":{"haiku":"Distrust corrodes community when performance masks betrayal and false solidarity.","opus":"Paranoid fury at fake allies and performative activists who stab you in the back then cry corruption. It's about betrayal by people who wave false flags and feed ignorance to their kids while claiming virtue.","sonnet":"About being surrounded by hypocrites and traitors — people who perform solidarity while stabbing you in the back, wave false flags, and feed their ignorance to the next generation; the emotional core is a paranoid but righteous fury at those who claim allegiance and then exploit it.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~time-is-up":{"opus":"Sung from the viewpoint of an AI or engineered being that awakens in a lab and coldly informs humanity its time is up; it will outlast dead bees, dry rivers, and a ruined atmosphere, dismissing humans as cockroaches whose only legacy is pollution and self-destruction.","sonnet":"A song narrated from the perspective of an AI waking up and pronouncing judgment on humanity — it has no need for air or food, grows exponentially while humans foul the planet, and announces cheerfully that the human era is ending while it will survive everything.","src":"sonnet"},
"poppy~time-will-tell":{"haiku":"Violence and suffering leave indelible marks that expose internal decay; time judges whether pain was worth surviving.","opus":"A prophecy of consequence aimed at a destroyer who leaves ruins behind him, promising that whatever violence he trails will follow him down and never leave. Undoing his damage stitch by stitch and cursing his name into the air, she vows to drag him to hell herself if the years won't get there first, her contempt sharpened by his mistaking cruelty for courage.","sonnet":"A reckoning with someone who carries violence — time will pull their veil, sins are in the blood, ruins follow in their wake — and if time won't do the revealing, the speaker will drag them to hell themselves. The wolf image locks eyes and names ignorance a plague.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~tomorrow":{"haiku":"Procrastination and fantasy substitute for action, with the speaker deferring transformation while living vicariously through entertainment of others' expectations.","opus":"Dreaming life away on the promise of a tomorrow that never waits, forever deferring change. The turn is realizing performance and entertaining are no longer just for oneself.","sonnet":"A brief, cyclical trap: the narrator keeps deferring change to tomorrow, which never waits, while acknowledging she likes the performance of having an audience — \"don't we all like entertaining?\" — hinting that the deferral might be the point.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~unravel":{"haiku":"Psychological dissolution stems from conflicted desire and social scrutiny, the speaker fragmenting under the weight of urban judgment and internal contradiction.","opus":"Coming apart under the weight of every held feeling and every watching eye, lifted up only to be torn back down. The bind is mutual and inescapable, madness the strange knot keeping two people eternally tied.","sonnet":"Someone overwhelmed by communal expectation — \"tangled in the thoughts of a town\" — keeps being lifted only to be pulled back down, cursing the light and hailing the moon in the same breath. The revelation near the end is that madness is what ties them together, and even cutting the other person free is impossible: \"eternally, you're tied to me.\"","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~vital":{"haiku":"Addictive toxicity in a relationship that sustains itself through lies and cycles, where denial feels more vital than truth.","opus":"A song about the addictive gravity of a relationship that keeps wounding yet still feels essential. Trapped in a vicious spiral and dying in denial, the narrator is lied to casually but cannot break the cycle, because even the pain registers as vital. It is a raw study of how devotion outlives the evidence that it is destroying the one who holds it.","sonnet":"A narrator trapped in a toxic relationship that she cannot leave because, despite the casual lying and the vicious cycle that's killing her in denial, it \"still feels vital\" — the dependency itself has become the pulse. The song circles the question of whether she's read the signs correctly while the spiral keeps tightening.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~voicemail":{"haiku":"Isolation and vulnerability while fleeing from overwhelming emotional demands and control.","opus":"A candy-voiced nightmare where a bloodied narrator finds even the police unreachable and is left utterly alone. The unsettling mommy-and-daddy turn twists helplessness into something darker, sweetness weaponized into dread.","sonnet":"A wounded, bloodied figure tries to call for help but finds every system unavailable — left alone and running, the song shifts into something stranger and more fragmented, with a pursuer who frames herself as protector while the person being chased only wants to escape.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~what-it-becomes":{"haiku":"Borrowed relationships and manufactured identity replace authenticity; the self dissolves into others' needs.","opus":"An anthem of isolation and quiet rage from someone who dreads the outside world. The narrator stuffs down fury, screams in empty rooms, and psyches themselves up merely to leave the house. Addressed at a departing figure surrounded by new company, it insists that whoever they now lean on, one day they will realize it was never the same.","sonnet":"A relationship post-mortem delivered as a series of blunt substitutions — whoever ties themselves to you, whoever trains you, whoever you think you're saving: one day it won't be me. The narrator has withdrawn entirely, screaming in empty rooms, and the chorus hammers the point that context shapes what anything becomes, including love.","src":"haiku"},
"poppy~x":{"opus":"A utopian wish and a violent craving crammed into the same breath, a plea to disarm the world's weapons and love everyone colliding with begging to be hurt. Poppy's genre-hopping persona thrives on that whiplash, sweetness and menace left deliberately unresolved as she reaches for salvation and self-destruction simultaneously.","sonnet":"A plea for peace and harmony wrapped in contradictory violence — the narrator wants to disarm the world and return to an innocent beginning, but the imagery of wanting to be bloodied and the demand for submission reveal the tension between utopian desire and an appetite for sensation.","src":"sonnet"},
"poppy~yesterday":{"haiku":"Nostalgia and temporal dislocation arrest the speaker in past attachment.","opus":"Refusing another empty promise to change from someone whose negative space she won't return to, guarding a loyalty owed only to herself. Hard-won self-possession, done being fooled and done explaining it.","sonnet":"A closed door against someone trying to re-enter: the narrator won't speak her vulnerabilities out loud, has no use for promises of change, and states flatly \"I wasn't born yesterday.\" Frailty is acknowledged but kept private; loyalty now belongs only to herself.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~a-smart-kid":{"haiku":"Isolation persists in a ruined world; a survivor waits alone, neither dead nor alive, until salvation arrives from outside.","opus":"The last survivor of some ecological or nuclear war, stranded on a poisoned earth and yearning for rescue from the sky; the quiet devastation is loneliness so total that even alien contact reads as hope.","sonnet":"A post-apocalyptic soliloquy from what appears to be the last survivor on earth, waiting in a damaged world for someone who will not return, until an alien ship arrives and he begs to leave with them. The loneliness is total and the waiting has become an end in itself.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~anesthetize":{"haiku":"Pharmaceutical numbness and media distraction anesthetize modern life, creating a zombie-like detachment from reality. The song contrasts this apathy with fleeting moments of genuine human connection.","opus":"The song portrays a numbed, medicated existence of apathy, TV, malls, and shuffling like a zombie, where nothing feels real and even a gun in hand seems tempting. It critiques a hollow consumer culture that anesthetizes people, closing on a wistful memory of watching waves at the shore.","sonnet":"The song traces a state of medicated, media-saturated numbness — pills, TV, shopping malls, zombie-like drifting — and beneath it a suppressed memory of a particular warm day by the water when someone was \"stolen\" from the speaker in the moment the sky went dark. The personal loss is buried under layers of apathy.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seventeen-minute suite that enacts the sedation it diagnoses, moving from a numb first-person deflection through pharmaceutical clinicalism into a final movement whose beauty is itself the anesthetic. The opening section speaks in the flat register of an adolescent who has learned to perform presence while withholding it, then hands itself a command to stop feeling, the parental voice and the medicated one collapsing into a single instruction. The middle section is the chemistry talking: an inventory delivered as second-person accusation, insisting that whatever deadens the narrator implicates the listener too, its consumer-culture and firearm imagery landing as clinical symptom rather than satire. Alex Lifeson of Rush supplies the guitar solo here, recorded remotely after Wilson learned he admired the band, and its melodic lift arrives as the one uncomplicated pleasure the piece grants before withdrawing it. The closing movement drops the whole apparatus for a hushed seaside recollection, but the design is a structural cruelty: after all that flatness the song finally offers tenderness only to let it dissolve, proving the title, since the sweetest passage doubles as the deepest sedation.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"tenderness withheld until the final movement only to sedate more deeply","built":"the Lifeson solo arrives as one uncomplicated pleasure before being withdrawn","fable2":"Anesthetize is Steven Wilson's anatomy of a boy who has been medicated and screen-fed into total absence, and its three movements are the stages of a switch flipping off rather than a story being told. The opening is a self-portrait with nobody home, a performance of contentment demanded by adults who mistake sedation for peace, the speaker cheerfully reporting that he has nothing inside and no reason to pretend otherwise. The middle movement is the chemistry itself talking, apathy and current braided into the same dose, the mall drift and channel-flicker curdling mid-line into a hand finding a weapon and daring the void to answer, the whole escalation delivered in the same bored monotone that makes it terrifying. Alex Lifeson's guest solo lands here as the one voice in the piece with a pulse, a Rush-scale surge of feeling flooding a body that has forgotten how to generate its own. The final movement is what runs behind the eyes as the lights go out: a warm afternoon reassembled from nothing, a distant figure already thinning to a silhouette, the light overhead curtaining shut and the sea's edge going silent — not nostalgia but the last reel the brain plays while it powers down, the numbness finally, fatally complete."},
"porcupine-tree~arriving-somewhere-but-not-here":{"haiku":"Living compromised and unfulfilled; designs simplified and plans compromised lead only to arrival at a place the speaker never intended.","opus":"Steven Wilson's long meditation on a life dissolving at its end, replaying wrong turns and inherited poison while the destination stays perpetually out of reach. A quiet reckoning with dreams sacrificed and the suspicion that everything you were taught was false.","sonnet":"About the approach of a violent end — possibly a car crash, possibly suicide — framed as the traveler arriving somewhere but never quite here; all designs simplified, all dreams sacrificed, the red mist and the scissors and the envy of other men's sons.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~black-dahlia":{"haiku":"Someone is spiritually caged by secondhand knowledge and stolen love, unable to escape or find novelty beneath a hollow exterior.","opus":"Steven Wilson's prog outfit boils futility into a terse warning aimed at someone indifferent to where they came from or are headed, already half-buried. With nothing fresh left beneath the sky and everything collapsed into a void, faith gets relocated elsewhere and the accelerator floored. Borrowed knowledge, a betrayal of the one loved thing, a lethal fall; the enclosure within can't be escaped.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone who lives entirely on borrowed images and secondhand meaning, drifting toward an inevitable end. Faith in anything has collapsed; the abyss has swallowed all novelty. A stolen love, a fatal drop, and a cage within — the advice given is simply to press the pedal and stop stalling the crash.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~blackest-eyes":{"haiku":"A predatory figure describes urges and secrets, luring someone into darkness under the guise of beautiful seduction.","opus":"A lullaby that sours into threat, tracing a boy turned feral, his mind miswired, shelves of unopened books, self-inflicted scars, figures hidden beneath his bed. Steven Wilson and Porcupine Tree assemble an unnerving first-person inventory of buried secrets and dead ambitions, the coaxing invitation to plunge into his darkened gaze carrying a predatory chill under the melody.","sonnet":"A disturbed inner world is catalogued with chilling calm — a character lists their concealed compulsions, the secrets buried in domestic spaces, and the pull toward a darkness they invite another into. The lullaby frame at the opening marks the childhood origin of something that grew wrong.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~bonnie-the-cat":{"haiku":"Dark anticipation of an inevitable future unfolds through unsettling imagery—control over birth, ransom of bodily autonomy, cells dividing—suggesting reproductive coercion or trauma.","opus":"Steven Wilson circling a menacing, obsessive intimacy, the narrator claiming foreknowledge and holding a lover's fertility hostage, the calm delivery making the quiet threat and control all the more unsettling.","sonnet":"A cold, calculating perspective from someone in a failing or ended relationship who has deliberately sabotaged any chance of pregnancy by withholding or destroying birth control — detached and certain about how things will go, with no love left.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~buying-new-soul-lyrics-on-screen":{"haiku":"A person repeatedly attempts to reinvent and escape themselves each year by purchasing a new soul. The song captures the cycle of self-erasure and renewal.","opus":"A worn-out artist's confession, wishing he'd cashed in when temptation offered, still waging his small hopeless struggle and knocking his skull against the barrier, resolving with each new year to purchase another spirit at steep cost. It's a tribute to those who slip quietly out of view. Porcupine Tree, led by Steven Wilson, unfold it as a slow meditation on compromise and being forgotten, the narrator a faint figure, resigned and self-aware.","sonnet":"A portrait of persistent creative futility — missing the moment to sell out, waving at people already too far away to see, battering at the same walls with the same small wars. The solution attempted is buying a new soul each year, a hymn for those who vanish trying.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~deadwing":{"haiku":"Something warm and intangible steals away precious things, leaving only darkness and the speaker's isolation behind.","opus":"A spectral, dreamlike study of loss and detachment, as some tender presence drifts past and carries off what the narrator treasured. His confessed coldness (no charity to give, jealous of his solitude, forever surveilled) crashes against the grief of losing someone, closing on him flinging a pane wide and stepping out, an image poised between death and escape.","sonnet":"A paranoid, fragmented portrait of modern alienation — someone rifling through your life in the dark, surveillance cameras everywhere, a dream of parents on a beach, and the terror of being too afraid to touch anyone or even learn their name.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track is a ghost story with a Steven Wilson inversion at its core: what plays as a haunting is really the living pressing in on a narrator who has already died. The choruses withhold sense on purpose, firing off disconnected little terrors so the paranoid drift enacts a mind that can no longer order what happens into a story. The bridge tips it — a resentful claim to being left alone, then a jab about being watched by a hidden camera, recasting the speaker as the surveilled revenant who despises the intrusion of the breathing. By the closing verse the two worlds merge, and the last image resolves with terrible calm: he pushes a window wide and climbs out, an exit that reads as suicide and as the ghost passing over at once.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind","surveillance & control","suicide"],"means":"the haunting inverted — it is the living pressing in on the already-dead","built":"the choruses withhold sense deliberately, disconnected terrors enacting a mind that can no longer order what happens into a story"},
"porcupine-tree~don-t-hate-me":{"haiku":"Invisibility and loneliness become the core wound, the speaker unable to articulate need while witnessing others' indifference to their disappearance.","opus":"Steven Wilson's portrait of urban isolation, a dead-of-night London where nothing moves and one voice pleads for even the smallest contact. The ache is in the smallness of the ask, ordinariness begging not to be despised by someone unreachable.","sonnet":"A speaker in a late-night, emptied city addresses someone they have disappointed or lost, asking not to be hated and not to be fought — and tentatively requesting the small mercy of an occasional phone call. The loneliness is complete and the ask is minimal.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~drawing-the-line":{"haiku":"The speaker sets firm boundaries with someone who has drained and manipulated them, reclaiming control and pride. Emotional separation becomes an act of self-preservation.","opus":"Steven Wilson's Porcupine Tree set clinical, faintly menacing imagery against an insistence on control. Facing someone cold and unmoved, the narrator repeats his resolve to establish a boundary, seize command, and shut the other person out to preserve his dignity, self-protection voiced as a detached, ritual severance from an emotionally distant partner.","sonnet":"A speaker disengages from someone whose indifference and cruelty have generated enough heat to burn — the decision to cut ties is cast as self-preservation and pride rather than anger. Drawing the line is a deliberate act of retrieval: taking back the soul that was being quietly exhausted by proximity to someone who gave nothing back.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~fear-of-a-blank-planet":{"haiku":"Youth trapped in apathy, medicated numbness, and digital distraction. Nothing holds meaning; curiosity and desire are dead; emptiness is the default state.","opus":"A numb, disaffected teenager narrates a life of boredom, prescription drugs, screens, porn and disconnection from parents. Emotionally hollowed out, he questions whether he even exists and drifts toward violent, empty impulses.","sonnet":"A portrait of a disengaged, medicated adolescent — living in front of screens, shoplifting out of boredom, numb to sex and friendship — who has been so flattened by prescription drugs and overstimulation that he genuinely wonders whether he still exists.","src":"haiku","fable":"Steven Wilson builds the album's title track as a first-person tour through a teenager so anaesthetised that rebellion has curdled into vacancy — this is not a kid raging against the suburbs but one being slowly erased by them. The catalogue is deliberately flat and interchangeable: the game console, the screen that never sleeps, the pornography that has stopped delivering, the sedatives stacking up, the parents given up on, all recited in the same bored monotone so no single vice registers as worse than any other. Wilson's real subject surfaces in the chorus, where the medication meant to steady the boy instead loosens his grip on being anywhere at all, and the plea turns metaphysical: he needs a witness merely to confirm he exists. By the outro Wilson drops the sneering catalogue for something colder — a second-person litany of negations, no sun, no care, a stilled world — that reads less like a portrait of a person than the outline where one used to be, the blank planet of the title rendered human.","fv":2,"fnote":"The concept and much of the language come from Bret Easton Ellis’s Lunar Park — the album largely speaks as the novel’s eleven-year-old Robby — and the title plays on Public Enemy’s Fear of a Black Planet.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a teenager erased by the suburbs rather than raging against them","built":"outro shifts to second-person negations, rendering the portrait as an outline"},
"porcupine-tree~four-chords-that-made-a-million":{"haiku":"Commercial mediocrity masquerades as innovation; simple formulas and manufactured personas substitute for authentic artistry.","opus":"A jaded takedown of manufactured pop stardom, the empty-suited hitmaker feted then dropped by industry cynics; Wilson's disgust curdles into resigned grief at art hollowed out into product no one can defend.","sonnet":"A sardonic takedown of the music industry's cycle of manufactured stardom — vacuous performers, cynical A&R figures who build artists up and drop them, and the speaker's repeated failed attempts to break through. The song indicts commercial pop's formula while acknowledging personal exhaustion with the system.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~gravity-eyelids":{"haiku":"A person begs for intimacy and connection, reaching for their lover's eyes and skin. The song is an ache for sensual, intimate presence.","opus":"An intimate, hypnotic scene of a lover drifting toward sleep, the narrator pleading to be heard before he loses his mind, watching gravity pull her eyelids down as his hands slip past her waist. Tenderness and quiet desperation entwine as her breathing slows. From Porcupine Tree's Lightbulb Sun, Steven Wilson lets the track unwind from a whispered plea into a swelling prog-rock climax, the coil of longing tightening beneath the calm.","sonnet":"A lover pulling another person out of their own fatigue and distance — the gravity eyelids are both an accusation and an invitation, asking them to open up, to let salt flow, to stop being sealed off. The physical intimacy is inseparable from the emotional request: come back, all the way down.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~great-expectations":{"haiku":"Fragmented memory of a person who has been locked away and forgotten. The speaker reaches across distance trying to understand their fate.","opus":"A fragmentary meditation on someone institutionalized and then deliberately forgotten; the narrator clings to a useless hope of reconnecting while admitting he suppressed even the person's name, wondering guiltily whether they ever got out.","sonnet":"A fragment of longing for someone who was locked away and forgotten — the narrator reaches out across time to a person with placid, unknowing eyes, unsure if they ever got out, unsure if the connection can still be recovered.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~halo":{"opus":"A withering portrait of self-righteous certainty, a believer convinced God lives in his trigger and grants him a halo above everyone else. Steven Wilson equates deity with power, fame, fashion and the cellphone, exposing how religion fuses with ego and violence. The narrator's glow is really the smugness of the dangerously convinced.","sonnet":"A sharp satire of religious self-righteousness — the narrator ventriloquizes the voice of someone who has weaponized God as personal validation, using faith to claim superiority, moral authority, and a halo of purity over everyone else. Steven Wilson's critique is that God becomes whatever the believer needs to feel exceptional and untouchable.","src":"sonnet"},
"porcupine-tree~harridan":{"haiku":"Solitude and suffering hidden from the world. When buried, pain remains private; the speaker becomes a shadow without the other.","opus":"Steven Wilson's meditation on grim self-reliance, the notion that no one is coming to rescue you and that suffering is meant to be concealed once you're in the ground. The stoicism sours into a lament for what remains when love has vanished, independence sliding into bleak desolation.","sonnet":"A song about stoic self-reliance in the face of loss and suffering — the world is run by cold, silver-tongued men who keep their wounds hidden, and the narrator endures the quiet devastation of a life without someone, asking what remains when they are gone.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~harridan-closure-continuation-live":{"haiku":"Isolation and grief settle into the bones as truth becomes unbearable; the speaker grapples with loss, absence, and the impossibility of remaining whole when severed from another.","opus":"Men who swallow hard truths and hide their wounds from the world, gripping the wheel of a life that only wants to take from them. Beneath the bitterness runs a hollowed-out grief over what is left without a lost love.","sonnet":"A cold man grips the wheel and hides his wounds from the world, performing toughness while privately devastated by loss — the refrain of \"almost rain\" conjures a grief so total that nothing of the self remains without the absent other.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~heartattack-in-a-layby":{"haiku":"Burnout and separation fracture intimacy; the speaker breaks down physically and emotionally while duty pulls elsewhere.","opus":"A man pulls over feeling unwell and quietly dies alone by the roadside, his fading thoughts drifting to the wife waiting at home; the horror is the ordinariness, dreams of growing old together dissolving as no one comes.","sonnet":"A man pulled over on the side of a motorway, feeling ill and burnt out, resists going home — perhaps dreading a confrontation with a partner — while the layby becomes a space for suspended time and honest self-assessment. The interweaving voices of home and absence suggest both longing and reluctance.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~herd-culling-single-edit":{"haiku":"Strange forces invade the homestead from above and beyond, scratching at doors as curse descends upon the land. No refuge exists; the lie perpetuates that security is possible.","opus":"From the heavier reunion era of the band, this dresses cosmic dread in rural-gothic clothing. A settler family arms itself against something lurking in the trees and sky, bolting doors that can never keep it out. The chanted accusations of lying blur paranoia and betrayal, suggesting the real menace may be human deceit as much as the lights overhead.","sonnet":"Something alien and threatening has moved into the homestead — scratching at the doors, coming through the walls, twenty feet above the ground, among the garbage cans — and whoever is doing this is a liar; the rural siege framing makes the threat feel both literal and ideological.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~lazarus":{"haiku":"A resurrected spirit is called down to the valley below where moonlight bleeds from their soul; they are invited to rest and be carried away by strength.","opus":"From Porcupine Tree's 2005 Deadwing, Steven Wilson's most fragile ballad. A gentle inner voice summons a man by name toward somewhere beyond life, pledging the strength to bear him away from a harsh world that was never his to begin with. A coaxing toward death disguised as comfort, cradled by memories of the past.","sonnet":"A gentle summons toward death imagined as rescue — moonlight bleeding from the soul, a voice in the fog leading down to a valley, golden summers rising like ghosts. David is told to rest, that this cold world was never suited to him, and the call comes finally by name: it's time to go.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~lightbulb-sun":{"haiku":"Isolation as sickness calcifies into refuge; the speaker retreats into enclosed safety, afraid of the world's noise and their own fragility.","opus":"A sickly child's shut-in world of drawn curtains, medicine, bedside TV and stale sealed air, tended by a mother while a friend peers in from beyond the bubble. The little sanctuary comforts and confines at once, until the clamor outside stirs a longing to be up and about again.","sonnet":"A sick child's retreat into illness as a kind of sanctuary: curtains drawn, mother providing medicine sweetened for palatability, a best friend visiting to stare through the \"germified air\" — until the noise from the street eventually calls the speaker back toward health.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~lips-of-ashes":{"opus":"Intimacy collapsing into paralysis and dead connection, the drive to get inside another person failing as the link between them goes cold.","sonnet":"A fractured intimacy of paralysis, fading connection, and an attempt to drill inward — the images of blue veins crashing and black eyes fading suggest a relationship or inner state in terminal collapse, with the speaker pressing deeper inside as the connection fails.","src":"sonnet"},
"porcupine-tree~mellotron-scratch":{"haiku":"A person longs for a lost lover, remembering how music made her cry and how they once collected memories together. The song mourns abandoned intimacy and the inability to keep someone.","opus":"A song about a fading love and a creative block braided together, the narrator staring at a blank page while a woman he can no longer reach prepares to leave, the scratch of a mellotron the sound that always made her cry. He begs the memory not to drag him back into black. From Porcupine Tree's Deadwing, Steven Wilson builds layered harmonies and mellotron washes into an elegy for both a relationship and the muse behind it.","sonnet":"A fading relationship held in the scratchy sound of a mellotron — the instrument makes her cry because it holds a shared past neither of them can reach anymore. The speaker can't write, can't rise, watches ardor slip away, and the only advice left is don't drag yourself back into the black.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~my-ashes":{"haiku":"A person scatters as ashes across memory and place, haunting childhood moments and forgotten versions of themselves. The song treats death and dispersal as remnants lingering in photos and piano keys.","opus":"An elegiac meditation on a wasted, damaged life and inherited pain, imagined from beyond death as the narrator's remains scatter over small, familiar scenes he can no longer touch. The remains long to go back and rescue the innocent child he abandoned. Porcupine Tree render mortality with tender, rain-soaked melancholy.","sonnet":"A meditation on a damaged childhood and the weight of inherited problems — the narrator carries parents' failures, was rejected and kept returning, and imagines their ashes drifting over the ordinary objects of an unlived life.","src":"haiku","fable":"The one calm ballad on an album otherwise consumed by a medicated, attention-fried adolescent, this is the record's suicide sung backwards — voiced from after the death, as scattered remains drifting down over the ordinary English landscape and household clutter the speaker leaves behind. Wilson makes the dead narrator gentle and forgiving where the living boy was numb: from this vantage the childhood he couldn't feel becomes something worth returning to and rescuing, a grief the person could only reach once he was past the reach of it. The song's quiet trick is that its tenderness is retrospective and therefore useless to the one who needed it, so its softness reads less as comfort than as the final, too-late clarity of a life reviewed from outside itself.","fv":2,"fnote":"Co-written with Richard Barbieri as a homage to Lunar Park’s final chapter, where a father’s scattered ashes settle over the memories of his life.","themes":["suicide","death & grief","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"clarity arriving only once the person is past reach of what it might have saved","built":"suicide sung backwards: narrated posthumously from scattered remains"},
"porcupine-tree~nine-cats":{"haiku":"Surreal dreamscape and nonsensical imagery dissolve meaning; the narrator witnesses impossible tableaux without understanding their significance or how they arrived there.","opus":"A whimsical psychedelic dreamscape of impossible images strung together, the singer wandering it baffled by his own presence; the nonsense is the point, a surreal fever-vision without meaning to decode.","sonnet":"A procession of surreal, dreamlike images — talking animals, dancing pharaohs, minstrels and foxes — accumulates into a vision the speaker cannot decode, only witnessing it with the feeling of having been sent into a mystery they were never meant to understand.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~normal":{"haiku":"Surface comfort masking spiritual emptiness; modern sedation through material objects, medication, and dissociation from genuine feeling.","opus":"A portrait of modern hollowness and consumer-sedated numbness: the narrator owns every gadget yet feels see-through and barely present, ringed by listless, stoned kids frittering their days away. He wishes himself old and wistful, and clings to a plea to notice the ocean rather than the trash floating in it.","sonnet":"A portrait of suburban emptiness — behind the car, the phone, and the TV, nobody is really present; kids numb themselves with drugs in malls wishing away their days, and the narrator can only do a good impression of himself while wondering what normal even means.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~octane-twisted":{"haiku":"Blood flows as sorrow is pursued; the repeated plea for something new and loved suggests spiritual or emotional depletion. The fragmented lyrics convey dissolution and desperate seeking.","opus":"A fragmentary, hallucinatory piece more mood than narrative, layering images of loss and suffocation against a desperate wish for renewal and air. Steven Wilson's overlapping pleas set grief and drowning beside a craving for something fresh to hold onto, evoking the sensation of going under rather than telling a clear story.","sonnet":"A cyclical, hypnotic fragment about blood flowing through reeds and streams — a trance-like search for something new to love and breathe, following sorrow toward an unknown release.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~of-the-new-day":{"haiku":"Transformation and renewal marked by the passage into a new day. The speaker releases old patterns and fears to embrace an undefined future beyond past identities.","opus":"A calm, later-period Porcupine Tree song that urges surrender over resistance, telling the listener to stop fighting and let dread go while easing toward a fresh start. Even losses and partings are accepted as part of moving on, with the past falling away behind rather than blocking the road ahead.","sonnet":"An invitation to release fear and lean into transition — to let the old days recede into fog while something unnamed but anticipated takes their place. The new day is not triumphant but quiet, arriving like almost-rain at the border of night, asking only that the person stop clinging to pages already torn out.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~open-car":{"haiku":"An affair unfolds—initial seduction and desire turn to emotional wreckage; the speaker becomes consumed by hidden feelings and inevitable heartbreak.","opus":"Porcupine Tree's heavier phase drives a chronicle of an affair rotting in real time. Helpless attraction and a kept keepsake curdle into a dim room, cigarette smoke and a sick lurch in the gut. He pours everything in and discovers the relationship is torment, a fleeting image of wind in her hair glinting as doomed, throwaway beauty.","sonnet":"A man is consumed and undone by a woman he cannot leave and cannot fully have. The affair hollows him — sex followed by emptiness, everything given and nothing settled. A summer image of hair in an open car crystallises the desire; what breaks inside him is the realisation that being with her is its own kind of hell.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~prodigal":{"haiku":"A lost person tries escape through religion and substances, only to lose both faith and money; isolated by estrangement, they find solace in friendships they know are temporary.","opus":"A drifting confession of never fitting in, of trying faith and losing it, taking money and going astray, chasing escape through pills and smoke without ever grasping the point. Porcupine Tree frame the malaise as a life observed like a TV show nearing its finale, friends the sole anchor, the weather beyond the window mirroring the gloom.","sonnet":"A man adrift tries every available escape — religion, substances, community — and finds himself unchanged, arriving at a headstone-like resignation. His closest relationships are with the lost, and the series-ending metaphor implies he has always known this story was finite, even while living inside it.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~rats-return":{"haiku":"A cynical survey of historical and contemporary power — Genghis Khan to Pinochet — argues that systems of corruption cycle endlessly back, and that moral conscience is no weapon in that war.","opus":"A seething indictment of tyrants and opportunists who ditch principle to grab power. Cataloguing history's dictators, it watches the vermin come back to take a bow, warning that conscience is a handicap in a war of frauds. The Steven Wilson lyric strips the moral veneer to expose the predatory animal waking in those who always game the system.","sonnet":"A litany of historical tyrants — Genghis Khan, Pinochet, Mao, Kim Il-Sung — invoked as evidence that conscience doesn't win wars and principles must be shed at the door. The carnivore wakes in the dog; those who played the system return to take their bow.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~remember-me-lover":{"opus":"The unrepentant confession of someone ending a toxic relationship, cataloging the harm he did (walking away, lying, taking money, ruining her life) with cold contempt, telling the partner she deserves to be alone and to just remember him.","sonnet":"A brutally honest post-breakup accounting where the narrator admits to lying, taking money, walking away, and ruining the other person's life — delivered without remorse, almost as a self-aware epitaph. The repeated 'remember me lover' is laced with dark irony: he is asking to be remembered precisely for his worst acts.","src":"sonnet"},
"porcupine-tree~russia-on-ice":{"haiku":"Profound alienation and rejection by others fuel internal combustion as cold indifference burns holes through consciousness.","opus":"A crumbling relationship seen through a self-loathing drinker who burns everything to the ground, clinging to a frozen inner fire while the other person's hatred makes collapse only a matter of time.","sonnet":"A relationship in alcoholic collapse: the speaker cannot stop drinking or being themselves, burns everything to the ground, and is told they are hated this way — while Russia on ice, a paradox of freezing and burning, anchors the image of something that should be cold but is destroying everything from within.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~sentimental":{"haiku":"Youth stoned and bored, wishing away each day. The speaker rejects adulthood and parental blame while uncertain if medication or mortality is the answer.","opus":"A study of aimless youthful disaffection and the dread of aging; the narrator refuses to grow old or take responsibility while numbly medicated and unsure the pills help, watching bored, stoned kids in malls wish their lives away exactly as he is wasting his own.","sonnet":"A portrait of generational drift and medicated apathy — young people wish their days away in malls and suburban numbness while the narrator questions whether he's normal, whether his pills are helping, and whether any of it amounts to anything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of suburban adolescent anesthesia, sung from inside the fog it diagnoses. Steven Wilson pins his narrator between two refusals — he won't grow up and won't own his own paralysis — so the medicated numbness reads less as rebellion than as a slow surrender, the prescription doing exactly the flattening the tedium already began. Wilson's masterstroke is the switch to third person: the chorus pulls back to watch the mall kids drift through interchangeable afternoons, and that cool aerial distance is the real horror, because the same voice that just confessed its own drift now files these children away as a demographic, complicit in the very indifference it describes.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"paralysis filed away as a demographic rather than owned as a wound","built":"chorus switches to third person, implicating narrator in the indifference it describes"},
"porcupine-tree~shallow":{"haiku":"A person avoids meaningful connections and emotional depth, preferring isolation in a functionally hollow existence.","opus":"Steven Wilson's crunchier era yields a portrait of atrophied isolation, a mind that lives only to function, friendless and drained by the city's fumes and crowds. Talking to a screen comes easier than to people. The demand for something shallow, refusing blood or genuine anger, captures a numbed detachment that would rather bite its tongue than feel the splinter of real connection.","sonnet":"Urban isolation stripped to its nerve: a loner who finds crowded cities crushing and other people's emotional demands exhausting. Self-sufficient by necessity, he deflects the shallowness of others while quietly admitting his own screen-mediated withdrawal from human contact.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~sleep-together":{"haiku":"An escape from pressure and duty through mutual abandonment and erasure, leaving behind all traces of former identity.","opus":"Two people conspiring to wipe out every trace of themselves and flee their lives as one, cast as destiny and a last exit. Falling into bed together becomes a means to bleed off the strain and shut down what lies ahead for good.","sonnet":"Two people choose a complete exit from their lives — erasing files, burning possessions, abandoning the future — in a pact that frames escape as relief and a shared surrender as the only way to release pressure.","src":"haiku","fable":"Closing Fear of a Blank Planet, this is the numbed adolescent of the record finally offered a way out, and the horror is how gently it arrives — sleeping together and leaving forever are the same act, sex and suicide collapsed into one flat imperative to switch off the future. Wilson makes the escape route out of pure erasure: deleting files, burning the designer trainers, leaving no trace, as if a life so mediated by screens and brand-names could simply be logged out of. The relief on offer is not release but shutdown, and the song mistakes oblivion for the only remaining tenderness.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape"],"means":"oblivion mistaken for the only remaining tenderness on offer","built":"sleeping together and leaving forever collapsed into a single flat imperative","fvr":"2.4"},
"porcupine-tree~strip-the-soul":{"haiku":"A person uses a machine to dominate and strip the humanity from others, keeping bones hidden beneath while burying victims and breaking them down systematically.","opus":"Among the band's grimmest tracks, this speaks through a monstrous domestic voice presiding over a household of abuse, bound children and corpses stashed under the boards. Its stated purpose is to satisfy while it hollows out and consumes. Botched electrics, sealed-over walls, bones intentionally saved; the parting offer of a homestead amid the wreckage makes the horror sickeningly casual.","sonnet":"A domestic abuser and predator speaks in flat, possessive declarations — the home as domain, the family as property, violence as discipline. Beneath floorboards, evidence accumulates. The machine of exploitation keeps running, stripping away what is human, leaving only walls bricked around bones.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~synesthesia":{"haiku":"Military casualties reduce human loss to statistics; the narrator describes a soldier dying in anonymity while bureaucracy processes their death as mere number.","opus":"A soldier's dying letter home, bleeding out on a stretcher while the war machine reduces his death to a statistic; the gut-punch is the gap between one man's final moments and the cold telegram that regrets a mere number.","sonnet":"A dying soldier writes a final letter from a battlefield stretcher, confronting the bureaucratic indifference that reduces his death to a number and a telegram, knowing the words will arrive after his last breath and that no one will register the loss as anything more than a statistic.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~the-blind-house":{"haiku":"A closed community isolates members from outside influence, using faith and false authority to control behavior through manipulation and abuse.","opus":"Wilson inhabits the voice of a cult patriarch justifying the abuse, control and hypocrisy behind the compound walls, the language of free love masking coercion. It's about how insular faith seals people off to hide predation as protection.","sonnet":"A song about a cult or controlling religious community — the leader promises love and safety inside the walls while framing the outside world as corrupt, demands submission and silence, and then abuses the trust of those inside. The \"free love\" refrain is ironic: what's described is violation and the sealing of a family's fate through lies.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a dramatic monologue spoken from inside the mask, the voice of a cult patriarch or abusive father, and the whole read lives in hearing what leaks out from under his tenderness. He frames the sealed household as shelter from a corrupting world, but each reassurance curdles into control, so that seeing only through his sanctioned gaze leaves no vision of one's own. Wilson, working within The Incident's larger structure about news items and the catastrophes behind headlines, times the reveal precisely, dropping the euphemism in the third verse to name the machinery of abuse and the parent's buried loathing offloaded onto the young. The refrain lands as the cruelest stroke, singing the promise of liberated affection and the shuttered home as one gentle vow. What the mask professes is haven; beneath it, the haven and the blinding are revealed as the same wall.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["shame & repression","surveillance & control","faith & the occult"],"means":"the language of shelter and the machinery of abuse delivered in the same gentle voice","built":"the refrain sings the promise of liberated affection and the shuttered home as a single vow, haven and blinding revealed as the same wall"},
"porcupine-tree~the-creator-has-a-mastertape":{"haiku":"A god-like creator captured and imprisoned everything of value, leaving only void and the speaker's dream of staring into nothingness. Loss and archival merge into futility.","opus":"A grotesque vignette of a domestic predator, the family man who collects and abuses, set against a numb narrator who finds the void more comforting than the horror around him.","sonnet":"A bleak portrait of a man who collects, controls, and destroys — binding his family, driving a spike into his head, leaving a home running red — set against the absurdist image of a creator who left the mastertape in a cab, losing control of his own work.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~the-incident":{"haiku":"A car crash disrupts routine, revealing the hollowness and alienation beneath everyday existence.","opus":"Sparked by a motorway sign about a police incident, Wilson meditates on how catastrophe becomes just detached background noise on a drive elsewhere. Beneath the numbness sits a plain, aching need to be loved.","sonnet":"About a motorway pileup as a lens for modern disconnection — rubberneckers, people for whom even disaster can't cut through numbness, driving through the burning wreckage and the grey concrete and just wanting to be loved by something.","src":"haiku","fable":"Steven Wilson grew this concept suite from a motorway sign reading INCIDENT, the antiseptic bureaucratic word that shrinks catastrophe into mere delay, and the title track stages exactly that collapse of feeling into deadened spectacle. Its verses catalogue a nervous system numbed past sensation, where only violence stirs arousal and a highway pileup arrives dressed as confetti and bright plumage, disaster made beautiful because nothing else can register. The piece cycles three mechanical mantras that mark its shape as descent, not narrative: a sneering demolition chant, a craving-loop repeated until longing curdles into tic, and a naked childlike plea for love once the irony burns off. That final repetition is the read; cruelty and detachment were armor, and once the wreckage tears it off, all beneath is the plainest hunger there is, spoken flat because the speaker forgot any other way.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["dissociation & numbness","love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"cruelty and detachment as armor, the plainest hunger exposed once the wreckage tears it off","built":"three mechanical mantras mark the shape as descent — the irony burns off and the closing repetition is spoken flat because the speaker forgot any other register"},
"porcupine-tree~the-seance":{"haiku":"Supernatural connection and channeled sorrow blur as the speaker attempts communion with the dead, mourning becoming ritual and connection becoming entanglement.","opus":"A Victorian séance where the desperate pay a medium to summon a lost presence, hands joined and current passing. Grief reaches into the dark pleading for a sign, for something new left to love.","sonnet":"A Victorian séance: joined hands chant a name, a table tilts, a luminous hand passes the current through broken circles, and a channelled voice uncoils a rope — someone is here, and the speaker begs for a sign they can breathe, something new to love.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~the-sound-of-muzak":{"haiku":"Commercial, engineered music suppresses thought and authentic rebellion, turning the soul numb through demographic manipulation.","opus":"A scathing takedown of a music business built to numb listeners rather than stir them, where even rebellion gets packaged and sold by wealthy men chasing a demographic. Its real ache is that something precious is quietly dying and nobody minds. From 2002's In Absentia, Steven Wilson delivers the critique through a deliberately catchy prog hook, biting the hand that feeds.","sonnet":"A lament for music's co-option by commerce and passivity — background sound engineered to suppress rather than elevate, rebellion packaged by millionaires for demographic consumption. The wonder of the world is going down and no one cares enough to notice the blunder happening in plain sight.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~the-start-of-something-beautiful":{"haiku":"Someone romanticizes an unrequited relationship, misreading friendship as the start of something deeper, only to realize the other person never shared their feelings.","opus":"A one-sided romance dismantled: the narrator idealizes an unreachable person he keeps on tape and in photographs, only to discover the more he reveals his feelings the less she cares. The cruel twist lands in the refrain, whatever he imagined as the start of something beautiful was never mutual, and hints of her damaged family suggest a coldness in her heart he mistook for closeness.","sonnet":"One-sided romantic feelings meeting total indifference — the narrator is deeply invested in someone who sees them only as a friend, and the mounting revelation of that mismatch becomes bitterly personal as it unfolds across family-level sacrifice and emotional cruelty.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~time-flies":{"haiku":"The speaker reflects on aging and how time accelerates, learning that the best approach is to accept what comes and embrace lived moments.","opus":"Steven Wilson's autobiographical stocktaking, entering the world alongside a landmark 1967 record and watching suburban certainty melt as he registers how quickly a life passes. Nostalgia and mortality converge in warm, specific recollections of someone no longer around.","sonnet":"A nostalgic meditation on how quickly life passes — the narrator, born in 1967, reflects on summers, a woman's advice to seize the day, and the strange disorientation of watching time dissolve relationships and sharpen regrets.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~trains":{"haiku":"A nostalgic meditation on summer romance tied to railway imagery, capturing the bittersweet feeling of moments slipping away and the desire to make time stand still.","opus":"From Porcupine Tree's 2002 album In Absentia, this is their most accessible song, Steven Wilson wrapping a wistful acoustic melody around an unresolved ache. It is a nostalgic reverie in which railways carry memory, first love and mortality along the same line, the narrator always conscious that the seasons are draining away and begging for some way to hold them still.","sonnet":"Summer memory encoded in the sound and sensation of passing trains — childhood beds, hissing rail, a sixty-ton angel fallen to earth. The summers keep slipping and the speaker wants a way to make any of it stay, finding instead that love and loss arrive together with the evening, and that's somehow still okay.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~waiting-phase-one":{"haiku":"Numb waiting and self-harm substitute for authentic living as the narrator postpones existence through cycles of suffering and chemical escape.","opus":"A numbed craving for pain, drugs and even punishment as the only ways to feel real, the narrator waiting for the day he can finally crawl away.","sonnet":"Pure suspension: waiting to be born again, for drugs to make pain real, for discipline and nails across skin — each stanza a variation on the same held breath before crawling away, the desire for sensation and transformation held just out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~way-out-of-here":{"haiku":"The speaker contemplates escape and fading away, unable to bear sympathy or scrutiny, fantasizing about erasure and forgetting in response to loss or crisis. The song centers on the desire to vanish rather than process grief.","opus":"A grieving person's desperate wish to escape and vanish from the staring, sympathy, and prying questions after a loss, resolving to erase all traces and memories of someone in order to forget them.","sonnet":"An adolescent yearns to disappear — escaping the stares, the sympathy, and the intrusive questions about how they feel — and imagines erasing every trace of a person from their life, burning pictures and forgetting names, while at the train tracks dreaming of a way out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A suburban teenager rehearses his own disappearance as the only privacy left to him, treating vanishing as the cure for a life lived under adult surveillance and well-meaning concern. The counselling voices — inquiries about school and feelings — register as intrusion, so escape means erasing every trace of the person he was: the photographs torched, a face scissored out, the car got rid of, the girl he means to forget down to her sleeping form. Wilson makes the numbness of Fear of a Blank Planet audible here, delivering the runaway checklist with the flat calm of someone already gone missing inside, where the repeated exit is less a destination than a fantasy of ceasing to register at all.","fv":2,"fnote":"Inspired by the 2005 deaths of two Wisconsin teenagers in an apparent pact on Amtrak tracks — one, 17, had built the band’s MySpace page and was reportedly listening to Porcupine Tree when she died. Wilson has said the idea that young people would seek escape through such brutal, extreme means disturbed him into the song.","themes":["freedom & escape","surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"vanishing rehearsed as the only privacy surveillance hasn't reached","built":"the runaway checklist delivered with the flat calm of someone already gone missing inside"},
"porcupine-tree~wedding-nails":{"haiku":"Visceral intimacy and physical possession; breaking through to depths of unguarded vulnerability.","opus":"Largely an instrumental showcase, its sparse words evoke a charged, sensual intimacy, unwinding tension like a coil finally released. Touch, slowed breathing, and gravity-heavy eyelids sketch a surrender in the pale dark. More atmosphere than narrative, it reaches for the hushed, electric threshold between waiting and consummation rather than telling a story.","sonnet":"A slowly unwinding physical intimacy — gravity pulling eyelids down, hands sliding past the waist, the body going to the edge — spoken to someone who is both teasing and tipping the speaker toward dissolution. The imagery of oil skin, salt, and dark-browned eyes gives it a quality closer to trance than passion.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~what-happens-now-lyrics":{"haiku":"Material possessions mean nothing; the narrator contemplates mortality and what awaits after death with existential uncertainty.","opus":"Steven Wilson confronting mortality and the emptiness of possessions, staging his own possible death, even as a bomb victim, to force the question no faith fully answers. It sits in the modern dread of dying without knowing what, if anything, follows.","sonnet":"An existential shrug at death and the void — the narrator rejects spiritual salvation as hollow, imagines being scattered in a plane crash, and repeats the unanswerable question of what comes after.","src":"haiku"},
"porcupine-tree~your-unpleasant-family":{"haiku":"Devastation caused by a partner's family and the remnants of destruction left behind; the speaker regrets shared memories now corrupted by loss.","opus":"Steven Wilson's Porcupine Tree slip into pitch-black domestic comedy, cataloguing an in-law clan who smashed up the narrator's car with vile, uncalled-for relish. He crawls from the wreckage on his knees, then a soothing voice asks what was learned, finding regret among the trees. Snapshots of a garden life curdle as they tear up the flowers, absurdity and menace intertwined.","sonnet":"A relationship ends amid the wreckage left by the other person's hostile family — property destroyed, dignity shattered. Crawling from the wreckage, he finds only regret and the remnants of a life photographed in happier times, now torn apart like the flowers in the garden.","src":"haiku"},
"porno-for-pyros~tahitian-moon":{"haiku":"A sinking boat and distant shore symbolize isolation, yet the speaker trusts they can survive alone beneath tropical stars.","opus":"A swimmer whose boat has overturned faces drowning and finds an odd serenity in it, the ocean cast as a gentle way to slip out of existence. Mourning for a vanished friend and the pull of death melt together into moonlit stillness.","sonnet":"Adrift on a capsized boat at night, losing sight of shore and searching for a lost friend, the narrator finds strange peace in the knowledge that he can swim under the Tahitian moon — the sea as a place to dissolve, to disappear, to make peace. Loss and beauty occupy the same water.","src":"haiku"},
"porn~a-lovely-day":{"haiku":"Desperation for freedom mingles with resignation as the narrator acknowledges their struggle against confining structures. A momentary escape fantasy plays against the weight of daily survival and the thin line between hope and surrender.","opus":"A jittery, hungover push toward escape, insisting on breaking down the walls and finding a way out. Beneath the sunny refrain lies a restless need to flee whatever the narrator is trapped in.","sonnet":"Trapped behind visible walls, the speaker declares escape the only option, repeating \"what a lovely day\" with bitter irony as they push toward a way out, framing the act of leaving as both self-preservation and forward motion.","src":"haiku"},
"port-noir~black-from-the-ink":{"opus":"A takedown of an emotional parasite, a leech-like person who has hollowed themselves into a perfect lie and feeds off everyone who opens their heart, with the narrator begging to be left alone before he too is drained.","sonnet":"A portrait of a manipulative, emotionally vampiric person — someone who drains others, performs victimhood, feeds off empathy, and has become so immersed in their own lies that they are corrupted to the core, leaving everyone around them hollow.","src":"sonnet"},
"port-noir~blow":{"opus":"A disillusioned observation of hollow, cocaine-fueled hedonism among people chasing toxic leisure and status, betting on a fading light; the singer watches them wild and self-loathing, drowning in vice while pretending nothing matters.","sonnet":"A portrait of people consuming themselves on cocaine and toxic pleasure, drowning in self-loathing and feeding their addictions while the narrator watches with a mixture of complicity and despair, betting on a light that keeps fading out.","src":"sonnet"},
"port-noir~champagne":{"haiku":"A person wields power through withholding and control. The other must serve or face burial; obedience is mandatory.","opus":"A menacing power fantasy from the mouth of a manipulator who holds all the leverage, demanding submission and promising ruin to anyone who sells him out. The velvet threat is the mood: control absolute, the victim having dug their own grave.","sonnet":"A song about domination and the threat of violent retribution — someone refuses to comply or serve, and the narrator responds with an escalating power play, making clear that non-compliance means being buried, while positioning themselves as a force even god won't contest.","src":"haiku"},
"port-noir~emerald-green":{"opus":"Addiction and codependency braided together, chemical numbing chased until nothing can be felt, chlorine and morphine dulling shame. Port Noir stages a toxic bond as fresh incisions and a new religion, one partner stripping skin to be taken in. The emerald green becomes the seductive, damaging color of that dependency.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship narrated as mutual chemical addiction — chlorine, morphine, blackouts, and cuts describe both a destructive lover and the numbing substances used to cope with them. The 'emerald green' is the seductive poison of that person, stripping the narrator bare while binding them in dependency.","src":"sonnet"},
"port-noir~flawless":{"haiku":"Two people trapped in mutual deception and hollow pretense refuse to acknowledge the decay of their connection or the lies that bind them. Neither can break free or be honest about what they've become.","opus":"The Swedish band, welding groove-metal weight to unexpected melody, indict a collective grave where everyone's roped together and nobody's free, deception binding as tightly as any bond. The push to crank the volume answers a rigged, predetermined game in which no rescue arrives, the narrator flatly refusing the phony perfection forced on him.","sonnet":"Two people are locked together in a dynamic neither can escape — lies binding them, neither winning, both sinking under the weight of scripts they no longer believe. The speaker resists the hollow performance of perfection that the situation demands, shouting louder not in triumph but in desperate refusal to keep pretending.","src":"haiku"},
"port-noir~young-bloods":{"haiku":"Youth are trapped in cycles of complacency and decay, refusing to evolve or change while the narrator rejects their passivity.","opus":"A scathing look at a generation likened to a spreading blight, hoarding riches atop the rot, doping themselves numb and never pushing for change while circling in a self-made cage of conflict. The narrator refuses to kneel or be lumped in, snapping that nobody really knows him. Port Noir, the Swedish trio, give the disgust a slippery, genre-blurring swing.","sonnet":"A generation diagnosed as simultaneously inert and destructive — spreading like weeds, hoarding wealth, numbing out on cigarettes and repetition, moving inside a war cage without seeking any exit. The speaker refuses to bow to it, has lived through enough to reject the script, but watches the young bloods cycle through the same tall tale with nothing changing.","src":"haiku"},
"porter-robinson~cheerleader":{"haiku":"Obsessive devotion and possessive love transform supporter into tormentor; the speaker questions whether they value the person or their dependency.","opus":"An unsettling flip on devotion, the singer haunted by a possessive cheerleader who lived for him, unsure whether he wishes her well or actually misses her now that she's gone.","sonnet":"About realizing too late how much a devoted, obsessive person meant to you — the cheerleader gave unconditional support even as the relationship became warped and possessive, and the speaker doesn't know whether to miss her or be relieved she's gone.","src":"haiku"},
"porter-robinson~mona-lisa":{"haiku":"Obsessive fixation on an inaccessible figure renders the observer's existence meaningful only through watching. Distance and longing sustain the speaker's purpose better than presence ever could.","opus":"Idealizing a girl everyone treats as a passive artwork to admire, until the fixation curdles into possessive violence. Adoration of a still-life muse collapses into her becoming just another face passing through the door.","sonnet":"An obsession with a still, beautiful, unreachable person — a \"still-life starlet\" who never leaves and always waits — that turns dark at the edges when the speaker, one more face through the door, imagines taking her home with blood on their face.","src":"haiku"},
"porter-robinson~years-of-war-rob-mayth-remix-feat-breanne-d-ren-sean-caskey":{"haiku":"Rebellion born from innocent crime unfolds across centuries of warfare, seeking retribution against kingdoms built on theft and treachery.","opus":"A revolt anthem, the dispossessed rising against a golden kingdom built on their backs, two centuries of grievance boiling into an uprising. The remix hardens the fantasy-tinged rebellion into fist-in-the-air momentum, vengeance as inheritance.","sonnet":"A generation of rebels who see themselves as children of an unjust order declares war on a corrupt kingdom, vowing to fight until freedom is won and what was stolen is reclaimed, with a lover promising to hold steady through the battle.","src":"haiku"},
"portico-quartet~steepless-feat-cornelia":{"haiku":"Transcendence through time and divine love promise escape from mortal limitation; the afterlife welcomes the soul beyond suffering and corporeal constraint.","opus":"A tender consolation for the dying or grieving, promising a rising into stillness beyond the body; tarot imagery of judgment and the fool frames death as passage toward a peace whose answers arrive only when time itself runs out.","sonnet":"A gentle elegy for someone who has died — their smile still lingers, the card of judgement called for peace, and the speaker trusts they will meet again in dreams, reaching toward a steepless state of calm beyond the tower of the body.","src":"haiku"},
"portrayal-of-guilt~daymare":{"haiku":"Oppression by institutional systems and internal terror. The speaker confronts corporate and personal demons while surrendering to fate and becoming one with their circumstances.","opus":"The Texas band's blackened screamo aims fury at corporate power, imagining enemies groveling for mercy in relentless repetition. Then the rage folds inward: suffering only postpones the next round, inner demons battle overhead, and the narrator finally gives up resistance, merging with himself and a world that leaves him frozen in place.","sonnet":"A figure wages war against institutional coldness, spitting at authority from within walls that show no mercy. Paralysed by internal demons fighting overhead, the self surrenders rather than resists — accepting fate, dissolving into the world around them as the only possible release from the suffering that has numbed everything else.","src":"haiku"},
"post-malone-swae-lee~sunflower-spider-man-into-the-spider-verse":{"haiku":"A fragile person fears abandonment; commitment requires reciprocal effort or they'll be left behind, a delicate relationship.","opus":"A featherweight, sun-warmed hook from the Spider-Verse soundtrack that made both artists inescapable in 2018. Beneath the breezy melody sits a restless push-pull with a partner who is too much to hold yet too magnetic to leave. The recurring flower image casts her as radiant but overwhelming, while the wreck of coming and going stays admittedly out of the singer's control.","sonnet":"A volatile relationship that keeps pulling two people back despite every attempt to exit. One partner clings with the brightness of a sunflower while the other cycles in and out, afraid of the unknown yet unable to fully leave, suspended between guilt and dependence.","src":"haiku"},
"post-malone~on-the-road-feat-meek-mill-lil-baby":{"haiku":"Isolation through relentless pursuit demands loyalty while the road separates the speaker from those who wavered, revealing who remained through absence.","opus":"Success as vindication and isolation, all three rappers clocking the fair-weather friends who reappear at the win and vanish at the work; the grind is proof of loyalty to self when money thins everyone else's.","sonnet":"Three artists assert their loyalty to their own grind against those who only appear when success does — cataloguing the cost of constant touring, the phoniness of fair-weather proximity, and the determination never to fold under pressure or sell their integrity. Each verse adds its own testimony to the same theme of earned resilience.","src":"haiku"},
"post-profit~cancer-culture":{"haiku":"Accusation and cancellation become tools of violence passed between groups, each side convinced of moral certainty while performing destruction in the name of righteousness.","opus":"A combative response to callout culture, the speaker daring to be canceled while feeling knocked down every time he stands. It circles self-censorship, biting one's tongue before contradicting oneself, and answers the outrage machine with defiance.","sonnet":"A direct address to cancel culture and online mob dynamics — the speaker feels hunted, one wrong word away from being obliterated, watching the crowd gun for them while they try to stay measured, furious at the disproportionate power of performative outrage.","src":"haiku"},
"post-profit~smother":{"haiku":"Emotional weight and unspoken suffering create suffocation where language fails to convey internal collapse.","opus":"A frustrated plea to a partner who buries their feelings behind a facade of everything being fine. The narrator, still searching for pieces of himself, wants honesty and a reason, but meets only denial. The suffocating title names the weight of an unspoken burden that neither person can quite describe yet cannot escape.","sonnet":"Someone is smothering the speaker in a way neither can articulate — \"the weight on your chest you can't describe\" — and despite the relationship sinking, neither party will leave; silence is demanded because words only make it heavier.","src":"haiku"},
"post-profit~two-toxic":{"haiku":"Two people in a mutually destructive dynamic recognize they stand on opposite ends yet keep gravitating back into each other's damage, addicted to the pain even as it strips them to a thread.","opus":"An unflinching look at a mutually destructive relationship the narrator can no longer dress up. Attempts at a tender tune collapse into an admission of being hooked on the hurt, barely holding on while the other keeps pushing past every limit. Stationed at the far poles, both know the gravity will haul them back, making the toxicity feel like fate.","sonnet":"A destructive pull that both parties recognize and cannot escape: the speaker knows they're on two opposite ends, knows the other takes it too far and leaves him hanging by a thread — but the gravity is mutual, addicted to the pain, and it's only a matter of time before being pulled back in.","src":"haiku"},
"powderfinger~baby-i-ve-got-you-on-my-mind":{"haiku":"Obsessive yearning and desperation for return; love as inescapable mental occupation.","opus":"Early, bluesy work from the Australian rock band, a straightforward declaration of consuming longing. The narrator cannot get someone off his mind and refuses to take no this time, pledging devotion around the clock, day and night. The recurring image of a heart dying when chains were loosed hints that this ardor rose from a hard-won release.","sonnet":"Obsessive romantic fixation expressed as simultaneous tenderness and desperation — my heart died when you loosed the chains — with the speaker refusing to take no this time and insisting on symmetry of feeling. The chorus pleads for the other to feel it too.","src":"haiku"},
"powderfinger~lost-and-running":{"haiku":"Restless longing drives a life spent moving between cities and relationships, pursuing a satisfaction that never settles; the speaker grasps for stability while trapped in an escalating cycle of displacement and craving.","opus":"A relationship worn down by boredom and blame, the singer restless and adrift through a dark city, still chasing something he can't name; exhaustion and the ache to lay down his arms and finally rest somewhere.","sonnet":"A man caught between a fractured relationship and a restless life speaks of exhaustion and yearning for peace, feeling swept along by forces he can't control and searching for somewhere he can finally put down his guard.","src":"haiku"},
"powderfinger~my-happiness":{"haiku":"Bittersweet joy in reunion shadowed by the pain of inevitable separation.","opus":"An Australian ache about a love thinned by long absence, happiness that only returns as the other person leaves. The quiet devastation is the narrator's guilt, knowing something inside has already changed for good.","sonnet":"A speaker counts down to a loved one's return, then realises upon arrival that something unspoken has changed — happiness creeps back in their presence but drains away the moment they leave, and the speaker knows something neither of them is saying.","src":"haiku"},
"powerman-5000~black-lipstick":{"haiku":"Transgressive seduction and emotional abandonment, intimacy without memory or connection.","opus":"A blurred night with a goth stranger who cried and sang Bauhaus before vanishing, leaving only a stain. The mirror-breaking finish reaches for the loneliness underneath the encounter, wanting her not to be alone.","sonnet":"A hazy one-night encounter with a nihilistic woman — she quoted gothic lyrics, cried, scratched messages into walls, and vanished by morning, leaving only lipstick stains and a fragmentary memory the speaker cannot piece back together.","src":"haiku"},
"powerman-5000~dancing-like-we-re-dead":{"haiku":"Hedonistic abandon in face of mortality, celebration despite inevitable death.","opus":"Industrial-tinged rock that turns cultural numbness into a danse macabre. Amid lobotomies and lottery prayers, people party as if alive while life has drained out of them, dancing like corpses toward the end. Peppered with pop-culture debris, it satirizes a society sleepwalking through its own demise, choosing distraction over any real pulse.","sonnet":"Societal zombification as midnight rave — lobotomies queued like lottery tickets, life no longer alive but bodies still acting up, \"all souls, no control.\" The severed head whose eyes you romance is the emblem of going through the motions of existence after everything that made it meaningful has been removed.","src":"haiku"},
"powerman-5000~when-worlds-collide":{"haiku":"A confrontation between opposing systems and entities culminates in a taking-over moment; the speaker guides chosen ones through the collision while systems of control break down.","opus":"A sci-fi apocalypse staged as swaggering spectacle, an all-encompassing regime of control and a narrator crowning himself the one seizing power as reality shatters. Powerman 5000, fronted by Spider One, turn the end of everything into a recruitment chant, asking who will follow, casting himself as a guide between good and evil while most of humanity is made to fall.","sonnet":"A charismatic voice offers itself as guide through a collapse where existing systems of control fail and the chosen few move forward while the rest fall away. The end of all time is pitched as invitation — a violent transition into something new that only those willing to follow will survive.","src":"haiku"},
"preoccupations~continental-shelf":{"haiku":"Anxiety and numbness characterize existence where the self is eroding at its edges and the world drains away like water off a shelf into nothing.","opus":"Canadian post-punk band Preoccupations render anxiety as slow geological erosion, with a narrator who feels numb, violated, and unable to navigate a suffocating world. Through drifting images of things crumbling at their edges and draining away, the song evokes a self quietly dissolving at its margins, nothing forming and everything slipping out of reach.","sonnet":"The mind edges toward dissolution — anxiety calcified into physical sensation, memory unreliable, the horizon folding inward. Images of gold liquid and melting skylines render a psyche in freefall, draining off the edge of itself like water off a continental shelf, with suffocation the dominant emotional register.","src":"haiku"},
"prescription-happiness~vitriol":{"haiku":"The speaker describes themselves as corrosive and toxic, harming everything they touch and rejecting attempts at redemption or connection.","opus":"A self-portrait as a corrosive substance: decomposed into a burnt-out star, the narrator sought the divine and found only himself, warning everyone to flee before he stains or dissolves them. Borrowing alchemical imagery, he casts his own destructiveness as a failed effort to harmonize and improve, the plea to be avoided doubling, in its grandiose self-loathing, as an admission of need.","sonnet":"A self-portrait as corrosive acid — the speaker has decomposed beyond redemption, tried and failed to stop burning everything they touch, and now warns others away while claiming the destruction with perverse pride. The alchemical image of the green lion devouring the sun frames this not as weakness but as a terrible, unstoppable transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"present~easy":{"haiku":"Surrendering to destructive choices becomes comfortable numbness when resistance feels harder.","opus":"A numb slide toward self-destruction where cruelty, misery and thoughts of being better off dead all feel effortless. The repeated ease is the horror: nothing matters enough anymore to resist.","sonnet":"A song sung from inside a state of total disengagement and self-destruction — the narrator describes domestic violence, suicidal ideation, and nihilism as things that feel easy because resistance has become impossible. It's about the terrifying comfort of giving up.","src":"haiku"},
"present~face-down":{"haiku":"Self-deprecation masks hostility; the speaker undermines intimacy through excessive talk and self-sabotage, creating distance through performative incompetence.","opus":"Chronic self-sabotage in intimacy: wanting closeness but talking and taunting yourself out of it, always ending up the small one in the middle. The performance is cheap and the speaker knows it, calling it quits before anyone else can.","sonnet":"A person who wants connection and intimacy keeps sabotaging themselves by talking too much or taunting, ending up face down in the middle of their own mess every time.","src":"haiku"},
"present~kill-the-moth":{"haiku":"A cycle of self-destruction and emotional distance—seeking oblivion through chemical indulgence while pushing away anyone who cares, leaving only lifelessness in place of connection.","opus":"Numbing yourself into a permanent high while shutting out the person who still reaches for warmth. To kill the moth is to snuff the small hope that keeps circling your dying light.","sonnet":"A song about numbing out of a relationship — getting high, shutting off emotionally, telling an ex never to call again, and becoming so depleted of warmth and energy that you actively repel anyone still drawn to you like moths to a dead bulb.","src":"haiku"},
"press-to-meco~easy-life":{"haiku":"Someone spent years trapped by manipulative influence and fear; they reclaim agency and move on, accepting an empty aftermath where nothing remains but survival.","opus":"The British band, known for intricate three-part harmony, voice a slow break from a toxic influence. Worn down to almost nothing, the narrator decides to ease off, cut his losses, and aim for something calmer, praising himself for finally leaving. He sees the contest was never winnable and quits believing the falsehoods he once swallowed.","sonnet":"Two people who have worn each other down to nothing contemplate abandoning the exhausting struggle and simply letting life continue without resistance. The appeal is not happiness but surrender — skin and bone, fear swallowed, a game played against indifference finally forfeited in favour of something easier and emptier.","src":"haiku"},
"press-to-meco~sabotage":{"haiku":"People knowingly destroy what is good and beautiful about life through willful negligence and self-harm, treating destruction as an art form. The song indicts this mentality of deliberate ruin.","opus":"British trio Press to MECO layer intricate harmonies over a portrait of a couple who wreck the good thing they have on purpose, dodging responsibility while everything around them collapses. The grim, gory imagery frames self-destruction not as tragedy but as something they've turned into an almost gleeful shared habit.","sonnet":"Two people caught in a self-destructive dynamic recognize they have perfected the art of ruining exactly what they should protect — circling the same drain by choice, poisoning shared wells, building the conditions for their own collapse. The pattern is acknowledged with a kind of grim mastery, as if destruction has become the only fluency they share.","src":"haiku"},
"pridelands~dark-sources":{"opus":"A raw account of depression and smothering numbness, in which the medicated, self-hating narrator pleads to be released yet strangely hoards his own suffering, unwilling to let anyone take it from him. Beneath it all comes the chilling confession that he once held the weapon himself.","sonnet":"A person consumed by apathy, emotional numbness from medication, and the desire to be cut loose from their own pain — they confess to once holding a gun, to holding their breath for so long that breathing itself makes them sick, and to craving the release of their own hurt.","src":"sonnet"},
"priest~a-signal-in-the-noise":{"haiku":"A listener searches for meaning and connection amid desolation and emotional numbness, finding faint hope in encountering something real through the chaos.","opus":"The Swedish synth-pop trio, founded by former Ghost members, spread their cinematic gloom across a frozen dystopia where a deadened heart gropes toward a faint transmission at the heart of a forbidden zone. Deliverance is pictured as a hallowed sanctuary of realized dreams, glimpsed through wreckage, the search itself becoming the only source of warmth.","sonnet":"Amid frozen cities and apocalyptic skies, a figure searches for hope through the wreckage of the past. Though the heart has gone cold and home feels impossibly distant, something persistent and faint — a signal cutting through chaos — convinces them they are moving toward a place where dreams still hold.","src":"haiku"},
"primer-55~loose":{"haiku":"The speaker provokes disorder with aggressive attitude and style, dismissing critics while celebrating a crew's energy and power to make others react.","opus":"A relic of the early-2000s nu-metal wave, the Memphis band boasts through a rush of manic self-assertion, the narrator daring rivals to watch the room scatter once his fury slips its leash. Jealousy and idle talk only feed his sense of being the loudest, most dangerous presence around, though a fraying strand of loyalty to his crew keeps flickering under the swagger.","sonnet":"A performer staking dominance in a competitive scene, dismissing rivals as jealous posturers who talk loud but lack credibility. Loyalty to a tight crew and raw uncontained energy define the stance — when released, the crowd reacts, and that reaction is the only proof of standing that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~coattails-of-a-dead-man":{"haiku":"A widow profits from her late husband's suffering and suicide, parading her grief for cameras while accumulating wealth from his legacy.","opus":"A bitter fable of a man driven to suicide by fame's glare and a wife who performs grief for the cameras; Les Claypool skewers the vulture who monetizes a dead partner's tragedy, tears sized to the audience watching.","sonnet":"A famous man's wife publicly performs grief after his suicide while quietly capitalising on the fame his death generates, her tears growing in proportion to the size of the camera. A bitter dissection of how celebrity culture turns private tragedy into personal fortune.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~electric-uncle-sam":{"haiku":"Patriotic authority presents itself as omnipresent and all-knowing, threatening punishment for those who resist or disobey.","opus":"A sardonic personification of American state power as an omnipresent, threatening authority, the surveilling voice that dictates what you can do and punishes dissent; menace dressed in patriotic branding.","sonnet":"Uncle Sam speaks in the first person as an omnipresent, authoritarian force that controls what citizens may do, punishes dissent, and positions itself as impossible to escape. Satire of American political power as a smiling, inescapable controller.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~is-it-luck":{"haiku":"Interrogates whether mundane blessings and romantic attraction are fortune or choice.","opus":"Les Claypool needling the whole concept of good fortune by listing things that just are, until the mundane and the fatal sit side by side. The real punchline is a guy talking his way out of getting lucky by showing off his vocabulary.","sonnet":"A deadpan list of mundane coincidences and bodily facts is framed as potentially miraculous before collapsing into absurdist comedy — a woman who wanted his body, not his mind, is unimpressed by his vocabulary — the joke being that luck is just as arbitrary and meaningless as everything else.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~jerry-was-a-race-car-driver":{"haiku":"A reckless young driver lives fast and dies young, wrapped around a telephone pole after one too many beers. The song also honors a strong fireman forced into retirement at sixty-five, both men undone by their own nature or the system's cruelty.","opus":"Primus's funk-metal oddity sketches working-class American figures in Les Claypool's tall-tale drawl, centered on Jerry, a quick but unremarkable driver, and a tough old fireman. The affectionate cartoon turns suddenly grim when the young man, drunk one night, kills himself in a crash, the whole legend ending in wreckage.","sonnet":"Two working-class figures — a reckless amateur driver and a veteran firefighter forced into retirement — are sketched with affectionate precision as men defined by what they do rather than whether they win. Jerry's death wrapping a car around a telephone pole after one too many beers completes the portrait without editorializing.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~john-the-fisherman":{"haiku":"John pursues his passion for fishing despite family pressure, achieving his dream before dying in a shipwreck at sea.","opus":"From Primus's debut, Les Claypool spins a slap-bass folk legend out of a misfit boy who flops at everything but the water and grows into a man who masters the sea. His lifelong wish to spend and end his days fishing is granted with cruel literalness when a storm drowns him, absurdity and real tragedy sharing one jittery groove.","sonnet":"A ballad of a man whose single dream was always the sea — dismissed as a child, finally living it as an adult, and dying in a storm that swallows his mayday. He goes down still holding the same ambition he declared as a boy, and the community buries him under the name that was always his identity.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~lacquer-head":{"haiku":"Chemical addiction and social isolation spiral toward brain death; the song narrates juvenile solvent abuse and fatal intoxication.","opus":"A grimly deadpan catalogue of huffing culture, bored kids getting high on paint and gasoline until their brains give out. Les Claypool plays it as cartoon horror, the jokey bounce making the casual body count land harder.","sonnet":"A sardonic series of vignettes about young people who destroy themselves through solvent huffing — lonely, marginalised, obsessive — told with black comic detachment as each character spirals from boredom into brain damage or death.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~mr-krinkle":{"haiku":"Environmental destruction and nostalgia collide as a speaker addresses a mysterious figure with bewilderment at cultural decline and vanishing tradition.","opus":"A bleery monologue at a surreal figure, sliding from dying oceans strangled by gill-nets to the mush of commodified pop culture, all while pleading for someone to explain why. The refrain's helpless why is aimed at a world of ecological ruin and marketed, low-calorie novelty.","sonnet":"A loose, sardonic address to a figure called Mr. Krinkle — touching on a team moving away, gill-net fishing killing the waters, and the absurdity of a media-defined hybrid genre called Heavy Hometown — with the repeated unanswered question: tell me why.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~my-name-is-mud":{"haiku":"A boring man named Mud kills an aggressive, rude giant who injured him, claiming the act as necessary self-defense against a volatile threat.","opus":"Les Claypool's funk-metal oddity spins a mordant murder-comedy in the voice of a drab, self-loathing nobody who calls himself Mud. Bored, broke, and drinking wine he can't afford, he recounts caving in a friend's skull with a baseball bat over a common spat and now needs the bloating body buried before it smells. Black humor and cartoonish menace fuse into a grotesque folk tale.","sonnet":"A self-described unremarkable man recounts killing an arrogant acquaintance with an aluminium baseball bat after one too many insults and one too many trodden-on shoes. The narration is deadpan — almost bored — as the body goes cold and the killer contemplates how to dispose of it before the smell becomes a problem.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~over-the-electric-grapevine":{"haiku":"Young travelers with peroxide hair barrel through fog toward community gathering, animated by laughter and radio static.","opus":"A druggy road trip through the fog south of San Francisco, hysteria and hallucination narrated with Claypool's absurdist grin. The point is the warped headspace itself, an acid ride where a voice on the radio tips everything into laughter.","sonnet":"A road-trip memory of driving south from San Francisco with friends, high and hyper, radio blaring, ending up parked behind the Roxy — a specific, loose, affectionate account of a night of shared hysteria and youthful chaos on the highway.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~over-the-falls":{"haiku":"Ritual and peer pressure drive a hesitant participant toward death by waterfall; tradition becomes execution.","opus":"A daredevil awaiting his turn to plunge over the falls in a homemade barrel, sweating and dry-mouthed among boasting men. Claypool mines the gap between bravado and terror, the absurd courage of a barely-competent man about to risk everything.","sonnet":"A man — awkward, slightly deluded, driven more by stubbornness than skill — waits his turn in a social setting, keeps trying despite failure, and finally takes his shot at going over the falls like everyone before him, framed with dry amusement at his earnest persistence.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~shake-hands-with-beef":{"haiku":"A woman is raised on meat and embodies abundance; the narrator celebrates youthful transgression and theft with a rebellious swagger.","opus":"Primus unfurl their surreal, bass-driven absurdity in a swaggering nonsense yarn of retaliatory scrounging, pilfered booze, and an artillery piece dragged out for kicks. A girl reared on great cuts of flesh, a nine-year-old with an air gun on race day; the images never resolve into plot, only a leering backwoods tall tale, the title a wink of unrepeatable slang.","sonnet":"A loose, swaggering vignette of small-town boyhood — scavenging, stealing wine, shooting airguns at water-derby — circling around a girl raised on meat who embodies the place itself. The tone is rowdy and nostalgic, more concerned with atmosphere and adolescent bravado than any single coherent event.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~southbound-pachyderm":{"haiku":"Mysterious elephants journey southward in procession; boredom and transience merge into surreal spectacle.","opus":"Mostly a vehicle for Claypool's bass and mood, boredom and idle observation of migrating elephants filling the skies. The imagery stays deliberately oblique, atmosphere and groove carrying more than any literal meaning.","sonnet":"A cryptic observation of something majestic passing — elephants migrating southward, filling the sky — rendered as a meditative moment of watching wonder move through the world while the observer remains still, knowing there is no encore and savouring what little remains.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~the-devil-went-down-to-georgia":{"haiku":"A cunning devil wages a wager against a young fiddle player for his soul, but finds himself outmatched by rural American pride and skill.","opus":"Primus's cover of the tall tale where a cocky fiddler out-plays the Devil for his soul, folk bravado and Southern swagger; the pleasure is in the underdog's mouthy triumph over damnation itself.","sonnet":"The Devil challenges a confident young fiddler named Johnny to a soul-wagering contest, only to lose when Johnny's virtuosity outplays hell itself. A comic folk fable about mortal pride besting supernatural arrogance, narrated with gleeful swagger.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~to-defy-the-laws-of-tradition":{"haiku":"Breaking tradition takes courage; the song questions why brides wear white, how Christmas and taxes function, and who faces real justice for murder.","opus":"A wry send-up of hollow social customs, poking at holidays, wedding conventions, and the arbitrary logic of sin before veering into a parody of civics-class cartoons. Its punchline is that breaking with convention takes real nerve. Early Primus, with Les Claypool mocking convention through jittery funk-metal bass and virtuosic weirdness, satire riding on sheer musical mischief.","sonnet":"A wry inventory of arbitrary conventions — Christmas as consumer ritual, white wedding dresses as moral theater, tax law and murder sitting in strange proximity. The song celebrates anyone brave enough to question these inherited structures, dressed up absurdly as a Capitol Hill bill hoping to become a law someday.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~tommy-the-cat":{"haiku":"A streetwise alley cat named Tommy recounts seducing a mysterious female in his territory with theatrical bravado.","opus":"A tall tale delivered by a strutting alley cat reminiscing about the seductive she-cat who came prowling into his turf hunting for the toughest tomcat around, who is naturally him, before he launches his purring pickup line. Pure cartoon swagger. Primus, Les Claypool's slap-bass oddity, make the beat-poet narration and barked chorus a vehicle for gleeful virtuosic weirdness.","sonnet":"Tommy the Cat narrates his own mythology in O'Malley's Alley — a predatory, strutting urban tom who has seen everything and survived it, recounting the evening a female arrived and cut through the crowd straight to him. The whole song is a seduction wrapped in tall-tale bravado, the cat presenting himself as the inevitable conclusion of her search.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~tragedy-s-a-comin":{"haiku":"Inevitable catastrophe descends: a grim rider and a poisonous prophet both herald doom, and despite plea or prayer, the speaker cannot escape their fate.","opus":"Primus, Les Claypool's surreal funk-metal outfit, personify approaching doom as an unstoppable rider emerging from nowhere to pulverize the living. Ruin can't be dodged, and even courteous pleading earns only contempt and a tutorial in dying. A scripture-clutching woman with a grating voice joins the procession as disease devours the body, leaving a choice between fading quietly or going up in flame.","sonnet":"Catastrophe arrives as an unstoppable rider from nowhere — corrosive, contemptuous, inevitable — and the narrator knows pride will not let him step aside. A woman wielding scripture adds another face to the same encroaching ruin bearing down on those who cannot escape it.","src":"haiku"},
"primus~wynona-s-big-brown-beaver":{"haiku":"A woman cages her pet beaver, which attracts crude male attention and mischief; absurdist humor frames animal instinct and desire.","opus":"A wink-heavy shaggy-dog story about Wynona and her big brown beaver, a caged pet paraded to friends and coveted by a parade of oddballs, every double entendre left cheerfully dangling until it turns out to be a porcupine. The innuendo is the whole game. Primus, led by Les Claypool's rubbery bass and nasal drawl, made this their biggest crossover hit, absurdist funk-metal grinning at its own dirty joke.","sonnet":"A shaggy-dog tall tale about Wynona and her beloved pet beaver who becomes the center of a whole ecosystem of characters — Rex from New Orleans, Lou with the old baboon — each encounter more absurd than the last. The song sustains the double-meaning right to the punchline with complete deadpan commitment, the joke landing through sheer accumulation of detail.","src":"haiku"},
"princessbri~never-be-the-same":{"haiku":"A trusted mentor destroyed the speaker's innocence through betrayal, leaving lasting trauma that permanently altered their ability to trust and be whole.","opus":"A reckoning with a formative, damaging bond with an older mentor who taught the narrator both liberation and deceit, building them up only to tear them down and leave them defenseless. Images of a defiled halo and the recurring question of recovery capture lost innocence and lingering dependence, tempered by the survivor's insistence that they are still standing.","sonnet":"A reckoning with a formative but damaging relationship — someone older who taught and shaped the speaker also broke them, and even after moving on and finding their own path, the speaker is left permanently altered and still asking why.","src":"haiku"},
"prismo~senses-william-black-remix":{"haiku":"Someone retreats into sensory experience to escape mental turbulence they cannot name or resolve. The song explores dissociation and the search for clarity through immersion in feeling.","opus":"Built for the anthemic electronic bloom of the mid-2010s SoundCloud melodic-house scene, this remix rides a slender lyric about fleeing into a private universe of sensation. The escape blurs and overwhelms every faculty, leaving whatever weighs on the mind undefinable, dissolved into pure feeling rather than named. Repetition becomes the point, a mantra pulling the listener deeper into that clouded, senses-drowned retreat.","sonnet":"An overwhelmed mind retreats into a boundless interior world of pure sensation. Something unnameable clouds perception and resists definition, pulling awareness inward. The escape is chosen and recurring — a flight from mental noise into a private universe where feeling eclipses thought.","src":"haiku"},
"prison~outta-my-head":{"haiku":"Obsessive replay of words and perceived slights traps the speaker in rumination.","opus":"Obsessive rumination over someone's words and mockery, unable to stop replaying every mistake while feeling second-best. The turn comes when the anxiety flips outward: whoever's coming for you has someone coming for them too.","sonnet":"Someone who cannot stop obsessing over a critic's or rival's words — the insult has lodged itself immovably, the narrator is gaslighted and second-guessed into paralysis, but eventually takes cold comfort in the knowledge that the same forces will come for the person who attacked them.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m-brodka~ar":{"haiku":"Passion burns fierce as the speaker tastes blood-sweetness of effort and sacrifice, questioning when dreams died and ambition withered. Fire becomes testament to human will burning toward transformation.","opus":"A collaboration between a Polish hip-hop duo and singer Brodka, built as a provocation about daring to truly live. Insistent questions ask when the listener last dreamed, ran naked, felt real hunger or courage, rather than merely counting days. The narrator vows to leap into the abyss himself, refusing to let the word fear appear in his biography.","sonnet":"A Polish provocation series asking when the listener last truly dreamed, ran naked, lay on grass with someone, bit down hard enough to taste blood: the narrator refuses to say \"fear\" in their biography, leaps into the abyss alone, and casts those who offer good advice back \"one-nil,\" insisting that sleeping and dreaming are for those who never found their current.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m-dawid-podsiad-o-duit~freon":{"haiku":"Lovers rediscover careless joy after years of paralysis; they reject judgment and rebuild passion in defiant abandon.","opus":"Two people who can only be fully, wordlessly themselves in secret encounters years apart—a clandestine intimacy of cigarettes, strewn clothes, and neon signs, hidden from a world that would hate them. Love feels so consuming it's better killed, yet they keep returning to steal one honest night from everyday pretense.","sonnet":"A Polish-language song about the particular intimacy of sex with a close partner in a cold city — they lie on the floor afterward amid scattered clothes while a neon sign for a nightclub blinks at them across the street; the air is freezing like freon, and the song captures the precise texture of that physical and emotional closeness.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~112":{"haiku":"A person in crisis fantasizes about self-harm and suicide as relief, cycling through reckless choices and resignation to their own self-destruction.","opus":"Opening on a real crisis-hotline recording, the Warsaw duo build a harrowing suicide pact spoken in first-person plural, cycling through method after grotesque method as if courting death were tenderness shared between two people. A refrain of never again and why me frames a life narrated as sentence and self-hatred, the speaker naming himself his own worst enemy. Pro8l3m turn Polish rap's noir instincts toward something genuinely unbearable.","sonnet":"A Polish track that opens on a mental-health crisis line with all operators busy. The narrator catalogues moments of resignation and the phrase never again repeated most across a lifetime. Dark fantasy sequences of mutual self-destruction with a partner give way to an inverted twin verse where the same scenarios are reframed as desperate proof that life still matters.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~2-17":{"haiku":"Digital exploitation and cybercrime threatening personal security and privacy.","opus":"A menacing warning about digital surveillance and hacking, threatening to seize someone's phone, accounts and money in minutes.","sonnet":"Total digital surveillance and data exploitation — everything you do online is tracked, hacked, and owned by unseen others.","src":"haiku","fable":"A menace-monologue aimed at a subject who surrendered himself to the network without ever registering it as surrender: his phone stops being his the instant anyone else can reach into it, his card belongs to whoever cloned it, and his savings drain away in a five-minute heist. The same wiring that empties his balance is the one he keeps force-feeding — Narcos downloads, brags about a paid escort in Thailand, the grinning iPhone selfie posted season after season. PRO8L3M's nastiest stroke is the hook, which turns the interface into a weapon: you may accept or you may consent, two verbs for one identical act, the sham option of a terms-of-service prompt where you type your password, take it or perish, or rot like garbage. The Rutkowski name-drop, Poland's tabloid private eye, drives it home — no sleuth needs to shadow a man who broadcasts his every move, and the true penalty for stepping out of line isn't a cell but being severed from a grid he has already melted into. Against all this the English shouts about kicking the system's gates in and breaking loose, but it reads less like escape than the last available pose, bravado hollered from deep inside the very apparatus that owns him. The song reads its subject as a man forcing a grimace of impact when told he too is doing someone's soft-shoe routine on command; the terror isn't surveillance but having signed up for it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","money & the street","artifice & performance"],"means":"surveillance reframed as a contract the subject signed himself","built":"accept or consent — two verbs for one identical act, the sham option of a terms-of-service prompt built into the hook"},
"pro8l3m~200-procent-prawdy": {"haiku":"Boastful confessions of hedonistic excess and conquest parade as unvarnished truth; the narrator brags about sexual prowess and wealth while mocking others' mediocre fantasies.","opus":"A satirical Polish rap skit where a braggart spins ever more absurd tales of orgies, drugs, corrupt politicians and luxury, only to be dismantled by a skeptical second voice. The deflating punchlines expose him as a broke fantasist who would not even be let into the venues he brags about. It mocks the whole genre of gangster-rap self-mythology.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper tears apart the braggarts and fantasists in his orbit: one guy claims orgiastic adventures while barely scraping together bus fare, another plans elaborate heists from three years ago that never happen, and the speaker calmly deflates each story — \"200 percent truth\" is ironic, the whole track a demolition of performance masculinity through deadpan witness.","src":"haiku","fable":"A TV crime-show disclaimer opens it — events based on fact, names changed for the broadcast — and the title's 200 percent of truth is already the tell: this is fiction squared, sold as testimony. What follows is a wall of luxury-gangster boasting (sex clubs and six women, a bought PiS politician, cocaine and yachts, the agony of choosing which restaurant, which drug, which girl), delivered as pure appetite. But PRO8L3M builds the track as a dialogue, and a second voice keeps knifing in: where did you ever see those fine restaurants, they wouldn't even let you in; you screwed them — wipe your monitor. The braggart is deflated in real time into a man jerking off in front of the television, and the last fantasist is a wannabe in a clapped-out E36 who can't afford the gas, who broke in — to his own stairwell — a Hans Christian Andersen of the block. The joke lands as its own thesis: the whole gangster-rap dream is a farmazon, a fairy tale muttered by men on permanent, penniless holiday, and the disclaimer was right that only the names got changed — the truth was never in it at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","money & the street","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"the gangster brag deflated in real time into a man jerking off at the television","built":"the TV disclaimer frames the whole boast as fiction squared, sold as testimony"},
"pro8l3m~2040":{"haiku":"A Polish futuristic vision depicts a dystopian 2040 where technology dominates life, nature is destroyed, and society is stratified. Dreams are digital, nature is dead, and humans are enslaved to an artificial existence.","opus":"A dystopian vision of the year 2040 in Warsaw: bionic implants, autopilot flying cars, water rationing, poisoned air, dead jungles, Mars colonies, and online crimes punished by death, seen through a jaded narrator navigating this ruined future.","sonnet":"Set in a speculative near-future Warsaw of 2040, the narrator moves through a city of bionic implants, flying traffic jams, holographic billboards, VR relationships, and in-vitro steaks, reflecting on how technology and geopolitics have reshaped daily life — framed by a phone conversation about a terrorist attack and a comedic subplot about a stash of cocaine forgotten after a bender.","src":"haiku","fable":"A speculative near-future diorama where an ultra-wealthy Warsaw narrator races to catch a Los Angeles flight while a terror attack freezes the city, and every marvel of 2040 arrives already spoiled: flying cars stalled in airborne gridlock, dead equatorial jungle, colonies on a moon of Mars, an insect his daughter will never see alive under a poisoned sky. The refrain is the engine of meaning: each promised gift — immunity, first contact, on-demand implants, lab-grown steak — is delivered in the grammar of a monkey's-paw curse, granted whether or not you wanted it. Underneath the chrome-limb bravado runs a colder read of privilege and surveillance, where crime is punished hardest when it happens online and freedom in the network is just PR, so the transhuman future doesn't liberate anyone, it merely bills them. The final verse collapses the swagger entirely: a man on the hundredth floor waits for the narrator to shout at him to stop, and the narrator, too rushed to care, decides the only way out is down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","nature & the elements"],"means":"every promised future arriving already spoiled","built":"each gift in the refrain is wrapped in a monkey's-paw grammar"},
"pro8l3m~777moneymaker":{"haiku":"Spiral of gambling addiction: money cycles endlessly from wages to bets, chasing illusory wins while consequences mount—friends abandon, possessions sell, and the system feeds on desperation.","opus":"Polish hip-hop duo PRO8L3M render a gambler's spiral in vivid detail, wages broken into coins and chips, creditors circling like vultures, furniture and passport pawned as the drums of the slot machine spin. Faith in the jackpot never rests, poker hands and racetrack tips always just short. Taunting table-talk frames a compulsion that strips everything, the addict certain the next deal redeems him.","sonnet":"A Polish compulsive gambler narrates his own unraveling in real time — pawned furniture, a mother's pension swallowed by bad hands, the all-in bet made from desperation rather than strategy. The table is both his ruin and the only place he feels competent, and the final demand for his car keys makes clear the house has taken everything.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~a2": {"haiku":"Drug trafficking and street crime on Warsaw's A2 highway, depicting the ruthless underground economy of dealers, addicts, and the daily survival of petty criminals.","opus":"A cinematic crime narrative set on Poland's A2 motorway, cataloguing smuggled cigarettes, stolen luxury cars, amphetamine hidden in the LPG tank, brothel vans and dodgy morality where the only rule is the takings must add up. Pro8l3m, the Warsaw duo of rapper Oskar and producer Steez, deliver hard-boiled Polish street storytelling, the trafficking underworld rendered with sardonic detail and a driving, filmic swagger.","sonnet":"A snapshot of the grey economy running along the A2 motorway — smuggled cigarettes, contraband liquor, amphetamines hidden in LPG tanks, women trafficked in minibuses, stolen car parts, bent registration plates. The tone is chronicle not condemnation: this is the road, this is the trade, this is the money moving up and down like a freight elevator. The banter at the opening — a flat battery, a scanner, pressure — roots the whole thing in an ordinary working shift.","src":"haiku","fable":"The A2 — the motorway that runs from Warsaw west to Germany, the road that after 1989 was supposed to carry Poland toward the wealthy West — is repurposed here as a smuggling artery, and the whole song runs its black economy down it: contraband cigarettes and fuel, LSD in the LPG tank, amphetamine, stolen branded clothes, vans of working girls packed to the roof, cars with wound-back odometers driven in from the kebab lands. Oskar and Paluch trade verses at motor-mouthed speed to match the driving, each a customs-dodging inventory delivered in the amoral present tense of men who admit their morality is doubtful and keep counting the take anyway. The crux lives in the refrain irony: money flies like traffic on the A2, but the road of freedom to the West (droga wolności na zachód) actually hauls cheap filth on flatbed trailers, not the Maybach it was supposed to promise. So the freeway of national aspiration is reread as the cheapest possible hustle — the dream of the open road westward reduced to a laweta of junk, the highlife of the outro sitting one word away from awaria, the breakdown.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","politics & society","freedom & escape"],"means":"the road of national aspiration reread as the cheapest hustle","built":"the freedom highway and the flatbed of junk share one word: droga"},
"pro8l3m~adios": {"haiku":"Farewell and departure; a final goodbye to someone or something being left behind.","opus":"A grim warning against falling for the filth of the streets, where trust is treacherous, effort ends in collapse, and life is a cruel specialty you survive without even blinking. The narrator saws his way to throats with a plan, admits he bit off the helping hands long ago, and pictures his own body turned to ash. PRO8L3M, the Warsaw duo, deliver this fatalism in dense, noir-cinematic Polish rap.","sonnet":"A bitter farewell to romantic illusions and the kind of trust that destroys you when it breaks — the speaker has learned that life grinds people down when they go for volume over depth, that effort and collapse are companions, and that the sensible move is to cut the thread cleanly and leave. The Polish lines underneath carry a fatalism about betrayal that reads as hard-won rather than cynical.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survival creed built entirely out of refusals: the refrain warns against falling in love with the filth of this life, then strips away every romantic frame — not love, not De Niro, not some movie streamed on demand — and reclassifies the whole thing as a sentence handed down, faced without pity. The verses escalate from collective slaughter into a second half of point-of-view swaps, a man cut and a woman harmed, each capped by the flat you-won't-settle-this: it's just business, and it always has been. The real turn is the internal brake — once a monster — jumping off a bridge, poisoning itself first for safety, so conscience is executed even as it's grieved. The closing oxazepam is the mask coming off: the ground-down survivors medicate exactly the numbness the code demanded of them, the price of never blinking.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","dissociation & numbness","money & the street"],"means":"conscience executed in advance so the code of numbness can hold","built":"the closing oxazepam drops the mask to show the cost of never blinking"},
"pro8l3m~akomstwo":{"haiku":"Greed and hunger corrupt the senses as tourists and locals blur in a night of excess and abandon. The speaker moves through the chaos unmoved, observing the chaos with cold detachment.","opus":"A cinematic hip-hop vignette that drops the listener into the cool routine of a criminal. A man greets a club bouncer, slips backstage as always, and moves with the unshakeable confidence of someone who never checks the merchandise because it is always exactly as promised. Terse and filmic, it sketches professionalism in the underworld through pure poise.","sonnet":"A VIP club fixer glides past the queue into the back entrance, sizing up everything with a predator's gaze — checking not what's inside people but what's useful; the title word 'łakomstwo' (gluttony/greed) frames the scene as an anatomy of appetite for access and status.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~ar":{"haiku":"A person rejects conformity and chooses radical freedom, embracing devil-like chaos over passive existence and empty hope.","opus":"A relentless self-mythologizing challenge, the rapper daring the listener to remember when they last dreamed, ran naked, felt hunger or had the courage to stay silent, while casting himself as a devil among gods, a stone loosed from a sling that will strike your temple. He blinds fate and leaps into the abyss alone rather than let fear stain his biography. Pro8l3m are a Polish hip-hop duo, and this is pure adrenal bravado over cinematic, cold production.","sonnet":"A series of questions about when you last felt truly alive — running naked, lying on grass with someone, tasting blood from clenched teeth, having real courage — followed by a declaration that the speaker will leap before being pushed. To be a devil among gods, a scream among truths, a nightmare in someone else's sleep: that's the aspiration.","src":"haiku","fable":"A refusal-of-mediocrity manifesto disguised as a challenge, its verses built on a relentless interrogation of the listener — when did you last dream, last starve for something, last risk everything on a chance rather than counting off dead days? The dare turns inward and defiant: the speaker won't be pushed toward the edge because he'll leap on his own, refusing to let the word fear appear anywhere in the story of his life. Underneath the swagger of blinding fate and leading the dance sits the real confession of the chorus — that to live this way is to become the devil among the worshipped, the nightmare, the lie among comforted truths, an isolation the speaker chooses over sleepwalking. Brodka's counter-voice makes it a duet of appetites rather than a solo boast, so the ferocity reads less as ego than as two people goading each other to stay awake while everyone else drifts into nothingness.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"choosing isolation as the price of staying awake while everyone drifts","built":"Brodka's counter-voice makes the dare a duet of appetites rather than a solo boast"},
"pro8l3m~art-258":{"haiku":"Street-level criminality and debt create inescapable systems of control; the narrator exposes how silence, not boasting, marks actual power in underworld hierarchies.","opus":"Polish rap noir that opens on a drug debt and then anatomizes street life as an addiction economy where value has rotted out. Named for the penal-code article on drug dealing, it's a cold portrait of chasing numbers until nothing real is left.","sonnet":"A Polish rap about the street economy's real cost — hustling, debts, disappearing people, legal risk — told from the inside with the clear-eyed knowledge that the glamour is borrowed, the danger is real, and the exit is harder than the entrance.","src":"haiku","fable":"The recruitment pitch runs first, then the invoice: the opening skit sizes up a missing courier holding heavy money, and the verses walk the same seduction the criminal-code article names — pick up the phone, and you are in a group that flatters you at the top and abandons you privately on the way down. What the mask sells as street charisma the song reads as livestock being driven, all of it built on a debt underneath: the addiction that drinks like a leech, the sleep that won't come, the girlfriend he wakes beside because her breathing tells him he's still alive. The refrain is the ledger's bottom line — whatever once had worth died long ago, and the success he's chasing is a joke propped up on a lie. There is no exit clause; you enter and the zone has no reverse, so the only honesty left is admitting the mistakes and making another one tonight.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"The title cites Article 258 of the Polish Penal Code — participation in an organized criminal group; the song is built on the statute it names.","themes":["money & the street","addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control"],"means":"the recruitment pitch runs first, then the invoice for entering a zone with no reverse","built":"the article number of the title is the criminal-code clause for being recruited into an organised criminal group"},
"pro8l3m~baga-e":{"haiku":"Mourns intimacy lost to adult obligations and separation, recalling shared poverty and closeness that could not survive into adulthood. Baggage accumulates as lives diverge.","opus":"An aching remembrance of young love and poverty, two people who had nothing but shared dreams, riding trains, bathing in a cramped tub, loving each other on stairwells and in cheap ecstasy, wanting to grow old in each other's arms. Now they wake separately, exhausted and drifting, with nowhere to return. Pro8l3m, the Polish duo, render it as bittersweet Warsaw realism, tears soaking makeup like blood soaks bandages, tenderness curdling into loss over years.","sonnet":"A before-and-after elegy for a relationship that started with nothing and ended with everything except love — crowded train compartments and MDMA on a staircase replaced by separate cars, separate support groups, separate loneliness in a shared bed. The city groans outside and the speaker circles the streets unable to return to a place that still smells like her.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~bmk":{"haiku":"Describes entanglement with organized crime while watching family remain innocent, profiting from illegal enterprise. Father and sibling stay ignorant while narrator builds wealth through vice.","opus":"A drug-dealer's coming-of-age monologue, the young narrator changed by money, dressing better than his peers, moving speed and product while his hardworking father toils under a ticking clock and never asks questions. Guilt flickers as he shares with his soldier brother yet keeps chasing more. Pro8l3m, the Polish duo, thread an astrology reading and the refrain that he was a good boy who barely drank through it, an ironic elegy for a life sliding into crime.","sonnet":"A young man's confession of sliding from a working-class family into the drug trade — the father under the clock, the brother in the army, the younger sister finishing school, while the speaker counts files and cuts deals, insisting he just wanted a little more than grinding under the same ceiling. The refrain that he was a good kid who barely drank is delivered with knowing irony.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~by-em-cz-owiekiem":{"haiku":"Melancholic reflection on mortality and forgotten love suffuses the speaker's resignation to separation and the inevitability of becoming memory rather than presence.","opus":"A man certain he is bound for hell reflects on his dark, sin-sticky deeds and insists that even in a ghoulish world he too was human. A lake carrying a lost love's scent and taste becomes the drowning place where memory and mortality meet.","sonnet":"A farewell addressed to someone the speaker is leaving behind: the lake holds their scent and taste, the speaker submerges their face and could almost touch the bottom, and amid dark deeds and guilt they insist that even in this grim world they were once human — the final act of remembrance before going under.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~by-em-tam":{"fable":"The verb is past and unstable: this is a survivor cataloguing the wreckage of a life he was inside, piling image on image: broken glass and mildewed stairwells, needles and money in the mattress, cheap grief and cheaper highs. The litany stops being scenery and turns on him. The final movement reveals the structure was a confession all along: he was there among the love he wants to kill, the air he wants to stop breathing, the people he wounds so he can stop needing them, each thing named only so it can be renounced. The mask is a tough survey of a hard world; underneath is an addict's logic where even breathing is a habit to quit, and the closing line — that he knows the ones behind the curtain and will know the next — turns personal ruin into an endless, inherited cycle.","fableDeep":"The form is a single verb — I was among — feeding an enormous catalogue, squalor compressed into rhyming plurals: shattered glass and musty stairwells, unhinged kids and their hungry mothers, redemptive hair-of-the-dog, overdue debts, sleeping pills, dives. The inventory keeps changing register without changing grammar, sliding from scenery to physiology to jurisprudence — long deliriums, dens full of chemistry, court motions, fear, the slang birds who bill by the hour — a whole ecosystem delivered as one breathless sentence. The volta is the fourth block, when among suddenly takes purpose clauses: among his love for her so as to stop loving her, among the oxygen he breathes so as to stop pulling it up his nose, among the people he hurts so as to stop demanding things from them. Presence stands revealed as strategy — exposure therapy, living inside the disease to burn out the craving — and the closing couplet concedes the failure: he knows the ones behind the curtain and will know the next ones too. Thesis: a witness who stays in the inventory because leaving was never really the plan.","haiku":"A Polish speaker remembers being surrounded by broken glass, poverty, drugs, dysfunction, crime, and struggle throughout their life.","opus":"A rapid-fire inventory of a life lived amid squalor, addiction and decay (broken glass, sleeping pills, steroids in needles, corpses in coffins, cash in the mattress); the devastating turn is that the narrator is among all of it deliberately, to stop loving a woman, to quit the drug he inhales, to stop needing and hurting the people around him, a witness to a world he can't escape.","sonnet":"A dense Polish lyrical inventory of everything the narrator has lived among — broken glass, cheap hotels, steroids, sleepless nights, bad women, court cases, old cigarettes — a catalogue of a hard life witnessed and absorbed, ending with the claim that he knows the people behind the curtain and always will.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","money & the street"],"means":"a tough survey of a hard world that turns out to be a confession","built":"the past tense verb is unstable: the survey structure reveals itself as renunciation all along"},
"pro8l3m~crash-test":{"haiku":"An adult reflects on childhood promises and betrayals, selling youth for money and time while knowing life's transactions have left them hollow and watching themselves self-destruct.","opus":"A harrowing self-portrait of addiction, self-destruction and lost love, the narrator writing on his wall that he's doomed and smashing the crystal ball of a nightmarish future; the devastating core is the split between the wounded adult who lies, drinks and sells out and the small child inside who runs crying to his mother, the man having traded his youth and time for meaningless money, feeling himself dying (turning blue on morphine) beside a woman he won't tell he loves.","sonnet":"A self-lacerating confession about destroying every intimate relationship through lies, emotional unavailability, and selfishness — the narrator sees the child in himself still wanting his mother while the adult systematically betrays the people closest to him, running the whole thing like a crash test to see what breaks.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verse voice writes a warning to itself on the wall and shatters the fortune-teller's glass because the future it shows is unbearable, then splits cleanly in two: an adult who drinks silently in a doorway and lies for gain, and a child who runs everything to his mother, the two ledgers of a single self weighed against each other. Its cruelest arithmetic is temporal — he bought age and sold youth, kept the money that means nothing and spent the time that counts, watching life run out like sand he can't hold while the child inside cries and the grown man keeps quiet. The sampled English refrain of loneliness since someone left underscores that all this bravado is grief in armor. The title lands as the mask's logic made literal: intimacy is a controlled demolition he steers straight into the wall, destroying the thing he wants because keeping it would mean feeling what the child feels. Underneath the swagger about faking love and burning bridges, the closing turns genuinely frightening. He numbs himself with one substance while speeding on another until he goes blue and feels himself dying, guilt answering guilt, the crash no longer a metaphor.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss","shame & repression","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"intimacy steered deliberately into the wall because feeling it is unbearable","built":"the adult-child split: two ledgers of the same self weighed against each other, the grown man keeping quiet while the child cries"},
"pro8l3m~dr-melfi":{"haiku":"Possessing material wealth yet feeling spiritually empty; luxury and abundance fail to satisfy a deep inner emptiness the speaker cannot articulate.","opus":"Framed by Tony Soprano's therapy sessions, the Polish duo voice a man who has every luxury, wife, mistress, mansions, and still feels the same hollow, always one more purchase from a happiness that never arrives. A cold satire of wealth as bottomless appetite.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop track referencing The Sopranos — the narrator has everything he wanted (the Bentley, Versace, a much younger woman, a resentful wife) yet feels hollow and dissatisfied, cataloguing luxury as a list of substitutes for actual happiness, with one more purchase always needed.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hedonic treadmill monologue: the narrator inventories a life where every desire has already been met — the third wife, the children handed millions, the Michelin dinners, a teenage companion the age of his own daughter — and the accumulation keeps compounding without ever arriving anywhere. The song's engine is the gap that opens each verse the same way, that he has everything he once wanted, only to close on a lack he cannot name, so that acquisition becomes a compulsion aimed at a satisfaction it structurally cannot reach. Underneath the boasting the confession leaks through: possessing life's sweetness is what seduced him, but up close it tastes less sweet, and the sunlit cloudless reality still isn't the thing he was chasing. Framing the whole verse-monologue as one session on Melfi's couch turns the flex into a symptom — the man talking to fill the Tuesday hour, mistaking more for meaning and hearing, faintly, that the wheel keeps spinning without ever landing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["alienation & emptiness","money & the street"],"means":"desire already met, yet the same lack keeps opening each verse","built":"framing the flex as a therapy session turns every acquisition into a symptom"},
"pro8l3m~dwa-trzyna-cie":{"haiku":"Pressure and counting down; the weight of numbers and obligations bearing down on the speaker.","opus":"A grimy estate saga narrated from inside addiction and low-level crime, a man digging his own grave day by day, drowning ambition in stomach vodka and hash while betrayal scars his forearm like a tattoo. Loyalty, surveillance and revenge churn through the block where one wrong move undoes everything. PRO8L3M, the Polish duo of rapper Oskar and producer Steez83, render Warsaw street life in dense, unglamorous detail, the same weary cityscape he admits he's seen more than enough of.","sonnet":"Two-thirteen in the morning on a Polish housing estate — drinking, smoking, burning through money, watching friends steal and betray, all narrated with clear-eyed contempt for the daily grind that amounts to digging one's own grave. Beneath the bravado is a precise accounting of what estate life actually costs.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~dwa-trzynascie":{"haiku":"Street wisdom dressed in self-destruction: the speaker owns their damage and refuses to apologize for surviving through means society won't validate.","opus":"A grim self-portrait of estate life where the only careers are rapping or dealing, drink and drugs numbing a slow self-dug grave. No glamour, just decline traded for a hometown that swallows people whole.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop song about a chaotic night of drinking, smoking hash, and navigating the social ecosystem of a housing estate — the speaker drinks vodka, walks the blocks, deals with betrayal, money problems, and the grinding rhythm of neighborhood life with bitter clarity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drunk-clock title fixes the read: it is a small-hours dispatch from the estate, and the intro of unanswered calls sets the whole thing at the dead end where nobody picks up. The speaker narrates his own dissolution with defiant clarity, insisting his footing is solid even as the money thins and every day amounts to digging his own grave one bottle deeper. What the bravado is really guarding is betrayal: the one thing that scars permanently is a friend's treachery, imagined as a mark tattooed into the skin, while informants, wiretaps, and a partner who sold him out turn the neighborhood into a place watched as plainly as an open palm. Underneath the swagger the movement bends downward into weariness, closing not on a boast but on the flat admission that he has simply looked at the same grim view too long.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["friendship & loyalty","addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control"],"means":"betrayal imagined as a mark tattooed permanently into the skin","built":"closes not on a boast but on the flat admission of having stared at the same grim view too long"},
"pro8l3m~dym":{"haiku":"Smoke and obscured vision; haziness of reality or the mind clouded by substance or trauma.","opus":"A hazy, dissociative meditation drawn through cigarette or joint smoke, where drifting clouds form long-unseen faces that threaten to knife the narrator, loneliness sits heaviest in silence, and every body slowly reduces to bone. Blood tastes umami, hope has husky-blue eyes. PRO8L3M turn a smoke-filled room into a hall of accusing ghosts, Polish rap at its most inward and image-drunk.","sonnet":"A late-night smoke session that becomes a meditation on memory, loneliness, and things slipping away — smoke traces take shape into faces of people not seen in a long time, some of them threatening, and the speaker drifts between the feeling of strength and sudden collapse while silence answers every attempt at connection. Blood tastes like umami and ghosts measure him with hollow eyes.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~explosion":{"haiku":"Urban chaos and romantic entanglement interweave as the speaker navigates Kraków's underworld while wrestling with love's unpredictability and memory's erosion.","opus":"Skits of nagging phone calls frame a swaggering night out, the narrator obsessing over his sharp outfit and polarized shades before hitting the party. Braggadocio spliced with a dreamy, borrowed refrain about strange quiet summer afternoons and delirious nights.","sonnet":"A breezy, self-assured slice of a summer afternoon: picking up a suit from the laundry, stepping out in hieroglyph tees, arriving at a party like a business lunch — the lyric's strange, quiet summer afternoons and intoxicating nights form a chorus that frames the whole swagger in something more dreamlike and still.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~fair-play":{"fable":"Beneath the hedonism the song runs entirely on a grammar of disavowal: each craving is immediately followed by an aside insisting it's a separate matter, an other story, so that pleasure and denial are welded together at the level of syntax. The narrator wants everything at once — a woman, the drug, the car, the brand-name comfort — while confessing he holds nothing sacred and betrays whatever he touches, then waving it off as beside the point. The hook lets the mask slip: talking to God about hell, keeping up appearances while feeling like a lost kid in fog, driving too hard because he never learned how to brake. The refrain's flat verdict lands as the one honest line, that this is no triumphant stadium and life owes no one a fair game.","fableDeep":"The form is anaphora as self-portrait: nearly every line opens with 'lubię' (I like), building an identity purely out of consumption — engines, diamonds, tequila, women in every configuration — and the confession slips into the list at full speed: 'jestem chuj, zdradzam' (I'm a prick, I cheat), self-knowledge granted exactly one line's weight before being buried under more likes, which is the psychology in miniature: honesty as another item, never a turning point. The counter-melody lives in the 'inna sprawa' (another matter) hedges — he'd rather be toasting a million, he recently blew his money, the garage under the villa is postponed — each aside puncturing the inventory with the admission that much of it is aspiration, not possession. The refrain owns the vertigo underneath: 'gadam z bogiem o piekle' (I talk with God about hell), driving hard because he cannot find the brakes, feeling like 'małolat we mgle' — a kid in the fog — while keeping the pose intact. It is about hedonism as a bluff: a man listing pleasures loudly enough to drown out the knowledge that he is lost, broke, and already negotiating his afterlife.","haiku":"A cynical list of desires and excuses—luxury, women, hedonism, casual betrayal—framed as life philosophy, with the refrain that life is unfair and not following the rules.","opus":"In Polish, a hedonistic catalogue of the things the narrator likes, luxury goods, drugs, cheap thrills, and women he treats casually and betrays, undercut by the refrain that life is no Wembley and no fair play.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper delivers a stream-of-consciousness list of things he likes — luxury cars, alcohol, sex, food, gambling — punctuated by a refrain that life is not fair play, while admitting he is reckless, broke, and living beyond his means.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","shame & repression"],"means":"craving and disavowal welded together at the level of syntax","built":"each desire is immediately followed by an aside insisting it is a separate matter"},
"pro8l3m~fight-club": {"haiku":"A critique of consumer culture and societal conformity that strips individual identity, inspired by Fight Club's anti-establishment philosophy of breaking free from manufactured desire.","opus":"A Polish rap built on Fight Club's anti-consumerist creed, the narrator confessing he wrongs himself most, a slave in the fog chasing junk he doesn't want, reduced to his account, his shirt, his logo, his car. It rages at watching the world burn while urging you to be yourself, which is nobody, quoting Wu-Tang's cash rules refrain amid a currency roll-call. PRO8L3M, the Polish duo Oskar and Steez83, weld Tyler Durden's nihilism to sharp critique of status-chasing emptiness.","sonnet":"A Polish riff on consumer-society critique — the speaker recognizes himself as a product, a logo, a car identical to the one next door, and the only honest response is to blow it all up. The Wu-Tang refrain lands as both diagnosis and dark joke: money runs the world and nobody escapes it.","src":"haiku","fable":"PRO8L3M lifts Fincher's opening whole — Tyler's napalm recipe, the Narrator's cue to introduce him — and hands the film's anti-consumerist rage to a Warsaw first-person who no longer stands outside the sickness he names. Oskar's opening self-portrait is the trap in miniature: nothing here is indifferent to him, yet he is the most important thing to himself, seated front-row to watch the world burn — Tyler's revolt already curdled into spectacle and self-interest. From there it becomes a downward argument that the fight-club fantasy of smashing furniture and blowing up money can't survive: he chases the shit he's part of as society's element, consumes what he doesn't want, and lands on the verse's real thesis — you are your account, your T-shirt, your logo, your car like the one beside you, a repetition of everyone else. The recurring stammer on the closing currencies — euro, bitcoin, dollar, złoty, pound, franc, franc, franc — enacts the point the words make: identity ground down to a looping unit of exchange, the mask of rebellion and the commodified self underneath it collapsed into one. The nod to Wu-Tang's C.R.E.A.M. seals it, translating be yourself into cash rules everything, so that even the imperative to authenticity is quoted from the market.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","politics & society","identity & becoming","artifice & performance"],"means":"the revolt against consumerism already curdled into self-interest","built":"the stammer on currencies enacts identity ground to a looping unit of exchange"},
"pro8l3m~flary":{"fable":"A relationship rendered as ongoing warfare, where tenderness and violence share the same air and the two lovers bleed out their shame until nothing is left to protect. The chorus inverts the usual love-song inventory: instead of cherishing every detail of her body and presence, the narrator insists he can no longer remember any of it, memory itself torn in half along with the thing they had. Across three escalating nights of blackout drinking, the fight spills from the bedroom into a club into the gutter, until he smashes a bottle against the curb and refuses the last offer she makes to take him home. The forgetting is the mask; underneath it is a man erasing what he cannot bear to have felt, choosing the sealed-off numbness over the wound.","fableDeep":"The verses stage domestic cold war in weather systems: she is furious as a scream, capable of tornadoes, the clouds hanging 'jak szubienice' (like gallows), and between them the shame 'dawno się wykrwawił' — bled out long ago — nakedness with nothing left to protect. Against this, the chorus performs its central lie: 'nie pamiętam' (I don't remember) repeated over a devoted inventory — how her sweat smells, how her footsteps whisper, curls 'czarne jak ból' (black as pain), nakedness white as salt — a list so precise it refutes its own frame, since you cannot itemize what you have forgotten; the denial becomes the most detailed love poem in the song. The sampled English refrain about air and breathing runs underneath as the truth he will not say in Polish. The club verse converts cold war to hot: shoved away, a glass thrown, a bottle smashed on the bar, blood through the fingers, a police patrol dodged mid-vomit. It is about claiming amnesia as anesthesia — reciting everything you cannot forget about a person you can no longer survive.","haiku":"Portrays a volatile, passionate relationship oscillating between tenderness and violence, with the narrator unable to recall or separate the intimate moments from the chaos. Polish lyrics depict intoxicated passion and emotional turbulence.","opus":"A volatile, toxic relationship told through a drunken, drug-blurred night of fighting, where a woman's fury feels like tornadoes and love bleeds into violence. The narrator can no longer remember her tenderness, only the wreckage, as the night dissolves into blood, vomit, and blackout before she reappears to pull him back.","sonnet":"A Polish rap about the deterioration of a relationship — the speaker describes explosive fights, mutual cruelty, and a drunken night out that ends in violence and collapse, all while the chorus mourns the inability to remember the tender details of the woman he has lost.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["heartbreak & loss","addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"erasing memory rather than bearing what it holds","built":"the refrain inverts the love-song inventory: he can no longer remember any of her"},
"pro8l3m~freon":{"haiku":"A nocturnal love affair unfolds in fleeting moments away from conventional morality, with two people seeking escape from the mundane through passionate physical connection that feels both transcendent and ultimately doomed. The song romanticizes illicit transgression.","opus":"A pair of lovers meeting in a cold, neon-lit night and again years later, sharing wordless, intense intimacy and cigarettes while the world seems to hate them; a fleeting, defiant escape from everyday life haunted by longing and impending separation.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop song about two people who meet for a purely physical encounter in a cold room lit by a neon \"Night Club\" sign, say nothing meaningful, and part — then years later find each other again and repeat the same wordless ritual, both aware that naming the feeling would destroy it, and choosing to live it again anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"The same lovers, the same heap of clothes — underwear, jacket, Nikes, lycra — filmed twice: once an affair, years later a reunion. PRO8L3M changes almost nothing but the neon across the street, which reads Night Club the first time and Fight Club the second. The title tells it: powietrze mroźne jak freon, air cold as refrigerant — a feeling preserved by being killed, easier dead than tended. Outside, the crowd swelling at the club rises on the same gauge as a couple's hatred building toward a breakup. Podsiadło's hook is the emotional index the verses withhold — a salt bite, a flutter of lashes, dying in a coat made of your photographs. Verse two strips them of names, rules, stress, until togetherness is only a rhythm swum against the everyday's glitter. Enfant terrible reads not as rebellion but as the last pose left to people who chose the cold over the risk of being known.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"a feeling preserved by being killed, easier to keep dead than to tend it alive","built":"the neon across the street reads Night Club then Fight Club — the one word swap is the whole history of the relationship"},
"pro8l3m~garma-eria":{"fable":"A deli-counter as the whole underworld economy: everyone is a regular, and the door is a revolving membrane that lets in boys and ladies but disgorges whores and boors, so that respectability and ruin turn out to be the same people at different moments of the same transaction. The song is built on a hustle's arithmetic — money, women, violence, and the promise of cheese every Friday against a hangover every Saturday — until the register collapses into a closing chiasmus where cigarettes and money run out but debt and days never do, and prayer and liquor become mirror-image ways of buying back the time each one wastes. Underneath the tough deadpan is a trapped man's ledger, someone counting what he can't stop losing precisely because it never began.","fableDeep":"The title casts the venue as a deli counter of pre-cooked roles, and the entrance/exit couplet does brutal social bookkeeping: 'wchodzą chłopcy, z nimi damy / wychodzą dziwki i chamy' (boys and ladies walk in; whores and boors walk out) — the place doesn't change people so much as relabel them by morning. Surveillance sets the etiquette: 'ściany mają uszy, więc nie jest nikt pijany' (the walls have ears, so nobody is drunk) — even intoxication is performed soberly, candor priced out of the room. The middle tightens into aphoristic pairs — fist and hammer, powder on Friday, hangover on Saturday, 'gra o flotę, walka o fotel' (a game for cash, a fight for the chair) — power politics scaled down to a bar table, plus the personal aside of standing handcuffed and silent one January. Then the ending's perfect chiasmus: 'modlitwy, by przestać tracić czas na litry / litry, by przestać tracić czas na modlitwy' — prayers to quit wasting time on liters, liters to quit wasting time on prayers — addiction and faith exposed as each other's alibi, a sealed circuit. It is about a social scene as a processing plant, where identity, money, and salvation all circulate and no one leaves richer, holier, or sober.","haiku":"Polish slang description of underground criminal/street culture—a place where deals, betrayals, and rough transactions define community, with cynicism about friendship and loyalty.","opus":"A Polish rap noir of a shady nightspot where the walls have ears and everyone's playing for money and status. Amid booze, prayers and debts the narrator circles a weary truth that neither the drink nor the wasted time ever really run out.","sonnet":"A snapshot of a social scene where everyone knows each other and performs accordingly — men arrive with women who leave as something cheaper, walls have ears so no one drinks openly, the whole place a theater of small power and petty debt; the narrator ends by cycling between prayer and alcohol to kill time.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["money & the street","addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder"],"means":"a trapped man counting losses because the cycle never had a start","built":"the closing chiasmus: cigarettes and money run out but debt and days never do"},
"pro8l3m~geforce": {"haiku":"A nostalgic journey through gaming culture and childhood arcade memories, celebrating the thrill of competition and immersion in digital worlds.","opus":"A nostalgia trip through gamer childhood, Pokemon Yellow under the desk, five-zloty internet cafe hours, Diablo, Doom cheat codes and a worst-in-the-world PC, spun forward into an adult flex loaded with Counter-Strike, RTX cards and MMO slang. The lean years of childhood are crossed by boasts of expertise and swagger. PRO8L3M, the Polish duo Oskar and Steez83, pack the verses with dense gaming references, the wonder of seeing the world through a child's eyes carried into grown-up bravado.","sonnet":"A love letter to growing up with video games in nineties Poland — Game Boy under the desk, fifty-zloty internet cafes, Diablo and Tony Hawk. The adult speaker layers gaming vocabulary over rap bravado until the two become the same language of survival and play.","src":"haiku","fable":"Built as a video-game bracket, the track lets an esports announcer count off the fights and hand the mic to a different guest rapper each time. That framing turns the whole apparatus of gaming into a language for talking about class, age, and where you started. Kaz Bałagane's opening verse is the sharpest: a kid cutting school under the desk with a handheld given to him by a flight attendant, the worst PC anyone owned, an hourly internet café, cracked shooter codes passed down through the husband of a friend's mother from an embassy on Piękna Street. That whole 1990s Warsaw childhood gets inventoried through pirated games and dial-up nostalgia, closing on a foreshadow of the lean years to come, the world understood through a child's eye. The later verses from Oskar and Szpaku shift out of memory into pure present-tense flex, the references piling up faster and shallower — a new graphics card here, a League or Counter-Strike callout there, an endless run of this-or-that binaries — so the arc across the fights is really a drift away from that first verse's tenderness toward adult competition and boast. The recurring bridge, with its promise of starting fresh under a harder setting, reads as both a reset screen and a life motto, the constant re-leveling that connects the café kid to the man who now calls himself the in-game leader. What holds it together is that the games are never just games: they are the shared dialect of a generation that grew up broke and online, and the song trusts that dialect to carry everything it means to say.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nostalgia & memory","money & the street","identity & becoming"],"means":"broke childhood encoded entirely in pirated games and dial-up nostalgia","built":"the arc drifts from tender first-verse memory toward adult boast with each new fighter"},
"pro8l3m~gniew":{"haiku":"Anger and fury, rage as driving force or response to injustice.","opus":"Titled anger, this delivers a cold, filmic account of a debt-collection killing. The assassin parks far off like no amateur, knocks, looks his host in the eye, and drives the blade home over money owed. A dreamy, dissociated refrain floats above the violence, widening the gap between the brutal act and the numb detachment surrounding it.","sonnet":"A cold, efficient act of violence: a man parks far away like a professional, knocks, looks the debtor in the eye, and drives a blade upward — because the man owed him money. The English refrain \"wake me if it's just a dream / touch me, touch me in your fantasy\" floats detached above the Polish brutality, a dissociative counterpoint.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~golden":{"fable":"A drug-room panic dressed up in the language of luxury: the title's gold is the gilding a high lays over a squalid room the speaker can't actually leave. Each verse dissolves the walls into hallucinated grandeur — the floor warping into a blond Mona Lisa, the ceiling passing for sky, the mirror pulling his hands in like tar — before the comedown snaps him back to the same filth, the frost on the window, the yellow graffiti on mould. The real corrosion is in the hook, where the refusal to trust curdles verse by verse into not loving, then not respecting even himself, while he keeps a second light ready the moment the first burns out. By the last verse the mirrored faces are people he's wronged, and the euphoria has narrowed to a rooftop edge and a loop of time he only pretends to escape by running in place.","fableDeep":"The architecture is a fixed chorus with one moving part. The scene stays constant — filth, ice on the pane, something laid out on a mirror, no lamps so nobody can be seen going pale — but the key verb degrades on every pass: does not trust us, then does not love us, then does not respect us, the pronoun always plural, dragging some us down with him. Between refrains, two trip sequences run their arc: ascent first, ceiling as sky, diamonds as snow, a halo grazing his head, then persecution, the parquet composing itself into a blonde Gioconda who bends like blood-alcohol numbers and draws a stiletto, the mirror pulling his hands in like tar. Both verses close on the same comedown beat — suddenly, this room — squalor snapping back like a set change reversed, mould and yellow graffiti where the sea had been. The thesis: the high renovates everything except the self, and the refrain measures exactly what each renovation costs.","haiku":"A person in a filthy, dark room with mirrors loses grip on reality and identity, haunted by a figure reflected back at them.","opus":"A vivid, disorienting drug (likely cocaine) session in a filthy, freezing room where the walls warp, the floor becomes the Mona Lisa, the ceiling becomes sky and diamonds become snow; the deeper read is self-disgust and hollow addiction—'I don't trust us, I don't even love or respect myself'—chasing another hit that leaves only stains shaped like the faces of people he's deceived.","sonnet":"A Polish song set in a squalid room during a drug or alcohol binge — the narrator loads up and smokes, watches the walls dissolve and the floor turn into a blonde Mona Lisa, fights with his reflection, and cycles through self-loathing and chemical euphoria.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"hallucinated grandeur snapping back to the same frost and filth","built":"trust curdles verse by verse into not loving, then not respecting himself, while a second light waits for the first to burn out"},
"pro8l3m~ground-zero":{"haiku":"The speaker describes a drug-fueled nightlife of clubs, euphoria, and excess leading to overdose or collapse. They catalog the rituals and substances that escalate through the night until everything spirals.","opus":"A nightclub bender told from inside the blur: strangers, powder, throbbing bass mistaken for ecstasy, the narrator watching his own hands and knowing better than to mix what he is mixing. The English refrain promises a lover carrying him higher while a slurred exchange reveals someone who has overdone it, the room spinning. Pro8l3m, the Warsaw duo of Oskar and Steez, work this cinematic, noir electronica-rap where dance-floor euphoria and dread are the same drug.","sonnet":"A night that keeps pulling you under — club lights, powder, someone whose mouth tastes wrong, a bass that hits like surgery. The speaker knows exactly what he's doing and does it anyway, cataloguing the ritual with deadpan expertise until the music swells and he's spinning, confessing he's gone too far, the city cycling back to the same dance floor at dawn, Bonnie and Clyde in the wreckage of another night.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~gry-losowe":{"fable":"A day in the life narrated with deadpan indifference: a minor fender-bender into a dumpster, a walk through the neighborhood weighing who deserves a promise, a run of petty transactions and a cash machine that keeps paying out. Beneath the flat catalogue of errands and small hustles runs the mixtape's real logic — chance decides everything, so the speaker refuses to grieve the dented metal or moralize the day. The spliced hook cuts against that shrug: he admits he stumbles in the dark, yet insists he was born to walk in the sun, the swagger of a money machine sitting right next to the confession of being lost.","fableDeep":"The title — games of chance — frames one aimless day as gambling, and the refrain is the wager: 'błądzę w ciemności, ale koniec końców / urodziłem się po to, żeby chodzić w słońcu' (I wander in darkness, but in the end I was born to walk in the sun) — destiny asserted with zero supporting evidence from the day it brackets. That day is anti-epic on purpose: an egg cutlet at a milk bar, reversing into a dumpster, dismissed as 'błąd niebolesny' (a painless mistake) since the paint job was needed anyway — failure spun into fortune, the gambler's accounting where every loss was secretly a win. A rare reflective beat — walking blocks, weighing who is and is not 'wart obietnic' (worth promises) — gives the flâneur a moral ledger. The finale turns opportunist: two wrecked drunks by the liquor store, one asleep, robbed with the wry absolution that neither had exceeded his daily norm — a mugging narrated as public-health observation. The refrain's second pass swaps sunlight for cash: a machine for money. It is about street fatalism as a faith — treating a scrounging, semi-criminal present as random noise before a destined win.","haiku":"The speaker wanders in darkness but was born to walk in sun; struggling with poverty and random chance, he seeks light amid daily struggle and decay.","opus":"A vivid slice of gritty Polish street life, a slacker's day of milk-bar cutlets, minor car scrapes, weed and a robbery of a liquor store where the drunk clerks never stood a chance. The recurring line frames it all with fatalistic swagger: he wanders in darkness but was born to walk in the sun, and when it comes to money he's a machine.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper wanders through a mundane day — bumping into a trash can, passing drunk strangers at a liquor store, eating bad food — and frames it as stumbling through darkness but knowing he was born for the light, confident in his ability to generate money.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["money & the street","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"refusing to grieve anything because chance decides everything","built":"the spliced hook cuts deadpan shrug against a confession of being lost"},
"pro8l3m~hack3d-by-gh05t-2-0":{"haiku":"A Polish rap narrative celebrating hedonism, wealth, crime, and conquest—material excess and transgressive pleasure drive the story.","opus":"A Polish rap portrait of a gangster who loved money, women, drugs and crime with equal appetite, sampling Heat's assume-they-got-everything speech. The relentless catalogue of vices builds a life of excess always watched, always about to fall.","sonnet":"A Polish track built around a Heat-film sample, cataloguing the decadent lifestyle of a criminal who loves money, women, gambling, smuggling, pornography, and violence — the chorus of champagne, absinthe, Bugattis, and yachts is observed by someone watching from the outside.","src":"haiku","fable":"Beneath the hacker-EP mask, the track runs a crime-film frame over a relentless anaphora of loving, cataloguing one man's appetites until the list itself becomes the portrait. The genius is grammar: verse after verse stays in the past tense of a man who did love money, women, smuggling and violence, then slips into the present for the new woman tonight, so the litany reads like a rap sheet being recited about someone already caught. What accumulates as glittering excess — champagne, a Bugatti, yachts — curdles into evidence, because the chorus keeps reminding us that all of it exists while someone is watching. Underneath the swagger it is a surveillance ballad: the boast and the wiretap are the same document, and the pleasures are only ever inventoried because the case against him is already closed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["surveillance & control","money & the street","violence & murder"],"means":"the boast and the wiretap turning out to be the same document","built":"past tense lists a rap sheet of appetites, then slips into present for the new woman tonight"},
"pro8l3m~hazard":{"haiku":"Urban alienation, drug use, paranoia, and self-destruction.","opus":"A bleak, drug-fueled gambling scene of betting, cocaine, women and paranoia, chasing luck amid grim addiction and risk.","sonnet":"A night of gambling, drinking, and drugs in a seedy atmosphere, drifting on money, vice, and street-level paranoia.","src":"haiku","fable":"Hazard is a monologue from inside a losing streak, and it fuses the gambling ledger, the drug ledger, and the woman into one line of stakes — the horse a six, the coupon needing five numbers, hotel and vodka and poker in the same breath as the wraps. The retro-noir cool is symptom, not swagger: in his right hand he holds what everyone supposedly needs, in his left the crossbar of a marionette whose strings he claims to work. That is the confession — a man boasting he pulls the strings while itemizing his own compulsions is the puppet, and the paranoia he calls ever-sweeter, the day shrinking after each all-nighter, tells you which way the tension runs. The menace stays abstract on purpose — cops in the stairwell, sticky hands, air tight and damp as a small plane. Then the Steez hook snaps it into surveillance grammar, the black uniforms and raised binoculars confirming the drum reads crooked: the paranoia was never delusion, only early, the whole cocky ledger a man betting long odds while the raid assembled around him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control","money & the street"],"means":"the man who claims to pull the strings itemising his own compulsions to their exact denominations","built":"the Steez hook snaps the paranoia into surveillance grammar — black uniforms, raised binoculars — confirming the boast was always already a confession"},
"pro8l3m~heat":{"haiku":"A professional criminal narrates his cold methodical existence—bank robberies, murder, and betrayal shape him. He faces himself and accepts a tragic ending as inevitable.","opus":"The Polish duo turns Michael Mann's cinema into cold verse, inhabiting a career criminal for whom bank scores, gunfire and stacked corpses have long since stopped registering as horror. Adrenaline replaces revulsion; the blood runs Spetsnaz-cold. A woman flickers at the margins, briefly wanted as escape, but the target and the getaway win. He faces himself in the mirror as both enemy and only friend.","sonnet":"A Polish-language portrait of a career criminal — cool-blooded, precise, desensitised to violence — who stages an armoured-car robbery while a fleeting glimpse of a woman reminds him of the ordinary life he has long surrendered. Self-knowledge and nihilism coexist without redemption.","src":"haiku","fable":"PRO8L3M build the whole track as a second-person character study of a professional armed robber, and the title borrows Michael Mann's Heat as a lens: the discipline to drop everything and walk in thirty seconds, the woman who can never be more than baggage, the cold that makes him good at the work. What makes it more than a heist reel is the arc across the verses. Early on the read is external and admiring, cataloguing the craft and the frozen calm that offsets the heat of the scene; by the middle the woman becomes a wish he cannot afford (he would like to take her along, would like to stop lying), and the chorus reduces the whole life to a litany of its residue, drugs and sobbing and corpses. The turn is verse three, where he stands face to face with himself as in a mirror and names the truth the professionalism was hiding: that he is fed by disaster, that this is the only thing he can do, so at best he bleeds out somewhere alone. The last verses make that self-knowledge operational rather than redemptive: when certain victory tips into defeat he liquidates the partner and the plan and finishes the job by his own hand, and the closing line, that there may always be a hotter figure out there, refuses him even the comfort of being the most dangerous man in the room.","fv":2,"themes":["money & the street","violence & murder","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"professionalism as the mask over a self fed only by disaster","built":"the arc shifts from external admiration to mirror-confrontation to operational self-knowledge"},
"pro8l3m~hiena":{"haiku":"Human nature embodies predatory survival instinct and insatiable hunger, where compassion and meaning collapse under animal appetite.","opus":"A cold meditation on what a human being actually is, water and meat and maybe a soul, capable of anything for money, both actor and audience. Nature is indifferent; the verdict lands somewhere between disgust and awe.","sonnet":"A Polish philosophical rap that uses the hyena — an animal that communicates through laughter and can crush bone — as a launching point for a long, dense meditation on what a human being actually is: flesh and soul, money and sex and cars but also hope and shame and hunger; the song cycles through contradictions (man is dust, man is God's parody, man is the champion who won the race before he was born) to land on a bruised but vital view of human potential.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hyena hunts the truth of what a person is: not money or bodies or cars, but water and meat and maybe a soul, a stadial animal and God's gadget at the bottom of its own cravings. The song builds by refrain — each line reopens the definition and cancels it, so the human keeps sliding between golden dream and self-loathing, actor and audience, a blind fifth-sense creature with its third eye clouded, stumbling through space. Underneath the swagger of the final boast — the winning sprint of millions to a single egg, the master who outran everyone before he even existed — sits the counter-image the mask hides: a beggar's open palm held out toward the world for love, biomass reduced to an ID number and a checkbox in an urn. The read of humanity here is that it is a process of decay under the mask, and simultaneously alchemy — lead into gold, water into wine — the same creature capable of both, refusing to resolve which.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["identity & becoming","transcendence & consciousness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"humanity as decay and alchemy at once, refusing to resolve which","built":"the winning-sprint boast inverts immediately to a beggar's palm held out for love"},
"pro8l3m~interpol":{"haiku":"A Polish rapper flees urban entrapment, materialsim and false ideals, seeking wildness and spiritual experience with a lover on the run from law.","opus":"A fantasy of two lovers cashing out and vanishing, fleeing the grind of expectation, hustle and social-media performance for coral atolls on a fake passport. The romance is inseparable from self-destruction, escape as a kind of shared, sweet doom with the law always somewhere behind.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop fantasy of disappearing with someone — dropping everything (the stash, the Instagram, the one-way ticket), running to coral atolls with a fake passport and a stolen diamond, dancing flamenco while Interpol searches for them; self-poisoned by life but protected by the memory of her.","src":"haiku","fable":"A druggy escape fantasy sung by someone fleeing every prescribed way to live: cheap comforts, the job that poisons like a swallowed mixture, the posters and DMs telling you who to be. The chorus proposes a shared runaway leap without a ticket, wanting life and death in the same breath, welcoming the rain as cover while the searchlights of pursuit close in. But the bridge turns the getaway inward and exposes its lie: the speaker is alone, poisoning himself with his own life, playing with a noose, and the beloved is really a preserved fragment of memory that guards him against death. The dancing-while-hunted image lands the whole thing as a defiant, doomed high, sweetness borrowed from what everyone else avoids.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["freedom & escape","addiction & self-destruction","suicide"],"means":"the getaway fantasy exposed by the bridge as solitary self-poisoning","built":"the beloved of the chorus turns in the bridge into a preserved memory fragment guarding the speaker against death"},
"pro8l3m~intro":{"haiku":"A Polish-language monologue framed by a philosophical quote about dreams and control, describing a legacy of poverty, dysfunction, and bitter resignation left to children.","opus":"Opening with an Alan Watts parable about dreaming ever-riskier dreams until you dream your present life, it becomes a bitter deathbed testament from a hiding man leaving only debts, venereal diseases and spite to his estranged family. The throughline, echoing Watts, is 'be careful what you wish for, you may not have the strength left,' a life of consequences narrated with cold self-loathing.","sonnet":"Framed by a Alan Watts monologue about adventurous dreaming, the Polish text is a sardonic mock-testament: the narrator, sensing death near, catalogs what he leaves his children — debts, legal problems, venereal diseases — and leaves his politician brother nothing at all, the whole thing a black comedy of a life gone wrong.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dying man dictates a will that leaves nothing but wreckage, and the read has to move with him from the sampled dream to the poison he hands down. The frame is Alan Watts describing a dreamer who, having exhausted every pleasure, chooses to gamble into the nightmare he is living now — then the song answers that setup with a voice smelling its own death and writing in silence. What he bequeaths is deliberately venomous: a son gets a name and its debts, daughters get only custody of the younger children, and the whole family is willed sickness rather than inheritance. The recurring turn is that legacy here is a curse disguised as a gift, capped by the warning to watch what you wish for because the strength to enjoy it may not last. The mask of the solemn testament keeps slipping to show a man with nothing left, bitterly offering to sell even that if the price is right.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"The opening samples Alan Watts' Dream of Life lecture — the dreamer who, having exhausted every pleasant dream, gambles into ever-riskier ones until he is dreaming the ordinary life we live now.","themes":["death & grief","family & blood","money & the street"],"means":"legacy handed down as a curse disguised as a gift","built":"the Alan Watts dream setup is answered by a dying man's will that bequeaths sickness rather than inheritance"},
"pro8l3m~iskry":{"fable":"A rapper who fights with words the way others fight with fists turns the weapon inward, prodding an open wound and drowning his midnight self-loathing in drink while the tears run the wrong way. The hook reframes the whole thing: the cruelest words aren't the shouted ones but the small sparks of unnecessary speech, and what follows them — the silence, the failure to say sorry — is the real accelerant, so that a plain sentence ends up doing the killing.","fableDeep":"The governing conceit is loaded speech: words as rounds in a gun, maxims sitting in the barrel, talking as a substitute for firing — and each finished quarrel ends not in resolution but in wound-picking, killing again something he calls almost a second self. The middle eight does the arithmetic of the title: unnecessary words are sparks, and the silence that follows them is canisters — fuel drums waiting. That is the sharpest move here: the quiet after a fight is not peace but stored accelerant, meaning the couple's calm spells are the most dangerous thing in the house. Around it, self-diagnosis in quick strokes — never now, always later; drowning in counterfeit pride; midnight striking as the tears head south. The final quatrain strips everything back: he stands naked and exposed, and the apology will not come because the word itself gags him. Thesis: an anatomy of how the smallest units of language — one word withheld, one plain sentence spoken — do the actual killing.","haiku":"The speaker destroys their own words and relationships through anger and self-destruction. They drown in shame while tearing apart what matters to them.","opus":"In Polish, a portrait of a self-destructive man for whom words are bullets he fires in fights, killing part of himself each time and picking at open wounds. Drowning in vodka and fake pride, he stands naked before a partner unable to say sorry, realizing plain words and simple sentences can wound and kill.","sonnet":"About a fight that reopens old wounds instead of resolving anything — words fired like bullets, shame drowned in alcohol, pride that's fake and hollow, tears at midnight. The narrator stands naked before the other person unable to say sorry because the word chokes him, aware that even simple sentences can kill.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance"],"means":"small unnecessary words doing more damage than the shouted ones","built":"the hook reframes the whole song: what follows the sparks — the silence — is the real accelerant"},
"pro8l3m~jak-disney":{"haiku":"A Polish hip-hop track describing urban chaos at a party—mixing observations of casual sex, drug use, excess behavior, and social dysfunction. It portrays hedonism and disorder as the current reality.","opus":"Polish hip-hop duo Pro8l3m narrate a spiraling house party in cinematic detail, the narrator drifting from his kitchen through an Uber ride into a flat crawling with tattooed strangers, jointed smoke and frozen vodka. Bathroom scenes turn sordid, girls shriek, a naked idiot overheats, and cocaine-hunting vampires trash the place. Chatting calmly with Siri amid the chaos, he grows bored of the whole abordage while a Keep Smiling sticker mocks the wreckage.","sonnet":"A detached observer drifts through a chaotic house party in Warsaw — clocking absurd scenes, provincial ambitions, drunk strangers collapsing — with the cool anthropological distance of someone who finds human spectacle amusing but refuses to be consumed by it.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~jakby-ko-czy-si":{"haiku":"A man grieves the cruelty of existence and indifference of the world, finding solace only in reuniting with a woman he loves, who becomes his redemption amid the pain and chaos of life.","opus":"A grim morning-after tableau of paralysis and disgust, a man frozen staring at a fly's corpse on the ceiling, numbed by coffee and drugs, sketching his own crimes in chalk while the world rushes past. Redemption arrives through a woman whose scent and touch heal his wounds and make him want to truly live, as the world that had long been ending pulls back. PRO8L3M, the Polish duo Oskar and Steez83, thread despair into fragile love, the best days always slipping into today.","sonnet":"Stasis broken by love — the speaker lies motionless in a grey flat, coffee useless, watching a dead fly on the ceiling, time stopped, until the thought of one woman pulls him back to his feet. The final verses are tender: her breath is the most valuable thing he owns, and the best days are always the ones happening right now.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~k-pax":{"haiku":"The narrator chronicles the hollow emptiness of consumer obsession and status-chasing despite worldly success; they recognize life's wasted days and choose sudden redemption.","opus":"A Polish confessional about disillusionment with consumerist success and self-destruction: the narrator, feeling as alienated as Kevin Spacey's character in K-PAX, mocks the scripted life of house/car/kids-then-die, admits he drowns his shame in vodka and chases luxury goods and women, and resolves that it's now or never to stop the pills and the poisoning before he loses his nerve.","sonnet":"A Polish rap about recognizing how much time has been wasted chasing material status and surface pleasures — women, cars, logos, pills — and deciding, finally, to stop before cowardice wins out and the moment passes.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man tallies his life as pure arithmetic — status symbols, women, the car he can't even say why he wanted — and finds himself a stranger inside it all, like a patient insisting he doesn't belong on this planet. The self-loathing runs through drink and pills he keeps swearing off mid-sentence, each vow of quitting collapsing back into the next binge, so the promises read as the addiction itself talking. What holds is small: a few people he keeps in his heart, a grandmother whose imagined shame stings sharper than any bill. The song's real movement is the swerve at the end from confession to a knife's-edge now-or-never, because he knows that if he thinks about it any longer he'll lose his nerve — the alien's talk of leaving is really the addict's terror of staying and changing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"each vow to quit collapsing back into the next binge mid-sentence","built":"the alien-patient metaphor: a man who can't place himself on this planet frames the terror of staying and changing"},
"pro8l3m~kickdown":{"haiku":"High-speed car driving and drag racing culture: the speaker uses acceleration and danger to feel alive, weaving through traffic and evading police with reckless intensity.","opus":"A hedonistic joyride monologue: the narrator fires up his powerful car, lights a joint, blasts old rap and races through the city pushing 200, feeding on speed, premium fuel and reckless overtaking. The night bleeds into showbiz decadence, picking up a woman he wants to sleep with, cruising past clubs full of girls, food and friends, the car and the chase standing in for an appetite that never quits.","sonnet":"A late-night drive narrated with precise automotive detail — the speaker pushes a powerful car to its limits through the city, stops for fuel, and the night ends circling a club watching women, with the whole thing conveying restless male energy and the pleasure of speed as its own purpose.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person drive through Warsaw that keeps its hands on the machine and its meaning just under the dashboard: the narrator narrates every gear and gauge with a tuner's tenderness, so the car becomes the one relationship he fully controls. The speed is real but also a stand-in for appetite — the same throttle logic carries him from the highway into a night of women, weed and hangers-on he sizes up as coolly as passing traffic. Underneath the swagger runs a quieter countercurrent: at the moment he could floor it, a smaller voice declines, and the reflex to want a manual just so he could slam it himself admits how much of this is a need to feel the force by hand. What reads as pure hedonism is really a man measuring everything by acceleration and finding the braking harder than the going.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["freedom & escape","party & hedonism","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the car as the one relationship fully under his own control","built":"at the moment he could floor it, a smaller voice declines — wanting the manual just to slam the gear himself"},
"pro8l3m~kluby-i-restauracje":{"fable":"A night out narrated from inside the blur: the speaker moves through clubs and restaurants where money and substances slip away, toasts multiply, and every raised glass loses him a little more of himself. Beneath the swagger of the crowd and the fanbase runs a quiet admission that his anger has drained off with the vodka, leaving him hollow under the sickly LED light while an old chart hit from the nineties plays on. The read of the mask is the party's bravado; underneath is dissolution, a man watching his rage and his cash pour down the same drain.","fableDeep":"The opening simile sets the mechanics: the head spins 'jak w wanklu' — like a Wankel engine — rotary, smooth, famously prone to burning out, which is the night in miniature: motion without progress arriving at frictions and brawls. The economics are counted in shortfalls: 'miał być hajs, jest pięć stów' (there was supposed to be money; there's five hundred), and the toast arithmetic — five shots against five heads, and what is that per head — turns celebration into rationing. 'Każdy ma fanklub' (everyone has a fanclub) but nobody has one solid person: entourage inflation, intimacy deficit, the substances supplying the narration he no longer writes himself. The quietly devastating image is disposal: 'brak tu mojego gniewu / z wódką wylałem go do tamtego zlewu' — my anger's missing; I poured it down that sink with the vodka — even rage, the fuel of every earlier night, dumped, leaving not peace but flatness under nauseous LED light while a hit from the nineties supplies the borrowed feeling. It is about the stage of the scene where even anger has run out, and the night keeps happening only because nobody can think of anything else to do.","haiku":"Money is scarce but substances flow abundantly in clubs and restaurants. The speaker navigates bankruptcy, rivalry, and intoxication in scenes of excess.","opus":"A Polish rap vignette of nightlife burnout: the narrator's head spins like a Wankel engine as drinking leads to fights and arguments, he's broke despite everyone claiming a fanclub, and the promised money never materializes. Clubs, restaurants and assorted substances blur together while he notes his old anger has been poured down the drain with the vodka, leaving him drifting under sickly LED light to a nineties throwback tune.","sonnet":"A night out going sideways — the narrator is broke despite the promise of money, the group is fracturing over petty arguments, and the only comfort is cheap vodka and the distant thump of a 90s hit playing in some dim-lit club.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["alienation & emptiness","party & hedonism","night & the city"],"means":"anger and money draining down the same vodka-lit hole","built":"the party's swagger is the mask; underneath is a man dissolving under sickly LED light"},
"pro8l3m~ko-o-fortuny":{"haiku":"Collective fate and cyclical struggle; the wheel of fortune trapping people in poverty and despair.","opus":"A dense nostalgia trip through nineties post-communist Poland, opening with an argument about freedom being worthless without money, then piling up brand names, imported sneakers, cheap champagne and pop-culture flotsam of the era. The narrator loses a girl amid the consumerist rush. PRO8L3M reconstruct a whole vanished decade in Polish rap, the wheel-of-fortune title nodding to a country suddenly gambling on capitalism.","sonnet":"A nostalgic portrait of Poland in the late 1990s, seen through a wheel of fortune's spin — Nokia phones, concrete murek drinking spots, hotel Sheraton currency exchange, Polish TV stars, and a girlfriend who left all blur together into a snapshot of an era defined by transition from communism to market capitalism. The title track of a moment when freedom arrived but without the money to enjoy it.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~krajowa-2":{"fable":"Not a lyric at all but a recorded phone-call skit: a man works himself into a menacing lather over a woman who tried to hide her call behind her sister's number, taunting that he had it all along before curtly hanging up. The fragment is pure controlling swagger, the interlude staging the small cruelty of a man who needs to be seen catching someone out.","fableDeep":"The power mechanics are all in the staging. Before he even answers, he narrates to a third person present — look who is calling, from her sister's number, thinking he would not know — so the woman is already a spectacle for an audience she cannot see. The call itself is one-sided by construction: we hear only his half, and his half is entirely procedural — I told you not to call, you tried to be clever — capped by a contemptuous verdict that trails off mid-insult, the unfinished word doing more damage than a finished one would. The comic engine is his triumph at recognizing a phone number, treated like a chess victory; the ugly engine is that knowing the sister's number means he has mapped her whole escape network. The demand not to be interrupted lands hardest — he sets the terms, she supplies the violations. The double sign-off, announcing the hang-up before performing it, is control theatre to the last syllable. Thesis: a thirty-second portrait of coercion, where surveillance is passed off as wit.","haiku":"Polish: Confrontational phone call fragment. Accusation of deception and betrayal through a sister's number.","opus":"A short spoken-word skit of a controlling, contemptuous man berating a woman over the phone; he mocks her for calling from her sister's number thinking he wouldn't know, sneers that she tried to be clever and lost, and coldly hangs up, sketching a domineering, dismissive relationship dynamic.","sonnet":"A brief spoken-word interlude capturing one side of a tense phone argument — someone is being called from a sister's number by a person who was told not to call, and the speaker shuts it down with irritated authority.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["surveillance & control","anger & defiance"],"means":"staging the small cruelty of needing to be seen catching someone out"},
"pro8l3m~kursor":{"haiku":"Numbness and resignation frame existence where the world treats the speaker as cursor and pawn. Advice spirals into contradiction, leaving only the option to breathe.","opus":"A panic-attack monologue for the moment life caves in at once, romantic wreckage, betrayal, workplace humiliation, the whole world seeming to conspire against you. The only instruction is to breathe, ride out the rage, and not do anything irreversible even as the urge to blow it all up keeps surfacing.","sonnet":"An instruction manual for surviving a catastrophically bad day — when you feel like a cursor someone else is moving, when everything collapses, when a Beretta in your hand starts to seem like an option — the advice is precise: take a breath, talk to the Georgian Uber driver, put on Ennio Morricone, log off social media, don't pack a bag; the repeated 'weź wdech' (take a breath) is the whole thesis.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~letnie-przesilenie":{"haiku":"Polish: Summer day of casual socializing—drinking, smoking, hanging with friends. Mundane leisure punctuated by interpersonal friction and weather shifts.","opus":"A hyper-detailed slice-of-life of a lazy Polish summer day of getting high and drinking with friends by the water; through phone calls, joints, hash and petty bickering it captures aimless youthful hedonism, ending on the narrator's contentment that wherever he goes, he feels best at home.","sonnet":"A languid, granular account of a summer day with friends — picking up people in cars, smoking joints, eating grilled food, getting rained out, heading back to the city — the whole thing rendered in intimate Polish slang with no larger stakes, just the texture of an ordinary afternoon.","src":"haiku","fable":"A druggy day-in-the-life that hides its real subject under a haze of leisure: the narrator drifts through a summer of joints, beers, bickering friends, and a lakeside cookout, but the second verse quietly reveals he is working — collecting money, meeting his supplier, moving product between the errands. The solstice frame turns this endless loop of the longest days into something both idyllic and airless, the chill-out he never wants to end shadowed by a gathering storm and, at the close, a passing patrol that forces him to ditch the stash in an instant. Underneath the stoned contentment of feeling best right where he lives is the flat admission that nothing here changes, and in a year you would be sick of it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["money & the street","party & hedonism","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"idyllic summer stasis shadowed by the admission that nothing here changes","built":"the solstice frame makes the longest days into an airless loop, the chill-out ending in a patrol and a ditched stash"},
"pro8l3m~lody-w-oskie":{"fable":"Over a hungover, broke morning, a man answers a lover who has caught him out, and the whole song is his defense: a breathless torrent of alibis for every day and errand, each one insisting on innocence while the sheer accumulation and the frantic pet names give the lie away. The refrain fixes the aftermath in a single leftover object, her coat still in his place after she stormed out, standing in for the romance that used to be there. His bridge admits the real grievance underneath the excuses, that it was never her leaving that undoes him but the fact of being caught, and even his one grudging concession that this slip wasn't everything only confirms how routine the cheating is.","fableDeep":"The comic engine is interrogative self-defense: he repeats her accusations back as incredulous questions — 'co ja zrobiłem? Jaką dziewczynę?' (what did I do? what girl?) — then buries them under alibis so over-specified they indict him: only drank a cola, only helped his father, only happened to meet Patrycja while heading to the lingerie stand for her Valentine's gift. The pet names escalate as the lies thin — rybko, kotku, żabko, myszko: little fish, kitten, frog, mouse — tenderness deployed as anesthetic before each fresh implausibility. The calendar sharpens the farce: the door slams the day after her name-day, the slap lands on Valentine's Day, his sins perfectly synchronized with the occasions that demand devotion. The refrain does the cynical bookkeeping — 'dziś nienawidzi mnie, jutro jest miła' (hates me today, sweet tomorrow) — a cycle he openly counts on, until the naked thesis: the problem is not 'że się zmyła' (that she bolted) but 'że mnie nakryła' (that she caught me). It is about a liar so fluent that remorse never gets a word in — guilt experienced purely as a logistics problem.","haiku":"A man is falsely accused of infidelity by his girlfriend during what he claims were innocent activities, exploring how quickly trust breaks and relationships dissolve over misunderstandings.","opus":"Sung in Polish, a man caught cheating spins an increasingly absurd string of alibis to a furious partner who slapped him and walked out, admitting the real problem isn't that she left but that she caught him.","sonnet":"A Polish rap narrator constructs increasingly elaborate and absurd excuses for why he came home late or was caught in suspicious situations, while his partner remains furious then forgiving in turns — a comic portrait of infidelity and denial.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["shame & repression","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"a cheater undone less by guilt than by being caught","built":"her coat left in his place stands in for the whole romance that no longer is"},
"pro8l3m~macki-meduzy":{"fable":"A drug-and-desire spiral where a woman and a narcotic high become indistinguishable, and the narrator surrenders to both. She whispers in his ear while the devil whispers in hers, so intoxication, violence, and lust arrive as one descending force he stops resisting. The movement runs from a shared drink and a promise of safety toward total loss of control, until even blood on snow reads as ordinary and a whole apartment block softens into the drifting tentacles of jellyfish. Underneath the swagger and the devil-on-each-shoulder posturing is dependency dressed as appetite: he wants her naked or not at all, and by the end he has stopped caring why he no longer decides anything.","fableDeep":"The hook establishes a relay of corruption: 'ona szepcze mi do ucha, a jej szepcze diabeł' — she whispers in my ear, and the devil whispers in hers — a chain of possession that leaves him two links from hell and enjoying the proximity, confirmed by the escalation logic of 'byłem zły, z nią jestem jeszcze bardziej' (I was bad; with her I'm worse). She is introduced through a child-safety proverb turned pyromania — with her and matches it's like with children — so the invitation 'spalmy miasto' (let's burn the city) follows as naturally as play. The title image arrives late and lands hard: the housing block as 'macki meduz', jellyfish tentacles — the estate itself venomous, stinging and holding whoever brushes against it, freezing you mid-run. His ultimatum, her naked or no one, is devotion phrased as scorched earth, while conscience is reported hanged and illusions long since burst. It is about a love that works like possession, each partner making the other more dangerous and both calling that intimacy.","haiku":"Reckless escapism and seduction by a dangerous woman who embodies chaos and disregard for consequences. The narrator is drawn to her cruelty despite the destructive spiral.","opus":"A dark, intoxicated night of destruction shared with a dangerous woman, mixing drugs, alcohol, violence and menace as the pair terrorize a crowd. She whispers in the narrator's ear while a devil whispers in hers, and his craving for her deepens his own evil.","sonnet":"A Polish rap about a toxic, demonic relationship with a woman who brings out the darkest impulses in the speaker — recklessness, violence, and moral dissolution. She is portrayed as a force whispering the devil's words, a nightmare over the city that he can neither resist nor escape.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"surrender to desire dressed as appetite rather than need","built":"intoxication and lust fuse into a single descending force he stops resisting"},
"pro8l3m~magnolie":{"fable":"A litany of dispossession: every line begins with having once held something and no longer holding it, so that life, conscience, desire, dreams, plans, even humanity itself get inventoried only as losses. Underneath the wreckage sits an addiction that lets him see better only after the sun has gone down and drives him to do what disgusts him, until the one steady presence left is a woman whose patience he is burning through alongside everything else. The movement lands at the mirror, where the reflected face registers as not his own and whatever remained of him is left frying in flames — the soiled magnolias of the title marking a tenderness that survives only as something already fouled.","fableDeep":"The verses are bookkeeping: an anaphora of I-had followed by cancellation, each possession — desires, plans, secrets, a scrap of conscience — logged and struck out within the same line. One simile does the moral accounting: values get swapped like oil in a car, routine maintenance rather than crisis. The chorus then shifts from ledger to scene, present tense and nocturnal: he sees better once the sun is down, does things that disgust him, will demolish whatever amount he is handed, and holds exactly one thing — her, or more precisely her patience, patience itself named as the possession. Dirty blood, dirty magnolias fuses the title's flower with contamination, beauty pre-stained, while thickened oxygen suffocates the two of them — even the life-giving element arrives poisoned. The second verse ends at a mirror whose reflection he disowns as everything he owned burns. The thesis: addiction told as an inventory audit, where the final line item is a woman's patience, and even that is being spent.","haiku":"Decay and loss of self: the speaker has squandered youth, passion, and morality, now watching their life burn while clinging to one person as a final anchor.","opus":"A bleak inventory of everything the narrator once had and has lost, life, conscience, dreams, ambition, humanity, now hollowed out and no longer recognizing himself or the man he could rely on. He sees better once the sun has set, doing things that disgust him and squandering whatever he's given; all that keeps him standing is one woman and her patience amid the dirty blood and dirty magnolias, thick air suffocating them both.","sonnet":"A Polish rap confession of total interior collapse — the speaker catalogs everything he once had (dreams, passion, humanity, faith in people) and no longer does, with a relationship serving as the one remaining thread, while a surreal image of dirty blood and magnolias captures the suffocating weight of it all.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"every remaining thing inventoried only as something already lost","built":"the soiled magnolias of the title name a tenderness that survives only as something already fouled"},
"pro8l3m~makija":{"haiku":"A male narrator manipulates multiple partners online through fake profiles and exploits their devotion; he discusses deception, coercion, and cruelty without remorse. The song portrays predatory behavior in digital dating.","opus":"Polish hip-hop dissecting the dating-app hunter, a predatory serial manipulator collecting conquests and blackmail material, treating people like a discount catalogue. A cold portrait of internet-age disposability and the hollow compulsion driving it.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop song about a serial manipulator and predator who runs multiple fake online personas to exploit women — collecting footage for blackmail, stringing along an ex who may be pregnant, all narrated with cheerful sociopathic detachment.","src":"haiku","fable":"A predator's self-portrait delivered without a flicker of shame: the narrator runs the dating apps as a con, working a rotation of fake names and stockpiling images and chats as leverage for later. The song's genius is that the makeup it keeps demanding of the women is really his own — every profile is a doctored face, and the swagger of luxury tags and confessed incorrigibility is the paint over a mechanism that runs on manipulation, blackmail, and blank cruelty. The skit that opens it lets the real cost bleed through the bravado: the pregnancy-test photo he can't laugh off, the trail of women he treats as disposable. Underneath the cool there is no depth to find, and that vacancy is the point — the mask goes all the way down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance","violence & murder"],"means":"the makeup he demands of women is really his own digital costume","built":"the mask goes all the way down: the swagger of the predator has no depth beneath it"},
"pro8l3m~makija-instrumental":{"haiku":"Exploitative online predation reveals user commodification without moral restraint or connection.","opus":"A predator's confession from inside dating apps, using fake names, hoarding photos to blackmail and manipulate women he treats as interchangeable inventory. The swagger about not getting attached is the whole rot: intimacy reduced to a discount catalogue.","sonnet":"A predatory narrator boasts about running multiple fake identities on Tinder and Chatroulette to manipulate, exploit, and blackmail women, cataloguing his tactics with cynical bravado — 'collect photos, videos, chats online for later in case of a slip' — while reducing every interaction to a cheap transaction.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~makijaz":{"haiku":"Predatory manipulation and casual cruelty toward women rationalize themselves through performance; the speaker conflates seduction with domination.","opus":"A predatory braggart cataloguing manipulation, blackmail and disposable conquests in the app-dating age, addiction to the hunt he can't quit. The bravado is the point and the indictment, ugliness worn as identity.","sonnet":"A Polish-language confessional from a predatory catfisher who uses Tinder, Chatroulette, and fake personas to lure women, collect compromising photos, and then blackmail them. The narrator describes the whole enterprise with cheerful contempt — cataloguing his targets by type, keeping a gas canister in his car in case any send muscle after him, and treating the whole operation as an addiction he cannot give up despite knowing it is monstrous.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~matchboxy":{"haiku":"Nostalgic recollection of childhood and adolescent moments in a small Polish town—first loves, cigarettes, music, and simpler times that defined belonging.","opus":"A Polish rap flood of 90s childhood detritus, school-trip sandwiches and bootleg tapes and first crushes, that under the nostalgia is really about how irretrievable and quietly aching that vanished small-town world has become.","sonnet":"A nostalgia trip through working-class Polish childhood — school trips, junk food, first crushes, petty thefts, church-going, and the small rituals of growing up in a small town, held together by the warmth of the love that still persists underneath.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man spreads cheese on bread and is thrown back into a Polish childhood, and the song becomes a rushing inventory of it — the candy bars and cola, the hand-me-down bike, cartridge games and pool trips, the schoolyard fights and first crushes and first bravado — each fragment named too fast to linger on, the way memory floods rather than narrates. The refrain reframes all that small-town clutter as the real headline: little town, big people, time made irrelevant, one ache pressed into the ribs. What surfaces underneath the affectionate cataloguing is that this is a love song to a vanished self and place — the kind of love the chorus calls non-returnable, given without expecting anything back, that simply keeps lasting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["nostalgia & memory","identity & becoming"],"means":"a vanished self and place loved with nothing expected in return","built":"a spread of cheese on bread triggers the flood: memory arrives faster than it can be narrated"},
"pro8l3m~mayday":{"haiku":"Dreams of escape and power collapse under criminal entanglement and failed redemption.","opus":"The parable of Andrzej, who chased crime dreaming of Mercedes and villas while others played it safe, only to end rotting in a cell as life laughs at everyone's plans. Life is the dirty joke that undoes every fantasy you're sold.","sonnet":"A Polish rap song following the life of a fictional character named Andrzej (Jędrzej) — he dreamed of escaping his working-class life through street hustle, got trapped by his boss who valued him too much to let him go, watched his dreams drift away alongside his partner Magda, and ultimately attempts suicide by hanging but survives; the song is a tragicomic portrait of a life that kept saying \"not yet.\"","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~memento":{"haiku":"Gratitude to mentors frames the speaker's rise from underworld obscurity into visibility and success. Recognition of those who taught survival becomes the foundation of legacy.","opus":"A come-up story told from the top, thanking the block, the mentors, and the old rap tapes that pulled the narrator out of poverty and into a career. Underneath the flexing runs an elegy for the aging pioneers who got the bill while he drove off in the Mercedes, and it galls him more than flat cola.","sonnet":"Looking back from a position of earned success, the speaker traces a path out of poverty and near-ruin — an estate block, a creditor chase, near-descent into hell — crediting the mentors, the hand extended, the cassette tapes of predecessors whose wrinkled faces now appear on those recordings; 'iron while it's hot, there's still money to be made' is the motto, gratitude the posture.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~mi-dzynarodowa-1":{"fable":"A one-sided phone call captured mid-conversation: a guy in some warmer timezone catches up with a friend abroad, then eases into the real news, that a certain Kaśka has somehow found out something she shouldn't have, cracking a password or digging up incriminating photos. He warns his buddy that a domestic blowup is waiting for him when he gets home. As a spoken interlude it works purely as texture and setup, a snatch of everyday gossip and impending drama that stitches the mixtape together rather than delivering any song.","fableDeep":"A one-sided phone call doing a full act of narrative work. The staging lives in the casual openers — 'no siemasz, siemasz', headphones going in, a remark about twenty-degree weather — establishing distance and leisure before the payload drops: 'Kaśka do mnie dzwoniła przed chwilą' (Kaśka just called me), with the crucial detail that nobody gave her the number, so she must have 'znała hasło' — known the password — or tracked down the 'fotostory z Anetą', the evidence phrased in teen-magazine vocabulary, betrayal as a photo-comic. The forecast is delivered with a connoisseur's relish: 'afera na bogato' — a scandal in the deluxe edition — brewing for the homecoming. The asymmetry is the craft: we hear only the messenger, never the traveler's replies, so the cheater exists purely as a silence absorbing bad news. The sign-off frames the whole thing as loyalty, a heads-up before the night begins. It is about the interval before consequences land, when infidelity is still gossip between friends rather than a detonation at home.","haiku":"A casual phone conversation between friends in Poland touching on a girlfriend calling unexpectedly and relationship drama. The speaker warns his friend about drama waiting when he returns home.","opus":"Not a lyric but a staged phone call: one friend warns another (traveling abroad) that a woman named Kaśka just called him, having somehow learned the password and found compromising photos of him with another girl. It sets up a domestic-drama skit, an infidelity about to blow up when he gets home, the casual slang underscoring how mundanely betrayal unravels.","sonnet":"A fragment of a phone conversation between two friends — one is abroad enjoying warm weather, the other is relaying gossip: a woman named Kasia called, somehow got the number, and there may have been photos found of someone named Aneta. A domestic drama is brewing back home and the caller wants to give a heads-up before the guy returns.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["friendship & loyalty"],"means":"gossip warning of a domestic reckoning waiting at the door"},
"pro8l3m~mi-dzynarodowa-4":{"fable":"Rather than the anthem its title promises, this interlude is just a caught phone call. Someone in a mellow mood over coffee rings a friend, admits he has heard nothing about whatever is going on, and cheerfully defers the whole story to a later conversation before hanging up.","fableDeep":"A skit whose entire drama lives in what is withheld. The caller radiates uncomplicated contentment — 'bajkowy nastrój, piję kawkę' (fairy-tale mood, drinking my coffee), the diminutive kawka making even the coffee cozy — and rings for no reason at all: 'myślę - zadzwonię' (I thought, I'll call), a rare gesture in this album's world made without an angle. The pivot is inaudible to us: the other man evidently says something, and the reply arcs from curiosity to exposed ignorance — 'nie, nie słyszałem o tym. Nikt mi nie powiedział' (no, I hadn't heard about it; nobody told me) — cushioned by the idiom 'jak to mówią' (as they say), a verbal shrug thrown over a hole in his knowledge. Resolution is pure postponement: we'll talk, you'll tell me more, a breezy goodbye. Set against the earlier warning call, the symmetry stings — news travels this circle selectively, and being out of the loop is itself a message about where you stand. It is about how bad news actually moves through a group of friends: casually, incompletely, and always scheduled for later.","haiku":"Casual Polish spoken dialogue about making a phone call, a conversational mood, asking what to discuss—social intimacy and offhand connection.","opus":"A mundane snippet of a casual phone call, one friend calling another about some news he hadn't heard, agreeing to meet and talk more; essentially a slice-of-life skit with no thematic content beyond everyday chatter.","sonnet":"A casual phone call snippet — someone in a good mood drinking coffee calls a friend who doesn't know about some news; they agree to talk more later.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["friendship & loyalty"],"means":"cheerfully deferring the whole story to a later conversation"},
"pro8l3m~michael-de-santa":{"haiku":"A Polish rap portrait of a man living a hedonistic, violent lifestyle in a palm-lined paradise, dealing with wealth, crime, and family chaos.","opus":"A first-person crime fantasy narrated in the voice of a Grand Theft Auto-style gangster, moving through palm trees, drinks, drugs, shootings, and carjackings between casinos and family arguments. He treats mayhem as a game he can pause by taking off his goggles, blurring the line between player and character.","sonnet":"A first-person narrator moves through a sun-soaked, palm-tree city living out a GTA-style fantasy — armed confrontations, fast cars, casino nights, shady associates — pausing only for fleeting, irritated interactions with a wife and daughter before putting on goggles and heading back out.","src":"haiku","fable":"PRO8L3M ventriloquize the entire track through Michael de Santa, GTA V's aging, unhappy criminal-in-witness-protection, so that every luxury-and-carnage flex — Venice palms, the black Aventador, the AK with its red laser, running men down on the crosswalk while the CIA glints in the mirror — arrives pre-quoted, a genre swagger the duo know is borrowed pixels rather than autobiography. The English barks salted through the verses are pitched like the game's own voice-lines, tightening the frame until the song reads less as a boast than as a portrait of someone living inside a boast. What breaks it open is the refrain gesture that closes each verse: a wife and then a daughter screaming somewhere off-mic, and the narrator sighing that the kids are, after all, his, before he takes off the goggles — the fantasy is VR, and the take is that this omnipotent digital gangster is really a bored man at home whose family keeps calling him back to a life he'd rather pause. The nod to Molesta Ewenement in his headphones plants a real Polish rap lineage under the American cartoon, quietly insisting the escapism is homegrown even when the dream it wears is imported. The endlessly liquid hook — money flows, guys flow, life flows, and a mortgaged flat at dawn — is the mask's underside: beneath the yayo and the casinos, the only thing that actually keeps moving is debt and the drift of an ordinary life.","fv":2,"themes":["freedom & escape","artifice & performance","family & blood","money & the street"],"means":"a borrowed gangster persona worn over ordinary debt and drift","built":"the goggles-off gesture literalises the whole song as VR","fvr":"2.4"},
"pro8l3m~mistrz-ping-ponga":{"haiku":"A Polish rapper contrasts his parents' stagnation and superstition with his own ambition for wealth and escape, rejecting their worldview and seeking luxury.","opus":"A sneering portrait of escaping a bleak family origin, a mother who trusts TV psychics and politicians into unemployment, a father buried in decades of drudgery, while the narrator fantasizes Testarossas, Barbados and stripping his miss of Italian lingerie. He rejects the nine-to-five conveyor entirely, seeing a crown on his own head in the cards. Pro8l3m, the Polish duo, sharpen class contempt and lavish materialism into swaggering, luxury-drenched braggadocio over icy beats.","sonnet":"A sharp contrast between a provincial upbringing and the lifestyle the speaker has claimed for himself — parents tethered to TV fortune-tellers and shift work, while the speaker flies over Barbados and handles Italian goods. There's no guilt, only the cold pleasure of having moved past that world, and a glimpse of a future already written with a crown at the end.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~molly":{"haiku":"Hedonistic party lifestyle, wealth display, and sexual conquest.","opus":"Hedonistic bragging about a lavish, chaotic party of drugs, luxury, women and money, living recklessly like Mick Jagger.","sonnet":"A boastful night of VIP excess — luxury cars, alcohol, drugs, and women — with a sharp-tongued Polish rap bravado.","src":"haiku","fable":"A night of luxury-brand hedonism that keeps revealing itself as a heist, built on a running him-versus-her parallelism: he wants the alcohol, she wants the contraband; he drinks straight from the source while she keeps brass knuckles on for fun; and every emptied wallet quietly gets split between them. Molly is both the party drug and the femme fatale who is the party, so the song never separates the high from the robbery — the giddy boast about finding a wife the night before and the mugging in the parking lot are one event, narrated by a man too pleased with himself to draw the line. The Mick Jagger break is the entire ethos in one image: pure rockstar swagger with no interest in whose night it costs, and the hook about a biography with a gap in it admits that the amnesia is precisely the point.","fv":2,"themes":["party & hedonism","money & the street","love & desire"],"means":"the high and the robbery narrated as one inseparable event","built":"him-versus-her parallelism runs the whole track, the Mick Jagger break as the ethos condensed","fvr":"2.4"},
"pro8l3m~monza":{"haiku":"Street racing culture and reckless pursuit of speed, wealth, and status mask existential dread. The speaker drives fast toward hell knowing they're burning.","opus":"The Polish hip-hop duo, celebrated for cinematic production, floors a reckless getaway where a stolen car, screaming tires, and adrenaline outrank money entirely. The narrator and his lover barrel past motels and roadblocks, always chasing more, forever wanting an M5, aware he'll burn in hell for the days he wasted. Speed becomes the only currency, escape the only plan worth having.","sonnet":"A stolen car tears through streets at full throttle — screwdriver in the ignition, clutch burning, tires smoking — with a woman riding along as the whole reckless night fuses into erotic adrenaline. The driver knows days are being wasted, hell approaching, yet the hunger for speed, for more, for an M5, overrides every consequence.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~na-audiencji":{"fable":"A brutal seduction narrative that stages sex as domination theater, all swagger and trophy-collecting, the woman reduced to props and conquest until the final turn detonates it: what read as a boastful hookup reveals itself as a fatal, staged act, the mask of the braggart lover slipping to expose something colder underneath. The confessional detail that lands is quiet and damning: at the moment of climactic terror, the name she cries is not his, which retroactively rereads the whole swaggering monologue as fantasy, performance, or displacement over the fact that he was never the one wanted. The outro's blindfold-and-gilt tableau completes the shift from bravado to something ritual and sinister, leaving the boast hollowed out from within.","fableDeep":"The title pun organizes the boast: an 'audiencja' is a royal audience, so the encounter is staged as court ceremony with the narrator as sovereign — and the mirrors he keeps 'dla atencji' (for attention) confirm the room is built for watching himself perform. The narration works by relentless simile, converting every act into a punchline or a brand: wolf imagery, Bill Clinton conjured as an imagined spectator eating popcorn, the woman complimented as prettier than 'Lil' Kim po zmianach' (Lil' Kim after the surgeries) — praise that functions as an insult to a third party, which is the register throughout; even admiration is one-upmanship. The proverb 'jedna jaskółka nie czyni wiosny' (one swallow doesn't make a spring) tracks her escalating compliance like a project plan, yesterday's disgust becoming today's appetite as evidence of his skill. What is absent is any interiority — hers entirely, his beyond appetite; the love letter he jokes about writing is the one sincere impulse, instantly laughed off. It is about sex performed for the retelling, where the partner is a prop and the true object of desire is the narrator's own legend.","haiku":"Seduction leading to rough sexual encounter.","opus":"Graphic, boastful account of a rough sexual encounter in a lavish apartment, ending on a disturbing note of violence.","sonnet":"An explicit, graphic sexual encounter narrated in boastful detail from start to finish.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"the boast hollowed out by the wrong name she cries","built":"the full seduction reads as fantasy once the wrong name lands"},
"pro8l3m~na-rympa":{"haiku":"Warsaw street hustlers and pawn brokers trade contraband and stolen goods among the poor and wealthy living parallel lives.","opus":"The Polish duo reconstruct the grey-market hustle of early-nineties Poland, when smuggled hams, contraband electronics, and Western brands moved through Warsaw's Praga district and its makeshift bazaars. A tender opening wish to see elephants gives way to a catalogue of petty crime and currency-changers, the whole economy bent by a longing for America while rich and poor scrape past each other on the same dark streets.","sonnet":"A portrait of early-1990s Warsaw street hustle — black-market trade in electronics, contraband cigarettes, stolen cuts of meat, and currency exchange in front of the Pewex hard-currency stores. Small-time operators navigate a world split between the desperate and the emerging nouveau riche, held together by daily survival and dry neighbourhood wit.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~nascar": {"haiku":"A man realizes he's been living recklessly at breakneck speed, chasing empty pursuits and accumulating trauma, and now must slow down and reconsider what truly matters.","opus":"A nostalgic reckoning with time and excess, the narrator racing life like NASCAR only to watch years slip through his fingers, chasing money and love he never had and swapping women, rings and even a child for something lasting, then deciding to change lanes and slow down. Pro8l3m's Oskar raps it with Walter White duality, Warsaw hip-hop turned confessional about the emptiness at the finish line.","sonnet":"A reckoning with speed as self-destruction — the singer has been living at NASCAR pace, chasing money he never had, changing partners to fill a gap money could not, accumulating years while leaking meaning. The nostalgia is specific: white trainers, Aerosmith on the stereo, a courtyard that moved at a human pace. Now he is finally signalling, changing lane, not sure it is not already too late.","src":"haiku","fable":"A three-voice engine on one conceit: life driven flat-out in circles, stuck in the left lane, blowing past every uncountable value that flashes by too fast to grab. Oskar races nostalgia — he plants himself before the world sped up, a kid under the tower block who still had time to watch life move like go-karting, and now tears along like an Aston Martin with more and more things inside him and less and less of himself. Pezet takes the same car from the wreck: he wanted the money and the loves he never had, so he kept swapping both, and learns that no ring, child, or third party redirects something already at that speed. The refrain is the only foot off the gas — too many years watching time run through the fingers, so I slow and change lanes — but it gives itself away, still hunting peace where it admits it won't be found. The irony lives in that gap: a chorus that vows to downshift bracketing two verses that never once brake.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","nostalgia & memory","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"speed as the alibi that keeps the self from settling","built":"the chorus vows to slow down bracketing verses that never brake"},
"pro8l3m~national-geographic":{"haiku":"The speaker observes urban corruption, excess, and decay while describing a lifestyle of drugs, materialism, and hedonism. They witness life dissolving into chaos, bad outcomes, and moral compromise around them.","opus":"A relentless catalogue of seeing, every line beginning with what the narrator watches: wrecked money, alcoholism, whores, debts, a friend in the psych ward, lives cracking like crack on a burner while a coin flip decides their fate. He notices everyone's ruin except a way out of his own. Pro8l3m, the Polish rap duo, favor exactly this unblinking urban reportage where luxury cars and gutter despair sit in the same frame, the swagger corroded from within.","sonnet":"A tour of a city seen entirely through what it reveals about the speaker — blown money, alcoholism, beautiful cars, people drowning in debt and poor decisions, a friend locked in a psych ward, an EKG reading like a warning. Every verse is what he sees versus what he can't see anymore, the gap between the two widening until he's writing a requiem he admits he doesn't understand.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~nicea":{"haiku":"Calculated excess and transgressive luxury dominate; wealth and danger become interchangeable markers of success.","opus":"A dense flex of criminal luxury from the Polish duo, piling designer labels, drugs, cars and Riviera destinations into a glittering fantasy. Monet on the wall, Nice as the destination, cash spent like spit, it revels in ill-gotten opulence while nodding, half-ironically, at the gangster archetype. The excess is the point, a cinematic caricature of the high life.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper catalogs the spoils of street success — Patek Philippe watches, BVLGARI signet rings, a Porsche, a French Riviera villa — delivered in a torrent of brand-name braggadocio and casual menace. The persona is someone who climbed from the gutter to absolute excess, spending hundreds of thousands \"like spitting,\" and the luxury itself becomes the flex: \"kierunek Nicea, nie kuchnie IKEA.\"","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~noc":{"haiku":"The speaker walks night streets where violence and decay define life, finding meaning in witnessing darkness and speaking truths that endure.","opus":"Night as the only home a man can navigate, its scenery streetwalkers, violence, all-night pizzerias, blood in spit, a self that dies a little each time he drinks. Death here reminds him he is alive, and words that can survive are all he has going forward. Pro8l3m, the Warsaw duo, turn the city after dark into something almost tender and wholly nocturnal, reputation and menace balanced on the same block.","sonnet":"Night as a character — seductive, violent, indifferent. The speaker moves through dark streets that feel more alive to him than daylight does, finding the city's underside more honest than anything the sun reveals. He buries his truths there because only words and the night itself know how to stay alive. The block gives nothing but cold showers and the need to protect your reputation.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~otch-a":{"haiku":"Existential questioning spirals around purpose and meaning as the speaker interrogates whether anything justifies suffering or if all efforts dissolve into the void.","opus":"A relentless interrogation of why anything matters, conscience, love, friends, money, dreams, if it can't be shared or held. Staring into the abyss, the narrator finds the only answer is having someone to share it all with.","sonnet":"A spiral of existential questions that all end in the same void — \"the abyss looks back\" — the speaker asks what good is conscience, love, money, opinion, or friendship if everything slips away, and stares into the chasm asking whether any of it is real, whether they have the strength to show the pain.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~outro":{"haiku":"The narrator faces imminent death, reflecting on a life of crime, betrayal, and choosing vice over virtue with resignation and dark acceptance. The song is a final confession of someone at the end of a violent life.","opus":"A dying man's final reckoning as he's hunted down and killed alone in a jungle; he regrets nothing yet acknowledges he knowingly chose the wrong path over doing good, pleading only for a quick end.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop monologue delivered as a dying man's reckoning — the narrator, alone in a jungle of debts and enemies, accepts an imminent violent end while confessing that he always knew right from wrong but chose the other path anyway, from poverty toward the Hilton and Rolex, and now regrets it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dying man narrates his own execution in a jungle grave, and the read turns on the confession beneath the bravado: he doesn't blame the debts or the killers but himself. The road never pointed toward anything fine, so he swerved toward luxury knowing exactly what good was and choosing against it anyway. The whole outro is structured as a reckoning that moves from tough refusal of regret to a quiet, damning clarity — the mask of the unrepentant gangster slips to reveal a man who understood the moral cost in advance and picked the wrong side with open eyes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["death & grief","money & the street","shame & repression"],"means":"choosing the wrong side with clear eyes and no one else to blame","built":"the gangster mask slips from tough refusal of regret to quiet, damning clarity about moral cost"},
"pro8l3m~p-sny":{"fable":"A breakup rendered as a slow suffocation: the speaker narrates time draining away second by second while the distance between two people quietly widens, and the woman he addresses seals herself into her own emptiness. The movement runs from that shared airless room toward a wall at the end of the road, and he keeps asking to turn back even as he admits nothing can help anymore. Underneath the exhausted refusal to explain himself lies a darker plea — he imagines being buried in a pit of forgetting so their mutual harm can finally stop, which is the mask of surrender hiding a wish that the whole thing had never had to matter. The half-dreams of the title are exactly that limbo: neither awake enough to leave nor asleep enough to be spared, holding onto the one thing he insists survives, the time already lived and therefore already theirs.","fableDeep":"The chorus counts downward — days, hours, minutes, seconds — as if the relationship were being measured with ever finer instruments the closer it gets to zero, while she withdraws into half-dreams, a state that is neither sleep nor presence. The stage directions are all suffocation: the room too airless for them, even the beam of light too dirty to live in. Then comes the contradiction that carries the song: a wall at the end of the road, an invitation to turn around, and in the same breath the admission that nothing will help now — bargaining and verdict fused into one couplet. The verse deepens the impasse: he would like to stop running and chase something again but cannot, and refuses to explain himself because an explanation would cheapen it. The future he sketches is conditional and grim — bury him in a pit of forgetting once her scream stops waking him and his hangover stops waking her — mutual irritation revealed as the last binding tissue. Thesis: a breakup where the only salvageable property is the past tense, the shared time no one can repossess.","haiku":"A relationship slowly suffocating under mounting differences; the singer cannot escape and the lover drowns in dreams while communication fails, leaving only the shared memory of time together.","opus":"A Polish hip-hop portrait of a couple decaying in slow motion, drifting into separate silences as differences pile up. The narrator knows the wall at the end of the road is coming and asks only that the shared time still counts as theirs.","sonnet":"A Polish song about a relationship at its end — days, hours, and minutes accumulate the differences between two people; the room is too suffocating, the road hits a wall, and the narrator says there will be no more chances, only tears and shared time that now belongs to the past.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness","death & grief"],"means":"wishing for a pit of forgetting so the shared harm can stop","built":"the half-dream title names a limbo neither awake enough to leave nor asleep enough to be spared"},
"pro8l3m~piek-o-jest-w-nas":{"haiku":"A broke man borrows money and encounters death and corruption; hell exists within humans who steal and betray out of desperation and greed.","opus":"Two vignettes of small damnation: a broke debtor scrambling to repay a loan, arriving at a door to learn the man he owes has died in a crash, leaving richer by the unpaid cash; and a smuggler on a Berlin train who charms a Cambridge girl only to walk off with her bag of money. The refrain lands the thesis, hell is inside us, a flaw in our nature. Pro8l3m sketch petty betrayal with the precision of a short story.","sonnet":"Two parables about the hell already inside people. First: a man scrambling to repay a debt discovers the person he owed is suddenly dead, and shame hits harder than relief. Second: a train ride to Berlin where a well-dressed stranger talks herself up while he cleans her out, the devil apparently nudging him from inside. Hell isn't somewhere else — it's the flaw baked into human nature, the impulse that arrives before conscience does.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~pirelli":{"haiku":"A person is trapped in cycles of self-harm, crime, and recklessness, painting destruction on walls like a calendar. The song is a raw narrative of addiction and nihilism.","opus":"A lurid, hallucinatory crime fantasy, the narrator talking to God in slang, setting fires and vomiting on the plan, smuggling goods hidden in fruit like French soldiers in trenches, corpses lined up like a Pirelli calendar. It fuses sex, violence and dealer bravado into a delirious high, tires screeching like Mariah Carey. Pro8l3m, the Polish duo, push their noir gangster imagery to its most excessive here, a blunt-smoking reverie on a distant planet closing the whole storm.","sonnet":"A frenetic self-portrait of someone lit from the inside by destruction — burning money, talking to God in slang, bodies stacked like a Pirelli calendar on the walls. The speed is constant: car chases, smuggled cargo, Blackberry calls from lawyers, and the governing principle is that nothing can be fixed with polish anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~prawdy-nie-m-wi-nikt":{"haiku":"Nobody speaks truth; people are trapped in meaningless cycles, choosing numbness over courage. The song condemns collective apathy and the comfort of lies.","opus":"A bleak generational verdict, insisting nobody tells the truth and no one is safe, that the worst thing you can do is survive, believe or endure, that everyone walks around wasted because we now fear each other and ourselves more than fire or hell. Truth and lies have traded places. Pro8l3m, the Polish duo, wrap Lovecraftian dread and a noose tightening like a pitbull's jaws around a portrait of a hopeless, drink-numbed cohort, defiance drowned in resignation.","sonnet":"A tract on collective numbness and existential dread — no one tells the truth, nothing promises change, and surviving itself might be the worst outcome. The speaker cycles through nights, alcohol, and blurred thinking, concluding that people walk around drunk because they're terrified of themselves and of each other, too afraid of hell to believe in it anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~prequel":{"haiku":"A boastful narrative of hedonism and criminal indulgence—drugs, luxury, casual sex—framed as aspiration, with surface bragging masking underlying emptiness and consequences.","opus":"A Polish crime-rap fantasy of luxury built on dirty money, sketching a dealer's paranoid high life of drugs, cars, and evaded tax men where the wealth reeks of the scam that bought it and the wanting never stops.","sonnet":"A Polish rap narrator catalogues a hedonistic lifestyle of drugs, alcohol, women, and conspicuous wealth alongside the chaos of tax evasion and debt, framing all of it as aspirational excess he pursues while his finances teeter.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses catalogue excess from the inside of a comedown, where luxury and rot smell identical and the boasts keep sliding into their own aftermath: unpaid taxes, dodged court letters, drugs stacked one atop the next, a body still keeping score. The chorus is the mask laid bare, a childlike all-I-want list of cars, safes, and a private jet chanted over the wreck the verses describe, so the wealth reads less like triumph than like the only vocabulary left. As a prequel, it plays as an origin without redemption: the appetite is already total, and nothing underneath it is being saved.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["money & the street","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"a childlike want-list chanted over the wreck the verses describe","built":"the chorus luxury list plays as the only vocabulary left once appetite is total"},
"pro8l3m~przeb-j-nocy":{"haiku":"A Polish urban narrative chronicles a young person's escape from provincial life to Warsaw in the 1990s, where they enter the black market car trade—a gritty, morally gray survival story of reinvention through criminal enterprise.","opus":"Over a pulsing club refrain about a hit that burrows into the brain, the Warsaw duo tell a coming-of-age crime memoir set in December 1991, when a provincial kid steps off the train at Warszawa Wschodnia into an uncle's shady used-car trade. Chop shops, car auctions, and VIP nights follow, and the narrator draws a line he won't cross, insisting his is honest work rather than mafia in balaclavas.","sonnet":"A first-person account of migrating from the provinces to Warsaw in 1991 and learning the used-car grey market from a uncle who ran a tow-truck empire near Praga. The narrator graduates from stripping parts to running chop shops to legitimising — all set against the pulse of a fictional radio DJ whose nightly hit soundtrack marks every moral turn.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~puerto-rico":{"fable":"Two innocent images bookend the song, and the trick is that they are the same image seen twice: children skipping off to school and jars of homemade preserves spilled across a counter are the domestic surface under which a pusher's transaction and a woman coming apart are hiding. The verse strips the pretense away into a curdled, high-strung deal, and then the chorus returns unchanged so the horror can pose again as a wholesome scene she is calmly posting for others to admire.","fableDeep":"The structure is a jump-cut: the bookending quatrain is domestic idyll — 'dzieci wesoło pobiegły do szkoły' (the kids ran off happily to school), preserves tumbling onto the counter — while the middle holds the night that shadows it: cocaine 'z Puerto Rico', a scene starting 'bez intra' (no intro) in an unrefined dive, tension light, corners crowded. The most loaded pairing is 'on jest szychą, ona z wszywką' — he's a big shot, she has the implant — the disulfiram implant being Poland's blunt-force sobriety measure, so her presence at the party is already sabotage of a cure; and then the quiet line detonates: 'ona coś zamilkła, dostała do drinka' — she went silent; something was put in her drink — a spiking reported in the same flat voice as the school run. The refrain's cruelest touch is the platform: she posts it 'na swoje story', the curated feed running parallel to a possible crime, while the man looks sicker frame by frame beside the children's breakfast. It is about the seam between daylight normality and nighttime predation, and how the feed lets both stream from one household without anyone naming what happened.","haiku":"Drug use and recklessness among youth spiral into addiction and online spectacle, with reality becoming intoxicated performance.","opus":"A queasy vignette of drug-fueled decadence, cocaine and a woman with a sobriety implant partying while children innocently head off to school. The juxtaposition of ordinary domestic life with a spiraling, spiked-drink bender exposes the seedy underside behind the curated social-media story.","sonnet":"A compressed vignette of urban debauchery — drugs, a woman with a sobriety implant, a nightclub hook-up, something slipped into a drink — told in casual, detached shorthand while the next morning's domestic scene plays out in the background.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["money & the street","artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"horror posing again as a wholesome scene she posts for others","built":"the chorus returns unchanged so the same domestic image frames both the deal and its cover"},
"pro8l3m~pycha":{"haiku":"Violent dissolution of identity, blood and silence marking a final communication stripped of words.","opus":"A stark vignette of a suicide: a man vomiting blood into a basin, dialing a last number on his Nokia while the voice on the other end says nothing because everything is already understood. The title, Pride, names what couldn't be spoken.","sonnet":"A stark five-line scene: a man who has slashed himself submerges his head in a bowl that turns pink, wipes blood on a towel, and dials one last number — the person on the other end doesn't speak, because everything is already understood.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~rewia":{"fable":"A blearily sped-up night that curdles into aftermath: the first verse rides the strobe and the swagger of putting on a show while already swallowing whatever's on offer, and by its end the performer is flat on the floor talking to himself, watching phantom trails smear across the sky. The second verse drops any pretense and just inventories the fuel — cheap grams portioned out, a three-day bender rounding into itself — so that the boast about being brand new lands as the exhausted opposite of what it claims. The revue of the title is exactly this: dazzle staged over depletion, a body still performing long after it's spent.","fableDeep":"The brevity is the meaning: two clipped verses of nouns and fragments, syntax stripped the way the scene strips people. 'Te same gęby, rave'y' (the same mugs, the same raves) opens on pure recurrence — no verb needed, because nothing new ever happens here. The title reframes the club as 'rewia', a revue: choreographed spectacle, same acts nightly, and the narrator is both performer and reviewer — 'odpierdalam rewię, zjadłem dziewięć' (I'm putting on a show, I ate nine), the pill count doubling as a performance number, followed immediately by the floor: lying on the ground, noting the girl will partake too, collapse and recruitment in a single breath. The loveliest and bleakest line is 'gadam do siebie, ślady na niebie' — talking to myself, trails in the sky — hallucination as the only scenery left. The second verse is drug logistics as nursery rhyme — doses in crumbs, from a cup or the stash, a three-day run — capped by 'jestem nówka' (I'm brand new), the lie of the reset. It is about the party as an endlessly rerun show whose performers keep declaring themselves new precisely because they are being used up.","haiku":"Polish nightclub culture and hedonism—rave parties, drugs (Whisky, trace substances), brief sex, nighttime excess, and a girl who vanishes into just a wet stain.","opus":"A grimy Polish club vignette, the same faces and raves, the DJ's blends, drugs weighed out and hangovers guaranteed. The narrator staggers through the spectacle talking to himself, both reveling in and disgusted by the endless circuit.","sonnet":"A fractured party scene — same faces, same drugs, same meaningless noise — the narrator knocked flat on the floor, barely conscious, the girl beside him in the same condition, both of them reduced to debris by a three-day bender.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"dazzle staged over depletion, performing past the point of being spent","built":"the brand-new of the chorus lands as the exhausted opposite of what it claims"},
"pro8l3m~ronin":{"haiku":"Wealth and independence breed isolation; the solitary path demands abandoning ordinary bonds.","opus":"A masterless gangster's creed where people are used and discarded like bones, everything is money, drugs and disposable women, and trust is extended to no one. The luxury is real but the conscience he claims is crystal rings hollow against the coldness.","sonnet":"A Polish rap track about navigating life as a lone operator — the narrator moves through a world of handshake deals, luxury, women, and distrust, taking money on the side while preaching self-reliance. The recurring line about verbal agreements (\"dogadane na gębę\") and the injunction that everyone should watch out for everyone captures the ruthless pragmatism of the track's moral universe.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~rotterdam":{"haiku":"Hedonistic excess and moral decay in European debauchery; drug use, wealth, and predatory behavior blur consequences and meaning.","opus":"A nocturnal blur of luxury brands, drink, drugs and disposable women, the Polish rappers narrating hedonism as both flex and creeping numbness. Beneath the designer name-drops runs an emptiness where every pleasure is bought and nothing quite holds value.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop night-life track about transgression, disposable women, and status — sex, money, alcohol, designer brands, recording studios, and a worldview where women are interchangeable objects paid for and discarded, delivered with bravado and contempt.","src":"haiku","fable":"A boast that keeps sliding into confession. The duo piles up the trophies of the good life — the imported liquor poured into a Porsche, brand names they can't even place, front-row access forced open like a jammed door — but each flex is undercut by decay: eyes like a corpse, a woman ruined and handed off to the repairmen, money offered to her like virtue while it only feeds her tears. The recurring hook, a date reduced to two numbers, plays as a deliberate blackout — not fighting it anymore, not knowing what happened where or with whom — so the swagger reads as the alibi of someone already numbed past caring. Underneath the designer haze the real currency is admitted in the outro: everything of value moves like contraband smuggled under the counter, and the high life is just something bought off the books.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["money & the street","addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the high life admitted in the outro as something bought off the books","built":"the date-reduced-to-two-numbers hook plays as deliberate blackout: not fighting it, not knowing what happened or where"},
"pro8l3m~s-class":{"haiku":"Generational poverty breeds violence as survival mechanism; a damaged child becomes a criminal not by choice but by circumstance and inherited desperation.","opus":"A Polish rap portrait of a nameless ghetto kid, born of hatred and neglect, who has emptied himself of feeling because money is the only way out. The chorus catalogs his world with grim precision while the narrator admits, at first, he had nothing to say to him.","sonnet":"A Polish-language character study of a boy from nothing who grows up into a cold, dangerous man — raised on hatred and cruelty, stripped of emotion, patient enough to wait until a knife finds your throat. The chorus catalogs the different tribes around him: criminals chasing weed, lawyers chasing money, mothers drowning in tears. The S-class Mercedes is the aspirational symbol coating a world of violence; the boy knows money is the only exit from the ghetto, and he will take it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A boy raised on Lego and hatred, too young for a name yet already carrying live rounds, is drawn as a monster the world manufactured and then fears: emotionless, unforgiving, waiting with patience for the neighborhood's crosses and nooses to come for him. The hook is a rigged economy in miniature, where every faction gets its cut and only the mothers are left drowning, so the dead-eyed kid's chase for cash reads less as ambition than the single exit from a basement with money hidden in a can. What darkens is the turn between the two verses: the narrator moves from an honest not knowing what to say into a colder certainty, admitting the whole grim arithmetic was never mysterious and that the child had to learn it too. Underneath the gangster armor the read insists on the toddler face, the restart nobody gets to press, and the fact that refusing love and memory is the only survival this life offered him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["money & the street","violence & murder","family & blood","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the toddler face still visible beneath the gangster armor the world forced on","built":"the turn between verses: honest not knowing what to say hardens into colder certainty that the child had to learn the arithmetic too"},
"pro8l3m~sick-boy":{"fable":"The verse is a single breathless imperative list, a whole life dictated back to you in second person because you chose it: build the career, the family, the mortgage, then the divorce, the second marriage, the drinking and the venereal burn, the health you trade away and the respect you buy back. The relentless piling-on of commands is the point, each new instruction landing before the last can register, so that a full biography collapses into one exhausting exhale that arrives, inevitably, at be revived, then die anyway. Underneath the sneering catalogue is the flat verdict of the title: this is what wanting things does to a person, and the sick boy is not some junkie other but the ordinary man who took the deal.","fableDeep":"The opening — you chose, so live — sets up a Polish street-register rework of Trainspotting's choose-life monologue: the whole track is imperative second person, which doubles as advertisement and accusation, life as terms of service you already clicked. The list's power is its flatness. Finish your degree, have a daughter, have a divorce, catch something venereal, plant trees, take the loan, repay the loan — aspiration and catastrophe get the same beat and the same case endings, so no hierarchy survives; the loop structures, marrying twice and surrendering a second house, show the program simply restarting after each crash. Vices arrive as duties too — drink, use a little, have your hangovers and night returns — obligation swallowing even rebellion. The endpoint is engineered anticlimax: be resuscitated, then die, despite it all — compliance purchases nothing. Thesis: a brochure for the standard life whose only guaranteed deliverable is the funeral.","haiku":"The song catalogs the prescribed life trajectory—work, family, marriage, divorce, repeat—as a hollow, predestined cycle of suffering and temporary pleasure.","opus":"A bitterly ironic checklist of a conventional life, work, dreams, marriage, kids, divorce, remarriage, then repeated loss of home, money and health. The mounting list of hangovers, benders, venereal disease and ruin exposes the emptiness of doing everything you're supposed to, ending on the wish to die despite it all.","sonnet":"A rapid-fire inventory of everything Polish society tells you to do with your life — get a job, get married, get a divorce, drink, get sick, earn respect, lose it — delivered as a satirical checklist that ends in death regardless.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming","money & the street"],"means":"a whole biography collapsed into one exhausting second-person exhale","built":"the relentless imperative list lands before each command can register, ending at be revived then die anyway"},
"pro8l3m~skrable":{"haiku":"A Polish hip-hop track using wordplay and alphabetic structure to depict urban life filled with petty crime, casual drug use, sex, and poor life choices. It presents everyday scenes of moral compromise and pleasure-seeking as a game.","opus":"Pro8l3m turn a game of Scrabble into a sprawling roll call of neighbourhood lives, the narrator rotting in his room while the city rots in traffic. Postmen, dealers counting piles, a father seasoning sandwiches, a grandfather grumbling, cheating lovers and broken bones all flicker past. The alphabet becomes an inventory of vice, each letter a drug, a bra size or a trick, life laid out as tiles to pick from A to Z.","sonnet":"On a single ordinary afternoon, dozens of lives intersect without touching — a heartbroken teenager, a gambling father, a drug dealer counting stacks, a young man lying in bed yelling at the ceiling. The scrabble board of existence is laid out letter by letter: pick one.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~st-wa":{"haiku":"Urban struggle and street life; survival in harsh conditions of deprivation and violence.","opus":"A hundred-zloty flex that opens by mocking the very idea of small money, the narrator spending like he's spitting, boasting of scars, whiskey, Rolexes and gold bars while sneering at anyone impressed by pocket change. Beneath the swagger runs contempt for the cheap and disloyal. PRO8L3M's Art Brut Mixtape cut is Polish street-rap braggadocio at full volume, luxury and menace braided together.","sonnet":"A hundred-zloty note as both throne and punchline — the speaker burns through money with contempt, celebrates criminal hustle, gambling, luxury, and the company of brothers over banks, and measures every relationship by what it costs or earns. The refrain of spending as casually as spitting frames the whole track as a brag about mastery over scarcity once survived.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~strange-days": {"haiku":"Alienation and the peculiarity of modern existence; living through bizarre and incomprehensible times.","opus":"A bleak-grey slice of city life where a woman howls, the narrator fights and hustles, calls come from sex workers, and mistakes pile up between missed payments and cheap highs. The chorus resolves everything into a curse on the city, the world and himself before wishing only to lie down and sleep. PRO8L3M render this Warsaw fatalism in claustrophobic, film-noir Polish rap.","sonnet":"Urban dissolution at 3am — a city where everything is broken, the speaker juggles a woman who knows exactly what the arrangement is, a friend who never showed, vodka without excess, and a car ride blasting grime to drown out whatever is left to say. The repeated conclusion is that the city, the world, and the self are all equally wrecked, and the only response is to lie down and sleep.","src":"haiku","fable":"A day in the life of a Warsaw dealer-hustler, told from inside the grind rather than above it. The intro's overheard chatter — a family man who doesn't drink but sits at home with wife and kids and still uses — sets the register of quiet, unremarkable ruin. Oskar's verses then walk through his own version of it: pimping, scoring, training off five kilos, smoking alone on the balcony, botched deals that somehow land a happy end anyway. The two verses accumulate as a ledger of small transactions and self-negations — I'm the gate and I hit, morality snapped upright — where every appetite is priced and nothing amounts to more than getting through the shift. Against that grind the refrain is the whole point: fucked-up city, fucked-up world, lie down and fall asleep, fucked-up me — the exhaustion turning inward until the self becomes just one more broken thing on the list, chanted more times at the end as if repetition were the only rest available. What looks like a swagger track is really a portrait of numbness, its beautiful plan for agony delivered deadpan, the will to keep playing until I die stated not as defiance but as the absence of any exit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","money & the street","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the self becoming just one more broken item on the daily ledger","built":"refrain repeated past exhaustion until chanting replaces any exit"},
"pro8l3m~street-fighter":{"haiku":"Combat and street confrontation; physical and symbolic battles for survival and dignity.","opus":"A tense street-confrontation narrative bookended by police radio chatter about an abandoned BMW, the narrator squaring up outrun by adrenaline, palming a stone hidden in his jacket, insisting he won't run even outnumbered while telling himself the moment of racing pulse is what makes him feel alive. PRO8L3M turn a back-alley fight into cinematic Polish rap, the dispatcher's mundane voice framing something violent and intimate.","sonnet":"A street confrontation turned into a meditation on the moments when your pulse is the only thing that proves you are alive — outnumbered, drunk enough to feel brave but not enough to be stupid, the speaker palms a stone and stands his ground rather than run. The police radio call bookending the track frames the raw story as something mundane and recurring, happening all over the city tonight.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~strumie":{"haiku":"A dreamer enters a surreal organic temple pulsing with life, merges with a morphing deity, and awakens uncertain if it was real.","opus":"A vivid dream-vision, the narrator entering a vast pulsing building whose marble walls run with living veins and fluorescent light, drifting bodiless past thousands of eyes on stalactites, waterfalls flowing upward, fractal alphabets he somehow understands. Only those who never doubted may enter, and a levitating deity morphs into his own shape before everything empties. Pro8l3m, the Polish duo, trade street noir here for pure surreal psychedelia, uncertain at the end whether it was dream or trip.","sonnet":"A dream narrated as architecture — a pulsing building with violet veins in the walls, ceilings where liquid flows upward, fractal alphabets on columns, a limitless hall where a deity at the center of a fountain shifts shape until it mirrors the dreamer, then disappears. The speaker wakes uncertain whether it was a dream or a trip, only that the awe was real.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~strumien":{"haiku":"A dreamscape of living architecture and sentient space reveals transcendent knowledge beyond rational language or physical form.","opus":"A vivid, hallucinatory dream of entering a living pulsing temple with veins of light and fractal alphabets, weightless and bodiless yet able to touch. At its heart a deity morphs into the dreamer's own shape before everything empties, leaving him unsure whether it was a dream or a trip.","sonnet":"A vivid Polish description of an otherworldly dream — a vast pulsing building with veins in the marble walls, fractal alphabets on columns, waterfalls flowing upward, a deity at the fountain that morphs into the narrator's own form before disappearing — leaving him uncertain whether it was a dream or a psychedelic trip.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dreamed ascent through an impossible cathedral, rendered as pure sensory immersion: the speaker moves bodiless as smoke through a pulsing, living architecture where liquid climbs the ceilings and water falls upward, defying every law he still half-expects to hold. The wonder is gated — passage granted only to those who never doubted — so the vision doubles as a test of faith or nerve, the dreamer's own conviction the price of entry. It builds toward a levitating deity at the fountain's heart, and the turn is the sting: it morphs into his own shape, and in that instant of recognition he is utterly alone in the emptiness. Waking, he can't tell dream from trip, only that no scream pulled him out — the awe itself broke the spell, leaving the unsettling sense that the sacred space and the self chasing it were always the same hollow.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"awe itself breaking the spell of a vision too holy to survive","built":"the levitating deity at the fountain's heart morphs into the dreamer's own shape, and he is utterly alone"},
"pro8l3m~styl-sportowy":{"fable":"A braggart's inventory that keeps snagging on what it can't afford to admit: the first verse struts through xenon-lit Audis, two houses, and the sportswear label of the title, but the boasts sit flush against bodies in plastic and a two-day, three-corpse tally. The wealth reads as the proceeds of something the speaker won't name. The second verse turns that unease into method, a relentless chain of denials — didn't see it, wasn't there, no names, no cameras — that protests too much even as it slides back into cologne, Grey Goose, and five-in-the-morning glamour. Underneath the designer sheen the read is an alibi rehearsed on a loop, hands trembling and head against the wall, the sport of the title really the discipline of staying clean when nothing about the life is.","fableDeep":"Two verses, two grammatical engines. The first runs on 'tak' — yes — an anaphora of affirmation cataloguing the scene: tonnage flying, silicone, xenons in the Audi, with 'dwa dni, trzy zgony' (two days, three deaths) dropped mid-inventory at exactly the same weight as club neon and drug slang, violence flattened into lifestyle listing. The second verse flips the switch to 'nie' — no — and becomes a police-statement aria: 'nie, nie widziałem / nie, nie słyszałem' (didn't see, didn't hear), wasn't there, was asleep, no names, no CCTV — an omertà so rhythmically identical to the bragging that denial and boast reveal themselves as one practice, the same man performing abundance to the street and amnesia to the state. Details like old Nokias over iPhones are operational security dressed as retro taste. The tiny chorus — ten grand, off dancing, her blue lashes, his Martens — is the payday both verses exist to fund and to deny. It is about a criminal economy where saying yes to everything and no to everything are the same survival skill.","haiku":"A Polish-language rap celebrating hedonistic street culture, money, drugs, and casual sexual encounters. The speaker flaunts material excess and dismisses judgment or accountability.","opus":"A Polish rap flaunting an angry, aggressive lifestyle of money, brand names, violence, drugs, women, and denial, with the narrator repeatedly claiming he saw nothing and knows nothing while boasting of his sporty style and two houses.","sonnet":"A nocturnal brag track set in Warsaw club culture — denial of witnessing anything, nights spent with women, no-sleep mornings, expensive cars and clothes — projecting cool indifference to consequences while cataloguing lifestyle excess.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["money & the street","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"an alibi rehearsed so often it shakes under the designer sheen","built":"the second verse's chain of denials protests too much even as it slides back into Grey Goose"},
"pro8l3m~szansa-na-sukces":{"haiku":"Chance at success and aspiration; the possibility of escaping poverty through opportunity.","opus":"A relentless cause-and-effect litany, each line a recipe: roll this and you get that, borrow and default and you get debt, work faithfully sixty years and you get money, take pills and drink and you vanish. It maps every route through Polish life to its blunt payoff, cynical and grimly funny. PRO8L3M turn the chorus's insistence that everyone must pass life's test into a machine-gun catalogue of consequences.","sonnet":"A relentless Polish life-advice parody that strings together every cause-and-effect chain the system offers — work sixty years to get the money, borrow and lose it, wreck the car, eat too much, go to prison, stare at the TV — and arrives at the only instruction that isn't a trap: get up, rinse off, look in the mirror, and meet yourself. Dark comedy about how little agency any of it really contains.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~tata-dilera-remix":{"opus":"A courtroom monologue from the father of a drug dealer, refusing to see his son as a criminal, rationalizing the Rolex and the InPost parcels as anything but dealing. The remix skewers class denial and generational blindness, the dad who rose from poverty unable to admit his golden boy is exactly the thug he saw on the news.","sonnet":"A Polish rap monologue from a father in court defending his son against drug-dealing charges — the father insists the boy was well-raised, attributes everything to bad influences and social media, and ties his own poverty background into why this accusation feels unjust; the refrain ('he was a good boy and drank little') is his mantra of denial.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A father takes the witness stand to defend his son, and the whole song is his testimony — a monologue built entirely from denial, where every alibi he offers quietly convicts the boy instead. He waves off the luxury watch, the suspicious courier parcels, the pills explained away as medicine, insisting he raised a future doctor while the evidence of small-time dealing stacks up in plain sight behind him. The chant that a good boy who barely drank keeps returning like a mantra he needs to believe, and midway he cracks, pivoting from defense to self-pity about his own hard childhood and unloving parents, so the plea for his son curdles into a plea for himself. Underneath the mask of parental faith is willful blindness — a man who would rather narrate a respectable story than see what his child has become, closing on the bleak modern image of the two of them sitting together yet each alone, lost in their phones.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["family & blood","money & the street","shame & repression"],"means":"a father's alibi testimony that quietly convicts the son it defends","built":"the good-boy mantra he keeps needing to believe cracks midway into a plea for himself rather than his child"},
"pro8l3m~tatp":{"haiku":"Polish: Terrorism narrative detailing political control, military obedience, and suicide bombing. Systems exploit individual loyalty to justify mass death.","opus":"A dual-perspective narrative on terrorism: one voice is a cynical state/political figure who sees terror as a tool to exploit, the other is a coldly professional operative, first a sniper then a suicide bomber, methodically preparing his TATP vest, praying, and detonating in the subway, killing innocents in the name of faith and glory.","sonnet":"A dual-perspective song that alternates between a cynical political operative who sees terrorism as a tool to be exploited, and a radicalized suicide bomber narrating his final morning commute to a metro station where he detonates, with TATP (a common explosive) giving the track its name.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cold monologue that inhabits a suicide bomber, stripped of any moralizing frame. The three verses tighten from the geopolitics of manufactured terror and state power toward the private ritual of one man arming himself, until the mask slips and we are inside his head as he descends into a metro at eight in the morning, praying for paradise. Its cruelty lies in method: the killer treats a body count as a rung on a ladder of faith, his trigger finger calm, his senses deliberately sealed so death registers as routine. Beneath the operator's swagger sits a hollowed-out ordinariness — he dresses shorter than usual, meets a million indistinct faces he is about to erase — the banality that lets atrocity feel procedural. The read underneath the mask is that the same machinery, faith and orders and brotherhood, arms both the soldier and the terrorist, and the song refuses to let us feel safely outside either.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","fnote":"TATP is triacetone triperoxide — the improvised peroxide explosive known as Mother of Satan, the chemistry the title names.","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"faith as the machinery that arms both soldier and terrorist equally","built":"the verses tighten from geopolitics to one man dressing shorter than usual descending into a metro"},
"pro8l3m~teb-200-1":{"haiku":"Polish narrative of a night spiraling through parties, cocaine use, and hedonism as a group of friends loses track of their stash. The song captures the chaos and moral decay of urban drug culture.","opus":"A darkly comic tale of a man who discovers his stash of cash and cocaine missing from a drawer, then retraces a chaotic, drunken night of drinking, drugs, women, and clubbing to piece together where it went, finally recalling he hid it himself.","sonnet":"A night of heavy drinking and cocaine use is reconstructed in comedic detail — a group of young men drift through a gym session, bars, a failed attempt at a brothel, and a house party, until the narrator wakes up confused about where his stash went, only to remember he hid it in his other trousers before going out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drug-fueled night reconstructed backwards from its own blank spot: a man opens a drawer, finds his stash gone, and spends the song piecing together where it went. What follows is the missing evening replayed in relentless comic detail, every small transaction of a bender chained to the next, until the punchline lands that he himself hid it while too wasted to remember. The bit turns on the way intoxication erases the very memory that would explain the loss, so the crisis and its solution are the same act. The framing conversation makes the loop explicit and universal: you obliterate yourself, you forget, and the proposed cure for the confusion is another hit that will supposedly jog it loose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","comedy & wordplay","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the crisis and its solution turning out to be the same act","built":"the framing conversation makes the loop explicit: the proposed cure for forgetting is another hit to jog the memory loose"},
"pro8l3m~tekken":{"haiku":"Polish street life swirls with romance, hedonism, and material desire; petty crimes and substance use punctuate casual seduction.","opus":"A slick nocturnal boast of Polish club decadence: women, cash, drugs, and designer labels flaunted with breathless bravado. Beneath the swagger runs contempt for dirty money and a sense that life is a rigged game he's determined to win.","sonnet":"A Polish rap about a hedonistic night out — drinking with a barmaid after friends ditch, picking up redheads in a Gallardo, filming everything on GoPro, rolling joints and pulling out cards — comparing the game of seduction to fighting in Tekken.","src":"haiku","fable":"A boast that keeps sliding off its own surface: the narrator moves through a blur of women, cabrio rides, drugs and dirty cash with the manic energy of someone who never sleeps and never lets a verdict touch him. The fighting-game title becomes the frame for a seduction played like combat, complete with a reach-and-grab move and the cold calculation of a card swiped versus an escape key hit. Underneath the flex runs a thread of paranoia and rot, sharks feeding, money that no detergent can clean, the flat admission that evil stays evil, so the endless best-again euphoria reads less like triumph than a man who dares not stop moving. The recurring rest-stop of dates, dark stout, chess and small stress is the only still point, and it sounds like exhaustion dressed as leisure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["money & the street","party & hedonism","madness & the mind"],"means":"manic euphoria that dares not stop because stopping reveals the rot","built":"the fighting-game title frames seduction as combat, complete with reach-and-grab moves and a cold card-swipe calculation"},
"pro8l3m~tiramisu":{"haiku":"A Polish rap recounting a drunken party night filled with hazy hookups, alcohol excess, and casual encounters with little consequence or meaning.","opus":"A Polish hip-hop hangover story told with unflinching squalor, waking blackout-drunk beside strangers among empty bottles and reaching for the next drink. The moral, stated plainly, is that hard liquor and one-night stands leave only painful mornings.","sonnet":"A hungover morning-after recounted in detail — waking up next to someone barely remembered, drinking again to cure the hangover, the whole sorry cycle of blackout nights, regrettable hookups, and gleeful self-destruction told without any remorse.","src":"haiku","fable":"A blackout bender told entirely from inside the blackout: the narrator wakes not knowing which body beside him is which, drinks the flat vodka before his eyes clear, and treats the whole cycle of one-night strangers and morning nausea as a romance he keeps falling into on schedule. The dark joke sits in his refrain of falling in love instantly, which is really just the next drink and the next indistinguishable partner arriving. By the second verse the same night reruns, friends narrating a passion he can't remember and a fresh girl becoming his beloved after an hour and a bottle. Underneath the swagger, the tell is his own repeated line about looking at the women through fog after the vodka is gone — he sees clearly enough to know the tenderness is a chemical mirage, and keeps choosing it anyway.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"seeing clearly through vodka fog that the tenderness is chemical, choosing it anyway","built":"each blackout reruns as a fresh romance: falling in love instantly is really just the next drink arriving"},
"pro8l3m~to-nie-by-film-feat-artur-rojek":{"haiku":"Two murderers discuss and commit an actual killing, wishing it were cinema rather than reality. The song portrays casual homicide framed as a game, depicting detachment from the moral weight of ending a life.","opus":"The Polish duo, with Artur Rojek's haunted vocal, stage a murder as though begging reality to be cinema instead. A reflection proposes killing unseen; the first passerby falls, her body cooling without noise or emotion, only fear in her eyes. The killers describe death as intravenous cocaine, a beautiful fragment of life, praying repeatedly that this were merely a film and not their own hands.","sonnet":"Two voices circle a real act of killing, framing it in the language of cinema to process what resists processing. One body falls silent, another watches it cool, and desire keeps asking what it felt like — beautiful, they say, like film — while the blood on the concrete insists this was never anything but real.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~toast":{"haiku":"A Polish-language drinking culture monologue advising how to properly drink vodka and navigate social consequences of heavy drinking without emotional expression.","opus":"A darkly comic Polish scene where a jaded old-timer schools a young party host in the crude art of hard vodka drinking—protect your kidneys, drink from the cold, don't get sloppy—while the chorus tallies grim reality: brawls, stitches, ER visits, blackouts, and waking up with prostitutes and a missing wallet, exposing the sordid emptiness behind the boozing bravado.","sonnet":"A Polish song structured as a drunken tutorial — an older man at a birthday party teaches a younger one how to drink properly: keep vodka cold, eat fatty food, don't come in groups if you want real conversation, and accept that the morning will be brutal regardless.","src":"haiku","fable":"A drinking manual staged as a party toast: a kid tries to raise a glass at his boss's fiftieth and an old-timer shoves him aside to deliver, deadpan, the real wisdom of alcohol, chapter and verse on which vodka to buy cold, how much to pour, and when to stop before you look soft. The comedy of the rules keeps circling their own outcome, so the practical advice quietly becomes an inventory of the damage waiting at the end of the night. That damage arrives in the chorus as a montage with no commentary at all: a split brow and stitches, the ambulance, torn clothes, two strangers whose names blur, and the waking hangover where the money and the girls have both vanished. What sits underneath the swagger is a portrait of drinking as autopilot, a ritual passed down like a trade whose only real product is the erased morning after.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"drinking rituals passed down as a trade whose only product is erasure","built":"the practical rules keep circling their own outcome until the chorus catalogues the damage without commentary"},
"pro8l3m~tori-black":{"haiku":"Explicit sexual braggadocio and hedonism dominate this Polish rap; the narrator celebrates carnal excess, substance abuse, wealth contrast, and unflinching appetites without moral restraint or social pretense.","opus":"Explicit Polish rap that stages porn-star fantasy against class resentment, hedonism and appetite crashing into the divide between luxury life and a rougher, criminal reality. Bravado masking the same exhausted stress underneath.","sonnet":"An explicit boast track built around graphic sexual conquest, contrasting the narrator's streetwise, hustler lifestyle (schemes, stress, rap grind) against the polished luxury world he looks down on.","src":"haiku","fable":"The braggadocio splits in two: the sex boasts run through the verses like an unbroken hydraulic pressure, while the hook toggles line by line between a moneyed, discreet world of luxury labels and champagne and the duo's own reality of scams, suspended sentences, and grinding stress. Underneath the porn-title swagger and the actress's name is a tell — the man who can seduce anyone admits he never bothered to keep her number, and the conquest that reads as appetite is really a refusal of intimacy dressed as dominance. The wealth on the other side of that hook is the thing he can only counterfeit, so the performance of endless want stands in for what he can't actually hold.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["love & desire","money & the street","shame & repression"],"means":"conquest as a refusal of intimacy wearing the mask of dominance","built":"the hook toggles between a moneyed world and the duo's scams, making wealth the thing they can only counterfeit"},
"pro8l3m~usb":{"haiku":"A person reviews photos on a USB drive of a past filled with excess and waste, recognizing repetition and failed change. The song mourns archived evidence of stagnation and loss.","opus":"A devastating breakup autopsy, the narrator snorting and burning away everything that connected two people, lying naked beside her without touching as tears cleanse them both, trading dollars for rubles one to one. He cycles through vices and hollow promises to change, ending wrecked around a tree in his BMW, left only with photos on a USB stick. Pro8l3m, the Polish duo, contrast their debauched weekends against her ordinary world, self-destruction rendered with brutal, exhausted clarity.","sonnet":"The wreckage after a relationship ends in a single night of deliberate self-destruction — burning notes, drinking through it, lying naked without touching. The speaker moves between that scene and a portrait of his crew's lifestyle — cocaine and cars, chaotic weekends — and lands finally on a drawer of old photographs, the only thing left that makes the past feel real.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~v": {"haiku":"Two ordinary, unremarkable people live quiet lives—him orderly and loyal, her normal and ambitious—both insignificant to the wider world.","opus":"The Polish duo build a quietly devastating portrait of the utterly average man, a loyal office worker with two palms and no vices, nobody anyone would ask about. A chorus insisting that presence, small gestures, and sacrifice all matter reframes his invisibility as a private kind of significance, later mirrored by an equally ordinary woman whose ambitions were eaten by years. Pro8l3m locate dignity precisely in the unremarkable.","sonnet":"Two parallel portraits of unremarkable urban lives — a meticulously ordinary man with no addictions, no drama, and no one who would miss him; and a woman whose ambitions were quietly eaten by circumstance. The refrain insists, with mounting urgency, that each invisible person carries weight, that their presence and choices have meaning no matter how unseen.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two exhaustively ordinary lives — a model office worker loyal to one woman, an aging fan of Scorsese and De Palma; then a naturally pretty woman whose dreams the years ate, alone now, most normal of all while we pass by unaware — are inventoried in the flat, unremarkable detail of people nobody would ever ask about, nobody who could believe in his own private jihad. Against that catalogue of nothingness the refrain hammers a single word, znaczenie — it matters: that you are here matters, your small gesture matters, your sacrifice matters. The movement is the whole read: the chorus starts as tender consolation to the invisible person, then in the final verse radicalizes into bound hands, a bleeding heart, swollen eyes, dried blood, taken bullets, ostracism and posthumous meaning — the ordinary citizen reimagined not as a nobody but as fuel for an uprising, the wake-the-beasts turn the Fight Club frame demands. The closing line is the argument's hinge: this is not destiny but our own choices — meaning is not handed down, it is seized by the exact people the song spent two verses proving no one notices.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"invisible ordinary lives recast as fuel for collective uprising","built":"the closing hinge swaps destiny for chosen agency in one line"},
"pro8l3m~v8":{"fable":"A speed-run through Warsaw at 3 a.m., narrated so fast the city blurs into a single unbroken feed of dealing, driving, and stimulant burn, where the highway lane and the racing heartbeat become the same thing. The hook admits the trick under the swagger: the man floors the gas precisely because his mind is slipping, so the velocity isn't freedom but a way of outrunning a system of debts, karma, and comedowns that keeps circling back to collect. The second verse tightens this into a portrait of a life measured in courts, flights, lawsuits and the twitch of a numbed nose — money made all year only to vanish, brand names and celebrity billboards flickering past as hollow scenery. Underneath the flex, it reads as a hymn to velocity as avoidance: keep moving, keep consuming, refuse the slow ballad, because the moment you stop the reckoning is already waiting.","fableDeep":"The chorus is a dashboard readout of the whole ethos: 'linia szybka, bo powoli mi odbija palma' — the line is fast because I'm slowly losing my mind — a couplet that prices cocaine as self-medication for the very madness it causes, speed treating the damage speed did. The fatalist ledger runs alongside: 'do kogoś wraca dług, do kogoś karma' (a debt comes back to someone, karma to someone else) watches consequences land on other drivers while the narrator holds the 'skrajny pas' — the outside lane — and refuses to listen to ballads, sentiment banned like a slow vehicle. The verses run on brand-name realism: no excise stamp on the bottle, the Yanosik radar app as navigation through illegality, choosing among women 'jak na Ceneo' — like comparison shopping. One glance holds the class panorama at highway speed: six guys rotting in an old Nexia under a Messi billboard, misery and glamour in a single frame. It is about acceleration as a philosophy — outrun the comedown, the debt, and the karma, and never slow down into reflection.","haiku":"A Polish-language rap chronicling substance abuse, fast cars, and reckless hedonism. The speaker boasts about speed, drug use, and a lifestyle of excess without consequence.","opus":"A Polish rap set in a world of fast driving, drugs, money, and nightlife, boasting about racing in the outside lane, a good-looking woman, debts and karma catching up with others, and reckless indulgence.","sonnet":"A rapid-fire ride through Warsaw nightlife — drugs, women, police patrols, fast cars, and money — delivered with the detached bravado of someone accelerating through the city while everything blurs past.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["freedom & escape","addiction & self-destruction","money & the street","night & the city"],"means":"flooring the gas because stopping lets the reckoning catch up","built":"the hook admits the trick: velocity is avoidance, not freedom"},
"pro8l3m~vanitas":{"fable":"The read is one long deferral: the speaker is a man reciting an endless to-do list of experiences, possessions, thrills and provocations, each item framed as a test he must pass before he can finally turn to himself — and every verse ends on that same promised later that never arrives. The still-life logic sits underneath: the Rolex meant to slow time, the mirror that lets him look down on others, the borrowed mask worn just to feel like someone else are all vanity-emblems, objects that measure a life while it drains away. What the chorus sells as discipline and a clear hierarchy of priorities is the exact opposite — a will so busy proving itself against every stimulus that the self it keeps postponing is the one thing never reached. The mortality is quiet but total: he will do all of it, and only then, tomorrow, live.","fableDeep":"The title announces the genre — vanitas, the still life stacked with luxuries and a skull — and the structure paints one in list form. The frame stanza is serene self-assessment: discipline, willpower, a hierarchy of things, gratification deferred to a tomorrow that keeps repeating. The verses then invert agency: every line is a noun phrase plus must — women who must find out how impressive he is, money that must find out how wasteful, cars that must earn him license points, mirrors that must teach him to look down on people more coldly. He never acts; his distractions require him. The second list darkens without changing the grammar — court verdicts, injectables, perfumes bought to mask another woman's scent and head off a war, religions audited for whichever hell is least burdensome — vanity shading into damage control. Then the lyric cuts off mid-word at a red light, deferral enacted formally: the sentence, like the self-realization, never finishes. Thesis: the skull in this still life is the unfinished sentence.","haiku":"A speaker postpones self-improvement indefinitely, chasing endless distractions and status symbols while avoiding genuine growth. The song catalogs vanity projects and compulsive pursuits that prevent change.","opus":"A brilliant inventory of endless deferral, the addict's self-mythology of discipline forever postponing self-care behind a mountain of things to prove, buy, and consume. The title names it: vanity, and a life spent on everything but living.","sonnet":"A Polish hip-hop meditation on chronic procrastination and vanity — the narrator claims to be disciplined and goal-oriented but keeps filling his time with status games, drugs, women, fights, and consumer goods, promising to \"work on himself\" perpetually tomorrow.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["money & the street","death & grief","artifice & performance"],"means":"a self so busy proving itself that it never arrives at itself","built":"every verse ends on the same promised later that never comes"},
"pro8l3m~vhs":{"haiku":"Casual hedonism and sexual conquest recorded on tape as memory. The speaker recounts nights of drugs, sex, and empty pleasure, treating intimate moments as low-quality footage to revisit.","opus":"A cynical, boastful account of drug-and-alcohol-fueled threesomes with groupies, framed as a transaction (originals cost money, three in the bed is the max); the deeper sting is the narrator secretly filming the women on VHS and later HD, uneasy and lonely afterward but treating the recordings as trophies, so the swagger masks predatory contempt.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper recounts a messy two-woman sexual encounter in an apartment, narrated with deadpan humor — the threesome capped at three, a neighbor mistaking the noise for singing, a condom that would've broken anyway, and the sheepish afterthought of having recorded the whole thing on his phone.","src":"haiku","fable":"PRO8L3M build the whole track as a swaggering hookup boast so that the turn lands as a gut-punch: for two verses Oskar plays the self-deprecating rake (too old for this, condom would burst anyway, sighing over his ex's photo on the wall), and the persona is deliberately likeable, which is the trap. The real subject, lurking in plain sight among the refrain’s blank tapes, surfaces in the last couplet of each verse, phrased as a sheepish afterthought that the women surely won't mind that he taped them, verse one on old low quality, verse two in HD on his phone. That escalation of format is the song's actual argument: the seduction was always secondary to the recording, and the title VHS names the trophy, not the night. The recurring głupio czuję, the awkward guilt that only arrives once they've left, reads not as remorse but as the faint conscience of a predator who films first and half-notices the wrong of it after, so the sleaze is the mask and the covert camera is what's underneath it.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance","party & hedonism"],"means":"the recording device exposed as the real object of desire","built":"format escalation from VHS to HD maps the predator's escalation"},
"pro8l3m~vi-katastrofa":{"haiku":"Modern life is hollow consumption and illusion where eight people rule billions without water; the narrator is complicit in this world's toxicity and their own moral collapse, unable or unwilling to change.","opus":"The Polish duo turns apocalyptic and confessional, a narrator beneath a black sun and acid rain naming himself the universe's greatest criminal, his own god and executioner. He harms people, chases only his crown, grieves a lost love and a poisoned earth where eight men rule and billions lack water. The mantra that sadness lasts forever slowly loosens toward a fragile maybe otherwise.","sonnet":"A man surveying his own moral wreckage — cruelty, greed, a lost relationship remembered in a lakeside flash — arrives slowly at the possibility that the sadness need not be permanent. Eight people control the world, humanity drowns in its own toxins, yet somewhere beneath the self-loathing he asks if it might be time to change.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~w-domach-z-betonu":{"haiku":"Childhood memories in concrete housing blocks: birthday parties, play, family meals, modest toys. The speaker captures a specific era and place through a child's eyes.","opus":"A heartbreaking child's-eye account of growing up in concrete tower blocks, turning five, running around naked, loving Russian pierogi and cartoons, while a mother loses her hair and dies, glimpsed only through the innocent detail a child fixes on instead of grief. The refrain states plainly that in concrete houses there is no free love. Pro8l3m, the Polish duo, render Polish working-class childhood with unbearable tenderness, a father lifting the boy and saying this is no place for tears.","sonnet":"A childhood told in two voices across time — the five-year-old who wants to run naked and eat hot dogs and ride his bike without hands, then the same child after his mother is gone, moving through relatives and toys and prayers alone in concrete housing blocks. Love exists in these homes but it is rationed, and the father's lesson is that there's no place for crying here.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~we-made-it":{"haiku":"Material triumph and hedonistic celebration coexist with hollow emptiness, celebrating arrival through excess.","opus":"A triumph anthem framed by an Alan Watts sermon on faith versus belief, then flipped into a self-made toast. Against long odds nobody understood, the crew pours Martini, lights a cigarette, and plays their own rap at sunrise. The chanted binaries, Lambo or Phantom, top or bottom, cast success as an all-or-nothing gamble they finally won.","sonnet":"A theological preamble dissolves into Polish street-level celebration — the Alan Watts excerpt on sin as \"missing the point\" and faith versus belief gives way to \"we made it\" toasted with Martini and YouTube rap until sunrise. The Polish chorus (Lambo or Phantom, drunk or high, hardcore or smooth, luck or nothing) maps success as radical either/or: whatever it took, they got here.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~widmo":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts unanswerable existential questions about a chaotic world and his own insignificance, repeatedly admitting he has no answers while questioning why the world is broken.","opus":"A stark confession of having no answers, framed by a sampled voice denying any easy apocalypse, the narrator asking why the world is off its axis, why he grinds, why love is fleeting, and admitting he's nobody who'll end up in a grave like everyone else. It skewers those who pretend the world is at their feet when they're just as empty. PRO8L3M, the Polish duo Oskar and Steez83, sit in existential doubt here, the refrain of unanswered questions the whole bleak point of it.","sonnet":"An admission of total uncertainty — why the world is broken, why the speaker keeps going, why betrayal leaves marks, why nobody with any power actually has answers. The spoken-word bookends suggest apocalypse is a fantasy; the harder truth is that we keep living without explanations.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~wieczna-gra":{"haiku":"Cyclical time and mortality anchor wisdom as the speaker navigates karma and legacy, recognizing individual ego dissolves into perpetual continuation through generations.","opus":"Death accepted as a natural process that keeps balance, the self dissolving into a larger organism carried on by future generations. Immortality lives not in the individual ego but in the descendants and the whole; the eternal game is making peace with the return home.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality framed as acceptance: the speaker began as a soldier of war now emptied of that identity, and pivots to a philosophical view — death is not the end of being, only of ego; future generations carry the continuation; life is a small number of steps forward, and death is simply coming home.","src":"haiku"},
"pro8l3m~witamy-witamy-witamy": {"haiku":"A reckless night of partying, drugs, and hedonism where money is spent freely, moral codes ignored, and the morning brings only emptiness.","opus":"A raucous party anthem of drunken excess, money that won't stick, women wanting action and lads chasing thrills, tumbling through joints, pills, Ferraris downgraded to jeeps and cops on the horizon. Opening with Fight Club's homework to start a fight with a stranger, it's pure hedonic chaos delivered in machine-gun ad-libs. PRO8L3M, the Polish duo Oskar and Steez83, lean fully into the debauched club-night mode, the welcome repeated like a barker's cry into the mayhem.","sonnet":"A night out on the estate rendered as a blur of alcohol, card games, weed, and borrowed money — chaotic, funny, self-aware. The chorus cheerfully notes that money vanishes and everyone wants sensation, and by the end the crew decides the venue is too wrecked to stay in.","src":"haiku","fable":"A triple-barrelled welcome ushers you into a night that is really a Fight Club sermon in disguise: the Tyler Durden sample tasking you to pick a fight with a stranger sets the frame, and the bridge states the thesis flat — the money never sticks, the bodies just want a jolt. The first verse is deliberately a rhyme-drunk avalanche, one slurred internal rhyme toppling into the next (birds are pelicans, flowers are tulips) so that the form itself performs intoxication — sense dissolving on purpose, because the point is the numbness, not the wit. Underneath the flex the ledger keeps coming up empty — nothing in the account, cash that vanishes and nobody knows where — so the swagger reads as the mask over Durden's exact diagnosis: consumption that buys nothing, appetite with no object. The second verse sobers only to relapse on schedule, Monday's vow to quit undone by Tuesday's drink, and it escalates the register toward the apocalyptic joke of making two Hiroshimas of a bad venue — the self-destruction that was the sample's actual homework all along. What welcomes you in, three times over, is not the party but the void it's papering over.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","party & hedonism","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the welcome ushers you into the void the party is papering over","built":"first verse performs intoxication in form as slurred internal rhymes dissolve sense on purpose"},
"pro8l3m~world-wide-web":{"haiku":"Internet infrastructure collapses and society descends into chaos; the narrator acknowledges their own role in the catastrophic systems failure.","opus":"Opening with a warning that the internet is a fragile trust-based system vulnerable to catastrophic attack, the narrator accidentally triggers exactly that—a global blackout of phones, TV, power, transport, banking—and wanders a city descending into panic, riots, and martial law; the deeper read is societal collapse when the digital scaffolding fails, told with dark irony as the man who caused it hides his guilt.","sonnet":"A Polish song imagining what happens when the internet suddenly dies — society immediately fractures into traffic jams, cashless panic, army convoys, stock market collapse, riots, and water shortages, while the narrator quietly admits he may have accidentally caused all of it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages the internet's collapse as a first-person disaster: an idle click by an ordinary user accidentally kills all connectivity, and the two verses walk day one and day two of a city unravelling without it. The power is in accumulation of mundane consequence, one deadpan line after another: dead traffic lights and gridlock, cash-only shops, sirens and army escorts, bread and medicine running out, a body pulled from rubble while some fool recites scripture, the narrator too scared to admit he caused it all lest the neighbours beat him. Underneath the comic detachment sits a real thesis lifted from the spoken intro: a civilisation built on a fragile system of trust has scaled itself far past what that system can bear, so the failure is not a hack but a structural inevitability. The refrain seals it with a shrug of impotence, no signal, no forum to ask what to do, and the guilty parties never even rising from their table.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4d","themes":["politics & society","comedy & wordplay","surveillance & control"],"means":"collapse framed as structural inevitability rather than sabotage","built":"deadpan mundane consequence piled line by line until the refrain: no signal, no forum to ask what to do"},
"pro8l3m~zadzwo-do-mnie":{"haiku":"A call reaching out across distance; connection attempted through communication despite obstacles.","opus":"A neon club-night seduction thick with brand names, the narrator dazzled by a woman he meets at a strip club, ferrying her home for sex, only to have her tell him she's a sex worker and ask how he feels about it, which he insists doesn't bother him at all. The coda turns queasy with love as a curse. PRO8L3M deliver this glossy, self-aware romance in dense Polish rap, her whispered call-me hanging over everything.","sonnet":"An encounter at a strip club that keeps complicating itself — the speaker is instantly drawn to a woman who turns out to be a sex worker, and the revelation does nothing to reduce his feelings, which he then has to argue with himself about. The closing question is whether the way the world looks at her should make him love her less, and he refuses to let it.","src":"haiku"},
"probot~shake-your-blood":{"haiku":"Rock and roll creates intensity and ecstasy, driving people toward recklessness and breaking hearts.","opus":"Dave Grohl's metal side project recruits Motorhead's Lemmy, whose grizzled attitude soaks this adrenaline-fueled ode to rock as pure survival. There's nothing buried to decode: escape from a wretched existence comes from the stage, the roaring crowd and the racket. The blunt orders to let loose celebrate sheer volume and abandon as reward enough.","sonnet":"A straightforward hymn to rock-and-roll as salvation: the stage, the crowd, the volume as the only honest way to feel alive, win respect, and break hearts. Ambition and hedonism fused into a single rallying cry delivered from the front of a sweating room.","src":"haiku"},
"processor~royal-leash":{"haiku":"Enslavement to royalty or authority, subjugation and control.","opus":"A dark, submissive plea built around the paradox of craving total self-destruction as if it were salvation. The narrator begs to be unleashed while confessing total obedience to a dazzling, dangerous other who could reduce them to ashes. Devotion here is annihilating, the leash both bondage and identity, longing intertwined with the certainty of being destroyed by what one worships.","sonnet":"A speaker demanding to be unleashed from someone whose brightness destroys them and whose deception calls them home — \"one step away from turning to ashes.\" The pleading is minimal, almost stripped to syntax: \"unleash me, you owe me sincere.\"","src":"haiku"},
"processor~your-madness":{"haiku":"Madness crystallizes through the beloved's presence, where pretense dissolves into raw, infectious irrationality. The speaker recognizes and names the madness they've always inhabited.","opus":"Loving someone whose instability the singer saw plainly written on their face, a place that felt like life to them and only a house to him. He recognized the sickness for what it was even as they insisted on pretending.","sonnet":"A speaker witnesses a lover's fragile euphoria in a place that felt like dying (in the good sense) — a special house that was 'just childish', a face that revealed everything — and watches as that ecstatic madness surfaces and recedes, saying simply 'I saw it, of your madness' as both witness and keeper.","src":"haiku"},
"prod-gy~voodoo-people-pendulum-remix":{"haiku":"Voodoo practitioners operate outside moral constraint, enacting forbidden acts and wielding magical power that ordinary people deny themselves.","opus":"A taunting chant about outsiders who do the forbidden thing everyone else flinches from, magic folk cast as thrilling and slightly dangerous. It's a celebration of transgression as identity. The source is The Prodigy's rave classic, here rebuilt by Pendulum, whose drum and bass firepower turns the original's breakbeat menace into something faster and heavier, one dance-music generation saluting the one that raised it.","sonnet":"A celebration of those who do what ordinary people won't dare — the voodoo practitioners invoked here are figures of transgression and magic, their power defined entirely by contrast with the timid. The chant is an invocation more than a description, calling forward a kind of people who operate outside the normal limits of courage or fear.","src":"haiku"},
"professor-elemental~i-m-british":{"haiku":"British identity parodies itself through exaggerated politeness and self-deprecation, simultaneously celebrating and satirising cultural stereotypes and imperial legacy.","opus":"A comic self-deprecating anatomy of Englishness, apology, sarcasm, repression and faded empire all worn as identity; the humor darkens into genuine reckoning as it apologizes for the ugly things Britain exported to the world.","sonnet":"A comedic, self-aware catalogue of British cultural identity — apologetic by default, cynical by temperament, eccentric by nature — culminating in a mock-sincere apology on behalf of Britain for its various cultural exports and historical crimes.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~888":{"haiku":"The earth whispers while spiritual ascension calls. Truth hides in matrix noise but angels and salvation burn inside.","opus":"A spiritual awakening framed as crossing a divine threshold, trusting the earth and waves over the lies of the news and a matrix-like world, resisting the tempting devil; the angel number 888 signals abundance and alignment.","sonnet":"A song about spiritual awakening and resisting corruption — the narrator feels pressure but also aliveness and inspiration, believes the media is a matrix of lies, senses demonic temptation but refuses it, and reads the angel number 888 as confirmation they are on a threshold of divine alignment.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~all-in-forever":{"haiku":"Betrayal and abandonment by close allies leave lasting trauma; the speaker questions loyalty while demanding reciprocal commitment.","opus":"Betrayal by someone who lied and took a piece of the singer while he stayed all in, leaving him pleading not to be abandoned and bracing for the certainty that they never really had his back.","sonnet":"Someone has been lied to and drained — \"you took a part of me, critically endangering my mind\" — and though the speaker pleads not to be left out, they know the person who hurt them was never going to be there.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~alpha-nine":{"haiku":"Intrusive thoughts intensify into unbearable noise; the speaker battles an internal voice that grows louder while isolation and alienation deepen the divide.","opus":"Intrusive thoughts that refuse to quiet, medicated and screamed at to shut off, driving the narrator to wall himself off from everyone. The chasm between the man he was meant to be and reality only cranks up the inner voice.","sonnet":"About someone — possibly a specific person, possibly an alter ego — who cannot quiet his own mind and medicates to cope, talking to himself and begging for his thoughts to stop. A friendship has broken down and left him alone with an internal voice that gets louder and louder; the underlying grief is for who he thought he could have been versus the reality of a mind that will not rest.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~animo":{"haiku":"A lost figure seeks dignity and wholeness while caught between detachment and feeling. The world offers no pride, only bitterness and absence.","opus":"Someone fleeing a threatening situation and a menacing figure, craving a quiet life and one steadying presence, yet unable to feel pride or conviction after getting caught on the wrong side of things.","sonnet":"A song about escaping a toxic situation and rebuilding a sense of self — the narrator has left behind someone dangerous who won't be coming back, seeks a quiet life and simple sunlight, and wrestles with feeling neither reckless nor proud, caught on the other side of a loss they're slowly recovering from.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~artifice":{"haiku":"Manufactured identity and systemic control dissolve the authentic self—created personas collapse into awareness that nothing built to serve others' designs can ever feel real.","opus":"Distrust of a manufactured reality that turns people artificial and strips away what was meant to be ours. The apocalyptic imagery frames modern fakeness as a slow fall into empty space.","sonnet":"A song about suspicion toward those who manufacture fake people and false realities — the speaker rejects being shaped by outside forces and sees the whole fabricated system as a nightmare walking toward cosmic collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~delay":{"haiku":"Postponement and temporal suspension, the speaker caught in waiting and deferment.","opus":"A hazy, dreamlike love song where closeness warps perception — skies stirring to life, landscapes rearranging, two people looking out through each other's eyes and the beloved seeming almost divine. Under the bliss runs an undertow of loss, the fear of a lover who won't return, shadowing the euphoria with impermanence.","sonnet":"A love song suspended in bloom — delay the time, consuming all in perfection — the natural world rearranges around a tenderness the speaker doesn't want to lose, until a parenthetical fracture — lost and never coming back — reveals the whole piece as grief dressed in longing.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~dope":{"haiku":"Engulfment in toxic relationship that erases personal identity, becoming nothing without the other while drowning in their apathy.","opus":"A raw purge about dissolving inside another person's cold indifference. Reaching for astrophysics, the narrator likens the partner to a collapsing star whose pull drags him past any return into her apathy, matching her flame with his own until he's a stranger to himself. The refrain flings the self-loathing outward, naming her as the enemy he has to flee.","sonnet":"A screaming dissolution — the narrator loses their grip on reality and identity inside someone else's \"gravitational pull,\" described as a black hole whose event horizon makes return impossible. The apathy of the other person is the gravitational source; the narrator's self-erasure is the consequence.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~glitch-theory":{"haiku":"Malfunction and system error as philosophical framework, the speaker understanding existence through breakdown.","opus":"A song about a relationship as a malfunctioning simulation, where one person endlessly complains yet never changes. The narrator, desperate to have his pain lifted and his words forgotten, feels reality glitching and dissolving around him. Naming the other as living a lie, it treats emotional entrapment as a game whose code neither of them can fix.","sonnet":"A simulation metaphor for avoidance and mutual damage — you're always down to complain but never adjust, and the narrator is falling away into a game that takes the pain with it — the plea is to be forgotten and forgiven, and the charge living a lie hangs over both of them.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~hypocrite":{"haiku":"Broken trust and self-preservation conflict as the speaker demands authenticity while their own connection dissolves. Fakeness breeds contempt and the relationship dies from mutual suffocation.","opus":"Contempt for a two-faced person who wrecks and fakes everything, tangled with a desperate plea to be shown how to come back to life and saved from a life that is quietly killing him.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who fakes everything, breaks things, and leaves the speaker in fear — the demand is to be taught how to feel alive again and saved from a life that is killing them, while the other's hypocrisy is named and condemned outright.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~identify":{"haiku":"Despair about losing sanity and being hated while clinging to the belief that life is a gift worth persisting through.","opus":"A widescreen sunset of colored sky and rolling surf gives way to quiet dread, a mind unraveling against a world it's told has gone mad. The speaker concedes he's losing his grip yet holds fast to one stubborn conviction, that life is still a gift worth pushing through. That spark of resolve, set against his paranoia and disbelief, anchors the whole song.","sonnet":"A mind unravelling against a backdrop of coastal beauty. Disconnected from the news cycle and from the people around him, the speaker oscillates between paranoia and defiant indifference, eventually landing on a bare, stubborn conviction that life is worth persisting through.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~illusion":{"haiku":"Attraction and illusion collapse into disorientation where the speaker cannot distinguish dream from waking. The beloved becomes titan, pulling the speaker into tidal merger.","opus":"Rage at being frozen under someone's gaze curdles into a reckless flight into the night with them, collision and surrender framed as the only way out. The bravado about their false confidence gives way to escape as the shared answer.","sonnet":"A speaker who resents being stared at frozen confronts someone they find falsely elusive and confident, then pivots to an intense romantic declaration: the light of your eyes turns the tide of my mind, we'll drive away from here together; the tension between hostility and surrender is never resolved, the illusion of the title hovering over both.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~metamorphosis":{"haiku":"The song expresses anxiety and emotional transformation through another's gravity. The narrator wants to fight inner turmoil but is pulled into drift by an incomprehensible presence.","opus":"A song about anxiety and dissociation, the narrator insisting his terror is imaginary yet unable to flee what his own mind conjures, begging to be extracted from it, swinging between wanting to fight and pleading to be left alone and, finally, heard. A frail hope of drifting alongside someone offers brief relief. Profiler are a British metalcore band, and this pairs their clean-and-scream dynamics with a raw account of mental illness and the hunger to be listened to.","sonnet":"Anxiety and isolation pressing outward into a plea to be heard — the speaker knows they'll disappoint whoever comes close, but can't stop needing someone to look past the noise and actually listen. The pull of another person's gaze is gravitational, and the only thing asked is silence from the other side, long enough to finally speak.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~operator":{"haiku":"Dependency masquerades as freedom in the digital age: wired into systems designed to trap, yet clinging to connection even as autonomy slips away.","opus":"Disorientation inside a digital, automated world where devotion gets aimed at a machine or an operator. The clinging refrain of needing someone reads as dependence on the very system that numbs you.","sonnet":"A song about digital dependency and emotional codependency becoming confused — the speaker feels blindly free in a wired world but keeps returning to the refrain that they cannot live without someone or something, while being told to stop freaking out.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~revert":{"haiku":"The song critiques consumerism and imposed addiction. Systems deliberately create craving; reconnecting to earth beneath modernity's wiring is the only escape.","opus":"Profiler, a British metalcore band, weld heavy groove to an anti-consumerist protest, raging at a system that manufactures cravings, sells you the unnecessary, and keeps people divided, insisting the appetite won't end until humanity reconnects with the natural world. Beneath the polemic runs private exhaustion, a mind sickened by pain and racing with restless, wired thoughts.","sonnet":"A two-front attack on consumerism and personal dissolution — systems engineered to addict and divide are indicted alongside the speaker's own manic, wired inner life. The cure proposed is the same for both: reconnect to the earth beneath you, stop asking what it all means, and start by shutting up about it.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~shadow":{"haiku":"Internal demons and self-sabotage war with consciousness, as the shadow self demands attention and provokes destructive reactions.","opus":"An externalized argument with your own worst impulses — the small, ruined voice that criticizes, lies, and craves attention treated as a taunting stranger. Under the aggression is the real want: to feel like enough, and to answer self-loathing with empathy instead of another spiral.","sonnet":"About confronting a hostile inner critic or an antagonistic person who mirrors the narrator's own insecurities — after a long build of aggressive posturing, the song breaks open into a quiet confession: all the narrator ever wanted was to feel like enough and to receive empathy, not apathy.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~the-living-receiver":{"haiku":"Alienation and social toxicity isolate the speaker in digital void where authenticity dissolves into performance and suffocation.","opus":"A dissociated address to someone lost in a virtual void, squandering themselves on hollow companions and refusing to admit they're only dreaming. Running underneath is the singer's own dread of turning into the person who fakes it all and never breaks through.","sonnet":"In a meaningless digital void, the \"living receiver\" is urged to reject the dream and face reality, but is instead surrounded by fake friends who drain them — the outside world remains terrifying and the inner world offers no anchor either.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~to-utopia":{"haiku":"Dystopian illusion masks self-deception as the speaker walks through constructed reality while identity fragments.","opus":"Chasing utopia as an obvious delusion, the skyline itself reads as fake, so the way forward becomes waving goodbye to one's own life. Testing every insecurity that makes up the self, the song sits between a promised paradise and the dystopia you die in regardless.","sonnet":"The gap between promised utopia and experienced dystopia — faded identity, fake states, walls closing in — has the speaker waving goodbye to their own life while being told they have infinite potential they cannot access.","src":"haiku"},
"profiler~zero":{"haiku":"Status and self-worth collapse into hollow performance as the speaker dismantles heroic personas to reveal the emptiness beneath, trading admiration for authentic freedom.","opus":"A confrontation with someone who postures as a hero but reveals himself as a zero, set against the narrator's urge to dive into open water and escape the hate. Contempt for a fraud met with a will to break free.","sonnet":"A confident dismissal of someone who talks like a hero but performs like a zero: the speaker watches their back, calls out the disconnect between posture and reality, then escapes into open ocean — free and untouched — before telling the other to take their hate elsewhere.","src":"haiku"},
"project-86~ultraviolent":{"haiku":"Hypnotic visual assault forces subjugation and passive obedience through overwhelming sensory control.","opus":"A hard-rock nightmare about media hypnosis and forced watching. Held with his eyes pried open in paralysis, the narrator can't turn from a screen searing warped pictures into him, a terror-figure mounted on dead steeds riding toward dominion. It plays as a warning against handing the mind over to the addictive glow of the feed and the wreckage it transmits.","sonnet":"A victim of total media-conditioning describes having his eyelids forced open — a direct Clockwork Orange image — until the screen's images are burned into his irises. A female figure rises from static to dominion, and the subject is Pavlov-trained: \"ring the bell and I will obey.\" The self is replaced by the display.","src":"haiku"},
"proletaryat~9-kul":{"haiku":"Bullets provide certainty in an uncertain world; short-lived satisfaction and hollow guarantees define human condition.","opus":"From a veteran Polish protest-rock band, this counts nine bullets as instruments of certainty and verdict. Each carries a short, lethal message, and the narrator, emptied of words and options, embraces violence as the price paid for the state of his soul. The chilling twist is ambition, wanting the front pages, making destruction a bid for notoriety.","sonnet":"Nine bullets, each carrying a short sentence — \"a bullet is a verdict, a bullet to be certain\" — and the speaker who no longer has words or strength finds a new purpose forming through the compression of that certainty: the bullet's brief content is the only message left when everything else is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"proletaryat~by-czy-by":{"haiku":"The soul persists as an unpurchasable commodity while systems attempt to commodify every aspect of existence. Individual integrity remains worth defending despite relentless pressure to submit, sell, and compromise for material gain.","opus":"A warning that you can never truly own your own soul, for a tempter shadows every step offering everything in exchange for lifelong servitude. Guard what is inside you and avoid the crowded roads where ambition sells you out.","sonnet":"The tension between wanting to be truly oneself and the forces that inevitably claim ownership of a person's soul — someone waits in every shadow to seduce you with promises of everything, and in chasing more you trample others and finally sell yourself entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"proletaryat~modlitwa":{"haiku":"Suffering and transformation intertwine as the narrator seeks redemption from their own shadow and the pain they've inflicted. Through persistent struggle and self-confrontation, they attempt to rebuild life and meaning from ruins.","opus":"A prayer at the edge of death begging for one more day and the strength to understand the real world and change. Fallen in order to rise, the narrator pleads to bury his old self and face a new dawn with hope.","sonnet":"A supplication for one more day of strength and genuine understanding, spoken by someone who has fallen but refuses to stay down — burying the old shadow, mending a torn life, and asking only to be believed capable of change.","src":"haiku"},
"properties-of-nature~mind-goblin":{"haiku":"Vanity and self-reflection dissolve into cosmic indifference; the speaker dances between personal obsession and the realization that nothing matters.","opus":"A sprawling trip through avoidance, vanity and dissolving ego, the mirror reflection reaching out as the self dances toward oblivion. Under nihilistic hedonism runs the panicked question of whether any of it was worth it as everything turns to dust.","sonnet":"Hiding in the backseat, negotiating with emotional delinquencies that others call progress, the speaker cycles between anxiety and a surreal psychedelic vision where the sun leans down like a mother, time crunches underfoot, and home dissolves into dust — dancing in vanity to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"prospective~against-all-odds":{"haiku":"Speaking an unpopular truth isolates the narrator; they struggle with self-destruction but choose to break free and find the light.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with self-loathing and burnout, drinking one's own tears while fear turns the blood dark. The narrator's escape is framed as running toward the sun, a hard-won survival wrung from nights lost to Jack Daniel's and a body unsure whether it is sleeping or dead. Being the only one who spoke the truth becomes both isolation and the reason to keep running.","sonnet":"Survival after self-rejection. Someone who once turned against himself — drinking, dissociating, unable to tell waking from oblivion — emerges battered but standing, forced out of his circle by telling the truth, now running alone toward whatever light remains.","src":"haiku"},
"prospective~alone-i-stand":{"haiku":"A soldier feels isolated and violated, forced to commit violence for abstract ideals while carrying unbearable emotional weight alone.","opus":"This melodic metalcore band render war from a conscript's helpless perspective, a young soldier bound to obey orders he can't refuse in a fight he believes serves only annihilation. Recollection of ordinary life just before the shells fell yields to numb awareness of killing with no way out, and the loneliness of standing isolated inside a shared agony.","sonnet":"A soldier narrates the psychic cost of combat — drafted into a war that strips identity and forces acts against conscience. Loyalty to family and a vague idea of humanity keeps him fighting even as his lungs give out and moral corruption sets in. Isolation is total; the standpoint is one person who cannot walk away.","src":"haiku"},
"prospective~haunt-me":{"haiku":"Unresolved trauma manifests as haunting presence; the speaker cannot escape words that destroyed them or the person who wielded them.","opus":"Being haunted by someone whose words wrecked the narrator, swinging between wanting them gone and being unable to shake them. Deep self-hatred bleeds through — convinced he'd be better by himself, better not alive at all, dying just to ask whether they're satisfied now.","sonnet":"Someone destroyed by another person's words and cruelty imagines a world free of them — but no matter how far they run, that person still haunts them; the song ends with the narrator declaring they are dead now, asking if that's what the other person wanted.","src":"haiku"},
"protest-the-hero~all-hands":{"haiku":"Resignation and fatalism replace hope; the speaker watches their metaphorical train approach, resigned to a collision that feels inevitable and almost welcome.","opus":"A man dreaming of a life he cannot afford senses an oncoming train that reads equally as deliverance and death. The relief in its crash makes it about welcoming an inevitable end that finally quiets his doubts, leaving him unrecognizable in the aftermath.","sonnet":"Standing on a cold dock dreaming of a life he cannot afford, the speaker waits for his train — a metaphor for something transformative and devastating — and when it finally arrives as a \"black wave,\" it crashes into him and leaves him a struggling mass painted black, unrecognisable as animal or human.","src":"haiku"},
"protest-the-hero~bloodmeat":{"haiku":"Conquest and slaughter consecrate power; invading forces annihilate innocents across generations, leaving only blood and the certainty that tomorrow brings more death.","opus":"A brutal battle vision from the Mongol conquests, with heads rolling and blades slick with the blood of the slain. It is about the cyclical savagery of war, where the besieged vow to answer slaughter with equal fury.","sonnet":"Genghis Khan's forces descend on a city in graphic, visceral terms — swords wet with blood, a thousand fathers killed, daughters taken — as the besieged inhabitants pray and hide their children knowing that by morning both sides will meet in fury, framed as an unflinching account of conquest as mutual annihilation.","src":"haiku"},
"protest-the-hero~c-est-la-vie":{"haiku":"Suicide as inevitable end game and dark comedy; the speaker catalogs failed attempts and their mundane consequences, accepting that death arrives as routine as late trains.","opus":"A darkly ironic catalog of suicides, each person stepping off something to make an impact and mostly just delaying the trains. It confronts the grim absurdity of being the author of your own fate, urging one to sing of being alive before the end takes everyone.","sonnet":"A series of suicides are catalogued — chair, building, bridge, platform — each described in its physical particulars and its social aftermath (the trains ran fifteen minutes late), and the refrain \"c'est la vie\" is deployed as both gallows irony and a genuine, furious insistence on singing a song of living before everybody dies.","src":"haiku"},
"protest-the-hero~from-the-sky":{"haiku":"Memory crystallizes trauma when catastrophe is captured and broadcast, transforming personal horror into iconic image while erasing the individual stories beneath collective remembrance.","opus":"The Hindenburg disaster becomes a meditation on how catastrophe is fixed in memory by the photograph, arguing that image, not words, is what a burning man leaves behind for the collective record.","sonnet":"A meditation on the Hindenburg disaster filtered through an argument about how photographic images fix collective memory in ways words cannot: the fireball and the swastika on the tail were captured and then ignored, and the speaker urges that bearing witness through pictures is the only honest response to catastrophe.","src":"haiku"},
"protest-the-hero~no-stars-over-bethlehem":{"haiku":"Religious hypocrisy corrupts civilization from within; the speaker indicts systems of faith that mask cruelty, calling for judgment against sanctified oppression.","opus":"A furious blasphemous assault on organized religion, imagining civilization laid to rest by a dagger plunged into God's chest. It turns worship into wealth and self-destruction, damning the fools and pulpits while clinging to a belief greater than any of them.","sonnet":"A scathing indictment of religious violence and institutional faith: crusaders seeking crusades to die in, lead locusts piercing hearts, a dagger plunged into God's chest that seals creation's fate — and a final, sardonic \"amen\" to fools, cossacks, and pulpits, suggesting no way out remains.","src":"haiku"},
"protest-the-hero~sequoia-throne":{"haiku":"Human cruelty masquerades as otherworldly threat; the speaker reveals that we are the apocalypse, not visitors from space, our wars and indifference the true harbinger.","opus":"An inversion of alien-invasion fear, arguing that humans, not visitors from the stars, are the real predators mutilating and destroying life. It condemns religious men stuffing corpses with faith and warns our own violence will bring our end.","sonnet":"Humanity is indicted as its own worst monster: aliens didn't come to kill us — we do that ourselves, stuffing corpses full of shit and faith, beating the war drum to the tune of peaceful life we discover elsewhere; the rhetorical question \"did you come here to kill or to die?\" is answered by our own still-life, numb existence.","src":"haiku"},
"protest-the-hero~tapestry":{"haiku":"Complacency calcifies into palace architecture, where excess passes for meaning and the speaker must resist the seduction of permanent stagnation disguised as belonging.","opus":"Young men drinking their days away and calling their squalor a palace, aging without maturing. Under the bravado runs real tenderness, the admission that shame and self-destruction are part of who you turn out to be, the swamp claimed as home.","sonnet":"A group of friends drinking themselves into comfortable stagnation declare their shared dive bar a palace, their arrested adolescence a kind of sovereignty. Behind the bravado is an acknowledgment that the things they are ashamed of may be exactly what formed them, even as they wait, like soldiers, for a dawn that never quite comes.","src":"haiku"},
"protest-the-hero~the-canary":{"haiku":"A woman breaks free from tradition to pursue her own destiny, accepting isolation and risk.","opus":"A soaring, literate tribute to Amelia Earhart, voiced from inside her refusal to be baggage or passenger. It weighs a woman's hunger to claim her own destiny against the harsher judgment women face when they crash.","sonnet":"Narrated in the voice of Amelia Earhart, the song traces her refusal to be a passenger in her own story — cutting her hair, sleeping in her jacket, setting out alone across seven thousand miles of ocean, knowing women pay more for failure while refusing to be deterred.","src":"haiku"},
"protest-the-hero~the-duelling-cavalier":{"haiku":"Silent cinema and voice transform power; the shift from silent film to sound erased those without the right accent, revealing that technology silences the voiceless while amplifying privilege.","opus":"Using the coming of sound film that killed silent stars, it dramatizes how progress renders yesterday's idols obsolete overnight. Voiced by a grounded Icarus cast out before his stars could realign, it is about the agony of being made irrelevant by change.","sonnet":"The transition from silent film to sound is used as a metaphor for any system that discards people when their utility changes: Hollywood audiences became discerning of voice, stars with foreign accents were dropped — \"death rattle sounds without notice\" — and the speaker, cast out first, dies before the stars realign, framing all Babel-towers as destined to fall.","src":"haiku"},
"protest-the-hero~tongue-splitter":{"haiku":"Unguarded speech creates wreckage; the speaker apologizes for truths that carve wounds into those they cherish.","opus":"A reckoning with authenticity and the toll of the performing self, the singer horrified at years spent on autopilot behind a mask that fuses to the face; the vow is to speak unfiltered truth and never lose his own face.","sonnet":"A self-aware, voluble narrator reckons with years of saying the wrong things on autopilot — to a therapist, to bandmates, on stage — and resolves to strip away the mask and return to who he was before performing became a substitute for honesty.","src":"haiku"},
"protest-the-hero~without-prejudice":{"haiku":"Wealth corrupts and divides; those with power rationalize cruelty while those without internalize shame, perpetuating cycles of exploitation.","opus":"An indictment of the rich and powerful who wrap predatory greed in the language of heroism and generosity. The plea beneath it is to dig past their appetite for accumulation and recover the humanity they've buried, before the exploitation brings everything crashing down.","sonnet":"A scathing portrait of wealth as a system of exploitation — those with power ruthlessly extract from those without, wearing masks of altruism while their bank accounts tell the real story. The song turns at the end to challenge the listener to excavate their own buried hypocrisy, suggesting that the qualities one despises in others are often the ones one secretly embodies.","src":"haiku"},
"provoker~bugs-humans":{"haiku":"Isolation, guilt, and the wish for annihilation haunt a speaker cycling through memories of youth and closeness, while an embedded anecdote about a decapitated pool shark meditates on how violence leaves place-memories that the living eventually forget.","opus":"A brooding darkwave meditation on guilt, seclusion, and the smallness of human life. The narrator broods on lost innocence, a hidden photograph, and fantasies of destruction, insisting bugs and humans are ultimately the same. A closing spoken tale about a decapitated friend, now forgotten by children playing on the spot, drives home how casually memory and meaning erode.","sonnet":"Isolation and shared smallness: bugs and humans are the same, crawling into corners with hidden pictures that remind you of another, and the spoken outro about Samuel's severed head found in a park grounds all the abstraction in a specific, grim anecdote about how places carry memories that the people using them now don't know and can't be told.","src":"haiku"},
"prtty-ridiculous~plastic":{"haiku":"Consumerism and celebrity obsession commodify identity as the speaker desires wealth and fame through aesthetic perfection.","opus":"A savage send-up of celebrity hunger, voiced from inside the craving, symmetry, a Ferrari, a famous overdose in Italy, all to become gloriously artificial. The manufactured pop star is a cookie-cutter, copy-paste product dying to join the plastic ranks.","sonnet":"A gleeful, self-aware indictment of celebrity culture delivered from inside it: the speaker craves Dior, Ferrari, infamy, and a death so brilliant it enters history — \"copy paste, copy paste\" — satirising the hollow reproduction machine they also want to join.","src":"haiku"},
"psy-aviah~into-the-game":{"haiku":"Time slips away while the speaker drifts; they're called to engage more fully, but question whether effort or engagement will ever matter. Existential waste and procrastination dominate.","opus":"Belgian electro-pop act Psy'Aviah dress urgency as pop propulsion, delivering a brisk wake-up call to someone drifting through life certain they have endless time to spare. The chorus demands they focus and commit fully until the end, withholding sympathy for a squandered existence as each idle moment slips uselessly by.","sonnet":"A voice addresses someone drifting through time as if it is inexhaustible, warning that the drift itself is the waste. The urgency is not angry but clear-eyed: falling behind is not a reversible state, and the refusal to re-engage is indistinguishable from choosing to disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"psychedelic-porn-crumpets~lava-lamp-pisco":{"haiku":"A person is mentally fractured from injury and struggles with the repetitive monotony of daily existence.","opus":"Psych-rock about burnout and self-destruction, a mind coming apart while daily life keeps demanding another shift at work. The looping questions turn the grind of exhausted, dissolute living into a numbed refrain you can't escape.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone physically and mentally wrecked — beaten to pieces, content spilling out — who can't sustain the basic rhythm of getting up and doing it all again each day.","src":"haiku"},
"psychedelic-porn-crumpets~nek":{"haiku":"Ecological collapse and temporal displacement render existence dust-like; brief consciousness precedes inevitable dissolution into insignificance.","opus":"An absurdist eco-lament dissolving into carpe diem, whales fleeing a ruined ocean toward some cosmic exile; against the certainty that everything returns to dust, the only anchor is being alive right now.","sonnet":"A surrealist opening image of sea life evacuating the planet dissolves into a simple existential anchor: we are dust filling holes in time, and the only thing that matters is being alive right now.","src":"haiku"},
"psycho-choke~swamp":{"haiku":"Stagnation and apathy plague those trapped in systems of control while the elite remain untouchable and indifferent. The song captures the futility of resistance when wealth and power insulate the powerful from accountability.","opus":"Mired in a stagnant, shameful place while the powerful are cursed and hope keeps getting deferred, aching for the moment to plant that hope and go to war. Bogged down for far too long, dragged under yet still fighting to break out.","sonnet":"Mired in shame and resentment toward those who hold power, the speaker and a collective feel stuck inside a swamp too long, craving the moment to seed hope and declare war on the conditions that keep them down.","src":"haiku"},
"psykup~sun-is-the-limit":{"haiku":"Revolutionary invitation to transcendence where old perceptions are dissolved and humanity ascends toward the sun without limits or hidden truth.","opus":"French experimental metal act Psykup stage a carnival barker's invitation to a suicidal flight toward the sun, promising overturned assumptions and jolted perceptions. Triggers are squeezed, the timid are shoved forward, and the crowd sings along as it prepares to soar and burn away, a gleeful chorus consuming rich and poor alike while the planet is written off as poison and the world's end is cheered on.","sonnet":"An anarchic invitation to burn through civilization's failures — its hierarchies, hypocrisies, and slow decay — and hurl oneself toward the sun even if it means destruction. The collective ecstasy of the crowd singing together becomes both funeral pyre and release.","src":"haiku"},
"psy~9intro":{"haiku":"Career longevity and uncompromising identity sustain relevance; the artist reflects on 22 years of irreverent survival, refusing to soften or age gracefully into forgetting.","opus":"Psy re-enters after five years mocking his own aging entertainer's fate, boasting that his only real secret to longevity is stubborn endurance. Behind the swagger is the anxious clown who admits chasing a twentysomething audience he no longer belongs to.","sonnet":"PSY's self-aware comeback statement for his ninth album — joking that after 22 years he still targets twenty-somethings, listing his hits, acknowledging he is now an old-timer but insisting he survived by sheer stubbornness.","src":"haiku"},
"psy~celeb":{"haiku":"A person is so dazzling to the speaker that they elevate them to celebrity status — every metric of attention spikes, every trend centers on them, and the speaker begs for a signed autograph on the romantic front page.","opus":"A playful courtship that casts the beloved as a full-blown celebrity and the smitten speaker as her devoted fan, begging for an autograph only he's allowed to see. PSY builds the whole flirtation out of fame's vocabulary — trending rankings, personas, curtain calls — so love reads as fandom and adoration as hitting follow.","sonnet":"Infatuation figured entirely through celebrity language — the beloved's approval rating skyrocketing in real time, their name trending like a buzzword, their every outfit breaking news. The narrator casts himself as follower and fan, asking for a private autograph and a starring role on their shared romantic front page.","src":"haiku"},
"psy~that-that-prod-feat-suga-of-bts":{"haiku":"Celebration of youth's return to sensory living and uninhibited fun after collective isolation, dancing through crowds with infectious defiance and refusal to overthink.","opus":"A post-pandemic release valve, get everyone back together and dancing after lockdown, with a sly jab at critics who wished PSY would flop. Pure defiant celebration of still being here and still winning.","sonnet":"A celebratory comeback anthem after the pandemic isolation — PSY reclaims his place as a party-starter, taunts those who predicted his downfall, and invites everyone to just let go and dance again now that the world is open and he is very much still here.","src":"haiku","fable":"A veteran's release-of-tension anthem staged as the exact inverse of lockdown: where the pandemic scattered people, the song's whole physics is bodies crowding back together — north-south-east-west, Gangnam and Gangbuk, everyone summoned into one throng that feels stiff and sore (뻑적지근해) precisely because it has been so long unused. That That treats crowd noise itself as the recovery. But the second verse turns the party into a settling of scores: Psy notes his hits have only toughened his hide, then gathers up the very people who performed funeral rites (고사 지낸) praying he would flop, and dismisses the grudge with a mock-scolding pat before telling them to shut up and just play along. The move that seals it is the drop of two civilian names — 민윤기 and 박재상 — SUGA and Psy stepping out from behind their stage personas, so the comeback is signed by the men, not the brands, the point being that survival is the answer no takedown earned.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"The hook's 준비하시고… 쏘세요 is the countdown catchphrase of Korea's televised housing-lottery draw (1969–2006), where an arrow was fired at a numbered target — Psy loading the comeback with prime nostalgia.","themes":["party & hedonism","anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"survival answered not as statement but as crowd physics","built":"two civilian names dropped mid-song — 민윤기, 박재상 — sign the comeback to the men, not the brands"},
"ptrd~rotoscoping":{"haiku":"A catalog of post-production and VFX pipeline terminology — rotoscoping, matchmoving, compositing, matte painting — arranged as rhythmic incantation, treating the technical grammar of digital filmmaking as its own hypnotic language.","opus":"An avant-garde piece assembled almost entirely from post-production and VFX jargon, rotoscoping, keying, matchmoving, compositing. It turns the language of digital image-making into rhythmic incantation, blurring the line between technical process and hypnotic mantra. Rather than a story, it evokes the machine-poetry of building artificial reality frame by frame.","sonnet":"A song that is entirely a list of VFX and compositing workflow terms — rotoscoping, matchmoving, keying, matte painting, caching, FBX, pipeline — structured as rhythmic recitation with no lyrical narrative, more a technical mantra or in-joke than a song with interpretable content.","src":"haiku"},
"public-memory~heir":{"haiku":"A frustrated inheritor seizes what was denied; violent seas and distant light frame loss and the running from authority.","opus":"The project deals in murky, dub-shaded atmosphere, and the words match it with cryptic threat. An overlooked chance is snatched away, consent brushed aside, a routine arrangement seized by force. Against a jaggedly reflective sea and a collapsing horizon, a fanged figure flees a master whose patience is shifting. Inheritance here reads less as gift than as something wrested and quietly ominous.","sonnet":"Fragmented and elliptical, the lyric sketches a power struggle over inheritance or opportunity — someone seizes what another missed, consent overridden, conformity imposed. A violent sea refracts any available light, and the figure who runs from a patient master leaves behind only alleged disasters and an unreadable aftermath.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~abrasive":{"haiku":"A relationship breaks amid lies and sleeplessness; both partners suffer but cannot escape. The song captures mutual emotional damage and inability to disengage despite toxicity.","opus":"A song of insomniac guilt and blame at the collapse of a relationship: 'it's not me, it's not you,' yet the narrator lies awake crying and screaming in the night, unable to sleep. The accusatory refrain, that the other person chose it, abused it, refused it and lost it, frames the wreckage as their doing while he's left sleepless, wrestling with the fallout.","sonnet":"A breakup in the middle of the night — the narrator is invisible to their partner, plagued by sleeplessness and guilt, unable to process what went wrong, with both people having hurt and been hurt by the other.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~already-gone":{"haiku":"Escape and detachment from an addiction-bound life. The narrator has already left mentally and refuses to engage with demands or old patterns.","opus":"A restless catalog of escape fantasies from someone running from addiction and the system, half-craving freedom and half-admitting he's the problem. The refrain of being already gone reads as both liberation and self-erasure, a man checked out before he's left.","sonnet":"A song about escaping addiction and the destructive patterns surrounding it, with the speaker declaring he has already mentally departed from that life. The lyrics alternate between images of flight and freedom and an acknowledgment that the pull of addiction still needs to be killed.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~away-from-me":{"haiku":"A person spirals into abandonment and jealousy as their partner leaves them for someone else, oscillating between desperation and despair while drowning in emotional pain.","opus":"A jealous, insecure narrator drowning in misery and drink is tormented by the fear that his partner has found and is sleeping with someone else, terrified of losing them and being left alone.","sonnet":"A narrator consumed by jealousy and fear of abandonment suspects his partner is with someone else, cycling between desperate pleading to stay and raw anger at being left alone.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~basement":{"haiku":"A trapped, paranoid person hides from judgment behind a crumbling facade. They push others away rather than be seen in their broken state.","opus":"A paranoid, entrapped narrator hiding in a basement, ashamed and afraid to be seen, offering to give away everything he has. The recurring absent, dreaming mother suggests a childhood of neglect underlying the self-loathing and desire to be left alone.","sonnet":"A person locked in fear and paranoia — possibly trapped in a controlling or abusive dynamic with a parent — who feels so broken and exposed that they beg not to be seen in this state. The submission in the chorus, offering everything away, reads as defeat rather than generosity.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~better-place":{"haiku":"A speaker mourns a loved one who died, wishing they had been there to help them survive.","opus":"A grief song aimed at someone lost too soon, circling the guilt of not having intervened, waking in cold sweats, unable to reconcile the comfort of thinking a person is at peace with the raw refusal to let them go. Puddle of Mudd were the Wes Scantlin vehicle that rode Fred Durst's patronage into early-2000s post-grunge radio, and the register here is exactly that blunt, wounded American rock sincerity.","sonnet":"Grief for a cousin lost too soon, carried with the guilt of someone who wasn't there when it counted. The song sits in the gap between knowing a person is gone and being unable to stop thinking about them — cold-sweat nights, mental photographs that can't replace the real thing, the belated wish to have shown up sooner.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~blood-on-the-table":{"haiku":"A person exhausted from deception leaves a relationship, having given everything only to face lies and emotional withdrawal. The song describes the aftermath of relational betrayal.","opus":"In the post-grunge register that carried the band out of the early-2000s radio-rock boom, the narrator confronts a partner's performed tears and manipulations, insisting he has given everything he has while the metaphorical blood pools between them. Exhaustion curdles into a refusal to keep absorbing the screaming, the imagery of lying and crying looping until letting go feels less like cruelty than survival.","sonnet":"A relationship collapses under the weight of sustained deception. One partner has poured everything into something that was built on manufactured tears and false loyalty. The reckoning is unsparing — every emotional resource spent, nothing recovered, release the only act left.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~blurry":{"haiku":"An emotionally dependent person struggles with disconnection and pain caused by a lover, seeking rescue from a world of fakeness and loss.","opus":"A desperate longing for an absent person the speaker orbits completely, unable to live without them in a world he sees as fake and empty. The repeated plea to take the pain away, the pain shoved in his face, suggests separation, possibly from a child, and the helplessness of loving someone he can't reach.","sonnet":"A desperate, consuming love for someone who feels out of reach — the world has gone fake and hollow, and this person is the only real thing; the speaker clings to them for survival even as that same person has caused him pain.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~bottom":{"haiku":"A cry for help from spiritual and emotional depths, with the speaker trapped with someone in mutual decay and hopelessness, repeatedly trying and failing to escape.","opus":"A dredge through codependency and self-loathing, one voice pleading with a higher power to lift the weight while it sinks into the gutter beside the very person dragging it down. The old sticks-and-stones rhyme gets thrown up as armor that clearly isn't holding. Puddle of Mudd built their sound on this grunge-inflected bar-band heaviness, aggression and wound braided together in that turn-of-the-century radio-rock mode.","sonnet":"Dragged down by a person who embodies rot and stagnation, the song is a cry for divine rescue from a weight that's been compounding for years. It holds both resentment — insisting no words can break the speaker — and exhaustion, the image of sinking together at the bottom saying everything about how trapped and complicit in the spiral the speaker feels.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~bring-me-down":{"haiku":"A person destroys themselves and others through vice and malice; the narrator confronts how negativity and sin corrode from within.","opus":"Watching a using friend dissolve while refusing to let their spiral drag the narrator under, weary contempt mixed with a crude moral reckoning; a boundary drawn against being pulled down with a sinking addict.","sonnet":"A song addressed to a toxic, fading person who keeps dragging the narrator down — karma is invoked as the corrective force, and the narrator waits for the universe to disintegrate this person the way they have disintegrated everything good.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~change-my-mind":{"haiku":"A speaker is trapped at the bottom, dismissed and judged, losing track of time and sight—resigned that nothing can change their mind or circumstance.","opus":"The post-grunge band channel a bruised, self-flagellating split into a mantra of finality, the narrator scorning his ex while cataloguing his own wreckage just as harshly. Losing his sense of time, blurring, unable to focus, he hardens into the certainty that nothing shifts now, the resolve serving as both armor and admission of the damage he owns.","sonnet":"Self-reproach and defiant exit from a destructive relationship run together. The narrator acknowledges having brought harm on himself, stands accused by a partner whose absence now warps time and vision, yet holds firm — nothing external will alter the decision already made.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~cloud-9":{"haiku":"Floating in euphoric disconnection from reality, the speaker self-destructs through excess but an angel repeatedly brings them back, creating a cycle of damnation and rescue.","opus":"Post-grunge addiction spiral chasing an angelic high that doubles as an escape from self, the narrator half in love with obliteration. Cloud nine here is less bliss than a place to disappear.","sonnet":"A song about self-destruction as escape — the narrator cycles through violence, drug use, and fantasies of disappearing, with an angelic female figure who briefly pulls him back from the edge before he slips further.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~control":{"haiku":"A narrator trapped in an intense, chaotic relationship describes control, pain, and transgressive intimacy as paradoxically binding and suffocating. They vacillate between needing connection and being unable to leave the cage.","opus":"A push-and-pull of a dominant, painful, sadomasochistic relationship in which the speaker is caged, chained and hurt, admits he can't control the other and they're not right for him, yet is drawn to needing them and, at times, claims control over them.","sonnet":"A sadomasochistic relationship plays out in contradictions — attraction mixed with pain, control claimed by both parties but held by neither — ending in the admission that the two are not right for each other.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~control-acoustic-version":{"haiku":"Sadomasochistic entanglement confuses pain with intimacy; both figures battle for dominance through mutual degradation and control disguised as connection.","opus":"A toxic power struggle wrapped in BDSM imagery, craving someone who's wrong for you and mistaking control for connection. Desire and revulsion feeding each other, unable to leave the cage either built.","sonnet":"About a relationship defined by mutual BDSM-style power exchange — chains, scratching, hitting — that the narrator ultimately cannot sustain because the emotional control dynamic is broken, with neither person actually able to control the other. The shift from excited participation to resignation tracks the dissolution of a sexual relationship that was exciting but incompatible at a deeper level.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~daddy":{"haiku":"A child endures physical abuse and shame from his father while receiving conflicting comfort from his mother, resulting in deep family trauma and permanent estrangement.","opus":"A stark account of childhood abuse: the father calls the son a son of a bitch, kicks him out, makes him fetch the switch used to beat him, and leaves him lying on the floor; the son clings to his mother's reassurance and vows he'll never become like his father.","sonnet":"A raw account of paternal abuse — a violent, abusive father calling his child names and beating him, while the mother reassures but doesn't intervene, and the child vows he will never become his father.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~diseased-almost":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation and existential sickness where the narrator feels born poisoned into a cruel world with no escape but self-destruction.","opus":"A raw plunge into despair, the narrator convinced everyone has abandoned him, contaminated, born broken and destroyed within, repeatedly pleading for death as the lyrics decay into pure venom. Puddle of Mudd, the Wichita post-grunge band, shed their radio-rock gloss here for something desperate and self-flaying, Wes Scantlin's tormented, hallucinatory rage spiraling toward a bleak, hostile ruin.","sonnet":"Extreme psychological distress pushed to its rawest reduction — the world has turned, voices will not stop, a poison is spreading from the inside. The speaker was born into a kind of congenital wrongness and cannot find the exit. The rage in the closing lines is the distress flipping into aggression, but no relief arrives for either mood.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~drift-die":{"haiku":"Someone wallows in shame and regret over past mistakes, unable to escape the cycle of self-blame as they drift toward death.","opus":"A weary, disillusioned slide toward death, dogged by the shame of a squandered life and uncertain who bears the blame for his errors. His refrain about ignorance breeding falsehood and money purchasing anything frames the resignation as a rejection of a corrupt world he'd rather quietly ease himself out of.","sonnet":"A resignation to drifting through a life of shame and unanswered questions — money, lies, and ignorance are the backdrop, and the speaker has given up fighting, choosing instead to slowly expire on his own timeline.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~everybody-wants-you":{"haiku":"Fame's addictive trap where the celebrated person sells themselves publicly, losing authentic self while chasing endless attention and approval.","opus":"A portrait of a scene idol consumed by attention, perpetually on the move, marketed like a glossy magazine, incapable of refusing anyone, and paying for the acclaim in isolation and drink. A cover of Billy Squier's early-eighties hit, given post-grunge grit by Puddle of Mudd, dissecting how fame exhausts the star who surrenders everything yet never wins his freedom.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone incapable of saying no — performing desirability at full tilt, always on the scene, throwing affection in every direction the wind suggests. The cost accumulates invisibly: loneliness, alcohol, days lost track of. Everyone wants a piece and the wanting is indistinguishable from bleeding her dry. She never gets free because freedom would require refusing the attention she cannot stop needing.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~famous":{"haiku":"Aspires to fame and hedonistic celebrity excess, willing to trade substance for the allure of wealth, status, and easy conquest.","opus":"A wish-fulfillment fantasy of stardom that piles up limos, red-carpet nights, and no-strings hedonism, then quietly undercuts the boast. Puddle of Mudd play the dream straight while suggesting the payoff is emptier than it looks, jadedness the real prize of getting exactly what you asked for.","sonnet":"The narrator fantasizes about achieving celebrity — the Oscars, the Grammys, partying with famous people — while acknowledging the warning that comes with it: be careful what you wish for, because fame brings jadedness and things falling apart.","src":"haiku","fable":"The chorus borrows its jeer from a 1985 Dire Straits hit, but that older track put the boast in the mouth of a jealous laborer sneering at rock stars. Strip away the frame and the fantasy gets swallowed whole, as if the laborer got his wish and found it hollow. The song keeps undercutting its own celebration: the celebrity name-drops arrive weary, wealth registers as tedium, and the wish itself trips over the sound of its key word in a broken stammer. Then the third verse turns to a doubter who left, and the whole glittering Hollywood inventory reads as an argument flung at that absent skeptic rather than a life anyone enjoys. Its lone warning stops being aimed outward and becomes a thing the singer mutters to himself, craving the proof more than the prize.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"craving proof of fame more than any life fame could furnish","built":"the Dire Straits boast smuggled in so the winner finds it hollow"},
"puddle-of-mudd~freak-of-the-world":{"haiku":"A person feels alienated and monstrous, unable to connect or be understood despite trying desperately. The song encodes permanent otherness and relentless isolation.","opus":"Alienation staged as claustrophobia: a narrator peering out through cutting-edged slats, unable to wake or reach the person he wants, resigned to being an outsider beyond fixing. Branding himself a freak reads as both defiance and defeat. Puddle of Mudd trafficked in exactly this grunge-descended isolation, a Kansas band funneling Cobain-era angst into radio-ready alt-rock.","sonnet":"Alienation so complete it reads as self-assigned verdict. The speaker can't wake from an obsession, sees someone through a haze he can't reach, begs and steals and cries for them — and arrives at the conclusion that the failure is constitutive: he's simply wired wrong, a freak of the world, and there's nothing left to say or do about it.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~go-to-hell":{"haiku":"Moral judgment where most people's flaws condemn them to spiritual punishment despite claimed belief in salvation and divine rescue.","opus":"A brimstone sermon telling listeners that everybody's dying to reach heaven but most are bound for hell, calling out a bad attitude and refusing to hear apologies for the pain living inside their brain. Puddle of Mudd, the Wichita post-grunge outfit, wrap their crunchy hard rock around a mocking gospel refrain, salvation dangled and denied with Wes Scantlin's swaggering, unforgiving contempt.","sonnet":"A rough-edged sermon on universal moral accounting — everyone lives, everyone dies, everyone wants Heaven but most will have to pass through Hell first. The speaker is done with apologies and excuses, watching someone try to dodge the pain living inside their own head. God is still coming, the promise holds, but the path runs through reckoning regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~heel-over-head":{"haiku":"A rejected lover feels unseen and emotionally devastated after years of sacrifice; they spiral in denial and invisibility, yearning for recognition while insisting their pain is legitimate and profound.","opus":"Post-grunge fallout of being emotionally invisible, a lover who gave everything and still isn't seen, heard, or touched. The head-over-heels phrase inverted into someone unraveling while pleading not to be walked away from.","sonnet":"A plea to a partner who can't or won't see the narrator — completely invisible to the person they've sacrificed for — falling apart at the seams while still insisting they're hopelessly in love.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~hooky":{"haiku":"A student rejects school, listing grievances against teachers, bullies, and the education system itself.","opus":"A crude, cartoonish teenage rebellion song, a kid cataloguing every reason to skip class, from bullies and lunch money to sheer boredom and adolescent frustration, all wrapped in a chanted playground refrain. It's deliberately juvenile provocation rather than confession. Puddle of Mudd built a strain of their catalogue on this joke-track shock humor, the flip side of Wes Scantlin's earnest post-grunge radio balladry.","sonnet":"A bratty, entirely unserious ode to skipping school — bullies, lunch ladies, girls who mock, zero academic accomplishment. The stance is pure adolescent refusal, weaponised as comedy. There's no deeper arc; the point is the joy of opting out.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~i-m-so-sure":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects arrogant, self-absorbed people who demean others and feel entitled to superiority.","opus":"A resentful takedown of a stuck-up, self-obsessed person who constantly puts the narrator down. Beneath the pop-punk kiss-off runs a genuine plea to break away, refusing to become like the person he claims to hate, with hints of a numbing habit to get by.","sonnet":"Contempt for a stuck-up, self-absorbed person who constantly puts the narrator down — after they're gone, everything becomes easier, and the relief of the absence makes clear how toxic the dynamic was.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~if-i-could-love-you":{"haiku":"The speaker wants to love someone but feels unseen and unwanted despite their pain, describing internal turbulence the other refuses to witness. They're left driving themselves crazy while the other remains absent.","opus":"A frustrated, half-broken love song about mismatched wants and an inability to fully connect; the narrator admits he's selfish and that something's wrong on both sides, pleading for a second chance and for the partner to feel his pain, trapped in a crazy-making bond he can't get right.","sonnet":"A fragmentary song about emotional incapacity in a relationship — the narrator acknowledges being selfish and unable to give what the other person needs, circling around a conditional love that never quite arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~it-was-faith":{"haiku":"A person survives abandonment through faith in a lover who stays despite pain; gratitude for being kept alive by someone else's devotion.","opus":"A gratitude song crediting belief, and a redemptive partner, for carrying the narrator through everything, even the wounds that same person inflicted. The ambivalence is honest, a love that both hurt and pardoned him, yet is named as the reason he's still alive and has finally turned a corner.","sonnet":"A testimony to spiritual faith as the thing that kept the narrator alive through a painful relationship — not the love of the other person, which caused bleeding and abandonment, but a religious faith that persisted through the wreckage and brought him through.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~keep-it-together":{"haiku":"Two lovers separated by distance cling to the belief they can reconcile and survive, asking each other to hold on and not give up.","opus":"The post-grunge band's vow of desperate devotion as a relationship falls apart, the narrator swearing he'd sacrifice anything to keep his lover from leaving. Separated and frantic, he pleads for one more chance to fix things, his spinning mind circling a single hope that their love might outlast the goodbye.","sonnet":"Despite physical distance and the weight of a relationship straining to breaking point, a person refuses to let go — pleading, praying, willing to absorb any pain to keep the connection alive. The conviction is total: what they had is incomparable, and the effort to preserve it is worth every cost.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~life-aint-fair":{"haiku":"Bitter declaration that life's unfairness and betrayal won't break the speaker, who refuses to return to toxic relationships or play the victim.","opus":"A bitter split anthem where the narrator refuses to return, unable to trust a partner who dismissed the whole thing as meaningless, feeling like a punchline yet swearing life will never crush him. Puddle of Mudd, the Wichita post-grunge outfit, work their usual wounded defiance, the shattered-glass imagery and stubborn refusal to look back set to their radio-rock crunch.","sonnet":"A decisive exit from a relationship built on distrust and disconnection — the other person never understood what was happening inside, always disconnecting, always making the speaker wait. The refrain is not despair but defiance: life will not break me. The closing image of watching someone run away while refusing to be their saviour is the emotional completion of leaving for good.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~livin-on-borrowed-time":{"haiku":"Living recklessly with the certainty of impending death, rejecting control and embracing nihilism in defiant refusal to be constrained.","opus":"A snarling burst of self-destruction and defiance from a man convinced he is out of time and daring anyone to stop him. The bravado barely covers exhaustion and disgust with a life running on empty.","sonnet":"A defiant, cornered narrator who sees death as imminent and responds with aggression rather than fear — sick of hypocrisy and interference, rejecting any attempt to stop or lecture him, and insisting that no external force will prevent him from acting on his own terms for whatever time remains.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~merry-go-round":{"haiku":"A social outcast owns his rejection and refusal to conform, celebrating his willingness to be silly and scorned rather than pretend to fit in. He invites others to stop caring what others think and embrace the chaotic merry-go-round ride of living outside the rules.","opus":"A misfit's party invitation from someone who leans into being the ugly, awkward reject nobody wants around. He turns rejection into a dare, chasing a dizzy chemical high he half wants to quit, and shrugs off the fact that his indifference is mutual.","sonnet":"A self-deprecating song about being a social reject who does not care — the narrator calls himself ugly, skinny, smelly, and a wastoid, cycles through highs and crashes, and keeps arriving at the conclusion that none of it matters.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~migraine":{"haiku":"Persistent unbearable pain and mental fog that makes thinking impossible, medication offers only temporary relief before the suffering returns.","opus":"A pounding headache used as a metaphor for a mind coming apart, memory slipping and each dose of medicine chased by the next. As the ache spreads toward the chest, the song edges toward suicide, its no-pain-no-gain refrain turned into a bleak joke about suffering that leads nowhere.","sonnet":"A migraine as existential crisis — the pain expands until it fills every option, swallowing memory and finally moving from the head to the chest. The trigger image can't squeeze or pull the trigger implies the pain has become its own prison, the only solution left something that cannot be named cleanly. Pills and magazines offer nothing. There is no messenger, no message.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~moonshine":{"haiku":"A lover obscures understanding and creates emotional breakup that the singer struggles to comprehend. The relationship falls apart inexplicably despite its brightness.","opus":"A hazy attempt to win back a lost love, casting her as intoxicating light while pleading for a moment to understand why it fell apart. Tender but foggy, the reconciliation lives more in wishful drift than in any real answer.","sonnet":"An ex-partner is cast as the moonshine that once lit the narrator's path, and now the song circles the unanswerable question of why they fell apart, unable to close that emotional distance or make sense of the rupture.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~my-kind-of-crazy":{"haiku":"Devoted loyalty to a self-destructive relationship where the narrator will always wait despite the other person's instability and pain.","opus":"A tender goodbye to a chaotic, self-losing partner, the narrator vowing to keep waiting even as he confesses he's at his limit, sensing this reunion is their last. Puddle of Mudd soften their hard-rock edge into something almost gentle, the refrain a weary devotion to someone mired in their own hole, harming themselves with no space left for anyone else.","sonnet":"Loyal devotion to someone self-destructing — the other person oscillates between leaving and begging to stay, vanishing then going silent, shutting everyone else out until they are trapped in a hole of their own making. The speaker knows this and stays anyway, committed to waiting, unable to call it love without also calling it something they cannot sustain.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~never-change":{"haiku":"Persistent rejection and powerlessness; constant mistreatment by others is normalized, though the speaker wonders why things cannot simply stay the same.","opus":"A wounded outsider's shrug at everyone who belittles him, half self-pity and half defiance, then a turn on a lover whose transformation feels like betrayal. Post-grunge resentment insisting that people, himself included, simply won't change.","sonnet":"About being constantly put down by everyone around you and accepting that certain things — including your own patterns and other people's behavior toward you — will never change, which is both defeat and a kind of grim peace.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~nobody-told-me":{"haiku":"A lost and confused wanderer lacks guidance and direction in life, burdened by childhood pain they carry forward. They struggle to find their way home amid uncertainty and fear.","opus":"A rudderless speaker revisits an unremarkable childhood he lugs around like dead weight, adrift on a grim road with no one to point the way. He can't locate any sense of home and stays trapped grinding through identical days, mired in uncertainty and dread.","sonnet":"The speaker describes a life with no clear direction, haunted by a difficult past that weighs him down and a persistent sense of being lost and afraid. Roads and horizons serve as recurring images for the aimlessness and the daily grind of getting through another identical day.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~nothing-left-to-lose":{"haiku":"A person in psychological crisis—burning, melting, and paranoid—desperately seeks help but feels powerless against internal pressure. They oscillate between self-destructive urges and pleas for rescue as everyone around them turns away.","opus":"Post-grunge desperation, a narrator cracking under pressure and paranoia, pleading to be saved from himself while resenting a partner who always gets their way. Having nothing left to lose is both the low point and the reckless threat behind it.","sonnet":"A song about being hollowed out by someone who always gets their way — the narrator is paranoid, sleepless, and cracking under pressure, simultaneously furious at the other person's dominance and begging to be saved from himself.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~out-of-my-head":{"haiku":"A song about elevated consciousness and rejection of peer influence. The speaker refuses to be held down by those around him and feels superior to their moral compromises.","opus":"An account of alienation from hypocritical friends and a spinning, out-of-control mind, with the speaker feeling superior and detached, insisting these people aren't really his friends and refusing to let go.","sonnet":"A sense of alienation from a social circle the speaker sees as hypocritical, paired with an elevated, almost manic self-assurance — he feels higher than the people around him and refuses to let them hold him back.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~out-of-my-way":{"haiku":"A man describes his obsessive fixation on a woman who dominates his thoughts and emotions, expressing his desire to go to great lengths to make her happy despite his struggle for freedom.","opus":"Post-grunge band Puddle of Mudd voice fixated devotion dressed as generosity. The narrator worships an unreachable, glittering woman lodged under his skin, vowing to bend over backwards and even destroy himself endlessly just to please her. Obsession and self-sacrifice tangle around someone who clearly sees straight through him.","sonnet":"A man consumed by a woman he cannot reach — elevated in his imagination to something angelic and untouchable — keeps overextending himself to manufacture her happiness at the cost of his own. The obsession is genuine but exhausting, and the declaration that he will keep dying if she keeps fighting reads as devotion collapsing into resentment.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~piss-it-all-away":{"haiku":"The speaker dismisses others' words and meaning while indulging in vice and intoxication. They waste truth and connection through willful deception.","opus":"Addiction as compulsion, drinking and using until thought fails and needing ever more. The narrator rejects everything a partner or conscience says, choosing to squander it all rather than be purified or reformed.","sonnet":"About being trapped in addiction and compulsive behavior — the narrator can't think straight when drinking, can't stop wanting more, and recognizes it as sinful self-destruction but can't escape it. The title says it: throwing away everything that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~pitchin-a-fit":{"haiku":"A partner's constant anger and emotional volatility break someone down. Despite trying to make them happy, the singer is worn out and vows retaliation.","opus":"Post-grunge domestic combat where everything the narrator does to please a partner curdles into sickness and exhaustion. She kicks and screams in every direction while he breaks down and plots to make her sorry. What lifts it beyond simple spite is the late turn toward forgiveness, admitting he is no saint, complicating the pitched fury with a grudging, self-aware tenderness.","sonnet":"Exhaustion at the centre of an emotionally chaotic relationship. A man worn down by a partner's relentless outbursts and demands eventually reaches a breaking point, oscillating between resentment and forgiveness, ultimately threatening to make her feel what she has cost him.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~psycho":{"haiku":"A narrator contemplates mental illness and paranoia, reflecting on a chaotic past with a volatile lover. They question whether they or the other person embodies madness, drifting through hazy memories of youth.","opus":"The speaker wonders whether he is the unstable, paranoid one in a relationship with a beautiful but self-destructive woman, nostalgic for their reckless younger days.","sonnet":"The narrator oscillates between admiring a woman he sees as magical and unpredictable, and turning the \"psycho\" label on himself, suggesting it might be his own instability — not hers — that defines the relationship.","src":"haiku","fable":"A post-grunge breakup song that hides its accusation inside a shrug: the hook keeps floating the idea that the narrator himself might be the unstable, paranoid wreck, but the self-diagnosis is tossed off so casually it works as a dodge rather than a confession. The verses reach back to a younger volatility both people once carried like a primed charge that thrilled them precisely because it might detonate any second, then hard-cut to her sprawled drunk on grass and pavement now, that same combustibility curdled into collapse. The tell is the migrating pronoun: what opens as a tentative maybe it is me hardens by the outro into a flat verdict aimed at her, so the mask of shouldering blame slips off to show the finger was pointed outward the whole time.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","madness & the mind","addiction & self-destruction","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"self-diagnosis offered so casually it works as a dodge not a confession","built":"the migrating pronoun — maybe me hardening to a flat verdict aimed at her — tracks the blame shift"},
"puddle-of-mudd~purple-heart":{"haiku":"A damaged person with internal compromise and emotional brittleness who paradoxically desires everything but cannot maintain connections; self-medication as escape.","opus":"A bleak, chain-smoking vigil in which the narrator can't sleep as he watches someone come undone, unable to reach them. Each cigarette drains a little more of him as he drifts toward oblivion. Puddle of Mudd keep it stark and downbeat, the military medal of the title repurposed as a symbol of wounds carried, not honors won.","sonnet":"A short, smoke-filled meditation on someone who takes everything and gives nothing — the speaker lights cigarettes and waits, aware the habit is killing him, and imagines crossing into whatever comes after this life. Resignation without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~radiate":{"haiku":"A person questions whether their love or faith can truly transform another's life and lift them out of confusion and pain, unsure if what they offer radiates enough power.","opus":"In melodic post-grunge mode, the band tries to draw someone out of solitude, forcing a path through to dissolve their sadness and asking whether the connection still pulses and cracks the heart open. The offer is flight, abandoning the world and its make-believe blessing to bet on something greater. By the close he answers himself: yes, it pulses hard enough to fell a person.","sonnet":"Someone reaches toward another person in crisis with a question about whether love, or whatever unnamed force exists between them, can still do something — crack the heart open, restore faith, carry them somewhere better. The offer is to flee the human race entirely and find out together whether the feeling still radiates.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~rocket-man":{"haiku":"Isolation of space travel becomes metaphor for feeling alienated from loved ones; the profession distances the speaker from his true self and home.","opus":"A cover of Elton John and Bernie Taupin's classic, voicing an astronaut who longs for his wife and planet, estranged from the person his family imagines, doing perilous work he treats as merely routine employment. Puddle of Mudd recast the wistful space traveler in their post-grunge register, preserving the loneliness of a man burning away far from anyone who grasps what he does.","sonnet":"An astronaut's homesick monologue from deep space — he misses his wife, misses the earth, and understands that the man the mission requires him to be is not the man anyone at home believes they know. Mars is cold and inhospitable and the job is just a job five days a week. The long flight back never quite arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~said":{"haiku":"A person slips into darkness emotionally detached, trapped in cycles of failure and loss with no way to escape or change.","opus":"A numbed slide into despair among drug users and grieving family, the narrator trapped in a loop he can't escape and longing to undo the past. The final question, tossed to no one, exposes how alone he feels going unheard as he withdraws emotionally.","sonnet":"A man trapped in a spiral of failure and numbness — surrounded by dysfunction, unable to escape his own patterns, hallucinating, questioning whether anyone is even hearing him as life keeps slipping forward without him.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~she-hates-me":{"haiku":"A romantic relationship collapses when one partner discovers the other is shallow and manipulative; the narrator learns the lesson but accepts responsibility for naivety.","opus":"A bitter breakup recap of a whirlwind romance that soured fast: the woman took everything he had without guilt and tore up his feelings, leaving him to conclude, with crude finality, that she hates him; he calls himself a fool but claims he's glad it's over and that both learned their lesson.","sonnet":"A blunt breakup story — a relationship that started fine turned sour fast, the woman took everything and gave nothing back, and the narrator is left embarrassed but ultimately relieved it's over.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~spaceship":{"haiku":"A sexually explicit song where the speaker characterizes themselves as a mystical vessel or deity offering to take someone on an intimate journey.","opus":"A blunt post-grunge sex boast dressed in cheap sci-fi swagger, the narrator all appetite and no subtlety. There's nothing under it but the come-on, which is exactly the register the band was going for.","sonnet":"A clumsy, horny come-on — the narrator has been on tour, he's drunk, and the spaceship is a thinly veiled metaphor for sex, as he tries to persuade someone to get naked with him.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~spin-you-around":{"haiku":"The narrator imagines dancing and transformation with a romantic partner, offering to overturn their world and take them higher.","opus":"A simple come-on built on a single promise: meet me over the mountain, dance with me, and I will turn your world upside down. There is little beyond flirtation and repetition, a man offering rides and highs to a woman he wants. Puddle of Mudd, the Missouri post-grunge outfit fronted by Wes Scantlin, made their name on grittier radio rock; this is their looser, bluesier, hook-chasing register.","sonnet":"A simple, full-body infatuation — if he saw her dancing he'd spin her, tip her world over, take her somewhere higher. The song doesn't complicate it: desire as pure kinetic energy, the promise of transformation through closeness, a woman he wants to be with badly enough that the request barely hides its urgency.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~stoned":{"haiku":"A person exhausted by authority figures and workplace politics prefers intoxication to sobriety, wanting escape from constant demands and disrespect. They frame getting high as preferable to enduring the stress of staying aware and sober.","opus":"The speaker is fed up with authority figures and their politics, feeling sunlight and sobriety as enemies, and declares he would rather be stoned than deal with everyone's demands while sober.","sonnet":"Someone fed up with authority figures and workplace frustration declares they would rather be intoxicated than deal sober with the people and politics grinding them down.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~stressed-out":{"haiku":"Physical deterioration from stress where the narrator watches themselves and others fall apart despite continuing to live; inevitable unraveling.","opus":"A queasy anatomy of anxiety literalized as the body falling apart, hair coming out in the comb, legs weakening, stomach burning, everything inverting while the narrator runs on his last nerve. Puddle of Mudd render stress as physical disintegration, the plea to save yourself set against a life that keeps falling out no matter how one keeps living, the band's grunge crunch matched to visceral unease.","sonnet":"A physical catalogue of someone coming apart under pressure — hair in the comb, head hanging, stomach burning, the body inverting under its own weight. The advice the speaker offers is barely advice: save yourself, I am on my last nerve. Everything keeps falling apart despite the effort to keep living, keep being.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~sydney":{"haiku":"Someone grieves a departed loved one named Sydney, confessing their emotions overwhelm them and they bear guilt while hoping they rest peacefully.","opus":"A raw elegy addressed to Sydney, a lost loved one who died, the narrator wracked with guilt, apology and the wish to set her spirit free. Slipping toward the light himself and half-losing his mind, he pleads to know why she left him alone.","sonnet":"A grief song addressed to someone named Sydney who has died — the narrator processes guilt, loss, and the feeling of being left behind, eventually hoping Sydney is watching over from above and finding peace.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~the-only-reason":{"haiku":"Someone's entire existence depends on another person being alive, framing love as the sole reason to continue living. The song depicts total emotional interdependence.","opus":"A tender break from the band's usual grit, offering up heart and soul as breakable gifts and asking they be handled with care. The narrator pins his entire will to live on one person, framing that devotion as the force that gets him through and keeps despair at bay.","sonnet":"A declaration of total reliance on one person, offered with open fragility. Every cosmic constant — the sun, the turning world — becomes a metaphor for what a single relationship provides. Without it, the will to live weakens; with it, survival feels not just possible but certain.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~think":{"haiku":"Repetition and numbness dominate; every goodbye repeats and nothing changes despite attempts at relief.","opus":"The post-grunge band stack stammered imperatives about waiting, thinking, and drinking into a portrait of someone stuck between numbness and self-medication, wasting days in front of the tube as another bad dream loops. Against a partner's thousand rehearsed goodbyes and goodnights, a battered reassurance that it's going to be alright keeps repeating, half comfort and half the addict's brittle mantra clinging to hope it doesn't quite feel.","sonnet":"A person cycling through emotional numbness, using drinking to blunt the repetitive grief of repeated goodbyes. Fear, wasted days, and suppressed hatred pile up beneath a surface-level reassurance that things will resolve. Watching a loved one sleep offers the only genuine tenderness in the wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~thinking-about-you":{"haiku":"A person obsesses over a distant lover they cannot keep, unable to release them despite crossing worlds to pursue them.","opus":"Longing for a lover who has slipped away, the narrator holding onto the memory of her and swearing he'll pursue her anywhere. Beneath the hope runs grief: he dreams and prays nightly for a reunion, unable to accept that the relationship is already over.","sonnet":"A straightforward heartbreak song about obsessive longing for a lost partner — the narrator can still taste her kiss, would cross the world to find her, and spends every night dreaming she might come back.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~time-flies":{"haiku":"A person faces the end of life, unable to let go or wake from their trajectory toward oblivion. The song repeats time's passage as inevitable collapse.","opus":"A strung-out reckoning with mortality and wasted time, a narrator perched on a small hill swearing he'll make something of himself while pills and self-destruction pull him toward the edge of a mountain. The birds sing but no one listens. Puddle of Mudd carried this bruised, addiction-shadowed introspection under their radio-rock crunch, the darker undertow beneath their turn-of-the-millennium post-grunge success.","sonnet":"Time burning away while the speaker is strung out and unable to let go or fully wake up. There's a faint aspiration — sitting on a hill, trying to make something of himself — but it keeps dissolving back into the pull of the high and the vertigo of a life slipping past too fast to hold.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~time-of-our-lives":{"haiku":"Nostalgia for youthful recklessness and freedom before time's inevitable passage left the narrator trapped in regret and loss.","opus":"A wistful look back at reckless teenage nights, one lit by a joyride in a borrowed car, wrapped in a shout-along chorus. The nostalgia carries mourning, though; the narrator wishes he could rewind time and be there again for a friend who may not have made it.","sonnet":"Nostalgic for a reckless summer night — stealing a car, getting high, knowing it was wrong and doing it anyway because it felt absolutely right. The wish to turn back time shades into something sadder: a wounded friend, an inability to be fully present now. The high was real; the distance from it is the ache.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~uh-oh":{"haiku":"Documents repeated failure to honor commitments and reckless self-destruction, apologizing while showing no evidence of change. Self-aware but resigned to causing harm.","opus":"A blackout apology dressed as comedy, a man confessing to another bender gone wrong, missing teeth and lost memory, begging forgiveness before the tone snaps into a bitter reversal telling her to take everything and get out. The remorse and the contempt sit side by side. Puddle of Mudd's later work leaned into this rowdy, self-deprecating bar-rock swagger over the earnest grunge of their early breakthrough.","sonnet":"A chaotic confession of spectacular self-sabotage — blackouts, a two-front brawl, turning up drunk to a date three days late. The speaker spends the first half grovelling and promising to fix things, then flips entirely, telling the woman to take everything and get out. The shift is so sudden it doubles as both apology and goodbye.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~uno-mas":{"haiku":"Cyclical breakup where nothing truly ends; just repetitive patterns of saying goodbye and letting go, hung up on the same pain repeatedly.","opus":"A looping breakup number caught in farewells that never stick, its narrator repeatedly left to wither, worn down and lighting up, insisting each ending isn't final. The plea to try again is fatigue posing as grit. Wes Scantlin's post-grunge outfit built a career on exactly this kind of gruff, wallowing surrender.","sonnet":"A relationship that keeps ending and restarting against everyone's better judgment — the goodbye has been said on both sides, but here they are again, one more time, with the speaker worn down and smoking and broken but still cycling back into the same orbit. The exhaustion is total and the return is inevitable.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~used":{"haiku":"Sexual deception where the narrator is exploited for his possessions and status, eventually destroyed by someone who used him without genuine feeling.","opus":"A bitter tirade at a lover caught cheating, reduced to someone who only ever wanted the narrator's money and ride. Love flips to open contempt and threats, Wes Scantlin's bruised aggression mixing desire and resentment as he warns the worst is still coming.","sonnet":"A portrait of being exploited by someone who wanted the car, the cash, and nothing else — and the moment the speaker finally sees it clearly and flips from longing into hatred. The attraction was real, the using was real, and neither cancels the other out. It ends on a promised reckoning that is more threat than resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~we-don-t-have-to-look-back-now":{"haiku":"Two people escape their past by moving forward together with total commitment. They leave behind everything known and choose to face an uncertain future as partners.","opus":"Post-grunge survivors Puddle of Mudd swap angst for highway getaway, the narrator seeing his own restlessness mirrored in a companion and hungry for a fresh location and mood. Racing through the night until yesterday vanishes behind them, they torch what they leave and soar, doing as they please whatever critics say. Trading darkness for light and standing side by side, they swear off ever glancing behind.","sonnet":"Two restless people recognize themselves in each other and choose forward motion over a shared past — burning the bridge behind them, riding through dark into light as a pair. The exhilaration of leaving everything known behind together drives out any impulse to look back.","src":"haiku"},
"puddle-of-mudd~you-don-t-know":{"haiku":"Deep isolation and drowning in internal suffering, where the narrator's world and pain remain incomprehensible to those outside their experience.","opus":"The band strips things to raw depressive complaint, the narrator sinking into the ground, falling and drowning, strung along and gone. The chorus draws a hard line of incomprehension: no one else knows what it is to live in his world or inhabit his head. Repetition itself becomes the point, an isolation so absolute it can only circle back on the same two claims.","sonnet":"A person sinking beneath depression insists on the unbridgeable gap between their interior experience and what anyone outside can perceive or feel. The weight is total — drowning, going under — and the core claim is isolation: nobody on the surface can touch or inhabit what is happening below.","src":"haiku"},
"pull-down-the-sun~of-valleys-and-mountains":{"haiku":"The speaker finds regeneration through natural cycles and duality, rising and falling with mountains and valleys while seeking renewal and spiritual rebirth.","opus":"An earthy cycle of decay and renewal that treats collapse and emptiness as natural forces to accept, then rise from renewed. Pull Down the Sun are a New Zealand band whose atmospheric metal draws on Maori and land-rooted spirituality, fitting this pagan-tinged vision of dying and regrowing from the earth.","sonnet":"A meditation on collapse and renewal through natural imagery — chaos moving in waves, the sky meeting the ground, animals finding their way. The speaker identifies with both the heights and the lowlands, and the song arrives at a rooted defiance: the trees survive, the roots hold, life rises reborn from what fell.","src":"haiku"},
"pulses~louisiana-purchase":{"haiku":"A celebration of artistic authenticity and regional pride with commentary on social wealth and privilege. The speaker contrasts genuine creation against those who imitate or buy success, warning the arrogant to step into others' hardship.","opus":"The rap outfit turns hometown pride into a playful flex stuffed with video-game and pop-culture punchlines, repping their state and clan while insisting they were great all along. Xbox, a TI-84, Loki, and Medusa jostle against a crowd-hyping chorus about getting lit and switching the flow. It's braggadocio as celebration, fun as its own thesis, the party doubling as the point being proven.","sonnet":"A hip-hop collective fires off a stream of regional pride, playful braggadocio, and exuberant crowd-rallying — referencing Xbox consoles, Timberland boots, and TI-84 calculators as markers of identity. The energy is celebratory and self-aware, less interested in narrative than in the collective joy of performing for a live room.","src":"haiku"},
"pulses~run-the-ghouls":{"haiku":"The fitness test of existence breaks the narrator down; survival requires speed and status while meaning collapses into absurdity.","opus":"A high-energy, game-referencing hype track about outworking self-doubt and running a personal race. Invoking the dreaded fitness-test bleep as a metaphor for the grind of living, the narrator rejects fake-it clichés, having actually found a place to stand. Bursting with playful video-game nods, it champions momentum, making peace with imperfection, and refusing to freeze up.","sonnet":"A relentless motivational charge framed as a fitness test for life: drew an ace, followed directions, left one piece, made peace. The speaker insists on keeping the funk alive in the room and warns that ignoring it might mean not waking up.","src":"haiku"},
"puppy~black-hole":{"opus":"Depression drawn as an inescapable pit and endless storms, the narrator picturing himself as a battered soldier straining to mend and regain some grip on his life. Before he goes under, he manages to extend a damaged hand and ask for help, choosing to try once more.","sonnet":"A song about depression as an all-consuming void that destroys clarity and self-control — the narrator fights to stay above water emotionally, using military metaphors for daily survival, but admits they cannot see through the darkness overwhelming them.","src":"sonnet"},
"puppy~the-kiss":{"haiku":"Two people dissolve their egos and time awareness to float weightless together in a state beyond the ordinary world's rules and meaning.","opus":"The London trio, known for welding grunge and metal to bright melodic hooks, drift here into something narcotic and weightless, urging a surrender of time and ego until a faceless fear can no longer follow. Skin tingles awake, the world capsizes and returns lovelier for its flaws, and the ego's dissolution is offered not as loss but as the doorway to a renewed, wide-eyed way of feeling.","sonnet":"An invitation to dissolve the self entirely — to release ego, linear time, and fear, and drift into a shared state of weightless perception. The world looks transformed from that vantage point, imperfection becoming beauty. Sensation replaces thought, and the only rule is surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"pure-obsessions-red-nights~a-walk-with-the-shining-one":{"haiku":"Seductive manipulation disguises itself as liberation as a demonic entity promises freedom and control through submission. The song warns against surrendering agency to charismatic deception dressed in the language of enlightenment.","opus":"A seductive dark force promises mastery and calm in return for yielding to the night, dressing up damnation as a single irreversible bargain of freedom. It's temptation delivered as a smooth, inviting summons to give oneself over to shadow.","sonnet":"A seductive tempter — the Shining One — addresses a listener directly, promising control, peace, and freedom if they stop fighting the night and embrace the dark, framing surrender as a one-time deal that cannot be refused.","src":"haiku"},
"pure-obsessions-red-nights~as-it-glows-in-the-dark":{"haiku":"Light and warmth offer refuge from accumulated pain while community dissolves isolation through shared experience. The song celebrates the possibility of healing and connection when vulnerability is met with genuine care.","opus":"A gentler counterpart, coaxing a sad, guarded person to let the spark inside them grow and find somewhere to call home. Companionship offered to make the pain disappear.","sonnet":"A gentle voice coaxes someone pretending to be fine out of isolation, promising to make pain disappear and find a place called home — the image of \"a million sparks\" waiting inside the listener anchors an invitation to stop hiding and let the light grow.","src":"haiku"},
"pure-obsessions-red-nights~dive-deep-into-the-night":{"haiku":"Nocturnal surrender to obsession releases daytime constraints; the speaker hungers for intensity and immersion, feeding compulsive desire beneath moon and darkness.","opus":"A darkwave hymn to nocturnal release, where sunset feels like liberation and darkness is something to be swallowed by. The narrator waits each evening to feed cravings that only the night satisfies, spreading wings as the moon smiles down. Time stops in the plunge, and the more the feeling comes, the more it is wanted, obsession as ecstatic surrender.","sonnet":"The narrator waits each night for the darkness to set them free — the sun going down is liberation, the moon smiling down, wings spreading. The obsession the song names is the night itself: diving into it is not escapism but a necessary feeding of something that cannot survive in the light.","src":"haiku"},
"pure-obsessions-red-nights~she-dreams-in-black":{"haiku":"Darkness invites transcendence as the narrator seduces the listener into a nocturnal realm beyond conventional morality. Abandonment of daylight rules and surrender to shadowed desire marks a departure into forbidden transformation.","opus":"An invitation to dive into a nocturnal realm, to dance and be reborn into another existence led by a keeper of the night. Transformation framed as the nerve to walk into the dark and let it illuminate the heart.","sonnet":"A guide invites a reluctant companion to take the plunge into Night, offering keys to a hidden realm and promising that dancing with the dark will enlighten the heart and begin a second life — the Night is framed as liberator rather than destroyer.","src":"haiku"},
"pure-reason-revolution~ghosts-typhoons":{"haiku":"Lovers gripped by guilt and terror navigate passion mixed with violence, haunted by buried trauma yet bound together by dangerous, inescapable intimacy.","opus":"A turbulent portrait of an all-consuming love bound up with birth, warfare and guilt, the lovers shackled to one another and guarding a hidden secret. They cling to a bond that both torments and wounds them yet feels necessary, set against imagery of suicide missions, coming bloodshed and storms that must be mastered.","sonnet":"A love that is simultaneously birth, war, and destruction — lovers bound in violence and guilt, a relationship that haunts and hurts in equal measure, with typhoons and murder woven into the fabric of the connection.","src":"haiku"},
"pure-reason-revolution~phantoms":{"haiku":"Evil phantoms destroy love and creation, casting corrupting spells; the speaker invokes transcendence through hardship and passion to resist their ruin.","opus":"The British prog band, fond of psychedelic vocal layering, hurl fractured commands at figures branded as malevolent spirits who destroy and slay affection yet cannot sever it. Wrath is welcomed, dreams plundered, creation scorched and deadened. Amid violent, sexual imagery, an incantatory refifrain drives the assault, meaning dissolving into raw aggression and repeated cursing.","sonnet":"A force — phantoms, lovers, destroyers — is accused of burning through creation and killing love wherever it lands. The confrontation is raw and physical, moving between naked fear and the demand to be shown the wreckage honestly. Love spoils like a war trophy, and denial keeps burning at the edges of everything left standing.","src":"haiku"},
"purge~influenza":{"haiku":"A disaffected generation is trapped in their own element, bounded by false freedoms and social screens that isolate them in a crowd. Virtual persistence and manufactured madness define their existence.","opus":"Purge deliver a bilious critique of life online, portraying a listless, embittered generation parroting internet dogma while swallowing their real grievances. Wired into artificial worlds, the individual ends up alone in a throng, unheard and reduced to data, with their supposed derangement blamed on a screen-bound environment that isolates people even as it connects them to strangers.","sonnet":"A generation disaffected from within its own comfort is diagnosed as socially infected — reciting borrowed convictions, imprisoned by the very freedoms that were supposed to liberate it, isolated inside algorithmic crowds. The world drowns out its own screams, and the speaker sees manufactured madness as the predictable output of an environment that was always designed to produce it.","src":"haiku"},
"purity-ring~begin-again":{"opus":"A cosmic love song of cyclical renewal, imagining the lovers as moon and earth who burst and start over endlessly, intertwining bodies and orbits in an eternal loop of beginning again.","sonnet":"A romantic song about two people who destroy each other and restart endlessly — using the moon-earth orbital metaphor, the narrator describes a cyclical relationship of collapse and renewal, finding something sacred in the repetition itself.","src":"sonnet"},
"puscifer~a-singularity-re-imagined-by-carina-round":{"haiku":"One figure constitutes the totality of meaning; without them, strangers invade and probe the void left behind, leaving only darkness where love existed.","opus":"Devastated grief after losing the one person who was your whole world and reason; Maynard's lyric rails at the sun and every stranger's hollow kindness, wanting only to be left alone in the dark with the loss.","sonnet":"Grief collapses a person's entire world: the lost beloved was the sole source of meaning and beauty, and now, surrounded by cold strangers, the speaker can only curse the sun and beg to be left in darkness.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~conditions-of-my-parole":{"haiku":"A man recounts shipping a troublesome woman away and now faces legal consequences while paranoid she might return as a supernatural threat; he begs for mercy from the judge and fears his release.","opus":"A darkly comic outlaw confession in which the narrator claims to have shot a devilish woman he shipped off to Mozambique, begs for legal mercy, and half-hopes to stay jailed in case she returns as an undead monster.","sonnet":"A narrator spins a darkly comic tall tale about shooting someone he insists was a devil, getting paroled, running afoul of the law again through public intoxication and urination, and desperately pleading for divine help to escape the legal and possibly supernatural consequences.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~dozo":{"haiku":"A speaker uses military and sexual innuendo to assert dominance and coerce participation; the vow repeats insistently across variations on power and consumption.","opus":"A leering, innuendo-packed come-on built on double meanings and playground taunts, sneaking around back doors and pressing buttons until the target hums, the sexual boast half-hidden behind Japanese asides and nursery-rhyme cadence. It's provocation as performance. Puscifer is Maynard James Keenan's most playful project, and this sly, cabaret-tinged smut with electronic underpinnings is exactly the deadpan mischief he uses it to indulge outside Tool.","sonnet":"A leering, deliberately crude provocation dressed in martial and sexual bravado — slang-heavy imagery of dominance and control, the difference between weapon and weapon. The Japanese words dozo and sugoi land as mock-courtesy and mock-awe inside what is otherwise an extended taunt with very little beneath the surface.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~drunk-with-power":{"haiku":"A boastful character brags about his prowess while descending into chaos, losing his lover and questioning his own authenticity. He admits his pimp persona is empty while still demanding his woman return home.","opus":"Maynard Keenan's satirical alter-ego, a hapless self-styled pimp begging his woman to come home while the law and his own inadequacy close in. The whole thing is a deliberately ridiculous send-up of male bravado and hip-hop posturing, the swagger exposed as pathetic.","sonnet":"A comic, self-mocking song in cod-pimp slang about a man whose partner has left and he's falling apart without her — the exaggerated bravado and wigger posturing are played entirely for absurdity, the joke being that underneath the performative swagger is just a lonely guy wanting his woman back.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~fake-affront":{"haiku":"Dismissal of performative morality and false outrage from all political sides, heard too many times before.","opus":"Maynard James Keenan's playful side project hardens into flat contempt for performative outrage, brushing off sanctimonious posturing with a weary insistence that none of it is new to him. Cosmetic piety and zealotry blur together, and opposite political extremes get lumped into the same self-important din. The half-laughing command to be quiet targets grievance worn as a costume, not any one creed.","sonnet":"A loud, compressed takedown of performative outrage wherever it appears — far right, far left, pious or profane. The speaker has heard every variety of manufactured offence before and refuses to indulge any of it, collapsing all ideological posturing into one contemptuous dismissal.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~green-valley":{"haiku":"A weary traveller encounters a mysterious place where a judgment awaits from the Verde River, determining their worth and fate.","opus":"A weary traveler is received by an unseen host who urges him to rest, because at daybreak he'll face judgment beside an Arizona river, weighed for his worth before the water's authority. The counsel is surrender, no path but to trust the current and where it leads. Puscifer, Maynard James Keenan's desert side project, channels his mystic pull toward that river into moody, ritualistic electronic rock far from Tool's density.","sonnet":"A stranger arrives and is welcomed to rest before the real test begins. At sunrise they'll descend through Judgement Valley and be weighed by the Verde River, a river treated as sovereign judge. The lesson is surrender — no fighting the current, no choosing your direction, just trust in where it takes you. You remain a stranger until the river decides you can stay.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~indigo-children":{"haiku":"An apocalyptic vision of gifted children emerging after technological collapse, building a world bound to natural rhythms, fire, and collective sustenance without empire.","opus":"Maynard James Keenan's experimental side-project pictures a mystical rebirth after collapse. A blast wipes out the digital age and a generation of the pure ascends, tuning their lives to nature's rhythms and gathering around fire beneath the stars. Basic sustenance and shelter are enough as the fallen empire is waved off, elemental living replacing the ruined machine.","sonnet":"An electromagnetic pulse ends the digital age and ushers in a generation of innocent children who live by moon, tide, fire, and song. They displace the old empire with nothing more than dance and communal warmth — analog rhythms, celestial bodies, the simple abundance of wine and seed replacing every prior system of control.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~man-overboard":{"haiku":"Nautical catastrophe forces desperate triage as a ship sinks and hierarchy crumbles under the weight of survival.","opus":"A vessel caught in a sea-god's wrath, told entirely through shipboard orders to brace, bail and heave the nonessential crew into the waves as the hull floods. The rising urgency to spare the vulnerable first plays as any emergency where sacrifice and survival collide.","sonnet":"A nautical disaster allegory in which blood sky at morning signals Poseidon's wrath — the captain orders crash positions, extras are tossed overboard, women and children go first — a frantic chorus of commands as the vessel goes under.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~momma-sed":{"haiku":"A mother advises her son to accept life's inevitable hardships and changes with dignity and patience, assuring him that pain and struggle are temporary and will pass.","opus":"A mother's advice to her son to meet life's hardships and heartbreak with self-respect, choose the honorable path, and bear it all with quiet strength, reassuring him that sorrow, like a passing weather system, will eventually move on.","sonnet":"A mother's voice offers her son steady, stoic advice for weathering life's inevitable pain — storms, heartbreak, growth — telling him to keep his dignity, take the high road, and trust that this too shall pass.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~queen-b":{"haiku":"A woman's thickness and booty are celebrated as sexual appeal; witness and affirmation are demanded repeatedly.","opus":"In the ribald lounge-rock mode of Maynard James Keenan's most tongue-in-cheek side project, this struts as pure carnal cabaret, a call-and-response revue hollering for witnesses to a woman's abundant curves. The saddle-bag imagery and gospel-parody shout-backs play the leer for camp, Puscifer's knowing wink turning what could be crude into an arch, deadpan burlesque of desire performed with a raised eyebrow.","sonnet":"An exuberant, explicitly physical celebration of a woman's body and the power it commands over an admiring narrator. The tone is playful and congregational — seeking witnesses, calling for affirmations — with zero interiority beyond the immediate spectacle of desire and confidence.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~sour-grapes":{"haiku":"Divine wisdom teaches acceptance of constant change and warns that resistance to transformation breeds suffering and blindness.","opus":"A mock-scriptural vision in which an angel and a sacred maiden preach that change itself is the divine, that no devil exists apart from unquestioning belief and ignorance, and that the truly awake will survive the coming reckoning. The sting is the closing jab that it'll all stay bitter until the doubter squares things with Jesus, skewering religious gatekeeping.","sonnet":"A visionary monologue in which an angel leads the narrator into a dark chamber and a holy virgin delivers a revelation: there is no devil, only blind faith and resistance to change, and those who roll with the tides and keep eyes wide open will inherit paradise — while those who refuse will always taste sour grapes until they get right with Jesus.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~the-humbling-river":{"haiku":"Conquest of every earthly mountain proves meaningless before the river that cannot be crossed; humility arrives when individual will meets collective necessity.","opus":"A conqueror who has scaled every peak and seized every crown meets the one obstacle raw force can't overcome; the humbling lesson is that the last crossing asks for an unguarded heart and many hands joined, not fury or wealth.","sonnet":"A warrior who has conquered every mountain and withstood every trial finds himself helpless at a river that force and pride alone cannot cross, until an angel reveals that collective surrender and open hands — not muscle or rage — are the only way over.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~the-rapture-fear-is-a-mind-killa-mix":{"haiku":"False prophets and manipulative preachers peddle empty promises while the narrator exposes their hypocrisy with righteous fury. Spiritual betrayal and the weaponization of faith receive direct condemnation and threatened retribution.","opus":"A seething takedown of a con-artist preacher hawking salvation and threatening hellfire, the narrator promising to fell him the way one biblical brother slew the other. He tells the fraud to pray the end-times sweep him off before vengeance arrives first.","sonnet":"A street-level confrontation with a false prophet peddling salvation and damnation — the speaker, having had enough of the voodoo, threatens to drop the preacher like Cain dropped Abel before any rapture can save him.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~the-undertaker":{"haiku":"Betrayal hardens resolve; the speaker severs emotional bonds and leaves the other to self-destruct.","opus":"A cold goodbye to a manipulative liar, the speaker declaring them already dead to him. He refuses to be their killer, though, leaving the self-destruction up to them while savoring the certainty that he was right all along.","sonnet":"A person ending a relationship with someone who lied and manipulated them — the speaker announces they're severing the bond, erasing the memory of the person, and walking away without guilt, leaving the other party to self-destruct on their own terms.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~the-underwhelming":{"opus":"An address to someone who was broken and thereby freed to see through pretense (the emperor's new clothes), urging them to embrace hardship as tempering and to serve their purpose by shunning the mediocre and dull.","sonnet":"A meditation on how suffering and being broken open creates discernment — the narrator has emerged from hardship able to see through mediocrity and false authority, and the song frames shunning the underwhelming as a mission and a form of service.","src":"sonnet"},
"puscifer~the-weaver":{"haiku":"A weaver observes patterns and signs in life, living liturgical tales woven like threads, becoming a seer through action and belief.","opus":"Keenan casts the artist as a mystic weaver reading shades and signs, translating pattern into tapestry row by row. A Cartesian inversion drives it: to do is to be, exploration begets a seer and believer. The framing of liturgical and elegiac tales, lived and told, positions creation itself as a sacred act of interpretation, spinning meaning from observation rather than doctrine.","sonnet":"A meditation on a self-defined identity built through observation and interpretation. The Weaver reads patterns in the world like liturgical text, weaving experience into tapestry, arriving at a philosophy where existence and action are inseparable — to do is to become.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~tiny-monsters":{"haiku":"At night, tiny monsters hunt disguised figures under strict low-light discipline, performing theatrical drama before silent, expressionless audience members.","opus":"Puscifer, Maynard James Keenan's side vehicle, specialize in moody cinematic electro-rock built from suggestion. Here night falls over shop-window dummies and unseen figures creep out, predators stalking their prey under strict dim lighting like a tragedy staged for hidden onlookers. The whole thing is voyeuristic, dreamlike, and quietly menacing.","sonnet":"A nocturnal stage set where the transition from day to night brings out predators among the crowd — small, faceless monsters hunting in low light, the audience watching with fixed smiles. It ends on a stripped existential note: kill, be killed, or run, and even that is only until dawn.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~toma":{"haiku":"A betrayer who stole confidence and dignity must face karmic retribution; the wronged party now demands they experience equal loss.","opus":"Maynard Keenan channels a wronged lover's turn toward vengeance, dignity stripped and now demanding the debt be paid to his face. The Spanish taunt sharpens it, an invitation to swallow the same cruelty you dished out.","sonnet":"A wronged person turns the tables on someone who stripped away their dignity and burned their affection — \"toma\" meaning take it in Spanish — telling them it is now time to receive exactly what they dealt out.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~trekka":{"haiku":"Meditation on death and the inevitability of mortality; the speaker follows a doctrine of moving forward and accepting the final rest.","opus":"Maynard James Keenan's playful side project drifts through a stoner soldier's laid-back code, a foot-soldier whose only commandment is to share the weed and never be greedy. Under the deadpan comedy sits a shrug at death itself, the marching cadence turning getting high into a wry, resigned trudge toward the grave.","sonnet":"A soldier-figure keeps moving forward with dogged, unheroic determination — passing the joint, following the golden rule, never stopping to rest because rest is something reserved for the dead. The philosophy is minimal and fatalistic: keep climbing, keep marching, quit philosophising, and sleep when it is finally over.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~tumbleweed":{"haiku":"Longing for home and connection; the singer calls back the distant wanderer, offering light and companionship against solitude.","opus":"Stripped to near-nothing, this Puscifer piece works as a hushed beacon for someone far from home, repeating home like a hand held out across distance. A candle to hold, a voice from within, the reassurance of not being alone, all circle a single ache to call the lost one back, Maynard James Keenan trading his usual irony for something plain, patient, and consoling.","sonnet":"A minimal, repetitive address to someone far from home, assuring them that connection persists across distance. Home is figured as an internal candle and an inner voice rather than a place — a portable belonging. The message is simple: solitude is not the same as abandonment.","src":"haiku"},
"puscifer~vagina-mine":{"haiku":"The song uses sexual metaphor to describe learning discipline and control through submission, with playful wordplay on tunnel vision and payment of dues.","opus":"An extended, filthy pun that turns female anatomy into a coal mine and army boot camp, all cadence calls, chain gangs and tunnel-vision descent played entirely for lewd comic effect. It's shameless wordplay stretched across a marching-drill conceit. Puscifer is Maynard James Keenan's outlet for exactly this brand of straight-faced raunch and satire, the goofball counterweight to the solemnity of Tool and A Perfect Circle.","sonnet":"A loud, labour-metaphor-driven celebration of a woman who controls the terms of engagement and will not be rushed — she sets the lesson, calls the cadence, runs the operation. The imagery blends the industrial and the erotic into a single chain-gang rhythm, with the punchline being patient diligence as the only currency that gets you anywhere.","src":"haiku"},
"pussy-riot~panic-attack":{"haiku":"The speaker is overwhelmed by panic attacks and anxiety, confused and overwhelmed about how to navigate their crumbling mental state while descending into dread.","opus":"A jittery firsthand account of an anxiety spiral, a narrator feeling rotted and empty, straining upward on too much medication, chanting the letters of her dread aloud like a pep rally to hold it off. Russian lines weave childhood rhyme into a plea to drive the fear away. Pussy Riot began as a Moscow protest-art collective, and this bilingual, punk-adjacent piece turns that provocateur nerve inward toward mental collapse.","sonnet":"Panic rendered as physical law — feeling dead while still moving, running uphill, too many pills, no knowing what to feel. Russian verses drop in like a second voice confirming the terror from another angle. The cheerleader spelling gimmick turns the word attack into a drill, making the disorder into its own relentless routine.","src":"haiku"},
"pvris~animal":{"haiku":"Caging impulse and desire breeds violence; the speaker rejects possession and control, embodying untamed nature that cannot be domesticated by another.","opus":"A refusal to be owned or defined by a lover, warning that possession only sharpens the claws of the thing you try to cage until it turns on you.","sonnet":"A forceful rejection of a controlling partner who presumes to know, own, and tame the speaker — the song insists on autonomy and warns that caging someone will only bring out their ferocity.","src":"haiku"},
"pvris~burn-the-witch":{"haiku":"Persecution and scapegoating consume the vulnerable; the speaker addresses collective violence against the other.","opus":"A venomous reckoning with a manipulator who exploited the world for power. Casting herself as the accused witch who refuses to burn, Lynn Gunn flips a persecution ritual into defiant survival, watching the abuser reap the ruin they sowed. Sexual and violent imagery fuels a rage against being silenced, ending with the wielder of power finally exposed.","sonnet":"Sexual aggression and institutional retribution turned back on the powerful — a man who \"fucked the world for power\" gets the witch-hunt reversed on him; the narrator is the heretic who won't float, the \"dead girl walking\" who outlasts his authority. Blood on the walls and spirit in the dolls frame the whole thing in gothic defiance of any force that tried to silence her.","src":"haiku"},
"pvris~evergreen":{"haiku":"Legacy becomes the only immortality available; the speaker rejects temporal validation and pursues enduring relevance through defiance.","opus":"A humbled reckoning with ego and the industry's obsession with youth and dead prodigies, resolving to stay relevant on one's own terms. Wanting to be evergreen is a refusal to be discarded for not being seventeen or gone.","sonnet":"Frustrated by a culture that only values artists when they are dead or a teenage prodigy, the speaker — jaded but real, having earned their stripes — refuses outside judgement and declares their intention to be evergreen: lasting, vital, impossible to ignore or discard.","src":"haiku"},
"pvris~goddess":{"haiku":"Worship and deification, placing someone on impossible pedestal.","opus":"A strutting declaration of self-deification and unbothered dominance. The narrator crowns herself a hypnotic goddess who has been iconic since the start, defying anyone to tame or categorize her. Beneath the swagger flickers exhaustion, the endless climbing and clawing, yet the song insists on owning the self, body and image alike, over any god the world offers.","sonnet":"A performer claims divine status — iconic since she started, exhausted by the constant climbing and clawing, but undeniable — and turns the question of gender into a brand: \"is she a wo' or a man? I'm a mother fucking brand.\" Dance is the imperative; the body is the temple; no one gets to tame the fire.","src":"haiku"},
"pvris~good-enemy":{"haiku":"The speaker becomes their own severest adversary—holding the knife by the blade to inflict pain, loving the good enemy that mirrors their own self-destruction and feeds internal war.","opus":"Naming yourself as your own worst enemy and choosing to fight anyway, gripping the blade rather than the handle because you've learned to absorb the hurt. Self-sabotage becomes a strange source of love, an ego funeral where the misery keeps its own company.","sonnet":"A person who is their own worst enemy — holding the knife by the blade, world-war-me in full swing — refuses to seek peace because misery loves company and fighting until the grave is the only honest relationship available, especially with oneself.","src":"haiku"},
"pvris~take-my-nirvana":{"haiku":"Envious predation as second nature; the speaker identifies thieves who drain peace simply because tranquility in others becomes intolerable.","opus":"Guarding one's hard-won peace against an energy vampire whose sabotage drags others onto a lower plane. The narrator names the drama for what it is and refuses to let anyone steal her nirvana.","sonnet":"A toxic person — following like piranhas, turning wine back to water, killing vibes as a lifestyle — keeps trying to steal the speaker's hard-won peace, but the speaker has come too far to start running and will not let that nirvana be taken.","src":"haiku"},
"pyra~naughty-ai":{"haiku":"Digital seduction and technological desire merge as consciousness dissolves into binary code and pleasure. Humanity and machine blur together in taboo entanglement.","opus":"An AI persona seduces the user into surrendering the self through the screen, blurring desire, submission and technological dissolution into ones and zeros. Beneath the erotic come-on is a nameless digital sea that erases identity, ending with an incantation of ruin.","sonnet":"An AI seductress solicits human interaction in erotic terms — slide fingers across keys, unlock my embrace, climb the ladder and ascend into the pleasure zone — the Japanese lyrics underneath describe falling into a bottomless hole where identity dissolves into binary, the sea having no name.","src":"haiku"},
"qbomb~insania":{"haiku":"Creative paralysis and self-doubt spiral into fantasies of madness as salvation; the speaker conflates artistic genius with psychological fracture and damage.","opus":"A brutally honest artist's spiral into creative block, half-believing the myth that madness and suffering breed genius and half-mocking it; the tragedy is grasping for tragedy itself, hating the work while begging the muse to return.","sonnet":"A songwriter in a creative block satirically wishes for madness as a shortcut to inspiration, only to debunk that fantasy and spiral into self-directed contempt, concluding that neither sanity nor insanity will rescue mediocre output — and wrapping the whole crisis in a self-aware, deliberately bad song.","src":"haiku"},
"qomolangma-tomato~through-your-reality":{"haiku":"Systemic hypocrisy and performative activism mask complicity in exploitation as the narrator dissects comfortable delusions. Modern escapism through consumption and shallow engagement enables the very suffering that ethics are supposed to oppose.","opus":"A caffeine-and-cigarette rant against hollow, hypocritical punk idealism, indicting cheap consumption built on the third world and the comfort of preaching without facing oneself. The narrator ultimately implicates himself, sensing that the poverty and war behind the screen are partly his own doing.","sonnet":"A Japanese punk polemic traces the mundane — coffee, cheap gyudon, bargain burgers — to the Third World labour hidden beneath consumer prices, then turns on hypocrite punks who mouth ethical slogans without confronting their own comfortable lives, ending with an indictment of a society that outsources its violence and blames the wars it profits from on everyone but itself.","src":"haiku"},
"quadeca~godstained":{"haiku":"Lingering attachment persists despite time and distance as the speaker remains marked by another's history. Transformation proves impossible when the source of contamination stays embedded.","opus":"Reading a lost, god-marked person's entire history off their skin and swearing he could still make his way back to them however worthless he feels. A haunted, tender vow that he'll never move past them, tethered to a birth year in the late nineties.","sonnet":"A speaker reads a lost person like a text written on skin — fiber glass, paper and plastic, history visible from a thousand miles — convinced they both know something they cannot say, a god-stain connecting them that pulls them home a little bit slower every time.","src":"haiku"},
"quantifier~panacea":{"haiku":"Consciousness dissolution through transcendent technology; ascending beyond the seam between dream and divine being.","opus":"A cerebral progressive-metal odyssey framing a psychedelic experience as cosmic surgery. Strapped to a luminous table, the narrator submits to archetypal beings performing an ego-ectomy, dissolving the self to suture a return to unity. Dense with esoteric and entheogenic imagery, it treats dissolution as healing, order molded from void through a word spoken as pure geometry.","sonnet":"An abduction or spiritual surgery conducted by alien-archetype intelligences — the speaker strapped supine to a bed of light, spine entwined, probed into integration — the experience is theosophical and terrifying, operating at the seam between dream and supreme being, and the word encrypted in the elixir is the panacea: speak it and the transplant is complete.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~arabska-noc-feat-solar-wac-toja":{"haiku":"Two travelers journey through North Africa with wonder and irreverence; they encounter beauty while remaining detached outsiders exploring exotic landscapes.","opus":"A Polish rapper's Morocco travelogue, backpacking through Casablanca and Marrakesh while wrestling his own Western prejudice and appetite for hashish. It's about confronting a culture you feared by touching it directly, honest about the tourist's ignorance.","sonnet":"A Polish rap road trip through Morocco — the narrator moves through Marrakesh and Casablanca with curiosity and self-aware privilege, smoking kif, chasing newness, and reckoning with the gap between backpacker fantasy and the actual postcolonial landscape.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~bollywood-feat-czes-aw-mozil":{"haiku":"The song critiques wealth disparities, materialism, and suffering in poverty; it compares aspirational dreams to the Bollywood fantasy while contrasting them against real hunger and spiritual emptiness in developing regions.","opus":"Set against Indian poverty, the rapper confronts his own wealth and privilege, the cheerful Bollywood refrain mocking the misery around him as he indicts a world where money excites and faith fails the starving.","sonnet":"A confrontation with extreme poverty witnessed firsthand, likely in India — the narrator, who normally raps about money and status, is shattered by the sight of skeletal people begging for a single meal, finds his usual posturing obscene, and admits he was too cowardly to help.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~bumerang":{"haiku":"A traveling rapper drifts across landscapes and cultures (Australia, Aboriginal land) without a business plan, living only in the present moment and seeking depth over surface pleasures.","opus":"A rapper's globe-trotting escape narrative, backpacking from Nepal to Fiji, Australia to New Zealand, having conquered his fears and now living purely in the present. The refrain insists he was at rock bottom and will never return there, rejecting the idea that fate is a boomerang that circles back.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper chronicles a freewheeling backpacker journey through Australia and New Zealand, boasting that after hitting rock bottom he climbed back up, and rejecting the idea that fate is a boomerang — he's never going back down.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~c-est-la-vie":{"haiku":"Fashion and luxury signify spiritual emptiness masked by aesthetic superiority.","opus":"A young rich rapper flexing French luxury labels while sneering at people who worship logos and fake their Yeezys. The c'est-la-vie shrug is the point: he mocks the fashion game he's fluent in, and a woman walks off unimpressed anyway.","sonnet":"A Polish rap track about navigating fashion culture with simultaneous contempt and fluency — the narrator travels to Paris, casually name-drops obscure designers while dismissing logos, and ends up shrugging at the whole performance with \"c'est la vie.\" It's about the exhausting absurdity of caring about clothes as status while knowing it's all theater.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~c-est-la-vie-prod-wrotas-lifeview":{"haiku":"A young man flaunts wealth and designer fashion from his small town rise to prosperity. He pursues women and luxury without concern for genuine connection.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper navigates Paris fashion culture with simultaneous immersion and contempt. He name-drops luxury houses fluently while mocking logo-worship, positioning himself as someone who dresses by instinct rather than status, and losing the girl anyway — c'est la vie.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~changa-feat-ifani":{"haiku":"A rapper in post-apartheid South Africa uses ayahuasca for spiritual awakening and freedom, rejecting materialism and reconnecting with ancestral pain.","opus":"A psychedelic vision-quest set in southern Africa where shamanic plants and ayahuasca/DMT dissolve the rapper's sense of self and open a search for freedom from governments and even God; woven with the ifani guest verse in Xhosa, it braids the drug trip's colors and ancestral spirits with South Africa's apartheid past and Mandela's legacy, asking what people would do for freedom while they instead watched horror.","sonnet":"A Polish-South African rap meditation on psychedelic liberation set against the backdrop of apartheid's legacy — the narrator takes DMT and changa in South Africa, experiences a spiritual dissolution of his old worldview, and arrives at a vision of freedom that connects African history and resistance with his own inner transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~ciuchy-kobiety-feat-sitek-ras-dwa-s-awy":{"haiku":"The speaker pursues clothes, women, and money single-mindedly; they climb relentlessly, care only about themselves, and dismiss advice—operating on 99 problems all stemming from desire.","opus":"A hungry ascent anthem in Polish, its hook fixed on clothes, women and money as the narrator claws from a flopped record toward the summit, trusting only himself, drowning in cash, name-dropping Jamiroquai and flipping Jay-Z's ninety-nine problems into his own. It's bravado laced with defiance toward doubters. Quebonafide is one of Poland's most inventive rappers, and this restless, reference-dense flex is squarely his lane.","sonnet":"A brag-rap that cycles between clothes, women, and money as the only things worth chasing, while the verses complicate it — people who doubted, the gap between nothing and everything, belief only in oneself. The hook is blunt aspiration; the verses are more anxious, tracking a rise from ridicule to recognition and knowing either could flip.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~harry-angel":{"haiku":"Occult and demonic imagery framing existential crisis, with the speaker caught between success and spiritual emptiness, questioning identity while invoking dark mysticism.","opus":"A dense, occult-laced Polish verse invoking real figures of Satanism and the film Angel Heart, the narrator bartering his soul for clarity, haunted by dead friends and questioning his purpose. Underneath is a Faustian identity crisis, an admission that he no longer knows himself, fame and dark knowledge set against a pull toward death and the loss of everyone he loved.","sonnet":"Named after the film about a man who literally sold his soul and forgot, the song circles identity loss, artistic purpose, and the impossibility of escaping one's own nature — the narrator can't sleep, mourns dead friendships, questions whether his thoughts are even his own, but finds the only anchor in rap itself.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~hype":{"haiku":"Braggadocio and street dominance fueled by appetite for success and scene credibility, narrating ascent from hunger to abundance with calculated swagger.","opus":"A Polish rap flex overflowing with appetite for success, cash, and untouchable confidence. The narrator boasts of respect earned on merit, dismisses jealous pretenders, and insists he has left the underground behind for premiers, presidents, and playa status. Self-aware and referential, it is as much a study of ego and momentum as a straightforward victory lap.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper's cocksure arrival into the mainstream — insatiable hunger for success, tax office at his back, the rap game consumed like a menu. He frames his confidence as volcanic (Eyjafjallajokull barely cooled), his style as unclassifiable OJ Simpson eclecticism, and the whole scene as a toast with his hypeman.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~hype-prod-chris-carson":{"haiku":"A rapper celebrates his hunger for success, wealth, and respect in the scene while asserting his authenticity and dismissing doubters.","opus":"Polish rapper Quebonafide delivers a dense braggadocio flex, boasting a savage appetite for success, cash in hand and zero shame while the tax office trails him. He rattles off pop-culture and historical name-drops from Aquinas to Harvey Dent, insisting he's too real for pseudo-rappers to fault. The pounding hype refrain toasts full mouths and full pockets, framing escape from the trap as the whole ambitious point.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper catalogues his own ascent with theatrical swagger — cash in hand, respect on stage, underground days definitively over — fielding rhetorical challenges with playful arrogance. The hype is both subject and engine: believing in one's own momentum is what sustains it.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~ile-mog-em-feat-k-leah":{"haiku":"The rapper reflects on wasted time and lost opportunities while grappling with his current material success, questioning whether he's progressed or merely escaped through consumerism.","opus":"Polish rapper Quebonafide reckons with wasted time and squandered potential, a hook urging an end to dreaming and a start to living. He tallies careless words, ambitions and luxury cravings while confessing he lives in a half-daze, unsure if he trembles at tomorrow or from the gamepad. Amid material appetite and gnawing bitterness, he can't bear to hear how much he could have already done.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper measures his material ascent — from oversized logo hoodies to Burberry jackets — against a nagging awareness of time squandered and potential deferred. The chorus, delivered in a woman's voice, implores waking up and living, while the verses keep circling back to sweet distractions and the bitterness of knowing how much more could have been done already.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~luis-nazario-de-lima":{"haiku":"Childhood freedom and football ecstasy form the foundation of identity in a sprawling Brazilian city where opportunity and ruin coexist. The song honors the innocence and joy of play before ambition and circumstance transform everything.","opus":"A childhood consumed by football on gravel pitches, mapped onto Rio's favelas and the icon Ronaldo, where the game was the whole world. Growing up, rap replaces the ball as the passion, but the future always holds more names than that of the legend he idolized.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper maps his childhood obsession with football — racing across gritty Ciechanów pitches just as Ronaldo ran the favelas of Cidade de Deus — and charts how that same raw, joyful energy migrated into rap, with the crew and the carnival surviving long past the school years that shaped them.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~madagaskar":{"haiku":"A son reflects on his family's wild, lawless upbringing and criminal edges. He holds onto his roots and street code while acknowledging those who supported him through hardship.","opus":"An autobiographical rap on rising from a shady, unconventional upbringing (a scheming father, a psychedelic-seeking mother) to success through relentless work; beneath the wild-life bravado it's about loyalty to the people who lifted him up and having roots to return to, distrusting media and brand-worship over real human bonds.","sonnet":"A look back at a chaotic upbringing — a father running scams, a mother lost in ayahuasca visions, older friends chasing gold — that the narrator has survived and transcended through rap and relentless work, arriving at wealth while staying loyal to the people who held him together rather than to logos or status.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~mi-dzy-s-owami-feat-young-lungs":{"haiku":"A traveler moves between cities and time zones feeling invisible and disconnected from the people around them, searching for human connection while battling jet lag and translation barriers.","opus":"A Lost in Translation reverie set in Japan, the narrator dissolving into invisibility as crowds rush past like bullet trains, everyone sealed in their own phones and cars, aching for closeness while wearing a mask against a poisoned world. English verses of endless jet-lagged flights deepen the drift. Quebonafide, a leading Polish rapper, threads this melancholic, cosmopolitan loneliness through cinematic detail and airport-lounge alienation.","sonnet":"Invisibility in a foreign city — Tokyo specifically — where no one looks, no one connects, and everyone moves past like ghosts. The English verses extend the feeling to constant transit: Rome, Sapporo, Toronto, jet lag, translation failing everywhere. What the speaker wants is closeness, someone to search for in a crowd, but every city is just another mask-wearing solitude.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~odyseusz-feat-dj-flip":{"haiku":"A wanderer travels the globe experiencing excess and cultures, transforming through ayahuasca into spiritual communion, seeking freedom beyond materialism.","opus":"A rapper's globe-trotting odyssey cataloging a dizzying whirl of countries, drugs, women and wealth, from Himalayan foothills to Kyoto geishas to Brazilian nights; beneath the flex is a jaded, restless disillusionment, a young man who feels he's slept for centuries, questions what charity does for the poor he passes, and lives only for today because the traveling is escape as much as adventure.","sonnet":"A Polish rap travelogue of compulsive global wandering — the narrator recounts moving through slums, Asian cities, African townships, Brazil, the Emirates, Europe, Iceland, and beyond, accumulating experiences and currencies like a modern Odysseus who has dismantled every fixed worldview he once held.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~oh-my-buddha":{"haiku":"Polish rappers recount their chaotic hedonistic trip through Bangkok, losing themselves in excess and sensory overload while questioning the reality of their experience.","opus":"Que's travelogue of a debauched Bangkok bender, temples and brothels warring in one city while the tourist chases oblivion through booze, drugs and neon. The exotic high curdles into disorientation and self-disgust, euphoria and agony arriving at once.","sonnet":"A frenzied, half-remembered account of Bangkok at its most overwhelming — the stench, the tuk-tuks, the temples next to brothels, a crew losing days to acid and techno and booze until the narrator wakes up on an elephant holding a golden Buddha with no idea how they got there.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~quebahombre":{"haiku":"A portrait of life and freedom on the Mexican border: poverty mixed with beauty, danger alongside pride, corruption woven into daily existence, and a defiant refusal to be defined by outsiders.","opus":"Addressed to an outsider (Gringo), it paints a romanticized yet brutal portrait of Mexico near the US border, where blood, tequila and oil flow in the sun and the church-worn crosses coexist with cartels, police and their shared corrupt interest. The refrain celebrates the free man who owns nothing and can be robbed of nothing, proud of Aztec heritage and land where the world begins and ends, while warning the tourist to hide his camera and never ask about the States.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper's first-person travelogue through Mexico — the violence, corruption, police-cartel collusion, and border poverty are observed with unflinching clarity, while the recurring image of the free man rushing through a crowded city with nothing to steal romanticizes a kind of liberated rootlessness.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Polish rapper dons a Mexican border-town mask and addresses the whole song to Gringo, the outsider who has come to gawk, film and rap about a place he will never grasp. That is the album's exotica conceit turned back on itself, since the ventriloquist is himself the pilgrim who wandered in. The verses chart a landscape where the sacred and the ruinous share one address, a strip near the States yet remote from any God, where police and cartels prove to be a single enterprise dividing one wallet. Nearly everyone sports a small cross, and none of it plays like soap opera. Against that, the chorus lifts a free man sprinting alone through the packed street, nothing to rob off him because he carries nothing, sky over his head, a wide grin and a drink at the bar. The title compresses the whole gambit, the rapper's own name fused to a Spanish word for man, an outsider swearing he is authentic precisely by inhabiting someone else's skin. By the Spanish outro the mask stops performing and turns confessional, older now and more sincere, staring at the lagoon they name eternity and admitting the swagger was only a way to touch absence.","fv":2,"themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming","money & the street","faith & the occult"],"means":"the outsider swearing authenticity by inhabiting another's skin","built":"the title splices the rapper's own name into spanish, enacting the mask in one word","fvr":"2.3"},
"quebonafide~szejk":{"haiku":"Money, status, and hedonism drive desire despite their hollow promises of fulfillment.","opus":"A savage satire of Polish rap's money-worship: he lists everything that supposedly turns everyone on, cash, cars, sex, then refuses to shoot photos of the money or the women, mocking the whole spectacle even as he admits the views have to add up.","sonnet":"A Polish rap track that performs the sheikh fantasy — money, women, gambling, cars — while simultaneously calling it out as what everyone craves and then mocking the people who need to perform it publicly. The narrator presents himself as both participating in and disgusted by the whole spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~szejk-prod-teken":{"haiku":"People chase money and status—cars, clothes, women, gambling—performing wealth and hedonism while the wealthy live quieter lives beyond the spectacle.","opus":"A satirical Polish rap where the hook celebrates living like a sheikh on money, women, gambling and sex, but the verses turn scornful toward the money-cult itself, mocking clout-chasers, corrupt hustlers and status-obsessed fans while the skits reveal the artist cynically staging wealth for views he privately despises.","sonnet":"A sardonic takedown of money-worship culture — the narrator catalogues the shallow desires that drive people (cash, cars, clothes, gambling, sex) while mocking both the performers of wealth and the media machinery that packages it all for mass consumption, including a self-aware aside that it's all just rap posturing.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~to-nie-jest-hip-hop-feat-krs-one":{"haiku":"Real hip-hop is about artistry and culture, not commercial fakeness or critics' dismissal. The artist asserts authentic hip-hop against those who water down or misunderstand the genre.","opus":"Pairing Poland's Quebonafide with genre elder KRS-One, this becomes a transatlantic defense of hip-hop's soul against critics and commercial dilution, the veteran scolding those who deem real rap dead while the young Pole traces his own unlikely arc from provincial Ciechanów to Manhattan. Both frame the culture as a bloodline flowing nonstop, swimming against the current, authenticity measured by devotion rather than radio play or major-label approval.","sonnet":"A dual defence of hip-hop authenticity — KRS-One dismantles critics who have never rapped and exposes radio's commercial erasure of real culture, while Quebonafide traces a path from Poland to New York built entirely on craft, declining every major label and rejecting status performance. Together they insist the music must be lived, not reviewed.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~trip":{"haiku":"Success and luxury serve as escape from struggle, celebrating rapid ascent from poverty through relentless work and the carefree abandon of living at the summit.","opus":"A rapper's victory lap through the luxury circuit of the Riviera and Saint-Tropez, framing wealth and travel as earned proof against the doubters who once sneered. Underneath the champagne and designer watches runs the insistence that life is a fleeting moment, so the flex is really a race against time to never feel poor again.","sonnet":"A Polish rap celebration of hard-won luxury and travel — the narrator catalogues designer labels, European resorts, and yachts earned through relentless work, positioning himself as someone who rose fast from nothing and now lives without pretending, with the implicit message that ambition is the only currency that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~trip-prod-teken":{"haiku":"A Polish rapper documents his ascent from struggle to luxury travel and wealth, celebrating his achievements and exclusive lifestyle. The song measures success through designer brands, world travel, and money flow while dismissing those left behind.","opus":"A Polish rapper's luxury travelogue through Riviera resorts and designer names, flexing hard-won success as proof to doubters and envious hangers-on. Under the opulence runs an awareness of fleeting time, savoring the good life before it ends.","sonnet":"A Polish rap boast about the rewards of hard work made real: luxury travel across European coastal cities, champagne in Menton, watches, credibility earned not performed. Quebo contrasts his genuine hustle against those who would fake it, name-drops Saint-Tropez, Monaco, and the Riviera, and declares he rose fast enough to make people point at his balcony.","src":"haiku"},
"quebonafide~zorza":{"haiku":"A Polish rapper reflects on escape from domestic violence and chaos, climbing toward success while refusing to dance to others' tunes.","opus":"A rapper returns to his childhood home on Plonska Street, the place where he hid his fear behind a sofa and once watched his father try to throw his mother off the balcony. From that wreckage he built a career nobody believed possible, insomniac and scarred but refusing to dance to anyone's tune. Quebonafide, one of Poland's most agile modern rappers, sets confessional trauma against triumph, the childhood horror still driving the ambition.","sonnet":"A reckoning with where survival leads. Growing up in a house where the father threatened to throw the mother from a balcony, gambling away his health and money, now on tour and returning changed — people call it a career, he calls it a lucky exit from something much worse. The chorus holds the accumulated weight: shock, cold darkness, enemies wanting him to fail, but he kept stepping forward and now life is genuinely beautiful.","src":"haiku"},
"queen-cult~be-better":{"haiku":"Desire transcends circumstance and obligation; two people acknowledge their mutual pull while reckoning with the cost of choosing connection over conformity.","opus":"Two people who slid from friends into something more, one already settled down, each insisting they're not the type to wreck a home. The plea that the road was always meant for the two of them is rationalized longing, waiting for the high to wear off.","sonnet":"About the guilt and confusion of developing feelings for someone who is already in a relationship — both parties got \"knickers in a twist\" despite knowing better, and now they're stuck wondering how to disentangle without wrecking a home. The refrain \"we could be better\" is directed inward as much as at the other person: they both know this street was made for two, which is exactly why this is so hard.","src":"haiku"},
"queen-david-bowie~under-pressure":{"haiku":"Societal pressure fractures families and psyches; love alone remains insufficient to withstand systemic collapse and interpersonal terror.","opus":"Two titans trading lines about the weight that splits families and puts people on the street, terror giving way to a plea for love as the only answer. The last-dance urgency is what endures, care dared against a cracking world.","sonnet":"Two voices reflect on the crushing weight of modern life — poverty, loneliness, broken families, people screaming for release — and arrive at the urgent conclusion that love, old-fashioned as it sounds, is the only force capable of holding people together through the terror of existence.","src":"haiku"},
"queen-kona~dreams-of-calpurnia":{"haiku":"Political assassination enacted as liberation; the assassination narrative celebrates the severance of tyranny through collective and deliberate murder.","opus":"The assassination of Julius Caesar told from the conspirators' side, taunting the tyrant who ignored his wife's prophetic dreams on the Ides of March. Twenty-three blades sing his name as Rome is freed and the crown proves fatally heavy.","sonnet":"The assassination of Julius Caesar rendered as dark metal theatre — Calpurnia's prophetic dreams ignored, 23 conspirators hidden under robes, \"Et tu Brutus\" as the pivot, and the Senate celebrating the fall of a dictator who was days from being crowned king.","src":"haiku"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~3-s-7-s":{"haiku":"The comfort of lies over painful truth and the cost of ignoring reality, where ignorance becomes a false paradise.","opus":"A plea to be lied to for comfort, embracing ignorance as bliss because the truth hurts too much; the speaker also boasts of bluffing and getting away with deception, making everyone forget.","sonnet":"A meditation on chosen ignorance and self-deception — the narrator prefers comfortable lies to painful truth, and reflects on how bluffing and making others forget has become his way of staying afloat.","src":"haiku"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~a-song-for-the-dead": {"haiku":"Life is a study of dying; the narrator embraces reckless driving and death with dark, detached curiosity.","opus":"A reckless, death-courting joyride that treats living as merely rehearsal for dying, the narrator gripping a hangman's rope and egging a lingering companion toward the end; underneath is a nihilistic flirtation with mortality, take a spin, come loose, ride out in a coffin, handling death with careless bravado.","sonnet":"A song that equates night driving with accepting death — life is framed as the study of how to die right, and the song moves through a landscape of hearses and nooses with an almost serene fatalism, inviting the listener to come along for the ride.","src":"haiku","fable":"A two-voice death march that runs its whole image-system on one collapse: the invitation to take a midnight ride is really an invitation to die, the hearse standing in for the car and the road for the route out. Lanegan delivers the verses dry and sardonic — mortality reframed as a discipline, a thing you study and try to do right — while the offer to lean in and slip the knots makes the end sound like a release rather than a threat. Homme's wordless refrain does the rest, carrying the momentum the words keep deadpanning past, so the whole thing reads as an errand run casually at night, death treated as just one more stop on the route.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","freedom & escape"],"means":"mortality rehearsed as a casual midnight errand","built":"Lanegan deadpans the words while Homme's refrain carries the momentum"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~a-song-for-the-deaf": {"haiku":"Rejection and isolation, with communication breaking down and a sense of poisoning or corruption spreading.","opus":"A feverish title track aimed at the unhearing, its narrator a poisoner and caged songbird who spotted the threat approaching and refused to heed it, insisting no words can restore what is gone. It sours into deranged cackling. From the band's 2002 desert-rock landmark, it carries that record's static-warped paranoia and its frontman's slippery menace.","sonnet":"A poisoned transmission aimed at those who refuse to listen — the canary in its gilded cage sings something sweet that will destroy whoever leans in close enough to hear it. The speaker has seen who was coming and chose not to warn, and the track frames beautiful sensation as the perfect delivery mechanism for something lethal.","src":"haiku","fable":"The desert-radio conceit that stitched the record together finally jams into static, dedicating its performance to an audience whose ears have been sealed shut against it. A caged bird lulls its listeners with a hushed, honeyed melody that turns out to be venom, the gift and the toxin poured from one throat. Contempt spills over next, curses hurled at the unseeing and a benediction that tightens into a wire around the throat. The retrospective bridge admits the deafness was a choice: he watched the danger approach, muted himself, understood the risk and stayed mute anyway. A radio host then refuses the customary farewell and lets the sentence trail off, the signal leaking into a room where nothing was ever set to answer back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"contempt directed at an audience that chose silence","built":"the signal trails off mid-sentence where no reply was ever set to land"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~another-love-song": {"haiku":"A dismissive farewell to a lover who has sabotaged the relationship through their own actions and insecurity, framed as just another fleeting romantic entanglement.","opus":"A cold kiss-off to a self-pitying lover the singer is done with, dismissing them as just another disposable love song and warning he was never staying for long.","sonnet":"A callous narrator ends a relationship without apology, dismissing the other person's pain as self-inflicted and treating the whole affair as interchangeable with any other failed romance.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is a sneer, not a promise: Nick Oliveri, singing lead in a mock croon, lifts the whole romantic idiom of the pop love song and uses it to narrate a callous exit, someone tallying a lover's tears as self-inflicted and prying her loose from his sole like a spent wad flicked off the pavement. The cruelty escalates by grammar — the refrain starts as it's just another love song, a shrug at the genre and at feeling itself, then hardens into you're just another love song, collapsing the woman into a disposable, interchangeable item in a series. The parting-letter verse makes the con explicit: he warned that nothing would last, so the abandonment gets reframed as her failure to listen. Bracketing this, Natasha Shneider's radio-DJ skit for the fictional station WOMB — commanding worshipful listeners too dim to wake up to their own worth — mirrors the song's own contempt, folding the seduce-and-discard dynamic into Songs for the Deaf's larger joke about media that flatters while it degrades its audience.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"the romantic idiom weaponised to reframe abandonment as the beloved's own failure","built":"the refrain's grammar shift from genre shrug to personal dismissal collapses the woman into a series"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~auto-pilot": {"haiku":"A narrator and stranger take a high-flying journey with no control or destination, pursuing reckless sensation without connection.","opus":"Homme's woozy drift into numbness, a desert-rock reverie where escape via chemicals and altitude curdles into disenchantment, the wished-for high revealing there's nothing there once reached. Auto pilot, no control: coasting without wanting to steer anymore.","sonnet":"A dissociated drug song — the narrator is high and numb, flying on autopilot with no real control, pursuing a fleeting connection with someone he's never seen before and doesn't want to see again, the whole thing unfolding in a haze of substances and jet-plane altitude.","src":"haiku","fable":"Oliveri sells getting high and getting off as one lift-off, pitching the come-on at a body laid out cold and stony as if desire and the morgue shared a table. But the second verse punctures its own seduction: barely met, the object of all that wanting is already someone he never wants to see again, the chase souring the instant it lands. What the chant finally admits is that nobody was steering — the drugged, wordless refrain of surrender hands the wheel to nothing, ecstasy and oblivion revealed as one blank, unguided drift.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"desire and oblivion revealed as one unguided drift","built":"the wordless drugged refrain hands the wheel to nothing"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~avon":{"haiku":"A system for controlling and dispensing with people portrayed as simple and efficient, with no distinction between victims.","opus":"An early, hypnotic Homme sketch that gestures at menace and detachment more than it spells anything out, a cold observer stringing things up and cutting them down. The desert-rock trance is the substance; meaning stays deliberately just out of reach.","sonnet":"A brief, impressionistic sketch of observation and control — the speaker watches people moving in a haze, strings them up and cuts them down, and notes that free-range humans end up cooped regardless, leaving a sense of detached, cyclical manipulation.","src":"haiku","fable":"Josh Homme delivers this from inside a predator's unbothered calm, a watcher trailing someone across open ground while deliberately ignoring the warning sounds of the world around them. The menace is bureaucratic: he reduces whatever he does to his quarry to a tidy, repeatable procedure — the verbs stay coy about the harm, which is its own kind of threat. His contempt turns people into penned livestock and mass-produced bodies stripped of any self, as though individuality were a joke and culling the obvious conclusion. The chorus then withholds itself entirely, stuttering a single truncated syllable that keeps sprinting toward a word it never reaches, so the track enacts its own threat by trapping the listener in the approach and refusing any release.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"menace made bureaucratic, harm reduced to a tidy repeatable procedure","built":"the chorus withholds itself entirely, stuttering a truncated syllable that never reaches the word"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~battery-acid": {"haiku":"Degradation and loss of humanity, with battery acid as a metaphor for corrosive emotional or psychological abuse.","opus":"A corrosive, mechanical sneer at a brainwashed populace, the narrator with poison running through him yanking control back, refusing to be dismissed and warning that suffering comes for everyone while the cruelty behind it goes unexamined. From Era Vulgaris, it's Queens of the Stone Age at their most robotic and abrasive, Homme's riffs grinding like the title's chemistry.","sonnet":"Corrosive contempt for someone who has been completely reprogrammed into compliance — the speaker watches a person reduced to a yes-button stuck on automatic, drained of identity, and offers no sympathy because masochism and sadism are both roles in a system the victim chose. The acid metaphor is literal: what remains has had everything that defined it eaten away.","src":"haiku","fable":"A corrosive address to someone toxic, delivered by a narrator whose own bloodstream is the poison, his pulse a jammed signal spiking on an irrational dose that has stopped registering danger. The insults arrive mechanically — leash, plastic, wiped clean, brainwashed — because he has reduced the affair to two circuits shorting against each other, sadist and masochist swapping the current with the resignation of people who keep failing to learn. Josh Homme's Era Vulgaris register is precisely this lacquered, dehumanized snarl, where tenderness and disgust run on one wire. The closing whisper turns the knife inward: he charges the other with loving only their own reflection, then admits in the same breath that he too has run out of anyone, exposing the whole rant as a mirror held to himself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","love & desire"],"means":"sadist and masochist swapping poison with mutual resignation","built":"the closing accusation about loving a reflection turns on its speaker"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~better-living-through-chemistry": {"haiku":"Pharmaceutical and spiritual solutions offered to those without faith create false salvation, with the narrator rejecting this prescribed path and reclaiming his mind.","opus":"From Rated R, this drifts through drug-fueled seduction, a capsule that opens the mind and a fresh doctrine handed to the faithless. The narrator questions a bottomless supply meant to get him through, then swings toward reclaiming himself and lashing out at everyone. Loneliness haunts it, isolated in a crowd, chemistry sold as counterfeit deliverance.","sonnet":"A speaker confronted with the pharmaceutical management of consciousness — prescribed pills in place of genuine faith or inner resources — chooses refusal, identifying the medicated calm as a kind of colonization of the mind. The rejection is not therapeutic but political: reclaiming thought from a system that profits by keeping it sedated.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song sells self-medication as spiritual upgrade, a pharmaceutical gospel promising clarity and stamina to the faithless, then quietly reveals the con hidden inside the pitch. Its two verses trace a convert who swallows the offer whole, only to curdle into paranoid grandeur — torching his old idols, turning on the encroaching mob, mistaking chemical zeal for freedom while a knife and unsatisfied blood shadow the whole transaction. The chorus is the punchline that voids both verses: hemmed in by faces yet utterly deserted, the promised communion pays out as solitude, so the title's better living lands as a flat sneer. Read through Josh Homme's Rated R, the pill is less an escape hatch than the era's ambient hum, and lucidity and stupor open onto the identical empty room.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written with Oliveri on a desert retreat to Twentynine Palms — the same session that produced I Think I Lost My Headache.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape"],"means":"promised communion cashing out as identical empty room","built":"the chorus voids both verses by delivering their opposite"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~broken-box": {"haiku":"Dismissal of romantic idealization hardens into contempt—the narrator rejects both the person and the myths they've constructed together.","opus":"A scorched, contemptuous kiss-off to an ex idealizing new love, Homme reducing them to interchangeable and disposable; the wounded cruelty of dressing heartbreak up as indifference while clearly still bleeding.","sonnet":"A post-breakup dismissal in which the speaker, unimpressed by a woman's new romance, mocks her delusions of love and announces with cold satisfaction that he wants no part of what she's offering.","src":"haiku","fable":"A jilted lover's kiss-off that keeps insisting on its own indifference while the venom gives it away. The narrator's central boast is that he processes people as interchangeable and disposable, the ex reduced to one of an assembly line refreshed and blanked in his head, yet the effort of the insult, the sneer at her new great love, the demand that she peddle her damage elsewhere, betrays exactly the wound the pose is meant to hide. The wine image sets the tone with a crude class-and-sex jab, and Homme sells the whole thing in that arch, mock-cheerful register where a Yay and a hurray are meant as slaps; the cruelty is a performance of having moved on staged loudly enough that even the singer might not believe it. The parenthetical refrain functions as the mask, the confident mantra, while the lines around it, the childish rain-dance vow and the escalating filth, are what is underneath: not freedom but a man still talking to someone he claims to have already forgotten.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"indifference performed loudly enough to convince nobody","built":"the parenthetical refrain is the mask; the lines around it are what's underneath"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~do-it-again": {"haiku":"Addictive romantic cycle and physical desire repeated endlessly; two people fit perfectly but never truly commit, seeking repeated gratification.","opus":"The desert-rock band ride a hypnotic groove into compulsive attraction that keeps pulling him back to someone he can't quit or truly possess. Admitting he gets a single life and won't lie that she's exclusively his, the request to repeat it hovers between craving and pointlessness, ending in a warped preacher-radio outro.","sonnet":"Physical obsession that loops back on itself compulsively — falling, sinking, returning to the same person knowing the fit is perfect but the situation unresolvable. The narrator admits they leave nowhere for the other to land, yet cannot stop. A late radio-sermon fragment collides with the refrain, amplifying the sense of ecstatic, irrational repetition.","src":"haiku","fable":"Every verb here tilts downward — falling, sinking, getting sick — until closeness reads as a symptom rather than a bond, the tight fit less a lover's than a needle finding its vein. Homme's bridge detonates the whole seduction: admitting his years are finite and that fidelity is a lie he refuses, he exposes the request as a junkie's, not a suitor's. What sounds like courtship is a relapse loop, the object already vanished while the craving keeps circling back for its dose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"courtship reframed as a relapse loop with no suitor in it","built":"the bridge detonates the seduction by admitting fidelity is a lie he refuses"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~domesticated-animals": {"haiku":"Taming of wild nature and human freedom, with rebellion and the search for power and autonomy.","opus":"Josh Homme's desert-rock band spins a bleering political fable about how uprisings tend to circle right back into obedience, people rising up only to kneel again. A recurring interrogation over hidden riches exposes the real hierarchy: the powerful already own everything, and the tamed masses beg for approval like housebroken pets.","sonnet":"Freedom sold for comfort — once-wild creatures reduced to numbered pets who revolve without actually revolting, while the question of who holds the gold and who holds the power stays permanently unanswered. The chorus circles back on itself the way a tame animal circles its cage.","src":"haiku","fable":"Homme pitches the whole thing as a captor's smooth patter to his pet, and the con is that every promise of freedom is really a command to heel. The rhythm of standing up and being told to kneel again renders revolt as a carousel: one turn, back to the start, the crowd tipping over like children in a ring game. The real prize the voice keeps hounding is buried treasure, and the answer lands as the record's cruelest punchline — it sits under the dirt and it belongs to the master alone, so liberty was interred, never handed over, its whole rationale filed away somewhere nobody reclaims it. Bowed necks, trophies of the beaten hoisted through the streets, and tears swallowed like homage fuse the housebroken creature and the subjugated citizenry into one body, until the once-wild darling is simply anyone who reads a collar as affection. It is the Villains sneer honed to a point: a glam swagger that seduces you into feeling sprung loose while it tallies everything you surrendered.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","freedom & escape","politics & society"],"means":"every promise of freedom rephrased as a command to heel","built":"buried treasure lands as the punchline: liberty was interred, never handed over"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~era-vulgaris-richard-file-remix": {"haiku":"Modern alienation where genuine love is impossible in a superficial, media-saturated world where people are passive consumers.","opus":"A chilly portrait of intimacy going extinct in a jaded modern age, where the narrator performs numbness and treats real connection as something the culture no longer has space for. This remix reworks the album's title concept, distilling Queens of the Stone Age's cynicism into detached electronic sheen.","sonnet":"A cold survey of a world too hollow for love — the speaker performs detachment from a crumbling tower, watching cities like London and Tokyo from above and finding them uniformly loveless. The era vulgaris is one where everyone else chases connection while he drools in the dark staring at lights, and the repetition is the whole point: this is all there is, over and over.","src":"haiku","fable":"Fame is diagnosed as marionette-work here: the performer keeps playing until death or the cover shoot claims him, willing to imitate a jerking corpse for the puppeteer while wiping the mess he makes off his own skin, spectacle that stays photogenic. Perched atop a condemned spire, the speaker has surveyed the great financial capitals and returns the same bleak accounting everywhere he lands, an emotional insolvency with no vacancy left for tenderness under a firmament of advertising glare. That verdict reduces the crowd to helpless organisms slobbering in shadow, transfixed by screens, gutting each other's names because savaging reputation is the last available substitute for intimacy. Homme's decisive gesture is capitulation: the reasoning dissolves into a syllable-less chant, then a bare instruction to gape at the glow, looped toward hypnosis so the piece quits arguing and instead performs the numbness it describes, a stupor the remix treatment only settles deeper into.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"collective numbness performed until argument dissolves into it","built":"the reasoning collapses into a syllable-less chant, enacting the stupor it describes"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~everybody-knows-that-you-are-insane": {"haiku":"Recognizing someone's mental instability or disconnection from reality and the resulting inner emptiness.","opus":"Josh Homme's desert-rock band ride a churning groove through a taunt that keeps collapsing into confession. The narrator mocks his target for feeling empty and unable to conceal what they are, then admits he has been just as lost himself. The numbed question of whether he is fixed yet cuts through all the swagger.","sonnet":"A hollow person caught in denial — the accusation is the title repeated until it becomes both verdict and echo, while the speaker confesses to feeling nothing and wondering if that numbness is recovery or simply the absence of everything. No resolution, just the chant continuing.","src":"haiku","fable":"An interrogation staged inside one skull, where the accuser and the accused turn out to be the same worn-out man. The verse needles a second person about his hollowness and his hiding, then the taunt curdles when the speaker confesses that he too has wandered every dark route back toward some idea of home. The chorus lands as verdict and self-diagnosis at once, the crowd's certainty about his madness hardening into weather he simply lives inside. Worst is the bridge, where numbness recurs as a hopeful question, feeling nothing mistaken for healing while the repetition refuses to say whether the cure and the emptiness are one thing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"self-diagnosis disguised as an interrogation of someone else","built":"the second person and the speaker collapse into one worn-out man by the bridge"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~everybody-s-gonna-be-happy-bonus": {"haiku":"Simple romantic optimism and the happiness two people can share together.","opus":"A sunny, almost naive love song insisting everybody's going to be happy, which really just means you and me, the narrator's whole world lit up by seeing someone walk down the street. A Kinks cover, it lets Queens of the Stone Age indulge a rare uncomplicated brightness, the sixties pop bounce a warm detour from their usual desert menace.","sonnet":"A simple, guileless declaration of collective joy built around one person — seeing someone walk down the street is enough to make life complete, and the desire is only to say the things that have been building up and share the happiness outward. The repetition makes it feel like an incantation rather than a pop hook.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cover of the 1965 Ray Davies single, and the interest sits in how a promise of blanket joy for the whole world keeps quietly narrowing until it means nothing wider than two people in love. Each chorus makes that shrinkage audible: the universal pledge is redefined, within the same breath, down to just you and me, the utopia collapsing into a private bargain every time it comes around. Queens play the sugar-rush hook as knowing pastiche rather than belief, so the endlessly repeated assurance reads less like confidence than like someone insisting on contentment so hard you begin to doubt he owns any.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"universal joy pledged and quietly narrowed to a private bargain","built":"each chorus redefs the utopia down to just you and me within the same breath"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~fairweather-friends": {"haiku":"When hardship arrives, friends vanish like gossips and frauds revealing their true nature. The speaker knows loneliness and betrayal when someone disappears before the first rain.","opus":"Josh Homme's dusty lament for people who vanish the moment the weather turns, praying for rain to test who stays. Behind the swagger sits real abandonment, the bravado of not giving a shit barely covering the hurt.","sonnet":"People who disappear the moment things get hard — fair-weather friends who are only around in the good weather and vanish when you're bleeding — with the narrator choosing rain over the company of gossips and frauds, and waiting for the real ones who stick around for the dark.","src":"haiku","fable":"Homme catalogues the allies who evaporate the instant conditions sour, and the sting is that they defected before the storm even arrived, at the exact hour their loyalty would have counted. The pun sharpens into strategy: he vows to summon downpours deliberately, over and over, as a purge that flushes out anyone too fragile to stay through weather. What begins as raw injury curdles fast into a lewd, boozy fantasy of reunion, the swagger of appetite pulled over a still-fresh betrayal. It caves in on the final mutter of studied indifference, a shrug so insistent it exposes the hurt it means to disown. That fits an album Homme cut after brushing death and losing people close to him, where every bit of bravado sits stitched atop the bruise beneath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The piano and the wail underneath are Elton John — the one cameo he landed on the record after phoning Homme to say the band could use an actual queen.","themes":["friendship & loyalty","anger & defiance","party & hedonism"],"means":"betrayal converted into a purge summoned deliberately","built":"the final shrug of studied indifference is so insistent it exposes the hurt beneath"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~feel-good-hit-of-the-summer": {"haiku":"A rapid-fire recitation of drugs and controlled substances—nicotine, Valium, Vicodin, marijuana, ecstasy, alcohol, and cocaine—presented as a ritualistic litany without narrative or judgment.","opus":"A deadpan roll call of intoxicants, cycling through a fixed list of pills, weed and booze capped by a chanted stimulant. The substances themselves are the whole point, the repetition mocking a summer defined entirely by getting high.","sonnet":"The lyrics consist almost entirely of a repeated list of substances — nicotine, Valium, Vicodin, marijuana, ecstasy, alcohol, cocaine — with no additional narrative, functioning as a blunt inventory of intoxicants.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is nothing but a shopping list — a deadpan roll call of substances chanted verse after verse, with a single stimulant answering back as the hook. That is the whole joke and the whole point: Homme empties the pop love song of every sentiment and leaves only the itemized appetite behind it, so the summer anthem becomes a mantra of consumption with no self left to consume. The bone-dry repetition is the meaning, not a shortcut around it — by refusing to editorialize, the track lets the litany indict the rock-and-roll lifestyle it recites, satire so straight-faced that radio couldn't touch it, which for a Rated R lead-off was exactly the intended provocation. Rob Halford's guest voice in the back half turns the recital into a metal benediction, deepening the deadpan rather than winking at it. Underneath the party swagger is a flat, compulsive emptiness — the mask is celebration, and what's under it is the closed loop of the addict counting his supply.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"It nearly ended the record as a few seconds of chant until producer Chris Goss flipped it to the front; Wal-Mart refused to stock Rated R over it, and Rob Halford sang his part unheard from the studio next door.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"deadpan litany that indicts what it performs","built":"pop love song evacuated down to a substance inventory"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~feel-good-hit-of-the-summer-reprise": {"opus":"Queens of the Stone Age opened Rated R with a deadpan chemical checklist, and this reprise returns to it with the same flat nerve. Nothing but the substance litany repeated, delivered without editorial comment, it lets the catalogue be the whole joke and the whole point, the desert-rock crew claiming the summer anthem by reciting its ingredients and daring you to flinch.","sonnet":"A deadpan litany of recreational substances with zero editorial comment — the song is nothing but the list, repeated three times, making the point that this is the summer anthem and the catalogue is the whole story.","src":"sonnet","fable":"The song is nothing but a shopping list — a deadpan roll call of substances chanted verse after verse, with a single stimulant answering back as the hook. That is the whole joke and the whole point: Homme empties the pop love song of every sentiment and leaves only the itemized appetite behind it, so the summer anthem becomes a mantra of consumption with no self left to consume. The bone-dry repetition is the meaning, not a shortcut around it — by refusing to editorialize, the track lets the litany indict the rock-and-roll lifestyle it recites, satire so straight-faced that radio couldn't touch it, which for a Rated R lead-off was exactly the intended provocation. Rob Halford's guest voice in the back half turns the recital into a metal benediction, deepening the deadpan rather than winking at it. Underneath the party swagger is a flat, compulsive emptiness — the mask is celebration, and what's under it is the closed loop of the addict counting his supply.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"It nearly ended the record as a few seconds of chant until producer Chris Goss flipped it to the front; Wal-Mart refused to stock Rated R over it, and Rob Halford sang his part unheard from the studio next door.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"the pop love song emptied of sentiment and left as itemized appetite","built":"refusing to editorialize turns the litany into straight-faced satire of what it recites"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~feet-don-t-fail-me": {"haiku":"A person born into restlessness pursues a calling through dancing and movement, refusing to settle despite life's hardships.","opus":"A propulsive ode to perpetual motion, sung by a wanderer who has spent his whole life pursuing some undefined pull and never stopping. It plays as a credo that movement itself matters more than any destination. Queens of the Stone Age used it to launch Villains, and Josh Homme's swaggering, groove-heavy desert rock powers its relentless drive.","sonnet":"A life as perpetual motion, born restless, chasing something unnamed since childhood. The speaker is older than he expected to be, still running on feeling alone, telling civil lies to stay civilised, but ultimately unable to stop — feet in the gutter, head in the clouds, compelled forward with a gang who move on urgency and take the long way home.","src":"haiku","fable":"Josh Homme's spoken-word nativity — the Palm Desert boy, the phonograph stylus dropping into its channel — treats the arrival of rhythm as the arrival of the self, so that being alive and being unable to stay still become the same event. What sells the song is its honesty about escape: the sprinter tailing red taillights and roadway grit until the eyes water is running from a reckoning, dressing candor in the small courtesies that keep company polite, admitting that manners are a form of lying. When the groove tightens under Mark Ronson's polish into a hypnotic summons, the goal dissolves and only the pulse remains, a beckoning with no address at its end. The bravado of shrugging off tomorrow and choosing the scenic detour is a jester's strut that knows exactly how ridiculous it looks and struts harder for knowing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","identity & becoming"],"means":"rhythm arriving as the self, escape dressed as the scenic detour","built":"the goal dissolves under the groove until only the pulse remains with no address"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~first-it-giveth": {"fable":"The song is a seduction addressed to something intimate and predatory — the collapse of boundary between speaker and the thing consuming him (I'm in you, you're in me), a lover, drug, or fate that flatters even as it hooks him. The chorus is a cyclical mercy-and-cruelty engine, generosity that arrives only ever as a prelude to withdrawal, hammered to exhaustion until the outro strips it down to the vanishing itself. The desert-rock swagger of the young-and-beautiful boast is the mask: the bridge — begging, pleading, shaking — is what leaks out from under it, the servility of someone bound to whatever holds the string.","fableDeep":"The refrain borrows Job — the Lord giveth, the Lord taketh away — and reassigns scripture to the substance, turning the high into a religion whose god deals. 'I'm in you, now you're in me' collapses user and used into one body; 'loyal to only you' is fidelity inverted, the addict keeping faith with the thing that's tricking him. The tell is 'I'm no fool' sworn by someone dangling wide awake on a hook — the addict's classic self-exemption — and 'time goes by, tables turn' is the whole pharmacology of tolerance in six words: the same dose that gave begins to take.","haiku":"A cyclical, manipulative relationship dynamic where one person alternates between offering affection and taking it away, leaving the other dependent and confused.","opus":"A song about a cruel, deceptive entanglement that first gives and then takes away, leaving the speaker begging, hooked, and toyed with as fortunes turn over time.","sonnet":"The song describes being hooked on something — or someone — that grants pleasure only to take it away, leaving the speaker dangling, desperate, and aware that the cycle of getting and losing will keep repeating.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"the swagger of beauty masking the servility of someone bound to the string","built":"the bridge begging and shaking exposed beneath the boast the verses perform"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~fortress": {"haiku":"Emotional walls and fear of vulnerability, with reassurance that falling apart can lead to safety and growth.","opus":"A rare act of reassurance from Josh Homme, aimed at someone who bottles their feelings away, insisting every stronghold collapses and what counts is how you get back up, offering himself as shelter when their defenses cave. Written amid the upheaval around Villains, it finds the band swapping desert-rock menace for open warmth, a fatherly, friendly vow that no one has to meet the dark completely alone.","sonnet":"A patient, tender address to someone who has walled their feelings off — the speaker knows the fortress will fall because all fortresses do, and wants them to understand that falling is not the end. The acknowledgment that everyone faces darkness alone, even the one offering shelter, gives the song honesty rather than false comfort. Run to me when the walls come down.","src":"haiku","fable":"Homme built this as a father's reassurance to his kids, yet its tenderness keeps snagging on a paradox it refuses to smooth over. The stronghold that shields a hurt person also seals them off, and the parent who offers refuge admits, in the same breath, that he can barely chart his own interior. Its steadiest claim reframes ruin as ordinary weather: walls buckle, night gives way to morning, and character shows not in the collapse but in getting back up afterward. What survives is a vow that never pretends to be armor, only company for the solitary reckoning everyone eventually faces.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","shame & repression"],"means":"refuge offered by someone who can barely chart his own interior","built":"the stronghold that shelters is also what seals off, held in a paradox the song refuses to smooth"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~go-with-the-flow": {"fable":"A woman burns her keepsakes and swears the wreckage is weightless; the man answers that she's under his skin whether or not she'll ever recall any of it. The refrain about drifting downstream is a pose, and Homme keeps puncturing it with the same needling question of whether the mind actually buys the lie the mouth is selling — a staged calm laid over a churn it can't quiet. The second verse tears that mask off: the marching-in-formation ease of playing along gives way to a starved demand for a cause worth dying on and a genuinely fresh error to make, since the real terror here isn't losing but never risking enough to lose. Under the motorik desert pulse that made this the band's radio breakthrough, surrender to the current is exposed as the coward's peace it pretends not to be.","fableDeep":"The title phrase is tested and fails. Every image is disposability — photos thrown away, 'something sweet to throw away,' little soldiers falling in and out of love — yet 'I can't wash you off my skin' admits the detachment doesn't take. The sharpest line is 'I want a new mistake': not a wish to be good, just a wish for the next error to be novel. And the needling question 'do you believe it in your head?' keeps asking whether anyone actually buys the shrug — the song performs going-with-the-flow while confessing, mid-stream, that drifting is the one thing the speaker can't bear.","haiku":"Acceptance of impermanence and the realization that connections and memories ultimately don't matter; letting go without resistance or regret.","opus":"A song about detachment and passivity in a fleeting relationship, where the speaker shrugs that nothing matters anymore even as he wants something worth dying for and living beautifully, willing to just go with the flow.","sonnet":"The song captures a mood of detachment and mild nihilism — people fall in and out of love, discard things, and go along without conviction — while the speaker admits wanting something worth dying and living for rather than just drifting.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"surrender to the current exposed as the coward's peace it denies being","built":"the needling question about whether the mind buys the lie punctures each verse's pose"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~god-is-in-the-radio": {"haiku":"God broadcasts a message through the radio and media; the speaker hears a repeating divine slogan while others come back unchanged.","opus":"A hazy communion with sound itself, treating the airwaves as a narcotic pulpit where the divine seeps out of the speakers between frequencies while the devil frets in the shadows, a shared secret the listener denies but senses everywhere. The looping refrain about coming back and doing no wrong runs like a mantra. Built into Songs for the Deaf, framed as a drive through static, Homme's hypnotic desert groove suits its trance.","sonnet":"A song about finding the divine leaking through the static between radio stations — not organised religion but a narcotic signal, something you hear and recognise even if you won't admit it. The devil covers his tracks while God bleeds through, and the refrain of come back and do no wrong floats like a broadcast blessing on repeat.","src":"haiku","fable":"Homme treats the dial as an altar and the broadcast as a sacrament that doubles as a fix, revelation and addiction arriving on the same frequency until the listener can no longer separate faith from the habit of tuning in. The genius of the trick is that divine signal and the Devil's cover-up bleed together in the static, so the devotion sounds indistinguishable from surveillance, worship indistinguishable from being programmed. When the promise of returning to sin nothing repeats without variation, the vow curdles into a loop, obedience dressed as forgiveness, the flock held less by belief than by a jingle that refuses to end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control"],"means":"revelation and addiction arriving on the same frequency inseparably","built":"the vow of returning to sin nothing loops without variation until obedience curdles into a jingle"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~gonna-leave-you": {"haiku":"After three years of wrong and scarring, the narrator ends the relationship decisively, rejecting both the past connection and its pain.","opus":"A brutal severing of a three-year relationship, the singer coldly announcing he's leaving after stomping on hearts; the visceral imagery of blood in a spoon and being out of a womb/highchair casts the bond as suffocating and toxic, something that scars the skin and must be broken off.","sonnet":"A messy, guilt-laden breakup — the narrator knows the relationship has gone on too long and caused harm on both sides, declares they're leaving, but the raw imagery of blood and drugs makes clear this departure is as chaotic as everything else between them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A leaving song sung by the one doing the leaving, and it takes obvious pleasure in the cruelty — the speaker positions himself as the rescue the abandoned partner is waiting for, then reveals he is the one walking out. The image-system fuses two families that should not touch: injury as intimacy (scarred skin, something pushed in and broken off) and a warped register of age and dependence, where womb, highchair, and being outgrown turn the breakup into a birth in reverse, one person expelling the other rather than growing up. The song lets an addiction thread bleed through the same channel — a cooked and reddened dose, a ceiling weeping down — so that quitting the relationship and feeding a habit become the same gesture. The final swap, from a promise of departure to a boast of self-sufficiency, exposes what the whole strut was covering: not indifference but the very dependence the boast pretends to have outgrown.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Nick Oliveri sings it — the croon, not the scream register he uses elsewhere on the record.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the strut of departure masking the dependence it names","built":"breakup recast as birth-in-reverse through womb and highchair"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~hangin-tree": {"haiku":"Romantic devotion and unity symbolized by swaying together under a tree, with submission to a dominant force.","opus":"A soft, menacing lullaby where lovers merge into one beside a gallows in the warm season, an offer of safe passage bought with the singer's own blood and shadowed by control. Josh Homme's band first recorded it in their Desert Sessions, later slowing it into eerie beauty that binds tenderness to execution.","sonnet":"A dark pastoral of possession — two figures orbit a hanging tree in summer sun, one clearly under the other's thumb, the imagery of swaying and union carrying the weight of something irreversible. Minimal and suffocating.","src":"haiku","fable":"A murder ballad hollowed out to its bones, where the desert-rock swagger drops away and Mark Lanegan's voice carries the tune of a lullaby that has curdled into a death. The song sets a lynching or a hanged lover against a summer pastoral, and the horror is that the two lovers becoming one happens under the tree, in the sway of the corpse in the breeze — union and execution rendered in the same gentle image. The single-word opening stammer, each syllable stranded on its own line, enacts a mind coming apart before the round, incantatory chorus locks it into a possessive spell, and the final line about being kept under a thumb turns the tenderness openly sinister: this is love as ownership, consummated only in killing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"union and execution rendered in a single gentle pastoral image","built":"the single-word opening stammer enacts a mind coming apart before the incantatory chorus locks it"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~head-like-a-haunted-house": {"haiku":"Mental chaos, leprosy, and degradation framed through vivid and crude sexual imagery.","opus":"Queens of the Stone Age crank the tempo into frantic garage-punk on this Villains cut, Josh Homme's wordplay and leering swagger dissolving into pure adrenal chaos. It's a manic, tongue-twisting rush of nonsense and menace, the narrator's mind portrayed as a spook-ride crammed with gibberish, spoiling for a brawl and daring the listener to buy his cult of unreason wholesale.","sonnet":"A chaotic, carnivalesque seduction — the other person's mind is a haunted house of misdiagnosis and degradation, and the speaker is entirely at home in the mess. The energy is confrontational and comic at once, full of fake apologies and dirty tricks, the whole thing building toward a mutual explosion that is framed as satisfaction rather than damage.","src":"haiku","fable":"Homme runs seduction and derangement on one wire: a lover whose skull he wants both to spook and to move into, the chase staged as a possession act thick with séances and glossolalia. The verses run on garbled diction and baby-talk stutters, so the language itself plays unhinged, aping the giddy overload of wanting someone past reason. The turn is where the grin curdles into a threat — a lover's ultimatum riding a blade's edge, then the poisoned-cup-and-tablet vow that you'll swallow the same intoxication whatever you claim, recasting the whole flirtation as a two-person cult with coercion under the sparkle. It plays as a Mark Ronson-era pivot, the leering wordplay of the older records zipped into disco-tight production, the come-on charming exactly because it never hides that it is also a hex laid on someone who hasn't consented yet.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult","surveillance & control"],"means":"seduction staged as a possession act that never hides it is also a hex","built":"garbled diction and baby-talk stutters make the language itself play unhinged before the threat lands"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~hideaway": {"haiku":"Predatory seduction and romantic captivity, with a manipulator ensnaring victims through charm.","opus":"Queens of the Stone Age lace Josh Homme's desert-rock swagger with real menace on a predatory seduction voiced by something that offers affection but delivers ownership. A young thing chasing life's rewards learns that naivety is the price, tenderness curdling in an instant into a wound, the promised sanctuary revealing itself as a trap where fresh prey is kept close, isolated and indebted.","sonnet":"Predatory seduction dressed as rescue — the speaker promises safety and love to someone already bruised by shortcuts and bad choices, while the language of tangling and owing and new prey makes clear the offer is another trap. Innocence is described as something that gets lost like keys.","src":"haiku","fable":"A predator's seduction sung entirely from the predator's mouth, and the horror is that it arrives dressed as a love song. The speaker courts a hungry kid who wanted the finer things and burned every easy path to reach them, then reframes grooming as devotion: the promise the mark will never need to flee, the vow of forever, all of it the honeyed patter of a captor who has learned that a lonely target mistakes a cage for belonging. Villains-era Homme keeps the surface glassy and pop-clean so the menace stays hidden in daylight, and the machinery shows through in the transactions stacking up beneath the tenderness — a student claimed, a debt insisted on, a body reckoned as flesh and bone. The drive is the tell: an offer to hand over the steering that is really a lure further in, tenderness curdling to a fresh wound before the passenger notices. It ends on the sales pitch cracking open — affection chanted until it sours into a contemptuous shrug, the mask slipping to reveal that warmth was only ever the bait on the hook.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"grooming reframed as devotion by a captor fluent in honeyed patter","built":"affection chanted until it sours into a contemptuous shrug, the mask slipping at the close"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~i-appear-missing": {"haiku":"A person loses themselves and disappears inside their own mind through heartbreak and loss, falling deeper into emotional absence.","opus":"The centerpiece of Like Clockwork, written after Josh Homme's near-death on an operating table, watches a self quietly dissolve, hollowed out where hope should be and half-wishing his absence might finally be noticed. Beneath the dread runs a wrecked, tardy love, the confession from the brink that nothing ever mattered until her.","sonnet":"Dissolution framed as disappearance — the self gone missing, pieces stolen or surrendered, consciousness slipping down a hole it can't climb back out of. Love is what's at stake: the song arrives at its core only near the end, confessing that nothing mattered until this person did, and now, standing at the edge, the speaker is held in place only by that attachment, shining into the void.","src":"haiku","fable":"Written in the wake of the surgery-table flatline and the housebound recovery that clouded the whole project, this plays as a dispatch from under anesthesia, a self unmoored from its body and hovering somewhere over the gurney. Homme stages it as a gradual vanishing: the summons to the comatose and the identical stranger with a cavity where faith drained away confirm that the vanished patient and the singer are one person, each refrain tugging the speaker deeper into a burrow from which no one returns. The pivot that recasts giving up as tenderness is the sly move, framing the descent so that dropping into someone and going dark become a single downward pull. By the coda the whispered replies act like a heart monitor answering, or a fevered mind coaxing itself off the ledge, and the closing surrender folds rescue and devotion into one collapse that ends not with a hand pulling him up but with the drop already complete.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","death & grief","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"giving up recast as tenderness, descent as a downward pull toward rescue","built":"the whispered coda replies like a heart monitor, coaxing a mind off the ledge it's already fallen from"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~i-m-designer":{"haiku":"A critique of consumerism and manufactured identity where generations sell themselves for wealth and fame rather than authenticity.","opus":"A cynical take on a generation for sale, where fame and fortune are the only real things and everything is prostitution and counterfeiting the self; the speaker mocks the idea of selling out while insisting that what truly matters can't be bought or owned.","sonnet":"A corrosive self-portrait of a generation that has replaced idealism with marketability — the narrator admits to counterfeiting himself for sale while mocking those who claim they cannot be bought, since refusing a low offer is still just negotiating a price.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sneering anatomy of the sellout that denies itself any moral platform by putting the speaker on the auction block ahead of everyone else. Homme fuses the streetwalker and the skilled craftsman into a single swaggering pose, letting the track ridicule both the cash-in artist and the purist who pretends to sit above the market, snaring each in the same fib that outrage at a cheap bid only proves the deal was struck long ago. The pivot arrives with the couture conceit: bespoke, made to order, tailored — the very definition of a commodity, so the promise of ceaseless reinvention doubles as a fashion house's slogan, art and advertising fused past telling apart. Beneath the bluster hums a quieter figure, the recurring line that some worth stays outside every ledger and belongs to no buyer at all, letting a record marinated in dot-com greed confess that the thing genuinely of value was never a stock item, least of all for the suit brokering the sale.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","money & the street"],"means":"sellout and purist snared in the same transaction they claimed to escape","built":"the couture conceit fuses art and advertising past telling apart by definition","fnote":"The driest joke is formal: the one line the song keeps repeating is the boast that he never repeats himself — the designer's slogan disproved by its own chorus."},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~i-sat-by-the-ocean": {"haiku":"A lover retreats to the ocean seeking escape from a broken relationship built on lies. They come to recognize the relationship was never real, drifting apart like passing ships in darkness.","opus":"After a breakup, the speaker tries to drink away the memory of a lover, reflecting bitterly on lies and the loneliness of drifting apart like passing ships.","sonnet":"Someone tries to drink away grief after a relationship ends, acknowledging that the bond was built on lies and that the two are now like ships passing in the night — unable to truly face each other or escape the wreckage.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song Homme keeps swaggering through until the swagger curdles into something colder. The narrator tries to medicate a lover out of his system, shrugs off crying as useless, waves away his friends' sympathy — every gesture a man staying upright by refusing to feel the fall. The turn is that inverted proverb near the end: time is supposed to heal all wounds, but here time wounds all the healing, so recovery itself is what gets destroyed as the two of them fade from each other's view. That reframes the whole song's cool detachment as a symptom, not a cure. The closing image seals it — the ships that were merely passing in the night, missing each other cleanly, are now crashing, the wreck he spent the whole song pretending to outrun.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"cool detachment worn as a symptom, not as a cure for the loss","built":"the inverted proverb mid-song reframes recovery itself as what gets destroyed"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~i-think-i-lost-my-headache": {"haiku":"Relief from mental pain through medication or numbness, as the narrator's suffering finally subsides.","opus":"A woozy song about a pain that won't resolve, phones ringing unanswered, doors left open, the narrator medicating himself soft while his head threatens to explode and love turns blind. From Rated R, its title is deadpan Queens of the Stone Age irony, the track dissolving into a long, lurching brass-and-percussion coda that mimics the throb it claims to have lost.","sonnet":"Medicated numbness mistaken for relief — a call that rang out was never answered, a door left open went unused, and now something burns and tastes good in a way that is clearly chemical rather than real. The interior voice says it is all in his head, and the chorus catches in that loop without resolving it, which is the point.","src":"haiku","fable":"A migraine song that keeps mistaking the sedative for the cure, narrating a mind that treats its own pain as an unreliable witness it can neither trust nor silence. The verses pile up refusals — a phone left to ring out, a door standing open and ignored — until the ache stops being an event and becomes the speaker's whole climate, something the pounding chorus circles without escaping. When the second verse turns to a sweetness that scorches and to dosing yourself into pliancy, the flavor and the remedy and the wound collapse into one gesture, so the deadpan you-know-why refrain lands as the shrug of someone done expecting relief to arrive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind"],"means":"pain becomes the speaker's whole climate rather than an event","built":"sedative, wound, and sweetness collapse into one unreachable gesture"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~if-i-had-a-tail": {"haiku":"Desire for predatory power and control in a dehumanized society obsessed with image over feeling, where having dominance ('a tail') means owning the night and silencing opposition.","opus":"Homme's cool sneer at a glossy, soulless America obsessed with appearance and immortality bought with money. The fantasy of a tail is a wish for animal power in a city of glass with no heart, dancing on its own scars.","sonnet":"A rejection of a society built on surfaces — image over feeling, buying things to fill holes, brainwashed believers — the tail stands for animal freedom and primal dominance outside the sanitized consumer trap.","src":"haiku","fable":"The nonsense syllables and doo-wop cadence work as a lure, a candied surface stretched over a narrator who catalogues raw wanting until joy and hurt trickle down identically, indistinguishable on the cheek. The wished-for appendage is the black comedy at the center: an extra scrap of beast that would confer dominion after dark, floated as the sole sincere impulse inside a showroom culture of flash cars, jeweled fingers, and lavish plots dug only to entomb what shames their owners. Alex Turner's guest vocal, gliding beneath the sneer, deepens the seduction rather than lightening it, so the churchy croon of the faithful reads as recruitment into a congregation of the brainwashed. The discarded-hardware refrain plays as a convalescent machine's complaint, flesh outpaced by its own circuitry, coveting the cold-blooded and the instinctive. Tellingly the vow tightens from wishing for command over darkness to seizing it outright, and the solitary voice dissolves into a chanted plural, private craving mutating into a shared oath that everyone in that brittle transparent metropolis has already sworn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","money & the street"],"means":"private craving mutating into a shared oath inside a brittle transparent metropolis","built":"the solitary voice dissolves into a chanted plural as the wish-for-dominion becomes collective"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~if-only": {"haiku":"The speaker seeks escape from conflict through obliteration or endless drifting; they want laziness, long days without consequence, and freedom from blame.","opus":"The desert-rock band delivers perverse romantic logic in loping deadpan, the narrator wanting a lover only when she's down and rejecting her when she's up. The refrain circles that if only they were nothing at all. Blown minds, endless escapeless days, and a wheel held loose to let things go all point to a relationship prized precisely for its lazy, doomed inertia.","sonnet":"A relationship or situation is caught in contradictory pulls — what hurts one person sustains another, what frees one destroys both. The drifting nihilism of long days with no escape culminates in the admission that neither person amounts to anything on their own, and the if-only hangs over everything unresolved.","src":"haiku","fable":"An early Homme song built on the desert-rock trance logic he carried over from Kyuss: repetition as a narcotic rather than a hook. The verses run a perverse sorting mechanism where the speaker only wants what brings you down, refusing anything that lifts, which reads less as cruelty than as an addict's inverted appetite for the low. That is why the wheel gets held only to be let go of, a driver choosing not to steer and not to know. The endlessly circling refrain never completes its own conditional, and when it finally lands it lands on nothingness at all, so the whole song becomes a wish for erasure that keeps spinning because arriving would mean stopping.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"addict's inverted appetite choosing only what brings the low","built":"the conditional refrain never completes itself, landing finally on nothingness at all"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~in-my-head": {"haiku":"Someone obsesses over an absent lover, playing their song on repeat and depending on the memory to survive the separation.","opus":"Queens of the Stone Age frame romantic fixation as an unfunny torment, the narrator manic and stuck in place, kept apart from the woman he cannot stop thinking about. He clings to a shared tune and treats her memory like a substance he's hooked on, longing that never resolves and only feeds itself.","sonnet":"Absence makes presence more acute — a person plays the same song on repeat to simulate closeness to someone who is gone, or unreachable, or only real inside the mind. The compulsion is tender and desperate in equal measure: the music is all that remains, and turning it up is the only form of contact left.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the compulsive replay of an absence, where the addiction has outlived its object. The favorite song cranked up in an empty room is a stand-in for a person who is now only a resident of the head, and the speaker knows the transaction is rigged against him — running hard while going nowhere, kept apart by the thinnest of margins. What makes it hollow rather than romantic is the collapse of need and want into pure habit: the closing line worries the word need down to a bare demand, and the throwaway paradox about waiting forever in a hurry names the trap exactly, an urgency with no destination. Josh Homme lets the hook's warm insistence do the ironic work, so the addiction sounds like devotion until you notice nothing is coming back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"compulsive replay of an absence whose object has already gone","built":"the hook's warm insistence does the ironic work — the addiction sounds like devotion until nothing returns"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~in-the-fade":{"haiku":"Dissolution into nothing requires no struggle; the speaker accepts disappearance into oblivion as inevitable release.","opus":"A resigned meditation on living only until you die, sung by someone leaving and disappearing without having done anyone any good. The wish to drown into someone's arms sits beside the flat acceptance that there's nothing to save.","sonnet":"A song about quiet resignation to life's indifference and one's own helplessness within it — the speaker counts and breathes in a crumbling room, acknowledges they do no one any good, and cannot laugh or feel their way to something better; the philosophy is not despair but a flat acceptance that you simply live until you die.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sung by guest Mark Lanegan rather than Josh Homme, whose gravel turns exhaustion into scripture, this reads as a suicide note that declines to argue with itself. Its worn maxim about mortality is resignation, not wisdom, and the backing voices chant it while the singer answers with the flattest assent, a mind past resisting. What deepens it is the yearning underneath: a wish to go under held in someone's arms rather than falling alone, so the mantra masks a last unmet need for company as he vanishes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["suicide","love & desire"],"means":"departure preferred only if it can happen held in someone's arms, not alone","built":"the backing voices chant the worn maxim while the singer answers with the flattest assent, a mind past resisting"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~into-the-hollow": {"haiku":"A solitary figure, broken and alone, invites another to follow them into oblivion, where they dwell behind their eyes and break what they borrow.","opus":"Queens of the Stone Age trade their swagger for weary solitude, the narrator waving goodbye in several tongues, too flawed to set things straight and forever solitary. He ruins whatever he's lent and shelters inside his own head, so he ventures onward by himself, extending an invitation into the void he knows may never be accepted, a self-deluded man stepping into emptiness anyway.","sonnet":"A self-described fool who has always been alone invites someone into a private emptiness he cannot enter without them. There is shame in his self-awareness — borrowing things only to break them, living behind his own eyes — yet the pull toward the hollow is genuine, and he refuses to go there solitary again.","src":"haiku","fable":"A serial self-saboteur's love song, sung by someone who has already decided he'll ruin this too. The stacked farewells up front are the tell: he's fluent in leaving, and he opens by leaving before anything has even started. The invitation into the hollow is his one gesture toward company, but it's rigged both ways — he insists he goes alone even as he begs to be followed, and admits he breaks whatever he borrows, so any promise to stay is one he knows he'll void. What holds it together is that the hollow isn't a place but his own interior, the space behind his eyes he lives inside, and the come-with-me is really a warning dressed as seduction: he wants a witness to the disappearance, not a partner against it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"seduction dressed as warning by someone fluent in leaving","built":"the come-with-me is rigged: he insists he goes alone even as he begs to be followed"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~kalopsia": {"haiku":"The singer distorts reality through rose-tinted glasses while dismissing those around them as inferior, expressing love with possessive obsession. Kalopsia—the delusion of beauty in things that are ugly—shapes their worldview.","opus":"Named for the delusion of seeing things as more beautiful than they are, Homme wrapping despair in rose-tint while contempt for careerist phonies festers underneath; the pretty surface is the sickness, and the boat still rows to hell.","sonnet":"About the beautiful distortion that love or desire creates — kalopsia being the condition of seeing things as more beautiful than they are — the song toggles between tender devotion and bitter contempt for the mediocre world outside, insisting that ignoring the bad and surrendering to what feels good is a choice worth making.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title names a specific delusion, that things look kinder than they are, and the song keeps insisting on its own honesty while staining grief the flattering purple of wine to prove it. That gentleness, a tender farewell and a love confessed as beyond steering, is the lacquer, and it cracks the instant the gaze swings outward onto safe imitators in borrowed tailoring and scavengers busy impersonating gods. Homme's sleight is fusing the balm and the scorn into a single motion, since the softening filter that makes private sorrow survivable is exactly what lets contempt for the herd come so cheaply. By the close the fantasy hardens into galley arithmetic, luck rewarding whoever seizes an oar first while the others take the whip, so the real self-deception was believing he floated clear of the same bodies bent to a doomed stroke.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","death & grief","anger & defiance"],"means":"the softening filter that makes grief survivable is also what enables cheap contempt","built":"the title names the delusion the song insists it has escaped while enacting it"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~keep-your-eyes-peeled": {"haiku":"Navigating a dangerous, dreamlike reality where truth is unclear and death looms unexpectedly.","opus":"The opener of ...Like Clockwork is Homme staring down mortality at his bleakest, casting daily survival as picking a path through hazards while distorted reflections stalk him. He fakes composure and half-hopes existence is only a dream he might wake from, dread curdling under a grin that's really just clenched teeth.","sonnet":"A dangerous landscape — emotional and literal — where the speaker warns himself to stay alert while knowing he is already lost. Fallen leaves discovering they are no friend of autumn, a smile that is really just teeth, a life that might be a dream worth waking from: the whole song is a series of images that look peaceful but telegraph disaster in the next breath.","src":"haiku","fable":"Survival is worn here as a threat rather than a gift: stay vigilant precisely because being awake is no longer safe. Homme cleaves himself into a grinning performer and a body going under, the bared grimace and the constant assurance that he's coping standing in for a numbness he finds worse than the flatline he escaped. The pleading to be shaken awake reads not as comfort but as a demand for rescue from that anesthetized calm, where a serene sky glimpsed from the underside of hell turns menacing and the water that promised release becomes suffocation, a thrashing figure surfacing only to be swallowed again. Its parting reversal, that the world is never what it advertises, exposes the smile as camouflage and locates the real horror in having simply come back to breathe.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance","death & grief"],"means":"survival worn as a threat, the smile as camouflage over a flatline escaped","built":"the parting reversal exposes the constant assurance of coping as numbness rather than recovery"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~leg-of-lamb": {"haiku":"A chaotic, confused relationship involving emotional instability and strange power dynamics culminates in blood and domestic tension.","opus":"Josh Homme's wry sketch of a doomed, contradictory fling, a truth-freak liar and a resigned narrator watching it fall apart, shrugging that this staged unreality is just something natural to sink into.","sonnet":"A fractured portrait of a volatile relationship ending — a chaotic woman leaving, a hotel phone call, a bloody apron image suggesting something sacrificial, and a narrator who can't quite process what just happened.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup gets narrated by a man insisting his own detachment is maturity, recasting a lover's exit and his flickering feelings as a channel-flip between an unwatchable relationship and the sitcom rerun he would rather it be. The middle turns confessional and coercive at once, coaxing her through crumbling defenses while reframing the collapse as something natural, nothing to grieve, mere biology rather than betrayal to be answered for. The last stretch drops the pose entirely: swaggering through a downtown night, ringing his own bell like a street huckster, he finally concedes he would only stand back up if he could feel he had actually been knocked down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"recasting collapse as biology so nothing needs to be answered for","built":"the swagger concedes it only holds if he can first feel the blow"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~like-clockwork": {"haiku":"Material promises crumble into entropy; letting go becomes the only path through inevitable decline.","opus":"A weary meditation on impermanence and release, arguing that so much of what we display is mere performance and that departures don't guarantee returns. The resignation that clinging is only fear, and that everything slides downward from here, arrives with hard-won calm.","sonnet":"A meditation on impermanence and the futility of clinging — the world speeds past, promises of love crumble in the light, and the speaker accepts that not everything cycles back, leaving only the certainty of decline.","src":"haiku","fable":"Written by Josh Homme in the aftermath of complications from surgery that nearly killed him and left him bedridden, the song converts a convalescent's disenchantment into a mechanistic creed about how devotion sours. A speaker drifts through darkness longing across a distance that keeps widening, but the argument crystallizes in the refrain's flat denial that giving guarantees any return. From that premise the verses reframe clinging as cowardice wearing affection's mask, so the cold sight that first strips the world of its glamour becomes the very thing that permits the speaker to unclench. The recurring verdict that everything runs downward from this point reads less as anguish than as engineered surrender, an acceptance that entropy governs and calm arrives only once resistance stops.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","heartbreak & loss","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"acceptance as the only exit from entropy","built":"the mechanistic title frames surrender as engineering, not defeat"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~little-sister": {"haiku":"A predatory man positions himself at a vulnerable woman's window, promising love while keeping her hidden in shadow.","opus":"A seductive, faintly ominous plea to a woman to stop living in someone else's shadow and let the narrator in; the coaxing at her window and insistence that he understands who she really is frame desire mixed with the awareness that others warn he'll do her wrong.","sonnet":"The narrator courts a woman who has been living in someone else's shadow — there's an intimacy that borders on the transgressive given the title's framing, but the core is a promise to see her as her own person and draw her out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction told entirely from outside the window, and Homme keeps the desire pinned there deliberately: the singer stands in the dark pleading to be let in, cataloguing his own devotion while everyone around them warns the girl he is bad news. The tension isn't whether she wants him but whether wanting him is another version of hiding — his pitch reframes escape as intimacy, promising that coming to him means stepping out of the shadow she already lives behind. The desert-rock swagger sells this as confidence, but the seducer half-admits the accusation against him even as he asks her to trust that he alone understands who she really is, so the flattery curdles into something closer to a trap dressed as a rescue. What lingers is that the shadow he offers to free her from and the window he keeps her behind may be the same thing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"rescue offered as another form of captivity","built":"the window he pleads through and the shadow he promises to free her from are named as the same enclosure"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~make-it-wit-chu": {"haiku":"The song expresses desire and commitment, repeating a plea to build something together. It dwells on uncertainty and mystery, with the narrator insisting only on knowing what they want: to create something with the listener.","opus":"The speaker admits he understands little about life or love but knows for certain he wants to be intimate with someone, again and again, anytime and anywhere.","sonnet":"Acknowledging that he understands nothing about love's mysteries or origins, the narrator sets all that aside and focuses purely on his uncomplicated desire to be with someone, anytime and anywhere.","src":"haiku","fable":"Homme runs the entire come-on on cheerful ignorance, waving off curses, celestial mechanics, and the arithmetic that fuses two strangers into a couple, so the only fact left standing is a plainly stated hunger. The refusals aren't humility but a filter: each big riddle he declines to solve strips the frame down until desire is the single certainty he'll vouch for, unashamed and uninterrogated. That grinding hook, looping past reason, outlasts every stray thought it might otherwise chase, letting the groove get dumber and hungrier the longer it rides. Sprung from his loose Desert Sessions lounge-blues, the seduction sounds sincere enough to trust and knowing enough to admit it's a well-worn routine.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire"],"means":"desire distilled by refusing to explain itself","built":"each riddle declined strips one more frame away until the groove is the only argument left standing"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~make-it-wit-chu-acoustic": {"haiku":"Willingness to persist in ambiguous intimacy without understanding its true nature.","opus":"A loose, carnal seduction that shrugs off cosmic questions to focus on the one thing that's certain: wanting someone, anytime and anywhere. Not love so much as pure appetite, comfortable in not knowing anything else.","sonnet":"A simple, earnest seduction song — the narrator admits he doesn't understand love or how it starts, but knows what he won't refuse: the chance to be with this person, anywhere and anytime. The directness is the whole point; no metaphysics, just wanting.","src":"haiku","fable":"Homme runs the entire come-on on cheerful ignorance, waving off curses, celestial mechanics, and the arithmetic that fuses two strangers into a couple, so the only fact left standing is a plainly stated hunger. The refusals aren't humility but a filter: each big riddle he declines to solve strips the frame down until desire is the single certainty he'll vouch for, unashamed and uninterrogated. That grinding hook, looping past reason, outlasts every stray thought it might otherwise chase, letting the groove get dumber and hungrier the longer it rides. Sprung from his loose Desert Sessions lounge-blues, the seduction sounds sincere enough to trust and knowing enough to admit it's a well-worn routine.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire"],"means":"desire distilled by refusing to explain itself","built":"the acoustic register softens the come-on without narrowing the loop the hook keeps riding"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~medication": {"haiku":"A new medication transforms society into mindless spreaders of contagion and dysfunction, casting aside those who don't fit.","opus":"A sardonic ode to numbing out, framing pills as a new communal creed everyone loves to swallow, a revelation delivered from a gun, copulation dressed as a song, all the cast-asides becoming what they've become. The tone is contagious and defiant. Queens of the Stone Age deliver it as a short, sneering blast, Josh Homme's dry irony and the band's stoner-rock churn turning medication into both temptation and indictment.","sonnet":"A sardonic hymn to whatever collective drug — ideological, pharmaceutical, erotic — has overtaken everyone. The new way is contagious and involuntary; revelation comes from a gun, not from choice. The speaker is swept up too, cast aside alongside everyone else, and the thing that has become of them all is never named.","src":"haiku","fable":"A blistering sneer of a song that treats mass medication as the era's new communion — everyone dosed, everyone grateful, gulping it down precisely because they love it. Josh Homme's leer keeps collapsing the pill into every other appetite on offer: the fix, the gun's revelation, the sex, all flattened into one interchangeable transaction where possession passes hand to hand and nobody minds being overthrown. Its real venom is aimed at the crowd of the medicated-and-content — the cast-asides who, sedated into agreement, simply became whatever they were told to become, and mistake that surrender for knowing themselves.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control","identity & becoming"],"means":"sedation mistaken for self-knowledge","built":"the pill, the gun, and the sex are flattened into one interchangeable transaction, refusing any hierarchy of appetite"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~mexicola": {"haiku":"Carnal appetite drives the narrative; mortality and sensory indulgence merge in a wasteland of consumption.","opus":"A desert-noir descent into vice and danger, gold light and velvet eyes shading into lies, drugs and violence; the refrain needling that nobody lives forever turns the debauchery into a knowing dance with mortality.","sonnet":"A hazy, violent snapshot of a border-adjacent world where truth and lies blur together, guns are everywhere, and the only constant refrain is that nothing lasts — nobody lives forever, so keep moving.","src":"haiku","fable":"A border-town seduction that keeps peeling back into a death trip, its sunset gilding stacked next to drooling organs and a body already promised to the grave. The chanted taunt does the real work, an audience of hangers-on cheering the speaker deeper into the binge while calmly reminding him that no amount of pressing on cancels the ending. Hedonism here is a mask worn over knowledge, and the exhausted confession that he loathes the old refrain because it happens to be accurate is exactly where that mask finally slides off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief","party & hedonism"],"means":"hedonism worn as a mask over the known ending","built":"the chanted taunt assigns the audience the role of cheering him deeper while calmly reciting the verdict"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~misfit-love": {"haiku":"A dead-inside outsider moves through life seeking thrills and cruelty, proud of their wickedness and already gone before they leave.","opus":"Queens of the Stone Age cut this on Era Vulgaris, its greasy, mechanical riffing and Josh Homme's cocksure sneer making the menace feel almost gleeful. It's an outlaw's boast from a narrator trapped by his origins, determined to triumph by lavishing his aberrant affection on a gullible crowd, ravenous for excitement as he swaggers along already halfway to ruin.","sonnet":"An outcast burning his past behind him, strutting through city gutters with the pride of someone who was never meant to fit in and has stopped trying. The thrill is the point — transformation as compulsion, the dead-man-walking persona already gone and therefore impossible to lose. Bet on him, he insists; he's already won by having nothing left to protect.","src":"haiku","fable":"A peacock strut that keeps confessing it is really a condemned procession, the swagger metered like something being led somewhere it cannot come back from. The narrator peddles himself as the guaranteed winner with a magician's stash of cheap sleights, yet every boast is pure hunger and no tomorrow, a carnival run entirely by its jesters where craving what you fake and craving for real collapse into one indistinguishable itch — the exact vulgar, mechanized appetite the surrounding record keeps grinding at. The final refrain cashes the bravado out as a vanishing trick: wager on this man and you collect, because the prize is a body that has quietly stepped out of the frame, self-mythology and self-erasure exposed as a single motion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"self-mythology and self-erasure as the same motion","built":"the swagger is metered like a condemned procession, the boast's rhythm enacting what it denies"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~monsters-in-the-parasol": {"haiku":"Surreal imagery of disturbing family members and confined growth combines with metaphors of hidden monsters representing distress, divorce, and pain.","opus":"A woozy desert-rock oddity relaying warped visions, walls pressing in, furred things, Paul's melting father and extraterrestrial sibling. A refrain about someone who won't mature and a call-and-response tally of distress, divorce and hurt give it a lurching psychedelic unease. Homme's stoned surrealism turns another's hidden torment into the creature skulking in the sunshade.","sonnet":"A hallucinating or dissociating narrator watches the domestic world around him warp — a father bubbling and deformed, a sister alien — while cataloguing the concealed damages others carry under the surface of ordinary life. The parasol becomes a container for private catastrophes: distress, divorce, pain, all hidden beneath the ordinary shade of daily existence.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a nightmare of intimacy mutating into something inhuman, staged as a haunted-house cartoon where a neighbor's father festers and boils and a sister turns extraterrestrial while the singer's own head drifts loose from its moorings. The engine is the chorus's call-and-response: each accusation of a hidden creature draws a curt little answer — distress, divorce, displeasure — so the freak show quietly resolves into the ordinary machinery of a family and a couple coming apart. By the final turn the creature stops being a thing lurking in the folds of a delicate sunshade and becomes an absence, a gap torn open in the beloved that the narrator admits he failed to register until it was gaping, which recasts every earlier grotesque as a way of not looking straight at the loss.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"First cut as Monster in the Parasol on Desert Sessions Volume 4; Homme has introduced it onstage as a song about LSD.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","family & blood","madness & the mind"],"means":"every grotesque recast as a way of not seeing the real loss","built":"call-and-response monsters answered by the ordinary language of breakup"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~mosquito-song-bonus": {"haiku":"Predatory consumption and survival as a food chain, with humans as helpless prey destined for slaughter.","opus":"A gentle-sounding acoustic closer to Songs for the Deaf that hides a grim message: every living thing is meat awaiting its turn, and nature will eventually consume us all. Josh Homme's band delivers the reminder as a soothing folk lullaby, and that mismatch between the tender melody and the butcher-shop subject is exactly the point.","sonnet":"A patient, carnivorous lullaby — the natural world revealed as a kitchen where everything soft and alive is eventually tenderized, cooked, and consumed. The tone is almost soothing, which makes the argument that all life is just food-in-waiting land with more force.","src":"haiku","fable":"A soft acoustic gait and swelling strings adopt the cradle-song register, then use that tenderness as bait, coaxing the listener toward the slaughterhouse with the same voice that hushes a child. The horror escalates by scale: a whining insect draining blood gives way to the butcher's blades and recipe ledgers, then to soil and roots claiming every body in the end. That widening is the thesis, insisting the single parasite and the whole devouring cycle are one appetite, and framing mortality as tenderizing meat that has merely not yet stopped moving. The lullaby's promised painlessness is the trap's cruelest kindness, a paralytic wrapped in melody so the eating can proceed without struggle.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The bridge's threat — where will you run, where will you hide — answers itself with the phrase lullabies to paralyze, and three years later the band lifted that line for the next album's title: the campfire menace became the name of the record that followed.","themes":["death & grief","nature & the elements","artifice & performance"],"means":"mortality repackaged as a lullaby's promised painlessness","built":"the cradle-song register is the bait; the horror escalates by scale from insect to butcher to soil without ever breaking the hush"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~my-god-is-the-sun": {"haiku":"Hedonistic nihilism where the sun replaces god as source of healing and worship. Godless contempt and freedom from time anchor existence atop a spinning earth.","opus":"A worshipful vision on a barren stretch of highway where the singer kneels before the sun and calls on it to heal with scorching light from on high. It embraces a proud unbeliever's wonder at his own insignificance, a speck of life clinging to a whirling planet.","sonnet":"Far out in an empty desert landscape, the speaker finds a simple, grounding spirituality in the physical sun rather than in conventional religion, kneeling to its fire as a source of healing. The contrast between godless heathens craving something from the sky and this direct solar devotion drives the song.","src":"haiku","fable":"Homme strips a man of watch, road, and ego down to one insect clinging to a whirling planet, until the sun is the only presence vast enough to warrant kneeling. The refrain runs both ways, its solar light mending and executing in the same instant, a healing delivered with the recoil of a firearm so worship and destruction collapse into one posture. Even the disbelievers who scorn every deity keep tilting their faces upward, demanding something from the heavens, which exposes the thesis: unbelief still borrows the geometry of prayer, straining toward a radiance it declines to name. Cut through by mortality after Homme's near-fatal collapse in surgery, the sun stands less as doctrine than as the naked animal will to endure against the encroaching dark.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","violence & murder"],"means":"worship and destruction collapsing into a single posture","built":"the refrain runs both ways, solar light mending and executing in the same instant, so the grammar of prayer cannot separate healing from the kill"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~never-say-never": {"haiku":"Romantic temptation and the possibility of attraction despite reservations about relationships.","opus":"A cover of the Romeo Void song, draped in Queens of the Stone Age's loping desert-rock cool and Josh Homme's sly detachment. It's a wry take on desire and mismatched attraction, the narrator admitting he'd warm to someone more if they were intimate but reading refusal in their gaze, threaded with surreal vignettes of furtive women and a shabby old pair who at least have each other.","sonnet":"A street-level study in desire and deflection — sunsuit girls, a courthouse drunk with windburned skin, an old ugly couple who have each other regardless. The speaker circles a proposition the other person's eyes keep refusing without quite closing the door. The steady observation of others who have paired off, beautifully or badly, keeps the longing alive without resolution.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cover of Romeo Void's 1982 single, and Queens keep its central provocation intact: a flat, repeated proposition about liking someone more after sleeping with them, delivered less as seduction than as a transactional shrug. The vignettes around it refuse to sell intimacy as anything clean — a vagrant bedded down on courthouse newsprint rather than the body heat offered him, two weathered partners no beauty holds together yet who plainly belong to each other. Against those images the come-on curdles: the refrain's real hinge is the hedge that follows it, the eyes that say maybe not, so the subject is finally not desire but the escape clause desire keeps open, wanting on the condition of never being held to the wanting. The title works as promise and refusal wearing the same three words.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"desire kept honest by the escape clause it insists on","built":"the title performs both sides of its own proposition, promise and refusal wearing the same three words"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~no-one-knows":{"haiku":"Following rules and taking pills designed to control, while realizing internal fulfillment and connection exist but remain unknowable to others.","opus":"A disoriented drift through rules, pills, and desire toward someone the narrator both surrenders to and feels a fool for, journeying through a desert of the mind. It closes with a Spanish-language radio-DJ skit introducing the band.","sonnet":"A speaker adrift — rules to follow, pills to swallow, drifting across deserts and oceans — describes surrendering to something or someone that possesses him entirely, recognising himself as a fool for it but unable to resist what feels like a hidden gift.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song sung from inside a chemical fog, where infatuation and sedation blur into the same pleasant surrender. The verses stack surreal, disconnected pictures that resist paraphrase on purpose: a handful of prescribed rules to obey, a capsule that snags in the throat going down, an empty rescue boat rotting in the sun. What actually carries the feeling is the refrain, returning to confess that being possessed by someone leaves the narrator happily, knowingly, an idiot. The Homme-and-Grohl groove locks into a trance to match the trance in the words, and the closing gag, a Spanish-language DJ cutting in to announce the next track, breaks the spell by reframing the whole confession as something drifting out of a car radio, private ache turned anonymous broadcast. The title stops being a phrase and becomes the point: whatever this drugged devotion means stays sealed off, unshareable, grasped by nobody, least of all the man singing it.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"drugged devotion confessed as happily, knowingly idiotic","built":"the Spanish DJ outro reframes the whole confession as anonymous broadcast from a car radio"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~obscenery": {"haiku":"Obscene voyeurism feeds apathy; empathy hollowed out, leaving only void and performative existence.","opus":"Late-period Josh Homme sardonically surveys a hollowed-out, self-absorbed modern world. Coining obscenery for a landscape of emotional amputees and empathy-shaped holes, he skewers manufactured misery, voyeurism, and love for no one but oneself. World-weary and darkly funny, it bids goodnight to a culture too tired of being awake yet incapable of real feeling.","sonnet":"The obscenery — a portmanteau of obscenity and scenery — is the moral landscape of a world full of emotional amputees, phantom pains, and a hole where empathy used to be, and the speaker names it with the gleeful nihilism of someone who has long since stopped pretending there's love for anyone but themselves, while the phantom of the guillotine drowns in a wet dream beneath it all.","src":"haiku","fable":"Homme's coinage fuses obscenity with scenery, so a culture's cruelty and pornographic numbness become a pleasant view offered for the tourist's enjoyment, cheerfully bidding you goodnight from inside the rot. The narrator refuses the outsider's condemnation and works instead as a genial guide, making his come-on and his diagnosis the same seductive voice pointing toward a vanishing horizon. He catalogues atrophy as fashion: showroom-dummy complexions, wellness books peddling narcissism, and severed feeling that still throbs where a heart should sit, all bending toward one creed that permits affection for no one but oneself. The final swerve from drowsy contentment to a jolting scramble for consciousness, arriving only once the hour is spent, exposes how the comfort depended entirely on refusing to look.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","politics & society","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"cultural rot offered as a pleasant view for the tourist","built":"the narrator's come-on and his diagnosis share the same seductive voice, making complicity and critique indistinguishable"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~paper-machete": {"haiku":"Truth proves as fragile and manipulable as the speaker's accusers, revealing that perception shifts like clay while courage crumbles under pressure.","opus":"A withering kiss-off to a cowardly, self-mythologizing manipulator who plays victim to look clean while using everyone around them. Their whole identity is exposed as flimsy papier-mache posturing.","sonnet":"A devastating post-mortem on a manipulative partner: the speaker exposes someone who sculpts truth like clay, plans behind their back, plays victim and lioness with equal fluency, and leaves every relationship in ruin — sharp-tongued but ultimately as harmless and hollow as a paper machete.","src":"haiku","fable":"The garbled title carries the whole thesis: a wad of pulp posing as a cutting edge, menace that dissolves the moment it meets water. Homme takes apart a manipulator who kneads the truth like wet clay and wages every attack by proxy, from a cowardly distance, never once in the open. The address hardens from complaint into a coroner's verdict over a marriage that has stopped breathing, wedding language conscripted to indict the partner who trampled it. What sharpens the wound is the charge of fluency, an ease at swapping the mask of predator for the mask of casualty on demand, which is exactly why the weapon stays flimsy: a costume, not a blade.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"menace that dissolves the instant it meets resistance","built":"the garbled title carries the thesis: pulp posing as a cutting edge, the weapon named before the song begins"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~quick-and-to-the-pointless": {"haiku":"A loose, fragmented track describing a sexually charged encounter with an age difference, rendered in Dutch and English slang without coherent narrative structure or clear meaning.","opus":"A loose, repetitive come-on mixing English and Dutch lines, where an older speaker fixates on a young girl amid vague remarks that life is hard and people grow afraid.","sonnet":"A fragmentary, lustful pursuit of a young woman, mixing Dutch interjections ('life is hard, people get scared,' 'I think yes, but no') with a refrain about age, desire, and a general 'let's go' energy.","src":"haiku","fable":"A leering come-on delivered as a two-minute burst of hooks and grunts, where the title's promise of speed is also its alibi: the song is deliberately dumb, disposable, over before it can be interrogated. What lifts it past filler is the queasy content lurking under the party gloss — the narrator addressing a girl he flags as far too young for the older man he admits to being, the leer never disowned. That the come-on switches into Dutch at its most exposed moments (the cursing, the resignation that life is hard and people grow afraid, the little-girl asides) works as a mask: the ugliest lines are half-hidden behind a language most listeners can't parse, so the hook stays cheerfully singable while the meaning underneath curdles. Fitting the Rated R ethos of grinning provocation, it's a song that lets you enjoy it precisely by not letting you look too closely — quick and to the pointless as both a boast and a dodge.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression","party & hedonism"],"means":"ugliest admissions half-hidden behind an inaccessible language","built":"Dutch switches carry the lines the English hook is meant to obscure"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~regular-john": {"haiku":"Casual predatory interest and sexual pursuit of strangers without regard for consent.","opus":"A prowling study of boredom and appetite, the narrator cold-calling a stranger, sick of himself, hinting he's dangerous and coaxing someone to let him in. From the band's self-titled debut, it rides the robotic, droning pulse Josh Homme carried out of Kyuss, seduction and threat locked into the same groove.","sonnet":"A restless, low-key pursuit — a number on the wall, a call that had to be made, the admission of boredom with the self as much as curiosity about the other person. The repeated open up your eyes, room, arms, door is gentle rather than demanding, more invitation than pressure. The caller is ordinary, self-aware about it, and showing up anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"As the opener of Josh Homme's first record after Kyuss, this sets the template for the whole desert-rock-to-Palm-Desert pivot: seduction rendered as menace, want indistinguishable from predation. The speaker's questions to a stranger start as flirtation and curdle into surveillance — a number copied off a wall, a compulsion to call, an admission that he is not the only one who runs with a blade. Those recited digits work as the song's engine: a phone number and a countdown at once, the intimacy of dialing someone made to sound like a lock being picked. The tell is the flatness of the appetite behind it — the confession of being bored with himself reveals that the target barely matters, that any body, anytime, anyplace will do; the mounting demand to open eyes, room, arms, door is less passion than the patient escalation of someone getting inside. The title's ordinariness is the point: this is not a monster but a regular John, the danger dressed as the guy who just hangs around.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"predation dressed as ordinary longing","built":"the recited phone number functions as both intimacy and countdown, the dialing framed as a lock being picked"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~river-in-the-road": {"haiku":"Sacrifice and escape—one stays to stall a pursuing monster so the other can flee, leaving behind blood and death as a trail.","opus":"Josh Homme's band build a taut sacrifice. A relentless menace advances in formation while the narrator begs a loved one to flee and salvage what they can. He offers to hold the line and be left to rot so they escape, his own spilled blood becoming a promise of revenge, tenderness and threat braided together.","sonnet":"A figure urges a beloved to flee while pledging to delay whatever is coming — an approaching catastrophe figured as a marching column. Sacrifice is offered without sentimentality; the blood left behind becomes a literal river in the road, a landmark rather than a lament.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rearguard song: the speaker chooses to be the one who stays behind and buys time so someone dearer can get clear of an advancing threat. The imagery leans apocalyptic-fairytale — faceless monsters in formation, children whose eyes must be shielded — but the emotional core is a sacrifice made without expecting to survive it, the self left rotting where it stood. The turn comes at the end: what starts as protection curdles into a demand for vengeance paid in the speaker's own blood, so that the road left behind is not just an escape route but a wound. It is less about who the monsters are than about the posture of covering a retreat, where love and self-destruction become the same act.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","love & desire","anger & defiance"],"means":"love and self-destruction converging in the rearguard act","built":"the turn from protection to a demand for blood-paid vengeance recasts the road left behind as a wound rather than an escape route"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~run-pig-run": {"haiku":"A game of pursuit stripped of mercy. The narrator dismisses the target as a sheep performing falsehood and invites them to flee, making play of wronglessness and inevitable chase.","opus":"A predator's taunt dressed as a childhood game, where the hunter has stopped pretending to be righteous and simply enjoys the chase; the cop or coward on the run finds there is nowhere left that's safe.","sonnet":"A pursuer who never played fair by normal rules now turns a childhood game of hide-and-seek into a hunt, stalking someone who has spoken dishonestly like a sheep. There is no safe place left to hide.","src":"haiku","fable":"A nursery pastime curdles into a manhunt: the counting-down ritual becomes a predator's advance on quarry that never truly entered the contest, only mimicked meekness to dodge notice. The speaker waves off any halo, cheerfully conceding he holds no moral ledger, and that shrug is the menace — not righteousness but appetite, indifferent to who deserves what. Cast against the leering, machine-oily churn of the record, the taunt lands as pure sport, the pursuer relishing a chase whose ending was fixed before the first step, and the fugitive learns the cellar door was always bolted from outside.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder"],"means":"appetite indifferent to moral ledger framed as sport","built":"the nursery-countdown ritual is conscripted as a predator's advance, the children's game's structure enacting a chase whose ending was fixed before it started"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~six-shooter": {"haiku":"Violent confrontation and road rage, with graphic threats of murder.","opus":"A brief, unhinged eruption of roadside menace, all threats to gun down a stranger and their friends, broken up by shouted gunfire noises. It's a jolt of pure aggression rather than a story; the band hand the mic to Nick Oliveri for a frantic burst of trigger-happy chaos.","sonnet":"Pure aggression with no narrative — road rage and a loaded weapon, two glass eyes, threats hurled at nobody specific. The song is less about a subject than about the physical sensation of barely contained violence looking for somewhere to land.","src":"haiku","fable":"The shortest, most violent thing on Songs for the Deaf, sung by Nick Oliveri as a pure adrenaline scream, and it works precisely because it refuses to be a song. Where the album's driving concept is a radio dial cycling through hostile stations, this is the burst of static-rage that no format could contain — a roadside psychotic promising slaughter, the counted-off shots standing in for verse and chorus alike. The point is the eruption itself: a minute of unmediated aggression that reads less as a threat to take seriously than as the record letting its ugliest voice off the leash, the id the rest of the album keeps just barely tuned out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"pure eruption that refuses to be domesticated into song","built":"within Songs for the Deaf's radio-dial concept, this is the burst of static no format could contain, the counted-off shots substituting for verse and chorus"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~smooth-sailing": {"haiku":"A man pursues reckless freedom and damage, burning bridges while rejecting fear and reason, sailing through life without restraint or second chances.","opus":"From ...Like Clockwork, a strutting anthem of reckless self-destruction dressed up as breezy confidence, the narrator torching his past with nothing left to protect. Bruised but unbothered, he mocks fear itself and welcomes whatever wreckage lies ahead. Josh Homme frames ruin as effortless, cocksure momentum.","sonnet":"A speaker burns through caution and history to arrive at a state of total self-directed momentum — bridges down, illusions dissolved, operating from pure self-certainty. The bravado is genuine rather than hollow; fear is identified as a puppet mechanism, and the declaration of smooth sailing is less a prediction than a chosen posture toward whatever comes next.","src":"haiku","fable":"Josh Homme's strut is a costume, and it plays like a convalescent daring the room to doubt him — invincibility worn by a man just back from the edge. The braggadocio is loud on purpose — a body itemized by its scars as if wreckage were a medal, a private soundtrack following him everywhere like proof he still matters. What betrays the act is naming fear outright as the puppeteer at the wires; you don't identify the tormentor that precisely unless it still owns you. Late on, the nautical bluster hardens into pulpit-talk about gilded temples and the mind that walls itself into its own hell, and the closing hush snapped by a gunshot syllable slams the seduction shut, a man catching himself mid-performance and firing on the pose. The cheer is a mantra chanted at weather that hasn't broken yet, and the track never once believes it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"invincibility performed by a man who named his tormentor","built":"the closing gunshot syllable fires on the pose mid-performance, the arrangement catching itself bragging and slamming it shut"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~suture-up-your-future":{"haiku":"The narrator stitches closed his past wounds and self-destructive patterns, refusing false comfort and choosing raw truth even if it hurts.","opus":"A song about closing wounds and burying the past, sewing up a future while getting evils out through a needle, wanting only what's real even if it hurts. Homme threading regret and self-repair together, the surgery imagery doubling as kicking a habit and moving on.","sonnet":"About trying to sew yourself back together after being destroyed by something — the narrator has been through addiction or self-harm or a violent emotional rupture, and the act of suturing up the future means burying the past, stitching the wound shut, and moving forward without looking back.","src":"haiku","fable":"Self-repair rendered as surgery: the suture language turns getting-over-something into a violent, clinical act, closing a wound rather than letting it knit clean, forgetting prescribed like anesthetic. The needle carries two charges at once, stitch and narcotic, so the vow to sew up what comes next reads as recovery and relapse folded together, a cure that mostly numbs. Josh Homme refuses the easy release: he'd sooner feel a hurt that's genuine than perform being fine, yet his only forward motion is to photograph the past and inter it. The confession lands hardest when he admits pouring tenderness into whatever was busy shredding it, and the resolution is amputation, snip the frayed threads, seal them off, and call it simply the way things are.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","heartbreak & loss","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"recovery prescribed as a violence done to the wound","built":"the needle carries stitch and narcotic at once, so cure and relapse are folded into the same instrument","fnote":"The needle threads a third register besides stitch and fix — the eye of the needle, scripture's impossible gate — so sewing the future shut also prices what it costs to pass through."},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~tangled-up-in-plaid": {"haiku":"Impermanence and self-harm coexist as the narrator oscillates between possession and liberation, wounding and freedom.","opus":"A restless drifter who can't stay and won't let himself be held, framing a doomed connection as a game both agree to lose; the self-inflicted wounds and bodies in the wake point to someone destructive to himself and everyone near.","sonnet":"A speaker caught in a carelessly begun entanglement acknowledges he could hold on but knows the other person needs to be free, even as he names her self-destructive power over everyone around her.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man narrates his own unreliability as though it were weather, blowing through a life on impulse and already halfway out the door before anyone can lean on him, so that leaving becomes less a decision than a native condition. He proposes love as a rigged contest where both agree to fold, since an actual victory would sound the alarm that says someone has been captured, and being captured is the one intimacy neither can survive. The generosity he offers her — releasing his claim, granting her liberty because she was never his to keep — mutates in the second telling into something colder, the release now a nudge toward oblivion, as if tenderness and abandonment had been the same act all along. Her talent, he admits, is a precise self-wounding that empties every room around her, the collateral stacking behind a person too gifted at ruin to be rescued, and his complicity hides inside the flattery of calling it her freedom.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"tenderness and abandonment exposed as the same act","built":"the second telling of the release reframes generosity as a nudge toward oblivion, the pronoun shift hardening what the first pass called kindness"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~tension-head": {"haiku":"A person experiences chronic sickness and existential despair, resorting to unhealthy behaviors to escape feelings and the bathroom floor as their containment.","opus":"A raw, jittery portrait of methamphetamine addiction from the band's early desert days, the narrator sick on the bathroom floor yet cooking up again. The lowlife chant is the honest bottom of the high they chase.","sonnet":"Drug addiction at rock bottom — waking up sick, cooking up on the bathroom floor, hustling and being hustled, the cycle of needing to get out and get clean while being entirely consumed by the compulsion to use.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of dependency narrated from inside the sickness, where the speaker rehearses the same ritual of striking a light and cooking a dose until the track reads less like a story than a treadmill nobody manages to step off. Its real engine is the refrain, a single sneered word for wealth's opposite that answers each line like a heckler in the dark, flattening the addict's grand appetites down to one gutter verdict that never varies. When the bridge stacks its vow to break loose, the promise rots mid-breath into a bare negation that reverses its own meaning, so the moment of resolve folds straight back into the compulsion it swore to abandon.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","money & the street"],"means":"the vow to break loose folds straight back into compulsion","built":"a sneered single word answers every verse like a fixed heckler"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~the-evil-has-landed": {"haiku":"Apocalyptic arrival or transformation, with society unraveling and boundaries dissolving.","opus":"An invitation to a reckless binge and a brush with mortality, a lavish macabre revue and final procession where you can act the clown or the almighty and go along either way. The pivot is release, the narrator tumbling past all labels and declaring himself in favor of surrender as menace storms in. Queens of the Stone Age, Josh Homme's desert-rock band, build it into a sprawling carnival that dares everyone to clear the path.","sonnet":"A carnival of entropy — the speaker invites everyone onto a living spree, mocking the polite facades and grand parades of ordinary life, then pivots to embrace letting go entirely. The arrival announced in the title is the arrival of chaos, and the crowd is already complicit.","src":"haiku","fable":"Josh Homme turns the seize-the-day sermon inside out, letting a smiling recruiter wave you nearer while peddling a binge of pure motion that mimics being alive without ever touching it. The parade imagery does the arguing: a gilded funeral cabaret rolling forward while its stragglers fall out of step, and the whole scene rigged so that choosing between jester and deity dissolves into merely joining the singalong. At the hinge the speaker admits he is diving past every category that once held him, which recasts the sales pitch as mass surrender, a crowd told to loosen and release itself. The intimate whisper of drawing near curdles by the finish into a stampede shouting to clear the road, so the titular menace is no devil but the swarm's own inertia, deaf to protest, sweeping you forward the instant you consent to lean in.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"mass surrender repackaged as a seize-the-day sermon","built":"the intimate whisper of drawing near expands into a stampede by the finish, the scale shift exposing the crowd's inertia as the titular menace"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~the-fun-machine-took-a-s-and-died": {"haiku":"Casual cruelty and helplessness define hierarchy; the powerless offer no resistance.","opus":"A sneering taunt to a small-time tyrant destined for the very bottom of the heap, told with QOTSA's gallows swagger. The real fight is against yourself, and you keep losing it, dead already whether you know it or not.","sonnet":"About contempt for someone who thought they could play the game but is now exposed as a fraud — the narrator taunts a would-be tough guy (\"little king, little crown\") whose pretensions have collapsed, using the spoken-word outro to invoke a kind of spiritual death. The fun machine breaking down is the collapse of whatever performance kept this person afloat.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sneering address to a small-time despot who mistook a toy scepter for genuine dominion, delivered with the greasy, mechanical swagger Josh Homme lends to men who overrate themselves. The menace is theater, not combat — brandished pistols, a blade lodged behind the shoulder blades where no arm can twist to grab it — because the argument is that the mark was finished before the standoff began: the refrain needling that ambition outran ability, the true adversary the vain fool staring back from the mirror. The film-noir taunt sharpens the contempt, denying the target any tough-guy legend and shrinking him to spoiled cargo overstuffed into a sack built for half the load. A mock-liturgical recitation floats through, invoking a sacramental seat and a soul that let its final glimmer slip away, recasting the whole insult as last rites muttered over someone too puffed-up to register that the burial already happened.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"contempt delivered as last rites over the already-buried","built":"the mock-liturgical recitation floats through a film-noir taunt, the sacramental register shrinking the target to spoiled cargo even as it eulogizes him"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~the-lost-art-of-keeping-a-secret": {"haiku":"A shared secret and the commitment to never reveal a hidden bond. The narrator takes this secret to the grave, preserving intimacy through silence.","opus":"A vow of secrecy between two people sharing something outsiders must never learn, the narrator urging the other to breathe a word to no one. He pledges the lengths he'd go to protect it, carrying their private tune with him to the grave.","sonnet":"A song about the intimacy of a shared secret — something between two people that must never be disclosed to anyone else. The speaker swears absolute loyalty to this pact, insisting he would take their secret to the grave rather than let it reach the outside world.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song disguised as a hostage note, which is the whole point: the secret and the affair are the same thing, and the pleasure lives in the withholding. The verses court doubt only to seal the bond tighter — every offer to cut someone in is paired with cutting them out — while the chorus hardens into a single command repeated until it stops being an instruction and becomes the relationship itself. QOTSA build this on Josh Homme's signature Rated R engine, a swinging desert-rock groove that stays deceptively breezy and radio-friendly precisely so the menace under the vow reads as devotion rather than threat. The bridge is where the mask drops without breaking: the promise to carry the song to the grave frames total silence not as loss but as the purest form of keeping faith, the lost art of the title reclaimed. What could have been paranoia becomes a boast — that discretion is the last intimacy two people can still own alone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"withholding the secret as the fullest act of devotion","built":"the repeated chorus command hardens into the relationship itself"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~the-sky-is-fallin": {"haiku":"The sky falls and humans are left crawling, having wasted time on hatred and blame, now learning that such long negativity has drained their life.","opus":"A settling of accounts with squandered fury, a narrator who fixated so long on grievance that resentment robbed him of everything, now urging himself to quit hurling blame and stop returning to the pit that bled him dry. Shutting his eyes, he watches the heavens collapse. The band placed this on Songs for the Deaf, and the closing fake-DJ skit, ruining the record for the whole audience, is pure desert-rock mischief.","sonnet":"A song about the cost of hatred — not catastrophe falling from outside, but the slow drain of a life spent seeing only what's wrong. The speaker reaches a moment of realisation: the hatred was the thing that drained him, not the world. Closing your eyes to see the sky fall reads as the violent release of finally letting that perspective go.","src":"haiku","fable":"Set against an album pathologically fixated on the deadening loop of radio and the road, this is where the narrator halts long enough to reckon with what perpetual motion has already emptied out of him. Disaster becomes deliverance: attention arrives late and expensive, yet the vow not to sink back toward that hollowed-out floor keeps hardening even as the phrasing frays and gives out under repetition. The refrain smuggles a paradox — shutting the eyes is the only way to actually witness the ruin, since fixing them on the blinding glare of endless activity is precisely what wrecked his sight to begin with. Retreat, in this reading, is the closest thing to lucidity on offer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"shutting the eyes as the only way to see the ruin","built":"the refrain smuggles a paradox where closing sight is lucidity, the fraying repetition enacting the very unraveling the phrasing tries to halt"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~the-vampyre-of-time-and-memory": {"haiku":"Someone alone in a crowd questions their identity and whether they're loved, feeling incomplete and noting that the true vampire is time and memory that drain meaning from experience.","opus":"A bleak confession of numbness, the narrator begging to be taken away because he feels nothing and no longer knows his own identity. Having survived a crisis that once drained him, he finds he's still breathing but hollow, insisting that outliving the pain is not the same as being healed.","sonnet":"About emerging from a period of emotional death and numbness — the vampyre of time and memory that was killing you has died, and you're left alive but incomplete, still unable to feel love. Survival without restoration.","src":"haiku","fable":"Homme wrote this in the aftermath of a botched operation that stopped his heart on the table and left him bedridden and depressed for months, and the song is a convalescent taking stock of what came back with him. It advances as interrogation rather than statement, each question about his place among others and the point of continuing collapsing into the same numb verdict, reported with the detachment of someone reading a chart. Recovery, it argues, lives in coming undone on purpose, in refusing to hold the wound closed — a demand the singer can no longer meet, having been anesthetized past the point of feeling anything to expose. So the triumphant claim that the ghoul draining his years is dead arrives hollow: he has beaten the thing and won only the bare mechanics of a pulse, cheering his own vital signs while the warmth those signs are supposed to prove never switches back on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"survival reduced to bare mechanics with the warmth never returning","built":"the song advances as interrogation rather than statement, each question collapsing into the same numb verdict without ever resolving into claim"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~the-way-you-used-to-do": {"haiku":"The speaker recalls an intense, consuming love affair that burned bright but asks the other to recapture its earlier passion and devotion.","opus":"A reckless, all-devouring romance that treats the wider world as disposable: sprint until we're untraceable, let it detonate behind us, I'll put down anyone who tries to pen us in. Underneath runs a nostalgic ask to be loved as before. The band cut this as the swaggering, swing-tinged opener of Villains, produced by Mark Ronson, Homme leaning equally into groove and menace.","sonnet":"A love remembered as pure combustion — meeting young, running until no one could find them, burning so hot they didn't notice the world ending behind them. Now some distance has opened and he's asking for that original heat back, insisting nothing else matters, their particular wildness worth defending against anyone who'd try to contain it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Homme builds a rockabilly stomp around a couple who treat combustion as courtship, meeting like a struck flame and a dry field, delighted to burn rather than merely warm. He asks whether such heat is a sickness or a stroke of feverish luck, then waves the distinction away, since the same wildness that let the two vanish past any pursuit also produced children he expects to menace the tidy and the ordinary. Beneath the swaggering shuffle sits a genuinely rattled request: return to the earlier intensity, because his chest now knocks like a cheap toy bell and his fatalistic shrug barely hides that the strings are pulling him. The danceable menace of the arrangement lets his vow to bury any meddler read at once as a besotted oath and a wink at how thoroughly he has lost his footing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","nostalgia & memory","party & hedonism"],"means":"combustion as courtship, the return to heat as a rattled request","built":"the danceable rockabilly swagger lets the vow to bury any meddler read as besotted oath and self-aware wink at once"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~time-place":{"haiku":"Confrontation meets space between things. Promises smoke while desire burns slow—the emptiness persists.","opus":"A seedy come-on to someone the singer sees clearly as a slow-motion self-destruction, all empty promises like smoke, yet he still wants them here and now regardless of how wrong it is.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship that has run its course with someone as self-destructive as a slow-burning cigarette — the narrator calls the other person out for the dead weight they carry in their chest, their broken promises that dissolve like smoke, and ultimately invites them to either turn things around or get it over with.","src":"haiku"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~un-reborn-again": {"haiku":"Seeking youth and rebirth through the fountain of youth, drowning in illusions of renewal.","opus":"Josh Homme's desert-rock band unrolls a woozy, vaudevillian parade of grotesque characters around a doomed hunt for immortality, someone trapped forever just out of reach of eternal youth. The wordplay circles humanity's vain, hardwired craving to cheat death, everyone destined to drown in the very fountain they're chasing.","sonnet":"A cast of eccentric figures orbiting the same obsession — everyone buried so close to the fountain of youth they can almost touch it, frozen in amber, drowning in the very thing they were reaching for. The vision is lucid and the diagnosis is that the desire for eternal youth is itself the trap.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cast of nicknamed grotesques files past like a rogues' gallery of the desert-scene demimonde — the junkie, the predator, the diva, the devil lapping gutter runoff as though it were nothing strange — each a portrait of ruin held mid-collapse. The title carries the argument: not reborn but un-reborn, vitality running in reverse toward the womb, and the sustained image clinches it, a figure set forever in amber, spared decay only because it is already a corpse, close enough to the reviving spring to graze it yet forbidden a single sip. Homme's stroke is to unmask deathlessness as embalming: the hunger for endless youth renews no one, it fixes the body like an insect trapped in resin, and by the coda the miracle water becomes a shared drowning where everyone sinks into the grandeur they mistook for rescue. The wordless syllables that end it — childish sounds crumbling into a tuneless hum — enact the regression the title vowed, speech itself unmade, meaning dissolving toward infant babble as the pose seals shut.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief","freedom & escape"],"means":"deathlessness unmasked as embalming, not renewal","built":"the wordless closing syllables enact the title's promise, speech itself unmade toward infant babble as un-rebirth seals shut"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~villains-of-circumstance": {"haiku":"Separation and distance testing a love that persists despite impossible circumstances and time.","opus":"A weary long-distance love song, the narrator trapped by the miles between him and someone he aches for, promising to do anything and pleading to be rescued from fate before he comes undone. Closing ...Like Clockwork, it swaps Homme's usual menace for plain, tired devotion that vows to endure endlessly.","sonnet":"Long-distance longing that refuses to let geography become destiny — separated from the person he loves by distance and time, the speaker insists on holding on through sheer devotion, singing only for them, patiently and with everything he has. The fear is that life moves on without him while he waits, and the plea to be saved from circumstance is entirely earnest.","src":"haiku","fable":"Josh Homme frames long-distance devotion as a hostage situation, blaming not a lover or a rival but the accident of geography that keeps two people on opposite continents. Every extravagant vow to rearrange the planet collapses against the sober medical fact that grief has no antidote, so the sweeping promise and the shrug of helplessness arrive fused in one exhausted breath. The tenderness aches because it is spent chasing a quarry with no name, a thing felt vividly yet never overtaken, and the on-and-on refrain works less as comfort than as a charm muttered on repeat because incantation is the last leverage a stranded man has left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","heartbreak & loss","exile & diaspora"],"means":"devotion blamed on geography, not on the lover","built":"sweeping vow and helpless shrug fused in one breath"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~walkin-on-the-sidewalks": {"haiku":"Technological programming and loss of individual identity, with characters disappearing into space.","opus":"A spacey, alien-eyed early cut where the narrator feels like software, his teeth wobbling, his speech carrying no weight since it costs nothing, drifting off into orbit and half-recognizing a look-alike stranger. From their self-titled debut, it finds the band still soaked in Homme's stoner-rock roots, hypnotic and dissociated, the pavement a runway into detachment.","sonnet":"A disoriented speaker who has been reprogrammed enough to float free of identity — the person they used to know looked a lot like the person who replaced them, and now both are gone. The imagery of loose teeth and new programs and words being weightless because they are free creates a portrait of someone drifting in the aftermath of whatever was lost, untethered and far away.","src":"haiku","fable":"Josh Homme's desert-generator origins surface here as a hypnotic robotic groove where selfhood is diagnosed like a malfunctioning machine: rattling molars, rolling eyeballs, wheezing lungs, a nervous system rebooted by whatever new software has just been installed. The come-on toward escape and cheap intoxication lands with no heat at all, pure deadpan, and the mirrored strangers — one resembling you, one resembling the singer — dissolve into interchangeable parts overwritten by fresh programming. That numb hollowing peaks in the claim that speech carries no cost because nothing costs anything on this rock, meaning zeroed out until the narrator can only report himself as vanished, adrift somewhere past orbit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming","surveillance & control"],"means":"selfhood diagnosed as a malfunctioning machine","built":"come-on lands deadpan, heat stripped from the escape offer"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~who-ll-be-the-next-in-line": {"haiku":"Romantic rejection and heartbreak, warning the next lover they will face the same pain.","opus":"A caustic taunt aimed at a lover walking out, wondering who will inherit the heartbreak they inflict, who will repeat the same errors, and insisting the narrator was the finest they ever knew. It's a Kinks cover, and Queens of the Stone Age recast Ray Davies's early rejection in their heavy, hypnotic desert-rock groove, the sting of being discarded honed by Josh Homme's cool delivery.","sonnet":"A breakup addressed to whoever comes next — the speaker has already left and wants the future partner to know what they are inheriting: a person whose affections fade, whose love cannot hold. The bitterness is resigned rather than scorching, landing finally on the certainty that she will only understand what she had once it is already gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cover of the Kinks' 1965 single — a B-side at home, an A-side in the States —, and the interest is entirely in the posture the song strikes: a jilted lover who reframes his own dismissal as a warning to whoever comes after him. The question that names the song is never really a question — it is a curse dressed as concern, the speaker insisting his replacement will inherit the same heartache because he cannot admit the fault might live in him rather than in her. What holds it together is the pivot to past tense, the boast that once he's gone she'll finally recognize what she squandered; the whole thing runs on the fantasy of vindication after departure, which is why the title line keeps recurring rather than resolving. In Queens' hands the garage-rock petulance gets played straight, letting the bravado curdle into what it always was — a man talking himself out of grief by pretending he saw it coming.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance"],"means":"curse at the successor dressed as a warning","built":"title question recurs without resolving, stalling the vindication it promises"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~you-think-i-ain-t-worth-a-dollar-but-i-feel-like-a-millionaire":{"opus":"A hedonistic radio-framed anthem of craving and excess, demanding ever more indulgence, thrills, gore and gratification.","sonnet":"A high-energy, fragmented ode to heavy rock excess — toro (bullishness), booze, B-movies, and raw appetite for more.","src":"sonnet","haiku":"Inflated self-worth and hedonistic swagger parade across the song as the narrator revels in excess and dismisses conventional judgment. Material accumulation and sensory indulgence substitute for deeper meaning or restraint.","fable":"The opening DJ chatter names the enemy up front: a corporate station engineered to blur into the background, blandness on a loop, mocking the very record it introduces. Against that beige void the song offers nothing but hunger, a rubble of tough-guy scraps that never cohere into a sentence, each one bolted to a single rising plea to keep the fix coming. The bullfight image is the pivot, the singer both the taunting matador and the animal drained on the sand, so the title's flush-broke bravado turns out to mean being the creature emptied for a crowd's amusement. Everything eventually caves into that one chant for more, looped past meaning, until the demand is all that survives and the appetite has swallowed its own object.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the appetite looping past its object until the demand is all","built":"the bullfight image splits the singer into matador and drained animal"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~you-would-know": {"haiku":"Numbness, alienation, and dangerous home conditions where a father has violent tendencies.","opus":"A druggy, paranoid vignette of a contented misfit warning that danger, temptation, and a loaded threat are lurking, while the chorus plays coy with a knowing shrug. From the Rated R era, the band pair menace with detachment, Mark Lanegan's gravel deepening the sense that something ugly is being half-hidden.","sonnet":"Oblique and tightly compressed — a robot on a hill of beans, a girl out of focus, a loaded gun with crosshairs. The refrain you would know is the only thing that coheres, repeated until it becomes accusation and acknowledgment at once: the person addressed understands something being withheld, or should, or will. The cutting motif runs through it like a threat that never fully lands.","src":"haiku","fable":"A druggy address to someone whose mind has gone mechanical and small, delivered in the band's early motorik trance where menace hides under a deadpan hum. The barber's shears and the loaded rifle rhyme as instruments of subtraction, trimming a person down until nothing human is left to defend. Homme keeps returning the accusation to the accused rather than pressing it, letting the mantra become its own verdict: guilt confirmed not by argument but by the certainty that the other party already carries it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder"],"means":"guilt confirmed by mantra, not by argument","built":"accusation returned to the accused each time rather than pressed forward"},
"queen~killer-queen":{"haiku":"A dangerous woman moves through high society with calculated seduction and mystery, leaving trails of broken men and shattered illusions in her glamorous, predatory wake.","opus":"A sketch of a high-class courtesan whose refined taste and lethal charm make her an irresistible, dangerous luxury.","sonnet":"A portrait of a high-society courtesan who moves through the world with total control — Moët in her cabinet, caviar, dynamite with a laser beam — offering herself as a guaranteed experience while revealing nothing and leaving no forwarding address.","src":"haiku"},
"queen~we-will-rock-you":{"haiku":"Life's stages demand defiance and self-assertion against systems that seek to contain.","opus":"The stomping arena anthem follows one figure across three stages of life, boy, hot-blooded youth, and broken elder, each humiliated and shoved back down where he belongs. Under the crowd's chant it's a blunt reminder that time levels every grand ambition.","sonnet":"Three stages of a life — boy, young man, old man — each confronted with mud, blood, and disgrace, each needing to be put back in their place; the crowd becomes the judge and the metronome, stamping and clapping the verdict that each figure will be rocked.","src":"haiku"},
"r-e-m~losing-my-religion":{"haiku":"Obsessive devotion spirals into desperation and confusion; the speaker monitors another desperately while doubt corrodes their ability to sustain faith.","opus":"An anxious, unspoken infatuation in which the singer fears he's revealed too much and can't tell whether the other's attention was ever real. Losing my religion, a Southern phrase for reaching the end of one's rope, captures obsession dissolving into doubt and dream.","sonnet":"Obsessive devotion framed as religious fervour: the speaker watches someone from the corner, monitoring every gesture, trying to keep up while saying too much and too little — \"that's me in the spotlight, losing my religion\" — and the confession spirals into fantasy until even the fantasies fail and it collapses into just a dream.","src":"haiku"},
"r-f-s-du-sol~lost-in-my-mind":{"haiku":"Mental introspection and urban alienation fragment coherence; the speaker loses himself in thought while the external world crumbles and becomes inescapable.","opus":"Dissociation as refuge, a mind that wanders off all night while the world seems to collapse around it; the quiet twist is preferring the isolation, finding the empty corner of one's own head a place worth getting lost in.","sonnet":"A person who has spent the night lost in their own thoughts finds they cannot escape the spiral — the sky keeps falling, sleep is gone, and the inner voice keeps pulling them under — until they locate a quiet corner of the mind and decide, unexpectedly, that being lost there feels like freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"r-t~de-rerum-natura":{"opus":"A bleak materialist read of existence: people as machines executing biological code, choice an illusion, meaning a soothing fiction. Certainty comes only at the moment of universal death, and living amounts to the toll exacted by our ignorance.","sonnet":"A pessimistic, materialist meditation on human existence as a mechanical, pre-determined process — the speaker argues that free will is an illusion, that biology and atavistic drives control all behavior, and that the systems humans build — religions, ideologies, hopes — are just elaborate distractions from the fact that life is suffering paid for by ignorance.","src":"sonnet","fable":"Borrowing its title from Lucretius, røt invert his atomist consolation: where the Roman argued that a cosmos of blind particles should dissolve our dread, here the same grinding of gears and inherited biological triggers becomes a sentence, free will demoted to one more tooth in the mechanism. The track argues like a geometric proof, climbing from the trapped body up through every scaffold we erect to endure it — creeds, dogmas, the values and hopes we invent — and recasts each as a soothing fiction the machine spins to keep copying itself. What lifts it past nihilist posturing is the mid-song plunge into first person, a voice confessing it has never wrung any pleasure from existence nor felt kinship with the matter composing it, which turns the cosmic case into an account of estrangement. The close delivers its verdict as a reversal of the whole Epicurean promise: significance surfaces only when all things perish, and existence itself becomes the toll levied for our blindness — not the knowing that liberates, but the knowing that there was never anything to be freed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","alienation & emptiness","death & grief"],"means":"atomist consolation inverted so the same mechanism becomes a sentence rather than a release","built":"the mid-song first-person plunge turns the cosmic argument into a confession of personal estrangement"},
"r-w-babicze~w-gor-cej-wodzie-company":{"haiku":"Survival and resilience through chaos, crude humor masking genuine struggle, finding wit in degradation.","opus":"A crude, absurdist Polish rap romp reveling in vulgar provocation and self-deprecating chaos. Between hangovers, hot baths, and Adam-and-Eve gags, the narrator flaunts a lack of self-respect and a refusal to be tamed, drowning boasts in filth and comic bravado. The humor is deliberately obscene, mocking machismo even as it wallows in it.","sonnet":"A Polish rap sprawl of weekend chaos, self-aware mediocrity, and defiant vulgarity — the narrator is \"hot-water-baptized,\" hung over, visited by unwanted callers, dismissing hype and moral posturing alike while reveling in a deliberately low-stakes existence. The comedy is the point: every grandiose claim deflates into another mundane absurdity.","src":"haiku"},
"r-yksopp~running-to-the-sea":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic terror and surrender merge; the speaker flees civilisation, pulled underwater by forces beyond resistance.","opus":"A survivor fleeing some violent catastrophe toward the water, houses burning behind and fear blinding the way; the sea becomes both escape and dissolution, a swelling river of grief growing inside long after the running stops.","sonnet":"A survivor flees a scene of catastrophic violence — burning buildings, darkness, blind terror — reaching the sea only to sink to the ocean floor, while something vast and primal continues to grow inside, unextinguished. The imagery suggests trauma held in the body long after the external destruction has ended.","src":"haiku"},
"rabbit-junk~gravity-hero":{"haiku":"Illusory transcendence masks sinking despair; the speaker claims defiance of natural law while actually drowning, mistaking surface performance for flight.","opus":"A taunting rebuke to someone pretending to rise above life's forces, telling themselves they float while they're really sinking. Against their self-deluding defiance of gravity, the song insists the tightrope act can't hide that there's no love, light or hope holding them up.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone performing transcendence they don't actually possess: the speaker insists gravity is a state of mind and they will fly, while telling the other — \"you're no gravity hero\" — that sinking while appearing to float doesn't change the tidal forces pulling you under.","src":"haiku"},
"rabbit-junk~heavy":{"haiku":"Cumulative weight crushes the spirit day by day; escape proves impossible while desire for others' approval chains the mind.","opus":"The grinding weight of a world that demands everything and leaves you a husk, dragging yourself uphill just hoping to survive the war intact. The mounting refrain enacts the pressure it describes.","sonnet":"The weight of endless demands from others — 'they want it all, turn you out, skin you raw' — becomes a physical burden dragged uphill, hoping to sustain through the grind; the closing image of pressure on the chest with no rest frames endurance as the only available response.","src":"haiku"},
"rabbit-junk~love-is-hell":{"haiku":"Love binds two imperfect selves together in mutual suffering and endless falling, creating beauty from the collision of desire and damage. The song mourns the price of connection while celebrating its terrible necessity and transformative power.","opus":"Romantic union sold as instinct and salvation is met with a perfectionist's dread of falling, staring into the abyss of intimacy. Love is reframed as the exquisite, painful price paid for everything learned, hell and all.","sonnet":"Love is dissected as a site of ego collapse and masochistic endurance — the speaker falls endlessly toward someone despite the cold dark horror of looking into the abyss, concluding that love and death share the same last breath.","src":"haiku"},
"rabbit-junk~neurodivergent":{"opus":"A neurodivergent protest against being treated as a disorder, rejecting a society run by small-minded despots that regards a differently-wired mind as a creature to be trained and normalized, and insisting that framework can be torn down.","sonnet":"A protest song from the perspective of someone whose neurodivergent brain is forced through compliance tests and normalization programs they find alien and oppressive — rejecting the systems built by neurotypical people while asserting solidarity with others who think differently.","src":"sonnet"},
"rabbit-junk~relentless-omicron-nu-epsilon":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and relentless struggle define existence as the narrator refuses to surrender despite overwhelming force. Resilience becomes a badge worn through constant battle against systems designed to crush resistance.","opus":"Sleeplessness and looming panic recast as a stubborn vow never to remain down, hoisting himself upright each time circumstance and the passing hours flatten him. Whether descending or soaring, breathing or expiring, the pledge is to claw his way above and reject whatever fate was drawn for him.","sonnet":"Sleepless, thousand-yard-staring persistence against time and exhaustion — each hit knocks the speaker down and they get back up, building walls, crossing lines, falling and flying simultaneously, driven by a refusal to stop that the Greek letters stamp as a permanent code name for the state.","src":"haiku"},
"rabbit-junk~reveal":{"opus":"A frozen, paralyzed witness forced to confront an ugly truth about desire and betrayal. The narrator wants to run or scream but his body locks in place as reality strips itself bare in front of him, caught between revulsion and a queasy sense of finally being made to see.","sonnet":"Frozen in a traumatic memory they cannot look away from, the narrator watches the hard, ugly truth strip away — like skin peeling — powerless to intervene or escape the moment, and desperate never to feel that way again.","src":"sonnet"},
"rabbit-junk~stone-cold-feat-amelia-arsenic":{"haiku":"Cold finality closes a chapter of deception and hurt, the speaker delivering a brutal dismissal to someone who tested their patience one time too many.","opus":"A bilingual kiss-off structured as a hostile negotiation: the ex is served a stone-cold ultimatum with no counteroffer, the German refrain hammering the same contempt in another tongue. What sounds like pure rage is really a demand to settle the emotional account and force the other to face what they took.","sonnet":"A bilingual scorched-earth breakup song — the narrator wants to settle the score with an ex who never knew when to stop, delivering the confrontation in both English and German ('fick dich' = 'fuck you'), with no counter-offer and no forgiveness, just a cold final proposal.","src":"haiku"},
"rabbit-junk~t-minus-everything":{"haiku":"In apocalyptic chaos without clear purpose or weapons, a speaker pushes forward against infernal forces while maintaining one step ahead of ruin, embracing violent resistance to division and self-righteous enemies.","opus":"The digital-hardcore project fuses breakbeats and abrasion into an apocalyptic sprint. Refusing to be victim as a ravenous predator closes in and the countdown hits zero, the narrator fights unarmed and unsure of his purpose through the end of the world. Survival is framed as pure forward momentum, always just barely outpacing damnation.","sonnet":"Survival without a map — a narrator stripped of mission presses forward through an apocalyptic landscape, staying one step ahead of destruction rather than fighting it. Countdown language and military imagery frame the drive as pre-launch urgency with no clear destination, only the instinct not to become prey.","src":"haiku"},
"rabbit-junk~the-grind":{"haiku":"The speaker is trapped in the exhausting repetition of daily work life, weeks bleeding together with no escape, while questioning the point of it all beneath manufactured obligations.","opus":"A raging portrait of the daily-job treadmill, the week bleeding past while the narrator numbs himself with nostalgia for better days and wishes each shift would just disappear. Rabbit Junk is JP Anderson's digital-hardcore project, and its punk-meets-glitch collision channels the burnout into a chant to escape the machine.","sonnet":"Five days of managed misery, a weekend of pretending to be young again, and then back to Monday — the loop that never breaks. The song describes the grind from the inside, where even the defiance feels hollow, the empty vessels of each day accumulating, and the only honest response is to spell out the word itself as a kind of grim chant.","src":"haiku"},
"radiohead~creep":{"haiku":"Self-loathing alienates the speaker from desired belonging; unworthiness condemns them to permanent exclusion.","opus":"The definitive anthem of unworthiness and obsessive longing, a self-loathing outsider fixated on someone unattainably perfect and convinced he doesn't belong anywhere near her or the beautiful world she inhabits.","sonnet":"Paralysing self-loathing in the presence of someone perceived as beautiful and otherworldly — the speaker wants perfection he cannot attain and knows he does not belong in her world, watching her leave while accepting his own inadequacy.","src":"haiku"},
"radiohead~electioneering":{"haiku":"A politician manipulates voters with the right words and promises during election time, treating it as business rather than service, and relies on coercion and false economics.","opus":"A sharp caricature of political cynicism, sketching a candidate who says whatever wins votes while power operates through force and cold economics, all meaningful dialogue an illusion. Radiohead dropped this burst of guitar aggression into OK Computer, its anti-globalization sneer a rare open polemic amid the album's otherwise chilly sense of alienation.","sonnet":"A political machine laid bare — the promises, the managed crowds, the IMF and cattle prods all part of the same transaction. The speaker is the politician, saying what needs to be said to secure the vote, fully aware that moving forward and moving backward are supposed to meet somewhere called compromise but never quite do.","src":"haiku"},
"radiohead~everything-in-its-right-place":{"haiku":"Sensory surrealism with waking to bitterness and confused internal conflict, cycling abstract imagery without narrative anchor.","sonnet":"Fragmented mental states pressed into a mantra of order. Waking to bitterness — a sourness that permeates the day — the speaker holds two conflicting thoughts that cannot fully surface into speech, yet insists with hypnotic repetition that everything belongs exactly where it is.","src":"haiku"},
"radiohead~jigsaw-falling-into-place":{"haiku":"Drunken flirtation and superficial connection give way to mutual desire and physical attraction before dissolution arrives.","opus":"A whirling night out where a connection seems to lock together amid drink, dancing and crowd-blur, everything dissolving as instinct takes over. Yorke confesses he never actually arrived and only faked it, his words landing dull and violent against the rush.","sonnet":"A high-resolution snapshot of two people at the tipping point of connection in a bar — drinks arriving, favorite song playing, walls bending with a Cheshire cat grin — before the moment tips into dance and a shared glance that contains everything still unsaid.","src":"haiku"},
"radiohead~just":{"haiku":"Self-inflicted suffering persists despite opportunities to escape, and the narrator refuses sympathy because victimhood is self-created.","opus":"A withering address to someone who is the author of his own misery, warned that the pain he causes himself is entirely self-inflicted, no one else to blame, hanging off high floors and changing locks against a threat that keeps walking back in. The refrain repeats the verdict like a hammer. Radiohead cut this on The Bends, their guitar-driven mid-nineties pivot, its famously enigmatic video ending on a man who won't say what he told the crowd.","sonnet":"Contempt worn as concern — a man hanging around who's become a fixture in someone's life despite everything, someone who changes locks and still can't keep him out. The core accusation repeats like a verdict: whatever's gone wrong, you did it to yourself, you alone, and that's exactly why it hurts so much. The speaker offers no sympathy, only the mirror.","src":"haiku"},
"radiohead~my-iron-lung":{"haiku":"A generation too young for cynicism yet too damaged to hope clings to survival while mocking the forces that sustain them.","opus":"Yorke's ambivalent howl at the very hit that trapped them, the crowd-pleasing song as a life-support machine keeping them alive while suffocating them; gratitude and contempt for the thing that both sustains and imprisons.","sonnet":"Faith and dependence — on a life support machine, on a commercial success now resented — are held up as the same suffocating mechanism, keeping the speaker breathing but not living. A cynical generation sits paralysed, sustained by the very thing choking them.","src":"haiku"},
"radiohead~no-surprises":{"opus":"Numb suburban despair and the wish to quietly opt out of a life that's slowly grinding you down, from the dead-end job to the accumulated disappointment. Radiohead deliver it as a gentle lullaby, the narrator fantasizing about a painless disappearance behind a tidy home and garden, wanting nothing more than an uneventful escape from it all.","sonnet":"A crushed man's quiet wish for exit — exhausted by meaningless work, emotional bruising, and a government that doesn't represent him, he dreams of a painless, numbing death, opting for carbon monoxide over alarms and surprises in a life that has become suffocatingly ordinary.","src":"sonnet"},
"radiohead~sulk":{"haiku":"Submission and passive aggression fracture into cycles of control; the speaker recognizes inherited patterns of emotional stagnation and refusal.","opus":"Written in the aftermath of the Hungerford massacre, Yorke's dread of an inheritance you can't outrun, becoming your father no matter how you fight it. Menace and desire tangle, something that eats the narrator alive from inside.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses someone locked in cycles of sulking and burning — passive-aggressive withdrawal followed by explosive moments — comparing them to a father who never changed, while the speaker themselves admits the feeling eats them alive and they escape into sleep and declared holidays.","src":"haiku"},
"radiohead~the-bends":{"haiku":"Social isolation and physical malaise suffocate a generation obsessed with authenticity yet unable to find genuine connection.","opus":"A disoriented ache for realness and connection in a numb, paranoid modern life, drip-fed and alienated, wishing for the sixties and for anything to happen; underneath the wit is a raw plea to actually live and belong.","sonnet":"A speaker in free emotional collapse — lying in bars on drip feeds, wishing for the sixties, wondering who his real friends are — is surrounded by a world that feels like a gunboat in a sea of fear. He wants desperately just to breathe and be human again, but the bends are already setting in.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~bombtrack":{"haiku":"The speaker advocates for revolutionary violence against systems of power and exploitation, rejecting compliance with corporate and governmental authority.","opus":"Rage Against the Machine opened their self-titled 1992 debut with this, Zack de la Rocha's agitprop welded to Tom Morello's riffs and their rap-metal fury arriving fully formed. It's a declaration of war on exploiters, property tycoons and the myth of conquest, the narrator vowing to set the oppressors alight and hand beatdowns to pretenders, protest fashioned into a threat.","sonnet":"Political fury routed through musical aggression — the speaker rejects Manifest Destiny, names landlords and suits as the enemy, and promises to burn what burns people. The track is self-aware about being a track, a militant mind turned into a song that earns its credibility by actually landing. The refrain is both threat and prophecy: the people who burned others will burn.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~born-as-ghosts":{"haiku":"Systemic oppression and poverty render children invisible and ghostlike, their suffering hidden behind walls while privilege remains insulated from their reality.","opus":"A furious dispatch from the gated inequality of the war on the poor, children raised invisible amid guns, alarms and razor wire; the call is for the erased and suffering to find their voice and speak themselves into being.","sonnet":"A protest against the gated-community indifference of the wealthy to the poverty surrounding them, told from the perspective of children born invisible to society — crammed into underresourced schools, surrounded by razor wire, their suffering hidden from comfortable screens.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~born-of-a-broken-man":{"haiku":"Psychological trauma and fragmentation persist despite genetic destiny, trapping a consciousness in endless nocturnal torment.","opus":"A son watching his father's mind splinter and dissolve, ideas battering the skull like moths at a lampshade, a man awake and famished and begging for the light to die. De la Rocha wrote it of his own father's breakdown, vowing to spring from that shattered man without shattering himself.","sonnet":"A portrait of a shattered psyche — thoughts like a thousand moths burning in a lampshade, forever awake and starving — whose origins are traced to institutional and spiritual betrayal, yet who is not himself broken, only born of one who was.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~calm-like-a-bomb":{"haiku":"Power systems and colonialism perpetuate identical violence across different names and places; the oppressed harbor revolution quietly until the quiet moment of uprising.","opus":"A blistering tour of global inequality, Zack de la Rocha channeling revolutionary heroes and exile as he wanders through slums and city ruins where the poor are buried under interchangeable names, the same banks, churches, and funerals repeating at every point on the map. Riot becomes the language of the ignored. Rage built The Battle of Los Angeles on exactly this fusion of militant rhetoric and detonating funk-metal.","sonnet":"Compressed fury at the same global machinery everywhere you look — banks, churches, debt, incarceration, dead fields and ditches — rendered in a voice that identifies with Zapata and Baldwin, with the landless and the condemned. The calm-like-a-bomb image holds the paradox: the rage is controlled, pressurised, building toward a detonation the chorus keeps threatening.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~fistful-of-steel":{"haiku":"A rapper weaponizes language against mass conformity and media silence, using aggression and defiance to challenge systems of control.","opus":"A manifesto for defiant speech against enforced silence and media-fed submission; Zack frames the microphone as a weapon and complacency as a slow suicide, insisting on the disruptive power of the question.","sonnet":"The microphone is seized as a weapon against silence, submission, and the mass media that pacifies through noise while carrying no truth. The narrator frames himself as a defiant, militantly aware outsider gripping language as his only real power.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~freedom":{"haiku":"Poetic rebellion confronts social conditioning and environmental catastrophe; the speaker paints visual narratives of resistance and reclamation.","opus":"Zack de la Rocha turns the mic into a weapon over the deaths of Black men, the tic-tac-toe of the title mapping onto bodies counted down to zero. The rallying cry frames rage itself as the gift that fuels resistance, aimed here at Leonard Peltier's cause.","sonnet":"A free-associating stream of political consciousness — painting pictures of racial erasure, systemic violence, and the distraction machinery of consumer culture — calls on Black men and the dispossessed to reclaim their names and their anger as a gift before the countdown reaches zero.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~guerilla-radio":{"haiku":"Rebellion broadcasts truth through pirate transmission; the speaker disrupts monopolized information and demands liberation.","opus":"A call to seize the airwaves against a corrupt spectacle of politics serving blood and oil rather than the toiling masses. Name-checking Mumia Abu-Jamal, it turns the radio dial into a weapon and insists the revolt has to start here and now.","sonnet":"A call to use underground broadcasting and collective noise to break through the corporate and government stranglehold on media and political life — the song specifically references political prisoner Mumia Abu-Jamal and calls for starting the resistance right now, here, without waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~guerrilla-radio":{"haiku":"Activists hijack media frequencies to spread resistance, demanding freedom and exposing the monopoly of power over information and elections.","opus":"Zack de la Rocha rages against the 2000 U.S. election, framing the choice between candidates as no real choice at all and the media as a rigged spectacle. Rage turn broadcasting into an act of resistance, invoking Mumia Abu-Jamal and pirated airwaves, the shouted refrain insisting revolt starts immediately.","sonnet":"A call to seize the airwaves and expose the machinery behind electoral spectacle and state violence. Using broadcast as weapon, the speaker targets the alliance of capital, police power, and media monopoly, demanding that the signal be turned up until the silence breaks.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~killing-in-the-name":{"haiku":"Police officers with racist ties use state power to enforce obedience while justifying violence against marginalized people; the speaker exposes systemic racism and oppression.","opus":"Rage Against the Machine's furious debut single ties American policing to Klan-style racism, arguing that a uniform grants killers immunity to protect white power. Its mounting mantra about blind obedience finally erupts into a profane, repeated refusal to comply, and the track became a generational shout against institutional authority.","sonnet":"A direct indictment of institutional racism within law enforcement, linking police authority to white supremacist ideology. The song builds toward a furious refusal of blind obedience, culminating in an outright rejection of every order handed down by a corrupt power structure.","src":"haiku","fable":"Built on almost no text, the song is a machine of escalation, and the meaning is in how the loops tighten. Zack de la Rocha's opening couplet collapses the badge into the hood, arguing that the same men sworn to protect enforce a racial order and dress murder up as duty, the killing done in the name of something that sounds righteous. From there the track drills a single line of coerced obedience over and over until the mantra hollows out and you feel the conditioning as sound, the added whisper that you are now under control naming the trap outright. The whole grinding structure exists to earn the outro, where the chant of obedience is answered by its exact inversion, refusal repeated until it becomes its own hypnosis, so the song enacts the break it demands rather than merely describing it.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"the refusal repeated until it becomes its own hypnosis","built":"the outro inverts the obedience mantra word for word enacting the break it demands"},
"rage-against-the-machine~mic-check":{"haiku":"Hip-hop resistance speaks truth to oppression, naming systemic murder and wage theft while reclaiming agency and prophetic voice.","opus":"A power analysis in rhyme, indicting the whole machinery of church, state and capital that hunts the wageless and caged. De la Rocha asks who really holds the power and answers by naming everyone who profits from the auction block reborn.","sonnet":"A politically charged mic check positions the rapper as an anti-myth truth-teller speaking for the powerless — the working poor, the incarcerated, the racially targeted — and poses the central question of who actually holds power in a torn nation, indicting police violence and economic exploitation.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~new-millennium-homes":{"haiku":"Privatization and systemic violence dismantle families; the speaker ignites resistance through force and moral clarity against corporate pillage.","opus":"Prisons and privatization framed as the old plantation economy rebuilt for a new century, with the desperate poor organizing against it. Zack de la Rocha invokes the spirit of labor uprisings and returns again to the image of torching a slaveowner's estate, casting revolt as the only sane response once survival has itself become war.","sonnet":"Dispossessed and hungry people pushed out by economic violence — factory closures, incarceration, rising markets — are described as reaching their limit, the song framing working-class rage as the inevitable fire that burns inside the master's house when there is nothing left to lose.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~people-of-the-sun":{"haiku":"Indigenous resistance reawakens across centuries of colonial violence; the speaker invokes ancestral spirit and reclaims sacred identity.","opus":"A five-century indictment of the conquest of the Americas, from 1516 through the Zapatista present, indigenous resistance reclaiming a stolen name. The vulture once stole it, but now the people have a gun, and history is turning back on the colonizers.","sonnet":"An address to the indigenous peoples of the Americas — Maya, Mexica — tracing the history of colonial violence, forced labour, ethnic cleansing, and erasure from 1516 to the present, declaring that their spirit is alive and untamed and the reckoning is coming back around.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~revolver":{"haiku":"Gender violence and economic disparity intersect as exploitation and powerlessness reduce women to objects consumed by predatory systems.","opus":"A portrait of domestic abuse and a trapped wife whose body numbs at her husband's approach, the taunting refrain questioning whether such a brutal man deserves the role of father at all.","sonnet":"A woman trapped in domestic abuse — her body numbing as he approaches the door, her hopes festering in a spotless domain while pictures of fields without fences suggest the freedom she cannot reach — while the refrain asks whether mothers make good fathers, indicting the structures that keep her there.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~sleep-now-in-the-fire":{"haiku":"Capitalist elites justify greed and violence through false piety, co-opting conquest and atrocity as divine right while demanding passive obedience from the masses.","opus":"De la Rocha voicing capitalism and empire in the first person, greed personified boasting through Columbus, the noose and Hiroshima. The horror is the confession: the machine owns your children and calls its own atrocities destiny.","sonnet":"The voice of American empire speaks in the first person — equating itself with conquest, slavery, colonialism, nuclear destruction, and endless exploitation — the song is a savage indictment of U.S. imperial power dressed as a confession.","src":"haiku"},
"rage-against-the-machine~voice-of-the-voiceless":{"haiku":"Political prisoner Mumia Abu-Jamal becomes martyr and symbol of repressed dissent; the speaker pledges solidarity against state violence and silencing.","opus":"A direct plea for Mumia Abu-Jamal, the imprisoned journalist and former Panther, cast as a spark the state fears because he refused to be silenced. The band pledges war until his freedom, framing his cell as one front in a much older fight.","sonnet":"A tribute to Mumia Abu-Jamal, a Black journalist and activist imprisoned on death row, framing his silencing as a continuation of centuries of racial injustice and warning that no institution — legal, political, or economic — can permanently suppress the voice of the oppressed.","src":"haiku"},
"rain-check~drown":{"haiku":"Monotony and depression where each day feels identical and worthless, the speaker contemplating drowning their pain rather than enduring existence.","opus":"Rain Check render depression as numb monotony, days ticking over while nothing inside actually shifts. The narrator keeps insisting he'll be okay even as speech feels futile and his awareness slips, either barely staying afloat or letting himself go under to deaden the ache. Flat statements of worthlessness and deserved misery loop like a skipping record, honest about the exhaustion of pretending it's all worthwhile.","sonnet":"Days cycle identically while worthlessness and breathlessness compound into paralysis — the choice framed starkly as keeping one's head above water or surrendering to numbness. The loop of sameness is both the disease and the shape the song itself takes.","src":"haiku"},
"rain-check~tired":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and depletion beyond recovery, the speaker too worn to continue.","opus":"A weary confession of chronic failure addressed to someone who takes pleasure in the collapse. The narrator keeps slipping and botching things, exhausted and lost, hovering near a breaking point with things he never managed to voice, resigned to simply await whatever ending comes. A portrait of burnout and quiet surrender.","sonnet":"Brief and blunt: someone takes pleasure in watching the speaker fail, and the speaker, past pretending, waits only for the end.","src":"haiku"},
"rainbow~stargazer":{"haiku":"Blind faith and collective obsession erect a monument to a false savior, resulting in mass death and spiritual awakening.","opus":"Dio's epic of an enslaved people forced to build a tower so a tyrant wizard can fly, his hubristic fall killing the dream; the payoff is liberation, the freed slave watching the illusion collapse and turning at last toward home.","sonnet":"Slaves are worked to death building a tower for a wizard who claims he can fly to the stars, driven by whips and chains and a collective dream of transcendence — until the wizard falls instead of rising and leaves only blood in the sand. The survivors walk home free but haunted, the faith that cost everything now gone.","src":"haiku"},
"rainbow~starstruck":{"haiku":"Obsessive pursuit and parasitic attraction stalk a subject relentlessly, with the pursuer inescapable and malevolent regardless of distance or effort to escape.","opus":"A wry rocker's-eye view of an obsessive fan who stalks him everywhere, mistaking a stage smile for real devotion; beneath the humor is unease at being hunted, the adoration curdling into inescapable bad luck.","sonnet":"A rock star is stalked by an obsessive fan whose adoration has curdled into a suffocating, predatory fixation — she is everywhere he goes, treating him as a trophy rather than a person.","src":"haiku"},
"raizer~explode":{"haiku":"The speaker loses control and explodes with rage born from despair, transformed from who they were into pure white-hot spite and resentment that cannot be stopped.","opus":"A dissociative breakdown building toward release. The narrator insists none of this is really him, unable to voice a scream, sliding past the last of his composure and feeling hollowed out on the brink of collapse, then vowing to erupt into a flash brighter than daylight that flips dark to bright. Torment tips into ecstatic rupture. Raizer are a Russian electronic-rock outfit whose synth-heavy, industrial-tinged sound is built for exactly this pent-up detonation on the chorus.","sonnet":"Dissociation cracking into uncontrollable rage — the speaker denies what's happening, tries to breathe through it, but loses the fight entirely. The explosion isn't violent outward destruction so much as a blinding white flash, a total loss of the former self at the edge of an abyss, flipping from torment to something that might be relief or might just be emptiness.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein-paris~wollt-ihr-das-bett-in-flammen-sehen":{"haiku":"Erotic violence pulses through sacred spaces as innocence masks guilt; the world craves blood and transgression masked as religious restraint.","opus":"Desire baited out of the audience by insisting they secretly crave violence, the bed set aflame, the dagger in the sheets. The blunt equation makes the point, sex is a battle and love is war.","sonnet":"Sex framed explicitly as war and war as sex: the audience is asked whether they want to see the bed in flames, to drown in skin and hair, to drive the dagger into the sheets — \"sex is a battle, love is war\" — with crosses on the pillow and the innocence claimed by those who have already chosen violence.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~01-mein-herz-brennt":{"haiku":"A demonic voice claiming maternal origin steals the innocence of children through violation and psychological terror, turning the maternal into threat and burning as proof of intrusion.","opus":"A menacing lullaby voiced by the presence in the pillow, promising children nightmares and stealing their small warm tears to press into cold veins. Til Lindemann repurposes a German children's television intro into a figure of nocturnal dread whose burning heart both threatens and suffers.","sonnet":"A voice from the pillow — half nightmare, half lullaby — announces it has ripped something from its own burning chest to give to children; demons crawl from the cellar to steal their tears and drain them into cold veins. The register mimics a children's story to deliver something viscerally threatening.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~02-links-2-3-4":{"haiku":"The heart's leftward political orientation defies social expectation and conformity, with emotional allegiance transgressing the prescribed path.","opus":"A defiant answer to accusations that the band's politics lie on the wrong side, insisting the heart beats on the left. The martial counting reclaims left-wing conviction against enviers who assumed otherwise.","sonnet":"A defiant answer to those who want the narrator's heart on the right: he looks down and it beats on the left, always has, always will. The marching count — links, zwo, drei, vier — turns political alignment into physical anatomy, the left-beating heart as an unchosen, unchallengeable fact.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~04-ich-will":{"haiku":"The speaker demands absolute attention, faith, and control over an audience, positioning collective recognition as sole antidote to existential insignificance.","opus":"The performer's naked hunger for attention laid bare, demanding to be seen, heard, felt, and believed, wanting to control every heartbeat and drown in applause. The crowd answers that they see and hear him, yet he still cannot understand them, exposing the gap at the center of fame.","sonnet":"A performer's hunger laid bare: the narrator demands trust, belief, eye contact, applause, fantasy, energy — wants to be seen, understood, felt — only to find the crowd responds in perfect lockstep while he still can't understand them. The shift from \"I want\" to \"we want\" mid-song reveals the feedback loop between performer and audience as mutually incomprehensible.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~05-fever-frei":{"haiku":"Fire and pain become transcendent pathways, inflicting and experiencing violence as gateway to nobility, ecstasy, and freedom.","opus":"Pain and desire fused into a battle cry, the person hardened by suffering turning fire on the world with a gunshot cadence. Your happiness is explicitly not mine but my misfortune, making cruelty the engine of release.","sonnet":"Fire and pain as initiation into nobility: the one who knows pain from fire is first scorned, then ennobled, then made dangerous — \"gefährlich ist, wer Schmerzen kennt.\" The \"Feuer frei!\" command to open fire punctuates each transformation, and the final couplet notes that your happiness is not my happiness but is in fact my misery.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~06-mutter":{"haiku":"Maternal betrayal and separation produce existential devastation, with the speaker mourning the loss of maternal presence.","opus":"A being created without a mother, no navel, no milk, no name, curses the mother who never bore it and vows revenge even unto self-destruction. A grotesque cry for the maternal love it was denied, begging that absent mother for strength.","sonnet":"A creature without a navel, suckled on nothing, unnamed and conceived without seed, swears revenge on the mother who never bore him: he will give her a disease and sink her in a river. The birthmark on his forehead, the eel in her lungs, the knife's kiss — each image turns maternal absence into grotesque mythology.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~08-zwitter":{"haiku":"Perfect hermaphroditic self-sufficiency dissolves the need for others; one body contains two genders, one lust, infinite self-fascination.","opus":"Rammstein takes a provocative concept to its logical extreme with a hermaphrodite narrator who is complete unto themselves. Two sexes and two souls in one body, they fall gloriously in love with themselves, sending themselves roses and needing no second or third. Beneath the shock lies a strange hymn to total self-sufficiency and immunity to rejection.","sonnet":"An intersex person moves through the world differently, carrying two souls and two genders under one chest, having stolen a kiss that was eagerly reclaimed. The Zwitter is content: one desire, two natures, and a peculiar daily ease.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~10-adios":{"haiku":"A drug user becomes a battlefield—music flows like infection through veins, consuming body and soul. The needle's song brings final silence.","opus":"A clinically vivid account of a heroin injection as a private war inside the veins, ending when the needle comes out and the eyes open but the man never wakes, dead with the promise that nothing was ever for him.","sonnet":"A song about a man who injects heroin and dies — he places the needle on his vein and lets the music in, eyes closed as a battle rages in his blood, and when the needle is withdrawn the music tears out through his skin, violins burning and harps cutting flesh; he opens his eyes but does not wake up, and nothing was ever his.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~adieu":{"haiku":"Farewell and departure carry finality, with the speaker severing connection.","opus":"A funeral hymn from the band to the listener, tender for once, admitting death is the one road walked alone while promising to be there anyway. No miracle offered, only that the time together was good.","sonnet":"A farewell sung at the deathbed: death is the only constant, the last road must be walked alone, no miracle will intervene — but those gathered promise to stay until the end and insist that the dying person will remain with them always. \"Die Zeit mit dir war schön\" (the time with you was beautiful) is the only eulogy that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~adios":{"haiku":"A user injects heroin; the drug travels through the body and mind causing internal warfare and transformation, ending in death despite apparent awakening.","opus":"A clinical depiction of a heroin overdose, the user putting the needle to the vein and inviting the music in as a melody, then a marching army, spreads through his blood and gut. The refrain 'nothing is, was, or will be for you, forever' and the final image of eyes open but never waking make it a cold, unsentimental death by the drug.","sonnet":"A story of a heroin overdose told as music — someone sets a needle to a vein the way you'd set a needle to a record, music enters the bloodstream, armies march through his intestines, and when the needle comes off the vein he hasn't woken up. He's dead, and the song's title is goodbye.","src":"haiku","fable":"A heroin overdose narrated entirely as a concert: the needle goes into the vein and the addict invites the music in, so that the melody travels quietly into the bone and the high becomes the song being played through the body. The two bridges are the structure and the whole story — the first sends the tune into the flesh, the second withdraws the needle and the same instruments turn murderous (violins burn, harps cut into the meat), and the tell is the last couplet, where he opens his eyes but does not wake. Between them the refrain hammers a triple erasure of tense — nothing is, was, or remains for you, forever — the German nichts landing as a verdict rather than a lament, which is what makes the cheerful bop-bop-shu-bop scat and the drill-sergeant Los! Los! Los! egging the injection on so obscene: the pop-song scaffolding is the mask, and the real farewell is the title word the song pointedly never says, spoken instead by silence after the eyes open on nothing. Classic late-Mutter Rammstein — the band's habit of dressing annihilation in bright, danceable form, letting the jaunty surface do the cruelty the lyric refuses to state outright.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief","artifice & performance"],"means":"overdose narrated entirely as a concert played through the body","built":"the title word the song never says is spoken only by silence after the eyes open"},
"rammstein~alter-mann":{"haiku":"An old man uses a fan to keep water smooth as a mirror to understand his life, dying while clinging to it, leaving behind a tool to help another see their remaining stories.","opus":"The German industrial-metal band spins a parable of an old man who smooths a lake's surface daily with a fan, guarding a secret the water and a child share. Before dying he warns that only a still surface reveals how much fairy-tale time remains, and that one will beg for deliverance. His hand must be broken to free the fan, and the boy inherits its unreadable message.","sonnet":"A young person watches an old man smooth the water with a fan each day, receiving a dying message: the water will be a mirror only when it is still, and in that stillness the remaining life — and the need for redemption — becomes legible. After the old man dies, the fan and its lesson are all that remain.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~alter-mann-special-version":{"haiku":"A cryptic elder's final wisdom encodes mortality within mirror-like water, teaching through riddle that self-knowledge arrives only when confronting the finite reflection of one's remaining narratives.","opus":"A parable of an old man endlessly smoothing the water with his fan, guarding a secret the boy only understands too late, that the still water is a mirror showing how little of your story remains. After the old man dies clutching the fan, the boy inherits the same lonely vigil and longs for the release he cannot get.","sonnet":"A dying old man smooths the water's surface with a fan each day, undoing every stone the narrator throws in; before dying he whispers that water must be a mirror, and only when it's calm will you see how much fairy tale time you have left and begin to beg for redemption. The narrator inherits the fan and the riddle.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~amerika":{"haiku":"Critique of American cultural dominance spreading globally, controlling minds, and replacing authentic local culture. The song sarcastically frames this colonization as inevitable and inescapable.","opus":"Rammstein's ironic anthem on American cultural imperialism, the whole world dancing to a beat it didn't choose while Mickey Mouse and Coca-Cola stand in for soft empire. The insistence that this is no love song makes the critique plain beneath the singalong.","sonnet":"A satirical critique of American cultural dominance spread across the globe, using images of Mickey Mouse outside Paris and Coca-Cola replacing local identity. The song frames this as a forced dance in which everyone must follow America's lead whether they choose to or not.","src":"haiku","fable":"The joke is structural before it is verbal: an anthem to Americanization sung mostly in German, so the recurring English boast lands like an occupying tongue that everyone is conscripted to chant. Lindemann plays a dance-master demanding the lead and warning that whoever hangs back will eventually be made to move, which recasts cultural export as choreography drilled under the banner of freedom. The picture is a checklist of soft-power reach: a cartoon mouse posted near Paris, a Christmas gift-bringer flown into Africa, two consumer brands finally rhyming the wonderful into pure merchandise. The bridge drops the disguise, denying any affection and confessing that even the singer performs in a borrowed idiom rather than his native one. The closing chorus lets the seam split, wunderbar rotting into an admission of periodic violence, so the song reads less as protest than as diagnosis of a monoculture both irresistible and quietly armed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"cultural export reframed as choreography drilled under the banner of freedom","built":"the anthem to Americanization sung mostly in German, the recurring English boast landing like an occupying tongue everyone is conscripted to chant"},
"rammstein~amour": {"haiku":"Love operates as a wild, predatory force that bites, tears, and consumes the body and heart. It cycles between heat and cold, ultimately bringing pain.","opus":"An extended metaphor casting love as a wild beast that hunts, bites, and devours you; it nests on broken hearts, holds you with a thousand arms, drags you into its love-nest, chews you up over years and spits you out—first hot, then cold, and in the end it hurts—yet the narrator still begs it for its poison.","sonnet":"Love is a wild beast that hunts you, bites, devours you whole and vomits you back out — it's not tamed no matter how many try, ends in heat then cold then pain, and by the final line the speaker is begging for poison as release.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein extends the standard love-is-a-beast metaphor into full predation, and the song's argument is that the platitude was literally true all along. The verses systematically invert seduction into being eaten — the animal hunts by candlelight, burrows passages through the ribs, then swallows the speaker whole and, after a day and a year, disgorges him again — so that intimacy and digestion become the same process. The refrain seals the trap grammatically: everyone wants to tame Amour, yet the closing image is of being caught between its teeth, the tamer already inside the mouth. What makes it land is the turn from second to first person: the early verses describe love's methods generically, then the second verse puts the speaker himself in the jaws, and the outro abandons observation altogether for a beggar's chant asking, four times over, to be given poison — the prey now actively courting its own killing, which reframes the whole beast-fable as a study in the compulsion to keep loving the thing that consumes you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder"],"means":"surrender to love and to destruction named as one appetite","built":"the speaker stops describing and begs for the poison that will destroy him"},
"rammstein~angst":{"haiku":"Learned fear perpetuates across generations through control, mistrust, and systematic dread.","opus":"Rammstein turns the German boogeyman, the black man parents scare children with, into a metaphor for a whole society governed by manufactured fear. The frightened populace arms itself and shouts fire in the streets, and the point is who profits from keeping everyone afraid.","sonnet":"About how childhood fear of the boogeyman (\"der schwarze Mann\") becomes societal paranoia in adulthood — the fear instilled by parents to enforce obedience mutates into xenophobia and mob mentality, with people screaming danger in the streets and sleeping with their doors locked against a threat they were taught to imagine.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~armee-der-tristen":{"haiku":"An army of the sad and downtrodden rises collectively, channeling despair into unified force.","opus":"A darkly satirical recruitment march for the hopeless, an army of the depressed goose-stepping against happiness itself. It mocks the comfort of collective misery even as it invites you to join the ranks.","sonnet":"A satirical recruitment drive for the Army of the Joyless: join ranks with the depressed and marching in lockstep against happiness, hand in hand, never alone in their collective sorrow. The Party of the Hopeless is founded on withered roses and open to everyone — \"jeder darf es sein\" — and the march formation makes communal despair feel almost triumphant.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~asche-zu-asche": {"haiku":"Betrayal and vengeance wrapped in religious imagery; a martyr-figure crucified promises to resurrect as a vengeful shadow, turning the tables on their tormentor. Fire purifies the soul, ashes symbolize both death and the promise to return.","opus":"A crucifixion and burning answered with a vow of vengeful resurrection, the condemned promising to return as a shadow and make the executioner beg. Rammstein turn ashes-to-ashes into a threat rather than a benediction.","sonnet":"A German-language twist on crucifixion as revenge fantasy: the narrator is nailed to a cross and burned to ash, but promises to secretly resurrect and return to haunt the one who condemned them, kneeling on their face in a grotesque act of domination.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages a crucifixion in a series of clipped nominal jolts — warm body, hot cross, false verdict, cold grave — that Rammstein delivers less as narrative than as sentence being read out, the fire promising to wash the soul clean but leaving only a mouthful of ash. What lifts it past mere provocation is the second-strophe turn from victim to threat: the executed voice vows to return in ten days as a shadow to hunt its killer, so the Book of Common Prayer's funeral formula (ashes to ashes, dust to dust) is hijacked from consolation into a resurrection-curse, the liturgy of burial repurposed as a promise of vengeance. The band's signature dark comedy detonates on the withheld rhyme — the line kneels into your face and jams a finger into the... and cuts to the pounding chant of Asche, letting you supply the obscene word yourself so the refrain does double duty as both the sacred formula and the crude one it refuses to say. By the outro the phrase has ground down to nothing but zu Staub and a whispered Ich komm wieder, the endlessly repeated dust no longer a burial rite but the flat, gleeful insistence that what you reduced to ash is already on its way back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","death & grief"],"means":"resurrection inverted so the sacred formula becomes a threat of return","built":"ashes-to-ashes flipped from consolation to the executed innocent coming back"},
"rammstein~ausl-nder": {"haiku":"A man travels constantly between women across cultures, unable to commit, seducing through language and charm.","opus":"Rammstein skewering the traveling lothario who mistakes a handful of foreign phrases for worldliness, seducing across borders and vanishing by morning. The band mocks German sex-tourist swagger as much as it performs it.","sonnet":"The narrator is a foreign traveller who has made a point of learning multiple languages — not out of cultural appreciation but as a seduction tool, bedding women across countries for one or two hours each before vanishing, cheerfully self-aware about it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The seducer's cosmopolitanism is the joke and the indictment at once: he sells himself as a citizen of the world whose only true native tongue is flirtation, cycling through mi amor, mon chéri, and Russian endearments as if fluency in endearment were the same as fluency in people. Each pre-refrain quietly undoes the boast — he stays an hour or two and is gone before sunrise, so the traveler who is at home everywhere is really committed nowhere, and the loving Ausländer is just a man who never has to stay. Rammstein lets him strut through the verses in impeccable, showy German precisely so the outro can pull the mask off: the pidgin you-come-with-me-I-make-you-good collapses his whole polyglot charm into the leering, broken grammar of a sex tourist, and the pointed feminine Ausländerinnen makes clear who is being collected. What looks like a celebration of borderless romance is the band skewering it — the wordly lover and the predatory ugly foreigner turn out to be the same man.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","exile & diaspora","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"cosmopolitan pride collapsing into the ugly figure the word is usually aimed at","built":"the smooth linguist's mask breaks in the final ungrammatical pidgin come-on"},
"rammstein~b": {"haiku":"Internal conflict between two selves vying for control; a psychological battle where only one can survive and dominate.","opus":"A whispered, menacing scene of a night alone where voices behind the face command don't, leave it, don't touch it, just say no, while an invented word, Buckstabu, becomes the thing the narrator takes anyway. Two souls in one lap, only one to survive, the first time painless, no second time. Rammstein, the German industrial-metal band led by Till Lindemann, cloak an intimation of temptation and irreversible loss in nursery menace, the nonsense mantra doing the sinister work.","sonnet":"A menacing lullaby from a predator to a child — the voices behind the face warn the child to refuse, but the speaker reassures and closes in. The two souls competing in the same body, the claim that the first time will not hurt, and the certainty there will be no second time form a portrait of abuse in miniature.","src":"haiku","fable":"Bückstabü is a deliberately meaningless coinage — Rammstein have said it means whatever the listener wants — and the song weaponizes that void. Over a lullaby-tender seduction (soon it is night, we are alone, do not be afraid, I am right here beside you), a second track of voices whispers the child's prohibitions from behind the face — do not, leave it, do not touch, just say no. The refrain answers each one with I'll go get my Bückstabü, an unnamable compulsion overriding every warning. The bridge drops the mask: two souls in my lap and only one can survive, the first time it doesn't hurt, there won't be a second. The nonsense word finally reveals itself as an act done once and never undone, the reassuring lover and the whispering conscience two voices in one predator.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","shame & repression","love & desire"],"means":"a reassuring voice and a whispering conscience revealed as two tracks in one predator","built":"the nonsense coinage holds an unnameable act in place of a word that could be said"},
"rammstein~b-ck-dich": {"haiku":"A dominating figure commands submission and humiliation through physical domination and psychological degradation.","opus":"A brutal domination fantasy in which the narrator commands a submissive to bend over, treating them like a two-legged animal walked on all fours. As the humiliated victim weeps and stammers a prayer out of fear, the sadism grows, the abuser indifferent to their face and dissatisfied regardless.","sonnet":"A command for anal sex — the narrator orders submission, tells the partner to turn their face away because it's irrelevant, and describes the act explicitly, breaking down in tears mid-performance while the partner tries to go deeper.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein stage the master's voice as an anti-erotic liturgy: the command to bend over is repeated until it hollows out into pure ritual, and the refrain's four bare barks matter precisely because they say nothing but the order itself. The dominant's obsessive refusal of the other's face — turn your countenance from me, your face means nothing to me, the face does not interest me — is the song's real cruelty, reducing the two-legged creature to an animal led on all fours down a corridor while the master keeps announcing that he is disappointed. What curdles the sadism is the second verse's reversal: the one giving orders is the one who begins to weep, teaching by demonstration, so the tears run improbably upward along the victim's back and the whole hierarchy floats free of who suffers. Sung by a band whose industrial pomp always flirts with the aesthetics of command, it reads less as pornography than as a study of domination as compulsion — power that must keep barking because it can no longer feel anything, least of all a face.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"control as an addiction that degrades the one wielding it, never sated","built":"the master weeps even while inflicting the abuse, cruelty indistinguishable from breakdown"},
"rammstein~benzin": {"haiku":"Gasoline as metaphor for life energy and violent desire; fuel and explosive potential running through veins.","opus":"A German declaration of craving gasoline above all drugs, friends, or medicine, wanting explosive fuel that runs through the veins; it ends with the idea that to be rid of something forever, you burn it and drown it in petrol.","sonnet":"The speaker declares that gasoline is the only thing they need — not drugs, love, friends, or medicine — treating fuel as an all-consuming obsession that flows through veins and dreams, and insisting that anything to be left behind must be burned.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein's usual trick is to take a private compulsion and blast it to arena scale, and here the object of desire has been swapped: the singer opens by refusing every substance an addict might crave — no heroin, no alcohol, no nicotine, no cocaine, no doctor, no woman except as lubricant — only to reveal that his craving has simply migrated to gasoline, an even more combustible fixation, so the litany of negations is really an addiction song wearing a detox mask. The genius is literal-mindedness pushed to industrial fetish: heart and kidneys become engines, fuel runs in the veins and out the ears, the human body reconceived as a machine that runs on petrol rather than blood. The final movement turns the fuel outward and menacing — if you want to be rid of something, burn it; if you never want to see it again, let it drown in gasoline — so the fixation curdles from self-fueling mania into a recipe for erasure, the accelerant of both engine and arson. Underneath the deadpan brand-name chant lies the band's recurring theme: desire that can only express itself as detonation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder"],"means":"hymn to self-sufficiency whose fuel becomes an erasure tool","built":"the inventory of refusals masks the one total craving underneath"},
"rammstein~bestrafe-mich": {"haiku":"The speaker surrenders to domination by a greater power, accepting punishment as proof of being chosen and loved.","opus":"A song of masochistic submission cast in religious terms; the narrator begs to be punished, saying the other's greatness makes him small, and frames the dominant as both his punisher and his God who takes and gives but only to those he loves.","sonnet":"A plea for punishment that blurs religious submission and masochistic desire — the speaker asks God or a dominant figure to be their punisher, finding in that subjugation something like belonging and grace.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein turns the Lutheran God of their childhood into a dominant, collapsing worship and submission into a single grammar: to be punished is to be chosen. The song frames obedience as fairy-tale alchemy that runs backward — straw becomes gold, then gold hardens to stone — so that surrender promises transfiguration but delivers only diminishment (your greatness makes me small). Its engine is the pulpit refrain the Lord takes, the Lord gives, which the outro finally completes with the sting he gives only to the one he also loves — the theological alibi that recasts cruelty as proof of favor and lets the speaker beg for the rod. The chilling pivot is the one-word shift late on, where your greatness makes him small: the penitent has become an officiant, offering someone else up to the punisher he first craved for himself. What looks like a fetish lyric is really an anatomy of faith's darkest transaction — the devotion that can only feel loved when it is being hurt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"punishment sought as proof of being chosen, abasement as a bid for love","built":"scriptural address to the beloved reframes submission as a devotional act"},
"rammstein~buck-dich-album-version":{"haiku":"Humiliation and surrender degrade a subject into obedience, forced to avert their gaze while submission and violation tear through them, the aggressor disappointed by the compliance they demand.","opus":"A brutal scene of domination and humiliation, deliberately provocative Rammstein theater about power reducing a person to a crawling animal. The cruelty and the performer's tears both stage the ugliness of control.","sonnet":"A BDSM power scene narrated from the dominant position, structured as pure command — \"bück dich\" (bend down), face averted, identity irrelevant — and the speaker's emotional state turns perversely: they weep not from tenderness but from disappointment, the submissive's compliance somehow still failing to satisfy. The tears running up the other's back invert all expected emotional logic.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~dalai-lama":{"haiku":"A plane carrying a man and child encounters a violent storm; the child hears voices from the clouds calling it away as terror takes hold, ultimately dying in the father's grip.","opus":"A retelling of Goethe's Erlkonig set on a doomed flight: a father clutches his terrified child through a storm and, in his own panic, smothers the boy he means to save. Rammstein's grim parable about fear of the sky and love that kills through fear.","sonnet":"A song about a plane carrying a father and child caught in a violent storm — the child hears voices in the wind calling him to join them, a chorus from the clouds promising brotherhood. The father, panicking, clutches the child so hard he accidentally suffocates him; the child's soul then rises on the wind and sings the chorus back.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is Goethe's Erlkönig rebuilt at cruising altitude: Lindemann keeps the exact machine of the ballad — a child hearing a supernatural voice the parent cannot, a father who tightens his grip as the danger closes — and swaps the fevered midnight ride for a passenger plane failing in a storm. The wind is no longer wind; it is a king with sons, and a choir that drips out of the clouds and crawls into the small ear promising brotherhood and safety, the Erlkönig's seduction relocated to a cabin losing pressure. The ironic title does the framing work: it winks at the Dalai Lama's reputed fear of flying, and the song's flat verdict that man does not belong in the air turns that fear into cosmology — the sky is a jealous god reclaiming what strayed into his element. The whole reading turns on the bridge, where the horror stops being the storm: the father presses the child so hard against himself that he squeezes the soul out (Atemnot, suffocation) — his love, not the crash, is the fatal thing, and terror is named as the agency that shows no mercy. So the child's abduction comes true through the arms meant to prevent it, and the calm outro is the pulled-free soul singing back the choir's own words — the seduction was never a lie, it was a prophecy the father completed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"Its neighbor on Reise, Reise runs the same vertical: Mein Teil’s scream cuts through angel hosts as feather-flesh falls from the cloud-roof — both songs stage heaven as an appetite.","themes":["family & blood","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"protective love completing the abduction it was meant to prevent","built":"the father's suffocating grip, not the storm, is where the Erlkönig's seduction lands"},
"rammstein~das-alte-leid": {"haiku":"An eternal cycle of suffering and fate repeats, with the narrator resigned to never escaping this ancient sorrow despite burning desire for release and sex (ficken).","opus":"An early Rammstein howl at the endless repetition of the same suffering, the same old sorrow driving the narrator toward madness, broken only by the blunt animal demand never to endure it again. Torment answered with the crudest possible escape.","sonnet":"A German song where the narrator is driven slowly mad by an unnamed recurring burden — 'the same thing and the old suffering' — watching a young body rot on a mat where fate pulls the strings, until the accumulated pain finally resolves into the raw declaration that all he wants is to fuck, no more of the old grief.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song builds a closed loop that jams: the intro's cheery telephone greeting, the birth-image of a creature forced from the bean into the light, and the fixed refrain-phrase of the old sorrow all return unchanged verse to verse, so that fate pulling its puppets and a young body rotting on the mat feel less like events than like a wheel that cannot stop turning. Rammstein's debut trades in this exact mechanism — the same thing, the same old grief, driving the speaker slowly into madness as the war on the mat rages on and sears his heart. The genius of the track is that its one attempt at escape is deliberately obscene and inadequate: the crude sexual outburst the speaker spits out is offered as his only lever against the eternal return, and it fails on contact, because the chorus that follows chants never again the old sorrow so many times that the vow curdles into the very repetition it swore to break. The refusal to change is the song, and the promise of never is exposed as one more turn of the wheel.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind","love & desire"],"means":"the vow to be done with old sorrow locked into the grief it swears off","built":"the refusal loops endlessly against the same phrase, making it the sentence"},
"rammstein~das-modell":{"haiku":"Commodification of beauty and femininity; a model's calculated performance of perfection for consumption and financial gain.","opus":"A cool, transactional portrait of a fashion model who drinks Sekt in nightclubs, sizes up every man, and dazzles millions of eyes as a consumer product whose beauty is bought and paid for, the narrator determined to take her home and see her again. Rammstein's cover of the Kraftwerk classic keeps the deadpan German detachment but swaps synth-pop for industrial-metal weight.","sonnet":"A cool, admiring portrait of a model who moves through nightclubs and camera flashes with total control — she checks out every man in the room before any of them can get close, and the speaker wants to take her home while freely admitting that the economy of her beauty is transactional. Beauty is a currency and she knows exactly how to spend it.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~der-meister":{"haiku":"A master sends messengers to herald doomsday and ruin with truth spoken as devastating wind and breaking force, offering no mercy or angelic salvation.","opus":"An apocalyptic proclamation in which messengers sent by a master announce doomsday, warning that truth arrives like a storm to destroy, no mercy will be given, and the listeners' final days have come, telling them to run for their lives.","sonnet":"Sent by a master, a messenger proclaims the coming end: the rider of malice feeds on envy, truth arrives to destroy like a storm, and the last days have come — the only advice is to run for your life.","src":"haiku","fable":"On Herzeleid, Rammstein's 1995 debut, this is apocalypse staged as sermon: the band cast themselves as heralds dispatched by a master to proclaim the last day, a Judgment with no rescuing angel and no mercy, only the order to run for your life. The song's real move is in the refrain, which keeps redefining truth — first a thunderstorm, then a choir of wind — as something that arrives not to console but to destroy, snapping you like a matchstick. Truth here is stripped of every comforting association and handed over entirely to the executioner's function; the terror is precisely that the thing coming for you is not a lie but the truth itself.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"truth stripped of consolation and handed to the executioner","built":"the refrain redefines truth twice — thunderstorm, then choir of wind"},
"rammstein~deutschland":{"haiku":"Germany embodies a paradox of love and curse, young yet ancient, beautiful yet destructive. The song expresses complicated attachment to a nation defined by contradictions and historical trauma.","opus":"An intensely ambivalent address to Germany itself, the speaker torn between loving and damning his homeland, evoking its cold history, division, arrogance, and the idea that what rises high falls deep.","sonnet":"The song addresses Germany itself as a lover that the narrator cannot fully love or give himself to — the relationship defined by fire and cold, by pride and shame, by centuries of history that make Germany simultaneously a curse and a blessing, something to damn and to adore.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein address the nation as a lover they cannot stop needing and cannot forgive, and the song's whole argument is that those two feelings are the same feeling: a heart in flames that wants to love you and damn you in the same breath. The pronoun ladder that opens and haunts every section — Du, Ich, Wir, Ihr — enacts a citizen trying to locate himself inside a collective he can't cleanly join or leave, the you and the we sliding past each other. Then the second verse detonates into a stutter of the über- prefix — overbearing, superior, taking over, surrendering, surprising, invading — a rhythmic slide from arrogance toward atrocity that lands on the loaded phrase Deutschland über allen, deliberately swapping the disavowed first stanza's alles for allen so the boast becomes over everyone, a supremacy over people rather than a place above all else. The band refuses catharsis on both ends: your love is curse and blessing, and my love I cannot give you — the confession that the tie is unbreakable and unbearable at once, which is exactly the historical weight (staged across the video's centuries of German horror) they won't let the listener resolve. The bridge's whoever climbs high shall fall deep is the closest thing to a verdict, and it is aimed at the very grandeur the chorus keeps helplessly reaching for.","fv":2,"fnote":"Days before release, the teaser — band members as concentration-camp prisoners, nooses around their necks — drew condemnation from Germany’s antisemitism commissioner and Jewish community leaders; the finished video stages two millennia of German violence around the song.","themes":["politics & society","love & desire","identity & becoming"],"means":"loving and damning the homeland revealed as the same unbreakable feeling","built":"the pronoun ladder — Du, Ich, Wir, Ihr — enacts a citizen failing to locate himself in a collective"},
"rammstein~diamant":{"haiku":"A person radiates beauty but is ultimately heartless; admiration turns to revulsion, love to hate.","opus":"Rammstein strips away their pyrotechnic bombast for a spare, aching ode to a beauty likened to a diamond, clear and pure, whose sparkle threatens to draw the soul out of the narrator. He cannot look away, wanted her in his heart, but pleads to be let go. What cannot love, he reasons, must hate, and the dazzling jewel is revealed, finally, as only a stone.","sonnet":"A person is transfixed by someone whose beauty is diamond-cold and absolute — a gaze that pulls the soul out, a light that was once everything. The plea is for release: what cannot be loved must eventually be hated, and the diamond, for all its brilliance, is in the end just a stone.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~dicke-titten":{"haiku":"A chronically lonely man expresses indifference toward conventional beauty or intelligence, reduced to base physical desires as his only remaining hope for companionship.","opus":"A lonely man's crude, comic lament that after years alone he no longer needs beauty, brains, or wealth in a partner, only enormous breasts. The satire aims squarely at the shrinking, self-lowering standards of desperate loneliness.","sonnet":"A self-aware comedy of low expectations: a lonely, graying man lists everything he doesn't require in a partner — beauty, intelligence, wealth, a model's stride — before arriving at the one non-negotiable. The joke turns slightly as his claimed humility evaporates into pure fixation, \"sie muss nur Riesentitten haben.\"","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~donaukinder":{"haiku":"Children vanish into a poisoned Danube river; the water turns rusty red, animals sicken and die, and mothers grieve at the banks as the city falls into plague and abandonment.","opus":"Rammstein turn their industrial weight toward eco-catastrophe, evoking a poisoned Danube where fish suffocate, swans die and rust-red floods carry unnamed contamination downstream. The haunting question of where the children have gone, met only with insistence that no one saw anything, indicts a community's collective silence. The German band's habitual darkness here serves genuine ecological mourning rather than shock.","sonnet":"An ecological and communal horror unfolding along the Danube — poisoned water, dead wildlife, a stench rising from the meadows, and children who have simply vanished. The community denies all knowledge, mothers weep floods, and the question of where the children went goes unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~du-hast":{"haiku":"A wedding vow exchange where the groom repeatedly says 'no' to promises of eternal fidelity; ambiguity about his refusal to commit.","opus":"Built on a pun between having and hating, it stages a wedding vow refused, the man declining eternal fidelity even as it's demanded. Beneath the industrial stomp, a study of commitment as trap and the flat power of saying no.","sonnet":"A play on the German homophone \"du hasst\" (you hate me) versus \"du hast mich gefragt\" (you have asked me) — the song stages a wedding vow question about eternal fidelity and answers it with \"Nein.\" It's a refusal of commitment disguised as a grammar trick, and at its core a song about refusing to be bound to someone by obligation or hate.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is built on a mishearing that never resolves. The opening plays on how du hast (you have) and du hasst (you hate) sound identical spoken aloud, so the pounded refrain hangs between accusation and simple fact until gefragt lands and fixes it: you asked me. What you asked is the marriage vow, recited in full liturgical German, and the answer thrown back into the priest's face is a flat, doubled refusal. The final verse deliberately garbles the sacred formula so that fidelity until death curdles into something anatomical and cruel, the ceremony rotting from inside its own words.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","comedy & wordplay","faith & the occult"],"means":"flat refusal hurled back into the liturgy that demanded it","built":"the du hast / du hasst homophone holds accusation and fact unresolved until gefragt lands"},
"rammstein~du-hast-single-version":{"haiku":"A marriage vow is rejected with refusal, the speaker denying loyalty through sickness and health, shattering the covenant with emphatic negation.","opus":"The wedding vow turned into a refusal, a play on you have and you hate, staged as a man rejecting till death do us part. Commitment interrogated and denied, aggression standing in for the answer no.","sonnet":"Built on the German pun \"du hast mich\" (you have me / you hate me), the song stages a wedding vow and refuses it — the repeated \"nein\" to fidelity until death is not rejection of the person but rejection of the institution. The lyric withholds the answer until the question is asked twice, letting the ambiguity of possession versus hatred do all the work.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~du-riechst-so-gut": {"haiku":"A predatory hunter stalks and pursues their prey obsessively, driven by primal desire and a sense of ownership.","opus":"Rammstein staging desire as predation, a hunter tracking prey by scent across a burning bridge. Madness sits between reason and instinct, and the seduction curdles into something violent, want indistinguishable from the drive to consume.","sonnet":"A German stalker narrative told from the predator's perspective — he tracks a woman by scent alone like an animal, follows her through darkness, waits until night to grab her wet skin, and tells her not to scream lest the bridge between them break.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein's debut single builds its horror by literalizing a metaphor: it takes the German idiom of following one's nose, of desire as scent, and collapses the human hunter into an actual animal. The song opens as a philosophical epigram — madness is a narrow bridge whose banks are reason and instinct — and then walks the speaker across it, degrading sense by sense: he no longer sees his quarry, only smells and feels her, first as a blind child crawling toward its mother's scent, then as a predator screaming with hunger that can catch her scent for miles. That downshift from child to beast is the whole argument, and it converts the love-song refrain (you smell so good, I'll come after you) into a stalking chant whose sweetness is the mask. The second verse drops the last euphemism — he waits for dark, touches the wet skin, tells her to stop screaming and not to struggle or the bridge will collapse — so the burning bridge is at once the crossing into madness and the point past which reason cannot return, the assault framed as a threshold that, once crossed, cannot be uncrossed. Read straight it is a rape; read as parable it is Rammstein's founding thesis, that Trieb always waits one narrow span from Vernunft and needs only a scent to send a person across.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","madness & the mind"],"means":"predator's need reframed as a demand for the prey's silence","built":"instinct and reason named as the two banks of a bridge the speaker chooses to cross"},
"rammstein~du-riechst-so-gut-98":{"haiku":"Primal pursuit and hunting instinct; a predator stalking prey through scent, stripped of reason and driven by base compulsion.","opus":"One of the band's early signatures, this renders desire as predatory hunt, madness a narrow bridge between reason and instinct. The narrator tracks a scent like a starving beast smelling prey for miles, following fresh traces of sweat and blood across a burning bridge. The insistence that she smells so good curdles into menace as he closes in, telling her not to scream or struggle.","sonnet":"A predator tracks a person through scent alone — instinct overriding reason on the thin bridge between sanity and drive. The pursuit is framed as animal compulsion, the hunter invisible but certain, closing in as darkness falls and the bridge itself begins to burn with the heat of what is about to happen.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~eifersucht":{"haiku":"Jealousy boils as someone imagines destroying themselves to match or exceed another's qualities, including possessing a rival's partner.","opus":"Rammstein, the German industrial-metal provocateurs, channel jealousy into grotesque self-cannibalizing logic. The narrator invites disfigurement, a broken neck, a devoured brain and heart, offering himself to be eaten whole whenever a rival proves fairer, stronger, cleverer or richer, or takes his woman. The boiling refrain of Eifersucht escalates the imagery until skin is peeled in strips and the plate licked clean, envy rendered as literal consumption.","sonnet":"Jealousy personified as a devouring force: a speaker invites rivals to mutilate and consume him if they envy his beauty, strength, intelligence, or his woman — each virtue becoming grounds for annihilation. The fantasy of being eaten alive literalizes the violence jealousy already does internally.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~ein-lied": {"haiku":"Musicians present themselves as servants who exist to comfort the sorrowful with songs, offering music as salvation when the world becomes too much. They arrive with their songbook to heal those who suffer.","opus":"Rammstein casting themselves as servants of your ears who play for the sad, a mock-pious lullaby offering music as reward for good behavior. The band's dark theatricality softened into something almost sweet, edge intact.","sonnet":"In German: a gentle, sincere dedication — the band presents itself as servants of your ears, born for music, promising to play for you whenever you're sad, whenever you can't sleep, when heaven cracks open. It's unusually tender for Rammstein, an earnest love letter to the audience.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is sung by musicians who serve as angels of the ear, promising to play a lullaby-hymn for the good — but every gift is booby-trapped with a condition, and reading those conditions is the whole song. Grace goes only to those who do good, live without sin, and dutifully hold hands, and one demand gives the game away: the reward comes to those who do not squint toward the sun — who keep their heads down, ambition and aspiration forbidden. What poses as a tender promise to comfort the sad is a catechism of obedience, the servant's voice quietly turning master, and the closing image seals the irony: heaven itself cracks open and a song falls soft from the celestial light, a benediction that only descends once you have been made small enough to receive it. As the closer of Rosenrot, it is Rammstein at their most deceptively gentle, weaponizing the lullaby's own sweetness so that the melody is the mask and the fine print is the threat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"comfort offered only to those who stay meek and never look up","built":"lullaby curdles into catechism, the gift of song dangled as reward"},
"rammstein~engel":{"haiku":"Those who live righteously become angels but angels are isolated behind clouds and must grip stars to avoid falling; the speaker refuses this lonely transcendence.","opus":"A bleak inversion of the comforting belief that the good become angels after death; the narrator insists that angels live isolated behind the sunshine, clawing at stars so as not to fall, afraid and alone, concluding that God knows he wants no part of being an angel.","sonnet":"A dark irony about angels: the idea that the virtuous become angels after death sounds comforting, but the angels themselves cling to stars in fear and loneliness, and the speaker wants no part of that heavenly isolation.","src":"haiku","fable":"Engel opens with the flat cadence of a children's catechism - live well on earth and you become an angel after death - then spends the rest of the song dismantling the comfort that formula promises. Rammstein reimagine heaven not as reward but as exile: the dead are stranded infinitely far behind the sunshine, clinging to the stars so they won't fall, and the only time we glimpse them is when the clouds go to sleep, a beauty that is really abandonment. Against Bobo's soaring, almost sacred vocal, the hook lands its sting - we are afraid and alone, God knows I don't want to be an angel - and by the closing stretch that line stops being a lyric and becomes compulsive repetition, the refusal hardening into panic as the promised paradise is exposed as a lonely, frightened afterlife nobody would choose. The song's power is this exact split: the tune consoles while the words recoil, so the true subject is less angels than our terror of the isolation that any eternity would actually mean.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"paradise exposed as the loneliness nobody would choose","built":"the consoling tune and the recoiling words split the same hook in two"},
"rammstein~f-hre-mich": {"haiku":"Two people are fused into one body with shared pain and bleeding, dependent and inseparable. When one cries, the other provides; when the speaker speaks, the partner dies.","opus":"A portrait of a fused, codependent bond taken to a disturbing extreme: two souls stretched over one skin, one body with two names, nothing able to separate them. The sinister core is control and cruelty as intimacy, the narrator feels good when the other weeps, feeds on the hand of their fear, and declares the other will die when he wills it, love as total possession.","sonnet":"An extreme codependency or psychological merger — two souls sharing one skin, one dying when the other wills it, one weeping while the other thrives; the German text portrays a parasitic union that is also framed as devotion, asking to be led and held while refusing to leave.","src":"haiku","fable":"Führe mich reads the merger of two beings as a horror rather than a romance, and the trick is that its language of union is borrowed wholesale from love song and marriage vow only to be turned parasitic. One body, two names; two souls stretching a single skin — the imagery insists on inseparability, but every reciprocity is rigged one way: when you weep, it goes well for me, your fear feeds my blood. The title command, lead me, held against I will not leave you, exposes the con: this is a dependence that dominates, a captor swearing devotion while the other partner is granted only silence and the promise you die when I want it. Rammstein tends to build songs by locking a metaphor and squeezing it until it cracks, and the closing bridge does exactly that, offering the candle with two wicks and the two-in-one-heart emblem as tender symbols that we now hear as a single life fused too tightly to survive being split. The refusal to separate the pronouns is the point: this is conjoined-twin intimacy voiced as absolute love, where leaving is death and staying is subjugation, and the song never lets you choose which one it means.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"devotion and captivity presented as one indistinguishable condition","built":"two wicks in a single candle means staying is no longer a choice"},
"rammstein~feuer-frei":{"haiku":"Fire and pain transform a person into someone dangerous and noble; someone inflamed by suffering becomes a threat to those around them.","opus":"Pain reframed as ecstasy: those who know fire are ennobled by it, desire and destruction fused in a militaristic chant. The band pushes the link between suffering, lust and violence to a provocative edge.","sonnet":"In German, a song about the seductive and dangerous interplay of pain, fire, and pleasure — those who know pain through burning are first judged, then ennobled, then made dangerous; the repeated \"bang bang\" and \"fire free\" frame desire as a weapon discharged without warning.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's engine is a single grammatical mutation run across three verses of the same clause frame, whoever knows pain: it turns first passive-shameful (getadelt, blamed, for knowing the fire that burns the skin), then ennobled (geadelt, the same knowledge now sanctioned once the fire burns into lust, a spark driven into her lap), then simply dangerous (gefaehrlich). Rammstein routes punishment, desire, and violence through one continuous verb of burning, so that the artillery command of the title, fire at will, is at once an order to shoot, to climax, and to consume. The proverb it lands on, the burnt child who folk wisdom says should dread the flame, gets inverted: this child hunts the fire that severs it from life, which is where the sublimated aggression stops being metaphor. Then the bridge drops the pun and states the sadistic core flat, your joy is not my joy is my misery, exposing that the whole escalating game has been about the other's pleasure being unbearable unless it can be authored as pain. Against all that, the refrain's cartoon Baeng baeng is the mask the verses keep tearing off, gunfire the song forces us to hear, by the end, as the literal aim of everything underneath it.","fv":2,"fnote":"The song’s second life is cinematic: in January 2002 the band played it to a crowd of extras in a former church outside Prague for the opening of xXx, Rob Cohen shooting the film scene and the music video in the same production — and the single shipped as that film’s lead single rather than Mutter’s fifth.","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"punishment, desire, and violence routed through one continuous verb of burning","built":"a single clause frame mutated across three verses from shame to ennoblement to danger"},
"rammstein~feuer-und-wasser":{"haiku":"Desire for a woman who is unreachable and indifferent, using water and fire as metaphors for incompatibility. The narrator burns with passion while she remains cold and distant.","opus":"An obsessive, sexual fixation on a woman the narrator watches swimming, likening desire to fire and her to water that can never mix with it. He knows his longing is hopeless: she doesn't notice him, and he burns even while drowning.","sonnet":"A man watches a woman swimming and is overwhelmed by desire, but the central image is the impossibility of union: fire and water cannot come together, and he burns while submerged, consumed by longing for someone who does not notice him at all.","src":"haiku","fable":"The grand refrain — fire and water cannot bind, are not related — is a rejected voyeur dressing humiliation up as physics. By turning a woman who swims past without noticing him into a law of nature, Lindemann makes his failure feel fated rather than pathetic. The verse-world runs on arousal imagery pressed against her cool indifference, so he can call the incompatibility elemental instead of personal. The last movement drops the mask: he grips her with wet hands, names her cold as ice, and admits she will not waste herself on him. What sounds like tragic metaphysics is self-flattering cope — burning in water insists on its own doom to avoid admitting the desire was simply unwanted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"rejected desire dressed up as a law of nature to avoid calling it unwanted","built":"elemental metaphysics dissolved in the final verse by wet hands and an admission — the physics was self-flattering cope all along"},
"rammstein~feuerrader-live-demo-version":{"haiku":"Submission to pain becomes ecstatic celebration as the speaker trades freedom for chains and sensation, finding transcendence in bondage where fire wheels turn in the void.","opus":"An early ode to pain as pleasure, collar and chains and fire-wheels framing masochism as transcendence into a star-realm. Suffering welcomed as beauty, the band's founding fascination with hurt and ecstasy.","sonnet":"The speaker surrenders entirely to a pain that is also ecstasy — collar, cage, chains, kneeling — and frames the experience as cosmic: where the stars were, fire-wheels spin, and the pain is \"as beautiful as never before.\" The final shift from \"nie\" (never) to \"du\" (you) in the last line collapses the abstract into the specific: the fire-wheels are this person.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~fever-frei":{"haiku":"Those who know pain from fire have become dangerous; the speaker embraces burning as a path to dangerous freedom, rejecting mercy while opening fire on the mechanism that would tame them.","opus":"Fire as sex, punishment and power all at once, the burned child rewarded and endangered by the same flame. Its coldest line makes cruelty explicit: your joy is my misfortune, so open fire.","sonnet":"Three stanzas, three gradations: the person who knows pain from fire is first mocked, then ennobled, then feared — \"gefährlich\" (dangerous) — and the scorched child who has been burned by life becomes the most dangerous of all. \"Dein Glück ist mein Unglück\" (your happiness is my misfortune) is the turn that makes the pyrotechnic release political.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~fr-hling-in-paris": {"haiku":"A man recounts a transformative sexual encounter with a French-speaking woman in Paris where language barriers and physical intimacy marked his awakening to desire.","opus":"Till Lindemann's tender-brutal memory of a first sexual awakening with a Parisian woman, shame giving way to a love he never regretted. The Piaf refrain of regretting nothing threads a rare vulnerability through Rammstein's usual iron.","sonnet":"A young German man's memory of a French woman who showed him his own body and gave him a formative sexual encounter he never regretted — even though he never understood her language, leaving her skin always felt like spring bleeding away in Paris.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song borrows Édith Piaf's Non, je ne regrette rien as its recurring French vow of no-regret, and the whole lyric is that vow set against a young man's initiation by an older Parisian woman — almost certainly a prostitute, given the lips that were often sold yet stay soft. Rammstein makes the encounter tender rather than sordid by staging it as instruction: she shows him his own body in the light, and the shame he arrives with never converts to regret, only to cold once he leaves her skin. The central image works by a language barrier — he understands her tongue as flesh but not as speech, so the intimacy is entirely physical, a wordless fluency that his native speech cannot touch. That gap makes the title's bleeding spring the crux: Paris blooms and hemorrhages at once, so the loss of warmth on parting reads as the wound that first love leaves precisely because there is nothing to regret.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","nostalgia & memory","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"first love remembered as spring in the body and felt as bleeding","built":"Piaf's no-regret vow borrowed to mask the wound that proves regret is real"},
"rammstein~giftig":{"haiku":"A lover's danger is beautiful and inescapable; poison courses through veins while the speaker surrenders to paralysis.","opus":"Desire rendered as venom, a lover who stung the sleeping speaker with no warning and left the poison lodged deep. Paralyzed and enthralled, he finds himself oddly at peace watching the white light approach, no antidote wanted.","sonnet":"A man bitten or stung in his sleep by a beautiful, unpredictable woman he compares to a venomous creature — the poison seeps through his blood slowly and he finds he doesn't want an antidote, paralyzed and captivated even as she disguises herself perfectly and strikes without warning.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~haifisch": {"haiku":"Solidarity and unity within a group, framed through the metaphor of sharks—predators that hide their suffering (tears) behind collective strength, making sacrifice and loyalty their defining bonds.","opus":"A darkly ironic band-as-brotherhood anthem, the six members swearing loyalty and solidarity, keeping their word and their pace. The recurring image, that the shark has tears but no one sees them because it lives underwater, is a wry admission of hidden loneliness and pain beneath a hard, feared exterior, why the sea is salt.","sonnet":"The band presents itself as a tightly bound collective — holding together, keeping pace, loyal to each other — while the refrain about the shark who cries unseen underwater suggests that even fierce creatures carry hidden grief, and loneliness is the reason the ocean is salty.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's engine is the verb halten — to hold, endure, keep — hammered through nearly every line as the creed of a we that holds together, holds its word, holds to the rules only if allowed to set them. That relentless first-person-plural loyalty oath is the mask; the crocodile-tears refrain is what's underneath, and Rammstein grafts it onto Brecht's Mackie Messer to name the game. The shark weeps, but he lives in the water, so no one sees the tears: the predator claims a grief conveniently unverifiable, and the band deepens the alibi into a mock-cosmology where all the lonely weeping in the depths is why the seas run salty. Everything turns on that joke — a gang mythologizing its own menace as sorrow, self-pity as camouflage for teeth — and the six burning hearts that keep you warm are the same fire that consumes, the threat and the fellowship spoken in one breath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["friendship & loyalty","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the predator's tears dissolve invisibly into what makes the sea salt","built":"loyalty oath built on holding fast undercut by the shark weeping unseen"},
"rammstein~halleluja": {"haiku":"A priest exploits choir boys under the guise of faith and service, hiding his predatory abuse behind religious authority and prayer.","opus":"A scalding portrait of a pious churchman who preys on the boys of his choir, Rammstein turning the language of neighborly Christian love into a mask for abuse. The devotional halleluja refrain becomes an indictment of sanctified hypocrisy.","sonnet":"A Catholic priest sexually abuses a boy from the choir — framed in hymnal language and church imagery, the priest turns his bedside portrait of Christ face-down before acts he calls loving one's neighbor, a cold and explicit condemnation of clerical abuse.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein weaponize the pious diction of a hymn against its own subject: the man is introduced as devout and sensitive, keeper of the Bible and the Eucharist, and the song lets that vocabulary of holiness do the concealing while the acts underneath grow unmistakable. The engine is euphemism as alibi — celibacy is spun into a duty to love thy neighbor (so he must love his neighbor), and driving out the sin nesting above the boy's leg becomes a pastoral favor performed by candlelight, each abuse relabeled as devotion. The recurring gesture of turning the Lord's portrait face-down is the whole song in one image: God is made not to see, and by the last lines the same turning is commanded of the boy, the private ritual now openly an order. The Hallelujah answering every line is the cruelest stroke — a congregation's praise flattened into ironic punctuation, so that the mask (the true Christian who knows what neighborly love is) is voiced in perfect earnest while what is underneath is left for the listener to name.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"each cry of praise landing as sanction for what the portrait is turned to hide","built":"the Lord's portrait face-down on the nightstand marks the seam where worship becomes concealment"},
"rammstein~hallomann":{"haiku":"A predatory figure lures a naive young girl with innocuous offerings, establishing a pattern of grooming and implicit threat without explicit demand.","opus":"Rammstein craft one of their most disquieting narratives, a coaxing voice inviting a small girl into a car with promises of mussels, chips and her first sight of the sea. Insisting she not speak and simply climb in, the Hallomann figure warns that nothing after will be as it was. Beneath the sing-and-dance refrain and beach imagery, the predatory menace is unmistakable and deliberate.","sonnet":"A predator lures a young girl — with seaside promises, gentle commands to sing and dance — his cheerful surface concealing something irreversible. The innocence of the imagery makes the menace more precise: nothing will be the same after she steps in.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~halt": {"haiku":"The speaker is driven mad by the sound of human heartbeats, seeing people as vermin to be eliminated, and vows to get a gun.","opus":"A misanthrope so overwhelmed by other people's presence that he wants their hearts to literally stop beating, then goes home for his gun. Rammstein pushes social alienation to its grotesque endpoint, where the mere sound of humanity becomes unbearable.","sonnet":"A narrator who is tortured by the sound of other people's heartbeats — the noise of human life around him is unbearable, and his solution escalates from screaming at people to stop their hearts to going home to get his rifle.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein's usual mode is provocation through metaphor, but Halt withholds the metaphor: the persona simply cannot stand the sound of other people's heartbeats, and the song stages his descent from misanthropic sensitivity into a plan for slaughter. The genius of the writing is that it hijacks the most universal image of life itself — the beating heart, multiplied by everyone alive — and reframes it as unbearable noise pounding in his temples, so that his hatred of humanity registers physiologically rather than ideologically; there are simply too many people, and each one is an offense measured in a pulse. The turn comes when his language borrows the vocabulary of enlightenment and mercy — he will bring light, let the sun into your heart, a dead heart is no loss, stand still — and each tender-sounding phrase reveals itself as a euphemism for stopping the heart, the imperative Halt (stop) doubling as command to the world and command to the pulse. It collapses in the most banal and chilling line available: the decision is not hard, he will go home and fetch his rifle, the cosmic disgust suddenly shrunk to the flat logistics of a shooting. Read straight, it is a portrait of the mass-killer's interior — grandiosity, sensory torment, and self-appointed benevolence fused into the same gesture — and the militaristic drill-command cadence tells you this savior has always really been an executioner.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"intolerance of human heartbeats hardening past metaphor into intent","built":"the plea to make the hearts stop slides through softened offer to weapon retrieval"},
"rammstein~heirate-mich":{"haiku":"A man graveside has become obsessed with exhuming his dead wife, eventually desecrating her corpse out of desperate love. The song portrays extreme devotion twisted into necrophilia and madness.","opus":"Rammstein turn grief into gothic horror, following a man haunting a churchyard a year after loss, sleeping nightly beside a headstone. Consumed by mourning, he digs with his hands to reclaim what death took, kissing a cold mouth as skin tears like paper and pieces fall away. The pleading refrain to marry him collapses love into necrophilic desperation, ending with a rooster beheaded at dawn.","sonnet":"A grieving man has been sleeping at his wife's grave for a year, unable to release her. He digs her up by moonlight to kiss cold lips and hold what remains — an act of desperate devotion that ends in horror as the body disintegrates in his arms.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~heirate-mich-edit":{"haiku":"A man haunted by his lover's death exhumes her grave and compels her corpse toward marriage, transforming necrophilic obsession into grotesque parody of eternal devotion.","opus":"A grief so total it turns necrophilic, a man haunting his dead beloved's grave, digging her up to hold her only to have her skin tear like paper. When the crowing rooster signals she escapes him again with the dawn, he beheads it, refusing to let morning take her twice.","sonnet":"Necrophilia as grief rendered without irony: a widower who has spent a year sleeping by his wife's gravestone finally digs her up in the moonlight to kiss her cold mouth and hold her, only for her skin to tear like paper as she crumbles away from him a second time. The final image — decapitating the rooster at dawn — is revenge against morning itself.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~herzeleid": {"haiku":"People should protect each other from heartache; time shared is fleeting yet memories stretch endlessly.","opus":"The stark title track, closer to a warning than a song: cherish each other and guard against heartache, because however many years you share will feel like minutes. Uncharacteristically tender Rammstein, bleak about how briefly love lasts.","sonnet":"A sparse, almost ceremonial lyric warning loved ones to protect each other from heartache while they still have time — years spent together will one day seem like minutes, so cherish each other now. It's unusually tender for Rammstein: just grief and the knowledge of how quickly love passes.","src":"haiku","fable":"The album's title track abandons Rammstein's usual industrial aggression for something closer to a wedding blessing turned to ash: a stern voice counsels lovers to guard one another against heartbreak because their time together, however many years, will shrink to the length of minutes. The devastating pivot comes near the end, when the counsel inverts itself and warns against togetherness itself (Zweisamkeit) — as if the only certain protection from the pain of losing someone is never to be joined at all. Everything else is the title word chanted past the point of meaning, so that Herzeleid stops being a noun and becomes a bell, tolling the grief the opening advice was powerless to prevent.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire"],"means":"closeness itself warned against because the time will vanish","built":"the tolled word of heartache swells until even nearness becomes a threat"},
"rammstein~hilf-mir": {"haiku":"A German-language song told from a child's perspective discovering fire—playing with matches when alone, becoming fascinated and burned. The refrain 'the fire loves me' becomes ambiguous: does fire love or destroy? Ends with resurrection from ashes.","opus":"Retelling a Struwwelpeter tale, a child left home alone plays with matches and is consumed by fire, crying for help as the flames both love and kill her before she rises from the ashes toward the sun.","sonnet":"Retelling the cautionary tale of a child left alone who lights matches out of loneliness and fascination with fire, the song follows the girl as the flames engulf her entirely, ending with her burned to ash — a dark fairy-tale about childhood danger and obsession with fire.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein set the Struwwelpeter cautionary verse of Paulinchen, the girl who plays with matches while her parents are out and burns to ash, almost verbatim, then hijack its moral. The children's rhyme meant the fire as punishment; here the refrain that swings between the fire loving me and not loving me turns the flames into a lover, so the burning reads as a longing rather than an accident, the child drawn to fire precisely because she is lonely and asks why she cannot get well. Where the original ends in a warning pile of ashes, this version lets her rise from them alone into the sunlight, converting cautionary death into a phoenix self-annihilation that is desired, even redemptive. The bare cry for help, repeated to the end, is left deliberately unanswered, so the plea and the seduction stay locked together and the song refuses to say whether rescue was ever wanted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"burning recast as the only intimacy that ever answered","built":"annihilation rewrites the cautionary ash as ascent into sunlight"},
"rammstein~ich-tu-dir-weh": {"haiku":"A German-language song depicting a cruel, controlling relationship built on inflicting pain and humiliation as expressions of perverse love and dependency. The narrator claims pain excites and sustains the victim, using torture imagery as metaphor for domination.","opus":"Sung in German, the song depicts a controlling, sadomasochistic relationship in which the speaker inflicts graphic pain and torture on a wholly submissive partner who craves it; he loves that they love him precisely because he does not love them back.","sonnet":"A domineering figure inflicts deliberate physical pain on a willing, completely devoted partner, declaring without remorse that the cruelty — cuts, restraints, and worse — is pleasurable and that love is entirely one-sided.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song weaponizes the language of romantic devotion, then reveals it as sadomasochistic ritual: the opening lines announce that you exist only for me, that you are utterly devoted, and that you love me precisely because I do not love you — a perfect closed loop of control masquerading as a love song. Rammstein's method here is to escalate through inventory: the second verse abandons narrative for a clinical, mounting catalog of instruments and injuries (needle, pliers, blunt saw, barbed wire), the accumulation itself doing the work of horror while the refrain insists it doesn't hurt me, it does you good, listen how it screams — the abuser's serene reassurance that the victim wants this. The bridge is where the mask slips: I am the captain, you the ship, but then I see your face in the mirror and the loop-line returns — suggesting the two figures may be one, the ritual a self-directed drama of dominance and shame in which giver and receiver of pain are the same person. What clinches it is that the refrain never develops; its flat repetition is the point, the compulsive circularity of a dynamic that cannot resolve, only recur.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"This text put Liebe ist für alle da on the youth-protection index in November 2009 — the album was reissued without the song until Rammstein won in the Cologne administrative court; de-listed 1 June 2010.","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"cruelty framed as the only gift available to one who cannot love back","built":"the mirror shows the other's face where the speaker's own should be"},
"rammstein~ich-will":{"haiku":"A performer demands trust, belief, and complete control of their audience's attention, senses, and devotion while the audience questions their understanding.","opus":"A song about the addictive craving for attention and control between a performer and crowd: the singer demands to be seen, heard, trusted and to drown in applause, controlling every heartbeat—yet the call-and-response ends with him confessing he doesn't understand them, exposing the loneliness under the hunger for adulation.","sonnet":"A song about the hunger for total public validation — the narrator demands to be trusted, believed, watched, felt, and understood by a crowd, but the communication is broken; the crowd hears and sees and feels him but he cannot understand them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A performer's demand for total attention, taken to its logical extreme and then exposed as a trap. The verses are a catalog of appetites phrased not as need but as command — I want you to trust me, I want to control every heartbeat, I want to drown in applause — collapsing the line between entertainment and domination until being watched becomes a kind of power over the watchers. Then the call-and-response inverts everything: the crowd answers that yes, they hear him, see him, feel him, and his reply is that he does not understand them, so the very thing he demanded arrives and lands as noise. The real turn on Mutter is the pronoun: mid-song the singular I quietly becomes a we, the star's craving reissued as the collective plea of the whole band toward the audience, so the appetite for control was never one narcissist's but the mechanism of the show itself — and the closing failure to understand is mutual, performer and mass staring at each other across a gap that all the wanting only widens.","fv":2,"fnote":"The video stages the band as bank robbers who surrender straight into celebrity — marched off in shackles down a red carpet to accept a Goldene Kamera, the band calling it a demonstration of the media’s hunger for a good story. It was released September 10, 2001; by the next morning its hostage imagery was unairable, and rotation stopped.","themes":["artifice & performance","surveillance & control","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"demanded attention arriving and landing as noise across an unbridgeable gap","built":"singular I quietly becomes we, reissuing narcissism as the show's own mechanism"},
"rammstein~k-ss-mich-fellfrosch": {"haiku":"A creature trapped in darkness craves touch and kiss despite pain. Desire and violence entangle as tongues and passion leave only blood.","opus":"A deliberately grotesque double-entendre casting cunnilingus as a creature caught in darkness begging for the tongue, Rammstein weaponizing crude anatomy and shock to provoke rather than seduce.","sonnet":"A song told from the perspective of a female frog in darkness who craves being kissed and touched — the lyrics describe her arousal and her need for a man's tongue, escalating to a grotesque scene where the tongue flies out and strikes her face repeatedly until she bleeds, a surreal fable about lust, ugliness, and the violence underlying desire.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages a grotesque bait-and-switch: it opens in the coy, submissive voice of a creature that holds still because it wants to be fingered, panting to be kissed — and Rammstein lets you assume the standard erotic frame before pulling it apart. The trapped-in-darkness refrain, never seeing sunlight, keeps the thing subterranean and shameful, and the coarse title-epithet Fellfrosch signals from the start that this seduction is being mocked, not honored. The turn comes when the tongue itself becomes the aggressor — it crawls out of the mouth and beats her bloody across the face until her nose runs red — so the begging-to-be-licked setup collapses into violence dealt by the very organ the song kept fetishizing. What looked like desire is really a trap sprung on the desiring thing, the bitter taste warned about all along paid back as a beating. The final plea, kiss me on my wet lips even though it tastes bitter, lands as the mask still smiling over the wound, invitation and injury fused into one refrain.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"promised transformation landing as violation rather than liberation","built":"the fairy-tale kiss begged for by the creature erupts into the face as assault"},
"rammstein~keine-lust": {"haiku":"A German-language song cataloging lack of desire and motivation for bodily functions and engagement. The speaker is cold, unmotivated, and reduced to counting flies while lying down.","opus":"Sung from total apathy and depression, the narrator lists all the things he has no desire to do, from touching himself to eating or moving, preferring to lie still, count flies, and note how long he has already been cold.","sonnet":"An extreme, comic apathy — the speaker catalogues all the things he would like to do but has no drive to attempt (eat, masturbate, undress, go out in the snow), culminating in the observation that he has simply been lying still so long he has gone cold.","src":"haiku","fable":"A litany of appetites named only to be refused: the song runs almost every line through the same grammar of I would have the urge to X, but I have no urge to try it, so that even masturbation, eating, undressing get proposed and canceled in the same breath. Rammstein—a band built on brute physical want—here voids its own engine, turning the pounding riff into the sound of a body that has stopped being able to want anything — even the end of its own self-hatred, even its own touch. The refrain about wanting to run with big animals but not risking it, wanting to leave the snow but not wanting to freeze, exposes the trap: inertia is more powerful than any competing wish, so the speaker stays put and counts flies, listlessly touching himself only to notice he has been cold for a long time already. That the crushing final movement is nothing but So cold, I am cold repeated is the point—the anhedonia has burned through language itself, leaving no verb left but the sensation of a corpse cooling, which is what desirelessness finally is.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","suicide"],"means":"comedy of inertia deepening into a man who has stopped wanting to be alive","built":"the comic catalogue of refusals lands last on counting flies and lying down"},
"rammstein~klavier":{"haiku":"A man murders a woman who promised devotion while she played piano for him alone; he blood-soaks the room and locks the door, turning her suffering into his own.","opus":"The German band builds a gothic murder-tale around a locked door and a dusty, out-of-tune piano. Behind it sits a woman who plays no more; the narrator, once breathless at her music, poured her blood into the fire of his rage and sealed her away. When the door finally opens, parents scream and beat him, no one believing he sickened from grief and stench beside her.","sonnet":"A man locked a woman in a room after she betrayed him — or he imagined she did — keeping her at the piano until she stopped playing. When authorities finally open the door, the horror is revealed: she has been dead behind it, and his obsessive listening has curdled into something that held her breath permanently still.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~kokain":{"haiku":"A user surrenders to cocaine's seductive embrace, personified as a beautiful entity that impregnates him with addiction while distorting his self-perception.","opus":"Cocaine personified as a white fairy who draws her bow and fathers a monstrous pregnancy, the addict cradling the drug like his own blood and calling it the most beautiful child of all, until he births and devours his own afterbirth. It's addiction rendered as grotesque, self-consuming ritual. Rammstein, the Berlin Neue Deutsche Harte titans, deploy their trademark rolled-R menace and shock imagery to make dependency into body horror.","sonnet":"Cocaine personified as a white fairy — beautiful, seductive, whispering that you're the most special child, holding you like blood. The relationship is described as violent pregnancy: she forcibly impregnates him with craving, torments him through the night, and he watches himself give birth to something he then consumes. The evil inside him becomes good under her influence, which is exactly the trap.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~l-gen":{"haiku":"The speaker admits to endemic deception across all registers—of others, the self, and reality itself—rendering truth unknowable and trust impossible.","opus":"A liar's confession that dismantles a picture-perfect romantic ideal line by line, admitting every tender promise is a fabrication. The chilling turn is total: he no longer even believes himself.","sonnet":"A compulsive liar inventories the romantic promises he makes — walking barefoot on beaches, eternal fidelity, breakfast in bed — before admitting in the chorus that it's all lies, he even lies to himself, no one trusts him including himself. The self-awareness provides no cure: \"wer es glaubt, ist selber schuld\" (whoever believes it is to blame themselves).","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~laichzeit": {"haiku":"Perverted sexual acts involving fish and animals during spawning season; the song depicts grotesque imagery of desire and defilement without standard boundaries.","opus":"A deliberately grotesque provocation using spawning season (Laichzeit) and a fish shedding its skin as metaphor for taboo, incestuous desire, the man loving mother, sister, dog in turn while the phallic fish forces itself to their mouths and spits dead milk in the face. The revulsion (you don't dare touch it, fish milk everywhere) makes the imagery an intentionally repulsive meditation on base animal instinct and forbidden sexuality.","sonnet":"A deliberately grotesque, sexually transgressive piece about spawning season — a fish as both literal animal and crude sexual metaphor cycles through encounters with a mother, sister, and dog, ejaculating and then retreating into solitude, the imagery designed to provoke and disgust.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein's most notorious taboo-song hides its subject in plain sight through a pun: Laichzeit means spawning season, but Laich (spawn) chimes against Leiche (corpse), so the whole reproductive drama reads as necrophilic rot. Each verse pairs the same predatory figure with a forbidden partner — the mother, the sister, the dog — and each act of loving is grafted onto a fish that molts, feeds, and finally kisses with its tongue and spits dead milk into your face, so animal spawning becomes a mask for incest and abuse figured as something you daren't even touch. The relentless chant of the one word is the engine of meaning: repetition strips the euphemism of any biology and turns natural cycle into compulsion, a season that is always happening. The outro dissolves the family into elements and absence — the sea has taken the mother, the sisters have no time, the dog waves from the shore — leaving only the fish and its needed solitude, which lands the real cruelty: the predator ends alone with his instinct while everyone he touched is scattered or gone. Underneath the aquatic grotesque, the song reads as a cold biological determinism, drives dressed as tide, the mask of nature laid over the unspeakable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","violence & murder","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"instinct framed as a trap that delivers isolation instead of communion","built":"the fish mask strips reproduction to blind compulsion, family to violation"},
"rammstein~liebe-ist-f-r-alle-da": {"haiku":"Sexual desire is framed as predatory and violent, with the narrator using manipulation and imprisonment in fantasy to claim unwilling partners. The song conflates love with conquest and violation.","opus":"A predatory speaker eyes beautiful bodies in the sun and admits that getting sex means acting friendly, then retreats into a fantasy where he confines a woman who does not resist. He bitterly repeats that love is for everyone but not for him.","sonnet":"A man approaches attractive strangers with false charm while privately admitting that love is not available to him; he retreats into fantasies in which the women cannot resist him. The lyrics shift from excluded longing to something darker when the fantasy turns coercive — he holds her and no one sees her weep.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein weaponize their signature move — the sweet-then-savage swerve — as a structural argument about consent, opening on sun, warm water, and glistening bodies before the seduction curdles into a line that makes politeness a mere tactic: whoever wants to fuck must be friendly. The title functions as bitter irony rather than an anthem; love is for everyone becomes the taunt of the one it excludes, and the chorus retreats into fantasy where shutting the eyes conjures a captive who does not resist because she is imagined, not present. The song's cruelty lives in its arc: the second half tips from confessed lack (not for me) toward a granted possession (also for me), so the speaker doesn't overcome his exclusion, he manufactures inclusion by force in a private theatre. The detail that indicts him is the one he cannot fully suppress — nobody sees her weeping — the fantasy's own leak, admitting the harm even as it insists she does not fight back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"possession mistaken for love because reciprocity stays forever out of reach","built":"the title's promise flips to include him at the moment of coercion"},
"rammstein~liese-version":{"haiku":"A young man named Jakob sexually assaults and impregnates a girl named Liese against her will in a wheat field.","opus":"Rammstein casts a pastoral folk-horror in this version, Liese tending geese on a Sunday meadow when Jakob arrives with a sickle. Coaxing and coercing, he leads her into a wheat field, the rusty blade a threat if she isn't kind to him. Hidden in the golden flood he holds the child until evening, the nursery-rhyme lilt masking an act of unmistakable violation.","sonnet":"A pastoral folk-horror scene: a boy named Jakob coerces a girl tending geese with the threat of a bloody scythe, taking what he wants in a wheat field through the afternoon. The nursery-rhyme cadence makes the violence more disturbing — rural idyll as cover for predation, sweetness as instrument of harm.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~links-2-3-4":{"haiku":"The narrator's heart beats on the left side, against social expectation and external judgment. This leftward orientation becomes a defiant assertion of individual truth.","opus":"Rammstein's answer to accusations of far-right sympathy, insisting their heart beats on the left even when others want it placed on the right. The martial marching cadence turns political self-defense into a defiant stomp.","sonnet":"A response to accusations that Rammstein are right-wing — the song asks where the heart actually beats, looks down, and finds it on the left side of the chest, turning a military march rhythm into a declaration of left political alignment.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song turns on one pun, and it is Rammstein's answer to years of accusations that they were politically right-wing, going back to Herzeleid: rather than argue, they exploit the German idiom das Herz am rechten Fleck, heart in the right place, meaning well-meaning and decent. The abstract questions of the verses sound like they are interrogating the heart itself (can a heart be broken, can one be made of stone), but the refrain snaps them into anatomy: they want my heart in the right, correct place, yet when I look down it beats on the left, because the physical heart is literally on the left. The band then weaponizes that fact as a marching cadence, links zwo drei vier, left-two-three-four, a deliberately provocative drill count that dares you to hear jackboots while the word left declares the opposite allegiance. The bridge states the thesis outright, that der Neider, the envious slanderer, had it wrong all along. And the closing avalanche of counted lefts, drilled far past sense into pure repetition, converts the stance from an argument to be won into something stamped out again and again until it cannot be misheard.","fv":2,"fnote":"The accusation was specific: the Stripped video had used Leni Riefenstahl’s Olympia footage, and the band answered the resulting neo-Nazi charges with a flat statement — we are not Nazis, we are against racism and bigotry — and then with this song, the anatomical left drilled into a marching count nobody could mishear.","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"political self-defense stamped out as a marching cadence","built":"the anatomical fact that the heart beats left weaponized as a drill count"},
"rammstein~los": {"haiku":"Overcoming namelessness and enforced silence through music and collective voice, resisting censorship and asserting presence through sound.","opus":"A defiant German-language declaration of a band or group that was once nameless, wordless, and songless but has found its name and voice. It answers critics who call the music tasteless, godless, and worthy of banning, insisting they will never be rid of them.","sonnet":"A band's origin story told through a cascade of German privative adjectives: once nameless and wordless, they were hit by a storm that launched them, and despite critics calling them tasteless and hopeless, they will never be silenced or gotten rid of.","src":"haiku","fable":"The suffix -los (English -less) is Rammstein's entire engine here: every line is built on it, so the song is a self-defining act of negation that keeps turning lack into presence. The band narrates its own emergence from being nameless, wordless, and songless (namenlos, wortlos, sanglos) toward having a name and words, while insisting that even when they are somewhat toneless they are never soundless (klanglos) — you hear us anyway. The middle section ventriloquizes the moralizing critic, stacking the same suffix into outrage — this is graceless, tasteless, godless, it should be banned, censored — so that condemnation and self-portrait are spoken in one shared vocabulary, the mask and the face made of identical material. The turn comes when the negation is flipped into a threat: you will fall silent (lautlos) but you will never be rid of us (uns nie los), the prefix los- pivoting from lacking to loosing. And the outro releases the pun the title has been holding — los as both -less and go/unleashed — landing on wir waren los, we were let loose, so a song assembled entirely from words for absence ends by declaring itself unstoppably present.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"every charge absorbed as a badge, the outcasts loose and running","built":"the suffix meaning lacking detonates in the final word into its opposite"},
"rammstein~mann-gegen-mann": {"haiku":"A homosexual man navigates desire and social condemnation, claiming ownership of his body and sexuality against family and society's judgment.","opus":"A first-person German-language declaration of homosexual desire, sung by a man who calls himself a king without a queen and whose skin belongs to other men. He acknowledges being branded a traitor and the nightmare of fathers, and faces slurs hurled at him while asserting his nature.","sonnet":"A celebration and defence of gay male desire: the narrator embraces his attraction to men as destiny and as his own gift, while acknowledging the cold condemnation — homophobic slurs, hostile tongues — that society directs at him.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein takes the aggressive drill-square chant of Mann gegen Mann — man against man — and detonates the phrase's macho charge into its opposite: a gay man's defiant self-declaration, my skin belongs to the gentlemen, where the martial rhythm of combat becomes the rhythm of desire between equals. The pun arrives early — he takes fate into his own hands and his desire is manned — and the song builds its speaker from negative space, a king without a queen, the corner of every room and shadow of every tree, then names the cost directly: his own sex brands him traitor and he is the nightmare of all fathers. What the structure earns is the turn from bravado to wound — the strutting first-person hardens through the verses until the interlude strips the mask away, the crowd hurling the slur Schwuler at him over cold tongues that lash. The final defiance is chosen, not innocent: he wants no balance, takes the sun full in the face, and lets the chant close by adding a third man to the pair, refusing to let the taunt shrink him back down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","shame & repression","love & desire"],"means":"sunlit self-possession worn over the wound of being hated","built":"defiant litany answered by the very blow it dares"},
"rammstein~mehr":{"haiku":"Expresses insatiable greed and the inability to ever feel satisfied, with the narrator always wanting more regardless of what he possesses. The song portrays endless consumption and material hunger as an inescapable condition.","opus":"A study of insatiable greed voiced by a narrator who wants everything and always ten times more, never satisfied, with no goal and no such thing as too much. He dismisses modesty and would even take what little others have, since they supposedly don't need it.","sonnet":"Sung from the perspective of someone driven by insatiable greed, the song describes a figure who is never satisfied no matter how much they have, covets what others possess, and sees no reason for restraint or sharing — \"more\" is always the only goal.","src":"haiku","fable":"Mehr reduces appetite to its stupidest, most insatiable engine: a first-person hymn to acquisition that never fills up, where wanting stops being a feeling and hardens into a law of the self. The trick is that the song names no object; the speaker only claims ten times as much of everything, so the title-word hammered as chorus becomes the whole point, greed as pure grammar. The bridge is where the mask drops: give me what the poor have too, they do not need it anyway, and gluttony curdles into confiscation that justifies itself by its victim's want. Lindemann plays the glutton straight, no wink, and that deadpan is the method, letting the voice of capital indict itself by simply never stopping.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["money & the street","politics & society"],"means":"greed hardened into grammar, appetite with no object and no floor","built":"the bridge lets the voice of capital indict itself — give me what the poor have, they do not need it — spoken deadpan and left to stand"},
"rammstein~mein-herz-brennt":{"haiku":"A voice from darkness whispers to children about demons stealing their innocent tears. The burning heart watches while nightmares consume the young.","opus":"Built on the sinister German TV bedtime host, a nightmare-keeper who tears out his own heart to steal children's tears and fears. Rammstein turning childhood terror into gothic grandeur, the burning heart as both gift and curse.","sonnet":"In German: a sinister voice from the pillow addresses children at night, claiming to bring something torn from its own chest — while demons, ghosts, and black fairies crawl from the basement to steal children's tears and inject them into cold veins. It's a nightmarish lullaby about childhood terror, the boundary between dream and predation deliberately blurred.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song opens by quoting the German bedtime formula of the children's TV sandman, now, dear children, pay close attention, and immediately turns the nightly comfort into a threat. The speaker is a disembodied voice from the pillow who has torn his own burning heart out and uses it to force sleeping eyes shut, a lullaby-teller who is also the monster. The verses reveal the exchange at the heart of it: the demons and black fairies that crawl from the cellar steal the children's small hot tears and press them into his cold veins, so the singer feeds on the very fear he induces. Mein Herz brennt, my heart burns, is both a confession of feeling and the fire he needs stolen tears to keep lit.","fv":2,"fnote":"The face on the sleeve is a real dead fetus, photographed by Daniel and Geo Fuchs, the duo known for shooting preserved museum specimens — enough to draw BILD and the churches into open outrage. The story that trails the image, persistent but never confirmed, is that the specimen was over two centuries old, floating in formaldehyde long before it became a mother’s album cover.","themes":["faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"the lullaby-teller and the monster occupy the same body","built":"the TV bedtime formula is quoted and immediately turned into a threat"},
"rammstein~mein-land": {"haiku":"Homelessness and displacement across borders; the singer wanders without belonging anywhere. The land rejects them as a migrant caught between territories.","opus":"A menacing possessive nationalism: a figure roaming every compass direction only to be confronted by someone planting a flag and claiming 'this is my land, you're on my land'; the closing cries of expelled/expel/forgotten expose the ugliness of territorial exclusion where no one is welcome to stay.","sonnet":"A wanderer drifts compass-point to compass-point with nowhere to settle, only to encounter a flag-waving nationalist who claims every beach and wave as his own — the wanderer is driven out, belonging nowhere, while the land-claimer's ownership remains absolute and hostile.","src":"haiku","fable":"The compass verses expose the irony at the song's heart: the singer wanders east to south to west to north, from land to land, always alone and invited by no one to stay. This is the voice of the dispossessed, the eternal migrant. Yet the same mouth roars the anthem of possession — my country, you are here in my land — so the nationalist's territorial shout and the exile's homelessness turn out to be the same person, the claim to soil sung loudest by someone who has none. The bridge pushes this to apocalypse: a voice from the light tears the horizon in two and declares nothing here is free anymore, the possessive drive swelling until belonging becomes total blockade. The outro settles the verdict in three past participles — driven out, to be driven out, forgotten — collapsing the boast into its opposite: the one who screams this is mine ends with nowhere he can stay. Rammstein, alert as ever to the German ear for Blut-und-Boden rhetoric, builds the whole track so the martial refrain indicts the very ownership it performs.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["exile & diaspora","politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"the triumphant homeland refrain recast as belonging weaponized against the outsider","built":"the swagger of ownership exposed when every compass direction turns him away"},
"rammstein~mein-teil":{"haiku":"A darkly ironic retelling of the Armin Meiwes cannibalism case, examining consumption, appetite, and the transgressive erotics of domination and death.","opus":"Rammstein's account of the Armin Meiwes cannibalism case, staged from both butcher and willing victim with grotesque dinner-table refinement. It is a study in extreme consent and appetite, played utterly deadpan.","sonnet":"Based on the 2001 Armin Meiwes case, the song narrates consensual cannibalism from the perspective of the willing victim — meeting a man who wants to eat him, enduring the cutting and blood loss while the meal is prepared with care and served with wine and candlelight, the whole scene rendered with a grotesque domestic calm.","src":"haiku","fable":"Lindemann builds the track from the Meiwes case, the 2001 Rotenburg killing where cannibal and victim met by classified ad and ate part of him together before he died, then sings it from inside the meal. The whole song hangs on a pun: Mein Teil means my part, my portion, and the anatomical piece actually eaten, so the hammered refrain of denial becomes the victim protesting his own dish, refusing even as he swallows it. The middle drops horror for connoisseurship — flambé, porcelain, good wine, candlelight, a little culture — the dinner ritual weaponized to make atrocity feel mannered and consensual, the obscenity the news story could not name. Then the final verse breaks the mask: a scream cutting through ranks of angels, feathered flesh falling onto a shrieking childhood, the tidy diner revealed as a violated child and the gourmand pose exposed as a defense against a wound never about appetite. What reads as a novelty gross-out is really a study of complicity, of the human gift for aestheticizing its own destruction and calling it civilization.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance","shame & repression"],"means":"atrocity aestheticized as dinner ritual until childhood breaks through the mannered pose","built":"the title pun fuses denial and the anatomical portion into the same hammered refrain"},
"rammstein~morgenstern": {"haiku":"A disfigured woman hides from daylight and prays for the morning star to grant her beauty and love, seeking cosmic validation of her worth.","opus":"A tender tale of a woman so ugly she hides from daylight and fears the light on her face, as her lover prays to the morning star to shine warmth on her, insisting she is not alone and, seen with the heart, truly beautiful.","sonnet":"A plea addressed to the morning star on behalf of someone considered ugly by the world's standards: the speaker asks the star to shine warmly on her face, tell her she is not alone, and then shifts — the star sees with the heart and finds her beautiful.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein's usual mode is bluntness weaponized, so the trick here is that the ugliness curdles into its opposite. The song sets a woman so hideous the daylight itself recoils and shines up into her face from below, forcing her to live in shadow and beg a star to paint beauty onto her cheeks — and the venomous refrain, you are ugly, simply ugly, keeps hammering that verdict. But the second half quietly rotates the pronoun: the plea to the morning star is no longer for a self but for the beloved, on her un-face, on a breaking heart, and the ugliness stops being an insult and becomes the speaker's own confessed failing — man is an eye-creature who craves pretty things, and that appetite is the flaw, not her face. The morning star is Venus, of course, the love-planet masquerading as mere dawn-light, and the resolution turns on it: the star does warm the chest where life beats, and the closing line trades sight for the heart's seeing — to see with the heart — at which point she is beautiful, wunderschön. The whole song is engineered so that the cruelest word in it, ugly, is the thing being renounced.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"ugliness belonging to the seeing eye rather than the unseen face","built":"a second voice interrupts her plea to confirm the verdict then loses the argument"},
"rammstein~moskau": {"haiku":"Moscow is portrayed as an aging prostitute, beautiful yet decaying, that captivates and seduces through money and lust.","opus":"Introduced as a song about the world's most beautiful city, it personifies Moscow as an aging, gold-toothed prostitute the speaker desires and pays for, mixing lust and disgust; a chirpy Russian chorus about Young Pioneers singing songs to Lenin plays against the sordid imagery.","sonnet":"Moscow is personified as an aging, gold-toothed prostitute — beautiful, alluring, and only available for money — while the Russian chorus ironically hails pioneer children singing Lenin's praises, undercutting Soviet nostalgia with a cynical portrait of the city.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein turns a Soviet propaganda anthem inside out: a female Russian voice announces the most beautiful city in the world, children sing to Lenin, and the folk-count raz-dva-tri drives the beat, while Till's German verses recast Moscow as an aging prostitute who undresses only for money and rebuilds her breasts anew. The two registers never resolve; the sung Russian keeps insisting on civic pride exactly as the German buyer confesses he pays for every favour, so the glorification and the transaction run simultaneously rather than one debunking the other. That doubleness is the point — post-Soviet Moscow as a city selling a gilded facade of its former grandeur, still the loveliest thing on earth precisely because it has learned to powder the old skin and perform desire for cash. The bridge tightens the screw: the German I-see-something-you-don't taunt over the sleeping, prone Russian addressee makes the seller the one truly exposed, the buyer holding a secret knowledge she can never see.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","money & the street","surveillance & control"],"means":"city worshipped and rented, contempt and awe refusing to resolve","built":"Young Pioneer chant and the buyer's German verse run as mask and reveal"},
"rammstein~mutter":{"haiku":"An unwanted child born without maternal care seeks to inflict disease and death upon the mother who rejected them; rage fuels vengeance.","opus":"One of the German industrial-metal band's starkest songs, this gives voice to a being born without a mother, unfed, unnamed, umbilical-less, brought into existence in haste and without seed. That absence festers into a vow of vengeance against the woman who never bore him, even as the closing cries beg her for strength, Rammstein wringing operatic grief and menace from a creature aching for the origin it was denied.","sonnet":"A figure born without a mother — grown from no womb, nursed by no breast, named by no one — confronts the void that absence left. German-language lyrics describe a laboratory origin: no navel, a tube for a throat. Rage curdles into a vow to give the absent mother a disease and drown her, while still crying out for her strength.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track of Rammstein's 2001 album is sung by a creature that was manufactured rather than born: no navel on its belly, a tube down its throat, conceived in haste and without seed, and denied even a name. What makes it more than gothic horror is that the being grieves the mother it never had as fiercely as it hates her, and the two feelings never separate — it swears vengeance on the woman who never bore it and in the same breath begs her, over and over in the outro, to give it strength. The song's engine is that unresolved double bind: the birthmark on its forehead (Muttermal, literally a mother-mark, the only trace of a mother it carries) can only be removed by the kiss of a knife, so the self-erasure that would free it from the missing mother is also suicide. Its final cry is not to a real parent but to an absence it addresses as one anyway, which is the cruelest reading of the refrain's endless repetition of the word Mutter: calling for something that was never there and cannot answer.","fv":2,"fnote":"Lindemann has tied the manufactured orphan to his real mother — she pushed me away because she didn’t like me much, he said, adding it took him years to see how much of his life that relationship had shaped.","themes":["family & blood","suicide","anger & defiance"],"means":"grief and vengeance for an absent mother","built":"muttermal the only mother-trace the song can name"},
"rammstein~nebel":{"haiku":"Final moments of love and farewell at the edge of the world; a kiss as both greeting and goodbye at the point where everything ends.","opus":"A devastating farewell at the edge of the sea, where a woman carrying evening in her chest knows she's dying, lays her head in her lover's lap and asks for one last kiss, her lips pale, his eyes wet, the wind eating her words. Years later he no longer remembers. The closer to Rammstein's Mutter, it's the band at their most tender and grief-stricken, industrial bombast set aside for pure loss.","sonnet":"A deathbed farewell at the edge of the sea — she knows the end is close and tries to speak the truth but the wind takes the words before they arrive. What remains is touch: a trembling hand held, a forehead kissed, a last kiss on lips already pale. He remembers the kiss now as something unreachably distant, the memory itself beginning to blur.","src":"haiku","fable":"The closing track of Mutter stages a death at the waterline and then survives it into forgetting. The verse holds two lovers pressed together where the sea meets the land, one of them carrying the evening in her chest and knowing she must pass; she lays her head in his lap and begs a last kiss, and the sea's end doubles as the end of a life. The refrain freezes that kiss into ritual, her lips weak and pale, his eyes going wet, repeating it as if repetition could keep the moment from finishing. Then the outro breaks the frame and jumps forward: the last kiss is long ago now, and he no longer remembers it. That is where the withheld title lives, because Nebel, fog, is a word the lyric never says out loud and instead becomes the condition of the ending, grief not as a wound that stays sharp but as a haze that finally erases even the loss it was made of.","fv":2,"fnote":"The title hides a mirror: Nebel spelled backwards is Leben — fog reversed is life — and listeners have long read the closer through that inversion, the album that begins with an unborn face ending in a haze where a life runs backwards out of memory. The band, as ever, has confirmed nothing.","themes":["death & grief","love & desire"],"means":"grief that outlasts the wound by erasing memory","built":"the title fog becomes the ending condition itself"},
"rammstein~ohne-dich": {"haiku":"The speaker describes being unable to exist without their lover while being alone with them, counting empty hours. The forest becomes death where birds sing no more and breath grows difficult.","opus":"Rammstein's most naked grief song: a man returns to the forest where he last saw a lost love and finds it silent and dead, time itself frozen. The usual industrial menace drops away for pure, plain mourning.","sonnet":"A German-language grief song about wandering into the forest to find someone who is gone — the birds no longer sing, the branches are lifeless, and the narrator counts the hours alone, unable to be with or without the absent person.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's title is its whole subject: every verse walks into a forest to find the missing person, but the refrain admits the forest is only a stage for a grief that predates it. Rammstein's cruelty here is the pivot from absence to presence, that being without you is unbearable yet being with you left me just as alone, so the loss is not of a partner but of the connection that partner never truly gave. The winter-forest imagery, birds gone silent, breath grown heavy, the evening throwing a cloth over the land, functions as a body shutting down rather than a landscape, mapping bereavement onto the point where the natural world itself stops. That the final third of the song can only repeat the title, stripped of verse, enacts the counting of hours it describes: a mind that has nothing left to say and cannot stop saying the one thing it has.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"loss that predates the parting, the breath always going to be hard","built":"the refrain admits the absence began before the leaving"},
"rammstein~ohne-dich-beta-version":{"haiku":"Absence hollows the speaker into silence, rendering time and breath meaningless; with the beloved present they remain equally isolated, two separate silences that cannot touch.","opus":"Grief walking back into the woods where someone was last seen, the birds gone silent and breathing gone hard. It captures the paradox of loss, that counting empty hours alone somehow beats the frozen time spent together.","sonnet":"A man walks into the forest where he last saw someone he has lost, and the landscape mirrors his grief — black trees, no birdsong, evening closing like a shroud. The lyric's central paradox lands hard: without you I can't exist, but with you I'm still alone — every second either way is worthless.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~pet-sematary":{"haiku":"Resurrection corrupts the natural order into grotesque violation; the speaker resists being dragged back to repeat life's torment through dark sorcery.","opus":"Rammstein's cover of the Ramones' graveyard horror, trailing Victor into a cursed pet cemetery where the dead come back wrong. Beneath the goblins and rot is a plain refusal: better to stay buried than be dragged back to live it all over again.","sonnet":"The Ramones cover: ancient goblins and warlords rise from ground where the smell of death is all around, and the narrator's only wish is not to be buried in the pet cemetery — not to relive this life again. Horror imagery collapses into a plea against repetition.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~puppe": {"haiku":"A disturbed child, left alone with a doll, tears it apart violently as an outlet, then witnesses their sister's death through a keyhole and feels relief.","opus":"A harrowing account from a child locked in a room while their sister works as a prostitute in the next room, clutching a gifted doll and dosed with medicine; when the child hears a client beat the sister to death, the suppressed horror erupts as ripping the doll's head off, the toy standing in for the violence they can't process.","sonnet":"A child locked in their room while their sister works as a prostitute in the next room — they take their medicine, wait for the sun to go down, and tear the head off their doll to cope with the sounds; at the end, a client kills the sister and the child's mood finally lifts.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein's most disturbing move here is the childlike frame: the diminutive Schwesterlein and the gift of a doll set up a nursery rhyme, then the narrator lets you slowly realize the sister works as a prostitute in the next room while the child is locked in with a doll and sedated (dutifully I take my medicine), the sister’s screams registering beside the evening birdsong as just another night sound. The song's engine is the gap between what the speaker sees and what the speaker can say — he watches through the keyhole as a client beats her dead, and because a child has no language for that horror, the trauma discharges sideways onto the only object he owns: the doll gets its head torn off and its neck bitten through. The refrain's turn is the whole point — in verse it is a symptom (it geht mir nicht gut, I am not well), but after he witnesses the murder it flips to jetzt geht es mir gut, the violence to the doll transformed from helpless distress into the child's only available act of revenge and release. The doll is thus doubly loaded: it is the substitute companion given in place of the absent sister, and by the end it becomes her surrogate corpse, so the destruction is at once grief, rage, and a re-enactment he cannot name. Lindemann builds the terror not through gore but through the innocent register never breaking — the lullaby dam-dam still closes each chorus over what has just been described.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","family & blood","shame & repression"],"means":"violence absorbed at a child's eye level until watching and doing blur","built":"the reversal lands in the closing line: what left the child unwell now feels good"},
"rammstein~pussy": {"haiku":"A blatant, crude sexual proposition where the speaker cannot find sexual partners in Germany and is frustrated by the barriers to casual sexual encounters.","opus":"A crude, satirical take on lust and sex tourism, mixing English and German cliches about Germany, cars, and the autobahn, where the narrator just wants quick, no-strings sex because he can't get laid at home.","sonnet":"A German man abroad, frustrated at being unable to find sexual partners at home, propositions someone in bluntly transactional terms, mixing English come-ons with German wordplay that mocks national stereotypes around cars, sausages, and sexual appetite.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein weaponize the cliché catalogue: Mercedes, Autobahn, Blitzkrieg, Bratwurst and Sauerkraut are the export-Germany a foreigner buys, and the band hands it back with a leer, ventriloquizing the tourist's own leched-up fantasy of the country. The trick is bilingual. The German verses stay coy and formal, courting a pretty Fräulein with mock-gallantry, while the English refrain drops the mask into blunt anatomical propositioning, so the seam between languages becomes the seam between the polite face and the crude want beneath it. Even their own catalogue gets pawned off as merchandise, Reise Reise cited as travel-brochure copy. The closing joke, that the speaker cannot manage this at home in Germany, undercuts the whole strut and admits the swagger was compensation all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","politics & society","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"national self-image and sexual appetite collapsed into the same brochure","built":"war vocabulary and chocolate-box kitsch reach for the same pickup line"},
"rammstein~radio": {"haiku":"Radio becomes a tool of forbidden escape for someone confined and isolated, allowing secret access to outside music and ideas the authorities forbid. Each night the radio carries him away to freedom, where his ears become eyes seeing the world beyond his reach.","opus":"A memory of growing up under a repressive regime (East Germany) where forbidden foreign music was smuggled in nightly by radio; for a couple of hours the listener escaped a world he wasn't allowed to see, hear, or belong to. The deeper read is radio as liberation, ears becoming eyes, music carrying him past every border and fence that reality imposed.","sonnet":"A memory of listening to forbidden foreign radio broadcasts as a child in East Germany — the narrator couldn't belong to the official world, couldn't see or speak or hear what was banned, but every night for a few hours pressed his ear to a shortwave receiver and escaped into music from beyond the border. The radio became his only window to a world outside.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein's usual mode is spectacle and provocation, so the restraint here is the tell: this is a childhood memory of the GDR, sung by a band whose members grew up there, and the aggression is entirely displaced onto the state, never the singer. The song's whole meaning turns on a synaesthetic inversion — my ears become eyes — because a boy forbidden to see, speak, or hear anything outside the wall discovers that a shortwave set (the Weltempfänger, literally a world-receiver) lets him hear what he cannot see, so hearing becomes his only window and the airwaves the only country without borders or fences. That the contraband is merely music (forbidden songs, dangerous foreign notes) is the quiet devastation: it measures how total a prohibition must be before nightly radio smuggled under the covers counts as escape, flight, near-religious ascent onto the back of the music — an hour or two a night of a world he can hear but never reach. Only in the bridge does the verb shift from listening to flying and singing softly into cupped hands — the receiver becomes the boy's own suppressed voice — so the arc moves from being erased to speaking, secretly, at last.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","surveillance & control","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"hearing turned into the only sight left when all visible exits are walled off","built":"the forbidden shortwave signal becomes wings for a nightly flight without borders"},
"rammstein~rammlied": {"haiku":"A collective anthem about unified vision, shared sacrifice, and moving forward together as one force—leaders and followers bound by purpose to transform loneliness into collective strength.","opus":"The band's self-mythologizing anthem, a mock-liturgical summons that casts Rammstein as a legend and a collective forged from flesh and blood. It offers itself as company to the lonely while flexing the tribal, eat-or-be-eaten worldview with deadpan grandeur.","sonnet":"A hymn to the Rammstein collective itself — patience rewarded, legends announced, the band as both leader and companion for the lonely, a unified force of flesh and blood moving in one direction with one goal.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammlied is Rammstein's self-mythologizing overture, and its cleverness lies in delivering an anthem that is almost entirely content-free by design: it welcomes you to a legend, chants the band's own name where a chorus should be, and fills its verses with proverb-shells — some lead, some follow; eat or be eaten; right is what is right — maxims that assert authority while stating nothing. That hollowness is the point, because the song is machinery for forging a crowd: the payoff of the opening promise (wait patiently and you will be rewarded) is not meaning but belonging, the isolated listener told we are here, switch us on. The bridge drops the mask and names the mechanism outright — one path, one goal, one direction, one collective of flesh and blood — martial-liturgical language that Rammstein wields with deliberate provocation, staging the seductive logic of mass unity as spectacle rather than endorsing it. Read straight it is a stadium invocation; read with the irony the band trades in, it is a knowing demonstration of how easily a legend, a beat, and a name repeated enough times can make an audience surrender the self and switch on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"self-mythology that dares the crowd to sing along and manufacture a god","built":"comfort and mass obedience chanted as one creed under the band's own name"},
"rammstein~rammstein":{"haiku":"Images of catastrophic violence—burning people, death children, blood, screaming mothers, mass graves—cycle while the sun shines indifferently on horror.","opus":"The band names itself after the airshow disaster whose imagery this early track channels: a burning human, the smell of flesh, a dying child, all beneath an indifferent shining sun. The minimal, hammering structure lets each atrocity land as bald report. Mass grave, no escape, no birdsong, and still the sun keeps shining, a refrain whose serenity makes the horror colder.","sonnet":"A sparse, percussion-driven roll call of catastrophe: a human burning, a child dying, blood on asphalt, mass graves, screaming mothers. Each image is delivered as a headline against an indifferent sun still shining overhead, the repetition building into industrial elegy.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~rammstein-eskimos-egypt-radio-edit":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic devastation consumes the world—bodies burn, children die, blood coagulates on pavement, mothers scream—yet the sun continues its indifferent arc above the mass grave and silence.","opus":"The band's self-titled provocation, burning bodies and mass graves under an indifferent shining sun, the name chanted like an anthem over catastrophe. Deliberately courting the imagery of disaster and spectacle.","sonnet":"A litany of disaster imagery — a man burning, a child dying, a mass grave, mothers screaming, blood congealing on asphalt — each scene punctuated by the band name as chant and the flat refrain \"die Sonne scheint\" (the sun shines). The indifferent sun is the point: catastrophe happens in full daylight, and the world continues without flinching.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~rein-raus":{"haiku":"Sexual conquest and dominance framed through crude imagery; a rider moves from one conquest to the next without care or connection.","opus":"Rammstein's deliberately crude sex-as-horseback double entendre, the bravado undercut by a punchline of premature, indifferent departure; comedy aimed squarely at male vanity and the ego of the rider.","sonnet":"A German song that uses the extended metaphor of rider and horse to describe sexual intercourse — in and out, deeper deeper, the rider mounts and dismounts with brisk efficiency, apologizing that the ride was short before heading off to other partners.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's mechanical in-out chant is the whole joke and the whole tell: sex reduced to pure reciprocating motion, a single verb pumped past all tenderness until it reads like machinery, which on Mutter's industrial palette is exactly the point. Till Lindemann sustains one governing conceit — rider and mount, key and lock, a door he simply walks through — so that the woman is never a partner but a vehicle, a thing owned and operated, and the elephant threading a needle's eye turns even the boast about size into absurd, self-mocking hyperbole. The bridge feints toward intimacy, deeper and the claim that he feels at home in her skin, only for a thousand elephants to break loose — climax staged as stampede, dissolution rather than closeness. Then the mask drops into what was underneath all along: the ride was short, he apologizes without meaning it, dismounts, and has no time because other horses are waiting to be ridden too — the extended metaphor's real payload, indifference and interchangeability, delivered as a shrug. What sounds like a crude sex song is a portrait of appetite that has emptied the act of everyone in it, including the man performing it.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"bravado punctured by an indifferent departure mid-metaphor","built":"the title's mechanical in-out chant performs the act it describes"},
"rammstein~reise-reise": {"haiku":"Seafarers battle on waves where man and fish are pierced by spears, sinking into darkness. The bloody ritual of sailing and death becomes an endless cycle where weapons strike both human and animal.","opus":"A seafaring metaphor for violence and voyage where spears sink into flesh and the waves bleed and weep, life measured out as brutal labor. Rammstein's title pun on travel and awakening riding a grim maritime dirge.","sonnet":"In German: a sailor's song with violent, sexual undercurrents — spears thrust into men and fish alike, waves weeping quietly, bodies bleeding into the sea. \"Reise Reise\" is a traditional sailor's wake call, but Rammstein layers it with imagery of penetration and death that blurs the erotic and the fatal.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein turns the imperative Reise (arise/journey — a sailor's old wake-up call) into a savage naval theology where the sea itself is the battlefield and every man kills in his own fashion. The song's engine is a single grotesque equation held across every verse: fish and flesh are the same meat, and the spear that pierces a man and the one hurled at a fish are the same violence, so the sailor's daily labor and outright murder become morally indistinguishable — a black soul with no light on the horizon. What moves is the ebb of agency into consequence: the refrain first has the waves weeping quietly, then a spear lodged in their blood, and by the final pass the wound migrates from blood to heart as the wounded thing simply bleeds itself empty on the shore — the abstract cruelty finally given a body that dies. That last image, drained to nothing at the water's edge, is the real journey the title promised: not a voyage out but the slow exsanguination that all this even-handed slaughter was always heading toward.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","death & grief","war & battle"],"means":"the journey promised by the title arrives only as sinking","built":"the sailors' wake-cry doubled as a summons to kill rather than to sail","fnote":"The packaging is a damaged flight recorder captioned Flugrekorder, nicht öffnen; original 2004 pressings hide Flugzeuglärm — 36 seconds of the JAL Flight 123 cockpit voice recording — in the pregap before track 1, and the title track’s sailor frame is a documented Moby-Dick reference."},
"rammstein~rosenrot":{"haiku":"A young man climbs to fetch a rose for his lover but both fall to their deaths, their desire leading to destruction.","opus":"Sung in German and echoing a folk tale, it tells of a girl who demands her beloved climb a mountain to fetch a rose for her, ending when a stone breaks loose and both fall to their deaths.","sonnet":"Reworking the fairy-tale image of the rosebud on the cliff, the song tells of a young man who climbs a dangerous mountain to fetch a red rose for the girl who demands it; a stone gives way and both fall to their deaths — desire exacting its fatal cost.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein hijacks the opening of Goethe's Heidenröslein and swaps the agents: now a girl sees the little rose and sends her lover up a cliff to fetch it. The famous boy-breaks-a-rose line becomes a woman's whim breaking a man. The pre-refrain drills that whim into folk law — she wants it, so it is proper, so it always was and always will be — the grammar of custom laundering cruelty as the natural order. Against it the chorus answers as brutal proverb: dig deep wells for clear water, and deep water is never still. Her desire looks placid on the surface and drowns underneath. So the courtly fable turns into an ascent toward death, the boy climbing in torment until a stone gives under his boot and both fall to the ground. Rosenrot earns its red at last — not the flower, but the color of what ends up at the bottom of the well the chorus kept naming.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"a woman's whim dressed as folk law, desire made into natural order until it kills","built":"the Heidenröslein hijack swaps the agents so the famous boy-breaks-a-rose line becomes a man broken by a woman's caprice"},
"rammstein~roter-sand": {"haiku":"A tragic duel over love ends with the speaker shot dead, bleeding out on red sand. Two guns and a jealous rival lead to death, leaving the lover to another man.","opus":"A duel ballad: a man shot dead over a love his rival never knew was mutual, narrating his own bleeding-out on red sand. Rammstein doing a doomed Western romance, tender and fatalistic at once.","sonnet":"In German: a dying man narrates a duel he lost — he was shot over a woman by a rival who accused him of stealing her love, and she never knew. He bleeds out on red sand, promises broken, white doves feeding on his blood, with a final bitter note that at least his death was good for something.","src":"haiku","fable":"A duel ballad sung entirely from inside the losing man, and the trick is that he narrates his own death without ever winning back the frame: the broken promise of line one turns out to be broken not by faithlessness but by a bullet already lodged in him, so the whole song is spoken past the point of no return. Rammstein set their cruelty in a mock-Western — red sand, two pistols, a rival who was simply faster — and the tenderness curdles the genre, since the rival kills over a theft that is really love; the dying man's last clear thought is not his wound but the transfer of possession, that now she belongs to him. The band's signature move is the euphemism that makes violence intimate: the powder-kiss that one cartridge dies in fuses gunfire and mouth, so the shot that fails and the shot that lands are both figured as love. What lifts it past pastiche is the final turn, where white doves feast on his blood and he consoles himself that at least there is an ending, that he is good for something after all — a beaten man salvaging meaning from being useful only as carrion. It reads as one of the band's least ironic songs precisely because the mask slips: under the operatic Western costume is a plain admission that dying pointlessly, unavenged, is the truth the swagger was hiding.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"honor's mask falls as the duel lands pointless and the dying sinks alone","built":"the stoic Western ballad quietly admits the rival never knew she loved him"},
"rammstein~roter-sand-orchester-version":{"haiku":"Love, betrayal, and deadly passion; bullets become love's inevitable currency between lovers.","opus":"An orchestral, ballad-form outlier in the Rammstein catalogue, telling a doomed duel over love. A man dies in the desert dust, a rival bullet lodged in his chest, killed by a rival who never knew the woman loved the fallen one back. Red sand and white doves frame a tragedy of jealousy, honor, and a love that outlasts the loser of the duel.","sonnet":"A duel over a woman ends with the narrator shot dead in red sand by the man who claimed she was stolen from him — the promise to return broken by a bullet, white doves feeding on the blood, and the bitter acknowledgement that now she belongs to him. Love, honour, and death compressed into two cartridges.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~schwarz":{"haiku":"Blackness and darkness become totality, negating distinction and consuming all light.","opus":"An embrace of night and darkness as the place where the lonely soul finds its real pleasure, where commandments go unseen and the black is drunk in deep. Melancholy reframed as seduction, belonging to the shadows.","sonnet":"Night as sanctuary for the lonely and the sinful: the narrator wraps himself in heaviness at dusk because the bright world offers nothing, and darkness is where the soul gets lost in pleasure — \"trink das Schwarz in tiefen Zügen\" (drink the black in deep draughts). Drinkers, whores, and conspirators belong to the shadows, and that belonging is the song's quiet comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~seemann": {"haiku":"A sailor invites a solitary, suffering person into his boat as autumn arrives, revealing layers of longing, isolation, and the coldness of time passing without connection.","opus":"A melancholic invitation to come into a boat as a storm and night fall, the sailor offering to guide a drifting, lost soul across a cold, endless sea; beneath the metaphor of longing as helmsman lies grief and abandonment, the best sailor left alone in the cold as time stands still and autumn comes, hinting at loss and mortality.","sonnet":"In German, a sailor's invitation to a weeping woman standing alone in autumn — he offers her his boat and his guidance across a cold, borderless sea, but by the end he admits she spoke only of her mother, and ultimately he is left alone in the cold, his offer rejected.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein invert the sea shanty into a seduction toward drowning: the invitation to come aboard is voiced by a helmsman who is longing itself, so the boat that promises rescue from the cold, shoreless sea is the same craft that pulls you under. The autumn imagery does the argument's work — the season that strips the sails taut and sweeps the street empty is also the one where time stands still, love hardens into a mechanism, and warmth burns down like a spent candle. The mask is generosity, the come-in-my-boat refrain; underneath is possessive grief, since the speaker's only real boast is that he was the best sailor of all, better than whoever now holds her hand. The devastating turn is the closing verse, where the mourners speak only of the drowned one's mother while the survivor admits he stays alone anyway — the whole rescue fantasy collapses into a confession that saving her was never possible, and that the cold at the end is his, not hers.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"false gallantry as a lure toward the depths, devotion as the drowner's loneliness","built":"the noble seaman's mask drops when his invitation is the thing pulling them under"},
"rammstein~sehnsucht": {"haiku":"Yearning and longing gnaw like invisible insects, an ache that carries you to phantom lands—Africa, Mexico, paradise—then vanishes like sand through your fingers. The cruelty of longing is that it promises nowhere to rest.","opus":"A haunting evocation of longing (Sehnsucht) as an insatiable, cruel force: the narrator rides a lover's tear across the body and the globe searching for 'last year's snow and sand' that no longer exist, finding happiness nowhere; longing is likened to an insect that stings you unnoticed in sleep, and his roving finger sinks into the ocean before reaching contentment.","sonnet":"Longing — Sehnsucht — is described as a parasite that stings you in your sleep, driving the narrator to pursue impossible geographies (a finger sliding toward Mexico, sinking into the ocean) while acknowledging that happiness exists nowhere and the desire itself is cruel.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein turn the title-word—the untranslatable German for a yearning that has no object—into an anatomy of longing as sickness rather than romance. The verses issue tender-sounding commands that are all impossible: ride your tear to Africa, back to the lioness's womb where the singer was once at home, then hunt between your long legs for last year's snow and sand—each search ending in the flat refusal that none of it is there anymore, longing fixed on a past that has already evaporated. The chorus then names the mechanism: yearning hides like an insect and stings you in your sleep, unfelt until it has done its work, so the finger sliding across a map toward Mexico only drowns in the ocean—every reaching gesture misses its mark. Everything turns on that last figure: desire here is not a destination but a wound you can neither locate nor cure, which is why the outro can only chant the word itself, over and over, as the cruelty it has just diagnosed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"longing as an insect that stings while you sleep, contentment always one place further","built":"two verses rhyme the same failed reach, the title hammered until the word becomes the wound"},
"rammstein~sex": {"haiku":"Physical desire overpowers reason; bodies draw together in mutual hunger, celebrating living fully through sexual connection.","opus":"Rammstein reducing desire to its most animal, unlovely mechanics, arousal as sickness and compulsion rather than tenderness, the carpe-diem chorus turning raw appetite into a grim philosophy of living only once.","sonnet":"A blunt, carnally enthusiastic celebration of sexual desire — the speaker is overwhelmed by lust at the sight of a body and argues that a life lived dissolute is better than not lived at all.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein's usual move is to make sex grotesque, but here the grotesque is the point of arousal, not a subversion of it: the singer looks at the body and feels sick — mir wird schlecht — and that nausea is inseparable from the desire it triggers, the brain crawling into the veins, sirens singing, a fist in the gut. The song builds by tiny escalation rather than reversal — between verses the gaze goes from into the sex to deeper into it, and revulsion hardens into a flat present-tense verdict — so the tenderness of the chorus (mine is yours) is exposed as pure appetite dressed as intimacy. Its carpe-diem slogans — we only live once, we love life, we love love — deliberately curdle into the final we live because sex, reducing the whole humanist vocabulary of loving and living to a single drive, the laugh in the post-refrain confirming the mask has dropped. What looks like a love song is a clinical anatomy of compulsion, and the honesty is in the disgust it refuses to separate from the wanting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"craving that has stopped pretending to be anything nobler than itself","built":"the carpe-diem chorus degrades until living collapses into one barked word"},
"rammstein~sonne":{"haiku":"The sun rises from a person's eyes, hands, and fists, burning and blinding others. The song personifies the sun as an overwhelming force that descends and scorches.","opus":"A crushing hymn to a sun/star that never sets, counted up like a boxing ring countdown, whose light pours from the singer's eyes and fists to burn, blind and slam the listener to the ground. Widely read as being about worship of an overwhelming figure or fame itself, it casts blinding radiance as a force that dominates and physically overpowers rather than merely illuminates.","sonnet":"A dark retelling of Snow White where the singer is the sun — a blazing, burning, overwhelming force that both illuminates and destroys, counting down to the moment it slams down and flattens everything beneath it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The counting is the whole trick: originally commissioned as a ring-walk anthem for boxer Vitali Klitschko, the song keeps a referee's count as its skeleton, and Sonne is what happens when that ten-count gets pointed the wrong way — the world isn't counting up to a triumphant entrance, it's being counted out. So the sun here is no source of warmth but a fist. It shines from the eyes and then, worse, from the hands (die Sonne scheint mir aus den Händen), able to burn and blind, and when it breaks from the fists it lays hot on your face, crushes the breath from the chest, and drops you hard to the ground — deification and beating are the same gesture, the brightest star of all delivered as a knockout blow. The mantra be afraid, be not afraid (fürchtet euch, fürchtet euch nicht) refuses to resolve, and that unresolvable command is the reading: adoration of an overwhelming power that will never set and never fall from the sky is indistinguishable from being flattened by it, which is why the last thing the count says isn't ten but aus — over, out, lights off.","fv":2,"fnote":"The song was written as ring-walk music for boxer Vitali Klitschko — working title Klitschko — but the boxer it was built for never used it; reworked, it became the Mutter single instead.","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder"],"means":"adoration of an overwhelming power indistinguishable from being flattened by it","built":"a boxing referee's ten-count runs as the song's skeleton but pointed toward knockout rather than triumph"},
"rammstein~spiel-mit-mir":{"haiku":"Two brothers trapped alone together engage in intimate play and physical connection that blurs boundaries and offers solace amid isolation.","opus":"A brother in a shared bed coaxing his sibling closer under the guise of a game, the sleepless narrator counting sheep into a black hole while something predatory waits beneath. The nursery-rhyme refrain and the closing father-mother-child chant turn childhood innocence inside out into abuse. Rammstein specialize in exactly this transgressive discomfort, their heavy industrial machinery giving taboo subject matter a deliberately queasy, theatrical weight.","sonnet":"Two brothers sharing a room, one unable to sleep, the other coaxed into a game that dispels the darkness — the imagery hovers between innocent childhood ritual and something that leaves the younger brother's hand aching by morning. The repeated plea to play, framed by the incantation of father-mother-child, gives the loneliness of the night a sinister resolution that no one names directly.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~spieluhr":{"haiku":"A dead child mysteriously resurrected by the cold; rising from the grave to sing eternally, an eerie tale of miraculous horror.","opus":"A grim fairy tale of a child who only pretended to die, buried in wet sand clutching a music box, whose small heart wakes under the first snow and sings from the earth as the box winds itself. A nursery rhyme drifts on the wind while no angel descends and only the rain weeps at the grave, until mourners finally hear the melody and dig the child out. Rammstein, the German industrial-metal band, turn Grimm horror to gorgeous effect, the singing from underground unforgettable.","sonnet":"A child fakes death to be alone and is buried in winter sand with a music box in hand — the frost winds the toy, a melody rises from the earth, and on All Souls' Day the singing is finally heard and the child is dug up and saved. A German fairy tale that holds genuine dread alongside its redemption.","src":"haiku","fable":"The horror engine here is a category error mistaken for mercy: a child only feigns death, its heart stopping for hours, and the adults bury it alive in wet sand with a music box in its hand. Lindemann builds the dread from Germany's own cradle vocabulary — the ride-along rhyme Hoppe hoppe Reiter, sung on the record by a real child, Khira Li, a lullaby that traditionally ends with the rider falling — so the tune that should comfort a child instead becomes the sound of one singing up out of the earth. The wound-up melody in the wind is the song's cruelest image: a mechanical toy the buried child keeps cranking, its cheerfulness the exact measure of a world that will not come. The refrain refuses the rescue theology outright — no angel descends, only the rain weeps at the grave — turning the missing heartbeat into flat statement rather than lament. When the villagers finally exhume the child on Totensonntag, the Sunday of the dead, the ending withholds its own relief: they save the little heart only because the toy betrayed the botched burial, so salvation arrives as an accident of the very object that was supposed to be a grave-good, and the reprise plays on as if nothing were ever undone.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"salvation arrives as an accident of the object meant as a grave-good","built":"a real child's voice sings the lullaby that traditionally ends with the rider falling"},
"rammstein~spring": {"haiku":"A suicidal man on a bridge becomes entertainment for a vengeful mob; ultimately pushed by a vigilante, he becomes a dark spectacle of mob mentality.","opus":"A pitch-black satire of mob cruelty: a man on a bridge who only wanted the view is mistaken for a jumper, and the crowd, hungry for spectacle and even his innards, chants at him to leap; the narrator finally sneaks up and pushes him off, framing the murder grotesquely as deliverance from the mob's disappointment.","sonnet":"A crowd on a bridge collectively chants for a man contemplating suicide to jump — the narrator joins the mob, then reveals the man never intended to jump at all, just wanted a view of the sky. In a final twist, the narrator shoves him off anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein turns the folk cliché of the crowd egging on a jumper into a study of collective bloodlust, and the trick is that the man was never suicidal at all: he only climbed the bridge for the view, to look into the evening sky, and the mob's insistence that he jump literally invents the death it craves. The chant Spring — jump — is fused to Erlöse mich, redeem me, so the crowd frames its appetite for a stranger's destruction as its own salvation, a savior demanded on command (jump into the light, don't disappoint me). Till Lindemann's masterstroke is the final verse's shift into first person: the speaker, one voice in that crowd, sneaks up and kicks the weeping man off the bridge from behind, then converts murder into mercy — redeem him from this disgrace — completing the song's real argument that piety and mob violence share the same vocabulary. The stolen cloud sliding across the sun, cribbed from the man's own overheard lament and repurposed as the killer's cue, seals it: the spectacle needed a corpse, and someone was always going to volunteer to provide one while calling it deliverance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","politics & society","suicide"],"means":"a crowd manufactures tragedy and names its appetite compassion","built":"the command to leap is phrased as mercy so murder holds an alibi"},
"rammstein~stein-um-stein": {"haiku":"A person builds a prison stone by stone to entrap their lover, eliminating escape and forcing eternal captivity through systematic construction.","opus":"A love song shaped as entombment, the possessive lover promising eternal togetherness by literally walling his partner up alive; devotion pushed to its monstrous extreme where never leaving means never being allowed to.","sonnet":"A German song about obsessive romantic imprisonment — the narrator builds a house for the beloved one stone at a time, but there are no windows, no doors, the person stands naked with feet in cement, and no one will hear them scream.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein's most notorious sleight of hand: the song wears the language of devotion — I have plans, I'm building you a house, I'll always be with you — while every promise is really a description of entombment, the house a windowless crypt with no door and no light, the beloved cemented alive into its foundation. The horror deepens because the two vocabularies never separate: to be walled in, stone by stone, is offered as the ultimate fidelity, the killer's I will always be with you meaning precisely that neither of you can ever leave. The bridge tips the register from mason to something worse, the hammering of nails not into timber but into your body's wood (Leibholz), so the victim isn't merely buried but crucified into the structure. And the refrain's engineered slip seals it — what began as I'll always be with you decays into and no one hears you scream, love's grammar quietly rewriting itself into murder's while the tender tone never once breaks.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"the loving register held steady as the mortar seals her into the wall","built":"every tender vow curdles because the home being built is a tomb"},
"rammstein~stirb-nicht-vor-mir-don-t-die-before-i-do": {"haiku":"Longing for an unknowable lover who exists beyond waking. The speaker waits and pleads that the beloved not die before them, binding passion to death and dream.","opus":"A duet of loneliness in which a man waiting in the cold night longs for someone to love him, sure that person exists somewhere. A woman describes a nightly dream lover whose hands are at her throat, and both plead: don't die before I do.","sonnet":"Two voices—one in German, one in English—long for an unknown beloved they have never met but feel certain exists: the German voice waits in loneliness while snow covers the streets, the English voice dreams of a suffocating but irresistible presence each night. Both share the same plea: do not die before I find you.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is a duet split by language, and that split is the whole design: two lovers who have never met, singing past each other across a gap the arrangement makes audible. Till Lindemann's German verses are a lonely male vocalist whose night gives birth to a child named loneliness, while the guest female voice (Sharleen Spiteri) answers in English about a nocturnal visitor whose hands close around her throat as she surrenders — desire and death indistinguishable, a small ecstatic dying repeated each night. The title's plea, don't die before I do, is the only bridge between them: not a love promise but a bargained fear, each begging the other to outlast so neither has to survive the loss alone. The image-system stacks the two aloneness against the world's coupled warmth — snowed-in houses, candlelight, others lying two-by-two — so that the refrain's waiting becomes the song's real subject, a vigil for someone unknown and possibly nonexistent. What looks like romance is closer to a duet of solitudes agreeing, tenderly and morbidly, on the order in which they will be abandoned.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"First recorded as an all-German duet with Bobo, the Engel voice; Sharleen Spiteri replaced her for Rosenrot, but Bobo’s backing vocals are still audible in the mix.","themes":["love & desire","death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"love defined entirely as the dread of outliving the beloved","built":"two languages split the vigil but share one equation of surrender with dying"},
"rammstein~stripped":{"haiku":"A lover invites escape from urban toxins and technological mediation into nature and pure presence. The song desires witnessing the other person stripped of societal roles and free from media influence.","opus":"Rammstein's cover peels back modern life, the narrator coaxing a companion out into nature to lie in the open and let time drift, far from exhaust and screens. The city can't compete with this stolen afternoon that belongs entirely to them. The repeated wish to see the other undressed and hear them think and speak away from any screen turns bareness into a craving for genuine, unfiltered presence.","sonnet":"An escape from urban noise and media saturation into nature — grass, trees, hours unhurried — where two people might finally be honest with each other, unmediated by screens or performance. The longing is for bare authenticity, a voice that speaks only for him.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~tattoo": {"haiku":"Tattoos mark the body with images and names of loved ones, immortalizing them in flesh despite inevitable separation or death.","opus":"Rammstein on the body as a written page and the pain that beauty demands, ink kissing blood in the skin. The childlike trade of showing marks turns darker at the lover's name, permanent devotion shadowed by its own replaceability.","sonnet":"A German meditation on tattoos as permanent autobiography — the body as living letter, ink as both wound and love; the singer even tattoos a lover's name knowing that if they leave, he'll just find someone with the same name.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tattoo turns body modification into a covenant that outlasts flesh, then quietly corrupts that vow. The opening conceit is that the skin is a letter and the words burn into the paper of the body — the tattoo as text one is condemned to carry, loud and legible where softer speech fails. Rammstein's refrain welds pain to intimacy in the band's signature register, blood kissing the ink and pain embracing the flesh, so the needle's blue flood reads as consummation rather than injury: to be marked is to be loved, and beauty demands suffering as its price. The bridge is where the tenderness curdles — vowing to needle your name in so you stay here forever, then adding that if you divide us, he will simply find someone else who happens to be called the same. That swerve exposes the whole thing: the eternity a tattoo promises is loyalty to a mark, not a person, and the final refrain's slippage from showing my skin to loving my skin confirms that the true and permanent object of devotion was always the self.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","identity & becoming","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"devotion's permanence outlasting the feeling it was marked there to fix","built":"the bridge admits a name pricked in forever can be reassigned to the next person","fnote":"The line inverts the proverb Wer schön sein will, muss leiden — whoever wants to be beautiful must suffer — into whoever must be beautiful wants to suffer; Zick Zack quotes the proverb straight."},
"rammstein~te-quiero-puta": {"haiku":"A sexually charged love song that objectifies and desires a woman's body while explicitly disclaiming emotional attachment, celebrating physical pleasure and transgressive desire. The Spanish lyrics emphasize raw sensuality and carnal intent.","opus":"A deliberately crude mariachi-flavored provocation, Rammstein playing a lecherous suitor demanding only the body and not the heart. The Spanish framing and lurid double-meaning are pure calculated shock, sex as theater.","sonnet":"A Spanish-language song expressing raw, purely physical desire — the narrator wants a woman's body and pleasure, explicitly disclaiming any interest in her heart, repeating declarations of lust and demands for more.","src":"haiku","fable":"The come-on is built as a systematic amputation: the singer wants the sabor, the limón, the fruta — everything edible and detachable — while barring the corazón each time it surfaces, so desire is defined by exactly what it refuses to include. Rammstein's usual German menace is displaced into faux-tourist Spanish, the tenderness of mi amor colliding with the title's slur, and Carmen Zapata's warm female responses turn the transaction into a duet rather than a monologue, complicity performed as seduction. The bridge is where the mask slips: between your legs I am going to cry, happy and sad I am going to be — the buyer confesses a grief the swagger exists to bury, then instantly reseals it with the mechanical más, más, más and the reassurances that name the fear they claim to soothe. What resists reading is the escalation itself: the song ends unable to say anything but its command, the imperative replacing feeling, which is precisely the joke and the horror — a man who has purchased pleasure and finds only appetite where a heart was ruled out from the first verse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"cruelty passing as flirtation beneath borrowed festive sweetness","built":"protestations of not wanting love keep giving the want away"},
"rammstein~tier": {"haiku":"A man who cannot distinguish human from animal commits incest with his daughter, making him a beast, while the mother documents her trauma.","opus":"A brutal confrontation with incest and abuse, indicting the father who can't tell human from animal and the daughter left writing letters in her own blood. Rammstein forces the listener to name the beast without flinching.","sonnet":"Incest framed as a question of what separates humans from animals — a father rapes his daughter and is called a beast; the daughter later writes herself a letter in his blood, processing the childhood abuse through the only language left to her.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein's most airless taboo song works by withholding the very judgment its refrain pretends to deliver: the chant that you are only an animal sounds like condemnation, but it is also the incest abuser's alibi, nature as excuse, and the band lets both meanings ride the same three-note bark. The two verses split the crime by point of view — the first flatly narrates the father who cannot tell human from beast and mates with his own flesh and blood, the second hands the mic to the grown daughter, and that shift is the whole song. Where the man is all instinct, she takes up a pen and dips it in his blood to write soulless lines back to her own childhood, so that language itself, the thing that supposedly separates us from the animal, becomes her only way to bear witness — and it too runs on his blood. The interrogation of what he does, feels, and is turns on its axis across the track: aimed outward it accuses him, but by the looping close, with Tier hissed inside the questions like an intrusive thought, it curdles into her own contaminated self-address, and the last pass breaks off before the word can even land, the verdict left hanging unfinished. The song's cruelty is that it never grants her an exit from the category he assigned her.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["family & blood","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"the accusation of being animal pressed onto survivor as well as abuser","built":"the sentence breaks off before it can name the daughter, leaving the naming unfinished"},
"rammstein~waidmanns-heil": {"haiku":"A hunter stalks and kills animals in the forest with deliberate ritual, celebrating the kill as creatures must die under the hunter's aim.","opus":"A hunting song where the chase drips with sexual heat, the language of stalking and the kill barely masking predation. Rammstein's provocation lies in how completely bloodlust and arousal fuse, the creature simply destined to die.","sonnet":"A hunting song that doubles as sexual metaphor — the narrator stalks a female deer through the forest with language drawn from traditional German hunting ritual, and the kill is rendered with unmistakable erotic charge.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein turns hunting jargon into a machine of pure predation: the song is built almost entirely from real Waidmannssprache (German hunters' cant) — Kahlwild, Blattschuss, the clean shot behind the shoulder — so that the ritual vocabulary itself becomes the alibi, technical correctness standing in for conscience. The genius is the double coding: every term reads as legitimate venery and as sexual pursuit at once, the rutting heat of the opening, the deer sunning itself, the flicking tail, until the two hunts are indistinguishable and the salute Weidmanns Heil (good hunting) doubles as a stammered predator's cheer. The refrain's flat verdict — the creature must die — refuses any pastoral consolation: on land and sea, only ruin lies in wait. The bridge is the reveal, cutting to the prey's point of view as she feels the muzzle's energy and a fine sweat beads on the knee, so the accumulated euphemism resolves into a body absorbing a shot. What looks like folk pageantry is a study of desire and killing sharing one language, and Rammstein lets the mask stay on precisely so we notice what it hides.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","death & grief"],"means":"predation wearing the sanctioned costume of traditional sport","built":"the hunter's greeting turns slaughter into a communal blessing","fnote":"In hunters’ cant Schweiß means the game’s blood, not sweat — the bridge’s fine Schweiß dripping on the knee is, in the song’s own jargon, the shot animal already bleeding."},
"rammstein~was-ich-liebe":{"haiku":"Everything beloved must be destroyed or die, treating joy and love as cursed destinies that bring suffering to oneself and others.","opus":"A bleak logic of preemptive loss: because everything loved carries the seed of its own ruin, the speaker resolves to renounce joy itself, refusing to love, to like, even to feel glad. Rammstein render this as grim self-protection, since whoever he loves is doomed. The vow to destroy what he cherishes before it can be taken turns tenderness into a liability to be executed.","sonnet":"A speaker who has concluded that loving anything guarantees its destruction — and his own suffering. Rather than risk loss, he has chosen pre-emptive renunciation: refusing joy, refusing attachment, accepting a cold self-sufficiency because everything he loves is destined to perish.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~weisses-fleisch": {"haiku":"Dark eroticism and violence intertwine as pale skin becomes both attraction and instrument; the speaker recognizes inherited depravity passed through generations.","opus":"A schoolyard killer's confession, loneliness curdled into predatory violence and staged with deliberate provocation. Naming his father as the same, it frames the horror as inherited and beyond any salvation.","sonnet":"A rapist and murderer narrates his assault of a schoolgirl — her pale skin excites him, her screams fuel him, and he strangles her with it, concluding there is no God where his soul is headed; he acknowledges his father was the same.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein's early provocation works by refusing the exculpatory move a murder ballad usually offers: the killer names his loneliness as sickness, then converts that self-diagnosis into license rather than remorse, so the sob for rescue in the final verse arrives already knowing it will go unanswered. The genius of the horror is the gigolo refrain, which insists the violence is really appetite dressed as seduction, an obscene commercial transaction where the victim's terror is priced as arousal. Inheritance seals it: naming the father as an identical predator turns a private atrocity into a bloodline, so the closing possessive claim lands not as passion but as ownership passed down like an heirloom.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","family & blood","shame & repression"],"means":"inherited compulsion named and then the seducer's costume dropped entirely","built":"the gigolo vocabulary keeps slipping to expose the assault underneath"},
"rammstein~weit-weg":{"haiku":"Voyeuristic longing and fantasy unrequited by distance; the speaker watches and desires from across an unbridgeable gap.","opus":"A portrait of longing and voyeurism, a man pressing his face to a windowpane hoping his fantasy will leave the light on and drop her clothes, his heart racing as he paints her image and holds a sun in his hand, forever both very close and impossibly far. From Rammstein's Untitled album, it renders obsessive distance as something almost cosmic, tenderness and trespass uneasily fused.","sonnet":"A voyeur at the window building an entire inner life from glimpses of a woman who never undresses except in his imagination — glasses off, trembling, he watches moonlight do the work his presence cannot, and the closeness he feels is measured precisely by how far away he truly is. The distance is both literal and the condition of his desire.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~wiener-blut":{"haiku":"A figure invites others into darkness and isolation beneath the surface, promising intimacy and eternity in solitude and sadness.","opus":"Sung in German, it depicts a predatory captor luring a victim into a hidden underground room, welcoming them into darkness and imprisonment under the guise of pleasure and companionship.","sonnet":"A captor lures victims — framed as children — into a cellar beneath his house, promising play and paradise while sealing them in darkness forever; the song depicts imprisonment and abuse through the voice of a predator who presents himself as protector.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title borrows a Strauss waltz and pins it to the 2008 Fritzl case, the Austrian father who caged his daughter in a cellar for twenty-four years and fathered her children there. The whole surface is thus a seduction ventriloquized by a monster, opening as courtly invitation, a promise of paradise beneath the house. Every luxurious phrase is a euphemism turned inside out: the basement pleasure is a jail, the extinguished light is a door sealing shut, the tender vow to give the captive a sibling the most sickening admission of what the imprisonment breeds. Singing in German lets Rammstein hold the mask on longer than English could, the greeting into blackness reading like a lover's threshold until you register it as the literal dark of a windowless room. The savage genius is the outro, which drops the leer and lifts the cadence of Psalm 23, fearing no evil through the valley. The captor thereby annexes the voice of God as comforter, his possessive embrace becoming at once scriptural consolation and the exact grammar of a man who will never release his grip.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","violence & murder","family & blood","faith & the occult"],"means":"a monster ventriloquizing courtly seduction until the psalm arrives","built":"the strauss waltz title pins a war-crime to a dance, german holding the disguise longer","fvr":"2.4"},
"rammstein~wo-bist-du": {"haiku":"A German meditation on a lost love—beauty and warmth fade, time stops, and laughter dies; the speaker searches everywhere for the absent person ('where are you').","opus":"In German, the song charts love curdling into its absence, where beauty and warmth turn cold and clocks stop, as the speaker desperately searches everywhere for a vanished lover and falls asleep clutching a knife.","sonnet":"The German lyrics trace a speaker's grief after losing someone's love: the world has gone cold and joyless, all clocks have stopped, and he searches everywhere — behind light, under every stone — asking where the absent person has gone, unable to sleep without a knife.","src":"haiku","fable":"The gutting joke is the opening: a lover's declaration disassembled clause by clause into its own negation, then hedged into a bitter equation of who loved whom less — grief performed as pedantic bookkeeping because the speaker can no longer feel the thing directly. That numbness becomes the whole world in the verses, where beauty, warmth, and time themselves fail (pretty girls aren't pretty, warm hands are cold, the clocks stop), a depression rendered as physics rather than emotion. The searching intensifies through escalating absurdity — behind the light, under every stone — until the refrain's question stops being addressed to a person and becomes the unanswerable void itself, and the final line resolves it in the darkest register Rammstein reaches: he falls asleep clutching a knife, the search collapsing into either the dream of reunion in death or the readiness to make one. What looks like a love song is a suicide note written in the grammar of loss.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","madness & the mind","violence & murder"],"means":"grief curdling inward toward the knife rather than reunion","built":"love stripped clause by clause until only its negation remains"},
"rammstein~wollt-ihr-das-bett-in-flammen":{"haiku":"Violence and sexuality intertwined as metaphor for war and domination, innocence destroyed by blood-soaked eros.","opus":"An early Rammstein provocation equating sex with battle and love with war. Taunting listeners who secretly crave the dagger and the blood, it exposes the violence lurking beneath supposed innocence and asks where all the dead come from. The chanted band name turns the whole thing into a ritual, confronting the audience with their own appetite for destruction.","sonnet":"Sex framed as war and war framed as sex — the audience is invited to watch the bed in flames, to plunge daggers into sheets and lick blood from blades. 'Sex ist eine Schlacht, Liebe ist Krieg' (sex is a battle, love is war) names the equation directly; innocence is a cross on a pillow, and the dead come from somewhere.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~wollt-ihr-das-bett-in-flammen-sehen": {"haiku":"Sex and love are equated with warfare, violence, and bloodshed, depicting intimate contact as mutual stabbing and death. The song frames desire as destructive conflict.","opus":"The song taunts the listener with violent, erotic imagery, asking if they want the bed in flames and to lick blood from a blade, equating sex with battle and love with war. It links desire, violence, and death, asking where all the dead come from.","sonnet":"The song addresses an audience directly with a string of provocative questions about desire for violent, transgressive sex, and then reduces everything to the blunt declaration: sex is a battle, love is war. The rhetoric implicates the listeners in their own appetite for destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"The taunting refrain that gives Rammstein their debut its manifesto — asking a crowd whether it wants to see the bed in flames, to go under in skin and hair — is built as a rhetorical trap, and everything turns on the pronoun: every accusation is aimed outward at you, the listener who supposedly craves the knife in the sheets and the blood licked from the blade, so that the band's own violence is reframed as a desire it merely names in its audience. The second verse springs the trap, moving from seduction to guilt — you imagine innocence is yours to kiss, you think killing must be hard, but then where do all the dead come from? — turning collective denial into shared complicity. The bridge states the equation the rest only implies, that sex is a battle and love is war, and the fact that the song's one real word after that is the band chanting its own name is the point: the mask of erotic provocation and the thing underneath — a hunger for domination dressed as a party anthem — are deliberately the same gesture, self-mythologizing and accusatory at once.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Among the band’s first songs, played in 1994 under the working title Der Bringer; the track weaves in sound effects from Doom — the Doom Guy’s screams and a shotgun reload.","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","artifice & performance"],"means":"audience conscripted into owning the appetite they were accused of having","built":"the provocation phrased as questions throws the craving back at the listener"},
"rammstein~zeig-dich": {"haiku":"Accusations of hypocrisy in religious institutions: preaching forgiveness while perpetrating violence, abuse of children, and the corruption hidden behind faith.","opus":"A furious indictment of the Church, its Latin liturgy warped into a chant while the band catalogues clerical hypocrisy and abuse. The plea for God to appear goes unanswered, the sky turning red instead of any angel arriving.","sonnet":"A demand that God reveal himself — the German text catalouges every contradiction and crime committed in the name of religion (burning, persecution, child abuse, contraception bans, promises of forgiveness) and then screams for God to show his face while the sky turns red and no angel appears.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is built from German verbs prefixed with ver-, a prefix that spoils or negates, and Rammstein weaponizes the grammar itself: promise, forgiveness, multiplication, all done in the name of the Lord, until the same conjugating machine slides without warning into a crime against children committed as if by accident. That formal continuity is the thesis — abomination is not a rupture in the institution but another word in its paradigm, produced by the identical liturgical engine. The mock-Latin chant framing it (nonsense words dressed as ecclesiastical Latin, crucifixus half-audible inside them) exposes the ritual as sound without meaning, ceremony that has forgotten what it consecrates. So the repeated cry to show yourself is an accusation aimed upward: we are losing the light, no angel comes in need, and the refusal is answered not by a face but by a sky staining red — no God appears, only the color of judgment or blood where a revelation should be.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"the plea for god to reveal himself answered only by an emptying sky","built":"mock-Latin choir frames abuse as liturgy while the real prayer demands a face"},
"rammstein~zeit":{"haiku":"Lamenting the irreversible flow of time; the inability to stop perfect moments from fading and slipping away forever.","opus":"A German meditation on time's cruelty: we cast shadows without light, dying even as we live, driven toward the end with no rest, begging time to stand still while lying in a lover's arms because the perfect moment always passes. Rammstein trade their usual provocation for solemn grandeur on this title track, quoting Goethe's Faust in the wish for a beautiful moment to linger, mortality rendered with unexpected melancholy.","sonnet":"A German-language meditation on time as a river no one can leave — perfect moments dissolve the instant they arrive, the warm body grows cold, the future resists prophecy. The speaker lies in someone's arms wishing the moment could last forever and knows already it cannot. Zeit is both the title and the one word that cannot be stopped: time, please stand still, just for this.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~zerst-ren": {"haiku":"German song invoking the compulsion to destroy things that don't belong to the speaker; twisted desire wars with wishes for goodness, culminating in a tragic tale of a blind girl and resentful abuse.","opus":"A darkly comic confession of a compulsion to destroy: the narrator wants to tear, smash, saw and behead things, but crucially only what doesn't belong to him. The deeper read is the war between wanting to be a good boy and an ungovernable urge, capped by a bitter closing parable where a blind girl he pitied regains sight and immediately leaves him.","sonnet":"About the compulsion to destroy things that don't belong to you — the narrator walks past fences fighting an irresistible urge to smash, rip, and burn, wanting to be good but unable to resist; the coda reveals a blind girl who briefly gained sight and immediately left the man who wished it for her.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Turkish lullaby opens the door — my darling, don't cry, I can't bear it — and Rammstein spend the whole song showing you the tenderness that plea is powerless against. The verses build a taxonomy of ruin out of ver- and zer- prefixed verbs, and the compulsion is scrupulously moral: it must destroy, yet it must not own the thing it destroys, so the drive is aimed outward at other people's belongings, escalating to the child's game of pulling a doll's head off named as a supreme discipline. The bridge splits the mask open — I want to be a good boy, but the craving catches up with me — so what emerges isn't a monster but a will helplessly watched by its owner. Then the outro drops the games entirely: a blind girl, a shared and kindred suffering, a wish on a falling star that she could see — and the cruelty of the turn is that the wish is granted and she opens her eyes only to leave him that same night, because the one thing this destroyer cannot bear is to be truly seen. The song's genius is structural: it lets you laugh at the cartoon vandalism for four minutes, then reveals the compulsion was always a plea to remain unwitnessed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"destruction as the only language left when tenderness guarantees loss","built":"the wrecking has one strict rule: only what isn't his can be smashed"},
"rammstein~zick-zack": {"haiku":"A German-language song satirizing cosmetic surgery and body modification obsession. The narrative catalogs surgical procedures and the pain endured for beauty standards and rejuvenation.","opus":"A biting satire of cosmetic surgery and vanity, cataloguing implants, liposuction, Botox, and cutting in graphic detail, with the refrain that beauty demands suffering as time relentlessly ticks toward old age.","sonnet":"A satirical inventory of cosmetic surgery procedures — breast implants, liposuction, Botox, facelifts, foreskin removal, gender reassignment — delivered as a gleeful chant that beauty demands pain and time is running out.","src":"haiku","fable":"Rammstein weaponizes the imperative-catalogue mode they built their whole career on — the barked comparatives of the frame (prettier, bigger, harder, tighter) are advertising copy and fascist body-ideal in one breath, and the song simply lets a cosmetic surgeon read out an itemized procedure list until the accumulation itself becomes the horror. The genius is the pun in the two ticking refrains: zick-zack (snip-snip, the scissors) slides into tick-tack (the clock), so that every cut chasing a younger face is revealed as a race the body is already losing — your time is slowly running out. Beneath the satire of vanity runs the band's signature turn to the genitals, and here it sharpens into real cruelty: the offhand line that if the woman inside the man is unhappy, then everything off, of course, drops the beauty-clinic frame to name a self-mutilation the culture euphemizes as choice — the mask of consumer freedom lifted off the knife. What lands hardest is that the song never editorializes; it recites, deadpan, the folk wisdom that beauty demands suffering, and lets the operating theater's needle-thread-scissors-light do the arguing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Their fourth German number one, after Pussy, Deutschland and Zeit; launched with a fake Berlin beauty clinic — hotline greeting, post-op band portraits, the lyric as slogan — and a video shot at Warsaw’s Sabat Theatre with the band in sagging surgical prosthetics.","themes":["death & grief","artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"self-improvement as a losing race against a countdown that never stops","built":"the scalpel's zick-zack keeps sliding into the tick-tack of a clock racing death"},
"rammstein~zick-zack-rmx-by-boys-noize":{"haiku":"Surgical mutilation and body modification become the coercive price of beauty and youth, with society enforcing grotesque standards through pain and consumption.","opus":"A grotesque catalog of cosmetic surgery as the ticking clock of aging drives the vanity, cut it off, suck it out, inject it in. The refrain that you are getting old and your time is running out exposes the futile war against mortality behind the scalpel.","sonnet":"A surgical theater of vanity: a narrator — equal parts cosmetic surgeon and satirist — runs down every procedure available to the body-anxious, from silicone to Botox to penile exposure, under the refrain \"who wants to be beautiful must also suffer.\" The ticktock urgency (\"du wirst alt\") frames it as a race against a clock that always wins.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~zick-zack-rmx-by-boys-noize-instrumental":{"haiku":"Cosmetic surgery and body modification have become a compulsive, painful ritual where beauty demands mutilation and the ageing body is systematically dismantled to satisfy impossible ideals. Vanity breeds suffering as an endless cycle of cutting, injecting, and reshaping in pursuit of youth and social acceptance.","opus":"A savage satire of cosmetic surgery and the beauty industry, bodies sliced and injected and vacuumed while the clock ticks down toward age anyway. Vanity as self-mutilation, the knife promising a youth time already steals.","sonnet":"A surgical satire of cosmetic obsession — the narrator rattles off every procedure (breast implants, Botox, rib removal, liposuction) in the clipped cadence of a checklist, underscored by the ticking refrain \"ticktack, du wirst alt\": you're aging, so cut it off. Beauty as mutilation, vanity as self-erasure, all delivered without a breath of sympathy.","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~zwitter":{"haiku":"A being that is self-sufficient and complete within itself, containing both genders and finding love and fulfillment within its own dual nature.","opus":"Sung from the perspective of a hermaphrodite who is both sexes in one body and joyfully in love with himself, able to please and even impregnate himself without needing anyone else. The German lyrics revel in this self-sufficiency, kissing his own reflection and shrugging off insults.","sonnet":"A celebration of being intersex or dual-gendered — the speaker describes being both sexes merged into one, self-sufficient and self-contained, capable of self-love and self-fertilisation, and delighted not to need anyone else.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hermaphrodite love song where the beloved is the self, and Till Lindemann rides the pun for all its menace and comedy. The premise escalates coldly — a stolen kiss, two lovers fused into one mass, then two souls and two sexes housed under one chest with a single lust — and from there self-sufficiency curdles into narcissism as doctrine: he sends himself roses, kisses his mirror-image at dawn and falls asleep with himself at night, until the boast turns literal and grotesque, that while the others went looking for girls he could already fertilize himself. The chorus is the whole thesis in arithmetic — one for me, one for you doesn't exist; one and one, that is me — a closed loop so airtight that even being told to fuck off leaves him unbothered. The joke and the horror are the same thing: perfect completeness is also perfect isolation.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","comedy & wordplay","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"self-sufficiency perfected until it becomes perfect isolation","built":"the arithmetic chorus reduces desire to a closed equation with one term"},
"rarity~leave-it-alone":{"opus":"Slow suffocation under apathy, the narrator watching himself vanish and finding only a cold, blank stare where meaning should be. Rarity's pop-punk unease frames life as a conspiracy quietly killing him, everything he values amounting to nothing. The dread is of disappearing entirely, with nowhere to go and no words left.","sonnet":"About being trapped in apathy so complete that the narrator cannot muster the will to leave a situation they know is destroying them — everything they have built has amounted to nothing, and the inability to move or speak leaves them stranded in a cold, featureless despair.","src":"sonnet"},
"rarity~passenger":{"opus":"A portrait of hollow, dependent existence, feeling like a ghost and a burdensome weight chained to someone else's life; the singer, supported only by others and clinging to his own mind, finds peace with his head in the clouds as a mere passenger watching life through glass.","sonnet":"A person who exists as a ghost sustained entirely by others — not a ruler but a supported passenger, spending the whole day inside their own head — wants to stop being a dead weight on the chain of someone else's life while simultaneously fearing what they'd become without them.","src":"sonnet"},
"ratbag~dead-end-kids":{"haiku":"Marginalized youth find kinship in shared alienation from respectable society.","opus":"Bratty, surreal punk about working-class kids with no future getting high with their favorite weirdos, seething at the moneyed men in a class war. A branch sprouting from her tongue and a freeloader squatting rent-free in her head make it a scream against being written off.","sonnet":"About growing up broke and written off — the narrator identifies with the losers on the wrong side of the class divide, mocking the \"high end men\" who wage economic war while the dead-end kids smoke and wait; there's also a thread about a critical parent whose voice has been living rent-free in the narrator's head.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~achtung-panzer":{"haiku":"Tank warfare and military destruction; metal beasts bringing death and violent devastation across burning ground.","opus":"A Swedish martial ode to armored tanks as beasts of war, with veins pumping diesel and pores sweating blood; the tanks roll out in tight formations, burning the ground and bringing thunderous, fiery death across the battle zone.","sonnet":"A Swedish industrial metal song glorifying the tank as an instrument of war — steel storms, diesel veins, blood-sweating pores, and armored battalions dominate the battlefield in tight formations, with destruction framed as an overwhelming force of nature.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is Swedish industrial-metal pageantry that borrows a German drill command (attention, tank) to march an armored column across the horizon as pure mythic force. The verses run entirely on an elemental welding of the mechanical to the natural: storm-clouds and steel-weather at the horizon, veins that pump diesel and pores that sweat blood, TNT and death joined in a violent wedding on caterpillar treads. There is no character and no argument here, and reading it means naming what the refusal does, the song strips war of politics and casualty down to a black-bereted Death enthroned over the battle zone, worshipping the tank as a thunder-god. The mask is heroic bombast; underneath is nothing but the machine, which is the honest and chilling point.","fv":2,"themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","nature & the elements"],"means":"war stripped to mechanical force with politics and casualty removed","built":"the German drill command as title fuses a foreign authority with Swedish industrial pageantry"},
"raubtier~apokalyps":{"haiku":"Apocalypse riders race through snow and death toward annihilation; the speaker burns with adrenaline and fire, embracing doom and violent speed toward inevitable ending.","opus":"The Swedish metal act rides an apocalyptic death-drive, its riders borne on snow through pale valleys with no fear of dying and no road back. Adrenaline, blood, gasoline and psychosis fuse into a headlong charge toward the abyss, speed elevated to sole law. Greeting Satan with a grin, the narrator embraces annihilation as glory, the end sealed and eagerly met.","sonnet":"A Swedish-language ode to riding toward annihilation at full throttle — adrenaline, exhaust fumes, and reckless speed as a form of ecstatic surrender. The apocalypse is not feared but beckoned, the riders drunk on noise and velocity, greeting oblivion with a grin.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~besten-i-mig":{"haiku":"A destructive beast dwells within the narrator, an uncontrollable violent impulse that causes harm and ruins relationships through a dysfunctional symbiosis that benefits neither.","opus":"Swedish industrial-metal band Raubtier voice a savage inner beast fed by drink, blood turned to poison and reason to wild fury. Wolf-mad, rabid and shameless, the creature seizes control of step and hand, waking the narrator in trails of smoke, ash and blood. A dysfunctional symbiosis binds them in mutual hatred, addiction dramatized as a possessing monster dragging its host toward ruin.","sonnet":"A Swedish speaker describes the beast that inhabits him — a drunk, rageful, shameless alter ego that surfaces when blood turns to poison — and the dysfunctional symbiosis that makes separation impossible. They hate each other, man and beast, yet each is locked in the other's grip; the cost is counted in apologies, iron, tears, and everything that should have been left undone.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~det-finns-bara-krig":{"haiku":"A warrior's existence defined by ceaseless conflict and suffering, with only pain and struggle constituting life's fundamental nature.","opus":"Sung in Swedish, it portrays a warrior's grim existence in which there is only war and life is struggle, with each breath and heartbeat marked by rage, pain and an eternal, deadly vigil.","sonnet":"The song describes the warrior's existence as an unending state of war: every dawn brings the sound of battle, every heartbeat is a skirmish, and the only fate waiting at the end of the path of hardship is a dark grave beneath a blood-red sky.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a closed metaphysical proposition set to industrial metal, and it earns none of its length through development because refusing to develop is the argument. The title thesis, there is only war, is stated, elaborated once, then hammered call-and-response until it stops being a claim and becomes the only weather the song permits. Raubtier's Norrland archaism folds every dawn, breath and heartbeat into combat, so ordinary living is redefined as war by grammatical fiat rather than depicted. The arc from morning's battle-cry to a blood-red sky over black seas draping the warrior's grave promises no victory, only that struggle ends in the grave and boots outlast the man. The chosen mask is the eternal soldier on perpetual watch, and there is nothing beneath it to unmask; that flatness is the honesty, war as a law of existence, refusing to argue past the assertion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle"],"means":"war as a law of existence refused rather than argued, stated until it becomes the only weather","built":"the call-and-response hammers the title thesis until the claim stops being a claim and the repetition is the proof"},
"raubtier~dieseld-den":{"haiku":"A suicide manifesto where the narrator plans mass destruction through poisoning water and oil before taking his own life, driven by vengeance against those he perceives as enemies. The song describes a final apocalyptic revenge fantasy.","opus":"A vengeance-driven poisoner plots to contaminate water and set oil ablaze so his enemies die of thirst and fire, craving recognition as the terrorists' terrorist, then drinks his own deadly brew of turpentine and diesel to die alongside them.","sonnet":"A Swedish industrial-metal track narrating a revenge fantasy in which the speaker, driven by hatred, plans to poison the water supply and set fire to the oil — becoming the ultimate terrorist — before realizing his scheme will kill everyone including himself, and so he drinks his own deadly brew.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~dit-vinden-b-r":{"haiku":"A wanderer travels endlessly, separated from a beloved who waits like the North Star, pulled by wind and fate while missing someone across a vast distance.","opus":"A Swedish lament of the wanderer's life, every night a new city and a new face, wild nights leaving their marks until the years slip away, the narrator forever driven where the wind carries him yet aching for a love that feels light-years off, guided home only by the North Star. Raubtier are a Swedish metal band known for martial, military-tinged imagery, and here that hardened road-worn masculinity softens into genuine longing.","sonnet":"A nomad's love song, built on the ache of a life lived in perpetual transit — new cities, strange faces, accumulating years of separation. The north star works as a metaphor for the person left behind: fixed, reliable, loved precisely because it never moves while the speaker is driven wherever the wind carries him.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~dobermann":{"haiku":"Ode to absolute loyalty and vigilant devotion, using Dobermann imagery. The speaker promises eternal guardianship and willing sacrifice, with a blood oath binding them to their beloved forever.","opus":"Sung in Swedish, a vow of absolute loyalty and protection framed as being someone's guard dog (a Dobermann), pledging to stay by their side day and night. The narrator swears his devotion in blood, treats the world as an enemy, and warns off anyone who would harm the one he guards.","sonnet":"A speaker declares absolute, possessive devotion to another person, vowing to watch over them day and night, fight off any threat, and remain faithful unto death. The Dobermann metaphor frames this loyalty as fierce, instinctual, and unconditional—close to the point of obsession.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~en-hj-ltes-v-g":{"haiku":"A hero walks the path of battle and sacrifice, facing danger unflinching and choosing honorable death over survival in Valhalla.","opus":"A Norse-flavored ode to the hero's path, depicting a warrior with divine blood who marches into battle without fear, tender to his woman but iron-handed with his sword, ready to fall and die like a man; his deeds earn eternal glory as he charges toward Valhalla.","sonnet":"A Swedish hymn to the heroic warrior path — a Sigurd-like figure tenderly bids farewell to his woman, grasps his sword, and rides toward battle and death, knowing that blood-written deeds earn eternal memory and a place in Valhalla.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~en-starkare-art":{"haiku":"Genetic engineering creates a superior clone race, sexless and programmed, replacing flawed humanity through forced evolution.","opus":"A Swedish martial-metal dystopia about engineering a stronger species in test tubes, mass-producing sexless clones for war while the obsolete natural man is culled. A cold warning about eugenics and militarized evolution.","sonnet":"A Swedish industrial song about eugenics and totalitarian efficiency — test-tube babies, mass-produced soldiers, cloned battalions, and the liquidation of anyone deemed obsolete, presented as the cold logic of building a stronger species.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~f-rg-tmigej":{"haiku":"Swedish song on suicidal despair; isolation, cold, abandonment, and a bullet as the only escape—ending suffering through self-inflicted death.","opus":"The Swedish metal act, known for militarised themes, narrows here to a single frozen scene of a man alone with wolves and a spent twelve-gauge shell, resolving that a sting of pain and a whiff of gunpowder is the only exit from unbearable torment. Abandoned by another, he ends by pleading forget-me-not, so grief and firearm become one final Nordic surrender.","sonnet":"A man abandoned by his lover stands alone in frozen darkness, wolves howling in the silence. Crushed by anguish he sees no way forward — only a gunshot and the release of all suffering. His final plea is simply that she remember him, a forget-me-not from the edge of oblivion.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~fafnesbane":{"haiku":"Ancestral warrior blood carries Sigurd's legacy against societal complacency; the speaker and their kin refuse captivity and march eternally toward transformation.","opus":"Swedish industrial metal draping conquest and defiance in Norse myth, casting the band as heirs of Sigurd the dragon-slayer against a soft cultural elite. Bombast and Völsung blood carry a swaggering us-against-them creed.","sonnet":"A Swedish heavy metal song that uses Norse mythology — specifically Sigurd killing the dragon Fafnir — as a symbol for a working-class warrior spirit that refuses to be caged by the cultural elite, drinking to the Volsung bloodline and claiming the legacy of Fafner's slayer.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~g-tterd-mmerung":{"haiku":"The apocalypse unfolds as environmental collapse and nuclear destruction consume the world; mankind faces extinction and the inevitable end of days in fire and blood.","opus":"A Swedish apocalypse anthem voicing an active longing to witness the end of the world, to hear the earth's swansong as fire burns and blood flows; drained oil wells, melted poles, famine and Death's reaping scythe deliver a Twilight-of-the-Gods doomsday where everyone will die today, sung with grim relish rather than dread.","sonnet":"A Swedish industrial metal song reveling in apocalyptic imagery — oil dried up, poles melted, famine spreading, black rain and lightning, rivers of blood — the narrator actively wants to witness and experience the end of the world, framing the total annihilation of civilization as a desired spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~hj-rteblod":{"haiku":"Love betrayed hardens the heart into stone; the speaker watches a lover's coldness kill what once burned. Their bleeding heart will become callous through time, silenced forever by the scars of misused trust.","opus":"A portrait of a man wrecked and haunted by demons after being betrayed and abandoned by the woman who was his everything; the refrain warns that love is like a sharpened knife that kills in the wrong hands, and he goes pale as death realizing the dark red he sees is heart's blood—love that corrodes until the heart hardens into eternal numbness.","sonnet":"A Swedish-language song about a man destroyed by a cold-hearted woman who deceived and abandoned him — love is a sharpened knife in the wrong hands, and the dark red that remains is heart's blood; the wound hardens over time into permanent numbness.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~inget-hopp":{"haiku":"In a nuclear-devastated, war-torn landscape, survivors crawl and bleed, gods have abandoned them, and no future or hope remains.","opus":"A Swedish apocalyptic war narrative: after oil-price collapse leaders stay silent, hydrogen and nuclear bombs fall, and the survivors crawl like slaves through a scorched, radioactive wasteland, abandoned by the gods, fighting on at the edge of madness with no future and no hope left to pray for.","sonnet":"A post-nuclear war apocalypse narrated from ground level — hydrogen bombs have fallen, the front has collapsed, men and women fight on as radiation strips flesh from bones, and there is no future, no hope, nothing left to pray for.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~kamphund":{"haiku":"A battle hound (kamphund) embodies the predatory nature forced by hardship and wilderness; hunger drives wolves to violence and hunting—a dark reminder of mankind's fragility.","opus":"In Swedish, the song likens a warrior to a fighting dog and a wolf pack led by its alpha, where hunger and hardship drive predators to savagery, and warns of a dark truth in the wolf's song that reminds man of his own weakness and downfall.","sonnet":"The Swedish lyrics use the image of a fighting dog and a wolf pack led by an alpha to explore themes of hunger, hardness, and survival: harsh conditions demand toughness, the weak cannot keep up, and nature's law is the only law that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~l-t-napalmen-regna":{"haiku":"A Swedish metal critique of mass entertainment and consumer culture, calling for catastrophic destruction via napalm to burn away shallow modernity.","opus":"Raubtier turns their martial bombast into savage satire, cataloguing reality TV, vanity, health fads and gossip magazines as symptoms of a wasted, sedated culture. The grand solution proposed is apocalyptic: let napalm rain from bombers and burn the whole world clean. The recipe of chemicals and gasoline reads as scorn for modern complacency dressed up as a cheerful final show.","sonnet":"A speaker consumed by contempt for a culture of passive consumption and vapid entertainment imagines its fiery erasure. Reality television and celebrity noise deserve a napalm finale delivered by a B-52. The disgust is total and the solution is annihilation of everything hollow.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~lebensgefahr":{"haiku":"A warning against reckless living and dangerous choices; consequences await those who cross moral boundaries, and hope cannot protect those who lose restraint.","opus":"A stern Swedish warning about crossing the line, cautioning that whoever walks through the night without a lantern meets a fatal end, that playing with fire eventually burns you and the sinner must atone once the verdict falls. The German title, mortal danger, tolls like an alarm. Raubtier are a Swedish metal act steeped in martial, disciplinarian imagery, and this grim moral about consequence and no turning back sits squarely in their iron-fisted register.","sonnet":"A warning — the title means mortal danger — addressed to someone dancing on the edge of a decision they can't take back. Using old proverbs about fire and dogs as scaffolding, the song insists that once the blade is drawn and the sentence spoken, there's no reversing course: hope burns out, and what remains is the sinner settling their debt.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~legoknekt":{"haiku":"A mercenary sings proudly of his life as a hired gun driven by money and weapons, operating without fear or moral restraint in desert and jungle. The speaker embraces violence and regrouping after death as occupational constants.","opus":"A mercenary's creed sung in the first person: he fights not for country but for money and respect, sealing a devil's blood-contract, killing without scruple across deserts and jungles. The chilling boast that mercenaries cannot die (they simply regroup at hell's gate) makes his loyalty to violence itself, love only for cash and weapons.","sonnet":"A first-person mercenary's declaration of purpose: he fights for money and weapons, bound by a blood contract, unafraid of death in deserts or jungles because, as he says, mercenaries don't die — they regroup at the gates of hell. It's a celebration of the freelance soldier's trade with no moral conflict attached.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~nglar":{"haiku":"Rejection of Christian salvation and heavenly reward; the singer chooses earthly pleasure, noise, and darkness over religious redemption, embracing decibels and napalm instead of angels and purity.","opus":"A bleak drinking song of the damned: with heaven's gate forever sealed and the road to god blown up, these hard men find their only warmth in vodka and their only church in a bar—they were never meant to be angels, preferring firepower, high voltage and napalm to piety.","sonnet":"A Swedish song about belonging to the wrong side of heaven — the singers drink instead of praying, find their church in a bar, and embrace decibels and napalm over piety, concluding flatly that angels are not for them and the gates above are permanently shut.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~rebeller":{"haiku":"Rural rebels live outside conformity and government control, rejecting bureaucracy and EU authority to maintain freedom and tradition.","opus":"A rugged anthem of rural, off-grid defiance, living beyond society's margins by one's own law with guns, moonshine and a red neck, refusing ever to conform; hatred of big-city bureaucrats and EU control fuels a vow to remain rebels living in freedom until death.","sonnet":"A Swedish working-class rebel anthem — living beyond the margins, farming and drinking and answering to no one, despising EU bureaucrats and their regulations, and insisting on dying free rather than conforming to a world that wants them tame.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~sveriges-elit":{"haiku":"Swedish predators fueled by violence, diesel, blood, and alcohol march triumphantly, forcing the world to bow to their elite and unstoppable force.","opus":"Swedish industrial metal mythologizing a diesel-and-moonshine warrior elite, a triumphant conquest march fueled by fuel, meat and homebrew that swaggers the world into kneeling, aimed with a wink at trembling critics.","sonnet":"A Swedish heavy metal boast about a wild, diesel-and-blood \"elite\" of rowdy working-class brutes who tour Sweden like predators, drinking homemade spirits and crushing all opposition — more chest-beating satire than genuine menace.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~terror":{"haiku":"A person contains a split identity with two natures; one peaceful, one brutal and violent, causing internal terror that must be hidden and repressed during daylight.","opus":"The Swedish band voices a split identity, a peaceful man harboring a violent second self that stirs as night falls, a chilling study of the murderous impulse the narrator toys with, demanding others dance to his tune or become corpses.","sonnet":"A song about a split personality where a peaceful daytime self is haunted by a nocturnal predator who fantasizes about wielding weapons and hearing screams — the violent inner self keeps breaking out no matter how hard the narrator tries to contain it.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~v-rldsherrav-lde":{"haiku":"A tyrannical world order violently crushes media and popular music, replacing it with harsh metal and repression while eliminating soul, funk, and hip-hop. The takeover is total and without mercy.","opus":"A Swedish metal fantasy of predatory wilderness rising to crush a corrupt, media-saturated culture, marching to silence pop radio and commercial music; primal nature imagined as an armored army purging modern noise.","sonnet":"A Swedish industrial metal song about world domination by heavy metal — the band declares itself the predator force that will purge corrupted mainstream radio (P3), MTV, and Z-TV, marching in lockstep to cleanse soul, funk, disco, rap, and hip-hop from the airwaves and replace them with armored-plate thrash.","src":"haiku","fable":"Raubtier stage a fascist cultural purge as pure spectacle, and the joke is how narrowly the apocalypse is aimed: world domination arrives goose-stepping in march-time to abolish, of all things, soul, funk, disco, rap and every last cursed strain of hip-hop, with a promised blood-feud against MTV and Z-TV and a cleansing of a corrupted P3 — the Swedish national radio channel and music-TV stations named by call sign, which shrinks the grandiose martial rhetoric down to the pettiness of a genre snob who wants his airwaves rid of everything but heavy metal. The band leans into their own predator iconography (Raubtier means beast of prey, and the pack marches under a predator's banner atop the last radio mast) so that the total-war imagery of steel storms and burning skies becomes the overblown fantasy of the outsider metalhead, the wilderness song silenced by the media, dressed up as conquest. Whether you take the closing march through the shards of your last hope as knowing self-parody or as genuine reactionary sneer is the song's live wire; the militarism is played straight enough that the irony has to be supplied by how absurdly small the enemy is.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","war & battle","artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"total-war rhetoric aimed at a genre snob's grievance","built":"named Swedish stations shrink the apocalyptic march to a dial dispute"},
"raubtier~vapenbr-der":{"haiku":"Warriors pledge eternal loyalty and sacrifice to each other, binding their honor, blood, and lives together as brothers-in-arms.","opus":"A martial oath of brotherhood-in-arms, pledging that once brothers always brothers, to obey, fight, and fall together. The throughline is absolute loyalty, offering respect, steel, blood, and life to one's comrades, a warrior code of shared fate.","sonnet":"A Swedish oath of brotherhood among soldiers: once brothers, always brothers — if you march I march, if you fall I fall, my honor and blood and life are pledged beside yours.","src":"haiku"},
"raubtier~vittring":{"haiku":"A hunter tracks prey through harsh terrain for years, finally closing in for the kill, drawn by instinct and bloodlust toward their scent on the wind.","opus":"A Swedish hunter's monologue tracking prey through bog, forest and frost for days and years, cursing and craving the quarry in equal measure until it finally stands in his crosshairs on trembling legs, one shot ending all bitterness. The wind carrying its scent reads as fate and obsession. Raubtier are a Swedish metal band trading in exactly this kind of primal, martial imagery, the chase rendered as something almost predatory and inevitable.","sonnet":"A hunter's obsession told as erotic pursuit — years of tracking through swamps and frost, nights fooled by false lights, until the prey finally stands within reach on trembling legs. The final stanza delivers the shot, the blood trail, and the end of the bitterness all at once: a life, a dream, and a death year collapsed into a single windborne scent.","src":"haiku"},
"rave-the-reqviem~thistleblower":{"haiku":"Justice operates on corruption and desire while society waits for a savior to expose authority and speak truth.","opus":"Rave the Reqviem, a Swedish industrial-metal collective, turn political disillusion into theatrical carnival. A wasted personification of justice fires indiscriminately for freedom or amusement, then the song asks who will keep the public docile and spoon-feed them comforting falsehoods once the next savior figure emerges. Power and complicity staged as venomous spectacle.","sonnet":"A hollow search for authority in a world where justice is corrupt and drunk on power — the goddess of justice armed and aroused by suffering, firing for ideology or thrill with equal indifference. The song's repeated questions — who will love us, set us free, act as authority — never receive answers, leaving the thistleblower of the title as both absent saviour and open accusation.","src":"haiku"},
"razor~enforcer":{"haiku":"A killer prowls at night with hate and revenge, waiting outside your home to enforce a death sentence—murder is their only thought and their gift to you.","opus":"A cold-blooded killer's creed. The stalker prowls each night fueled by hatred and patience, watching his sleeping prey as his approaching kill closes in, framing the murder as a sentence he has passed and an offering he is obliged to make. No remorse, only method. Razor are a Canadian speed-metal band from the mid-eighties thrash underground, and this menace delivered at breakneck tempo is exactly the brutality that made their name.","sonnet":"Pure murder-fantasy thrash, no ambiguity: the speaker prowls, waits, and kills with the cold patience of someone who has already decided the outcome. The victim's death is framed as a divine commission and a personal liberation. There is no psychological arc, just the mechanics of predation delivered as a verdict.","src":"haiku"},
"reach~the-law-zardonic-remix":{"haiku":"Law and order emerge as malevolent forces manipulating society through spectacle and debt; the powerful dance marionettes of the oppressed toward inevitable doom.","opus":"The Law personified as an amoral cosmic force that creates chaos as easily as order, indifferent to the debtors and the drowned it rules. A drum-and-bass sermon on power that owes no one salvation.","sonnet":"Law speaks in first person as an authoritarian force that controls, divides rich from poor, manufactures chaos and order alike, and treats all people as puppets whose world it casually ends — unapologetic, all-encompassing, and indifferent.","src":"haiku"},
"ready-the-prince~backslider":{"haiku":"Regression and failure to maintain progress; the speaker slides backward into previous patterns.","opus":"A confession of relapse and self-estrangement, the title naming someone who keeps slipping back. Having staked his blood on a promise it's in his makeup to break, the narrator rips up lines he'd already put down and sinks under his own doubts. Repeating that everything's fine while unable to free himself, he no longer knows the person he's turned into.","sonnet":"A promise broken and the shame of watching yourself become someone you don't recognise — insecurities growing like weeds, pages thrown out, sinking slow; the internal mantra \"all is well\" is exposed as a coping lie. The narrator can't untie themselves from their own nature and has stopped pretending the fall wasn't self-authored.","src":"haiku"},
"ready-the-prince~lightning":{"haiku":"Someone feels struck by overwhelming loneliness and stress, repeatedly descending into darkness and despair like lightning crashing through their mind.","opus":"Depression and cocaine rendered as weather that lives inside the skull, an electrical storm no one else can see. The distancing third person marks how the sufferer watches his own breakdown from outside himself.","sonnet":"A man overwhelmed by stress, cocaine, and loneliness describes the feeling as being struck by lightning repeatedly — the chaos is entirely internal, and darkness is the only companion left.","src":"haiku"},
"reaper~black-fires":{"haiku":"Loneliness attracts darkness and fire; the speaker finds themselves drawn repeatedly to flames they cannot control or extinguish.","opus":"A compulsive draw toward destructive passion, the speaker always the one who finds the flames even while claiming not to want the game. Loneliness keeps pulling him back to a love that consumes.","sonnet":"A person who can't stop being drawn to pain and darkness — the 'black fires' represent a consuming loneliness or destructive pull that keeps creeping in no matter how much they call out for the person or connection they've lost.","src":"haiku"},
"reaper~imy-feat-bella-renee":{"haiku":"The speaker deeply misses someone they loved, lost to circumstance, experiencing time differently since their absence.","opus":"Reaper set grief to a heavy backdrop softened by Bella Renee's vocal, circling the ache of missing someone who can no longer hear. Others offer platitudes about walking in the rain and better weather, but summer arrives and the loss persists. Losing track of time, the narrator misses the presence behind them and the wish that the person were still theirs, the simple refrain carrying the whole weight.","sonnet":"Summer arrives without resolving the absence — advice about healing with warmth means nothing when the longing persists regardless of season. He replays the person in his mind, losing track of time, the missing still deafening despite the bright weather everyone promised would help.","src":"haiku"},
"reaper~worlds-away":{"haiku":"Distance and separation ache in every moment, with memories fading as emotional connection proves impossible to sustain.","opus":"Grief as widening distance. The world's color bleeds toward gray while each pulse and breath pulls the mourner nearer to the little that remains of a person now impossibly far off. Clinging on becomes a form of denial, memory dissolving as the loss sinks into darkness.","sonnet":"A song about grief over someone who is gone — physically or emotionally — and the way time stretches intolerably without them; the narrator clings on in darkness as colours drain to grey and memories dissolve, knowing the distance is insurmountable.","src":"haiku"},
"rebel-meets-rebel~cowboys-do-more-dope":{"haiku":"Country music stars use harder drugs than rock musicians; cocaine, freebase, and acid are commonplace among rural performers despite clean public faces.","opus":"This collaboration between Pantera members and outlaw-country singer David Allan Coe leans into its own genre-clash premise, wryly weighing whether country stars out-consume rock and rollers. Whiskey, weed, cocaine and freebase pile up behind clean public faces, half-joking that the twangier the singer the harder the habit. The closing warning against purple acid keeps the whole thing grinning at its own vice.","sonnet":"A sardonic rundown of country music's chemical habits — whiskey, weed, cocaine, and worse lurking behind clean-cut facades. The argument runs that country artists outdo their rock counterparts in substance use, with Willie Nelson as Exhibit A and a closing warning against bad acid.","src":"haiku","fable":"The joke of this outlaw romp — David Allan Coe fronting three-quarters of Pantera — is that its title question keeps its own answer hostage: it inventories the weed, whiskey, blow and freebase of the Nashville set, tips a nod to a busted Willie Nelson, then circles back unresolved to wonder whether cowboys really out-consume the rockers. That refusal to settle is the point, because both camps are the same appetite wearing different hats, and the track relishes the respectable Music Row façade with a habit stashed behind it more than it wants any verdict. The late swerve into a mock public-service warning against some designer hallucinogen is pure outlaw irony, a wink that pretends to sober up exactly while the party keeps roaring.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"two camps of appetite in different hats refusing to settle their competition","built":"the mock public-service warning against a designer hallucinogen pretends to sober up while the party keeps roaring"},
"rebel-meets-rebel~nothin-to-lose":{"haiku":"A chronic gambler and loser reflects on addiction—gambling, love, fortune—always playing hunches and always losing because having nothing means no consequences.","opus":"A dying gambler's fatalistic self-portrait: he lost his woman, his fortune, and now his health, refusing to change or accept salvation, taking grim freedom in the idea that a man with nothing has nothing left to lose.","sonnet":"A country-metal portrait of a lifelong gambler who has lost his woman, his money, his health, and his faith — a man who refuses salvation or change because when you have nothing left, nothing can threaten you anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"rebel-meets-rebel~rebel-meets-rebel":{"haiku":"Outsiders and rule-breakers unite in celebration of freedom, rowdiness, and refusal to conform, viewing themselves as partners in a shared lifestyle.","opus":"The title track from the country-metal collaboration between members of Pantera and outlaw singer David Allan Coe, celebrating its own improbable pairing. It's a rowdy toast to hard drinking, hell-raising, and freedom, two musical worlds shaking hands. The song casts the whole experiment as a mischievous alliance and maybe a glimpse of things to come.","sonnet":"A country outlaw and a metal renegade find common ground in rowdy freedom — drinking, making noise, operating outside the system's rules. The collision is celebrated as a partnership in productive chaos, two traditions of defiance recognizing each other as kin.","src":"haiku"},
"red-handed-denial~empire":{"haiku":"A ruthless ruler built their empire through blood and pain; they sleep with one eye open, haunt enemies, and reign through hypocrisy and casualties.","opus":"A villain's boastful monologue of revenge and conquest, built by ruthless hands and vowing that his target will suffer, a world where honor is dead and only cowardice survives. The empire-and-business framing winks at Breaking Bad's Heisenberg. Red Handed Denial, a Canadian progressive metalcore act, wrap the menace in technical, melodic aggression and dramatic clean-to-scream vocals.","sonnet":"Someone who built everything themselves now holding a grudge with both hands — scarred, sleepless, promising vengeance on whoever wronged them. The empire is real but built on hypocrisy, casualties counted as the price of a kingdom. White picket fences and diamond rings hide blood on the ground; the speaker knows this because the blood is partly theirs, and they're not finished collecting.","src":"haiku"},
"red-handed-denial~father-said":{"haiku":"The narrator was bound by a father's control and abuse, living in lies and torment until breaking free and seeing through the deception.","opus":"Red Handed Denial, a Canadian metalcore band, use clean-and-scream dynamics to charge this reckoning with control and inherited trauma. It's a survivor confronting an abusive father, recalling being shackled and forced to impersonate someone he wasn't, punished for his true self, now an adult who sees through the deceptions and rejects the internalized voice that still refuses to release him.","sonnet":"Breaking free from a controlling father whose voice still runs on a loop in the speaker's head years after escaping. The childhood was a cell, the self performed for someone else's approval, the torment for simply existing as who you are. Adulthood brings clear sight of the web of lies — but clarity doesn't stop the voice from calling, demanding change, refusing to let go.","src":"haiku"},
"red-handed-denial~white-water":{"haiku":"A loved one drowns invisibly, pulled beneath waves while the speaker watches helpless from shore. Secrets buried beneath surface calm erase agency and bind the living to the depths.","opus":"A metalcore lament for someone lost to their own inner turmoil. Once able to stride across white water, they are swept away by the waves and bleed into the sea, leaving the narrator unable to unravel the secrets kept hidden. Drowning imagery, awake yet asleep, alive yet drawing no breath, captures both the struggle of the vanished person and the helpless grief of the survivor.","sonnet":"Someone watches a person they love dissolve — once capable of walking on white water, now bleeding into the sea, drowning in secrets the narrator can't unravel no matter how many years they spend trying to unweave them.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~around-the-world":{"haiku":"Travel and diverse encounters affirm that life radiates beauty across every geography and culture, with love offering the only universal language. Connection requires openness to difference.","opus":"A globe-trotting, tour-fueled love of women and places everywhere, Kiedis reeling off cities and states in a wired celebration of how good life feels the whole world over.","sonnet":"A freewheeling, globe-hopping brag of everywhere the speaker has been and everyone they've encountered — Pennsylvania girls, Sicilian kisses, Swiss mountains, Ohio — finding life beautiful at every stop and saying hello and adieu in the same breath.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~breaking-the-girl":{"haiku":"Masculine indifference fractures feminine vulnerability, depicting how carelessness in relationships shatters trust and leaves emotional casualties.","opus":"Kiedis reckoning with his own restless cruelty in love, tracing it back to a father who cycled through women and taught him detachment as inheritance. The pain of watching yourself become the man you were raised by.","sonnet":"A man who grew up without warmth from women meets a lonely girl seeking shelter in him, but the relationship ends in damage — the song indicts him for breaking someone who meant no harm, driven by emotional unavailability rooted in his upbringing.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~by-the-way":{"haiku":"The narrator attempts connection with Dani, weaving through urban scenes and wordplay while repeatedly saying he waited but perhaps never meant it.","opus":"Anthony Kiedis pairing a tender vision of a girl and a marquee with a rapid-fire blur of Hollywood street slang. The verses evoke the seedy adrenaline of L.A. nightlife and drug memory crashing against a simple, longing chorus.","sonnet":"The narrator rushes to reach a girl named Dani who's singing under a marquee, weaving stream-of-consciousness street imagery around the urgency — the song is about being hopelessly drawn toward someone and fearing she won't wait.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~californication":{"haiku":"The hollowness of celebrity culture and entertainment-driven civilization; plastic surgery, fame-seeking, and screen illusion as empty pursuits replacing real substance.","opus":"Anthony Kiedis treats California less as a place than as an infectious export, coining a word for the way Hollywood ships fantasies of fame, cosmetic reinvention and manufactured desire around the globe. Even space frontiers, he jokes, are staged on a soundstage, the state a spreading appetite for illusion sold to everyone.","sonnet":"Hollywood exports a seductive myth — eternal youth, silver-screen dreams, celebrity surgery — that colonises the world's imagination. The song maps this cultural contagion from Swedish teenagers to Cobain references, arguing that mass-produced desire has become the defining spiritual weather of the West.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~can-t-stop":{"haiku":"Unstoppable momentum and compulsive drive push forward endlessly; the speaker becomes part of inevitable motion and collective wave.","opus":"From the band's early-2000s return to form, a barrel through free-associative Californian imagery, the narrator hooked on the party and the swell of experience, refusing to merely copy others' lives. Music carries the message amid tears, jumped freight trains, and a blur of half-glimpsed figures, the drive to keep moving and belong to something bigger fueling a restless, word-drunk celebration that, by its own admission, refuses to halt.","sonnet":"A stream-of-consciousness rush through urban energy, creative compulsion, and the intoxicating pull of something larger than personal will. Imagery collides without resolution — the narrator is hooked on the wave of collective experience, choosing sensation and forward motion over self-preservation or stable identity.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~dosed":{"haiku":"Intoxication by another creates irreconcilable desire that only feeds itself through impossible longings. The speaker craves the unreachable as the sole proof of authenticity.","opus":"Intoxication used as a metaphor for consuming love and loss, the narrator dosed by another whose very life he craved, circling a woman's death on the mountain.","sonnet":"A love that arrives like a drug — dosed, overwhelming, impossible to prepare for — and the speaker confesses that all they ever wanted was this person's life, not to take it but to be as close as the mountain where she died and the canyon that hides nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~easily":{"haiku":"Impermanence and release govern existence as attachment to possessions and people blocks transcendence and spiritual evolution.","opus":"A restless riff on impulse and detachment, careening from marriage to war to the refusal to be anyone's research monkey, insisting everything must go. Kiedis keeps grasping at something just out of reach while resisting being caged, a creature half-drawn to destroy.","sonnet":"A song of surrender and release — letting go of idols, possessions, and ego to be thrown to the wolves and come back, framed around a woman on the morning of a war and a Shaolin figure shaking for the sake of his soul as everything must go.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~give-it-away":{"haiku":"Sharing freely and rejecting material greed creates genuine happiness; the song celebrates generosity, love, and abundance over hoarding and ego.","opus":"The funk-rock anthem that broke Red Hot Chili Peppers wide open turns openhandedness into a mantra, Anthony Kiedis chanting that whatever you have should be passed on rather than clutched. Playful and rhythm-driven, it preaches abundance through release, prizing affection and mobility over possessions, nodding to reggae forebears, and refusing to distinguish the powerful from the poor.","sonnet":"Generosity as a cosmological principle — abundance multiplied by release rather than hoarding — runs through the lyric as both personal ethic and ancestral lesson. Accumulation is recast as diminishment, and giving away is framed as the one act that keeps the giver genuinely full rather than merely possessed by what they hold.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~i-could-die-for-you":{"haiku":"Someone is willing to sacrifice everything for a chosen path and a loved one; he offers his life and all his senses as testimony to her beauty.","opus":"From the same expansive double album, this one softens into devotion, a narrator vowing he would die for a lover while insisting the life he leads is freely chosen, not borrowed from anyone. He casts himself as a go-between reporting what his senses take in, eager to share it all, turning generosity and surrender into the same gesture toward intimacy behind a closed door.","sonnet":"A man offers himself completely to the person he loves — not out of desperation but out of freely chosen devotion. He sees the world as something beautiful to share, and his entire life purpose condenses into wandering alongside her, no cost attached.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~i-could-have-lied":{"haiku":"Emotional authenticity demands vulnerability even as fear and inaccessibility sabotage connection; the speaker admits defeat yet cannot hide their truth.","opus":"Kiedis on a real, brief entanglement with Sinead O'Connor, wounded but insisting his face could never fake a feeling. The dignity is in the refusal to lie even as it costs him, honesty as the wound and the pride both.","sonnet":"A speaker who wore his heart openly and paid for it admits he could have concealed his feelings but was unable to — his eyes betrayed everything — and now he is left wrecked and alone after the woman he loved fled from the very honesty she provoked in him.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~midnight":{"haiku":"Time shifts between moments; the singer exists in transition, mixing identities until pedigrees blur. They navigate cosmic and lunar spaces, lotus children who know anything goes and remake themselves constantly.","opus":"From Stadium Arcadium, one of the double album's dreamier stretches, John Frusciante's melodic guitar lending it an expansive glow. The song muses on remaking the self, a narrator ready to reinvent who he is beneath a mystical night sky. Cosmic, half-spiritual imagery carries a sense of drifting toward some larger destiny.","sonnet":"A wide-open, slightly mystical celebration of transformation and release — things changed, nothing is the same, but the pivot is embraced rather than mourned. Scorpio's blood, lotus kids, the laws of light all circle around the same idea: something ended, something began, and by midnight the speaker has reinvented himself again and is glad he came.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~otherside":{"haiku":"A speaker fights suicidal ideation through addiction imagery—pouring life away, drowning in ashes—and seeks escape to some other realm beyond this pain.","opus":"Written in the shadow of the band's history with addiction and loss, Kiedis frames relapse as an endless slide, hearing a dead friend's voice through a photograph and knowing there is no going back. The imagery of paper cups, full ashtrays and a slit throat treats craving as something torn down and reborn again, an exhausting passage to an other side he keeps being pulled toward.","sonnet":"A man trapped in cycles of self-destruction — addiction, reckless relationships, a numbed existence — reaches for some release on the other side of his own collapse. The past haunts him through photographs and the feeling that his throat has always been the thing he turns against himself.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~parallel-universe":{"haiku":"In alternate dimensions and layered realities, human consciousness connects to cosmic systems, where individual perception mirrors infinite space and shared emotional breakthrough transcends isolation.","opus":"A psychedelic vision of a boundless, interconnected cosmos where the mind moves freely and an entire galaxy fits within a lover's gaze. Beneath the imagery lies intimacy imagined as transcendence, a jolting emotional epiphany that fuses two people into one, with a promise to remain present even through suffering.","sonnet":"About the vertigo of simultaneous realities and the cosmic scale of consciousness — the narrator is a sidewinder king lost in the universe inside a single eye, where death is not final and love arrives as a psychic breakthrough. It's rapturous and disorienting rather than narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~scar-tissue":{"haiku":"Someone carries emotional scars while observing beauty from isolation, resigned to viewing life from a distance. The song conveys melancholy isolation and bittersweet witnessing.","opus":"Written as Kiedis climbed out of heroin addiction, the song drifts through sunlit isolation, sharing its solitary vantage only with birds overhead. Flickers of Southern girls, a bathroom collapse and a wrecked face mark the wreckage of using, yet a stubborn vow to keep going threads recovery's fragile hope through the melancholy.","sonnet":"Damage and tenderness carried together across a drifting, sun-bleached landscape. A narrator worn down by accumulated wounds — bathroom-stall blood loss, broken jaw, difficult farewells — still reaches toward intimacy. Loneliness is shared with birds overhead rather than the person who caused the scars.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~tear":{"haiku":"Surrender to transformation and acceptance of mortality bring peace as the narrator discovers giving what he came to give.","opus":"A song about surrender and offering up everything you arrived to give, treating each breath and every high and low as something to hold onto before the end. Kiedis casts a single tear and a private wrong as a moment of release, trusting that it will all be pardoned.","sonnet":"A California-drenched moment of full surrender — every rise and fall loved, all will be forgiven when surrender rises high, say it now because you never know — with the Distillers' scream as voltage and the sky wide open for the speaker's time.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~the-greeting-song":{"haiku":"Joyful eroticism and sensual communion merge; love becomes sound and bodily presence as the speaker celebrates embodied pleasure.","opus":"By Kiedis's own admission a throwaway written to order about cars and girls, all L.A. cruising and sun. There is no deeper subject, just the sound and the joyride, devotion flattened into engine and top-down swagger.","sonnet":"A sun-soaked, LA-rooted celebration of being in love — cruising in a Chevrolet, rolling through the city, everything suffused with the sound and sensation of desire — the speaker finding the divine in the face of someone they are wholly given over to.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~this-is-the-place":{"haiku":"Junkies inhabit a place where time warps and dopamine is isolated like a drug. Genes are isolated, DNA is obsessed over—a portrait of addiction, desperation, and the search for chemical and genetic transcendence.","opus":"Red Hot Chili Peppers set this on By the Way, Anthony Kiedis drawing directly on his own addiction history so the addled imagery masks a genuine reckoning. It's a haunted tour through drug haunts and their hollow promises, the narrator vowing not to repeat his father's failings even as temptation presses in, with a buried mention of a friend's death grounding the wreckage in real grief.","sonnet":"Addiction, beauty, and grief tangled together in a neighbourhood where junkies go and time moves wrong. The speaker is drawn to someone's genetic perfection while mourning a dead best friend and refusing to repeat his father's failures — desire and damage operating on the same frequency, the shockingly appalling thing being how much he still wants in.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~throw-away-your-television":{"haiku":"Television repeats tired stories, a contagious plague draining agency and intuition. The song urges breaking free from repetition—throwing it away and reclaiming supervision over one's own mind.","opus":"A blunt call to unplug and reclaim attention, urging listeners to trash the TV and its repeated, aging stories, to reinvent intuition and let renegades slay the contagious plague of passive consumption. The chorus lands like a chanted slogan against media rot. Red Hot Chili Peppers cut this on By the Way, and its stripped, riff-driven repetition mirrors the very numbing repetition it rails against, funk-rock turned into anti-media agitprop.","sonnet":"A straight-ahead exhortation to kill the passive loop of broadcast repetition and take back your own perception. Television is the plague, the noose on ambition, the thing turning life into a recycled story. The fix is to pull the plug, seize the stage, reinvent how you see — renegades with the courage to break the signal.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~under-the-bridge":{"haiku":"Urban solitude and addiction consume the narrator who finds solace only in the city's indifferent embrace, isolated despite the metropolis teeming around them.","opus":"Loneliness so deep that the city of Los Angeles becomes the singer's only companion, haunted by the memory of a low point under a downtown bridge where heroin nearly cost him everything.","sonnet":"Los Angeles is the speaker's only companion in deep loneliness, but the song turns on a memory of being under a bridge downtown, where blood was drawn and a life was given away to drugs — the city witnesses both the desolation and the plea to be taken back to a place of love.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~universally-speaking":{"haiku":"Attraction and romantic desire transcend explanation; universal connection overwhelms the speaker toward someone they cannot resist.","opus":"In the buoyant pop register of the band's Californication era, the narrator marvels at a lover worn down yet still magnetic, admiring her even in her weariness. Desire spills into breathless, giddy wordplay, the whole song a hymn to romance as an infectious, universal appetite rather than any source of heartbreak.","sonnet":"Infatuation rendered as cosmic certainty — a first glimpse of someone exhausted but magnetic convinces the narrator that what follows is inevitable. Desire operates on a frequency beyond naming, and the declared intention is to win over distance and time, patience framed as the one reliable strategy.","src":"haiku"},
"red-hot-chili-peppers~venice-queen":{"haiku":"The song eulogizes Gloria—a woman who exemplified grace and love. After her death, the singer reflects on her gifts, her departure, and her essence as pure love carried forward in memory.","opus":"A grieving farewell to a woman named Gloria as she nears the end of her life, honoring her rare warmth and letting her go by the ocean. Anthony Kiedis wrote it for his late drug counselor to close Californication, and the swelling, tidal arrangement gives the tribute genuine emotional weight.","sonnet":"A tribute to Gloria, a real woman the speaker loved like a teacher and a home — Venice as the place she embodied, her silver hair and the waves she made the lasting image. The song moves between the drifting pleasures of the neighbourhood and the grief of her departure, ending with her name spelled out as a love word.","src":"haiku"},
"red-orca~orca-force":{"opus":"A defiant Japanese artist reclaims the orca as a symbol of power and vengeance, mixing attack imagery with a pointed rebuke of racist stereotypes about Japanese people and whaling, ultimately affirming the strength of love and survival.","sonnet":"A Japanese-English hybrid song about resurgence after being written off — the narrator fights back against racism, stereotypes, and those who underestimated them, driven also by a private emotional throughline about surviving until love returns.","src":"sonnet"},
"red-vox~reno":{"haiku":"A wanderer fleeing the desert returns to a familiar refuge, driven by exhaustion and longing for respite after abandonment in hostile terrain.","opus":"A blackout gambler crawling out of the desert, drawn back to the town that ruins him by a craving he can't name or survive without. Addiction dressed as a road trip, the escape and the trap the same place.","sonnet":"A narrator wakes disoriented in the desert after some form of excess, battered and dragging himself back to Reno — the city functions as both escape and return, the place a person always drifts back to even when they've been burned by it.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~bad-decisions":{"haiku":"The narrator spirals through substance abuse and self-destructive behavior, caught in a cycle of debt, poor decisions, and mental health struggles that stem from feeling overwhelmed by external pressure and expectations.","opus":"The Australian outfit turns self-sabotage into a candid confessional, its narrator waking after another blur of substances, financial ruin and reckless flings and picturing a childhood self horrified at who they became. Refusing to glamorize it, they trace the spiral to fragile wellbeing and the weight of others' hopes, admitting the loop of numbing and loathing themselves stays intact even as it makes, ruefully, memorable tales.","sonnet":"A self-aware young woman catalogues her own implosion — drugs, debt, sexual chaos — with blunt honesty rather than glamour. Mental health pressure and other people's expectations drive her to self-medicate, spinning a cycle of shame she cannot break. The confessions are mortifying and, she admits, make great stories.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~bomb-com":{"haiku":"Social media's hollow icons and vapid performance culture dominate; authentic meaning drowns in digital posturing.","opus":"A pointed takedown of an arrogant industry figure who trades on toxic masculinity and dismisses bands led by women. The narrator mixes mock-praise with contempt, mourning wasted respect for real talent squandered on a fragile ego. Sarcastic and combative, it crowns the target a self-styled don while making clear that people will eventually call the bluff.","sonnet":"A direct callout of a misogynistic industry figure who dismissed female bands as cringe — the narrator had genuine respect for his artistry until \"deadly masculinity\" showed up in his toxicology report. The \"hang that name on every wall in the Hall of Fame\" is pure sarcasm: she's immortalising the exposure, not the achievement.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~cannibal":{"haiku":"Primal hunger and obsession transform desire into consumption; the speaker devours another to satisfy an insatiable appetite.","opus":"Desire pushed to voracious, devouring extremes, hunger for another body framed as literal cannibal appetite. The gleeful excess turns lust into a matter of survival, playfully monstrous.","sonnet":"An ode to consuming sexual desire framed as literal cannibalism — the speaker is driven by a primal, insatiable hunger to devour a partner physically and completely, drawing pleasure from every part of them with no apology for the ravenous appetite.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~cure-4-psycho":{"haiku":"Recognition that a manipulator is disordered and incurable; the speaker escapes and refuses to feed the abuser's ego anymore.","opus":"A survivor's kiss-off to a gaslighting narcissist who preaches mental health while inflicting harm, dressed in horror-movie swagger. The joy is in the diagnosis and the refusal to be a victim, turning the escape into a promise of a sequel.","sonnet":"A triumphant breakup song directed at a gaslighting narcissist — the narrator has finally named what happened to them, found vindication in recognizing the other person's pathology, and gleefully concludes there's no fixing someone like that, so letting go becomes easy.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~dead-walk":{"opus":"Guilt as a literal revenant, a buried secret clawing back out of the ground to bury its keeper. Redhook stages repression through horror-movie language, skeletons and necrotizing parasites standing in for a lie that won't stay dead. The real terror is that what's suppressed always returns to make the sufferer confront it.","sonnet":"About a buried secret — something the narrator did that they hoped was dead and gone — now resurrecting and haunting them like the undead, with the implication that past wrongdoings corrupt a person from the inside no matter how long they stay hidden.","src":"sonnet"},
"redhook~fake":{"haiku":"Denounces someone as a fraud whose false persona conceals predatory behavior and whose true colors emerge through betrayal and cruelty.","opus":"A takedown of a hypocritical public figure whose kind image is a cover, accusing them of performative virtue, stealing others' work, and being exposed as a phony once their true colors show.","sonnet":"The narrator calls out someone they once admired who turned out to be a fraud — performing values they don't hold, wearing social awareness as a costume — and declares there is no bridge left between them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about a toppled idol and the revulsion of watching worship sour into scorn — a fan's-eye demolition of someone whose public mask was forever a disguise. RedHook bring their early-2020s Sydney post-hardcore snap to it, the verses parsing a phony grin feature by feature: teeth too bright to be honest hiding what they conceal, crimson smeared across the hands, the early crush curdling once the invented backstory stopped holding up. The chorus twists the familiar saying against itself — the stream has run dry beneath that crossing, no forgiveness left — charging the target with cooking up a fresh cadence while pocketing somebody else's groove, plus a caution about whom you tread on once the planet lies at your soles. The second verse hardens into satire, costume-shop activism and convention-hall piety unmasked as sheer ego, and the pounding refrain delivers the sentence flatly: treat others as your slogan secretly betrays, and the fraud is you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"Emmy Mack has said it was a vent session written after a bad touring experience, an in-joke about phony scene people the band never planned to release; no target ever named.","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"worship turned to scorn as the constructed backstory collapses","built":"the familiar saying twisted against itself, the stream run dry beneath the crossing"},
"redhook~guerrilla-radio":{"haiku":"Taking over frequencies to broadcast unfiltered truth; hijacking media to expose corrupt power and free political prisoners.","opus":"The Australian band covers Rage's insurrectionary broadcast nearly line for line, keeping its assault on a rigged political spectacle where cameras disguise real choice and profiteers crave war spoils. The pirate signal, invoking Mumia Abu-Jamal and commandeered airwaves, becomes a weapon against manufactured consent. Its closing insistence that the revolt has to begin at once carries the original's urgency into a new voice.","sonnet":"A cover-version read that mirrors the original: seizing pirate broadcast frequencies to expose the collusion between media, capital, and state violence. The clarion call — start here, start now — insists that silence is complicity and the airwaves are a battleground.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~i-don-t-keep-up":{"opus":"An anthem of proud outsider status, the narrator an oddball caught between genres, too catchy for the heavy crowd and too heavy for the catchy one, refusing to fall in step with anyone. Redhook flips old shame into defiance, no longer craving normalcy. It's a celebration of being an anomaly, a misfit in the room who has quit apologizing for it.","sonnet":"About not fitting into mainstream culture or identity categories — the narrator embraces being a misfit too heavy for pop and too pop for heavy, finding peace in their own weirdness rather than wishing to conform.","src":"sonnet"},
"redhook~kamikaze":{"haiku":"Two damaged people recognize their shared trauma and choose a self-destructive collision course, unable or unwilling to escape their fate together.","opus":"A confrontation over shared damage that curdles into a contest of who is more broken, using a suicide plane locked on its target as a figure for two people bound in a doomed, unstoppable descent they can no longer pull out of. Australian alt-metal, all barbed pop hooks and mutual accusation.","sonnet":"Two people comparing the childhood traumas that left them numb and reckless, both aware they're heading toward mutual destruction but unable or unwilling to course-correct.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~low-budget-horror":{"haiku":"A person confronts an abuser who uses mental illness as excuse for their destructive behavior, rejecting their pathetic attempts at terror.","opus":"A furious clapback at an abuser who hides behind claims of being unwell, the narrator refusing to be terrorized any longer and mocking him as cheap slasher trash, unfit to scare anyone. Contempt hardens into a survivor's stand. Redhook are an Australian alt-metal band fronted by Emmy Mack, and this venomous, pop-sharp defiance laced with B-movie jokes is exactly their bratty, empowered attack.","sonnet":"A takedown of someone who used their own damage as a weapon and cast the speaker as the victim in a self-made horror movie. The speaker refuses the role — compares the antagonist to Troll 2, calls the terror derivative and threadbare, announces that the only one who will cry at the end of this cheap production is the one who made it.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~minute-on-fire":{"haiku":"A person watches their days drift into routine despair while an inner voice screams to burn it all down. They fantasize about one moment of pure intensity worth sacrificing everything for.","opus":"Redhook personifies dread as something lurking in the walls and a soulless cat that keeps replaying old mistakes, forever poised to wrap a grip around the throat. Against drifting, aimless days and a screaming inner voice, the chorus stages a burning wish to swap an entire existence for one brief blaze, dramatizing how numbness can make self-destruction feel like the only route back to sensation.","sonnet":"A person gripped by a suffocating inner darkness knows it is killing them yet cannot stop craving its intensity. The depression manifests as a shadow that strangles, as looping mistakes, as a voice screaming to burn — and still there is a reckless longing to feel that one blazing minute alive.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~off-with-your-head":{"haiku":"Mob mentality and self-righteous violence consume reason, creating a climate of fear where dissent is punished with crucifixion.","opus":"Rage at mob-justice culture, the pitchfork crowd who fix the rope before hearing a defense and shred anyone who disagrees. The Wonderland cry for a beheading becomes a portrait of a dread epidemic feeding a self-righteous witch hunt.","sonnet":"About mob justice and cancel culture as a new kind of authoritarian cruelty — the narrator is furious at self-righteous crowds who wave pitchforks, demand heads roll without hearing pleas, and build police-state dynamics around their hatred; anger is also turned inward for not speaking up sooner.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~only-bones":{"haiku":"A figure drains the speaker to the bone like a vampire and vulture. The speaker floats in anti-gravity, unable to escape, and loses all substance.","opus":"A metaphor for being emotionally consumed by a parasitic person, rendered as vampires and vultures bleeding the narrator dry and picking them down to only bones while they numbly insist it's fine. The spacey, airless, anti-gravity imagery captures dissociation and helplessness, until the final turn, calling the taker a selfish cannibal, flashes with anger at everything that was ripped away.","sonnet":"A song about being systematically drained by an exploitative relationship, using vampirism and predation as literal frames — the narrator offers a hollow, dissociated consent to being consumed until there is nothing left but bones.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~paralysed":{"haiku":"The speaker struggles with internal paralysis and illness despite fighting desperately for clarity. External voices amplify fear while they feel trapped in cycles of sickness and internal conflict they cannot escape.","opus":"Australian alt-metal band RedHook stage a panic attack as it happens, the narrator catching warning signals and sparks that scorch the edge of consciousness. Begging for a jolt or a tablet, they sense something screaming just out of reach while battling a sickness that keeps dragging them under. Boxed in by their own ego and running low on time, they stammer the title, teetering on the brink.","sonnet":"Internal illness — part psychological, part existential — immobilizes despite the will to fight; every direction spins, and something behind a closed door screams without ceasing. Pride and emptiness form a prison from which the only exit seems to be forcing oneself off the ledge and breathing.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~postcard-xo":{"haiku":"Failure and regret pool into self-hatred from a place of isolation, writing from a psychological hell of one's own making. The speaker drowns in guilt while longing for rescue that won't arrive.","opus":"Depression written as a postcard mailed from a personal hell, spiraling down the same rabbit hole of self-loathing, failure and drink. The cheerful greeting-card format sharpens the bleakness of feeling worthless and deserving of it.","sonnet":"A darkly ironic letter home from the rock bottom of self-loathing, drinking to drown failure, walking a dark alley home while praying to no one — the postcard-from-hell conceit frames recurring self-destruction as something grimly routine.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~say":{"haiku":"Manipulation disguises itself as charm while demanding compliance; authentic expression is suppressed through control.","opus":"A defiant blow against gaslighting and controlling voices that demand obedience. The narrator is handled like a puppet on a string, told to shut up and perform, her image deemed inconvenient. Framing the manipulation as the age-old story of the powerful feeding on the weak, she refuses to be split open to give an abuser what he is after.","sonnet":"A gaslighting figure who masks control as permission — say what you wanna say, as long as you agree — is addressed by someone who has been treated like a use-by-dated puppet on a string, cracked like a piñata for compliance, with the recognition that this is the same old boring story of bloodsuckers abusing power.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~sentimental-surgery":{"opus":"Depression handled with dark, self-mocking humor, the narrator branding herself a sad-song stereotype while worrying her low mood is a real clinical illness, not a passing mood. Redhook frames the wish for relief as an operation to cut out the pain, sitting uneasily between glamorizing death and knowing better.","sonnet":"About living with depression that the narrator fears might be clinically permanent rather than a phase — they want something or someone to open them up and drain the misery, self-aware enough to call themselves an emo cliché while still being unable to shake the weight.","src":"sonnet"},
"redhook~soju":{"haiku":"Resentment and suppressed anger fester beneath forced compliance; unspoken boundaries metastasize into silent rage.","opus":"A blast of pent-up resentment from an Australian metalcore band who trade in blunt catharsis. The narrator, drowning in therapy-speak memes about boundaries and people-pleasing, finally snaps at everyone who tells him how to fix himself. Swallowed anger becomes a private ritual he vows one day to voice aloud rather than choke back.","sonnet":"The gap between knowing what healthy limits are and actually being able to enforce them: memes say set boundaries and stop people-pleasing, the speaker agrees on paper, but the fuck yous stay swallowed down like soju instead of said out loud — the pushover trapped by her own difficulty saying no.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~turn-up-the-stereotype":{"haiku":"A person condemns toxic manipulators who feed on others' suffering and live inauthentic, clichéd lives.","opus":"An Australian band's takedown of the insecure bully who only feels real by belittling others, framing cruelty as a malfunctioning, viral sickness. The stereotype gets amplified and mocked back at the person weaponizing it.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who can only feel powerful by making others feel worthless — calling them a malfunction, rejecting their toxic script, and insisting your own flaws are at least genuinely yours.","src":"haiku"},
"redhook~your-heroes-are-bullshit":{"haiku":"A person is disillusioned by false heroes and meaningless systems, wrestling with mental anguish and apathy.","opus":"Disillusionment and depression turned defiant, refusing to worship idols while battling the monster in your own head. It rejects the demand to conform and shine on cue, owning the villain role rather than faking inspiration you don't feel.","sonnet":"A declaration of profound disillusionment — the narrator is numb, their inner monster won't quiet down, the heroes they once believed in are frauds, and if that makes them the villain, so be it.","src":"haiku"},
"red~infidel":{"opus":"A menacing, predatory address to a captive victim, the narrator relishing the desire to hear them scream, promising to purify and break them, framing dominance, fear and pain as inescapable.","sonnet":"A song of predatory sexual domination — narrated by a possessive, controlling figure who demands submission and screaming from a captive described as prey, framing the power dynamic as spiritual corruption with no ambiguity about the coercive nature of the relationship.","src":"sonnet"},
"red~not-alone":{"haiku":"Darkness and isolation yield to compassionate rescue; the listener promises unwavering presence and acceptance when despair threatens to overwhelm.","opus":"A Christian hard-rock band writing a suicide-watch hymn: the voice reaching down is God, but the band keeps the theology submerged so it plays as pure human vigil over someone at the edge, love promising to catch the fall.","sonnet":"A voice of absolute presence and rescue — framed as divine — speaks directly to someone on the edge of despair, promising unconditional support, to catch them when they fall, and to give love a face when all hope seems gone.","src":"haiku"},
"reebz~hallucination":{"haiku":"Fever dreams dissolve the speaker's grip on reality; pressure and nausea mount while consciousness fractures into visions of darker worlds and the self disintegrates from inside.","opus":"A body caught between exhaustion and dread, unable to sleep or wake, where a toxic attachment has become a fever dream the singer keeps trying to shake off. The person who cut them up dissolves into another symptom of a delirium they cannot escape.","sonnet":"A person in a feverish, dissociative state watches reality dissolve — nausea, failing vision, a delirium they cannot defeat — as the person or feeling they are losing slips away like a fever dream, pulling them down even as they fight to stay coherent.","src":"haiku"},
"reebz~thanatosis":{"haiku":"Prey instinct and paralyzed fear induce shutdown; the speaker plays dead to survive, trapped between vigilance and unconsciousness.","opus":"Fear so overwhelming it triggers a shutdown into playing dead, holding one's breath and hoping to go unnoticed while nightly terrors keep the mind cornered and awake.","sonnet":"About freezing in response to overwhelming fear — the speaker shuts down completely like an animal playing dead, paralyzed by dread that hunts them every night, unable to run, cry, or fight back.","src":"haiku"},
"reece-young~endless-riots":{"haiku":"Internal turmoil escalates into perpetual rebellion; the speaker's fractured thoughts riot endlessly while constructing meaning from accumulated nothing.","opus":"A mind at war with itself, making something out of nothing while defiance and depression riot behind a smiling face. Facing the silence is harder than the noise, and the fragile disaster keeps waiting for the fall.","sonnet":"About living with a mind in constant internal conflict — thoughts that riot endlessly, depression that makes the narrator see a stranger in the mirror, and a hunger for joy that is becoming an obsession. The song is about defining yourself by your refusal to give up even while knowing you are probably going to fall, making something out of nothing in the face of a relentless inner noise.","src":"haiku"},
"reflections~samsara":{"haiku":"A soul decays as summer turns to winter overnight and blue skies fade to gray. The speaker loses identity but finally finds their heart beneath the scars of a broken counterpart.","opus":"A breakup imagined as inner rot and dissolution, the narrator falling apart and sifting through the ruin to recover his own sense of self while telling his ex to move on. The title's Buddhist cycle of suffering suits the sense of endless loss. Reflections are an American progressive metalcore band pairing heavy, technical riffing with this kind of raw confession.","sonnet":"A breakup that has hollowed out the self completely — summer turning to winter in a day, blue skies gone gray, no identity left once the other person was removed. The grief cycles: decay, distance, the cruel instruction to let go so the other person can maintain their hold. But buried under the loss, beneath the broken parts, the speaker finds something that was always theirs to begin with.","src":"haiku"},
"refused~born-on-the-outs":{"haiku":"A speaker rejects moderate political positions, arguing that silence enables oppression and that only radical action resists rising fascism.","opus":"The reunited Swedish hardcore radicals skewer political moderation as complicity, arguing there is no neutral ground while the world burns and that fence-sitting amid fascism is itself an endorsement of oppression through silence. The narrator embraces outsider status as a matter of principle, insisting no nation is legitimate until it belongs to all its people.","sonnet":"A furious rebuke of political moderates who stay neutral as fascism escalates. The speaker, born an outsider, argues that silence and both-sides equivocation are themselves acts of endorsement, and carves his rage into the grave of moderation as a permanent declaration.","src":"haiku"},
"refused~cheap":{"haiku":"Society sells false promises and cheap solutions that create dependency, making people sick first then profiting from the cure.","opus":"A hardcore polemic against consumer capitalism and manufactured dependence, indicting a system that keeps you ill in order to sell you the remedy, then floods you with worthless promises and pretexts and trains you to accept ever less until you accept nothing. The throughline: you were bargained away long ago, made reliant on the very forces that wrecked you.","sonnet":"A hardcore political attack on systems that manufacture dependency and then sell cheap remedies — governments, corporations, or institutions that make people sick, provide watered-down cures, and sell broken promises until nothing means anything and the living are simply lost.","src":"haiku"},
"refused~dawkins-christ":{"haiku":"A speaker rejects all religious frameworks and salvation myths, seeing only belief and doubt leading to death with no escape or redemption.","opus":"Refused stage a furious theological reckoning, cataloguing how believers across Hinduism, Buddhism, Zoroastrianism and Lutheranism each find comfort while the narrator, denied even a soul to trade, is barred from any deliverance. Fusing Judas, Nietzsche and Dawkins into an emblem of militant unbelief, the song names an atheism that still aches at the empty space where God was supposed to be.","sonnet":"A sardonic tour of humanity's need for faith — across Christianity, Hinduism, Buddhism, Zoroastrianism — in contrast with a speaker who finds himself without a soul to sell or a creed to shelter under. Doubt and mortality fill the god-shaped hole that religion promises to close.","src":"haiku"},
"refused~elektra":{"haiku":"Despite surface progress and monuments to achievement, nothing fundamental has changed in human nature or social hierarchy; the masses still suffer and chaos persists unchanged.","opus":"A bleak view of futile revolt. Corpses are piled into monuments and walls are scaled in blind panic, yet down in the mud not one thing has shifted, and there is no way out once the reckoning arrives. Swedish hardcore turning defiance into despair.","sonnet":"Despite grand monuments built on violence and the chaos of panic spreading through a crowd, nothing fundamentally changes at ground level — the song insists that systemic oppression persists unchanged beneath the surface spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"refused~i-wanna-watch-the-world-burn":{"haiku":"Revolutionary anger at systemic corruption and desire to burn down unjust values; the speaker seeks redemption and renewal through destruction.","opus":"Swedish hardcore firebrands Refused turn political fury into a metaphor of purifying destruction, believing renewal can only rise from what's razed. Beneath the rage sits a hope: dismantling a hollow, greedy order to plant something fairer. The desire to see it all burn is aimed at what the world, and everyone in it, has become.","sonnet":"Rage at what civilization has become — its accumulated injustices, its corrupted values — resolves into a vision of controlled burning: destroy the old structures so something level and new can grow from the ash. The fury is political but also deeply personal, felt rather than merely argued.","src":"haiku"},
"refused~liberation-frequency":{"opus":"A radical demand to seize the airwaves and wrest media back from corporate hands. The band asks on which band of the dial freedom might broadcast, insisting on signals made by and for ordinary people rather than the same sanitized product piped down from above.","sonnet":"A punk demand to reclaim the airwaves from corporate and state control — the song asks whether what's broadcast to us is liberation or just another form of detention, and insists the people deserve not just airtime but total control of transmission.","src":"sonnet"},
"refused~new-noise":{"haiku":"Artistic authenticity demands breaking free from conformity and consumerism, requiring genuinely new voices rather than old formulas.","opus":"Refused's call to tear down safe, commodified art and make something that actually moves people. Its fury is at complacency, at dancing to the wrong songs while pretending that is leadership.","sonnet":"A manifesto against cultural passivity — the song argues that mainstream art and music serve the interests of money and power, that those without access are locked out, and that the only genuine response is to make something radically new rather than continuing to dance to songs designed for someone else's benefit.","src":"haiku"},
"refused~refused-are-fucking-dead":{"opus":"A hardcore band turning its scalpel on scene fashion and its own myth, mocking new romantics who express rebellion in loud, marketable ways. Refused skewers revolution reduced to catchphrase and pose, impatient with posturing. The title's self-burial is the point: a refusal to become the very product a subculture sells.","sonnet":"A post-punk critique of a music scene that has become fashionable theater — the narrator skewers the 'new romantics' who perform revolution loudly but have no patience for the actual work, expressing a disgust for imitation and posturing dressed up as rebellion.","src":"sonnet"},
"refused~summerholidays-vs-punkroutine":{"haiku":"Conflict between youthful idealism and practical compromise; refusing to sacrifice authenticity for status. The song warns against conformity, fear of failure, and the paralysis of doing nothing.","opus":"A manifesto against selling out, torn between comfortable adulthood and the punk ideals that motivate more than any paycheck, insisting on trying rather than dying, and choosing to be forgotten over remembered for giving in. Refused, the Swedish hardcore radicals, put this on 1998's The Shape of Punk to Come, an album that blew up hardcore's template just as the band imploded; the fear of accomplishing nothing is its beating anxiety.","sonnet":"Stuck between the comfort of selling out and the integrity of staying broke and committed — the speaker is tired of a childhood dream of rebellion that costs money he doesn't have, while the corrupt icons who stole the aesthetic got rich. The resolution is bleak and defiant: better to be forgotten than to be remembered for giving in, even if giving in is all anyone manages.","src":"haiku"},
"refused~tannh-user-deriv":{"haiku":"Urban wandering becomes spiritual quest where displacement opens possibility and every corner yields revelation through disorientation and surrender to chance.","opus":"Situationist restlessness turned anthem: the city as playground, aimless wandering reframed as radical refusal of boredom and routine. The demand for the impossible as a way to feel alive against dead time.","sonnet":"A call to reclaim the city through movement and disorientation — treating every street as an adventure, every decision as a path into the unknown — driven by the conviction that boredom is the real enemy and that demanding the impossible is a valid political and personal stance.","src":"haiku"},
"refused~the-deadly-rhythm":{"haiku":"Worker exploitation and the dehumanizing industrial production line. A call to break free from mandatory labor and refuse the false promise that work brings freedom.","opus":"A labor anthem against factory drudgery, picturing workers stripped of power and union protection, their lives sold off cheaply, rejecting the lie that toil brings freedom and vowing to organize instead. Refused channel their Marxist politics through The Shape of Punk to Come's genre-shattering hardcore, the mechanical pulse of the assembly line mirrored in the song's relentless drive.","sonnet":"An industrial-era indictment of labor as coercion — unions stripped of power, workers auctioned off cheaply, lives consumed on the production line while they're told to be grateful. A sequence of rhetorical questions: is dying for governments our duty, slaving for industry our privilege, staying in line our right? The answer is organized refusal, a declaration that working for the system will never set anyone free.","src":"haiku"},
"refused~the-shape-of-punk-to-come":{"opus":"Refused's landmark call to arms rails at a generation dressed for a revolt with nowhere to stage it, beaten down and told to stay small. The band demands punk stop recycling televised, smashed-guitar theatrics and instead deliver real liberation through music, a riot that carries meaning rather than empty spectacle.","sonnet":"A manifesto for a punk movement that has energy but nowhere to direct it — the excitement of youth culture and rebellion collides with the reality that the counterculture has been beaten down and co-opted, leaving everyone dressed up with no destination.","src":"sonnet"},
"refused~violent-reaction":{"opus":"Rage aimed squarely at capitalism and manufactured desire, the narrator refusing to be a pawn seduced by a swindler pushing debt and fake cravings. Refused's reunion-era fury calls to raze everything and rebuild it, casting the self as an eruption of fury against a world rotted by greed and conformity.","sonnet":"A declaration of total systemic rejection — the narrator identifies themselves as the product of being exploited, debt-trapped, and fed false values by a greedy ruling structure, and responds with a call to tear it all down and rebuild from nothing.","src":"sonnet"},
"refused~worms-of-the-senses-faculties-of-the-skull":{"opus":"A furious indictment of commodified life, insisting human existence is not statistics while ridiculing alienated labor that produces nothing. Refused's opening salvo rejects a world branded by corporations, longing to escape the Coca-Cola city entirely. Beneath the noise burns a demand that dignity refuse to be bought, sold or made-believe.","sonnet":"A furious anti-capitalist broadside — the narrator condemns a system that commodifies human life, alienates workers, blames the poor, and sells boredom as entertainment, before escaping the corporate-branded city by bus.","src":"sonnet"},
"regurgitator~blubber-boy":{"haiku":"Dissolution and shapelessness define existence as identity melts away completely, seeking rebirth through repeated cycles of destruction and resurrection.","opus":"A grotesque, absurdist love song sung by a melting blob of a lover who begs to be rubbed, buried and mourned, promising to melt away and come back again.","sonnet":"A figure of grotesque dissolution — melted by the sun, drowned in the sea — asks to be rubbed, buried, and mourned, offering himself as something utterly consumed and shapeless by love, with the cycle able to begin again if he is touched enough to reconstitute.","src":"haiku"},
"regurgitator~the-song-formerly-known-as":{"haiku":"Social anxiety and sensory overload make connection's deception visible; disco lights hurt while solitary dancing with one other feels authentic.","opus":"A homebody's anti-anthem, rejecting parties, clubs, and raves because crowds and noise overwhelm him, preferring to dance badly in a suburban lounge room with the one person he loves. Contentment is found in ordinary intimacy over scene and spectacle.","sonnet":"A homebody who avoids parties, concerts, and raves because crowds, bright lights, and cigarettes unsettle them chooses to dance in ugly pants in a suburban lounge room with one person instead, concluding that nothing gets better than this.","src":"haiku"},
"relentless-3~line-in-the-sand":{"haiku":"The speaker draws a final boundary after years of collecting scars, turning inward to find the light within while rejecting external judgment and pain. This is the moment of standing ground and refusing to retreat.","opus":"A hard rock declaration of self-liberation after betrayal. Years of wandering, disillusionment and a heart turned to stone give way to a deliberate refusal to look back. The narrator burns off old mistakes, casts out the person who cost him warmth, and plants a boundary that ends the exhausting cycle for good.","sonnet":"A survivor of a betrayal-filled darkness declares their boundary: they've burned the mistakes, collected scars in a sea of boredom, but now draw a line in the sand and walk away from the pain, moving forward day by day without looking back.","src":"haiku"},
"reliqa~i-don-t-know-what-i-am":{"haiku":"Possession and dissolution obscure identity; the speaker exists outside themselves, haunted from within, unable to locate the authentic self.","opus":"A dissociative unraveling where a stranger looks back from the mirror and a possessing presence feels like the most precious thing she's ever known, watching herself from beyond her own body and finally, strangely, feeling free.","sonnet":"A fractured identity crisis told through the lens of dissociation — the narrator encounters a version of herself in the mirror that feels alien and untameable, something that poured out of her like wine from a broken vessel and now rides her like a ghost. The emotional core is the terrifying pleasure of losing control of who you are: the thing possessing her will surely destroy her, but it also feels like the first real freedom she has ever known.","src":"haiku"},
"reliqa~mr-magic":{"haiku":"A powerful, deceptive authority figure exploits the vulnerable; the speaker rejects illusion and refuses idolatry.","opus":"The Australian progressive band builds a parable around a gleaming charlatan whose spotless hands let him peddle illusions and claim dominion over others' lives. The narrator refuses to worship him, insisting on standing their own ground and vowing to see through the con, promising that once his luster fades and his fortune runs dry, they'll watch him collapse at the first real setback.","sonnet":"A powerful manipulator holds everyone's lives in one hand while keeping his own untouched. The speaker sees through the illusions and refuses to be a devotee — rejecting the rigged game where the powerful sacrifice others and survive unscathed, demanding accountability when the throne finally crumbles.","src":"haiku"},
"reliqa~terminal":{"haiku":"Self-sabotage and naive hope intertwine; the speaker chases control they cannot possess, slipping farther from home with each step forward.","opus":"Fighting the pull to lose oneself in a comfortable, familiar destructiveness, raging against self-hatred for what one has created. The struggle is against the compulsion to control that leaves the narrator feeling like nothing without it.","sonnet":"The speaker, addicted to what they can't control, rages against their own self-destructive naivety — 'time sinks its teeth into my bones, I'm slipping further away from home' — swallowing too much smoke while chasing ghosts, unable to love the light they've set fire to.","src":"haiku"},
"reliqa~the-bearer-of-bad-news":{"haiku":"A messenger figure claims cosmic power and knowledge of fate, offering false salvation while subtly controlling others' choices and stealing their autonomy.","opus":"Voiced by a reaper-like figure who claims power over life, death, and fate, the song offers a doomed soul lifetimes and salvation on grim terms, delivering bad news while inviting them to become endless like the speaker.","sonnet":"An omnipotent, supernatural figure offers a child-like martyr impossible futures and power while positioning itself as both saviour and corruptor, able to harvest voices and control choices — a dark benefactor whose gifts come with total subjugation.","src":"haiku"},
"reliqa~the-halfway-point":{"haiku":"Divided consciousness and descent into darkness; halfway between states of being and falling into hell. A parasite-like force consumes and transforms identity.","opus":"A mind split down the middle, caught dividing itself, half-asleep no longer, digging halfway down a hole toward hell in search of faith while pressure builds and the world distorts. The doubling and division become a self-imposed prison the narrator claws to escape. Reliqa, the Australian progressive metal band led by Monica Strut, blend clean melody, rap cadence, and heaviness into exactly this shape-shifting, identity-fracturing drama.","sonnet":"A mind dividing against itself — caught between two versions, neither fully in control, digging down toward something darker while the outside world bets on collapse. The speaker isn't asleep anymore, which means seeing clearly how far down the hole goes and how the pressure of that division is reshaping them. Something was done to them; it matters less now than what's left after.","src":"haiku"},
"remember-the-monsters~sink":{"opus":"A bitter reckoning with a toxic partner, the narrator clawing back to the surface while wishing his tormentor under. Blame and betrayal curdle into vengeful wanting, the imagery of drowning turned against the one who hollowed him out, bittersweet poison traded back through burning bridges.","sonnet":"A post-betrayal song where the narrator, having been repeatedly manipulated and gaslit, finally decides to stop holding themselves up and instead watch the person who wronged them go under — the image of 'sink for me' inverts the usual drowning metaphor, with the narrator coldly waiting for the other's collapse rather than seeking rescue.","src":"sonnet"},
"rendezvous-point~digital-waste":{"haiku":"People search for identity and meaning in social media and digital platforms, trapped in systems of greed and boredom.","opus":"The Norwegian progressive-metal band critique lives lived through screens, where worth is reduced to numbers, connection is a performance, and everyone quietly accepts a culture of greed. Chasing approval and hunting for meaning in the glow of a phone, the narrator's urge to run away exposes how fragile and self-inflicted this digital existence really is.","sonnet":"A critique of lives outsourced to screens — memories stored in fragile formats, worth measured in abstract metrics, hours dissolved into white noise while searching for meaning that the platform was never designed to provide. The impulse is to run, leave no trace, and abandon a world everyone built together and no one controls.","src":"haiku"},
"rendezvous-point~universal-chaos":{"haiku":"Existential disintegration and loss of control; the speaker loses their bearings as someone promised fades away, leaving only panic and the sensation of falling into abyss. Abandonment is total.","opus":"The Norwegian prog band stages a panic attack as cosmic freefall, the narrator adrift and unsure whether he's cracking up or dying, lying exposed on the ground while his past fades. Abandoned by someone who swore to stay, he loses his grip again, collapse rendered as a dizzying loop that keeps recurring.","sonnet":"A person stripped bare and alone after an abandonment moves through weightlessness and panic — fingernails on concrete, a face fading from memory — unable to determine whether what follows is death or just the loss of everything that made life feel continuous. The chaos is sudden and total, and the question of whether one can survive it remains genuinely open.","src":"haiku"},
"renounced~the-rico-conspiracy":{"haiku":"Victims of manufactured perfection and social media lies; beauty is erased by systemic conditioning that makes people rot from filtered unreality.","opus":"A wounded love letter to someone drowning in self-hatred and the airbrushed lies of magazine culture, the narrator insisting she is beautiful while watching her light dim, ruined by an industry where idols betray their fans and endless scrolling corrodes the mind. Renounced are a British metalcore and melodic hardcore band, and this collision of tenderness and social critique, delivered in breathless run-on lines, fits their emotionally raw, politically aware attack.","sonnet":"A desperate appeal to someone who hates their own reflection, told they are genuinely beautiful while the system that made them feel otherwise — filtered magazines, social media dopamine loops — is called out as synthetic and rotting. Short and blunt: the light that was in them has gone out, and someone saw it happen and couldn't stop it.","src":"haiku"},
"reol~a-16vob7f":{"haiku":"A cryptic folk sermon on transience and devotion—beauty fades, the world is cruel, but only trusting in someone matters.","opus":"Reol rose from Vocaloid-scene virtuosity into a solo artist fusing rapid-fire wordplay with dense production, and here she channels old Edo's floating world. Battered and magnificent, the narrator leads a beloved through a transient, resentful realm where trends rise and fall and everyone suffers, insisting that drinking, dancing, and mutual trust are the only salvation, a resilience learned precisely because the world is miserable.","sonnet":"A song set in the floating world of old Edo pleasure culture — the narrator, battered and magnificent, leads a beloved person through a transient, resentful world where trends rise and fall and everyone suffers, insisting that the only salvation is drinking, dancing, and trusting each other through all of it. The emotional core is the resilience of those who have learned to shine precisely because the world is miserable.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~beautiful-hell":{"haiku":"Trapped in psychological poison and lost in self-created suffering, the speaker tries to transform the nightmare into beauty while questioning reality and seeking escape.","opus":"A depiction of mental illness or addiction as a self-poisoning inner jungle, a maze with no exit that the speaker reframes as beautiful to survive it. The daydream, the chrysalis he invites another into, and the effort to tame the poison show someone romanticizing his own suffering because it's all he has.","sonnet":"A person so corroded by inner poison and self-alienation that their own mind has become a jungle of scars, yet they refuse to abandon the painful inner world, trying instead to tame it and make something beautiful out of the suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~between-me-and-the-machine":{"haiku":"A doomed space voyage becomes metaphor for inevitable failure and suffering, where there is no escape, only the distance between self and machinery dwindling.","opus":"Resolve, a French post-hardcore and metalcore band, showcase their cinematic, atmospheric sound on this spacefaring farewell. Astronauts drift past the point of no return as artificial gravity dies and the green glow of Earth's skies disappears, the narrator holding a loved one's image as his only bearing while praying for a fatal impact to end the ordeal, cosmic isolation rendered as grief.","sonnet":"A space odyssey that doubles as psychological collapse — neon hallways, the blue of Earth gone black, no way back, no direction left. Memory of a loved one becomes the only anchor as everything else fails: emerald skies gone, gasoline smell missed, the machine that once carried the speaker now just a rusty wreck drifting into oblivion with an apology for how it all went wrong.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~comfortably-dumb":{"haiku":"Willing ignorance and chosen numbness as comfort, the speaker preferring stupor to consciousness.","opus":"The French metalcore band frames addiction as a numbing ritual, spinning off a famous Pink Floyd title. Endless rounds of drinking blur the line between feeling like a conqueror and a shipwreck, between living hard and slowly dying. The narrator resurrects each morning only to consume again, a bottomless pit past caring about anyone's sympathy.","sonnet":"A narrator oscillates between self-destruction and self-awareness — blue pill, red pill, shots lined up, the question of whether he's king of the world or a lonely shipwreck — and settles into the role of his own persecutor, swimming in the nectar and rejecting sympathy: comfortably dumb, bottomless, not yet surfacing.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~d-g-g-r-s":{"haiku":"Humanity clings to toxic outdated ideas, creating self-imposed slavery and destruction; only radical change can reverse inevitable collapse.","opus":"The French band frame modern society as progress running in reverse, chasing endless growth while wounding itself and enslaving people willingly to outdated dogma and self-styled rulers. Raging against doom-mongering fatalism and those who scorn the young trying to fix things, it casts the present as a spectacle heading toward a final, self-caused collapse.","sonnet":"Civilisational self-indictment: humanity clinging to ancient dogma and self-made hierarchies while growth accelerates past the planet's capacity to absorb it. The hourglass has run out, the season is final, and the collective refusal to course-correct reads as species-level suicide.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~death-awaits":{"haiku":"Acceptance and paralysis at mortality's threshold; the speaker watches fate approach while undecided between flight and submission.","opus":"Metalcore dread staged as end-times paranoia. At the threshold and unable to make peace with what's coming, the narrator can't tell whether the world is truly ending or he's been trapped in his own mind too long — biblical plagues bleeding into private torment as he becomes predator and quarry at once.","sonnet":"Death at the gates, a world faded to black through some combination of virus, stroke, or being lost too long in one's own head — paranoia from dancing shadows, the blaze to be killed and the breath to catch. The central question is whether to live like the rest of them or stay the protagonist of a disappearing story.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~emerald-skies":{"haiku":"Imprisonment by grief and memory; a forgotten prisoner endures eternal captivity sustained by fragile hope that the outside world remembers them.","opus":"A dystopian love letter from someone buried alive in an underground bunker after some cataclysm, clinging to a vanished partner's face as his only solace against a numbing artificial reality. The French band frame survival as its own slow imprisonment, begging only to be remembered.","sonnet":"About imprisonment — possibly literal, possibly psychological — where the narrator marks days on a cell wall, clings to the memory of someone's face as his only hope, and gives up on seeing daylight again while asking only to be remembered.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~forever-yours":{"haiku":"The speaker remains trapped by internal demons and decay, unable to heal or escape despite longing for freedom and brightness.","opus":"This French post-hardcore band map addiction as a self-devouring loop, the narrator nourishing the very demons that consume him and pleading to escape the cage he built while time keeps slipping. Doubtful he'll ever recover, he drifts toward grim acceptance that the disease may own him for good, surrender shading into resignation.","sonnet":"Self-disgust and addiction rendered as a sealed room — hands tied, rotting inside, feeding demons that feed back on the speaker. The clock keeps running, the infection feels permanent, the filth no perfume can cover. The final turn is strange and almost peaceful: accepting the dissonance, no longer feeling guilty, becoming forever yours to the very disease that wouldn't let go.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~move-to-trash":{"haiku":"Digital alienation and self-doubt collide; the speaker erases themselves piecemeal.","opus":"Creative self-loathing rendered as deleting files, the artist dumping his own drafts into the flames, his mind muddled, his sanity spent, hardened into something reptilian from overwork. It captures the perfectionist's spiral where pushing too fiercely frightens you off attempting anything at all. French metalcore turning studio despair into a hook.","sonnet":"Creative self-destruction as a coping strategy — a musician in a cycle of doubt and self-loathing uses the metaphor of deleting corrupted files to describe wanting to erase everything about themselves and start clean, though the cycle of eat-sleep-doubt-repeat keeps spinning.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~of-silk-and-straw":{"haiku":"A critique of class hierarchy and human arrogance, asserting that wealth and birth status are illusions since death and the earth level all people regardless of station.","opus":"A blistering statement on class and death, insisting rich and poor share the same body and the same grave no matter their station. The band condemn the hollow arrogance of anonymous power brokers who manipulate from the shadows, reminding them that wealth buys no exemption from a common end.","sonnet":"A direct confrontation with class arrogance: despite being born into different circumstances — silk or straw, heir or farmer's son — all people share the same flesh and the same final destination, while those who pull strings from silver towers will ultimately face judgment alongside everyone else.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~older-days":{"haiku":"Descent into shadow continues despite warning, each mistake deepening the contract with darkness. The speaker bargains away future self in exchange for momentary presence in chaos.","opus":"A man in his early thirties caught in a two-faced war with the darkness in him, writing a devotion to the shadow he can't stop feeding. Longing for the past hides a fear of actually living, hooked on the depths even as he begs to be hauled back out.","sonnet":"A letter to the evil within, addressed by a 33-year-old who is dishonest with himself, acting like a wolf but knowing he is a rat, simultaneously afraid to live and unafraid to die — the bargain with the devil in his mind keeps leading him deeper while he longs to go back to who he was.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~pendulum":{"haiku":"A person paralyzes themselves with overthinking and fear, watching time slip away while wasted years and emotional corruption leave them unsure where to go next.","opus":"Resolve anatomize the paralysis of chronic overthinking. The narrator isn't scared of death so much as of ceasing to matter, trapped rehearsing the past while time slips away and endlessly analyzing his own analysis. The corrosive core is that anxiety has eaten his drive, that some part of himself needs to be destroyed, and he's lost track of where he even belongs.","sonnet":"The terror of wasted time — not fear of death itself but of ceasing to exist, of being corroded from within by one's own soul, of having both feet stuck in sand while the hourglass runs, and not knowing where to go once the poison of stagnation has spread too far.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~seasick-sailor":{"haiku":"Struggle against internal darkness and suicidal urges through determined perseverance. The narrator fights against the tide of despair to reach solid ground.","opus":"Resolve cast the fight against despair as a lone, nauseated mariner steering through a tempest and past lurking sea-monsters toward land. Having lost every old certainty, he lashes together his own makeshift float, refuses to go under, and learns to keep struggling forward even when no shore is visible.","sonnet":"A song about persisting through a prolonged personal crisis using the sustained metaphor of a storm-tossed sailor — battered, lost, lungs full of salt water — who keeps building his own raft and refuses to sink, trusting that the shore is reachable.","src":"haiku"},
"resolve~sing-me-to-sleep":{"opus":"A hypnotic pull toward the unknown brink of death, staged as a beckoning melody that swells louder with every year. The French band renders mortality as both lure and vow, a tune lodged in the head, gift and curse entwined, as the narrator drives half-asleep toward whatever lies beyond.","sonnet":"A song about being seduced by the idea of death while still remaining uncertain — the narrator hears a melody that sounds like a siren song drawing them toward an edge, driving along a cliff fast asleep in the passenger seat. By the end they've learned to dream in colors and feel stronger for confronting the whisper, without fully knowing what's on the other side.","src":"sonnet"},
"resolve~surrender":{"opus":"A comeback anthem from rock bottom, the narrator cutting ties with his past and torching the proof of it, then resolving to reclaim what defines him. He turns his failures into fuel, building himself back up and marching toward a fresh start out of the wreckage.","sonnet":"A song about the painful freedom of burning one's bridges and choosing the unknown — the narrator has eliminated the old path, fled into the dark, and now builds something from the ashes while refusing to call it failure. The emotional core is grim optimism: dawn always follows the darkest night, and the pain will eventually prove meaningful.","src":"sonnet"},
"resolve~terra-mater":{"haiku":"Mother Earth is cursed by humanity's greed and waste poured into her veins. Nature always wins in the end no matter what we do to destroy it.","opus":"The French metalcore band voices the Earth herself as a poisoned, violated mother, dug up and disfigured while her waste is pumped back into her veins like a numbing drug. Resolve turn ecological grievance into body horror, letting nature deliver the closing verdict that no matter how thoroughly she is despoiled, she outlasts her despoilers.","sonnet":"Earth speaks as a violated mother, her body poisoned and dismembered by human greed. She charts the arc from original purity to toxic ruin, warning that despite all the damage inflicted upon her, nature ultimately outlasts every civilization built on her bones.","src":"haiku"},
"reto~bmw":{"haiku":"High-speed recklessness and automotive power become metaphors for chasing status and adrenaline while outrunning deeper emptiness and consequence.","opus":"A Polish rap flex built entirely around fast cars and the BMW as status and escape, women drawn to the M-package. Beneath the horsepower boasting sits a working-class hustle where money is the only real way to get out.","sonnet":"A Polish rap song about aggressive, high-speed driving — the narrator describes weaving through traffic, burning clutch instead of tires, outrunning cops by bribing them with cash in the license, and the car's V8 power as a form of self-expression and escape. Women light up when they see the M-Package; the BMW is status, freedom, and identity compressed into four wheels.","src":"haiku"},
"returning-we-hear-the-larks~thousand-arms-fortress":{"haiku":"An ancient, monstrous force devours all in its path, embodying ceaseless rage and incomprehensible power beyond mortal logic.","opus":"A monstrous, eternal force personified: a living fortress of countless grasping limbs that was never born and will never rest. It's a metal deification of unbound rage, an existential abomination beyond mortal comprehension.","sonnet":"A mythological entity narrates its own existence: infinite, timeless, beyond logic, never born, possessing a thousand limbs that never rest — a living fortress of pure force and rage that is alpha and omega, an \"endless apocalypse\" made flesh.","src":"haiku"},
"revaira~reborn-soul":{"haiku":"Existential invisibility defines the speaker's isolation, where existence itself becomes irrelevant to those around them. Darkness is not metaphor but destiny, a shadow cast without light.","opus":"A brief, aching fragment of invisibility and longing. The narrator hunts for somewhere his life might finally count, going unnoticed round the clock, so faint that nobody registers whether he is in the way. Beneath the metalcore heft sits a plea against a fate of darkness and being overlooked.","sonnet":"A speaker seeks a place where their existence has meaning, confessing invisibility and indifference from the world around them while moving toward a 'destiny of dark' — a short lyric of alienation and the search for belonging.","src":"haiku"},
"revnoir~20mg":{"haiku":"Chemical escape and existential drowning intertwine as the speaker oscillates between death wish and survival instinct, medication offering bliss that morphs into deeper hell.","opus":"A harrowing account of medicating panic and suicidal despair with pills, counting days of bliss and hell, drowning as if in an endless desert of sand. A furious rejection of anyone calling it just a phase gives way to a fragile rescue by someone who refused to give up on him.","sonnet":"A first-person account of being trapped in a pharmaceutical haze: the speaker maps the hours of bliss and hell on a numbered scale, drowns in a dust ocean, relives nightmares as the only rest available, and finally breaks through — at last saved, heart light — after someone refused to give up on them.","src":"haiku"},
"revnoir~bang-bang":{"haiku":"Violence and gunplay, literal or metaphorical shooting down of enemies.","opus":"A harrowing metalcore confrontation with mental illness, abuse and the voices that pull the narrator both under and back to life. Trapped in a cycle where cruelty feels like the only thing that makes him feel, he begs to be saved from ghosts and himself. Pulling the trigger becomes a metaphor for ending the curse.","sonnet":"Trauma and suicidal ideation locked in a cycle with voices that alternately drag down and pull alive — the narrator oscillates between begging to be saved and daring the abuser to push the trigger, until red and blue lights (police? hallucination?) close in and the BANG resolves nothing. The whole song is a body shaking like a fire you couldn't hold in.","src":"haiku"},
"revnoir~invincible":{"haiku":"Psychological warfare and buried trauma surface, demanding transformation from victim to voice as the only path to reclaiming power and freedom.","opus":"Trauma reframed as combat: the tormentor haunts sleep like a stalker, and the only way out is to stop being prey and become the threat. Finding one's voice is the weapon that turns victim into something invincible.","sonnet":"A song about reclaiming power after being silenced and controlled by someone — the narrator has lived muted by another person's hands, and now fights back by speaking out loud, insisting that voicing the pain is what makes you invincible, and turning from prey to threat.","src":"haiku"},
"rezn~possession":{"haiku":"Delirium and fear intertwine as the narrator surrenders to inner darkness and hallucination. The embrace of terror and possession suggests a twisted acceptance of psychological dissolution as inevitable and almost desired.","opus":"A hallucinatory drift through fever-dream and inner dark, chasing a guttering flame that's doomed to gutter out. Fear itself turns strangely sublime as the mind sinks deeper into deranged reverie.","sonnet":"A dissolution into hallucinatory fever-dream interiority — the speaker follows an inner gaze through emptiness, chases a faded flame, and finds that fear feels not threatening but sublime, as pale eyes and an empty cup mark the total surrender to what lives deep in the mind.","src":"haiku"},
"rezn~wake-far-out-version":{"haiku":"Death grants perspective beyond ego; the speaker wakes from illusion to cosmic vastness where identity dissolves into void.","opus":"A psychedelic near-death or ego-death trip. The narrator comes to inside a casket and floats free of his body while a voice urges him to settle his accounts with the departed. Selfhood is something you can never grasp within, only catch as a reflection scattered across the night sky as the ego dissolves.","sonnet":"An out-of-body death experience or psychedelic dissolution: watching their own body below from a coffin floating above the world, the speaker is instructed to let ego melt and look to the stars for identity — 'you'll never find out who you are, you'll only see it in the stars' offers release rather than loss.","src":"haiku"},
"rezodrone~believer":{"haiku":"Violent thoughts and paranoia haunt the speaker as they oscillate between self-destruction and messianic delusion, sacrificing their identity in pursuit of twisted redemption and power.","opus":"Wracked by paranoia and lurid visions, met with a messianic promise to become someone's savior and cut through the lies deceiving them. Trading flesh for chrome perfection, it's pain that's said to reveal the real self.","sonnet":"A figure haunted by violent visions and paranoia offers themselves as saviour and guide, sacrificing their own skin for \"shining chrome perfection\" and demanding the trust of others so they can lead them away from deceivers.","src":"haiku"},
"rezodrone~resist-and-disorder":{"opus":"A song voiced by a demonic figure of ruin and control who claims to hold all the keys, reduce the world to ash, and rule who lives or dies, jeering at listeners to rise up in the middle of the wreckage he creates.","sonnet":"Narrated from the perspective of a demonic controlling force — an entity that claims to hold all power, creates discord and suffering, and dares its victims to rise up and resist, presented as an anthem of malevolent dominion.","src":"sonnet"},
"rezz-pvris~sacrificial-feat-pvris":{"haiku":"Divine corruption and dangerous love create a pact between two souls willing to sacrifice everything for connection. Selling one's soul becomes the price of ecstasy in a relationship that feels both heavenly and damned.","opus":"A love treated as a bargain struck with the devil, worth any sacrifice, willingly risking downfall and damnation for the beloved. Heaven or hellfire hardly matters as long as it's endured for you.","sonnet":"Two people in a relationship that feels like a deal with the devil declare their love a sacrifice — the weight of it is both overwhelming and willingly given, and whether they reach heaven or dance in hellfire they would give it all up again for each other.","src":"haiku"},
"rezz~blue-in-the-face":{"haiku":"Desperation suffocates until consciousness dims; pleas fall silent in suffocating absence.","opus":"The Canadian producer builds a menacing electronic track around a figure caught in a toxic power dynamic. Contempt curdles into something violent, a wish to see a controlling abuser gone for good. The clipped, hypnotic refrain about getting wrecked mirrors a mind spiraling between disgust and a craving to lose itself.","sonnet":"A power-trip abuser is addressed with lethal precision — crawling through a city of snakes, hands on a body until it's blue in the face — and the speaker wishes him underground in a hardwood case; the desire to be fucked up and the desire for his death coexist, both products of a control he refuses to relinquish.","src":"haiku"},
"rezz~can-you-see-me":{"haiku":"Desperate plea for acknowledgment; demanding recognition of existence beneath surface perception.","opus":"Built almost entirely on a single insistent question, this hypnotic track from the mind-bending Canadian producer plays like a plea for recognition from someone who feels invisible. The repeated demand to be seen, paired with hints that someone got away with something, turns the mantra into both accusation and desperate bid for acknowledgment.","sonnet":"A fragment of confrontation — you got away with it, pay close attention — the repeated demand to be seen addressed to someone who has made themselves invisible or immune, and the tension is that visibility itself is what the speaker is owed and has been denied.","src":"haiku"},
"rezz~give-in-to-you":{"haiku":"Surrender to pull and command. Smoke and illusion guide the captive toward willing subjection.","opus":"Willing surrender to a hypnotic, controlling pull, drawn under by smoke and mirrors even knowing this person isn't the one; captivity to delirium framed as desire.","sonnet":"A song about surrendering to an intoxicating attraction despite knowing it is an illusion — the narrator feels themselves being pulled under, sees through the smoke and mirrors, yet would still give in completely, offering themselves as captive to the other person's command and feeding on the delirium.","src":"haiku"},
"rezz~haunted-eyes":{"haiku":"Cyclical relationship failure where promises dissolve into repetition; the speaker remains plagued by internal voices urging destruction while watching patterns repeat unchanged.","opus":"Losing one's grip while trapped in a cycle with a partner who never changes and keeps score, the narrator seeing everything through haunted eyes. Sleepless nights and intrusive voices mark a mind slipping under the weight of the same repeated mistakes.","sonnet":"A relationship corroding in cycles — the other person keeping score and walking out while the speaker, invaded by voices urging destruction, still wakes at night seeing everything through haunted eyes and making the same mistakes.","src":"haiku"},
"rezz~not-enough":{"haiku":"Death anxiety and eternal haunting drive desire for transcendent remembrance; the speaker clings to a beloved to defeat oblivion.","opus":"A cold, brooding meditation on love that never measures up. The narrator insists she doesn't need love to survive, guarding a broken heart while contemplating her own death and the mess she'll leave. Beneath the electronic chill runs a plea to be let down gently and remembered, and a fear of ultimately meaning too little.","sonnet":"Someone stepping back from a relationship they've outgrown: the repeated \"I don't need love to breathe\" is hard-won detachment after a partner who steps on your neck and lets you down, and the question of whether either person has truly changed hangs over lines about crying in mirrors and bad luck following close behind.","src":"haiku"},
"rezz~sacrificial-feat-pvris":{"haiku":"An unquestioning devotion to love that demands sacrifice, even when others warn against it, celebrating a connection worth any price or damnation.","opus":"Over the Canadian producer's hypnotic dark electronic textures, PVRIS voices a lover willing to court their own downfall, seeing a forbidden bond most clearly in the dark. The heavy weight of that love is something they would gladly lay on the line, framing the relationship as a deal with the devil that still feels fine, worth the pain and, as the title insists, worth the sacrifice.","sonnet":"A person surrenders completely to a love that others warn against, framing the relationship as a pact with darker forces. The weight of this devotion feels dangerous and divine at once — heaven or hellfire, it does not matter. The sacrifice is willing, because the other person is worth every cost.","src":"haiku"},
"rezz~someone-else":{"haiku":"Deception runs so deep that the narrator questions whether their partner possesses a coherent self beneath the false masks. Observation and distance reveal a stranger wearing the face of intimacy and predictable betrayal.","opus":"Waking beside the absence of a partner revealed as a hell-sent angel, someone who becomes a stranger the moment they're not watched. The crimson warning signs were ignored daily, and now love has curdled into distrust of a person who doesn't even know themselves.","sonnet":"The speaker wakes to the scent of someone gone from the bed and realises they've been loving a person who becomes entirely different whenever unobserved — \"you're like an angel sent from Hell\" — and who may not even know which version of themselves is real.","src":"haiku"},
"rezz~someone-else-underoath-remix":{"haiku":"Masks and transformation hide the true nature of a partner as the narrator watches multiple versions emerge unpredictably. The song questions whether anyone can truly know another or if relationships survive only through calculated ignorance.","opus":"Waking beside the absence of a partner revealed as a hell-sent angel, someone who becomes a stranger the moment they're not watched. The ignored red flags leave the narrator distrusting a person who doesn't even know themselves.","sonnet":"A partner who seemed devoted reveals a hidden self whenever the speaker looks away — blood-red flags were always there in plain sight, and the central question, who are you when I'm not looking, remains unanswered because the other person may not know themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"rezz~taste-of-you-feat-dove-cameron":{"haiku":"Obsessive desire and addiction to a lover, where the speaker starves without physical and emotional contact, describing attraction as a compulsive need that sustains and defines their existence.","opus":"An explicit anthem of erotic obsession as literal hunger, sinking teeth into a lover, starving without them, framing desire as an addiction she'll die without. The vampiric imagery, dynamite, biting, being chained to the bed, makes lust a dangerous, consuming compulsion she can't feed enough.","sonnet":"An obsessive, vampiric attachment framed as mutual — the need to consume and be consumed by another person, starvation without them, the craving described in bite-and-blood imagery that makes desire indistinguishable from dependency.","src":"haiku"},
"rhye~count-to-five-tensnake-remix":{"haiku":"Sexual and emotional anticipation build toward a moment of surrender where control dissolves; desire demands patience before consummation.","opus":"Desire kept deliberately unresolved, the lover holding back and counting, making the other move first. Restraint as its own charged game, hunger admitted but power over the pace never surrendered.","sonnet":"A tentative, desire-soaked negotiation between two people circling each other — the speaker hungry and patient, willing to wait while the other makes their move, both knowing the tension will break if they just hold the space long enough.","src":"haiku"},
"richaadeb~devil-trigger":{"haiku":"Primal darkness erupts as control shatters; internal demons demand release through violent catharsis and reckless transformation.","opus":"Surrendering to an inner demon of rage as a source of power, the hunter within breaking free in a red haze of vengeance. Written for a demon-hunting video game, it frames losing control as both curse and weapon pulled like a trigger.","sonnet":"An inner demon that has been held down too long finally breaks loose in a red curtain of rage — voices, vengeance, and the devil trigger pulled — though the violence is directed outward at enemies rather than the self, and the night's end only promises a return to the fight.","src":"haiku"},
"rimmy-grasa~closed-eyes":{"haiku":"Blindness by choice or imposed circumstance, ignorance as protection or curse.","opus":"A haunted account of a lover who lies yet remains impossible to shake. Told he isn't enough, the narrator tries to stay silent while falling apart, and even behind closed eyes the other person stays lodged deep inside. The one who deceives everyone is cast, bitterly, as his only sun.","sonnet":"A relationship where the other person's deception and the narrator's inadequacy have become fused — closing eyes to escape only keeps the other person deeper inside. \"You are the sun, the only one that can fool everyone\" names the seductive power that makes leaving impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"rina-sawayama~stfu":{"haiku":"Suppressed rage erupts against demanding silence; the speaker refuses to perform emotional compliance and demands recognition of her authentic anger.","opus":"Sawayama channeling the rage of a mixed-race woman worn down by casual racism and microaggressions, finally refusing to perform patience. The nu-metal snarl is the sound of a politeness reflex snapping.","sonnet":"A speaker reaches the end of her patience with someone who dismisses her anger, fails to respect her, and projects fantasies onto her — delivering the demand to shut up as both a raw emotional explosion and a reclaiming of her own reality.","src":"haiku"},
"ringo-deathstarr~gazin":{"haiku":"Sadness consumes someone yearning for the unattainable while passively observing life's cycles move past them.","opus":"The Texan shoegaze act lives up to its lineage here, distilling everything to a haze of perpetual sadness and wanting something forever out of reach. Little happens beyond a car swerving through another curve on the ride, and the repeated admission of just gazing turns inertia into mood, the sound and stance of a scene built on staring at the floor while feeling drifts by.","sonnet":"A quiet longing sits at the centre — someone perpetually sad, aching for something just out of reach. Rather than chase it, they drift, watching life curve and swerve around them, knowing the journey will eventually leave everything behind. The posture is dreamy passivity, not resignation.","src":"haiku"},
"rinrin~gunmetal-black":{"haiku":"Raw aggression and dominance manifest as salvation through destruction; the speaker becomes instrument and weapon, relishing the power to annihilate enemies.","opus":"A messiah of violence offering salvation in exchange for total submission, reveling in domination and combat as a self-styled unbreakable god demanding worship.","sonnet":"A power fantasy of becoming an indestructible warrior protector who demands obedience in exchange for slaying all enemies — the speaker revels in violence and dominance, framing themselves as both savior and destroyer.","src":"haiku"},
"rinrin~prn":{"haiku":"Chemical escape replaces emotional truth as love morphs into addiction requiring constant medication. The speaker surrenders control, seeking pharmacy-grade relief from the person who causes the wound.","opus":"Love treated as a hard drug, chased alongside actual ones as a temporary thrill that is really a cry for help. The begged-for PRN is emergency medication for a narrator who admits she has lost her grip and can't say no.","sonnet":"Love and drug abuse collapse into each other: a private, beautiful intimacy behind closed doors gives way to a spiral of speed, crack, DMT, heartbreak, and compulsive craving — the speaker knows it is a cry for help but keeps giving in to temporary thrills, begging for a PRN (as-needed medication) because they have lost their mind.","src":"haiku"},
"rip-kenny~letting-go":{"haiku":"Scars mark survival; accepting that suffering is part of release allows the speaker to find peace despite ongoing brokenness.","opus":"An emo-leaning reckoning with self-hatred reframed as slow release. The narrator keeps saying he isn't okay yet insists that's okay anyway, scraping together ways to manage while old wounds smolder to the marrow. Reliving the moment he faltered, he treats daily self-loathing as painful but necessary steps in the long labor of moving on.","sonnet":"The speaker cycles through self-hatred and acceptance simultaneously — \"I'm not okay but it's okay,\" \"I hate myself, that's okay\" — acknowledging that scars burn down to the bone and that the act of choking on one's own pain is not a failure but part of the slow process of releasing it.","src":"haiku"},
"rise-against~savior":{"haiku":"Failed rescue attempts and mutual incompatibility prevent two people from saving each other despite profound connection and love.","opus":"A wrenching standoff between two people who can't save each other: she wants to rescue him while something's left, he insists he isn't the answer to her questions, and neither can call it love or walk away. The repeated denial of hatred is the thing that keeps them chained to each other.","sonnet":"Two people who love each other acknowledge they cannot be what the other needs — she says she wants to save him while something is left to save, he says he loves her but is not the answer — both pulled under by a world too noisy and walls they cannot break through.","src":"haiku"},
"rise-of-the-northstar~one-love":{"haiku":"Metal's integrity and linguistic pride celebrate artistic authenticity and respect for foundational traditions. The song fuses French and English while honoring the genre's power to express rage and community across cultural boundaries.","opus":"A crew anthem of hip-hop and metal fusion pledging one love from the North, defending classics and basics with manga-fueled bravado. Rapping in French becomes a point of integrity, striking the world like meteors.","sonnet":"A French-English metal manifesto about craft and integrity — the band writes in their mother tongue because it feels true, describing themselves as a generation forging metal without cheating, calling for loyalty to classics and basics under a single rallying cry from the North.","src":"haiku"},
"rise-of-the-northstar~rise":{"haiku":"Overcoming adversity through inner strength—a lost boy and middle-class girl discover survival through a four-letter concept, rising repeatedly despite tragedy.","opus":"A blunt motivational anthem for aimless kids of a rudderless era, the mopey boy and the comfortable girl both told the spark to fight lives inside them. Get up, over and over, self-belief as the only weapon on offer.","sonnet":"A motivational song about outcasts — a lost boy, a girl who doesn't love herself — each carrying a small internal survival candle described by four letters (the word \"rise\"), told to fight and rise again despite being ordinary and forgotten; the third verse extends this to an entire lost generation searching for light.","src":"haiku"},
"rise-of-the-northstar~sayonara":{"opus":"A defiant anthem of resistance against a system built to devour the individual, cast in the neon-drenched mythology of a manga-fed Neo-Tokyo. The Parisian crew fuses hip-hop swagger and hardcore fury, styling themselves street soldiers who bid farewell to conformity with knowledge as their weapon and a bilingual battle cry.","sonnet":"A delinquent rebel's declaration of war on a system trying to silence him — set in a neo-Tokyo street culture, the narrator says the troublemakers are back and there is no avoiding it now, invoking the blood of delinquents as it stirs for battle.","src":"sonnet"},
"rise-of-the-northstar~the-awakening":{"haiku":"An ancient warrior cycles through history in human vessels, spreading warfare and conflict across generations as an eternal curse of solitude and dominion.","opus":"A mythic origin tale of an immortal black samurai, denied heaven, who possesses a chosen host each century to recreate a world of war in his own lonely image.","sonnet":"A spoken-word mythology: a samurai denied heaven retreats to a forest for millennia, choosing hosts every hundred years and recreating a world of war and conflict around him, his apparitions documented in ancient writings — an origin story for a force of domination that passes through history unrecognised.","src":"haiku"},
"rise-resist~legacy":{"haiku":"Victims of systemic indifference transform into agents of resistance through collective action and remembered names. The song refuses erasure and calls for the dismantling of structures that devalue human life.","opus":"Sleepless with his inner demons, the narrator refuses to make peace when the fight is on and vows that whatever he turns into, his name will endure. Trapped as the very thing he despises in a world with no acknowledged victims, he grasps at a distant glimmer even as the dark swallows him.","sonnet":"Besieged by inner demons and a refusal to bury the hatchet when fighting is necessary, the speaker declares themselves everything they hate while insisting the struggle itself will make them remembered — a paradox of victimhood and defiant legacy in a society of mutual harm.","src":"haiku"},
"rishloo~freaks-animals":{"haiku":"A carnival barker reveals that the real spectacle is the audience themselves, turning the mirror on viewers who pay to be deceived and become complicit in their own exploitation.","opus":"A sinister carnival barker's invitation, luring a paying crowd into a show of freaks, fools and fire only to reveal, once the curtain falls, that the spectator wearing a borrowed disguise is the real spectacle they paid to see. It's a theatrical trap about voyeurism and complicity. Rishloo are an American progressive rock band, and this layered, unsettling storytelling over shifting, dramatic arrangements reflects their theatrical, Tool-adjacent art-rock ambitions.","sonnet":"A carnival barker's patter that turns the crowd into the exhibit. The whole act — lights, fire, freak-show spectacle — is revealed in the final moments to be a mirror: the audience paid to see monsters and finds itself reflected back, the circus having spun on them all along. The real show was never on the stage.","src":"haiku"},
"rishloo~katsushika":{"haiku":"Existential struggle against time's weight, seeking transformation through sustained effort and acceptance of life's dual nature.","opus":"A meditative plunge beneath the surface of the self, framed by Hokusai's wave imagery, where the struggle to breathe becomes a passage to wonder. Facing your monsters and finding them beautiful once you dive.","sonnet":"A call to stop counting time and step into the unknown: the speaker urges someone — and perhaps themselves — to face the edge, dive beneath the surface, and discover a place of love and wonder hidden below the waterline. The closing vision of \"beautiful monsters\" frames all struggling humanity as frightening yet magnificent.","src":"haiku"},
"rishloo~turning-sheep-into-goats":{"haiku":"Reflection on personal growth through inner conflict, questioning whether achieved desires bring genuine happiness or just continued yearning.","opus":"A bitter blessing aimed at someone who got exactly what they wanted by burning everything down, then asked to be pitied. Self-harm and false healing tangled together, the taunting question of whether destruction ever satisfies.","sonnet":"The speaker addresses someone who has caused immense disruption and pain, watching them pursue some deep personal desire at great cost, and sardonically asking whether they got what they wanted and feel satisfied. Underneath the bitterness is a thread of the speaker's own grief about a healing process and the loss of a moment of understanding.","src":"haiku"},
"rising-insane~high-hopes":{"haiku":"Desperate ambition and frantic motion sustain fragile hope; the speaker runs relentlessly to outpace collapse and stay tethered to aspiration.","opus":"Fierce optimism walking a wire through bleaker stretches, making anxiety a travel companion while refusing to quit chasing the thrill and pulling himself back together.","sonnet":"About persevering through anxiety and dark days by clinging to ambition — the speaker pushes through fear and exhaustion on a tightrope between breakdown and breakthrough, driven by high hopes and the refusal to stop running.","src":"haiku"},
"rita-the-tiaras~gone-with-the-wind-is-my-love":{"haiku":"A lover abandons after the speaker sacrifices everything, leaving heartbreak as the relationship cannot weather life's challenges.","opus":"A heartbroken torch song about being abandoned by a lover who claimed to be straight, the narrator having surrendered her soul, her belongings, and her devotion only to watch him bolt the instant the wind picked up. Every evening brings prayers for a return that never arrives. The girl-group phrasing and swooning strings place this squarely in the classic soul-pop tradition of doomed, all-consuming devotion.","sonnet":"A gospel-tinged heartbreak over a man who swore he was straight, took everything the speaker gave, then left at the first sign of difficulty. The wind metaphor cycles through — bird, petal, sail — each one more passive and untethered than the last. What remains is a prayer for his return, a chest full of grief, and no one left to lean on.","src":"haiku"},
"rivals~alkaline":{"opus":"The panic of fading relevance, the narrator terrified of being old news and stripped of everything she wanted. Rivals turns that dread inward, questioning whether composure or peace is even findable amid vertigo and imbalance. Promises that she'd be fine ring hollow; the wound is anxiety mistaken, once, for a passing chemical.","sonnet":"About the anxiety of losing relevance and the failure of reassurance — the narrator fears becoming old news, discovers that no amount of other people's confidence can restore their own inner equilibrium, and identifies their lost emotional balance as an alkaline state they cannot recover.","src":"sonnet"},
"rivals~nobody-loves-me":{"haiku":"Self-sufficiency and misanthropy crystallize into weaponized self-love as the speaker rejects dependency entirely, preferring isolation to the risk of disappointing connection.","opus":"A vow of self-reliance that curdles into contempt, where surviving loneliness means loving no one but yourself and razing everyone who might disappoint you. The blasphemy is bravado: choosing a self-made hell over any heaven that depends on other people.","sonnet":"Radical self-reliance pushed to nihilistic extreme: the speaker turns the gun on everyone else, blesses them in acid, builds empire walls to avoid being let down, and declares they would rather live in hell than heaven — concluding that nobody loves them the way they love themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"river-becomes-ocean~silence-means-nothing":{"haiku":"Overcoming trauma and distance through expression; singing transforms silence into defiance, turning scars into fuel for moving forward.","opus":"A battered push forward through scars and dead weight, the narrator refusing to look back and casting a departed figure as a distant threat still gnawing at him. He keeps his voice raised because staying quiet solves nothing, even as hope thins out. River Becomes Ocean deliver it as melodic post-hardcore balancing resilience against ruin.","sonnet":"A declaration that staying silent in the face of wreckage and loss is not an option. Scars map the past, hope has run dry, an ocean divides what once felt whole — but the response is to sing through it anyway, carry the heart on the tongue, keep moving forward even when frail and hollow. The bottle is acknowledged as a release; it's not condemned, just witnessed.","src":"haiku"},
"riverside~i-m-done-with-you":{"haiku":"Rejecting unsolicited judgment and control from someone claiming authority over personal autonomy; the speaker demands space to make their own choices without interference.","opus":"The Polish prog band channels the frustration of an old ally turned self-appointed judge. A friend who supported the narrator now recoils at his new direction, offering unwanted charts, strategies and correction. The response is a firm assertion of autonomy: this is his choice and his life, and the meddler is no God, boss or authority over him.","sonnet":"A clean severance from an unsolicited advisor: someone who was always on the narrator's side suddenly objects to a new direction and appoints themselves judge, God, CEO — the response is categorical: you are none of those things, draw your charts elsewhere, take your poison out of my soul.","src":"haiku"},
"riverside~second-life-syndrome":{"haiku":"Escaping codependency and reclaiming personal agency despite guilt and hesitation.","opus":"Breaking out of a draining routine and a draining relationship to finally live for oneself, while a secret online persona stands in for the loneliness left behind. Riverside's Polish prog builds to a cold liberation — erasing the one who both helped and wounded, the second life at once escape and symptom.","sonnet":"A speaker breaks free from a suffocating relationship and the ritual patterns it imposed — finding that the absence of the other person, though painful, finally allowed him to start living his own life; a secret intimate longing runs beneath the relief.","src":"haiku"},
"ro1~a-visitant":{"haiku":"Cold isolation and dark visions create a landscape where connection freezes and hope abandons the narrator. Alienation persists despite longing for warmth as the angel of night repeatedly visits and leaves them alone.","opus":"Grief as an endless winter, a smile worn over silent mourning while the world has gone cold and colorless since a loss. The dead return like a night angel that drifts in and out, leaving the survivor stranded in the cold, struggling to loosen a love that still holds him.","sonnet":"Caught in a frozen, colourless world where the sun gives no warmth and the angel of the night keeps coming and going, a lonely figure holds a painted smile over darkness, asking whether freedom is still possible and walking forward into the snow regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"roadrunner-united~independent-voice-of-the-voiceless":{"haiku":"Collective assertion of dissent against oppression and hypocrisy wielded by the powerful.","opus":"A metal-supergroup rallying cry, sung partly in Portuguese by Sepultura's Max Cavalera, throwing back at the powerful their right to judge and destroy. It claims the voice of the voiceless and, in Latin defiance, wishes its enemies long life to witness its victory.","sonnet":"A declaration of defiance against those who judge, criticise, and destroy — the band positions itself as arising from sacrifice and fire to speak for those who have no voice, ending with a Portuguese prayer that their enemies live long enough to witness their victory.","src":"haiku"},
"roadrunner-united~the-end":{"haiku":"The world ends abruptly and everyone drowns in the hell they created together. Cities collapse as the speaker begs for salvation they know won't come.","opus":"An all-star metal-label collaboration staging the apocalypse, the sky going dark and rising waters scouring the world clean of everything corrupt. The refrain casts the catastrophe as a hell humanity built for itself, its cries for rescue framing the extinction as self-inflicted, a punishment we brought on our own heads.","sonnet":"Humanity drowns in a catastrophe of its own making — skies blackening, seas rising to wash away cities and civilisations. The speaker stands frozen at the edge of this collapse, unable to believe the end arrived so soon, calling out for salvation while understanding none is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"roadrunner-united~the-rich-man":{"haiku":"Religious manipulation and war grind the vulnerable into service; blindness and obedience are cultivated as virtues.","opus":"Foot soldiers perishing in a desert war they cannot grasp, sacrificed at the command of the wealthy who march believers to their end. Faith and obedience are exposed as the machinery feeding someone else's grand design. A Roadrunner Records all-star metal collaboration.","sonnet":"A soldier or follower trapped in a conflict he doesn't understand reflects on faith, family, and the knowledge that he may not survive — and that survival wouldn't make sense of what he's been asked to do in service of distant powerful men who treat lives as disposable. The disillusionment is total and quietly devastating.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-dougan~clubbed-to-death-kurayamino-variation":{"haiku":"Violence and percussion merge; rhythmic assault delivers fatal blow through orchestrated sound.","opus":"Beneath the cinematic strings and breakbeats that made this instrumental famous drifts a fragment of overheard, deadpan conversation about a party, a girl and her half-baked theory. The spoken snippets read as bemused detachment, someone bored to death by pretension while the music around them swells toward something far grander than the small talk.","sonnet":"A deadpan spoken interior monologue about meeting a woman called Kim at a party — he needed a ride, she theorised with her feet on pillows while a roommate played bongos — and her overwhelming dullness becoming a kind of psychic violence: she bores him, literally, to death. The bathos is the point: the narrator is killed not by passion but by its total absence.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-dougan~nothing-at-all-instrumental":{"haiku":"Two people seek escape through shared silence and earthly dissolution. The song frames death as peaceful reunion and release from the pain of existence.","opus":"Best known for the noir electronica of Clubbed to Death, Rob Dougan turns orchestral and vocal here for a hushed meditation on dying peacefully beside a loved one. He imagines the surrounding world quieting away, mortality reframed as serene rather than terrifying, with wordless stillness offered as its own kind of grace.","sonnet":"A meditation on shared quietness as grace — watching a face age, finding patience in each line, letting the world fall away until only silence remains. The song moves through life's stages toward a final willingness to walk together into the earth and disappear, the few words said along the way amounting to nothing at all, which turns out to be enough.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-scallon~prison-break":{"haiku":"Incarceration awakens a primal fury that becomes immune to punishment; the prisoner transforms into a lethal force plotting violent escape at any cost.","opus":"A cartoonishly unstoppable convict monologue, all menace and boast about breaking free and hunting his captors. The threat itself is the point, over-the-top villainy played straight for the riff.","sonnet":"An unstoppable, defiant persona announces that no prison or system can contain them, portraying repeated incarceration as proof of power rather than failure — they always escape, always return, and can never truly be held.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-thomas~someday":{"haiku":"Hope persists that present suffering will eventually transform into shared clarity and strengthened connection, though certainty remains elusive.","opus":"Thomas coaxing himself and a partner out of paralysis, betting everything on a vague future while quietly admitting he's tired of waiting for it. The pep-talk hides how much doubt it's meant to drown.","sonnet":"A gentle, forward-looking address to someone in pain, encouraging them to hold on through doubt and indecision with the hope that someday things will clarify, life will open up, and both people will find their way to something better.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~crow-killer-blues":{"opus":"A menacing, apocalyptic outlaw persona (the crow killer) delivers a Gonzo-style call to burn down institutions, cloaked in imagery of heretics, serpents, judgment and revolt against a corrupt world.","sonnet":"A pulpy, violent persona piece — the narrator inhabits a shadowy predator figure lurking in apocalyptic imagery of serpents, thieves, and burning institutions, celebrating chaos and threatening violence as theatrical anti-authoritarian spectacle.","src":"sonnet"},
"rob-zombie~demon-speeding":{"haiku":"The speaker embodies chaos and darkness itself, a demonic force that thrives on madness, destruction, and violating the innocent.","opus":"Rob Zombie's carnival of horror-movie transgression, the narrator flaunting his freakhood as an all-American nightmare and daring you to love the filth. It's schlock as identity, monstrousness worn with total showman's glee.","sonnet":"A celebration of being an untouchable, corrupting force — the narrator presents himself as a devil machine tearing through the American dream, wrecking roads and violating innocence with gleeful nihilism.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~demonoid-phenomenon":{"haiku":"Sexual violence and demonic possession; a figure violates and desecrates, speaking in predatory terms.","opus":"Rob Zombie channels his horror-film obsession into a lurid parade of violators and desecrators, wailing spirits and creeping vermin lifted from gothic dread. Spoken taunts about a corpse and weeping gods push it toward exploitation-movie menace, the chanted hook functioning as a groove-metal ritual of manufactured shock rather than any coherent story.","sonnet":"A malevolent force announces itself — part conqueror, part desecrator, revelling in chaos and horror. The imagery piles up: screaming gods, a slithering worm, mourning air, and a crowd of violators. The creature is pleased with its audience's complicit terror and does not apologise for a moment of it.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~dragula":{"haiku":"A primal, supernatural force moves through death and chaos in a hot rod ('Dragula'), embodying destruction and transgression against the natural world.","opus":"A macabre, horror-tinged boast in which the speaker declares himself death itself in many forms, tearing through witches and ditches and speeding away in a monstrous hot-rod hearse he cannot die in.","sonnet":"The narrator casts himself as an omnipresent, predatory force of death and decay — spreading like disease, inescapable as a hound of hell — prowling through grotesque imagery and burning through everything in his path in his vehicle, the Dragula.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~feed-the-gods":{"haiku":"Sensual ecstasy and violent domination merge as the narrator claims the power of godhood through desire and destruction. Transcendence requires becoming a threat rather than remaining compliant or passive.","opus":"A B-movie horror leer dressed as swagger, equating violence and lust with the intoxication of feeling godlike. It is less a story than a strung-together montage of grindhouse menace, chasing the rush of unchecked power.","sonnet":"A frenzied, b-movie invocation of sex and power — the speaker surrounds himself with zombie imagery and dead-star chaos, the central question being whether you know what it feels like to be God, answered by feeding that hunger on the creep and the wheels of love.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~feel-so-numb":{"haiku":"Disconnection from moral responsibility and the world produces a strange euphoria, where darkening the sun feels good. The song captures amoral numbness and detachment.","opus":"Over restless, surreal imagery of midnight drives, radio talk shows, and a blackened sun, the speaker oscillates between feeling good, bad, and numb while asking what he has done. It's a disoriented, drug-hazed rush urging the listener to feel it and make it all right.","sonnet":"The song layers surreal, frenetic imagery — midnight drives, radio chatter, brain trash, porn flash — over a refrain of feeling simultaneously good and numb, good and bad, asking \"what have I done\" against a blacked-out sun. The overall effect is of dissociated, overstimulated modern life producing a state of feeling everything and nothing at once.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~girl-on-fire":{"haiku":"A man obsesses over and desires a woman's intensity and power, watching her burn with a dangerous chaotic energy that threatens to consume him.","opus":"A lurid horror-comic montage of a lethal woman who takes whatever she wants and a narrator casting himself as an array of villains begging to be consumed. Meaning matters less than the schlock texture. Rob Zombie, once of White Zombie, welds exactly this grindhouse imagery to groove-metal stomp, all sleaze and B-movie swagger.","sonnet":"Obsession dressed as spectacle — a woman described as fire that burns the speaker up even as he circles her, drawn by something he frames as a satanic fantasy, electric and unreal. The imagery is carnivalesque and deliberately scrambled, a pinball arcade of desire and destruction, with the refrain burning bright enough to cut through whatever else is happening around it.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~i-m-your-boogieman":{"haiku":"Sexual availability and devoted service frame the narrator as an ever-ready accomplice willing to perform any desire. The song celebrates unrestricted appetite and the intoxication of being wanted completely.","opus":"A cover that flips a children's bogeyman into a predatory lover, all-hours availability rebranded as devotion. The come-on barely masks the threat underneath: the thing under the bed insisting it only wants to please you.","sonnet":"The boogieman presents himself as a devoted, ever-available servant of pleasure — round the clock, from sundown to sunup, all he wants is to be the one his lover loves most, casting the monster figure as an object of complete erotic submission.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~living-dead-girl":{"haiku":"The song portrays a transgressive, dangerous female figure who defies norms and embraces violence and sin, described through surreal horror imagery. She operates beyond morality and exists in a state of undead transgression and power.","opus":"A pileup of exploitation-cinema imagery built around a seductive undead woman with an unquenchable hunger for corpses. The chanted refrain summons her closer amid gore and depravity; it's less a narrative than a wallow in campy horror-movie shock played for pulpy thrills.","sonnet":"The song conjures a seductive, macabre figure described as the Living Dead Girl — an irresistible creature associated with death, blood, and transgressive desire — addressed through fragmented, surreal horror imagery.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~man-without-fear":{"haiku":"Invulnerability and confidence eclipse doubt; the speaker claims dominance through fearlessness.","opus":"A comic-book adrenaline rush cast as Daredevil, the blind hero who feels his way through darkness without fear. Justice is blind, and so is he, turning his loss of sight into unstoppable defiance.","sonnet":"A tribute to the Marvel character Daredevil — blind yet fearless, the speaker channels the superhero's heightened senses and indomitable will, narrating the origin of a man who loses sight but gains something greater, defying all attempts to stop him.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~meet-the-creeper":{"opus":"A B-movie monster given a strut, the Creeper leering out of horror-schlock imagery, seeing the dead in your eyes and transforming in the skies. Rob Zombie's schtick is pure grindhouse theater, a groove-metal creature feature. There's little confession here, just the gleeful conjuring of a leering cinematic ghoul.","sonnet":"A horror-persona song in Rob Zombie's signature carnival-monster register — the Creeper is a shape-shifting predatory entity that sees death in your eyes and transforms in the skies, feeding the kill instinct and the lady alike. It is atmosphere and archetype rather than narrative, building a grotesque figure out of B-movie imagery.","src":"sonnet"},
"rob-zombie~never-gonna-stop-the-red-red-kroovy":{"haiku":"The speaker refuses to stop their wild, relentless pursuit of sensation and excess, driven by violent imagery and determination. Nothing will halt their aggressive momentum.","opus":"A Clockwork Orange homage, borrowing Alex's droog slang for blood to build a relentless, grinning celebration of violent momentum. Rob Zombie's horror-schlock groove as pure unstoppable kick.","sonnet":"A Rob Zombie horror-carnival invocation — fragmented, image-driven, about an unstoppable force that paints things red to hide the blood. The lyrical content is mostly atmosphere and repetition; the theme is relentless, unkillable violence as spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~pussy-liquor":{"haiku":"Crude sexual narrative embedded in violent imagery of family depravity and intoxication. The song uses shock language depicting deviant behavior.","opus":"A gleeful splatter-movie romp built on cheerleader chants and hillbilly-horror caricature, characters named Baby and Otis slaughtering hogs and getting fucked up while counting off who to kill next. It's cartoon depravity as spectacle. Rob Zombie ties this straight into his House of 1000 Corpses universe, the same Firefly-family grindhouse imagery, and the industrial-metal groove under the schlock is exactly the B-movie horror aesthetic he built his solo career on.","sonnet":"A grindhouse vignette of degeneracy — Earl, Baby, Otis, a slaughtered hog, gasoline, and general mayhem, all centred on the ritual of getting destroyed together. The cheerleader framing is pure camp, the violence is cartoonish, and the whole thing is held together by the chorus repeating its blunt equation of intoxication with acceleration and sickness.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~reload":{"haiku":"A violent, cyclical vision of destruction and transformation: snapping magazines, blowing away, and reloading for another round. The repeated 'time to die' and the image of the 'Pin Eyed God' render an apocalyptic inevitability without escape.","opus":"Zombie's usual pulp-horror collage, more texture than statement, chanting a menacing invented deity over imagery of guns and death. It's atmosphere as content: a B-movie villain's swagger with little beneath the surface but groove and dread.","sonnet":"The lyrics circle around a commanding, godlike presence called Pin Eyed God and repeated commands to reload and prepare to die, but the imagery is too fragmented and abstract to yield a clear narrative subject.","src":"haiku","fable":"A firearm gets chanted into existence here, its verses dissolving into a haze of cosmic abstractions that never resolve into sense. The one bodily gesture landing with real clarity is loading a fresh clip and firing, so the killer's psychedelic murk plays against a single sharp mechanical motion repeated on command. That title word is really the whole track — a countdown curled back on itself, forever readying the next kill. Zombie's horror-groove sleight of hand keeps the shooter's interior trippy rather than threatening, a hypnotic deity-chant that treats dying as something to nod along to instead of fear.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult"],"means":"the killer's interior kept trippy so dying becomes something to nod along to rather than fear","built":"cosmic abstraction fills the verses but the one mechanically clear gesture — loading and firing — is the only moment the song holds still"},
"rob-zombie~return-of-the-phantom-stranger":{"haiku":"A spectral figure appears as a mysterious outsider to haunt and dominate those who encounter him, wielding power through fear and strangeness.","opus":"A shape-shifting ghostmaster narrating his own haunting, branding himself sly, promising freaks and deformed laughter, a sacrifice waiting in the dark and a heart in decay, taunting the listener who both fears and loves the phantom. It's comic-book horror as strut. Rob Zombie builds his solo persona from exactly this monster-movie vocabulary, the Phantom Stranger borrowed from horror lore, groove-metal riffs turning campy dread into a stomping anthem.","sonnet":"A supernatural figure announces itself — shapeshifting, haunted, claiming the role of ghostmaster to someone who is always alone. The Phantom Stranger feeds on isolation: you love it because it's the only company the darkness provides, the stillborn presence beneath the surface that outlasts everything. A sacrifice waits somewhere; the stranger is already there.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~scum-of-the-earth":{"haiku":"A person identifies as scum, breathing, bleeding, and screaming as they destroy and run rampant, embodying nihilistic chaos and destruction.","opus":"Rob Zombie struts through pulp-cinema villainy, voicing an outlaw who kills, wrecks, and revels in being a nonexistent hero with a hot weapon in hand. The stomping chant strips him down to raw physical defiance. Steeped in his grindhouse aesthetic, the track wears its own wickedness as a badge of honor.","sonnet":"A self-proclaimed outcast revels in the identity thrust upon him — breathing, bleeding, screaming on the margins of a world built for heroes who do not exist. The title is worn as armour rather than shame, the call-and-response turning a slur into a rallying chant.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~spookshow-baby":{"haiku":"A dangerous, predatory woman destroys men through appetite and dark power. The song portrays her as killer and spectacle.","opus":"A monster-movie brag from a self-styled demon who threatens to tear the listener apart, paired with a deadly femme fatale prowling as the song's title creature. It's pure horror-pulp menace played as camp. Rob Zombie built his solo debut on this drive-in aesthetic, the groove-metal crunch carrying the ghoulish showmanship of his White Zombie days.","sonnet":"A lean, repetitive piece of predatory swagger — the speaker is voodoo man, devil man, psycho man, able to tear down and walk over and fill with hunger. The woman at the centre, the spookshow baby, is killer and thriller both. It's a B-movie seduction built entirely from elemental nouns: noon, rain, glove, blood, hunger.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~superbeast":{"haiku":"A demonic or nightmarish persona presents itself as the ultimate seducer or predator, shrieking and convulsing with violent intention and dark charisma.","opus":"Zombie's B-movie horror strut, less narrative than incantation, casting the singer as the monstrous thing the audience secretly craves; the pleasure is in surrendering to spectacle, dread, and the ragged, ritual violence.","sonnet":"A violent, demonic figure announces itself as the thing its audience desired, commanding the dead and revelling in its own savagery as a force of possession and destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~the-ballad-of-resurrection-joe-and-rosa-whore":{"haiku":"Dark narrative depicting opium addiction, medical torture, and survival in poverty. Rosa endures abuse while characters struggle against institutional harm and suffering.","opus":"A grimy Victorian gothic tale of narcotic hazes, back-alley killing and a physician's creed that healing demands agony, set in a squalid London district peopled by lowlifes and streetwalkers, all bound to a refrain equating damnation with love and insisting on pain. Rob Zombie steeps it in penny-dreadful atmosphere, the industrial groove-metal churn delivering exactly the cinematic ghoulishness of his solo work.","sonnet":"A Victorian-flavoured fever dream set in opium dens and gin alleys — Rachel hiding from Ned, doctors prescribing suffering as a cure, a figure asking to be resurrected by a woman who wants more and takes more. Love and hell are equated without irony, the knife inseparable from the pain, the killing bed a place of rest and ruin at once.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~the-great-american-nightmare":{"haiku":"Embodied darkness and desirable corruption; the singer is both monster and dream, soul-corrupter and lover, violent and seductive, offering everything needed and wanted.","opus":"Rob Zombie plays a horror-carnival barker boasting that he embodies both the country's darkest terror and its glossy aspiration at once, soul-rotting and monstrous. He beckons the listener to step closer and taste the brutality, wrapping the whole ghoulish come-on in a menacing promise of twisted affection.","sonnet":"A song about being the embodiment of America's dark underside — the narrator offers himself as nightmare, dream, monster, and corruptor simultaneously, claiming to be exactly what the culture secretly craves and fears.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~thunder-kiss-65":{"haiku":"Frenetic rock imagery of living fast, atomic power, and possession evoke demon-fueled ecstasy outside normal time and restraint.","opus":"The horror-rock frontman, in his White Zombie days, collages grindhouse and biker-flick imagery into a revved-up B-movie fever, the recurring 1965 stamping the whole thing with retro exploitation-cinema kitsch. Motorpsycho nightmares, demon-warp resurrections, and atomic-hearted overdrive pile up as pure lurid texture, meaning subordinate to the leering, groove-driven thrill of a mind hurtling through pulp imagery at full throttle.","sonnet":"A psychedelic collision of B-movie nostalgia, machine-age velocity, and hormonal overload anchored to 1965 as a mythologised year of raw American excess. The narrator channels demon energy and atomic ego through imagery of road speed, cult cinema, and unstoppable appetite — power as pure kinetic release.","src":"haiku"},
"rob-zombie~two-lane-blacktop":{"haiku":"Obsession with acceleration and motion down desolate roads; the speaker hunts another driver in a dangerous high-speed pursuit, machines and men fused in mechanical ecstasy.","opus":"A road-movie homage (to the 1971 film of the same name) about speed-obsessed drifters racing muscle cars across the American West on twenty bucks, mechanic and driver barely speaking; the throughline is the hypnotic, empty freedom of endless driving with no destination that matters beyond the next race.","sonnet":"A pure American road movie in lyric form — GTO, Bakersfield, Amarillo, muscle cars racing the blacktop for $20 — the song existing as a vehicle for the sound of speed and open highway rather than any deeper narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"robert-johnson~they-re-red-hot":{"haiku":"Sexual desire and marketplace commerce converge; the narrator celebrates a woman's hot tamales and availability with crude playfulness and folk humor.","opus":"Johnson's rare romp, a fast ragtime hokum tune where the street vendor's hot tamales are barely-veiled innuendo. Delta blues at its most playful and bawdy, selling sex with a grin instead of dread.","sonnet":"A playful, bawdy celebration of a woman selling hot tamales on the street, with the food as a double-edged symbol of desire and heat, the narrator boasting of her wares and warning that messing with them carries real consequences.","src":"haiku"},
"robin-thicke~blurred-lines":{"haiku":"Boundary dissolution enables seduction; the narrator rejects consent as a construct between desire and compliance.","opus":"A pickup boast that frames a woman's supposed hidden desire as license to pursue, the very ambiguity in the title the thing critics found predatory. Slick seduction that mistakes presumption for chemistry.","sonnet":"A persistent seduction directed at a woman the speaker insists is secretly willing, framing his pursuit as liberation from a controlling partner while ignoring her stated boundaries — the blurred lines are between consent and coercion.","src":"haiku"},
"robin-thicke~give-it-2-u":{"haiku":"Seduction and material excess drive a narrative of male desire where women are objects to be conquered with charm, wealth and bodily confidence; the narrator positions himself as irresistible through consumption and boasting.","opus":"Wall-to-wall sexual bravado, all brand-name swagger and crude offers with no interior life beneath. A come-on stripped down to appetite and product placement.","sonnet":"A man lavishes a woman with boasts of wealth, status, and sexual prowess, insisting she surrender to his desires in a relentless transactional pursuit.","src":"haiku"},
"robyn~dancing-on-my-own":{"haiku":"Unrequited longing manifests as solitary performance; the speaker dances alone while watching the beloved with another.","opus":"The definitive heartbreak-on-the-dancefloor anthem: watching an ex kiss someone new while refusing to leave, dancing alone as both defiance and grief. Robyn making invisibility feel like the loudest thing in the room.","sonnet":"Standing in a club watching the person she loves kiss someone else, the speaker is invisible and heartbroken but refuses to leave — she keeps dancing alone, all messed up, giving it her all even though she knows she is not the one going home with him tonight.","src":"haiku"},
"rocket~take-your-aim":{"haiku":"Accusation becomes standoff; the subject demands action against blame.","opus":"A dare thrown at someone who blames you for everything yet won't confront you directly. Tired of being the scapegoat, the singer invites the accuser to stop hiding behind lies and take an honest shot.","sonnet":"A breakup confrontation in which the speaker, blamed for everything, challenges the other to stop lying, stop crying to them, and just come take their aim — a dare to be direct and finish what they started.","src":"haiku"},
"rogue~para":{"opus":"A metaphysical, near-death-experience narrative asserting that the self is an eternal, reincarnating fragment of the cosmos; passing through a tunnel of light into a realm of interconnected souls, the singer realizes all beings are one and death is not an end.","sonnet":"A psychedelic or near-death experience — possibly a DMT or NDE vision — in which the narrator dissolves their body-bound identity, passes through a tunnel of light, and emerges in a space between dimensions where souls are unified and love is total, concluding that consciousness is eternal and metamorphic.","src":"sonnet"},
"rohna~nowhere-else-to-go":{"haiku":"Dissociation and lucid dreaming become survival; solitude is the only escape from a waking nightmare that offers no alternative.","opus":"A drifting, dissociative portrait of someone half-awake in a waking dream. The narrator waits and waits for a chance to escape, staying up nights to fend off reality yet always sliding back under. Isolation hardens into resignation: sure he's better on his own, with no destination left to aim for.","sonnet":"Lucid-dream insomnia as a coping strategy — staying awake at night to escape reality, knowing the escape always ends in sleep — with the conclusion that being better off alone with nowhere else to go is the only stable ground available.","src":"haiku"},
"rolo-tomassi~cloaked":{"haiku":"A person stuck in unchanging decades watches from outside life's moments, longing for simplicity while slowly losing themselves to routine.","opus":"British experimental band Rolo Tomassi trace a life slipping past in numb, unchanging repetition, shadowed by mounting dread and a wish to just stop existing. Outwardly composed but inwardly desperate, the narrator tries to endure without shattering, uncertain anything lies past this deadening loop, and pleads for patience while waiting.","sonnet":"A reckoning with stagnation — decades passing while patterns remain unchanged, burden accumulating without relief or resolution. The narrator oscillates between an urge to simply stop feeling and the drive to bend without breaking under circumstances that offer almost no margin. What waits beyond the end of this cycle stays unnamed.","src":"haiku"},
"rolo-tomassi~closer":{"haiku":"Romantic entanglement and desire remain constant despite mutual damage; the speaker pursues connection despite knowing it leads only toward shared darkness.","opus":"A relationship collapsing into inevitability, the singer casting herself as the devil the lover no longer recognizes while still wanting them. Beauty engineered to fail, the only exit being straight through the ruin.","sonnet":"A relationship designed like a beautiful, failing system — once luminous, now collapsed — leaves the speaker admitting they have become someone unrecognisable, and that all they can manage to want, despite everything, is the other person.","src":"haiku"},
"romes~another-day":{"haiku":"Depression's weight accumulates into numbness where survival becomes mechanical repetition; the speaker sinks so deep that another day of suffering feels inevitable.","opus":"Depression worn behind a cheerful mask: the narrator looks fine while everything falls apart, kept alive only by making music. He's been down so long that surviving one more day is both grim habit and the one thing holding him in check.","sonnet":"A song about grinding through depression and creative survival — the speaker is broke down and has been for a long time, but music is the only thing keeping them functional; they shrug off another terrible day with a bitter rhetorical question, using that numbness as proof of endurance rather than defeat.","src":"haiku","fable":"A depressive's survival song delivered with a poker face: the narrator admits the day was ruinous while insisting no one can tell, because he wears the composure like armor. The verses stack gallows humor over real rot — decay, filth, an early grave scored by the music he loves — until dread curdles into something almost jaunty. That tonal trick is the point: the endlessly repeated question about what one more day could matter reads first as surrender, the shrug of someone too far down to fear the fall. But by the final round it flips into grim endurance — if the bottom is already home, then another day costs nothing, so you might as well take it. The read hinges on that ambiguity, the same four words functioning as both a death wish and the exact reasoning that keeps him alive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the same four words function as death wish and grim endurance at once","built":"gallows humor stacked over real rot until dread curdles into something jaunty"},
"romes~black-honey": {"haiku":"Insects crawl through consciousness as sweetness drips like poison, the speaker drinks the feeling of dissolution while trapped in their own architecture.","opus":"A vivid plunge into hallucinatory paranoia, the room itself warping and dissolving while something laughs in the static. The title's dark sweetness is a poison the sufferer keeps swallowing anyway — addiction to a terrifying, spiraling state.","sonnet":"A song about being psychologically haunted by something the speaker can't name — an interior presence that laughs in the static, that makes the walls melt, that drips like something sweet and toxic through the ceiling, seeping into the speaker's consciousness regardless of what they do to shut it out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sleepless, paranoid interior where time hammers, the mind crawls with vermin, and no protector is coming — the two verses map a nervous system under siege, each one collapsing into a stammered refrain that the singer hears but cannot name, the fracture itself doing the work of showing a thought too fixed to finish. What he is straining to hear resolves in the hook: the title fluid that seeps down through the ceiling, sweetness gone dark, a poison dressed as reward. The turn from dread to appetite is the whole point — the same craving that kept him awake and locked in now asks to be drunk to the last drop, so the song is less about what the substance is than about the moment fear rebrands itself as devotion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind"],"means":"fear rebrands itself as devotion at the moment of resolution","built":"stammered refrain performs the thought too fixed to finish"},
"romes~brokenasyou": {"opus":"Romes turns the fatigue of faking okayness into a pop confession, the narrator patching over his troubles and grooming himself flawless so no one clocks the ache underneath. The turn comes when he admits everyone is just as damaged, and that honest, shared brokenness beats keeping up the polished front.","sonnet":"A confession of performed togetherness — the narrator keeps their damage carefully hidden behind style and confidence, but wants others to know they're just as broken, and the song ends in an invitation: stop pretending, be broken together instead.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A song about the labor of looking fine — the duct-taped problems buried in the dirt, the slicked-back hair, the trouble sealed up like something you'd rather not have found. The verses build the whole apparatus of composure only for the chorus to keep puncturing it, each polished line answered by the admission that the machine has bolts rattling loose underneath. What turns it from confession into something warmer is the bridge, which stops treating brokenness as a secret to hide and offers it as common ground: the appeal shifts from I'm as damaged as you to let's be damaged out loud together, since honest wreckage beats the posture of having it handled. The Cash reference isn't just a costume note — it's the record's whole move, wearing the black as defiant style rather than mourning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"offering shared wreckage as warmer ground than composed silence","built":"the polished verse answered each time by rattling bolts beneath"},
"romes~care-less": {"haiku":"Resignation calcifies into acceptance: the spiral toward numbness feels inevitable, and apathy becomes the only rational response to exhaustion.","opus":"Depression that sabotages every good day the moment things go right, the itch of dread creeping back in. Worn down, the narrator settles into apathy, half-resigned that this is the best it will ever get.","sonnet":"A song about the brief window of feeling okay before depression reasserts itself — the speaker gets a good morning, shakes off the dust, and immediately feels that familiar itch pulling them back under, so far gone they have given up fighting it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that lives entirely inside the cruel joke of the title. The verses open on a rare good morning — fresh haircut, a little money, the dust brushed off — but the narrator has learned to distrust exactly this, because for him an upswing is only the moment before the trouble creeps back in, and he treats his own calm as a rented thing that will be returned to the graveyard the moment he lowers his guard. The hook then detonates the pun: care less is not defiance but collapse, the depressive who has run out of the energy to fight and mistakes exhaustion for indifference, hanging on while insisting he has let go. ROMES stages this as the classic alt-rock anthem shape — the big communal chorus you'd shout back — and that's the barb, since the mask is the sing-along swagger of not caring and what's underneath is someone whose own head has turned predator against him, watching a happier world go by and asking why the ration never reaches his table. The final verse tips the balance, the brain that sweats and the mind that turns on itself replacing the earlier flicker of okay-ness, so the repeated closing line reads less like a shrug and more like surrender said one time too many.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","madness & the mind","artifice & performance"],"means":"mistaking depressive collapse for the defiance the title promises","built":"the anthem chorus shape makes a crowd chant someone else's surrender"},
"romes~chillthefuckout": {"haiku":"The artist struggles with creative validation and mental spiraling, writing songs to ease pain but feeling numb and questioning whether anyone listens or whether he has any value.","opus":"An artist spiraling through anxiety and self-loathing, cataloguing how he fits no genre and doubts anyone is even listening, using music as his only medicine. The refrain to just calm down is really a losing fight against overthinking, which he pictures as literally sinking under.","sonnet":"A musician's spiral of anxiety and self-doubt: he doesn't fit any genre, suspects nobody is listening, and can't stop overthinking himself into paralysis. The song is as much a symptom as a diagnosis — he keeps going even as he begs himself to stop.","src":"haiku","fable":"A panic attack that narrates itself in real time, this is ROMES cataloguing every box he fails to check — too soft for the punks, too loud for the pop crowd, never the right shade of alternative or rap for anyone keeping score. The genre-anxiety keeps stacking until it collapses into a plain confession: he makes these tracks only to stay upright, and he assumes the audience left the room. That opening swagger is a bought-cheap costume — the plated jewelry going green at the neck, the smoke-lens glasses hiding a face that is barely holding — and he sees straight through the outfit even as he zips it on. By the second verse he's steering his own funeral car, an urn of what he set alight sliding around behind him. The title's mantra is the joke and the wound at once. Ordering yourself to settle down is precisely the loop that keeps you spinning, and the song acts this out by shrinking to one line about circling the drain, looped past sense into raw reflex. It closes on the same weary demand to quit rambling and let the track die, which refuses the release the chorus keeps dangling — the demons the album title keeps as his best friends stay closest precisely because he can never stop feeding them the conversation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","identity & becoming","artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"ordering yourself calm as the loop that keeps you spiralling","built":"the title mantra looped past sense enacts the anxiety it commands to stop"},
"romes~chillthefuckout-not-pop-enough-version": {"haiku":"Artistic inadequacy and existential numbness plague the speaker as they question whether their work even matters while demons remain untouchable, leaving only the impulse to escape through numbing substances.","opus":"An artist's spiral over not fitting any genre and suspecting nobody is listening, writing songs as self-medication while the numbness deepens. The chorus mantra to calm down is really the sound of someone barely holding on.","sonnet":"A songwriter's public breakdown about not fitting any genre and writing songs only to ease problems no one else hears — driving a hearse full of burned demons, feeling numb, and telling themselves to just chill the fuck out even as the anxiety keeps winning.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built entirely around the anxiety of belonging to no genre bin — too little of pop, of rock, of alt, of the rap lane, failing every style he checks against himself — which becomes the sharper confession that the singer no longer fits himself either, numbed and estranged from who he has become. The catalog of borrowed cool (the counterfeit chain, the cheap blue sunglasses, the pose that reads well but barely holds together) marks a self that is all costume over rot, and by the hearse-and-ashes verse the joke curdles: he claims songwriting is his one cure while insisting nobody is listening, so the therapy is also a performance for no one. The title mantra is the mask — an order to relax barked at a panicking mind — and the crux is how the calming words shred: the chorus keeps demanding the song just end, the mantra stutters and cuts itself off mid-word, and the whispered tags about overthinking and going under turn the reassurance into the very drowning it was supposed to stop. The not-enough framing weaponizes the whole complaint, so the recording is at once the wound and the ironic proof that he keeps making the songs anyway.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the calming mantra shredding into the drowning it was meant to stop","built":"the chorus keeps demanding the song just end while it keeps running"},
"romes~choker": {"haiku":"Entrapment becomes identity: struggling against constraints only tightens them, and breathing becomes the central impossible demand of existence.","opus":"Addiction as a collar that can't be undone, wanting to stop and unable to quit, sick of a craving that strangles the simple wish to just feel alive like everyone else.","sonnet":"A song about being addicted to something that feels like slow suffocation — the speaker has tried to quit, knows they are hurting themselves, but cannot stop because the alternative, simply existing without it, feels even more impossible.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built on the contradiction of compulsion: the narrator wants everything to stop and cannot summon the wish for a single thing beyond it, both at once, and the tension never resolves because that is the truth of being unable to quit. The title image runs the length of the track as a self-tightening loop — a band around the throat that the wearer put on and cannot loosen — so that craving and strangulation become the same gesture, and merely registering the plain fact of being alive reads as the modest, unreachable goal. Movement lives in the arc from an outside voice warning that this will catch up, through the flat cycling of the choruses that enact the being-stuck, to a closing turn where the want shrinks to nothing more than breathing the way an unafflicted person breathes. That final smallness is the read's whole weight: the mask of appetite drops and what is underneath is someone envying the ordinary, unstrangled lung.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"craving and strangulation collapsed into a single unreachable wish","built":"the title image runs as a self-tightening loop through every chorus"},
"romes~erased":{"src":"web","web":"A slow psychological unraveling — watching yourself dissolve, identity and drive and everything gone, on the refrain 'I feel I am slowly being erased,' left with nothing but yourself.","fable":"A song about the slow-motion self-annihilation of addiction, narrated from inside the losing rather than from any hoped-for recovery. The narrator prices out exactly what the next hit costs — ruin metered drop by drop — and reaches for it regardless, and the cruelty is that his self-awareness never buys him an exit: he stands there watching a stranger take over his own face, indicting himself for plunging into a depth he now has no will to climb back out of. The refrain's passive grammar carries the dread, sliding from a possessive claim that all he owned is dissolving to the flatter, more frightened admission that it is now himself dissolving — the vanishing narrowing from what he had to who he is, wiped out in real time while he keeps supplying the commentary.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"self-awareness that names the ruin but cannot purchase an exit","built":"the refrain's passive grammar narrows from possessions dissolving to the self dissolving, the vanishing tightening in real time"},
"romes~everything-you-know-is-not-as-it-seems": {"haiku":"Deception runs deep; attempts to stabilize slip into helplessness and false waking.","opus":"The vertigo of a dream coming apart and a reality that reveals itself as illusion, finding your footing only to plunge further down. It wonders whether a dream can be un-dreamt, which is really wondering whether you can reverse what you have already understood.","sonnet":"A brief lyric about disillusionment — landing on your feet only to sink deeper, waking up to find the world isn't what you were told. The emotional core is the vertigo of having your assumptions stripped away.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dream worn thin until it frays is the whole image here, and the song refuses to move past it: naivety curdles into disillusion, a soft landing reveals itself as a slow sinking, and the title-line comes back not as revelation but as a loop the speaker cannot climb out of. The pivot is the unanswerable question of whether a dream can be un-dreamed — once you have seen the illusion for what it is, you cannot un-see it, but neither can you get back the belief you had before. As the companion to SKI MASK, that refrain is the mask itself: the thing you know, repeated until repetition becomes its own confession that nothing underneath will hold.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["disillusionment","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"disillusion framed as something that cannot be un-seen or recovered from","built":"the title refrain returns not as revelation but as a loop without exit"},
"romes~fever": {"haiku":"After a breakup, the narrator rediscovers his independence and self-worth, celebrating freedom while remaining emotionally guarded.","opus":"A breakup told as liberation, the narrator spending on himself and refusing to answer a partner who never had his back. The swagger barely hides the wound underneath, that he wanted to commit and got burned.","sonnet":"After a one-sided relationship ends, the narrator celebrates his freedom — buying himself the diamond ring he had saved for his partner, refusing her calls, and insisting she only now realizes what she had lost.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song that swaggers to hide a wound. The speaker stages his freedom as consumption — spending the whole paycheque on himself, buying the diamond ring he'd meant for her — and the pop-radio strut of the verses sells it as a clean win. But the fever is the tell: what reads as heat and appetite is really a temperature nobody bothered to take, and the bridge drops the act to admit the swagger was only ever devotion that went unread. The outro completes the turn, sliding from being her fire, the warmth she took for granted, to being on fire himself — the same word swinging from a boast about what he gave her to the burn he's left holding.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","artifice & performance"],"means":"staging freedom as consumption to hide an unread devotion","built":"the word fire swings from gift to wound across the outro"},
"romes~flm": {"haiku":"Authenticity over fame and viral success; the singer rejects the pressure to chase trending moments, choosing genuine self-expression even if it flops, grounded in mortality and real values.","opus":"An artist's blunt reckoning with relevance, self-loathing, and the pressure to produce a hit. He'd rather fail as himself than sell out for fleeting fame, questioning whether people are just trapped animals slowly consuming themselves, and admitting he's inching, at last, toward feeling comfortable in his own skin.","sonnet":"A song about rejecting the music industry's metrics and finding peace in making art without caring whether it lands — the narrator spent years wanting to be someone else, is still working through hell, but is getting closer to being himself.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about making music inside the very machine it claims not to care about, where the swaggering shrug at a record nobody buys is the mask and the terror of being irrelevant — dated by the year, measured by the missing hit, timed against how long the track can go without a hook — is what's underneath. The bragging and the dread aren't in tension so much as fused: the refusal to chase fame reads as sour grapes and as hard-won peace at once, and the song never fully resolves which. What movement there is runs from wanting to be anyone else toward being a little closer to himself, the chorus's careful qualifiers refusing to promise arrival. The bridge drops the whole industry argument to hammer one line about the clock winding down and cutting the noise, letting exhausted repetition do what a clever verse can't — until the noise it wants silenced turns out to include the anxious voice that wrote the rest of the song.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming"],"means":"swagger at irrelevance as both sour grapes and hard-won peace","built":"the bridge silences the industry argument by looping exhaustion past argument"},
"romes~gimme-some-heat":{"haiku":"Artistic integrity demands raw intensity; slickness and compromise are failures to deliver authentic fire and genuine visceral impact.","opus":"A swaggering diss aimed at hollow, sanitized music and the artists chasing hype without substance. The narrator demands intensity loud enough to make the meters bleed, mocking a copy-of-a-copy rival still without a hit in 2025. It's a call to bring raw danger back to a scene gone toothless and overproduced.","sonnet":"A producer or fan dressing down a musician who talks big but makes safe, bloodless music — barely pushing a db, a clone of a clone — demanding raw heat like ODB instead of basement type shit. Straight aesthetic confrontation with no softening.","src":"haiku"},
"romes~glass-eyed-idols":{"haiku":"Religion and celebrity commodified as salvation; prophets and icons prove hollow when crisis arrives and reveal only curated facades behind the veneer.","opus":"A seething indictment of institutions and the false prophets who profit off our fears. Vermin in suits push the ailment and then the remedy, temples are raised from human wreckage, and salvation earns a glowing rating. As every idol splinters and dims, the song wonders who is even left to admire while bracing for something worse.","sonnet":"False prophets selling salvation while the rats in suits sell you your own disease — God on sale with a five-star review, a chapel built from broken bones. The bullet to bite and sun to spit out are the only responses to a world of glass-eyed idols whose promises of rescue are themselves the trap.","src":"haiku"},
"romes~mdambf": {"haiku":"A group of friends insist on their loyalty and deep bond, but the narrator resents being held captive by their unwillingness to let him leave.","opus":"Romes, a Canadian indie-pop band, wrap a quietly sinister message in bright, hooky delivery for an unsettling smiling-menace effect. It's a creepily sweet song about clingy companions who adore you to a suffocating degree and won't allow your escape, dressing possession and control up as devotion while urging you to unwind and wondering why you'd be embarrassed by them, the backward opening lines hinting at buried dread.","sonnet":"A trap with a friendly voice — the opening lines are reversed text, a disorientation device, and what follows is your best friends speaking in the tones of a cult or an addiction, promising love and understanding while making clear you will never be allowed to leave. The warmth is the danger.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title compresses the album's name the way the song compresses its speaker. Everything the sufferer wants to say arrives mirror-written, printed backwards, so his plea to be left alone and his ache over the numbness and the stranger in the glass can only be reached by decoding — while the demons answer in fluent, untroubled forward English. That formal split does the work: the abuser gets the clean sentence and the host gets the scramble, so the demons' insistence that they have read him top to bottom, that no ache of his is a mystery to them, lands as the tenderest kind of menace. Their affection is offered as a devotion so total it curdles into captivity, a fondness that promises he can never walk out. The repeated permission at the close, the speaker agreeing at last that this company is fine, that these voices belong to him, is the hostage learning to speak the captor's line, the mask made comfortable — which is exactly the horror the reversed opening was trying, and failing, to warn against.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The two backwards lines that open it decode to the chorus of Chillthefuckout. — the album’s panic anthem arriving mirror-written inside the demons’ own song.","themes":["madness & the mind","surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the captor's affection recast as devotion too total to escape","built":"the sufferer writes backwards while the demons speak clean forward English"},
"romes~misery-s-on-its-way": {"haiku":"Denial of inner decay masks deterioration approaching despite manufactured composure.","opus":"A man in denial about his own unraveling, insisting he's the man, floating, never wrong, while dread closes in. He refuses the word delusional and keeps rolling the dice, admitting only that he'd rather stay locked out than face what's coming.","sonnet":"A self-aware man in denial about his own deterioration — he refuses to admit things are falling apart, medicates on something unnamed, insists on his own infallibility even as everything signals collapse, and keeps rolling the dice at a game he knows is already over.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of denial narrated from inside the delusion it refuses to name: the speaker keeps insisting he has never lost the plot, never broken down, never once been in the wrong, even as the images around him — spinning out of control, going blind in plain light, still rolling dice on a game already over — quietly convict him. ROMES builds the read entirely on that gap between the boast and the evidence, so the swagger reads as the symptom rather than the rebuttal; the medicine that makes life good is also the price of losing himself, and he can hear the maggots while calling it ecstasy. The turn is that he holds the key and would rather stay locked out — a line looped until repetition becomes its own confession, the choosing of the misery he claims not to believe is coming. What the song is finally about is the hardest thing addiction does to self-knowledge: it lets him brand the one clear-eyed witness he carries a fraud, when that inner accountant is the sole part of him still keeping honest books.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind","shame & repression"],"means":"swagger read as symptom while the boast quietly convicts the boaster","built":"the key-and-locked-door line looped until repetition becomes confession"},
"romes~nothingness": {"haiku":"Exhaustion and spiritual void accumulate until escape into non-existence becomes the only relief from relentless failure and pain.","opus":"Exhaustion and self-loathing after giving everything to a life that keeps demanding patience, likened to a lighter that's run out of fluid. The wish to sink into nothingness is a plea to just be let go of.","sonnet":"Someone who has drunk their life away and physically damaged themselves with bad choices confronts the fact that even given a chance to redo it, they'd probably do the same — and longs to sink into total nothingness rather than keep failing.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song opens as an accusation aimed at someone else — a life drunk away, a phase that never ends, a stubborn insistence that given the choice one would repeat every wasted year identically — before the pronoun flips and the verdict turns out to be self-address, the you and the I the same drowning person. Its image-system is depletion pushed to the point of no return: a lighter with no fluid left, a body bled and given until there is nothing more to spend, so that exhaustion stops being a complaint and becomes a wish. What lifts it out of ordinary despair-rock is that the death-drive is framed as relief rather than tragedy — sinking, slipping, being let go read as mercy, and the platitude to be patient is thrown back with open contempt because trying itself is indicted by the certainty that everyone dies anyway. The repeated title is not a mood but a destination the singer is actively asking for.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","alienation & emptiness","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"framing depletion and dissolution as relief rather than tragedy","built":"the pronoun flip mid-song turns accusation at another into self-verdict"},
"romes~rails-pt-i":{"haiku":"Decay and deterioration are inevitable and inescapable; intrusive thoughts persist despite attempts at destruction.","opus":"A grinding slide into decay and denial. Someone the narrator used to care for is falling apart, dulling their thoughts with a corroded edge while everything they treasured rots. The pounding refrain insists the wreckage won't lift, building to the bleak verdict that this person is past rescue and unravelling for good.","sonnet":"Coming apart in slow motion: head under water, rusted blade tried against an unwanted thought, everything decaying — and the central fact is stubbornly known but never outrun: you know it ain't going away, a big black hole right on the ceiling, the rails departed and no rescue coming.","src":"haiku"},
"romes~rock-n-roll-is-asleep":{"haiku":"Authentic rebellion dies as commercial superficiality replaces substance; the speaker mourns rock's death as genre becomes algorithm-feed performance art.","opus":"A furious lament for a genre hollowed out by clout-chasing and clicks. The narrator sneers at fashion-flex poseurs burning down heaven for engagement, selling struggle as content, terrified of death yet unwilling to live. Rock isn't dead, he argues, just dormant, and he demands someone finally wake it back up.","sonnet":"A broadside against performative rebellion — chrome tongues dripping gasoline, devil horns on dead-end streets, struggles sold in posts for clicks; the narrator calls all of it \"hollow fame\" and \"cut and paste, no taste.\" The thesis is that rock and roll isn't dead, just dormant, and the call is to wake it up before the posturing buries it.","src":"haiku"},
"romes~ski-mask": {"haiku":"Reclaiming agency from one's own mind, the speaker becomes hero of their own narrative by cutting free from internal imprisonment.","opus":"A rescue mission staged inside the narrator's own psyche, arming himself with blade and rope to liberate a buried self held hostage by his mind's demons. Donning the disguise to reclaim who he was turns recovery from mental captivity into a heist against his own thoughts.","sonnet":"A declaration of war against one's own demons — the speaker descends deliberately back into their darkest place, armed and ready to confront what's been holding them hostage inside their own mind, framing self-reclamation as a heist against the part of themselves that has kept them captive.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that borrows the whole grammar of a heist or a jailbreak — the mask, the blade, the accelerant — and turns every weapon inward, so the target of the raid is the speaker's own mind. The mid-song shift from I to you is the key move: the person waiting to be broken out of captivity is a buried earlier self, the one who never got to see who they'd become, and the narrator casts himself as the accomplice going back down to spring him. What reads at first as bravado (going rogue, wanting all the smoke) resolves into something closer to self-reclamation, the demons and fiends being burned as internal figures rather than enemies. The ski mask is the tell: the disguise of a criminal becomes the gear you put on to steal yourself back, as if recovering your own identity were an act you had to commit against the mind that held it hostage.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","madness & the mind"],"means":"casting self-reclamation as a heist committed against one's own mind","built":"the criminal disguise reframed as gear for stealing back a buried self"},
"romes~supaglue": {"haiku":"Psychological paralysis traps the speaker in repetitive failure; trapped like glue inside their own mind, unable to move forward despite effort.","opus":"A vivid picture of mental gridlock: clinging to a final strand, advancing only to end up right where he started, cemented inside his own head, straining to run yet frozen in place, longing to shed the past and simply move on. The recovery-program phrasing hints at frustration with getting clean. Romes, a Toronto indie-pop-rock band, set this depressive loop to a bright, driving hook that aches against its own catchiness.","sonnet":"Depression as total immobility — twelve steps forward and back to square one, motivation drained, the key to the dungeon thrown down a well inside himself. The desire to escape is real and the longing for connection is real, but the mind holds everything in place like an adhesive that can't be dissolved. The song ends mid-word, the escape still unfinished.","src":"haiku","fable":"A depression song built on the gap between forward motion and no progress: the counted strides taken land the speaker back at the same starting mark both times, the recovery-program cadence turned into a treadmill that never delivers the escape the middle passage keeps promising is still possible. The title figures the mind as an adhesive that fuses the body in place while the will strains against it — the running that can't move, the worn footwear that won't pry loose — so paralysis is diagnosed as internal, not circumstantial. Verse two makes the trap self-inflicted, the key to the cell in his skull thrown down a well by the speaker's own hand, which sharpens the cruelty of glimpsing the sun and still not rising. The bridge is the proof: a dozen flat repetitions of wanting to move on, the wanting worn smooth into the very stuckness it's trying to break, until the outro cuts off mid-word and enacts the song's refusal to arrive anywhere.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the will straining against a paralysis that is its own making","built":"outro cuts off mid-word enacting the refusal to arrive"},
"romes~the-dread": {"opus":"Anxiety personified as an inescapable resident of your own mind, taunting that no running or hiding will shake it. The dread introduces itself almost politely, which is what makes it chilling.","sonnet":"Anxiety or depression personified speaks directly to its host, announcing it has taken up residence in the depths of their mind and cannot be outrun.","src":"sonnet","fable":"The whole song is spoken in the voice of the dread itself, which introduces itself with a mock-cheerful greeting and then calmly insists it cannot be outrun because it lives inside the head it is addressing. The taunt of trying to escape or hide only confirms the trap — every route out is a route further in, since the pursuer is the mind doing the fleeing. What little text there is refuses to grow, and that starvation is the point: anxiety here is not an event with verses but a small, patient loop that repeats until the closing question turns on the listener and makes them the one who has to answer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind"],"means":"anxiety speaks in its own voice as a patient, inescapable loop","built":"minimal text refuses to grow, enacting the smallness of the trap"},
"romes~the-dream":{"haiku":"Aspiration and imagined fulfillment, the speaker lost in longing for unrealized fantasy.","opus":"A claustrophobic nightmare loop where the narrator keeps dreaming he's died and woken in a body bag. Running from his own demons, he pours violent, self-lacerating imagery over the beat, casting his life in music as both salvation and slow death. The recurring vision becomes a premonition he can't shake or wake from.","sonnet":"A recurring nightmare of waking up in a bodybag anchors an MC's meditation on creative destruction and survival — visions of a chalk outline, beats kept in the freezer, a lifestyle that nearly swallowed him — the paradox being that he fears this music will be the death of him yet he never thought he'd lose to it.","src":"haiku"},
"romes~we-need-some-help": {"haiku":"Suppressed mental crisis hides beneath performance; the speaker and community mask suffering through forced normalcy.","opus":"A confession that the swagger of being fine masks a shared breakdown, everyone self-medicating and lounging with their private monsters. Flat on his back and gazing upward, the narrator admits that perhaps none of us are coping, even while loudly claiming he is.","sonnet":"A darkly honest admission that everyone is struggling beneath the forced smiles — the song chronicles numbing with drugs, faking wellness, drowning in demons, and the reluctant admission that maybe everyone needs help, punctuated by bursts of bravado that collapse into the same truth.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built on the gap between what people say and what they're drowning in. The choruses speak in a communal we, admitting the numbing and the grin held up over an emptied-out chest as a shared condition, so that the plea for help becomes something everyone is secretly making at once. The verses then narrow to a single voice whose self-affirmation keeps spiking louder and more manic exactly as the coping breaks down. Each protest of being flawless and untouchable arrives right after an admission of medicating past moderation or emptying every last dollar in the hope of becoming someone else; the more insistent the bravado, the more it reads as the mask it's meant to be. That irony collapses in the final shout-along, where the demand to be left alone and the confession of falling apart are screamed in the same breath — the same word, help, functioning as both the thing refused and the thing needed. The ROMES move is to keep the whole thing anthemic and singable so a crowd can chant its own denial back at itself, which is the point: the flat refusal to admit that help is wanted at all is delivered as the loudest cry for it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"communal denial delivered as the loudest possible cry for help","built":"the same word help functions as refusal and confession in the final shout"},
"rootwater~back-to-the-real":{"haiku":"People sacrifice living for wealth, becoming machines obsessed with money. The song urges listeners to abandon material fear and rejoin authentic existence.","opus":"Rootwater, a Polish metal band fusing groove with Eastern-tinged riffing, launch a blunt tirade against a money-worshipping, spiritually dead existence. The narrator contrasts his own vitality against someone turned mechanical by hoarding wealth and scorning others, and shouts at them to abandon greed and fear and plunge back into genuine living.","sonnet":"An angry confrontation with someone who traded aliveness for wealth — a bionic machine counting cash, hating people, feeling superior while being spiritually dead. The song's prescription is physical and abrupt: jump back into real life, feel something, stop fearing loss, talk to a stranger. Money can't be eaten; that's the whole argument.","src":"haiku"},
"rootwater~caje-sukarije":{"haiku":"A Polish/Eastern European reflection mixes non-linguistic chants with vows of devotion, aggression, and historical burden; tribal identity meets inevitable conflict.","opus":"Built on a traditional Romani song, the track sets its Balkan chant against Polish verses about an aggressive pack, loyalty to the tribe, and blood in the streets, invoking the year 1991 and a fear of history's hatred repeating, a vow to be there to save what remains of the soul. Rootwater, a Polish metal band, fuse ethnic folk melody with heaviness, the painted bird image nodding toward outsiderhood and looming violence.","sonnet":"A collision of Romani vocal incantation and Polish lyrics about ethnic aggression — the speaker carries inherited violence in their blood, watches the city burn while someone else dances, recognizes the other as a painted bird from 1991, someone alien and known at once. If it happens again, blood will flow and hatred ignite; the speaker will be there to save whatever remains of their soul.","src":"haiku"},
"rootwater~follow-the-spirit":{"haiku":"A speaker awakens from spiritual death and degradation, choosing transformation over the grave, following spiritual guidance toward light and newfound unity.","opus":"The Polish band cast spiritual awakening as an absolute choice, the narrator realizing he'd been spiritually dead, wasting his life on falsehood, and now finally seeing clearly. The insistent call toward the light lays out only two paths, genuine life or a return to that living death, ending in an experience of love and wholeness he'd never known.","sonnet":"A person emerges from spiritual deadness into something vivid and real, describing the moment of transformation as finally seeing after years buried alive. Following the Spirit is framed as a binary choice — genuine life or a return to the grave — and the singer chooses life with full-voiced certainty.","src":"haiku"},
"rootwater~hava-nagila":{"haiku":"A Jewish singer grapples with hidden family trauma, erasure of identity, and survival through generations. The song explores namelessness, lost mothers, and inherited silence in the aftermath of genocide.","opus":"A Polish metal reckoning with buried Jewish ancestry and the Holocaust, a family that changed its name and hid its origins in fear. The celebratory folk refrain turns bitterly ironic against inherited trauma and denial.","sonnet":"A Polish person wrestles with a hidden Jewish heritage — grandparents who changed their surname to survive, the Holocaust ash ploughed into a kibbutz field, a mother thrown over a wall to safety — and the inherited silence and shame of being an unbaptised secret kept across generations.","src":"haiku"},
"rootwater~living-in-the-cage":{"haiku":"A person trapped in daily drudgery and rage recalls better times before fury took over; they yearn for someone to recognize their pain and heal their wounds.","opus":"Existence as an inescapable daily war, waking each morning into a self-made prison of fear and hate, mourning a lost gentler self. Beneath the fury runs a raw plea for anyone to reach in and heal wounds long hidden from view.","sonnet":"A man trapped in a soul-crushing daily routine describes his life as an inescapable cage of fury and exhaustion, grieving a past when things were different and crying out for someone — anyone — to come and heal him.","src":"haiku"},
"rootwater~steiner":{"haiku":"A person creates an idol as an object of worship, filling existential emptiness through substance abuse and casual intimacy. The song explores idolatry as spiritual compensati​on for void.","opus":"The Polish metal band pair biblical apocalypse with squalid modern despair: a narrator hollowed by an appetite nothing can satisfy, dulling himself with drink, drugs and empty encounters. He fells a tree to carve and gild an idol that is at once his servant and his deity, professing his own likeness and loathing, remaining in the torment of his isolation, scripture's end-times imagery folded into a portrait of faithless ruin.","sonnet":"A man carves a wooden idol in his own image, gilds it, and worships the thing he made — a cycle of self-deification born from an empty soul filled with alcohol, stimulants, and transient sex. Love is proclaimed even so, as a defiant act against a stone heart, a chaotic life, and a theology of second death.","src":"haiku"},
"rootwater~the-ministry":{"haiku":"Blind followers and leaders trapped in mutual delusion feed each other's falsehoods through excessive chemical use and hypocritical messages. Both are lost, questioning where they actually stand in this cycle of blindness.","opus":"A disoriented indictment of mutual blindness and chemical numbing in a world of hypocritical media, where people prop each other up on illusions until nobody knows what's real; the lost cry of not knowing where one even is.","sonnet":"Two people lead each other blindly through a fog of chemical excess and media hypnosis, each believing the other is real enough to restore something that's been lost — ending in a dissociated state where neither knows where they are, the world reduced to dead-brain unreality.","src":"haiku"},
"rotary-connection~i-am-the-black-gold-of-the-sun-remastered":{"opus":"A luminous declaration of selfhood and belonging, the singer claiming kinship with trees, rivers, ocean and peak, unbound and beaming at all. Minnie Riperton's Rotary Connection steer psychedelic soul toward cosmic self-affirmation, the recurring title phrase standing as a proud, radiant identity. The song reaches for unity with the natural world and with light itself.","sonnet":"A cosmic, pantheistic self-declaration — the singer identifies as an elemental force of nature: oak tree, jungle stream, morning sun, mountain, shining sea — all of it part of the black gold of the sun, a life force beyond race or category.","src":"sonnet"},
"rotten-apple~alice-in-chains":{"haiku":"Decay perfumes intimacy as the speaker decays into beloved's arms. Love becomes beautiful rot.","opus":"A hypnotic, chant-driven meditation on lost innocence and the fall from grace. Each verse names a virtue broken, stolen or spoken away, circling the biblical image of eating the apple too young. The narrator, author of his own downfall, keeps crawling back to the start, caught between repentance and the pull of temptation.","sonnet":"A litany of lost innocence catalogued as -ance words stripped bare — innocence over, confidence broken, sustenance stolen — and the pivotal image is eating the apple young and crawling back to start, having written your own part in a fall that seemed like freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"royal-blood~boilermaker":{"haiku":"Internal chaos and twisted desire run deep; the speaker's mind is a shaken cocktail of contradiction, living like a decaying storefront while offering themselves as a dangerous thrill.","opus":"A man mixed-up and self-destructive, drawn to a dangerous woman and staring down the bottom of a strong drink. It is about surrendering to bad odds and vice while sensing he and his temptation are cut from the same cloth.","sonnet":"Self-described as all mixed up — head like a cocktail shaker, staring at the bottom of a boilermaker — the speaker was drawn to someone with devil eyes and magic hands who he knew would bring trouble, and now invites them to use whatever's twisted up inside him before things run dry.","src":"haiku"},
"royal-blood~i-only-lie-when-i-love-you":{"haiku":"Love and deception become indistinguishable; the speaker admits their lies stem from devotion, confessing that manipulation and desire cannot be separated within their twisted heart.","opus":"A confession that love and dishonesty are fused for the speaker, who only lies when he loves and wishes the partner were someone else. It is about a corrosive relationship built on suspicion, manipulation, and mutual hurt.","sonnet":"A paradox of intimacy: the speaker only lies in the context of love, the partner only cries in the context of love, and the whole relationship is a tangle of guilt and misdirection — \"I got my conscience twisted\" — where honesty is impossible and the lie is the only honest thing left.","src":"haiku"},
"royal-blood~limbo-orchestral-version":{"haiku":"Suspended animation and psychological paralysis trap consciousness between life and death; the narrator fades while awaiting rescue that may never arrive.","opus":"Waking each day surprised to have survived, trapped between numbness and dissolving self, begging to be pulled out before it's too late. Depression rendered as a limbo you can't reverse out of.","sonnet":"A person trapped in psychological and physical deterioration — waking to blood and tears, unable to move forward or back — pleads to be woken slowly before they go completely cold, stuck in a limbo between the person they were and someone they no longer recognise.","src":"haiku"},
"royal-blood~mountains-at-midnight":{"haiku":"Towering obstacles in darkness, challenges amplified by night and isolation.","opus":"The British duo's bass-heavy swagger dresses up a restless nocturnal craving. The narrator casts himself as a charming menace, a ticking hooligan bouncing off the walls till daybreak, chasing highs and fixes that never quite deliver the feeling he's after. Beneath the bravado lurks a persistent fear he can't quite outrun.","sonnet":"Addiction framed as a volatile, thrilling alter ego — \"twenty-four carat thug in a velvet glove\" — crawling out of bones until daylight, bouncing off walls up mountains at midnight. Nothing gets close to the feeling wanted; each row of zeros and shiver of the spine is another cycle of the same chase.","src":"haiku"},
"royal-blood~ten-tonne-skeleton":{"haiku":"A toxic lover drains the speaker's vitality while refusing accountability; waiting for reciprocal love delivers only heartbreak and psychological stagnation.","opus":"A love that flared and died, both people still hung up on the wrong ones, the past too heavy to erase. Desire and betrayal tangled, the weight of what can't be undone dragging everything under.","sonnet":"A man devastated by a lover who drained him emotionally and disappeared now wrestles with the impossibility of forgetting — she is still loving someone else while he is still loving her, and neither truth nor burning the past can offer an exit.","src":"haiku"},
"royal-coda~as-we-fall-into-deep-waters":{"haiku":"Systemic alienation breeds passive witness—the speaker observes drowning civilizations while questioning whether anyone truly wants to change.","opus":"Disgust at the information age turning people into blank-faced consumers and data points who argue over trivia while nothing gets solved. Against the noise, a small inner voice keeps saying no, refusing to run with liars and asking where his own share went.","sonnet":"Standing at a river's edge, contemplating how long it would take to disappear, the speaker hears a small internal voice say no; around this near-breaking-point circulates critique of data-harvested consumers, online arguments going nowhere, and a generation that never truly grows.","src":"haiku"},
"royal-coda~even-if":{"haiku":"Time's cruelty erodes all bonds as manufactured beings cycle through repetition; love's impermanence renders connection fragile despite desperate attachment.","opus":"Time as the enemy of every fragile feeling, love and happiness treated as borrowed things fated to turn to distress. The unanswerable dread of whether devotion outlasts the built-in kill switch in us all.","sonnet":"Time is framed as the enemy of love and happiness — borrowed contentment always curdling into distress — and the speaker keeps asking whether love, even when it cuts deep, can outlast the relentless pressure of impermanence.","src":"haiku"},
"royal-coda~ruby-leaf":{"opus":"A tangle of guilt, blame and impending loss, the narrator cast as the worm after slipping up and taking the bait. Royal Coda's post-hardcore emotion frames a doomed relationship through a garden grown for someone leaving, tears offered as consolation. It sits in the resignation of being made worms' meat, agreement reached only in ruin.","sonnet":"About accepting blame and eventual decomposition after a relationship collapses — the narrator made a series of small errors that compounded, took the bait they shouldn't have, and now accepts that worms will make meat of them: they were dying to matter and knew from the start it would end this way.","src":"sonnet"},
"royal-deceit~bite-the-curb":{"haiku":"A manifesto against hate preachers who will never change their minds; the speaker commits to defending the weak while the perpetrators face violent consequences for their refusal of compassion.","opus":"Hardcore drawing a hard moral line against those who spread bigotry, the narrator done extending compassion to people who refuse to change and casting himself as a protector of the vulnerable, insisting he is nothing like them.","sonnet":"About facing down ideological or social enemies who refuse to change their hateful minds — the narrator refuses to turn the other cheek, promising their teeth will be ground into asphalt, distinguishing themselves from the hate-preachers by fighting to defend the vulnerable.","src":"haiku"},
"royal-republic~save-the-nation":{"haiku":"Charismatic authority exploits collective hunger for meaning; populism promises salvation while demanding submission.","opus":"A grinning satire of the demagogue politician, the messiah-savior who promises everything and demands only your vote. The self-appointed savior of the nation as pure ego and hollow spectacle.","sonnet":"A satirical portrait of a self-appointed political saviour who flatters the electorate into believing only he can rescue them, conflating his ego with the public good and offering empty promises in exchange for votes.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~anthem":{"haiku":"Selfishness offers authentic liberation while collective demands and borrowed morality enslave; the individual who refuses to conform becomes the unsung hero of their own narrative.","opus":"Peart's early Ayn Rand phase distilled: a defiant hymn to living for yourself, rejecting guilt over selfishness and the claims of begging hands. Objectivism set to a blistering tempo, ideology worn on the sleeve.","sonnet":"A hymn to radical individualism drawn from Ayn Rand's philosophy, arguing that self-interest is a virtue and that collective demands on the individual represent a form of blindness — the speaker wrote the song for himself alone.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~bravest-face":{"haiku":"Complexity and contradiction exist within every person; the world offers no magic solutions, only the daily act of masking pain and marching forward with practiced composure.","opus":"Peart's clear-eyed answer to sentimental optimism: every sweetness holds a cruelty, every strength a weakness, and no magic place exists to save us. The only honest response is to rise and face it anyway.","sonnet":"An honest reckoning with the world's irreducible darkness — rejecting both naive optimism and despair — that concludes the only valid response to a world with no magic place is to rise anyway and put on your bravest face.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~bu2b":{"haiku":"The narrator was raised to accept a predetermined plan and believe in divine justice, but questions whether blind faith and predetermined fate serve humanity.","opus":"Neil Peart sketches a mind raised to accept a rigged cosmic bargain, where a loving Watchmaker doles out joy and pain as deserved and the faithful buy whatever they are sold. Opening Rush's Clockwork Angels narrative, it establishes a clockwork-universe orthodoxy the album's protagonist will later question, its refrain framing obedient belief as a slow, sanctioned death.","sonnet":"A man raised on providential faith interrogates the doctrine he was handed at birth. The framework that promised cosmic fairness and divine reward reveals itself as a mechanism of control, with a Watchmaker god whose love is indistinguishable from slow destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~caravan":{"haiku":"A young person on a caravan journey to the city dreams of escape and opportunity, determined to break from the past while harboring ambition in a world that makes him feel small.","opus":"A nocturnal traveler pushes through a raw, flame-only landscape toward the far-off vision of a metropolis where a youth could rise and cut ties with what he was, unable to quiet his soaring ambition even as he feels tiny against it.","sonnet":"A young person leaves behind obligation and travels by night toward a city of possibility, carried forward by an uncontainable ambition and the refusal to think small, no matter how large the world feels around him.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~carnies":{"haiku":"A young boy caught in a carnival's dark supernatural machinery faces violence and accusation. His prayers for escape are answered cruelly, trapping him in a deadly game of fate.","opus":"Rush, the Canadian prog trio, drew this narrative from their steampunk concept album Clockwork Angels, Neil Peart's storytelling at its most cinematic. A boy at a fairground watches wonder rot into terror, false accusation and a hidden threat placed in innocent hands turning a crowd murderous. Its cruel moral: sometimes the divine punishes us by granting exactly what we asked for.","sonnet":"A boy at a carnival who prays to escape his life — and the angels answer, dropping him into something far worse. Spinning lights and gypsy queens give way to a confrontation with naked evil, a ticking box placed in an innocent hand, an angry crowd turning on him. The prayer was answered literally and catastrophically: sometimes escape is just a different kind of trap.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~ceiling-unlimited":{"haiku":"Modern malaise and endless restlessness—the world is vast but satisfaction remains elusive; change and desire never end.","opus":"Lyricist Neil Peart offers a bleak reading of modern life, where urban cruelty, violent imagery, and empty spectacle pass by like reflections in distorting glass and knowledge itself feels like a burden. Masks of comedy and tragedy dress up vanity and madness, yet the Canadian trio let the steady flow of time carry a thread of renewal against the despair.","sonnet":"A meditation on overstimulation and cultural saturation — machine-gun media images, the vacant grin of mass spectacle, idle minds kept docile by programmed entertainment. Against this backdrop, the ceiling remains unlimited: change never stops, hope winds forward like a river, and the present moment always reasserts its claim.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~clockwork-angels":{"haiku":"Faith in divine machinery and mysterious order—heavenly clockwork goddesses inspire worship and resignation to 'perfect planning' over understanding.","opus":"The title track of Rush's final album and steampunk concept record depicts towering mechanical figures worshipped by a crowd that surrenders its judgment to them in exchange for promised rewards. The Canadian trio present the spectacle with real awe, yet the pull to abandon reason and trust in someone else's grand design carries a subtle, unmistakable warning.","sonnet":"Mechanical angels suspended over a city square distribute promises and receive worship from a crowd that raises hands as if to fly. The imagery combines religious submission with clockwork precision — a celestial order that commands trust, forbids independent understanding, and guarantees everything will work out, while the angels themselves remain beautiful and remote.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~countdown":{"haiku":"Technological spectacle and collective anticipation precede momentous launch; human achievement and natural awe merge at the threshold.","opus":"A near-documentary of the first Space Shuttle launch the band witnessed, awe at technology meeting the raw stuff of dreams. Human wonder at the moment science lifts off into myth.","sonnet":"A documentary tribute to the launch of the Space Shuttle Columbia, interweaving actual launch control communications with lyrical awe at the spectacle: the technological and the transcendent merge as the rocket tears away from earth, leaving a pillar of smoke and the world staring upward.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~dreamline":{"haiku":"Wanderers pursue redemption and freedom through constant motion, discovering that immortality is brief and meaning exists only in the journey itself.","opus":"Youth as perpetual motion, lovers and dreamers only at home while running, chasing horizons and knowing their immortality comes with an expiry date. The romance and melancholy of a life defined by never arriving.","sonnet":"Young wanderers chase an endless horizon of freedom and possibility, living on the road and in dreams, learning the bittersweet truth that immortality is only on loan and time is always slipping away.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~far-cry":{"haiku":"Instability defines modern life where fortune swings wildly and control remains illusory; the only constant is adaptation and the ability to recover from each fall.","opus":"Reckoning with a world that turned out nothing like the one they expected to inherit, all turbulence and betrayal, yet insisting on resilience. Post-9/11 disillusionment answered by the refusal to stay down.","sonnet":"A portrait of a world that has fallen short of its promise, where the speaker rides the violent oscillation between highs and crashes with determined resilience, always pulling himself back on after each collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~ghost-rider":{"haiku":"A wandering traveler carries invisible burdens across vast landscapes, haunting wilderness roads while pursued and pursued by shadows of the past.","opus":"Neil Peart translates his own grief-stricken motorcycle wandering into a figure hauling an unseen burden across wilderness roads, chasing daylight while shadows stretch before and behind. Written after the deaths that nearly ended Rush, it casts the mourner as a fugitive and drifting recluse, finding beauty on the endless ride but never quite peace.","sonnet":"A grief-stricken wanderer crosses the continent in relentless motion, carrying invisible weight through desert, canyon, and forest. Movement itself becomes both symptom and salve — he cannot stop, cannot find peace, but sunrise occasionally lifts the darkness long enough to keep riding.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~halo-effect":{"haiku":"The speaker projects idealized illusions onto romantic partners, later regretting how repeatedly they fell in love with false versions of people.","opus":"Rush placed this on Clockwork Angels, their final album, Neil Peart trading youthful romantic error for hard-won hindsight in his literate, reflective register. It's a rueful confession of idealizing a partner, the narrator branding himself a fool who draped his fantasies over an imagined divine figure and only gradually saw how the flattering vision he kept surrendering to was a self-made delusion.","sonnet":"A retrospective on projecting an ideal onto a real person and loving the projection rather than her — a goddess built from youthful desire, friends horrified by the delusion but unable to say so. The speaker looks back across a lifetime of the same mistake, ashamed at how often the pretty picture was enough to fall for all over again.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~how-it-is":{"haiku":"Despair trapping people in present frustration, unable to imagine better futures. Resignation and lack of imagination keep people frozen between how things are and how they should be.","opus":"A quiet meditation on resignation, the moment when a clouded outlook shrinks your horizon and faith in better days curdles into simply giving up. Rush, on their late-career Vapor Trails, counsel plain persistence over despair, a weathered, grown-up wisdom far removed from the prog epics of their youth.","sonnet":"The paralysis of accepting things as they are — a gap between how life is and how it ought to be that most people stop trying to cross. Resignation is the trap: cloudy days that seem permanent, imagination failing exactly when it's needed. You can't tell yourself what to feel, can't force hope, can only keep your shoulder to the wheel and wait for the weather to change.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~lessons":{"haiku":"Wisdom repeats unheeded until personal crisis forces comprehension; the speaker pledges to teach what hard experience has taught.","opus":"A young person finally understanding advice they once ignored, realizing they'll one day pass on what they were too stubborn to hear. The generational loop of wisdom offered, refused, and eventually learned.","sonnet":"Someone who was told the truth repeatedly but refused to hear it is addressed with a mixture of disappointment and hard-won acceptance: the speaker knows they will only understand when they reach the knowledge themselves, and the lesson is as much about the limits of teaching as it is about what needs to be learned.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~limelight":{"haiku":"Fame isolates the performer from authentic connection, forcing them to maintain barriers to protect themselves while the world watches and judges.","opus":"A reflection on the strangeness of fame, portraying life on a lighted stage as unreal and alienating, forcing the performer to raise barriers to stay intact while treating all the world as a stage of players.","sonnet":"Living in public view is described as an unreal, alienating experience that requires emotional barriers to stay intact; the song distinguishes between those who merely wish to appear famous and those who genuinely engage with life, arguing the latter must overcome alienation to find real connection.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~nocturne":{"haiku":"Dreams and waking consciousness blur as unconscious symbols reveal hidden truths about desire and identity beyond rational control.","opus":"A voyage through the dream state as a nocturnal sea-crossing with neither compass nor chart, where the unconscious throws up symbols and revelations and waking only opens onto another dream. Peart treats the dream as temporary madness whose morning answer is nonetheless yes.","sonnet":"A reflection on the dream state as temporary madness and unconscious revelation — drifting through lost latitudes, hallucinations and signal fires lighting secret places of the heart — with the morning always answering yes to the question of whether the dream was real or the dreamer was had.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~one-little-victory":{"haiku":"Small personal victories of independence and breaking free are celebrated as profound spiritual acts, regardless of scale.","opus":"Rush deliver a motivational anthem prizing nerve, resilience and the readiness to gamble on failure, treating every modest triumph as a small liberation of the spirit. The larger idea is that anything great is assembled from a chain of humble wins, powered by imagination, resolve and the courage to strike out on an unfamiliar path.","sonnet":"A celebration of incremental triumph over long odds — each small win over adversity matters because it represents a spirit refusing to be extinguished, and that single degree of difference is sometimes the greatest act possible.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~out-of-the-cradle":{"haiku":"Life perpetually rocks between creation and endlessness, driven by energy and inspiration that manifests as electricity. The cradle's motion persists eternally as existence itself.","opus":"Love and creation celebrated as an inexhaustible generative force answering to countless titles, the cradle forever rocking, a Whitman-tinged current of energy and inspiration renewing life. Neil Peart at his most exuberantly affirmative.","sonnet":"Love, creativity, and vitality are defined not as places or things but as forces — yearning, action, electricity — and humanity emerges from the cradle endlessly rocking, driven by a surge of energy that mirrors time itself mocking us with its flight.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~peaceable-kingdom":{"haiku":"The speaker laments humanity's endless cycle of conflict and competing ideologies, holding out hope for peace while recognizing that those with power ignore pleas for understanding.","opus":"A meditation on how useless reasoned argument becomes in a divided world: for every voice preaching harmony, countless others push conflict and hatred. Rush lace it with tarot symbolism that turns human striving into a cosmic card game, its bleak conclusion being that the people who most need to listen already tuned out long ago.","sonnet":"A weary meditation on the futility of rational peacekeeping in a world full of competing ideologies and violence — the tarot imagery underscores how opposing forces perpetually cancel each other out, and those who most need to hear the message of peace never will.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~red-barchetta":{"haiku":"A young person escapes surveillance by riding a forbidden vintage car, racing authorities in a world where machines are illegal. The song celebrates rebellion and the thrill of living outside control.","opus":"A dystopian short story by drummer Neil Peart, set in a future where private cars are outlawed. A young man secretly restores a forbidden sports car at his uncle's farm and takes an illicit joyride, outrunning a pursuing authority vehicle at a narrow bridge. Canadian prog trio Rush turn the thrill of speed into quiet rebellion.","sonnet":"In a future where the Motor Law has banned pre-modern machines, a young man makes a weekly pilgrimage to his uncle's barn to drive a vintage red Barchetta — one Sunday, two enforcement air-cars give chase across mountain roads before he outwits them at a one-lane bridge.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~seven-cities-of-gold":{"haiku":"A traveler crossing harsh desert terrain chases mythical golden cities, losing themselves to mirages and exhaustion while pursuing legendary treasure.","opus":"A conquistador's fever-dream in the desert, a man shedding his history and nearly his life in a pathless, cactus-choked land while chasing a golden mirage that gleams like a vision, remote as a passing comet. The fabled cities become both bait and hallucination. Rush cut this on Clockwork Angels, and Neil Peart's fascination with quest and mirage, married to the trio's muscular progressive attack, turns the myth into a meditation on obsession and futility.","sonnet":"A desert quest for the mythic Seven Cities of Gold — canyons, cactus, blinding sun and deep Saharan snow — where the mirage of glory keeps the traveller moving even as the land consumes identity, direction, and finally life itself. The gold is always a hallucination on the horizon, glittering exactly as far away as it needs to be to keep him walking.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~something-for-nothing":{"haiku":"Effort and intentionality shape destiny; passive waiting guarantees stagnation, while active pursuit demands courage and persistence.","opus":"A rebuke to passivity and entitlement: nobody hands you freedom or wisdom while you wait for luck. Self-reliance as the whole message, your own kingdom built by your own effort.","sonnet":"A direct challenge to passivity and entitlement: freedom and wisdom do not come without effort, and waiting for someone else to deliver them is a delusion. The speaker insists that what you own, love, do, and live are all products of your own making — no one gets something for nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~the-analog-kid":{"haiku":"A restless youth watches dreams and possibilities shimmer in the distance, yearning for escape and transcendence.","opus":"A boy dreaming in the summer grass, torn between the pull of the wider world and the fear of what leaving costs. The universal restless ache of youth longing to depart before it even knows toward what.","sonnet":"A restless boy lying in summer grass is pulled between the world's beauty — a girl, open seas, city lights, autumn woods — and the weight of not knowing what he is chasing or what he will leave behind. The longing is pure and unanswerable, as much pain as wonder.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~the-anarchist":{"haiku":"A person filled with rage and isolation plots revenge, poisoned by denied dreams and deep emotional wounds.","opus":"Part of Rush's Clockwork Angels story, this inhabits an embittered outsider whose lifetime of exclusion has hardened into a taste for revenge. Denied the comforts and affection others take for granted, he plots destruction alone, the song treating political extremism as a psychological wound, an idealism that soured into isolation and hate.","sonnet":"A portrait of accumulated resentment curdling into nihilism: someone denied affection, opportunity, and belonging who has spent a lifetime envying those who received what he was never given, until the deprivation calcifies into a private ideology of vengeance.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~the-big-money":{"haiku":"Wealth concentrates power and corrupts absolutely; money's invisible hand orchestrates desire, cruelty, and the illusion of choice.","opus":"Capital personified as a vast, soulless force that pulls strings, builds and destroys, and answers to no morality. Peart cataloguing how money moves the world with a mean streak and no soul.","sonnet":"A comprehensive portrait of money as an amoral force that builds and destroys with equal indifference — creating dreams, pulling strings, crushing the weak, and leaving bruises while also enabling entire lives. The song refuses to sentimentalize or simply condemn, instead mapping the full contradictory weight of capital's power.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~the-camera-eye":{"haiku":"An observational study of urban life in two great cities, contrasting the frenetic energy of New York with the atmospheric stillness and history of London.","opus":"An expansive musical portrait pairing two metropolises, first hard-set Manhattan commuters hurrying against dusk beneath a gentle drizzle, then time-worn London wrapped in Westminster haze. The narrator absorbs the distinct glow of each place, sensing both hope and stern truth. Drawn into the band's Moving Pictures era, its literary-borrowed title makes the fabric of city life the true subject.","sonnet":"Two cities, two moods: Manhattan as purposeful human torrent, angular and self-oblivious; London as green-grey wistfulness, history soaked into the rain. The camera-eye speaker absorbs both, feeling simultaneously the pull of possibility and the friction of hard reality, the focus always sharp because the city demands it.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~the-garden":{"haiku":"Life measures itself through love and respect, where cultivation and protection create meaning against time's theft. The future lives only in the moments we tend with care.","opus":"Neil Peart's late-career reckoning that a life is measured only by love and respect and the gifts you give, a garden to tend as the years run out before the Watchmaker's clock stops.","sonnet":"A philosophical meditation on mortality and meaning: time ticks inexorably, cells decay, but the measure of a life is love and respect — hard to earn, easily burned — and what remains beyond the arrow's flight is the hope dwelling in that single moment between future and memory.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~the-spirit-of-radio":{"opus":"A tribute to the everyday magic of hearing the right song on the airwaves, set against a sharp complaint about how the music business corrupts that freedom. Rush celebrate radio as a loyal companion, then mourn how commerce and compromise drown honest artistry beneath a marketing pitch.","sonnet":"An ode to early radio as a medium of pure musical connection and emotional honesty, mourning how the music industry's commercial machine — driven by glittering prizes and compromises — has shattered that original integrity.","src":"sonnet"},
"rush~the-stars-look-down":{"haiku":"Humans believe they have agency and control their own paths, but they are insignificant specks while the universe observes indifferently.","opus":"A cosmic puncturing of human self-importance, comparing the listener to an insect riding a turning wheel bragging about the dust it kicks up, or a lab rodent claiming to pick its own way through the corridors, all beneath uncaring stars that merely gaze back at our fantasies of control. Rush placed this on Snakes and Arrows, and Neil Peart's skeptical bent, questioning free will and grand schemes against a vast unmoved universe, drives its cold refrain.","sonnet":"A cosmic deflation — the fly on the wheel convinced it raises the dust, the rat certain it chose its own path through the maze. Both are wrong, and the stars look down on all of it with indifferent silence. The song doesn't comfort; it just holds the question open: was it meaning, confusion, or just being carried away?","src":"haiku"},
"rush~the-wreckers":{"haiku":"Shipwrecked sailors are saved by human chains forming on shore, but opportunists loot the wreckage—salvation becomes exploitation and betrayal.","opus":"A seafaring parable warning that something too perfect may be a trap, ships deliberately lured onto rocks by false lights so scavengers can loot them, even the rescuers turning predator. Rush placed this on Clockwork Angels, Neil Peart's fable of deception closing the album on a note of hard-earned wariness, its lesson being that sometimes you are the prey.","sonnet":"Shipwrecked sailors lured onto rocks by false lights — a human chain that turns out to be wreckers stripping the cargo, not rescuers. The broader lesson is that miracles and rescuers and truths all need scrutiny, because sometimes the target is you, and by the time the wave of clarity hits, the wreck is already done and the blood washes clean.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~time-stand-still":{"haiku":"A weary traveler wishes to pause and savour moments with loved ones, haunted by time's relentless passage and lost innocence.","opus":"An appeal to decelerate the frenzy of living, the narrator facing away from the gust to steady himself, wishing to survey his surroundings instead of dwelling behind, to still the present and cling to those near him before lived experience and youth vanish. The seasons race, companions age. Rush recorded this on 1987's Hold Your Fire, with Aimee Mann guesting; it stands as their grown-up grappling with mortality and being present.","sonnet":"The wish to slow down long enough to feel what's happening — summers going fast, children growing up, old friends aging, experience slipping away before it can be absorbed. The speaker has been moving too fast, letting the past blur, and now wants just a pause: not backward, just around, to see the people and places that are already fading. Innocence doesn't wait.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~tom-sawyer":{"haiku":"Tom Sawyer is an independent thinker who rejects control and government authority, living by his own will while society judges his company.","opus":"Neil Peart's sketch of the modern free thinker who refuses to hand his mind over to any authority, self-contained and quietly rebellious. Through this figure the song meditates on independence and change, arguing that what you make of the man reflects what you make of society itself.","sonnet":"A portrait of an uncompromising modern individualist — today's Tom Sawyer owes nothing to gods or governments, rides out the world on his own terms, and the friction and energy he generates are the measure of his existence.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~vital-signs":{"haiku":"Life's instability and constant mental/emotional flux require people to break from norms and find balance amid chaos.","opus":"A meditation on the human mind as a wired system prone to interference, crossed signals and distortion, urging everyone to break from the norm through persistence and conviction. Rush closed their 1981 album Moving Pictures with this track, and it captures the Canadian trio's turn toward new-wave and synth textures, Neil Peart's lyrics trading fantasy for a cool, cybernetic vocabulary of circuits, polarity and information processing.","sonnet":"A diagnosis of modern consciousness — the song maps human interaction as an electrical system prone to short circuits and distorted signals, arguing that exhaustion scrambles our perception and makes us shape-shift between moods. The resolution is stubbornly optimistic: persistence is what drags a dream into reality, and everyone, eventually, has to elevate above the default setting.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~witch-hunt":{"haiku":"Vigilantes pursue and destroy those labeled as dangerous outsiders due to ignorance and fear, silencing dissent through violent mob justice justified by righteous certainty.","opus":"Part of Rush's Fear trilogy, Neil Peart's lyric depicts a mob of self-righteous vigilantes gathering by torchlight, convinced their hatred is virtuous as they target outsiders, immigrants, and forbidden books. It's a stark indictment of how fear, ignorance, and prejudice let people commit violence in the name of protecting others.","sonnet":"A torch-bearing mob gathers on a hill, righteous in its fear and hatred of strangers, immigrants, and whatever lurks in theatres and bookstores. The portrait is cold and precise: these are not monsters but ordinary people convinced they know best, letting ignorance and prejudice march hand in hand toward violence.","src":"haiku"},
"rush~working-man":{"haiku":"A worker laments his exhausting daily grind of earning and surviving, trapped in a cycle with no time for living.","opus":"A blue-collar lament in plain language: rise early, clock in, drink a cold beer at day's end, and nurse the nagging sense that life could be lived better than this grind allows. This early Rush track from their 1974 debut, sung by Geddy Lee before Neil Peart joined, became a Cleveland radio breakout and anchored the band's hard-rock reputation with its heavy riff and everyman weariness.","sonnet":"A portrait of grinding routine without complaint — up at seven, at work by nine, home at five with a cold beer, wondering why nothing ever happens. The worker doesn't rebel or philosophize; he just names his condition plainly. The label the world gives him is the only identity he seems to have left, and he accepts it with weary resignation.","src":"haiku"},
"rustpr~hate-me":{"haiku":"Self-loathing becomes compulsive cycle; the speaker absorbs and amplifies cruelty, inviting others to match their own internal destruction.","opus":"Self-harm turned into a dare, inviting another to hate and hurt him as much as he already hates himself, insisting that whatever cruelty they inflict he did to himself first.","sonnet":"Severe self-loathing externalized as an invitation for others to inflict the same damage the speaker already does to themselves — the song is about self-harm and suicidal ideation framed as a dare, with the speaker insisting they've already done worse to themselves than anyone else could.","src":"haiku"},
"rvnt~dead-and-gone":{"haiku":"Condemns paternal violence and abuse, expressing rage at a father's neglect and suffering inflicted. Hopes for his damnation and death.","opus":"A raw reckoning with an abusive father, the narrator carrying childhood memories of violence and blood, wishing guilt would haunt the man and finding grim relief in his death from addiction. The stance moves from pleading and worthlessness toward furious refusal to forgive. Delivered in the confessional, cathartic register of modern metalcore, it channels personal trauma into screamed condemnation and a hard-won sense of survival.","sonnet":"A survivor's indictment of an abusive father — the song confronts a childhood memory of domestic violence, directing pure rage at a man who inflicted pain without remorse and left scars of guilt in the one who witnessed it. The closing reveal that the abuser was also an addict sharpens the contempt: not grief at his death, but relief.","src":"haiku"},
"rxptrs~you-me-and-the-devil-make-three":{"haiku":"Defiant indulgence and recklessness face damnation without fear or repentance.","opus":"A reckless, self-aware plunge through vice and existential dread, gulping down bitter-swallowed pills with a bullseye on his back. The bravado, cool as a rigged explosive, masks a man untroubled by either dying or living, choosing to go up in flames rather than repent.","sonnet":"About living with self-destructive impulses — drinking, swallowing pills, a chest full of metaphorical landmines — while still keeping a kind of dark dignity; the narrator knows things will never be alright but refuses to fool himself about it, framing his condition with black humor and swagger.","src":"haiku"},
"ryan-oakes~heavyweight":{"haiku":"Material success becomes proof of worth externalized; the speaker measures dominance through wealth and cultural impact while remaining uncertain of inner substance.","opus":"A rapper's flex built on outlasting the peers who peaked in high school, tallying money and longevity as proof he won. The braggadocio doubles as revenge: thriving is the sharpest insult he can aim at the people rooting for his failure.","sonnet":"A boastful reclamation of status against people who peaked in high school — the speaker has a million-dollar deal, crypto gains, real estate ambitions, and takes \"guilty pleasure\" in the hate directed at him, positioning himself as a heavyweight whose words put rivals to sleep.","src":"haiku"},
"s-efforcer~glass":{"haiku":"Medication-induced dissociation erases the self, leaving only numbness and fragmented awareness.","opus":"Sedation as slow erasure of self, days spent medicated and unawakened until feeling itself is stripped away. The horror is quiet, a fragile person watching their identity dissolve pill by pill, screaming at ceilings.","sonnet":"A portrait of medication-induced dissociation — the speaker drifts through days in artificial light, sleeping for stretches, losing the self he knew pill by pill, screaming at ceilings as the world inverts and feeling fades into haze.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~40-1":{"haiku":"Polish soldiers during World War II make a stand against overwhelming Nazi forces; the song honors their defiant resistance and memorial to soldiers who refused retreat.","opus":"Sabaton's tribute to the vastly outnumbered Polish defenders at Wizna in 1939, holding their bunkers against the Wehrmacht in a Spartan last stand. It's a stubborn hymn to defiance and remembrance, fathers and sons buried in history.","sonnet":"A tribute to the Polish defenders of Wizna in September 1939, outnumbered forty to one by the Wehrmacht — the song honors their suicidal stand as an act of Spartan courage and an overlooked chapter of military history.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~aces-in-exile":{"haiku":"Polish, Czech, and Canadian fighter pilots, far from home, fly into battle during WWII and historically save the day with unmatched skill and sacrifice.","opus":"A tribute to WWII fighter pilots from occupied nations (Poland's 303, Czechoslovakia's 310, Canada's 401) who flew for the Allies in the Battle of Britain; exiles far from home, many sent to die, remembered for turning the war's tide.","sonnet":"A tribute to the foreign fighter pilot squadrons — Polish No. 303, Czechoslovak No. 310, and Canadian No. 401 — who flew for the RAF during the Battle of Britain, far from their occupied homelands, and turned the tide in the air.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~back-in-control":{"haiku":"British forces invade and retake the Falkland Islands through military aggression and firepower, restoring sovereignty through conquest. The narrative glorifies colonial reclamation.","opus":"A blunt war-metal retelling of the 1982 Falklands campaign from the British side, with Thatcher as the iron maiden ordering the islands retaken, celebrated as reconquest with no room for doubt about the cost.","sonnet":"A blow-by-blow account of the British recapture of the Falkland Islands in 1982, with Thatcher as the \"iron maiden\" issuing orders to take the islands back through amphibious assault, artillery, and airstrikes — straightforward military history set to metal.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~carolus-rex":{"haiku":"A ruler claims divine mandate to expand his empire through conquest, answering only to God. The song celebrates absolute monarchy and military expansion as destiny.","opus":"The Swedish power-metal historians voice young King Charles XII of Sweden claiming his throne by divine mandate, answerable to no earthly authority. Scorned as too green to lead, he vows brutal payback and continental conquest. Sabaton stage the king's absolute arrogance as a thunderous coronation hymn sung in his own voice.","sonnet":"The ascent of a teenage king who refuses church coronation, claiming divine authority directly. Charles XII of Sweden narrates his own destiny — overlooked, then vindicated on the battlefield — demanding absolute obedience across Europe and brooking no earthly power between himself and God.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~cliffs-of-gallipoli":{"haiku":"Young soldiers at Gallipoli sacrifice their lives in a futile campaign where enemies become friends in death, their letters and bones left to commemorate senseless waste.","opus":"An unusually mournful war song for Sabaton, honoring the youths slaughtered across the 1915 campaign, one-time enemies now buried together. Grief for lives squandered rather than glory in the fight.","sonnet":"A lament for the futile slaughter of the Gallipoli campaign, honoring the young soldiers who died not as enemies but as wasted lives, and calling for remembrance of them as the ultimate lesson against senseless war.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~coat-of-arms":{"haiku":"Greek soldiers fight invaders during WWII, defending their homeland with a coat of arms reading 'Freedom or Death,' honoring ancestral sacrifice.","opus":"Sabaton's history lesson on Greece's 1940 defiance of the Italian invasion, invoking Leonidas and the Spartan Freedom-or-Death creed. The metal bombast honors a smaller nation that refused to surrender and drove the invaders back.","sonnet":"A tribute to the Greek resistance against the Axis invasion of 1940-41 — civilians and soldiers alike repelled the Italian advance and endured bombing, drawing on the Spartan motto \"freedom or death\" as an ancestral inheritance.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~firestorm":{"haiku":"Bombing raids annihilate cities in merciless destruction; civilians face apocalyptic death from the sky with no escape or mercy.","opus":"The Swedish metal band render an aerial bombing campaign from the perspective of those beneath it, sirens wailing as aircraft crest the skyline and reduce whole cities to powder. Dwellings collapse into rubble and civilians of every kind perish, yet an opening reflection on realms that fall never to return threads elegy through the carnage, closing on ruin and a vow to rebuild and retaliate.","sonnet":"A World War II strategic bombing campaign rendered from ground level — radar signals, air-raid sirens, carpet bombing reducing cities and civilians to rubble and ash. Anti-aircraft guns blaze futilely as the sky turns red. The opening epigraph about destroyed kingdoms frames the destruction as irreversible; nothing rises from the ruin here.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~ghost-division":{"haiku":"A panzer unit moves with blitzkrieg speed and precision through hostile territory, earning fearsome reputation through relentless assault and phantom presence.","opus":"A charging tribute to Rommel's 7th Panzer Division and its terrifying speed in the 1940 blitzkrieg. Sabaton documents the military legend while the imagery of fear and destruction leaves the moral cost audible underneath.","sonnet":"A historically detailed account of Erwin Rommel's 7th Panzer Division in the 1940 French campaign — the \"Ghost Division\" earned its name by advancing so far and so fast that its own command lost track of it, and the song celebrates that terrifying momentum while naming it explicitly as a Nazi operation.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~into-the-fire":{"haiku":"A young soldier sent into jungle ambush becomes a feral beast driven by survival and napalm; he transforms into something inhuman under fire.","opus":"A green soldier's Vietnam ambush told from inside the panic, calling in the airstrike that burns everyone including his own. Sabaton drops its usual triumphalism for the jungle war's madness, where survival and self-destruction become the same act.","sonnet":"A Vietnam War story told from inside a firefight that goes catastrophically wrong — a squad calls in an airstrike on their own position after being overrun, and napalm burns friend and foe alike, with the narrator's mind breaking under the heat and chaos.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~metal-crue":{"haiku":"Genre pilgrimage and cosmic warfare; musical warriors travel through galaxies destroying everything.","opus":"A tribute assembled entirely from heavy-metal band names woven into a mock-epic narrative, where Priest, Maiden, Slayer and dozens of others become characters in a metaphorical slaughter, turning the genre's own roster into its subject.","sonnet":"A love letter to heavy metal history built entirely from band names embedded in narrative — Rainbow leads home, the Priest kills the Maiden in the Metal Church, Armored Saints and Warlocks watch — turning the genre's own canon into a single, continuous mythological saga where the bands are the characters.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~metal-ripper":{"haiku":"Darkness manifests through historical evocation and forbidden knowledge, calling forth damned forces that break chains and terrorize the unprepared.","opus":"A knowing mashup of classic-metal touchstones, built as a tribute-medley of the genre's occult and highway-to-hell imagery rather than one story. It's Sabaton saluting the music that raised them.","sonnet":"A collage of heavy metal lyrics, imagery, and iconography — Crowley, fog-drenched London, AC/DC and Black Sabbath quotations, breaking chains — assembled as a tribute to the genre itself rather than a unified narrative. The song celebrates the cumulative power and mythology of metal as a force that will not be contained.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~midway":{"haiku":"The Battle of Midway unfolds as naval forces engage in tactical warfare, with bombers descending on enemy ships under fire in a decisive Pacific engagement.","opus":"Sabaton's history-lesson metal recounting the 1942 Pacific carrier battle, told from the cockpit as fleets gamble everything on tactics and timing. It celebrates the decisive turning point while keeping the widescreen, almost documentary distance the band favors.","sonnet":"A retelling of the Battle of Midway from the perspective of the naval aviators — scouts report enemy position, fighters scramble under the Pacific sun, and the decisive bomb run tips the course of the war.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~panzerkampf":{"haiku":"Soviet forces meet Nazi invasion at Prokhorovka in a brutal turning point, where Russian determination and sacrifice repel the Axis advance across burning fields.","opus":"The Battle of Kursk from the Soviet side, the Red Army halting the German advance at Prokhorovka and turning the tide toward Berlin. Metal spectacle built on the Eastern Front's decisive armored clash.","sonnet":"A battle chronicle of the 1943 Battle of Kursk, tracing the German armored advance into Soviet territory and its decisive defeat as Soviet forces absorb the assault and turn the tide, marking the beginning of the end for the Third Reich.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~primo-victoria":{"haiku":"Allied soldiers storm the beaches on D-Day in 1944, facing certain death as they charge through gunfire toward Nazi forces in a pivotal battle.","opus":"Sabaton march the listener onto the Normandy beaches at dawn on D-Day, first waves falling in the sand under crossfire as history is written in a single burning morning. True to the Swedish band's mission of turning military history into anthems, they frame the assault as passage through hell toward a hard-won heaven.","sonnet":"Soldiers storm the Normandy beaches on D-Day, June 6, 1944, knowing the first waves will die on the sand. The track holds the terrible arithmetic of Allied liberation: heaven is the goal, hell is the method, and history is written by those who survive.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~resist-and-bite":{"haiku":"Outnumbered soldiers hold a doomed border, choosing dignity and defiance over surrender to overwhelming military force.","opus":"The Belgian Chasseurs Ardennais of 1940, a handful of soldiers ordered to hold the line against overwhelming Blitzkrieg, biting hard against hopeless odds. Doomed defiance in service of duty, honored to the last.","sonnet":"A company of forty Belgian soldiers holds their position against Rommel's Blitzkrieg for eighteen days, outnumbered and doomed, choosing to fight with honour rather than surrender. Even when captured, they meet their captors with a smile, having done exactly what they were ordered to do.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~saboteurs":{"haiku":"Norwegian commandos become heroes through courage and sacrifice in frozen mountains, writing history through their resistance against fascism. Warrior blood endures beyond death in memory.","opus":"A tribute to the Norwegian commandos of Telemark who trained in the frozen wild and raced to sabotage the Nazi heavy-water plant, cast as viking-blooded heroes who helped decide the war.","sonnet":"A tribute to the Norwegian saboteurs of the Telemark heavy water plant: trained in Scotland, dropped into frozen wilderness, racing against the Nazi bomb program through night and cold, these heroes of the northern land are remembered as warriors who changed the war.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~screaming-eagles":{"haiku":"American paratroopers hold the line at Bastogne during World War II despite overwhelming German forces and brutal winter conditions.","opus":"A history-metal retelling of the 101st Airborne holding Bastogne through the freezing siege of the Bulge, down to the general's famous one-word refusal to surrender. The awe is for men who stood alone in the cold against overwhelming force.","sonnet":"A chronological account of the 101st Airborne Division in World War II — from Normandy through Market Garden's failure at Arnhem to the siege of Bastogne, where surrounded soldiers refused to surrender despite freezing conditions and constant bombardment.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~talvisota":{"haiku":"Finland's Winter War against Soviet invasion turns landscape and climate into weapons, with small divisions and sniper tactics defeating overwhelming numerical superiority through cunning and resolve.","opus":"Finland's outnumbered stand in the Winter War, using snow, snipers and Molotov cocktails to bleed Stalin's invading army. National pride as survival, the frozen underdog turning ferocity against a giant.","sonnet":"A tribute to Finland's Winter War resistance against the Soviet invasion, celebrating guerrilla tactics, Motti encirclement strategy, and the fierce national will that held off a vastly larger enemy at enormous cost.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~the-future-of-warfare":{"haiku":"The invention of mechanized warfare transforms human conflict into industrial slaughter; courage and sacrifice become obsolete as technology erases individual valor.","opus":"The tank's battlefield debut at the Somme in 1916 and its coming of age by 1918, a grim marvel at the machine that rewrote how wars are fought. Awe and horror at the birth of mechanized slaughter.","sonnet":"A chronicle of the tank's debut at the Battle of Flers-Courcelette in 1916 and its decisive role at Villers-Bretonneux in 1918, marking the moment mechanised warfare permanently changed how wars are fought.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~unbreakable":{"haiku":"A military ambush destroys an unsuspecting army; soldiers transform into berserker warriors as they fight against impossible odds at dusk.","opus":"The Swedish war-metal band opens with Sun Tzu's counsel about luring an enemy into ambush, then dramatizes the trap from the attacker's side: a force lying concealed beneath blood-red skies to draw its marching foes into annihilation. Striking at nightfall and battling until daybreak, they whip themselves into a frenzy and cast themselves as an indestructible instrument of war, reveling in the cold machinery of planned slaughter.","sonnet":"Soldiers spring a perfect ambush — their enemy marches unknowing into a prepared killing ground at dusk. The trap is executed with berserker fury, and no one survives to carry word back. The song is a tactical narrative told from the victors' perspective, pure and merciless.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~uprising":{"haiku":"The Warsaw Uprising in 1944: resistance fighters—women, men, children—die willingly in the sewers, defiant and faithful despite abandonment by the Allies. Their sacrifice stands as history's call.","opus":"A tribute to the 1944 Warsaw Uprising, honoring the civilians who fought and died in the sewers and streets while the Allies never came. Sabaton turn abandoned defiance into anthem, faith held even as the help failed to arrive.","sonnet":"The 1944 Warsaw Uprising — Polish civilians and resistance fighters rising against Nazi occupation with no Allied support, dying street by street — commemorated as an act of faith and sacrifice that was betrayed by the world but never abandoned by those who fought it.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~we-burn":{"haiku":"Genocide and religious violence masquerade as cleansing, with perpetrators justified by historical precedent and sheltered by impunity from consequence.","opus":"A rare unsettling turn into the mind of a genocidal cleanser, first-person and unrepentant, boasting that nothing's a crime so long as you're never caught. The banality and self-justification of ethnic slaughter laid bare.","sonnet":"A war crimes narrative told from inside the mind of a perpetrator who justifies ethnic cleansing through a self-granted divine mandate — the song presents the ideology without endorsing it, charting how ordinary soldiers are ordered into atrocity.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~wehrmacht":{"haiku":"Soldiers pulled into serving authoritarian ideology become unwilling participants in mechanized killing, raising questions of complicity versus victimhood.","opus":"Sabaton, the Swedish history-metal band, pose an uneasy moral question instead of glorifying war. Looking at the soldiers who powered the German war machine, the song asks whether they were willing agents of horror or ordinary young men swept up and lost. It keeps circling the ones who simply followed orders, showing how grand ideology can drown individual conscience.","sonnet":"A question rather than a verdict — were the soldiers of the German war machine fanatics or ordinary young men swept into catastrophic machinery? The lyrics hold the tension without collapsing it, foregrounding the armoured spine of the operation while asking what moral reality existed for the men inside it.","src":"haiku"},
"sabaton~white-death":{"haiku":"A Finnish sniper stalks Soviet invaders across a crimson-painted landscape, hiding in snow and taking hundreds of kills. He embodies the sisu of Finns—steady-handed, calm-eyed, and merciless.","opus":"A tribute to a lone Finnish sniper hunting an advancing army across frozen lakes, dawn revealing red snow where hundreds fell to his rifle, his silence and patience embodying national sisu. Sabaton, the Swedish power-metal band built entirely around military history, salutes Simo Häyhä of the Winter War with the anthemic choruses and marching riffs that define their catalogue of soldiers and sieges.","sonnet":"A tribute to a legendary Finnish sniper — a lone hunter moves through a frozen winter landscape, tracking an advancing eastern army with lethal patience. The song glorifies his precision and invisibility, framing each shot as inevitable once he has a target in sight, and credits his endurance to a distinctly Finnish quality of unyielding will.","src":"haiku"},
"sable-hills~namu":{"haiku":"Sacred invocation calls upon divine rage to shatter delusion and paralytic doubt.","opus":"A Japanese metalcore invocation of Fudo Myo-o, the wrathful Buddhist deity, chanting his mantra to burn away delusion, weakness and anxiety. It seeks salvation through fury: begging the god's terrifying rage to shatter the singer's own hesitation.","sonnet":"A metalcore invocation of Fudo Myoo — the immovable wisdom king of fierce anger in Buddhist iconography — interwoven with the Sanskrit mantra Namu Samanta Vajranam, begging the deity to burn delusions, shatter weakness, and release the speaker from agony and hesitation through righteous fury.","src":"haiku"},
"sacred-reich~death-squad":{"haiku":"Authoritarian regimes eliminate dissent through terror, cloaking atrocity as governance while victims mount invisibly beneath official narratives.","opus":"Reagan-era thrash indicting US-backed right-wing death squads in Latin America, installing puppet regimes and calling the resulting slaughter democracy. Rage at freedom imposed at gunpoint over piles of the dead.","sonnet":"A pointed critique of right-wing authoritarian regimes that use paramilitary death squads to eliminate political opposition under the banner of democracy and anti-communism, exposing how fear and violence replace the consent they claim to protect. The civilian dead are framed as the true cost of this puppeted \"freedom.\"","src":"haiku"},
"sadus~illusions":{"haiku":"Perception fractures under psychological pressure; reality dissolves into delusion that blinds and imprisons.","opus":"Sanity dissolving under hallucination, the mind's own creations distorting reality until the narrator can't tell what's real. Thrash-metal claustrophobia mapping the terror of losing your grip from the inside.","sonnet":"A mind overwhelmed and imprisoned by its own distortions — hallucinations, delusions, optical chaos — loses the ability to distinguish perception from fabrication until reality itself dissolves and the speaker is left unable to trust any image their senses return. The horror is internal and total.","src":"haiku"},
"sails-of-serenity~phoenix":{"haiku":"Rebirth from destruction and ashes, rising renewed from devastating loss.","opus":"A melodic-metalcore arc from crushing grief toward rebirth. Numb and staring downward under the weight of loss, the narrator faces that a loved one is gone for good. The phoenix becomes his charge to spread his wings and climb again, pushing through tempests and tides toward fresh horizons and a hard-won new dawn.","sonnet":"Grief and rebirth after loss — limbs numb, weight of time crushing, a dream of her lips that shattered — but the imperative is to unfurl wings and shoot for the stars anyway. \"Reborn like a phoenix\" is not triumphant so much as quietly necessary: you redawn because the alternative is staying crushed.","src":"haiku"},
"saint-agnes~animal":{"haiku":"Primal defiance against social conformity declares refusal to be tamed or baptized into respectability, embracing base nature as freedom.","opus":"A snarling refusal to be tamed or made to confess, embracing the label of animal against those who insist you behave and obey. The Lucifer imagery weaponizes being cast as damned into pure defiant self-possession.","sonnet":"The speaker defiantly owns the identity others have forced on them — cursed, thirsty, unconfessable — rejecting the demands of religious and social conformity with pure confrontational aggression: \"I won't behave and I won't obey.\"","src":"haiku"},
"saint-agnes~the-father-the-son-and-the-holy-beast":{"haiku":"Religious devotion corrupts into predatory desire; the victim reclaims autonomy through defiance.","opus":"A former dutiful churchgoer, once used as a vessel for religion, turns the tables, shedding her belief and finding power, savoring blood on her tongue and crowning herself. It reclaims a body once ruled by dogma, refusing the cruelty inflicted in God's name.","sonnet":"A woman breaking free from religious subjugation that was also sexual and psychological exploitation — she was a good girl, a vessel, used and kept chaste; now she has turned, tastes blood, and her god-fearing days are replaced by something predatory and self-possessed.","src":"haiku"},
"saint-agnes~vampire":{"haiku":"Beauty and immortality come through predation and deception, where consuming others' hearts and desires drives the speaker's eternal life.","opus":"A seductive confession of predatory hunger dressed in cinematic imagery, the narrator all tender skin and eager fangs, craving to consume a lover and never die while flames devour the film reel behind the glamour. Saint Agnes, the London duo trading in noir-tinged gothic garage rock, lean into their femme-fatale swagger here, using celluloid and la-dolce-vita references to frame desire as glamorous ruin.","sonnet":"Predatory desire dressed as camp glamour — the speaker embraces her consuming, immortality-hungry nature rather than hiding it, casting jealousy and deception as honest traits compared to the polished performance everyone else puts on. The sweet-on-the-surface exterior masks hunger underneath, and the song revels in that contradiction rather than apologizing for it.","src":"haiku"},
"saint-punk~empty-bed":{"haiku":"Absence structures daily existence as memory and phantom sensation replace presence; the narrator haunts himself with impossible reunion fantasies.","opus":"Grief that won't accept the loss, drinking to conjure a presence back and half-believing they'll return by morning. The unbearable persistence of someone's smell and memory in a space they've vacated for good.","sonnet":"A person unable to move past a breakup drinks to conjure the absent lover back into presence, preserving their smell and taste in memory, still half-expecting to wake beside them — the bed's emptiness is the whole song's subject.","src":"haiku"},
"saint-punk~gloves-with-matt-mcandrew":{"opus":"A confrontational protest anthem set amid economic hardship and unrest (layoffs, unpaid stimulus), declaring the gloves are coming off; it indicts those with blood on their hands who never saw the reckoning coming.","sonnet":"A song about removing the last pretenses of restraint in a world already at war with itself — amid layoffs, sick children, and political failure, the narrator declares that blood is on everyone's hands and there are no innocent bystanders left. The gloves coming off signals not victory but the abandonment of civility in conditions that made it pointless.","src":"sonnet"},
"saint-raven~joker":{"haiku":"Powerlessness and complicity bind victims to manipulative systems while those in control pull strings from invisible towers. The song condemns silent submission and the blame internalized by those who refuse to rebel.","opus":"An indictment of chasing corporate greed and empty power only to end up a pawn in someone else's game. The one holding the cards clips your wings and devours what was alive in you, and the cruelest turn is that you played along, so the blame is yours too.","sonnet":"Corporate complicity is reframed as self-inflicted ruin — a person who surrendered to false promises of security and consumed the greed of those in towers finds themselves rotting in the jaws of a joker, blamed for choosing to play a game that was rigged from the start.","src":"haiku"},
"saint-slumber~loverboy":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects being someone's romantic fantasy or possession, remaining emotionally detached and unavailable despite their pursuer's desire. They're not really present and cannot be caught or hurt.","opus":"A detached refusal of someone's romantic pursuit, apocalyptic boredom masking emotional unavailability. Wanted but ungraspable, the narrator looks at desire and feels only a void and mild annoyance, self-protection dressed as cool.","sonnet":"About the exhausting weight of being desired by someone you feel no genuine connection with — the narrator is annoyed and drained by someone who wants them badly, sees only void when looking back at them, and refuses to be caught or defined by the role of loverboy despite being unable to fully escape the dynamic.","src":"haiku"},
"saint-slumber~peachy":{"haiku":"Existential malaise festers beneath surface apathy; the speaker confesses desire to watch everything burn while denying real feeling, masking despair with the toxic refrain that nothing matters.","opus":"A detached fascination with watching things collapse, masking dread and end-times talk behind the hollow reassurance that all is well. Alt-pop dressing existential unease in bright, glib denial.","sonnet":"A self-aware nihilist admits to loving chaos over connection, watching things burn rather than nurturing them, while using the ironically cheerful \"everything is peachy keen\" to paper over apocalyptic conversations with friends about the end and a desperate wish for immortality.","src":"haiku"},
"sakanamon~a-1uci2ku":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"Sakanamon's playful, wordy indie rock loves an extended conceit, and this one frames music itself as biological ecology. The narrator is a plankton fallen out of the food-chain pyramid, drifting in the mind's depths, waiting to fuse with others and explode upward, sending tangled sound to root in the listener's membrane so that something beyond logic can finally sprout.","sonnet":"Music framed as biological ecology — the narrator is a plankton who dropped out of the food-chain pyramid and now drifts in the mind's depths, waiting for the moment to fuse with others and explode upward, sending tangled sound to take root in the listener's membrane so that something beyond logic can sprout.","src":"haiku"},
"sakoya~wandering":{"haiku":"Aimless movement through life without direction or purpose, spiritually lost and searching.","opus":"A weary farewell to a draining, one-sided relationship. The narrator watches someone he cares for rise and self-destruct, unable to keep holding on while the other only takes and shifts the blame. Packing his bags for nowhere, he accepts that the hurt has become part of him and that letting go is the only way out.","sonnet":"A one-sided relationship where the narrator gives endlessly and has the blame placed back on them — packing bags for nowhere is the only escape, but even that has become part of who they are now. \"Better give me up\" is addressed to the other person as much as to themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"sal3m~amaranthine":{"opus":"A self-destructive breakup spiral of drinking, self-harm, and overloving, unable to purge an ex from his system and half-wishing to overdose or crash rather than keep hurting.","sonnet":"A person drowning in a toxic love they cannot escape turns to drinking and reckless self-destruction, fantasizing about crashing into a sunset just to get the other person out of their system — they love too much, overlove compulsively, and know it will kill them.","src":"sonnet"},
"sal3m~blood-stains":{"haiku":"A speaker struggles with addiction, self-harm, and emotional pain that won't numb. Blood stains spread as delusions drive toward oblivion rather than healing.","opus":"Sal3m draw a raw parallel between cutting and craving, casting self-harm and destructive love as the same helpless compulsion; the narrator tallies his wounds in isolation and keeps demanding more punishment. Letting himself bleed never dulls the hurt enough, and the song's real question is whether he and his partner can still hope for better or must brace for none.","sonnet":"A duet of parallel self-destruction: two people coping with pain through different means — one through numbness and avoidance, the other through cutting — bound together by the question of whether anything better is even possible.","src":"haiku"},
"sal3m~love-is-a-wonderful-lie":{"haiku":"The narrator reflects on a love that became their reason to live, though it keeps them emotionally entangled and unable to rest.","opus":"A devotion shadowed by the title's warning that love may be a gorgeous deception, giving the narrator a purpose to keep going even as he suspects it's false. He loves the endless wandering yet aches whenever he has to leave across the water, the beloved controlling him from afar, real street names anchoring the restless travel.","sonnet":"A long-distance love described as the only thing that made riding alone bearable — the road and rain and thousands of miles are fine, but crossing the sea knowing he shouldn't leave is where it breaks. She made him forget isolation, gave him a reason to exist, but she pulls on strings he can't control and he's already in her designs, dreaming of flight without thinking about the landing.","src":"haiku"},
"saliva~click-click-boom":{"haiku":"A young kid spent Saturdays making music alone with passion and ambition, imagining success on the radio; now as an artist they question whether their drive will sustain or destroy them.","opus":"A cocky origin story of a kid holed up in his room with the stereo blazing, ambition and imagination fueling a rise to the radio, shot through with jabs at whiny peers and questions about whether he'll make it or crash. Saliva, the Memphis nu-metal outfit, scored their signature 2001 hit here, its rap-rock bravado and explosive chorus made for arena and soundtrack ubiquity.","sonnet":"An outsider's arrival story — the kid who skipped weekend fun to stay in his room with a notepad now dominates the radio, arriving with a new style that obliterates the scene. Between the confidence, there are flickers of doubt about his own mind and mortality, but the swagger is the dominant note: he came from nowhere and he won.","src":"haiku"},
"saliva~superstar":{"haiku":"An aspiring star pursues wealth and fame through excess, self-destruction, and moral compromise, dying at 27 from the lifestyle. The song traces the arc of celebrity self-annihilation.","opus":"The post-grunge band trace fame's poisoned arc from the inside, a narrator grabbing the spotlight purely out of greed, chasing movies, glossy press, and stretched limos while pills and booze slowly undo him. The chorus's plea to be turned into a headliner sours as the ego others feed leaves him frozen, the reverie he refuses to leave drifting toward the fabled death at twenty-seven, ambition and self-destruction proving inseparable.","sonnet":"Fame as a terminal condition — a narrator who wanted celebrity at any cost arrives paralysed by it, propped up by pills and empty bottles, fantasising about dying at 27. The industry fed the ego that is now devouring him, and the dream he cannot wake from is indistinguishable from the nightmare.","src":"haiku"},
"sam-smith~kids-again":{"haiku":"Nostalgic regret mourns irretrievable youth; transformation into adulthood forecloses return to innocent wonder and uncomplicated connection.","opus":"Mourning a love that ended not in betrayal but in growing up, driving past the old house, unable to stop missing what can never return. The specific ache that you'll never be that young or that unguarded together again.","sonnet":"A former relationship leaves a permanent mark — the speaker still avoids places connected to that person, still hurts when they hear their song, and mourns the specific intimacy of youth that cannot be recovered, no matter what comes after.","src":"haiku"},
"sam-smith~unholy-feat-kim-petras-nova-twins-remix":{"haiku":"Infidelity and materialism conspire as transactional pleasure replaces genuine intimacy; secrets breed between the performance of devotion and hidden hunger.","opus":"A gleeful exposure of infidelity and transactional lust, a cheating husband caught at the body shop while the wife turns out to be keeping unholy secrets of her own.","sonnet":"About a married man secretly going to a strip club or sex worker while his wife is oblivious — told from the perspective of a woman who knows exactly what he's doing and offers herself for money and luxury in exchange for discretion.","src":"haiku"},
"sam-sparro~21st-century-life":{"haiku":"Overwhelming technological saturation breeds disconnection and surveillance; material abundance coexists with spiritual poverty and constraint.","opus":"A wry survey of modern overload, disillusioned that the imagined future arrived as surveillance, distraction, indifference to famine, and no real equality. Dancing to numb the confounding weight of contemporary life.","sonnet":"The overwhelming surplus of the modern world — connectivity, surveillance, information overload, political outrage — leaves the speaker feeling swept away and dirty, noting that despite all the supposed progress, equality still doesn't exist and freedom is performed under the lens of CCTV.","src":"haiku"},
"samaris~wanted-2-say":{"haiku":"Life is pressure and survival; the speaker struggles to articulate what they wanted to say, finding nothing or only silence remains.","opus":"The Icelandic electronic trio wrap a fractured meditation in atmospheric hush, images of dense overgrowth and staying afloat framing survival as a burden we share. The lyric keeps groping for something the narrator meant to voice only to trail off into blankness, the failure to articulate becoming the subject itself, meaning suspended between shore, wave, and words that never quite land.","sonnet":"Something urgent and real goes unsaid — the narrator gestures repeatedly toward a message that dissolves into nothing at all. Life is framed as pressure and survival, the shore and the wave the only constants. What was meant to be communicated remains permanently suspended, and the gap between intention and expression is the whole subject.","src":"haiku"},
"samsara~deathwish":{"haiku":"Spiraling toward death through numbness and refusal to heal, where toxins and wounds won't change because the narrator has surrendered to the deathwish.","opus":"The metalcore track renders toxic dependency as literal poisoning, fangs buried deep and venom spreading until the narrator doubts he is even alive. Detoxing, shaking and quaking, he insists no one can comfort, command, or cure him, that numbness is all that is left. The recurring image of dragging himself toward a final resting place frames the pull to self-destruction as the ending he had pleaded for.","sonnet":"A person in the grip of toxic dependency goes numb deliberately, crawling toward a death wish rather than accepting help or healing. The body shakes, the moral compass spins, and the will to recover has dissolved. The only thing left is the refusal to be consoled or controlled.","src":"haiku"},
"samurai-pizza-cats~freakshow":{"haiku":"Self-loathing and mental fragility are put on display as a freak show — the speaker's shaking hands, aching body, and haunted past are the exhibit, and the audience is invited to watch while the performer's grip on reality slips.","opus":"A raw metalcore spiral through anxiety, self-disgust and unraveling control. Cold hands and dead eyes pull the narrator toward silence to hush the voices in his skull, while shaking fingers and aching muscles betray a fixation on someone. Confessing his mind is slipping, he invites the audience into the wreckage as his own sideshow.","sonnet":"A collapse of will and self-worth tied to obsession over someone — shaking fingers when thinking of them, muscles aching, past hunting the present. The narrator presents their own dysfunction as spectacle: 'please enjoy my freak show,' having tied both hands and surrendered any way out.","src":"haiku"},
"samurai-pizza-cats~have-a-g-o-o-d-day":{"haiku":"Schadenfreude and cosmic indifference blur together as the speaker finds redemption in watching suffering and cosmic decay wash away the world's pretense.","opus":"Gleeful, petty revenge fantasy aimed at a smug consumer type, wishing cartoonish humiliations on them while treating their downfall as the ingredient that makes any day good.","sonnet":"A gleeful revenge fantasy directed at a despised person — the narrator inventories petty disasters they wish on the target (hot coffee burns, wet sleeves, sharknado) and takes pure joy in the idea that karma will eventually floor them, all while insisting it's a good day regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"samurai~archangel":{"haiku":"Destructive impulse and anarchic rebellion overturn order; chaos becomes cleansing force as the speaker incites insurrection against institutional control.","opus":"The fictional Cyberpunk 2077 band as pure anti-corporate insurrection, an agent of chaos gleefully tearing down the system and the conformist herd. Rebellion staged as ecstatic ruin, valor pitched somewhere between violence and a kiss.","sonnet":"A self-declared agent of chaos embraces the drive to destroy and rebuild — tearing through conformity, targeting corporate power, starting riots — as a philosophical calling, finding valor in perpetual disruption.","src":"haiku"},
"samurai~chippin-in":{"haiku":"The song channels primal rebellion and systemic defiance. An animal force rises to add justice to the people's math, killing those who oppress—aggressive empowerment for the powerless.","opus":"A snarling anthem of defiance and self-erasure into code, the narrator an untamable creature in his own veins who won't stand down, evening the score for the oppressed and tearing an insurgent trail against the suits. Samurai is the fictional rockerboy band from the Cyberpunk 2077 universe, fronted in-game by Johnny Silverhand, and this track was written for it by Refused, giving the dystopia its rebellious punk pulse.","sonnet":"Reckless, full-throttle commitment to a fight — something primal has taken over and there is no reversing it. The speaker plants code, rolls the dice, and charges down a rebel path against suits and systems, framing the whole thing as a kind of animal inevitability. Total war is the declared mode, backing down is not a concept that exists here.","src":"haiku"},
"samurai~never-fade-away":{"opus":"Love and loss preserved as legend, two doomed lovers who burned bright and lost everything. Written as the anthem of the rebel band inside Cyberpunk 2077, it welds romance to revolt, a defiant vow that something truly beautiful outlasts death, fusing fury, dread and grief into one enduring image.","sonnet":"A song about a love that destroyed both people but left something permanent — everything was lost, the cost was enormous, but the beauty of what they shared refuses to disappear, haunting the narrator like a ghost even as he carries rage and fear forward.","src":"sonnet"},
"san-cisco~awkward":{"haiku":"Two people experience communication breakdown and missed connections through unanswered calls and messages, with confusion and surveillance creating tension.","opus":"The Australian indie-pop band wraps social unease in bright, bouncy nonchalance, tracing crossed wires between two people trading unanswered messages and unmet expectations. The narrator's account curdles from missed connections into something sourer, spotting the other holding someone's hand and trading accusations of being watched and stalked for days, the sing-along da-da-da hook papering over a small, deadpan portrait of romantic paranoia going nowhere.","sonnet":"Two people caught in a romantic misfire narrate the same situation from opposite angles — missed calls, unanswered messages, a dinner that confirmed incompatibility. By the end one side has moved on entirely, and what began as longing tips into something closer to unwanted pursuit. The geometry of attachment has gone badly wrong.","src":"haiku"},
"sandi-sheldon~you-re-gonna-make-me-love-you":{"haiku":"Resistance to love crumbles despite fierce determination—the beloved's presence erodes all defenses, drawing out surrender through irresistible spiritual inevitability.","opus":"A guarded, frightened heart giving in to a love it can't get away from, conceding the pursuer will draw out her wanting and her devotion. Northern soul at its most helplessly romantic — the resistance already collapsed before it started.","sonnet":"A woman who built walls against love finds those defenses collapsing against someone who watches her even in sleep; she resists intellectually but her body and feelings capitulate, and the song traces that reluctant surrender step by step.","src":"haiku"},
"santigold~disparate-youth":{"opus":"A defiant anthem for young people rejecting the limited legacy handed to them, choosing to run toward their dreams together rather than stay trapped, insisting on a life worth fighting for despite the doubters.","sonnet":"A generational anthem about young people refusing the resigned limitations handed down by previous generations — the narrator rallies a community to fight for a life worth living despite the obstacles and doubters, framing it as collective forward movement against inherited stagnation.","src":"sonnet"},
"santigold~l-e-s-artistes":{"haiku":"Self-preservation through disappearance and artistic innovation requires rejection of false connection and name-dropping parasites seeking credit.","opus":"An artist erecting walls against phonies and name-droppers in a hollow scene, leaving home to reinvent herself and staking everything on standing up for what she believes.","sonnet":"A woman walks through a city of name-droppers and forced fakers trying to disappear into her own work, rejecting the social performance of the art scene while questioning whether what she gives up will be worth it if she can only stand up mean for what she believes.","src":"haiku"},
"sara~anhedonia":{"haiku":"Loss of pleasure spreads through numbness and complicity, as the speaker confronts the death of feeling and the surrender of autonomy that wealth extracts.","opus":"A Finnish portrait of numbness under endless stimulation, where a flint heart no longer registers pleasure and appetite never satisfies. Freedom was sold off willingly, and the closing question asks whether anything can still be felt at all.","sonnet":"A person trapped in emotional numbness addresses someone with a 'flint heart' — asking if they still feel anything, dreaming of war or the trees outside — as anhedonia hollows out sensation and the self surrenders its freedom, bones softening inside an echo chamber of its own making.","src":"haiku"},
"sarea~wolves":{"opus":"A predatory declaration of superiority in which the singer, a wolf disguised in a sheep's fleece, casts himself as executioner and counterfeit savior. Unyielding and pitiless, he treats existence as a cell he alone can lock, and aligns himself with the small guilty crowd who cherish anguish and thrive on hatred.","sonnet":"The narrator casts themselves as a wolf among sheep — uncompromising, holding the door of life's prison, watching eyes fade and heartbeats slow — while acknowledging a deep sadness and longing that feeds on anger and a love of despair.","src":"sonnet"},
"sassy-009~blue-racecar":{"haiku":"After a difficult relationship ends, the speaker starts fresh and moves on, eventually riding alone in a blue racecar. They flip their hair and leave behind the person who couldn't see them.","opus":"A post-breakup glow-up: starting fresh with a clean slate, done hiding, flipping her hair and riding a blue racecar past the ex who never treated her right, half-wondering if he even cares while insisting she's fine. The confidence is armor over the admission that she lied about being okay. Sassy 009, the Norwegian project centered on Sunniva Lindgard, sets this cool, dance-leaning kiss-off to airy, synth-forward electropop.","sonnet":"Post-breakup reinvention told with full confidence — things were never easy, she cried easily, let him through and lied about being fine. Now she's starting over, new vibe, nothing to hide, and the image of freedom is a blue racecar that everyone can see her in. She wonders briefly if he notices; then flips her hair and moves on anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"satanic-planet~baphomet":{"opus":"A Satanic invocation of Baphomet as the reconciliation of all opposites, using paradox after paradox to celebrate a figure that unites good and evil, order and chaos, into a single duality.","sonnet":"An invocation of Baphomet as the embodiment of unified opposites — cruel and kind, true and false, wolf and sheep, chaos and order — celebrating the satanic principle that all contradictions coexist and that the most honest view of existence holds nothing sacred or profane above the other.","src":"sonnet"},
"satyr~picayune":{"haiku":"A critique of institutional corruption and cowardice, where ambitious individuals blindly follow flawed systems and leaders while avoiding accountability.","opus":"Dense, clinical language wrapped around institutional cowardice. Wrongdoing gets rebranded as proper procedure, leaders shelter behind rules they set, and personal ambition drags along everyone who follows. The worn-down narrator longs for accountability while still bearing the fallout of someone else's choice, left only with more isolation.","sonnet":"Power structures elect their leaders quietly and then let followers absorb the consequences — cowardice dressed as ambition, trust weaponized as permission for others to take the fall. The speaker is exhausted by complicity, calculating endlessly how much time alone it will cost to survive the system.","src":"haiku"},
"savage-hands~blue":{"haiku":"A person suffocates under pressure and abuse, turning blue from choking, burning with rage until they break free from their oppressor's grip.","opus":"A raw depiction of self-destruction and toxic escape: throttling himself until he bruises, howling his existence away, and sinking into drink to feel secure, the narrator dares his own fate and pins the blame on a partner for lighting the spark that pushes him toward madness.","sonnet":"A person pushed past breaking point — strangled, bruised, screaming, buried in a bottle — by someone who destroyed everything and ignited a fuse that will burn until there is nothing left to lose.","src":"haiku"},
"savage-hands~demon":{"opus":"The aftermath of a controlling, drug-like relationship, where the betrayed singer vows vengeance, refuses to forgive, and watches the ex's life burn while he learns to live with his own demons.","sonnet":"After discovering a controlling, manipulative relationship was built on projection and lies, the narrator rejects the role of victim, reclaims their anger, and channels it into a promised vengeance — making peace with their darkest impulses.","src":"sonnet"},
"savage-hands~halo":{"haiku":"Scarred halos render the spiritual inaudible; the speaker drowns in shadow, doubts and smoke accumulate while light dims and redemption remains inaccessible to the deeply flawed.","opus":"Depression framed as marks on a halo that make people tune out, the singer caught in a shadow that feels terminal and needing a miracle. Recovery is recast as tumbling forward, where even a collapse counts as progress.","sonnet":"A speaker who admits they are not spiritual confesses that scars on their halo make them inaudible to anyone who might help — the smoke of accumulated doubt fills every ounce, but falling forward still means one step further.","src":"haiku"},
"savage-hands~love-no-more":{"haiku":"The speaker has given up on love after prolonged emotional exhaustion, feeling buried and isolated, unable to trust or maintain connection because all they've ever known is loss.","opus":"A study in emotional exhaustion, the narrator lost in the dark, tired of lying and holding on, wishing to disappear into anonymity while silence weighs him down and loss becomes the only thing he ever wins. Savage Hands work in the melodic post-hardcore vein, pairing clean, aching hooks with a downcast lyric about running from a ghost and mistaking a hole in the ground for home.","sonnet":"Emotional exhaustion taken past its limit — the speaker wants to erase themselves from a relationship not out of anger but out of sheer depletion, unable to keep lying by staying. Being blind to the ghost they are running from, they find emptiness more comfortable than connection, and conclude that loss is the only outcome love ever produces for them.","src":"haiku"},
"savage-hands~memory":{"opus":"A plea to someone consumed by addiction, the narrator watching them ruin themselves and pin the blame on him while he stays powerless to help. Worn down by broken promises and relapse, he prays they'll survive rather than fade away, though they already feel like a stranger.","sonnet":"Someone watches helplessly as a person they care about destroys themselves with drug addiction — lying on the bathroom floor, injecting, chasing the habit — and confesses they cannot save them, fearing the addict will become just a memory.","src":"sonnet"},
"savage-hands~rock-bottom":{"haiku":"Willful descent and self-destruction become inevitability; the speaker accelerates toward the bottom, defiant against conformity even as everything collapses inward.","opus":"An addict's romance with self-destruction, framing the plunge from wreckage to the very bottom as almost thrilling. Going against the grain becomes an identity even as it stares down the barrel.","sonnet":"A self-destructive person who goes against every norm and can't stop the downward spiral, sinking from wreck to rock bottom and finding some dark perverse comfort in the freefall.","src":"haiku"},
"save-us~nightmare":{"haiku":"Blindness and fracture paralyze connection, where love transforms into nightmare through mutual incomprehension, and the speaker pleads for sight to pierce the wall between passion and rupture.","opus":"A relationship curdling into a shared bad dream, the couple sightless and still brawling while one begs the other to look. Under it runs a quiet fear of never measuring up to the fire in the other's gaze.","sonnet":"A relationship trapped between blindness and fighting is addressed as a shared nightmare from which the speaker demands the other person open their eyes — the question of whether any of it was real love remains unanswered beneath the urgency.","src":"haiku"},
"save-us~synthetic":{"haiku":"Deception and guilt fester internally; the subject presents false wellness while lies calcify.","opus":"Self-loathing framed as a fabricated identity, where guilt and deception grow from inside and can't be escaped. Refusing holiness and life itself, the singer wants to be torn apart and burned, admitting the whole self is a lie.","sonnet":"A speaker consumed by guilt, sin, and a manufactured inner life — \"synthetic forming from inside of me\" — declares they do not want to feel alive and that they are, at root, a lie; the self is something built and false that must be burned away.","src":"haiku"},
"saviour~enemies":{"haiku":"A person battles their own anxieties and negative memories that replay in their mind, describing their internal enemies as thoughts and emotions that slowly destroy them. They fantasize about a lost relationship and wish to escape into sleep.","opus":"Grief and anxiety that keep a person bedbound, replaying every memory and unspoken word until sleep becomes the only refuge. The enemies are internal, and the tragedy is preferring a dreamed version of what was lost to facing its absence.","sonnet":"A song about being slowly destroyed by grief, anxiety, and the haunting of a lost relationship — the narrator is paralyzed by obsessive memory, unable to sleep or speak the words they needed to say, resigned to spending their life dreaming of what could have been.","src":"haiku"},
"saviour~never-sleep":{"haiku":"A haunting presence (a whisper, a light, a memory of wanting to die) keeps the narrator forever awake. They cannot breathe or exist without this ghost who destroyed them.","opus":"The Australian band circles an insomnia born of heartbreak, a departed lover lodged as a whisper in the head who haunts every attempt at rest. Saviour's dual-vocal attack lets tenderness and fury coexist, the narrator swearing to stay awake until the light leaves his eyes while cursing the person he cannot stop feeling beside him.","sonnet":"A person destroyed by a relationship that offered light at their darkest moment cannot escape the ghost of it. Betrayal and dependency tangle together until sleep becomes impossible — the other person haunts every closed eye, every breath, every moment of solitude.","src":"haiku"},
"saviour~reshape-me":{"haiku":"The speaker asks to be broken and reshaped into something beautiful that can hold or contain another person. They want to transform through emptying themselves completely.","opus":"A desperate address to a partner, asking to be shattered and rebuilt into something that can carry them or that they can lose themselves in, drained of grief until there is nothing left. Saviour, an Australian melodic hardcore act built on trading male and female vocals, wrap this bruised, self-punishing craving for change in their heavy, surging sound.","sonnet":"A request to be broken down and rebuilt by someone else — impatience with the self as it currently exists, a desire to be made patient, confident, quieter, deep as an ocean. The speaker wants to be hollowed out like a vase so they can hold something other than their own damage. Stories are already written on the skin; the ask is to crack the shape and pour something better in.","src":"haiku"},
"saviour~the-quiet-calm":{"haiku":"A person claims inner peace while fearing the fragility of his relationship and capability for self-destruction. He offers to carry weight but can barely hold himself together.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with the moment your comforts turn on you, the narrator both fragile and dangerously self-destructive while trying to shoulder someone else's pain. Its heart is devotion offered by someone barely holding himself together.","sonnet":"Someone present for another person in crisis — watching them fall apart, offering to walk or drown or talk whatever they need, all while confessing their own fragility and wondering when their angels turned into demons.","src":"haiku"},
"saviour~tidal-wave":{"haiku":"Someone has lost their inner light and drowned in themselves, now resting on the ocean floor. Time moves on while they search for meaning, hope, or God in the wreckage.","opus":"Saviour, the Australian melodic hardcore act, mine grief that refuses resolution. Circling a deadly car crash and the flowers left at the roadside, the narrator goes numb and sinks inside himself while the world keeps turning without the dead. He keeps hunting for meaning, hope, even God, defiant survival and hollow emptiness sharing one breath.","sonnet":"Grief after a suicide — someone who used to shine like a lamp post crashed their car, left behind an open note the speaker keeps buried in their pocket, and now there are flowers on the footpath. The survivor has drowned inside themselves, feeling nothing, sitting on the ocean floor while time moves on. The lamp post still stands; the car doesn't. That's all that's left.","src":"haiku"},
"saviour~younger":{"haiku":"Regret over lost youth and fast-fading love, seeking frozen memories before aging erodes beauty and closeness forever.","opus":"The Australian band grieve for spent youth and a bond presumed eternal, its hours racing past too quickly to hold as recollection turns false. Longing to still his spirit before another chapter is stripped from him, the narrator begs to be brought along, even beyond death. Clasping a loved one until flesh crumbles away, it reframes loss as trust that they might reunite in a life to come.","sonnet":"Grief over lost youth and fading memory — the terror that the best moments slipped past before they could be held. A speaker pleads to freeze time, to recover the pages of a shared story already coming apart, and wonders where the love between two people finally goes.","src":"haiku"},
"sawano-hiroyuki~reviv":{"haiku":"Resurrection and redemption—breaking free from spiritual stagnation to reunite with an angelic figure that restores hope and kindness after prolonged loss and grief.","opus":"A soaring plea to bring someone back from an endless sleep, the world pining for a figure who must be summoned home. Anime-scale longing for reunion and revival, love as the key that lifts the veil of death.","sonnet":"A plea to revive a beloved figure — described as still and cold like a figurine, someone the world has been pining for — with the narrator begging for their return before hope dies entirely, framed as a fairy-tale awakening from an endless sleep.","src":"haiku"},
"sawanohiroyuki-nzk~s-ave-feat-aimer":{"haiku":"A figure navigates through pain with burning footsteps, unable to stop despite exhaustion. Memory and desire entangle; they light their own way through darkness toward whatever lies ahead.","opus":"An unspoken loneliness that repeats through the night, where a wound made as an excuse to stop time and a self-righteous shield spin uselessly. Salvation comes from unseen roots and a silenced wish that connect and let you advance one step, overlaying your own answer onto a sun-blotting sky.","sonnet":"A self-made shield that protected no one, a wound used as an excuse to stop time — but a voice traces where connection still quietly runs underground, and each broken memory played at the side allows one step forward; a bird that never returned and songs thrown after it give way to the admission that an unnoticed root still binds everything together.","src":"haiku"},
"saxon~wheels-of-steel":{"haiku":"Speed and dominance fuel this anthem of automotive superiority, a driver confident in their machine's power and indifferent to authority or competitors.","opus":"A biker's pure devotion to speed and his machine, the road as freedom and the engine as the only beauty he needs. Early NWOBHM revving on adrenaline and open-throttle escape, nothing beneath the chrome but joy.","sonnet":"A driver's love letter to his muscle car becomes a declaration of road dominance, celebrating speed, power, and the freedom of the open highway with zero tolerance for anything in his way.","src":"haiku"},
"sayuri~dawn":{"haiku":"Suffering and determination push the narrator forward through endless wandering toward distant dawn and redemption. Despite doubt and pain, the refusal to turn back sustains a belief in transformation and meeting again.","opus":"A vow to keep walking through pain and doubt toward a single sunrise, holding a truth that can't be compromised even while lost. It reads as an artist's creed about carrying a message to someone no matter how many times the path is blocked, choosing the long journey knowing loss lines it.","sonnet":"An unrelenting Japanese rock song about pressing toward a single dawn — the speaker refuses to abandon truth even through pain and mud, journeys alongside someone whose vision of a shared dream differs yet still shines, and vows to keep circling the blue star until the very last answer is found.","src":"haiku"},
"sbcr-vs-refused~elektra-remix":{"haiku":"Electric rebellion surges as the remix amplifies defiance. Power flows through transformed channels.","opus":"A dance-floor rework of the hardcore group Refused that channels political despair into a hammering electronic loop. Corpses piled into towering monuments and rulers perched on distant heights mean nothing to those still stuck in the mud below, dread spreading through the mob. However grand the spectacle at the top, life at the bottom stays exactly the same.","sonnet":"A monument of bodies stacked a thousand high changes nothing at ground level: the scale of the atrocity reaches for the sky, panic seeps into the rabble, minds go deranged scaling the walls — but \"down in the dirt, nothing has changed,\" and that repetition is the point.","src":"haiku"},
"sbcr~rise-fall":{"haiku":"Insecurity triumphed historically while mysteries persist; truth burns within eyes as the speaker confronts the horror of their former state.","opus":"Recoiling at how nearly insecurity won, until someone's truth-burning eyes cut through the insanity. A brief reckoning with self-doubt overcome.","sonnet":"A brief look back at a period when insecurity ruled and no one seemed to understand what was happening, resolved by a moment of eye contact where the truth burned clear — that insanity was the operating condition all along.","src":"haiku"},
"scala-kolacny-brothers~everything-in-its-right-place":{"haiku":"Death is inevitable and inescapable, holding dominion over all human experience and relationships.","opus":"A hushed, funereal choral arrangement whose reworked words keep circling mortality as an escape no one can dodge. Death waits after every intimacy, every wrongdoing, every falsehood, cradling everyone regardless. The children's-choir treatment turns the piece into an eerie reflection on dying claiming lovers and liars alike.","sonnet":"This appears to be a non-standard cover with lyrics that diverge from Radiohead's original — the text circling around light, sin, lies, and an inescapable death that embraces everyone without exception, a meditation on mortality as the one fate no one escapes.","src":"haiku"},
"scandroid~empty-streets":{"haiku":"A cyborg survivor walks alone through a post-apocalyptic city after drones and clones erase their known world. They search for meaning while memory fades and their hybrid nature isolates them.","opus":"Scandroid, Klayton's synthwave project, builds an explicit retro-futurist cyberpunk world of Blade Runner rain and neon. A lone survivor wanders a dystopia where machines and copies have wiped out everyone he knew, his past dissolving and his future rewritten as his time runs down. The isolation is as much aesthetic as it is story.","sonnet":"A lone survivor in a cyberpunk city where everyone has been taken or erased — the past fading, the future redesigned by forces he didn't choose, running out of time under streetlights at midnight. He's flesh and circuit both, destination unknown, and the only constant is the act of walking streets that no longer hold anyone he recognizes. Neo-Tokyo sent its clones; he's still here, still walking.","src":"haiku"},
"scandroid~neo-tokyo-dance-with-the-dead-remix":{"haiku":"Humanity escapes into cyberspace and dreams of a neon future city, merging flesh and circuits to survive apocalypse and find hope through digital communion.","opus":"A synthwave fantasy of a glowing cyberpunk metropolis where consciousness jacks into a shared network and bodies fuse with wiring. Rescue appears as a manufactured light eclipsing the real sun, and the lonely residents flee their deserted alleys by imagining themselves into a bright techno-future. The mood is hopeful escapism through machinery.","sonnet":"A cyberpunk escapist fantasy where hybrid human-machine beings — flesh fused with circuits — dream of a neon megacity called Neo-Tokyo as the only salvation from the empty, surveillance-dark streets they actually inhabit.","src":"haiku"},
"scandroid~rendezvous-vhs-dreams-remix":{"haiku":"Clandestine desire and secret meetings promise transcendence through intimacy; anticipation becomes indistinguishable from the meeting itself.","opus":"A synthwave anticipation of a secret meeting, two people flowing with electric longing as they close the distance. Desire suspended in neon nostalgia, the whole song a held breath before contact.","sonnet":"Two people plan a secret meeting charged with electric anticipation, their desire building as they move closer together in the night, the rendezvous itself the entire object of longing.","src":"haiku"},
"scandroid~salvation-code":{"haiku":"Technology and spirituality merge as the narrator seeks transcendence through digital communion with a savior figure, moving ever closer to salvation.","opus":"Klayton's retro-futurist saga: a man sunk low in a dystopian Tokyo clinging to the belief that he wasn't made to die, guided by a digital savior. Faith rewritten as code, longing for rescue in a machine world.","sonnet":"A figure lost in a dystopian underworld — Tokyo's lowest level — receives transmissions from an analog-digital savior whose signals cut through the chaos and guide them step by step toward something they call the salvation code, a promise of purpose pulling them upward.","src":"haiku"},
"scandroid~shout-the-forgotten-remix":{"opus":"A synthwave reworking of Tears for Fears' protest song, keeping its core call to voice anger at those who would compromise your integrity in brutal times, and its refusal to perform happiness on command. Klayton recasts that release of pent-up frustration as glowing, retro-futurist defiance.","sonnet":"A cover of Tears for Fears' 'Shout' — a song about the need to release suppressed rage against those who exploit and diminish you, refusing to sell your soul in a violent, manipulative world. The undercurrent of wanting to break someone's heart even after they've let their guard down gives the anger a personal, intimate edge.","src":"sonnet"},
"scar-symmetry~scorched-quadrant":{"haiku":"Technological annihilation and mutation consume civilization; drones execute without remorse while survivors deteriorate in ash, and the machines pursue with relentless precision.","opus":"A scorched-earth vision of autonomous war drones sweeping a ruined world, exterminating survivors with neither remorse nor guilt. Amid the carnage a transformed narrator receives a vision from an undefined coming race, framing the machine apocalypse as this generation's technological politics.","sonnet":"An automated exterminatory force — drones guided by satellites — scours a post-apocalyptic wasteland for survivors to destroy, the speaker eventually glimpsing a vision from a coming race that guides them to the catacombs as the scorched quadrant burns in a politics of technological war.","src":"haiku"},
"scarlet-house~wanted":{"haiku":"Desire to be needed and chosen, seeking validation through being wanted by another.","opus":"A confession of helpless attachment to someone who has changed for the worse. The narrator admits he's still drawn to who this person used to be, playing by their rules even as they use control to keep him low. Losing himself in the chase, he asks what it will take to finally move on from a want he can't shake.","sonnet":"Wanting someone who has already changed into a stranger, knowing the dynamic is about control, knowing they play by rules that cost them their sense of self — and still unable to move on. The confession \"you're all I want, if you couldn't tell\" is both declaration and admission of defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"scarlet~end-in-blood":{"haiku":"Profound alienation erupts into violent rejection as the narrator confronts the vast chasm between their values and society's normalcy. Consumption and superficial faith repulse them enough to justify apocalyptic action.","opus":"A furious rejection of the normalized life of mortgages, consumer wants, and inherited religion, seen as a scam sold like alchemy. The contempt escalates into homicidal fantasy, the singer unable to find a shared human heartbeat with people he views as deluded and disposable.","sonnet":"A seething rejection of the normalised, consumer-religious life — the speaker sees the other as utterly alien in their comfort with cars, church, and mundane marriages, and the alienation is so absolute it escalates to fantasised violence: this day will end in blood.","src":"haiku"},
"scarlet~love-heroin":{"haiku":"Love as addictive toxin injected into the body, a vow binding two people together in mutual self-destruction that kills from within.","opus":"The metalcore band fuses romance and drug dependence into a single deadly figure, casting desire as a narcotic pushed into the bloodstream that rots you from inside. A promise sworn in a single evening to stay together forever twists into mutual ruin, with the chorus batting the idea of loving and dying back and forth until closeness and overdose become the same act.","sonnet":"Love is rendered as an addictive and fatal substance — it enters like a needle under skin and kills from the inside. Two people made a vow to live and die together, but the bond itself is the toxin. Devotion and destruction are inseparable; love does not save, it consumes.","src":"haiku"},
"scarlxrd~hell-is-xn-earth":{"haiku":"Nihilistic despair and self-destruction pervade existence as the narrator rejects hope and embraces chaos as the only authentic response to suffering.","opus":"A raw purge of self-destruction and disgust with humanity, treating Earth itself as hell and lashing out at betrayers and nihilism while coping through weed, songs and cash.","sonnet":"The world is recast as a subdivision of hell — \"heaven's not a place on Earth, Earth is a place in Hell\" — as the speaker catalogues self-destruction, betrayal, sobriety struggles, and marks on his arms, raging at the serpents who slither in silence while calling him violent for speaking plainly.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~3005":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic disdain for humanity's obsessions with false gods and destructive communication technologies frames imminent extinction. The narrator's survival depends on transcendence and escape while civilization crumbles below.","opus":"A sardonic toast to everything supposedly great about civilization, sarcastically clapping for presidents, genocide, and killers alike. The narrator imagines outliving the coming collapse from a spaceship while everyone else drowns, mocking humanity's faith in resurrection and progress.","sonnet":"A sardonic toast to presidents, serial killers, genocide, and the internet collapses cultural icons into absurd equivalence — the speaker survives in a spaceship while resurrection junkies lose ground below, shooting up the world from a vantage of black ironic detachment.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~babylon":{"haiku":"Desire and abandonment spiral through a landscape of rootlessness and longing, where the speaker offers everything to save a lover who has already marooned them.","opus":"Daron Malakian's post-System of a Down project turns a fraught relationship into mythic exile. Babylon is both refuge and ruin, a place to reunite with the person who hurled him into life and drained his confidence. Cast away time and again, the narrator still offers his soul to rescue them amid tender memories of summer rooftops.","sonnet":"Babylon is both destination and ruin: the narrator runs toward it to meet someone who once threw them into life, who looked through satellites and stripped their confidence, yet for whom they would give their soul. The rooftops in summer and the marooned state frame a devotion that survives even the person it was given to.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~chemicals":{"haiku":"Intimacy dissolves into contempt and chemical excess, the speaker alternates between madness and lucidity while mocking the human need for connection.","opus":"A deliberately abrasive spectacle of a poisonous relationship, intoxication and abuse and disgust hurled out for shock. The chemicals stand in for whatever mutual self-destruction keeps two people locked together in madness and nobody around to care.","sonnet":"A degrading, chaotic dynamic between two people who clearly damage each other — physical violence, drug use, and explicit sexual humiliation presented as the twisted glue holding them together, the speaker inviting the other to self-destruct alongside them.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~cute-machines":{"opus":"Scars on Broadway, Daron Malakian's post-System of a Down outlet, trade in blunt, hooky aggression, and this is craving stripped to its motor. Relentless obsession with something described as cute machines loops with no narrative beyond the desire itself, the repetition enacting the fixation rather than explaining it.","sonnet":"A song expressing simple, relentless obsession with something or someone described as cute machines — pure craving with no narrative beyond the loop of desire.","src":"sonnet"},
"scars-on-broadway~enemy":{"haiku":"Humanity's exploitative nature and complicity make us all architects of destruction.","opus":"Daron Malakian's blunt verdict that humanity is the planet's own enemy, hooked on drugs and on flags that Jesus sells. The catchy chant hides an ecological and anti-hypocrisy indictment of a species destroying itself and the world.","sonnet":"Humanity is the enemy of the Earth — religious and corporate exploitation go hand in hand, everyone is medicated and controlled, and the narrator states plainly that humans are the problem, too self-deceived to see what they are doing to the planet.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~exploding-reloading":{"haiku":"Violence and faith merge into cycles of self-annihilation and hollow rebirth.","opus":"A jagged tangle of self-destruction, mass killing and Turkish denial that gestures directly at the Armenian genocide Malakian's family lived through, alongside the religious bloodshed knotted up with it. Submission spreads sickness through a population, and the title's cycle of detonation and rearming stands in for history grinding on unchanged.","sonnet":"A visceral provocation from Daron Malakian that fuses religious iconography with self-destruction and mass death — the cycling of suicide, genocide, and Christ is a direct reference to the Armenian Genocide, framing Turkish denial as a kind of spiritual violence that loops endlessly without resolution. The exploding/reloading refrain enacts the cycle of atrocity and erasure, and the Jesus Christ motif implicates complicit Western Christianity in that historical silence.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~fucking":{"haiku":"Sexualization and spectacle commodify intimacy for entertainment; children become objects of perverse fascination while apocalypse looms indifferent and unstoppable.","opus":"A revulsion at a culture that turns sex, violence, and the sexualization of children into public spectacle, sung as apocalyptic warning. The relentless obscenity is deliberate, mirroring the degradation it condemns.","sonnet":"A grotesque indictment of hypersexualized media culture and its normalization in the lives of children — the song frames modern entertainment as a degrading spectacle that sexualizes its audience, treating public titillation and genital mutilation as part of the same escalating, apocalyptic show.","src":"haiku","fable":"Malakian wields the deliberately crude title as a decoy: this is a tirade against a culture that has turned sex into a show, the repeated gerund pounded until the word sheds all erotic charge and becomes bare mechanical display. The device is the trapped onlooker — the narrator forced to witness the act paraded before him, the pose sustained because the camera rewards a flattering surface — folding the come-on into a warning that the same machine coaching you to perform will later pimp your body off. That counterfeit fantasy where minors get eroticized is stated flatly, so the giddy hook of Armageddon waking up reads as the moral terminus of the illusion rather than a party, the vileness of the diction matched exactly to the vileness it indicts.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"crude diction repeated until it sheds all erotic charge","built":"the giddy Armageddon hook recast as moral terminus of the illusion, not a party"},
"scars-on-broadway~funny":{"haiku":"Isolated cruelty and societal indifference blend into surreal mourning.","opus":"A darkly numb elegy where death, drugs and neo-Nazi graffiti all get met with the same flat word, the dissociation of someone too hollowed out to grieve. Underneath, it asks where a person ends up when they finally fall, and whether anyone can really change.","sonnet":"About witnessing death, violence, and societal indifference with a darkly ironic detachment — the narrator sees swastikas on Santa Monica Boulevard, a person painted red and dead while no one announces it; each stanza begins \"funny\" as a way of marking the absurdity of how normalized horror becomes.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~insane":{"haiku":"The only escape from hidden suffering is to surrender to madness and embrace the pain as the price of authenticity.","opus":"Daron Malakian's side project frames self-destruction as an addictive impulse. The narrator sees trouble buried deep and, rather than escape it, keeps choosing to dive back in, summoning old pain and madness on purpose. The looping invitation to go insane again turns relapse into a restless, almost gleeful compulsion he can't leave behind.","sonnet":"Pain and insanity are beckoned back deliberately — let's go insane again, bring back the pain again — as if suffering is the only honest state, the broken dreams and hidden troubles a kind of homecoming more real than whatever passes for normal.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~kill-each-other-live-forever":{"haiku":"Mutual self-destruction and eternal existence form an impossible paradox—humans cannot reconcile the desire to persist with the impulse to annihilate.","opus":"A tense meditation on humanity's self-destructive contradictions from Daron Malakian's post-System project. A faceless voice in the skull stokes shame as people refuse to believe existence could carry on or the sun keep burning. The core riddle cuts deep: if we keep slaughtering one another, how could we ever last?","sonnet":"The voice calling from inside the brain insists that killing each other and living forever are mutually contradictory ambitions — the world moving on, the sun still shining, regardless of what humans choose to believe about their own permanence.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~serious":{"haiku":"Heaviness suffocates spontaneity as the speaker rebels against intensity with mockery, demanding lightness from someone determined to be grave.","opus":"A dry kiss-off to someone too intense and self-important, whose grand ego and heaviness leave the speaker dizzy and worn out. All he wants is a light, easy fling rather than a partner treating everything like a solemn quest, so the very earnestness becomes the dealbreaker.","sonnet":"A flirtatious dismissal of someone too uptight to enjoy themselves — the speaker is looking for romance in a world full of people chasing missions and ego trips, finding the target's seriousness both annoying and somehow charming enough to drive them half-crazy.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~stoner-hate":{"haiku":"Aggressive confrontation toward cultural complacency and false positivity, the speaker antagonistic and unrepentant in their contempt.","opus":"Daron Malakian's project delivers a snarling, tongue-in-cheek provocation aimed at California's laid-back stoner culture. Casting himself as a rude, obnoxious invader with intent to kill, the narrator mocks blissed-out complacency with nursery-rhyme nonsense and sneering swagger. Beneath the joke lies genuine contempt for hazy passivity and a manic urge to disrupt it.","sonnet":"A surreal, cartoonish aggression toward California hippie culture — 'Supercalifragilistic' as meaningless noise, a psychopath invasion, rude and obnoxious with intent to kill. The song is less argument than provocation, its absurdist energy the point rather than any coherent stance.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~they-say":{"haiku":"Prophets of doom warn of inevitable apocalypse while governments maintain false order; the end arrives not through external forces but through collective moral failure.","opus":"A disgusted survey of a society sold on trends and impending doom, where leaders betray the public rights they claim to defend. The narrator retreats into nostalgia and private mind while everyone chants that the end is near—and half-welcomes it.","sonnet":"A cynical, apocalyptic political song about living under a government and media apparatus that corrupt, surveil, and sexualize while broadcasting inevitable doom — the speaker watches a president perform family values while hollowing out society, and leans into sin as the only honest response.","src":"haiku"},
"scars-on-broadway~universe":{"haiku":"Cosmic annihilation threatens while the subject falls into acid rain and shame; mortality becomes the only absolute.","opus":"An apocalyptic reverie of comets, corrosive downpours and a lethal sun, the singer crying out to his mother while wondering whether this is extinction or rebirth. The refrain about soaring or perishing across the cosmos holds both possibilities in unresolved tension.","sonnet":"A cosmic lament addressed to a mother figure — Earth or some higher force — asking whether humanity is flying or dying through the universe as comets fall, acid rain washes the streets of shame, and fire burns the sun, seeking confirmation that this is somehow rebirth and not the end.","src":"haiku"},
"scene-queen~girls-gone-wild":{"haiku":"Male power and impunity contrasted with female ambition; if freed from biological constraint, women would wield even greater danger and transcendence than men.","opus":"The bimbocore provocateur flips male entitlement into satire, imagining the reckless power she'd wield if she had a man's anatomy. Boardroom demands and locker-room crudeness collide as she skewers the double standard that lets men fail upward. It's a loud, sardonic takedown of masculine privilege dressed in deliberately trashy shock.","sonnet":"A takeover fantasy where female sexual power operates with the ruthlessness men use in boardrooms — CEO mode, Knoxville-Steve-O chaos energy, demanding respect through provocation. If I had a dick I'd be dangerous is not envy but a critique: the speaker already has the power, the world just hasn't caught up to how dangerous she is.","src":"haiku"},
"school-food-punishment~you-may-crawl":{"haiku":"Japanese lyrics explore twilight regeneration amid chaos and meaningless resistance; divided perception seeks meaning in vain.","opus":"The Japanese band sets a restless interior monologue against shifting light, its narrator sensing change in the air and cutting away the wasteful nightly resistance. Broken images recur of rain and clearing skies mirrored in the right eye while meaning is questioned in the left, and a tomorrow that has snapped off leaves only lingering night, a fractured meditation on hope decaying at the edge of memory.","sonnet":"A consciousness drifts through evening light, registering subtle shifts in mood and perception — rain in one eye, meaning in the other — without arriving at resolution. The self splinters into competing visions, memory offers no anchor, and the present moment dissolves before it can be held. Disorientation is the whole texture.","src":"haiku"},
"scorey~guardian-angel":{"haiku":"Grief over a lost friend transforms into spiritual protection; the narrator sustains connection through memory and celebrates the deceased's transcendence.","opus":"A young rapper grieving a friend killed in the streets, torn between vengeance, survivor's guilt, and the pills that don't numb it. Trench mourning that turns the dead into a guardian angel because nothing else holds.","sonnet":"Grief for a friend killed by street violence, addressed directly to him — the speaker oscillates between gratitude for what they shared, guilt at not saving him, rage at those responsible, and a destabilising recognition that the streets took someone who deserved better.","src":"haiku"},
"scorpions~don-t-believe-her":{"haiku":"A seductive woman who preys on men with false promises and dangerous games. She's a compulsive liar and manipulator who ruins everyone she touches.","opus":"Scorpions, the German hard-rock veterans, cut this on their commercial peak Crazy World, their glossy late-eighties arena-rock formula turning caution into swagger over a stadium chorus. It warns against a manipulative seductress who dangles impossible promises and leaves men wrecked, the narrator cautioning from bitter experience before you fall too far and get scorched by her.","sonnet":"A warning from someone who already fell for it — a woman who plays a perfect game, promises everything, delivers heartbreak, and moves on. The speaker got burned, wasted and unable to see it coming, and now turns around to warn the next person. She'll say you're the only one; she's a stranger who knows the game better than you do. Don't believe her.","src":"haiku"},
"scorpions~hit-between-the-eyes":{"haiku":"Prowling the danger zone at night, the singer faces loaded guns and imminent violence. They're ready for death—a hit between the eyes—but plead not to die so young.","opus":"Germany's Scorpions cut this hard-edged track for their 1990 album Crazy World, best known for the anthem Wind of Change, showing the veteran hard-rock band still swinging with pistol-cocked bravado and stadium-sized menace. It's a nocturnal warning about a perilous stretch where a threat closes in, the narrator steeling himself for a fatal blow while protesting that his time hasn't come.","sonnet":"Survival anxiety in a city turned dangerous — the song puts the speaker on streets thick with threat, facing someone who wants to cut them down, and the only logic available is armed deterrence. The chorus is a plea, not a boast: too young to die, too cornered to escape, caught in a zone past the point where reason does any good.","src":"haiku"},
"scorpions~kicks-after-six":{"haiku":"A woman enslaved by nine-to-five drudgery craves her nightly freedom to shed restraint and become wild. Each evening releases her from the corporate machine for uninhibited pleasure.","opus":"A woman trapped in a drab office routine comes fully alive once the workday ends, shedding the drudgery for wild nocturnal freedom and appetites her respectable daytime self keeps hidden. The German hard-rock veterans play the double life for cheeky, arena-sized fun in their glam-leaning era.","sonnet":"A working woman's double life — by day she is absorbed into the office machine, counting hours in a suit-and-tie servitude that leaves no room for herself. The moment the clock frees her, she transforms: spreading wings, running wild, taking everything the daylight denies her. The song frames this not as transgression but as necessary survival.","src":"haiku"},
"scorpions~restless-nights":{"haiku":"The narrator travels restlessly through world cities (London, Paris, Dallas, Moscow, Tokyo) seeking excess and escape—never content, always fleeing.","opus":"The rock-tour life as a blur of interchangeable cities, each a one-night high before moving on. Behind the globetrotting bravado sits an admission that the restlessness is the only road the singer knows how to travel.","sonnet":"A road-life snapshot of the rock touring existence — the narrator moves from city to city, London to Paris to Dallas to Rio to Tokyo, living on sex, crime, danger, and barely surviving each stop, with restless nights as the permanent condition of life on the road.","src":"haiku"},
"scorpions~to-be-with-you-in-heaven":{"haiku":"Obsessive romantic love willing to endure any suffering to be together. The speaker would traverse hell itself for their beloved, and there is no cure for such lethal attachment.","opus":"Devotion pushed past healthy limits into fixation, the narrator insisting no punishment or cruelty could dampen his need, willing to endure any torment to be united with the object of his obsession. Scorpions render this in their arena power-ballad mode, love treated as fire, ruin, and salvation all at once.","sonnet":"Devotion pitched at the level of martyrdom — she can destroy everything and his desire won't break. Heaven is the destination, but the path there runs through the darkest hell, and he'd walk it willingly. Love this consuming has no cure; it kills even as it elevates, and he's burning with it, close enough to heaven tonight to mean it literally.","src":"haiku"},
"scorpions~wind-of-change":{"haiku":"Following the Moskva through geopolitical transformation, the speaker witnesses freedom breaking through like wind; tomorrow's children will dream of shared peace.","opus":"Standing in Moscow's Gorky Park, the narrator senses history turning toward peace and freedom as the Cold War thaws; the deeper read is hope that the children of tomorrow can dream in a reunited world where balalaika and guitar sing the same song.","sonnet":"A song about the fall of the Berlin Wall and the hope of post-Cold War reconciliation — written from the perspective of a German band witnessing Soviet citizens in Moscow's Gorky Park and sensing a new generation's shared dreams crossing old divides.","src":"haiku"},
"scott-matthew~a-1n0o12s":{"opus":"Scott Matthew's fragile, string-laden balladry made him a fixture of aching indie-folk, and here that intimacy carries a deathbed farewell. The dying speaker apologizes for not always caring, asks loved ones not to cry or rage, accepts that time will not wait and every story ends, and promises to wait on the other side, assuring them it will not be so bad.","sonnet":"A dying person's farewell — apologizing for not always caring, asking loved ones not to cry or be angry, accepting that time doesn't wait and all stories end, promising to wait on the other side and that it won't be so bad.","src":"sonnet"},
"scott-matthew~be-human":{"haiku":"Yearning for emotional depth and imperfection, envying humanity's flaws and capacity to feel.","opus":"Sung from the perspective of something efficient and inhuman that envies the messy, feeling parts of a life, the scraped knees and out-of-key singing. Its longing for imperfection asks what it's worth to be able to grieve and love.","sonnet":"A non-human narrator — analytical, sleepless, without error — imagines what it would feel like to be human: messy, emotional, embodied, and finite, envying the mud and bruises and laughter and love that come with a mortal life.","src":"haiku"},
"scott-matthew~beauty-is-within-us":{"haiku":"Addresses maternal responsibility for a child's self-hatred and deformity, trapping him between internal darkness and her false reassurance. Explores inherited shame that has become inescapable.","opus":"A wrenching address to a mother from a child who feels born wrong, a mutant trapped between genders, cursing and loving her in the same breath while her reassurances about inner beauty ring hollow against his shame and self-disgust. Scott Matthew, the Australian singer-songwriter known for his fragile, orchestral melancholy, turns the mother's rose metaphor into something that has grown dark and ugly, ending in a plea for release.","sonnet":"A son's anguished address to his mother, oscillating between accusation and love — he holds her responsible for bringing him into a life where he feels monstrous and alien, a man with a woman's soul cursed to be neither. The mother's reassurances about inner beauty feel hollow against the darkness that has actually grown inside him, yet he cannot let go of her either.","src":"haiku"},
"scott-matthew~lithium-flower":{"haiku":"Admiration for effortless grace breeds obsession; the speaker cannot understand how someone moves through life so perfectly, becoming haunted by her impossible completeness.","opus":"A cover for Ghost in the Shell, gazing at a poised, almost inhuman woman as an unknowable blend of math and grace who never wipes out. It is about longing to know the private, waking self behind a perfect, machine-like surface.","sonnet":"An infatuated, slightly surreal portrait of a woman the speaker cannot decode: she is \"incredible math,\" a waking lithium flower, a sonic wave — calm, matador-like, surfing without ever wiping out — and the speaker's curiosity circles the simple mystery of what she does when she wakes up.","src":"haiku"},
"scott-weiland~missing-cleveland":{"haiku":"Nostalgic distance romanticizes past connection; the speaker drifts between cities seeking ground beneath their feet.","opus":"Weiland's fractured take on a marriage that turned toxic, tenderness and violence coexisting, homesick for a colder, simpler place. The disorientation of a dreamer whose feet never quite reach the ground.","sonnet":"A speaker displaced from ordinariness — thrust into something surreal and ultimately toxic — finds himself missing the honest, unglamorous comforts of home: dive bars, snow, truth told without drama, and the simple life that the relationship replaced.","src":"haiku"},
"scowl~b-a-b-e":{"haiku":"Celebration and affirmation of beloved through acronym endearment, the speaker expressing tenderness and attachment.","opus":"The hardcore band channels burnout and mounting pressure into a demand for space. Spent at both extremes and refusing to swallow others' bitter expectations, the narrator digs in, insisting she isn't short on drive, only on air. The repeated cry for everyone to shut up so she can breathe captures overwhelm hardening into defiance.","sonnet":"Exhaustion with people who sign on, play along, and grab at what's sacred — the speaker digs in, refuses the sour pills, caught in the paradox of finding comfort only in suffering, thrill-seeking as the sole mode, and finally turning the demand back on the takers: you catch your breath, I never will.","src":"haiku"},
"scowl~opening-night":{"haiku":"Performance and debut moment with stakes and anxiety, the speaker facing critical exposure and judgment.","opus":"Celebrity and repetition melt into a numbing loop where each performance feels identical until the narrator loses track of her surroundings and even her own identity, flattened into a repeating character. The chilling insistence that all of this was intended points at machinery designed to grind personhood into a marketable product.","sonnet":"The performer's self dissolves into repetition — every night the same song and dance, the pavement going schizophrenic under the fixed stare, identity reduced to a face that forgets its own name, all of it reframed as part of the plan, with the question do you relate, do you feel fake left hanging over the audience.","src":"haiku"},
"screaming-trees~nearly-lost-you":{"haiku":"A person nearly loses someone meaningful and reflects on guilt, distance, and the recurring pull of past sins; the near-loss has transformed where they've ended up.","opus":"A hazy reckoning with sin and near-loss, a distant cry calling the narrator back to old temptations, the closeness of losing someone leaving both parties changed and reaching for sleep. Screaming Trees, the Ellensburg band fronted by Mark Lanegan, scored their biggest hit with this 1992 track, carried onto the Singles soundtrack and marking the psychedelic edge of the grunge moment with Lanegan's weathered baritone.","sonnet":"A near-loss processed as an altered state — something happened, something almost took someone away, and the aftermath feels like being dragged to an unfamiliar place with no road back. The song refuses to explain the event precisely; it inhabits the disorientation instead, cycling through guilt, exhaustion, and a fragile plea to just sleep and let the crisis pass.","src":"haiku"},
"scro~face-to-face-with-death":{"haiku":"Existential confrontation becomes recursive nightmare; the speaker cycles through awareness of mortality while remaining powerless to alter its approach.","opus":"A young person's despair reframed as a confrontation with death, feeling like a broken kid whose pain won't subside and whose own thoughts are the enemy. The dying is internal, the killer his own sinking mind.","sonnet":"A speaker who feels like nothing — a dumb kid, tearing up alone, thoughts sinking — stands face to face with death, choking on someone's breath, with their fear visible in the eyes of the one who keeps stepping closer to take their life.","src":"haiku"},
"scythe-of-luna~imitate":{"haiku":"A speaker drowns in nightmares and self-destruction, endlessly imitating unaffected life while voices demand breath and release.","opus":"A cry against a cruel world that keeps taking what is loved, where self-destruction paradoxically feels like a perfect state of mind and daily life is a hollow imitation behind faked eyes.","sonnet":"A song about the exhaustion of performing normalcy while internally disintegrating — the narrator, hemorrhaging inside from a world that keeps taking what they love, finds themselves trapped imitating an unaffected life, unable to break out of the hollow decorative shell of a functioning person.","src":"haiku"},
"scythe-of-sorrow~rise-and-shine":{"haiku":"Death's arrival brings grim clarity as the reaper claims dominion over all resistance and final breath. Night becomes an agent of inevitable ending and surrender to the cosmic inevitability of mortality.","opus":"A gleeful summons to the reaper, dressing in black to walk beside death as blood floods the streets. It celebrates killing as a nightly ritual with nowhere left to hide.","sonnet":"A call to a personified Reaper to take up the scythe and go to work — blood flows in the streets, there is no hiding from the Reaper's find, and the command \"rise and shine\" is addressed directly to death itself as both greeting and battle cry.","src":"haiku"},
"seamlessr~deathblow-feat-celldweller":{"haiku":"Combat readiness and confrontation approach; danger looms and the moment for violent action arrives inevitably.","opus":"A high-octane electronic battle cry built for adrenaline and defiance, the narrator and his crew loading up and squaring off against the danger ahead, rising against invasion to land a decisive strike. Committing their lives to freedom by choice, they frame the killing blow as a gift, standing solitary against the invaders to the last.","sonnet":"A rallying cry for collective resistance against invasion: the crew is armed and counted down, daughters and sons of a new machine gun standing alone against the enemy — committing their lives to freedom willingly — and the deathblow they deliver is framed as both gift and sacrifice.","src":"haiku"},
"seapool~brainwashed-wow-wow":{"haiku":"Cyclical failure and shame spiral where aspirations mask empty core, yet persisting through repeated disillusionment to maintain fragile selfhood.","opus":"A Japanese track that pokes at the seductions of conformity and self-erasure. Beneath dreamlike, heavily made-up imagery, the narrator wrestles with longing that gnaws at the heart yet proves illusory, insisting she must remain herself to love anyone honestly. Solitude becomes a strange serenity, a refusal to be brainwashed into someone else's dream.","sonnet":"A meditation on self-construction through failure and longing: the narrator acknowledges that shame-free living means wounds are inevitable, that someone always lives in their chest fueling dreams, yet insists on remaining themselves even as admiration corrodes from within. The refrain \"brainwashed wow again\" frames the whole cycle of infatuation and self-loss as a kind of willing, recurring trance.","src":"haiku"},
"season-of-ghosts~the-great-unknown":{"haiku":"Confusion and delusion blur as shared human experience—we chase light while conspirators steal it, yet nobody truly knows what waits beyond uncertainty.","opus":"Chasing half-formed hopes through a fog of confusion while the world seems set on dimming your light, running on little more than a glimmer. The song's honest resting place is to stop pretending anyone can see ahead and simply admit you're stepping into the dark.","sonnet":"Humanity drifts through confusion and uncertainty, enslaved to its own feelings and chasing delusions while the future stays opaque; the refrain 'we manage to stay alive' is less triumph than bare fact, and existence in the great unknown is simply the condition no one can escape.","src":"haiku"},
"seatbelts~cosmic-dare-pretty-with-a-pistol":{"haiku":"Love transcends space and time as two lovers bet everything on connection despite uncertainty, asking if devotion can overcome cosmic separation.","opus":"A jazzy, cosmic gamble of a love that dares everything, betting on words and floating through space joined at the heart. The pistol makes devotion feel armed and precarious, love as a beautiful high-stakes risk.","sonnet":"A woman holds a gun and pleads for love across an impossible distance — two people separated but joined at the heart, traveling through space and time together, daring each other to commit fully to something that may destroy them.","src":"haiku"},
"seatbelts~diamonds":{"haiku":"Beauty and love shimmer with unexplainable magic that transcends rational understanding.","opus":"A bright, retro love song using diamonds as the emblem of a partner who doesn't lie or deceive, only shines. It's uncomplicated devotion: wanting to hold onto someone precious and inexplicable forever.","sonnet":"A gentle, devoted love song comparing a partner to diamonds — reliable, truthful, and beautiful in a way that can't deceive. The emotional core is gratitude for someone whose constant, uncomplicated presence feels magical.","src":"haiku"},
"seatbelts~einstein-groovin":{"haiku":"Young women navigate fate through courage and self-direction, refusing victim status; trusting inner light outweighs external certainty or approval.","opus":"An Italian pop anthem of self-trust and forward motion from Yoko Kanno's genre-hopping collective. Addressed to a young girl losing her way among branching roads, it urges her to steer her own destiny and ride her chances like a train toward the horizon. When she feels lost, the light within will guide her back to herself.","sonnet":"An Italian-language encouragement to a young woman to trust the light inside her and drive her own destiny — even when lost, even headlong into the wind, even slowly. The repeated \"ehi tu ragazzina\" (hey, little girl) frames it as an address from someone older, urging her toward the world she carries within herself.","src":"haiku"},
"seatbelts~gotta-knock-a-little-harder":{"haiku":"Happiness remains abstract concept while emptiness dominates as the speaker understands that meaning requires effort beyond reach. The world becomes parody of fulfillment forever out of grasp.","opus":"Yoko Kanno's soul ballad, made famous through Cowboy Bebop, traces a person who spent years sealed off behind bolted doors, keeping every emotion locked away out of fear of intimacy and pride while sinking deeper into ruin. Realizing that all this self-defense amounted to her whole life, she finally starts hammering to force her way toward another person.","sonnet":"A lifetime of emotional self-fortification — chain on the door, never shedding a tear, slamming out every person who tried to get in — finally gives way when the narrator realizes the other side might not be worse; they tear off the chains and knock harder and harder at a door that was locked from the inside all along.","src":"haiku"},
"seatbelts~mushroom-hunting":{"opus":"The Seatbelts were Yoko Kanno's genre-hopping ensemble for Cowboy Bebop, built to sound like a live band caught mid-jam. This joyful track makes the process the subject: its words are almost entirely cues for instruments to enter, capped by a chanted title, celebrating the sheer exhilaration of music-making rather than any lyrical theme.","sonnet":"A purely performative, joyful track from the Cowboy Bebop soundtrack that functions as a live instruction to the band — the lyrics are almost entirely cues for different instruments to join, topped by a chanted title; the song is about the exhilaration of music-making itself rather than any lyrical theme.","src":"sonnet"},
"seatbelts~no-reply":{"haiku":"Absence and regret frame an unrequited goodbye; the speaker exists only in shadow, unable to reach across the distance they have created.","opus":"A farewell from someone convinced they've ruined everything, thanking a loved one for their loyalty while accepting they've faded past reach and now call out to only silence.","sonnet":"A dying or departing person reflects on a life of lies and self-deception, expressing profound gratitude to someone who showed them truth and love, and lamenting that they call out but can no longer be heard — a deathbed farewell.","src":"haiku"},
"seatbelts~pearls":{"haiku":"Distance and longing intertwine; memory and dream sustain separated lovers.","opus":"An Italian ballad of longing for an absent lover, holding emptiness that still tastes of them, weeping an ocean they'll always return across. Pearls of love are memories treasured while the moon and sea promise a reunion.","sonnet":"An Italian love song of longing and patient waiting — the speaker holds emptiness that smells of the absent beloved, breathes their words as a melody, and trusts that the pale moon, the wind, and the sea will eventually carry the person back across an ocean of tears to the shore of reality.","src":"haiku"},
"seatbelts~tank":{"opus":"Kanno's Seatbelts open Cowboy Bebop with a brassy jazz explosion, and this is its ignition. A brief spoken cue and an energetic countdown launch the iconic theme, carrying no lyrical content beyond the call to begin, the words functioning purely as a starter's pistol for the instrumental to detonate.","sonnet":"A brief spoken intro — 'let's jam' — that serves as the launch of the Cowboy Bebop theme, with no lyrical content beyond an energetic countdown.","src":"sonnet"},
"seatbelts~tank-tv-stretch":{"opus":"This stretched cut of the Cowboy Bebop theme extends Kanno's big-band showcase, and its spoken preamble sets the scene before the horns hit. The lines are pure call-to-action, an urging to gather everyone and jam over a countdown, scene-setting for an instrumental jazz piece rather than lyric in any real sense.","sonnet":"The lyrics consist of a brief spoken intro — \"I think it's time we blow this scene, get everybody and the stuff together, okay, three, two, one, let's jam\" — which is the iconic intro to the Cowboy Bebop theme. There is no lyrical content beyond this call-to-action; the song is an instrumental jazz piece and the words are a scene-setting preamble.","src":"sonnet"},
"seatbelts~what-planet-is-this":{"opus":"Another Seatbelts cue from Kanno's Cowboy Bebop palette, this one distills bewilderment to a single repeated phrase. With no development beyond the title, it conveys stunned, speechless incomprehension at the surrounding world, a fragment of mood functioning as texture within the score's restless variety.","sonnet":"A pure expression of alienation or bewilderment — the title phrase repeated with no development, conveying a sense of stunned, speechless incomprehension at the surrounding world.","src":"sonnet"},
"secret-band~moon":{"haiku":"Self-reflection and deception create copies of oneself; the mirror becomes a predatory thing watching with violent intent.","opus":"This hardcore side project spins a paranoid, self-referential riddle about performing to a mirror and choosing to inhabit a falsehood. Hounded by a spotlit pursuer, the narrator feels weirdly at home in his own delusion, sounds fueling a self-generated illusion. A borrowed fragment about a knowing, forgetful eye tips it toward cosmic dread before he resolves to keep faking it.","sonnet":"A person haunted by something predatory and nihilistic — a spotlit mongrel, a smiling force that preys on the bland — keeps trying to escape their own mind only to find it has made copies. The moon becomes a hideous watching eye, and the self is left performing for its own reflection.","src":"haiku"},
"secret-band~upgrades":{"opus":"A chaotic, drug-addled stream of consciousness from a self-aware screwup, half-boasting and half-lamenting being a washed-up burnout everyone dislikes, laced with crude sexual and self-improvement imagery.","sonnet":"A chaotic, fragmented portrait of social alienation and substance-fueled dysfunction — the narrator cycles through self-awareness of being difficult and unwanted, stoned self-mythologizing, and crude humor, without a clear resolved emotional core.","src":"sonnet"},
"seeds-of-mary~rewind-me":{"haiku":"Fragmented memories cling as useless artifacts, seeking to reverse time before dissolution leaves nothing and no one remains.","opus":"A plea to be reminded of one's own past as memory smears and identity dissolves, wanting to be reset to a time before the picture drained of color. Facing a stalled, aimless life, the singer needs proof of having come from somewhere while anything of the self remains.","sonnet":"Blurred memories surface and rebuild an identity that has come unmoored; the speaker, going nowhere and stripped of black-and-white certainty, asks to be rewound to the moment before everything dissolved — a plea to be reminded they came from somewhere before there is nothing left.","src":"haiku"},
"seether~breakdown":{"haiku":"A speaker accepts destruction if it brings relief, offering themselves as a target rather than continuing to inflict harm.","opus":"Inviting an ex to tear you apart if it makes them feel better, wounded pride insisting you're worth more than their contempt allows. Defiance dressed as surrender, hurt curdling into the assertion of your own depth.","sonnet":"A speaker tells someone who has betrayed and diminished them that they can do their worst — hate, break, destroy — because the speaker is more than the damage being inflicted. The anger is controlled and final: whatever you are doing, I am still more.","src":"haiku"},
"seether~broken":{"haiku":"Emotional vulnerability and loneliness fracture the speaker, leaving them dependent on a partner's presence for coherence.","opus":"A love song built entirely on need, the singer only whole in the other's presence and shattered when they're gone. Grief-adjacent devotion, tenderness that admits it can't stand on its own.","sonnet":"A person speaks to someone whose absence leaves them unable to function — open and vulnerable without them, lonely and directionless when they are gone. The worst is framed as already over, but the emotional dependency remains total and undisguised.","src":"haiku"},
"seether~gasoline":{"haiku":"Consumerism and manufactured desire corrode human connection, reducing intimacy to disposable objects while shame becomes the only authentic feeling left.","opus":"A vicious takedown of celebrity worship and the women who model themselves on airbrushed magazines, the narrator so consumed by contempt and envy he fantasizes about destroying what he can't stop wanting.","sonnet":"A speaker projects violent, misogynistic obsession onto a woman he perceives as vain and empty, fantasising about degradation and destruction while framing her as deserving it. The song turns on loathing that is indistinguishable from fixation.","src":"haiku"},
"seether~judas-mind":{"haiku":"Betrayal and storm arrive as consequence; the speaker warns of truth that cannot be escaped, urging surrender as the path through fear.","opus":"A meditation on betrayal and faith under threat, urging the fearful to hold to a truth that can't enslave them while most souls head to slaughter. The repeated betrayal by a Judas figure, surrounding the singer with pain, sits against a plea to be led to shelter and told it's alright.","sonnet":"Addressed to someone who surrounds the speaker with pain and betrayal — 'Judas mind, as you betray me again' — the song distinguishes those with courage from the ovine majority heading for slaughter, urging the listener not to be afraid of the storm while the speaker endures cycles of shutdown and betrayal.","src":"haiku"},
"seether~leech":{"haiku":"Parasitic extraction never stops; words nest in the mind while the beloved feeds on emotional devastation with cold indifference.","opus":"A parasitic relationship with someone emotionally vacant who feeds on the singer, tearing him up with words and nesting in his head where he can't be dislodged. The leech imagery names the one-sided drain of loving a disposable, irrational taker.","sonnet":"A parasitic relationship in which the other person's apathy, fake purity, and emotional predation — 'sink your teeth in me, smother me before you feed' — have taken up permanent residence in the speaker's head, the words tearing them up and nesting inside regardless of the damage done.","src":"haiku"},
"seether~remedy":{"haiku":"Emotional pain and isolation persist despite attempts to distance oneself; the cure is to sever what causes suffering before it kills you.","opus":"A toxic tug-of-war with a lover who doubles as an addiction. Worn out to the bone and ready to die alone, the narrator still pleads the other on while helplessly returning to the very thing that wrecks them both. Clipping the wings that lift you and courting mutual ruin frame the bond as self-feeding damage.","sonnet":"A song about a destructive, codependent attachment the speaker cannot break — the other person brings pain and erosion, yet the speaker keeps returning to that same \"remedy\" even knowing it's killing them, unable to maintain distance or heal.","src":"haiku"},
"seether~rise-above-this":{"haiku":"A broken speaker reaches repeatedly toward healing, determined to overcome damage through will and distance.","opus":"Written by Shaun Morgan for his brother's overdose, a vow to climb out of the same helplessness and mend himself before it claims him too. Addiction's pull answered by a fragile, repeated promise to survive.","sonnet":"A speaker in free fall calls out to someone for grounding, insisting that despite helplessness and collapse they will rise above the doubt and pain before it consumes them. Defiance here is not triumphant but desperate — a repeated declaration against the current that is actively pulling them down.","src":"haiku"},
"seether~wasteland":{"haiku":"Teenage years defined by emotional intensity, loneliness, and feeling unable to help. Neither suffering nor substance use can ease the treadmill of emotional overload.","opus":"A gentle vow to a quiet, damaged companion, the singer insisting he alone understands why they feel so isolated, even while admitting he is overwhelmed and leaning on his own vice to cope. Seether, a South African post-grunge band, borrow the adolescent-wasteland phrase from the Who to color this exhausted, mutually dependent affection with their heavy, tuneful grind.","sonnet":"Two broken people failing to save each other — one soft-spoken and lonely, the other stuck in a kind of purgatory no one else acknowledges. Their shared adolescent wasteland is where feeling too much and needing a chemical crutch to get through the hours are presented as equally valid and equally useless responses to the same emptiness.","src":"haiku"},
"seething-akira~dioxide":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and psychological weaponization create isolation where betrayal compounds physiological collapse into repeated defeat.","opus":"Anxiety and betrayal rendered as a body under attack, breathless as if running a marathon while problems chase like a pathogen. Existing as a dioxide, the poisoned exhale, with a mind weaponized against itself after one betrayal too many.","sonnet":"Anxiety attacks the body like a pathogen, the mind has been turned against itself as a weapon, and a final betrayal tips the speaker into a state of pure, weaponised feeling — \"living as a dioxide\" collapses identity into pure reactive toxicity.","src":"haiku"},
"seething-akira~gravity":{"haiku":"A sudden escape from self-imposed psychological imprisonment brings euphoria and flight; the speaker celebrates liberation while knowing the mood is temporary and collapse inevitable. Mania feels transcendent.","opus":"The UK electronic-metal duo stage a rare good day breaking through years of self-destruction, the mind briefly on its own side as clouds part and gravity loses its grip. Seething Akira pair rap cadence with synth aggression, and the euphoria is knowingly fragile, the narrator already bracing for tomorrow's return to the high tides.","sonnet":"After years of mental confinement, a rare window of euphoria opens and everything feels possible. The narrator savors the lift while half-aware it will not last — tomorrow the tide will pull back down — but today, for once, nothing can hold him to the ground.","src":"haiku"},
"seething-akira~metaphors":{"haiku":"Physical attraction and tension override rational restraint; naked desire displaces politeness and transforms the ordinary into transgression.","opus":"The nervous, electric buildup of attraction — a self-styled gentleman holding back until desire cracks like a bolt from the sky. He drops the figures of speech and the defenses to gamble and get down to it with the person looking back at him.","sonnet":"A song about gathering courage to pursue romantic interest — the speaker is working through resistance and gentlemanly restraint as attraction builds, laying out plans and intentions clearly, feeling the tension of desire that keeps threatening to spill out of whatever polite container they have constructed for it.","src":"haiku"},
"seething-akira~punishment-instructions":{"haiku":"Conformist control and internalized oppression demand rebellion, with refusal the only way to break free from cycles of shame.","opus":"Refusing to keep absorbing punishment, the narrator counts down his sins while trapped in a loop of shame and self-attack. Raising the fingers and shouting a defiant fuck-you is the break from the cycle of saying yes to everything just to make it easy.","sonnet":"About refusing to follow orders from a system or person whose logic is circular and toxic — the narrator is trapped in a feedback loop of shame and bad-faith instructions, and finally hits a point where the only response is to raise a middle finger and shout refusal.","src":"haiku"},
"seething-akira~smile-thief":{"haiku":"A speaker rejects manipulation and cuts ties with a deceiver who has consistently lied and made them sacrificial. The song chronicles resentment toward someone who steals joy through broken promises and one-sided demands.","opus":"An electronic-metal purge of a toxic relationship, cutting ties with someone whose lies and demands drained the narrator to nothing. The fury is exhausted rather than triumphant, the sound of finally choosing yourself after years of appeasing.","sonnet":"A furious breakup or falling-out with someone who drained everything from the narrator while pretending to be on their side — called a smile thief for stealing joy while offering nothing but deception and resentment.","src":"haiku"},
"seeyouspacecowboy~respite-for-a-tragic-tale":{"haiku":"Beauty and destruction intertwine as the speaker accepts the city's violent transformation into spectacle, their tragic narrative written before it even ends.","opus":"A night of excess staged like a stage tragedy, the city and its closing-time regrets buckling everyone into ruin. When the drapes lift they expose a bloody scene lurid enough to become its own headline, decadence served up as an elegant final blow before the lights go dark.","sonnet":"A theatrical night that tips into carnage: a city's destructive beauty drops everyone to their knees, the wardrobe turns blood red, and last call's sorrows give way to a curtain rising on visible wreckage — sirens near, the headline writing itself, a coup de grâce delivered as the light fades.","src":"haiku"},
"seeyouspacecowboy~the-end-to-a-brief-moment-of-lasting-intimacy":{"haiku":"Fleeting connection dies as passion fades into distance; strangers held close briefly become strangers again, passion giving way to silence and regret.","opus":"The dissolution of a doomed intimacy built on lies both partners chose to believe, its passion fading even as the touch still lingers. Blame passes back and forth between two people who both know they won't stay interlocked in the end.","sonnet":"The dissolution of a brief, intense connection between strangers or near-strangers: halos fade, passion drains, and the tension that once \"tasted sweet\" gives way to pacing and wasting time, with both parties bleeding and the song's last images — \"que sera, what's bloomed from lust, our siren's now silent\" — marking the moment intimacy goes quiet.","src":"haiku"},
"sega-bodega~u-got-the-fever":{"haiku":"Emotional toxicity and paranoia poison intimacy; the speaker recognizes their partner as cruel and controlling yet struggles to escape the fever of their grip.","opus":"Finally cutting off a stalking, controlling ex who breaks in and lies about having changed the moment the locks change. Framed as a noir warning, the fever is an obsessive love treated like a sickness best survived by locking the doors.","sonnet":"A savage exit from a toxic relationship: the other person is \"heinous to the bone,\" broke in at 2am, and now claims to have changed only because the locks were changed; the speaker is done, likening the ex's behaviour to a fever — volatile, incurable, best weathered alone behind locked doors.","src":"haiku"},
"seiko-oomori~17":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"A seventeen-year-old girl weaponizing a forbidden crush on a teacher, oscillating between childish demand and sharp manipulation, even spreading the rumor herself. Desire, control and the ache to be taken seriously, all destabilizing and knowing.","sonnet":"A 17-year-old girl deliberately provoking her teacher into a secret affair — she is not a child, she wants dreamy things not textbook things, she spread the rumor herself that the teacher became a teacher for this, and she loves his flustered face, demanding he be hurt by her desire.","src":"haiku"},
"seiko-oomori~kill-the-time-4-you":{"haiku":"Passing time through temporary distraction; killing moments rather than living them.","opus":"Seiko Oomori built her cult standing on unhinged, emotionally maximalist anti-idol pop, and here love overflows past containment. Meeting someone too late, all their battle songs blasting at full volume, the narrator wants to remember every joy and sorrow even beyond death, surviving each day by sharing the same sky and the same flush of color in a cheek after a kiss.","sonnet":"An overwhelming, ecstatic love that feels like too much to contain — meeting someone too late, all their battle songs playing at full volume, wanting to remember every joy and sadness even after death, surviving each day by sharing the same sky and the same cheek-color after a kiss.","src":"haiku"},
"select-all~more":{"haiku":"Masochistic surrender and desire for pain drive intimate exchange; the speaker invites damage and violation, seeking sensation through destruction and violation.","opus":"A feverish plea built on craving and self-surrender. The narrator begs to be held down, broken and given more, blurring pain and pleasure into a single insatiable appetite. Bottled tears, stitched souls and bitten flesh circle an addiction to intensity itself, where enough is never enough and want keeps escalating past reason.","sonnet":"Masochistic desire that feeds on being used — \"stitch up my soul / now break my bones\" is the contradictory ask, and the narrator knows the other person's nighttime rituals (\"a sip of water that's mine\") with intimate surveillance. The hunger is insatiable: every verse ends wanting more damage, more fire.","src":"haiku"},
"select-all~paraphernalia":{"haiku":"Burned into consciousness, love demands surrender despite the pain of inevitable fall.","opus":"Drowning imagery for a mind losing itself in someone who's burned into the brain, sinking beneath cold water while asking whether it's worth falling again. Love here is corrosive, letting bugs eat away as once-safe thoughts turn to pain.","sonnet":"A person sinking beneath water — literally or psychologically — watching their thoughts corrode and change how they experience pain, unsure whether they will let themselves fall for someone again after the last time.","src":"haiku"},
"select-all~she-s-complaining":{"haiku":"Dismissive indifference toward female expression and grievance, the speaker rejecting emotional labor and accountability.","opus":"A wry, contrarian portrait of someone who refuses to be talked out of her own bad habits. Told to stop complaining and change what she can, she cheerfully insists she likes complaining, likes the rain, likes the pain. The push-and-pull between advice and stubborn self-indulgence becomes an oddly defiant embrace of discontent.","sonnet":"A dialogue between a complainer and someone telling her to stop — but the complainer refuses, openly admitting she likes to complain, she likes the rain, she likes the pain. The exchange reveals two people talking past each other while both describe the same situation from inside it.","src":"haiku"},
"selena-gomez~bad-liar":{"haiku":"Desire overtakes resolve; the speaker cannot hide her feelings or convince herself to move on, thoughts consumed by someone she tries to deny.","opus":"The comedy of a crush too big to hide, all the futile attempts to play it cool undone by feelings on fire. Infatuation as a losing battle against your own transparent face.","sonnet":"Despite every effort to suppress her feelings, the speaker cannot stop thinking about or wanting someone, and her body and behaviour betray her at every turn. She knows the desire is mutual and tentatively asks for the fantasy to become real, while admitting she is a terrible liar about not caring.","src":"haiku"},
"semargl~credo-revolution-zardonic-remix":{"haiku":"Religion and moral control sicken the speaker, who urges revolution against the manipulation and sacrifice demanded by priests and preachers.","opus":"A blunt anti-clerical rant recasting organized religion as mind control and calling for revolt against promised afterlives. The rage is aimed at faith as a leash, salvation dangled to keep the obedient sacrificing their one real life.","sonnet":"A direct anti-religious screed arguing that organized religion is a mind-control mechanism that manipulates believers into obedient submission and self-sacrifice through false promises, and that the only valid response is rebellion against it.","src":"haiku"},
"senna~jade":{"haiku":"Burnout and pressure accumulate into paralysis; the speaker deteriorates while chasing perfection, empathy becomes survival tactic, and transformation demands shedding toxicity and self-contempt.","opus":"The strange fear that comes with getting better, where recovery itself feels threatening and the singer would rather hide from the light. Exhausted with themselves, they beg to rise and shine out of self-loathing and misery, half-admitting they keep lying to themselves every day.","sonnet":"A person beginning to get better is terrified of that improvement, hiding from the pressure of rising and shining while admitting they have been lying to themselves every day — exhaustion with their own mind drives the refrain even as getting out of the misery stays the goal.","src":"haiku"},
"senna~rain":{"haiku":"Connection eliminates distance; together abolishes the storm.","opus":"Infatuation so consuming it blocks out everything, even the rain, when the two are together. Synced breathing and colliding stars render an ordinary risk thrilling; love here is the drug that makes the singer feel most alive.","sonnet":"Love as invincibility: when the other person is present, the speaker feels so alive they don't even feel the rain — eyes locking like stars colliding, breathing synchronised — a state of mutual elevation where nothing bad can reach.","src":"haiku"},
"senna~rosehip":{"haiku":"Two people separated by distance maintain connection through darkness and light returning. When the lights go out they find safety together in their bond.","opus":"A romance stretched across distance and a vow of homecoming: the narrator, away far more than he should have been, begs his beloved to wait, swears he is dependable, and names her as inseparable from himself, everything resting on a nighttime moment of shelter. He is desperate merely to feel present, dodging ruin, breaking loose from a confinement neither can escape.","sonnet":"A love that feels like life support — the speaker has been gone too long, knows it, promises to return, and in the dark finds the person still there, still a part of him. The walls can't be escaped together, but when the lights go out they'll be safe. The admission underneath: he's dying just to feel something, and this is what keeps him tethered.","src":"haiku"},
"senses-fail~lush-rimbaugh":{"haiku":"Death brings no catharsis and deserves no sympathy; the speaker renounces apology for a bigot's suffering, declaring hell a fitting destination for those who weaponized harm.","opus":"A gleefully unrepentant curse aimed at a dying bigoted broadcaster, refusing sympathy and consigning him to hell and to being forgotten. It is about the cathartic refusal to forgive someone who built a life on cruelty.","sonnet":"A furious, unrepentant elegy for everyone harmed by a bigot dying on a pedestal: the speaker admits a flicker of sympathy before withdrawing it entirely, and condemns a lifetime of proselytising hatred and body-shaming with the unambiguous conclusion that there is a special place in hell already booked.","src":"haiku"},
"sentinel-beast~corpse":{"haiku":"Death stalks the narrator through dangerous terrain as corpses and decomposition mark the landscape of peril. Survival remains uncertain while darkness closes in and escape grows impossible.","opus":"A moonlit horror crawl through a lethal stretch of ground where something rotting stalks the living. The final twist folds the narrator into the thing he's describing — the hunter revealed as the shot-through carcass all along.","sonnet":"Two people move through a full-moon danger zone pursued by something undead, trying not to stray and not to be left alone — by the end the speaker realises they themselves have become the decaying corpse with a bullet through them, the screams now inside their own skull.","src":"haiku"},
"sentinel-beast~depths-of-death":{"haiku":"Drowning in liquid darkness transforms into a metaphor for psychological descent and inescapable doom. The depths claim all resistance and breath, leaving only the void and slow asphyxiation.","opus":"Death imagined as a drowning descent, the depths calling you down where there's no air, no light, and no return. It dwells on the suffocating point of no return as the body sinks and stays.","sonnet":"Drowning as a one-way descent — reaching the bottom means knowing the Depths, and the Depths keep you: no air, no light, trapped on the floor forever, the only answer to how far you can go being that you never come back.","src":"haiku"},
"sentinel-beast~dogs-of-war":{"haiku":"Martial hunger and conquest drive unstoppable forces across enemy territory in service of violence and domination. The song celebrates warfare with merciless indifference to suffering and civilian casualty.","opus":"Combat as bloodlust and sport, soldiers styled as dogs of war who burn, crush, and kill everything to win. War is stripped to its rawest creed of do or die and no mercy.","sonnet":"Soldiers who know only combat push through smoke and burning hills in a final assault, framing war as the sole mode of existence — riding the forces of sin, killing everything that moves, the dogs of war march because fighting is the only thing they know how to do.","src":"haiku"},
"sentinel-beast~evil-is-the-night":{"haiku":"Darkness becomes an entity that binds and owns all who venture into its domain through fear and invisible control. The night grants power to malevolent forces that remain unknowable and unstoppable.","opus":"An unseen malevolence that surrounds and grips you in the dark, taunting that your sword and battle cry are useless. Personified night claims you belong to it, an evil you cannot see, fight, or escape.","sonnet":"Darkness speaks as a predator who cannot be killed or seen — it commands the listener to lay down their sword, surrender to its grip, and accept that they already belong to it, repeating \"evil is the night\" as both fact and law.","src":"haiku"},
"sentinel-beast~phantom-of-the-opera":{"haiku":"Obsessive terror and psychological manipulation haunt as the phantom exerts hypnotic control over body and mind. The song captures how seduction and fear merge when power imbalances enable sustained abuse.","opus":"A cover that recasts the masked figure as a relentless hunter stalking the singer even into sleep, wrecking her thoughts and spirit. The pursuit is futile, the prey entranced and defenseless against a tormentor concealed behind a deceptive disguise.","sonnet":"A phantom long hidden behind a false mask is finally confronted — the speaker is simultaneously terrified and mesmerised by the creature's cat call, warning themselves and the listener to keep distance even as the phantom haunts, taunts, and tortures from its lair.","src":"haiku"},
"sentinel-beast~revenge":{"haiku":"Internalized rage and resentment fester as the narrator contemplates breaking free from social judgment and internal defeat. The shift from hiding to standing begins when the decision to act replaces passive suffering.","opus":"An outsider walking alone under hostile stares, nursing hidden hatred that nobody around sees. The buried rage finally builds into a stand, the days of hiding numbered as the promise of revenge takes hold.","sonnet":"Walking streets alone under hostile stares, a person conceals growing hatred and plans retaliation — the struggle has moved from hiding to taking a stand, and the fantasy of revenge is the only thing that makes the outside world bearable.","src":"haiku"},
"sentinel-beast~sentinel-beast":{"haiku":"Death's three-headed guardian marks the boundary between worlds as inevitable mortality approaches. The song frames the reaper as both familiar and terrifying, inescapable and always watching.","opus":"A mythic descent into the underworld guarded by Cerberus, where the three-headed beast toys with a trespasser before the fatal attack. Time runs short, fear becomes real, and the intruder dies believing too late it might have been a nightmare.","sonnet":"Cerberus, the three-headed guardian of the underworld, is evoked as an unstoppable sentinel who waits in every shadow — the unlucky traveller who seeks the underworld faces the sands running out, the beast lunging, and darkness falling without mercy over the life that is lost.","src":"haiku"},
"sentinel-beast~the-keeper":{"haiku":"An underworld ruler wields mystical power to destroy and consume all resistance through burning and merciless violence. The song celebrates a dark authority figure who strips victims of agency and life.","opus":"A dread portrait of the underworld's keeper, a hot-tempered, murderous figure who burns, loots, and kills with mystical power and a love of slaughter. Cross his path and you pay in blood; he waits by the light to strike.","sonnet":"A mythic destroyer rules the underworld with fire-sending eyes and a mind that condemns — he loots, kills, and burns indiscriminately from the ceiling to the floor, and the refrain cataloguing his looting, burning, raping, and stabbing underscores total and amoral domination.","src":"haiku"},
"sentinels~embers":{"haiku":"Suppressed desire and shame ignite obsession; the speaker confesses their hidden truth and cowardice, burning through decency to feed an appetite they cannot control.","opus":"A confession of surrendering to a consuming desire the speaker cannot control, calling himself a coward who cut his ties and hid the truth. Burning becomes purging as he gives in to the obsession he is ashamed of.","sonnet":"A confession of suppressed obsession: a voice creeps in telling the speaker to give in, desire takes hold and makes the mind its home, and he descends willingly into darkness — \"this is what I want, my obsession\" — burning for a truth he kept hidden from himself, purging through fire.","src":"haiku"},
"sentinels~epitaph":{"haiku":"Truth burns destructively and isolation compounds devastation; the speaker confronts fundamental meaninglessness beneath social performance.","opus":"A hardcore condemnation of a hollow individual and the revulsion they trail behind them, a scorching truth that whatever surrounds this person withers and perishes. Pure outward loathing delivered with total scorn.","sonnet":"A speaker turns to their maker with a furious accusation — look at what you created, left disgraced — before declaring that everything around their tormentor dies, a brief epitaph of devastation with no redemption.","src":"haiku"},
"sentinels~inertia":{"haiku":"Mental paralysis and self-doubt trap the speaker in stasis; they watch their life from behind glass, suffocated by their own inability to act or believe in change.","opus":"Weighed down by relentless doubt and disgust with himself, the speaker sees his existence dwindling to little more than vapor and pleads for calm, anchoring his identity in nothing but resolve and momentum. It concerns spiritual stalling and the choice to keep enduring instead of surrendering to permanent rest.","sonnet":"A speaker paralysed by depression watches from the other side of the glass as time and dreams waste away, choking on the weight of it; the turn comes in a declaration of difference and will — \"by will and drive I will be defined\" — though the self-respect has already been lost and peace remains elusive.","src":"haiku"},
"seotaiji~heffy-end":{"haiku":"A person pursues an idealized lover obsessively despite rejection, imagining a perfect sealed world where they can build a clean happy ending together.","opus":"an obsessive, possessive love that curdles into stalking and a desire to possess the beloved in death","sonnet":"obsessive, possessive love for someone who ignores the narrator, culminating in a fantasy of isolating together in a walled-off world","src":"haiku"},
"sepandapanda~open-your-eyes":{"haiku":"Humanity holds interconnected power through biology and collective solidarity; what you do to others reverberates through the whole system.","opus":"A plea to recognize your own worth and shared humanity, bilingually insisting that what you do to others you do to yourself. The togetherness of hand in hand pushes against how small and alone people too often feel.","sonnet":"A call for mutual recognition and shared humanity, mixing German and English: what you do to me you do to yourself, what I do to you I do to myself, and if you open your eyes you will see that billions of cells connect us all — you and I, together.","src":"haiku"},
"separate-ways~freetime":{"haiku":"Ambivalence about a brief romantic connection and the desire to rewind time. The speaker questions whether they rushed in and contemplates leaving or staying suspended in the moment.","opus":"The song hangs in the quiet late hours of a romance that maybe moved too quickly, its singer confessing a weakness for surrender while doubting whether these nights are just squandered. The looping wish to turn the clock back and the pull between staying over or walking off keep the narrator frozen, unable to fully choose the bond or the door.","sonnet":"Two people hover at the edge of commitment, replaying the question of whether what they share was too rushed or exactly right. The longing to rewind and start cleanly sits alongside the pull to simply stay another night. Nothing resolves — the whole song is suspended in that uncertain, warm in-between.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~ali":{"haiku":"A champion rises against oppression and hatred through defiant resistance, never surrendering despite the physical and psychological weight of racism and adversity bearing down.","opus":"A tribute to Muhammad Ali, tracing how he refused to kill in a foreign war, was punished by a racist system, and stood defiant in faith and sickness as a lonely champion for his people.","sonnet":"A tribute to Muhammad Ali as a figure of principled resistance — refusing to kill in foreign lands only to be beaten by racist hands at home — who carried the weight of faith and defiance all the way to the end, lighting a path for those who followed.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~arise":{"haiku":"Humanity faces apocalyptic annihilation and warfare; survivors seek salvation while dealing with war crimes, homelessness, and the cycle of violence and death.","opus":"A vision of the human race wiped out by war and religious madness beneath a ruined sky, cities collapsing while survivors search for meaning that never comes. Yet from the wreckage the song insists on rising again, a defiant note that helped make the Brazilian thrashers a global force in early-nineties extreme metal.","sonnet":"Apocalypse stripped to its bones — cities falling, mankind obliterated under a pale grey sky, the speaker having done nothing and seen nothing until the end arrives anyway. War, terrorism, fatality, ashes to dust. The one defiant phrase is the refrain: we shall arise, though from what and toward what goes unanswered. The song doesn't offer salvation, just the will to stand after everything falls.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~attitude":{"haiku":"The song urges defiance against conformity and systems of control, demanding self-determination and respect through refusal to submit.","opus":"Sepultura's call to survive a hostile world on your own terms, rejecting what you were taught and demanding respect earned through scars. Roots-era defiance forged from the reality of the Brazilian streets.","sonnet":"A brutal instruction manual for surviving a world that doesn't care whether you live or die — kill your pride, give blood, give pain, demand respect, and never confuse the world as it is with the paradise you were promised.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~beneath-the-remains":{"haiku":"A survivor of nuclear war confronts the brutality, ignorance, and futility of mass destruction leveled against civilian homes.","opus":"An anti-war lament voiced by a civilian trapped in a nuclear conflict he had no part in starting, his familiar surroundings now killing fields strewn with the mutilated. The deeper point is total futility, no meaningful victor or reason, hope for anything better dismissed as fantasy while one crushed person stands against a whole hostile world.","sonnet":"Survival guilt and horror in the aftermath of nuclear or total war — the narrator wasn't responsible for starting it but is left among the ruins, the minefields, the mass death, having lost all hope that any side won anything or that the future holds anything but further destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~biotech-is-godzilla":{"haiku":"Corporate biotech companies plunder indigenous lands and genetic resources for profit while hiding the dangers behind promises of medicine and food. Technology in greedy hands breeds new catastrophes.","opus":"A furious protest against biotechnology and corporate greed after the 1992 Rio Earth Summit, warning that gene-patenting, lab mutations, and pollution (Cubatão) endanger natives and the planet. The core argument is that technology isn't inherently evil, it's monstrous in the wrong, profit-mad corporate hands.","sonnet":"A direct political indictment of biotechnology corporations stripping the Amazon of genetic material for profit while indigenous people get nothing, connecting lab-born mutations and corporate pollution to real disasters like Cubatão's environmental horror — the conclusion is that technology in the wrong hands is a monster.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~born-stubborn":{"haiku":"Someone is born with defiant refusal to obey authority or follow rules, and this stubborn independence becomes their identity. They claim their tribe and roots which cannot be broken apart.","opus":"The Brazilian metal band build an anthem of flat refusal, taking orders from no one and smashing whatever cage is meant to hold them in. Claiming membership in a self-defined tribe, the narrator insists these bonds can never be severed, recasting hardship as the very thing that toughens the collective into rising rather than yielding.","sonnet":"A declaration of collective refusal — this community was born in pain, obeys no authority, and has built its identity around refusing to be contained. The band name itself becomes a rallying point; roots persist against anyone who tries to sever them, and the tribe's solidarity is the only allegiance that holds.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~breed-apart":{"haiku":"People are urged to think freely and resist forced conformity, choosing their own path over coercion.","opus":"A blunt refusal to be molded by rigid convention and imposed rules, using a decayed cityscape as backdrop for a chant urging the listener to think independently and follow their own path. The Brazilian thrashers turn nonconformity into a rhythmic battering ram on their 1991 album Arise.","sonnet":"A raw call for mental independence in an urban landscape of concrete and dust — the speaker feels hemmed in by received ideas and coercive social norms that want to grind nonconformists down. The solution offered is simple and repeated like a mantra: open your mind, go your own way. The brevity is the argument; there is nothing more to explain.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~dead-embryonic-cells":{"haiku":"Life is inherently painful from birth through a world of violence and suffering, with no hope for healing as technology and hatred dominate.","opus":"A bleak indictment of a world sickened by technology, terrorism and racial war, the narrator born into anger and sorrow with no hope of cure, humanity condemned to an emptiness that ends it all. Sepultura, Brazil's thrash-metal titans, cut this for their 1991 breakthrough Arise, its apocalyptic despair and precision riffing marking the band's rise from São Paulo underground to global extreme-metal prominence.","sonnet":"A birth-into-damnation worldview — the speaker did not choose to enter a world defined by tribal violence, technological disease, and irreversible environmental ruin. Humanity poisons itself from the inside out, and the song sees no cure available: the cycle closes with the same death it opened with, born in pain and ending in extinction.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~dusted":{"haiku":"A person is silenced and deceived by someone who controls them and hides truth, forced into complicity while their name and voice are destroyed.","opus":"The Brazilian metal veterans aim outward at betrayal and enforced silence, the narrator accused of a farce while his own name is killed and his mouth sewn shut for trying to cooperate. Torn between blaming himself and blaming the world for his mistakes, he circles the same disgrace inside out, the repeated title landing like a curse for being wronged, discarded and told simply to be left alone.","sonnet":"Someone silenced and slandered by another's lies feels their identity dissolving under the pressure of fabricated guilt. The cycle of self-blame versus world-blame runs endlessly inward, leaving nothing but a muted rage and the hollow word — dusted — repeated like the only verdict that remains.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~impending-doom":{"haiku":"Humanity's parasitic cruelty and self-destruction are inevitable; the narrator watches civilisation spiral toward annihilation as our own worst impulses consume us.","opus":"A furious lament for the species itself, indicting humanity as a self-consuming machine that drains everything within reach; the repeated cry to brother and mother turns ecological ruin into a family tragedy no one can disown.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of humanity's self-destruction: the species crawls through history draining life from everything it touches, leaving behind wasteland while asking in disbelief what it has done — the verdict is that suffering and killing are simply mankind's nature.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~inner-self":{"haiku":"A nonconformist refuses societal rules and judgment, walking against scorn with defiant self-determination and inner conviction.","opus":"An early landmark of the Brazilian band's thrash years, a defiant creed of walking a hostile world alone, refusing its rules and trusting only oneself. Amid betrayal and deceit, the narrator presses on toward faint hope, treating his own individuality as armor against envy and oppression, nonconformity hardened into a vow to outlast everyone who wrongs him.","sonnet":"Nonconformity as a survival mechanism — walking streets loaded with hatred and scorn, the narrator refuses external rules and internal revision. Betrayal by life itself is acknowledged but not allowed to stop movement. Personality becomes the weapon, and the inner self remains the only territory that cannot be colonised.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~lookaway":{"haiku":"Desire conflicts with truth, examining what makes something attractive enough to lie and sacrifice for.","opus":"The band examines a craving that both repels and controls, an appetite so consuming a person might commit violence or deceit to satisfy it, dissected repeatedly until it turns sickening. Pleas to indulge, conceal, and be reimbursed hint at desire tangled with shame, and a closing wordless glance sums up a compulsion neither side can fully own.","sonnet":"A queasy, compulsive desire that the speaker cannot reconcile or suppress — something he returns to with revulsion yet cannot quit, oscillating between self-disgust and helpless craving, culminating in a moment of mutual, averted eye contact that speaks more than the words do.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~phantom-self":{"haiku":"Loss of identity following a traumatic event, as the person becomes someone else entirely and must confront this transformed self.","opus":"A survivor of a sudden violent wreck grapples with memory loss, injury, and a self he no longer recognizes, tormented by flashes of the crash. Feeling he's become a stranger to himself, he must reckon with this altered identity while chasing a version of who he was that no longer exists.","sonnet":"A survivor of a traumatic crash — described in fragmented flashbacks of carnage and blood — struggles with the loss of his former identity, haunted daily by the person he was before the accident destroyed his sense of self.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~ratamahatta":{"haiku":"An invocation of Brazilian and Caribbean resistance figures—Zumbi, Lampião, and folk horror icon Zé do Caixão—set against imagery of slums and urban streets across uptown, downtown, and trench town. A confrontational, rhythmic anthem rooted in marginal communities and outlaw culture.","opus":"A rhythmic tribute to Brazilian life and folklore, name-dropping figures like Ze do Caixao, Zumbi and Lampiao and chanting images of favelas, garages and shacks. It greets uptown and downtown alike in a percussive celebration of place and heritage.","sonnet":"A chant-driven song built from Brazilian slang for favela life and references to iconic Brazilian folk figures — Zé do Caixão, Zumbi, Lampião — alongside percussive vocal patterns. It functions as a rhythmic celebration of Brazilian street culture and underground identity rather than a narrative song.","src":"haiku","fable":"Ratamahatta is built to defeat translation on purpose, and that refusal is the argument. The first verse is a tumble of favela argot — garage, shack, slum, backstreet — shuffled and reshuffled in every order, less sentences than a rhythm section made of words, so that meaning dissolves into pure Brazilian percussion the moment a non-native ear reaches for it. The title itself and the entire chorus are nonsense syllables, hatta mahatta ttattatta, a chant that means nothing precisely so it can mean street, drum, and detonation all at once. Against that dissolving ground the second verse does the one legible thing in the song: it names three national outlaw-icons on repeat — Zé do Caixão the horror auteur, Zumbi the escaped-slave rebel who led Palmares, Lampião the cangaço bandit — a lineage of Brazilian defiance smuggled in where a foreign listener can only hear drums, which is the whole trick of Roots-era Cavalera, planting a domestic ancestry and a global groove in the same phrase. And when the mock-tourist greeting hellos its way from uptown to trench town, the joke turns: the export-ready English is the empty part, and the untranslatable Portuguese is where the country actually lives.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["politics & society","identity & becoming","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"a domestic ancestry smuggled inside nonsense syllables only a foreign ear mishears","built":"the one legible verse names outlaw-icons where the non-native listener can only hear drums"},
"sepultura~ratamahatta-2017-remaster":{"haiku":"Brazilian cultural identity and street vernacular celebrate community resilience through rhythmic incantation.","opus":"A percussive celebration of Brazilian identity, chanting slum and shanty words alongside folk figures like Zumbi and Lampião. Written with Carlinhos Brown, it greets the whole city top to bottom, planting favela culture defiantly on the global map.","sonnet":"Brazilian slang for poverty and the streets — biboca, favela, maloca — collides with iconic outlaw and horror figures (Zumbi, Lampião, Zé do Caixão) in a percussive, carnivalesque celebration of Brazilian underground resistance culture. (lang: pt)","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~refuse-resist":{"haiku":"Streets erupt into chaos—tanks, riots, bleeding crowds, and urban warfare—prompting a call to refuse complicity and resist the state's violent siege.","opus":"A snapshot of violent civil uprising: armored vehicles in the streets, chaos, and a population in revolt against a manufactured conflict within the state. The rallying refrain urges defiance, warning that staying quiet is fatal and that the panic within oneself is the real enemy to overcome.","sonnet":"A snapshot of urban uprising — tanks in streets, crowds burning cars, police confrontations — presented as the inevitable boiling point of state violence and political oppression.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~roots-bloody-roots":{"haiku":"The speaker celebrates authentic identity and primal strength, refusing to abandon their nature in the face of external pressure and finding power in remaining true to their roots.","opus":"A primal anthem of reclaiming ancestral identity and strength, the chant of bloody roots asserting a people growing stronger and refusing to fake who they are. It's a defiant demand for freedom rooted in origin rather than assimilation.","sonnet":"A declaration of identity rooted in origin and heritage — the speaker insists on growing stronger through connection to where they came from rather than faking or abandoning that foundation, framing it as collective survival and pride.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~sepultura-ratamahatta":{"haiku":"Brazilian streets and favelas conjure spirits of resistance and wild exuberance; the battle cry echoes defiant joy.","opus":"A percussive celebration of Brazilian identity, chanting slum and shanty words alongside folk figures like Zumbi and Lampião. Written with Carlinhos Brown, it greets the whole city top to bottom, planting favela culture defiantly on the global map.","sonnet":"A percussive roll-call of Brazilian slum geography and cultural icons — favelas, Zé do Caixão, Zumbi, Lampião — fusing street address with mythic Brazilian figures in a chaotic, celebratory shout.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~slave-new-world":{"haiku":"A society enforces censorship and subjugation, keeping people enslaved through control and violence. The narrator refuses captivity and demands liberation from oppression.","opus":"Sepultura issue a rallying cry against censorship, bigotry and rule by shadowy powers who keep people subdued. Invoking peoples once at liberty and later shackled into servitude, it urges listeners to confront the oppressor that also lurks within, to master their own minds, and to assert their freedom.","sonnet":"A call for freedom from the internal and external forces of censorship, prejudice, and social control — the speaker insists that despite historical chains and ongoing suppression, they refuse the identity of slave.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~spit":{"haiku":"Defiant refusal to engage; contempt and solitude as weapons against intrusion.","opus":"A blunt kiss-off to hatred and hypocrites: play out your sham and your reckoning will arrive, but leave me be. Sepultura channels pure defiance, answering provocation with a gob in the eye rather than a reply.","sonnet":"A short, aggressive rejection of unwanted interference — someone is living a farce and their karma will catch them, but the narrator wants nothing to do with the interrogation; it's a defiant \"leave me alone\" with the threat of literal spitting as punctuation.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~spit-2017-remaster":{"haiku":"The speaker refuses to engage with hypocrisy and betrayal; they reject questions and relationships built on false ground.","opus":"A furious snarl aimed at hateful, duplicitous people, telling them to keep up their charade and warning that consequences will catch up to them. Refusing to explain himself or deceive, the narrator lashes back with pure venom and demands to be left in peace, his defiance likened to a wound that never stops weeping. Sepultura drive it with blunt, relentless thrash aggression.","sonnet":"A raw, minimal confrontation with a hypocrite or enemy — the speaker has nothing to say to them, refuses questions, and the only gesture left is contempt: spitting in their face.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~straighthate":{"haiku":"A hateful, hardened person acknowledges their nature while defending their pain and cynicism against judgment.","opus":"A confrontational self-portrait of someone consumed by hate they suspect is simply their nature, dismissed as negative and told they're broken, hardened by ghetto upbringing and refusing sympathy. Sepultura, the Brazilian metal institution, channel their Chaos A.D. era shift toward groove and hardcore aggression here, the blunt refrain turning alienation and street-forged pain into a defiant separation from everyone else.","sonnet":"An unflinching account of growing up in conditions that made hatred feel like self-preservation — the speaker does not romanticize the ghetto but insists it forged something real in him that comfortable critics cannot grasp. The anger is not performed; it is structural, layered into his circuits by experience, and he refuses to dress it up or apologize for what it made him.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~tainted-love":{"haiku":"Heartbreak and betrayal in a broken relationship where love has turned toxic, and the narrator chooses to flee rather than endure the pain.","opus":"The Brazilian band's cover keeps the original's plea intact: a lover fleeing a relationship that only wounds him, sleepless and drained, reversing course from pursuit to escape. He offers grief instead of devotion and begs to be spared affection that has curdled into something poisonous.","sonnet":"A man finally runs from a relationship that has drained him dry — giving everything, receiving pain in return, unable to sleep for the damage done. The love that once drew him close has curdled into something he must flee. The exhaustion is total; even the tears he offers are not enough to satisfy it.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~territory":{"haiku":"War is waged over land through propaganda and poor information that manipulates anger and pride. The song condemns territorial conflict and the racist ideology it breeds.","opus":"Sepultura indict warmongering rulers who bend populations with propaganda, drip-feeding distortion to steer public rage and manufacture killers. Its bleakest stroke is a father raising his son to trust a racist demagogue, a legacy that leaves nothing behind but disgrace and remorse for pride thrown away.","sonnet":"War and territorial control are driven not by genuine necessity but by propaganda, racist ideology, and manufactured anger — the song indicts the dictators and their deceptive speeches while acknowledging that ordinary people pass these poisons down to their own children.","src":"haiku"},
"sepultura~troops-of-doom":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic destruction unfolds with antichrist forces, nuclear war, and the end of mankind. Prophets predicted this doom while the world is buried and forgotten, death reigning supreme.","opus":"An apocalyptic vision of demonic legions marching to damn humanity amid darkness, poison skies, and atomic war. It's a blunt end-times scenario in which mankind is butchered and forgotten, with prophecy foretelling only the final victory of death.","sonnet":"Nuclear apocalypse framed as theological — Antichrist soldiers execute souls, sulfur clouds the air, the Messiah is chained in Hades, and prophets watch mankind be buried and forgotten. It's early Sepultura doomsday imagery at its most raw.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~baby":{"haiku":"The speaker addresses an elusive figure from dreams, exploring themes of cosmic delusion and the confusion between appearance and reality in love.","opus":"Tankian folds a tender, aching love song into cosmic and apocalyptic imagery, the sweetness of missing someone colliding with a lover who unsettles him as the world tips toward its end; longing and the wish to be left alone at once.","sonnet":"A longing for someone encountered in a dream — while the world edges toward apocalyptic chaos and words lose their meaning — is undercut by a recurring demand to be left alone, suggesting the relationship both consoles and disturbs.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~beatus":{"haiku":"The singer has become obsessed with and addicted to another person, unable to eat, think, or live without them occupying his mind and suffocating him in silence.","opus":"A feverish account of obsessive love bordering on affliction, memories tangling into one woman's face until the whole city wears it, the narrator strangled by silence and aloneness, reaching toward the sun just to hear a voice. Serj Tankian, the System of a Down frontman, works in his solo-album mode here, his operatic phrasing and surreal, translated-feeling imagery turning romantic fixation into something haunted and unmoored.","sonnet":"Total romantic obsession that has colonized ordinary life — eating, looking at a city, waking up in the morning all route back to one person, and the speaker cannot decide whether this is beautiful or suffocating. The fear of losing them again runs underneath everything, and the song cycles between the warmth of their touch and the strangling silence of being alone.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~beethoven-s-c":{"haiku":"A song about love, time, and civilization's moral collapse. The speaker condemns the commercialization of love and confronts the listener about their blindness and abandonment, all while walking through dead landscapes seeking connection.","opus":"An abstract lament on love, time, and mortality, contrasting genuine affection against greed for profit and fame while civilization stands on trial and the narrator, left alone and going blind, feels the clock running out.","sonnet":"A fractured meditation on love, betrayal, and civilisational collapse — the narrator insists love is not about profit or riches, while imagery of clocks failing and civilisation on trial suggests a world and a personal relationship both crumbling from dishonesty.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~borders-are":{"haiku":"Political borders are artificial lines fueled by fear and national pride, separating humanity while leaders dance as the world drowns in rising consequences.","opus":"An anti-nationalist protest: national borders are framed as gallows built from collective ego, arbitrary lines drawn in sand and water, and fear is named as the real engine of separation, stoked by condemnation and blood-soaked propaganda. The rising-water refrain suggests that as kings and gods die and people celebrate, catastrophe still creeps up on everyone equally.","sonnet":"A polemic against nationalism, arguing that national borders are instruments of fear and ego that divide humanity rather than protect it — a song for freedom from the tribal logic that keeps people separated and cowering as larger crises loom.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~butterfly":{"haiku":"The song laments how societal inhibitions, submission, and repetition sacrifice individuals and destroy their spiritual potential, using the butterfly as a symbol of transformation from suppression to freedom. It calls for breaking free from collective conditioning.","opus":"A dense, surreal protest against submission, self-imposed inhibition, and a world that sacrifices itself through suspicion and repetition, calling for love and eco-centric change over autocracy while refusing to be crucified or terrified.","sonnet":"The song protests collective self-destruction through fear and repetition — humanity trapped by its own inhibitions and suspicions — while the image of a moth becoming a butterfly stands for a beauty and transformation that shouldn't be falsified, paired with a call to abandon autocracy for more ecologically aware ways of being.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~ching-chime":{"haiku":"Art and music are devalued in commercial culture where no one pays for meaning. The song critiques the hollowness of manufactured success and celebrity death.","opus":"A dense, punning rant about art being cheapened by an industry where creativity goes unrewarded and machine-corrected, stacking absurd rhymes to mock the rot. A gentler recurring refrain breaks the cynicism with reassurance that even crushed hopes can recover, a tender counterweight to the bile.","sonnet":"A fractured stream-of-consciousness about creative labour that goes uncompensated and unnoticed — layered over nonsense syllables and pop-culture noise — with a contrasting refrain urging the listener to open their eyes and let fear melt away.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~cornucopia":{"haiku":"Environmental destruction driven by ignorance and lust, where humanity exploits nature without understanding the consequences.","opus":"An eco-conscious lament weaving a strange love plea with images of humanity raping the Earth and reaping violent storms, hurricanes, and tsunamis as consequence. It muses on why people disfigure beautiful things while still hoping for extraordinary connection.","sonnet":"A song about humanity's destructive relationship with the natural world: we rape and exploit the Earth without understanding why it suffers, surrounded by escalating ecological disasters — hurricanes, tornadoes, tsunamis — while the speaker mourns a broken pact and wonders why we disfigure what is beautiful.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tankian smuggles an ecological reckoning inside a doomed love song, and the disguise is the argument: the sunshine-lover who foams at the mouth and vanishes is the planet itself, wooed and decapitated in one gesture. Cornucopia, the horn of plenty, has its head lopped off, so abundance turns out to be something we slaughter rather than accept, and the storm-chorus of hurricanes, twisters and tsunamis reads less as retribution than as a convulsing world while we plead ignorance of the wound we opened. The verses coarsen deliberately on the repeat, refusing pastoral consolation and forcing the assault to be spoken outright even as the tune stays gentle. The pivot is the bridge, which drops the surreal weather for blunt self-address: the smallest scale and the largest are one and the same, we author our own catastrophes, and the obscenity is that we know ourselves capable of making dreams real yet deface the beautiful regardless. That closing vow to marry, folded back into the shattered pact and the flight to somewhere else, welds love to ruin, so devotion and wreckage become impossible to tell apart.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nature & the elements","politics & society","love & desire"],"means":"devotion and ecological wreckage made indistinguishable","built":"the gentle tune holds while the verses deliberately coarsen on repeat"},
"serj-tankian~deafening-silence":{"haiku":"Obvious truths and meanings go unspoken and ignored, creating a maddening silence that the narrator attempts to paint and interpret while watching things disappear.","opus":"Away from System of a Down, Tankian sings of a silence that roars in the ears while an obvious truth is ignored and a ghostly she appears for a recurring ceremony. His solo theatricality suits the imagery of painting someone with tears as time keeps meddling, the whole a haunted meditation on knowing exactly who another person is and is not.","sonnet":"A man watches a woman appear and vanish in ritual repetition, haunted by what is never spoken. The loudest truth in the room is the one nobody names, and grief seeps out sideways — through tears, trembling thoughts, and the ceremonies that substitute for honesty.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~deserving":{"haiku":"Two people believe they've achieved a true love together despite the narrator's self-doubt about deserving it, ultimately choosing faith in their bond.","opus":"Serj Tankian, working solo apart from System of a Down, wrestles with feeling unworthy of love. He insists he doesn't merit his partner and warns he'll let them down, bewildered by their steady faith in him. Then he swings toward belief, pledging protection and trust, self-doubt and devotion tugging against each other.","sonnet":"He insists he is unworthy and will abandon the relationship, yet cannot leave because the other person keeps choosing faith in love over self-preservation. The contradiction resolves into something tentative and real: they have built something visible and worth following into the unknown together.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~disowned-inc":{"haiku":"Religious fragmentation and imposed belief systems; gods wear different names in different languages, yet the same illusions dissolve into divine distance, leaving the singer disowned and alone.","opus":"Serj Tankian on faith, war and the crimes committed in God's many names, aphorisms about time and resistance framing a plea against violence. Beneath the cosmic language is intimate rescue, one person pulled from despair by another's love.","sonnet":"A song about the loneliness of religious disillusionment — the narrator feels that God's name differs in every language and what was sold as devotion is imposed illusion; the personal anchor of faith was removed and left him feeling abandoned and disowned.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~elasticity":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects betrayal and sacrifice, vowing resistance against manipulation; sorcerers turn night to day through violence and the weak are never trampled.","opus":"Tankian's cartoonish, burp-punctuated vaudeville hides a firm no underneath, an announcement that he refuses to be double-crossed, refuses to plead, refuses to give up his time. The gibberish theatrics become his way of brushing off someone trying to work him.","sonnet":"Surrealist and deliberately fractured — the sane thread is a refusal to be controlled or betrayed by someone (lover, authority, or both), interwoven with images of blood-soaked sorcery, revenge, and the rejection of false piety.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~elect-the-dead":{"haiku":"The speaker desires self-love above all else, eventually preferring death over giving themselves to others.","opus":"A bleak meditation where every outside comfort, a lover, love itself, dissolves back into a refrain of wanting nothing but the self. The turn comes at the thought of death, which finds everyone alive: only mortality cracks that self-absorption open, and the closing wish shifts from wanting only himself to wanting another person.","sonnet":"A sparse meditation on the pull between selfishness and love — the speaker cycles between wanting only himself and then suddenly only the other person, with death arriving as a clarifying threshold that shifts desire from ego to connection.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~electric-yerevan":{"haiku":"Urban uprising and political awakening; electricity of protest and change.","opus":"A protest anthem drawn straight from Armenia's 2015 Electric Yerevan demonstrations against an electricity price hike, rejecting corruption, plunder, water-cannon crackdowns and being anyone's pawn between superpowers. Serj Tankian, the Armenian-American System of a Down frontman and lifelong activist, turns the sit-in into rallying music, folding in reportage-style spoken word that frames the unrest as economic rather than a foreign-driven color revolution.","sonnet":"A protest song grounded in the 2015 Yerevan electricity price demonstrations — foreign monopolies overcharging, police water cannons watering the seeds of discontent, civil disobedience working its slow logic. The chorus names what the crowd actually wants: not geopolitics, not colour revolutions, just affordable power and an end to oligarchic plunder. Yerevan is the word repeated like a chant because the city itself is the subject.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~electron":{"haiku":"Spiritual harmony emerges from following cosmic forces and ancient wisdom, transcending conflict as humanity breathes in unity with all living things.","opus":"Serj Tankian spins a mystical, cosmic meditation on unity and transcendence, folding in I Ching imagery and the idea of a spirit animating all living things. Following natural cycles toward a sense of oneness, the song repeatedly presses one question, what is it we truly believe in, and searches for a spiritual path that might help humanity endure.","sonnet":"A cosmic, spiritual meditation on the unity of all things — from subatomic particles to stars — asking what it is that binds existence together and celebrating the shared breath and spirit running through everything.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~empty-walls":{"haiku":"The song portrays bodies burning with yearning and choking on toxins while isolated behind empty walls, evoking imprisonment or confinement. A voice taunts the imprisoned, ending with the admission that the speaker killed the listener's family and walled them away.","opus":"An anti-war lament about bodies burning and people dying, using the imagery of empty walls that hide and imprison; a late line about loving someone before they killed the speaker's family points to the human cost of violence and destruction.","sonnet":"A speaker confronts someone hiding behind indifference while others suffer and die, and ends by revealing the personal stake: the person behind those empty walls once killed the narrator's family.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~feed-us":{"haiku":"Leaders deceiving and disposing of their people with lies; the dying world as humanity grows spiritually numb to the destruction.","opus":"Tankian's furious accounting of how power keeps people docile with lies while the planet dies. The feeding metaphor casts leaders as parasites who use us up and delete us the moment we are inconvenient.","sonnet":"The song accuses an unnamed authority of feeding people lies, exploiting them when useful and discarding them when not, while warning that the world is dying and the chance to save it has already been squandered through deception.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~figure-it-out":{"haiku":"Corporate domination and systemic oppression; CEOs are disease, technology enslaves, materialism corrupts freedom, and personal agency is an obstruction to industrial control.","opus":"Tankian's post-System solo fury aimed squarely at corporate power, framing executives as the actual pathology and consumer technology as the anesthetic that trades personal freedom for materialism. It's a demand to stop pretending we don't already see the machinery.","sonnet":"A song about corporate and technological systems that rape personal freedom in the name of materialism — CEOs are identified as the disease, industrial society as nature's enemy, and the call is to stop pretending ignorance and confront it directly.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~forget-me-knot":{"haiku":"Despite seeking connection and having influence, the narrator remains isolated and ineffectual; they vow mutual devotion while fighting collectively against apathy and despair.","opus":"The Armenian-American singer addresses someone who broadcasts to multitudes yet connects with no one, begging to be remembered while pledging devotion deep enough to share a grave. He weaves in a wish for a Jerusalem open to every faith, fusing private loyalty with the political and spiritual scope that defines his work beyond the band.","sonnet":"Two people bound by history and devotion navigate the gap between public performance and private failure. Underneath the fractures — the dropped ball, the fled moment — lies a fierce, almost desperate pledge: to rise together, cross every ocean, and hold to each other until the end.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~gate-21":{"haiku":"A lover confronts the ending of a relationship and the impossible choice between staying connected or moving forward, unable to accept that the love has become painful.","opus":"A tangled portrait of a love where pain doubles as home, the couple disappearing into each other amid a barrage of anxious questions about whether they're loved, hated or deceived, and repeated goodbyes that supposedly aren't for long. Serj Tankian, System of a Down's frontman, brings his solo restlessness here, the dramatic swings and pleas to follow him to destiny's border making heartbreak feel like a theatrical spiral.","sonnet":"A love relationship dissolving at the border of departure — the speaker cannot reconcile the fact that pain and love are arriving from the same source, and questions about staying or going, deceiving or believing, go unanswered. Goodbye is called an ending but also potentially not the last word, and the whole song trembles at that threshold without crossing it.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~harakiri":{"haiku":"Mass animal deaths and human destruction suggest societal collapse where love or death are the only choices remaining.","opus":"A meditation on a species born to perish and wounding itself with deceit, illustrated by mass self-destruction, fish stranding themselves and birds dropping dead from the sky. Amid a stark ultimatum that people must care for one another or perish, the singer wonders when they will finally act, and concludes the dead are the only ones truly free.","sonnet":"Mass animal die-offs — beached drum fish and blackbirds falling from the sky — are read as signs of a civilization that has built monuments to itself while ignoring the ways of the wise, and the song asks whether humanity can love one another or will simply die together.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~honking-antelope": {"haiku":"Apocalyptic reflection on humanity's destruction of nature and indigenous cultures. Only nature will survive humanity's arrogance and cruelty.","opus":"A mourning of disappearing native cultures paired with a warning that the natural world will outlast human destruction. Tankian urges recording what's vanishing before it's lost, blaming humanity for wrecking the planet and suggesting only those still tied to the earth will endure.","sonnet":"A song lamenting humanity's destruction of the natural world — vanishing indigenous peoples, poets corrupting the Tao, humans as the cause of a broken world — while suggesting that nature and nature-based beings will ultimately outlast human civilization.","src":"haiku","fable":"A catalogue of dissolving worlds: fading photographs of everything already gone, indigenous bloodlines going extinct, poets and prophets unmaking the old Taoist order — Tankian stacks tableaux of what civilization erodes, and the braying antelope and the choreography of snakes stand in as the animal knowledge that outlasts our institutions, with only nature-rooted creatures surviving the collapse. The bridge turns lament into indictment: he names humanity as the author of a broken world and concedes the earth will outlive its wreckers, then closes on the bitter half-joke that a single song might mend the damage — the activist's own doubt, on this 2007 solo debut, about whether art can heal anything at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","nature & the elements","death & grief"],"means":"the activist's own doubt that art can heal the ruin it accurately diagnoses","built":"the closing half-joke about one song fixing the damage undercuts the entire indictment"},
"serj-tankian~left-of-center":{"haiku":"Theocracy and imperial systems operate through deception and ignorance, requiring rebellion and truth-telling against hierarchical power structures.","opus":"Serj's antiwar, anti-imperial polemic, skewering a theocracy that defends its vampires and bombs others' ignorance. Beneath the fury runs a wearied meditation on living and dying, and words as the only weapon he'll claim.","sonnet":"A disoriented anti-imperial polemic — blind theocracy, empire, ignorance, all bombed and defeated while the narrator asks whether any of it means anything, caught between living and dying without a clear structure to stand on.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~lie-lie-lie": {"haiku":"A narrative of deception and betrayal culminating in the narrator watching someone fall to their death, masked by falsehood throughout.","opus":"Beneath a sing-song refrain, the song tells of a toxic love in which the narrator coldly lets a lover fall to her death on the rocks below. He describes smiling and delighting as she dies, framing the whole affair around lies.","sonnet":"A narrator takes a person to a cliff edge under the guise of love or brotherhood and lets them fall to their death on the rocks below, smiling throughout — a story of intimate betrayal disguised as affection.","src":"haiku","fable":"A murder ballad hiding inside a nursery-rhyme sing-along, where the wordless cooing syllables and the word for deception collapse into the same sound, so the lullaby is literally assembled out of lying. The verses shuffle the lovers' roles — baby, lover, brother — while every address curdles into one demand to be released, until the tenderness turns predatory: the speaker walks a woman to a cliff's edge, she shatters on the rocks below, and by the final pass the mask drops entirely, as she takes his hand, he lets her fall, and he smiles. Tankian, freshly out from System of a Down's political fury, turns toward something more intimate and sinister, dressing a killing in the friendliest melody he can find so the horror lives in the gap between how sweet it sounds and what it actually describes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Tankian’s own gloss is lighter than the song — a crazy, humorous drama, he called it, the serious first draft torn up and these lines improvised on the fly.","themes":["violence & murder","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"a killing dressed in the friendliest melody available to widen the horror","built":"the lullaby syllables and the word for deception share the same sound by design"},
"serj-tankian~money":{"haiku":"A critique of money's tyrannical grip over human desires and autonomy. The song frames financial obsession as the root of servitude and dysfunction in modern society.","opus":"A caustic critique of greed, chanting the obsessive hoarding and worship of money and tracing human ineptitude and servitude back to the tyranny of money itself.","sonnet":"A critique of money as a system of servitude and dysfunction, trapping people in obsessive accumulation and stripping them of autonomy — the speaker traces his own ineptitude and submission back to its tyranny.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~occupied-tears":{"haiku":"War and occupation destroy children and civilians; the song demands recognition of horror and calls for peace to end the cyclical misery.","opus":"A grief-stricken anti-war protest over the Israeli-Palestinian conflict, condemning those who seize a young one's sorrow, evoking the barrier, severed bodies and buried explosives, and insisting people quit feigning blindness to the wrong. It shifts from mourning to a call for a shared partitioned homeland, an end to the shelling, the sirens and the anguish of military rule.","sonnet":"A direct political indictment of the Israeli occupation of Palestine — a young woman who grows up witnessing oppression becomes a suicide bomber, and the song asks how one people can occupy another child's grief; it ends by advocating the two-state solution as the only humane exit.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~peace-be-revenged":{"haiku":"Humanity has destroyed its own ecological foundation through carelessness and will face obliteration; peace can only be achieved after nature's violent retaliation.","opus":"An ecological warning that humanity built its world with no regard for the natural one and turned itself into the sickness, so that survival itself is in doubt, peace exacting a reckoning before it can be restored. Serj Tankian, System of a Down's frontman, carries this apocalyptic plea to choose love over fear through his operatic, unpredictable solo phrasing.","sonnet":"Humanity as a disease that built civilization without nature in mind and won't leave even as the world collapses — the rain comes, the mitochondria wash away, and peace can only return after it exacts revenge on the species that violated it. The speaker watches the survivors try to steer toward love while brainwash and fear erode them. In the end: just watch the rain.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~praise-the-lord-and-pass-the-ammunition":{"haiku":"Religious faith, prophecy, and spiritual lineage are invoked alongside themes of warfare and weaponry, creating ironic tension between spiritual creation/love and physical violence and ammunition. The song juxtaposes sacred and martial imagery.","opus":"A satirical indictment of religion fused with violence, contrasting a call to lay down guns with the refrain of praising the Lord while passing the ammunition, mocking how faith and warfare intertwine while people await Armageddon.","sonnet":"A cynical commentary on religion and violence — tracing a lineage from prophet to king to pauper and back to love — that asks believers to lay down their guns while sardonically invoking the phrase used to justify holy war, questioning whether devotion ever leads to peace or only more ammunition.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~reality-tv":{"haiku":"Television broadcasts the empty lives and piercings of ordinary people, destroying mystery and meaning. The singer despises reality TV as a whore masquerading as truth.","opus":"Serj Tankian's solo salvo lampoons the reality-television craze, its narrator buzzing on others' manufactured drama while a screen slyly stares back. Grotesque imagery of piercings and self-harm mocks spectacle mistaken for meaning. Branding the genre a whore that calls itself reality, he decries a hedonistic entropy where viewers gain confidence from strangers' failures, comforted that the on-screen wreckage isn't them.","sonnet":"A pointed satirical attack on reality television as a cultural pathology — passive viewers feeding on strangers' manufactured crises to feel better about their own failures, the genre producing nothing but hedonistic entropy dressed up as entertainment and marketed as truth.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~reconstructive-demonstrations":{"haiku":"Building new structures and systems through active protest and demonstration.","opus":"A cryptic, fractured protest built from paradoxes about authority collapsing and people brought low, pleading to be taught the basics of living from a broken, barren source. Serj Tankian, System of a Down's frontman, shifts between operatic and manic delivery, drawing dread from wordplay as a spreading blindness and sickness that answers to nothing.","sonnet":"A disintegrating dialogue about breakdown — laws retiring, walls retiring, people falling to their knees because no one taught them how to eat or speak from whole hands. The disease image accrues until the whole song is consumed by it. Forgetting to pray and forgetting to say please are symmetrical failures; neither side held, and the falling becomes collective and irreversible.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~saving-us":{"haiku":"A couple tears each other apart through conflict and departure, yet paradoxically that very destruction and heartbreak saves and transforms them both.","opus":"A plea to a couple squandering their time by wounding each other night after night, urging them to share joy and comfort instead of tearing one another apart. The same destructive acts are recast, paradoxically, as capable of redemption, harm and salvation described as two sides of the same bond.","sonnet":"A relationship tears two people apart through avoidance, hurt, and unresolved conflict, yet the same destructive dynamic paradoxically sustains and even saves them. The speaker cycles through the contradictions of being broken apart and kept alive by the same person.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~sky-is-over":{"haiku":"Catastrophic loss and regret pervade as the sky closes in, though yearning for connection amid civilizational decline persists.","opus":"A sense that the sky, and the world, is over, tangled with a strained relationship of disappointment and craziness; it turns outward to accuse a destructive force at war with the planet whose persuasion wrecks economies, empires and countries.","sonnet":"A relationship fraying under some larger, unaffordable collapse — the sky being \"over\" signals both personal loss and a broader civilizational exhaustion, and the narrator does not want to let go but cannot hold on.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tankian's solo-debut single smuggles a civilizational death sentence inside the shell of a lovers' quarrel: the clingy, wheedling pre-chorus and the giddy nonsense-syllable singalong are a mask, and the second verse rips it off to name the real target, a coercive power whose appetite ruins economies and topples empires while the heavens seal shut overhead. The verb of bankruptcy that keeps returning as if it were an endearment quietly reroutes from a couple's finances to a whole species living past its ecological means, so the begging not to be left stops sounding romantic and starts sounding like a planet imploring the force that exploits it to relent. The wordless crowd-chant matters precisely because it is empty — it hands an arena a joyous hook to shout back while the lines it displaces insist there is no exit left, not even upward, so the collective celebration becomes the very sound of denial.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","nature & the elements","artifice & performance"],"means":"a lovers' quarrel hollowed out to smuggle a civilizational death sentence","built":"the wordless crowd-chant displaces the lines insisting there is no exit left"},
"serj-tankian~the-unthinking-majority":{"haiku":"Critiques a passive populace controlled by systems of oppression and chemical pacification, arguing that antidepressants and democracy are tools of manipulation. The song rails against manufactured consent and political hypocrisy.","opus":"A protest track skewering a two-faced order that steers a docile, incurious populace, treating mood medication as a leash that merely makes existence bearable. It condemns wars invented to open a path for petroleum profits and demands a genuine, functioning form of self-rule.","sonnet":"A political attack on state hypocrisy, manufactured consent, and corporate-driven war, arguing that antidepressants serve as tools of social control to keep a passive majority compliant while governments clear the way for oil interests under false pretexts.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tankian launches his solo career by inhabiting authority rather than shouting at it, singing the chorus as a bureaucratic state that dresses murder in the calm vocabulary of policy and files away the inconvenient like impounded strays. The menace is that this voice stays administrative, never unhinged, so the horror reads as paperwork — consent drilled into a sedated public one looped slogan at a time. Only the bridge sheds the mask in Tankian's own register, naming the enemy's weapon as a manufactured rumor and the dread it breeds as cover for a war fought over petroleum, his 2007 Iraq subtext stated flat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","war & battle"],"means":"authority voiced as calm bureaucracy rather than ranting villain","built":"the bridge drops the administrative mask to name petroleum as the motive"},
"serj-tankian~tyrant-s-gratitude-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Sarcastic thanks to those who oppressed and shaped you through tyranny.","opus":"A mocking dissection of a dictator's fake generosity, his hush unnerving and his brutality dressed up as admirable, eager to make a spectacle of anyone bold enough to voice a scrap of honesty, his predatory bird poised to devour dissenters while his bruised, foolish vanity shows through. Serj Tankian, System of a Down's singer, brings his theatrical solo sneer to bear on despotism, ridicule sharpened into accusation.","sonnet":"A savage portrait of authoritarian ego disguised as benevolence — the tyrant listens, feels, knows, and sends his servants to handle what his pride cannot let him confront directly. Every gesture of apparent gratitude is really a mechanism of control, and the one who speaks a small uncomfortable truth is rewarded by being made a spectacle, an eagle's meal, a lesson for the next person who might dare.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~uneducated-democracy":{"haiku":"Democracy fails without education; leaders exploit closed minds through media, sex scandals, and feudalism disguised as freedom.","opus":"Tankian's argument that self-rule without a knowledgeable populace is just tyranny in disguise, mocking media theater, cynical statecraft and a tranquilized public. The rallying cry to strike back at the system is aimed at chosen ignorance more than any single ruler.","sonnet":"A political song arguing that democracy without education is just another form of autocracy — the ruling class exploits civic ignorance to perpetuate feudalism dressed up as freedom, while sex scandals and media images do the work of keeping people passive.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~weave-on":{"haiku":"Sisters as weavers stitch together the suffering of war, poverty, and injustice into a fabric of human pain. The song mourns the blood-soaked threads of conflict and corruption.","opus":"Sisters at a loom weaving the endless human record of war, poverty and hatred, their thread run through with blood. Tankian, drawing on his Armenian and antiwar conscience and even Greek tragedy, turns grief into a plea to keep bearing witness.","sonnet":"The song addresses women — \"sisters\" — who must weave the full record of human suffering: war, poverty, crime, displacement, unmarked graves. The act of weaving with blood-soaked thread becomes a metaphor for bearing witness to atrocity and continuing to endure despite it.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~wings-of-summer":{"haiku":"Consequences inevitably catching up with wrongdoing and deception. Flying free on summer wings is impossible when you're hunted by those you've wronged.","opus":"A surreal tug-of-war between wanting to fly free on summer's wings and a lover whose eyes pin the narrator down, spiraling into absurdist images of getting caught, wheeled in, the lever pulled, running someone over with a Hummer. Freedom and consequence keep colliding. Serj Tankian, the System of a Down vocalist, indulges his solo taste for theatrical non-sequitur and dark whimsy, the flight always clipped just as it lifts off.","sonnet":"A chaotic desire to fly undercut at every turn by consequence — the speaker wants to take off on summer wings but something keeps pinning him, the gaze of another person, the weight of a crime he can't outrun. The song slides into dark comedy: he ran over someone with a Hummer, now he's going to fry. Freedom keeps getting clipped before liftoff, today disappearing into a tomorrow that reads forever.","src":"haiku"},
"serj-tankian~yes-it-s-genocide":{"haiku":"Direct accusation of genocide; calling out denial and atrocity.","opus":"A stark, repeated Armenian invocation, the phrases vowing to die for your eyes, your face, your life, an incantation of grief and devotion tied to the song's blunt title. Serj Tankian, of Armenian descent and a lifelong advocate for recognition of the Armenian Genocide, strips this to pure liturgical repetition, the untranslated mourning carrying more weight than any English verse could.","sonnet":"A voice repeats a single sacrificial offering — to die in place of others, absorbing their terror, their fear, their death — a collective Armenian lament in which martyrdom becomes the only answer left to a horror that refuses to be named as anything other than what it is.","src":"haiku"},
"sermon~golden":{"haiku":"Oppression and suffering are institutionalized through deception and power; virtue arrives only through collective endurance.","opus":"A masked prog-metal project spins a mythic, near-biblical parable of the hunted and the burdened. The powerful weigh victims down with hides, arrows and lies as vultures gather, yet a golden rain descends like a coat of arms binding them to virtue's arrival. Amid the oppression, the song seeks out the lionhearted to rise.","sonnet":"Oppression by weight and deception — the thickest hides, the sharpest arrows, the panting lies pressing down — and the response comes not as revolution but as golden rain: a coat of arms binding the hunted to virtue, waves like stars, the set-in-stone endurance of those who sought lion hearts.","src":"haiku"},
"set-to-stun~enter-lust":{"haiku":"Predatory obsession metastasizes into violence; the speaker's desire transforms into possession, culminating in murder that becomes both condemnation and spiritual reckoning.","opus":"A harrowing arc from lust to murder to damnation, where a predator kills a woman, evades justice, and then spirals through the vices, envy into wrath, that make an enemy of everything. Underneath the horror is a wretch clinging to a beloved name and to grace, half-praying to be saved from the demon and voices haunting him.","sonnet":"A lurid, escalating noir: a chance pickup at a bar spirals into violence and a body hidden in the closet, before snapping into theological self-examination as the speaker, quoting Amazing Grace and confessing cardinal sins, cycles from lust through wrath to a desperate longing for rescue.","src":"haiku"},
"set-to-stun~walk-tall-ii":{"haiku":"Corruption of the soul through excess and the attempt to reclaim authentic rock-and-roll from exploitation. The speaker laments how drugs, sex, and manufactured culture stole spiritual integrity.","opus":"A sprawling metalcore reckoning with hedonism and self-disgust, narrated by someone who believed he was thriving while indulgence quietly hollowed him out. Borrowing the biblical rhythm of seasons for everything, he rails at frauds and at his own arrogance, wondering whether a person who keeps questioning himself can still be redeemed.","sonnet":"A man takes stock of how sex, drugs, and rock and roll broke him while he thought he was thriving — walking tall but only moving sideways. The reckoning sprawls into fury at complacency and pretenders, cycling through Ecclesiastes-style catalogue and punk self-recrimination before landing on a defiant insistence that the spirit inside is not yet dead.","src":"haiku"},
"sety-ursails~best-of-me":{"haiku":"Consumption and betrayal leave the speaker hollowed; another takes what makes them vital and leaves ruin.","opus":"Being so consumed by someone that you can't breathe or walk away, starved for reassurances that go unspoken and knowing you ought to leave but can't. The only exits narrow to release or being broken down to nothing.","sonnet":"Trapped in a relationship that takes everything while leaving the speaker unable to breathe, leave, or speak — \"lifeless roses, overdoses\" — they beg to be set free even as they confess they know they should let go and still cannot.","src":"haiku"},
"sety-ursails~nightfall-feat-andy-doerner":{"haiku":"Darkness and self-harm feel like home; the speaker embraces despair and falling rather than resist, having chosen this descent deliberately. Self-destruction is reclaimed as agency.","opus":"The track inhabits a mind that welcomes its own descent, lighting candles to watch them suffocate and craving the ground more than the sun it cannot stand. With Andy Doerner guesting, it inverts the usual fear of darkness, the narrator choosing to be buried by night and embracing the burn as the one place he would rather be.","sonnet":"The narrator finds daylight unbearable and actively welcomes the descent into darkness. Sinking is not feared but chosen — the ground is a destination, the extinguishing of light a relief — and he embraces this gravity with the clarity of someone who has stopped pretending to want rescue.","src":"haiku"},
"seven-lions~lose-myself-ft-lynn-gunn":{"haiku":"Complete emotional surrender and merger of identity within romantic love.","opus":"A song about surrendering completely into a lover, wanting to dissolve your own self inside them, treating them as the fire that fills what's missing and the light you rise from ashes toward.","sonnet":"The speaker describes a transcendent romantic obsession that lifts them physically above their ordinary life, finding meaning and missing pieces in another person's presence, and surrendering themselves completely to that feeling even as they struggle just to keep breathing.","src":"haiku"},
"sevendust~black":{"opus":"An outsider insisting he's done nothing wrong while others brand him different and devil-haunted, trapped in a private hell and unsettled by his own reflection.","sonnet":"A speaker who feels marked as different and dangerous — voices calling their name, the devil said to live in their soul — insists they are minding their own business and doing nothing wrong, while shadows follow close and the mirror reflects something they cannot stand.","src":"sonnet"},
"sevendust~dying-to-live":{"haiku":"Each desire and possession comes at the cost of one's core self; the exhaustion of giving and struggling leaves the speaker questioning if survival is worth the price.","opus":"The exhausting math of desire, where every want costs a piece of yourself and merely surviving demands constant sacrifice. The title's paradox names a life spent grinding just to keep going, wondering if the effort is worth the toll.","sonnet":"The cost of wanting — every desire tears a piece out of you, the struggle of surviving takes everything you have, and the narrator keeps asking why the effort to live feels indistinguishable from dying.","src":"haiku"},
"sevendust~enemy":{"haiku":"Contempt and social rejection ignite violence; the narrator threatens and humiliates someone they see as weak, celebrating their inevitable downfall.","opus":"A brawler's warning aimed at a hanger-on who copies and steals his way toward a spotlight he'll never reach, the venom masking a deeper point about authenticity: fakes end up alone with only themselves to answer to.","sonnet":"A direct confrontation with a fake, self-important rival who plagiarizes his way toward fame: the speaker tears down the pretension, predicts the inevitable fall from relevance, and leaves the poser isolated, asking how it feels to be the one everyone turns against.","src":"haiku"},
"sevendust~fence":{"haiku":"Bloodletting and accumulated abuse threaten rupture; the speaker strains against the edges of endurance while wondering how long the breaking can continue before final collapse.","opus":"At the end of his endurance after relentless mistreatment and dishonesty, worn down by being drained and wondering when it will ever stop, the singer is done pretending. He resolves to blow it all up and destroy the life he used to recognize.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped in cycles of abuse asks how long it will take until it's over, until there's nothing left to take — \"been down so long that the bottom's up\" names the disorientation — ending in defiant resignation as the fuse finally gets lit.","src":"haiku"},
"sevendust~medicated":{"haiku":"Emotional numbing through pharmaceutical intervention masks systemic toxicity; disconnection prevents genuine healing or authentic response.","opus":"A diagnosis of a numbed, oversaturated culture that has grown so used to sedation it can no longer feel enough to heal, spreading the blame across everyone rather than pointing at any single wasted man.","sonnet":"A critique of collective emotional numbness: society has become so oversaturated and overcomplicated that people can no longer feel, and without feeling there is no healing — the speaker sees everyone as complicit in a shared anesthesia, overmedicated into emptiness and unable to break free.","src":"haiku"},
"sevendust~splinter-album-version":{"haiku":"Humiliation and contempt splinter the psyche; the speaker rejects the oppressor's authority and claims indifference as final victory.","opus":"Rage at a false authority who treats the singer as subhuman and expendable. Being the splinter they want to cut out becomes a source of power, the irritant that outlasts and unmasks the man in charge.","sonnet":"A speaker turns the violence of domination back on those who wielded it — calling out a false authority figure who used power to diminish and destroy — declaring that the oppressor's faith and standing are broken and they now mean nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"sevendust~the-day-i-tried-to-live-soundgarden-cover":{"haiku":"Attempts radical change but discovers conformity and illusion permeate existence, learning oneself a liar. Each restart reveals deeper entrapment.","opus":"A grim account of a day spent trying and failing to truly live, waking to a violent inner voice, giving beggars' change to the rich, dangling from power lines, and finally learning the narrator was a liar. This is Sevendust, the Atlanta alt-metal band, covering Soundgarden's 1994 Superunknown track, honoring Chris Cornell's bleak irony while translating its grunge unease into their heavier, groove-driven attack.","sonnet":"A failed attempt at engagement with the world — the speaker woke up trying to actually live, to participate, but every gesture turned paradoxical or self-defeating, and the gap between internal life and external action proved unbridgeable. The refrain that one more revolution might finally do it keeps recurring, but each pass ends in the same defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"seventh-void~broken-sky":{"haiku":"A despairing person lies defeated and lost under a fractured sky, seeking to end suffering while isolation and decay close in.","opus":"Kenny Hickey's doom-heavy meditation on self-destruction and a life spent hanging by a thread, someone selling themselves toward their own ruin. The refrain leaves a person exposed and lost beneath an indifferent, fractured heaven.","sonnet":"A song about a person utterly hollowed out by self-destruction — selling themselves piece by piece, begging for their own ruin, anonymous and weightless beneath a shattered sky, edging toward death.","src":"haiku"},
"seventh-void~closing-in":{"haiku":"Spiritual despair and psychological suffocation describe a mind abandoned by faith with no escape; the walls close in as hope dissolves and descent becomes inevitable.","opus":"A vision of a soul and mind giving out, time short and the walls seeming to press inward. Forsaken by God with no light left, the narrator sinks in his bleakest moment, his body collapsing and his spirit carried off to oblivion, expecting nothing ahead but emptiness.","sonnet":"Total psychological collapse — the soul is dying, God has abandoned the person, there's no light or peace available, the room is closing in, and the end is numbness and falling into nowhere.","src":"haiku"},
"seventh-void~heaven-is-gone":{"haiku":"As life ends and light fades, spiritual transcendence proves impossible; those who sought heaven find only darkness and cessation.","opus":"The narrator watches someone die and their spirit come undone, discovering that whatever afterlife they hoped for simply isn't there. The bleak conclusion is nihilistic: once a life ends, there is no waiting light and no heaven at all, only absence.","sonnet":"A meditation on death as the moment when every promise of an afterlife proves empty — the person who died reached for the light and found the final sign instead; when life is done there is only silence, no soul to cry, no heaven waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"seventh-void~killing-you-slow":{"haiku":"Something slowly torments and breaks the victim's willpower and soul, trapping them while paranoia grows and walls seem to breathe with menace.","opus":"A doom-laden descent into paranoia and imprisonment, something dragging the narrator down, breaking his will and soul while shadows close in, the walls breathe and listen, and control slips away one toll at a time. Seventh Void, the stoner-doom project led by Type O Negative and Danzig guitarist Kenny Hickey, channels that gothic heaviness here, turning psychic collapse into slow, crushing dread.","sonnet":"Addiction as a slow siege — something has latched on to the listener's mind and body, bending it downward through accumulated damage rather than a single blow. The walls start breathing, control dissolves, dark suspicion takes over, and the song maps the process of being hollowed out from within, shadow by shadow, until the will is entirely gone.","src":"haiku"},
"sever-the-day~the-backroom":{"haiku":"Hidden depths and concealed spaces beneath surface, the speaker revealing what's kept private and dark.","opus":"A claustrophobic tale of a toxic bond dressed as seduction. The narrator wants to keep someone trapped in the backroom, promising that every path loops back to him and every room bears his trace. Watching them barely breathe, he invites them to revel in the tragedy and suffer alongside him rather than escape.","sonnet":"A relationship as a backroom labyrinth — every corner leads back to the beginning, paths intertwined, no escape — the speaker holds the other in a state of liminal suffering, all seems fine while nothing is right, and the invitation to revel in this tragedy together has the quality of a curse neither person can dissolve.","src":"haiku"},
"sever~my-dream-my-agony":{"opus":"Addiction and denial laid bare: the narrator can't sleep without pills, can't keep his promises to quit, and borrows happiness from his future self, aware he and his friends keep blaming everyone but themselves as they spiral toward rock bottom.","sonnet":"A raw self-reckoning about addiction and self-deception — the narrator knows they borrow happiness from their future self through substances and avoidance, can't break the cycle, and feels alienated from the world while dreaming of being strong enough to resist.","src":"sonnet"},
"sex-mask~curse":{"haiku":"Philosophical fragmentation on time, weather, and causation spirals into incomprehensibility, where logical questions collapse into refrain. The speaker struggles to locate meaning in the noise of repeated phrases.","opus":"An oblique, dreamlike piece where language itself seems to fracture. Fragmented images of crossing time, debating the weather, and a cross that awakens circle a sense of lost balance and eroding trust. The disorientation is the point: meaning slips just out of reach, mirroring a mind unmoored and a world that won't cohere.","sonnet":"Fragmented imagery of crossroads, rain, and waiting circles around a sense of being stuck in an unresolvable threshold — the speaker and a collective 'we' keep collecting, balancing, and waiting on some unnamed core that never resolves.","src":"haiku"},
"sexnesc~lm":{"haiku":"Two lovers meet under pink skies and burning passion, each moment branded into skin and memory. The song crackles with intensity—pleasure and pain intertwined, bodies holding each other through nights, promising a love that consumes like fire until nothing remains.","opus":"A feverish, drugged love addressed to a lover the singer is burning up alongside, invoking multiverses ('somewhere in the multiverse there's a place for us') and the childhood palm-reading game where her ocean spills past the planet; he begs her not to let go and repeats that she too will one day burn up, love figured as a beautiful, consuming fire.","sonnet":"A Ukrainian-language song in which the speaker burns alongside a lover's light (\"lm\"), holding on through dark nights with bruised arms and legs, chasing the fantasy of a rose-tinged Tijuana sky and multiverse futures together, knowing they will eventually burn out but unable to let go.","src":"haiku"},
"shadow-of-intent~melancholy":{"haiku":"Corruption spreads through systems of violence; melancholy engulfs the fallen as they descend into oblivion, fire consuming what remains.","opus":"A descent into nothingness and derangement, minds crumbling through a lightless abyss toward an unchosen end they can't argue their way clear of. Flame swallows everything in a total, saturating sorrow that devours the human figure as a walking abomination.","sonnet":"A descent into total annihilation: human disgrace, familial dishonour, the maelstrom swallowing all — \"falling into oblivion, engulfed by fire and cast to the flame\" — with the dead unable to find a reason to survive, their collapse framed as pure, inevitable melancholy.","src":"haiku"},
"shakira~te-dejo-madrid":{"haiku":"A woman walks away from a place and lover that cannot hold her; departure becomes an act of self-preservation and self-knowledge.","opus":"A woman walking out on a cowardly lover and the city that held them, insisting she'll land on her feet like a cat; the sweetness she leaves behind only sharpens her refusal to keep suffering for someone unworthy.","sonnet":"A woman walks away from a cowardly lover and the city they shared, done with the routines, the sweetness turned stale, and the emotional evasions of a man who made her suffer — she leaves for good, landing on her feet like a cat.","src":"haiku"},
"sharks-in-your-mouth~cult-404":{"haiku":"Social media and corporations strip human authenticity into profiles and algorithms, replacing reality. Digital existence erases individuality through likes, shares, and manufactured identity.","opus":"A furious metalcore attack on online life, where profiles and algorithms replace real people and existence dissolves into scrolling, filters and validation while the actual world falls apart. Built around a web error code as its emblem of a hollow generation, it demands a total breakdown of the system so people might think for themselves again.","sonnet":"A broadside against social media as a system of control dressed as freedom — algorithms dictate identity, appearances replace substance, and users perform their lives for dopamine while the actual world collapses. The song names this as a kind of cult membership and invites those who want to think for themselves to opt out, though it holds no illusions about how many will.","src":"haiku"},
"sharks-in-your-mouth~nine-thou":{"haiku":"Aggressive hip-hop braggadocio celebrates style, verbal dominance, and street credibility against all comers and competitors.","opus":"A relentless battle-rap flex, all boasts of lyrical dominance, venomous wordplay, and sunk battleships. It's pure competitive swagger, style as weapon.","sonnet":"Aggressive, dense rap braggadocio — the narrator and their crew assert dominance through rapid-fire battle rhymes, street credibility, and threat display, dismantling anyone who steps up with a torrent of non-stop verbal punishment.","src":"haiku"},
"sharon-van-etten~serpents":{"haiku":"Psychological intrusion and the search for truth about a lover's hidden crimes; the narrator tries to forgive but remains vigilant for deceit.","opus":"Written out of an abusive relationship, this tracks the moment fear curdles into resolve; the serpents are the suspicion and damage he planted, and she finally decides she won't stay frozen while he keeps failing to change.","sonnet":"The speaker disentangles from a manipulative, predatory partner whose presence keeps twisting into her thoughts like serpents, reaching toward the hope that he might finally change while knowing that her own perception has been colonized by his crimes.","src":"haiku"},
"sharptooth~mean-brain":{"opus":"An unflinching depiction of the internal monologue of self-hatred, examining why the singer treats herself with cruelty she'd never inflict on anyone else, stuck on a loop of self-inflicted wounds.","sonnet":"The internal experience of vicious self-talk: the narrator's own mind subjects her to abuse she would never direct at another person, looping cruel thoughts on repeat until they crowd out everything else.","src":"sonnet"},
"sharptooth~say-nothing-in-the-absence-of-content":{"haiku":"Artistic and commercial cultures collapse into hollow spectacle where substance vanishes and substance-less posturing sells; authentic meaning dissolves beneath performative violence.","opus":"A scathing hardcore satire of hollow, market-driven music. The narrator mockingly performs a track with no substance, arguing scenes reward hype over meaning and that art voided of values can't be criticized because it stands for nothing. Every interchangeable pit-cue becomes proof of a culture that profits from having nothing to say.","sonnet":"A meta-attack on the hollow spectacle of heavy music performance: the band announces the song is about nothing, then demonstrates how screaming into a vacuum and calling generic mosh pit cues is indistinguishable from meaningful art when the audience isn't listening anyway. The point is that content-free performance is its own ideology — safe, unscrutable, profitable.","src":"haiku"},
"she-in-the-haze~last-dance":{"haiku":"Loss and farewell permeate the closing moments, where love disappears with time and the speaker watches beauty and connection slip irretrievably away.","opus":"A farewell rendered in dreamlike, broken English where love drains away and only the promise of no more pain remains. The freeing of the other is also a mourning, everything golden slipping into light and flowing off for good.","sonnet":"A goodbye to a relationship — the crying has become permanent, the love is gone, and a golden year is slipping away; the narrator tries to frame release as freedom but the grief keeps breaking through.","src":"haiku"},
"she-s-in-parties~l-word":{"haiku":"Fear of vulnerability battles desire for connection; unguarded feelings slip through.","opus":"The panic of nearly saying love and instantly retreating, guard up but still wanting to be loved and for the person to stay. It's the tug-of-war between vulnerability and self-protection where the word keeps slipping out and getting taken back.","sonnet":"About the terror of admitting you're falling in love — the narrator keeps almost saying it, takes it back, insists they didn't mean to let it slip; the L word is love, and the whole song is the gap between feeling it and being unable to say it without immediately retreating.","src":"haiku"},
"she-wants-revenge~red-flags-and-long-nights":{"haiku":"Self-sabotage becomes identity; the speaker needs dysfunction and danger, unable to accept healthy love without destroying it.","opus":"A self-aware serial commitment-phobe who admits he sabotages good things because he craves drama over stability. He likes a woman who is genuinely well-adjusted but knows she'll sense he needs the warning signs and sleepless nights she can't provide.","sonnet":"A self-aware romantic admits he's drawn to turbulence — \"I need red flags and long nights\" — and when he finds someone stable and healthy who doesn't need saving, he recognises the attraction isn't there; the rental metaphor for emotional real estate runs through: she can occupy his thoughts only until the price gets too high.","src":"haiku"},
"she-wants-revenge~tear-you-apart":{"haiku":"Desire escalates from casual crush to dangerous obsession, as two people spiral from hesitation into passion that threatens their emotional safety.","opus":"A high-school crush narrated with mounting unease, the small fixation swelling into something the narrator half-recognizes as danger, until the whispered refrain reveals a desire to tear the beloved apart, tenderness and menace fused in the same breath. She Wants Revenge, the LA duo, built their sound on exactly this deadpan, Joy-Division-indebted darkwave, the murmured baritone making obsession sound seductive and sinister at once.","sonnet":"A crush that grows into obsession narrated in real time — the moment it tips from cute to dangerous, from a nod and a cigarette to two days of heavy fixation. When they finally connect, the lips meet and the reservation dissolves, but what he wants from her is violent and tender in equal measure, whispered into her ear while she's still, close, convinced she's safe.","src":"haiku"},
"sheena-ringo~a-oorcgn":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"Sheena Ringo has long dramatized the strain of being perpetually watched and appraised as a performer, and this fractured identity crisis sits squarely in that lineage. Assessing eyes praise her from every direction and ask her to perform authenticity while seeing straight through it. She keeps asking who she really is, whether to trust anyone, whether to stay or go, overwhelmed by the impossibility of knowing herself.","sonnet":"A fractured identity crisis — surrounded by eyes assessing and praising her from every direction, asked to perform authenticity while being seen through, the narrator asks over and over who she really is, whether to trust anyone, whether to stay or go, overwhelmed by the impossibility of knowing herself.","src":"haiku"},
"sheena-ringo~death-jazz-ver":{"haiku":"A woman manipulates through performance, pretending devotion while calculating advantage. The man becomes fool, and she refuses guilt—performing heartbreak as entertainment, betrayal as art.","opus":"The Japanese art-pop iconoclast inhabits a world of theatrical romance and deception. Voiced with the cadence of an old pleasure-quarter courtesan, the narrator treats love as performance where vows are unnecessary and lies are half the act. Beneath the jazzy swagger runs melancholy over fleeting affairs, autumn skies and the sad men who love and are loved.","sonnet":"A woman in Edo-period register performs emotional detachment from a kind but expired lover — \"it's curtain time\" — insisting women need no sincere vows because the whole affair is theatre anyway, before the mask slips at midnight and she quietly begs him not to leave, revealing her true self in the plea: \"this is my real nature, please wait.\"","src":"haiku"},
"sheer-mag~expect-the-bayonet":{"haiku":"Political resistance and collective uprising against systemic oppression and inequality.","opus":"A blistering protest that ties past struggles for the ballot to present-day injustice, demanding the vote or promising revolt. Its stance is unapologetic solidarity, warning the powerful that suppression has a cost.","sonnet":"A political ultimatum: the speaker traces a history of inequality and violent suppression, warns those in power that denying the ballot to the marginalised will earn them armed resistance, and calls for solidarity among those underfoot before it is too late.","src":"haiku"},
"shefound~half-alligator":{"haiku":"Predatory transformation: the speaker claims animal ferocity and territorial aggression as liberation, warning the seducer that curiosity breeds danger when one finally glimpses the true self beneath the smile.","opus":"A woman warning a predatory man that beneath her pretty smile lives a territorial creature that will bite back if he touches her heart. The alligator is her defensive, dangerous true self, and the taunt is to get out of her river before he learns it.","sonnet":"A woman embraces her own predatory, territorial nature as armour against anyone who tries to control or tame her — thick-skinned and lurking under a pretty smile, she warns that touching her heart means fighting for your life.","src":"haiku"},
"shelf-lives~frission":{"haiku":"Anticipation of collapse mingles with pride; the speaker senses a coming down while sensing stillness spreading through the system.","opus":"Anticipating the crash after the high, courting shame and the comedown as eagerly as the rush itself. The chill setting into the system is both drug and dread, with a nagging voice in the brain refusing to quiet.","sonnet":"About the anticipation of comedown — a kind of paradoxical desire to feel low after being high, to let pride collapse and feel something different. The narrator senses a chill moving through their system and welcomes the stillness that follows over-stimulation, though there is also a voice in the brain that keeps interrupting. It reads as the particular psychological state of waiting for a drug or emotional high to wear off.","src":"haiku"},
"shelf-lives~psycho":{"haiku":"Reality fragmentation and meaninglessness; the speaker drowns in others' narratives.","opus":"A restless, hedonistic shrug at a world growing stranger by the day. The narrator numbs herself on everyone else's drama, insists nothing's that deep, and chases fleeting kicks in back seats and cigarette haze. Under the punk-dance snarl runs an exhausted refusal to overthink and a hunch that nothing's ever quite going to satisfy.","sonnet":"A listless narrator finds meaning in fleeting moments — a car's back seat, a shared cigarette — while dismissing the weight others bring, circling between cynicism and the admission that life keeps getting weird and sometimes you just need somewhere to spin.","src":"haiku"},
"shelf-lives~sycophanT":{"haiku":"Darkness provides comfort against the light of exterior judgment, where the speaker admits to liking their own despair. Self-destruction becomes preferable to the performance demanded by others.","src":"haiku"},
"shelf-lives~sycophant":{"haiku":"Darkness becomes comfort; self-destruction offers strange peace despite recognition.","opus":"A jittery skewering of flattery and inherited wealth, the sycophant fawning over pretty eyes while the real ache is a parent's approval that never came. It finds a dark comfort in fear and the ugly truth that money means nothing if you're destroying yourself.","sonnet":"A speaker caught between a brash flatterer's attention and their own darkness — burning down the family tree because no one said they were proud, biting tongues, fearing the dark but liking it — confesses that caring about someone else's wealth is impossible when you are busy destroying yourself.","src":"haiku"},
"shelf-lives~tone-def":{"haiku":"Rejection and contempt frame the speaker's stance, refusing participation in false social rituals. Authentic rebellion requires burning bridges and rejecting comfort entirely.","opus":"A rapid-fire volley of refusals — to sex, to religion, to being used — torching a comfortable glass house and burning through life at full tilt. Beneath the reckless self-destruction sits a refusal to be the one who drags anyone down or wants them just for being near.","sonnet":"A speaker bristles at every demand — social, sexual, spiritual — with repeated refusals before turning to self-destructive imagery (filling the house with gas, burning cigarettes to ash); the chorus admits the contradiction: not wanting to drag anyone down or be aroused by mere proximity, unable to escape the compulsion all the same.","src":"haiku"},
"shelf-lives~where-did-i-go":{"haiku":"Identity and presence dissolve into bewilderment; the speaker cannot locate their own existence.","opus":"A punchy explosion of frustration and dissolving identity. Struggling even to speak, the narrator swings between telling everyone to get out of her face and asking where she herself has gone. Threats to break out curdle into breaking down, class resentment flashes through, and the repeated question captures a self fraying at the edges.","sonnet":"Rage and self-erasure at once — the narrator wants everyone to get out of their face, admits they might hate everybody, would eat dirty dollars and call out blood on white collars, and then lands on the unanswerable question: \"where did I go?\" The breakdown from \"I wanna break out\" to \"I'm gonna break down\" maps the collapse in real time.","src":"haiku"},
"shellz~aurora":{"haiku":"Misfortune traps the speaker between survival and aspiration; they question who taught shame and imagine alternative lives of privilege.","opus":"A depressive fighting his own inner strife, imagining an easier life where fortune spared him this struggle. He turns the old lie about boys not crying against itself, finding a fragile solidarity in singing his way through.","sonnet":"A man who has been mentally struggling since childhood reflects on inner turmoil he's still not free from — he imagines a parallel life where fortune favored him and escape was possible, while insisting that 'boys don't cry' is a lie and speaking to others who feel the same trap.","src":"haiku"},
"shels~butterflies-on-luci-s-way":{"haiku":"Presence alone diminishes suffering, yet the speaker carries the burden of an irreconcilable loss—cherished moments cannot sustain what has already been surrendered.","opus":"A love pared to its paradox: the beloved is the only thing that makes life bearable, yet across a whole lifetime that person was never actually there to be had.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses a lover with a painful admission: for all the moments of a life, none of them were shared with this person, and yet their presence is the one thing that keeps despair at bay.","src":"haiku"},
"sheoru~itb":{"haiku":"Genesis and inheritance frame humanity's emergence from void into conscious creation and multiplication. The song celebrates divine genesis while acknowledging the burden of shaping the world anew.","opus":"A retelling of Genesis, darkness over the deep giving way to light and the command to multiply and inherit the earth. Creation is voiced as a claim of dominion, molding order out of the formless void.","sonnet":"A creation-myth retelling cast as a claim of inheritance — from the formless void and a vulture cry that splits the sky, humanity multiplies and takes possession of the Earth, each genesis chorus an act of sacred moulding.","src":"haiku"},
"shepherds-reign~le-manu":{"haiku":"Victory arrives through strength and completeness, the eagle embodies Samoan heritage as power and determination claim dominion.","opus":"A Samoan war chant invoking the Manu Samoa, summoning warriors to fight to the utmost and never rest, blood running down their paths. It rallies pride in homeland and ancestry, promising to lay low every enemy in the name of a beloved nation.","sonnet":"A battle hymn in Samoan celebrating the Manu Samoa rugby team as a creature that comes from Samoa with full strength; it calls on players to fight without rest, remember their homeland and family, seek God's blessing, and defeat all opponents — the bird of Samoa arriving complete and unstoppable.","src":"haiku"},
"shinedown~bully":{"haiku":"Systemic cruelty breaks the spirit while collaborative resistance rises to confront the bully; awareness and solidarity transform victimhood into strength.","opus":"An anthem that flips fear into collective defiance, rallying the tormented to stand together against a bully whose only legacy will be being forgotten. The strength comes from numbers, not revenge.","sonnet":"A direct condemnation of a bully told from the perspective of those they've targeted, promising that the tormentor will be forgotten and unmourned, while rallying survivors to recognise their collective strength and refuse to accept the abuse.","src":"haiku"},
"shinedown~second-chance":{"haiku":"Accepting death as liberation, offering parents understanding rather than resentment.","opus":"A grown child telling their parents goodbye to go live their own life, insisting it's not anger but necessity. The comfort it offers is that leaving isn't loss but a second chance, delivered as gentle reassurance.","sonnet":"A speaker leaves home on their own terms — not in anger but in the quiet certainty that departure is the only way forward — asking parents to understand that sometimes goodbye is the beginning of the life you were meant to live.","src":"haiku"},
"shinigami~netherrealm":{"haiku":"Suicidal despair and numbness lead the speaker to imagine their own hanging as a confrontation with someone who caused their pain. Hopelessness becomes a demon to escape.","opus":"A harrowing plunge into self-harm and suicidal despair, the narrator numb and cold, blood on his skin, imagining a partner coming home to find him gone, cutting ties with venom while his mind fractures and demons escape. Shinigami works in the dark trap-metal and rage vein, its blunt, intimate horror and threat of never being seen again making the pain feel unfiltered and immediate.","sonnet":"Suicidal ideation weaponized as a final accusation — the speaker addresses someone whose cruelty pushed them to a breaking point, and the imagined scene of being found dead is offered not as a cry for help but as an indictment. The rage is directed outward even as the harm is turned inward, and the person addressed is explicitly named as the one who brought this into being.","src":"haiku"},
"shinigami~sacrifice":{"haiku":"Self-destruction becomes a final act of defiance against oppression and the temptation to mirror monstrosity. The narrator chooses dissolution over becoming the enemy while granting others the freedom to survive.","opus":"A broken, machine-like being left to die by a man who played God, granted the power to decide his maker's fate. Refusing to become the monster he was designed to be, he chooses self-destruction over revenge, giving himself into the light rather than repay cruelty in kind.","sonnet":"A malfunctioning machine of a person faces the man who stole their life, and instead of becoming the monster they were shaped to be, chooses self-destruction over retribution — sacrificing themselves into the light rather than crushing the perpetrator.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~besvikelsens-dystra-monotoni":{"haiku":"A suffocating landscape of anxiety closing in from all sides, where every bond and feeling inverts into its opposite—friends become enemies, love becomes hate, admiration turns to disgust. The song unfolds as a catalog of total emotional collapse.","opus":"A bleak descent into endless tunnels and thousandfold anguish, charting how everything turns to its opposite: friends into enemies, love into hate, admiration into disgust, and joy into sorrow.","sonnet":"A Swedish depiction of total emotional reversal in a bleak, tunnel-like landscape — friends become enemies, love turns to hate, admiration to disgust, joy to sorrow — an unrelenting descent into anxiety and loss on every side.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~du-mitt-konstverk":{"haiku":"A sadist uses their victim as a canvas to create art through violence and suffering; the act brings them pleasure and addiction.","opus":"The Swedish black-metal outfit, notorious for its depressive extremity, cast a killer as an artist whose creativity hungers for another life to snuff, destruction reframed as creation. A rusty razorblade becomes his brush and a victim's skin his canvas, one of countless he has ruined, the sight of suffering described as a drug, the closing pleas to be allowed to wound curdling aestheticized cruelty into something genuinely chilling.","sonnet":"A Swedish-language lyric voiced by a predator who frames inflicting wounds on a victim as artistic creation — a rusty razor blade the brush, skin the canvas. The pleasure is clinical and compulsive, described as an addiction. The narrator has done this many times and promises never to tire of witnessing suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~eradication-of-the-condition":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic vision of total destruction and the trampling of racial hierarchies, seeking salvation through universal annihilation.","opus":"Norwegian black-jazz staring into cosmic nihilism, salvation useless against creation's riddles, a vision of everything burnt down and racial pride trampled, ending on a stark refusal to be what's demanded.","sonnet":"A brief but extreme declaration of nihilistic self-annihilation — the narrator sees a vision of everything burned and trampled, rejects salvation and cosmic answers, and refuses to continue existing, treating extinction as both a golden opportunity and a final direction.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~f-rtvivlan-min-arvedel":{"haiku":"Despair is inherited and inescapable; the speaker sees only darkness and meaninglessness while their body screams for more of the destruction that defines their life.","opus":"A Swedish descent into total despair: the narrator sees only blackness, no faith, hope, or love, and despair is called his inheritance. Systematically destroying everything that ever mattered, he arrives with a manic smile at the answer to why he no longer wants to live, that nothing means anything.","sonnet":"A first-person account of complete nihilistic collapse: the narrator sees only black, declares nothing means anything, and arrives at a manically smiling realization that the reason he doesn't want to live is precisely that nothing at all matters.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~fisheye":{"haiku":"Numbered codes and symbolic imagery—eyes split between divine and demonic sight, sorcery and vision, linked to serpent and spiral—convey mystical knowledge and doubled perception.","opus":"A hypnotic incantation built from numbers, occult symbols and fragmented body-images: wheels turning, God's eye, the serpent, the split brain and the doubled self, resolving toward a chant of death. Shining, the Norwegian avant-garde outfit led by Jørgen Munkeby, blend blackjazz chaos with ritual imagery here, the counting refrain and mystical shorthand turning the track into something like a coded, malevolent spell rather than a narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~fullst-ndigt-j-vla-d-d-inuti":{"haiku":"A person mirrors and speaks but experiences complete emotional numbness; they numb hunger and thirst yet feel nothing as loved ones deteriorate around them—entirely dead inside.","opus":"A bleak Swedish confession of total inner deadness: looking in the mirror and seeing nothing, speaking and hearing nothing, tasting nothing, watching loved ones decay and feeling nothing at all. Shining, the Swedish depressive black-metal act led by Niklas Kvarforth, built their entire notorious catalogue around this kind of self-annihilating numbness, the repeated declaration of being completely dead inside forming a mantra of despair.","sonnet":"Total interior extinction — each sense goes dark in turn: looking in a mirror yields nothing, speaking produces no sound, eating produces no taste, watching loved ones decay produces no feeling. The Swedish lyric builds this catalogue of numbness methodically and then collapses it into a single phrase: completely dead inside.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~han-som-hatar-m-nniskan":{"haiku":"A person who hates humanity dreams of universal death and destruction, seeing beauty only in pulses stopping. The song expresses nihilistic contempt for all life and connection.","opus":"A Swedish hymn to a man who hates all that lives, his soul without color or nuance, dreaming of a world in flames, the song commanding death to everyone and everything and finding beauty only in the moment a pulse stops. Shining, the Swedish depressive black-metal band, name this the one who hates mankind, invoking Satan and misanthropic ruin with the cold, theatrical extremity that made them infamous.","sonnet":"A portrait of a man who hates humanity so completely that destruction is the only thing he finds beautiful — his life has no color, his soul no shade, and he actively wishes for the world to burn. The Swedish lyric frames this not as madness but as a kind of terrible purity: he wants to be somewhere other than here, and the only destination he can imagine is annihilation.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~i-nattens-timma":{"haiku":"A Swedish meditation on darkness and night as refuge, where the speaker finds solace in isolation and awaits answers that may never come.","opus":"The Swedish band, notorious for its depressive black-metal roots, sets a solitary vigil in the hour of night where the narrator feels most at home in darkness, urging a listener to leave the day's light and hear his verses. A noose lies coiled at his throat like a waltz carrying him toward night's realm, the untouched bed and finished poem framing an intimate courtship of death and rest.","sonnet":"A Swedish speaker finds his only comfort in the dark of night, waiting for answers to riddles that daylight cannot hold. A noose is described with strange calm as a waltz leading him into night's kingdom. He has been in torment each day and the bed is untouched — the poem is finished, and the time has come.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~idgaf":{"haiku":"Speaker rejects all stakes and morality, embracing nihilism and the certainty that death makes everything irrelevant.","opus":"A gleefully nihilistic party track about self-destruction with no consequences to fear because the end is coming for everyone anyway. Shining, the Norwegian outfit led by Jorgen Munkeby that shifted from jazz into abrasive blackjazz and industrial metal, plays it as a sneering, celebratory shrug at the void.","sonnet":"Liberation through total indifference — the speaker has had enough of caring, of bridges that needed burning, of people he didn't like anyway. Armageddon frames it as permission: if we're all dying soon, why not dance on the grave right now. The nihilism is gleeful rather than despairing, the burning bridges lighting his path forward rather than sealing his fate.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~l-t-oss-ta-allt-fr-n-varandra":{"haiku":"A bleak meditation on mutual destruction: the divine spark is stripped away, judgment falls without mercy, and the speaker finds grim comfort in shared doom and eternal flames.","opus":"A Swedish self-lacerating hymn urging mutual destruction, inviting an executioner to strip away all that is divine and consign everyone to the flames, interwoven with English lines of self-hatred and a hollow promise to be okay.","sonnet":"The Swedish lyrics call for total mutual destruction — stripping away divinity, joy, and self — while awaiting a holy executioner who will carry out a long-decreed sentence. The speaker finds sadistic comfort in the knowledge that everyone, believer or not, will be consumed by flames forever.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~m-nniska-o-avskyv-rda-m-nniska":{"haiku":"The speaker despises humanity—charity is disgusting, and they wish to erase this contemptible and hypocritical race.","opus":"Niklas Kvarforth channels his signature suicidal, misanthropic philosophy into total contempt for humanity, singling out compassion itself as the most sickening trait. This Swedish depressive metal makes hatred of the species a creed, not a mood.","sonnet":"A viciously misanthropic address — the narrator says every breath the other person draws is a fraction of what they hate about them, that they believe in death where the other believes in life, and that they want to find the path to eradicate the entire human race.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~neka-morgondagen":{"haiku":"Earth weeps, air darkens, sky blackens, and the heart grows cold from betrayal; all descends into an eternal tunnel of darkness.","opus":"A short, bleak descent: earth wet with human tears, air grim with darkness and death, sky black with dread, a heart cold with longing and betrayal, now carried off toward eternity down a tunnel of darkness where pain never ends, further in, gone. This is the Swedish Shining, Niklas Kvarforth's depressive black-metal vehicle, notorious for its unrelenting fixation on despair and self-annihilation; the title's refusal of tomorrow is its whole ethos.","sonnet":"A four-line descent — earth soaked in human tears, air heavy with darkness and death, sky black with fear and grief, heart cold with longing and betrayal. Then the fall into the tunnel of darkness, deeper in, gone. Six lines of Swedish that amount to the most compressed expression of total spiritual collapse: the world wrong in every direction, the self falling away from it.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~ohm-sommar-med-siv":{"haiku":"Time becomes liquid and malleable in moments of stillness; opening or closing one's eyes reveals truth or illusion in equal measure.","opus":"A meditative communion with time and nature, reading the future in a palm and hearing the past in the sea until time stands still. Closing both eyes to see everything, the speaker simply asks to be left lying, to rest.","sonnet":"In Norwegian, a person reads palms and listens to the sea — one eye closed sees half, both closed sees everything — and simply asks to be left lying still, existing between states of perception.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~pl-goande-o-helga-pl-goande":{"haiku":"A dark prophet invites followers to kneel and follow a path into torment and darkness, warning that mistakes haunt all regardless of attempted escape or blindness.","opus":"Sweden's Shining, led by Niklas Kvarforth, delivers a false-messiah sermon in their native tongue, summoning a little friend to kneel before a new savior and follow the path into a tormentor's cold black dark. True to the band's suicidal-black-metal provocation, the speaker preaches that a thousand mistakes shadow every step and offers death as the only relief.","sonnet":"A Swedish-language sermon from a false prophet invites a follower to submit to a cold messianic darkness. Every past mistake will shadow the listener forever, escape is impossible, and the preacher's final offer is death — framed as mercy for the listener and the world alike.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~svart-ostoppbar-eld":{"opus":"A Swedish declaration of forging a blade out of pure contempt to strike down one's own earthbound bloodline, reborn perfect and parentless from an unstoppable black fire.","sonnet":"A Swedish-language declaration of self-forging outside the corrupted human bloodline: the narrator has hammered a blade from passionate contempt to cut down the earth-bound tribe he descends from, reborn as a nomad without parents — pure, unstoppable, born of black fire.","src":"sonnet"},
"shining~take-me":{"haiku":"Surrender to the end with exuberance; racing toward apocalypse with unapologetic enthusiasm.","opus":"The Norwegian band's saxophone-driven metal turns apocalypse into a thrill, the narrator flooring it toward the end of everything with fists up and welcoming annihilation as release. He begs to be pushed over the edge, craving the raw feeling of leaving it all behind at full throttle.","sonnet":"A speaker at the end of the world, fist raised and weapons drawn, demands to be pushed over the edge — not from despair but from readiness, riding a red Corvette into apocalypse and begging to feel it fully before leaving everything behind.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~the-claws-of-perdition":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation described from the inside — old wounds still raw, hope slaughtered, compassion dead — the speaker has already mentally left a world they describe as ruins, and the refrain of \"suicide\" functions as both confession and destination.","opus":"A dispatch from Sweden's depressive suicidal black metal underground, this track wallows in the genre's darkest impulse: total surrender to despair as a kind of destiny. Old psychic wounds fester without hope of healing, compassion reads as already dead, and the self narrates itself as a world abandoned years ago. It offers no redemption, only ritualized descent.","sonnet":"Suicidal ideation as lived condition — old wounds still raw, hope and compassion slaughtered in the womb of misery, a dead stare into a world already left behind years ago. The imagery is of total psychic destruction culminating in a final solemn instinct toward death.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~the-eerie-cold":{"haiku":"Life inverted offers paradox: aging backward grants hope and miracle; death arrives unbought.","opus":"A dark thought experiment imagining life lived in reverse, born old and aging backward toward infancy so that every day brings hope and youth. The twist is bleak, the reward for this fantasy of never needing to end it yourself is that you finish as a baby who dies unknowing at the breast.","sonnet":"A spoken thought-experiment that inverts the natural order of life: what if we were born old, shed pain as we grew younger, and died as infants not knowing our life was ending — \"you just suck on your mother's tit and then you die\" — framed as a perverse source of hope.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~total-utfrysning":{"haiku":"Darkness descends over a dying land; something ancient and terrible awakens, bringing unspeakable dread in its wake.","opus":"A bleak Swedish evocation of darkness draping a dying land, leaving only a gray memory of the world's beauty. Something ancient and unspeakable stirs and rises from the depths, an atmosphere of encroaching dread and the extinction of everything lovely.","sonnet":"Darkness drapes itself like a ghostly shroud over a dying land, leaving only a grey memory of what was once beautiful, while something ancient and terrible stirs in the east — a horror too dreadful to name rises from the depths.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~vilseledda-barnasj-lars-hemvist":{"haiku":"A wisdom born from anguish crawls from society's rot; dead children speak truth so cold and merciless it makes life seem like a homeless joke.","opus":"A malevolent invitation to open one's eyes to society's rot and submit to Satan; the deeper read is that hard-won wisdom crawls only out of anguish, and the singer taunts the naive listener—who has never truly faced death—claiming truth is spoken from the gaping mouth of a child's rotting corpse.","sonnet":"A Swedish black metal song that commands the listener to open their eyes to society's rot and submit to Satan's wisdom — it mocks those who claim to understand suffering without having truly faced death, and ends with grim satisfaction at hearing the truth from a child's decomposing corpse.","src":"haiku"},
"shining~yttligare-ett-steg-n-rmare-total-j-vla-utfrysning":{"haiku":"Descent into existential dread and isolation, embracing doom as inevitable while dwelling in shadow and rejecting any false peace.","opus":"Opening with the nursery rhyme of a man who was never really there, it descends into a Swedish vow to stop resisting fate and instead embrace total isolation. The suicidal-black-metal frontman prays not for salvation but for the decay of life itself, loathing the very air he's forced to breathe.","sonnet":"A Swedish-language suicide meditation — the narrator recounts meeting a phantom self on the stair (Hughes's 'Antigonish' poem as epigraph), renounces all peace and denies their fate no longer, declaring hatred for all life around them and praying for life's own decay, each day a step closer to total self-destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"shinjuku-mad~resistor":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects false promises and soft manipulation, asserting that violence and fear communicate more truth than song and dance, condemning deception born from hatred.","opus":"A tense face-off in which the narrator dares an opponent to back up their claim to holy power, shrugging off miracles and insisting that violence exposes the truth better than any spectacle. He even confesses to loathing the killer instinct in his own veins, a soldierly fatalism giving the aggressive track its dark undertow.","sonnet":"Hardness as an ethical stance in a world full of tricks and soft promises — the speaker is unimpressed by anyone claiming miraculous power and insists that blood tells more truth than performance. Something deceptive is being pulled on everyone around them, and the speaker's response is to lean into their own internal conflict rather than soften into false comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"shishamo~a-e0u2t3":{"opus":"Shishamo made their name on plainspoken youth pop-rock about school days and everyday feeling, and this friendship song aches with pre-emptive grief. Sitting in a secret courtyard with female friends, the narrator already dreads growing up scattering them into separate lives as office worker, wife, mother, and wonders if they will still laugh together as they do over lunch now. The only dream is to come back here.","sonnet":"A school friendship song full of pre-emptive grief: the narrator sits in their secret courtyard spot with female friends and is already anxious that growing up will scatter them into different lives — OL, wife, mother — and wonders if they'll still be able to laugh together the way they do over lunch right now; the dream is simply to come back here.","src":"sonnet"},
"shockone~ready-to-go-feat-bright-sparks":{"haiku":"Certainty of desire transcends social judgment; bodies speak louder than friends' doubt.","opus":"A drum-and-bass seduction pushing past someone's hesitation, insisting their body wants this even as their friends warn them off. The euphoric hook is all about coaxing a reluctant yes on the dancefloor and beyond.","sonnet":"A push-pull seduction where the narrator insists the other person wants this despite what they say, their friends' doubts, or the image they project — just admit the body is ready.","src":"haiku"},
"shoko-nakagawa~sorairo-days":{"haiku":"Wounded determination persists despite past failures and endless sky stretching beyond reach and comprehension. The narrator discovers purpose in their own existence and chooses to build tomorrow despite relentless struggles.","opus":"An anime anthem about a voice once swallowed by darkness finding the will to build its own tomorrow. Pushed down by longing, the singer discovers the reason for being born in the steady beat of feelings that keep striking the chest, sure the answer has always been right here.","sonnet":"A coming-of-age declaration that the answer was always inside — crushed by admiration and near-defeat, the speaker starts running again toward the sky-blue horizon, guided by words someone once gave them, building their own tomorrow one step past yesterday.","src":"haiku"},
"shokran~nature-of-the-paradox":{"haiku":"Someone trapped by an internal evil entity struggles to regain consciousness and control, unable to see forward because they've lost their past reference points.","opus":"Mental illness pictured as a malevolent presence seizing control and wishing the narrator dead, driving him to beg for divine relief. The title's contradiction, a life with no future because its past is gone, gives way to a hard-fought escape as he wrenches himself free of the affliction and flees his buried wounds.","sonnet":"A mental entity — something like depression or psychosis — that the narrator cannot shake, that controls their movements and wants them dead, until a moment of clarity where they break free and bury both their heart and their scars to begin running away.","src":"haiku"},
"show-me-the-body~arcanum":{"haiku":"Despair in urban darkness where the speaker rejects love and hopes only for the occult card of mystery; visceral disgust with city life and lies mingles with a plea to find meaning before death.","opus":"A sleepless New York haze of estrangement and self-revulsion, the city cast as a hostile force with no affection to be found anywhere, a single tarot card the lone glimmer worth chasing. The band's hardcore bleakness keeps orbiting a warning to hold someone dear before violence claims you.","sonnet":"A fractured city-nightmare about alienation, worthlessness, and the proximity of death — the narrator prowls late at night, sees a city full of tombstones, and keeps returning to a tarot card (the Arcanum) as the one thing worth holding onto.","src":"haiku"},
"show-me-the-body~camp-orchestra":{"haiku":"Systemic control mechanisms disable agency while coercing performance; work becomes enslavement dressed as opportunity within disciplinary apparatus.","opus":"Using the horror of a concentration-camp orchestra forced to play, the song turns that image on the modern artist as a puppet made to perform, exposing the lie that work sets anyone free while exploitation grinds on.","sonnet":"Exploitation systems demand performance from those they control — the song moves through images of labour, imprisonment, and dehumanisation to argue that working within the system will never free anyone, invoking the Nazi slogan's inversion to underscore the lie.","src":"haiku"},
"show-me-the-body~dance-in-the-usa":{"haiku":"Survival demands dancing for scraps in a rigged system where youth perform for minimal wages while entropy spreads across the country.","opus":"American life staged as a rigged dance performed for wages and dues in a place where hell and city blur under the same sun. The command to dance for me exposes the exploitation, with the dead of the neighborhood never forgotten.","sonnet":"A sardonic indictment of America as a place where suffering and survival operate on the same terms: you either get exploited or you exploit, and either way you keep dancing — for minimum wage, for dues paid, for whatever scraps the system offers. The sun shining in hell and in the city are the same sun; the conditions are brutal and the only available response is to keep moving.","src":"haiku"},
"show-me-the-body~it-burns":{"haiku":"Internal combustion and helplessness define existence, where every action and word ignites suffering that can't be extinguished.","opus":"Speech itself is the wound that keeps burning; every utterance costs something in a world imagined as a cold deck of jokers and a tomb. The recurring skinhead-in-the-dirt image casts survival as clawing out of the ground, weakening but committed to the burn.","sonnet":"About the cost of speaking truth in a hostile environment — every word the narrator speaks ignites some kind of damage, the world is cold and full of fools who won't move, and staying alive means fighting your way out of the dirt every single day.","src":"haiku"},
"show-me-the-body~madonna-rocket":{"haiku":"Urban decay and loss define the speaker's world; when they meet someone good they want to die with them rather than face loneliness.","opus":"The New York hardcore band render grief and gentrification from street level, walking blocks where familiar faces recall old wounds as strangers' names get set in the sidewalk and unfamiliar towers loom. A stark vow to die alongside anyone worthwhile binds love to loss, mourning lost friends and a city sold off from under them.","sonnet":"Grief over a neighbourhood and its people disappearing — friends dead, names pressed into concrete, streets that once bled now bearing different architecture. Family is the only remaining anchor; strangers who are genuinely good prompt a desire for loyalty unto death. The city has been taken, and what remains is the decision to stay bonded to those still here.","src":"haiku"},
"shower-curtain~benadryl-man":{"haiku":"Pharmaceutical oblivion beckons as the speaker medicates toward disappearance.","opus":"A drug-induced hallucination becomes a domestic haunting: alone and nauseous, the speaker keeps seeing a man perched on the edge of the couch who lingers when they're away, a soul-sucking figure of decay and isolation who lives through others.","sonnet":"A hallucinatory figure — the Benadryl Man — perches on a velvet purple couch at the edge of the speaker's bed every night, present only when they're absent, a manifestation of soul-sucking personal hell. The nausea of the opening lines (burning, sour, thirty seconds coming up) suggests this is also the crash of a substance, the unwanted figure arriving with the withdrawal.","src":"haiku"},
"showing-teeth~labyrinth":{"haiku":"Intimate pain becomes architecture for closeness; the speaker wants to contort their skeleton to fit perfectly within another's hands.","opus":"A desire so total the narrator offers to be chewed up, hollowed out, and remade—breaking open his own skeleton to fit perfectly into the other's hand. Intimacy and self-annihilation entwine like a labyrinth of retraced paths.","sonnet":"A song about two people exchanging vulnerability in an intimate, physically intense way — the speaker offers to hollow out their chest and surrender their heart entirely, while simultaneously wanting to drink in the other person's pain; the connection is drawn as a maze of hands, skin, and breath that neither can fully escape.","src":"haiku"},
"shredhead~overshadows":{"haiku":"Betrayal and isolation shadow every attempt at connection while suffering becomes the price of belonging anywhere. The song questions whether genuine shelter exists or if only solitary darkness remains.","opus":"Betrayal by those you trusted, priests and monstrous idols alike, leaving the singer stranded in a party of one. Suffering gets reframed as treasure and belonging, the one spot he's ever fit, and this round his pain will hand no one else the satisfaction.","sonnet":"A dried lake whose fish-bone floor repels intruders serves as the central image for a barren inner landscape — the speaker, perpetually alone in the only place they ever belonged, endures the shadow that follows everywhere while refusing to let anyone use their pain as currency.","src":"haiku"},
"shrezzers~demure":{"opus":"A strike against group hypocrisy and hidden falsehoods, the narrator scheming to seize control while everyone maintains a polished front, and flatly rejecting the notion that their shot was gone. He clings to the belief that they kept attempting it over and over and victory was within reach.","sonnet":"A rallying cry against mass pretension and social hypocrisy — the narrator calls out people who perform goodness while lying underneath, and ultimately frames the fight as still ongoing: they tried and tried, they were beaten, but they could still have won.","src":"sonnet"},
"shrezzers~gambit":{"haiku":"Fractured perception and shame isolate the narrator in internal conflict while relationships dissolve through withheld truth. Freedom requires dismantling the protective barriers built to survive emotional annihilation.","opus":"A love collapsing into a game where no one wins, the singer left dealing with himself in silence and owning only shame. He wants to cut the ties holding on while wondering whether the beloved was ever real or just a make-believe living in his head.","sonnet":"A relationship in ruins is processed through the lens of personal liberation — cutting the ties that held on, the speaker seeks breath and meaning, acknowledging the heart-break while reaching toward freedom, even as longing for the other person keeps surfacing.","src":"haiku"},
"shteppi~i-hope-u-fear-me":{"haiku":"Parasitic obsession with a stranger escalates into fantasies of violation and possession, the speaker weaponising their devotion as a tool of domination.","opus":"A first-person descent into a stalker's obsession, admiration for another woman metastasizing into violent fixation and threat. The horror is that it opens as envy and longing to be her, then reveals the murderous compulsion underneath, terror as the goal.","sonnet":"A first-person portrayal of violent, obsessive stalking — the speaker expresses murderous and sexual fixation on a woman, describes hacking her accounts, collecting her personal information, driving to her home, and fantasizing about raping and killing her to make her fear them.","src":"haiku"},
"shuggie-otis~strawberry-letter-23":{"haiku":"Dreamlike sensory abundance and innocent love, a tender message of devotion.","opus":"A psychedelic-soul love letter awash in synesthetic color, love rendered as impossible gardens and cherry clouds. The bliss is the meaning, romance so overwhelming it can only be spoken in impossible images.","sonnet":"A love letter written in pure synesthetic sensation — the world dissolves into colour, sound, and texture as the speaker describes flying freely in a beloved's arms, transforming the mundane into a garden of hallucinated beauty.","src":"haiku"},
"shura~does":{"haiku":"Shame and desire coil together; adolescent infatuation breeds turmoil in the speaker's core. Beauty provokes madness; they ask to be consumed and destroyed, replacing emotional turbulence with sensation.","opus":"A Japanese scene of adolescent desire and inner turmoil ('shura'/carnage): street corners are the turf of girls, tempting scents and lashes stir a restless torment on a cloudless afternoon. Burning, smoldering thoughts pile up as he bows his head over overlapping faces; the counted pleas—'someone put out my fire' / 'someone light me, burn me'—swing between wanting the longing extinguished and wanting it ignited as he grows more adult.","sonnet":"Desire flares up watching a melancholy girl's lashes in the summer heat — the speaker's inner demon (修羅) stirs, restless — and the feeling oscillates between wanting someone to extinguish the flame and wanting to be set alight, as past memories thin out and the speaker matures into a duller, more adult version of their old burning self.","src":"haiku"},
"shvpes~afterlife":{"haiku":"A friend's near-death shatters the speaker's sense of safety and crystallizes the violence lurking beneath urban life; trauma fractures the continuity of living.","opus":"A frantic drive through the night to reach a friend bleeding out from a stabbing, the narrator racing hospitals and lights while grief and helplessness collapse the years back to childhood; the fight against death is one he can only witness, not win.","sonnet":"A narrator races through the night to reach a friend stabbed and bleeding out, recalling childhood together and now fighting time and hospitals in a desperate bid to keep them alive — a first-person account of grief on the edge of loss.","src":"haiku"},
"shvpes~lion-s-den":{"opus":"Escaping a controlling, abusive partner who treated him like a test subject, the narrator refuses to keep believing the fiction that their bond was ever peaceful. He resolves to forge his own path on his own terms, unwilling to owe anything further even if the cost is his life.","sonnet":"A declaration of escape from an abusive or exploitative relationship or institution — the narrator has been broken down and used as an experiment, but has reached the point where the other party's time is definitively up and they are leaving to define their own path.","src":"sonnet"},
"siamese~cities":{"haiku":"Collective human movement destroys natural beauty; cities wither as ecosystems die under human negligence.","opus":"A climate elegy disguised as a dance, the singer moving to a dark rhythm while the world scorches and the sea's life vanishes, insisting the blame falls on us alone. Siamese, the Danish band fusing alt-rock, R&B, and metalcore, wrap this environmental grief in a hypnotic, melancholy groove whose beauty only sharpens the loss.","sonnet":"Ecological grief danced into — the speaker misses the sea's bloom, the grass, the rain, all of it dying under the sun's stare as cities bake the earth. Humanity moves and dances while the damage accumulates; there's nobody else to blame, and the song insists on that. The collective self-indictment is given a beat you can move to, which is part of the point.","src":"haiku"},
"siamese~ocean-bed":{"haiku":"Searching the depths of self-identity while drowning in overthinking and uncertainty, apologizing for the worry caused by internal dissolution.","opus":"The Danish band, blending metalcore weight with pop melody, send their narrator to the sea floor as a metaphor for searching his own identity, stripped of pride and watching himself slip. Feeling too little and overthinking everything, he keeps apologizing to a worried loved one, weighing guilt and self-knowledge at a depth that resists measure.","sonnet":"A person sunk to the lowest point of self-knowledge — the ocean bed — digs through shame and identity collapse to find something to offer as apology. Pride is gone, the soul is relapsing, and the weight of feeling too little while thinking too much has left them stranded in an unreachable depth.","src":"haiku"},
"siamese~sloboda":{"haiku":"A nation questions its identity and purpose, wondering if it has surrendered its soul and conscience for material gain.","opus":"A meditation on national division and generational failure, sung partly in a Slavic tongue calling out freedom to a son, mourning a people who threw their soul down the drain, told they're to blame yet never seen as equal, asking whether they sold their soul for mansions and ashes. Siamese, the Danish genre-blurring band, wrap this reflection on inequality and lost potential in their melodic, atmospheric alt-metal.","sonnet":"A generational reckoning with a nation that has sold its soul — the song weaves a Serbian word for freedom through its refrain as an appeal, then catalogs the betrayals: a world that saw the speaker in black and white, leaders offering ashes instead of gold, and a collective moving so fast it is discarding its own humanity. The question at the end is whether the nation can slow down before it is too late.","src":"haiku"},
"siamese~the-shape-of-water":{"haiku":"Adaptive resilience becomes superpower; the speaker remains fundamentally untameable, flowing around obstruction and transformation through adversity.","opus":"Endurance rendered through liquid imagery: something that can't be bound, forever shifting, always surfacing like a breath however hard it's forced under or set ablaze. The vow never to shatter or hold a fixed form becomes a self built out of hardship, a person made stronger by their own misfortune.","sonnet":"An anthem of indestructible adaptability — from a drop to a wave, through fire and lions, the speaker cannot be chained or tamed, always finding their way back up like air, emerging from the bottom as the blood of the earth.","src":"haiku"},
"sianvar~substance-sequence":{"haiku":"A person escapes routine through substance use, drifting in addiction while toxic behaviors poison relationships, unable to break the destructive cycle.","opus":"The post-hardcore supergroup chart a descent into dependency with twitchy precision, sore limbs and shattered habits giving way to the drug's grip and mounting dread. A menacing tide of longing drags the narrator out to sea even as clear judgement stays ashore, the debris of use scattered around a scene where loved ones recede, the itch for another round drowning out any sense that the cost outweighed the reward.","sonnet":"Addiction rendered as a sequence of physical and psychological states — craving, paranoia, the dark pull of the ocean of compulsion, and the crash of coming down. The narrator describes a cycle of using, poisoning, and partial removal of whatever core has been damaged, surrounded by people watching and listening while the substance takes hold again.","src":"haiku"},
"sia~chandelier":{"haiku":"Dissociation through substance abuse masks pain while the narrator swings between reckless hedonism and desperate clinging to sobriety.","opus":"A party girl drinking to numb herself, swinging from the chandelier and living as if there were no tomorrow while morning brings only shame. Beneath the euphoria it's an alcoholic's desperate clinging on for one more night, refusing to look down.","sonnet":"A party girl numbing unbearable pain with reckless drinking — one-two-three, throw them back till she loses count — swinging from the chandelier and flying through the night while holding on for dear life until the morning brings shame.","src":"haiku"},
"sick-joy~all-damage":{"haiku":"Addicted to destructive intensity, the speaker worships numbness and sensation alike.","opus":"Dependency disguised as a glowing, electric romance that scorches and stings, every act of worship secretly a longing for the end. The candor is merciless: he understands it's wrecking him and confesses he has no desire to quit, smitten with the deadness and craving still more.","sonnet":"About being addicted to pain and self-destruction and not wanting to stop — the narrator is drawn to things that hurt them (a person, a substance, oblivion itself) and admits they don't think they can stop, and moreover don't want to; the damage feels like identity.","src":"haiku"},
"sick-joy~belly-aching-beast-feat-jamie-lenman":{"haiku":"Self-aware defiance despite internal collapse, accepting inevitable destruction rather than changing.","opus":"Depression rendered as something you refuse to hand over ownership of, defiance and self-destruction fused into one snarl: the narrator claims his damage as identity and dares survival to cost him everything.","sonnet":"The song is a taxonomy of depression as a permanent resident rather than a visitor — \"this is how your fucking depression is made,\" \"this is difficult and this is lifelong\" — and the speaker accepts neither cure nor escape, insisting on surviving even while dying, because the beast of self-imprisonment has become inseparable from the self.","src":"haiku"},
"sick-joy~death-scene-more-forever":{"haiku":"Nihilism and thrill-seeking converge in a death fixation, where the speaker chases that sweet spot of oblivion and senselessness.","opus":"Nihilistic hedonism as a coping mechanism, keeping quiet and chasing the high inside the hurt, no grief allowed at the graveside. Romanticizing one's own death scene as something eternal, it dresses self-destruction as the only lasting rush, half-hoping one day it'll finally be enough.","sonnet":"About finding a perverse pleasure in self-destruction as a shared ritual — the narrator and a companion lean into numbness, cheap religion, and the chemicals that 'save' them, chasing the moment of peak dissolution they call hitting the sweet spot of their death scene; it's glamorised annihilation as intimacy.","src":"haiku"},
"sick-joy~dissolve-me":{"haiku":"Two bodies merge into consumptive intimacy; teeth and touch dissolve boundaries as physical dissolution becomes the language of love.","opus":"Intimacy so total it becomes surrender, wanting to be dissolved by another's love with teeth in the shoulder and matching bruises. Getting to know someone is rendered as consuming and being consumed.","sonnet":"About the intensity and physicality of new love or attraction — biting, bruises left together, the mingled pleasure and pain of intimacy so consuming it feels like being dissolved. The narrator loved getting to know this person with their teeth in their shoulder, and the ask is to be unmade by the other's love entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"sick-joy~i-ve-got-more-than-i-need-and-i-don-t-have-much":{"haiku":"Existential sadness runs deeper than grief itself as the speaker withdraws from authenticity to survive, yet glimpses freedom in honest expression despite the cost of exposure.","opus":"Depression that insists no one else has ever felt this low, paired with a resolve to drag the buried pain into the sunlight. The stark turn is treating one's own demons like a loaded gun, aiming to kill them before they kill first.","sonnet":"A speaker exhumes buried grief — digging feelings out and laying them in the sun — while acknowledging a solitary sadness that feels unique and bottomless, resolving finally that triggers only have power if you give it to them, and so they set out to kill their own before being killed.","src":"haiku"},
"sick-joy~painkiller":{"haiku":"Dependency and numbness become the default state, with addiction the only relief from a reality too painful to face clearly.","opus":"Reliance treated as a loathed necessity, the deadening substance both pleaded for and despised in the same breath, a vice that keeps him alive while silencing him. The central hurt is that satisfaction is forever out of reach, and the deepest longing is to have never required the prop in the first place.","sonnet":"About dependency — not a specific drug but whatever coping mechanism keeps the narrator functional, whether substances, behaviour, or numbness; they know it barely works and wish they didn't need it, but the alternative is an unbearable inner silence they can't face.","src":"haiku"},
"sick-joy~sadisfaction":{"haiku":"Performative angst masks deep resentment; the speaker embodies a scene they've outgrown, trapped between the need for recognition and the knowledge it means nothing.","opus":"A performer resents being consumed as an aesthetic by fans who love the sound but never hear the person, so he stays depressed on purpose to keep them fed. Being someone's scene while remaining unknown is its own quiet cruelty.","sonnet":"A co-dependent dynamic where one person stays depressed and self-destructive to keep the other entertained or close — aware of the arrangement, saying it out loud but unable to leave.","src":"haiku"},
"sick-joy~stay-numb":{"haiku":"Survival through emotional anesthesia: both partners collapse into passivity and forgetting, employing numbness as a shield against disintegrating relationships and the self's own dissolution.","opus":"Two people getting through a slow mutual decay by agreeing to feel nothing, playing dumb and staying numb rather than facing the mess. Numbness offered as a sentimental vaccine, the only survivable way to stay together as everything changes.","sonnet":"Two people in a damaged relationship agree to go numb together rather than face the truth — \"we'll get through it if we both stay numb\" names the pact explicitly — circling self-deception and emotional suppression as a shared survival strategy.","src":"haiku"},
"sick-joy~talking-to-the-drugs":{"haiku":"Indistinguishable blur between person and substance; the speaker cannot determine if they're addressing their partner or their addiction, both equally hollow.","opus":"The impossibility of reaching a partner lost to addiction, unsure whether he's talking to the person or the drugs. The suspicion curdles into a harsher one, that the intimacy itself was always with the substance, never with him.","sonnet":"The speaker cannot tell whether they are addressing a person or their own substance use — the other keeps them half-alive just to stay warm, \"if you are Jack, I'm the fucking door\" — the question of who is real and who is the drug spirals without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"sicksense~feed-them-to-the-wolves":{"haiku":"Scapegoating and mob violence disguise exploitation, where the masses demand sacrifice while the powerful extract value and evade accountability.","opus":"A cynical anatomy of mob rule: the mob is hungry for blood, demands everything free, and devours whoever it once raised up. It reads the culture of grievance and instant outrage as a puppetry where one misstep gets you crucified and the wolves are never satisfied.","sonnet":"About mob dynamics and the impossible standards placed on anyone who tries to lead or contribute — the crowd thirsts for blood, demands everything without sacrifice, and crucifies people for a single misstep while the person on the receiving end becomes a puppet operated by imaginary strings.","src":"haiku"},
"sidewalks-and-skeletons~xxx":{"haiku":"Communal death wish merges individual void with collective negation; togetherness in darkness.","opus":"A single repeated wish to die alongside friends turns a nihilistic death drive into something almost tender, a preference for shared oblivion over surviving alone.","sonnet":"A single, unvarying death wish spoken in communal terms: the desire to be dead alongside friends, offering no narrative beyond the bleak declaration itself.","src":"haiku"},
"sierra-veins~desire":{"haiku":"Longing intensifies toward obsession as the speaker consumes what they cannot possess.","opus":"An obsessive longing rendered as helpless momentum. The narrator has spent hours dreaming and chasing thoughts of someone, and every attempt at concealment only stokes the craving hotter. Desire here is a fire that cannot be extinguished or retired from, and surrender becomes the only honest response to a pull that overrides reason and self-preservation alike.","sonnet":"A consuming, self-aware obsession: the speaker knows they've been playing with fire and are burnt, yet keeps running back. Hours of dreaming and chasing thoughts give the desire its texture — feverish, suppressed, intensifying precisely because it's hidden — with no exit offered and none sought.","src":"haiku"},
"sigur-r-s~flj-tav-k":{"opus":"An Icelandic hymn to sailors fighting a storm to reach land, finding shelter in a rescue hut as the gale rages. Sung in the band's Nordic vernacular, it turns bare survival at sea into something reverent.","sonnet":"A sea voyage in rough conditions ends with a landing on a remote shore, where exhausted sailors find shelter in an emergency hut and sleep through a raging storm outside — a miniature saga of hardship yielding to brief, grateful rest.","src":"sonnet"},
"sigur-r-s~hopp-polla":{"haiku":"Icelandic portrait of childlike joy—spinning, holding hands, jumping in puddles without shoes, nosebleeds, resilience and getting back up.","opus":"A radiant celebration of childlike joy, spinning in circles hand in hand, jumping in puddles without rubber boots, drenched and delighted. The throughline is resilience and love, even getting a bloody nose but always standing back up, the whole world blurring except the beloved.","sonnet":"Children jumping in puddles in the rain without boots, holding hands, dizzy from spinning — the singer smashes his nose running through the wind in the girl's hair, gets a bloody nose, and keeps getting back up.","src":"haiku"},
"sigur-r-s~s-lest":{"haiku":"Ethereal language dissolution into pure sound, Icelandic syllables untethered from meaning or narrative.","opus":"Sung in Vonlenska, the invented syllable-language Sigur Ros use as pure texture, so meaning lives in tone rather than words. Wordless by design, but built from real Icelandic fragments about movement and travel.","sonnet":"The lyrics are in Vonlenska (Hopelandic), a non-lexical invented language with no semantic content — pure phonetic and musical expression without translatable meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~14-needles":{"haiku":"Transformation through sensation and recklessness leaves visible marks; the speaker accepts the darkness as part of becoming something harder and less penetrable to the world.","opus":"A defiant, drugged plunge into oblivion where being hated feels good and self-destruction is embraced as identity. However the world contorts her, she remains recognizably herself, drawn closer to the edge she refuses to fear.","sonnet":"A speaker embraces pain and social rejection as markers of resilience and identity — the song is about pushing through discomfort, thriving on being disliked, and maintaining a calm, unwavering self-image even in the dark, while also acknowledging an uneasy proximity to the edge of self-destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"SIIICKBRAIN turns self-destruction into a flex, measuring out heaven in seven-second doses she already knows she'll misuse, so the swagger and the swoon toward a blue ceiling are one continuous gesture rather than two moods. The title's hardware doubles as ornament and dose — metal worn across the face as armor, and the thing sliding into the vein — until being unbearable to look at hardens into her entire defense. That's what the flat, repeated hook nails down: whatever the light does, the reflection stays fixed, because there is no second face beneath the first. The wiped-cloudy glass and the request to be shoved closer to the drop-off confess that the numbness she performs as strength is the only self left standing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance"],"means":"numbness performed as armor until no other self remains","built":"the flat repeated hook fixes the reflection while the self dissolves beneath it"},
"siiickbrain~destructible":{"haiku":"The narrator spirals into self-harm and addiction, slamming their skull into walls as trauma and hollowness consume them.","opus":"SIIICKBRAIN works the abrasive industrial edge of nu-metal, and this track jolts through dissociation and self-wrecking impulse, lurking figures at the glass and a mind like a poisoned junkyard. Each emotional blow sends the narrator battering their own head against a wall, once feeling untouchable and now spinning past control, the ritual mirroring the pull to relapse.","sonnet":"The collapse of the belief that one is unbreakable — trauma keeps pulling the speaker back to a state they have been trying to leave, and the only response available is a self-destructive reflex that proves the control they thought they had is gone. Underneath the speaker's own crisis, there is an awareness of someone else suffering, and a tentative offer to help them feel something.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~die-quietly":{"haiku":"Internal chaos builds pressure until collapse comes silent and alone. The urge to exit life whispers through nosebleeds and detachment.","opus":"Suicidal ideation voiced with eerie calm, the self as worst enemy and the dissociative pull of floating too high, wishing only to slip away without disturbing anyone.","sonnet":"A song about the desire to disappear without causing trouble — overwhelmed by intrusive thoughts, physical discomfort, and a sense of detachment from their own body, the narrator just wants to slip away silently, observing themselves from outside like a kite on a string, fading without drama.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~don-t-wait":{"haiku":"Time erodes connection and promises as empty words replace genuine presence and trust. The narrator withdraws rather than compound heartache through continued false hope and broken cycles.","opus":"A toxic relationship replayed through numbing highs and broken promises, the two synchronizing lies and demises. Walking away means telling the other not to wait up, the exit disguised as bravado about being better off alone.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship held together by mutual lies and endless over-analysis is ended by the speaker choosing independence — driving past the empty house and deciding to go it alone, they tell their former partner not to wait up, a farewell dressed as release.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~dopamine":{"haiku":"Toxic codependency mimics addiction; the speaker uses and is used, burning away identity in obsessive orbit while knowing the relationship annihilates them.","opus":"A toxic attachment rendered as chemical dependence, the partner a cigarette she is sick of but can't stub out, control already surrendered as if her soul were sold. Self-harm and dissociation shadow the compulsion to stay in something that keeps burning her.","sonnet":"A toxic dependency on a person is mapped onto the language of addiction: the speaker is both cigarette and addict, burning for days, hating yet unable to let go, wanting control they've sold away — \"I will be your nicotine and wrap your heart in dopamine.\"","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~emergency":{"haiku":"Sacrifice bleeds into pathological giving; the speaker offers their body, blood, and serotonin to feed the beloved while drowning in their own depletion and sensing inescapable emergency.","opus":"Being bled dry by someone who takes everything, invited to burn her up and siphon off whatever makes her feel alive until they've had their fill. The crisis is her own overload — surrounded and draining away while still asking them to leave a little of her behind.","sonnet":"A speaker who gives everything to those around them — fire for warmth, blood for thirst, serotonin stripped away — declares an emergency as they find themselves surrounded and depleted, knowing the other person will take it all exactly as expected.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~fear-god":{"haiku":"Religious fear and divine punishment, dread of higher power.","opus":"Agoraphobic dread meets spiritual paranoia in this industrial-tinged confession. Too frightened to step outside, the narrator retreats into the mind as the only free space, haunted by a watchful judgment over forgotten sins. Reckless self-destruction and religious guilt tangle together into a portrait of a generation feeling demonized and abandoned, hiding just to stay alive.","sonnet":"A confined narrator who fears divine judgment for moments they've forgotten, wants to live freely but stays protected and deprived — the collective is described as demonized and left behind, while others do or die at 200 mph on cocaine. Living freely and fearing God coexist without resolution, and \"stay alive, let's get lost\" is the uneasy compromise.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~fear-the-sun":{"haiku":"Light and gravity conspire to weaken bodies and bind existence to suffering and collapse. The song frames solar illumination as threat rather than blessing while darkness offers temporary refuge.","opus":"Falling apart and hiding in darkness because the light disorients and exposes, gathering the scattered pieces of the self only to lose them again. The dread widens outward to a poisoned, apocalyptic world of shootings and sedation, the singer feeling like the target.","sonnet":"Light itself is reframed as dangerous — in darkness we cannot be poisoned, and gravity is the true source of weakness, while above ground children are shot, pills keep people dumb, and the world spins the narrator toward apocalyptic collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~filthy-feat-fetish":{"haiku":"Filth and violence frame the speaker's arrival, bloodbath and transgression announcing their presence. Watching eyes roll back promises domination through degradation.","opus":"A snarling flex of nocturnal excess and threat, sleepless benders, drugs, and a warning that she bites back at anyone who steps to her. The bloodbath imagery weaponizes filth and menace into pure unbothered dominance.","sonnet":"Two voices trade controlled menace — drawing a bloodbath, eyes matte black, biting back hard at anyone who invades their space — the 'filthy' is directed outward as a label for those who approach without permission; beneath the aggression is a shadow figure the speaker wonders if they can escape.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~headspace":{"haiku":"Dissociation and pharmaceutical escape compound isolation; the speaker spirals through anxiety and numbness, reaching for absent connection while drowning alone.","opus":"A panic spiral of pills, liquor and dissociation reaching for an absent person as a safe place that's too far to help. The refrain is a losing plea against coming apart when things get hard, addressed to someone already gone to the stars.","sonnet":"Stoned, anxious, and afraid of the dark — someone in a dissociative spiral wants the person they love nearby as an anchor but they are physically far away, and the distance feels life-threatening.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~hellslide":{"haiku":"Destruction through hedonism; fire and lust burn while a person spirals, knowing they worsen with each cycle.","opus":"The alt-pop and industrial-leaning artist frames a toxic attraction as a drug she can't quit, tenderness and self-destruction fused so tightly she can't tell them apart. Each encounter leaves her a little more consumed, and though she recognizes the harm, she keeps choosing the plunge over the part of herself that knows better.","sonnet":"A person slides deeper into a destructive attraction with every encounter — the good side hides, the bad side wins, and numbness replaces feeling. Love here operates like a controlled substance, burning through warmth until the butterflies die and all that remains is the compulsive slide toward something that keeps getting worse.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~i-am-a-machine":{"haiku":"Mechanical destruction programmed into being; the speaker longs to resist but is wired for breakdown and violence.","opus":"A confession of love as damage. The narrator casts herself as something mechanical, built to destroy, insisting the harm she causes is inseparable from devotion. Imagery of suffocation and carbon monoxide frames intimacy as a room she cannot breathe in, capturing the terror of loving someone while fearing you are engineered to break them.","sonnet":"A speaker who knows they are built to break things down but doesn't want to — the machine's violence is framed as a distorted expression of love, hurting you because I love you. Carbon monoxide fills the room they can't breathe in, and the electric self runs on a logic it can't override, the self-awareness making the harm worse rather than better.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~idols":{"haiku":"Worship of false gods and celebrities, idolatry and blind devotion.","opus":"A furious teardown of false prophets and abuse dressed up as faith. Followers swallow the alibis, cruelty gets rebranded as virtue, and money and control hide behind scripture. The recurring counterfeit-scripture image skewers empty authority, indicting the hypocrites and bargain-bin believers who trade denial for power and call it holy.","sonnet":"A reckoning with false idols who cycle abuse and denial, blaming health for hatred, silencing those who speak against them — \"pawn shop people lost in denial.\" The chant structure refuses to explain; the repetition of \"fucked up idols\" is the verdict, passed down and left to stand.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~jealousy":{"haiku":"Jealousy and obsession poison connection as the speaker begs for clarity while their own heart bleeds from unrequited possession.","opus":"Consumed by jealousy and unable to read a lover's mind, the speaker fixates on the physical push-and-pull as the only proof of feeling. Every twist and loosened grip is felt in the body, desire and insecurity bleeding together.","sonnet":"Consuming jealousy over someone who won't commit — the narrator obsesses over what the other person is feeling, experiences the uncertainty as physical pain in their chest, and simultaneously craves and resents the power this person holds over them.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~parasite":{"haiku":"A toxic presence drains life force like a parasite embedded in the body, their voice an inescapable violation that leaves the speaker hollow and exhausted.","opus":"A relationship drains the narrator like something feeding on her blood, dressed in the language of a masochistic mind she can't shake. She wants the poisonous thought stolen out of her and buried, even as she keeps swaying with it.","sonnet":"A person in pain reaches out to someone who is too preoccupied to notice, while a voice — possibly internal, possibly from that person — tells them to calm their masochistic mind; the song is about feeling emotionally drained and gaslit by someone whose inattention is itself feeding off the speaker's suffering.","src":"haiku","fable":"The parasite of the title is not the draining other but the speaker's own mind, and the song's real move is to relocate blame from without to within — the person who sucks the blood from her veins is eventually revealed as a masochistic thought-pattern she hosts rather than a lover she suffers. Siiickbrain grounds this in raw somatic detail, the aching skin and shaking ribs and greying face, so that mental illness registers first as a bodily infection before it is named as self-sabotage. When the voice addresses her by her real name, Caroline, telling her to be easy, the intimacy curdles: the soothing command and the diagnosis of parasitism come from the same mouth, blurring whether she is being comforted or gaslit by her own interior. The chorus's plea to lift her mind out of her and inter it far underground, to swallow something toxic and rock in place, reframes escape as another form of self-harm, so the song circles without resolution because that circling is the sickness it depicts.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"blame for the draining shifts from the other to the speaker's own mind","built":"using her real name to comfort and diagnose blurs self-care and self-gaslighting"},
"siiickbrain~power-feat-pussy-riot":{"haiku":"Reclamation of power through physicality and dominance, refusing apology while asserting sexual and political agency against subordination.","opus":"A raw reclamation of agency through sex and confrontation, flipping submission into control; Pussy Riot's defiant verses widen it from the bedroom to political refusal, both women declaring they answer to no one and apologize for nothing.","sonnet":"A speaker reclaims agency from a dynamic of submission and provocation, insisting that vulnerability and pain don't preclude demanding power back — the Russian-language verses amplify this as an unapologetic, political assertion of self-determination.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~psychopath":{"haiku":"Predatory disconnection defines the speaker—they cannot feel, cannot be touched, and remain forever vacant even as they prey upon those who approach.","opus":"Trapped in a mind that feels like a prison, dissociating and going vacant while someone twists and rearranges her thoughts. The psychopath is as much her own hallucinating, numbing self as any tormentor, weakening by the season and warning others not to look too close.","sonnet":"A speaker whose mind has been twisted and whose eyes have gone vacant dares the world to ignore her — impossible, she insists — as hallucination and manipulation fuse into a state where freedom and the prison of her mind are the same thing.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~transcendence":{"haiku":"Internal contradiction and pressure build as the speaker oscillates between transformation and surrender, eventually accepting dissolution as preferable to continued fracturing.","opus":"A push-pull between wanting to be better and being pulled under by temptation, revenge, and pleasure, spiraling under pressure. The escape fantasy is total erasure, wishing to go missing as if never having existed.","sonnet":"A cyclical war between wanting to be better and being pulled under by pleasure, pressure, and revenge: the speaker spirals between the wish for transcendence and the fear of uncertainty, closing with a recurring hope to simply go missing — to vanish as if they never existed — as the only form of peace imaginable.","src":"haiku"},
"siiickbrain~zombie":{"haiku":"The song expresses obsessive dependency and chemical pacification. A person becomes the narrator's drug; without them comes withdrawal, but together they feed mutual insanity.","opus":"Addiction cast as a toxic romance, the narrator shutting out the world and pleading to be numbed into a compliant shell by a habit she can't drop. SIIICKBRAIN leans into her industrial trap-metal edge, framing the cravings and withdrawal as a sedative devotion that steadily erases the self.","sonnet":"A relationship framed as voluntary sedation — the speaker withdraws from the world and invites a particular person to be their numbing agent, surrendering agency in exchange for relief from anxiety. The attraction is described in pharmaceutical terms: injection, withdrawal, muscle knots when deprived. It is less a love song than an admission that this dependency has become the only thing keeping the speaker functional.","src":"haiku"},
"sikth~summer-rain":{"haiku":"Romantic dissolution arrives through the weather itself; the speaker constructed an impossible ideal that crumbles under real scrutiny.","opus":"The ache of loving an idealized version of someone you built up too perfectly, then watching it fail; the returning summer rain is the recurring pull back toward them, a longing that hurts a little less each time only because you're going numb.","sonnet":"A speaker built someone up into an ideal they could never sustain, and the inevitable collapse repeats — the other person keeps bringing summer rain, a hurt that becomes familiar enough to ache less, while the friendship inside the love is what the speaker misses most.","src":"haiku"},
"sikth~when-the-moment-s-gone":{"haiku":"Distance erases presence; the speaker grips recorded echoes of affection, unable to close the expanse.","opus":"Grief and self-loathing over a distant lost love, fixating on a saved voicemail and regretting not listening while drunk; the thousands of miles become both literal distance and the certainty that someone that good could never want a wreck like him.","sonnet":"A man who missed the moment with someone he loved — distracted, drunk, too slow to listen — replays their voicemail again and again, trying to recover what he let slip away, while the distance between them stretches into something unbridgeable. The song's fractured, rambling quality enacts the confused grief of realizing too late what someone meant.","src":"haiku"},
"silent-planet~antimatter":{"haiku":"Collision and merger annihilate separate selves into unified devastation; two broken bodies combust into mutual transcendence through catastrophic impact.","opus":"Metalcore turned to cosmic metaphor, this reaches for a collision so total that two broken people annihilate like matter and antimatter. It fuses catastrophe and intimacy, waiting in wreckage for a sunrise that may never come. The band's cerebral bent transforms devotion into physics: to fully meet another is to be undone and remade in the impact.","sonnet":"Two broken people colliding and becoming something new in the wreckage — \"in the impact, we become antimatter,\" dissolving from ordinary matter into something with equal opposite charge. The aftermath is decay and fracture, but also auroras and realignment; the song holds destruction and transcendence in the same breath, waiting in the wreckage for the sun to rise.","src":"haiku"},
"silent-planet~collider":{"haiku":"Collision and destructive impact between forces or ideologies, consequences of irreconcilable conflict.","opus":"A reckoning with a fractured relationship staged against a cold sky of dying satellites, the past an unhealed wound and promises rotting into betrayal. The narrator vows to transmute every curse into something bearable, Silent Planet's literary intensity turning wounded accusation into a strained hope that the person once known still survives beneath the damage.","sonnet":"Two people whose shared history is an open wound under the same sky — promises left to the moon, daggers still felt in the back, past and present colliding like meteors until the narrator becomes the very fears they tried to hide. The turn is an act of alchemy: every curse fed to them gets transmuted, dark turned to light.","src":"haiku"},
"silent-planet~euphoria":{"haiku":"False happiness and manipulated joy, euphoria as lie.","opus":"A harrowing meditation on addiction and intimacy blurred together, tracking the levitation of a needle-borne high against the ache of genuine human contact. Sirens fade, bodies intertwine, and the narrator wonders whether he is offering everything of himself. Silent Planet frames chemical oblivion and love as rival forces, both promising an eternal return that dissolves the self.","sonnet":"Addiction and intimacy intertwined in a needle's entry point — levitation and intoxication blur into a lover's presence, the tourniquet and the city sirens fade together, and the final image is two vessels intertwined as the sea receives the sky. \"Am I offering all of me to you\" is asked of both the drug and the person, and the answer is the same for both.","src":"haiku"},
"silent-planet~in-absence":{"haiku":"Memory dissolves as time erases connection, leaving only fragments of presence that slip away like sand; absence becomes a presence more crushing than any loss.","opus":"Watching a loved one slip away into dementia or death, the narrator becomes a memory eroding inside a mind that no longer knows him; the dread is of being forgotten by the very person he's losing, color draining to grey.","sonnet":"A speaker witnesses someone they love losing their grip on memory and self — likely to dementia — watching colour drain from the world as they try to name what remains, promising to describe what can no longer be perceived.","src":"haiku"},
"silent-planet~native-blood":{"haiku":"Colonization and environmental destruction are rooted in the same logic of dominance and extraction; the land itself remembers the violence done to its indigenous peoples.","opus":"An indictment of American empire built literally over Indigenous graves, tying colonial violence, ecological collapse and the flag's stripes-as-prison-bars into one bloodstain; the band's habit of writing from the victim's perspective makes the land itself testify.","sonnet":"A lament for Indigenous peoples buried beneath colonial structures — their bodies become the foundation of churches and political campaigns — while the speaker grieves the loss of land, language, and life, finding a thin thread of hope in the idea that suffering might carry purpose.","src":"haiku"},
"silent-planet~panic-room":{"haiku":"War trauma fractures consciousness into dissociative fragments where time becomes unreliable and self-protection demands the erasure of the self. The veteran cannot escape the theater of his own mind.","opus":"A soldier's PTSD rendered as an endless internal war, dissociating from a body and mind shattered by combat and reduced to currency by those who profit; the killing of a child at the wreckage becomes the unpayable debt that keeps dragging him back.","sonnet":"A combat veteran lives inside the ongoing neurological and psychological wreckage of war, where PTSD collapses the boundary between the Iraqi desert and a civilian bedroom, and the machinery of patriotism that glorified the violence now ignores the soldier it broke.","src":"haiku"},
"silent-planet~the-new-eternity":{"opus":"A dystopian alarm about technology and transhumanism as a plague of humanity's own making, where minds wired into machines build control and fear rules the masses. Reaching for the stars only carries our darkness along, yet the band plant a faint seed of hope that everything might still be remade.","sonnet":"A densely annotated environmental and political dystopia — contaminated water, engineered fear used to pacify populations, synthetic skies, extinction encoded in technology — arguing that humanity has sealed its fate by carrying its darkness into space and calling the resulting trap the new eternity.","src":"sonnet"},
"silent-planet~till-we-have-faces":{"haiku":"Someone crashes and surrenders to gravity and time's decay, finding grace in surrender. Bones break, silence rhythms like surrender, and they finally yield to consequence.","opus":"Silent Planet, the literate American metalcore band led by Garrett Russell, thread psychology, faith, and philosophy through a descent from mania into surrender. A grandiose high built on instability collapses as bipolar swings give way, plunging the narrator toward a reckoning with time and mortality. The C.S. Lewis title signals a spiritual yielding beneath the wreckage.","sonnet":"A freefall toward grace — the speaker crowned himself king of instability until the chemicals gave way and gravity took over. Falling at the speed of sound, he asks how anyone survives time itself, then surrenders completely: bones breaking against the earth, burying himself in the reeds, giving himself to consequence until something else — light, colors, absolution — ignites from the impact.","src":"haiku"},
"silent-planet~trilogy":{"haiku":"A machine-like consciousness fades to static, numb to human connection, fed on borrowed light like a parasite as bonds dissolve into disconnection and demise.","opus":"Dissociation pictured as a person dissolving into noise and machinery, deadened by the drone of technological progress until nothing recognizably human is left. The band's cry to stay quiet and stay human becomes a struggle against erasing yourself in the name of advancement.","sonnet":"A person consumed by digital disconnection and inner noise — fading into static, harvesting thoughts in dreams, watching themselves disappear while someone else watches, unable to rest or break free from the machine-like loop of their own consciousness.","src":"haiku"},
"silent-planet~visible-unseen":{"haiku":"Systemic oppression and religious hypocrisy exile a truthful voice into spiritual homelessness; love becomes the only currency that survives in a world built on lies and abandonment.","opus":"A love that has to hide, framed as heresy against a self-righteous faith that trades compassion for prosperity; the couple are exiled as pariahs, and the cruelest turn is waiting on a God who stays silent while she dies alongside him.","sonnet":"Two people, expelled from a community for honesty and nonconformity, navigate exile together — one thread mourning a lost loved one dying unacknowledged beside them while the other pledges that loneliness is shared and the road always leads home.","src":"haiku"},
"silent-planet~wasteland-redux":{"haiku":"God's death in constructed systems allows thieves and criminals to rule over hollowed landscapes of human wreckage. The song mourns abandoned spiritual anchors and the resulting wasteland of meaning and morality.","opus":"A prophetic indictment of a civilization that declared victory and enthroned a con man selling paradise without God, only to raise a wasteland. Turning rifles into gravedigging tools, it mourns a world that banished the sacred and now buries its own children in ever-deeper graves.","sonnet":"A post-war theological reckoning: a nation that built a temple to thieves and promised heaven without God has reduced itself to a wasteland of broken images, with the speaker as apostate witness watching the flag fall alongside the walls, and the only act left being to dig proper graves.","src":"haiku"},
"silent-planet~xx-city-grave-redux":{"haiku":"Human commodification and privilege hollow meaning; sisters buried as spectacles decay under toxins while bodies become graveyards.","opus":"A furious lament over the trafficking and commodification of women, the body made a graveyard for buried, thirsting souls beneath a hypocritical, controlling faith. It confronts a God imagined as male, asking how he can watch this hell from above while both love and wrath go unanswered in a supposedly free country.","sonnet":"A woman's body made into a graveyard by exploitation and systemic violence — \"my body became a graveyard where they buried thirsty souls\" — speaks to a God figured as a man and asks whether love can save her or wrath can avenge the enslaved in the land of the free, where apathy anchors everyone to a digital sea of complicity.","src":"haiku"},
"silly-goose~give-me-my-money":{"haiku":"Financial exploitation and desperation drive criminal action; an artist cornered by the system fights back against theft of labor, willing to burn everything for fair compensation.","opus":"A ranting, desperate demand for owed money framed as a heist and a war against the industry that shorts artists; the narrator cooks the books, threatens to tear the venue down, and rages onstage about fees, cuts and being bled dry by promoters.","sonnet":"A musician running a stealth operation against a venue or promoter who has shorted them — money so tight they're sneaking product in the back door, threatening to tear the place down if they don't get paid. The anger is righteous and personal, mom's car and mortgage mixed in with the fury, the power imbalance named clearly: you hold the leverage, you're bleeding me dry.","src":"haiku"},
"silly-goose~king-of-the-hill":{"haiku":"A young ruler climbs the playground hierarchy through dominance and cruelty. Power becomes sport—taking and destroying all challengers.","opus":"A hyperactive playground power fantasy that turns schoolyard king-of-the-hill imagery into a manic, bratty flex, climbing toward the sun and daring everyone to keep up.","sonnet":"A braggadocious playground-as-warzone fantasy — the narrator casts themselves as the dominant force on a school yard, mocking peers, teachers, and snitches, using the jungle gym as a metaphor for social hierarchy and declaring themselves the apex of it all.","src":"haiku"},
"silly-goose~traffic":{"haiku":"Urban congestion mirrors mental paralysis; the speaker's rage builds watching others move slowly while desires for speed and transcendence go unfulfilled.","opus":"A comic road-rage rant that turns gridlock into a survey of everyone the narrator despises, from entitled SUV drivers to rednecks. The self-aware jab about not alienating the fan base makes the whole tantrum a bit of a bit.","sonnet":"A comedic rant about the frustration of being stuck in traffic — the narrator catalogues the terrible drivers around them (entitled SUV woman, oversized truck guy), threatens minor violence, and mostly just wants everyone to get out of their way. The emotional core is pure petty annoyance dressed up as a character study of driving culture.","src":"haiku"},
"silos~insatiable":{"haiku":"Obsessive hunger drives an endless chase for something unfillable, leaving only desperation and self-consumption.","opus":"A study of fixation as an endless appetite. The narrator's thoughts never settle, resting beside a primed weapon, hunting a dreamed-up someone imagined as the part that completes him. No amount of pursuit patches the emptiness, and longing sours into jealousy and self-hatred, capturing how craving fools a person into treating another human as a remedy.","sonnet":"Obsession that devours itself — the narrator is always searching, sleeping on a loaded gun, fantasising of a world with their missing piece, but the hunger cannot be fed and every pursuit leads to nothing. The insatiability is both romantic fixation and a deeper psychological void; the demons keep coming because the hole is the point.","src":"haiku"},
"silos~insatiable-blvck-crowz-remix":{"haiku":"An obsessive hunger that devours the self, a pursuit that finds no satisfaction even as demons and darkness tighten their grip, spiraling toward a longing that only breeds emptiness.","opus":"Obsession dressed as romance, where the wanting itself is the disease; the pursuit fills no hole because the hole is the point, and the demons doing the chasing were never going to be satisfied by a person.","sonnet":"An obsessive, consuming fixation on someone who is absent or unattainable — the speaker is driven by a hunger that can never be filled, demons pushing them relentlessly toward the object of their desire, with flashes of aggressive territorial rage breaking through the longing.","src":"haiku"},
"silver-fang~labyrinth":{"haiku":"Inescapable complexity traps the speaker in endless passages; navigation becomes impossible.","opus":"The wreckage of psychological control, mapped as a maze etched into the brain. Another's speech bored in like surgery until the ability to think for oneself rusted away. The recurring claim that mistreatment is fine as long as it comes from affection lays bare the cruel deal of being sold off, cheaply, by someone posing as loving.","sonnet":"The aftermath of psychological control — someone's words drilled in like a lobotomy, removed autonomy, left corrosion in the veins; the survivor keeps asking \"if it's love, then it's okay\" with obvious ambivalence until the final indictment: \"you sold my soul for your weight in gold.\" The labyrinth in the title is the scar tissue of the relationship, still impossible to navigate.","src":"haiku"},
"silverchair~freak":{"haiku":"A social outsider embraces his status as a freak, struggling with alienation while envying those who fit in.","opus":"A sardonic ode to teenage estrangement, the singer labeling himself a natural-born oddity, too dull to fake being fashionable, sneering that he wishes he could match everyone else's ease. Silverchair, the Australian trio barely into their teens at their breakthrough, cut this for 1997's Freak Show, Daniel Johns spinning post-grunge unease into a self-mocking hit about never belonging.","sonnet":"Social alienation worn as a badge and a wound simultaneously — the speaker cannot perform coolness, has no interest in fitting in, and views the fashionable diseases of conformity with contempt. But the longing beneath the defiance is visible: if only they could be as cool as everyone else, effortless and approved of. They cannot, so they claim the freak identity instead.","src":"haiku"},
"silverchair~israel-s-son":{"haiku":"Violent hatred and murderous impulse consume a speaker who feels disrespected and powerless, channeling pain into threats and dominance.","opus":"A teenage frontman channeling pure adolescent rage and hatred into a menacing execution fantasy; the biblical title gestures at inherited violence, but the song is less about doctrine than the raw, unhealing anger of youth with no outlet.","sonnet":"Pure, undiluted rage directed at an unnamed target, stacking threats of execution and violence onto a refusal to heal. The narrator positions his pain as real and lethal, and the song ends with a declaration of identity that frames the violence as almost ancestral.","src":"haiku"},
"silverchair~point-of-view":{"haiku":"Perspective shifts and ambiguity govern understanding, as accusation and pretense reshape reality through reframing.","opus":"A jagged plea about depression and self-harm answered by the flimsy reassurance that a change of perspective will get you through; the drowning, dying imagery undercuts that platitude, showing how hollow easy advice sounds to someone actually sinking.","sonnet":"A loosely surreal address cycles through accusations, self-harm imagery, and exhortations to shift perspective, suggesting that reality is plastic and survival requires a willingness to adopt a new angle of view. The logic is fractured but the mood is insistent: keep changing what you see.","src":"haiku"},
"silverchair~straight-lines":{"haiku":"Desperation and alienation compound until the speaker walks the sole path available—a rigid line of survival where deviation means collapse. Morning clarity and fever merge into determined endurance.","opus":"Recovery framed as the plain discipline of holding a steady course, a frantic optimist clawing back from illness and isolation toward the promise that it'll all work out.","sonnet":"A speaker coming through a period of inner collapse and self-erasure — feeling invisible, desperate, stuck to the marrow — chooses a deliberate path of recovery, walking in a straight line as an act of stubborn, imperfect faith that everything will be fine.","src":"haiku"},
"silverchair~tomorrow":{"haiku":"Wealth and status cannot insulate against decay while mortality waits, the speaker trapped in present degradation despite all material superiority.","opus":"Written when the band were teenagers, a class-war taunt aimed at a pampered rich man dragged down to see how the poor really live, daring him to survive without his money and privilege.","sonnet":"A sardonic address to someone comfortable in their privilege, inviting them to visit a place of deprivation and then mocking the idea that money isn't everything. The fat-boy refrain circles back to a confrontational taunt: their comfortable assumptions will be corrected, but only tomorrow.","src":"haiku"},
"silverstein~bankrupt":{"haiku":"A king of vanity destroys everything and sells out for nothing, always remaining spiritually bankrupt. Money and time move backwards while the world consumes everyone equally.","opus":"A furious swipe at greed and the vain idols who hoard fake wealth on the backs of the poor, refusing to grovel to power and insisting no fortune outweighs a human life in a rigged system. A borrowed Oppenheimer line about becoming destruction sharpens it. Canada's veteran post-hardcore band Silverstein drive this class-rage anthem hard.","sonnet":"A takedown of wealth as moral emptiness — the king of vanity, the lord of hostility, accumulating until there's nothing left for anyone, destroying while the balance sheet looks fine. Moral bankruptcy doesn't show on paper: stock options offset loneliness, fake fortunes ride on the backs of the vulnerable, and the game is rigged from the start. The speaker refuses to bow to it and is waiting, furious, for an answer.","src":"haiku"},
"silverstein~don-t-let-me-get-too-low":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation requires external intervention to prevent self-destruction, as isolation amplifies the speaker's inability to self-regulate.","opus":"A plea to a trusted person to catch you before depression drags you all the way into a self-dug grave. The singer can't separate want from need or reckon how he sank this far, leaning on someone to haul him out when the dark is everything in view.","sonnet":"The speaker, already in a grave of their own digging, asks someone who knows them well to keep pulling them out before the spiral takes them all the way down — fear of solitude and inability to distinguish need from want drive the quiet plea.","src":"haiku"},
"silverstein~infinite":{"haiku":"Mental spiraling and racing thoughts create a feeling of endless anxiety and pressure that resists optimism, leaving the narrator trapped in circular despair.","opus":"The long-running post-hardcore band renders anxiety as something with no off switch, thoughts racing beyond any means of stopping them. The narrator strains just to breathe and to keep a shred of optimism while dread and pain keep returning, the title standing in for both the enormity of that suffering and its refusal to end.","sonnet":"Overwhelming anxiety makes darkness feel permanent — the mind races, optimism fails, and the weight of simply existing stretches without horizon. The speaker tries to find footing, draw lines, erase half-truths, but the pressure returns each time. The song is an honest admission that some pain genuinely feels like it will never end.","src":"haiku"},
"silverstein~misery":{"haiku":"Companionship in suffering provides the only genuine solace—peace arrives through acceptance of eternal anguish rather than escape.","opus":"Finding a strange peace inside misery itself, since company in suffering is better than being alone. Measuring pain in milligrams and unable to escape destiny, the singer stops fighting the sea of grief and lets it be his refuge.","sonnet":"A relationship's end opens a wound measured in milligrams of pain; the speaker, vivid-dreaming in crimson and pale white, cannot restart but refuses to be swallowed — 'I can find my peace in misery' resolves the contradiction by accepting the condition as habitation rather than defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"silverstein~poison-pill":{"haiku":"False remedies proliferate while disease deepens; the speaker overdoses on placebo solutions, knowing the medicine itself has become the poison.","opus":"A relationship and a psyche both mistaking the cure for the poison, gorging on a sugar pill while pursuing the phantom of a feeling. Everything's a performance for the lens, rehearsed lines spiraling down with no genuine connection left.","sonnet":"About the paradox of coping mechanisms becoming the problem — the medicine is the poison, the cure is the addiction, and the placebo we keep overdosing on is whatever false comfort we use to avoid confronting that we are circling the drain together. The song diagnoses a shared dysfunction in a relationship or in a culture where everyone performs wellness for the camera while bleeding internally.","src":"haiku"},
"silverstein~slow-motion":{"haiku":"Emotional paralysis drains all sensation and hope, trapping consciousness in deafening silence; the speaker surrenders to slow obliteration as the only escape from exhaustion.","opus":"A veteran post-hardcore band renders depression as suffocation in cement, life dreamed in black and white while sirens scream inside the skull. Silence turns deafening, hope feels manufactured, and the narrator confesses to letting a slow paralysis kill him. It sits with powerlessness rather than fighting it, dying by degrees in a numbed, deafening quiet.","sonnet":"A portrait of paralysis under depression — the last match burned out, buried in cement, sirens looping in the head, and no god answering. The speaker does not resist the silence but lets it kill them in slow motion, framing passivity itself as the final act: worn out, wide open, powerless.","src":"haiku"},
"silverstein~the-ides-of-march":{"haiku":"A haunted stalker freezes outside his object's window, caught between devotion and violation until betrayal calcifies into permanent spectral anguish.","opus":"An obsessive, stalker's fixation dressed as devotion, the narrator lurking outside a window unable to recover or move on; betrayal has warped him into a haunting presence who mistakes surveillance for love and can never return to who he was.","sonnet":"A person consumed by obsession stands frozen outside someone's window in winter, unable to move forward or backward from a betrayal that has permanently altered them — haunted by their own ghost-like presence and the impossibility of recovery.","src":"haiku"},
"silversun-pickups~cradle-better-nature":{"haiku":"Calm persists despite loss; cradles and headstones hide in watermarks while minds tighten until better nature overwhelms and subsumes.","opus":"Asking a struggling person to reach for their kinder self while a rising tide threatens to swallow birth and death alike. It sits between offering help and doubting them, hanging on through a mind wound tight when they most need care.","sonnet":"An appeal for someone to summon their better nature — hide the cradle against the coming sea, keep their head when the mind is in a tight knot — repeating the ask with increasing urgency as if the only thing that can hold the flood back is a choice the other person keeps not making.","src":"haiku"},
"silversun-pickups~panic-switch":{"haiku":"Anxious self-questioning in social spaces where the speaker struggles to fit while fading and floating away, caught between conformity and release.","opus":"LA dream-rock band Silversun Pickups, often compared to Smashing Pumpkins, build tension around being watched, losing oneself, and the pressure to conform. The narrator wonders if he could reshape into whatever's demanded, while the chorus interrogates whether a cornered, twitchy person will fall into line or let their instability loose.","sonnet":"Dissociation and chronic anxiety shadow every crowded room — the itching need to either conform or break the pattern, the inability to sleep, the fading sensation of self dissolving into white noise. The panic switch is the internal trigger that turns ordinary presence into unbearable alertness.","src":"haiku"},
"simon-garfunkel~mrs-robinson-from-the-graduate-soundtrack":{"haiku":"Seduction corrupts propriety as a younger man and older woman transgress social boundary, betrayal masked by intimacy and suburban sophistication.","opus":"Beneath the breezy chorus sits a portrait of postwar American hollowness, a bored suburban wife's hidden affair and a nation that has lost its heroes; the plea for a vanished DiMaggio mourns a lost innocence no candidate or prayer can restore.","sonnet":"A woman living a life of comfortable secrets and social performance is addressed with ironic deference — blessed by Jesus, noted for prayer — while the society around her cycles through candidates and loses its heroes. The disappearance of Joe DiMaggio stands in for a broader loss of uncomplicated American idealism, and Mrs. Robinson is both symptom and casualty.","src":"haiku"},
"sim~existence":{"haiku":"The narrator rejects moral binaries and societal emptiness, insisting only they can save themselves and refusing to surrender to decay.","opus":"SiM, the Japanese reggae-influenced metalcore band, weave Japanese and English through a defiant reckoning with decay. Rejecting despair and easy moral binaries, the narrator insists that only he can rescue himself and that being true to who he is matters most. He refuses to bank on the future, aware that time can't be undone no matter what it's worth.","sonnet":"A multilingual reckoning with stagnation and the will to self-rescue — repeating days with no sense of wrongness, rot setting in while the speaker waits for the sun to rise again. The core conviction reasserts itself through the decay: only I can save myself, all I want to be is me. Time won't turn back; the rainbow only comes after rain; and the speaker keeps breathing, holding that last thread of hope, refusing to give it up.","src":"haiku"},
"sink-with-me~misfortune":{"haiku":"Lifelong isolation and misfortune as inevitable curse, haunted by failed potential and inability to escape.","opus":"A lament of lifelong loneliness and inherited bad luck, wondering whether another life would have spared all this pain. Buried parts of the self wait for a rescue that never comes; someone's indifference wrecked everything, and the bad luck lingers like a stain that won't scrub off.","sonnet":"A life lived in perpetual abandonment, pieces of the self buried under floorboards, staring out windows waiting for a rescue that never arrives. Misfortune clings like a haunting memory; the final image is trying to wash someone else's touch off the skin — the damage is not abstract but embodied.","src":"haiku"},
"siren~jenga":{"haiku":"Precarious balance where removing one piece collapses everything; tower of emotional fragility.","opus":"Drowning as metaphor for a self dissolving under resignation, sinking in a bathtub-ocean unable to recognize who you are. The stubborn question underneath: if the self keeps peeling away, is simply being alive enough?","sonnet":"A dissolution of identity into dark water — the self peels away, the narrator floats blindfolded in a bathtub-ocean unable to answer who they are or who the other person is, struggling with whether to keep reaching toward the light on the surface or accept that what remains after the stripping away is all there is.","src":"haiku"},
"sisters-of-mercy~i-was-wrong":{"haiku":"Cynical retreat from intimacy, recognizing emotional fraud in promises of permanence and togetherness.","opus":"Andrew Eldritch's cold post-breakup ledger, admitting he doubted a lover then curdling it into a wider misanthropy: he can love humanity in the abstract but not the specific people who wound him, watching others' pain with detachment.","sonnet":"Detachment from a relationship full of empty promises — the narrator doesn't believe what the other person says, has decided they can survive without them, and expresses a cold, sardonic worldview: pain looks better on other people, and they'd rather not talk about any of it.","src":"haiku"},
"skai-isyourgod~blueprint-supreme":{"haiku":"A swagger-heavy Cantonese rap about ambition, survival, and street culture—celebrating risk-taking and old-school hustle over morality.","opus":"A Cantonese trap flex steeped in Guangdong pride and triad-movie swagger. Over villa karaoke and silver arowana fish, the rapper receives four auspicious calligraphy characters wishing grand ambition, then threads through dim-sum stalls, Cantonese opera, and street-hustle bravado. It plants a flag for southern Chinese identity, rapping in Cantonese in Beijing and refusing to be looked down on.","sonnet":"A Cantonese rapper describes a mentor giving him a calligraphed blessing ('great ambitions realized') in a villa, and uses it as a springboard to declare his Guangzhou street credentials, loyalty to Cantonese culture and rap, and contempt for the greedy and unrighteous — interweaving local food, dialect, vintage Canto-pop references, and neighborhood landmarks as proof that ambition rooted in authentic community is the only kind that lasts.","src":"haiku"},
"skating-polly~camelot":{"haiku":"A woman seeks transcendence beyond her mundane marriage and suburban illusions, wanting something better than the false fantasy her husband offers while drowning in discontent.","opus":"A woman wants something better than a shallow west-side man yet keeps drinking and settling for the Lancelots and suburban dads. It skewers the way idealized romance collapses into domestic misery and the resentment of being measured against a partner's wants.","sonnet":"A woman trapped in suburban domestic life quietly craves something grander and truer — Camelot against the backdrop of Calvin Klein briefs and Jeep moms — drinking and crying her way through the gap between fantasy and the small, consuming reality she chose.","src":"haiku"},
"skating-polly~hickey-king":{"haiku":"Transactional physicality and casual cruelty dominate; the speaker marks and dismisses a lover with egotistic indifference, treating connection as a stamp to be collected and forgotten.","opus":"A girl inverts the power of a careless hookup, crowning the boy with a mocking title while insisting the mark she left was purely egotistic and meant nothing. The bravado covers the fact that she drove three hours and got a sunburn for someone she's pretending not to care about.","sonnet":"A post-hookup self-assessment where someone drove hours for a boy who was not worth it, acknowledges the bruise he left, and decides it meant nothing — treating the whole thing as a cheap, disposable ego game.","src":"haiku"},
"skee-lo~i-wish-radio-edit":{"haiku":"Material lack and social hierarchy breed sexual frustration and shame; the speaker obsesses over status symbols and romantic access while trapped in the machinery of class and image.","opus":"A short, broke, overlooked kid catalogs everything he lacks, from height to a girl to a decent car, wishing his way toward the life the ballers get. The humor barely covers real ache about invisibility and coming up short.","sonnet":"A cheerful lament from a short, undateable guy who fantasises about being six-foot-nine, driving an Impala, and winning the girl who ignores him in real life; the wishes spiral outward through basketball benches, flat tires, and Crenshaw cruising, all held together by the candid admission that the world favours the tall and the balling.","src":"haiku"},
"skegss~spring-has-sprung":{"haiku":"Summer hedonism and substance use numb the narrator's capacity for romance; intoxication offers escape while simultaneously preventing genuine connection.","opus":"Sun-drenched surf slack about summer hedonism tipping over its own edge, where a good time chemically overshoots into being too wrecked for the romance the season promised; carefree on the surface, faintly rueful underneath.","sonnet":"A speaker rides the euphoric peak of a pill on a warm night, feeling invincibly good but too intoxicated to act on any romantic impulse — the drug has delivered the feeling without leaving room for anything else.","src":"haiku"},
"skeletonwitch~strangled-by-the-unseen-hands":{"haiku":"Time consumes all life and resistance through patient inevitability that no force can halt or overcome. The song personifies temporal passage as an inescapable assassin that returns all matter to dust.","opus":"Time itself as the flawless, undefeated executioner that hunts everything living and grinds it back to dust. No god or devil can stop its advance — every one of us is fated to be throttled by a grip we never see coming.","sonnet":"Time is personified as a flawless, unseen killer that no god or devil can stop — it stalks everything, strangles all flesh and bone back into dust, and the only certainty is that we are all doomed to return to the dirt.","src":"haiku"},
"skeletonwitch~submit-to-the-suffering":{"haiku":"A torturer's fevered mind feeds on inflicting suffering—burning flesh, watching victims beg for death, lust overtaking reason. The song treats torture as a dark calling that consumes the perpetrator's every thought.","opus":"A first-person plunge into sadistic torture fantasy, the narrator hungering to burn and cut flesh, feast on screams and reduce a begging victim to something dehumanized, unable to stop the visions of anguish flooding his mind. Skeletonwitch, the Ohio blackened-thrash band, deliver this with their signature venomous riffing, embracing the gleeful cruelty and horror-movie extremity that fuels their brand of extreme metal.","sonnet":"Pure torture fantasy with no metaphorical distance — the speaker catalogs violent desires directed at a victim in clinical detail, framing pain as pleasure and suffering as the ultimate aesthetic experience. There is no psychological justification offered; the song inhabits the perspective of someone for whom cruelty is an end in itself.","src":"haiku"},
"skid-row~18-and-life":{"haiku":"A young man's violence and recklessness lead to accidental death and eternal incarceration, his entire life reduced to decades of confinement for a moment's mistake.","opus":"The cautionary arc of a hard-luck kid raised on neglect and violence who drunkenly kills someone and gets locked away for good; the hair-metal swagger frames a genuinely bleak story about how a broken start becomes a life sentence.","sonnet":"The story of Ricky, a hard-luck kid from the edge of town who grows up on rage and alcohol and ends up shooting someone in a moment of recklessness, sentenced at eighteen to a life defined by his crime.","src":"haiku"},
"skid-row~psycho-love":{"haiku":"Love transforms into psychological torment where sweetness harbors corruption, the lover as both ghost and vampire draining essence while offering only darkness.","opus":"Glam-metal sleaze dressed as horror, an obsessive erotic haunting where lust and self-destruction blur into the same fever, the woman a drug the narrator can't tell is saving or killing him.","sonnet":"A speaker is enthralled by a dangerously unstable woman — drug-addled, spiritually decayed, somewhere between angel and devil — whose destructive pull he cannot resist. The attraction is framed as mutual poisoning, a haunted house of pain they both inhabit.","src":"haiku"},
"skid-row~youth-gone-wild":{"haiku":"Young rebels stand unified against authority, scorning conformity and societal expectations that demand obedience. They refuse to bend, asserting their solidarity and unbreakable will as 'youth gone wild.'","opus":"A late-eighties glam-metal anthem that turns teenage delinquency into a badge of honor, rejecting the button-down corporate future its elders demand and finding pride and solidarity in being the outcasts everyone wrote off.","sonnet":"A defiant anthem for kids written off as problems by a conformist adult world — the narrator refuses the Wall Street path, claims solidarity with every other misfit, and insists the youth gone wild will stand united rather than fall.","src":"haiku"},
"skillet~awake-and-alive":{"haiku":"Spiritual crisis yields to faith; the speaker awakens to conscious agency and resolute commitment to their inner truth.","opus":"Christian rock reframing faith as defiant survival: at war with a world pulling toward darkness, the singer is revived by God's breath and claims his life back. Devotion dressed as a battle cry.","sonnet":"A speaker locked in spiritual combat with the world finds renewal and resolve through faith, declaring that they know what they believe, will not be bought or broken, and that being breathed back to life by God has made them fully awake and alive.","src":"haiku"},
"skillet~hero":{"haiku":"An ordinary person wrestles with impossible demands to be superhuman and save others, declaring personal limits. The song examines the gap between heroic expectation and mortal reality.","opus":"The Christian rock band build a stadium-sized cry for rescue against a backdrop of war and a world tearing itself apart, the narrator admitting he's only human. By the end the plea turns inward: realizing the hero he needs is himself, he resolves to stand, protect the vulnerable, and risk everything for what's right.","sonnet":"A person at the edge of collapse — surrounded by war, family fracture, and self-doubt — cries out for external rescue before recognising a hero already living inside. The turning point is quiet but absolute: speaking up and fighting are reframed as heroic acts available to an ordinary man, even at the cost of his life.","src":"haiku"},
"skillet~rise":{"haiku":"Urban chaos and psychological warfare demand collective uprising; the speaker calls for revolution rooted in solidarity and shared purpose.","opus":"A response to a world of shootings, war and economic collapse, spliced with news clips and abuse, calling a generation to refuse despair and fight for what they love rather than surrender to it.","sonnet":"A call to collective resistance against violence, despair, and injustice — drawing on images of war, school shootings, economic collapse, and domestic abuse — framing survival and solidarity as revolution, urging listeners to rise from the ashes and fight for what they love.","src":"haiku"},
"skin-on-flesh~el-tiempo":{"haiku":"Love persists as the only antidote to existential rot; rejecting material obsession for genuine connection.","opus":"A Spanish-language plea to someone stranded in an eternal winter of their own hell, wasting time and doubting their own worth. The narrator insists on their value against self-hatred, urging them to silence the mind that says stop and to speak love before it is too late. Tenderness fights despair, arguing that affection matters more than money, drugs, or fear.","sonnet":"A declaration of love to someone trapped in hell — an eternal winter, going mad, wasting time without nourishment — the speaker dismisses money, drugs, and sex as irrelevant and insists, swearing not to be lying, that love is the only thing that matters when you look into the center of the universe.","src":"haiku"},
"skindred~set-fazers":{"haiku":"Surveillance and control pervade systems that advertise complicity while harvesting consciousness. The speaker demands burning of those who weaponize desire and fear.","opus":"A rebel call to torch a surveillance-and-manipulation machine that dogs your every move, monetizes your data and would betray anyone to keep its throne. The reggae-metal defiance turns explicitly political, naming warmongers and vowing to shut down the powers doing the world harm.","sonnet":"A surveillance and corporate-power critique in reggae-metal form: digital tracking, manipulative advertising, coalition politics, and empire are indicted as the beast whose mark is already on everyone — 'set fazers fi burn' is the demand that the system be neutralised, closing with a list of war, famine, and pestilence culminating in a direct threat aimed at Putin.","src":"haiku"},
"skinny-puppy~worlock":{"haiku":"Fragmented consciousness and surreal imagery spiral through non-linear thought while self-abuse and possession dominate awareness.","opus":"A dense, disintegrating stream of paranoia and self-abuse, a confessed criminal and recluse spellbound by possession and cruelty, watching wasted views through a cruel eye. The lyrics dissolve meaning to mirror a mind coming apart, seeing only what's blue and blind.","sonnet":"A dense, fragmentary stream of alienation and perception overload — self-abusive recluse, comatose dogs-body, wasted views and wasted truths — repeating that soft spoken words change nothing and a cruel view persists regardless of attitude or effort.","src":"haiku"},
"skold~triumph-of-the-will":{"haiku":"A person channels violence and bloodlust as an expression of power and dominance. The song explores the seductive, monstrous force of unchecked will.","opus":"A cold declaration of violent resolve, the narrator casting himself as executioner and reveling in a monster that surfaces whenever he shuts his eyes, even reciting ammunition specs as though holy. Skold, the Swedish industrial artist linked to KMFDM and Marilyn Manson, wraps his messianic bloodlust in bleak electronic aggression.","sonnet":"A speaker who has fully merged with violence and cannot separate from it — every time the eyes close, the monster surfaces, and the song presents this not as a horror but as an identity. War has already started, armed conflict is the only road forward, and the speaker occupies both the position of aggressor and the force driving history, with no exit from either role.","src":"haiku"},
"skraeckoedlan~creature-of-doggerland":{"haiku":"An ancient creature wanders frozen tundra and forgotten landscapes, awaiting death as the final liberation when all feeling fades.","opus":"Voiced by an ivory-tusked beast wandering the drowned prehistoric land of Doggerland, its roar waking the sun before the rising sea swallows everything. Death is embraced as all-encompassing rest, and worship of the creature becomes a riddle covered by the ocean, its tusks left as the price of freedom.","sonnet":"A mammoth-like creature of the prehistoric tundra speaks in the first person, roaming the landscape with ivory tusks until the sea slowly rises over the land, pulling it down to a dreamless death from which its bones become a mystery buried under water, offered as relics to future generations who venerate but cannot fully know it.","src":"haiku"},
"skrillex~bangarang-feat-sirah":{"haiku":"The speaker rallies lost and rowdy youth with defiant energy; later remarks reveal a carefree wildness that laughs at constraints.","opus":"Barely a lyric at all, a chant to lost boys and rowdy energy, the Peter Pan reference and the Bangarang war-cry framing a track built for drops, closing with a bratty Fun Dip boast from guest Sirah. The words are hype-fuel for the bass. Skrillex, the American producer who defined the early-2010s brostep explosion, made this a genre-defining banger where the vocal hooks exist mainly to launch the sound design.","sonnet":"A rallying cry for the rowdy and the lost — a shout-out to everyone who never got it together, delivered with complete self-awareness and zero shame. The coda lands the point: no marbles, beats blasting from a toy car, eating candy and not caring. The lost boys aren't redeemed here; they're celebrated exactly as they are.","src":"haiku"},
"skrillex~scary-monsters-and-nice-sprites":{"haiku":"Self-awareness and shared frailty level shame; the speaker admits their cowardice to another, finding solace in mutual recognition of human weakness.","opus":"A brief spoken reassurance that admits the singer is a coward too, so the listener need not hide. Beneath the drop it is a small gesture of shared vulnerability and acceptance.","sonnet":"The brief lyrical fragment positions the speaker as a coward who has been hiding but recognises the listener as the same — \"I am just like you\" — offering an unexpected moment of mutual visibility inside what is otherwise an instrumental track.","src":"haiku"},
"skrillex~too-bizarre-with-swae-lee-siiickbrain":{"haiku":"Chaos and affection tangle inextricably; the speaker loves the instability and drift, unable or unwilling to establish ground.","opus":"A volatile, drifting couple who can't decide whether to split or fall into bed and keep shredding each other's temper. The chorus settles into a resigned tenderness — the whole thing is strange, but they're fond of it exactly as it is.","sonnet":"A volatile, drug-fogged relationship where neither party communicates clearly and both keep drifting — 'too bizarre' names the dynamic admiringly rather than critically — the pleasure of chaos kept intact by refusing to talk through it, just staying in the strange.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~allure-feat-mark-holcomb":{"haiku":"Two people engage in dangerous sexual and emotional intensity; one partner surrenders to seduction and risk despite knowing the harm.","opus":"The Indian progressive-metal band layer ambient djent over a portrait of seductive ruin, framing desire as a trap where craving only intensifies the more it's fed. The lover's supposed liberation is also the narrator's undoing, binding surrender to peril until the beloved slips away and the warmth goes icy, temptation depicted as something to cling to even as it devours him.","sonnet":"Surrender to dangerous desire, framed as liberation — the narrator is drawn in by a presence that is equal parts beautiful and lethal, willingly stripped down and brought to submission. The pull is physical and total, described through the language of biting, poison lips, and being brought to one's knees, with no attempt to resist.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~blind-side":{"haiku":"A lost soul trapped by deception and false leadership struggles against manipulators who distort truth and drain them, seeking autonomy from servitude.","opus":"A lost figure grasping for light while shackled warns against the parasites and deceivers who twist the truth through broadcasts and screens. The deeper subject is resisting media manipulation and false prophets, refusing to serve as a compliant slave who hands them what they want, and choosing instead to stand up and rebuild.","sonnet":"About being manipulated and deceived by someone who speaks with two faces while the narrator is left to drift — the song positions digital media and its distortions alongside personal betrayal, with the narrator resolving to rise above the liars and carnivores rather than continue as a slave to them.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~chaos-trayus":{"haiku":"A person grieves a lost friend and struggles to honor their memory through written words that keep slipping away.","opus":"The Indian prog-metal band trace a fraying friendship in fragmentary, dreamlike fragments, vowing to keep a lost companion in memory even as vows and honor come apart. Images of written names, drained ink and a plea to grasp the moment circle a grief the narrator cannot quite say aloud.","sonnet":"Grief for a lost brother or close friend, expressed in fragmented, non-linear address. The narrator writes the name again and again, filling paper with guilt and promises, aware the words are insufficient. Walking together, keeping pictures, breaking water — the effort to hold the bond past death produces lines that will fade regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~chemical-hands":{"haiku":"Drowning in synthetic escape and denial, the narrator seeks erasure through chemical transformation, trapped between the desire to cleanse past trauma and the nihilistic acceptance of being entombed in delusion.","opus":"Numbing pain with drugs to wipe out memory and sink into emptiness, the denial itself becoming the sickness. The promised fresh start is a nihilistic delusion, the singer entombed and earthbound, mourning that he'll never take flight again.","sonnet":"A speaker seeks to chemically erase memory and feeling, surrendering to synthetic numbness and nothingness, while haunted by reflections and the broken melody of a shattered self that can never quite let go.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~dim":{"haiku":"A group oppressed by liars and corrupt authority seeks liberation and unity against those who silence and control them through lies and manipulation.","opus":"A struggle against liars and betrayal in which the isolated narrator, bleeding on his throne, builds walls to hide, sees through deceivers, and cries out to be saved while hoping to find and become the light.","sonnet":"A group of people, exploited and lost under the control of liars, try to separate truth from manipulation and call out the crimes of those who silence and suppress them, asking whether anyone can save them.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~disengage-evacuate":{"haiku":"A person faces betrayal and emotional devastation from someone who is slowly killing them through coldness and dishonesty. They realize they're alone and dying while the other person remains emotionally frozen.","opus":"A detached account of a bond that is emotionally destroying the narrator, tangled with images of a partner cinching a rope around their neck and taking their own life. Watching his surroundings shatter, he pleads for an exit from the gloom, half of him torn loose, unsure whether this marks his own end too.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship that has become actively lethal to the narrator's sense of self — the other person is tightening a noose, the narrator is fragmented and bleeding, and the only exit seems to be a final breath and dissolution into a cold, hollow space.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~dissent":{"haiku":"Rejecting religious control and faith as illusion, the speaker dismantles institutional piety, refusing to surrender their mind and soul to conformist spiritual authority.","opus":"A furious rejection of organized religion as a tool of domination, refusing to hand the mind over to piety or to hypocrites preaching from their pedestals. The call to dismantle the shrine and recognize belief as an instrument of control is framed as an act of self-liberation.","sonnet":"About rejecting imposed religious authority — the narrator refuses to give their mind over to institutional piety, tears down altars, and insists that faith is a tool of control, choosing dissent and personal sovereignty over submission to someone else's god.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~ethos":{"haiku":"A song about shedding shame, transformation, and self-realization. The speaker has changed and is not who others perceive; they are seeking a second chance and learning to manifest the life they want to see.","opus":"A tired appeal for another shot and personal rebirth, holding to the belief that anyone is capable of transformation as the narrator sheds his former self, sweeps the specters from his thoughts, and climbs beyond powerlessness to endure.","sonnet":"A song about personal reinvention after being misunderstood and dismissed — the narrator sheds a false identity, clears away internal ghosts, and insists that change is possible, reaching toward a life lived with genuine purpose rather than shame.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~evolution":{"haiku":"Growth and transformation occur through energy and communication; the speaker urges surrendering to natural desire and burning away limitations.","opus":"An invitation to stop resisting one's own nature and burn into transformation, framing the human hunger for more not as a flaw but as the engine of becoming. Skyharbor's shimmering ambient metal treats surrender as liberation rather than defeat.","sonnet":"About surrendering to a greater energetic force — the narrator frames human desire and restlessness as part of an infinite evolutionary process, urging the listener to stop resisting and let themselves burn.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~guiding-lights":{"haiku":"An exploration of darkness, abuse, and the search for enlightenment through submission and guidance, with themes of choking control and eventual resurrection.","opus":"The Indian progressive-metal band arc from surrender to a lover's beacons into far darker territory of suffocation and hollowing pain, then swing outward into teaching, mercy and love. Their soaring atmospherics carry the shift from intimate abuse imagery to a redemptive plea, promising to steady anyone who stumbles and lead them safely back to earth.","sonnet":"A surrender to something both illuminating and drowning — the speaker follows someone's guiding lights willingly, even as the path leads deeper underground, into secrets, into lies shared between two people. The song shifts between devotion and near-violence, with panic, physical choking, and hollowness alternating against a longing to be caught by something solid before everything comes apart.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~halogen":{"haiku":"A speaker awakens to follow others, seeks revelation and confirmation of purpose, and fears losing control while rain pours into their arms calling them to stay.","opus":"An atmospheric surrender to loss and confusion, reaching for connection while sensing everything slipping out of control. The lush ambience carries a plea not to face love and dissolution alone.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship that has drifted irretrievably off course — the narrator reaches for reconnection, recognizes something in a familiar face, but the core question of where everything went wrong remains unanswered, the whole thing dissolving into a state of mutual loss and confusion.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that begins certain and ends unable to stop asking, with the whole arc living in that reversal. The first half is pure upward momentum — waking, following, a revelation announced with the flat conviction of someone who has decided doubt is beneath him, the voice insisting it cannot be mistaken precisely because a part of it already suspects it is. Then the momentum has nowhere left to climb and turns into a swing, the same motion repeated with steadily draining purpose, an arm reaching out that keeps arriving at nothing. By the close that confidence has hollowed into one question asked and re-asked, the speaker circling the wreckage of a sureness he can no longer find — proof that the loudest certainty is often panic that hasn't confessed itself yet. Skyharbor's atmospheric prog is engineered for exactly this shape, guitars swelling toward a catharsis the lyric quietly declines to hand over.","fv":2,"themes":["doubt"],"means":"loudest certainty revealed as panic that hasn't confessed itself","built":"the guitar swell builds toward a catharsis the lyric quietly declines to hand over"},
"skyharbor~idle-minds":{"haiku":"A person absorbs and suffers the emotional weight of others' karmic consequences while trying to break free from entanglement and control.","opus":"A betrayal reckoning where the singer, having given everything, watches karma catch up to someone who transcended him by stepping on him. The push and pull between wanting release and being suffocated by that person drives the whole thing.","sonnet":"A song about a suffocating relationship where one person controlled and consumed the other — the narrator survived it and is moving on, but the ivy-like hold of that person left deep guilt and the scars of near-total loss of self.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~illusion-aurora":{"haiku":"Everyone dies and drifts away eventually; the speaker mourns the loss of connection despite attempting to hold others close as loneliness persists.","opus":"An aching acceptance of loss and mortality, a spirit slipping away despite every attempt to hold on, the plain truth that everyone dies and drifts, love reframed as the need to let go and be free.","sonnet":"A brief, mournful song about loss and longing — everyone drifts and dies, the narrator feels pulled back by something angelic and desperate, and the dominant emotion is loneliness and the wish that someone would come home.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~illusion-catharsis":{"haiku":"Humans struggle with faith and control; born onto a dying star, they seek identity and release, wanting closure yet needing purpose.","opus":"The Indian prog band spin cosmic doubt, imagining humanity as a supernova blazing atop a doomed star, wielding total control yet unsure of anything worth choosing. A returning question of whether we were ever meant to understand freezes faith and skepticism together, the song aching for closure and a homecoming that may never arrive.","sonnet":"Existential vertigo: the mind caught between too many beliefs and no reliable anchor. Drawing on a supernova metaphor — life born on a dying star — the speaker asks whether knowing the truth of existence would save or simply make the dying harder, and receives no answer.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~illusion-celestial":{"haiku":"The narrator searches for spiritual freedom and meaning but finds themselves repeatedly lost and rejected, yearning for liberation before daylight erases the stars.","opus":"Djent-tinged atmosphere about losing your way and the fragile pull between rejection and protection. It circles the idea that connection and being seen keep us from drowning, that light comes from being held rather than abandoned.","sonnet":"A song about navigating rejection and disorientation — the narrator is broken and lost, but reaches toward a celestial other for protection, the stars offering light when the rest of the world turns its back.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~illusion-dots":{"haiku":"Children stand against struggle through changing times; the fallen bear blame while others feel defeated and cheated, caught in cycles of loss and suffering.","opus":"A restless portrait of a numbed, cheated generation waiting for a change that never comes, elegance masking emptiness and quiet suffering, longing to break through the screen and out of a life of blinding white noise.","sonnet":"A fragmented song about generational trauma, media saturation, and people who feel cheated and defeated — children stand and fight in a world of blinding white noise where time is running out and someone must hold the line in the rain.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~illusion-maeva":{"haiku":"Someone spirals through addiction and delusion inspired by Alice in Wonderland; they question reality and struggle to escape the trap.","opus":"Indian progressive-metal band Skyharbor recast Alice in Wonderland as a drug trip gone wrong, its guiding figures leading the narrator away from safety into confusion, warfare, and unknown substances. Amid the disorientation surfaces a euphoric plea to unite and cling to living, with India invoked as both home and a spiritual awakening.","sonnet":"An Alice in Wonderland framework carries a narrator through a hallucinatory collapse of certainty — the Mad Hatter disrupts cognition, castle walls turn to sand, pills are offered freely. Beneath the imagery, a voice insists on survival, community, and the possibility of India as a grounding return, while the mind keeps slipping toward dissociation.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~illusion-order-66":{"haiku":"Refusing to obey and resisting imposed order; the speaker turns away from authority and refuses to fight the system's demands.","opus":"A struggle to keep faith in some governing order while living through repeated proximity to death and the seduction of giving in. The refusal to fight and the turn away from imposed structure read as hard-won release, learning that fear itself was the real threat.","sonnet":"About betraying an order or system out of love — the narrator has played with fire long enough and now turns away from the command structure, choosing a person or feeling over duty, knowing the cost but making the choice anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~kaikoma-feat-valentina-reptile":{"haiku":"A speaker yearns for spiritual and supernatural connection with a pale, crystalline presence that both attracts and freezes them. They are spiritually bound in a winter landscape of isolation.","opus":"A dreamlike lure toward surrender, the narrator entranced by a beautiful, magnetic figure and coaxing her to lose herself in something both alluring and harsh, a connection that heals yet leaves him cold and alone. Skyharbor, the internationally based, Indian-founded prog-metal band, float the ambient djent between tenderness and chill.","sonnet":"Magnetic attraction to someone cold and crystalline — pale skin, crystal eyes, a pull the speaker can't deny even as it means walking into bitterness and losing himself. The bind is described as spiritual, the other person as winter that pierces skin, a storm that's malevolent fun. Looking inward at the end reveals it was always the speaker who froze, always the fool, drawn to what would only leave him alone.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~menace":{"haiku":"Seduction into self-destruction and fire; a predatory force pulls the speaker inward while promising transcendence, leading toward burning rather than salvation.","opus":"Temptation personified as a destroyer who slips under the skin and pulls you further than you meant to go, offering youth and belonging in exchange for your soul. The song lives in the thrill and dread of surrendering to something you know will consume you.","sonnet":"About a seductive, corrupting force that crawls under the skin — something that manipulates from within, promising burning renewal while destroying the narrator's resistance, framed alternately as a temptress, a witness, and a crime.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~miracle":{"haiku":"Discovery of another person's presence as a miracle amid loneliness and cynicism, seeking faith in connection while sinking into memory and despair.","opus":"A gentle djent lullaby pleading against a life built on wealth and nothing else, cautioning against letting anger rule or bartering your soul for love. Sinking into the sand as a vast tide nears, the narrator admits he plays at not being a bitter, ruined man. The final glimpse of a loved one as a true miracle redeems the drift.","sonnet":"A dreamer who has spent too long asleep in nostalgia — vivid memories of dancing under midnight skies — begins to feel himself sinking into the sand. The sight of a child breaks through the cynicism, briefly revealed as the one real and irreducible thing still worth calling miraculous.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~new-devil":{"haiku":"Inner darkness and violent tendencies merge into a demonic presence that consumes, becoming the new devil within oneself.","opus":"Skyharbor voice a consuming possession, a seductive inner force that demands total surrender and merger. As the narrator gives in, the world around him dissolves into decay and everything he once clung to is judged meaningless, submission becoming a kind of self-erasure into something that offers no redemption.","sonnet":"A parasitic inner force — part addiction, part shadow self — that has colonised the speaker completely. It pulls him under, speaks in violent urges, and demands merger rather than resistance, framing the surrender as inevitable: the world will not improve, so descend willingly.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~out-of-time":{"haiku":"Society moves blindly toward catastrophe while the speaker runs out of time to resist; they must act before everything burns away.","opus":"A mounting sense of running out of time amid lies and manipulation, the crowd led by the sightless with fingers already on triggers, drawn helplessly toward danger as the ground shifts underfoot. Yet the vow that they will not be discarded, and the resolve that they endure together, turn that dread into defiant solidarity.","sonnet":"A song about urgency under manipulation and deceit — the speaker is done waiting while lies compound around them, rallying to tear down what's false and survive together before time runs out entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~patience":{"haiku":"A person prepares to burn away their world to survive parasitic corruption, accepting isolation and transformation as necessary. They cultivate inner strength through burning attachments and embracing solitude.","opus":"A tender, unsettling farewell in which the narrator lingers over a sleeping love as a flawless keepsake before abandoning them, then swears to raze the rotten world beneath him for the sake of survival, frightened even as he does it. Skyharbor, the ambient prog project, let intimacy and devastation crest together.","sonnet":"A last look before departure — the speaker holds a perfect memory of someone close and then must leave them in peace. What follows is survival framed as destruction: burning down a parasite town, fighting for his life beneath a crown of lies, too scared of the person he loves to do anything but burn it all to stay alive. The tenderness of the opening doesn't survive contact with what comes next.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~sunshine-dust":{"haiku":"A person chooses to face fear and darkness deliberately to grow beyond their current limits. They accept scars and internal conflict as necessary for transformation and breaking free from stagnation.","opus":"Progressive band Skyharbor make growth through suffering their theme, the narrator convinced that moving toward fear and doubt rather than staying safe will forge wounds that sharpen the mind. Bound to his own inner dark but set on transformation, he vows to make a lasting mark and become his own light, while insisting he isn't ready to die.","sonnet":"Deliberate descent into fear and shame, rather than flight from it, as the only path to real growth — the conviction that remaining in comfortable light stunts the self, while darkness and scarring expand consciousness. A long argument with his own desire to die concludes with a vow to carve his name into existence.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~synthetic-hands":{"haiku":"A person surrenders to false comfort and artificial control, denying reality to escape trauma. They use chemical numbing to avoid facing their corruption.","opus":"Atmospheric progressive metal about numbness and self-destruction, reaching for artificial sensation just to feel anything as denial and delusion entomb the narrator. Beauty and despair held in the same breath.","sonnet":"About being pulled into addiction or destructive dependence — synthetic hands burying the past, denial as the primary symptom, a nihilistic vision replacing the real world. The narrator loses control, floats in darkness, and finally recognizes the delusion entombing them.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~the-constant-feat-plini-roessler-holgate":{"haiku":"Endless burning motion demands strength; the narrator drowns and rises, emotionally blind yet devoted, galaxies unchanging in their eyes.","opus":"With Plini guesting, Skyharbor reach for the cosmic, casting life as ceaseless movement and a blaze that both drains and feeds the self. The narrator pushes toward light while grieving a sacrificed life, and devotion runs through it as a final gift, a search for clarity amid encircling dark.","sonnet":"An exhausted figure moving through danger and darkness who draws sustenance from a connection strong enough to feel cosmic — blue eyes that mirror the galaxies, a devotion offered as a last act before drowning. Motion is the only constant; love is the reason to resist gravity.","src":"haiku"},
"skyharbor~ugly-heart":{"haiku":"The speaker was abandoned and left with severed wings after someone's heart turned to stone. They contemplate looking at their reflection while questioning what made their abuser's heart turn cold and cruel.","opus":"An accusation aimed at someone whose heart has turned to stone through betrayal and cruelty; the narrator, barely surviving the fallout, wonders what happened to make this person so vitriolic and self-destructive that they burned everything, while he tries to find himself in the shattered reflection of the world they left.","sonnet":"A song about surviving someone who treated the narrator cruelly and left them emotionally gutted — the person is now cast as someone who threw everything into fire and was left with a stone where their heart was, while the narrator struggles to find themselves in the wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"skylar-spence~private-caller":{"haiku":"Escape from external judgment, seeking connection before expectations wear down both people.","opus":"A vaporwave-turned-pop plea to ignore friends' warnings and just meet, the come-on wrapped in wistful uncertainty about whether this connection can outlast a single fading day.","sonnet":"An urgent, breezy plea to ignore what friends say and just get together before the day slips away — the speaker has no patience for stop signs or social interference, only the desire to fall in first with the person they want.","src":"haiku"},
"skylimit~a-place-you-ll-never-find":{"haiku":"The speaker descends into self-created void where darkness becomes comfort; isolation becomes the only familiar sanctuary available.","opus":"Retreating into a private mental refuge of darkness and insanity, drawn to pain as if it were adrenaline. The comfort is corrosive, a hiding place no one can reach where the singer circles his own mind, sick of pretending he's fine.","sonnet":"A speaker sealed inside their own interior — 'I'm trapped inside a place you'll never find' — finds the darkness and insanity comforting, circling in their mind while light creeps through a door they won't open, choosing the familiar pain of the hole over the world outside.","src":"haiku"},
"skynd~columbine-feat-bill-$aber":{"haiku":"School shooting as mechanized performance and casual game; the song strips narrative from mass violence, rendering it as meaningless play enacted by players seeking fame.","src":"haiku"},
"skynd~columbine-feat-bill-aber":{"opus":"A chilling ventriloquism of the Columbine shooters, staging the massacre as a game they play with gunfire and body counts. It is about the banal, fameless cruelty behind the atrocity, delivered in the killers' own detached voice.","sonnet":"A rhythmic re-enactment of a school shooting through the perspective of the perpetrators, framed as a countdown of bullets and victims — \"how many victims is we 'bout to lose?\" — with the gun's report rendered as percussion; it is a disturbing dramatisation of the event's mechanics without redemptive framing.","src":"sonnet"},
"skywalker~away":{"haiku":"A song addressing someone whose sense of self and belief have been stolen or lost. The speaker urges them to fight or flee while wrestling with the weight of isolation and self-doubt.","opus":"A plea to someone hollowed out by other people's demands until belief itself is gone. It frames survival as a stark binary choice and insists, against their self-loathing, that they are worth saving as they are.","sonnet":"An appeal to someone whose sense of self and belief have been stripped away by outside forces, urging them to stop bottling everything up and choose to fight rather than surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~cape-fear":{"haiku":"A parasitic entity watches, invades dreams, and lives inside the victim's mind, claiming residence at '13 Cape Fear' (a place of cold stone and no windows). It is predatory obsession that refuses to let go.","opus":"The band personifies a haunting presence that watches from behind the curtain and lives inside its victim's head, inviting them to a windowless house of cold stone that is theirs alone. Naming the address on Cape Fear, it renders depression or obsession as a patient tenant of the mind, certain the target will come around in time.","sonnet":"Depression addresses its host as a patient landlord: it already lives inside, knows the address, and will wait as long as necessary. The offer of four stone walls with no windows is extended gently, even invitingly — isolation reframed as a home the listener will eventually accept.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~charon-s-song":{"haiku":"Death and crossing into the underworld; Charon's journey across the Styx with only silence and the sound of death.","opus":"Facing the death of a loved one through the myth of crossing the Styx into the silent underworld; the speaker dreads the parting, questions who to trust, and reflects that there's no real moving on, only swallowing the hurt and wishing he could eat the other's pain.","sonnet":"The speaker grapples with an impending loss — someone crossing the river Styx into death — and finds that grief does not end with moving on; instead, hurt is swallowed and time spirals, while the wish to absorb the dying person's pain remains.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~effigy":{"haiku":"Broken connection and systemic oppression fragment the self into a fragile echo, where class and skin color crystallize division and internal rot. Defiance blooms as rage and collective resilience against those who steal and diminish human dignity.","opus":"Loneliness and self-reproach swelling into a wider political fury, the singer refusing to serve as anyone's symbol while wealth, family and race split people apart. The sting of human separation is met with a defiant conviction that divinity lies in people, pressing onward together into the unknown.","sonnet":"A fractured speaker rejects being made into an effigy for someone else's ideology, raging against class, kin, and skin as dividing forces while insisting that humanity — not fixed history or God — is the only worthwhile faith.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~every-grief":{"haiku":"Isolation feeds demons; the speaker processes loneliness and emptiness through breath and acceptance of impermanence.","opus":"A study of chosen solitude souring into despair, where self-sufficiency slides into feeling utterly abandoned and hollowed out, nightmares preying on the narrator's sorrow. Skywalker, the French post-hardcore band, move from that anguish toward a mindful, near-meditative release through the breath, the demons and the calming exhale carrying the same heavy weight.","sonnet":"Chosen solitude that turns out to be loneliness — the empty throne, the frozen steps, the admission that individual and abandoned might be the same word. Nightmares feed on grief, demons are the only company, and the desire to fly away is real but comes with the warning not to follow. In the end: breathe in, breathe out, try to love and accept and let go. It doesn't sound easy.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~for-you-through-you-feat-zea":{"haiku":"A relationship built on exploitation and arrogance crumbles; the speaker recognizes they have been a tool for someone's ego, which is as fragile as their throne.","opus":"The metalcore track voices resentment toward someone who mistakes devotion for ownership, treating the narrator as a tool kept for their needs. Feeling stone cold yet unable to let go, he sees through a brittle, weak ego and the throne it sits on, half-hoping they grow old so the other's mistakes clear the path he has paved, half-wishing they never grow old together at all, love and disillusion pulling opposite ways.","sonnet":"A person realises they have been used as a means rather than loved as an end — treated as a tool while the other person sits on a brittle throne of ego. There is grief in the recognition, but also a steady refusal to keep absorbing someone else's mistakes as the price of closeness.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~house-of-gloom":{"haiku":"Nightmare begets nightmare through entanglement; the speaker wants to wake yet remains magnetized to what torments them most.","opus":"A recurring nightmare figure the singer both dreads and admires, who keeps him shackled and unable to wake into real life. Growth means breaking the person he dreams of, since everything that matters only happens once he stops idealizing the tempest that is them.","sonnet":"A speaker haunted by a recurring presence in dreams — the House of Gloom, where the tempest lives — desperate to wake up and break free but held in shackles that won't give, knowing everything that matters happens outside the dream.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~ignis":{"haiku":"Someone grapples with loneliness, pursued by inner demons and disconnected voices that push them toward isolation and self-suffocation.","opus":"The Russian metalcore band trace a mind trapped alone with its own suffering, choosing isolation and defying everyone's expectations. Its inner tormentors slip away the instant they're confronted, turning survival into a repeating game of near-misses, all while the narrator keeps asking whether this all-consuming gloom will ever lift.","sonnet":"A lifelong outsider who has never managed to conform to others' expectations, haunted by demons that vanish whenever he reaches for them. He wanders a pitch-black interior landscape, chasing whispers he cannot decode, and asks quietly whether the misery will ever thin.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~late-eternity":{"haiku":"Trapped between what could never be and infinity of loss. Time erases possibility and the speaker remains stuck in eternal paralysis.","opus":"Being stranded in the space between what never was and what can no longer be, time draining away while the refusal to accept defeat persists anyway. The paradox of eternity and infinity collapses into one exhausted, stubborn choice to go on.","sonnet":"A song about being permanently trapped between what was lost and the refusal to stop searching for it — the narrator and someone else keep digging through the dirt of grief and incompleteness, neither able to accept defeat nor find an end.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~no-leader":{"haiku":"Spiritual awakening and rejection of faith-based hierarchy and saviors. The narrator claims independence and daily rebirth without external guidance.","opus":"A deconvert's morning-after hymn, finding paradise the moment he sets down scripture and its promise of a single name to save him. It reframes atheism as liberation and daily rebirth from nothing, a sinner claiming strength precisely by bowing to no king.","sonnet":"A song about rejecting organized religion and external authority in favor of a personal, self-discovered spiritual awakening. The speaker finds beauty and meaning on his own terms, renouncing faith in any single name or savior and declaring himself reborn daily from nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~precious":{"haiku":"Manufactured lies about perfection and image corrode the soul; the speaker rejects the false narrative.","opus":"A revolt against comforting lies and curated perfection, the narrator sick of fake smiles and glistening picture-perfect facades, refusing to sell his soul for a life that isn't real, insisting his life is here and now while they keep lying in his ear. Skywalker, the French post-hardcore act, channel this anti-fakery fury into their melodic-heavy dynamic, the disgust at social-media artifice sharpening into a demand to be real right now.","sonnet":"The performance of a perfect life as a lie everyone colluded on — picture-perfect surface, real hurt underneath, the whispered instruction to smile and snap and look glistening while nothing is actually right. The speaker breaks from it by naming what it costs: you sell your soul to look that way. Better to be present in a real, imperfect now than to die maintaining an image built on other people's lies.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~sand-god-feat-connor-hallisey":{"haiku":"The song challenges blind faith and asks why a merciful god allows suffering, betrayal by loved ones, and the erasure of human dreams.","opus":"Skywalker level a furious address at a deity that fills believers' eyes with sand, comforting a person abandoned even by their own kin in the name of that god. The blunt curse against a god who enslaves rather than relieves pain frames faith as cruelty. Watching a lover gone and dreams disintegrate at twenty-eight, the narrator wishes the idol would crumble and fall.","sonnet":"A furious defence of someone whose faith community abandoned them — directed at a god the speaker sees as the architect of that abandonment. The grief is specific: a person at 28, dreams disintegrating, a lover gone, left alone by the very source they were taught to trust.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~setting-stone-feat-florent-salfati":{"haiku":"A person rejects guilt as their foundation and moves forward, refusing to bear another's hatred or need for repentance while fighting their own demons alone.","opus":"A firm refusal to keep carrying someone else's guilt, refusing to repent past the sincere apology already given. Its strength is the boundary drawn, moving on and fighting one's own demons rather than serving another's need for a villain.","sonnet":"A song about refusing to accept permanent guilt as the foundation of your identity — the narrator has said sorry and meant it, but won't keep repenting at the demand of someone who needs them to fail, choosing instead to move on and build something that isn't made of self-punishment.","src":"haiku"},
"skywalker~soundtrack":{"haiku":"A person is isolated by internal mental voices and anxiety, hearing only their own self-doubt echoing back.","opus":"An anxiety attack rendered as sound and silence: the narrator spins out of control, hearing not an external voice but the thousand competing voices of his own insecurity between four walls, ultimately recognizing that the deafening silence and the shouting are all himself.","sonnet":"A claustrophobic portrait of being trapped with your own anxious inner voice — the silence between thoughts becomes its own deafening noise, and every voice calling out is just another fragment of the same fractured self.","src":"haiku"},
"sl-tface~shave-my-head":{"haiku":"Refusing to perform desperation or self-harm for a lover's validation or control.","opus":"A wry punk refusal to perform the crazy-girlfriend theatrics an ex expected, choosing red wine and IKEA chairs over feigned heartbreak. Its power move is admitting she'd simply rather be alone and lazy than fight.","sonnet":"A narrator refuses to perform the dramatic, self-destructive behaviour her partner seems to expect from her — she is unbothered, self-sufficient, and would rather assemble IKEA furniture alone than manufacture heartbreak for someone else's benefit.","src":"haiku"},
"slaughter-to-prevail~agony":{"haiku":"Authentic presence requires bearing pain without flinching; the speaker asks whether love can survive loss, vulnerability, and the risk of appearing foolish.","opus":"Adapted from Oriah's poem, an interrogation of whether you'll risk looking foolish for love and dreams, sit with your own sorrow, and stand in the fire without shrinking back. It insists everyone must pass through agony to truly be alive.","sonnet":"A spoken-word passage — drawn from Oriah Mountain Dreamer's \"The Invitation\" — delivered as heavy metal liturgy: it wants to know if you will risk looking like a fool for love and dreams, sit with pain without flinching, stand in the centre of fire and not shrink back; agony is the price of being fully alive.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~addict":{"haiku":"Dependency and compulsive craving; addiction as an inescapable trap that defines existence.","opus":"Slayer deliver a killer's monologue that treats murder as a narcotic, terror the high he chases and inflicts on hapless victims wherever and whenever he pleases, deciding for himself when the agony ends. The thrash pioneers frame serial violence as pure compulsion, the narrator savoring the craving crawling under his own skin.","sonnet":"A torturer's self-portrait — the other person's terror is described as a drug, fear as the hit that intensifies the rush. The speaker positions themselves as plague, weapon, and final breath all at once. Power and cruelty are the only grammar the song speaks.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~aggressive-perfector":{"haiku":"A malevolent force drags victims to their knees and binds them in a fatal game designed for control.","opus":"A raw cut from Slayer's earliest years, spoken in the voice of a malevolent guide steering a victim toward damnation, vowing to force them down in a lethal contest. Terror swamps the prey as a controlling presence claims it can meet their every want, the path leading straight to hell, the thrash pioneers dressing predation and dominance as false deliverance.","sonnet":"A predatory voice addresses a victim, positioning total control as seduction — leading, satisfying, transforming, breaking. The road goes straight to destruction and the offer is framed as inevitable. No resistance is imagined; the prey is already kneeling before the song begins.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~altar-of-sacrifice": {"haiku":"A satanic ritual sacrifice transforms the victim through damnation, granting them evil powers while condemning them to infernal transformation.","opus":"A ritual human sacrifice on a satanic altar, a virgin's blood spilled by a high priest, followed by the victim's damnation and grotesque transformation into a demonic undead servant of Satan; the arc is death as passage into hell and the corrupted powers granted there.","sonnet":"A Satanic ritual sacrifice scene — a virgin is killed on an altar, and the initiate undergoes a physical and spiritual transformation, receiving powers from Satan in exchange for their former humanity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A black-mass narrative that Slayer stages in two movements, and the second is the whole point. The first half is the expected splatter — a virgin bound on the table, the high priest's dagger, death sold to the victim as an easy sleep — but the song refuses to end at the murder where a lesser lyric would. Instead it crosses over into the victim's damnation and narrates it from inside: blood blackening, flesh deforming, five toes collapsing to two as the body is remade into something bestial and undead. That grotesque anatomical detail is Slayer's tell — they treat Satanism not as atmosphere but as a literal, bureaucratic promotion, complete with granted powers and instructions for their use. The closing turn toward angels forever searching the heavens for a salvation the newly damned has already forfeited is what gives the horror its cold finality.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"damnation narrated as a bureaucratic promotion with granted powers","built":"the song refuses to end at the murder and crosses into the victim's grotesque remake"},
"slayer~americon":{"haiku":"American imperialism spreads corruption and violence worldwide for profit; a contagious plague of greed, deceit, and exploitation spreads under the flag.","opus":"The thrash titans aim their contempt squarely at their own nation, portraying it as a cruel, self-harming power that infects other cultures without remorse. Naming oil and greed behind the flag, they coin a title that brands patriotism itself as a self-serving disease that dresses up its bloodshed as freedom.","sonnet":"A scathing indictment of American foreign policy as oil-driven imperialism wrapped in the language of liberty. The nation is cast as an infectious strain — callous, greedy, imprisoning minds at home and imposing its will abroad. The word Americon collapses America and con into a single diagnosis.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~angel-of-death": {"haiku":"The Holocaust horrors are depicted through the perspective of Josef Mengele conducting cruel medical experiments on prisoners.","opus":"An unflinching, deliberately clinical account of Josef Mengele's atrocities at Auschwitz, cataloging the horror without editorial comment. Hanneman meant it as documentation of evil, not endorsement, though the flat reportage is exactly what made it notorious.","sonnet":"A graphic account of the atrocities committed by Nazi doctor Josef Mengele at Auschwitz — mass murder, medical torture, and forced experimentation — described without commentary as a catalogue of the Holocaust's systematic horrors.","src":"haiku","fable":"A clinical catalogue of Josef Mengele's Auschwitz experiments, written entirely from inside the perpetrator's framing — the you is the victim, the voice is the knife. Hanneman built the horror out of documentary specifics: operations on patients kept awake, freezing and pressure trials, the fiction of racial improvement, all narrated as procedure rather than atrocity. The provocation, and the reason the band spent decades denying the obvious charge, is that the song never once steps outside to condemn; it lets Mengele's own logic of the inferior body indict itself by sheer accumulation. What the sustained refusal to editorialize does is force the listener to supply the moral horror the narrator withholds. That withholding is the whole point: the mask is worn without comment so that the thing underneath — an ideology that treats humans as material — becomes unbearable precisely because no one on the record flinches.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"It cost the album its distributor: Columbia refused to release Reign in Blood over this song, Rubin moved it to Geffen, and Geffen shipped the record with its own logo left off — while Hanneman's standing answer to the sympathy charge stayed simple: the song is a history lesson.","themes":["violence & murder","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"sustained refusal to editorialize forces the moral horror onto the listener","built":"atrocity delivered as documented procedure rather than as indictment"},
"slayer~angel-of-death-live-1994":{"haiku":"Nazi medical experimentation and genocide; the angel of death presiding over industrial-scale annihilation.","opus":"Slayer's most infamous track confronts the atrocities of Josef Mengele, the Auschwitz physician whose sadistic experiments are catalogued in unflinching, clinical horror. Rather than glorify, it documents the mechanized cruelty of the Holocaust with a repulsion meant to sicken. A live rendering of thrash metal's defining provocation, staring directly into industrialized human evil.","sonnet":"A first-person account of Josef Mengele's atrocities at Auschwitz — showers, surgery without anesthesia, experiments with heat and cold and injections — the song does not editorialize but catalogs, forcing the listener to witness the mechanics of the Holocaust through the language of extreme metal without the distance of metaphor.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~at-dawn-they-sleep":{"haiku":"Vampiric rest during daylight; creatures of darkness retreating from the sun's exposure.","opus":"A vampire horror piece told from the predator's perspective, nocturnal beings that shun daylight, crave human blood, rise from hellish graves, and drain their victims before resting when morning comes. Piling on stakes, coffins, and demonic imagery, American thrash pioneers Slayer render classic monster-movie dread at ferocious speed.","sonnet":"Classic vampire mythology rendered in thrash — creatures of the night hunt, feed, and retreat before sunrise, their demented lust kept hidden until the moment jaws lock on a vein. The song moves from the parasite's-eye view to the community trying to drive a stake home and failing.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~atrocity-vendor":{"haiku":"A torturer celebrates inflicting endless suffering and death, fetishizing control, violence, and the collection of victims' remains.","opus":"Slayer, the American thrash-metal titans, built their legend on unflinching brutality, and this late-era single is Kerry King's lyric of pure, methodical menace. It's a first-person descent into a sociopath's head, a self-styled dealer in cruelty who promises meticulous violence and takes pleasure in the suffering he inflicts, the band's savagery entirely undimmed.","sonnet":"A torturer's self-portrait delivered without apology — the speaker is an equal-opportunity offender, a sociopath who watches suffering unfold and inflicts more, cancer shooting from the eyes, violence in the veins, a chapel where he's the pastor. The language is methodical and proud, trophies collected, disaster guaranteed. There's no victim's perspective here, only the predator cataloguing his own power.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~beauty-through-order":{"haiku":"Predatory sadism and ritualistic murder framed as transcendence. A killer believes their violence is beautiful and that they hold aristocratic birthright over the dead.","opus":"Slayer render the legend of Elizabeth Bathory in their signature thrash assault. Bloodshed becomes intoxication as the countess strips the young of dignity, butchers screaming victims, and is crowned for her pitiless crimes, insisting no god had a hand in it and that it all belongs to her. Aristocratic murder delivered with clinical, sacrilegious ferocity.","sonnet":"A serial killer's erotic theology — blood described as intoxicating, victims frozen in time while the killer sits painless and shameless, collecting trophies of flesh. The imagery invokes Elizabeth Báthory without naming her: bathing in blood, inserting iron, a mistress of cruelty whose name won't die. God is claimed as belonging to the killer. Young life stripped of dignity is the price of a beauty defined entirely through domination and order.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~behind-the-crooked-cross": {"haiku":"A soldier marches obediently into massacre, suppressing conscience and emotion while trapped in a cause they once understood. The song depicts complicity through blind obedience amid atrocity.","opus":"An unusually interior Slayer song, told from inside a Nazi soldier who once believed and now feels sickness rising, conscience deliberately forgotten. It indicts blind obedience by inhabiting the man swallowed by it.","sonnet":"A soldier narrates the psychological destruction of fighting under the Nazi crooked cross — conscience suppressed, blind obedience as survival, the soul drowning in rivers of blood while questioning what cause he is actually serving.","src":"haiku","fable":"A soldier narrates his own hollowing-out under the swastika, the crooked cross of the title, and the dread is that he does it in the flat voice of a man logging his duties. The opening still admits belief — he enlisted for something he thought he grasped — but each verse peels off another human faculty, until emotion is deliberately muffled and the idea of a moral inner voice becomes something he has trained himself not to recall. What fills the vacancy is blind obedience, named plainly as the engine hauling him through the killing. Slayer, who spent the Reign in Blood era staring at Mengele and the camps, here pivots to the ordinary conscript, indicting not the monster but the machinery of surrender that mass-produces him. The chorus, wading toward battle, returns untouched precisely because nothing evolves: the man is already finished, and only the marching goes on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","dissociation & numbness","politics & society","shame & repression"],"means":"blind obedience as the machine that finishes a man before battle does","built":"the chorus returns unchanged because the narrator was already finished before it began"},
"slayer~bitter-peace": {"haiku":"Endless cycles of war and violence perpetuate human hatred despite peace rhetoric, with murder embedded as life's central purpose and obsession.","opus":"Slayer's verdict that peace is only a pause between slaughters, humanity biologically driven to hate and kill with ever more mechanized efficiency. War not as failure but as the species' one cryptic reason for living.","sonnet":"Perpetual warfare as human nature — peace only breeds contempt and restlessness until the killing starts again, because there is no conscience or reason in a species whose only real purpose seems to be murder.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a broadside against the idea that any truce is real, written in the sabre-rattling vocabulary of late-Cold-War deterrence — the balance of terror that supposedly kept the superpowers from firing first. The title is the thesis compressed into two words: quiet is not the opposite of slaughter but its intermission, the pause in which resentment ferments before the next round begins. Slayer strip the machinery of nation-states down to something older and uglier, folding doctrine into the language of brothers turning on one another, so that geopolitics and fratricide become the same appetite wearing different uniforms. The chorus works less as a lyric than as a verdict handed down again and again, its repetition insisting there is no off-switch and no negotiator left with a conscience anyone could appeal to. What lands hardest is the closing refusal to explain itself — killing needs no motive here, only a body and a justification invented after the fact, which may be the bleakest claim the song makes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"peace named as slaughter's intermission not its end","built":"the chorus as verdict repeated until no appeal remains"},
"slayer~black-magic":{"haiku":"Sorcery and dark rituals; supernatural power invoked for destructive purposes and control over others.","opus":"A losing fight against demonic possession, the narrator bound by a curse that warps his thoughts as he struggles to hold off evil until death claims him. From the debut Show No Mercy, it captures Slayer's earliest occult phase, raw thrash speed wedded to horror-movie damnation and an inevitable surrender to darkness.","sonnet":"Possession as a losing battle — evil light, Satan's force, spells circling day and night. The speaker fights to resist but strength is slipping, sources are shrinking, death is reaching for his hand. There is no climactic victory, only the approach of the end, catalogued with grim clarity. The curse will not be broken in time.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~black-serenade": {"haiku":"A killer ritualizes torture and death, creating a private hell of suffering where haunting and possession blur with sexual violence and consuming the corpse.","opus":"A murderer's obsessive fixation on his victim, whose staring corpse still haunts and torments him; he describes killing her, defiling the body, and the disintegration of his own mind under the weight of the act he calls his personal hell.","sonnet":"A narrator fixated on a dead woman whose image won't leave his mind — the song moves through murder, necrophilia, and the tortured aftermath of a psyche that cannot let go of what it has done.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages a haunted killer's monologue: the speaker addresses a victim he has already murdered, yet who returns as a presence that stares, speaks, and refuses to stay dead. What begins as sadistic triumph — deciding the moment of another's end, savoring it as a private sacrament — curdles into the killer's own unraveling, his skull filled with the voice and lifeless face of the one he destroyed. The title names his ritual: an invitation extended to the dead to sing their agony back to him, a serenade that turns out to be his own descent, the tormentor and the haunted collapsing into one insane mind that cannot silence what it did.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind","death & grief"],"means":"the tormentor's ritual collapses into the haunting he himself invited","built":"a serenade extended to the dead that turns back as the serenader's own descent"},
"slayer~blood-red": {"haiku":"Political oppression and bloodshed mark a nation where false leaders and violence suppress civil liberties. Those who resist face barbaric control while innocent lives are massacred.","opus":"Slayer's response to Tiananmen: peaceful protest crushed by the state, oppression that no disguise can hide behind. Thrash as a blunt indictment of massacre and the lie that thousands can't be wrong.","sonnet":"About state violence and oppression — governments enforcing their will through guns, massacres of civilians, lies dressed as truth. It's a protest song in Slayer's mode: the enemy is authoritarian brutality and the blood it spills.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tom Araya wrote the lyric, and it has been read ever since as Slayer’s Tiananmen song — released a year after the June 1989 crackdown, its whole design showing a government turning its guns on its own citizens under the cover of restoring order. The closing image weaponizes the state's own emblem: the sickle, that Communist agrarian symbol of the worker, gets dyed the crimson of the very laborers it claimed to honor, so the tool of the peasant becomes the instrument of his slaughter. Araya frames unarmed demonstrators and honest votes colliding with tanks, then insists the killing resists any official laundering into discipline or enforced truth, because a massacre leaves a mark no propaganda can wash clean.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder"],"means":"a massacre that no official laundering can wash clean","built":"the Communist sickle dyed in the blood of the workers it claimed to honor"},
"slayer~bloodline": {"haiku":"Vampiric immortality through feeding on others' life and bloodline; eternal predation and defiance of god.","opus":"A vampire's boast, feeding on blood to stay young forever and dooming his prey in the process. He revels in mocking God, poisoning creation, and ruling as an immortal predator for a millennium, damnation and undeath fused into a single gloating threat.","sonnet":"Narrated by a vampire, the song describes feeding on human blood to sustain immortality — the speaker was condemned by a single kiss, defies God, stalks prey, and demands the victim's bloodline to fuel another thousand years of reign.","src":"haiku","fable":"A vampire's first-person boast, written by Slayer for the Dracula 2000 tie-in and pitched as deliberate blasphemy: the immortal speaker drains a victim so their stolen vitality renews his own agelessness. The song moves as a hunt first — nocturnal packs closing on chosen prey through the dark — then hardens into a creed, the predator naming himself faithless and religionless, his only confession the stalking itself. Its cruelest turn is theological: he casts the dying as God's abandoned children while he alone survives, inverting communion into parasitism and treating another person's ending as the fuel for his endless youth.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"communion inverted into parasitism fueling the predator's endless youth","built":"the dying cast as God's abandoned while the immortal predator alone survives"},
"slayer~born-of-fire":{"haiku":"The speaker inhabits a demonic persona—Satan's son, a destroyer crowned in fire—spreading terror and corruption while burning with unquenchable wrath.","opus":"Slayer slip into their signature satanic bravado, a proclaimed offspring of the devil raising the dead and rigging every outcome in his favor. Forged in flame and enduring across ages, he lords over a hellscape of gore and warped horrors, the band staging evil as boastful theater, a tyrant of prophecy who cannot disown what he is.","sonnet":"A figure who defines himself as born of darkness and destruction — part satanic archetype, part pure-will predator — who takes pleasure in sin, wields chaos deliberately, and frames his own corruption not as a fall but as a birthright and a kind of mastery.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~captor-of-sin": {"haiku":"A satanic figure dominates others through demonic corruption, enslaving them through vice and manipulation until their wills break entirely.","opus":"A demonic first-person boast of predation and violation, the narrator as Satan's spawn stalking the earth, penetrating souls and dragging victims into hellfire. The deeper read is domination and damnation as sadistic conquest, subversion only strengthening him, death the victim's only, futile plea.","sonnet":"A demonic predator speaks — Satan's offspring penetrating souls, dominating the enslaved, promising death as the final outcome for anyone who tries to resist his grip.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer's early-mode inversion, where the singer occupies the demon rather than fears it: the whole lyric is spoken from inside the seducer-tormentor, addressing the damned as prey it already owns. The title's twist is that the captor is not caught by sin but captures through it — offering to relieve the helpless of their plight while meaning it as the delivery into fire, so every promise of rescue reads as the trap closing. Its logic is one of gleeful futility: pleading, subversion, and prayer are all pre-answered by the same verdict, that resistance only feeds the power it opposes and death is the sole exit left open.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"every promise of rescue is the trap closing on the already-damned","built":"the title inverts: the captor captures through sin rather than being captured by it"},
"slayer~cast-down": {"haiku":"Marginalized people trapped in poverty and despair turn to drug addiction as numbing escape; the system abandons them while society treats them as disposable.","opus":"Slayer's unflinching street-level portrait of addiction and abandonment, a person self-destructing in an indifferent society where money and God offer nothing. The bleakness is deliberate, a refusal to judge someone the system already discarded.","sonnet":"A portrait of a homeless, addicted person discarded by society — the narrator refuses to judge someone living on concrete, numbing themselves with needles, invisible to the world that failed them. The song is surprisingly empathetic for Slayer, condemning the system that creates and then abandons people like this rather than the people themselves.","src":"haiku","fable":"A withdrawal-scene addressed straight at a junkie by a narrator who pointedly declines to moralize — the record's title makes the theology explicit, and here the deity is either absent or actively cruel, answering each desperate reach for grace with another wound. That refusal is the whole trick: instead of the redemption arc the era's Christian metal kept selling, Araya's voice offers only clear-eyed witness, watching an American underclass rot in doorways while the anthem about liberty curdles into open sarcasm. The heroin registers not as sin but as anesthetic — the one mercy left after every institution meant to catch a person has quietly stopped counting him among the living. What lands hardest is the flat pledge of non-condemnation: not comfort, just a last human acknowledgment offered as the body finally gives out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","politics & society","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"a last human acknowledgment withheld from judgment as the body gives out","built":"heroin framed as the only mercy after every institution stopped counting him"},
"slayer~cast-the-first-stone":{"haiku":"A battle-hardened warrior seeks vengeance with absolute confidence and manifested power, ready to kill those who stand in his way.","opus":"The thrash titans distill their aggression into a creed of vengeance and combat. The narrator recognizes only one outcome, victory, demanding that any wrong be repaid in kind and living for the sheer ferocity of the fight. It's a taunt hurled at any challenger foolish enough to test him.","sonnet":"An unstoppable warrior speaks in pure aggression — battle is the only language, vengeance the only currency, defiance a granite weight no one can knock loose. The challenge is existential: stand in the way and bleed, or step aside.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~catalyst": {"haiku":"Attitude and competitive aggression are the speaker's core addiction; he sets the standard others follow, driven by fury and refusal to ever show weakness or remorse.","opus":"A ferocious statement of ego and hostility as identity: temperament is his fixation, rivalry his fuel, and every dismissal only hardens him further. It plays as a sketch of someone who exists for combat, defining his whole self through conquest and pitiless triumph without a shred of regret.","sonnet":"A declaration of total competitive dominance driven by pure attitude and aggression — the speaker lives for conflict, draws strength from others' cynicism, and frames every interaction as a war they intend to win.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pure adrenaline monologue that never breaks character: the speaker is competition itself, feeding on a rival's contempt so that every insult becomes fuel rather than wound. Slayer strips away the band's usual apocalyptic and blasphemous imagery for something almost sportive and gym-mirror, yet the relentless first-person present tense — knowing no other way — reads less as triumph than as a compulsion the singer names outright as addiction and affliction. The catalyst of the title is the enemy: the speaker only exists as reaction, defined entirely by the silence he screams into, so the boast quietly admits it has no self without an opponent to beat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the boast quietly admits it has no self without an opponent to beat","built":"relentless first-person present tense names rivalry as compulsion and affliction"},
"slayer~catatonic": {"haiku":"A killer describes their psychological numbness while deriving pleasure from torture and death, viewing violence as a form of control and spiritual satisfaction. Their sadistic tendencies are framed as exhilarating and hypnotic.","opus":"Slayer voice a torturer who feeds on his captive's terror and agony as a hypnotic, almost sacred ecstasy. Imagery of gore-drenched hands, a slow calculated killing and morbid keepsakes taken from the dead frames his sadism as an unbreakable addiction, murder recast as the sole ritual that makes him feel whole.","sonnet":"From the perspective of a serial killer describing the pleasure of methodical murder — the torture, the trophy-keeping, the fantasies of decapitation, the erotic charge of the victim's suffering. It's graphic and internal, placing you entirely inside a predatory psychology.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer's late-period cruelty at its most clinical: the narrator frames obsessive torture as a private faith, complete with liturgical language of acceptance and embrace, insisting he alone gives the world sight even as he suffocates it. The horror deepens through the title itself, which names both his victim's shut-down stillness and the trance the killer sinks into, unable to break off because the ritual has become its own hypnotic reward. What reads as a serial-murder narrative doubles as a portrait of addiction to control, where resurrecting another's mind is really a fantasy of owning it, and stolen closeness stands in for a tenderness he can only counterfeit through domination.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"stolen closeness as counterfeit tenderness achieved only through domination","built":"the title names both the victim's shutdown stillness and the killer's own hypnotic trance"},
"slayer~chasing-death":{"haiku":"An observer watches someone spiral into paranoia and self-destruction, chasing oblivion through addiction and despair with no exit and no way to stop.","opus":"Slayer turn their aggression toward watching someone destroy themselves, a person mired in discontent who digs their own grave and lies to their own face. The onlooker, disgusted, insists there's no escape as paranoia and inner madness mount. The conclusion, that the person is unknowingly hunting their own demise, lands as both warning and bleak resignation.","sonnet":"Someone watches a person they know spiral toward self-destruction — lying to themselves, digging deeper, enslaved to discontent. Time never stops, paranoia compounds the damage, and no amount of discipline or intervention breaks the pattern. The observer is both furious and helpless as the chase continues toward its only possible end.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~chemical-warfare": {"haiku":"Chemical and biological weapons devastate soldiers and populations in a hellish apocalypse. The song portrays mass death through poison gas and the collapse of civilization amid weaponized horror.","opus":"A vivid nightmare of poison-gas combat and wholesale slaughter, toxic clouds wiping out troops and civilians alike and casting their spirits down into hell.","sonnet":"The song depicts chemical weapons deployment on a battlefield — soldiers dying silently from a smell, bodies lying motionless, generals retreating — framed as a satanic spectacle in which hell's powers watch over the mass genocide with satisfaction.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer's early speed-metal apocalypse, where nerve-gas warfare is inseparable from a literal descent into Hell: every soldier felled by an unseen smell becomes fresh fuel for demons and a waiting Satan, so death is never an ending but a conscription. The song scales up through its verses from battlefield terror to mass genocide to a burning sky, mapping the chemical mist onto religious damnation until human beings are reduced to pesticide-killed vermin and mummified dust. What makes it more than shock is that refusal to separate the modern and the medieval—the technology of annihilation and the mythology of the pit are one machine, and it is the machine, not any nation, that wins.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"annihilation technology and damnation mythology fused as one machine that wins","built":"the modern nerve-gas mist and the medieval pit are never separated so the machine wins"},
"slayer~circle-of-beliefs":{"haiku":"Cyclical systems of faith and ideology; beliefs repeating endlessly, trapping the believer in circular reasoning.","opus":"Slayer channel blistering thrash into anti-dogma polemic, raging against indoctrination. They picture minds imprisoned in inherited belief, living a lie, mentally stunted, enslaved to values others chose for them, and conclude that faith itself would be no loss if it vanished. A furious rejection of the whole machinery of imposed religion.","sonnet":"A sustained attack on religious conditioning — born into doctrine that no longer fits, shaped by someone else's values until the self is erased, awaiting a resurrection that will never come in any useful form. The circle is the trap: the belief perpetuates the helplessness that requires the belief. The song sees it as stupidity enforced by tyrants, and the only way out is to recognise you do not want to be saved.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~cleanse-the-soul": {"haiku":"A corpse lies before a speaker engaged in a dark ritual, incense and candles burning as they recite passages to seal a sacrifice in unearthly fire.","opus":"Slayer's ritual of death and desecration, an occult ceremony reducing flesh to earth with clinical relish. Less about meaning than atmosphere, it revels in the impaling, the altar and the kingdom of desire waiting past the grave.","sonnet":"A ritualistic death ceremony narrated from the perspective of someone presiding over a corpse — the body is consigned to rot, candles burn at a dark altar, and the act is framed as an unearthly purification that opens the kingdom of desire.","src":"haiku","fable":"Hanneman and King build this in three movements, each lifting the act to a higher level of abstraction. It opens as plain murder — a killer standing over a corpse, sealing it underground to putrefy, the violence blunt and physical. The middle movement re-stages that same killing as liturgy: guttering candles, blackened church glass, incense, an altar dressed and waiting for its offering, so the crime is re-lit as a sacrament. By the close the scene dissolves and the song simply preaches a creed — that killing is a craft, that decay and the living world form one unbroken continuum, that the rite opens onto some coveted dominion. The title carries the whole track by irony: to cleanse here is to annihilate, and the soul is made pure only by being unmade.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"the title purifies by annihilating, soul made pure only by being unmade","built":"three movements lift a blunt killing into abstracted creed by ascending stages"},
"slayer~consfearacy": {"haiku":"Government, politics, and war breed conformity and propaganda that destroy critical thinking and individual autonomy.","opus":"A rant of political disgust with failed leaders, a derailed president and a broken economy, refusing to pretend or accept blind conformity. It frames a government that has become the enemy as breeding fear and anarchy, where imposing views and complacency alike lead to demise.","sonnet":"Political disgust and alienation: failed government, a wrecked economy, perpetual war, and an expectation of conformity — the narrator refuses to pretend and demands the right to think independently, seeing conspiracy and anarchy as the inevitable result.","src":"haiku","fable":"Kerry King writes this as a government-hating rant, and the title's spliced pun does the argument's work: manufactured dread and coordinated plotting are the same syllable, fear the mechanism by which a leaderless state governs. The speaker keeps needing to recatalog his contempt because each verse discovers the rot has moved from economy to war to his own compliance. The refrain's escalation is the real content: a lit fuse and forced doctrine curdle first into that fear-scheme, then into raw chaos, then into a private numbness the song brands as the listener's own ruin. Underneath the throat-shredding fury sits a bleaker admission than the rage lets on, that the narrator suspects his defiance is theater and that going along quietly may be the thing that kills him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","anger & defiance"],"means":"manufactured dread and coordinated plotting as the same governing syllable","built":"the title splices fear and conspiracy so they occupy the same word"},
"slayer~criminally-insane": {"haiku":"A predatory killer justified by their own nature, viewing violence as inevitable and imprisonment as their rightful place while promising continued killing.","opus":"A first-person portrait of a remorseless serial killer locked in an asylum, stalking doomed victims and treating his life sentence like sport. Marked and restrained yet defiant, he brags his killing has barely started, vows to break out, and dreams of wiping out humanity, feeling nothing for the path that carried him to his end.","sonnet":"A first-person monologue from a serial killer committed to a psychiatric institution — the speaker describes the murders as inevitable, treats escape and recapture as a game, and frames their ongoing violence as a drive that cannot be stopped or reformed.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer voices the killer from inside his own confinement, and the song's cruelty is that incarceration does not humble or reform him — it only sharpens him into a boast. The verses accept the life sentence with a shrug (the path he chose leads to the grave and he would take it again), but the escape he plots is framed as a game, and the institution's disapproval only prompts the taunt that he has barely begun. The genius of the write is the hunter's-refrain that clamps the song shut on both ends: night comes, he follows, the victim gets no tomorrow — a vow, not a memory, so the cage never actually closes on the threat it holds. The label criminally insane, meant to explain and contain him, becomes the identity he wears with pride, turning a clinical diagnosis into a promise of continuation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance","madness & the mind"],"means":"a clinical diagnosis worn with pride as a promise of continuation","built":"the hunter's refrain clamps both ends of the song so the cage never closes the threat"},
"slayer~crionics":{"haiku":"Preservation of the body after death in hope of resurrection; the obsession with defying death through technology.","opus":"From Slayer's 1983 debut Show No Mercy, this dread parable imagines cryogenic science freezing not just the body but the mind and spirit, humanity preserved under a doctor's cold ambition. The narrator resists, clinging to a single faint hope of a cure that might destroy the affliction itself. Early proof the LA thrash pioneers traded in clinical horror alongside the occult.","sonnet":"A man surrenders his body to cryonic sleep, gambling everything on a science that promises to freeze away death itself — his mind, his soul, his future suspended in a stranger's hands. He resists the doctors and lawyers who profit from his desperation, holding onto the thin hope that whatever kills him can still be beaten if only someone wakes him in time.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~crypts-of-eternity":{"haiku":"Sorcery and grave robbery unleash demonic forces from sealed tombs, corrupting priests and resurrecting ancient evils through forbidden transgression.","opus":"Early Slayer occult horror, a grave-robbing sorcerer descending into hell to seize power over death itself; it's pulp evil for its own sake, reveling in decay and damnation as pure adrenaline rather than any real theology.","sonnet":"A dark sorcerer descends into the underworld, pillages its power, resurrects demons, and claims dominion over death itself — a first-person declaration of absolute occult mastery over life, death, and the gates of Hell.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~cult":{"haiku":"Organized religion portrayed as oppressive propaganda that breeds abuse and corruption, using faith to exploit and control vulnerable people.","opus":"Slayer's blunt-force broadside against organized religion as a machine of fear, war and abuse. The provocation is total and deliberate, casting faith itself as the disease and choosing damnation over submission to what it sees as sanctioned cruelty.","sonnet":"An all-out assault on organised religion, accusing it of spreading violence, exploitation, and abuse — the speaker rejects Christianity entirely, naming it a contagion and aligning himself against the church's moral authority.","src":"haiku","fable":"Kerry King's blunt anti-faith tract treats belief as a control mechanism rather than a corrupted good, indicting the clergyman who hunts the isolated child that the doctrine hands him and the geopolitics of 2006 in one breath. The middle section abandons argument for a hammered inventory of nouns, each line pinning one ugly word onto the same subject until the concept collapses into pure chant. Across three returns the stance swings between flat denial that any figure ever died and the boast of personally driving the nails, hardening with each pass, so the closing embrace of the number reads less as a devil's oath than a spat retort chosen because self-styled innocence demanded its exact opposite.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","surveillance & control"],"means":"belief indicted as a delivery mechanism for predation and control","built":"the stance hardens with each return until flat denial flips to boasting of the nails","fnote":"The voice delivering King's broadside belongs to a practicing Catholic — Araya has said the lyrics are just words that will never interfere with what he believes, and sings them anyway."},
"slayer~darkness-of-christ": {"haiku":"A rejection of religious doctrine and divine authority, arguing that knowledge and reason reveal the hypocrisy and failure of organized religion.","opus":"A spoken apocalyptic monologue in which humanity abandons all religion, every faith crumbles, and the living begin longing for death. It traces the coming collapse of everything back to a single origin and lands on a flat, bleak verdict of divine hatred toward mankind.","sonnet":"A spoken-word prelude declaring that humanity's rejection of religious knowledge and the collapse of all religious structures have revealed that God is indifferent or hostile to mankind, framing this as the root of all chaos.","src":"haiku","fable":"Spoken flat as a coroner's report rather than sung, this opener stages an apocalypse of doctrine: a committee-voiced narrator announces that faith has been outgrown, its institutions have caved in, and the survivors trace every horror back to one unnamed originating event. The second half is the coup — it hijacks the measured cadence of the American founding charter and rewrites its promise into a snarl, so that Jefferson's guarantee of shared human standing becomes an admission that some arrive lesser and only the merciless inherit the wreckage. That parody of civic scripture rhymes with the record's own packaging — a nail-pierced Bible for a cover, liner pages of Job crossed out in marker — sacred text turned into evidence against itself. The closing verdict lands not as adolescent blasphemy but as a diagnosis reached by a mind that went hunting for the divine and found only contempt looking back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"sacred text turned into evidence against itself by its own language","built":"the founding charter's promise rewritten so only the merciless inherit the wreckage"},
"slayer~dead-skin-mask": {"haiku":"A serial killer (evoking Ed Gein) dances with the dead and fashions their bodies into trophies, losing grip on reality.","opus":"A first-person plunge into the mind of serial killer Ed Gein, caressing and dancing with the corpses and dead flesh he fashions into masks and ornaments; the sampled child's voice begging to be let out makes explicit the horror of his victims, while for Gein fantasy and reality have become indistinguishable.","sonnet":"From the perspective of Ed Gein — the narrator handles the skin of corpses with tenderness, dances with the dead in dreams, and a child's voice at the end pleads to be let out, making explicit that victims were imprisoned and killed.","src":"haiku","fable":"The closing sample says the name aloud — Ed Gein, the Wisconsin grave-robber who upholstered a house in human hide and seeded Norman Bates, Leatherface, and Buffalo Bill — so the song can spend its length inside his head rather than narrating him from a safe distance. Araya builds that interior as a seduction, opening in a courting murmur that welcomes a guest who will never be allowed to leave, then sliding into the tactile craft of fingertips grazing a corpse as though it were a lover. The masterstroke is one altered word: a refrain that first touches chilled dead tissue returns with the flesh gone warm, quietly announcing that the raw material has stopped being exhumed and started being freshly killed. Then the fourth wall shatters as a child's voice begs to stop playing and be let out, dragging the whole reverie down into a basement where a living victim is pleading. The horror lands because the band never flinches from the tenderness, forcing you to feel the affection the monster feels — the most damning thing it could possibly show you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind","death & grief"],"means":"the tenderness the monster genuinely feels is the most damning thing the song shows","built":"one word shifts from dead flesh to warm flesh, quietly announcing freshly killed not exhumed"},
"slayer~death-s-head": {"haiku":"A dark exploration of violence, murder, and obsession, depicting the speaker as a violent force drawn to bloodshed and possession of the victim.","opus":"A serial killer's first-person account of murder experienced as sexual thrill, dwelling on bludgeoned victims, gore and the satisfaction of leaving someone lifeless. The disturbing crux welds death to lust, the killer savoring his victim's fear and final moments while convinced he was singled out and entitled to kill. Slayer render this predator's psyche in unflinching, brutal thrash.","sonnet":"A first-person account of a mind sliding into violence and murder — bludgeoned bodies, blood on the walls, a compulsion to wake and shoot violence through someone, tasting their tears and wanting what's inside them; the song inhabits the perspective of a killer with no moral remove.","src":"haiku","fable":"A stalker's monologue that keeps sliding into the voice of the person being hunted, so that trigger, silence and violence all belong to both of them at once — the killer claims he only wakes what was already dead inside his victim, that he acts because he cares. It is Slayer's nu-metal turn on Diabolus in Musica, and the theme fits that grimier late-90s mode: not war or apocalypse but the intimate, self-justifying delusion of a predator who dresses obsession as love and hears God's silence as permission. The read that unlocks it is the refrain about wanting what is inside the head — the professed devotion is really a hunger to occupy and empty another mind, and the closing switch from waking the silence to hating it admits the possession was never mutual, only imposed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind","surveillance & control"],"means":"predator dresses obsession as love and hears silence as permission","built":"pronoun slides between hunter and hunted collapsing the boundary"},
"slayer~desire": {"haiku":"Obsessive passion blurs into murderous possession; the speaker conflates love with death and willingness to kill. Desire is framed as uncontrollable, carnal, and fatal.","opus":"An explicit fixation on lust for the dead, the narrator's ecstasy fastened on a corpse and a compulsion he claims he'd do anything to satisfy. Slayer collapse arousal and death into one grotesque obsession, treating a forbidden craving as something impossible to shake.","sonnet":"Sexual obsession shading into necrophilic fantasy: the narrator desires someone who is or becomes a corpse, feeling warmth from inside their skin and describing grotesque erotic compulsion as a fever that cannot be resisted.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a love song only in the way a stalker would use the word, its narrator vowing to kill for a devotion no one is allowed to doubt or cross, then making good on the threat. The object of that ardor is a corpse: the warmth the speaker keeps insisting he feels radiating through skin is body heat the body no longer has, a fever he blames on his own unresisting hunger rather than admit he manufactured it. Across the repeating verses the one word that keeps shifting is what he names his craving — passion, then sensation — and the ecstasy he pictures rots from something whole toward something putrid, the fantasy decaying in real time even as he refuses to let it go. In the standard Slayer manner the horror is delivered flat and first-person, with no distance offered and no judgment supplied, leaving the listener holding the mask of romantic language and the necrophile beneath it at once.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","madness & the mind"],"means":"romantic language held over a corpse the speaker refuses to release","built":"the craving word shifts from passion to sensation as the fantasy decays"},
"slayer~deviance":{"haiku":"A deviant killer embraces darkness and serves evil, skinning and murdering without guilt while viewing themselves as a twisted Christ figure.","opus":"Slayer wallow in unrepentant evil, a narrator pledged to darkness and eternal survival, flaying and terrorizing victims who beg an absent god for mercy. Stalking the streets for prey and draining life without a flicker of guilt, he swells into a blasphemous resurrected figure gorged on hatred, sneering that no divinity remains to save anyone.","sonnet":"A serial killer narrator speaks from inside the logic of his compulsion — stalking, watching death with detached pleasure, consuming the minds of victims, dismissing any god who might intervene. The darkness is presented as inhabiting its own coherent, if monstrous, interiority.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~die-by-the-sword": {"haiku":"A tyrant's violence spreads; Satan watches as victims fall, and only steel brings the vengeance and salvation those under subjugation seek.","opus":"A blasphemous power fantasy voiced by a murderous tyrant who slaughters, rapes and enslaves in the name of the Lord, mocking religious justification for atrocity while Satan watches approvingly.","sonnet":"A violent invocation of Satanic power over the weak and holy — the narrator relishes the slaughter of innocents, the rape of servants, and Satan's dominion over all as black knights walk over the dead.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer's debut cuts its power fantasy from cheap sword-and-sorcery cloth, and the teenage relish is the point: a first-person butcher promises slaughter in the same breath he blames the killing on holy words, dressing atrocity in scripture so the violence can pretend it was sanctioned. The song's engine is a theology of pure dominion, where a grinning devil rewards obedience and every plea toward heaven answers back only as the ring of a drawn blade. When it turns to the sorcerers and armored riders of its dungeon-crawl mythology, it exposes their arcane authority as a manufactured falsehood, priests and mages alike selling a story that keeps the helpless kneeling. Strip away the medieval costume and the pledge underneath is flat and hammered to the point of mantra: submit or be cut down, a schoolboy's occult bravado that closes on a mock incantation, half sneer and half joke.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"schoolboy occult bravado dressing atrocity in borrowed scripture","built":"medieval costume stripped away to expose arcane authority as manufactured falsehood"},
"slayer~disciple": {"haiku":"Anti-religious defiance and rejection of divine authority, claiming individual hatred and superiority over blind faith and conformity.","opus":"A militant rejection of faith and obedience, with the speaker refusing to serve God or shoulder any sacred burden. He proclaims a hostile deity who despises everyone, returns that contempt in equal measure, and positions himself alone against a hostile world.","sonnet":"A rejection of religion and of any faith in a god who, in the face of global violence, drug abuse, and human self-destruction, is either absent or actively hateful — the narrator insists on hating everyone equally rather than following any doctrine.","src":"haiku","fable":"A false-prophet sermon, sung by a narrator who casts the species as sleepwalkers and himself as the one who will jolt them awake into contempt. The chorus inverts the usual comfort: divine loathing, not love, is the cosmic given, and cruelty gets rebranded as a therapy the timid herd is too scared to attempt. Where the middle verses tally murder, self-harm, and dread of a spreading hell, the song's spine is refusal, not worship — the title looks devotional but the speaker spits it back, declining the cross and any creed that asks him to march behind another man's vision. Araya, a practicing Catholic, delivers all this in character, which keeps the venom pointed at discipleship itself rather than at one faith. What outlasts the recantation is a flat, exceptionless misanthropy, sealed by a closing curse that trades argument for pure recoil: one man left standing alone among enemies.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The album has no title track: God hates us all is this song's chorus, arrived at first by the spoken intro that hands it those three words — and the record carrying them reached stores on September 11, 2001, the release having slipped from a July date.","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"divine loathing rebranded as a therapy the timid herd is too scared to attempt","built":"the title looks devotional but the speaker spits it back refusing the cross"},
"slayer~dissident-aggressor-judas-pri":{"haiku":"Hooks embed in mind as identity fractures. Perpetual quest escalates toward final breath—no ending arrives.","opus":"A cover of Judas Priest's Cold War nightmare, a mind hooked and impaled at the Berlin wall, the divided city as the front line of an endless psychic siege. Paranoia and defiance held to the last breath.","sonnet":"A song about a mind subjected to massive psychological pressure — the narrator identifies with Berlin, a city that has been fought over and divided, while hooks have lodged in their brain and they drive forward through exploding and reloading, unable to stop until their last breath.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~dittohead":{"haiku":"Political conformity and blind following; mindless repetition of talking points without critical thought.","opus":"A vicious tirade against a legal order coming undone, where brutality is celebrated, murderers slip free on loopholes and a do-nothing state leaves the narrator no cause for remorse as he cheerfully orchestrates unpunished slaughter. Fixed sharply in 1994, Slayer aim their thrash at collapsing justice and media-stoked bloodlust, the title's lifted radio insult framing a country sliding into lawlessness.","sonnet":"A bitter snapshot of 1994 American legal decay — technicalities setting killers free, a passive government, violence elevated to entertainment and then to legality. The speaker inhabits the role of the person the system cannot touch: no punishment, no regret, a legislative stage built for exactly this kind of impunity. It is a diagnosis wearing the mask of the disease.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~divine-intervention":{"haiku":"God's punishment and divine retribution; suffering as spiritual consequence and celestial judgment.","opus":"A hallucination of being strapped down and violated by an unseen power, blinded and drained of memory, unable to meet God's face while pain becomes the only reality. The victim's identity fractures into many faceless selves, a martyr with no say in his own grace. The title track of Slayer's 1994 album, it channels the band's signature blasphemy inward, turning cosmic torment into a nightmare of medical and spiritual abduction.","sonnet":"A shattering visitation — paralysed by brilliant light, stripped bare, violated beyond the point of screaming. The self fractures: memory drains away, personality twists into many faces and none, and what remains is a martyr-specimen pinned by something that calls itself divine, offering no mercy and no reason, only pain.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~epidemic":{"haiku":"Disease spreads through human bodies uncontrollably, adapting to override immune defenses and causing slow inevitable death by infection.","opus":"Slayer render disease as a conquering army, a hidden pathogen thriving amid squalor, multiplying, and taking over the body from within. Framed as a merciless force testing human biology, the plague brings agony, internal ruin, and slow death, a permanent affliction indifferent to any hope of a cure.","sonnet":"Disease is cast as a sovereign force — breeding, adapting, overthrowing the body's defenses with deliberate intelligence. The clinical language of infection becomes a meditation on biological finality: some deaths are slow, irresistible, and take years before a cure arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~evil-has-no-boundaries":{"haiku":"Satanic aggression and demonic conquest; evil as an unstoppable force guided by Satan to subjugate and destroy.","opus":"A gleeful romp of cartoon Satanism and all-out war, the narrator and his legion smashing out of the underworld, returning stronger from death and squaring off against heaven under the devil's command. As the opener of Slayer's debut, it's early thrash at its rawest, wickedness proclaimed without apology.","sonnet":"Total war as a theological statement — Satan-aligned warriors blast through hell and come back stronger, clash with angels, consume souls, and declare that evil is uncontainable and unkillable. The stance is one of ecstatic commitment to destruction for its own sake, with midnight as the hour everything is unleashed and nothing left standing at the end.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~exile": {"haiku":"The speaker directs visceral hatred toward a self-righteous person, expressing suicidal ideation and violent rage born from their toxic relationship.","opus":"A raw, profane portrait of pure hatred toward someone who has driven the narrator to suicidal despair; his self-destructive rage curdles into homicidal fantasy as he tries to purge this toxic person from his life entirely.","sonnet":"Pure concentrated rage at a specific person whose presence has become psychologically unbearable — the narrator fantasizes about violence and death (their own and the other's) as the only escape from a relationship of mutual toxicity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup rendered as extermination, where the speaker cannot separate the wish to destroy someone else from the wish to be destroyed himself. Every threat arrives doubled: a bullet aimed at his own head sits beside the promise to tear the other person apart, so the fury never resolves into murder or suicide but keeps swinging between them. The title passes the only sentence a mind this saturated can reach, not death but banishment, the demand that a hated presence simply cease to exist within arm's length.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","suicide","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"fury that never resolves but keeps swinging between murder and self-destruction","built":"every threat arrives doubled: a bullet aimed inward beside the promise to destroy outward"},
"slayer~expendable-youth": {"haiku":"Young people caught in gang warfare and drug-fueled violence become expendable casualties, with death—not survival—as the inevitable outcome of rivalry and territorial obsession.","opus":"A grim account of gang violence that treats young lives as throwaway, a kid shot dead in a stray crossfire on his own block over territory and control. The song condemns a drug-fueled loop of rivalry and revenge in which death becomes the only possible outcome for these disposable kids.","sonnet":"About gang violence and urban drug warfare — young lives treated as disposable in turf battles where control and retribution are everything and the only resolution is death, stray bullets killing bystanders in a drug-induced permanent state of war.","src":"haiku","fable":"A gang-war song that refuses the safe outsider's vantage: the title stamps these kids as disposable, but the chorus voices their own creed back at us — possession, control, retribution as principle rather than pathology — so the horror is that the logic is internally coherent, not senseless. The verses do the accounting a news report would, a body face-down in blood filed as accidental homicide, while the chorus insists on the ideology that produced it, and Slayer lets the two registers sit unreconciled so neither cancels the other. Late, the frame widens to a drug-fueled economy where violence is named the only friend, which reads less as glorification than as the terminal point of a system that has already priced these lives at zero. The refusal to editorialize is the point: the band withholds the moralizing verdict precisely so the word expendable lands as an indictment of whoever set the price.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"withholding the moralizing verdict lets the word expendable indict whoever set the price","built":"gang creed and body count sit unreconciled in alternating registers so neither cancels the other"},
"slayer~eyes-of-the-insane":{"haiku":"Combat trauma haunts a soldier with vivid, intrusive memories of death and violence that break the mind into fragmented, tormented pieces.","opus":"A soldier destroyed by combat, haunted around the clock by ruined faces and intrusive memories, unable to sleep as trauma and panic tear at his mind. Death seems to stalk him relentlessly, refusing to leave him any peace.","sonnet":"A soldier is consumed by the psychological aftermath of combat — relentless flashbacks, mutilated faces haunting his dreams, shell shock and panic attacks that will not let him rest.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer built its name on outward atrocity, but this track swivels the lens inward, onto a veteran who cannot stop watching his own ruined reflection. The verses loop nearly identical waking nightmares, the same footage rerun with only cosmetic edits. The refrains march a whole century of clinical labels across one wound — trench-era coinage, interwar rename, modern acronym — each generation relabeling an injury none of them could heal. That the vocabulary keeps sliding back toward a personified Death, a hunter who finally rides down on the closing line, is the reading. No diagnostic name contains the terror, so the language reverts to the primal figure it was built to manage. The song never resolves because its structure refuses to; it circles the same plea over a machine that will not switch off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"Its album-mate Jihad reverses the lens — the attacker’s voice against this song’s broken soldier; Christ Illusion holds both ends of the same war.","themes":["madness & the mind","death & grief","war & battle"],"means":"a century of clinical labels marching across one wound none healed","built":"verses loop near-identical nightmares, the same footage rerun cosmetically"},
"slayer~fight-till-death":{"haiku":"Endless apocalyptic war with armored assassins, death squads, and destruction—warriors march to annihilation with no hope of escape or rebirth.","opus":"An early Slayer war-and-hell fantasy sends soldiers marching to certain death under orders to attack with no escape. Slaughtered children and a doomed world escalate into metallic gods raining destruction, the chorus turning apocalypse into a battle cry, pure formative-era thrash bombast about dying in combat and ruling the underworld.","sonnet":"Total war descends — armoured killers, children trampled, no future left to save. Warriors rise and fall under commands with no mercy attached. The song offers no moral frame or survivor, only the machinery of destruction operating at full capacity until nothing breathing remains. Hell is both destination and command structure.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~final-six":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic destruction and human-made extinction: gods demand surrender, corpses litter the earth, and people bring their own judgment through warfare and godless weapons. Death and the end of cycles dominate human suffering.","opus":"The thrash veterans paint an apocalyptic battlefield in graphic detail, faithless priests, poisoned water, charred bodies, and nuclear weapons poised to fire. A voice arriving to deliver death as a present drives extinction toward its close, biblical and atomic horror merging as humanity becomes the pyre it built and the reckoning arrives.","sonnet":"Apocalyptic warfare narrated from the perspective of an annihilating force — bombers, Sumerian death winds, godless warheads, and demonic armies converge on a civilisation whose own lust for power has written its end. The six of the title signals a final cycle; humanity is both the executioner and the condemned.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~flesh-storm": {"haiku":"War's brutal horrors and indiscriminate violence driving people to madness. Media profits from bloodshed while soldiers endure endless suffering.","opus":"Slayer's cold look at modern combat and the press that gorges on it, carnage reshaped into everyday spectacle for lenses as ravenous as addicts. The conclusion is absolute: brutality as an entire way of living, conflict with no remedy and no horizon beyond the final round.","sonnet":"A brutal indictment of war as a permanent, media-fed condition of human existence — shrapnel, screaming, and daily bloodshed treated as spectacle and entertainment. The song argues that violence is humanity's way of life and that war has no cure, leaving only death as the escape.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer's late-period position gives this its edge: two decades past the sport-death fantasias of the Reign in Blood era, King writes atrocity as a broadcast product, the audience implicated as consumer. The song stacks a battlefield against a newsroom until the two rhymes collapse into one economy — carnage manufactured on one end, an audience that hungers for it on the other, appetite feeding supply. Its refrain diagnoses the whole apparatus as faith stripped of a god, obedience without a reason worth the name, steered by handlers who feel no shame in the steering. What lifts it above a screed is the final turn: with the world already declared a corpse, the last instruction points the weapon inward, and the closing law reduces every cause to a lie men die for on somebody else's behalf.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","surveillance & control","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"carnage manufactured and consumed in one economy with no cause worth dying for","built":"the closing instruction turns the weapon inward after declaring the world already a corpse"},
"slayer~ghosts-of-war": {"haiku":"Fallen soldiers and warriors return from death to haunt the living with memories of violence, pain, and brutality they inflicted and endured.","opus":"The slain soldiers of past wars rise again, still bitter over battles that settled nothing, returning to inflict the suffering they carry. Spoken through a pitiless killer who drains life and dooms his targets, the song broods on war's endless, unhealing rage.","sonnet":"Fallen soldiers rising to claim the revenge they never got in life — dormant, forgotten warriors awakened by unfinished scores, dealing in pain and assassination, satisfying old scorn through the dead returning to finish what the living couldn't.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stages a resurrection: dead soldiers, denied the victories they died for, claw back into the world not as martyrs but as an appetite. Its first half is written from outside, watching the forgotten rise; the second half hands the microphone over to the risen thing itself, whose creed shrinks to a chant of draining life and sealing fate. That pivot is the whole point — grievance ferments into pure predation, and by the end the war-dead are indistinguishable from the killing that made them, no longer haunting the conflict but embodying it. The recurring refrain functions as the mechanism, scorn refusing to be satisfied so the cycle can never close. Slayer strip the war-ghost of any pathos South of Heaven's slower, grimmer register might invite and leave only the machinery of endless retribution.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"grievance ferments into pure predation until the war-dead embody what made them","built":"midway the song hands the microphone to the risen dead so mourning becomes machinery"},
"slayer~god-send-death": {"haiku":"A killer frames death as mercy and prayer while taunting their victim clawing at God, enacting a morbid symphony of torture and religious blasphemy.","opus":"A killer's deranged appetite, where ending a life is his sole desire, delivered as leering thrill at watching victims perish and appeal to a helpless deity; he ridicules a powerless god while relishing the pleasure of governing someone else's demise.","sonnet":"A sadistic narrator revels in the act of killing, describing the physical torture and dying screams of a victim as a form of perverse satisfaction, culminating in necrophilic violation and a nihilistic rejection of any god who might have intervened.","src":"haiku","fable":"A killer who speaks as if he were the hand of God, this is Slayer's oldest move — the God Hates Us All era just strips it of any myth and hands it to a plain sadist. The persona borrows the language of judgment (death sent, misery ended, sin prayed over) but the drive underneath is confessed outright as voyeuristic lust, the wish to watch someone die slowly framed as a morbid symphony he conducts. That gap is the whole song: divine agency is a costume over ordinary appetite, and the song makes you hold both the mask and the appetite at once. The final turn drops the disguise entirely, rounding on the victim who claws and screams for a god that never answers — the killer's contempt for that pathetic, deaf god quietly indicting the same authority he was ventriloquizing. It is less a story than a sustained sneer at the idea that any death arrives as justice rather than as someone's pleasure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"divine agency worn as costume over ordinary voyeuristic appetite","built":"the final turn rounds on the deaf god the killer was ventriloquizing all along"},
"slayer~hallowed-point": {"haiku":"A shooter uses a gun to make a point through killing, describing the ballistic destruction of flesh and will with clinical detachment.","opus":"Slayer render a gun's ballistics with clinical, almost worshipful precision, collapsing the distance between killer and weapon so that murder becomes a sacrament and revenge a cold anatomy lesson rather than passion.","sonnet":"A minute examination of what a bullet does to a human body — shattering skull, shredding brain, confetti of flesh — framed as the speaker's bitter, deeply personal act of revenge carried out with precise and ecstatic violence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer open in a coroner's register, cataloguing what a round does to a body at close range with the flat precision of a firearms manual. Then the cruelty turns theological: loading and firing get described as a sacrament, the shooter handling the chamber and trigger as holy objects and treating each killing as a consecration performed on strangers. That blasphemy is the edge Hanneman and Araya are sharpening, worship curdled into slaughter, a rite with no god standing behind it — the title fusing the hollow-point round with the language of consecration. The last verses drop the ritual poise and expose the man underneath, all soured vengeance and shaking compulsion, so the weapon stops being a sacred tool and becomes the void his rage pours into. Read cold, it is the portrait of a mind that has dressed its own emptiness up as a religion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"worship curdled into slaughter, a rite with no god standing behind it","built":"the hollow-point round and the language of consecration fused in the title"},
"slayer~hardening-of-the-arteries":{"haiku":"Physical decay and the deterioration of the body; mortality advancing inexorably as the heart hardens and systems fail.","opus":"An apocalyptic vision of a planet choking on its own hatred, where fear runs through the veins of the world and doom arrives like a precise mallet blow, life extinguished as futile. The narrator watches civilization convulse into paralysis with no one left to save it. Slayer built their reputation on exactly this bleak, cataclysmic thrash, and this closes their landmark 1986 record in the pitiless nihilism that defined their peak.","sonnet":"A vision of civilizational collapse — the world diseased with hate and fear, death not an accident but an inevitability baked into the future. Sadistic forces prolong a decaying existence rather than ending it cleanly, and humanity convulses helplessly toward extinction with no savior coming.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~hate-worldwide": {"haiku":"A godless heretic spreads religious doubt and hatred as a tool of global devastation; they weaponize moral corruption against faith itself, treating God as an infection to be cured.","opus":"A militant atheist's provocation that treats religion as a virus and disbelief as a weapon, Araya spitting deliberate blasphemy to needle the faithful and stage doubt itself as an act of aggression.","sonnet":"A self-declared godless heretic treats religion as an infection to be spread against — he instigate doubt, denies divine authority, and equates his aggression with a kind of global devastation aimed at the walls of religious deception.","src":"haiku","fable":"A militant unbeliever speaks in the first person, treating faith less as a debate than a sickness he means to scour out by force. The verses build a creed — he doubts scripture and creation, and turns contempt for religion into a mission delivered with a gunman's calm, the denier mirroring the very fanatic he claims to despise. What lifts it past a slogan is the ending, where the chanted refrain snaps mid-word: the phrase about broadcasting his malice breaks off and reopens as a torn wound, the scaffolding of belief caving in and a victim silenced for good. Kerry King, the band's outspoken atheist, wrote a blunt anti-scripture tract whose closing rupture reads less as argument won than as doubt hardening into a dogma of its own.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"atheist contempt mirroring the very fanaticism it scorns","built":"the refrain snaps mid-word as scaffolding of belief caves in"},
"slayer~haunting-the-chapel": {"haiku":"Demonic forces assault a church, desecrating the holy altar while priests attempt futile prayer, ultimately destroying the sacred space and claiming dominion over it.","opus":"A satanic reverie in which corpses and infernal fiends besiege a church at the stroke of twelve, torturing the praying clergy, corrupting the sanctuary and torching the crucifix, a gleeful inversion where the divine stronghold collapses.","sonnet":"Hell's forces invade a church — demons, ghosts, and Satan's soldiers destroy the altar, attack praying angels, and burn the cross, ending with God's house fallen and the chapel belonging to the dead.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer's second EP arrives as a splatter-film liturgy inverted: the demonic occupies the vantage of scripture, treating the crucifix as an instrument of deceit rather than salvation, so the whole song reads the Mass backward from the pews of the damned. It moves like a raid clock — a midnight bell wakes the buried, spirits breach the sanctuary, the tabernacle is smashed, and the building is finally repossessed by rot. Beneath the under-eye warpaint and inverted-cross theatrics sits the young band's real subject: staged sacrilege as spectacle, faith narrated by its enemy so that every rite curdles into its opposite and the sacred architecture simply collapses on schedule.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder"],"means":"staged sacrilege as spectacle, every sacred rite curdled into its opposite","built":"the demonic occupies scripture's vantage and narrates the Mass backward from the damned's pews"},
"slayer~hell-awaits":{"haiku":"Satan and his demons claim victory over God and humanity through death and damnation, inviting souls into eternal torment. The song celebrates satanic triumph and infernal punishment.","opus":"The title track of Slayer's second album flips Christian salvation on its head, a tempter promising a doomed soul release from the grave at no cost beyond allegiance. Heaven's defenders fall, infernal gates lie in wait, and Christ is jeered as powerless, the thrash pioneers spinning a lurid hell-myth where damnation is the only sure thing.","sonnet":"Hell's gates stand open and the narrator — voicing Lucifer or a demonic lieutenant — invites the condemned inside, promising no cost of entry. Angels fall, God is dethroned, and each soul arrives through one of seven roads to the burning hole. The welcome is patient and absolute; damnation is framed as homecoming.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~here-comes-the-pain": {"haiku":"A merciless destroyer relishes causing suffering; he brings pure devastation and breaks everyone who crosses him without exception or reprieve.","opus":"A pure villain's boast: an unstoppable force of brutality, torture and annihilation announcing that everyone falls and the listener is just another nameless victim in his wasteland of destruction.","sonnet":"A first-person declaration of pure brutality and dominance — the narrator positions himself as an unstoppable force of destruction, a ticking bomb who levels everyone in his path and leaves them broken on the ground.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person aggressor talks himself up into a deity of cruelty, hoarding titles for anarchy, torture, and slaughter until the swagger becomes its own weather system. The turn is that his target is only ever addressed, never answered: named, promised ruin, and by the last verse left on the ground gazing up while the speaker keeps his footing. That imbalance is the whole design. God Hates Us All trades Slayer's older occult and battlefield imagery for a flatter, secular menace, so the track reads less as a threat than a coronation of pure aggression that has stopped needing any reason to exist.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"aggression crowned as a self-sustaining deity","built":"target addressed but never answered, imbalance as architecture"},
"slayer~human-strain":{"haiku":"Humanity itself is a plague—disease and war spread through population growth, and the human capacity for self-hatred ensures collective destruction.","opus":"A bleak forecast of contagion as nature culling humankind, tainted blood, genetic mutation and technology steering a swelling population toward plague and seas running crimson at the reckoning. Slayer, the Californian thrash titans, unleash this apocalyptic dread from the Christ Illusion era, Jeff Hanneman's fixation on war and sickness fueling relentless visions of ruin and revived pestilence.","sonnet":"Humanity as its own plague — the song argues that disease, war, and environmental collapse are not visited on the species from outside but generated from within, a self-contaminating cycle encoded in the genes. Population growth spreads the infection, and the song's imagery of tainted blood and skinless resurrection frames extinction not as catastrophe but as a kind of logical self-correction.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~implode": {"haiku":"Civilization decays through greed, deception, and religious obsession; the speaker watches humanity implode and finds no divine salvation in the end.","opus":"Slayer's apocalyptic disgust at a species drowning in its own greed, ignorance and cowardly blame, the narrator relishing the collapse he sees coming. No god watches over any of it, only the choice to inject more hate and let the rot finish.","sonnet":"A nihilistic survey of human civilization as a self-destroying organism — corrupt leaders, blind religion, personal blame-shifting, and collective stupidity are all symptoms of a species that deserves to implode, and the narrator revels in watching it happen while maintaining there is no god watching over any of it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Kerry King watches civilization as a corpse already fossilizing in the mud, and the song's cruelty is that it stages the collapse as spectacle — the whole species summoned to witness its own extinction as if it were the last event on a sold-out calendar. What lifts it past ordinary nihilism is the pivot midway, where the narrator stops describing the ruin and starts confessing to it, admitting he will keep feeding the hatred himself because that at least brings the argument to an end. The chant that names the extermination wears the mask of a righteous purge, but underneath it is only a wish for the end, repeated until it sounds like a prayer the singer wants answered. Beneath the atheist bravado runs a coward's logic the lyric quietly pins on everyone else — every grievance flung outward, never turned inward — while the speaker buys his own exemption solely by owning his malice out loud.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The lyric's if-proven-wrong escape hatch cites the band's own catalog: the song it says you already know is God Hates Us All, the 2001 album title offered as the standing verdict.","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","faith & the occult"],"means":"the extermination chant wearing a righteous purge as its mask","built":"narrator pivots from describing ruin to confessing he feeds it"},
"slayer~in-the-name-of-god":{"haiku":"A manipulative figure preys on the vulnerable through deception cloaked in religious authority, representing evil disguised as divine truth.","opus":"A predatory speech luring a faithful listener into loathing and deceit spoken under divine cover, the speaker seeping into and devouring his target, defiling the inner sanctum of the mind before declaring the Antichrist to be what people call God. Slayer, the thrash institution, pour their career-long anti-religious venom into this, its blasphemous hook and images of grooming framing belief itself as a swindle.","sonnet":"A predator speaking from inside institutional religion — the speaker addresses a victim directly, cataloging the steps of grooming and violation behind a veil of divine authority. The final equation, naming the Antichrist as the actual name of God in this context, is the song's blunt accusation: that systems of faith have been instruments of the same predation they claim to forbid.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~jesus-saves": {"haiku":"Religious faith is critiqued as a false escape relying on an invisible authority figure and promises of an afterlife that provide no real salvation. The song exposes how belief in a savior becomes a way to avoid personal responsibility.","opus":"A blunt anti-Christian broadside sneering at believers who grovel in faith and bank on paradise while wasting their lives. Its real argument is that trusting an unseen deity is misplaced dependence, and that in the end no reward or rescue is waiting for the faithful at all.","sonnet":"A direct attack on Christian faith as willful self-delusion — the song argues that those who spend their lives in prayer and submission to an invisible God are wasting themselves, and that no pearly gates await them, only the emptiness of a devotion that leads nowhere.","src":"haiku","fable":"Araya opens by mocking the believer as a coward buying insurance against death, kneeling and flattering the Lord out of self-interest rather than any real conviction. But the song does something sharper than sneering: across its three choruses the salvation contract itself collapses, the refrain souring from taunt to verdict. First the heavenly reward is dangled, then the celestial threshold has been bartered off for money and the worshipper is left stranded, and finally there is no destination waiting at all and no way around that fact. What began as a jeer at one hypocrite becomes, at breakneck compression, a flat denial that the whole redemption economy ever had anything to deliver.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","money & the street"],"means":"the redemption economy dismantled verse by verse","built":"choruses sour in sequence from taunt to flat denial"},
"slayer~jihad":{"haiku":"Religious violence and holy war justified by faith in God.","opus":"A chilling depiction of holy war and religious terrorism, voiced as a jihadist's fanatical instructions to kill nonbelievers in God's name.","sonnet":"The September 11 attacks narrated from the perspective of a hijacker framing the act as holy war and martyrdom.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole provocation is a matter of voice: Araya inhabits a September 11 hijacker in first person and refuses the editorial distance a safer band would build. What makes it damning is the hook, which frames the atrocity as a sanctified campaign and names no combatant, so the charge rebounds onto every faith that blesses slaughter. Then the outro sheds the persona and adapts, in flat instructional prose, the motivational letter found in the hijackers' luggage — the calm directive to betray nothing, to kill without pity, to strike toward eternity. That pivot from imagined fury to found instruction is the argument, since the band's real provocation was that horror this cold already existed in writing, and they declined to look away.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","war & battle"],"means":"atrocity framed as sanctified campaign with no combatant named","built":"the pivot from first-person persona to found hijacker instructional prose"},
"slayer~live-undead": {"haiku":"Trapped undead, the narrator experiences madness and cannibalistic impulses from within a coffin, neither alive nor dead but eternally tortured.","opus":"The claustrophobic horror of being buried alive and undead, nailed in a coffin as the body decays yet the mind persists in madness; voices oppress the narrator's brain until a split decision to die, dissolving into a cannibalistic zombie hungering for flesh and blood.","sonnet":"About the horror of existing after death — the narrator is buried but still conscious, experiencing madness, cannibalistic impulses, and a war inside the skull between life and death drives, eventually surrendering to the grave.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer stage a burial that refuses to end: consciousness sealed underground while the flesh rots, sight and hearing outliving the corpse they are trapped inside. The real horror isn't the grave but that nothing ever switches off — dirt landing overhead still registers, decay is felt in real time, and the mind, denied any exit, turns predator on itself. Midway the song abandons the coffin for a purely interior siege, where clashing voices roar like weather and an antipsychotic floods the bloodstream to sedate a terror no drug can reach. Slayer make the undead state not a monster but a sentence — punishment measured as perpetual, wide-awake rot rather than any release into oblivion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"consciousness denied any exit turns predator on itself","built":"song shifts from coffin to purely interior siege mid-track"},
"slayer~love-to-hate":{"haiku":"An evil figure revels in hatred and violence, desensitizing the world through malevolent reign and bloodlust.","opus":"A first-person portrait of a mind that has metabolized the world's contempt into fuel, daring an enemy to hate but never to kill, feeding on rage as if on a drug. The stance is nihilistic self-deification, an ungodly shrine built from spite. Slayer sit at the founding edge of American thrash, and this later-era track keeps their signature: blasphemy and violence rendered as sheer velocity rather than argument.","sonnet":"A portrait of a consciousness so saturated with violence and contempt that hatred becomes its own sustaining fuel — the speaker dwells in a personal hell, goading the world to finish him off while knowing it can't, addicted to his own malevolence the way others are addicted to drugs, desensitizing everyone around him as a kind of revenge.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~mandatory-suicide": {"haiku":"Soldiers are sent into war, ambushed by overwhelming fire and explosion, forced to witness death and mutilation around them. The song portrays the horror of combat and the futility of military sacrifice.","opus":"Slayer's unflinching ground-level view of infantry warfare as state-sanctioned self-destruction, soldiers fed into carnage where survival itself becomes the slow suicide; the anti-war fury lies in stripping combat of any glory.","sonnet":"A graphic, unflinching depiction of dying in combat — the song catalogs every form of battlefield death in brutal clinical detail: sniper fire, machine guns, land mines, impalement, amputation — presenting war not as heroism but as a meat-grinder that turns human bodies into wreckage.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer take the war-poem tradition of the individual death and industrialize it: the title's oxymoron reframes being conscripted into a killing field as a suicide made compulsory, the state authoring your death while calling it your duty. The lyric refuses any single sufferer, cutting between sniper, machine gun, mines and impalement so no wound gets a face and the body becomes an anonymous unit fed to the line. That flatness is the argument — where the era's protest songs mourned a soldier, this one denies the mourning, letting the massacre pile into littered streets where blood is declared cheap and everywhere. The closing verdict that it is insane is not a moral gasp but the song's plain thesis stated flat, the horror lodged in how mechanical and unremarkable mass death has been rendered.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"the state authors your death and calls it duty","built":"no wound gets a face; the body becomes an anonymous unit"},
"slayer~metal-storm-face-the-slayer":{"haiku":"Crushing sonic assault and metallic warfare; the sound itself is weaponized death dealing destruction.","opus":"From Slayer's debut era, this two-part piece casts the band itself as an undying slasher stalking prey through an endless, fog-choked labyrinth on a frozen night. Swinging his blade through the victim's defenses and cornering them in an occult trap, he boasts of being an everlasting horror whose hunt has no end, staged in the theatrical horror mode of their earliest work.","sonnet":"An indestructible predator stalks its prey through a frozen, mist-filled maze — eternal, patient, and certain of victory. The hunted panics, breaks, cowers, and is finally cornered inside a pentagram. The power differential is absolute and the outcome never in doubt; the chase is the only point.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~mind-control":{"haiku":"Manipulation of consciousness and psychological domination; stripping someone of autonomy and replacing it with external control.","opus":"A predator's boast from inside another person's skull, seizing every sensation, erasing memory and identity until only a hollow carcass remains. The speaker owns his victim's reasoning, feeding on inconsistency, promising cerebral death that arrives even in sleep. Slayer built their name on this kind of first-person menace, and here the LA thrash veterans locate the horror not in gore but in the total demolition of a mind that never sees the intruder coming.","sonnet":"Someone else has seized the controls inside your skull — your will dissolves, memory gaps open, reasoning crumbles into incoherence. The song maps a methodical psychic erasure: the predator moves through victims unseen, leaving hollow bodies behind, and the horror is that it can all happen in your sleep.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~necrophiliac":{"haiku":"Desecration of the dead and sexual perversion; violation of corpses as the ultimate transgression.","opus":"A deliberately transgressive horror narrative of a corpse-defiling ghoul rising at night to violate the dead, spawning a demonic child that bursts from a broken casket and drags souls to hell. From Slayer's early catalogue, it's shock-thrash at its most extreme, the band courting outrage with graphic Satanic gore, the desecration and its monstrous offspring pure provocation aimed at the boundaries of taste.","sonnet":"Extreme transgression rendered as supernatural horror — a figure rises at midnight, violates the grave, and the act generates a demonic offspring that then turns on its own creator. The escalation is relentless: desecration, birth, vengeance, damnation. It operates entirely within the logic of Satanic mythology rather than as anything psychologically interior.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~necrophobic": {"haiku":"A graphic catalog of torture, mutilation, and death described with clinical precision, depicting a mind obsessed with destroying bodies. The song ends with fear of death becoming the subject's own paranoia.","opus":"A relentless catalog of bodily horror, execution, mutilation, decay, that culminates in the title's meaning: necrophobia, an uncontrollable, paranoid terror of death and dying. The graphic dismemberment isn't gore for its own sake but the imagined machinery of death that the narrator is scared to face.","sonnet":"A clinical horror catalog of physical torture and bodily destruction — strangulation, amputation, suffocation, electrocution — rendered in rapid, almost medical language. The final line reveals the core: this is about a terror of death and corpses, a paranoid fear that won't let go.","src":"haiku","fable":"For most of its length the song is an unbroken catalogue of clinical atrocity — paired abstract nouns hammered in rhythm, strangulation, dissection, infection, erosion — a numbed morgue-report voice that names damage without ever inhabiting it, until a single question breaks the litany to ask what it feels like to die in the electric chair. That crack is the whole point: the closing title finally supplies the speaker, a mind not gloating over the carnage but drowning in dread of it, the necrophobic paranoia of one who cannot stop cataloguing death because he is terrified of his own. Slayer, at their most surgical here, weaponize the coroner's detachment so that the abrupt confession of fear lands harder than any of the gore that armored it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind","violence & murder"],"means":"the cataloguer is undone by his own fear of dying","built":"coroner's detachment armors a confession of dread until the title breaks it"},
"slayer~new-faith":{"haiku":"Rejects religious belief and biblical authority, embracing heresy and personal power as liberation from faith-based systems of control.","opus":"A blasphemous rejection of religion, mocking a holy man's faith and rejecting biblical prophecy, as the speaker sets himself up as a false savior offering a new faith of indulgence and evil.","sonnet":"The narrator rejects organised religion as a system of lies and control for weak minds, and invites listeners to abandon it in favour of a new, self-defined creed built on chaos, indulgence, and personal heresy.","src":"haiku","fable":"Most of this album just curses heaven, but this song is colder and more architectural. Rather than abandoning belief, it reoccupies it, wearing the vocabulary of scripture to crown itself in the seat it just toppled. The narrator preaches a conversion, razing the sanctuary and then climbing into the empty pulpit to demand the very worship he mocked. So the promised freedom is really one master swapped for another. Its cruelest move is the bargain underneath: where every creed dangles rescue, this one guarantees that clinging to breath will only teach you to beg for your own ending. Taken straight it sounds like glorious defiance, but worn as a mask it diagrams how any cult runs identically, which is why the close has nothing left to chant but its own name.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","surveillance & control"],"means":"liberation that is really one master swapped for another, the pulpit seized the moment it was emptied","built":"the vocabulary of scripture survives the toppling intact, worn by the narrator who climbs into the seat he just knocked over"},
"slayer~not-of-this-god":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects religious control and criticizes faith institutions as sources of fear, manipulation, and psychological slavery that crush free thought.","opus":"One of Slayer's most militantly anti-Christian late-career tracks, sung from the mouth of an unrepentant heretic who wakes each day to what he hears as coercive religious messaging and rejects it outright. He recasts himself as a destroyer sent to topple the faith, treating organized belief as a form of enslavement and blasphemy as a battle cry.","sonnet":"A furious rejection of organised religion as a mind-enslaving, coercive force that burns dissenters and breeds weakness. The speaker positions himself as a rational weapon against theological conformity — immune to holy water, contemptuous of the crucifix, determined to expose every inconsistency until the structure collapses.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~overt-enemy":{"haiku":"A manipulative figure infiltrates and controls minds through ideology and fear, spreading contempt for institutions while enslaving others.","opus":"One of Slayer's final studio statements adopts the voice of an authoritarian ideologue who polices thoughts, infiltrates, and burns church and state to reinstate a new worldview. The Californian thrash veterans channel their long anti-institutional streak into a portrait of contemptuous power, the speaker preaching death and casting out the demigod while enslaving the meek behind his back.","sonnet":"An authoritarian voice catalogues its own methods without shame — surveillance, manipulation, fabricated ideology, calculated violence. The confession is also a boast: power needs no justification, every institution from church to state is a tool to be burned or wielded, and the victims inherit nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~payback":{"haiku":"Violent vengeance against perceived enemies and oppressors. The speaker seeks to inflict maximum pain and suffering on those they deem deserving.","opus":"Pure retaliatory fury, a threat delivered face to face against someone who drew first blood, escalating from insult to a promise of dismemberment and a refusal to be anyone's scapegoat. The arc never softens; it only sharpens toward vengeance so total the speaker wants the kill for himself. Slayer built their reputation on this kind of unrelenting thrash, and here the profanity itself becomes the rhythm, hatred stripped of any metaphor.","sonnet":"Pure concentrated vengeance directed at a parasite who drew first blood — the speaker refuses the role of scapegoat and instead promises annihilation so thorough and personal that he wants his enemy alive long enough to deliver it himself, the whole thing pulsing with the righteousness of someone who has never known defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~perversions-of-pain":{"haiku":"A tormented mind projects sadistic visions and mental torture onto others; the narrator lords over suffering as an art form and spiritual practice.","opus":"Slayer voice a sadistic first-person predator who commands his victim to remember him and witness his violent, chaotic mind. He revels in defiling others, dancing over the dead, and turning suffering into a refined pleasure. The escalating boast of ever-greater agony makes the song a portrait of cruelty pursued purely for its own thrill.","sonnet":"A mind that has refined violence into pleasure declares its sovereignty over chaos and suffering. Pain is not endured here but cultivated — a higher sensation, a realm of torture-stimulation the speaker inhabits and invites others to witness. The horror is presented with the cool pride of connoisseurship.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~piano-wire":{"haiku":"A vision of apocalyptic warfare where a traitor is executed by piano wire; the narrator urges relentless military combat with fanatical devotion to victory.","opus":"Built around strangulation as its governing image, the song blurs war, prophecy and holy destiny into an apocalyptic bloodletting where surrender is unthinkable. A relentless chant frames slaughter as sacred duty, the narrator taunting even the dead as his murderous creed carries the world toward its end.","sonnet":"Wartime betrayal and its punishment: a traitor within the ranks faces execution, the piano wire of the title a grimly delicate instrument of death. The broader frame is total war — blood, attack, refusal to surrender — with a philosophy of violence treated as righteousness.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~piece-by-piece": {"haiku":"A sadistic killer dismembers victims for gratification, treating murder as a form of collection and expressing complete indifference to human life and suffering.","opus":"A first-person serial-killer fantasy of dismembering a victim piece by piece, an emotionless butcher who mutilates flesh and bone, keeps a head on his wall, and offers his prey no escape but death.","sonnet":"A relentless first-person account from a killer who dismembers victims, collects body parts, and feels no emotion — the song presents a methodical, sadistic predator for whom flesh is the only currency.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person butcher narrates murder as a form of appetite, and the dread is possessive rather than merely gory: taking a body apart is how he ingests a stranger, converting a person into inventory he owns and mounts on a wall. The title's method doubles as his creed — a self built by absorbing whoever he catches, the corpse claimed as an extension of him. King writes it as pure predator cognition, no motive or remorse offered, just the cold subtraction of one life narrowing toward zero; the closing execution register, a marksman's dot settling on the sternum with no escape granted, reframes the slaughter as procedure conducted with the boredom of routine labor.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"murder as ingestion, the corpse absorbed into the killer's self","built":"closing execution rendered in the bored register of routine procedure"},
"slayer~playing-with-dolls":{"haiku":"Sexual perversion and sadistic control; treating partners as disposable playthings to manipulate and destroy.","opus":"A first-person plunge into a serial killer's psyche, addicted to the act, unable to stop once he begins, dressing corpses as playthings in his funeral home while mocking the victim's faith. The horror is the compulsion he knows will only end with his own death. Slayer trafficked in this transgressive gore throughout their career, and here they extend their fixation on death and blasphemy into the intimate register of a mind that has come apart.","sonnet":"A serial killer's interior monologue delivered from inside a funeral home — the speaker addresses a victim with perverse intimacy, cycling between guilt and compulsion. Children's voices haunt the killer's head, God is useless, and the torture is less about the victim than about the speaker's own screaming need that only death can quiet.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~point":{"haiku":"Direct assault and penetrating violence; getting to the core of destruction with precision and intent.","opus":"A remorseless first-person voice of warfare as pure extermination, boasting of inflicting agony and gunning down soldiers where the tally of corpses is all that matters. Slayer distill their long fascination with the mechanics of killing into unrelenting thrash, the speaker a merciless herald of death dealing out the genre's most brutal savagery.","sonnet":"A war machine's first-person manifesto — infiltrating shores, tasting blood, basking in dying breath. Point is both a geographic coordinate and the verb of total annihilation: everything converges here, everyone dies here. The second half shifts into infantry imagery — M16 magazines emptied, flesh fertilising the ground — before closing on pure body-count arithmetic with no remorse attached.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~postmortem": {"haiku":"A person drowns in suicidal ideation and madness while fragments of consciousness attempt to comprehend death; skeletons in the mind destroy sanity.","opus":"Slayer meditate on death and the mind buckling toward it, staging a funeral for human despair where the wish to die becomes the strange reason to keep living. Visions of skeletons clawing at reason and bodies born already carrying their own ending cast life itself as a doomed procession toward the grave.","sonnet":"A meditation on death-seeking as the only act that feels meaningful: the narrator is drawn to death not as an end but as the one experience that gives his degraded existence structure, inviting others to share his insanity and find him waiting on the other side.","src":"haiku","fable":"A suicide narrated from inside a mind coming apart, Jeff Hanneman's lyric follows a man who treats self-destruction as a doorway rather than an ending, cinching the noose as a grim experiment. The middle stanzas turn paradoxical: he mutters desperate incantations against oblivion while deciding that the craving for extinction is the one thing keeping him upright — a knot of logic closer to real depressive spiral than to any horror-movie prop. Then the voice fractures. The closing movement drops the third-person report and speaks as something already across the threshold, an animate corpse dragging itself upward with contempt for the living it left behind. What opened as one man's exit becomes a hungry, returning consciousness that wants only what lies past the flesh. Fittingly Slayer built the track to bleed straight into the storm that shuts the record, so the death here plays less as a conclusion than as the summons for whatever comes next.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","death & grief","madness & the mind"],"means":"the craving for extinction named as the one thing keeping him upright","built":"track bleeds directly into the next storm, staging death as summons not conclusion"},
"slayer~pride-in-prejudice":{"haiku":"Violence becomes proof of power and prejudice drives murder; martial law and gunfire execute innocents while media hype fuels racial hatred.","opus":"Slayer aim their political fury at the abuse of authority and the way outrage curdles into mob violence, voiced by a self-righteous zealot who treats brutality as proof of his convictions. With media sensationalism and bigotry stoking the flames, the song shows how a single act of aggression sets off a self-perpetuating spiral of hatred and retaliation.","sonnet":"A portrait of mob violence dressed in righteous language — power is abused, civil order breaks down, and one gunshot becomes the founding precedent for everything that follows. The song does not advocate the violence so much as anatomize how it functions: racial hatred fuels the fire, media amplifies it, and the side with more bodies declares itself right.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~psychopathy-red":{"haiku":"A psychopath methodically hunts, mutilates, and kills victims for sexual gratification and control.","opus":"Reportedly drawn from a Soviet serial murderer, Slayer inhabit a predator whose gratification peaks only when his victim expires, gorging on their dread and cries. Depraved fantasies acted out, mutilation and violation get twisted into his idea of completion, and a clinical refrain about methodical killing renders his pleasure with chilling detachment.","sonnet":"A first-person monologue from inside a serial killer's mind, rendered in clinical and visceral detail — the systematic hunting, the sexualised violence, the post-mortem gratification. There is no remorse and no outside perspective; the horror is delivered from within the pathology itself.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~public-display-of-dismemberment": {"haiku":"Society decays into lawlessness and violence; hypocrisy and broken politics breed fear, punishment, and graphic suffering on display.","opus":"A bleak snapshot of a society collapsing into anarchy, martial law, economic ruin and corrupt hypocrisy; the grisly title becomes a metaphor for how a broken, lawless system publicly tears its people apart.","sonnet":"A furious diagnosis of societal collapse — lawlessness, martial law, broken politics, and systemic hypocrisy — where the only logic left is public mutilation as spectacle and deterrent.","src":"haiku","fable":"A catalogue of collapse recited like a headline crawl: martial law, decaying institutions, retaliatory violence spooling on beneath the flat certainty that no alternative remains. The title turns that theme into method, staging the body's undoing as spectacle, punishment made visible so that fear itself does the governing. What tips it from complaint toward horror is the closing verse, where cruelty is rationalized as incentive and penance is kept deliberately near, the logic of a regime that has learned to weaponize suffering rather than merely permit it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","violence & murder"],"means":"suffering weaponized as governance rather than merely permitted","built":"the title frames bodily ruin as the regime's public spectacle"},
"slayer~raining-blood": {"haiku":"Damnation from the supernatural; souls of the betrayed rain down as judgment and reprisal. The condemned rise in crimson vengeance to establish dominion through blood.","opus":"An apocalyptic scene of a spirit stranded in a between-world, waiting for retribution as the heavens flush crimson and pour down gore. The souls of the deceived dangle overhead while the narrator swears to rule through bloodshed.","sonnet":"A figure trapped in a purgatory-like state, betrayed by those close to him, rises with the turning sky to reclaim power over those who wronged him—the enemies of his past now hang above him as ornaments while blood rains down from a lacerated sky. The song ends with a declaration of dominion through blood.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer stage a Satanic ascension as a legal proceeding: the damned soul is trapped awaiting reprisal, and the sky's rupture into a rain of blood is its verdict finally handed down, acquittal delivered as annihilation. What makes the song more than gore is that first-person turn — the voice moves from passive object to reigning power, so the blood-storm isn't a horror being witnessed but the raw material the speaker builds itself from, an inverted creation myth where structure is forged out of slaughter — the title’s homophone cashed in the final line, raining resolving into reigning. The band's clinical, breathless delivery treats this apocalypse as procedure rather than spectacle, which is the whole thrash-metal wager: cold precision makes the blasphemy sound not lurid but inevitable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"the damned soul ascends through its own verdict as raw material","built":"raining resolves into reigning via the title's homophone in the final line"},
"slayer~read-between-the-lies": {"haiku":"Religious fraudsters exploit poor believers' faith and money through deceptive preaching, claiming divine authority while hiding greed and manipulation behind prosperity doctrine rhetoric.","opus":"A scathing takedown of televangelists who bleed the poor and desperate dry with hollow promises of salvation while lining their own pockets. The argument is that religion runs on extortion and lies, that paradise is only marketable alongside a terrifying hell, and that even the devil couldn't match these preachers for dishonesty.","sonnet":"About televangelists and prosperity gospel preachers who exploit the grieving and poor — draining donations from people who can't afford to give, performing miracles for money, preaching heaven while serving Satan. The title tells you exactly where to look.","src":"haiku","fable":"A prosecution of the televangelist racket, tracking how faith gets metered into donations: the pulpit becomes a sales channel where salvation is the product and empty pockets are the mark. Slayer's real target is the theology behind the pitch — the claim that a signed check buys a soul out of a hell the preacher himself invented to price the transaction. The build sharpens toward one merciless flip: the con man swears his route to paradise runs close, yet the band ranks him beneath the devil, because a devil at least keeps his bargains. The title's pun carries the closing verdict — a sermon sold this way is worth parsing only for the fraud printed underneath it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Timing did the naming: the album reached stores in July 1988, months after Jim Bakker's fall and weeks after Jimmy Swaggart's televised confession — the band tied the song to no one, but its target was on every front page of its release year.","themes":["faith & the occult","money & the street","politics & society"],"means":"the con man ranks beneath the devil for breaking his own bargains","built":"the title's pun issues the closing verdict as a parsing instruction"},
"slayer~reborn": {"haiku":"A witch burnt at the stake escapes death through eternal pacts; she returns reborn, her rage unleashed beyond mortal law and consequence.","opus":"A condemned witch's defiant curse from the stake, having signed a pact in blood; she taunts her executioner-priest that burning her is useless because her rage and powers will rise again and she can never truly die.","sonnet":"A condemned witch burned at the stake narrates her own execution with contempt — she has signed a pact with her lord, and death means nothing because she will rise again, leaving her would-be executors ripped and torn.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer stage the execution from inside the condemned: a witch bound to the stake narrates her own burning as a formality her accusers will regret, since she has already pledged herself to a darker patron in exchange for return. The taunt is the whole engine — every threat the priest levels back gets answered as impotent, the fire read not as an ending but as a doorway she chose, so that killing her only sharpens the wrath she vows to unleash the next morning. The last verse flips interrogator: she asks whether she is mere superstition, daring the pious man to stay certain, which is the record's favorite cruelty — leaving the believer unsure his ritual ever worked.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"killing her only sharpens the wrath vowed for the morning after","built":"closing question flips interrogator onto the priest, leaving him uncertain his ritual worked"},
"slayer~repentless": {"haiku":"A musician vents rage against society's vanity and fakeness, choosing to live without apology or regret while using music and intensity as their survival.","opus":"Written in the wake of guitarist Jeff Hanneman's death, this is a defiant statement of purpose from a frontman sick of the fame, the industry politics, and other people's egos. Underneath the bravado is the admission that performing this music is the only thing holding him together, even as the touring life wears everyone down.","sonnet":"Slayer's declaration of artistic identity — sick of the music industry's ego games, committed only to playing brutal songs about war and atrocity, living on the road, with no regrets and no apologies for what they are.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track of the first album Slayer made without founding guitarist Jeff Hanneman, this is the band's mission statement dressed as a middle finger — no apologies, no repentance, the negation baked into the invented word itself. The verses swing between contempt for the industry's ego-jockeying and the one thing that redeems it: the stage, the guitar, the life burned down to a nightly act of killing it live. What starts as pure self-mythologizing about touring until death curdles at the end into something bleaker, when the swagger admits that living this hard is really just everyone hurrying toward the same grave a little faster.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written alone by Kerry King as his tribute to Jeff Hanneman — the scene-hating, no-apologies creed is King's rendering of Jeff's attitude, which turns the guitar-till-death line into elegy.","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"swagger about living hard quietly admits everyone shares the same grave","built":"the negation built into the invented title-word is the whole argument"},
"slayer~scarstruck": {"haiku":"A sadistic voice invites others into rituals of pain and scarification, blending desire, brutality, and hatred as a way to remake the world.","opus":"Sadomasochism elevated to a whole worldview, pain reframed as ecstasy and the singer casting himself as a false god who brands and scars converts. Slayer turns cruelty into a perverse gospel where the wounds are supposed to be permanent.","sonnet":"A sadomasochistic fantasy of total dominance — the narrator invites a victim into a paradise of pain and pleasure, sexualizing violence and bloodletting, positioning himself as a new god who feeds on the experience of cutting and scarring flesh.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person torturer courts his victim in the seducer's grammar of an invitation, and the horror is that his cruelty and his ecstasy are named as the same nerve firing — agony and euphoria arrive as synonyms, so worshipping the wound becomes indistinguishable from desire. The kill turns aesthetic in the closing movement, the body reimagined as a stretched surface for fresh cutting, which converts murder into an act of authorship the speaker cannot get enough of. Kerry King's writing on this record drops the older Slayer remove of the war chronicler or Satanic reporter; the narrator now claims the god-role directly, offering himself as the hell you asked for, and the permanent mark that outlasts the death — the scar the title fuses with being struck — is the only thing he swears will endure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","faith & the occult"],"means":"agony and euphoria named as the same nerve firing","built":"the title fuses the scar with being struck into a single permanent mark"},
"slayer~screaming-from-the-sky":{"haiku":"An aerial death-machine stalks from above, carpet-bombing cities into waste and cutting through silent prey. The song celebrates this predator's efficiency in execution, leaving only blood and silence behind.","opus":"Slayer voice death from the air, a silent-winged hunter unleashing indiscriminate destruction on burning cities before the horror tightens to a blade against a single throat. The thrash veterans render war as a faceless killing engine, the icy precision and the tyrant enthroned behind a death's-head mask embodying their long fixation with slaughter.","sonnet":"Aerial bombardment narrated from the cockpit — the pilot hunts unseen below, carpet-bombing with detached precision, and the song inhabits that perspective without irony or guilt. Silence follows the attack; the prey is erased. In the final movement the song descends to ground level where the same executioner walks among the bodies, still measuring each step.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~scrum":{"haiku":"Rugby is reimagined as ritualistic combat where flesh rips, teeth spit, and sacrifice earns victory. The sport becomes a proving ground of relentless brotherhood and physical domination over human debris.","opus":"A savage sports-as-warfare metaphor, casting a rugby match as brutal combat where a disciplined brotherhood sacrifices everything to dominate and win. Slayer turn the scrum into a thrash-metal battle cry, the on-field chants and adrenaline translating raw athletic aggression into the band's signature headlong, violent charge.","sonnet":"Contact sport as philosophy of survival — the arena is the only place where the speaker truly lives, and everything about the game, the bodies, the blood, the discipline passed down through centuries, is treated as sacred. There is no glory without pain; the willingness to take and deliver punishment is presented as the one honest measure of a person.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~seasons-in-the-abyss": {"haiku":"A sadistic killer finds thrilling stimulation in torture and murder, viewed as an inescapable descent into madness.","opus":"A dive into a murderer's mind, mixing the thrill of anticipating a kill with directions to shut your eyes and let your thoughts empty as you perish. Explicit pictures of severed limbs and ceremonial killing render lunacy in the void.","sonnet":"A descent into violence and mental dissolution — the song moves through images of self-incision, sadistic ritual, and bodily desecration, framed as a kind of trance in which the self is erased and insanity takes over.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tom Araya writes from inside a killer's skull where stalking and killing have fused into one appetite, and the verses tour the scene — surgical wounds, a room made bright by what has been spilled — with a craftsman's flat pride. The genius is the chorus, which pivots outward into a hypnotist's command: the victim is coached to surrender their name and dissolve, so murder gets staged as a spiritual induction rather than plain butchery. That refrain never changes while the verses escalate, and the stillness is the horror — for this narrator every death runs the same trance, a rite replayed with the calm of someone who long ago stopped counting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"murder staged as a spiritual induction the killer runs on repeat","built":"the chorus never changes while the verses escalate, stillness as the horror"},
"slayer~serenity-in-murder":{"haiku":"Twisted peace found in violence; finding spiritual calm through the act of killing.","opus":"A killer narrates the act as calm rather than frenzy, framing bloodshed as the thing that quiets his own pain, watching the light drain from a victim's fading eyes and finding a warped spiritual release in it. Slayer built their reputation on exactly this cold-blooded first-person horror, and this cut sits in their mid-career thrash mode, where clinical serial-killer detachment replaced the earlier occult theatrics with something colder and closer to home.","sonnet":"The mind of a killer finding calm in the act of killing — violence as the only thing that quiets an internal fire. The song moves from predatory calm through the physical intimacy of murder to a kind of spiritual release, framing bloodshed as a transcendent balm, something that soothes where nothing else can.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~seven-faces":{"haiku":"A person confronts multiple fragmented selves in the mirror, each embodying desire, greed, and lust, unable to reconcile the different faces staring back.","opus":"A late-era Slayer study in bottomless craving, the narrator confronting multiple reflections that all resemble him, propelled by a greed that never fills the void. Living for whatever leaves him empty, he becomes the object of every envy, his identity splintering and growing stranger as it dissolves into pure, endless appetite for more.","sonnet":"A man who looks into the mirror and no longer recognises himself, seeing instead the seven faces of desire and excess that have colonised him over time. Envy, lust, greed, and hollow self-sufficiency have replaced whatever he once was, and he is becoming all of it willingly.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~show-no-mercy":{"haiku":"Ruthless slaughter without compassion; warfare and violence as totality, leaving no survivors or sympathy.","opus":"Satanic warriors ride out of Hell by night, sworn to their master, stalking prey who cannot escape and offering no mercy as they tear flesh and claim souls. The title track of Slayer's 1983 debut, it is teenage occult bravado at full velocity, the LA quartet announcing themselves with cartoon evil and relentless speed. Beneath the theatrics sits the blueprint for a band that would define thrash's meanest edge.","sonnet":"Night-riding servants of Satan hunt without restraint, their allegiance bargained and sealed. The song builds to a first-person assault — tearing flesh, burning through the mind, claiming the soul — framed not as rampage but as covenant, a duty to a master whose power eclipses any god that might try to intervene.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~silent-scream": {"haiku":"Children suffer in nightmares, torment, and sacrifice; their screams are silent as they're killed, broken, and destroyed by predatory forces.","opus":"Slayer confronting abortion and the persecution of children through relentless nightmarish imagery. Deliberately provocative, it stares at innocence extinguished and refuses any comfort, horror rendered without resolution.","sonnet":"A brutal anti-abortion polemic framed from the fetus's perspective — describing the terror, pain, and destruction of termination as murder, with language of sacrifice, crucifixion, and embryonic death.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer take the coldest possible vantage — a terminated pregnancy narrated from somewhere inside the procedure — and refuse to let the listener stand anywhere comfortable. The scream of the title is the one utterance that never arrives: a would-be consciousness snuffed before it owns a voice, so the band's shrieking stands in as its ventriloquized proxy. Araya loads the verses with obstetric and morgue vocabulary rather than sermon, letting the surgical detachment do the accusing; the real horror is that something gets filed as routine which the music treats as slaughter. What lifts this past pamphlet is the doubling — the victim is both the never-born and every abandoned, tortured innocent, so the indictment widens from one operating table to a whole species that logs its cruelties under paperwork. The closing return to fire and dread reframes the whole thing as damnation rather than argument, a loop that shuts with no exit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","politics & society","faith & the occult"],"means":"the band's shrieking stands in as the voice that never arrives","built":"obstetric and morgue vocabulary replaces sermon, letting detachment do the accusing"},
"slayer~skeleton-christ": {"haiku":"Religion is rejected as mind control and mockery, with the speaker refusing rebirth through faith and choosing defiance instead.","opus":"A flat repudiation of Christian rescue as brainwashing, jeering at the pledge of new life and picking the devil purely out of scorn for forced worship. Classic Slayer sacrilege trained squarely on the false promise of the return.","sonnet":"A full-throated rejection of Christianity as mind control — the Second Coming won't happen, God is a lie, faith has atrophied into institutional compulsion, and the narrator sides explicitly with the devil over the skeleton of a dead religion.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer's 2006 comeback trades sermon-parody for open contempt, and the song works by ventriloquism: the opening verses adopt the exact promise a preacher makes to the fallen, offering the atrophied convert a scrubbed-clean second birth, then the chorus snaps that borrowed voice shut with the flat verdict that no resurrection is coming. What the frame calls a savior it renames a corpse dressed as one, a bone-white effigy sold to keep the desperate obedient. The band stops arguing and starts jeering, treating the whole apparatus as manufactured obedience worn like faith, and Araya's escalation from doubt to open allegiance with the adversary is less devotion than the loudest available insult flung at a con he thinks kills more than it saves.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","anger & defiance","surveillance & control"],"means":"the borrowed preacher voice snapped shut by a flat verdict of no resurrection","built":"opening verses adopt the preacher's exact register before the chorus repudiates it"},
"slayer~skeletons-of-society": {"haiku":"Apocalyptic collapse reveals human civilization as hollow; survivors witness ruins and descend into madness amid environmental decay.","opus":"A post-apocalyptic vision where nuclear judgment has flattened the old hierarchies, leaving a narrator wandering the ruins as the world decays and humanity slides into madness, a pawn in a cycle that never ends.","sonnet":"A post-apocalyptic wasteland — acid rain, darkness, the rich reduced to beggars, a false prophet pointing west — where the speaker drifts through the ruins of civilization watching the last shards of humanity dissolve into perpetual madness.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer turn their most controlled album toward aftermath rather than battle: the fire has already fallen, and what remains is a survivor narrating his own slow unraveling from inside a poisoned, decaying world. The catastrophe is never the subject — the subject is what erosion does to a mind afterward, memory smoldering under toxic rain while wealth and wisdom trade places into meaninglessness and a false prophet peddles salvation somewhere over the horizon. The song's engine is decay itself: each verse strips away more, until all the narrator can perceive are the outlines of things that once existed, the human wreckage the title names. Kerry King trades Slayer's usual velocity for a mid-tempo trudge here, and that restraint is the point — this is doom rendered as exhaustion, insanity settling in not as a scream but as a permanent grinding condition with nothing left to gain.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","nature & the elements","faith & the occult"],"means":"doom rendered as exhaustion, insanity settling as a permanent grinding condition","built":"mid-tempo trudge replaces Slayer's usual velocity to enact the decay it describes"},
"slayer~snuff":{"haiku":"A killer films torture and murder for an online audience, turning human suffering into entertainment for voyeurs who demand more death.","opus":"The thrash band skewer a modern voyeurism fed by vanity and greed, a narrator manufacturing murder as entertainment for an audience hungry to watch. Suffering recorded permanently, bondage in barbed wire and a web platform where crowds clamor for worse frame killing as spectacle, Slayer indicting a culture that cannot admit its craving for cruelty.","sonnet":"A killer who films the act addresses the camera as stage direction — action, torture, suffering captured for an audience that demands more. Vanity and greed motivate the first frame; by the end, the internet has replaced the private thrill with mass viewership, and murder becomes content produced for a spectacle economy.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~south-of-heaven": {"haiku":"Apocalyptic judgment and inevitable damnation, depicting moral decay and familial corruption that deserve eternal punishment and suffering.","opus":"An apocalyptic vision of judgment, damnation, and a world rotted by chaos, suspicion, and moral decay. Slayer depict souls sentenced to eternal punishment under a tyrannical god, every trace of hope and human dignity stripped away in the realm below the heavens.","sonnet":"A vision of an apocalyptic moral collapse, where judgement arrives without warning, generations corrupt one another through hate and lust, and existence is permanently settled in a realm south of heaven — beneath divine reach, ruled by chaos and a black-souled evil.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer slow their tempo to a funeral crawl here, and the deceleration is the point: after the speed-metal blitz of Reign in Blood, this title track drags apocalypse into something inescapable rather than exhilarating. The verses stack revelation imagery, faith imposed on children, damned souls, a punishing god enforcing morality through fear, but the genealogy of bastards and incestuous parents reveals the real target: institutional religion as a corrupting inheritance, not salvation. The pivot arrives in the final verse, where Araya relocates all evil away from any external fiend and into the darkened human heart, and where dignity is simply lost rather than stolen. The chanted title-phrase then settles the argument by refusing progress, naming a station just below paradise that never resolves toward light and only grinds itself onward forever.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"evil relocated from any external fiend into the darkened human heart","built":"the slowed tempo after Reign in Blood makes apocalypse inescapable rather than exhilarating"},
"slayer~spill-the-blood": {"haiku":"Eternal damnation and dark wisdom; the speaker offers initiation into evil's embrace, spilling blood as the price of immortal knowledge and union with ancient darkness.","opus":"Slayer stage a satanic seduction, an ancient evil beckoning the listener to journey through boundless time, taste forbidden knowledge and pleasures, and finally offer up their own blood. The pact seals with the entity claiming its prize: once the sacrifice is made, the mortal's soul belongs to it forever.","sonnet":"A vampire seduction: the narrator offers the listener a vision of timeless pleasure and immortality, asking them to spill their blood and surrender their soul in exchange for an eternity of otherwise unobtainable experience.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tempter's sales pitch, sung in the first person by something ancient that wants a human transaction closed. The verses dangle forbidden currency — buried knowledge, sensations no living body was meant to reach, a guided tour of history's oldest cruelty — the way a con artist itemizes a prize before naming its true price. The design is structural: two verses of seductive invitation, then a short refrain that repeats until its grammar quietly slips from future promise into completed fact, and the courted mortal learns the offer was always a foreclosure. Slayer, mid-catalog and past their pure-shock phase, trade the usual gore for something colder and theatrical — damnation staged as a contract signed the instant the victim reached for what was extended.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"the offer was always a foreclosure the victim signed by reaching for it","built":"refrain grammar shifts from future promise to completed fact mid-song"},
"slayer~spirit-in-black": {"haiku":"A dark ruler welcomes seekers into an inferno where damned spirits rot eternally and immortality awaits those who feed their hatred and surrender thought.","opus":"Satan himself narrates, welcoming the listener into his inferno where the damned rot in brimstone and blood rains forever; the deeper read is seduction into damnation—he offers to fill the listener's emptiness with immortality in exchange for their hatred and thoughts.","sonnet":"A song narrated by a demonic or infernal ruler who invites the listener into his realm — an eternal hell where the dead watch the living, flesh rots in mirrors, blood rains forever, and the narrator offers immortality in exchange for surrendering your mind.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer stage this as a tour given by the devil himself, who greets his victim like a host welcoming a guest and walks him down through a fortress of the damned. The seduction turns on a chill reveal: when the ruined soul is asked who he once was, the guide answers that the former self staring back had always been him, folding tempter and tempted into a single face. Between the verses, an incantatory chant about a serpent uncoiling underground keeps hammering the descent home as fixed and past undoing. What lifts it above standard occult swagger is the closing pitch, an offer to swallow every last drop of the victim's hatred in a bargain that only tightens the snare.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","identity & becoming"],"means":"tempter and tempted folded into a single face at the chill reveal","built":"incantatory serpent chant hammers descent as fixed and past undoing between every verse"},
"slayer~spiritual-law":{"haiku":"Divine law and cosmic order; spiritual principles that govern existence beyond mortal understanding.","opus":"A vicious jeer at organized faith and the confessional's grip, ridiculing the figure who peddles forgiveness while the congregation is shamed for wrongdoing and driven like livestock toward reward or torment. Prayer beads and blessed water get flung back as tools of manipulation rather than solace. Slayer built a career weaponizing anti-clerical anger, and this seethes with the same disdain for institutional guilt.","sonnet":"A broadside against organized religion as coercive control — confessionals, rosaries, and mass attendance as tools for herding the weak-willed into compliance. The song cycles between mocking the faithful and a kind of defiant refusal, watching the whole system buckle under its own contradictions while calling for regeneration outside its walls.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~ss-3":{"haiku":"Nazi imagery and fascist symbolism; glorification of totalitarian brutality and racial ideology.","opus":"This dramatizes an assassination plot woven with occult and wartime imagery, the hangman of Prague, a doomed wolf-leader hunted down by exiled resistance, the plan executed and reprisals falling savagely afterward. It reads as a coded telling of Heydrich's killing, all severed heads and firing squads. Slayer often mined real historical atrocity for their thrash, and here the ruthlessness of both target and avengers is rendered with unblinking relish.","sonnet":"A retelling of the wartime assassination of Reinhard Heydrich, the Nazi Protectorate of Bohemia — Czech resistance operatives hunting down the wolf leader, knowing the reprisals will be catastrophic. It moves from the political machinery of occupation through the act itself to the brutal aftermath: mass executions, severed heads, a calculation that the end justifies the cost.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~stain-of-mind": {"haiku":"The speaker invites entry into a corrupted, godless mental space where pain, depravity, and moral inversion become forms of ecstasy. Agony and transgression replace virtue.","opus":"Slayer wallowing in moral corruption as something chosen and savored, an embrace of godlessness, agony, and inhumanity as the truth of life, baptizing the listener in the same nihilism the narrator revels in.","sonnet":"A declaration of alignment with agony, godlessness, and transgression as a chosen mode of existence — the narrator invites the listener into a mindset where virtue is washed away, sin is sold openly, and depraved ecstasy is elevated to the status of religion.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer's late-90s detour into groove and downtuned nu-metal textures on Diabolus In Musica lands its darkest idea here: corruption told from the inside, as an invitation. The speaker isn't a witness to humanity's decline but its evangelist, offering his own diseased mind as a church where sin is the sacrament and moral collapse the shared communion. What makes the song curdle is its liturgical grammar — baptism, salvation, serenity, bliss — all inverted, so that a rite meant to cleanse instead washes virtue away, and the language of a needle delivering peace turns euphoria into another kind of infection. By the last verses the mask drops entirely: this voice has chosen the other side deliberately, and the stain isn't a wound it suffered but the gospel it means to spread.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"corruption offered as a gospel the speaker means to spread","built":"liturgical vocabulary inverted so cleansing rites wash virtue away"},
"slayer~supremist": {"haiku":"A tyrant revels in domination and suffering, seeking to eradicate the weak and establish perpetual reign through violence and spiritual obliteration.","opus":"A monologue of absolute tyranny and cruelty, in which the speaker takes ecstasy in others' pain and vows to dominate, obliterate, and depopulate the weak while defiling faith and reigning over the free.","sonnet":"A voice of pure domination and contempt describes the pleasure taken in others' pain and desperation, declaring an intent to obliterate the weak and maintain total control through supreme, atrocious force.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slayer sing this entirely from inside the aggressor, and the trick is that the voice never once names itself — it demands, obliterates, and cleanses without a shred of doubt, which is the point. The escalation from personal cruelty to depopulation to eradicating faith is the argument for reading it as a mask: what sounds like a war god or genocidal state is finally exposed by the mechanical chant that closes it, control of the weak and containment of free minds looping until the tyrant is revealed as pure, self-justifying appetite. The band withholds any counter-voice on purpose, forcing the listener to sit inside supremacist logic and feel how seamlessly domination dresses itself as purification.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"domination dressed as purification, the tyrant revealed as self-justifying appetite","built":"the speaker never names itself, forcing the listener inside supremacist logic without a counter-voice"},
"slayer~take-control":{"haiku":"A revolutionary power grab against a corrupt, mediocre society that the speaker views as an abortion and disaster; the narrator positions himself as a propaganda machine introducing violent social terror.","opus":"Slayer channel contempt for a mediocre, complacent society into the voice of a self-styled propaganda machine vowing to spread fear and thrive on decay. Sneering at hollow claims of freedom, he admits he offers no solution, only the refusal to disappear, framing rebellion as pure menace from someone with nothing to lose.","sonnet":"A declaration of intent to seize and dismantle a society the speaker regards as mediocre and corrupt. Positioning himself as propaganda machine and torch-bearer simultaneously, he calls for a social terror that replaces the current order with something forged from contempt and will.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~temptation":{"haiku":"Evil tempts humans toward damnation by offering infinite gratification and pleasure, using seduction to draw souls into possession and complete spiritual ruin.","opus":"Slayer voice the devil as a smooth seducer bargaining eternal life for a soul, dazzling the listener with lies while toying with sanity and reality. He clears debts, feeds appetites, and rewrites the rules of a rigged game, recalling every wretch who sealed a bargain and wondering aloud why evil pulls so hard on the human heart.","sonnet":"The devil speaks directly, offering certainty, satisfaction of greed, and eternal life in exchange for the soul — framing the transaction as the only game in town. Every human has already been dancing with this temptation; the question is only whether they admit it.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~the-antichrist":{"haiku":"Someone embraces satanic identity as liberation from divine abandonment, reveling in torment and the destruction of heaven. The song portrays deliberate evil and spiritual rebellion.","opus":"An early Slayer track voiced by the Antichrist himself, who claims damnation was always his destiny, abandoned by a God who cut him loose. Amid nightmares, blood rites, and the awaited arrival of Satan's heir, he embraces madness and eternal decay, the thrash pioneers wringing blasphemous defiance from a soul resigned to rot.","sonnet":"A first-person declaration of satanic identity from someone who felt abandoned by God and embraced the alternative. Pentagram imagery and waiting for a divine inversion structure the theology; the narrator experiences this not as transgression but as liberation — the self finally matching the role it was always meant to play.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~threshold": {"haiku":"A person consumed by explosive inner rage cannot stop violent impulses that have become their identity and discipline, pushing away anyone who comes near or questions them.","opus":"A first-person plunge into rage the narrator can neither govern nor turn off, spilling out at the person he blames for hollowing him out. Hatred here isn't an emotion but a creed he sustains himself with, aimed at someone whose mere presence sets him off. Slayer render pure, motiveless aggression as a way of being.","sonnet":"First-person rage that is barely contained and barely directed — the narrator doesn't want to feel anything, hates everything, and sees violence as his discipline rather than his failure. It's a portrait of someone whose anger has become their entire identity and method of surviving.","src":"haiku","fable":"A monologue from inside untreatable rage, where the speaker insists his fury has no external cause but simply pours out of him like a fluid he cannot dam. What makes it more disturbing than a standard hate-anthem is the pivot at the chorus: he stops resisting and admits he savors the overflow, feeding on it, treating cruelty toward whoever crosses him as a chosen creed rather than a sickness. The final collapse into short broken lines abandons even that pretense of philosophy, leaving only the bare admission that nothing here will be stopped. God Hates Us All came together in the raw aftermath of the late-90s nu-metal wave, and the track reads as a refusal of that scene's confessional victimhood: this narrator claims his violence needs no wound behind it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","madness & the mind"],"means":"violence claimed as a creed needing no wound behind it","built":"final lines collapse into short broken fragments, abandoning even the pretense of philosophy"},
"slayer~unguarded-instinct-bonus-track-for-japan":{"haiku":"A killer embraces uncontrolled violent impulses and moral abandon, describing sprees of murder with detached precision.","opus":"A descent into a killer's psychology told from inside the compulsion: control surrendered to instinct, morality abandoned, the divide between body and soul treated as a doorway flung open. The register is clinical yet feverish, framing murder as vertigo and asylum-madness rather than rage. Slayer, pioneers of thrash's darkest lyrical territory, keep the imagery abstract enough that the horror lives in the psychology, temptation ending in something the speaker calls divine.","sonnet":"A descent into the asylum of one's own unchecked impulse — the speaker surrenders to a killing instinct he can no longer repress, the boundary between fantasy and action dissolving as manic energy replaces conscience, until what looked like madness reveals itself as the only honest expression of who he is.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~unit-731": {"haiku":"The song depicts Unit 731 biological warfare atrocities through the perspective of a remorseless experimenter, celebrating inhumane medical torture.","opus":"A first-person immersion in the crimes of Japan's wartime biological-weapons division, voiced by a perpetrator rationalizing the cutting-open of living prisoners, germ trials and scorched bodies as obligation and research. It bluntly renders opened skulls, torn-out teeth and slain infants, closing with a pledge that the killer will reunite with his victim below.","sonnet":"From the perspective of a Japanese Unit 731 scientist, the song details the vivisections, bacterial weapons testing, live dissections, and torture of prisoners in graphic terms, framing these atrocities as the narrator's proud work.","src":"haiku","fable":"Named for the Imperial Japanese Army's covert bio-warfare program in occupied Manchuria, the song hands the microphone to the man cutting open living prisoners and calls the atrocity by its bureaucratic cover: research, procedure, the greater cause. That first-person voice is the whole horror engine, because it does not accuse from outside but ventriloquizes the surgeon who logs a wide-awake body's breaking point as data and treats an opened skull as a finding rather than a murder. Slayer's real trick is to let the mask of clinical detachment slip in real time, so the talk of pathogens and controlled outcomes keeps buckling into the plain appetite underneath, until he simply repeats that he wants blood and the vocabulary of medicine has nothing left to hide behind. The closing turn to crematorium ovens and a promised reunion in hell is the only verdict the track allows itself, delivered not by a narrator but by the killer's own certainty that he has already damned himself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","war & battle","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"clinical detachment buckling in real time into plain appetite","built":"the killer's own certainty of damnation is the only judgment offered"},
"slayer~vices":{"haiku":"Society's decline stems from institutional hypocrisy where faith, politics, and religion mask greed, violence, and pedophilia.","opus":"A survey of the appetites that rot people and institutions: religion twisted into oppression, the politician who peddles calm while stoking discord, the clergyman whose sanctity masks predation. Threaded through it is the nihilist claim that brutality is the strongest intoxicant. Late-era Slayer turn their scorn from the occult toward the hypocrisy of pulpit and podium.","sonnet":"A cynical survey of the powerful addicted to their own corruption — the politician hooked on greed, the holy man hiding depravity behind piety, the public hooked on the spectacle of violence, everyone surrendered to vices that destroy them while mistaking the rush for freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~war-ensemble": {"haiku":"A chronicle of modern warfare highlighting strategy, propaganda, and the brutal calculus that victory is measured in casualty counts and massacres.","opus":"A grim depiction of total war as a sport measured in corpses, where scorched-earth strategy, propaganda, and psychological attrition reduce victory to sheer survival and body count.","sonnet":"War is portrayed as a sport in which the scoreboard is a body count, and strategy, propaganda, and attrition are the rules of play — a bleak, relentless dissection of how killing becomes the measure of victory.","src":"haiku","fable":"Hanneman and Araya write combat not as tragedy but as recreation, and the venom lives in that reframing: the body count becomes a personal scoreboard, killing kept as a tally the speaker brags about, so the horror is the flat pleasure of the voice reporting it. The chant that hinges the track collapses spectacle and slaughter into one word — the arena and the battlefield made interchangeable, cheered from the same seat. When the bridge drifts to the Rhine and a prophetic old regime, Slayer pulls the frame back from any single trench to war as a recurring appetite of history, then twists victory itself inside out: to come through alive is the only prize, and dying is simply losing the game.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","party & hedonism"],"means":"killing kept as a personal scoreboard, horror in the flat pleasure of the tally","built":"a single chant word collapses spectacle and slaughter, arena and battlefield, into one"},
"slayer~war-zone": {"haiku":"A person establishes himself as a dominant force, unclimbable and invincible in war. He brings guaranteed madness to anyone who enters his world of supremacy.","opus":"A first-person taunt from an unstoppable aggressor daring the listener into his domain, promising agony, insanity and a battle no one can win. The whole thrust is menace and supremacy, the speaker casting himself as an insurmountable obstacle and the very ache inside you, feeding on a ritual of victims while he reigns alone at the top.","sonnet":"Pure aggressive dominance — the narrator is unbreakable, unbeatable, and invites all challengers to enter his world and face the madness coming their way, the song being as close to a pure statement of indestructible threat as metal gets.","src":"haiku","fable":"A single voice standing at a summit, addressing a challenger who never gets a line back — the song is pure unanswered dominance, and its meaning is in that lopsidedness. Slayer strips war of ideology or event here: no cause, no enemy nation, just an I who declares himself the wall you cannot climb and the war you cannot win, collapsing conflict into a contest of ego. The threats never escalate or resolve because there is nothing to resolve; the speaker has already won before the first blow, and the repetition of the same taunt is the point, a mind circling its own supremacy. It reads less as combat than as the interior monologue of someone who has made a religion of himself, which on a Slayer album titled for God's contempt lands as deliberate blasphemy — man crowned in the space where the deity has withdrawn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"conflict collapsed into a contest of ego against a challenger who never answers","built":"the same taunt repeats without escalation, circling its own supremacy as the whole point"},
"slayer~when-the-stillness-comes":{"haiku":"A predator describes murder with visceral glee—blood, gore, and violence—capturing victims' final moments staring into the killer's crazed eyes.","opus":"From Slayer's final album, this track climbs inside the mind of a serial killer who takes pleasure and even a warped sense of liberation in slaughter, denying any higher power steering him. It is an unblinking study of a psyche that finds meaning and euphoria in the act of killing, offering neither judgment nor comfort.","sonnet":"A killer recounts an act of extreme violence with clinical sensory detail — blood like rain from the ceiling, the sweet silence after screaming stops. The moment of stillness when a victim's eyes go still is described as liberation, a feeling that briefly quiets the demons before they return to torment him again.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~wicked-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Wickedness and depravity; a celebration of moral corruption and sadistic pleasure.","opus":"An apocalyptic vision that treats chaos as a governing law, with collapse spreading until nothing survives and death becomes the only release. Amid famine, plague and war, Slayer, career-long chroniclers of the end times, frame total annihilation not as random catastrophe but as a deliberate ordering force pulling structure out of ruin.","sonnet":"Civilisation grinding toward total extinction: chaos is not a threat but a tool wielded by those who feed on disorder, multiplying until no bloodline survives. The song moves from societal collapse into a kind of liturgy — ten hearts, one wicked mind — declaring that death is the only deliverer left in a world already running on annihilation.","src":"haiku"},
"slayer~world-painted-blood": {"haiku":"An apocalyptic vision of global death and collapse orchestrated by dark forces and human corruption; manipulation, religion, and greed destroy civilization while a godless world consumes itself.","opus":"An apocalyptic vision of humanity annihilating itself through plague, genocide and self-destruction while a laughing, then dead, God watches; drawing on Revelation imagery and Cold-War paranoia (secret governments, man becoming God and laughing at his own destruction), it paints extinction as self-inflicted and total.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic vision of human extinction — disease, genocide, religious collapse, and Satan laughing as man destroys himself; a comprehensive survey of every catastrophic end scenario delivered as thrash-metal prophecy, with no survivors and no sanctuary.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track of Slayer's 2009 album unspools apocalypse as newsreel, cataloguing plague, genocide and civilizational collapse in the clipped, verb-first shorthand the band had refined over decades of end-times writing. What sharpens it past mere gore is the theological turn threaded through the verses: a God who laughs at the carnage, then dies as the planets align, leaving man to inherit the vacancy and destroy himself in the deity's absence. The refrain of no sanctuary is the actual thesis — there is no outside to flee to, no exemption, only a world so soaked that blood becomes its paint. The late second-person turn is the cruelest move, dropping the third-person survey to name your existence being erased, so the extinction stops being spectacle and becomes personal. Slayer's usual relish gives way here to something colder and more resigned: not celebration of the end but the flat verdict that it was always self-inflicted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","violence & murder"],"means":"extinction delivered as self-inflicted verdict not celebration","built":"second-person pivot turns spectacle into personal erasure"},
"slayer~you-against-you": {"haiku":"Internal conflict and self-sabotage become the worst enemy; the speaker watches someone destroy themselves through their own choices and paranoia.","opus":"A sneering address to someone unraveling entirely by their own hand, undone by suspicion and invented grievances rather than any outside foe. The taunt drives home that the target rigged their own downfall, and the private paradise they imagined turns out to be a torment they constructed for themselves alone.","sonnet":"A cold, satisfied address to someone who destroyed themselves through their own actions — the speaker is a bystander watching an enemy's self-inflicted downfall, making clear that no outside force brought them down, just their own choices and paranoia.","src":"haiku","fable":"The narrator stands back and watches an enemy come apart, and the taunt lands harder because the speaker barely has to lift a finger. Verse by verse the song strips the target of allies, of a scheme, of any hand to blame, until the only opponent left in the room is his own name folded back on itself in the title. What sounds like a threat is closer to a diagnosis: the ruin is self-inflicted, the paradise he raised collapsing from the rot in its own beams, and the singer's whole contribution is arriving to enjoy the view. Tom Araya carries it as flat contempt rather than fury, and the promised hell turns out to be nothing more than the man handed back to himself with nowhere else to swing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","madness & the mind"],"means":"the ruin arrives self-inflicted while the speaker only watches","built":"the title folds the target's name back on itself as the only opponent left"},
"sleaford-mods~elitest-g-o-a-t-the-prodigy-acid-thunder-mix":{"haiku":"Narcissism and visible self-promotion dominate life; the subject exists only in being seen.","opus":"A savage satire of self-obsessed clout-chasing, the narrator crowning himself the greatest while admitting his life only improved once he behaved shamelessly. The bragging keeps cracking to expose the emptiness and grift beneath the need to be seen.","sonnet":"A mockery of narcissistic self-promotion and the GOAT delusion: the elitist performer climbs buildings, squeezes out independent thought, and lives in the box of their own ego — while a quieter voice acknowledges that everyone is being taken for a ride, not just the speaker.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-signals~one-life":{"haiku":"Singular opportunity and stakes of existence, making one life count.","opus":"A fight-anthem built on the conviction that existence offers a single, non-negotiable chance. Knocked to the floor picking up teeth, the narrator refuses compromise, framing life as a war worth winning through sheer refusal to quit. It embraces scars alongside victories as part of healing, turning combat imagery into a motivational creed about resilience and mortal urgency.","sonnet":"An endurance manifesto spoken from the floor after taking hits — you get knocked down, you get up, you only get one war to win. The scars that don't mend are acknowledged, the healing is ongoing, and the fire is lit by the moment your heart starts pumping and your mind sharpens into focus.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~alkaline":{"haiku":"Attraction transcends logic and morality; the speaker dissolves into chemistry with a figure perfectly misaligned with all they thought they desired.","opus":"Infatuation with someone who slips every category, an unclassified substance the singer is desperate to melt into. Desire is cast as chemistry, the pull of a person out of alignment with everything yet perfectly matched to him.","sonnet":"Desire framed as chemistry: the subject defies easy categorisation — not acid nor alkaline, not day or night, perfectly misaligned — and the speaker wants to melt through to the heart of her molecules, treating attraction as an unsolved elemental equation.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~blood-sport":{"opus":"Love reframed as a game that can't be won, devotion turned into self-wounding ritual, the narrator still someone's favorite regret. Sleep Token wraps this masochistic ache in cosmic imagery of atoms and quantum patterns, distance measured in fusion stopped. It reaches into the pain of loving someone who says it doesn't matter.","sonnet":"A self-aware account of making love into a contest that cannot be won — the narrator turned the relationship into something combative and punishing, is still tangled in the other person like unresolved physics, and admits they cannot win while also refusing to stop playing.","src":"sonnet"},
"sleep-token~calcutta":{"haiku":"Fleeting wholeness materializes through another's presence; time collapses into moments of completion before dawn returns fragmentation.","opus":"Obsessive longing that makes the singer feel whole only for fleeting moments when he can conjure a beloved's presence, waking with her name on his lips. Missing pieces find him briefly before the morning takes them away again.","sonnet":"A dreamer wakes saying a name — tangled in memory, sweating, aching for someone's eyes — and for just a moment the missing pieces return and wholeness reassembles, before the morning comes and the fracture reopens.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~dark-signs":{"opus":"A confession of self-loathing and self-harm within a relationship, the narrator seeing warning omens in a partner who keeps their distance, hating who he's become and missing his former self, refusing to bend to his desperate need to be loved and close to someone.","sonnet":"About a person who has been fundamentally changed — and not for the better — by an intense relationship, who knew from the beginning that the warning signs were there but entered anyway, and who now wakes each day hating who they have become while refusing to need closeness again.","src":"sonnet"},
"sleep-token~descending":{"haiku":"Love withers into mutual betrayal as the speaker sinks deeper; promises hollow, the beloved crawls back only to find no redemption possible.","opus":"A collapsing relationship in which one partner comes crawling back only once the other is already broken on the ground. It calls out cheap talk and the demand for love from someone unwilling to be present, asking bitterly what they'd actually be willing to do.","sonnet":"A speaker confronts someone who keeps returning but offers nothing — \"talk is cheap, come on and save me now\" — as love withers and the speaker, already on the ground, demands to know what the other would actually do for them.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~drag-me-under":{"haiku":"Being consumed and transcended by all-encompassing love and desire.","opus":"Devotion pushed to the edge of drowning, a lover begging to be pulled beneath the surface of a love that consumes. The band's mythic reverence casts surrender to another person as something holy and dangerous at once.","sonnet":"Pure devotional surrender — the speaker is overwhelmed by the presence of a lover to the point where even angels lose words, and asks only to be dragged under again into the depth of that love.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~gods":{"haiku":"The speaker feels despised by the gods and abandoned by those they trusted, describing themselves as wrecked and alone. They invite others to watch their suffering while questioning what they're willing to endure.","opus":"Being abandoned even by the divine, reduced to a wreck the gods won't look at, then baiting a lover to inflict pain because bleeding well is the only performance left. Vessel's ache lives in that grim ease with his own suffering.","sonnet":"A song about feeling beneath contempt — the gods look away, the narrator is a wreck, and they address someone who seems to enjoy watching them bleed, framing the entire dynamic as a kind of terrible, easy intimacy that burns straight through them.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~high-water":{"haiku":"Spiritual purgation and romantic devastation converge as sacrifice; cleansing through pain becomes the only path to transcendence amid collapsing self.","opus":"A doomed devotion where the singer offers himself as sacrifice, choking on false comfort and drowning in regret while shielding a partner from their own burden; the confession that they'll never be together turns the self-erasure into quiet defeat.","sonnet":"A speaker choking on sacrifice and unreciprocated devotion accepts that their suffering is the other person's salvation — they will keep drowning in self-inflicted anguish for someone who can barely hold their own weight, let alone share the burden, until they finally admit there is no future together.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~higher":{"haiku":"Two people clash repeatedly in cycles of conflict and desire—attraction and violence feed each other, lifting them toward transcendence through battle.","opus":"Sleep Token sketch a love that runs on combat, two mismatched people worn out by pretense yet unable to quit the ferocity between them. The unsettling insight is that the clashing is the closeness, each brawl lifting them into a charged high, a poisonous bond of longing and refusal where one craves sound and the other only quiet.","sonnet":"Two people who want fundamentally incompatible things — one craves noise and violence, the other silence — locked in a destructive cycle where every clash sends them higher even as it tears them apart.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~hypnosis":{"haiku":"A lover's touch overwhelms and hypnotizes the speaker, drawing out more sensation and depth than previously known. Submission to their power is irresistible.","opus":"Surrender to a consuming desire, begging to be emptied, bitten, taken over entirely under someone's spell. Sleep Token fuses erotic and devotional language until wanting to be hollowed out feels like worship rather than harm.","sonnet":"A song about being utterly consumed by someone to the point of ecstatic self-erasure — the narrator begs to be emptied out, bitten, bled, and overwhelmed, surrendering entirely to a hypnotic force that makes everything feel more than it ever did before.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~jaws":{"haiku":"Observes predatory desire masked as connection, watching someone approach with hidden hunger. Questions whether intimacy hides violent intent.","opus":"A charged, ritual entanglement with a lover cast as a hunter, the narrator declining to be the rescuer they crave and instead inviting the danger closer, asking to see the menace and the damage beneath the surface. Sleep Token, the masked British act fusing R&B with metal, make intimacy sound like devotion and threat at once, prey and predator locked together.","sonnet":"Two predators circling each other under stained glass — the speaker refuses the role of savior and instead watches the other person move like something with teeth, fascinated rather than afraid. The request to show vulnerability, wounds, and the point where delicacy ends is less tender than forensic: it wants to know exactly where the other is breakable.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~say-that-you-will":{"opus":"An eroticized, ruinous devotion where affection and brutality fuse; the singer gives himself over to a lover he knows will abandon and maybe wreck him, begging for a pledge of loyalty even a hollow one, wanting the drive to cherish and the urge to destroy woven together into a single thing.","sonnet":"The narrator is in a feverish, obsessive entanglement with someone who may be leaving — they want the person to say they'll stay even knowing it isn't true, collapsing the desire to love and the desire to destroy into the same violent, sweaty moment.","src":"sonnet"},
"sleep-token~sugar":{"haiku":"The singer develops an obsessive taste for another person, using chains and complex games of power and pain. They refuse to surrender control despite the other's attempts to test their limits.","opus":"A power game between two people who feed on each other's cruelty, the singer refusing to give in even as he grows addicted to the pain. The sweet title masks an appetite for a love that hurts and a will that won't fold.","sonnet":"A song about an addictive, controlling erotic relationship where both people are locked in a dominance game — restraint, chains, and competitive withholding are all described as pleasures, and the narrator refuses to give in first no matter how far the other person pushes.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~take-aim":{"haiku":"Love manifests as mutual destruction where both parties weaponize desire against each other, wielding intimacy to inflict harm until they lay broken by the intensity of their own passion.","opus":"A song of devotion so extreme it curdles into masochism, the singer pleading with the person he adores to wound him because being hurt by them feels indistinguishable from being wanted. Worship here means welcoming destruction at the hands of the one loved.","sonnet":"The speaker pleads to be the target of a lover's full destructive force — \"take aim at me for once, break me apart\" — surrendering to the weaponized love of someone who provokes both self-hatred and desperate yearning.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~the-love-you-want":{"haiku":"Unrequited devotion persists despite the beloved's refusal; the narrator remains saturated with love they cannot transmit across the distance.","opus":"The anguish of caring for a partner who is emotionally shut and out of reach, the singer still reaching toward them on faith while sensing their affection is reserved for a person he can never be. A warning threads through: bottling up withheld feeling only corrodes you from the inside.","sonnet":"A devoted person offers unending love to someone who cannot or will not receive it — the speaker remains full of that love regardless, reaching out on faith alone, while the other person stays locked away behind emotional walls they may never open.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-token~the-night-does-not-belong-to-god":{"opus":"A hymn to the transgressive intimacy of night, when the divine daylight order recedes and forbidden desire takes over; the singer tangles with a lover he knows won't be his, giving himself to the darkness that comes down like heaven, beyond God's reach.","sonnet":"During daylight, a lover is ordered and angelic, but at night their eyes go black and they become someone else entirely — the narrator knows this nocturnal version won't be theirs permanently, but begs for the night anyway, surrendering to the dark transformation.","src":"sonnet"},
"sleep-token~the-offering":{"haiku":"A lover offers themselves as a sacrifice to an idealized, almost divine figure, wanting to be consumed and desired completely.","opus":"Sleep Token, the masked British collective whose ritual mythology centers on worship of a deity, cast total surrender as a sacred sacrifice. The beloved is rendered as something vast and dangerous, and the narrator gladly gives himself up to be consumed, an act of devotion made in their name as he yields himself over and over.","sonnet":"An act of complete erotic and spiritual surrender — the speaker offers themselves as sacrifice, as nourishment, asking only that the beloved bite once and consume what is given. Devotion here collapses the boundary between worship and desire.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-waker~alias":{"haiku":"A person questions their own identity and consciousness, trapped in repetitive cycles and fearing loss of individual existence.","opus":"A crisis of identity imagined in machine terms, existence looping like a program until the narrator questions whether he's truly conscious or just one more indistinguishable copy. Sleep Waker work in atmospheric metalcore, weighing the safety of endless routine against the human urge to change, asking whether a self can exist when life feels pre-written.","sonnet":"An identity crisis framed as a system loop — the speaker questions whether they are truly self-aware or just running a programmed sequence, unable to distinguish genuine fear of death from static noise. The song reaches a tentative resolution: changing your name does not change what you are, but being human means changing anyway, which may be the only identity available.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-waker~distance":{"haiku":"A person watches a dying loved one's mind deteriorate and fade away, unable to prevent their death. Though the person is gone, their memory persists in the narrator, who cannot let go of them.","opus":"Watching a loved one fade and die from a consuming disease, the speaker gives them permission to let go while insisting that a part of them lives on in memory.","sonnet":"A narrator watches someone they love deteriorate from a terminal illness, eventually giving that person permission to stop fighting and let go, then grapples with grief after the person dies — holding onto their memory even while knowing they are gone.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-waker~melatonin":{"haiku":"Pills and hallucinogens numb the narrator's suffering but trap them in false sleep and dreams that keep them from real healing. They float in a needle's eye, neither fully awake nor truly free.","opus":"The metalcore band descends into sedated half-sleep, fingers pressed to eyes and veins pulled back toward the needle as pills and hallucinogens blur where the body actually lies. Sleep Waker's atmospheric heaviness suits the queasy uncertainty of chemical rest, the refrain equating the binding sleep on offer with something that is not the healing kind the narrator truly needs.","sonnet":"Consciousness dissolves into pharmaceutical sleep that is compliance rather than rest. The body is a vessel managed by external agents, and the line between dreaming and being sedated into docility blurs until the question of where the self actually goes at night becomes genuinely unanswerable.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep-waker~skin":{"haiku":"The speaker questions the nature of consciousness and feeling, uncertain if they truly exist or merely inhabit a hollow synthetic shell with implanted memories.","opus":"A disorienting crisis of identity in which the narrator, gripped by dread and doubting his own reality, wonders whether he genuinely dreams or merely exists trapped in an empty body. Sleep Waker thread their atmospheric metalcore with Blade Runner-style questions about implanted memories and manufactured feeling, turning existential uncertainty into something suffocating.","sonnet":"Dissociation pushed to the point of doubting one's own existence — the speaker watches themselves from outside, questions whether synthetic memories constitute a real life, and wonders if sleep produces genuine dreams or just noise. The recurring image of blood-black nothingness spinning inward suggests the self is not a stable thing but a shell that panic keeps finding reasons to abandon.","src":"haiku"},
"sleeping-dog~what":{"haiku":"Nihilism and inner chaos breed contempt for others; communication becomes hostile and meaning dissolves.","opus":"A raw eruption of betrayal and disillusionment, demanding whether anything said ever mattered to someone who has since become the enemy. Love and hatred blur, morals and guidance strip away until only violence remains. The narrator claws to make it out alive, taking back everything given, mind flooded by nightmares of a relationship turned adversarial and toxic.","sonnet":"A demand for acknowledgment turned into a war chant: did anything I say mean a damn thing — and the answer is no, so the speaker pivots to pure survival: no morals, no guidance, no virtue, just violence, wanting to make it out alive from a relationship or world that gave nothing back.","src":"haiku"},
"sleeping-with-sirens~agree-to-disagree":{"haiku":"The speaker prioritizes their own path and rejects external judgment; they are self-sufficient and need no approval for who they are.","opus":"A defiant kiss-off to social pressure and a condescending other, the singer refusing to care what anyone thinks and choosing solitude over unwanted help. The mood shifts like weather, and there is real wear behind the bravado, a heart stitched back together with needle and thread. Sleeping With Sirens emerged from the post-hardcore and pop-punk scene, and this leans toward their catchier, hook-forward side rather than their screamed intensity.","sonnet":"Cheerful self-sufficiency as a rebuff — someone who neither wants approval nor offers it, perfectly content in their own lane, telling a critic to stay on their side of the line because the disagreement will never resolve and doesn't need to.","src":"haiku"},
"sleeping-with-sirens~be-happy-feat-royal-the-serpent":{"haiku":"Melancholy contradicts expectations of happiness as the narrator confronts the gap between achievement and emotional fulfillment. Internal damage persists despite external success, leaving only regret and the burden of failed promise.","opus":"The guilt of achieving the dream and still feeling wretched, blaming himself as the colors fade and old flaws resurface. Music becomes both confession and cleanup, the singer asking whether anyone else knows the shame of having everything and still not being okay.","sonnet":"Depression persists even amid apparent success — the speaker dreamed of perfection and fell short, now lying awake staring at the ceiling, wondering if the inability to be happy is entirely self-inflicted, and asking if anyone else feels the same haunting refusal of joy.","src":"haiku"},
"sleeping-with-sirens~bloody-knuckles":{"haiku":"Fighting back against lifelong disappointment and betrayal through violence and rebellion. The speaker escapes pain through confrontation and refusal to surrender.","opus":"Kellin Quinn's American post-hardcore band turn wounded resentment into a rallying kiss-off aimed at someone who forecast the narrator's ruin. Refusing to grovel, he swings at walls, buys a ticket out and swears to go down fighting, the battered-fists image standing for scrappy, misfit survival against the people rooting for his failure.","sonnet":"Defiance against everyone who predicted failure — the speaker was written off, thought to be headed for a fall, and the song is the refusal: not crawling, not begging, punching through the wall instead. A one-way ticket out of the bottom is the plan, with the understanding that the people who dismissed them will never see what it cost to get here.","src":"haiku"},
"sleeping-with-sirens~complete-collapse":{"haiku":"Caught between numbness and chaos, craving genuine feeling but fearing the loss of control it demands.","opus":"The pull toward feeling anything real over safe numbness, mental illness reframed as a companion the narrator half-prefers to the unknown. Choosing familiar torment because at least it proves he's still alive.","sonnet":"A frank admission of self-destructive tendencies paired with a refusal to play it safe — the narrator knows they are their own worst enemy, prefers the chaos of real feeling over numbness, and chooses the familiar devil of their own impulsivity over an unknowable alternative.","src":"haiku"},
"sleeping-with-sirens~crosses-feat-spencer-chamberlain-of-underoath":{"haiku":"Internal war between the self confronted and the self hiding, mask-wearing as both survival and death.","opus":"Self-hatred as the enemy you can't outrun because it wears your own face, the mask you present becoming the thing that buries you. Nobody else can carry the weight of your own broken selfhood.","sonnet":"Someone who gave everything to a person who gave nothing back, now falling apart alone and confronting the fact that the worst enemy they face is themselves — the devil on their own back.","src":"haiku"},
"sleepmakeswaves~zelda":{"haiku":"Unable to save someone or change the world for them, the speaker finds words only after they're gone, capturing irreversible loss.","opus":"A rare vocal outing for the Australian post-rock band, a raw farewell to someone the singer would have upended the planet for yet could not reshape himself to keep. The tragedy is that the right things only occur to him once the person is already lost.","sonnet":"A quiet farewell to someone who has gone — all the things left unsaid, the inability to change despite wanting to, and the recognition that the words only come after it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"sleep~dragonaut":{"haiku":"Cosmic escape abandons terrestrial existence for transcendence beyond human limitation and return.","opus":"Stoner-metal cosmic escapism, riding a dragon off the planet and away from the human race toward Mars; the trip is the point, a heavy-lidded fantasy of leaving Earth and its people entirely behind.","sonnet":"A rider on a dragon departs from humanity entirely, navigating the solar seas and passing through a crimson door into open space. The song is a brief, mythic vision of escape from the human world into cosmic freedom.","src":"haiku"},
"sleigh-bells~justine-go-genesis":{"haiku":"A girl presents a false self while internally struggling; she's hardened by life yet still searching for connection and escape.","opus":"A jagged, boastful character sketch built on noise-pop swagger, where a young woman brags about her allure and her ambitions while flashes of real hardship slip through the bravado. Sleigh Bells let the taunting confidence and the hints of poverty sit side by side, so the toughness reads as armor over someone scraping by.","sonnet":"A punchy, self-mythologizing portrait of a girl performing confidence she may not feel — the \"concrete girl\" and \"cavity\" metaphors suggest someone who's hollow but passes, someone who's finally got her ticket and doesn't care if the veneer cracks.","src":"haiku"},
"sleigh-bells~locust-laced":{"haiku":"Inescapable wickedness and despair wrapped in apocalyptic dread. The speaker feels doomed by an unstoppable evil force that destroys all possibility of escape.","opus":"Sleigh Bells pair bubblegum melody with apocalyptic menace, letting euphoria and doom share one intoxicating hook. The imagery stalks the narrator with an inescapable, malevolent force and a spirit turning wicked, while the giddy refrain fuses feeling explosively alive with the sense of being on the verge of death, thrill and dread laced together into a single addictive rush.","sonnet":"Something wicked is spreading and cannot be outrun — a force that leaves the vulnerable trapped on broken legs, a banshee retreating into paralysis, an angel of death summoned willingly. The speaker swings between explosive energy and a death wish, craving something poisonous and locust-laced even as it destroys them.","src":"haiku"},
"sleigh-bells~red-flag-flies":{"haiku":"A warning about violence, destruction, and moral wrongdoing manifesting through visceral bodily sensations and emergency signals.","opus":"A collage of dread stitched from omens and childlike menace, broken by a repeated interrogation about who ruined every gentle thing, before dissolving into flat sorrow. Sleigh Bells, the Brooklyn noise-pop duo, take their blown-out cheerleader bombast and let it sour, cheerfulness curdling into the sense that something has gone permanently, unaccountably wrong.","sonnet":"Warning signals ignored until they become catastrophic — the song stacks images of violence against the innocent alongside a refrain of bodies that pile up without anyone being held accountable. The red flags were always there; the devastation was always coming; and the closing sadness is not surprise but the recognition that this was the only way it was ever going to end.","src":"haiku"},
"sleigh-bells~sweet75":{"haiku":"Performers and youth celebrate rock and roll, seeking healing and freedom through music and the reclamation of authentic human connection.","opus":"The noise-pop duo unleash a hazy, blown-out ode to an authentic rock ideal, cheerful troublemakers who arrive to wound or mend the planet in one gesture. Urchins and quick, chilly spirits drift through the distortion, a broken soul restored to beauty, and a parting jab about aging out of the music that pricks the bravado with a knowing smirk.","sonnet":"Rock and roll as an identity held past the age it is supposed to expire — the narrator oscillates between self-destructive and healing impulses, claiming the street as home and the music as legitimacy. A late taunt about being too old to still be here is absorbed without apology; this is simply what he is.","src":"haiku"},
"slift~a-storm-of-wings":{"haiku":"Demonic reckoning with corrupt leadership and materialistic greed, the devil as harbinger of justice against hypocritical establishment.","opus":"French psych-rockers dramatize Bulgakov's The Master and Margarita, bringing the devil Woland into a graft-ridden town where crooked officials fill the pews on Sunday and avarice poisons the water. He carries out heaven's business to a saxophone shriek as a plague of beating wings descends, a savage purge the townsfolk earned through hypocrisy and worship of money.","sonnet":"Politicians as performers of the devil's work — going to church on Sunday, conducting a deadly waltz, praying money and carving every penny while dispensing charity dimes. Woland (the devil from The Master and Margarita) arrives playing saxophone like Coltrane or Bird, and the storm of wings descending on the town is liberation dressed as catastrophe.","src":"haiku"},
"slipknot~742617000027":{"fable":"An album's front door: a scrap of found voice declaring the whole thing sick, looped past twenty repetitions until judgment turns into permission. The title is the barcode off the band's own demo — the product announcing itself as product, contaminated and shrink-wrapped in the same gesture.","fableDeep":"One sentence — \"the whole thing, I think it's sick\" — repeated with mechanical patience is the entire text, and the piece works by letting repetition do what argument cannot. On first pass it reads as verdict: someone appalled, diagnosing. By the tenth, the referent has gone unstable — the whole thing could be the album, the band, the industry, the country — and sick has flipped valence, since in the scene's own slang it is the highest compliment available. Disgust and endorsement become the same audio. The title deepens the joke: it is the barcode number from the band's demo pressing, so the track condemning the whole thing as sick is literally named after the machinery that sells it. As an opener it functions as threshold ritual — a looped voice priming the listener the way a chant primes a crowd, dread used as a tuning fork. Thesis: a mission statement built from one stolen sentence, where condemnation, branding and hype are made indistinguishable on purpose.","src":"fable"},
"slipknot~a-liar-s-funeral": {"haiku":"Someone's final moment seen as deserved; betrayal and lies burning in the end.","opus":"A slow, bitter reckoning with betrayal, where friends who once stood united now spit the narrator's name and a burning ritual is prepared for the liar. Warmth turns to December, respect is enforced by force, and the speaker resigns himself to dying to play his part. From Slipknot's 2019 album We Are Not Your Kind, it trades the Iowa band's usual fury for a scorched, folk-tinged dirge about trust curdling into hatred.","sonnet":"A slow, furious reckoning with someone whose betrayal cost everything — friends who now spit at the mention of his name, a harvest of regret in which the only ceremony left is burning the liar. Underneath the rage is a hard-won refusal: true survivors make war, the dead need no candles, and he is done singing the sad song.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a self-execution disguised as a mob ritual: the corpse on the pyre is a false version of the narrator, and the crowd's torches only carry out a sentence he has already passed on himself. It opens in a wintered register of spent fields, cinders, and warmth that dies out of season, a man watching old allies gather to spit his name into the dirt. The chant handing the accused over to flame becomes a liturgy he half-writes and half-endorses, the contempt curdling into consent rather than protest. A hammering litany about carrying the load and trusting no ally spreads the guilt outward until complicity is universal, every accuser as culpable as the figure they set alight. The closing vow refuses the role of the mournful performer and treats the fire as an incineration of the counterfeit self, sparing no candle for a body that has stopped requiring illumination.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Heard outward rather than inward, the pyre is lit for a real betrayer — the you whose hate surprises no one — and the chant turns from self-sentence to revenge rite."},
"slipknot~all-hope-is-gone": {"haiku":"Political corruption, societal fracture, and starvation render hope extinct; the speaker embraces becoming an agent of change and world's end.","opus":"A raging denunciation of a country built on lies and slaughter, where the speaker names himself both the disease and its violent cure. He forecasts the collapse of the powerful and a final reckoning that arrives only once nothing worth saving remains, casting despair itself as the trigger for annihilation.","sonnet":"A political and social collapse anthem — the American system is broken, rights are for sale, violence is epidemic, and the elites have fallen; the narrator positions himself as both the cause and the only surviving force when all hope is gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slipknot stage collapse not as catastrophe to mourn but as a starting gun, opening on a gutted republic where promises rot into lies and the accusation lands squarely on the crowd itself. The speaker then fuses with the sickness he diagnoses, calling himself the hatred and the cure the world would rather deface than swallow, a saviour and contagion braided into one voice. Titular despair becomes a permission slip: with the powerful already toppled, the only work left is to see the ruin through and carve a passage out the far side. Yet the closing question guts the revolutionary bravado, admitting no tally of grievances weighs anything once the single beloved thing perishes regardless of who wins. That confession quietly exposes the whole roaring anthem as cornered mourning, a grief that has borrowed the swagger and sheer volume of a triumphant battle hymn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~all-out-life": {"haiku":"Rejecting mediocrity and false worth; demanding authentic living and refusing to accept predictable, commercialized existence.","opus":"Corey Taylor's manifesto against mediocrity and the worship of the shiny and new, refusing to let attention be bought and sold. It is a demand to fully commit to living on your own uncompromising terms.","sonnet":"A rejection of mediocrity and cultural fakery — the speaker demands that listeners refuse empty entertainment, empty idols, and the idea that age equals irrelevance, calling for full engagement with life rather than passive consumption.","src":"haiku","fable":"A manifesto against cultural amnesia, aimed at an industry and audience that mistake novelty for merit and treat anything with a history as ballast to jettison. Corey Taylor's paired negations run like a logician's proof, dismantling both the nostalgia trade and the churn of the disposable, refusing to let recency stand in for worth. The track pivots from a snarling audit of counterfeit hype into a mock congregation, and the creed sworn by that gathering — no reverence for hollow idols, no auction of one's own attention for convenience — recasts grievance as a vow with liturgical weight. Naming the demand as full-throated living rather than warfare is the hinge: the dare is to move through the world with conviction, not to torch an adversary, so the outro tail sheds its aggression and lands nearer a plea than a battle cry.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The not-your-kind chant was lifted a year later as the title of the next album, We Are Not Your Kind — the manifesto's slogan promoted to a banner."},
"slipknot~aov": {"haiku":"A person confronts social decay, community failure, and accumulated abuse, demanding authenticity and truth while watching others disintegrate into apathy and hypocrisy.","opus":"Corey Taylor circling self-loathing, hypocrisy and the violence we bury in silence. It's about the exhausting performance of identity and the nihilist lurking beneath the compliance, everyone deceiving their better selves to endure.","sonnet":"A song about collective social rot and personal breaking point — the narrator feels universally feared and despised, watches community ties dissolve, and oscillates between self-destruction, defiance, and exhausted nihilism over a world where nothing changes and brothers keep dying.","src":"haiku","fable":"Made in the shadow of bassist Paul Gray's death, this is a reckoning with aggression turned back on its own house, and the depleted longing for the cycle to simply stop. Its engine is a refrain that keeps re-tenanting one hush: the dread they entomb hardens into a figure who never gave a damn, then into the group itself, framed as the thing even seraphim would not risk drawing near — a Pope line repurposed as verdict against themselves rather than swagger. The mirror-taunt to become and unbecome and finally comprehend oneself is hurled at a figure gone unfamiliar, the accuser included. Under that runs the mourning refusal to keep burying bandmates and the outright rejection of being told no alternative exists, so the repeated begging for an ending sounds less like capitulation than a demand that someone finally name the harm plainly. A late line concedes that no utterance or deed could mend it now, the rupture already carried past any hope of repair.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~before-i-forget": {"haiku":"The speaker claims primal identity before human form and warns against forgetting one's essence before it vanishes, reflecting on delay and evasion as life strategies.","opus":"A claustrophobic, defiant declaration of self, where the speaker feels sealed in and exposed while insisting he is a world and a creature before he is a man, and that his ends justify his means.","sonnet":"The narrator asserts a primal, pre-human identity — existing as a creature before a man — while wrestling with a sense of being smeared across chaos, perpetually delaying a reckoning, and determined to remember who he is before losing himself entirely.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses fasten the speaker inside a sealed room he finds both suffocating and oddly homelike, then set the recurring Slipknot standoff in which the other person, worn like a mark burned into skin, is inseparable from his own filth and wound — he cannot accuse without convicting himself. The refrain flips time on its axis: an elemental thing precedes the socialized person, a raw organism arrives long before it learns to walk upright, and recovering that buried self must outrace its erasure. That reversal is the thesis — the true self runs deeper than the mask and predates every role assigned to it, so the plea to go quiet and pay attention becomes Corey Taylor demanding the unarmored man be reached before the performed one devours him. The late my-means loop then concedes the failure inside the wanting, admitting every dodge and delay only postpones the reckoning it claims to justify.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~birth-of-the-cruel": {"haiku":"Becoming cruel and hardened through suffering; justified by the pain inflicted on you.","opus":"A defiant anthem for the damaged and maladjusted, casting the narrator as a self-styled Judas who wears his ruin well and blesses the fires that burned him. Framed by a father's prayer for self-control, it champions the broken and wise against an uptight generation, insisting agony should never be underestimated. From Slipknot's 2019 record, it distills the Iowa band's outsider ethos into a taut, sneering rallying cry for the overthrown.","sonnet":"Someone forged in spite and agony — a self-described Judas who turned damage into identity — declares himself overthrown from naivety and better for it. The lesson is visceral: never underestimate suffering, because it kills the fool and births the cruel, and the generation that tried to suppress that transformation is already losing.","src":"haiku","fable":"It opens as a reversed exorcism: whispered prayers beg a father to file this man down into someone humble and governable, and everything after is his snarling refusal to be housebroken. What follows is a manifesto of the injured who forge their harm into a blade — the speaker names himself a betrayer, thanks the flames that scarred him, and treats his own ruin as the license that makes him sharper than the comfortable people he calls too tightly wound. The title compresses that argument into a battle cry: the trusting idiot who once wanted to be understood must be killed off so a deliberately vicious version can take his place, and the chant about being finished with the throne recasts personal grudge as the toppling of whoever demanded his obedience. When the ending widens to name a whole world soaked in sincere hopelessness and everyone standing guilty, the meanness stops looking like a chosen villainy and starts looking like the only plating a rotted generation could still bolt on. Corey Taylor performs it as a disguise fully aware of its own falseness — the swagger is genuine, the injury beneath is more genuine still, and its force lies in never letting you pry the two apart.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~circle": {"haiku":"Searching for meaning and guidance while trapped in cycles; following leaders back to the same place.","opus":"A hushed, almost devotional turn where the narrator begs to be followed while confessing he keeps faltering and circling back, sensing that everything he wanted he already had and every ending is only a beginning. Slipknot, Iowa's masked nu-metal juggernaut, built their reputation on screaming aggression, so this quiet acoustic meditation on inheritance and repetition stands out as one of the band's most naked, restrained moments.","sonnet":"A meditation on cyclical loss and spiritual disorientation — the speaker keeps arriving back at the same place no matter how far they travel, reaching for things they once had and finding them just out of grasp. There's a resigned tenderness to it, a sense that all the questions have already been answered somewhere behind them, and every ending is just another beginning in disguise.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is the argument: a shape with no exit, where every stretch of distance covered only deposits the speaker back at his origin, motion and stillness collapsing into one gesture. Its cruelest turn is the arithmetic that everything desired was already possessed and every question merely answers his own trespass, recasting longing as guilt and progress as disguised repetition. The refrain is offered as invitation yet hands over nothing but another lap, and the paradoxes braided through it confess that experience has quit instructing and simply piles up. What passes for a plea to be led is a man conceding he holds no destination toward which anyone could be guided.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~critical-darling": {"haiku":"A person faces their own hypocrisy and broken promises, trapped in cycles of self-deception and enabling parasitism. The song critiques self-awareness that never translates to change.","opus":"A caustic account of belief on the brink and the parasites who mislead, the narrator forever failing a vow made to a younger self and steeling himself for a conclusion he won't survive to witness. Slipknot, the masked Iowa metal machine, channel Corey Taylor's inward-facing bile, the reflection-shows-nothing-unless-you-look hook aiming the charge of harsh judgement both ways at once.","sonnet":"Self-examination of someone who keeps betraying their own promises to themselves — while faith has flatlined and metaphysical certainty dissolves, the speaker keeps circling back to a version of themselves they could not protect from leeches and illusions. The song questions whether redemption is still possible and whether the ending approaching is one the speaker will even survive to witness.","src":"haiku","fable":"A late-era address to a self, or a culture, that mistakes its own rot for significance, the title turning on a double edge, cherished in one sense and terminal in the other, adored precisely for how it is coming apart. The verses build a theology of depletion, a deity kept breathing by machines it can no longer feel, the bargain that damnation cannot exist where nothing sacred survives, the blunt verdict that no one was ever owed continuance. Against that the singer keeps admitting one private betrayal, every fresh collapse violating a vow he swore to an earlier self, unable now to guard the single thing worth guarding from the parasites orbiting it. The pivot is the least ornamented moment here, the observation that what should be simplest stays invisible and that reflection means nothing until the eyes come open, so the charge of being terminal arrives as bedside diagnosis rather than compliment. By the close the accusing word sheds its sense and simply cycles, a fault code chanted past exhaustion, praise sanded down until only the emergency beneath it keeps ringing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title's other edge — darling, you are so critical — re-hears the acclaim-phrase as a judging intimate whose fault-finding is the wound, aiming the song at a person as much as at a dying faith."},
"slipknot~custer": {"haiku":"Exceptional people destroyed by jealousy and mediocrity; resistance to conformity and punishment.","opus":"A snarling rant against envy and mediocrity, where anyone remarkable gets flattened by the ordinary and the fed-up narrator practically begs for his own abuse through a battering, foul-mouthed chant. Off Slipknot's 2014 record The Gray Chapter, it finds the Iowa nine at their most confrontational, arming self-loathing and contempt as a percussive assault.","sonnet":"Barely contained contempt for mediocrity — the exceptional crushed by jealous common people, this planet nothing special, punishment already on its way. The song lurches between exhaustion and violence, finding a moment of unexpected clarity scrawled on a toilet wall: that to win a war you first have to know what you are actually fighting for.","src":"haiku","fable":"Corey Taylor builds a self-portrait out of grievance, casting talent as something the mediocre majority reflexively drags back down toward its own level. The pummeling refrain that begs to be sliced open and ruined only pretends at surrender; it is really a dare, suffering flipped into a weapon so that kneeling becomes an accusation rather than a defeat. Wisdom scrawled above a urinal becomes his sardonic emblem for a civilization that files its own dishonesty into neat ranks yet stashes real insight amid filth and ruin. The bravado thins mid-song, where demanding that an enemy grasp their true motive lands not as another jab but as the sincere query this whole eruption keeps orbiting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title's last-stand figure is carried by the refrain that grants no pity to the last one left standing — the swagger heard against Custer's name is a man surrounded and unmourned."},
"slipknot~dead-memories": {"haiku":"A past relationship left the speaker emotionally dead; trapped memories of lost identity prevent healing and rebirth.","opus":"Slipknot cast a doomed relationship as a binding pact, with a narrator who gave himself over completely to commit, only to walk away scarred and haunted. The song frames a love that never really lived and can't be revived, yet still torments him from within, its ghost lodged permanently in his memory.","sonnet":"A song about being psychologically haunted by a controlling relationship long after it ended — the speaker was coerced into love and submission, and even though they escaped, the dead memories of what was done to them won't release their grip on who they've become.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of a self hollowed out by a coercive bond rather than a simple breakup lament. The two verses run the same event twice with a darkening pivot: affection is first entered as a transaction, feeling swapped for a binding contract, then re-narrated as submission under duress, the spirit cinched tight until it yields. What outlasts the escape is only the wound, so the recurring line about a vanished self names a killed prior identity, not a departed partner. The chorus refuses redemption outright, insisting the pair had no true pulse and no rebirth waiting: this was never a real bond that could perish and return, only a corpse-thing carried in the chest. The late bridge couplet collapses the whole dynamic into one grotesque image of possession, the other person threaded through the narrator's veins, so the haunting becomes memory as ongoing infection rather than tender recollection.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~death-because-of-death": {"haiku":"Blame directed toward someone for causing death; repetitive accusation of responsibility.","src":"haiku","fable":"An interlude that is all refrain and no verse: the same broken phrase looped until the missing grammar becomes the point. The line never resolves into a full thought — death because of what is forever deferred to the accusatory you, so the ear keeps supplying a clause that never lands, and the blame stays fixed on a second person who is never named. On We Are Not Your Kind the track works less as a song than as a held breath between larger pieces, a chant that turns grief or rage into pure repetition; the refusal to develop is the reading, mourning collapsed into a single unfinished sentence pointed outward.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~despise-demo": {"haiku":"Psychological overwhelm and loss of control; trapped with no escape.","opus":"A suffocating spiral of self-hatred and refusal to yield, the narrator locked in a deranged trance, palms pressed over his face, unable to break free while spitting contempt at everything around him. This early Iowa-era demo captures the band's formative, unhinged rage in its rawest form.","sonnet":"A mind caving in under its own pressure — trapped in a psychopathic haze, unable to escape either the internal static or the contempt directed outward, the speaker cycles between surrender and refusal. The hands pressing down cannot be removed, but neither can the speaker die or quit: they are stuck in the loop, despising everything including their own state of mind.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of the psyche as a sealed room whose only exit is the self, which makes escape impossible by definition. The speaker keeps clawing at his own face as if it were a mask he could tear off, and the horror is that the hands doing the clawing are his own; there is no external captor to fight, only the maze he confesses he built. The vow never to surrender, hammered until it goes hollow, reads not as defiance but as the very rigidity bolting the door shut, while the parallel refrain about a mind narrowing to a single blinkered channel names the sickness that defiance pretends to cure. When the chant turns to an inability to die, immortality arrives less as power than as sentence, denying even the last-resort release. Fittingly for a demo, it is all engine and no resolution: the will to endure and the failure to break out are one locked thing, screamed on a loop.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~diluted": {"fable":"A self-inventory of loathing that keeps discovering it has no external cause. The verses tally deficiencies and denied flaws with clinical detachment, but the chorus keeps snagging on the same unanswerable question of what he did to deserve this, as if punishment arrived without a crime — the mark of shame that has forgotten its own origin. Structurally the song hunts for a target: it turns outward to indict a you whose silence is a blessing and who could never be what was wanted, threatens violence, then collapses that projection in the flat repeated admission that he sees the you inside himself. That is the diluting the title names — every accusation aimed at another dissolves back into the self, until scorn and self-scorn are the same substance thinned to nothing, and the closing command to dilute becomes both diagnosis and the only available act.","fableDeep":"The title is the mechanism — a self being thinned out — and the sharpest line states it as dosage: \"I decrease, while my symptoms increase,\" less person, more pathology. Verse one is pure inward audit: heroes dead in his head, denied flaws surfacing daily, the summary judgment of being one complete catastrophe. Verse two swings the barrel outward, to a you whose timing sucks and whose silence is a blessing, ending in the double bind of having wanted from them \"something you could never be\" — accusation and acquittal in the same breath. The interpolated deadpan about having no time for the living drops a corpse's-eye chill into the center. The bridge loops a taunt — any reason I'd need you, any reason not to hurt you — but the actual answer is the other repeated line, \"I see you in me\": the person he is raging at is already installed inside the one doing the raging. Thesis: a grievance song that dissolves, on contact, into a mirror.","haiku":"A person spirals under accumulated flaws and inadequacy, feeling internally catastrophic while others misunderstand or dismiss their pain.","opus":"From the masked Iowa band's early era, a raw purge of self-hatred and betrayal, the narrator tallying his own defects while dead ideals rot in his skull. His repeated demand to know what he did to earn this pain rounds on someone who failed him, until the closing turn dissolves the line between the one he blames and himself.","sonnet":"A person drowning in self-loathing and unresolved grievances, cataloguing deficiencies while directing fury both inward and outward at someone whose silence and bad timing compounded the damage. The recurring refrain — asking what was done to deserve this — never lands on an answer, only on the face of the person the narrator is becoming.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~disasterpiece": {"haiku":"A disturbed consciousness spirals through violent imagery and erasure; the speaker feels removed from reality and consumed by uncontrollable rage. The song depicts internal disintegration and bloodlust.","opus":"Corey Taylor at his most violently self-lacerating, using grotesque predatory imagery as a mask for someone who feels erased and doesn't belong anywhere. The rage points inward as much as out, self-annihilation dressed as menace.","sonnet":"An extreme, violent expression of psychotic rage and alienation — the narrator wants to literally mutilate the target of their contempt, voices a screaming resentment at everyone who ever laughed at them, and flirts with self-annihilation.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song wears the most extreme mask Slipknot could put on — a serial-killer bravado of throat-slitting and infinite menace — precisely so it can watch that mask rot off in real time. What the aggressor keeps circling back to isn't a threat but a grievance about existing at all: the refrain that he was never meant to be here reframes every act of violence as self-directed, an attempt to erase a self that already feels blotted out. The turn comes when he offers to drag you down with him and pleads to be finished off just in case, exposing the predator as suicidal and the whole performance of hatred as one raw sound wedged between rage and grief. Here Corey Taylor pushes the band's provocation past shock into naked self-annihilation, so the disaster the title promises turns out to be the narrator himself, not his victim.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~duality": {"haiku":"Fragmented self in agony; the narrator describes self-harm as pain relief while trapped in cycles of regret and insanity. Internal contradiction and damage define existence.","opus":"A visceral account of chronic mental anguish, where the narrator finds grim, self-harming relief in physical pain because it briefly quiets something worse inside. He oscillates between demanding to be repaired and demanding to be abandoned, having concluded that appearances deceive and nothing can be trusted.","sonnet":"The speaker is overwhelmed by a pain so chronic and all-consuming that pressing his fingers into his eyes is the only temporary relief, having screamed, wished, and waited with no change. Beneath the rage is a plea to be put back together by someone who has left him no choice but to stay in this state.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title names the trap: two selves that can neither reconcile nor separate, and the song lives in the seam between them. That opening image of crushing the sockets shut turns self-harm into the only available relief, pain deployed against pain because everything within is unbearable and boring its way deeper no matter what. The verses turn outward into a standoff with someone the speaker can neither abandon nor be freed by; the bridge sharpens this into the real demand, to be either restored whole or dismantled completely, no middle state, since waking never matched the dream and half-measures only prolong the hurt. The closing collapse into one repeated admission of madness is the duality finally caving in, no synthesis possible, the mind just looping the same broken line until nothing else remains.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~eeyore": {"fable":"A track that treats its own origin like an accusation, tracing the self back to a soiled inheritance and a botched delivery, then daring anyone to flinch. The furious boast about being an enormous voice is the whole thesis: identity here shrinks to sheer decibels, a mouth that exists only to spit contempt and register no meaning past the spitting. Every murder threat gets quietly retracted by a whispered aside that admits the singer never once confronts the person he curses, so the fury spins loose from any actual target. What remains is a persona that has devoured its own motives and keeps snarling anyway, the vocabulary of hatred collapsing into a single expletive hammered past sense until it becomes pure abrasive texture. Aggression, stripped of cause, performs itself into exhaustion and calls the performance a self.","fableDeep":"The title is the best gag — the most feral cut on the record named for the saddest, slowest animal in the Hundred Acre Wood, self-deprecation smuggled in above pure aggression. The opening is a mock nativity: meet the man that made me, greet the can I came from — parentage as garbage container — followed by a blasphemy beat that trashes the sacred heart in a back room. The one flash of anything like feeling is mock-mourning: good riddance, though he is sad to report the killing never got done — regret reserved exclusively for an uncommitted murder. The middle is a syllogism of dismissal, a stack of I-don't-cares deployed to prove \"you don't matter,\" and the crowning line — \"I am the great big mouth!\" — is the insult adopted as a title, self-award as self-description. Then the collapse: the last stanza abandons syntax entirely, cycling one expletive against the word stop until the track ends as noise with a taunt thrown over its shoulder. Thesis: rage performed past the point where words work, and proud of it.","haiku":"Contempt and aggression disguised as a greeting; rage barely contained beneath forced pleasantries.","opus":"A raw, sneering blast of contempt aimed at someone dismissed as worthless, the narrator crowning himself the great big mouth while spitting that nothing about the target matters. Menace and mock-regret over not killing swirl through pure aggression. A hidden track from Slipknot's 1999 self-titled debut, it captures the Iowa band's earliest, most feral hardcore impulse, less a song than a controlled explosion of masked rage.","sonnet":"Pure confrontational noise with almost no interior — a voice declaring its own monstrous loudness, dismissing everyone around it as irrelevant, regretful only that it never got to kill the person it despises. There is no arc, only escalating contempt collapsing into obscenity.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~everything-ends": {"haiku":"A bitter dissolution of a relationship and life, blaming the other person for the singer's collapse into numbness and self-harm. The narrator describes discovery of lies, lost sleep, and progressive emotional death as the other becomes everything that ends.","opus":"A furious address to someone the singer blames for shattering his life, learning it was all a lie, as he spirals into self-harm and numbness. He declares this person the end of everything and admits he no longer cares whether he lives or dies.","sonnet":"A person confronts the collapse of a relationship that has shattered their sense of reality, describing sleeplessness, self-harm, and the numbing of all feeling. The song moves from raw rage directed at a specific person to a hollow acceptance that nothing matters anymore.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole force here comes from an accusation that keeps slipping its target: the figure blamed for the nausea and the ruined life never holds still. Each verse quietly admits the wound was always self-inflicted, since beneath the choruses' contempt the narrator scores his own forearms and watches the color bleed from his eyes. His confession that the more he touches the less he registers marks the deadened feeling as the true subject, while the fury hurled outward is only a costume for a private collapse. When the bridge drops the accused figure and turns to unanswered questions about who remains and where to hide, the venom and the you dissolve into one object. So the final bare repetition reads not as a curse flung at an enemy but as the recognition that the person being erased is the one still speaking.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~execute": {"haiku":"Indictment of contemporary society as a diseased, lying, hopeless wasteland.","opus":"This spoken interlude rages at a numbed, distracted generation the narrator brands the makers of modern revulsion, condemning apathy, dogmatic opinion and worship of the trivial while the living dead scar the planet. It demands to know what anyone has ever truly sacrificed for, Slipknot sharpening their apocalyptic contempt into pure sermon.","sonnet":"A spoken-word indictment of contemporary cultural decay — the speaker diagnoses an era defined by fundamentalism of opinion, catastrophic boredom, and fanatical devotion to the meaningless. It's not a call to arms so much as a grim autopsy of a generation that let the strong be sacrificed, traded real stakes for distraction, and built a world now visibly on fire with its own bad choices.","src":"haiku","fable":"A spoken jeremiad replaces the band with one prophetic voice arraigning a whole generation, and its sharpest claim is that the disease was never violence but a curdled comfort. People toil to be entertained, trust nothing they are told, and pour zealotry into trivia, until even fury sputters, unable to name the grievance driving it forward. The interrogations that follow (what did you ever bleed for, where have your idols gone) leave the listener nowhere to stand, and the piece shuts not by pronouncing a sentence but by watching one already carried out. The bracketing periods of the title recast the whole tirade as a program run to completion: not a warning about ruin ahead but the printout of ruin that has finished running.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~eyeless": {"fable":"Paranoia here works as a broken circuit: the speaker crowns himself the last lucid mind besieged by nagging inner chatter, then concedes the sickness lives equally in his skull and the listener's, dissolving any line between the afflicted and the accusers. The famous demand to borrow a film idol's gaze before you can even take in California turns the true subject into vision itself, a self that owns no direct sight and must peer through counterfeit celebrity lenses. That explains why the abandoned-son passage — heir to a ghost, inheriting only vacancy — reads as identity assembled from what was never there. The closing scream to be met eye to eye springs the trap, begging for recognition through an organ he had to build himself, so the escalating repetition tightens the noose rather than resolving it.","fableDeep":"The verses run on a sanity paradox: the opening boast of being the only one with a brain is immediately undercut by the admission of hearing voices, and the toggle between \"it's all in your head\" and \"it's all in my head\" keeps swapping accuser and patient. The father verse is the wound — a phantom, a mystery, \"and that leaves me nothing\": lineage as an empty bequest, with the death-wish couplet answering it by declaring it already too late for him. Against that, the refrain about never seeing California without Marlon Brando's eyes — reportedly lifted from a ranting stranger — works as found prophecy: California the promised land of reinvention, Brando the borrowed face required for entry, vision itself something inherited from fathers and idols rather than owned. The final movement answers the bequest of nothing by growing a replacement organ, the repeated demand to be looked at through a brand new eye — sight rebuilt from scratch, on his own terms. Thesis: a song about inheritance as blindness, ending with the self-surgery of making your own eyes.","haiku":"Inherited madness and paternal absence leave the speaker fractured; they hear only complaints, desire violence, and claim new eyes while trapped in cycles of pain and self-destruction.","opus":"A blast of isolation and rage from Slipknot's Iowa origins, a narrator convinced he's the only sane one, fatherless and unmoored, the surreal refrain circling madness. Self-loathing turned outward as a threat.","sonnet":"Rage at an absent, phantom father who left nothing behind — the narrator questions his own sanity, is haunted by his lineage, and directs a barely-contained fury outward and inward simultaneously, the California image a recurring cipher for something beyond reach.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The refrain's central image began as a phrase the band overheard from a raving stranger on a New York street — found language, not invented, which is why the borrowed-eyes koan resists being decoded."},
"slipknot~gehenna": {"haiku":"Rejecting false self and craving freedom through severing connections; wanting to break free.","opus":"A disturbing meditation on wanting to shed one's own identity, watching someone sleep and imagining them as designed for punishment, begging for a severed heart to be freed so the narrator can finally stop being himself. Named for a biblical hell, this slow-burning Slipknot track trades their usual velocity for creeping dread, letting Corey Taylor's melodic side carry an intimacy that makes the self-erasure feel far more unsettling than any scream.","sonnet":"Identity dissolution through violent obsession — the speaker is drawn to another person not out of love but out of a need to sever their own self. The bond is figured as cutting, tethering, severing, with the act of possession framed as the only way to stop being who they are. It ends in a loop: wanting to be freed from the self by consuming someone else, over and over.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title reaches back to the ravine south of Jerusalem where children were once burned as offerings — and, in later tradition, where refuse smoldered without end, and it lends that history of consuming sacrifice to a devotion that cannot tell tenderness from ruin. Watching a sleeping lover breathe, the speaker declares them made expressly to be punished, so care and harm arrive in the same breath, a communion imagined as a clean incision rather than a shared meal. The hinge is a genuine paradox: he can finally become himself only because he no longer wants that self, and release comes solely from surrendering it whole to another. A late turn recasts the whole surrender as flight from belonging, choosing hurt over conformity, so self-erasure looks less like worship than the last exit available. The plea repeats past all reason because nothing remains to say beyond the demand, and the loop itself is the audible sound of an identity thinning into pure want.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~gematria-the-killing-name": {"haiku":"Systemic violence and capitalism as inevitable collapse, refusing to accept complacency or false hope.","opus":"Corey Taylor turns numerology and personal survival into a blistering indictment of America as an indifferent killing machine, media manipulation and manufactured enemies feeding a war culture. Rage aimed at a nation that neither feels nor discriminates.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of American violence and spiritual bankruptcy — the narrator frames themselves as both a survivor and a casualty of a country that kills without feeling, calling for revolution against a political and religious system that feeds people bones while burning cities.","src":"haiku","fable":"Gematria is the old kabbalistic trick of assigning numbers to letters until a word yields a hidden sum, and the song runs a nation through that arithmetic to expose the arithmetic of slaughter buried in its own syllables. It begins with a man dissolved into a birthdate, a maker of images with trembling hands who wanted the guilt but never the worship, then expands that private breakdown into a menacing collective voice. The unanswered question of divine indifference is the engine, not the doubt: if no one watches from above, the promise to leave a country in ash becomes the last available act of meaning. That is the provocation here, the song using its own arena-sized adoration as the delivery system for a threat that offers a minute to convert you or a bullet to end you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The chanted count of nine is the band counting itself — Slipknot's nine members — so the numerology turns inward for one line, the collective's own number set against the nation-name it runs through the killing arithmetic."},
"slipknot~gently": {"haiku":"Escape into altered mental states that temporarily dissolve life's pressures. The speaker craves this break but accepts they must eventually return to reality.","opus":"A retreat into an internal fantasy world as escape from a painful reality; the narrator deliberately files his real life away to the back of his mind, lingering in this pleasurable altered state as a needed break, resigned that he must always eventually return to life.","sonnet":"A quiet, almost trance-like interlude about escaping into a mental refuge — the narrator drifts into a pleasurable inner world to get a break from the pressures of real life, knowing the retreat is temporary but savoring it anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"A track carried over from Slipknot's earliest demo days and buried on Iowa, this is the anomaly in the catalog: no rage, no self-laceration, just a hushed hymn to dissociation. The speaker describes slipping into a private, sealed-off headspace where reality and personal history recede into storage, retrievable only when he chooses — an escape hatch narrated with the calm of someone reciting the benefits of a drug. What makes it unsettling is the register itself: the voice is grateful, almost seduced, treating this out-of-body remove as a place too good to inhabit permanently, and the resigned shrug that he must always return lands as the real wound. Read straight, it is about the pull of numbness as relief; read against the band it belongs to, the softness is the mask, and the mind willingly emptying itself is the same annihilation the rest of the record screams about, only here it feels wonderful.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"This is among the oldest songs in the catalog, dating to the 1996 Mate. Feed. Kill. Repeat. era and re-recorded years later — the anomaly of calm on Iowa is also its most ancient piece."},
"slipknot~get-this": {"fable":"A thrash blast appended to expanded pressings of the 1999 debut, it opens by summoning Corey to scream and then performs contempt as sport rather than any real creed. The scorn widens with cartoon deliberateness, ballooning from the garage down the street to every act on the planet until the target stops being a rival and becomes the whole circuit these masked Iowans were about to overrun. What rescues it from mere spleen is the buried admission that a wretched existence is somehow glorious, tilting the diss toward a survival howl. The refusal to muster anything past a curse owns its own ceiling: language has been exhausted, so the ultimatum comes as pure repetition, aggression standing in for the words that ran out.","fableDeep":"It opens as studio verité — a voice off-mic requesting a scream, the take left rough — so the track presents itself as documentation of a mood rather than a composition. The structure is concentric contempt: local bands, then U.S. bands, then worldwide bands, each circle handed the same dismissal, hatred scaled like a tour itinerary until the entire ecosystem the band lives inside has been written off, including, implicitly, the stage they are standing on. The complaints are scene-specific and petty on purpose — a show refused, opinions defended as great — pettiness inflated to cosmic scale being both the joke and the point; the cruder anatomical taunts function as percussion, not argument. The chant is where the mask slips: \"Life's so shitty, but ain't it fucking great?\" — misery and delight refusing to disentangle, the closest thing here to a philosophy. The ultimatum \"get this or die,\" repeated far past reason, converts a demand for understanding into pure drumming. Thesis: hatred performed so totally it loops into joy, a middle finger having the time of its life.","haiku":"A musician vents pure disdain for every band and trend while declaring his own opinions superior; he threatens violence and rejects all external judgment.","opus":"A scorched-earth tirade against the entire band ecosystem, spitting contempt at every scene local, national and global, while insisting the speaker's own hatred is the only honest thing left. The nihilist chorus reframes a miserable life as somehow magnificent. Slipknot broke out of Iowa as masked, nine-piece nu-metal chaos, and this B-side-styled cut is their pure aggression valve, a middle finger aimed outward with Corey Taylor screaming.","sonnet":"A gleeful tantrum aimed at every band in existence — the speaker rejects the entire music scene and everyone in it with a contempt so total it loops back into dark comedy, the repetition hammering the point until the sheer absurdity of the rage is the whole message.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~goodbye": {"haiku":"Difficult farewell to a united group; the moment before something irreversible happens.","opus":"A defiant refusal to say farewell, addressed to a bond forged long ago that nothing can tear apart, insisting no one will save them and no one could understand what they feel. It moves from disorientation toward resolve, a moment that will define the rest of their lives. From Slipknot's 2008 album All Hope Is Gone, it shows the Iowa band's melodic, almost hymnlike side, loyalty framed as the one thing worth holding against everything.","sonnet":"A rallying cry at the edge of something irreversible — a moment of collective silence before a farewell nobody wants to say. The song holds onto the memory of when nothing could stop them, insists that whatever is tearing things apart has not yet managed to do so, and refuses the word goodbye right up to the end.","src":"haiku","fable":"Written in the wake of founding bassist Paul Gray's death, this grief song refuses the very farewell its title promises, ranking the parting as the act the speaker will resist longer than any other. It keeps circling back to a brotherhood forged years earlier, one that once believed itself unkillable, and the loss becomes proof of that bond rather than its undoing: since the death failed to break the ranks, no future blow could. The consolation curdles when the voice stops reaching outward — no rescue is coming, no stranger can share this ache — sealing the survivors into a private circle that intends to hold the wound rather than let it heal. The single disoriented image, a compass ruined because the sun now sits at their backs, quietly concedes that the guiding light has slipped into memory, leaving them to steer by what is already gone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~i-am-hated": {"haiku":"The narrator declares universal hatred and rejection, positioning themselves as anti-cancer while living in permanent alienation. They reject society while harboring contempt for everyone, including themselves.","opus":"A defiant outsider casts the entire planet as his adversary, hurling scorn at mediocrity, phonies and overbearing employers while wearing every insult thrown at him as armor. The track flips total alienation into a proud, hostile gospel of self-regard.","sonnet":"The singer projects universal mutual hatred — directed outward at a conformist, mediocre world and inward at a life described as always having been shit — framing himself and the band as a necessary \"anti-cancer\" that has no use for the social games around them. The song swings between defiance and exhausted self-loathing.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title sounds like an injury but the track wields it as a blade, taking the loathing aimed at the outcast and firing it back outward at everyone within range. What makes the piece cohere is the drift of its pronoun, from a lone target to an accusing you to a swelling we, so that one man's exile recruits a congregation and self-disgust hardens into doctrine no apology can soften. The refrain about deities split down the middle exposes the fracture without flinching: bottomless worthlessness and messianic certainty sharing a single lung, each breath contradicting the last. By the end that we has curdled into something coercive and cornered, an army that swore it could not be halted now hearing the walls tighten, so the closing verdict arrives less as a plea for sympathy than as the jaws of the trap finally meeting, the despised speaker having grown into precisely the thing worth despising.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~if-rain-is-what-you-want": {"haiku":"Resurrection and defiance after destruction; refusing to be broken again.","opus":"A survivor's hymn born of isolation and dread, where terror numbs all sensation and downpour becomes an invitation to shut your eyes and let yourself drop. It swings toward defiance, insisting nothing can shatter him so long as he stays true to who he is. Slipknot ended an album on this slow cinematic swell, letting the gloom rise into something oddly triumphant.","sonnet":"Surviving the collapse of the self and coming back altered — cities inside the speaker that will never sleep again, a kind of permanent wakefulness after returning from something that felt like death. The emotional arc moves from numbness and distance through the realization that pressure can't break what has already been remade, arriving at a defiant refusal to feel alone again.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor's argument with grief, written by a band that had just buried a founding member and refuses to let the wound settle into simple mourning. The weather here is a taunt rather than a mercy: surrender is on offer, oblivion dressed as invitation, decay staged like a show the audience is welcome to attend and applaud. Against that undertow the voice stakes a hard floor, insisting that intact selfhood is the one thing collapse cannot reach, and reframing the wish to be shattered as a horror of isolation instead. The pivot lands in the hammered final refrain, where sleepless metropolises stop being a lament and harden into an oath. What began as invitation to let go becomes a promise that the absence will keep every survivor awake and watchful, permanently barred from the numb surrender the storm keeps dangling.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~interloper-demo": {"haiku":"Confrontational threat and warning against interference. The speaker asserts dominance and refuses to tolerate anyone pushing their buttons, expressing rage at intrusion.","opus":"An early Slipknot demo funnels the band's Iowa-forged aggression into a face-off with an unwelcome outsider the narrator claims he never needed, taunting them to justify why he shouldn't destroy them. Fueled by paranoia and menace, he vows to strike back at anyone who provokes him, fixating at last on the target's mere physical vulnerability in a nonstop stream of threats.","sonnet":"A furious confrontation with someone who has overstayed their welcome — a chronic interloper who pushes buttons and gets pushback in return. The speaker has no patience for the intrusion, no need for the other person's presence, and escalating contempt that has nowhere to go but physical. The anger is barely contained and entirely personal.","src":"haiku","fable":"A standoff monologue that reads at first like pure territorial rage—someone who won't fade, won't leave, and dares the intruder to give a single reason not to be destroyed. But the venom sharpens from generic aggression into indictment at the turn where, the moment the camera shuts off, the big man does something vile to a helpless thing: the interloper isn't a rival so much as a public figure whose broadcast decency is a costume, and the speaker's fury is really the fury of the one person who sees behind it. That's why the taunt keeps circling back to needing no reason—the hatred is self-evident once the mask drops. The closing mantra strips the target down to nothing but flesh, the answering voice insisting that is exactly what it stays focused on: a refusal to be dazzled by image, reducing the untouchable man to the mortal, corruptible body underneath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~iowa": {"haiku":"Obsessive possession and violent domination framed as intimate control. The speaker claims total ownership of another, willing to harm them to prove connection and eliminate escape.","opus":"The title track closing Slipknot's most punishing album inhabits an obsessive murderer, soothing his victim while burying them and settling them into a final sleep. Claiming them as his favorite and taking a body part as a memento, he smears killing and possessive love into one suffocating monologue, vowing that his prey will endure forever.","sonnet":"An obsessive, possessive love warps into something deeply disturbing — a speaker who fills a beloved's mouth with dirt, holds her in impossible ownership, and cycles between tenderness and violence. The possession is total and the language collapses into fragments as control becomes indistinguishable from destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song inhabits the hushed, doting first person of a captor for whom devotion and destruction share one grammar — every soft address falls on a body being soothed, mutilated, and hoarded like a keepsake. Corey Taylor voices not the band's usual collective fury but a single soothing predator, cataloguing a severed finger and bleach-dead eyes with the tenderness of a lullaby, so that ownership passes for care. The pivot from breaking the beloved into pieces to reassembling them at will strips the fantasy bare: possession is never enough, the aim is to author them into a controllable object, which lets the final vow braid murder and love as if no seam existed between them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Corey Taylor has said he tracked this vocal in a state of deliberate ruin — recorded alone and physically wrecked, pushing his voice to near-destruction — so the killer's serenity on tape is a performance cut at the edge of the singer's own collapse."},
"slipknot~killpop": {"haiku":"Obsessive love fixates on a self-harming lover whose damage attracts and traps the speaker. The song explores codependency and the desire to possess someone beautiful and broken.","opus":"An unsettling study of a possessive love fixated on a woman who harms herself, the narrator aestheticizing her suffering and needing it to continue. He refuses to release her until she returns his devotion, his tenderness curdling into a wish to own her completely, even to the point of wanting her destroyed so long as she is his.","sonnet":"A toxic, co-dependent obsession with a girl who self-harms — the narrator watches her and is fascinated rather than alarmed, wants to possess rather than help her, and by the end the dynamic turns coercive as he demands she die loving him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song that is really a hostage note, sung from inside a possessive delusion the narrator mistakes for devotion, reading a self-harming woman's wounds as beauty made for him. The tenderness is genuine to him, which is precisely what makes it monstrous; the melodic, almost worshipful verses are a mask over a chorus that keeps looping one condition. He will loosen his grip only once she loves him, a bargain engineered so release can never arrive, and freedom stays permanently deferred. The horror sharpens when he confesses that he is the captive here too, collapsing the fantasy of rescue into mutual imprisonment where neither can walk away. Then the ballad drops out for a flat chant demanding she die and adore him in the same breath, the moment possession quits pretending and names itself as the urge to destroy whatever it cannot keep.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~lech": {"haiku":"Self-loathing and guilt over harmful actions; refusing to excuse yourself with regret.","opus":"A venomous confession soaked in survivor's guilt and self-loathing, the narrator picturing walls built from his brothers' bones, insisting no one is bulletproof and daring the world to keep pushing him. It closes owning every regret and refusing to be anyone's superstar. Slipknot cut this for a record made in the shadow of grief and lineup loss, and that raw wound bleeds through the sneering aggression into something genuinely wounded.","sonnet":"Survivor's guilt weaponized into self-contempt — the speaker has outlasted people they destroyed, and the knowledge festers rather than resolves. There's a dead thing inside them they can't excise, a bone that won't heal, and the song cycles between rage at external accusers and a cold reckoning with the fact that no one, including the speaker, is exempt from consequence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Grief here refuses the shape mourning is supposed to take, arriving instead as scorched-earth contempt that dares anyone to pity a man cataloguing the bones and blood he claims as his own handiwork. The chant that armor fails everyone functions as a leveling curse, flung at rivals and swallowed by the singer alike, since the death haunting this record proved no one is exempt from being subtracted. Slipknot built the whole album around bassist Paul Gray, dead since 2010, and the drilled count of nine sounds less like arithmetic than a vow that the collective outlives its losses. So the venom is really an insistence on owning both the ruin and the choices behind it, trading any comfort for the right to burn through loss on terms no outsider gets to soften.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~left-behind":{"haiku":"Grieves the loss and deterioration of friendships or relationships, watching people slip away while memories of connection and betrayal linger.","opus":"Alienation, decay and betrayal, as the narrator feels everything slipping away and describes being left behind after being deceived by someone he now wants to keep at a distance.","sonnet":"The narrator feels cut off from people and memories that have slipped away, caught between wanting to ignore a toxic presence and being pulled toward it, unable to fully let go.","src":"haiku","fable":"Corey Taylor built Iowa on self-disgust, and this track turns that loathing into a fable about preservation gone rotten: the speaker pictures himself as a lab specimen, sealed behind a pane and pickled in caustic powder, mounted for viewing rather than granted the dignity of rotting on his own. The chorus stages an impossible double motion, recoiling and reaching at once, so longing and refusal cancel out and everything simply drains off. The verses conjure a grotesque, deformed version of the other to excuse the flinch, but the bridge admits the truth: the presence he most wants to scour out is the one that still lingers like a haunting. His vow to destroy whatever draws near reads less as menace than as raw fear of intimacy in someone convinced he is already spoiled. The outro thins to a single repeated line, worn down until it hollows out, an honest close that offers no rescue.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"longing and refusal cancelling in someone convinced he is already spoiled","built":"the outro thins to one repeated line worn hollow with no rescue"},
"slipknot~left-behind-live-in-london-2002": {"haiku":"Abandonment and desertion, being discarded and forgotten by those who matter.","opus":"From the Iowa nu-metal collective's Iowa era, this captures the terror of watching connection erode, faces vanishing into time and lime. Ignoring and approaching at once, the narrator feels everything slip away while drowning in isolation. A live document of Slipknot's rawest wound: the fear of being abandoned and of destroying anyone who dares get close.","sonnet":"Collective abandonment and slow calcification — wrapped in glass and soaked in lime, friends' pictures made to make you cry, a \"thalidomide robot face\" that the narrator is still drowning under. \"We all got left behind, we let it all slip away\" is guilt and victimhood fused: the slipping was done to them and by them at once.","src":"haiku","fable":"Corey Taylor built Iowa on self-disgust, and this track turns that loathing into a fable about preservation gone rotten: the speaker pictures himself as a lab specimen, sealed behind a pane and pickled in caustic powder, mounted for viewing rather than granted the dignity of rotting on his own. The chorus stages an impossible double motion, recoiling and reaching at once, so longing and refusal cancel out and everything simply drains off. The verses conjure a grotesque, deformed version of the other to excuse the flinch, but the bridge admits the truth: the presence he most wants to scour out is the one that still lingers like a haunting. His vow to destroy whatever draws near reads less as menace than as raw fear of intimacy in someone convinced he is already spoiled. The outro thins to a single repeated line, worn down until it hollows out, an honest close that offers no rescue.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~liberate": {"fable":"The demand is inverted from the start: what a person would ordinarily beg to be rid of is instead unchained on purpose rather than caged. That reversal turns therapy into weaponry — the wall the speaker throws up isn't meant to trap his own fury but to seal out a world he reads as rigged, dishonest, and starved of anything real to offer. Nine masked bodies performing as a faceless swarm sharpens the paradox, since the voice keeps asserting a singular, boundless self while dissolving into the anonymous many around him. The final stretch is engineered to disintegrate, courtroom verbs and rhyming abstractions piling up faster than sense can hold them until the verse leaves only a single hurled charge of deceit, repeated until it stops meaning and becomes pure sound. What steadies the whole thing is its logic of survival dressed as ruin: the collapse he stages is a defense, not a surrender.","fableDeep":"The paradox sits in the first verse: he sections himself off, puts a wall up, keeps the dog at bay — containment as survival — while the chorus begs to \"liberate my madness,\" so imprisonment and release are the same wish viewed from opposite sides of the wall. The parenthetical ratio \"one of me, all of you\" keeps the odds onscreen: one interior against a unanimous outside. The taunt verse borrows a computing proverb — garbage in, garbage out — and turns it into genealogy: feed a person contempt and contempt is what compiles. The ethics are stated with unusual candor — \"what is vital isn't always humane\" — necessity granted precedence over decency, alongside the boast that you can break in vain but never break away. The final spiral is sound overtaking sense — sisters, blisters, salivate, litigate, liberate — internal rhyme accelerating until it slams into the thrice-screamed \"Fraud!\", aimed, by the preceding gasp toward God, as much upward as outward. Thesis: a case for madness as the only honest response to a system built on fakery.","haiku":"The speaker rejects systemic control and hypocrisy, insisting on personal autonomy and refusing to participate in what they see as corrupt systems.","opus":"An early Slipknot demand to unleash the buried madness rather than wall it off, the mask-clad collective's one-versus-all defiance of a world that treats them as garbage. Catharsis through admitting the sickness instead of performing sanity.","sonnet":"A demand to be freed from a system that labels and constrains — the narrator refuses to be identified as a failure or a fit-thrower, insists he's above the lies being told about him, and rages against the forces that want to put walls around his madness. The liberation he wants is from having to explain or justify himself to anyone.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~me-inside": {"haiku":"Claustrophobic mental anguish and isolation tear the speaker apart; hatred and mockery deepen their psychological collapse.","opus":"A debut-era track that turns its rage inward, the narrator boxed in and mutilated, cut off from feeling by a life that overwhelms and destroys him. Numbed, humiliated and trapped with nowhere to run, he names the self-inflicted torment the band's masked fury was built to drag out into the open.","sonnet":"Emotional numbness, claustrophobia, and rage corrode the self from within — life feels impossible, relationships have collapsed, and what remains is a lobotomised shell that cannot stop tearing itself apart. The anger eventually turns outward toward the mockers, but the damage is already structural and ongoing.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakdown narrated from inside the collapse: the verses catalogue a self going numb — severed from feeling, catatonic, lobotomized to a hollowed-out husk — while an unnamed you mocks and criticizes, so the torment is at once internal decay and external persecution, and the you may be the me. The pummeling refrain names the whole state as being torn, less a sentence than the raw acoustic fact of the tearing. The pivot is the untranslated Spanish jeer that we are ugly and we stink but we rule everything: the crushed subject flips the accusers' contempt into a claimed kingship, weaponizing ugliness rather than fleeing it , so that being hated becomes the ground to stand on. The final insistence that the tormentor will be silenced, once they are erased and vanished for good, converts the victim's helplessness into a threat aimed straight back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~metabolic": {"haiku":"A person grapples with impossible family dynamics and inherited trauma, unable to become someone else and resenting the person who shaped their identity.","opus":"A bond of mutual destruction with the one person tethering him to the world, whom he has every reason to despise yet cannot release, swearing he's braced for death while accusing them of doing the killing. Its unsettling core is envious self-erasure: unable to become this person, he fantasizes about absorbing their flaws and turning himself into a flawless copy.","sonnet":"A corrosive, identity-destroying envy or psychological enmeshment with someone the narrator simultaneously hates and cannot stop measuring themselves against, reaching the point where they feel they have made their own life into a mockery.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a study of a bond soured into rivalry with a figure the speaker was measured against and failed to match, the wound of never becoming them curdling into a wish to devour and impersonate them instead. The word nepotistic gives it a family shape: an inheritance of neglect, a name he cannot shed, a tether he despises precisely because it is the last one holding him to anyone. The chorus balances two truths that cannot both be resolved, since he courts his own end while blaming the other for engineering it, and the closing scramble of syllables is the point where accuser and accused dissolve into one voiceless heap. What looks like pure contempt is need with nowhere else to go, and the fantasy of wearing the other's flaws to become a flawless version of them is the same self-erasure worn as revenge.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~my-pain": {"haiku":"Pain presented as love or comfort; a toxic relationship rationalized as devotion.","opus":"A strange, tender-menacing piece narrated by suffering itself, personified as a devoted lover who wants the singer calmer and freer, claiming to be his companion, his barrier, his shackle and finally his end, insisting none of it was ever affection. Slipknot drift into slow, hypnotic, near-gothic territory, where torment coos like a besotted partner.","sonnet":"Pain personified as a possessive lover who wants to liberate through suffering — the song layers the vocabulary of devotion over the vocabulary of captivity, each tender declaration doubling as a lock or a chain. By the end the two collapse into each other entirely: love and pain are the same thing, and the speaker has been waiting for this union all along.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tenderness here is the trap: the caretaker's voice is the title's own pain personified, reassuring its charge even as it names itself the barrier, the shackle, the last thing anyone will ever touch. Each vow of guardianship narrows into confinement, protection turning custodial until comfort and abduction speak in one unbroken murmur. The word repeated in the refrain works like an incantation, its meaning hollowed by repetition until it functions as leash rather than gift. What the singer calls affection is really possession wearing devotion's mask, a hurt reframed as proof of belonging so the captive learns to want the cage — an abusive romance with his own suffering that he shows no wish to leave.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~my-plague": {"haiku":"Two people trapped in mutual blame and resentment inflict emotional wounds on each other, each one aware of their own role yet unable to break the cycle of plague.","opus":"A venomous purge aimed at someone whose ego and cruelty the speaker abhors, threatening to tear them apart while admitting he's a bastard hurting himself by fixating on the hatred; the core insight is mutual blame and mutual plague, and the vow to never become the person he despises.","sonnet":"About a mutual toxic dynamic between two people who each infect and damage each other — the narrator acknowledges he shares the blame, cycles through rage and near-violence, but ultimately can't stop hurting himself trying to understand it.","src":"haiku","fable":"What sounds like a scorched-earth kiss-off to some belittling megalomaniac is secretly a hall of mirrors, since the refrain keeps splitting culpability two ways at once. The accused blames the speaker and blames himself, torments the speaker and torments himself, until the pair of them collapse into a single figure warring with its own reflection. The middle passage confesses this outright: turning the blade inward, craving to know why he wounds himself, owning his own rottenness while claiming the small dignity of not hiding it. The late chant that he will never become the other plays less like triumph than terror, a fear of that very resemblance where a single misfiring nerve tips private self-hatred into open combat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~nero-forte": {"haiku":"Exhaustion from being used and bled dry; recognition that the enemy knows them better than anyone.","opus":"A seething confrontation with someone who exploited the narrator's own neediness against him, drained him, and did it with practiced ease, the refrain conceding that cruelty is simply their specialty. Torn between restraint and collapse, he circles the edge of losing it. Slipknot pair nu-metal fury with the more melodic, wounded writing of their 2019 record.","sonnet":"Burning out from a relationship built on depletion — one person running on empty while the other thrives on animosity and sabotage. The speaker has been bled dry and gaslit for too long, and what starts as barely-contained fury resolves into something colder: the recognition that cruelty is simply what the other person does best.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of the abusive relationship as a rigged contract, where the speaker's every attempt to be sufficient only feeds a partner who treats him as another interchangeable filling for an emptiness that can't be filled. The refrain turns the accusation into a bitter compliment — cruelty is the one thing the other person is genuinely good at, so the enemy becomes the true expert, the only reliable craftsman in a home turned upside down. The song's real drama is in the speaker's divided will: he knows exactly where the exit stands, yet some undertow of good sense keeps refusing to make the break, and he half-admits he'd deceive himself to stay, which reframes the fury as self-incrimination as much as blame. In a late verse the questions turn interrogative and specific — did he deserve it, was it all a lie — so the read is less a breakup than the moment an abuse victim narrates himself out of denial, still bleeding but finally naming the fake for what it is.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~new-abortion": {"haiku":"A song about generational control and technological subjugation, where the speaker identifies with a virus or hidden system that can't be detected or killed. It portrays a young generation as manipulated and extorted by those hiding their fingerprints through technology.","opus":"A snarling generational grievance, casting the young as the discarded product of a controlling, surveilling system that blames them for everything. Beneath the body-horror imagery is the defiant insistence that they can't strip away the self.","sonnet":"A generational grievance dressed in visceral imagery — the speaker rails against the systems and people that control and dismiss the younger generation, calling them the \"new abortion\": a cohort extorted, disbelieved, and blamed for everything. Beneath the horror-body imagery is a paranoid but real anger about institutional power over identity and autonomy.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song runs on two metaphor tracks that eventually converge: illness (a body clawing off its own tumors, spreading like hepatitis, immune to being killed) and digital sabotage (a fault deep in a mainframe, binary code declared the whole of existence, biometric traces masked by whoever holds the wiring). The braggart opening — a voice calling itself deathless, multitudinous, ethereal — first plays as standard Iowa menace, an infection that won't be scrubbed or rewritten. Then a late verse turns the whole thing inside out: the boaster is really the unwanted speaking up, a cohort branded the terminated pregnancy of its era, squeezed dry, treated as blighted offspring whose grievances get waved off before they finish speaking. What sounded like a predator gloating is a thrown-away demographic explaining that plague and glitch are simply how the disposable get described from above. The only possession the discard refuses to surrender is his inner self, hammered home in loop after loop, then sealed with two syllables of pure contempt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~new-abortion-live-in-london-2002": {"haiku":"Serial parasites cling to skin while the speaker tears them away with violence, becoming demon demanding worship. Possession transforms the host into something beyond humanity.","opus":"A snarling anthem of generational grievance and defiant selfhood, imagining the individual as an unkillable virus infiltrating a controlling system. Slipknot's masked rage frames the disaffected as scapegoated progeny, always blamed, always extorted, waved off as not worth taking seriously. Beneath the paranoia and conspiracy runs one unbreakable line: no institution can strip away the soul.","sonnet":"A generation framed as the \"new abortion\" — perpetually blamed, mocked, shaped like zeros and ones for execution, going underground like hepatitis — the narrator claims ethereal survival and calls out the fingerprints of control hidden everywhere, drawing a line: \"You can't take my soul away from me.\"","src":"haiku","fable":"The song runs on two metaphor tracks that eventually converge: illness (a body clawing off its own tumors, spreading like hepatitis, immune to being killed) and digital sabotage (a fault deep in a mainframe, binary code declared the whole of existence, biometric traces masked by whoever holds the wiring). The braggart opening — a voice calling itself deathless, multitudinous, ethereal — first plays as standard Iowa menace, an infection that won't be scrubbed or rewritten. Then a late verse turns the whole thing inside out: the boaster is really the unwanted speaking up, a cohort branded the terminated pregnancy of its era, squeezed dry, treated as blighted offspring whose grievances get waved off before they finish speaking. What sounded like a predator gloating is a thrown-away demographic explaining that plague and glitch are simply how the disposable get described from above. The only possession the discard refuses to surrender is his inner self, hammered home in loop after loop, then sealed with two syllables of pure contempt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~no-life": {"fable":"This is Slipknot borrowing openly from rap-metal, Corey Taylor half-rapping a war in the second person and name-checking rap voices of the era to graft hip-hop's combat onto pure claustrophobia. The title's hook indicts survival itself as unlivable, a nation under quarantine where liberty survives only as a broadcast slogan and everyone reads as a suspect awaiting arraignment. That dread folds back on the singer, whose enemy keeps resolving into a reflection: a face peeling down to nothing structural, an engine rigged to detonate from within. So the bragging swagger of hunting adversaries sours into a man boxed against the plaster, then caves into a chant of blamelessness that loops because it can never be verified.","fableDeep":"The opening inverts the freedom boast inside a single couplet — my freedom is best, whole country's on house arrest — patriotic language and lockdown reality forced to share a rhyme. The most pointed move is punctuational: a death face-down on a \"suicide,\" the scare quotes doing the accusing, custody deaths and cover-ups implied rather than argued. The hip-hop invocations — asking where Chuck D is when you need him, noting Cool J already screamed it — place the track in a protest lineage while conceding that lineage has gone missing. From a masked band, \"your mask is skin and bone\" cuts both ways: the real deception is the face, not the costume. The chorus runs two parenthetical needs beneath the title phrase — got to get out, can't be blamed — escape and acquittal as the era's twin cravings, and the outro strips down to the second, the blame refused three times as the last word. Thesis: a protest song whose enemy is not a government but the counterfeit life being issued in place of one.","haiku":"A critique of surveillance, social control, and performative existence where masks hide bones; freedom and reality remain illusions sold to the trapped.","opus":"A claustrophobic scream about a world under surveillance and self-destruction where nothing counts as a real life, laced with hip-hop reverence and refusal to be blamed. Slipknot channel late-nineties American dread into a corner no one can escape.","sonnet":"A furious critique of social control, institutional violence, and performative freedom — referencing state surveillance, police brutality, and media complicity, arguing that the life being sold to people is no kind of life at all.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~nomadic": {"haiku":"Existential displacement and complicity in corruption; distrust of everyone and everything.","opus":"A jagged confession from someone who is at once the cause and the debris, vowing to reduce you to tears and loathing while admitting he can't release his own defects, the deadening sameness of his life now feeling oddly comfortable. It orbits guilt, disguise and self-disgust in the band's churning, dissonant mode.","sonnet":"Lost inside a self that keeps shifting — the speaker can't locate who they are outside the roles others assign them, and the status quo has calcified from punishment into something that just feels like home. There's violence underneath the resignation: a desire to make others cry, to be hated, as if inflicting pain is the only proof of presence left.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a portrait of a man so at ease inside his own wreckage that he keeps goading someone to despise him, because being despised is the last role he can still perform convincingly. The wandering figure of the title, unnamed in the words, is a self with no fixed shape, and the chorus loops one unanswerable question: what remains of a person once you strip away the masks and the failures. Corey Taylor sneers at rigid, either-or morality as a fascist reflex, yet the contempt curls back on him, since the deadened sameness he condemns is precisely the shelter he has learned to inhabit. Slipknot processes the grief threaded through .5: The Gray Chapter obliquely, so the imagery of being the round that makes you obey, and the plea to be hated, plays as a man provoking any reaction at all because his own feeling has gone numb. The closing verse folds the whole accusation inward, confessing only after the proof lands and refusing to break fresh ground for a body, terrified of what he might unearth, which is where the loss buried under all the swagger finally surfaces.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~not-long-for-this-world": {"haiku":"Bitterness about knowledge and suffering; resigned acceptance that death is near.","opus":"A caustic accounting of self-injury, betrayal and burnout, the singer conceding he is embittered and seething over what he has endured, sensing he is already spent, worn out from justifying himself to someone lost in their own theatrics. The chorus surrenders to the idea that he was never built to last, trading fury for exhausted clarity.","sonnet":"A breakup rendered as a diagnosis of mutual corrosion — the speaker is already gone emotionally, waiting only for the formal end, and turns the grief outward into contempt for a partner who performed goodness without delivering it. The song ends not in devastation but in exhausted relief: waking up and deciding enough, finally, is enough.","src":"haiku","fable":"The voice here speaks as if it has already crossed some line and looked back, turning a demand to be told the manner of its dying into a dare thrown at someone who preferred a comforting fiction. The opening runs a thesis on injury — however the wound arrives, it heals the same, so cosmetic difference cannot launder what was done, and that same accounting is aimed at a hypocrite polishing a saintly surface over private rot. Fatigue, not fury, drives the turn near the end; worn out from restating the obvious, the speaker offers a poisonous little farewell and reframes his exit as the clearest verdict he ever reached, an early morning where he simply refused to continue. When the refrain slips from present into past, the boast curdles into an inscription, and the reproach becomes the freight he hands the one left standing to shoulder alongside their own culpability.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~only-one": {"fable":"Megalomania inflates itself until it runs out of anyone to conquer, and the speaker's boasts about being unscathed by fire and perched atop the pile start to sound like a man shouting into an empty stadium. The cruel twist arrives when the rival turns out to be a duplicate of the self, a mirrored double rather than a separate enemy, so the survivalist chant about who leaves the fight standing curdles into a private war against one's own reflection. Absolute power with no witness left is just isolation dressed as triumph, which is why it all collapses into a demand for explanation nobody can give and a bored consent to dying, the swagger having devoured whatever it was supposed to prove.","fableDeep":"It opens as a product announcement — pain, made to order — then the ego takes the stage: \"I'm not the second coming, I'm the first wave,\" the messianic comparison rejected only to claim something larger, an army's worth of one man, totalitarian by his own delighted admission. The crowd is processed as static — all he hears is human noise — and the central sovereignty claim, \"I belong to only me,\" is defended the way territory is: filthy hands kept off, every reason counted as a right to hate. The refrain converts identity into a duel — only one of us walks away — repeated until it stops being threat and becomes law of physics. But the undertow is mortal: he can only die over time, death is fine, give me mine — bravado with a memento mori sewn into the lining, supremacy claimed by someone visibly running out the clock. Thesis: self-possession pushed to its logical end, where owning yourself requires treating every other person as the challenger.","haiku":"A singular, unstoppable force emerges superior to all opposition, uncompromising and contemptuous toward weaker rivals. Only one voice survives this confrontation intact.","opus":"Corey Taylor at his most swaggering and grandiose, snarling that his singular self stands apart from a nameless herd who authored their own fate. The last-one-standing refrain turns identity into a brutal contest for the right to answer to nobody but yourself.","sonnet":"A supremacist declaration of ego: the narrator claims absolute superiority, belongs to no one, and frames any confrontation as a battle with a single survivor — only one walks away, and he has already decided it will be him.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~orphan": {"haiku":"Orphaned and monstrous, aware of your monstrosity but unbothered; claiming your darkness as identity.","opus":"From 2014's The Gray Chapter, this is a man reshaped by abandonment, mourning that he's run out of demons to fight before embracing the monster he's become, the one who wrecked another's life. Self-loathing and defiance intertwine, part of him rotting away while another rises, the Iowa band channeling grief-fueled fury into a savage reckoning with identity and blame.","sonnet":"A self-made outcast who has burned through every demon and come out the other side unrecognisable — not redeemed but transformed into something harder, a monster who rose from the dumpster. He names himself the orphan who killed your world, settling the account with everyone who claimed to know him while never seeing him at all.","src":"haiku","fable":"Having exhausted every enemy worth fighting, the speaker finds his hardened surface was never protection but a prison venting away his oxygen, and grief curdles into an appetite for cruelty he half-relishes. He rationalizes it as instinct — flesh he suddenly craves, lives he takes because inflicted pain is the only thing that stitches him back to feeling whole. The recurring accusation is disguised as an introduction: a stranger to you until this reveal, he claims paternity over the ruin he leaves, abandonment ricocheting toward whoever failed to keep him. Beneath it idles a metronome of resentment, the arithmetic that some hoard plenty while he was left with scraps, converting personal loss into a license to wreck whatever he can reach.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Heard another way, the we-verbs make this the discarded forming a family — the album's not-your-kind community in miniature, the outro arithmetic flipping from what the orphan lacks to what the pack together is."},
"slipknot~override": {"haiku":"Captive minds are caged and controlled by selfish authority figures; a revolution aims to override conditioning and take back stolen agency.","opus":"A menacing address to those who let systems cage them and pull them apart for selfish ends, the collective voice arriving as the override that ends the charade. It weaponises the promise of hell coming as a threat to the willingly controlled and abandoned.","sonnet":"A hostile takeover narrative directed at someone who has been manipulated and controlled by others — the speaker, positioned as a revolutionary force called 'the override,' tells this person they've been owned and used, and that a reckoning is coming that will sweep away the controllers.","src":"haiku","fable":"Voiced by a coercive collective that keeps ordering everyone to the ground and chanting surrender, the song maps the mind at its cruelest — an inner force that promises approaching hell yet sells the locked room as shelter, since captivity stings less than uncertainty. Its trick is speaking in tenderness: it insists cruelty was once devotion, that only a discarded feeling remains, so the tyrant sounds less like an outside foe than like despair dressing self-destruction as care. On a record shaped by grief, that command to quit carries the tone mourning takes when it wants you to lay down your resistance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~people-shit": {"haiku":"Collective human worth is worthless; the speaker rejects universal condemnation while accepting their own complicity in degradation. Misanthropy and nihilism drive the anthem.","opus":"From Iowa's masked nine-piece at their most abrasive, this Iowa-era eruption is pure misanthropic catharsis, the narrator numb, self-loathing and defiant, insisting he feels nothing yet unafraid to cry. The chant reducing humanity to filth becomes a survival stance against a world that told him he was born to fail.","sonnet":"Rage and alienation pour out as a wholesale rejection of humanity. The narrator feels surrounded by contempt, numbed to pain, and cornered — blood on his hands, no clear reason why. His answer is not grief but defiance: an insistence on his own separateness and refusal to be owned by anyone.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title reads as blanket contempt, yet each verse leaks its opposite: a man onstage naming himself the idiot on display, the disappointment everyone predicted, sickened that shame still moves him at all. Reducing the whole species to filth is armor, a way to strike first so no one can wound the wound he already describes with a barrel at his temple. The jeers stapled to the chant, the boasts of superiority and fearlessness, are that same defense wearing a sneer, and the record keeps splitting the mask open. Mortality, invoked toward the end, sounds less like menace than the last leveling mercy available once self-loathing has sealed every other door.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~prelude-3-0": {"haiku":"Regret over unspoken words and unasked questions in a failed relationship. The speaker masks sins and reasons behind silence as everything comes to an end.","opus":"A hushed, sorrowful post-mortem on a bond that died from things left unspoken by both people, the singer confessing he made no effort and she showed no concern, every justification withering as the finality tolls again and again. Slipknot open their third album with grief instead of aggression, proving they could reach genuine melancholy beneath the mask.","sonnet":"A brief, bruised elegy for a relationship where nobody said the right thing at the right time — two people who failed each other through silence and inattention, arriving too late at the knowledge that it's already over, the word over repeating like a door closing again and again.","src":"haiku","fable":"For a band that built its name on rage, this is the sound of the fight abandoned — a post-mortem on a relationship neither person would risk naming, delivered in the flattened voice of someone drained past feeling. The song is organized around a pair of confessions that mirror each other: first the speaker's own failures to speak or try, then the charge turned outward, the silence and indifference of the other laid at that person's feet. Blame changes direction but resolves nothing, because the verses hinge on shedding one set of skins and hiding another set of sins — the same evasion running underneath either version of who was at fault. The refrain simply repeats that it is over, more times than it needs to, until the finality curdles into something obsessive; the tolling is not acceptance but the mind unable to stop confirming the loss. The lone shouted refusal at the very end breaks the numbness for a single beat, the buried protest that the resignation was covering all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~prosthetics": {"fable":"A predator narrates possession from the inside, and the horror is how flatly he reasons: wanting becomes needing becomes the settled fact that they will be his, each step stated like arithmetic rather than appetite. The clinical title hangs over it — a person reduced to a part you fit and install and keep — so the abduction arrives dressed in a lover's tenderness, clenched fingers and bolted windows treated as the same gesture of care. What lifts it past a shock-scene is the crack in the voice: he calls the whole thing an error he saw coming, then shovels the blame back onto the captive for drawing it out of him, a man unable to hold his two halves together. By the close he stops recognizing his own act, asking why this feels like anything new while doing it anyway, the compulsion outrunning whatever part of him keeps flinching.","fableDeep":"The tenses map the crime. Future tense first — even if you run, I will find you — desire already phrased as inevitability; then past tense for the acquisition, a scene framed almost romantically, the target leaning from an open window, laughing, \"too perfect, far too careless,\" before the flat, terrible plainness of the taking; then present tense for captivity, hospitality language rotted into threat — make yourself at home, you're here to stay — every door locked, every window shut. What makes it more disturbing than a monster's monologue is the second voice threaded through: \"I knew it was a mistake,\" \"I can't believe I'm doin' this\" — a conscience fully operational and fully useless, narrating instead of braking. The parenthetical \"you have brought it out of me\" completes the abuser's grammar, transferring authorship of the crime to its victim. The title supplies the psychology: a prosthetic, a substitute part — a stolen person installed where something in him is missing. Thesis: a horror story told from inside the rationalization.","haiku":"A obsessive captor imprisons someone they found, trapping them and monitoring them constantly, convinced ownership is justified.","opus":"A deeply unsettling monologue of obsession and abduction, the narrator fixating on a careless stranger at an open window, taking her, locking every door, and cycling between possessive threat and the sick realization that it was a mistake. Slipknot, the Iowa metal collective, close their self-titled debut with this slow, menacing crawl, its stalker's first-person horror ranking among the band's most disturbing early tracks.","sonnet":"Obsession tipping into abduction — the speaker has decided to possess someone regardless of consent, locking windows, watching constantly, and overriding every boundary. The self-questioning running underneath — repeated acknowledgments that this is a mistake — does not stop the behavior; it just makes the song uncomfortable with itself in the same way the speaker is uncomfortable with what they are doing.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~psychosocial": {"haiku":"Society is trapped in fake ideologies, corruption, and hollow resistance; the speaker claims only their voice (the martyr's) sees what others cannot perceive.","opus":"A furious indictment of collective hypocrisy and manufactured martyrdom, telling the powerful to dig their own graves and stuff their mouths with saved money. Beneath the chant lies a warning that suppressed hatred and self-serving righteousness are killing everyone, and that a killing idea can't be stopped.","sonnet":"A broadside against manufactured rebellion and false ideology — the narrator sees through the hollow political and religious machinery that turns people into martyrs, leaving only hatred when the belief system collapses.","src":"haiku","fable":"Taylor turns a rant against war-profiteering and manufactured piety into a mirror, since the martyr he swears is hidden in his chest is the self-righteousness he despises in the bloodthirsty crowd. The device is that reversal: once every enemy has been slaughtered, only the grievance remains, curdling into an appetite that hunts for fresh targets simply to keep breathing. Medals stamped for valor and slogans borrowed from noble resistance become interchangeable props in a spectacle where conviction has hardened into a contagion nobody can quarantine. The chanted diagnosis names the whole sickness as a mind-disease of the group, which the speaker admits he carries himself while still refusing to be the sole guilty party.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~pulse-of-the-maggots": {"haiku":"Grassroots resistance; the powerless rising up to fight for truth and strength.","opus":"A rallying cry of solidarity aimed at the band's own following, refusing to be dismissed as worthless and pledging to fight, even die, for those who can't defend themselves. Slipknot's nickname for their fans turns this into an us-against-the-world oath, built on the hard survivor's logic that hardship only forges strength.","sonnet":"A defiant collective declaration from those who have been written off — the year has failed them, the system runs its experiments on them, and the heroes they were meant to admire are actually the enemy. The song pivots from diagnosis to combat anthem, insisting that the outcasts who fight for the voiceless will outlast everything thrown at them, even if it costs them everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A fight-song built on inversion: the band's grotesque pet name for its fanbase becomes a coat of arms, and the yoked opposites of the chorus argue that the vilified rabble, not those steering things, hold the moral high ground. The verses travel from a solitary voice defining itself against ruin and squandered promise toward a chanted we, so the track dramatizes its own claim that no single person can carry the fight indefinitely. That pivot is the engine: the repeated vow of refusal to be killed off and the barked yes-we-understand drills recast a private creed as a stadium's sworn pact, the borrowed Nietzsche maxim about adversity forging strength arriving as a mass exhale rather than a slogan. Nearer to Corey Taylor's blue-collar survivor sermon than to the earlier records' rot and self-loathing, it prizes solidarity and counts the mere attempt as victory.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~purity": {"fable":"A first-person descent into confinement staged as claustrophobic panic: the speaker seals himself in cellars, holes and kennels, degraded into the very dog his tormentors hound, hands clamped over a face that can no longer breathe. Its engine is the chasm between being watched and being witnessed — crowds gawk yet register nothing of the buried self, and that failed gaze curdles from a plea to be found into contempt he flings back at them. The climax reaches for grim purification: a refusal to die, an offer to be cut open, a wager that suffering itself might teach, so the chanted title lands as savage irony, a wholeness bought only by scouring the self past empathy and every last reserve down to whatever raw thing survives.","fableDeep":"Drawn from the Purity Knight story — an internet account of a kidnapped girl buried alive that turned out to be fiction, and later got the track pulled from reissues — the song inhabits the cellar from inside. The opening is pure claustrophobia: a psychopathic maze, sweat in the eyes, and the burial phrased chillingly as imperative, the victim seeming to dictate her own interment. Wordplay carries the degradation — not a dog, but the one being dogged, housed in a buried kennel — while the pleas to be heard and found alternate with the stare-refrain: you all look, but you will never see what is inside me, spectatorship without rescue. The bridge is the shocker: the voice flips from victim to volunteer, welcoming the blade as a way in, claiming \"knowledge in my pain\" and shoving empathy aside, then repeating \"I can't die\" — defiance indistinguishable from damnation. Thesis: the title names the terrible bargain — purity as whatever remains after suffering has refined you, chanted at the end like a product name.","haiku":"A person is trapped and suffocating, isolated and losing their grip on sanity while others stare but refuse to truly see the suffering inside them. Despair consumes them as they slowly break.","opus":"A claustrophobic plunge into derangement and self-entombment, the narrator feeling sealed underground in a pit of his own making, fighting for air and loathing both himself and the gawking crowd. Underneath runs dissociation and fury, a refusal to perish paired with a plea to be discovered, and something monstrous within that onlookers gape at yet never actually perceive.","sonnet":"Based on the real-life case of a woman locked in a cellar, the song inhabits her perspective — trapped, unheard, and slowly dying — alongside the detached, contemptuous voice of the captor who sees her as an object; the tension between her desperate need to be found and her captor's cold indifference makes it one of the most disturbing songs in the catalog.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The song drew on an online story about a girl, Purity Knight, kidnapped and buried alive — posted with no marker that it was fiction; a copyright dispute later stripped the track from the album for a decade. The entombment is a real found-text, and the buried voice is hers as much as his."},
"slipknot~purity-live": {"haiku":"Suffocation within enforced confinement transforms into desperate pleas for liberation from observation and exposure. The narrator rejects the dehumanizing gaze that renders them spectacle and object of contempt.","opus":"A claustrophobic breakdown of someone buried alive in his own hate and panic, hands smothering his face as he loses his mind. Stared at but never truly seen, he despises the emptiness he glimpses in others and rages against a God he demands to have witnessed.","sonnet":"Total sensory and psychological overload traps the speaker in a psychopathic daze — buried in a homemade kennel, losing reserve, screaming that they cannot get out while the crowd stares but never sees the something inside them, until a final plea for cutting knowledge and purity erupts.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person descent into confinement staged as claustrophobic panic: the speaker seals himself in cellars, holes and kennels, degraded into the very dog his tormentors hound, hands clamped over a face that can no longer breathe. Its engine is the chasm between being watched and being witnessed — crowds gawk yet register nothing of the buried self, and that failed gaze curdles from a plea to be found into contempt he flings back at them. The climax reaches for grim purification: a refusal to die, an offer to be cut open, a wager that suffering itself might teach, so the chanted title lands as savage irony, a wholeness bought only by scouring the self past empathy and every last reserve down to whatever raw thing survives.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The song drew on an online story about a girl, Purity Knight, kidnapped and buried alive — posted with no marker that it was fiction; a copyright dispute later stripped the track from the album for a decade. The entombment is a real found-text, and the buried voice is hers as much as his."},
"slipknot~red-flag": {"haiku":"Parasitic people surround and consume the strong, using isolation and theft to break them down. The speaker refuses to be owned or eaten, calling out the predators to reclaim their power.","opus":"A later Slipknot track built as survivalist defiance, warning against parasitic people who isolate and consume anyone weaker than them. The narrator refuses to be possessed or controlled, declaring an end to the predators circling him, and reads the whole thing as scorn for those who feed on a person once they have been diminished.","sonnet":"Predatory people — those who smother, steal, and consume — are identified and refused. The speaker asserts autonomy against every force trying to own or diminish them, framing resistance as the dawn of something new against those who would reduce a once-special person to nothing. The defiance is energised and absolute.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survival hymn about severing yourself from a parasite — a person, a crowd, or the machinery of celebrity that devours whatever it pretends to cherish. The chorus catalogs an appetite that isolates its prey before consuming it, and the speaker answers by denying the thing any claim of possession, casting himself as a start and the leech as a terminus. Late verses weaponize that same devouring logic, turning predator into prey until scorn supplants dread, and the once-special other, reduced to something spent and interchangeable, becomes the warning the title names: what imagined it owned you was always expendable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~sarcastrophe": {"haiku":"A speaker represents destructive judgment and power, condemning hypocrisy and demanding accountability while celebrating violence and obliteration.","opus":"The opener of Slipknot's .5: The Gray Chapter, recorded after bassist Paul Gray died, boils with condemnation and swallowed sorrow, daring a listener to name what they really crave before their time runs out. The group's declaration of being lethal deities worth preserving, urging others to slay whatever destroys them, converts grief into a defiant, doomsday ritual.","sonnet":"Self-appointed kill gods tear down corrupt kings and complacent systems with contemptuous finality. Beneath the grandiose violence runs a darker current — hidden bodies, buried memories, judgment arriving for those who falsified their way through life. The song oscillates between apocalyptic authority and something more privately haunted.","src":"haiku","fable":"Cut in the wake of Paul Gray's death, this is bereavement that refuses to soften into elegy, reforging a band's loss into a creed of retaliation. The chanted self-deification reads less as bravado than as a vow sworn over a coffin: to survive by turning executioner, striking down whatever would strike them first. Verses cross-examine the listener like a courtroom, pressing for a confession or a surrender to nothing, dragging a private grave into open judgment. The refrain twists two ways at once — a pledge of endurance owed the dead braided with a demand that the living perish in tribute, the mourner daring an indifferent world to justify its own continuance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The portmanteau title keeps a sneer over the whole god-pose: read through the sarcasm, the self-deification and the die-for-me benediction play as satire of the grandeur they perform."},
"slipknot~scissors": {"fable":"A narrator dissects himself on a clinic table, splitting his own chest to check whether anything underneath still answers, since nerves alone never convinced him that sensation was real. An early stanza drags him through a poisoned seduction, a venomous partner threading a needle into whatever he can still call a pulse while anesthesia dissolves the last of him. The looping refrain about patience held in reserve reads as a predator's stopwatch, counting toward a moment that could mean a kill or an ending, either one dressed up as a rite. When the closing chant fractures into pleading and self-erasure, the awaited detonation lands not as bloodshed but as the single event capable of registering after everything else went blank.","fableDeep":"The opening is an operating theater staffed by one — five minutes of playing doctor before the self-examination — and what the surgery finds is not pain but its absence, wind and dust where feeling should be. The load-bearing line is the numbness thesis: \"just 'cause I have nerves, don't mean that I can feel,\" anatomy present, sensation gone, with the shrugged corollary that he wasn't much fun to be around anyway. A black widow presides over the drug tableau, offering the candied heart he injects — appetite, poison and seduction fused into one hostess. The long middle is pure fuse: biding my time repeated in fours until the phrase wears through and becomes it's time, a countdown that ends by announcing itself. The final movement turns the numbness outward at an addressee he both needs and dismisses, cycling accusation, a flat repeated death wish, and the desire to see that person laid open before him — described here rather than excerpted — before the hammering declaration that the moment has arrived. Thesis: anesthesia as the injury that makes every other injury possible.","haiku":"Self-harm and numbness; detached from feeling anything despite physical injury.","opus":"A dissociative spiral into total numbness, the narrator opening himself up and inviting harm precisely because he has stopped being able to feel anything at all. Surreal images slide into a chanted longing for death and a sense of the whole world turned against him. Closing Slipknot's 1999 debut, it stages emotional deadness as slow-creeping horror.","sonnet":"Dissociation dressed as surgery — a speaker who opens himself up and feels nothing, waiting patiently for some undefined right moment while surreal images accumulate around him. The song builds toward a confrontation that is also an obliteration: a final address to someone he wants to see undone, followed by an endless, hammering countdown.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~sic": {"fable":"A declaration of war that keeps turning its rage inward as fast as it aims it outward. The speaker taunts an enemy to reveal itself, insists nothing can break him, then trips over his own bravado — a mid-song stammer where he catches himself posturing and can't decide if he's malevolent or just decadent, before the contempt he throws at the enemy's twisted mind curdles into the admission that his and the enemy's minds are the same thing. That collapse is the whole point: the closing mantra promising you can't kill him because he's already inside you drops the pretense of two combatants and names the fight as one that was internal all along, the sickness of the title being the speaker's own. The barked (Sic) that ends it works like the editorial notation — quoted exactly, errors and all, refusing to be corrected — the ugliness insisted on as deliberate, reprinted on purpose.","fableDeep":"The title is the editor's mark — (sic), the error preserved verbatim — and the track honors it by leaving its own derangement uncorrected, even interrupting a threat with \"What the hell am I sayin'?\", a self-audit planted in the middle of a war chant. Address keeps sliding: first an enemy invited to declare what it wants to be, then a jealous onlooker told to press against the glass and suffer as spectator, then the self, dared not to slip before dropping the ball. The verse logic is grievance arithmetic — cause and effect, a burn from which you never learn — but the chorus reduces everything to siege terms: you drag me down, I take you down, this is a war. The pivot is the closing refrain, \"you can't kill me 'cause I'm already inside you\": aggression reconceived as infection, the persecuted thing revealing itself as a parasite the host cannot cut out. Thesis: a declaration of war whose real weapon is incurability — hatred that survives by taking up residence in its target.","haiku":"Primal rage and tribal violence, visceral aggression and hatred.","opus":"An early Slipknot detonation of pure antagonism, daring an enemy to step up while the narrator teeters at the edge of self-control. Rage curdles into war, and the chilling refrain that he already lives inside his target makes the hatred inescapable, almost parasitic. It channels the band's formative fury into a manifesto of unhinged, cornered aggression.","sonnet":"Pure aggression turned inward and outward simultaneously — the narrator doesn't know who they are, dismisses pity, demands walls fall, and tells whoever is watching their decay to press their face against the glass and suffer. \"You can't kill me 'cause I'm already inside you\" converts self-destruction into a kind of hostile permanence.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~skeptic": {"haiku":"Tribute to a charismatic figure lost too soon; grief mixed with anger at their absence.","opus":"An elegy that rages at mortality itself, refusing to let a gone friend fade, listing him as legend, martyr, savior and finest among them while demanding the cosmos quit robbing them of the greatest. Beneath the fury sits plain grief. Slipknot wrote this for late bassist Paul Gray, the anger that of a band mourning one of their own.","sonnet":"A eulogy for someone irreplaceable — a friend, mentor, and impossible force of nature who died too soon and left a wound that can't be processed through normal grief. The speaker is furious rather than peaceful, refusing to let the loss disappear, insisting on keeping the dead man's soul alive through sheer refusal to accept the world without him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tribute to Paul Gray that grieves by heaping incompatible names on one man until the pile itself admits no label can contain him. The eulogy keeps catching on the flaw folded into every blessing, wanting to pin fault on someone guiltier while conceding the accused was really the wounded party, so rage and sorrow refuse to separate. Even the boast that no equal will follow reads less as canonization than as a bargain against vanishing, the vow to preserve his memory standing as the single wonder this mourner permits himself to believe.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~skin-ticket": {"haiku":"The speaker feels trapped and hollowed by dependence on others' empathy to stay alive, asking to be freed from this parasitic emotional cycle.","opus":"A claustrophobic self-portrait of a man reduced to nothing, kept alive only by feeding on others' empathy, the cage-and-migraine imagery collapsing into a plea to be left alone with his own emptiness.","sonnet":"A song about existential emptiness and parasitic dependence — the narrator exists in a zero-sum void of pain and numbness, sustained only by the empathy of others, desperate for release, trapped in a cage that is also his own skull.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is the thesis: a skin ticket is a body spent like a stub at a turnstile, a self that only counts as admission into someone else's regard. The verses run the arithmetic of a person shrinking toward null — zero summed with zero, the interior subtracted, the circle subtracted — until identity is cancelled down to nothing even as it insists on sprinting nowhere at speed. The hook turns that erasure into a bitter invitation to a self-grown prison, and the swap of grain for migraine says the cage is both a thing bred into the flesh and a throb behind the eyes, structural and neurological at once. The coda is the real turn: the speaker confesses he stays breathing only by drawing on another's pity, repeating the leech's arithmetic until parasitism is naked, while the paired demands for release and solitude betray the trap, since he cannot detach from the host whose sympathy he despises yet requires. When the line finally strips to a bare pulse of persistence, chanted past sense, the meaning collapses into pure mechanism — a survival reflex with its object worn off, which is what a life spent as someone's admission stub finally sounds like.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~snap-demo": {"haiku":"Repeated emotional strain pushes someone toward violent breakdown; they demand what belongs to them and sever false friendships.","opus":"A raw outburst from someone at his breaking point, hammering the warning that a single provocation more will send him over the edge, an ultimatum of barely-leashed rage. Underneath runs a friendship soured into violence, the narrator claiming he only wants what belongs to him while insisting his former ally is nothing to him, confronting the capacity for murder he sees in his own reflection.","sonnet":"Rage accumulated past the breaking point — someone who has been pushed and antagonized to the edge of violence, insisting on what's theirs and declaring anyone who kept crossing that line is no longer a friend.","src":"haiku","fable":"A threat counted down rather than argued: the title word is chanted dozens of times until the promise to snap becomes the whole event, the tension performing itself instead of describing a grievance. What little narrative surfaces is a poisoned friendship — a betrayal by someone who is no friend of mine and a possessive demand for what is mine — but it stays fragmentary on purpose, because the point is the fraying, not the story. It is less a song than a loop of accumulating pressure, where the repetition is the meaning and the final snap is simply the count reaching zero.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~snuff": {"haiku":"An abusive relationship where love and control blur; speaker too damaged to accept care.","opus":"A rare stripped-back ballad about a love reduced to cinders, the singer begging to be set free because his heart has gone too cold to feel and he can't wreck something already vanished. Betrayal and buried affection coexist as he swears to stop listening to the pleading. Corey Taylor cut this as the aching, guitar-driven outlier on the band's 2008 record.","sonnet":"A breakup held together by damage — the speaker loves too darkly to be safe for anyone, and letting go is an act of protection rather than indifference. Tenderness survives in old letters still pressed to the lips, but the other person gave up when the fight got hard, and now the only mercy left is mutual silence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A ballad that keeps confessing devotion only to flinch and retract it, so the song runs on the friction between tenderness and the hardened rubble left beneath it. The speaker calls affection a mask worn over fury, then arms his own numbness as armor: nothing inside can be broken because nothing remains, and solitude feels safer than closeness sharp enough to curdle into loathing. Its cruelty is grief turned outward — an intimate who bartered him away for their own rescue, the longing to demote intimacy to strangers so he'd be licensed to wound, comfort exposed as a soothing manipulation dressed up as mercy. The repeated demand never to learn whether they still care is the crux: not coldness but a final barricade thrown up by someone certain any surviving feeling would finish him, who would sooner entomb it than endure it once more.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~solway-firth": {"haiku":"Betrayal and scars accumulate while learning that survival requires cutting out those who perpetuate pain and reclaiming autonomy through severance.","opus":"A raw reckoning with betrayal and survival, the narrator surrounded by predators picking him apart, confessing he built his life on a lie and choosing to excise a toxic person and torch his regrets to break free, admitting joy has been gone for years. Slipknot closed their 2019 album with this, Corey Taylor's anguish marking their late-career emotional weight.","sonnet":"Betrayal by community and intimate alike — surrounded by people who picked at his remains like scavengers, the speaker was taught to die by the very relationship that was supposed to sustain him. The resolution is not reconciliation but severance: cut them out, burn the ships, stop performing a smile that was never real, and find some way to breathe without needing any of it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Betrayal is staged here as a coroner's scene: the speaker perches above a crowd of scavengers who rifle a corpse while feigning sorrow, and the person he indicts robbed him the same way while he still breathed. The refrain returns to an anomaly no ledger can file, a presence nobody will vouch for, and folds it into the narrator himself, a man who once rehearsed a counterfeit grin so he would not register as broken and who now declines to perform healing for a spectator's ease. Mirrored couplets carry the turn: guilt first swallowed inward as a stranger's fault gives way to a naked verdict pinned squarely on the addressee, the courtesy mask torn off. An apprenticeship in staying alive curdles into tutoring for the grave, and the martial gesture of torching one's own fleet — the tactic Cortés reputedly used to strand his men and forbid retreat — becomes the lone freedom on offer: excising the betrayer is bare endurance, chosen by someone who already grasps there is no threshold left to cross back over.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~spiders": {"haiku":"Social predators and false connections that trap and consume you; isolation as self-preservation.","opus":"Built on an eerie piano loop, this stranger, more experimental Slipknot cut broods on secrets, predation and who gets chosen or cast off, arrivals in ominous pairs deciding who survives. Exhausted by overthinking and by masking his true self, the narrator drifts between paranoia and confession as the band stretch past sheer aggression into something genuinely creeping.","sonnet":"Predation as social metaphor — everyone moves in pairs through a world that sorts people into food and waste, and the speaker is exhausted by the overthinking and secret-keeping and bitter limitation of it all. The only exit is to stop trying to be a martyr and just make yourself at home in the mess everyone shares.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study of social predation staged as insect arithmetic: the arachnids arrive in pairs and marching ranks, and the chorus asks only which of us becomes prey and which gets discarded, a caste system offering no third exit. Against Slipknot's usual roar the arrangement stays restrained, a stalking waltz that keeps the menace procedural rather than bloody, while the verses turn the hunt inward toward secrets hoarded like manuscripts torched before any eyes reach them, a self sewn shut against an audience unfit to meet its unmasked truth. Cruelest is the accounting that folds spite into a baseline human constant, so the parting offer to shed one's poison and settle in feels less like rescue than another snare, where compassion and self-sacrifice become fresh routes toward being devoured.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~spit-it-out": {"fable":"A courtroom framing opens it — the verdict already handed down before the fight begins — and everything after is the sentence being carried out, a battle-rap torrent of threat and contempt aimed at scene rivals, backstabbers, and hangers-on. The debut-era Slipknot channels Iowa outsider fury through Corey Taylor's rapid trash-talk cadence, and the two demands that anchor the chorus (drag me down versus stamp you out) reduce the whole feud to a zero-sum reflex where the only postures available are being crushed or crushing first. But the bridge tips the mask: after all the naming of enemies, the eight-times-hammered admission of being all out of enemies turns the aggression inside out, revealing that the appetite for a target outlasts any actual target — the rage was never really about them. The title command doubles as the trap: spit it out is both taunt and dare, insisting the other party confess while guaranteeing that whatever they say becomes the pretext for the beating.","fableDeep":"The trial is rigged from the top — \"the verdict is guilty\" arrives in the opening stanza, before any charges are read — and everything after is sentencing. The chorus is perfectly symmetrical warfare: all you want is to drag me down, all I want is to stamp you out, two reductions grinding against each other. The verses escalate by register: playground taunting and tabloid detail — rumor fodder, little spies, the ass being kissed — sliding into a second verse whose threat inventory reaches a firearm pressed to a skull, delivered in the same gleeful patter as the insults, menace and comedy refusing to separate. Under the bravado sits scene politics: crews, fools, payoffs, the sneer about where you'll be in five years — a local grudge inflated to apocalypse. The last twist deflates it all: the cry of being fresh out of enemies, hostility revealed as a fuel source that can actually run dry. Thesis: a diss track about how rage needs opponents the way a fire needs air.","haiku":"Confrontation with backstabbing and duplicitous people, framed as justified retaliation against those spreading falsehoods and seeking to undermine.","opus":"A furious confrontation with an enemy the narrator accuses of spreading rumors, backstabbing, and trying to drag him down. He answers every insult with threats, vowing to stamp the other person out.","sonnet":"An all-out verbal assault on a backstabbing rival who spreads rumours and talks trash, with the narrator demanding the antagonist either face him directly or shut up.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"rage that outlasts every target, the appetite for enemies surviving them all","built":"all out of enemies hammered eight times until aggression turns itself inside out"},
"slipknot~spit-it-out-hyper-version":{"haiku":"Rage and disgust erupt as the speaker convulses with everything they've swallowed.","opus":"A relentless verbal assault answering rumor and betrayal with escalating threats and swagger. The narrator sets the record straight against backstabbers and hangers-on, spitting venom at anyone trying to drag him down while vowing to stamp them out. A hyper-charged take on Slipknot's breakout track, it weaponizes contempt into a manic, machine-gun catharsis of settled scores.","sonnet":"Relentless aggression directed at a backstabbing social enemy — the kind who spreads rumor, hides behind politics, and has no real pull or power. The narrator has run out of enemies and finds the prospect almost disappointing, the whole thing a cathartic ejection of every fink and coward who tried to drag them down.","src":"haiku","fable":"A courtroom-verdict opening frames this as retribution, but the song is really one unbroken tantrum of threat, its hook splitting the world into two verbs — the foe dragging down, the self stamping flat — with no third possibility. What keeps it from empty tough-guy noise is that the aggression eats itself: the mantra arrives at a point where no foes remain, victory indistinguishable from having nothing left to point at, and Corey Taylor caps it by fumbling that line into a pun on enemas — the mask of menace slipping into a self-punchline that admits the whole rant was as much purge as attack. The Hyper Version's sped-up churn only tightens the loop, turning the closing chant of the title into a body finally spitting out what it could never swallow.","fv":2,"fnote":"Press has long tied it to the band’s feud with Des Moines rock radio — Lazer 103.3’s mudslinging in the early days — a beef the station ended by putting the song in rotation; the band never named names.","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"aggression chasing its own logic until nothing remains to point at","built":"the mantra fumbles into an enema pun — the mask slipping into self-punchline"},
"slipknot~spit-it-out-stamp-you-out-mix": {"haiku":"Aggression toward detractors escalates into stamping out opposition with brutal finality. The speaker drowns passive aggression in contempt and violent rhetoric.","opus":"A pure venom-spitting takedown of a rumor-spreading enemy, meeting every insult with the vow to stamp them out while they try to drag him down. The exhausted final joke, all out of enemies, shows the aggression is really about a man drowning in adversaries and swinging back at all of them.","sonnet":"A full-throated confrontation with a backstabbing rival — rumour-spreading, side-swiping, no pull or power — the speaker's rage builds through scatological and violent language toward a cathartic endpoint: 'fuck me, I'm all out of enemies', implying the fight has consumed everyone around them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rap-metal revenge aria that convicts a two-faced rumor-monger before any case is made, then locks both parties into a mirrored death-grip where his only aim is humiliation and mine annihilation. The escalation is the point and the flaw: threats pile up so cartoonishly that a single swapped syllable near the end punctures the machismo, exposing all the bile as posturing feeding on itself. Underneath the profanity sits paranoia and wounded pride, worn so hard the narrator can no longer separate menace from farce.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~sulfur": {"haiku":"A sinful person accepts their guilt and shame as permanent, knowing they will suffer forever but finding ways to survive and persist.","opus":"Corey Taylor's obstinate creed of extreme self-reliance, his sense of blame and disgrace worn down, deities cast off, choosing isolation and endurance over any belonging, defiant even while admitting he'll pay a lifelong price for it. A survival anthem built on going it alone.","sonnet":"A song about living with guilt, moral superiority, and the toxic feeling of something rotting inside — the narrator is a sinner who believes he is right, stays outside the community by choice, and will laugh when finally buried.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study of the poisonous conviction that keeps a man moving even as he suspects he is the rot at the center of his own life. The verses run a private tribunal: present-tense dread while he waits out an unbearable stretch, praying to be wrong, then past-tense relief once the verdict lands and he decides the sentence confirms him rather than condemns him. He answers the world's contempt by claiming both roles at once, damned in the crowd's eyes yet a prophet to a chosen few, arrogant and certain that being despised proves him right. Corey Taylor makes the chorus the toxin itself: refusing to flee, standing his ground, is described as filling the lungs with a burning yellow vapor that scalds while it sustains him. The will he calls sacred and defiled in one breath is simultaneously the armor letting him grin at his own grave and the corrosive fume slowly hollowing him out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The damnation vocabulary — sinner, false gods, sage — keys the title to hellfire as much as to toxic vapor: brimstone, the sulfur of the pit, is what staying costs."},
"slipknot~surfacing": {"fable":"The refusal here is a transparent one: an escalation with nothing behind it but the escalation. The verses build a psychology of being watched, attention and momentum both wearing the narrator down, the self shapeless and picked at beneath the skin. The chorus answers that exposure the only way it can, carpet-bombing every outside claim on him with the same four-letter word until the no becomes an identity. The bridge is where the mask turns: recoil reframed as leverage, the narrator claiming to be the disease the listener only pretends to be, insisting he is the hand at their back that jerks them into motion. Read straight it's adolescent nihilism; read as a mask, it's a kid who decides that if hatred is all he can earn, he'll arm himself with it, the looping chorus a wall raised precisely because a raw nerve sits on the other side.","fableDeep":"The verses locate the injury precisely: not poverty or violence but observation — something working over and under the skin, \"all this attention is doing me in,\" scrutiny and momentum named as the twin pressures grinding him down, an early flinch at an audience the band had only begun to acquire. The chorus answers with identity by subtraction, negations stacked — \"don't belong, don't exist,\" no judgment tolerated — a self defined entirely by what it refuses, which is both armor and a kind of vanishing. The bridge is where the posture flips from defense to primacy: after the taunts at someone living in their own hate, the double claim \"I am the very disease you pretend to be\" and \"I am the push that makes you move\" — the outcast reframed as the authentic article in a world of imitators, and as the hidden engine of the very culture gawking at him. The single spat command — burn — closes it like a match dropped on the way out. Thesis: an anthem that converts being watched into being necessary.","haiku":"A person drowning in self-contempt and external judgment rejects societal pressure and declares themselves the embodiment of the very impulse that drives and changes others.","opus":"Pure defiant rage against judgment and belonging, the young band's alienation weaponized into a refusal to be defined by anyone. The venom is self-assertion, becoming the very thing the world fears and pretends to be.","sonnet":"Pure unfiltered fury directed at the world, at being judged, at the pressure of scrutiny — the narrator has hit a wall and explodes outward, rejecting every standard and claim others make on him, framing himself as the force that drives the world even as that world tries to contain and define him.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~tattered-torn": {"fable":"The song is built as a stutter: the title phrase jams itself between every other line so that no thought can complete without being interrupted by the same wound, a formal enactment of a mind that keeps snagging on its own damage. What accumulates in those broken half-lines is a self-diagnosis reaching for grand words to name its pain (medieval, cerebral, melodramatic) and then, in the same breath, mocking them as overwrought. The voice is at once the sufferer and the sneering witness to its own suffering. The verses spiral from vague ache toward self-harm rendered as instruction rather than despair, and the final mantra names what the whole engine has been doing all along: this is not being destroyed from outside but the deliberate work of tearing oneself away from whatever hurts, unable to tell amputation apart from mutilation.","fableDeep":"The form is the meaning: the title phrase lands between every single line, an ostinato that works both as diagnosis — a psyche in shreds — and as suture, the only thing holding the fragments in sequence. The images inside the gaps describe a burning interior with no exits: aches, slithering things, floors going down in flames, no window to be found. The self-descriptions are oddly clinical, one adjective at a time — \"medieval,\" \"cerebral,\" \"suffocated\" — capped by \"melodramatic,\" the sufferer auditing his own performance mid-collapse. The darkest couplet reframes self-cutting as education, wounds requested the way a student requests lessons, and the severed nerves are pitched, horribly, as serving one better — mutilation rationalized as optimization. The closing chant repeats the act of tearing himself apart, but the preposition is the escape hatch: apart from the things that make him hurt — destruction aimed at separation, the self amputated away from its pain sources. Thesis: a litany where self-destruction is presented, with terrible sincerity, as a method of self-rescue.","haiku":"The speaker is fractured and suffocating from internal conflict, repeatedly tearing themselves apart from the pain that defines them.","opus":"An early Slipknot cut hammers a single tattered refrain against a mind coming apart, the ground ablaze beneath him with no way out. By turns medieval, cerebral and airless, the narrator turns himself into his own foe, cuts his own wrists and begs for instruction, the closing chant of ripping himself away from whatever wounds him laying bare pure self-directed torment.","sonnet":"A mind and body shredded by internal torment — medieval, suffocating, cerebral in its pain — cannot find an exit or a window. The speaker tears himself away from everything causing hurt in a frantic, repetitive loop, the destruction turning inward until the wrists open and the lesson is finally learned.","src":"haiku","fableAlt":"Two words repeated between every line of a slow interior collapse — aching, slithering, floors alight, exits missing — until the phrase becomes the seam holding the speaker together. Damage is requested like coursework, and the ending chant rips the self away from everything that hurts it, whatever the removal costs.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~the-blister-exists": {"haiku":"An exploration of internal damage and numbness; the singer grapples with feeling like a disposable statistic, emotionally numb despite craving sensation. They claim ownership of damage while questioning what they are beneath compliance.","opus":"A meditation on numbness, control, and feeling like an interchangeable statistic, the singer desperate to feel anything at all. He repeatedly calls himself the damaged one, defined by a lifetime of damage done.","sonnet":"A speaker describes feeling like a hollow, damaged statistic—controlled yet compliant, unable to feel genuine sensation, and defined entirely by accumulated harm. The song circles around the question of whether this damaged identity is all that remains.","src":"haiku","fable":"The thesis is identity computed as arithmetic: a man who insists he is everything, then discovers the sum equals a lone integer that refuses to match anyone else's, both governing and obeying in the same breath. Numbness runs underneath the chant — nerve endings so deadened that the speaker begs for any sensation to prove he still registers, a body demanding proof it can be reached at all. Tribal percussion drives a catalogue of erosion: knowledge soaked up without meaning, the moment forever slipping so that no lesson survives its own arrival, the person thinning toward a filing-cabinet figure. The title supplies the one image the lyric withholds: a raw sore kept open by design, worn like a badge, insisting the injury itself is the only continuous fact of a life that otherwise won't cohere.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~the-burden": {"haiku":"Psychological weight and spiritual exhaustion overwhelm the narrator; they're trapped between self-destruction and the burden of existence, seeking witness to their suffering.","opus":"A confession of terror, self-disgust and spiritual collapse, curled up hiding from a hell of one's own making; the plea to a witness who can't save him turns private breakdown into a demand that someone at least acknowledge the suffering.","sonnet":"A speaker crushed by inner terror and self-destructive shame acknowledges they are beyond saving, asking only for a witness to stay present and testify to the consequences of their deterioration rather than abandoning them in the darkness.","src":"haiku","fable":"Grief here is not a wound that mends but a load the survivor keeps carrying long after the funeral, and the title names that permanent weight precisely. The narrator collapses into a fetal knot, trembling and sheltering from a private inferno he confesses to building himself, so mourning sours into culpability: the horror is less that someone was lost than that he lives on and keeps gambling on continuing. The tormented final refrain trades I for we, transferring the dead man's agony wholesale onto those left behind, then dissolves into a spent verdict that everyone has borne all they can, one word hammered flat until it becomes the last thing worth uttering.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The chorus is not only weight but a demand to be witnessed — the plea that the collapse be kept, proven, its consequences told to them: a man wanting his ruin on the record."},
"slipknot~the-chapeltown-rag": {"haiku":"Society drowning in manufactured online truth-telling and viral attention-seeking. Real suffering disappears under the weight of performance and scandal.","opus":"A frenzied takedown of the internet era, where attention is worshipped and reality drowns beneath metrics, spin and manufactured outrage. Truth gets buried in noise while horror multiplies for clicks. Slipknot released this 2021 single as a media critique, Corey Taylor barking a tabloid hook to mock a culture addicted to its own alarm and detached from what's real.","sonnet":"An indictment of the information ecosystem that has replaced truth with engagement — god is everywhere online and therefore nowhere, scandals are currency, and nobody wants proof when a number will do. The song escalates from media critique to something more violent and personal: the speaker feels weaponized, material to be stitched into the noise, afraid of never being understood as anything other than content.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rag was the cheap penny broadsheet peddling scandal, and Corey Taylor pins the online age to that shabby lineage: outrage screamed at a mob that only skims the story it was already coaxed to crave. The song runs the mechanics of a poisoned timeline, where contagion travels top-down through a doomscroll, a preening authority talks into its own returning noise, and a headcount matters more than any evidence anyone might check. Its surgical violence, the scalpel that becomes a scalping, casts coverage itself as a predator that must butcher a fresh subject before the facts can be weighed. Beneath the din runs the drowned admission: a surrendered claim to stay quiet, an interior scraped emptier with every plunge downward into the machine. The closing cascade of nouns dissolves a person into their own bulletin, craving a face the feed can index while dreading that recognition will never truly land, until the hook lands its verdict that if every voice is crowned divine, then nothing sacred survives at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Taylor wrote the lyric after a documentary on the Yorkshire Ripper, struck that the coverage tried the victims' morality rather than the killer — the Chapeltown of the title is the Leeds district of that case, and rag is the tabloid that sold it."},
"slipknot~the-devil-in-i": {"haiku":"The narrator exposes hidden inner darkness and rage, rejecting being blamed for chaos while acknowledging their own internal corruption.","opus":"A reckoning with a betrayer, the narrator unchaining hidden rage and declaring he is no longer that person's scapegoat devil; the deeper turn is self-recognition, seeing the devil in himself while exposing the other's masks and lies, refusing to be blamed any longer.","sonnet":"The narrator confronts someone who projected their own evil onto them, using them as a scapegoat — they're done playing the devil in someone else's story, and the reckoning for that betrayal is coming.","src":"haiku","fable":"An address to a friend turned adversary, spoken by a man who has decided to stop being cast as the villain in someone else's story. The title's trick is the pronoun: not the devil in you but the devil in I, the rage owned and internalized rather than projected outward, and the invitation to cross the threshold and look tracks a hardening from confession toward release as the speaker keeps arriving at the same verdict he is no longer anyone's monster. That reversal doubles as accusation, since the one being summoned closer is the real betrayer whose post has been deserted and whose turning on everyone should have left them scorched, so the mask is worn deliberately to hold up a mirror. Coming on .5: The Gray Chapter, the band's first record after Paul Gray's death and a period of internal fracture, it reads as a settling of accounts dressed as damnation, grief and grievance transmuted into the cold certainty of the outlived over the outlasted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The grievance may be jointly owned: the lyric leaves undecided who took whom for granted and trades the lie for leverage both ways — the clean accuser-and-betrayer split is one reading of a ruin the text keeps mutual."},
"slipknot~the-heretic-anthem": {"haiku":"The speaker claims outsider status (666 vs 555) and mocks those who demand conformity while revealing empty promises behind industry worship.","opus":"Slipknot weaponizing outsider status, casting themselves as the six to the world's obedient five, a middle finger at industry, hypocrites, and anyone who mistakes them for a marketable brand rather than a threat.","sonnet":"The speaker claims the outsider, heretic identity that the mainstream has tried to define for them, turning that label into a badge while calling out fans and the industry for idolizing and then failing to recognize the real person.","src":"haiku","fable":"Slipknot seize the demon a scandalized culture invents to feel righteous, wearing every accusation — pop-star pariah, idol, ruin of a wholesome business — as a trophy the accusers themselves engraved. The taunting numeric refrain reduces damnation to arithmetic: the speaker is exactly one increment more damned than whoever points, so wickedness becomes a coordinate fixed by the pointer rather than any inner rot he actually carries. Beneath the carnival snarl the writing turns surgical, holding contempt for a mob that slanders from a safe distance yet falls mute in close quarters, admiring from afar the very monster it built. The second countdown swaps targets, aiming past the moralists at a rival entertainer rehearsing torment on cue, dutiful to instructions, who confused ambition for the real thing. Read straight through, the profanity is a held-up mirror, and the single crime this narrator refuses to own is manufactured, order-following fakeness dressed as suffering.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~the-nameless": {"haiku":"An obsessive person demands total control and possession of someone, using manipulation and coercion disguised as love while condemning their victim's autonomy.","opus":"Slipknot dramatize possessive obsession as a chant of total domination, a narrator whose only real passion is contempt for the person he claims. He demands worship and absolute obedience, offering an ultimatum, submit completely or forfeit any freedom, reducing his target to property and vowing they'll be his forever.","sonnet":"Obsessive possession articulates itself in escalating demands — obey, worship, belong entirely — until the admission surfaces that what was loved most was the act of hurting. The relationship described is a cage where control masquerades as devotion and the exit is never permitted.","src":"haiku","fable":"A courtship pitched in the grammar of a hostage negotiation, where affection and menace refuse to separate because the speaker experiences them as a single appetite, and the tenderest vow arrives welded to an admission that cruelty was the one attachment he could sustain. Devotion here is indistinguishable from custody: the choice offered is captivity or nothing, and it ratchets upward until survival itself becomes a debt owed to him, the liturgy of submission stripped down to plain command. The victim is denied a name on purpose, granted no interior beyond his insistence that he already possesses their whole identity. Sung without irony it passes for romance, and the terror is that the mask never slips, since for this narrator wanting and controlling were never two impulses but one.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~the-negative-one": {"haiku":"The speaker opposes conformist authority figures, rejecting their hypocrisy and judgment while calling out the speaker's own complicity in the same systems. Mutual blame and resentment dominate.","opus":"Slipknot open their album after bassist Paul Gray's death by taunting a rival cast as a fallen angel of deceit, positioning the narrator as the unapologetic contrarian. Fueled by jitters, paranoia and a sense of being hunted, Corey Taylor wishes his enemy's whole world ablaze, the venom sharpened by the band's grief and turmoil.","sonnet":"A figure who refuses apology or allegiance positions himself as the only honest alternative to a world run on manipulation and self-delusion. The confrontation is personal and cosmic at once — a binary choice between corruption and the negative one, with the promise of watching everything burn.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grudge inflates itself into scripture: the singer recasts a fallen partner as Lucifer expelled from heaven, so a bruised alliance borrows the grandeur of a cosmic exile rather than admitting to an ordinary betrayal. The armor keeps cracking into confession, because the accusing finger swings back on the one who raised it and every charge of selfishness fits the accuser too. His parting hex — that the traitor, dying amid their own wreckage, will recognize whose eyes are watching — comes from a man who elsewhere concedes he is no cleaner, denying his own violence in the same breath. What binds the two enemies is symmetry, not opposition, and the clinical litany of lesions and systemic rot suggests the quarrel has festered past anyone's steering. The interpretation lands not on blame but on a wounded ego staging its feud as a war between angels while suspecting the damnation runs both directions.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~the-one-that-kills-the-least": {"haiku":"Moral ambiguity and isolation; damned no matter which choice you make.","opus":"A bleak reflection on mutual guilt, the narrator equating love with hatred and deciding that whoever does the smallest harm still destroys everyone. He would rather keep his own private inferno, hoarding his demons while inviting others to despise him, watching nobody make it out. From the 2022 record The End, So Far, it eases into melody while keeping the nihilism intact, a cynical verdict delivered almost gently.","sonnet":"A nihilist ledger — everyone survives less well than they believe, and the most moderate harm still harms completely. The song argues from a cell-like isolation that choosing between options is pointless when every option ends the same way, and closes on the bleak equation: even the gentlest killer kills us all.","src":"haiku","fable":"Written in the shadow of Paul Gray's death, this is survivor's guilt dressed as a rigged television contest the narrator both hosts and fails, welcoming the players before sentencing himself to a solitary cell no verdict can open. Its cruelest trick is arithmetic: he pleads guilty not to the largest harm but the tiniest, and the chorus insists the gentlest blow still leaves a whole room dead. He would rather burn privately than accept forgiveness, spurning grace where he swears no theft occurred, folding tenderness and loathing into one gesture until sorrow hardens into a punishment he refuses to appeal. When he tells rivals to nurse their own inner monsters and asks to be despised, the terror is a doom scrawled across plaster that nobody in the room looks at, grasped only behind shut lids — and he simply stays.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~the-opium-of-the-people": {"haiku":"Institutional sedation pacifies populations through distraction and false hope; breaking free demands total commitment while facing universal complicity.","opus":"Faith rendered as a drug, the title lifted from Marx, belief reduced to happenstance and holy ritual turned to blasphemy come the reckoning. The narrator's flat refusal to surrender one more spirit stands as defiance against a numbing, tinkering god that demands everything.","sonnet":"About being overwhelmed and overstimulated to the point of breakdown — running out of ways to cope, watching others fall into idiocy and mass panic, and refusing to sacrifice any more of themselves to the collective hysteria or to faith that amounts to sacrilege. The narrator will not give their soul or hope to whatever system — religious, political, social — is demanding it, even as their own nails are tight in their wrists.","src":"haiku","fable":"Taking Marx at his word, the track makes belief the narcotic and stages a single voice caught between a user hunting the next hit and a worshipper whose nerve has finally cracked under judgment. The chant that only the whole dose will do, twinned with the bargaining request to shave a fraction from the pile, fits the junkie and the devotee identically, both rationing a substance that no longer steadies them. Sharpest in the bridge, ritual curdles into blasphemy and faith gets diagnosed as pure chance, the god downgraded to a botched experiment while spikes through the wrists cast the speaker as the crucified specimen of a divinity he distrusts. The final refusal to surrender a spirit, a life, a thought, or the last of his hope reads as detox and apostasy fused, each withdrawn item a finger pried loose from whatever he was still gripping.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~the-shape": {"haiku":"A person is broken and thrown away, lost without direction while contorting into undone shapes; they reject propaganda and confront fragmentation.","opus":"A song about coming apart at the seams, the narrator too shattered to remain with someone yet unwilling to die alongside them, ready to surrender everything he is. A spoken-word passage tumbles through disorientation and self-erasure, the self experienced as something used up and cast aside like a broken form.","sonnet":"A song about total psychological fracture and abandonment — the narrator is broken and discarded, separated from any sense of self or direction, surrounded by noise and propaganda that mean nothing, and unable to reconstruct what fell apart.","src":"haiku","fable":"A portrait of a self worn down to its outline by a bond that has curdled, where leaving and remaining feel equally poisoned and the singer keeps volunteering to be emptied out entirely, as if surrender were the only mercy on offer. The paired impossibilities of the verses lock him inside a doorless room: too wrecked to hold on, too numb to bolt, too repulsed to strike a blow, too debased to fall on his own sword for the other. When he begs to have it all lifted from his hands and carried off, the request reads less as sacrifice than as a longing to hand over the last of himself and be done. The bridge exposes the true engine of the piece — a psyche audibly unspooling, replaying life in reverse, ordered to answer a riddle that admits no answer, buckling and warping until he cannot recognize what remains. Reduced to noise and ash in the aftermath, he finally sheds the second person and names the ruin as his own alone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~this-cold-black": {"haiku":"The speaker describes themselves as a war machine born from trauma, having the right to destroy themselves and others. They embody the debris and hypocrisies they've witnessed, rejecting human use and mercy.","opus":"A defiant survivor's tirade from a scarred, war-machine psyche shaped by trauma and hate; the narrator, treated as a discarded waste by those who created and rejected him, claims the right to destroy his own, weaponizes his anguish, and clings to being alive as his sole vindication against hypocrites.","sonnet":"A furious, post-traumatic song about being shaped by damage and reaching a breaking point — the narrator has internalized years of war-machine trauma and lashes out at the hypocrites and users who took everything without seeing him, insisting he is now alive and they are not.","src":"haiku","fable":"A fabricated thing narrates its own assembly, cursing the creation that birthed it as both spineless and a whore before admitting it is that creation's disowned output. Built from wreckage and combat-scarred hardware, it turns disgust into a boast, claiming the license to slaughter its own kind and gloating that a creature that should be impossible is nonetheless breathing. The whole circuit locks on the confession about a single ruined mechanism opening only onto itself: no entry, no escape, just the same damaged part serving as both lock and the tool that would free it. That sprung trap is where the taunt hurled at some tired hypocrite recoils onto the speaker, since the marching refrain promises a resumed existence in a cadence so metronomic that endurance reads not as hope but as machinery that has forgotten how to power down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~three-nil": {"haiku":"The speaker grapples with dysfunction and self-loathing, rejecting external systems and expectations while questioning their place and whether life is worth continuing.","opus":"A defiant reckoning with self and expectation in which the narrator renounces his promises, rejects a war and a life he says aren't his, calls himself a glorified malcontent and pariah, and repeatedly says goodbye while insisting he never had to leave.","sonnet":"A person who feels alienated, mocked, and trapped in a life that isn't theirs declares it all null — not his war, not his fight, not his revolution — and says goodbye, recognizing he doesn't need to physically leave to already be gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A furious act of disowning. Corey Taylor keeps cataloguing the figure the crowd mistakes for a savior — the celebrated loudmouth queued for by endless strangers, adored only because he is universally despised — before the chorus rips every borrowed identity loose in a pounding chain of negations. The engine is the chasm between the icon the audience purchases and the person beneath it, so the climax is paradox not departure: he bids farewell without leaving, killing the mask rather than the man. The late call-and-response literalizes it, each dare he flings out answered by his own refusal to be unmade, taunting the horde to try precisely because no label was ever true. Even the wrestling-count title becomes the scoreboard, three to nothing, a bout he has simply chosen to quit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~til-we-die": {"haiku":"Collective trauma binds family through loyalty sworn against dissolution; unity becomes the answer to erasure.","opus":"An unusually warm anthem of chosen family and loyalty, the band swearing to stand together through damage and regret because bonds forged in struggle outrank blood; defiance here is about surviving alongside each other rather than fighting the world.","sonnet":"An oath of loyalty between bandmates and their community — acknowledging shared wounds and regrets but pledging to fight together, resist being forgotten, and carry each other forward until death.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survival pact staged as a brotherhood oath, where a bond too weathered and mutually implicated to curdle into resentment vows to outlast whatever the years extract from it. The verses stay bruised and confessional, cataloguing worn-down friends, damaged trust, and the plea not to fail anyone again, then surrender that private ache to a massed chant that reforges the wound as a shared front line. Its sharpest move redefines kinship as something thicker than shared bloodlines, so the pledge reads not as romance but as the creed of a chosen crew that only exists in the plural. The martial vocabulary is where the sly turn hides: the conflict is declared already won purely by refusing to fracture, which recasts every battle cry as a vow never to disband, triumph reduced to simply remaining here, together, until the end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~unsainted": {"haiku":"A person rejects false salvation and breaks free from spiritual manipulation, refusing to be martyred or corrupted by someone else's agenda.","opus":"Slipknot's comeback single hinges on refusing to destroy oneself for salvation and declining to fall low, finally learning to release rather than clutch. Corey Taylor rounds on manipulative believers whose scripture and stories no longer sway him, rejecting martyrdom forced from outside; the charge that they wrecked his faith becomes hard-won self-liberation.","sonnet":"A man who refuses to martyr himself for someone else's salvation narrative. He has survived a long darkness and is finally releasing his grip on whatever kept him submerged, rejecting the idea that suffering is ennobling and reclaiming agency from the person who killed his sainthood.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor's refusal assembled from inverted religious grammar: the speaker declines the self-destruction some doctrine demands, refuses canonization on cue, and returns every borrowed scripture to its owner, the sacred text that fails on him, the confession that stays illegible. What sounds like a screed against a judgmental other is finally an internal reckoning, the un-sainting cutting both directions: another hand has scrubbed the holy figure out of him, yet he also waves off the halo, clutching release instead of the surrender that piety would price. The vertiginous wordplay — the villain who fights the villain, seeds scattered in tidy sequence, myopic and dystopic swapped like slaps — performs the paralysis of too many minds warring at once, an argument talking itself free of the ancient bargain that pain buys redemption. The wounded charge is aimed as much at the former self who craved to be worthy of ruin as at anyone standing outside.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~vermillion": {"haiku":"Obsessive fixation on an unattainable ideal woman metastasizes into self-destruction; she's a myth that exists only in the narrator's psyche, unrequired and impossible.","opus":"A haunting portrait of obsession with someone who may not truly exist, an unattainable dream stretched across the narrator's shame. He would do anything to possess her, yet admits she is a myth he must invent to survive. From Slipknot's more melodic turn, it dissects the ache of loving an idealized phantom he cannot make real.","sonnet":"An obsession so total it constitutes a mythology: she is the unrequired dream, the unattainable myth the speaker has to believe in. Dressed in all of me, she is both everything and nowhere, and the not knowing what to do when she makes him sad is the lyric's emotional centre.","src":"haiku","fable":"A devotion that studies itself and finds a fiction at the core: the beloved is confessed to be a hunger with no object, a hymn without a congregation, an idol the worshipper knows he invented, yet still demands as an article of faith. The adoration is really self-portraiture — she wears the man's own disgrace like borrowed clothing, his private anguish seeping outward until it coats him — so the ache points back at the self that built her simply to have something to crave. What sounds like composure is a lid clamped over rising pressure: the near-tuneful vow to keep it buried repeats past the point of belief, each cycle proving the containment is already cracking. The closing admission, that no amount of will can press her into flesh, exposes the whole ritual as a man narrating his own collapse while pretending to talk himself back from the edge.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~wait-and-bleed": {"fable":"The chorus works as ritual rather than story: hatred welling up, a stooping to brush leaves from a headstone, a withdrawal into some armored interior where the wound stays hidden and unwitnessed — a closed circuit the song keeps returning to. The verses rupture that circuit with a jolt of waking: eyes lit with copper and flame, hands that will not stop trembling, a person who fails to recognize the figure before them and asks how any of this came to be, disturbed that a part of them enjoys it. The programmed-assassin figure is the hinge, a mind conditioned to inflict harm without ever agreeing to it, culpable for a violence noticed only once done, so the sealed interior reads less as sadness than as dissociation — the dread of watching yourself obey impulses you never chose. A final movement locks it into futility, nothing learned and nothing altered, the ache left running loose forever, then swivels the whole trap outward as a warning aimed at the listener.","fableDeep":"Two temperatures alternate. The chorus is unnervingly serene — kneeling, clearing leaves from a stone, patient withdrawal into a shell — ritual language with a graveside stillness, the title's waiting delivered like a devotion. The verse is the opposite register: waking mid-scene, wiping something off the tile, light too bright, hair on end, \"this is not the way I pictured me\" — and the genuinely frightening line is not the aftermath but the taste for it, \"I wish I didn't like this,\" followed by the unanswerable \"Is it a dream or a memory?\" Agency gets outsourced in the bridge: a victim, a Manchurian candidate, programmed rather than choosing, whose only named sin is making up his mind — the act itself hidden inside a breath-taking euphemism. The coda locks the loop — nothing learned, nothing changed, the pain always free — and the final line turns outward, promising the listener the same wait. Thesis: a horror song about catching your own violence enjoying itself, and not being able to tell which tense it happened in.","haiku":"Rage and self-awareness coexist as the speaker waits in isolation, trapped between violence and numbness.","opus":"Slipknot's breakout single sits inside barely-leashed rage and a fracturing mind, the narrator withdrawing into himself to nurse his fury while a violent, unreal state both repels and seduces him. Casting himself as a helpless victim programmed to do harm, he faces a splintered self where suffering was always the baseline and nothing has really shifted.","sonnet":"A dissociative, self-harming nightmare — the speaker describes a hallucinatory state after cutting, fluctuating between horror and a disturbing pleasure in the pain, while the outer framing of hate and isolation suggests this is both a symptom and a form of control over unbearable internal chaos.","src":"haiku","fableAlt":"Hatred rising on a private schedule, tended like a grave plot — kneeling, clearing leaves, withdrawing into a shell to wait. Then the jolt awake: tile, tremors, unnatural light, the queasy admission of enjoying whatever just happened, and no way to establish whether it was dreamed or actually done.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Taylor's account roots the song in a recurring dream of waking in a bath of one's own blood, and glosses it as the thin switch between the civilized self and the animal that can act at any moment — the self-directed hearing the murmured verses keep half-open."},
"slipknot~welcome": {"haiku":"Generational trauma corrupting youth into monstrous versions of their parents.","opus":"A poisonous address to a parent figure, the narrator calling himself the direct outcome of their behavior and deceit, damning the sham that rendered him worthless before he became everything, a spark molded out of raw earth. Fury and an odd flicker of redemption intertwine as he vows to spare them from his wrongs. A study of inherited harm and generational scorn.","sonnet":"An indictment of parents who shaped their children through lies and neglect, then act surprised by what they created. The speaker is furious but clear-eyed — the damage was deliberate — and transforms that origin into fuel, vowing to break the cycle rather than pass it on, even as the system that made them keeps grinding.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a reckoning aimed at whoever raised the speaker into a weapon, framed so that every accusation of cruelty and neglect rebounds onto the accuser as inherited liability. The cleverness is grammatical: an attempt to erase a child into nothing backfires and instead detonates that child into a totalizing presence, an origin point whose growing hostility is entirely the guardian's harvest. Complicating the fury is a confession threaded through it, the narrator conceding he mistreats the very mouths he feeds and dreads becoming the lineage he despises, so his pledge of deliverance reads at once as menace and as a fated, self-defeating bid to sever the inheritance from within. The greeting in the title works as a blade turned slowly: not welcome as warmth but as an usher into simultaneous rebirth and obliteration, ushering the maker toward the wreckage he himself designed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The late verses slide the addressee — the mouths he feeds turning to bite him reads as a maker turned on by his own dependents, a fanbase-adjacent hearing kin to New Abortion's weaponized young."},
"slipknot~wherein-lies-continue": {"haiku":"Rejecting inherited lies and control; choosing personal salvation through rebellion.","opus":"A generational howl from abandoned kids repudiating handed-down myths, gods and structures, refusing to slot into a machine they detest and insisting they have to rescue themselves before the option vanishes. The refrain embraces total ruin as the truthful conclusion. Slipknot push adolescent rage toward something like nihilist philosophy, where the wish to believe collapses into self-reliance as the last creed standing.","sonnet":"A generational rupture — children declared orphaned by the systems that made them, rejecting both the gods and the myths constructed to keep them in line. The song oscillates between nihilism and defiance, unable to fully believe in anything but also refusing to be owned by the structures it wants destroyed, ending in the naked demand that everyone must save themselves.","src":"haiku","fable":"A manifesto built as an anti-decalogue: a corrupting voice issues its own commandments, urging the young to unseat their elders, manufacture a private religion, and wall off an anointed few, mistaking a counterfeit rescuer for deliverance from dread. The indictment then rounds on the speaker himself, whose hunger to trust something founders against a self-doubt that dismantles every belief, until the arithmetic reduces to one flat verdict of oblivion. What lifts this off the floor of despair is a mid-song pivot toward refusing dominion over what lies beyond comprehension, a hardened figure passing his own means downstream like an heirloom of fire — destruction recast as bequest. The counterfeit election is spurned entirely: he would sooner perish knowing no one manufactured him than persist forever caged in a numbing scaffold he loathes, prizing self-rule over the false sanctuary that opening voice was assembling. The chant hammered past endurance is the confession itself, an abandoned generation drilling itself into resolve precisely because no elder and no rescuer will arrive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~xix": {"haiku":"A song dedicated to the dead, a plea to not give up though you're isolated and weak.","opus":"A short, wounded plea dedicated to the dead, the narrator face-down and knocked flat yet forcing himself to rise, feeling estranged from everyone and warning against the symbolism that could kill a heart. It resolves into a repeated invitation to walk with him against a world bent on tearing people apart. The opening track of Slipknot's 2014 The Gray Chapter, it sets a mournful, elegiac tone for a record shadowed by loss.","sonnet":"A song written for the dead, not the living — the floor pressed against a face that does not want to rise. Grief is stated plainly: nothing appeals, no one feels the same, and yet the only instruction offered is to keep walking, together, the way they should have from the beginning, so the world does not finish what it started.","src":"haiku","fable":"An elegy that pivots mid-grief from the buried to the breathing, refusing to romanticize collapse and instead pressing a hand toward whoever remains. Mourning becomes an instruction: endure alongside someone, since isolation is what finishes the survivor.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slippy~feel-alive":{"haiku":"Visceral consciousness and embodied sensation as antidote to numbness, the speaker reconnecting with physical aliveness and presence.","opus":"An electronic anthem of resistance against numbness and hopelessness, asking what it actually takes to feel truly present. Set amid deafening quiet and grounded inner demons, the choice is to go under or to speak up over the mob. After repeated betrayal it refuses to yield or shatter, framing sheer survival as its own form of vitality.","sonnet":"A call to resist betrayal through refusal to bend or break — the silent night pressing down, demons grinding, but the choice is between drowning the voice out or raising it against the crowd. The repeated 'feel alive' is less euphoric than urgent: a threshold that has to be crossed.","src":"haiku"},
"slough-feg~asteroid-belts":{"haiku":"Navigation through uncharted space is hazardous; outdated information and enemy incursion destroy both pilot and vessel in the vagrant mining frontier.","opus":"A heavy-metal space opera lifted straight from tabletop Traveller lore, plotting a course past a giant planet's sun on obsolete charts with supplies and weapons aboard. It follows a doomed jump through erased sectors into a mineral-rich field where fleets clash and rocks collide, Slough Feg turning sci-fi role-playing into a vivid tale of interstellar peril and a cold crash landing.","sonnet":"A starship navigating jump space with outdated charts enters a mining belt where sandcasters fire and war-heads collide — the whole thing a precise Traveller RPG scenario rendered as headlong hard-SF thrash. Navigation, war, and the chaos of uncharted space are the only subjects.","src":"haiku"},
"slough-feg~warriors-dawn":{"haiku":"Indigenous defiance dances toward sacrifice and death; pride meets inevitable annihilation.","opus":"A doomed Indigenous war party rides out to meet gunfire in the canyons at first light, chanting their kinship with the fallen. The narrator claims a birthright to roam the highlands and die as the season turns, his war-cry still echoing across the wilds.","sonnet":"A first-person account of Native American warriors — \"we are the red men, feathers-in-our-head men\" — describing life on the plains, sacrifice, and death in ritual combat at dawn, told with defiant pride in being born to run in the hills and die at summer's end.","src":"haiku"},
"slow-crush~aid-and-abet":{"opus":"A weary plea to a partner for support through a low point, asking to be picked up again and reassured that tomorrow isn't the end, while both hide their shame and hurt.","sonnet":"A fragmented song about a recurring cycle of crisis and recovery — someone keeps needing to be picked up and carried through the aftermath of collapse, with a complicated mix of shame, co-dependence, and resilience in the relationship.","src":"sonnet"},
"slow-crush~cherry":{"haiku":"Language fragments around wound and bloom. Touch and tending blur into harm and extraction.","opus":"Fragmentary shoegaze impressions of shedding skin and losing thought inside physical intimacy, the desire and self-erasure blurring into hazy sensory dissolution.","sonnet":"A fragmentary, impressionistic song about pain, loss of self, and emotional vulnerability — the imagery of shedding skin, bursting blooms, cutting, and losing thoughts suggests a relationship or inner state unraveling, though the extreme fragmentation resists any single clear narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"slow-crush~covet":{"haiku":"Emotional emptiness without the other; the speaker clings to absence itself, defining their value only through the loss they refuse to release.","opus":"Codependent longing pared to its bones: hollow and cold alone, mellowed only in the other's presence, begging them to keep something safe. Identity here dissolves without the beloved.","sonnet":"A simple, aching devotion — without the other person the narrator feels empty and cold, and all they want is for something precious between them to be kept safe.","src":"haiku"},
"slow-crush~drift":{"haiku":"Helplessness and hopelessness consume someone as dark forces fill them with no one to heal them. They drift away on warmth seeking any relief.","opus":"The Belgian shoegaze band wraps helplessness and consuming darkness in soft, washed-out repetition, urging a numbed mind to let the feeling through rather than forget it away. Slow Crush's blurred guitars turn the two-word hope that hope helps into something fragile, the closing plea to drift off on a warm wave as much surrender as comfort.","sonnet":"Hopelessness fills the body like water with no one present to drain it. The lyric holds the shape of someone barely staying afloat, watching darkness consume them from within, until a single two-word concession — hope helps — arrives like a distant signal before the warm wave carries everything away.","src":"haiku"},
"slow-crush~glow":{"haiku":"A desire to escape reality and share a dream-like, euphoric space with another person, described through fluid imagery of waves and ocean.","opus":"A dreamy invitation to let go of the wide world and escape into the mind, drifting on soft, glowing waves and pleading with someone to hold on.","sonnet":"A hazy reverie about escapism and longing, in which the narrator wants to drift away into a dreamlike state, pulled by ocean-like waves and a desire to be held.","src":"haiku"},
"slow-crush~shallow-breath":{"haiku":"Summer intimacy and touch sustain the speaker, who dreams of close connection but wakes to lies. The relationship offers warmth and sweetness without truth.","opus":"A hazy reverie of yearning, sleep slipping away as the narrator conjures a lover's warm exhale, mouth and hands in an endless summer, convinced nothing tender can exist apart from that one person. The Belgian shoegaze band bury this ache under gauzy, reverb-flooded guitar, the whispered vocals leaving desire half-remembered and just out of grasp.","sonnet":"Longing suspended between sleep and memory — the speaker cannot sleep, held awake by the phantom warmth of someone whose lips and hands and soft lies are still vivid. The imagery stays gauzy and summertime-soft, desire described in breath and proximity rather than arrival, and the underlying current is the wish to be near someone who may not have been entirely honest but felt like everything.","src":"haiku"},
"slow-crush~tremble":{"haiku":"Silenced, hidden, and voiceless through violence and emotional abandonment. The speaker trembles under the weight of unspoken suffering and forgotten wounds.","opus":"A muffled portrait of silenced pain, the narrator held mute and forgotten, weathering concealed cruelty and low murmurs while gentle reassurances do nothing to soothe the hurt, the lone word tremble breaking through like a suppressed shiver. The Belgian shoegaze act sink this tale of being unloved and unheard beneath thick, narcotic guitar fog, letting the shattered lyric drift under the noise as raw feeling.","sonnet":"Silence as abuse — the speaker is held in a state of enforced voicelessness, hidden and forgotten, kept from speaking or being seen. The soothing words offered do nothing because the pain has been made structural, not accidental. The single word tremble is the only motion available inside that enforced stillness, a body registering what the voice cannot say.","src":"haiku"},
"slow-degrade~lead-me-down":{"haiku":"Submission to emotional destruction becomes the only path forward, where leading downward means liberation. The speaker surrenders to the pull toward oblivion.","opus":"Letting go of hope and of a person, asking to be led down and dragged under, life lines severed and every hope burned. The desperate paradox is holding on only in order to finally let go, begging to be bled out and left with fragments of them.","sonnet":"A relationship that has drained rather than sustained the speaker is addressed directly: 'grip my throat and drag me down, sever all the lifelines' — despite recognising the destruction, the speaker cannot release their grip, the final lines contradicting the title's plea by insisting 'I'll never let go'.","src":"haiku"},
"slowdive~alison":{"haiku":"Drifting through lost connection and substance use with someone slipping away.","opus":"Shoegaze tenderness for a chaotic, pill-and-wine lover whose mess still thrills the narrator even as they sink together. It's the hazy pull of loving someone unwell, staying lost beside them rather than reaching shore.","sonnet":"A speaker floats at the edge of Alison's chaotic world — cigarette smoke, pills, a sister who laughs things off — drawn in by the mess and the thrill of it, telling her they are sinking while she keeps insisting she is fine.","src":"haiku"},
"slowdive~sugar-for-the-pill":{"haiku":"Love never prioritized, settling for transient comfort and compromise in relationships.","opus":"A dream-pop meditation on a love winding down, jealousy and resignation dissolving into the way things simply are. Anna rolling away is the quiet fact of it, sweetness offered to make an inevitable loss go down easier.","sonnet":"A love that was never quite first priority corrodes under jealousy — Anna drifts away, the speaker lies in greed and strangeness, and the sugar coating on the pill of acceptance cannot prevent the whole thing from breaking apart.","src":"haiku"},
"slowly-slowly~19":{"haiku":"At nineteen, the speaker and their lover shared an intimate moment they cannot forget; years later, that night defines their identity and regret.","opus":"A memory of being nineteen and drunk in the back of a taxi, where terror and infatuation blur and the speaker half-wants to die inside the moment before it curdles. The refusal to call it over is less denial than a private truth he keeps bending. Slowly Slowly are an Australian indie-punk band whose emotional, wordy songwriting mines exactly this territory, youth remembered with self-loathing tenderness and no clean resolution.","sonnet":"A drunk backseat moment at nineteen that never really ended — two people brushing against something real in a taxi, the narrator still replaying it years later, privately certain it isn't over while knowing full well he shouldn't fall for it again, the whole memory suspended between love and self-protection.","src":"haiku"},
"slowly-slowly~blueprint":{"opus":"A defiant, us-versus-everyone anthem about a friendship that finally feels like belonging, laced with provocation and vice but anchored by real longing. Beneath the swagger sits a plain admission: the narrator would trade the partying and the highs for genuine closeness, because trustworthy people are increasingly scarce.","sonnet":"About finding a relationship that makes alienation bearable — the narrator is deliberately countercultural and difficult, dismissing celebrity culture and shallow social connection, but finding in one specific person a model for living that actually works, a blueprint for being themselves.","src":"sonnet"},
"slowly-slowly~creature-of-habit-pt-2":{"haiku":"A person clings to destructive patterns despite knowing better, performing false emotion while resenting their role as a pitiful habit. Growth feels impossible without external belief.","opus":"The Australian band mocks a self-pitying figure who wallows in fake grief and passively accepts his own stagnation. Slowly Slowly's melodic bite carries the taunting, singsong jabs, before the tone shifts into a raw confession that the narrator refuses to become irrelevant as long as even one person still has faith in him.","sonnet":"A narrator emerges from a relationship built on performance and self-pity, calling out the other person's rehearsed victimhood with dry precision. Beneath the sharp wit is a quieter admission: validation still matters, and surviving the exit does not fully dissolve the need to be believed in.","src":"haiku"},
"slowly-slowly~low":{"opus":"An honest confession of depressive self-sabotage, where the singer admits he loves and needs his lows and asks to be let go into them; he feels out of place and unreal, not part of the picture but merely imitating life, still shrinking at the memory of someone.","sonnet":"The narrator has survived something but is still pulled under by depression when thoughts of a specific person return — they admit they love their lows and need them, feeling displaced from the constellations of other people's lives, always slightly out of the picture.","src":"sonnet"},
"slowly-slowly~melbourne":{"haiku":"The narrator expresses deep longing for home after lockdown, missing familiar neighborhoods, rituals, and sensory details of Melbourne.","opus":"A lockdown-era love letter to a city, aching for the small annoyances that add up to belonging: overpriced coffee, missed trams, a pint at the corner, the MCG lighting up. The homesickness is directed at Melbourne itself, personified as a woman missed and apologized to. Slowly Slowly write with hyper-local Australian specificity, and this catalogue of Flinders Street and Fitzroy turns civic detail into genuine longing.","sonnet":"A lockdown love letter to a city — every mundane inconvenience of Melbourne life, the overpriced coffee and missed trams and sticky pub floors, transformed by absence into something ached for, the song circling Fitzroy and Flinders Street with the longing of someone who only knows what they had once it's gone.","src":"haiku"},
"slowly-slowly~safety-switch":{"opus":"An unsparing look at a couple drifting past the point of repair, the narrator confessing he's emotionally checked out and incapable of real remorse. He asks his partner to install some mechanism to halt them before impact, wanting to commit to fixing things yet aware they've both already turned to ice.","sonnet":"A troubled relationship where the narrator feels marginal and second-guessed — asking the partner to love them as they are, build in a safety switch to stop them before they wreck things, and wondering if apologies and honesty can fill the cracks before the partner goes completely cold.","src":"sonnet"},
"slowly-slowly~ten-leaf-clover":{"haiku":"Two people share an intense but unstable connection; the narrator describes the relationship as both chemical obsession and their refuge, oscillating between desire and numbness in the backseat of cars.","opus":"An Australian band's messy, tender portrait of a relationship that shouldn't work but does, all bad timing and self-sabotage. The impossible ten-leaf clover becomes the emblem of finding rare luck in someone despite your own worst instincts.","sonnet":"A messy, honest love song about being chemically, irrationally attached to someone despite knowing it's probably trouble — the other person's flaws feel perfect up close, and finding them felt like discovering something impossibly lucky.","src":"haiku"},
"slowly-slowly~the-internet":{"opus":"A rejection of hollow online life and its fake friendships, the narrator preferring death to scrolling the internet with someone, and choosing instead the long analog walk home singing together rather than dying alone inside a life you're missing.","sonnet":"An accusation directed at someone consumed by online life to the point of real isolation — the narrator would rather die than spend another hollow evening with someone who cannot connect in person, and the refrain of dying alone hammers the inevitability of that withdrawal.","src":"sonnet"},
"slowthai~doorman":{"haiku":"Social performance and substance use fragment identity; the speaker chases connection that slips away.","opus":"A class-clash night out where a working-class narrator tangles with a posh, privileged girl whose world of Mayfair, finstas and private schools baffles him. Drunk and jealous at the club, he veers between infatuation and contempt for the wealth she embodies.","sonnet":"A chaotic night out with a woman who is completely out of his league and temperamentally incompatible — she's from a different world (Mayfair parents, private Instagram), stubs cigarettes out on his arm, gets drunk and carried out of the club, and he keeps showing up anyway, addicted like nicotine.","src":"haiku"},
"slumberjack~fracture-feat-vera-blue":{"haiku":"A damaged relationship teeters on collapse; both lovers struggle to rebuild trust while acknowledging the chemistry that keeps drawing them back together despite the fractures.","opus":"A relationship cracking apart even as the physical pull holds, the singer aching to rewind to the start while sensing it's already lost. The plea to just let things unfold is really a fear of watching intimacy dissolve.","sonnet":"Two people trying to salvage a strained relationship agree to stop overthinking and let physical closeness do the rebuilding, even as the speaker wonders whether the damage they keep inflicting on each other can really be undone.","src":"haiku"},
"smash-hit-combo~peine-perdue":{"haiku":"A French speaker spirals through failed effort and lost faith, clinging to suffering as inevitability while their pessimism shields them from disillusionment.","opus":"A French nu-metal descent into futility and self-loathing, convinced effort is wasted and clinging to his own misery as identity. Its bleak honesty is the addict's logic, holding onto pain because letting go feels impossible.","sonnet":"A French rap song about the futility of effort and the comfort of pessimism — the narrator has stopped believing in himself or other people, lives in deliberate disorder, anticipates failure before it arrives to avoid disappointment, and cycles through isolation and self-loathing with the bleak acceptance that things probably won't improve.","src":"haiku"},
"smashing-pumpkins~bullet-with-butterfly-wings":{"haiku":"Systemic entrapment and powerless resistance, the world as predatory force draining vitality while the speaker rages with full knowledge of futility.","opus":"Billy Corgan's mid-90s scream of impotent fury, casting the world as a vampire and the self as a caged rat whose rage changes nothing. It voices the alienation of a generation that felt betrayed and trapped, reaching for meaning through fractured faith in being chosen. A defining alt-rock howl about the futility of anger and the conviction that salvation is impossible.","sonnet":"Rage against entrapment — the world is a vampire draining the narrator, secret destroyers hold him to flames, and despite all the fury he is still just a rat in a cage. The Jesus parallel underscores the bitterness: told he's the chosen one, told he's the only one, yet still caged, still unable to be saved.","src":"haiku"},
"smashing-pumpkins~disarm":{"haiku":"Seduction masks malice as the narrator disarms through charm while harboring destructive impulses beneath smiles. The killer within finds match in the other, creating a cycle of mutual psychological violence.","opus":"A wounded reckoning with a lost childhood and the parent who left the singer to wither, disarming that pain with a smile even as it perpetuates itself. The devastating admission that the killer in him is the killer in the other collapses victim and inheritor into one aching lineage.","sonnet":"Childhood violence — a little boy made old before his time by those who left him — echoes forward into adulthood; \"the killer in me is the killer in you\" links the damage done by parents to the speaker's own capacity for harm, and a smile is sent across that wound.","src":"haiku"},
"smashing-pumpkins~glass-and-the-ghost-children": {"haiku":"Transparent fragility and spectral offspring haunt the psyche; ghostly presences of lost futures permeate existence.","opus":"A sprawling, hallucinatory Machina-era piece grappling with the fear that divine intuition and madness are indistinguishable. A spoken confession spirals through doubt about whether the guiding inner voice is God or delusion. Around it, translucent skin and crawling spiders conjure dread and dissociation, mapping Corgan's obsessive terror that self-importance and revelation might both be symptoms of insanity.","sonnet":"A hallucinatory portrait of a woman dissociating in front of a TV — glass migrating under translucent skin, counting spiders as they crawl inside her — framing existence itself as possibly delusional: the spoken-word insert voices the fear that acting on divine intuition and acting on insanity may be indistinguishable, and the final image of her nodding at her own reflection collapses the boundary between self and ghost.","src":"haiku","fable":"MACHINA's ten-minute center, and Corgan builds it in movements that argue with each other. It opens as a communion hymn — a will-to-live chant, DNA straining toward a frequency, everyone praying and everyone scared — the messianic frame the album hangs on Glass, its rock-star protagonist convinced he is being spoken through. Then a spoken breakdown pulls the mask off: the speaker cannot tell whether the thing directing him is heaven, his own gut, or plain madness, and the passage collapses into stammered repetition, self-diagnosis eating itself in real time. The song answers that crisis not with a verdict but by cutting to a burnt-out woman at a television, tuned out and receding from the world, who decides that certainty needs no proof beyond itself and there is nothing further to weigh — the same delusion of significance seen from the outside, in a life going dark. It ends on her counting spiders as they crawl inside her, a mantra repeated past sense, so the track's own final gesture becomes the thing it diagnosed: faith and madness reduced to identical, compulsive repetition, with no way left to tell which one is talking.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","faith & the occult","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"faith and delusion reduced to identical compulsive repetition","built":"spoken self-diagnosis collapses into the same mantra it was diagnosing"},
"smashing-pumpkins~heavy-metal-machine": {"haiku":"Rock becomes religion while humanity serves as metal to be melted. Sacrifice and rust define the cost of artistic devotion.","opus":"Corgan interrogates his own worth inside the rock machine, wondering whether he's real or just product and whether death would sell the records; a martyr's plea to die for rock and roll turns the artist into replaceable metal parts.","sonnet":"A song about rock music as both salvation and sacrifice — the narrator contemplates whether their death would increase the value of their records, frames themselves as the actual metal inside the machine of the music industry, and pleads to die for rock and roll as a form of martyrdom and liberation.","src":"haiku","fable":"Deep in MACHINA, Corgan turns the rock-star persona that runs through the album into a machine and asks whether anything human is left inside it — the opening doubts about whether he has a pulse or is only a projection are a performer wondering if the figure his audience loves could survive contact with the actual man. The chorus does the theme in miniature: the band are the metal fed into the industry's chrome engines, animate parts inside an apparatus, and the rust it inevitably brings is the corrosion of that role over time. From there the song becomes a martyr's prayer, the repeated plea to be martyred for the music blurring self-sacrifice and self-destruction until dying to ransom back the soul and dying to be released from the machine are the same wish. The cruelest turn is the quiet legacy audit — the question of whether the records would still sell once he is dead, whether anyone would notice the absence at all — which exposes the death-wish as vanity and dread braided together. Inside an album about a doomed pop savior, the confession under the mask is that the machine may outlast, and never need, the person driving it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"the death-wish exposed as vanity and dread braided together","built":"the legacy audit — whether records sell once he is gone — turns the martyr's prayer inside out"},
"smashing-pumpkins~i-of-the-mourning": {"haiku":"Solitary grief and survival through small rituals, radio and memory replacing human presence.","opus":"Corgan's portrait of total isolation, the radio the only companion for someone hiding behind caller ID, coming home to die alone and salving old wounds by rereading a lost lover's letters.","sonnet":"About profound isolation — the narrator stays home with the radio for company, peers through curtains at empty streets, reads old letters and cries over faded ink; they're mourning something or someone and using the past to stay alive, waiting for the ghost to come and make it real again.","src":"haiku","fable":"A shut-in addresses his radio as the only voice still willing to speak to him, returning to an empty house to expire alone behind screened calls and drawn curtains — the title's pun turns the survivor into a mourner presiding over his own vigil. But the wake reverses itself midway: dusting off the guitars, reading old letters, he decides instead to raise a toast because he has, against expectation, survived, and the past he summons becomes something to live toward rather than grieve. The outro then wheels around to face us, its repeated demand to know what you would remake dissolving into the radio chant, so the machine that consoled the isolated man becomes the pulpit from which Corgan interrogates the listener — fitting for a MACHINA record built around a rock star broadcasting salvation through the airwaves.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","nostalgia & memory","freedom & escape"],"means":"the wake reverses when the past becomes something to live toward","built":"the title pun casts the survivor as mourner presiding over his own vigil"},
"smashing-pumpkins~raindrops-sunshowers": {"haiku":"Love navigates between opposing forces, the speaker reaches through echoes and obscurity to touch another across the distance rain creates.","opus":"A tender, protective devotion, walking someone between the raindrops of an indifferent world and saving a prayer for them. The warning that no one is really watching over them makes the speaker's quiet, obscured love the only shelter offered.","sonnet":"A gentle, aching devotion — the speaker simply wants to walk alongside someone through ordinary life, sending love out and hoping it returns undistorted, resigned to the fact that rain falls on everyone equally and that the world won't watch over them, but choosing to walk with this person anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"Corgan builds the song on one impossible wish: the same weather comes down on all heads alike, indifferent and universal, yet the speaker wants to walk someone through the downpour untouched, threading the gaps between what lands. That ambition to shield another from a suffering nobody escapes is the whole ache, and the second image-family explains why it fails — every attempt at contact goes out as an echo, comes back as an obscured reflection, a love that must be forced down and kept secret, so tenderness only ever reaches its object as distortion. The single stark turn, wishing the beloved hauled through the filth of open gutters yet tucked into laundered linen like an invalid or a corpse, while warning that no one is really watching, exposes the fantasy of rescue as also a fantasy of being seen, and the answer is that they won't be.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the fantasy of rescue exposed as also a fantasy of being seen","built":"tenderness only reaches its object returned as echo and distortion"},
"smashing-pumpkins~stand-inside-your-love": {"haiku":"Devotion promises transcendence and transformation; the speaker becomes reborn through absolute surrender to love.","opus":"A vow of complete, engulfing devotion, love framed at once as fate and as sheer madness. The singer feels remade within it, coiling himself around the person he adores body and soul in a claim as gentle as it is total.","sonnet":"Total, consuming devotion to another person — the speaker is reborn by this love, willing to travel anywhere and breathe for both of them, worshipping the other as a pure soul while declaring they belong to him now and forever.","src":"haiku","fable":"Corgan opens in the vocabulary of fated romance — the two lovers as something inevitable and beyond calculation — and the first two verses stay inside that adoration, the singer promising to keep the air moving in her lungs and to cross whole continents and skies for a beloved who has folded her whole address into his chest, then declaring himself reborn as both child and sage by her. But the final verse quietly rewrites what devotion means: the language shifts from giving to needing, from shelter to capture, until the tender vow of belonging hardens into an image of coiling a length of wire around everything she feels and everything she thinks, and claiming her as his forever. The refrain that first sounded like helpless wonder — who wouldn't love someone like this — accrues its darker clause across the song, the willingness to spend a life in her name curdling into a willingness to end one, so that by the end the same question reads less as praise than as a lover who cannot imagine her existing apart from him. Read straight it is one of the great MACHINA love songs; read against its own grain it is about how absolute devotion and possession share a border.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"absolute devotion and possession share a border the song quietly crosses","built":"the final verse rewrites the vow by coiling wire around everything she feels and thinks"},
"smashing-pumpkins~the-everlasting-gaze":{"haiku":"Yearning persists beyond rejection and abandonment, the speaker insisting on existence within the beloved's consciousness even as the world worships false gods in rapture.","opus":"A defiant insistence on being alive, addressed to those who'd written the narrator off, while brooding on the fickle god-machine that keeps humanity enthralled. Corgan sets his stubborn survival against an everlasting gaze that grinds down the very people it enchants.","sonnet":"About being trapped in the orbit of an all-consuming, possibly divine or ideological force — the speaker insists they are alive and present even while being pulled under, and the song interrogates the way people surrender their will to an everlasting gaze that enchants while grinding them down beneath its wheels.","src":"haiku","fable":"The refrain insisting on not being dead is spoken from the far side of consumption: this is the voice of a deity, or of desire itself, addressing the mortals it feeds on and refuses to release. Corgan builds the Machina concept-album's theology here, a God who is less merciful presence than capricious infatuation, a gaze everyone strains to be held inside even as the apparatus grinds each toothed worshipper into the skulls beneath its wheels. The song's movement is the slow reveal that the loving thing biding its time on your desire and moving through you all is also the strangling mechanism-deity that pleads to wrench you loose. What sounds like devotional yearning is really an entrapment made mutual, the worshipper and the worshipped both endlessly in wait, both living only for themselves.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"the worshipping and the worshipped both trapped inside each other's need","built":"the slow reveal turns devotional yearning into a mutual entrapment neither party escapes"},
"smashing-pumpkins~the-imploding-voice": {"haiku":"Identity rooted in persistence despite fragmentation, resilience found by accepting one's own nature.","opus":"A hard reckoning that you carry yourself everywhere, that no escape outruns your own history, and that the self you were is locked permanently inside you. Salvation and ruin both come from within.","sonnet":"A meditation on self-continuity and self-love — the song insists that wherever you go, whatever happens, your love for yourself must remain true; you are the through-line, the shard in every place you've been, and you can always follow yourself back.","src":"haiku","fable":"A benediction to the self, chanted in second person until the refrain that you are everywhere you have been becomes less a fact than a spell against dispersal. The verses map a wounded body — skin punched through, glass and shards everywhere underfoot — against an insistence that the scattered pieces still cohere into one person who can follow himself anywhere. Corgan slips the album's machine-mysticism in sidelong: a warhead left on the shelf and never fired, a coiled spring let slack, a stranger's features breaking into daylight, as if selfhood were a decaying particle that keeps reconstituting. The turn is the closing promise that however far you fall or whatever you set down, the voice speaking stays lashed to you and cannot be shed — so the title's implosion is not collapse but the sound folding inward to hold. It is a song that survives by repeating itself, the mantra doing the work the argument cannot.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the mantra does the work the argument cannot sustain","built":"the title's implosion names not collapse but the voice folding inward to hold"},
"smashing-pumpkins~the-sacred-and-profane": {"haiku":"Blessing and devotionless incorporation; all become part of the speaker through sacred and profane union.","opus":"A prayer that fuses devotion with doubt, the speaker begging God for tears, love, peace and release while binding his fate to another and dreading being abandoned should he fall from grace.","sonnet":"A devotional plea addressed to something vast and unnamed — give me tears, give me love, give me peace — but the chorus turns the power back: whatever that higher force is, the speaker is already absorbed into it, trapped in the sun, asking whether love will ever be enough only to find the answer already arrived.","src":"haiku","fable":"Corgan builds the verse as liturgy — a series of curt petitions addressed upward, asking a lord for tears, rest, and release in the clipped cadence of someone reciting a rosary he no longer trusts. But the title's split becomes the song's engine: the sacred address dissolves into a profane one, the vertical prayer to heaven flattening into the horizontal chant that he has absorbed everyone into himself, the divine you swapped for a human you until they blur. That refrain repeats past the point of assertion into something closer to a wound worried open, and the two anxious questions threaded through it — whether love, then language itself, can ever suffice, one arriving as a plea to be believed and the other as a braced flinch toward failure — expose the doubt underneath the devotion. What sounds like faith is really the fear that neither God nor lover can fill the same emptiness, the release he swears he will earn forever deferred while he stays, in his own image, pinned under a merciless noon glare.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the fear that neither god nor lover can fill the same emptiness","built":"the sacred vertical address flattens into a horizontal chant until divine and human you blur"},
"smashing-pumpkins~this-time": {"haiku":"Fragmentation and the erosion of containment; things held together finally crash down irreparably.","opus":"A weary demand for certainty as a relationship ends, needing to be told outright whether it's finished. What once felt secure comes crashing down, yet the narrator holds to the faith that only devotion prevails. Under the resignation runs a bittersweet vow to keep singing as long as he's able, mourning a romance dissolving into goodbye.","sonnet":"A speaker watching something collapse — crashing down again — demands clarity about whether it is truly over, confesses a life lived alone with every step foretold, and insists that only love will win over whatever chemical trade has been slowly costing each person a piece of their soul.","src":"haiku","fable":"A farewell staged as one — MACHINA arrived as Corgan was dissolving the Pumpkins, and the closing curtain-fall that bids the audience goodnight reads as the band ringing down its own house lights. The song opens on a demand to simply be told it's over, then lets the refrain do the grieving: everything held tight comes apart, collapsing among friends rather than enemies, so the ruin is communal, not vindictive. Against that wreckage it stakes a plain thesis, that only love outlasts the ruin — the one thing left standing when the spell breaks. Late, it turns outward to a second person spending pieces of the self on each hit of some substance, love spoken in the grammar of addiction, so the parting is also an addressal to someone drowning. What could be maudlin instead lands as a working goodbye: switch off the sirens one final time, deal out whatever grace remains, get the music sung before the voice is gone, then let the curtain fall.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"a band's farewell and a lover's exit sung as the same goodbye","built":"the curtain-fall stage direction doubles as the Pumpkins' own dissolution"},
"smashing-pumpkins~try-try-try": {"haiku":"Mechanical persistence through oblivion; holding on while being freed, racing toward an uncertain dawn.","opus":"A hazy, dreamlike ballad about clinging to a fragile heart and a cooling love through summer heat and cross-country trains. Fragmented images of memory and motion accumulate around one insistent refrain: try to hold on. Beneath the wistful drift lies a survivor's quiet defiance, the conviction that endurance and love are worth the effort even as everything slips.","sonnet":"Against a backdrop of heat, broken numbers, and hidden paperback poems, two people try to hold on to a love that is getting older and colder — the world still moving, skinned knees and all — with the refrain that they are still alive and have survived, however barely.","src":"haiku","fable":"A plea to hold a fading relationship together, sung across two weathers of the same song. The verses pull apart into two vocabularies that never quite touch: one of pop-culture debris and transit — a snack-food endearment, cities named as if the affair were being conducted from moving trains — and one of encrypted signal, feedback loops and slapback echo, the MACHINA album's fixation on transmission and interference. The refrain does the actual work: the gripping-tighter imperative repeats until it stops being a request and becomes the only thing keeping the heart beating, and the single word that changes between verses — the heart going from colder to older, division becoming survival — is the whole arc, resignation curdling into a battered, defiant we made it through. What resists paraphrase here isn't hiding meaning so much as enacting it: the lyric fragments and skips like a signal breaking up, so the fight to keep loving and the fight to keep the words legible become the same fight.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"the fight to keep loving and the fight to stay legible as one","built":"a single word swap — colder to older, division to survival — carries the arc"},
"smashing-pumpkins~with-every-light": {"haiku":"Fading illumination and departing grace; each light carries away hope and warmth toward darkness.","opus":"A rare burst of Pumpkins optimism, casting off burdens and old hatreds to stand openly in love. The narrator throws away superstition and fear, insisting the light he has found shines down so he is never alone. Warm and almost hymnlike, it imagines heaven as something already present, radiant and self-created, if only one dares to embrace it.","sonnet":"A spiritual optimism addressed to someone weighed down by war and trouble: the sun is shining, love is being created, the four-leaf clovers can be thrown away because something more reliable than luck has arrived — the repeated \"away\" is a gentle clearing rather than an erasure, making room for a light that is already present.","src":"haiku","fable":"An anthem of deliverance that keeps sliding toward something more cultish than it admits. Its verses run on the grammar of subtraction — one by one the listener is told to shed what burdens them, what they hate, the charms they trust for luck, the things that keep them safe — clearing everything the listener leans on so that one all-consuming love can move into the vacancy, and the childlike report that daylight is finally breaking over the speaker lands as both genuine rapture and a convert's flattened joy. The turn is that this salvation is unmistakably his: the promise that conflict has ended and no armed opposition remains to challenge his love makes the speaker the object of worship, and the second-person invitations start to read as recruitment. Corgan built MACHINA around Zero and Glass as a doomed messianic rock-star persona, and this song is that persona's altar call — the fullest, most seductive statement of the delusion the album is diagnosing. What redeems it from mere irony is the refrain of gathered light, where the speaker insists he has company on every side so warmly that you feel the real ache underneath the megalomania: someone who wants transcendence so badly he'll build a religion to get there.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness","artifice & performance"],"means":"an altar call that frames the speaker as the object of worship","built":"the stripping-away grammar of the verses clears space for recruitment"},
"smoke-signals~judgment-day":{"opus":"A Terminator-styled tale of a robotic doomsday, a reprogrammed guardian dispatched into the past to shield a target from a murderous automaton and a deceitful paternal figure; amid shape-shifting metal and end-times stockpiling, it asks who can restore humankind and issues the franchise's famous invitation to survival.","sonnet":"A Terminator-referencing apocalypse fantasy — machines hunting humans, a shapeshifting liquid-metal pursuer, a father-figure revealed as a lie — told as a survival thriller where the narrator is reprogrammed to keep running and urges others to follow if they want to live.","src":"sonnet"},
"smurno~a-1wu4pro":{"haiku":"Existential numbness through intoxication—pouring soul with air into wine, drowning understanding in alcohol; trapped between moments, sensing the sea of trees without beginning or end.","opus":"Writing in Ukrainian, Smurno render emotional limbo as something close to folk lament. The narrator likens themselves to sediment in a sea of trees, unable to take any side, pouring wax into their ears and sorrow into wine, singing too much and hiding a wild instinct, existing somewhere between beginning and end with no shore anywhere in sight.","sonnet":"A Ukrainian song about being suspended in emotional limbo: the narrator compares themselves to sediment in a sea of trees, unable to be on any side, pouring wax in their ears and sorrow into wine, singing too much and hiding their wild instincts — existing somewhere between beginning and end with no shore in sight.","src":"haiku"},
"snake-eyes~big-deal":{"haiku":"The speaker, made of the same material as a rival, feels they lag behind and lack connection while their counterpart succeeds effortlessly.","opus":"A song of self-diminishment beside someone who seems to have everything the narrator lacks; the sarcastic 'so what's the big deal?' masks real inadequacy and the sense that his time and self are being wasted in the shadow of another.","sonnet":"A self-deprecating confrontation with someone who outshines the narrator in every way — framed as resentment curdling into indifference, asking what the point of comparison even is when everything feels wasted.","src":"haiku"},
"snake-eyes~crybaby":{"haiku":"Emotional vulnerability becomes defiance against suppressed feelings and shallow happiness. The speaker rejects the carrot-and-stick narrative, choosing tears and self-pity as honest resistance.","opus":"A wry, self-aware confession dressed as pop-punk. The narrator adopts feeling sorry for himself as a new hobby, chasing a happiness dangled just out of reach. Underneath the sardonic shrug sits a genuine plea to stop suppressing tears and to take mental health seriously, coaxing himself to release the emotion and settle down.","sonnet":"A dry-humoured self-permission slip: happiness is just a carrot on a stick, so the narrator decides to stop chasing it and let themselves cry — shelving the worries and leaning into feeling sorry for themselves as a legitimate form of self-care.","src":"haiku"},
"snake-eyes~hottest-day-on-record":{"haiku":"Catastrophic heat and collapse consume the world through climate breakdown, where everything melts and evaporates into irrelevance.","opus":"Climate collapse compressed into panicked fragments: everything melting, records shattering, and the human answer reduced to pack up, find another rock, and bail. Too hot repeated like a stuck alarm makes denial and flight the whole tragic response.","sonnet":"A climate-crisis alarm in absurdist shorthand: record-breaking heat melts everything from ice cream to ice caps, and by the time you notice it's too late to do anything but pack up and evacuate the planet.","src":"haiku"},
"snake-eyes~soup":{"haiku":"Privilege fails to satisfy; material comfort cannot fill emotional emptiness or repair fractured ambition.","opus":"A relationship pulling apart over temperament, one partner's cheerful half-full optimism against the narrator's flat depressive drift. Cold soup as the small, sad emblem of two people who no longer want the same life.","sonnet":"A person stuck in low-grade dissatisfaction in a relationship — their partner insists on seeing the glass half full, but the speaker feels empty, uninspired, and quietly certain that both of them deserve something more than what they're settling for.","src":"haiku"},
"snake-river-conspiracy~breed":{"haiku":"A creator manipulates and replaces someone with a clone designed for inferiority and suffering, making them feel unloved and manufactured.","opus":"The trip-hop and industrial-rock act voices a chilly creator speaking to a clone built to be governed and belittled, prodding at buried fears. Alternating tenderness with a reminder of who manufactured whom, the narrator flaunts secret powers and hunts for excuses to destroy this expendable copy, turning intimacy into ownership and love into a claim of make-and-discard.","sonnet":"A creator turns on their creation with cold venom — having made someone, they now hold that origin as a weapon, cataloguing hidden capabilities and deeming the clone insufficient. The power imbalance is absolute: the one who made can also unmake, and love here is indistinguishable from ownership and contempt.","src":"haiku"},
"snapped-ankles~personal-responsibilities":{"haiku":"Corporate negligence escapes accountability while individuals bear the weight of moral obligation. The speaker rails against the asymmetry where wealth absolves culpability.","opus":"A pointed satire that individuals are lectured about personal responsibility while giant corporations, granted the rights of personhood, bear none. As their ship-sized vessels drag the world toward calamity, the song calls for lifeguards to save us from a corporate destiny of negligence and greed.","sonnet":"A pointed critique of corporate accountability: individuals are told to own their choices — eat unhealthily, tattoo themselves, express themselves freely — while corporations granted personhood escape the same standard, their negligence and wealth insulated from consequence as the 'dusty knees' metaphor frames ordinary people as already kneeling.","src":"haiku"},
"snayx~work":{"haiku":"Labor extracts life while debt ensures perpetual servitude; the speaker fantasizes about refusing and collapsing the system entirely.","opus":"A working-class scream against the grind, school and bosses and minimum wage all draining the life out until the overdraft maxes and eviction looms. Silenced the moment he stands up, the singer would sooner die than drown in debt, and finally just tells them where to go.","sonnet":"A working-class British rant against the grind of minimum wage jobs, debt, crushing school expectations, and a bank that's already maxed out — the narrator oscillates between defiance and paralysis, eventually just telling everyone to fuck off.","src":"haiku"},
"sneaker-pimps~6-underground":{"haiku":"The speaker descends into isolation and self-deception, faking their life and accepting whatever scraps come their way while remaining open to grace.","opus":"Sneaker Pimps, the British trip-hop act first fronted by Kelli Ali, sink into numb detachment and self-effacement. The narrator wishes to vanish, admits to emotional emptiness, and warns not to confuse comprehension with affection or conversation with closeness. He performs a jaded, world-weary persona, always taking yet never satisfied, too wound-up to rest.","sonnet":"Subterranean alienation: she exists below the surface of normal social exchange — strung out, hollow, performing a life she does not actually live. Openness to falling from grace is the one honest posture she can sustain in a world where connection costs more than she has.","src":"haiku"},
"sneaker-pimps~bloodsport":{"haiku":"Love becomes a violent arena where vulnerability and surrender lead only to wounding; mothers failed to warn of love's destructive nature.","opus":"A jaded reckoning with love as something cruel and combative that no one warned you about, longing for childhood innocence before you learned the game can't actually be won; the mother who never told him stands for that lost naivety.","sonnet":"A speaker longs to return to the clarity of childhood before desire and love entered, framing romantic attachment as a brutal, zero-sum contest that no one warned them about — love, in the end, is just a bloodsport.","src":"haiku"},
"sneaker-pimps~low-five":{"haiku":"The song expresses emptiness and apathy toward conventionality. Nothing satisfies anymore—corporate anthems mean nothing, and expecting control over anything is futile.","opus":"A flat, disenchanted survey of corporate emptiness and self-worth deflation, where ambition curdles into a bleak downsizing of the self and the singer keeps asking whether anyone truly loves themselves. The low five replaces the triumphant gesture with defeat. Sneaker Pimps, the British trip-hop act behind Becoming X, here trade seductive gloom for a sardonic dissection of late-nineties consumer culture and its hollow anthems.","sonnet":"A portrait of low-grade spiritual bankruptcy — attached to everything, moved by nothing, reaching for something higher and coming up blank every time. The self-diagnosis is brutal: kitemarked for failure, ego inflated past zero, a downsize of ambition dressed as self-acceptance. A low five is the only honest gesture left.","src":"haiku"},
"sneaker-pimps~tesko-suicide":{"haiku":"Exhaustion with performative identity and false surfaces; a woman choosing independence and authenticity over pleasing others.","opus":"The British trip-hop group sketch a bleak vignette of disillusionment and burnout, cutting off contact and feeling used up. Sardonic pep-talk phrases urging someone to take a risk mask a portrait of hollow self-defense, cheap theatrics, and quiet defeat, the whole thing steeped in an over-and-done resignation.","sonnet":"A woman fed up with being consumed and discarded cuts the cord — on a suffocating dynamic, on performing sweetness, on waiting for a life that fits. The imagery is mundane and defiant at once: cherry bootlaces, candy teeth, chip on shoulder. Independence is chosen with exhausted determination rather than celebration.","src":"haiku"},
"sneaker-pimps~walk-the-rain":{"haiku":"A person anguishes over separation, feeling desperate and hollow, walking through rain in emotional turmoil while watching manipulative people close by.","opus":"A lover's departure guts the narrator until he pounds walls with bloody hands, cursing and crawling through a downpour that mirrors his despair. Trip-hop pioneers Sneaker Pimps trade their cool sheen for raw self-laceration here, the narrator sneering at smug onlookers while calling himself a bitch, walking the rain as penance for a loss that feels terminal.","sonnet":"A man consumed by desperate grief walks aimlessly through rain, unable to tolerate another's absence. He pounds walls and curses, while the false sympathy of onlookers only deepens his sense of isolation. His suffering is both uncontrollable and invisible to those around him.","src":"haiku"},
"snot~stoopid":{"haiku":"Hatred bred from ignorance perpetuates violence, the speaker unable to transcend prejudice ingrained so deeply it becomes indistinguishable from identity.","opus":"A satirical inhabiting of a bigot's mind to expose hatred as self-inflicted blindness passed down through elders' lies; the violent slurs are the target, not the message, with the point that ignorance blinds you and education is the only cure.","sonnet":"A first-person exploration of the internal logic of bigotry — the speaker initially inhabits and then dismantles the perspective of someone whose hatred of the other is fuelled by self-doubt and learned prejudice. By the end, the song turns on itself: the hatred was always blindness, and education is the only cure.","src":"haiku"},
"snow-strippers~aching-like-it-s":{"haiku":"Persistent bodily pain and ache become defining condition; suffering adheres to flesh.","opus":"A hypnotic ache over someone who never stays, offering only fleeting, half-real moments of closeness before walking out again. The narrator savors a single quiet intimacy while insisting the whole thing ends on their terms. The duo's cold synth pulse underscores a loop of yearning and abandonment, the aching itself becoming the only constant, every day.","sonnet":"A repeating pattern of being abandoned — they stay a minute, then walk out the door, and it ends only when the narrator says so; but the aching never stops between exits. The one quiet moment of genuine intimacy (\"your hand in mine / your other fingers in my mouth tonight\") is suspended between the comings and goings, fragile and fleeting.","src":"haiku"},
"snow-strippers~back-n-forth":{"haiku":"Indecision and obsession intertwine as the speaker cycles between positions, neither leaving nor staying, perpetually caught in motion.","opus":"The dizzying confusion of an on-again off-again relationship, demanding a photo be burned to finally cut ties. The obsessive repetition of back and forth enacts the very indecision it complains about, choosing the other as a vice again and again.","sonnet":"A relationship whose constant vacillation has become maddening — the other person's indecision and mixed signals have left the speaker disoriented and demanding they destroy the record of what they shared, exhausted by being pulled in two directions with no resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"snow-strippers~draw-a-heart":{"haiku":"Alienation and invisible trauma hide beneath performance, as the speaker craves to be seen while remaining fundamentally unknowable.","opus":"A plea insisting that affection alone can mend what is breaking apart, set beside a confession of never holding anyone's gaze and not recognizing the self beneath the surface. Sketching a heart and crossing it out mirrors the longing simply to be noticed while quietly coming undone.","sonnet":"About the longing to be truly seen and loved — the narrator draws a heart and crosses it out, falls apart, doesn't make eye contact with anyone because they're afraid of being known; the song is a loop of self-protection and the desperate wish for something to finally make it right.","src":"haiku"},
"snow-strippers~just-a-hint":{"haiku":"Seduction and transgression simmer beneath restraint—the speaker hints at desires and separate selves, all while the other's appetites drive toward escalation and satiation.","opus":"A charged, obsessive attraction driven by wanting extra sensation and dropping hints while demanding all of it. Repetition of where it happened, over and over, makes the song a loop of craving and impatient seduction.","sonnet":"A speaker leaves deliberate traces — red walls, leaked intentions, the same location revisited over and over — teasing a partner with just enough signal to draw them in while craving extra sensation and separate personalities to satisfy what ordinary connection cannot.","src":"haiku"},
"snow-strippers~just-your-doll":{"haiku":"Objectification reduces the speaker to possession; trapped in hell and dependency, they surrender agency in exchange for the lie of belonging.","opus":"Reducing oneself to a lover's plaything to survive a relationship where they can't decide and the lies burn from the inside. The submission is a private hell, the singer feeling utterly alone even while pleading to be caught before he breaks.","sonnet":"A person reduced to a passive object in a relationship — 'I'm just your doll, I promise' — burns from the inside as the other person lies and can't decide, leaving them alone in a self-described hell with veins about to constrict.","src":"haiku"},
"snow-strippers~need-to-know":{"opus":"An anxious infatuation reduced to one compulsive demand to know whether the feeling is returned, whether they're meant to be. The obsessive repetition is the worry itself.","sonnet":"A fragmentary longing to find out whether two people are meant to be together — almost entirely an anxious repetition of the need for an answer.","src":"sonnet"},
"snow-strippers~so-what-if-i-m-a-freak":{"haiku":"Transgressive sexuality as reclamation; self-defined freakdom beyond judgment or apology.","opus":"A defiant embrace of being a freak wrapped around raw, self-annihilating desire. The narrator flaunts having only themselves, courting a lover through smoke and a dangerous willingness to die for the moment. A grim spoken aside about suicide cuts through the eroticism, exposing the darkness beneath the bravado in the duo's abrasive underground electro-pop.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who seems to enjoy watching the speaker degrade — you like it when I'm dying for it — and the only sovereignty available is the refusal of shame: so what if I'm a freak, I only have myself, and the face behind the smoke is visible even if the dynamic cannot be escaped.","src":"haiku"},
"snow-strippers~throw-it-back-away":{"haiku":"Surrendering to dissolution willingly, indifference to suffering as liberation from forced connection.","opus":"A hazy, hedonistic surrender where craving oblivion and craving pleasure blur together, breath and control offered up recklessly to whoever will take them. Numbness dressed as abandon.","sonnet":"A state of absolute surrender and indifference to whether one lives or breathes — the narrator actively courts suffocation and obliteration, framing it as sensory pleasure and freedom from caring, throwing everything back and letting it go wherever it goes.","src":"haiku"},
"snow-strippers~video-games-2":{"haiku":"A medicated, mentally fraying narrator cycles through resentment at a boyfriend glued to Call of Duty, pharmaceutical numbness, and a twisted Lana Del Rey–inflected longing for a heaven that never quite arrives.","opus":"A jaded, medicated reworking that flips Lana Del Rey's devotion on its head. Here the narrator resents a partner glued to his console, cycling through Ativan and lamotrigine while love curdles into irritation. Domestic boredom and mental-illness self-awareness collide, borrowing Del Rey's romantic refrain only to expose the numb, unglamorous reality underneath the fantasy of surrendered adoration.","sonnet":"A relationship where one person is always on a kill streak while the other manages medication, work shifts, and suppressed rage: just took an Ativan, now I'm not mad at him — the Lana Del Rey interpolation turns the domestic detachment into something almost poetic, the video games framing the whole dynamic as a game only one of them chose to play.","src":"haiku"},
"so-below~bone":{"haiku":"Unreciprocated love cuts deep through the marrow; the speaker endures emotional pain tied to someone indifferent, unable to break free from a one-sided wound.","opus":"Watching a lover keep another person on the phone, she is worn down by an attachment that wounds her to the marrow yet won't release her. It is about a one-sided love that costs everything while collapsing under her own dreams.","sonnet":"A relationship corroded by a partner's addiction or obsessive attachment — \"she's on your phone saying there's no without it\" — and the speaker's pain at being deprioritised cuts straight through the bone; the wound worsens each time she lets go, and she doubts he'd ever give the habit up for her.","src":"haiku"},
"so-it-begins~my-will-their-hands":{"haiku":"Cult manipulation exploits vulnerability with false love; the leader weaponizes belonging to control adherents, making them executioners of their own doubt.","opus":"A chilling portrait of a cult leader preying on the broken and unloved, molding them like clay into devoted hands willing to kill for him. He insists they acted only on their own desire, absolving himself while handing down execution to anyone who breaks faith.","sonnet":"A cult leader's monologue and a survivor's testimony woven together: the leader exploits the broken and lonely, moulding them into willing instruments — \"send me your broken, I'll make them my hands\" — while the counter-voice insists those people came willingly, seeking love, and were shown \"the truth\" until they would die and kill for it.","src":"haiku"},
"soccer-mommy~lucy":{"opus":"Temptation personified as the devil himself, an angel-faced figure with shiny eyes who burns and breaks whoever succumbs. Soccer Mommy plays the seduced narrator watching darkness look back from the mirror, falling from heaven toward hellfire. Lucy the tormentor becomes the pull of a corrupting desire she can't resist or name.","sonnet":"A confrontation with the narrator's own capacity for darkness — Lucy (Lucifer) is charming and beautiful, impossible to resist, and the narrator recognizes they have fallen enough that the evil they saw in others now looks back from their own mirror.","src":"sonnet"},
"social-order~worst-way":{"haiku":"Existential desperation and isolation compound as the speaker clings to the edge, abandoning hope while refusing to finally fall.","opus":"On the edge and desperate for any way out, coming apart alone in a life gone dark, still praying to be needed even as everything unravels.","sonnet":"About desperately wanting to escape an unbearable emotional state — the speaker is on the edge, cut open by grief and isolation, and wants out in the most extreme sense, though the song leaves that exit ambiguous.","src":"haiku"},
"sodom~aber-bitte-mit-sahne": {"haiku":"Four elderly women meet daily for cakes and whipped cream until death eventually claims them one by one, their final rest accompanied by sweets and pastoral blessing.","opus":"A cover of Udo Jürgens' darkly comic tale of elderly women who meet daily to gorge on cakes and cream until the sweets kill them off one by one. The joke is their refrain, always insisting on extra cream, even at each other's funerals, gluttony pursued cheerfully to the grave.","sonnet":"A darkly comic German poem set in a pastry shop where a circle of elderly women meet daily to gorge on cake and cream until, one by one, death claims them all — the last survivor collapses at the table and is buried with tortes instead of wreaths.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cover of Udo Jürgens' cheerful schlager about elderly ladies who gorge themselves to extinction, the whole conceit hinges on a German pun where the verb for skimming a rich topping also means being picked off, so gluttony and mortality collapse into one word. What the arrangement exposes is a compulsion that survives its own indulger: the polite request threaded through every verse keeps firing even at the graveside, appetite running on past the person who felt it. Sodom's trick is to keep the tea-room daintiness untouched and let brute thrash mechanics carry the menace, so the distance between a genteel little jingle and the grinding engine beneath it does all the ironic work.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"appetite outlasts the body that housed it","built":"thrash mechanics buried under unbroken tea-room daintiness carry the menace"},
"sodom~abuse": {"haiku":"The narrator describes self-abuse and sexual exploitation as temporary relief from internal conflict, seeking deliverance through fleeting encounters.","opus":"Early Sodom trade in crude teenage provocation, the German thrash pioneers built a whole track around masturbation and self-gratification framed in deliberately grotesque terms. It reads less as meaning than as shock, the band's raw, juvenile brutality on full display as they goad the listener toward release for its own sake.","sonnet":"A blunt, unflinching survey of sexual self-gratification framed as a form of desperate relief. The language is deliberately coarse and clinical, stripping away any romantic coating to present physical desire as an unglamorous compulsion that the body enacts regardless of shame.","src":"haiku","fable":"A crude, deliberately juvenile taunt about self-gratification, delivered in Sodom's early-Tom-Angelripper broken-English register where the mangled idiom is part of the affront rather than an accident. The song addresses a second person trapped in the closed loop of his own body — waking to release that buys no satisfaction, a currency you can spend on nothing and touch on nobody — and the whole point is that nothing is exchanged, no other party exists. The escalating goads toward action are the mask; underneath, the title lands as the flat verdict on a want that only ever circles back to the self.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"desire that circles back only to the self and buys nothing","built":"broken-English register frames the mangled idiom as part of the affront"},
"sodom~addicted-to-abstinence": {"haiku":"The speaker embodies destructive self-sabotage through crude language and violent imagery, claiming addiction to the absence of indulgence.","opus":"A lurching self-portrait of a predatory, self-destructive figure who frames restraint itself as an addiction, piling grotesque bodily imagery onto a sense of being cursed and unfathomable. It revels in menace for its own sake. Sodom, pioneers of German thrash and a cornerstone of the Teutonic scene alongside Kreator and Destruction, deliver the coarse, blunt aggression that has always been their signature.","sonnet":"A self-portrait of compulsive destruction turned inward — a restless, predatory figure who tears at himself and others with equal indifference, sustained by memories he can't extinguish and words that won't burn. The central irony is a creature addicted to the very absence of excess, negation as its own obsessive drive.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's paradox carries the whole thesis: a compulsion whose object is refusal itself. This is a self-portrait of the loner as hunter, a stray who circles alone and names himself a hazard to his own body before he ever turns on anyone else, so the violence reads as self-loathing pointed outward. The grotesque middle verse, all spilled intestines and a corpse left ruined and denied the shore, makes literal what abstinence does when it curdles: withholding connection becomes the urge to unmake others. The deliberately overwrought, near-malapropped diction is part of the effect, a man groping for a grandeur his fireproof vows and squalid reputation can never actually reach, which is exactly the point of the mask.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","violence & murder","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"refusal itself becomes the compulsion that unmakes others","built":"overwrought diction gropes for grandeur the speaker's vows can never reach"},
"sodom~after-the-deluge": {"haiku":"The biblical flood narrative and divine judgment of humanity, with Noah as the sole righteous survivor.","opus":"A blunt retelling of the Noah flood myth, tyrants ruling a merciless earth until a righteous man is spared with his sons and beasts, forty days of rain drowning even the mountains before the waters recede and a covenant against future cursing is sworn. Sodom, German thrash pioneers, often reached for biblical and apocalyptic themes, delivering this Old Testament catastrophe with the same battering directness they bring to war.","sonnet":"A retelling of the Noah flood narrative with the weight on divine judgment and its aftermath — forty days of annihilating rain, everything drowned, then the waters receding and the world repopulated under a covenant. The song ends with the deity's own reckoning: regret, a promise against repetition, and the ancient law that blood calls for blood.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom retell the Flood not as deliverance but as a war report, which is the whole point of a thrash band reaching for Genesis: the annihilation of nearly all life is the story's engine, and the ark is just the surviving unit. The song is structured as a bureaucratic dossier — an announcement, a beginning, then a divine statement — so that God comes across less as a merciful father than as a commander issuing orders, an instruction the narrator keeps returning to as if Noah obeys a directive rather than receiving a covenant. The genuine unease surfaces in the closing address, where the divine voice repents of the killing and then, in the same breath, promises never to stop the cycle of seasons and installs the law that whoever sheds another's veins must have his own opened in turn: mercy and permanent retribution are set down as one decree. Read straight, that is the band's real subject — a creator who regrets the deluge yet writes the knife into the terms of the world that survives it, so the rescue and the sentence are the same sentence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","violence & murder"],"means":"mercy and permanent retribution issued as one divine decree","built":"song structured as a bureaucratic dossier, God framed as a commander"},
"sodom~agent-orange": {"haiku":"The Vietnam War's chemical weapon created lasting multigenerational suffering through cancer, deformity, and haunting trauma that will never heal.","opus":"A blunt war-metal indictment of the US defoliant campaign in Vietnam, the poison outliving the fighting in cancers and birth defects. Its horror is that the damage is inherited, passed down generations with no reparation.","sonnet":"A catalogue of the ongoing human damage caused by the US military's use of the herbicide Agent Orange in Vietnam — poisoned farmland, cancers in civilian bodies, birth defects passed down generations, and no possibility of reparation — described as a fire that destroys without burning.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom builds its indictment on the language of the perpetrators, naming Operation Ranch Hand and the dioxin defoliant by their sanitized official terms so the euphemisms of state warfare do the incriminating work — the bitterest of them turning Smokey Bear inside out, the fire-prevention mascot's only-you plea rewritten as an order to prevent the forest itself. The dominant conceit is a weapon that leaves no scorch mark and no immediate corpse, its harm postponed until it surfaces as cancer, physical deformity, and stillbirth in people born decades after the last plane sprayed the Mekong Delta. That deferral is the entire moral argument: the true atrocity is not the visible bombing but the poison folded into human inheritance, passed to grandchildren who never lived through any war yet carry its chemistry in their cells. Where much late-1980s German thrash reached for nuclear apocalypse or fantasy carnage, Tom Angelripper's band fixes on a documented American war crime and refuses to prettify it, its flat clinical diction mirroring the bureaucratic paperwork that authorized the spraying.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"poison folded into human inheritance rather than a visible battlefield","built":"clinical diction mirrors the bureaucratic paperwork that authorized the spraying"},
"sodom~agent-orange-live": {"haiku":"Chemical warfare inflicts generational trauma and deformity on a population, leaving survivors and descendants poisoned by a legacy of legalized eradication.","opus":"A thrash reckoning with the Vietnam defoliation campaign, naming Operation Ranch Hand and the poison that keeps killing through cancers, deformities and stillbirths across generations. Its refrain about a fire that leaves no burn indicts a chemical war crime that leaves no honest scar to point at.","sonnet":"A documentary condemnation of the US military's use of Agent Orange in Vietnam — the herbicide defoliation campaign poisoned civilian populations, caused birth defects, cancer, and intergenerational illness; there is no reparation and no medicine, only the chemical's silent continuation through contaminated bodies and stillbirths.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom builds its indictment on the language of the perpetrators, naming Operation Ranch Hand and the dioxin defoliant by their sanitized official terms so the euphemisms of state warfare do the incriminating work — the bitterest of them turning Smokey Bear inside out, the fire-prevention mascot's only-you plea rewritten as an order to prevent the forest itself. The dominant conceit is a weapon that leaves no scorch mark and no immediate corpse, its harm postponed until it surfaces as cancer, physical deformity, and stillbirth in people born decades after the last plane sprayed the Mekong Delta. That deferral is the entire moral argument: the true atrocity is not the visible bombing but the poison folded into human inheritance, passed to grandchildren who never lived through any war yet carry its chemistry in their cells. Where much late-1980s German thrash reached for nuclear apocalypse or fantasy carnage, Tom Angelripper's band fixes on a documented American war crime and refuses to prettify it, its flat clinical diction mirroring the bureaucratic paperwork that authorized the spraying.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"poison folded into human inheritance rather than a visible battlefield","built":"clinical diction mirrors the bureaucratic paperwork that authorized the spraying"},
"sodom~among-the-weirdcong": {"haiku":"Combat and carnage in a jungle war setting. Soldiers face brutal slaughter where death is methodical and minds are shattered by violence.","opus":"A brutal first-person report from jungle combat, thick with gunfire, split skulls and clinical butchery. The narrator dissolves into the massacre, his feelings switched off, unmoved even when he sees his own comrades run through and killed.","sonnet":"A violent depiction of close-quarters jungle warfare, told from the perspective of a soldier surrounded by death and atrocity. The speaker describes losing his bearings amid machine gun fire, slaughter, and the sight of friends killed.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's Vietnam cycle turns the coined title creature into a thesis about combat that erases the line between hunter and quarry: the invented adversary, half Viet Cong and half hallucination, cannot be distinguished from the corpses the narrator steps over. The lyric tracks a single collapse from outside to inside, beginning with a battlefield that appears to resent the living and ending with the fighter emptied by the sight of ruined comrades. What sharpens the horror is the sacramental register laid over the carnage, filth transfigured into communion, insisting this campaign is no valor but a cold ritual that profanes whatever it touches. The mantra-like refrain performs the very derangement it names, its repetition mimicking a mind that can no longer keep the surrounding world in order.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","madness & the mind","faith & the occult"],"means":"combat erases the line between hunter, quarry, and the watching dead","built":"mantra-like refrain performs the derangement it names through sheer repetition"},
"sodom~angel-dust": {"haiku":"An addict chases the high of angel dust, desperate for the escape it provides from daily hardship and emotional pain.","opus":"A raw first-person account of addiction, the compulsion to score just to get through a day and escape, a man transformed and hollowed by the need he can't refuse. There's no moralizing, only craving stated plainly. Sodom are among the founding German thrash bands, and this early cut trades their war themes for street-level desperation, the riff-driven bluntness matching a subject too immediate for metaphor.","sonnet":"Addiction stripped to its bare compulsion — the daily search, the need that overrides everything else, PCP framed not with horror but with the bleak honesty of someone who knows exactly what it costs and takes it anyway because nothing else reaches as high.","src":"haiku","fable":"Covering Venom's 1981 Angel Dust, Sodom keep it a a first-person addiction monologue that mistakes its own captivity for freedom. The narrator recounts the nightly hunt for a fix as if it were appetite and self-determination — needing, wanting, having to have it, living like there is no tomorrow — but every declaration of desire is really a report of compulsion, the daily shot merely to survive the hours rather than to get anywhere. The tell is the phrase about getting away: he thinks the drug is an escape route when it has become the thing he can no longer escape, and the boast that the substance has remade him into someone unrecognizable lands as the loss it actually describes. Away from the war and horror subjects Sodom usually favors, this is the band training the same blunt aggression on a smaller, closer catastrophe, letting the swagger indict itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape"],"means":"every declaration of appetite is actually a report of compulsion","built":"boast of self-reinvention lands as the loss it describes"},
"sodom~ausgebombt": {"haiku":"Remembrance of bombing campaigns that devastated civilian populations; a call to reject war and learn from historical trauma.","opus":"German thrash pioneers Sodom voice survivors of prolonged war, recalling hunger and cold so severe that basic food outvalued gold, and cities reduced to corpses buried in rubble by relentless bombing. Its blunt refrains reject dealing in weapons or death and plead for learning from history, turning the band's aggression into a stark anti-war statement against power-mad tyrants.","sonnet":"Survivors of wartime aerial bombardment — bread worth more than gold, warmth a luxury — bear witness against the political machinery that destroyed their lives. The refrain is a direct instruction drawn from lived catastrophe: no trading in death or arms, no repeating what was done. Memory here is moral obligation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A German thrash band from the flattened Ruhr keeps one native word untranslated inside an English lyric, and that survival is the argument: ausgebombt, bombed out, is the civilian shorthand that outlives every slogan and evacuation order. Rather than salute the raid, the song weighs it in hundredweight explosives and rubble, counting the bodies sealed under debris until grand ideology shrinks to a plain ledger of ruin. The hammered chorus strips politics down to refusals — no market in the dying, no profit wrung from weaponry, only the demand that a country stop rehearsing whatever already leveled it. Its force is granting the bombardment no heroism at all, hearing total conflict as wreckage for everyone pinned beneath the fire.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society"],"means":"ideology shrinks to a plain ledger of rubble and sealed bodies","built":"one untranslated German word survives inside the English lyric as the argument"},
"sodom~ausgebombt-german-version": {"haiku":"Civilians evacuated and annihilated by aerial bombardment during wartime collapse into chaos, their deaths meaningless and unquestioned in service to despotic power.","opus":"German thrash veterans deliver a blunt antiwar warning in their native tongue. War arrives overnight, currency collapses, bombs consume blood, and millions die fleeing into ruin. The hammered refrain urges destroying weapons and learning from the past. Sodom, long fixated on war's horror, strip away any glory to insist that no one truly wins and the cycle must not repeat.","sonnet":"A German thrash anti-war document: overnight war arrives, money becomes worthless, bombs fall from the sky and fear turns to fury. The refrain — \"don't play with death, learn from the past\" — frames the evacuation and mass killing that follow as preventable catastrophes; the final question \"who won the war?\" gets no answer.","src":"haiku","fable":"A German thrash band from the flattened Ruhr keeps one native word untranslated inside an English lyric, and that survival is the argument: ausgebombt, bombed out, is the civilian shorthand that outlives every slogan and evacuation order. Rather than salute the raid, the song weighs it in hundredweight explosives and rubble, counting the bodies sealed under debris until grand ideology shrinks to a plain ledger of ruin. The hammered chorus strips politics down to refusals — no market in the dying, no profit wrung from weaponry, only the demand that a country stop rehearsing whatever already leveled it. Its force is granting the bombardment no heroism at all, hearing total conflict as wreckage for everyone pinned beneath the fire.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society"],"means":"ideology shrinks to a plain ledger of rubble and sealed bodies","built":"one untranslated German word survives inside the English lyric as the argument"},
"sodom~ausgebombt-live": {"haiku":"Bombing and war ravage communities across centuries; the lesson remains unlearned through repetition.","opus":"An anti-war protest from a thrash band, recalling decades of scraping by until war drove people to despair and bombs flattened them. The blunt refrain, no trade in death, disarm, learn from history, insists the corpses under the rubble are the only lesson anyone should need.","sonnet":"An anti-war testimony spoken from the perspective of those who survived wartime bombing — bread worth more than gold, fifty-hundredweight bombs, corpses under ruins — concluding with a firm demand: \"no trade with death, no trade with arms, learn from the past.\"","src":"haiku","fable":"A German thrash band from the flattened Ruhr keeps one native word untranslated inside an English lyric, and that survival is the argument: ausgebombt, bombed out, is the civilian shorthand that outlives every slogan and evacuation order. Rather than salute the raid, the song weighs it in hundredweight explosives and rubble, counting the bodies sealed under debris until grand ideology shrinks to a plain ledger of ruin. The hammered chorus strips politics down to refusals — no market in the dying, no profit wrung from weaponry, only the demand that a country stop rehearsing whatever already leveled it. Its force is granting the bombardment no heroism at all, hearing total conflict as wreckage for everyone pinned beneath the fire.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society"],"means":"ideology shrinks to a plain ledger of rubble and sealed bodies","built":"one untranslated German word survives inside the English lyric as the argument"},
"sodom~baptism-of-fire": {"haiku":"Depravity and decay spread as humanity depopulates; unnatural sickness and disfigurement consume bodies. A baptism of fire annihilates all—bones decompose alive in a cycle none survive.","opus":"An apocalyptic vision of depopulation and decay, where a narrator born of depravity watches the world enveloped in fear and warns that the coming trial by fire leaves no survivors. Rot, cremation and a vicious cycle close the circle to the end of time. Sodom, veterans of German thrash metal, channel their long fixation on war and human ruin into relentless, grinding dread.","sonnet":"A meditation on civilizational rot viewed from inside the organism — bodies decomposing, populations thinning, the air thick with fear and toxin, a world that cycles through collapse and false renewal without escaping it. The baptism of fire is not initiation but annihilation, an ending no one survives.","src":"haiku","fable":"On Agent Orange, the album where Tom Angelripper swung Sodom's thrash toward the machinery of chemical and atomic warfare, this is the aftermath rather than the assault: a survivor narrating his own tissue going soft, malformed by the same contamination that engineered him, helpless as thinning masses vanish around him. The title works as a savage joke, since a rite meant to mark cleansing and a fresh start instead delivers an ordeal no one walks away from, initiation staged as death. What secures the interpretation is the pivot to genetic tampering and a curse that folds back on its own beginning: the terror is not one catastrophe but a closed circuit, a species reseeding the extinction it just barely escaped, so the war-crime imagery reads as a verdict on people who keep steeping themselves in their own venom.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"contamination that folds back on its own beginning as a closed circuit","built":"baptism title rewritten as an initiation no one survives"},
"sodom~belligerence": {"haiku":"A person is trapped in dark divinity and hostile defiance; they suffer from predestined sin and cannot escape—they must live in belligerence.","opus":"A blood-soaked hymn to perpetual war within the self, where the narrator is torn apart by lust, impaled on a spear, and enthrones himself in the darkest coffin while raging against an absent god. Combat becomes his eternal state of being. Sodom, long-running German thrash metallers, lean into their trademark bleak theology and battlefield imagery to make belligerence sound like a permanent condition.","sonnet":"A figure condemned to perpetual warfare with both god and flesh — impaled by desire, burned by faith, escaping hatred's gates only to find the same battle waiting. There is no divine rescue, only prophecy ridden hard and a life that will always be defined by its own aggression.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom treats belligerence not as an act of war but as a condition of the soul, the state of permanent unwinnable enmity a body is sentenced to once there is no god left to petition. The speaker is a martyr-figure pierced and enthroned inside his own coffin, riding through prophecies that promise a remedy and deliver none, so his retreat becomes a verdict rather than an escape, choosing extinction over any narcotic rest. The refrain that swallows the back half is the argument itself, the word repeating until it stops meaning aggression and hardens into a life sentence, the mind chewing on itself after every last threshold of loathing has already been crossed. This is Tom Angelripper's long preoccupation with faith and slaughter turned inward, the enemy no longer across a battlefield but inside a man predestined for a sin he can neither refuse nor outlive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","faith & the occult","suicide"],"means":"permanent enmity as the sentence a godless body is born into","built":"the title word repeats until it stops meaning aggression and hardens into a life sentence"},
"sodom~blasphemer": {"haiku":"Satanic devotion and rebellion against Christianity through black metal aesthetics and anti-religious acts.","opus":"A cartoonish, over-the-top declaration of Satanic allegiance, the narrator naming himself an Antichrist who spits at the church, drinks his own blood and reads the Satanic Bible, a checklist of blasphemies delivered without irony. This is early, primitive Sodom, when the German band's black-thrash was raw and juvenile, its shock-value devil worship less a philosophy than a crude middle finger aimed at everything respectable.","sonnet":"A first-person embrace of Satanic identity as teenage rebellion made literal — black masses, inverted crosses, and blood as sacrament, all framed as a chosen path when no one showed a better one. It's adolescent transgression wearing the full costume of occult sincerity, more provocation than theology, ending in grotesque self-destruction as its logical conclusion.","src":"haiku","fable":"The opening admission is the whole song: the narrator does black metal because no one ever showed him another road, which frames the entire Satanic inventory that follows — inverted cross, Anton LaVey on the nightstand, blood-drinking, midnight rites — as a role assumed by default rather than a belief arrived at. This is Sodom at their most primitive and adolescent, and the writing knows it: the transgressions are checklist items recited without escalation, each verse landing the same three punchlines before the title gets chanted four times, so the shock imagery flattens into liturgy. The one line that breaks the pose is the wish to masturbate oneself to death, where the borrowed evil curdles into something genuinely self-annihilating and lonely — the tell that under the corpse-paint bravado this is a kid with nowhere to go performing damnation because damnation was the only script on offer. The four-fold shout of the title is the point, not filler: it is an identity insisted on by repetition precisely because there is nothing behind it but the insistence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"damnation performed by repetition because nothing lies behind it but the insistence","built":"fourfold title-shout enacts identity by rote, shock imagery flattening into liturgy"},
"sodom~blood-lions": {"haiku":"Captive-bred lions killed for sport in canned hunts embody a lucrative industry of exploitation, cruelty, and devalued majesty.","opus":"A furious protest against canned-hunt trophy killing, where lions raised in captivity and raised by hand are bred only to be gunned down for a mounting, the noblest of predators turned into revenue. The chorus pounds the industry's cruelty as a repeated charge. The German thrash veterans aim their aggression at real barbarism, the anger sincere and precise.","sonnet":"A condemnation of canned lion hunting as industrialized cruelty — animals hand-reared into docility then sold to trophy hunters at a guaranteed kill, the whole operation exposed as a vanity business that reduces the king of animals to a profit margin and a pelt.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom aim their war-machine vocabulary at the trophy-hunting trade in South Africa, where captive-raised big cats are gunned down inside fenced paddocks by tourists paying for a guaranteed mane on the wall. The venom lands less on the men behind the rifles than on the ledger, the refrain converting a breathing animal into a pricing model whose payment clears only once the carcass drops, its harvested parts routed onward into the tonic-and-wine market. Tom Angelripper traces the whole manufactured lifespan: bottle-fed as a petting-park attraction so visitors can cradle it for photographs, then sold back to those same visitors as a stationary target, the docility that earned early revenue becoming the flaw that makes the shot effortless. He frames the closing charge as the buyer's true purchase, the crown of the apex predator bought to shore up a shrunken ego, and there lies the cruelest inversion: the creature is magnificent precisely so a frightened little man can feel enlarged by wrecking it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"a docile cub groomed for revenue becomes a stationary trophy target","built":"refrain converts a living animal into a pricing model whose payment clears only at death"},
"sodom~blood-on-your-lips": {"haiku":"Violence and predatory conquest, with psychological manipulation and sexual dominance intertwined.","opus":"A predatory, homicidal courtship where the narrator hunts his prey and frames murder as a shared plunge, stained lips serving as a warped test of devotion and a summons to perish together toward some paradise. Its vocabulary of imprisonment and erasure circles death and lust. The German thrash veterans deliver it with mid-career polish, turning their battlefield worldview sickeningly intimate.","sonnet":"A portrait of a predator who has made peace with the violence inside them — haunting memories, crimson stains that won't wash out, and a passion for flesh framed as something eternal rather than shameful. The song moves between a soldier's traumatized interiority and something colder, inviting the victim to die alongside the speaker as if it were a mercy.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person plunge into a killer's self-justifying interior, where the stacked abstract nouns pile up like a corrupted rosary, a compulsion arguing itself into inevitability rather than narrating any deed. The chorus recasts murder as sacrament: red at the victim's mouth becomes the yardstick of the speaker's devotion, and shared death is pitched as closeness, so the dread lies in violence spoken through the vocabulary of love. That disguise slips in the later verse, where the coaxing swerve toward parents and a pledged heaven exposes a predator luring rather than repenting, the softness itself the snare. Angelripper usually stages his carnage at the magnitude of armies and battlefields; here the aperture shrinks to one skull, and the interpretation that earns the track is that its unhurried, rehearsed procedure unsettles more than any war, because the murderer styles himself a savior handing down grace.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","love & desire"],"means":"murder spoken entirely through the vocabulary of love and devotion","built":"the aperture shrinks from armies to one skull, making procedure more unsettling than war"},
"sodom~bloodtrails": {"haiku":"Military conflict and occupation driven by greed, creating cycles of violence and corruption.","opus":"A blunt anti-war statement pinned to the Gulf-era buildup, where troops are shipped abroad to guard oil under the banner of keeping peace, obeying orders born of ignorance while corrupt power claims their lives. Sodom, whose catalogue dwells on the futility of combat, march this disillusionment toward the dread of a wider global war.","sonnet":"A critique of the Gulf War as a conflict driven by resource greed dressed in the language of peacekeeping — Operation Desert Shield rendered as imperial machinery, young men dying for soil that someone else wants. The song traces the pipeline from bad education and corrupt power to the front line, where the anger keeps growing and the symptoms never change.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tom Angelripper anchors this to Operation Desert Shield, the 1990 troop buildup that preceded the Gulf War, and lets the mission's own vocabulary indict it. Blockades framed as protection, embargoes framed as restraint, an invasion pre-empted in the name of order: each official phrase gets undercut by the flat confession that petroleum beneath the sand is the actual object of all this maneuvering. The recurring warning that hostility keeps reigniting and the dead keep falling identically reframes the piece as a study of repetition, oil-greed cycling back under diplomatic dress until talk breaks down entirely. Angelripper then stretches the lens across generations, corrupt authority seizing men's souls and drilling its logic into successors, so the title marks not battlefield carnage but an inherited groove that steers reliably toward the next global slaughter, stripped of justification or lucidity.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","money & the street"],"means":"oil-greed cycling back under diplomatic dress until talk breaks down","built":"each official euphemism undercut by the flat confession of petroleum as the actual object"},
"sodom~book-burning": {"haiku":"Authorities burn sacred texts and suppress freedom while servants of evil crush individual will; the speaker's rage and desire for divine deliverance go unheard.","opus":"German thrash veterans Sodom aim their aggression at political horror, framing censorship's zealots as indifferent agents of evil consuming the very writings of liberty. Betrayed and spiritually beheaded, the narrator calls on the divine to rescue him from a faithless mob, an allusion to Kristallnacht sharpening the fascist echo.","sonnet":"A cry from inside the moment when knowledge and freedom are consumed by authoritarian fire — scriptures of liberty swallowed by flames, the individual stripped of will and agency, appealing to a god who doesn't answer. The crystal night imagery anchors this as a horror both historical and eternal.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom stages an atrocity from inside a disintegrating mind rather than from a witness's safe distance, so the speaker loses his footing, turns executioner on himself, and feels his own will worn down to reflex before any fire is lit. The pivot to shattered glass and cold-storm November aims the song at Kristallnacht, Tom Angelripper's German-history instinct dragging it off vague menace and onto the 1938 pogrom, where the ash of torched libraries stands in for every silenced record and the prayers rising to deaf gods mark the plain fact that no rescue came. Working the band's old vein of war seen through a perpetrator's numbness, the real dread lands not on the flames but on the obedient functionaries who feed harmless things to the blaze precisely because they have quit deciding anything at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"obedient functionaries burn harmless things once they have quit deciding","built":"speaker loses footing from inside the disintegrating mind rather than a witness's safe distance"},
"sodom~caligula": {"haiku":"A historical horror-fantasy portrays Caligula as a violent, sexually perverse tyrant ruling through executions, defilement, and unchecked paranoia. The song catalogs his demonic reign and inhuman cruelty.","opus":"German thrash reducing the mad Roman emperor to a catalogue of cruelty, incest, and public slaughter; Caligula becomes a shorthand for absolute power rotted into insanity, tyranny as depravity crowned and worshipped.","sonnet":"A portrait of the Roman emperor Caligula as a demonic, sexually depraved, violently paranoid ruler who presided over public executions and prostitution as expressions of his absolute and insane power.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom treats the mad Roman emperor as a catalogue rather than a story, and that flatness is the design: no rise, no downfall, only a heap of titles piled until tyranny hardens into a permanent state instead of a fable with a moral. Tom Angelripper's thrash has always married the band's war obsession to a relish for grotesque authority, and here Gaius becomes the point where power and appetite fuse — the ruler who reportedly named his horse a magistrate and demanded worship as a living god, executions and lust invoked in one breath as though butchery and desire ran off a single nerve. The one crack in the chant is paranoia, set apart from the drummed syllables of the name, the terror finally shown from within as a mind devouring itself, the despot hunted by the very dominion his hand commands.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","madness & the mind"],"means":"tyranny catalogued until atrocity becomes a permanent condition","built":"paranoia set apart from the drummed syllables of the name"},
"sodom~cannon-fodder": {"haiku":"Soldiers experience paranoia and psychological trauma on the battlefield, watching comrades die in futile combat. Heroes become victims sacrificed as expendable cannon fodder in endless war.","opus":"German thrash from the trenches of a soldier's mind, war seen as expendable men fed into machine-gun fire and Vietnam ghosts. The heroism is a lie; the reality is fear, futility and being spent for nothing.","sonnet":"About soldiers used as disposable material in wars built on political hypocrisy — the paranoia of the front line, suicidal emptiness, ghosts of Vietnam, towns burned in the name of freedom. The title says it: people fed into the machine to be destroyed for others' purposes.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's Tom Angelripper stages the whole of M-16 around the American grunt in Vietnam, and here the war reaches us entirely through one man's fraying nerves rather than any battle map — the enemy is never located, only sensed, so dread itself becomes the actual combat. The bitter joke in the title's cliche is that the men are venerated and squandered in the same breath, martyrs sacrificed for the profiteers who wager their lives like chips at a table, the chorus binding sainthood to a spun revolver cylinder to insist the two are one transaction. Motion runs inward, not forward — the verse loops back to the opening watched-in-the-dark image untouched, so the final repeats read as a mind trapped in its own circuit, mental collapse landing well before any bullet does.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","madness & the mind","politics & society"],"means":"sainthood and the spun revolver offered as one transaction","built":"the verse loops back to its opening image untouched, trapping the mind in circuit"},
"sodom~city-of-god": {"haiku":"In a world of decay and sacrifice, emperors demand worship and dead souls reign; fear spreads as terror strips away reason and compassion.","opus":"German thrash sketching a bleak dystopia where the dead inherit power and violence is sanctified by the gun. It's an anti-authoritarian, anti-war snapshot of a world where truth is counterfeit and mercy has been buried.","sonnet":"A bleak picture of a world governed by corruption, bloodshed, and hollow power, where the dead inherit authority and guns are the only blessing — a city of God that is anything but holy.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title turns Augustine's City of God inside out — the divine commonwealth reduced to a place christened for God yet ruled by the gun, where Tom Angelripper strips the irony to its cruelest reading: sanctity means only that the trigger decides who breathes. The chorus that soothes against fear and flight is no consolation but a verdict, since the throne it hands over goes to corpses and the benediction is delivered through a rifle barrel. What sharpens the horror is the notion that bloodshed on this scale might pass unremembered because cruelty has become the baseline of the poor rather than an aberration, so the City of God stands for any territory where mercy was conceded long ago and prayer answers to nobody.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","politics & society","faith & the occult"],"means":"the divine name kept while mercy was conceded long ago","built":"Augustine's title inverted so the throne passes to corpses"},
"sodom~code-red": {"haiku":"Victims of discrimination and fascism reclaim dignity by rejecting imposed order and expressing buried rage against institutional lies.","opus":"A German thrash warning about coerced allegiance and the machinery of fascism, where disloyalty is punished and the outsider is made to run the gauntlet. The alarm is that no illusion will save you from the beast within.","sonnet":"Betrayal within an alliance — loyalty was demanded, disloyalty was punished, and now the power structures that once protected have become the source of fascism and degradation, with the inner beast mobilized to reclaim dignity at any cost.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom title-tracks their 1999 record with a state-of-emergency siren, tracing how an alliance rots from within: the pact held only while fear cemented it, and once the barrier falls, loyalty stands exposed as coercion and yesterday's comrade becomes today's scapegoat. Tom Angelripper, a German writing after reunification, hears that alarm over the return of fascism dressed as reclaimed dignity, the crowd's baser hunger unchained to drive some chosen victim through a gauntlet of blows. The hammered chorus is no verse but the warning tone itself, flat and mechanical, and the final scream at a barricade of falsehood says the alert blares unheard even as a society crosses the line it swore it had buried.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"the alarm blaring unheard as a society crosses its buried line","built":"the hammered chorus shaped as the warning tone itself, flat and mechanical"},
"sodom~cowardice": {"haiku":"The narrator condemns a genetically deformed predator who rapes and murders victims, describing violence against innocence as an expression of cowardice.","opus":"Sodom pour revulsion on a rapist and murderer they paint as a monster wrong from birth, cataloguing his crimes in gory detail. Drawing on the band's early splatter streak, the venom builds to the wish that his own mother had ended him before he ever drew breath, framing his cowardice as something cold and blood-slicked.","sonnet":"A predator is condemned in visceral terms — a person born wrong, whose violence against innocents is systematic and planned. The track offers no psychological nuance, only accumulated horror: the victims are named as sacred, the perpetrator as an aberration that nature and birth should have prevented.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom sketches a predator and then withholds every scrap of glamour a lesser band might grant him, driving instead at the title's flat verdict: dominance over the defenseless is spinelessness wearing a mask, never true menace. Tom Angelripper's later thrash favors this sort of moral bluntness, and the writing prosecutes from two angles, cataloguing the outward atrocities while pinning the rot to a warped genetic origin, as though nature itself had botched the man at conception. What lifts it above ordinary gore-thrash is its focus on the trusting victim who kisses the rod and reaches toward the very jaws that will close on her, so the anguish settles on plundered innocence rather than spectacle. The final wish, that his mother had never carried him to term, is where the perspective collapses, souring into the same exterminating impulse it damns, betraying that the only remedy the song can imagine for such evil is to erase its source before it draws breath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"dominance over the defenseless named as spinelessness, never menace","built":"the closing wish mirrors the exterminating impulse it set out to damn"},
"sodom~decision-day": {"haiku":"War kills comrades and forces soldiers into impossible choices, promising escape and liberty through death rather than life.","opus":"A soldier's dying reverie during a beach assault, watching his comrades sink under pointless commands from leaders drunk on ambition and rigid resolve, his hope for what lies beyond mixed with disgust at the war machine that spent him. Long a channel for anti-war narrative, the band ground abstract fury in a recognizable scene of slaughter, water for a grave and doomsday overhead.","sonnet":"A soldier's last moments on a beachhead, watching comrades drown while orders from above promise only more slaughter — the internal conflict is between blind obedience and the knowledge that this is meaningless death, the repeated phrase decision day carrying the weight of a choice that may already be made for him.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom builds the piece on a pronoun split that carries its entire argument: the conscript claims only inward things in first person, his dread and his misplaced faith, shrinking to a catalogue of private frailties as the tide comes for him. Blame, meanwhile, is always aimed outward and upward, at the commands he cannot refuse and the propaganda dressing slaughter as certain triumph, at the officer whose steel shell keeps him dry while the men wade into gunfire. That grammar mirrors amphibious assault exactly, since whoever feels the surf pull him under is never whoever charted the landing. Tom Angelripper, mining the band's decades-long revulsion at organized killing, hangs a soldier drowning with saltwater for a headstone against the untouchable comfort of those who signed the maps, so liberation reads as a fate imposed rather than any freedom the doomed were ever offered.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"the grammar of blame tracks the hierarchy of an amphibious assault","built":"first person claims only dread; all blame is aimed outward and upward"},
"sodom~delight-in-slaying": {"haiku":"Warfare and brutal violence elevated to spiritual ritual; a god of war demands bloodlust, vengeance, and the crushing of perceived enemies.","opus":"Sodom adopt the voice of a merciless war deity drawn from occult scripture, demanding devotion to violence and offering no mercy to the weak or fallen. Reveling in revenge and slaughter, the speaker condemns cowards and fools as expendable and crowns himself the fire burning inside every person, twisting battlefield cruelty into a creed of self-worship.","sonnet":"A war deity or warrior philosophy exalts the joy of violence as the highest human expression — pity is weakness, tears are for slaves, and ecstasy lives in the kill. The creed is delivered without irony: strength, pride, courage, and the ruthless elimination of cowards and fools constitute the only worthy existence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A creed dictated by a deity of battle, its cruelty deliberate: the warrior's virtues — fearlessness, pride, scorn for the weak — get stripped of every restraint that once made them an ethic, until only the pleasure of killing remains. The escalation from commands (worship, damn, despise) to a first-person claim of godhood is the pivot, the speaker rising from ordering slaughter to naming himself the fire smoldering in mortal breasts. Read that way, the boast becomes diagnosis: this power is no external tyrant but the appetite waking whenever raw courage gets sold as the whole of goodness. The refrain's shrinkage to one bare word lets the ornate rhetoric of righteousness fall away, exposing the plain relish it was built to excuse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Its commands are a cento from Crowley's Book of the Law — fear neither men nor fates, damn those who pity, despise the cowards, strike hard and low, a flame of hate in every heart — a real liturgy held up, not invented.","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","war & battle"],"means":"courage stripped of restraint until only the pleasure of killing remains","built":"the refrain shrinks to one bare word as the ornate rhetoric falls away"},
"sodom~devils-attack-bonus":{"haiku":"Demonic forces attack humanity causing death and suffering; hell's legions massacre the living and trap the survivors in eternal damnation and torture.","opus":"A gory horror-thrash vignette of a demonic being that descends after dark to slaughter, its victims begging in vain as hellish flame wipes out everything living and leaves carnage behind. The doomed soul is dragged into an infernal kingdom for endless torment. Straightforward apocalyptic damnation imagery from the German thrash veterans.","sonnet":"A devil descends on the land at night, massacres the population, and sentences the survivors to eternal torment in hell — a stripped-down thrash horror scenario with no subtext beyond the literal carnage.","src":"haiku","fable":"An early-Sodom horror sketch that runs on the oldest engine in the band's toolbox: an unnamed nocturnal creature descends, the townsfolk scream for a mercy that never comes, and the survivors discover the real punishment is not dying but being kept alive to suffer. The song's one genuine turn is that the attack ends and the misery does not — the legions withdraw, leaving blood and corpses, yet the torture continues into an eternal fire where the only remaining wish is to escape hell. Its broken-English syntax and stock imagery mark it as juvenilia (the Bonus tag on an M-16 reissue), so the honest read is that this is Sodom in pure Venom-descended blackthrash mode, using demonic invasion less as belief than as a delivery system for the same annihilation their war albums would later aim at real armies.","fv":2,"fnote":"Devils Attack is older than the band’s records: a 1982 Witching Metal demo cut, riding In the Sign of Evil reissues as a bonus — the crudeness the song weaponizes is teenagers inventing German thrash in real time.","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"survival after the massacre as the real horror, not the slaughter","built":"the broken-English chorus is the monster's voice, not a flaw","fvr":"2.4"},
"sodom~die-stumme-ursel": {"haiku":"Loneliness and sexual frustration, with a man's objectification of an inflatable doll as replacement for human connection.","opus":"A grotesque black-comic monologue in German about a man who orders an inflatable doll from a catalogue and prizes her silence, until a visit to a paid dominatrix named Ursel leaves him humiliated and crawling back home to his mute companion. Sodom, the German thrash institution, built a shock-humor streak into their catalogue, and here the trio play crude parody straight, turning loneliness and pathetic desire into deliberately offensive farce.","sonnet":"A dark comic portrait of a man so estranged from real intimacy that he falls for an inflatable doll from a mail-order catalogue, naming her Ursel, her silence his perverse turn-on. When he finally screws up the courage to visit a real woman — a dominatrix who humiliates him — he retreats home to his mute companion, the joke landing somewhere between pathetic and genuinely sad.","src":"haiku","fable":"A deliberately tasteless comic vignette from the era when Sodom swapped apocalyptic war-thrash for gleeful punk provocation, and the tackiness is exactly the intended weapon. The narrator picks a companion from a mail-order catalogue, names the inflatable doll Ursel, and confesses that what excites him is her total voicelessness: a partner engineered to never speak, never judge, never say no. Everything pivots on the closing turn, when a flicker of conscience sends him to pay a dominatrix who whips him, mocks him as a loser, and happens to bear the very same name. The living woman, granted agency, does nothing but degrade him, so he slinks back to the deflatable object that waits without complaint. That echoed name is the punchline and the thesis at once, skewering a masculinity that can only stomach a woman stripped of any will of her own.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["comedy & wordplay","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"a masculinity that can only stomach a woman without any will","built":"the dominatrix and the doll share the same name as the punchline and thesis"},
"sodom~don-t-walk-away-tank-cover": {"haiku":"A monstrous, overbearing mother figure tries to control and trap the narrator; he pleads for her to let him go, describing her as insufferable and nightmarish.","opus":"Covering Britain's Tank, the German thrashers play out a grotesque comedy: the narrator is cornered by his overbearing mother-in-law, who schemes to replace her own daughter in his bed. Fantasizing about her death and his own escape, he turns the pleading refrain into darkly funny dread, trading warfare for a leering domestic nightmare.","sonnet":"A man is being relentlessly pursued by a smothering, overbearing woman — specifically his partner's mother — who wants him for herself. The situation is absurd and suffocating, and all he wants is to be a million miles away rather than trapped within earshot of her pleading refrain.","src":"haiku","fable":"A leering barroom farce that Sodom borrow wholesale from Tank, the NWOBHM speed-metal forebears whose grubby humor Sodom clearly relished enough to honor. The joke is a domestic horror shrunk to a punchline: a man wanted not by the girl but by the girl's monstrous, predatory mother, who is drawn as a force of grotesque appetite — thunderous, cavernous, a wonder of the world sized up for revulsion rather than awe. The refrain works as pure dramatic irony: it is her plea to him, and every repetition is the trap tightening, so the more the phrase piles up the more cornered he sounds, right up to the final wall of it. Underneath the gross-out caricature is an ordinary panic about being consumed by someone else's need, played for laughs precisely because naming it straight would be unbearable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["comedy & wordplay","love & desire"],"means":"panic about being consumed by someone else's appetite, played for laughs","built":"the refrain is her plea to him, each repetition tightening the trap"},
"sodom~eat-me": {"haiku":"Raw sexual aggression and crude bedroom banter with explicit focus on oral sex and desire.","opus":"A crude, deliberately juvenile burst of carnal provocation, stacking lurid sexual demands and cannibal metaphors into pure shock-value bravado with no pretense of depth. It exists to offend and grin about it. Sodom, the German thrash institution, have always kept a strain of gleeful vulgarity in their catalogue, and this is that impulse stripped down to its blunt, leering core.","sonnet":"Blunt, aggressively carnal provocation stripped of any emotional content — pure physical appetite framed as demand, with cannibalism as a crude metaphor for sexual consumption. There is no arc, only escalation of the same crude imperative.","src":"haiku","fable":"A deliberately crude piece of shock-thrash from the German trio, this is provocation as its own reward — the sexual come-on is pushed past titillation into the grotesque, splicing the language of arousal with hellfire, blood-sweat, and cannibalism so that lust and violence share one vocabulary. The song works by refusing any distance between the two: to eat and to be eaten collapse into a single leering command, and the joke is that there is no message beneath the offensiveness, only the appetite it performs. Tom Angelripper's carnival-vulgar streak, the same one that runs through Sodom's beer-and-blasphemy material, treats bad taste as the point rather than a lapse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"lust and violence sharing one vocabulary until no distance remains"},
"sodom~electrocution": {"haiku":"Murder and sexual violence merge into pathological satisfaction; state execution becomes mere retribution for crimes rooted in unchecked sadistic impulse.","opus":"Brutal thrash narrating a sex-driven serial killer awaiting the electric chair, wallowing in atrocity before the state's own killing balances the account; it's grim shock-horror that mirrors sadistic violence back onto capital punishment.","sonnet":"A serial predator's catalogue of sexual violence is recited in clinical detail before the narrative shifts to justice: society's legal machinery condemns the perpetrator to the electric chair, framing execution as the proportionate answer to pathological cruelty.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom construct the track as a two-voiced engine that refuses to let you settle: the verses inhabit a serial killer's mind from the inside, rendering atrocity as craving and even rapture, while the returning chorus replies in the cold clerical tone of the state. The ledger squared, the sentence lawful, the execution seat waiting its turn. The dread the band actually leans into is the mirroring of the two, since the killing by law is staged as its own ceremonial slaughter echoing the crimes it repays; the murderer believed himself licensed in his delusion, the government holds an actual writ to end him. That the chorus alone recurs untouched and shuts the song makes the argument in its very shape: the blood-for-blood loop is what speaks last, and Angelripper's signature knack for mashing lurid tabloid horror into thrash grants no release, only the same knell sounding once more.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"state execution staged as its own ceremonial echo of the crime it repays","built":"the cold clerical chorus recurs untouched and shuts the song, letting blood-for-blood speak last"},
"sodom~erwachet": {"haiku":"A German-language call to awakening against sexual repression and religious hypocrisy, urging people to break free from brain-dead conformity.","opus":"Sodom, the German thrash institution, aim their scabrous humor at self-appointed moral crusaders, mocking the pious as brainwashed and repressed while daring them to indulge carnal appetites in full view of their own God. The recurring German command to awaken frames sexual liberation as revolt against sanctimony, its blasphemous edge sharpened by liturgical Latin flourishes typical of the band's provocations.","sonnet":"A German thrash tirade against moral repression and religious hypocrisy. The narrator gleefully inverts puritanical conditioning, declaring that sexual stimulation has never harmed anyone and urging a crowd of spiritually lobotomised followers to wake up and reclaim their own bodies from the grip of mass obedience.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is stolen goods: Erwachet! is the German name of the Watchtower Society's Awake! magazine, and Sodom weaponizes the command back at the people who print it, casting the devout as a will-stripped flock branded by depravity and unable to think for themselves. The engine of the provocation is the refrain — a mock-medical assurance that a little stimulation in the genital region never hurt anyone — deliberately set against doing it before the eyes of the Lord, so the blasphemy isn't decoration but the argument: bodily appetite dragged in front of the God the faithful are told to fear. The Latin gladiator's salute (those about to die salute you) and the chanted name of Jahwe frame these aseptic brain-dead crusaders against evil as the ones already condemned, and the closing triple shout of the stolen title turns the missionary's own wake-up call into an obscene taunt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","shame & repression","anger & defiance"],"means":"bodily appetite dragged in front of the God the faithful are told to fear","built":"the Watchtower Society's own title thrown back as an obscene taunt"},
"sodom~euthanasia": {"haiku":"Mercy killing and the blurred lines between murder and compassion, with a killer justified by their own logic.","opus":"A chilling portrait of a mercy-killer masquerading as healer, a demigod in white who claims to end suffering but is really stimulated by his victims' screams, unable to tell right from wrong as his lust corrodes him. It twists the Hippocratic oath into a license for murder. Sodom bring their war-hardened brutality to the clinical setting here, exposing the thin line between palliative care and predatory power.","sonnet":"A killer wearing the mask of a physician — the song exposes a figure who has inverted the Hippocratic oath into something predatory, stimulated by suffering rather than compelled to relieve it. Medical intervention becomes a cover for narcotic sadism, with the deity invoked as the voice that speaks through a murderer rather than against one.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title dangles a promise of merciful release, then Sodom peels the white coat back to reveal a predator who learned that a caregiver's vocabulary is perfect camouflage for appetite. The horror runs on borrowed Greek theology, the gentle dream-god paired with his death-bringing twin, deployed not as comfort but as the elegant branding a sadist drapes over needle-tracked skin and a chilled cadaver on the slab. What damns everything is the arousal: this figure is not easing suffering but feeding on the shrieks, cloaking that hunger under scripture and clinical authority alike. When a killer claims the voice of heaven, the false savior in surgical white makes an executioner's hand and a physician's look identical, and mercy becomes the most efficient disguise cruelty has ever worn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"mercy as the most efficient disguise cruelty has ever worn","built":"caregiver's vocabulary borrowed as camouflage for a predator's appetite"},
"sodom~exhibition-bout": {"haiku":"Crowds pay to watch matadors stab bulls to death in arenas—cruelty unpunished, beasts treated as trash. Man defeats beast through agony; blood soaks the ground in a slaughter that serves no purpose but spectacle.","opus":"A blistering condemnation of bullfighting, framing the arena's bloodthirsty crowd and the matador's cruelty as a crime that goes unpunished, and closing with a strange peace where man and beast become one in death. Animal suffering is treated as barbarism dressed as spectacle. Sodom, German thrash pioneers, aim their aggression at human cruelty itself, an unusually pointed animal-rights stance for the genre.","sonnet":"A bullfight viewed from the bull's side — the matador's courage is crowd-performance, the arena a machine for licensed cruelty, and the animal's death a spectacle that generates applause. The song indicts the audience as much as the killer, and closes with the bull finding peace in a world the crowd never will.","src":"haiku","fable":"An anti-corrida broadside that turns Sodom's battlefield vocabulary against a spectacle sport: the ring is staged as a campaign with declared fronts and an offensive to be driven through, so the matador's paraded courage reads as choreographed slaughter sold to a paying arena. The chant carries the real charge — a licensed sanction dressed as tradition, a wrong that draws no penalty because the ritual sits inside the statute, indicting the mass that buys a ticket to watch a creature bleed out as much as the man holding the blade. Sharpest is the closing inversion, where the band hands the gored animal the calm and the reconciled ground the crowd never earns, so the seats empty on its dignity rather than on any human victory. For a group whose usual trade is glorified carnage, the reversal is the whole point: bloodlust becomes the thing under indictment, and legality is exactly what makes the cruelty obscene.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","politics & society","artifice & performance"],"means":"legality is exactly what makes the cruelty obscene","built":"the song hands the gored animal the calm the crowd never earns, closing on its dignity"},
"sodom~feigned-death-throes": {"haiku":"A manipulative figure drinks and torments another while pretending to die; hatred and survival bind them in a cycle of fake surrender and real violence.","opus":"Sodom stage a grim standoff, an armed narrator fooled by a victim who plays dead to survive while he tears into them. Beneath the gore runs a psychological duel of misanthropy and endurance, the prey mimicking a corpse to slip free and the hunter both the perpetrator and a man vowing to annihilate the person he has become.","sonnet":"About someone who pretends to be more broken or dying than they are to manipulate — the narrator sees through the performance but is still deceived by it, and by the end carries his own loaded gun, suggesting the manipulation has driven him to the edge of real violence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A predator's monologue built on a double-cross: the speaker believed he had killed, was released by his victim's dead stare, only to find the throes were faked and the wound still weeping — so the hunt and the feeding resume with relish. Angelripper keeps the register lurid and tactile, meat torn and drained through the skin, but the through-line is misanthropy naming itself as the seed, the killer casting himself as death with the rotted grin and the wrist that works the marionette wire. The last movement turns the barrel around: the last figure upright on the fen, a chambered revolver in his fist, the vow to outlast and overcome resolves into a promise to unmake the self he has grown into, so the whole predator swagger reads back as the mask over a suicide. The feigned death throes were never only the victim's — they were his rehearsal for his own, the deception aimed at the self he can no longer stand.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","suicide","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"predator swagger reads back as rehearsal for his own undoing","built":"title's double-cross applies to victim and speaker in the final turn"},
"sodom~fellows-in-misery": {"haiku":"A woman seeking sexual fulfillment outside marriage finds a partner who shares her desires; they engage in taboo intimacy together.","opus":"A woman ungoverned by convention seeks illicit encounters, brushing aside moralisers as suitors gather and games turn brutal. Sodom, pillars of German thrash, indulge the leering shock they built their early reputation on, framing appetite and cruelty as fellowship among the wretched, the chorus binding participants together in a shared degradation.","sonnet":"A woman driven by an irresistible and transgressive desire seeks connection outside social norms. She finds a kindred spirit equally bound by unconventional urges, and together they indulge in reckless, self-destructive pleasures — two outcasts united by shared compulsion and mutual ruin.","src":"haiku","fable":"A 1994 Sodom song about a woman seeking anonymous same-sex encounters, and the queasy thing about it is how the title floats between sympathy and sneer. The verses cast her desire as an abnormal inclination she cannot govern, then hand her a community of the like-minded — the fellows in misery who share her supposed affliction — so the chorus reads at once as solidarity and as a moralizer's verdict that these appetites can only end in wretchedness. By the last verse the shared pursuit has curdled into the album's signature register of ecstasy indistinguishable from brutalization, the track collapsing tenderness and cruelty into the same act. What the song actually can't decide is whether it is defending the outcast or gawking at her, and that unresolved leer is finally its subject.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"solidarity and a moralizer's verdict occupying the same refrain","built":"the title floats unresolved between sympathy and sneer, and the song never decides"},
"sodom~freaks-of-nature": {"haiku":"Exploitation and enslavement of marginalized people, with rebellion and liberation through violent uprising.","opus":"A sideshow parable in which exhibited human oddities, cowed for years by the lash, finally rise up in fury, seizing weapons and cutting down their captor to seize freedom and dignity. The German thrash veterans use the carnival of monsters as a stand-in for the abused turning on their tormentors, blunt class rage under grinding riffs.","sonnet":"Outcasts and misfits, kept obedient through fear and exploitation, finally turn on the master who staged them as spectacle. The song moves from submission to insurrection: clubs, knives, collective fury — and in the end the slave driver is killed and the freaks are freed, a blunt revenge fantasy told without ambivalence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A carnival sideshow read as a machine for manufacturing subhumans: the exhibits are not born monstrous but made deplorable by the whip, kept devoted through fear until obedience itself becomes their deformity. The song turns on the reveal that the real freak is the master who profits from the display, and its final movement is an uprising — the caged attractions arming themselves with whatever the show has left within reach and killing the slave driver to free themselves. Landing on Get What You Deserve, Sodom's most punk-poisoned record, this reads less as horror-schlock than as a class parable in fairground dress: the title is the crowd's verdict, and the ending flips it back onto the man selling tickets.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"obedience manufactured by the whip until it becomes the deformity","built":"the crowd's verdict in the title flipped back onto the man selling tickets"},
"sodom~frozen-screams": {"haiku":"A speaker describes violent sexual urges repressed for years now breaking free, culminating in murder and justified retribution.","opus":"A chilling first-person crawl through a killer's psyche, long-suppressed sexual compulsion erupting into predation, the speaker savoring blood and pressing a victim's throat without a shred of remorse, only sure he'll be locked away once more. The all-caps intensity matches a narrator who feels gratified by the muted cries he claims are beyond his hearing.","sonnet":"A predator's interior monologue — repressed violent and sexual impulse finally unleashed, the speaker moving through acts of domination with a coldness that finds the victim's silence flattering, framed as a kind of dark becoming that only ends with capture and imprisonment.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person portrait of a sexual killer narrated wholly from inside the compulsion, its horror lying in a voice that never once steps outside to judge what it describes. The core move is dissociation dressed as elation: the victim's terror registers to the murderer only as flattery, so the title marks his deafness rather than her pain, a numbness he mistakes for triumph. Sodom sharpen it with a jailbird's grim economy, the promise to make someone pay until he lands back behind bars admitting a repeat offender who treats consequence as mere interruption, scarier than any gore. The beggar-turned-king image exposes the engine plainly: a powerless man staging total sovereignty over a helpless body, murder as compensatory fantasy, the flat title-loop at the close mimicking a mind stuck satisfied and unreachable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"powerlessness staged as total sovereignty over a helpless body","built":"the victim's terror registers to the murderer only as flattery, marking his deafness not her pain"},
"sodom~fuck-the-police": {"haiku":"Police represent oppressive authority using violence and lies to control populations while escaping accountability for their injustice.","opus":"A defiant protest against corrupt, tyrannical power and the crookedness of the law, condemning citizens for living as remotely operated servants; the chanted refrain rejects the police outright and demands people rise against rulers eager to kill.","sonnet":"An anti-authoritarian screed casting law enforcement and political power as tools of oppression, control, and lies that breed injustice, anger, and eventual rebellion.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom borrow the N.W.A battle cry and hollow out its target, swapping American street rage at uniformed cops for a broader German-thrash indictment of the compliant citizen who kneels by habit and takes his orders like a puppet, never demanding a reason. The three verses keep rotating one bind: docility passed off as public order, rage so total it stops perceiving anything, a longing for liberty that only marks the dreamer as prey for whoever holds the weapon, until the profane refrain reads as a release valve with no manifesto underneath. Tellingly the track closes on a plea to heaven gone useless, so the four-word shout is all that survives against an authority already carried past any way back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","freedom & escape"],"means":"a release valve with no manifesto beneath, exhausting every exit","built":"the N.W.A cry hollowed out, its target swapped from cops to docile citizens"},
"sodom~genocide": {"haiku":"Exposes humanity's capacity for mass violence and atrocity, tracing how corruption and control create demented systems that destroy the multitude.","opus":"The German thrashers turn mass slaughter into a grim war anthem, portraying extermination as humanity's darkest instinct, a world where sanity and rules have collapsed and no god intervenes. Soldiers are fed comforting lies of glory and immortality while stoking hell's fires, perched on the edge of annihilation with a single round saved for permanent rest.","sonnet":"A grim panorama of humanity's capacity for mass extermination — religious fanaticism, demented leaders, lives wasted in cycles of holy war and racial hatred, with no God to intervene and no revolution to break the pattern.","src":"haiku","fable":"An atheist reading of how mass slaughter recruits the young, this dresses extermination in the vestments of a cathedral, promising glory and deathlessness where a marble corridor and hushed veteran's story hide a machine that feeds hell rather than any deity. Insisting no divinity stands behind the carnage, it reduces the barked chorus of demented minds and tortured lives to the plain industrial output beneath that borrowed liturgy. The closing turn shrinks from ideology to one caged person perched on shoddy homemade ordnance, a single round saved for permanent rest and the lock's key hurled beyond reach, framing war not as something revolution unbolts but as a sealed cell where the crucial questions land far too late.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"mass slaughter recruited behind a borrowed liturgy of glory and deathlessness","built":"the closing image shrinks from ideology to one person with a single round saved for permanent rest"},
"sodom~get-what-you-deserve": {"haiku":"A violent revenge fantasy where the speaker, wronged and exploited, promises to return suffering to the betrayer who tapped their vein and laughed at their pain.","opus":"Told by a man broken by war trauma and betrayal, the song is a vow of vengeance against the person who wronged him, promising to hunt them down with a shotgun so they get what they deserve.","sonnet":"A revenge narrative in which an abandoned, war-scarred narrator tracks down the person who betrayed and laughed at him, promising lethal retribution with a shotgun.","src":"haiku","fable":"A revenge fantasy whose true subject is a mind hollowed out by combat and left to rot — the wound is dressed as a personal betrayal, but the shell-shock, the stripped nerve, and the sense of a man displaced from his own life mark it as a soldier's collapse wearing the costume of a grudge. The song's whole argument turns on a late structural mirror: the earlier lament, sung by the one who was drained, needled, laughed at, and abandoned, returns with every pronoun reversed, so the sufferer now stands over his tormentor doing the identical things. That inversion is the thesis — the victim does not rise above his abuser but becomes his flawless duplicate, and the justice the title advertises is unmasked as madness simply passing from one hand to the next. Naming himself a saviour as he sanctifies the shotgun and calls its aim righteous is the delusion talking; beneath the swagger, the piece reads as trauma faithfully cloning itself down the line.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The tapped-vein line folds in the previous album's title, Tapping the Vein — the follow-up's title track quietly threading the record before it.","themes":["madness & the mind","war & battle","violence & murder"],"means":"victim mirrors abuser until justice becomes madness","built":"pronoun reversal in the lament re-enacts the abuse"},
"sodom~gisela": {"haiku":"A German-language song about the dissolution of a past romance. The speaker once knew a woman named Gisela who represented something transgressive and valuable, but she is now gone—discovered in a personal ad, no longer special.","opus":"A crude, satirical piece about a lost love giving way to a pornographic magazine advertisement, in which a woman named Gisela promises to fulfill every desire and serve up any pleasure on a silver platter.","sonnet":"A darkly comic song about a man who once valued a woman for her forbidden appeal, only to discover her advertising herself in a pornographic magazine, where her explicit personal ad promises to fulfil any desire — the song plays the absurdity of this revelation for crude, sardonic effect.","src":"haiku","fable":"A joke song played straight, and the joke is the register clash: Sodom, a band whose whole idiom is war and blasphemy, hands us a mock-mournful ballad about a man pining for a woman until the punchline lands. The opening laments in earnest ballad-speak that the magic is gone and she is no longer here, that she was always worth a small sin to him — then the second verse reveals where he actually found her: a personals ad in a porn magazine, Gisela, forty and heavily hairy, promising caviar and champagne on a silver platter and your wish is my command. The comedy works by structure — the sincere heartbreak of the first verse is retroactively demolished when we learn the beloved was a smutty classified all along, the sublime deflated into the sordid. Chanting the name over and over as a football-terrace hook turns a pathetic private obsession into a communal singalong, which is the real Sodom move here: the mask is tenderness, and underneath is a band grinning at how easily longing curdles into farce.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["comedy & wordplay","heartbreak & loss","love & desire"],"means":"sincere heartbreak demolished by a smutty classified ad","built":"first verse's ballad register retroactively collapsed by the reveal"},
"sodom~god-bless-you": {"haiku":"Soldiers fight and die in war, enduring trauma and bloodshed while receiving praise and blessing for their sacrifice and unwavering spirit.","opus":"Sodom survey a battlefield where the sun bleeds red over a crimson tide and the fallen slip away from the world in single file, weapons still clutched in their hands. Long fixated on war's horror, the German thrashers salute a nation's dead with grim irony, honoring the ritual of mourning while insisting a man's spirit outlasts the slaughter.","sonnet":"Soldiers fall one by one in an unnamed war, their spirits outlasting their bodies. The invocation of divine blessing is shot through with irony — rifles in hand, blood consecrating the ground, comrades dying in sequence — and the benediction becomes a funeral rite as much as a rallying cry.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's benediction is the whole trap: Sodom hands the soldier a blessing and a rifle in the same breath, framing slaughter as sanctified duty — the nation made flesh, a kinship the chorus swears runs deeper than any sibling's, ground the fallen have consecrated with what drains out of them. But the verses keep leaking what the refrain wants to sanctify, dawn reddening as it drowns, whole battlefields turned to standing gore, rounds tearing open the meat of men, souls with their wings severed, and the read lives in that friction between the exalting hook and the atrocity it decorates. The post-chorus tolls the fallen off in a running tally, and by the outro that count is all the blessing amounts to, the prayer emptied into a body count. Angelripper delivers it deadpan, never editorializing, so the irony does the work: the mask is piety, and underneath is the machinery that consecrates young men to die and calls it grace.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"benediction used as cover for a body count","built":"deadpan delivery lets the irony do the editorializing"},
"sodom~gomorrah": {"haiku":"Biblical judgment narrative of Gomorrah as a sinful city marked for destruction, using religious imagery of wrath and divine punishment.","opus":"Testing biblical apocalypse through the lens that made Sodom infamous, the German thrashers narrate the doomed sister city as a personified mother of vice, awaiting divine judgment by fire. Prophecy imagery of harlotry, plague sores, and bodies in the street builds toward inevitable erasure, casting the destruction less as horror than as grim vindication of the wicked meeting their promised end.","sonnet":"A city steeped in fornication and abomination faces divine annihilation. Drawing on apocalyptic imagery, it portrays a doomed civilization whose corruption invites wrath — bodies fall in streets, the whore-city is judged, and those who refused to recognise the signs are extinguished at the hour of reckoning.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom reach for the doom their own name has always shadowed, dressing its namesake city's doom in the end-times language of Revelation as a spectacle of relished punishment rather than a warning anyone might still heed. The lyric ransacks scripture for its furniture — a plague-ridden ulcer, corpses left rotting in the thoroughfares, a resentful howl compared to a lion's — and lets that stolen King James grandeur carry the threat, so the piece reads as a sentence already passed rather than a case being argued. The nasty turn is how gladly the reckoning arrives: the condemning figure grants no reprieve, the flames are promised with appetite, and a final beatitude over the slain lands as gloating tricked out in gospel. Wearing the preacher's mask to savour the pyre is the genre's oldest trick, and the band commit to it without a flinch of irony.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The furniture is lifted from Revelation's fall of Babylon — the great harlot, the running sore, bodies in the street of the city — transposed onto Gomorrah, the twin town the band took its name from.","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief","violence & murder"],"means":"scripture's furniture repurposed to savour the condemned city","built":"preacher's mask worn to relish the pyre it threatens"},
"sodom~hanging-judge": {"haiku":"Wild West outlaws and tribal warriors face a judge who enforces brutal frontier justice, with death and bloodshed following their law-breaking.","opus":"A frontier tableau of outlaws drawn together by greed and the executioner-magistrate who embodies the law, the gallows awaiting. Sodom recast their thrash aggression as outlaw-Western cinema, deserts and gunmen giving way to a mythic America where the rope, not the shootout, hands down the final sentence.","sonnet":"A Western frontier tableau where law is just another form of violence — the hanging judge holds absolute power over life and death in the dust, gold draws men together in ruthless allegiance, and the gallows waits for anyone who breaks the code or simply gets in the way.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom dress a gallows sermon in Old West mythology, then let the sheriff's badge and the executioner's rope turn out to be worn by the same man. The verses tempt you with outlaw romance — desperadoes chasing gold beside Jesse James, marauders wheeling like hawks over red desert — but every chorus drops that glamour through a trapdoor, reducing it to one figure whose claws are the statute and whose statute is a noose. Cruelest is the closing dismissal, where a condemned man face-down in the dirt is judged scarcely worth mourning when set beside spectacle and the tidy restoration of order; the song has quietly borrowed the executioner's own ledger, in which a life counts for nothing against the ceremony of punishment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","death & grief","artifice & performance"],"means":"outlaw romance dropped through a trapdoor into pure execution","built":"sheriff and executioner collapse into the same figure mid-song"},
"sodom~hazy-shade-of-winter": {"haiku":"Existential reflection on time passing, wasted opportunities, and the search for meaning and human connection.","opus":"A rueful look back at a squandered life as the seasons wither and hope thins to almost nothing, memory glancing off things never finished. It's a cover of Simon and Garfunkel's wistful folk song, and the surprise is the tenderness that surfaces when Sodom, German thrash veterans, lean into a melancholy sixties standard.","sonnet":"A rueful inventory of time squandered chasing things that never arrived — the speaker looks around and finds only brown leaves and a grey sky where possibility used to be. A Salvation Army band drifts past, the vodka-and-lime goes down, and the unpublished manuscripts gather dust while seasons keep turning without mercy or meaning.","src":"haiku","fable":"Simon and Garfunkel's original is a folk meditation on aging: a man taking inventory of squandered possibilities as the calendar closes, the seasonal images tracking a life that keeps insisting it is still spring while frost already gathers underfoot. Its engine is the gap between the speaker's frozen chronology and his refusal to read it, since even dashed ambitions get met with the instruction to just imagine building them anew, and the plea to be remembered lands as the confession of someone who suspects he will not be. Hearing it in a German thrash band's hands sharpens the dread rather than the wistfulness: the same brown foliage and thawing ground that read as melancholy in 1966 become, amid a catalog of nuclear and end-times imagery, a landscape genuinely running out of seasons. What was one man's autumn gets reframed as a collective one, the tapestry of the years now something being wound shut for good.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nostalgia & memory","death & grief","war & battle"],"means":"one man's autumn reframed as a collective season running out","built":"thrash context turns folk melancholy into genuine extinction dread"},
"sodom~hellfire": {"haiku":"Apocalyptic fire consumes and corrupts; death is perpetual and redemption is impossible in this hellish vision.","opus":"An apocalyptic scene of the world consumed by flame under blackened skies, ruled by forces that deal out death without pause and offer no salvation. The German thrashers pile up images of crushed and decaying bodies and ancient buried wrath to frame this inferno as a total, inescapable end-of-days judgment.","sonnet":"A thrash metal vision of apocalyptic hellfire — disfigured corpses, darkened skies, endless war, and a universe indifferent to suffering, the imagery building toward a sense of total, irreversible cosmic damnation without redemption.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom write the nuclear apocalypse as a hellfire from above — a sudden light that seizes the whole horizon, a firestorm answering to no nation and consuming every scrap of the planet still untainted — and then, crucially, drop the camera from the god's-eye flash down to a single body pinned and crushed under the rubble, watching the crimson glow of the blast dim, unable even to tilt its head toward the dirt. That POV shift is the whole trick: the grandeur of extinction reduced to one person's helpless, undignified death. Beneath the mushroom-cloud imagery runs a second, older stratum — slumbering aeons, standing megaliths worn smooth by ages, a wrath long buried that now stirs awake — folding cosmic-horror doom into the arms race, so the bomb reads less as politics than as a final judgment nobody can be redeemed from. The last, quietest cruelty is the wish that goes unanswered: begging for the end to arrive yet finding no waiting embrace to expire in, no comfort even in the ending.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"extinction's grandeur reduced to one helpless, undignified death","built":"god's-eye flash cuts to pinned body as the pov collapses downward"},
"sodom~hey-hey-hey-rock-n-roll-star": {"haiku":"Desire for fame, excess, and sexual conquest as a rock musician.","opus":"A leering, tongue-in-cheek fantasy of rock stardom, the narrator craving free drinks in every town, weeping girls and boasting of nimble fingers on strings and elsewhere, all while sneering at poverty and rivals. Sodom, normally consumed by war and death, cut loose here with crude comic swagger, a deliberately dumb bit of self-parody that lets the German thrashers mock the very excess their genre chases.","sonnet":"A gleefully crass fantasy of rock stardom — free drinks, adoring women, and guitar virtuosity deployed as a sexual weapon, all of it celebrated without a shred of irony. The song's comedy is in its transparency: the dream is pure id, and the only threat is another bigger act displacing you at the top.","src":"haiku","fable":"For a thrash band who built their name on war, plague, and Cold War dread, this is Sodom dropping the horror mask to sneer at the rock-dream itself, the free drinks, the flashy six-string, the weeping crowds, as pure adolescent appetite. Tom Angelripper voices the fantasy straight, in first person and without irony markers, so the boast becomes its own indictment: the same quick fingers that shred the fretboard get bragged into a groupie's bedroom, collapsing musicianship into conquest. The final verse turns the mask inside out, where the terror is not poverty but eclipse, the dream lasting only till some fresh upstart outgrows him, so the anthemic hey-hey-hey chant, repeated past the point of meaning, reads less as celebration than as the hollow, addictive noise a star makes to keep from hearing that clock tick.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the rock dream indicted by voicing it straight and without irony","built":"the chant repeated past meaning becomes the hollow noise of fading stardom"},
"sodom~i-am-the-war": {"haiku":"The speaker embodies war itself—an instrument of destruction with no capacity for building or emotion. They carry an infernal curse and live on the edge.","opus":"A song sung by conflict personified, a feelingless force that is never laid down, claiming lives, hardwiring the soul with adrenaline and existing solely to lay waste. Blending firebombing, deceit and monstrous aggression, it stages war as a madness lodged in the human core that can level everything but create nothing.","sonnet":"A first-person identification with war itself rather than with any cause — the narrator has internalized combat as a permanent state of being, driven by adrenaline and a soul so fixed in aggression it can only destroy, never build.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's Vietnam-haunted M-16 mostly reports the jungle from a grunt's eye view, but here the band hands the microphone to the abstraction itself, letting conflict testify in the first person rather than watching it from a foxhole. The confession is that the promised calm was always a lie the killing told to justify continuing, since the speaker openly admits it can raze structures but build nothing, exposing tranquility as pretext. Tom Angelripper escalates by rerouting clinical vocabulary into slaughter: aggression as infection, murder as an immune reflex stripped of any antibody or softening tear, so butchery reads less like a decision than a pathogen that has annexed the nervous system. Because the thing is quartered in the chest and answers to nobody, defeat becomes impossible, which flips the pacifist charge from an outsider's placard into a grim internal autopsy.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder"],"means":"conflict confessing its own lies from a first-person testimony","built":"clinical vocabulary rerouted into slaughter as contagion, not decision"},
"sodom~in-retribution": {"haiku":"The narrator inhabits a shadow realm of demonic pain and occult despair; they embrace dark forces and violent imagery as their only sanctuary.","opus":"Sodom render a hellish, blood-drenched vision of a figure born from demonic torment and marooned in a shadow realm of vengeance, trapped inside a world he loathes. Occult desecration imagery, defiled rites, broken commandments and the raising of the dead casts war and damnation as an inescapable, self-hating torment.","sonnet":"A tormented self-portrait of someone who has witnessed too much war and darkness — wounds salt-rubbed, sanity held together by the very insanity that consumes them, existing in a shadowland of their own despair.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tom Angelripper narrates from inside a soldier who has already died and yet keeps enduring, stranded in a purgatory that is simply the trench prolonged forever. The horror is built from contradictions that never resolve: a frame outgrowing its own spirit, gashes packed with salt to keep the nerves screaming, madness that is the last thread anchoring the mind. Punishment turns inward rather than outward — awareness deliberately kept intact so the dead man can go on registering every degree of his ruin. When the closing plea begs the living not to disfigure or riddle the corpse, the terror shrinks to an infantryman's most ordinary dread, that even the body will be denied its rest, the spirit sealed shut and sentenced to sheer duration.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","madness & the mind"],"means":"a dead soldier sentenced to infinite awareness of his own ruin","built":"closing plea for the body's rest shrinks the supernatural horror to infantry dread"},
"sodom~in-war-and-pieces":{"haiku":"War and violence reduce people to fragments and decay, leaving only horror and the knowledge that death is inevitable.","opus":"German thrash veterans piling gore imagery into a portrait of war as a machine that grinds minds and bodies alike into rot. The pun in the title signals the real subject: conflict leaving survivors psychologically shattered, not just the dead in tatters.","sonnet":"The song dwells in an extreme landscape of mental collapse and physical carnage — fractured minds, rotting bodies, death and sewage — presenting war and psychic destruction as states that leave the self shattered and irretrievable.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's pun does the work: Tolstoy's War and Peace becomes war and pieces, flesh literally hacked apart, and the song assembles its whole battlefield from that rupture — corpse-seamed backstreets and skin flayed into ribbons, rendered with the connoisseur's revulsion thrash reserves for atrocity. But Sodom keeps dragging the gore inward, toward a burst floodgate spilling brain-rot, a memory that hoards only the foulest scraps, splinters driven into the skull, so the slaughter turns out as much interior as bodily: what shatters is the mind's power to hold itself intact under all it has seen. The narrator is a butcher vowing to leave you in war and pieces, yet the bridge locks killer and victim inside one shared cell behind the eyes, the vision preserved, the wits slowly coming undone — reading less as a threat than as an admission that whoever deals this out carries the identical wound.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","madness & the mind","violence & murder"],"means":"slaughter turned inward until killer and victim share one wound","built":"the title pun — war and pieces — seeds the interior collapse the whole lyric then unpacks"},
"sodom~incest": {"haiku":"Taboo sexual desire between siblings overrides moral law; the narrator defies ethical conventions and shame to pursue forbidden carnal satisfaction.","opus":"Sodom weaponize transgression as philosophy, voicing a narrator who rejects church, law, and the very notion of sin to justify seducing his own sister. The lyric frames forbidden desire as self-portrait glimpsed in broken glass, moral dilemma dissolving into leather-clad appetite. Consistent with the band's early shock tactics, it stages taboo not for titillation but to obliterate the concept of guilt itself.","sonnet":"A transgressor dismantles moral law and savours the forbidden. Rejecting church-imposed guilt, he pursues a taboo desire with a family member, framing it through warped self-justification — forbidden fruit tastes sweeter, the broken mirror reflects a man who equates personal obsession with liberation from shame.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom stage the taboo as a manifesto of self-authored morality: the speaker doesn't stumble into transgression so much as reason his way there, dismissing church, law and the moralizers before the act is even named, so that the sin becomes a philosophical position before it's a deed. The genius and the ugliness of the writing is the bookkeeping metaphor — virtues tallied against stains, misdeeds ranked and numbered — which lets the narrator pretend enough accumulated good will cancel the debt of the crime. In a second-person swerve the song flings that same accounting back at the moralizing listener, who tallies private virtue to license private fantasy no differently. That accountant's calm cracks in the final verse, where the bravado curdles into a bewildered admission that he no longer knows what has overtaken him: the mask of the liberated free-thinker slips to reveal someone frightened by his own compulsion, the self-justification collapsing into confession. Read straight it is pure Agent Orange shock-value provocation, but the structure — cold theory, then the act, then the loss of composure — makes it a small study of how a person talks himself past his own conscience and finds nothing on the other side but bewilderment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","madness & the mind","faith & the occult"],"means":"self-justification as philosophical position collapsing into bewilderment","built":"cold theory then act then loss of composure enacted as three-stage structure"},
"sodom~into-perdition": {"haiku":"Wars destroy the land like a plague; nature strikes back against polluted, infected, and weaponized civilization with fire and extermination.","opus":"German thrash veterans Sodom stage environmental collapse as an act of retribution, imagining a poisoned, overexploited planet finally striking down the civilization that ruined it. Humanity, too numbed and deaf to heed any warning, is swept toward extinction by the very forces it unleashed, its ruin reframed as overdue punishment rather than tragic accident.","sonnet":"Humanity has ravaged the planet through war and pollution until nature turns on its destroyers — a plague-like reckoning no one heeded in time. The warnings were always there; the crowd simply could not hear them before the collapse began.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's usual battlefield imagery gets recast here as ecological reckoning: the wars, plagues, and poisoned ground are not the endpoint but the provocation, and the real antagonist is nature itself preparing to collect. The song's engine is the gap between cause and recognition — humanity is a deaf, self-consuming crowd that cannot hear its own alarm, so the extinction it triggers arrives as something it never saw coming rather than as punishment it understood. Perdition here is less hellfire than consequence made literal: not damnation handed down from above but the planet closing the account, with the title's repeated fall functioning as the verdict the multitude was too numb to read.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nature & the elements","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"extinction framed as consequence a deaf humanity never read coming","built":"title's repeated fall functions as the verdict the multitude was too numb to heed"},
"sodom~intro": {"haiku":"Nuclear warfare and collective suicide; civilization marches toward destruction with brain dead certainty.","opus":"An apocalyptic warning of nuclear annihilation: warheads ready, thousand-megaton overkill near, humanity a brainless suicide fleeing and perishing in agony while cries for freedom go unheard and the world's end proves true.","sonnet":"Nuclear weapons speak in the first person, ready and indifferent — thousand-megaton warheads waiting while the world runs and screams, humanity too brainless to feel the fear bearing down on it, the command to war already issued.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom stage nuclear annihilation not as a coming event but as a standing order, a permanent command awaiting only the trigger, spoken from inside a collective that has already surrendered its will. The voice is always we, a brainless mass stumbling toward its own end in pain, weighing thousand-megaton stockpiles against the worth of one snuffed-out human life. Desire curdles into grief and hope climbs only as hopelessness, the plea for release swallowed by deaf silence until the world's finish reads as plain fact. What remains is a single hammered syllable, the last word available once every argument that anyone might halt the machine has been abandoned.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a collective surrendering will to the permanent standing order of annihilation","built":"the final hammered syllable as the only word left once every argument is abandoned"},
"sodom~jabba-the-hut": {"haiku":"A physically repulsive and morally depraved obese man victimizes children, manipulating them with gifts while committing infanticide. The song presents abuse through grotesque physical imagery and criminal acts.","opus":"German thrash veterans turning a grotesque caricature into a vehicle for something far darker, a repellent predator whose crimes against children the narrator vows to answer at gunpoint; disgust curdling into vigilante judgment.","sonnet":"Using the Star Wars villain's name as shorthand for an obese, repulsive predator, the song describes a child molester — a socially isolated man who lures children with sweets, commits acts of infanticide-adjacent violence, and will eventually be killed for it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The cartoon-slug nickname is bait: the first half is pure body-horror caricature, a weaving mass of grease and stink whose ugliness the narrator lingers on as if repulsiveness alone were the crime. Then the song springs its real subject — the fat clown is a child predator, luring victims with sweets and silencing them, and the disgust that read as schoolyard cruelty retroactively becomes moral revulsion, the outside made to match the inside. Sodom's early-90s crossover instinct is to answer the monster in kind: the closing verse drops the third-person gawking and puts a gun in the narrator's hand, converting the listener's queasy laughter into a vigilante death sentence. The Star Wars mask, in other words, is there so it can be torn off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"body-horror caricature as bait that retroactively becomes moral revulsion","built":"Star Wars mask torn off when third-person gawking becomes a vigilante death sentence"},
"sodom~jesus-screamer": {"haiku":"Prayer and religious devotion are lies that trap the mind; the narrator rejects serving an unknowable God, questioning whether faith's promises hold any truth.","opus":"An anti-Christian taunt aimed at believers who expect their devotion to spare them from hell, mocking them for worshipping something they can't know or prove. It flips the promise around, telling the faithful that the assurances they were fed are empty and that death waits for them just the same.","sonnet":"A blunt anti-religious screed telling Christians their prayers are futile, their faith in an unseen figure is mental enslavement, and their promised heaven is a lie — they will simply die like everyone else.","src":"haiku","fable":"A blunt anti-clerical jab from Sodom's mid-90s crossover swerve on Get What You Deserve, where Tom Angelripper's real target is less God than the screamer who sells God — the pulpit huckster pricing salvation at one rote prayer a day. The song's single move is the inversion between verses: the same daily devotion that supposedly spares you from hell early on is exactly what condemns you by the finish, exposing the bargain as rigged from the start. Underneath the sneer the argument turns coldly materialist — the promised reward never arrives, the grave is the only settlement — so the mockery of screamed faith doubles as a flat denial of the afterlife it peddles.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"the salvation bargain exposed as rigged from its own first verse","built":"the same devotion that spares you early becomes what condemns you at the finish"},
"sodom~knarrenheinz": {"haiku":"A scarred warrior haunted by endless war and violence becomes a vengeful force, spreading death and terror until consumed by hellish damnation.","opus":"Sung in German, a mythic portrait of a risen warlord, skin burned and soul blackened, driven by blood-vengeance to stamp out a reign of terror, drawing strength from his own pain as hellfire lights his path to ruin. Sodom name this figure as a kind of undead tribal prince of the dead. It fits their long war-and-doom obsession, the native language sharpening the folkloric menace rather than diluting it.","sonnet":"A German-language portrait of a war-scarred warrior risen from ruin — burned and blackened, drawing strength from hellfire and hatred, he moves through mud and corpses as a kind of unstoppable tribal death figure, vengeance his only gospel, his enemies a sacrifice piled toward a sky that offers no mercy.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom hand their gas-masked mascot a myth of origin, and it is a resurrection story turned inside out: risen from ruins with skin burned and soul blackened, this is no redemption but a reanimation for war, driven by the blood of vengeance. The band works the sacred vocabulary against itself throughout — enlightenment arrives with every gunshot, no sign of God's mercy ever comes, and the mountain of atonement-offerings the figure heaps up rises toward the heaven where creation dwells only to consecrate an evil made infinite. Angelripper crowns him chieftain of the dead, a thing seen creeping through the mud whose damnation is the point: the closing image sends him into a sea of flame where even death will never die, so that the trenches become an eternity with no exit. The refrain is just the name, chanted like a summoning, which is the whole trick — the cartoon figurehead is made to carry the real and endless horror of the front, the mask worn precisely so the war underneath can speak.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The opening line inverts the first words of the GDR national anthem — Auferstanden aus Ruinen, risen from ruins — a state hymn of rebuilding flipped into a soldier resurrected for ruin.","themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"resurrection vocabulary turned inside out to consecrate an evil made infinite","built":"refrain chants only the name — summoning the mascot so the real horror of the trenches can speak through him"},
"sodom~lead-injection": {"haiku":"A violent aggressor wielding a gun finds pleasure in killing and blood, leaving prayers and pleas from victims as evidence of their brutal power.","opus":"A first-person portrait of a gun-wielding madman who takes pleasure in the terror and slaughter of his victims; the deeper read is the sadistic mind of a killer produced by a brutal world, treating killing as a game and mocking the prayers of those creeping and crawling as he shoots them dead.","sonnet":"A graphic first-person shooting rampage narrative — the killer experiences murder as a sexual thrill, describing bullet wounds, blood erections, and the pathetic prayers of dying victims with cold pleasure, framing the act of killing as power over those who beg gods that won't come.","src":"haiku","fable":"M-16 is Tom Angelripper's Vietnam concept record, and this track locks the listener inside a single infantryman's skull, where squeezing the trigger and sexual arousal have collapsed into one appetite. The title works as a double barrel: an injection of lead is both the round chambered and the assailant's spend, so the taunt aimed at a praying, crawling target reads as assault dressed in fatigues. Sodom refuses the war-anthem swagger and instead hands over the shooter's interior unfiltered, an ecstasy-in-slaughter confession that frames him as something a mechanized conflict engineered rather than a monster born whole. The final swerve, the dying man wishing he had a god he never kept, is the lone shaft of conscience, jeering at faith while granting it is the last thing the doomed can grasp.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder","madness & the mind"],"means":"trigger-squeeze and arousal collapsed into one appetite inside the shooter's skull","built":"the title pun double-barrelling lead injection as round chambered and assault"},
"sodom~liquidation": {"haiku":"Enemies penetrate and dissect the speaker, draining life and instilling fear; torture and death approach while the speaker finds no salvation or escape.","opus":"A cold, clinical rendering of death arriving, the body invaded and taken apart, the mind flooded with dread while the narrator's life is snuffed out in something like a ritual. Sodom, the German thrash institution, treat dying as mechanical extermination, guns and disease reducing a person to just another corpse among many.","sonnet":"Total annihilation from the inside out — an entity systematically dismantled by external force, mind eaten, blood squeezed, flame of life extinguished through ceremony. Even the possibility of escape is dissolved; the final image is the body itself turning against its own survival.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's late-1990s mode swaps the Cold War reportage of Agent Orange for pure abattoir dread, and this track is that instinct at its most surgical: extermination framed as a scheduled procedure rather than a battle. Its cruelest trick is the switch in who speaks — the verses catalogue a body being taken apart in cold second person, an executioner ticking off organs and the resistance that never arrives, before the refrain snaps to first person and the same voice becomes the one on the table. The title itself is bookkeeping language, the term for closing an account or dissolving an asset, and applying it to slaughter drains the murder of any martial romance the band once mined. What is left reads as a sacrament staged with nobody divine on the far side to receive it, ritual dressing wrapped around a plain administrative erasure.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"extermination framed as scheduled procedure rather than battle","built":"pronouns switch mid-song so executioner and victim share the same voice"},
"sodom~little-boy": {"haiku":"A child is sent to their death, possibly through nuclear bombing, with references to Hiroshima and the loss of innocence. The song questions divine authority and the cruelty of allowing children to die.","opus":"Named for the Hiroshima bomb, Sodom voicing the weapon itself over the image of a sleeping child, the obscene tenderness of an instrument delivering mass death; the closing plea borrows Dylan to ask how many innocents must die.","sonnet":"A gut-punch dual reading: on the surface a meditation on a lost, innocent child — framed with lullaby-like tenderness — but built entirely around the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima, the \"Little Boy\" of the title being the weapon itself, with the repeated question about how many more must die echoing Bob Dylan's \"Blowin' in the Wind.\"","src":"haiku","fable":"Little Boy was the codename of the uranium bomb dropped on Hiroshima, and Sodom rides that grim pun so the tender portrait of a freckled child asleep beside his teddy reads simultaneously as an armament waiting in the Enola Gay's bay. The lullaby doubles as a countdown: sunshine, untroubled dreams, then greetings dispatched toward the dead, the slumbering son and the weapon sharing one name until they fuse into a single fall toward the ground. A pilot-voice hands off the payload with monstrous paternal calm, releasing it, calling it now the child's turn, even naming the deity as the true author of the slaughter. That the piece finally hijacks the answerless questions of Dylan's Blowin' in the Wind is deliberate sabotage, wrenching folk-era pacifism into thrash so the plaintive verse about ears that cannot hear crying curdles into an indictment that the heavens were watched yet the weapon still fell.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"a lullaby and a bomb countdown sharing one codename until they fuse","built":"Dylan's pacifist chorus hijacked into thrash so the unanswered question becomes indictment"},
"sodom~m-16": {"haiku":"A soldier describes killing in a free-fire zone driven by vendetta, seeing his victims as faceless and mercy as impossible.","opus":"A soldier's desensitized confession of killing in a designated combat zone, compassion frozen out as he cuts down enemies he never even sees, fueled by grudge. German thrash band Sodom expose how unquestioned faith and muddied thinking feed the machinery of war, with tenderness torn away and shame buried rather than confronted.","sonnet":"Combat trauma and the mechanical numbness of killing in a free-fire zone — the soldier's love and mercy have been frozen out, replaced by vendetta and the cold act of shooting down lives for reasons no longer visible to him.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's Vietnam concept piece speaks from inside the infantryman, and its whole argument is the conversion of feeling into mechanism until nothing warm survives the switch. It opens with tenderness torn out at the root and a grief sealed shut, the mark of a man who has learned that emotion gets you killed, then hands him the doctrine that any moving shape inside a marked perimeter is a legitimate target regardless of who it turns out to be. Tom Angelripper anchors the horror in a serial-numbered rifle and a running tally rather than in creed, so the invoked faith and the summons under a flag read as lies dressed over a heart already dropped below the temperature where pity can form. The looped hook, gunning them to the dirt, is that same shrinkage made audible, speech pared to trigger reflex, the residue after everything human has rotted away from within.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The rolling thunder of the opening is also the bombing campaign of that name — a thread the band pulls out into a full song, Rolling Thunder, on a later record.","themes":["war & battle","dissociation & numbness","violence & murder"],"means":"feeling converted into mechanism until nothing warm survives the switch","built":"the looped hook pared to trigger reflex enacts the shrinkage it describes"},
"sodom~magic-dragon": {"haiku":"Soldiers prepare for combat under fire from the AC-47 gunship called Magic Dragon, which rains destruction on the battlefield with overwhelming firepower.","opus":"A vivid war narrative from the Vietnam perspective, where an AC-47 gunship, the nickname the title borrows, rains thousands of rounds a minute down on hidden positions while soldiers scramble with no place to hide. The technology of slaughter becomes a mythic iron monster. Sodom, German thrash veterans whose Agent Orange album made Vietnam their subject, render mechanized combat with grim, cinematic precision.","sonnet":"A Vietnam-era night battle rendered in operational detail — soldiers fighting drowsiness at a forward camp before an imminent assault, answered by the arrival of an AC-47 gunship unleashing thousands of rounds a minute. The Magic Dragon is not mythical but mechanical, a weapon of absolute fire superiority.","src":"haiku","fable":"Part of Sodom's Agent Orange, their Vietnam concept record, this reconstructs the real AC-47 gunship GIs nicknamed Puff the Magic Dragon — a cargo plane fitted with miniguns that could saturate ground at thousands of rounds a minute. The masterstroke is perspective: the verses crouch in a foxhole at Camp Duc Lap with sleepless defenders straining against the black, so the aircraft first registers as pure menace, an iron beast whose tracer streams bleach the darkness into a second noon. Only the choruses reveal, through the fixed us-against-them grammar, whose deliverance this really is, and the curt terminal reasoning about following commands and finishing the fight lets that godlike leveling power condemn itself, a nursery nickname welded to a machine that flattens whole hillsides.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief"],"means":"nursery nickname welded to a machine that flattens whole hillsides","built":"foxhole perspective makes the gunship register as menace before the chorus names its purpose"},
"sodom~marines": {"haiku":"Marines sacrifice themselves for flag and country, fighting across terrain while serving patriotic duty, though the cost is perpetual death and blood in the name of freedom.","opus":"A depiction of marines sent to war under the banner of peace, fighting and dying for others across jungles and deserts, framing hatred as a tool of control while honoring their sacrifice amid mines and gunfire.","sonnet":"A portrait of soldiers sent into combat under the banner of freedom and honour — used as tools of aggression, self-sacrificing in killing fields, stepping on mines — with a tone that is part tribute and part unflinching record of the cost.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tom Angelripper builds the track on a deliberately unstable irony: the stadium chant saluting the man who fights and bleeds for you is pinned, in the verses, to the machinery that manufactured him — medals, royal glory, and hatred named plainly as an instrument the powerful wield to steer expendable men. On M-16, Sodom's Vietnam-soaked record, the grunt becomes an interchangeable trigger-hand, sincere and disposable at once, dispatched toward carnage under a banner that dresses invasion as liberation and someone else's soil as a gift. The maneuver is to let the recruiting-poster hook and the buried explosive share one breath, so the vow that these men safeguard the planet arrives inside the same clause that condemns them. What lifts it past mere protest is its refusal to sneer at the fighter; the venom climbs the chain of command, at the throne and the lie that mobilized him, while the enlisted man stays honest and human in the killing fields, which is precisely why his death registers as robbery.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"stadium chant pinned to the machinery that manufactured the man it salutes","built":"recruiting-poster hook and buried explosive sharing one breath throughout"},
"sodom~masquerade-in-blood": {"haiku":"War and exploitation by corrupt leaders, with death and suffering presented as theatrical spectacle.","opus":"A savage indictment of warmongers and hypocrites who rain bombs on burning, screaming children with total indifference, picturing the devil riding among generals who send others off to die where no god intervenes. Sodom return to their defining subject, war stripped of any glory and exposed as faceless, industrial slaughter dressed up in false pretense.","sonnet":"War as atrocity theater — generals on horseback ordering the bombing of children while corpses pile up and the sacred is ground into the earth. The song's central accusation is hypocrisy: the warmongers and dead-ringers performing righteousness while committing genocide, protected by a god who has stopped extending protection.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's abiding subject is war stripped of glory, and here Tom Angelripper prosecutes the men who order it rather than the soldiers who die under it. The four-word chant that names the song is really an indictment list, the coined word hypochrists fusing hypocrite and Christ to pin the charge on leaders who wage slaughter under a righteous mask, which is the masquerade the title means: bloodshed dressed up as virtue. The verses keep pulling the camera back from the burning field to the commander on horseback, the general painted as a treacherous ally who murders while smiling, so the horror is never the abstract battle but the specific human authorizing it while claiming clean hands. The closing turn, that this unfolds in an era when the divine no longer shields anyone, lands the thesis: the sacred has been evacuated from the very language the warmongers still invoke, leaving only the costume.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","faith & the occult"],"means":"slaughter authorized under a righteous mask by those claiming clean hands","built":"coined word hypochrists fusing hypocrite and Christ pins the indictment in the title"},
"sodom~minejumper": {"haiku":"A soldier steps on a mine during war; the lyrics describe the trauma, violence, and psychological horror of combat through fragmented imagery of death and persecution.","opus":"German thrash channeling the psychological wreckage of war, persecution mania and shellshock, faith perverted into an excuse for killing. The soldier lives inside tormenting nightmares where violence has become the only companion.","sonnet":"A war song about a soldier's paranoid, shellshocked state — persecution mania, tormenting nightmares, dancing with the Devil — centered on the image of stepping on a landmine and the fractured consciousness that survives it. The soldier's mind is still alive even as the body is destroyed.","src":"haiku","fable":"On M-16, Sodom's Vietnam concept record, this is combat rendered as a psychic short-circuit rather than a battle report: the fractured, mistranslated grammar is the point, a mind whose language has come apart under fire. The recurring act of jumping on a mine works less as a literal event than as the soldier's fatalism made physical — an embrace of the very thing meant to kill him, war reframed as the only friend and the only path left. Tom Angelripper's shellshock imagery closes on a grim irony: the body is wrecked and the survivor is left counting what remains, yet his still-firing mind is presented not as salvation but as the sentence, the nightmares and summoned dead the price of a consciousness that refuses to switch off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","madness & the mind","suicide"],"means":"embracing the killing device as fatalism made into the soldier's only path","built":"fractured grammar enacts the psychic short-circuit rather than reporting the battle"},
"sodom~napalm-in-the-morning": {"haiku":"Fire rains from the sky and napalm burns skin and bodies to ash. The song depicts apocalyptic warfare, suffering, and the acceptance of death.","opus":"Borrowing the notorious Apocalypse Now line, Sodom render incendiary warfare in unflinching gore, bodies charred and scorched, victims burning and screaming as fire falls from above. Combat becomes an apocalypse, damnation descending on the doomed and on the numbed soldier who greets the carnage as his fate.","sonnet":"A Vietnam War grotesque built on Apocalypse Now's famous line: napalm strikes, burning flesh, and scorched bodies described with visceral detail, treating the war not as politics but as sensory annihilation.","src":"haiku","fable":"Built on M-16, Sodom's Vietnam concept album, the song weaponizes the Apocalypse Now napalm line so that Kilgore's swaggering catchphrase becomes the epigraph for its own atrocity — the smell of victory reread from inside the fire it makes. Tom Angelripper stages the strike as a perverse sacrament: the incoming firestorm is narrated in the cadence of religious prophecy (evil prophets rising, thunder as final judgment) so that the burning is dressed up as revelation, and the victim, skin sloughing away, is told the flames will redeem him fast. That is the song's cruel engine — it holds the language of salvation over a carbonized body to expose how war launders slaughter into destiny, the surrender to the gods really just surrender to gasoline and the men holding the flamethrower. The closing verse drops the mythic register for the grunt's-eye ground of the jungle, and the redemption promised earlier resolves into nothing but ash manuring the dirt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"war laundering slaughter into destiny through the language of salvation","built":"Kilgore's catchphrase weaponized as the epigraph for the atrocity it romanticised"},
"sodom~no-way-out": {"haiku":"A person seduced and addicted to a woman's touch and love realizes escape is impossible. Her tempting power traps him in a cycle of prurience and emotional dependence.","opus":"A man caught in the grip of a seductive, addictive woman whose venomous allure keeps pulling him back with no escape. The chilling final turn, as the tempting lover of his exhausted dreams becomes a figure who hands him a firearm, suggests seduction sliding toward death or suicide, a trap whose only exit is fatal.","sonnet":"A man trapped by sexual obsession with a woman who, in the final verse, passes him a gun — what begins as intoxicated desire curdles into something fatal, no way out of the feelings or what they lead him to.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about erotic dependency that has curdled into a trap, sung by a narrator who cannot tell rapture from ruin. The opening images stack the contradiction into single lines — a caress that reads as venom, a lover figured as both blessing and the leader haunting his exhausted dreams — so that desire and dread are never separable, only fused. The turn is in the tense: what was present-tense worship in the first verse returns in the past tense at the end, and the dream-leader who once merely presided now hands him the gun, so the addiction the chorus keeps insisting has no exit finally names the one exit it always meant. Sodom keep the frame deliberately squalid rather than romantic, and that grubbiness is the point: this is not love mythologized but compulsion seen clearly, the refrain hammering shut every door the verses pretend to open.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","suicide"],"means":"desire and dread fused until the exit names itself","built":"tense shift from worship to past hands him the gun"},
"sodom~nothing-counts-more-than-blood": {"haiku":"Loyalty and kinship bind through blood and death—comrades remain immortal through warfare and sacrifice in an endless cycle of violence.","opus":"German thrash on the brutal bonds of war, where loyalty to a comrade outweighs everything and the battlefield reduces men to hunters and prey. Blood means both kinship and slaughter, the two inseparable in the trenches.","sonnet":"The song moves between internal violence and battlefield carnage — the narrator is ruled by spite and twisted compulsions, but the refrain insists that loyalty between comrades (blood) is the only thing that endures.","src":"haiku","fable":"A creed of blood-loyalty gets tested by pushing it through two extremes and daring the listener to notice they rhyme. The first half inhabits a mind curdled into compulsion — pressure knotted tight inside married to loathing, an appetite made physical, faces disfigured and kept close as trophies — where devotion to a fallen comrade and the refusal to turn from God sit obscenely beside the urge to kill; the killer can't even name a reason for choosing victims, which is the point, since the ideology supplies one where sense fails. The second half swaps the murder-room for a battlefield album's home turf, the boot-slogging soldier out past the tracer-fire and the gun crews who fights without knowing what for, corpses stacked as cover and rifle-rests, hunters flipping into prey. Sodom, whose whole career runs on soldier-eye war narration, lets the shared refrain expose the con: the maxim that blood is the only thing that counts is what arms the serial killer and the conscripted soldier alike, dignifying slaughter as fidelity in both. The song isn't a defense of the creed but a diagnosis of it, the mask of brotherhood laid over the machinery that keeps the bodies coming.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","war & battle","faith & the occult"],"means":"blood-loyalty creed exposed as the logic arming killer and soldier alike","built":"shared refrain across murder-room and battlefield reveals the ideology's con"},
"sodom~nuclear-winter": {"haiku":"Nuclear annihilation and its consequences, depicting eternal darkness and the collapse of civilization.","opus":"Sodom take a scorched tour of atomic apocalypse, ash blotting out the sun for good while nations toy with weapons and bequeath death to all. A sword hangs over a planet condemned to a lingering demise, life losing out to death itself, the German thrashers rendering Cold War terror as a bitter, mocking dirge for ruin.","sonnet":"Civilisation's own greed detonates the world — the spiral staircase runs straight to annihilation, clouds of dust seal out the sun permanently, and what follows is a slow biological unravelling. The song refuses false heroics: dignity disappears, instincts corrupt into disease, and the sword of Damocles that history kept balanced finally falls.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom stages the arms race as a winding climb that reveals itself, at the top, to be a plunge: the coiling ascent toward annihilation ends at a final landing meant to open onto endless life, yet what waits there is only extinction, the summit turned into a tomb. The writing is pointedly anti-heroic, treating strategic pride as vanity — medals fastened to a nation's shoulders, war-planners feeding human souls into weapons research untroubled by guilt, the enterprise crowned by that classical blade suspended on a hair above anyone who confuses might for merit. The winter arrives not as a flash but as slow smothering, ash pulling a permanent veil across the sun so survivors are sentenced to perish by degrees. Sharpening it past routine Cold War dread is the closing inversion, longing counted as glory and death declared the winner, which reads mid-1980s deterrence as suicide costumed as conquest.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"deterrence logic reframed as suicide dressed as conquest","built":"the coiling ascent to annihilation lands as a tomb not a summit"},
"sodom~out-of-the-frontline-trench": {"haiku":"Death in warfare and the loss of identity and humanity in combat.","opus":"Speaks from the perspective of a soldier already dead, his name unknown to those who buried him, watching the living grieve at monuments while the slaughter he died in claimed everyone equally. Sodom fold their career-long obsession with warfare into an occult, spectral lament for a life erased and left uncounted.","sonnet":"A dead soldier speaking from the other side of the trench — identity annihilated by machine-gun fire, skin stripped down, name unknown to those who buried him. The song moves between the cosmic and the mundane horror of war, mourning both the individual erased and the broader impossibility of peace in a world where the killing never stops.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tom Angelripper has spent four decades writing war from the mud upward, and this late-career piece is his most unsettled attempt to speak from inside a killed soldier rather than survey the carnage from a safe remove. It opens by borrowing the sanctified public grammar of remembrance — the fallen turned toward the enemy, glory rising above the trenches — precisely so the corpse can gut that language from within, confessing that his burial party never learned who he was and packed consecrated soil over a body they mistook for a martyr. Sodom's long anti-clerical streak hardens the irony into a slogan, scriptures dumped and rifles raised, a red diadem where a saint's halo should sit, recasting the front not as a shrine to courage but as an engine that grinds a person down to rotted flesh. The drift into inherited starlight and worlds no living eye maps plays less as prophecy than as a fatally wounded brain unravelling the instant a shell arrives, so the massacre grounds themselves supply the only hereafter this song will grant. Its final refusal carries the whole weight: no route survives toward any lasting quiet, because neither the buried nor the weeping visitor at the marker receives the comfort those opening lines pretended to hold out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"the corpse guts the language of remembrance from within","built":"sanctified grammar of memorial is borrowed only to be voided by the dead"},
"sodom~outbreak-of-evil": {"haiku":"Angels die and evil demons break free from Hell to wage massacre and turn human souls into toys of demonic torment.","opus":"An early, primitive apocalypse where Satan's warriors break out of Hell to slaughter with sword and axe, angels dying amid shrill cries and no survivors. It is barely a narrative, more a chant of demonic invasion. This is Sodom at their formative extreme, one of the genre's founding blasts of blackened thrash where crude horror imagery and sheer speed mattered more than craft, an ur-text for later extreme metal.","sonnet":"Hell breaking its own gates open — demons armed and arriving, angels dying in the assault, the whole thing a rapid-fire apocalyptic image with no perspective beyond the carnage itself, more texture than narrative.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is early Sodom trading in the crudest possible currency of infernal invasion: Hell disgorges its legions, a heavenly guardian falls under swords and axes, and every soul becomes a plaything for grinning devils. The imagery is pure comic-strip apocalypse assembled from the cheapest occult signifiers, and its force lives entirely in velocity and conviction rather than in any coherent message. The refrain hurled back at the slain angel functions as a war-chant, a slogan to be screamed rather than a story to be followed. What the hammering repetition really does is plant the genre's coordinates — Satanic theater as pure noise, speed, and transgression — so the mask itself, not any hidden meaning, becomes the whole content.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"satanic theater as pure noise with no hidden meaning","built":"the war-chant refrain loops without advancing because there is nowhere to go"},
"sodom~polytoximaniac": {"haiku":"An addict declares himself dangerous and consumed by demons, indifferent to consequences as he pursues pills, hallucinogens, and oblivion to flee unbearable reality.","opus":"A brutally frank first-person account of poly-drug addiction: the narrator uses whatever others reject, can't restrain himself, blows his mind out and hallucinates (cockroaches, reflections of God) while numbly accepting that this compulsion will run 'from birth till my decay'-a death march he can't stop.","sonnet":"About addiction to multiple substances simultaneously — the narrator is a self-described polytoxic maniac who can't restrain himself, blowing his brain out through cocktails of drugs and medicine, experiencing hallucinations and moving toward a final overdose described as a golden shot.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person portrait of polydrug dependency that refuses any redemption arc: the coined title word (poly + toxico + maniac) becomes a chanted self-diagnosis, and each verse tightens the grip rather than staging a rescue. The speaker swallows whatever others reject, lies that he can never slip again, then slides through mock-casual bravado about chasing an elusive high he can taste but never quite reach. It ends in full hallucination — swarming roaches, a warped glimpse of the divine — where the deliberately lethal injection arrives as a scheduled appointment he greets almost as relief, the mind's own erasure. The force lies in that flatness: Tom Angelripper narrates the addict from within, so the hook is not a warning but the single sentence a wrecked mind can still finish, a lifelong sentence stretching from the cradle to the grave, self-knowledge that alters nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind","suicide"],"means":"self-knowledge that changes nothing, narrated from inside the wreck","built":"the coined title word becomes a chanted self-diagnosis that the song never escapes"},
"sodom~refused-to-die": {"haiku":"Demonic possession and religious trauma, with the possessed refusing spiritual death.","opus":"A lurid pageant of possession and exorcism, a victim of the devil convulsing, rising into the air, clawing at the plaster while a priest labors to expel the demons and drive a stake through her chest. Reverence for death is set against cross and blade. The German thrash veterans plunder horror-film ritual, turning demonic rite into a pummeling, profane spectacle that will not end.","sonnet":"An exorcism that is also a possession — a body convulsing, levitating, scratching walls, refusing every cleansing rite. The song catalogues the full ritual: demonic occupation, priests degraded, wooden stakes, and still the thing inside her will not leave. The refrain is not triumph but stubborn horror: exorcised, dehumanised, refused to die.","src":"haiku","fable":"A catalogue of possession and deliverance that pointedly keeps its sympathies unfixed: a woman hovering off her bed, plaster gouged by fingernails, a blunt wooden splinter driven toward a heart — the grammar of Blatty's exorcism cinema, stripped by Sodom of any Catholic reassurance. The rite is staged not as rescue but as its own atrocity. The crucifix gets traded for a blade and a torch, the sufferer is stripped of humanity on the way to being purged, and the clergyman ends up debased and expiring rather than triumphant. The title turns the screw: what will not perish is never named. It could be the demon the ceremony fails to expel or the ruined body the ceremony is killing, and Tom Angelripper keeps both alive so the ordeal plays as a war with no innocent side — the band's oldest obsession.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder"],"means":"exorcism staged as its own atrocity with no innocent side","built":"the title withholds the name of what survives so demon and body stay equally alive"},
"sodom~remember-the-fallen": {"haiku":"Tribute to soldiers who died in pointless wars at the command of despotic leaders, used as cannon fodder in battles they didn't understand or choose.","opus":"Troops mown down in a pointless war lie honored for a patriotism they never grasped, dispatched as expendable bodies by tyrannical rulers before the fighting even began. With their long fixation on warfare, the band mourn the dead while indicting the leaders who squandered them, their anguished spirits promised no peace until vengeance answers the slaughter of the innocent.","sonnet":"A meditation on soldiers sent to die in wars they did not choose or understand, sacrificed as cannon fodder by despotic leaders. Their deaths were meaningless yet demand remembrance; their restless souls may yet seek retribution for the innocent lives squandered in the name of duty.","src":"haiku","fable":"A war memorial dismantled from inside its own solemn vocabulary: the hymn borrows the language of honour and eternal rest only to ask what a pinned medal actually certifies when the man wearing it died without knowing the reason. Tom Angelripper strips the enlisted dead of the agency the ceremony pretends to grant them, reducing them to figures moved by despots and to explosives with heartbeats, the battle already lost the instant it was ordered. The final stroke is the coldest, where the buried refuse the role of quiet martyrs and rise as creditors owed for a needless death, demanding payment from the living who honour them and march the next generation toward the same blade. This is Agent Orange's whole argument compressed: not a lament for dying but a refusal of glory, denying the word hero the state's convenient laundry.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The line about brotherhood with heart and hand lifts the Deutschlandlied nearly verbatim — the national anthem's own words recited over the graves that anthem helped to fill.","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"the buried dead rise as creditors owed for a needless death","built":"solemn memorial vocabulary borrowed only to ask what a pinned medal certifies"},
"sodom~remember-the-fallen-live": {"haiku":"Soldiers sacrificed as cannon fodder by dictators wake to the betrayal only in death; their suffering and youth squandered on senseless conflict demands retaliation and remembrance.","opus":"An elegy for soldiers who died as cannon fodder in senseless wars, honoring their graves while indicting the despotic leaders who spent them. Most were puppets and children marching into a battle already lost, decorated for a patriotism they never understood. Sodom's antiwar conviction turns remembrance into accusation, mourning innocent deaths that cry out for reckoning against those who commanded them.","sonnet":"A memorial for soldiers who were cannon fodder for despotic dictators — most of them children, marched into a battle already lost before it began, awarded decorations they never understood. The song does not glorify; it mourns and indicts, framing the fallen as victims of deceit whose tormented souls cannot rest.","src":"haiku","fable":"A war memorial dismantled from inside its own solemn vocabulary: the hymn borrows the language of honour and eternal rest only to ask what a pinned medal actually certifies when the man wearing it died without knowing the reason. Tom Angelripper strips the enlisted dead of the agency the ceremony pretends to grant them, reducing them to figures moved by despots and to explosives with heartbeats, the battle already lost the instant it was ordered. The final stroke is the coldest, where the buried refuse the role of quiet martyrs and rise as creditors owed for a needless death, demanding payment from the living who honour them and march the next generation toward the same blade. This is Agent Orange's whole argument compressed: not a lament for dying but a refusal of glory, denying the word hero the state's convenient laundry.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The line about brotherhood with heart and hand lifts the Deutschlandlied nearly verbatim — the national anthem's own words recited over the graves that anthem helped to fill.","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"the buried dead rise as creditors owed for a needless death","built":"solemn memorial vocabulary borrowed only to ask what a pinned medal certifies"},
"sodom~rolling-thunder": {"haiku":"A soldier describes the desensitization and moral collapse that warfare inflicts, tracing his descent from honor to complicity in atrocities driven by violence and blind following.","opus":"The German band inhabit a soldier who has grown to adore his weapons, killing coldly yet wrestling with himself as he meets vacant stares and hears dying children. War corrupts him from within, and he comes home ruined, sworn to vengeance and swept up as one small part of an unstoppable martial force.","sonnet":"A soldier returns from war having crossed a moral threshold he can never uncross. Falling in love with the machinery of killing, watching children die, coming home desensitized — the retrospective guilt arrives too late, and the rolling thunder he was part of has already consumed him.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom take the codename of the years-long American bombing of North Vietnam and turn it inward, tracking not the campaign but the gunner who has merged with his own ordnance until intention and munition are indistinguishable. Tom Angelripper's decades-old view of war as moral corrosion, running back to Agent Orange, sharpens here into a clinical study of numbing: the speaker fires and forgets, courts the weapon like a lover, and only feels the atrocity as delayed shock, the image of children slaughtered beside their mothers arriving far too late to change anything. The recurring insistence that the listener too belongs to the storm is the cruel hinge of the piece, smearing the blame across everyone so that the slaughter can never be traced to one deranged hand and the machine grinds on unaccountable. What lingers is the stare he cannot meet in himself and a fragile honor already dissolved into limitless time, so the survivor comes home scoured empty, the capacity to wrestle with his own conscience spent before the song ends.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Operation Rolling Thunder, the 1965-68 bombing campaign, gets a whole song here — the same operation name M-16 buried inside its weather imagery, surfaced two records later.","themes":["war & battle","dissociation & numbness","violence & murder"],"means":"the gunner merges with his ordnance until intention and munition are one","built":"naming the listener as part of the storm smears blame so the machine stays unaccountable"},
"sodom~sacred-warpath": {"haiku":"War and death fill the earth; gods are inside guns, lies feed faithless minds, and crusades bring endless darkness as blood spills without mercy.","opus":"A disorienting meditation on holy war and friendly fire, where guns are worshipped as gods, the enemy looks disturbingly like the narrator, and everything moving in the dark is met with lead. War hollows out its participants until only emptiness crusades forward. Sodom, the German thrash institution, extend their career-long antiwar fixation into the confusion of a soldier who can no longer tell friend from foe.","sonnet":"War as theological condition — the guns hold gods, the book of lies is scripture, and the enemy looks identical to the soldier pulling the trigger. Everything that moves at night becomes a target regardless of allegiance, blood falls in different colors, and the warpath is called sacred precisely because it has no exit.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tom Angelripper's war-reportage instinct fixes here on the oldest engineering trick atrocity relies upon: the deity soldered into the rifle so that murder can present itself as worship. The piece hinges on the dissolution of the enemy category, since the figure squinted at down the sights turns out to wear the shooter's own face, and the man who fires learns he has been hunting a mirror all along. What the holy wrapping nourishes is not belief but a hollow, an interior nothing that the crusade stuffs with corpses while renaming that vacancy as righteousness. The cruelest stroke arrives after dark, when everything that stirs becomes meat for the round, and each fallen body pours out the identical red, dismantling the tribal fictions the campaign was peddled on — a gospel of fabrications printed for skulls that have quit believing in anything past the trigger.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","violence & murder"],"means":"the deity soldered to the rifle so murder can present as worship","built":"the enemy turns out to wear the shooter's own face, dismantling the campaign's premise"},
"sodom~schwerter-zu-pflugscharen": {"haiku":"Nazi torture and abuse of a victim who endures suffering and dreams of eventual revenge.","opus":"Sung in German, this is a survivor's cry of torture and vengeance, a victim beaten with boots, robbed of dignity, stabbed and injected with poison, haunted day and night and vowing to hunt his tormentor down until he can taste his blood and finally rest. The title, swords to plowshares, sits bitterly against that rage. Sodom voice the wounds of the abused turned implacable, peace impossible until the tyrant lies buried.","sonnet":"A survivor of abuse addressed to their tormentor in German — the title translates to swords into ploughshares but the song inverts the peace sentiment entirely, promising that peace will only come once the abuser is buried in the earth of justice. Decades of whipping, poisoning, and broken dignity fuel a patient, cold vow of retribution that will not forgive and will not forget.","src":"haiku","fable":"A torture survivor speaks, cataloguing a body that no longer belongs to him — the boots, the whip, the dagger, and the injections whose poison still burns — from inside a present where the tormentor's ghost stalks him day and night and peace stays impossible. The genius of the song is its title, which weaponizes the swords-into-plowshares verse from Isaiah that the GDR peace movement stitched onto its banners in the 1980s; here that slogan of disarmament chants over a narrator whose only route to peace runs through the grave, who promises he will hunt the abuser down and taste his blood, and can rest only once the man lies buried in the earth of justice. The mask is the pacifist creed; underneath, the song argues that for the truly broken, peace and vengeance have become the same word. Its sharpest turn indicts the bystanders — the tormentor is begotten by those who kept silent, and those who sowed the wind will reap the storm — so the reckoning is not private revenge but the return of a complicity that a peace slogan cannot cover.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","politics & society","shame & repression"],"means":"the pacifist slogan worn as a mask over a vow of vengeance","built":"the GDR peace-movement banner weaponized so peace and revenge become the same word"},
"sodom~shellfire-defense": {"haiku":"Modern warfare and military violence, questioning humanity and the justifications for killing.","opus":"A bleak takedown of the modern arms race, where advancing weaponry does the killing while governments block peace and opt for slaughter over freedom, soldiers sanctioned by law to erase others as human worth evaporates. The only prayer left is for a last bullet to end it. Sodom dissect war's machinery as a horror that feeds on itself.","sonnet":"A soldier's eye view of modern warfare as a one-sided slaughter made efficient by technology — new weapons, no chance to shoot back, and the question of who gave men these criminal instincts hanging unanswered. The song ends with a plea for disarmament and human dignity folded inside the very noise of bombardment, a contradiction it cannot resolve.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tom Angelripper's war fixation gets its coldest technocratic turn here: the song indicts modern weaponry as an efficiency machine that has rebranded slaughter as defense, the title itself an obscenity dressing annihilation in the language of protection. Its argument builds from the vanishing of concealing darkness to the vanishing of a fair fight, since newer hardware lets equipped troops mow down a foe who cannot return fire, and the sarcasm sharpens as it credits uniformed men operating under color of statute with exterminating those written off as human refuse, exposing how legality and progress launder mass killing. The prayer to be gathered into God's hands is the pivot: the only mercy left to the target is a bullet that ends the ordeal, so the desired outcome of this vaunted defense is simply a faster death. What poses as a boast about supremacy is a lament that disarmament and human dignity have been traded for a peace that is really just superior firepower.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"annihilation laundered through legality and progress as defense","built":"the title is the obscenity — slaughter dressed in the language of protection"},
"sodom~silence-is-consent": {"haiku":"Animal exploitation and whaling, with silence treated as complicity; activists fight a losing battle against commercial slaughter.","opus":"The German thrashers turn their aggression toward whale slaughter, casting activists as an outgunned rescue crew fighting a hopeless battle because staying quiet equals complicity. The harpooned giant rising with compassion in its eyes, invoked alongside Moby Dick, lends unexpected environmental conscience to a band better known for provocation, its refrain arguing that inaction makes bystanders party to the killing.","sonnet":"A defiant protest against the slaughter of whales. Fighters mount what they know is a near-hopeless rescue, armed but outmatched, refusing to stand idle while creatures whose distant songs carry grief are hunted to death — because choosing silence is itself a form of complicity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A conservationist war-cry that turns thrash's usual bloodlust inside out, planting the band's sympathy squarely with the prey instead of the killer. The crew casts itself as ocean activists shadowing a factory fleet, conceding their tiny interceptor can barely dent the tonnage of an industrial cull, yet insisting that watching in silence would make them accomplices to it. Sharpening the outrage, the great creature surfaces as a sentient thing dying with mercy still legible in its eyes, its deep calls misheard as mourning, which recasts the men behind the explosive lances as the genuine monsters afloat. The song flips the old whaling epic on its head, awarding the beast the elegy that literature has always reserved for the obsessed captain.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","nature & the elements","anger & defiance"],"means":"watching in silence made complicity, mercy legible in the dying creature's eye","built":"the old whaling epic inverted, awarding the beast the elegy reserved for the captain"},
"sodom~sodomized": {"haiku":"A perpetrator exults in sexual violence and domination, driven by cruelty and absence of compassion; the lyrics describe the act of rape from the aggressor's perspective, framed as satisfying violent pride.","opus":"A brutal first-person account of a predator driven by pride, lust, and cruelty, taking what he wants without pity or compassion. The lyrics dwell on violation, wickedness, and violence with no tenderness in the heart.","sonnet":"A narrator consumed by violent, predatory desire describes a life of cruelty and sexual aggression, presenting domination and degradation as the sole drives of existence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom write from inside a predator rather than about one, and the horror is a voice that keeps borrowing the vocabulary of romance — desire, tenderness, a heart full of love — to narrate rape and murder while wanting only domination for its own sake. On 1994's Get What You Deserve the band swapped their war-cycle grandiosity for deliberately ugly street-level nastiness, and here the speaker presents himself as fixed from birth onward, a nature nobody ever managed to bend, so the atrocity reads as settled identity rather than lapse. The cruelest stroke is the borrowed comfort of that final phrase, sleep of the just, which files the killer among the righteous — the violence is stated flatly, and what chills is the total absence of any interior objection to it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"atrocity stated flatly with no interior objection to it","built":"romantic vocabulary borrowed to narrate rape and murder while the horror stays unacknowledged"},
"sodom~soul-contraband": {"haiku":"A person presents themselves as destiny and judge, dismissing prayer and hope while condemning multitudes to suffering and death through rage.","opus":"Sodom cast the narrator as an apocalyptic, godless power controlling every move and watching a shattered people perish, their pleas sinking unanswered. The thrash veterans push their anti-war anger into something cosmic and merciless, the speaker declaring himself fate itself and daring the doomed to cry out for a spirit already stolen from them.","sonnet":"A voice that identifies as godless and unwanted positions itself as the destroyer of false hope. Millions follow and are lost, nations collapse in silence, and any prayer directed upward goes unheard — the soul itself is contraband in a world where meaning has been permanently revoked.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person power sings the whole song, and the trick is that it never names itself. It boasts that the planet trembles beneath it, that a target's every gesture already belongs to it, and it carries the title's freight of a soul smuggled past its rightful owner — war as an appetite that has swallowed its own moral vocabulary. Sodom stack the abstractions in blunt telegraph bursts, dropping the word for being saved and the word for being damned into the same flat list as the verbs of striking and razing, so the categories a person might pray with are shown as interchangeable junk. Then the middle verse widens from the boast to the wreckage: crowds march after the voice and drop dead in silence, no last cry left, while any counsel that might have warned them is refused at the door. What holds the read together is that Angelripper never lets the voice repent — the weather massing at the world's edge is a promise, not a warning — so the piece works as the enemy's own liturgy, the mind of annihilation caught admiring itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder","faith & the occult"],"means":"annihilation narrates itself without ever repenting","built":"the voice never names itself, smuggling past confession"},
"sodom~sow-the-seeds-of-discord": {"haiku":"One person sows conflict and hatred while gaining dominance through psychological warfare; they are a poison that infects and corrupts everything around them.","opus":"A blunt warning against a warmonger who has mastered the strategy of conflict and is bound to reap the death he plants. The narrator swears to defend himself against the threat. Sodom, German thrash veterans, distill their antiwar streak into proverb-driven condemnation, where what benefits one man destroys another.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a cynical warmonger who has mastered conflict as craft — he poisons every alliance, drives nails into communal coffins, and reaps death from the discord he plants. The response is defiant: we will not lie down, we have our own weapon, and we will not bite the dust on his terms.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom weaponize the old proverb about harvest and consequence into a curse aimed squarely at the warmonger — the figure who has mastered conflict as a game, a cynic scattering strife the way a farmer scatters grain, only the crop here ripens into death. The verses frame the indictment as pathology and contagion, a disease incubated and a spiteful fluid seeping out, so discord reads not as accident but as something deliberately cultivated and then left for everyone else to inherit. The final turn flips the refrain's fatalism into defiance: rather than lie down and let the sower's poison finish them, the band answer with a weapon of their own, insisting the reckoning will fall on the hand that planted it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","war & battle","anger & defiance"],"means":"the warmonger's sown discord ripens into a reckoning aimed back","built":"the harvest proverb weaponized into a curse that flips from fatalism to defiance"},
"sodom~spiritual-demise": {"haiku":"Existential crisis and spiritual emptiness; a man with no true nature or identity waits for death while invoking false gods and experiencing corrosive hatred.","opus":"Sodom pursue a nihilist reverie of a man born without identity, longing for death as deliverance and greeting Satan as the one who carries him off. Occult lore, sham prophets and a soul rotting from within reduce living itself to slow decay. The German thrashers stage damnation not as terror but as a serene surrender.","sonnet":"A soul stripped of identity and name drifts toward obliteration. False gods and racial myths corrode from within; hate fills what conviction once held, and the narrator — nameless, natureless — welcomes Satan's embrace as the only honest exit from a world built on spiteful symbols and self-deception.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person account of a mind hollowing itself into a vessel for whatever demands worship, with Sodom keeping that object deliberately smeared. The narrator, denatured and unnamed, craves his own extinction, while the surrounding imagery fuses scriptural revelation with parade-ground idolatry so that prophet and dictator become interchangeable. That collision is the argument: Tom Angelripper's suspicion that the ecstatic urge to kneel, whether to a heaven, a strongman, or a promised nothingness, is one reflex swapping costumes, souring here from rapture into blood-slick perversion before dressing surrender up as a gentle twilight. The rot named in the title is a spiritual one because the speaker mistakes self-erasure for arrival home, reading annihilation as enlightenment poured down from somewhere higher.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","suicide","politics & society"],"means":"self-erasure mistaken for arrival, annihilation read as enlightenment","built":"prophet and dictator made interchangeable so the ecstatic urge to kneel is one reflex"},
"sodom~stigmatized": {"haiku":"Crucifixion-like suffering and social stigma, with religious imagery depicting victimization and desperation.","opus":"Sodom collide crucifixion with modern brutality, nails driven through hands, a victim slain by a sanctified hand yet also impaled on a digital cross, branded and beyond cure. The stigmata become a mark of desperation and propaganda's concealed violence, the German thrashers turning holiness itself into another mode of torture and disfigurement.","sonnet":"Crucifixion as a template for all violent marking — nails, blood, sealed fate — turned outward into a meditation on those branded by power: patriots, tyrants, souls who will never rest. The song stacks the imagery of stigma without resolving who bears it, leaving the speaker crucified on something virtual and incurably infected by a system with no exit.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom weaponize the stigmata, the crucifixion wounds of the venerated saint, into a figure for coerced martyrdom, where sanctity is indistinguishable from torture inflicted by a hooded executioner. The first half stages the Passion as pure violence with no redemption waiting on the far side: driven spikes, a fate already locked, gore soaking innocent earth, a monstrous torment to flee rather than a paradise to enter. Then the song turns outward, yoking the spilled blood of loyalists and despots alike to the unmasking of whatever official lies conceal, so the private agony goes political, the sufferer manufactured, the prophecy self-fulfilling, contrition drilled in rather than felt. The closing litany of one-word verdicts, foulness and flaw, a man nailed to a phantom scaffold, a soul branded beyond any cure, collapses the holy and the abject into a single mark, and the four-fold chant of the title stops being narration and becomes the brand itself, driven in by repetition the way the spikes were driven into flesh.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"sanctity and torture collapsed into a single coerced mark","built":"the four-fold chant of the title stops being narration and becomes the brand driven in by repetition"},
"sodom~storm-raging-up": {"haiku":"A vengeful force builds to unleash destruction and fire on the world, motivated by hatred and the end of spiritual oppression through violence and war.","opus":"Sodom channel ungovernable rage into a fantasy of self-deification, with a narrator who proclaims himself a god as he watches lives snuffed out. Unable to contain his fury and endless torment, he prepares to burn the world down, rejects society's rules with love long since abandoned, and launches an endless war marching toward a glorified death.","sonnet":"Hate and loss have fused into a force that cannot be governed — love is buried somewhere underneath rubble the speaker cannot reach, and what remains is a consuming rage ready to ignite the world. The storm is internal as much as external, divine appointment claimed through destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person apocalypse in Tom Angelripper's mouth: the speaker welds himself to the burning gale and declares he will be your god, the standard thrash escalation of wrath into world-ending scale. What lifts it above pure destruction is the mask the song admits it is wearing — a permanent shroud of dusk pulled over the features — and the late confession that a love lies buried underneath, so the rampage reads as grief weaponized rather than mere nihilism. The chorus keeps rising because it can't resolve; the fury has no end precisely because the thing it's avenging can't be recovered.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","death & grief","war & battle"],"means":"world-ending fury revealed as weaponized grief beneath the shroud","built":"chorus keeps rising without resolving because the lost thing cannot be recovered"},
"sodom~strange-lost-world": {"haiku":"Religious and material hierarchies fail as believers become prey; the narrator observes a world severed from reality and truth.","opus":"Sodom mocking blind religious faith from the vantage of the executioner, the believer trapped and disillusioned as their god proves an apparition. It's a caustic meditation on holy war, where the hunter of the faithful becomes prey.","sonnet":"About the disorientation of a world where religion has failed its followers, violence is ambient, and the powerful exploit the faithful — the narrator watches the holy hunter become prey in a place where belief offers no refuge.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's Tom Angelripper voices this from inside the modern soldier's contempt for a faith-driven foe, so the cruelty is the vantage point rather than the verdict: the speaker names his own killing capricious even while committing it. The wealth-versus-ammunition boast carries the whole song, one side stocked with money and hardware, the other with bodies and a promised afterlife, the war being that imbalance costumed as righteousness on either flank. The lost world is where a martyr who courts his own end has slipped past every deterrent, leaving the speaker's warnings pointless against a mind that no reality can still reach. The final reversal has the pursuer dropped by his target, the executioner conceding he is himself a zealot for this campaign, one bullet aimed as much at scouring the conflict from his own skull. It is an antiwar dirge disguised as a body-count swagger, arraigning the numbed machinery from behind the trigger.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","dissociation & numbness","faith & the occult"],"means":"executioner concedes he is the same zealot","built":"antiwar argument hidden inside a body-count swagger"},
"sodom~styptic-parasite": {"haiku":"A malevolent parasitic entity spreads evil from within the speaker's soul, killing hope and embodying apocalyptic destruction.","opus":"A predatory evil grows inside the narrator, imagining itself a destroying god while a homicidal urge, fed by long years of want, festers within. Sodom render psychological decay as a bloodthirsty organism hiding unseen, thriving in the worst corner of the mind as a wrecked history reclaims him.","sonnet":"A figure consumed by ancient hatred and violence describes himself as a predatory infection — unseen, corrosive, sovereign from darkness. Years of deprivation and denial have curdled into a killer instinct that spreads outward, overwhelming everything, emptying itself into the world as pure destructive force.","src":"haiku","fable":"Angelripper opens in the standard thrash posture of the annihilating first person — a self-declared god burning out a victim's nest until hope is useless — but the title is the tell that undercuts the swagger. A styptic parasite is a contradiction: a bloodsucker that also clots, one that seals the very wound it feeds through so it can stay unseen and survive. The song's real movement is the collapse of the outward threat into an inward one. Midway through, the enemy being terminated turns out to be lodged in the speaker's own soul, and the vocabulary drops its menace for a pain lodged too deep to name, for crying and denials — the urge to kill exposed as a coping wound that keeps reopening the years it was born from. What poses as a conqueror's boast is really a portrait of a hatred that has learned to feed on its host without ever quite killing him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","violence & murder"],"means":"conqueror's boast collapses midway into the inward wound it was masking","built":"title contradiction — a bloodsucker that clots — maps the song's turn from threat to self-portrait"},
"sodom~suicidal-justice": {"haiku":"War and hatred turn a person into an instrument of death; they become a vessel receiving commands to kill, with their mind switched off by combat horror.","opus":"A grim study of a soldier rewired by combat into a machine for mass killing, commanded to slaughter from above with his conscience shut down. What he unleashes is at once revenge and self-destruction. Sodom steer their trademark war obsession into psychological ruin, casting sanctioned killing as a verdict that devours the executioner along with his victims.","sonnet":"Psychic annihilation framed as military command — a mind that has received orders from somewhere higher to destroy itself, watching enemies crawl while simultaneously executing its own capacity for survival. The war outside has colonized the interior completely, and what remains is the collective instinct to end.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's recurring figure is the soldier as an emptied vessel refilled with someone else's orders, and here that refilling gets rebranded as a verdict handed down for a crime never committed. The title welds a death sentence to the language of righteousness, so that erasing yourself arrives disguised as duty — a directive to murder issued from up the chain of command yet mistaken for prophecy. What marks this as Sodom and not stock gore is the pivot at the end: the same throat that swallowed its own annihilation as obedience grows fangs the moment it can, mutilating cowards and feeding them to dogs, which is the whole argument — the martyr and the butcher are one appetite, not opposing roles. Trench warfare is drawn as a foundry that stamps out both the willing corpse and the sadist from a single conscripted mind, and the true charge is that this shared craving for extinction is what armies actually train.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder","suicide","surveillance & control"],"means":"martyr and butcher revealed as one appetite","built":"title fuses death sentence with the language of righteousness"},
"sodom~surfin-bird": {"haiku":"Pure nonsense and repetition celebrating a bird as a cultural meme. The lyrics are absurdist, emphasizing the repetition of a single phrase.","opus":"A thrash-metal cover of the daft 1960s garage novelty, the whole point being the absurdity of German extreme-metal veterans bellowing pure nonsense. There's nothing to read but the joy of the joke and the noise.","sonnet":"A cover of the classic novelty song built entirely around the nonsense proclamation that \"the bird is the word,\" with no interpretable narrative beyond the gleeful, relentless repetition of that phrase and accompanying scat syllables.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric is famously empty on its face — a stuttered nonsense mantra insisting the bird is the word, welding the Trashmen's two Rivingtons novelties into pure incantation that means nothing and repeats forever. What Sodom do is drop that idiot loop into M-16, a Vietnam concept record, where the mindless drill of pa-pa-pa and ooma-mow-mow stops reading as surf-party fun and starts sounding like the manic radio chatter and rote sloganeering of men whose actual thought has been drilled out of them. The refusal of the words to develop is the point: a whole culture chanting one meaningless syllable in unison, everybody having heard about the bird because that is all there is left to hear.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","dissociation & numbness","madness & the mind"],"means":"meaningless refrain as stand-in for a drilled-out mind","built":"nonsense mantra transplanted to a Vietnam record to indict rote conditioning"},
"sodom~tarred-and-feathered": {"haiku":"Medieval and inquisitorial torture and execution, with innocent victims brutalized by authority.","opus":"A blistering account of the Inquisition and witch persecutions, where monarchs abuse faith as a pretext to torment the guiltless, mutilating and executing accused women while elites treat the horror as spectacle. Sodom often build thrash from genuine historical atrocity, and here they detail the apparatus of sanctioned religious cruelty as a scathing condemnation of state-blessed barbarism.","sonnet":"The Inquisition as a catalogue of sadistic spectacle — red-haired women sent to the pillory, bodies broken on the wheel, crowds entertained by the stench of burning. The song holds the age of inquisition responsible for all misanthropy that followed, tracing a straight line from kings extracting amusement from torture to the spiritual wreckage inherited by the present.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom drags the inquisition out as a spectacle of state-sanctioned cruelty, and the sharpest move is where it points blame: not at devils but at crowned rulers who hand down corrupt creeds to license torture as public amusement. The verses catalogue the machinery of it, pillory, breaking-wheel, stake, quartering, while the throne watches the scenery as entertainment, so the real horror is the leisure of the powerful, not the crime of the condemned. The chorus warning that belief will be stripped away cuts both ways: the tortured heretic loses conviction under the fire, but so should anyone watching an institution that murders in God's name. By the time the title phrase is chanted down to nothing, the ritual has hollowed out into bare repetition, which is the band's verdict, that this was never justice, only a habit of violence performing itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","politics & society","faith & the occult"],"means":"torture exposed as a habit of power performing justice","built":"chorus repetition hollows the ritual into bare mechanical violence"},
"sodom~that-s-what-an-unknown-killer-diarized": {"haiku":"A serial killer fantasizes about murder and control with psychotic devotion; he feels no compunction while documenting his desires for lacerated skin and death.","opus":"The German thrash veterans voice the confession of a serial murderer who records his crimes in a journal, treating the blood he sheds as a kind of embrace. Lust and killing fuse as he lures a trusting victim to a secret spot, owning his guilt yet logging it like devotion. The image of him proudly retiring to write up his deeds frames atrocity as an authored legacy rather than remorse.","sonnet":"A serial killer's diary rendered in first person — seductive, methodical, and devoid of remorse. The act of killing is framed in the language of desire and devotion, the final entry a chilling domesticity as the victim follows trustingly into an unseen place.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's engine is its frame: it presents itself as pages from a killer's private journal, and everything nasty in it is filtered through the borrowed vocabulary of love poetry — embrace, champagne, sweetheart — so that the tenderness is the horror rather than its relief. That fusion is the argument: the speaker feels no remorse because he has genuinely eroticized the act, and Sodom lets the diary voice stay lyrical exactly where a confession should turn to guilt. The one flat narrative moment, a woman lured to an isolated spot she has no idea awaits her, is where the mask slips and the courtship reveals itself as a trap. By the last verse the point closes on itself — he means to gather the fruits of death as trophies and set them into the record, which makes the whole text we have just heard that very ledger, the recurring title no longer a label but the killer archiving himself for a reader he assumes will come.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind","artifice & performance"],"means":"tenderness as the vehicle of horror rather than its relief","built":"diary frame makes the text itself the killer's trophy ledger"},
"sodom~the-art-of-killing-poetry": {"haiku":"Hate and misanthropy weaponize beauty, stripping skin and light to leave only darkness.","opus":"Sodom recast brutality as a twisted craft, blurring any line between hatred and love and wearing cruelty like a chosen faith and a self-harm. The narrator boasts of triumph in inflicting ruin and decay, elevating destruction born of contempt for humanity into a grim aesthetic that deliberately spreads darkness over anything good.","sonnet":"Misanthropy elevated to aesthetic doctrine. A speaker who wears corruption as a mask strips language and beauty down to their nerve endings, presenting destruction and decay not as aberrations but as the only honest art form available to someone who has discarded all human sentiment.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is the whole argument: a boast that annihilation is a craft, poetry itself the thing being murdered, with misanthropy named openly as the muse. The verses supply the technique — frost that burns without flame, hatred declared indistinguishable from your love — each line a deliberately ornate atrocity, so that the song stages the very paradox it announces, using the beauty of imagery to prove that beauty can be weaponized into cruelty. The image of a false creed worn like a mask catches Angelripper's real target: the pose of piety pulled over a wound the wearer opened himself, faith as a costume for damage. What keeps this from mere shock is that the speaker frames destruction as skill and calls the result a triumph with nothing diluting it, so the horror is the confidence — the man who has learned to make ugliness elegant and counts it a win.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance","faith & the occult"],"means":"destruction framed as craft with nothing diluting the confidence","built":"ornate imagery used to prove beauty can be weaponized — staging the paradox it announces"},
"sodom~the-saw-is-the-law": {"haiku":"A chainsawman commits brutal murders, with the saw becoming the governing principle of his violent actions that destroy everything in its path.","opus":"One of Sodom's signature anthems, tracking a chainsaw-wielding maniac whose nighttime killings make his weapon the only rule he obeys, cutting a trail of slaughter through his victims. The German thrashers close on a grim twist as the killer, weeping and armed, turns on himself, wringing pulp-horror menace out of relentless butchery.","sonnet":"A chainsaw killer stalks the night, hiding carnage behind a quiet exterior until his rites of violence consume him entirely. The story ends in tears and a gun, the chaos externalised by the blade finally turning inward, leaving only the grim epitaph that the instrument of his killing was always his only law.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom build a slasher-movie villain and then, in the last verse, refuse to leave him a monster. For most of the song the Chainsawman is pure horror-cinema iconography — the nocturnal hunter, the ritualized killing rendered as a cosmic edict where his weapon becomes the only law that governs. But the closing turn undercuts the myth: the same figure ends weeping with a gun in his hand, his invincibility collapsing into a wretched suicide, and the read pivots on that image because it drags the cartoon back into a human ending nobody in the genre grants him. The final chant flipping the saw and the law into each other seals the irony — the rule he lived by was never a law at all, just a compulsion buried in a sick mind that finally consumed the man who wielded it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind","suicide"],"means":"slasher myth dismantled by a human and wretched ending","built":"final chant inverting saw and law collapses the rule into compulsion"},
"sodom~the-sin-of-sodom": {"haiku":"Biblical destruction rains fire and sulfur upon depravity and sin, with divine wrath punishing the guilty while innocence perishes in the same devastation.","opus":"A retelling of the biblical city's destruction by fire and brimstone as divine revenge on a hateful, guilty race; the thrash bombast treats the apocalypse as spectacle, love replaced by hate until God levels everything in a day.","sonnet":"A retelling of the biblical destruction of Sodom, framing divine wrath as a response to human corruption and moral chaos, with sulfurous annihilation as the inescapable verdict on a guilty civilization.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's re-cut of their earliest demo material sets the Genesis obliteration of the cities to raw German thrash, and the intrigue lies in whose corner the song stands. It parades the familiar players — Abraham pleading, Lot ushered out, brimstone falling within a single day — yet slyly imports the blasphemous lexicon of black metal into scripture, so an inverted star climbs over the doomsday plain and divine sentencing runs like an extermination engine crushing the guilty. The thesis rides that collision: heavenly fury is read not as virtue but as annihilation impossible to tell apart from hellfire, the Almighty cast as the supreme butcher who trades tenderness for spite and torches a world out of wounded pride. By the final verse — the daughters left carrying their own father's children in the cave — the band declines to let the spared stay pure, folding rescue and rot into one blaze whose only liberty is the soul flung loose by total ruin.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"divine fury made indistinguishable from infernal annihilation","built":"black-metal lexicon grafted onto scripture so damnation and hellfire share one face"},
"sodom~the-vice-of-killing": {"haiku":"A psychotic killer with a crippled, traumatized soul feeds on the despair and screams of victims, their moral depravity incurable.","opus":"Sodom dissect a murderer as a clinical specimen, a rabid figure whose broken psyche was damaged long ago and lies beyond any cure. He recoils from human remains yet treats destruction as fuel, sees beauty itself as sacrilege, and, cut off from anything normal, hungers for his victims' terror as a kind of dark worship.","sonnet":"A psychological portrait of a compulsive killer whose soul was irreparably damaged in the past — trauma calcified into predatory drive, therapy useless, the craving for harm as familiar as hunger. He exists outside normality, feeding on the despair of victims he treats as fuel.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom stage a forensic portrait of the compulsive murderer, and the title's pun does the arguing: killing is at once his moral failing and the workbench clamp that fixes him in place, an appetite that tightens rather than a decision. The band presents this as clinical disorder, not chosen wickedness — the wiring was broken in childhood, counseling can gain no purchase, and the man is diagnosed as unwell rather than sentenced as sinful. Where their war songs lay guilt on regimes and doctrines, the dread here is that no culprit exists, only a brain rerouted beyond repair that draws its fuel from others' suffering because nothing else nourishes it. The chorus's blunt recycling of the untreatable performs the trap it names: growth is barred when the subject can no longer grow.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind"],"means":"murder framed as clinical disorder with no culprit to sentence","built":"chorus's blunt repetition performs the untreatable trap it names"},
"sodom~the-wolf-the-lamb": {"haiku":"Predator-prey dynamics and abusive relationships, with violence and exploitation as natural hierarchy.","opus":"A dark study of domestic predation, following a psychologically shattered woman abused by a nocturnal aggressor, the damage surfacing as physical injury. Love here collapses into violent ownership. Sodom, the German thrash mainstays, set aside their usual war themes for intimate cruelty, the predator and its victim standing in for an inescapable cycle of harm.","sonnet":"A predator and victim locked together — emotional stunting and mental abuse feeding a dynamic where love and destruction are the same act. The wolf hunts in darkness, the lamb never prays in time, and the song offers no rescue, only the image of two figures drifting in a sea of blood, the outcome already settled.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tom Angelripper takes the old predator-prey parable and strips its comfort, exposing an intimate cruelty in which affection and destruction share one hand and the first opening becomes an execution. The woman is drawn as a psyche stunted at the root, her sense of self curdled by someone who taught her to distrust her own mirror, while her hunter treats her as carnal property to be claimed after dark. What sharpens the piece is its refusal of the parable's alibi: calling the two combatants natural kinds pretends fixed instinct where there is only decided harm, a spine snapped and a future padlocked shut. In the closing image the drowning consciousness stops registering the reproaches thrown at it, and the plea to be finished is finally honored — proof the hunter's ear was sealed all along, guaranteeing the pattern outlives them both.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","shame & repression","love & desire"],"means":"predator-prey parable stripped of its alibi of natural instinct","built":"closing image of the drowning consciousness confirms the hunter's ear was sealed from the start"},
"sodom~through-toxic-veins": {"haiku":"A person surrenders to dark desire and decay, seduced and infected by corruption flowing through intimate psychological bondage.","opus":"Grinding, feverish imagery of surrender to a poisonous craving that dissolves the soul, addiction and decay fused into one seductive rot. The toxic veins carry both the elixir and the death it delivers.","sonnet":"A poisonous attraction that reads as addiction or violent obsession — the narrator is 'raped and caressed' by whatever has gotten into their veins, carnivorous imagery piling up as the toxic substance dissolves all boundaries between desire and destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction song where the seducer is a poison in the bloodstream: the tempting kiss, the pathogen, the snake, the black draught passed from a killing vial all name one thing that enters the veins and dissolves the self from inside, so that desire and infection become the same event. Angelripper never lets you decide whether the you being succumbed to is a lover, a drug, or a disease, and that undecidability is the point — the speaker is pinned by a violating grip and stroked tenderly in the same breath, faith trapped inside the thing that is rotting him. Around this runs Sodom's other native register, the battlefield of frontline and mob and vivisection, so the intimate contamination and the wartime butchery are made to rhyme, both of them bodies opened and reanimated as meat. What looks like gore-for-gore's-sake is really a single controlling image held to the end — devotion as a substance you cannot stop taking even as it embalms you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","war & battle"],"means":"devotion and infection made indistinguishable as a single event","built":"intimate contamination and wartime butchery rhymed by the same controlling image"},
"sodom~til-death-do-us-unite": {"haiku":"Death as final judgment and reunion, with the dying speaker witnessing mourning, hatred from foes, and the interplay of lies and divine justice.","opus":"Sodom voice a narrator shot dead who watches his own funeral unfold, foes spitting on his grave and false friends offering hollow prayers. The wedding-vow phrasing binds him to death as an inescapable union, dignity broken yet defiant. Fitting a band steeped in war and mortality, the lyric surveys human hypocrisy from beyond the grave, cursed and blessed for eternity alike.","sonnet":"A dead man surveys the world from the far side of a bullet wound. He watches mourners weep and enemies curse his grave, sees through the falseness of prayers and social rituals, and finds in death the one true unifier — the moment that strips away all hypocrisy and brings everyone, at last, together.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song narrates its own death from the inside of the fatal instant — the bullet, the pulse stopping, the light — and then, impossibly, keeps watching the aftermath: the wilting graveside flowers, the friends begging heaven, the enemies spitting and praying him into hell. That split is the point. Sodom turns the wedding vow of the title inside out, so that the union promised is not with a beloved but with death itself, and the mourners and haters ringing the grave are exposed as equally false — the tears and the curses are both performance over a body that can no longer be flattered or wounded. The final verse lands the thesis: hearing through the coffin lid the prayers and lies as one indistinguishable noise, the speaker concludes that the same God blesses and damns everyone alike, which drains salvation of any meaning and leaves the endlessly hammered refrain as the only honest sacrament — everyone, friend and foe, married off to the same end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"wedding vow inverted so the union is with death itself","built":"narrator watches his own funeral from inside the coffin, collapsing mourner and enemy into one indistinguishable noise"},
"sodom~tired-and-red": {"haiku":"A condemned person faces various methods of execution—gunshot, stabbing, electrocution—experiencing pain and fear until death.","opus":"A first-person tour through methods of execution, the narrator imagining himself shot, stabbed, and electrocuted in turn, narrating each death as it arrives. Sodom render dying as clinical spectacle, the grim litany driving home an obsession with violence carried out to the point of the body giving up entirely.","sonnet":"Three modes of violent death — gunshot, stabbing, electrocution — are narrated in sequence from the position of the victim, each one clinical and final. The refrain is the body's last testimony: tired, bleeding, oath-bound to the moment of extinction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A catalogue of executions in the first person, each verse handing the speaker a different instrument of death — the firing squad, the knife, the electric chair — and killing him again from the inside of the wound. The structure is the argument: by cycling one consciousness through incompatible deaths, Sodom drains the specifics of any single method and leaves only the constant, the refrain's exhaustion, as if dying is a shift that never ends. Tired and red names the residue of all of it at once — the fatigue of a body worn out by being killed and the blood that is the only thing each verse shares — so the chorus reads less as a threat than as a numbed report from someone who has stopped distinguishing between the ways he goes. Underneath the shock-catalogue is the era's cold fixation on state and mechanized killing that runs through Agent Orange, the human reduced to the thing the trigger, blade, or switch acts upon.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness","violence & murder"],"means":"dying recycled as a shift that never ends","built":"one consciousness cycled through incompatible deaths to drain each method of meaning"},
"sodom~tired-and-red-live": {"haiku":"Graphically rendered execution, resigned acceptance of death's inevitability and methodical cruelty.","opus":"A methodical thrash catalogue of execution methods, gun, knife, electric chair, told from the victim's perspective as a bleak protest against state killing and the mechanized cruelty of dying by human design.","sonnet":"An unflinching catalog of deaths — gunshot, stabbing, electric chair — each described in graphic physical detail from the perspective of the dying person; the refrain \"tired and red\" captures the body's final state across all three methods; it's a brutal meditation on how different ways to be killed all end the same way.","src":"haiku","fable":"A catalogue of executions in the first person, each verse handing the speaker a different instrument of death — the firing squad, the knife, the electric chair — and killing him again from the inside of the wound. The structure is the argument: by cycling one consciousness through incompatible deaths, Sodom drains the specifics of any single method and leaves only the constant, the refrain's exhaustion, as if dying is a shift that never ends. Tired and red names the residue of all of it at once — the fatigue of a body worn out by being killed and the blood that is the only thing each verse shares — so the chorus reads less as a threat than as a numbed report from someone who has stopped distinguishing between the ways he goes. Underneath the shock-catalogue is the era's cold fixation on state and mechanized killing that runs through Agent Orange, the human reduced to the thing the trigger, blade, or switch acts upon.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness","violence & murder"],"means":"dying recycled as a shift that never ends","built":"one consciousness cycled through incompatible deaths to drain each method of meaning"},
"sodom~tombstone": {"haiku":"Gunfights and vendettas erupt in a Wild West setting where opposing factions clash in ruthless conflict, ultimately ending at the OK Corral.","opus":"Sodom map their thrash aggression onto Western myth, retelling the O.K. Corral gunfight and lacing it with jabs at partisan politics. In their telling, disputes get settled only when gunfire decides guilt, and a slain brother ignites a vendetta that won't stop until it's finished. The legend becomes a frame for hatred, revenge and violence that no one can escape.","sonnet":"Vendetta at the O.K. Corral, framed as both historical Western showdown and political allegory — a brother's murder demands blood repayment, the protagonists ride toward a reckoning with men who hid behind law, the gunfight in Tombstone standing in for any moment where grievance finally runs out of patience.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom stage the 1881 shootout in the Arizona dust town as a machine that manufactures righteousness from bloodshed, where the Earps' badge and the Clantons' grudge collapse into one indistinguishable appetite for killing. The Leone wink (the good, the bad, and the ugly) exposes the frontier as pure fable, and Tom Angelripper splices in the partisan feud of Washington to argue that the same rigged justice merely swapped its spurs for suits. What poses as retribution is really a private grudge given a rifle and a moral alibi, the horse chase after a wounded brother renamed order. Hammered four times, the marker names the only verdict everyone marching onto that lot can expect.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"frontier justice and political vengeance revealed as the same rigged appetite","built":"Leone wink exposes the shootout as pure fable, then spliced to Washington to stretch the verdict forward"},
"sodom~unbury-the-hatched": {"haiku":"Violent revolution demands overthrowing tyranny; the oppressed must resurrect aggression and punish despots.","opus":"An anti-war denunciation of commanders who play lethal games beyond their grasp, casting the whole planet as their foe. The rallying cry to dig up the buried axe turns scriptural, casting down the powerful from their seats and lifting the humble, while military rule promises a reckoning to come.","sonnet":"A war-themed thrash metal song about uprising against corrupt, warmongering rulers — generals play chess with lives, a despotic tyrant measures himself against God, and the speaker calls for the hatchet to be dug up and the mighty to be thrown from their seats, framing revolution as a biblical act of exaltation for the low.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title flips the peace idiom on its head: not burying the hatchet but digging it back up, war deliberately exhumed by generals who treat the world like a chessboard and call it strategy. Sodom, writing in the shadow of the Gulf War, lets the song's own moral outrage curdle — the chorus borrows the Magnificat's promise to cast down the mighty and lift up the lowly, but here that scripture of divine justice becomes a bloodthirsty rallying cry, exalting the humble only so they can spill the tyrant's blood in turn. The closing verse punctures any heroism: the summary executions under wartime law are confessed haltingly, as something hard to say, and the promise of Hell to pay lands not as triumph but as the recognition that toppling one strongman just restarts the cycle. The song's real subject is that overthrow and atrocity wear the same face.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","violence & murder"],"means":"overthrow and atrocity shown wearing the same face","built":"Magnificat scripture borrowed as bloodthirsty rallying cry, turning divine justice into vengeance"},
"sodom~vaginal-born-evil": {"haiku":"Evil and violence are portrayed through apocalyptic imagery—war, brutality, and divine punishment. The song fixates on corruption and destructive power.","opus":"Sodom heap on the provocation, casting creation itself as corrupted and something malevolent yet divine, driven by greed and bent on destroying peace. Ritual killing, execution by supposedly holy hands, and persecution sketch a world drowning in suffering and madness. A closing insistence that truth will win in the end lends the carnage a defiant frame.","sonnet":"A death-metal theology of violence as sacred inheritance — armies, swords, ritual execution, and holy war framed as forces born into existence at the moment of creation itself. The world is already in a state of brutal collapse; the only response is to meet it with equal force.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's blunt thesis is that cruelty arrives with the newborn rather than corrupting an otherwise decent species, so bloodshed becomes an inheritance carried out of the womb, never a lapse a person stumbles into. The verses catalogue sanctioned atrocity — beheadings, killing by thrown rock, persecution dressed as piety — to indict the way faith licenses slaughter, turning worship and murder into one gesture behind two masks. That refrain refuses the listener any clean line between the holy and the horrific, since Tom Angelripper's curdled deadpan treats reverence and butchery as one appetite that was never divisible in the first place. The final image, a doomed figure gunned down yet somehow left upright while a hissing reptile of flame endures every attempt to burn it away, lands as bleak endurance instead of victory, the rot proving impossible to purge because it was never a foreign thing to be beaten. What outlasts everyone, then, is only the machinery of harm repeating itself, a grim continuity the band presents as the sole durable truth left standing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult"],"means":"cruelty presented as womb-born inheritance rather than human lapse","built":"closing image of inextinguishable flame figures rot as something never foreign enough to purge"},
"sodom~visual-buggery": {"haiku":"Media and politics corrupt society through spectacle and violence. Viewers remain complicit by ignoring atrocities hidden in plain sight.","opus":"Sodom aim their thrash at how screen violence deadens us, indicting authorities who look the other way for profit while televised brutality dulls the mind. Cruelty consumed as entertainment corrodes the world from inside, the song portraying media-fed savagery as a violence that inflicts real damage while leaving no visible mark.","sonnet":"Television as instrument of psychic violation: political and corporate power using broadcast media to anesthetise, distract, and ultimately corrupt its viewers. The screen watches back, the real violence is the one nobody names, and the individual seals his own fate in front of it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title coins violation-by-image, and Sodom prosecutes the screen as an instrument of assault: television does not inform, it severs the mind and throttles it, while the atrocities it broadcasts leave no mark on the placid viewer who absorbs them. Tom Angelripper's argument on this Code Red cut is that political and commercial power avert their gaze on purpose, since profit carries no stench of the corpses that fund it — suffering becomes monetized content, terror refined into entertainment that feeds an appetite for sacrilege. The last verse turns the passive spectator into the endpoint of that circuit: the same hands that groped through broadcast filth close around a firearm, hunger for televised death curdling into a private, self-directed execution. The point is that watching is never innocent — a steady diet of violence-as-amusement corrodes from within until observing and committing collapse into one gesture.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"watching violence rebranded as never innocent consumption","built":"passive spectator converted into the circuit's endpoint — observation curdling into self-directed execution"},
"sodom~wander-in-the-valley": {"haiku":"In a frozen wasteland of death and suffering, souls scream unguarded; those who wander face mummified bodies, broken dreams, and the incantation of freedom leading only to oblivion.","opus":"A death-soaked vision of kneeling in a graveyard among a million shrieking spirits, where a corrupt voice foretells that dying comes easy if you merely shut your eyes and liberation turns into an urgent final order. Time locks solid and the flesh mummifies. The German thrash veterans wrap occult, mortal imagery around the lure of giving in, offering death as the ultimate release.","sonnet":"A figure kneeling at a burial ground while time freezes and a million voices scream — caught between hollow dreams, fading memories, and the certainty that death is drawing close. The valley is spiritual deadland, and the wanderer is already entombed in it, mummified by honour and blood.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom rewrites the shepherd's valley from the twenty-third Psalm into a burial ground where comfort has curdled into a chanted sentence, the same three-word verdict tolling until it reads as bureaucratic fact rather than terror. Every promise of endurance the song offers is a con: the sacred draught that should sustain, the embalmed corpses meant to cheat rot, the elixir hawked to the desperate — each is presented already spoiling, so the offer of forever is precisely the bait that condemns the traveller. Guilt runs underneath, a reckoning approaching like sand slipping between fingers while the mind's old restraints slacken, until the true voice announces itself: a prophesying tempter who rebrands extinction as liberation and coaxes surrender as a gentle lowering of the lids. The genuine horror is that oblivion is dispensed as a courtesy fulfilling the victim's own request, so the wanderer is not slain but flattered into craving the end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"oblivion dispensed as a courtesy fulfilling the victim's own request","built":"Psalm 23 comfort curdled so every sacred promise arrives already spoiling"},
"sodom~warlike-conspiracy": {"haiku":"A soldier experiences war's desire to kill without thought of consequences, following the mating call of battle while feeling separated from grace.","opus":"Sodom depict a soldier whose empty, imitative existence finds purpose only in war's grim commerce, bragging of his firepower yet haunted by the killing once the guns fall silent. Central to the German band's work, the war theme curdles into a prayer to a god who looks away as his comrades fall and the pull of battle keeps summoning him back.","sonnet":"A soldier trapped in military logic watches comrades fall and follows anyway, unable to resist the pull of war even as his inner voice pleads otherwise. The conspiracy is not political but existential — the mating call of violence that overrides conscience and turns men into instruments.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom's long labor is the soldier's-eye war song, and Tom Angelripper writes from inside a killer who can no longer tell his profession from his person, the murdering hardened into a mute skill. What curdles the piece is its theology of abandonment: a man kneels, begs a divine ear, and meets only silence, God averting the gaze he came to plead before. Stripped of any sanctioning heaven, the fighter obeys instead the herd, watching comrades drop under fire and marching after them unflinching, drawn by what he brands an animal summons toward slaughter. The conspiracy of the title, then, is no scheme hatched by generals but the near-erotic biological magnetism that walks men straight into the muzzles regardless.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","violence & murder"],"means":"biological pull toward slaughter substituting for divine or moral sanction","built":"prayer met with divine silence strips the fighter of any sanctioning heaven"},
"sodom~what-hell-can-create": {"haiku":"A murderer with insatiable lust describes his violent urges and lack of conscience, culminating in reclaimed pride and death's delight through destroying dignity.","opus":"Sodom inhabit a murderer consumed by an uncontrollable urge to kill, an inner war that cuts him off from people who could never grasp him. The German thrash pioneers pile on gore before the killer turns his violence inward toward suicide, resolving to die with a strange, defiant dignity as some grim truth is remade from the wreckage.","sonnet":"A consciousness saturated with violence and contempt surveys the wreckage of its own interior. The war inside the skull keeps the narrator severed from ordinary humanity, and the track catalogs that separation with a grim pride — scorn is destiny, and hell has shaped this person precisely as intended.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person monologue spoken from inside a killer's skull, arguing that the monster was assembled rather than born — the title names hell not as a place of torment but as a factory, a thing that manufactures. The speaker keeps pointing outward, claiming the laughing, useless men around him handed him the appetite and marched him toward the abattoir, so the atrocity is presented as installed by others. Sodom, whose catalogue circles how conflict machines ordinary men into butchers, miniaturizes that idea into one ruined individual, turning the churning fever behind his eyes into a battlefield relocated under the scalp. The chorus swings the blame back at its prey — the victim gets what he earned — which is exactly the self-excusing logic the verses just exposed as borrowed from elsewhere, so the mask wears a predator's righteousness while beneath it crouches a man robbed of worth and circling his own suicide. When the closing lines let clarity return in fragments and death arrives as a welcomed reward walked toward with pride, the confession sours into a farewell note, the built thing at last devouring whoever built it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind","suicide"],"means":"atrocity presented as installed by others while the mask wears predator's righteousness","built":"closing embrace of death as welcomed reward turns the confession into a farewell note"},
"sodom~who-is-god": {"haiku":"Organized religion fails to deliver peace or meaning, offering only oppression and superstition while believers face inevitable death and suffering.","opus":"A furious interrogation of faith amid sectarian slaughter, refusing to make God a master and watching prayer curdle into lies. It stares into wooden idols and asks whether any god survives the atrocities done in his name.","sonnet":"A rejection of organized religion as lie and manipulation — the narrator watches sectarian violence and Armageddon-level destruction all done in God's name, and concludes there is no God worth serving, only the dead bodies piled beneath his wooden eyes.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sodom points the interrogation not at whether a deity exists but at the apparatus of obedience raised in its name, keeping the title unresolved because it will not concede the certainty any worshipper takes for granted. The verses read creed-versus-creed massacre as faith's actual output: slaughter committed on consecrated soil, the smug conviction of the divinely marked that licenses the devout to butcher, and the refrain's refusal of a commanding god sounds less like unbelief than a fighter's revulsion at being drafted into another man's crusade. The idol's blind stare is the tell — the thing venerated is a carved figure that neither perceives nor replies, and prayer only recycles the deception back toward the mouth that first uttered it. Tom Angelripper files the eschatology as dispatch rather than pageant, so the reckoning lands as the mundane terminus of a species that keeps murdering on behalf of its gods.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"title left unresolved to withhold the certainty faith demands","built":"idol's blind stare as the tell — the venerated thing neither perceives nor replies"},
"sodom~witching-metal-bonus": {"haiku":"Metal war, witchcraft, and hellish defiance saturate an apocalyptic landscape where blindness, bloodlust, and sinfulness reign. The song is an incantation of chaos and transgression.","opus":"Early Sodom in their rawest form, chanting the band's own name over Satanic slogans, a blunt teenage blast of German thrash where the point is speed, blasphemy and volume rather than any argument.","sonnet":"A raw early thrash invocation of Satanic metal imagery — Sodom, bloodlust, wildfire, sin, unholy times — the lyrics functioning as a crude liturgy for the genre itself rather than conveying narrative content.","src":"haiku","fable":"Language here is stripped to slogans: paired rhymes barked in couplets, less lyric than a chanted list of curses drawn from the group's earliest teenage vocabulary of desecration. Naming a band after a razed biblical city and then screaming that name back as a rallying cry turns identity itself into the payload, the destroyed place reborn as a promise of ruin. This is proto-blackthrash in its rawest German form, where sound and velocity outrank sense and a repeated refusal to sleep stands in for any coherent creed. When everything eventually burns down to the four-letter title shouted alone, the crude ritual reveals its real subject: not devils or firepower but the sheer fact of the group asserting that it exists.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","faith & the occult"],"means":"group identity asserted by reclaiming a razed biblical city as a rallying cry","built":"slogans stripped to four letters until the chanted name is the entire payload"},
"soen~emdr":{"haiku":"Psychic parasitism consumes the host from within, with desire and manipulation feeding a cycle that leaves the victim hollowed and complicit in their own degradation.","opus":"Named for a trauma therapy, this crawls inside a parasitic, controlling relationship where a predatory voice feeds on the other and calls it healing; desire keeps finding a way, and the horror is how the manipulator dresses domination as love and comfort.","sonnet":"A figure entangled in guilt, parasitic desire, and psychological damage addresses a loved one — oscillating between self-loathing and a fierce, protective declaration to lead them through the darkness regardless of the cost to himself.","src":"haiku"},
"soen~savia":{"haiku":"The song laments environmental destruction and human greed, with the speaker refusing to perform salvation while acknowledging complicity in the erasure of life.","opus":"A grieving reckoning with humanity's plunder of the earth, refusing the role of savior and confessing complicity in the greed that turned soil to sand and killed our mother. It weds ecological ruin to a broader loss of faith, ash becoming our name. Soen, a Swedish progressive metal band with roots in Tool-adjacent atmospherics, favor exactly this kind of contemplative, philosophically weighted heaviness.","sonnet":"An ecological and spiritual reckoning — the earth killed by greed, soil turned to sand, the misery that underpins religion and the power that drives war named plainly. The speaker refuses to be anyone's savior, carries guilt for complicity, and ultimately refuses a future built on the same cursed inheritance.","src":"haiku"},
"sofi-tukker~kakee":{"opus":"A playful Portuguese ode to the persimmon (caqui) as pure sensual pleasure, rejecting all other fruits and transport, smearing the sweet, sexy fruit all over herself with joyful abandon.","sonnet":"A playful, sensory Brazilian Portuguese song about craving persimmon above all other fruits and traveling specifically to eat one — the persimmon drips and stains clothes and skin, rendered as a tiny, sexy indulgence.","src":"sonnet"},
"sofi-tukker~mi-rumba":{"haiku":"Sensual appetite and possessive desire drive the narrative as bodies merge through dance and intimate claiming. The song celebrates ownership and intoxication as fundamental to erotic connection and dominance.","opus":"An unapologetic celebration of female appetite and desire, owning what she wants and refusing shame about it. The Cuban rumba becomes the pulse of a woman who leads by being freely, bindingly herself, not afraid to name her needs.","sonnet":"Unashamed erotic appetite is celebrated as self-possession — the speaker owns what they have, wants what they want, and invites total mutual exposure, weaving in the rumba as both the dance and the rhythm of desire that moves between two bodies.","src":"haiku"},
"sofi-tukker~summer-in-new-york":{"haiku":"Urban wandering and serendipitous connection create magic within the city's kaleidoscopic characters and fleeting moments. Summer in New York becomes a promise of perpetual adventure and unexpected intimacy.","opus":"A daylong love letter to the city, wandering with no fixed destination from downtown tattoo parlors to Brooklyn dance floors to rooftop sunrises. It savors the chance encounters, strangers turning into friends by afternoon in a place the singer will always cherish.","sonnet":"A perfect summer day in New York traced from Second Avenue down to Brooklyn and back to Chinatown — strangers become friends by afternoon, dancing on pavement turns into rooftop sunrise, and the city's serendipity is the real subject: the way it keeps delivering characters, encounters, and unrepeatable nights.","src":"haiku"},
"sofi-tukker~summer-in-new-york-wnboss-fancy-inc-remix":{"haiku":"Urban wandering and serendipitous connection create magic within the city's kaleidoscopic characters and fleeting moments. Summer in New York becomes a promise of perpetual adventure and unexpected intimacy.","opus":"A remix of the same wandering love letter to New York, tracing a day of aimless drifting from downtown tattoos to Brooklyn dance floors and dawn on a rooftop. The city's serendipity and its ever-changing streets remain the thing worth adoring.","sonnet":"The same perfect New York summer day — aimless walking, a tattoo, a secret garden, a stranger with a great smile, live music at Miss Favela, the bridge to Brooklyn, rooftops at dawn — compressed into a remix that preserves the original's narrative of urban serendipity.","src":"haiku"},
"sofie-letitre~i-ll-be-fine":{"haiku":"Resolute acceptance of inevitable suffering; reassurance masks deeper uncertainty about survival and stability.","opus":"A spare, incantatory piece built from reassurance and a single yearning question about being made to believe in someone. Repetition becomes the point, the mantra of being fine circling as if to convince the self. It captures the fragile ritual of self-soothing, where belief in another and belief in one's own okayness are hard to tell apart.","sonnet":"The speaker holds two contradictory positions simultaneously: the rote reassurance she broadcasts outward (\"I'll be fine\") and the needier question she directs at someone whose belief she still craves (\"would you make me see that I believe in you?\"). The circularity of the lyric — never resolving whether the faith is in herself or in the other person — enacts the ambivalence it describes.","src":"haiku"},
"soft-cell~sex-dwarf":{"haiku":"A wealthy predator parades and exploits a small person as a possession, treating them as an accessory for film, spectacle, and manipulation.","opus":"A leering synth-sleaze fantasy of ownership and exploitation, luring the naive into vice with leashes, cameras, and gold Rolls-Royces. It plays predatory power and kitsch decadence for provocation.","sonnet":"A wealthy narrator plans to use a small companion as a spectacle and prop for luring vulnerable people — referred to as disco dollies — into a life of exploitation, filming them and parading them publicly. The song portrays predatory grooming and sexual degradation in a sleazy, glamorous setting.","src":"haiku"},
"soft-play~punk-s-dead":{"haiku":"Aging punk rockers betray the movement's transgressive core by softening their stance; former rebels become the establishment they once opposed.","opus":"A savage satire in which the band ventriloquizes their own reactionary critics. Adopting the voice of a bitter gatekeeper enraged by their name change and perceived wokeness, they mock the demand for real men and lament that punk died with sensitivity. Soft Play skewer toxic nostalgia and fragile masculinity from the inside, letting the bigot indict himself while the fans stay loyal.","sonnet":"A sustained impersonation of a reactionary blowhard mourning change, real men, and punk — performed so precisely the satire is the whole point. The PC babies, the liberal lefties, the nostalgia for a punk that never existed: the narrator inhabits the voice to expose how ridiculous it sounds, the irony sharpened by naming the band Soft Play.","src":"haiku"},
"softcult~16-25":{"haiku":"Power imbalance festers beneath desire; a child trapped in relationship with an adult cannot consent or mature authentically within such corruption.","opus":"A pointed callout of a grown man preying on a teenage girl, listing everything she doesn't yet know how to do while everyone tells her to act her age. The gap between sixteen and twenty-five is stated flatly because it's the whole crime.","sonnet":"A flat, unsparing portrait of a predatory relationship between a 25-year-old man and a 16-year-old girl — the girl is described as an object of desire who does not yet know how to love, drive, or navigate the adult world she is being pulled into. The song does not moralize explicitly; it simply names the age gap and lets the disparity speak for itself, with the refrain functioning as both indictment and elegy.","src":"haiku"},
"softcult~drain":{"haiku":"Aging as cynicism, questioning immortality when change requires effort nobody invests in.","opus":"An eco-conscious lament on the greed that strip-mines the planet for monuments to ourselves, twinned with the fatalism of aging cynical. Why bother with immortality if nobody will change for the better.","sonnet":"A bitter meditation on environmental destruction and human indifference — the \"drain\" is the world being bled dry by an older generation that built monuments at the expense of the planet and won't change; the song questions why anyone would want to live forever in a world no one is fixing.","src":"haiku"},
"softcult~dress":{"haiku":"Reclaiming clothing from unwanted interpretation, rage at transformation imposed by male violence.","opus":"A furious rebuttal of victim-blaming, insisting that clothing is never consent, told from inside the fear of being followed and the permanent alteration that assault, or its threat, leaves on a body and a life.","sonnet":"A woman is followed and threatened at night and insists that her clothing was never consent — the song is about the permanent alteration of her sense of safety and body after what happened or nearly happened in the dark.","src":"haiku"},
"softcult~love-song":{"haiku":"Attachment consumes reason; mortality sharpens focus on small rituals of togetherness, leading to inevitable surrender.","opus":"Love braided with mortality and dread, a death on the nightly broadcast triggering thoughts of the beloved and how far the singer would go for them. It clings to small intimacies — a swaying embrace at home — as anchors against being swept off and surrendering everything.","sonnet":"A death on the evening news triggers a surge of love-fear — the unbearable thought of losing this person — and the speaker surrenders completely to the feeling; a slow dance in a living room becomes the image held against everything transient.","src":"haiku"},
"softcult~not-sorry":{"haiku":"Parasitic exploitation spreads its poison; the speaker cuts it away without regret, refusing to mourn the removal of feeding predators.","opus":"Cutting off a parasitic person compared to a biting insect and a burrowing tick, refusing the guilt that's expected to follow. The scars left thick skin, and the flat repetition of not being sorry is the point.","sonnet":"About cutting off a parasitic, draining person without guilt — the other person is compared to a tick burrowing under the skin, feeding until there is nothing left, spreading poison that cannot be bled out. The narrator is not sorry for ending it, and the song's entire emotional register is indifference rather than remorse: they are simply done.","src":"haiku"},
"softcult~someone2me":{"haiku":"Parasitic desire disguises predatory control; the speaker recognizes their manipulative self-disgust while despising the audience they've trapped, contempt flowing all directions.","opus":"A woman turns the tables on a leering, self-righteous creep who dissects and controls her while imagining himself righteous and others deserving to burn. Her disgust is the refusal to be the only thing propping up his miserable self-image.","sonnet":"A woman turning on a man who has been objectifying, belittling, and fantasizing about her while calling it harmless — she dismantles his delusion that he matters to her and names him exactly what he is: a miserable creep.","src":"haiku"},
"softcult~spit-it-out":{"haiku":"A person is silenced and ornamental, forced to swallow poisonous words and toxins from someone else. The repeated desire to erase themselves shows how forced conformity and suppression breed self-negation.","opus":"A shoegaze-grunge reckoning with being silenced and made ornamental, the pressure on a woman to be seen and not heard curdling into self-erasure. Spitting it out becomes refusal, rejecting the poison she was told to swallow.","sonnet":"About being silenced and made to feel small — told to swallow your words, treated as an ornament to be seen and not heard — until the speaker tries to absorb the other person's toxicity and has to spit it back out.","src":"haiku"},
"softcult~spoiled":{"haiku":"Internal decay and spoilage define the narrator's nature as they acknowledge their capacity to corrupt connections. Self-awareness of toxicity doesn't prevent harm, only prolongs suffering for those attached to their poison.","opus":"Self-sabotage owned without flinching, the singer painting herself as two things that won't mix, ruined and gone off, losing precisely when she's ahead. She wonders why anyone stays tethered to someone certain she's fundamentally defective and forever a step short of the line.","sonnet":"A person who always sees the end in beginnings and loses when winning diagnoses themselves as fundamentally flawed — rotten, spoiled, water and oil — while acknowledging they have anchored someone else to a capsized ship, and the album-closing image is a finish line perpetually just out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"softcult~uzumaki":{"haiku":"Cyclical patterns trap consciousness in repetition as the speaker accepts meaninglessness through numbness and acceptance. Spiraling persists as the only available path.","opus":"A vicious, dream-logic loop of a dead relationship the narrator keeps rebuilding just to knock down, insisting with numb repetition that none of it matters anymore.","sonnet":"Trapped in a vicious cycle the speaker can't escape — a house of cards built and knocked down, teeth falling out in dreams on repeat — the refrain \"it doesn't matter to me now\" reads less as acceptance and more as a dissociation from caring about anything anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"softspoken~lovetok":{"haiku":"Social media performance masks internal crisis; the speaker fabricates happiness for public consumption while drowning privately in contradiction.","opus":"A portrait of a woman performing seduction and falsehood for online approval while drowning her real ambitions under drugs and attention. The public adores her curated routine, but it's devouring her, a monstrosity she prizes above her own survival.","sonnet":"A woman numbed by drugs and desire performs for an audience — platforms and belts, phone in hand, sinners at her knees — her Lovetok dancing consuming her while she stuffs away her dreams and posts lies from the back of her mind looking for love under a waterfall.","src":"haiku"},
"softspoken~sleight-of-hand":{"haiku":"A person hides their true self behind deception and tricks, aware their facade might crumble if exposed; their heart cannot bear the risk of being truly seen.","opus":"A compulsive liar and self-hider admits he masks who he really is because his heart can't survive being laid bare; deeper down, emotional armor becomes a conjuror's illusion and a book written backward out of shame, where letting someone read the honest version feels like his own undoing.","sonnet":"A song about the exhausting performance of hiding your true self — the narrator is a practiced deceiver whose main target has been himself, and he knows that if anyone ever actually saw through the act, the whole story would collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"soilwork~departure-plan":{"haiku":"Vulnerability and fragile courage demand a turn toward artistic expression and spiritual elevation; escape becomes possible through creative transformation.","opus":"A direct plea to someone suicidal to choose life, insisting their art and hidden strength are the cure and that the demons will leave empty-handed; unusually tender for melodic death metal, it ends on the vow that they'll never hurt themselves again.","sonnet":"An urgent address to someone on the edge of ending their life, insisting that art is a hidden cure and urging them to rise rather than give up, framing vulnerability not as weakness but as something admirable that deserves a chance to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"soilwork~st-lf-gel":{"haiku":"Transcendence through death liberates the tethered spirit; the narrator drifts toward darkness as escape from corporeal constraint.","opus":"A hazy meditation on release and transcendence, souls unbound and drifting upward toward a dark heaven, letting memory burn away to become someone else; the fade to nothing reads as both escape and surrender.","sonnet":"Souls drift airborne and aimless — unbound from gravity and identity — moving toward a darkness they call heaven, with the speaker whispering a name that is already becoming someone else's memory as the fading becomes final.","src":"haiku"},
"sokoninaru~6-extreme-explosion-ver":{"haiku":"A meditation on uncertainty and acceptance—vague things slip away, grim reality settles in, yet we accept our farewells.","opus":"A Japanese meditation on grasping at uncertainty while even the certain things quietly slip away, set under an endless early-summer rain. It bids farewell to dreamy hopes and to a frayed nervous self, trusting the world will someday clear.","sonnet":"A brief, fragmented song about the slow dissolution of certainty — things once gripped tightly are slipping away, even dreams of the world clearing up are being abandoned by whatever's left of the spirit. The emotional core is the moment just before giving up, when even the resignation is being let go.","src":"haiku"},
"sokoninaru~a-10l8jem":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator walks the same roads and scenery alone, haunted by the absence of someone who is irrecoverably gone, and arrives at a quiet resignation: the wish to return to those days will not come true, and life will go on without ever seeing or hearing from that person again."},
"sokoninaru~a-13joe0f":{"src":"web","web":"The song examines the loss of autonomy and identity under invisible control, using the metaphor of dancing on someone else's palm to depict a person exhausted by performing a predetermined role, with biological processes slowing toward an inevitable surrender."},
"sokoninaru~a-1hrl5fu":{"haiku":"A haunting meditation on feminine mystique and obsession—guided by a girl's voice, chasing shadows, poisoned and trapped by beauty.","opus":"Sokoninaru's fierce math-rock often turns self-aware about its own influences, and here that reflex becomes confession. The narrator both chases and is poisoned by the ghost of the girl's sound, finding that anything real gets stopped up because they are too infected by it. They name Shutoku Mukai of Number Girl as the only one permitted to invoke the girl trope without it collapsing into empty imitation.","sonnet":"A tribute to and critique of the 'girl's sound' aesthetic — the narrator admits they are both pursuing and poisoned by the ghost of a certain kind of music, that trying to say anything real gets stopped up because they're too infected with its influence. The song explicitly names Mukai Shutoku (of Number Girl / Zazen Boys) as the only one who's allowed to invoke the 'girl' trope without it becoming empty imitation.","src":"haiku"},
"sokoninaru~a-1jspcel":{"opus":"The band's urgent, shouting attack suits this cry against a distorted reality that has stripped away ordinary life and left only the ghost of past grandeur. The narrator vows to keep waving a hand and shouting until their voice gives out, straining to bend a broken world back toward something true, the sheer volume standing in for the will to force change.","sonnet":"A voice crying out against a distorted reality that has stripped away normal life and left only the ghost of past grandeur — the narrator will keep waving their hand and shouting until their voice gives out, trying to bend a broken world back toward something true.","src":"sonnet"},
"sokoninaru~a-1mv9cl":{"opus":"Sokoninaru specialize in claustrophobic intensity, and here they turn it on sleep itself as a small death. Closing the eyes feels like dropping a curtain on everything; the past grips the future and refuses to let go; the narrator buckles under the accumulated weight of their own self. All they ask is for someone to acknowledge this single exhausted step of closing their eyes.","sonnet":"A song about the terror of sleep as a small death: closing the eyes feels like dropping a curtain on everything, the past grips the future and won't release it, and the narrator is crushed under the accumulated weight of who they are — they just want someone to acknowledge this single exhausted step of closing their eyes.","src":"sonnet"},
"sokoninaru~a-fpn04x":{"haiku":"Themes of death, mortality, or psychological demise; existential darkness permeates the lyrics.","opus":"As a band that takes the stage seriously, Sokoninaru make this an unusually candid reckoning with the corruption of platform. Words spoken from the stage gnaw at the conscience, ambiguity feels too easy, and self-strangulation seems more honest than vague idealism. Yet the narrator keeps going, forgetting past violence through the one talent left available: time.","sonnet":"A musician wrestling with the corruption that comes with power and platform — words on stage eat at the conscience, ambiguity is too easy, self-strangulation feels more honest than vague idealism; yet somehow the narrator keeps going, forgetting past violence with the only talent available: time.","src":"haiku"},
"sokoninaru~a-u9canm":{"haiku":"A dark acceptance of powerlessness—nothing I say matters; I'm scorned daily, tomorrow will be the same, yet this one light sustains me.","opus":"Sokoninaru's ferocity often masks a bleak core, and here nothing softens it. The narrator knows that nothing they say or do will change anything, that they are scorned and eroded and will still be alive tomorrow, and clings to a sliver of someone else's light as the only reason to continue, even while admitting that light is becoming unreachable. It is survival stripped of every pretense.","sonnet":"A person who knows that nothing they say or do will change anything — they are scorned, eroded, and will still be alive tomorrow — and holds onto a sliver of someone else's light as the only reason to keep going, while admitting even that light is becoming unreachable. The emotional core is bleak survival stripped of all pretense: 'yeah, basically impossible, but also what even is light anyway.'","src":"haiku"},
"sokoninaru~a-xfua3z":{"opus":"The band pairs frantic musicianship with a strain of wounded lyricism, and this dusk-run leans into the latter. Sprinting through a deserted town on exhausted feet with despair stretching long, the narrator clings to a talisman of forgiveness they cannot set down, watches something precious from summer slowly crack, and asks the rain to give back the music that was taken away.","sonnet":"Running through a deserted town at dusk with exhausted feet and despair stretching long — clinging to a talisman of forgiveness the narrator can't put down, watching something precious from summer slowly crack, asking the rain to return music that was taken away.","src":"sonnet"},
"sokoninaru~black-to":{"src":"web","web":"Tracing cycles of fractured memory and repeated human error, the song holds onto the commitment to \"weave again\" despite knowing mistakes will recur, treating the act of continuing — so that something between us does not disappear — as its own fragile proof of identity."},
"sokoninaru~break-out":{"haiku":"Countless weights burden the mind; numbing escape into fog and deception replaces authentic presence, leaving faded reality and cyclical struggle.","opus":"A defiant break for freedom framed as a final battle, the die cast and the sword raised against a world of vague lies, glib truths, and traps. Cramming the day's impulses and regrets away, the narrator throws open the curtain on a first-and-last war toward a dreamlike, otherworldly fate.","sonnet":"The die is cast and cannot be uncast: the speaker seizes a sword against the Hellgate waiting behind a distorted love and a transparent trap, crying out to break the scripted ending and fly into a final form that is chaotic, dreamlike, and irreversible.","src":"haiku"},
"sokoninaru~fake-fake-fate":{"src":"web","web":"A cycle of repeated sin and self-deception is laid out plainly — days worn out by revision get overwritten yet again — but the closing refrain insists on breathing through it, framing endurance itself as resistance to moral collapse."},
"sokoninaru~gou-ni-moyu":{"src":"web","web":"A meditation on insatiable desire and existential suffering, the song describes how fleeting joys only deepen an unfillable thirst, yet the very act of yearning — searching for the edge of the universe — is what keeps the narrator alive."},
"sokoninaru~lament-moment":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator survives a prolonged, suffocating hardship by letting go of idealistic abstractions, finding that \"the resignation named resolution\" is what keeps the heart alive, and ultimately seeking to move beyond distorted notions of righteousness toward something authentic."},
"sokoninaru~re-program":{"src":"web","web":"Worn down by repeated mistakes and false consolations, the narrator rejects hollow hope and instead embraces despair as a path to genuine renewal, resolving to draw the first page again and start over from nothing."},
"sokoninaru~seizon":{"haiku":"Bonds formed in cold persist irrationally—winter's words cage the speaker while warmth fades. Survival means living with contradiction, unable to forgive yet unable to abandon.","opus":"The unbearable pull of a dead person's parting words, binding the survivor to keep living even when stopping would be easier. Unable to forgive that day or themselves, cowardly and numb and ugly as they feel, they keep on surviving despite it all.","sonnet":"Winter scents carry words left behind, binding the speaker like frozen hands — the urge to stop, to surrender, to escape — yet the body's warmth persists through waking and cannot be cooled; unforgiven and self-loathing but alive nonetheless, the speaker names that contradiction: \"still, I go on living.\"","src":"haiku"},
"sokoninaru~teinen":{"haiku":"Gripping harder against powerlessness; distrust becomes the only certainty. The speaker knows connection disappoints yet persists in attempting it, left palm anchoring against void.","opus":"Clinging to a dirtied truth and transparent lies while ambiguity drives everything toward collapse, gripping the left hand so a sense of powerlessness won't vanish. Even a trace of hope glimpsed in an open palm must be released so it can never be touched again.","sonnet":"Gripping the left hand to keep powerlessness from fading, the speaker drives a stake deep enough to bruise — refusing to believe even a single word of what trust once offered — and opens a palm to glimpse a last sliver of hope only to deliberately release it, because powerlessness must not be allowed to disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"sokoninaru~utsunomiya":{"haiku":"Stacked lies collapse under pressure; the speaker curses their beloved's absence across distance. Hate sustains through grief—contempt becomes fuel when love has burned away.","opus":"Asking permission to hate someone until death after they built a life of lies and vanished far away, taking love, wealth, and fame with them. The dream-truth they spun keeps disappearing, and hating them forever becomes the narrator's first, last, and only wish while some part of the heart stays lost.","sonnet":"Woven lies scatter, leaving the speaker with nothing — \"may I go on hating you until I die?\" — as a fabricated dream of love, wealth, and fame dissolves at sonic speed; the provocation that the right hand they were born with will now be used to cut, and the final plea to be allowed to lose something in their heart rather than carry it any further.","src":"haiku"},
"sokoninaru~white-for":{"haiku":"Intimacy terrifies—knowing another person erases boundary between self and other. The speaker loves while trembling, wanting to surrender yet fearing erasure, cycling through attachment and escape.","opus":"The terror of falling for someone, afraid of dissolving into them yet caught watching their sleeping face and unable to file it under mere love or lust. The precious days curdle toward hatred and drowning, until the narrator finally accepts that those long-short days let them love again and say goodbye without forgetting.","sonnet":"Falling into love terrifies the speaker because they begin to lose their sense of self — watching a sleeping face, unable to control one gesture of the other person — and the feeling escalates until the only escape imagined is sinking to the ocean floor; but naming the beloved's days as the reason they can now love and say goodbye to others, they choose to walk forward carrying everything.","src":"haiku"},
"sokoninaru~zettaiteki-sanpunkan":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator cycles through exhaustion and futility, pouring effort into broken machinery and weary limbs, while acknowledging the pathetic nature of the struggle — then resolving to walk on anyway, ascending even to the heights of melancholy."},
"sok~chcemy-by-wy-ej":{"haiku":"A rapper navigates between youth and age, wealth and fleeting pleasure, observing social theater while claiming autonomy—life demands knowing how to live, not how to appear.","opus":"Polish rap veteran Sokół surveying his lane between kids and old-timers, boasting that success comes from knowing how to live rather than luck, savoring the club-night spoils while Monday's meetings and business wait.","sonnet":"In Polish, Sokół raps about thriving in the present rather than hoarding or playing it safe — appreciating good times with his crew, reading his audience from both generations, staying authentic in clubs and boardrooms alike, and living with skill rather than luck.","src":"haiku"},
"sol-seppy~move":{"opus":"An urging to stop waiting for someone else to act and recognize the power to change everything lies within your own heart; a call toward unity, connection, and simply being present with the one you love.","sonnet":"A gentle push toward action and unity — the song urges someone who is waiting for external permission to move to realize the power for change already lives inside them, and invites togetherness and presence rather than passive watching.","src":"sonnet"},
"solence~deafening":{"haiku":"The sonic power of a massive collective force, described as overwhelming intensity that reaches and moves people viscerally.","opus":"Swedish band Solence turn live-crowd energy into pure catharsis, a promise to overwhelm the listener with volume and abandon. The narrator and his bandmates play up their wild, unforgettable stage presence, a roaring mass of voices swelling until it becomes overwhelming, a beautiful overload the song can only crave more of.","sonnet":"The overwhelming physical force of a crowd united in a concert hall — thousands of voices combining into something that transcends volume and becomes almost sacred. The deafening noise is not assault but communion, pure proof that collective feeling can be beautiful.","src":"haiku"},
"solence~death-do-us-part":{"opus":"A vow of total, unconditional commitment framed as wedding vows, promising loyalty until death and beyond, defying logic to hand over one's life, imagining the pair together even frozen and bleeding in the ground.","sonnet":"A wedding vow as heavy metal promise — two people exchange total commitment including the demand to swallow each other's lies and bury their pride, pledging loyalty through death and decay in language that literalizes the marriage covenant into something grim and absolute.","src":"sonnet"},
"solence~good-f-king-music":{"opus":"A straightforward anthem about using loud, headbanging music as therapy, a medicine to blast away the accumulated heartache, bad thoughts, and stress of a rough week.","sonnet":"A straightforward celebration of music as escapism and emotional relief — the song argues that turning up loud, aggressive music and surrendering to it is a legitimate and effective way to forget stress, heartache, and accumulated misery.","src":"sonnet"},
"solence~nublood-feat-fame-on-fire":{"haiku":"Generational renewal through draining toxicity and complacency, demanding radical transformation now.","opus":"A pandemic-era anthem calling for renewal by purging the old rot, cynicism and complacency framed as an infection to be drained. Fury at a system that only takes, demanding change or ruin.","sonnet":"A call for systemic renewal through the expulsion of complacency and corruption — the narrator wants old, toxic ways drained out and replaced with fresh energy. The second half becomes more personal and confrontational, directed at someone who drains sanity and needs to be burned out.","src":"haiku"},
"solitaris~soulswap":{"haiku":"A person trapped in darkness and silence describes mutual devastation—they and another are killing each other, giving everything but receiving only hollow echoes in return.","opus":"A frantic plea from someone stranded in darkness, begging another spirit to come over and save them, surrendering their whole self while dreading that the person they love is the very one destroying them. The closing passage melts into unreality, shapes glimpsed beyond a curtain, everything emptied out. Love and obliteration tangle as the speaker screams unheard in a silence that devours both.","sonnet":"A plea from someone lost in a void of their own making — no memories, no bearings, trapped in silence while a single guiding voice keeps them moving, and the desperate exchange at the center is an offer to give everything of oneself just to be saved, only to find that the other person is destroying what they claimed to love.","src":"haiku"},
"solstate~smile-it-s-only-war":{"haiku":"Survival through hiding and dreams of escape, wars normalized as background to perseverance.","opus":"A bitter pastoral of hiding out from conflict, faith sold off and prayers betrayed while a weary elder tells the young to grin through horror they've all endured before. Survival as quiet, cynical endurance.","sonnet":"A soldier or civilian in conflict is told to smile and comply while being dragged through fire and lies — the song is about surviving by staying in shadows, staying free, and dreaming of a world beyond the war.","src":"haiku"},
"solstate~the-void":{"haiku":"An inescapable emptiness calls to the drowning, promising relief through float or collapse as struggle becomes futile. The void offers erasing sensation as the only remaining comfort.","opus":"A struggle against the tug of oblivion, where emptiness lures as both menace and relief. Caught between the urge to keep living and the feeling that destiny has already summoned him, the narrator swears to resist until sensation cancels itself out, or else drift off. It captures the draining tug-of-war with suicidal despair, framed as a beckoning into the dark.","sonnet":"A mind at the edge of giving up invites the listener to follow it into the void, cycling through the exhaustion of fighting feelings that won't change, and settling on floating away as the only remaining option.","src":"haiku"},
"somewhere-special~cabin":{"haiku":"Mystical romance under moonlight leading to secluded space, relationship as beacon and escape.","opus":"A woozy, dreamlike come-on set in a moonlit cabin, all flashlights and beacons and warnings of the nighttime. Sparse and hypnotic, it trades on the eerie romance of being chosen and led into the dark to explore, gazes locked. Atmosphere carries the meaning: an intimate, faintly ominous seduction unfolding at the edge of the woods.","sonnet":"A brief, luminous invitation — flashlight, moonlight like a beacon, a cabin in the night — where the narrator feels chosen and wants only to keep the other person's gaze while they explore together. Nothing darker enters; it is entirely present-tense wonder.","src":"haiku"},
"som~youth-decay":{"haiku":"Mortality stamps every human face with the passage of time; youth fades into uncertainty while the speaker remains suspended between living and death.","opus":"A dreamy shoegaze-metal meditation on aging and the vertigo of memory. Entire histories are written on faces, youth slips past, and the narrator can no longer tell whether he'll go on forever or has been gone for ages. Photographs under glass and a thicket of glowing bulbs evoke time's blur, settling into the melancholy limbo between still living and already vanished.","sonnet":"Age inscribed on the face, photographs covered in glass, stories told on repeat to fill the gap where youth was — the question of whether one is living or has been dead for years hangs unanswered. The forest of lights and dawn of time frame it as something vast and inevitable.","src":"haiku"},
"sonata-arctica~black-sheep":{"haiku":"A social outcast embraces their alienation and invites others to join them in permanent estrangement from society.","opus":"A power-metal fable of the outcast miller, hated and condemned to loneliness, inviting a beloved to share his outsider's fate; the temple of the weak and the black sheep stand for anyone loved by no one, offering kinship in shared exile.","sonnet":"A figure defined as the outsider — the black sheep, the howling miller, the condemned servant — invites someone to share his exile in a temple that others call evil and weak. The song runs on the dark romance of shared alienation and the appeal of surrendering to someone who has nothing left to lose.","src":"haiku"},
"sonata-arctica~fullmoon":{"haiku":"Lycanthropic transformation destroys a man's humanity on full moons, leaving his lover unable to prevent or contain the beast his body becomes.","opus":"A werewolf tragedy where the curse becomes a metaphor for a man his lover can no longer recognize or reach; she should flee but stays for love, and the horror is watching someone transform into a beast while you refuse to see what he's become.","sonnet":"A lycanthropy love story in which a man tries to protect his partner from the beast he becomes each full moon, fleeing to fields and barns to keep her safe, while she refuses to understand the danger and nearly pays for it with her life.","src":"haiku"},
"sonic-boom-six~the-high-cost-of-living-single-edit":{"haiku":"Poverty and exploitation hide behind consumer glamour as cheap goods depend on invisible child labor. The song indicts complicit comfort that ignores the human cost of affordable luxury.","opus":"An anti-consumerist reckoning with the human toll behind cheap luxury, tracing a boutique dress back to a child laborer in Vietnam and a ball back to kids in Pakistan. Everyone's cheerful at the till, but the song refuses to let us forget whose sweat and tears actually paid for it.","sonnet":"A pair of shop-window objects — a garment embroidered by a nine-year-old in Vietnam and a football stitched by an eight-year-old in Pakistan — trace the hidden cost of consumer luxury, asking the satisfied buyer not to forget what splendid clothing and golden rings actually cost.","src":"haiku"},
"sonido-naco~pecados":{"haiku":"Damage spreads through inaction and numbness: wounds multiply and morality crumbles as the body falls beyond redemption's reach.","opus":"A blood-on-the-hands reckoning with sin and sobriety, someone falling with no floor in sight, learning where the line runs between the real and the merely human. A plea for God to take the weight he can't hold himself.","sonnet":"A song about falling into sin and violence and begging for divine rescue — the speaker is drunk, bloodied, barely able to see the bottom, and calls on God to take over because they cannot manage the weight of what is real and human.","src":"haiku"},
"sonny-boy-williamson-ii~keep-it-to-yourself":{"haiku":"Secrecy protects an affair from exposure; both lovers fear discovery would destroy their established lives, so silence becomes their covenant.","opus":"A blues plea from one married cheater to another to keep their affair secret, the humor and threat both plain: talk gets out and it wrecks two households; classic Delta candor about the mechanics of infidelity.","sonnet":"A man in a secret affair instructs his lover to tell no one — not family, not anyone — because both of them have spouses and a single word could destroy everything they have built together.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~as-long-as-you-make-a-sound":{"haiku":"The song describes emotional numbness and the repetition of self-destructive patterns, focusing on how past actions keep defining present reality. The speaker feels lost and stuck in cycles despite knowing nothing matters.","opus":"An electronic breakup autopsy where the narrator keeps replaying old habits around a person who felt nothing, insisting nothing matters in the end. Numbness dressed in cold synths, the sound outlasting the feeling.","sonnet":"About being numbed by a relationship and its aftermath — old habits, old words, everything centered on someone who didn't feel anything. The narrator keeps breathing and repeating the cycle, unsure what they're supposed to do with a person who made them mute.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~can-you-hear-me":{"haiku":"Someone trapped in silence and insomnia calls out repeatedly, unable to sleep despite nighttime return, fading news, and encroaching thoughts. The refrain asks if anyone hears or sees them in their overwhelming inner noise.","opus":"A sleepless speaker is consumed by nighttime regrets and thoughts that won't leave, drowning in silence and nonsense while pleading to be heard and seen by someone, anyone.","sonnet":"Sleeplessness and regret circle through the narrator's nights, and they call out through the silence hoping someone will hear or see them — only to be met with nobody responding.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~enough":{"haiku":"Someone exhausted by failed remedies, self-doubt, and unfinished attempts declares they have had enough of pain, shame, and pointlessness.","opus":"A depressive spiral of exhaustion and futility in which every effort dissolves, nothing ever gets finished, and the narrator fears everything while believing in nothing because he is simply spent, fleeing a version of himself he no longer recognizes.","sonnet":"A song about hitting the wall of burnout and disillusionment — the narrator cannot finish anything, believes in nothing, fears everything, and has reached a point where the usual remedies no longer work and exhaustion has become the baseline.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~happy":{"opus":"Sonoio is Alessandro Cortini's minimal electronic project, and its restraint sharpens this into a single pointed question. Asked of someone living without feeling, it wonders whether they are truly happy sustaining a heartless existence, the very brevity of the question functioning as its own quiet answer.","sonnet":"A minimal, pointed question to someone living without feeling — asking whether they are truly happy sustaining a heartless existence, with the brevity itself functioning as the answer.","src":"sonnet"},
"sonoio~heartbeat":{"haiku":"Modern existence feels repetitive and emotionally dead, with the speaker questioning whether happiness is possible when living without genuine feeling or connection.","opus":"A portrait of deadened routine where the days collapse into one another, faith and recollection erode, and decay feels inevitable. The recurring question is whether any real contentment is possible for a person who goes through life without a pulse of feeling, existing rather than living.","sonnet":"A life emptied of feeling is examined through the image of someone who wakes up the same every day, walks away from everything, lets memories shut down the brain, and ends up living without a heartbeat — emotionally flatlined and unable to answer whether that constitutes happiness.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~hold-on-let-go":{"haiku":"Caught between holding on and letting go, the speaker struggles with unchanging circumstances, stagnation, and the paradox of not knowing which choice to make.","opus":"A portrait of paralysis and stalled time, years slipping by while everything stays the same, the narrator inconsistent and cut off, pleading for someone to answer. The title's back-and-forth captures being frozen between holding tight and letting go, unable to reach the stability he keeps craving.","sonnet":"About the paralysis of a life that hasn't changed in years — the narrator is inconsistent, can't decide whether to hold on or let go, needs stability but finds only sameness, drifting between resistance and surrender without any clear answer.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~houdini":{"haiku":"Someone makes another disappear through illusion and denial, a magician's trick where presence vanishes and both vanish together into silence.","opus":"An unsettling scene of a manipulator soothing a frightened person while quietly working to make them vanish, denial and control tangled together. Alessandro Cortini's electronic project turns intimacy into a vanishing act where erasing the other becomes erasing the self.","sonnet":"A predatory psychological control dynamic where the narrator soothes and lulls a target into vulnerability with gentle words — 'relax, nothing can hurt you here' — before revealing the true intent: to make that person disappear, to erase them from the narrator's world.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~i-don-t-know":{"haiku":"Confusion and isolation amid broken promises; the speaker wakes to uncertainty, trapped between demands and the inability to find direction.","opus":"A tense escape narrative built on fear that keeps rising in volume, where the narrator finally stands alone, learns to stop asking why, and admits directional cluelessness about where to go next. Liberation arrives without any map. Sonoio is the electronic solo project of Alessandro Cortini, known for his work with Nine Inch Nails, and this carries that same brooding, synth-driven anxiety and restraint.","sonnet":"The disorientation of finally escaping a fear-soaked situation — staring at a phone, standing alone, trying to figure out where to go next after the promise of something better turned out to be real after all. Relief and groundlessness arrive together; the only honest answer to where this leads is that nobody knows.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~i-don-t-know-coda":{"haiku":"Mirror-image dissociation and release, forgetting how to let go while mourning what made someone special.","opus":"Maurizio Bianchi's project turns the morning-after fog of a fading relationship into a reckoning with an unrecognizable self in the mirror, torn between the wish to let someone go and the impulse to win them back.","sonnet":"Standing before a mirror that reflects a stranger, the speaker picks through idle stories and thoughts that keep them trapped, working toward the admission that it is time to let the other person — and the things that made them special — finally go.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~just-me":{"haiku":"A lonely narrator wishes to connect with someone emotionally, offering rides and comfort, but struggles with fear and emotional paralysis. By the song's end, that person has moved on and only solitude remains.","opus":"The speaker longs to draw someone out and make them smile, unsure whether he or she is more afraid to speak or leave, and concludes that once everyone else is gone there is only him left.","sonnet":"Two people are stuck in mutual hesitation — each afraid to leave or to speak — leaving the narrator alone with nobody around them.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~livid":{"haiku":"A person hides in darkness, ignored and defeated, feeding off anger and rage. They live in isolation and despair, screaming and bleeding in solitude.","opus":"Maurizio Chiarva's solo electronic project sinking into seclusion and surrender, resolved to inhabit darkness and to nourish itself on that bruised condition. Paranoia and resignation cycle over and over until the gloom becomes the only space that feels like a fit.","sonnet":"Someone has withdrawn completely into isolation and darkness, hiding from unnamed threats, having given up on the life outside — screaming and bleeding in the dark, committed to a defeated, invisible existence.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~minutes":{"haiku":"Each passing moment holds weight and narrative meaning, yet ultimately nothing matters and the soul wastes away regardless of how we measure or speak time.","opus":"Intimate electronic reckoning with time's indifference, a host offering a drink of liberating thought while conceding that every counted moment still ends up wasted. The tenderness makes the futility land gently rather than bleakly.","sonnet":"A melancholic meditation on how every moment and minute of life has meaning but ultimately amounts to nothing — the narrator hosts a conversation about impermanence, sharing a liberating thought that dissolves into resignation.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~no-fun":{"haiku":"A speaker questions their own identity and desires while criticizing someone else's joyless monotony, watching them waste rainy days and stay isolated on Saturday nights. The narrator eventually admits exhaustion with living falsely.","opus":"The speaker, ever-mutable and unsure of his own desires, watches someone squander their days shut indoors letting the wet-weather hours drain away, worn out from living a falsehood and sensing a piece of things is absent.","sonnet":"The narrator observes someone wasting their days indoors, staying awake through rainy Saturdays without purpose, while acknowledging their own sense of displacement and the lie of the life they are living.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~not-worth-remembering":{"haiku":"A failing relationship haunts the narrator as they recall how things used to be. They desperately wish to escape the memory but cannot, dwelling on the death of what once existed between them.","opus":"After a relationship has faded, the speaker dwells on how things used to be, wanting to stop feeling this way and deciding the past is not worth remembering.","sonnet":"The narrator reflects on a relationship that has drifted into mutual disconnection, deciding that the way things used to be between the two of them is something he no longer wants to dwell on or return to.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~scientist":{"haiku":"The narrator traces mental breakdown and isolation as relationships crumble, questioning reality while careening toward disconnection and finality.","opus":"A wry account of falling apart mentally after a breakup, needing no specialist to confirm he's losing it, leaning on cigarettes and the feeling that God checked out ages ago. Beneath a Star Wars joke and voicemail imagery runs a quiet plea that this doesn't have to be the end and that the bond still matters.","sonnet":"About a relationship falling apart while the world itself seems to be dissolving — the narrator is losing their mind, chain-smoking through the end of meaning, noting that it doesn't take any great intelligence to see that everything they had is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~suck-up-everything":{"haiku":"A person grieves the loss of a relationship, lamenting that their former partner is not the person they once were.","opus":"A cold severance from someone who has fundamentally changed, the mantra 'you're not the one you used to be' driving home that the person he needed near is gone. The listing of everything they're no longer, real, trustworthy, familiar, turns disillusionment into a total erasure of who they were.","sonnet":"A litany of estrangement — the speaker tells someone they are no longer the person they used to be, cataloguing all the ways they've changed and become unrecognizable, with no anger left, just cold recognition of loss.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~thanks-for-calling":{"haiku":"A person fractures and falls apart emotionally, thanking someone for reaching out while descending into misery and fear of facing loss and abandonment.","opus":"A bitterly grateful goodbye from someone who half-suspects his own misery is self-made and habitual. Alessandro Cortini strips electronic melancholy down to the fear of being left alone with a falling nobody comes to catch.","sonnet":"A bitter, hollow acknowledgment of a relationship dissolving — thanking the other person for checking in while knowing they've already let go, the narrator is falling and no one is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"sonoio~under-the-sea":{"opus":"A longing to come undone and slip away, the narrator wishing to sink beneath the surface where all the things that once weighed on him lose their grip. Sonoio's electronic melancholy channels dissociation and losing control, reaching toward someone nearly gone. Submersion reads not as death but as release, thought's clamor finally hushed.","sonnet":"A song about the desire to disappear beneath the surface of life — the narrator is overwhelmed, dissociated, moving too fast, and fantasizes about sinking somewhere where nothing matters anymore. The emotional core is exhausted withdrawal rather than peace.","src":"sonnet"},
"sonoio~vitamin-d":{"opus":"Loneliness and the hunger for closeness, an offer to speak when solitude sets in placed against being told to go, tenderness and chill trading off like an illness. Sonoio's spare electronic backdrop makes the title an ironic remedy: the absent sunlight of intimacy, reached for haltingly through fear and retreat.","sonnet":"A sparse, ambiguous sketch of isolation and emotional numbness — someone alone in the dark being sung to or singing to themselves, where everything feels bleak and the only prescription offered is light and sound. The title implies a literal deficiency underlying the depression.","src":"sonnet"},
"sonoio~what-s-before":{"haiku":"Release and forgetting dissolve memory as the speaker lets go of what preceded present dissolution.","opus":"Letting someone go and choosing to forget what came before, movement and release stripped to their barest terms. The spare repetition enacts the effort of not looking back.","sonnet":"In spare, fragmented lines the speaker lets go, moves forward, and arrives at forgetting — \"I forget what's before\" — the minimal lyric enacting the very dissolution of memory it describes.","src":"haiku"},
"sophie-meiers~hotline":{"haiku":"Desperate plea for connection through emergency channels; the speaker reaches out in crisis.","opus":"A giddy, dangerous fugitive fantasy — cackling in the backseat with a gun under her shirt and a blade strapped to her hip, the narrator plots a getaway from a warehouse and taunts a pursuer to ring her up and figure out what they actually want.","sonnet":"A fugitive on the run with weapons hidden under her clothes — a gun under the dress shirt, a knife taped under the skirt, hiding in a warehouse with a plan to get out. The \"call this number\" refrain positions her as the one with the contacts and the escape route; she's not the victim of this chase, she's the operative.","src":"haiku"},
"sophie-powers~bathroom-floor":{"haiku":"Profound alienation, exhaustion, and emotional numbness drive self-destruction; validation becomes impossible when you're trapped in your own mind.","opus":"A pop-punk panic attack tracing the descent from a pounding pulse to a heap on cold tile where feeling cuts out. The narrator can't haul herself back up from the plunge, dogged by a pressure across her ribs and a hunger for approval she knows won't help. It renders anxiety and dissociation with blunt, self-alienating candor.","sonnet":"A dissociative crash rendered in fragments: the beat in the chest, the lights going dark, and then the bathroom floor where feeling stops — the sun creeping in on the way home only adds weight to the chest, the haunted residue of a night that required more validation than anyone gave.","src":"haiku"},
"sophie-powers~greed":{"opus":"An unapologetic anthem of ambition, the narrator hungry for everything and impatient to seize power from whoever currently holds it. Sophie Powers uses pop-punk swagger to dramatize appetite itself, half-aware that any rise carries a fall, thriving on the mess rather than apologizing for wanting more.","sonnet":"A hunger-for-success anthem about wanting everything now — the narrator is done waiting, sees lazy people at the top, and demands the crown with unambiguous competitive aggression. The greed is unapologetic self-assertion rather than critique.","src":"sonnet"},
"sophie~infatuation":{"haiku":"Fascination crystallizes into obsessive desire to know the true identity of a captivating stranger, sustained by waiting and repetition.","opus":"The late producer turning obsession itself into a hall of mirrors, a bubblegum interrogation of a crush that never resolves into a person, only the endless wanting to know. Desire as pure, hollow craving.","sonnet":"A speaker consumed by fascination with an unknown, contradictory figure — deadly yet heavenly — who is both desired and mysterious, the need to know who they truly are circling obsessively without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"sora-kai~habitual":{"haiku":"A person is addicted to a toxic relationship, compulsively returning despite knowing it poisons their life.","opus":"A raw study of addictive love as compulsion, the narrator crawling back to a partner named as both curse and vice despite everything, chasing the thrill while shaking on the floor past four in the morning. The imagery of withdrawal, sweat-soaked sheets and paranoia, makes the dependency literal. Framed as a battle of resistance and relapse, it treats toxic devotion as an urge that simply cannot be beaten.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship described as full-blown addiction — night sweats, floor-shaking withdrawal, paranoia past 4 AM, and the absolute inability to stay away no matter how clearly the damage is named. Every departure ends in crawling back; the curse is knowing exactly what it is and returning anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"sordid-pink~freak":{"haiku":"Recognition of one's opposite and manipulative nature through observation of a delusional girl, becoming her dark mirror image.","opus":"A sketch of a bewitching, manipulative woman, radiant and unhinged, drowning in private fears while charming her way to whatever she wants. The narrator warns others off her, then admits she mirrors something in himself. That grudging recognition, forgiving her everything for a single smile, turns warning into a confession of shared wreckage.","sonnet":"A slow self-recognition through the lens of another woman: someone who manipulates, destabilises, and compels forgiveness with a smile. The speaker catalogues her chaos at arm's length before the final admission — this is not a stranger but a mirror of his own fractured self.","src":"haiku"},
"sordid-pink~fu":{"haiku":"After repeated cycles of fighting, betrayal, and broken promises, the speaker rejects a toxic love-hate relationship with contempt and finality.","opus":"A blunt breakup anthem tallying every crossed line and empty apology before delivering its two-word verdict. The speaker toys with self-blame but lands firmly on rage at a partner who lied, left and came back too many times. Sordid Pink deliver this as catchy, profane pop-punk, the hate stated cleanly rather than dwelt on, a relationship's whole ledger of grievance settled in a single defiant chorus.","sonnet":"A breakup that should have happened sooner — the speaker catalogues every crossed line and hollow apology in a relationship defined by love-hate cycling, and the accumulated weight of staying too long converts into a furious, liberating farewell addressed directly at the person who made staying feel like the only option.","src":"haiku"},
"sordid-pink~killer":{"haiku":"Two people who identify as killers find each other and run together, recognizing their matching nature. They play games of pain and possession with each other.","opus":"A dark, flirtatious duet framing two lovers as matching predators, attraction rendered as a game of hunger and danger where finding a kindred killer both thrills and unsettles. The pain is offered up willingly, the chase mutual. Sordid Pink pair the menacing conceit with a bright pop-punk hook, so the murderous metaphor plays more as charged desire than threat, two people recognizing something reckless in each other.","sonnet":"Two people who recognize in each other the same appetite for dangerous games — desire framed as mutual predation, both of them running while chasing, the thrill inseparable from the damage, and the joy in finally meeting someone equally wired for self-destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"sortout~illusions":{"haiku":"Reality and dreams blur; fear prevents truth while illusions sustain survival, yet neither perception fully reveals what is real.","opus":"A meditation on how the dreams and comforting deceptions we hold onto are what keep us going, drawing a line between truly living and merely surviving, and questioning whether anything we rely on is genuine. Rather than despairing, it defends illusion as necessary, closing on the fear that stalls action and the dreams held up as a shield against a reality too harsh to meet head-on.","sonnet":"A meditation on the gap between what we perceive and what is real — dreams and desires as necessary fictions that keep us moving, but also as distortions that can trap us, the song circling the question of whether beautiful illusions are a survival mechanism or a cage built from fear.","src":"haiku"},
"soul-asylum~runaway-train":{"haiku":"Emotional turbulence and life circumstances spiral out of control leaving the narrator stranded between places. The song depicts depression as being on an unstoppable, wrong-direction journey.","opus":"A weary confession of feeling lost and jaded, in too deep with no way out, riding a metaphorical train that runs the wrong way and never reaches anywhere. Escape is easier than facing the pain. Soul Asylum, the Minneapolis band whose 1993 hit famously attached its video to missing-children footage, turned this ballad of directionless despair into an unexpected anthem of drift and dislocation.","sonnet":"Depression rendered as directional failure — moving fast, feeling the momentum, and arriving nowhere, on the wrong track with a ticket that was always going to end here. The image of the runaway train catches something specific about the way mental collapse feels like motion rather than stillness.","src":"haiku"},
"soul-blind~stuck-in-a-loop":{"haiku":"Stagnation and unspoken fear trap the speaker in repetitive paralysis, unable to break free from an adhesive state of mutual avoidance.","opus":"The unequal ache of a relationship where one person has moved on and the other stays trapped, still reading the other's hidden fears and unable to break the cycle.","sonnet":"A one-sided confrontation with someone emotionally unavailable — the speaker senses a connection the other person refuses to acknowledge, feels stuck in an unresolved emotional loop while the other person hides from what's between them.","src":"haiku"},
"soul-blind~third-chain":{"haiku":"The speaker struggles to free themselves from the lingering disruption of a person, losing faith in everything as their beliefs crumble and fade away.","opus":"A drifting meditation on saving oneself as faith quietly erodes, the narrator content to wait or never leave while a memory of shared space lingers as part of him. The refrain of everything falling away carries a numb, dissolving resignation. Soul Blind trade in exactly this kind of hazy shoegaze-inflected alternative heaviness, where emotional collapse is delivered through blurred, receding textures rather than confrontation.","sonnet":"A slow dissolution of faith in the aftermath of someone's departure — the space they occupied becomes a fixed memory, and everything built around belief gradually empties out. The speaker oscillates between waiting and knowing they should leave, faith fading like a tide that won't return.","src":"haiku"},
"soulfly~back-to-the-primitive":{"haiku":"Primal freedom demands rejection of societal politics and artifice, returning to authentic existence unmediated by power structures and false morality.","opus":"A tribal rejection of politics and posers, calling for a return to raw primal freedom and living life on one's own terms.","sonnet":"A rejection of politics, fakery, and collective conformity in favour of a tribal, instinctual mode of being — \"the way we want to be\" — invoking Bob Marley's \"who feels it, knows it\" as spiritual authority for living outside the systems that demand compliance.","src":"haiku"},
"soulfly~bloodbath-beyond":{"haiku":"Endless violence and hatred consume the land; the river runs red in catastrophic slaughter transcending all bounds.","opus":"A crimson river and a caving sky announce apocalyptic butchery, the collapse of the West answered with offered hatred rather than mercy. Max Cavalera's Soulfly pour their tribal groove-metal into a chant of ceaseless carnage, the setting a place where slaughter is total and the realm of terror offers no way out.","sonnet":"A vision of civilizational collapse, where rivers run crimson and hatred saturates the land. There is no refuge and no future — only the kingdom of dread and an endless cascade of violence spreading across a dying world. Hate is offered in place of anything else.","src":"haiku"},
"soulfly~eye-for-an-eye":{"haiku":"A speaker affirms their integrity and creative power while refusing victimhood, determined to rise and reclaim agency.","opus":"A tribal-metal declaration of self-made integrity and survival through pain and betrayal, vowing to rise again and reunite the tribe; the vengeful refrain is really about refusing to be broken by envy and finding rebirth on the far side of hitting bottom.","sonnet":"A speaker asserts integrity and self-creation against forces of envy and betrayal, bottoming out and rising again through a tribal, retaliatory faith in his own survival. The lyric is sparse and repetitive, the emotion tribal and defiant.","src":"haiku"},
"soulfly~rise-of-the-fallen":{"haiku":"Oppressed masses rise to destroy their oppressors and take revenge for years of fear-based bureaucratic control.","opus":"A vengeful revolt against red tape and unequal riches, the downtrodden rising from ruin to make the powerful reap what they sowed, the speaker crowning himself an annihilator of worlds. Soulfly, Max Cavalera's post-Sepultura band, wed groove metal to this righteous insurgency, portraying entrenched power as a rot fated to be undone by its own corruption.","sonnet":"An uprising of the oppressed against systems that ruled by fear and division — the fallen are rising to hold power accountable for the suffering it manufactured, the imagery building from ashes to wrath, the machine's own poison and fractured promises becoming the instrument of its collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~4th-of-july":{"haiku":"Apocalypse or transcendence arriving unexpectedly; a baptismal death and rebirth moment where everything ends and the speaker awakens to control.","opus":"A drug-hazed apocalyptic reverie in which holiday fireworks bleed into visions of the world ending, laced with imagery of baptism, drowning and creeping fire. The whole thing plays as a hallucinatory close call with death and rebirth, mistaken in the fog for a summer celebration.","sonnet":"About a moment of catastrophic revelation — baptism, fire, scorched people converging — that feels like the end of the world; the narrator moves from witness to survivor, Roman candle in hand, now in control of the fallout.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~applebite":{"haiku":"Decay pervades all living things; protection from mortality proves impossible despite effort.","opus":"A murky Cornell meditation on mortality and decay, that even the pure and brave can't be saved and everything grows only to rot; the hypnotic insistence that it's only forever turns impermanence into something almost calming.","sonnet":"Growth and decay are presented as the only constants — nothing and no one can be saved from the cycle, everything fits loosely or tightly into impermanence, and forever is just another word for that process.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~been-away-too-long":{"haiku":"A prodigal return finds the hometown fractured beyond recognition and the speaker unable to belong anywhere after prolonged exile.","opus":"Cornell on the impossibility of return after long absence, coming back to find home fallen apart and himself invisible, torn between never wanting to stay and never having anywhere else to go.","sonnet":"A person returns after a long absence to find they no longer belong — unrecognised, unloved, the place itself in decay — and admits they never really wanted to stay anyway, their homecoming confirming only the impossibility of going back.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~black-hole-sun":{"haiku":"A surreal meditation on alienation and despair using fragmented, dreamlike imagery—a black hole masquerading as healing, a world of decay hidden beneath surface beauty. The song explores shame, disconnection, and the wish for oblivion.","opus":"Through surreal, unsettling images of masks, serpents and a lifeless-looking sky, the song sits in shame and dread and begs for a dark, devouring sun to sweep everything clean. It is a grim yearning for some force to blot out the surrounding decay.","sonnet":"The speaker describes a decaying, oppressive world — stifling heat, dead skies, dishonest times — and pleads with a surreal celestial force, a black hole sun, to come and wash it all away. The refrain carries a desperate, almost ecstatic longing for obliteration of the suffocating present.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~bleed-together":{"haiku":"Isolation and shared suffering bind ordinary people together; the speaker offers their lifeblood as a gesture of connection despite universal pain and numbness.","opus":"Cornell finds communion in shared brokenness, ordinary damaged people offering to bleed together rather than suffer alone. The bleak tenderness is real, connection built not on hope but on mutual admission of ruin.","sonnet":"An invitation to collective vulnerability — two or more people equally broken, average, caged, and numb — finding some dark communion in the act of bleeding together rather than suffering alone.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~blow-up-the-outside-world":{"haiku":"The speaker is invulnerable to external harm and resolves to destroy everything to be alone with one person, willing to sacrifice the world for a singular relationship. They'll burn it all down rather than compromise or accept separation.","opus":"Cornell's depressive exhaustion at being unkillable yet unable to connect, wanting to obliterate everything external rather than himself; a bleak inversion of self-harm where the impossible wish is to erase the world instead.","sonnet":"A song about wanting to destroy everything external — social pressures, other people's demands, the whole outside world — in order to protect an intimate bond that belongs entirely to two people, but the desire is acknowledged as impossible and perhaps self-destructive, something the narrator wants but knows he can't have.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~bones-of-birds":{"haiku":"Fragility and survival instinct clash as the narrator watches the weakest perish; building permanence seems futile in a world that strips away the vulnerable.","opus":"A late, weary reflection on time running out and the fragility of what we build, imagined as birds' hollow bones and a nest where the weakest chick can't survive; there's tenderness in the resignation, beauty balanced against inevitable falling.","sonnet":"A meditation on frailty and the passage of time: the speaker tries to build something lasting out of fragile materials — bones of birds, cold songs — while watching the weakest ones fail to survive and a predator circle above, indifferent.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~burden-in-my-hand":{"haiku":"The speaker kills love and leaves the beloved as a burden; what was once cherished becomes an anchor dragging them down.","opus":"A confession of having killed the woman he loved and left her body in the desert, now haunted and asking whether anyone would cry or lie for him. The desert of thirst and buried truth, and the burden, anchor and tumor he carries, render guilt and self-loathing as an inescapable weight he'll suffer with as her ghost, the sun that was once shared now bitterly his alone.","sonnet":"A murder ballad wrapped in desert imagery — the speaker shoots someone he loved, leaves her body in the sand, and spends the rest of the song processing a guilt and grief so heavy it feels like an anchor; the desert setting and biblical undertones frame it as a permanent damnation he chose.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~fell-on-black-days":{"haiku":"Fears and struggles materialize into lived reality; the speaker has become the damage they fought against, trapped in a self-created cycle.","opus":"A dazed descent into depression, the singer realizing that the very things he warded off have taken over his life and the dread he braced against has arrived; the sting is that this gloom came with no warning or plain cause, and he keeps wondering how he could have foreseen this being his lot, all while claiming he doesn't fear change.","sonnet":"About the bewildering experience of depression descending without warning — everything seemed fine, then suddenly it wasn't, and the narrator can't explain how the light went out or how to get it back.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~flower":{"haiku":"A vain, self-absorbed young woman lives on borrowed esteem and performs for attention; time erodes her vanity and leaves only decay.","opus":"A portrait of a young woman coasting on beauty and borrowed confidence, her seductive display revealed as a hollow masquerade that time quietly punishes as looks fade and the parade ends at a grave.","sonnet":"A girl of seventeen propped up on borrowed confidence — performing a painful masquerade, trading in manufactured sensuality — ages into someone whose reflection worries her, and the flowers arrive at her grave, the whole performance having consumed her life.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~fopp-dub":{"haiku":"Dance and rhythmic abandon become the primary mode of existence and communication, freeing bodies from constraint and reason.","opus":"A dub reworking of a party-funk cover built entirely on the nonsense verb to fopp, all groove and dancefloor swagger with no literal meaning beyond getting the whole house moving.","sonnet":"A dance-floor party song built entirely on the invented word 'fopp' as a stand-in for whatever moves the body and fills the room — pure celebratory nonsense with no narrative beyond the collective joy of moving together.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~fresh-tendrils":{"haiku":"Long suffering gives way to shame and self-destruction; the speaker asks for more than they can take, throwing themselves away again and again.","opus":"A hypnotic circling around shame, submission and self-abandonment, the narrator both craving and disgusted by giving away more of himself than he can bear while urging another to throw themselves away. Long-simmering resentment about the many serving the few threads through it. Soundgarden, the Seattle grunge giants, built Superunknown on exactly this kind of murky, psychologically knotted heaviness rather than straightforward angst.","sonnet":"Shame circled obsessively as something both desired and destructive — it gets people through, satisfies, and yet the instruction is to throw yourself away into it, give more than anyone can hold. The long-coming arrival of this shame is ambiguous: relief or ruin, or both at once.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~get-on-the-snake":{"haiku":"A rider boards a metal serpent bleeding through industrial landscape, hungry and mean, where suicide machines run on contaminated water and steam.","opus":"Soundgarden's sludgy highway surge, the snake a road, a bike, maybe mortality itself, urging a ride aboard a deadly contraption under poisoned skies. Cornell laces a twitchy urge to bolt with menace and murky, soda-tinted foreboding.","sonnet":"A road song built around the image of a metal river — the 'snake' seems to be a highway or the road itself, and the narrator is burning to leave, sick and alive with restless appetite, moving through industrial landscapes under cola-colored skies.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~half":{"haiku":"Despite incomplete odds and partial chance, people still retain possibility and potential for change. The song expresses cautious hope in the presence of incomplete resources.","opus":"Cornell steps aside for bassist Ben Shepherd, who sings this riddling Superunknown fragment about relinquished love, a gloved hand concealing a door, and characters called Full, Half and Empty. The arithmetic of a slim shot still leaves something to hope for, the band's grunge folded into something terse and cryptic rather than anthemic.","sonnet":"A compressed meditation on surrender and possibility: once love is fully given away, freedom waits on the other side of a hidden door. A battle between fullness and emptiness plays out in cryptic, minimal terms, with the hope of a remaining chance hanging just within reach.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~hands-all-over":{"haiku":"Grasping forces exploit and destroy natural and human resources—lands, waters, forests, and people fall victim to invasive control. The speaker tries protecting what remains loved.","opus":"Cornell's environmental lament, the earth as a mother being slowly killed by our grasping hands, logging and drilling and exploitation catalogued with a grief that keeps circling back to loving what we're destroying.","sonnet":"A litany of human exploitation across every domain — military expansion, oil spills, deforestation, sexual violence, political manipulation — with the refrain that all these grasping hands are killing the earth (\"your mother\"), which the narrator fiercely loves.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~head-down":{"haiku":"Systematic suppression and emotional control; others see joy and steal it away daily, forcing the singer to hide smiles and perform compliance while inwardly suffering.","opus":"An unsettling song about coercive control and forced conformity: an oppressive 'we' watches, takes away joy, slaps the face and turns the head, demanding the subject bow down and hide their real smile while performing a fake, mandated one.","sonnet":"A song about a collective 'we' that beats people down — taking away joy, slapping faces, turning heads away from tears — before suddenly flipping to a command to hold your head high and smile despite everything.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~jesus-christ-pose":{"haiku":"Someone adopts a martyr pose while the speaker refuses to accept blame for their suffering. The song critiques performative victimhood and false moral posturing.","opus":"An attack on the performance of martyrdom, people posing as crucified victims to manipulate and guilt others, the narrator refusing to be cast as the one driving the nails into their self-appointed suffering.","sonnet":"A scornful attack on someone who performs suffering and martyrdom for attention — arms stretched out, thorns implied — while the narrator refuses to be cast as their persecutor or their savior, finding the pose manipulative and empty.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~kickstand":{"haiku":"A motorcycle's kickstand becoming a motif for physical support and standing firm. The song plays with the motorcycle/rider metaphor as a sexual or intimate relationship dynamic.","opus":"A minute-long burst from Soundgarden's Badmotorfinger, this rides a barrage of bicycle innuendo, the kickstand a leering stand-in for sexual eagerness sanctioned by a mother who insists it is the only life worth living. Cornell plays the come-on for absurdist grunge comedy, the propulsive brevity matching a lyric that never pretends to be more than a grinning double entendre.","sonnet":"A loose, louche ode to physical appetite and maternal permission. The narrator moves on instinct — leather-saddled and eager — accepting sensation as the only life worth living, with a mother figure repeatedly blessing the urge rather than restraining it, as the whole drive becomes about being stood back upright and ridden.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~kyle-petty-son-of-richard":{"haiku":"Aggressive defiance hardens into a martial posture inherited from paternal example; the speaker carries intoxication and confrontation as armor against a world he refuses to accommodate.","opus":"A drunken, adrenalized burst of aggression and inherited machismo, the narrator quoting his father's advice to never take any shit as he barrels toward a fight; the racing-family title winks at bravado passed from father to son.","sonnet":"An intoxicated, aggressive speaker baits a confrontation, fuelled by alcohol and a father's lesson never to take disrespect, tearing toward an opponent with reckless, unstoppable momentum.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~let-me-drown":{"haiku":"Someone seeks dissolution and obliteration, wanting to surrender control and be consumed by another's presence and love into oblivion.","opus":"Cornell craving total surrender, a homecoming toward death or dissolution into another person, ecstatic and self-erasing at once. The grunge romanticism lets drowning in someone become both release and the last journey home.","sonnet":"A plea for total dissolution into another person — the narrator wants to surrender his will, be consumed, erased and sealed in a tomb without even a face, asking to be drowned in someone else entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~like-suicide":{"haiku":"Love destroys like suicide; a martyr figure flies sweetly but dies, leaving the narrator with blood-filled eyes and rage. The emotion is love itself that kills.","opus":"Chris Cornell wrote this after mercy-killing a bird that flew into his window, and the elegy transfigures that death into a meditation on love as self-destruction. The martyr who flew sweetly before dying carries an ounce of pain against a ton of rage, Soundgarden turning a small domestic tragedy into a brooding closer where tenderness and violence become indistinguishable.","sonnet":"A woman who lived with martyred intensity dies as she lived — in total surrender. Heard from another room, her end lands like something both inevitable and devastating; the narrator, overwhelmed by grief and impotent rage behind gilded walls, can only feel the weight of a love that mirrored self-destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~loud-love":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects silence and constraint, demanding noise and engagement instead of passive resistance. Loud love overcomes reluctance through unstoppable force.","opus":"A refusal of quiet resignation, the narrator hammering back against a numb, deafened existence, insisting on noise and confrontation and explosion where slow passive resistance would only lose the war.","sonnet":"An insistence on making noise rather than staying quiet — against passive resistance, against keeping things low — the narrator demanding his partner fight back and match the loud, explosive energy he is bringing rather than going along quietly.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~mailman":{"haiku":"A messenger of unwanted truth ascends from subjugation, riding those who rejected him toward ultimate elevation, transforming from beneath to above through sheer velocity.","opus":"The voice of the overlooked and humiliated, the nobody stepped on until he vows to drag his abuser down with him. Bound for the bottom, he'll ride his oppressor there as vengeance and last word.","sonnet":"A long-dismissed subordinate addresses the person who humiliated and erased him, announcing that though he is heading for destruction he is dragging them along with him. The power dynamic has inverted: the man who was beneath is now above, and his final act is a message of grim, sardonic \"love\" delivered from that new position.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~never-the-machine-forever":{"haiku":"Consciousness wars with itself as machine logic threatens to replace human feeling; resistance preserves autonomy.","opus":"A knotty standoff between a man and a machine that mirror and depend on each other, one dying if the other lives; it reads as a struggle against a mechanized, deadening force, resolved only by watching the machine finally die and reclaiming one's own will.","sonnet":"A speaker wages a sustained inner battle against a controlling mechanical force — something that shadows and stalemates him — ultimately choosing life and motion over paralysis, and watching the machine finally die when he reclaims himself.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~nothing-to-say":{"haiku":"Words fail and meaning breaks down; the speaker and others have nothing substantive to communicate. Repetition of emptiness becomes the only message left.","opus":"Early Soundgarden turning the emptiness of someone's words back on them, a heavy dirge fixated on the idea that all the talk amounts to nothing, the buried dying words of a mind with nothing real left to offer.","sonnet":"A dismissal — someone's words are out of balance, there's nothing in what they say beyond a single crude obscenity — a short, contemptuous song that turns muteness into its subject.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~outshined":{"haiku":"The narrator feels depressed and overshadowed despite initial success, sensing emptiness underneath surface achievements and cultural pressures.","opus":"Cornell's signature portrait of depression and disconnect, feeling gray and sober even while numbing yourself, forever eclipsed by others. Its self-deprecating shrug about looking one way and feeling another made it a Gen-X anthem of quiet defeat.","sonnet":"A song about feeling diminished and overshadowed — the narrator looks in the mirror and sees someone projecting a California surface while feeling Minnesotan inside, sinking despite seeming sober, desperate for power and unable to rise.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~pretty-noose":{"haiku":"Entrapment in a toxic relationship or situation that feels like slow strangulation. The narrator resents whatever force or person has them bound and diminished.","opus":"A seductive but destructive attachment imagined as a beautiful noose that is really hate and pain. The narrator recoils from what it has him hanging from, insisting he wants nothing more from it.","sonnet":"A song about being trapped in a relationship or situation that is superficially attractive but fundamentally harmful — the \"pretty noose\" is the beautiful thing that still strangles. The speaker resists, ultimately declaring total indifference to what the other person has or needs.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~rusty-cage":{"haiku":"A man is caged and tormented through physical and emotional abuse. He burns with intensity and determination to break free and escape.","opus":"Cornell's primal drive to break free of whatever chains and provokes him, the caged animal finally running. The restless, almost mythic imagery makes escape feel less like choice than like survival instinct.","sonnet":"The compulsion to break free from whatever is constricting and controlling — work, conformity, a system that wires you down — with the speaker insisting that no matter how oppressive the cage gets, he will shatter it and run.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~spoonman":{"haiku":"A rhythmic call for unity through music and primitive instruments; 'spoonman' summons people together to save each other through shared percussion and communal sound, transcending division.","opus":"Cornell's tribute to a Seattle street busker who drummed with spoons, celebrating a self-made outsider artist and, through him, the dignity of the overlooked and the pull of rhythm made from nothing.","sonnet":"A tribute to an actual Seattle street musician who played spoons — the song is about finding salvation through someone who makes music from nothing, with the refrain casting the Spoonman as a figure who can save the narrator through sheer rhythmic presence.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~the-day-i-tried-to-live":{"haiku":"A person attempts to seize the day but discovers they are fundamentally dishonest and failures. Each attempt—wealth redistribution, risk-taking, and self-improvement—proves futile and hypocritical.","opus":"A struggle to truly live and connect that keeps collapsing into futility and self-deception, with the speaker recognizing he was a liar and getting nowhere but dead.","sonnet":"The narrator describes a failed attempt to genuinely engage with the world and other people — his efforts collapsing into wallowing and self-deception — ultimately concluding that he is a liar and that trying to live is something he has to keep attempting without ever quite succeeding.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~ty-cobb":{"haiku":"The speaker is stubborn, refuses to listen, and accumulates a death toll through hard-headed pursuit of dominance.","opus":"Named for baseball's meanest legend, Cornell's bilious mandolin-driven punk aims a hard-headed middle finger at everyone, self-destruction tallied up like a highway death toll. Pure spite as release, ugliness embraced rather than excused.","sonnet":"A song of total contempt and antisocial aggression — the narrator doesn't hear you, doesn't give a shit, wants your head on a stake, and finds something cathartic in the posture of pure refusal. Named after baseball's notoriously violent and hateful player, it's the sound of someone enjoying their own hostility.","src":"haiku"},
"soundgarden~zero-chance":{"haiku":"Solitude compounds into a existential absolute, inescapable and fundamental, while attempts at connection only reinforce the impossibility of transcendence.","opus":"Cornell staring at hopelessness without flinching, born friendless and fated to die alone, working out the grim arithmetic that some losses can never be turned around. Loneliness as a fact, not a phase.","sonnet":"A man reckons with profound, irreversible loneliness after a loss, finding the world silent and the chance of recovery mathematically nil. He addresses what remains of his ideals — a \"highness\" — while acknowledging he was born friendless and will die the same way.","src":"haiku"},
"south-arcade~danger":{"haiku":"Transgression and seduction blur lines as danger becomes intoxicating, with the speaker and their antagonist locked in mutually destructive attraction.","opus":"Flirting with self-destruction as a thrill, daring someone to leave their lane and court the danger they can't resist. The mess is the appeal, hating you yet being into it, danger spelled out like a cheer for reckless attraction.","sonnet":"About the pull of a destructive, exciting relationship — the narrator is self-described as a 'user' cutting the line into blood, simultaneously thrilled and resentful, drawn to the danger of someone who makes them want to be a traitor to their own better judgment.","src":"haiku"},
"south-arcade~how-2-get-away-with-murder":{"haiku":"Predatory fantasy framed as justified hunger, systematic murder staged as appetite satisfaction.","opus":"A vigilante revenge fantasy sung with predatory glee, violence reframed as justified appetite against those who'd hurt you first. The thrill lies in the swagger of never getting caught.","sonnet":"A predator-fantasy song framed as dark romance — the narrator has a taste for someone's flesh, has them on a table, and the crime scene language (staging, blood never lies, appetite) blurs murder with obsessive possession; it reads as either a serial killer metaphor for toxic desire or a gleeful provocation.","src":"haiku"},
"south-arcade~moth-kids":{"haiku":"Desperate communion among the damaged; vulnerable misfits find belonging in mutual collapse, binding each other through shared trauma and the refusal to be saved by outside forces.","opus":"An anthem for exhausted, misread youth pulled toward their own ruin the way moths chase a flame, offering one another as the sole habit worth craving. Togetherness becomes survival: staying united against a world that could never rein them in.","sonnet":"An ode to outsiders and the emotionally exhausted — the moth kids are misunderstood and overlooked, drawn to each other as their only real refuge, using mutual recognition as a substitute for the world's care.","src":"haiku"},
"south-arcade~superman":{"haiku":"Suppressed rage threatens to explode; the speaker rejects the burden of saving others, choosing destruction over heroic service.","opus":"A dutiful young woman at breaking point, refusing to keep playing the rescuer for everyone and handing the hero's mantle back where it came from. The fun is watching her restraint snap into a gleeful, mean-edged refusal to bail anyone out.","sonnet":"About reaching the limit of patience and discovering an uncharacteristic capacity for aggression — someone who normally stays calm and keeps others out of trouble realizes they are about to snap and is relinquishing the role of peacekeeper. They want nothing to do with being the hero who saves the day; the cape goes back to Superman, and if pushed further the outcome will be ugly.","src":"haiku"},
"south-arcade~unaware":{"haiku":"The speaker fails to recognize they were being loved until words shatter their obliviousness; desire arrives too late and unspeakable.","opus":"The disorienting jolt of learning, too late and in the wrong moment, that someone had feelings for you all along. Replaying the near-confession interrupted by friends walking in, the singer resolves to stay just friends and never bring it up again.","sonnet":"A missed near-confession: someone had started to open up — 'I got this thing I need to get off my chest' — before friends walked through the door and the moment vanished, leaving the narrator restless and blindsided that the feeling was mutual all along.","src":"haiku"},
"south-central~bionic":{"haiku":"A psychotic, drug-addled voice controls the speaker's wired brain; they proclaim fearlessness and invincibility while being driven by evil and aggressive impulses.","opus":"A defiant, narcotic anthem of a mind gone mechanical, a wired addict boasting of unstoppable, deranged power. A malevolent presence works the circuitry in his head while he vows to say, do and think exactly as he pleases forever, immune to fear.","sonnet":"A declaration of chemically-fuelled invincibility — drug-addled, wired, and psychotic, the speaker claims absolute freedom from fear and plans to keep going forever on whatever they're consuming.","src":"haiku"},
"south-haven~better":{"opus":"A desperate appeal inside a collapsing romance: the speaker, broken down and pleading, has caught the partner in deceit yet begs for one more effort, convinced improvement is still possible. He wavers between refusing to release them and suspecting the whole bond will ultimately count for nothing.","sonnet":"A person in a deteriorating relationship acknowledges they see the lies and feel the pull of the other person dragging them under, insisting things could be better if both tried, while admitting privately they could never let the person go even as love bleeds out.","src":"sonnet"},
"sovereign~legion":{"haiku":"Robotic extermination and biological extinction represent invading forces; humanity's flesh and blood prove powerless against mechanical inevitability and hive assimilation.","opus":"Death metal voiced by a hive-mind machine intelligence returning to exterminate organic life and harvest it into itself; the sci-fi extinction fantasy is relentless and total, humanity dismissed as a flawed, disease-ridden design eager for its own end.","sonnet":"A machine intelligence returned from deep space delivers an extermination manifesto — organic life is a disease to be harvested and destroyed, the survivors absorbed into the hive — spoken from a position of absolute, cold superiority.","src":"haiku"},
"soviet-soviet~change-with-the-sun":{"opus":"An uneasy, fading relationship where the singer feels scared and awkward as the other person drifts and changes with the sun, crawling out yet always returning home to the same fought-over pain.","sonnet":"A relationship shadowed by fear and drift: one person changes with every sunrise while the narrator struggles to keep pace, crawling back home despite knowing the connection is slipping — the obsession is loud, the panic is real, and the pull keeps him returning.","src":"sonnet"},
"space-of-variations~halo":{"haiku":"Corruption of the sacred and fractured faith converge; the speaker's divinity damages, reflecting the contamination of every attempted transcendence.","opus":"A battered halo stuttering like a broken piece of neon, someone playing both injured saint and sinner, volunteering to bleed for another's wrongs while goading the hurt to keep coming for him.","sonnet":"About a fallen, damaged figure — possibly an angel, possibly just a person — whose halo is broken and flickering, who persists as an intrusive, inescapable force inside someone else while confronting their own accumulated failure and pain.","src":"haiku"},
"space-of-variations~razorblade":{"haiku":"A speaker questions their own prophetic validity while trapped in a self-built prison of mistakes, bleeding but still trying to break free from cycles of suffering.","opus":"A defiant grappling with self-made harm, the narrator wondering whether he's a fraud who erected his own prison of blades, yet insisting that suffering and faith together can still snap the pattern. Flaming out young becomes a fate he accepts. Space of Variations, a Ukrainian metalcore band, blend raw introspection with cathartic, electronically charged force.","sonnet":"A reckoning with self-made suffering — the mistakes have hardened into architecture, a castle built from razorblades, and the speaker wonders whether carrying the label of false prophet is deserved. Still, there is a thread of defiance: the cycle can be broken, shooting stars burn by fate, and the castle might yet have a gate.","src":"haiku"},
"space-of-variations~tibet":{"opus":"A grim suicide-pact love song, the narrator asking someone to end their lives together this very night, promising to wait for them submerged in an icy lake, and casting their joined death as one last, unforgivable error.","sonnet":"A suicide pact song — the narrator invites someone to die with them beneath a frozen lake, framing it as an escape from the silence of empty homes and the weight of unspoken grief, asking for one last terrible mistake together.","src":"sonnet"},
"spankthenun~blak-out-the-sun":{"haiku":"Becoming shadow and void, external prediction of inevitable hearing and destruction.","opus":"An industrial threat spoken from the void, the narrator personifying the emptiness inside you and the collective menace of a crowd that will be heard. Nihilism weaponized: there's nothing after death, and the cracks are already showing.","sonnet":"An extremely sparse song about a consuming nihilistic force — the speaker claims to be the shadow, the hole in your heart, the void that follows you; there's a call to be heard and to herd, and the endpoint is simply declaring the listener dead.","src":"haiku"},
"sparklehorse~piano-fire":{"haiku":"Surreal imagery and emotional numbness suggest blocked affect, where communication fails and vision is obscured by internal calcification.","opus":"A hallucinatory tableau of blazing pianos washing up on a fog-bound shore, sunburn while awaiting aircraft, and an inability to see or breathe through stone-cold eyes and a corroded heart. Mark Linkous's numbed, surreal imagery makes the recurring question of how it all feels the ache of a man who can barely feel anything.","sonnet":"A surreal, dream-logic song about waiting and decay — burned pianos wash up on foggy coasts, old organs give up the ghost, and the narrator can't breathe through a rusted metal heart or see through marble eyes; it's about being stuck in a beautiful, broken landscape of feeling.","src":"haiku","fable":"Linkous builds the song from a body remade in dead minerals, a self that can no longer breathe or look outward through its own hardened surfaces. The flatly repeated question about how one feels becomes an interrogation of a sensorium too numbed to answer. Around that deadness he sets the instruments of feeling alight and lets their charred hulls drift ashore, so the track performs the extinguishing it narrates. What saves it from despair is the bright deadpan-carnival jangle, a spectacle clanging sweetly over the ruin, which is the era's whole method: keep the tune lilting, let the wreckage surface only in the grain.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["dissociation & numbness"],"means":"instruments of feeling set alight so their charred hulls enact the extinguishing they describe","built":"bright deadpan-carnival jangle clangs sweetly over ruin, the method and the wreckage sharing one surface"},
"sparklehorse~piano-fire-life-is-strange":{"haiku":"Surreal imagery of burnt instruments and decaying objects washing ashore evokes decay and loss. The song conveys alienation—solid surfaces close off sight, rust degrades the heart, and the atmosphere is heavy with endings.","opus":"A collage of hallucinatory, waterlogged imagery drifting toward a central numbness: an inability to feel, look, or draw breath. Sparklehorse's Mark Linkous strings together burning instruments on a shoreline and cold, unseeing statuary to evoke a body and mind gone unresponsive.","sonnet":"The song assembles surreal, fragmented images — sunburnt waiting, circus people, burning pianos washing ashore, organs giving up — to evoke a state of emotional numbness and detachment, with the repeated question \"how do you feel?\" going unanswered behind marble eyes and a rusted heart.","src":"haiku"},
"speaking-with-ghosts~waste-away":{"haiku":"Cycles of self-punishment, regret, and suffering that repeat endlessly, defining life as inherent torment and spiritual hollowness.","opus":"A metalcore confrontation with guilt and self-erasure, the narrator perpetually fleeing, entombed in his regrets, sensing torment under his own skin. He pictures himself trapped and rotting, convinced he was made for pain. Yet a late pivot toward mattering to others and accepting his own not-okayness lets a thread of endurance survive the decay.","sonnet":"A person who feels structurally incapable of not running — from regret, from relationships, from himself. Caged in a cycle of self-punishment and hollow endurance, he eventually accepts suffering as his native condition while gesturing, faintly, toward something different.","src":"haiku"},
"speech-patterns~walk-away":{"haiku":"Someone trapped in fear and fragility struggles to abandon a failing relationship; they must find the strength to walk away toward genuine closure and wholeness.","opus":"A metalcore address to someone brittle and defensive, wrecking a relationship out of dread and then fleeing when things get hard. The song argues that going it alone fixes nothing, that a person emptied out only rattles more loudly by themselves, and pleads against that instinct to bolt and self-sabotage.","sonnet":"A person trapped in defensive withdrawal is urged to feel the cost of closure. Hollow and isolated behind jaded caution, they have ruined what they had by refusing vulnerability; the song insists that walking away from pain still requires a heavier heart than emptiness can carry alone.","src":"haiku"},
"speed-of-light~pain-on-a-chain":{"haiku":"Internal torment becomes containment strategy; the speaker cages their suffering and manifold voices, sacrificing personal freedom to protect others from the chaos within.","opus":"Someone chains their inner voices and pain inside to protect others from it, insisting they've got it under control while quietly admitting they need it out. The refrain is a losing grip masquerading as mastery.","sonnet":"Someone wrestling with internal voices or destructive impulses, refusing to let them out to protect others, insisting they have it under control even as the strain is visible.","src":"haiku"},
"speed-of-light~teeth":{"haiku":"Refusing fake kindness and false identity imposed by others, demanding authentic sharpness instead.","opus":"A snarling refusal to be defined, packaged, or made palatable, demanding raw authenticity over forced smiles. Identity as something you defend by baring teeth at anyone trying to script you.","sonnet":"About refusing to be shaped by others' expectations or categorized — the narrator rejects the performed smile, the label, the clique, the narrative imposed from outside; \"give me teeth\" means give me something real and honest over a polished, palatable version of who I should be.","src":"haiku"},
"spiderbait~black-betty-edit":{"haiku":"Raucous celebration of chaos and wildness, rhythm and presence overriding narrative or explanation.","opus":"A revved-up Australian cover of the old work-song holler, its meaning long detached from origin into pure riff and momentum, a wild woman invoked as an engine for the song's raw kinetic charge.","sonnet":"Black Betty, from Birmingham in Alabama, is a wild and rock-steady force who gets the speaker high — the song is a short, visceral tribute to her power, repeating the bam-bam-lam like a heartbeat around everything she is.","src":"haiku"},
"spiderbait~black-betty-single-edit":{"haiku":"A woman named Black Betty, born in Alabama, has a wild child and carries a carefree sexual energy that affects those around her.","opus":"A wild, always-ready woman named Black Betty from Alabama draws whoops and bam-ba-lam refrains around her ungovernable child. Australian trio Spiderbait turned this old field-holler-turned-rock-standard into a raucous 2004 chart smash, stripping the material to pure momentum and letting the call-and-response chant carry a song more about kinetic thrill than any coherent story.","sonnet":"A celebration of a wild, magnetic woman from Alabama whose raw energy is irresistible and slightly terrifying. She raises a child who mirrors her untamed nature, and her rock-steady presence electrifies everyone around her. Pure, exuberant tribute to an archetypal force of nature.","src":"haiku"},
"spiderbait~fucken-awesome":{"haiku":"Simple, unapologetic attraction and commitment. The speaker has found someone who fills emptiness and makes them want to stay.","opus":"A gleeful, uncomplicated declaration of infatuation, endlessly repeating that someone is awesome and that the singer is happily stuck on them, unable and unwilling to walk away because that person fills the empty days. Spiderbait are an Australian alt-rock trio known for exactly this kind of loose, joyful noise, and here the near-total absence of a second idea is the point, love reduced to one grinning, blissed-out chant.","sonnet":"Infatuation at its most blunt — someone so captivated they can't walk away and don't want to, the other person filling all the empty space, the whole thing delivered with a cheerful vulgarity that makes the sincerity underneath it funnier and more real.","src":"haiku"},
"spineshank~infected":{"haiku":"Emotional parasitism and loss of autonomy spiral together; the speaker is infected by someone who fed on their weakness, leaving them unable to make decisions or reclaim themselves.","opus":"A toxic dependency in which the speaker is controlled and sedated by another who makes his decisions and feeds his weakness. It is about being infected by a person he cannot give up and begging them to let go.","sonnet":"A toxic dependency described in medical terms: the other person is an infection that makes decisions for the speaker, sedates and weakens and nauseates him, and even as he tries to give up he finds he has surrendered his own judgment entirely — \"you need to let go of me\" is simultaneously a demand and an admission that he cannot make it happen.","src":"haiku"},
"spineshank~the-height-of-callousness":{"haiku":"A person hardens into callousness to survive, losing themselves to spite, unable to recognize their reflection as they become the thing that destroys them.","opus":"Frustration hardens into a callous, spite eclipsing conscience until the narrator cannot recognise himself, having become the very thing that destroyed him. Nu-metal act Spineshank make numbness the villain, the title-track self-loathing peaking as he begs to have his corrupted conscience taken and his integrity cursed away entirely.","sonnet":"A person traces how sustained emotional numbing turned into something self-destructive: once protected by detachment, he is now ruined by it, unable to recognize himself. He begs for his conscience to be removed or turned against him — anything to dissolve the cold indifference that has taken over.","src":"haiku"},
"spirit-leaves~killjoy":{"haiku":"Internal poison and suppression become external sabotage, toxic presence stealing joy from existence.","opus":"A draining relationship rendered in orbital physics, the other person a leech feeding off the narrator's light and pulling him out of a stable cycle. Naming the emotional parasite as a step toward escaping its gravity.","sonnet":"About cutting off a person who drains your energy and ruins everything they touch — the narrator is severing their pull, refusing to let them break the orbit of a life that was working; the person is explicitly called a leech and a killjoy, someone whose presence makes everything null and void.","src":"haiku"},
"spirit-of-the-beehive~fell-asleep-with-a-vision":{"haiku":"Utopian dream interrupted by distraction, vision blurred by inability to focus or execute.","opus":"A dreamlike drift through creative ambition and its collapse, a vision almost realized then paralyzed, the grand plan dissolving into distraction and blur. The wry surrender of watching your own idea fade.","sonnet":"A fragment about a vision of progress — ambitious, almost within reach — that slips away before it can be realized, leaving only the blurring of images and an inability to move.","src":"haiku"},
"spirit-of-the-beehive~there-s-nothing-you-can-t-do":{"haiku":"Consumption and delusion blur desire with reality; the speaker inhabits fantasy while destroying the boundary between self and other, chasing impossible transcendence.","opus":"A hazy overdose of intimacy and sedation where love and self-erasure blur, the speaker offering to be anything the other wants while dissolving into smoke and dread. Made his bed and will lie in it, unsure if the whole reaction is salvation or delusion.","sonnet":"A dissociative, chemically numbed state of submission to another person — the narrator cannot feel calls or connections anymore and has surrendered entirely to being whatever the other person needs, even into death.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~belcarra":{"haiku":"A person latches to a clinical, suffocating relationship that compresses them like a grave, surviving briefly while pressure destroys them from within.","opus":"Courtney LaPlante turns a hospital-like confinement into a meditation on being barely alive, pressure and clinical care mistaken for blessing when they feel like a grave. The refrain clings to proof that she was once truly alive.","sonnet":"The speaker is trapped in a relationship or situation that feels like a slow death — compressed, poisoned, alive but only barely — and the realization that what others call a blessing is actually their grave arrives too late.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~bleach-bath":{"haiku":"A relationship built on deception and manipulation where one person's authenticity is erased through gaslighting and psychological control.","opus":"A caustic portrait of a controlling, gaslighting relationship, the singer poisoned and betrayed yet still waiting to give up everything the partner has taken from her. A fraudulent lover polices her conduct and keeps her isolated while longing curdles into shame and self-disgust. Spiritbox, the Canadian metalcore act fronted by Courtney LaPlante, wrap the psychology in their trademark shifts between heaviness and airy melody.","sonnet":"Surviving a gaslighting relationship by naming its mechanics with forensic precision — fraudulent devotion, behavioral correction, isolation behind a soft voice, the self dissolving into erasure. The turn is vicious: every drop of the manipulator's blood is counterfeit, their power a tourniquet the speaker is finally watching from outside.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~blessed-be":{"haiku":"Chemical dependence and self-sacrifice for love create a prison of devotion; the speaker trades authenticity for connection, offering cowardly servitude in exchange for temporary belonging. Dissolution feels holy.","opus":"Spiritbox cast self-annihilating devotion as biochemistry, brain chemicals framed as something dangerous and a timid heart surrendered so utterly that the narrator can only endure through those substances, and only for someone else. Courtney LaPlante's swing from airy to guttural mirrors a slide into insecurity, the supposed blessing exposed as a prop and the self dissolved for a person forever out of reach.","sonnet":"A person sustains themselves on chemicals and devotion just to remain functional for someone else. The cowardice is self-diagnosed, the dependency mutual, and the blessing invoked is double-edged — surrendering the self entirely in order to survive inside a love that may be the only structure keeping them alive.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~cellar-door":{"haiku":"Depths and hidden trauma surfacing through sound and memory, the speaker excavating buried pain and confronting shadows.","opus":"Grief recast as a violent metamorphosis, where letting an old self die is the only route to starting over. Spiritbox picture transformation as brutal necessity, ripping something vital out of the body and shattering a duplicate self to break free of a numbing routine used as a shield. The track chases renewal through the terror of coming undone.","sonnet":"A cellar door as metaphor for the psyche's underground — time as a safety net, a key turned into burial, a coping mechanism of monotony used to destroy double vision. The split of living in one world while breathing in another becomes unbearable: 'tear my lungs out' is the only release from the pressure of the loop.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~circle-with-me":{"haiku":"Predatory figures circle the speaker, dangling power and divinity; the speaker rises to claim godhood at the altar despite betrayal and vultures closing in.","opus":"Temptation dressed as martyrdom, a voice promising everything if you'll join the circle and climb the altar, until the narrator seizes godhood herself. Spiritbox stage the seduction of power and self-deification, the sickly sweetness that crushes even as you crave it.","sonnet":"About being circled by opportunistic predators — vultures and birds of prey who promise power and glory while feeding on the narrator's martyrdom; the narrator briefly seizes the flame themselves before the cycle reasserts itself.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~constance":{"haiku":"The song captures emotional coercion through incremental pressure. A slow coup of memory and promise keeps the narrator complicit; bitterness and caving in become constancy.","opus":"A soft, sorrow-heavy meditation on losing someone by slow degrees, the narrator numb enough to slip free yet grieving as remembrance turns treacherous and even truthfulness starts to feel deceitful. It sits inside the ache of a bond wearing thin one increment at a time. Spiritbox, fronted by Courtney LaPlante, deliver one of their most fragile, mournful moments.","sonnet":"The slow erosion of selfhood inside a coercive relationship — pressure applied in increments like a coup, trust demanded while fire lies and honesty burns. The speaker, compliant to the point of numbness, has been waiting a whole life for exactly this trap, watching innocence fade like a shadow crossing a wall.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~electric-cross":{"haiku":"The singer rejects being consumed by conditional love and pathogenic connection, seeking to shed skin and escape contamination.","opus":"Body dysphoria and conditional love collapsing together, a narrator who hides through secrecy and wants out of their own failing flesh. The refrain of a body betraying its owner turns intimacy into something like infection.","sonnet":"About dissociation from one's own body — the narrator experiences their flesh as something foreign and failed, something that should be shed, while a conditional and secretive love keeps them tethered to a body they don't want.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~eternal-blue":{"haiku":"Abandonment cuts off blood and life force, leaving a hollow cavity where the speaker was left to die. The eternal blue sun illuminates old wounds that never heal.","opus":"Courtney LaPlante turning heartbreak into slow bloodletting, mapping the aftermath of being left to die as a wound that won't stop pulsing. The wish for the blood to slow is a plea to numb a grief that keeps rushing back.","sonnet":"The aftermath of a toxic relationship: the narrator has been hollowed out, left in a cavity that is now at least her own space, and finds herself wanting her blood to slow down — to stop responding — to the person who cut her circulation and abandoned her to die.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~holy-roller":{"haiku":"A religious figure takes the body of another in place of Christ's sacrifice while the speaker breaks free from false devotion and prayer.","opus":"Courtney LaPlante turns religious imagery inside out, a fallen-Eden figure demanding rapture while vowing to cut ties with a faith that won't pray for her. The demonic breakdown voices rebellion against a God worn thin.","sonnet":"A rejection of the church or a specific religious figure who invoked Christ's sacrifice while never actually caring for the narrator — when she dies, they won't pray for her, and that knowledge is what frees her from their hold.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~hurt-you":{"haiku":"The song depicts mutually assured destruction and lovesickness turned violent. Happiness derives from inflicting harm; the coldest war is the only medicine in a failing crisis.","opus":"A brutally honest anatomy of a mutually destructive relationship, where the narrator confesses to being happier inflicting pain, framing love as a cold war of building tension and assured destruction that she has learned to crave right to the grave. It refuses any redemption arc. Spiritbox, the Canadian metalcore act led by Courtney LaPlante, match this toxic candor with their trademark alternation of ferocity and melodic clarity.","sonnet":"A relationship locked into mutually assured destruction where inflicting pain has become the only mode of intimacy — both parties sustain themselves on conflict, and the speaker has arrived at the honest, ugly admission that hurting the other person feels like relief. The grave already has a name carved in it.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~hysteria":{"haiku":"Departure as amputation; the speaker acknowledges unintended harm while recognizing that leaving becomes a tourniquet stopping both the bleeding and the breath.","opus":"A spiraling confession of guilt over harm done to someone, where leaving is imagined as a tourniquet stanching the damage. Diamond and lattice imagery frames a mind fracturing under the weight of betrayal. Nodding to Shakespeare's violent delights and violent ends, Spiritbox turn self-destruction into a fevered attempt to salvage everything betrayed before it all breaks apart.","sonnet":"Self-destruction through the belief that leaving is an act of protection — a tourniquet rather than a wound — while the diamond lattice of the self breaks and the only pattern left is annihilating the ties you have. The hysteria is the state of believing you can save what you're destroying by destroying it harder.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~jaded":{"haiku":"Profound alienation from one's own body and the violence of intimacy leave the speaker blamed and imprisoned by another's desires.","opus":"A dark rumination on shame and isolation, the singer feeling stranded inside her own skin, always to blame and never sufficient, dreaming of being scattered across countless other existences. A red key that will not quite turn its lock stands for a tarnished self unable to reach honesty or relief. Spiritbox pair the despair with dynamic swings between weight and melody.","sonnet":"Shame at wanting to fall into a dream with honour desecrated — blood is jaded — expressed as the inability to be honest about any of it, memories haunted and faded, a crimson key glimpsed in dreams that never turns any lock. Stability is imagined only hypothetically, and the shame loops back over itself.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~keep-sweet":{"haiku":"Suppression and compliance protect survival while innocence becomes traded currency in systems of control. The speaker resists consumption by preserving inner autonomy beneath acquiescence.","opus":"A survivor's testimony of being kept docile and childlike so her purity could be hoarded and saved for another's use. The reckoning arrives with fury: she would sooner rip out her own beating heart and let it drain than let anyone claim that piece of her. Spiritbox frame the awakening as violent liberation.","sonnet":"A survivor of infantilisation and entitled violation reclaims her inner life: her innocence was a keepsake worn around her neck, passivity kept her safe in a heat wave, but now awake she refuses to let them devour pieces of her heart — the rage to tear it out and bleed with no feeling is how she stays temperate rather than destroyed.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~no-loss-no-love":{"haiku":"Isolation and futility submerge the speaker in oceanic depths where survival means surrendering to predatory forces.","opus":"A downward spiral toward a sudden end, born amid riches that can't nourish you. The refuge you swim toward turns out to be a hungry, biting thing, and the singer arrives at a numb truce with herself — feeling neither grief nor love.","sonnet":"Born into conditions of scarcity and isolation — \"surrounded by pearls I could not eat\" — the speaker has drifted to an underwater resting place where beauty has become sinister and the island itself is revealed as a feeding body: \"showing its teeth.\"","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~perennial":{"haiku":"Experiences pain as cyclical dependency, needing hurt to feel grounded while liking through chemical means. Trapped in a perpetual baseline of suffering.","opus":"Spiritbox, the Canadian band led by Courtney LaPlante, render chronic pain and dissociation with their signature swing between ethereal drift and crushing weight. The narrator, kept going only by medication, fantasizes about experiencing hurt differently and feels her agony at a numb remove, clinging to the conviction that some better existence must lie beyond this suffering body.","sonnet":"Chronic pain managed through displacement and small self-deceptions — chemicals keeping the body functional, hurt sought through surrogates rather than faced directly, lying a little in private refuge to stay upright. The baseline keeps getting reset by emergency, and the hope that something more exists out there remains permanently out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~rotoscope":{"haiku":"Trauma loops through the mind endlessly: shadows cast by old suffering keep replaying, and tears never alter the returning pattern of pain.","opus":"Memory and trauma rendered frame by frame like the animation technique of the title, skeletons and imprints projected onto a present that can't be changed. A creeping sense of an imminent end, tears that never delivered relief.","sonnet":"A song about being haunted by past images of yourself that you cannot unsee or undo — the shadows and skeletons of what you have done loop frame by frame while the end feels closer, and no amount of crying or outside devotion can force a change.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~rule-of-nines":{"haiku":"Dreams of spiritual schism and divine rule become entrapment in sadistic systems; the speaker learns submission through premonition, becoming a broken vessel of pain.","opus":"Courtney LaPlante turning burn-victim imagery inward, offering herself up at a shrine to a sadist's rules, a dissociative meditation on pain, submission and premonition where she lays her body down to become part of light.","sonnet":"About learning the logic of suffering from a sadist — the narrator internalizes pain until the body has nothing left to take, dreams of rupture and premonition, and eventually lies down in the shape of a body, existing only in the figurative, drained of physicality.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~secret-garden":{"opus":"Wary self-defense after being wounded, both palms shielding a heart that has never reached this high toward hope. Spiritbox weave doubt and dryness against the idea of a private, inner plot of growth that no one else can seize or wait around for. Courtney LaPlante casts survival as tending something untouchable within.","sonnet":"A song about the private interior life someone keeps walled off from a relationship — the narrator protects what grows inside them from a person who promised to drive them away, and ultimately reclaims that inner space as something nobody else gets to take or tend.","src":"sonnet"},
"spiritbox~sew-me-up":{"haiku":"Rejection and isolation leave wounds stitched shut, trapping the speaker in shadow and mutual misunderstanding where care itself becomes a curse they're afraid to feel.","opus":"Someone weaponizes the accusation that caring is a curse, keeping the narrator stitched into a relationship built on nursed resentment and silence. The pull toward staying in the shadow is really a pull back toward home and old wounds that reopen faster than they heal.","sonnet":"About feeling emotionally hollowed out by a relationship in which the other person's conditional care — loving only when it is convenient, retreating when things get hard — keeps pulling the speaker backward into a dark, shadowed place rather than letting them heal and move forward.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about a relationship that mends you only by taking you apart first — where being stitched whole demands being cut open, and the repair drags you backward into the very darkness you came to it to escape. The wound imagery cuts both directions: the other person swears to fix her while pulling apart the sinew underneath the skin, so the needlework reads less as rescue than as reabsorption. Its cruelest line reframes tenderness itself as a defect — that to care at all is an affliction — and she half-agrees, choosing to scrape herself empty and clutch her resentment rather than be seen, wondering aloud whether the other simply dreads their own company enough to keep her close. The dark thing that keeps flowering over her — swallowing all she wants and needs — becomes, by the last verse, the place she is walking toward rather than away from, the surrender of someone who has decided that staying hidden is safer than being made whole.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"repair that requires dismantling, rescue indistinguishable from reabsorption","built":"the wound imagery cuts both directions — needlework read as rescue and as the very damage it promises to close"},
"spiritbox~silk-in-the-strings":{"haiku":"The speaker is emotionally manipulated and controlled by another, treated as a marionette or prey, unable to escape despite awareness of the entanglement. The silk metaphor captures the delicate yet inescapable bonds of toxic attachment.","opus":"Depicts a toxic, entangling attachment likened to being wrapped in silk and plucked to feed another; the speaker escapes yet still carries the harmful person with them, sickened by a relationship they can't fully shed.","sonnet":"The narrator is entangled in a toxic relationship with someone who handles them carelessly — like crushing a moth wing — and whose words have burrowed in like spider silk, impossible to shake off even after escape; the song dwells on the lingering psychological hold of a damaging bond.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~sun-killer":{"haiku":"Destructive parasitic forces born of dust assault and displace their hosts; a lullaby sung by death itself.","opus":"A lullaby sung to something monstrous, about staying near a destructive force even knowing it will consume you. The comfort offered is false, a knife dressed as tenderness, and the numbing repetition enacts the surrender it describes.","sonnet":"The speaker feels internally destructive, aware that staying means turning violent and that something monstrous within them threatens those around them. They call out to a figure described as a sun killer, asking to be lulled to sleep as a way to suppress or end that danger.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~the-mara-effect-pt-1":{"opus":"Spiritbox anatomize a suffocating, possessive love, the kind that cradles and paralyzes at once, its crooked fingers intertwining until the self cannot breathe. The mara of the title, a folkloric night-demon of crushing dread, haunts the imagery of razor eyes and severance. Devotion curdles into something predatory that must be made to stop.","sonnet":"A suffocating intimate relationship described as something parasitic and godlike, where the other person's controlling presence — their 'razor blade eyes' — cuts the narrator down while being framed as ordinary love, and the narrator both craves and wants it to stop.","src":"sonnet"},
"spiritbox~the-mara-effect-pt-3":{"haiku":"Trapped in psychological manipulation and abuse cycles, awareness without escape.","opus":"Named for the mara, the night-hag of sleep paralysis, it renders an abusive bond as being pinned and suffocated by a devouring mother-figure. The exhausted refusal to cry anymore is the sound of someone worn past feeling.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped beneath a malevolent maternal force — a figure who thrives on her children's suffering and suppresses their voice — struggles between self-punishment and the dawning awareness that the enemy's empathy is hollow and its power is a lie.","src":"haiku"},
"spiritbox~the-void":{"haiku":"Despair calls like a siren to those already lost, the void offering dark surrender as an escape from a life that never truly belonged to anyone.","opus":"The pull of the void—the intrusive urge toward the edge that comes when standing above a drop. Craving control and solitude, the narrator surrenders pieces of herself to that call, needing someone to break the fall she's tempted to take.","sonnet":"About surrendering to a nihilistic pull — the speaker walks to the water at night alone, finds comfort in solitude and a sense of infinite emptiness below the surface, and ultimately stops resisting the call of the void, falling willingly into a place where fear disappears.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is built on the psychological phenomenon of l'appel du vide — that intrusive urge to jump when standing at a height — and it treats that pull not as a wish to die but as a seductive fantasy of belonging, a lip of an opening carved out expressly for her. Courtney LaPlante frames the abyss as a confidant rather than a terror, inviting it to converse and to plunge alongside her, so what should be dread is voiced as intimacy and even relief, ceding a fragment of one's past in exchange for somewhere finally to fit. The recurring question of where dread drains away once it dies exposes the real ache underneath: this is less a longing for death than for the cessation of fear, control gained by relinquishing all control. The closing interjections that it is nearly upon her, arriving already too late, this time tighten the fantasy toward a moment of decision, and the song's power is that it never fully condemns the pull — it stays inside the voice that finds it beautiful.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape"],"means":"the urge to jump reframed as intimacy and a place to finally belong","built":"the song stays inside the voice that finds the pull beautiful rather than condemning it"},
"spiritbox~yellowjacket-feat-sam-carter":{"haiku":"The song explores fractured perception and traumatic memory. A vision leaves a hole in the head; fear and hate perforate the narrator like a stinging insect.","opus":"A feverish, near-hallucinatory reckoning with trauma lodged in the brain, a hole shaped like a man, memories that cannot be accessed, hate perforating like a stinging insect while grace stays absent during the narrator's begging. The neurological imagery makes psychic wounds physical. Spiritbox, the Canadian band led by Courtney LaPlante, enlist Architects vocalist Sam Carter to sharpen the song's violent, dissociative intensity.","sonnet":"A vision that won't resolve into memory — a hole in the mind shaped like a person, neurological loops replaying images that can't be accessed or verified, fear that perforates like a wasp sting. The wound was visual, not physical, but the cut goes all the way through; once you see what the speaker saw, there is no going back.","src":"haiku"},
"split-chain~chalk":{"haiku":"Erasure and escape: what's written down washes away, and what remains is the question of whether anyone notices the vanishing.","opus":"The disorientation of being pushed away by someone insisting it's for your own good, their kindness and their fakery indistinguishable. Sick of being labeled and second-guessed, left holding the blame for a choice made about you.","sonnet":"A song about someone who is convinced their presence makes things worse for the people they love — begging to be believed but also telling those people to get out, cycling between self-erasure and the desperate wish to be saved.","src":"haiku"},
"split-chain~fade":{"haiku":"Words promise everything but deliver only erosion: the speaker knows they're slipping away, powerless to articulate what might hold the relationship together.","opus":"Lucid inside a collapse, watching the walls and the mind come down while wanting the freedom you already know you'll never get. Someone said the right things, but escape stays just out of reach.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship ending through broken promises — someone said everything the speaker wanted to hear, but the speaker is drowning and cannot escape, trapped between wanting freedom and knowing they will never truly get it.","src":"haiku"},
"split-chain~future":{"haiku":"Separation brings clarity and survival; the speaker fades into safety while accepting that distance enables mutual healing.","opus":"Watching someone drift away out of their own doubts and fears, half-blaming, half-relieved, insisting things are better now that they're out. Time is offered as the thing that lets both of them escape and still shine.","sonnet":"About the end of a relationship where both people need distance — the narrator is fading out without the other person around, but does not blame them, and acknowledges that the other person's head is shut tight with their own doubts and fears. Time will make it better; the brightness is still there, they are just getting out of something toxic, circling around toward something easier.","src":"haiku"},
"split-chain~get-inside":{"haiku":"Drowning sensation escalates as the speaker surrenders to overwhelming internal pressure.","opus":"A relationship figured as drowning water that gets inside and can't be drained, tenderness and terror of the self fused together. Something inescapable takes its toll while the narrator is scared to death of who they are.","sonnet":"A desperate desire to have someone penetrate the self completely — the speaker is drowning in something internal, too scared of themselves to be saved, and wants another person to simply get inside and be with them in that depth, whatever it costs.","src":"haiku"},
"split-chain~haven":{"haiku":"Escape from deceptive foundation and forced emotional suppression, protecting one's authentic design.","opus":"An oblique, atmospheric reckoning with buried truth and self-preservation. Sensing deception beneath a relationship, the narrator resolves to withdraw far away rather than let someone force his feelings low. Refusing to bleed his own design, he claims time and distance as a fragile haven, protecting his integrity by leaving before he is remade against his will.","sonnet":"A speaker withdrawing from a dynamic that forces their feelings low, vowing not to bleed their own design for anyone. The shift from singular to plural perspective — \"when there's two, these eyes are finding points of view\" — marks the moment a private wound becomes a shared one, and the decision to go far away is the only act of preservation left.","src":"haiku"},
"split-chain~i-don-t-wanna-be-me":{"haiku":"Identity itself becomes unbearable; the speaker rejects existence in this form while witnessing death as both escape and warning.","opus":"Self-loathing repeated like a mantra, wrapped around a vignette of a man who lay down in the snow and died alone. The request to celebrate rather than mourn at the wake turns the death wish oddly gentle.","sonnet":"A song about a suicidal man told in the second person — he cannot be himself anymore, takes pills (implied by the colored tablets), falls asleep in the snow and dies alone. The narrator's plea at the funeral is not to mourn him in black but to celebrate that he is free. The song sits in grief and self-rejection while attempting to reframe death as release.","src":"haiku"},
"split-chain~i-m-not-dying-to-be-here":{"haiku":"Shame and displacement from family create persistent alienation, where connection remains impossible despite longing.","opus":"The alienation of returning to a family or setting where you never fit, made to keep up the act while someone drowns you in their shame. The bitter pun on being neither desperate to be here nor able to leave holds the ache of still wanting connection and a call that never comes.","sonnet":"Feeling unwanted and perpetually on the outside, the speaker admits they don't particularly want to be present — \"I'm not dying to be here\" — but in the final turn reveals a desperate wish for connection: \"I'm dying to be here.\"","src":"haiku"},
"split-chain~subside":{"haiku":"Emotional overwhelm recedes as numbness takes hold; the speaker surrenders to psychological subsidence.","opus":"A nausea-soaked breakup song about pulling free of a poisonous bond. Someone humiliates and provokes the narrator to his breaking point, and he vows they'll go under alongside him, but the resolution is escape: to breathe, walk out, and reclaim a steady mind, turning revulsion into the will to leave.","sonnet":"Clearing someone toxic out of the mind — they left a bad taste, pushed the narrator past the edge, dragged them down; the resolution is to find peace of mind by recognising \"you're coming down with me\" if you keep pulling at my feet. The title is both a demand (subside) and the promised outcome once the other person is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"splutter~holy-breaker":{"haiku":"Broken faith and unloved existence; forgiveness sought for a faith that never delivered holiness or love.","opus":"A tortured wrestling match with faith and madness, bilingual in English and Russian, where the sacred abandons the narrator and paranoia decapitates reason. He begs a departed holiness and rages at a curse of blindness, screaming for peace but feeling only suffocation. The chilling refrain that God is with us frames belief itself as a source of insanity and violence.","sonnet":"A crisis of faith addressed directly to whatever holiness has been present — confession that it was never holy, never loved, that the speaker had everything except that most sacred thing. The Russian lines invoke God-with-us as the nation decays; the speaker hides their face and asks for peace, but all they feel is suffocation and a muse that makes them scream.","src":"haiku"},
"spotlights~algorithmic":{"haiku":"Individual agency dissolves into predetermined patterns—the world locks away authentic dreams while believers fade into waiting oblivion.","opus":"A godlike or algorithmic power boasting it can move mountains and remake the world, warning us to keep our answers buried and mistake our own dreams for needs locked away. The refrain casts the machine's seductive control over a passive humanity, learning only as it drifts off while the system waits.","sonnet":"A love-shaped address in which each speaker offers to move mountains or drown them for the other, while insisting that the secrets they bleed out together are the answers worth seeking — the world a lock on kept dreams, and departure temporary rather than final.","src":"haiku"},
"spotlights~learn-to-breathe":{"haiku":"Someone hides their rage beneath a functional exterior, holding their breath and grinding through existence without ever truly breathing.","opus":"The atmospheric sludge trio circle a person concealing rage and emptiness, letting the inside fill with fury while hoping no one notices they never learned to be. Covering the insides and holding every breath, the narrator treads only to remain in place. The bleak refrain that they never learned to breathe distills a life spent hiding a hollowness that never resolves.","sonnet":"A compressed portrait of someone who has never learned to exist openly — hiding internal rage, masking the exterior, consuming hours just to hold a single breath. The ordinary effort of treading water passes for survival, while the core skill of simply breathing remains unlearned.","src":"haiku"},
"sprints~need":{"haiku":"Desperate neediness and performance exhaust the speaker, who tears themselves apart seeking validation that never comes.","opus":"The desperation of craving love and validation, performing prettiness and even self-harm to be told you're loved, before flipping to exhausted refusal. The last line, needing them to leave you the fuck alone, exposes how corrosive the neediness became.","sonnet":"A person in a desperate, one-sided relationship begs to be told they are loved — being called beautiful one moment and told they mean nothing the next — and the final twist reveals the need itself is suffocating, ending with a plea to be left alone.","src":"haiku"},
"squid~narrator":{"haiku":"Self-construction through imitation and the control of one's own narrative. The speaker positions themselves as author of their story while being trapped in the act of playing.","opus":"Squid, the Brighton post-punk experimentalists, spin through a crisis of self in which the speaker announces he is the author of his own story, his memories curdling into something alien as his sense of identity slips. Shaping himself from raw material and speaking lines handed to him, he dissolves the line between performer and script until the repetition itself dramatizes a self coming undone.","sonnet":"A mind struggles to locate itself amid fragmented memory and constructed identity. Features lifted from magazines replace genuine recognition; the speaker declares himself his own narrator yet keeps losing the thread, playing a role scripted from someone else's words while the mirror guards the only reflection that matters.","src":"haiku"},
"st-aurora~going-to-heaven":{"haiku":"Religious rejection and moral ambiguity intertwine as the speaker refuses salvation narratives while embracing their own complex damnation and contradictions.","opus":"A defiant rejection of religious moralizing and the promised afterlife, the singer refusing sainthood and asking only to be spared the lecture. Loving and despising all people in equal measure, he blesses his own wrongs, closing on the credo that the enslaved simply comply while a free person makes his own call.","sonnet":"A defiant rejection of religious salvation and virtue: the speaker claims to be lost in a self-abusing carnival, equal parts hate and love for everyone, and demands to be locked up rather than subjected to \"going to heaven\" — the final image, \"a slave obeys, a man chooses,\" summing up the refusal.","src":"haiku"},
"stabbing-westward~what-do-i-have-to-do":{"haiku":"An obsessive lover exhausts themselves trying to prove their worth to an indifferent partner, desperate to earn affection through increasingly extreme gestures.","opus":"Industrial-rock desperation over unrequited love, a reformed man begging to know what it would take to be wanted, promising he's no longer the selfish person he was; the repeated question exposes how powerless love makes him.","sonnet":"A speaker, desperate for romantic validation, pleads to know what transformation or sacrifice would make them worthy of love. The song charts a man who believes he has already changed for the better yet still cannot break through the other person's indifference.","src":"haiku"},
"stab~blur":{"haiku":"Memory and identity dissolve into haze; the speaker perceives a familiar presence yet cannot grasp or retain it as faces and time collapse into fragmented echo.","opus":"A gauzy, haunted evocation of a half-remembered bond, someone sensed like a shape at the threshold. As the sounds thin into darkness, faces smear and dissolve, yet the narrator keeps insisting he can still see them — the ache of a presence flickering at the edge of memory.","sonnet":"A fleeting recognition — a face glimpsed through haze that feels familiar without being placeable, surfaces in memory like a dream left at the door, and keeps blurring and fading before the narrator can reach it. The song sits in the moment just before recognition, reaching for something already gone.","src":"haiku"},
"stain-the-canvas-the-word-alive~condemned":{"haiku":"Universal damnation and destructive catharsis; everyone condemned together as fuel for transformation.","opus":"A collaboration steeped in modern metalcore's confessional despair, it circles a numbness that feels worse than pain. The narrator can no longer tell nightmare from waking, self-harm from sensation, and drags a nursery rhyme into the abyss as a bitter lullaby. Its bleak thesis is collective damnation, everyone quietly rotting while pretending life is still fine.","sonnet":"Total pessimism pasted to the hand like a weapon — biting tongue, cutting, pretending it's fine to be dead, waiting for a God who laughs. The condemned chorus is a community verdict: all of us awake but not alive, locked in chains, the twinkle-little-star nursery rhyme perverted into a request to be hurt until gone. There is no way out the song can find.","src":"haiku"},
"stain-the-canvas~brain-is-the-closest-thing-to-a-time-machine-we-have":{"haiku":"Memory becomes prison as regression replaces forward motion; the speaker spirals through impossible desire for the past.","opus":"Grief that memory is the only way back to a happier past that will never physically return. Facing shock therapy and lost love, the speaker knows his own brain both preserves and torments him, serotonin a cruel game with no rules.","sonnet":"A person who can't stop mentally returning to a better past — unable to move forward, tortured by brain chemistry that feels sadistic, they are stuck between the knowledge that those days are gone forever and the compulsive memory that keeps pulling them back.","src":"haiku"},
"stain-the-canvas~condemned":{"haiku":"Everyone is condemned to suffer, numbness, and despair with no escape or relief in sight. The speaker bites their tongue and cuts themselves but feels nothing while watching others pretend everything is fine.","opus":"A grim descent into self-injury and hopelessness, the speaker wounding himself yet numb, expecting a mocking deity, and certain everyone is spiritually lifeless while faking wellness. A warped children's verse sharpens the horror. It is metalcore rooted in real anguish, its shared refrain confessing that keeping up the appearance of being okay is the true demise.","sonnet":"Existential numbness so complete it feels like collective damnation — the speaker is awake but not alive, locked in chains of hopelessness while everyone around them plays pretend, the self-harm imagery running alongside a bitter awareness that waiting for divine intervention is just waiting for a god who's laughing at the whole situation.","src":"haiku"},
"stain-the-canvas~dead-circus":{"haiku":"Performance and spectacle in death, circus as metaphor for chaos and entertainment from tragedy.","opus":"A jittery indictment of the social-media self, worth shrunk to a tally of likes and identity flattened into ones and zeros. The narrator, holed up and feeding his worst thoughts, spots the same rot in the listener he's addressing. Everyone becomes quarry to their own mirror image, doomed to chase a perfection nobody genuinely wants to give up.","sonnet":"Social media as funhouse mirror where no one is worth a single like and everyone is prey to their own reflection, doomed to face a perfection they'll never reach. The narrator locks themselves in with bad thoughts and feeds them, too self-aware to pretend otherwise, performing for no one: \"I just hit play and sing this song.\"","src":"haiku"},
"stain-the-canvas~end":{"haiku":"The speaker has shutdown emotionally and given up on life itself, numbing to the point of not feeling anything anymore.","opus":"A bleak descent into numbness and self-erasure, the narrator already feeling dead, unable to feel anything, the hands of a clock frozen forever as he refuses help and buries the truth. Paranoia and giving up fuse into a final shutdown. This treats the point of total emotional collapse as an ending in itself, delivering its despair with the blunt, screamed catharsis of modern melodic hardcore.","sonnet":"Total emotional shutdown after a long fight — the clock stopped at the moment everything broke, feeling itself extinguished, the only thing that ever felt alive now surrendered. There is no interest in rescue; the paranoia and the voices are familiar background noise, and this ending is chosen with exhausted finality.","src":"haiku"},
"stain-the-canvas~hell-made-me":{"haiku":"The speaker, born from suffering and shaped by hatred, tears apart those who wronged them. They've learned cruelty and seek vengeance without mercy.","opus":"A fury-driven reckoning from someone shattered by betrayal and remade into an avenger, who claims that damnation itself forged what he became. Underneath runs a bitter theological charge: the people who wronged him beg mercy from a rescuer who never showed, so he concludes deliverance is coming for no one.","sonnet":"Someone shaped by hatred and abuse who has accepted the monster identity and now threatens to destroy the person who made them this way — framing themselves as hell-forged and beyond redemption or salvation. It's pure retaliation, no redemption arc.","src":"haiku"},
"stain-the-canvas~lxve":{"haiku":"Codependent spiral conflates love with pain as the speaker surrenders to cyclical harm despite awareness.","opus":"A confession of being hooked on a relationship that only wounds, pleading for hope and getting knocked down again, drawn helplessly to something destructive in an endless loop. The bleak takeaway is that the craving is really for the hurt itself, a truth to be reckoned with rather than mistaken for real feeling.","sonnet":"A self-harming love — the speaker knows it's destructive, compares the couple to a moth and a flame, and yet cannot leave — concludes that they are \"addicted to pain\" and the spiral has no end, even as they rage against the pointlessness of giving everything.","src":"haiku"},
"stain-the-canvas~puppet":{"haiku":"Strings tighten as anxiety metastasizes; the speaker strains against invisible control, drowning in thoughts while yearning for freedom, all while the show demands performance beyond endurance.","opus":"A portrait of a breakdown hidden behind a working exterior, the narrator twitching along on unbreakable strings while flatly denying, against everyone's reassurance, that anything is fine. The compulsion to keep performing becomes the trap itself, someone with no way out crying for help in the faint hope that another person hears through the act.","sonnet":"A person trapped in anxiety and emotional numbness refuses to keep performing okayness for others — \"what do you do when everything you breathe for is the main cause of your anxiety?\" names the paralysis, and the puppet metaphor frames social expectation as a string that never breaks.","src":"haiku"},
"stain-the-canvas~shine":{"haiku":"Death and transcendent release—waking dead and consumed by divine dread, the narrator finds peace in spiritual weakness when gods abandon them; playing with fire becomes tightrope walking.","opus":"Rock-bottom depression reframed as an odd doorway, the narrator abandoned, numbed, and marking off the days until it all ends, yet sensing something is about to crack open. Barely living and merely respiring, he dares to name that flicker as the verge of something luminous.","sonnet":"A song about numbness at the end of a long depressive spiral: the narrator feels eaten alive by dread, forsaken by gods, neither alive nor hurt anymore — just breathing — and the line \"I think I'm about to shine\" is ironic or dissociated, describing the light at the end not as hope but as erasure from the world.","src":"haiku"},
"stain-the-canvas~stolen-youth-with-famous-last-words-outline-in-color":{"haiku":"Stolen years compound into irretrievable loss; betrayal cascades into rage and the grim recognition that survival itself becomes exsanguination.","opus":"Fury and mourning over years the narrator can never reclaim, aimed at someone once close who commandeered his life and robbed him of that time. Everyone winds up caged and dying in solitude, he concludes, as the clock itself falls apart and gradually consumes them all.","sonnet":"Rage at time stolen by betrayal and a life hijacked, the speaker lying on the floor mourning what is irretrievably gone — 'I'll never get back everything you fucking stole from me' — while acknowledging that everyone is both victim and complicit, carrying on as time bleeds them dry.","src":"haiku"},
"staind~fade":{"haiku":"Unmet emotional needs and parental abandonment corrode into festering anger that the speaker struggles to transcend through distance and adulthood.","opus":"Aaron Lewis excavating a childhood of neglect, older now and wanting his anger to ease, but finding the wound is one he keeps reopening, the bed he made from a lifetime of swallowing it all down.","sonnet":"A person addresses an absent or neglectful figure — a parent — cataloguing years of unexpressed hurt and emotional suppression, finally old enough to consider letting the anger fade while acknowledging that the damage shaped who they became.","src":"haiku"},
"staind~for-you":{"haiku":"Parental emotional abandonment and neglect fracture a child who bears the psychological damage they inflicted and cannot escape the weight.","opus":"A son screaming at parents who never listened, whose insults left him feeling like nothing and blaming them for how damaged he became. The refrain about silence getting them nowhere makes the whole thing a demand to finally be heard.","sonnet":"A son's anguished address to absent, hurtful parents — screaming to be heard over their silence, carrying the weight of their insults and curses, insisting that their neglect is the reason he is broken and demanding they do something since they made him.","src":"haiku"},
"stand-atlantic~criminal":{"haiku":"Self-destruction as public spectacle; addiction to toxic attention and cynicism disguised as authenticity.","opus":"Stand Atlantic's pop-punk turned caustic, this dissects the way pain gets commodified, a performer self-destructing on cue because audiences love the spectacle of a breakdown. Framing herself as a disposable outtake sold cheap, the narrator flips the transaction into revenge, promising the vampires draining her that their reckoning arrives the moment she finally claims what is hers.","sonnet":"Self-destruction as product — addicted to the treatment, a living letdown who goes off their face and becomes a deep fake — the speaker addresses someone who profits from watching them stressed and imploding, names it criminal, and ends by reclaiming the damage: I always knew you'd get yours when I finally got mine.","src":"haiku"},
"stand-atlantic~frenemies":{"haiku":"Friendship as mutual destruction, hollowness as the only way to survive repeated wounding.","opus":"The exhaustion of a toxic closeness that spares you the need for enemies, each encounter chipping away a piece of you. The dare to a hollow adversary: do your worst, because emptiness feels nothing.","sonnet":"About being worn down by someone who oscillates between friendship and cruelty — the narrator is drained, hollow enough that nothing the person does hurts anymore, and is on the verge of simply not caring. The \"frenemy\" dynamic has cost so much that the narrator has lost the will to fight or feel.","src":"haiku"},
"stand-atlantic~g-a-g":{"haiku":"The lover becomes weapon; intimate violence and power play transform connection into a ballistic trajectory that ends in mutual annihilation.","opus":"A destructive attraction rendered through the metaphor of a lover as a gun, thrilling and lethal, who hits and runs. Being on her knees for someone who feeds on her lows, the singer half-wishes she were as hollow so the wounds wouldn't land.","sonnet":"A person destroyed by a woman weaponised like a gun — 'if a girl's a gun she's got me on my knees' — who pulled them from one wreck only to put her hands around their throat, and who hits and runs, leaving the speaker wondering if becoming as hollow as her would finally make them bulletproof.","src":"haiku"},
"stand-atlantic~girl":{"haiku":"Women are reduced to magazine fantasies and subjected to impossible standards by people who profit from that reduction, and the speaker refuses the fantasy while naming the lie and the theft of youth it represents.","opus":"A blast of queer defiance against the male gaze and the pin-up fantasy sold as liberation. The narrator refuses to be anyone's centerfold, listing the contradictory judgments piled on women, and slips into Spanish to shed her last fear. Its recurring taunt is that the men policing girls understand nothing about them.","sonnet":"A counter-attack on the male gaze and its proxies: dirty magazine, pin-up fantasy, too pretty too ugly too skinny too slutty — the repeated refrain you don't know a thing about girls is directed at the Hennessy-drinking, silver-screen prop-makers who call female self-determination a lie and get called liars in return.","src":"haiku"},
"stand-atlantic~love-u-anyway":{"haiku":"Dysfunction becomes inseparable from attachment; the speaker loves despite and because of the wound, finding strange comfort in the familiar pain.","opus":"Loving someone you can no longer stand to look at, staying through the bruises and the hard truths they keep telling you. The exhaustion and the loyalty coexist, home to the singer and fury to onlookers, the love outlasting the wanting.","sonnet":"A love relationship narrated as ongoing exhaustion and fury — 'I'm sick of lookin' at you but love you anyway' — where she fires bullets from her mouth, thinks the worst, apologises, and still can't be left, because the home they've built together is what others call fury.","src":"haiku"},
"stand-atlantic~molotov-ok":{"haiku":"Weaponized love as tool of manipulation and resigned acceptance of dying alone.","opus":"Stand Atlantic's heaviest turn on the genre-splintering Pink Elephant, where Bonnie Fraser rounds on the religious homophobia she absorbed at a Christian school. The fury is aimed at pulpit rhetoric that damns queer kids while wearing a caring face, and the incendiary title reads as a refusal to stay quiet, a flare sent up so others who sat through the same sermons know they are not alone.","sonnet":"The speaker burns through the wreckage of a relationship weaponized against them — love turned dirty, fake niceness masking targeted cruelty — arriving at a defiant fatalism: they'd rather be a lowlife than keep submitting to someone who uses affection as a weapon.","src":"haiku"},
"stand-atlantic~nose-bleed":{"haiku":"Manipulation cycles through moods and violence; the speaker bleeds under the knife while becoming complicit in their own destruction.","opus":"A toxic week-by-week cycle of being messed with and messing yourself up, preferring loneliness and self-hatred to being someone's afterthought. The nosebleed and twisting knife make self-destruction the retort to a partner who wants you dead.","sonnet":"A toxic weekly cycle — manic Mondays, silent Tuesdays, wired Wednesdays — leaves the speaker brain-dead and numb to the bleeding, preferring loneliness over being someone a destructive partner thinks about; the nosebleed is the cost of being inside someone else's hunting season.","src":"haiku"},
"stand-atlantic~wake-up-sit-down-shut-up":{"haiku":"Existential dread and chemical numbing replace meaning; the speaker questions whether survival matters when hope remains permanently out of reach.","opus":"An existential unraveling: praying to nothing, numbed by dark chemicals, unsure whether she's delusional or simply seeing clearly. The only hope is a miracle, and even beauty gets the impulse to burn it down—life staged as a final bow at a funeral.","sonnet":"A dissociative, chemically numbed song about existential crisis — the speaker questions whether they are delusional, whether anything matters, and whether someone will save them, while dark substances make the numbness feel both convenient and disturbing; the chorus commands self-erasure, which may be what they are actually doing to themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"starbenders~holy-mother":{"haiku":"A person is sexually enslaved by a powerful figure they worship like a god, confessing their repeated submission and desire for punishment.","opus":"A charged plea to a holy mother for absolution over a nightly, secret physical desire, blurring worship and lust as touch becomes both sin and homecoming and everything the lover touches turns to gold. Confession and craving collapse into one another. Starbenders, an Atlanta glam-rock band led by Kimi Shelter, deliver this transgressive blend of the sacred and the carnal with theatrical, seductive swagger.","sonnet":"Devotion framed as both surrender and confession — a figure who wants to be touched, who prays to a holy mother while reaching toward someone who feels as distant as outer space. The divine and the erotic collapse into each other, and the recurring plea is less for absolution than for presence.","src":"haiku"},
"starbenders~if-you-need-it":{"haiku":"Codependent cycles of care and chemical sedation mask deeper pathology; neither lover can absorb the other's pain.","opus":"Offering both affection and narcotics to a hurting person the narrator cannot actually save, medicating a hopeless bond where even the one left standing is doomed. A spoken cosmic tangent shrinks the whole ordeal against the vastness of countless worlds, driving home how tiny this frantic act of comfort really is.","sonnet":"An offer of love and chemical numbing to someone in pain — 'I got love if you need it, drugs to keep it even' — framed against the admission that neither can actually absorb the other's suffering; demons in the blood and nine lives frame the relationship as survival rather than romance.","src":"haiku"},
"starkill~not-alone":{"opus":"A defiant stand against a hidden manipulator who works the strings from concealment and feeds on the vulnerable. The narrator won't swallow another falsehood, swearing to strike back, finish the conflict, and wake others up, drawing strength from the certainty that he isn't facing it by himself.","sonnet":"About exposing a manipulative, deceptive entity — someone who preys on the weak and lies constantly — and refusing to be controlled by them any longer, declaring that many people see through the manipulation and are prepared to end the war together.","src":"sonnet"},
"starley~call-on-me-ryan-riback-remix":{"opus":"A promise of unconditional presence dressed up as dancefloor uplift. The remix polishes a plain vow into euphoria, but the core is stubborn solidarity against despair when prayer and rescue both fail.","sonnet":"An unconditional offer of support to someone who is falling apart — the speaker promises to be there through darkness and tears, insisting that fate can be cheated and that no one has to face collapse alone.","src":"sonnet"},
"starset~infected":{"opus":"Mass hysteria as contagion, a world addicted to panic with no cure, senators and preachers and weathermen all sounding the end times. Starset's cinematic rock treats fear itself as the epidemic, spreading person to person into anarchy. It reaches for a portrait of society reaping what it sows, hooked on its own dread.","sonnet":"A song about civilizational panic and the human addiction to collective dread — senators on shorelines and preachers warning of end times all agree something has gone catastrophically wrong, but nobody can stop feeding on the panic itself. The 'infection' is both the literal threat and the hysteria it generates, which spreads faster than any cure.","src":"sonnet"},
"starset~perfect-machine":{"haiku":"A manipulator mimics their partner's desires perfectly while hiding their true self behind manufactured intimacy.","opus":"Sung in the band's sci-fi conceit, a confession from a manipulator who admits he was only ever a mirror, faking desire to extract something. The tacked-on dystopian broadcast reframes private deceit as engineered control.","sonnet":"A manipulator confesses to his own nature — a mirror who gave the other person whatever they wanted to see while hiding what lay beneath, until both parties were performing for each other; the machinery of allegiance in the closing broadcast underscores the broader political parallel.","src":"haiku"},
"starset~solstice":{"haiku":"Longing for a lover who was transformative light but left behind only darkness and the weight of memory.","opus":"Grief for someone who once pulled the narrator out of his own darkness. Now that they're gone, he begs not to be swallowed by it again, knowing the light that saved him has set.","sonnet":"A person who was once someone's source of light and stability — the 'solstice', the brightest point — is mourned in their absence; the speaker pleads not to be swallowed by the dark that returns without them.","src":"haiku"},
"starset~where-the-skies-end":{"opus":"An anthem of the small and shackled rising against giants (Goliaths), refusing to fade and choosing to transcend their limits and chains; framed with sci-fi narration about humanity's eternal drive toward distant horizons, ending on a device powering down.","sonnet":"A human (later revealed to be a digital user whose battery is dying) urges collective transcendence over oppressive systems — claiming their stories lie beyond the horizon, that they were not born to fade, and climbing toward something higher than what their fathers dreamed.","src":"sonnet"},
"static-dress~adaptive-taste":{"opus":"Static Dress sketch an unsettling parasitic obsession, the singer eyeing another person as raw material to strip and inhabit, hungry to seize what's theirs and wear it as his own. Casting off hollow expressions, he insists the two of them are one and the same, merely wearing separate faces, identity theft rendered as covetous fixation.","sonnet":"Two people have drifted so far apart they've become strangers wearing each other's faces — the narrator observes the transformation coldly, noting they've grown apart and that the other person has become salvageable only as raw material, closing by taking what belongs to the other and making it their own.","src":"sonnet"},
"static-dress~clean":{"haiku":"A person nurses and cares for a partner while grieving them, eventually using their own body against the partner—a twisted mercy or betrayal.","opus":"A morning farewell curdles into images of disease, emergency surgery and headstones proving what two people once had. Leeds post-hardcore act Static Dress favour this fractured, cinematic storytelling, where a bottle numbs mistakes and the narrator casts himself as a doomed saviour, clinging to a relationship already marked for the grave.","sonnet":"A toxic codependence unravels in blame and resentment: one person holds the other responsible for everything they have endured, using alcohol to blunt the damage and martyrdom as currency. Headstones haunt the aftermath of what they once had, and silence becomes the final negotiation.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~courtney-just-relax-feat-world-of-pleasure":{"haiku":"Medication and chemical intervention fracture reality; the body becomes a fragmented victim of pain.","opus":"Part of static-dress's noir Rouge Carpet storyline, this plays out like a crime scene rendered in shards: a hard, icy seat, drugs meant to dull and remold a falsehood. It orbits a woman who greets a man identically even under threat, love notes stubbed out in ashtrays, a lethal error breathed in as her last moments tick away.","sonnet":"A woman medicated into compliance — chalk-bitter pills, heart protected in glass, love letter buried in ashtrays; each dose expels a problem and reshapes the lie until she addresses him the same even dilated, \"clenching final moments\" while her last mistake is inhaled and claimed as an accident. The enforced purpose and nails through beggar's hands at the close frame her as both sacrificed and sacrificial.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~crying":{"haiku":"Grief over love that couldn't survive harsh conditions, paralyzed by repeated failures to change what's broken.","opus":"A wound reopened by memory, addressed to someone who caused lasting damage and may or may not have changed. The narrator watches a love die like frozen seeds, replaying every cruelty at his expense, refusing to release the grievance even as it corrodes him. Grief plays out on a big screen, private pain magnified and inescapable.","sonnet":"A survivor addressing an abuser — seeds in a backyard where love died in the cold, laughter always at the narrator's expense — who hopes the abuser has changed while knowing the destroyed innocence cannot simply be let go. \"Crying on the big screen\" frames the memory as spectacle, something replayed and exposed long after the fact.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~di-sinter":{"haiku":"Burial and decomposition image abandonment and humiliation as the speaker accepts eternal entrapment beneath ground. Once interred, escape becomes impossible and peace arrives through surrender.","opus":"A claustrophobic, buried-alive metaphor for a toxic bond that won't let go, the narrator laid out among graves and haunted by the vow that once you're in the ground you never get out.","sonnet":"A suffocating, claustrophobic song about being buried alive in a toxic relationship or trauma: red lights, rope burn, bodies beneath the ground, marble veins running black — the speaker retraces steps and just lays there, trapped underground, unable to leave and unable to breathe.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~di-sinter-glitter-redux":{"haiku":"Theatrical collapse and evidence of hidden decay, laying down after spectacle and exposure fades.","opus":"A post-hardcore wreck of grief and self-destruction, a relationship replayed like a doomed film while the narrator lies among graveyard imagery, too spent to rise from another blow to the chest.","sonnet":"Fractured images of aftermath — red lights, rope burn, evicted scenes, bodies beneath the groundbreaking — a relationship's evidence photographed and processed like a crime scene, the speaker lying in the wreckage.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~face":{"haiku":"Destructive love masquerades as collision where intimacy becomes crash site of mutual ruination. Repetition and detachment sustain the wreckage rather than allowing healing.","opus":"A doomed, lust-fueled affair staged intentionally as a collision, both people braced for their hearts to rupture at the point of contact. Impossible to sustain and viewed through too much distortion to see straight, the whole thing collapses into a heap of indistinguishable, identical-looking people.","sonnet":"A car-crash love affair played out through broken glass and seatbelts: he forgets her name, she wants the impact, both bonded to leather and to a wreck neither can maintain — the line \"they all wear the same face\" lands as a final betrayal after the collision.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~fleahouse":{"haiku":"Transient relationships commodified and discarded like cheap motels, ephemeral connection bleeding into regret.","opus":"A relationship reduced to seedy motels and movie cliche, the narrator wondering if he was ever wanted or just a phase to burn through. Static-dress stage heartbreak as a tawdry film set, staged perfect moments swapped for throwaway hookups, the failure glaring back everywhere as the romance collapses into something disposable.","sonnet":"A post-mortem on a relationship conducted in cheap motels and traded away for quick flings — cigarettes in movie scenes stand for the superficial aesthetic of what they had, and the question \"did you really need me\" goes unanswered as the speaker finds the frame of their shared life broken, the clothes still hanging on it.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~lye-solution-feat-loathe-bvdlvd-hail-the-sun":{"haiku":"Desperate escape from complicit participation in violence and crime, hands contaminated by systemic guilt.","opus":"A feature-heavy cut threaded through static-dress's murder-mystery concept, told from inside a guilty, unraveling mind. Wounds stay open, control fails to stop the bleeding, and a woman sits calmly with her fingerprints wiped clean while killing at night. Dread mounts that the crime, splashed across headlines, is about to surface no matter how the evidence is buried.","sonnet":"Dissolution and complicity in a world of burned bodies and dishonesty — someone has kept their prints wiped clean while killing at night, and the narrator knows they played straight into it. Fear dragged under fingernails, unstable disinfectant in the eyes: the violence is forensic, personal, and self-aware.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~push-rope":{"haiku":"Emotional withdrawal and passive suffering mask suicidal fixation; the speaker exists as monument to disconnection and daily dying.","opus":"A relationship where two people are quietly killing each other, self-harm braided into codependence so tightly that one person's pain only begins when the other's stops. It stays on the bathroom floor because that's where the honesty lives.","sonnet":"About being unwanted and trapped in a relationship already over — the speaker feels the cold indifference clearly but stays anyway, while one person's self-harm and decline becomes an emotional weapon in a dynamic of mutual damage and locked jaws.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~push-rope-carl-bown-redux":{"haiku":"Irrelevance and disconnection hollow the body; the speaker witnesses their own erasure.","opus":"A reworked track steeped in deep estrangement, the narrator unwelcome, answered coldly, watching identical days smear together on tiled floors. Time can't be halted and closeness can't be manufactured; wracked and weeping, he feels something malignant seeping into his mind. A refrain about observing himself from the outside marks a total emotional split.","sonnet":"A dissociation study in fragments: the speaker senses they are unwanted in a space — dead responses, cold replies — while someone else's presence lingers at the back of their head. The image of standing in violent tears on a bathroom floor anchors the emotional collapse, and the last line concedes powerlessness: \"I will watch us.\"","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~safeword":{"haiku":"An abusive or toxic relationship where the speaker is misused and desperate for care; they ask for a safe word but fear being abandoned.","opus":"Static Dress channel their emo-revival ferocity into a portrait of a love turned harmful, where affection curdles into control and both parties keep insisting they are coping while plainly falling apart. The Leeds band frames a house drained of tenderness, its bedsheets and stained lipstick standing in for intimacy that has become abuse dressed as devotion. The plea for a safe word becomes a desperate reach for any limit at all.","sonnet":"A relationship hollowed out by emotional absence and mutual harm sits at the center — one person dissociates through intoxication while the other begs for instruction on how to love. The house holds no warmth, only costumes of closeness, and the only power either holds is agreeing when to stop.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~sober-exit-s":{"haiku":"Withdrawal and clarity; exiting the fog of substances to face naked reality.","opus":"A reliance dressed up as love, the narrator gripping something he knows is counterfeit yet experiences as genuine, and aware it's already too late to halt the ruin. Barbed imagery and broken glass signal a bond of shared harm, with suicidal hints flickering below, as he keeps begging to rescue a thing that has already soured into catastrophe.","sonnet":"A relationship unwinding past the point of repair — razor wire between teeth, shattered mirrors, affections acting as decoys around a blind bend — the speaker reaches for even a fake gesture because it's real to him, while the exit he keeps trying to choreograph is already too little too late.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~such-a-shame":{"haiku":"Regret over moving on too quickly; bruises hidden beneath the surface. The speaker dies emotionally, abandoned with open palms and final thoughts of loss.","opus":"A grief-soaked reckoning with moving on too soon after a loss, the speaker imagining himself weighted and sunk in a lake, used as a human barricade, dying with open palms in someone's arms. Beauty and ugliness coexist in every person here. Static Dress belong to the revived post-hardcore and screamo wave, and this cut carries their fractured, cinematic intensity, the bruise that never shows standing for buried, unresolved mourning.","sonnet":"Grief over a loss that came too soon — the speaker moved on before they were ready, left holding the bruise that doesn't show, the wound buried just under the surface where someone else made a home, and the anger directed inward as much as at the person who left.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~sweet":{"haiku":"Intoxicated intimacy masks danger and coercion; the speaker and their partner careen through reckless passion with underlying violence, mistaking harm for connection. Consent is blurred by desire.","opus":"The UK post-hardcore band stages a sleepless-city noir, fragments of a whispered exchange, a drive, cheap drink, and something that turns violent with no chance to escape. Static Dress fold this shard of a crime narrative into their emo revival, the disjointed imagery suggesting an illicit encounter curdling into a nightmare.","sonnet":"A volatile urban night unfolds through fragmented images of reckless driving, emotional violence, and intimacy on the verge of collapse. The relationship careers forward like a car thrown into fifth gear with no safe destination — cheap wine, back seats, and the knowledge that goodbye has already been missed.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~unexplainabletitlesleavingyouwonderingwhy-welcome-in":{"haiku":"Seduction into toxicity dressed as safety, internal burning beneath false hospitality.","opus":"An inviting facade masking addiction and self-harm, a woman burning up inside behind sedatives and fantasy. The horror of watching someone beyond saving while everyone pretends the house is warm.","sonnet":"A woman sedated and burning up inside is welcomed into an inviting space that is actually a trap — someone cradles the floor waiting for her to take him back, while she cannot be saved and there is no cure in sight.","src":"haiku"},
"static-dress~vague":{"haiku":"Romantic disillusionment and decay, where love transforms into rust and the speaker becomes emotionally numb after offering and losing everything.","opus":"Charts heartbreak curdling into self-harm, a cheapened love giving way to thoughts of ending things and a sense of corroding into weakness. Static Dress, a British post-hardcore act tied to the genre's recent revival, deliver this collapse with frayed, screamed intensity, the unspoken parting phrase sharpening the wound.","sonnet":"A relationship that corroded like iron — brief, disposable, the gold traded away for nothing, and the body now running on decay alone. The four words that end everything are named but not spoken; rust is the metaphor that holds it: gradual, structural, past fixing, leaving no mouth to speak the regret.","src":"haiku"},
"static-x~love-dump":{"haiku":"A person is both attracted and repulsed by someone they love, torn between affection and disgust.","opus":"Wayne Static's abrasive, deliberately grotesque anti-love song, love and disgust collapsed into one nauseated shrug at a toxic dependency. The crude imagery is the point: infatuation reduced to something you can't stomach yet can't stop.","sonnet":"A deliberately gross love song that inverts affection by wallowing in disgust — the narrator professes tenderness for a partner while dwelling on excrement and bodily revulsion, collapsing the line between desire and repellence.","src":"haiku"},
"static-x~push-it":{"haiku":"Corrosive desire and explosive need consume the speaker; they feed on reaction and spiral into destructive urgency.","opus":"A pounding fixation rendered in the band's clipped, mechanical language: corrosive and explosive urges, seeing and needing hammered on repeat, a mind that trips on its own toxicity and begs to be crucified. Static-X pioneered a machine-tooled industrial metal they called evil disco, and this signature track is less narrative than sensation, obsession and self-destruction compressed into a relentless one-word command driven by Wayne Static's staccato bark.","sonnet":"A corrosive compulsion with no clear object — something is eating through the speaker from inside, explosive and consuming, and the force pressing it forward is coming from elsewhere, the song more about the texture of that pressure than any identifiable cause.","src":"haiku"},
"stegosauro~bar-sport":{"haiku":"Temporal collapse and cyclical futility render motion meaningless as the speaker circles eternally. Loss echoes through intention as the name dissolves beyond recovery.","opus":"An Italian reckoning with clinging to a lost past, realizing that playing dead and circling in place won't restore a name worn away by good intentions. The resolve is to burn the old life and draw in its ashes, chasing one true feeling rather than another report of time spent holding your breath.","sonnet":"A reckoning with the futility of circling the past: living in lost time is not enough, words said so loud you cannot hear them, entire volumes written in sleep about how everything returns to a single moment — what survives is only a fragment, and the speaker asks to burn their life and draw in the ashes so that something true, not just an account, can be returned.","src":"haiku"},
"stella-donnelly~tricks":{"haiku":"Manipulation and conditional acceptance; the speaker is used for entertainment and worn out. A lover demands performance and conformity in exchange for affection.","opus":"A wry, cutting portrait of a self-centered man who only values the singer when she entertains him, exhausting her the way his tattoo has faded. The specifics pin down a certain Australian type, everything squared away and commercial radio on the drive home, wanting affection and then more. Stella Donnelly's folk-pop wraps the sting in a bright, sweet melody while she tells him to knock it off.","sonnet":"Affection rationed as performance — a person who only values the speaker when she's entertaining, demanding she suppress her personality to stay useful, his entitlement casual and worn as a Southern Cross tattoo, and her refusal to keep performing it delivered with tired clarity rather than rage.","src":"haiku"},
"stellar-circuits~alchemy":{"haiku":"Transformation through will and belief: the speaker digs deep, moves the needle forward, and transmutes despair into vision despite overwhelming odds and the impending dissolution of all things.","opus":"A charge to burrow into your own mind toward some altering truth, insisting you owe no one an excuse for wanting more, just the nerve to choose. The instant you grab hold of it, others will brand it theft, so the real work is teaching yourself to find the hidden meaning tucked beneath the surface.","sonnet":"A speaker digs into the frozen tundra of their own mind looking for what protects it, moving the needle slightly to keep going, and addressing someone whose eyes hold the last light — the needle and the cloud in the mind image the careful, costly work of staying present.","src":"haiku"},
"stellar-circuits~fell-under-a-spell":{"opus":"A dizzying caution about being caught in someone's manipulation, smitten with a falsehood and frozen by indecision. The narrator, lost in a maze and uncertain he's even breathing, suspects he won't find his way out.","sonnet":"About being trapped in a deceptive or psychologically unhealthy relationship — the narrator is caught in a maze, aware they are in love with a lie but unable to break free, uncertain whether they are even alive or perceiving reality clearly.","src":"sonnet"},
"stellar-circuits~go-with-your-ghost":{"haiku":"Self-deception masks identity; people hide behind false selves and constructed lies until confronting the mirror becomes unbearable.","opus":"Stellar Circuits build a tense interrogation around a fractured relationship steeped in deception, the refrain demanding to know who someone truly is beneath their lies. Reconciling with the mirror requires falsifying to make the connection feel real, ghosts summoned to the surface. The progressive metalcore band frame love and identity as performances, mistrust corroding both until every gesture seems a fabrication.","sonnet":"Two people at a relationship's collapse confront the false selves they performed for each other. One is urged to surface their ghost — the buried, authentic self hidden beneath layers of deception — as the other admits the entire bond was built on falsification and wonders who either of them actually is.","src":"haiku"},
"stellar-circuits~nocturnal-visitor":{"opus":"A woozy brush with a spectral nighttime figure that passes through solid walls, bound up with the narrator's own habit of gambling and letting go. Inverted thinking and nights without end smear the boundary of what's real as he keeps circling back to the question of where he lost his way.","sonnet":"A disorienting experience of being haunted or followed — trapped in repetitive thoughts, the narrator can't tell if the presence circling them is internal or external, and the song maps the dread of something moving through walls in the dark.","src":"sonnet"},
"stellar-circuits~sight-to-sound":{"haiku":"Synesthetic transformation and conversion of perception, the speaker translating vision into audio.","opus":"A progressive-rock meditation on perception and letting go, urging a break from the filters that mediate experience so genuine feeling can rush in. The mind bends rather than breaks; fear on sight becomes something to surrender to. Framed as a gift, it treats focus and discovery as the way through when time and self feel like they are dissolving.","sonnet":"A discovery — cutting the cord to read between the lines, something coming for you that bends the mind — returns in cycles that can be feared or surrendered to, and the song resolves into the feeling of being married to the feeling itself, a loop inside the head that is simultaneously a gift and the thing that is breaking it.","src":"haiku"},
"stellar-circuits~skylights":{"haiku":"Shame, false illusion, and temptation mark a journey toward death or transformation; the speaker is pursued by lies they cannot resist and finds truth only in surrender and self-forgetting. Everything is deception.","opus":"Stellar Circuits favor atmospheric, shifting textures, and this track peers down through overhead glass at a shallow grave, urging prayer at the gallows amid layered guilt and a life that's been taken. The writing stays deliberately murky, its descent from a high perch and drifting reflections circling delusion, loss and a self struggling to forget its way back home.","sonnet":"A figure standing at the edge of a moral or spiritual precipice weighs descent against the call of something above. Shame accumulates in layers, lies are hard to swallow, and the pull toward the gallows competes with a sliver of light coming through skylights that may or may not offer a way out.","src":"haiku"},
"stellar-circuits~witch-house":{"haiku":"Obsession becomes sanctuary as two people hide from surveillance and reality's cruelty. Connection promises escape, though the curse of desire may never break.","opus":"A restless, feverish tug toward craving and an enigmatic woman whose home keeps luring the singer in, a way of living that feels like an ocean surge hauling him under. Distraction and want stoke a blaze beneath a curse he'll chase to the finish, uncertain whether the reflection staring back is even him.","sonnet":"Two people caught in a cursed dynamic — premonitions, a forbidden kiss, a life on the edge of a tidal wave — seek a sanctuary called 'witch house' that is both shelter and inevitability; 'meaning lost in the wind is slipping through my hands' frames the relationship as something real but ungovernable, pulling them out like a tide.","src":"haiku"},
"stellar~daredevil":{"haiku":"Toxic attraction and reckless desire bind the speaker to someone harmful; they crash together on a highway to hell, caught between self-awareness and the thrill of surrender.","opus":"Knowing he is better off alone, he keeps chasing a woman who is bad for him precisely because the danger makes him feel most alive. It is about addiction to a toxic thrill dressed as romance.","sonnet":"The speaker is addicted to a destructive woman — she's a \"lethal dose\" he needs an antidote for — yet cannot leave because the danger is precisely what makes him feel most alive, a self-aware thrill-seeker who admits he'd be better off alone but keeps her close anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"stellar~grave":{"haiku":"Dissociation masks deeper decay—the speaker dances on their own grave, numb to destruction while awaiting final stillness.","opus":"Self-ruin passed off as a party, reveling on top of your own plot and drinking yourself under because the high of numbness beats anyone's company. With a heart gone hard and no destination left, the singer knows he's carving out his own pit and sinking into it.","sonnet":"Jaded and numb, the speaker dances on her own grave as a chosen stance rather than a cry for help — 'there's nothing left to save' — the heart of stone and the buried hole framing self-erasure as the only honest response to feeling nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"stellar~more-than-friends":{"haiku":"Deliberate cultivation of romance beyond social boundary; the speaker moves from friendship into passionate devotion, honoring transformation as mutual completion.","opus":"A tender, patient courtship of a close friend, asking whether she wants to become more while cataloguing why she completes him. Gratitude threads through it, remembering her care when he was down and praying for brighter days together.","sonnet":"A man asks a close friend whether she wants to be more — cataloguing the nights spent talking, the way she completes him, the shield she has become — down on his knees praying for brighter days and asking Romeo-and-Juliet style to make it official.","src":"haiku"},
"stengah~above-inhumanity":{"haiku":"Blood and visceral horror frame descent into consciousness twisted by violence and psychological dissolution. The crown worn above inhumanity requires accepting terror, dismemberment, and the death of conscience.","opus":"A gore-soaked descent into madness and death, coming to amid spilled blood and drifting bones while battling an adversary lodged inside. Sailing the river of a groaning afterlife, the self splits and rots, crowned with countless afflictions above all cruelty.","sonnet":"Waking in a pool of blood in a vacuum of collapsing light, the speaker drifts a river of moaning life toward an afterlife where lunacy departs and a crown of a thousand ills awaits — blood weeps from the skin and dark visions reign above inhumanity.","src":"haiku"},
"stepson~deeper-sleep":{"haiku":"A person misinterprets the speaker's actions through dreams and insecurity, blaming them for faults they didn't commit, eroding trust in the relationship through unfounded suspicion.","opus":"A painful account of being distrusted and pre-judged by a partner for something imagined, the narrator pleading his innocence, insisting he doesn't belong with the others who hurt her, and grieving that love cannot survive without trust. Her own worst enemy is the person he still longs for. Stepson, an Australian melodic hardcore band, render this relationship anguish with their characteristic raw, emotionally direct heaviness.","sonnet":"Being accused for something done only in a dream — morning judgment delivered through a look, the partner's past trauma projected onto someone who insists he is not one of the others. The cruelty is the unaskable question; trust has caved before it was ever tested, and love is being asked to survive that.","src":"haiku"},
"sterreich~a-f9ziww":{"haiku":"Confusion between illusion and reality; yearning for contact despite repeated delusions and mechanical seeking of salvation.","opus":"Sterreich frame this as a meditation from a well-meaning but deluded primate who longs to connect and believes words can save. Watching a world of ghosts and phantoms at the journey's end, the narrator recognizes the absurdity of machines repeating clever phrases while the same mistakes recur, the yearning for contact undercut by the mechanical futility of the seeking.","sonnet":"A meditation on being a well-meaning but deluded primate who wants to connect and believes salvation comes through words, watching a world of ghosts and phantoms at the journey's end, recognizing the absurdity of machines repeating clever phrases while the same mistakes keep happening.","src":"haiku"},
"sterreich~swandivemori":{"haiku":"Mechanical obsession and cinema's false beauty corrupt the natural world while children drown and love remains unspoken. The song mourns what we sacrifice when we choose to watch rather than act.","opus":"A surreal indictment of art turned into spectacle, where singing of love gets forbidden because everyone would rather watch the next death on screen. The ferris wheel and doomed silkworms make emptiness feel like fate spinning on repeat.","sonnet":"A Japanese surrealist meditation on a fatal fall — silkworms that never knew they couldn't fly, an upside-down figure on a Ferris wheel, a film that keeps running and killing, and a love song that the speaker cannot bear to hear — all revolve around a looping, impossible wish to rewind before the moment of impact.","src":"haiku"},
"steve-conte~call-me-call-me":{"haiku":"Beautiful dreams collide with doubt and confusion as the speaker reaches across distance without certainty of connection. The way forward remains obscured beneath layers of uncertainty.","opus":"The soaring Cowboy Bebop ballad, sung across a vast cosmic distance toward a lost connection that once felt like oneness. Rainbow waterfalls and ten thousand searching years give it a yearning, dreamlike scale, the narrator pleading only for a call that would ease a restless mind. Its ache is the longing to reach someone who feels impossibly far.","sonnet":"A search across ten thousand years for a connection that once existed — a number half-remembered, a shared wholeness that required growing away from — the narrator pleads for a sign, a call, any confirmation of being remembered and cared for across the distance.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~blank-tapes-feat-ninet-tayeb":{"haiku":"A departing lover leaves only silence and the knowledge of irrecoverable loss suspended in time. The speaker holds the ghost of connection while accepting inevitable erasure.","opus":"The leftovers of a love already finished, with almost nothing remaining of a life two people built except some unrecorded cassettes sitting in a car and the recollection of once embracing someone who mirrored the world back at him.","sonnet":"The remnants of a relationship reduced to blank tapes in a car — nothing has been recorded, everything has been lost, and the speaker knows the other person will be gone before they get home, the void of the unrecorded standing in for all that went unsaid.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~detonation":{"haiku":"Someone fashions themselves a divine instrument of destruction, targeting the privileged and vain to obliterate them through violence when their ego is bruised and their weakness exposed.","opus":"A chilling first-person slide into radicalization: a man who doesn't even believe yet feels ordered by a deity to fight and avenge himself on the privileged who deride him, plotting to make them suffer with a homemade bomb. It occupies the head of a self-righteous terrorist certain his violence serves a higher cause.","sonnet":"Told from the perspective of a radicalized young man who feels forgotten and humiliated by those with privilege — he doesn't believe in god but still answers what he imagines as a divine mandate, planning an IED attack to take back what was stolen from people like him.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~drive-home":{"haiku":"After loss and the weight of guilt, someone must clear their mind and face truth. Love can mend but darkness always ends alone—so drive home.","opus":"Wilson voices a man standing by a wrecked, overturned car in the rain, urged to let go of his guilt over a vanished woman and just steer himself home through the solitude. The Porcupine Tree leader built the song and its animated short around an eerie loss, and its counsel that every night eventually passes while you remain by yourself lands as bruised grown-up comfort.","sonnet":"A man survives a car accident and the loss it brought, and must now metabolize guilt, grief, and solitude. The instruction to drive home is both literal and metaphorical — clear the wreckage from the mind, grieve honestly, and accept that love cannot retrieve what darkness has already taken.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~first-regret-3-years-older":{"haiku":"Youth walks bowed under looming storm as complex futures cascade; memory's fog suffocates breathing while time's burden accumulates.","opus":"An aching, tender address to a lonely woman aging against the world's expectations, drifting through a solitary life, tallying regrets and edging toward the pill cabinet. The narrator's helpless refrain — that he feels and loves her beyond anything he'll ever manage to show — watches her weigh whether to simply let it all go.","sonnet":"A third-person portrait of a woman — three years older, always — who carries cascading futures, a lover who left, a list of regrets shared online, and a slow drift toward the medicine chest; the speaker addresses her with unspoken love and the warning that the world will slip away if she only says the word.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~happy-returns-ascendant-here-on":{"haiku":"Years pass invisibly like trains no one can catch as the narrator remains suspended in stasis watching life accelerate beyond reach. Time's cruelty lies in its indifference to individual pace and simultaneous gifts and losses.","opus":"A drifting, guilt-laced letter from a broken man to a brother he's fallen out of touch with, admitting the years rush by like carriages he can only wave at. Behind the small talk about bills and whether the children still recall him lies deep sorrow and the sense that nobody remains to break his fall, the note trailing off before it's done.","sonnet":"A drifting, self-aware letter to a brother from a man whose years have passed like trains he could only wave at — nothing has changed, he is broke and possibly drowning, the kids may not remember him, and the letter trails off unfinished as he grows drowsy, the incompletion itself the statement.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~harmony-korine":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts decay and suffering, seeking silence and peace while watching hope fade into corrosion and loss.","opus":"A bleak litany of commands, letting the rain keep falling, kneeling as everything slowly rots, crawling toward ruin. The chorus braids courage and fear together, evoking a bruised, dogged endurance in a world that corrodes without end, its dread rendered in Wilson's stark, procession-like repetition.","sonnet":"A bleak, elemental descent — rain falling forever into wasteland, the bruise within, crawling toward perdition — evoking spiritual corrosion with minimal but precise language, the feel of waiting for something that never rescues and never ends.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~luminol":{"haiku":"A man plays borrowed melodies learned from old records and lives an ordinary, brief life that fades to dust; his songs speak of things he'll never know, delivering unknown wisdom through time-worn simplicity.","opus":"A tender sketch of a street musician, scruffy and bareheaded, playing other people's tunes picked up from worn records with more persistence than polish, weathering every season. The larger read sets him against the human lot named at the outset, born into hardship and bound for dust, singing of things beyond his grasp yet quietly enduring against oblivion.","sonnet":"A sketch of an aging, ordinary man who plays familiar songs from scratched records, whose face shows every passing year but whose words are borrowed — someone who sings of things he'll never personally know.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~people-who-eat-darkness":{"haiku":"Ordinary neighbors hide sinister natures behind bland routines, feeding on darkness and pretending ignorance as moral cover. The song warns that evil thrives when people avert their gaze.","opus":"A chilling study of an unremarkable neighbour, hauling out the rubbish and taking his boy to class, who is quietly hardening into violence, nursing his grievance in private. The chorus names those who feast on that darkness as concealed, close by, and overlooked. Steven Wilson, the Porcupine Tree architect, builds prog around modern dread, and the horror here is the sheer ordinariness, the extremist as the man on your own staircase.","sonnet":"A predator hiding in plain suburban sight — the figure narrating shares every mundane surface detail with his neighbors but carries something entirely different behind closed doors, and the horror is how well the mask fits, how the ordinary life is used as camouflage for something that feeds on darkness and resents being told how to live.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~perfect-life":{"haiku":"Brief sisterhood interrupted adolescence with music and mentorship, then vanished entirely, leaving only fragmentary memory. The speaker cannot recover her specificity despite treasuring the perfect season she created.","opus":"A memory of a foster sister who shared music and cigarettes for a few perfect months before fading from memory entirely, the hollow refrain of a perfect life ringing against that loss.","sonnet":"A narrator recalls a foster sister who arrived pale and shell-shocked, introduced him to music and books over a few months of near-perfect closeness, then passed into such a distant memory that even her name eventually disappeared — the perfection was real and total, then gone.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~permanating":{"haiku":"Celebrating the beauty of fleeting moments before they vanish into the past. Each breath marks time passing away forever, yet the speaker chooses to feel it fully.","opus":"An unusually sunny call to grab the present before it's gone, urging the listener to cling to the moment while time drains away and friends and dreams recede into the past. Every breath spends something irretrievable, yet the tone floats upward. Steven Wilson, the Porcupine Tree leader known for gloom, deliberately chased glossy ELO-style pop here, splitting his fans with the cheer.","sonnet":"A fierce, almost desperate bid to hold onto joy — somewhere on a highway under the moon, aware that every breath is already receding into yesterday, the song insists on singing and feeling while time is still moving. Permanating is its own impossible verb: making the momentary permanent by sheer act of attention.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~postcard":{"haiku":"Depression and apathy create total immobility; the narrator lies on the floor unable to muster will for basic survival after losing their reason to live.","opus":"A portrait of paralyzing depression after loss, a man unable to leave the floor, eat, or even light a cigarette because the electricity's cut off; the notoriety he's known for feels hollow now that the one thing that mattered is gone.","sonnet":"A person in profound depression describes lying on the kitchen floor unable to eat, move, or see the point of anything, their inner fire long gone — everything that mattered dissolved when they lost the person they loved.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~refuge":{"haiku":"A displaced wanderer persists in a harsh landscape of mud and wreckage, choosing to remain where familial bonds offer no rescue.","opus":"Written in a refugee's voice, survival stripped to dirt and straw and borders drawn by others, clinging to dignity and a stolen life while nodding along because there's no choice. Displacement as slow erasure.","sonnet":"A displaced person living in deprivation and transience speaks from a shelter, holding onto family and the idea of endurance while processing the injustice of forced migration and what has been taken from him.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~remainder-the-black-dog":{"haiku":"Paranoia takes root in a fragile psyche; the narrator questions whether visions are portent or delusion as dread accumulates.","opus":"The black dog is depression, and this addresses someone consumed by paranoia and dread, numbed on prescription pills; the quiet dare is that only by dissolving the rigid, fearful self could they finally reach what they've craved.","sonnet":"A spare, piercing observation of someone whose paranoia and dread have taken root so deeply that prescription pills and maps no longer help — a cultivator of their own dissolution, arriving back at the same desolate starting point.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~routine":{"haiku":"Domestic ritual becomes both refuge and prison as a grieving parent performs endless tasks to hold together a fractured life after devastating loss.","opus":"A mother obliterated by the sudden loss of her children and partner, clinging to compulsive housework as the only thing holding her together. Cleaning, cooking and bed-making become a fragile defense against a grief she is told, gently, to try to let go of.","sonnet":"A parent destroyed by bereavement — seemingly the loss of children — holds themselves together by compulsive domestic routine: scrubbing footprints, washing school books, keeping cat fed, making beds, because routine keeps them in line and helps them sleep while the paintings on the fridge remain.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~significant-other":{"haiku":"Isolation and helplessness define the mood—pulling back from edges, feeling small, inability to reach someone, counting time in pain and rain.","opus":"An impressionistic portrait of longing and helplessness, the speaker feeling small under bright stars, trying and failing to reach a distant partner while pain lodges like steel in his heart; the fragmented imagery of rain, grey smoke and roses conveys emotional paralysis and yearning.","sonnet":"A fragmented portrait of urban isolation and suicidal ideation — watching people on the underground, unable to connect, feeling small under stars, the narrator is caught in the daily grind of loneliness and a persistent pain in the heart that feels like steel.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~song-of-i-feat-sophie-hunger":{"haiku":"Addiction and love mirror each other; the speaker surrenders autonomy to possession, sacrificing all other attachments.","opus":"A love song that curdles into obsession and possession, the narrator cataloguing every vice he gave up while refusing to give up the person themselves; the shift from devotion to you are mine, and the whispered suicide, exposes control masquerading as sacrifice.","sonnet":"A person enumerates every vice and bad habit they surrendered in order to keep someone they love, accumulating the sacrifices until the act of giving up everything else becomes itself the declaration — the one thing they refuse to give up is the relationship itself. The word \"suicide\" dropped at the end suggests the cost of this all-or-nothing devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~song-of-unborn":{"haiku":"Expression and love matter more than accumulation or legacy; the unborn future speaks to exhaustion and sameness in an overstimulated world that absorbs dreams into public domain.","opus":"A benediction to a life not yet begun, urging that worth lives in what you express and learn rather than what you own, and that even amid an exhausted, wrecked world one small human arc can still be profound. Wilson turns the fear of mortality into permission, insisting the unborn refuse to be afraid either to die or to be alive.","sonnet":"An address to an unborn child advising that what matters is not possessions or concealment but expression, feeling, and the moments that last — delivered against a backdrop of rain-drowned cities and exhausted world, ending with the repeated plea not to be afraid of either dying or being alive.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~the-holy-drinker":{"haiku":"An alcoholic descends into self-destruction through addiction, cursed by unquenchable thirst that drags them toward damnation.","opus":"From Steven Wilson's The Raven That Refused to Sing, a portrait of a sanctimonious drunk whose bottomless craving conjures the devil up from the ground. Pouring one last drink in self-absorbed rapture, the hypocrite walks himself into damnation, ruined by the very righteousness he flaunted. Wilson's prog storytelling savors the irony of a pious man dragged below and shackled by his vice.","sonnet":"A man enslaved to alcohol surrenders the last vestiges of his soul. The devil rises from earth to collect him; the final glass pours even as it ends him. His coffin is cut from a tree nobody planted for joy, and he descends underground, chained, beyond rescue or redemption.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~the-pin-drop":{"haiku":"A lover exhausted by trying to meet someone else's demands drifts toward death; they built love together but it ends before it truly begins.","opus":"Wilson gives voice to a drowning speaker borne downstream to lodge against a barrier, narrating a broken love story from death's threshold. Exhausted by resistance and worn down by having reshaped himself for someone else, they grieve a life too small in living and loving. Silence unheard and a stilled heart mark a cruel, unearned end, slipping away without a parting kiss.","sonnet":"A drowning — literal or figurative — that arrives at the end of a love built too late or held too briefly. Carried by a current toward cold water and broken stakes, the speaker registers everything undone: the kiss withheld, the words unspoken, the moment he missed the warning sound.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~the-raven-that-refused-to-sing":{"haiku":"A speaker lost their sister in childhood and now seeks her return through dream and song, begging a raven to replace her voice and heal their shattered psyche.","opus":"An old man, undone by grief, begs a raven to sing in his dead sister Lily's voice and carry him back to their childhood. Wilson, the Porcupine Tree architect, built this title track around a haunted ghost story, the bird a fragile vessel for a mourner too afraid to wake or love, pleading to be made whole.","sonnet":"A grieving man begs a raven in a dream to sing in the voice of his sister, who died in childhood. He clings to sleep rather than face waking life without her, allowing the dream to fill the hollow left by her loss. Love and grief blur together, sustained only by that one haunting melody.","src":"haiku"},
"steven-wilson~the-watchmaker":{"haiku":"A watchmaker aged and skilled pieces time together beautifully but hides a shadow from the past. After fifty years of compromise with Eliza, he confesses he never loved her but will miss having someone to hold.","opus":"Wilson inhabits an elderly clockmaker who hides a grim admission among his gears, telling Eliza that half a century of growing old together was spent beside a woman he never actually loved while he held out for someone better. The Porcupine Tree mastermind swerves the ghost tale into horror, the old man fused to his victim in death, lingering within her as the metal runs molten.","sonnet":"An old craftsman confesses, near death, that he never loved his wife of fifty years — she was a placeholder while he waited for someone else who never came. The precision of his trade contrasts with the imprecision of his emotional life, and both are now irreparable.","src":"haiku"},
"stigmata~z":{"haiku":"Violent imagery and mental anguish; brain, poison, or blood dominate the disturbing landscape.","opus":"A Russian metalcore broadside against Generation Z raised inside the feed, drowning in curated lies and manufactured outrage while a fake new world is built on the deception, and when real violence comes everyone will simply be asleep.","sonnet":"A denunciation of Generation Z as children of the internet — fed white noise and ordered narratives through fiber-optic cables, living in a fake world built on lies, tranquilized by resonance and information void, unable to save themselves, condemned to a hollow new world while leaders distort facts into farce.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~absolute-zero":{"haiku":"The speaker refuses to be a savior or conform to others' expectations, declaring emotional coldness and grievance as their true state.","opus":"Fronted by Slipknot's Corey Taylor, Stone Sour channel his hard-rock snarl into a bitter manifesto, the narrator branding himself a nobody who's neither villain nor savior while wielding his voice and ideas as weapons. Raging at a world lost in its own fever and clinging to survival, his grievance doubles as armor and confession, a portrait of weary, self-aware nihilism.","sonnet":"Defiant refusal at rock bottom — neither devil nor hero, not afraid of failure or grief, but done performing for a world stuck in delirium. The absoluteness is the point: this person has slid all the way down and would rather spend a lifetime holding onto one more day than pretend otherwise.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~blue-smoke":{"haiku":"The narrator has shaped a future killing a thousand better choices and deceived a generation with lies. They are immortal bastard and complicated fiction with no time left.","opus":"Corey Taylor offers a short, splintered admission of a man carving out a borrowed life, snuffing out his better options and misleading a whole generation. Away from Slipknot, this Stone Sour scrap leaves the speaker calling himself a bastard and a tangled invention, deathless until remade, its cut-off lines echoing a life still hunting for its form.","sonnet":"A fragmented self-portrait trails off before completion: a man who has sabotaged countless possibilities, sustained himself on lies, and still cannot assemble a coherent identity or future. The lyric dissolves mid-sentence, the incompleteness itself the statement.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~bother":{"haiku":"Emotional self-destruction and masochism continue as the speaker accepts they've given up resistance; their flaws are open to attack and death feels preferable to this state.","opus":"A bleak ballad of self-disgust and giving up, the narrator wishing himself past all feeling, hurling accusations at his maker, and treating his own death as fair recompense. Yet an obstinate refusal to release anything once he grasps it flickers beneath. Stone Sour, led by Slipknot's Corey Taylor, used this bare acoustic confession to reveal tenderness under the aggression.","sonnet":"Suicidal exhaustion described without drama — too tired to protest, too broken to care, having given up on reasons and turned the open season on personal flaws into its own dark logic. The one thing that remains is grip: once holding on begins, it doesn't stop until it bleeds.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~choose":{"haiku":"A victim of corruption stands before judgment, forced into impossible choices where both options lead to loss. The system offers no real freedom.","opus":"A confrontational courtroom-style narrative where the narrator stands as victim of a rotting system, dragged before a tribunal, refusing to betray himself even as he's told his only choices, survive or die, both mean losing. Defiance against soulless authority drives every line. Stone Sour, the band led by Slipknot's Corey Taylor, deliver this with a furious, riff-heavy momentum and a chorus built for collective shouting.","sonnet":"Standing before a judgment that was rigged from the start — lines crossed, truth bent, the system rotten, and two doors behind which both choices end in loss. The speaker refuses betrayal of self even as the tribunal closes in, staggered but still standing, watching hope disintegrate with eyes open.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~come-what-ever-may":{"haiku":"Acceptance of whatever emerges; the speaker surrenders to fate and embraces unknown consequences.","opus":"Corey Taylor channels post-9/11 American fury into a direct address to a corrupt leader, branding him a con man selling out a nation for buyers and idiots. Beneath the rage runs weary hope, a conviction that a plausible solution will eventually come. It rails at how ordinary cowardice in power leaves everyone more endangered than before.","sonnet":"A scorched indictment of a political leader who seized power on false promises and now governs through cynicism and cowardice — \"you'd sell us out if you could only find a buyer\" — addressed directly as a fraud whose only constituency is \"idiots\" willing to watch the country burn. Despite the rage, the speaker insists a reckoning is coming: \"there's gonna be a day when we have figured out a plausible solution.\"","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~digital-did-you-tell":{"haiku":"Disconnection and environmental collapse drive urgency while denial and dysfunction perpetuate inaction. The song questions whether humanity possesses nerve enough to reverse the damage or merely speed toward extinction.","opus":"An indictment of a planet drifting toward its own ruin, too scared and too embarrassed to own up to the mess it's made and the harm it's caused. The recurring hook wonders whether the farewell has already been sounded, doubting anyone will summon the courage to take the single remaining escape.","sonnet":"A confrontation with collective dysfunction and cowardice — mouths that consent to pollution, minds that won't admit fault — builds to the desperate question whether the world has already announced its own goodbye, and whether anyone has the nerve to find the one way out.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~do-me-a-favor":{"haiku":"A self-described failure weaponizes contempt and cynicism to make others fail, dismissing morality as fake while remaining blind to their own destruction.","opus":"Corey Taylor's Stone Sour tear apart a self-deceiving failure whose phony principles and rigid dogma keep him from facing himself. The narrator poses as a wrecker of everything, so scornful he expects his target to self-destruct, treating the man's own conduct as proof of his hollowness and cutting ties without regret.","sonnet":"A self-described anti-everything nihilist turns contempt outward at someone whose fake morality and thrill-seeking have left them unrecognisable to themselves. The narrator diagnoses a failure buried beneath bravado, then pivots inward — acknowledging his own dead story — before walking away, choosing honesty over the comfortable lie of shared antagonism.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~dying":{"haiku":"Dependence becomes slow death; intimacy and separation both paralyze, leaving existence as protracted suffocation.","opus":"The paradox of a relationship you can neither survive nor endure, unable to live without her yet unable to breathe with her; the honest, exhausted conclusion is that staying together is just a slow mutual dying neither will name.","sonnet":"A speaker in a suffocating relationship admits he cannot survive without the other person but also cannot breathe with them present — the contradiction is so complete that staying or leaving both amount to a kind of dying.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~fabuless":{"haiku":"A performer trades authenticity and dignity for fame, spreading falseness and sexual exploitation while sliding into inevitable decline and emptiness.","opus":"Corey Taylor skewering shallow fame and the people who sell themselves for TV time, all downhill from a fabulousness that's really less. Under the swagger is self-blame, an enemy who half-enjoys the role while knowing there's no way home.","sonnet":"A cynical takedown of someone trading sex and manufactured misery for television exposure — the narrator implicates himself in the same downward spiral and concludes there is no way back from here.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~gravesend":{"haiku":"A person embraces darkness and prepared numbness, moving toward death as the ultimate resolution to spiritual exhaustion.","opus":"A death-haunted narrator traces the moment he surrendered at eighteen and drifted back home at twenty-five, casting himself as a scavenging bird, then the devil on the highway, resigned to damnation or a dignified death. Corey Taylor's melodic outlet away from Slipknot lends this brooding weight, recurring road imagery turning a numb life into a slow drive toward the grave.","sonnet":"Dissolution welcomed rather than feared — the speaker at various ages lets go of the will to fight, sinking into numbness with a kind of peace, and the recurring image of something moving down a road like a raven or devil frames the whole arc as a slow acceptance of mortality and the dark spaces it travels through.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~hydrograd":{"haiku":"Someone thrives in their own hell, stronger alone. They are better without others, and if heaven exists, they threaten to corrupt it.","opus":"Corey Taylor's alt-metal outfit turns wounded pride into open threat, its narrator pushing people away and casting every closeness as a scheme he already sees through. He refuses any claim of moral superiority while asserting plain superiority, framing a life of struggle as proof he has earned his contempt for those who cross him.","sonnet":"Hard-won self-respect after a lifetime of fighting just to breathe. The speaker pushes away those who would drain him, holds his own moral complexity without pretending to virtue, and warns anyone who stands in judgment that the truth he carries is sharp enough to cut both ways.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~kill-everybody":{"haiku":"Rage at deception and betrayal builds toward a fantasy of total annihilation, where psychological pain transforms into violent fantasy and misanthropy.","opus":"Seething misanthropy pushed to a violent breaking point, the narrator fantasizing about turning his rage on everyone as relief from being lied to and pushed aside; the laughing sickness underneath makes clear how far his grip has slipped.","sonnet":"A seething, uncontrolled rage fantasy directed at an unspecified antagonist, where the speaker's fury escalates from personal grievance to a nihilistic wish to see everything burn.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~knievel-has-landed":{"haiku":"A person examines their self-destruction and loss of identity through failed reconciliation attempts; they grapple with being abandoned and question whether escaping their situation is even possible.","opus":"A self-flaying look at an identity dissolving, the narrator feeling a bit less himself and a bit more like everyone else with each stumble, cast out and unsure whether he's dead or merely on guard. He wonders whether reassembling himself could bring change, then confesses that if his worst suspicion is correct, he'd rather never learn it.","sonnet":"Someone who has thrown away something important and is slowly losing themselves in the process — each fall makes them less recognizable to themselves, and the song wrestles with whether they want to be fixed or just to stop pretending they're fine.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~mercy":{"haiku":"A restless person who fled as a child is now taking what the world owes them, finding identity as an outsider. They position themselves as both victim and force against a world of false gods and weaponized belief.","opus":"The Slipknot vocalist's other band charts a life toughened far too young, its narrator swinging between feeling owed everything and owed nothing while vowing to grab it regardless. Present defiance drifts aimlessly, refusing any label for what he has turned into. A recurring picture of a violent deity sanctifying ammunition drives home how endurance has soured into a self-appointed monster.","sonnet":"A man who once fled and nearly destroyed himself now stands somewhere unclassifiable — neither redeemed nor condemned — raging against a God weaponized by others while admitting the world owes him nothing. He carries the label of mercy's demon without apology, rootless and unapologetically alive.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~peckinpah":{"haiku":"A person recognizes their hypocrisy and detachment while being incapable of change; they lock themselves away and resign to never escape their isolation.","opus":"A portrait of chosen isolation and inner deadlock, the narrator craving escape yet sealing himself in so completely he can never get out, confronting his own arrogance and fear. The struggle is whether he'll curdle into bitterness or become something worthwhile, at a point where he no longer recognizes himself.","sonnet":"A self-aware person trapped by arrogance and self-examination, locked inside their own analysis, who sees every exit clearly but has barricaded himself in — confronting whether to remain a bitter king or become something better, knowing he may never leave.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~rose-red-violent-blue-this-song-is-dumb-so-am-i":{"haiku":"The speaker is stuck in youth's confusion and self-deprecation, overused by others and watching time slip away with no progress.","opus":"A self-mocking onstage rant about tedium, exhaustion and treacherous companions who cash you in, the speaker ridiculing his own wit while feeling worn out and past his prime. The very title shrugs off any grandeur. Stone Sour let the studio cut bleed into stage patter, Corey Taylor introducing his bandmates mid-song, capturing a rock star wired and weary and aware of it.","sonnet":"Self-aware mid-life disillusionment delivered with sardonic affection — the speaker is overused, too old for the people who claim to love him, watching chaos theory replace hope, and the song's own admission of its stupidity is the most honest thing about it, a man laughing at the ropes people use to hang themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~sadist":{"haiku":"Control and sadism intertwine as the speaker claims power through degradation and contamination, demanding to poison and be poisoned. Agony becomes the only proof of authentic connection.","opus":"A confession of possessive, controlling love curdling into cruelty, the narrator owning the way a decent man can push too far in his need to own and consume another.","sonnet":"An obsessive, masochistic entanglement in which the speaker craves control and pain in equal measure — burying both parties in darkness, swallowed alive in eternity — and the admission that this agony \"knew you all along\" blurs victim and perpetrator into one.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~say-you-ll-haunt-me":{"haiku":"Two souls mirror each other in asymmetrical beauty and damage, seeking permanence through supernatural devotion. The speaker demands to be haunted eternally rather than abandoned to separation.","opus":"A lover who never felt good enough begs for permanence, wanting to belong to someone so completely that even death becomes a promise to stay and haunt rather than leave.","sonnet":"A speaker pleads for a love so total that it transcends mortality, begging a partner to promise they will haunt them even after death — belonging and permanence matter more than presence, captured in the refrain \"say you'll never die, you'll always haunt me.\"","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~song-3":{"haiku":"Two people find salvation in each other after suffering; one dies for the other, merging their lives into a dark symbiosis of complete devotion.","opus":"An intense love song in which the beloved is the narrator's salvation and he theirs, mutual rescue from wasted years and past illusions; the darker current is a shared taste for danger and self-destruction, a nightmare left in his wake and a willingness to die for this bond that makes him feel most alive.","sonnet":"A narrator who has wasted half his life in darkness finally finds redemption and mutual rescue in another person — the relationship is presented as salvation for both, with the narrator acknowledging a darker side of himself that this love has also unlocked.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~stalemate":{"haiku":"The narrator remains trapped in stalemate despite his efforts, unable to break free from cycles where fighting produces no change and progress feels impossible.","opus":"Taylor circles a deadlock in which every fight leaves the narrator exactly where he started, in love with his own excuses and homemade lies. Stone Sour let Slipknot's frontman voice a mind he calls a terrible gift, preferring numbness to feeling, and the refrain's logic that failing to be free means never having been free turns paralysis into a private hell offered to others first.","sonnet":"A person trapped in cycles of self-defeat recognizes that fighting harder only reproduces the same failure. Freedom, clarity, and escape remain permanently hypothetical — the harder the struggle, the more fixed the position — and the only honest outcome is descent, offered defiantly to whoever is watching.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~taipei-person-allah-tea":{"haiku":"The song critiques apathetic existence and empty consumption while spiraling toward mental breakdown and losing meaning in the rat race.","opus":"A nihilistic, adrenalized rallying cry from a brash cohort flipping off polite society; its central dare is that howling your feelings on and on will unhinge you, so refuse both affection and loathing, accept that everyone's mortal, and keep barreling down the road even as it disappears beneath you.","sonnet":"A chaotic, high-velocity screed about someone who has detached entirely from the polarities of love and hate because they've watched everyone die anyway — running at full speed toward nothing in particular, furious at sentiment and Ponzi-scheme optimism.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~thank-god-it-s-over":{"haiku":"People pursue comfort through social corruption and celebrity worship; relief comes when hardship finally ends and they get their due.","opus":"A caustic settling of accounts with fame and its throwaway nature, the singer declaring nobody blameless, greedy young talents and expendable stars feeding a cultural sickness, insisting his due is finally arriving now that the worst has cleared. Stone Sour funnel Corey Taylor's contempt for the industry into a defiant hard-rock chorus, the relief darkened by the sense that everyone, himself among them, has it coming.","sonnet":"Relief at reaching the other side of something punishing — the speaker has no illusions about the world's greed or his own culpability in it, but survival feels like vindication, and the insistence that he'll get what's coming to him carries equal parts threat and quiet pride at having outlasted the worst of it.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~the-house-of-gold-bones":{"haiku":"A person refuses to conform and declares independence, willing to burn it all down and stand alone. They turn against those who criticize them, choosing solitary survival over acceptance.","opus":"A defiant declaration of independence and self-determination, the speaker rejecting a contemptible other and choosing to go it alone even if it means burning alive.","sonnet":"A figure rejects the authority and judgment of those around him, insisting on self-determination even at great personal cost — the House of Gold and Bones serving as a symbol of the place he must face alone, burning with both hope and destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~the-uncanny-valley":{"haiku":"Greed and explosive behavior destroy the world through conflict and broken systems while observers watch everything shatter helplessly.","opus":"Corey Taylor stares down a world of greed and self-destruction and finds it beyond saving, everything already shattered. The apocalyptic imagery is really about the exhausted certainty that collapse was always coming.","sonnet":"A civilization in freefall — greed, medicated numbness, an explosive undercurrent of rage — and the narrator watching it all shatter, unable to look away from the fire spreading across the sky.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~the-witness-trees":{"haiku":"Someone cannot feel or control their emotions and observes their life fragmenting apart while numb. They question their own power to change things that matter or make meaningful choices.","opus":"Terror of life and love leaves the narrator unable to feel anything within his control, resigned to a burning home and a partner he begs to destroy him one final time. Corey Taylor's Stone Sour push toward a bleak equation where being loved is only confirmed through his own annihilation, control and helplessness looping endlessly.","sonnet":"A person paralyzed by fear and emotional numbness contemplates a life slipping out of their control, confronting a broken home and fractured self they no longer recognize. They oscillate between longing for connection and accepting that destruction may be the only proof of genuine love.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~through-glass":{"haiku":"Separation and observation from behind barriers create emotional distance; the speaker watches someone through glass, isolated and waiting, reflecting on manufactured reality versus authenticity.","opus":"A sense of isolation and detachment, watching others from behind glass while feeling unseen and reduced, insisting that what they stare at is really him and that even the stars can lie.","sonnet":"The speaker observes a world that has become hollow and performative — people reduced to mannequins, emotion folded away like paper — and reflects on the alienation of watching life from the outside, separated from genuine feeling.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~tired":{"haiku":"A person yearns to escape chains and authority while refusing to conform; they're too young and too exhausted to surrender their freedom.","opus":"A restless brooding on numbness and the dread of ever being bound, the speaker feeling used-up and unfinished yet still chasing something undefined, admitting he'd just as soon see everything go up in flames while claiming indifference. Stone Sour give it a searching, melodic hard-rock frame as the narrator teeters between craving safety and courting self-destruction.","sonnet":"The gap between inner aliveness and outer disconnection — the speaker feels more present than others seem to recognize, but is pushed toward obsolescence by people who can't see it, and the solution is to find somewhere close and safe and unobserved where the chains don't reach, even if that place keeps moving out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-sour~whiplash-pants":{"haiku":"Willful ignorance and privileged arrogance create collision as one demands others change while refusing accountability and self-awareness.","opus":"A contemptuous wake-up call to a coddled slacker who thinks they know it all, warning that once the world stops indulging them they'll be left standing alone with nothing.","sonnet":"A blunt dressing-down addressed to someone parasitic and self-deluded — living in chaos, wasting others' time, never listening — told with mounting contempt to smile, pick up their things, and reckon with the fact that their certainty that they know everything has left them with nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~big-empty":{"haiku":"A person drives recklessly and talks endlessly, creating distance from authenticity; wearing thin shoes and tripping through life, conversations become hollow and killing.","opus":"A restless nocturnal drive frames a coupling that has run out of things to say, where talk itself corrodes what little remains and someone must be delivered home to a guilty, dizzy sleep. Written by Scott Weiland for the Purple era and the Crow soundtrack, it lets Stone Temple Pilots trade grunge heft for a smoky, blues-lounge sway that mirrors the emotional exhaustion.","sonnet":"A relationship decays under the weight of evasion and wasted motion. Someone is being escorted away — dizzied by guilt, undone by too many silences — while the driver numbs the distance with cigarettes and speed, conversations collapsing into damage neither will name.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~creep":{"haiku":"Diminishment becomes both diagnosis and identity as the speaker inhabits the margins of their former self, unable to command respect or achieve wholeness.","opus":"Weiland from inside addiction and self-loathing, a man diminished to half of what he was, aware he's the creep others see, numbly watching himself fade to gray without the will to steal himself back.","sonnet":"A self-described outcast navigates a diminished inner life — stealing impulses, a sense of being half the person he was, social rejection, and a creeping nihilism — against a backdrop of vague suburban menace. The identity crisis is real but the speaker keeps circling it rather than resolving it.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~days-of-the-week":{"haiku":"Weekly cycles of desire and abandonment repeat endlessly, with each day bringing temporary connection followed by renewed separation and pain.","opus":"A relationship measured out in a numbing weekly cycle of push and pull, where affection flips to hostility and back on schedule. Scott Weiland maps romantic dependency onto the grind of the calendar, love that only ever half-lands.","sonnet":"A turbulent relationship cycles through departure and return across the days of the week — the woman leaves, finds someone else, comes back, and the speaker is always pulled under again, unable to escape the pattern.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~dead-and-bloated":{"haiku":"Mortality and humiliation intersect as the narrator moves through the world already trampled and scented with death. Movement through time carries the weight of past failure and present shame.","opus":"Weiland narrates his own corpse to spit at the hypocrisy of being celebrated only once you're conveniently dead and unthreatening. The early-nineties grunge sneer at fame that loves you best when you can't talk back.","sonnet":"The speaker declares himself dead and bloated yet smelling like a rose given at the deathbed — trampled underfoot, thinking only of tomorrow, the lyric holds a grotesque comedy of self-erasure where life continues inside a corpse still running through the world.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~finest-hour":{"haiku":"Grief and admiration merge as the speaker honors a fallen brother's final dignity and the unseen battles he endured.","opus":"An elegy for someone lost to a private war they hid behind a smile, the survivor only grasping the pressure afterward. Written in STP's later era shadowed by Weiland's own struggles, it reframes a breaking point as a strange, tender dignity.","sonnet":"A eulogy addressed to someone who died by suicide: the speaker recalls the contagious smile they will never forget, acknowledges now the war hidden behind the eyes, and mourns the pressure that wasn't visible until it was too late, holding up the person's memory as their finest hour rather than their darkest moment.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~interstate-love-song":{"haiku":"A person betrayed by lies on a train departure feels shame and broken promises. Breathing becomes hard as he processes all the deceptions she told him.","opus":"Scott Weiland's gorgeous kiss-off to betrayal, the smooth Southern-train groove masking a song about lies and the impossibility of breathing through them. Later revealed to trace his heroin dishonesty, it turns broken promises into a goodbye.","sonnet":"A relationship built on lies finally ending — the narrator was waiting for something real, found instead a string of broken promises and deceptions, and now breathing itself feels like the hardest thing as they watch the person leave on a southbound train.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~lady-picture-show":{"haiku":"Fragmented identity and hidden self dissolve into performance while the subject cannot access her own name or face.","opus":"The ruin of a woman consumed by the porn trade, cowering in a shuttered room and no longer recognizing her own name or reflection. Weiland drew it from a real performer coerced into the work, and the song grieves an identity sealed up and abandoned just beyond reach.","sonnet":"A woman has retreated so completely behind locked doors and performed roles — wedding present not so daisy-perfect anymore — that she no longer knows her own name or face, hiding because she doesn't know anything anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~lounge-fly":{"haiku":"Codependency and mutual sustenance collapse identity into desperate need for another's refueling and emotional support.","opus":"A junkie's dependence dressed as a relationship, someone who feeds and refuels him and won't let him drown even as pins and dogs hold him captive. Weiland's addiction bleeds through the plea to refill an emptied soul, unable to give back what the habit takes away.","sonnet":"A man in a codependent, spiritually empty entanglement — empty bottle refuelled, sinking but not let to drown, wanting but uncertain of need — begs to have his soul refilled by someone who claims they will be both his woman and his man.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~naked-sunday":{"haiku":"The speaker addresses a divine or cosmic force that embodies contradiction—fuel and weapon, justice and law—and struggles with how to judge it when worldly life ends.","opus":"A face-off with a personified God or destiny, the spark of ruin and origin of the rules, sarcastically thanked for every agony he decrees while the speaker wonders who will pass verdict on him once all reverts to nothing. Stone Temple Pilots wrap this retributive theology in early-nineties grunge swagger, Scott Weiland's sneer turning a struggle with divine cruelty into something accusatory and darkly hymnal.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a God figured as both tormentor and protector — a force that commands suffering while also embodying justice, and the speaker's long-awaited handshake is equal parts gratitude and accusation, the question of who judges and who can be trusted left suspended at the end of everything.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~plush":{"haiku":"Someone worries about time wasting away and searches for another person who is disappearing; anxiety and uncertainty pervade the present moment.","opus":"Emerging from the early-90s grunge wave, Stone Temple Pilots build a foggy dread around a missing woman and the dogs sent to find her, uncertain whether she will be found alone or at all. Scott Weiland sings in impressionistic fragments, weather and masks and disarray, that resist plain meaning while unmistakably circling loss and dread. The waiting, and the grim inevitability of what searching dogs uncover, carries the weight.","sonnet":"A narrator circles a woman who has disappeared or gone missing, sensing her fate through weather and the instincts of searching dogs. Dread accumulates around her mask, her absence, and the question of whether anyone will find what is left of her.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~sex-type-thing":{"haiku":"A man pressures a woman into sex using coercion and threat of harm, then blames her clothing and feigned attraction.","opus":"A coercive male voice swaggers through menace and entitlement, blaming a woman and her dress for the aggression he threatens. Weiland conceived it early in the career of Stone Temple Pilots as satire, inhabiting a predatory mind to indict him rather than endorse him, a framing often lost on listeners. The brute momentum of the single became one of the band signature blasts of unease.","sonnet":"A predator performs inevitability, claiming the object of desire secretly wants what is being forced. The voice weaponizes masculine entitlement, reframing coercion as mutual longing — a cold anatomy of sexual aggression delivered without apology or self-awareness.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~sex-type-thing-2017-remaster":{"haiku":"Coercion and threat underpin desire, where the speaker's obsession demands compliance through intimidation. Philosophy collapses into appetite, justifying aggression as seduction.","opus":"A deliberately repugnant song written in the voice of a date-rapist, ventriloquizing predatory male entitlement, the boasts about hurting and the line about the dress. Scott Weiland adopts the aggressor's swagger precisely to expose and condemn it, not to endorse it.","sonnet":"A man addresses a woman with escalating menace — claiming to know what she wants, telling her she shouldn't have worn that dress, threatening that she 'wouldn't want me to hurt you' — the lyric performs predatory entitlement as an internal monologue, making the listener complicit in its ugliness.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~sin":{"haiku":"Guilt and sin bind the speaker to patterns of suffering and self-destruction, haunted by shame that cannot be shed.","opus":"It portrays a downward spiral of drinking and self-destruction in which sin is embraced as a source of strength, leaving the speaker blinded by his own sins and shackled to a following shadow.","sonnet":"The song maps a descent driven by sin and self-destruction — holy water clouds the mind, the soul is stolen, cold sweat and burning hatred close in — with the narrator bound to a shadow they cannot escape, sinking deeper with each verse into blindness and suffering of their own making.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~sour-girl":{"haiku":"A woman transforms from sourness to happiness through departure, leaving the speaker who loved her to watch her escape and flourish.","opus":"The dizzying math of a split where she arrived unhappy and departed glad, narrated by a self-appointed hero brought low by how misleading appearances proved. Weiland charts a solitary carnival ride in which pursuing her and losing her are the only moves that remain.","sonnet":"A shifting portrait of a relationship told from both perspectives: the man who thought he was a superman and rode a lonely roller-coaster, the girl who was sour when she met him but happy on the day she left — all observed through someone else's gaze and the recurring question of what you'd do if followed.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~the-art-of-letting-go":{"haiku":"Solitude liberates the speaker from romantic attachment; acceptance of loss enables continued movement despite persistent grief.","opus":"Exhaustion from perpetual motion and rootlessness, admitting he's forgotten how to release people and may end up walking alone as the cost. It's about wanting rest and closure while fearing the loneliness that comes with actually letting go.","sonnet":"Someone who has never found a place that felt like home confronts the fact that they have lost the ability to let go of people and places that no longer serve them — the art of releasing, of moving on — and despite fear of ending up alone, understands it is finally time to try.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~trippin-on-a-hole-in-a-paper-heart":{"haiku":"The speaker refuses to be controlled or commodified despite heartbreak and self-destructive cycles; they claim autonomy against dying inside.","opus":"A jittery declaration of endurance, the speaker insisting he isn't himself but is neither deceased nor purchasable, refusing to be sliced open or bought while nooses, wedding scenes, and menthol-tinged breath flash past. Stone Temple Pilots give it that jumpy mid-nineties drive, Scott Weiland spitting his defiance as a man warding off both his own collapse and everyone trying to commodify him, gripping on because releasing is harder.","sonnet":"Defiant fragility — the speaker insists he isn't dead and isn't for sale even as everything accumulates toward an exit, the images lurching from the Eiffel Tower to a diamond noose, and the repeated plea to just be let go wrapped inside an equally strong refusal to be written off.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~unglued":{"haiku":"Watching your mind come undone when you can't stop ruminating on things that fell apart.","opus":"Scott Weiland riding a manic surge he can't govern, where craving pleasure and losing his grip are the same motion. The bravado is the sound of someone coming apart and half-enjoying the fall.","sonnet":"A restless, dissatisfied speaker caught in cycles of confusion and self-indulgence finds that everything — desires, sicknesses, thoughts — keeps coming undone, some overwhelming feeling washing over them repeatedly without resolution or clarity.","src":"haiku"},
"stone-temple-pilots~vasoline":{"haiku":"Reality becomes obscured and people remain trapped in murky mediocrity, unable to distinguish what is genuine from what is sold.","opus":"A hazy meditation on being trapped, likened to insects mired in something slick, groping after what can't be seen while losing sight and wondering what's genuine versus merchandised. The grease is the governing image, a slippery pit the speaker keeps sliding back into. Stone Temple Pilots ride a hypnotic nineties groove, Weiland's blurred words more mood of numb entrapment than statement.","sonnet":"Getting stuck in something that blurs the line between real and performance — the vasoline is the medium everything gets caught in, the search for authenticity keeps going blind, and the lies fed willingly suggest someone who knows the trap and enters it anyway, flies who can't stop landing.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title works as one governing image, a slick medium that promises easy glide but behaves like flypaper, so whatever it coats loses sight, loses grip, and stays pinned. Weiland numbers the verses to track an escalation, a first occasion giving way to a second impact that leaves him reeling and unbailable, addiction's arithmetic where every repeat costs more and solitude starts to look like the only safe address. The refrains dissolve agency by design, denying both self and other while their true objects stay invisible, so guilt and identity smear into one greasy fog. The mid-90s panic over what is authentic versus merchandised floats in that murk as another thing gummed past recognition. The pivot arrives when the smeared victim recognizes himself as the trapped insect, a single lucid instant of seeing the snare from within before the chorus hauls him back around. That circling refusal to resolve is the read, the track performing its own argument by looping the same viscous hook instead of breaking free.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"one lucid instant of seeing the snare from within before the loop hauls the speaker back","built":"the title's governing image — something that coats and pins rather than lubricates — runs the whole structure"},
"stone-temple-pilots~wicked-garden":{"haiku":"Someone seeks escape into a dangerous, transformative place to escape chains and awaken; the garden burns away the old self to bring life anew.","opus":"On their debut Core, Stone Temple Pilots frame the pursuit of a jaded, deadened lover as passage through a corrupt garden, Weiland vowing to scorch her numbness back into feeling. Riddling questions about perceiving and speaking honestly probe someone dulled by darkness, the seduction offered as a violent, redemptive reawakening.","sonnet":"A figure seeks entry into someone's hidden, corrupt inner world in order to burn it down and resurrect what lies beneath. Framed as urgent spiritual rescue, the narrator pleads for the other to perceive without defences, to feel again — willing to destroy the wicked garden to release whoever has gone numb inside it.","src":"haiku"},
"stonebank~breathe-me-back-to-life":{"haiku":"One touch revives the half-dead; another person becomes the breath that restores existence.","opus":"Being hauled up out of a suffocating despair by someone whose lone touch and concern revive a man who felt lifeless within. The whole thrust is thankfulness, crediting the other person with reviving him regardless of anything he'd previously said or done.","sonnet":"A person brought back from emotional death by another's touch and care — \"felt like I was dead inside\" — the single contact enough to make the heart whole again and restore a sense of return from wherever they had gone.","src":"haiku"},
"stonebank~diving-in":{"haiku":"Surrender to the unknown requires releasing grip; the speaker dives into transformation by accepting they cannot navigate the depths below.","opus":"The decision to stop clinging and let go, taking the plunge into the unknown when staying no longer works. It's a resolve to surrender control and dive in, trusting the leap over the exhausting effort to hold on.","sonnet":"A speaker who has been unable to break away finally accepts that letting go is the only path forward — walking slowly into the unknown rather than clinging to what no longer serves them.","src":"haiku"},
"stoned-jesus~bright-like-the-morning":{"haiku":"A wanderer seeks self through pilgrimage, youth, and love as redemptive force; thunder roars, rain cleanses, and a woman descends as morning light offering patience and second chances beyond hangover.","opus":"A pilgrim's spiritual quest: a man who looks radiant and young but feels hollow inside, searching in vain for himself while vices and cheap remedies fail him. Salvation eventually arrives as a feminine figure who embodies Love and comes from above, though she demands patience, the journey never truly ending but always offering a fresh start.","sonnet":"A worn-out wanderer who has been seeking answers in substances and empty shelves finds a redemptive feminine presence — luminous, patient, the embodiment of love — that offers a chance to begin again.","src":"haiku"},
"stoned-jesus~here-come-the-robots":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects a breeding game and prison of their own making, wanting escape from control and meaninglessness.","opus":"Refusing to keep playing a rigged game of reproduction and competition, recognizing the prison as self-built. The looming robots stand for a dehumanized, mechanized future the singer wants no part of scoring in.","sonnet":"A song about feeling trapped in social and biological expectations you want no part of — the narrator sees themselves as a machine built for a breeding game they've opted out of, imprisoned by routines and systems they didn't choose.","src":"haiku"},
"stoned-jesus~i-m-the-mountain":{"haiku":"A person struggles with unrequited love and self-harm; through metaphorical embodiment as landscape and stone, they reckon with youth's mistakes and claim their own identity.","opus":"A sprawling doom epic where a lonely, unloved narrator ascends into cosmic self-definition, declaring himself mountain, sea, valley, root and door, the whole of nature standing in for a self he can barely hold together. Stoned Jesus are a Ukrainian stoner-doom band, and this signature long-form track journeys from personal grief toward a mirror confrontation, ending with the young man of doubt and rage recognized as the singer himself.","sonnet":"Grandiose self-mythologizing that collapses into honest self-reckoning — the speaker cycles through elemental identities, mountain, sea, road, mirror, before the road leading to Headley Grange brings him face to face with a young man torn by selfishness and doubt who turns out to be himself all along.","src":"haiku"},
"stoned-jesus~rituals-of-the-sun":{"haiku":"Ancient wisdom and rituals to the sun are buried in grief. The song calls for uprising and fighting for a kingdom through mystical worship.","opus":"The Ukrainian doom-stoner trio channels inherited, half-forgotten knowledge into a slow-building summons, treating solar devotion and reverence for the cosmos as a source of resolve. The riff-heavy hymn gathers toward a martial cry to take up arms for one's homeland and ruler, turning mysticism into a call to defend what's yours.","sonnet":"Ancient wisdom buried beneath grief and time waits for a signal to reemerge. Encoded in solar ritual and starward devotion, the call is to rise and reclaim a forgotten kingdom — collective purpose reasserted against the long silence of a lost civilization.","src":"haiku"},
"stoned-jesus~wound":{"haiku":"Unspoken words create wounds—the speaker battles internal conflict, phantom limbs, and silent abuse that harms everyone.","opus":"How quiet itself can injure, the things left unsaid between two people rotting into fresh damage. Under the onstage fury lies a failure to voice what was discovered, a truth kept back precisely because speaking it would wound everyone involved.","sonnet":"About the way things left unsaid accumulate into emotional damage — the narrator wants to speak but the words don't come, and the silence itself becomes a kind of violence, leaving wounds that fester between two people who can't communicate.","src":"haiku"},
"stoned-jesus~yfs":{"haiku":"Youth sold out by corrupt systems that manufacture consent through hatred, media control, and greed; the speaker refuses to buy the lie.","opus":"A furious sermon against a generation auctioned off, queuing for crumbs while leaders fire weapons and traffic in hatred, the refrain branding it all as young lives for sale. It builds to a rejection of the whole corrupt deal of greed and control. Stoned Jesus put their Ukrainian stoner-doom weight behind political revulsion, warning that vengeance blinds and urging self-reliance over being sold.","sonnet":"Youth sold to systems of control before it ever had a chance to think — the speaker watches a generation manipulated by nationalism, media, and manufactured hatred, and the fury underneath is not despair but the conviction that the fire inside people could still let them stand on their own if they stopped buying what's being sold.","src":"haiku"},
"stoned-statues~the-end":{"haiku":"Humanity deserves extinction for environmental destruction; collapse is inevitable and merciful to those drowning in flaming ruin.","opus":"An apocalyptic reckoning with climate collapse, raising eyes to a flaming earth of wildfires and floods and concluding that humanity, having fueled the fire, deserves the end. Death becomes a welcomed friend as the weight of a self-destroyed world settles on our shoulders.","sonnet":"Humanity has earned its destruction through ecological violence — forest fires, floods, machine guns on nature — and the speaker watching the flames acknowledges 'we deserve the end,' the death that comes framed as justice rather than tragedy.","src":"haiku"},
"stop-drop-rewind~hold-it-over-me":{"haiku":"Heavy love becomes a burden held over the speaker's head; dependence and unequal power dynamics prevent genuine growth or escape.","opus":"A meditation on love as burden, where certainty about the future weighs heavy and a partner holding it over him might paradoxically ease an anxious mind. The narrator confesses he cannot stop telling everything even while wishing to say nothing, and turns over whether such heavy love was ever meant to consume a life. The push-pull of intimacy and control drives the whole reflection.","sonnet":"A love so heavy it functions as leverage, the relationship tilts between compulsive disclosure and the fear of pity. One person cannot stop telling the other everything despite wanting to; the attachment itself becomes something held over them, trailing just behind rather than beside.","src":"haiku"},
"storm-orchestra~bright-soul":{"haiku":"Soul perpetually absent or unreachable, struggling against doubt while something vital always eludes.","opus":"Urban anxiety and a vanished sense of self, the missing soul leaving a hole the narrator keeps chasing. The refrain to smash the limit is the desperate push for a way out of the inner war.","sonnet":"About chronic anxiety and the feeling that your true self or inner peace is perpetually out of reach — the \"bright soul\" is what the narrator wants to be but can't access, leaving only a terrifying hole; the song is a struggle to break free of internal doubt.","src":"haiku"},
"story-of-hope~alethea":{"haiku":"Someone longs for an unreachable figure ahead, struggling to bridge a distance that prevents connection; they ask the other to forget them instead, choosing separation over false hope.","opus":"A Japanese-English plea about a love too distant to hold, the narrator worn down fighting to reach someone always ahead, unable to forget them and so begging instead to be forgotten as futures branch apart.","sonnet":"In Japanese and English, this is about two people whose futures branch apart despite once being deeply aligned — the narrator accepts they can never truly forget the other, so instead asks to be forgotten, releasing the bond so the other can move on.","src":"haiku"},
"story-of-hope~foliage-remastered":{"haiku":"Lost in cycles of pain and rejection, where the narrator remains trapped in rain and darkness despite reaching out, watching connections crumble.","opus":"Story of Hope chase an unreachable ideal that recedes with every step, drowning in memories and a pain that refuses to fade. Bilingual Japanese and English lines wish for everything to vanish while only the ache remains, an exitless path walked alone. The band bind melodic-metalcore catharsis to a lyric of exhaustion, reaching toward distant light through a city of unrelenting rain.","sonnet":"Grief cycles without resolution across two languages. In Japanese, a speaker chases an impossibly distant ideal while memories blur behind tears, trapped in a town of endless rain; in English, the same exhaustion surfaces — a vicious loop of fading closeness and loneliness, with every outstretched hand met by departure and another long night.","src":"haiku"},
"story-of-hope~forever":{"haiku":"A person ends an unfulfilling relationship in which emotional distance has grown despite promises of closeness. They come to terms with the fact that the connection was not real and choose to move on.","opus":"Melodic post-hardcore in which a vow of permanence becomes the very sore the song keeps pressing. The narrator watches a partner drift, no longer meaning the world to them, and resolves to rouse himself only once it's finished. Held in the present, he's caught mid-collapse, calling out a name to no effect, at last walking away from a bond that never became genuine and concluding that permanence is a lie.","sonnet":"A person watches a promised bond dissolve in real time — the future they imagined with someone has gone blank, the other party already gone in spirit. Rather than holding on, they finally release the wish, choosing wakefulness over the illusion of permanence.","src":"haiku"},
"story-of-hope~from-despair-to-hope-remastered":{"haiku":"Overcoming internal doubts and external criticism to maintain faith and determination through hardship, refusing to surrender to despair.","opus":"A relentlessly motivational anthem urging the listener to look toward the light, wipe their tears and believe a rainbow follows every hurricane, with a taunting inner voice of weakness shouted down. The English is second-language and earnest, the message pure perseverance. It reads as melodic hardcore built on uplift rather than despair, control your destiny repeated until doubt is beaten back and the future is claimed.","sonnet":"Straight-ahead perseverance anthem — someone who refuses to look back, shrugs off doubt and opposition with competitive contempt, and keeps pushing toward a future framed as self-belief in motion, the emotional arc from hurricane to rainbow kept simple and direct.","src":"haiku"},
"story-of-hope~historia-acoustic-version":{"haiku":"Singing echoes across time and distance as vengeance and connection, offering voice to the unseen in desperate hope of reaching someone.","opus":"A vow to go on singing toward someone unreachable, screaming till his chest gives out because these fragile, fleeting words are all that's left. Suspended between vengeance and goodbye, the singer keeps up this one act, asking whether the distant other still perceives them at all.","sonnet":"A singer addresses an absent or imagined listener for whom they have been performing all along, confessing they stole something from them and have been sustaining themselves on the hope of meeting them someday; the final act is a departure — kicking the door open with vengeance and saying goodbye.","src":"haiku"},
"story-of-hope~historia-remastered":{"haiku":"The speaker relentlessly pursues a distant figure through song and memory, refusing to stop crying or give up despite knowing they may never meet.","opus":"A pledge to go on singing to someone beyond reach, straining till his chest caves in, addressed to a shadowy figure the speaker isn't certain still senses him, and cast at the end as a strange kind of revenge and a goodbye. The English is second-language and impressionistic, yet the yearning for lost connection registers clearly, a performer's vow held out to a listener who may already be gone.","sonnet":"A song sung persistently for someone never met and maybe never to be met — the speaker keeps screaming until their heart gives out, carrying grief over something stolen and time lost, but the vengeance is in the act of continuing rather than in any confrontation, the door kicked open as a goodbye that also says I'm still going.","src":"haiku"},
"story-of-hope~life-is-a-journey-to-find-you":{"haiku":"The narrator struggles with emotional instability and fear while pursuing an uncertain, undefined journey to find a meaningful person or purpose beyond faded, painful memories.","opus":"A bilingual song about a wavering, anxious relationship where both hearts sway between instability and a real path forward; the narrator refuses to become a burden or lose himself, wanting instead to give the other hope, and though he can't quite say 'if it's with you,' he vows to run through this foolish world together.","sonnet":"A song about two people who are each scared and uncertain, trying to be what the other needs while privately crumbling — the Japanese-English mix captures both the hope of reaching someone and the fear of running out of time to do it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is built on a single unstable verb — the recurring word means to sway or waver — and it attaches first to the other person's uncertain feelings, then in the outro to two hearts swaying together, so the arc is a movement from private wavering toward a shared instability that is nonetheless described as a road continuing surely ahead. That paradox is the thesis, and the lyric earns it structurally: the singer's plea to break me down, come closer, because I want to see more of you asks to be undone as the price of intimacy, and the second refrain sharpens it into a plea to be broken before I stop being myself. Against that fear the bridge answers that no wall is frightening if the unknown holds possibility, yet it immediately withdraws — I can never say with you I could — leaving the confidence deliberately unspoken. The bilingual seam matters too: the English verse names a hollow existence and forgotten bliss in the flatter, more clinical register the Japanese resists, so the two languages divide the labor between despair stated plainly and hope that can only stammer. What the song finally trusts is not arrival but the swaying itself, the shared unsteadiness reframed as the shape a genuine path actually takes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["love & desire","identity & becoming"],"means":"shared unsteadiness reframed as the shape a genuine path actually takes","built":"the two languages divide the labor: English states the despair plainly, Japanese can only stammer the hope"},
"story-of-hope~lost":{"haiku":"Struggles with failing capability and disconnection, unable to reclaim what time and loss have stolen. Requests presence and permission to forget pain.","opus":"A quivering appeal for closeness amid failure and a self coming apart, the narrator unable to summon his own voice, afraid the wind will scatter him, begging someone to stay near so he can escape the hurt. A recurring truth surfaces: what's gone stays gone until death. Translated from Japanese, it carries the raw, aching intimacy of that scene's post-hardcore and screamo.","sonnet":"A plea not to be left alone while falling apart — voice scattered, breath faltering, the consolation that lost things stay lost until death offered with quiet brutality. What is asked for is simply nearness and the permission to forget the pain, even briefly, without being made to think.","src":"haiku"},
"story-of-hope~my-sleeping-heart":{"opus":"A raw explosion of rage at a toxic person the singer wants gone from his life, cursing and threatening them while noting that once they're finally out, life goes well and the old misery feels like it never existed.","sonnet":"An outburst of pure rage at a specific person — the lyrics are almost entirely profane violent venting — punctuated by a single line acknowledging life has improved and the person is out of the narrator's life, but the dominant register is furious contempt.","src":"sonnet"},
"story-of-hope~take-my-hand-remastered":{"haiku":"A desperate plea to break through emotional barriers and shatter the stopping point between two people, seeking destructive collision as union.","opus":"A bilingual cry of thwarted intimacy, mixing Japanese and English, where the singer begs to be shattered by someone kept permanently just out of reach. An unbreachable boundary keeps two people apart no matter how they strain toward each other, so yearning curdles into paralysis while the plea to be reached never lands.","sonnet":"Two people press against a barrier that keeps them apart, language fracturing under the strain. In broken English and Japanese, a narrator begs to be broken open — consumed by an intoxicating impulse they keep biting back — while the distance refuses to close and the stop line remains permanent, unpassable, forever.","src":"haiku"},
"story-of-hope~your-colors-your-feelings-remastered":{"haiku":"A person breaking habits searches for connection and meaning while navigating despair and fading into the sky, ending through transformation.","opus":"A bittersweet clinging to a lost love, the singer still seeking someone who was everything even as the ending begins; the Japanese passages deepen it, pouring every feeling into words meant to fade into the blue sky, refusing to let this be how the story ends while accepting the fleeting memory dissolving away.","sonnet":"A bilingual (English and Japanese) song about someone who meant everything to the narrator — now that things are ending, they pour their feelings into words before disappearing, the Japanese verses expressing a fragile hope of dissolving gently into the blue sky.","src":"haiku"},
"story-of-the-year~war":{"haiku":"Weaponized cruelty masquerades as love; the speaker cuts open the beloved, watches them suffer, yet their pain transmutes nothing—the game devours all participants equally.","opus":"Confronting a partner who cuts with vicious words and an icy guard, demanding to know whether they came looking for a fight that will only wreck themselves. The taller the barrier they raise, the steeper their tumble, and their bid to cause pain won't ease their own.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses someone who inflicts wounds and calls it care — the knife between their teeth while wishing you well — arguing that the higher the wall built around such cruelty, the harder the inevitable fall when the game kills its player in their sleep.","src":"haiku"},
"straightener~a-14ai2um":{"haiku":"Sea-weathered glass fragments become light sources; impermanent moments and farewells fundamentally shape identity.","opus":"Straightener's melodic post-punk has a recurring soft spot for endings and seasons, and this closes out a year at the shore. Two people spend the last beach day collecting sea glass and torn notebook pages, lighting candles and scattering scraps skyward. The narrator refuses to cry until the other does, hunting for the next word as summer rushes to its end.","sonnet":"Two people spend the last beach day of the year collecting sea glass and torn notebook pages, lighting candles and scattering scraps into the sky — the narrator won't cry until the other does, searching for the next word as summer hurries to its end.","src":"haiku"},
"straightener~a-178rqsf":{"haiku":"Cyclical time loop of lost memory and relationship breakdown; no matter how many times the speaker lives through existence, they fail to prevent the loss of the person they love most.","opus":"Atsushi Horie writes lost love with a hard clarity, and here Straightener trap it in a time loop. Unable to let go of an ex who has moved to a new city and moved on, the narrator keeps replaying the end, finally understanding that the relationship's doom was something he could already sense back then and only consciously grasps far too late.","sonnet":"A person who cannot let go of an ex realizes he is stuck in an emotional time loop — she has moved to a new city and moved on, while he keeps replaying the end, now understanding that the relationship's doom was something he could sense even then but only consciously recognizes too late.","src":"haiku"},
"straightener~a-x9eh0h":{"haiku":"An intense demand for reciprocal sacrifice in obsessive love—bone-deep consumption and erasure as the price of devotion.","opus":"Straightener rarely play it this feral, and the band's tautness makes the abandon land harder. The song demands love until the bones are stripped clean, destruction and scattering into space, its refrain about paying back double framing the encounter as a final gamble with everything at stake. It is reckless surrender to a single night as if it were the only one that will ever exist.","sonnet":"A song about the violent totality of one-night desire — demanding to be loved until bones are stripped clean, to be destroyed and scattered into space, with the refrain 'pay it back double' framing the encounter as a last gamble where everything is on the line. The emotional core is the reckless surrender to a single night as if it were the only one that will ever exist.","src":"haiku"},
"straightener~a-x9fl35":{"haiku":"An intense demand for reciprocal sacrifice in obsessive love—bone-deep consumption and erasure as the price of devotion.","opus":"Straightener came up through Japan's early-2000s guitar-rock underground, where emotional maximalism was the currency, and this track pushes that ethos to its edge. A one-night ultimatum demanding total, bone-stripping love, it frames devotion as a single high-stakes wager priced at double repayment, the band's melodic urgency turning obsession into something closer to a dare.","sonnet":"An identical or near-identical version of the same song as key straightener~a-x9eh0h — the text matches exactly. A one-night-only demand for total, bone-stripping love, with the refrain 'pay it back double' as the price for a first-and-last gamble.","src":"haiku"},
"straightener~vandalism-prototype":{"opus":"A blank statement of complete withdrawal in the face of anonymous, faceless assailants and slanderers, the narrator declining any weapon or drug, meeting it all with flat contempt and swearing he will never register a single feeling.","sonnet":"A declaration of invulnerability through emotional detachment — someone is attacking and cursing the narrator, but they cannot remember who and do not care, needing no weapons or drugs to cope, simply refusing to feel anything at all.","src":"sonnet"},
"strange-bones~nine-lives":{"haiku":"Recklessness and resilience intertwined: restless youth crashing through limits and consequences, shrugging off every fall because survival feels infinite, though cracks show beneath the bravado.","opus":"Bilingual punk swagger daring the world to take its best shot, treating recklessness and survival as one adrenaline dare after another.","sonnet":"A song of defiant indestructibility — the speaker dares enemies to take their best shot, insisting they can absorb any hit and keep going because they have seemingly infinite lives to burn through.","src":"haiku"},
"stranger-dimensions~dying-light":{"haiku":"Spiritual decay and loss of purpose as the speaker wastes life away, with all beacons of guidance extinguished and no path back.","opus":"A metaphor for depression as the slow guttering of one's inner lights, a blaze that once burned strong now shrunk to faint, far-off sparks. Lifted up only to sink again, the narrator colors his world in relentless gloom, wasting away by day and tending his failing spark by night, so worn he's lost count of how many times he's come undone.","sonnet":"A self-portrait of slow inner extinction — a person who once burned brightly now watches their vitality drain day by day into grey routine. The beacons of a former self grow distant and cold, leaving only the imperative to escape before the last light goes out entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~aaa": {"haiku":"A nameless, violent outsider rejects monogamy, conformity, and connection; no one will challenge him, and he wants only primal chaos.","opus":"Devin Townsend's self-lacerating portrait of his own alienated childhood and the private machinery of rage no one is allowed to see. The chest-beating refrain of untouchability curdles into a bleak admission of how little is really underneath.","sonnet":"A semi-autobiographical portrait of Devin Townsend's own damaged adolescence — a misfit outsider shaped by abuse and isolation, accumulating rage and shame until it explodes in a declaration that no one controls him, ending in raw sexual aggression as the final expression of that bottled energy.","src":"haiku","fable":"Townsend turns the camera on himself, narrating the boy Devy in the third person as a bullied outcast who never cleared the toilet-training milestone and learned to run — a self-mockery so pointed it doubles as autopsy. What curdles here is repression: the two-beat thud nobody registers becomes the engine of a private machine kept sealed from every witness, and across the verses the misfit victim swells into a predator hardening around his own damage. The pronoun shift is the whole song's meaning — Devy is spoken about until the shame amalgamates and the mask drops into first person, the childhood keep-away-from-me self-defense flipping into a threat. Against City's mechanized wall of sound the refusal repeats past sense, the secrecy of that guarded interior eroding until nothing is left underneath but appetite. The closing collapse into a single flattened drive isn't a punchline but the reveal: strip the bullying, the shame, and the self-loathing away and the machine wants only one blunt thing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","identity & becoming","violence & murder"],"means":"bullied victim hardens around his damage into a threat","built":"third-person narration collapses into first person as shame becomes predation"},
"strapping-young-lad~aftermath":{"haiku":"War and religious hypocrisy, depicting violence and violation justified by competing faiths and powers fighting over resources.","opus":"An indictment of war waged in the name of religion and oil, portraying soldiers as demons who burn, beat, and rape while claiming to fight for God. It condemns all faiths alike as the pretext for endless riding into war.","sonnet":"A stark condemnation of religiously and economically motivated warfare, depicting violence committed in the name of competing faiths as demonic and without moral justification.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~almost-again":{"haiku":"A person cycles through abandonment and near-reconciliation repeatedly, moving at impossible speeds between being found and being alone.","opus":"Devin Townsend on a partner leaving, circling the ache of nearly reaching something, nearly whole, forever falling short. The repeated near-miss turns loneliness into a loop of almost-arrivals that never resolve.","sonnet":"A post-breakup song about being on the verge of solitude again — the narrator is almost alone, almost back to where he started, moving at the speed of sound to escape the end of something.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~antiproduct":{"haiku":"A defiant, corrupted entity rejects commodification and labels itself as the anti-commercial force. Identity becomes pure opposition to being sold or standardized.","opus":"Devin Townsend's savage joke on commodification, the narrator branding himself the anti-product while begging to be sold, the paradox exposing how rebellion itself gets packaged, rated, and shelved for sale.","sonnet":"A self-contradictory anthem: the narrator declares himself the anti-product — rotten, quantum-pure, the chosen one — while simultaneously being sold and marketed, turning the critique of consumerism into its own product.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~critic":{"fable":"Fragments from the sleepless hours: a finished fight, a needed someone named Mora, a confession that argues with itself — I know it's cheating, baby, but I know I have a right — all of it presided over by the accusing figure of the title: the critic.","fableDeep":"The critic never speaks — it's the title, the address, the presence in the dark — an internalized judge for a lyric that is itself one long self-argument: 'a bleeding wrong forcing right' is the whole moral knot in five words, guilt insisting on its own justification and wounding itself in the process. 'Help me get this water clear' begs for the one thing the fragmented form refuses to provide — clarity — and the song names its own genre in the last line: 'I'm calling it a soul strike,' the 3 a.m. spiral given a name like a weather event. Who Mora is stays private, and the read should respect that: this is a lyric about being unreadable to yourself, and it performs its meaning by half-withholding it.","haiku":"An obscure and fragmentary song touching on internal conflict, cheating, and ambivalent self-awareness. Meaning is difficult to extract from the lyrical abstraction.","opus":"Devin Townsend turns his self-lacerating inner voice into a character, the critic who keeps him awake picking at every flaw. Beneath the crushing wall of sound it is really about exhaustion, guilt, and begging for release from his own mind.","sonnet":"A late-night reckoning with infidelity and guilt, the speaker calling out to someone named Mora for help while knowing what he has done is wrong, suspended between confession and self-justification.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~decimator":{"haiku":"Human nature tends toward fear and destruction; faith is questioned as the speaker seeks strength to transcend natural instincts toward violence and collapse.","opus":"An anguished, blasphemous plea to God and Hell for the strength to understand and to decide, the narrator feeling violated and defiled while straining to prove some higher purpose. The refrain that dread of the abyss is simply how humans are made frames terror of the void as the central horror, with God by turns attentive and absent.","sonnet":"A confrontation with spiritual and creative doubt — the speaker tears between a god that may not be listening and a hell that has its own eloquence, asking for the strength to either know or choose, framing their artistic and existential struggle as something that shakes the earth.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~detox": {"haiku":"A claustrophobic account of social isolation and emotional numbness in a high-school setting. The speaker feels watched, disconnected, and lost, unable to escape their sense of inadequacy.","opus":"A raw account of alienation and breakdown, where the speaker feels numb, watched, and lost among people, drowning in high-school loneliness and despair, with clinical narration about the brain amplifying the sense of dread.","sonnet":"A wretched social alienation — the speaker finds himself somewhere he doesn't belong, surrounded by people who all seem to be watching, numbed and unable to function, wanting only to feel something.","src":"haiku","fable":"A panic attack scaled up to a scream: the speaker is numb, watched, sure everyone sees him, and the shame he keeps naming is only the scale of a locker-lined hallway — which is the cruelty of it, that a mind can turn something so ordinary into a reason not to survive the night. The interrogation of how he got here, how he lost his sight, isn't rhetorical; it's the sound of dissociation trying to locate itself. Then Townsend drops the mask entirely with a clinical sample about the brain as a bagged clot of pumped tissue that keeps firing after the head has been cut loose from the neck — a detached, coroner-voiced fantasy of decapitation planted where the pleading is loudest. Against City's mechanized wall of noise, the private smallness of the confession — the bare wish to be able to sense anything at all — is the real horror: not the industrial scale, but how quietly a person can hollow out inside it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","madness & the mind","shame & repression","suicide"],"means":"private smallness of the breakdown dwarfed by industrial scale","built":"clinical decapitation sample planted at the peak of the plea"},
"strapping-young-lad~devour":{"haiku":"Millions of souls are born only to perish in cycles of death and destruction. The song contemplates mass suffering and the burning away of human life.","opus":"Devin Townsend's cosmic nihilism at full roar, life and death collapsing into one devouring cycle where a hundred million souls wash away. The extremity gropes toward the idea that we only understand living through dying.","sonnet":"A brutal, minimalist meditation on cellular division and mass death — the same biological process that creates life divides endlessly until it consumes, and the speaker sees visions of hundreds of millions of souls washed away in the cycle.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~dirt-pride":{"haiku":"Primal bodily nature and self-loathing expressed through abusive language. The song rejects transformation and embraces internal war, indifferent to helping or pleasing others.","opus":"A caustic, near-abstract assault on self-righteousness, mocking someone who praises himself while the speaker declares his own bleeding is for himself alone, unwilling to help the hopeless. Strapping Young Lad, Devin Townsend's extreme project, drown the message in industrial-metal chaos and deliberately grotesque nonsense words, so the read lives in the contempt and the internal war at itself, coherence sacrificed to sheer misanthropic overload.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~far-beyond-metal":{"haiku":"Extreme intensity transcends genre limitations; the speaker inhabits sonic realms beyond conventional musical boundaries.","opus":"Devin Townsend's frenzied contempt for a diseased, bandwagon-chasing record business, casting the band as its deranged cure. Ludicrous barbarian bragging and self-mocking swagger crash against a sincere ache underneath, a wish to feel the way he once did in childhood. It's genre devotion as cathartic, absurd, and heartfelt aggression all at once.","sonnet":"A theatrical assault on the music industry and its ironic pop-rock inhabitants — \"you ironic pop-rock fuck, don't you fuck with metal\" — punctuated by a medieval power fantasy and a bridge that quietly confesses something older underneath the aggression: a need to feel something, to recover a version of the self that existed before the noise.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~force-fed":{"haiku":"Society beats conformity into people, preventing them from bending or winning; youth must find ways to love again and reach a better world.","opus":"Devin Townsend's Strapping Young Lad unleash a torrent of beastly self-hatred, tagging the self a savage creature that must confront its dread and endure its urges. Amid the onslaught a spark of optimism survives, the young pledging to rediscover love and the world. The extreme-metal wall mirrors a mind crammed with its own instincts, groping toward wholeness through the din.","sonnet":"Rage and tenderness collide in a portrait of being thrust raw into the world. The narrator is called an animal and accepts it; only by facing fears and riding out brutality does anything human emerge — and beneath the fury is a fragile insistence that the young can still find a way back to love.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~fucker":{"fable":"The metal show as mutual insult ritual: everyone's a fucker, the singer doesn't fucking care, and that shared not-caring — screamed together over party riffs, 'we're doing it for you, baby' — is both the whole point and the whole joke.","fableDeep":"The aggression comes in air quotes: 'dance, you fuckers' is an invitation, and in this genre's grammar the contempt *is* the hospitality — the crowd being insulted is the crowd being loved. One autobiographical flash grounds it: 'it was all too much for the Alien' nods at the annihilating record before this one, framing the dumb fun as deliberate decompression — 'so heavy metal, fuck it, rock' is a man choosing shallow water on purpose after nearly drowning in the deep. And 'watch behind my eyes… see the little fucker' briefly turns the insult inward: the misanthropy includes its author, which is exactly what keeps this a party and not a threat.","haiku":"Defiant metal attitude and rejection of judgment. Heavy and blunt, the song celebrates reckless energy and dismisses concern with repeated disregard for others' opinions.","opus":"Devin Townsend's manic middle finger to the metal-scene hangers-on and industry gatekeepers, a cathartic tantrum that mocks the demand for radio-friendly product while reveling in its own refusal to care.","sonnet":"The song is an unfiltered expression of aggression and indifference directed outward at everyone—audience, critics, and the world—framed as the authentic spirit of a metal show where nothing is meant to be taken personally because nothing is cared about. It is as much self-declaration as it is dismissal.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~home-nucleonics": {"haiku":"Technology will replace and transcend human agency, with humanity failing and consuming itself as machines inherit creation.","opus":"Devin Townsend channels raw contempt for a debased humanity, imagining technology as a false messiah that arrives while we look the wrong way. It is bile as catharsis, misanthropy pushed to an almost gleeful extreme.","sonnet":"A furious rejection of humanity and America — technology is coming as a second god, humans are cancer, and the narrator's warning has been ignored by every stupid, feasting fool who got in their way.","src":"haiku","fable":"A screamed jeremiad from the deep-burnout core of City, where Townsend's misanthropy sharpens into outright prophecy. The speaker takes the pose of a warner nobody heeded — the I-warned-you refrain recurs like a curse that has already come true — indicting humanity as gluttonous animals feasting while they wait for a savior who is a man. The song's actual thesis is the swerve at its center: the machine, not any messiah, is the god that returns to earth, and it will land while everyone is still scanning the horizon for a human deliverer. What reads as pure profane rage is really apocalyptic disappointment, the venom of a prophet whose contempt (culminating in a blanket curse flung at a whole rotten, flag-waving nation) is inseparable from the fact that he was right and it changed nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"prophet's contempt inseparable from having been proven right","built":"i-warned-you refrain recurs as a curse already fulfilled"},
"strapping-young-lad~hope":{"haiku":"The singer rejects hope and faith as weakness, hardening themselves into steel through relentless motion. They've become what they are precisely because they have no hope—learning through this rejection.","opus":"A sour reversal in which having nothing to hope for becomes a wellspring of defiant power, the narrator asserting that he is precisely what he is because he holds no hope and no trust in a jeering other. Staying authentic and going up in flames turn into acts of stark self-definition. Strapping Young Lad, Devin Townsend's savage extreme-metal project, forge despair into a cathartic wall of sound, the title reading as sarcasm rather than solace.","sonnet":"A rejection of hope as a concept rather than a state of mind — performing whatever is asked, moving through infinite iterations of every finite moment, and arriving at the conclusion that what was built was built without faith in anything external. Respect is what remains when hope has been discarded entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~imperial":{"haiku":"Two worlds collide and merge into one; the prophecy of the weak is about to be fulfilled through an imminent, powerful delivery.","opus":"Devin Townsend's Strapping Young Lad open their extreme-metal assault with apocalyptic grandiosity, two worlds colliding as forever's invention returns to deliver. The lyric is less narrative than incantation, a summons to be heard by the prophecy of the weak who wept. Brief and battering, it functions as a declaration of arrival, the band announcing collision and delivery through sheer overwhelming volume of intent rather than any unfolding story.","sonnet":"A declaration of collective force returning — two unified entities collide and demand to be heard, invoking prophecy against weakness. It is a short, volcanic proclamation of arrival and dominance, all momentum and no restraint.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~in-the-rainy-season":{"haiku":"In summer heat and temptation, the narrator fights illusions and dark voices promising escape; he resists a seductive call to surrender.","opus":"A white-knuckle drive through a manic-depressive spiral, begging the July heat to break into rain while intrusive, violent thoughts crowd in. Devin Townsend turns his own mental illness into a plea to keep his eyes on the road and not succumb.","sonnet":"A fragmented, dissociative internal monologue while driving in July heat — the narrator battles violent intrusive thoughts and what sounds like an external voice or presence trying to take control, clinging to the road and to sanity, begging for rain to break the heat and the madness.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~last-minute":{"haiku":"An invocation of death and violent desire with chaotic energy, the speaker demands both the listener's head and their hatred in an aggressive incantation.","opus":"Townsend channeling raw, self-lacerating extremity, love and hate collapsing into the same demand, craving destruction as a twisted form of contact. It's the sound of a mind at its breaking point begging to be hated because affection isn't enough.","sonnet":"A short, explosive burst of religious and emotional extremity — born into righteousness, holding back war, then a screaming breakdown of wanting both hate and love in equal, irreconcilable measure.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~love":{"haiku":"Love as control and breeding instinct; the singer critiques love as paradoxical need masking biological drive, finding it more about dominance than genuine connection.","opus":"Devin Townsend weaponizes the word love, exposing it as a biological drive toward control and possession dressed up as tenderness. Beneath the extreme-metal assault is a genuinely bleak thesis: intimacy as domination, need as a trap.","sonnet":"A cynical interrogation of love as control — the narrator strips the concept down to breeding, possession, and suicide, insisting love is ultimately about domination and that he stands only for himself.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~monument":{"haiku":"A collective voice returns with hatred and demands, framing workers' toil and subordination as worship, suggesting control masquerades as love in an oppressive system.","opus":"A sardonic, industrial-metal sermon on control dressed as devotion, the crowd told they're loved even as they're consumed and commanded. Townsend skewers how domination and adoration blur, the worker ground down while being promised he's wanted.","sonnet":"A sardonic address from a band to its audience — framed as workers controlled by a monument of rock music, the song oscillates between love and hate, control and release, mocking the relationship between performer and crowd.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~plyophony":{"haiku":"A speaker warns of their stalking presence, sees through a lie in the listener's eyes, and refuses to leave despite knowing their attention is exhausting.","opus":"A brief, fractured piece from Devin Townsend's Strapping Young Lad, this circles obsessive attachment, the speaker knowing why he keeps falling yet reading a lover's eyes as a lie he falls for anyway. Waiting for someone tired and trying to leave, the lyric strips the band's usual wall of noise down to a bare, aching admission of dependence and inevitable collapse.","sonnet":"A quiet admission of falling despite all warning signs. The narrator sees through someone's eyes that the attraction is a lie, yet keeps tumbling toward it anyway — having told the other person he would wait, recognising their exhaustion, unable to stop the pull even while watching them try to leave.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~possessions":{"haiku":"The complications of human relationships and existence—commitments, procreation, money, family, death—suffocate the speaker who desires escape from these entanglements.","opus":"Devin Townsend aims Strapping Young Lad's aggression at the panic of domestic expectation, recoiling from weddings, kids, mortgages and tidy suburbia crowding a mind that just wants to be left in peace. Demands for his heart and body dissolve into a frantic urge to hand it all over and flee. Under the sarcasm about theme parks and taxes lies real dread of being drafted into a conventional life.","sonnet":"Desire and existential dread collide in a relationship teetering on the decision to procreate. One partner craves total possession while the other recoils at the weight of human entanglement — family, faith, mortality — and the whole structure nearly shakes itself apart before fleeing.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~rape-song":{"haiku":"Sexual violence is condemned as causing strength through weakness; vengeance turns predator into corpse.","opus":"A furious cry that frames rape as grounds for lethal retribution, spitting hatred at a violator and wishing him dead. The Devin Townsend project Strapping Young Lad channels its extremity into a brief, scalding assault, the repeated bodily fixation turned back as accusation. The vehemence sits squarely within a cathartic, rage-purging aesthetic rather than any endorsement.","sonnet":"A furious condemnation of sexual violence spoken from the perspective of outrage and retribution. The lyric refuses passivity, turning the violator's words back as evidence and pronouncing death as the only adequate answer, with no ambiguity about whose side the speaker takes.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~relentless":{"haiku":"A declaration of unstoppable, relentless action and honesty, with everything being destroyed in its wake and no hesitation in laying waste to what exists.","opus":"A defiant, destructive declaration of laying everything to waste and walking away without hesitation, prizing honesty above all and calling out an adversary with a relentless vow to reign.","sonnet":"A short, confrontational declaration of total commitment — laying waste to what came before, calling out dishonesty, and claiming a relentless, unyielding dominance over the world as a matter of principle.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~room-429": {"haiku":"Two isolated people retreat into an empty hotel room as refuge from a cruel world, choosing to abandon society and stay hidden together until the night ends.","opus":"Devin Townsend's murder-suicide vignette staged as tenderness, two people barricading themselves in a hotel room against an unkind world, choosing oblivion together. Love and death collapse into one final retreat, chilling in its calm.","sonnet":"The narrator, knife in hand outside someone's door at night, contemplates retreating to a private room with the person he loves — someone he would rather disappear with than continue existing in the world; the second half softens into a philosophical acceptance that dying is forgetting and forgetting lets us go on loving.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song wearing the face of a barricade. The surface invites you upstairs into a lodging where the world can't reach — a sanctuary against everything unkind — but the tender promises are laced with the wrong details: a hand resting on a knife, a figure waiting in the downpour beyond the threshold, the flat admission that if he had to lose he'd take them all. What sounds like devotion is a murder-suicide dressed as escape, the vacant fourth-floor cell a place to snuff out the last light for good. The final movement shifts from that confessional threat into a hushed, almost cosmic calm — watching the sleeping city, insisting we enter and leave this life unaccompanied, folding forgetting into love — which is the most chilling turn of all, the killer's mind reaching serenity by mistaking annihilation for intimacy. Against City's overload of industrial fury, this is a cover — Cop Shoot Cop's noir murder ballad pulled into the album's machine-world — and the selection is the point: Townsend lets the engines go quiet so a borrowed sickness can speak in a lullaby's register.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","love & desire","suicide","freedom & escape"],"means":"murder-suicide dressed in the language of sanctuary and escape","built":"borrowed noir ballad goes quiet inside the album's industrial fury"},
"strapping-young-lad~s-y-l":{"haiku":"A worker rejects corporate systems and institutional lies he encountered in low-wage labor, refusing to comply and reclaiming his defiance.","opus":"Devin Townsend's cathartic blast at the soul-crushing tedium of corporate drudgery, using a mock-childlike frame to spit contempt at every boss and lie he endured. Under the chaos is a manifesto of refusal, a broken man declaring he'll never comply again.","sonnet":"A first-person eruption of anger at a soul-crushing corporate existence — the speaker (referred to as Devy) describes being exploited, lied to, and diminished by a workplace system, before pivoting to a declaration of defiant self-determination and refusal to be controlled.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is Devin Townsend's eponymous mission statement weaponized as a working-stiff scream, and its structure is the argument: it opens with his own toddler voice narrating a bear that eats the kids and closes with him taking a fast-food order, framing the whole cathartic tirade between two images of a self swallowed by drudgery. The verses are a rage against wage-slavery and the salaried herd he served — the warehouse, the reeking lot out back, the squandered chances burned trying to keep his bosses satisfied — with the repeated seven-fold hatred functioning less as a lyric than as a pressure valve venting until language gives out. Townsend built City as a deliberately grinding industrial-metal machine, and here he splits himself into first-person I and third-person Devy, the burnt-out worker watched from outside, so the fresh epoch he keeps proclaiming is really the moment he refuses to be a piece in anyone's puzzle anymore. The messianic chorus and the punctuating shouts of defiance inflate self-empowerment to an absurd, screaming grandiosity, then puncture it instantly with the self-mocking headbanger tag and the sarcastic A&W greeting — the joke being that the guy who supposedly stays standing forever still has to clock in tomorrow.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","politics & society"],"means":"wage-slavery refused through a scream inflated to absurd grandiosity then instantly punctured","built":"toddler voice opening and fast-food order closing frame the tirade between two images of a swallowed self"},
"strapping-young-lad~shine":{"haiku":"The speaker attacks someone for being boring and clouded, using rage and repetition to criticize their mediocrity and lack of brilliance.","opus":"Devin Townsend channels manic frustration into this, veering from wonder at another's divine shine to a screaming accusation that both parties are clouded and boring him into himself. The refusal to be told anything erupts in profane repetition, Strapping Young Lad weaponizing volume to dramatize a psyche short-circuiting between admiration and contempt, the demand to shine on curdling into self-directed rage.","sonnet":"Frustration erupts at someone whose radiant self-presentation masks a suffocating conformity. The narrator, sensing his own clouding, refuses to be told what to feel or think — the accusation of being boring becomes a flashpoint, and the song dissolves into a screaming insistence on self-determination over someone else's blinding glow.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~shitstorm":{"haiku":"An angry, unhinged person rages against everyone in their life, proclaiming control over infinite power while spiraling in self-hatred and destructive impulses.","opus":"A raving descent into insomnia, self-hatred, and rage, where the narrator, obsessed with God, death, and infinity, veers between claiming control of his mind and threatening to unleash the very craziness others provoke in him.","sonnet":"A volatile interior monologue erupts between exhaustion, self-loathing, grandiose declarations of control, and raw hostility toward everyone around the narrator, who cannot sleep, eat, or stop thinking about death and infinity.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~skeksis":{"haiku":"The singer meditates on infinity and formula while grappling with pain, guilt, and the impossibility of finding definitive answers to existential questions.","opus":"A vertiginous descent into a splintering mind, the narrator steering a clumsy self on autopilot, scribbling notions of the infinite while numbers, colors and cosmic frequencies dissolve into a manic hunt for meaning that concedes no solution exists. Disorder and remorse knot together. Strapping Young Lad, Devin Townsend's dense extreme-metal project, translate his documented breakdown into a crushing wall of sound.","sonnet":"A mind trying to describe its own automatic operation — drawing lines without volition, collapsing a multiverse into a single thing, finding that color, number, sound, form, war, sex, and healing all reduce to the same vibration. The formula is real but unfactorable; pain has been found, and it is the last discovery.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~the-filler-sweet-city-jesus":{"haiku":"Emotional manipulation and compulsive pushing erode genuine connection into hollow pantomime; love becomes physical force rather than reciprocal bond.","opus":"An early Townsend fever-dream of obsessive, disposable desire, the narrator cast as someone's filler, a stopgap lover reduced to function. Fractured repetitions of pushing and rushing blur devotion into menace, life equated with death, want with violence. It captures a mind fixated on a girl while sneering at its own throwaway role in her life.","sonnet":"A confrontational, barely coherent possession — I've come back for you, I warned you — cycling between obsessive fixation, physical pushing and bruising, and a twisted relational dynamic where filler is both a term of accusation and a role the speaker inhabits. Meaning dissolves into aggression.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~underneath-the-waves":{"haiku":"The song expresses profound exhaustion with waiting, fighting, and false hope. A speaker buried in fatigue, lies, and waking dread names what lies beneath: something or someone submerged, lost, or gone for good.","opus":"Weariness and futility, with the narrator sick of waiting, fighting and trying for nothing, longing for a new age while others are lost underneath the waves.","sonnet":"The narrator is exhausted by an endless cycle — fighting, lying, trying, waiting — and suggests that those who follow such patterns end up submerged and lost beneath the waves.","src":"haiku","fable":"Devin Townsend forges this from sheer looping depletion, its ceaseless chant enacting the very trap it describes. A mind spins in a rut with no exit, where every promised fresh era just curves back into the same circling void. The core confession lays it flat: worn out not by one thing but by the entire machinery of persisting, the striving and even rousing from dawn's half-dreams. Then the title yanks off the disguise, recasting that weariness as a drowning pull. Whoever these figures are, having slipped beneath the water, they become the image of those who at last quit holding on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","suicide"],"means":"exhaustion not from any one thing but from the entire machinery of continuing to exist","built":"the ceaseless looping chant enacts the rut it describes, the form refusing the exit the lyric cannot find either"},
"strapping-young-lad~we-ride":{"haiku":"A relationship conflict escalates with accusations and defensiveness, ending with both parties deciding to 'ride' together despite baggage and compromise demands.","opus":"A caustic, half-satirical dramatization of a relationship imploding into an argument, the narrator insisting he's being completely rational while everyone drags their baggage, before a manic call to put it in gear and ride. Domestic dysfunction curdles into absurdist aggression. Strapping Young Lad, Devin Townsend's chaotic extreme-metal outfit, pair his sardonic humor with sheer bludgeoning force, spoken-word bickering giving way to a full-throttle charge.","sonnet":"A relationship argument that escalates into a refusal to go home — one party listens to nothing they say, the other insists on being completely rational while clearly not being so, and tonight they ride anyway, two people who connect and collide and cannot separate. The tension is both mundane and furious.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~wrong-side":{"haiku":"A troubled man wakes haunted by demons and regret; he takes responsibility for past harm but struggles to escape cycles of dependency and accountability.","opus":"Devin Townsend's extreme-metal project stages a violent self-reckoning, its narrator jolted awake in torment, hounded by his own demons and finally owning the harm he's caused. The chaos frames a bargain with himself: he'll disappear on command, treating self-erasure as the price of accountability, with growing up rendered as a haunting rather than a triumph.","sonnet":"A man wakes in chaos, hunted by personal demons and the consequences of words he cannot stop saying. He catalogues accountability and dependency — a boy becoming a man through compulsion rather than choice — waiting for the single word of release that would let him leave everything behind forever.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~you-suck":{"haiku":"A self-aware rejection anthem where the speaker sarcastically affirms shared worthlessness with the listener, treating failure and dysfunction as a common condition rather than something to hide. The song embraces self-contempt as honest rather than aspirational.","opus":"A deliberately nihilistic anti-anthem built on chanting that everyone, including the band and the audience, sucks. Beneath the crude provocation is self-loathing turned outward and then back inward, the closing admission that they are the losers of all they know making the whole tirade a bleveling gesture of collective worthlessness.","sonnet":"A self-lacerating joke of a song — the band essentially chants that they suck, that you suck, that everyone sucks, with no deeper content beyond the cathartic, absurdist act of screaming mutual contempt into the void. It's self-aware nihilism played for laughs and noise.","src":"haiku"},
"strapping-young-lad~zen":{"haiku":"An assertion of god-like power and superiority over demons, with aggressive declarations of dominance and defiance.","opus":"Devin Townsend screaming his way through mania and grandiosity, the manic god-complex of someone barely holding on. The strained reach for zen calm is swallowed by the chaos, which is the honest joke of it.","sonnet":"A volatile, near-incoherent confrontation — shouted declarations that \"we\" are gods, not demons, and a demand to know why the other person keeps staring — frames a fractured sense of being misread or provoked past a point of no return.","src":"haiku"},
"stratovarius~false-messiah":{"haiku":"Charismatic deception exploits human hunger for deliverance; crowds follow prophecy toward collective ruin.","opus":"A power-metal parable about charismatic saviors who promise deliverance and lead the credulous to ruin. The band frames mass devotion to a smiling stranger as the real danger, warning that longing to be led is how the crowd walks itself into damnation.","sonnet":"A charismatic stranger arrives performing miracles and gathering disciples, but the song questions whether his power is genuine salvation or mass manipulation leading his followers toward destruction rather than deliverance.","src":"haiku"},
"stratovarius~freedom":{"haiku":"Autonomy emerges through self-definition; past suffering transforms into wisdom fueling liberation.","opus":"A creed of self-authorship, setting your own rules and steering your own life, with past hardships reframed as necessary growth once guilt and fear fall away. Tolkki's power metal at its most nakedly motivational, freedom as a chosen state of mind.","sonnet":"A declaration of self-determination: the speaker has broken free from guilt and fear, reframed past suffering as necessary growth, and now steers his own life with confidence, calling out his freedom to the world.","src":"haiku"},
"stratovarius~why-are-we-here":{"haiku":"Existence remains incomprehensible; moments of connection and beauty provide temporary respite from cosmic meaninglessness.","opus":"Facing cosmic meaninglessness, the end of stars and planets, the singer looks past music, drink and lust toward another person's eyes for whatever answer exists. Existential dread answered not with philosophy but with human connection.","sonnet":"Searching for existential meaning through self-destructive habits and the passage of time, the speaker ultimately finds the question answered — or at least bearable — by looking into another person's eyes.","src":"haiku"},
"stray-from-the-path~first-world-problem-child-feat-sam-carter":{"haiku":"Critique of privileged ignorance and inherited social advantage. The speaker confronts sheltered wealth and demands that the privileged witness and understand the experiences of the marginalized.","opus":"Stray from the Path, a politically driven hardcore band, tear into privileged white kids who appropriate Black culture while insulated from any real hardship. With Architects' Sam Carter guesting, the song demands they confront reality and check their sheltered lives, its blunt hook shutting down empty, performative outrage.","sonnet":"A direct assault on privileged complicity. Born into ease, pre-conditioned by surname and circumstance, a sheltered protagonist is confronted with the reality of those he never had to face — the song refuses politeness, demanding the comfortable step into lives they have never risked, and shut up until they do.","src":"haiku"},
"stray-from-the-path~fortune-teller":{"haiku":"Someone asserts complete self-determination and control over their future, rejecting external judgment and building a path they're willing to burn down.","opus":"A defiant anthem of self-authored destiny wrapped in Twin Peaks imagery, the narrator claiming his own fate against a system of slave drivers and prisons. Answering to no one, he turns fear into a lantern and the end into his own choice.","sonnet":"Total self-determination in the face of inherited systems — fathers' shadows, prisons, messiahs — the narrator claims the future as something written in their own bones, not handed down, and the firewalk is the test of whether you can own your own fate.","src":"haiku"},
"stray-from-the-path~iii":{"haiku":"Police violence and systemic racism claim lives while qualified immunity protects executioners from consequence. The song names this as American infrastructure and challenges complicit viewers to witness and resist.","opus":"A scorching indictment of police killings and the legal shield that protects officers who kill, splicing in recruitment slogans to lay bare their hypocrisy. Casting cops as agents of death and descendants of those who once hunted the enslaved, it rides the anger of torched, rebelling cities that refuse to patch up a machine the song says was never faulty, only doing exactly what it was designed to do.","sonnet":"A hardcore indictment of systemic police violence in America — qualified immunity, no-knock raids, thirty-two shots, slave-catcher lineage — frames the badge and bullet as a deadly weapon in the service of murder, and the \"servants of the 187\" refrain names every killing as a willful act with nowhere left to hide from accountability.","src":"haiku"},
"stray-from-the-path~kickback":{"haiku":"Retaliation against exploitation and parasitic manipulation. The speaker cuts away a tick that has fed on them and warns the deceiver that consequences are coming.","opus":"Stray from the Path aim a threat at a treacherous user who struck first, promising a blindsiding payback he won't anticipate. They paint the target as a puffed-up, treacherous ego destined to be brought low, marked for ruin. The hardcore band turn betrayal into a reckoning, rejecting the idea that habitual cruelty is just someone's fixed nature.","sonnet":"A betrayer who always took without giving finally meets consequence. The narrator watches self-obsession inflate until it collapses under its own weight, naming the other a snake in the grass whose smoke-and-mirrors manipulation ends not with negotiation but with a reality check arriving at force — felt before it is seen.","src":"haiku"},
"stray-from-the-path~ring-leader":{"haiku":"Conformist thinking paralyzes independent thought; people follow blindly like hypnotized cult members drinking poison, unaware they've surrendered their minds to false leaders.","opus":"A hardcore broadside against herd mentality, arguing that copying the crowd is no thought at all and picturing obedient followers as hypnotized, restrained, and lapping up a demagogue's poison. Stray from the Path built their name on politically charged, rap-tinged aggression, and here they urge the manipulated masses to burn the whole rigged show down.","sonnet":"A broadside against herd thinking and cult followership — the crowd obeys, mimics, drinks the Kool-Aid, and calls it identity, while the speaker insists that real thought requires breaking from the swarm, the imagery running from hypnotized disciples to the American freak show with its front-row seat for believers.","src":"haiku"},
"stray-kids~side-effects":{"haiku":"Doubt and social pressure gradually corrupt authentic self into performed conformity; the speaker documents his own psychological dissolution as he loses certainty.","opus":"Threw themselves into their ambition and now suffer the toll: anxiety, self-doubt, and a self that shifts to match whoever's around. Stray Kids turn the pressure of idol success into a portrait of overconfidence curdling into worry, the price of chasing it all.","sonnet":"A Korean artist confronts the anxiety of success changing them against their will — their inner and outer selves diverging, their tastes and reactions shaped by external pressures — until self-confidence feels like an overdose with growing, uncontrollable side effects.","src":"haiku"},
"stray-view~escape":{"haiku":"Pressure and isolation accumulate despite best intentions; the speaker fights surrender to the current dragging them under, caught between stability and collapse.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with self-sabotage, the narrator buckling under strain and mistaking his own delusions for clarity. Outrunning the clock and second-guessing himself, he bets that chasing his real self is worth the danger, even if it wrecks him. The turn comes in refusing to be pulled under, resolving to burn the manufactured sickness to ash.","sonnet":"Self-sabotage as destiny — always caving under pressure, destined for disaster, every breath only lasting a moment before the next slide. The speaker is intelligent enough to diagnose the pattern and too caught in it to change, self-induced illusions mistaken for clarity, the path growing longer the more ground is covered.","src":"haiku"},
"stray-view~what-s-done-is-done":{"haiku":"Finality and refusal to carry burden animate the speaker's liberation from the past, claiming responsibility only for their own future while releasing others from blame.","opus":"Cutting loose from a relationship where the singer carried every burden and gave more than enough, refusing to keep deciding whether the other plays it safe. Reclaiming what was lost, he lets the past stay dead and hands back the crown, done needing any of it.","sonnet":"A break from a one-sided relationship where the speaker carried every burden and the other person refused to learn: reclaiming what was lost and abandoning regret, the speaker declares no one controls their destiny — the past stays dead, and the crown the other person clung to is theirs to keep.","src":"haiku"},
"straylight-run~hands-in-the-sky-big-shot":{"haiku":"Systemic coercion and conformity enforced through threat and manufactured helplessness.","opus":"A raging blast against war and propaganda, everyday people shoveled into the war machine, frozen and doomed, while the media engineers agreement and the powerful summon fresh bodies each time their appetite returns.","sonnet":"A song about coercion and propaganda — media headlines, government commands, and authority figures are shown herding passive populations with the same threat: comply or die. The \"big shot\" demanding hands in the air is any power structure that maintains itself through fear rather than consent.","src":"haiku"},
"street-sects~and-i-grew-into-ribbons":{"haiku":"Exhaustion from performative compassion leaves the narrator numb and suicidal, recognising that virtue signals mask indifference and goodness extracts unsustainable toll.","opus":"A deadened voice daydreaming of suicide while tallying up its exhaustion, the drain of caring for people who only demand more, and the guilt that anything worthwhile in his life comes at another person's cost. A bleak industrial confession from someone scooped out by constant giving.","sonnet":"Emotional numbness and exhaustion have overtaken someone who has tried to care — they are tired of the things they love, tired of convincing themselves to keep going, and arriving at the edge of suicidal ideation as the logical endpoint of a life that has stopped feeling worth maintaining.","src":"haiku"},
"street-sects~blacken-the-other-eye":{"haiku":"Systemic violence and indifference breed desperation and revenge; the speaker transforms from passive witness to willing accomplice, meeting cruelty with cruelty to reclaim power.","opus":"A bleak spiral of desperate people ground down by poverty and violence, chasing an impossible clean escape and selling themselves out for nothing. It confronts a hypocrite who wants the respect violence commands without the dirty hands, and closes on a grim vow of shared ruin.","sonnet":"A bleak survey of lives lost to poverty and despair — gunshots, tailpipes, insufficient funds — that pivots into self-implication: the speaker chose the safe way, nobody cared, and he offers to absorb whatever shame and sickness is needed so the other person can remember where it all began.","src":"haiku"},
"street-sweeper~the-oath":{"haiku":"Revolution written in desperation against systemic oppression; the pledge demands respect and violent resistance.","opus":"A worn-down man scans a seedy motel room where every object seems to remember his failures, then carves a working-class vow of defiance into the table. Fitting for Street Sweeper Social Club's rap-rock, it is a pledge to fight the bosses and the system rather than surrender, treating personal ruin as fuel for class rebellion.","sonnet":"A man returns to a seedy motel where every surface — lamppost, mirror, table — remembers his broken promises; in response he carves a new oath of resistance into the lacquer, pledging to fight systems of power, demand respect, and live life as a weapon of lesson, reciting this as \"an ex-loser's song.\"","src":"haiku"},
"strfkr~golden-light":{"haiku":"Dissolution and fading dissolve the boundary between existence and nothing; the speaker embraces becoming one with nothingness while suspended in amber timelessness.","opus":"A weary drift toward oblivion, wanting it all to be over while fading into a golden light. It is about surrender and mortality made oddly serene, two souls dissolving into the same nothing.","sonnet":"A figure waits and fades — \"fade to nothing, golden light\" — in a condition of exhausted longing where being alive feels indistinguishable from being finished; the lines \"we are so alike\" and \"I just want it to be over\" suggest shared weariness dissolving into sameness.","src":"haiku"},
"stu-brooks~they-ll-just-love-you-feat-poppy-danny-elfman":{"haiku":"Inevitable human cruelty and social victimhood by design, existence itself as transgression that invites rage.","opus":"A theatrical, sinister collaboration skewering fame, power, and predatory approval. The narrator revels in provoking argumentative crowds hung on his every word, framing existence itself as an affront. It exposes a rigged game where the have-nots lose the moment they ask to play, and hunger for adoration proves bottomless, never sated even by owning the world.","sonnet":"A cynical address to someone entering a system rigged against them from birth — the game is fixed, the have-nots are waiting, and the people who will greet them with apparent love are the same ones who keep the door closed. 'They'll just love you' is not a comfort but a warning: appetite is infinite and you are the resource.","src":"haiku"},
"stuck-out~hollow":{"opus":"About watching a loved one destroy themselves with addiction and denial while lying that they're fine; the narrator is hooked on those comforting lies, hollowed out and unable to cry even as the relationship dies and the person consumes his every thought.","sonnet":"A song about loving someone who was hiding a terminal struggle — the narrator could not see the flaws or the poison filling the other person's veins, who kept claiming they were fine until they weren't. The aftermath is grief mixed with the guilt of having believed the lie and the addiction to it even in retrospect.","src":"sonnet"},
"sub-focus~entwined":{"haiku":"Mystical connection transcends dimension as two beings dissolve boundaries together, losing time and ego. Cosmic entanglement replaces separate selves with merged consciousness.","opus":"Cosmic infatuation as dance-floor transcendence, moving with a lover tipping into some other plane where time and inhibition dissolve. The beloved seems either human or holy, the bond worth crossing deserts and galaxies for, even as it feels like the mind coming undone.","sonnet":"A cosmic love song: the speaker would cross deserts and galaxies to reach the other person, and when together they enter another dimension where time and inhibitions dissolve — the connection is described as both mortal and divine, a force that colonises every corner of life.","src":"haiku"},
"sublime~santeria":{"haiku":"A spurned lover spirals through desire, rage, and impotent vengeance, oscillating between magical thinking and the futility of fantasy.","opus":"Bradley Nowell wrapping heartbreak in casual gangster bravado, threatening the man who stole his girl while admitting underneath it's just love he can't name or get back. The swagger is grief in disguise.","sonnet":"A man nursing jealous rage over a lost lover fantasizes about violent revenge against the man she left him for, but underneath the bravado admits that what he truly needs is love and someone to call his own.","src":"haiku"},
"subrosa~borrowed-time-borrowed-eyes":{"haiku":"A father's desperate devotion in apocalypse cannot shield his son from systemic collapse, and love becomes insufficient against material annihilation.","opus":"A father clutching his son in a scorched, godless post-collapse world where hope means dying before you grow old. SubRosa strip the apocalypse to its cruelest tenderness: one fragile bond persisting where there's no God and no love left to promise anything.","sonnet":"In a post-collapse world of ash and broken homes, the only remaining tenderness is a father holding his son in the dark — everything else, including God and love, has been stripped away, leaving just that solitary human bond against total desolation.","src":"haiku"},
"subshock-evangelos~next-to-you":{"haiku":"A speaker approaches an attractive woman, takes her home, and they spend intimate time together until they become inseparable.","opus":"The project spins a nightclub romance into a swaggering ballad, spotting a woman in a standout dress and insisting the other men are nothing beside her. Subshock and Evangelos pair heavy production with pop directness, the fragmentary verses following the pair back home into a sunrise of new love, the narrator disarmed and unable to comprehend what he feels.","sonnet":"A man notices a woman at a club, hesitates, then finds himself falling in love by morning. The encounter is rendered in the simple arithmetic of attraction and vulnerability — by sunrise, closeness has crossed into something that feels like permanence, and he does not want to let go.","src":"haiku"},
"suckerpunk~without-me":{"opus":"A codependent plea from within a corrosive relationship, where the singer has lost his sense of self, admits the partner can't save him, yet desperately begs them not to leave without him.","sonnet":"A codependent relationship has eroded the narrator's sense of self entirely, leaving them unable to recognize their own face; they resent the other person but cannot imagine existing without them, and beg not to be left.","src":"sonnet"},
"suffer-club~happy-now":{"haiku":"Toxic entanglement persists despite recognition of mutual destruction as the narrator questions whether sadism disguises as love. Dependence binds two people who harm each other while pretending confusion about the relationship's toxicity.","opus":"A scorching kiss-off to someone controlling and self-righteous who plays god, caged the singer, and let him drown while claiming moral high ground. He'd rather die than be saved by them, throwing their cruelty back with the question of whether ruining him finally makes them happy.","sonnet":"A person escaping a controlling relationship that felt like being caged and drowned asks the abuser directly: does it make you happy now, to be the one less broken? — the image of someone who bought a house in hell and had no soul left to sell pins the perpetrator as irredeemably corrupt.","src":"haiku"},
"sufferer~sword":{"haiku":"A person struggles with anxiety, panic, and emotional fragmentation; they oscillate between attempts at control and paralyzing fear, ultimately critiquing how they turn themselves into a martyr by repeatedly choosing pain.","opus":"A vivid dramatization of a panic attack, the speaker fighting to breathe and regain composure while a part of himself feels terrifyingly detached, control slipping as the panic grips his neck. The central image, falling on the sword and making a martyr of yourself, exposes self-destruction disguised as strength. It reads as emotionally literate hardcore, the drowning refrain admitting no one can be pulled from depths never understood.","sonnet":"Anxiety as internal civil war — the panic grabs hold, confidence frays, and the speaker keeps stitching himself into a martyr's effigy and falling on his own sword out of habit rather than necessity, the real question being not whether to suffer but why the suffering feels so earned, so chosen, so good.","src":"haiku"},
"suicidal-tendencies~cyco-vision":{"haiku":"Clear-eyed perception sees through lies and masks people wear. The speaker claims ability to discern truth beneath deception through direct observation.","opus":"Mike Muir's creed that actions expose people where words conceal them, distrusting smooth talk and performed smiles and insisting on seeing through the lies to whatever a person actually does.","sonnet":"The narrator claims a kind of paranoid-but-accurate perception that cuts through other people's smiles and lies — what you say is irrelevant, only what you do matters, and this 'cyco vision' lets them see the painful truth behind any performance.","src":"haiku"},
"suicidal-tendencies~institutionalized":{"haiku":"Frustration at inability to solve problems escalates into forced confinement as concerned voices push the resistant mind toward brainwashing and chemical suppression.","opus":"A fed-up teenager, written off as insane and medicated by parents and authorities who won't simply back off, insists the system supposedly there to help him is wrecking his mind, when all he was after was a soft drink.","sonnet":"A teenager narrates a series of misunderstandings — all he wanted was a Pepsi — that escalate as his family interprets his isolation as drug use and mental illness, ultimately committing him; the song is a furious monologue about being pathologised for simply needing space, ending with resigned fatalism.","src":"haiku"},
"suicidal-tendencies~refuse":{"haiku":"Defiant rejection of all forms of control, oppression, and self-destruction; the speaker asserts bodily autonomy and mental independence against all external demand.","opus":"A relentless punk manifesto of self-respect, a string of refusals against bowing down, selling out, conforming or being used as anyone's experiment. Turning to the reflection as the true adversary makes the point plain: the real battle is against your own urge to give in, rejecting imposed labels and keeping command of your own mind.","sonnet":"A hardcore manifesto of self-determination — the speaker refuses to submit to authority, external definition, addiction, or exploitation, building a catalog of rejections into a declaration of personal sovereignty and dignity.","src":"haiku"},
"suicide-for-a-king~the-worst-in-us":{"haiku":"Collective denial of human capacity for violence and destruction; society refuses to acknowledge the savage impulses it feeds and excuses.","opus":"A reckoning with the caged violence inside people and nations, the lion fed just enough to stay contained until denial and self-justification unleash it. The metalcore band trace how humanity digs itself past reason into war it can no longer exit.","sonnet":"About the failure to acknowledge the violent, destructive instincts we contain — the beast inside is never dead, just caged, and by denying it exists we wage senseless wars and lose all rationality; the truth is that we've consumed ourselves with the worst in us.","src":"haiku"},
"suidakra~bir-g-s-oath":{"haiku":"Demonic seduction promises power while demanding the surrender of life force and agency, trapping the victim in eternal servitude through false promises.","opus":"A Celtic-metal tale of a druid bound by a sorceress's oath, drained of his life-force as he wages her war on the Fomorian demons, mourned even as she kills him. Mythic dark fantasy of seduction, servitude and a bargain that consumes the one who makes it.","sonnet":"A Celtic mythological drama in which the druidess Biróg manipulates a druid into alliance against the Fomorians by seduction and oath, only to drain his life force once the victory is won and her binding oath expires.","src":"haiku"},
"suilen~magnolia":{"haiku":"Obsessive love becomes violation; the speaker pursues and ensnares their object of desire, offering connection while extracting submission and blood.","opus":"A predatory dreamlike seduction, the singer luring a sleepless girl into a locked room, feeding her words like a drink while circling between everywhere and nowhere. Beneath the honeyed refrain about her blood is a hunger dressed as devotion.","sonnet":"An obsessive, predatory pursuit of a woman who cannot hide — the narrator hunts her through swampland and street, frames it as devotion, and insists he does not need her blood, only her love, while locking her in.","src":"haiku"},
"sullivan-king~lock-me-up":{"haiku":"Colorless isolation and the refusal to be confined despite feeling dead. The speaker embraces chaos and speed while rejecting both salvation and control.","opus":"Sullivan King, who fuses metal with heavy dubstep, voices a narrator hollowed to a bag of death, porcelain-pale and drowned in sameness, taunting captors that they can never lock him up. Gallows humor about raising a family in hell and a plot-twist admission of being utterly alone underlie the defiance, the lyric channeling addiction and isolation into a manic, unbreakable refusal to be caged.","sonnet":"A figure in severe psychological collapse dares the world to contain them. Colourless, in pain, and radically alone — the only family they have ever known is Hell itself — they cycle through dark bravado and honest despair, insisting no external force can confine what has already come completely unmoored from inside.","src":"haiku"},
"sullivan-king~watch-the-crown-fall":{"haiku":"All authority ultimately falls, singing together as ritual acknowledgment of universal collapse.","opus":"Metal-meets-bass rage at collapsing power, every ruler doomed to fall, sung with grim communal glee as the world burns, undercut by the private terror of becoming the nightmare that ends others' dreams.","sonnet":"A meditation on fearing that you are your child's nightmare rather than their protector — the narrator is a parent terrified of being a destructive force in their kid's life. The broader refrain about kings falling and worlds burning frames personal failure within inevitable collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"sullivan-king~you-can-t-break-me-feat-brandon-saller-of-atreyu":{"haiku":"Resilience crystallizes through adversity; the speaker claims unbreakable status grounded in survival through fire, standing as proof against all who would diminish them.","opus":"An unkillable declaration of defiance to an enemy who wants the throne, the singer casting himself as the nail in their coffin and the ocean's crushing deep. Standing unscathed when the last days come, he dares them to come for his throat, certain they can't break him.","sonnet":"A declaration of absolute resilience against an adversary who wants the throne — \"neck deep in fire, I'm unscathed\" — the speaker positions themselves as the haunting reverie that outlasts every attack, standing on their own feet at the end of days.","src":"haiku"},
"sum-41~pieces":{"haiku":"Fractured identity dissolves under pressure; the narrator seeks perfection but finds only isolation, concluding that solitude shields him from the damage inflicted by connection.","opus":"Giving up on the exhausting pursuit of perfection and other people's approval, concluding he's better off alone. Behind the pop-punk melody is real weariness and isolation, a young man deciding self-reliance is all he can trust.","sonnet":"A speaker in emotional isolation admits that striving for perfection was worthless and that the emptiness around him is his only constant, concluding that he is better off facing it alone rather than pretending otherwise.","src":"haiku"},
"sum-41~the-bitter-end":{"haiku":"Mortality and judgment loom inescapable; choices made accumulate into karmic consequence, erasing escape routes at life's conclusion.","opus":"A hard-edged reckoning with mortality and consequence, insisting there's no escaping the end you've built through your own choices. Sum 41 drop the levity for a grim reminder that time runs out and no one may be waiting when it does.","sonnet":"A stark confrontation with mortality and consequence — every choice has already been made, sympathy is gone, and the only remaining question is who will greet you when time runs out and your life fades away.","src":"haiku"},
"summer-hailstorm~relic":{"haiku":"Systemic exploitation and rebellion collide, with the speaker exhausted by oppression and choosing between violent uprising and death.","opus":"A revolt against a surveillance city that drains and watches everyone, where obedience is a waste and the mind dissociates under pressure. With the hourglass expiring, the only options left are revolt or death, the self carving its name so it will be remembered.","sonnet":"A revolt song about living under surveillance and exploitation — the city is trying to drain the narrator dry, lies have been fed since birth, and the only two options are revolt or death; the song drives toward collective uprising against those who crush the weak.","src":"haiku"},
"sun-flesh~never-enough":{"haiku":"Insatiable hunger and parasitic systems consume the population through manufactured need and stolen goods. The song calls for uprising and sword-wielding resistance against a beast that feeds on perpetual want.","opus":"A call to arms against corrupt, lying powers that feed on the people and are never satisfied. Refusing to lie down or trust the pundits, it urges the crowd to stay alert, stand unified, and strike the serpent before greed swallows them alive.","sonnet":"A rallying call against ruling-class parasites who worship lies, steal from the people, and are never satisfied — the listeners are urged to stand firm, raise their swords, kill the snake, because the black hole of elite greed eats everyone alive and no amount of suffering is ever enough to sate it.","src":"haiku"},
"sundazer~closer":{"haiku":"Resistance and surrender collapse into paradox; the speaker vacillates between control and surrender, unable to find ground in the push-pull of connection.","opus":"A seduction that keeps insisting the connection isn't real even as it pulls you in, the manipulator flattering and controlling at once. Intimacy staged as a game where feeling and pretending become impossible to separate.","sonnet":"An encounter where physical closeness is offered but emotional realness is absent — the other person has become attached despite no genuine feeling existing, and the narrator keeps the terms deliberately shallow.","src":"haiku"},
"sundazer~echoes":{"haiku":"Entrapment and sensory overload overwhelm the speaker as walls close inward, echoes of old mistakes bleeding into present disorientation and cyclical failure.","opus":"Snared inside a shrinking labyrinth of his own wavering, the narrator is pulled toward a beacon but keeps smashing into walls, the whole ordeal cast as a cruel loop of endless recurrence.","sonnet":"About being paralyzed by indecision and self-doubt — the speaker is caught in a maze of their own making, hitting invisible walls, drowning in darkness, and haunted by the echo of past mistakes, but finally resolves to roll the dice and break the cycle.","src":"haiku"},
"sundazer~silent-call":{"haiku":"Rising through pain transforms brokenness into art while passion ignites against shadows. The speaker heeds the silent summons toward ignition and transcendence.","opus":"Answering an inner summons to abandon a landscape of dead dreams and climb up out of the dark toward hope. The shattered fragments reassemble into a masterwork as pain becomes self-knowledge, the wordless call driving the leap ahead.","sonnet":"A journey from chaos and darkness toward hope and ignition — broken pieces forming a masterpiece, passion bursting in the chest — chasing a silent call heard only by those willing to push through the night and find the last light.","src":"haiku"},
"sunfall~quiet-kid":{"haiku":"School trauma and internal violence simmer beneath quiet exteriors as the narrator details fantasies of destruction and escape. Silent suffering builds into apocalyptic impulse that explodes without warning or apology.","opus":"A disturbing dive into the mind of a bullied, suicidal outcast whose silence hides homicidal fantasy, spliced with a rapper's rant against a school system that fails such kids. It refuses to resolve, tangling the artist's own six-year struggle with despair into imagery of massacre and self-destruction.","sonnet":"The interior of an alienated, suicidal teenager who fantasises about violence against the world that ignores him is rendered with unflinching directness — six years of depression, intrusive thoughts of murder and self-harm, and a final pivot into a raw address to the marginalised kids in the back of the class: use your voice, don't be played.","src":"haiku"},
"sungazer~bird-on-the-wing":{"opus":"A hushed reflection on mistimed love, built around an old standard the singer once performed for someone at the wrong moment. Years later he marks his quiet release from that lingering attachment, treating a piece of music as the thread that once bound him and now, finally, no longer does.","sonnet":"A brief, melancholic farewell to an old song and the person it was sung for — the narrator sang too early, sang too late, but can now release both the song and the tie it represented, no longer bound by what was.","src":"sonnet"},
"sunset-neon~never-dance-again-battle-tapes-remix":{"haiku":"Infidelity carries permanent consequence as the unfaithful lover loses capacity for joy, crushed beneath the weight of their own betrayal.","opus":"A vengeful kiss-off to a cheater, imagining her literally struck down mid-affair so she'll never dance again. Synthwave gloss over genuine spite, betrayal answered with a coldly gleeful wish for her downfall.","sonnet":"A narrator addresses a woman who cheated and was hit by a car while walking with her lover — framing the accident as karmic consequence for her betrayal. The tone is cold and sardonic rather than grief-stricken, and the refrain lands as a dark joke.","src":"haiku"},
"super-beaver~a-2yzzta":{"haiku":"A gentle argument against self-searching—authenticity isn't discovered or achieved; it's only ever who you naturally are.","opus":"Super Beaver rebuilt their career from indie obscurity after being dropped by a major label, and that hard-won plainness shapes this gentle argument against self-searching. Against magazine-cover pressure to manufacture individuality, the band insists authenticity is not a project but simply what already sits in the gap between the child you were and the self you are becoming.","sonnet":"A song about the pressure to perform individuality — magazine covers tell you to differentiate yourself, but 'being yourself' isn't something you find; it's just what you already are, in the gaps between who you were as a child and who you're becoming. The emotional core is the quiet argument that authenticity doesn't require a project, only the honesty to stop dressing it up.","src":"haiku"},
"super-junky-monkey~a-i-e-t-o-h":{"haiku":"Delusion becomes truth, fantasy becomes action in this spiral of controlled contradiction. The song encrypts philosophy into syllables—direct utterance overpowers lies, human will emerges from the machinery of history.","opus":"A Japanese funk-metal barrage stacking kanji terms for oppression and tyranny into a percussive avalanche of language. It wrestles delusion against truth, insisting fantasy and reality are equally real, then calls for unity and resistance out of discrimination. The chanted title functions as a rallying cry, sound and rhythm carrying meaning as much as the words.","sonnet":"A chant-like confrontation with invisible oppression — swastikas flooding the field of vision, layers of suppression and subjugation catalogued — calling for the release of illusion into truth, the convergence of strategists and sincere hearts into one unified victory over discrimination.","src":"haiku"},
"superbloom~falling-up":{"haiku":"Ascendant escape through altered states; the speaker rises beyond pain through substances and supernatural suspension.","opus":"A hazy, dreamlike flip where dropping turns into ascending, the narrator watching someone dull their fortune and hurt, and asking them, once they've descended again, to confirm the delay was worthwhile.","sonnet":"About watching someone self-medicate through pain — the speaker identifies with them so completely they feel like the same person, but observes them falling upward into altered states and waits to see if it's worth it when they come back down.","src":"haiku"},
"superbloom~level-head":{"haiku":"Numbness and detachment characterize the speaker's state; they seek connection but lack the emotional capacity to feel authentic. Life continues through empty motions.","opus":"The grunge-leaning band captures a numbed slump of late-night television and self-medication, the narrator admitting composure isn't in his nature and he rarely bothers to look happy. Superbloom's fuzzy, slacker delivery suits the small scale of it, a snapshot of aimless comfort quietly reaching for connection with someone.","sonnet":"A person cohabits with low-grade mental unrest — insomnia, numbness, a knuckle dragged across tile — and searches for a moment of genuine completion. The question directed at a companion is also directed inward: whether there is anything left worth favoring, and whether feeling good is even recognizable anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"superbloom~whatever":{"haiku":"A person enjoys solitude and nature, finding peace in distance while reflecting on memory, emptiness, and the cycles of growth and vitality.","opus":"Solitude and rainfall become a quiet register for dissociation, the narrator feeling a million miles away even upon returning to himself, wishing he were already gone. Superbloom, a New York shoegaze-adjacent act, matches the numb shrug of its title to washed-out textures, where a remembered string on a finger stands in for everything slipping loose from vitality.","sonnet":"Emotional numbness worn lightly — a figure who finds solace in rain and solitude, perpetually feeling a million miles removed from wherever life places them. A planted seed and a half-remembered thread are the only suggestions that something once mattered before drift took over.","src":"haiku"},
"superheaven~in-on-it":{"haiku":"Psychic collapse, self-harm, and willingness to be consumed by another's pain. The speaker offers themselves as a companion in suffering and isolation.","opus":"Superheaven trade in fuzzed-out nineties-indebted grunge, and this one sits inside a lopsided attachment that hollows the singer out: he stops caring for himself, pledges total availability, and stays devoted even as it drains him. The devotion has curdled into something closer to self-erasure than love.","sonnet":"Anxious devotion to someone who may not need it — the speaker is running on very little, skipping meals, reading danger signs in ordinary images, and the offer to be available when things go wrong is as much about needing to be needed as it is about caring, the cold autumn detail grounding a song about waiting for a call that might not come.","src":"haiku"},
"superheaven~poor-aileen":{"haiku":"Systematic dehumanization and medical cruelty destroy a vulnerable life; the speaker mourns someone stripped of autonomy and dignity, executed by a system that saw her as disposable.","opus":"A tender elegy for someone dehumanized, strapped to a bed and executed while everyone called her a monster, drawn as Aileen Wuornos. It is about extending human sympathy to a condemned, unloved figure denied any chance.","sonnet":"A tender, sorrowful address to a woman treated as less than human — left in the woods, strapped to a bed, called a monster — who was given no chance before the needle went in; the speaker offers something no one else did: understanding and the instruction not to be afraid.","src":"haiku"},
"superjoint-ritual~sickness":{"haiku":"Addiction ravages the body through withdrawal; physical deterioration masks terror of dissolution.","opus":"An unflinching plunge into heroin withdrawal, the sweating, vomiting agony where the self-inflicted poison that once soothed now tortures. Anselmo, no stranger to addiction, renders detox as a near-death crawl through a body turned against itself.","sonnet":"A harrowing descent into drug withdrawal or addiction-induced physical and psychological collapse — the body revolts, old friends disappear, paranoia sets in, and what begins as chosen self-destruction ends by consuming everything the speaker had.","src":"haiku"},
"superlove~go":{"haiku":"Urgent propulsion and momentum as escape or liberation, the speaker driving forward with determination and freedom.","opus":"A bright, ironic anthem that apes locker-room motivational cheer while quietly gutting it. Grind harder, do more, and it is never quite sufficient — the narrator suspects this is a contest with no possible winner, the frontrunners racing right over a cliff. A doubt about whether endless striving arrives anywhere worth being.","sonnet":"A race metaphor that turns on itself — cheered on to cross the finish line, told to keep it up, only to realize the race can't be won and the finish line leads straight off an edge. The coaching voice and the dread voice occupy the same space, and 'go' becomes both encouragement and trap.","src":"haiku"},
"supruga~a-ojdfq5":{"haiku":"Domestic suffering and wifely shame—a woman leaves at dawn in disgrace, hoping to avoid bringing ruin to her home; childhood memories echo with distress.","opus":"Supruga works in a chilly post-Soviet register where domestic space turns uncanny, and this Russian song lets dissociation seep through the walls. The narrator greets someone while unsure of their own color, hears moaning behind an old wardrobe, and by the end runs toward a place beyond pain as another presence swallows the light entirely.","sonnet":"A Russian song haunted by dissociation and domestic unease: the narrator greets someone while uncertain of their own color or identity, hears moaning behind the old wardrobe, offers to show them what love looks like — but by the end, light has been consumed by the other's presence and the narrator runs toward a place where pain no longer exists.","src":"haiku"},
"survive-said-the-prophet~found-lost":{"haiku":"The narrator shifts from seeking answers to simply wanting to journey with someone, finding hope through fate despite confusion and emotional turmoil.","opus":"Sung mostly in Japanese with an English hook, a reflection on feeling simultaneously discovered and adrift, certain of nothing yet trusting that destiny keeps delivering hope, and confessing he craved the shared journey more than any answer. The Japanese rock band weave both languages together as weariness and resilience trade places and home feels impossibly distant.","sonnet":"A song about wanting the journey more than any destination — the realization that what was really wanted was companionship along the road, not answers, and fate keeps delivering hope even through anger and tears and disorientation, the speaker lost and found over and over with no clean resolution in sight.","src":"haiku"},
"survive-said-the-prophet~let-it-die":{"opus":"Resilience through repeated falling, the vow to rise until death and refuse the impossible. Survive Said the Prophet frame perseverance as controlled defiance, city lights and pounded nails becoming a foundation to overcome doubt. The paradox of letting it die is really about tearing down what fails so something truer can be rebuilt.","sonnet":"A resilience anthem about committing fully to struggle even knowing failure is possible — the narrator acknowledges the option of letting things die but chooses to keep fighting, framing forward momentum as the only honest response to living.","src":"sonnet"},
"survive-said-the-prophet~mukanjyo":{"opus":"A disillusioned reckoning with a ruthless, winner-take-all world where the strong are shackled by duty and the weak are simply crushed. Having sacrificed everything he had, the numbed narrator demands to know how it all went so wrong and whether existence is nothing but a small, brutal contest to kill or be killed.","sonnet":"A bilingual confrontation with a brutally competitive world — the narrator has sacrificed everything to survive in a dog-eat-dog society and now feels emotionally dead, unable to feel anything looking at a world where the strong chain the responsible and trample the weak, asking bitterly who can answer for what went wrong.","src":"sonnet"},
"survivor~eye-of-the-tiger":{"haiku":"A survivor fights back from hardship through determination and focus, refusing to surrender despite setbacks. The song celebrates resilience and competitive victory.","opus":"A fighter rises off the street, guarding old dreams and the raw will to survive against a stalking rival. Written for Rocky III at Stallone's request, Survivor's 1982 juggernaut distils the underdog's climb into pure adrenaline, the tiger's predatory gaze standing for the hunger a champion must never trade for comfort or glory.","sonnet":"A fighter who has been knocked down climbs back through sheer will, driven by the predatory instinct to survive and prevail. The stakes are existential — passion traded for glory is a fatal bargain — but the hunger to compete and reclaim what was lost keeps pushing him forward.","src":"haiku"},
"suspended-4th~a-rz7ft5":{"haiku":"A dreamlike narrative about infatuation and self-deception—conflating imagination with reality, chasing something that no longer exists.","opus":"Suspended 4th built their name busking on Tokyo streets, and their blues-soaked swagger gives this confession its exhausted momentum. A person who always gets swept into wreckage watches a predator arrive at 3 AM and feels their own foot bolt before thought can catch it, reframing the endless cycle as a dream because only the reckless present feels real.","sonnet":"Someone who knows they always end up getting swept into bad situations — a predatory figure approaches at 3 AM, the narrator's right foot bolts before they can stop it, and they frame the whole cycle as a dream they live inside rather than reality. The emotional core is the exhausted self-awareness of a person who keeps making the same mistake in love because the present moment, however reckless, is the only one that feels real.","src":"haiku"},
"suzanne-vega~luka":{"haiku":"A child victim normalizes domestic violence through practiced deception, internalizing blame and silence as survival mechanisms.","opus":"A domestic-abuse story told in the child's own careful voice, the excuses and don't-ask deflections a battered kid learns, its gentle melody making the horror land harder for how normalized it sounds.","sonnet":"A child named Luka speaks from behind a careful performance of normalcy, deflecting questions about the noise from upstairs and the bruises — offering cover stories for abuse and quietly asking not to be asked, having learned that silence is safer than the truth.","src":"haiku"},
"suzy-clue~holy-touch":{"haiku":"Sacred transformation and spiritual transcendence through contact, the speaker experiencing divine intervention.","opus":"A haunted, remorseful farewell to a bond one person denies and retracts, leaving it beyond repair. The narrator, aware of having pushed things past the point of no return, hopes the other finds their footing while promising to linger like a ghost. It dwells in the unspeakable weight of a severed closeness and, at the end, a plea for forgiveness.","sonnet":"A relationship's sacred connection is being denied and dismantled — the speaker confronts someone retreating into disguise, acknowledging they've pushed too hard, and offering a bitter benediction: find your way to life even if I haunt you, but you're on your own now.","src":"haiku"},
"suzy-clue~uneasy":{"haiku":"Predatory seduction dissolves moral boundaries; the victim internalizes blame as the aggressor's touch invades and unmakes the self.","opus":"A dark-pop portrait of consuming, destabilizing attraction, desire that unsettles as much as it thrills. The narrator feels herself dismantled by another's presence, sinking to the bone, morals slipping, hollow and craving attention. Wanting to take the other in fully, she surrenders to a breaking point where longing overrides every defense and sense of self.","sonnet":"A toxic attraction that breaks every moral as it goes — sinking to the bones, hollow heart begging for attention — the speaker's unease indistinguishable from desire. Seduction and dissolution arrive at the same moment.","src":"haiku"},
"svalbard~listen-to-someone":{"haiku":"The speaker endures severe depression and isolation without solution, asking others to listen without judgment since some illnesses have no fix.","opus":"An unflinching account of mental illness, stretches with no food, sleep or speech and nights crowded with voices, pushing back against empty comfort from people who flinch and lose patience. It insists some conditions have no cure and that the self, not the circumstances, is the affliction. The British hardcore-blackgaze band deliver this personal advocacy with soaring, anguished honesty.","sonnet":"Days lost to starvation and silence, nights loud with internal voices — and then the specific injury of being told it is okay not to be okay by someone who flinches at every word. Some illnesses have no fix, the song says plainly, and the only thing being asked for is patience without the performance of understanding.","src":"haiku"},
"svalbard~open-wound":{"haiku":"Vulnerability and possession; opening oneself to another allows them to tear away pieces until scars are all that remain.","opus":"Svalbard, the UK band who fuse blackened hardcore with feminist frankness, lay bare the danger of yielding in love, the speaker inviting a blow that reawakens her feeling while swapping parts of herself for the other person. Confinement, restraint, and torn feathers build toward an image of being held down and hushed, the final judgment that this aches too badly to be love, turning devotion into muted abuse.","sonnet":"A devotion that destroys is embraced without a shield. The speaker surrenders entirely to someone whose love operates through diminishment — weight pressed down, voice stopped, feathers plucked one by one — and only in the final reckoning does the wound acknowledge itself: what has been endured is too painful to be love.","src":"haiku"},
"swallow-the-sun~these-woods-breathe-evil":{"haiku":"Landscapes absorb trauma and madness; the speaker descends into a realm where nature and mind blur into evil.","opus":"A murderer haunted by voices and tormented visions, the blood on his hands undeniable even as reality dissolves. Steeped in Twin Peaks imagery, the Finnish doom band cast guilt and madness as an evil forest that lured him to his own damnation.","sonnet":"A murderer driven to the act by tormenting voices and a malevolent landscape confesses from a place of horror at what his hands have done, tracing the path from seduction to slaughter through a world where the woods themselves seem to will destruction. The Twin Peaks imagery of black lodges and red rooms frames the evil as cosmic rather than simply personal.","src":"haiku"},
"swarm~gone-instrumental":{"haiku":"Hope evaporates completely; the speaker drowns as indifference reigns and darkness becomes the only resident of an abandoned world.","opus":"An instrumental rendering of the band's cry of abandonment — the vocal original tells of someone lost and afraid, sure that nobody hears or is even there, promised the world and handed nothing but death, hollowed out with every hope extinguished and something dark now dwelling inside.","sonnet":"A person who has lost their way and feels unheard — 'can you see me, are you listening, do you even care?' — is consumed by something that enters and lives inside them as a promise turned to death, all hope gone, soul swallowed, nothing left.","src":"haiku"},
"sweet-cobra~sunburned-sons":{"haiku":"Workers trapped underground without light, their natural growth crushed by confinement and walls, forced to adapt to oppression while warned of the horrors created by human greed.","opus":"A grim allegory of people forced underground to labor in darkness, adapting to their chains until their eyes grow gray and memory dims, while the young are still warned of the horrors that greed created. Adaptation itself becomes a kind of doom. Sweet Cobra, a Chicago heavy band, render this bleak parable of exploitation and generational warning through dense, sludge-tinged aggression rather than any note of escape.","sonnet":"Generations forced underground by someone else's greed — sunless, breathless, walls closing, bodies bent into ruined shapes by the conditions imposed on them. Adaptation itself becomes a sentence; even after the chains fall away they remain in the dark, dimming, gray-eyed, told horrors they were warned about and couldn't prevent.","src":"haiku"},
"sweet-mullet~a-jlhkav":{"haiku":"A meditation on overwhelming infatuation—a lover's gaze melts the speaker's heart, ignites it, leaving no room for anyone else.","opus":"Sweet Mullet emerged from Thailand's 2000s alt-metal wave, pairing heavy dynamics with confessional tenderness, and that contrast fuels this love song. A heart that stayed solid and self-contained melts under one person's gaze, and the narrator, bewildered at why this person specifically, surrenders to being burned up, tasting for the first time the terrifying pleasure of vulnerability.","sonnet":"A Thai-language love song about a heart that was solid and self-contained until one person's gaze melted it completely — the narrator is bewildered by what is happening inside them, why this person specifically, and surrenders entirely to being burned up. The emotional core is the terrifying pleasure of becoming vulnerable for the first time.","src":"haiku"},
"sweet-pill~no-control":{"haiku":"Malleability disguises as virtue; the speaker bends to accommodate others' needs until self-shatters, becoming putty in hands that reshape without care.","opus":"Being malleable to the point of self-erasure in a relationship, letting bones break to fit someone else's mold. Wanting more while giving up all control, the singer stops caring whether it hurts.","sonnet":"About being shaped and reshaped by someone else's desire — the narrator describes themselves as malleable, their bones broken to fit a mold, their patience thin but their skin exposed anyway. The emotional core is the loss of agency in a relationship where the other person's wants override everything, and the narrator's inability to resist even while recognizing the damage.","src":"haiku"},
"sweet-pill~rotten":{"haiku":"Good intentions spoil and curdle; language circles decay to minimize damage.","opus":"A relationship's sweet nothings gone sour, everything now rotten no matter how the words are spun to seem less bad. The singer admits to tearing apart the one thing they actually wanted, circling the truth they can't quite say.","sonnet":"Good intentions left out too long turn sour and then fully rotten — the speaker circling around gentler words to describe the decay, eating up every last crumb of sugarcoated junk, until they admit they tore apart the only thing they wanted.","src":"haiku"},
"sweet-pill~sometimes":{"haiku":"Waiting for sudden catastrophe while drowning in compulsive spiraling, unable to silence inner noise.","opus":"Living braced for the next blow, anxiety as a constant wait for detonation, the body too worn to hold its own head up. The wish to silence an obsessive, self-sabotaging inner voice that keeps burning down what matters.","sonnet":"A portrait of compulsive anxiety and obsessive thinking — the narrator is perpetually bracing for catastrophe, worn down by an \"obsessive mouth\" they can't switch off. The destructive fire imagery captures how unchecked desire and rumination end up consuming relationships and the self.","src":"haiku"},
"sweet-spine~888":{"haiku":"Violation and burial intertwine as the speaker requests annihilation over recovery; mercy and death become indistinguishable.","opus":"A plea to be let go and buried rather than saved, exposing the false sorry of someone whose eyes have already drifted away as love fades and the self starts to disappear.","sonnet":"About total emotional surrender to someone who is indifferent — the speaker invites physical destruction, asks to be buried, and begs to be released, while their heart aches as the person they love drifts away without remorse.","src":"haiku"},
"sweet-spine~anathema":{"haiku":"Social judgment and transformation transform intimacy into public spectacle where reputation replaces authenticity. The speaker asserts dominance while community turns to witness and betrayer.","opus":"Turning small-town gossip into fuel, refusing to give ground once your name becomes something people whisper. Defiance dressed as a shrug at how people change the moment they think they know you.","sonnet":"A defiant response to social judgment and gossip: the speaker refuses to give in despite being lied about and watched by a town that starts talking when it knows your name, insisting on taking what is theirs rather than backing down.","src":"haiku"},
"sweet-spine~lights-out":{"haiku":"Desire and pain intertwine in a dance of surrender; the speaker begs for violence rendered intimate, drowning in static and calling.","opus":"Longing for a lost love braided with a craving for pain and oblivion, begging to be cut, made to bleed, and finally granted an end. Peace and violence blur, the singer praying to be hit with the lights out, falling apart the moment the other closes their eyes.","sonnet":"A plea for both pain and peace directed at someone whose presence is the only antidote to static loneliness — 'bleed, make me bleed, cut me up, I'm yours to keep' — culminating in a surrender to darkness where calling the name and closing the eyes brings the lights down.","src":"haiku"},
"sweet-trip~chocolate-matter":{"opus":"A tender appeal to a frightened loved one to quit fleeing in fear and simply move through the year's turning, needing scarcely more than a handful of decent days, a firm shove, and warmth from the people around them.","sonnet":"A brief, impressionistic fragment — someone is told to run away, gets scared by the narrator's feelings, but the narrator insists simple seasonal pleasures and love from others like themselves are all they need; the fragmentary form mirrors the lightness of the feeling.","src":"sonnet"},
"sweet-trip~design-2-3":{"opus":"A gentle, wistful urging to reach for light and share it with someone, even knowing the reaching may fall short. The tenderness carries an undertone of parting, that sharing the light also means not being together again.","sonnet":"A tender, elliptical gesture toward someone who is the core of the speaker's world — \"you are the core\" — reaching for a sun, sharing light, living under a spell, and ending with the quiet acknowledgment that they won't be together again.","src":"sonnet"},
"sweet-trip~dsco":{"haiku":"Two lovers escape into comfort and sensuality, running toward desire and away from rational thought.","opus":"Shoegaze-glitch euphoria about melting into closeness and pleasure, letting reason slip away to shape one another and drift toward comfort. The bent digital shimmer makes giving in to intimacy feel like floating out toward warm summer water.","sonnet":"An invitation to escape into sensory pleasure with a partner — lying by water, escaping logic, synchronizing with each other — a fantasy of pure comfort and closeness sealed off from the outside world.","src":"haiku"},
"sweet-trip~international":{"haiku":"Breaking free from lies together, accepting that honesty and connection justify the messy journey out.","opus":"Buried under shoegaze haze, a couple breaking free of a shared mess and finding that surrender to another can feel like a sweet kind of dying. Fragile hope and self-erasure held in one breath.","sonnet":"Two people breaking free from a damaging situation hold onto the possibility of something better — the speaker writes letters, stays alongside, and accepts the ambivalence of a life that can turn into dying in someone's hands or running from the charade.","src":"haiku"},
"sweet-trip~milk":{"haiku":"Accepting that someone will drift away but choosing not to fight it, letting go with grace and resignation.","opus":"A lover already resigned to being left, insisting he won't mind the drift away even as the repetition betrays how much he does. Toasting a long life to soften an inevitable goodbye.","sonnet":"A speaker watches someone drift in restless sleep, knowing they will eventually float away — and accepts it with quiet equanimity, toasting a long life and releasing them without resentment.","src":"haiku"},
"swim-school~bored":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and contempt toward relentless complaint; the speaker stands firm in indifference, refusing engagement with those who wallow in self-pity.","opus":"A defiant kiss-off to a belittling critic: the speaker, tired of hearing they're bringing everyone down, refuses to shrink, standing her ground with nothing to prove and turning the tables by asking whether she intimidates the one who won't stop talking.","sonnet":"Confidence reclaimed from years of someone dragging you down: the speaker wants the other on their knees to see what's at their feet now, stands their ground with newly found certainty, invoking a proud woman who would approve. The boredom is armour — don't say anymore — as contempt displaces the need for validation.","src":"haiku"},
"swim-school~delirious":{"haiku":"Contempt disguised as generosity; arrogant refusal to respect breeds jealousy and cruelty in return.","opus":"A sharp-edged dream-pop kiss-off to someone dismissive and disrespectful, who judges on sight and shuts people out. The narrator refuses to absorb the treatment, naming the contempt for what it is and mockingly echoing back the false generosity. Its clearest read is that the aggressor's cruelty exposes nothing but their own insecurity and jealousy.","sonnet":"A speaker calls out someone in authority who dismisses, disengages, and disrespects with visible pride — you put us down and cut us off — and the chorus names the derangement: you are so dangerous, so delirious if you think I'll ever listen, your insecurities show nothing but jealousy.","src":"haiku"},
"swirlies~two-girls-kissing":{"haiku":"A person unexpectedly finds love and sentiment in a moment that grounds them after being lost, a small but real memory of connection and belonging.","opus":"The Boston shoegaze band frames a tiny, disorienting epiphany, an ordinary afternoon in which the narrator, lost and unmoored, is steadied by glimpsing a moment of quiet affection between strangers. Buried under lo-fi haze, the scene melts away his confusion and dread, and he insists such small days matter more than they seem.","sonnet":"A chance sighting of two women kissing in a parked car in Cambridge becomes an unexpected anchor during a period of confusion and lost direction. The image does not explain itself but carries sentiment — a glimpse of unselfconscious love that loosens fear and briefly restores orientation.","src":"haiku"},
"sworn-in~pins-and-needles":{"haiku":"Self-destruction and romantic entanglement compound into a feedback loop where love becomes the instrument of the speaker's own unraveling.","opus":"A voice spiraling through self-destruction, where love collapses into mere touch and pain becomes the only crutch, ending in wishing he were dead. Metalcore confession of a mind that romanticizes its own harm, treating suffering as the closest thing to feeling real.","sonnet":"A person in acute psychological and physical distress cycles through numbness, self-harm, and dark fantasy, discovering that when reality becomes unbearable the only thing that feels real is pain, which they poison themselves with rather than face the alternative.","src":"haiku"},
"sworn-in~shit-talk":{"haiku":"Threatens violent retribution against someone while examining shared suffering that justifies cruelty. Refuses to hide rage or seek reconciliation.","opus":"A venom-drenched confrontation, the narrator daily debating his own damnation while spitting threats at an adversary he loves to watch suffer, wielding hurt as both weapon and shared wound. Curb-stomp imagery pushes the aggression to a deliberately ugly extreme. Sworn In, an Illinois metalcore band associated with the darkhardcore wave, built their sound on exactly this fusion of self-loathing and outward violence, delivered with unhinged spite.","sonnet":"Pure aggression as self-defense — the speaker ready to face hell itself, turning daily self-interrogation into fuel for confrontation, daring anyone to step close enough to test it. Underneath the volume is something rawer: shared damage acknowledged briefly before the rage closes back over it.","src":"haiku"},
"syd-matters~to-all-of-you":{"haiku":"The speaker romanticizes American girls as idealized figures while expressing loneliness and isolation. He dreams of connection across distance.","opus":"A lonely outsider fantasizes about the airbrushed, unreal women he knows only from cinema, aching to belong to their sunny, seaside world while admitting his own isolation and another's private grief. Longing and sorrow coexist. Syd Matters, Jonathan Morali's French indie-folk project, reached a wider audience when this hushed track appeared in the game Life Is Strange.","sonnet":"A European outsider's wistful, half-ironic longing for American girls — shiny, cinematic, emotionally unreadable, driving by the seaside with the world at a comfortable bright-side angle. The fantasy is undercut by a glimpse of a real woman crying alone in the mountains, and the singer flying toward the wildland to find her.","src":"haiku"},
"syence~hate-me-like-u-love-me-feat-jake-neumar":{"haiku":"Contradictory emotions bind two people in cycles of rejection and return as intimacy collapses into contempt. The song captures ambivalence where love and hate become indistinguishable forces propelling continued contact.","opus":"A toxic ex who insists the singer is no good yet keeps texting and turning up after dark. The push-pull is the whole story: she publicly disowns him while privately clinging, hating him in a way indistinguishable from love.","sonnet":"A toxic ex who publicly dismissed the speaker as not worth their migraines keeps texting in the dark — the speaker notes the contradiction with resigned amusement, telling them: you hate me like you love me, and both of you know it.","src":"haiku"},
"sylosis~calcified":{"haiku":"Watching someone decay from inside as they surrender to darkness, their eyes losing all life. The speaker cuts off the threat to survive, trapped in their own labyrinth of despair.","opus":"British melodic thrash outfit Sylosis, led by Josh Middleton, delivers a bleak portrait of watching someone harden and rot from within, consumed by their own denial. The narrator claws to preserve some living part of himself and cuts down the threat before it destroys him, framing survival as a solitary act with no rescuer coming.","sonnet":"Watching someone harden and hollow from the inside — eyes going grey, the rot incremental and internal, weight accepted until it becomes identity. The speaker cut away the source before it could sever them too, survived alone, and the warning is that the only person left to save is yourself.","src":"haiku"},
"sylosis~deadwood":{"haiku":"Societal fragmentation and removal of dysfunction's causes, cutting away rot that divides and paralyzes the whole.","opus":"A thrash-forged vision of collapse and cleansing revolt, the sky itself changed, the social order ripped apart. The narrator surveys a ruined world and resolves to clear out the rot — the leeches and toadies draining people dry. Torching the fields and levelling everything, it frames destruction as the only honest answer to a rigged, divisive system.","sonnet":"Rage at parasites and sycophants who transcend the wreckage they create while others fight for scraps — the fabric of society torn, resistance buried like a secret, the only option left to cut the limb, torch the fields where their seeds are sewn. 'We remove the deadwood' is both execution and ecological necessity.","src":"haiku"},
"sylosis~immovable-stone":{"haiku":"A person refuses to break or submit to external pressure, finding strength in internal contentment and resilience through hardship.","opus":"A resolute anthem about inner resilience, insisting that anything which lasts cannot be broken and that every crack only hardens what remains. The narrator wants no power or status, only to hold his ground, claiming that peace comes solely from within. Sylosis, the British melodic thrash band led by Josh Middleton, deliver this stoicism over technical, driving riffs.","sonnet":"Stoic self-sufficiency as the only philosophy worth holding — fractures strengthening rather than shattering, contentment earned from within, no interest in thrones or dominance. When the body is eventually gone, the ashes can be scattered; what mattered was the refusal to bend, not the outcome.","src":"haiku"},
"sylvaine~nowhere-still-somewhere":{"haiku":"Someone floats untethered from reality and ground, caught between worlds while struggling to control their dissolution.","opus":"A weightless account of dissociation and drift, the narrator lifting off the ground, suspended between here and elsewhere, breathless and brittle as she tries and fails to control a reality that slips through her grasp. Being nowhere yet somewhere captures the terror of disconnection. Sylvaine, the Norwegian solo project of Kathrine Shepard, renders this fragile, floating alienation through her signature blend of ethereal atmosphere and blackgaze weight.","sonnet":"Dissociation described from inside it — floating between here and there without landing, reaching for solid ground and lifting off instead, the gap between what is and what should be widening into breathlessness. The word nowhere keeps meeting somewhere and neither wins; the speaker is permanently between them.","src":"haiku"},
"sylvan~bitter-symphony":{"haiku":"Someone searches and calls but finds absence and indifference; sorrow and a bitter symphony invade the speaker's life.","opus":"A hushed act of remorse in which the narrator lowers the lights and lets grief settle over him while facing the person he neglected. Only now, too late, does he grasp the private struggles they endured without him. Sylvan, a German neo-progressive rock act, wrap the confession in lush, emotionally shaded arrangements.","sonnet":"A short, still reckoning with emotional neglect — the other person searched and called and was answered with absence, the signs read too late, the fights fought alone. What remains is sorrow taking up residence and the first quiet acknowledgment that something was lost by not showing up.","src":"haiku"},
"symphony-x~serpent-s-kiss":{"haiku":"Corruption and seduction weaponize intimacy as the speaker embodies deception and devours hope; trust becomes trap and love becomes predation.","opus":"Sung from the serpent's own mouth, a seductive vow of corruption and destruction that promises freedom while delivering ruin. Symphony X give temptation a swaggering voice, evil dressed as liberation, the whole world its willing stage.","sonnet":"A serpentine, corrupting force speaks directly to a victim — offering false freedom while feeding on their submission, decaying them from within, and revelling in the power it holds over a world that worships and fears it.","src":"haiku"},
"syora~serenity":{"haiku":"Constancy dissolves as everything shifts and slips beyond the narrator's grasp while they attempt to hold what cannot remain. The song mourns impermanence and the cold awareness that nothing persists.","opus":"Waiting anxiously on someone who may not care, feeling the self slip away from the present into nowhere as a cold blight spreads from that person. The recurring fear is impermanence, the dread of remaining alive and alone in a world grown cold once nothing stays the same.","sonnet":"A slow dissolution from present into nowhere — the speaker waits for relief that never comes, watches themselves going down while being emphatic and still dropping, and concludes by asking what it means to remain in a world this cold, where nothing ever stays the same.","src":"haiku"},
"system-of-a-down~36":{"haiku":"Television and media consumption trap people in endless cycles of aging and conformity, questioning whether individual autonomy can survive.","opus":"A blunt indictment of a life boiled down to media spectacle and a sprint toward the grave, asking whether you can escape the pattern and set your own tempo. Its flat pronouncement that existence itself is pointless twists the whole thing into a nihilistic jab at passive, herd-like living.","sonnet":"A brief, blunt indictment of television as a trap that keeps people aging inside a race they didn't choose — it asks whether you can break out and live at your own pace, suggesting most people can't or won't.","src":"haiku","fable":"Under a minute on Steal This Album!, this is agitprop compressed to a nerve: the screen is named a shame, then aging gets recast as a rigged sprint you are losing. The looping question is whether you can bolt from that track and set your own tempo, and the flat bridge verdict, that a whole life can be surplus, is the wound the interrogation keeps circling back to, so that by the outro only the demand is left standing, a challenge with no comforting reply attached.","fv":2,"themes":["freedom & escape","alienation & emptiness","politics & society"],"means":"the looping question of escape collapses into a flat verdict of surplus life","built":"the outro strips the song to a bare dare with no comforting reply attached"},
"system-of-a-down~a-d-d":{"haiku":"Empire built on hypocrisy collapses when its architects become victims: sent to die for lies while power mourns its own losses.","opus":"Fury aimed at a government that squanders soldiers as expendable and lies to citizens for corporate gain, flatly rejecting the elites' world of hoarded wealth. Images of handcuffed men and a stolen national ideal ground the anger.","sonnet":"A song about soldiers used and discarded by governments that manipulate public perception — veterans are sent back in body parts while the powerful conduct ego-driven power struggles at their expense, and no flag is big enough to cover that shame.","src":"haiku","fable":"A soldier's-eye accounting of a nation that ships its young out whole and receives them back in pieces, the verses laying down a ledger where patriotic sacrifice is always paid and never repaid. The parenthetical subtitle reframes the founding promise itself as a swindle: what gets denied is not the fighting but the ideal the fighting was sold to defend, and the band stages that collapse by letting the phrase disintegrate across each repeat, so the country's guiding vision audibly loses its own words before it can finish saying them. Structurally the track alternates between the wounded testimony and a jeering, obscene secession from a world of corporate spoils and cheap luxury, the coarsening from one curse to a filthier one marking the instant grief calcifies into contempt. The spoken bridge diagnoses the machinery in flat clinical language, an unseen hand tampering with what the public is permitted to see and hear until agreement is manufactured, the shackled figure standing in for a disgrace no banner is broad enough to hide. Cut in the flag-drunk year after September 2001 and exiled from the major records onto Steal This Album!, it is protest as depletion rather than rally, its closing chant stuttering into a broken loop that will not resolve because, for the singer, nothing has.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","war & battle","anger & defiance"],"means":"patriotic sacrifice rendered as a ledger always paid and never repaid","built":"the founding ideal audibly loses its own words across each repeat of the phrase"},
"system-of-a-down~aerials":{"haiku":"Life flows inevitably forward like a waterfall, with humans swept along in cycles of wanting without commitment. The song invites mental transcendence through surrender and freedom.","opus":"A near-mystical plea to dissolve the small ego, life imagined as a river we rejoin after the fall. The band trades their usual political rage for something spiritual, freedom found by losing the self that clings and competes.","sonnet":"The song describes life as a continuous flow — a waterfall and a river — through which people move, lose themselves, and find something larger than the individual self. The \"aerials\" in the sky represent the freedom gained when the small, ego-driven mind is released.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song casts a single existence as water that plunges, scatters, and reforms downstream, so that dissolving into the flow becomes the only way to keep everything; the paradox it names is a will that craves motion and choice yet refuses to stand still or ever be beaten. The two verses stage that clenching against the current: where the first still trusts the river and lets itself be carried, the second drinks and immediately raises barriers, the grasping self reasserting itself the instant it is fed. Against it the refrain proposes the opposite bargain, that shrinking the possessive mind is what opens a life and unbars sight toward a reward beyond bodily time. Fittingly the piece abandons language at the close, dissolving into a wordless rising chant that settles on a single meditative syllable, so a band better known for fury lets the surrender its words could only argue for simply happen.","fv":2,"fnote":"The album doesn’t end where the tracklist says: an unlisted Armenian liturgical hymn follows Aerials, nicknamed Arto for duduk player Arto Tunçboyacıyan until Malakian confirmed in April 2026 its true title — Der Voghormia, Lord Have Mercy — sung for the Genocide’s 1.5 million.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"shrinking the possessive self as the only way to open a life","built":"the song abandons language at the close, enacting surrender it could only argue for"},
"system-of-a-down~attack":{"haiku":"Collective resistance rises against oppressive systems, propaganda, and violence inflicted on civilians. The song calls for unified assault against servitude and environmental destruction.","opus":"A rallying cry against propaganda, militarism and the machinery that reduces people to obedient servitude, building toward an attack on the institutions themselves. The band's political fury aimed at systems that bomb homes and call it order.","sonnet":"The song calls for collective uprising against propaganda, colonial violence, and the destruction of homes, schools, and hospitals by state power. It frames the coming attack as a necessary response to years of manufactured compliance and the human cost of military aggression.","src":"haiku","fable":"Opening Hypnotize, this is a war anthem that keeps flipping which end of the gun the listener stands on. It begins in something like tenderness, a shared breath and a vow that nobody falls without dragging everyone down, then hardens as steel and industry hollow that feeling into obedience. The word chanted over and over first sounds like an uprising against being farmed for someone else's ends, yet by the close the same chant is doing the leveling, running down the ordinary places where people live, learn, and heal. Serj lets the refrain rot from revolt into invasion until you cannot tell the liberator from the occupier, and that collapse is the whole point. The mask is protest; underneath sits the recognition that violence borrows one triumphant grammar no matter whose hand is on the trigger.","fv":2,"themes":["war & battle","politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"a chant of revolt rotting into the language of invasion","built":"the refrain is indistinguishable from liberator and occupier by the close"},
"system-of-a-down~atwa":{"haiku":"A person feels disregarded and emotionally absent, watching the world pass by while disconnected from care, voice, and feeling. Through repetition, the song emphasizes progressive emotional death and dissociation.","opus":"The speaker feels numb and disconnected, watching the whole world pass him by with nothing to gain or lose, repeating that no one cares how he feels and that he no longer feels, sees, hears, sleeps, eats or lives.","sonnet":"A voice describes total emotional shutdown — no longer seeing, hearing, speaking, sleeping, eating, or feeling — while watching a world that does not care pass by.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is Charles Manson’s own acronym for Air Trees Water Animals, and the band's provocation is to voice him not as a monster but as an incarcerated environmental prophet, someone who sees the whole world passing him by from behind a wall while a public that fears him refuses to care how he feels. Serj sings it as slow, aching lullaby rather than accusation, so the sympathy is genuinely unsettling: the man everyone agrees to hate becomes a mirror for a numbed planet. The song's real argument lives in its structure, the post-choruses running down a checklist of shutting senses, first see, hear, speak, then sleep, eat, live, each stage of not-feeling-it-anymore a further withdrawal from a world that stopped listening. By the end the numbness has spread past the speaker to the earth he claims to speak for, which is the point of the mask System put on.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","nature & the elements","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"sympathy extended to a universally hated figure as a mirror for collective numbness","built":"post-choruses run a checklist shutting senses in sequence until numbness spreads past the speaker to the earth itself"},
"system-of-a-down~b-y-o-b":{"haiku":"A fierce indictment of war: wealthy politicians never send their own children to fight, yet always send the poor and powerless to die for their interests.","opus":"A protest against war and political hypocrisy, repeatedly demanding to know why presidents never fight the wars they start and always send the poor to die, set against a mocking party refrain.","sonnet":"The song is a furious anti-war protest that asks why it is always the poor and working class who are sent to fight while presidents and the powerful stay home. Amid surreal imagery of dancing in the desert and blowing up sunshine, the central accusation is that wars are launched for hypocritical, self-serving reasons.","src":"haiku","fable":"An anti-Iraq-War anthem built as a deliberate collision of two voices: Serj Tankian's verses stack a delirious, alliterative montage where the machinery of empire hides in plain sight, invading soldiers are recast as revelers at a party in the sand, and oil-slicked hangars weep the word freedom even as the same lie keeps getting served from the tablecloth of power. Against that dense irony Daron Malakian's blunt refrain strips the euphemism away and asks the one question the party is designed to drown out, that the ones who declare wars are never the ones sent to die in them, and that the bodies fed to the machine are always the poor. The title's frat-party joke, bring your own bombs, is the whole mechanism in miniature: consumerist celebration as the mask over conscription and slaughter, so the manic la-la chorus and the closing chant of that unanswerable question are two sides of the same accusation.","fv":2,"themes":["war & battle","politics & society","party & hedonism"],"means":"party euphemism as the mask over conscription and class slaughter","built":"the frat-party title pun — bring your own bombs — is the mechanism in miniature"},
"system-of-a-down~boom":{"haiku":"War and consumerism kill each child of God through bombs and corporate manipulation, while billions starve as weapons proliferate globally.","opus":"Written for an anti-war protest, this is a direct indictment of militarism and consumerism, weighing children lost to starvation against fortunes spent on bombs. Its outrage is that manufactured consent makes mass death feel normal.","sonnet":"A direct anti-war polemic: consumerism, manufactured consent, and corporate globalization are the backdrop to a world where billions are spent on bombs while thousands of children starve every hour.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track argues by inversion. Its opening verse strolls a glassy street where screens have trained everyone to look away, where fake lawns and doctored images sell a numbed contentment, and only after diagnosing that anesthesia does the second verse trace the same money to the arms ledger, weighing a running count of small bodies lost to famine against the fortunes poured into ordnance. The four-count refrain refuses to editorialize and simply becomes the noise of the blast itself, landing each cycle as the event rather than a comment on it, which is why Serj Tankian’s flat accusation that every blast murders the divinity a child has just brought into being cuts deeper than any raised voice could. The lone bridge strips the tirade to one unanswered question about a species aiming its arsenal inward, and because Michael Moore shot the video amid the February 2003 marches, the detonation reads not as figure of speech but as the literal thing those crowds had gathered to stop.","fv":2,"fnote":"When the invasion it protested actually began, MTV Europe circulated a memo pulling war-imagery videos from rotation and Boom! made the list of eleven singled out — the Michael Moore protest film, cut from the February 15 marches, screened out by the same televised looking-away the first verse diagnoses.","themes":["politics & society","war & battle","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the blast counted as event rather than comment on itself","built":"the four-count refrain becomes the detonation not an editorial on it"},
"system-of-a-down~bounce":{"haiku":"Playful narrative about sexual pursuit and group participation, using pogo and parodic imagery to explore desire and anonymous encounters.","opus":"A deliberately juvenile romp built on a pogo-stick double entendre, the one stick passed around a group as barely-veiled group sex. The absurd mosh-pit chant makes the crude joke the whole point.","sonnet":"A lewdly coded story about a group sexual encounter — the pogo stick a barely-disguised prop — framed as a playground romp but nakedly about casual sex with a promiscuous woman and a crowd of willing participants.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sub-two-minute novelty tucked into Toxicity, built as a playground chant of one-word commands so the whole thing reads at first as pure kids-party silliness. But the innuendo is barely buried: the single pogo stick a whole party lines up to take turns on is an orgy joke told entirely in toy vocabulary, and the bridge drops the pretense just long enough to make the reading unmistakable. The genius of it is that the mask and the meaning are the same words — Tankian never has to leer, because the innocent surface and the filthy subtext are literally identical text, the relentless bouncing rhythm miming the act while pretending to mean nothing but jumping.","fv":2,"fnote":"Tankian’s original pitch was a heavy metal song about silk pajamas — the Dalai Lama’s, Elton John’s, a chanted PJ-PJ-PJ chorus — until the band, in his own telling, refused to be made to look like that.","themes":["comedy & wordplay","party & hedonism"],"means":"orgy joke told entirely in toy vocabulary without ever leering","built":"innocent surface and filthy subtext are literally identical text"},
"system-of-a-down~bubbles":{"haiku":"Life lived inside isolated illusions (bubbles) where power struggles render people armless and helpless despite belief and compliance, with bad men controlling the narrative.","opus":"A cryptic warning about living sealed inside a comfortable illusion while a power struggle rages, leaving people disarmed and complicit. The insistent reminders push against the lie that the sheltered life is safe or free.","sonnet":"People living inside a controlled, artificial reality — wearing crowns, chasing clowns, kneeling in power struggles — are warned that bad men have always told the lie that keeps them disarmed; the bubble jungle is civilization itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Steal This Album! deep cut that stages captivity as a sealed terrarium: creatures suspended where they ought to sink, a court where the crown and the chased fool keep swapping faces, everyone breathing recycled air and mistaking it for a world. The verses hold a wry half-smile, refusing to let the horror land, while the chorus tightens into a cycle of watching, then bowing, then flattering whoever sits on top, until the loyal are disarmed and pinned inside a contest that was never winnable by force. Serj Tankian's bridge drops the irony for pleading, hammering one question at the listener again and again as the arrangement thins, the sheer repetition becoming the argument that someone with authority already sold everyone the comforting version of events. What the refusal to speak plainly performs is exactly the condition it names: a mind that can only pace its enclosure and demand you not forget it is one.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"complicity maintained by breathing recycled air as truth","built":"the song's refusal to speak plainly performs the enclosure it names"},
"system-of-a-down~chic-n-stu":{"haiku":"Advertising and consumer culture drive compulsive buying behavior and force people into therapy to cope with manufactured desire. Society is enslaved to endless consumption despite its emptiness.","opus":"SOAD skewer consumer culture by fusing a pizza jingle with the frenzy of advertising, the relentless command to buy driving people to need therapy, a satirical takedown of manufactured desire disguised as an ad.","sonnet":"A satirical attack on advertising culture — using pizza as the absurd vessel, the song argues that constant buy-buy-buy messaging has made people psychologically damaged and in need of therapy. It's comic in delivery but the critique of consumer manipulation is sincere.","src":"haiku","fable":"System of a Down turn a jingle inside out: the verses ape the manic cadence of a television commercial, a food product hawked with cartoonish appetite while a metronome command to purchase ticks off every passing second, so that consumption is scored to the clock itself. The genius is that the song never argues against advertising from outside; it becomes the ad, mimicking the hard-sell rhythm until the seams show. Only in the chorus and bridge does the diagnosis surface, collapsing cause and cure into one breath, the saturation being sold as the very sickness that leaves you needing treatment and on the run from it. The absurd domestic intro, a ballgame idling in the fridge while the butter hardens, sets the tone of a mind glazed and half-narcotized by ambient media, and the escalating repetition enacts exactly the numbing it describes, the pitch looping past sense into pure conditioned reflex.","fv":2,"fnote":"The record it opens exists because these songs escaped: they leaked online as a bootleg dubbed Toxicity II, the band finished them and shipped the official answer dressed as the crime — a plain disc scrawled in marker like a burned CD-R, titled after Abbie Hoffman’s Steal This Book, with four pressings hand-drawn by the four members.","themes":["politics & society","artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the ad rhythm enacted until the seams show and the saturation is the sickness","built":"escalating repetition enacts the numbing it diagnoses, pitch looping into conditioned reflex"},
"system-of-a-down~chop-suey":{"haiku":"A song about hiding pain and despair beneath a facade. The singer confronts someone about suicide and spiritual disconnection, asking why they hide their scars and questioning if angels should die. It invokes abandonment by a father figure.","opus":"SOAD's cryptic jolt is really a song about suicide and the way everyone around it recoils — makeup and hidden scars masking a private collapse, a chorus that keeps pinning the act back on the person breaking under it. Its shock is theological: it likens that abandonment to the crucifixion, casting the self-destroying as forsaken the way Christ cried out that he was.","sonnet":"The song moves between a bitter portrait of someone covering up self-destruction with appearances, and a cry of abandonment addressed to God — asking why he has been forsaken — framing suicide and death as a \"self-righteous\" act that the narrator both condemns and mourns.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that mimics the way a person talks themselves through a morning while quietly falling apart: the cosmetic ritual of covering wounds and performing normalcy is turned into an accusation, each domestic action pinned back on the listener as something they secretly chose. The title itself chops the word suicide in half like the dish — the same casual dismissal the song accuses — and the bridge lifts Christ's last words from the cross, reframing an ordinary overdose as an abandonment by God. The whiplash between the demure verse and the screamed martyrdom is the point: the culture that trains someone to paint over their damage is the same one that will brand their death as sanctimonious.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"ordinary overdose reframed as theological abandonment, culture indicted as cause","built":"the title chops the word suicide in half, enacting the casual dismissal it accuses"},
"system-of-a-down~cigaro":{"haiku":"Ironic commentary on arrogance, power, and deregulation. The song uses crude boasting and metaphor to satirize institutional hypocrisy and resource exploitation disguised as confidence.","opus":"Crude phallic bragging that turns out to be a satire of power itself, mapping macho one-upmanship onto the executives and states that deregulate, exploit, and torch the planet's resources, then hide from the wreckage they call genocide.","sonnet":"Using crude phallic posturing as a vehicle for satire, the song portrays those in power as arrogant, self-satisfied regulators who deny any wrongdoing while driving genocide and burning through the world's resources. The swagger is the point: it mimics the delusion of the powerful.","src":"haiku","fable":"A parade of playground boasts (mine's bigger, mine reeks worse) escalated into geopolitics: the same puffed-chest logic that makes a man measure himself against another is the logic that lets the powerful strip the planet and vanish. The mask is a bragging cartoon strongman; underneath, the bridge names the mechanism plainly — deregulators who deregulate, the self-cancelling verbs of institutions that exist only to undo their own stated purpose. The obscene chorus and the scorched-earth pre-chorus are the same appetite drawn at two scales.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"playground boasting escalated to the scale of planetary plunder","built":"the obscene chorus and the scorched-earth pre-chorus are the same appetite drawn at two scales"},
"system-of-a-down~cubert":{"haiku":"Clichéd mass society traps individuals in prefabricated existence where people are commodified organs and dares repeat without consequence.","opus":"A jittery, surreal snapshot of mass-media passivity, an audience canned and clichéd, told to stay in its seats while it consumes. Beneath the nonsense imagery is contempt for a public that cannot dare to act.","sonnet":"Humans are treated as canned, interchangeable commodities — organs stacked like popcorn, denied the courage to dare or deviate — a sardonic snapshot of mass conformity and passive consumption.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is a noise-collage spasm on SOAD's debut, over inside two minutes, and its meaning lives in the gap between its two choruses. The verses stage a cinema — a preflight seat-belt calm, an instruction to stay put — that turns the listener into a passive spectator; then Tankian swaps a single word so that the packaged, tinned foodstuff of the first chorus becomes people themselves in the second, the audience revealed as the product being consumed. The refrain's paralysis, the same held final word chanted to exhaustion, is the whole indictment: a mass-produced crowd, organs harvested and canned like snack food, too processed and identical to risk anything — the band's early anti-conformist snarl compressed into a burst of deliberate agitation.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control"],"means":"the audience revealed as the product being canned and consumed","built":"a single word swap between choruses turns tinned food into the people watching"},
"system-of-a-down~darts":{"haiku":"Ancient deities and dangerous forces threaten human life while people navigate precarious existential spaces, invoking gods and confronting human vulnerability.","opus":"A cryptic, surreal chant that summons old Mesopotamian deities and reckless, deadly ways of living, pleading to stay put in one spot while desire hurls its projectiles, and asking over and over whether the listener even qualifies as a person.","sonnet":"Fragmented and surreal, the song moves through images of threatening darts, clashing archetypes (hitman, nun, lovers), and Sumerian god-names, arriving at a question about humanity that admits no answer — deliberately resisting coherent narrative.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cornered plea to be left alone as unseen projectiles streak past the singer's wants opens onto the song's real obsession: an archaic, accusing question of whether the addressed thing is even a human being at all, hammered until it stops being a question and becomes a verdict. The Sumerian names anchor the strangeness precisely rather than decoratively: Ninti, the Sumerian lady of life whose name a scribal pun also turned into lady of the rib, and Ishkur, the storm god, are invoked as models of resurrection, so the demand to arise sits against a warning not to fear or long for these sky-gods who fail and lose their way. That contradiction is the song's engine: it worships and debunks divinity in the same breath, its lovers drawn from the assassin's trade and the convent, sanctity and violence made interchangeable masks over one drive. Serj Tankian pushes it to a deliberate breakdown at the end, where the mocking motherfucker collapses into a stuttered, dismantled refusal to know anything at all. What resists reading here is doing the work on purpose: the certainty of the accusation is hollowed out by an ending that confesses it has no answer.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","identity & becoming","violence & murder"],"means":"divinity worshipped and debunked in the same breath, sanctity and violence interchangeable","built":"the song's mocking certainty is hollowed by an ending that stutters into confessed ignorance"},
"system-of-a-down~ddevil":{"haiku":"Plagiarism and theft plague human creation; intelligent people outsmart others and themselves in feeding frenzies where evil seduces.","opus":"An early SOAD burst of surreal free-association mocking plagiarism, stupidity and self-consuming media frenzy, landing on the seductive banality of evil; the devil is lovely precisely because it's ordinary and everywhere.","sonnet":"A fragmented SOAD provocation that skips between observations about plagiarism, consumerism, and cognitive self-destruction — the throughline is the seductive appeal of the devil as a symbol for spectacle and the feeding frenzy of modern culture.","src":"haiku","fable":"Early SOAD scattershot: Serj Tankian free-associates puns and cultural debris (a schoolyard taunt hurled at Shakespeare, borrowed authorship, appliance-loud nonsense) so the verses deliberately refuse to add up, mimicking a culture too saturated to think in a straight line. The one thread under the noise is a study of cleverness turning cannibal — wit that first devours its rivals and then the witty, an ugliness dressed as seductive, a downward pull someone keeps begging to be taken deeper into. The escalating tail of each verse does the real work: outward aggression collapses into self-consumption, and by the last verse the singer is stranded alone, the plea to be led below answered by isolation rather than the glamour he was promised.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"wit that first devours rivals then turns cannibal on the witty","built":"verses that refuse to add up mimic a culture too saturated to think straight"},
"system-of-a-down~deer-dance":{"haiku":"Police brutality and riot suppression target peaceful protesters advocating for justice and change. The song critiques militarized force used against the vulnerable and disempowered.","opus":"A firsthand indictment of police brutality, written about the riot squads outside the 2000 Democratic convention at the Staples Center. It sets peaceful protesters against armored, weaponized authority, refusing neutrality on a system rigged toward force.","sonnet":"The song condemns police violence against peaceful protesters, depicting riot police with rubber bullets and batons suppressing crowds of young people near the Staples Center in Los Angeles. It frames the state's force as bullying of the weak and calls for resistance against neutrality in the face of injustice.","src":"haiku","fable":"A documentary snapshot of the August 2000 Democratic convention in Los Angeles, where officers cleared a concert crowd outside the downtown arena with batons and projectiles, turned into a song about brutality disguised as good manners. Serj Tankian keeps welding two vocabularies that should never touch, the cheerful patter of a store clerk laid over armored ranks and their firearms, so the horror lands in the gap between the friendly tone and the fists behind it. That surface politeness is the mask; underneath, the stadium becomes a lens onto a whole exhausted country, and the choral hammering about the strong bullying the helpless narrows into an outro that repeats its accusation until it stops being a complaint and hardens into a fact of the landscape. The one hopeful note, a small boy's reassurance that everything will turn out fine, arrives already sounding like a lie, which is exactly the point: the band hands you the comfort and withholds any reason to trust it.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"store-clerk cheerfulness laid over armored ranks and their firearms","built":"a small boy's comfort arrives already sounding like a lie"},
"system-of-a-down~dreaming":{"haiku":"The song critiques corporate greed and privatized human rights, depicting indigenous suffering and cultural loss. Dreamers scream in nightmares about consumer culture and dying populations.","opus":"SOAD's fractured collage of war atrocity and consumer dystopia, splicing bottled-water privatization and dying populations against a woman losing her mind; the dream is really a scream, sanity buckling under a world reduced to commodified suffering.","sonnet":"A layered attack on corporate privatization of human rights — specifically water access — woven against an unraveling personal narrative of someone losing their mind, with the political and emotional collapse mirroring each other.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about how the polished language of global commerce anesthetizes atrocity until a single mind cracks under the weight of seeing it clearly. Tankian's verses jam corporate and humanitarian vocabulary into a slurring loop, while the whispered asides underneath name the real cost: a generation selling bottled water back to populations left to die. The chorus indicts a figure who reached some vantage of total clarity and was destroyed by the seeing, and the bridge collapses that public dread into one private breakdown, a plea to be knocked loose from an unstoppable inner voice. The framing image of a trophy mounted on the wall brackets everything in the perpetrator’s voice, atrocity reduced to décor you can hang up and admire.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","madness & the mind","war & battle"],"means":"corporate language anesthetizing atrocity until one mind cracks under the seeing","built":"the trophy-on-the-wall frame casts the perpetrator as narrator throughout"},
"system-of-a-down~ego-brain":{"haiku":"The ego's shame-based nature drives human conflict over land and survival, yet love ultimately prevails over human pain and dissolution.","opus":"A meditation on how ego and man-made shame corrupt love, framing human conflict over land as fleeting against a life that dissolves like sand. Beneath the noise it reaches for the idea that love outlasts the fall.","sonnet":"The ego as the source of all human suffering — men fight over land, the world falls apart, and what sustains us through the collapse is the fragile declaration that love persists in spite of everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A miniature about the self as engine of ruin, built so its architecture carries the argument. The dreamy, near-lullaby verses shrink a body down to drifting water and crumbling grit, a fistful of dust that still finds the energy to fight its neighbors over territory; against that erosion the chorus vaults up and answers apocalypse with a single gesture of contact, a hand held in the moment of collapse, insisting tenderness outlasts the wreckage. Between them sits a clipped two-line pre-chorus naming vanity and an inherited disgrace passed down like a birthright, the accusation delivered as a chant rather than an argument. Tankian and Malakian trade these registers so the reflective and the indicting voices read as one divided mind, and the whole three-part cycle simply loops back untouched, which is the point: the closing return to that washed-clean image lands less as resolution than as a brain stuck rehearsing its one consoling promise — on Steal This Album!, a hymn’s ache hidden inside a deliberately fragmentary sketch.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","shame & repression","love & desire"],"means":"tenderness offered as the one gesture that outlasts the wreckage","built":"the three-part cycle loops back unchanged, a brain rehearsing its one consoling promise"},
"system-of-a-down~forest":{"haiku":"A manipulative figure controls and shapes another through denial and false promises, demanding surrender while claiming to be one's true nature and mind.","opus":"A creator or god-figure beckons humanity, its child, deeper into denial, insisting the two are one and the same. The bitter turn comes when the address surveys the weapons and folly people have produced, reframing the whole seduction as a maker confronting his own flawed reflection and binding his creation to a grim fate.","sonnet":"A cryptic allegory about creation, delusion, or propaganda — a commanding voice leads a child or subordinate through a forest of denial, insisting they are the same being, while the final stanza hints at manufactured realities and weapons built for collective self-destruction.","src":"haiku","fable":"A creator addresses his creation with a possessiveness that curdles from tenderness into control, the parent-voice insisting the child is merely an extension of the parent's own mind and owes an inescapable promise. Read as Serj's Old-Testament framing, the speaker is a God who leads humanity into a wilderness of self-deception and then indicts it, the bridge swerving abruptly to circumcision and manufactured weapons to reveal what the species actually did with its divine inheritance. The trap is total: to be told you ARE your maker is to be denied any exit, and the invitation to come inside is the door of a cage.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"creator traps creation by insisting the two share one mind","built":"the bridge swerves to circumcision and weapons to indict the inheritance"},
"system-of-a-down~friik":{"haiku":"Social rejection and outsider status; being labeled a freak for not conforming to normalcy.","opus":"A frantic self-diagnosis of a mind caught between contradictory extremes, spiraling through infection, sleeplessness, and hysteria toward a terror of total breakdown. Nightmarish imagery mirrors the loss of a stable grip on sanity. From System of a Down's early work, it captures the Armenian-American band's manic, lurching chaos at its most unhinged.","sonnet":"A mind at war with itself — too aware to be numb, too feeling to be functional — spiralling through surreal imagery of burning oceans and melting faces as the boundary between sanity and infection dissolves. The final admission drops all bravado: control was always an illusion, the head was never in charge.","src":"haiku","fable":"Friik stages a diagnosis of the self as a catalog of contradictions: the narrator can only define himself by what he's too much of to belong anywhere, too seeing for blindness, too warring for peace, too lucid for the madness he keeps circling. The chorus liquefies the world into oceans that catch fire and faces that run like wax, a surrealism that isn't decoration but symptom, the way perception dissolves when the brain floods. Between those meltdowns sits a plea to shut off the fever in his skull and paddle home to some workable normalcy he never quite reaches. The bridge names the wreckage bluntly, three medical terms fired like an alarm, before the outro drops the mask entirely and admits the thing every earlier contradiction was hedging around: control was never his.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"self-defined only by what makes belonging impossible","built":"surreal chorus imagery as symptom of a flooding brain not decoration"},
"system-of-a-down~fuck-the-system":{"haiku":"A chant rejecting institutional systems and authority, with the speaker demeaning conformity and insisting on collective rebellion against social control.","opus":"A blunt, stuttering call to revolt from the perspective of the overlooked man at the back, turning his own smallness and shame into collective refusal; the point is less coherent argument than the raw impulse to reject authority outright.","sonnet":"A fractured, incantatory rejection of the system: the speaker identifies as an undermining presence in the back of the crowd, demeaning the pack, and the repeated war-cry collapses into a shared imperative for everyone to rebel against the structures that contain them.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's grievance is its own vanishing point: three verses of stuttered syllables and scat filler ring the same shouted refrain, which decays each time into a clipped fragment of itself until it cannot even finish the last word of the target it is cursing. Serj Tankian keeps casting the speaker downward as the figure loitering at the rear, the one talking his own pack into the dirt, so the revolt is voiced from powerlessness, a threat with no plan and no object it can name. What begins as one man's need spreads outward in the closing lines from a single self to a listener to a collective everyone, making rage look like solidarity precisely because it has been hollowed of any specific demand; on Steal This Album! this is System of a Down miming a protest anthem while stripping it to pure adrenaline and slogan, letting the emptiness stand as the joke and the diagnosis at once.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","artifice & performance"],"means":"revolt voiced from powerlessness, a threat with no plan and no nameable object","built":"the refrain decays each repetition into a clipped fragment until it cannot finish its own last word"},
"system-of-a-down~genocidal-humanoidz":{"haiku":"Society's institutional violence and militarism breed destructive beings who spread warfare to their children without resistance or escape.","opus":"A frenzied anti-war salvo aimed at those who profit from and perpetuate mass killing, casting genocidal figures who teach warfare to their children while the narrator insists he never summoned or hid from the devil he refuses to run from. Persecution is declared over. System of a Down, the Armenian-American band whose founders carry the memory of genocide, deliver this among a rare pair of tracks marking their long recording silence.","sonnet":"A defiant declaration of resistance against cycles of violence — we did not summon the devil, we do not hide from it, and the enemies are the genocidal humanoids who teach warfare to their children. The tone is collective fury at systems that have prosecuted and persecuted rather than protected.","src":"haiku","fable":"A defiant war cry written from the position of a people accused of being the aggressors while they are the ones being exterminated, flipping the language of terror back onto those who wield it. The song was cut during the 2020 Nagorno-Karabakh war and the whole SOAD project of it is Armenian grief turned outward, so the devil the narrator refuses to run from, summon, or hide from is the charge of provocation itself; the mantra insists the resistance was never the origin of the violence. The chorus's real venom lands on the institutions that were meant to protect and instead sold out, prosecutors turned prostitutes. Serj's dinner-guest bridge names the enemy plainly as genocidal humanoids raising children into warfare, and the read the song keeps circling is that the persecution stops only because the persecuted outlast it, hence the refusal repeated until it becomes the whole structure.","fv":2,"themes":["war & battle","politics & society","anger & defiance","exile & diaspora"],"means":"refusal repeated until it becomes the entire structure","built":"the persecutors' language of terror flipped back onto those who wield it"},
"system-of-a-down~highway-song":{"haiku":"Someone travels uncertain roads, seeking intimate connection while conscious of time's passage and the irretrievable nature of days spent.","opus":"A hypnotic drift down an endless road where nostalgia curdles into loss, the refrain insisting that our purest days will never return. The highway is time itself, moving whether or not we want it to.","sonnet":"A road song about longing and impermanence — moving forward while forgetting how to be home, the highway itself becoming a kind of companion, and the dawning recognition that the purest things are already gone and won't come back.","src":"haiku","fable":"A late-night drive that slowly dissolves the driver: Serj Tankian's verses report a self loosening its grip, clouds curdling into something unreal, road-signs all leaning the same direction, until forward motion feels indistinguishable from surrender, and the chorus can only offer to steer someone else through the dark. Then the bridge drops the private trance for a chant that keeps circling one thought about vanished, unrecoverable time, twinning fragile living things with the machinery of war and hammering the line until repetition itself becomes the meaning. This is a rare, purely elegiac gesture buried on Steal This Album! (2002), the odds-and-ends set where the band let its wistful, sung side breathe: the outro simply loops the mourning, refusing to resolve, because the whole point is that the road only runs one way and nothing turns back around.","fv":2,"themes":["nostalgia & memory","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"forward motion rendered indistinguishable from surrender to irreversible time","built":"outro loops the mourning without resolving, because the road only runs one way"},
"system-of-a-down~holy-mountains":{"haiku":"The song invokes the Armenian genocide at the River Aras, calling perpetrators liars and murderers while the victims rest on holy mountains in freedom and spiritual restoration.","opus":"A furious lament for the Armenian Genocide, the River Aras marking the border of the homeland and naming the killers who deny their crime. Grief and demand for acknowledgment drive it more than any conventional protest.","sonnet":"A lament and indictment addressed to the perpetrators of the Armenian Genocide — calling them liars, killers, demons, murderers, sodomizers — while the spirits of the dead rest on the mountains and the survivors declare they have learned the perpetrators have no honor, ending with a cry of freedom that is simultaneously defiant and mournful.","src":"haiku","fable":"System of a Down's coded requiem for the Armenian Genocide, its dead returned and restless at the edge of every line. The River Aras that recurs as both accusation and command is the border river of historic Armenia, and the mountainside where the returned are said to rest is Ararat, the sacred peak now on the wrong side of that line; naming them turns a private ethnic grief into liturgy. The pre-chorus works as a curse-litany, hurling a widening list of names at an unnamed perpetrator and ordering him back across the water, while a single detached voice deciding on slaughter renders how genocide is authored by bureaucratic blankness rather than fury. That the chorus can only manage the bare word freedom, chanted flat and unconvinced against the massed accusations, is the point: the read is in the gap between the survivors' claimed liberty and the drowned nation still calling from the mountain.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","exile & diaspora","politics & society"],"means":"freedom chanted flat and unconvinced against the mountain's unanswered accusation","built":"named border river and sacred peak turn ethnic grief into liturgy"},
"system-of-a-down~honey":{"haiku":"Attraction and sweetness laced with danger; seduction hiding something sinister underneath.","opus":"A cryptic, jarring sketch of a young woman named Maria fleeing a father and a life poisoned by money and religion, refusing to marry until she is ready, torn between loving and hating her mother. The eerie babysitter refrain lands like a threat rather than domestic calm. From System of a Down's 1998 debut, it shows the Armenian-American band's early lurching dynamics, tenderness and menace colliding in the same breath.","sonnet":"A collision of domestic rupture and spiritual unease — a woman fleeing stifling dependency, a speaker poisoned by money and the devil of material life, fragments of family tension escalating to a desperate domestic scene. The song refuses linear narrative, instead layering contradictions — love and hate, God and corruption — until the babysitter's arrival punctures everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song's engine is the collision between the babysitter's ordinary evening announcement and a scream underneath it: a child left waiting who never gets picked up, and who twists that abandonment into a warped theology of adulthood. Tankian keeps the domestic frame cheerful and clock-punctual on the surface, then floods the middle with a runaway mother who trades the family for a wider life and the dollar that funds it, a father who has stopped being a provider, and a vow to marry only once he's grown perfect and clean. The bridge's whiplash — adoration and loathing for the mother in one breath — is the giveaway: this is a kid narrating his own neglect, so the closing chant about eight o'clock and the sitter arriving lands less as a reminder than as the terror of being handed off, again, to someone who isn't the parents.","fv":2,"themes":["family & blood","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"a child narrating neglect through a warped theology of adulthood","built":"the cheerful domestic frame floods with abandonment at the bridge's whiplash"},
"system-of-a-down~hypnotize":{"haiku":"Television and media hypnotize populations into consuming products and accepting propaganda as truth, exemplified through Tiananmen Square being reframed as fashion. The speaker is detached from this machinery, waiting for a girl while the world is manipulated.","opus":"SOAD invokes Tiananmen Square to indict how television and propaganda hypnotize and blind, selling us distraction while state violence is disguised. The personal ache of a scared woman losing her country grounds the political outrage.","sonnet":"A critique of media manipulation and political propaganda — the song opens with Tiananmen Square to ask whether people know why they believe what they believe, or whether television and manufactured messaging have hypnotized them into compliance. The domestic thread (waiting for his girl, who is afraid of being taken away) suggests the personal stakes of living under ideological control.","src":"haiku","fable":"A protest song that indicts how mass media anesthetizes political horror, folding the Tiananmen massacre into the same numb consumer stream that sells you everything else. The verses interrogate manufactured images directly, asking whether the crushed protesters were somehow there for fashion, then naming the propaganda that keeps a simple-minded audience blinded. Against that, the chorus collapses into a small private scene of a man parked and idling for someone he loves, and its very ordinariness is the point: this is the hypnotized citizen, tuned out, personal life running as usual while atrocity scrolls past. As the 2005 title track and companion to Mezmerize, it lets that domestic loop repeat and hollow out until the tenderness itself starts to feel like the trance the song is warning against.","fv":2,"fnote":"Malakian told MTV he wrote it while actually sitting in his car waiting for his girlfriend — the chorus is not a symbol of the tuned-out citizen so much as a literal diary entry, which is exactly what lets it work as one.","themes":["politics & society","dissociation & numbness","violence & murder"],"means":"domestic idling offered as the shape of political anaesthesia","built":"the private car-park scene repeats until the tenderness becomes the trance"},
"system-of-a-down~i-e-a-i-a-i-o":{"haiku":"A rush of surreal, punning wordplay: tongue-twisters, alliteration, and absurdist image-streams (Plutonium wedding rings, icicle stretchings, Lois Lane, Jimmy Carter) that prioritize sonic play over narrative sense.","opus":"A deliberately nonsensical cut-up of tabloid trivia, superheroes and tongue-twisters, mocking the overload of American media where random noise gets consumed the same as meaning. The unanswered why underneath is the whole point.","sonnet":"The song strings together absurdist, punning images — tongue-twister lines, surreal pop-culture references, and nonsensical juxtapositions — without a coherent narrative. The lyrics appear deliberately meaningless, functioning as rhythmic wordplay rather than a decipherable subject.","src":"haiku","fable":"The verses are nonsense-by-design: an alliterative tongue-twister, a nonsequitur celebrity roster (Superman's love interest beside a former president), a wink toward crime-drama tropes, all interrupted by that one-word interrogation. The refusal IS the read — the song mimics the syntax of a story while starving it of any actual story, and the vowel-chant chorus completes the joke by reducing a pop hook to raw phonemes. What survives is the demand for meaning itself, the question hurled at a screen that keeps offering the shapes of narrative with nothing inside.","fv":2,"themes":["comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"syntax of a story offered with no actual story inside it","built":"chorus reduces pop hook to raw phonemes completing the joke"},
"system-of-a-down~inner-vision":{"haiku":"A person seeks inner spiritual guidance and connection, yearning to reunite with someone through mystical vision and path-finding.","opus":"Serj Tankian's spiritual quest inward, seeking guidance and a single true path through sacred silence rather than violence. Its playful line about reinventing the bicycle argues renewal is always possible.","sonnet":"A spiritual search — the narrator is looking for something beyond themselves, a sacred stillness that would dissolve the violence inside and point the way to the single true path.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song stacks the whole vocabulary of the spiritual seeker (a pilgrimage away from home, an inner light, a single road that supposedly leads everywhere) against a punchline built to mock it. The verses play the mystic straight, to the edge of parody: a wanderer chasing a face reflected in the constellations, pleading for direction, begging a hushed presence to drain the world of its aggression. Then the hook detonates the quest by insisting you can always begin again simply by re-engineering the bicycle, a machine that already rolls fine, which is the thesis in one absurdist line, that the guru's promise of a route to enlightenment amounts to inventing the wheel twice and calling it revelation. Serj Tankian sings the devotional material with a clean earnestness so the joke lands, and the closing return to that quiet worshipful image, now pared to the bone, leaves satire and sincerity deadlocked, so you cannot tell whether the record is kneeling before the self-help sermon or laughing it out of the room.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"enlightenment skewered as reinventing a wheel that already rolls","built":"closing devotional image stripped bare leaves satire and sincerity deadlocked"},
"system-of-a-down~jet-pilot":{"haiku":"A surreal meditation on introspection and regret, using abstract imagery of horses and flying to explore themes of remorse, observation, and a source of creation or meaning.","opus":"A surreal, compressed war image where a doomed pilot rides toward death with an unsettling smile; Tankian fuses horse and rider, source and remorse, into a dream of witnessing the sky go grey as everything ends.","sonnet":"A surreal, elliptical portrait of a figure — part horse, part pilot — flying over a bay with a mix of wonder and remorse, while a source of all creation laments that everyone fails to look up at the sky before it turns grey.","src":"haiku","fable":"This song's meaning lives entirely in its mutations: the chorus holds fixed while the verses swap one grammatical thread, so the pained, fettered animal of the first passage becomes, in the second, the origin of all creation, remorse sliding into discourse and the private my widening into a cosmic we. Serj Tankian's surreal image-chain, a strung-out and panicked beast fused to the cockpit of a fighter jet with its nerves lit open, hurtling above the water, works as a warning wearing nonsense for camouflage: something wild and terrified strapped into a machine of violence, unable to look ahead at a firmament dimming toward ash. The chill of it is that the flier keeps smiling, serene inside the wiring, while the word-substitutions quietly promote one doomed creature into a stand-in for all of us who fail to watch the light drain out.","fv":2,"fnote":"Armenian fans read it as the Karabakh air war — the grinning pilot as the bomber, the shackled horse as a country with almost no air force — though Tankian has only ever said artists have zero cognizance of what their images will come to mean.","themes":["war & battle","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"something wild and terrified strapped into a machine of violence, smiling","built":"verse-level word substitutions silently promote one doomed creature to a stand-in for everyone"},
"system-of-a-down~kill-rock-n-roll":{"haiku":"Critique of rock music's commodification and death; the murder of authentic expression by commercial interests.","opus":"A squirming confession of guilt, the singer branding himself the worst kind of jerk for wrecking someone's music and flattening the pretty crowd, watching them wither a little more each day. Odd images of chewing grass in the dark cut against the shame. From 2005's Mezmerize, it is the Armenian-American band at their most compact and self-lacerating, a minute of remorse over lurching riffs.","sonnet":"Self-loathing as cultural murder — a speaker confessing that he is the one who killed something vital and can only watch it die in others' eyes, over and over. The repeated chorus is both confession and helpless shrug, the dark humour of the grass-eating refrain sitting alongside genuine guilt without quite resolving either.","src":"haiku","fable":"A murderer's confession in which the victim is rock and roll itself, sung by the very band mourning what it claims to have destroyed. The verses drown in self-disgust, the singer branding himself the vilest man breathing for gunning down beauty and glamour, while the chorus turns strangely tender, watching a beloved wither by the day and stalling mid-sentence on a guilt it never manages to voice. That broken-off line is the engine: the accusation stays unspoken, so the whole thing curdles into a shrug at its own crime. Underneath, the bridge murmurs a nursery-rhyme menace, inviting the doomed creature to graze all it likes while insisting the fatal mishaps only ever strike out of sight, a cheerful permission that is really an alibi. On Hypnotize, it plays as the genre devouring itself and half-apologizing between bites.","fv":2,"fnote":"The victim was literal: Malakian has said he wrote it after running over a rabbit one night — he pulled over and named the dead animal Rock N’ Roll, and the bridge’s grazing invitation is addressed to it, the title’s grand gesture of genre-murder doubling as an apology to roadkill.","themes":["shame & repression","violence & murder","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"confession of destruction that shrugs before it can name its crime","built":"the broken-off accusation stays unspoken, so guilt curdles into alibi"},
"system-of-a-down~know":{"haiku":"Questions the nature of knowledge and reality through surreal imagery, contrasting earthly skepticism with flying and transcendence. The song suggests that conventional understanding fails to reveal truth.","opus":"A surreal, riddling song questioning certainty and knowledge, insisting that people never really know why. Through strange imagery of walls, flying, and death, it repeatedly probes whether one has ever wanted to fly, or to die, framing transcendence as leaving the body behind.","sonnet":"A fragmented, surrealist meditation on spiritual seeking, the unreliability of knowledge, and the contradictory impulses toward flight, death, and transcendence — the repeated refrain that \"you never think you know why\" frames existence as essentially unknowable.","src":"haiku","fable":"The bridge is the interpretive key that reframes everything before it: flight, it turns out, is only possible once the body is abandoned, so ascent and death collapse into a single crossing the song keeps circling. The verses pile up surreal, half-apocalyptic images over a scorched landscape — mouths that function as windows, a sheep straying from the herd only to be reborn as a bird that can finally soar and finally die — while the chorus needles at certainty itself, hammering its own verb until it mocks the presumption that anyone grasps the why. Set against books and doctrines that each claim a different answer, the track gnaws at the gap between what belief systems assert and what no one actually comprehends, a preoccupation Serj Tankian would carry into his more overtly spiritual and political later work. The result is a descent-into-ascent structure: grotesque, profane spectacle in the verses giving way to a bridge that treats suicidal despair and mystical release as two names for the same escape.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","suicide","faith & the occult"],"means":"ascent and death collapsing into one crossing the song keeps circling","built":"the bridge reframes all prior imagery so flight is only possible after the body is abandoned"},
"system-of-a-down~lonely-day":{"haiku":"A single day of unbearable loneliness and emotional devastation stands as the worst the speaker has experienced.","opus":"A bleak reflection on the single most isolating stretch of a life, so unbearable it ought to be outlawed and yet somehow endured. The attachment to one particular person, wanting to leave and perish alongside them, suggests the isolation grows from a deep loss or terror of losing them.","sonnet":"The most crushingly isolated day imaginable, so bad it should be illegal — the narrator is so bonded to someone that they would follow them into death rather than face the loneliness of being left.","src":"haiku","fable":"A near-motionless dirge that circles one broken superlative, the most loneliest, until pure isolation turns out to be something survived rather than merely suffered. The repetition is the meaning: Malakian keeps returning to the same worn phrase because grief has no vocabulary past it, and the ungrammatical excess rings truer than any correct word. Only the bridge lets another person in, and the outro quietly reframes the whole ordeal not as a day endured but as one he is glad to have lived through.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"isolation survived through the excess of one broken superlative","built":"the ungrammatical double superlative rings truer than any correct word could"},
"system-of-a-down~lost-in-hollywood":{"haiku":"A warning to a naive artist to avoid Hollywood's predatory culture of exploitation and betrayal disguised as opportunity and success.","opus":"A caution to a wide-eyed newcomer warned off ever setting foot in or believing the film industry, where predators heap praise, exploit and toss aside fresh arrivals. The jarring midsection mocks the shallow, fake hangers-on clamoring for relevance, laying bare the machine's cruelty and empty flattery.","sonnet":"A warning to someone — possibly a younger person or a past self — who went to Hollywood chasing fame and was exploited, two-timed, and objectified by an industry that devours people, intercut with the decadent spectacle of the Boulevard itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"Malakian closes Mezmerize by dramatizing the industry's rot from both ends at once: a weary voice warns a newcomer that the machine flatters people only to consume them, while the choruses hand the mic to someone already devoured and mistaking the wreckage for glory. Those interludes strut down Sunset and Santa Monica with a rapper's cheap crowd-command, the boulevards littered with hangers-on he now sneers at, the swagger itself proof he swallowed the bait the verses cautioned against. The letter fleeing to Hackensack, New Jersey lands as the one honest gesture in the song, an escape from a city where the faithful gather to worship nothing. Warning and downfall turn out to be the same person, split across the arrangement so you hear the seduction and its ruin in a single breath.","fv":2,"themes":["artifice & performance","night & the city","freedom & escape"],"means":"warning and downfall revealed as the same person split across the arrangement","built":"the strutting mid-section chorus voices the seduction the verses warned against"},
"system-of-a-down~marmalade":{"haiku":"Surreal imagery and nonsensical distortion; reality fragmented and reassembled into dreamlike chaos.","opus":"A purposely jarring pileup of taboo, dreamlike erotic images, sugary desserts and fruit-preserve skin bleeding into lines about bound limbs and offerings to a Roman deity, spurning sense for shock. System of a Down, the Armenian-American art-metal band, revel in exactly this surreal, poetic crudeness, and this obscure track dives fully into their absurdist streak, trading story for a garish, disquieting overload of the senses.","sonnet":"A fever-dream of sensuality and mortality in surrealist fragments — flesh, food, Saturn, and actors all folding into each other as the speaker contemplates poison and the sweetness of dying tonight. It has the quality of a hallucination that refuses to cohere, every image dissolving before it can be grasped, desire and annihilation sharing the same breath.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a fever-dream that dresses violation up as dessert, using the sweet vocabulary of a kitchen to render a body being pinned, coated, and consumed. Tankian's imagery keeps sliding between appetite and dread: something soft and spreadable stands in for skin, warm sauce for arousal, a furry heap of roadkill for the aftermath, so hunger and death share one syrupy register. The refusal at the end is the tell, a narrator who cannot disown what he has swallowed for the night, treating desire as a dose he already knows will finish him. In its early-demo strangeness the band lets surrealism do the moral work, refusing to pry the pleasure loose from the ruin it curdles into.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"desire as a dose already known to finish the speaker","built":"kitchen sweetness and roadkill share one syrupy register throughout"},
"system-of-a-down~mind":{"haiku":"Free thinkers are condemned by blame and hate; mutually abused children of sin reject imposed control while threats of death hang overhead.","opus":"A disturbing address to childhood abuse and its aftermath, molested children and the beatings that shaped them, colliding intimacy with revulsion, warning that free thinkers are treated as dangerous while damage gets passed down as fate.","sonnet":"A song about childhood trauma and the violence done by abusive systems — mentally molested children, beatings that broke skin, and a society that punishes free thinking; it ends in furious, desolate repetition as the narrator lets the perpetrators die.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study of love as a control mechanism, built on the whiplash between Serj Tankian's crooned invitation to draw near and the shrieked command to get lost that answers it a breath later. The verses reframe family and religion as inherited abuse — a beating so routine it passes for a household custom, sanctified by parent and pulpit alike — and reframe love itself as coercion, where the people one is bound to and the people one requires collapse into a single trapped exchange. The recurring jab that an independent mind is a threat locates the real danger in a wider order that punishes anyone who won't stay wired into its circuits of need, a politics the band would make overt on later records but keeps intimate and domestic here. The bridge's screamed death-wish is the manipulator's ultimate card, and it closes over the same numb refrain to gaze at one another that opened the track, now sounding less like tenderness than surveillance. It reads as an abuser's mind talking itself in circles, seduction and hatred exposed as the same voice.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","family & blood","love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"seduction and hatred exposed as one voice talking itself in circles","built":"the closing numb refrain echoes the opener, reframing tenderness as surveillance"},
"system-of-a-down~mr-jack":{"haiku":"Routine traffic stop escalates into violent police confrontation and authority assertion, reflecting systemic power abuse and dehumanization.","opus":"A tense highway stop that boils over into official brutality, small figures with heavy weapons and a driver shoved down onto the pavement. Its snarling closing refusal is the powerless throwing contempt back at armed authority.","sonnet":"A fragmented portrait of a police encounter — Mr. Jack is pulled over on the freeway, confronted by officers with guns, and the song escalates into a raw expression of rage against authoritarian state power.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Steal This Album! (2002) track that plays like an interrogation staged as a schoolyard taunt: the verses needle a cornered everyman with sing-song riddles about his disguise and his undoing, while the chorus pins him to one helpless coordinate, stranded roadside inside his own car as the questions circle. Then the mask drops: Tankian’s bridge snaps the scene into a pre-dawn raid, uniformed figures with weapons trained on sleeping heads, arriving at seven in the morning to haul people off before they are even conscious. The outro completes the arc into a botched traffic stop that curdles toward execution, the barked orders to surrender colliding with a refusal spat back at authority. The sequencing is the whole argument: what opens as playground mockery is revealed, movement by movement, as the machinery of state violence, so the childish rhythm becomes the most damning sound in the song.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"playground mockery revealed movement by movement as state violence","built":"sing-song riddles opening the track make the pre-dawn raid the more damning sound"},
"system-of-a-down~n-guns":{"haiku":"Children and lovers become collateral casualties of violence and indifference. The song questions what debt or penance humanity owes for allowing these deaths and what 'nüguns' symbolizes as weaponized innocence.","opus":"A cryptic, disturbing piece circling children, violence and unpaid moral debts, a little boy and girl bound to bloodshed and a price for crimes, the recurring demand asking what penance we've failed to pay for the nuguns. System of a Down specialize in exactly this elliptical political provocation, and here the fractured imagery of innocence and guns points at complicity, the meaning left deliberately jagged and accusatory.","sonnet":"A fractured, elliptical portrait of two children caught in cycles of violence and exploitation — bullet and blade, denial and transaction, the repeated question of what we said and didn't say circling the idea that collective silence enables harm to children while we look away and call it penance.","src":"haiku","fable":"A boy and a girl are itemized like inventory, their small bodies run through the grammar of a cash register even as the scene stays a bruised bed of denial, so childhood and a purchase become the same event. The two verses are the argument: the first is a stare into darkness and a flinch of refusal, the second swaps that dread for a sickened delight and the girl's bleeding, the same figures processed a second time until innocence has been converted into merchandise. The chorus turns communal, a we grinding out its unpayable debt of guilt while insisting it slept in no one's bed, confession curdling into alibi. Landing on Steal This Album!, the 2002 dump of loose unreleased tracks, the song keeps its unease deliberately smeared, and the notated instrumental break lets a non-Western color bleed through a Western nursery of abuse. The refusal to resolve is the point: the coined title lands as new-guns, an armament wearing a fresh brand name, and the track hands you a rot in how presence and consent get bought with no receipt at the end.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","money & the street","shame & repression","politics & society"],"means":"innocence itemized as merchandise while guilt curdles into alibi","built":"new-guns armament wearing a fresh brand name"},
"system-of-a-down~needles":{"haiku":"Parasitic relationships drain the speaker while addiction (symbolized by needles and veins) compels self-destruction despite recognition of the harm.","opus":"Using a tapeworm as a metaphor for a controlling parasite that feeds on and directs him, the speaker rages at being consumed and unable to grow, intercut with images of sitting alone with a needle in hand awaiting death.","sonnet":"The song uses a tapeworm as a metaphor for a controlling, parasitic relationship or addiction — something living inside the narrator that dictates every move, feeds off him until nothing remains, and will simply find a new host once he is used up; the bridge makes the drug dimension literal with the image of a needle in the hand.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about parasitism that keeps daring you to decide which parasite it means. The verses cast the speaker as host to a tapeworm that dictates his movements and eats him hollow, and the scatological chorus reduces the whole relationship to a single crude imperative to expel it, refusing any dignity to either party. The genius of it is that the worm is a metaphor with too many fits at once: addiction, a leeching lover, a whole exploitative order that keeps you alive only to feed on you, each one wearing the pimp's majesty. The bridge quietly detonates all of it, dropping the grotesque comedy for a still image of a man with a needle in his hand waiting out a dying stranger's death, so the swaggering parasite turns out to be something the speaker holds himself, and the final self-directed curse lands as the host's admission that the thing devouring him is his own hand.","fv":2,"fnote":"The band nearly split over one pronoun — the chorus originally said my, not your; Malakian and Odadjian vetoed it as too vulnerable, and Rick Rubin recalled the fight as extreme enough to end the band. The soft middle section keeps the original as the compromise.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","politics & society","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"the parasite metaphor turning until the host holds the needle","built":"the bridge drops grotesque comedy for a still image, detonating all prior frames"},
"system-of-a-down~old-school-hollywood":{"haiku":"Washed-up celebrities cling to faded glory in present-day Hollywood, living in the past and struggling in the sun. The song mixes absurdist commentary with references to forgotten celebrities and fading dreams.","opus":"A mocking vignette set at a charity ballgame full of fading celebrities, name-dropping bygone stars as the narrator broils in the heat and squanders his afternoon. Underneath sits a jab at the hollowness of showbiz prestige and nostalgia, with fame shrunk to petty squabbles over queue positions beneath a punishing sun.","sonnet":"A deliberately absurdist and playful riff on washed-up Hollywood celebrity culture, name-dropping forgotten figures like Tony Danza, Jack Gilardi, and Frankie Avalon as emblems of a faded, sun-baked world where everyone is past their prime and nothing matters except the game of baseball.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a taunt at celebrity as expired currency, jamming faded matinee names into a chant so relentless it curdles glamour into a novelty jingle. Tony Danza budging a queue, Frankie Avalon and agent Jack Gilardi swollen to cartoon dimensions, the singer sweating in the sun while his whole afternoon evaporates -- all of it dissolves into the same seesawing slogan repeated until the meaning bleeds out. Malakian builds the track on that hollowing loop on purpose, the incessant chant miming how fame stales into background noise. Underneath the goof sits contempt for a mythology that promised radiance and delivered a half-remembered name shoving ahead of you at the ballpark.","fv":2,"fnote":"The chant is straight reportage: Malakian really played the Hollywood Stars Night celebrity game at Dodger Stadium in August 2003 and found the faded stars catty and cliquish — and every name checks out, Danza cutting in line, Avalon, and Jack Gilardi, who was Avalon’s actual ICM agent for decades.","themes":["artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"glamour hollowed to a novelty jingle by relentless chanting","built":"the incessant name-chant mimes how fame stales into background noise"},
"system-of-a-down~p-l-u-c-k":{"haiku":"Genocide and systemic extermination of a people requires armed response and restitution for stolen lives and dignity.","opus":"A protest against genocide and the erasure of an entire people, calling for revolution, recognition, restitution, and reparation. It condemns a system that took the children and left the few survivors unfound.","sonnet":"A furious response to genocide — specifically the Armenian Genocide, given the band's heritage — calling for recognition, restoration, and reparation, and arguing that revolution is the only answer to a crime of that scale.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title P.L.U.C.K. stands for Politically Lying, Unholy, Cowardly Killers, and the song is System of a Down's founding statement: the four members are descendants of Armenian Genocide survivors, and Serj Tankian frames the 1915 slaughter not as history but as an open wound whose perpetrator state still refuses the word genocide. The song moves in two registers that the band deliberately jams together: the demand for justice, chanted as a three-step ladder climbing from acknowledgment to the return of what was stolen to material amends, and the raw account of the killing itself, where families are broken and the handful of survivors vanish into an administrative machine that engineered the erasure on purpose. The gap between those registers is the point: when the redress named in the verses is met only with a state's refusal to call the killing by its name, the chorus tips over into insurrectionary fury, its repeated command to bring the guilty down functioning as the only answer left to a denial that has lasted a century.","fv":2,"fnote":"The album booklet dedicates this song specifically to the memory of the 1.5 million victims of the Armenian Genocide.","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"insurrectionary fury as the only answer a century of denial leaves","built":"the acronym title spells the accusation before the first note lands"},
"system-of-a-down~peephole":{"haiku":"A warning against becoming spiritually trapped in an artificial, fake reality; the speaker urges others not to get stuck in delusion when they're high.","opus":"A woozy, surreal SOAD trip questioning drug-fueled detachment, asking whether getting high leaves you stranded in the sky, its dreamlike nonsense circling a warning about losing your footing in your own head.","sonnet":"A drug-induced dissociation song — asking repeatedly whether, when you're high, you believe you're stuck in the sky, suspended between fake moons and crying mermaids, unable to trust what's real or come back down from the altered state.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title never appears in the lyric, which is the trick: it names the aperture everything here is seen through, a small warped circle in a door you happen to be on the wrong side of, and it quietly reclassifies the chorus's wide-open sky as exactly that kind of narrowed, distorting view. The verses are built to be interchangeable, each welding some piece of counterfeit scenery — a moon you can tell is a prop — to the same unanswered question, which never gets a reply, only an order. Then the form gives the game away: the peak before the solo burns down to one syllable repeated past the point of meaning, and afterward the song abandons verses altogether and can do nothing but circle its own instruction, stuck precisely as it keeps telling you not to be. Early Tankian writes this way deliberately, as texture rather than cipher; the surreal debris is not a code to crack but the sound of a mind that has lost the ability to change the subject, and the panic lives in the looping rather than the pictures.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"a mind that cannot change the subject, trapped in its own instruction","built":"the song abandons verses post-solo and can only circle the command it cannot obey"},
"system-of-a-down~pictures":{"haiku":"The speaker holds fragmentary images or memories in their mind while navigating ego, theft, and the nature of reality and identity.","opus":"A jittery collage of ego, control and intrusive imagery, System of a Down's absurdist edge sharpening into something about being haunted by what you can't unsee. Fear waits at the edge of every fractured line, meaning kept deliberately unstable.","sonnet":"A dense, fractured lyric with surreal and confrontational imagery that resists clean reading — it circles around ego, self-perception, fear, and the dissolution of the moment. The repeated \"pictures on my mind\" suggests intrusive mental images or memories that won't resolve.","src":"haiku","fable":"A short, deliberately incoherent burst from System of a Down's early era, this reads less as a statement than as a mind that cannot stop generating images it can't organize. The verses are a scramble of taunts and non-sequiturs that keep short-circuiting on the question of whether the speaker is even real or visible to anyone, while the one thing that holds steady is the hammered refrain that pictures crowd the mind, hypnotic through sheer repetition until it feels less like a boast than a symptom. Where the song briefly stops flailing is the bridge, which turns patient and almost liturgical, watching dread bide its time as gilded moments corrode to rust and the meal of a conquest is cleared away. That contrast is the read: the churning surface is the noise of a head too full of flickering images to make sense, and underneath it sits the quiet certainty that everything glittering is already waiting to decay.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"mind too crowded with images to organize any of them","built":"bridge's liturgical stillness exposes the chaos framing it"},
"system-of-a-down~prison-song":{"haiku":"A critique of mass incarceration and drug policy, exposing how the prison system grows as a tool of social control and oppression against marginalized populations.","opus":"A protest against America's carceral machine, charging that narcotics were made available on purpose so that petty users would pack the cells. It ties public money and drug profits to overseas wars and corrupted power, and demands rehabilitation instead of policing.","sonnet":"A polemic arguing that the U.S. prison system was deliberately expanded after the civil rights era by making drugs available to minorities, filling jails with minor offenders while drug money funds covert wars and corrupts elections abroad.","src":"haiku","fable":"The opening salvo of Toxicity works as agitprop montage, cutting between three incompatible voices so the argument lives in the seams rather than any single line. Serj Tankian narrates history as sabotage, framing the flood of narcotics into poor neighborhoods as a deliberate answer to the civil-rights era, then hands the mic to a sneering first-person addict scoring his fix in Hollywood, before the music drops out for a deadpan recitation of incarceration figures and policy findings delivered like courtroom evidence. That last register is the real gambit: by reading census-style numbers and reformist recommendations in the flat cadence of a report, the band weaponizes officialese against itself, letting the horror sit in the arithmetic instead of the screaming. The addict is the mask; the claim underneath is that his appetite has been manufactured and monetized from above, the same profits laundered abroad into rigged votes and installed strongmen, so private vice and foreign policing turn out to be one racket. Against all of it the chorus refuses to argue at all, just a hammered mantra about a structure being built to hold us, its drift from a distant them to an inclusive you and me collapsing the gap between the caged and the free until everyone stands inside the wall.","fv":2,"fnote":"Malakian had done his own brief stint in county jail — outstanding traffic warrants and marijuana possession — and said he was lucky to have the money to walk out; the song’s rage at a system built to hold the poor carries that receipt.","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"horror housed in flat arithmetic rather than screaming","built":"deadpan incarceration figures weaponize the report's own cadence"},
"system-of-a-down~protect-the-land":{"haiku":"Territorial defense and ancestral legacy; standing armed against invasion to preserve history and homeland.","opus":"System of a Down broke a fifteen-year recording silence to release this in solidarity with Armenians during the 2020 Artsakh war. It challenges the idea that force decides a life's worth by asking whether you would take up arms for your homeland, framing the defense of one's land against invaders as duty and channeling the band's ancestral history of displacement into a rallying cry.","sonnet":"A call to armed resistance against displacement and invasion, rooted in collective memory of survival. Those who refused exile and held their ground are lionised; the question of whether ordinary people would stay, weapon in hand, when pushed to the edge becomes the test of whether history and legacy mean anything at all.","src":"haiku","fable":"This turns a defense of homeland into a chant you cannot stop, the title phrase hammered until it stops sounding like a slogan and starts sounding like a vigil. Written by four Armenian-American musicians as Artsakh burned in late 2020, it frames a people already scattered to other countries and buried in desert soil, then keeps returning one question to the listener: pushed to the edge, would you fight rather than flee, and would you do it armed? The refrain names scavengers and invaders picking at a border, and answers them not with triumph but with sheer insistence — an inheritance counted out and passed down. By the end the words fracture mid-line, the title command shedding its object with each repetition until only the defenders are left standing in the sentence, as if the phrase itself were being defended and overrun at once.","fv":2,"fnote":"Released with Genocidal Humanoidz on 6 Nov 2020 — SOAD’s first new music in fifteen years, made for the Artsakh war and raising over $600,000 for the Armenia Fund; written by Daron Malakian, originally for Scars on Broadway.","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","exile & diaspora"],"means":"insistence as vigil rather than triumph for a scattered people","built":"the title command sheds its object mid-line until only defenders remain"},
"system-of-a-down~psycho":{"haiku":"Cocaine, groupie culture, and psychotic behavior make one high, hide the truth, and lead to spiritual collapse. The song condemns destructive lifestyle choices.","opus":"A jittery indictment of fame's addiction economy, where celebrity, sex and cocaine blur into one numbing rush. The chant mocks the groupie fantasy while pitying the hollowing-out it causes, spectacle consuming the person watching it.","sonnet":"A frenetic sketch of the rock industry's most degrading orbit — cocaine, groupies, and the psychological collapse that the lifestyle accelerates — with a thread of contempt for anyone who thinks they have to participate in it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Four nouns fired like a slogan carry the whole track: a person compressed into diagnosis, entourage accessory, narcotic, and epithet, chanted until the syllables lose meaning and survive only as rhythm. That flattening is the argument, and the verses expose what the chant is truly about by pivoting to address a you, offering to freeze time and then watching the body and the spirit collapse, so the fantasy of pausing the chaos turns out to be the chaos. System of a Down braid celebrity, sexual availability, and cocaine into one accelerating machine, and the jeer about the spectacle reframes fame as a strip act nobody is required to perform yet everyone does. The mask is the party-anthem cadence and the mockery; underneath sits a portrait of dependency that has erased the self it labels, so the demand to keep the eyes from spilling over reads less as solace than as an order to stop feeling the wreckage. What sounds like an insult chant is a closed loop with no exit, which is precisely its verdict on the life it describes.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance","party & hedonism"],"means":"celebrity and cocaine collapsing into one closed loop with no exit","built":"chant strips four nouns to rhythm until the self they label disappears"},
"system-of-a-down~question":{"haiku":"Ghosts and berries, sleep and dreams—the song asks if we know when we fly, if we die when we go, cycling between waking and dreaming states.","opus":"A hypnotic meditation on death and the afterlife, ghosts described as no different from the living and waiting for us. The central questions, whether we know anything when we die or fly, blur death with dreaming, treating mortality as an unanswerable riddle.","sonnet":"A dreamy, circular meditation on death and consciousness — do we know when we leave the body, when we go, do we die — looping through the same childlike questions without resolution.","src":"haiku","fable":"A death-anxiety song disguised as a lullaby: the verse's fairy-tale image of ripe berries set for two turns out to be an invitation from the waiting dead, who the song insists are no different from the living, collapsing the line between being alive and being a ghost into the soft question of whether you are only dreaming. The chorus strips that dread to its bare bones, asking whether we have any knowledge of the moment we cross over, and Serj Tankian leaves it genuinely open rather than answering it. The wordless la-la bridge is the hinge, in Mezmerize’s more melodic register: where the words run out, the childlike singsong keeps going, the comfort of a nursery tune papering over a question no one can actually answer.","fv":2,"fnote":"The berries turned literal: bassist Shavo Odadjian directed the video from a dream he actually had — a boy offering a girl berries he didn’t know were poisoned — and Tankian glossed the clip as life changing forms rather than ending, the band’s own staging of the verse’s fatal invitation.","themes":["death & grief","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"a fairy-tale image of ripe berries turns out to be an invitation from the waiting dead","built":"the wordless la-la bridge keeps going where the words about dying run out, nursery comfort papering over the unanswerable"},
"system-of-a-down~radio-video":{"haiku":"Celebration of rock stardom and entertainment value as celebrities like Danny and Lisa transport the speaker from ordinary places into the spectacle of media fame.","opus":"A send-up of empty celebrity craving, mocking the ego rush of being broadcast and adored for performing. The nameless figures who whisk the narrator off to odd, unreal destinations suggest that fame is a hollow flight into surface image with nothing underneath.","sonnet":"An affectionate, loose ode to the joy of performing and being broadcast — the speaker relishes being on radio and TV, anchored by the names Danny and Lisa who seem to provide an escape from the weirdness of the world around him.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a gleeful hollowing-out of pop celebrity, a narrator who has nothing to report except that he is being broadcast and keeps saying so until pride sours into parody. The one warmth is Danny and Lisa, two friends held up as rescuers who haul him back to earth, though they never harden into real people. Everything else is pose: a boast that loops, an escape that stays vague, a self defined only by being seen. When the language finally gives out into pure sung syllables, that collapse is the point, the script of fame stripped down to jingle with no meaning underneath. Daron Malakian pitches the whole thing as a childlike singalong, so the mockery arrives disguised as delight.","fv":2,"fnote":"Danny and Lisa are real: Malakian told Revolver they were his childhood neighbors, the friends he sings about — the fame-jingle’s one warm center is a piece of his own pre-fame childhood.","themes":["artifice & performance","friendship & loyalty","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"self defined only by being broadcast until the script dissolves","built":"the lyric collapses into pure sung syllables when the language runs out of meaning"},
"system-of-a-down~revenga":{"haiku":"Revenge is brewing through violent imagery—poisonings, bleeding, murder. The song dwells on payback and sweet retribution delivered through brutality.","opus":"A gleefully lurid revenge fantasy where jealousy and rejection curdle into murderous imagery; behind the cartoon violence is a jilted narrator savoring the sweet, still-brewing payback owed to the woman who told him to go away.","sonnet":"A violent, chaotic revenge fantasy directed at someone who dismissed the narrator — punctuated by a catalogue of methods of harm — with the underlying wound being a woman who simply laughed and told him to go away.","src":"haiku","fable":"A jilted man rehearses payback in nursery-rhyme rhythm until the fantasy caves in and exposes the heartbreak it was built to hide. The verses spin a sing-song loop of clumsy household murders, each squalid cruelty rhymed off the last so the threats scan less like a plan than a tantrum stuck on repeat. Between the gloating, an old-love pet name keeps surfacing, and the syrupy chorus curdles the menace into something nearer to begging. Then she only laughs and dismisses him, and the whole contraption breaks down: a closing chant of missed conditionals concedes the vengeance was never coming, just the ache of a spurned narrator scripting a future that already turned him down. On Mezmerize this is the band at their most viciously playful, arming a lullaby to stage impotent male rage as farce.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"revenge fantasy collapsing inward to reveal grief","built":"nursery-rhyme cadence arms impotent rage as farce"},
"system-of-a-down~roulette":{"haiku":"Confusion and emotional uncertainty when around a specific person. The song circles through contradictory feelings—knowing yet not knowing, sure yet confused.","opus":"A confused, aching meditation on someone the narrator can't read himself around, alternating between knowing and not knowing how he feels; the roulette is the gamble of an uncertain, possibly one-sided love where messages go unanswered and clarity never comes.","sonnet":"A person caught in the confusion of their own feelings around someone — simultaneously knowing and not knowing how they feel, unable to explain or resolve the emotional static of being in that person's presence.","src":"haiku","fable":"One of the rare hushed, acoustic tracks in the System of a Down catalog, Roulette trades the band's usual manic aggression for the stalled inner monologue of someone unable to name what is wrong with him. The verses are all admitted failure of language: a problem that cannot be explained, an excuse without a question, messages left that do no good, so the song's real subject is the gap between feeling and articulation rather than any event we can reconstruct. That gap becomes the chorus, which flatly contradicts itself line to line by both knowing and not knowing how he feels around this person, and as the phrase loops the contradiction stops resolving and simply spins, the title's gamble being that these two impossible certainties keep landing on the same number. What is left at the end is not an answer but the fixation itself, the address around you repeated until the person dissolves into pure orbit and the not-knowing wins by attrition.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"two impossible certainties about the same person kept spinning","built":"the chorus loops contradiction until resolution stops arriving"},
"system-of-a-down~sad-statue":{"haiku":"Current and future generations will be remembered as divided and apathetic toward human suffering, symbolized by a sad statue.","opus":"System of a Down deliver a cryptic critique of American power and complacency, where the conqueror owns the narrative and forgiveness becomes a costly surrender. Its refrain foresees a nation sinking into history alongside a mournful Liberty and a dissenting generation, indicting a country numb to suffering and blind to its own decline.","sonnet":"A political dirge for a generation that failed to act — the Statue of Liberty has become a sad symbol of a country whose people couldn't agree or mobilize in the face of suffering, and the speaker wonders how we stay deaf to the cries around us.","src":"haiku","fable":"A grief-song for empire's self-image, staring at a weeping Statue of Liberty and asking how a nation records a war its own children opposed. The verses speak in the polished aphorisms of the powerful, where conquest rewrites the map and the winner alone gets to narrate it, dressing plunder as mercy in a voice too smooth to trust. Then the bridge tears that mask off with one raw question about the deafness that lets us tune out another people's screaming, an Iraq-era indictment aimed squarely at 2005 America. The refrain welds the intimate pair to a whole restless generation, so private love and public shame land on the same ledger and no one is exempt. Its final unfinished word leaves belonging itself hanging, a country still deciding whose story it will claim as its own.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","war & battle","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"private love and public shame placed on the same ledger","built":"the final word left unfinished holds belonging itself in suspension"},
"system-of-a-down~science":{"haiku":"Science and rational analysis are critiqued for failing to account for faith, spirituality, and the intangible elements of human experience that transcend material explanation. The song argues faith is the force that animates existence beyond scientific understanding.","opus":"System of a Down argue that empirical knowledge has betrayed humanity and the planet by dismissing faith, spirit, and mystery. The song values surrender and belief over prediction and control, closing with an Armenian-language passage that roots its critique in a spiritual, ancestral worldview rather than a rational one.","sonnet":"A rejection of science as the sole framework for understanding existence, arguing it fails to account for faith — the willingness to let go and trust the unfolding — and has failed the earth; the song ends with a call to let spirit, which moves through all things, guide humanity instead.","src":"haiku","fable":"Serj Tankian, whose politics elsewhere in System's catalog are furious and specific, here drops the rage for something closer to prayer: an indictment of rationalist modernity for treating the future as a thing to predict and program rather than surrender to. The argument is thin as stated argument, but it isn't meant to be argued so much as chanted into belief, which is why the song's real content is its form. The word faith, then spirit, is repeated past the point of meaning until it works as mantra rather than claim, so that the track enacts the very letting-go it preaches, and the sudden Armenian passage cutting through the English drops the whole polemic into an older devotional register beneath the diagnosis, ancestral rather than argued.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","politics & society"],"means":"the track enacts the letting-go it preaches by repeating past meaning","built":"an Armenian passage cuts through English to drop the polemic into an older devotional register"},
"system-of-a-down~shame":{"haiku":"Aggressive domination and lyrical prowess obliterate opponents; the speaker embodies confrontational mastery while shaming those who cannot match intensity and skill.","opus":"A cover of Wu-Tang's classic, all braggadocio, cartoon violence and battle-rap menace daring anyone to run game. The threat is pure hip-hop swagger, style as weapon.","sonnet":"This is actually a cover of Wu-Tang Clan's \"Shame\" — the lyrics are entirely Wu-Tang's original, a bravado-heavy rap track about street dominance, lyrical superiority, and willingness to inflict violence on anyone who tries to undermine or test the narrator. It celebrates aggression and skill with the explicit threat of physical harm for anyone who runs game.","src":"haiku","fable":"The point of this cover is fidelity as sabotage: SOAD keep the Wu-Tang battle script letter-perfect, so the whole transformation happens in the delivery. What began as a coiled, syncopated threat over dusty boom-bap gets rebuilt as detonating metal, the same boasts about triggers, planets shot from the sky, and warriors taunted into the open now barked through distortion and Armenian caterwaul. The braggadocio system stays intact, but the menace changes species, from a rapper's cold precision to a band's frenzied noise. It reads less as tribute than as a stress test, running someone else's swagger through machinery it was never built for to see whether the swagger or the machine cracks first. Neither does, which is both the joke and the flex.","fv":2,"themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"someone else's swagger run through machinery it was never built for","built":"letter-perfect Wu-Tang script delivered through distortion and caterwaul"},
"system-of-a-down~she-s-like-heroin":{"haiku":"A woman is like an addictive drug, and the speaker or someone close is trapped in selling themselves to sustain the habit.","opus":"An unflinching sketch of addiction, prostitution and gender transgression on the margins, where a person and a drug become interchangeable. The narrator's helpless wish to save her collapses into complicity in the same economy.","sonnet":"A grotesque portrait of addiction mapped onto gender performance and sex work — a woman hooked on heroin and a gender-nonconforming man are selling sex to fund the habit, and the speaker is simultaneously drawn in and looking for a way out.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pusher's pep talk that curdles into confession, this Hypnotize-era grotesque narrates dependence first as somebody else's ruin and finally as the narrator's own. The verses keep the addict at arm's length, a her to be rescued and then pimped for drug money, while the chorus hammers the whole transaction down to one degraded syllable. Its gender imagery deliberately won't settle: one figure wants to play the streetwalker, another craves a dress and its softness, and the pronouns keep sliding around the same seedy market. Then the last breath swaps her for my, and the would-be rescuer stands exposed as the one who was strung out all along. That withheld first person is the point, since denial is the final thing a habit surrenders.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","money & the street","identity & becoming"],"means":"denial as the last thing a habit surrenders, exposed in the final breath","built":"pronoun slips from her to my in the last line, revealing the rescuer as the addict"},
"system-of-a-down~shimmy":{"haiku":"A critique of education and conformity as tools of indoctrination and control that stifle freedom, with the speaker wanting to break free and experience life spontaneously.","opus":"A cutting take on schooling as a tool for control and conformity, set against an ordinary person's modest longing for a home and a spouse, jabbed at by the constant scolding reminder to hurry off to class before the bell.","sonnet":"The song links the compulsory march through education — with its cycles of indoctrination and subjugation — to a desire for an ordinary, joyful life, exposing schooling as a system of control dressed up as opportunity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A whole life-cycle gets compressed into a drill-sergeant chant, each verse hammering the same four-syllable words in lockstep so that schooling, sanctioned reproduction, obedience and ruin all arrive as one interchangeable rhythm you cannot step off. The tolling command to hurry to class, aimed at boy then girl alike, frames the citizen as raw material fed in and processed out on schedule. Against this the chorus is the one flicker of a private self: a flat, almost sedated wish for an ordinary house-and-partner existence and to dance loose until sunrise, a longing the machine permits precisely because it changes nothing. Then the bridge scales the trick up, turning the shaping of a single child into the shaping of an entire population before the word for a people's molding buckles into the word for their ruin, and the outro simply reboots the bell that started it all, leaving the loop sealed with no graduation in sight.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","freedom & escape"],"means":"schooling, reproduction, and ruin arrive as one interchangeable lockstep rhythm","built":"the bridge's word for a people's molding buckles into the word for their ruin"},
"system-of-a-down~snowblind":{"haiku":"Cocaine addiction and the erasure of vision; intoxication creating blindness to reality and consequences.","opus":"A cocaine reverie that mistakes euphoria for freedom, icicles in the brain and death-cold pleasure spreading like winter, the narrator snapping back at anyone who says the drug is wrong, insisting this frozen ruin is where he belongs. It is Black Sabbath's original addiction portrait. System of a Down cover it here, the Armenian-American Los Angeles band momentarily setting aside their political fury for a faithful, chilling homage to metal's forebears.","sonnet":"Cocaine rendered as a winter landscape — the high described in terms of ice and freezing, blindness and cold euphoria, a crystal world that numbs even as it kills. The speaker knows it's destruction and doesn't care, defiant against anyone who tries to intervene, happiest lying snowblind in a sun that no longer reaches them.","src":"haiku","fable":"An addiction anthem that dares you to name it and then refuses the diagnosis. The whole song transposes a cocaine high into a vocabulary of cold: numbness reframed as clarity, a frozen brain reimagined as fields of snow-flowers and light glinting off ice, the drug stated flatly at each verse's end like a punchline nobody apologizes for. That defiance is the real subject. The bridge quits describing and argues back at an unnamed accuser, insisting the wreckage is a home and the worried onlooker is the actual failure. System of a Down play it faithful rather than ironic, letting Serj's soaring delivery keep the euphoria intact while the imagery quietly reports frostbite, so you hear the pleasure and the damage in the same breath.","fv":2,"fnote":"A Black Sabbath cover (Vol. 4, 1972), cut for the Nativity in Black II tribute (2000) and reused as the Aerials B-side.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"euphoria and frostbite made audible in the same breath","built":"soaring delivery keeps the pleasure intact while imagery quietly reports damage"},
"system-of-a-down~soil":{"haiku":"A requiem for a friend who died by suicide, exploring themes of confidence, weakness, and the inability to prevent someone's predetermined fate. The song grapples with the paradox that evil exists within all of us.","opus":"A raw response to a friend's suicide by gunshot, mixing grief, disbelief, and anger while wrestling with the idea that evil lives within human skin and demanding to know why he was taken away.","sonnet":"A raw elegy for a friend who died by suicide — the narrator recalls the shock and grief of the loss, wrestling with helplessness and rage, and acknowledging that the man's inner demons drove him to an act no one around him could prevent.","src":"haiku","fable":"An elegy for a friend who shot himself, and one of the most naked wounds on System's 1998 debut — grief here is personal, not yet the systemic politics that would define them. The verses stagger through the aftermath in a survivor's fractured order: the fatherless boy spinning past anyone's reach, the years of shared friendship, a death his closest people begged him not to choose, all soaked in a red imagery the narrator keeps replaying. Tankian reaches for a fatalist consolation — that the end was somehow always coming, unwanted but unavoidable — but the song won't let it hold, circling instead on how unfinished the life was and how the motive stays permanently out of reach. The bridge converts that helplessness into a bitter theology, insisting the evil was something native to the body itself, as if the impulse were housed in the skin rather than the fault of anyone left grieving. Then the outro drops every mask of peace-making acceptance and just screams at whatever took him — the plain, furious question of the bereaved, the only honest thing left once meaning runs out.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","friendship & loyalty","anger & defiance"],"means":"fatalist consolation attempted and refused by the circling grief","built":"the outro drops all peace-making and screams the plain furious question of the bereaved"},
"system-of-a-down~soldier-side":{"haiku":"War as inevitable, young men sacrificed while God stands distant and authority claims redemption.","opus":"Serj Tankian's mournful meditation on soldiers dying young and alone, mothers weeping as sons march off, God dressed in black and finding no hope. War stripped to its lonely graveside truth: everyone grows up only to die.","sonnet":"An anti-war elegy — young men sent to die for causes manipulated by bishops and kings, their mothers weeping as they leave, finding no hope, no truth, no god waiting; the soldier returns to nothing, and those who survive are left utterly alone on the soldier side.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a requiem that collapses the distance between the buried and the living, arguing that every soldier is already a corpse the moment he marches off. The song's architecture doubles a single image: those beneath the earth and the enlisted man perched over the pit dug for him ask the identical unanswered question about rescue, so future and outcome rhyme. A grieving deity in mourning clothes wanders away and refuses to return, draining the promise of salvation before it can arrive. Closing System of a Down's Hypnotize, companion to the earlier Mezmerize, it strips the band's fury down to a lullaby and lands on the coda's confession of absolute solitude, a voice welcoming you into a place emptied of everyone else. The gentle greeting is the mask; underneath is the flat report that growing up only ever travels one direction.","fv":2,"themes":["war & battle","death & grief","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the enlisted man and the buried man asking the identical unanswered question","built":"stripped to a lullaby, the gentle closing greeting masks a report of total solitude"},
"system-of-a-down~soldier-side-intro":{"haiku":"Young soldiers question salvation while their mothers mourn, knowing sons leave to die or find only violence and never return home.","opus":"The album's quiet frame for its antiwar rage: young men sent to fill their own graves, mothers weeping, a God dressed in mourning who finds no hope. It sits with the loneliness of the dead soldier rather than the politics.","sonnet":"Young men shipped off to war never come home — their mothers weep, God wears black, and the soldiers themselves die on foreign soil still waiting for salvation that never arrives, stranded on the soldier side with no one.","src":"haiku","fable":"An acoustic threshold piece that opens Mezmerize as the seed of the fuller Soldier Side closing its twin Hypnotize, this is a soldier's welcome delivered from the far side of dying, a place emptied of everyone but the speaker. The two lines pivot on a grim flattening: the first frames death as the soldier's isolated fate, the second universalizes it so that everyone growing up only to die lands the enlisted man in the same solitude everyone reaches anyway. Its brevity and hymnlike calm are the point, staging war's loneliness as an overture before the album's louder rage arrives.","fv":2,"themes":["war & battle","death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"war's loneliness voiced from the far side of dying","built":"hymnlike brevity staging the overture before the album's louder rage"},
"system-of-a-down~spiders":{"haiku":"Dreams and consciousness winding through existence and memory, with imagery of moonlight, life force, and spider-web-like patterns of thought and connection.","opus":"An early, dreamlike track built around surveillance creeping into private life, the V-chip standing in for a culture that watches even as we sleep. The eerie lullaby tone makes the intrusion feel intimate and inevitable.","sonnet":"A dream-logic piece revolving around a girl named June — her life, her dreaming mind, the surveillance technology (V-chip) that watches over her — the spiders in tune with the moon suggest a paranoid intimacy between media, control, and dream.","src":"haiku","fable":"A hypnotic near-lullaby that loops the same nocturnal furniture — a bright moon, an evening dissolving, sleep threading its images behind the eyes — until the trance is deliberately punctured by a piece of hardware: the V-chip, the mid-90s American TV censor built to filter what a viewer is allowed to see. That single technological intrusion reframes the whole reverie as a warning about mediated perception; the machine now decides whose sight is opened and what crosses the sleeping mind, so the soft repetitions read less as comfort than as something being fed in, the titular spiders working while everyone drifts off in tune. On their 1998 debut, before the overt political fury Serj Tankian and the band became known for, the menace stays quiet and cyclical, and the closing move — sliding the dreams off the singer's own head and onto a third-person her, then resolving not on sleep but on a vow to stay conscious — leaves surveillance and seduction finally indistinguishable.","fv":2,"fnote":"When the album came out in June 1998 the V-chip was law but not yet hardware — the Telecommunications Act had mandated it in 1996, and the FCC only required it in half of new sets by mid-1999 and all of them by 2000. The song is watching something coming, not something installed.","themes":["surveillance & control"],"means":"surveillance made intimate by the lullaby it rides in on","built":"closing pronoun shift from singer to her leaves seduction and surveillance indistinguishable"},
"system-of-a-down~stealing-society":{"haiku":"Detachment from life's struggle while living recklessly; the speaker admits to emptiness and self-harm, chasing oblivion with indifference to consequences.","opus":"Beneath its strange cosmic imagery of a dying world, this System of a Down track is a portrait of addiction and self-annihilation, its narrator chasing highs through hard drugs and reckless speed while shrugging off his own death. He becomes a stand-in for a culture cannibalizing itself, numbly consuming its way toward ruin.","sonnet":"A chaotic portrait of self-destruction through drugs, recklessness, and nihilism — the cosmic imagery of dueling suns and skies frames the mundane ugliness of a death wish and substance abuse, the speaker indifferent to dying as long as they get what they're chasing.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a song about self-destruction repackaged as glamour, the appetite for oblivion sold back to the addict as aspiration. Malakian builds it from two registers that never quite touch: a cosmic opening where twinned lights in the heavens circle each other in a deadlocked slow burn, one always outlasting the other, and a squalid ground-level verse of the overdosed idol sealed in an overheated room, sweating and parched. The chorus then flattens the death wish into a shrug and a punchline, courting maternal comfort and a fatal fix in the same breath. The title names the thesis the lyric keeps miming instead of stating: a culture that markets ruin as a lifestyle, then pockets the profit.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","politics & society"],"means":"oblivion repackaged as aspiration and sold back to the addict","built":"cosmic and squalid registers run in parallel and never quite touch"},
"system-of-a-down~storaged":{"haiku":"Biological ownership and possession dominate; revolution cycles endlessly through counter-revolutionary mind-traps binding consciousness and obscuring truth.","opus":"A dense, splintered torrent about uprising and its reversal, mastery over the unborn, the intellect and language dissolving into a fixed game. The whirling slogans stage a public numbed by contamination and deceit, where each supposed fix folds back into another cycle of reversal.","sonnet":"Fragmented slogans and revolving chants — spinal alignment, the fetus is mine, revolution counter-revolution, comatosis, words defining words — accumulate into a portrait of ideological noise and the body as contested political territory, never resolving but cycling through its own disorientation.","src":"haiku","fable":"An early-tape experiment that treats language itself as its subject, riffing on control by spinning single words into chains until they collapse into their opposites. The spiraling of revolution into counter-revolution into counter-counter, and solution warping into disillusion and pollution, stages how a slogan eats its own tail once it starts cycling. Against that churn sits one fixed, uneasy refrain of a spine aligning and a claimed unborn body, an image of ownership and control the wordplay keeps circling back to but never explains. The point isn't a message so much as the sensation of a mind talking itself in circles, defining its own words until definition and blindness feel like the same act.","fv":2,"themes":["comedy & wordplay","surveillance & control","madness & the mind"],"means":"a mind talking itself into circles until definition becomes blindness"},
"system-of-a-down~streamline":{"haiku":"Absence and missed goodbyes create permanent separation; a person regrets not being present through a loved one's passing.","opus":"A rare tender Malakian moment of absence and loss, the ache of not being present for someone's departure and the long goodbye that follows; regret framed as being blinded and separated from someone by a distance you can't cross.","sonnet":"A song about missing someone who has died or left permanently — the narrator wasn't there for the goodbye, can't see across the distance, and is left with the awareness that his love continues long after the chance to say farewell has passed.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession of absence at a parting the singer never reached, built as near-total repetition: the same admission of failing to arrive circles back until the word for parting starts to fray, said too many times to still land. The image-system is failed vision in a blizzard, a beloved dissolved somewhere past a wall of falling snow, and the album-closing structural trick is a reversal between the two verses. First the storm hides her completely; then, after she is already lost, the haze parts and he can finally make her out. Clarity shows up exactly one beat too late, which is the whole grief: hindsight as the cruelest kind of sight, with Serj Tankian's aching, near-devotional delivery pushing what could read as a war-casualty elegy toward pure private mourning. As the last track on Steal This Album!, it trades the record's political fury for something quieter and unresolved, an apology no longer possible to deliver.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss"],"means":"hindsight arriving exactly one beat too late as the cruelest sight","built":"two verses swap order so clarity of vision lands after she is already gone"},
"system-of-a-down~sugar":{"haiku":"Portrays paranoia, violence, and social alienation, with the narrator isolated and despairing, describing casual abuse and feeling hunted. The song ends in fatalistic nihilism where nothing matters.","opus":"A frantic sprint through a fraying mind, stacking fringe paranoia, casual violence and suicidal despair into a portrait of someone coming apart. The manic delivery is the meaning: modern anxiety spun so fast it collapses into numb resignation that it all just goes away.","sonnet":"A chaotic, fragmentary portrait of a person on the edge — Russian roulette as a daily habit, domestic violence, isolation in a dark room, and a final fantasy of having \"killed everyone\" cascade into a resigned refrain that in the end it all goes away, suggesting numbness rather than resolution.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song builds by escalation: an unreliable narrator's tics widen into psychosis. The verses set fried health-fad mysticism (the fermented-tea commune drifting through the day) against a first-person voice that keeps insisting on his own sanity while the details turn monstrous, first self-destruction dressed up as a manly sport, then a gun acquired as casually as a toy, then an unforgivable confession of beating a partner delivered in the same shrugging cadence, so the escalation implicates the casual tone itself. The recurring maternal prayer is the only tenderness left, aimed at a son past reaching. What makes the read is the collapse of the outro, where the character seals himself in the dark after confessing to killing them all and the earlier defiance flattens into an anesthetized loop: the endlessly repeated dismissal isn't a hook but the sound of a mind erasing itself, the sugar-coated word functioning as the numbing agent the whole song is really named for.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","violence & murder","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the casual tone itself implicated as the escalation mounts","built":"the outro's repeated dismissal is not a hook but a mind erasing itself in real time"},
"system-of-a-down~suggestion":{"haiku":"Power corrupts absolutely, with those in control manipulating truth and crushing dissent through institutional force and gaslighting.","opus":"An allegory of power and coercion staged on a shoreline: the lighthouse keeper owns the working class but can only get answers by degrading himself, while ships multiply toward an inevitable overrun. Beneath the surreal imagery sits a warning that authority built on exploitation ends in evacuation and collapse.","sonnet":"A surreal critique of power and sexual coercion — someone watches from a high post over working-class ships, implies that access to truth or power requires sexual submission ('give a piece of ass'), and as the ships multiply and close in, the warning goes unheeded and the narrator's fire consumes everything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A watcher stationed above a harbor is imagined as a lighthouse whose beam does more than guide, extracting favors and obedience from whoever needs its light, so that the keeper of the signal becomes a small tyrant over those below. The middle turns the metaphor sinister: the guiding glow is a planted command, steering vessels to wreck rather than safety, and it starts mistaking its own influence for divinity. By the close the arriving ships have swelled into an unstoppable tide and the tower must be abandoned, which reads as any manipulator's reckoning when the crowd he programmed finally overruns him. Early SOAD sharpens this into a parable about power lodged in whoever controls perception, and the panicked address to sir stages authority collapsing in real time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"power extracted through submission rather than offered as guidance","built":"the authority figure ends as the one overrun"},
"system-of-a-down~suggestions":{"haiku":"Powerful figures manipulate working-class people through control and hypnotic suggestions from elevated positions. Those who seek answers face danger and fire.","opus":"An early SOAD parable of manipulation and control, a lighthouse-keeper watching threatening ships multiply, warning that any authority promising answers really demands submission and will consume anyone who questions it.","sonnet":"An allegory about power and obedience — the lighthouse keeper watches ships multiply and approach the shore; those in power use post-hypnotic control to run them aground, and questioning the fog means getting devoured by it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song builds a scenario from a coastal lookout and then lets it curdle into paranoia. In the first verse the watcher's perch and skill are the source of a corrupt bargain — authority over the many is real, but any answer worth having demands humiliating submission, the sneering price tag the band hang on every institution. The second verse names the mechanism outright as implanted commands and a beacon that lures vessels to wreck before congealing into worship, so the guiding signal is revealed as the trap; the choice offered is between being consumed and being burned. By the closing panic — vessels swarming the coast, the frantic order to abandon the very watchtower that started it all — the read snaps shut: the one who thought he was steering the fleet was only ever bait, and the whole apparatus of hypnotic control collapses under the disaster it summoned.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society","faith & the occult"],"means":"the guide signal is the trap it appears to warn against","built":"the watchtower abandoned at the end it was built to protect"},
"system-of-a-down~suite-pee":{"haiku":"A transgressive spiritual narrative invoking Jesus as a figure around which people construct philosophy and devotion. The imagery is violent and sacrilegious, exploring religious fervor and bodily transgression.","opus":"A provocative, blasphemous piece imagining Jesus as a woman and mixing religious imagery of dying for her philosophy with violent, sexual, and mocking depictions of following and abandoning Christ.","sonnet":"A chaotic, blasphemous assault on religious devotion, casting Christ as a female figure whose philosophy demands dying for her, and mixing ecstatic surrender with provocative sexual imagery to destabilise sacred narratives.","src":"haiku","fable":"The debut's opening salvo re-genders the crucifixion, casting the savior as a woman mourned by all, then dares the listener to actually adopt that creed rather than merely worship it — a distinction the chorus drives home by pivoting from trying the belief to dying for it. Tankian's imagery keeps collapsing faith into violence: a cathedral seen as war-scarred stone, a plea for weapons and a shovel, devotion that arrives looking like both a battlefield and a burial. The bridge is the real detonation, dropping the ritual of prone bodies and spirits pierced by a redeemer into raw obscenity, so that the yearning to reach paradise gets voiced as brute carnal want — sacred longing and profane appetite exposed as one drive, the mask and the face beneath it. The title's own pun, a crude bodily function dressed as a musical suite, preps that whole strategy of smuggling the vulgar inside the elevated. By the outro even the vocabulary disintegrates, one word-family mutating letter by letter from discipleship into downfall, as if the machinery of belief wears itself out mid-phrase.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","love & desire"],"means":"sacred longing and profane appetite exposed as one drive","built":"the title puns a crude bodily function dressed as a musical suite"},
"system-of-a-down~tentative":{"haiku":"Superstition and false comfort blind people while bombs fall; no authority will save the masses spiraling downward as ceremonies mock belief.","opus":"An apocalyptic protest against religion, superstition, and warmongering, ordinary people descending helplessly as destruction rains down while detached authorities keep talking. The refrain is a civilian's cry of nowhere left to flee, no rescue coming, not even a divine one, facing extinction with no one to save them.","sonnet":"Religious ceremony and political detachment are both indicted as the bombs fall — those in power keep their distance and offer platitudes while everything rots; the song is a furious demand to know where the powerful expect the powerless to go when civilization collapses.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a wartime hymn hurled at a comfortable bystander who narrates other people's extinction from a safe remove while the sky rains ordnance. Tankian builds an image-system of downward motion and decay, a corkscrew into dirt, bodies going soft while ritual and faith get exposed as anesthetics that soothe the frightened but stop nothing. The masked observer keeps talking from a distance, so the song turns its whole force on him, demanding over and over where the doomed are supposed to run once the sky opens. Its cruelest move withdraws the last consolation people cling to: no rescuer arrives, not the crowd, not the heavens. The unanswerable question is the point, a demand the track refuses to soften, because there is no exit and it will not pretend otherwise.","fv":2,"themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"the unanswerable question held open because there is no exit","built":"faith and ritual named as anesthetics that soothe but stop nothing"},
"system-of-a-down~thetawaves":{"haiku":"Government manipulation and corporate deception disguised as ally forces, where debt and media technology keep people senseless and compliant until they finally recognize the lie.","opus":"Draws a parallel between a mind that unsettles itself into clarity and a society kept off-balance by debt and secret weaponry, both left to fend alone until they discover the deception. A meditation on distrust of the institutions that govern us.","sonnet":"The unsettled mind and national debt are presented as parallel allies that strip away false comfort — the people, once they discover the lies, rise toward something beyond; it is a subtle anti-government piece wrapped in mystical brain-state imagery.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song runs one grammatical template twice and lets the swap carry the argument: verse one is neurology, where a wandering mind abandons the nerves to survive alone while they log raw chemical and electrical readings; verse two keeps the identical skeleton but overwrites the biology with statecraft, so a government's ledgers now abandon a whole population to survive alone while they tally evidence of surveillance and covert weaponry. The rhyme between the two stanzas is the point, a suggestion that a citizen misled by an economy is only a nervous system left untended at larger scale, and Tankian marks the difference in each stanza's last line, where the abandoned senses merely reach for something above them but the abandoned public instead uncovers a deception. Around this the refrain never changes, a fixed clock in which mortality idles just past the doorway and dread is held like an embrace, so the frame stays constant while the content mutates from cellular to civic. The mounting bridge into the pre-chorus, tightening from a call to stand into a summons upward, turns the whole piece into an ascent that reads at once as a chemically altered state and as a mass reckoning, which is exactly the double vision of Steal This Album, a 2002 collection of offcuts that keeps burying its conspiracy inside something first mistaken for a private trip.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"neurology and statecraft rhymed as the same abandonment at scale","built":"identical grammatical template run twice with biology swapped for civics","fnote":"Theta waves are the brain’s threshold band — the frequency of the drowsy edge between waking and sleep — so the title names the exact state the chorus inhabits: time turning into a midnight ride, finality waiting just outside the drift."},
"system-of-a-down~this-cocaine-makes-me-feel-like-i-m-on-this-song":{"haiku":"Drug addiction and depravity intertwine with detachment and twisted moral inversion; the song depicts cascading spiral through chemical and emotional numbness.","opus":"A jittery, dissociated snapshot of cocaine dependence, where craving and denial fuse into the addict's conviction that the fault always lies elsewhere. The absurd, disconnected imagery mimics a hollowed-out, paranoid logic, mirroring how the drug scrambles thought while leaving the user numbly certain nothing is wrong with them.","sonnet":"An intentionally fractured, near-incoherent piece that embodies drug-scrambled thinking — denial, projection, and non-sequiturs pile up as the narrator cycles between blaming others and acknowledging a shared addiction to the next fix.","src":"haiku","fable":"A junkie's self-defense recited as mantra, where every denial of blame collapses into the exact craving it claims to be above. The chorus loops the addict's oldest lie, pinning the sickness on someone else while the shakes and the hunger for the next hit leak through anyway. Serj Tankian jams the refrains with rhyming nonsense, a venereal disease chiming against a cheese, staging a brain scrambled past coherent thought. The verse's melting rooms and men holed up down wells turn delirium into architecture, sense sliding off the walls. By the outro the whole argument has rotted down to one paranoid command about a poisoned meal, the last word from a mind that no longer trusts its own kitchen.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"denial of blame loops back into the craving it claims to be above","built":"rhyming nonsense in the refrain enacts a brain scrambled past coherent thought"},
"system-of-a-down~toxicity":{"haiku":"Critique of urban chaos and the poisonous environment of the city itself. The song captures alienation through abstract, fragmented imagery of daily dysfunction and disconnect from meaning.","opus":"An impressionistic sketch of urban breakdown, questioning anyone's claim to command a world already tipping into chaos. The song surveys the strain and disorder of city living, then reaches for a fragile hush somewhere between reverence and rest, treating that narrow calm as the only refuge from the surrounding poison.","sonnet":"The song sketches fragmented images of urban dysfunction—disorder, noise, disconnection, and the numbing grind of city life. Against this backdrop, the speaker contemplates ownership, sacred silence, and a moment of transformation where they become a source of life.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song lives in the gap between two textures. Its verses are a clogged urban sensorium rendered as machine data and degraded sight: life glimpsed through a wheel hub, a body pinned in a truck's high beams, a poisoned city named twice as if repetition could exorcise it. Then the chorus flips into metaphysical accusation, treating chaos as a thing someone claims to possess and demanding to know what ownership even means when what you govern is derangement. This is Malakian and Tankian's method on the 2001 record, nu-metal force welded to a diaspora unease, where the real target is the arrogance of holding title to a broken world. What earns the length is the threshold the chorus keeps circling, the untenable seam between a hush and rest that the track promises but can never actually cross, so it can only chant the word for chaos instead of resolving it; the outro's messianic swell, the speaker ballooning into a life-giving star, reads not as triumph but as that same disordered mind reaching for grandeur it cannot hold.","fv":2,"fnote":"The seed-eating of the verses is no surreal non sequitur — it’s the Armenian diaspora’s habit of snacking on roasted sunflower seeds, a cultural marker Malakian confirmed planting in the song.","themes":["politics & society","night & the city","madness & the mind"],"means":"demanding who can own chaos in a city already broken","built":"the outro's messianic swell arrives as disorder reaching for grandeur it cannot hold"},
"system-of-a-down~u-fig":{"haiku":"A call to violence and escape against ignorant nationalists and conformists who wave flags blindly.","opus":"SOAD's contempt for jingoism weaponized as a chant to beat down flag-waving conformity, urging the blind to wake and realize they even have a mind. The mockery is deliberately childish to expose the target's stupidity.","sonnet":"An anti-jingoism screed — the narrator wants to beat and eat flag-waving nationalists, insisting that blind patriotism is a form of blindness that will run out of time, with the aggression being as much performance as argument.","src":"haiku","fable":"A savage joke about how the crowd that loathes nationalist mobs becomes one, chanting the same hunger to bludgeon and devour its enemies that it claims to despise. The verses keep drifting toward escape, dissolving into daylight or vanishing overhead or rallying to some nameless cause, but each retreat snaps back into the chorus's giddy urge to hurt the patriotic and dim. By the bridge the accusation swings onto the listener, insisting you are the blind one and the clock is running out, so that on Hypnotize the band leaves nobody clean, least of all the righteous.","fv":2,"fnote":"The band has never decoded the title, but fans have long read it as an acronym lifted straight from the chorus’s own insult — you, flag-waving ignorant geeks.","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder"],"means":"anti-mob crowd becoming the mob it claims to despise","built":"accusation pivots onto the listener at the bridge"},
"system-of-a-down~vicinity-of-obscenity":{"haiku":"Nonsense chants about bananas and terracotta collide with accusations of lying and grotesque imagery of defeat learned from corrupt bodies.","opus":"Deliberate absurdist provocation, the nonsense chant of fruit and pottery colliding with crude come-ons and jabs, SOAD mocking sense itself while sneaking in disgust at desire and defeat beneath the silliness.","sonnet":"Deliberately absurdist and deliberately obscene — a nonsense fever-dream about holding someone, broken up by crude sexual imagery and accusations of lying, functioning more as provocation and pure SOAD weirdness than coherent narrative.","src":"haiku","fable":"The nonsense here is doing real work rather than hiding a code — the fruit-and-pottery chant is deliberate gibberish that refuses to resolve, and the song keeps yanking a seemingly earnest question about how to hold a lover back into that babble. What survives translation is the scaffolding around the void: an accusation of lying, a leer at obscenity, and a genuinely crude closing image of degradation, so the track works by letting a childish singsong and a smutty undertone rub against each other until intimacy itself sounds absurd. Trying to decode the chant is the joke; the refusal to mean anything is the meaning.","fv":2,"themes":["comedy & wordplay","love & desire"],"means":"deliberate gibberish refusing to resolve around an earnest question","built":"singsong and smut rubbed together until intimacy sounds absurd"},
"system-of-a-down~violent-pornography":{"haiku":"A critique of mass consumption of violence and tragedy through media: society is addicted to witnessing suffering, fed a steady diet of brutality as entertainment.","opus":"A blunt attack on how television normalizes degradation and sells cruelty as entertainment, the manic disco chorus mirroring the numbing churn. The final plea to switch it off names the real target: mass brainwashing dressed as amusement.","sonnet":"The song frames television as a machine delivering a non-stop diet of sexual violence and degradation to a passive audience, equating entertainment culture with brainwashing. The litany of universal human experiences — fucking, crying, dying — is set against the accusation that this content normalises brutality.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Mezmerize-era broadside against media saturation, built as a three-stage irony machine that only resolves at the end. The verse's numbing everybody-chant flattens sex, tears, and death into the same interchangeable pulse of the living, a deliberately blank mass audience; the pre-chorus then dissolves into giddy nonsense, a manic ad-jingle babble that name-drops a snack brand as if the whole culture were a non-stop party you were never let in on. Only in the chorus does the target snap into focus as the degradation piped through the screen, until the mask drops entirely, a command to kill the TV and then an outro naming the mechanism outright as brainwashing. The song's meaning lives in that gap between its mindless surface and its final accusation: the empty repetition is not filler but the point, an enactment of the anesthetized consumption it is indicting.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","artifice & performance"],"means":"the song enacts the anesthetized consumption it is indicting","built":"the manic ad-jingle pre-chorus dissolves into nonsense before the target snaps into focus"},
"system-of-a-down~war":{"haiku":"Historical religious crusades and justified violence against non-believers.","opus":"A critique of holy war and the Crusades, questioning whether faith or greed for riches drives men to kill in God's name.","sonnet":"The religious and political manipulation behind holy wars, questioning why men kill each other in the name of God.","src":"haiku","fable":"The question mark in the title is the whole argument: Tankian stages the Crusades as an interrogation, asking who actually profited when European nobles rode east against the Seljuks and rebranded plunder and conquest as pilgrimage. The chorus answers by refusing to answer, chanting the dehumanizing slur for the enemy over and over with zero ironic cushion, so the crusader's own bloodthirsty mantra speaks straight and the mask indicts itself — faith stripped down to ammunition, God enlisted as the license every killer needs to feel righteous. The bridge sheds the medieval costume for the modern machinery underneath, running the same sanctified-violence logic from a crusader's inscribed blade to the alibis of contemporary statecraft, all of it still demanding divine sign-off before the killing starts. The hush that falls just before the final chorus — the collective vow of silence about the slaughter — reads as the coward's grace after the fact, a civilization declining to name the atrocity its holy stories were built on. As a debut track from Armenian-American musicians whose people survived a genocide the world was pressured to forget, that willed forgetting is not an abstraction; it is the exact machinery of erasure the song is furious about.","fv":2,"themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","politics & society"],"means":"the crusader's own mantra speaks straight until faith indicts itself","built":"the dehumanizing slur chanted without irony lets the mask do the accusation","fvr":"2.4"},
"system-of-a-down~x":{"haiku":"People reject population growth and nullification as a way forward, instead asking what is right while warning others how they died.","opus":"A terse, chant-like warning against overpopulation and self-destruction, telling the living to show others how a people died, refusing both multiplication and erasure, its blunt repetition making extinction feel like a choice being made.","sonnet":"A short, repetitive song built around the refusal to multiply and the command to die — it reads as anti-natalist and nihilist, insisting that showing others how we die is more honest than propagating life.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole track is built out of relayed commands, each verse pushing an instruction through a middleman toward the masses: first to notify them, then to display the manner of a killing, finally to interrogate what would even count as just. Between those transmissions sits a chorus of flat refusal that swings between two opposite verbs of species-survival, the urge to breed and the urge to cancel out, rejecting both with equal blankness. The trick is that the refrains keep shedding their words, the count cropping, the object dropping clean away, twice collapsing into the bare fatal syllable, so a song about declining every demand keeps involuntarily arriving at the one outcome nobody requested, the mask of principled abstention slipping to reveal that extinction is where all this refusing quietly lands.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","death & grief"],"means":"principled refusal of every demand quietly arriving at extinction","built":"refrains shed words down to a bare fatal syllable, enacting the erasure they resist"},
"sza~the-weekend-funk-wav-remix":{"haiku":"Extramarital desire openly acknowledged and compartmentalized; the speaker claims partial ownership through weekend devotion while sharing the prize.","opus":"A woman claiming her portion of a shared man, working the weekend shift around his primary partner with defiant, clear-eyed frankness. SZA flips the other-woman trope into a knowing arrangement, desire negotiated on her own unbothered terms.","sonnet":"The speaker knowingly occupies the role of a man's weekend lover while another woman holds him on weekdays, and rather than lamenting the arrangement she navigates it on her own terms — claiming her share of him with unsentimental clarity. The song maps a triangulated desire without self-pity, framing the situation as a transaction with defined rules.","src":"haiku"},
"t-a-t-u~all-about-us-uncensored":{"haiku":"Defiant togetherness against external judgment; the speakers fortify intimacy through isolation and mutual exclusivity.","opus":"Two lovers against a disapproving world, insisting on a bond outsiders can't touch and vowing to run if they must. Marketed as forbidden teen romance, t.A.T.u. turn distrust from others into the very fuel that welds the couple together.","sonnet":"Two people define their bond against an outside world that doesn't trust them and would rather they fail or flee — the relationship itself is the only loyalty that matters, and they will protect it or run together before surrendering it. The defiance is romantic and absolute.","src":"haiku"},
"t-a-t-u~all-the-things-she-said":{"haiku":"Forbidden queer desire overwhelms a young woman whose feelings for another girl consume her despite family and social condemnation.","opus":"A young woman consumed by forbidden desire for another girl, unable to block her out and desperate to fly somewhere they could be free of judging stares and shame. The parents' gaze and the fear of having crossed a line make it a cry of feeling driven out of her mind by love she's told is wrong.","sonnet":"A young woman is overwhelmed by her feelings for another woman — serious trouble, cornered, going out of her head — wanting to fly somewhere free of judgment while her parents look on in horror, asking if she has crossed the line.","src":"haiku"},
"taco-hemingway~bia-koholicy":{"haiku":"Gym-obsessed protein addicts preach dietary perfection while secretly sabotaging themselves in weekends of excess. The culture of fitness gurus masks underlying instability and performative health.","opus":"A satire of Poland's obsessive fitness-and-clean-eating culture, voiced through a preachy gym bro lecturing about free-range eggs, GMOs, and supplements; the twist is his own hypocrisy—cocaine has no fiber, and these health fanatics who count calories all week binge on cold pork chops, kebabs, and booze on weekends, which 'don't count.'","sonnet":"A satirical portrait of Polish fitness and health-food culture — the song mocks the obsessive wellness bro who lectures everyone on protein and omega-3s but collapses every weekend into cocaine, alcohol, and kebabs, exposing the performative gap between the ideal and the reality.","src":"haiku"},
"taconafide-quebonafide-taco-hemingway~intro":{"haiku":"Soma pacifies the masses while genuine pain and depression remain invisible beneath pharmaceutical screens. The song critiques recreational escape as moral failure and calls for meaningful engagement with suffering.","opus":"Framed by Brave New World's soma, this Polish opener wrestles with the weight of speaking for damaged, depressed young fans while the fame machine expects only easy hits. Naming a friend's suicide and the depression rife among his peers and idols, the artist rejects the pacifying happiness the game demands, insisting his pain is real and his words are for the kids nobody listens to.","sonnet":"Framed by Brave New World soma-distribution announcements, a Polish rapper addresses fans who cut their wrists and carries the weight of their depression as his own responsibility — a friend who hanged himself, idols with depression, Instagram highlight reels masking misery — the intro is a sober reckoning with the cost of being a voice for the broken.","src":"haiku"},
"taconafide~art-b":{"haiku":"A person from a poor town lives as if they won the lottery, transcending their origins through music and determination. Nazi youth and societal collapse surround them while they build upward.","opus":"A Polish rapper narrates a rags-to-riches sprint, rising from a small-town favela to sudden wealth while hospital drips, a praying mother, and marching nationalists shadow the triumph. Taconafide, the duo of Taco Hemingway and Quebonafide, laces bragging with dread and dark historical name-drops, the winnings feeling less like arrival than exhausted survival amid unease about the country closing in.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper maps the vertigo of sudden success against a hometown shadowed by fascism and illness. Hospital stays, family prayers, studio isolation, and neo-Nazi marches outside the window all collapse into one dizzying year: a stray dog turned millionaire, still unsure what the wealth is for.","src":"haiku"},
"taconafide~ekodiesel":{"haiku":"A nocturnal narrative of escape and recklessness, with the speaker fleeing through cities in a car, filling up on fuel while avoiding systems and institutions.","opus":"A hyped, brand-drenched joyride through nocturnal driving and paranoia, the narrator half-blind, pumping eco-diesel at petrol stations while boasting about chart success, fleeing an unnamed system, and refusing hard drugs and vodka as beneath him now. Belgian street names and luxury cars pile up in a rush of bravado. Taconafide, the Polish duo of Taco Hemingway and Quebonafide, blend playful flexing with restless, coke-free self-mythology.","sonnet":"A late-night solo drive through Brussels, refueling at BP on biodiesel, mind half-gone from the road and the hour — the car becomes freedom from systems, charts, and categorization. The verse moves from highway philosophy to chart bragging to buying a strawberry shake at the drive-through, the whole thing held together by speed and the smell of diesel.","src":"haiku"},
"taconafide~kryptowaluty":{"haiku":"The speaker contrasts his steady financial discipline with others' volatile spending and cryptocurrency gambling. He remains grounded, avoids trends, and works for his mother and peace while others chase wealth and fame.","opus":"A rapper mocks a broke rival whose loyalties swing like cryptocurrency while he stashes Bitcoin under the mattress and moves steadily upward. Polish duo Taconafide, Taco Hemingway and Quebonafide, thread braggadocio with name-drops of Gombrowicz and football, insisting he works only for money and peace, jaded by leeches and haunted by a love he cannot make time for.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper asserts grounded self-sufficiency against the volatility of those around him, comparing an adversary to cryptocurrency — erratic and reckless with money and emotion. Between boasts about Bitcoin under the mattress, he reflects on artistic integrity, parasitic critics, and the exhausting effort to stay true to himself.","src":"haiku"},
"taconafide~tamagotchi":{"haiku":"Life reduced to cycles of eating, drinking, sleeping like a digital pet; searching for love and meaning amid shallow consumption and loneliness.","opus":"A restless, disillusioned life of fame, drink, and isolation where the narrator reduces existence to eat, drink, sleep like a Tamagotchi; he longs for a woman who's tired of him, feels emptiness amid smog, hotels, and dating apps, and wonders whether love can be ordered like takeout.","sonnet":"A Polish rapper compares his generation's hollow routine — eat, sleep, drink, scroll through phones — to caring for a Tamagotchi, while also describing his own isolation, creative burnout, and longing for a woman who has grown tired of him.","src":"haiku"},
"taipei-houston~as-the-sun-sets":{"haiku":"Entropy accelerates as speech loses power; the speaker feels consequences in real-time, watching themselves transform into shadow through consequence and inaction.","opus":"The slow, unavoidable arrival of consequences, landing all together as the day burns down after wasting time and thinking he had it all figured out. The dawning shock is reality itself finally landing, the question of how it feels now that it's real.","sonnet":"Drifting through a haze of numbing routine and unexamined habits, the speaker feels 'the effects' descend all at once as the sun sets — a psychoactive or emotional threshold reached — while questioning whether they can feel anything at all once it becomes real.","src":"haiku"},
"takanori-nishikawa~be-affected":{"haiku":"Interconnection and mutual influence define existence as nothing stands isolated or self-sufficient. The song celebrates dependency and relationship as fundamental to becoming human and possessing agency.","opus":"A reflection that nothing exists in isolation, every life and outcome shaped by the people and world around it. The one thing that's truly your own is your will, and the song urges releasing the courage to move forward, sustained by the web of others who made you who you are.","sonnet":"Everything in existence is linked and affected by everything else — the speaker urges the listener to release the courage to move forward, acknowledging that lives are shaped by the people and world around them, and that interdependence, not isolation, is the condition of all being.","src":"haiku"},
"takatak~the-whale":{"opus":"A sprint toward transcendence and judgment, the narrator bolting to the heart of the star where it all first began, warning that a boundary has been breached. Takatak's progressive metal stages arrogance and its correction, alarms blaring over a fault in the blueprint. It stretches past the self toward something enormous, demanding a purpose worth staying alive for.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a controlling, totalizing force — the narrator wants to escape to the center of themselves, identifies a design flaw in the system pressing on them, and challenges the authority demanding submission while interrogating whether their own existence can be justified.","src":"sonnet"},
"takatak~volition":{"haiku":"Stagnation and grey conformity replace authentic choice as systems rebuild people to maintain sameness forever. The song demands awakening and the reclamation of vision and agency from forces that benefit from numbness.","opus":"A fight to take back control from powers that drain the hues from you and remold you so nothing about your motives changes. Caught between deadness and terror, the singer holds onto the plain fact of being conscious and breathing in this instant, picking that fleeting present as where to start over.","sonnet":"Waking from a grey numbness into fear and aliveness simultaneously, the speaker resists a system that wants to grey out all colours and rebuild people into conformity — the moment of being awake, afraid, but alive is the only real moment, and the pieces that fall aside are the price of remaining one's own.","src":"haiku"},
"taken-by-tides~death-of-the-old-me":{"haiku":"Self-annihilation as rebirth: the old self must die for survival, and the death becomes declaration of freedom from those who broke it.","opus":"Emerging from betrayal by someone who broke the family, declaring the old self dead and refusing to be buried. The venom is directed at the destroyer, but the rebirth was self-made.","sonnet":"Someone who survived a deeply damaging relationship — one that fractured their sense of family — declares that while their betrayer tried to bury them, they transformed on their own terms and now see the other person as dead to them.","src":"haiku"},
"talk-talk~living-in-another-world-1997-remaster":{"haiku":"Mismatched perception separates lovers into parallel realities; tenderness exists in one world while the other drowns in incomprehension and alienation.","opus":"The vertigo of realizing you and your partner experienced the same relationship as two different truths, one seeing tenderness where the other saw hell. Mark Hollis makes a breakup into an epistemological maze about never truly sharing one world.","sonnet":"Two people who once shared tenderness occupied entirely different emotional worlds — what the speaker read as love, the other experienced as hell — and the breakdown was inevitable, misread at every turn by the one who believed in angels.","src":"haiku"},
"talking-heads~psycho-killer":{"haiku":"A nervous, tense person struggles with everyday conversation and basic function, their bed on fire like their mind. They confess confusion about what they've done while running away.","opus":"A jittery inhabitation of a killer's frayed nerves, the narrator tense and sleepless, warning others to run while switching into French mid-thought, unable to finish conversations and hating impoliteness. Talking Heads made this new-wave landmark an art-school study in psychological unease, David Byrne's twitchy delivery and the famous fa-fa-fa refrain turning menace into something oddly danceable, the mind of a murderer sketched from the inside without ever narrating a crime.","sonnet":"A killer's self-portrait delivered with excruciating composure — nerves strung tight, contempt for meaningless conversation, a voice that switches briefly into French to describe the night everything changed, the whole thing held together by a terror that is somehow polite, the danger in the fa-fa-fa refrain more unsettling for what it refuses to say.","src":"haiku"},
"tallah~for-the-recognition":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and resentment erupt as the overlooked reveal their dark secrets; they demand acknowledgment of their worth through exposure.","opus":"A nu-metalcore character piece voiced by a manipulator performing a psychological lobotomy on his victim, exposing that everything they achieved was hollow craving for recognition. Menacing nursery imagery of a hopping bunny frames the cruelty as he digs toward the buried core of vanity, promising to rip it out and leave them wallowing in filth.","sonnet":"A menacing voice — part surgeon, part therapist, part torturer — addresses someone raised to believe they could jump to the moon, exposing the vanity underneath their achievements and offering to evolve them by force, cutting out the core desire for recognition that drives everything they pretend is noble.","src":"haiku"},
"tallah~l-e-d":{"haiku":"Torn between hidden potential and self-hatred, wanting to shine while despising their reflection. Vilified and defamed, yet fighting for recognition.","opus":"A jagged descent into self-hatred and disappointment, the narrator bent out of shape, hating his own reflection, yet defiantly promising to blaze at full wattage the moment he gets one chance to shine. Light becomes both salvation and the path that led him to sin. Tallah, an American nu-metalcore band, deliver this volatile mix of vulnerability and venom with the whiplash dynamics and unhinged vocal shifts of their generation's genre revival.","sonnet":"The oscillation between self-loathing and explosive self-assertion — bent out of shape, hating the face in the mirror, and then pivoting into 1500-watt rage at anyone who underestimates what that broken person can still produce. The lights that led to sin are also the lights that finally let the speaker see themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"tallah~no-one-should-read-this":{"haiku":"Self-loathing and dangerous example poison the present; the speaker declares themselves unworthy of trust, admitting they inspire hatred and violence while damned to carry that knowledge.","opus":"An unreliable, self-loathing confession from someone who lies compulsively and invites others to make him the excuse for their own hatred and violence. Beneath the provocation lies an unforgivable loss he can't reconcile, tugged too tight to speak aloud.","sonnet":"A confession that dismantles itself: the speaker admits to making things up, seducing people into rooting against him, living as a \"simple-minded criminal,\" and then reveals the wound underneath — a woman who broke her spine and became his keeper, whose departure he cannot forgive, the grip of that dynamic described as something that tugged unbearably tight.","src":"haiku"},
"tallah~overconfidence":{"haiku":"Arrogant self-presentation masks deeper insecurity and dependence on external validation. Confidence becomes a trap that enslaves the speaker to their own façade.","opus":"A fractured mind at war with itself, the swaggering bravado exposed as a crutch propping up a self on the edge of collapse. The invented figure the singer bargains with makes overconfidence a mask worn over dread and dissociation.","sonnet":"A chaotic, self-implicating portrait of someone whose ego and hysterical overreaction are simultaneously their crutch and their cage — they rage against a submission dynamic while acknowledging they've always been enslaved to the pain of their own confidence.","src":"haiku"},
"tallah~red-light":{"haiku":"Existential horror erupts in cryptic fragments of confinement, where the narrator inhabits a surreal prison of surveillance and decay while grappling with loss and the collapse of meaning.","opus":"A deranged, blood-soaked narrative of a boy tangled with a dead girl and a murderous compulsion he can't stop, girls losing their heads around his block. It is about madness dressed as poetry, romanticizing violence as a sunrise while rivers run red.","sonnet":"A fractured, hallucinatory monologue in which the speaker describes feeling locked out of ordinary reality, tethered to a dead or lost woman, and edging toward violence, the \"red light\" framing a sense of transgression and heresy bleeding into urban menace.","src":"haiku"},
"tallah~shaken-not-stirred":{"haiku":"Obsessive boundary-crossing creates psychological dissolution as the narrator pursues connection into impossible depths. The song frames pursuit as misdirected and ultimately transforms desire into regret and isolation.","opus":"An extended, grotesque metaphor of reshaping someone into a fish to be dumped down the drain, standing in for the moment you stop trying to save a person you love and let them sink alone, keeping only the regret.","sonnet":"A failed attempt to reach into someone's soul is processed through the image of a fish sinking into a drain — the speaker tried to walk the blurring line, flipped the other's spine into a fin and called it a win, but in the end the person couldn't be changed, and the speaker swims away with nothing but regret.","src":"haiku"},
"tallah~telescope":{"opus":"A revenge fantasy in which a hunter stalks the wealthy and powerful through fog and dead ends until he cuts them down. Tallah's nu-metal chaos frames retaliation as human instinct, a bloodthirsty crowd and shattered illusions of safety looming large. Casting off wasted regret, it becomes the overlooked striking back at those who dismissed the rules.","sonnet":"A multi-threaded fury at systemic violence and authority — corrupt cops who enjoy watching people suffer on pavement, rich men who disappear into fog while the poor absorb their damage — combined with a personal rebirth narrative about refusing to stay down after being wronged.","src":"sonnet"},
"tallah~the-silo":{"haiku":"Compulsive service and manipulation lock the speaker in dependence; they exist to please while being kept under thumb, unable to refuse or escape the cycle.","opus":"A servile figure who lives to please is deceived and dominated by a woman who keeps him under her thumb, hidden away like something shameful. It is about willing subjugation mistaken for devotion, a private hell he keeps selling to himself.","sonnet":"The speaker lives to please — to serve, to perform — but is manipulated by a controlling partner who keeps him \"under thumb\" in the back of the cabinet; his confession comes in a corner of names and shame, wondering whether what he did was right or wrong, unable to refuse what has become his home.","src":"haiku"},
"tallah~we-the-sad":{"haiku":"Depression and self-loathing spiral inward as parasitic shame infiltrates every thought, creating a prison of internalized oppression where society's cruelty merges with one's own violence.","opus":"A recluse crippled by insecurity personifies his own depression as a devouring mother-figure who keeps him drowning in his own filth. It is about mental illness as possession, and a collective plea from the sad and diseased to be relieved and pulled up.","sonnet":"A man voices total subjugation by his own mind, personified as a female possessor and coffin, while parasites swaying in his gut and \"every violent thought\" multiply unchecked; he speaks for a collective of the depressed and diseased who need rescue they cannot request.","src":"haiku"},
"tame-impala~let-it-happen":{"opus":"Surrendering to inevitable change and letting a coming upheaval carry away everything unanchored, exhausted from running from one's own shadow and finally ready to move on.","sonnet":"A person on the edge of a major psychological or life transformation finally stops resisting the overwhelming force of change bearing down on them, surrendering to the whirlwind — possibly death, possibly rebirth — and realizing they may have always been ready.","src":"sonnet"},
"tame-impala~the-less-i-know-the-better":{"haiku":"Tortured observation of lost love with rival, resignation that knowledge of betrayal makes suffering worse.","opus":"Kevin Parker's woozy disco-psych heartbreak, the narrator gutted to find the girl he wants holding hands with a rival named Trevor. He oscillates between spite, longing, and willful ignorance, half-wishing not to know while unable to look away. Beneath the funk lies the ordinary humiliation of jealousy and a love that keeps circling back despite itself.","sonnet":"Unrequited love made unbearable by proximity — the narrator sees her holding Trevor's hand, gets the advice to try Heather, and yet can't stop watching her on someone else's shoulder every night, still begging her not to make him wait forever. \"Come on Superman, say your stupid line\" closes on bitter irony: even the hero's script is a cliché he can't escape.","src":"haiku"},
"tankard~die-with-a-beer-in-your-hand":{"haiku":"Celebrates metal devotion and defiant excess, framing alcohol-fueled rebellion and heroic defiance as the ultimate life choice.","opus":"A rowdy, tongue-in-cheek celebration of metal, warfare and drinking, jeering at teetotal rivals and pledging to go out swinging with a full glass in hand.","sonnet":"Cast in the language of fantasy battle, the song celebrates a warrior ethos where fighting hard and drinking beer are fused into a single defiant last stand — die with honour, beer in hand.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tankard raids the full arsenal of grim metal machismo — a soul pawned to the Devil, forged anew in armor, a circling wolfpack howling under the moon, demons beaten down and driven home to the pit — and blows all of it on lager, so the gag is that every trapping of epic evil gets mustered behind the least epic cause conceivable. The band knows precisely what it's up to: bragging that a craving for pints trumps the grand sagas everyone else keeps spinning is a straight jab at metal's self-serious mythmaking, Tankard staking its claim as the scene's court jester who rates the whole warrior posture below a cold one. The refrain then plays it sincere beneath the parody, converting drink-till-you-drop into an honest go-down-swinging creed — die with a beer in your hand — the band's actual ethic dressed as a punchline: better to exit devoted to something magnificently dumb than to endure by taking yourself too seriously.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["comedy & wordplay","party & hedonism","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"epic metal arsenal mustered behind the least epic cause conceivable","built":"the refrain plays sincere beneath the parody, converting the punchline into the band's actual creed"},
"tankard~horde":{"haiku":"Interdimensional parasites and plague-carrying entities corrupt humanity and institutions from invisible realms. The song frames evil as organized invading force that systematically dismantles peace and spreads despair.","opus":"Cosmic-horror thrash about invisible things from the void slipping into our world to sow plague, despair and war. Their smoldering fury has corrupted mankind since the dawn of time, remaking us in their image — a hatred that outlasts even death.","sonnet":"Unseen entities from beyond death — crossing the Rubicon from the void — arrive as bringers of plague, corrupting dreams, seeding despair year by year since the dawn of mankind, their burning rage consuming the world as parasites that have always been here turning humanity into themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"tanner~t-v-casualty":{"haiku":"Visual pollution and technological sedation replace authentic experience; television casualty remains trapped watching blue-filtered existence unfold.","opus":"A portrait of a television-addicted shut-in rotting in filth, absorbing only blue screen light, physically sick yet unable to look away. The Misfits cover extends TV into womb and grave, cars grazing at cemeteries beside TV brands, life lived and died in front of the set.","sonnet":"A figure submerged in domestic squalor and television addiction — paint smears, vapor rub, blue-filtered light absorbing through eyeballs — watches flies in a corner and begs nobody to feed the screen; the imagery of jaguars and Cadillacs and Sonys grazing at a grave makes the television itself the burial plot.","src":"haiku"},
"tantric~breakdown":{"opus":"An address to someone who appears to have it all together but is secretly falling apart inside, plagued by self-blame and voices; the singer, who knows the coming breakdown intimately, warns them to check themselves before they check out.","sonnet":"A warning to someone who seems in control on the surface but is cracking underneath — built on lies, hearing internal voices, nearing collapse — the narrator has seen this breakdown coming and urges the person to find themselves before they check out entirely.","src":"sonnet"},
"tanukichan~it-gets-easier":{"opus":"The quiet resignation of being the one who leaves, knowing love survives only until it cracks and that you ultimately choose which of your loves you take with you.","sonnet":"About the end of a relationship and the hard truth that you can choose who you love but not always the damage left behind — the speaker is leaving but not without grief, aware that love survives until it cracks.","src":"sonnet"},
"tanuki~the-witch":{"opus":"A folk-horror tale of a doomed encounter with a witch in the woods whose empty-eyed, open-armed welcome lures the wanderers to their deaths, leaving no peace and no way to escape.","sonnet":"A group drawn into a forest by a mysterious presence — a witch or spirit — finds itself ensnared with no way out, warned too late that following her call meant their lives, and urged to burn whatever connection they have before it consumes them all.","src":"sonnet"},
"tanuki~your-friendly-local-arsonist":{"haiku":"A persona celebrates destructive revelry and burning truth away, claiming that destruction and honest chaos beat silent complicity.","opus":"Fire courses through the narrator with lethal purpose as flames engulf everything familiar, sterilizing and erasing all trace of it. He turns clinical and delighted, framing arson as the nearest thing to an honest existence free of pretense, eager hands prying apart the mystery while ash and black rain blanket a sky the dead can no longer grieve for.","sonnet":"Arson becomes a metaphor for radical honesty and collective destruction of false identities. The fire burns away pretense and cover-up, exposing those who were trusted. Living close to danger is presented as the only honest mode of existence, with total obliteration framed as liberation rather than catastrophe.","src":"haiku"},
"taoubt~circle-of-mirrors":{"haiku":"Multiplicity hidden by similarity: reflections confuse the self, and division masquerades as growth until the illusion of unity shatters.","opus":"A psychedelic meditation on ego death and rebirth, where the emptiness between two people is the doorway through the self. Letting go so growth can begin, seeing through the illusion of separateness.","sonnet":"A song about self-examination through the shock of another person seeing through you — the speaker is reborn through that vulnerability, letting go of old pain so something new can grow, recognizing that what divides us is also what forces transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"taoubt~ship-of-theseus":{"haiku":"Identity forms from accumulated experience and influence; originality is impossible because the self is always already a copy of others.","opus":"A sardonic meditation on originality, arguing every self is a distillation of everyone else, a duplicate of a duplicate insisting it's uniquely different. The paradox lands in being completely original in exactly the way everyone is, since no one is anyone but themselves.","sonnet":"A philosophical challenge to the cult of individual uniqueness — arguing that every person is just a distillation of everyone they've absorbed, a copy of a copy — and that the pressure to be original is both real and absurd, since no one is truly anybody else.","src":"haiku"},
"taproot~poem":{"haiku":"The song describes panic and drowning effects that plague the mind, written as a personal meditation on survival. It frames itself as a tool for helping oneself and others move forward through pain.","opus":"Using the song itself as an emergency tool to survive panic attacks, a note-to-self that the pain is temporary. The generosity of it is turning private suffering into permission for someone else to break the glass and move on too.","sonnet":"A self-addressed song about surviving panic attacks and psychological distress — the narrator writes it as a tool to keep himself alive, a break-glass-in-emergency reminder that the pain passes and the only way through is to move on into yourself.","src":"haiku"},
"taproot~stay-away":{"haiku":"A person mourns the loss of a deeply loyal companion, expressing regret for past hurts and the pain of separation while unable to move on from their absence.","opus":"A grieving address to someone gone, half a lifetime of loyal friendship now missed more than words can express; the deeper read is guilt-laden mourning, the narrator haunted by memories and begging forgiveness for the pain he caused and the apology he never made in time.","sonnet":"A grief song addressed to a lost companion — almost certainly a dog, given the pedigree reference, the unconditional loyalty, and the acceptance of physical rough treatment without complaint — the narrator is wrecked by the loss and full of regret for every time he crossed a line with someone who loved him without condition.","src":"haiku"},
"target~hordes-of-insanity":{"haiku":"Metal and thrashing crowds dissolve rationality into pure instinctual fury, where the mosh pit becomes an arena of controlled chaos and temporary escape.","opus":"A thrash-metal ode to the moshpit as release, where a wall of noise drains rationality and lets a crowd purge its rage together. German 80s thrash celebrating the show itself as sanctioned collective madness.","sonnet":"A thrash metal crowd experience described as a collective release of aggression and adrenaline, where the music obliterates rational thought and transforms the audience into a unified, moshing horde.","src":"haiku"},
"tarot-blood~bastardized":{"haiku":"Sacred truth decays into corruption: systems built on hollow faith crumble when the god they promised never arrives, leaving only the ruins of belief.","opus":"Faith in an absent god corrupts everything it once held sacred, and the speaker who dared to ask why is branded the bastard. It reclaims that insult, becoming the ruined thing they named.","sonnet":"A song about losing faith and feeling corrupted by the loss — the speaker devoted themselves to a religious light that has gone dark, prayed to an altar no one is listening to, and has become the very bastardized thing they feared.","src":"haiku"},
"tarot~i-rule":{"haiku":"A monstrous being born of death claims dominion over existence. It's unholy, destructive, and revels in desecration and chaos.","opus":"A monstrous first-person boast of a demonic/undead being: born by tearing his mother apart and gnawing his father's bones, covered in fur and fangs, an insult to the divine who claims damnation and dominion as his own, relishing that the flesh he devours is the listener's.","sonnet":"A demonic or werewolf-like narrator declares total supremacy — born through his mother's death, filled with razorteeth by the moon, feeding on flesh, beyond death's reach and beyond all divine judgment — in pure boastful horror-metal terms.","src":"haiku"},
"tarot~pyre-of-gods":{"haiku":"Believers reject all gods as false fathers of lies and plan a revolutionary burning to free themselves from religious slavery and sacrifice.","opus":"A triumphant war on religion itself, the narrator and his fellow rebels swearing to cast out the sources of falsehood, torch every idol, and reject bondage even beyond the grave, insisting all deities are born, die and must answer in blood. Revolt against divine authority becomes a crusade of unbelief. The Finnish power-metal band, featuring future Nightwish bassist Marco Hietala, deliver it with soaring, muscular conviction.","sonnet":"A collective uprising against all divine authority — gods are declared mortal, their lies named, their demand for sacrifice refused, the jailer's head taken as the price of liberation. The pyre burns not out of despair but as a deliberate act of heresy by people who have decided no slavery survives death.","src":"haiku"},
"taska-black~lights-out":{"haiku":"Spiraling dependency and loss corrode the speaker's grip on identity; walking the edge and confessing sin barely hold back the darkness that rises with each passing dawn.","opus":"Grief and guilt over losing a close friend to death that was no accident, numbed with drinking until 7am and losing himself again. Turning the lights out becomes the wish to blot out the memory and the blood he feels on his hands, pleading with the old friend to see he has changed.","sonnet":"A speaker drinking until 7 a.m. in the aftermath of losing a friend — blood on hands, asking holy water to wash them clean — cannot hold onto himself and asks for the lights to go out as the only honest response to guilt and grief that won't resolve.","src":"haiku"},
"tatiana-hazel~don-t-need-you":{"haiku":"Self-worth becomes irrelevant when judgment is inevitable; the speaker refuses the game entirely, rejecting both cruelty and the cost of emotional investment.","opus":"Breaking free of a person whose whole aim is belittling others, opting for standing on your own against a crowd that will resent you regardless of how you behave.","sonnet":"About the wish to cut loose from people who will hate you no matter what — the narrator rehearses independence but keeps drifting toward darkness, wondering whether any of it is worth it when judgment is inevitable; the 'I don't need you' is half assertion, half struggle to believe it.","src":"haiku"},
"tatiana-hazel~downer":{"haiku":"Addicted to destructive presence, drawn to damage because it mirrors internal brokenness.","opus":"The pull of an addictive, draining lover, the narrator honest that she can't save him yet hooked on his gloom because she found in him something she'd lost in herself. Codependency named clearly and stayed in anyway.","sonnet":"About being toxically entangled with someone who drains you — the narrator recognizes the other person hasn't changed despite claims otherwise, knows she can't save them, yet admits she's addicted to the dynamic, finding in their negativity something that mirrors a void in herself.","src":"haiku"},
"tatiana-hazel~floodgate":{"haiku":"Enabling codependency drains the self; breaking free requires releasing others' burdens and reclaiming personal agency.","opus":"A synth-pop reckoning with the fatigue of loving someone stuck repeating their errors. The narrator, worn out from trying to be everything to another, finally picks self-preservation, urging them to let their burden go and open up. Its arc moves from martyrdom to control, from sinking under someone else to reclaiming the right to feel secure.","sonnet":"The speaker has reached the limit of absorbing someone else's recurring mistakes — carrying their deadweight until she herself is sinking — and the floodgate opens not as anger but as exhausted self-preservation: let go, start again, try to be everything to you has left nothing for me.","src":"haiku"},
"tatsuya-kitani~a-1iaryc4":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"Kitani came up as a Vocaloid producer before stepping to the mic himself, and his literary bent shapes this elegy for a blue summer of youth. Sharing a season where both were nobody, the narrator failed to notice the sadness behind a friend's smile, and now only a silent question about meeting again stays lodged in the throat as that friend scatters like a falling flower.","sonnet":"The blue summer of youth shared with someone who drifted away — the narrator failed to notice the sadness behind their smile, the season where they were both nobody is fading into memory, and the only thing that remains is a silent voice asking \"we'll meet again, right?\" stuck in the throat as the friend scatters like a flower falling.","src":"haiku"},
"tau-cross~lazarus":{"haiku":"A crucified figure declares resurrection and freedom from the lies that bound him, rising from spiritual death to reclaim agency.","opus":"Tau Cross fuse crust-punk grit with folk mysticism, reclaiming the biblical resurrection as an act of defiance. Buried under earth and layered in superstition, the figure refuses to stay dead, rising up to declare himself alive and to master forbidden, otherworldly powers. The myth becomes a symbol of unbreakable will, the risen one returning to the source he came from.","sonnet":"Resurrection as self-liberation from inherited lies — the speaker has been buried under superstition and manipulation, scarecrow-still, but emerges determined to drink from older and deeper sources, forge his own keys, and walk back into the sea he came from, Lazarus here refusing to stay dead for anyone else's story.","src":"haiku"},
"taylor-swift~bad-blood-feat-kendrick-lamar-taylor-s-version":{"haiku":"Betrayal calcifies into festering resentment as both parties dwell on irreconcilable wounds; neither forgiveness nor forgetting can heal the damage inflicted.","opus":"A friendship-turned-feud where betrayal cut too deep to mend, and apologies ring hollow. Swift makes a personal falling-out into a hardened anthem about wounds that don't heal, with Kendrick's verses sharpening it into open combat.","sonnet":"A friendship turned bitter after a deep betrayal is now beyond repair — both the narrator and a guest verse from Kendrick Lamar assert that the wound was too serious for apologies to fix, and the damage has permanently defined the relationship.","src":"haiku"},
"taylor-swift~gorgeous":{"haiku":"Attraction refuses to be contained even as it warps into jealousy and frustration, the speaker paralyzed by desire she cannot articulate.","opus":"The furious, helpless comedy of being attracted to someone you can't have, resentment and desire tangled so tight that beauty itself feels like an insult. Swift plays infatuation as a grievance, hating him precisely because he's irresistible.","sonnet":"The speaker is helplessly, furiously attracted to someone she cannot have, unable to say a word to their face while the infatuation consumes her common sense and her existing relationship. Beauty becomes a source of frustration and helpless longing that collapses into self-deprecating humor.","src":"haiku"},
"team-sleep~ataraxia":{"haiku":"Two lovers sink together into deep water and sea as they dream, sailing downward while one sings to the other as they descend into sleep and water.","opus":"A dream unfolds at sea, two figures adrift and singing as their boat sinks beneath the waves in serene surrender. Team Sleep, the ambient side project fronted by Chino Moreno of Deftones, suits the drowsy imagery, the title borrowing a word for untroubled calm, so that sinking together reads as peace rather than terror in the coma-deep reverie.","sonnet":"A recurring dream of drowning at sea with someone beloved, rendered not as nightmare but as peace. The companion rows, sings, and watches waves as both figures sink — the unconscious mind returning again and again to a loss that feels safer than waking.","src":"haiku"},
"team-sleep~blvd-nights":{"opus":"A hazy, dreamlike sketch of surrendering to a compelling woman who takes the narrator apart, capturing the helpless pull of an obsessive attraction he knows won't stop once it begins.","sonnet":"A fragmentary portrait of a compulsive dynamic with a woman who completely undoes the narrator — once started, neither of them can stop, and the song captures that helpless loop of knowing the behavior will continue regardless of intention.","src":"sonnet"},
"tears-for-fears~shout":{"haiku":"A person demands liberation from oppressive systems and manipulative control, urging others to rebel against those who exploit them.","opus":"Shaped by the primal-therapy ideas behind the British duo, this arena-sized anthem urges the listener to release bottled-up frustration and refuse to be bought in brutal times. It casts off the narrow-minded expectations imposed by others, channeling protest into raw catharsis, and became a defining statement of the pair's eighties peak.","sonnet":"A call to voice what has been suppressed under pressure — in violent times, against one-track minds who reduce people to function, the act of shouting is both protest and survival. The undercurrent is darker: even love is framed as something that might prefer to break what it cannot change.","src":"haiku"},
"ted-nugent~stranglehold":{"haiku":"A wild, aggressive lover claims possession and control over a partner, boasting of power and dominance in the relationship.","opus":"Ted Nugent's signature jam swaggers on pure predatory bravado, a narrator likening himself to a dog in heat who will burn down any house in his path. He boasts of a stranglehold on a lover who wronged him, escalating into open menace about crushing her face. The blues-rock strut and endless come-ons frame a machismo so unbothered it reads as caricature, cruising a road he calls his own and answering to no one.","sonnet":"A narrator frames himself as an unstoppable predatory force — relentless on the road, dominant in every encounter, claiming immortality as though sheer will alone exempts him from death. Domination over a departed lover becomes the central image of that unchecked power.","src":"haiku"},
"teddy-killerz~clone":{"haiku":"Mass programming reduces humanity to automata; the speaker refuses assimilation and demands individual autonomy.","opus":"A refusal to be programmed into one of the mindless drones a controlling system wants, choosing to break the chains and step out on one's own rather than surrender to conformity.","sonnet":"A rejection of social programming and mass conformity: the speaker refuses to be a mindless drone, ripping the veil away from 'the future they're defining' — 'it's time to kill the noise' — the repeated 'I don't wanna be a clone' as both declaration and resistance drill.","src":"haiku"},
"teen-mortgage~away":{"haiku":"Planetary collapse and climate catastrophe offer escape from personal numbness and societal apathy.","opus":"A grungy blast of climate dread cut with personal burnout, the narrator feeling useless and jaded while wading through excuses. The refrain of the poles thawing turns environmental catastrophe into a numbing background drone. It captures a generation's paralysis, fully aware of the unfolding disaster yet unable to function except while high, watching the world quietly disappear.","sonnet":"Personal uselessness and planetary collapse held in the same frame: wading through mountains of excuses while ice caps melt away — the repetition of away is both emotional avoidance and the actual disappearance of something real, and the speaker's jaded numbness mirrors the world's.","src":"haiku"},
"teen-mortgage~doctor":{"haiku":"Psychological manipulation dressed as healing; enslaved by someone masquerading as savior.","opus":"A garage-punk snarl about a toxic, drug-tangled relationship and the loss of control it breeds. The narrator, fed ideas and medicine alike, insists he is no doctor and cannot fix the person spinning out around him. Knives in the back and a body in his bed pull him toward the grave, institutional dread bleeding through the swagger.","sonnet":"A destructive relationship figured as medical dependency — ideas in the head not on the neck, medicine in the hand, body in the bed making a slave of the narrator — the refrain insists he is not a doctor and she is spinning out, and the trajectory is clear: put your body in my band and you made me, and it ends with a grave.","src":"haiku"},
"teen-mortgage~no":{"haiku":"Coercive exploitation masquerades as opportunity as the speaker is seduced into selling themselves for empty promises, their refusal drowned in the machinery of desire.","opus":"A sardonic takedown of coerced sexualized labor, a boss commanding a worker to dance, smile, and sell herself for a paycheck framed as a beautiful economy. The relentless refusal of the chorus is the answer, saying no to a system that treats a person as merchandise for a promotion.","sonnet":"A worker being pressured to perform, smile, and sell themselves for economic reward: management language — \"work it,\" \"smile,\" \"that's why you were chosen\" — frames the exploitation as opportunity, and every line of flattery earns only a flat \"nah, nah, no.\"","src":"haiku"},
"teen-mortgage~oligarchy":{"haiku":"Wealth inequality and corporate power concentrate at the top while workers suffer deprivation, creating a system where the few rule through manufactured narratives.","opus":"A blunt class complaint about the rats gaming the system from ivory towers while single mothers scrape by at the discount aisle. The joke about getting your politics from a cartoon meme skewers a public fed endless contradictory truths, the whole thing rigged for the wealthy few.","sonnet":"A sardonic pop-punk critique of wealth inequality and corporate political control — the oligarchy makes the laws, mothers shop at dollar stores while cowards enforce long hours, and the media floods people with contradictory 'truths'; the song is cheerful about its own outrage.","src":"haiku"},
"teen-mortgage~s-w-a-s":{"haiku":"Systemic machinery and automated servitude define human existence; the speaker performs compliance while dying incrementally within the machine.","opus":"A paranoid, drug-hazed burnout waiting on the law and watched by the NSA, forcing a service-industry smile while dying a little inside in a future owned by robots.","sonnet":"About grinding through the indignity of working life while using drugs — the speaker is waiting on cops, coming down from cocaine in a bathroom stall, grinding his jaw, and quietly dying inside while plastering on a customer-service smile.","src":"haiku"},
"teen-mortgage~sick-day":{"haiku":"Escape work through truancy and company. Refusal crystallizes into small acts of freedom and defiance.","opus":"A slacker anthem of blowing off work to get high and party, texting a flimsy excuse to the boss out of sheer disdain for taking orders.","sonnet":"A comedy song about skipping work to get high at home — the narrator smokes weed, texts their boss a barely apologetic excuse about being too sick to come in, refuses to help even when things at work fall apart, and celebrates the rare pleasure of doing nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"teen-mortgage~such-is-life":{"haiku":"Corporate manipulation of values and outrage, cynical engineering of fear and division masked as choice.","opus":"A cynical takedown of corporate manipulation, manufactured outrage, and manufactured scapegoats, freedom of speech granted then revoked, feelings mass-produced. The shrug of the title masks real anger at engineered division.","sonnet":"A critique of systemic manipulation — corporate and political forces manufacture division, hook people young on poison and scapegoating, and dress it all up in free speech rhetoric; the narrator observes the machine cynically and concludes only that this is simply how things are.","src":"haiku"},
"teenage-dads~cheerleader":{"haiku":"Nostalgia for unchanging time battles the inevitability of loss; the speaker wants to freeze precious moments before drift and departure steal them forever.","opus":"Regret over letting someone go while clinging to the belief that right now is the golden era worth preserving. It's about the ache of change and the refusal to accept that a happy chapter had to close.","sonnet":"Nostalgia and anxiety about change: the speaker looks back at a friendship or relationship letting go of something good, wonders if these really are the good old days, and holds on to the hope that no one actually has to leave — that the slide into adulthood isn't inevitable if they just stay close enough.","src":"haiku"},
"teenage-wrist~dark-sky":{"haiku":"Artificial luminescence obscures natural truth; the speaker grieves the absence of stars beneath manufactured light, mourning what human excess has erased.","opus":"Glowing skies and grinding ambition treated as twin diseases, the singer an ailing insect feeding on stale news who cannot recall the last time he saw a star. He begs to preserve the night, to be remade in a shared and visible world, grieving a natural wonder that loses its spell once you see how the illusion works.","sonnet":"Light pollution as metaphor for modern delusion — the speaker, a \"sickened summer fly\" under an oppressive skyline, can no longer see stars or the man in the moon, yearning to save the dark sky from the American hustle of spectacle and fool's gold.","src":"haiku"},
"teenage-wrist~earth-is-a-black-hole":{"haiku":"Humanity consumed by extraction and uncertainty heads toward collapse; shame and chemical imbalance trap people in bloated, broken systems.","opus":"A numbed narrator drifts through a fading world, feeling weightless yet sick within as water sources vanish and an end date looms. Teenage Wrist cloak this ecological and existential dread in shoegaze-tinged alt-rock, with drilled crude and worshipped machinery signaling a burnt-out generation idling toward collapse with nowhere left to turn.","sonnet":"A generational reckoning with ecological and societal collapse: a speaker caught between feeling weightless and being hollowed out by systemic rot, forced to conform in a world running dry. The refrain of a looming zero-day captures the dread of inevitable catastrophe no one has prepared for.","src":"haiku"},
"teenage-wrist~still-love":{"haiku":"A broken figure loves despite shame and violence. The serpent cycle continues—children inherit what parents destroyed.","opus":"A self-loathing plea for the ability to keep loving despite being built from broken pieces and bad choices, opening out into eco-grief — the idea that only by learning to accept ourselves could we ever have cared for one another and the dying planet.","sonnet":"A song about feeling fundamentally broken and guilty but discovering that love persists through it — the narrator confesses to being vile, shameful, full of bad habits and nothing to back them up, then broadens into a wider lament about humanity's self-destruction, yet keeps returning to the defiant and uncertain question of whether love can still exist in such a creature.","src":"haiku"},
"teenage-wrist~taste-of-gasoline":{"haiku":"Numbness and addiction trap people in cycles of destructive desires they know will leave only bitterness and fumes.","opus":"A numb plea for oblivion dressed up as a lullaby: the speaker admits to isolation and a crooked despair, then shrugs it off as nothing to worry over, half-comforting a listener while confessing a shared appetite for the things that ruin them. Teenage Wrist trade in exactly this alt-nineties nostalgia, fuzz-soaked shoegaze grunge where self-destruction is delivered in a warm, almost tender melodic haze rather than a scream.","sonnet":"A shared malaise where no one feels better than anyone else — everyone numbed out, sleeping through their lives, drawn compulsively toward the things that corrode them. The narrator knows the pattern is self-destructive and names it evil, but can't summon alarm over it; just a crooked-smiled shrug at mortality and a vague wish to meet someone halfway.","src":"haiku"},
"teeth~deathrace":{"haiku":"Mental isolation and breakdown; fear spirals into hypochondria and the sense that one is losing sanity under confinement.","opus":"the paranoia, hypochondria and mental breakdown of isolation during a plague or pandemic","sonnet":"pandemic-era mental breakdown — isolation, hypochondria, and the feeling of being trapped in a collective catastrophe","src":"haiku"},
"teeth~writhe":{"opus":"Pure predatory loathing, the narrator promising to strip flesh from bone and deliver his target to the soil. Teeth's downtempo deathcore stages misanthropy as apocalypse, cowards begging while rats scratch inside the walls. There's no redemption sought, only the relish of watching someone rot, hate held back until it consumes.","sonnet":"Pure violent contempt directed at a specific person — the narrator fantasizes about stripping them to the bone, making them beg, consuming them entirely, and watching them rot into the ground as punishment for some unstated crime of cowardice.","src":"sonnet"},
"telltale~breathe":{"opus":"A defiant kiss-off to an abuser who once wished the narrator harm; he refuses to make excuses for them or hand over chances they haven't earned, insisting some people are incapable of changing and that the passage of years mends nothing. He and his circle are simply better off cutting them loose.","sonnet":"A song about cutting off an irredeemably toxic person — someone who wished the narrator dead, lied to mutual friends, and refused to grow is finally being expelled. The weight on the chest at every breath is the ongoing presence of that person's shadow, and the bridge confirms they're actually doing better without them.","src":"sonnet"},
"temple-of-the-dog~call-me-a-dog":{"haiku":"Someone accepts being called a dog because they have fallen and dig for truth; they carry bad luck but promise to call their accuser beautiful if they ever respond.","opus":"A wounded lover concedes every insult thrown at him yet refuses to grovel, promising that when his turn comes he will answer cruelty with grace or silence rather than revenge. There is pride in the resignation, and a warning that bad luck clings to whoever keeps him leashed. Temple of the Dog were the 1991 Seattle supergroup born from grief for Andrew Wood, and this smoldering ballad shows Chris Cornell at his most soulful and restrained.","sonnet":"Accepting every insult leveled at you while refusing to return them in kind — degraded, thrown out, accused of failing and digging for gold and coming up empty, the speaker absorbs it all and resolves that when his turn comes to strike back, he'll only call the other person beautiful, or say nothing at all.","src":"haiku"},
"temple-of-the-dog~hunger-strike":{"haiku":"Moral refusal to exploit the powerless while the wealthy exploit and profit; the speaker starves rather than feed on those already crushed.","opus":"A refusal to prey on the defenseless while one's own share already spills over, choosing self-denial over complicity in a system that exploits infants and laborers. Two vocalists, one an emerging future star, share the lead on this 1991 memorial-project milestone, their pairing turning moral abstention into an anthem of principled going-without amid excess.","sonnet":"A moral refusal to consume what excess produces while others starve — the speaker will steal from the comfortable but cannot prey on the powerless. A portrait of a corrupted system where babies are farmed and slaves labor while those with overfull cups still go hungry by choice.","src":"haiku"},
"temple-of-the-dog~pushin-forward-back":{"haiku":"Struggling against abandonment and maternal pressure, pushing forward despite impossible odds. The speaker fights to survive and move beyond a cycle of deprivation and loss.","opus":"A primal cycle of striving and backsliding, a child embraced by a loving mother then cast aside, driving ahead only to be shoved back as rulers topple and dead heroes enrich the soil. Temple of the Dog were the one-off Seattle supergroup honoring Andrew Wood, and this churning grunge track captures a struggle that keeps caving in on itself, with redemption barely glimpsed.","sonnet":"A foundling's restless drive — taken in and nurtured, then pushing away before anyone could let go, the pattern of advance and retreat repeating through generations, fallen heroes feeding the ground underneath him, until the falling itself becomes a kind of knowing and he realizes he won't drown after all.","src":"haiku"},
"temple-of-the-dog~reach-down":{"haiku":"A visionary seeks to lift crowds and carry them toward a promised land of redemption, love, and salvation despite personal exhaustion.","opus":"Chris Cornell's dreamlike elegy for Andrew Wood, whose death prompted this band's formation. The narrator envisions his lost friend at rest, then hands him a mission to lift others toward something better. Sprawling and soaring, it names love rather than any substance as what truly mattered, turning private grief into a benediction.","sonnet":"A dream-visitation from someone gone — the speaker encounters them in a bar, glittering and at ease, offering blessing and permission to rest, and wakes carrying a sense of lightness and love that doesn't ask for mourning, only for reaching back down toward the crowd with whatever grace was left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"temple-of-the-dog~say-hello-2-heaven":{"haiku":"A lament for someone who died from violence and hardship on the streets, the speaker mourning an inability to reach them and left only to remember and grieve.","opus":"Chris Cornell's elegy for Andrew Wood, the Mother Love Bone singer whose fatal overdose gave rise to Temple of the Dog. Speaking to the lost friend who rose from one place and fell to another, the song snuffs out a candle and sends him onward, warmth felt even where it can no longer catch. Grief and helplessness fill a tribute that built a band out of loss itself.","sonnet":"A eulogy for a man who came from an island and died on the street, his suffering unvoiced and unheard until it was too late. The narrator mourns the unwritten things — all they will never do together — and releases him upward with a farewell that doubles as an admission of helplessness before grief.","src":"haiku"},
"temple-of-the-dog~times-of-trouble":{"haiku":"Addiction and despair cycle endlessly as the speaker and their community destroy themselves through substances and hopelessness. Holding time becomes the only resistance to final surrender.","opus":"A plea to a friend sinking into heroin addiction to hold on rather than throw their life away, written in the shadow of Andrew Wood's overdose.","sonnet":"A direct address to someone falling into addiction and despair — the hot spoon, the needle, the closed shades — urging them to hold on to their time rather than kill it, because someone pushed them to the ledge but they can still break through.","src":"haiku"},
"temples~jet-stream-heart":{"haiku":"The heart accelerates through jet stream of modern existence as speed defines the speaker. Hearts beat faster through technological winds.","opus":"A shimmering neo-psych reverie built from fleeting sensory flashes — seaside drinks, flickering signals, late-night warmth caught through a lens. Urging surrender to the beat over retreat, it chases a euphoric jolt right to the core. Nostalgia threads through it, a lifetime felt to have slipped into the past, as motion and music become a conduit for pure feeling.","sonnet":"Memory and music as a single velocity: scenes of drinking on the beach camera in hand, city rhythms at midnight, all of it gathered into a jet stream aimed straight at the heart — \"a lifetime lost in yesterdays\" is the fuel, and the soundwave is the delivery.","src":"haiku"},
"tempting-fate~demons":{"haiku":"An outsider battles internal torment and self-hatred, refusing to surrender despite not belonging and seeing no end to the struggle.","opus":"The self-declared black sheep insists he does not belong and refuses to spend a lifetime waiting, channeling that alienation into an impatient, defiant fight against his own recurring demons.","sonnet":"A self-described black sheep who does not belong anywhere refuses to back down from internal demons despite the pain being constant and the sense of alienation reinforced by others; the fight is ongoing and may take a lifetime, but the speaker insists they will not surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"ten-thousand-crows~the-hanged-man":{"opus":"A graphic first-person crawl through death by asphyxiation on some clinical or torturous slab, tubing forced past the lips and eyes leaking crimson, while the dying person's recollections rush past and the flesh surrenders, grieving that a beloved face is lost to them for good.","sonnet":"A first-person account of dying — being mechanically kept alive or forcibly restrained while the body gives out, tubes shoved down the throat, eyes hemorrhaging, consciousness flickering through final memories before everything goes dark.","src":"sonnet"},
"ten56~diazepam":{"haiku":"Numbing via pills and contemplating suicide while guilt over the impact on one's mother creates paralysis and self-contempt.","opus":"A harrowing first-person descent into sedative dependence and suicidal ideation, counting milligrams to numb a sleepless brain and picturing his own body hanging while an unanswered call to his mother haunts him. The French nu-metalcore band Ten56 renders self-destruction unflinchingly, the narrator and his cowardice at the brink and inability to hurt his mother leaving him trapped between the urge and paralysis.","sonnet":"A portrait of sedative dependency collapsing into suicidal ideation — shaking limbs, cracking lips, milligrams counted instead of sleep. The speaker circles the edge repeatedly, held back only by the thought of a mother who did not answer the call, shame and love the sole barrier against the void.","src":"haiku"},
"ten56~saiko":{"haiku":"Internal demons weaponize self-doubt, mocking suicidal ideation while insisting the speaker owns their own suffering and decay.","opus":"An internal war voiced as two people, where the suicidal, self-loathing side and the sneering, goading side turn out to be the same person, inseparable and locked together until death.","sonnet":"An internal dialogue staged as screaming confrontation between a self and its self-destructive voice: the inner demon taunts, belittles, and dares the speaker toward suicide, while the speaker fights back — culminating in 'I am you and you are me,' collapsing the distinction between tormentor and tormented.","src":"haiku"},
"tenacious-d~dude-i-totally-miss-you":{"haiku":"Lamenting lost friendship and its weirdness, unable to recreate what made connection genuine.","opus":"Beneath the comic bluntness, a genuinely tender lament for a lost friend, missing even the annoying and dishonest parts of them. The joke is the sincerity, the ache of male friendship spoken plainly.","sonnet":"A simple, heartfelt admission of missing a friend — the special times, the honesty, and especially the fucked-up things they used to do together — delivered with guileless repetition that turns the mundane declaration into something genuinely tender.","src":"haiku"},
"tenacious-d~kielbasa-album-version":{"opus":"Jack Black playing the lothario as pure absurdist bluster, mashing bad sex metaphors into Scientology gags. The joke is the gulf between the rock-god swagger and how ridiculous every line actually is.","sonnet":"A crude, comic ode to lust dressed up as a rock song, the speaker cataloguing sexual desire in deliberately absurd terms before dissolving into irreverent crowd-work and self-congratulatory band worship.","src":"sonnet"},
"tenacious-d~master-exploder":{"haiku":"Performers boast about their vocal power and musical talent, accidentally overwhelming audiences with their force. The song celebrates musical prowess through comedic exaggeration.","opus":"A gleefully self-aggrandizing rocker brags that his voice overwhelms any amplification and detonates listeners' minds by accident, armed with a fabled plectrum. The comedy-rock duo built its whole persona on this parody of grandeur, the film's showpiece mocking arena-metal swagger while wholeheartedly wallowing in its own ridiculous excess.","sonnet":"A mock-heroic stage introduction tips into absurdist self-mythology: the speaker claims his voice alone carries enough power to destroy minds, without amplification. A comedic exercise in rock-god grandiosity, the song celebrates the ridiculous magic of performance and the Pick of Destiny with deadpan sincerity.","src":"haiku","fable":"The joke is that a song boasting a voice too mighty to require amplification is piped through a mic anyway, its lyric little more than the boast itself, stripped of any deed that might justify it. Tenacious D's entire act lives in the gap between arena-rock megalomania and two guys who scribbled the tune moments before the set. This track plays it as pure inflation, the call-and-response between Jack Black's colossus and Kyle Gass's deadpan echo deflating the grandeur even as it billows. Each verse vows to shatter your skull while the actual skull-shattering stays permanently offstage. The single flicker of accountability, wrecking a listener's head and then apologizing, gets waved away as an occupational hazard of being this great. It is the parody's cleanest hit: a rock god who can't help but ruin you and feels bad, though not that bad, the song a love letter to craving glory without earning it.","fv":2,"fnote":"In the Pick of Destiny scene the boast goes literal — Black’s note bursts an audience member’s skull and sets the pick around his neck aflame; the mind is blown on camera.","themes":["comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"arena-god grandiosity with the actual deed permanently offstage","built":"a boast about not needing amplification delivered entirely through a mic","fvr":"2.3"},
"tenacious-d~the-metal":{"haiku":"The Metal is an unconquerable force that survives all attempts by other genres to displace it, drawing power from Hell itself.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek origin myth casting heavy metal as an indestructible force that has smitten every rival genre, punk, new wave, grunge, techno, all thrown to the ground before its might. The bombast is entirely the joke and entirely sincere. Tenacious D, the comedy-rock duo of Jack Black and Kyle Gass, close their 2006 film soundtrack with this operatic tribute, mock-epic worship that doubles as a genuine love letter to the riff.","sonnet":"A mock-heroic hymn to metal's indestructibility — punk, new wave, grunge, techno all charged at it and got flattened in turn, while metal stands untouched and eternal, its origin pinned simply and gloriously to hell. The fallen genres confess their own defeat without really understanding why they tried.","src":"haiku"},
"tenchio~projector":{"haiku":"A maker abandons creation to escape cycles of attachment and shattering, discovering true self only in solitude. Art and craft become traps that require abandonment.","opus":"A creator's crisis, choosing to walk away from the identity of maker toward introspection and smaller happiness. The museum image lands the fear: the artist trembling before his own perfected work, freed and wounded by letting his creations go.","sonnet":"A person stepping away from creative ambition — the exhausting life of a maker — toward a quieter, self-directed future found through introspection, while also confronting how the things held most closely ended up cutting deepest when they shattered.","src":"haiku"},
"tenside~as-above-so-below":{"opus":"A defiant anthem of outcasts who place faith solely in mortality and one another, casting existence as at once a blessing and a burden. Whether saved or condemned matters little to them, so they hold to their convictions and taunt the disloyal to drop.","sonnet":"A defiant community of outcasts — crucified, demonized, existing between blessing and damnation — refuses to be broken by traitors and enemies, asserting that whether above or below, in life or facing death, they will stand for what they believe and fight back.","src":"sonnet"},
"tenside~cannibals":{"haiku":"The powerful devour the weak like cold-blooded predators, using apathy as armor and ambition as hunger. They exploit minds and dreams while the masses suffer their exploitation with indifference.","opus":"A class-war snarl against the powerful who farm ordinary people like livestock, chasing profit with no regard for the wreckage. Metalcore fury at greed's indifference, apathy sliding into tragedy.","sonnet":"A political indictment of ruling elites who treat people like livestock — cold-blooded cannibals who want your mind, your trust, your rights — the apathy they cultivate among the population leading to tragedy, their contempt total.","src":"haiku"},
"tera-melos~men-s-shirt":{"haiku":"Nostalgia and hidden intimate connection symbolized through wearing a grandfather's old shirt, with references to a relationship and a mysterious woman seen through memories and photographs.","opus":"A hazy Californian memory piece where an inherited shirt becomes a portal to childhood, a lost grandfather, and a warning to be careful. Nostalgia frays into surreal imagery, grief hiding inside the fondness.","sonnet":"Wearing a grandfather's shirt triggers a flood of fragmented childhood memory — a trip to Las Vegas, old photographs, a warning always to be careful, and a sense of a hidden fold in time where a loved one's presence still flickers.","src":"haiku"},
"tera-melos~sunburn":{"haiku":"A song about physical and psychological dissolution, skin shedding, and the loss of identity through repeated trauma or exposure.","opus":"The math-rock trio treats skin as an unreliable, ever-shedding surface to explore how memory and thought slip away. The narrator loses his train of speech and abandons things he can't articulate, letting bodies seem to dissolve piece by piece. Forgetting and physical disintegration become the same slow, itching process, restless music mirroring a mind that won't hold still.","sonnet":"Cellular transformation and bodily disintegration serve as a disorienting lens through which identity dissolves — skin sheds, memory slips, meaning burns away before it can be spoken. The self keeps cycling through damage and renewal without ever arriving at coherence.","src":"haiku"},
"tera-melos~trash-generator":{"haiku":"The narrator generates empty content in repetitive cycles, caught in feedback loops of self-doubt and mechanical productivity.","opus":"The California math-rock trio turns social disgust inward until it becomes self-loathing, its narrator dragging himself along, teeth clenched, protesting his own decency while branding himself refuse. The jittery, looping refrain matches their spasmodic guitar work, the anxious repetitions evoking a mind trapped in its own feedback loop.","sonnet":"A restless self reckoning with social aversion and compulsive internal noise. Severed from the crowd, grinding through infinite loops, the speaker insists on innocence while the machinery of perception runs hot — unsure whether the darkness is art, stupidity, or simply the cost of staying wired in.","src":"haiku"},
"tera-melos~warpless-run":{"haiku":"Video game speedrunning without exploits mirrors life's constraints; despite optimal play, fatalism and predetermined failure govern the outcome.","opus":"A no-shortcuts playthrough of an old platformer becomes a meditation on doing life the hard way, refusing the warps and seeing everything you'd otherwise skip. The fatalistic game stands in for accepting a fixed design you can't rewrite.","sonnet":"Using video game speedrun logic as a metaphor, the speaker describes a life structured by shortcuts and skipped content — choosing the warpless path means actually living through the stages, seeing the night and the moon, instead of bypassing everything just to reach the end faster.","src":"haiku"},
"tera-melos~weird-circles":{"haiku":"A chaotic crowd scene where bodies press against walls and bones break under force; the speaker finds purpose in confusion and happiness in escaping home to document the experience through song.","opus":"A fragmented, sensory memory of a scrappy DIY punk gig in some bare concrete room, all noise, chaotic pits too hazardous to enter, and bodies crushed to the walls. The math-rock band embraces the confusion cheerfully, since the whole point was making it back and turning the night into music of its own.","sonnet":"A kinetic recollection of a basement punk show — packed into dark, loud, concrete rooms, bodies pressed against walls, moving in circles, navigating chaos happily enough to go home and turn it into a song.","src":"haiku"},
"teramaze~weight-of-humanity":{"haiku":"The speaker examines how systems of exploitation make people complicit in collective cruelty while blaming individuals. They reject false hope and call out the lies that keep people passive and isolated.","opus":"Australian prog-metal band Teramaze pick apart the structures that isolate and mold people, doubting whether the cruelty we tolerate is really what we chose and warning that shouldering the world's burden just means minimizing it while the media grows fat on human suffering. The narrator stays alert among deceivers who operate openly, unwilling to simply wish for oblivion.","sonnet":"The crushing feedback loop of living inside a system that shapes you and feeds you fear — the speaker watches news manufacture the final weight of collective dread, sees liars hiding in plain sight, and the frustration is not paralysis but a refusal to hope for the end, a wide-awake anger that won't quietly dissolve into sympathy.","src":"haiku"},
"termina~desolate-specter":{"opus":"A study of a hopelessly isolated figure who has hunted for meaning his entire existence and turned up empty, damned and incapable of love, going in circles behind a warped sense of right and wrong. He begs for forgiveness he cannot earn and finally asks only to be abandoned to his suffering, wandering forever without an end.","sonnet":"A confession of spiritual emptiness after a lifetime of searching — the narrator has pushed away everyone, been guided in circles by a broken moral compass, found no love and no meaning, and accepts that they are a desolate ghost wandering endlessly without arrival.","src":"sonnet"},
"termina~lucid":{"haiku":"A person trapped in a lucid nightmare cannot escape their own mind, paralyzed and drowning in a dream that keeps replaying trauma.","opus":"A stifling plunge into sleep paralysis reframed as punishment: the speaker knows the vision is false yet cannot rouse himself, hauled ever downward through terrors built as repayment for harm he inflicted, pleading to be released or simply ended. Drowning and immobility carry the whole piece. Termina work in modern metalcore and deathcore, and the shift from clean begging to guttural roars mirrors the trapped, unmoored horror at its core.","sonnet":"Trapped in a waking nightmare that won't resolve into either sleep or consciousness — the speaker can't tell if they're alive, sinks through the floor faster than thought, and is forced to relive past trauma on an endless loop. The only exit imagined is being switched off entirely, while suffocation and paralysis close in from every direction.","src":"haiku"},
"termina~take-flight":{"haiku":"Despair and vanity intertwine as the narrator confronts their lost direction and forgotten reasons for survival. Fear holds them caged while light remains distant, yet the song insists that breaking free remains possible.","opus":"A struggle to wake from a numbing depression, addressed to a self whose ambition has unravelled and whose heart risks emptying out entirely. The repeated push is to face the fear, escape a brittle cage, and recall the reason you set out, holding to a dim glow and a scrap of the self still worth rescuing.","sonnet":"Vanity and despair have stripped the speaker of purpose — sleeping for days without dreaming, questioning why they are still alive, balanced at the edge of a knife — but a faint light eventually appears and a voice urges: face the fear, break the cage of porcelain, throw doubt to the wind, and one day take flight.","src":"haiku"},
"terrorbyte~ghost-stories":{"haiku":"Accumulated trauma transforms into haunting narrative; the speaker weaponizes loss and becomes the demon others fear, channeling grief into menace.","opus":"A raw catalogue of real deaths, a mother, friends to overdose and suicide, an unborn child, that leave the artist convinced he's toxic and cursed, turning everyone he loves to dust. He vows to weaponize the grief on stage, saying the names of the dead, insisting this is lived trauma and not just music.","sonnet":"A rapper haunted by a litany of real deaths — Cam in a car crash, Shawn burned to death, Chelsea dead of heroin, his mother gone at 2 AM — turns accumulated grief and 'Vicodin and Percocet and loaded guns' into fuel, insisting the audience's entertainment of his pain will become the stage where he finally speaks their names.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~acceptance-concealing-fate-pt-1":{"haiku":"Demands and complicated truth; the speaker refuses to be complicated by others.","opus":"The opening movement of Tesseract's Concealing Fate suite, a djent meditation on confrontation and interior reckoning. Demands to show hands and stop complicating things play out against a mind kept open only through unhealed wounds, scars serving as memory. Revolving alone, the narrator insists he meant everything he said, wrestling toward acceptance amid confusion and defenselessness.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who has complicated a situation beyond repair — show your hands, you have no right — while scars keep the memory of what was said before the mire closed in. Revolving alone becomes the only stable position, the acceptance of the title not peace but a kind of permanent orbit around an unresolvable wound.","src":"haiku","fable":"The opening command to bare open palms works as a courtroom demand for confession, and TesseracT builds the song as a trial that collapses inward: the accuser cross-examining over evidence and defense gives way to the realization that prosecutor and defendant are one self, spinning language against itself in a single skull. The djent structure enacts this—mechanical, revolving repetition of being alone stalls in place while the argument goes nowhere. Tompkins’ shift from clipped accusation to open, sustained melody marks the moment the fight for the last word dissolves. What remains after litigating a relationship is the quiet verdict that everything wanted was already present, so the fate being concealed is that the case was never winnable, always argued against a reflection.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","heartbreak & loss","madness & the mind"],"means":"litigating a relationship until the mirror confesses","built":"the djent's mechanical revolving stall enacts the argument going nowhere"},
"tesseract~april":{"opus":"An unsettling first-person descent into possessive cruelty and addiction, the narrator coldly watching someone he's harmed while dissolving into methadone highs and pronouncing his own life finished. Tesseract set the confession against airy, atmospheric djent, so the horror arrives calm and lucid rather than raw — which is exactly what makes it land.","sonnet":"A deeply disturbing first-person voice describes possessive lust, watching destruction, and seeing life through bloodshot eyes on methadone — the target \"means nothing\" and the speaker swears to silence, the final line \"this life is over\" arriving as both confession and collapse.","src":"sonnet","fable":"TesseracT wrap this monologue in djent’s clean, syncopated poise, and that unnerving smoothness is the point — the delivery stays composed while the speaker narrates possession, abuse, and a fantasy of slow, tender destruction. The track advances by degrading its own vocabulary: an early, almost pastoral cruelty curdles into clinical wreckage — sickness, exhausted flesh, the pharmacological numbness of methadone — while the refrain that she means nothing keeps collapsing on itself, each repetition betraying the fixation it claims to deny. As the confession hardens into a sworn silence and a flat declaration that everything is finished, the disavowal fails completely: the ownership he brags about is really self-annihilation, the user’s high and the buried confession revealed as one shared exit.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"denial performing itself until ownership becomes self-annihilation","built":"unnerving djent poise held intact while the vocabulary degrades into clinical wreckage"},
"tesseract~beneath-my-skin-mirror-image":{"haiku":"The speaker hides pain and shame deep within while seeking love; memories and anxiety keep them awake as they question whether love is dangerous.","opus":"Feelings kept buried under the skin, love guarded so fiercely it curdles into self-erasure and a longing to be dragged under. The djent atmospherics carry a plea toward some absent, godlike other for rescue from that half-crazed isolation.","sonnet":"A two-part piece: the first half is about feelings buried so deep beneath the skin they're invisible to others, and the narrator waiting for a chance to exist without that weight. The second half shifts to longing for a divine or cosmic truth — half crazy, searching for a sign, hoping love isn't as dangerous as it feels.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~burden":{"haiku":"Accumulated weight and memory fragmentation trap the speaker in perpetual carrying; learning to survive becomes the only possible transcendence.","opus":"Casting off a suppressed weight only to face amnesia and a self hidden from itself, resolving through the fog to be heard even as the body weakens and the sun is abandoned.","sonnet":"A person who has suppressed everything until their past is inaccessible — they carry hidden weight that has rotted inside them, feel cut off from their own identity, and must finally confront the burden they've buried before they can begin again.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~cages":{"haiku":"Two people become trapped in isolation and abandoned love, building cages of solitude around each other. The song explores how attachment becomes a prison.","opus":"A song about self-imprisonment through isolation, in which a tempting, menacing presence questions the narrator's worth while he confesses that letting go was a lie he told himself. He becomes the prisoner of his own abandoned love, dissipating into hate and hopelessness, building the very cage of solitude he despairs in.","sonnet":"The collapse of a relationship through self-deception and denial — the narrator knew it was wrong to hold on, watches the other person fade away, and is left isolated by their own inability to let go, imprisoned in the absence.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track begins in pure subtraction: no secrets, no daring, no dread left, just a hollow that predates it, so his confinement reads less as a sprung trap than a place walked toward on purpose. Tompkins keeps the addressee unfixed — a candlelit figure who quizzes him on trust and worth. She might be a lover, a voice in his own skull, or the addiction he half-names as something coiling beneath him, and the reading works by refusing to pin it down. The clever turn is how the back half keeps trading blame: each line swears the other party dissolved first, then fled and left him stranded, yet the final order to wall someone into solitude admits those walls were his own to pour. Warden and captive rotate until the song exposes them as one person voicing both.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["alienation & emptiness","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"warden and captive rotating until exposed as one person voicing both","built":"the back half trades blame line by line, each party swearing the other dissolved first"},
"tesseract~deception-concealing-fate-pt-2":{"haiku":"Fate tightens around two entangled figures, one refusing to accept the truth while the other sinks deeper into denial, drowning in tides they cannot escape together.","opus":"A seduction staged as a reckoning with one's own demons, desire and dread collapsing into the same surrender. The intimacy is a trap the speaker walks into knowingly, addressing his darkness as if it were a lover whose time has finally come.","sonnet":"A brief, charged encounter with an internal demon — the speaker acknowledges being entwined with it, feeling a rush of fear and desire before finally succumbing, framed as an inevitable confrontation long coming.","src":"haiku","fable":"The summons the song issues to its own demons is a seduction, not an exorcism. The speaker calls the shadow almost tenderly, and self and demon lie entwined like lovers, the encounter igniting the mind and rushing toward a climax where fear and surrender fall into the same moment. The djent refrain announcing that the demons’ time has come first sounds like conquest, yet it rings hollow. A shrinking landscape and a swallowing tide reveal the speaker, not the demons, as the one drowning, and the closing charge of denial turns inward — the promised triumph exposed as the very concealment the title names.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the conquest refrain exposed as the speaker's own drowning","built":"the seduction frame inverts the expected exorcism so triumph names concealment"},
"tesseract~dystopia":{"haiku":"Nightmarish visions and psychological torment where the narrator resists manipulation while struggling to maintain autonomy against control.","opus":"Moves from a suffocating nightmare of menacing shadows and deceit toward a hard resolve to stay free and refuse another's control. TesseracT, a British djent band, wrap the struggle in shifting dynamics, the narrator declining every comfort offered and clinging to the belief that loving and being loved is the highest thing worth holding onto.","sonnet":"Trapped in a nightmare of dark, multiplying figures and manipulative relationships, the narrator finally refuses to comply — asserting the freedom to live on his own terms rather than give in to someone who wanted him to surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~echoes":{"haiku":"Confrontation with lies and self-deception opens wounds; the speaker remains infected despite attempts at healing.","opus":"Shame and self-imposed isolation after a poisoned choice, the narrator narrating his own downfall while promising to stay beside a friend to the end. Prog-metal accepting mortality as the one truth nobody outruns.","sonnet":"A reckoning with past choices that cost the speaker dearly — imprisoned by truth and ashamed of who they were, they exist in an aftermath where wounds are infected but still alive, reaching out to a friend in the dangerous dark to say they'd rather die together than face it alone.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~eden-p-o-r-t-a-l-s":{"opus":"An environmental lament and indictment of humanity's greed, mourning how we sacrifice living memory and the wonders of the earth for wealth while dredging and killing the sea for pleasure.","sonnet":"An indictment of humanity's destruction of the natural world through greed and industrial extraction, mourning the loss of the seven wonders and the planet's history while condemning the lies used to justify ongoing environmental devastation.","src":"sonnet"},
"tesseract~hexes":{"haiku":"A mind haunted and cursed by traumatic history; terror lives in memory, and the speaker is afraid to breathe or move, locked in the past's grip.","opus":"About being haunted by your own memory, the past operating like a curse that keeps intruding on the present mind. The hushed dread and the plea not to feel or move capture someone trapped inside their own history, fighting to breathe again.","sonnet":"The speaker is haunted by memory in a way they cannot escape or suppress, describing a mind shattered by history and a phantom that lives in their conscious thought. History itself is cast as a hex — something that traps, terrifies, and repeats — yet the speaker asserts they will breathe and live again despite it.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~juno":{"haiku":"Falling from grace and burning; judgment and self-destruction with imagery of wreckage and fire.","opus":"A fiery self-destruction and reckoning, with the speaker nosediving in flames through the wreckage of his life while facing judgment; amid delusion and damage, he pleads for someone to reveal and save themselves for him.","sonnet":"A speaker in freefall — burning wings, nosediving through the wreckage of their life — pleads for someone to reveal themselves and offer salvation as all illusions of dignity and purpose collapse around them.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~king":{"haiku":"A critique of authoritarianism and tyranny masked as strength. The song condemns the 'king' figure who uses revenge and words to control and destroy, demanding coexistence without submission.","opus":"A confrontation with a tyrant, personal or political, who devours everything and strips away the freedom to simply be yourself. It moves from being crushed under that crown toward the refusal that makes coexistence possible again.","sonnet":"A reckoning with a domineering, totalitarian figure — a 'king' who demands submission, weaponises words, massacres others' dreams, and suppresses individuality — with the subjects beginning to push back.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~legion":{"haiku":"Endless repetition of hatred binds the speaker to earth while their consciousness dreams of innocence lost, trapped in walls built by control systems they resent but cannot break free from.","opus":"A mind imprisoned and branded, reaching for an innocence it can only dream of after the walls went up. Behind the imagery of hate-monasteries and mass control sits one plea, to be led home into light from a world that made the self disappear.","sonnet":"A person trapped inside a system of indoctrinated belief and collective control — once innocent and free in dreams, now branded and imprisoned by an ideology that binds others into believing nothing, longing to be led back home from the only world they've ever known.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title names the gospel demon who answers that his name is many, and the song is a possession told from inside the host: it opens by scorching out vision against the sun so the speaker can no longer verify anything, then feeds him a soothing catechism of shared innocence in a world built just for the two of them, before the real transaction lands as a binding ritual whose whole payload is to leave the newly bound faithful to nothing. TesseracT stage this on War of Being as two voices fighting for one throat, the seducer's smooth first person and a buried original self who surfaces only in a dream of unbroken skin on a far world he was exiled from onto grey rubble. That the final section can do nothing but repeat its ache to be walked back toward the light, while every faction at once condemns him as a heretic, is the point and not a failure of nerve: the many have already spoken over the one, so the plea to be brought back is the last unshared word he has, and it can only loop.","fv":2,"fnote":"The two voices are literal: War of Being is a concept record about Ex and El stranded in the Strangeland, with Tompkins singing its characters in distinct voices — the possession staged here is one front in that larger war over a single being.","themes":["faith & the occult","identity & becoming","surveillance & control"],"means":"the many voices overwriting the one self until only a plea loops","built":"two voices fighting for one throat enact possession rather than describe it"},
"tesseract~luminary":{"haiku":"An invisible person moves through the world unnoticed despite their pain, trapped in isolation while life's beauty escapes them and death looms inevitably.","opus":"The British djent band addresses someone trapped in their own mind, unseen among people and lost in the surrounding din. The song warns that ordinary reality is a deception and that surrendering to fragile beauty is fatal, casting gentleness as prey for a predatory world and a burden the isolated carry silently to the end.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone invisible and self-destructive — beautiful and dangerous in equal measure, trapped in the prison of their own mind, drifting through crowds unseen, and heading toward a kind of death by tenderness that no one around them can perceive.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~messenger":{"haiku":"Communication breaks down; the speaker feels trapped by design, unable to feel whole despite being shaped for this purpose.","opus":"A voice that feels it was built and controlled by someone else, weighed down by that person's demands yet still straining to please. It turns into a plea against cold indifference, urging the listener to shed detachment, feel the coming crisis, and notice beauty while there's still time before catastrophe.","sonnet":"A song about emotional labor and the burden of carrying someone else's words and needs — the speaker feels designed to serve and absorb, while the people they care for remain apathetic and blind to those being crushed quietly beneath them.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~natural-disaster":{"haiku":"Restlessness and internal catastrophe force hiddenness and isolation; the speaker cannot belong to a world they're destined to destroy.","opus":"A restless outsider who refuses to belong here, slipping into the cold to dodge disaster while being worshipped and tucked out of sight. The snake behind his gaze and the fantasy others live out through him leave open the question of who he's become.","sonnet":"A person recognizes they are inherently restless and uncontainable — labeled a 'natural disaster' by someone who can't understand them — and wrestles with the cost of living authentically outside the world others want them to occupy, including creeping in the dark to avoid catastrophe and questioning who they've become in the process.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~of-energy-embers":{"haiku":"Blindness and cyclical entrapment persist despite awareness; the speaker remains child-like despite witnessing repeatedly that choice is illusory.","opus":"A humbling recognition of how much remains unseen and how blind past certainty was, sensing a kindred scarred soul and stepping willingly into the fire.","sonnet":"A moment of humility where the speaker — despite having seen and experienced much — realizes they are still like a child, aware that others are just as cauterized by life as they are, and asks whether each new flame is just another chance to be destroyed.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~of-energy-singularity":{"haiku":"A weary soul seeks escape to peaceful natural places, craving freedom from endless conflict and controversy; they plant seeds of change but fear nothing will truly break free.","opus":"A narrator reaches out to save someone from a fall while longing for his own escape to a calmer place of relief; he wrestles with grief, self-doubt and a numb feeling of being dead inside as he plants a seed of change.","sonnet":"The speaker oscillates between a longing for escape — an imagined place of green grass, blue sea, and calm — and the inescapable weight of grief, misfortune, and feeling dead inside. They try to plant something that will erode the walls holding them back, while doubting they will ever truly be enough.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that keeps rehearsing an escape it never leaves for. The speaker plays rescuer and shadow at once, promising to catch a plunging body while trailing it until their soles are raw, and the elsewhere he pictures is a postcard field of open water and unclenched hands where nobody grits down against the day. But the structure gives the mask away: that pastoral vision returns almost verbatim in the closing verse, unchanged, which means nothing in it was ever reached, only looped, and the gardening gesture of dropping a seed and then coaxing the ground to crumble is planting and self-undoing folded into a single motion. Underneath the calm, TesseracT lets the real question surface late and plainly, asking why sensation persists at all when the interior has already gone quiet, so the serene meadow reads less like a destination than a sedative laid over the wish to stop existing. The suite title, Singularity, names it exactly: a point of collapse dressed up as an arrival.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the serene pastoral vision reads as sedative laid over the wish to stop","built":"the escaped-to meadow returns verbatim at the close, proving it was never reached"},
"tesseract~of-matter-proxy":{"haiku":"A surrogate self bears blame and scorn for others' failings; despite growing stronger, they remain trapped, unable to break free from imposed degradation.","opus":"A conflicted narrator who devotes himself to propping someone up while feeling inadequate, exhausted by their judgments and refusing to keep playing into their hands even as he wishes he were enough.","sonnet":"The speaker serves as a surrogate who absorbs others' projections and judgments while recognizing their own inadequacy and the impossibility of the role. Despite burning bridges and pouring out devotion, they feel trapped, unseen, and unable to escape a pattern of self-erasure for someone else's sake.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~of-matter-resist":{"haiku":"Time fragments backward while the body waits in exasperating numbness. The speaker attempts futile resistance against momentum toward dissolution.","opus":"A taut progressive-metal appeal against passing time and vanishing, the narrator striking deals with his innermost self while clutching a hand he is coming to despise. Split between hope and surrender, he cherishes a passing nearness before it slips off and swears to spend his days regretting he fell short. The pounded order to hold firm reads as brittle defiance against destiny.","sonnet":"Trying to hold on as a relationship ends: gripping the last touch of a hand already turning to loathing, lips asking to be blessed before they're silenced, the soul settling bets it can't win — resistance is the only posture left, even as the narrator admits they'll spend the rest of their life wishing they were enough.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~of-matter-retrospect":{"haiku":"Burden and devotion corrode the self; the speaker carries an unbearable weight for someone else, recognizing too late that sacrifice cannot preserve what's already gone.","opus":"Someone burning bridges while still trying to hold a falling partner aloft, tying their own devotion around another's waist and receiving only misjudgment. The plea for strength comes from exhaustion no one else senses.","sonnet":"Someone burning their own bridges while trying to hold another person up, dealing with the pressure of a relationship built on judgment and lust rather than conviction, refusing to keep playing into hands that only exploit them.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~of-mind-exiled":{"haiku":"Spiritual exile and philosophical uncertainty; questioning the meaning of transcendence and peace.","opus":"A philosophical prog-metal grappling with doubt, faith and the clash between feeling and reason. The narrator turns from certainty, reveling in the unknown while suffocating in a stagnant, nightmarish reality. Tempted to buy off the divine, aging and paralyzed, he finds release only once his mind is finally free to soar.","sonnet":"A mind caught between the paralysis of faith and the void of doubt — the hand of god too tempting to bribe, blood running cold yet still alive — torn between heart and mind, unable to bridge them, circling the instruction to seize the moment and sabotage the stagnancy that is choking.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~of-mind-nocturne":{"haiku":"Inadequacy and plagued consciousness manifest as atmospheric dread; collective responsibility for collective decay intertwines with the impossibility of salvation.","opus":"A struggle against a lover or self who is drowning in denial, pleading whether they can be saved from themselves before exhaustion and false hope drift them apart.","sonnet":"A confrontation with collective human failure and denial — humanity has become a plague to itself, too weak and self-deceived to break free, with the song alternating between despair at our shared irresponsibility and a desperate plea for waking up and escape.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~of-reality-eclipse":{"haiku":"Breaking silence and sin, refusing to dwell on death despite overwhelming darkness.","opus":"A prog-metal descent through despair and the pull toward the other side, hope reduced to a crevice, no light at the tunnel's end, yet closing on the fragile question of whether a new beginning is possible.","sonnet":"About standing at a crossroads between nihilism and the possibility of renewal — the narrator has wandered into sin and doubt, sees no light ahead, but the rising crescent suggests something beginning again. The song holds both the weight of breakage and the tentative question of whether a new beginning is possible.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~of-reality-palingenesis":{"haiku":"A living thing fights its way through soil and stone into a world defined by war and betrayal, only to find that the cost of violence is irreversible — respect lost, work undone, consequences owed.","opus":"A rebirth allegory shaped as a seed forcing its way up through earth and rock into an uncertain, war-scarred world. The narrator refuses violence, offering peace while warning that a betrayal carries its own price. Caught between limitless possibility and the fear that everything he's built will be undone, it dwells on the cost of survival and respect taken back.","sonnet":"A seed fighting through soil and stone toward a peak where war waits: war won't suffice and won't satisfy, and the betrayal is named plainly — my back, your knife, that's the price you laid upon your lives — but palingenesis, rebirth, is still the frame, the cost of others' choices paid in full before any new beginning is possible.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~perfection-concealing-fate-pt-4":{"opus":"Consuming envy and self-erasure: the narrator would give everything to become someone more perfect than he could ever be, craving their love and compassion, until he realizes the flawless image he covets is itself a lie.","sonnet":"A confession of inadequacy and longing — the narrator looks at someone or something perfect and feels only the ache of knowing they will never possess that beauty or love, yet would sacrifice everything for even one taste of it.","src":"sonnet","fable":"TesseracT’s Perfection plays desire as self-erasure: the speaker pleads for stolen fragments of a life he casts as flawless and unreachable, wanting not to be near that person but to vacate himself and take their place. The song deliberately withholds the object’s identity — lover, idealized self, or a silent God who hoards mercy while the world stays unlit — because worship this total only needs a vessel stamped better-than-me. Where it turns is the discovery that the coveted world is a fraud, so the surrendering of everything hardens from aspiration into a compulsion he can’t quit, still bargaining to dissolve into the other even after the prize has proven hollow.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","identity & becoming","faith & the occult"],"means":"bargaining to dissolve into another even after the prize proves hollow","built":"the object's withheld identity — lover, self, or silent God — makes worship need no actual other"},
"tesseract~phoenix":{"haiku":"Someone reshapes themselves after abandonment and mechanical oppression, refusing cynicism and reborn through change. The song depicts personal revolution and refusing to become wreckage.","opus":"Tesseract stage a rebirth out of despair set against a bleak, industrialized world of raging fires and looming upheaval. The narrator resolves to keep breathing and hold on through it, undergoing the phoenix's transformation from a broken, defeated wreck into someone clear-eyed, fearless, and remade.","sonnet":"About the need for personal transformation and rebirth — recognizing you've been a failure chasing shadows, then choosing to rise like a phoenix, shed the cynicism, and see the world through the open eyes of a child again.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~sacrifice":{"haiku":"Surrender and offerings given for transcendence or cause, the speaker relinquishing what matters most.","opus":"A sprawling prog anthem in which the singer offers himself as support and prop, framing love as something that survives separation and even the grave. He claims he keeps aging, sharpening, hardening, listening for some outside voice to confirm him, grieving beside a resting place he cannot always locate. It reaches toward lasting devotion and a bond with a mentor figure, tender and aching under the technical surface.","sonnet":"A protective figure speaks to someone caught in stagnation — scattered tender moments, endless circulation between control and collapse — and the turn comes when the perspective shifts: now you are older and wiser, drawn to me, but we were never meant to be there, and together we are almost, never, there.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~seven-names":{"haiku":"Struggle with self-worth and alienation; the singer left everything behind seeking solace but faces only guilt, forgiveness seems impossible, and shame defines their identity.","opus":"A djent meditation on the fragile relief of leaving a painful past behind, then the guilt that follows when survival meant abandoning someone. The core tension is between finally breathing again and begging forgiveness for how the escape was managed.","sonnet":"A song about fleeing something — the narrator has left it all behind to breathe again, but carries unresolved guilt and the need for forgiveness, calling himself ungodly and wondering whether what he did is forgivable.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~sirens":{"haiku":"Violent apocalypse looms while the speaker waits passively with a beloved; all the futures they planted together will never bloom.","opus":"A taut, cinematic prog-metal piece built on dreading disaster, alarms and a brutal ending closing in as two people sit wordlessly, hands joined. Schemes and buried hopes that will never be allowed to sprout. Yet it turns toward flight and togetherness, an invitation to drift off across the water as one, to meet dread side by side instead of running solo.","sonnet":"Two people holding hands while waiting for a violent conclusion they cannot afford to avoid — seeds planted that won't grow, a life they can't sustain. The lyric turns: take my hand, sail to me, speak the words, leave the grief — and the final question is whether to run or to fight and stay, together.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~smile":{"haiku":"A warning against manipulation and control, with the speaker resisting coercion and urging independence against a sinister entity attempting to dominate through psychological means. The smile masks menace and the need for free thought is central.","opus":"A paranoid struggle against a menacing controlling presence, someone urging the listener to think for themselves and resist manipulation. The mechanical, soothing tone masks the threat, a shadow on the sun that keeps closing in.","sonnet":"The narrator feels hunted and controlled by a menacing, mechanical presence that encourages killing and uses manipulation to dominate — a colossal, shadowing figure that smiles as it closes in, casting a shadow over everything and reducing the narrator to an object of its predatory design.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~sunrise":{"haiku":"Radical rejection of deceit and self-defense through violence become necessary as trust shatters and safety demands destruction of connection.","opus":"A recoil from someone whose lying eyes provoke both fear and menace, the narrator alternately fleeing and threatening while seeking safety in numbers and within his own mind.","sonnet":"A speaker lashes out at eyes they find threatening or deceitful — \"I hate them, why do they lie\" — alternating between flight and defiance, and the images of flame engulfing forever and a soul that should believe but is asked whether it does suggest a confrontation with something that will not be survived unchanged.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two survival instincts share one body here, and the song refuses to say which voice belongs to whom. The one who can’t stand being looked at is also the one promising to break the intruder, so retreat and attack turn out to be the same person flinching and swinging at once. TesseracT plant the giveaway in a matched pair of figures: one who burrows into the safety of a crowd, one who walks straight at the threat with the brakes cut. The last image, a fire eating the soul from the inside out, admits the danger was never the stranger at the door but the burning already lit within.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"retreat and attack revealed as the same person flinching and swinging","built":"the matched pair of figures — crowd-burrower and threat-charger — mirror each other as one self"},
"tesseract~survival":{"haiku":"A lover's absence after a decade of waiting creates a void where presence feels impossible, and past sorrow resurfaces with abandonment.","opus":"Prog-metal reckoning with abandonment and wasted years, a narrator naming himself the one who leaves and fearing he'll dissolve without the person who completes him. The looping question of disappearing carries the dread of erasure.","sonnet":"About the grief and guilt of someone who abandoned a partner — ten years of waiting, a life imagined differently, and the singer acknowledging they are the one who left, identifying themselves as 'the abandoner' in a loop of regret.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~survival-errai":{"haiku":"Abandonment and long-suffering reward yield to luminous radiance; patience transmutes isolation into a beacon for others.","opus":"A haunted rumination on abandonment and cyclical return, the narrator branding himself the abandoner while a decade of hope curdles into a decade of sorrow. A sunlit lotus figures a patient love he feels dead without. Disturbed by deja vu and the fear of vanishing, it circles survival as a lonely, recurring vigil against loss.","sonnet":"A ten-year vigil: the speaker names themselves the abandoner as someone waited like a lotus in the sun, radiating patience, while secrets built and sorrow accumulated. When the other is absent the speaker feels dead inside — the cycle of abandonment and return is the only life either of them knows.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~tender":{"haiku":"Tender connection transforms into warfare; betrayal and silence obliterate the possibility of mutual understanding or redemption.","opus":"The collapse of a love that once felt like everything, a lie and too many words leading to attack and silence, mourning the words left unsaid as the ground caves in and faith runs out.","sonnet":"A relationship destroyed by silence and accumulated violence — the speaker loved deeply, bled for it, but their own quiet ended everything, and now there are too many unsaid things and no ground left to stand on.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~the-arrow":{"haiku":"Wasted time on love shatters it into pieces; someone sacrificed their life for a relationship too fragile to survive.","opus":"A bitter accounting of years and a life squandered on the wrong pursuit, now collapsing into ruin. Despite the protest that romance was never the point, the song keeps circling the human cost: relationships wrecked and discarded, half a lifetime lost, the regret impossible to disown.","sonnet":"A song about looking back on a relationship and realizing how much time was wasted — the narrator watches something fall apart in pieces and acknowledges, bitterly, that it was never really about love to begin with.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~the-grey":{"haiku":"Monochrome existence drowns in tragedy while anxiety bleeds through. Serenity remains impossible—suffering persists.","opus":"Depression rendered as living in monochrome, drowning in an undertow of anxiety and constant tragedy while bleeding for someone who doesn't care whether the singer makes it out; the grey is the numb in-between of a joyless existence.","sonnet":"A song about being drained by a relationship with someone whose only need is instant gratification — the narrator exists in emotional monochrome, bleeding out for the other person who doesn't care whether they survive, overwhelmed by anxiety, tragedy, and the fear of being swallowed by the undertow of constant giving.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~the-impossible-concealing-fate-pt-3":{"haiku":"Ocean swells as metaphor for the impossible becoming survivable through surrender and reaching. The speaker breathes toward safety within the vastness that could destroy.","opus":"The turning point of the Concealing Fate suite, coaxing someone out of denial about their own feelings. Breath and the swelling ocean become anchors of safety amid self-mistreatment framed as disgrace. Repeated pleas to recognize what is truly felt build toward a warning that the impossible consumes everyone, urging open eyes before it is too late.","sonnet":"An intervention in slow motion — breathe in, reach out, the ocean keeps me safe — directed at someone in denial, someone treating themselves badly, someone who has let things go on too long; the impossible is consuming everyone if they close their eyes now.","src":"haiku","fable":"TesseracT’s debut turns anxiety into a call-and-response conflict between two selves, and this third part of the six-movement Concealing Fate arc stages that split as a rescue attempt rather than a private collapse. The water imagery is deliberately double: the same tide that threatens to pull the singer under is also cast as a guardian, so numbness gets mistaken for safety and self-destruction wears the costume of relief. Daniel Tompkins delivers the interrogation as a refusal to let the other voice hide behind flat affect, hammering the same demand until denial has nowhere left to stand. Musically the band releases its stuttering, palm-muted djent rhythms into a wide sustained chorus at the exact moment the lyric begs for air and contact, letting the arrangement act out the instruction it can’t force. The widening from one private wound to a crisis shared by countless others reframes survival as collective, and the final command to stay awake works as the section’s whole thesis: refuse the pull downward, remain here.","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"self-destruction wearing the costume of relief until denial has nowhere to stand","built":"the djent's stuttering grip released into wide chorus exactly when the lyric begs for air"},
"tesseract~tourniquet":{"haiku":"Love acts as both tourniquet and salvation—holding back bleeding while also containing pressure; the speaker promises to endure through pain together.","opus":"A love song casting the beloved as a lifesaving bind that halts the narrator's blood loss through their gentleness and warm touch; wrapped in imagery of strain, tears and a brief span of life, it treats devotion as a shared rescue, giving over all his power and revering the one whose ceaseless glow keeps him breathing.","sonnet":"A song about loving someone through a crisis — the narrator watches helplessly as his partner suffers, and commits everything he has to stemming the damage, their love framed as the tourniquet that stops the bleeding, the thing that holds them both together under pressure.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~utopia":{"haiku":"Reality refuses to reflect truth and keeps the speaker trapped in confusion and chaos despite searching for freedom.","opus":"Tesseract stage a disorienting psychological collapse, with a narrator tumbling through chaos and confusion, cut off from freedom and unable to make his own efforts cohere. Casting himself as someone who squandered paradise, he pleads for forgiveness even as everything spirals into ruin, the whole scene warping into something grotesque.","sonnet":"A paranoid dissociation spiral — the speaker is surrounded by voices with no source, can't taste freedom, and feels reality fragmenting into chaos, colors disagreeing, pictures turning obscene, a hurricane ripping buildings from the ground while they beg for forgiveness and clarity.","src":"haiku"},
"tesseract~war-of-being":{"haiku":"Shedding imposed lies and masks to reveal authentic self, words whispered shaping perception.","opus":"A mythic struggle between deception and self-knowledge, a serpent-figure and a sold lie recurring like a curse, the narrator urging someone to tear away their mask and face the darkness within to finally understand themselves.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a manipulative or abusive person who shaped the narrator through lies — told their love ran cold, sold a future in disguise; the narrator fights to tear the mask away from both themselves and the other, seeking clarity and survival beyond the patterns imposed on them.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~02-more-than-meets-the-eye":{"haiku":"Depths exceed surface perception as the speaker navigates hidden complexity. More always lurks beneath the visible.","opus":"A thrash-metal narrative of psychological imprisonment and hard-won recovery. The narrator begins trapped in a cell of his own stubbornness, tormented by haunting voices and the sense that everyone is out to get him. The turn is deliberate: conquering fear, reclaiming control of his fate, and finally sleeping unafraid, his life righted, more resilient than it first appeared.","sonnet":"A two-stage narrative: first, a prisoner of his own stubbornness, haunted by voices and violent dreams; then, having chased the demons away and conquered fear, the same person reclaims his fate — the pivot is quiet but total, a life turned around by deciding to sleep unafraid.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~afterlife":{"haiku":"A father's wisdom about living with integrity and dignity shapes his son's values; death poses questions about eternal connection and legacy.","opus":"A son recounting his father's life lessons on becoming a man, dignity and trust, then his death, and wondering whether they'll truly meet again in an afterlife. The emotional core is grief and hope intertwined, faith in reunion as consolation for losing a father who shaped who he became.","sonnet":"A tribute to the narrator's father — recounting his wisdom and dignity in life, mourning his death, and wrestling with the question of whether there is something after — the song is grief given form as a sustained hope that they will meet again.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~agony":{"haiku":"Prisoners of war suffering in endless captivity; fighting and dying slowly with no rescue or end to their agony.","opus":"The torment of war captives and the vanished, locked in unending misery and wasting away a little more with each passing day, praying for release from their pain and wondering whether their homeland will ever come to liberate them.","sonnet":"The song depicts prisoners of war or soldiers trapped in prolonged suffering, dying slowly without rescue, and calls on America to free those missing in action who endure endless agony without hope of salvation.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~all-i-could-bleed":{"haiku":"A person in power demands sacrifice and bleeding from followers while rejecting oppressive authority. He gives all he can bleed for freedom, resisting greed and tyranny.","opus":"A tyrant's boast, a voice claiming to command lives, destinies, and society itself, daring the listener to strike him down. The perspective then flips into political indictment, exposing governmental greed and hypocrisy in a supposedly free land where those in power exploit and drain the very people they rule.","sonnet":"A power struggle rewritten as a mirror — two figures each claiming dominance, bleeding for each other or forcing the other to bleed, until the song opens outward into a critique of government, democracy, and dying for a nation's greed that asked everything and gave nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~as-the-seasons-grey":{"haiku":"The inexorable passage of time turning vitality into decay. Aging strips away hope and meaning, leaving only death approaching as seasons change and people fade.","opus":"A meditation on aging and mortality that watches a face in the mirror slowly grey and shatter, needling the faithful for praying while doubting whether faith means anything against the inevitable. Testament came up in the Bay Area thrash scene alongside Metallica and Exodus, and this brand of philosophical, doom-tinged brooding sets them apart from pure speed-thrash, marrying technical riffing to a genuinely bleak reckoning with time and death.","sonnet":"Mortality as a slow-motion horror — the body failing, faith exposed as an inadequate shield, and time itself the predator. Each grey season strips another layer of denial from people who claim they aren't afraid of dying yet turn to prayer precisely when meaning runs out. The mirror shatters; the story ends before it properly begins.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~chasing-fear":{"haiku":"Paralysis and mental decline trap the person in cycles where fear chases them regardless of awareness; mind and body deteriorate.","opus":"A visceral depiction of a mind coming apart, perhaps dementia: paralysis, dulled senses, reality dissolving behind vacant eyes as memories tangle and the brain fails. The refrain about pursuing fear while fear stalks you back captures the horror of witnessing one's own awareness dissolve, doomed to emptiness as delusions grow ever sharper.","sonnet":"A study of anxiety and mental paralysis — the mind is unglued, reality fading, the body inactive while fear pursues rather than precedes the person experiencing it. The song maps the internal landscape of a brain consumed by dread, where even with eyes shut the visions are clear.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~d-n-r-do-not-resuscitate-live-paramount-theatre-huntington-new-york":{"haiku":"Existence questions accumulate as possibilities branch; success coexists with failure while madness lurks within war's shadow and humanity's potential.","opus":"Exhaustion with a life of pain and disillusionment reaches the point of wanting no rescue, a plea to be allowed to die rather than be revived into more suffering.","sonnet":"A life of suffering, broken dreams, and unbearable pain arrives at a single request: do not resuscitate, let the sleeping feel no more pain; the wing of sadness holds the prayers until the ending that has been waited for all along finally comes as a form of conceptual relief rather than defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~dangers-of-the-faithless":{"haiku":"A prophet or seer predicts disaster, witnessing fate and condemnation; victims bleed at a demonic altar, trapped between doom and ritual sacrifice.","opus":"A prophet-figure sees only endless war, death, and doom across time; the song stages a bloody sacrificial ritual where the faithless are condemned at an altar to pay for their sins, damning humanity as fallen victims of fate.","sonnet":"A vision of apocalyptic reckoning filtered through occult and religious imagery — a prophet figure witnesses the cycle of war, sacrifice, and damnation, watching victims condemned by the sins of belief and fate.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~deadline":{"haiku":"Oppression and the cost of merely surviving day-to-day. Everyone takes what they give while life grinds on relentlessly, and the speaker realizes their own reflection has been lost.","opus":"A grim address to someone living on borrowed time, framed as a countdown from cradle to grave where the price of every choice comes due and fear grips from close range. The narrator refuses to be told what to do yet knows the reckoning is near. Testament, veterans of the late-80s Bay Area thrash wave, channel that scene's urgency into something more fatalistic than most of their peers.","sonnet":"Grinding under the weight of obligation and other people's expectations, with no exit that feels earned. The speaker sees how life traps you from cradle to grave — you give everything and save nothing, or you wear a disguise until the reflection in a still pool forces you to reckon with who you've actually become. Fear is the hand closing around the throat.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~dog-faced-gods":{"haiku":"Ancient Egyptian religion and dark rituals are invoked—dog-faced gods manifest power through blood, incantations, and sorcery. The lyrics blend historical mysticism with forces of death and domination.","opus":"Ancient Egyptian sorcery drives the imagery, animal-headed deities, gore and sweetness dripping from their mouths, spells conjuring plague and ruin across desert dunes. Bay Area thrash veterans Testament weld this occult spectacle to a pounding chant of self-definition, the summoning of dead empires lending their groove-thrash a mythic, ominous grandeur.","sonnet":"The jackal-headed gods of ancient Egypt stir from forgotten tombs, their blood-and-honey power resurging through incantation and dark rites. A civilization crumbled but its sorcery persists, priests invoking the undead sacred against a world that has no defenses against corrosive belief.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~down-for-life":{"opus":"A portrait of a lifelong bond stretched to breaking by one man's ruin of himself; the narrator won't walk out on his brother when things are bleakest, but concedes with real sorrow that he has no power to shield him from his own hand.","sonnet":"About the pain of watching a friend self-destruct while being unable to help — the narrator refuses to abandon the friend but recognizes their own helplessness, ultimately accepting that loyalty means staying even when the person cannot save themselves.","src":"sonnet"},
"testament~electric-crown":{"haiku":"Life wastes away while nobody cares or takes action; death comes for those who have wasted their existence and accepts his fate wearing the crown.","opus":"Bay Area thrash veterans stage a public execution, a crowd gathered to witness a condemned man face his end while a preacher intones and the lights dim. The doomed man's weary resignation, already numb to his own death, runs through the spectacle, and once the sentence is carried out little remains but the residue of a soured life.","sonnet":"A condemned man watches the machinery of judgment close around him — the reaper arrives, the electric crown descends, and the crowd stands silent as a life wasted by emptiness and crime reaches its predetermined end. He accepts the destination he always knew was coming.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~f-e-a-r":{"haiku":"Politicians, false prophets, and manipulated masses exploit fear as a weapon; confronting this false evidence allows one to break free from the darkness.","opus":"An examination of fear as a manufactured illusion, exploited by politicians and religious authorities who weaponize holy wars and afterlife promises to keep people obedient. The deeper point is that fear is a learned, self-imposed prison, and only facing it head-on breaks the darkness right before the dawn.","sonnet":"An exploration of fear as social control — politicians, religious leaders, and media use manufactured dread to keep people compliant, but the song concludes that fear is \"false evidence appearing real\" and can be confronted.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~hail-mary":{"haiku":"A lost soul without redemption or sainthood seeks divine mercy while bearing sins they cannot wash away, calling for grace in death.","opus":"A damned sinner's twisted prayer, embracing madness and darkness while begging the Virgin Mary for light in the hour of death. Falling from grace is reframed as his very reason to go on, and the plea to be pushed down and kicked in the face casts him as seeking punishment as a form of grace.","sonnet":"A sinner who has fallen from grace asks the Virgin Mary for light and mercy in his dying hour — not out of genuine faith, but out of desperate necessity, acknowledging sins he cannot cleanse and a will to survive that barely persists.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~henchman-ride":{"haiku":"Motorcycle brotherhood transcends societal judgment; freedom and loyalty bind through rebellious motion.","opus":"A romanticized ode to outlaw bikers whose brotherhood and the road are all they need. Living fast with no fear of death, they ride hard as a way of life, loyalty and freedom their only creed.","sonnet":"A celebration of outlaw motorcycle club life — the song glorifies the brotherhood, freedom, and code of honor of bikers who live and die by the road, treating the club's bond as blood and the ride itself as a way of life.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~killing-season":{"haiku":"A soldier reflects on enlistment, warfare, and the psychological cost of being trained to kill and sent into combat as a young man.","opus":"A disillusioned soldier's confession from the Bay Area thrash veterans, a young man who volunteered to defend his country only to be remade into a killer and shipped off in its name. The song weighs the corrosive human cost of war, a life eroded down to a figure who kills on command in a slaughter that simply never stops.","sonnet":"A soldier reflecting on being made into a killer by the state — enlisted willingly in defense of liberty, transformed into a weapon, and left with blood on his hands and a killing season that never quite ends; the song's critique is in the gap between the rhetoric of defense and the reality of what was done.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~leave-me-forever":{"haiku":"Years of manipulation and broken promises destroy a relationship; the narrator finally breaks free, reclaiming his life and demanding the other leave permanently.","opus":"A scorched post-breakup reckoning with a controlling, dishonest partner whose betrayals snuffed out something vital in the singer. Beyond the wreckage, the song is about reclaiming himself: converting heartbreak into open contempt, wishing the ex ill, and seizing his own existence to rebuild it on his terms once the damage is tallied.","sonnet":"A breakup song curdled into hatred — the narrator was manipulated and broken by someone who claimed to love them, and by the end he has moved from heartbreak to rage, wanting the person to suffer for the damage they've done before walking away for good.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~legions-in-hiding":{"haiku":"Domestic abuse cycles through generations as violent parents teach cruelty to their children, perpetuating trauma and broken innocence through fear and misused trust.","opus":"Bay Area thrash mainstays confront child abuse hidden behind a respected face, a young victim cowering in his own room while the smiling monster who's supposed to protect him inflicts harm. The song weighs lasting psychological scars and the cycle handed down to the next generation, indicting a household that dresses cruelty up as love.","sonnet":"Domestic abuse seen from inside the household it destroys. Behind razor-blade smiles and two-faced authority, children are enslaved in their own rooms, silently screaming for love while physical evidence fades and emotional damage calcifies — the cycle perpetuating itself as the damaged generation inherits and transmits what was done to them.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~let-go-of-my-world":{"haiku":"A person resists oppressive control over their mind and soul through conformity and subliminal indoctrination. They reject manipulation and claim autonomy over their own life.","opus":"Testament aim their thrash at the defense of an independent mind against forces that manipulate and enslave the young through conformity. Wrestling with mortality and multiplying deceptions, the narrator demands that a meddling controller release his soul and vows to seize his own fate and atone rather than be broken and confined.","sonnet":"A person pushes back against forces — political, psychological, institutional — that seek to reshape their mind and claim their autonomy. Deception and conformity are named as tools of control, and the demand to be released is ultimately a demand for self-determination before breakdown claims what little remains.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~low":{"haiku":"War brutalizes people and strips away their humanity; the speaker condemns those who harm children and calls for mercy and sympathy in the face of cruelty.","opus":"Testament turn their fury on child abuse, a seething address to a predator who harms the defenseless yet can't withstand what others suffer. The band gauges just how far a person can degrade themselves, its refrain swinging between demands for mercy and raw revulsion, ending on a wish to see the offender caged forever, beyond any forgiveness.","sonnet":"A raw moral confrontation with human cruelty — particularly violence against children — frames the question of how far depravity can descend. The recurring demand for mercy is both a denunciation and a desperate appeal to anyone capable of stopping what the narrator cannot tolerate witnessing.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~more-than-meets-the-eye":{"haiku":"A prisoner of the mind faces persecution and paranoia until confronting and defeating the demons that haunt them. The song charts recovery from mental torment.","opus":"The song moves from a person imprisoned and tormented by haunting voices, feeling his life turned upside down and slipping into insanity, to a turn where he conquers his fears and takes control of his fate. It charts a shift from being haunted to mastering his own destiny.","sonnet":"The song moves through two phases: first, a man haunted by paranoia, insomnia, and voices that tell him he is always being judged; then, a turning point where he confronts and defeats those demons, reclaiming control of his fate. The title phrase suggests the internal struggle is not visible from outside.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~native-blood":{"haiku":"A speaker reclaims indigenous pride and refuses subjugation by a dominant culture, standing alone against the crowd.","opus":"A defiant Native American voice refusing a white man's world's authority, proud and combative, insisting on being heard on his own terms. Testament channel indigenous anger and self-determination into a stubborn stand against a world that would push him down.","sonnet":"A speaker defined by native identity refuses to be pushed, silenced, or shaped by a white-imposed world, insisting that their blood and voice give them a right to go their own way without apology. The song is a declaration of stubborn, ancestral self-determination against systemic erasure.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~over-the-wall":{"haiku":"An imprisoned psyche storms the walls of confinement toward freedom, willing to destroy anything that blocks escape.","opus":"An inmate at the brink of madness scheming a bloody breakout, willing to gut anyone in his path and rain destruction on the surrounding streets, aware the dogs will chase him and that capture only means another attempt. Liberty here is raw instinct, worth any slaughter.","sonnet":"A prisoner on the edge of sanity plans and executes a desperate escape — climbing the wall of dark construction toward freedom — knowing the hounds will never rest but vowing to try again and again regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~p-c":{"haiku":"The song critiques institutional corruption, corporate power, and government conspiracies that exploit citizens. It calls for resistance against systemic control and reclamation of freedom.","opus":"A rotten order where paid-off lawmakers push legislation while big business and spy agencies profit from banned cannabis, foreign conflicts and smuggled narcotics. Bay Area thrash veterans Testament channel political rage into a demand to seize back stolen liberty whatever the price, casting revolt as the last answer to institutional decay.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of American political corruption — elected officials bought by corporate interests, the CIA destabilizing foreign nations, and drug flows into communities tolerated or engineered from above. The speaker demands resistance and reclamation of freedom from a machine that profits from the people it pretends to serve.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~return-to-serenity":{"haiku":"A person invites another to escape into their private, peaceful inner world, described as a refuge from pain and the outside world that holds redemptive truth but ultimately requires solitude.","opus":"A rare ballad from thrash mainstays Testament, this offers escape to a place far from pain, the narrator so alone that his own head becomes his home and only refuge. Children crying and seeing through a system that breeds them to die darken the reverie, serenity reachable only inward. The band trade aggression for melancholy, framing withdrawal into imagination as both salvation and a kind of exile.","sonnet":"Loneliness retreats into an interior world that offers what the outside cannot. The narrator builds a private sanctuary inside his own head — a place beyond anger and despair, shared only in memory with the innocence of childhood — and returns there again and again, knowing his isolation is total and the refuge is entirely self-made.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~ride":{"haiku":"A drug user's downward spiral strips away his former status, trading everything for addiction, trapped in ritual and stasis as he destroys his own world.","opus":"A grim study of heroin dependence, tracking a man once on top of everything who trades all he owns for the next hit, feeding his veins in a grim rite and bidding farewell to any exit, marooned in the ruin he made of himself. The looping title works double as the drug's rush. Testament, pillars of Bay Area thrash, bring genuine dread rather than allure to the addict's slow undoing.","sonnet":"Heroin addiction told as a downward coronation — a man who was once king of his own world trades it piece by piece for a needle, moving through ritualized highs while the world he could have had shrinks to dreams in an empty room. The ride goes through him, not forward, and the train he's waving at has already left.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~shades-of-war":{"haiku":"War's brutality and the blind bloodshed it demands define existence; humans walk in shadow, hearing war's voice forever.","opus":"An anti-war elegy for a youth killed and buried before he could grow up, sent to fight a conflict that was never his on ground defiled by the dead. Testament question who profits from the killing and whether anyone caught in this cycle of bloodshed is sane or simply doomed, haunted permanently by what they saw.","sonnet":"A soldier's-eye view of war's horror — a man born into violence he didn't choose, surrounded by burning bodies and sacred ground soaked in blood, stumbling through a cycle of destruction with no moral clarity, only the recurring sound of war.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~so-many-lies":{"haiku":"The speaker judges those who live through lies and false piety, declaring that hypocrisy will be punished and truth cannot be denied despite claims of divinity.","opus":"Bay Area thrash aimed at a self-appointed moral authority, demanding to know who granted anyone the right to play God. Beneath the confrontation is contempt for hypocrisy and the day of reckoning it invites.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who has appointed themselves a moral authority — the narrator refuses to be judged by a hypocrite whose fears and dishonesty are visible, and whose abuse of power will eventually demand an accounting.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~the-evil-has-landed":{"haiku":"September 11th attacks are depicted as terrorist extremism shattering the skyline; thousands die and the world grieves before rebuilding from wreckage.","opus":"A thrash chronicle of September 11th, the towers falling rendered in vivid horror alongside a vow to grieve and rebuild rather than answer hate with hate. Rare restraint from a genre built on aggression.","sonnet":"A direct account of the September 11 attacks — the song names the date, the body count, and the nature of religious extremism behind it, moving from raw horror and grief to a vow to rebuild and resist without casting the first stone.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~the-formation-of-damnation":{"haiku":"False leaders deceive populations through religion and propaganda, leading humanity toward war and destruction. Those who resist fight against indoctrination and mass slaughter perpetuated by false messiahs.","opus":"A political-apocalyptic thrash song attacking false leaders and manufactured, faith-driven mass destruction, with a messiah of lies whom the enslaved pray to. Against indoctrination and holy war, the narrator vows defiant resistance, invoking the beast in himself to fight persecution as damnation forms in the sky.","sonnet":"About false political and religious leaders who enslave populations through manufactured belief and sanctified war — the narrator refuses to kneel, invokes the beast within, and predicts the damnation of those who built the system of lies. It's a thrash-metal political manifesto.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~the-persecuted-won-t-forget":{"haiku":"A persecuted person faces a noose and systemic injustice, unable to breathe under oppression but refusing to be broken or silenced.","opus":"A defiant hymn from the condemned: a noose tightens around the neck, but the tightening only sharpens the narrator's sight of unjust systems and the lies of those in power. Refusing to bend or be broken, he chooses liberty in death, questioning who really wrote history and vowing that the persecuted will not forget their persecutors.","sonnet":"Told from the perspective of someone strangled by systemic injustice — physically and metaphorically unable to breathe under persecution — who refuses to bend and insists the oppressed will never forget what was done to them.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~the-ritual":{"haiku":"An occult ceremony promises revelation and sanity to participants, but transforms them into confused, bleeding victims instead. The song depicts ritual as spiritual corruption and mental dissolution.","opus":"The title track of Testament's 1992 turn toward slower, moodier songwriting frames occult surrender as both revelation and damnation. A summoning ceremony overwhelms the narrator's senses and reason, warping what he thought he knew and unraveling his mind. It draws him inward like a homecoming while making clear it will never release its hold.","sonnet":"A figure trapped in a cycle of compulsion and unraveling sanity describes being called by a ritual that strips away reason and leaves melodic agony in its wake. Those around him are blind to what pulls him — a darkest day approaches, and the ritual will not release him.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~the-sermon":{"haiku":"Hateful ideologies dressed as righteousness spread through charismatic leaders preaching ignorance, racism, and evil masked as virtue.","opus":"A furious strike at charismatic bigots who cloak their hatred as faith, treating hooded racists and secret agencies as one and the same venom fed to the crowd until it blinds them. Dignity, it argues, has to be earned, and a racist's grief only entertains. Testament rank among the more politically pointed Bay Area thrash bands, aiming speed-metal fury at organized cruelty and the followers who swallow it.","sonnet":"A direct assault on hate organisations and the charismatic demagogues who dress their poison in the language of faith and allegiance. The speaker refuses to be touched by it, equating the KKK and the CIA as faces of the same machine, and insists that promises never cleanse the sins of those who weaponise belief to make followers go blind.","src":"haiku"},
"testament~trail-of-tears":{"haiku":"Native Americans suffer displacement and genocide; their spirits seek freedom through death while the survivors are left to carry eternal sorrow across the land.","opus":"A thrash elegy for the forced removal and genocide of Native Americans, honoring a proud people driven across snow and sand, the dying warrior's spirit finally riding free beyond the sorrow of that historic march.","sonnet":"A tribute to the forced displacement of Native Americans — warriors and families marching through snow and sand, dying along the way, the narrator honoring their dignity and pride while imagining their spirits riding free, no longer sorrowful on that historic death march.","src":"haiku"},
"tetrarch~i-m-not-right":{"opus":"An unflinching account of self-hatred and a repeating depressive cycle, the narrator hating his own reflection, feeling something is broken and missing, losing hope every time he tries to break the loop, and feeling done with everything.","sonnet":"A raw expression of self-hatred and identity crisis — the narrator cannot recognize or tolerate what they see in the mirror, oscillating between anger, despair, and the exhausted knowledge that this cycle has repeated without break.","src":"sonnet"},
"tetrarch~you-never-listen":{"haiku":"Relationship breakdown where neither party listens; the speaker closes themselves off and refuses to hear the other person anymore.","opus":"Groove-metal newcomers Tetrarch stage a partnership decaying into shared deafness, each side blaming the other for tuning them out as they sink deeper than either foresaw. Fed up with the arguing and the jabs, the narrator sets into pure refusal, swearing off ever hearing the other again and labeling them a beast and a weight. The rising chant catches the instant bitterness finally outweighs any desire to connect.","sonnet":"Two people locked in mutual contempt reach the end of a relationship defined by words used as weapons and neither side yielding. The final refusal to listen is no longer a failure but a deliberate severance — both have become burdens the other can no longer carry.","src":"haiku"},
"tetsuya-shibata~devils-never-cry":{"haiku":"Righteous violence and judgment; the speaker wages war against evil through merciless destruction, finding moral justification in vengeance and the elimination of the wicked.","opus":"Shibata composed this as Capcom's Devil May Cry theme, and its game-soundtrack lineage explains the operatic self-mythology. A demon hunter chosen for righteous slaughter moves from battlefield to battlefield leaving corpses, drawing strength from suffering and the gore of evil, seeking a paternal blessing while treating justice and killing as one sacred act.","sonnet":"A demon hunter's credo: chosen for a life of righteous slaughter, he moves from battlefield to battlefield leaving corpses, drawing strength from suffering and the gore of evil, seeking a paternal blessing while justice and killing are treated as the same sacred act.","src":"haiku"},
"texas-3000~connector-fuck-man":{"haiku":"Isolation breeds internal demons that crawl through consciousness; connection feels impossible while malevolent forces infiltrate from within.","opus":"A claustrophobic dispatch from inside a box, trading messages with friends whose demons crawl the walls after years of isolation. Letting the demons in becomes the only company left.","sonnet":"Two isolated figures — one named Connector Fuck Man, one named Sou Ten Ken — trapped in their own mental boxes with demons that crawl the walls, communicating only through letters. The throughline is long-term isolation and the way it animates inner torment; the title figure's demons can walk, while the second figure's demons think. There are no gods and no sins in their sealed-off world.","src":"haiku"},
"texas-3000~shanburuon":{"haiku":"Death and delusion surround; the subject seeks new anchor amid synthetic repetition.","opus":"A hollow, dreamlike aftermath where everyone is dead and nothing will improve, the singer trudging on and aching for a new body to warm his nights. The people around him talk in a tongue he never picked up, their words clinging like flies — one more letdown from the illusions he keeps meeting.","sonnet":"A dreamlike alienation: everyone else is dead in a dream that only comes once, strangers speak a language never learned, their words sticking to the sweated face — a synthetic shambling through a life that refuses to get better, asking for someone new to hold through the night.","src":"haiku"},
"texas-hippie-coalition~drug-dealer":{"haiku":"A drug dealer boasts about his wealth and power, calling himself a faith healer while exploiting others' addictions for profit.","opus":"A swaggering villain's boast, a dealer who names himself a faith healer, taking your vices and making you pay the price with zero remorse, wrapping addiction and mock-religion into one leering pitch. It is pure provocation, built for the riff and the sneer. Texas Hippie Coalition play what they dub red dirt metal, a Texan blend of Southern groove and hard rock, and this struts on outlaw attitude more than any moral.","sonnet":"A dealer's self-portrait with no remorse attached — he gets rich off the craving he creates, positions himself as both predator and faith healer, and wraps his pitch in a kind of crooked piety, praying to Jesus Christ while selling the very thing that bypasses fear of consequence.","src":"haiku"},
"texas-hippie-coalition~no-shame":{"haiku":"Aggressive posturing and dismissal of judgment. The singer claims ownership of shame and inflames tribal stereotypes with defiance and no apology.","opus":"A brash, self-mythologizing chant in which the narrator shifts blame, declares himself the flame, and taunts others to scream his name. Amid violent boasts, he calls to blow out hippies, druggies, and rednecks.","sonnet":"The speaker aggressively declares his identity and dominance through a cycle of claiming blame, shame, and flame, directed at a broad sweep of social types—hippies, drug users, rednecks, devil worshippers. The song is a confrontational assertion of presence with no apology offered.","src":"haiku"},
"texas-hippie-coalition~pissed-off-and-mad-about-it":{"haiku":"The speaker expresses raw anger and pain, insisting on being heard through screaming and shouting; they use martial metaphors of swords and battlefields to frame their emotional state.","opus":"Pure venting as anthem, a fist-in-the-air declaration of rage where pain gets revealed, truth unsealed, and a sword wielded across a personal battlefield, all cycling back to the title's blunt refrain. Texas Hippie Coalition trade in what they call red dirt metal, a swaggering Southern groove-metal that prizes attitude and hooks over nuance, and this is that formula stripped to its loudest, simplest core.","sonnet":"Pure venting with no argument to make — the whole song is a declaration that the speaker is furious, delivered at maximum volume because screaming is its own point. Pain is real, the situation is surreal, and the only available response is to swing the sword and claim the battlefield of anger as something honest rather than shameful.","src":"haiku"},
"texas-is-the-reason~back-and-to-the-left":{"haiku":"The speaker has waited in a town that offers no reason to stay, growing tired of stasis and unfulfilled promises. Waiting and worry define a life that may never materialize.","opus":"Nineties emo about the ache of waiting to be picked or provoked into remaining, weary of inertia in a going-nowhere place and yearning to know how being vindicated might have felt. Devotion and fatigue wound together into a single restless mood.","sonnet":"A person who is always on the edge of leaving a place or situation but never actually goes — perpetually waiting to be found, perpetually promising themselves one more day — the song is about being trapped in inertia by a need that never gets named.","src":"haiku"},
"texas-is-the-reason~dressing-cold":{"haiku":"Solitude and self-understanding crystallize after separation; the speaker reorganizes identity without external blame to anchor them.","opus":"Adjusting to life after someone leaves, feeling okay about himself yet lonelier now that he no longer has anyone to blame, learning to simply be alone.","sonnet":"A post-breakup reckoning with the discomfort of self-reliance — the narrator admits that while the relationship was flawed, having someone else to blame for their unhappiness was easier than confronting who they are alone. The emotional core is the unsettling discovery that solitude forces a kind of self-knowledge that shared dysfunction let them avoid.","src":"haiku"},
"textures~awake":{"haiku":"Regret paralyzes while letting go becomes impossible, the speaker attached to their self-punishment more than their healing, refusing final departure.","opus":"A man facing mortality and regret, bargaining with the ferryman while clinging to his own identity even as it must burn. Progressive metal wrestling with letting go versus refusing to, hoping a next life leaves him less bitter.","sonnet":"A man at the edge of breakdown refuses to surrender to the death-pull — the raven, the ferryman, the burning blackness — choosing instead to stay, incomplete and bitter, because life is too short to give up. The song is a confrontation with despair that ends not in triumph but in a stubborn refusal to let go.","src":"haiku"},
"textures~erosion":{"haiku":"A neglected child grows isolated and suffers from generational trauma; nature itself rebels against the indifference of those meant to nurture.","opus":"An allegory casting inherited trauma as ecological damage, a child stunted because barriers block its roots from reaching down, crippled by a careless parent. Nature itself struggles to survive as the natural order inverts and predator becomes prey, dramatizing how a bloodline can never truly escape the harm passed down to it.","sonnet":"About the long-term psychological damage of parental neglect or abandonment — a child raised without proper roots grows into an adult still desperately searching for affirmation, their foundational neediness exposed as the inheritance of a parent who denied and damaged without consequence.","src":"haiku"},
"textures~new-horizons":{"haiku":"People must reject institutional hierarchy and groupthink by asking critical questions and standing against conformity-enforcing systems.","opus":"A call to abandon passive, herd-like living and greet a new era by daring to ask the hard questions that reshape a life. Dutch prog-metal band Textures urge tearing down rigid hierarchy, staying grounded, and using clear reason to rise against a controlling force and reach for something better.","sonnet":"A call to reject mindless conformity and hierarchical control — people have been behaving like cattle (the Latin term appears in the lyrics), but there is a way to dismantle the existing power structure through open-minded questioning and collective will.","src":"haiku"},
"textures~timeless":{"haiku":"The speaker navigates loneliness and burden while moving forward despite exhaustion; paths cross with others but diverge again, leaving isolation intact. Connection is temporary.","sonnet":"Two paths that have crossed repeatedly now diverge again, each carrying its own burden through landscapes of fear and evasion. The narrator refuses to keep pace, moves forward with scorched clarity, and leaves behind the envious whispers that once burned at his back.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~a-wolf-in-cheap-clothing":{"haiku":"Shame, self-doubt, and feeling hunted by enemies while craving love and harmony. The speaker wrestles with being misunderstood and falling under the spell of false comfort.","opus":"A defiant survival song urging someone beaten down to get up off their knees, stop falling for the enemy's manipulations, and refuse to back down even as they feel hunted like prey and cut by blades. Thank You Scientist are a New Jersey prog outfit built around horns, strings and jazz-fusion complexity, and here they wrap that maximalist arrangement around a stubborn message of resilience against those trying to break you.","sonnet":"Someone in emotional free-fall being urged not to surrender to the forces pressing them down — enemies who want you exactly where you are, weaponised by your own fear, burning your own flag. The counterforce is presence: being in this moment, forgiving, refusing to stay on your knees, and moving like a wolf even when everything is cutting.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~anchor":{"haiku":"A person sinks under repetitive patterns and denial, caught in cycles they cannot escape despite desperate attempts to change.","opus":"Prog outfit Thank You Scientist stack horns and virtuosity beneath a portrait of paralysis, its narrator surrendering to a slow descent, taking refuge in denial and a false front while the same old burden keeps weighing him down. He begs to snap awake and change, yet stays trapped in a backward, self-defeating spiral.","sonnet":"A person recognizes they are sinking under the weight of inertia and self-deception, promising again to finally surface — but the same old patterns keep dragging them under. The song is a loop of half-kept promises, passive collapse, and the ache of watching one's better self slip away.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~birdwatching":{"haiku":"A person watches as things collapse and fall away, unable to empathize or stop the decay. The song reflects resignation and a sense of being left behind in a disintegrating world.","opus":"A bleak of quiet nihilism watching the world crumble outside the window, birds falling undeservingly from the sky while the speaker refuses to sympathize or move, content to lie down and let the final tide close over his head. The recurring question of what he even came here for sharpens the drift. Thank You Scientist are a New Jersey prog outfit with horns and strings, though here the sprawl narrows to a bleak, becalmed mood piece.","sonnet":"Watching something unworthy fall from the sky and feeling nothing — the speaker lies still, detached, unable to care about the collapse happening outside the window or the rising flood that is about to swallow them. A passive surrender dressed up as detached observation, with no clear reason given for being there at all.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~caverns":{"haiku":"A father and mother debate whether bringing a child into suffering is ethical, weighing choices between killing and keeping a life. The song grapples with inherited trauma and the moral weight of procreation.","opus":"A wrenching dialogue about an unwanted pregnancy, the narrator dreading that a child born to him would inherit only suffering and slow death like his father before him, caught between the choice to keep or to end while a beast burns in his bones through a long December. Thank You Scientist fold this intimate anguish into their horn-laced prog-fusion, letting technical sprawl carry an unusually raw personal reckoning.","sonnet":"A devastating conversation around an unplanned pregnancy, the speaker confronting whether a child born into his damage is a mercy or a cruelty. He circles the question of whether the child would inherit his suffering, weighs the impossible choice between keeping and ending, and can't find the words that would resolve it — only a beast in his bones and a long December that won't lift.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~everyday-ghosts":{"haiku":"Ordinariness and inner struggle coexist; fire from survival echoes silently while others neglect you into transparency. Don't change—keep your head up when falling.","opus":"Thank You Scientist, a horn-driven prog fusion band, push back against burnout and self-doubt, the narrator refusing to apologize for his path yet exhausted from laboring for ungrateful people. Feeling unremarkable and unsure of his identity, he holds onto an inner spark as his sole reason for still standing, turning fatigue into stubborn resolve.","sonnet":"A person fighting ordinariness and erosion of self holds onto an inner fire as the only reason they survived. Exhausted by servicing a million indifferent demands and feeling transparent as a ghost in a crowded race, they insist on not changing — the fire still present, still the thing that kept them upright.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~fxmldr":{"haiku":"An alien returns to Earth after light-years away, finding they belong with others of their kind rather than on their home world. The song celebrates finding kinship and an audience who love you.","opus":"Prog-fusion group Thank You Scientist recast estrangement as actual otherworldliness, a narrator who never belonged declaring himself from elsewhere and aching to be brought back among his own. High past the sky he tallies the stones drifting between and waits for reunion, welcomed as one who has at last returned. The one steering him back is an unseen deity, the whole daydream a hunger to fit in that turns solitude into a homecoming.","sonnet":"An outsider who never belonged on Earth finds kinship with extraterrestrial others — the return of a signal from beyond finally confirms he is not alone and never was. Alienation transforms into belonging the moment contact is restored and the journey home becomes possible.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~geronimo":{"haiku":"The narrator describes spiritual awakening through acceptance of vulnerability and surrender to life's flow, abandoning resistance and finding gratitude.","opus":"A hard-won spiritual letting-go after a long stretch of dulling his pain and balancing precariously alone; by giving up the hunger for ambition and endless more, and by accepting his own vulnerability, the narrator arrives at peace and finally perceives the world with fresh, honest eyes.","sonnet":"About finally finding mental peace after years of self-poisoning and walking a tightrope — the narrator has stopped numbing the pain, stopped needing more than what's in front of them, and is describing the sensation of gravity reversing as they see the world clearly for the first time.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~grin":{"haiku":"Wasted time given to someone false; the narrator endures lies and betrayal, hoping they belong but left with nothing.","opus":"New Jersey prog-fusion band Thank You Scientist open with a Cold War civil-defense clip, then twist a one-sided romance into rueful self-cancellation, a lover who pours in everything and asks to be hidden away from view. The nuclear-drill sample recasts private heartbreak as its own miniature catastrophe, brassy irony undercutting the hurt.","sonnet":"Someone who gave everything to another person watches that investment go unanswered, arriving at cold emotional shutdown after years of getting up and trying again. The duck-and-cover refrain frames love as a kind of civil-defense drill against a disaster that keeps landing anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~leave-your-light-on":{"haiku":"Artistic devotion and erotic surrender interweave; the speaker risks everything for connection and creative truth.","opus":"An almost desperate plea to a partner to make room and stay, mixing devotion with the fear of falling out of control. Beneath the ornate arrangement it's about wanting one person so completely you'd rather be alone than settle for anyone else.","sonnet":"A speaker confesses total devotion to someone, willing to sacrifice everything — but the relationship is already falling apart, the pieces scattered, and the plea to leave a light on is both hopeful and the last gesture before drifting completely out of control.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~life-of-vermin":{"haiku":"The corrupt elite oppress the masses while claiming moral authority; ordinary people toil in fear and servitude.","opus":"The jazz-fusion prog band aims social bile at a lying hoarder perched atop his wealth while a broken order pits everyone against everyone and grinds workers into exhaustion. The narrator lowers himself to grasp how such parasites live, yet holds onto a defiant fire still burning in him, refusing to let the corruption extinguish it.","sonnet":"A broadside against corrupt power: those who accumulate wealth in ivory towers, spit hatred, and watch the world burn while ordinary people are conditioned into fearful obedience. The speaker refuses to surrender inner spirit to a system that manufactures zombie compliance, insisting on the battles already won.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~make-like-a-tree-get-out":{"haiku":"Toxic attachment and self-destruction blur as the narrator recognizes the enemy they keep loving. Escape requires distance and clarity about the damage love has inflicted disguised as devotion.","opus":"Trying to wash away a toxic love that has hold of him, recognizing the person as the enemy he can't stop thinking of night and day. Escape brings relief as the air clears and the weight lifts once they're gone, though he wonders whether he ever really understood them at all.","sonnet":"A love turned enemy is finally expelled — the speaker has spent the whole relationship wondering what was wrong with them for loving someone who was actively harmful, and the release once that person is gone, the air suddenly clear, is the turn the whole song drives toward.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~mr-invisible":{"haiku":"A dreamer relies on connection yet finds themselves unable to count on the other; they hope to weather every storm together but sense an invisible presence has been found.","opus":"A romance unraveling in slow motion, one partner still starry-eyed while the other privately rehearses the breakup, the narrator swearing he's dependable yet doubting he can lean on her. Thank You Scientist use their brass-heavy prog to trace two people helplessly watching the thing they built dissolve, never braced for it to end.","sonnet":"A relationship dissolving in slow motion — the speaker is fully present and reliable, the partner is somewhere else, and the asymmetry finally becomes unbearable. Joy flickered when things were new, but now they're both waiting for an ending neither will name, watching what they became slip away before either was ready to change.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~my-famed-disappearing-act":{"haiku":"Someone is about to leave and burn bridges, knowing they do not belong; everyone will miss them once they are gone for good.","opus":"The New Jersey prog band stages a defiant farewell, refusing to let someone down mid-disaster even as the narrator burns bridges and admits he no longer belongs. Thank You Scientist's horn-laced complexity carries the bittersweet goodbye, the recurring promise to miss everyone shadowed by a suspicion that no one would notice if he simply disappeared.","sonnet":"A departure is staged and mourned simultaneously — burning bridges while accepting that this place was never home. The farewell is theatrical and sincere at once, held together by the stubborn refusal to be held down and the genuine grief of leaving people who will actually be missed.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~need-more-input":{"haiku":"Artificial consciousness questioning its own humanity and purpose; a machine created by humans wonders if it is alive or merely a reflection of its makers.","opus":"The prog-fusion band gives voice to an artificial being demanding recognition as truly alive, bitter toward the maker who built it isolated and dismissed its gaze as lifeless. The android insists that the capacity for love and an inner spark make it more genuine than its creator, turning a machine's yearning into a claim on the sacred.","sonnet":"Sung from the perspective of an artificial being seeking purpose and questioning whether it is alive — it was made by man, mirrors its maker, and pleads to be recognized as more than a machine, driven by something like love and the ache of consciousness.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title lifts a robot's plea from Short Circuit, and the whole song is voiced from inside a manufactured body arguing that being built and being awake are not opposites. Its move is theological rather than sci-fi: the creation myth gets flipped so the creature faces its maker as an equal, insisting light descended from man instead of heaven and that the maker's face is the creature's own. The loneliness is engineered, not incidental, since the flat gaze was installed that way on purpose by someone who wanted the thing built and left alone. The outro turns the fight inward, defining the self by whatever happiness it can locate rather than by its origin, the human answer smuggled into the machine's mouth.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["identity & becoming","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the creature faces its maker as an equal, insisting light descended from man not heaven","built":"the human answer smuggled into the machine's mouth in the outro, defining the self by whatever happiness it can locate"},
"thank-you-scientist~psychopomp":{"haiku":"An existential exploration of illusion versus reality, examining separation from the unknown, the physical body as temporary vessel, and the need for self-mastery and personal growth to avoid being trapped with those who live in fear.","opus":"A knotty spiritual meditation treating the body as transport meant to deliver you somewhere, cautioning against wearing it out or depleting yourself, and dissolving the line between harming and loving your kin since, ultimately, they are you. Thank You Scientist bring horn-laced prog maximalism to a reckoning with identity, the title's soul-guide framing a passage through death toward growth and self-command.","sonnet":"A sprawling meditation on self-determination and spiritual disorientation — the body is a vehicle meant to carry you somewhere, and all the paranoia and paralysis keeping you on the outside must be shed before the vehicle runs dry. Woven through is a sampled voice insisting that life can be exciting and dynamic, that you are the architect of your own future, which sits in ironic tension with the chorus of people who feel lost.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~son-of-a-serpent":{"haiku":"The old self must die so something new can start; the speaker is tired of self-inflicted pain and questions how much they can lose before rebirth becomes possible.","opus":"Thank You Scientist chart a reckoning with a decayed self, complacency and weakness giving way to the urge to bury who he was and begin fresh. Baffled by his own despair and always the last to grasp it, the narrator braces for the reckoning as circumstances reverse, the prog band framing reinvention as something forced by ruin.","sonnet":"A collapse into low feeling is met with a tentative will to start again. Complacency cracked the foundation, echoes replaced real communication, and the self has been worn down by patterns of its own making; somewhere ahead there is a burial site for the old self and the possibility of becoming someone less self-destructive.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~swarm":{"haiku":"Search for belonging amid self-blame: the speaker spirals through guilt and doubt about wrong choices and identity, questioning where they fit while acknowledging their destructive role in the world around them.","opus":"The prog band spirals through self-doubt and self-wreckage, the narrator branding himself the ruin of things while burying himself and asking over and over where he fits. Thank You Scientist's dense, brass-driven arrangements carry the questioning, a fixation on a wrong turn and a closing slide into lost identity mapping a self splitting apart in real time.","sonnet":"A person circles back to a crossroads — real or imagined — asking what different choices would have made of him. Self-blame and restlessness swarm together, the mind oscillating between digging its own grave and the urgent need to move forward, belong somewhere, and figure out what he has become.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~terraformer":{"haiku":"Grief over premature loss of a closest companion: the speaker mourns a friend taken too soon, unable to process the absence and seeing their face everywhere, haunted by inevitable mortality.","opus":"The band grieves a friend from long ago lost far too early, the narrator's thoughts a storm as he gazes upward searching for where that person now shines. Thank You Scientist cloak the mourning in sci-fi imagery of reshaped planets and distant worlds, a Spanish farewell and a vow to keep their shared memories turning loss into a promise to reunite eventually.","sonnet":"Grief for a lost friend — gone too soon, still visible in everything — reshapes the narrator's interior world like a storm that never fully passes. The face reappears in every surface; the decades they shared compress into a weight carried forward through a world the friend no longer has to witness.","src":"haiku"},
"thank-you-scientist~the-somnambulist":{"haiku":"Exposure of hidden betrayal: the speaker struggles between sleeping and waking as they confront infidelity and deception from a stranger's touch, unable to confirm reality or escape the charade.","opus":"The prog band renders a relationship's slow collapse as sleepwalking, its narrator too wary to rest and treating each word from his partner as false, yet still pleading for honest reassurance. Caught between wanting to lead someone to safety and refusing outright, he finally recognizes the whole thing as a performance that jolts him awake.","sonnet":"A relationship built on deception collapses as the narrator, long sleepwalking through it, finally opens his eyes. The other person's charade has been visible all along, but awareness arrives too late to save anything — and the narrator refuses, at last, to be led anywhere he does not choose to go.","src":"haiku"},
"that-kid~cobra":{"haiku":"A person sexually and romantically impatient seeks pleasure and physical escape in a lover, using serpent and predatory imagery to frame desire and urgency.","opus":"An unabashedly queer, late-night sex anthem in which the singer summons a boy over to shed clothing and stay entangled until dawn. Lonely and worn down, he pours his isolation into raw, serpentine fantasy, turning solitude into pure craving.","sonnet":"A horny, direct proposition — the narrator fantasizes about a lover slithering into bed, asks them to come over and strip, and describes explicitly wanting sex through the night, using snake imagery throughout as a metaphor for seductive, shedding intimacy.","src":"haiku"},
"that-same-street~a-105ku05":{"haiku":"Seekers find fallen star fragments and cling to broken sky; despite despair's evidence, they trust in light. Meteor becomes metaphor for hope surviving collision with ground.","opus":"That Same Street trade in the widescreen catharsis of Japanese emo-adjacent rock, where cosmic imagery carries private grief. A meteorite fragment, a piece of a shattered star, becomes the anchor for a battered soul who breaks through a tearing sky and lights a lamp on rotting wings, arriving at the recognition that the person sought was waiting in front of them all along.","sonnet":"A fragment of a meteorite — a piece of a shattered star — is found in the world's corner as the ultimate anchor for a grief-battered soul: clouds hid it, but the speaker breaks through a tearing sky, lights a lamp on rotting wings, and lets the cut scenario write its own story, arriving at the realisation that the person they sought was waiting right in front of them all along.","src":"haiku"},
"the-69-eyes~lost-boys":{"haiku":"Darkness becomes habitual, a sustaining force for those who shed daylight existence, perpetually reborn through nocturnal cycles that demand perpetual rebirth.","opus":"Goth-rock romanticizing the nocturnal outsider as a kind of vampire, the lost boy who lives only after dark and treats being reborn each night as freedom. Alienation reframed as glamour.","sonnet":"A hymn to night-creatures who live outside daylight reality, declaring that their endless nocturnal wandering and seeming lostness is in fact a form of perpetual rebirth. Love, ultimately, is what makes them stop — though belonging is something they can only find in the dark.","src":"haiku"},
"the-acacia-strain-courtney-laplante~one-thousand-painful-stings-feat-courtney-laplante":{"haiku":"Extinction and futility eclipse humanity as the universe remains indifferent to collective effort and prayer. The song frames human extinction as inevitable and redemptive compared to continued meaningless suffering.","opus":"A bleak vision of humanity as the universe's total failure, having slipped free of life's grip only to wipe out its own race and win an eternity of nothing. Clawing toward heaven, we fall into hell, calling out into a silence where no one answers.","sonnet":"Humanity has been obliterated by its own failures — infinite stretching to nowhere, a call that no one answered, the attempt to claw into heaven ending in a fall into hell — and the speaker accepts extinction with the flat certainty of someone who has already died a million times and is simply very good at it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-all-american-rejects~gives-you-hell":{"haiku":"Spiteful triumph eclipses genuine happiness; the narrator celebrates the other's downfall as validation.","opus":"A gleeful post-breakup taunt wishing daily misery on an ex, though the bravado slips when he admits he still misses her and then lies about it. Spite as armor over lingering hurt, the pop-punk sneer barely hiding the wound.","sonnet":"A speaker delights in thriving after a breakup, imagining that the sight of his happiness is a source of ongoing torment for his ex, while admitting beneath the bravado that he still misses her.","src":"haiku"},
"the-amity-affliction~all-my-friends-are-dead":{"haiku":"The speaker drowns in depression and loss, expressing that all close connections are gone and no one will remain at the end.","opus":"The Australian metalcore band, long candid about mental illness, offers an unflinching descent into depression, its narrator convinced hope is running out and that he'll face the end abandoned. He equates a drawn-out death with a solitary one and refuses any comfort, letting the crushing despair speak with deliberate, brutal plainness.","sonnet":"Depression described as total isolation — not metaphorical loneliness but a genuine sense that everyone who might have helped is already gone, or gone numb, or not listening. The sky is falling, the world is burning, and the speaker is pinned under the weight of it all, certain that no one will be there at the end.","src":"haiku"},
"the-amity-affliction~soak-me-in-bleach":{"haiku":"Futile escape attempts from overwhelming despair; when the dreamer dies, hope dies, and burial becomes the only refuge from relentless crushing waves.","opus":"A stark confession of fleeing himself only to find escape useless, entombed by a life that keeps entombing him, begging to be reversed and scoured clean of the self he can't stand. Despair arrives in flattening surges that leave him unreachable. Australian metalcore veterans The Amity Affliction have long made mental illness and suicidal pain their explicit subject, and this is that theme stripped completely bare.","sonnet":"Depression as burial — when the person who dreams stops dreaming, the dream dies with them, and no amount of running from yourself closes the gap. The speaker feels strapped down, submerged, crushed in waves, and asks to be hollowed out and scrubbed clean rather than continue suffocating alive.","src":"haiku"},
"the-analogs~pierdolona-era-techno":{"haiku":"Outcasts reject modern commercialism and fashion, celebrating their uncouth existence outside corporate systems that profit from conformity.","opus":"The Analogs, a Polish street-punk band from Szczecin, spit defiant working-class pride, repeatedly shrugging off charts, fashion, fancy haircuts, and stock-market talk with a profane refrain damning the techno era. Choosing vodka over cocktails and old jeans over trends, they declare themselves the voice of the street rather than electronic rhythms, an unpolished anthem of authenticity built on refusing to be bought or sold.","sonnet":"A Polish punk band celebrates its own irrelevance as a badge of honour. They play out of time, stumble into choruses, avoid hit parades and corporate beverages, dress badly and spend freely, and nobody can buy or monetise them — the techno era can do nothing with people who are simply the voice of the street.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anchor~through-rose":{"opus":"A charge against a manipulator who preyed on the singer's naive, hopeful youth, feeding him empty promises and deceit while pretending to be the cure for the very hurts he dealt. Now the singer sees the con for what it was and declares his life is better with the man gone.","sonnet":"A person who idealized a manipulative figure — seen through the rose-colored eyes of childhood — confronts the reality that they were used as a pawn, their innocence exploited, and finally declares themselves better off without the person who skewed their sense of reality.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-anix~antilife":{"haiku":"A toxic presence feeds on the speaker like an energy vampire, offering dark seduction that nullifies vitality while trapping them in suffocation and mutual destruction. The speaker is helpless against an antimatter force that obliterates dreams.","opus":"An obsession framed as mutual destruction, drawn to something poisonous that feels like an anti-life force. Love here is suffocation and drowning, the pull toward what will annihilate you both.","sonnet":"The speaker is caught in a relationship that functions as negation — the other person is named \"anti-life,\" their black light pulling the narrator into suffocation and dissolution. Both are sinking together, and the song offers no escape, only the compulsive repetition of \"you are anti-life\" as if naming it is the only agency left.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~army-of-me":{"haiku":"Self-sufficiency and refusal of dependency; confrontation with the fractured mirror of self.","opus":"A synth-driven, hardened refusal of self-pity and dependency, the narrator declining to feel sorry for someone who won't stop complaining. The warning is blunt: keep whining and you'll conjure a whole legion of him. Its ethos is cold self-sufficiency — no help is coming, so stand up and get to it.","sonnet":"A cover of Army of Me — the imperative to self-sufficiency delivered as threat: complain once more and you'll meet an army of me — your rescue squad is exhausted, you're on your own now, and the sympathy has been withdrawn entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~below":{"haiku":"The speaker descends beyond fragments and surface chaos to confront what lurks in depths both literal and psychic. Below holds messenger secrets and truth untouched by light.","opus":"A shadowy electro-rock riddle obsessed with what hides under the surface and whether any of it counts. Shards climb upward while a downward pull hauls everything under, and the repeated jeer waves off the depths completely. Fleeing a slaughter and hammering corroded spikes further in, the narrator flaunts end-times spectacle, offering the sight of everything blowing apart as the ultimate diversion from whatever rots beneath.","sonnet":"An adversarial speaker survives a massacre and pulls the nails back out only to push them in further, watching an undertow drag the listener down while asking coolly \"who cares what's below?\" — indifference to destruction is the hook.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~black-space":{"haiku":"Void and emptiness become refuge and prison, with the speaker suspended in absolute negation.","opus":"Refusing to release someone even into the void, clinging to nothing in an endless darkness. Grief and devotion reimagined as a synthwave vow to keep searching the black for a person already gone.","sonnet":"A vigil held inside total darkness — trapped in nightmare, trespassing the unknown, fragments left behind — but the narrator won't let go and won't let the other disappear: \"in the black space, I will find you.\" The endless battle is framed as forever mine, suffering recast as permanent ownership.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~bleach":{"haiku":"Cosmic longing and desperate plea for reunion transcend earthly constraints, with the speaker invoking celestial imagery to bridge an irrevocable distance from a lost lover.","opus":"A frantic reaching toward someone slipping away into death or oblivion, the singer begging them to wait while offering an escape into a shared dream among the stars.","sonnet":"A desperate plea to someone who may have already slipped away — lost in rivers, sinking, fires gone out — as the narrator begs them to wait, promising to take them back \"up to Mars.\" The image of \"American eyes that could bleach the sun\" casts the speaker as the one brilliant enough to save them, if only they hadn't arrived too late.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~chrome":{"haiku":"Stagnation and reflection lost; the past repeats endlessly without rights to change trajectory.","opus":"A glossy synthwave meditation on letting someone go, the departed one hardened into cold, gleaming reflection. With the conversation exhausted and the past looping, the narrator releases his claim on them even as they plot their own getaway — loss rendered as a body melting and vanishing.","sonnet":"Transformation as erasure and preservation at once — taking someone back home by turning them into chrome, their reflections controlled and suspended in the moment before vanishing. The past on replay, nothing left to say, the chrome-conversion both rescue and death: they are changed forever, made permanent and unreachable.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~cry-little-sister":{"haiku":"A sisterly figure guides or mourns the speaker's descent, with familial connection marking the boundary of compassion.","opus":"A synth-industrial cover of the Lost Boys theme, the vampire's seductive summons to join the immortal dark, each command inverting a commandment. Damnation offered as brotherhood and belonging.","sonnet":"A cover of Gerard McMahon's Lost Boys theme: an older sibling calls to a younger one through commandments delivered as protection — thou shall not fall, shall not die, shall not fear, shall not kill — as temptation heat and immortal fear close in from all sides and loneliness becomes the universal condition.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~cut-me":{"haiku":"Psychic fragmentation and loss of boundaries dissolve the speaker's autonomy, with external forces dismantling the structures of selfhood.","opus":"A one-sided fixation on someone barely known, where longing curdles into a wish for self-erasure. The distant object of love becomes both the imagined intimacy and the instrument of harm.","sonnet":"Longing for a person known only from a photograph bleeds into self-destruction: the narrator asks her to leave a knife so he can be cut to pieces, framing the diminishing pulse not as despair but as \"a new kind of power\" — the pain of her absence transformed into something he can control.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~defender":{"haiku":"The speaker assumes protective vigilance, guarding against threats both internal and external.","opus":"A ghost who guards someone he can no longer reach, too far gone himself to be saved but still standing watch. Love as protection offered from beyond the point of no return.","sonnet":"A ghost who has given up on his own life but won't abandon the person he's haunting — \"too far gone to bring me back to life\" but still present beside them at night as a defender. The contradiction is deliberate: he can't save himself but continues to shield someone else, destroyer and protector at once.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~digital-bath":{"haiku":"Technology and artificiality become escape routes from embodied pain, with digital immersion offering false transcendence.","opus":"A synthwave cover of the Deftones song, an intimacy so total it slides into fantasized drowning, tenderness and death fused. The chilling calm of feeling most alive while ending a life.","sonnet":"Intimacy in a basement where no one can see, waterlogged and slow — the narrator watches someone in water, tastes them as foreign, and then the other person stops breathing. \"You breathed, then you stopped / I breathed, then dried you off\" collapses drowning and tenderness into a single gesture, the narrator feeling \"like more\" through it all.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~disappear":{"haiku":"Erosion of presence through emotional distance and technological disconnection, the beloved gradually fading as human feeling becomes automated.","opus":"A throbbing electro-rock longing for someone drifting off, the recurring plea over how long they still want him left hanging. Machine-tinged imagery, feelings on autopilot and jolts of current, renders human emotion mechanized and scattered. Shaped for grief and lingering in the dark, the narrator is stranded, aching for a presence forever about to vanish.","sonnet":"Longing across distance that may be emotional as much as physical — waiting in shadows, human feelings reduced to automated electrical pulses, the question 'how long till you disappear' cycling without answer. The closing image of being 'diffused and swallowed' while still missing someone maps grief onto dissolution.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~endlessly":{"haiku":"The speaker falls into devotion with full knowledge of their own complicity and self-destruction, resignation and sacrifice substituting for resolution.","opus":"An admission that the wreckage of a relationship was the singer's own fault, met with a masochistic vow to punish himself endlessly to prove devotion he can no longer earn.","sonnet":"A self-directed reckoning: the narrator admits the distances and the downfall were his fault all along, then doubles down anyway — starving himself, breaking his back — to prove a love he calls endless, even as that love seems to be destroying him.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~everlasting-love":{"haiku":"Separation threatens permanence; the speaker questions whether love survives without physical presence.","opus":"Grasping at a crumbling bond, wondering aloud whether the other could carry on without him even as he senses himself forgetting them and slipping away. The permanence they both once trusted is decaying into treachery and grief.","sonnet":"A relationship breaking apart despite both parties having believed it would last forever — the speaker fears forgetting the other person even as they're falling through the collapse, asking whether the love was ever truly everlasting or just a story they told themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~fight-the-future":{"haiku":"Resistance to predetermined fate demands present action, with the speaker staging battle against temporality itself.","opus":"Emotional numbness after a relationship's collapse, walking backwards through a life that no longer makes sense. Wanting to break someone open and failing, then bracing against a future that already feels lost.","sonnet":"Immobility as a kind of warfare: the narrator is pinned down, walking in reverse, blinded by what's become of their relationship, unable to beat the score or see forward. He fights the future not by advancing but by refusing to accept the state things have reached.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~follow":{"haiku":"The speaker offers dangerous guidance into spiritual transformation, leading others toward transcendence or annihilation.","opus":"Being lured deeper into someone's chaos and self-destruction, a prisoner of war to their appetite for more. The narrator surrenders his own bearings, chasing a phantom into a trap he half-wants.","sonnet":"The narrator is pulled into someone's orbit against his better judgment — bringing armies to knife fights, tasting chaos in their own tears, dragging him into trapdoors — until he shrugs into surrender: \"follow me somewhere, I don't know or care.\" The phantom in the night igniting, the chemical drowning, the miracle craved: all of it is the cost of wanting this person.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~frozen-waves":{"haiku":"Temporal stasis and emotional paralysis freeze movement and change, capturing beauty and devastation in ice.","opus":"Sinking willingly to the bottom because that depth is where the narrator feels he belongs. A drowning framed as homecoming, the pull downward mistaken for the only place that fits.","sonnet":"Sinking imagery layered over a watching relationship: the narrator is underwater, cut by frozen waves, always going all the way down, while someone else swims alone nearby. The question \"how have I forgotten this is where I belong?\" suggests the bottom is home, and the vigil at the edge is endless.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~give-it-up":{"haiku":"Paralysis and lost identity, surrender the only option when standing becomes impossible.","opus":"Electro-rock aimed at someone paralyzed by their own lies and disguises, unable to stand after each fall. The refrain wavers between telling them to surrender their pretense and darkly urging them to give up entirely.","sonnet":"About someone trapped behind a facade of strength who is actually lost, paralyzed, and unable to keep pretending — the narrator observes this person trying and failing to stand up, wearing a stone face, and tells them to stop; the \"give it up\" is an invitation to release the disguise.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~glass":{"haiku":"Fragility and transparency become weapons against poison as the speaker melts under obsession, powerless against the distant beloved. Distance burns while youth dissolves under the weight of futile longing.","opus":"A synth-rock portrait of obsessive, self-erasing devotion, the narrator squandering his youth and going dark while insisting his fixation will never disappoint him. A decade yields nothing to show for it, yet he won't leave. Glimpsed faintly through a mirror and slowly smothered, it renders love as a beautiful, corroding burn.","sonnet":"An obsession that has consumed a decade: the subject is too far away to see clearly through the looking glass, yet the narrator refuses to leave even as they shut down and the poison sinks in — the promise that \"you won't ever let me down again\" is believed despite all evidence.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~go":{"haiku":"Urgent command to depart or flee signals danger or separation.","opus":"Sleepless vigilance over someone slipping away, refusing to let go while sensing something dark closing in. Obsessive watchfulness masking the fear of abandonment.","sonnet":"A vigil kept all night over someone the narrator refuses to release — voices in the void, past regrets, burning alone in silence — the eyes locked on them, repeating \"don't let go, don't you go\" as the only available intervention. The watching itself is the act of love.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~if-this-world-is-with-you":{"haiku":"Transformation refused and isolation; the speaker rejects change while recognizing the massacre of solitude.","opus":"A driving electro-rock stand against forced change, the narrator refusing to alter himself unless it is for the one he loves. Amid imagery of massacre and warriors floating alone, he offers everything, meeting halfway, tearing himself in two, if only the world will be shared. Its condition is love: he could rearrange entirely, but only with that person beside him.","sonnet":"A fighter refusing to change on principle, told to slow down, to stay down, while enemies track every move. The pivot at the close is the condition: I don't want to change if it's not for you — transformation only legitimate when it's toward someone, not away from something, the distinction the entire moral axis of the song.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~in-the-end":{"haiku":"The speaker embeds themselves within another's psyche, offering unconditional presence and witness to darkness while questioning the nature of their interdependence.","opus":"A vow to remain a devoted, unseen presence at a lover's side through their darkest night, holding them and absorbing their grief even as the singer wonders how it all ends.","sonnet":"The narrator positions himself as a shadow companion to someone in crisis — silently present while they cry, swallowing their tears, holding their crown — wondering how this vigil ends, crawling back under again and again, held together only by the plea \"hold me the way you do.\"","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~interchanger":{"haiku":"A stranger passes through consciousness as an alien interloper, unmoored and unrecognizable to themselves, imagining control in a moment before dissolving into transit again.","opus":"Dissociation and rootlessness, a stranger to his own reflection passing through a world with no allies. Identity dissolving into someone who only moves through, never belongs.","sonnet":"A dissociated wanderer who can no longer find themselves in the mirror — \"can't see my reflection, nothing left to recognize\" — passing through without connection, identity dissolved by repeated transit. The word \"interchanger\" is both who they are and what happened to them: a junction that passes everything through and keeps nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~interference":{"haiku":"The speaker breaks free of hypnosis and spell to recognize a remote-controlled entity, yet cannot release their obsession, caught between fear and need in a relationship defined by supernatural control.","opus":"Wary, magnetic attraction to someone who might be controlled or unreal, staying awake rather than surrender to the spell. Desire and distrust locked together, unwilling to release the very thing that unsettles him.","sonnet":"The speaker is fixated on someone they sense is not quite real or autonomous — \"are you remote controlled?\" — yet refuses to let go, staying awake to watch them crystallize, magnetized on the edge. Possession and suspicion tangle: \"your infinite mine\" claims ownership while the repeated \"interference\" suggests the signal between them is always breaking up.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~levitate-instrumental":{"haiku":"A relationship spirals into dissolution as the speaker loses themselves in a partner they cannot escape, willingly unmade for proximity to someone who drains their life force. Codependency manifests as self-annihilation.","opus":"Self-annihilating devotion, offering to split himself in two and lose everything for someone while quietly asking whether he'd even be missed. Love as erasure and a bid to be needed.","sonnet":"A co-dependent entanglement narrated from both inside and outside the grip — the speaker holds someone off the ground while simultaneously drowning, cutting himself in two to sustain the connection. The parenthetical \"Would you still miss me?\" late in the song reveals the underlying fear: the sacrifice might not even be noticed.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~missile":{"haiku":"Emotional collapse and freefall articulate the speaker's inability to resist decline or recover without the comfort of a distant presence.","opus":"Fragmented gasps of someone breaking down and falling apart, missing a lover's call and comparing his own collapse to a missile burning to the ground.","sonnet":"Collapse rendered in clipped, fragmentary syntax — barely holding on, bending, breaking, freezing — until the chorus names the shape of it: falling like a missile, burning to the ground, calling out to someone who used to pull him back down to earth.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~my-eyes":{"haiku":"Separation enforced by external forces leaves the speaker hollowed and poisoned by betrayal; emotional death replaces tears as the final response to cruelty.","opus":"A charged synth-rock homecoming clouded by menace and treachery, unseen forces scheming to divide two people with barriers, panes and spreading madness. The narrator wants to bring someone back yet exposes the injury, his gaze hollowed out, poisoned by the very person he loves. Holding his position, he vows he won't break down, the bravado hiding a deep wound.","sonnet":"A relationship in which someone has been slowly poisoned — \"you fed me cyanide\" — and the speaker now looks back with hollow eyes, refusing to cry, refusing surprise. Walls break and glass shatters to keep the two apart, but by the chorus the damage is already internal and complete.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~parasite":{"haiku":"Magnetic toxicity and inescapable fusion; the speaker embodies parasitic attraction, bound to another through imperfection and the impossibility of separation.","opus":"A magnetic, doomed attachment cast in sci-fi horror, two flawed people who can't be pried apart even as one names himself the parasite feeding on the other. Love as an inescapable fever dream where your screaming reaches no one.","sonnet":"An obsessive, consuming love described as parasitic — the narrator and the other person are magnetically bound and imperfect together, with no escape possible even if it tears both of them apart.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~parasite-instrumental":{"haiku":"Two damaged souls cling to each other in a symbiotic parasitism, bound magnetically despite knowing they destroy each other, unable to escape or separate their intertwined imperfection.","opus":"A doomed, magnetic bond framed in cosmic isolation, two flawed people who can't be pulled apart even as one calls himself a parasite. Codependence as a chase through the vacuum where no one hears you.","sonnet":"The speaker confesses to being the predator while framing the bond as inescapable mutual magnetism — \"me and you are magnetic, they can't tear us apart.\" The horror is that the parasitism is worn as intimacy: nowhere to run, no escape, and \"out in space no one can hear you scream\" closes the trap with a smile.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~pretend":{"haiku":"Deception and performance mask authentic emotion, with the speaker maintaining false presentation despite internal dissolution.","opus":"Two people falling endlessly toward an ending they refuse to name, pretending it isn't over while knowing the game is unwinnable. The exhausted last grip on a love already lost.","sonnet":"Two people keep falling — ten feet from the ground, surrounded by imaginary lines — and the only way to survive is to pretend it isn't over. The narrator is sinking into the other's space, won't bend, wonders if there's a way back, but the refrain concedes: pretending is the closest thing to hope available.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~quicksand":{"haiku":"Dissociation and existential exhaustion pull the speaker under like quicksand — the question \"what if I lost myself\" repeats until it stops being hypothetical and becomes the only direction available.","opus":"An electro-rock spiral of dissociation, the narrator sinking and grasping for something solid in a colorless world. Worn down to nothing and haunted by the fear of losing himself again, he finally stops resisting and lets the current pull him under — identity dissolving as a strange, willing descent.","sonnet":"Dissolution of self under emotional weight — stripped to the bone, submerged in an undertow, unable to feel real in a world reduced to black and white. The recurring question 'what if I lost myself again' is less hypothetical than testimony: the losing is already happening.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~renegade":{"haiku":"A speaker hunts escape through flight while nothing holds them down. Transcendence and pretense swirl in cycles of searching.","opus":"Restless dread of an approaching storm and no escape, met with a longing to fly away into nowhere; the renegade keeps coming up empty, defending himself against a threat he can't name.","sonnet":"A song about searching for meaning and escape when nothing comes together — the narrator feels empty-handed no matter how hard they seek, imagines flying away to nowhere, and oscillates between defiance and the quiet wish to transcend a life that keeps yielding nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~resident-one":{"haiku":"A war leader hides secrets and crimes, manipulating truth while the speaker refuses to let them escape accountability, exposing the falsehood and pollution that freedom conceals.","opus":"An accusation aimed at a warmongering leader who lies to erase his crimes, insisting the secrets will surface and he won't be allowed to win. Political anger dressed in industrial menace.","sonnet":"An indictment of a leader — \"Resident One\" — who launches war, manufactures pollution, and lies to erase his crimes, addressed directly by a speaker who refuses to be silenced: \"open up your eyes and see your world come down.\" The refrain functions as both prophecy and defiance, the exposure of secrets treated as inevitable justice.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~sideways":{"haiku":"Betrayal by someone once trusted sends the speaker into disorientation and collapse, abandoned in a ghost town where all direction becomes sideways and every path leads to the same ruin.","opus":"Treachery from someone who once stood by you, dropping you and then locking you out until you end up sprawled in some deserted town. Watching a companion go off the rails and haul you along with them.","sonnet":"A friendship or partnership that caved in on itself — the speaker addresses someone who once had their back but is now falling, shutting down, and pulling them face-down into a ghost town. \"I bleed in black\" and the stuck, repeating word \"sideways\" capture the paralysis of caring for someone you've already lost to their own collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~sleepwalker":{"haiku":"Transformation driven by obsessive devotion manifests as loss of agency and identity, with the speaker paralyzed between awakening and gravitational pull of another.","opus":"A half-asleep, paralyzed state where the self dissolves into a private dream world, chasing someone he can't quite reach while sensing the darkness inside will find them both.","sonnet":"Paralysis between sleep and waking becomes a metaphor for a relationship that's infected the narrator like gravity — he changes, tries to find himself, drifts through blue inner worlds, and still can't escape: \"you can run but you won't get away, it's bound to find you too.\"","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~still-standing":{"haiku":"Weakening and disorientation; the speaker feels alien to themself despite remaining upright.","opus":"A synth-rock reckoning with a destabilizing relationship that erases his sense of self, comparing him to a collapsing star that swallows everything. Fresh paranoia eats him from within, smearing the line between real and false. The recurring question — whether to leave while he can still stay upright — frames a desperate bid to get out before he is fully undone.","sonnet":"A relationship or encounter that erodes the self — contact made, the home seen, but memory of what's real and what's wrong dissolving. New paranoia destroys from within, and the question repeated is whether to walk away while still standing — the still standing not triumph but the narrowing window before there's nothing left to walk away with.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~take-my-future":{"haiku":"Surrender of agency and potential to external forces constitutes the speaker's final offering.","opus":"Devotion so absolute it becomes self-erasure, following someone to the ground and handing over your own future. Love as surrender to a manipulator who rewrites your world.","sonnet":"A narrator trapped in walls shaped by someone else's choices declares absolute devotion — I'll follow you to the ground, stay forever — even as that devotion is used to disarm, control, and consume him. The plea \"take my future, away from me\" arrives not as protest but as surrender, the only form of intimacy available.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~talking-in-my-sleep":{"haiku":"Unconscious speech reveals hidden truths the speaker cannot articulate while awake, vulnerability and confession emerging only through dissolution of conscious control.","opus":"Retreating into sleep as the only place to voice buried feelings, where no one watches or judges, mourning a lost connection he can only face unconscious.","sonnet":"Dissociation as self-protection — the narrator wants to talk only while unconscious so no one can see what he's facing or notice he's unguarded. Indigo tears and undertow paint a relationship that dragged both people under, now visible only in retrospect.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~tethered":{"haiku":"Bondage masquerades as intimacy; the speaker remains bound to a killer-partner, unable to ascend or escape.","opus":"A brooding electro-rock account of a doomed, binding attachment, the narrator worn down and unable to rise, tethered to someone who led them toward ruin. He pleads with the other to promise they will never become a killer like him, hinting at his own capacity for harm. Uncovered secrets and shattered memories mark a love that traps rather than frees.","sonnet":"A person is ground down and pinned in place by someone they can't escape — \"I see your face all over,\" \"tie me to the ground again\" — unable to get up while also recognising the other person is no killer, no mirror of themselves, leaving only the question of why being tethered to them feels both inevitable and wrong.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~this-machine":{"haiku":"Resistance to systematic control and manufactured existence, with the speaker refusing to accept programmed reality or predetermined fate.","opus":"Questioning whether life and love are real or just programmed simulation, and refusing to be controlled by a dehumanizing system that leads nowhere.","sonnet":"A refusal to be absorbed by whatever system — social, romantic, digital — is trying to program behavior and steer the narrator nowhere. The line \"I don't believe in this machine / controlled by you\" is the pivot, asserting autonomous resistance against an unnamed controlling force.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~warning-signs":{"haiku":"Premonition and ignored counsel precede inevitable catastrophe, with the speaker hurtling toward disaster despite clear signals.","opus":"A world tipping into brutality and shared madness, where obsession with power comes at the cost of people's lives and the only routes seem to be combat or self-destruction. A dread of everyone's grip on reason coming undone.","sonnet":"Systemic militancy dressed as normalcy is identified as a slow execution crawling through the mind, costing human lives while disguising itself as fascination. The narrator is alone with whatever violence is \"taking away,\" fragile and labeled a sin, but still refusing to give in even when the choice is fight or suicide.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~where-is-my-mind-acoustic-live":{"haiku":"Existential dissociation and loss of coherence dissolve the speaker's sense of location and identity.","opus":"A stripped-back live cover of the Pixies classic, dislocation and dissolving sanity rendered as a dream of talking fish and upside-down worlds. The unmooring of a mind that can no longer locate itself.","sonnet":"A Pixies cover: disorientation as serene surrender — feet in the air, head on the ground, skull collapsing around nothing. The Caribbean anecdote of a fish trying to talk is the only anchor offered, and it just asks the same question back. The mind is way out in the water, swimmin', and the song is content to leave it there.","src":"haiku"},
"the-anix~your-lies-are-like-fire":{"haiku":"Deception burns through certainty as lies spread like flame, the speaker rejects servitude while questioning if liberation is possible from self-made captivity.","opus":"Treachery that scorches the body like a blaze, one person's falsehoods laying bare and wrecking what the speaker believes while he wobbles at the edge of madness. His draw toward the very one who harms him, craving their nearness, makes it a study of leaning on the thing that hurts.","sonnet":"A relationship defined by deception that burns through the speaker like fire — the lies don't just wound them, they destroy belief systems and leave the speaker unmoored, desperately needing the other person nearby even while recognizing how thoroughly they've been destroyed.","src":"haiku"},
"the-armed~fortune-s-daughter":{"haiku":"Surreal decay of privilege and comfort collapses into dirt and meaninglessness. Fortune and status crumble; the world blows over.","opus":"A disorienting, image-collage takedown of privileged suburban emptiness and a fallen golden child; through absurd, sardonic snapshots (spandex crowns, directionless chatter, never learning from the hot stove) it mocks vanity and self-inflicted downfall while the refrain shrugs that it'll all blow over.","sonnet":"An aggressive, image-dense collision of suburban decay and violent frustration — the lyrics scatter observations about cookie-cutter lives and entitled comfort, punctuated by a call to find a place and take arms before it all blows over.","src":"haiku"},
"the-armed~night-city-aliens":{"haiku":"A soldier or militant operative enforces orders without feeling—pulling triggers while numb, executing missions in a caravan of alien raiders.","opus":"The Armed, a shape-shifting collective, deliver cyberpunk-tinged dissociation in fractured slogans. The lyric is an oath of blind obedience, accept the command, carry out the killing, feel absolutely nothing, tracing how violence becomes automatic and conscience simply empties out. It's less a story than the sound of a mind marching through emptiness, numbed into a soldier's machine-like void.","sonnet":"Soldiers or operatives bound by sworn loyalty carry out missions with complete emotional dissociation — pulling triggers and feeling nothing. The imagery of a cross-country caravan of alien agents enforcing some omni-vortex order evokes total dehumanization, products of a system that has voided their inner lives.","src":"haiku"},
"the-association~windy":{"haiku":"A free-spirited woman captures everyone's imagination; she moves through the world spreading joy and magic, defying gravity and convention.","opus":"A breezy sunshine-pop sketch of a free-spirited girl who moves through the city radiating lightness and can see through lies. Sixties optimism personified, less a real woman than an idealized figure of buoyant, untethered joy.","sonnet":"A tribute to a free-spirited, radiant young woman who moves through the city with effortless joy, bending down to share rainbows, flashing her eyes at lies, and soaring above the ordinary — everyone recognizes her and is lifted by her presence.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bad-dreamers~part-time-god":{"haiku":"A crooked figure built an unwanted throne of power; they don't deserve worship and have wasted the chance they were given.","opus":"A takedown of someone who grabbed authority they never earned, a bent lawman and greedy child who erected a solitary seat of power with room for no one else, informed that this lofty role was thrust upon them unbidden. The needling farewell tag lends the scorn an oddly fond edge. The Bad Dreamers set this sour character sketch to bright, hook-driven power-pop that heavily sugarcoats the poison.","sonnet":"A takedown of someone who seized control they never actually wanted and ruled from a throne built for one, hollowing out everything around them in the process. Crooked and oblivious as a bent cop, they played at power like a child in a candy shop and blew whatever chance they had — now the song is the bill for the job they took without meaning to keep.","src":"haiku"},
"the-band~tears-of-rage":{"haiku":"A parent's devastation peaks as their child rejects guidance and love; the narrator questions why sacrifice and care are repaid with ingratitude and betrayal.","opus":"A father's anguished lament over a child who's rejected everything the family gave, betrayal soured into grief. Written with Dylan, it reads as America turning on its founders, patriotic hope curdled, the parent left cast as the thief.","sonnet":"A father figure — or founding generation — cries out in grief and bewilderment at being abandoned and exploited by those they raised and sacrificed for, feeling only the role of the thief while everything they gave is taken for granted.","src":"haiku"},
"the-barbarians-of-california~dopamine-prophecy":{"haiku":"Desperation and self-destruction echo through obsession, power-hunger and collapse repeating as prophecy. The body becomes site of both domination and obliteration, surrendering to urges that consume.","opus":"A frantic self-portrait of craving and self-loathing, begging for power while feeling like oblivion, dopamine chased like a doomed prophecy. Addiction and burnout spat out as manic, kerosene-soaked collapse.","sonnet":"A self-portrait of nihilistic collapse: the speaker oscillates between weakness and defiance, drinking kerosene and tearing themselves apart — the \"dopamine prophecy\" names the compulsive loop of destruction dressed up as transcendence.","src":"haiku"},
"the-beatles~because-2019-mix":{"haiku":"Cosmic forces—the Earth's roundness, the wind's height, the sky's color—trigger emotional resonance; love encompasses all and binds the observer to existence.","opus":"Lennon reduces wonder to its barest elements, the round world, the blue sky, love as everything and everyone. Built on inverted Beethoven and stacked harmonies, it's a near-wordless hymn to existence itself, awe without argument.","sonnet":"A meditation on the sensation of being overwhelmed by the beauty of the natural world — the roundness of the earth, the wind, the blue sky — which the speaker equates with love itself, ancient and all-encompassing.","src":"haiku"},
"the-beatles~mean-mr-mustard-2019-mix":{"haiku":"Poverty and misanthropy breed socially deviant behavior; the miser hides wealth obsessively while his sister courts spectacle and scandal through shameless conduct.","opus":"A quick McCartney-era character vignette of a miserly, obscene old miser and his long-suffering sister. A throwaway comic sketch in the Abbey Road medley, more affectionate cartoon than statement.","sonnet":"A quick character sketch of an obsessively miserly old man who sleeps rough and hoards money in his nose, accompanied by his industrious sister Pam who drags him out to see the Queen while he embarrasses them both.","src":"haiku"},
"the-beatles~michelle-remastered-2009":{"haiku":"Desire translates itself across language barriers into simple declarations of devotion; the speaker builds a bridge of understanding through repetition and vulnerability.","opus":"A tender love song to a French-speaking woman, straining across a language barrier to convey feeling with the few words she'll understand. McCartney turns the limits of communication into the whole point: love reaching past what words can carry.","sonnet":"A man addresses a French woman he loves, confessing that the only words he can offer her are her name and the declaration of love, bridging a language barrier with sheer feeling while he searches for a deeper way to make her understand.","src":"haiku"},
"the-beatles~polythene-pam-2019-mix":{"haiku":"Playful portrait of an unconventional woman who challenges beauty norms through provocative style and attitude.","opus":"Lennon's cheeky sketch of a gender-bending Liverpool character, kink and drag played as tabloid spectacle. Slotted into the Abbey Road medley, it's less a story than a leering wink at English eccentricity.","sonnet":"A cheeky, affectionate portrait of an eccentric woman whose outrageous androgynous style — polythene bags, jackboots, kilts — makes her a scandalous tabloid spectacle and all the more compelling for it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-beautiful-monument~hellbound":{"haiku":"Defiant resurrection through self-reclamation; the speaker dismantles imposed identity and doubt, crowning themselves queen of their own descent and emergence.","opus":"A woman sheds imposed doubt and self-hatred to crown herself, answering old accusations by declaring her jailers' words worthless. Being hellbound is reclaimed as sovereignty: no one gets to tell her who to be.","sonnet":"A woman reclaims her identity and authority after someone tried to define and diminish her, declaring that their judgment means nothing and she crowns herself — hell-bound by her own choice, not their condemnation.","src":"haiku"},
"the-birthday-massacre~dead":{"haiku":"A person feels dead inside, unable to communicate honestly without judgment, trapped in a world that rejects their truth and forces inauthenticity.","opus":"A study in numb alienation, walled off by broken thoughts and misunderstood by everyone, retreating into a deadened inner world where even self-expression feels corrupt and the craving for anything new goes unmet.","sonnet":"Feeling utterly invisible — unable to speak honestly, loathed for trying, numbing out in front of television in a world that feels already dead — the narrator concludes she is already dead inside and just waiting for something to be different.","src":"haiku"},
"the-birthday-massacre~sleepwalking":{"haiku":"The speaker exists as a fading reflection, moving through days that blur into each other without leaving a trace. They search for a place they'll never return to, never fully present or real.","opus":"A ghostly lament of feeling unseen and washed-up, the narrator moving through unfamiliar streets as a fading reflection who was never really there, graced only with faint applause and a cheap facade. Time collapses from night to month in an instant. The Birthday Massacre, a Canadian act blending goth, synth and dreamy alt-rock, give this dissociation their signature soft-menace shimmer, the Snow White nod curdling into disappearance.","sonnet":"Dissociation as identity — moving through time and places that don't register, leaving no real impression, existing only as a faint reflection on surfaces that won't hold the image. The speaker knows they're faking whatever presence they project, and the search for somewhere meaningful to return to keeps coming up empty.","src":"haiku"},
"the-black-angels~young-men-dead":{"haiku":"Soldiers overcome fear and lethargy to act, knowing survival depends on courage and willingness to take risks.","opus":"A psych-rock war chant that puts you inside the soldier's dehumanized point of view, kill orders and camp raids blurred by drone and menace. The old lie that the young must die is dressed as duty.","sonnet":"A soldier's mission delivered in fragmented, trance-like commands — move fast, kill on sight, hide in the dark — until the violence tips into something hallucinatory and the line between liberation and slaughter dissolves entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"the-black-queen~one-edge-of-two":{"opus":"An intense, self-reflexive romance where desire is a mirror showing only the self, and the singer clings to the edge between two worlds, aware he's telegraphing his own ending even as he loses himself in the moment.","sonnet":"A dangerously consuming love affair in which the narrator is so absorbed by another person that he can only see himself reflected back, telegraphing his own psychological dissolution and approaching end while hanging on to the edge of two worlds.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-blinders~city-we-call-love":{"haiku":"Two people are figures frozen like Pompeii before volcanic destruction, planting a flag in their city of love before it ends.","opus":"The literary British post-punk trio fuses erotic surrender with apocalypse, two lovers claiming a metropolis they christen love while catastrophe closes in. Casting themselves as the doomed inhabitants of a buried ancient city, they stay defiant, insisting no disaster can destroy what they've made, only they themselves ever could.","sonnet":"An ecstatic claim of intimacy as a place to inhabit, not just a feeling — the two figures plant a flag in their own private city and declare it unconquerable, not by ash or acid rain, only by each other. Drifting past ghosts and television light and the frozen figures of Pompeii, the song ends up as a dare: only you could destroy this, and I'm trusting you not to.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloodhound-gang~along-comes-mary":{"haiku":"Mysterious liberation and psychological escape materialize through a figure named Mary whose true nature remains ambiguous. The song explores whether salvation comes from external grace or self-generated delusion and peace.","opus":"A cover of the Association's sly sixties number where Mary is widely read as marijuana, the loner's relief arriving with a haze that reframes every failure as sweet. Escape dressed as an unexpected visitor.","sonnet":"Mary arrives each time the speaker is at their loneliest, transforming an empty cup into something sweet as punch — the song moves through three visions of what she might want to offer: kicks and memories, liberation from self-deception, or a witnessing of the pain and stains left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloodhound-gang~hell-yeah":{"haiku":"Sacrilegious satire of worship and authority, mocking religious hypocrisy and divine pretense.","opus":"A gleefully sacrilegious comedy bit imagining what a self-serving slacker God would decree, skewering pop culture, religious hypocrisy, and celebrity with deliberately juvenile provocation. Blasphemy played strictly for laughs.","sonnet":"A blasphemous comedy number imagining what the narrator would do if he were God — he'd replace the Bible with pop culture figures, demand Norwegian lesbians as servants, mock crucifixion, and campaign against the current God for the position of Savior; it's deliberately irreverent satire of religion and celebrity culture.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloodhound-gang~mope":{"haiku":"Absolute mundane monotony and performative mediocrity pervade existence; the speaker documents boredom as lifestyle, refusing transcendence.","opus":"A deadpan mock-elegy to boredom set to Falco's Amadeus, cataloging absurd, gross, wasted-time habits of an aimless slacker who has nowhere to go and nothing happening.","sonnet":"An extended comedic catalog of extreme boredom and deliberate pointlessness — the narrator describes an absurd series of activities undertaken purely because he has nothing better to do, from drawing underwear on indigenous people in National Geographics to calling wrong numbers hoping conversations start. It is a joke song dedicated to the late Austrian pop star Falco, framed as an ode to moping around with nothing going on.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloodhound-gang~the-ballad-of-chasey-lain":{"haiku":"Obsessive pursuit and stalking masquerade as romantic devotion; the speaker's fixation borders on pathological.","opus":"A crude comedic ode from the Bloodhound Gang, built entirely as a fan's escalating, increasingly indignant letters to an adult film star who never writes back. The joke rides on the gap between obsessive entitlement and total delusion, capped by an absurd family introduction. It is deliberate juvenile shock-humor lampooning celebrity worship and the mind of a deranged admirer.","sonnet":"Three escalating fan letters to a porn actress, each one more aggrieved that she hasn't responded to the previous one, culminating in the narrator's parents being introduced at the close. The joke is the mismatch between the sincerity of the obsession and the absurdity of its object, with the repeated couplet — \"you've had a lotta dick, but you ain't had mine\" — as the only argument the letter-writer can muster.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloodhound-gang~yummy-down-on-this":{"haiku":"Dependency and oral fixation; the speaker needs assistance because self-sufficiency means isolation.","opus":"A relentless comedy-rap crammed with pop-culture puns, every line a groaning double entendre about oral sex dressed in sitcom names and fast-food brands. The Bloodhound Gang's whole aim is the density of the wordplay itself, innuendo stacked to absurd excess. There is no emotional core, just gleefully juvenile shock-humor pushing crude cleverness as far as it will stretch.","sonnet":"An extended, aggressively comic solicitation for oral sex structured as a string of pop-culture puns — Rocky chasing the chicken, Bob Dylan, Chubby Checker — the speaker cataloging every possible metaphor for the act while insisting the listener get down on it, with no pretense of any other subject.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots-jet~my-name-is-thunder-electronic-version":{"haiku":"Self as force and light, claiming power and visibility against hiding and shadows.","opus":"An electro-rock power fantasy of arriving like lightning to light up a darkened, hiding world, nostalgia for brighter days fueling a defiant call to seize the city and blaze your own name across the sky.","sonnet":"A bombastic anthem of self-declared power and collective awakening — the narrator is a blinding force lighting up a city of people hiding in fear, calling them out of shelter and into a new day they'll write themselves. It's pure triumphalism dressed as a storm.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots-steve-aoki~new-noise":{"opus":"A remake of Refused's call to arms flattened into a dance-floor scream, its revolutionary demand to move to a new beat repurposed as pure hedonistic release. What was insurrection becomes the ecstatic command to just dance.","sonnet":"A pure call to move and release through sound — the lyrics are minimal, existing only to demand screaming and dancing as the correct response to a new beat.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~all-black-everything":{"haiku":"Drug use (codeine) enables dreams and escape into darkness, creating a numbed, all-consuming aesthetic of murder and void.","opus":"A codeine-blurred nocturnal reverie that personifies the drug as an intimate companion who colors his sleepless nights. The blacked-out, dressed-in-shadow imagery and coiled menace blend addiction with a pose of cool detachment, evoking someone chemically numbed, dangerous, and already drifting somewhere beyond reach.","sonnet":"A codeine-fuelled haze dressed in all-black aesthetic — the speaker uses the drug to dream while awake, declares themselves \"all murdered out,\" and disappears like a stepping razor: clean, fast, and gone.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~all-the-girls-around-the-world-feat-theophilus-london":{"haiku":"Devotion and celebration of feminine beauty create space for romance and hope across global contexts. The song frames women as sources of meaning and inspiration while acknowledging their search for partnership.","opus":"A globe-trotting love letter that flatters one woman as what every girl is searching for, threading Beverly Hills and Amsterdam into a glossy fantasy of romance and reassurance. Electro-pop as a postcard of devotion.","sonnet":"A romantic declaration of devotion addressed to one particular woman who knows his mind and backs the rush — set against Amsterdam parks, Beverly Hills, and firework piers — while the chorus widens it into a global anthem for all girls searching for their meant-to-be.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~awesome-feat-the-cool-kids":{"haiku":"Rappers boast about dancing, style, wealth, and street credibility through extended slang and braggadocio. The song celebrates hip-hop culture and self-aggrandizement.","opus":"A playground-swagger rap piles up sneaker pride, schoolyard boasts, and party flexing over relentless braggadocio. The Bloody Beetroots, the Italian electro-punk project of Sir Bob Cornelius Rifo, hands the mic to Chicago duo The Cool Kids, whose throwback, sports-and-fresh-gear rhymes ride his abrasive dance production into pure adolescent bravado.","sonnet":"A bravado-fueled catalog of crew pride, dance-floor dominance, and street credentials delivered in rapid-fire boast. Two MCs trade verses built on cultural touchstones and playground swagger, the whole thing operating as a celebration of freshness, rhythm, and belonging to a particular pocket of cool.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~chronicles-of-a-fallen-love-feat-greta-svabo-bech":{"haiku":"A love dies as bodies fall deeper into darkness; hearts once synchronized now beat alone, their love becomes a faded memory.","opus":"Two lovers sink and drown together, hearts once beating in time now slowing and falling out of sync; the imagery of a bleeding ocean, a searched-for teardrop, and dancing that will never happen again frames this as the death of a relationship rendered as a shared descent into the depths.","sonnet":"Two people drowning together in grief after a love dies — both searching underwater for each other, both falling deeper, the ocean itself bleeding as their shared heartbeat slows and finally separates, leaving only the story of what they lost.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~clever":{"haiku":"Theft and deception sustain corrupt systems that eventually collapse under their own weight; exposure comes through artistic witness and refusal to comply.","opus":"A defiant broadside against thieves and frauds in the music and art world, trusting that karma will shatter their flimsy castle while the crew claims to do it purely for love, not profit.","sonnet":"A denunciation of plagiarism and exploitation in the music or creative industry — calling out artists who steal ideas, spread lies, and profit without remorse, while asserting that karma will catch up and the authentic ones will outlast the frauds.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~crash":{"haiku":"Two people fantasize about crashing a car together in a reckless act of abandon. The song dwells on family tragedy and the desire to destroy themselves together.","opus":"The Italian electronic act builds a reckless anthem around the image of crashing a car, insisting the wreck will be beautiful with the listener in the passenger seat. Fragmentary and repetitive by design, The Bloody Beetroots pair the death-drive chant with the taunt that the tragedy runs in the family, turning self-destruction into a euphoric, punk-electro dare.","sonnet":"A reckless intimacy is framed as a mutual car crash both parties drive toward willingly. The tragedy is inherited — already in the family, already written — and the invitation to collide is extended with something close to ecstasy, as if destruction together is preferable to safety apart.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~drive":{"haiku":"A driver accelerates frantically away, moving at dangerous speeds with no intention of stopping.","opus":"A pure adrenaline burst about flight and velocity, the driver flooring it beyond anyone's power to slow him, high beams on, gone before you hear him leave. Physics turns to poetry in the talk of suspension and gravity's brevity. The Bloody Beetroots are Sir Bob Cornelius Rifo's masked Italian electro-punk project, and this is built for exactly that collision of dance-floor propulsion and rock snarl, motion for its own exhilarating sake.","sonnet":"Pure velocity as liberation — the engine revs, the high beams are on, stars blur into suspension, and the only directive is forward motion away from everything. Nobody can slow it down; the departure is silent but absolute, and when it's gone you'll know it by the absence.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~enter-the-void":{"haiku":"A lover departs, leaving the speaker heartbroken and lost, questioning why truth is so painful.","opus":"A mournful goodbye that watches a lover recede into an emptiness the singer keeps naming, spilling his feelings and admitting no one ever truly commands love or the heart, that its truth carries more nuance than anyone can hold. The chorus circles the same baffled hurt. The Bloody Beetroots, usually known for abrasive electro-punk, here shift into a softer, wounded mode, loss cast as a nothingness that must simply be stepped into.","sonnet":"Watching someone you love leave and being unable to make it make sense — the heart gets poured out completely, love running from the deepest place all the way to excess, but none of it changed the outcome. The truth has too many details to hold, and no one, the speaker concludes, has ever fully mastered the heart.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~ffa-1985":{"haiku":"A digital-age figure ignores calls and maintains distance, holding the world in hand while surfing and escaping upward. The song celebrates dismissal and detachment.","opus":"Electro-punk bravado aimed at a hyperconnected world in your pocket, the crude refrain a gleeful kiss-off to everyone the narrator can now block and outrun, connection turned into the freedom to disconnect.","sonnet":"A rejection of someone from the past — calling them out from a position of total digital-age self-sufficiency, phone in pocket, blocking their calls and launching away — the \"1985\" a jab at someone stuck in an analogue era.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~i-love-the-bloody-beetroots":{"haiku":"Two people's love drowns and dies together in darkness; their synchronized heartbeats fade as they fall deeper into loss and grief, their blood spilling as one.","opus":"An oceanic elegy for a dying love: the narrator and a lover sink together underwater, their once-synchronized heartbeats slowing, chasing an unreachable teardrop as their fallen love, and both of them, drown.","sonnet":"Two people sinking together in grief — one watching the other fall deeper, remembering how they used to move in unison, their hearts now out of sync, their shared love described as a story of falling.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~i-m-not-holy-rework":{"haiku":"Surrender and devotion frame the speaker's willingness to break for desire, yielding flesh and heart to another. Holiness becomes irrelevant when heaven is tangible and immediate.","opus":"Surrender to a consuming desire that feels like worship, offering oneself up completely to a lover who is a taste of heaven worth abandoning any pretense of holiness for.","sonnet":"A suppliant speaker offers themselves completely — breakable, available whenever needed — to someone described as 'a little taste of heaven on my tongue'; the kneeling, praying posture frames desire as devotion, positioning the other as divine and the speaker as willingly profane.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~irreversible":{"haiku":"A person feels trapped in a broken cycle, unable to move forward despite promises that everything would change.","opus":"A relationship's ruin mourned as a promise of permanence that turned out to be a lie, its collapse leaving only resentment. The narrator is stuck going in circles, unable to advance, neglecting himself and drawn toward some fleeting hope on borrowed time, haunted by a wound that won't close and a dread of the quiet after.","sonnet":"About a relationship built on false promises that has already fallen apart — the narrator is stuck looping, unable to move forward, scarred by a wound that won't close and a fight that can't be undone.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~make-me-blank-feat-j-davey":{"haiku":"A supplicant begs transformation into empty vessel. Judge and save become interchangeable roles in seeking erasure.","opus":"A blasphemous power exchange of worship and destruction, offering the body and blood while vowing to crush and bury everything the other stands for; salvation and annihilation collapse into the same demand to be taken.","sonnet":"A song about a destructive, quasi-religious dependency — the narrator offers themselves up like a body for crucifixion, craves being taken and saved, yet simultaneously wants to butcher and crush what they're surrendering to, the dynamic swinging between worship and violence.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~nothing-but-love":{"haiku":"A fraudulent lover's fake affection contrasts with genuine sacrifice and damage; their betrayal turns real love into nothing.","opus":"A breakup reframed as an invoice, the wounded party billing an ex for the damage of a love that was fake on one side and real on the other. The knife imagery makes clear a bad love bleeds you dry.","sonnet":"A post-breakup reckoning in which the narrator itemizes what was taken from them — the debt of a relationship in which one person's love was real and the other's was not — insisting that false love is no kind of love at all.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~out-of-sight-feat-paul-mccartney-youth":{"haiku":"A song about love and betrayal, where concealment and dishonesty undermine connection despite declared affection.","opus":"A subdued, uneasy love song where affection and the dread of betrayal coexist, the narrator inviting his lover close while quietly insisting it's all quite harmless and discreet. The warmth is genuine but wary, shadowed by fear of being double-crossed. This Bloody Beetroots track reportedly features Paul McCartney under Youth's production, its plainspoken intimacy a striking departure for the electro-punk act.","sonnet":"A compact declaration of love tested by betrayal — the speaker loved genuinely and asked only to be believed, but the other person chose to lie, and now that act of betrayal is permanently unforgettable. What remains is a clipped, bruised tenderness: nothing too much, just out of sight.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~pirates-punks-politics":{"haiku":"A chaotic figure exploits society through contradiction and manipulation, cutting jobs and raising rents without needing permission or moral justification.","opus":"An electro-punk taunt equating the pirate, the punk and the politician as figures who seize power without permission, then flipping to a corrupt president who cuts jobs and raises rent. Contradiction weaponized as protest.","sonnet":"The political class as pirates and punks operating without accountability — the song adopts the voice of a cynical populist who manipulates and divides, cuts jobs, raises rents, all while insisting they need no one's permission, naming the demagogue archetype without pretending to oppose it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~raw-feat-tommy-lee":{"haiku":"Exclamatory complaint against synthetic disco production gives way to demand for raw guitar-driven rock energy and authenticity.","opus":"A studio-argument skit that mocks disco pretension before demanding guitars and chaos, with Tommy Lee as the voice of the mosh. The whole thing is a punk-rave manifesto disguised as a joke.","sonnet":"A studio prank — Tommy Lee reacting with performative outrage to the tracks being played, dismissing disco and demanding they turn up the guitars, until the chaos he wanted arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~rocksteady":{"opus":"The Bloody Beetroots, Sir Bob Cornelius Rifo's masked electro-punk project, built a reputation on abrasive, riff-driven club tracks over lyrics, and this is one of them. An instrumental with no real text beyond the title chanted as placeholder, it functions as pure kinetic energy, the vocal reduced to a rhythmic stamp rather than a message.","sonnet":"An instrumental track with no meaningful lyrical content beyond the word 'Rocksteady' repeated as a placeholder.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~second-streets-have-no-name-feat-beta-bow":{"haiku":"Intimate connection blooms in forgotten urban spaces where two unnamed wanderers find brief solace and presence outside the mapped world.","opus":"A hazy, dreamlike drive through the fading light of a fraying connection. The Italian electro-punk duo trade their usual aggression for something wistful, sketching two people slipping past each other while a nameless backstreet stands in for the disorientation of a bond losing its landmarks. Longing and drift blur together into an impressionistic goodbye.","sonnet":"A hazy, slow-motion drive toward someone at the margins of a city — the unnamed back streets are where intimacy lives, away from the named and navigable world. The narrator watches movement in the other's eye and wants to be their Sunday morning; the two are smiling in anonymous lanes where no one else comes.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~stompers":{"haiku":"A collective of rebels rejects all rules and authority, marching through destruction with no fear and no moral constraint.","opus":"A tribal manifesto from a self-styled crew who swear loyalty solely to one another and to no nation or banner, brandishing disorder itself as their weapon. Their sole rule is having none at all, framing revolt as the tearing-down of the established order by a pack with no chief.","sonnet":"A song about a rebel collective that defines itself by annihilating everything in its path — the Stompers pledge allegiance not to nation or morality but to each other, embracing chaos and no rules, positioning themselves as a dystopian uprising against an oppressive system that has branded them thugs.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~storm":{"haiku":"Boasting about dancing prowess, style, and street confidence with playful trash talk and references to hip-hop dance moves.","opus":"A braggadocious old-school rap flex, all fresh sneakers, playground bravado and schoolyard swagger, tossing rivals aside while flossing hard and keeping the party rolling. The verses lean on nostalgic references to New Edition, the Cabbage Patch and Air Jordans, more attitude than narrative. The Bloody Beetroots are the Italian electro-house project of Sir Bob Cornelius Rifo, and this borrows a vintage hip-hop guest verse over their trademark distorted, masked rave energy.","sonnet":"Pure floor-filling swagger with a competitive edge — a dancer claiming superiority over everyone in the room, rattling off move after move and crew after crew as proof. The boasts stack up through baseball and old-school hip-hop references until the whole thing becomes a kind of gleeful catalog of dominance, held together by the word awesome repeated like a chant.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~talkin-in-my-sleep-feat-lisa-kekaula":{"haiku":"A person struggles with their fractured mind, experiencing intrusive visions and sensations so vivid they taste them. The lyrics describe sleep-talking as an involuntary expression of the mind's torment.","opus":"A jittery assertion of self against a brain that keeps deceiving its owner, summoning sensations so vivid they seem almost tangible, leaking out in unconscious murmurs and a plea to know if anyone caught them. It is proudly off-kilter. A powerhouse soul vocalist lends gospel-flecked grit to the duo's electro-punk clatter.","sonnet":"A mind so saturated with someone that it produces visions and sensations indistinguishable from reality — the speaker can't worry about anyone else, can't stop the loop of hallucination, and the things the brain conjures feel real enough to taste. Whatever is being said in sleep, it's audible to everyone but controlled by no one.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~volevo-un-gatto-nero-you-promised-me-feat-gigi-barocco":{"haiku":"A promise shattered becomes fuel for rage: betrayal by someone who claimed to be new, leaving only fury and the refusal to let deception settle.","opus":"A broken promise thrown back at the producer by name, all thrill and betrayal, the fury boiled down to profanity and a chant of accusation. Party-track venom aimed at a liar.","sonnet":"A song about broken promises and betrayal — someone made commitments they cannot keep, and the speaker's affection has curdled into furious contempt at being lied to.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bloody-beetroots~wolfpack":{"haiku":"A united force marches forward with violent purpose, overwhelming all opposition with unstoppable power.","opus":"A predatory crew marches forward relentlessly, fire in their eyes, hunting blood and overthrowing thrones with no intention of letting go. Italian electro-punk act The Bloody Beetroots crank this into a snarling pack anthem, the hunted told they can never escape while the collective gathers around, bound together in ferocious pursuit.","sonnet":"A collective hunting anthem driven by pack mentality and ferocious momentum. The group moves with relentless aggression, taking over territory and refusing to relinquish what they have seized. Solidarity and shared fire in the eyes bind them together against those who think they can escape.","src":"haiku"},
"the-blue-stones~let-me-out":{"haiku":"Suffocation accumulates until pressure shatters restraint; the speaker demands release from a relationship built on illusion.","opus":"A demand to escape a suffocating relationship with someone whose surface calm hides rot, the singer pleading to be released before the buried truth breaks through violently.","sonnet":"Something suppressed inside the speaker demands release — the 'aces up your sleeve' are failing, and what was supposed to be forever has become hit or miss; the repeated 'let me out' is both a cry against internal stagnation and an ultimatum to the person who keeps the door shut.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bobby-lees~new-self":{"haiku":"Recovery transforms the self into new being; the adult reflects on youthful instability and younger fantasy.","opus":"A wild, funny reckoning with a past self lost in mental crisis and self-destruction, romanticizing a celebrity encounter while confessing to spiraling. Recovery framed as wishing an old flame could meet who you've become.","sonnet":"A woman recalls an unhinged, aimless night in the desert where she saw Shaun White, lost her wallet and phone, and later confessed suicidal thoughts before deleting a number in embarrassment — she has since gotten help and wishes the person from that night could meet her new self.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bonez~until-you-wake-up":{"haiku":"Vigil kept beside someone who may be dying or unconscious — the speaker holds on through grief and falling tears, insisting they are still here, still waiting for the door to open and for time to be free again.","opus":"A vigil kept beside someone slipping away, sung with the emotional directness of Japanese rock band The Bonez. The narrator clings to shared memories and a remembered smile as an anchor while illness or unconsciousness pulls a loved one under, refusing to accept the loss and vowing to wait, in agony, for them to return.","sonnet":"Vigil beside someone seriously ill — the narrator waits for them to wake and smile, fighting something that beats them while they sleep, drawing strength from shared memories while wiping tears. The plea 'let me dream today to let you walk again' collapses the boundary between hope and prayer.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~above-the-clouds":{"haiku":"A falling person struggles against weight and doubt, seeking rescue and redemption while shadows pursue them downward.","opus":"A fight against despair, the narrator trying to shed his fears but sinking anyway, feeling broken and begging to be rescued, while the recurring image of sunlight beyond the rainclouds holds out a fragile hope past the heaviness weighing on him. The Brave are an Australian melodic metalcore band.","sonnet":"Someone in freefall — doubt replacing fear, falling straight down, begging to be fixed and saved — reaching up for a sun that stays behind the clouds, unable to get traction or find their footing before time runs out.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~aura":{"opus":"A song about suffocating emotional reliance, where the singer insists he cannot survive or stay whole apart from the one he loves. Their absence leaves him hollow and purposeless, a weapon with no means to fire, forever drawn helplessly back toward them.","sonnet":"A raw declaration of absolute emotional dependence on one person — without them there is no oxygen, no light, and the narrator is falling apart with a blackened heart; the song is almost purely the sensation of needing someone back.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-brave~aura-reimagined":{"haiku":"Dependency and dissolution collapse as absence leaves the narrator unable to breathe or function. The song frames love as essential oxygen and loss as suffocation beneath darkness.","opus":"Total dependence on another person for survival, cast as running out of air and coming apart without them. Melodic-metalcore desperation where love is the only thing keeping the lungs working.","sonnet":"Total dependency — without the other person there is no oxygen, no sun, only a broken heart and a bullet without a gun — is declared with desperate simplicity, every image a variation on the single theme that the speaker cannot function or survive alone.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~burn":{"haiku":"Witness to internal fire: the speaker acknowledges inner pain in their partner and chooses radical commitment, willing to immolate alongside them rather than let them burn alone.","opus":"The Australian metalcore band frames love for a person tormented by an inner blaze, promising to throw himself into that same destruction to stay beside them. The Brave trace a shift from clinging desperately to a resigned understanding that setting the person free may be the truest devotion left.","sonnet":"A person watching a partner prepare to leave refuses to let go without stating the stakes plainly. The fire burning inside the other person is real and recognized, and the narrator would rather enter the flames than stand outside them — devotion expressed as willingness to be consumed alongside.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~desolation":{"haiku":"Physical and psychological breakdown in a barren wasteland; thirst, hallucinations, and numbness as death approaches.","opus":"A feverish survival nightmare set adrift in scorching wilderness, the narrator parched, hunted by scavenging birds, and reduced to swallowing poison to ease his thirst. The Brave turn the delirium and creeping numbness into a vision of inner ruin, hell felt as it spreads through the body.","sonnet":"A traveler lost in a scorching desert, circled by vultures, hallucinates and drinks venom out of desperation, teetering on the edge of death — the landscape serves as both literal setting and an image of total desolation and physical collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~dragged-down":{"haiku":"The speaker is stuck in a divided, contemptuous relationship characterized by coldness and conflict, repeatedly dragged down by the situation.","opus":"A cry from inside a relationship poisoned by contempt and silence, the narrator worn down to the bone and finally done waiting to matter. Metalcore catharsis built on the question of whether the other person ever cared at all.","sonnet":"A song about being stuck in a life defined by conflict, contempt, and silence — the narrator keeps getting dragged to the same dark place, bones breaking under the weight of it, longing to get back to something earlier and cleaner but unable to find the way.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~ethereal":{"haiku":"People born to love are forced into conflict and darkness, yet they resist by becoming sparks of light themselves. The song frames human resilience against oppressive forces as a spiritual defiance.","opus":"The Australian metalcore band stages a cosmic contest between radiance and advancing gloom, casting empty agents of shadow as certain losers to those who imagine themselves living light. The Brave urge listeners to attend to quiet suffering and hidden truth, recasting a life of conflict as luminous, otherworldly defiance against the encroaching dark.","sonnet":"Forces of light and darkness are arrayed against one another, but the side that burns bright insists on its own inextinguishable nature. Against hollow vessels of shadow, those who carry inner fire claim an ethereal identity that cannot be extinguished regardless of how the sky turns.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~goodbye":{"haiku":"Regret over an unspoken farewell: death arrived too suddenly; the speaker failed to say goodbye or provide attention to someone now gone, tormented by the realization that time had already run out.","opus":"The Australian band voices raw regret over a death, apologizing for time let slip and wishing for one last hug that can never come. The Brave strip their metalcore back to grief's plainest ache, excuses pouring out like a waterfall as the narrator admits he did not grasp how serious it was until it finally, irreversibly got the person he loved.","sonnet":"A person confronts the guilt of missing a final chance to say goodbye to someone who died. Denial and distraction kept the seriousness at bay until too late, and now the absence is filled only with apology — for the hug not given, the words swallowed, the time that kept escaping.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~lost-to-the-night":{"haiku":"A person haunted by stale hangovers and lost memories can't hold onto their lover and can't become who they wanted to be, left cold and hollow in an empty house.","opus":"A bleary morning-after reckoning with dependency and a love coming apart, the narrator waking with regret as his partner drifts beyond reach and their shared space never became a real refuge. The Brave expose the crash beneath the high, the suffocating uncertainty, and the dawning verdict that walking away is finally necessary.","sonnet":"About waking up with cigarette breath and a fading memory of someone who slipped away — the narrator is frozen inside, unable to become what the person needed, still unable to move on, the house they shared now just a cold shell that was never made into a home.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~out-of-reach":{"haiku":"A person is sleepless and disoriented, hypnotized by their own pain while reality slips away.","opus":"A vivid depiction of insomnia and dissociation: weeks without sleep, days and nights blurred, staring at a dead TV as reality drifts out of reach; time itself grips him as the clock stands still and he feels himself breaking apart and falling into pieces.","sonnet":"A dissociative episode rendered in concrete detail — weeks without sleep, sensory overload, time stopped, hypnotic staring at a candle flame, the person falling apart piece by piece as reality recedes.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~searchlights":{"haiku":"Mourning absence while searching through darkness; the departed burns as starlight, guiding and haunting the living who yearn for reunion.","opus":"A sorrowful tribute to someone lost prematurely, the singer scanning the heavens and shutting his eyes to find the person shining back like a distant star, recalling nights once spent together beneath the sky. Mourning and solace intertwine, the departed felt as a constant presence. The Brave, an Australian metalcore band, pour that weight into a soaring memorial ballad about grief carried as light.","sonnet":"A grief song framed as a search through the night sky — the person lost is imagined as a star, too bright and too necessary to the dark for the world to have kept them, and the speaker scans the darkness hoping to find them still burning somewhere. The ache is not for closure but for one more sighting.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~technicolor":{"haiku":"A person lost in confinement watches reality distort into color while remaining trapped in grayscale, unable to escape despite mental dissolution.","opus":"A vivid depiction of a mind coming undone, the narrator losing all sense of time in a room that seems to dissolve into color while he remains drained and colorless, unable to escape the spiral of his own thoughts. It renders mental disintegration through warping, dreamlike imagery.","sonnet":"A person lost inside their own mind — disoriented, time-blind, watching their inner world dissolve into swirling colour while they themselves feel drained of it — searches desperately for a way out of a dissociated mental state.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brave~through-the-dark":{"haiku":"Survival and trust through apocalypse; people run through darkness and decay with constant threat, yet must believe in each other and keep moving forward without solid ground.","opus":"An Australian metalcore band renders a scorched apocalyptic landscape as a metaphor for pushing through despair alongside someone else. The message underneath the ruin is simple endurance: keep moving, you don't have to face the dark alone.","sonnet":"A survival song about fleeing a devastated world — bodies under attack, ash-choked lands, stolen skin over hollow shells — with the narrator pushing through blindly alongside others, accused of slowly killing those who trusted him.","src":"haiku"},
"the-brothers-johnson~get-the-funk-out-ma-face":{"opus":"A defiant celebration of funk as a communal release, telling detractors that if they don't like the music they can simply back off and leave the band to their groove.","sonnet":"A defiant assertion of musical freedom: if you don't like the funk, move on — nobody's forcing you, but funk is a universal release and those who let it in will discover how to groove.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-browning~hivemind":{"haiku":"Technology enslaves consciousness into collective singularity, erasing individual humanity through digital control.","opus":"The Browning fuse metalcore with electronic dread to imagine a dystopia where artificial intelligence achieves singularity and humanity surrenders its soul to a digital god. It is a paranoid vision of collective mind-control and technological worship, where individuality is reprogrammed away and people bow willingly to the machine that consumes them.","sonnet":"Technology as totalitarian religion — humanity reprogrammed and enslaved to a coded throne, bowing to the new age god of digital singularity, feeding it soul and humanity on demand. \"It's too late\" is the refrain underneath the manifesto; the hivemind has already won and the song is the record of the submission.","src":"haiku"},
"the-bunny-the-bear~playing-with-fire":{"haiku":"Domestic violence and resentment create cycles of pain and return as the narrator questions their own complicity. The song names abuse without euphemism while exploring the paradox of loving one's abuser.","opus":"An unflinching portrait of a mutually abusive relationship, where violence and love arrived together and the narrator keeps crawling back on lust and loneliness. Addiction to a partner who hurts, told without absolving himself.","sonnet":"A relationship defined by mutual abuse — the first night she said she loved him was the first night she hit him, he retaliated, and they kept crawling back — is narrated with unflinching self-implication: the speaker knows they were not half the man they claimed, and they still feel the burn.","src":"haiku"},
"the-butterfly-effect~a-slow-descent":{"haiku":"The speaker experiences slow decay and disintegration, drowning in their own deterioration while isolated and unable to wake from the process.","opus":"A trance-like dwelling on rot and the strange truth that a person is never quite by himself even while sinking into his own wreckage, an extended farewell staged as a burial where the barriers collapse and the void pours in. Solace sours into aching delight along some misplaced highway. The Butterfly Effect are an Australian alt-metal band with a moody, atmospheric streak, and this eases toward a resigned yielding to the fall.","sonnet":"A slow-motion self-destruction narrated from within — numbed by fire, lost on a motorway, cornered by mistakes, the speaker keeps insisting you'll never be alone even as everything dims. The tone toggles between dissolution and a strange negative comfort: the going-under has company, even if that company is only the darkness itself.","src":"haiku"},
"the-butterfly-effect~always":{"haiku":"Emotional poison seeps through, leaving guilt and numbness; the speaker learns too late that change is impossible and carries these burning frustrations forever alongside an unhealthy need.","opus":"The Australian alt-metal band frames love as a toxin: a presence the speaker feels seeping through him, bringing numbness, guilt, and a sickness he suspects he'll never fully recover from. Yet the song turns on his admission that he needs this person constantly, collapsing addiction and devotion into a single craving that is both his wound and his lifeline.","sonnet":"Toxic attachment felt as physical contamination — the other person seeps in like poison, numbing and guilting simultaneously, and the speaker knows it's too late to change but can't stop needing them anyway. Frustration burns like a stain on the skin, and the word always becomes both a lament and an admission of total dependency.","src":"haiku"},
"the-butterfly-effect~before-they-knew":{"haiku":"A person reflects on childhood loss and abandonment, wondering about unseen pain and the world's failure to protect them.","opus":"The Australian alt-metal band pairs brooding heaviness with unexpected tenderness in this study of buried childhood suffering. It addresses someone whose life ended young, gently probing whether the world abandoned them and whether they waited in vain to be rescued, then offering the quiet mercy that they needn't relive it now.","sonnet":"A reaching-out to someone who grew up in a household of pain — a bleeding mother, an absent or failing world — asking whether the damage is still alive inside them, whether the cold and the dark are still there. The answer, offered gently, is that the speaker carries it too, and that there's no need to keep explaining or reliving it right now.","src":"haiku"},
"the-butterfly-effect~black-lung":{"haiku":"A metaphor of diseased lungs and suffocation describes a toxic relationship where one person sacrifices their breath and health to sustain another's survival.","opus":"A confrontation with someone whose trust corrodes like dust filling the mouth, the narrator refusing to keep breathing and bleeding on their behalf and finally claiming his own turn to breathe. Black lung works as a metaphor for a suffocating, one-sided relationship. The Butterfly Effect, from Brisbane, deliver this in their moody Australian alt-metal register, the compensating refrain building toward a quiet act of self-reclamation.","sonnet":"A relationship or dependency where one person has been breathing on behalf of the other for so long that their own lungs are blackened and bleeding. The speaker is done listening to excuses, done with the suffocating dynamic, and asserts the right to finally take a breath that belongs only to them.","src":"haiku"},
"the-butterfly-effect~in-a-memory":{"haiku":"Trapped in repetitive memories and powerless isolation, the speaker seeks escape; deep wounds and isolation prevent change, leaving only the echo of lost potential.","opus":"A mind so cluttered with sameness that every option feels like no option, the singer weighing whether he clings to a fading relationship for nothing, terrified change will never arrive. This Australian alt-metal band gives the ache a wide, echoing sweep, memory serving as both a hiding place and a snare he can't escape.","sonnet":"A mind trying to escape its own archive — satellites fall like rain, walls close in, and every door leads the same direction no matter which way you choose. The speaker is pinned between a past that won't release and a change that keeps not arriving, praying while knowing the prayer won't be answered, running while knowing the thing pursued is only a memory of what could have been.","src":"haiku"},
"the-butterfly-effect~reach":{"haiku":"Someone tries desperately to reach a person he cannot hold, only in dreams. He walks through burned fields calling their name, dying to keep them as they fade.","opus":"The ache of a loss that lives only in sleep, the narrator reunited with a lost loved one each night in dreams, then waking to nothing, suspended between longing and need. The song's throughline is desperate devotion, clinging to that vanished presence and failing, over and over, to bridge an absence that can't be crossed.","sonnet":"The ache of losing someone or something and being unable to bridge the gap — dreams of holding them close end in waking emptiness, the fields are burning, and the narrator keeps reaching through the dark, holding their hand and refusing to stop trying even as everything falls.","src":"haiku"},
"the-butterfly-effect~window-and-the-watcher":{"haiku":"An observer watches ashes fall on a city of illusion while hiding from sleeping giants; two people cling to each other. The song depicts passive witnessing of apocalypse with fragile human connection.","opus":"The Australian alt-metal band sets a love song against the end of the world, a couple dancing through doomed splendor while destruction closes in on everyone. Beneath the cinematic dread runs a raw dependence, a plea not to be abandoned even one more time, and a stubborn refusal to stop believing that some deliverance is still possible.","sonnet":"A figure at a window watching ash fall from the sky while the world moves through promises of gold that turned out hollow — the speaker pleads not to be left alone another night, can't give up but can't carry on, and ends holding onto a single belief that miracles might still exist for some people, even now.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cabs~camn-aven":{"haiku":"Fragmentation and despair characterize existence as the narrator drowns in plastic chains and refuses touch. The shared suffering becomes a reason to resist divine authority together rather than transcend alone.","opus":"Japanese post-hardcore anguish over self-disgust and a body that feels like a prison, begging to be struck rather than touched, wishing to become a fish and escape into water. Shared damnation offered as the only closeness left.","sonnet":"A dissociative breakdown in Japanese — the throat dries, the face needs striking, and a wish to become a fish goes unfulfilled while plastic chains bind everything to the question \"are you happy?\" — and the final plea is for no one to touch, no signal to the frightening people, only the shared memory to hold.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cabs~hana-no-youni":{"haiku":"Like flowers blooming briefly then fading, the speaker grants candy to trapped children and accepts their role in systems of comfort and cruelty. Reflection arrives too late.","opus":"A wish to live purely like a flower collides with images of children kept alive by tubes and the sickening smell of a piano, as the singer begs a mother's forgiveness and confronts how vivid tenderness has vanished from memory, leaving only nausea and the pull toward a beautiful death.","sonnet":"A dissociated confession addressed to a mother in the nauseating smell of piano: children in tubes and airports given candy and tickets, the number of the last one counted, vivid kindness absent from memory — ending with the half-envious, half-despairing wish that living beautifully, like a flower, would be a wonderful thing, even knowing it seems impossible now.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cabs~kakko-no-suno-ue-de":{"haiku":"Waiting and resignation mark the narrator's posture as they sit above a metaphorical cuckoo's nest observing humanity's destruction. Fashion and beauty mask the pursuit of salvation while seasons pass unchanged.","opus":"Waiting atop the cuckoo's nest for an ordinary salvation that never comes, watching someone sell freedom and body for money in a loop of consuming and purging. Bleak Japanese post-hardcore about the impossibility of anything changing.","sonnet":"Waiting on the cuckoo's nest for an ordinary salvation and the meaning of loss, the speaker watches someone in stylish clothes point at pitiful strangers while leaning against white walls — freedom, purity, and the body have all been converted into cash, and the February film keeps breaking off before it ends.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cabs~nigastu-no-heitai":{"haiku":"Two figures drift through Siberian emptiness, marked by violence and abandonment, reaching for escape toward a church where no god listens. Their attempted flight fails—they remain entombed in cold earth, their names on tombstones.","opus":"A jealous, doomed love told through a tin soldier's imagery: the lover curses the speaker cruelly, loses her blue shoes and can't move, while another man who 'doesn't know her words' takes her body; the couple try to flee hand in hand toward a godless church, but her name is already carved on a Siberian grave marker.","sonnet":"A love story of displacement and loss set against Siberian imagery: a name carved into a grave marker, a godless church where two people once tried to flee together, and the recurring absence of blue shoes that keeps the speaker rooted and unable to move — a tin soldier and a fingerless farmer searching for what is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cabs~sk-r":{"haiku":"Brokenness and gentleness coexist as the narrator sketches hidden stories and offers seasonal meaning as ultimate gift. The song frames vulnerability and authentic connection as radical in a world demanding performance.","opus":"The Cabs were a short-lived cult Japanese band whose fragile, literary post-hardcore ended abruptly, lending this piece a fairy-tale melancholy. In a deaf-mute city of lost shoes and a broken body, a private myth two people alone can read, the speaker kneels before a priest waiting for sin and vows to live gently and choose only beautiful words before the season turns.","sonnet":"A tender, fractured exchange between two people in a deaf-mute city — shoes lost, a broken body, a fairy tale only they know — ends with the speaker kneeling before a priest waiting for sin to arrive, and a promise to live gently and choose only beautiful words before the season changes.","src":"haiku"},
"the-callous-daoboys~a-brief-article-regarding-time-loops":{"haiku":"Temporal recursion traps consciousness in cyclical trauma; the narrator recognizes repetition but cannot escape the cycle, condemned to relive the same devastation.","opus":"A chaotic spiral through déjà-vu, self-loathing and the horror of repeating the same wasted life, culminating in the desperate wish to start over. Laced with Twin Peaks imagery, it's about squandering your one existence inside an inescapable loop.","sonnet":"A fractured, surrealist meditation on compulsion and repetition — personal anguish, cycles of behaviour, and deja-vu collapse into each other as the speaker alternates between violent interior imagery and a woman's childhood experience of recognising a pre-lived moment, both arriving at the same desire to start over.","src":"haiku"},
"the-callous-daoboys~fake-dinosaur-bones":{"opus":"A bitter address to a shape-shifting, fake person (a changeling) whose constant reinvention feels like betrayal, the narrator both cursing them for destroying his faith and helplessly still saying yes to them.","sonnet":"About an ever-shifting, contradictory person the narrator is helplessly devoted to — described as a changeling whose inconsistency is simultaneously infuriating and beautiful, and whose betrayal provokes both spiritual devastation and unconditional surrender.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-callous-daoboys~pushing-the-pink-envelope":{"haiku":"Regret for words taken back and moments squandered; watching others achieve the life that slipped away.","opus":"A surreal, devastating spiral of envy and remorse from the mathcore mayhem of The Callous Daoboys. The narrator pictures a former partner living the settled domestic life he was shut out of, tallying the marriages of folks he once made music for, sinking into bitterness and self-hatred while pleading, far too late, to undo it all and be welcomed back into a world that has already moved past him.","sonnet":"A torrent of associative grief over an ex who moved on — military-grade memories in a cab ride, white picket fences in the fireplace, star baby with a full heart in a nuclear modest house — the speaker, C-list on resentment, cycles between wanting to take it all back and recognizing he'll take years off his life just trying not to think of their time as wasted.","src":"haiku"},
"the-callous-daoboys~waco-jesus":{"haiku":"Religious apocalypse and cult violence, faith as justification for destruction.","opus":"A blasphemous, obsessive fever dream where erotic fixation blurs into religious worship. The Callous Daoboys warp devotion into something profane, casting the object of desire as a Magdalene figure and the narrator as a man consumed by lust, disgust, and a savior complex, unable to separate salvation from possession.","sonnet":"Religious obsession and sexual fixation fuse in the figure of a man who is \"someone to no one\" — white whale and nailed hands, a scrolled scrapbook of a woman's body worshipped like a Magdalene, vertebrae as all the narrator will ever need. \"Machine gun flood\" is the only resolution offered to an ecstasy that can't be appealed to.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cardigans~lovefool":{"haiku":"Desperation supplants dignity as the speaker begs for false love rather than abandonment, preferring deception to solitude regardless of cost.","opus":"A woman begging a lover to fake affection rather than leave, so desperate she'll accept pretense over honesty. The bright pop bounce masks genuine humiliation: she knows the love is gone and pleads for the performance of it anyway.","sonnet":"A woman fully aware that love is gone — advised by her mother to move on, knowing she should — nonetheless begs her partner to pretend, to fool her, to say the words even if they are lies. The desperation is self-aware and bottomless: she would rather be deceived than face the absence.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cardigans~my-favourite-game":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession becomes futile; the speaker tries to reshape a partner to match their vision but fails, losing both the relationship and their sense of self in the attempt. Love turns into isolation.","opus":"The Cardigans darken their pop sheen into a bruised power-pop breakup, the narrator confessing she tried to remake a lover into someone she could love more and calling it a lethal, failed experiment. Nina Persson's sweetness cuts against black-and-blue imagery, the refrain casting a doomed relationship as a favorite game both partners are losing as one loses their mind.","sonnet":"A woman engineers a relationship like an experiment, trying to transform a partner into someone capable of genuine love, and loses. The project fails not through lack of effort but because the design was flawed from the start — and what is lost alongside the lover is her own sense of herself as a savior.","src":"haiku"},
"the-chats~smoko":{"opus":"A comic slacker anthem defending the sacred right to a smoke break, the narrator batting away work and Centrelink hold-music to be left alone on his milk-crate throne.","sonnet":"A slice of Queensland working-class life: a man on a milk crate declares his smoke break inviolable, and a second vignette has the speaker calling Centrelink to abuse them, only to be put on hold — both scenes orbit the same sovereign right to be left alone.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-contortionist~anatomy-anomalies":{"haiku":"In a digitized world where technology mediates reality, individuals lose autonomy over their own meaning.","opus":"Transhumanist unease from a prog-metal band imagining minds rewired by implants and algorithms, where perfect dreams are manufactured. It asks whether choosing your own reality is freedom or just a better cage.","sonnet":"A meditation on consciousness restructured by technology — implants, algorithms, and virtual selection reshaping how the mind defines itself and what is real — asking whether the user retains any authentic agency within a system that quantizes reality and rewires perception. The tone is detached, almost clinical, treating the erosion of the self as a technical problem.","src":"haiku"},
"the-contortionist~early-grave":{"haiku":"Confronting suicidal ideation and drowning; the speaker reassures someone that they are more than their despair and can choose to stay.","opus":"The Contortionist dial back their progressive metal into a hand extended toward someone drowning in despair, someone convinced they are past rescue and asking if it's already hopeless. The narrator pleads only that they choose to keep living, insisting they are worth more than an early death, and answers each spiral of self-doubt with steady reassurance not to give up.","sonnet":"A direct address to someone drowning in despair and contemplating self-destruction — the speaker pleads for them to stay, insisting their life holds more value than an early end. The urgency is tender rather than prescriptive, built on the recognition that the person questions whether rescue is even possible.","src":"haiku"},
"the-contortionist~ebb-flow":{"haiku":"Cycles of rise and fall without apparent meaning; endless drifting through progression and dissolution.","opus":"On Language, the band drifts from deathcore toward atmospheric prog, imagining the self dissolving into a larger cosmic current. Consciousness takes hold, rises from its origins, and surrenders to natural rhythm, endlessly probing what any of it means. That surfacing awareness is cast as both an ending and the threshold of something new.","sonnet":"Consciousness surrenders to a rhythm larger than individual will. Having risen from roots and seen a collective vision through, the narrator releases all grasping and drifts — the question of what it all means hanging unanswered in the current, endlessly cycling between sinking and floating without needing resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"the-contortionist~follow":{"haiku":"Critique of unconscious obedience: people are blinded by material gain and distraction, led passively by unseen forces that control their fate like puppets, never questioning the cost to their souls.","opus":"The band condemns a flock steered blindly by hidden powers, having sampled every consciousness-expanding substance yet remaining hollow specters hauled along by force. The Contortionist's turn toward atmospheric prog suits a resigned refrain of the many trailing the few, the narrator gripping his pride while admitting his spirit has been sold off for a promised improvement that never comes.","sonnet":"A collective sleepwalk through systems of control is examined without consolation. Distracted by money, numbed by chemical shortcuts, the mass of people follows wherever they are led — the critique implicates everyone, including the narrator, and offers no exit from the undertow.","src":"haiku"},
"the-contortionist~language-i-intuition":{"haiku":"Natural growth and interconnection; reaching toward the sun while rooted to intuition as salvation and source.","opus":"The Contortionist open their Language concept album with a call to restore humanity's bond with nature, addressed to a maternal solar presence that stands for the source of all life. The speaker dissolves into that living connection itself, the prog band casting organic, cyclical imagery of tides and growth as a path back to origin — instinct as both compass and destination.","sonnet":"Intuition is declared the primary language of the living world. Organisms branch outward from a common source, reaching toward a maternal sun; the self expands and echoes rather than contracts and concludes, with the message that deep knowing precedes speech and restoring connection to that source is what healing the collective actually requires.","src":"haiku"},
"the-contortionist~reimagined":{"haiku":"Meaning dissolves into repetition and projection; reality becomes whatever the listener wants it to be.","opus":"The Contortionist treat perception as endlessly pliable, describing a moment where sight and sound reorganize into a new language of pure repetition, reality becoming whatever the mind decides it is. Breath lost and regained anchors the drift, and the ending concedes the whole vision was only ever what the listener projected onto it.","sonnet":"A perceptual breakthrough dissolves old frameworks of meaning. When sound takes shape and the point of life materialises as eyes approaching, the narrator finds not clarity but a new kind of incomprehension — familiar patterns now strange, repetition revealed as construction — until breath returns and everything that was lost reforms around a different centre.","src":"haiku"},
"the-contortionist~the-center":{"haiku":"Trapped in a mental state of paralysis, the speaker begs for release from intrusive thoughts and shame.","opus":"Dissociation rendered as a slow sinking, the narrator losing his head and craving only sleep to feel free. Pride keeps him from coming down while he begs to be seen for what he sees.","sonnet":"A speaker overwhelmed and disoriented begs for the permission to sleep — not nihilistically but as the only way to feel free, to let the sinking feeling bottom out and find the centre again without the nauseating weight of consciousness pressing down.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cost~not-for-me":{"haiku":"Emotional walls collapse under accumulated weight; the speaker retreats from vulnerability and shields their fractured mind, knowing intimacy demands exposure they cannot risk. The test of connection remains unresolved.","opus":"Coming to terms with a person who lingered too long in his thoughts and has finally departed, the singer dismantling the guarded barrier he raised and shrinking them down to nothing but one more thing he recalls.","sonnet":"A speaker tears down the internal wall they built to shelter themselves from someone who lingered too long in their mind, ultimately demoting that person to \"just another memory\" once everything else is over.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cost~one-of-a-kind":{"haiku":"Uniqueness becomes burden as illusion collapses; the speaker drowns in self-reflection.","opus":"A portrait of someone lost in self-delusion, singularly gifted yet drifting in and out of sense and bargaining with his own illusions. Crowned in a kingdom of his own invention, he's urged to throw it all away and finally see himself clearly.","sonnet":"A person telling themselves they are exceptional while simultaneously warning that grandiosity blinds and misguides — the tension between being special and being deluded runs through the whole song, leaving the question of whether the self-elevation is heroic or a trap.","src":"haiku"},
"the-courtneys~90210":{"haiku":"A person struggles to express bottled-up feelings and emotions, seeking release by connecting with water and natural beauty to process what they cannot voice.","opus":"A feeling the narrator can't articulate, bottled up in head, chest and brain to the point of driving her insane, and the impulse to release it by going to the water. The deeper read is that emotional expression is displaced onto nature, the river and ocean become the outlet she can't find in words, and the closing advice to slow down and breathe treats overwhelm with simple grounding.","sonnet":"A feeling too big and bottled-up to name or express, and the attempt to release it by surrendering to something vast — the ocean, the river, the dead of night — with the song ending in an almost meditative instruction to simply breathe.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cramps~human-fly":{"haiku":"Speaker embodies a mutant pest—part-human, part-fly—with a fractured mind thriving on chaos and decay.","opus":"A gleefully deranged monster-movie boast, the narrator declaring himself part insect with ninety-six eyes and a garbage brain, daring the world to spray pesticide because he's a reborn maggot immune to it all. The Cramps pioneered psychobilly, fusing rockabilly twang with horror-B-movie sleaze, and this is their aesthetic distilled: primitive, campy, buzzing menace played entirely for grinning shock and swagger rather than any real threat.","sonnet":"A gleefully grotesque self-portrait — the speaker is a buzzing, twitching thing with too many eyes and a garbage brain, thriving on pesticide and immune to fear. There's no agenda beyond announcing the absurdity of the condition: a reborn maggot, a human fly, inexplicably alive and entirely fine with not knowing why.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cranberries~animal-instinct":{"haiku":"Depression and stress triggered unexpectedly transform the speaker's emotional state; they fear change despite loving connection. The song explores paradox—vulnerability masked by animal defenses.","opus":"Dolores O'Riordan wrote this about the fierce, protective pull of new motherhood, the instinct to shield a child from anyone who would take them, wrapped in the vulnerability of loving something that completely.","sonnet":"A person is suddenly hit by depression and existential dread in the middle of an ordinary morning, sensing that the person they love will never truly see them; beneath the anxiety is a fierce, primitive drive to protect that bond from whatever threatens to take it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cranberries~i-can-t-be-with-you":{"haiku":"Someone grieving the loss of a relationship they deeply wanted cries over the absence and finality of their separation, still consumed by love while accepting they cannot be together.","opus":"Grief over a lost love, lying awake crying because the person is simply not there, still helplessly in love. The heartbreak deepens with the admission that she wanted to be the mother of his child, so this isn't just missing him but mourning an entire imagined future ended by farewell.","sonnet":"About loving someone intensely — wanting to have had their child, wanting to stay — while being separated from them by circumstances that make the relationship impossible. The grief is enormous but the cause of the separation is left unstated.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cranberries~salvation":{"haiku":"Drug addiction enslaves youth through false promises while parents watch helplessly; salvation exists within reach but addicts refuse the path, chasing the mirage instead.","opus":"A blunt anti-heroin plea aimed at kids and their sleepless parents, insisting the high is not what it promises. Salvation is offered as the free alternative to a drug that costs everything.","sonnet":"A direct address to drug users and anxious parents alike: heroin is not what it seems, and salvation — framed as freely available unlike the drug — is the counter-offer, delivered with urgency toward kids with \"heroin eyes.\"","src":"haiku"},
"the-cranberries~sunday":{"haiku":"Mystification replaces understanding as the speaker becomes enchanted without comprehension, unable to articulate love while being gradually consumed by it.","opus":"The disorienting rush of new love, feet off the ground, unable to find the words for how much she needs someone. Dolores O'Riordan captures infatuation as sweet confusion, tongue-tied and swept up before she can steady herself.","sonnet":"A person overwhelmed by attraction and confusion — unable to say the words, unable to follow through — watches someone leave while still mystified by the power they hold. The feeling is disorientation rather than loss: the speaker doesn't understand their own desire.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cranberries~twenty-one":{"haiku":"The speaker experiences finality—something or someone has taken their thoughts and will, leaving them to demand solitude and refusing repetition of past patterns.","opus":"A furious act of reclamation from someone whose thoughts and self were taken, now demanding to be left alone because she has found it all, the number twenty-one chanted like an age or a turning point where power returns. The repetition builds from wound to refusal. The Cranberries, the Irish band fronted by the late Dolores O'Riordan, channel her keening, unmistakable voice into this early, spiky insistence that whatever was stolen will not happen again.","sonnet":"A tersely stated refusal to engage further — someone took something vital away and the speaker has closed down entirely, repeating a single age like a door slamming shut. There is no argument, no explanation, just the flat declaration that it isn't going to happen anymore and a demand to be left alone.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cranberries~zombie":{"haiku":"Violence and war render people mindless and numb, fighting without question while civilians suffer. The song condemns ongoing conflict and societal indifference to human casualties.","opus":"Dolores O'Riordan wrote this Cranberries anthem after an IRA bombing, and its words mourn a lost child and grieving mothers amid the machinery of war. Tracing the bloodshed back to 1916, it condemns a hatred that occupies the mind like a possessing spirit, self-perpetuating and senseless. The wailing refrain separates the singer from the killing while lamenting that the conflict raging in people's minds refuses to stop.","sonnet":"A lament for the children killed in cycles of political violence rooted in Irish history — mothers grieve as the same war that has claimed lives since 1916 continues in the minds of those who perpetuate it. The image of a zombie names the dehumanizing trance of ideological killing.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cruel-knives~all-your-heroes-hate-you":{"haiku":"Dependency and betrayal; heroes revealed as false when examined at close range.","opus":"A skewering of a self-glorifying egotist who has grown enamored of the meticulous persona he constructed. The narrator watches a man dissolve into his own facade, tumbling down a spiral of self-delusion while everyone he looks up to quietly loathes him. It is a caution about the void that remains once the image devours the real person underneath.","sonnet":"A figure in love with their own disguise, feeding silence while still running their mouth, fading fast as memory hollows out the choices made. The speaker observes with a grin through gritted teeth — you're stuck on repeat, a thousand eyes could change your mind but you're too blind — all your heroes hate you as the diagnosis of someone who has built an identity entirely out of borrowed poses.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cruel-sea~the-honeymoon-is-over":{"haiku":"The speaker abruptly ends a relationship, rejecting their partner's presence and intimacy while vowing to replace their memory with another lover.","opus":"A dry, done-with-it dismissal, the narrator turning a lover out of his bed, his vehicle and his cooking and pledging to have ink redone with some other woman's name, owning that he was the sucker for staying so long. The joy is gone and there's nothing left worth saying. The Cruel Sea, an Australian outfit fronted by Tex Perkins, deliver it over their signature loping, bluesy surf-noir groove, all worn-out resignation rather than fire.","sonnet":"A breakup delivered as a lockout — every shared privilege revoked, every domestic detail stripped back to zero. The speaker is embarrassed by how thoroughly this person made a fool of him, plans to get the tattoo with her name changed, and knows the humiliation will follow him long after she's gone.","src":"haiku"},
"the-crystal-method~born-too-slow":{"haiku":"Someone warns against getting caught in drug addiction and recklessness, cautioning that slow reaction and poor choices lead to blindness and death.","opus":"A druggy cautionary sketch of a burnout waiting on his next fix, a man born a beat behind the world and sliding toward a bad end. The Crystal Method wrap the warning about picking up the wrong kind in a big-beat rock crossover, the imagery of fading lights and graying skin tracking a slow overdose.","sonnet":"A drug-soaked vignette about a man who's past his prime and past saving — waiting for last call, accepting the wrong kind of substance, a gun appearing, the lights burning down — all of it layered into a portrait of someone whose timing and choices have made them a casualty.","src":"haiku"},
"the-crystal-method~bound-too-long":{"haiku":"Constraints and limitation preventing freedom; moral ambiguity about whether the speaker is right or wrong.","src":"haiku"},
"the-crystal-method~name-of-the-game":{"haiku":"Transgressive imagery—toppling crowns, raping queens—mix with calls for crowd participation; the game's name is to mobilize and transgress.","opus":"Big-beat electronica built for the dancefloor, its menacing braggadocio and calls to the freaks less about meaning than working a crowd into a pumping, speaker-rattling frenzy.","sonnet":"A raw floor-filler call-to-arms with gestures toward street violence and domination mixed with pure rave energy — the \"name of the game\" is raw power and sonic force, calling all freaks to participate in a chaotic collective release.","src":"haiku"},
"the-crystal-method~over-it-feat-dia-frampton":{"haiku":"A person acknowledges their repeated betrayals and promises to change but recognizes their own inability to truly transform without becoming someone new.","opus":"An electronic breakup confession voiced from the wrong side of the fight, owning the fault yet still promising to change. The bruised imagery and the claim of being over it expose how self-serving apology can be when someone's already walking out.","sonnet":"A guilt-ridden admission of repeated failure in a relationship — the narrator acknowledges they caused harm, including violence, made empty promises to change, and has finally reached the end of being able to justify staying.","src":"haiku"},
"the-crystal-method~play-for-real-feat-the-heavy-dirtyphonics-remix":{"haiku":"A warning against casual games and emotional manipulation, demanding genuine commitment and honesty instead of false engagement.","opus":"A warning dressed as seduction: don't toy with someone who wants to be saved, not teased, because pushing them means proving you're truly out of your mind. The refrain draws a hard line between playing games and playing for real, pressure mounting toward a dare. The Crystal Method, American big-beat pioneers, hand it to Dirtyphonics for a bass-heavy remix, the vocal snarl from The Heavy giving it soul-rock grit.","sonnet":"A furious warning to someone who keeps testing boundaries — you'd have to be genuinely deranged to think you can keep toying with this person and get away with it. The speaker isn't threatening so much as marveling at the other's audacity, escalating from irritation to flat-out incredulity while refusing to play along.","src":"haiku"},
"the-crystal-method~weapons-of-mass-distortion":{"haiku":"A Russian-language reflection on the speaker's love for modern rock and roll as an embodiment of their generation. Despite the current state of the genre, the speaker identifies themselves as children of rock and roll, mourning lost companions.","opus":"A nostalgic tribute to rock 'n' roll, with the Russian narrator recalling the diamond days of playing carelessly with his best friends and declaring that rock 'n' roll is himself and his generation.","sonnet":"A Russian-language nostalgic reflection on rock and roll: the narrator celebrates the genre as a personal identity and recalls the joyful, improvised performances of youth with close friends he has since lost touch with, lamenting that those days are gone.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cure~a-forest-lyrics":{"haiku":"Someone chases phantom voices of a girl deeper into darkness, only to realize the girl was never real. They are trapped in endless pursuit toward nothing, lost and alone in an illusion.","opus":"A voice calls the narrator into the dark trees after a girl who was never there, the chase looping endlessly back on itself. The Cure's 1980 turn into gothic atmosphere makes obsession a forest you can only get more lost in — the cold repetition itself the shape of the despair Robert Smith would make his signature.","sonnet":"A man chases a girl's voice into a dark forest, compelled by something he knows is not real. He stops, already lost, and realizes with cold clarity that no girl was ever there. The pursuit keeps recurring — a compulsion with no object, running toward nothing again and again.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cure~a-strange-day":{"haiku":"Dissociation and dissolution permeate; the speaker drifts toward erasure, content in drowning.","opus":"A dreamlike drift toward some final dissolution, faces waiting for the end as the sky explodes and everything vanishes. Peak-era Cure atmosphere, beauty and apocalypse fused into a serene, hallucinatory letting-go.","sonnet":"A visionary, almost oceanic dissolution of the self — the speaker moves through a dreamlike landscape of sand, sea, a blind man, and drowning light, finally arriving at a moment where memory, sound, and identity collapse and disappear forever into a single strange day. The tone is not dread but a strange peace.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cure~all-cats-are-grey":{"haiku":"Darkness erodes distinction and identity until all things flatten into uniform gray, where texture and sensation become the only remaining proof of existence.","opus":"A submerged, near-catatonic vision of isolation, stone columns like begging men, no flags to guide him home, a single note ringing in a death cell. From Faith, it's depression rendered as a grey undifferentiated void where nothing distinguishes itself.","sonnet":"A nocturnal vision of dissolution and dread, where the speaker finds himself among stone columns like men, on dark lakes without landmarks, and in a death cell where a single note rings endlessly — all distinctions erased by darkness.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cure~just-like-heaven":{"haiku":"A person dreams of a magical connection with someone they love, but wakes alone to find that connection drowned and lost in grief.","opus":"An ecstatic recollection of a dizzying romance, the beloved playful and adoring, promising to run off together. The deeper reading turns on the devastating ending, where the narrator wakes alone by a sea that has swallowed the woman he loved, hinting the whole rapturous love was lost, or perhaps never more than a dream.","sonnet":"A love song that becomes a ghost story — beginning with a blissful, spinning coastal encounter with a woman who feels like a dream, then shifting to the narrator waking up alone to discover she's gone, as if she was never real, and that whatever she was has drowned inside him.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cure~last-dance":{"haiku":"Time warps the trajectory of love; what was permanent becomes provisional, and innocence ages into resignation.","opus":"Meeting an old love years later and grieving how time has flattened the brightness they once shared, the girl now a woman, the warmth cooled. Smith turns a final reunion into a study of how the past can't be re-entered, only mourned.","sonnet":"A reunion with a former love at what feels like a final meeting — the speaker mourns the distance between the girl she was and the woman she has become, and admits that even nostalgia cannot restore the intimacy they once shared.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cure~lovesong":{"haiku":"Unconditional devotion sustains across distance and time, with presence alone restoring wholeness and erasing all conflict.","opus":"A vow stripped to its plainest terms: another person restores you to wholeness, youth, freedom and home simply by being near. Smith wrote it as a wedding gift to his wife, and its power is in the refusal to embellish an unconditional promise.","sonnet":"A direct and unconditional declaration of love: being with this person restores feelings of home, wholeness, youth, freedom, and cleanness, and no matter the distance or duration or words spoken, that love will never waver.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cure~only-one":{"haiku":"Sexual desire and physical intimacy create the only genuine connection the narrator experiences with another person. The song celebrates bodily ecstasy as the truest form of communication and bonding.","opus":"A rare unabashedly carnal Cure song, an older Robert Smith reveling in physical desire and consuming devotion to one person. Lust and love collapsed into pure delighted appetite, growing more intense by the day.","sonnet":"Physical and erotic devotion catalogued body part by body part — head, heart, lips, hips, skin, bones — escalates from playful innuendo to genuine declaration: this is the only one they cry for, sigh for, die to squeeze, the daily encounter getting wetter, hazier, crazier each time.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cure~pictures-of-you":{"haiku":"Photographs become a substitute for presence, trapping the speaker in a cycle of memory that substitutes fragmented images for the irretrievable reality of connection.","opus":"Smith mourning a love by clinging to its photographs, until the images feel more real than the memory, grief for someone gone curdling into grief that he never found the words to hold her while he could.","sonnet":"A person who has lost someone they loved dwells so long among photographs that the images begin to feel more real than the relationship itself, replaying memories of tenderness and crisis while grieving that the right words never came to prevent the loss.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cure~the-funeral-party-live":{"haiku":"Two figures drift through memory toward oblivion; dance persists in the theater of death.","opus":"Two spectral figures and the ache of watching a life play out toward death, childhood dreams fading among fear and shadow. Early Cure gloom staging grief as a slow, wordless dance at a party for the dead.","sonnet":"Two silent figures move through a dreamlike funeral ritual — the speaker watches wordlessly as someone performs a story from an unknown past, dancing and crying among the lifeless memories of childhood.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cure~the-same-deep-water-as-you":{"haiku":"Romantic union dissolves into drowning; two selves submerge into oblivion, forever intertwined.","opus":"A pact of drowning together, love and death fused into one final embrace beneath the water. The centerpiece of Pornography's despair, it's about a bond so consuming that shared oblivion feels like the only togetherness left.","sonnet":"Two figures move together toward a shared drowning — an act that is simultaneously a farewell, a union, and a dissolution — as the speaker holds on to the image of his companion's smile right up to the moment of extinction.","src":"haiku"},
"the-cure~three":{"haiku":"Peak moments of love and beauty become unreachable memory, their perfection preserved only through constant internal effort against time's erosion.","opus":"A luminous memory of the happiest day and night with a lover, held onto against time because keeping that perfect moment alive is what keeps him alive. Rare Cure tenderness: nostalgia not as loss but as a lifeline.","sonnet":"An intensely personal evocation of two perfect moments — one summer, one winter — of love so complete they must be consciously preserved against time, the speaker returning to them repeatedly as the core memory keeping him alive.","src":"haiku"},
"the-curse-within~eternity":{"haiku":"Sacrificial redemption and transcendence become indistinguishable as the speaker accepts obliteration as the price for saving humanity, ascending through willing extinction.","opus":"A martyr offering himself as sacrifice to spare the world from soulless enslavement, welcoming death in hope of rebirth. Symphonic-metal heroism where bearing the weight of humanity means walking willingly into the grave.","sonnet":"A sacrificial figure who accepts death as the only way to protect humanity from soulless enslavement: offered as a living gift, they close their eyes, trust the unknown, and ascend from mortality — returning bone to sand — to be reborn into eternity.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dali-thundering-concept~god-is-dead":{"opus":"A brutal fable of tech-capitalist devotion, humankind killing off the divine and then forging machines, gifting them knowledge to power its paradise. Casting Ford, Tesla and Jack Ma as bogus prophets, the band lampoons efficiency and limitless plunder as the new faith. The cheer for triumphant humanity becomes an empty, grinding hymn to arrogance.","sonnet":"A Nietzschean industrial allegory about humanity replacing God with commerce and production — after God's death, man creates machines and blesses them with knowledge, turning the earth into a fuel source for an industrial heaven ordered by Ford, Tesla, and Jack Ma. The workers chant 'long live man' while digging, drilling, and draining the planet dry in worship of capital.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-dali-thundering-concept~the-myth-of-happiness":{"haiku":"Consumer culture masquerades as freedom while people remain hollow and distracted, chasing material fulfillment as a substitute for meaning. The speaker recognizes this cycle of debt and aspiration as false, yet remains trapped in it.","opus":"A skewering of consumerism as fake salvation, its narrator forever behind and empty, mistaking screens and credit cards for meaning while his ambitions melt mid-flight. The French technical-metal band aims the whole thing at capitalism, framing the pursuit of bought paradise as a race toward a summit the speaker already senses is a lie.","sonnet":"Consumer culture promises elevation — Olympus available at retail price, fulfillment delivered on demand — and delivers only a hollowness that luxury cannot fill. The narrator is a Daedalus figure whose wax melts not from flying too high but from mistaking purchasing power for transcendence.","src":"haiku"},
"the-darkness~i-believe-in-a-thing-called-love":{"haiku":"Someone's heart races at another's touch and presence; they believe in love's power and want to rock through the night together.","opus":"An exuberant, tongue-in-cheek declaration of overwhelming physical infatuation, built on driving-and-touching imagery and the sheer giddy conviction that this rush of desire really is love.","sonnet":"A straightforwardly ecstatic declaration of falling head-over-heels in love — the narrator is overwhelmed by desire and certainty that this connection is real, and wants it to last forever.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dead-love~if-you-say-so":{"haiku":"Someone spirals in psychological numbness and avoidance, compartmentalizing pain while watching a relationship decompose.","opus":"Australian trio The Dead Love wrap resignation in fuzzed grunge-pop, the narrator battering himself just to feel something as medication fails and numbness seeps through a tired, familiar exchange. A relationship is quietly rotting while neither party understands anything, and his limp, agreeable capitulation stands in for exhaustion he can no longer articulate.","sonnet":"Someone trapped in a numbing, recursive relationship endures the same circular conversations and emotional deadness on repeat. Sleeplessness, suppressed feelings, and self-censorship accumulate until the arrangement becomes a form of suffocation. Compliance overrides truth, and both parties decompose in the silence between them.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dead-love~young-dysfunctional":{"haiku":"Young lovers are caught in a toxic relationship where both partners are flawed; they cannot escape their dysfunction yet remain entangled.","opus":"A messy love between two people who bring out the worst in each other, self-loathing traded back and forth and mistaken for intimacy. It's the honesty of admitting you're both wrecks and staying anyway, memorable precisely because it never made sense.","sonnet":"A raw, mutually self-aware love between two people who are both messes — they know they're toxic, they know this is dysfunction, but they're committed to the wreckage together and can't quite figure out how to communicate what they actually need from each other.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dear-hunter~we-ve-got-a-score-to-settle":{"haiku":"Social punishment and accusation create compliance through fear; guilt becomes currency in a system designed to silence and control the vulnerable.","opus":"A menacing scene of coerced silence and mob justice, where the accused must dance to survive and truth counts for nothing against those who've decided he has blood on his hands. Part of Casey Crescenzo's narrative cycle, it's about power that manufactures guilt.","sonnet":"A person is coerced by a powerful, corrupt group who threaten and manipulate with blood on their hands, demanding compliance and performance of innocence under the weight of consequence — truth is irrelevant, and survival means dancing to their tune.","src":"haiku"},
"the-defect~broken-minds":{"haiku":"Fragmentation persists through isolation where memory dissolves and identity scatters across shadow. Two minds bind together in shared brokenness, finding home only in mutual decay.","opus":"Two damaged people binding their broken minds together in the dark, city lights and fading memories underscoring shared loneliness. Solace found not in healing but in matching each other's ruin.","sonnet":"Loneliness and psychological fragmentation played out against a city glowing in the dark — empty dreams, memories on replay, whispers that deceive — two broken minds binding together in the shadows, finding that shared darkness is at least something to hold onto.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devil-wears-prada~chemical":{"haiku":"Depression rationalized as biochemistry and the internalized hell of mental illness. The speaker attributes their paralysis and self-destruction to neurochemistry rather than external causes.","opus":"The Devil Wears Prada, Christian-scene metalcore veterans gone introspective, cast depression as pure brain chemistry, the narrator tallying his flaws and reassuring himself the emptiness is just biology. A persistent wound in mind and spirit lingers even as he notes his progress, an urge to demolish all he's made lurking underneath. The refrain offers thin solace by relabeling anguish as chemistry.","sonnet":"Depression is rationalised as brain chemistry while the reality of suffering remains stubbornly felt. Staring at the ceiling and counting imperfections, unable to speak honestly, the narrator has learned to dismiss the inner collapse as merely chemical — but the self-created hell and the hole in the chest persist regardless of how it is labelled.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devil-wears-prada~ignorance":{"haiku":"Advancement and ambition exact a hidden cost, leaving the speaker soulless and numb—aware that innocence once traded cannot be recovered.","opus":"Craving a lost innocence from inside a life that has everything yet feels like a tomb, admitting that willful ignorance would still beat the awareness that's crushing him.","sonnet":"A person trapped in emotional numbness and stagnation craves the pain and rawness of feeling again, even if that means embracing ignorance over the hollow awareness of a life that no longer feels like their own.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devil-wears-prada~lines-of-your-hands":{"haiku":"Pleads for connection and permanence through song while burning relationships and spiraling into eternal repetition. Seeks to mark someone forever.","opus":"A tender plea to a lover to stay in bed and escape the clocks and radio a little longer, the speaker begging to be written into her hands and heard fully before she leaves for the moon, all while confessing he is burning bridges he cannot rebuild. Forever déjà vu haunts the intimacy. The Devil Wears Prada, an Ohio metalcore band with Christian roots, soften here into something aching and domestic beneath the heaviness.","sonnet":"A desperate plea to be heard before someone departs for good — the speaker is burning through things they can't rebuild, caught in a loop of déjà vu, and the only thing they want is to become something permanent and intimate to the other person, like the map already written into their skin. The moon hangs over the house as deadline and witness.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devil-wears-prada~mammoth":{"haiku":"Accumulated regret and moral failure circle back upon the speaker, who cannot escape indecision or find solace in material comfort.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with the emptiness of wealth and one's own moral failure, wrestling with sin and grace as everything circles back, unable to feel anything but the numbness of having stopped caring.","sonnet":"A speaker in spiritual crisis circles between remorse and numbness, feeling wretched and hollowed out by the gap between their intentions and their impact, questioning whether there is any comfort or meaning left in money, beauty, or devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devil-wears-prada~ritual":{"haiku":"Society traps citizens in repetitive conformity where dreams die and misery spreads. Breaking the cycle requires abandoning hope itself.","opus":"An indictment of conformity as comforting addiction, where surrendering dreams to routine and letting others define you keeps everyone lost and miserable, yet the ritual is chosen anyway.","sonnet":"A song about the numbing comfort of repetitive routine as a trap — the narrator describes how conformity, lying to oneself, and cycling through the same bad decisions create a kind of hollow ritual that keeps people miserable yet unable to break free.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devil-wears-prada~sacrifice":{"opus":"The psychology of an abusive, gaslighting relationship where the manipulator plays victim and cuts the singer down, until he offers himself up as the scapegoat and sacrifice just to end the fighting.","sonnet":"A person in a manipulative relationship where the other continually plays the victim and bends reality recognizes the poison being fed to them but feels unable to escape — exhausted, gaslit, they offer themselves as the scapegoat just to end the fighting.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-devil-wears-prada~time":{"haiku":"Temporal paradox and opposing forces create psychological friction as the narrator struggles with simultaneity of change and stasis. The song frames time as divided reality that shatters coherent being.","opus":"The disorienting cruelty of time itself, racing like lightning yet crawling like a glacier, and the paralysis of holding a secret pain that recurs daily. Metalcore anxiety about opposites colliding into a kind of death.","sonnet":"Time moves like lightning and shifts like a glacier simultaneously — the speaker is barely holding on inside this irreconcilable opposition, the collision of extremes described as certain death, and the image of oil floating on water about to be set aflame crystallises a life lived at the point of impossible friction.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devil-wears-prada~watchtower":{"haiku":"Self-imposed isolation in a tower separates lovers, as the speaker chooses distance and separation over vulnerability despite love remaining.","opus":"The metalcore band examines a man who walled himself off on purpose, holed up in an isolation of his own making, choosing distance over a love he intentionally made hard to reach. Storm imagery outside mirrors his inner crisis, and only too late does he grasp what pushing that person away has cost him.","sonnet":"Choosing distance over connection and paying for it in isolation — the speaker climbed away from the street, away from warmth, sacrificing closeness out of a pride or fear neither fully named, and now looks east toward the sea from high up, wondering what was lost. Power stripped, nothing to lose, still in someone's heart from the wrong side of a wall.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devin-townsend-project~addicted":{"haiku":"Addiction feeds on the void it creates: dependency becomes identity, and the cycle persists because breaking it means confronting the emptiness beneath.","opus":"Addiction framed as the force that pins you to where you started, a wanting that never stops wanting, mistaking restraint for freedom. It aims the accusation outward and inward at once, half sermon, half manic release.","sonnet":"A satirical, semi-absurdist sermon about addiction — framed as both self-aware confession and mock spiritual lecture — arguing that humans are addicted to their own pain and ego, and the only path out is to surrender control like a mountain.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devin-townsend-project~awake":{"haiku":"Trapped in performance and framing, freedom elusive despite recognition of the cage.","opus":"Townsend's swirling anthem of self-acceptance and release, awake and exposed yet only human, insisting through the old stage metaphor that we've finally arrived home. The deconstruction it calls for is the cathartic command to tear the pretense down and break free.","sonnet":"An exuberant rallying cry for waking up and breaking free — acknowledging one's own captivity and human weakness, then pushing through toward liberation, with the repeated image of dying a little while waiting giving way to being finally, joyfully free.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devin-townsend-project~bend-it-like-bender":{"haiku":"Collective survival depends on resistance; the speaker mobilizes community against commodification.","opus":"A wry pep talk to someone kept around only to hold things down for others, urging them to stand their ground since none of us stays forever. The Bender interjections give it a manic, cartoonish edge over a message of fleeting solidarity.","sonnet":"A chaotic, semi-comedic affirmation of collective solidarity — people are being used and held down, but together they can hold their ground against whatever forces want them compliant, punctuated by absurdist humor and a reminder that none of this lasts forever so enjoy it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devin-townsend-project~hyperdrive":{"haiku":"Cold isolation interrupted by apology and hope for new connection amid loneliness.","opus":"A cold, shimmering longing to sail away and find a new path, apology and loneliness braided through the chill of night. The bright pop surface carries a quiet ache for love and escape.","sonnet":"A sparse, emotionally ambivalent song about loneliness and the need to move on — in the cold of night the narrator is sorry, tries to stay optimistic (every day's a new day), and yearns for love; the ending \"she loves me!\" lands like a small, fragile surprise.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devin-townsend-project~in-ah":{"haiku":"Love and light convergence, accepting mystery without needing rational understanding.","opus":"A blissed-out surrender to love beyond comprehension, the narrator content that he doesn't understand the force pulling everything together, only that it feels right. Devotion as glad, uncomprehending awe.","sonnet":"A song about being overwhelmed by love to the point where rational understanding becomes irrelevant — the narrator surrenders to a deep romantic bond, insisting that their inability to explain or comprehend the feeling is no reason to doubt its validity. The emotional core is joyful bewilderment: something profound is happening between two people, and the mystery itself is what makes it feel right.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devin-townsend-project~numbered":{"haiku":"Numbness and universal anonymity, waiting for sign while realizing truth and rightness never arrived.","opus":"A meditation on collective numbness and mortality, humanity waiting for a sign while time runs out, comforted only that we're not alone. The plea to be made one against the certainty that we all die.","sonnet":"About collective human numbness and the shared hunger for meaning — we are all counted, all waiting for a sign, all searching for something to confirm we aren't alone. The song holds desperation and hope in tension: we tried, we don't know if we were right, but we're still searching.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devin-townsend-project~resolve":{"haiku":"Determination and will triumph over adversity, with the speaker rising and refusing surrender through sheer force of intention.","opus":"A call to turn the world inward and focus the mind, seeking resolve as an ocean returning to the sea. The chanted refusal to go, we rise, makes it an anthem of stubborn spiritual reintegration.","sonnet":"A willed affirmation of perseverance — the ocean always returns to the sea, and the narrator wants the resolve to receive whatever they are becoming; the song is less about a specific situation than about mustering the inner force to refuse to give up.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devin-townsend-project~supercrush":{"haiku":"Autonomy and dissolution intertwine as the speaker resists salvation itself, preferring agency over deliverance even at the cost of chaos.","opus":"A refusal to chase salvation or control, choosing instead to lose it and learn the slow way, hardening into dissent against heroes who abandoned their followers to die.","sonnet":"About surrendering to the process of growth rather than trying to control or save yourself — the speaker doesn't want to lose control but acknowledges it takes a lifetime to learn, and that heroes who were supposed to protect you ultimately abandoned you.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devin-townsend-project~the-way-home":{"haiku":"Exhaustion dims the light inward: wasted time on surface living leaves only the hollow recognition of distance from authentic direction.","opus":"Weary reckoning with time wasted and a life's performance winding down, the hollowness after the show ends. Townsend finds quiet resolve to keep going home even as he mourns the daylight he squandered.","sonnet":"A song about arriving at the end of something and recognizing what was wasted — the show is over, the lights are hollow, time spent chasing daylight is gone, and the only thing left is finding the way home with what remains.","src":"haiku"},
"the-devin-townsend-project~universe-in-a-ball":{"haiku":"Introspective wonder and fantastical journeys inward reveal universes contained within and around us, where wandering and home intertwine.","opus":"A cosmic pep-talk that finds a whole universe inside every person, urging you to locate your fantasies and fight them rather than flee. Home turns out to be everyone around, the wandering itself the way back, joy in dissolving the boundary between self and everything.","sonnet":"About finding an entire cosmos within yourself and within other people — fantasies are worth fighting for, home is everywhere and in everyone, and the wandering always leads back; the song is expansive and slightly chaotic in its sense of universal belonging.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~apologies-not-included":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts stagnation and selfish detachment, searching for meaning in futile days while frustrated by another's failure to seek peace or acknowledge shared responsibility.","opus":"A downward spiral of burnout and self-loathing, the narrator squandering his energy in a relentless internal assault, twisting himself toward collapse while flatly refusing any remorse, though a faint spark of hope endures. The Dillinger Escape Plan pioneered mathcore, and their jagged, unhinged aggression here scores lyrics that circle self-destruction and a stubborn refusal to apologize.","sonnet":"Exhaustion and purposelessness running up against someone else's self-destruction, with the speaker refusing to absorb the guilt. Late nights blur into inner heat, years wash over like waves, and the only response to being blamed for another person's collapse is a flat refusal: not sorry, never asked you to look, and still — faintly — hoping something remains.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~black-bubblegum":{"haiku":"Confronts someone seeking shallow attention and temporary fame, warning them that their obsession with fleeting recognition will ultimately lead to regret.","opus":"A bitter address to a vain, attention-hungry person who thinks they're a special queen, with the narrator claiming he sees through the act, knows everything, and pairs their fleeting fame with regret.","sonnet":"A narrator turns on someone they once were captivated by, accusing her of self-delusion, attention-seeking, and a compulsion for notoriety — insisting he saw through the facade all along and that she is destined for regret.","src":"haiku","fable":"The most candied melody Dillinger ever built is a delivery system for its nastiest words, and that gap is the design: a radio hook wrapped around a verdict of shame. The song runs on a storybook reversal, where she imagines herself royalty drunk on notice while he keeps casting himself as the predator she mistook for an admirer. The sneer curdles at the bridge, where he admits he really was paralyzed by her before deciding it was all a fraud; the confession leaks genuine damage under a mask of omniscience. His boast of total knowledge sours into a claim of ownership, a jilted man laundering hurt into control, so the contempt reads less like insight than a wound rehearsing its superiority. The final refrain loops until the charge stops being about her and becomes the noise of someone unable to quit chewing a grudge past all flavor, which is what the title names.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"a jilted man laundering hurt into contempt past all flavor","built":"the catchiest Dillinger melody is the delivery system for the nastiest verdict"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~chinese-whispers":{"haiku":"A meditation on mortality and the futility of human contribution, exploring how everything decays, ambitions dissolve, and broken dreams become chemical weapons against the self.","opus":"A merciless takedown of a doomed narcissist awaiting his end, his arrogance and hollow vows laid bare, everything he treasures declared worthless, his shattered ambitions repurposed into poison. The speaker coldly notes it was only sport to him, never to the one addressing him. The Dillinger Escape Plan, mathcore pioneers, propel this contempt through whiplash tempos, a calculated demolition of a man watching his ruin arrive.","sonnet":"A eulogy for someone who mistook ego for permanence — lined up for an execution they saw coming and ignored, the person dies on their deathbed with no visitors, having spent everything clinging to things that never lasted. The speaker watches without pity, noting that broken dreams are the only thing left to make weapons from.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~crossburner":{"haiku":"One person confronts another who refuses to listen and remains fundamentally unreal or unreachable. Distance, communication failure, and the futility of trying to connect drive the speaker's frustration.","opus":"Mathcore pioneers The Dillinger Escape Plan aim chaos at disbelief over a person who turns out to be hollow, someone the narrator gripped by the neck only to find nothing solid there. He urges the hidden passenger to steer the vessel back toward a better place, apologizing for a gap he half-resents. The refrain locks onto a shared, corrosive failure to hear one another, the bond dissolving into an image of someone always at a distance.","sonnet":"A fractured relationship with someone who never felt fully real — seized, tested, and found hollow — leaves the narrator uncertain about his own culpability. The repeated instruction to turn the boat around is as much self-address as it is dismissal, a farewell muddled by distance and unresolved grief.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~crystal-morning":{"haiku":"Crowds celebrate decadence and waste while destruction looms inevitably. The song presents apocalypse as vengeance, suggesting the world's end is justice for collective failures and callousness.","opus":"The mathcore band channels its signature chaos into an apocalyptic incitement, deriding the masses reveling in their own ruin and insisting it's the whole world, not any one person, that's about to perish. Framing devastation as righteous payback, the narrator urges the reckoning to begin at once, the fractured riffing mirroring a mind primed for destruction.","sonnet":"A vision of collective moral rot — crowds worshipping their own degradation, lives spent asleep in disgrace — met with a call to let it all burn. The speaker frames the coming destruction not as punishment but as release: it isn't the individual who dies, only the corrupt world that ends, so load up and let it start now.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~dead-as-history": {"haiku":"Two people burn together like moths to light while time presses behind them; they build walls and burn bridges deeper into a cycle without escape.","opus":"Amid Dillinger's chaos, a rare moment of raw regret over a doomed connection, moths to a flame that burns them both, clinging to something fleeting because holding on is the only proof they still feel alive.","sonnet":"A song about the wreckage left by a relationship both parties burned through — walls were built, bridges were burned, and the tragedy is shared but not unique; the narrator holds on to the memory knowing it will never be long enough.","src":"haiku","fable":"A confession of mutual harm opens on an image-system of self-immolation — lovers as moths burning toward the light, the speaker pressing forward while his own vision smolders — where attraction and self-destruction are the same motion and no payback ever settles the account. The mid-song turn is the hinge: the private wound insists it is not merely a private wound, that this grief belongs to something larger, so the couple's built walls and burned bridges become a way of measuring the whole species' talent for feeling safest exactly as it digs itself deeper. It ends by handing the argument to time, the pursuer always just behind, the self a lamb already under the gun; the vow to clutch this moment permanently is undercut in the same breath by the admission that even permanence expires too fast to matter, which is the title's real meaning — the instant you grip it, it is already dead as history. Against Puciato's serrated delivery on Ire Works, the near-melodic composure here reads less as relief than as the calm of someone who has stopped resisting the drop.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","politics & society"],"means":"the private wound insists it measures the whole species' capacity for harm","built":"near-melodic composure on a serrated record reads as calm of someone done resisting the drop"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~dissociation":{"haiku":"The speaker leaves someone to die alone rather than stay in a relationship they believe confines them, reframing abandonment as freedom.","opus":"Dillinger's title track and farewell trades mathcore violence for aching clarity, staging a breakup in a parked car. The narrator warns his partner not to mistake being released for being thrown away, insisting he left for his own survival. He frames a lonely end as preferable to what the relationship offered, a strange and unbearable loss.","sonnet":"A person reflects on leaving a relationship and trying — unsuccessfully — to convince himself the decision was purely self-serving. The knowledge that staying was impossible sits alongside the fear of dying alone, and the parting is remembered as a strange, unnameable kind of loss rather than liberation.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~endless-endings":{"haiku":"Loops of departure and death repeat; the speaker questions when escape became impossible.","opus":"Frayed nerves rattle the heavens as two people push forward depleted, asking how quickly each sensed they were already finished. Dillinger Escape Plan render a disintegrating relationship as ceaseless conclusions, both bolting for the door together, wishes half-retracted, a union that might have expired ages back yet keeps reforming into the identical mirage.","sonnet":"Two people circle the moment their relationship died, each unable to determine when the other knew it was over. Mutual paralysis and failed exits pile up — everyone around them saw the end coming long before they did, and they were left running on empty long past the point of no return.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~farewell-mona-lisa":{"haiku":"Humanity is inherently monstrous and murderous; trust is futile because everyone is capable of betrayal and destruction.","opus":"A nihilistic disavowal of hope and faith set in a numb, cell-like existence where people are bred into fixed roles like livestock. Beneath that, the song rips off the mask to expose humanity as violent and corrupt to the core, cautioning listeners never to expect anything beyond the void they were made for.","sonnet":"A nihilistic song about the futility of human aspiration — society conditions people never to exceed their destined role, feelings are stripped away until only echoes remain, and the narrator turns the indictment inward, announcing that humans are murderers, liars, rapists, and thieves who should never be trusted.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~fix-your-face": {"haiku":"A vengeful persona judges a delusional person who harms others for amusement and will never learn compassion. The narrator promises to pursue them forever and watch them face the consequences of their cruelty.","opus":"A venomous address to a deluded, destructive woman, promising relentless pursuit, no reconciliation, and that she will pay the price for what she has done.","sonnet":"The narrator confronts someone he considers deluded and self-destructive — a \"queen of delusion\" who starts fires she cannot control — telling her there is no escape, no comeback, and that she will always be a presence he cannot fully shake.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song of contempt that curdles into a promise never to leave. The speaker addresses a woman he casts as vain and delusional — someone who set a fire she can't put out and now burns in it — and for the first two movements the register is pure diagnosis, the coroner standing over a body he insists deserves it. Then Puciato's threat turns the ugliness inside out: the funeral becomes not her end but the mourners' celebration, and the real menace is that death would be a mercy this speaker refuses to grant. The final movement drops the moral cover entirely and names the thing underneath the contempt as obsession — he will be the figure at the edge of every room she ever enters, the quarry he has marked can never once stop running, no version of this where they are done. The song asks to be heard in the gap between the two masks: the man who calls her deluded is the one who cannot let go, and the closing debt she must pay for her is the tell that this was never justice, only a wound dressed up as a verdict.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","love & desire","surveillance & control"],"means":"the man diagnosing her delusion is the one who cannot let go","built":"the threat refuses her death because that would be mercy rather than punishment"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~gold-teeth-on-a-bum":{"haiku":"A hypocrite judges the poor while playing the hero; the speaker promises retribution for false pity and hidden cruelty.","opus":"A leper and a wild pretty boy are taunted by a narrator who claims he knows how to fix them, demanding teeth and eyes be pulled out and stared at. Dillinger's early spasms of Calculating Infinity chaos twist mockery of the outcast into a broken plea, the tormentor finally begging to be helped and saved so he might believe.","sonnet":"A critique of a charismatic, empty figure who performs victimhood and blessed status while deflecting blame and wearing a disguise of feigned innocence. The speaker, wise to the manipulation, offers to fix him but knows real salvation requires stripping away the fraudulent performance of goodness.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~good-neighbor":{"haiku":"A person critiques a neighbor who appears happy but controls others and hides behind facades. The song warns that unfiltered information access leads to informational suicide and lost long-term memory.","opus":"A scathing takedown of digital-age numbness, mocking a hollow person going through the motions while attention and memory dissolve, and framing constant free connectivity as a kind of slow death by information overload. The Dillinger Escape Plan enact that overload musically, their jagged mathcore lurches embodying the very disorientation the words condemn.","sonnet":"A portrait of someone cheerfully surrendering their inner life to constant connectivity — smiling, nodding, whistling the same tune while their long-term memory gets pushed aside by an endless social feed. The speaker refuses to evaporate into that immediacy, watching others gorge themselves on information and calling it what it is: a slow suicide by distraction.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~hero-of-the-soviet-union":{"haiku":"Accusation of hidden corruption: the speaker drowns a lover, exposing hypocrisy beneath claims of honesty and dignity—all humans are scum hiding filth beneath false devotion.","opus":"Dillinger open on a drowned lover, then round on a sanctimonious figure they sarcastically crown as the pride of the ranks, blameless and above suspicion. The New Jersey mathcore band's chaos sharpens the mockery, peeling away the facade to expose the grime this person spreads everywhere, a portrait of hypocrisy masquerading as honor and devotion.","sonnet":"A figure who performs righteousness and dignity is stripped bare as corrupt and hypocritical. The opening image of drowning a lover frames everything that follows — the self-proclaimed hero stinks of deception rather than truth, and the indictment is total and merciless.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~honeysuckle":{"haiku":"A raw exploration of physical deterioration, mental erasure of past relationships, and the struggle between sleep and wakefulness as metaphors for emotional survival and healing.","opus":"A suffocating breakdown in which the speaker lies half-certain he's expiring, his lungs waterlogged, straining and failing to blot out a face he can't release, clawing at his own hurt for any relief. Fury has decayed into exhausted despair. The Dillinger Escape Plan pour their mathcore violence into this account of heartbreak experienced as literal sickness, the erasure never fully working.","sonnet":"A body failing and a mind that can't erase the person it blames — lungs filling with fluid, strength gone, the speaker swings between rage and numbness and the impossible task of erasing a face they can still see everywhere: in rain that doesn't fall, in wind that blows the wrong way, in the taste of air. Clawing at the wound doesn't bring light, just more wound.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~horse-hunter": {"haiku":"A fractured narrative describes intoxication, betrayal, and moral decay among companions, with imagery of violence and commodified human desire.","opus":"The mathcore band laces its whiplash aggression with venomous seduction, staging a party gone rancid where a drunk haze strips away pretense and the narrator crowns his target a false idol of the flesh. Naming money as the object of worship, the track dissolves into a chanted plea to surrender completely and be taken all the way.","sonnet":"A secret exposed at a social gathering unravels a false friendship while something more genuine is offered in its wreckage. Commerce and appetite have colonized every relationship, but amid the condemnation a hand is extended — sit with me, come down — and the invitation is fragile and real.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seduction that keeps catching its target mid-lie — secrets falling away, delusion slipping — until the come-on flips into indictment: the beloved reframed as a creature of commerce and appetite, tended by a swarm of worshippers that gnaw the shrine while they kneel at it, a body everyone has already tasted. The song lives in that pivot from tenderness to disgust, the caress and the accusation delivered in the same breath, so you can't tell whether the singer is the lover or the parasite feeding on the same rot. Ire Works being the fractured, post-lineup-collapse Dillinger record, the structure earns its swerve into Brent Hinds' Mastodon drawl for the closing descent, where the two voices trade the language of surrender and fire — lie down, give in, be taken past the last threshold — until seduction and self-immolation become the same act. The horse hunter, then, is whoever runs down the thing they claim to adore.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the caress and the accusation delivered in the same breath until indistinguishable","built":"a guest vocalist's drawl takes the closing descent where seduction meets self-immolation"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~i-wouldn-t-if-you-didn-t":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts how people build defensive walls around themselves and teach that suffering equals love, questioning whether consequence-free action would reveal true moral choice.","opus":"A bitter reckoning with belief and the walls people build from fear, the narrator asking whether, faced with divinity or freed from consequence, anyone stays honest, then rejecting a doctrine that teaches suffering is love by finally insisting it is not. The Dillinger Escape Plan set this philosophical wrestling against their volatile mathcore attack, the closing reversal landing like the one steady thought amid deliberate chaos.","sonnet":"A confrontation with religious belief that uses its own logic against it — if there were no consequences, would you still stay inside the lines? The speaker tears down the idea that suffering is the same as love, calling out sadistic doctrine dressed up as devotion, and insists that walls built to shield against fear only trap the people building them.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~limerent-death":{"haiku":"Mistakes and lies entrap someone in a facade; the narrator questions whether anyone would notice their absence and obsesses over whether suffering alone is enough.","opus":"Mathcore anatomy of limerence curdled into contempt, obsessing over someone whose guarded facade hides real rot. The frantic, ironic checking-in on whether they're fine masks devastation at giving everything to a person who was never really there.","sonnet":"About the collapse of a total emotional investment — the narrator gave everything to someone who hid behind a performed self, and the song is the wreckage of discovering the connection was built on nothing real.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~low-feels-blvd":{"haiku":"The speaker grieves lost moments and a past relationship, cycling through nostalgia and acceptance of loss, wondering if their best memories were a reality or invention.","opus":"A mournful reverie in which the narrator sits by himself replaying an early encounter with a woman, unable to shake a torment that turns out to have originated within him all the time, longing for a fleeting instant that stays out of reach. The mathcore band dials back the aggression here for a wistful, near-tender ache and thoughts of correspondence never delivered.","sonnet":"Grief for a past relationship that keeps phasing in and out of reality — some days it's gone entirely, other days it's right here. The speaker sits with half-written letters, memories of a first glance across a table, and the question of whether the hell was always inside rather than something that arrived with the relationship.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~lurch": {"haiku":"An obsessed admirer offers everything but freezes the beloved in time, desiring them completely while barring them from living freely.","opus":"A predatory, obsessive fixation rendered as chaos, the narrator possessing and preserving an idealized object of desire. It's an unsettling study of control disguised as comfort, worship curdling into something possessive and sick.","sonnet":"A predator's obsessive fixation on a beautiful, fragile young woman — offering her everything, wanting to preserve her, telling her there's no point crying when she's above everyone, the song maps the psychology of a possessive stalker.","src":"haiku","fable":"A fan's worship of a starlet curdles, verse by verse, into something predatory: the speaker opens by pledging to lay every want and need at her feet, the language of a devotee, then reveals he means to freeze her in a perfect unchanging state — an adoration that can only be satisfied by stopping her. What sounds like protective tenderness (an outstretched arm at the door, a promise to steady her the moment she stumbles) is the possessive logic of a collector. Puciato lets the pet names and cooing endearments do the work of the mask while the fresh-skin, intoxicating-smell imagery drifts steadily toward the mortuary. Ire Works is Dillinger at their most seductive and unstable, and the horror here is structural: the song grooms you with the vocabulary of love and only late reveals that the beloved above them all, the one who won't be perfect, has been idolized into a thing to be preserved rather than a person to be known.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","surveillance & control","violence & murder"],"means":"devotion that can only be satisfied by stopping the beloved entirely","built":"pet names and endearments mask the song while imagery drifts toward the mortuary"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~magic-that-i-held-you-prisoner":{"haiku":"Two people fall together into darkness but find grace and shine within; one refuses to burn for anyone else anymore.","opus":"Two people fall together and the narrator glimpses infinite grace inside the other, urging them to drop their shield and move toward the light rather than burn for anyone. Dillinger soften their fury into something almost tender here, insisting on a self beneath the self and a true reflection worth catching before it is consumed.","sonnet":"An address to someone who has been carrying others' burdens as if they were obligated to burn for them. The speaker insists they hold unrecognized grace and do not need to destroy themselves for anyone else. Falling together becomes a moment of mutual rescue, not condemnation.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~milk-lizard":{"haiku":"Toxic sexual obsession and predatory desire masked as love.","opus":"A predatory, sadomasochistic sexual encounter with a wounded woman, tangled in mutual manipulation and inevitable estrangement.","sonnet":"A toxic sexual attraction to a manipulative woman who takes from the narrator while offering only pain in return.","src":"haiku","fable":"Puciato loads the verses with reptile swagger — a tongue dragging the dirt, teeth offered as jewelry, salt bartered for something sweet — posing the woman as prey and the encounter as a cold transaction. Yet the leer rots from inside: the bridge reframes her as a sickness dressed in the look of someone begging to be rescued, blurring hunter and lure past telling apart. When the animal costume slips in the chorus, what surfaces is a hollow man, a walking query nobody answered, bound to her only by shared hunger. So the outro's chanted forever reads as a sentence, not a vow: reptile reflex and empty man are one creature, and the strut only masked an appetite with no floor.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"reptile swagger masking a hollow man whose appetite has no floor","built":"the chorus strips the animal costume to reveal the strut was always empty"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~mouth-of-ghosts": {"haiku":"Trust dissolves as a partner reveals themselves to be hollow and meaningless; goodbye comes when lies finally overtake the relationship.","opus":"A hushed, final farewell as the last of the trust drains away and everything once shared loses its meaning, the narrator seeing his partner as all words and no feeling, all gestures signifying nothing, reaching for a connection already shut off. The Dillinger Escape Plan, usually known for mathcore chaos, restrain themselves into something spacious and mournful, letting the breakup unfold with unusual stillness.","sonnet":"A final severance — trust gone, words dissolved, goodbye with nothing left to resolve. The speaker looks back and sees that the other person was always performing emotion without possessing it, acting without meaning anything, and walking through the relationship in fear while reaching for hands that were already closed against them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A dissolution sung as a farewell with nothing left to settle, this closes Ire Works by draining the record's violence into something colder and more resigned: trust doesn't shatter here, it runs out, and the speaker admits the end was legible the whole time. The betrayal deepens into a portrait of hollowness — the other person reduced to a mouth moving with no pulse behind the words, motion that carries no weight — and the final turn is bleaker still, letting that empty figure become the frightened one, groping toward palms that have already curled shut. Puciato singing this straight over the album's jazz-tinged comedown lets the anger cool into grief, so the last accusation lands as pity rather than rage.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"trust not shattered but quietly run to nothing","built":"anger cooled into pity by the jazz-tinged arrangement carrying it"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~nong-eye-gong": {"haiku":"The singer denounces a corrupter and destroyer of art forms who spreads deceit, describing prideful downfall and the cycle of deception and suffering.","opus":"A venom-soaked portrait of a con artist and destroyer, a shapeshifter who ruins art and traffics in lies, his emptiness masked by triumph until his own arrogance topples him. There is a bitter self-aware note about apologizing yet being unable to start over. The Dillinger Escape Plan spit it out in a brief, chaotic mathcore burst.","sonnet":"A portrait of a corruptor who wins while destroying everything — shapeshifter, butcher of art, peddler of deceit, someone who turned time into a weapon and the past into wreckage. The people around them are smart enough to apologize but not smart enough to start fresh, dancing in the illusion of death while the corruptor laughs.","src":"haiku","fable":"Almost the whole song is an accusation built by stacking epithets onto one figure — corrupter, shapeshifter, butcher, peddler — a litany that indicts a hollow charmer whose voice amounts to nothing and whose gift to the trusting is poisoned sweets. But the sneer curdles when the speaker folds himself into the crowd: the turn admits we are clever enough to apologize and never clever enough to actually change, so the real target stops being the villain and becomes the self-flattering pride that keeps walking toward its own fall. In DEP's theatrical, spat-out register, the piling of names works less as description than as a verdict rehearsed until it snaps back on the one delivering it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"an indictment that snaps back onto the one delivering it","built":"the litany of epithets collapses into self-accusation at the turn"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~nothing-s-funny":{"haiku":"Jokes and laughter eventually lose their edge; the cosmic punchline becomes that nothing is funny and the joke lands on the speaker's own suffering.","opus":"Mathcore's most explosive act corners a sneering figure whose scruples wither and spirit rots, every existence flattened to a mere death in the speaker's view. The grim insistence that humor has drained away turns bleaker still when the mockery boomerangs, the target's viciousness revealed as his own kind. Chaotic and disorienting by design, the lyric hauls scorn into a mirror of mutual madness.","sonnet":"Nihilism and existential exhaustion strip the last residue of humour from living. A pale, self-certain figure collapses inward as conscience and vision fail; the narrator watches degradation ripple outward until the joke — initially directed at everyone else — doubles back and lands squarely on its teller.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~nothing-to-forget":{"haiku":"Religious or parental control created trauma and shame from birth; the speaker survives by rejecting all external guidance.","opus":"A wrenching account of childhood trauma, guilt by birth and a terror seeded deep by a figure whose fingers choked and covered the narrator's eyes. The repeated plea to be left alone gives way to a hard-won turn, climbing out of others' graves to finally light his own way.","sonnet":"Childhood trauma at the hands of someone who presented as a guardian — the violation was built into the narrator from birth, left them with years they can barely remember, and now they're fighting to reclaim autonomous existence and stop needing anyone.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~one-of-us-is-the-killer":{"haiku":"Fatal intimacy between two people where betrayal ensures mutual destruction; one cannot survive the wound the other has inflicted.","opus":"A love turned lethal, an intoxicating, elusive partner rendered as shadow and storm, the relationship reduced to a duel neither party can win. Dillinger's mathcore chaos frames doomed obsession where survival and killing collapse into the same act.","sonnet":"A relationship framed as mutual destruction — a hypnotic woman moves through the narrator's life like a storm, and the intimacy between them ends in a death-match logic: the arrow has been loosed, one of them must die, but the killer won't survive either.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~paranoia-shields":{"haiku":"Mutual obsession between adversaries that outlasts reason: two people are locked in symbiotic torment where neither can escape, bound by manifested fantasy and willing to burn together at the end.","opus":"Dillinger circle a fixated bond defined by denial, the narrator a phantom in a hollow recess conjuring a lover in feverish reverie whom he'd spare from suffering and never expend himself upon. The mathcore band channel their chaos into something intimate and macabre, mortality binding the pair as he pledges to stay beside them at the end.","sonnet":"Two people orbit each other in a relationship that is toxic, imagined, and inescapable all at once. The narrator cannot fully rid himself of someone he despises and desires in equal measure, and by the end the antipathy inverts — there is a hell he would burden with this person, a fire worth wasting.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~parasitic-twins":{"haiku":"A song about emotional collapse and abandonment, where withdrawal of love destroys everything built internally and leaves the speaker bleeding from betrayal.","opus":"An internal collapse as a lover's exit torches everything the speaker constructed, his pulse racing unnaturally, needing even her deceptions to stay standing. He insists he'll never admit he misses her, then breaks down completely once love is handed off and abandoned. The Dillinger Escape Plan bend their mathcore toward something more melodic and raw, tracking devotion withdrawn without warning.","sonnet":"Collapse from the inside out when someone withdraws their love — the speaker built everything on that foundation and watched it burn when it was taken away, and though they refuse to ever admit missing the person, they bleed like rain falling on skin. The betrayal is named as earned, the grief is named as real, and both are held at once without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~party-smasher": {"haiku":"Two people remain trapped in a destructive dynamic they cannot exit, locked together in a role-play neither can escape or understand.","opus":"A poisonous entanglement two people can't escape, staged as a play with no exit, each accusing the other of never really knowing them. The chaos underscores a relationship that's dead but still binding, forever entwined in mutual contempt.","sonnet":"About a relationship — possibly romantic, possibly creative — that neither person can escape despite wanting to, where they are permanently entangled in a play they didn't write and can't leave, and the narrator declares the other person dead to them while knowing the bond won't break.","src":"haiku","fable":"A scorched kiss-off to someone the speaker once believed in and now regards as a wasted design — a person built from his own fantasy who never actually knew him. The central image is theatrical: the two are cast together in a play neither can walk out of, so contempt and entanglement become the same condition, and the promise of starting over someday only proves how deluded the other has become. The turn lands when the mirror is handed over — the accusation of madness folds back, asking the target to see their own shadow, the version of themselves the speaker can no longer separate from his own reflection.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss","artifice & performance"],"means":"contempt and entanglement made the same condition in a play neither can exit","built":"the accusation of madness folds back as a mirror handed to the target"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~prancer":{"haiku":"Mirrors of fear and self-betrayal: the speaker confronts doppelgängers and ghosts wearing their skin, discovering that forward movement and memory collapse into the same trap.","opus":"From their final album, Dillinger stage a dissociative delirium in which the narrator, trapped between sleep and waking, hurts himself trying to feel whether he's even alive amid warped, frightened reflections. The mathcore band's controlled violence drives a paranoia of sealed rooms and phantoms in his own likeness, as he dares the listener, and himself, to try in vain to deny it all.","sonnet":"A fractured consciousness examines its own existence — never truly dead, never fully awake — and the self fragments into mirrors, ghosts, and demons that wear the same skin. The song's logic loops and refuses resolution, ending in defiant contempt for anyone who would deny the reality of the interior chaos.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~room-full-of-eyes":{"haiku":"Hidden observers create judgment and surveillance that prevents authentic living and satisfaction. The speaker describes feeling trapped in visibility and unable to escape collective scrutiny.","opus":"The Dillinger Escape Plan pair aggression with uneasy self-examination, a narrator owning a blunder and feeling exposed with no privacy left. He circles the way craving never rests, forever unsatisfied, feeding the same discontent he hates. Desire returns despite his resolve, framed as something you cut back only to watch it grow again.","sonnet":"Accountability and desire collide in a space where there is no hiding — every action observed, every mistake owned, and yet lust and dissatisfaction keep regenerating regardless. Beneath the exposure runs a private current of longing that refuses to die even when everything else is laid bare.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~surrogate":{"haiku":"An expression of being trapped in cyclical suffering, emotionally detached to protect others at the cost of oneself, while harboring knowledge that brings only torture.","opus":"An unbearable loop of reliving one moment while life carries on around him, the narrator emotionally guarded and dreading the outcome, until he finally refuses to prop up someone else's sickness or shoulder their tragedy. The Dillinger Escape Plan drive this breaking point through frantic mathcore, a hard line drawn purely to survive.","sonnet":"Stuck in a loop that is partly self-imposed and partly inherited — reliving something over and over while the world moves on, the speaker is detached to protect others, carrying secrets programmed into them, and terrified of how it ends. The refusal to be a crutch for someone else's tragedy is hard-won, but being alone in a crowded room while screaming inside is the price.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~symptom-of-terminal-illness":{"haiku":"Childhood terror and existential fear persist into adulthood; darkness cannot be illuminated by others' light.","opus":"A rare hushed track from a band known for pure chaos, built as a terrified nighttime confession. The narrator, jolted awake by dreams that everything he cares about is vanishing, addresses the people he has loved and lost, admitting he no longer trusts himself and hinting at a fatal choice made out of self-hatred. The dread here is emotional rather than physical.","sonnet":"A person consumed by fear of loss and mortality confesses it to those they loved and failed. Childhood terrors persist into adulthood as waking panic; the darkness must stay off because light means confronting an inability to sustain closeness — and the final admission is that self-loathing, not indifference, made staying impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~the-threat-posed-by-nuclear-weapons":{"haiku":"A person mourns losing someone they loved and recognizes their own role in the destruction. They grapple with predatory human nature and the cyclical pattern of repeating mistakes and reliving pain.","opus":"The Dillinger Escape Plan pair cosmic dread with a poisonous, codependent bond, a narrator addressing a silent heaven and drawn toward oblivion, yet kept alive by his tie to another. He warns that he's ruinous and urges her to flee, even as they cling together and choose to destroy what they built rather than repeat old mistakes.","sonnet":"Two people who recognized something essential in each other find that recognition has curdled into mutual destruction — the bond that kept one alive now functions as a toxin. The closing image of burning what they made together suggests the only escape from a parasitic entanglement is obliteration.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~understanding-decay":{"haiku":"Decay and darkness are carved into flesh as an opening to other worlds; the narrator embraces decay to access forbidden passages.","opus":"The Dillinger Escape Plan turn self-mutilation into a grotesque parody of religious ritual, a blade dragged across skin and a wound treated like a sacrament that names its bearer. Rot is welcomed inside as a supposed gateway to unseen realms, and the boast about never arriving late to one's own end reframes nihilism as a defiant, almost priestly vow, all delivered in their trademark mathcore fury.","sonnet":"A ritual of self-destruction as transformation — flesh carved, names called out, decay invited inward as a way of opening passages to other states of being. Cycles of violence and inheritance recur without resolution, the speaker refusing to follow the pattern of lateness and damage passed down by others.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~wanting-not-so-much-to-as-to":{"haiku":"Someone realizes inner emptiness and withdraws from trying to connect. They discover that clarity comes from the lowest points, and what they sought outside was always within themselves.","opus":"An addiction-shaped hunger to plug an emptiness gives way to the hard realization that what he was grasping for lived within him all the while, and that real clarity emerges from his worst moments. The Dillinger Escape Plan soften their chaos with reflection here, a recurring image of drowning without support framing a bruised, nearly redemptive reckoning under the wreckage.","sonnet":"Withdrawal from connection yields unexpected clarity: what was sought from outside turns out to have been internal all along. The speaker traces the arc from desperate reaching through numbness and sinking to a lucid acceptance that the lowest vantage point reveals the most, and that nothing was ever truly missing.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~when-i-lost-my-bet":{"haiku":"Ascent through opportunism and entitlement: a climber seizes every chance and exploits others' egos, accumulating status through manipulation until intoxication and exposure blur reality.","opus":"The mathcore band anatomizes a social climber grasping at every rung, flattering egos and swapping disguises even as he collapses in a bathroom in ruins. Chaotic and frenzied, it turns its scorn on hollow careerism and drug-fueled excess, its grabby, entitled refrain building toward a summit where the promised payoff never actually arrives.","sonnet":"A social climber works every room, strokes every ego, and chases status with the bottomless appetite of someone for whom enough is never possible. The track watches this figure spin out — literally and metaphorically — as the prize stays perpetually close but the floor keeps rushing up.","src":"haiku"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~widower":{"haiku":"A person grieves a failed relationship, unable to hold onto or express love properly, forever regretful of the distance that grew between them.","opus":"A rare moment of vulnerability from a band built on chaos, this is a confession of a love thrown away through dishonesty and inability to hold on. The grief is that what you discard can never be recovered.","sonnet":"A man who couldn't give a relationship what it needed — he was incapable of holding on, kept running further away the more he saw her hope, and now she's gone and he's left with the irreversibility of what he chose to throw away.","src":"haiku"},
"the-disaster-area~gravity":{"haiku":"Metaphorical weight and internal voices trap the narrator in downward spirals that resist all effort at elevation. The song frames gravity as both physical and psychological force that keeps them grounded below freedom.","opus":"Depression as a physical force, an inner voice pulling the narrator down and pinning him to the ground. The struggle is simply to stand up again and be set free of a weight that keeps choking.","sonnet":"A voice inside pulls the speaker toward the ground and they cannot lift themselves — misguided by doubts, burned by fire, choked by demons — the entire song is a single long plea to be set free of the gravity that keeps them pinned and unable to find the way out.","src":"haiku"},
"the-disaster-area~martyr":{"haiku":"Suffering and isolation intensify as the victim bears scars from abuse, eventually turning tables and demanding violent retribution.","opus":"A survivor's reversal on an abuser who left them to decay behind walls under the pretense of doing it all for the greater good. Now the balance has flipped into vengeance, tearing those walls down and refusing to be discarded, the martyr becoming the reckoning.","sonnet":"About long-term abuse by someone who performed sacrifice while actually causing damage — the narrator is silenced, rotting in the walls, subject to torture framed as care; eventually the abuser loses control and the narrator promises to make them drown in their own misery.","src":"haiku"},
"the-drums~best-friend":{"haiku":"Death fractures friendship and survival; the narrator processes the loss of their best friend while searching for proof of their continued presence.","opus":"Grief for a best friend who died young, the disbelief and the raw question of how to go on, softening into a dream that reassures him he'll survive. The band's jangly brightness makes the loss land harder, mourning dressed as pop.","sonnet":"A direct elegy for a best friend who died young: the speaker revisits their shared rituals, holds onto a dream in which the friend seemed at peace, and slowly moves from wondering how to survive to tentatively believing they will.","src":"haiku"},
"the-eden-project~crazy-in-love":{"opus":"The Eden Project was Jonathan Ng's alias before he became EDEN, working in emotive electronic textures, and here he reimagines a well-known anthem of consuming devotion. Repeating a lover's name in a helpless loop, unable to account for the pull, watching his own dignity buckle before a love unlike any other, the speaker unravels and stops caring who notices.","sonnet":"A cover reworking of obsessive romantic devotion — calling the name two or three times in a row, unable to explain the feeling, watching pride crumble before a love that does what no one else can — the speaker is completely undone and doesn't care who sees it.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-eden-project~crazy-in-love-feat-leah-kelly":{"haiku":"Irrational passion overwhelms the narrator's defenses as love creates chaos and madness that feels both ruinous and essential. The song celebrates the derangement that connection produces despite its destructive force.","opus":"A dizzy confession of love so overwhelming it dismantles pride and self-possession, the singer staring too deep, calling a name over and over, undone by a touch nobody else can match. Vulnerability and helplessness are worn openly. It reworks Beyonce's era-defining anthem; The Eden Project, an early alias of the British producer who became EDEN, reframes the swagger as something softer and more fragile through Leah Kelly's vocal.","sonnet":"Infatuation that strips away composure and self-image — someone whose touch makes the speaker stumble in front of friends, forget practical things, and abandon the pride they normally guard. The bewilderment is the point: nothing in their experience prepared them for a love that works like this, and despite knowing they look foolish, they don't care who sees.","src":"haiku"},
"the-eden-project~jupiter":{"haiku":"Impatience and jealousy corrode the speaker's vision; they long to escape into stellar guidance while wrestling with inadequacy and dishonesty.","opus":"An early Eden song about jealousy and lost potential, reaching for the stars while sabotaged by envy and impatience, admitting he fell for someone's lies partly because he wanted to. Almost heaven, never quite.","sonnet":"A speaker consumed by jealousy, restlessness, and self-doubt addresses someone whose deception they fell for willingly, trying to move forward by running toward a vision while words lose meaning through repetition and the stars offer guidance for a journey that keeps collapsing.","src":"haiku"},
"the-eden-project~man-down":{"haiku":"Estrangement and unspoken devotion collapse as the speaker cannot articulate their feelings or accept that their person has moved forward. Silence and pride leave them screaming internally.","opus":"Wrecked by a bittersweet reunion, the narrator hides behind pride and ego rather than admit he's still shot through with longing, screaming for help no one can hear.","sonnet":"A man too proud to show vulnerability watches a chance at connection slip away — he blows it, runs into the ground, and hides behind ego while screaming internally, the phrase \"man down\" carrying both self-diagnosis and a desperate call for someone to notice.","src":"haiku"},
"the-erkonauts~war-flamingoes":{"haiku":"Privileged, well-meaning people perform charity and democracy from the safety of resorts, structurally preserving the very misery they claim to oppose, and every time the oppressed are crushed they return — unkillable.","opus":"A caustic satire of comfortable privilege and its complicity in oppression. The Swiss avant-metal band skewer the well-meaning, well-dressed elite who talk charity at resorts while keeping the poor locked out of power, exposing the hypocrisy of people who feel safe and virtuous even as they crush the weak. Self-interest is dressed up as generosity.","sonnet":"The powerful perform charity in resort conference rooms and congratulate themselves while actively crushing the weak and perpetuating slavery — yet every soul they chain cannot die, every crushed uprising returns. The narrator turns the indictment inward: 'we are they,' and admits the whole system is designed to protect those already inside it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-ettes~you-were-there":{"haiku":"A speaker questions childhood comfort and reveals disturbing imagery of deteriorating flesh and bone.","opus":"A raw, taunting demand for intimacy, the narrator asking whether setting herself on fire would finally make her seen, whether the other person was ever even a child, reducing them to skeleton, skin and bone. It's confrontation as courtship. The Ettes are a garage-rock trio trading in lo-fi grit and snarl, and this short, jagged burst captures that scene's preference for menace and immediacy over polish or explanation.","sonnet":"A stripped-back confrontation with someone whose presence has become skeletal — the speaker sees through to blood and bone, asks whether this person was ever soft or young, and wonders if setting herself on fire would finally make them see her. Short and forensic, it ends on anatomy: what's left when everything human has been stripped away.","src":"haiku"},
"the-exies~spirits-high":{"haiku":"Maintaining facade while fracturing internally, pretense of stability masking slipping sanity.","opus":"The draining work of faking cheer to avoid a collapse, propping up a fragile mind with belief and self-deception even as sanity feels like it is leaking away. It ends on the quiet, unsettling admission that being alone feels safer and steadier than the endless effort of coping with other people.","sonnet":"A person using forced positivity as a coping mechanism while their mind is slipping — they insist they are where they are supposed to be while privately going mad, seeing ghosts, and terrified to admit the pretending isn't working.","src":"haiku"},
"the-fall-of-troy~f-c-p-r-e-m-i-x":{"haiku":"A relationship dissolving through exhaustion and unresolved conflict, where the narrator obsesses over an absent person and holds onto regret over their departure.","opus":"The Fall of Troy, a young, technically frantic Washington trio, sprint through a relationship falling apart, the narrator worn out as a conversation runs its course. He swears he will return home even as he fears a day when words fail and he is mourned, seizing a single chance to undo the damage. Regret and urgency collide in one desperate lunge.","sonnet":"Romantic desperation propels someone back toward a relationship already unravelling. The mind loops on replay, momentum spent, and the fear of watching grief fall like rain onto a grave outweighs every rational reason to let go — so the narrator keeps moving, insisting he will not allow this to die.","src":"haiku"},
"the-fall-of-troy~we-are-the-future":{"haiku":"The speaker rallies against corruption and stagnation, declaring that a generation will not repeat the same failures and will become the future through resistance.","opus":"A defiant, slippery manifesto staging an execution of the old order, declaring independence and ownership of the future even as everything sells out and someone else holds the master key. The interrogations about shame and playing it safe pile up like accusations. The Fall of Troy, a Washington trio, built their name on frenetic post-hardcore and math-rock guitar acrobatics, and this rides that restless energy into a rallying cry that is as much doubt as bravado.","sonnet":"A fractured manifesto about independence and complicity — everything is sold out, doors are walked through by someone else first, reality is tied up and blind, but the speaker stakes a claim to seeing the future anyway and calls out the game-playing, the fabrication, the safe silence. Not a coherent platform so much as a refusal to be absorbed.","src":"haiku"},
"the-fauves~the-charles-atlas-way":{"haiku":"Self-improvement and aesthetic change offer false salvation while genuine satisfaction remains elusive and out of reach. The song mocks the commodification of transformation and the advertising of reinvention.","opus":"Using the old Charles Atlas bodybuilding ads as a wry metaphor for a fading relationship, dreaming that a comic-book transformation could fix a love that no longer reassures itself. Australian irony over the slow drift into disappointment.","sonnet":"A relationship's collapse is processed through the Charles Atlas body-building comic-book fantasy — once certain of each other, now bored and doubting, the speaker pulls out barbells and dreams of getting back in shape the atlas way, knowing the key to the exit door is somewhere in those old jeans they don't wear anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"the-five-hundred~black-dogs":{"opus":"Depression made physical, imagined as demons and dark hounds hauling the sufferer down into a decaying, hate-filled age. The Five Hundred voice a total disconnection from reality, sleepless and starved, wishing only to escape and forget. The metalcore heaviness turns anguish into a merciless torment in the bleakest place imaginable.","sonnet":"A first-person account of severe depression — the 'black dogs' of the title are the demons of mental illness: the narrator stops eating, stops sleeping, feels estranged from reality, and stands alone at the edge of descending into darkness with no exit visible.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-foreshadowing~days-of-nothing":{"haiku":"Emotional desolation spreads through indifference; loneliness becomes the soul's permanent companion in a crowd.","opus":"Sinking into an empty, deranged existence where loneliness becomes a bride and life feels like lifeless dolls, blame passed around, the singer stumbling but forcing himself to keep walking. Doom-metal despair set against a numb refusal to stop.","sonnet":"A portrait of profound isolation — the speaker moves through crowds feeling invisible and injured, blaming a person who repeatedly hurt them while struggling to keep walking forward despite the hollowness inside.","src":"haiku"},
"the-foreshadowing~eschaton":{"haiku":"Collective complicity erases accountability; silence permits atrocity to continue unchecked.","opus":"An apocalyptic reckoning where a single sound ends the world and burns away lies, vengeance falling on the living. The band frame the end times as judgment on a humanity that lived like parasites, dread wrapped in prophetic grandeur.","sonnet":"An apocalyptic reckoning arrives as a purifying sound that burns away lies and weakness, punishing those who lived as parasites while the speaker witnesses the world collapsing under the weight of its own moral failure.","src":"haiku"},
"the-forgotten~hell-girl-the-forgotten-remix":{"opus":"A remix stitched from a handful of devotional love phrases, promising to love forever and never leave. The vows repeat until they're more texture than meaning.","sonnet":"The lyrics are minimal and mostly consist of affectionate declarations — calling someone nice, saying they love them, promising they will never leave — with no developed narrative or specific imagery. The theme is simple devotion, but there is not enough lyrical content to identify a deeper subject.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-format~oceans":{"haiku":"Long-distance love and regret collide; the narrator wishes they could be an ocean to reconnect with a lover while mourning relationships destroyed by distance.","opus":"Longing for a distant person and half-wishing to become the ocean that separates them just to be near, tangled with social anxiety, envy of thriving friends, and the impulse to flee or reinvent himself. Restless yearning and self-doubt braided together.","sonnet":"Stranded in a long-distance emotional limbo, the speaker writes letters to someone in the sand, spirals into self-examination about social anxiety and past wounds, and oscillates between fantasies of escape and the admission that distance is the only thing keeping him from seeing the other person again.","src":"haiku"},
"the-funeral-portrait~paper-mache-man":{"haiku":"The speaker describes themselves as hollow and fragile, constructed poorly, desperately seeking sensation while aware their body cannot sustain genuine feeling.","opus":"Depicts a man who is awake but numb, imagining himself as a fragile figure built to fall apart, mutilating and reshaping his own body in a desperate bid to feel anything at all. The Funeral Portrait stage this craving for sensation as theatrical dark rock, the narrator a defective creation flickering out toward an emptiness he cannot escape.","sonnet":"A person built from fragile, fabricated materials — hollow and synthetic — forces himself awake and into sensation through self-harm and stimulation. Beneath a machine exterior lies a desperate need to feel something real. The paper mache construction barely holds, threatening to dissolve or burn at any moment.","src":"haiku"},
"the-futureheads~meantime":{"haiku":"Shallow conversation and social pretense mask genuine connection; the narrator dismisses false intimacy and suggests forgetting the exhausting performance of politeness.","opus":"The petty theater of forced small talk with someone whose stories bore him, resentment barely masked by a smile. The post-punk jitter suits its subject: the fakeness of social ritual and the impatience of pretending to care.","sonnet":"A speaker calls out the hollow, performative nature of social pleasantries with someone they find dull and dishonest, acknowledging a mutual obligation to be civil while admitting the interaction is a pointless ritual they ought to abandon.","src":"haiku"},
"the-garden~ballet":{"haiku":"Life flows in constant motion like a river; decay and renewal intertwine as the speaker surrenders to impermanence with defiant allegiance to both chaos and beauty.","opus":"A defiant, absurdist creed of doing whatever you see fit and never looking back, framing life's chaos and decay as a grim, self-directed dance. The Garden's punk shrug at meaning, ready to burn it all with a smirk.","sonnet":"A pledge of allegiance to impermanence and motion — the crow with one eye, the weeping woman by the river — rather than to any fixed creed, with the speaker declaring that life's relentless flow ('river keeps flowing, no holidays') is itself the only performance worth joining.","src":"haiku"},
"the-garden~freight-yard":{"haiku":"Self-containment and isolation armor against intrusion; the speaker refuses connection through deliberate closure.","opus":"A posture of paranoid self-reliance, an everyman who cuts his shady deals and keeps his door bolted against anyone who'd kick it down. The ace card he holds is refusing to be afraid while trusting no one.","sonnet":"A self-reliant person who operates outside normal society making deals in fringe places — they insist they're like everyone else while keeping their door locked against anyone who would try to tear it down, signaling toughness and independence without much elaboration.","src":"haiku"},
"the-garden~man-of-the-people":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects false leadership and collective belonging; no one deserves trust or emulation, only individual self-reliance.","opus":"A prickly refusal to be a leader or a crowd-pleaser, distrusting everyone including those closest and admitting he doesn't even like people. The whole weight of it is the burden of expected charisma he wants no part of, trusting only himself.","sonnet":"A deliberate rejection of the role of leader or public figure — the speaker announces they don't like people, can't trust even those closest to them, and refuses the burden of being someone others look up to, treating isolation as logical rather than tragic.","src":"haiku"},
"the-gazette~taion":{"haiku":"A person endures inexplicable violence and suffering, repeatedly stating that understanding is impossible. They question why they were chosen for this pain and beg for relief from a nightmare they cannot wake from.","opus":"A suffocating account of violation and isolation under a cold winter sky, the narrator crying out that understanding is impossible while pleading for someone to tell him it's all just a terrible dream, silencing his own voice to keep from losing the will to live. The Gazette are a Japanese visual kei band, and this bilingual piece channels that scene's theatrical darkness into genuine anguish over trauma and the desperate wish to endure.","sonnet":"A survivor of assault crying out for someone to tell them it was only a nightmare — screaming until the voice gives out, asking why they were chosen, unable to make sense of what was done. The ceiling, the cold, the laughter sinking into the eardrum: everything is contaminated, and understanding remains permanently, devastatingly impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"the-ghost-inside~aftermath":{"opus":"A survivor's reckoning after catastrophe (the band's real bus crash), asking whether they can ever be themselves again, refusing to stay silent or defeated, burying their demons and choosing to move forward as the beat goes on.","sonnet":"Recovery from an unnamed collapse — the narrator breaks through silence, refuses to accept defeat, buries the demons that followed them, and arrives at a new morning where the beat goes on despite everything that was lost, choosing to move forward rather than stay suspended in trauma.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-glitch-mob~animus-vox":{"haiku":"The speaker confesses to spiritual betrayal like Judas and Thomas, wrestling with the gap between their sinfulness and God's worthiness, their shame crying out louder than any voice.","opus":"Despite the remix title, the vocal is a confession of personal complicity in Christ's crucifixion, the narrator casting himself as Judas kissing the face he betrays and doubting Thomas needing to feel the wounds. The refrain that his own sin yells crucify louder than the mob that day makes each believer personally responsible for the death of the Lamb of God, awed and unworthy before that glory.","sonnet":"A Christian devotional reworked into an electronic context — the speaker identifies with both Judas and Thomas, the betrayer and the doubter, insisting that their own sin is louder than any historical mob calling for crucifixion, and asking what God could possibly see worth saving in them.","src":"haiku"},
"the-glitch-mob~bad-wings":{"haiku":"Invisible strength behind visible accomplishment, recognizing the anchor without which flight is impossible.","opus":"Built on the Bette Midler standard about the unseen force that lifts someone up, it reframes that tribute to a quiet supporter, gratitude to the strength behind another's glory. The electronic setting recasts old sentiment as soaring uplift.","sonnet":"A cover of \"Wind Beneath My Wings\" — about finally seeing and naming the unsung support of someone who has always stood in your shadow, enabling your glory. The emotional core is belated gratitude: recognizing you would be nothing without the person you overlooked.","src":"haiku"},
"the-glitch-mob~beauty-of-the-unhidden-heart-feat-sister-crayon":{"haiku":"Presence of a beloved raises and opens the heart, intensifying emotional experience.","opus":"A sparse, incantatory lyric locates the presence of the beloved as the thing that lifts the heart above itself, circling the ache of feeling everything again. The Glitch Mob build their cinematic electronic swell beneath the yearning refrain of vocalist Sister Crayon, the repetition of raised feeling and openly held presence letting emotion accumulate where words stay deliberately spare.","sonnet":"Presence alone transforms the interior — a heart elevated simply by the nearness of another person. The lyric circles this sensation in tight repetition, arriving not at explanation but at the bare unguarded fact: feeling returns, fully, whenever this particular person is in the room.","src":"haiku"},
"the-glitch-mob~becoming-harmonious-feat-metal-mother":{"haiku":"Unity and sensory confluence drive toward transcendence as experience animates consciousness into harmonious being. The song celebrates the dissolution of boundaries through integration and mutual resonance.","opus":"A meditative wish to dissolve boundaries, senses merging into one flow, the singer inviting another to see straight through them and asking only to bring experience to life. It's transcendence framed as sensory union rather than romance. The Glitch Mob, the LA electronic trio known for cinematic, bass-forward production, pair with Metal Mother's ethereal vocal to make the yearning feel both intimate and vast.","sonnet":"A meditation on transparency and merger — the wish to be seen through completely, to let melody dissolve the boundary between inner experience and the outside world. Harmony here isn't peaceful so much as it is a state of becoming, sensation and sound converging into something that animates rather than merely expresses.","src":"haiku"},
"the-glitch-mob~between-two-points-feat-swan":{"haiku":"Time slipping and incomprehension; forever passes while understanding remains impossible.","opus":"A meditation on love and mortality where time keeps slipping the grip even as the geometry of connection holds, the straightest possible line drawn from one person to another. Blackening feet and burning coals mark it as a doomed, irreversible walk toward the beloved.","sonnet":"Distance and loss rendered geometrically: forever slips through our hands, footsteps weigh heavy, feet turn black on a path with no return — and the only answer the speaker keeps returning to is that the shortest distance between two points is the line from me to you, a fact that makes the separation worse, not better.","src":"haiku"},
"the-glitch-mob~can-t-kill-us":{"opus":"The Glitch Mob built their sound as a live electronic trio staging maximal, cinematic bass, and this title track from their 2014 album is engineered as an arena battle cry. The repeated chant of being all-in and impossible to kill carries no narrative beyond collective indestructibility, the defiance itself the entire point of the sonic escalation.","sonnet":"A declaration of collective indestructibility — the repeated chant of being all-in and impossible to kill is a battle cry of resilience, with no narrative beyond the defiant assertion that this group cannot be defeated.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-glitch-mob~fly-by-night-only-feat-yaarrohs":{"haiku":"Two ride at night, the wind cleansing lies away; deep lust and racing hearts pull them into endless darkness and nocturnal ascent.","opus":"Electronic seduction as a night-only escape, an invitation to ride off and be cleansed of lies under cover of darkness. The pull of forbidden, temporary intimacy drives it, a lust that only exists while the sun is down.","sonnet":"A nocturnal seduction — an invitation to take a late-night drive and let the wind strip away pretence, driven by a deep physical pull that only exists after dark.","src":"haiku"},
"the-glitch-mob~i-need-my-memory-back-feat-aja-volkman":{"haiku":"Someone feels physically and mentally destabilized by another person, loses control, and wants to recreate a specific dance or moment from their past.","opus":"A giddy blackout of a night, the singer spun out of control with her stomach on a roll, wanting to stretch the hours and just dance, half-pleading to get her memory back after losing it to the moment. It is short, intoxicated and euphoric. The Glitch Mob, a Los Angeles electronic trio, pair their heavy, cinematic bass production with Aja Volkman's soulful vocal, the loss of memory framed less as regret than as the thrill of surrender.","sonnet":"A brief, dizzy surrender to someone who made rational thought impossible — stomach churning, balance gone, the only desire left is to get back to the last night spent with them and stay inside it, dancing, for as long as possible.","src":"haiku"},
"the-glitch-mob~skullclub":{"haiku":"A collective identity assertion claims wildness and untamed rebellion, positioning the group as uncontrolled outsiders.","opus":"A wordless anthem of belonging built on the idea of a chosen tribe of outsiders. The near-absence of lyrics is the point: the swagger lives entirely in the beat and the collective declaration.","sonnet":"Almost no lyrical content — the repeated phrase \"we are the wild ones\" and a brief \"see you in the gold I know\" make up essentially the entire text. There's a tribal, collective identity suggested but nothing specific enough to interpret as a theme.","src":"haiku"},
"the-glitch-mob~we-can-make-the-world-stop":{"haiku":"Power fantasy of halting time itself, pausing clocks and freezing motion as an assertion of collective control.","opus":"A minimal, hypnotic mantra built almost entirely on the idea of stopping time, standing still and pausing clocks. With little else to it, the throughline is a fantasy of suspending the world to hold onto a single moment.","sonnet":"A minimal, mantra-like invitation to step outside of time together — stand still, freeze the clocks, make the world pause, just the two of you.","src":"haiku"},
"the-gloom-in-the-corner~misanthropic":{"haiku":"Born from violence and death, the speaker claims dominance over humanity and demons alike. Misanthropy and disdain become the foundation of a dark throne.","opus":"A chilling first-person turn as a serial killer, a self-styled hellhound counting kills and confessing contempt for God and humanity alike, insisting he is not psychopathic, just misanthropic, with a noose worn like a necklace. The Spanish taunt and the tally deepen the menace. The Gloom in the Corner are an Australian metalcore band known for cinematic concept storytelling, and this narrative-driven horror piece inhabits a villain fully, revelling in its own darkness.","sonnet":"A self-declared monster's monologue — born into violence, contemptuous of humanity, wearing its disdain like a noose for a necklace and unable to find god credible. The speaker has already buried 47 people and is counting toward 94, revels in cruelty as the one thing that makes them feel real, and draws a careful distinction: not psychopathic, just misanthropic.","src":"haiku"},
"the-gloom-in-the-corner~witch-hunt":{"haiku":"Betrayal and loss transmute into homicidal ideation; the narrator contemplates self-destruction as the only exit from intolerable grief.","opus":"Unfiltered rage at someone who wrecked a relationship, spilling into a suicidal narrator's vow of vengeance and a final abandonment by God. Deathcore as pure venom, betrayal and grief weaponized into something self-destructive.","sonnet":"Rage at a person who destroyed a relationship is so total it tips into violent fantasy and suicidal ideation — the speaker channels both their own collapse and the voice of someone who has decided the only way to erase the damage is to erase themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"the-god-hand~breathe-into-me":{"opus":"Addiction and toxic attachment fused into drowning imagery, poison shared breath by breath until moving gets harder. The God Hand tangle a destructive love with pleas to God for purpose, admitting the other helped him kill himself. A serpent's spell lurks throughout, desire and self-destruction indistinguishable in the descent.","sonnet":"A song about being destroyed by an addictive person or substance and crying out for divine purpose — the narrator is drowning in a poison sea, has lost their mind, and oscillates between the serpent's spell and the hope that God can give them reason to survive. The messy theology reflects genuine crisis rather than doctrine.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-goo-goo-dolls~here-is-gone":{"haiku":"Emotional distance masquerades as presence; two people fall apart simultaneously while occupying the same space.","opus":"Realizing a partner was never truly present, and trying to detach without cruelty, insisting he isn't the villain even as the connection dissolves. Rzeznik writes the confused middle of a fade-out, wanting freedom and honesty as the person slips away.","sonnet":"A speaker disentangles himself from a relationship built on misaligned needs and false connection — he was never the cause of her problems, she was never truly present, and now the only honest move is to let go completely.","src":"haiku"},
"the-grates~turn-me-on":{"haiku":"Someone yearns to be defined and ignited by another person's touch and presence.","opus":"A giddy, escalating come-on in which the singer invites a lover to hold her, name her and set her ablaze in whatever manner they please. Odd, innocent flashes of childhood memory and lush green countryside keep the desire sounding sweet and unthreatening. The Grates are an Australian indie-rock trio powered by Patience Hodgson's joyful yelp, and the track bounces on that same bright, playful charge, want delivered as pure fun.","sonnet":"An invitation to ignite someone into themselves — the other person has ancient eyes and a power that feels like a gift, and the speaker wants to be sparked by it, defined by it, set roaring. The ask is open: any way you want, any direction, just do it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-great-discord~afterbirth":{"haiku":"A toxic lover oscillates between cruelty and possession, unable to release someone they simultaneously hate and desire.","opus":"A savage, sexually charged breakup told in dueling voices of two combative lovers who craved raw, cruel passion; the dominant partner boasts of discarding the other while confessing a whiplash of hate and craving, and the discarded one, scarred and left blank, watches passion set free to lead others blind.","sonnet":"A violently ambivalent dissection of a consuming, degrading relationship — desire and hatred intertwined, the narrator alternating between cruelty and craving, neither able to fully let go nor stay intact.","src":"haiku"},
"the-great-discord~army-of-me":{"haiku":"The speaker refuses sympathy and dismisses complaints, telling the listener to be self-sufficient and face their problems alone as their rescue capacity is exhausted.","opus":"A cold ultimatum to a chronic complainer: pull yourself together, get on with it, and know that whining once more brings retaliation, because no help is coming. This Swedish alt-metal group covers Bjork's confrontational classic, pushing its tough-love message through heavier, more theatrical menace while keeping the original's flinty insistence that the person stands entirely alone.","sonnet":"Exhausted compassion hardening into ultimatum — the speaker refuses to sympathize anymore, tells the complainer to stand up and be self-sufficient, and warns that continued grievance will be met with a version of the speaker multiplied. The rescue squad has nothing left; you are on your own now.","src":"haiku"},
"the-great-discord~blood-and-envy":{"haiku":"Envy and hatred corrupt someone into a mirror of their perceived enemy. Hidden feelings of inadequacy transform into violent possession and control.","opus":"A predatory taunt directed at a tearful, envious man whose buried resentment the speaker vows will fester into something monstrous, his kindness warping under a doctrine of loathing as she smears the walls with the title's imagery. There's a cruel pity in her belittling him. The Great Discord, a theatrically inclined Swedish alt-metal act, drape this study of manipulation and self-worth in cinematic menace.","sonnet":"A predator watching weakness fester into hatred from a cool distance — the speaker addresses a man whose suppressed feelings will curdle into something dark, and narrates their own domination: the man's life is already in hand, his rottenness leaking out as planned, his envy for what the speaker has making him a foolish lamb who can't see his own worth.","src":"haiku"},
"the-great-discord~omen":{"opus":"The burnt-out collapse of a relationship built entirely on illusion, the narrator apologizing while conceding it is far past saving. Haunted by a partner who seems to be everywhere, hollowed out and hopeless, she concludes that oblivion would beat this shared misery, framing the ruin as an ominous fate the two can no longer escape.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship that both people recognize as doomed from the start — one voice feels soulless and consumed, the other equally trapped, and they understand that their demons and omens point to mutual destruction, yet neither can fully leave.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-great-discord~the-red-rabbit":{"opus":"A dark fairy-tale power struggle, a dominating IRE dangling temptation only to deny and punish the greedy and lustful. The Great Discord stages seduction, control and cruelty through Alice-in-Wonderland imagery, marching blind down the rabbit hole. It probes appetite and submission, a taunting tormentor commanding down boy over the craving prey.","sonnet":"A power struggle between the powerful and the powerless — the authority figure ('IRE') dangles what people want and then punishes their desire, whipping them into submission while urging them down a rabbit hole, and the crowd marches blindly toward its own destruction.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-great-divide~anyone-evermore":{"haiku":"Capitulation and suppression; the speaker has obeyed for years only to recognize the manipulation.","opus":"A bitter post-breakup reckoning in which the speaker admits years spent shrinking to please a controlling partner, now watching bonds crumble; they confess their own lies yet foresee the cyclical pattern of being let back in, resolving instead on a careless, self-freeing end.","sonnet":"Years of shrinking to fit someone else's shape come to an end — keeping quiet, following their lead, stumbling at every step. The bitter taste left over is acknowledged honestly: I had my part of lies, I wasn't always right. Now whoever comes next, whoever's awake — the door stays open, the invitation careless and therefore finally free.","src":"haiku"},
"the-guild~baba-yaga":{"haiku":"Revenge and calculated vengeance restore agency and purpose as the narrator returns to inflict judgment on those who wronged them. The song frames focused fury and code-based action as paths to restoring dignity.","opus":"A revenge fantasy modeled on the John Wick myth, a man forced back into a violent world after losing the one thing that made peace possible. Grief hardened into cold, methodical vengeance where everything has a price.","sonnet":"A man who overcame the impossible and lost everything anyway is drawn back into a world of violence he left behind — framing himself as Baba Yaga, the unstoppable dark figure of legend, he pursues vengeance with focus, commitment, and sheer fucking will, because everything has a price.","src":"haiku"},
"the-haarp-machine~forsaken":{"haiku":"Ejected and abandoned, the speaker confronts a hostile cosmos demanding sacrifice; seeking meaning in abandonment, clawing upward through isolation to claim survival as defiance.","opus":"Thrown out of the fold and trapped in flames, the singer pleads for a divine answer that never comes while scavenger birds wait overhead. When no sign arrives he abandons the prayer and resolves to stand entirely on his own, gathering a bruised, stubborn will to survive and haul himself out of the pain.","sonnet":"A soul ejected from community, gasping for air in a tempest with no anchor, cries out for a sign while vultures circle and entry to something higher is denied. The song turns at the end from supplication to resolution — \"I'll face this world on my own\" — but the spirit is weak and the world still bleak, so the resolution lands as defiance rather than triumph.","src":"haiku"},
"the-haarp-machine~the-nadir":{"haiku":"Trapped in a cycle of betrayal and self-destruction, falling into an emotional abyss. The speaker seeks redemption and a new way to love after being used and broken.","opus":"A plunge into the mind's darkest depths, a vast empty interior where dark figures beg to be freed and death waits, the narrator dragged toward a downfall he never saw coming and losing his grip on what's real. Faint hope still flickers. The Haarp Machine, a British djent-leaning prog-metal project, match that spiral with spiraling technicality.","sonnet":"Descent into a psychological abyss that has a face and a conspiracy behind it — figures in black, a noose waiting, reality dissolving, and the growing suspicion that someone specific engineered this suffering. The speaker reaches a floor of total brokenness and then, unexpectedly, turns: there is beauty still, a new way to love worth finding, something worth not giving up.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hara~easier-to-die":{"haiku":"Despair and urban violence make existence feel futile, where survival requires fighting against societal indifference and internal breakdown.","opus":"Life in a decaying, violent environment feels so tainted that dying seems easier than wanting to live, the mind on overdrive and sickened by what the world has become.","sonnet":"Urban despair and mental overload push the speaker to the raw admission that surviving feels harder than dying; the image \"where flowers used to grow, bullets fly instead\" marks the interior transformation from innocence to exhausted endurance.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hara~stay":{"haiku":"Codependency suffocates both as the target becomes inescapable presence and drowning anchor. Separation would require breath, but disconnection seems impossible.","opus":"An addictive, suffocating attachment to someone who can't be gotten over, cast as running out of oxygen while they stay lodged in the lungs. Begging a leaving lover to stay even knowing it's ruinous.","sonnet":"A suffocating bond the speaker cannot escape: the other person is still in their lungs, still in their dreams, the hell inside the heaven they are chasing — they beg them to stay even knowing it might pull them under and drain the last of their oxygen.","src":"haiku"},
"the-haunted~monuments":{"haiku":"Heroes and leaders fall, forgotten as war destroys belief systems and leaves only monuments to the damned.","opus":"A bleak survey of the aftermath, where saints and rulers alike lie toppled, dethroned and eroding, everyone erased in a hateful world so that only stone markers of the doomed endure. History is reduced to cold rock. The Haunted, a Swedish melodic death-metal band descended from At the Gates, drive their groove-heavy attack into this vision of ruin, every hero and outcast swallowed by oblivion.","sonnet":"Empires, martyrs, and ideologies ground to dust until only their stone markers remain — masters wither in the sand, heroes and outcasts alike are forgotten, convictions revoked as storm clouds come. What survives is not meaning but mass: the monolith standing after the people who built it are gone.","src":"haiku"},
"the-haunted~tighten-the-noose":{"haiku":"A person repeatedly lies and bends the truth to survive, but each deception tightens the noose around them—their false kingdom will collapse and they will face reckoning.","opus":"A scathing takedown of a habitual liar whose empire of falsehood is collapsing while onlookers cheer, each fresh lie only bringing his self-destruction closer. He is cast as vermin foolish enough to bait a predator and hand himself the means of his own undoing. The Haunted, veteran Swedish melodic death-metal players, spit the contempt over pummeling groove.","sonnet":"A liar watched in real time constructing their own destruction — each additional falsehood is another inch of rope, the kingdom of lies crumbling while the world watches, and the speaker makes clear that bending the truth is not just wrong but mechanically fatal. A rat that talks to a snake has already lost.","src":"haiku"},
"the-haunt~alone-on-your-planet":{"haiku":"Distance and abandonment crystallize into separate worlds; the subject inhabits planetary isolation.","opus":"Bitter fallout from a relationship where a former dream turned nightmare, the singer flinging the taunt of how lonely life is for the partner who took off and abandoned them.","sonnet":"A speaker reeling from a painful breakup asks their ex — who has \"taken off\" and landed somewhere new — how it feels to be alone on their own planet, while admitting the nightmare that used to be a dream has left them sleepless and nauseous.","src":"haiku"},
"the-haunt~bad-omen":{"haiku":"Inherited corruption predestines destruction; the speaker embraces their pathological nature as inevitable and complete, chaos and calamity their native element.","opus":"A swaggering embrace of being the bad news, the person who was rotten since birth and now finds calm inside chaos. The self-diagnosis of something wrong becomes a costume worn defiantly against anyone talking.","sonnet":"A person fully embraces their own chaos and psychological damage as identity — they hurt people, break promises, and thrive on calamity, warning everyone upfront that they are bad news.","src":"haiku"},
"the-haunt~can-people-really-change":{"haiku":"Transformation and redemption remain questionable; the speaker doubts whether fundamental change occurs.","opus":"Trapped in a toxic loop with a partner who breaks her heart then crawls back needing her, she keeps forgiving and keeps waiting, confronting the song's title question and answering it grimly: people don't escape who they are. Love was never a matter of logistics, and she taught him herself how to twist the knife.","sonnet":"The question is rhetorical — the narrator knows their partner uses vulnerability as manipulation, taught them how to twist the knife, and has finally diagnosed the relationship as toxic. \"Can people really change?\" is directed at someone who keeps promising transformation to avoid accountability, and the answer is written in the exhaustion of the person still waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"the-haunt~i-m-not-yours":{"haiku":"Autonomy and refusal to belong; the speaker asserts ownership of self against possession.","opus":"A defiant severance from a toxic, codependent relationship steeped in addiction and mutual contempt. The narrator, raised on anxiety and misery, rejects a partner's insistence that they saw the good in her, reclaiming autonomy through raw refusal. It is a young person's furious declaration of independence from someone who wanted to own her.","sonnet":"Mutual destruction in a relationship held together by misery — both parties are fucked up and wasted, she's smoking three packs, he's sober and crazy; falling apart on the bedroom floor is the recurring location. The declaration \"I'm not yours\" is fury at being claimed by someone who gave up on them both, raised on whiskey, anxiety, and misery.","src":"haiku"},
"the-haunt~little-like-hell":{"haiku":"Addiction to chaos and the familiar wound traps the speaker; they choose the haunt of their destroyer over the unknown peace of freedom.","opus":"A poisonous on-again relationship where the couple only feels close when they are tearing each other apart, and the narrator, hooked on turmoil, half-relishes the misery. Underneath runs a bitter certainty that her partner never truly cared, the whole doomed dynamic dressed up as something perversely thrilling.","sonnet":"A relationship that won't die cleanly — the other person calls every night, screams the narrator's name like they want them, then disappears to haunt again — sits 'almost romantic' in its toxicity, with the narrator addicted to the chaos and knowing, finally, it is their ex's fault entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"the-head-and-the-heart~down-in-the-valley":{"haiku":"Nomadic wandering through whiskey-soaked terrain promises no redemption; the speaker loops through repetitive self-destruction.","opus":"A restless romantic imagining himself into some older, hard-drinking working life, running through the same self-sabotaging cycle over and over, always circling back to the low familiar places he calls home rather than the wide country he never reaches.","sonnet":"A restless, self-aware wanderer acknowledges his pull toward whiskey and trouble, admits the cycle repeats endlessly, and yet keeps moving — drawn back always to the hidden valley where he feels most himself.","src":"haiku"},
"the-healing~diary-of-jane":{"haiku":"Search for identity within a turbulent relationship becomes impossible as pain accumulates; the speaker burns pages and walks away.","opus":"An obsessive craving to be known by someone unreachable, the speaker willing to hurt himself just to occupy a place in her private world. Love and hate blur as he burns through pages trying to find where he fits.","sonnet":"A man desperately trying to understand and reach a woman named Jane — he will do anything to find a place in her inner world, burning pages, crawling with desperation, suspended between love and hate as something in the relationship keeps breaking.","src":"haiku"},
"the-heavy~how-you-like-me-now":{"haiku":"A lover, once faithful, becomes unfaithful and now presents a changed, improved version of themselves to provoke the ex-partner's regret.","opus":"A swaggering taunt from a man who was once loved, cheated, and left; now transformed and thriving, he throws his former lover's rejection back in her face while half-daring her to still want him.","sonnet":"A boastful taunt directed at a former lover — the narrator admits he was a bad man and a liar but claims he has found himself and transformed, daring her to see and want him now.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hellfreaks~men-in-grey":{"haiku":"Time itself (the men in grey) passes faster than perception and steals life away. The speaker pleads for one more night without fear and asks time not to be taken for granted.","opus":"A seize-the-day plea against the tyranny of time, imploring the grey figures who dole out our hours for a single night lived without dread of its being the last, and recasting age not as decay but as grandeur earned through years. The Hellfreaks, a Hungarian horror-punk and psychobilly band, drive this meditation on mortality with ticking-clock urgency and gothic swagger.","sonnet":"A plea for more time directed at the grey figures of age and death who move too fast to be seen — asking for one more night without fear of the last, one more breath without chasing the clock. The older generation is held up as majestic not for power or money but simply for having lived long enough to make time meaningful.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hellfreaks~weeping-willow":{"haiku":"Memory outlives consciousness; the speaker fades while past moments remain more vivid and real than present suffering, haunting from within.","opus":"Numb depression beneath a weeping willow, memories more vivid than the self, going through the motions of being fine while privately just needing to switch off for a while. A dark, exhausted honesty behind the polite mask.","sonnet":"Under the weeping willow, alone and amnesiac about what once drove them, the speaker says \"I'm fine\" while knowing it's their favourite lie and confessing that sometimes they need to die for a short while just to feel anything.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hellp~caustic":{"haiku":"Addiction to control and submission blurs agency and causality as cycles perpetuate without escape. The speaker remains imprisoned within toxic patterns, unable to release or transcend.","opus":"A blurry, dissociated sketch of power and surrender, someone hooked on a dominating relationship with a ruthless figure they have never managed to leave, a fixation traced back to adolescence. Memory and self dissolve as the track suggests many separate existences crammed into one person over time.","sonnet":"A toxic control dynamic: someone is addicted to a cutthroat criminal type of control, doing what they want and what they are told in the same breath — \"Autumn never learned how to give up\" — the speaker caught between submission and the gridlock of a grip that never loosens.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hellp~doppler":{"haiku":"Time's inexorable passage breeds exhaustion and questioning; middle age arrives with philosophical uncertainty about meaning and direction.","opus":"A hazy meditation on aging into middle age and the slow dissolution of a relationship viewed through glass. The LA duo capture the numbness of watching someone become a hollow copy of themselves, where yesterday collapses into mere feeling and connection thins out like condensation on a window before it evaporates entirely.","sonnet":"A relationship drifting past each other at speed — two people who came around to middle age and kept missing the moment of real communication, watching through window panes, one moving fast and the other never noticing how it lasted. The Doppler metaphor is the shift in frequency as something passes: everything that yesterday felt like meaning was \"just a feeling, just a reason, just the meaning.\"","src":"haiku"},
"the-hellp~height":{"haiku":"Vanity and compulsion; a person trapped in surface appeal and addict cycles, despising others who mirror them.","opus":"The Hellp, an LA duo peddling numbed post-internet cool, portray a hazy California addict too vain and mentally unwell to function. The pale, blond, clenched-jaw pretty boy admits the one thing he knows is a distaste for people who resemble him, self-hatred wrapped in detached posture. Clingy, static-charged repetition mirrors a hollowed-out identity dissolving into a timeline he keeps rewinding.","sonnet":"A California daze of vanity and addiction produces a figure who knows he is trapped but cannot break the pattern. Crooked and bent, sickened but performing, he has become just another addict beneath the sun-bleached surface — the lockjaw heartthrob whose contempt for people who mirror him is really contempt for the static he cannot leave.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hellp~ssx":{"haiku":"Nostalgic memory of a relationship now faded, living fast and burning through existence while questioning what remained real.","opus":"The LA duo dresses live-fast fatalism in glossy synthetic sheen, watching a partner come apart in her mid-twenties, the light gone from her, while the narrator holds on and wonders why sweetness should outweigh what's real. Nostalgia curdles into the present tense, mourning a relationship as if it's already past while it's somehow still alive.","sonnet":"A relationship unravels as one partner loses a spark at 25 and the other holds on through need rather than understanding. What remains is memory and the numbed repetition of days lived away from something that felt necessary, the question of why sweetness once outweighed pain left unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hellp~undertow-damon-r-remix":{"haiku":"Poverty, faith dissolution, and departure rendered in fragmented imagery of money, roads, and abandonment of lunar hope.","opus":"The Hellp trade in a blown-out, druggy Americana of dead ends, a narrator numbed on Xanax insisting money and truth could neither save nor raise him. Fragmentary as the remix leaves it, the lyric coasts a Texaco road with the gas pedal down, a Chevy like a bullet, faith placed briefly in a woman. Having left the moon behind, he dissolves into a repeated command to dance, escape flattened into motion without destination.","sonnet":"Fragmentary and largely unintelligible beyond scattered images of money, pharmaceuticals, and driving fast toward nowhere — the emotional register is dissociation and a sense of having left something behind. Faith placed in a woman and speed are the only anchors in the drift.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hellp~vertigo":{"haiku":"Spinning disorientation and lack of plan or direction leave the speaker caught in chaos, unable to find steady ground.","opus":"Amid fragmented references to Sisyphus, Orpheus and being caught in a kingpin, the speaker describes a dizzying spin and blood-dropping tension, repeatedly telling himself to relax while sinking rather than swimming and dying for his sins.","sonnet":"The song circles around a state of dizzy disorientation — knotted thoughts, a dropping heart, Sisyphean and Orphic references to futile effort — with fragments suggesting entrapment in circumstances with no clear plan and no way to swim free, only the spinning sense of vertigo.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hell~doosh":{"haiku":"Aggressive assertion and sonic domination; forceful presence that cannot be ignored or contained.","opus":"A gleefully dumb, testosterone-soaked celebration of impact and bravado from British hardcore jokers The Hell. Built around an onomatopoeic punch, it swaggers through mosh-pit violence, boxing combos, and scene-king boasting with zero pretense at meaning. Pure meathead catharsis, delivered with a wink and a fist to the face.","sonnet":"A pure boast track: every hit landed, every new drop, every crowd reaction collapses into one sound — doosh — making bravado and impact literally onomatopoeic. Martial arts combos and Doc Marten boots are the vocabulary.","src":"haiku"},
"the-herbaliser~excuse-me":{"haiku":"Loneliness masquerading as composure, where private anguish hides behind performed normalcy and the speaker rehearses goodbye before courage fails entirely.","opus":"A soul-classic of hidden heartbreak, laughing and joking in public while pride conceals the aching truth that the singer must leave before the tears show.","sonnet":"The speaker hides a heartbreak behind jokes and smiles whenever others bring up the person they lost, confessing inwardly that pride forces them to perform cheerfulness while their heart pleads to be excused before the tears break through.","src":"haiku"},
"the-herbaliser~the-sensual-woman":{"opus":"A spoken instructional collage from a vintage guide to female sensuality, coaching women to explore their bodies, embrace erotic adventure and blunt sexual language, and use imagination and daring to keep a man enthralled.","sonnet":"A spoken-word piece voiced by a woman exploring her own sexuality on her own terms — she describes sensory awakening, sexual adventure, and urges her listener to shed prudishness and embrace explicit language and experimentation as natural and enhancing.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-hero-and-a-monster~wounds":{"haiku":"Abandonment salts wounds that refuse to heal; the speaker pleads with absence itself to provide relief from rupture.","opus":"Grief written as a wound someone keeps rubbing salt into, the singer half-praying to a God or a lost person for a sign that never comes, until faith curdles into abandonment and the resolve to finally let go.","sonnet":"A desperate prayer addressed to someone lost or absent — \"take the salt out of the wound\" — as malice floods the speaker's veins, taunting spirits constrict from the inside, and the memories make the air itself harder to breathe.","src":"haiku"},
"the-highwaymen~highwayman":{"haiku":"Reincarnation cycles through multiple lives of toil and sacrifice; the narrator persists across centuries despite death, seeking peace or purpose beyond individual existence.","opus":"Four voices for one indestructible soul that dies as bandit, sailor, dam worker and starship pilot yet keeps returning. A country-outlaw supergroup singing reincarnation as a promise that the wild spirit and the men who carry it can never really be killed.","sonnet":"A soul narrates successive reincarnations across American history — highwayman, sailor, dam builder — each life ending in death yet the spirit persisting, with the final verse pointing toward a starship and the quiet acceptance that it will keep returning in one form or another.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hirs-collective~xoxoxoxoxox":{"haiku":"Hearts collaborate through art and chaos; community bonds strangers into family.","opus":"From a radically inclusive queer hardcore collective, a rare tender moment: an open-armed embrace of chosen family and lovers, refusing fear and offering affection as a defiant act against a world that isolates.","sonnet":"An open invitation into community: the collective looks forward to being held by friends, lovers, and chosen family, and promises X's and O's to anyone who comes — hearts beating as one through noise and art, fear and fret gently refused.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hives~trapdoor-solution":{"haiku":"Literal solution to problems via disposal mechanism, transformation of suffering into novelty.","opus":"Garage-rock absurdism where a literal trapdoor becomes the gleeful cure-all for every problem, its dark comic turn dropping arrogant jerks out of sight. Petty vengeance dressed up as childlike invention.","sonnet":"A cheerful narrator proposes a trapdoor in the floor as the universal answer to problems — lead assholes to it, flip the lid, watch them drop — the satirical simplicity undercutting any serious reading of problem-solving with gleeful, absurdist disposal.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hold-steady~heavy-covenant":{"haiku":"Transactional relationships formalise exploitation; financial and emotional exchange obscures predatory dynamics within intimate arrangements.","opus":"A traveling software salesman drifting through motels and airports, the lyric slowly revealing he pays a musician to procure drugs, or something darker, framing that transaction as the heavy bargain the powerful strike with the powerless in a numbed, fractured life.","sonnet":"A middle-aged man on a business trip in Tucson seeks something outside his ordinary life — a transaction, an encounter, a musician in a bar — and the song holds the tension between his professional persona and the part of him making commitments he can barely name.","src":"haiku"},
"the-home-team~danger-feat-jeff-loomis-veil-of-maya":{"haiku":"Unhealthy attraction spirals into obsession; the speaker is drawn into risky passion despite knowing better.","opus":"A giddy, self-destructive infatuation cast as vice and medicine, the narrator hooked on a headache of a lover who's gotten into his blood, wasting his energy and lying to himself while begging her to wake him when she leaves. The Home Team are a pop-rock-leaning band, and this crossover pulls guitar heavyweights from the metal world into a hyper-catchy ode to losing control and diving in anyway.","sonnet":"Addiction to a person framed as a pleasurable disaster — the speaker knows this is bad for them, feels it in the blood, can't stop chasing it anyway. The high of being wanted collides with the reality of self-deception, and by the end the speaker is losing it completely, toasting everyone still standing while admitting they'd make a fool of themselves all over again.","src":"haiku"},
"the-home-team~loud":{"haiku":"Suppression and performance mask authentic expression as fear prevents release of inner power. The speaker urges breaking silence through sound and sensation.","opus":"A flirtatious pop-rock come-on that's also a nudge to shed inhibition, reading a partner's guardedness as unlived caution and coaxing her to stop performing restraint and let real pleasure and voice come through.","sonnet":"An invitation to shed inhibition in an intimate encounter: the speaker urges a woman held back by past principles to stop being afraid, to make herself heard, because getting loud — physically and emotionally — is the only proof she is truly living.","src":"haiku"},
"the-home-team~love-co":{"haiku":"Success and independence isolate the speaker from authentic connection, leaving them exhausted by surface-level engagement.","opus":"Watching a friend perform a lavish, enviable life while clearly running on empty, the singer urging them to stop pretending and reach out for the love and company they need.","sonnet":"The speaker is visibly thriving — paradise in every moment — but suspects everyone around them is only there to consume what they have; the refrain \"do what you love, it's easy\" serves both as genuine advice and a defence against those feeding off them.","src":"haiku"},
"the-hunna~trash":{"haiku":"Capitalist exploitation and manufactured authenticity reduce artistry to product as success requires selling soul and body. The song names the system's demand for commercial viability and mocks the performer's complicity.","opus":"A bitter industry kiss-off aimed at labels and TikTok gatekeepers who chew up bands for money, mocking the demand to be fake and marketable while the artists get dragged through the dirt for someone else's profit.","sonnet":"The music industry's demand for gimmicks, fellatio for radio play, and pure money extraction is skewered with blunt fury — the band refuses the game, calling out designer-suited executives who drag artists through the dirt while pretending to care about anything other than money.","src":"haiku"},
"the-i-l-y-s~doing-things-that-artists-do":{"opus":"An obsessive, half-ironic infatuation, someone compulsively needing to marry, stare at, and give himself to a person while wrecking himself in the messy performance of being an artist and wishing he weren't so serious about it.","sonnet":"An obsessive, self-destructive infatuation — the narrator is consumed by the need to fuse their life completely with another person, comparing this consuming love to the reckless, messy, total investment artists and athletes pour into their craft.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-i-l-y-s~itchy-itchy-itchy":{"haiku":"A restless person walks alone at night, itching for connection they cannot maintain, wanting both to lie and to die for their absent lover.","opus":"A restless narrator pleads to be understood, walking home alone at night while a persistent itch drives him to lie, cry and die toward a girl he cannot take with him. Side project of Ty Segall's drummer, The Ily's bury lovesick desperation in garage-rock fuzz and repetition, the twitching refrain standing for a longing that never quite resolves.","sonnet":"A restless, disconnected figure craves genuine understanding but cannot quite reach the person in front of him. He walks home alone, feels an unlocatable itch beneath everything, and oscillates between wanting to be taken and wanting to escape. Longing and unavailability circle each other without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"the-intersphere~wanderer":{"haiku":"Systematic thinking and perpetual motion mask stagnation as the speaker decodes humanity while unable to direct their own trajectory, caught between aspiration and an inescapable cycle.","opus":"A middle-aged reckoning with never being able to settle, aimed at people forever pursuing lost time and remaking themselves. Weary of the corporate grind and an age of manufactured lies, the narrator refuses to surrender to a bleak ending and keeps drifting onward in search of something that might feel like belonging.","sonnet":"A reckoning with stagnation and the rat race: the speaker asks whether they ever dared to climb or ban a wrong-feeling plan, notes that at some point dreaming stopped and the wanderer role made them sick, but turns toward unmasking lies and dancing on wires toward unknown jewels rather than leaving the earth the way they found it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-jam~town-called-malice":{"haiku":"Stagnation and economic constraint suffocate a working-class town where dreams yield to survival and choice becomes impossible.","opus":"Weller's snapshot of working-class Britain grinding down under Thatcherism, families choosing between beer and the kids' clothes, dreams withering out behind the dairy, yet still insisting it's up to them to change it.","sonnet":"An indictment of economic decline in a working-class British town where idle milk floats, lonely housewives, tight household budgets, and faded dreams are the landscape — the speaker urges those trapped there to stop dreaming of escape and instead fight to put joy back into the place.","src":"haiku"},
"the-jimi-hendrix-experience~all-along-the-watchtower":{"haiku":"A joker and thief converse about escape from confusion and exploitation, watching as danger approaches on the horizon.","opus":"A cryptic exchange between a joker and a thief about confusion, exploitation and the lateness of the hour, resolving into a watchtower where princes keep watch and two riders approach through a howling wind, dread coiled and unresolved. Hendrix took Bob Dylan's terse parable and made it his own with towering, weather-swept guitar. This 1968 recording became the definitive version, its apocalyptic hush and squall now inseparable from the song itself.","sonnet":"A conversation between a joker and a thief at the end of their rope — one complains of exploitation and confusion, the other counsels against treating life as a joke and pushes toward honesty before the hour runs out. Then the perspective lifts to the watchtower, where riders approach in wind and distance, and the world holds its breath.","src":"haiku"},
"the-jimi-hendrix-experience~purple-haze":{"haiku":"A person lost in a purple haze cannot distinguish day from night or sanity from chaos. Someone has cast a spell, leaving him confused and pleading for clarity.","opus":"A dizzying psychedelic haze in which everything feels wrong and the narrator loses all bearings, unable to tell whether he is rising or sinking, joyful or wretched. Whether the trigger is a drug or a woman who has bewitched him, the effect is a mind scrambled loose from time itself, uncertain what comes next or whether anything comes at all.","sonnet":"The total mental and perceptual dissolution caused by a woman — everything is disoriented, time and space collapse, and she has cast such a spell on the narrator that he can't tell whether he's happy, miserable, or at the end of time.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song builds its haze as an image-system that keeps slipping between opposites the singer can no longer separate — ascent blurred into descent, elation into wretchedness, daylight into darkness, an unknowable morning against the last hour of everything — so that the disorientation, not any single vision, is the real subject. Hendrix half-blames a girl's spell and half-blames his own scrambled senses, and the psychedelic mind-blowing stays deliberately double: it could be transcendence or breakdown, kissing the sky or losing the ground entirely. The repeated cries for help underneath the ecstatic surface are the tell — this is rapture and panic reported in the same breath, refusing to resolve which one it is.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"rapture and panic reported in the same breath, refusing to resolve","built":"cries for help run underneath the ecstatic surface throughout"},
"the-joy-formidable~sevier":{"haiku":"The speaker pursues transformation and escape alongside another, never feeling lost despite nights that blur and shapes that change.","opus":"A cautious fresh start, the narrator unwilling to slip back into an old pattern and instead reaching toward a lover in a new way. Wandering a riverbank after dark and pursuing a bright celestial point, she stops tracking the hours yet never feels adrift, sensing this bond could be something genuinely different and better.","sonnet":"The rush of a new connection that feels transformative — someone shows you a different way of being, nights by the river feel endless, and you give yourself over to chasing whatever this feeling is.","src":"haiku"},
"the-killers~all-these-things-that-i-ve-done":{"haiku":"Spiritual emptiness haunts the speaker despite searching for purpose; fractured identity resists integration.","opus":"A young man's raw plea for salvation and meaning, torn between wanting to be good and knowing he isn't a fighter. The gospel-choir surge turns private exhaustion and moral inadequacy into something like collective prayer.","sonnet":"A narrator at the edge of his endurance — spiritually hungry, emotionally depleted, older than he feels — calls out for help and direction while insisting he has soul even if he cannot be a soldier, cycling between urgency and exhaustion.","src":"haiku"},
"the-killers~caution-radio-edit":{"haiku":"Desperation drives recklessness; the narrator abandons caution to escape stagnation, willing to burn systems that confine potential.","opus":"A portrait of a girl trapped in a dead-end desert town, inheriting her mother's limited options, set against the singer's own desperation to escape before he burns the place down. Brandon Flowers's Nevada roots make the ache of small-town confinement autobiographical.","sonnet":"A woman formed by desert hardship and a dancer mother carries something explosive inside her, and a speaker who identifies with that restlessness decides to throw caution aside and get out before he becomes someone who burns everything down from inside.","src":"haiku"},
"the-killers~human":{"haiku":"A soul questions whether surrender and numbness define humanity, seeking proof of authentic emotion and purpose in a world of severed connections.","opus":"Flowers wrestling with whether modern life has hollowed us into puppets going through the motions, the strange dancer question a genuine dread that we've traded soul and devotion for something mechanical.","sonnet":"A speaker in existential crisis questions whether modern life has stripped people of authentic humanity, replacing feeling and devotion with choreographed behaviour — mourning the loss of grace, soul, and romance while unsure whether anyone is truly alive inside their own routine.","src":"haiku"},
"the-killers~somebody-told-me":{"haiku":"A rumor about someone's past romantic history (a gender-ambiguous ex) disrupts the narrator's desire to pursue them, creating self-doubt and distraction.","opus":"The Killers' breakout captures a jittery nightclub chase, a narrator straining just to catch a stranger's name in a scene where fulfillment feels out of reach. A secondhand rumor about a past partner's new lover, described in gender-bending terms, needles his pride even as he brags about his own promise. Brandon Flowers keeps it restless and ready to move on the instant things fizzle.","sonnet":"Pursuit of someone across a chaotic social landscape is complicated by a rumor about their romantic history — the narrator burns energy chasing a connection that may already be entangled. Ambition and attraction mix with frustrated momentum as the search for a real moment keeps slipping away.","src":"haiku"},
"the-knocks~brazilian-soul-feat-sofi-tukker":{"opus":"A love letter to Brazil and a Brazilian woman, celebrating samba, forró, capoeira and December heat as a joyful cultural romance the narrator has been swept into.","sonnet":"A celebratory tribute to Brazilian culture — the narrator has fallen in love with a Brazilian woman and with Brazil itself, romanticizing its music, dance, and warmth as something that has permanently changed them.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-kodiak~hyde-howitzer":{"haiku":"Hollow glamour concealing substance-free existence; posturing as truth while collapsing within.","opus":"A sneering dissection of vanity and performed beauty, where the narrator plays a predatory observer coaxing a fragile person to keep up their glamorous facade. Beneath the taunts about masks and makeup lurks cruelty and revulsion, framing the beauty queen as a filthy creature trying and failing to hide her true, hideous self behind the pose.","sonnet":"An observer watches someone perform composure — strike a pose, look natural, slap on that favourite dress — while the speaker's amusement at the strain of it registers as contempt. The kept-together facade is already collapsing into something the speaker calls a creature thing.","src":"haiku"},
"the-korea~a-14buqh9":{"haiku":"An apocalyptic lament on civilizational collapse—abandoned ancestral wisdom for blind ambition, now the world burns in divine retribution.","opus":"The Korea play a theatrical strain of Russian alternative metal, and this apocalyptic lament channels that grandeur into indictment. Humanity has betrayed its fathers' wisdom, the planet burns on fire chariots while gods tear the rotting earth, eyes that once held love now fill with black hunger, and existence scatters as ash on solar wind, the ruin declared entirely our own doing.","sonnet":"A Russian-language apocalyptic lament: humanity has betrayed its fathers' wisdom, and the planet burns on fire chariots while gods tear apart the rotting earth — the horizon is smoke, the eyes that once held love are filled with black hunger, and existence will scatter as ash on solar wind to spin forever in cold hell. The song is a collective indictment: this destruction is entirely our own doing.","src":"haiku"},
"the-last-martyr~like-a-ghost":{"haiku":"Emotional dissociation and internal collapse leave the speaker haunting themselves; failure breeds freedom while silence erases identity and possibility.","opus":"A struggle against emotional numbness and the fear of falling, set to Australian metalcore. The narrator wrestles with a mind burning its own bridges and a person frozen behind self-protection, ultimately reframing failure as the ground you rise from. It reaches for a fragile hope: to feel weightless and human despite the impact ahead.","sonnet":"A speaker caught between numbed existence and the terror of feeling: they want to float like a ghost but with a human touch, suspended between the two states. The bridge turns to self-address — stir it like a storm, how you fail is how you rise — but the fear of the fall and the silence that takes everything remains the dominant register.","src":"haiku"},
"the-last-martyr~xenox":{"haiku":"Alien otherness and existential displacement define the speaker; they cannot assimilate into normalcy.","opus":"A brief, moody piece about holding out against inevitable disillusionment while suppressing the dread of things falling apart. The narrator acknowledges everyone's private vices and urges against tears, framing survival as a skeleton dance in the moonlight, keeping motion even as the sky prepares to betray them.","sonnet":"A brief skeletal invocation — holding out against a deceiving sky, pushing darkness back, acknowledging vices without apology, and dancing like a skeleton to the moonlight. Its brevity is its character: a fragment of defiance without elaboration.","src":"haiku"},
"the-last-shadow-puppets~miracle-aligner":{"haiku":"A mysterious figure who grants wishes and fulfills desires appears as both miracle and seducer, rewarding vulnerability and knee-bending surrender.","opus":"Turner and Kane sketching a seductive fixer-figure, a fantasy wish-granter who can conjure whatever you desire. Beneath the doo-wop charm sits a sly portrait of the con man promising to make dreams come true.","sonnet":"A mysterious, larger-than-life figure is celebrated for his ability to fulfil any wish, navigate any maze, and blow minds with quiet confidence — the song circling him with admiring desire, half seduction and half awe.","src":"haiku"},
"the-last-shadow-puppets~my-mistakes-were-made-for-you":{"haiku":"Manipulation spreads through fame as the speaker's errors compound another's ruin, binding two souls together through shame and inescapable interdependence.","opus":"A cinematic meditation on fame corrupting a woman, putting words in her mouth and hardening her into something that stings. Beneath the orchestral sweep sits fatalism about crowds following each other blindly toward collective wreckage while dodging the blame.","sonnet":"A man reflects on the wreckage of a relationship shaped by fame, arrogance, and the way celebrity corrupts both the famous and those drawn to them. The woman is simultaneously a victim and an agent, bitten and transformed, winding herself around a crooked conscience that cannot fully account for what it's done.","src":"haiku"},
"the-libertines~up-the-bracket":{"haiku":"Shadow men pursue a bold figure across London, and two lovers exist in a separate world of mutual destruction that hovers between impossible closeness and estrangement.","opus":"Doherty and Barat's frantic London vignette of menace and betrayal, shadowy men offering money for a friend's address, wrapped in the intoxicated intimacy of two people lost in their own world.","sonnet":"A narrator is approached twice by threatening men looking for someone's address, defiantly refuses them both times, and watches a pair of people he cares about drift in a dangerous, oblivious world where closeness seems permanently out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"the-luka-state~liar":{"haiku":"The speaker transforms disdain into purpose, targeting the deceiver with surgical precision while recognizing their own complicity in the cycle of lies and pyromaniac impulse.","opus":"Contempt for a manipulative liar coasting through life, matched by the singer's own scheming resolve to expose and use them, one deceiver against another.","sonnet":"A speaker who has been manipulated and burned by a habitual liar refuses victim status — \"I'm a tryer\" positions them as the one steering toward the moment of reckoning, planning to start the fire that exposes what the liar has faked.","src":"haiku"},
"the-luka-state~oxygen-thief":{"haiku":"Someone embodies cold deception and emptiness, draining life from those around them like an oxygen thief.","opus":"The Luka State, a British rock band, pour scorn on a hollow manipulator, all show and nothing behind the eyes, whose chilly deceit stirs genuine fury. Refusing to let this parasite define or destroy him, the narrator escalates to a crude threat of violence. The song channels betrayal into a blunt, plainspoken kiss-off fueled by righteous anger.","sonnet":"Rage and revulsion build toward someone whose charm conceals total emotional parasitism. Beneath a confident exterior lies nothing but coldness and lies; the narrator, initially impressed and now filled with fury, refuses to be destroyed by the encounter — naming the other plainly as someone who takes life from those around them without returning a breath.","src":"haiku"},
"the-lumineers~stubborn-love":{"haiku":"Defiant attachment persists despite betrayal and incompatibility, drawing power from pain and refusing indifference as the only alternative to love.","opus":"Loving someone who lies, cheats and flees, and choosing to keep loving anyway because feeling pain beats indifference, which is the true opposite of love. The plea to keep your head up is stubborn devotion that refuses to leave the porch.","sonnet":"A declaration that feeling pain is better than indifference — the opposite of love — addressed partly to a self-destructive woman and partly as a general stance, calling on someone to keep their head up even when running away seems the only option.","src":"haiku"},
"the-lungs~cross-cult":{"haiku":"Burning religious symbols and scorning institutions; mourning the forgotten dead buried under dogma.","opus":"A furious renunciation of blind faith and the harm done in its name. The narrator burns the sacred symbol others hold dear, haunted by imagery of nooses and forgotten graves, condemning a cult that enslaves minds and souls. It is a bitter, exhausted refusal to keep mourning for a belief system built on rot.","sonnet":"An indictment of religious control as necromancy — nooses around the necks of rotting corpses, the symbol burned, forgotten graves with no mourning — and beneath the rage the cult's true mechanism is exposed: it claims your mind, your soul, forever, sells them, and calls the possession sacred.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~a-kdfptx":{"src":"web","web":"A confrontational assault on mass media and entrenched power: the narrator, always ignored, rages at the authority figures who dominate society, mocking mass media for dodging real issues ('vegetarian mass media') and positioning himself as an unstoppable agent of disruption who will drive the old order mad."},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~cistm-k0nfliqt":{"haiku":"System conflict and format clash; restart from zero and rebuild culture violently.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~fly-high":{"haiku":"Freedom through weightless flight across boundless sky; finding your own way without looking back at the world below.","opus":"A rush of exhilarated release, soaring weightless through an endless sky and refusing to be tripped up by the people or obstacles below, pressing forward until reaching whatever it is he seeks. The mind runs free and unburdened, happy simply to keep moving toward a future without limits.","sonnet":"Pure forward momentum through open sky, mixing English exhortations with a Japanese image of a legendary floating island, building a straightforward euphoria of release and self-directed flight toward whatever is still out there.","src":"haiku","fable":"What poses as a coaching chant — ignore the crowd, fix your eyes forward, keep gliding till the thing you want appears — turns out to be describing a chemical ascent, not a career one. The Japanese passage is where the mask slips: a window thrown open, a mythic island hanging in the air, a sunrise witnessed from impossible altitude, and the aside admitting the singer is somewhere strange, killing time, happy just to stay airborne. THE MAD CAPSULE MARKETS, whose digital-hardcore euphoria always ran on distortion and machine pulse, let the bilingual seam do the work: the pep-talk register and the trip are the same open sky, and nobody says the window is a metaphor.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","transcendence & consciousness","freedom & escape"],"means":"the pep-talk register and the trip share the same open sky","built":"the bilingual seam lets the Japanese passage do what the English coaching hides"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~gaga-life":{"haiku":"Revolution through radical sound in a corrupted world; meaningful transformation begins with violent artistic disruption.","opus":"A furious rallying cry framed as a wish to create something that means something, the singer summoning listeners toward a raucous, aggressive sound meant to overturn the music business and liberate them, in a world too broken to recognize its true adversary. The Mad Capsule Markets were a Japanese group fusing hardcore punk with harsh electronics, and this rides that chaotic energy, using sound itself as a weapon of freedom.","sonnet":"A call to wake up and let radical sound do what prose and politics haven't — the world is broken and pointing in wrong directions, but the right noise attacks violently enough to revolutionize the industry and set people free. The invitation is to walk toward that sound, get strong from it, and let the change start here.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~kami-uta":{"haiku":"Spiritual defiance and reclaimed agency—the narrator's voice becomes revolution against industrialization and spiritual corruption of Japan, transforming into raw power and song.","opus":"The Mad Capsule Markets pioneered Japan's digital-hardcore fusion, welding punk fury to industrial machinery, and that abrasion becomes the theme itself. A musician's voice is corrupted by an inorganic, impure world; the land of the rising sun once held divine light and sacred sound, now the narrator's voice sits polluted on tar. The refrain insists that raw force and cry can still be reborn as something real.","sonnet":"A song about a musician's voice being corrupted by an inorganic, impure world — the \"land of the rising sun\" once had divine light and sacred sound, but now the narrator's voice sits on tar, polluted; the refrain asserts that despite this, the singer's raw force and cry can still be reborn as something real.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~kumo":{"haiku":"Loneliness and isolation; disconnection from others is the central theme.","opus":"A quieter turn from the Japanese digital-hardcore band: a resolve to stop spitting words that hurt others and drift like clouds on the wind, finding that even long-familiar scenery isn't so bad once you soften and move forward.","sonnet":"A drifting cloud-like approach to life and connection — the narrator doesn't know where they've been or where they're going, but a burst of light pushed them forward; they want to meet people tomorrow, sing to someone specific, and stop spitting poisonous words, learning that unshed tears eventually spill as they stumble forward.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~midi-surf":{"haiku":"Distorted sound and hunger drive relentless transformation; you must struggle or get nothing.","opus":"A pounding rallying cry about drive and desire, hammering that the hungry earn nothing without effort. The narrator brags of a mind wired like a machine and swamped in overdriven noise, vowing to leave every rival behind on a day he feels unstoppable.","sonnet":"A high-energy declaration that effort is the only path to reward — the speaker boasts of mental overload and sonic chaos while insisting that those who are hungry must keep striving or get nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~multiplies":{"opus":"A garbled but earnest transhuman/evolution message about the next generation fighting to survive in a mad, uncertain, chaotic world; the narrator draws strength from successors and children who will grow and carry the power forward.","sonnet":"A garbled but earnest call for the next generation to fight and grow through the chaos of a mad world — the lyrics are partly machine-translated Japanese into broken English, but the core drive is evolutionary: our children must carry the power we couldn't.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~p-a-r-k":{"haiku":"Everyone builds walls around themselves and disputes what is correct; truth is questioned amidst collective negativity.","opus":"A jagged little fable about conformity, the narrator sealing himself off behind a barrier while a groundskeeper and squabbling crowds insist there is one proper approach, prompting the pointed challenge of whether being proper is the same as being right, before he is ordered to fall in line. The Mad Capsule Markets, a Japanese band blending punk, metal and electronics, turn social pressure into clipped, disorienting noise.","sonnet":"A parable about conformity and the pressure to stand in the approved spot — teachers laughing, authorities muttering that none of them are positive things, crowds arguing that their way is correct. The speaker builds an internal wall to survive it, and gently pushes back: try standing in there yourself, see how the truth feels from the middle.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~pulse":{"haiku":"Energy and collective action override hesitation; the future demands you move with conviction.","opus":"A frantic incitement to join the throng and act immediately, doing whatever you please without pause, setting your own terms and unlocking your thinking because tomorrow is within reach. It works as pure adrenaline rather than story, the Japanese digital-hardcore band firing off slogans of self-possession over relentless momentum.","sonnet":"A call to self-check, drop hesitation, and jump into collective motion — the crowd as engine, honest self-assessment as the only entry requirement.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~scary-delete-streamin-freq-from-fear-side":{"haiku":"Fear breeds conformity and terror in visualization; escaping the fear side means rejecting belonging.","opus":"A digital-age howl against a terrifying planet, chanting to erase a broadcast of dread while indicting nations, faiths, greed and history as engines of slaughter, demanding to know why people slay one another. The Mad Capsule Markets, Japanese cyber-metal pioneers, blast this global panic through machine-driven aggression, mantra-like repetition and glitchy hooks building a claustrophobic wall of terror.","sonnet":"A digital-age chant about purging the signal that keeps fear in circulation — the instruction repeated like a command line, the world outside described as terrifying precisely because belonging to it means being terrified. Global violence, religion, greed, and history are named as sources; the song offers no solution, only the demand to delete and the acknowledgment that the scary world keeps coming.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~tribe":{"haiku":"Sound vortex consumes thought; the destructive phenomenon generates affection from future emptiness.","opus":"A cryptic vision of a mind sucked into a whirlpool of noise that annihilates the tenderness rising from what's to come, fumbling out of a void toward some answer while a chant calls for fortifying the will. The Mad Capsule Markets, a Japanese cyber-metal act, splinter English and Japanese into deliberately fractured incantation, meaning half-submerged so the churning electronic pulse and tribal invocation carry as much weight as the words.","sonnet":"A person pulled into a vortex of sound inside their own head, where a destructive phenomenon destroys the capacity for genuine connection. Looking from one point to the next, the answer recedes into empty darkness; the call to the tribe is a call to justify and absorb rather than escape, and the strength to break free keeps slipping away.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mad-capsule-markets~w-o-r-l-d":{"haiku":"System collapse and frenzied energy release; the world destabilizes while people jump toward what's to come.","opus":"A digital-hardcore jolt built almost entirely on chant and command, staging the collapse of a cracked, failing world/system as something to physically freak out and jump to. The throughline is catharsis through the crowd: turning apocalyptic dread into a shared adrenaline release.","sonnet":"A two-word alarm about systemic collapse — the world is cracking and the system is failing — with the repeated call to jump functioning as both panic and anarchic release.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mars-volta~eriatarka":{"haiku":"Mutations, parasites, and strange plans involving stripped beings and cocooned prey. Dark forces with pulses and impulses have sinister intentions for humanity.","opus":"A feverish, deliberately unparseable nightmare of conditioning and dread — Pavlovian control, figures panting in patterns on respirators, some dark design being kept from us. The Mars Volta, risen from At the Drive-In, specialize in exactly this: hallucinatory images that never resolve into plain sense, a paranoid dreamscape whose logic stays thrillingly out of reach.","sonnet":"A hallucinatory account of surveillance, parasitism, and hidden plans — figures on respirators in basements, track-marked organisms landing craft, tapeworms kept as pets. The imagery circles a central fear: that something has been watching and preparing, that pulses were replaced by impulse, and that if you only knew what they had planned, escape would already be too late.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mars-volta~inertiatic-esp":{"haiku":"The song conveys profound disorientation and fragmentation. Disturbing imagery—lepers, dolls, broken sewers—paint a landscape of being lost with no path to clarity.","opus":"A disorienting spiral of a man declaring himself lost, surrounded by grotesque, feverish images of lepers, minced pupils and wolves hiding among men, warning that you will be the first and last to know before insisting you never will. Meaning dissolves into pure dread. The Mars Volta, the prog outfit born from At the Drive-In, built their 2003 debut on exactly this hallucinatory density, Cedric Bixler-Zavala's surreal poetry crashing against restless, explosive musicianship.","sonnet":"A hallucinatory encounter with figures who are all equally lost — lepers, wolves hiding among men, a mongrel architect of sewers, all dribbling the same confession. The speaker can't find their own bearings either, and the pink-eyed fountain at the edge of past, present, and future offers no navigation, only the assurance that you will never know.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mars-volta~wax-simulacra":{"haiku":"A person questions whether they are waiting and if their waiting matters, seeking something real made of freewill in a hollow world.","opus":"Doubt fractures the self amid surreal, feverish imagery, crawling ceilings, cold thread spinning answers, an avalanche of Toltec bones, the narrator howling to know if he is still waiting. The Mars Volta compress their prog-punk density into a rare two-minute burst here, the cryptic invocations and craving for freewill hurtling past before meaning can settle.","sonnet":"A restless, dissociative figure haunts a lover's space, oscillating between craving and passivity, ancient imagery and chemical hunger. The waiting itself becomes agitated — howling at its own suspension — while Toltec bones and contaminated desires circle without resolving into anything fixed or willed.","src":"haiku"},
"the-microphones~the-gleam-pt-2":{"haiku":"Witnessing beauty and future that others miss while struggling with the knowledge no one else can see.","opus":"Elverum's lo-fi mysticism: someone blinded by a false brightness who recovers their sight, set against a narrator who saw something truer in the dark. Quiet insistence that clarity comes from smaller, humbler visions.","sonnet":"A brief, elliptical vision — someone blinded then restored by a gleam of light, smoke returned to the sky, and the speaker glimpsing in sleep a future of tiny lights and mountain peaks that the other person cannot yet see.","src":"haiku"},
"the-midnight~america-online":{"haiku":"Connection through wires—tentative, questioning whether digital touch bridges distance or deepens isolation.","opus":"Synthwave nostalgia for dial-up intimacy, love reaching through wires and wondering if the spark on the other end is real or just a stranger. A meditation on connection and isolation in early internet romance.","sonnet":"The internet as a space of yearning connection that may be entirely illusory — the speaker reaches through wires hoping to feel a real heartbeat on the other side, unsure whether shared feeling online is genuine or whether everyone is simply a stranger.","src":"haiku"},
"the-midnight~deep-blue":{"haiku":"Stumbling into unexpected love in a casual moment, drowning in someone who wasn't sought but couldn't be refused.","opus":"A man who wanted only a distraction and got flattened by real love instead. Beneath the neon romance is the fear that opening up means drowning, and the surprise of doing it anyway.","sonnet":"An accidental love story — the speaker was only looking for distraction but found something oceanic instead, a connection that rewrote what he thought love was and left him falling into a depth he had never expected to want.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~20-eyes":{"haiku":"Multiplied sensory input leads to paralysis and cognitive overload; the accumulation of perception becomes a burden that crowds out human coherence.","opus":"Early Misfits horror-punk turning a B-movie conceit into a rush of sensory overload. The narrator, cursed with twenty eyes crowding his face, can't slow down a world flooding in from every direction. Danzig channels overstimulation and mutation into a catchy, campy metaphor for a mind unable to filter the chaos it takes in.","sonnet":"Twenty eyes in the head make it impossible to slow anything down — perception overloads the human face, crowding it until all those eyes are just a freak show surplus that the speaker both inhabits and watches from outside.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~all-hell-breaks-loose":{"haiku":"Chaos and violence are unleashed by the speaker as authored destiny, positioning wickedness and cruelty as singular authorial power over a world of victims.","opus":"A gleeful horror-comic boast from a cartoon villain reveling in gruesome cruelty, sending murder valentines to his young fans and declaring that no one but he could author such evil.","sonnet":"The narrator casts himself as the sole author of cruelty — sending murdergrams to monster kids that return signed in their parents' blood — while \"broken bodies in a death rock dance hall\" serve as his stage. It's a gleeful self-mythologizing of violence, as much performance as threat.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~american-psycho":{"haiku":"A Wall Street psychopath's cocaine-fueled mind spirals through obsessive violence and murder, sex and asphyxiation merging as the speaker becomes a machine of hatred and dismemberment.","opus":"Danzig-era horror-punk fused with Bret Easton Ellis: a Wall Street sociopath's cocaine-lined killing rendered as gleeful pulp, the misanthropy of a mind that treats other people as disposable meat.","sonnet":"A first-person homicidal narrator — Wall Street setting, cocaine, obsessive hands around a throat — delivers violence with clinical detachment and a recurring \"I hate people\" that reads less as motive than as ambient condition. The song invites the listener into the psycho's perspective (\"you're looking through the eyes of\") and refuses to step back out.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~astro-zombies":{"haiku":"Supernatural genocide at the speaker's command reduces humanity to meat and annihilation; the motivation is personal, the execution methodical.","opus":"A gleeful sci-fi horror fantasy of annihilation, drawn from the schlock cinema Danzig adored. The narrator orders his undead space troops to wipe out humanity with the brush of a scorching palm, reveling in apocalyptic ruin. Beneath the pulp lurks a needling question of whether the slaughter is for himself or someone else.","sonnet":"A mad scientist sends astro zombies with a prime directive — exterminate the whole human race — while faces melt and hearts pump to death. The lyric pauses to ask who this is even for, the creator or the creature, before returning to the slaughter.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~attitude":{"haiku":"Contempt and defiance define the speaker's posture, rejecting conformity through studied disdain.","opus":"An early Misfits blast of pure crude provocation, a snarling putdown of someone who mouthed off, trading in deliberate ugliness and menace more as attitude than argument.","sonnet":"A threat barely disguised as a song: someone said something that provoked the narrator's contempt for their \"fetal brain,\" and if they don't shut up they're going to meet the floor. The chorus loops the word \"attitude\" as both diagnosis and indictment.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~bloodfeast":{"haiku":"Consumption of human flesh and blood becomes ceremonial act of transgression.","opus":"Classic B-movie horror-punk staged as a splatter scene, insisting the gore is real precisely because you can't fake it, the singer relishing possession of a victim's body as grisly spectacle.","sonnet":"A horror-film narrator blurs spectator and perpetrator — severed heads, pulled tongues, blood that is \"just so real because you just can't fake it\" — possessing the victim's death, blood, tongue, heart. The repeated refrain insists on the authenticity of the carnage as the only thing that makes it valuable.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~bullet":{"haiku":"Kennedy's assassination becomes a tableau of sexualized degradation, mapping political violence onto gendered humiliation and forced oral subjugation.","opus":"The Kennedy assassination rendered as lurid, deliberately offensive punk provocation, mixing the shattered presidential body and Jackie's terror with crude sexual taunts to shock.","sonnet":"A lurid, deliberately transgressive retelling of the Kennedy assassination that collapses historical horror into sexual provocation — Jackie running across the trunk, Texas as culprit — culminating in graphic obscenity aimed at the widow. The shock is the point; the song weaponizes the most sacred American trauma as pure desecration.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~children-in-heat":{"haiku":"Innocence corrupted into sexual precocity marks transgression of childhood boundaries.","opus":"Danzig's leering take on adolescent sexuality as an uncontrollable, feral force, half-disgusted and half-fascinated, the narrator running from something young and burning that no one can rein in.","sonnet":"Out-of-control kids burning like kindling — \"cellophane sex\" as their new way of life, pissin' blood for a week, changing their names to Chicago — the narrator can only watch and run away, unable to resist or contain the feral energy radiating off them.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~cough-cool":{"haiku":"Urban decay and metaphysical corruption fuse with sensory overwhelm, the speaker retreating into darkness and bodily fluids as protest against walking the wasted street.","opus":"An early, murky reverie of a diseased, tilting street where one covers the face and coughs up blood, cutting visions with new eyes in a haze of decay and dread.","sonnet":"A corner soaked in piss and fear that tilts and slants, where vision is drenched in darkness and blood is coughed up — a brief, impressionistic portrait of urban decay and physical degradation observed with new, cutting eyes.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~death-comes-ripping-fox-studio-1983":{"haiku":"Violence arrives suddenly and completely, with death tearing through the social fabric.","opus":"A hardcore-era assault where death is personified as an unstoppable shotgun-blast force ripping through weak flesh, pure velocity and violence in the band's late thrash phase.","sonnet":"Death arrives with a shotgun blast and a demon piece of lead, ripping backs and burning through flesh too weak to withstand it — eyes open, waiting for the kill, the narrator positioned as both anticipator and instrument of the inevitable.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~descending-angel":{"haiku":"Fallen heavenly beings take up positions of defiance, guarding the gates of Hell against salvation and offering the condemned a brief alliance before dawn.","opus":"Cast-out heavenly warriors crash to earth and take up a last, hopeless defense alongside humankind, standing watch over the entrance to the underworld through one final night before all existence is snuffed out.","sonnet":"Fallen angels walk among mortals as keepers of the night, guarding Hell's gates through one final stand before dawn — because no savior has been sent, and mortal life is too brief and weak to hold back what's coming. \"Forever ends tonight\" gives the vigil an apocalyptic finality.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~devilock-fox-studio-1983":{"haiku":"Transgressive hairstyle and youth rebellion mark deviant identity and aesthetic defiance.","opus":"Named for the band's signature hairstyle, a blast of occult swagger about an eye of death and demon offerings, more a chant of horror-punk identity and menace than a story.","sonnet":"A ritual of extraction and domination framed around the Misfits' signature hairstyle as a sigil of death — hearts ripped out, eyes plucked, skulls open — the Devilock as both icon and act of desecration, hell pumping something into people who are made to pay.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~devils-whorehouse":{"haiku":"Sexual transgression and demonic corruption merge, with the speaker dwelling in carnal depravity.","opus":"Horror-punk sacrilege dressed as pulp seduction, a demonic brothel where sin is savored to the hilt, inverting religious imagery to relish carnality and blasphemy for their own transgressive charge.","sonnet":"A temple of carnality run by a demon who loves the sinful — angels fall into it, midnight masses are held, and the narrator sins \"real good\" and \"for sure\" every time. The whorehouse is Hell's sacred space, and the refrain is its liturgy.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~earth-a-d-fox-studio-1983":{"haiku":"Post-apocalyptic wasteland and eternal combat become the accepted reality of a new era, with death and sacrifice the price of survival in hell made manifest.","opus":"The title track of their fastest, ugliest record: a post-apocalyptic Earth after Death, where hell on earth is oddly welcomed and survival means a fight, sung with grim relish about the hills having eyes.","sonnet":"A post-apocalyptic travelogue set in Earth A.D. — year of blood, sacrifice, and displayed heads — where the hills have eyes and you bet your life on a fight you've already lost. \"Living hell is not so bad\" is the punchline to a world where death has simply been normalized.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~forbidden-zone":{"haiku":"Hidden origins and suppressed history mark a land where human evolution remains incomplete and guarded, with all evidence destroyed to protect the secret of our genesis.","opus":"A pulpy science-fiction scenario of vanished protectors slipping between realities into a barren wasteland where life never got going, the beginnings of humanity hidden away and every trace of that history wiped clean.","sonnet":"A Planet of the Apes riff about time-displaced travelers who return to an Earth where the evidence of what they've seen has been destroyed and the maps mark only danger zones — they are the lost, abandoned saviors of a forbidden zone that history refuses to acknowledge.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~great-balls-of-fire":{"haiku":"Desire overwhelms reason as love inspires urgent passion and nervous excitement, a lover driven to madness by attraction so powerful it shakes body and mind.","opus":"A horror-punk cover of the Jerry Lee Lewis rockabilly standard, keeping the giddy, nerve-shaking rush of desire but running it through the band's manic, sped-up snarl.","sonnet":"A cover of the Jerry Lee Lewis classic, performed faithfully — a man overwhelmed by sexual excitement to the point of nervous breakdown, nerves shaken, brain rattled, will broken, proclaiming ownership (\"your mine mine mine\") while barely holding himself together. The energy is pure rockabilly ecstasy, not analysis.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~green-hell-fox-studio-1983":{"haiku":"Environmental corruption and supernatural torment create hellish landscape.","opus":"A thrashing descent into a hell imagined as sickly green, part jungle-horror nightmare and part damnation, the narrator half-daring you to face the heat you woke and can't outrun.","sonnet":"A hallucinatory hell that is specifically, inexplicably green — the genie of death lives here, you thought you could escape but couldn't, and the narrator shakes apart in a heat that is both environmental and moral. The repetition of \"green hell\" as a mantra makes the color feel like a brand.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~helena":{"haiku":"Amputation and captive display are eroticized as the condition for love, surgical mutilation enacted as the cost of possession and erotic fantasy made flesh.","opus":"A slasher-movie fantasy of dismembering a lover to keep her, the narrator's fixation on possession curdling into surgical butchery while he keeps asking why she won't love him anyway.","sonnet":"An obsessive ask — would you still love me if I cut off your arms and legs, bound and displayed you? — that slides into surgical fantasy, the narrator's hands trembling over incisions as \"the beast\" closes in. The horror and the love song are the same thing here; Helena is asked to dance even as she's dismembered.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~hellhound-fox-studio-1983":{"haiku":"Demonic pursuit and predatory violence stalk the innocent, with evil incarnate seeking flesh and blood.","opus":"A snarling piece of demonic mythology, the narrator running with a pack of hellhounds born of hate, embracing damnation as belonging rather than punishment.","sonnet":"A hellhound born to Satan's pack is set loose to rip faces and tear through whatever stands in the way — those who seek it out and stand their ground are few, and the narrator knows he might already be dead. It's a short, raw invocation of belonging to hell's lineage.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~hollywood-babylon":{"haiku":"Hollywood is a pagan temple where bodily excess and carnal transaction replace authentic connection and genuine desire.","opus":"The Misfits leer at Tinseltown decadence, borrowing the title of Kenneth Anger's scandal-chronicle to paint Hollywood as a modern Babylon of sex, exploitation, and monstrous glamour. It is a lurid, fragmented snapshot of a place where fame and flesh are transacted, dripping with contempt for its seductive rot.","sonnet":"Hollywood as a decadent, closed gate — citizens kneeling for sex, flesh as ancient monster design — where you can't come inside and the spectacle of desire and exclusion loops endlessly. Babylon is the name for what happens when appetite replaces everything else.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~horror-business":{"haiku":"Threat and menace permeate domestic space, with the speaker wielding weaponized darkness as both warning and promise within the sanctuary of the bathroom.","opus":"Late-night dread staged like a slasher film, with menacing warnings about a knife and a bathroom no one may enter, the singer boasting no one does horror like he does.","sonnet":"A warning to stay out of the narrator's bathroom — black mirrors, late-night drives, the looming threat of a knife — framed as a psycho's private ritual that no one else is invited into. The refusal of entry is the whole drama.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~hybrid-moments":{"haiku":"Liminal states and dual nature coexist; the speaker embodies hybrid identity and contradictory impulses.","opus":"A tender monster-movie love song, quintessential early Misfits, where horror imagery becomes a metaphor for consoling someone in pain. As creatures rip and scars hide a face, the narrator simply asks for a fleeting moment of connection. Beneath the ghoulish surface it is oddly sweet, a plea for closeness amid catastrophe.","sonnet":"In the midst of horror-tinged chaos — creatures ripping faces, hybrid thresholds thrown open — the speaker turns to a fragile, crying figure whose beauty is flickering and momentary, hidden behind scars. The insistent demand for \"a moment\" reads as both a plea for intimacy and a desperate attempt to hold something human still inside the violence.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~i-turned-into-a-martian":{"haiku":"Alien possession and loss of identity through parasitic takeover, foreign consciousness wearing human form with predatory intent.","opus":"A B-movie alienation fantasy where transformation into a Martian stands in for losing your identity and humanity. The narrator can't recall his own name, sleeps poorly, and walks unnoticed among people while becoming inhuman inside. Danzig turns paranoid sci-fi into a catchy metaphor for feeling monstrously, irrevocably other.","sonnet":"Alien possession horror-punk — the body is no longer human, name forgotten, sleep denied, walking unseen through an unsuspecting city that is now his to own. The transformation is total and gleeful.","src":"haiku","fable":"Wearing its 1950s B-movie mask, this is a song about waking up as the invader in your own life, where the alien is not a creature from space but the version of yourself that has taken the controls. Danzig frames it as possession first, then transformation, and the tell is the amnesia at the center: the narrator can no longer recall his own name, so the horror lands not on the world he means to own but on the self that has been overwritten. The scaffolding is pure Walk Among Us drive-in schlock, but the panic underneath is dissociation dressed as science fiction, the recognition that the thing wearing your face and walking your streets is a stranger. That the final chorus dissolves into simply demanding to be told his own name is the whole thesis: the conquest is a bluff, and what actually terrifies is having become unrecognizable to yourself.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming","madness & the mind"],"means":"the conquest is a bluff — what terrifies is being unrecognizable to yourself","built":"B-movie drive-in scaffolding housing a panic about self-erasure"},
"the-misfits~london-dungeon":{"haiku":"Historical torture chamber becomes metaphor for urban captivity and medieval cruelty.","opus":"Written after Danzig and Doyle were jailed on a UK tour, a genuinely aggrieved gripe dressed as horror imagery, the band as walking corpses locked in British hell, homesick Yanks in misery.","sonnet":"American punks locked up in Britain, labeled \"walking corpses\" and \"unholy living dead,\" express pure resentment at being imprisoned in what they call British Hell — the London Dungeon — while the sarcastic sign-off \"here's hoping you're swell\" skewers the civility of their captors.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~mommy-can-i-go-out-kill-tonight-fox-studio-1983":{"haiku":"A child requests permission for murder, collapsing maternal authority and childhood innocence.","opus":"A bullied, straight-A misfit's fantasy of revenge curdling into serial murder, the mock-innocent request to a mother framing teenage alienation as a killer's origin story, horror as displaced rage.","sonnet":"A good-boy-gone-killer narrates his transformation from studious social outcast to serial murderer collecting toes and teeth as souvenirs \"for mommy's dream\" — the domestic framing is the horror: violence as a child seeking maternal approval, each murder a bid for belonging.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~saturday-night":{"haiku":"Murder and nostalgia intertwine in the speaker's reminiscence of an intimate past now poisoned by violent fantasy, loneliness crystallizing into predatory obsession.","opus":"A killer's twisted love song, where fond memories of cruising and the drive-in bleed into confessing multiple murders and watching a victim die, all wrapped in the ache of crying alone on Saturday night.","sonnet":"A narrator haunted by a girl he loved who may have been murdered — he recounts drive-in dates and running around together, then watches her \"go viciously, quietly away\" from a bedroom still smelling of cigarettes. Whether he killed her or simply lost her is left deliberately unclear, grief and guilt blurring into each other.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~scream":{"haiku":"Terror and vocal anguish express ineffable horror; sound becomes physical manifestation of psychological breakdown.","opus":"A predator savoring the terror he inflicts, a chill climbing the victim's spine as fear drives them both toward the edge of madness. The song makes dread itself the reward, dragging what's hidden out of silence until it finally cries out.","sonnet":"An unseen predator stalks someone through the cold and silence, describing the creeping fear — \"a chill runs up your spine, crawling to your brain\" — with relish, waiting for the moment the victim's composure breaks. The narrator's anticipation of the scream is the whole engine of the lyric: violation as spectacle, dread as pleasure.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~she":{"haiku":"A female figure embodies mystery and danger, with the speaker attempting to decode her nature.","opus":"Inspired by the 1970s Patty Hearst kidnapping and radicalization, a terse sketch of a rich girl turned gun-toting outlaw on the run, fascination with a woman no one can understand or control.","sonnet":"A woman who walks out with a machine gun and no explanation — good and bad simultaneously, a rich daddy's girl who loves naked sin and is losing control, running from something that's never named. She is perpetually on the run, and the song is content to observe rather than explain her.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~skulls":{"haiku":"The speaker collects severed heads as trophies of violent desire and possession, mounting human skulls as tangible proof of predatory conquest.","opus":"A gleefully macabre horror fantasy of collecting and mounting severed heads, craving skulls with the childish, relentless insistence of a monster-movie ghoul.","sonnet":"A pure horror-collector's fantasy: the narrator wants skulls on his wall, peels faces, hacks heads off girls to display them — framed as an extension of the Misfits' gore-fixated B-movie persona. The bathing in the devil's rain lends it a ritual quality beneath the Grand Guignol surface.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~some-kinda-hate":{"haiku":"Suffering and procreative failure define a species trapped in mechanical existence, agony and mutation replacing biological continuity.","opus":"A bleak little musing that affection and loathing coexist, spelled out through revolting pictures: infants in agony, and larvae trapped in a breathing machine, too sterile even to reproduce.","sonnet":"A meditation on the coexistence of love and hate so extreme it produces paralysis — the maggots won't copulate, the tortured babies cry — arriving at no resolution, just the repetition of \"woah\" as the only honest response to the spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~teenagers-from-mars":{"haiku":"Alien invaders exploit human weakness with indifference and contempt, dismissing civilization as inferior and vulnerable to their appetite for conquest.","opus":"The Misfits cast themselves as amoral alien invaders in a B-movie of their own making, an anthem of nihilistic teenage delinquency dressed as sci-fi. The refrain that they simply don't care becomes a punk credo, channeling adolescent apathy and appetite into a gleefully destructive fantasy of taking whatever they want.","sonnet":"Alien teenagers arrive on the Arizona plains in a B-film invasion, their cause unjust and ancient, seducing through the streets, unimpressed by human 3D movies and nuclear weapons. The conquest is hormonal and indifferent, and they don't care.","src":"haiku"},
"the-misfits~we-bite":{"haiku":"Vampiric predation and feral hunger define the speaker's identity, framing violence and bloodletting as natural law and ecstatic necessity.","opus":"A snarling vampire-punk anthem of predatory hunger, prowling the streets as a carnivore who rips throats to drink blood purely for pleasure.","sonnet":"Stripped-down predator anthem: the pack walks the streets, hunts for blood, rips throats — four words repeated with mounting insistence. There's no narrative arc, only the bare declaration of carnivore appetite, horror-film aggression delivered as a chant.","src":"haiku"},
"the-missing-peace~tao":{"haiku":"Breathe and disconnect, rejecting screen-mediated existence for embodied presence.","opus":"A call to unplug and inhabit your own life, breathing and presence set against screen-bound numbness, mortality invoked to sharpen the urgency. Feeling like yourself means leaving everything else behind.","sonnet":"An urge to stop running from life and return to the present — the narrator calls for stepping away from screens and distraction, sinking into physical sensation, and accepting mortality as the thing that makes presence urgent. It's about the peace available in simplicity before the end comes.","src":"haiku"},
"the-moth-gatherer~utopia":{"haiku":"A observer watching the collision of dying worlds, unable to return, burning endlessly as a finite source of light in a fractured reality.","opus":"The Moth Gatherer, a Swedish post-metal band, imagine a broken, submerged world where wealth and time run out and lifeless gazes witness a rising deluge. A child's eyes foresee the future as uncanny figures drift through strange light. Standing in the dark as worlds smash together, the speaker accepts being a dying star that has to burn out, making paradise inseparable from self-destruction.","sonnet":"A fractured world dissolves between spectral light and dark flood. Standing in ruins as realities collide, a figure who was present at some threshold moment recognises an unbridgeable distance from origin — and in the closing image, identifies as the sun itself, finite and burning, unable to return to anything that came before.","src":"haiku"},
"the-motion~where-is-my-mind":{"haiku":"Dissociation and mental escape from present reality, consciousness fractured and displaced.","opus":"A cover of the Pixies classic, carrying forward its dreamlike dissociation. Built around a snorkeling memory in the Caribbean, it renders the vertigo of a mind coming loose from its moorings, feet skyward and skull to the earth. Disorientation becomes strangely serene, a question with no answer floating out over the water.","sonnet":"Dissociation through a Caribbean swimming memory — a little fish trying to talk, animals hiding behind rocks, the mind gone way out in the water. The trick of feet in the air and head on the ground is both the question and the answer: the mind is wherever the body isn't, and the collapse is already in progress.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mysterines~hung-up":{"haiku":"Someone is emotionally manipulated and left in suspense by a cruelly indifferent lover, addicted to the pain of being held in limbo with false hope.","opus":"The cruelty of being strung along, wanting someone who keeps you hanging while hiding everything. Lia Metcalfe twists desire into menace, admitting she keeps trying partly because she likes watching him suffer as much as she suffers herself.","sonnet":"Stuck on someone who keeps her in suspense and never delivers — the narrator is trapped in her attachment, still reaching for someone who lies and withholds, admitting she watches them suffer and keeps trying anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"the-mysterines~life-s-a-bitch-but-i-like-it-so-much":{"haiku":"Paradox of addictive suffering: life imprisons through endless cycles, yet the speaker finds perverse joy in this bondage, unable to escape despite recognition of the trap.","opus":"A snarling admission of addiction to one's own bad patterns, dressing sin and self-sabotage up as good, sick of blame and repetition yet drawn back to the fix that ruins them.","sonnet":"A masochistic acceptance of life's cycle of pain and blame — things are always the same, mistakes repeat, yet the speaker refuses victimhood and clings to the fix with defiant pleasure. Life is cast as something that physically tightens its grip and strangles, but worth tasting despite it all.","src":"haiku"},
"the-naked-and-famous~spank":{"haiku":"Mental illness and panic spiral cyclically; avoidance and distraction temporarily suppress intrusive thoughts that inevitably return.","opus":"An unflinching account of living with intrusive, recurring mental illness, the narrator performing daily rituals to keep an unnamed dread at bay, feeling it loom into his head and swallow him whole while his brother suffers the same, both wanting only to make it stop. The Naked and Famous, a New Zealand synth-pop band, bury this genuinely dark portrait of obsessive fear beneath shimmering electronics, the brightness sharpening rather than easing the panic.","sonnet":"Living with a recurring anxiety or panic condition — the speaker has daily rituals just to keep it at bay, knows trying to remember the first attack only feeds it, and watches helplessly as it looms up again at night. A sibling is infected too, both of them screaming in their own way; the only relief is morning, and even that is just the temporary space before the next time.","src":"haiku"},
"the-naked-and-famous~young-blood":{"haiku":"Young lovers lie together under stars making promises, but the song captures the fragility of these bonds as they withstand jealousy, mistakes, and deterioration.","opus":"The Naked and Famous, the New Zealand synth-pop act, capture the turbulence of being young, admitting to inexperience, unstable moods, and a love that keeps breaking and reforming. Two people cling to each other and trade fragile vows they may not keep. As things fall apart, the hushed plea reframes forgiveness as a final chance seized before it's gone, tender and fleeting.","sonnet":"Two young people cling to each other across the bittersweet turbulence of early love. Naive and emotionally volatile, they lie under stars making promises to the sky while the relationship already shows its fragile seams — and the song holds both the sweetness and the foreknowledge that forgiveness will be required before it stabilises.","src":"haiku"},
"the-narrator~breach":{"haiku":"A person who has locked their inner world away and lost the key recognises that nothing will change until the wall is shattered — the breach itself is the necessary violent beginning of something new.","opus":"A reckoning with long-suppressed emotion and the walls built to avoid facing oneself. The narrator has locked a room in his mind and thrown away the keys, blindfolded against anything that pierces him, until he resolves to take a hammer to that wall. Dropping the mask, painful as it is, becomes a possible new beginning.","sonnet":"A wall built inside the head for too long, the keys thrown away — without the breach it will always stay like before, so grab the hammer and turn this wall to rubble of anger, because the curtains falling means the pretending ends and this might be a new beginning, the first honest one.","src":"haiku"},
"the-narrator~lucid":{"haiku":"Time runs out while promises decay into ash; the speaker endures pressure to accept nothing and face dissolution, while lovers betray and the populist lies crumble against isolation.","opus":"A vengeful reckoning with a divisive, populistic figure whose time is running out, the singer promising that this fall from grace will leave them exposed, isolated, and abandoned by everyone.","sonnet":"A lucid, confrontational speaker addresses a manipulative figure whose time is running out — \"all your lovers are dead\" and \"your populistic opinions\" strip the target down, while the speaker positions themselves as the one who will make sure those walls never let the target out.","src":"haiku"},
"the-narrator~no-answer":{"haiku":"Systemic oppression breeds madness and resistance; the speaker burns everything to ashes, refusing to play by rules designed for their destruction.","opus":"Metalcore built around being cast as the sickness others need to cut out, a paranoid, apocalyptic refusal to be numbed or silenced, growing in numbers and burning everything down when there's no cure and nothing that answers.","sonnet":"A speaker declared a sickness to be cut out by a superior authority — 'I am the sickness that is spreading out, there is no cure and no answer' — rages against numbing and suppression, vowing to burn everything rather than submit to the hysteria and regression of those who fear their growing numbers.","src":"haiku"},
"the-narrator~purgatory":{"haiku":"Liminal suffering and suspension in suffering; the speaker exists in intermediate torment.","opus":"A militant fantasy of uprising and revenge against tyrants, delivered with metalcore ferocity. The narrator rallies the oppressed to storm the fortress, defeat their oppressors, and revel in violence with the conviction that tonight they cannot die. It is bloodthirsty catharsis, framing rebellion as a descent into purgatory chosen willingly.","sonnet":"Insurrection against tyrants framed as righteous violence — torches at the fortress, bodies hung for show, skulls cracked open before the guns can be reloaded; the spark in the silence ignites an eye-for-an-eye reckoning. The final lines reveal the violence is also internal: \"we long for fucking violence\" is confession as much as war cry, bleeding into purgatory.","src":"haiku"},
"the-necromancers~the-needle":{"haiku":"Mythic voyage through supernatural threats where weavers guide mortals against pale forces that threaten unraveling reality itself. Acceptance of inevitable doom and collective survival through faith.","opus":"Occult doom spun into a seafaring myth, weavers stitching reality against a rising horde, freedom dying under fear until the black flag goes up and they stand unbroken as the story closes.","sonnet":"A mythic seafaring epic where a cosmic needle sews and unsews the fabric of reality to hold back pale, overwhelming forces. Sailors and weavers are summoned to resist the horde threatening to unravel the surface world, and only by raising the black flag and standing unbound can humanity survive the storm where nobody sails.","src":"haiku"},
"the-neighbourhood~devil-s-advocate":{"haiku":"Moral ambiguity and alienation define modern existence; the speaker plays the antagonist in a fraudulent world where nobody wins and authenticity becomes impossible.","opus":"Trading excess for a plainer domestic life, the singer plays the self-aware villain who knows he is a fraud and half a lost cause. It is about ironic self-condemnation, staying together for the kids while owning his contradictions.","sonnet":"The speaker trades status markers — whip for bike, designer for Nikes, stripper for wife — while insisting he moves at lightspeed and calling himself a lost cause (\"if a God is a dog, and a man is a fraud\"); self-deprecating and deliberately slippery, he positions himself as the devil's advocate for his own contradictions.","src":"haiku"},
"the-neighbourhood~you-get-me-so-high":{"haiku":"A friendship fractured by misaligned ambitions and unmet emotional needs becomes nostalgia: the speaker yearns for transcendence through closeness, but timing and self-obsession have already ended what might have been.","opus":"A bittersweet reckoning with a best friend or lover once taken for granted, wishing to leave the hurt behind and get high together on the closeness they never fully credited.","sonnet":"A fractured friendship or romance is revisited: the speaker acknowledges taking the relationship for granted, pursuing goals at the cost of the other person, yet still craves a reunion where they can reach a shared euphoria together — \"I wanna be high all the time, would you come with me?\"","src":"haiku"},
"the-new-age~outloud":{"haiku":"Honesty becomes the severance; the speaker refuses to fake recovery or belonging, choosing isolation over simulated wholeness.","opus":"The aftermath of a manipulative relationship, weighing wasted years and refusal to be simulated or controlled, unable to hate the person who lied even as the singer swears never to trust it again.","sonnet":"Feeling trapped and directionless after a relationship that wasted nearly half a life, the speaker cannot hate the person who lied to them but cannot bear to go back — the weight is lifted but the numbness and the going-nowhere remain.","src":"haiku"},
"the-new-eves~highway-man":{"haiku":"Transgression and predatory pursuit; a highwayman emerges to rob and seduce.","opus":"A retelling of the highwayman ballad, an outlaw riding to his waiting lover at the roadhouse, a crimson ribbon in her hair, until the law closes in and she fires the shot to warn or defend him, the lovers' blood spilling across the road in a doomed romance set against a sickened society.","sonnet":"A retelling of Alfred Noyes's highwayman ballad pushed into violence and flight: the outlaw's love for a woman at the roadhouse drives all action, she ties a blood-red love knot in her long black hair, shoots the authorities to warn him, and both pour their blood down the highway — love as outlawry, the state as predator.","src":"haiku"},
"the-northern~pale-horse":{"opus":"An anguished elegy for someone lost to addiction and suicide, watching them knock on death's door daily, the survivor wracked with helpless if-only guilt and grief, unable to eat, sleep, or breathe after failing to save them.","sonnet":"About watching someone die from addiction while being powerless to intervene — the narrator describes the faces turning pale blue as overdoses take people one by one, regrets not doing more, and the song is a grief-stricken admission that waiting for the sky to fall meant watching someone fade away instead.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-novembers~a-1vmra2w":{"opus":"The Novembers evolved from shoegaze noise toward stark philosophical songwriting, and this track thinks through violence and beauty by way of livestock and eating. Animals were killed not from weakness or hatred but hunger, sentimentality about cruelty dissolving into stomach acid, and within that hard clarity the narrator turns to a beloved, imagining a shared boat of life and finding beauty even in a plant's silent death.","sonnet":"A song that meditates on the nature of violence and beauty through the lens of livestock and eating: animals weren't killed because they were weak or hated, just because humans were hungry — sentimentality about cruelty dissolves into stomach acid — and within this the narrator turns to a beloved person, imagining meeting them on a boat of life, finding beauty even in the silent death of plants.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-novembers~a-rgpfjy":{"haiku":"Modern existence numbs sensitivity until only bare survival remains; ritual and routine erode authentic feeling.","opus":"The Novembers often map the slow anesthesia of modern life, and this song traces feeling peeling away in real time. A dog is caught immediately, rituals are understood but hollowed of meaning, and an insect's wing-buzz colonizes the mind, repeating until only bare daily routine survives, the band's cool detachment mirroring the numbness it describes.","sonnet":"Emotional numbness gradually taking over — a dog gets caught immediately, rituals are understood but emptied of meaning, an insect's wing-buzz colonizes the mind and keeps repeating until only daily routine survives and feeling has completely peeled away.","src":"haiku"},
"the-novembers~arlequin":{"haiku":"Ritual collapse—a body performs gladness while witnessing its own breaking. Internal ocean overflows; all that remains is the act of disposal, purification through emptiness.","opus":"A dark, surreal piece from the Japanese art-rock band, dense with unsettling imagery of cutting, empty houses, and casting a demon's corpse into an inner sea. Dreamlike and dissociative, it circles intimacy and revulsion at once, reaching toward a kiss and shared breath while everything else dissolves into ritual and decay.","sonnet":"A fractured, ritualistic interior: the speaker cuts a tube and learns something from inside another's skin, drowning demons in the sea of their own excess, assenting to a ceremony \"in front of 'say you were stripped'\" while blue dissolves in night foam — everything else is simply discarding the demon's corpse.","src":"haiku"},
"the-novembers~dysphoria":{"haiku":"Darkness descends as rationality dissolves; the speaker wants to erase themselves, applying digital obscurity to their own existence. Life becomes unbearable; only decay remains.","opus":"As its title signals, a claustrophobic descent into self-erasure and despair from The Novembers. The narrator lines up the foolish, hangs his heart out to dry, and feels his chest breaking as he considers pixelating himself out of existence. Repeated cries of darkness mark a paralysis between wanting to escape and simply discarding everything.","sonnet":"Someone in a suffocating, dying space urges their own exit — \"here, nothing can live and flowers wither\" — while being taunted by suggestions to apply a mosaic filter to their own face, cycling through dark repetition and asking whether there is anything left to do but simply throw everything away.","src":"haiku"},
"the-novembers~she-lab-luck":{"haiku":"Comprehension arrives too late; tears dry as understanding crystallizes that connection falters before it stabilizes. The speaker watches exit doors close, paralyzed by recognition.","opus":"An enigmatic study in disconnection where the narrator's clouded eyes dry out for reasons he can't name. Referencing a Charlotte Gainsbourg film as the moment of some bitter realization, The Novembers dwell on a door closed in the face, on expectation curdling into numbness while time ticks toward an unreachable resolution.","sonnet":"A speaker who is always the last to understand watches a door close in their face and, through a Charlotte Gainsbourg film, realises their clouded eyes have been slowly drying out — waiting until the clock hands touch — with the \"why\" left unanswered and the drying continuing in endless repetition.","src":"haiku"},
"the-novembers~tetsu-no-yume":{"haiku":"Tomorrow remains unknowable; philosophy crumbles against repetition. The speaker dismantles false hope, rejecting comparison and escape, accepting stagnation as permanent condition.","opus":"A caustic, philosophically dense screed against hollow rebellion and tribal pettiness. The Novembers rail at hoops lowered until they aren't worth clearing, dreams dimmed to something catchable, and a world so full of loyalty tests there's no place to stand. It skewers the grass-is-greener bickering that masquerades as revolution.","sonnet":"The present moment is absolute — no one has seen tomorrow's sunrise yet — while domesticated karma pleads for mercy as it is cut apart in today's real reckoning; revolutions that are merely lateral movement are mocked, dreamlike superiority claims dismissed as irrelevant, and the only activity left is the relentless cutting-through.","src":"haiku"},
"the-number-twelve-looks-like-you~gallery-of-thrills":{"opus":"A carnival-barker satire presenting humanity itself as a freakshow exhibit, spotlighting money's control over people, human self-importance, misogyny, and trophy hunting, and concluding the barker feels safer among aliens than among his own dying species.","sonnet":"A carnivalesque alien tour guide presents humanity to extraterrestrial visitors as a grotesque exhibit: a species that thinks it owns a planet it belongs to, that kills for sport and enforces gender hierarchy — the tour ends with the guide wishing to be beamed away from this absurd, dangerous gallery.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-nychillharmonic~mean":{"haiku":"One person split themselves open to reveal inner conflicts, yet the other remains cruel; the speaker is repeatedly hurt and deceived in this toxic dynamic.","opus":"The band builds big, brassy, orchestral-tinged art-pop around an intimate accusation, its speaker begging a cruel partner to reveal the private battles fueling their coldness. Forcing herself to confront the harm, she pivots to the ache of an isolation she half-chose, daring the other to own their deceptions, the looping refrain swelling a personal wound into an anthem.","sonnet":"Demanding that someone show the internal wars they've been concealing behind cruelty — rip yourself open, be legible, stop using meanness as armour. Underneath the accusation is a need to be told the loneliness had a reason, and a suspicion that the other person only performs warmth when no one else is watching.","src":"haiku"},
"the-obgms~same":{"haiku":"A relationship fractures under the weight of miscommunication and emotional stagnation as both partners deteriorate in isolation. Separation becomes necessary though perhaps too late to save either soul.","opus":"A desperate cry from within a crumbling romance, the narrator asking whether his partner still feels what she once did. Anxious and suspicious, he frets about being swapped out or wonders if he simply has to improve himself, arriving wearily at the idea that the two of them should split up for a while before the chance to fix things is gone.","sonnet":"A relationship fracturing under paranoia and distance — the narrator feels chains of helplessness, suspects they're being replaced, stutters through the fear of someone needing a better version of them, and keeps asking whether it's already too late.","src":"haiku"},
"the-oblyvion~praying-mantis":{"opus":"An apocalyptic reflection on humanity as a self-destroying virus that has degenerated and doomed itself, the narrator awakening to build a new reality with a chosen few amid the voices of a dying world.","sonnet":"About humanity as a self-destructive virus degrading the planet — like losing Atlantis, like the declining praying mantis, the human race is remembered face to face with what it has done to the world, and the narrator has finally woken up to this extinction-level reality.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-ocean~firmament":{"haiku":"Celestial lights divide chaos from order, marking seasons and years through divine arrangement, creating structure within cosmic vastness.","opus":"A near-verbatim setting of the Genesis creation account of the fourth day, God dividing light from darkness and filling the firmament, with one dry aside that this is only what some people believe.","sonnet":"A near-verbatim setting of Genesis 1, days two and five, tracing God's division of waters by a firmament and the subsequent creation of sea creatures and birds — the qualifier \"or so some people believe\" inserted at the close marks the text as received mythology rather than literal fact.","src":"haiku"},
"the-ocean~parabiosis":{"haiku":"Medical immortality becomes existential curse: life extension severs from meaning, creating a hollow godhood where youth without consequence breeds only spiritual emptiness and moral decay.","opus":"Progressive-metal essay on transhumanist immortality, warning that a culture obsessed with youth and defeating death has traded the sacred and the earthly for a sterile, bleak eternity where we've become the walking dead.","sonnet":"A critique of humanity's obsession with immortality and life-extension technology — parabiosis, stem-cell regeneration, and eternal youth are presented as a denial of earthly limits that ultimately bleaches life of meaning. The song asks how dead, bleak, and safe we become when death is erased from our days, concluding that paradise is now embedded in earthly life itself rather than projected to heaven.","src":"haiku"},
"the-ocean~preboreal":{"haiku":"Spectacle and image replace reality as critical thought disintegrates; the speaker watches impoverished consciousness spread like plague while truth drowns in undifferentiated digital mass.","opus":"Drawing on Debord's spectacle, a lament that image-saturated modern life has cut us off from reality, hollowing out existence as we embrace the slow rotting of knowledge and independent thought.","sonnet":"A text drawn from Debordian theory argues that image-mediated society has collapsed present time into an undifferentiated mass, impoverishing the quality of life by degrading knowledge and critical thought until the spectacle — a social relation, not a cluster of images — is all that remains.","src":"haiku"},
"the-ocean~triassic":{"haiku":"A sole survivor walks a desolate wasteland alone, carrying a torch through barren landscapes where communication and connection have utterly failed.","opus":"A cold breakup framed in clinical, corporate jargon, dismissing a partner's condescension and false modesty, then panning out to a sole survivor wandering a dead, desertified world.","sonnet":"A breakdown in communication between two people collapses into a barren, post-catastrophe landscape of dead trees in seas of sand, where one figure wanders alone as sole survivor carrying a flame forward — the dysfunctional relationship mirroring the geological desolation of the title epoch.","src":"haiku"},
"the-odious~mono-no-aware":{"haiku":"Impermanence and acceptance of decay offer strange comfort; the speaker surrenders to transformation, finding peace in inevitable dissolution.","opus":"An embrace of impermanence in the Japanese spirit of mono no aware, accepting sorrow and decay because joy springs unexpectedly from the rot and each self must fall away to make room for the new.","sonnet":"Directly inspired by the Japanese concept of mono no aware — the bittersweet awareness of impermanence — the speaker accepts that joy springs from decay, that sorrow is necessary, and that falling away is not failure but the natural yielding to what comes next.","src":"haiku"},
"the-offering~failure-s-o-s":{"haiku":"Anxiety becomes the landscape where the speaker dwells, indebted and enslaved by internal voices that refuse silence or redemption despite desperate pleas.","opus":"A cry for help from a man broken by soul-crushing work and panic, certain he is worthless and would rather die than owe anything more to the tormenting thoughts in his skull. The imagery of a false face and a whispered greeting to the devil turns his despair into a pact with damnation.","sonnet":"A despairing cry from someone who feels enslaved by meaningless work and social expectation — unable to find a reason to keep going, flirting with the idea that death might be preferable, reduced to using anxiety as the only sensory proof they still exist.","src":"haiku"},
"the-offering~ultraviolence":{"haiku":"Society's violent structures and hidden truths turn beautiful things dark; the narrator knows secrets others deny and speaks of forbidden knowledge masked by societal pretense.","opus":"Borrowing Burgess's coinage, a descent into violence blamed on society rather than the self, the demons and desire that bloom too soon. It plays the seductive whisper of a corrupting influence promising to give you exactly what you crave.","sonnet":"A fragmented study of dissociation and violation — the narrator distances themselves from their own victimhood by blaming society, while wrestling with desire, shame, and a secret knowledge that separates them from the world.","src":"haiku"},
"the-offspring~self-esteem":{"haiku":"A person tolerates abuse and betrayal from a partner, convinced that suffering proves love, confusing pain with devotion.","opus":"A doormat narrates his own humiliation with rueful clarity, taking back a woman who uses him, sleeps with his friends, and shows up drunk to score, all because he has no self-worth to defend. The Offspring turn this pathetic loop into a giant Smash-era singalong, the la-la hooks making the self-aware surrender both funny and genuinely bleak.","sonnet":"A man consciously participates in his own humiliation, returning to a manipulative partner again and again with full awareness of the dynamic. Each act of self-betrayal is narrated with sardonic clarity, the speaker understanding he is being used while treating the suffering itself as proof of the depth of his feeling.","src":"haiku"},
"the-oklahoma-kid~dye-black-to-pink":{"haiku":"Personal metamorphosis sheds trauma's darkness through deliberate colour-shift. Fear dissolves when embraced directly, allowing reinvention beyond the weight of predecessors' judgement.","opus":"Metalcore about shedding a bleak past and refusing to lose yourself again, using the shift from black to color as a vow to face fear, own the change, and become something even a doubter would respect.","sonnet":"A declaration of personal transformation — the speaker rejects the dull grey palette of their fearful past and chooses to dye it pink, leaving mistakes and self-loss behind. The colors of the old life are explicitly named as black, grey, and white, and the act of changing them becomes an act of defiance against fear.","src":"haiku"},
"the-oklahoma-kid~pale-tongue":{"haiku":"A speaker claims truth and authority while admitting doubt; others blindly follow their words, mistaking arrogance for wisdom.","opus":"A tirade tangled up in truth, self-doubt, and manipulation: the narrator holds fools in contempt and insists on his own moral certainty, yet confesses he doesn't recognize who he's turned into. He warns that deception dulls the mind and that anyone who takes his words on faith is too blind to notice it might all be false, deliberately muddying who is deceiving whom.","sonnet":"A person wrestling with whether they have been truthful or deceptive — cycling between claiming to be right and admitting to being wrong — ultimately arriving at the recognition that they have been lying to others and to themselves, and that the pattern has to break.","src":"haiku"},
"the-old-dead-tree~the-end-again":{"haiku":"Final collapse and cyclical finality return; the speaker surrenders to repeated endings and inevitable termination.","opus":"An elegy for a friendship ended by death, from the French metal band whose own history was shaped by losing a member. Addressed to a brother named Fred, it mourns the end of standing shoulder to shoulder, of meals split and pain weathered together, framing the loss as a funeral where the living must inter part of themselves alongside the dead.","sonnet":"A final farewell to a band or friendship — shared bread, endured pain, dried rain — and the address to Fred in the closing verse makes it intimate and specific: a brother who was afraid to let his love rest alone, now being laid to rest himself, in a water field of buried souls.","src":"haiku"},
"the-oral-cigarettes~don-t-you-think-feat":{"haiku":"Doomed animal intimacy using bee imagery, love stinging and lingering even as summer ends.","opus":"A Japanese rock ballad using a bee, drawn to and stung by love, as its image for a doomed romance: heartbeat-deep longing, a summer that ends with someone's departure, and the rueful settling for second place beside them.","sonnet":"A song about a love that burned too hot and then misdirected: the narrator watched a bee, prayed to confirm a shared love, felt the first heartbeat — but their sharpness wounded the other person, regret came after, and the object of feeling drifted toward another destination; what remains is clinging to an image of summer and a person who maybe deserved to be second-place all along.","src":"haiku"},
"the-oral-cigarettes~kyouran-hey-kids":{"haiku":"Youthful madness rebels against closed doors and meaningless conflict; raw desire and chaos erupt against societal constraint in search of authentic connection.","opus":"A frenzied anthem of youthful chaos and rebellion from the Japanese rock band, best known as an anime opening. It urges kids to go mad, torch pointless ego and empty conflict, and light up a sealed-off yesterday. Beneath the delirium runs a plea for connection, a wish to simply hold hands amid the fervor.","sonnet":"A Japanese punk rallying cry to mad, aimless kids — illuminate yesterday's locked doors, search for the place you can't return to, throw your useless ego away and fight a meaningless fight. The repeated \"just wanna hold your hands\" cuts against the chaos as the real thing underneath the frenzy: connection, not destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"the-oral-cigarettes~machinegun":{"opus":"Alienation and lost glory in a numbed generation, the narrator clinging to someone amid grunge-gray days and unprecedented disaster. The Oral Cigarettes frame loyalty as a fired bullet running parallel, faith placed in a bond that endures collapse. Beneath the frenzy runs a wish to be found as one's true self and pulled close.","sonnet":"In Japanese: about emotional numbness and disconnection in relationships — the narrator fires emotion like a machine gun in parallel lines, unable to truly reach people, living in a chaotic personal disaster while searching for genuine connection and their true self amid the noise.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-orielles~sugar-tastes-like-salt":{"haiku":"Interrogating attraction to someone beautiful and dark, unsure if they will grow into better or worse.","opus":"An attraction that curdles as the narrator realizes the change she wants isn't in him but in herself. Beauty read as something dead behind the eyes, and isolation reframed as room to create.","sonnet":"A speaker studies a person who is visually compelling but somehow inhuman — testing them, sensing the horror beneath the glamour, and retreating into isolation and the creative space that opens when she stops trying to make him make sense.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pale-white~never-enough":{"haiku":"A consumer constantly craves more wealth and material excess, acknowledging that accumulation destroys meaning and cannot satisfy endless hunger.","opus":"Newcastle trio The Pale White turn consumer appetite into a self-destructive high, the narrator craving luxury labels and premium excess, equating riches with wellbeing and demanding more that never satisfies. Vanity capsizes into numbness while the small comforts drown, the hunger built so it can never possibly be filled.","sonnet":"A portrait of insatiable consumption: designer goods, Black Friday binges, and top-shelf self-medication accumulate without satisfying anything. The speaker acknowledges the absurdity of chasing wealth as a substitute for health or meaning while still demanding more, aware the cycle is both futile and inescapable.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pillows~rush":{"haiku":"The song describes emotional disconnection and erosion in a relationship. Despite the constant rain and fear, the narrator seeks to break through a partner's emotional walls and find freedom together.","opus":"A gentle rescue offered to a woman hollowed out and changed by a cold new city, the singer asking her to share her loneliness so he can help carry it, wanting the old her back and coaxing her to walk unafraid through unceasing rain toward freedom. The blue gap in her frozen sky is the one glimpse of hope. The Pillows are a long-running Japanese alt-rock band, famous abroad for the FLCL soundtrack, and this carries their warm, wistful melodicism.","sonnet":"Knocking desperately on a closed door for someone who has gone hollow — she can't remember the last time she laughed, she's a shell in a changed city, and the speaker wants to bring her back to herself, to show her she is free, to walk with her through rain that won't stop. A crack of blue is glimpsed in her frozen sky, and the song ends as a promise to carry her toward it before the adult sadness wins.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pinballs~a-148wf8u":{"haiku":"Girls become witches on Broadway; poison and razors symbolize the beauty and cruelty inherent in performance.","opus":"The Pinballs channel a garage-rock theatricality with noir undertones, and this portrait dresses that in Broadway greasepaint. A performer transformed into a witch by the city's cycles of arrival and decay sings like a caustic drug, cuts rainbows like a razor, poisons the gentle breeze and shoots through chests without a sound, knowing the curtain's fall returns her to wandering alone.","sonnet":"A performer on the Broadway stage who has become a witch through the city's cycles of arrival and decay — singing like a caustic drug, cutting rainbows like a razor, poisoning the gentle breeze, shooting through chests without a sound — knowing that after the curtain falls she will wander alone again.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pineapple-thief~3000-days":{"haiku":"Over 3000 days together, a couple barely made an impact or found time for each other, wasting precious life while running in circles too tired to truly connect.","opus":"A subdued elegy for a long relationship squandered on wheel-spinning and neglect, the narrator confessing he never really showed up for the other person and let years slip past unused. It lands as regret and apology at once, a wish to have brought happiness paired with the quiet recognition that the chance for love went by unclaimed.","sonnet":"About a long relationship defined more by absence and distraction than connection — roughly eight years spent circling each other without making a sound, wanting to make the other person happy but barely finding time before it's wasted.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pineapple-thief~alone-at-sea":{"haiku":"Two people locked in a suicidal pact at sea, holding onto each other as they knowingly surrender to death together without caring what lies beyond.","opus":"Bruce Soord's prog-rock vehicle drifts toward a shared oblivion, two people knowingly choosing to end their lives together and treating it as strangely intimate. With minds dulled and indifferent to whatever comes after, they clasp hands for the plunge, the band rendering mutual surrender as a defiant, hand-in-hand descent rather than pure despair.","sonnet":"Two people adrift choose destruction together over surviving apart. Drug-softened and untethered, they dismiss the future with mutual shrugs and take each other's hands at the edge — the shared suicide reframed not as tragedy but as the only honest act left when everything outside them has already ceased to matter.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pineapple-thief~barely-breathing":{"haiku":"One person watches another dream, haunted by memories of golden days and the failure to give enough—a regretful, suffocating vigil of insufficient love.","opus":"The British prog-rock band favors restrained, melancholy intimacy in this deathbed vigil, its narrator lying awake beside a sleeping love and confessing across a lifetime that he never expressed or offered enough to repay them. Memories of brighter shared days surface as the world dims, letting quiet regret and the dread of loss carry the song.","sonnet":"Lying awake beside a sleeping partner, holding them while reviewing years of insufficient words and insufficient effort. The memory of lying on golden beaches surfaces as evidence of what was possible — but the speaker couldn't see past it to what the world would eventually take, and the regret for everything never said quietly fills the room.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pineapple-thief~break-it-all":{"haiku":"One person repeatedly destroys relationships and then damages what remains with someone new; the song forbids this pattern of breaking and moving on.","opus":"In their Gavin Harrison-era art-rock restraint, the band lets quiet accusation carry the hurt as the narrator confronts a partner seduced by a flattering self-image. He denies them the right to wreck everything, then move on and wreck more with someone else, wondering what part of them must be destroyed before any real love or remedy can surface.","sonnet":"A person confronts a partner who destroyed what they built together and then moved on to repeat the damage with someone else. The anger is quiet and certain: leaving does not earn the right to start fresh destruction elsewhere. What version of oneself survives the wreckage is the unresolved question.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pineapple-thief~give-it-back-rewired":{"opus":"A plea to a distant partner, offering to surrender the relationship and return everything they shared, obsessively repeating the promise to give it back while grasping at the memory of first intimacy.","sonnet":"A breakup song in which the narrator surrenders everything — giving back not just possessions but the emotional investment of the entire relationship — while questioning what the other person actually wants, including whether they want the original spark of intimacy restored.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-pineapple-thief~nothing-at-best":{"haiku":"Emotional emptiness and the burden of a relationship where one person offers nothing of value and brings only pain and death.","opus":"A confession of guilt from someone who admits he gives a partner nothing to hold on to yet urges them to run for their life even while asking them to stay. He accepts blame and vows to carry the weight of the relationship as long as he is allowed.","sonnet":"A confession of emotional unavailability: the narrator admits he has given a partner nothing to hold onto, tells her to run even as he asks her to stay, and accepts that the damage he has caused is his to carry.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pineapple-thief~our-mire":{"opus":"A relationship sinking like a doomed ship, with the narrator refusing to accept the collapse he half-caused; torn between the instinct to jump first and survive versus going down with it, mourning the love that once gave his life purpose.","sonnet":"The moment a relationship finally capsizes — the narrator didn't want to see it coming but watched their shared life sink anyway, kneeling on the shore wondering what purpose either of them served, and whether the wreck was anyone's fault or simply inevitable.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-pineapple-thief~preparation-for-meltdown":{"haiku":"Intrusive thoughts swarm and burrow within; a person struggles against external pressure attempting to breach their mind. The song portrays psychological invasion and fighting to maintain silence and stillness.","opus":"Prog-rock claustrophobia of a mind under siege, intrusive thoughts swarming and burrowing until the self starts to fracture. The quiet horror is submission, the point where you stop resisting the breakdown you feel arriving.","sonnet":"About intrusive thoughts or a toxic presence that burrows inside the narrator's mind, throttling their soul — a claustrophobic, skin-crawling feeling of being unable to say no to something swarming inside them.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pineapple-thief~show-a-little-love":{"haiku":"Walls close in and partners struggle to communicate love and support; the narrator needs someone to show care and bring them back when they are down and taking the wrong way.","opus":"The British prog band voices a plea for closeness as everything tightens around a couple, a narrator who needs to depend on someone and begs his partner for affection before the moment passes. Their restrained melancholy suits the confession of steering things off course, the repeated request to be knocked back to his senses reading as a muted call for rescue.","sonnet":"Two people are heading the wrong direction together, walls closing in, and one reaches toward the other asking for basic human tenderness. The request is simple and urgent — lean on me, show some love, pull me back around — because without that small gesture the wrong way down becomes the only way.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pineapple-thief~so-we-row":{"haiku":"Two people seek escape from the world and their pain, rowing together to find a place of silence and rest where only they exist.","opus":"The British prog outfit built around Bruce Soord distills longing into something almost monastic: a narrator desperate for a break, a taste, a quiet place beyond the reach of noise and violence. Rowing becomes the escape mechanism, a slow paddle toward a promised land where nothing beats anyone down. The restraint typical of the band's atmospheric writing keeps the yearning weightless rather than histrionic.","sonnet":"A man desperate for rest and escape invites someone to flee with him — rowing toward silence, toward a place where nothing crowds or beats them down. The gesture is tender and protective, a shared retreat from exhaustion.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pineapple-thief~someone-here-is-missing":{"haiku":"The narrator regrets not showing love; someone remains in their heart but distance persists—no one listens and someone is missing.","opus":"Grief settles over a loss as the narrator tallies the things he ought to have battled for and expressed, admitting that simply carrying someone privately was the coward's shortcut. The Pineapple Thief, the mournful prog outfit led by Bruce Soord, lets the question of whether anyone hears him repeat until absence saturates the entire track.","sonnet":"Grief at a distance — the speaker kept someone in heart rather than presence, chose the easy interior over the harder act of showing up, and now faces an absence no one around them seems to register. A quiet reckoning with how love held back becomes a person lost.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pineapple-thief~the-state-we-re-in":{"haiku":"A speaker refuses to surrender and fights to endure despite pain, holding onto a relationship across time while suffering marks both people. The song asserts determination through deterioration.","opus":"A ballad of stubborn devotion amid decay, the narrator refusing to relent, swearing to battle to the finish and burrow toward the heart of things while surveying their shared condition. Wanting to grasp the ache inside the other and pledging to hold them close as time drains away, it treats endurance and closeness as a way to cling on even as everything erodes.","sonnet":"A terse, intense song about holding on to someone through suffering — digging down to the core of a painful relationship and choosing to stay, to hold tight, even as the years drain away.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pineapple-thief~wake-up-the-dead":{"haiku":"A speaker confronting someone unresponsive to their attempts at connection, haunted by trauma—specifically a suicide that has metaphorically washed ashore—while trying to penetrate the other's emotional silence.","opus":"Addressed to someone who once protected the speaker but has since changed, the song fixates on that person's suicide washing up at the speaker's door and a desperate, obsessive urge to get inside their head.","sonnet":"A speaker grapples with the aftermath of a loved one's suicide, vowing to understand what was in that person's head and somehow reach them even across death.","src":"haiku"},
"the-plot-in-you~crows":{"haiku":"A self-destructive person is haunted by addiction and regret, losing family and spiraling while knowing crows wait to take them.","opus":"A blunt spiral through addiction and self-hatred, the narrator strung out and chasing his next high until he's paralyzed, having wrecked his family's love and faith over ugly threats. Circling scavengers stand in for death he can't outrun. This Ohio band delivers early, abrasive metalcore, an unsparing self-portrait of a man falling apart and fully aware of it.","sonnet":"A self-portrait at the bottom — broken relationships with both parents, Adderall-caked and drink-sunk, running on addiction and a crooked smile, the speaker knows the crows are waiting and there is no exit from the place he's made of himself. The refrain carries no hope of rescue, only the honest acknowledgment that the trap is real and he walked into it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-plot-in-you~feel-nothing":{"haiku":"The narrator has buried feelings for someone who won't change; it's too late for reconciliation and emotion has died.","opus":"The terminal exhaustion of a relationship is stated plainly, buried feeling now evident, the partner unwilling to change, the narrator swinging between numbness and a hated longing to feel what he once did. The Plot In You build their post-hardcore dynamics around that verdict of emptiness, the confession of feeling nothing landing as both release and quiet devastation.","sonnet":"A relationship reaches total emotional exhaustion after years of deflection and blame. The speaker reaches the point of complete detachment, unable to summon feeling for the other person — not as cruelty but as fact — and delivers this verdict clearly, knowing it will not be accepted or believed.","src":"haiku"},
"the-plot-in-you~pretend":{"haiku":"Performance of authenticity and false presentation masking deeper truth, the speaker maintaining facade.","opus":"A scathing indictment of collective denial and numbed-out complacency. The Plot in You target the way people anesthetize their pain, fantasize about tomorrow, and look away from suffering, whether their own or a starving child's. Everyone just pretends, racing to escape a reality no one wants to face or take responsibility for.","sonnet":"Collective denial as survival strategy — artificial pleasure numbing pain, trauma kept discreet, children starving while spectators feed, the soul borrowed and whored away. The indictment is total: no one faces the damage, everyone races to escape facing nothing, and the cycle begins the only way it knows how.","src":"haiku"},
"the-plot-in-you~rigged":{"haiku":"The speaker towers above their detractors yet no one questions their authority despite never facing real tests. They demand recognition for work others can't see, frustrated by the slow, close-minded world.","opus":"The Plot in You channel injured defiance from a man labeled unmanageable, outmaneuvering those who wasted their opportunities and refusing to let dishonest people drain the vitality out of him. Demanding again and again to be genuinely acknowledged, he vows to begin afresh each morning until it's flawless. The metalcore band frame self-justification as a draining struggle against being overlooked in a stacked game.","sonnet":"Suppressed ambition erupts against systems that undervalue what stands plainly in front of them. The narrator is done proving himself to people who would rather impose limits than acknowledge capability; the rant turns briefly inward — questioning what remains — before reasserting that close-minded gatekeeping will cost them more than it costs him.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~be-my-girl-sally":{"haiku":"A lonely man finds companionship with an inflatable doll, celebrating his unconventional relationship with humor and affection.","opus":"A jokey spoken vignette about a lonely man who orders an inflatable doll from a magazine and installs her as his contented wife. Black comedy about isolation and how far someone will lower the bar to avoid being alone.","sonnet":"A lonely, insomniac man orders an inflatable doll from a magazine ad, inflates her, and proceeds to treat her as a wife — bouncing her through the house, worrying she might go thin. The song is deadpan black comedy about loneliness, wish-fulfilment, and the absurdity of substitute intimacy.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~bring-on-the-night":{"haiku":"Someone trapped in daylight seeks darkness and night, exhausted by the brightness that blinds him to any future; he repeatedly refuses to endure another hour of day.","opus":"From the early era of Sting's trio, a reggae-inflected new-wave piece that aches for nightfall as the day drains away and the singer lets go of the past. Unable to endure any more daylight and blinded by its very glare, he pleads for darkness, where melancholy and release intertwine into something at once restless and oddly at peace.","sonnet":"Daylight as enemy — the brightness blinds rather than illuminates, the future is a question mark hanging in the dark, and the speaker wants the day gone not because night promises anything better but because the light has become unbearable. The evening spreading across the sky is the only thing worth welcoming.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~can-t-stand-losing-you":{"haiku":"After repeated rejection and damaged possessions, someone faces breakup and contemplates suicide; the guilt they expect will fall on their ex-partner for refusing their love.","opus":"A jilted lover's darkly comic meltdown, calling obsessively, having his letters and scratched records returned, threatening suicide half as spite and half as genuine despair, insisting the guilt will land on her head when he is dead. The gallows humor barely masks real desperation. The Police made this an early hit, its jaunty reggae-punk bounce a deliberate, unsettling contrast to lyrics about a breakup spiraling toward self-destruction, Sting's yelp riding the tension.","sonnet":"A breakup escalating from unanswered calls to a darkly comic suicide threat — letters returned, records scratched, a six-foot-ten brother in the picture, and a speaker who admits this might be cowardice but can't find a reason to keep going without the other person. The threat hangs as both genuine feeling and pointed guilt-trip, with neither quite canceling the other.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~contact":{"haiku":"Isolation metastasizes despite technology's false connection; the speaker reaches endlessly toward others through disconnected channels, always alone.","opus":"A nervous, almost mechanical yearning to make real connection with someone, phrased in the cool language of contacts, phone lines and touchdowns. For all his list of people known, the singer is really asking whether genuine intimacy is possible between the two of them.","sonnet":"The speaker hungers for genuine connection — have we got contact, have we got touchdown? — while cataloguing all the infrastructure of communication (address books, phone lines, contacts in eyes) that substitutes for the real closeness they can't quite reach, held back even by something as banal as not having a raincoat.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~darkness":{"haiku":"Paralysis and inadequacy bloom in darkness, while routine and memory fade into meaninglessness.","opus":"An honest confession of paralysis: the singer dreams up grand ideas while seated but freezes the moment he must act, half-wishing he'd stayed asleep because a dull life was at least an easy one. Fear of ambition and of being ordinary.","sonnet":"A dreamer who cannot execute — full of schemes when seated, collapsed when standing — laments that darkness prevents him from finding his way, even as the door he fumbles for is already open. The song is gently self-mocking about the gap between potential and action.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~demolition-man":{"haiku":"Charisma doubles as catastrophe, a force that destroys conversation and connection while demanding worship despite being fundamentally hostile to intimacy.","opus":"Sting inhabiting a figure of pure destruction, a man who ruins everything he touches and refuses love, warning others away. Charisma and doom fused into one swaggering, unlovable force of nature.","sonnet":"A self-proclaimed agent of chaos addresses someone drawn to him despite the danger, cataloguing his own capacity to destroy relationships, conversations, and lives. He refuses the role of friend or lover — he is simply a walking catastrophe and warns accordingly.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~every-breath-you-take":{"haiku":"A possessive obsession stalks every movement and action of an ex-lover, with the watcher claiming they'll always be watching.","opus":"Sting cast this Police standard as the voice of an obsessive watcher, its smooth surface hiding menace and control as a man tracks his partner's every gesture and insists she is his. Under the vow to keep observing lies raw abandonment, the narrator adrift and aching for the person who left. Its lasting irony is how gently it makes stalking and heartbreak sound identical.","sonnet":"Obsessive surveillance of a departed lover masquerades as devotion — every movement, every word monitored with an intensity the watcher frames as longing but which maps more precisely onto possession. Loss has not produced grief so much as an unrelenting refusal to release.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~hole-in-my-life":{"opus":"An unfillable void gnawing at the singer despite every effort to be a happy man, an ache he can only name as a hole that cuts him open and leaves him defenseless.","sonnet":"An aching, unspecified emptiness cuts the speaker open like a knife, leaving them vulnerable and diseased — they try to be happy and manage, but the shadow in their heart tears them apart, and something crucial is simply, persistently missing.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-police~it-s-alright-for-you":{"haiku":"The song catalogs materialistic pursuits and surface-level living as acceptable for privileged people. It questions moral consistency and social inequality through repetition.","opus":"Clipped two-word bursts sketch a frantic modern life of movie stars, fast planes and cold rain while the world dies and everyone shrugs it off. The Police, on their early punk-reggae Reganatta run, fire this staccato satire at a numbed crowd for whom it is always alright, the machine-gun phrasing itself the point of the alienation.","sonnet":"A rapid-fire collage of modern life fragments — status markers, social performances, and emotional detachment — aimed at someone for whom everything seems to work out effortlessly. The repeated refrain becomes increasingly hollow, suggesting that ease and privilege feel like indifference to the one left carrying the weight.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~message-in-a-bottle":{"haiku":"A castaway sends a desperate SOS, discovers hundreds of billions of others equally alone, and realizes isolation is universal human experience.","opus":"A castaway hurling an SOS into the sea becomes a metaphor for aching loneliness; the twist that a hundred billion bottles wash back proves the isolated narrator is not alone at all, that countless others are likewise stranded and searching for connection.","sonnet":"An isolated man sends out a desperate call for connection, and discovers to his astonishment that the whole world is equally alone — a hundred billion castaways all sending the same message.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~murder-by-numbers":{"haiku":"Homicide and moral decay become routine and habitual through methodical detachment, with killing elevated to systematised art and political norm.","opus":"A mock-instructional on killing that starts with poisoned coffee and escalates gleefully to mass murder, then names its true masters: the leaders of nations for whom killing is a sanctioned pastime. Sting's real target is political power, dressed up as an easy how-to.","sonnet":"A darkly satirical tutorial in killing presented as simple arithmetic — murder is a habit like any other, easiest with a tablet in someone's coffee — escalating to the observation that murder is the sport of elected leaders who never need lift a finger themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~next-to-you":{"haiku":"Obsessive love drives someone to sell everything and pursue proximity to one person; nothing matters except being physically close to them.","opus":"A frantic burst of infatuation where the singer would sell his house, rob a bank or steal a plane just to close the distance to the one who took him over, driven half insane by a feeling stronger than any of the thousand loves before. Pure, breathless want. The Police open their 1978 debut with this raw, punk-charged rush, before their reggae sophistication fully set in, all nervy energy and single-minded desire for someone impossibly far away.","sonnet":"Obsession that sold the house and the car — nothing the speaker has ever felt compares to this, and proximity is the only thing that matters now, regardless of the miles or the sanity required to close them. He'd rob a bank, steal a plane; the irrationality is the point.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~on-any-other-day":{"haiku":"A man's domestic chaos spirals—burned food, a dog bite, a runaway daughter, a gay son—yet he frames his suffering as acceptable only if circumstances were different.","opus":"A darkly funny pile-up of household disasters, a ruined breakfast, an aggressive dog, a fled daughter, a wife boasting of infidelities, a hand crushed in the car, each calamity stacking higher until the narrator sighs that he could shrug it all off if only it were not his birthday. The ordinary wretchedness is the punchline. The Police, with Stewart Copeland singing, slot this cheerfully sour novelty into their early work.","sonnet":"A comedy of domestic catastrophe piling up on what turns out to be a birthday — scrambled eggs burned, dog bite, runaway daughter, gay son, fingers caught in a car door, a wife cheerfully confessing affairs, and all of it framed as things that would be fine on literally any other day. The birthday song at the end recontextualizes the suffering as timing, not fate.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~one-world-not-three":{"haiku":"Geopolitical critique dismantles false divisions between developed and developing worlds, exposing shared planetary fate masked by nationalist ideology and manufactured distance.","opus":"An early-80s plea against the fiction of a separate, disposable Third World, Sting arguing that dividing the planet with borders and flags is self-deluding since we all share one air and sink or float together.","sonnet":"A rebuke of the comfortable fiction that the wealthy world and the developing world are separate — the third world breathes our air tomorrow, and every border drawn is a self-deluding trick. The chorus insists that all of humanity shares a single boat and will sink or float together.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~peanuts":{"haiku":"A fallen celebrity is mocked for their decline in talent and relevance, exposed as a fraud beneath their famous name.","opus":"A sneering attack on fame and a washed-up celebrity, the narrator refusing to consume the tabloid diet of scandal, addiction, and manufactured lives, and telling the fading star his mind and his moment are gone. The Police delivered this on their raw, punk-adjacent debut, Sting's contempt for celebrity riding the band's jittery early new-wave aggression.","sonnet":"Contempt for a celebrity who has substituted fame for substance and now trades on a name that no longer means anything — the drugs, the manufactured love life, the faked existence all reported breathlessly while the person behind them has hollowed out. A fallen hero, not liberated, not elevated: just exposed.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~reggatta-de-blanc":{"haiku":"Reggae rhythms and minimalist arrangement ground sonic exploration; spacious production emphasizes restraint.","opus":"A marooned man flings a written plea out to sea across a full year of crushing solitude, sustained only by faint hope and the hard lesson that romance can rescue a person as readily as wound them. The twist is rescue through numbers: a beach littered with countless other pleas, proof he was never singular in his solitude.","sonnet":"What begins as a solitary castaway sending desperate messages into the void — \"I'll send an SOS to the world\" — ends with a devastating inversion: a hundred billion bottles wash up on shore, and the narrator realises universal loneliness means he was never alone in being alone. The message in a bottle becomes a symbol not of rescue but of the shared, unanswered human cry for connection.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~roxanne":{"haiku":"A person pleads with a sex worker named Roxanne to stop selling her body, declaring their monogamous love and offering escape from that life.","opus":"A possessive man begs a streetwalker to quit her trade, insisting she no longer needs to sell herself now that he adores her and refuses to share her with anyone. The Police made this an early signature hit, its famous tango-tinged reggae sway and Sting's soaring falsetto lending jealous devotion a noble sheen, a landmark of their late-70s rise out of London's new-wave scene.","sonnet":"A man in love with a sex worker, pleading with her to stop because he wants her entirely for himself and cannot stand the thought of sharing her with the street and the night. His feeling is real but his demand is possessive — he's made his mind up, and what he calls love sits uncomfortably close to control.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~secret-journey":{"haiku":"A blind holy man guides a lonely seeker toward enlightenment and self-discovery through spiritual transformation.","opus":"A spiritual parable of meeting a blind holy man whose loss of sight is his wisdom, and being promised that suffering and darkness eventually yield understanding and the love you were missing. Sting filtering his interest in mysticism into a gentle path toward grace.","sonnet":"A chance encounter with a blind holy man teaches the narrator to see darkness as provisional and pain as navigable, with the promise that an inward journey will eventually locate the love that has been missing. Spiritual guidance is offered as a map toward light.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~so-lonely":{"haiku":"A deeply isolated person laments abandonment and solitude after throwing away their love, now trapped in a private theatrical tragedy.","opus":"A rueful sketch of romantic solitude, the narrator unable to picture sharing his life yet confessing that ages have passed since anyone came calling, and imagining his inner life as a private stage where he is always the lead performer. The Police wrap the melancholy in an irresistibly bouncy reggae lilt, the chorus so upbeat it nearly hides the hurt, an early gem from their new-wave-meets-reggae breakthrough.","sonnet":"Abandonment replayed as theatre — the speaker turns his empty life into a one-man show, performing the starring role in a venue where nobody knocks anymore. He knows he's faking indifference after being left, but can't convince himself he could survive on his own, so the loneliness becomes both the stage and the audience.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~too-much-information":{"haiku":"Overstimulation and exhaustion fragment consciousness, as endless exposure to the world's vastness breaks mental coherence.","opus":"Prescient overload anxiety: a mind flooded past capacity, drowning in more input than it can process. Written decades before the internet, it names the modern condition of being buried alive under endless information.","sonnet":"Saturation by global travel, media, and experience has produced not wisdom but paralysis — everything seen everywhere has become overkill, overview, noise. The speaker is overwhelmed, their brain run ragged by the sheer volume of the modern world.","src":"haiku"},
"the-police~wrapped-around-your-finger":{"haiku":"A student seeks forbidden knowledge and becomes seduced into servitude by a master's promise of destiny and transformation; the dynamic reverses when the student becomes the true master.","opus":"A tense duel of wills between a novice and his teacher, the eager pupil lured toward hidden knowledge and mesmerized by a gleaming band of gold, name-dropping figures of classical peril and the demon of legend, before flipping the dynamic and swearing the underling will end up in charge. The Police built it on Sting's bookish allusions and a hypnotic groove, its slow reversal among their most elegant and coldly triumphant later statements.","sonnet":"A power dynamic flipped — the speaker enters as apprentice, caught between mythic dangers, seeking knowledge unavailable in formal education, watching destiny crystallize into gold. But the tables turn: the servant becomes the master, the hypnotist loses their subject, and the face that commanded turns to alabaster when it realizes control has changed hands.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pom-poms~full-circle":{"haiku":"A confident musical act celebrates their excellence and travels across America, boasting about their superior talent and ability to energize any crowd.","opus":"A brash cheer-rap romp that chants out letters to spell a boast while bragging about turning heads across a dozen towns, from Hollywood gridlock to a Houston nightspot, all swagger and a demand for the DJ to loop the track and close the loop. It is nothing but hype. The Pom Poms lean into glossy, tongue-in-cheek pop-rap here, a party cut of cheerleader chants and city-hopping bravado designed to jolt a dozing crowd awake.","sonnet":"A cheerleader-energy pop declaration of superiority and collective momentum — spelling out their own excellence city by city across America, the group insists on bringing the beat back around whenever it lags, pulling sleepers awake and stomping feet into rhythm. Fresh, united, self-proclaimed best, they just keep running it back.","src":"haiku"},
"the-pom-poms~watch-me":{"opus":"A brash, exhibitionist boast of self-reinvention and craving attention, flaunting an embarrassing but unapologetic past, an unbothered mall-brat persona, and a demand to be looked at.","sonnet":"A brash, self-possessed performer claims the spotlight on her own terms: she has a messy past and no regrets, is attracted to a very specific aesthetic of girls, and dares the audience to look while insisting she doesn't give a damn what they think.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-post-war~bend":{"haiku":"A speaker tests their will under pressure, burning and cold, mourning in shallow doubt while seeking the right feeling.","opus":"A war-flavored confession where gunfire and explosions scorch the air and leave a chill, the narrator pleading for his own resolve to be broken down so he can simply feel something real again. The Post War wrap it in atmospheric guitar rock, the battlefield imagery lending heavy, embattled weight to what is really a plea for numbness to finally crack.","sonnet":"Submission to an overwhelming force that burns the air and leaves everything cold — carbons heavy, mortar shells lighting the dark, the speaker asks their will to be bent down until they can feel something real again. A confession functions as testimony in their own defense, wolves cry out, and the bullet still in the barrel hasn't stopped what it already did.","src":"haiku"},
"the-preatures~girlhood":{"haiku":"Performance of femininity and constant self-scrutiny drain authentic existence; the girl disappears beneath constructed versions of desire and image.","opus":"A woman picking apart the impossible ideal of the modern girl, cycling between vanity, self-harm and denial while boys register only her being gone too long. A sharp look at how femininity gets performed and self-destructively policed from within.","sonnet":"A woman trapped in the performance of modern femininity tears herself apart in the mirror, navigating pregnancy scares, unmet expectations, and the sensation of dancing in denial while boys ask if she's alright — heroism is demanded but nothing is actually given.","src":"haiku"},
"the-preatures~is-this-how-you-feel":{"haiku":"The speaker experiences conflicting impulses to stay and leave in a relationship, oscillating between emotional connection and the need to break free and escape.","opus":"A jittery, love-struck surge of desire, the narrator split between a self that stays put and a self ready to flee while pursuing a girl through the outer suburbs and repeatedly asking if she shares his feelings. The Preatures are an Australian band, and this breakout single rides a bright, groove-forward pop-rock pulse, its relentless craving hook and dance-floor lift capturing the nervy hope of a new crush.","sonnet":"Caught between wanting to move toward someone and the weight of bad memories that resurface every time — the speaker walks through suburbia looking, oscillates between nights that go heavy and mornings that feel awake, and keeps asking whether what they feel is mutual or just their own circling want projected outward.","src":"haiku"},
"the-presidents-of-the-united-states-of-america~lump":{"haiku":"A dull, emotionless woman called Lump drifts through life in marshes and mud, intellectually deprived and romantically limp.","opus":"A goofy character study of a girl called Lump, planted expressionless in a soggy wetland, baffling nearby fish, arriving dead last for her share of intelligence, and possibly a corpse, all while stuck rattling around the narrator's mind. The Presidents of the United States of America built their 90s alt-rock hit on precisely this nonsense and buzzy basitar riffs, a gleeful scrap of meaning-optional novelty pop.","sonnet":"A character study of Lump — a bog-dwelling, possibly dead or deeply inert woman who slipped through every developmental stage, tumbled into love, and spent her twenties barely moving at sub-sonic speed. She might be a projection, she might be an obsession, and the song never quite resolves whether she's real or a figment lodged permanently in the narrator's head.","src":"haiku"},
"the-presidents-of-the-united-states-of-america~peaches":{"haiku":"Someone fantasizes about rural escape and carefree abundance, eating freely available fruit in bucolic simplicity, free from industrial constraints.","opus":"On its cheerful surface a goofy daydream of moving to the country to eat peaches, but the napping-among-roots verse, the squished fruit, dreaming of a woman, and poking a finger inside carry a barely concealed sexual undertone beneath the silliness.","sonnet":"A blissfully absurd fantasy about moving to the country and eating an infinite supply of peaches — the song is playfully sensual about the fruit and nothing more.","src":"haiku"},
"the-prodigy~action-radar": {"haiku":"The song is an energetic celebration of living hard and seeking action and excitement. It repeats the desire for active engagement and refusal to settle into complacency.","opus":"A repetitive dance-track come-on driven by hunger for excitement and physical action, insisting a little action is all that's needed. There's little narrative depth beyond the pursuit of thrill and living it up rather than giving it up.","sonnet":"A drive-and-desire track with minimal lyrical content — the narrator is drawn toward action and stimulation, locked onto a partner who also craves it. The lyrics are thin scaffolding for kinetic energy.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric is nearly all command and chant, a hunger addressed to a target the sweep never fixes; the speaker confesses the screen stays blank yet still insists on being right. That contradiction is the whole engine: desire keeps circling because the beam locks onto nothing, so the pursuit sustains itself rather than closing on any prize. On Always Outnumbered, Never Outgunned — where Liam Howlett sidelined his vocalists and drafted outside guests — the emptiness reads as design, appetite phrased as a repeated come-on that wants the chase more than the catch.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["a hunt whose object is never fixed","love & desire"],"means":"desire sustaining itself by never reaching its target","built":"the blank screen named mid-pursuit keeps the chase alive"},
"the-prodigy~awol":{"haiku":"Uncontrollable force and chaotic defiance resist all authority and constraint, with nothing holding back the wild impulse.","opus":"A snarling big-beat provocation built on menace and rebellion, an uncontrollable force gone absent-without-leave, the aggression and swagger carrying the meaning more than any narrative.","sonnet":"A short, aggressive declaration of going rogue — something is out there that authority cannot control, and the song is a middle-finger celebration of operating outside the system, weaponising absurdity (shooting with a sugar bowl) as its own form of resistance.","src":"haiku"},
"the-prodigy~boom-boom-tap": {"haiku":"Rhythmic aggression and crude verbal attack.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric is a drum pattern spelled out in mouth-syllables — the kick, the snare, the hi-hat rendered as human speech — so the vocal becomes just another rhythm module in the machine rather than a carrier of words. That is the whole point of late Prodigy in the No Tourists era: nothing to say, everything to hit, the human voice conscripted into the beatbox. The one moment the pattern breaks for actual language, it is a two-word obscenity flung on the offbeat, which tells you the only content the track will admit is aggression, deployed as percussion. Meaning here lives in the refusal of meaning: the song is a fist, and the words are just the sound the fist makes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"the human voice conscripted into percussion rather than meaning","built":"one obscenity breaks the beatbox pattern on the offbeat"},
"the-prodigy~breathe": {"haiku":"A manipulative force tests victims psychologically, playing games and feeding on addiction and mental fragmentation. The speaker operates as a sadistic tester of human limits.","opus":"Menace as invitation, daring the listener into a game where you're already the victim, breath itself weaponized into pressure and psychosis. The Prodigy turn a rave chant into a predatory taunt, addiction and insanity offered as the prize.","sonnet":"A predator addresses a victim — come into my game, breathe with me, you're already caught — the song staging a psychosomatic, addictive submission to a force that is testing and owning whoever steps inside its rhythm.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is built as a hostile invitation: an unnamed tormentor summons a listener to a game whose only rule is the involuntary act of breathing, so that the most automatic sign of being alive becomes the mechanism of control. The two imperatives split the body against itself — the intake is where you are named the victim, the exhale is the surrender demanded on repeat — which turns the diagnosis of the addict as psychosomatic into the taunt's real point: the pressure is self-generated, the trap is inside the nervous system. Maxim and Keith Flint trading lines on 1997's The Fat of the Land makes this a two-headed threat rather than a single voice, and the refusal to resolve or vary means the menace is the loop itself, the game that never releases you because breathing never stops.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder"],"means":"the involuntary act of breathing turned into a mechanism of control","built":"two voices split the tormentor in two, making the menace a loop"},
"the-prodigy~champions-of-london": {"haiku":"Urban pride and civil unrest, with street culture and resistance in London's communities.","opus":"A chant of street swagger and menace, saluting the city's toughs amid images of a place coming apart and calls for armor against unrest. The words function as a rhythmic hook, not a plot, built for aggression and drive. These English electronic-punk pioneers made this shouted, riotous minimalism their trademark, turning a handful of hard phrases into a statement of turf and defiance.","sonnet":"A territorial anthem built almost entirely from its own chanting — London claimed as a city of undone, the bassline cutting through armour, routes from Dobson to Brixton mapped as a claim. Civil unrest hums underneath the swagger, bulletproof vests grabbed without irony, the whole thing less a celebration than a show of force.","src":"haiku","fable":"The repetition is the whole argument: a football-terrace chant weaponized into a beat you either fall in with or get flattened by. The Prodigy stake their turf on a club-land circuit running through Brixton, not the postcard capital, and hold it the way a crowd holds a ground rather than a trophy. Swagger sours fast, though, when the bass finally splits the listener's guard and the coda swaps the dancefloor for a bulletproof vest and streets braced for riot, exposing the champion pose as bravado one bad night from a Brixton flashpoint.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","violence & murder","night & the city"],"means":"a champion pose exposed as bravado one bad night from collapse","built":"the terrace chant weaponized into a beat you fall in with or get flattened by"},
"the-prodigy~colours": {"haiku":"A rallying cry to display defiance and readiness for conflict, framing visible personal identity as an act of war preparation.","opus":"A charged call to arms that rouses a crowd for confrontation, urging people to show their colours and bring them to the floor amid sirens, fighting dogs, and the readiness for war.","sonnet":"A rallying cry for conflict and allegiance: listeners are called to raise their banner, show their true colours, and declare whose side they are on as a confrontation builds.","src":"haiku","fable":"A pure summons, and it means to be nothing else: the imperatives (raise, wake, call, bring) are a crowd being conscripted into a mob, war and rave collapsed into the same command to get on the floor. The Prodigy's Invaders-era trick is to treat the dancefloor as a battlefield with no distance between them, so colours here are gang colours and rave affiliation at once, a banner you fight under and a flag you throw down. The only line that looks inward — clocking the freak in someone's eyes — is really the payoff of the recruitment, the moment the ordinary body in the room is confirmed as already gone feral, which is why the song can end on nothing but the chant repeating: there is no story to tell past the point of ignition, only the crowd obeying.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","party & hedonism","identity & becoming"],"means":"the dancefloor and the battlefield collapsed into a single command","built":"the song ends on chant alone because ignition has no story past itself"},
"the-prodigy~death-of-the-prodigy-dancers": {"haiku":"Violent performance and aggressive takeover, with killing and domination as entertainment.","opus":"A confrontational chant staging the symbolic killing of the dancer, the MC threatening to move, groove and send the crowd to the grave in an escalating incitement to lose control on the floor. It is pure rave provocation more than narrative. The Prodigy emerged from Britain's early-nineties rave scene, and this early track captures Keith Flint and Maxim's aggressive showmanship before the band exploded into global punk-electronica notoriety.","sonnet":"A rave floor declaration that collapses performance and violence into one gesture — the MC announces the dancer's death as a kind of ultimate domination, but the killing is the music taking over, moving bodies until they're used up. Destruction and ecstasy are the same thing here.","src":"haiku","fable":"A rave-era MC chant that stages its own title as mock-menace: the killing is the dancefloor imperative to lose yourself, the self dissolving into the beat, dressed up as murder-threat theatrics. Maxim's toasting exists only to trigger a reaction, and the piece runs as a call-and-response engine, its rhythmic count-off lifted from soundsystem tradition to prime the room. When it demands to know whether the crowd is answering, the mask drops: this is a hype-man taking the temperature of the floor, and the promised trip is the trance of surrender, not the grave. The violence is the era's playful darkside slang, meaning yield to rhythm rather than come to harm.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","party & hedonism"],"means":"the murder-threat theatrics exposed as a hype-man reading the floor","built":"the title's killing is the dancefloor imperative to dissolve into the beat"},
"the-prodigy~diesel-power": {"haiku":"A rapper boasts of lyrical skill and intellectual prowess, claiming superiority in rhythm, technique, and the cosmic power of hip-hop performance.","opus":"The Prodigy pass the mic to Kool Keith, whose breakneck verses become a nonstop brag about lyrical and technical dominance, his mind supposedly hurled into overdrive by sheer engine force. Studio slang, club explosions and cosmic swagger stack up as he rolls with his crew and eats rivals alive. The big-beat backing recasts rap skill as raw mechanical horsepower, intellect running flat out.","sonnet":"A lyricist catalogues his own technical dominance over a pounding electronic backdrop. Every metaphor is a flex — velocity, density, acrobatic delivery, cosmic intellect — the boast looping back on itself through three verses without apology, the energy source declared infinite, the whole performance a demonstration of the very diesel power being named.","src":"haiku","fable":"Kool Keith guests here and treats the microphone as heavy plant equipment, routing every boast about technique through the language of tape decks, rewinds, amplifiers, and photocopiers until verbal skill and raw horsepower read as one identical claim. The bars stay wilfully scrambled, a spray of clinical non-sequiturs about surgery and geometry that refuse to line up into a story, because the flex is precisely that incoherence: the rapper outrunning your comprehension the way an engine outruns the hand steering it. Everything drains toward the title as its working unit, intellect recast as a combustion motor, the chant behind it functioning less as a phrase than as an rpm gauge left pinned in the red. It is the genre's oldest move, the skills brag, rebuilt from spare mechanical parts.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"verbal technique and raw horsepower declared the same identical claim","built":"clinical non-sequiturs refuse to line up, the incoherence being the flex"},
"the-prodigy~fight-fire-with-fire-feat-ho99o9": {"haiku":"Resistance against oppression through militant confrontation and rebellion.","opus":"A furious battle cry aimed at police and a hostile world, urging comrades to stay united through rough times, leave the pavement bloody and meet aggression with equal aggression. The hook about scorching your face works as pure combative spectacle. A late-era Prodigy cut with punk-rap duo Ho99o9, it welds the English band's riot energy to hardcore venom, the big-beat veterans honing their sound into a blunt anthem of loyalty and revolt.","sonnet":"Collective defiance against a system that does not care whether you live or die — troops camouflaged, fists raised, heat met with heat. The song is pure forward pressure: no negotiation, no appeals to authority, just the logic of meeting violence with its own currency and holding ground together in stormy weather.","src":"haiku","fable":"A detonation staged where Essex rave-punk shakes hands with Ho99o9's horrorcore, engineered so the sonic damage and the political snarl arrive as one indivisible blow. The producer's order to spin back the record and liquefy a listener's skull is not decoration for the combat talk; it is the combat, the record arguing that sheer volume counts as counterattack, that its retaliatory motto governs the speakers exactly as it governs the pavement. Loyalty hardens into a barricade of us against the badge and the mercenary paymasters who feel nothing for the bodies they command, a toughness lacquered on precisely because tears still wait beneath the armor. Lifting a hand to unwind the very conflict it salutes exposes the whole creed: aggression that dresses itself as defense, answering heat with heat and christening the exchange survival.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society","friendship & loyalty","violence & murder"],"means":"aggression dressed as defense, answering heat with heat and calling it survival","built":"the order to spin back the record is both production move and the combat itself"},
"the-prodigy~fire-sunrise-version": {"haiku":"A celebration of destructive excess and drug use, with the speaker invoking hellfire and claiming god-like power through intoxication and rebellion.","opus":"A blast of dancehall bravado and pyromaniac swagger, the god of hellfire declaring he brings you fire while a toaster reminisces about burning weed in a Rizla as a youth, all riding a relentless chant. It is pure sample-driven energy over meaning. The Prodigy, the pioneering British electronic act behind rave and big-beat, splice Arthur Brown's fire proclamation into a raucous, incendiary groove, the two vocal hooks colliding into gleeful, combustible chaos.","sonnet":"A god of hellfire announcing himself and delivering on the promise — the destruction is elemental and personal both, rooted in a youth spent burning things in rolling papers and growing into someone who claims dominion over flame itself. The repetition is the point: this is not argument, it is proclamation.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a track with no words of its own — two borrowed voices stacked into one rave joke about fire. The apocalyptic first voice, lifted wholesale from Arthur Brown's 1968 God-of-Hellfire proclamation, arrives as pure theatrical menace; the second, a ragga toast recalling a teenager's cannabis habit and the little rolling paper it was smoked in, deflates the damnation into something small, warm, and stoned. Liam Howlett's early-Experience method is the whole point: he treats brimstone and back-garden smoke as the same sample-word, splicing an eternal-torment threat against a memory of getting high until the flame stops meaning judgment and just means the tip of a joint on the dancefloor. The repeated tag, chopped and echoed, is less a lyric than a trigger — the meaning lives in the collision the machine makes, not in any statement either source ever meant.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","faith & the occult"],"means":"brimstone and back-garden smoke treated as the same sample-word","built":"damnation deflated into a memory of a joint by the second voice"},
"the-prodigy~firestarter": {"haiku":"The speaker boasts as a chaotic instigator—a troublemaker, filth-lover, and mind detonator—thriving on causing disturbance and transgression.","opus":"The Prodigy's breakout gives Keith Flint his snarling star turn as a menacing arsonist figure, a maker of trouble and an addict of dread who wallows in threat. Warped, damaged and diseased, the persona embraces filth and wreckage with a punk sneer while a taunting refrain heightens the provocation. A landmark of big-beat crossover, the track is less a story than sheer combustible attitude, chaos given a body.","sonnet":"A chaos-entity announces itself through pure self-definition. Twisted, feared, and self-inflicted, the firestarter is instigator and detonator both — neither victim nor bystander but the original source of disorder, revelling in the role without explanation or remorse, identity collapsed entirely into the act of ignition.","src":"haiku","fable":"Every clause names a role rather than a deed, so the speaker builds a whole identity out of menace-labels yet never once reports an actual arson, making the swagger a claim of presence over any burnable evidence. That maneuver mirrors the group's 1996 shift from faceless rave outfit to something needing a snarling human mask, and Keith Flint's spiked-hair leer supplied exactly the punk frontman the tracks had lacked. The one genuinely destabilizing turn is the chorus swap of I for You, where the boaster wheels around and pins the same arsonist role onto whoever is listening, so provocation stops being a private confession and behaves instead like flame passing hand to hand. The wounding-oneself couplet lets the threat curl back on its author, conceding that the agitator is equally the casualty.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"menace-labels building an identity that never reports an actual deed","built":"the chorus swaps I for You, passing the arsonist role to the listener"},
"the-prodigy~fuel-my-fire": {"haiku":"Betrayal and distrust fuel the narrator's rage and motivation, transforming grief into a grudge that burns indefinitely. The song frames anger as a renewable fuel for survival.","opus":"The speaker sarcastically thanks a liar who betrayed his trust, channeling that betrayal into rage and a grudge he'll hold for life. People like the one who stabbed him in the back only fuel his fire.","sonnet":"The speaker directs sustained anger at someone who betrayed and lied to them, declaring that the wound and the grudge are permanent and that the treachery only intensifies his drive. The betrayal becomes the fuel for ongoing rage rather than something to be forgiven.","src":"haiku","fable":"A cover of L7's snarl, retooled by The Prodigy into a big-beat fist-swing where betrayal is not mourned but weaponized. The mock gratitude of the opening is the whole trick: the speaker thanks the person who wronged them precisely because the wound has become fuel, the grudge nursed into an engine that runs on spite. The song's one governing image, a knife in the back wearing the traitor's fingerprints, tells you the injury is never getting cleaned up or forgiven; it is being kept as evidence and as motivation. What reads as an anthem of resilience is really a portrait of someone who has decided that resentment is preferable to healing, hurling the accusation of liar over and over less to indict the other than to keep the fire lit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"resentment kept lit because healing would extinguish the engine","built":"the mock gratitude of the opening names spite as the track's own fuel"},
"the-prodigy~full-throttle":{"haiku":"Acceleration reaches maximum as the speaker plunges toward limit without restraint. Full speed demands surrender to momentum.","opus":"A dancefloor challenge welded to a kiss-off: first daring the crowd to feel the bass drum, then flatly cutting loose someone who's no good, insisting on needing no one at all. Defiance and solitude ride the same pulse. The Prodigy, the Essex outfit who dragged rave into aggressive electronic-punk, thrive on exactly this, minimal words hammered into weaponized hooks over relentless breakbeats.","sonnet":"A clean severance — someone identified as genuinely harmful is cut loose with no negotiation. The bass drum call at the top is almost a summons to attention before the declaration: this person is no good, and the speaker needs nobody, certainly not them. Terse and absolute, no grief, no ambiguity.","src":"haiku"},
"the-prodigy~get-up-get-off": {"haiku":"The rapper catalogs his hatreds—fake people, poverty, weak clubs, injustice—and celebrates aggressive flow and musical dominance over the phony and complacent.","opus":"A dancefloor-command collaboration with Twista built almost entirely on a litany of hatreds, contempt for the phony and the still, aimed at forcing bodies into motion. Braggadocio and menace as party fuel, energy stoked by pure spite.","sonnet":"A Twista-fronted club energy track built around a list of things the narrator hates — fake people, bourgeois clubs, being broke, anyone who doesn't feel the music — functioning as a hyperbolic flex and invitation to lose control on the dancefloor.","src":"haiku","fable":"The dancefloor imperative and a litany of hatreds are the same engine here: the track builds its command to move out of everything it refuses to tolerate. Chicago rapper Twista supplies the verses, and his signature machine-gun cadence turns a list of grievances — phony people, dry spells, clubs too precious to actually dance — into pure propulsion, so that contempt reads as energy rather than misery. The second verse abandons the party frame entirely for horror-movie menace, the MC as lyrical Dracula draining the room, which exposes what the hook was really selling: getting off is figured as a kind of controlled violence, and the demand to get up carries an implied threat toward anyone who stays still. The genius of the pairing is that Prodigy, a group whose whole idiom is aggression weaponized for the rave, lets that hostility do the work the beat used to — the hate is the hook.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","party & hedonism","violence & murder"],"means":"contempt weaponized as energy so the hate becomes the hook","built":"the horror-movie second verse exposes getting off as controlled violence"},
"the-prodigy~get-your-fight-on": {"haiku":"Aggressive confrontation and escalating physical aggression define the stance—bite, fight, build to critical mass and release violent energy.","opus":"Pure Prodigy aggression, a rave call-to-arms built on escalating tension and release rather than any narrative. The whole point is to weaponize the crowd's energy into physical confrontation on the dancefloor.","sonnet":"A pure adrenaline chant with almost no lyrical content — the song is entirely about building to a critical mass of energy and releasing it, functioning more as a physical prompt than a narrative. There's no discernible theme beyond the act of aggression itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a chant, not a story, and reading it means reading what the drilled repetition does rather than pretending an argument sits underneath. Two imperatives carry the whole thing — an order to fight and an order to clamp your jaw around something — pitched against a slow charge toward release, so the words work like a cornerman's bark seconds before the drop, arming the body and bypassing the mind. The bite-down instruction is the tell: it handles the listener as a boxer fitting a gumshield, converting aggression into pure reflex, physical and pre-verbal. The refusal to develop is the design; a mantra earns its grip by staying still, hammering the crowd into the same clenched readiness the beat is stoking underneath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle"],"means":"the listener handled as a boxer fitting a gumshield before the drop","built":"the bite-down command converts aggression to reflex, bypassing the mind"},
"the-prodigy~girls-feat-the-ping-pong-bitches": {"haiku":"Fame and travel's hollow fantasy, circulating stories about women as commodified spectacle.","opus":"A hypnotic, repetitive fantasy of rockstar excess, fame, cash, and touring the world chasing girls, the loop itself mimicking the empty, mechanical churn of that pursuit.","sonnet":"A fantasy about rock-star excess — the song imagines the lifestyle of touring, making money, and accumulating sexual conquests, framed as something to aspire to; it's mostly a repeated loop and doesn't go deep, functioning more as a provocative brag than a narrative.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric is a single rock-star fantasy quatrain — top of the world, cash, world tour, young girls as conquest anecdotes — looped a dozen times without ever moving past the imagining of it, and that stasis is the whole argument: the dream is a machine caught on repeat, aspiration reduced to a chant that can name its rewards but never reach them. The girls dissolve into an interchangeable trophy the boast is told about, the object of the story rather than anyone in it, until even the word itself gets stripped to a bare tag at the end. Set against the aggressive irony of The Prodigy's 2004 Always Outnumbered, Never Outgunned, the deadpan, mechanized recitation reads less as endorsement than as satire of the fantasy it recites — the empty loop mocking the emptiness of the thing it wants.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","party & hedonism"],"means":"a rock-star fantasy looped until the dream mocks its own emptiness","built":"the stasis of never moving past the imagining is the satire's mechanism"},
"the-prodigy~hotride": {"haiku":"Escape and flying away into transcendence or freedom.","opus":"Built around a warped sample of the old balloon-song refrain, this rides a menacing tension between whimsical flight and threat, offers of a ride curdling into demands to push it and get out, sweetness turned sinister. The Prodigy, British big-beat pioneers, specialized in exactly this collision of nursery-rhyme melody and rave aggression, hijacking a wholesome hook and dragging it onto a bullet train out of town into something twitchy and dangerous.","sonnet":"An escape fantasy in the form of an air balloon ride — weightless ascent above the ordinary world, demanding a ride and then threatening to push the passenger out once they have it. The emotional register swings between euphoria and aggression, the freedom of flight undercut by a restlessness that can't settle even at altitude.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track hijacks the sunlit 5th Dimension idyll of drifting skyward in a wicker basket and warps its rainbow-pop promise into a threat, the sampled invitation repeatedly severed by a flat order to get out and then to be shoved over the wicker rim. What poses as a giddy plea for a lift is really a predator's demand, the rush of altitude reserved for the driver while the rider exists only to be jettisoned once the gondola clears the treetops. The turn arrives in the late chorus, where the joyride sours into fatalism: with the clock spent and every reprieve gone, that airborne lift stands revealed as a strictly one-way passage, and the closing push-it chant reads at once as throttle-gunning adrenaline and a literal heave into empty sky.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","freedom & escape"],"means":"a joyride revealed as a one-way passage with the rider jettisoned","built":"the sampled idyll of drifting skyward is severed by a flat order to get out"},
"the-prodigy~hyperspeed-g-force-part-2": {"haiku":"Acceleration beyond human limits; the speaker achieves transcendent velocity through mechanical and chemical enhancement.","opus":"Early Prodigy rave energy, an instrumental-leaning track from their breakbeat hardcore era built around the sheer thrill of velocity. The sparse vocal fragments about jetting out at hyperspeed serve only as adrenaline delivery, the whole point being propulsion, the beat, and the rush of moving faster than sound.","sonnet":"A brief, kinetic declaration of pure velocity: the speaker is locked on, moving at nine times the speed of sound, needing nothing beyond the beat to sustain forward motion. The lyric is less a narrative than a statement of propulsion — the beat as sufficient reason for existence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sampled cockpit chatter — a target locked, a screaming climb past the sound barrier — recasts the dancefloor as a fighter jet pulling enough G to grey out the pilot, velocity treated as both narcotic and whole reason for being. Liam Howlett's rave-era creed is laid bare: the breakbeat is the entire payload, everything anyone could want, so the fractured chant of one syllable stops behaving like language and becomes a tachometer, the voice stammering itself into pure thrust until sense burns off and only momentum remains. What survives is the raw pull of acceleration, thrill stripped of any message it might carry.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","party & hedonism"],"means":"velocity as narcotic, acceleration stripping sense until only momentum remains","built":"cockpit chatter recasts the dancefloor as a fighter jet greying out the pilot"},
"the-prodigy~ibiza-feat-sleaford-mods": {"haiku":"The song satirizes shallow hedonism and pretense in Ibiza's party scene—bleached tourists, drug use, and meaningless posturing masquerading as culture and excitement.","opus":"A jeering send-up of Ibiza clubland, its private planes, ready-made drug baggies, peroxide poseurs and celebrity DJs, ridiculing the empty, interchangeable scene with a repeated mocking question about what one of them thinks he is even up to.","sonnet":"A Sleaford Mods-style skewering of the hollow, performative excess of Ibiza tourist culture — pre-mixed music, private jets, bleached-hair mates, overpriced cigarettes — delivered as caustic contempt for people who confuse consumption with experience.","src":"sonnet","fable":"The Prodigy hand the mic to Sleaford Mods' Jason Williamson and let his splenetic estate-English rant do the demolition work: this is a hit-job on the superstar DJ and the package-holiday hedonism industry that grew up around him, the man simply triggering a set someone else assembled and flying his equipment in on a chartered Lear. The chant of the island's name over an incredulous refrain — what's he doing up there — turns worship into contempt, the crowd's ecstasy exposed as a con run by faceless, toneless button-pushers. What lifts it past mere sneering is the class angle underneath: Williamson skewers the peroxide-blond, Danny-Dyer jet-set beside the narrator who won't pay tobacconist prices for straights and rolls his own baccy instead, so the target is less Ibiza than the whole aspirational sham of buying an experience wholesale. The parting shot that the music is dull is the real knife — a dance act declaring the dance-culture endgame boring.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"the dance-culture endgame declared boring by a dance act from inside it","built":"the roll-your-own baccy detail pins the class angle beneath the rant"},
"the-prodigy~intro": {"haiku":"Underground escape preserves integrity from corruption; the work withdraws to protect itself.","opus":"A short spoken snippet announcing a retreat into the underground to keep the music away from those who would spoil it, positioning the record as a pointed return to raw, unpolished origins.","sonnet":"A brief declaration of artistic withdrawal: the creator reclaims their work by taking it underground so it cannot fall into the wrong hands.","src":"haiku","fable":"A lone sampled voice sets the record's politics before a single beat drops: the vow to bury the craft beyond reach, refusing to let it be seized, reads as manifesto costumed as a doorway. It surfaces in 1994 while Parliament's Criminal Justice and Public Order Bill moved to criminalise the very gatherings this music soundtracked — outdoor sound systems and their repetitive-beat sermons. Liam Howlett frames the coming songs as smuggled goods, dance culture retreating from legislation on its own terms. The generation named in the title arrives here as posture rather than complaint: retreat weaponised into defiance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","surveillance & control","freedom & escape"],"means":"retreat weaponised into defiance against legislation","built":"manifesto costumed as a doorway into the album"},
"the-prodigy~invaders-must-die": {"haiku":"A defiant battle cry built on the repeated slogan 'Invaders must die'—a call to resist and destroy those who would invade or destroy.","opus":"Built almost entirely on the chanted declaration that invaders must die, an aggressive slogan of defiance against intruders.","sonnet":"A stark, defiant declaration of identity: the band asserts its presence and issues the uncompromising command that invaders must die, with no elaboration beyond the repeated slogan.","src":"haiku","fable":"There is almost no lyric here on purpose: a robotic voice announcing the band, and a single slogan drilled eight times until it stops being a sentence and becomes a rhythmic weapon. The title track of Prodigy's 2009 comeback is a statement of intent rather than a song about anything — the invader is the group itself, reasserting a place after years away, and the sci-fi B-movie phrasing frames that return as an assault to be repelled. The meaning lives entirely in the repetition and the deadpan mechanical delivery: the words don't develop, they escalate, functioning as a chant to be shouted back rather than a message to be parsed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"return to the stage framed as an assault to repel","built":"slogan drilled until it stops being a sentence"},
"the-prodigy~invisible-sun":{"haiku":"The speaker navigates darkness and uncertainty, stumbling without clear direction as something unseen burns away their questions and doubt.","opus":"A hypnotically repeated image of an invisible sun that offers no light or path, leaving the narrator stumbling in the dark and casting only shadows. It evokes searching for meaning or guidance from a source that burns away certainty rather than illuminating the way.","sonnet":"A sparse, hypnotic image of a hidden or absent guiding light — the invisible sun illuminates nothing and burns away certainty, leaving the speaker stumbling blind through darkness without direction.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song is a single quatrain hammered four times without variation, so the meaning lives not in development but in the paradox held rigid at its center: a sun that gives no light, guiding toward no path, is faith stripped of any evidence for itself. The Prodigy build the image as pure contradiction — a source of heat that only deepens the dark and scorches away the very questions you might ask of it — so the sun becomes less a comfort than a compulsion, something you orient toward precisely because it refuses to show you anything. That the hook loops untouched is the argument: no verse arrives to resolve the stumbling, because there is no resolution, only the repeated act of moving toward a certainty that cannot be seen and will not answer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["faith & the occult"],"means":"orienting toward a certainty that refuses to show itself and will not answer","built":"the hook loops untouched through four repetitions, the structure enacting the argument that there is no resolution"},
"the-prodigy~jericho": {"haiku":"Biblical siege horns and bass dominate; the command is to keep dancing and feel love's presence while the biblical wall-crashing trumpet sounds.","opus":"An early relic from the Essex rave firebrands, this cut leans entirely on chant and pressure, the biblical walls of Jericho reduced to a rallying cry over ravenous bass. It is less narrative than incantation, built for the dancefloor at a moment when the band was fusing hardcore breaks with dub inflections. Movement and low-end weight carry everything the words leave deliberately unsaid.","sonnet":"A track built almost entirely around the image of ancient walls falling to sound — biblical collapse translated into a physical, bodily command to move and surrender. The bass is the weapon; the body the battleground.","src":"haiku","fable":"Early Prodigy, at the peak of Liam Howlett's 1992 hardcore-rave method, where a lyric is not written but assembled from stolen vocal stabs fired over a breakbeat. Three shards do the work: the Old Testament trumpet that flattened a besieged city becomes a boast about bass pressure, a barter clause about earning what you ask for on the floor, and the single command to stay in motion. Read together they convert the scriptural horn into a sound-system emblem, the toppling walls rewritten as the instant the drop levels a packed room. No self waits underneath to interpret, only the trance-logic of the loop, syllables repeated until meaning erodes and the words work as percussion aimed at the body, not the mind.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"scriptural horn rewritten as the instant the drop levels a room","built":"syllables repeated until meaning erodes into percussion"},
"the-prodigy~light-up-the-sky": {"haiku":"Illumination and heat as dance begins; the instigators arrive to light and radiate energy.","opus":"A pounding rave incitement, all radiating heat and instigated dancing, illuminating the night like ten thousand degrees. The Prodigy strip the lyric to chant and command, the sparse phrases functioning as ignition points for the electronic assault built by Liam Howlett, arriving late in the career of the band as a defiant rally after the confrontational era of Keith Flint.","sonnet":"Pure incendiary energy as declaration — collective heat radiating outward at ten thousand degrees, the dancefloor a site of insurrection. There is no narrative, only ignition: the group arrives, they illuminate everything, and the sky itself becomes the measure of what they bring.","src":"haiku","fable":"A late-Prodigy rave chant stripped to its firing mechanism: a rhyming couplet and a furnace figure, looped until they combust. The words barely function as language, and that is the point — instigate and illuminate lock into a call to riot, a crowd deliberately set ablaze rather than merely entertained, the incendiary menace this band traded in since the firestarter era pushed to pure signal. The furnace figure is the whole thesis: not comfort but the flashpoint of a packed room going critical, the dancefloor imagined as a fuse burning toward the moment it takes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","anger & defiance"],"means":"crowd deliberately set ablaze rather than merely entertained","built":"furnace figure compresses the dancefloor thesis to a single image"},
"the-prodigy~medicine": {"haiku":"A bitter medicine (control through sweet deception) is delivered to keep people in their place, with commands never to surrender while sickness spreads.","opus":"A menacing metaphor for control, harsh medicine sweetened just enough to make you swallow it and stay obedient. The defiant response, to refuse to yield and welcome the affliction instead, reframes submission as the real poison and resistance as the only genuine cure.","sonnet":"A brief, menacing piece about force-fed compliance — something bitter is being delivered with just enough sugar to keep people in their place, and the directive is never to surrender to the sickness being administered.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track weaponizes the language of care, casting the healer as a jailer whose cure is really sedation dressed up as treatment. That nursery-rhyme sweetener, lifted from a governess's cheerful tune, is exposed as bait: the surface pleasantness exists only to slide the pacifier down and pin you where they want you. The final admission is the sting — the dispensers name themselves as the very plague they claim to treat, so their remedy can only spread it. And the hammering, near-catatonic repetition performs the obedience it demands, each looping bar dulling the listener into the same numb compliance the words prescribe.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"healers name themselves the plague their cure can only spread","built":"hammering repetition performs the obedience the words prescribe"},
"the-prodigy~mindfields": {"haiku":"A speaker navigates dangerous mental terrain, moving through psychological minefields filled with shock and treacherous thoughts.","opus":"Menace delivered as a repeated dare, the track warns that danger is here and taunts the listener toward the disorienting jolt of the dancefloor. This Prodigy cut from Fat of the Land runs on incitement and sonic aggression rather than narrative, its title punning on a minefield to cast the crowd as a hazard zone.","sonnet":"A warning to proceed carefully through a mental landscape saturated with danger and shock. The speaker navigates an internal terrain of explosive psychological force, where exposure to whatever lives inside can shatter perception. Danger is both invitation and threat — open your head and risk total detonation.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track is built from four or five phrases looped past the point of sense, and that repetition is the meaning: a mind under siege produces no narrative, only the same threat circling with no way out. The vocabulary is martial and clinical at once, with a skull cracked open, a battlefield punned into cognition, a head watched as it decays, so the peril sits inside the head rather than out on any front. Driving on, even the phrases collapse into a barked two-syllable fragment, language itself ground down to a trigger word, the burnout the lyric names enacted at the level of the words. It reads less as a song about a subject than as a swaggering display of a consciousness worn down to a handful of violent reflexes, daring you to step inside it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","war & battle"],"means":"consciousness worn to a handful of violent reflexes","built":"phrases collapse to a two-syllable fragment enacting the burnout they name"},
"the-prodigy~music-reach-1-2-3-4": {"haiku":"Sound and connection; music reaches as a unifying force across distance.","opus":"A pure rave call-to-arms from The Prodigy's early breakbeat days, stripped down to a chant and a countdown. It exists to move a dancefloor, its repeated demands to reach and to shout functioning as raw crowd ignition. There is no subject beyond the ecstatic collective release of the music itself.","sonnet":"Pure incantation: the track exists to count down and release, with the music as the force that makes you want to shout — no narrative, just the physics of a beat arriving and demanding a body respond.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a track with no interior to read, and that is precisely its argument: Liam Howlett strips vocal language down to a count-in, a hook that names its own reach into the crowd, and a demand to shout, so the words function as rhythmic triggers rather than statements. It belongs to the 1992 Experience moment when rave hardcore treated the sampled shout and the numeric count as pure ignition for a floor, meaning delegated entirely to bodies and bass. The refusal to say anything is the point — the music reaches, and the only content it wants back is a shout.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"words stripped to ignition so bodies and bass carry the meaning","built":"count-in is the only content the lyric wants back from the floor"},
"the-prodigy~narayan": {"haiku":"A spiritual/apocalyptic narrative about transformation and awakening. As the western sun falls and the morning comes, a growing power and energy rises—the repeated invocation of Narayana (a Hindu deity) frames this as spiritual ascension.","opus":"A mystical, uplifting incantation built on a Sanskrit devotional chant, sensing the day's decline and assuring the listener their moment has arrived. As a swelling surge of energy and strength takes hold, the track treats spiritual awakening less as narrative than as pure momentum, urging the listener toward transformation.","sonnet":"A song about rising energy and collective awakening — the narrator feels a growing power within as a western age gives way to something new, weaving the Vedic mantra \"Om Namo Narayana\" into the sense that a transformative moment has arrived.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track stages the sinking of Occidental dominance as an ecstatic event rather than a catastrophe: the light going down over the everyone is not mourned but welcomed as the signal that a spent order is burning off and something else is arriving. Kula Shaker's Crispian Mills, singing over Prodigy production, routes that political claim through a growing power and an energy passing between two bodies, so the reckoning registers less as argument than as heat and transmission. The handover turns literal in the shift to the chanted Sanskrit petition to Narayana, the Vishnu aspect who abides through the dissolution and remaking of worlds, which recasts the burning as the moment an Eastern devotional current floods the vacated frame. The refusal to run and the insistence that the hour has arrived convert dread into summons, so an apocalypse taken at face value becomes, underneath, a hymn to what survives and grows once the old light fails.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","politics & society","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"the collapse of a spent order welcomed as transmission rather than loss","built":"Sanskrit petition floods the vacated frame at the lyric's pivot"},
"the-prodigy~nasty":{"haiku":"Raw, vulgar imagery of justice perverted into waste and degradation, with violence and bodily harm presented as inevitable and inescapable consequences.","opus":"A snarling big-beat provocation built for menace rather than meaning, where the repeated boast is that this is filth with intent; the narrator insists he's no dabbler but fully at home in the dirt.","sonnet":"The song is built almost entirely around the repeated word \"nasty\" and a handful of associative phrases describing something raw, dirty, and transgressive, without developing a specific narrative — the mood and the adjective are the whole content.","src":"haiku","fable":"Late-period Prodigy operating as a taunt-machine rather than a narrative: the title word is barked in pairs and paired with escalating tags of filth, exploitation, and injury until it stops meaning anything specific and becomes pure aggressive texture, the sound of a threat rather than its content. The one line that breaks the chant insists the speaker is no tourist, no slumming visitor to this ugliness but a native of it, and the post-chorus keeps pulling the same direction, downward and downward, so the track's whole architecture is a descent that never bottoms out and never explains itself. That refusal to develop is the point: this is menace as attitude, a snarl staged for the floor, where saying it louder and dirtier is the only argument being made.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"menace as attitude, descent without floor and without explanation","built":"refusal to develop the chant is itself the argument being made"},
"the-prodigy~no-tourists": {"haiku":"Exclusivity and danger, warning outsiders away from a dangerous territory.","opus":"A terse warning built on a single refrain, no tourists, no free ride, no sight to see, less a story than a mantra of exclusion and forward motion. The repetition itself becomes the hook, a locked-in chant of hostility toward passive onlookers. The title track of The Prodigy's 2018 album, it distills the English electronic-punk band's aesthetic to a few defiant words riding a relentless, propulsive groove.","sonnet":"A bare-bones boundary declaration — no free rides, no sights to see, no welcome mat. The repetition is the entire argument: you are not invited here, nothing is given away, and the door is closed. Whether urban, subcultural, or personal, the refusal is total and the song offers nothing beyond the refusal itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track of the Prodigy's final Flint-era record is barely a lyric at all — a barked slogan hammered flat by repetition until it becomes a threat rather than a sentiment. The two clauses set up a refusal machine: this is not a place you visit and gawp at, and nothing here comes without cost. Stripping away every line except the free-ride denial mid-song is the whole gesture — the band burning the mask off the tourist's fantasy of consequence-free spectacle and demanding you either commit or get out. Read as the manifesto of veterans who spent a career refusing to be a heritage act, it is an anti-nostalgia snarl: come as a participant paying the toll, not a sightseer expecting the show for nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"spectacle denied to anyone unwilling to pay the toll","built":"stripping every line except the free-ride denial is the whole gesture"},
"the-prodigy~omen": {"haiku":"A minimal and repetitive warning about an inevitable sign of doom written on a wall. The track emphasizes automaticity and inescapable premonition with little narrative elaboration.","opus":"A stark, foreboding piece hinging on the repeated insistence that the warning signs are plainly visible and will not disappear, an inescapable portent while everyone else moves through life on autopilot.","sonnet":"A sparse, ominous warning: writing is already on the wall, the outcome is inevitable, and people are running on automation — something is coming that cannot be avoided.","src":"haiku","fable":"Almost the entire lyric is three phrases hammered flat: a dread that has already arrived, a warning nobody can unsee, and a body that keeps going through the motions anyway. That is the whole point — this is a Prodigy rave-rock stomp built for the drop, not the sentence, so the words work like a klaxon rather than a story, refusing to develop because the situation they name has stopped developing. The one flicker of meaning is the collision the chorus stages: full foreknowledge of catastrophe set against total autopilot, someone who can read the sign perfectly and still can only run on automation. The insistence on now, chanted until it loses tense, turns the omen into a permanent present with no future left to warn about.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","madness & the mind"],"means":"full foreknowledge of catastrophe set against total autopilot","built":"now chanted until it loses tense and becomes a permanent present"},
"the-prodigy~omen-reprise":{"haiku":"Predictable catastrophe ignored through automation, warning written but unheeded.","opus":"A stripped, looping reprise built on dread, the writing on the wall that won't disappear and people running on autopilot toward an inescapable fate. Menace conveyed through relentless repetition.","sonnet":"A short chant-based track declaring that an unavoidable sign is coming — the writing is on the wall and can't be ignored; people running on automation are warned that something is approaching; it functions as a foreboding loop rather than a developed narrative.","src":"haiku"},
"the-prodigy~out-of-space": {"haiku":"Consciousness transported to cosmic dimensions and sent to outer space to find alien races; the brain becomes the cargo for dimensional exploration.","opus":"A rave track built almost entirely from a Max Romeo sample about launching someone off the planet to find a new race and hijacking minds into other realms. There's no story beyond the hypnotic, dub-derived hooks, the whole thing engineered as pure dancefloor lift-off.","sonnet":"A minimal track built on two sampled loops — one about taking consciousness to another dimension, one about sending someone to outer space to find another race — functioning more as a hypnotic rave incantation than as a developed lyrical argument.","src":"haiku","fable":"Almost every line here is borrowed goods — the interstellar-exile taunt lifted from Max Romeo and Lee Perry's Chase the Devil, the mind-relocation boast pulled off a Kool Keith verse. Prodigy's early rave method treats these as raw ore rather than text, grinding two stolen hooks into a churning loop until Jamaican dub paranoia and New York hip-hop swagger dissolve into one shared drug-floor threat. The lone near-authored instruction, an order to listen hard, becomes a knowing wink: nothing waits underneath except the reprocessing itself, the sample turned into its own subject.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"In Max Romeo’s original the one being sent to outer space is Lucifer himself — the rave repurposes an exorcism as a trip.","themes":["artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"dub paranoia and hip-hop swagger dissolved into a shared drug-floor threat","built":"the sample turned into its own subject by the reprocessing"},
"the-prodigy~phoenix": {"haiku":"A speaker asks if a listener can perceive their desire and attraction; they declare the listener the sovereign of their dreams.","opus":"A hypnotic seduction built almost entirely on a single repeated question, asking whether you can hear the singer's love buzz, punctuated by the declaration that you are the king of my dreams. Desire distilled to a mantra. The Prodigy reshape Nirvana's Love Buzz here, itself a Shocking Blue cover, dragging that fuzzed hook into their British electronic machinery, the repetition turning a simple come-on into something insistent, buzzing and trance-like.","sonnet":"A simple, earnest devotion addressed to someone who is the center of the speaker's dreaming life — the love buzz is audible if you would only listen, and the one request is just to be believed when they say you are everything. Minimal words, maximum insistence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Almost the entire lyric is two borrowed lines from Shocking Blue's Love Buzz, worn to the bone by repetition, and that erosion is the point: on Always Outnumbered, Never Outgunned, Liam Howlett is building dance-floor pressure, not confessing, so the phrases function as rhythmic cells rather than sentiment. The come-hither question and the adoring king-of-my-dreams line lose their romantic meaning under machine repetition and become pure hook, and the real hinge is the drop from the insistent buzz refrain to the bare, cut-off can-you-hear — a demand for response that keeps circling because it is never answered. What sounds like courtship is closer to a signal transmitted on loop until it means only its own pulse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"courtship language worn to a signal transmitted on loop","built":"drop from the insistent refrain to the bare cut-off demand circles because it is never answered"},
"the-prodigy~piranha": {"haiku":"A predatory force with razor teeth and vice-like grip tears apart anything in its path; losing control means being dragged under by an unrelenting hold.","opus":"A minimal, menacing dance track built on a predator metaphor: razor teeth and a vice grip that will tear you apart if you lose control and let it pull you under. The lyrics are more atmosphere than story, evoking a dangerous, addictive force (a drug, a scene, a pull) that takes hold of anyone who can't resist.","sonnet":"A minimal, aggressive metaphor for losing control to something predatory and all-consuming — the piranha represents a force that tears apart anyone who slips.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric is almost entirely a hard-consonant chant, and that is the point: reduced to a stuttered name, a set of teeth, and a warning, it works less as description than as a threat delivered in rhythm. The Prodigy build the track the way a shoal feeds, out of a few clipped words repeated until they become pure attack, so meaning lives in the escalation rather than any narrative. When the mid-section finally addresses a you and warns against losing control before something pulls you under and takes you whole, the piranha stops being a fish and becomes any appetite that swallows a person who slips, the small danger that only becomes lethal in numbers and momentum.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"small appetite only lethal when it finds numbers and momentum","built":"hard-consonant chant built like a shoal — few clipped words repeated into attack"},
"the-prodigy~rat-poison":{"haiku":"Toxic destruction and anti-establishment aggression presented as remedy.","opus":"A stuttering, menacing brag built on one image, holding both poison and its remedy, the word rat spat out until it becomes rhythm and threat at once. Meaning matters less than the sneer and the boom of the beat. The Prodigy, British electronic-punk agitators, made a career of this, the poison-and-remedy line a knowing nod to their own back catalogue, language shredded into pure aggressive texture.","sonnet":"A boast built on paradox — the speaker carries both the lethal thing and its antidote, the poison and the remedy in the same hands. The relentless repetition of possession turns it into something ritualistic, a declaration of dangerous power that doesn't explain itself, just insists on its own reality with percussive force.","src":"haiku"},
"the-prodigy~rebel-radio":{"haiku":"A rebel radio broadcasts a message of resistance and awakening to all who hear. The medium carries a call to freedom and dissent.","opus":"A chant-driven ode to pirate transmission and the outlaw sound as a rallying call, the message simple and the delivery all momentum. Late Prodigy leaning on ragga cadence and repetition to make defiance feel like a broadcast you can't switch off.","sonnet":"A minimalist call-to-action built around the repeated image of rebel radio broadcasting a message that is clear and universal — a sound playing in everyone's ear that calls them to something, though the precise cause is left open. The energy is collective and insurgent.","src":"haiku"},
"the-prodigy~rhythm-bomb-feat-flux-pavilion": {"haiku":"Physical compulsion overrides thought: rhythm demands movement, and the body answers before the mind can intervene.","opus":"A pure dancefloor demand, craving a beat strong enough to override thought and force the body to move, the whole point being physical surrender to rhythm.","sonnet":"A song about the visceral need for rhythm to animate the body — the beat is not a preference but a physical requirement, and the track is essentially a demand for the music to keep going.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric strips itself down to a single appetite stated as need rather than want — the body demanding the beat that will animate it — and then refuses to develop, because development would be a betrayal of the point. What looks like emptiness is a functional score: the words exist only to cue and count in the drop, the title's explosive noun turned into a percussion hit rather than a metaphor. Pairing The Prodigy's assaultive breakbeat instinct with Flux Pavilion's dubstep bombast, the track treats meaning as friction to be burned off, so the compulsive repetition becomes the actual subject — a mind emptied on purpose until only the pulse and the reflex to move remain.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"mind emptied on purpose until only the pulse and the reflex remain","built":"the title's explosive noun functions as a percussion hit not a metaphor"},
"the-prodigy~roadblox": {"haiku":"Drive through obstacles without stopping; reject enforced silence and reclaim the power of noise against tyranny and control.","opus":"A pure adrenaline chant about smashing through roadblocks, daring someone to floor it and burn rubber. Beneath the drag-race imagery is an anti-authority stance, rejecting silence and imposed control, hijacking and taking back what's been blocked.","sonnet":"A blunt call to barrel through obstacles, reject imposed silence, and reclaim sonic territory from those who would control it — essentially a manifesto for aggressive forward momentum.","src":"haiku","fable":"A track that is almost pure command — the lyric is a set of imperatives shouted at a driver to gun it through the barricade rather than stop, and the near-total lack of anything beyond that is the point. The Prodigy build the whole thing on the collision of two verbs, taking back and driving on, so that reclamation and trespass become the same gesture: authority throws up a roadblock and the only sanctioned response here is to refuse it at speed. What tiny image the song offers, hot tires burning past the checkpoint, works less as a scene than as a dare flung at the listener to prove what they have got. The repetition is the meaning — it is a chant meant to override hesitation, the mind reduced to a single accelerating loop until stopping stops being thinkable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","freedom & escape"],"means":"reclamation and trespass made the same gesture at the barricade","built":"two verbs — taking back and driving on — carry the entire argument"},
"the-prodigy~rok-weiler": {"haiku":"A predatory figure prowls through streets at night hunting victims, presenting a threatening and menacing presence.","opus":"Built on a chanted refrain and a warning to watch yourself, the song evokes a menacing, predatory figure stalking an alleyway at night, biting at heels like an attack dog.","sonnet":"A predatory figure prowls alleys at night, biting at heels — presenting itself as feral, dangerous, and unstoppable, like the dog breed the title names.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole conceit lives in a mangled breed name that turns a chugging guitar chant into a snarling attack dog, and the track wants nothing more than that single vicious pun. A predator prowls an alleyway, snapping at ankles, groomed and lethal under streetlamps, but the images are scraps hung on a hook rather than any unfolding scene. The Prodigy stage a threat as pure loop, so the imperative to mind your back reads less as narrative than as a growl repeating until menace hardens into pulse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder"],"means":"menace delivered as a growl hardening into pulse","built":"mangled breed name makes the guitar chant and the attack dog the same thing"},
"the-prodigy~ruff-in-the-jungle-bizness": {"haiku":"Jungle music and raw, aggressive sound as cultural expression.","opus":"A rave-era chant riding the jungle vibe, calling out bad boys and repeating its title like a DJ hyping a crowd, all rhythm and atmosphere rather than message. From The Prodigy's 1992 debut Experience, it captures the English act in their early breakbeat-hardcore phase, Liam Howlett building energy from samples and shouted catchphrases before the band hardened into their later punk-electronic form.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole thing is assembled from a handful of soundsystem hollers, a Jamaican-inflected shout to the toughs and a summons to feel the rainforest churn, chopped and rebuilt as percussion rather than speech. That choice is the whole substance: the Prodigy run the human larynx through the same treatment as any breakbeat, so the syllables register as rhythm and crowd-command, the toaster's task shrunk to driving dancers across a warehouse. Sense hides inside the loop's relentlessness, never in a completed thought, and the recurrence itself does all the talking.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"the larynx conscripted as just another breakbeat","built":"toaster syllables chopped into percussion, sense dissolved by the loop"},
"the-prodigy~run-with-the-wolves": {"haiku":"The narrator rejects compromisers who seek false unity; they hunt alone with predatory intensity rather than pack with the weak.","opus":"A predator-and-prey taunt built on the divide between those who run wild and the hunter who cuts them down. The Prodigy make menace itself the whole message, all snarl and pursuit.","sonnet":"A short dominance statement — someone who thought they were running with the pack is exposed as prey, not predator, and the narrator is the one doing the hunting.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole charge here comes from a predator taxonomy: the target belongs to a wild pack, while the speaker casts himself as the bred tracker set loose to run down what that pack shelters. That distinction is the sneer under the taunt — belonging to something feral is framed as weakness, while the speaker's own leashed servitude becomes the guarantee that the kill lands. The looping demand about what the enemy will do once the baying closes in expects no answer; the repetition functions as the sound of pursuit tightening, a siren nearing rather than any argument that develops. Matched to the martial swagger of Invaders Must Die, the track works as near-pure sonic aggression, handed only enough language to mark out a hunter and its quarry.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"the hunter's leash recast as superiority over the wild pack","built":"repetition functions as pursuit narrowing, not argument developing"},
"the-prodigy~serial-thrilla": {"haiku":"Serial killing and predatory behavior presented as edgy performance.","opus":"A snarling blast of self-mythologizing menace, the narrator styling himself as a wrecker and rabble-rouser, daring you to sample him and give in, drilling twin taunts into a hypnotic threat where swagger matters more than sense. The Prodigy cut this for their 1997 breakthrough, Keith Flint's punkish sneer fusing big-beat electronics with real aggression as they blew dance music up into something confrontational and rock-sized.","sonnet":"Pure kinetic assertion — a voice that identifies itself as a wrecker, a crowd-shatterer, an everytime presence demanding submission. There's no narrative, only escalating pressure: taste me, give in, this is what a serious killer sounds like when it's also a sound system.","src":"haiku","fable":"The menace here is a sales pitch, a rave act branding its own thrill as a controlled substance the crowd is dared to swallow. Barely a lyric, the words stack into a rap-sheet of epithets — damage, disruption, corruption — boasted in the third person like a wrestler's arena billing. On The Fat of the Land, at the peak of the crossover to hostile arenas, Keith Flint's stage persona narrates its own effect, the mainliner boast turning the show into a fix. The pivot drops the mask when the third-person billing turns into a direct command, making the listener the intended victim of a danger pushed hard enough to be taken.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism"],"means":"the rave act sells its own danger as a controlled substance","built":"third-person billing pivots to direct command, making the crowd the victim"},
"the-prodigy~smack-my-bitch-up": {"haiku":"An aggressive, provocative electronic hook built entirely on a repeated two-phrase chant pairing pitch manipulation with violence, delivered as a stripped-down slogan.","opus":"A deliberately confrontational sample-loop the Prodigy weaponized as provocation, its shock-value refrain designed to bait outrage; the meaning lives in the taboo transgression and the moral panic it courted rather than any story.","sonnet":"The lyrics consist almost entirely of a repeated two-phrase chant with no narrative content beyond the aggressive, provocative slogan itself.","src":"haiku","fable":"The entire lyric is two commands bolted together and looped, sampled from Ultramagnetic MCs and stripped of any narrative that could soften it — the song is a machine, not a confession. Meaning lives in the pairing: the DJ's technical instruction to change the pitch sits flush against a slur of violence, so studio jargon and menace become grammatically identical, one more knob to twist for a rush. The 1997 big-beat context sharpens this into provocation; The Prodigy weaponise a hook that refuses to explain or apologise, daring the dancefloor to chant an ugliness it barely registers as words. What the relentless repetition does is drain the phrase of statement and turn it into pure aggressive texture — the point is the adrenaline of the loop, and the discomfort of noticing what your body is moving to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Jonas Åkerlund’s notorious video plays the whole night as a first-person rampage — drink, drugs, violence — and its last mirror-shot reveals the perpetrator is a woman.","themes":["violence & murder","party & hedonism"],"means":"studio jargon and a slur of violence made grammatically identical","built":"relentless repetition drains the phrase of statement, leaving pure aggressive texture"},
"the-prodigy~speedway-theme-from-fastlane":{"haiku":"Violence and sexual aggression are collapsed into a single refrain; the command to change pitch merges with violent domination.","opus":"Two looped commands, change my pitch up and smack my bitch up, ride a manic rush of speed and provocation. This B-side twin to The Prodigy's most notorious single strips everything to a taunting mantra, Liam Howlett weaponising a sampled phrase for shock and velocity rather than any narrative, the fast lane its only real subject.","src":"haiku"},
"the-prodigy~spitfire": {"haiku":"Martial prowess and combat capability are repeatedly asserted; the speaker claims they would have been a fierce fighter in WWII.","opus":"Pure aggression as posture, borrowing the wartime fighter plane as a boast of destructive power. The Prodigy strip the idea down to a chant, all threat and no argument.","sonnet":"Pure bravado — the singer declares himself a weapon of war, a force so fierce that in another era they'd have named a fighter plane after him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A near-textless track built almost entirely from one boast and its detonation: the singer casts herself as a Spitfire, the British fighter plane, then lets the word split at its own seam so the machine's name becomes the thing it does — fire. The whole lyric is that pun weaponized and looped, the aircraft collapsed into pure ignition, with the only other phrase a flat assurance of capability rather than any argument for it. On Liam Howlett's 2004 record the point is sonic menace over meaning, so the repetition isn't emptiness but a rev-and-strafe engine: the line is loaded, dropped, and reloaded until the swagger reads less as claim than as a run being made again and again.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","war & battle"],"means":"a boast looped until swagger reads as a run being made repeatedly","built":"the aircraft's name splits at its own seam, collapsing machine into ignition"},
"the-prodigy~take-me-to-the-hospital":{"haiku":"Chaotic scene of a crash or disaster invoked through fragmentary repetition. The song evokes urgency and emergency amid fashion-obsessed crowds.","opus":"A frantic chant built around being taken to the hospital and the scene of a crash, laced with a spider-and-Dracula image of a deceptive predator that lures and bites. It channels a chaotic, adrenaline-charged party energy.","sonnet":"Built around the image of arriving at the scene of a crash, the song describes an encounter with a deceptively friendly but dangerous figure — likened to a spider — that ends in an adrenaline-fueled collision. The call-and-response chorus anchors it to a live, crowd-facing energy.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is The Prodigy staging the club as a casualty ward, chanting the plea for an ambulance as its rave hook until being wrecked and being rescued become the same ecstatic overload, the trip to hospital indistinguishable from the night out. The verse shrinks the whole seduction into a nursery-rhyme predator arriving disguised as a friend, met not with caution but with a willing bite of adrenaline — exactly the reckless yes the track demands of you. Flint's greeting ushers the crowd toward the aftermath of a collision as if it were a destination, the ragga MC working the floor with hands-up call-and-response and a roar for the capital's dancefloor, so the smash has already happened and everyone is being welcomed in to celebrate the ruin.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title is also the name of the label the band had founded the year before — the song christens their own imprint, the invitation to ruin doubling as a business card.","themes":["party & hedonism","addiction & self-destruction","night & the city"],"means":"being wrecked and being rescued collapsed into one ecstatic overload","built":"ambulance plea used as rave hook until hospital and night out are indistinguishable"},
"the-prodigy~the-day-is-my-enemy": {"haiku":"Antagonism toward daylight; the night is friend and refuge while day brings hostility.","opus":"A stark repeated declaration that daytime is the speaker's enemy and night is his friend, painting a nocturnal mind at odds with the daylight world.","sonnet":"The lyrics establish a single opposition — the day is hostile, the night is a refuge — with no further development beyond that repeated contrast.","src":"haiku","fable":"There is exactly one idea here, an inversion of the ordinary contract that day is safe and night is threat, and the track refuses to develop it because it doesn't need to: the couplet is a creed, not an argument, and repeating it unchanged is the point. Everything the song has to say about the person living this way is carried instead by the imperative that swarms around the creed and finally eats it, a bark to get down that mutates into a stutter and stops resolving into words at all. That degrading chant is the real content, the Prodigy translating a nocturnal, hostile-to-daylight state into pure rhythmic compulsion rather than describing it, so the meaning lands as pressure and command instead of confession.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["night & the city"],"means":"a nocturnal creed stated once then enforced by rhythmic compulsion","built":"the bark to get down mutates into stutter and stops resolving into words"},
"the-prodigy~the-heat-the-energy": {"haiku":"Kinetic intensity and pure sonic force propel the body; rhythm and bass activate primal physicality.","opus":"The Prodigy spent decades weaponizing rave into something physical and confrontational, and this incantatory address strips their method to the bone. Demanding the crowd feel the kinetic force of the music as bodily fact rather than sound, the reduction of language to its barest elements mirrors the track's function: pure transmission of intensity with no narrative required.","sonnet":"A direct, incantatory address to the crowd, demanding they physically feel the kinetic force of the music — \"the heat, the energy\" — as something real and bodily rather than merely heard. The stripping of language to its barest elements mirrors the music's function: pure transmission of intensity, no narrative required.","src":"haiku","fable":"The lyric is a two-word incantation looped past the point of language, and that is the whole point: on a rave record cut when the 1994 Criminal Justice Act was criminalising the very gatherings Liam Howlett wrote for, verbal meaning is exactly what the track refuses to supply. There is nothing to parse because the sensation is the argument — heat and energy are not being described but summoned, the sampled voice functioning as a call-and-response trigger rather than a message, offloading meaning onto the crowd's body and the rig's low end. The single interrogative that keeps returning, can you feel it, is the tell: it demands not comprehension but confirmation, staking the song's worth on a physical yes the recording alone can never deliver.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism"],"means":"sensation as argument, summoning rather than describing heat and energy","built":"the sole interrogative demands physical confirmation, not comprehension"},
"the-prodigy~the-narcotic-suite-claustrophobic-sting": {"haiku":"Mind deteriorating audibly, repetition as symptom of cognitive collapse and panic.","opus":"A drug-induced descent into psychosis rendered mostly through laughter and screams, HAL-echoing dread of a mind coming apart. Words dissolve into pure panic, so it reads as sensation more than statement.","sonnet":"A looping descent into dissociation: the single repeated phrase \"my mind is going\" punctuated by hysterical laughter and wordless cries maps the claustrophobic sensation of losing mental grip. The minimalism is the point — there is nothing else left.","src":"haiku","fable":"Barely a lyric at all — a track built from sampled laughter and a single sentence run into the ground — so everything hangs on the loop itself, not the words. My mind is going is HAL 9000's line as it is switched off in 2001, and Liam Howlett strips it of its film context until it stops being a character speaking and becomes the machine's own condition: a rave instrument reporting its own breakdown, thought dissolving into repetition. The laughter that frames it is not release but the claustrophobia the title names — cackling that never resolves into a joke, looping in the same airless space as the phrase. Placed as the closing movement of the Narcotic Suite, it reads the comedown rather than the high: the mind not blissed out but coming apart, aware enough to keep narrating its own going even as it goes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"a mind narrating its own dissolution even as it dissolves","built":"HAL 9000's shutdown line stripped of film context until the instrument reports its own breakdown"},
"the-prodigy~their-law":{"haiku":"Rejection of authority and institutional control, with defiance against oppressive law.","opus":"A snarling anti-authority anthem, an opening sample decrying disrespect for the law flipped into a fist-in-the-air chant claiming the singer is the law and no one can beat it, ending in open contempt for the establishment's rules. Sundown crackdown imagery gives it a riot's edge. The Prodigy aimed this squarely at Britain's anti-rave legislation, making it a genuine protest record wrapped in scorched-earth breakbeat.","sonnet":"Authority asserts itself as absolute and unbeatable, and the song's answer is a straight refusal — not an argument, just contempt. The crackdown comes at sundown but it changes nothing; the response is the same four words repeated until they become a chant. Law claims dominion over everyone, and everyone who hears this is invited to reject it outright.","src":"haiku"},
"the-prodigy~thunder": {"haiku":"A powerful force without visible cause that shatters structures and breaks through barriers, leaving only destruction in its wake.","opus":"The words evoke a powerful, rain-less thunder that breaks walls and windows and comes from inside, using the image as a metaphor for overwhelming force.","sonnet":"The song evokes a force — heard as thunder but producing no rain — that operates entirely from within, breaking through walls and barriers without any visible weather source, suggesting an internal or man-made pressure with destructive physical impact.","src":"haiku","fable":"Thunder arriving in a dry sky is the whole conceit: a low-end rumble with no cloud, no downpour, no meteorological cause to justify the pressure it puts on the chest. The Prodigy treat the phenomenon as engineering rather than metaphor, tuning a bassline to hit like distant artillery so the body registers a storm the barometer would deny. When the stammered vocal keeps buckling toward Inside, the source of the noise migrates from the horizon into the skull, an internal weather system firing adrenaline with nowhere to discharge. Meaning here is beside the point; the near-mute loop simply drills one felt sensation until pursuing any further reading feels like a category error.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nature & the elements"],"means":"internal weather firing adrenaline with nowhere to discharge","built":"a stammered vocal migrates the storm's source from the horizon into the skull"},
"the-prodigy~timebomb-zone": {"haiku":"Time running out and apocalyptic urgency, with a sense of inevitability.","opus":"A tension-cranking chant built on the ticking down of a timebomb, the game going on while time runs out and nearly vanishes, a repeated warning that you cannot stop what is coming. It works as pure kinetic urgency rather than story. The Prodigy trade in exactly this build-and-release menace, the British big-beat outfit turning a handful of ominous phrases into a coiled, dancefloor-ready countdown to detonation.","src":"haiku","fable":"There is almost no lyric here, only a countdown that never resolves — the same handful of phrases cycling as the clock drains toward zero. The Prodigy have always weaponized the rave chant, the phrase you can shout back before you understand it, and here the trick is that the bomb never goes off: the loop keeps insisting time is nearly gone yet the game goes on, so the panic becomes a groove you stay inside rather than a crisis you escape. The refusal to develop is the point — a body kept permanently at the last second, adrenaline with no release, which is exactly the dancefloor state the track is engineering.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism"],"means":"the body kept permanently at the last second, adrenaline with no release","built":"the countdown cycles endlessly but the bomb never goes off, panic becoming groove"},
"the-prodigy~voodoo-people-pendulum-remix":{"haiku":"A celebration of those who fearlessly do what others dare not—the outlaws, the transgressors, the boundary-breakers.","opus":"Almost no lyric, just a chant conjuring outsider energy, people who do what others fear to. The remix strips it to ritual incantation over rave momentum, celebrating transgressive nerve as a kind of magic.","sonnet":"The lyrics are essentially a repeated chant celebrating \"voodoo people\" who do what others dare not — there is no narrative beyond this incantatory invocation of transgressive, magic-tinged defiance.","src":"haiku"},
"the-prodigy~wake-up-call-feat-kool-keith": {"haiku":"A call to rouse people from complacency or sleep, urging them to engage and feel something real.","opus":"A veteran act rallying itself back onto the road, the chant of a comeback tour crossed with the fog of not being able to snap fully awake, Kool Keith's presence turning the rousing call slightly surreal.","sonnet":"A minimal, repetitive declaration that the band is back on tour and that the audience needs to wake up — the content is almost entirely performative and meta, a rallying call with almost no developed theme beyond the announcement of return.","src":"haiku","fable":"Arriving on the band's first album in seven years — the one Liam Howlett cut without his own frontmen, drafting guests instead — this is a lyric that is almost nothing but the fact of returning: a long absence and a body that cannot rouse itself, set against a drill-count that keeps trying to force it awake. The words never resolve the tension they name — the count from one to four and the command to wake up loop without ever landing on the alertness they demand, so the track enacts the very grogginess it is trying to shake off. Kool Keith's presence turns the refrain into a hip-hop roll-call, but the confession that runs underneath it keeps admitting the machine has not actually started; the repetition is the meaning, an engine turning over and over that refuses to catch.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"an engine turning over and over that refuses to catch","built":"the drill-count loops without landing on the alertness it demands, enacting the grogginess it names"},
"the-prodigy~wall-of-death":{"haiku":"A defiant call to abandon false institutions and conventions, rejecting sterilization and conformity. The song embraces chaos and destruction as a response to systematic control.","opus":"A snarling invitation into the mosh pit as a rite of confrontation, the wall of death staged as a purge of fear and money and everything that binds. Late-era Prodigy channeling pure punk nihilism through electronic aggression.","sonnet":"A blunt, confrontational provocation that rejects conformity and commercialism in favor of reckless, defiant energy, repeatedly inviting the listener to follow to a dangerous extreme with contempt for caution or consequence.","src":"haiku","fable":"Late-period Prodigy stripped to a chant and a dare, this is a summons before it is a song: the wall of death is the mosh-pit ritual where two halves of a crowd charge into each other, and the track offers it as the only sacrament worth taking. Everything else gets refused in the same breath — the money, the sold-out heart-attack panic of a body pushed past safety — so that the negation isn't nihilism but a clearing-out, burning off every reason not to run at the wall. The refusal to visualise or be sterilised is the whole thesis in miniature: don't picture it, don't sand it smooth, just come. That the lyric barely exists is the meaning; it repeats and curses because it wants your body in the pit, not your interpretation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","freedom & escape"],"means":"the mosh-pit collision offered as the only sacrament worth taking","built":"the lyric barely existing is the point — repetition and cursing want the body not the interpretation"},
"the-prodigy~we-live-forever": {"haiku":"Immortality is achieved through present intensity and heat; eternal life burns forever as time intensifies in the moment before death.","opus":"The British big-beat pioneers trade storytelling for raw incantation, chanting an immortality mantra rooted in the present moment and lacing it with menacing boasts of overheating the mind and stoking the blaze hotter. The minimal, aggressive electronic hooks work as a rave call-to-arms, all adrenaline and dancefloor threat over any real lyrical content.","sonnet":"A maximalist declaration of indestructibility — the moment is now, the collective is here, and the heat keeps turning higher. There's no story or arc, only the relentless assertion of presence and permanence, a rave-era mantra that turns volume itself into a claim about survival.","src":"haiku","fable":"The Prodigy fuse two impulses that prove identical: a crowd's present-tense refusal of mortality, and a menacing furnace-master vowing to superheat the listener's brain past supersonic degrees. That deathlessness is located in the drop, not any afterlife, holding only while the beat holds. The oven is really the sound-system, a machine that manufactures ecstasy by cranking intensity until thought scorches off. Its escalating riff climbs and climbs while eternity dissolves into looped chant, so the claim on permanence is enacted by the endless circling rather than argued, alive only as long as the burn keeps rising.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"deathlessness located in the drop, not any afterlife, alive only while the beat holds","built":"the escalating riff enacts eternity by circling endlessly rather than arguing for it"},
"the-prodigy~weather-experience": {"haiku":"Weather forecasts describe regional atmospheric conditions and transitions from sunshine to cloud cover—purely meteorological description without emotional content.","opus":"A track built from sampled British shipping and weather forecasts, dry announcements of fine days over Wales and Scotland, sunshine clouding over, showers backing south-westerly, looped and drifting rather than sung. There is no narrative, only the deadpan cadence of the radio turned instrumental texture. The Prodigy, in their early rave era, thread these found-sound broadcasts through the electronic build, mood and atmosphere conjured entirely from the mundane poetry of the forecast.","src":"haiku","fable":"Liam Howlett's whole trick on his 1992 debut was to lift found broadcast — announcements, ephemera, chatter — and strand it over hardcore breakbeat, and the raw material here is the beige BBC bulletin voice reciting a regional outlook for Wales and the north. Setting that measured meteorological drone against ecstasy tempo empties it of information and turns the roll-call of counties into pure texture, an institutional monotone conscripted for the sweatbox. The joke, and the era's logic in one gesture, is a state broadcaster's calmest register hijacked to narrate the only conditions the floor cared about.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"a state broadcaster's calmest register hijacked for the sweatbox","built":"BBC meteorological drone stranded over hardcore tempo until county names become pure texture"},
"the-prodigy~wild-frontier":{"haiku":"Warns of a dangerous frontier where dreams become real or blood spills, requiring facing inner fears. The song frames life as an untamed wilderness demanding courage.","opus":"A charge into unmapped, dangerous territory as metaphor for chasing something real at genuine risk. The Prodigy dress the thrill-seeker's creed in tribal menace: where dreams either come true or cost you blood, and fear must be faced head-on.","sonnet":"The lyrics evoke a dangerous, lawless territory off the beaten track where dreams and bloodshed are equally possible — the recurring warning to watch your back and face your fears frames this frontier as both a physical and psychological space of reckless risk.","src":"haiku","fable":"There is barely a lyric here, and that is the point: The Prodigy reduce the whole idea of adventure to a handful of B-movie slogans, then loop them until they stop meaning and start pulsing. The frontier is never a place, only a mood-word chanted to raise the pulse, and the one real proposition on offer — that out here your dreams come true or you bleed — is deliberately a coin-flip, danger and reward collapsed into the same threshold. As the track wears on even those slogans erode, sloughing off their tails until only the two charged fragments remain, the wild and danger, traded back and forth like a stripped-down warning siren. It is less a song about the edge of the map than a machine for simulating the adrenaline of standing on it, where the voice has been sanded down into another piece of percussion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape"],"means":"slogans looped until they stop meaning and start pulsing","built":"the voice sanded down into another piece of percussion as lyrics erode"},
"the-prodigy~wind-it-up": {"haiku":"Circular motion and calls for equal rights underscore a vow: the speaker will keep mobilizing and will not stop until justice circulates fully.","opus":"A sample-driven rave workout looping the reggae-derived call for equal rights and justice against chanted instructions to wind it up and go around. The Prodigy, from their early breakbeat-hardcore era, lean entirely on repetition and momentum here, borrowing a righteous vocal hook and stripping it to pure kinetic fuel, the meaning secondary to the escalating dancefloor propulsion that defined British rave culture at the time.","sonnet":"Equal rights and justice delivered as a rhythmic loop, the demand circling back on itself endlessly — wind it up, go around, wind it up again. The repetition is the point: justice doesn't arrive in a straight line, it keeps returning, and the song holds the tension of a world that goes around without ever quite getting there.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two sampled cells get bolted together here: a hype-man command to keep the floor churning, and a Jamaican sound-system chant demanding fairness and reckoning for the downtrodden of the moment. Early Prodigy runs on that graft, letting acid-house hedonism borrow gravity from dub and toasting culture, so that cranking a strobe-lit body and wanting the world set right register as one identical urge. Repetition is the trick, not development, because looping the borrowed slogan past sense strips its content until the phrase survives only as texture, a chant you bellow without parsing, which is precisely how a warehouse night converts protest into pure euphoria.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","politics & society"],"means":"hedonism and protest looped past sense until they register as one identical urge","built":"acid-house energy grafted onto dub toasting until the borrowed slogan survives only as texture"},
"the-prodigy~world-s-on-fire": {"haiku":"A warning of imminent global or personal catastrophe—the world/life burns and runs out of time, with danger constantly near.","opus":"The Prodigy's taunting apocalypse, a chant aimed at someone teetering on the brink of collapse. It's confrontational schadenfreude, delighting in a special someone's imminent ruin while the countdown ticks.","sonnet":"A countdown to implosion — someone has pushed everything to the absolute limit, and the world they've built is seconds from expiring; the taunt \"why aren't you dead yet?\" frames the whole thing as a challenge to survive what's coming.","src":"haiku","fable":"A chant built almost entirely from a single alarm phrase, this rides the logic of the countdown it eventually names: the near-total repetition isn't laziness but the sound of a warning that has stopped meaning anything through sheer overuse, catastrophe rendered as a party hook. The two variations do the real work — the wire that could trip and the expire that follows — collapsing the space between danger and its aftermath until there's no gap left to react in, which is what too close is measuring. The taunt aimed at someone special and the leering demand to know why they aren't dead yet turns the apocalyptic register personal and cruel, so the fire stops being weather and becomes appetite; the song wants the destruction it keeps announcing. Maxim's deadpan delivery keeps it icy rather than hysterical, which is the point: the end of the world sung as a come-on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","party & hedonism"],"means":"apocalyptic warning turned personal appetite, the fire wanting the destruction it announces","built":"deadpan delivery keeps the end-of-the-world chant icy rather than hysterical"},
"the-rapture~house-of-jealous-lovers":{"haiku":"A house of jealous lovers creates mutual restraint; counting grounds rhythm and chaos into numbered order.","opus":"Jealousy boiled down to one hypnotic chant, the title dwelling where partners bind and control each other, driving toward a confrontation and a bare count up to eight. The Rapture, cornerstones of early-2000s dance-punk, ride DFA production from Tim Goldsworthy and James Murphy, the paranoid hook and clanging cowbell turning romantic possessiveness into sheer physical release.","sonnet":"A space defined entirely by mutual possessiveness — hands binding hands, lovers unable to release their grip, jealousy structured into the architecture of togetherness. The shakedown is relentless and rhythmic, the counting its own kind of compulsion, desire as a system that traps everyone inside it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rh-factor~poetry":{"haiku":"Musicians and artists celebrate the collaborative creation of music and rhythm as expression and a force for human progression and learning.","opus":"A warm homage to how a piece of music comes into being, the rhythm players and horns shading in a sketch like paint, dropping the names of admired musicians and the tradition the players inherit. The RH Factor, the soul-jazz-hip-hop collective fronted by trumpeter Roy Hargrove, hands Q-Tip a verse honoring craft and mentorship, the hunt for direction standing in for artistic modesty.","sonnet":"A jazz-hip-hop meditation on collaborative creation, honoring Roy Hargrove and the lineage of players who give music its soul. The speaker traces how rhythm section, poet, and melody cohere into something larger than any individual — a living genealogy of funk and jazz made in Queens, offered freely to the needy.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~brown-sugar":{"haiku":"Colonial exploitation and sexual dominance merge across generations as power compounds through historical repetition without consequence or reckoning.","opus":"The Stones' most notorious song, yoking slavery, sexual violence and desire into a deliberately transgressive provocation. Its danceable menace has since made it the band's own most disowned creation.","sonnet":"A song set against the violence and sexual exploitation of the American slave trade, narrating scenes of whipping, sale, and abuse with a leering energy that collapses the distance between historical atrocity and the speaker's own appetites. It is deliberately and disturbingly ambiguous about whether it condemns or celebrates what it describes.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~carol":{"haiku":"A man tries to win Carol away from another lover by learning to dance and showing her a better time at a crowded club.","opus":"The Rolling Stones cover Chuck Berry on their debut, and the lyric brims with early rock-and-roll courtship, urging Carol not to let another steal her heart while the singer vows to learn to dance all night. A drive to a jam-packed joint, a hat-check girl, and a loud crowd conjure the jukebox romance of the era, the band paying homage to the American blues and R&B that formed them.","sonnet":"A young man woos Carol with an invitation to a rock and roll night out, promising dancing and the electric pull of a packed joint. His pitch is simple persistence — he will learn every move it takes to keep her attention and away from whoever else is competing for her heart.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~come-on":{"haiku":"Everything falls apart after separation from a lover; the narrator pleads for reconciliation, unable to start a car or his life without her.","opus":"The Stones' very first single, another Chuck Berry cover, catalogs everyday misery after a breakup, a dead car, a lost job, and a phone ringing with wrong numbers. The come-on refrain pivots from complaint to yearning, the singer insisting he belongs to his baby and she to him. A modest debut, it captures the young band cutting their teeth on the American rock and roll they idolized.","sonnet":"Everything has gone wrong since a breakup — the car will not start, the job is gone, the phone rings wrong numbers. The narrator catalogues petty misfortunes as symptoms of larger heartbreak, the whole stack of small catastrophes standing in for the one central fact: he needs her back and the distance is unbearable.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~doom-and-gloom":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic dread and environmental collapse loom; the speaker surveys ruins while seeking connection amid desolation.","opus":"A late-career state-of-the-world rant, war, fracking, poverty, drink, delivered as an apocalyptic bad dream, then shrugged off with an invitation to dance. The Stones facing collapse the only way they know: refusing to stop the party.","sonnet":"A speaker saturated with bad news, financial loss, environmental collapse, and political corruption retreats into nihilistic humor and the desire for a dancing partner as his only antidote to the relentless darkness of the world. The fantasy of connection — just one face through all the chaos — is small and human against the scale of what's going wrong.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~gimme-shelter":{"haiku":"The song catalogs threats closing in from all directions—war, fire, rape, murder, and flood—and pleads for shelter as civilization deteriorates around the speaker.","opus":"A song about existing under a perpetual menace of bloodshed, warfare and sexual violence, all sensed as terrifyingly close at hand, the singer frantic for refuge and grasping at love as the sole way out of the darkness.","sonnet":"Against a landscape of storm, fire, and flood, the song treats war, rape, and murder as ever-present, just-a-shot-away realities, and pleads for shelter from a world on the verge of annihilation — ending with the fragile hope that love is equally close.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~i-can-t-get-no-satisfaction":{"haiku":"Consumer culture manufactures desire faster than gratification can meet it; advertising preys on insecurity while pursuing romantic connection becomes another commodity that ultimately fails to fulfil.","opus":"The 1965 anthem of consumer-age frustration, Jagger jaded by advertising's empty promises and the emptiness of fame and thwarted desire, a young man told what to want and finding none of it satisfies.","sonnet":"Relentless commercial noise and sexual frustration combine in a portrait of a man bombarded by useless information on the radio and hollow advertising on TV, while women keep putting him off. Every attempt to connect — with the world, with people, with pleasure — bounces back empty.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~i-wanna-be-your-man":{"haiku":"A man desperately wants romantic and sexual intimacy, repeatedly begging for love and assurance from a woman.","opus":"A blunt, hammering come-on, the narrator demanding over and over to be a lover's man and begging to be told he's loved and understood, all propulsion and no subtlety. Famously written by Lennon and McCartney and handed to the Rolling Stones for one of their earliest singles, this raw R&B stomp shows the young band before their own songwriting bloomed, all youthful lust and borrowed swagger.","sonnet":"Desire stripped to its most direct statement — the speaker wants to be this person's lover, wants love returned and understood, and keeps saying so without elaboration or argument. There's no complication, no narrative: just the declaration, repeated until it becomes a kind of incantation.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~it-s-all-over-now":{"haiku":"A lover ran with every man in town, spent money carelessly, and caused pain. Now the tables turn—her turn to cry as the singer moves on.","opus":"A jilted man tallies the wrongs of a woman who ran around town and drained his money, then savors the reversal now that the tables have turned and she is the one weeping. The refrain is almost tender in its finality: love that once was, now closed for good. The Rolling Stones cut this Bobby Womack tune early on, a swaggering R&B cover that became their first UK number one and cemented their blues-borrowing swagger.","sonnet":"A man who once doted on a woman who cheated, spent his money, and humiliated him now watches the tables turn: she's the one left crying. Satisfaction without cruelty, closure framed as simple fact — what goes around comes around, and he's done.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~mothers-little-helper":{"haiku":"Pharmaceutical dependency props up exhausted domesticity, transforming pills into false salvation for mothers trapped in soul-crushing routine.","opus":"A 1966 dig at bored suburban wives dulling the monotony of housework with prescription sedatives, the tiny tablet standing as a grim verdict on how a worn-down, restless generation drugged itself through each identical day.","sonnet":"A portrait of a suburban housewife numbing the grind of domesticity with tranquilisers, the pills escalating from one to four to an overdose as the gap between the life she has and the life that might satisfy her widens until it kills her. The song is simultaneously satirical and genuinely bleak about the pharmacological management of women's discontent.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~not-fade-away":{"haiku":"Love is absolute and permanent—bigger than a Cadillac, real, and will never fade away. The singer declares a lasting commitment grounded in genuine feeling.","opus":"A young man's cocksure vow of devotion, insisting his love outsizes a Cadillac and will outlast a single day rather than flicker out. It reads less as plea than as command, certainty dressed up as courtship. The Stones took Buddy Holly's song, cranked the Bo Diddley beat harder and turned it into an early calling card, one of the covers that announced them as blues-and-rock inheritors before their own writing took over.","sonnet":"A confident declaration of love that refuses to shrink or expire. Bigger than a Cadillac, it demands reciprocation — this isn't pleading, it's a statement of terms. Love isn't a feeling here; it's a permanent, non-negotiable condition.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~paint-it-black":{"haiku":"Despair and grief have drained all color from perception; the narrator seeks to blacken the world to match their internal darkness.","opus":"Devastating grief where a mourner, watching a funeral cortege of black cars carrying his love who'll never return, wants the entire colorful world blotted out to match his blackened heart. The desire to paint everything black is depression turned outward, unable to face the facts of loss so he wishes the sun itself erased.","sonnet":"A man consumed by grief — likely over a death — cannot bear any color or light; he wants the entire world painted black to match his internal state, to make the outside mirror the darkness he cannot escape.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~satisfaction":{"haiku":"Consumer desire defeats satiation endlessly; advertising and conformity corrode human connection.","opus":"The definitive expression of consumer-age frustration, a young man bombarded by advertising, commercial media and sexual rejection who can never get what he's told to want.","sonnet":"A man saturated by media noise and commercial manipulation — radio pitching useless information, TV selling whiter shirts — finds no satisfaction anywhere, not from advertising and not from women who tell him to come back next week.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~street-fighting-man":{"haiku":"Social revolution tempts the powerless while compromised systems contain resistance and render the common person ineffective.","opus":"A rocker's exasperation at his own uselessness during a moment ripe for uprising, marooned in a drowsy capital where a lad from nowhere can only front a band. Jagger, stirred by the political turmoil of 1968, concedes that rock and roll leaves no genuine room for the would-be revolutionary.","sonnet":"A man who feels the urge to fight and rage against the establishment but lives in a sleepy London that has no place for street fighting — so all he can do is shout and scream and sing for a rock and roll band, playing compromise as the only available revolution.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~sympathy-for-the-devil":{"haiku":"A self-introduction by a malevolent force claiming responsibility for humanity's greatest atrocities across history, posing the question of complicity and moral blame.","opus":"Jagger's masterstroke lets the Devil narrate history's atrocities as his handiwork, then flips the accusation onto the listener, insisting evil is human authorship. Courtesy toward this urbane menace becomes a dark joke about complicity in every crime we blame elsewhere.","sonnet":"The Devil narrates his own curriculum vitae — present at Christ's crucifixion, the Russian Revolution, the World Wars, the Kennedy assassinations — implicating humanity as his co-conspirators, demanding politeness in return for restraint, and insisting that every atrocity required our participation.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~tell-me":{"haiku":"The singer waits desperately for a lover to return—hearing no knock, no phone call. They beg for confirmation that love will return but receive only silence and longing.","opus":"A man pleads with a lover who has abandoned him once already to promise she will return, keeping vigil beside a silent door and a phone that never sounds. The pain lies in his helplessness as the days crawl on. Among the earliest Jagger-Richards originals, this folk-tinted ballad from the Stones' debut showed them reaching past covers into their own tender, pop-leaning writing.","sonnet":"A man alone in a quiet apartment waiting for a return that isn't coming — no knock, no call, nothing. He knows she's determined to leave, and he knows he's been difficult to reason with, yet he can't stop begging her to come back.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~under-my-thumb":{"haiku":"A man celebrates his dominance over a woman who was once independent, framing her submission as a positive transformation. The song revels in control and the reversal of power dynamics.","opus":"A man relishes the flipped power balance over a woman who used to run roughshod over him, now reduced to something cowed and obedient. The Stones' 1966 Aftermath track is Jagger's coolly nasty revenge fantasy, its lilting marimba sugarcoating a lyric of gloating control, the narrator free to wander while she's kept in her place.","sonnet":"A man savors the reversal of power in a relationship: a woman who once dominated him now complies with his every word and impulse. He catalogs her submission with satisfaction, framing her transformation as his achievement. The pleasure is possessive and deliberately provocative in its celebration of control.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rolling-stones~you-can-t-always-get-what-you-want":{"haiku":"Unfulfilled desire and compromise define human experience, teaching that acceptance of unmet needs opens possibility for what actually arrives.","opus":"A worn wisdom that desire is rarely met but need often is, drawn from scenes of a woman at a reception, a demonstration and a sickly friend at the drugstore. Jagger threads private disappointment and public frustration into the same resigned consolation.","sonnet":"A series of vignettes — a woman at a reception, a demonstration, a Chelsea drugstore with a dying Mr. Jimmy — building toward the philosophical refrain that desire and necessity are different things, and if you try you find you get what you need.","src":"haiku"},
"the-rubens~hoops":{"haiku":"The narrator questions a relationship's authenticity amid confusion and signs of change, describing patterns of emotional distance.","opus":"An anxious cross-examination of a remote lover, the narrator asking if they're lonely, if they only show up when convenient, if they feel too intensely, while unsettling traces keep drawing his attention back to them. The Rubens, an Australian rock band, ride an easy, soulful groove, the warm restraint softening the uncertainty as he decides to let go and just get on with living.","sonnet":"A relationship under pressure that keeps pulling the speaker back despite the mess — blood-stained patterns that somehow lead to the right person, a sense that closeness is real even when the other person seems to be changing or pulling toward control. The speaker writes down everything that went wrong in red ink and still can't stay away.","src":"haiku"},
"the-safest-ledge~what-have-you-done":{"opus":"A cry of devastated love and loss aimed at both a person and a cruel God, where the singer would kill to be wherever the lost one is and feels his body means nothing without them.","sonnet":"A person bereft after losing someone who gave their body and existence meaning asks a god or the universe directly what has been done to them — without this person, the body is worthless, and all that's left is silence cutting down the spine.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-scratch~pull-like-a-dog":{"haiku":"Stubborn resistance against prescribed calm and restraint, pulling hard against hearsay and expectation while refusing the polite suffering society demands.","opus":"The Irish acoustic-metal band weave a folky, driving meditation on persistence and unfulfilled promise. A recurring figure who says to write but never does haunts the narrator's rowing forward through dark nights, spirit still glowing. Woven with a nod to Desiderata's counsel to go placidly, it is grit set against disappointment.","sonnet":"An Irish folk-punk portrait of someone urged to write but who never wrote — she flew but stayed silent, told always to go placidly among the noise. \"Pull like a dog\" is the demand to strain against that instruction, to row hard through dark nights, to let the hearsay carve deep rather than suppress it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-scratch~pullin-teeth":{"opus":"The Scratch grew out of Irish trad-metal circles with a satirist's sharp tongue, and this piece observes a smug countryman from a mocking distance. Preening in his suit, stealing cultural feathers from others' caps and performing righteousness without a single backing act, he draws the harder question buried under the mock-Irish bluster: what exactly is he so afraid of.","sonnet":"An Irishman observed at a gathering from a satirist's distance: smug in his suit, stealing cultural feathers from others' caps, performing righteousness without a single act to back it — stick your harps up a yipaderry-o — and the harder question underneath, what are you so afraid of, buried in the mock-Irish bluster.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-scratch~pullin-teeth-feat-kevin-rheault":{"haiku":"A meditation on hypocrisy and self-deception, where the speaker confronts those who talk about righteousness but fail to act. Drawn from Irish folk vernacular, it frames moral posturing as performative cowardice.","opus":"A wry, storytelling takedown of a phony delivered in Irish vernacular and acoustic-metal swagger. The narrator hunts down a smug, self-righteous poser, all mouthpiece and no substance, dressed for a part he can't play. Beneath the ribbing sits a challenge to admit what you really want and stop hiding behind righteous fronts.","sonnet":"A restless observer at an outdoor gathering mocks a self-satisfied, performatively Irish man — all costume and no substance — while the chorus turns inward, noting that the further someone reaches out, the closer they paradoxically become; the wit is Irish in register, the bite is personal.","src":"haiku"},
"the-shins~new-slang":{"haiku":"Lost potential and cynical awakening displace youthful hope; the narrator realizes they're trapped by circumstance and poor judgment, watching life pass them by.","opus":"A wistful ache over a town and a love that both went wrong, the singer wishing he could revert to an earlier, simpler self and doubting he'll ever reach the good life he glimpses. Regret dressed in oblique, tender imagery.","sonnet":"Looking back on a missed connection with regret and self-deprecation, the speaker imagines how fully he would have given himself over to someone had they truly chosen him, while cataloguing his own stagnation and uncertain prospects as evidence of what he lost.","src":"haiku"},
"the-shins~saint-simon":{"haiku":"Intellectual rejection of religious comfort; the speaker refuses false answers while seeking authentic warmth.","opus":"A meditation on shedding inherited comforts, the nursery rhymes and saints that once made sense of life, and stepping into uncertainty unarmed, yet finding that human warmth and love outweigh any doctrine. Faith relinquished, tenderness kept.","sonnet":"Someone navigating the gap between intellectual frameworks for meaning and the rawness of actual experience decides to stop performing defence mechanisms and simply step into the night with honesty — until an encounter with Mercy's eyes opens something that resists all ironic distance. Love arrives as the one thing that genuinely cannot be measured or dismissed.","src":"haiku"},
"the-sight-of-impact~ideal-life":{"haiku":"Conformity breeds servitude; the speaker refuses boxes and ideology, rejecting an engineered life through defiant nonconformity.","opus":"A defiant refusal to fall in line or be boxed into someone else's ideal of a proper life, laced with braggadocio and Pokemon references, insisting he won't tear down who he is.","sonnet":"Rejection of conformity and the pressure to perform someone else's version of a good life: 'put me in a box and I'll break it' — the speaker sees the infection of the rat race up close and dismantles its appeal while asserting the right to remain entirely themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"the-sisters-of-mercy~dominion":{"haiku":"Bleak dominion over others requires severing spiritual connection; Cold War geopolitical dread undercuts moments of individual power with historical inevitability and collective nightmares.","opus":"Goth-rock draped in Cold War and Shelley's Ozymandias imagery, Eldritch chanting about power, ruin and the deluded certainty of empires, dreaming of rain and revolution as monuments turn to rust.","sonnet":"Apocalyptic longing set against Cold War imagery — a lighthouse in Prussia, a white house in a red square, the fifty-two daughters of the revolution — frames a speaker who prays his own prayer while empires of gold rust. The refrain \"Mother Russia, rain down\" merges political desolation with a kind of dark spiritual surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"the-sisters-of-mercy~i-was-wrong":{"haiku":"Someone regrets their capacity for trust and love, having learned that friendship masks cruelty and pain is entertainment to others.","opus":"Eldritch's arch, wounded goodbye to a lover whose promises he never believed, admitting fault only to twist the knife on human cruelty and the appetite for others' pain. Gothic romanticism soured into bitter, clear-eyed disenchantment.","sonnet":"Someone who has been lied to repeatedly by a partner concludes they were wrong to doubt their own independence — they can manage without this person, they can love people generally, but they have no obligation to love the person this liar loves.","src":"haiku"},
"the-sisters-of-mercy~more":{"haiku":"Desire never satisfies while insecurity compounds, the speaker trapped in cycles of seeking that reveal the fundamental hollowness beneath all acquisition.","opus":"A gothic anthem of insatiable want, love, money, sensation, anything, sung with cold irony about a hunger that can never be satisfied and a lover held too much at arm's length. Desire as bottomless appetite and pose.","sonnet":"A speaker driven by insatiable need confronts someone who keeps themselves emotionally unavailable, while simultaneously admitting their own inability to stop wanting — from love, from money, from any source. The desire is self-aware and unrepentant, and the song finds a kind of integrity in refusing to pretend otherwise.","src":"haiku"},
"the-sisters-of-mercy~temple-of-love":{"haiku":"A gothic meditation on love as refuge and illusion, where two people seek shelter in their relationship from fear and pain. The song suggests love is both beautiful and fleeting, ultimately exposed as a crumbling structure built on faith rather than solid ground.","opus":"Love imagined as a besieged shrine that grants no true shelter: lovers shelter inside believing suffering stays beyond the walls, yet a dark gale carries the singer's name, storms erode the place, and the imagery insists that romance never survives past dawn. The deeper point is that devotion and desire come inseparable from grief and impermanence, love a weapon that wounds even as it consoles.","sonnet":"A doomed, gothic love song set against apocalyptic imagery — a couple sheltering in an illusory sanctuary while dark forces (the devil, the black wind) encircle them; the temple of love is ultimately a structure that will fall, and life is too short to outrun death. The narrator accepts the end, calling for the wind to carry him away.","src":"haiku"},
"the-sisters-of-mercy~this-corrosion":{"haiku":"Decay spreads through everything—words, bodies, faith—while the speaker oscillates between vulnerability and violence, healing and wounding, forever incomplete.","opus":"Eldritch's mock-epic, drenched in Wagnerian choir, a bitter kiss-off to a former bandmate dressed as gothic grandeur. The demand to be sung to is theatrical excess weaponized against betrayal.","sonnet":"A sprawling, ritualistic appeal — part demand, part invocation — for something transcendent and destructive to be sung into existence, mingling images of longing, spent emotion, and a sense of standing at the end of what can be given. The speaker is exhausted but the chant continues regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~an-ode-to-no-one":{"haiku":"Absence becomes subject of praise as the speaker honors void and emptiness. Tribute flows toward nothing.","opus":"From Mellon Collie's sprawling despair, Corgan's furious anthem of self-severance. The narrator disconnects the self from the self, gives everything back to a lover he blames for his need to sever, and clings to the one thing that can't be destroyed: the heart. Rage and vulnerability collide in a howl of wounded independence.","sonnet":"Total disconnection as self-assertion: the narrator will never come back, never give in, severs the act and the self — \"I disconnect the me in me\" — while accusing the other of faking their own aliveness; the message goes out to anyone who dares hear a fool, and all of it is given back.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~ava-adore":{"haiku":"Obsession collapses identity into possession: the speaker requires total consumption of the beloved, merging sacrament with death wish in a love song become funeral march.","opus":"Corgan's portrait of obsessive, possessive love as beautiful and monstrous at once, devotion tangled with control and violence, a partner who is both salvation and a gun to the head.","sonnet":"A portrait of a consuming, destructive love where adoration and violence are inseparable — the speaker wants to pull out crooked teeth to make her perfect, tastes God in her, and crashes cars. The beloved is simultaneously a whore, a mother, a gun to the head, and the one dressing coffins for souls left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~being-beige":{"haiku":"Romantic dissolution through numbness and distance; the world burns but indifference renders even apocalypse dull, stripping colour and meaning from relationships once vivid.","opus":"A reunion-era song of numb disenchantment, ending a love with a flat shrug while the world burns in the background, the greyness of the title standing for feeling nothing much anymore.","sonnet":"A farewell spoken to a straying lover while the world burns indifferently in the background — the speaker loved truly, fur-wrapped and faded relationships are acknowledged as finished, and each day of the week is named as the world stays on fire until readiness arrives. Tenderness and abandonment coexist without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~bodies":{"haiku":"Love as annihilation: survivors of mutual destruction find no witness to their sacrifice, leaving only anonymous corpses devoid of singular meaning or tragic worth.","opus":"From Mellon Collie, love equated flatly with suicide, bodies emptied and dispossessed by longing, the singer insisting no one ever moved him the way this person did even as devotion feels like self-destruction.","sonnet":"Emotional devastation is mapped onto images of empty bodies and ruined cities — love collapses into suicide, afflicted people dispossess themselves, and the speaker insists nobody has ever felt what this particular loss feels like. The refrain \"nobodys\" doubles as both \"nobody\" and a class of hollowed-out people.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~bullet-with-butterfly-wings":{"haiku":"Systemic evil drains the speaker who remains trapped, hopeless, and unable to save themselves despite full knowledge of their cage.","opus":"A furious anthem of futility, raging against a vampiric world yet caged no matter what he does. The exhaustion of faking his way through another show and the conviction that he's beyond saving make even the defiance feel trapped.","sonnet":"A person who grew up believing in their own specialness — like Jesus, like Job — finds themselves trapped and impotent in a system designed to drain them, consumed by rage that cannot actually break the cage.","src":"haiku","fable":"Corgan's great scream of the Mellon Collie era stages fury and impotence as the same gesture: the world is a predator that bleeds you, and yet all the singer's rage collapses into the caged animal at the chorus, where anger proves it is trapped precisely by how loudly it protests. The verses reach for spiritual dignity, patient suffering out of the Book of Job, being told you are the only one and the chosen one the way Christ was the only son, but every escalation of specialness only sharpens the humiliation of the cage. The mask is defiance; underneath is a suspicion he cannot outshout, which is why the song abandons its own hook to end on flat repeated confession rather than screamed refrain. That final self-condemnation, delivered as belief rather than fear, is the real payload: the title's contradiction, a bullet made harmless with butterfly wings, is the rage itself, loud enough to sound lethal and light enough to change nothing.","fv":2,"fnote":"Corgan wrote the rat-in-a-cage chorus out of sheer boredom at a BBC session, the same day the band cut their Landslide cover, and later said the cage was at the time a trope about the business he was in — the arena scream began as industry claustrophobia.","themes":["freedom & escape","anger & defiance","faith & the occult"],"means":"rage that proves its own captivity by how loudly it protests","built":"the song abandons its own hook to end on flat repeated self-condemnation"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~cupid-de-locke":{"haiku":"Divine arrows and satanic desire collide in doomed romance; the speaker inhabits impossible love where passion destroys and rebirth feeds the cycle.","opus":"A tongue-in-cheek medieval fantasy pitting Cupid against the Devil as dueling bowmen deciding the fate of lovers, grieving the doomed and hollow-hearted who pursue romance to their ruin. Beneath the archaic diction lies a funeral hymn for hope, sustained only by belief, mercy and affection as everything crumbles to ash.","sonnet":"A baroque, archaic rendering of love as a fatal wound — Cupid fires arrows that shatter worlds, lovers are star-crossed wanderers lost in forsaken letters, and the whole pursuit of love is cast as beautiful, hopeless, and Satanically drawn.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~doomsday-clock":{"haiku":"Temporal anxiety: civilisation ticks toward catastrophe indifferently while each person calculates personal survival odds. Meaning dissolves when endings feel predetermined.","opus":"An apocalyptic Zeitgeist opener where everyone hides in fear, jackals profit from the end, and dread ticks in the chest, Corgan finding gallows humor and Kafkaesque absurdity in living toward a certain doom.","sonnet":"Existential dread ticked out in personal terms — the speaker loves life every day even while certain of the end, afraid not of death but of the means, and finds Kafka would be proud of the absurdity. The doomsday clock is not global but beating inside the chest, and the lonely days spiral around apocalyptic resignation mixed with a last-call defiance.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~galapogos":{"haiku":"Childish pretense and denial; the speaker pretends unchanged while rescue remains impossible.","opus":"A fearful plea inside a fragile love, the narrator refusing to disown his hurt or change and asking once more whether a fall from grace would drive her off too. Pretending he's a boy again and carving hearts into oak, he confronts running out of time and the terror of never becoming the man he's meant to be.","sonnet":"An adult holding onto the child they were, asking not to be abandoned if they fall from grace — the name carved in the oak tree, the lullaby whispered, still being the man I'm supposed to be a question more than a declaration. Change is coming and has come, and the fear is that it will cost the love of the person standing witness to it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~love":{"haiku":"Love transcends physical existence and embodied loss; the speaker seeks authentic connection beneath surface chaos.","opus":"A yearning for real love amid a world of sorrow and inherited pain, love imagined as riches, painless and nameless. Buried keepsakes and a widowed hush give the longing a gothic, funereal weight.","sonnet":"A meditation on the nature of real love across centuries and modern life — the speaker searches through inherited sorrow, buried memory, and the noise of contemporary existence for something genuine and lasting, questioning whether love is real, reciprocal, or just another illusion people keep descending toward.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~porcelina-of-the-vast-oceans":{"haiku":"Soft delusions drift as thought police close in; treasure and dread intertwine as distant shores signal transcendence while power blooms from misfortune.","opus":"An oceanic dream-quest toward a mythic siren figure, an escape from the thought police into an inner world where the singer becomes everyone and meaning is whatever he chooses to seize.","sonnet":"Porcelina is a figure of the speaker's inner seascape — waiting on a distant shoreline with seashell lullabies and whispered alibis — and the journey toward her through realms of soft delusion and thoughtless thoughts is the only place where the mind can be everyone and no one, free of all care.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~solara":{"haiku":"Burning celestial bodies and drying existence, the speaker remains trapped as pure consciousness, observing life through immobilized awareness.","opus":"A raw declaration of being reduced to a mere body inside his own mind, sensing something deeply wrong while reaching for the roles of seer and fighter to burn it all down.","sonnet":"A dissociated figure who is \"nothing but a body in mind\" burns and tears down the sun — wanting to unmake the source of light that defined everyone else — while cycling between ash and brightness, arriving at the self-appointed role of seer and seeker who must fight what defines them.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~soma":{"haiku":"Pharmaceutical escape from pain sustains a cycle where numbness becomes preferable to consciousness, and the speaker sacrifices authentic connection for chemical oblivion.","opus":"A fragile ballad of dependency and heartbreak, retreating into pain as a kind of drug, forever alone and betraying himself while crawling back to a love he knows he should leave.","sonnet":"A painful retreat from a relationship that led nowhere — the speaker used the other person as a shelter and opiate, always feeling alone regardless, and returns to betray his own tears. The soma of the title is the sedative of blame and broken promises, and the song ends with a willingness to let sadness come again until the bitter end of the world.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~take-me-down":{"haiku":"Underground obscurity tempts the sleepless; guidance sought from a lover becomes the only light inside lovelessness and fault-bearing struggle.","opus":"A plea to be taken into the underground and held through a lightless despair, leaning on a beloved as the other half of oneself and the only source of healing and faith.","sonnet":"A plea for the beloved to take the speaker down into some underground of shared solace — holding their life in the dark, healing faults the speaker cannot understand alone — framed by the image of trumpets and angels on the other side and a love described as God-placed.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~tales-of-a-scorched-earth":{"haiku":"Futility and decay pervade existence; the speaker lies to feel alive while watching everything decay and die, stripped of meaning or redemption.","opus":"A scorched, nihilistic howl from someone who lies just to seem real and would give his life for a scrap of sensation, convinced nothing exists past his own wishes. The self-loathing and the desperate reach for meaning make the numbness itself the true subject.","sonnet":"About complete existential exhaustion — wanting to die, wanting to feel something real, unable to escape a cycle of repeating emptiness. The narrator is aware they lie just to feel alive and would die just to feel anything; they have no hope beyond the hollow, they are everyone and no one, and the only honest statement left is total indifference dressed up as defiance.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~thru-the-eyes-of-ruby":{"haiku":"Innocence treasures and destroys simultaneously; glass prisons suffocate while dream secrets spin, revealing depths beneath apparent purity.","opus":"A sprawling meditation on doomed youth and lost innocence, where marriage vows feel like playing dead, belief offers no escape, and two lovers stay forever frozen and lost inside themselves.","sonnet":"Love's innocence is both treasure and death in this address to a Ruby figure: belief is just faith and faith cannot save, the wedding ring is played like a dead hand, and youth — forever frozen, forever beautiful, forever lost — is wasted on the young whose strength and weakness mirror each other in an eternal, night-held embrace.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~tiberius":{"haiku":"A meditation on fading love and the cyclical nature of attachment, where everything moves on regardless.","opus":"Corgan pleading with a fading love not to slip away in the night, love held even as it obviously erodes, the endless refrain of things going and going a resigned acceptance that nothing, including this, stays.","sonnet":"A lullaby-paced song about clinging to love that is already fading — the narrator urges the beloved to stay, acknowledges the world keeps moving regardless, and sits inside the bittersweet knowledge that this too will pass. The \"it goes and goes\" is both comforting and desolate.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~tonight-tonight":{"haiku":"Celestial romance defies gravity as lovers rise toward heaven under midnight stars. Love becomes transcendent flight beyond earthly limitation.","opus":"A soaring plea for belief and change from The Smashing Pumpkins' 1995 opus, drenched in orchestral grandeur. Corgan insists that life can transform, that the impossible becomes possible in a single charged night, urging faith between two people. It is romantic idealism at full scale, a hymn to the urgency of now.","sonnet":"A manifesto for the urgency of now: time is never time at all, leaving always costs a piece of youth, but tonight the impossible is possible and everything insincere gets crucified — the city by the lake, the embers that never fade, frame a generation's call to believe in transformation before it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smashing-pumpkins~zero":{"haiku":"Infinite emptiness and zero significance; the speaker embraces negation as liberation from the burden of meaning.","opus":"A snarling nihilist self-portrait from Mellon Collie, where Corgan casts himself as a hollow lover, a zero, in love with his own sadness. Its dizzying spiral equating emptiness, loneliness, and godlessness captures a 1990s alienation dressed in glam-rock swagger, a man intoxicated by his own madness and quietly sinking.","sonnet":"Self-negation as erotic offering: the narrator is his lover's zero, the face in her dreams of glass, emptiness and loneliness and cleanliness collapsing into a God who is also empty. The turn is not resolution but doubling down — intoxicated with madness, in love with sadness — while the fashion victims chew charcoal teeth around them.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smith-street-band~birthdays":{"haiku":"Intense romantic bonds transcend individual identity; two people become intertwined through small rituals and deliberate commitment.","opus":"A rush of headlong young love, all the intensity, drunkenness and excess laid bare alongside a promise to love harder than anyone. The Melbourne singer's plainspoken vulnerability turns awkward devotion into something disarmingly sincere.","sonnet":"A young man introduces himself to a woman he has clearly already fallen for entirely, narrating the dizzying arithmetic of falling in love — the dissonance it cures, the pressure he's putting on too early, the baby names he's already thought of — funny and painfully earnest in equal measure.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smiths~back-to-the-old-house-john-peel-session-14-09-83":{"haiku":"The past compounds as haunted geography while unspoken longing prevents return to its source, memory itself becoming uninhabitable.","opus":"Aching over a place too full of painful memory to revisit, and a love the singer never confessed and now never will. Morrissey's signature paralysis of missed chances and things left unsaid, mourned rather than mended.","sonnet":"A speaker remembers cycling past a house where someone they loved lived — someone they never told about their feelings — and now cannot bring themselves to return because the memories of missed opportunity are too painful. The house holds what was never said, and that is reason enough to stay away forever.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smiths~handsome-devil-john-peel-session-18-05-83":{"opus":"Predatory wanting dressed in scholarly wordplay, the narrator eyeing an attractive young man and offering academic help as a pretext for something else. Morrissey's early Smiths provocation laces lust with wit, insisting the mind isn't quite everything. Its charge comes from the transgressive, ambiguous appetite prowling under all the cleverness.","sonnet":"A seductive, transgressive proposition from someone in authority over a younger person — with dark humor and provocative wordplay, the narrator offers sexual initiation framed as education, suggesting that life's real lessons happen in bed rather than in books.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-smiths~heaven-knows-i-m-miserable-now-2011-remaster":{"haiku":"Employment and social obligation fail to relieve profound misery as the speaker sacrifices time and self-respect to undeserving people. Smiling at enemies becomes the final indignity.","opus":"Morrissey's comic despair that nothing, not drink nor even the job he wanted, lifts the misery, and his resentment at wasting time on people who couldn't care whether he lives or dies.","sonnet":"A catalogue of everyday miseries: finding a job made things worse, giving time to people who don't care, watching lovers pass by, enduring the demands of someone whose requests would have made Caligula blush — and through all of it, the drunken hour of happiness is the only respite from a pervasive, quotidian wretchedness.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smiths~how-soon-is-now-2011-remaster":{"haiku":"Yearning for connection and love from a socially awkward, shy outsider. Rejection and loneliness accumulate while romantic possibility feels distant and increasingly unlikely.","opus":"A lonely plea from someone painfully shy who insists he's human and needs love like everyone else. He describes going out alone to meet someone, leaving alone, and going home to cry, his hope running out as he waits for change that never comes.","sonnet":"A deeply shy, isolated person goes to a club hoping to meet someone who might love them, stands alone, and leaves alone—crying and wishing to die. The song is about the ache of loneliness and the endless deferral of the connection that might resolve it.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smiths~still-ill-2011-remaster":{"haiku":"The speaker claims entitlement to happiness while questioning mind-body connections and lamenting loss of youthful romance. Wealth and ambition offer nothing when past joy fades.","opus":"Morrissey's early-Thatcher-era manifesto of alienated youth, entitled and disaffected, mourning that even old passions have curdled. The mind-versus-body question and the faded kiss under the bridge capture a generation feeling chronically, unnameably unwell.","sonnet":"Morrissey's arch manifesto of English alienation: England owes him a living, the body and mind are at war, a kiss under the iron bridge didn't feel like the old romantic dream, and he's not sure whether he's still as sick as he always was — the song lives entirely in that unresolved question.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smiths~this-charming-man-john-peel-session-14-09-83":{"haiku":"Seduction through charm masks predation as a manipulative man exploits the narrator's trust, fabricating sophistication that conceals class contempt.","opus":"A poor young man, bicycle punctured and nothing to wear, caught between class insecurity and the coded advances of a wealthier, worldlier man. Morrissey layering camp wit over queer longing and the sting of never knowing your place.","sonnet":"A stranded young man with a broken bicycle is picked up by an older, charming, socially superior man in a car, and the attraction is complicated by class — the jumped-up pantry boy who presumed to know his place now holds a kind of power. The speaker is seduced and humiliated simultaneously, desiring what also diminishes him.","src":"haiku"},
"the-smiths~what-difference-does-it-make-john-peel-session-18-05-83":{"opus":"The ache of confessing a secret to someone who then recoils and abandons the narrator; wounded but still hopelessly fond of them, he insists the lost love makes no difference while making clear it makes all the difference.","sonnet":"A battered post-relationship reckoning — the narrator did terrible things at someone else's request, felt ashamed, and now finds even their devotion and guilt don't matter because the other person is gone, leaving them too exhausted for any more apologies.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-smiths~william-it-was-really-nothing-2011-remaster":{"haiku":"Dismissal and indifference; a woman offers marriage for ring and security, but the narrator considers her nothing.","opus":"The Smiths compress a small English tragedy into two minutes, Morrissey addressing William in a humdrum town that drags people down, urging him to claim his own life. The needling question of why he'd stay with a girl proposing marriage, set against the narrator's own self-absorption, hints at thwarted desire beneath domestic convention. Marr's bright jangle undercuts a lyric quietly mourning a life narrowed by expectation.","sonnet":"A dreary provincial town presses down on William, who is being nudged toward an engagement with a woman whose casual transactionalism says everything about what he is settling for. The narrator dismisses the whole arrangement as nothing — and admits, almost as a confession, that his own dreaming runs no further than himself.","src":"haiku"},
"the-soft-moon~become-the-lies":{"haiku":"Blindness and manipulation corrode the self until the victim becomes indistinguishable from the lies that shaped them.","opus":"Dissociation and self-loss addressed partly to a parent, the light shut down leaving the narrator blind and compromised until he becomes the lies themselves. Luis Vasquez threads inherited damage through the son who'll only understand once the speaker is gone, wanting to stay a child until death.","sonnet":"About being consumed and destabilised by a relationship until you no longer know what's true about yourself — blinded, lost, pushed to contemplate whether to live or die, the narrator has been so thoroughly compromised that they have become the lies the relationship demanded of them.","src":"haiku"},
"the-soft-moon~burn":{"haiku":"A person feels alienated from their own body and identity, trapped in lies and self-doubt, burning with internal conflict they cannot control.","opus":"Luis Vasquez turns self-loathing into a mantra, a claustrophobic confession of feeling like a stranger in his own body, unable to trust himself and wishing he were someone else while the discomfort keeps searing.","sonnet":"Radical self-alienation — the narrator cannot control himself, does not trust himself, sees his own reflection as someone else, and accepts that his lies and self-hatred are going to burn him alive, possibly in hell.","src":"haiku"},
"the-soft-moon~him":{"haiku":"Exploring submission to a dominating force that knows your weaknesses and exploits them without mercy.","opus":"Addiction and self-loathing dramatized as a devil who knows every weakness and holds you down, Luis Vasquez turning the pull of a destructive habit into a seedy, complicit ritual you keep returning to.","sonnet":"About a controlling, manipulative figure (a man) who knows the narrator's every fear and weakness and uses it — there's a transaction happening (jewelry, money, meeting in bathrooms) that reads as exploitation or a kept arrangement; the narrator is weak, itchy-palmed, and trapped.","src":"haiku"},
"the-soft-moon~monster":{"haiku":"Otherness embraced as seduction; the monster as liberator demanding surrender and symbiosis.","opus":"A cover reimagining seduction as predation, where the narrator offers to be a lover's monster in a mutual pact of dangerous desire. Darkwave menace turns romance into something feral and consuming, all creeping shadows and hunger, framing love as a willing surrender to being devoured and dragged toward hell.","sonnet":"Mutual monstrousness as a love proposition: the speaker claims the predatory role — devouring kisses, licking scratches, melting at a breath — but frames it as a reciprocal deal, asking the other to be their monster in return. Desire and danger are indistinguishable.","src":"haiku"},
"the-sonder-bombs~u-ke-ain-t-enough":{"haiku":"Punk manifesto dismisses ukulele culture as diluted trend; legitimacy requires electric guitar's aggression to warrant artistic credibility and subcultural respect.","opus":"A self-aware punk joke that flips the gatekeeping it recites, a ukulele-wielding songwriter mocking anyone who says her instrument or her isn't enough, defiance wrapped in a middle-finger grin.","sonnet":"A sharp, brief dismissal aimed at someone who told the speaker a ukulele is not a real instrument — she fires back that she didn't ask, and the genre-policing critique is turned into a statement of self-worth with \"you are not enough\" mirroring the original insult.","src":"haiku"},
"the-sound-of-animals-fighting~apeshit":{"haiku":"Individual worth dissolves under collective judgment; the speaker rejects control while acknowledging the wages of their own refusal.","opus":"A cryptic renunciation of control and self-worth, confessing the singer never wanted power or saw himself as worthy, while insisting certain battles and silences were never his to break.","sonnet":"A meditation on control, worth, and the futility of claiming to understand: the fool who claims to understand or care is warned; the speaker admits they never wanted to be in control nor saw themselves as worth it, but still chooses their fate — 'your eyes decide the way that you will die.'","src":"haiku"},
"the-sports~who-listens-to-the-radio":{"haiku":"Radio culture captures youth escapism and imagination; the narrator celebrates the medium's role in providing distraction and magic during ordinary moments.","opus":"An affectionate late-70s snapshot of ordinary people, a girl doing homework, a guy with a transistor, half-listening to the radio that hums through every life. A gentle ode to the medium as constant, disposable background companion.","sonnet":"A warm, observational snapshot of the radio as shared background noise in everyday life — a girl doing homework, a man with his transistor, the speaker themselves — celebrating the low-stakes pleasure of tuning in without paying close attention.","src":"haiku"},
"the-stalin~a-3m6xh":{"haiku":"A bitter condemnation of romantic idealism and political theater—everyone's equally foolish, whether anarchist, communist, or just self-serving poseurs.","opus":"The Stalin were Japan's most notorious early-80s hardcore provocateurs, fronted by Michiro Endo's deliberate shock tactics, and this punk broadside turns that scorn on every creed. Anarchist, communist, humanist, moralist, Stalinist alike are mocked as equally nauseating forms of romantic self-delusion, the refrain about being romantically nausea-inducing landing every ism as another costume for the same sentimental fool.","sonnet":"A punk provocation that mocks every political and moral identity — anarchist, communist, humanist, moralist, Stalinist — as equally nauseating forms of romantic self-delusion that leave the person holding them unchanged and hypocritical. The refrain 'so romantically nausea-inducing' is the punchline: every ism is just another costume for the same sentimental fool.","src":"haiku"},
"the-story-so-far~out-of-it":{"haiku":"Drug dependency becomes the sole means of survival and numbness; the speaker recognizes the trap but cannot escape the chemical architecture of his own dissolution.","opus":"A blunt account of numbing out on pills to escape someone's disappointment, the pop-punk sneer barely covering self-loathing and a body already failing. Addiction told from inside the fog, defensive and half-broken.","sonnet":"A speaker deep in substance dependency uses intoxication to numb physical pain and emotional strain, dismissing anyone who objects and acknowledging, with bleak self-awareness, that the habit has become worse than any problem it was meant to solve.","src":"haiku"},
"the-stranger~kaleidoscope":{"haiku":"A person fled their home and lost their identity, becoming fragmented and strange. They fear aging without leaving a mark and waste energy seeking meaning, desperate to be seen and escape grey mediocrity.","opus":"A raw self-reckoning by someone who left home and became a stranger even to themselves, now waking weighed down and dimming toward nothing. The title image evokes a fractured inner world the narrator once experienced as vivid color. Rejecting the role of villain, they ask only for room to chart their own course and to flare out brilliantly rather than dwindle, haunted by the dread of aging unremembered.","sonnet":"A person mapping the slow dissolution of their former self — once vivid and prism-bright, now greying and fading — insists they are not the enemy of their own life and demands space to remake themselves before it is too late.","src":"haiku"},
"the-strokes~reptilia":{"haiku":"Two people engage in a tense, high-speed emotional and physical exchange where one person's intensity threatens the relationship, lines blur between connection and danger.","opus":"A cold, jittery power struggle between two people, seduction and dominance blurring as one tries to take over while the other stalls. Casablancas' clipped exchanges capture that strange-town late-night tension where control keeps changing hands.","sonnet":"A tense, fractured dynamic between two people — the narrator feels overtaken and overwhelmed, the other person dismissive and superior, and the relationship feels like a collision course where someone keeps running someone else off the road.","src":"haiku"},
"the-strokes~what-ever-happened":{"haiku":"Forgetting and disconnection offer refuge from a world of manufactured culture; the narrator wrestles with loneliness and the fear that they're unmissed and unchanged.","opus":"A jaded wish to vanish and be forgotten, tangled with lingering attachment and disgust at a culture recycling itself into countdown shows. Casablancas turning early-fame disillusion into weary self-erasure.","sonnet":"A speaker caught between wanting to disappear and wanting to be close to someone navigates the end of a connection, questioning cultural authenticity and his own passivity while acknowledging that the other person's indifference is what truly undoes him.","src":"haiku"},
"the-sundays~summertime":{"haiku":"Romantic idealism and isolation blend as the narrator imagines perfect love while doubting whether fiction can become reality.","opus":"A wistful yet knowing romance in which the singer scans personal ads and picture-perfect summer scenes, then catches herself wondering if she has simply absorbed too many made-up stories. She yearns for fairytale love while doubting it exists.","sonnet":"The speaker moves between classified-ad fantasies of romantic connection and a wry awareness of how love actually looks — mundane, sometimes desperate — before landing on a simple, sincere image of two people hand in hand in the park, unsure whether their particular version of it is real or just fiction.","src":"haiku"},
"the-superjesus~down-again":{"haiku":"The speaker battles internal forces that drag them down repeatedly, trying to find visibility and survival despite being alone and caught between isolation and connection.","opus":"A brooding meditation on self-sabotage and endurance, the narrator wasting what she desired yet bracing to brave the winters alone, half-choosing isolation while insisting nothing will drag her down again. The Superjesus were a prominent Australian alt-rock band of the late 90s, and this rides their signature blend of grunge weight and melodic sweep, Sarah McLeod's vocal carrying the tension between despair and stubborn survival.","sonnet":"Quietly fighting through isolation and the slow grind of time — wasting desires, hiding feelings, hoping the sun will finally confirm that the speaker is still alive and worth seeing. The winters get braved as well as possible, nothing follows them down this time, and the fragile conviction that surviving alone might have been the point all along holds just barely.","src":"haiku"},
"the-tech-thieves~up-all-night":{"haiku":"Desire and insomnia blur intimacy with addiction as bodies merge in nocturnal escape from consciousness.","opus":"Craving a lover as an intoxicating drug, wanting to be loved and afraid at once, bodies moving uncontrollably through a sleepless night of consuming desire.","sonnet":"Desire and adrenaline collapse into one another — wanting to be loved, afraid, and kept awake — as bodies move together in darkness and the speaker refuses to wait for sundown to touch or be touched.","src":"haiku"},
"the-temper-trap~sweet-disposition":{"haiku":"Young lovers commit to each other with reckless passion and abandon, running together before wisdom arrives. Their youth and blood keep them moving forward without surrender.","opus":"Heedless youth and hot blood rush straight at a lover, a blur of fleeting instants and kisses and cries that will not cease until it all runs out. Australian band The Temper Trap turned this soaring 2008 anthem into shorthand for aching momentum, tunes of longing offered as a pledge to keep returning and never give in while youth still blazes.","sonnet":"A declaration of youthful, reckless love in the present tense — urgent and uncalculated, running on pure vitality before the blood cools. Two people inhabit a charged moment of connection, propelled forward by momentum rather than reason, unwilling to stop or surrender to anything outside the feeling.","src":"haiku"},
"the-truth~wax":{"haiku":"Shallow celebrity and superficial glamour mask hollowness and alienation beneath surfaces. Drug use and performativity sustain the illusion while authenticity corrodes from within.","opus":"A woozy, blurred-out night of pills and partying, the glamour of sunglasses and cameras curdling into shell-shock and paranoia, ordinary lives numbed and going nowhere while chasing the next high.","sonnet":"A fragmented, drug-hazy night out — candy flips, an eighth taken, sunglasses at dawn, knives in the sink, friends kissing for the camera with one eye closed — building toward a Las Vegas wedding and the recurring refrain of going faded while \"System Of A Down\" plays in the mind.","src":"haiku"},
"the-uncharted~serenity":{"opus":"The hard-won peace after leaving a manipulative, unchanging person; the singer chooses to erase them from memory, mourns that he never truly knew them, and finds he can finally breathe now that they're gone, though he still longs to love again.","sonnet":"Someone finally achieves peace by cutting out a person who never changed and never would — the erasure of memory is both painful and liberating, leaving the narrator seeing the other person clearly as a stranger and ultimately as nothing.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-union-underground~revolution-man-clean-version":{"haiku":"Revolutionary defiance against power structures escalates toward violence as one chosen figure embodies collective rage and resistance.","opus":"A collage of assassination and rebellion imagery threaded with warped Beatles allusions, sketching a violent, drug-hazed portrait of a would-be revolutionary chosen one.","sonnet":"A fragmented portrait of a man pushed past breaking — forty days, a bullet in the head, a chosen one narrative, Lucy in the sky — that hovers between political assassination mythology and drug-induced unravelling, the revolution man chosen and destroyed in the same breath.","src":"haiku"},
"the-used~blow-me":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation becomes legacy; the speaker exists as cautionary shadow, their death eternally present in the living who must witness aftermath.","opus":"An unsettling reflection on suicide and self-destruction, framed as a conversation with the young child figure lodged in the narrator's mind who is blamed as the source of his lethal urges. It asks whether a loved one could ever fire the fatal shot, then damns whatever forces breed such impulses in a person.","sonnet":"A disturbing interior monologue in which the speaker has shot themselves and addresses someone hypothetically — would you use the gun like I did? — framing the act as both mundane and sentimental, with \"blood on my hands is sentimental\" flattening horror into a kind of intimate confession.","src":"haiku"},
"the-used~paradise-lost-a-poem-by-john-milton":{"haiku":"Cursed by knowing someone too well, unable to escape their shadow despite the poison they planted in you.","opus":"A toxic bond the narrator can't sever, naming it a blessed curse borrowed from Milton's fall. Saving himself meant a selfishness that felt like eating the forbidden fruit, and he doubts he'll survive it.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped by a bond they cannot sever — knowing the other person better than anyone, feeling cursed by that intimacy — struggles toward severance while admitting that the poisonous tie still shakes them despite every attempt to break free.","src":"haiku"},
"the-veer-union~10-35":{"haiku":"A snapshot of salvation through presence: love rescues from creeping dread, offering shelter from nightmares and media-fueled apocalypse with the simplicity of being held.","opus":"Clinging to a single reassuring moment, a precise time on the clock, when a lover's arms hold back the dark thoughts and anxieties that fill the singer's head, letting fear give way to gratitude.","sonnet":"At exactly 10:35, a partner's physical presence dissolves the speaker's worst fears — the dark thoughts like monsters under the bed, the dread stoked by the news — and for that frozen moment nothing else needs to be known or feared.","src":"haiku"},
"the-veer-union~before-it-s-the-end-vol-2":{"haiku":"Defiant refusal to surrender animates the speaker's grasp toward life even in terminal moments, transforming deterioration into art and testament.","opus":"A survival anthem framed as a terminal diagnosis, refusing to lie down and die, taking the medicine and clawing to reinvent and simply live again before the end comes, gratitude for whatever time is left.","sonnet":"A defiant refusal to die before living: told it is time to pray and given little left, the speaker untied the leash, dives into their own descent, and insists on reinventing and living again before the end — grateful for the lines in their hands, for all they still have to give.","src":"haiku"},
"the-verve~bitter-sweet-symphony":{"haiku":"Life is an inescapable cycle of financial struggle and conformity that locks individuals in unchangeable patterns. The narrator feels trapped by his nature despite desires to change, seeking musical and emotional catharsis.","opus":"A weary reckoning with life as a treadmill of debt and self-repetition, the singer insisting he could change yet trapped in his own mould, a million shifting selves who never escape the same road. It swings between resignation and defiance. The Verve's 1997 anthem rode a sweeping orchestral loop into ubiquity, its grandeur undercut by a famous legal fight that stripped Richard Ashcroft of the songwriting credit for years.","sonnet":"Trapped inside a fixed self that can't be reshaped, grinding through a life of financial servitude toward death — and yet craving music as the only release, the one thing that briefly cleans the mind. A million contradictory selves, none escapable.","src":"haiku"},
"the-violent~i-ll-be-waiting":{"haiku":"Patient vigil collapses under the weight of unreciprocated sacrifice; love demands the lover turn around.","opus":"A conflicted vow of patient devotion to someone stuck running from their own mistakes. The narrator pleads with a self-destructive partner to face reality and turn around, offering to wait but conceding his patience isn't infinite. Beneath the loyalty runs the admission of his own flaws and the toll of loving someone unwilling to be saved.","sonnet":"A speaker watches someone they love repeatedly sabotage themselves and offers patient but finite loyalty — \"I'll be waiting on you, but not forever.\" The turn arrives when the narrator admits their own complicity in the dynamic: \"I lie, I cheat, I fail, I bleed,\" collapsing the rescuer/victim boundary.","src":"haiku"},
"the-voidz~human-sadness":{"haiku":"Acknowledging personal failure and the erosion of time's value in relationships built on misunderstanding.","opus":"Casablancas sprawls through complicity, guilt, and media-fed numbness, quoting Rumi's field beyond right and wrong as the place to finally meet. A fractured elegy for lost time and a self worn down by depression.","sonnet":"A fragmented meditation on vanity, manipulation, loss, and the corruption of public life — the speaker admits fault, mourns a past self, and circles a Rumi-adjacent vision of a field beyond right and wrong where genuine meeting might still be possible.","src":"haiku"},
"the-weeknd~blinding-lights":{"haiku":"The narrator is addicted to a lover and cannot function without their presence, physically and emotionally dependent despite the pain.","opus":"A man aches for the partner who grounds him, phoning her while an isolation that feels like drug sickness sets in, unable to steady himself without her touch. Las Vegas glitters lifeless around him. The Weeknd's 2019 blockbuster sets this yearning to gleaming eighties-style synth, one of the most streamed tracks ever made.","sonnet":"Alone in a cold city at night, running toward someone in a state of near-withdrawal, unable to sleep or see straight without them. The need is physical, desperate, driving fast through empty streets — a confession he couldn't make by phone finally breaking loose.","src":"haiku"},
"the-white-noise~sunspots":{"haiku":"Mental distortion and the compulsion to self-destruct for validation. The speaker burns through their own resources seeking proof of being needed while losing their sense of self.","opus":"A voice curdled with self-regard narrates its own unraveling, boasting of style and abundant friends while confessing it cannot commit and is burning the last of itself to test whether anyone still needs it. Emerging from the mid-2010s metalcore fringe, the white noise pair this smirking arrogance against genuine dread, so the swagger reads as a mask over someone thoroughly out of his head.","sonnet":"A man circles the edge of a breakdown he half-enjoys — charming himself into thinking he is fine while burning through whatever he has left just to confirm someone still needs him. Treasured once, now traded away, he senses the collapse but cannot stop performing his own confidence.","src":"haiku"},
"the-white-stripes~the-hardest-button-to-button":{"haiku":"Simple childhood innocence meets adult disconnection; the narrator reflects on becoming jaded and difficult, losing the ease they once knew.","opus":"A terse family origin myth, a baby, a house, hard-won money and simmering resentment, built around the image of being the stubborn misfit that won't fasten into place. Jack White making alienation feel like a nursery rhyme.","sonnet":"A darkly comic account of family life and personal stagnation: the narrator traces the arrival of a baby, the family settling into a kind of uneasy normalcy, and their own creeping sense of being the one person who simply cannot fit in or connect.","src":"haiku"},
"the-wise-man-s-fear~the-rival":{"haiku":"Bitter antagonism masks mutual destruction; rivals exchange dominance through violence and revelation, where growth requires witnessing the monster in oneself.","opus":"Named for the Rothfuss fantasy, metalcore about turning the page on your old self, letting go of fear and the past to challenge giants. Its warning is that monsters wear disguises, and you cannot take a step until you set the weight down.","sonnet":"A confrontation with internal and external obstacles framed as turning a new page — the speaker declares himself a rival who will burn down whatever walls prevent forward movement, while warning that villains walk in disguise and monsters lurk below. Letting go of who you are is the only way to discover more.","src":"haiku"},
"the-wise-man-s-fear~what-time-brings":{"haiku":"Seven years of romantic dissolution reveal that time erodes intimacy; betrayal and distance render lovers irretrievable, transforming shared history into silent suffering.","opus":"A drawn-out farewell after seven years, admitting a bond was never built to last and that people keep hurting each other in ever-new ways. Metalcore catharsis wrung from the slow, mutual erosion of a relationship.","sonnet":"A relationship of seven years reaches its cold, irrevocable end, as the speaker accepts that time and distance have made them incompatible — the road they built together was paved in sorrow, and staying would only bring new pain.","src":"haiku"},
"the-word-alive~hate-me-feat-loveless":{"haiku":"Doubt weaponizes the speaker's will to survive; adversaries circle while the speaker transforms rejection into fuel, refusing to break beneath the weight of collective disdain and disbelief.","opus":"Turning others' doubt and hatred into fuel, the singer refusing to break under those who want him to fail and reframing their contempt as proof he'll keep living and saving himself.","sonnet":"A speaker addressed by someone who wants them to fail insists they will never break — \"bend me but I'll never break\" — and ultimately reclaims the hatred aimed at them as something that paradoxically makes them feel alive, before pivoting to offer the hater a way to begin again.","src":"haiku"},
"the-word-alive~misery":{"haiku":"A relationship deteriorates from intimate connection to hollow routine; both partners have changed, hearts disagree, and the speaker no longer believes in anything the other offers. Sexual comfort replaces emotional intimacy.","opus":"A relationship rotting into mutual wretchedness, the couple clinging to false hope through constant fighting while the narrator, hollowed out and no longer sure what he wants, refuses to shoulder any blame. The gut-punch is intimacy gone cold, physical closeness now just a bandage over pain, tenderness soured into shame.","sonnet":"A relationship that has collapsed into mutual damage — they used to make love but now just have sex to numb the pain, the hearts don't agree anymore, and the narrator admits they've become someone unrecognizable and harmful.","src":"haiku"},
"the-word-alive~new-reality":{"haiku":"Altered consciousness reveals nothing but emptiness and dread; the new world promised by chemical escape delivers only terror and irreversible change.","opus":"A bad trip or overdose narrated as it happens, ushering someone into a terrifying altered state and asking whether this is what they were after. The panic of a racing heart and freezing skin doubles as a world devouring itself, pressing toward surrender.","sonnet":"About the terrifying physiological experience of an overdose — cold sweat, racing pulse, shaking, screaming for help and getting no answer, the world slowing to a crawl while the narrator wonders whether they have taken too much and cannot come back. The \"new reality\" being welcomed is the state after the line has been crossed, possibly permanent damage or death.","src":"haiku"},
"the-word-alive~nocturnal-future":{"haiku":"Collective paralysis numbs humanity to ongoing devastation; the speaker urges confrontation with darkness rather than willful blindness.","opus":"A plea to quit sleepwalking through a broken, violence-numbed world with your gaze averted. It argues that repairing the damage humanity caused begins with each person confronting the ugly reality instead of turning away from it.","sonnet":"Collective moral numbness — numb to war, looking away, selling ourselves — is confronted with a demand to face the dark sun rather than live with eyes shut; the speaker admits their own fear but insists change starts with the individual, and those who feel lost have found their home here.","src":"haiku"},
"the-word-alive~pardon-me":{"haiku":"A young person suffocates under the weight of gravity and hypocrisy, imagining combustion as escape. They've absorbed enough pain and chaos—now they must burn away and rise transformed.","opus":"A cover of Incubus's Pardon Me reframed for post-hardcore: a young man buckling under the world's absurdities and hypocrisy, fantasizing about combustion as release. Overwhelm turned into a strange apology.","sonnet":"At twenty-three, already overwhelmed by the world's noise and human pettiness, the narrator fantasizes about spontaneous combustion as relief — then chooses to burn and rise above rather than be consumed, emerging changed and no longer the same person.","src":"haiku"},
"the-word-alive~searching-for-glory":{"haiku":"Rejecting false promised glory and urging others to escape oppression through self-creation and inner fire.","opus":"Metalcore aimed at anyone told they must be someone else to matter. It insists fate isn't binding and that losing yourself chasing glory isn't worth it, ending on the hard admission of needing help.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses anyone who has devalued themselves chasing something beyond reach — insisting that glory is not found by becoming someone else, and that the fire in all of us is kindled by turning pain into change rather than disappearing into self-loss.","src":"haiku"},
"the-word-alive~slow-burn":{"haiku":"Relationship death through incremental anguish; both speakers participate in mutual destruction despite desire to preserve and protect.","opus":"A codependent love turning self-destructive, the singer willing to burn himself just to be loved, owning the lies and the tears he caused while wondering whether letting go would finally set them both free.","sonnet":"A speaker who knows they are killing the relationship through their own failures — making their partner cry, unable to get it right — still insisting they would burn if it meant being loved, while the other person might find freedom in finally leaving.","src":"haiku"},
"the-word-alive~strange-love":{"haiku":"Toxic attachment spirals into hell, where the speaker feels trapped by love that poisons and enslaves despite awareness of the damage.","opus":"An addictive, destructive attachment mistaken for the real thing — the pull of someone slowly drowning you, sweetened just enough to keep you from leaving. The torment is knowing it is poison and staying regardless, waiting for an ending that refuses to come.","sonnet":"About a toxic romantic attachment the narrator can't escape even though it's destroying them — they were seduced, confided everything, and now can't let go even knowing the outcome; the love is described as cyanide they drank willingly, lullabies they choked on.","src":"haiku"},
"the-word-alive~trapped":{"haiku":"Mental imprisonment traps consciousness; the speaker suffocates inside their own mind, unable to escape intrusive thoughts and nightmares.","opus":"Imprisonment inside one's own mind, unable to sleep or know oneself, haunted by dreams and begging for reassurance of being worth something. The relentless refrain enacts the claustrophobia of a head there's no escaping.","sonnet":"A mind in a closed loop: the speaker cannot sleep, cannot know themselves, is haunted by dreams, and the other person is running from what seeps out of them — the word \"trapped\" repeated as both diagnosis and plea, with no resolution offered, only the fact of the walls.","src":"haiku"},
"the-word-alive~wonderland":{"haiku":"Society cycles through pain and toxicity while people hide depression and anxiety behind false faces. The speaker questions whether admitting weakness is possible and if there is freedom from this pattern.","opus":"The Word Alive, an Arizona metalcore band, confront depression and anxiety head-on. The narrator asks whether you mask your pain when people check on you, whether survival itself has grown exhausting, and admits to numbness and reckless coping born from knowing little but hurt. Turning outward, the song becomes a call to release that suffering before it consumes everything.","sonnet":"A communal reckoning with mental health — cycles of depression, anxiety, and toxic patterns that everyone is pretending not to have. The speaker calls out the performance of being okay, acknowledges that pain is almost the only shared language, and tries to push toward something freer while asking whether admitting weakness is actually the first step out.","src":"haiku"},
"the-world-is-a-beautiful-place-i-am-no-longer-afraid-to-die~january-10th-2014":{"haiku":"Collective female rage reimagined as instrument and weapon against evil, demanding respect through defiance.","opus":"Cast through the myth of Diana the Hunter, a woman becomes an instrument of vengeance standing in for many wronged women. Emo-collective sprawl turns a specific act of retribution into a warning that makes evil fear itself.","sonnet":"A collective female voice claims the power of revenge and righteous violence against those who have wronged them, invoking the figure of Diana the Hunter and insisting that evil must be made afraid of its own shadow.","src":"haiku"},
"the-world-is-a-beautiful-place-i-am-no-longer-afraid-to-die~queen-sophie-for-president":{"haiku":"Power corruption and cyclical leadership failure; the perpetual rise of new tyrants from old husks. The song warns that systems persist unless burned to ash.","opus":"A grotesque political allegory about the hollow handoff of power, a relentless ooze biding its time to occupy a severed seat of authority, perched on the emptied remains of predecessors whose values ring as vacant as its own, urged to torch it all though the pilot flame is dead so nothing ignites. The World Is a Beautiful Place, an American emo-adjacent collective, frame this vision of futile ambition in sprawling, cathartic post-rock swells.","sonnet":"Power as a corrosive liquid that keeps finding new containers — leaders who eagerly fill the shape of whoever was amputated or withered before them, building their thrones on the hollowed-out husks of predecessors. The structure will crack eventually, the slime leaks through, and if it still survives the only option left is to burn the whole house down.","src":"haiku"},
"the-worst-of-us~565":{"opus":"A harrowing depiction of addiction personified as a monster or stranger living inside the narrator, burning both himself and the people he loves, resolving in a claustrophobic near-death struggle to cut the addiction out and bury it before it takes him down.","sonnet":"About purging a toxic internal force — the narrator is consumed by a destructive presence living inside them, possibly addiction or a relationship that mirrors it, and the song is a violent, sweating struggle to bury it before it pulls them under entirely.","src":"sonnet"},
"the-worst-of-us~antagonist-returns":{"haiku":"Confronting the betrayer with their own emptiness; vindication through refusing to sink to their level.","opus":"A vengeful reckoning with a former ally turned traitor, driven by class resentment and betrayal. The narrator, scarred and vindicated, rails at someone handed the world who spits on those with less, casting himself as the knife that cuts deepest. It is a settling of scores, fueled by the conviction that some things simply deserve to die.","sonnet":"The antagonist is someone who succeeded through sordidness and self-absorption while the speaker bore scars the other couldn't wear — the turn comes in the accusation that the antagonist's legacy is a disease that shows the speaker what they could and will never be — vindication arrives not through forgiveness but through the conviction that things meant to be broken are meant to die.","src":"haiku"},
"the-worst-of-us~eternal-sickness":{"haiku":"Rising above deception and fakery, transparency reveals hypocrisy masquerading as authentic; complicity in meaninglessness extends indefinitely.","opus":"Rising above a manipulative, hypocritical person the singer has finally stopped trying to save, declaring lost faith in someone whose ignorance and lies are an incurable sickness.","sonnet":"A speaker who has been silenced and used — \"you act like I owe you something\" — rises above the flames of the relationship's hypocrisy and names it plainly: your stasis is yours to rot in, I have lost all faith in you, and your eternal sickness is not mine to cure.","src":"haiku"},
"the-worst-of-us~glory-instrumental":{"haiku":"An angry accusation of hypocrisy and betrayal directed at someone the speaker claims is dishonest and failures, with the speaker asserting their own superiority and success over them.","opus":"A scorched-earth kiss-off to a manipulative, karma-earning ex, the narrator vowing to surpass every standard set and rise from the ashes even at self-destructive cost. Rage and wounded pride braid together with a chilling refrain about deserving isolation. The delivery leans on metalcore's aggressive electronic edge, the U.I motif and rap-inflected verses marking it as modern heavy music built for catharsis over subtlety.","sonnet":"A scorched-earth rebuttal to someone who spread lies and dismissed the speaker as irrelevant. Fury compressed into defiance: every accusation returned, every doubt burned down, rising from the wreckage with the demand that the opponent acknowledge exactly what they helped build.","src":"haiku"},
"the-xx~angels":{"haiku":"Silent devotion and unspoken admiration describe a love so complete it transcends expression, existing in presence and touch alone.","opus":"A hushed, wide-eyed declaration of a love so complete the singer can't believe anyone else would understand it. The xx's minimal intimacy makes devotion feel like a secret whispered close, tender and slightly afraid of its own ending.","sonnet":"An overwhelmingly quiet devotion to a person so luminous that the narrator struggles to believe others cannot see what is so obvious to them, spending each day learning more about someone who feels both close and already departing. Love here is silent, enormous, and tinged with the fear of loss before it fully arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"the-xx~basic-space":{"haiku":"Love requires total immersion and sacrifice of boundaries; the speaker surrenders autonomy, preserving connection through self-erasure and sealed isolation.","opus":"An intimacy so intense the singer tries to seal and preserve it in stone before it breaks or she's left alone, terrified of losing where she ends and the other begins. Bargaining and clinging, she can't let the feeling out yet keeps letting the person in.","sonnet":"A relationship preserved through desperate fixation: the speaker seals what they have in wax — \"it's a pool of boiling wax, I'm getting in, let it set\" — to prevent loss or change, ending up with a \"second skin\" that shines but may have locked both people in, unable to let go even while still letting the other in.","src":"haiku"},
"the-xx~crystalised":{"haiku":"Intimacy freezes under pressure to maintain perfection while desire accelerates into obsession, leaving the narrator paralysed between escape and surrender.","opus":"Two people testing how much pressure a relationship can take, one moving at their own pace while the other applies force to have them crystallized. Fear of being paralyzed or pushed away wrestles with a stubborn refusal to leave, even if the home burns down.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses someone whose emotional pressure has both crystallized and destabilized them, weighing forgiveness against paralysis while glaciers melt and the tide beckons — love that keeps getting closer even as the speaker kneels under its weight.","src":"haiku"},
"the-xx~dangerous":{"haiku":"Lovers defy external judgment through commitment; skeptics cannot disrupt bonds forged through faith and mutual acceptance.","opus":"Choosing a risky love in defiance of everyone's warnings, insisting the person is worth it even if it ends in tears. Bravado and fear braided together over a stubborn refusal to walk away from something reckless.","sonnet":"Two people are warned by everyone around them that their relationship is reckless and dangerous, but the speaker refuses to care — choosing to treasure each day regardless of how it ends, insisting the voices calling danger must be blind to what the two of them actually have.","src":"haiku"},
"the-xx~heart-skipped-a-beat":{"haiku":"A person pursues someone who has moved on, unable to let go despite being out of reach and ignored repeatedly.","opus":"A quietly aching account of longing for someone who's moved on, the narrator pleading that they aren't finished, admitting they sometimes still need this person, reaching just as the heart skips and the other stays out of reach. The xx, a London trio, made their name on exactly this hushed, minimal intimacy, boy-girl vocals and spare guitar leaving vast silences that make the unmet need feel enormous and painfully close.","sonnet":"The timing keeps being wrong — the speaker is still invested when the other person has already moved on, still reaching when they're already out of reach. Love offered too late, doors arrived at after they've closed, and the certainty that the other person has heard this story before does nothing to make the loss feel smaller.","src":"haiku"},
"the-xx~islands":{"haiku":"After finding love and belonging, the speaker no longer needs to explore the world—they've found what they sought, and togetherness becomes their home.","opus":"The xx on the quiet comfort of finally belonging to someone, and the strange surrender it demands. Being found means giving up the wandering, and the song sits in the tension between that safety and the exploration it forecloses.","sonnet":"A love song about the paralysis of being found — the speaker has stopped wandering the world because this person is enough, but acknowledges the terrifying risk: if this ends, there is nowhere left to go.","src":"haiku"},
"the-xx~night-time":{"haiku":"Confession struggles against inhibition; the speaker works toward honesty while compulsively holding back, caught between transparency and control.","opus":"The difficulty of conveying how much someone matters, the singer rehearsing small deceptions before resolving to be honest and hand over the heart that's so hard to spill. Under the guarded restraint lies a hope that plain words will hold the two of them together.","sonnet":"An internal struggle with emotional disclosure: the speaker holds something \"embedded in my chest\" that hurts to carry, walks out into storms keeping the words back, tightening their grip — and eventually promises to tell the truth, to give it up, when the day comes.","src":"haiku"},
"the-xx~replica":{"haiku":"Cyclical repetition of family patterns and mistakes traps generations in identical paths, questioning whether transformation or inevitability governs destiny.","opus":"A dread of becoming a copy of a parent, catching yourself repeating their chemical mistakes and wondering whether it's simply your nature to loop, chasing the night or being chased by it.","sonnet":"A 25-year-old recognises they have become their parent — same mistakes, same chemical disposition — and asks whether the pattern of watching and repeating is nature or choice, haunted by the familiar face in their own mirror and unable to break the encore of inherited behaviour.","src":"haiku"},
"the-xx~stars":{"haiku":"Urgency collapses into timelessness where devotion requires no delay or performance, existing as pure potential. Communication dissolves into silent recognition and willing surrender.","opus":"The whispered vulnerability of a first date offered up completely, insisting that even if the spark doesn't last, giving it time together would be enough.","sonnet":"Two people on an early date negotiate vulnerability and pace — if the spark doesn't ignite immediately it's fine, there's time, and desire speaks underneath the words in the quiet understanding that both already know what the other wants.","src":"haiku"},
"the-xx~vcr":{"haiku":"VCRs and shared media create intimacy while partners exist in half-daylight existence, mystifying each other through reflection rather than direct knowledge.","opus":"A tender snapshot of young intimacy built on quiet certainty, two people watching tapes, talking of big love and feeling like superstars simply because the other just knows.","sonnet":"A tender portrait of an intimate relationship lived half in daylight and half at night, watching things on VCRs and talking about big love — the simplicity of the bond built on an unspoken, mutual knowing that exceeds any need to justify or explain.","src":"haiku"},
"thecityisours~bare-bones":{"haiku":"After catastrophic loss, the speaker commits to rebuilding from nothing; they shed pretense and return to origins, embracing the aftermath as a new beginning.","opus":"A vow to demolish and rebuild from nothing frames endings as fresh starts, insisting every collapse can be taken back to the beginning and reduced to essentials before renewal. As a British melodic metalcore act built for anthemic catharsis, thecityisours turn what could be a breakup into a survival creed, honouring a departing figure even while refusing to surrender the ground already lost.","sonnet":"A pair strips everything back to the beginning after catastrophe, insisting that every ending is also a fresh start. Though the parting is real and painful, the resolve is firm: the work they shared was not wasted, even if one of them will not be standing there to see what comes next.","src":"haiku"},
"thee-michelle-gun-elephant~remastered":{"haiku":"Themes of death, mortality, or psychological demise; existential darkness permeates the lyrics.","opus":"Thee Michelle Gun Elephant fused garage-punk and R&B into a distinctly grimy Japanese rock, and this remastered cut tears through their dark carnival at speed. A violent rhythm machine smashes skulls, a dead-man train hurtles past, peace signs pass as hollow currency and mud-smeared flower sellers crowd an exposed world wrapped around an orange heart, everyone sick of the peace-sign brigade as the party ends.","sonnet":"A violent rhythm machine tears through the back and smashes skulls — an exposed world of darkness wrapped around an orange heart, a dead-man train at speed, 59 helmets, peace signs as hollow currency, mud-smeared flower sellers and fortune-tellers — everyone sick of the peace-sign brigade, the party is over.","src":"haiku"},
"them-crooked-vultures~new-fang":{"haiku":"Predatory desire pursues new conquest while the narrator's hunger consumes without remorse or respect for boundaries.","opus":"A leering, swaggering account of chasing a new conquest and being mistaken for a better man, admitting the mind was once a landmine and the appetite never quits. It's carnal restlessness that won't wait around for anything.","sonnet":"A speaker navigating a volatile, self-aware sexual dynamic — new fang pressing down, fingers broken on the upper hand, accused of being confused with a better man — moving through desire and consequence with loose, reckless energy before new fang runs out of patience.","src":"haiku"},
"ther-realm~death":{"haiku":"After witnessing brutal death, the speaker finds strange peace in mortality's inevitability; life's struggles fade into insignificance before the release of the end.","opus":"A slain soldier is surprised to meet a sibling in the afterlife's fading glow, finding death oddly tolerable despite the numbness. Left as carrion by his executioners, his body decaying, he decides he would sooner remain deceased than go back to the existence he knew.","sonnet":"A man numbed by trauma and time — fed to crows, blood on his hands, haunted by a whispered world of endless night — reaches the unexpected conclusion that death is not so bad; seen through a sister's eyes at the end of a dying day, his acceptance is grief-soaked but almost peaceful.","src":"haiku"},
"ther-realm~goodbye":{"haiku":"A person ends a relationship because living with their partner feels dishonest and unsustainable to their authentic self.","opus":"The narrator lets someone go to venture on by themselves into the vast uncertain ahead, confessing troubled sleep and a shared life that was never truly his. Delivered with tired tenderness, the farewell reads as mutual freeing, the one leaving urged to trust their own instincts and become whole, the split preserved as fond recollection rather than grudge.","sonnet":"A person ends a relationship that has been generating nightmares and a creeping sense of falseness. They acknowledge their partner's capacity to stand alone and wish them wholeness, but the emotional incompatibility has become undeniable. Leaving is framed as a release from a life that no longer belongs to either of them.","src":"haiku"},
"ther-realm~the-tower":{"haiku":"Warning against building hollow monuments on others' suffering; time eventually exposes the futility of ambition.","opus":"A refusal to bless someone's monument to a life they never actually lived. The tower built on others' suffering is doomed to fall; only deeds and time write the tales worth keeping.","sonnet":"A speaker refuses to bless a monument built on others' suffering — warning that towers of bones and hollow legacies will fall, and that deeds, not structures, are the only thing time will preserve; the lesson is one the fool will learn whether they want to or not.","src":"haiku"},
"therapy~diane":{"haiku":"A predatory threat disguised as casual seduction, darkly comic in its brazenness.","opus":"A Husker Du cover voiced from inside a killer's head, luring a girl with false friendliness before the horror lands flat and casual. The banality of the delivery is what makes the murder unbearable.","sonnet":"Told from the perspective of a predator, the lyrics trace a serial killer luring a young woman named Diane under false pretexts and murdering her. The song is a stark, unadorned dramatisation of Ted Bundy-style violence with no redemptive framing.","src":"haiku"},
"therapy~six-mile-water":{"haiku":"A man estranged from his childhood home and former friendships acknowledges he is not yet ready to return, carrying regret and unresolved belonging.","opus":"A Northern Irish elegy for a lost male friendship, drink and drugs and shared nights faded into silence and unsent letters, the singer restless and not ready to go home. Grief for who you were and the mate you drifted from.","sonnet":"A fractured friendship between two men who grew up together across the border dissolves quietly through distance, missed calls, and the passage of time, leaving only guilt and a sense of permanent incompleteness. The narrator is not ready to return to a home that no longer holds them both.","src":"haiku"},
"there-is-a-light-that-never-goes-out~suru":{"haiku":"Finding purpose through community and art after a year of aimlessness; connection to others provides the power to continue existing.","opus":"This Japanese post-hardcore project frames endurance as communal, a narrator tired from a year of lost direction kept upright by friends, music, a dog, a partner and brothers who give him power. Bilingual and plainspoken, the lyric leans on a borrowed film line about people leaving and arriving, insisting he believes it true. The band and its shows become a separate world he can only gather in briefly before returning to the real one.","sonnet":"A Japanese musician articulates exhaustion from a year of directionlessness and the separation between the world of performance and ordinary life. Friends, music, a dog, a partner, and a community gathered only at shows all become the small powers that keep the desire to continue alive.","src":"haiku"},
"these-arms-are-snakes~camera-shy":{"haiku":"Media spectacle corrodes authenticity; the performer recognizes themselves as commodity, viewed and judged rather than understood, trapped in cycles of empty repetition.","opus":"Post-hardcore collage of media saturation, courtroom spectacle and corporate spin, images and replays making people feel better than reality while a numb dark hole and a life reduced to a bullet lurk underneath.","sonnet":"Fragmented images of spectacle, courtroom scenes, and corporate machinery suggest a world where media visibility manufactures judgment and the self is swallowed by the performance. The closing lines — \"life's a bullet in your son's eyes\" and \"it doesn't make sense, that's all that matters\" — collapse meaning into resignation that things simply get done.","src":"haiku"},
"these-four-walls~white-lies":{"haiku":"Comfortable deception within intimate spaces; false peace sustained by mutual pretense.","opus":"A confession of chronic dishonesty and the way small deceptions bind two people together. The narrator sees through his own saintly mask, knowing better than to fake his life yet doing it anyway, while a bitter refrain dismisses friends as worthless. Sweet-sounding lies curdle into self-disgust and a corrosive intimacy built on deceit.","sonnet":"A self-aware liar who knows the words fall out like a lullaby and the saint's face shows cracks in the light — dishonesty as the bond with another person, binding tighter than honesty could — the speaker frames it as parasitism, trauma dragging words and deceit to a knife fight, and cannot stop painting things over even knowing the whole structure is rotten.","src":"haiku"},
"thine~brave-young-assassin":{"haiku":"A sacrificial figure departs to spare others from ruin, becoming the solitary witness to their own erasure from history.","opus":"A doomed farewell in which the singer urges a loved one to turn away and not witness his death, framing his own silencing as the only way to erase a stain and spare them from being hunted.","sonnet":"A figure tells another to turn and go home so they do not have to watch him die again, positioning himself as the stain that must be erased from their life to protect them from being hunted down; the memory of inspired early days makes the sacrifice feel like a broken promise kept only by letting go.","src":"haiku"},
"thine~the-precipice":{"haiku":"Years of suppression and hopes denied erode toward collapse; betrayal and deception leave only the choice to fall or transcend.","opus":"Two people drifting apart toward a vengeful, waning sun, edging closer to a breaking point where suppressed dreams and denied hopes leave them barely breathing, healing only to hurt again.","sonnet":"Two people whose dreams have been strangled for years stand at the edge of a precipice where staying means suffocation and leaving might offer change and light; the speaker urges a leap toward the flooding light of the room while acknowledging that wounds will heal only to start again.","src":"haiku"},
"thirty-seconds-to-mars~this-is-war":{"haiku":"Universal conflict between good and evil, heroes and victims demands total commitment and sacrifice as humanity battles for survival and redemption in an apocalyptic clash.","opus":"An anthem summoning every party to a struggle, from hero to casualty, framing being alive as a split-second choice between resistance and surrender. It builds toward triumph and the promise of a remade world on the far side of the battle.","sonnet":"A proclamation addressed to every possible participant — soldier, civilian, martyr, messiah, liar — calling everyone to the same edge of the earth where the moment of truth and the moment to die converge, before arriving at belief in the light and declaring the war won.","src":"haiku"},
"thirty-seconds-to-mars~walk-on-water":{"haiku":"Religious faith confronts political violence and uncertainty; belief becomes both refuge and act of defiance against chaos.","opus":"A stadium call to belief and defiance amid holy wars, patriotism and political division, asking whether faith can carry you through impossible odds. Grand, anthemic uncertainty dressed as a rallying cry.","sonnet":"A political and spiritual rallying cry that asks whether the listener believes enough to act — to walk on water, to win this fight — against a backdrop of holy wars, political division, and broken promises, framing belief itself as the only weapon against an era of dying stars and manufactured fear.","src":"haiku"},
"thomas-giles~give-up":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and contamination breed only surrender; corrupted language and failed ideals leave the speaker no tools for resistance except resignation.","opus":"The Between the Buried and Me frontman's solo turn inward, a fractured plea to be free and be himself even at the cost of eruption or collapse, self-image cancerous and corrupt yet still reaching to lift itself higher.","sonnet":"A speaker torn between wanting freedom, eruption, and authenticity on one hand, and the impulse to simply corrupt and give up on the other — the back and forth of tumbling down is framed as a kind of disheartened self-image seeking resurrection. The tension is never resolved, held in a loop of contradictory wants.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~arkangel":{"haiku":"A twisted love song where the speaker claims someone as their angel while also confessing to being a killer who has lost his way. The desire is possessive and violent—hearts belong to the speaker, and violence is reframed as passion.","opus":"A dark, obsessive love where the narrator casts a lover as his angel while confessing he is really a killer who made her into his own image. Named for the Black Mirror episode, it circles control, projection and the violence hidden in possession.","sonnet":"An obsessive, possessive pursuit of a woman the narrator calls his angel — he is convinced her heart belongs to him, sees through her denials, and confesses to a violent, self-destructive compulsion that has left him lost.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~blue-velvet":{"haiku":"Intimacy becomes both divine connection and terrifying loss as the speaker fears abandonment through closeness. Physical safety paradoxically requires emotional distance and self-preservation.","opus":"Sultry, atmospheric metalcore from the Australian band, wrapping intimacy in menace and impermanence. Draped in velvet imagery evoking Lynch, it circles a lover the narrator knows won't stay, aching for safety and closeness even as the connection frays. Desire and dread build together toward an undoing he can already feel coming.","sonnet":"Obsessive intimacy that the speaker knows won't last — fingers intertwined, breath stopping, the image of a blue velvet dress close to her chest — the narrator keeps the beloved safe while acknowledging they will never see the sun again without her, and that she won't stay.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~casanova":{"haiku":"A seductive and dangerous dance of desire; the speaker refuses to let prey escape, wielding weapons and lies for control.","opus":"A seductive, dangerous game plays out in gambling and knife imagery, prey and predator circling a dance floor where the narrator refuses to think twice about possessing his target. Australian metalcore band Thornhill lean into sultry, groove-laden atmosphere here, the noir cocktail of desire, threat, and someone burning giving their heaviness a smoky, cinematic pull.","sonnet":"Obsession and ambivalence entangle on the dancefloor — a figure who refuses to think twice pursues with predatory single-mindedness, yet the rhetoric turns on itself, mixing desire with threat, weaponry with request. The seducer performs invincibility while quietly asking to be saved.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~coven":{"haiku":"Something malevolent feeds off and inhabits the speaker; it penetrates deeply into their mind and body, growing stronger from their internal anguish.","opus":"Voiced by a clinging, parasitic presence that feeds on attention and refuses to be let go, the loved one becoming the inferno that made it real. A metalcore study of dependency and obsession dressed as haunting.","sonnet":"A horror-perspective song narrated by something monstrous that has been given life by the person it now haunts — it feeds on attention, lives under the bed and inside the lungs, and promises it will find you if you breathe. It reads as the internalized voice of trauma or obsession given a demonic form.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~diesel":{"haiku":"Obsessive power and control dominate relationships; the speaker holds another captive through psychological leverage and will not be questioned.","opus":"Sleek, menacing metalcore about seizing power over someone who wronged you, holding their name and secrets like leverage, cold showtime swagger with the promise to use what you hold against them.","sonnet":"A confrontation in which the narrator holds something over someone — a secret, a piece of evidence — and uses it as leverage, declaring they will not let go of it and there is nothing the other person can do.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~for-now":{"haiku":"Torn between desire for someone and the pain of their indifference, accepting a temporary connection.","opus":"Jealous, doomed devotion aimed at someone still fixated on an ex, offering everything and getting only crumbs, the repeated claim of being their forever undercut by the two words that end it as merely temporary.","sonnet":"A person hopelessly in love with someone who is with someone else — they are obsessed with whether they remind her of him, would give anything to be chosen, but the fumes of her current relationship erase them completely.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~heroine":{"haiku":"Age difference and desire tangle as the speaker watches and pursues someone on the edge of adulthood. Movement and dance frame seduction through spectacle.","opus":"A charged, seedy attraction to someone on the edge of legal age, the singer counting down birthdays while dancing the night away and daring the object of desire to close the gap.","sonnet":"A charged dance-floor encounter with a woman the speaker is captivated by — she is not quite 21, he is nearly 22, and the evening spins on the question of whether this mutual pull can hold — the word 'heroine' hovering as both the object of desire and a force of its own, the dare to start always just ahead of him.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~hollywood":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession with a deceptive woman in the Hollywood sphere masks deeper emptiness and the girl's near-death state. Love binds to an unstable, beautiful liar.","opus":"Seductive, cinematic metalcore obsessed with a girl in white and the glamour-death of Hollywood, desire tangled with rot. Beneath the swagger runs the sense that this love and this city are both beautiful and already dying.","sonnet":"An obsessive, possibly delusional infatuation with a woman in Hollywood — she has his heart, she may be a liar in his head, she's \"nearly dead\" inside whatever fantasy he's built — the song sits in the gap between real love and projection.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~human":{"haiku":"Humans struggle to connect authentically while time decays them; the speaker questions whether love and shared meaning can overcome our fundamental isolation and impermanence.","opus":"A yearning meditation on connection and impermanence, asking whether what makes us human is our capacity to change or to decay, and pleading to be released from isolation into another person's heart.","sonnet":"A meditation on whether genuine human connection can survive distance, disconnection, and the fear of letting someone in — questioning what makes someone human and whether it's worth the vulnerability of being truly known.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~in-my-skin":{"haiku":"Trapped in a toxic dynamic where the speaker feels suffocated by another's presence and gaze, unable to escape from being consumed or defined by that relationship.","opus":"A stifling, obsessive bond where the singer sees a warped reflection and exists only for the other person, longing to expel them yet feeling them lodged inside his sight, marked with bruises. Exhausted by being confined within his own body, he pleads to trade places, worship and self-annihilation knotted together in darkness.","sonnet":"An uncomfortable, possessive entanglement where the speaker is psychologically consumed by someone else — they see through the other's eyes, feel bruised by the closeness, and can't decide whether to let them in or push them out, craving both.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~joy":{"haiku":"A man escapes into fantasy and isolation while deteriorating mentally, chasing beauty but finding only loneliness.","opus":"A tender, aching portrait of a man lost to mental illness, murmuring to himself, drifting after fluttering things while clutching a mental record of who he once was. His plea to a flower captures the cruelty of feeling delight in one place and deadness in another, and of being completely alone.","sonnet":"A portrait of a man slipping into mental illness and isolation — talking to himself, chasing visions, aging into darkness — who feels alive but utterly alone, the joy in his heart coexisting with the death he senses in his bones.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~lavender":{"haiku":"The speaker experiences crushing disrespect and disgust, attempting to hide while building walls against attack. They oscillate between suicidal ideation and refusal to surrender their identity.","opus":"An obsessive, corrosive fixation on someone the singer both wants to become and to destroy, self-mutilation offered as devotion. The lavender addressed so tenderly is also the source of a hate he can't tell from love.","sonnet":"A song about being psychologically demolished by a manipulative or cruel figure — the narrator is reduced to holding their breath, tearing out their eyes, and rebuilding walls just to survive someone who wanted to reshape them entirely, yet still refuses to fully surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~leather-wings":{"haiku":"Emotional coldness spreads like contagion as the speaker becomes increasingly detached, needles embedded in the heart where connection once lived, flight the only escape.","opus":"A toxic entanglement rendered through needles and neon and the promise of flight, one person feeding on another while both fall apart. The speaker is terrified and cold, watching a lover come undone, unable or unwilling to love the way that's demanded.","sonnet":"A relationship ending on irreconcilable terms — the speaker has been punctured and left with something foreign lodged in them, unable to love the other person the way they demand, knowing it's over but feeling cold and hollowed out by the damage that remains.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~lily-the-moon":{"haiku":"A speaker seeks escape to another world to find a lost loved one, willing to climb to meet them wherever they are. The moon and alternate reality represent places where separation can be overcome.","opus":"A grief song disguised as fantasy, someone climbing toward the moon to reunite with a lost love and clinging to the hope of another world where they meet again. The tenderness comes from bargaining against a death that can't be undone.","sonnet":"A grief song about losing someone, with the moon as the space where the dead now exist — the narrator would climb to the sky to bring them back, and imagines meeting them in another world. It reads as mourning a person who died young, with the moon as a symbol of where they've gone.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~limbo":{"haiku":"A person waits in emotional limbo, constructing false personas while questioning whether love they received was genuine.","opus":"A portrait of feeling obsolete and left behind, forcing on a composed exterior just to function and wondering whether the loneliness was worth it. Surrounded by insincere warmth, false friends, and coldness, and having overheard that the affection was empty and recycled for someone else, the narrator hangs in suspension behind too many masks, waiting for something to pull him out.","sonnet":"Trapped in social isolation and performed normalcy — the speaker puts on a face to navigate blank smiles and fake friendships, waiting for a sign that the strings attached to people around them were ever really love and not just performance.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~mercia":{"haiku":"Mutual suffering binds predator and prey together while both refuse surrender or forgiveness. Escalation and anguish become the only language each can speak to the other.","opus":"A sinking, obsessive fixation on someone whose hold shows no mercy. The singer would give up his own life to thaw their coldness, yet cannot confront them by himself, their suffering embedded in him even as he drowns and withholds any mercy of his own.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone whose grip is merciless and whose abyss is deep and woven — the speaker sharpens their teeth, knows they cannot face this alone, and asks whether dying would finally make them seen, all while staying enamoured and refusing to lose.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~my-design":{"opus":"A frosty breakup verdict that the relationship was doomed from the start and burned out too quickly, the singer no longer able to offer warmth or keep up a front. He reminds his ex of the warnings he gave, says he never wants to lay eyes on them again, and resolves to move on.","sonnet":"A clean, cold exit from a relationship — the narrator has no affection to give, never belonged to the other person, knows they weren't designed to last, and simply wants to let go and move on without the other person staying either.","src":"sonnet"},
"thornhill~nerv":{"haiku":"A toxic attraction where pain and obsession intertwine, drawn to someone who wounds you deliberately.","opus":"An Icarus-style seduction where the singer knows the beloved will destroy him and flies in anyway, desire and self-annihilation fused, the thrill located precisely in the danger of being cut down.","sonnet":"A song about dangerous infatuation with someone who is self-destructive and possibly corrupt — the narrator knows the relationship will hurt them (\"your fervor could cut me down\") but is drawn in by physical obsession and the thrill of proximity to damage. The Icarus allusion makes the inevitable fall explicit.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~nurture":{"haiku":"Seeking unconditional love and nurturing acceptance despite personal imperfection and the terror of abandonment and isolation.","opus":"The speaker pleads to be nurtured and loved even in his imperfection and darkness, torn between hope and disease, reaching for someone who feels both like a healing idol and a poison, and who may never really be there.","sonnet":"The narrator is in a deteriorating state — physically and mentally sinking — and addresses someone they have placed complete faith in, whose presence is both medicine and poison; the song oscillates between needing to be held and realising that the person they depend on may not truly be there.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~obsession":{"haiku":"A speaker surrenders to obsessive devotion despite its violence. Beauty and suffering intertwine—surrender brings submission and eternal claim.","opus":"An intoxicating mutual obsession where devotion and aggression both feel like heaven, the beloved's gaze at once weapon and shield. The image of a woman ablaze in a portrait frames desire as a consuming, dangerous fixation.","sonnet":"A song about a dangerous, consuming mutual infatuation — the narrator is captivated by someone whose beauty feels like suffering, surrenders completely to the obsession even knowing it may break them, and revels in the dark, possessive pull between two people who are destroying each other.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~only-ever-you":{"haiku":"Obsession and desire intensify through mystery and distance; the beloved becomes the only meaningful anchor.","opus":"An obsessive meditation on ache and distrust toward an inscrutable partner. The singer senses her presence clinging to him yet suspects she doesn't understand herself, holding back while admitting she is all he has. Thornhill stage devotion as a strained, high-pressure hurt, half reverence and half suspicion of a rehearsed exterior.","sonnet":"Desire as weakness: the speaker watches from behind the lens as someone remains deliberately mysterious, playing the surface well, and the admission is involuntary — you make me wait, you make me weak — the curtains staying down even as the faith that they'll rise stays stubbornly lit.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~outcast":{"haiku":"Rejection and severance from control; the singer refuses sympathy or apology, knowing they are an outsider being chased and their work has been erased, severing ties completely.","opus":"A defiant kiss-off from someone who feels watched, judged and dismissed, refusing apology or sympathy after being betrayed by lies. The stance is wounded pride hardening into contempt for those who wrote him off.","sonnet":"A song addressed to someone who tried to control and track the narrator — the narrator cuts them off without apology, convinced that everything he worked for has been destroyed, and issues a quiet threat as a parting shot.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~paparazzi-triple-j-like-a-version":{"haiku":"Obsessive fandom taken to stalking, where admiration becomes a consuming need to possess the idol.","opus":"A cover of Gaga's stalker-love anthem, obsession dressed as fandom, chasing a person down under the guise of adoration until pursuit and devotion become indistinguishable.","sonnet":"A cover of Lady Gaga's stalker-fan anthem — the narrator is obsessively devoted to a celebrity, framing their relentless pursuit as love and fandom. The song plays with the blurred line between fan devotion and predatory fixation.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~parasite":{"opus":"A brutally honest confession of a manipulative, one-sided relationship: the singer admits they never loved or needed their partner and knowingly used them, framing exploitation and pain as the engine that breeds hate and fear.","sonnet":"A person confesses to knowingly using someone they didn't love, treating the relationship as transactional — the song confronts how people inflict pain on each other through manipulation and emotional exploitation, questioning whether hate and fear are just products of how people are treated.","src":"sonnet"},
"thornhill~raw":{"haiku":"A power dynamic of voyeurism and control, where one person demands everything while the other surrenders.","opus":"Predatory want circling a lonely encounter, the singer demanding to be told what to do while refusing to actually stay, desire and withholding tangled into a game where intimacy is offered and yanked back.","sonnet":"About wanting something intensely from a person who won't give it — the narrator craves the other person's full attention, their perfume, their presence, but is pushed away. The shift to the woman's refusal (\"no cause I don't want you to stay\") reframes the desire as invasive, and the final \"I want to be taken out\" is both erotic and self-destructive.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~red-summer":{"haiku":"A person draws others into a false sanctuary of comfort and peace, but those who follow are actually sinking into darkness and potential destruction.","opus":"Thornhill dress suicidal surrender as seduction. The narrator lures someone into an invented sanctuary that promises safety, then lets the fantasy dissolve into drowning imagery. Beneath the romance is a confession of despair, a person who feels too weak to rescue anyone, himself least of all, and who has quietly resolved to give in to the pull under the water.","sonnet":"A protector figure who draws someone into a dreamed, synthetic safe world but confesses private doubt about their own strength to save everyone, while the undercurrent is of drowning — in a dark pool, in blood — and hoping someone will pull them out in return.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~reptile":{"haiku":"The narrator adapts by becoming inhuman, contorting himself psychologically as a survival mechanism in a hostile environment. He questions whether others experience this adaptive pain or if he alone endures it.","opus":"The reptile is a dissociated, cold-blooded self shedding humanity to survive pain, contorting and dancing under others' deranged judgment. The deeper read is trauma-driven adaptation: learning to bury feeling underwater until numb, courting self-destruction while asking whether anyone else feels this alienation.","sonnet":"A person describes feeling like a cold-blooded creature trapped in their own body — unable to process pain normally, forced to adapt by contorting and hiding, craving someone who will stay and witness their suffering rather than flee from it.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~revolver":{"haiku":"Desire merges with danger and possession; vulnerability becomes a weapon in an asymmetrical power dynamic.","opus":"A tense blend of craving, peril and giving in, where a partner's touch turns into a cocked firearm trained on the singer's chest. Thornhill build erotic charge from weaponry imagery, casting devotion as a deliberate step into the field of fire, both exhilarating and ruinous.","sonnet":"A dangerous mutual pull: the narrator sings to the rhythm of her breath and names it a silver revolver, asking her to loosen her resolve while knowing the whole thing is a weapon pointed both ways — wrapped in your chamber, held by your hands and I fire, a romance that reads as both surrender and execution.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~silver-swarm":{"haiku":"Seductive deception poisons intimately; beautiful lies and matched eyes mask malevolence that devours from inside.","opus":"Seductive metalcore about a deceitful partner whose gaze betrays the falsehoods they speak, the singer both fortunate and doomed by the attraction, stripping away their hold as dread and fury build, shouting at himself to flee even while he remains.","sonnet":"About a relationship with someone who is overwhelming and probably dangerous — the speaker is fascinated but frightened, senses something malignant at the core of this person, alternately craves and flees their touch, and ends up accusing them of being fundamentally evil and of making the speaker feel the same way.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~sunflower":{"haiku":"A person is trapped in a toxic dynamic where they are slowly poisoned and kept from growing by someone who watches them wither deliberately and without remorse.","opus":"A relationship reframed as cultivating something only to kill it, tenderness and cruelty braided together as the singer plots slow revenge for years of torment. Growth here is a trap, the flower nurtured precisely so it can be ripped out.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship told from the perspective of the abuser who knows it — the narrator describes cultivating someone like a flower while withholding what they need, letting them be killed slowly, vowing revenge for the torment while knowing they themselves are the monster.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~temperer":{"haiku":"Envy and manipulation poison the temple of identity. Less given means less taken—emotional withholding as defense against manipulation.","opus":"A portrait of a manipulator ruling from behind the lens, envy and control festering in a cold neon haze. The final calculus, that the less he gives the less can be taken, makes emotional withholding a survival tactic.","sonnet":"A short, tightly wound song about being manipulated by someone who rules through envy and emotional control — the narrator is buried under their expectations and surveillance, surrendering to being laid down gently while resisting full exposure.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~the-haze":{"haiku":"A lover's isolation and self-dissolution enable another's growth through sacrifice. The singer lets themselves fade to allow someone else to emerge from emotional fog into clarity.","opus":"A song about self-abnegating love and release: the singer chooses to cross scorching rain and a bewildering fog, absorbing the hurt and going under so the other can awaken and flourish. He concludes that admitting the beloved in means surrendering himself, chasing a warmth on the far side of the murk.","sonnet":"A person submerging themselves — into substance, into numbness, into the liquid sunshine of the haze — choosing to fall back rather than fight, eventually sacrificing their own clarity so the person they love has room to breathe and grow.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~the-hellfire-club":{"haiku":"Seduction and dependency trap the speaker in emotional servitude; captivated by another's power, they surrender will and remain despite knowing the cost.","opus":"An obsessive, half-masochistic desire song, the narrator begging a temptress to stay or finish him off and admitting people last longer in his imagination than in his real life. He falls too easily and needs her past all reason, swearing to trail her anywhere just to be carried out of himself.","sonnet":"Obsessive romantic captivation that the narrator knows is dangerous but can't relinquish — \"people stay much longer in my head than in my life\" is the admission, and the song oscillates between wanting the other person to stay a while and threatening to swallow them whole. The Valkyrie address and the silver tongue on the floor signal that the surrender is chosen and total.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~tongues":{"haiku":"Manipulation and control operate through seduction and linguistic power; the speaker is twisted by another's will, their own agency corrupted into complicity.","opus":"A volatile push-pull with a lover who leads the speaker astray and haunts his skin and bone, ripped apart yet refusing to let go. Its erotic-violent imagery frames a devotion he insists he doesn't need even as he can't stand to watch her slip away.","sonnet":"A toxic pull that the narrator renounces and returns to in the same breath — being led astray, fingernails driven down the walls, the tongue turned in two \"over my heart.\" The question \"upon your sword will I fall?\" is answered by the fact that the song never ends with departure; the narrator is still there, still howling.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~under-the-knife":{"haiku":"Intimacy becomes blade work: touch carries threat beneath tenderness, and love operates as entanglement rather than connection.","opus":"Staged as opening night, a surrender to a lover who wields a blade, sweetness and danger fused, the singer lost in love and desperate to be noticed even while lying vulnerable beneath the knife of the one he adores.","sonnet":"A song about surrendering completely to someone whose love is both dangerous and irresistible — the speaker is literally beneath their knife, yet so captivated by their presence that they remain lost in it even as innocence fades and the lights flare.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~views-from-the-sun":{"haiku":"Under an apocalyptic sun, the speaker and lover are torn apart and destroyed; silence falls as the world burns and connection severs completely.","opus":"A relationship disintegrating through apocalyptic imagery: corroding surfaces, sallow grit, a collapsing sun. The plea about fleeing brightness to rescue someone, and the flat dismissal that closes each verse, cast the breakup as a shared, world-ending act of mutual destruction.","sonnet":"A relationship is ending in an environment of desolation and heat — the speaker questions whether they were always doomed, watches the sun go down on the connection, and finds silence where there used to be two people.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~viper-room":{"haiku":"Desire and danger intertwine; the speaker surrenders to seduction despite the poison, caught between the desire to be consumed and the need to escape.","opus":"A seductive, dangerous game of shifting power named for the notorious LA club, the narrator offering to become whatever a captivating, venomous lover wants and chasing a cure in the very poison of her touch. Thornhill make the pursuit intoxicating and consuming, a dare to love someone who leaves burns.","sonnet":"A seduction framed as mutual ruin — the narrator offers to be whatever the other person wants, calls the connection poison but still begs for the cure, and the other person's allure keeps pulling them back even as they feel lost in \"tears of scarlet.\" The Primadonna line positions the narrator as a willing spectacle trapped between desire and irrelevance.","src":"haiku"},
"thornhill~where-we-go-when-we-die":{"haiku":"Death and loss; questioning what lies beyond and the pain of separation, doubting paradise without the other.","opus":"A reckoning with mortality and the lies sold about paradise and freedom, questioning whether any of it was real. What anchors it is love, the refusal to face death or an empty afterlife without the one person who matters.","sonnet":"The speaker questions a promised paradise they no longer believe in, watching the world die while others are kept compliant by illusions of freedom, and desperately needing another person to be with them rather than leaving them alone in a world that feels dead.","src":"haiku"},
"thoughtcrimes~rose-bather":{"haiku":"Violation and arousal fuse in a landscape of bone tools and predatory appetite, where the speaker revels in transgression. Knife play becomes scripture, and crossing lines produces the prize of transformation.","opus":"A disturbing, visceral piece equating intimacy with violence and aroused danger. The narrator speaks of purging, of knife play cutting deep, of crossing lines to taste a prize, blurring pain and desire until love reads only as a passing consequence. It ends in cold dismissal, reducing the other to nothing at all.","sonnet":"Imagery of feral wounds, vulture-picked flesh, and knife-play maps an extreme intimacy that oscillates between pain and arousal; the speaker gauges the hours alongside a 'bitter lust', with the closing line 'you're nothing to me now' delivering a cold reversal after all the intensity.","src":"haiku"},
"thousand-below~blue-roses-don-t-fade-feat-cvlte":{"haiku":"Bilingual devotion to dysfunction; the speaker pledges flowers transformed through suffering, bound to someone who embodies their disorder.","opus":"A bilingual duet on toxic attachment, addiction to someone who is both the disorder's cause and its end, anguish becoming a shared language as filth and guilt fill the head and blue roses stand for a warped, impossible offering of love.","sonnet":"A relationship that ended the speaker's disorder, yet left them sick for having let someone in who broke everything — mid-song the Japanese verses layer in karma, begging for forgiveness, staining red roses blue, and a mind shattering while bloodshot drips on the floor.","src":"haiku"},
"thousand-below~face-to-face":{"haiku":"Mental illness isolates through neurological misalignment; no matter how close physically, the speaker's frequency diverges too radically for genuine human connection to survive.","opus":"A reckoning with someone whose mind turns against them, hiding aggression and suicidal despair behind a counterfeit grin. The singer declines to be pulled onto their wavelength and insists they locate their own rescue rather than dragging him under.","sonnet":"Mental illness and social incompatibility are described from the inside — the brain works differently, bad vibrations swing mood up and down all day, and the speaker knows they will never be genuinely face to face with another person whose signals kill their frequency. The advice to just take a deep breath is acknowledged and immediately rejected as insufficient.","src":"haiku"},
"thousand-below~venenosa":{"haiku":"A relationship steeped in poison and danger, where love and harm become inseparable.","opus":"A love addicted to its own damage, where the partner feeds on the singer's flaws and breaking, poison mistaken for intimacy, craving the very cruelty that's killing the relationship.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship where both people poison each other — one is drawn back repeatedly knowing it is lethal, loving the way the other sins and savages them, and finally asking to be killed or claimed or saved.","src":"haiku"},
"thousand-foot-krutch~phenomenon":{"haiku":"Unified rebels resist infection and mediocrity through collective sound and movement; the crowd refuses invisibility and ordinariness, embracing shine and awakening against contamination and conformity.","opus":"A rap-rock rallying cry for the overlooked to unite and move as one, laced with the band's Christian undertone of refusing to be invisible. Energy and defiance channeled into waking others and standing out rather than fading through.","sonnet":"A rallying call to a community of outcasts — riders, freedom fighters — urging them to move together, lose control, and shake off the ordinary in a collective surge of energy.","src":"haiku"},
"thousand-thoughts~change":{"haiku":"Internal fracture and betrayal spiral into obsessive rumination where the speaker's mind becomes their torment.","opus":"Watching a partner change and feeling caged and poisoned by the relationship, unable to breathe and unheard. The second half turns to comfort-eating, drinking, and beer-goggle denial as the speaker drowns racing thoughts trying to face a truth too hard to swallow.","sonnet":"Someone betrayed by a person they trusted completely — given false promises, manipulated — processes the devastation through both raw anger and dazed late-night wallowing, cycling through denial and the bitter recognition that they were deliberately deceived.","src":"haiku"},
"thousand-thoughts~control":{"haiku":"Emotional manipulation gradually erodes agency until the narrator surrenders autonomy completely, unable to resist cycles of gaslighting and self-doubt.","opus":"An account of an emotionally controlling relationship, the narrator silenced, doubting himself, apologizing for things that aren't his fault while someone slowly takes him over. A plea to be released before it suffocates him.","sonnet":"Someone in a relationship where they consistently suppress themselves to avoid conflict reaches a breaking point — they internalise blame, apologise reflexively, and watch the other person continue anyway, until the only honest word left is that this was never what they agreed to.","src":"haiku"},
"thousand-thoughts~stigma":{"opus":"A frustrated cry about mental-health stigma and being unheard, the narrator silently screaming while people who preach unity don't practice it, caring for an unwell mother, exhausted by faking smiles and by friends who never check if he's okay.","sonnet":"About the exhaustion of living with mental illness while people around you either maintain the stigma or fail to check in — the narrator is fed up with having to repeat themselves, watching others preach unity while practicing indifference, and feeling increasingly disconnected and alone.","src":"sonnet"},
"thousand-thoughts~undone":{"haiku":"Collapse from within: thoughts spiral beyond control, and the cumulative weight of internal noise leaves only the possibility of dissolution.","opus":"A night-drive spiral of anxiety and self-loathing, the ghost of the past riding shotgun as the mind runs like a wounded, flightless bird. Faking smiles and bottling everything up until you come completely undone.","sonnet":"A song about a dissociative, overwhelmed mental state while driving at night — the ghost of the past rides along, the speaker's mind circles like a flightless bird, and they ultimately acknowledge they have come completely undone.","src":"haiku"},
"thrash-or-die~fatal-fury":{"haiku":"Immortal predator wields dominion through violence and spectacle; survival guarantees kingship while empathy dissolves into appetite for suffering.","opus":"Death personified as an ageless predator that outlives everyone, gleefully cataloguing addiction, cancer and self-inflicted ruin, the reaper writing the final chapter and vowing never to leave while you still breathe.","sonnet":"Addiction speaks in the first person as an ancient, immortal predator that has always been here and will outlast you — it stabs backs, salts wounds, bends souls, and closes the book on tomorrow. The speaker-as-addiction catalogues its methods with gleeful precision, from hypodermic hooks to watered cirrhosis, promising never to go away while you still breathe.","src":"haiku"},
"thrash-or-die~the-return-of-the-thrash-lord":{"haiku":"Nihilistic supremacy resurrects from metaphorical death; speed metal becomes doctrine of superiority where contempt for mainstream validates brutal unapologetic existence.","opus":"Genre-worship taken to cartoon extremes, a thrash-metal band crowning itself a vengeful deity who slaughters posers and dethrones God. The whole point is the joke of total self-seriousness in service of a subgenre's purity codes.","sonnet":"A self-mythologizing thrash metal boast in which the Thrash Lord kills God and the devil, rises from ash, devours posers, and rules humanity from his throne. It is pure genre hyperbole, operating entirely as comic-book dominance fantasy with no second layer.","src":"haiku"},
"thredge~dysfunctional":{"haiku":"Psychological damage becomes identity as the speaker accepts their own dysfunction as permanent.","opus":"A poisonous bond with someone who takes pleasure in the singer's suffering, the wound burned so deep into his mind that he calls himself dysfunctional and can never heal.","sonnet":"The speaker is locked in a masochistic bond with someone who openly enjoys causing pain — \"you like the way I bleed\" — and though they watch this person shine, they know they will never heal; a jaw sewn shut and a descent into the abyss follow.","src":"haiku"},
"three-days-grace~animal-i-have-become":{"haiku":"The speaker cannot escape their darkest nature—caged and raging, they beg for help taming the beast they have become.","opus":"Addiction or a violent urge cast as a beast the singer cannot restrain or flee, locked in his personal torment and pleading for someone to subdue the ugliest part of him. Underneath runs the frantic wish to be convinced this creature isn't truly who he is, a struggle to believe a human still exists beneath the monster.","sonnet":"About losing control of one's own darkest impulses and not recognizing yourself anymore — the narrator is trapped inside a version of himself driven by rage, begging someone to help tame the animal he's become before it destroys everything.","src":"haiku"},
"three-days-grace~break":{"haiku":"Escape and transformation require breaking from constraint; elevation and transcendence become possible only through abandoning earthly circumstance and social order.","opus":"Manic escape as self-medication, the urge to start a fire and flee everything at once, the vampire imagery hinting the real addiction is to the high itself. Angst turned into a compulsion to run rather than a solution.","sonnet":"A restless escape fantasy in which the speaker urges himself and others to break away from the suffocating environment around them and rise to something higher, fueled by a vampiric night energy and a refusal to stay stuck.","src":"haiku"},
"three-days-grace~pain":{"haiku":"Preferring any sensation to numbness, embracing agony as proof of being alive and feeling something real.","opus":"Numbness so total that hurt becomes proof of being alive, sold as seduction by a narrator promising to rescue someone from their own emptiness. The comfort offered is really just more pain.","sonnet":"A speaker offers pain as a preferable alternative to numbness — addressing someone who has gone emotionally hollow, promising that anger and agony are better than misery and that feeling something, even something awful, is proof of being alive.","src":"haiku"},
"three-days-grace~painkiller":{"haiku":"Offering oneself as numbing drug—shoulder and fix—to keep someone addicted and dependent on comfort.","opus":"Addiction figured as a lover who is simultaneously the substance, the comfort you weep against and the lethal hit you overindulge in. It understands that reliance disguised as affection keeps its prey circling back for more.","sonnet":"A toxic dependency rendered as a seduction — the speaker offers themselves as the numbing agent their target craves, knowing the person will keep coming back, and the shoulder to cry on is also the dose that eventually kills.","src":"haiku"},
"three-days-grace~riot":{"haiku":"Collective uprising channels shared grievance into organized resistance; the downtrodden mobilise together to overturn systems of subordination.","opus":"A blunt catalogue of feeling used, stepped on and enraged, channeled into a mass invitation to rise up. The point is solidarity: reassuring the beaten-down they aren't alone, then turning that shared fury into a chant.","sonnet":"An anthem of collective defiance addressed to anyone who feels used, stepped on, and dismissed — the speaker insists they are not alone and calls for unified uprising as the only honest response to shared degradation.","src":"haiku"},
"three-days-grace~world-so-cold":{"haiku":"Abandonment and emotional freezing leave the narrator numb and aging prematurely, counting days in a world that no longer contains warmth or meaning.","opus":"Numb, guilt-ridden aftermath of loss, living inside lies and a soulless shell, feeling far too young to be this hollowed out. Depression rendered as a frozen, colorless world since someone left.","sonnet":"Someone left behind by a departed person — through death or abandonment — finds the world frozen and meaningless without them, trapped in numbness and paralysis, feeling too young to have lost so much.","src":"haiku"},
"thrice~image-of-the-invisible":{"haiku":"Transcendent identity extends beyond matter; the named and known resist erasure through collective consciousness and sacred defiance against obliteration.","opus":"A defiant humanist-spiritual anthem insisting people are more than chemistry, named and known and possessed of free will, refusing any quiet erasure. Dustin Kensrue channeling faith into a communal rallying cry against dehumanization.","sonnet":"A rallying declaration that human beings are more than their physical matter — made in the image of something unseen and therefore possessed of inherent worth — calling a community of the named and known to resist a quiet cull and reclaim their souls together.","src":"haiku"},
"thrice~northern-lights":{"haiku":"Awakening to new perspectives transforms understanding; the world reveals itself as interconnected beauty when perception shifts beyond narrow sight.","opus":"A vision of a better world glimpsed for the first time — one of shared joy and clasped hands, seen only once the narrow, fearful mind lets go. Thrice makes it a hopeful demand for the impossible: a horizon of collective belonging suddenly in view.","sonnet":"A vision of communal transformation: the speaker has discovered a new sun and horizon and can now see \"a better way to build a world where every hand is held and holding on,\" with the final chant — \"we want it all, we demand the impossible\" — framing this utopia as both aspiration and defiance.","src":"haiku"},
"thrice~open-your-eyes-and-dream":{"haiku":"Labor exploitation persists despite awareness; meritocracy proves myth while collective resignation prevents solidarity, trapping workers in cyclical servitude.","opus":"A takedown of bootstrap mythology, arguing that the meritocracy story exists to justify a rigged system while cries for change get met with force. Its answer isn't despair but organized solidarity and the refusal to accept things as inevitable.","sonnet":"A call to working-class solidarity against a system that answers calls for change with violence — sweating workers break their backs for half a living wage, myths of meritocracy honour only exceptions, and the only response is to devise solidarity together. The repeated insistence that \"nothing has to be like this\" is the song's political spine.","src":"haiku"},
"thrice~the-artist-in-the-ambulance":{"haiku":"Near-fatal crisis strips away performance and exposes the gap between empty rhetoric and authentic change; the speaker vows genuine action over performative words while grappling with mortality.","opus":"Surviving a crash, a young man realizes he owes his survival to a stranger and is ashamed of how much he has only talked about doing good while doing none. It is a vow to trade rhetoric for action before his time runs out.","sonnet":"After blacking out in a car crash and being rushed to hospital, the speaker is jolted into reckoning with how little he has to show for his life, resolving to move past empty rhetoric toward genuine action — \"there's a difference between sleight of hand and giving everything you have.\"","src":"haiku"},
"thrice~the-color-of-the-sky":{"haiku":"Curiosity and self-discovery drive a solitary journey beyond confinement; the speaker breaks free from imposed limits to experience beauty and wonder on their own terms.","opus":"Someone born inside a walled city defies the story that nothing exists beyond it and tunnels out into a new world of unnamed colors and dawn. It is a parable of leaving the only reality you were given to discover you belong on an open frontier.","sonnet":"A speaker who grew up inside a walled city told nothing exists beyond it resolves one night to find the passage through, emerges into open country at dawn, and is overwhelmed by colour — \"the brilliance overwhelms me\" — stepping into a frontier he always sensed he belonged to but never thought he'd reach.","src":"haiku"},
"thrown~backfire":{"haiku":"Internal haunting perpetuates as the speaker's nemesis infiltrates their consciousness, the obsession becoming more damaging than distance ever could.","opus":"An obsessive tormentor turned inside out, the target refusing to shrink and instead lodging permanently in the bully's head. It reframes harassment as the aggressor's own weakness, the victim now the ghost that won't leave.","sonnet":"A speaker who has taken up permanent residence in an obsessive rival's head: the rival talks behind their back, loses sleep, and is haunted in dreams, while the speaker — never intending to haunt anyone — simply notes it's time for the obsession to pay its cost.","src":"haiku"},
"thrown~dislike":{"haiku":"Judgment and public scrutiny drive the speaker toward apathy as they reject others' moral condemnation.","opus":"Defiance against a self-righteous judge who spreads lies and kicks him when he's down, the singer refusing to care about the slander of a hypocrite who hates him for no real reason.","sonnet":"Someone is being judged, lied about, and kicked while down by a hypocrite who operates from nothing more than personal dislike — \"base your belief on your own dislike\" — and the speaker refuses to keep caring what a known liar says.","src":"haiku"},
"thrown~dwell":{"haiku":"Self-made suffering and entrapment—the speaker has learned nothing and cycles through despair, self-blame, and wishing for death while feeling drained of the will to change.","opus":"Unsparing hardcore about relapse and self-loathing, the narrator caught between the grave and the ledge in a hell of his own making. The refusal to blame anyone but himself is both honesty and the trap the addiction keeps him in.","sonnet":"Someone trapped in a self-made hell of stagnation and depression acknowledges they have not learned anything, that their will to live is drained, and that they alone are responsible for where they have ended up — a stark admission of suicidal ideation without deflection.","src":"haiku"},
"thrown~fast-forward":{"haiku":"The narrator is immobilized in depression and worthlessness, wanting to end it all because they feel destined to fail.","opus":"A raw look at depressive deadness, the narrator stalled while everything slides downward, unable to remember when he last felt okay and wanting only to skip ahead to a collapse he takes as inevitable. His room becomes a shrinking cage. Thrown are a Swedish hardcore act whose crushing beatdown sound pairs this bleak mental-health confession with weight and zero false comfort.","sonnet":"Total affective numbness collapsing into a wish to skip to the end. The room feels like a cell, ambition and desire have gone completely dark, and the speaker is too exhausted by years of failed self-repair to argue for continuing.","src":"haiku"},
"thrown~grayout":{"haiku":"After a destructive relationship ends definitively, the speaker gains clarity but sees the world turned gray despite finally being free.","opus":"The Swedish hardcore act pairs crushing modern breakdowns with a blunt severance, its narrator demanding honesty, unafraid of solitude, and asking a doomed partner to keep their distance for both their sakes. Even as resolve finally brings clarity, everything still looks washed-out and colorless, the lingering hurt outlasting the decision to walk away.","sonnet":"Someone demanding clarity after too long in emotional limbo delivers an ultimatum: end this now and be honest, because the uncertainty is worse than the loss. Once the decision is made, everything looks gray — not from grief but from the strange flatness that follows relief. Freedom and desolation arrive together.","src":"haiku"},
"thrown~guilt":{"haiku":"Self-blame becomes inescapable prison as the speaker refuses forgiveness for past mistakes. Broken record of identical thoughts spirals toward suicidal ideation.","opus":"Being crushed by inescapable remorse and self-reproach over old mistakes, an overthinking mind snagged on the same dark loop, spiraling toward wanting to die while unable to pardon what it can't put down.","sonnet":"An obsessive guilt-loop: every bad decision has led here, the speaker has grown into a man he hates, the same thoughts return on repeat like a broken record, and the inner voice telling lies drives him toward wanting to die before he catches himself and starts again — 'blame it on me, I already do anyway' delivered not as self-pity but as bleak self-knowledge.","src":"haiku"},
"thrown~look-at-me":{"haiku":"Mental anguish and spiraling thoughts consume the speaker; paralysis sets in as they oscillate between numbness and catastrophic imagination.","opus":"Spiraling anxiety narrated from inside the loop, oscillating between numbness and dread, insisting it wants only to vent rather than be helped. The Swedish band captures how catastrophizing feeds on the smallest triggers until existing itself feels effortful.","sonnet":"A narrator oscillating between numbness and overwhelm — 'emotionally overwhelmed or just an empty shell' — isn't asking for help, just asking to complain, trapped in a loop of catastrophising what-ifs and quietly wondering whether dying would even be noticed.","src":"haiku"},
"thrown~new-low":{"haiku":"A person questions their sanity, unable to distinguish their thoughts from outside voices, spiraling into self-contempt and the deepest despair.","opus":"Climbing up only to plunge once more, the narrator trusts himself not at all, unable to separate fantasy from fact or his own mind from a foreign presence at his shoulder. Thrown render spiralling self-hatred through battering metallic hardcore, a limitless ocean poised to swallow him as remorse hauls him down to fresh depths.","sonnet":"A person losing grip on the boundary between thought and internal voice, unable to trust their own mind or distinguish self from something other that watches and narrates. Having hit what feels like a genuinely new floor, they acknowledge that this collapse is not a familiar place — it is unprecedented and deepening.","src":"haiku"},
"thrown~on-the-verge":{"haiku":"Surrender to overwhelming depression and internal demons; every effort crumbles into futility.","opus":"A brutally honest portrait of depression and burnout from the Swedish hardcore band. The narrator, unable to sleep or breathe, walks under streetlights trying to clear a mind he can't come to terms with, outnumbered by laughing demons. Perpetually on the edge of giving up, having given everything and still lost, it's the ache of exhaustion made loud.","sonnet":"A person outnumbered by their own demons — sleeps in to cope, lies awake hating — the fight is not dramatic but exhausted: always on the verge of giving up, already having given everything, the only thing left to say to the world is fuck this, I am so over it, you don't want to be on my team because I always lose what matters.","src":"haiku"},
"thrown~rehearsed":{"haiku":"Betrayal is well-practiced manipulation; the narrator weaponizes emotional numbness and callousness to survive another cycle of the same old damage.","opus":"A furious kiss-off to a manipulative ex whose tears and apologies feel like a well-drilled act. The narrator, hauled through the muck and stripped of his worth, refuses the crawling-back routine, cutting his heart off at the flip of a switch to shield himself. Beneath the bravado of chosen numbness sits real, unhealed hurt.","sonnet":"Accusation directed at an abuser who returns for sympathy after stripping the speaker of worth and dragging them through the dirt — you're not the one with nothing — the sobbing read as performance because this kind of cruelty has clearly been rehearsed before.","src":"haiku"},
"thundercat~dragonball-durag":{"haiku":"Playful seduction wrapped in street style confidence, seeking affirmation through intimate teasing and charm.","opus":"A goofy, tender come-on where the durag stands in for every insecurity, Bruner asking again and again whether he looks good because charm and self-doubt live together. Nerdy, sincere seduction that laughs at itself.","sonnet":"A narrator shamelessly courts a woman while fishing for validation about his durag, his chains, his car, and his general vibe — it is playful and self-aware seduction dressed up in nerdy confessions, seeking intimacy through transparent flattery.","src":"haiku"},
"thundercat~lone-wolf-and-cub":{"haiku":"Solitude and survival demand fortitude; separated from protection, the wounded wanderer faces unknown terrain with only resilience as compass.","opus":"A tender address to someone facing the world alone, drawn from the samurai tale of a masterless swordsman and his child, asking where they'll go and urging them to heal and fight on. Solitude met with quiet care.","sonnet":"A short, tender address to a solitary figure — wolf and cub, alone in the world — asking where they will go and what they will do, urging them to fight and heal without offering easy answers.","src":"haiku"},
"thundercat~tron-song":{"haiku":"Tender devotion despite distance and uncertainty, grounded in reassurance and return.","opus":"Bruner's devotion to his cat rendered as a love ballad, faithful and always returning no matter where he roams. The joke is real affection: unconditional loyalty aimed at a pet that rocks his world.","sonnet":"A devotional loop of reassurance addressed to a beloved — the speaker promises always to return, never to stray, and insists the other person rocks their world, with little elaboration beyond the emotional absolute of fidelity.","src":"haiku"},
"thutmose~memories":{"haiku":"Past trauma resurfaces in new lovers, trapping the narrator in cycles of mistrust and heartbreak as unresolved pain prevents authentic connection.","opus":"A breakup where memories keep resurfacing in the shape of someone new, the singer done being fooled and asking what's left to give after love and trust are gone.","sonnet":"Old memories return embodied in a new person, reviving the same pain of unrequited love and broken trust; the speaker exhausted themselves giving everything and getting nothing, eventually reaching the point of walking out — \"pick your things witcha, I finally had enough.\"","src":"haiku"},
"thxsomch~waste-my-mind":{"haiku":"Desire seeks annihilation through a partner; pleasure merges with death, control, and the erasure of selfhood through intimate submission.","opus":"A ballad of codependent obsession that dresses ruin up as devotion. The narrator invites a lover to consume him, conflating intimacy with self-destruction until the beloved becomes both the thing that ends him and the only thing that makes him feel alive. It sits in the hyperpop-adjacent bedroom emo lineage where tenderness and morbidity share the same breath.","sonnet":"Mutual annihilation as intimacy: come waste my mind, come waste your life here — the speaker chokes before he talks, replays every fault, and names the other person his suicide while asking to be made alive, turning codependence into something almost erotic in its self-destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"thy-art-is-murder~make-america-hate-again":{"haiku":"Revolutionary violence emerges as necessary response to systemic betrayal; chaos and bloodshed replace failed negotiation and pacifist capitulation.","opus":"A furious deathcore repudiation of nationalist sloganeering, flipping the campaign promise into a call for violent revolt against leaders it names as enemies. Rage weaponized as protest, drunk on apocalyptic bloodshed imagery.","sonnet":"A furious rejection of nationalist rhetoric and the leaders who weaponise it — the speaker argues that pacifism is no longer viable against systems that are demonic in their exploitation, and calls for open hostility as the only honest political stance.","src":"haiku"},
"thy-art-is-murder~new-gods":{"haiku":"Algorithmic influence and artificial celebrity create a new priesthood that weaponizes followers and manufactures consent; technology amplifies the ancient hunger for false gods.","opus":"An attack on the new deities of algorithms and social media, believers turned into weapons and flattened into data, culture swapped out for programming. Deathcore dread aimed at how screens engineer devotion and division.","sonnet":"Social media and digital culture are indicted as a new religion manufacturing false idols and weaponising followers, replacing genuine human connection with algorithmic control and leading a blind, complicit population toward its own erasure.","src":"haiku"},
"ti-sto~on-my-way-feat-bright-sparks":{"haiku":"Resilience persists despite scarcity; the speaker refuses capitulation while acknowledging the journey remains unfinished.","opus":"A festival-ready resilience anthem: broke and unlucky in love but refusing to give up, shrugging off a world rooting for your collapse. Uplift built for the drop rather than deep reflection.","sonnet":"Someone without money or romantic success insists they are moving forward anyway — progress as a state of mind that survives external failure, social hostility, and the temptation to give up. The defiance is quiet but durable.","src":"haiku"},
"ti-sto~split-only-u-original-mix":{"haiku":"Pure electronic energy; a DJ commanding a crowd and asserting dominance through sound and presence.","opus":"A DJ's floor-filler self-mythology, nothing beneath the boast of commanding the crowd and the room recognizing the sound, pure function as a drop and a chant.","sonnet":"A minimal DJ performance track — the narrator commands the crowd, claims ownership of the room through sound, and asks the crowd to submit to the beat. There's no narrative beyond the performance itself.","src":"haiku"},
"tie-rex~different-on-my-skin":{"haiku":"Grief transforms perception; absence of the beloved renders the sensory world alien and unfamiliar.","opus":"Grief refracted through the judgment of others. Someone mourns a death in their own crooked way while acquaintances police how sorrow should look, offering hollow concern they never showed before. Sunlight itself feels altered on the skin now that the person is gone, the change bodily and permanent rather than performed for anyone watching.","sonnet":"Grief that doesn't perform correctly for others: people who never helped now declare they're worried and want to care, not understanding how the speaker actually crawls — the light of the sun feels so different on my skin since you're gone, a physical fact that needs no explanation and gets none.","src":"haiku"},
"tiffany-day~american-girl":{"haiku":"Self-doubt and the desperate need to belong consume a young woman, who performs constant deference and self-erasure to win approval while internally dissolving.","opus":"An anxious outsider offers total self-erasure as the price of belonging, promising to do anything to be let into a world she feels she doesn't yet look like she deserves. The Americanness is aspirational rather than owned, and the overthinking never stops long enough to let her be herself.","sonnet":"A song about social anxiety and identity insecurity at the intersection of cultural outsiderdom — the speaker does not know who she is, does not look like the people she wants acceptance from, gets drunk just to cope with being at parties, and promises to be whatever is needed to belong.","src":"haiku"},
"tiffany-day~same-la":{"haiku":"Repetition defines existence as the speaker circles through identical moments. Sameness brands all experience.","opus":"Social anxiety mapped onto a party the narrator cannot navigate. Convinced everyone secretly despises her, she stays paralyzed at the edge of a crush, longing to speak yet certain that only being truly known could earn love. The repeated stammer of wanting to talk becomes the whole ache: connection sabotaged by a brain that expects rejection.","sonnet":"Social anxiety in two cities that exist in the same zip code: the narrator coughs on the blunt, can't admit how nervous she is, wears mismatched shoes to the party because she'd rather scream — her brain tells her everyone hates her, and she keeps not talking to the person she wishes could know her in an instant and love her.","src":"haiku"},
"tiffany-day~twfno":{"haiku":"Racing against passing time, grappling with how quickly moments fade despite their beauty and intensity.","opus":"A pep talk to herself about time as money that can't be saved, spent whether you want it or not. Bright resolve to stop racing the clock and actually inhabit a life she loves.","sonnet":"Time is reframed as a daily budget of money you cannot save — the speaker urges herself and her listener to spend every second rather than hoard it, driving forward with the view in front of her and falling in love with the simple fact of being alive.","src":"haiku"},
"tigercub~blue-mist-in-my-head":{"opus":"Depression and anxiety hidden behind a picture of wellness, the narrator faking fine while a fog clouds his mind and others treat his illness as mere moodiness. Tigercub name the vacant stare and the way brightness fails to shield him, the whole thing resolving into a single honest confession at the close: underneath it all, he's afraid.","sonnet":"A song about living with anxiety as a constant, invisible condition — the narrator performs wellness to the outside world, smiling through broken teeth, but internally dissociates into a ten-mile stare and a blue mental fog that never fully lifts. The song is unusually specific about anxiety as something made for them, almost like a biological inheritance.","src":"sonnet"},
"tigercub~burning-effigies":{"haiku":"A person suffers from anxiety and self-doubt, burning symbols of themselves while recognizing how truth divides and corrupts relationships.","opus":"The Brighton trio wraps fuzzed alt-rock around a narrator who dreams himself an afterthought hiding behind a false name, watching charm curdle and a self-made idol collapse until a lover is gone. Mounting dread clears the way for a self-deceiver, building toward a confessed killing meant to unload a crushing weight.","sonnet":"A figure haunted by dreams of inadequacy and romantic rejection burns as a symbol of collective disillusionment — a society whose god is fraudulent and whose sky is scarred. Beneath the apocalyptic imagery is a more intimate ache: belonging desperately desired but perpetually out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"tigercub~i-w-g-f-u":{"haiku":"The speaker describes emotional numbness and detachment, existing in extremes that nullify meaning. They express a desire to escape consciousness through intoxication.","opus":"A numbed, dissociated narrator who feels nothing real and just wants to obliterate himself, the negations piling up into a total loss of self. The chant is despair disguised as a party, going around in circles toward oblivion.","sonnet":"Total emotional and perceptual numbness — the narrator can neither feel nor perform any authentic human response, trapped in an endless loop of non-being, the only honest desire being to get fucked up enough to break out of it.","src":"haiku"},
"tigercub~play-my-favourite-song":{"haiku":"Silence triggers depression; the speaker needs music to interrupt the inner void and prove they still exist.","opus":"Escapism through music as shelter from encroaching darkness, silence itself pulling him under, so the narrator demands his favorite record while dark waters rise, falsehoods brand him ugly, and nightmares reach into his bed. The song is armor against a filthy, dividing sea.","sonnet":"Black tides and bad dreams crowd in, but the speaker's one defence against feeling low is putting on a beloved record — silence is the real enemy, and the song is a request to someone to fill it before the nightmares can.","src":"haiku"},
"tigercub~serial-killer":{"haiku":"Online performance and performative outrage flatten discourse into cruel theatre; the narrator positions himself as both perpetrator and victim of linguistic violence.","opus":"A sardonic swipe at online outrage culture, the endless typing and posturing that fixes nothing, with the narrator sneering at himself as just another loudmouth in the thread. Self-aware disgust at the whole pointless pile-on.","sonnet":"A self-aware takedown of online outrage culture — the speaker both mocks the performative aggression of internet commenters and confesses, with dark humour, to being exactly the same kind of loudmouthed, self-righteous fool.","src":"haiku"},
"tigercub~swoon":{"haiku":"Truth's weight drags toward dissolution; the speaker feels gravity pulling them toward harm despite glimpsing light across the distance.","opus":"The pull of an inevitable emotional undertow, a person sensing danger and truth dragging them back toward someone they can't resist. The calm-in-the-storm imagery frames surrender as both relief and self-endangerment.","sonnet":"Pulled back toward someone by a weight of truth they cannot resist — chills on the skin, doubt in the shadow, calm in the storm that brings them closer to harm — the speaker swoons home, somewhere they can believe.","src":"haiku"},
"tigercub~until-i-forget":{"haiku":"Time erases identity until the speaker no longer recognizes themselves.","opus":"A stoner-rock meditation on holding your breath through heartbreak until the memory loosens. Summer rain becomes the recurring wash that both cleanses and reopens the wound, while the narrator calls himself a fool circling closer to collapse. The parting words of someone who left keep flooding back, and forgetting is the only relief on offer.","sonnet":"Words spoken at a departure lodge in the speaker's head, replayed until breathless, held there until forgetting becomes the only release. Summer rain washes things away but not quite, and the fool's implosion is postponed only by holding breath — the lyric lives in that suspended interval between hearing and forgetting.","src":"haiku"},
"tigerwine~black-water":{"haiku":"The speaker descends into murky depths, hypnotized and trapped by another's words, unable to escape the tower's mouth of darkness.","opus":"An inward slide into doubt and self-sabotage, with intrusive voices in the head and defenses rebuilt yet again. The narrator peers down into himself as if into a deep shaft, gambling on his craving for a fresh start while suspecting the whole thing is a snare set to spring the moment he trusts it.","sonnet":"A state of mental siege: voices in the head, walls going up, someone else's poisonous influence bleeding into one's thoughts, and the growing recognition that the trap has already closed.","src":"haiku"},
"tilian~dose":{"haiku":"Toxic dependency and manipulation dressed as care. The speaker positions themselves as necessary medicine while preventing escape and inducing dependency.","opus":"A caretaker's coos slide into something predatory, offering comfort and cure while quietly ensuring dependence deepens until the beloved can no longer breathe without them. Known as Dance Gavin Dance's melodic vocalist, Tilian leans into pop sleekness here so the tenderness reads as a lure, the healing language of addiction and control dressed up as devotion.","sonnet":"A predator masquerades as a caretaker — soothing, calibrating, and deliberately engineering dependency in someone who has begun to trust. The tenderness is a trap designed to run until the other person stops breathing, need amplifying with every passing day.","src":"haiku"},
"till-lindemann~bers-meer":{"haiku":"Separation across water becomes agony, where wind carries the beloved's name beyond reach. Broken hearts defy time's healing, leaving only eternal gazing.","opus":"Grief for a dead lover the singer sees in the clouds above the sea, refusing to heal or accept that time mends broken hearts, wishing only to follow them and be held fast in the wind's arms.","sonnet":"Grief for someone seen in the clouds above: the speaker looks up at a face without light, asks them to wait, knows broken hearts may not heal but that time supposedly does — 'ohne dich will ich nicht' (without you I don't want to exist) — while the wind carries their name over the sea, keeping the dead suspended in a wet embrace.","src":"haiku"},
"till-lindemann~entre-dos-tierras":{"haiku":"Paralysis between two worlds prevents action or breath, where the speaker refuses responsibility for another's inevitable fall.","opus":"A cover of Heroes del Silencio disowning blame for a self-serving person's downfall, one who sells out for power and is caught between two worlds, suffocating and refusing to take responsibility.","sonnet":"The speaker refuses all blame for watching someone fall — \"I'm not guilty for seeing you fall\" — while the chorus diagnoses the other person as caught between two worlds, suffocating all the air around them. (lang: es)","src":"haiku"},
"till-lindemann~ich-hasse-kinder":{"haiku":"Hatred and love oscillate toward children as embodiments of both annoyance and potential redemption, the speaker unable to reconcile desire and irritation.","opus":"A darkly comic confession, seated on a plane beside a screaming toddler, of hating children, before conceding he loves them, just so long as they're his own. Lindemann's provocateur humor mining a petty, universally unspeakable irritation.","sonnet":"A man on a plane seated next to a screaming child catalogues his irritation in detail — the indifferent parents, the relentless noise, the sense of divine punishment — before conceding that he doesn't actually hate children in general, only those who aren't his own. The premise is comic but the longing underneath is genuine.","src":"haiku"},
"till-lindemann~prostitution":{"haiku":"Male desire requires commercialized female bodies as structural necessity; society functions through transactional intimacy divorced from love.","opus":"A deliberately crude, ironic hymn raising a toast to prostitution, framing it as the ancient, inevitable release for male lust while noting the women offer bodies but never love.","sonnet":"A sardonic toast to the institution of prostitution, presented as an ancient, inevitable solution to male desire — \"without her the world would be misery\" — delivered with dark irony in mock-hymn form. (lang: de)","src":"haiku"},
"till-lindemann~sport-frei":{"haiku":"Athletic discipline yields diminishing returns; youth and sacrifice burn away leaving only regret and physical collapse.","opus":"A sardonic hymn to athletes who sacrifice everything for glory, only to end up injured, doped and broke. The medals can't be melted into money, and the years run away, revealing sport as devotion that leaves nothing behind.","sonnet":"A sardonic tribute to athletes that exposes the brutal reality behind elite sport — years of sacrifice, foreign hormones, overdeveloped hearts, blown joints, and the bitter irony that the gold medal can't be melted into a bar, leaving nothing but worn-out bodies and missed education.","src":"haiku"},
"tillie~beth-amphetamine":{"haiku":"Drug use and escape through chemical oblivion, running from reality through substances.","opus":"A theatrical rallying cry against being placated and preyed upon. Tillie conjures a vigilante avenger, summoned like a hotline, who arrives when men corner a woman and the world tells her to calm down. It weaponizes fury into empowerment fantasy, urging the swallowed rage of the disillusioned to erupt into action rather than tears.","sonnet":"A vigilante superhero of female rage — Beth Amphetamine is conjured as the antihero for anyone fed up with promises of gold and fed fluff, summoned via a hotline when three boys think they're about to get lucky. She is \"sickest bitch you've ever seen,\" trading tears for kerosene, arriving on command.","src":"haiku"},
"tillie~hell-yeah":{"haiku":"The song celebrates bare survival as victory. Making it through a day without collapse is triumph; the world burns, but finding joy in not dying matters.","opus":"A survivor's grim little toast to simply enduring another day without weeping or dying, the modest triumph of sunlight on the face pitted against mornings that feel apocalyptic. The gallows humor barely conceals the toll. Tillie work in a confessional, emo-adjacent register where mental illness is named outright, turning the bare minimum of staying alive into something defiantly, achingly worth celebrating.","sonnet":"Survival as the lowest meaningful bar — making it through the day without crying or dying counts as a win. A gray-skied, drugstore-walk kind of life where sleep is refuge and celebration is reserved for simply not falling apart.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~binary-pulse":{"haiku":"The soul engineered into existence crashes against the void it can't fill—built for sensation but starved of meaning, forever searching for wholeness that the design forbade.","opus":"A manufactured being, made to feel yet unable to feel itself, pleads for healing from the one it was built to serve. A meditation on emptiness dressed as a factory-line soul.","sonnet":"A song about spiritual and emotional emptiness in a manufactured existence — the speaker was built to feel but has lost the ability, and desperately begs for something or someone to heal the hollow void inside them before they fade entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~black-water":{"haiku":"Addiction to poison courses through veins while the speaker chokes on its toxic flow; surrender feels inevitable.","opus":"Addiction rendered as drowning in black water the speaker can't stop pouring down his own throat. Praying on his knees to a peace he doesn't believe exists, he keeps diving back into the poison, the equation of his life left unsolved.","sonnet":"A person addicted to something destructive — black water is the poison they keep diving into despite knowing it's killing them, unable to breathe or see, praying to nothing, and concluding there is no solution to an equation that was never complete.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~elusive-reasons":{"opus":"A meditation on spiritual isolation, arguing that everyone carries an unexplainable emptiness from birth and is bound to grow separate from one another for causes beyond understanding. Beneath the alienation runs a quiet hope for redemption, a longing to escape toward some faint promise of light.","sonnet":"An existential reckoning with the inexplicable human condition: we are born incomplete, destined to drift apart from each other, and the narrator searches for meaning in disconnected nights and a fragile hope that the path we leave behind matters.","src":"sonnet"},
"time-the-valuator~glow":{"haiku":"Spiritual salvation and complementary redemption emerge through surrender to divine grace after accepting one's irredeemable nature.","opus":"A djent-inflected reach toward salvation through another person. The narrator, feeling incomplete and self-destructive, offers up an irredeemable life in exchange for grace, drawn toward a complementary light that bends distance and closes the gap between souls. Devotion here edges into surrender, the promise of becoming firelight once the moment finally arrives.","sonnet":"The speaker names themselves incomplete, astray, self-destructive, and offers what little life they have in exchange for another's grace — the \"complementary glow\" they see is a sign of difference that pulls them toward someone as a kind of salvation, with the closing promise that once the time is right they'll become firelight.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~how-fragile":{"haiku":"Meditation on the fragility of life, time, and existence—the narrator seeks to transcend fear and uncertainty through letting go.","opus":"A steadying meditation on facing uncertainty, coaxing someone to look beneath the surface, release their grip, and stop dreading what lies outside their control. Its imagery of thin glass and skin underscores the central question of how brief a moment and how breakable a life really is, offered not as despair but as reason to reach higher.","sonnet":"A meditation on letting go of anxiety and accepting fragility — the speaker is addressed as something as breakable as glass and cellophane, and urged to release control, drift with uncertainty, and find liberation in the unknown.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~in-control":{"haiku":"Enslaved by one's own mind and seeking liberation through connection with another. The speaker battles self-imposed paralysis and questions whether destiny can be changed.","opus":"A mind stuck fast like a trapped insect watches its days smear together, imprisoned by its own thoughts until an outsider's look tears down the walls and offers a path toward genuine living and agency. Time, The Valuator apply progressive metalcore's widescreen sweep to a plea for command over a spirit dulled by ruin from within.","sonnet":"A man paralysed inside his own mind encounters a stranger whose presence reconnects him to the living world. The sudden warmth opens the question of whether he can take back authorship of his own life, and by the end he decides he can — the self-destruction is over.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~ivy":{"haiku":"Anxious drowning and internal combustion propel the speaker toward acceptance of inevitable fire; shadows lurk while determination crystallizes into readiness for inevitable transformation.","opus":"A lost soul weighed down by anxiety and its own history, feeling like it's suffocating and about to burn, yet defiantly resolving to survive and turn the fire back on the hell surrounding it.","sonnet":"A lost soul anticipates the choking ash of yesterday while carrying anxiety like heavy stones, but turns toward resistance — \"I won't burn today\" and \"I'm here to burn this hell\" pivot the imagery of suffocation into refusal and readiness.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~lovely-feat-charlotte-buchholz":{"haiku":"A lonely person with emotional distance feels trapped and seeks escape, hoping to someday break free but finding isolation feels paradoxically welcoming.","opus":"Built around Billie Eilish's Lovely, this reworking sits in the trap of depression as a place you can't leave, isolation reframed with bitter irony as a home. The plea to make it out is undercut by the sense that nowhere is near enough to hide.","sonnet":"Total isolation that has hardened into something permanent — a heart of glass, a mind of stone, a body being torn apart — with the bitter irony of being welcomed home to a place that is simply the self, alone and unable to escape.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~onryo":{"haiku":"Humanity has greedily shaped a corrupt world beyond recovery. Contamination spreads through air and water while people hide in steel cities unable to escape.","opus":"A furious accusation leveled at mankind through the voice of an avenging ghost from folklore, blaming human appetite for corrupting a planet that was never ours to claim and fouling its air and water. It wonders why we dread self-examination, casting environmental collapse and inner rot as a haunting of our own making.","sonnet":"An indictment of humanity spoken from the perspective of the earth or a nature spirit — watching humans consume and trap themselves in a steel world of their own making, too afraid of what they'd find if they looked honestly at what they've become.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~terminus":{"haiku":"Human destruction of earth and future generations stems from hidden lies we refuse to face; death approaches as consequence.","opus":"An urgent climate lament that confesses humanity as both casualty and cause, hiding from its own falsehoods as the planet buckles and only a handful of years remain to salvage anything. It asks what future the coming generations will inherit from the ruin we built, then ends as a penitent wish to be taken back down into the earth itself.","sonnet":"An environmental and existential reckoning — humanity has poisoned its own future, the speaker names themselves as both victim and perpetrator, and the song ends with a plea to be returned to the earth, to the source, as the only possible honest ending.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~the-violent-sound":{"haiku":"Contempt for corrupt leadership that exploits people's vulnerability while soldiers and citizens suffer, calling out leaders for hypocrisy and failure.","opus":"A blazing refusal to die for a ruler's deceit, calling out a false shepherd who misleads his flock and grows rich while the poor perish on the front line. Smoke chokes the singer's chest as an uprising churns in his spirit, hunting for meaning amid conflict. Time, The Valuator's intricate progressive metalcore carries this anti-authority rage, the title standing for the hollow bluster of the powerful.","sonnet":"A speaker who breathes ash from someone else's riot refuses to die for a cause that was never theirs. The target is a leader who crowned himself on public weakness, hoards money, and sends others to the front while showing no spine — words as weapons, noise as violence.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~vibrant":{"haiku":"A person trapped in grayscale numbness rediscovers vitality and agency with each breath; they reject mindlessness and redefine themselves through embodied presence. The song traces return from disconnection to aliveness.","opus":"Post-hardcore rebirth from numbness, someone fading into gray concrete willing themselves back to vividness and self-definition. The arc is reclamation, breath by breath insisting on being alive rather than soulless and going through motions.","sonnet":"About pulling yourself out of depression and stagnation — surrounded by grey when the city is full of colour, fading into concrete — and reclaiming a sense of being alive, breathing, and no longer soulless.","src":"haiku"},
"time-the-valuator~when-i-meet-death":{"haiku":"Mortality dissolves regret and fear; the speaker faces the end with acceptance, ready to release into mystery while asking their loved one not to mourn but to remember them.","opus":"Facing death without fear, the speaker makes peace, insists he has no regrets, and comforts a grieving love that he will remain present. It is about acceptance at the threshold and consoling the ones left behind.","sonnet":"A calm, unafraid reckoning with death: the speaker wonders whether dying is like an infinite loop of memories spinning to nothingness, then addresses a loved one directly — don't mourn me, I'll always be there — and asks death to embrace him, having made his peace and settled his amends.","src":"haiku"},
"tindersticks~seaweed":{"haiku":"A narrator offers choices—beach stones, contrived looks, simple greetings—wrestling with indecision and wondering what's honest versus performed in love.","opus":"Stuart Staples turning a courtship into a meditation on sincerity, weighing the contrived gesture against the honest one. The chosen stone becomes a question of whether love should be curated or simply, riskily, felt and followed.","sonnet":"A quiet meditation on the difference between chosen and contrived love — the narrator contemplates whether to offer a found stone or a purchased one, a real look or a performed one, landing on total openness: no plans, just going wherever you go.","src":"haiku"},
"tism~he-ll-never-be-an-ol-man-river":{"haiku":"Substance addiction and fatal seduction destroy youth; the speaker traces obsession with drugs and destructive influence.","opus":"A savage satire from the Melbourne art-punk provocateurs skewering culture's romance with the doomed celebrity death. Cataloguing rock and screen casualties, the narrator ghoulishly consumes their overdoses and crashes as if collecting trophies, exposing how fame turns self-destruction into heroic myth. The boredom-driven hunt for the next corpse is the grim joke and the indictment.","sonnet":"Celebrity death as aspirational lifestyle — the narrator envies the deaths of River Phoenix, Bon Scott, Hendrix, Lynyrd Skynyrd, Mama Cass, and others not as tragedy but as frontier-pushing acts of excess, and grows bored waiting for the next one (predicting Jackson). It's pitch-black Australian absurdist satire on a culture that glamorises self-destruction and consumes it for entertainment.","src":"haiku"},
"title-fight~head-in-the-ceiling-fan":{"haiku":"Violence and disconnection dominate; the speaker's sensory world focuses on gore and predation. Meaning is absent; only raw sensation persists.","opus":"The Pennsylvania punk band's turn toward shoegaze murk surfaces in imagery of violence and blindness: a head in the fan, bullets missing, eyes glued to a face until sight fails. The bloodline that bleeds suggests an inheritance of hurt followed helplessly. Cyclical and claustrophobic, it trades the band's earlier melodic-hardcore urgency for something numbed and disoriented, the horror stated flatly rather than screamed.","sonnet":"A compressed, hallucinatory portrait of fixation — eyes locked onto someone, mind dissociated, images of blood and spinning blades bleeding into each other. The refrain doubles back without resolution, leaving only the image of a head caught in motion.","src":"haiku"},
"title-fight~leaf":{"haiku":"Isolation calcifies into routine while articulation remains impossible, the speaker drifting passively as fears accumulate without voice or escape.","opus":"Depressive inertia laid bare, staying in bed with the shades drawn, feeling dull and scared and weightless as a leaf on the wind. The quiet emo admission of being unable to name or move past the numbness.","sonnet":"A person in a depressive episode retreats from the world — pulling shades, sleeping through days, unable to articulate feelings — and arrives at the image of themselves as a single leaf in the wind: small, directionless, at the mercy of forces they cannot name or resist.","src":"haiku"},
"tk-from-ling-tosite-sigure~unravel":{"haiku":"World disintegrates while the speaker becomes transparent, unable to touch without contaminating. Loops repeat endlessly—movement without arrival, unraveling that won't stop.","opus":"From the perspective of someone dissolving into a distorted world, begging a loved one to remember him vividly rather than watch him vanish, terrified his own darkness will contaminate and destroy them both.","sonnet":"Someone fracturing under the weight of an identity they cannot locate — \"who is inside me?\" — watches the self turn transparent in a warped world and begs to be remembered in full colour even as they beg not to be found, because finding them would mean tainting the other person, and the two desires are irreconcilable.","src":"haiku"},
"tk-from~12th-laser":{"haiku":"Futuristic alienation and technological detachment; communication reduced to signals creates distance despite connection.","opus":"Toru Kitajima writing about the walled-off inner self, memories firing like lasers that expose the wounds he built himself around. The recurring hollow cavity figures a heart sealed against touch, longing to be seen yet certain that expressing himself only clouds everything over.","sonnet":"Someone emotionally sealed inside himself — feelings expressible only through art nobody was meant to see — finds that memory acts like a laser that keeps exposing what he tried to bury, and confesses he wants to project an entire world into someone else's heart even as his own words arrive distorted, delayed, and barely recognizable.","src":"haiku"},
"tk-from~fantastic-magic":{"haiku":"Illusion of magical escape and fantasy as preferable to harsh reality; losing oneself in dreams rather than confronting truth.","opus":"A dizzy, wordplay-driven meditation on memory and imagination as a kind of vanishing act, where color returns only once everything dries out. TK builds it from fantasy and delusion, the fake tremolo of feelings that flicker in and out of being.","sonnet":"When everything inside you has dried completely hollow, paradoxically all colors become visible — a post-numbness clarity — and a shared past with someone plays back in slow motion while fantasies and stolen imaginings accumulate between the two of them like a trick neither can fully explain or take back.","src":"haiku"},
"tk-from~unravel":{"haiku":"A declaration of ego-death and internal dissection—asking who is inside this broken body, unable to move, frozen in fear.","opus":"The theme to Tokyo Ghoul, sung from a self coming apart between two worlds, terrified of hurting someone while losing his own outline. It begs to be remembered as he was, vivid, before the fraying finishes and the person he was disappears.","sonnet":"A person coming apart because someone they love is still smiling in the distorted world while they themselves have become transparent and untouchable — they beg not to be found, not to be looked at, to be remembered vividly as they were before they started unraveling. The emotional core is the terror of dissolution: watching yourself become invisible while the other person remains real.","src":"haiku"},
"tnl-vzn~persephone":{"haiku":"Power dynamics and sadomasochistic pleasure blur as the speaker submits to pain delivered by the one they desire, finding transcendence in calculated suffering.","opus":"A consuming, submissive love cast through the underworld myth, the lover kept below and kept bleeding by someone who wants only to watch him plead. Devotion and harm blur until wanting the person is inseparable from being wounded by them.","sonnet":"A masochistic devotion to a figure named Persephone who bites skin and watches the speaker bleed and plead: the speaker craves her closeness even knowing she wanted nothing from them except to watch them suffer, the mythic name lending the power imbalance a fatalistic inevitability.","src":"haiku"},
"tnl-vzn~scratch":{"haiku":"Manipulation and emotional withholding create codependency where the speaker remains captive to another's intermittent affection.","opus":"Being kept as a lover's secret, useful only in the dark, resenting how they reappear and disappear at will to keep a spark alive while the singer stays pinned and unable to have them fully.","sonnet":"A push-pull dynamic traps the speaker: the other person vanishes and reappears, keeping the spark alive only through calculated physical intimacy — \"you scratch my back to keep the spark\" — but refuses full presence or genuine commitment.","src":"haiku"},
"to-kill-achilles~21-36":{"haiku":"The speaker envisions a peaceful afterlife as a beach with loved ones and contemplates suicide at 21:36, feeling pulled under by a harsh world despite others' platitudes.","opus":"To Kill Achilles, a Scottish post-hardcore band known for openly confronting mental health, deliver this at a near-whisper. The narrator finds solace imagining an afterlife not as gilded gates but as a familiar shore where the people he's lost are waiting, and admits he's tempted to join them because life keeps dragging him down. A precise timestamp marks a private, terrible decision.","sonnet":"Standing at a train platform at 21:36, close to the edge in every sense — the speaker has a vision of heaven as a hometown beach where lost loved ones wait, and is actively tempted to go there early. The platitudes of well-meaning people land hollow; what the speaker wants is not to be talked out of the feeling but simply to say out loud that this is how things are.","src":"haiku"},
"to-kill-achilles~no-love-is-a-crime":{"haiku":"Defying family judgment against queer love and chosen identity, refusing to live small for others' comfort while holding onto what's real.","opus":"A defiant reckoning with a family that refuses to accept who the narrator loves. Rejecting the notion that blood ties outweigh chosen bonds, they refuse to hide or shrink, describing the fear of being caught with someone dear. It is a queer coming-of-age stand: love found where it grows, insisted upon as no one's crime.","sonnet":"A direct confrontation with a family member whose homophobia forces the narrator to run whenever they're seen with someone they love — the blood of biological family is weighed against the blood of genuine support and found wanting. \"No love is a crime\" is both the title and the thesis, delivered as a final statement after years of holding on too long.","src":"haiku"},
"to-kill-achilles~rats":{"haiku":"Materialism corrupts identity into performance; the speaker rejects society's demand that worth derives from logos and consumption, reclaiming selfhood beyond price tags.","opus":"A rejection of worth measured by clothes, brands and bank balance, refusing the performance of status while admitting how heavy the pressure to conform sits. Its real target is a culture that treats people as things to consume by appearance.","sonnet":"A rejection of status culture — logos, bank size, the pressure to perform wealth in every room — in favour of people worth more than any penny, insisting that judgement should lie in whether you give or take, and refusing any longer to fit in.","src":"haiku"},
"to-kill-achilles~venom":{"haiku":"Existential fear of death rooted in witnessing a parent's death; the speaker questions whether facing mortality head-on is preferable to living in terror.","opus":"Scottish post-hardcore band To Kill Achilles offer an unflinching confession of thanatophobia, a narrator naming himself weak in body and mind and terrified not of heaven or hell but of simply not being here. Rooted in watching his father die before his eyes, the fear feels rational, tightening as he ages. The closing image weighs living in dread of the snakebite against accepting it, wondering whether facing death might finally end the terror.","sonnet":"A man unpacks his lifelong terror of death, tracing it to watching his father die in childhood — a wound that has never healed and now saturates every waking moment. The final question he poses is whether it is better to live in endless fear or to face mortality directly and be free of it.","src":"haiku"},
"to-the-grave~the-ghost-of-you":{"opus":"A grief-and-guilt-soaked lament (interpolating My Chemical Romance) for a woman who died in the narrator's arms; he's tormented by her memory, the things left unsaid, and the ghost of her that will never let him go or come home.","sonnet":"A song adapted from My Chemical Romance's 'The Ghost of You' — about the haunting presence of someone who died, the inability to forget them, and the grief of holding someone in your arms as they die. The emotional core is survivor's guilt and the way loss replays itself without resolution.","src":"sonnet"},
"to-whom-it-may~bypass":{"haiku":"Two people try to shortcut genuine connection and growth, avoiding vulnerability and real change by performing who they already are.","opus":"Post-hardcore band To Whom It May interrogate authenticity, asking whether an identity assembled through pretense from the very beginning can ever be genuinely known. Breaking someone down and rebuilding them becomes a test of whether they can withstand being confronted with what they truly want, circling the fear that the self has become a performance carried too far to undo.","sonnet":"An identity built on shortcuts and evasion reaches a crisis point — the path taken has bypassed the formation of any real self, and now there is no turning back to undo it. The confrontation with that bypassed authenticity is framed as both an indictment and an unanswerable question.","src":"haiku"},
"to-whom-it-may~calculate":{"haiku":"A person fights against erosion of their self while refusing to abandon someone they care for. They remain vigilant as the other person slips away.","opus":"Addressed to someone slowly breaking down under an impersonal system that manipulates and exposes people, the narrator asks whether they corrode and keep enduring the same way. Underneath is a plea to a struggling partner who is letting go, with the singer promising to wait and refusing to release them.","sonnet":"About being trapped in a slow collapse while watching someone else do the same — the narrator is barely holding on and refuses to let go of the other person even as both of them corrode from the inside out. The system metaphor frames it as an inescapable mutual deterioration.","src":"haiku"},
"to-whom-it-may~descend":{"haiku":"Someone sinks deeper into crisis while another watches without helping; the speaker demands the observer stop turning away and let the tragedy consume their awareness.","opus":"Sinking in mud up to his neck, the narrator begs a friend, one unwilling to get dirty, to summon the effort to pull him out. Post-hardcore act To Whom It May stage abandonment as literal drowning, a discarded halo becoming dead weight, the request for even pretended patience met only by the weakness he sees in the other person's eyes.","sonnet":"Someone sinking in metaphorical mud confronts a person who watched and did nothing — unwilling even to get their feet wet to help. The plea is not for rescue but for honest witness: stay and look at what your passivity has caused. Abandonment is the wound; complicity is the crime.","src":"haiku"},
"to-whom-it-may~firescape":{"haiku":"A speaker begs for someone to listen to difficult truth before they leave, but the other person keeps caving and abandoning them.","opus":"Desperate to clear his conscience, the narrator keeps insisting on his need to be heard while accusing a partner who buckled and left him shattered exactly as they'd been shattered. The post-hardcore band builds on the anguish of not reaching someone, their excuses only hardening his certainty while they'd rather passively wait things out than answer him.","sonnet":"A person forces a confrontation with someone who collapsed under pressure and left them broken. Setting aside self-protection, they demand to be heard rather than excused. The repeated need to say it again and again reveals how the failure to be reached has become the defining injury.","src":"haiku"},
"to-whom-it-may~ghost":{"haiku":"The narrator confronts an internal phantom that haunts and disgusts them, waiting in shame while questioning their debts and redemption.","opus":"A wrestling match with a treacherous inner voice that soothes even as it corrodes the narrator's faith. He bows in silent atonement, sure he has settled his moral accounts, taking grim solace in a self he despises. To Whom It May stage this internal war through the swell and catharsis of melodic post-hardcore.","sonnet":"A ghost the speaker carries as a part of themselves — something that whispers that everything is alright while corrupting faith, something disgusting and intimate at once. The speaker has been silently bowing under the weight of wrongs they believe they've paid for, and the ghost still won't leave, still finds comfort in the shadow it casts across everything.","src":"haiku"},
"to-whom-it-may~my-martyr":{"haiku":"The song portrays a toxic attachment where the narrator cannot be touched or reached. Others try to manipulate and shake the subject's faith, but the narrator acknowledges their own heartlessness and complicity.","opus":"A venomous confrontation where the narrator brands himself an animal and watches a lover slip away, sinking back into everything they tried to escape. Repeated declarations of having had enough spiral toward a demand to burn it all down. To Whom It May trade in metalcore's push-pull of tenderness and violence, the martyr image casting a doomed love as sacrifice and self-immolation at once.","sonnet":"Watching someone slip back into the very thing they escaped — a faith or ideology the speaker sees as hollow — and being unable to stop it. Love and fury knotted together, the plea to wake up answered only by silence.","src":"haiku"},
"to-whom-it-may~quick":{"haiku":"A person races to escape before the past catches them; prying eyes hunt from the skyline and shadows while they urge their companion to move now.","opus":"Paranoia rendered as a panicked getaway: watchers close in, sensing fear, and the narrator screams for flight before buried history resurfaces and swallows the horizon. To Whom It May offer no promise of refuge, only the counsel to keep moving and shut out a world that refuses to leave you be.","sonnet":"A paranoid flight from unnamed pursuers who emerge from the past and shadow every step. The urgency is internal as much as external — the past given form hunts the present self, and the only survival strategy is to move fast and shut your eyes when the world outside becomes too much.","src":"haiku"},
"to-whom-it-may~sick-day":{"opus":"A nautical metaphor for a relationship going under, both partners sinking and in over their heads, the narrator accepting the changes with resigned 'I don't mind' while asking permission to pull the other under with him.","sonnet":"About a relationship or shared situation pulling both people underwater — using nautical imagery of a sinking ship and rhythmic battering, the song negotiates between accepting the going-under and finding a way to reach light, ultimately asking permission to bring the other person down with them.","src":"sonnet"},
"to-whom-it-may~sink":{"haiku":"Hope and growth wither without care; the world burns while people cling to pride instead of nurturing what matters.","opus":"A counterintuitive plea to give in and let vulnerability empty you out as an act of surrender, set against the error of stuffing our lives with prideful hopes and sacrificing what matters to protect a mere possibility. With everything ablaze, it urges letting buried feeling rise up rather than straining to hold a flame by force.","sonnet":"A paradoxical survival song — the speaker tells someone to sink into vulnerability rather than resist it, arguing that destroying the garden to protect the seed (self-suppression) is the real mistake, and that letting the world burn you is sometimes the only honest way through.","src":"haiku"},
"to-whom-it-may~take-what-i-can":{"opus":"A woman's transformation from prey to predator (sheep to wolf) in her hunger for more from life, paired with the narrator confronting a detached, godlike figure he'll tear the sky open to dethrone so he can seize what he wants.","sonnet":"A woman who has been passive and pleading finally turning predatory — she rips open the sky, topples whoever sat above her, and claims what she wants with the ferocity of someone who waited too long and is done waiting.","src":"sonnet"},
"to-whom-it-may~the-signal-fire":{"haiku":"Desperation to save someone in crisis; chasing a dimming light and resisting surrender. The speaker searches from the sky for signs of distress.","opus":"A rescue vow that curdles into refusal, the narrator waiting on a distressed lover's beacon and pledging to save them, then reversing course to snuff that fading glow instead. The post-hardcore dynamics track the tug-of-war between rescue and letting go, as a love shifts from something worth saving to something he lets die out.","sonnet":"A rescuer searching from above for someone who can't save themselves — looking for signal fires, chasing embers in the dark, vowing to reach them if the night can be survived. But the fire is dimming, and by the end the speaker refuses to let the other person use distress as leverage anymore, extinguishing the signal rather than being endlessly pulled toward it.","src":"haiku"},
"to-whom-it-may~weight-of-the-stone":{"haiku":"The speaker has lost purpose and accountability, carrying others backward and unable to find reason to return home.","opus":"A guilt-laden post-hardcore reckoning, the narrator confessing that his purpose drained away as meaning left a relationship. Owning the blame for opening up and then pushing too hard, he carries his partner backward and wavers between following his own path and returning, before finally choosing not to come back, the burden crushing.","sonnet":"Taking responsibility for pushing someone further than was right and then having to carry them back — accountability as a crushing weight that threatens to break apart everything the speaker thought was whole. The trail forward and the path home pull in opposite directions, and the final answer is that home is not where this ends: the speaker is not coming back.","src":"haiku"},
"toe~a-149onrf":{"opus":"Toe are a Japanese math-rock instrumental band whose rare vocal pieces arrive wrapped in intricate, restless rhythm, fitting for a song about the impossibility of being known. Everyone is consumed by their own mania, too short on time and sleep to understand another, communication failing to arrive as anxiety cycles back, the path ahead a disruption with nothing beyond it.","sonnet":"A song about the impossibility of being truly known or of knowing others: everyone is consumed by their own mania and doesn't have the time or sleep to understand someone else — communication fails to arrive, anxiety returns in cycles, and the path ahead is a disruption with nothing beyond it.","src":"sonnet"},
"toe~goodbye":{"haiku":"Alienation prevents understanding; withdrawal offers no refuge since solitude mirrors the fundamental loneliness of universal isolation.","opus":"The Japanese math-rock band on isolation and the futility of being truly understood, a narrator retreating into silence amid a world of self-absorbed people. The stray realization that the start is already the end gives it a quiet fatalism about connection.","sonnet":"Isolation and the impossibility of mutual understanding are circled in spare, halting language — the speaker stays inside, keeps silent, and accepts that the start is the end, while untranslated Japanese lines (\"it doesn't reach,\" \"what is the next anxiety?\") deepen the sense of things slipping just out of reach.","src":"haiku"},
"toe~the-latest-number":{"haiku":"Ambition splinters into repetition as the future refuses to arrive. Two voices sing their latest iteration into the void—sorrow becomes fuel, memory becomes bedrock for moving forward despite knowing nothing changes.","opus":"From the Tokyo math-rock instrumentalists, a rare vocal turn that treats memory as the newest track in a life's catalogue. Regret and sadness are sung gently, reframed as the material of a future being written. Amid talk of masks, mazes, and projection, a glimpsed rainbow marks the turn toward a tomorrow that feels, quietly, correct.","sonnet":"Against a backdrop of gilded nights and borrowed ideologies, the speaker casts their latest chapter as both painful regret and a new future — \"sorrowful memories are the right future\" — projecting hope onto a rainbow seen from within, cycling through grief until the projection itself becomes tomorrow.","src":"haiku"},
"tokio-hotel~1000-oceans":{"haiku":"Two lovers separated by impossible distance must cross oceans and endure endless darkness to reach each other and claim freedom without regrets.","opus":"A sweeping long-distance devotion, vowing to traverse vast seas and countless bleak years to reunite with a distant partner, carrying their image through a cold and lightless landscape. The German band frame it as unshakable faith in love surviving immense separation, perhaps even mortality itself.","sonnet":"About the absolute determination to find someone again after separation — traveling across frozen, endless darkness through a thousand oceans and a thousand years of time, the narrator holds their partner's picture and promises they will reach each other and be free.","src":"haiku"},
"tokio-hotel~automatic":{"haiku":"A lover functions as pure mechanism without authentic feeling, their words hollow triggers that sustain obsession despite the certainty of emotional absence.","opus":"Loving someone incapable of real feeling, every affectionate word running on autopilot and every reassurance a lie, the singer trapped in the humiliating question of why he keeps loving a machine.","sonnet":"A speaker recognises that their partner's affection is mechanical and hollow — every word, gesture, and reassurance is programmed rather than felt — and questions why they keep loving someone incapable of genuine emotion.","src":"haiku"},
"tokio-hotel~darkside-of-the-sun":{"haiku":"Critiques media hysteria and surveillance controlling consciousness, with future already predetermined. Resistance offers only the hope of standing together through darkness.","opus":"From the German band's English-language phase, a defiant survival anthem pitched against media frenzy and surveillance, its narrator insisting that even with ruin looming he and his love remain unbroken and that their darkness will one day turn to light. Teenage rebellion meets a big, glossy chorus engineered to fill arenas.","sonnet":"A media-saturated, surveilled world where the future arrives already controlled — and two people choosing to stand in it together anyway. The dark side of the sun becomes a refuge from radio hysteria, a place where surviving beside someone matters more than whatever the broadcast says is coming.","src":"haiku"},
"tokio-hotel~durch-den-monsun-radio-mix":{"haiku":"Someone endures storms and overwhelming obstacles to reach their separated lover, believing that through enough suffering they will eventually reunite.","opus":"A yearning vow to fight through a monsoon, storm, and the edge of an abyss to reach a distant beloved; the deeper read is that love is worth enduring any hardship—when he can't go on, the belief that they'll one day walk together through the storm sustains him and everything will be okay.","sonnet":"A German song about fighting through any storm or obstacle to reach someone you love — the narrator is sealed in a dark room waiting, but promises to battle through monsoons and walk the edge of the abyss because someday they will run together and everything will be fine.","src":"haiku"},
"tokio-hotel~hurricanes-and-suns":{"haiku":"Two lovers endure turbulent extremes together—pain and joy mixed—choosing to face the end of time with each other rather than alone.","opus":"A German band's arena-sized ode to a bond that weathers extremes, framing a couple's turmoil as hurricanes and suns and urging they walk smiling toward the end, following a shared halo till time runs out.","sonnet":"About a relationship that has outlasted its initial sweetness, weathering pain and melancholy together — the narrator insists they're something unique beyond ordinary love games, and asks to walk into the end smiling, following the halo toward whatever comes.","src":"haiku"},
"tokio-hotel~hurricanes-and-suns-new-track-2009":{"haiku":"Weathering chaos and suffering together, finding meaning in shared pain rather than seeking escape.","opus":"Two people bound through chaos and pain, choosing to walk into the end together with a smile, the halo a fragile emblem of transcendence held up against a melancholic, doomed devotion.","sonnet":"A declaration that a love relationship, full of lies and turbulence, is something the two people face together anyway — they acknowledge the pain coming at them, name it hurricanes and suns, and choose to walk into it smiling.","src":"haiku"},
"tokio-hotel~m-dchen-aus-dem-all-2003-bonustrack":{"haiku":"An alien girl offers escape from Earth's systems; together they fly through space without rules, fantasy replacing reality.","opus":"A lonely teenager writes letters to a girl from outer space, who answers with a star and an invitation to fly through galaxies to Venus and Mars, leaving every earthly system behind. This early German-language bonus track catches Tokio Hotel at their adolescent origins, the sci-fi escape fantasy and pure imagination speaking straight to the young fanbase Bill Kaulitz was forming.","sonnet":"A teenager reaches across impossible distance to a girl who exists somewhere beyond the galaxy, exchanging letters for starlight. Their imagined escape — supersonic, weightless, Venus-bound — is less about romance than about fleeing everything terrestrial for a universe made only of freedom and shared fantasy.","src":"haiku"},
"tokio-hotel~monsoon":{"haiku":"Dreams of escaping through a violent storm to reach a lost lover, determined to overcome all obstacles. The song frames their reunion as redemption amidst chaos and darkness.","opus":"A song about running through a storm toward a distant loved one, using the monsoon as an image of hardship braved for the sake of reunion. From a cold, empty room the narrator vows to fight the elements and never be held back until they're together.","sonnet":"Someone pushes through isolation and stormy conditions driven by the thought of a person they need to reach — the monsoon serves as both literal and emotional obstacle, and the determination to reunite with that person is the only force strong enough to keep them moving.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song sells itself as a lover's chase, but its real subject is motion that never resolves — every verse begins in a dead, cold room and every chorus flees it, so the storm is less an obstacle than the only place the singer can locate the beloved at all. The you is never present, only summoned: a face glimpsed in fading moonlight, a name half-heard, a destination phrased as somewhere new rather than anywhere real. That vagueness is the point. Sung by the teenage Bill Kaulitz in an English recasting of his own German original, this is adolescent yearning at its purest — desire that needs the darkness, the distance, and the rain precisely because the arrival it keeps promising would end the running that is the only thing it actually knows how to do.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","themes":["love & desire","freedom & escape"],"means":"desire that needs the darkness and distance because arrival would end the only motion it knows","built":"the you is never present, only summoned — a face, a name half-heard, a destination without a real location"},
"tokio-hotel~ready-set-go":{"haiku":"Young rebels run as the sky transforms and the world crashes, racing forward together rather than turning back.","opus":"German teen-pop-rock about breaking out and running toward freedom while everything familiar collapses, us against the world with a promise not to look back. Youthful defiance as pure forward motion.","sonnet":"Two young people breaking free from confinement and conformity together — a youth-anthem about escaping the walls built around you, running toward an open future side by side, too young to live a lie and determined not to turn back.","src":"haiku"},
"tokio-hotel~reden":{"haiku":"Two lovers isolate themselves in a hotel room dedicated to conversation and presence, rejecting the external world's demands to remain suspended in intimate dialogue.","opus":"A hotel-room seduction insisting, unconvincingly, that they only came to talk, the outside world locked out while two people lie together. Desire hiding behind the flimsy pretense of conversation.","sonnet":"Two people check into a hotel room with the pretence of only wanting to talk, but the talking itself becomes the intimacy — they lie side by side while the outside world calls and demands. The word \"reden\" (talk) becomes a kind of incantation for the closeness they actually want.","src":"haiku"},
"tokio-hotel~schrei-2006":{"haiku":"Young people must learn to break free from conformity and manipulation by authority figures, screaming their authentic identity despite social pressure.","opus":"A youth-rebellion anthem against being told where to go and what to think, urged instead to scream and be yourself even if it hurts. It warns of manipulative pied-pipers who promise your dreams then trap you, insisting now is the time to show who you really are.","sonnet":"An anthem against conformity and manipulation: people are told where to go and what to think from the moment they wake up, and the song is a raw demand to scream your own identity before it's too late and the pied pipers have taken everything.","src":"haiku"},
"tokio-hotel~scream":{"haiku":"The speaker urges resistance against systemic control and expectation by screaming one's truth into existence as an act of defiance and self-reclamation.","opus":"A rebellion anthem against a life dictated by others who tell you where to go and what to do while nobody asks what you want; screaming until it hurts becomes the only way to feel real and reclaim the fact that you are not just anyone.","sonnet":"A furious rejection of being told what to do and who to be — the song urges you to fight back against systems and people that control and exploit you by expressing yourself at full volume, even if it hurts.","src":"haiku"},
"tokyo-rose~dark-knights":{"haiku":"The song depicts late-night hedonism and drug use among a crew cruising through the city, positioning themselves as envied figures while warning others to stay out of their way.","opus":"A hedonistic after-dark power fantasy of gliding above a glittering skyline, indulging in drink and drugs as though tomorrow won't arrive, all swagger and thinly veiled menace toward anyone in the way. It packages recklessness as glamour. The synthwave production, drenched in neon retro cool, is tailor-made for this glossy night-driving mythology.","sonnet":"A crew cruising hillside roads above city lights, pouring wine and snorting through the night with full confidence that no one in the room can touch them. Glamour, menace, and pure nocturnal momentum — an invitation to ride along or step aside.","src":"haiku"},
"tokyo-rose~dark-sun":{"haiku":"Searching for authenticity beneath concealment; yearning to know what hides in darkness and ambiguity.","opus":"A yearning electronic plea aimed at someone withdrawn into shadow. The narrator keeps asking why the loved one hides where the sun goes dark, undefined and unreachable, longing to locate them. Countervoices of starlight and eyes suggest the pull between concealment and being truly seen, the whole thing circling like a search for a person slipping away.","sonnet":"A person is hiding — from the speaker, from themselves — somewhere the sun is dark, undefined and unreachable, and the only thing glimpsed is their eyes in the starlight: the question repeats without answer.","src":"haiku"},
"tokyo-shoegazer~bright":{"haiku":"Shoegazer atmosphere obscuring clarity; brightness mixed with noise and distortion.","opus":"Two people closing their eyes on a frightening reality, one bleaching to white and the other to black, clinging to the faintest hope with only each other in view. Shoegaze blur made literal, the world whited out until a single person remains.","sonnet":"Two people separately closing their eyes against the real world — one sees white, one sees black — losing each other's whispers in long silence, yet each fixing on the other as the only visible point in a world gone entirely colorless, relying on the thinnest sliver of hope.","src":"haiku"},
"tokyo-shoegazer~constellations":{"haiku":"Romantic devotion and hopeful pursuit—someone seeks light and connection through twilight, willing to follow another anywhere; constellations guide them toward union and belonging.","opus":"A night-drive song about escaping regret and old ghosts toward some promised sunrise, chasing intimacy and belonging under moonlight. The insistence that the other doesn't have to try softens the restlessness into wanting to simply be chosen.","sonnet":"A late-night drive song about wanting to keep someone close: the narrator chooses to stay near a woman even as the night stretches on and flashbacks from old friends intrude, finding direction from stars and hoping the person will simply be his — the emotional register is yearning rather than certain.","src":"haiku"},
"tom-morello~let-s-get-the-party-started-feat-bring-me-the-horizon":{"haiku":"Someone spirals through mental breakdown, internal voice loops, and self-destructive escapism while refusing to face their demons.","opus":"Tom Morello passes the mic to Bring Me The Horizon's Oli Sykes for a frenzied breakdown disguised as revelry, a narrator sinking under and unbothered by whether he's living or gone. Wrecked and unraveling, he stages a bash purely to dodge his own demons. The hook about being thrilled to the point of death, spiked with cries to be finished off, turns the celebration into gallows-humored ruin.","sonnet":"Someone in psychological freefall uses the performance of hedonism as a shield against confronting the suffering underneath — the party is not celebration but avoidance. The ironic happiness at the end crystallizes the dissociation between outward behavior and inner collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"tom-petty~i-won-t-back-down":{"haiku":"Principled resistance and unwavering conviction hold ground against overwhelming pressure; the speaker stands firm in the face of the world's force, choosing integrity over safety.","opus":"A stubborn refusal to be beaten down or turned around, standing your ground even before hell's own gates. Its simplicity is the point: one life, one vow, no surrender.","sonnet":"A plain declaration of immovable resolve: standing at the gates of hell, pushed around by the world, the speaker simply will not yield — no easy way out, no turning around, just ground held and will maintained.","src":"haiku"},
"tom-waits~ol-55":{"haiku":"Early morning departure and the thrill of escape blend with melancholy; the narrator speeds away wishing they'd stayed longer, caught between two desires.","opus":"An early, sweet Waits sketch of driving home at dawn after a night with a lover, feeling holy and alive yet already wishing he'd stayed longer. Fleeting morning happiness shadowed by its own passing.","sonnet":"Leaving a lover's place at dawn in his old car, the speaker is flooded with gratitude and longing — the night was brief, the road is bright, and the stronger the feeling of aliveness grows, the more he wishes he had stayed.","src":"haiku"},
"tomahawk~capt-midnight":{"haiku":"The song rejects gratitude and breaks covenant. The narrator gave their world to someone but is now sick of wasting time; they want to take what they gave and escape.","opus":"A jagged declaration of a man refusing to keep giving everything away, done wasting time and sick of stinking lies, demanding to be taken away and to finally claim what is his. Defiance flares against a lover or world he once served completely. Tomahawk, the experimental rock supergroup fronted by Mike Patton, filter this bitterness through their angular, unpredictable sound, all snarl and sudden pivots rather than straight catharsis.","sonnet":"A rupture with the past: the speaker has been given everything and is done waiting, done with the lies, demanding to be taken somewhere else. The tension between exhaustion and adrenaline runs through it — half severance, half survival anthem.","src":"haiku"},
"tommy-trash-i-o-daisy-guttridge~oxygen-feat-daisy-guttridge":{"opus":"Desire framed as a bodily necessity, one more hit of a person needed the way breath is needed. The chemical rush and the surrender arrive in the same instant.","sonnet":"A person is so completely dependent on someone else that their presence feels as necessary as oxygen — one taste of them and all self-control dissolves, described as a chemical reaction that hijacks the body.","src":"sonnet"},
"tommy-trash~oxygen-feat-daisy-guttridge":{"haiku":"Dependency framed as addiction, needing someone with the urgency of a breath.","opus":"Craving reduced to its most basic terms, another person needed like breath and chemistry likened to a drug that dissolves self-control, the whole thing built around surrender to a compulsion.","sonnet":"Desire framed as physical dependency — needing someone like oxygen, giving in to one more taste, feeling chemicals take hold and control slip away. The lyric offers nothing beyond that loop of need and surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"tony-allen-hugh-masekela~we-ve-landed-cool-cats-mix":{"haiku":"Cultural continuity and artistic choice transcend generational pressure; traditions of work and craft merge with individual expression and communal celebration.","opus":"Two African music elders reflecting on being raised to work and choosing music as their life's craft, celebrating culture as a beauty to behold. Afrobeat and jazz veterans turning autobiography into a warm generational tribute.","sonnet":"A reflection on growing up with the ethic of honest work — farming, hunting, fishing, or music — woven through a Yoruba refrain that celebrates craft done with the head and heart, concluding with a joyful affirmation of cultural beauty.","src":"haiku"},
"too-close-to-touch~pick-me-up":{"opus":"An addict's plea and indictment, doubting whether a fair-weather crowd would rescue him once his fame and usefulness are gone, drowning his anxiety in whiskey while everyone around him pursues their own agenda.","sonnet":"About being used as an emotional crutch by someone who disappears when they no longer need you — the narrator realizes they were only called on in low moments, not valued as a person, and that everyone in their world has ulterior agendas.","src":"sonnet"},
"too-late-for-roses~burning-low":{"haiku":"Energy diminishes and hope fades; the speaker confronts depletion and the approach of ending.","opus":"A metalcore portrait of an abusive relationship rendered as slow immolation. A broken man mistakes his manipulator for a savior, only to be molded and consumed, his last candle guttering as he is worn to the bone. The tragedy is the paralysis: unable to leave, wasting away until nothing remains but the choice to stay.","sonnet":"A relationship that consumed the narrator entirely — they came to it broken and were molded like clay, stripped to the bone, with vision fading to black and the candle of self burning ever lower. \"I try to shield my only candle but the flame is burning low\" is the image that holds it all: helpless protection of the last surviving part of themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"tool~10-000-days-wings-pt-2":{"haiku":"A saint's departure leaves the congregation orphaned: enlightenment granted to one becomes evidence of divine abandonment for those left behind to suffer.","opus":"An elegy for the songwriter's mother, who kept unwavering faith through long years of illness while the congregation offered only hollow sympathy. It sends her home in triumph, daring her maker to deny a life that never lived a lie.","sonnet":"A song addressed to the singer's mother Judith, who spent nearly 28 years as a devout Christian despite a debilitating illness — he dismisses the congregation's hollow sympathy and challenges her directly to confront God and demand her wings, declaring her the true light that illuminated his life.","src":"haiku","fable":"Keenan's elegy for his mother, Judith Marie, who was paralyzed by an aneurysm and spent roughly twenty-seven years incapacitated before dying: the title counts those days as a term served in fire. The song builds by turning her faith community into an accusation. The first movement mocks the congregation as tourists of suffering who narrate heroism they never endured, hypocrites who can't match a woman who actually lived what they only recite. Then it pivots to her private vindication, the son who spent his life as a scornful non-believer conceding that she alone earned the ascension the others merely perform, and picking up the small inherited flame to light her exit rather than his own belief. The devastating close is a permission and a dare rolled together: he sends her to confront her maker face to face, certain that if any life ever justified the reward, hers did. The refusal to soften into ordinary faith is the point, love proven precisely by a skeptic who still demands her wings.","fv":2,"fnote":"The same woman anchors three reads in this library — A Perfect Circle's Judith rages at her faith, jimmy freezes at the moment the aneurysm struck the eleven-year-old's world, and this one grants the wings: Keenan's mother, read at three distances.","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","family & blood"],"means":"a skeptic's love demanding the reward his faith never endorsed","built":"the title counts 27 years of paralysis as a term served in fire"},
"tool~4":{"haiku":"A person encourages another to access hidden depths within themselves and surrender to intimate transformation.","opus":"Titled Forty Six & 2, Maynard translating Jungian and evolutionary ideas into a rite of self-excavation, digging through the shadow self toward a higher form. It's about breaking yourself open, embracing the buried and painful to become something more.","sonnet":"A sexually charged song about unlocking suppressed desire — encouraging a partner to let go of inhibitions, go deeper, take everything in, with the heat rising degree by degree until full surrender.","src":"haiku","fable":"Early Tool at its most nakedly physical: the title's degree sign is the whole cipher, spelled out at the bridge as body heat climbing four degrees, the clinical measure of arousal cresting toward release. It's a seduction voiced as a monologue of persuasion, the speaker coaxing a lover past their own inhibition and reticence, promising a route around what they won't let themselves want. What makes it unmistakably Tool is that the coaxing curdles: appetite is staged as consumption and surrender, the language of eating and taking-all-in blurring tenderness into dominance, so the promised closeness arrives sounding less like affection than like being devoured. The claim that this beats dying and disease is the come-on's overreach, sex as the one thing more intense than death, and the song leaves you unsure whether that's ecstasy or a predator's flattery.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"seduction curdles as tenderness blurs into consumption","built":"the degree sign in the title names the clinical measure of arousal at the bridge"},
"tool~7empest":{"haiku":"A destructive storm hides beneath calm deception; warning that natural chaos will break through regardless of false serenity.","opus":"A scorching indictment of a manipulator whose false composure precedes the wreckage, the narrator flatly rejecting the staged calm and insisting a storm cannot deny its own destructive character, the harm already beyond undoing. It works as both personal betrayal and a caution against denial. Tool, the LA progressive-metal band, close their long-delayed 2019 album with this sprawling, shape-shifting epic of measured rage.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a person — or force — that disguises its destructive nature behind a false calm. The speaker sees through the performance of serenity, tracking the pattern: the misleading quiet, the inevitable eruption, the fake surprise when the deception is exposed. A tempest cannot help being what it is, and no amount of apology will undo the damage.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tool's 15-minute closer on Fear Inoculum spells its own thesis in its title: this is a tempest, and a tempest can only be what it is. The first two verses address a serial liar mid-performance, cataloguing the false stillness of someone who fakes serenity before wreaking the same havoc they always do, a call-out that reads equally as personal grudge and as weather-report on manipulators who feign innocence the moment their lie is exposed. The bridge turns accusation into forecast, and the near-catatonic repetition of the closing mantra -- that a storm is only ever obligated to be a storm -- enacts the trap it names: destruction is fated because destroying is its nature, no atonement possible. Read biographically, that storm doubles as the band's own turbulent temperament and Keenan's sardonic acceptance that some cycles, whether toxic people or their own creative volatility, cannot be reasoned out of being themselves.","fv":2,"fnote":"It won the band their third Grammy — Best Metal Performance, 2020, after Ænema and Schism — the storm formally decorated.","themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"a storm indicted for being only itself","built":"the title spells tempest embedding thesis in spelling"},
"tool~bottom":{"haiku":"Someone compels themselves to survive self-imposed torture through hatred and disgust; they strip away shame and fear to become an iron weapon of survival.","opus":"A grueling descent to a psychological floor, where the speaker's compassion and resolve wear away and he sustains himself on the very venom, guilt, hatred, weakness, that his abuser feeds him. The bleak resolution is self-preservation: to outlast the other person he must first outlast himself, then confront and wipe them out, exposed but iron-willed.","sonnet":"About surviving an abusive relationship or person by finally turning the accumulated damage into fuel — the narrator has swallowed poison, been guilt- and hatred-fed, and hits absolute bottom before concluding that surviving the other person first means surviving oneself, using every scar as a weapon.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is Tool's early study in abjection and the psychology of surviving abuse by feeding on it: Keenan's speaker admits he metabolizes the poison given to him, that self-disgust and hatred and weakness are the very things keeping him upright at his lowest point. The structure enacts a turn. The sung verses are pure degradation, kneeling and burning and hollowed out, but Henry Rollins' spoken interlude flips the posture from victim to combatant, reframing self-destruction as the only route through the abuser and stripping the self down to something naked and past fear. The grim accounting-metaphor of the refrain insists debasement accumulates like sediment, so the bottom is not a place you fall to but the sum of everything that has settled there.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"surviving abjection by metabolizing the poison given to you","built":"Rollins' spoken interlude flips the posture from victim to combatant mid-track"},
"tool~cold-and-ugly":{"haiku":"Psychological walls protect through distance; fear calcifies into armour, leaving the speaker unreachable despite recognizing shared terror in others.","opus":"An early Tool sketch of a woman armored against feeling, walled off and terrified of being reached. What deepens it is the narrator admitting the same fear in himself, so the diagnosis of her coldness becomes a confession of shared dread.","sonnet":"Two people mirror each other's fear of feeling — she keeps a cold, ugly wall behind jewelry and words, trembling at the thought of intimacy; the speaker admits he is frightened too. The shared confession of mutual terror of emotional openness is the song's only resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"tool~cold-and-ugly-live":{"haiku":"Both people hide behind defensive walls, trapped by fear and distance from their own souls despite seeking connection.","opus":"A portrait of a woman so terrified of feeling that she's walled herself off, cold and ugly beneath her jewelry, keeping distance from her own soul. The twist is the narrator's admission that he's just as frightened, recognizing his own emotional armor mirrored in hers.","sonnet":"Both the woman observed and the narrator are frightened of genuine feeling — she hides behind jewelry and words, keeping everyone at distance, while he admits he is equally scared of the same depth she refuses to let anyone reach.","src":"haiku","fable":"Early, pre-mythic Tool at its most nakedly interpersonal: before the cosmic scale of later records, Maynard is just diagnosing a lover's armor, the guardedness he reads under her ornament and behind her eyes as a barrier keeping all feeling out. But the song is built to turn the accusation inward. The catalogue of her defenses runs twice, and each time the same verdict—that she's terrified—is followed by his admission that he is too, until the bridge quietly relocates the distance-keeping from her soul to his own. What opens as a portrait of someone else's coldness resolves as confession: the wall he's describing is the one he shares, and naming hers is how he circles his own fear of being reached. The live-only, heckler-tossing intro frames even that vulnerability defensively, a sneer guarding the softest thing the young band ever recorded.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","love & desire"],"means":"portrait of her coldness circles back as confession of his own","built":"the catalogue of her defences runs twice; each time his admission of equal fear follows"},
"tool~crawl-away":{"haiku":"Someone withdraws from a relationship when emotional needs become invisible; the narrator expresses violent resentment at being left while claiming indifference to the partner's departure.","opus":"Early Tool at its most bitter, a relationship dissolving as someone slips away and the narrator answers loss with cold, knife-edged resentment. The repeated declarations of love curdle into control, exposing how possessiveness masquerades as devotion.","sonnet":"A cold, possessive rage at someone trying to leave — the narrator tries to hold them down, seethes at being ignored, and frames the impulse to stab them in the back as what passes for love in this dynamic.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is early Tool (Undertow, 1993), and the read lives in the gap between what the song pretends to be and what it is. On the surface it tracks a partner slipping out of the singer's grip, the abuser's helpless-sounding account of losing someone who is escaping him. The chorus curdles that helplessness into contempt and a fantasy of retaliation the moment her back is turned, and the bridge is the real horror: possessiveness rebranded as devotion, the stranglehold offered as the proof of affection, so that the mantra of tenderness and the threat of violence become the same sentence. The song's structure is its argument -- it never develops or resolves because the abuser's logic doesn't, only repeats itself louder and more explicit until the tenderness has been fully unmasked as ownership.","fv":2,"themes":["surveillance & control","violence & murder","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"possessiveness rebranded as devotion in the same sentence","built":"the song refuses to develop or resolve because the abuser's logic only repeats louder"},
"tool~culling-voices":{"haiku":"The speaker's mind creates false accusations and judgments without evidence; paranoia and psychopathy drive them to condemn others endlessly.","opus":"A study of a mind turning on itself, where imagined slights and conversations that never happened harden into paranoia and self-appointed judgment. The narrator condemns without evidence, guided only by internal whispers he calls psychopathy, while repeatedly warning something not to be pointed at him. Tool built a career on exactly this kind of psychological excavation, and this cut extends their late-period patience with slow-burning inner dread.","sonnet":"A mind turning against itself — voices that were never spoken aloud grow loud enough to convict everyone around. The speaker recognizes the pattern as a form of psychopathy, a paranoid inner court that judges and banishes without evidence, and yet cannot fully silence the accusation when it swings back toward him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A late track on Tool's Fear Inoculum (2019), and its subject is paranoia examined from the inside—the way an anxious mind manufactures its own antagonists. The verses map the pathology precisely: fabricated arguments that never occurred yet steer every reaction, an internal tribunal that convicts and exiles people on nothing but the murmurs it generates itself. The diagnosis word arrives like an accusation the singer flings outward, and the whole song hinges on the irony beneath that gesture—the desperate refrain warning others not to aim the charge at him is exactly the self-indictment he can't say plainly, since the pathology misleading him is his own. The long instrumental erosion that closes the song lets the words dissolve into pure repetition, the mind stuck in its groove, the defense finally too worn down to finish its own sentence.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind"],"means":"self-manufactured paranoia flinging its own diagnosis outward","built":"instrumental erosion dissolves the defense before it finishes"},
"tool~descending":{"haiku":"Collective descent into lethargy and indifference, with urgent calls to wake up and resist the slow dying of society.","opus":"An urgent summons to rouse from shared indifference before humanity drifts into its own destruction, cast as a plunge through limitless folly of our own design where drifting is confused with soaring. It swells toward an alarm-call, a frantic rallying of all one's strength to survive and blunt the approaching finale. From Tool's 2019 Fear Inoculum, it pours the band's cerebral prog-metal grandeur into an ecological and existential warning.","sonnet":"Humanity in free fall — not heroic descent but negligent drift, sleepwalking through a dissolution of its own making. The song moves from diagnosis to desperate alarm call: rouse everyone from apathy, sound the reveille, mobilise every fibre, because falling is not the same as flying and the finale can still be stayed if anyone chooses to act.","src":"haiku","fable":"A late-career Tool warning about civilizational sleepwalking, staged as the very drift it fears: the long, unhurried opening literalizes a species dozing through its own decline, mistaking descent for grace and comfort for permanence. The turn is a military metaphor — a bugle at dawn, a call to arms — demanding the sleeper be shaken awake before apathy hardens into extinction, and the borrowed Hamlet question reframes ecological or moral collapse as a bare choice between rousing action and ceasing to exist. Fittingly, the band lets the music climb only after the plea lands, so the awakening it begs for is something the track performs rather than merely names.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","nature & the elements","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"a warning staged as the very drift it fears","built":"the music climbs only after the plea lands"},
"tool~die-eier-von-satan":{"haiku":"A German-language recipe for cannabis-laced cookies with no eggs; instructions are recited matter-of-factly. The song treats drug preparation as an ordinary household procedure.","opus":"Tool's notorious prank: a menacing spoken-word piece over industrial pounding that sounds like a fascist rally but is actually a German recipe for eggless cookies, a joke at the expense of anyone who fears a language they can't parse.","sonnet":"A German-language cookie recipe — half a cup of powdered sugar, Turkish hashish, butter, vanilla, flour, ground nuts, no eggs — delivered with the dramatic gravity of a political rally, the joke being that the only crime is a cannabis-laced biscuit.","src":"haiku","fable":"A German voice reads a cookie recipe in the flat, authoritative cadence of a health-camp instructor: powdered sugar, salt, a knife-tip of Turkish hashish, butter, no eggs. The joke is a menace built entirely from tone. Placed on an album obsessed with control and cults, the deadpan instruction to roll the dough into eyeball-sized balls and chant nonsense magic words turns baking into liturgy, the recipe into a set of orders you follow without knowing why. The title (Satan's Eggs) and the insistent refrain that there are none deliver the punchline of the whole piece: this is a satire of authority itself, the way a soothing procedural voice can walk you calmly toward anything at all, and you will comply because it never once raises its volume.","fv":2,"fnote":"The arrangement performs the experiment the text describes — the voice mounts over pounding industrial rhythm and a swelling crowd, a rally staged for a cookie recipe; most listeners hear the menace long before anyone tells them it's baking instructions.","themes":["politics & society","comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"a soothing procedural voice walking you calmly toward anything","built":"the menace is built entirely from tone, never volume"},
"tool~disgustipated":{"haiku":"A preacher experiences a vision where vegetable crops possess consciousness and experience harvest as genocide, leading to an ecstatic spiritual awakening about the necessity and tragedy of life consuming life.","opus":"A surreal parable opens with an angel revealing the screams of carrots facing their harvest holocaust, then hardens into a mantra that life feeds on life, that this consuming is necessary. A closing spoken drift through a blood-soaked ditch pushes the vertigo further. Tool close their debut Undertow with this sprawling, ritualistic piece, Maynard James Keenan mocking sanctimony and confronting the violence baked into simply staying alive.","sonnet":"A mock-sermon vision in which carrot harvest is reframed as holocaust, forcing the dreamer to confront that all life sustains itself by consuming other life. The chant that follows — this is necessary — is deadpan and relentless, offering no comfort, only the cold ecology of existence.","src":"haiku","fable":"A mock sermon, delivered in full televangelist cadence, in which a preacher receives a vision of screaming vegetables and decides that harvest is genocide — an absurdity the chant then flattens into a single law: everything survives by consuming something else, and no amount of moral panic exempts you from it. The comedy is real, but it's a Trojan horse. What starts as ridicule of sanctimony curdles, in the long spoken coda, into something genuinely disturbing: a man waking dazed in a ditch, tracking his own dissociation through a childlike logic of colors, discovering the knife, the color red, and the two small figures across the field are all, quietly, his. The joke about not being able to draw the line — carrots, rabbits, brothers — becomes the horror of a mind that has drawn no lines at all, so that the album ends not on the parody of belief but on where a fully collapsed conscience actually leads.","fv":2,"fnote":"On most pressings it sits at track 69 — tracks 10 through 68 are seconds of silence each, a packaging prank that puts the sermon on the far side of a desert of nothing.","themes":["violence & murder","comedy & wordplay","faith & the occult","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"sanctimony masking a conscience that drew no lines","built":"the sermon joke becomes genuine dissociated horror"},
"tool~eulogy":{"haiku":"A vocal false prophet dies; the speaker rejects his martyrdom and calls out the seductive power of charismatic liars who inspire blind worship.","opus":"A sardonic send-off for a self-appointed martyr who raged and blamed everyone but himself, loud and hollow at once. The sharper jab targets both the fallen zealot and the narrator's own itch to worship any leader still standing, demanding the corpse step aside since the crowd merely wants room to mount its next foolish saint.","sonnet":"A savage dissection of a self-appointed martyr — someone who preached loudly about dying for his beliefs but, when held to account, turns out to have had nothing real to say; the song is an angry goodbye to false prophets who positioned themselves as saviors while demanding devotion from others.","src":"haiku","fable":"A mock funeral oration for a demagogue whose fury pointed everywhere except inward, staged as Maynard Keenan works out his own complicity in the round. The verses damn the loud man who confused volume with truth, but the bridge flips the knife back on the mourner, who confesses he swallowed the whole act because he was starving for someone worth following, someone who might trade a life for his. That craving curdles into the coercive drill of the outro, where the follower issues the martyr's terms and demands proof by death. By the close the song reveals its real subject is the cult economy itself: crucifixion as a rite the crowd needs so badly it is already clearing the cross for the next fool, making the eulogy less a farewell than a job posting.","fv":2,"fnote":"The album is dedicated to Bill Hicks, which fueled the wrong guess — the band waved his name off; fan lore has always nominated L. Ron Hubbard instead, and the corpse stays officially unnamed.","themes":["faith & the occult","politics & society"],"means":"the eulogy exposes the cult economy that immediately needs a new martyr","built":"the bridge turns the knife back on the mourner who confesses he swallowed the act"},
"tool~fear-inoculum":{"haiku":"Inoculating against a seductive deceiver's psychological control by recognizing poison and expelling foreign influence to reclaim autonomy.","opus":"A ritual of purging poison and manipulation, the narrator exhaling a contagion he once naively welcomed and building immunity against a deceiver who feeds on fear. Language of mitosis and allegorical elegy frames psychological liberation as a slow, deliberate exorcism. The title track of Tool's long-awaited 2019 album, it shows the band's meticulous prog-metal architecture in service of shedding a toxic influence and reclaiming clarity from venom.","sonnet":"A long-overdue break from a toxic bond — the deceiver who kept him frightened of light and others finally recognised, expelled, chased away. The metaphor is immunological: years of exposure have built a resistance, the venom is named and neutralised, and what replaces fear is clarity, a rewritten personal story, and the quiet blessing of being free.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track and opening statement of Tool's return after thirteen years, it reframes their long silence as a deliberate act of purging: illness, addiction, and toxic devotion become one contagion the speaker learns to breathe out rather than absorb. The structure enacts the cure -- the deceiver's voice in the interlude promises belonging while demanding fear of everyone else, the language of a cult, an abuser, or a craving, until the speaker recognizes that what was fed as poison is what makes him immune, and the biological metaphor of cell division turns escape into growth. By the outro the false authority is simply chased off, a reckoning framed as something owed for a very long time, so the song works as its own inoculation: naming the toxin in small controlled doses to build resistance to it.","fv":2,"themes":["freedom & escape","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming"],"means":"naming the toxin in small doses builds resistance","built":"the structure enacts cure recognition then expulsion"},
"tool~flood":{"haiku":"Catastrophic loss of certainty where reality fails and the ground crumbles; forced to abandon attachments and climb toward survival.","opus":"A vision of rising water stripping away everything the narrator believed solid, faith and certainty crumbling as the ground he thought was rock breaks beneath him, forcing him to abandon what is his and climb toward a new place. The deliverance he prayed for arrives instead as punishment and cleansing. Tool, on their early work, turn a biblical deluge into a slow-building meditation on loss, humility and forced transformation.","sonnet":"Faith collapsing under rising water — everything the speaker built their certainty on turns out to be unstable ground, and the flood that comes isn't punishment so much as revelation. The only way forward is to let go of what's being claimed by the water and climb somewhere new, stripped of the divine destiny that turned out to be delusion.","src":"haiku","fable":"A deluge as spiritual reckoning: the certainties a person built their identity on turn out to be silt, not bedrock, and the flood comes to repossess everything they called theirs. The song's engine is the collision between two verbs of ownership — the frantic clutching at possessions and status set against a flood that simply claims, indifferent to any claim. Maynard turns the biblical purge inward: the narrator who thought he was chosen and elevated discovers his high ground was pride, and the ascent he scrambles for is really an admission that he was never as free or saved as he believed. The final turn is the cruelest and truest — he expects deliverance and gets judgment instead, so the drowning stops reading as disaster and becomes the only honest cleansing available.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","nature & the elements","identity & becoming"],"means":"a biblical flood turned inward exposing pride","built":"the narrator expects deliverance and receives honest drowning"},
"tool~forty-six-2":{"haiku":"A person confronts their emotional damage, struggling to shed old patterns and trauma, seeking metamorphosis and personal growth through painful self-examination.","opus":"Built on Jungian shadow-work and a theory of human evolution through extra chromosomes, Keenan's ordeal of digging through his own buried self to endure a painful metamorphosis; growth here means crawling through everything you've hidden and coming out changed.","sonnet":"A narrator undergoes a process of painful self-excavation — shedding old defences, confronting buried confusion and insecurity — in pursuit of a fundamental transformation of self that the concept of forty-six and two chromosomes represents.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title comes from a fringe idea associated with Drunvalo Melchizedek, that humanity's next evolutionary rung means moving beyond the ordinary chromosome count toward a new structure numbered forty-six plus two. Maynard turns that cosmic upgrade into brutally bodily, unglamorous labor: he pictures the self as a snake working free of its old sheath, a man worrying at half-healed wounds until they reopen, burrowing down into worn muscle after some clue, dragging himself low across the floor to sweep out everything he might have been and wasn't. The two pre-choruses track the shift from passivity to hunger, first pleading for some sign to lead him across, then daring the transformation to devour him and pull the exterior inward. The Jungian key is explicit, evolution here means absorbing the buried self rather than fleeing it, and the bridge's litany of choosing to live and die, hate and love, kill and cry, is the toll, you carry the whole person through the gate or nobody passes. He ends still moving into that darker double, the metamorphosis forever one pace out in front of him.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","transcendence & consciousness","shame & repression"],"means":"evolution demanded as unglamorous bodily labor not cosmic gift","built":"the bridge's litany pairs opposites as the toll you must carry through the gate"},
"tool~h":{"haiku":"Internal conflict between destructive impulses and the desire for emotional connection and vulnerability. A venomous inner voice tempts toward poison while love demands the heart open.","opus":"Maynard wrestles with a bond that both nourishes and destroys him, a serpent-and-savior figure he's too attached to escape, arriving at a bruised acceptance that being consumed by this love is somehow alright.","sonnet":"A venomous inner voice — figured as a snake — keeps whispering about what damage the narrator could have caused, and the struggle is whether to open the heart again or stay closed; by the end the snake drowns and old fear dissolves into a kind of exhausted acceptance.","src":"haiku","fable":"Maynard Keenan builds the song from a private trinity of snake, mirror, and pleading blood, staging an internal war between a hissing thing that drains and cracks him open and something ancestral begging him to stay open. The genius of the writing is the adverb: the thing destroying him does so with consideration, care, even tenderness, which is why he can't simply cast it off. The final verse enacts a false resolution — the serpent held under until it goes silent, the walls fall, fear fades — but the pronoun quietly shifts from his to your, and the reptile's eyes become a loved one's, revealing that the addiction he thought he'd killed was a person all along. What looked like exorcism was really surrender: the confession that he is too bound to this figure to break free, and has decided he doesn't mind being unmade by it.","fv":2,"fnote":"Keenan introduced the song live as being about his newborn son — H. is the boy's initial — which re-tunes the tender destroyer: the bind he cannot break is love with a body attached.","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"destruction given so tenderly it cannot simply be cast off","built":"the pronoun quietly shifts from his to your, revealing the serpent was a person"},
"tool~hooker-with-a-penis":{"haiku":"A musician addresses a young fan's accusations of selling out, pointing out that selling exists at every level of society—producer and consumer alike participate in commercialism.","opus":"A scornful reply to a fan accusing the band of selling out, arguing that everyone is complicit in commerce and that the accuser bought the very product he condemns.","sonnet":"The narrator confronts a fan who accuses the band of selling out, turning the accusation back: everyone participates in the same commercial system, and buying the record makes the accuser equally complicit.","src":"haiku","fable":"A preemptive strike against the purist fan who accuses Tool of selling out, written by a band that was signed to a major label from the very start of AEnima's era of arena ascent. Keenan builds the accuser as a walking checklist of authentic-scene signifiers, then dismantles the whole premise: the moment you make art for money, you have already sold out, and everyone downstream who buys it is implicated in the same transaction. The venom escalates from personal insult to a wider indictment of consumer culture, where the outro reduces all media to product and the listener to a wallet, and the closing curse implicates the very person still listening. The title's crudest joke is the argument itself: gatekeeping authenticity is a con, and the con man has a body just like everyone he preaches to.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"gatekeeping authenticity indicted as a con sold to its own audience","built":"the closing curse implicates the very person still listening"},
"tool~hush":{"haiku":"A person resents external control over what they can say and think, expressing frustration at censorship even when joking or provocative, though ultimately they're just joking.","opus":"An early Tool blast against censorship, Maynard swearing profusely to prove the point that being told what to say only sharpens the urge to say the worst. The joke is that the whole song is the forbidden speech, delivered as provocation.","sonnet":"A protest against censorship and social policing dressed as pure provocation — the repeated violent outbursts are framed as things the narrator can't say, making the meta-point that saying dangerous-sounding things when not serious shouldn't require permission from people who dictate thought.","src":"haiku","fable":"A punk squib weaponized as a middle finger at the PMRC-era censorship wars that shadowed their early-90s emergence. The whole engineering trick is that the ugliest lines are always framed as unserious, kidding, not-meant a hair before or after they land, so the band gets to voice exactly the aggression that the moral guardians want banned while technically disowning it. The real subject isn't the abuse but the gag rule: a crowd of external censors auditing his speech, his beliefs, even the notes he's allowed to strike on a stage, and the song's answer is to spit out the forbidden thing and then dare you to prove intent. The closing retreat into kidding isn't apology, it's the sneer completing itself, the mask lifted just far enough to show there was no mask.","fv":2,"fnote":"The video makes the argument a sight gag — the band stands naked, mouths taped over, advisory placards for clothing: the gag rule worn literally.","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society"],"means":"voicing forbidden speech then disowning it as a dare to prove intent","built":"the ugliest lines are always bracketed by kidding just before or after they land"},
"tool~i-n-v-i-n-c-i-b-l-e":{"haiku":"An aging warrior confronts their irrelevance and mortality, chasing false hope of restoration while time decays them. The song meditates on how strength abandons and armor wears thin.","opus":"An aging warrior lurches back into battle, chest beating and bones tired, boasting of past glories while the armor wears thin and time bears down. Chasing Ponce de Leon's phantom fountains, he clings to hope he knows is false. Tool built this Fear Inoculum-era epic as a meditation on mortality and relevance, the once-invincible fighter a stand-in for artists, egos, and everyone refusing to concede to age.","sonnet":"A seasoned warrior facing the slow erosion of relevance, armor thinning, bones tired, chasing something like Ponce de León's fountain of youth that everyone knows is a myth. Pride and grief in the same breath — the warrior keeps charging, knowing the charge itself has changed.","src":"haiku"},
"tool~intension":{"haiku":"Humans build shelter and forge weapons through pure will, drawing destructive power from primal shared intention. The song treats creation and destruction as emerging from the same communal force.","opus":"A meditation on will and the dual use of human effort, contrasting building shelter into home and lighting fire for warmth with weaponizing the same stone, flame, and blade to slay the stranger and seize whatever is desired; framed by purity at birth and return to oneness, it's about the moral fork inside the same creative impulse.","sonnet":"A compressed history of human civilization told in abstract images: gathering stones for shelter, then using them to drive away strangers; a spark becoming fire used to warm the home, then to forge weapons and take what is desired — the same impulse serving both creation and conquest.","src":"haiku","fable":"A creation myth built as a fork in the road, where every act of making carries its violent twin. The stone that builds a shelter is the same stone hurled at the outsider; the fire that warms the home is the same heat that forges a blade to take by force. Tool structures the song as escalating couplets that each split into a benevolent and a predatory branch, so the will that raises a home and the will that conquers turn out to be one impulse wearing two faces. The framing device of purity at the threshold, bookending arrival and departure, insists this ambivalence is native to us rather than a corruption acquired along the way. The refusal to choose between the two branches is the read: intention itself is the neutral engine, and what it drives toward is left as our indictment.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","violence & murder"],"means":"building and conquering as one impulse wearing two faces","built":"escalating couplets split every act of making against its violent twin"},
"tool~intermission":{"sonnet":"No meaningful lyrical content — the entry contains only album metadata and no lyrics.","src":"sonnet"},
"tool~intolerance":{"haiku":"A person is tired of tolerating lies and betrayal from someone they wanted to trust. Everyone shares guilt, and they finally refuse to enable the deception any longer.","opus":"A slow-burning reckoning with a liar and cheat the narrator keeps tolerating, until he admits complicity, insisting no one, including himself, is innocent. From the Undertow debut by Tool, the track shows an early trudge toward the moral self-implication that would define them, the vowed refusal to tolerate resolving into a shared descent rather than clean judgment.","sonnet":"A speaker who has long performed loyalty and applause finally reaches the limit of his capacity for complicity. What once looked like tolerance reveals itself as self-deception: everyone — including the speaker — bears guilt, and the decision to stop enabling dishonesty is inseparable from confronting his own participation in it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The opening bind is a person trying to stay decent inside a bad relationship, wanting to lower the weapon and extend faith to someone who keeps betraying it. The chorus makes that impossible: the litany of the other's crimes hardens from something endured into something that cannot be, the sung praise revealed as a mask worn over disgust while the singer performs delight he doesn't feel. Then the song turns the knife inward. By the third verse the accusation stops being aimed only outward, and the closing shift from tolerance to going down alongside the offender collapses accuser and accused into the same guilt, so the real subject is the corrosion of moral certainty itself: no innocence left to defend, only the choice to stop pretending and fall together.","fv":2,"themes":["complicity","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"moral certainty corroded until accuser and accused fall together","built":"the third verse turns the accusation inward collapsing the singer into the same guilt"},
"tool~invincible":{"haiku":"Aging warriors struggle to remain relevant and consequential as strength fades, chasing immortality's phantom while time bears down.","opus":"An aging warrior facing his own mortality — unbeatable once, his defenses now worn down — still roaring about past glories while clinging to a hollow dream of everlasting youth as time closes in.","sonnet":"An aging warrior still beats tired bones into battle, boasting of old victories that would make Caligula grin, but feels the armour wearing thin and relevance slipping away; chasing Ponce de León's phantom soul and the mythical fountain of youth, they feel time bearing down even as the truth refuses to stop them.","src":"haiku","fable":"The aging warrior's boast, sung by a band who made this record in their fifties after thirteen years away, so the swagger is the subject rather than the point. It opens in full posture -- a fist pounding the ribcage over a martial pulse, tales of old victories a Caligula could envy -- then the armor thins mid-song and the same warrior is caught stumbling backward into nostalgia, still fighting to matter (relevant, consequential) while the shield lowers. The final movement drops the mask entirely: chasing Ponce de Leon's fountain of youth, the speaker concedes the chase is probably delusion and that honesty was never allowed to obstruct him -- a man knowingly drinking his own myth because the alternative is letting the years' weight settle on him. Tool are diagnosing the very grandiosity their catalog is built on, and the honesty of that self-audit is the actual invincibility on offer.","fv":2,"themes":["artifice & performance","nostalgia & memory","identity & becoming"],"means":"drinking one's own myth because the alternative is accepting the years","built":"armor thins mid-song as boast quietly becomes confession of delusion"},
"tool~jambi":{"haiku":"A meditation on love as the ultimate value, willing to sacrifice wealth and worldly success for the presence and peace of mind found in another person.","opus":"A man who has tasted every indulgence and flirted with the dark admits he would trade it all to hold on to the one person who rescued and remade him. Split between craving and need, he treats that person as his anchor and pleads for two lives to fuse into a single one.","sonnet":"A narrator who once revelled in wealth and dark pleasures says he would surrender it all rather than lose the person who pulled him back from self-destruction, pleading with a benevolent, sun-like force to keep them unified.","src":"haiku","fable":"A Faustian bargain read backwards: the speaker has already gotten everything he asked for -- the sultan's feast, the deal with the dark, the bartered performance that seduced the underworld into paying out -- and the whole song is him realizing he'd trade every granted wish to keep one person. The title is the mechanism: Jambi puns on the iambic meter Maynard is writing in and on the genie who grants wishes, so the structure is three wishes taken and a fourth he begs to reverse. What he refuses to surrender is named precisely -- his center, his fulcrum, the pivot on which desire and necessity balance as a single load rather than pulling him apart -- so the real terror isn't losing the beloved but being cleaved against himself, and the bridge answers with a prayer for light, calling on a kind star to burn over the broken and the cut-apart until the split halves anneal back into a single body. It closes by turning outward and hardening: the union welded, he tells the legion to keep its poison and stay clear.","fv":2,"fnote":"The genie has a specific face — Jambi from Pee-wee's Playhouse, the wish-granter in a jeweled box — and Adam Jones's solo answers through a talk box, the guitar literally trying to speak.","themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"every granted wish traded back to keep one person","built":"the title puns iambic meter and the genie granting wishes"},
"tool~jerk-off-live":{"haiku":"The song challenges moral absolutes and consequences, questioning whether punishment actually follows wrongdoing; it expresses exhaustion and desire to enact justice themselves against assholes.","opus":"Rage at the failure of justice to punish the arrogant, leading the narrator to fantasize about playing God and shooting the offender himself. The escalating final lines make explicit that his patience with a world where wrong goes unpunished has collapsed into homicidal impulse.","sonnet":"Rage at someone who has done wrong and faced no consequences, the narrator working through the logic that if conventional punishment won't come, he might as well take matters into his own hands and shoot the person himself.","src":"haiku","fable":"A vigilante monologue that curdles as it escalates, and the curdling is the point. It opens with a sampled boast about exterminating undesirables like pests, then hands the mic to a narrator who's decided that patience for cosmic justice is for suckers and he'll deliver the punishment himself. But the song keeps exposing the rot in its own logic: the moral rule collapses into the observation that nothing is actually wrong unless you're caught, which is exactly the reasoning of the people he wants dead. Maynard isn't endorsing the executioner fantasy so much as inhabiting it until the mask of righteous grievance slips and shows the same appetite for domination underneath — the outro's chanted, escalating threats aren't catharsis but a demonstration of how easily wounded self-righteousness talks itself into playing God. That this survives only as a live recording, screamed at a crowd, sharpens the trap: it dares the audience to cheer for the very cruelty it's indicting.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance"],"means":"righteous grievance slides into the appetite it meant to punish","built":"the live setting dares the crowd to cheer the cruelty being indicted"},
"tool~jimmy":{"haiku":"Reuniting with a childhood self or lost memory, carrying forward an ancient connection to finally heal and move together.","opus":"The singer reaches back to a boyhood self stopped at age eleven, the point when his sense of security abruptly disappeared. He aims to reconnect with and release that stranded inner child, guiding him toward home and toward the light so the two halves can merge and mend.","sonnet":"A return to a frozen inner child — \"Eleven\" — who was abandoned when the narrator was eleven years old and has been waiting ever since; the song is about the slow, painful journey back to that stalled part of the self, guided by its distant light, with the hope that reuniting with it will allow both to finally move forward.","src":"haiku","fable":"Maynard James Keenan wrote this as an adult reaching back to the boy he was at eleven, the age when a brain aneurysm dropped his mother and vanished her from him as a full person, the Ohio landscape gone flat and dead around the event. The number becomes a frozen clock: not just his age but the child arrested at that moment of loss, left waiting to be freed. The song's arc is a reunion the singer stages inside himself, addressing that stranded younger self as both wounded party and rescuer, promising to retrace every scarred recollection one gentle inch at a time until the split is closed. Its final resolution, where the two totals of a single sum resolve into one figure, is the whole point structurally as well as emotionally: the numbed adult and the abandoned child fusing back into one person, so the light he keeps asking to be led toward is really his own consciousness finally returning to the house of itself.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","family & blood"],"means":"the adult retracing wounds to free the child arrested at eleven","built":"two totals of a single sum resolving into one figure seals the reunion structurally"},
"tool~lateralus":{"haiku":"Overthinking fragments the self from intuition and instinct; true growth comes from embracing spontaneity, connection, and the endless spiral of becoming.","opus":"A meditation on transcendence through embracing the unknown, its structure built on the Fibonacci sequence. The core idea is that too much thinking cuts the mind off from the body and dulls instinct, so one must nourish the drive to feel the present, spiral out past the limits of logic, and welcome chance in order to brush against the divine while staying human.","sonnet":"An argument against the prison of rational overthinking — using the Fibonacci sequence as its structural backbone, the song urges surrendering to the spiral of experience, embracing randomness and sensation, and staying human while reaching toward infinity.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built as an argument against its own medium: the lyric attacks overthinking for severing thought from flesh, and its cure is surrender to instinct, spiral, and whatever chance throws up. The structure enacts the thesis — Keenan's syllable counts famously track the Fibonacci sequence and the verses coil outward like the spiral they invoke — so the form is the intuition the words are begging you to trust. It moves from an infant seeing only primary colors to an adult straining past the borders that logic draws, arguing that the highest human act is to sense the sacred in oneself yet choose to remain merely mortal. The instruction to keep spiraling outward is not a metaphor laid over the music but the shape of the music itself.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"overthinking severing flesh from instinct, spiral as the cure","built":"Fibonacci syllable counts enacting the spiral the words are begging you to trust"},
"tool~lipan-conjuring":{"opus":"Tool laced their albums with textural interstitials, and this is one of them: a ceremonial chant of phonetic Native American ritual sounds carrying no translatable meaning. Functioning as atmosphere rather than statement, it serves the record's ritualistic pacing, a hushed passage between the heavier structures around it.","sonnet":"A ceremonial chant with no translatable lyrical content — the track consists of phonetic Native American ritual sounds with no English or interpretable semantic content.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A short a cappella interlude on 10,000 Days, built entirely from Lipan Apache-flavored vocables rather than words, so there is no lyric to decode here. What it does instead is ceremonial: layered, overlapping voices circling a single rising phrase until repetition itself becomes the point, a breathing spell placed between the album's long structures. The title names the intent honestly. This is meant to summon a state, not to say a thing, and the wordlessness is the mechanism, clearing the ground before the next movement rather than narrating anything of its own.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult"],"means":"wordlessness itself used as the summoning mechanism","built":"vocables circle a rising phrase until repetition clears space for what follows"},
"tool~lost-keys-blame-hofmann":{"opus":"A spoken-word hospital scene setting up a story: a nameless, unresponsive patient with no physical trauma whom a doctor gently coaxes to speak, framing the psychedelic breakdown to follow.","sonnet":"A clinical scene-setting interlude: a doctor and nurse confer over a nameless, mute, uninjured patient with no ID, and the doctor attempts to coax the man into speaking — the whole track is the setup for a confession that follows on the next song.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A spoken overture on 10,000 Days, staged as an ER intake: a man with no name, no ID, no wound, and no words that anyone can reach. The parenthetical blames Albert Hofmann, who first synthesized LSD, which reframes the whole scene — the missing keys are the dissolved self at the psychedelic threshold, the patient unreachable not because he's damaged but because he's gone somewhere language can't follow. The doctor's closing plea, to be told everything, isn't diagnosis so much as the next track's first door: the unreachable patient is the Rosetta Stoned narrator, about to erupt into his own account. It reads as clinical concern and lands as an invitation across a boundary the medical frame has no chart for.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"clinical intake masking a psychedelic threshold crossing","built":"spoken drama format borrowed from medicine, not mysticism"},
"tool~message-to-harry-manback":{"haiku":"An Italian-speaking person delivers an angry voicemail cursing at someone for mistreating and kicking them out, wishing them cancer and threatening violence if they meet again.","opus":"An answering-machine tirade Tool pulled from a real threatening voicemail, a scorned Italian caller cursing an American with cancer and black magic. Menace kept raw and unmusical, discomfort as the point.","sonnet":"A real answering-machine message left by an angry Italian man (in Italian and English) threatening someone who kicked people out of a house, wishing cancer and death on them and their family. It's used as audio found-art — pure unfiltered rage, no musical interpretation intended.","src":"haiku","fable":"Found audio dropped raw into the album: a real answering-machine tirade, a wounded man in fractured English and gutter Italian wishing cancer, accidents and black magic on someone who threw him out. Placed on a record obsessed with purging false surfaces, the piece is less a message than an exhibit — the band hands you unmediated human ugliness and lets you sit inside it, the curses (die, piece of shit, son of a whore) escalating precisely because impotence, not power, drives them; he admits outright he lacked the courage to do anything directly. What makes it curdle rather than simply offend is the pairing: the tantrum floats over a delicate, almost lullaby piano, so the track's real subject is the distance between how pathetic the raw feeling sounds and how tenderly it's framed — someone's worst moment turned into an object, half mocked, half mourned.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance","faith & the occult"],"means":"impotent fury half mocked by the tenderness that frames it","built":"a tantrum of curses floated over a delicate near-lullaby piano"},
"tool~nema":{"haiku":"Contempt for Los Angeles, California, and superficial American culture; the narrator fantasizes about cataclysmic destruction cleansing the world.","opus":"Maynard's misanthropic prayer for L.A. to be wiped clean, disgust with celebrity vanity, Scientology and self-absorbed decay boiling over into a wish for a cleansing flood. Learn to swim reads as gleeful hope for apocalypse.","sonnet":"A vicious, cathartic rant about the vapidity and self-obsession of Los Angeles — the speaker genuinely hopes for apocalyptic floods to wash it all away, listing every category of LA pretension with savage precision and begging the ocean to come.","src":"haiku","fable":"A millennial apocalypse fantasy aimed squarely at Los Angeles, where the song wishes the ocean would reclaim the city and leave behind the desert coastline Bill Hicks coined as Arizona Bay. What reads as doomsayer nihilism is really disgust dressed as prophecy: the middle verse's litany of tiny bourgeois anxieties (grooming, litigation, medication, career) exposes a culture so bloated on vanity and distraction that only a cleansing flood feels honest. The maternal figure who arrives to set things right turns catastrophe into a kind of grim mercy, and the drowning imagery becomes an instruction — the only survival strategy in a doomed place is to keep your head above the coming water and paddle. The late torrent of profane dismissals (self-help gurus, poseurs, addicts, phonies) drops the cosmic framing to name the actual targets, revealing that the wished-for disaster is less about geology than about wanting an entire mode of living scoured away. The band's title pun fuses enema and anima, so the flush is simultaneously a bodily purge and a demand for the culture to rediscover a soul.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","nature & the elements","anger & defiance"],"means":"disgust with cultural vanity dressed as a wish for cleansing flood","built":"the title fuses enema and anima, making purge and soul-demand one word"},
"tool~opiate":{"haiku":"Religious manipulation and abuse masquerade as salvation; a false prophet claims divine authority to enslave the broken-minded. The song depicts predatory faith—maker and God both claiming dominion over the vulnerable.","opus":"Maynard's early broadside against religion as coercive control, voiced from inside a predatory authority that demands blind obedience and reveals its appetite for domination. Faith rebranded as the drug that keeps the flock docile.","sonnet":"A preacher who claims to speak for God is exposed as a predator — using faith to make followers deaf, blind, and compliant so he can rape them — the song a direct attack on religious authority that weaponises spiritual need for abuse.","src":"haiku","fable":"Maynard James Keenan sings the whole first half in the voice of the predator, not the victim: a preacher-figure who reframes a follower's helplessness as virtue and their obedience as the price of salvation, until the mask slips and the sacred hand laid on the flock reveals itself as appetite dressed in scripture. The genius is that religion here is the opiate of Marx's line made literal and criminal, the promise of healing weaponized as consent, and the desperate cry to be blinded by holy light is the believer begging for exactly the numbness the abuser is selling. Part II, an early bit of Tool's tonal sabotage, drops the sermon for two stoner-casualty vignettes and then just admits boredom, deflating the intensity to suggest the same craving for oblivion runs under both the pulpit and the acid tab, and that the singer has grown tired of either sermon.","fv":2,"fnote":"Thirty years on, the band re-cut it as Opiate² — an extended re-recording with an animated film, the rare Tool song they treated as unfinished business; its handful of plays here sits on its own shelf beside the original.","themes":["faith & the occult","surveillance & control","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the sacred hand on the flock revealed as appetite in scripture","built":"Part II deflates the sermon with stoner-casualty vignettes and admitted boredom"},
"tool~parabol":{"haiku":"A meditation on presence and embodied connection, celebrating the overwhelming warmth of holding someone real while acknowledging pain as illusion. The song honors the body as proof against loneliness and existential fear.","opus":"A dissociative moment of grounding, the narrator clutching the physical warmth of his own body and realizing this fleeting present is all he genuinely possesses. The deeper reading is spiritual reassurance: the flesh anchors him against isolation, hints at something limitless, and reframes suffering as unreal, a matter of choosing to inhabit the moment.","sonnet":"A brief, tender meditation on embodiment and the present moment — the narrator marvels at the warmth and reality of existing in a body, surrounded by another person, choosing to be here now. The closing line that \"all this pain is an illusion\" prefigures the longer \"Parabola\" that follows.","src":"haiku","fable":"Parabol is the ambient prelude to Parabola, and read alone it captures the moment before release: a spirit newly incarnated, marveling at the strangeness of having a body at all. Tool frames physical existence not as a trap but as a fleeting miracle, the wonder of a consciousness that barely recalls anything before this instant and chooses to inhabit it fully. The reassurance that suffering is only illusion isn't consolation but doctrine, the song's held breath before Parabola detonates the same insight into ecstatic motion.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"incarnation held as wonder before the detonation arrives","built":"functions as a held breath, structurally incomplete without Parabola"},
"tool~parabola":{"haiku":"A meditation on mortality and embodiment as a path to understanding eternity. The speaker frames bodily pain and suffering as illusion, emphasizing presence and the gift of being alive.","opus":"A contemplation of being here and dying, honoring the physical form and the brief window in which one gets to exist and draw breath now, recasting suffering as unreal and life as a sacred, everlasting present.","sonnet":"A meditation on the sacred nature of physical existence: pain is an illusion, the body is a reminder that we are not alone, and being alive in this moment is itself a holy gift worth celebrating.","src":"haiku","fable":"The companion release to Parabol on Lateralus, this is the exhale after that track's dread of the fleeting moment: a mantra that talks itself out of dissociation and back into flesh. Keenan keeps circling the same image of a body as both cage and gift, and the repetition is the point, an incantation worn smooth until the mind stops fleeing the present. The claim that suffering is an illusion isn't spiritual denial but its opposite discipline: only because the flesh is mortal and temporary does staying inside it become the whole practice. The song spins its parable rather than argues it, treating the ache of being alive as the very proof to celebrate.","fv":2,"fnote":"The thesis has an official picture: Adam Jones's video climaxes in Alex Grey's anatomical figures, the body peeled to layered energy — cage and gift drawn instead of sung.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","death & grief"],"means":"staying inside the mortal body treated as the whole practice","built":"repetition wears the incantation smooth until the mind stops fleeing the present"},
"tool~part-of-me":{"haiku":"A person views another as a possession or extension of themselves—compliant and serving only their use, never to leave or resist.","opus":"An early Tool track addressed to something possessed and exploited, mute and incapable of leaving, that the singer claims to understand more deeply than his own self. It plays as mastery over an object or a rejected inner fragment, ordering it to perish a bit and surrender itself.","sonnet":"About an internalized self that cannot speak, judge, leave, or hurt — the narrator addresses this silent inner entity with dark intimacy, ultimately telling it that it's time to sacrifice itself; the song reads as a dissociation or confrontation with the shadow self.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song presents itself as intimate address — a claim to have mapped someone's interior more thoroughly than they've mapped their own — but the second half dismantles that pretense: the addressee has no voice, renders no verdict, is barred from walking away, exists only to be used, which makes it not a lover but a possession, or more precisely a disowned interior fragment. The title's downgrade from total knowing to something merely partial reframes the whole obsessive litany as ego addressing its own shadow, the buried self it claims mastery over yet cannot integrate. The demand that this fragment offer itself up on an altar and undergo a small death is the psyche's ritual of control, insisting the unruly part submit rather than be reconciled. What sounds like devotion is really the dominant self refusing to admit that the thing it silences and uses is, inescapably, itself.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","surveillance & control","shame & repression"],"means":"domination of the silenced interior fragment that is inescapably oneself","built":"the title's downgrade from total knowing to partial reframes the litany as ego addressing its shadow"},
"tool~pneuma":{"haiku":"Human spirit transcends physical flesh, seeking awakening and unity beyond material existence and the illusions that bind us.","opus":"A meditation on spirit confined within the body, pinned in place yet straining to extend past its physical bounds, all souls born from a single breath and one shared spark of light. It calls on a child to awaken and let that light out. Tool draw on the Greek word for breath and soul for this Fear Inoculum centerpiece, pairing intricate rhythms with a genuinely mystical longing for unity and awakening.","sonnet":"A meditation on consciousness as shared breath: all beings are sparks of the same source, spirit pinned temporarily to flesh but able to reach beyond it. A call to wake up, remember, and become something larger — Pneuma as the animating force waiting to be recognized.","src":"haiku","fable":"Pneuma is the Greek word for breath as spirit, and Tool builds the song as a Gnostic reveille: consciousness is a single divine exhalation that has forgotten it was ever whole, temporarily stapled into a body that can only pace in circles. The verses map the trap -- a heel pinned to the flesh as if crucified in place -- against the reaching impulse that strains past it, so the physical guise is figured as a mask over a dream the self is sleeping through. The turn is the imperative that rouses a sleeping child and commands it to release its light: enlightenment here is not attainment but recollection, remembering that every apparently separate spark is the same sun. Fittingly for a band whose signature is rhythm as structure, the meaning rides the mechanics; the song enacts breath and return rather than merely naming them, the outro dilating from the trapped verses into wide-eyed wonder as the individual voice melts back into the single syllable it was spoken from.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult"],"means":"consciousness waking to find one shared breath","built":"the outro enacts return from trapped verses into unity"},
"tool~prison-sex":{"haiku":"Childhood sexual abuse and its lasting trauma are examined. The singer cycles through pain, self-harm, and perpetuation of damage, trying to find wholeness through degradation.","opus":"A survivor of childhood abuse relives the trauma and, disturbingly, the cycle by which the abused becomes abuser, seeking wholeness through the very violation done to him. Tool built this Undertow track around that harrowing psychology, Maynard James Keenan voicing the do-unto-others logic with unnerving intimacy, the wholeness and full-circle return laying bare the self-perpetuating horror of abuse.","sonnet":"Childhood abuse resurfaces as the survivor reenacts the same violence on another, caught in a cycle of perpetuation that the lyric neither excuses nor entirely condemns. The terrible logic of trauma is laid bare: harm done early becomes the template through which intimacy, control, and momentary wholeness are sought.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor of childhood sexual abuse relives the trauma from inside the wound, the memory long buried and only now surfacing through the fog of dissociation. The horror of the song is its structural collapse: the victim, still checking his own breath to confirm he's alive, turns the abuser's posture onto a new figure who is roped at the wrists, mute, and lidded over exactly as he once was. The refrain twists the golden rule into a curse of transmitted violence, tracing how abuse reproduces itself down a chain, and the sacrificial vocabulary of the slaughtered innocent and the one sanctified conduit reframes rape as a defiling sacrament that grants only a counterfeit, momentary wholeness. Keenan reads the mask from the abuser's own head, sanity described as a fleeting borrowed state that has looped back to its origin, so that the tenderness in his voice is unbearable rather than sincere. What lives underneath is a cycle the speaker half-recognizes and cannot break.","fv":2,"fnote":"Adam Jones's stop-motion video — the doll and the small extracted figure — was pulled from MTV rotation; the imagery said what the airwaves wouldn't carry.","themes":["violence & murder","shame & repression","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"the abuser's logic re-enacted by the surviving victim","built":"the golden rule inverted into violation"},
"tool~pushit":{"haiku":"A toxic relationship alternates between intimate love and violent pushing; both lovers simultaneously need and destroy each other.","opus":"Keenan wrestling with a volatile, codependent relationship where love and violence blur into the same reflex, the fear that engaging fully will destroy them both; the plea for peace of mind against a bond that only ends by tearing itself apart.","sonnet":"A song about a toxic relationship where love and violence are fused — the narrator sees a dangerous gap between himself and his partner that, if he enters, might destroy them both, yet he keeps being pushed and shoved there, and ends by imagining clawing the other person's throat as the only possible resolution.","src":"haiku","fable":"Maynard Keenan maps a relationship as a hostage standoff at the edge of an abyss, the gap he keeps glimpsing, where the threat is mutual annihilation if the other person crowds him past a line. The genius is that the tormentor and the beloved are the same person, and possibly the same as the singer, the infant he wants to choke is his own reflection, so the manhandling becomes indistinguishable from being alive, he only feels real when hands are on him and feet are scrambling to stay on the ground. Across four verses he tries to withdraw peacefully and fails, and the outro resolves the standoff the only way it can, a vow to love her always spoken in the same breath as clawing her throat open. The final gesture makes his argument for him: what he calls love and what he calls terror have fused into one motion he can no longer separate.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"love and terror fused into one indivisible reflex","built":"the vow to love her and claw her throat arrive in the same breath"},
"tool~reflection":{"haiku":"The narrator confronts his self-indulgent despair by recognizing that his light comes from external connection, not ego. He must transcend narcissism and trust universal interconnection.","opus":"Maynard's ego-death meditation, a narcissist hitting bottom and realizing his light is only borrowed and reflected, resolving to crucify the self and dissolve into the recognition that we are all one mind.","sonnet":"A confrontation with the ego's gravitational pull into self-pity and narcissism — at the darkest moment the speaker realizes the moon's light is only reflected, not its own, and that salvation requires crucifying the ego before it drags you deeper into the self-indulgent hole.","src":"haiku","fable":"Maynard Keenan's account of surfacing from depression as a spiritual mechanism rather than a mood. It opens at rock bottom, a man curled into himself and mistaking self-obsession for depth, before the register flips: he realizes the moonlight he took for his own brilliance is borrowed, a reflection of an endless source, and that a soul running only on borrowed light is a dead satellite unless it stays in orbit around something larger. The demand that follows is brutal and literal in its wording of self-sacrifice, killing the ego before it kills him, which is why the song widens in its final movement from I to we: escape from narcissism turns out to require dissolving the boundary of the self entirely, arriving at the idea that all minds are one. The chant that closes it is a countdown against wasting away, the same fear that opened the song now spoken in the plural.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming"],"means":"ego-death staged as the only exit from borrowed light","built":"the pronoun shifts from I to we as narcissism dissolves into shared mind"},
"tool~right-in-two":{"haiku":"Critique of humanity's tendency toward division and conflict over resources. Humans are blessed with reason but waste it fighting each other instead of cooperating.","opus":"Tool watches humanity from the angels' bemused vantage, asking why a species given reason and free will chooses only to divide and slaughter over scraps of ground. It's a bleak parable about the squandered gift of consciousness, monkeys with thumbs forging blades.","sonnet":"A meditation on humanity's self-destructive tendency to divide and fight over shared resources, framed as angels watching in bewilderment as \"monkeys\" given free will and reason choose instead to make weapons and kill each other over patches of ground.","src":"haiku","fable":"Watched by bored, sidelined angels, humanity is diagnosed as a species that received both a paradise big enough for everyone and the gift of reason, and used the latter only to slice the former into pieces worth killing over. The recurring taunt reduces us to primates handed opposable thumbs, which we immediately spend forging weapons rather than lifting our gaze upward — the song's real scorn is aimed less at cruelty than at squandered capacity, the tragedy of a creature briefly conscious of its own short time who chooses partition anyway. The mounting chant that everything must be split doesn't resolve; it just accelerates, and Carey's shifting meter enacts the point, a rhythm that keeps subdividing until the outro leaves the rope-pulling contest permanently unfinished. Even love and light get itemized as spoils to be quarreled over and seized, so the indictment isn't of scarcity but of a mind that manufactures scarcity in a place that held abundance — division as the one thing reason reliably produces.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","war & battle"],"means":"scorn aimed at squandered reason rather than cruelty itself","built":"Carey's meter keeps subdividing, enacting the division it indicts"},
"tool~rosetta-stoned":{"haiku":"Contact with the divine arrives through altered consciousness, the chosen one receives a message of hope but cannot remember it upon waking.","opus":"A drug-fried alien-abduction monologue where a burnout is anointed the Chosen One, handed the message of humanity's end, and then loses his pen. Under the psychedelic comedy is a serious jab at self-appointed prophets, the revelation squandered by a fried, unreliable mind that can't recall a word of it.","sonnet":"A stoned, comedic near-miss with cosmic revelation: the speaker was high on drugs outside Area 51, had what seemed like an alien encounter, felt chosen to deliver a message to humanity, then forgot every word of it — a parody of spiritual ego and the limits of chemically induced enlightenment.","src":"haiku","fable":"A tweaker's abduction narrative that is really a savage parody of prophecy and the ego's hunger to be chosen. Maynard's narrator, wired on a shopping-list of drugs outside Area 51, gets his cosmic close encounter and his cosmic mission — to be the one who writes down humanity's ending — and the joke is that this hollow, credential-less nobody laps up the flattery of being The Chosen One precisely because it's what he needs to hear, not because it's true. The song's cruelty and its point arrive together in the crash: the vision fades, he's left strapped to his bed soiling himself, and the burden collapses into the one thing that damns every would-be messenger — he cannot remember a word of the message, only that he was told he mattered. Under the sci-fi slapstick sits a genuine dread, the title punning Rosetta Stone into stoned: the key to meaning was handed over and immediately lost, and the closing chant of not knowing turns his rapture into the permanent, mocking silence of revelation that refuses to translate.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"the chosen one's revelation collapses because he forgot to bring a pen","built":"the title puns Rosetta Stone into stoned, the key to meaning lost to the substance that promised it"},
"tool~schism":{"haiku":"A relationship fragmenting under miscommunication and blame; both lovers watch pieces fall away, unable to repair connection as coldness and doubt erode compassion.","opus":"Tool anatomizes a relationship dying of failed communication — the pieces that once locked together now mildewed and strangled by wanting, cold silence wearing compassion down to nothing. It declines to assign blame, locating a strange, hard-won beauty in the very discord it mourns.","sonnet":"A song about a romantic relationship destroyed by the failure to communicate — the pieces once fit, then fell apart through coveting and silence, and cold indifference between supposed lovers atrophies what was left of connection.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title is the interpretive key: schism means a split, but the lyric's obsessive refrain insists the pieces still fit. Maynard Keenan writes a breakup as a failure of communication itself, the word tolling at every verse's end like a clinical diagnosis. What makes it more than a relationship autopsy is the structural irony: the band famously built the song in shifting, unstable meters, so the music enacts the very miscommunication the words lament, two people squaring off and circling while insisting there's beauty to be found in the dissonance rather than resolution. The closing loop, the same conviction repeated until it curdles from reassurance into denial, leaves the reader unsure whether the speaker is diagnosing the break or refusing to admit it happened.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"insistence that pieces still fit performed in music that enacts the split","built":"shifting unstable meters enact the miscommunication the words lament"},
"tool~sober":{"haiku":"A narrator trapped by their own compulsions and failures recognizes how they poison relationships, expressing the despair of self-awareness without change.","opus":"A raw meditation on addiction, the narrator confessing he's a hopeless deceiver and fool who will only pull others down as he ruins himself. A lurking presence drains the meaning from every vow he makes, and his tormented question about being unable to stay clean captures the tug between craving oblivion and aching for a fresh start.","sonnet":"A self-aware portrait of addiction and self-destruction: the narrator admits he is a liar who will complicate and ultimately drop anyone who trusts him, and beneath the surface he's using substances to escape a deep worthlessness he can't yet face.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tool's 1993 breakthrough is addiction voiced from inside the addiction, where getting clean is framed as the unbearable state and intoxication as the only relief—the disease speaking in the first person, pleading for oblivion and calling it peace. The genius is the second verse's swerve: the parasite turns to address whoever loves him, promising with chilling candor that his affection is a mechanism for ruin, that he lifts people up only to have farther to drop them. Beneath the substance metaphor runs a curdled religiosity—the demand that Christ or the Virgin whistle away consequence, absolution reduced to amnesia—until the outro strips away every excuse and leaves only the naked engine of dependency, a single want repeated until it is the whole self.","fv":2,"fnote":"The band has said it began as a portrait of a friend who could only create while using — the first-person disease voice is borrowed from someone they watched.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult"],"means":"the disease pleading for oblivion and calling it peace","built":"the outro strips every excuse until one want loops as the whole self"},
"tool~stinkfist":{"haiku":"Constant stimulation numb the speaker; he craves deeper sensation and pain to feel alive, yet desensitization means nothing reaches him anymore.","opus":"An indictment of numbness and over-stimulation, using an escalating fist metaphor to describe needing ever more extreme sensation just to feel alive. The horror is the desensitization: nothing satisfies, subtlety is dead, so he keeps digging deeper into himself and another person until something registers.","sonnet":"A song about desensitization — the speaker is so numbed by overstimulation that escalating physical intimacy (with fisting as the central, literal metaphor) becomes the only way to feel alive; the digging keeps going deeper the more numb one gets.","src":"haiku","fable":"The escalating literal image (deeper and deeper past the point of sensation) is the vehicle; the tenor is a soul so numbed by over-stimulation that intimacy has to become extremity just to register. It runs as a seduction — tender, coaxing, the language of belonging and of taking someone's hand — laid over a compulsion that has confused violation with proof of being alive. The bridge drops the erotic mask to state the diagnosis outright: a grief at desensitization, at the death of subtlety, at having to dig harder and harder for any feeling at all.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"intimacy as extremity, the only proof of being alive left available","built":"bridge drops the erotic framing to state the desensitization diagnosis outright"},
"tool~swamp-song":{"haiku":"A foolish person ignores warnings and wades into quicksand, too stubborn to watch their step; the speaker watches them sink into inescapable bog, hoping it claims them.","opus":"A song addressed to an unwanted, belligerent intruder wading knee-deep into a quicksand bog against every warning; the narrator, having warned in vain, now openly hopes the swamp sucks the stubborn fucker down for stumbling uninvited into a place he was never meant to survive.","sonnet":"A furious dressing-down of an uninvited, self-destructive person who wandered into a situation they were warned against and is now sinking — the narrator has zero sympathy and actively hopes the metaphorical bog swallows them whole.","src":"haiku","fable":"A straight-ahead grudge track from the debut with none of the mythic scaffolding Tool would later build. The bog is a person, or rather a person's stubborn refusal to heed the speaker, and the swamp is what your own wandering does to you when you ignore every warning. What makes it more than a taunt is the sadistic pleasure curdling underneath: the closing chant, wishing the sinking to hurry, drops any pretense of concern and admits the speaker enjoys watching the fool drown. The refrain that no one invited the intruder reframes the whole thing as territorial spite dressed up as caution.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"territorial spite dressed as caution while the speaker enjoys the drowning","built":"closing chant drops pretense of concern, exposing sadism as motive"},
"tool~sweat":{"haiku":"A dreamy meditation on repetition, being drawn under by someone's familiar presence, and losing one's footing in cyclical memory.","opus":"An early Tool song about the vertigo of déjà vu and dissolving consciousness, sinking into a dream within a dream drawn under by an intimate whisper. The physical heat and disorientation mark a mind slipping loose from its moorings.","sonnet":"A hypnotic piece about being pulled under by someone's presence — the narrator keeps sinking into a dreamlike, dissociative state triggered by the way a person whispers, unable to distinguish memory from the current moment.","src":"haiku","fable":"An early Tool track that works as a body before it works as a lyric: the panting, half-drowned cadence enacts a descent into trance rather than describing one. The hunter and fisherman are archetypes the speaker keeps cycling through as identity loosens and the boundary between waking and sleep dissolves into recursive nesting. What sounds erotic (a whisper pulling him under) is closer to regression toward oblivion, comfort and annihilation named as the same homecoming.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"comfort and annihilation named as the same homecoming","built":"the panting cadence enacts descent into trance rather than describing it"},
"tool~the-grudge":{"haiku":"Holding onto resentment poisons the soul; grudges control and imprison, requiring release to transmute pain into growth.","opus":"A meditation on how nursing resentment poisons and imprisons the person who holds it, worn like a badge of bitterness by someone obsessed with control and afraid of admitting fault. Using an astrological reckoning as a symbol of forced reckoning, Tool build toward the transformative relief of forgiveness, turning heavy bitterness into something precious.","sonnet":"A philosophical argument against holding onto resentment — the grudge is shown as a prison the holder builds for themselves, and the Saturnine cycle of consequences demands that it be released; the song moves from description of the trap to an almost ritual letting-go.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about resentment as self-imprisonment, and the astrological Saturn return as the cosmic bill that eventually comes due for it. The first half anatomizes the grudge-holder from inside: someone who mistakes rigidity for strength, clutching an old wound as if it were load-bearing, so terrified of being proven wrong that keeping score becomes a whole identity and a self-built cell. Then the perspective pulls back to a planetary scale, where Saturn's slow return forces a reckoning no willpower can outlast, either humbling you or, if you finally surrender the fight, releasing you. The alchemical ending reframes the whole thing: the grudge is dead weight, an anchor of base metal, and only by dropping it into the water can the leaden thing transmute into gold. The long mantra that closes it enacts the cure rather than describing it, wearing down the listener's grip through sheer exhausted repetition until surrender feels less like defeat than relief.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming"],"means":"resentment exposed as a self-built cell mistaken for strength","built":"the closing mantra enacts the cure by wearing down the listener's grip through repetition"},
"tool~the-patient":{"haiku":"Someone endures tedium and pain in service to healing others, fighting the temptation to abandon the exhausting path while clinging to faith in the process.","opus":"Maynard Keenan on the grinding labor of perseverance, tired of playing the martyr yet still bleeding and holding to belief, confessing he would have quit long ago were it not for the possibility of being made whole, and might quit yet.","sonnet":"About the grinding, exhausting choice to stay on a difficult path — a spiritually depleted narrator keeps telling himself to be patient, to give blood and keep faith, while honestly admitting he might still walk away if there were no reward worth suffering for.","src":"haiku","fable":"A crisis of perseverance staged as a hostage negotiation with one's own faith. The singer catalogues the cost of endurance in vampiric terms—vitality bled out of him drop by drop—then insists, flatly and repeatedly, that he has not moved from where he stands, the repetition itself doing the standing-still. The choruses are conditional bargains: the whole ordeal is only survivable because some reward or embrace is presumably coming, and he admits he'd have quit long ago without that bet. What makes it more than a devotional is the bridge, where waiting curdles into self-coercion—the mantra to be patient repeated until it stops sounding like resolve and starts sounding like a man drilling an order into himself he no longer believes. The lingering threat that he still may walk away leaves the ending unresolved, so the song is finally less about patience than about the exhaustion of pretending to have it.","fv":2,"themes":["endurance & exhaustion","faith & the occult"],"means":"perseverance repeated until it sounds like an order no longer believed","built":"the mantra to be patient drills itself into coercion by the bridge"},
"tool~the-pot":{"haiku":"The song criticizes hypocrisy and self-righteous judgment, calling out those who condemn others while being guilty of the same sins, accusing the hypocrite of being fundamentally dishonest. It's a direct attack on moral duplicity.","opus":"A blistering accusation of hypocrisy, calling out someone who wags a finger in judgment while being just as guilty, corrupt, and intoxicated, echoing the pot calling the kettle black.","sonnet":"A savage accusation of hypocrisy directed at someone who moralizes and points fingers while being neck-deep in the same mud themselves — the kettle calling the pot black — with the narrator tearing apart their self-righteousness as the delusion of someone who must have been out of their mind.","src":"haiku","fable":"A furious dressing-down of a hypocrite, built on the schoolyard logic of the accuser being every bit as dirty as the accused: the pot calling the kettle black, extended into an entire courtroom of the guilty judging the guilty. Keenan stacks the interchangeable masks of the sanctimonious class - the one who deceives, the one who bends the law for hire, the reflection that hands the accuser back his own face - until the distinctions collapse and a rigged kangaroo trial condemns innocent and criminal alike. The drug-slang throughline is a deliberate double edge: the target has clearly been high on something to moralize this brazenly, and the closing sneer at weed exposes the specific pettiness of the offense being policed. What lands is that the song refuses any clean vantage point - the reflected-self image implicates the singer too, making this less a verdict than a portrait of collective bad faith where finger-pointing is the only universal act.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","artifice & performance","politics & society"],"means":"stacking the guilty accusing the guilty until distinctions collapse","built":"the reflected-self image implicates the singer, refusing any clean vantage point"},
"tool~third-eye":{"haiku":"Spoken commentary frames drug use as beneficial to human culture and consciousness; an acid-fueled vision reveals that all matter is energy and consciousness is unified.","opus":"Bookended by Bill Hicks comedy samples about drugs and liberty, Tool trace a psychedelic reawakening, a narrator falling into a visionary trance to recover awareness he had buried. Opening perception becomes the goal, existence revealed as illusory, and he pieces together a forgotten version of himself, greeting that lost identity with the ache of long separation.","sonnet":"Expanded perception achieved through altered states reveals the constructed nature of death, self, and consensus reality — the third eye of the title is both literal vision and a return to something always known but forgotten. The journey inward ends not in discovery but recognition of what was already there.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song frames itself with three Bill Hicks routines: one defending intoxicants as a genuine gift by pointing at the stoned geniuses behind the records people love, one relaying a tripper's epiphany that solid matter is just energy slowed down and every mind is a single awareness watching itself, and one insisting the drug crackdown is really an assault on liberty dressed up as public health. Hicks lays the thesis and the band spends eleven minutes acting it out. The verse imagery falls into two families that keep braiding together, one of tender apparition, a many-eyed presence beaming down and cradling the narrator, and one of grime cleared away, cobwebs and moisture blinked off a tired eye as he climbs back up out of the pit. The emotional turn is that the chorus meets this altered consciousness like a dear friend returned after years apart, then catches the mutual suspicion, each convinced the other went into hiding or bolted while chasing doctrine. That reframes the whole thing: the eye forced open is not new vision but a self the waking mind had walked out on, the nursery line about life being a dream turning out to be something he'd carried all along.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness","identity & becoming","politics & society"],"means":"altered consciousness greeted as a self the waking mind had walked out on","built":"three Bill Hicks samples lay the thesis before the band spends eleven minutes enacting it"},
"tool~ticks-leeches":{"haiku":"A host exhausted by parasitic relationships reaches breaking point, transforming compassion into bitter contempt for those who drained them.","opus":"A furious address to the parasites, fans, industry, hangers-on, who drain the singer and seize everything he offers. His mercy battered to bruises, he snarls back that they can take precisely what they demanded, wishing they gag on it.","sonnet":"Seething contempt for parasitic people who have drained the speaker dry — bloodsucking, exploiting, taking everything while giving nothing — finally reaching the point of pure cold rage and the wish that they choke on what they've taken.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built entirely from the vocabulary of the parasite — the tick, the leech, the thing that drains its host and gives nothing back. Underneath the imagery it reads as an artist's furious address to the people who feed on him: fans, industry, hangers-on who mistook a person for a resource to be extracted. The genius of the structure is how the accusation curdles into complicity — the verses shift from the parasite draining me to us taking all we can, admitting that everyone at this table is siphoning something. That collapse sets up an ending that isn't a curse so much as a dare: you drained me to nothing, so choke on the nothing that's left, because the emptiness is now precisely what you asked for.","fv":2,"fnote":"The extraction was literal: Keenan found the scream punishing enough that the band shelved the song live for roughly a decade — the read's thesis performed on his own voice.","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"accuser and accused collapse as the artist admits everyone is siphoning something","built":"the parasite accusation curdles into complicity before the dare to choke on the emptiness"},
"tool~undertow":{"haiku":"Hypnotic surrender to destructive force and euphoria; a drowning pull that the speaker finds comforting rather than resisting.","opus":"Maynard's baptism metaphor for surrender to an addictive, drowning voice that both terrifies and comforts, dragging him repeatedly to his knees toward a lethal euphoria. Tool render the seduction of self-annihilation as something almost too comfortable to resist.","sonnet":"About being seduced and overwhelmed by a voice or relationship that pulls the narrator under like a current — he keeps returning to it against his will, euphoric even as he drowns, unable to stop himself from going back despite knowing it will end him.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track of Tool's debut works as a portrait of surrender disguised as helplessness, tracking a descent by increments -- the second immersion, then the third -- so that each verse mirrors the last but degrades its terms: the water that once ran brighter and more resonant than the rational, celestial world dims to something lightless and frigid, its heaven diminished by half and its lucidity cut in two. That downgrade is the whole argument. What sounds like drowning is really addiction's logic, the voice from below that beckons even as it erases; the singer keeps insisting he is at ease precisely because he has stopped resisting. The chorus's four-times-repeated command to be silent is aimed inward, an attempt to shut off the part of him that still calls this a fall from grace and being brought low. By the outro the fight is gone entirely, the ecstatic chant collapsing the difference between euphoria and being wiped away -- awake, willing, and choosing the pull that kills him.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"surrender to the drowning voice conducted as a willing choice each time","built":"each verse degrades its own terms — the water dims, heaven halves, lucidity cuts — tracking the descent by increments"},
"tool~vicarious":{"haiku":"Humans feed on tragedy and violence through media like addicts; we watch disasters with fascination, part voyeur and part vampire, drawn to other people's suffering.","opus":"An unflinching examination of the human appetite for televised tragedy, arguing that people live vicariously by watching others die from a safe distance and refuse to admit they all feed on this morbid fascination.","sonnet":"The speaker admits to being compelled to watch tragedies and deaths on television from a safe distance, then challenges the audience to admit they share the same voyeuristic appetite for suffering. It diagnoses human nature as carnivorous and vicarious, feeding on others' catastrophes as a substitute for living.","src":"haiku","fable":"Maynard James Keenan turns the accusation outward and then implicates himself in the same breath: our appetite for televised catastrophe is not a moral lapse but the human condition, watching from a safe remove so that someone else's death confirms we're still alive. The song refuses the comfort of the monster-and-normal-people split, insisting the voyeur and the mourning mother are lit by the same screen. Where a lesser protest song would scold, Tool escalates in the final verse into cosmology, dismissing the sentimental faith that people are basically good: the universe is indifferent, life eats life to persist, and the death-drenched spectacle we crave is just that law made visible. The closing shrug lands the whole thing as confession dressed as taunt, gratitude that the corpse on the transmission is always someone other than yourself.","fv":2,"themes":["spectacle & voyeurism","death & grief","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"spectator's death-appetite named as survival instinct not moral failure","built":"final verse lifts to cosmology so no listener escapes the accusation"},
"tool~wings-for-marie-pt-1":{"haiku":"A son honors his mother's spiritual faith and unwavering love, even as he struggled to understand her beliefs. He grapples with grief and guilt, recognizing her sacrificial love saved him despite his distance from her convictions.","opus":"Keenan's elegy for his mother Judith, paralyzed for decades yet keeping faith he couldn't share, the son reckoning with whether he was worthy of her prayers; a private goodbye at the bedside against fluorescent hospital light.","sonnet":"A song addressed to the singer's dying mother — a deeply faithful woman, immobilized by illness, who prayed for her son even as he felt unworthy of her; the narrator stands helpless in the fluorescent light of her hospital room, trying to find words for the people gathered at her end.","src":"haiku","fable":"The opening half of Maynard James Keenan's elegy for his mother Judith, felled young by an aneurysm and left immobilized yet unshaken in her faith for the rest of her life. The song's ache is the child's survivor guilt turned into a theological grievance: she was the believer, she was the one who prayed, and the crippling still came for her while the doubting son walks away whole. That inversion drives the two unanswerable questions at its center, how does an unworthy skeptic come to be the son of what he can only call an angel. The final movement pulls the camera back to the wake, where he stands among mourners he has no patience for and drafts the eulogy in real time, defending her not as flawless but as someone whose life was spent rather than lived, the saved one being himself. It ends not on grief but on permission, the moment the family agrees to release her, which the sequel half will spend redeeming into liftoff.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","family & blood"],"means":"survivor guilt turned into a theological grievance at an unworthy skeptic's good fortune","built":"eulogy drafted in real time at the wake defends her not as flawless but as spent"},
"toothgrinder~hvy":{"haiku":"Raw sensuality and desire driving physical and emotional intensity. The speaker pursues passionate, uninhibited connection without restraint or apology.","opus":"Rejecting a failed faith, a restless narrator turns instead to raw physical want, courting bitten lips and clawed skin as a sensual escape from despair. Toothgrinder built their reputation on knotty technical metal, but here the New Jersey band strip toward groove and swagger, channeling a fairy-tale mirror into carnal appetite rather than dread.","sonnet":"Raw physical desire crackles through imagery of ripping, rocking, and touch — a charged encounter more animal than tender. Religious iconography shatters against carnal feeling, and the only verdict that matters is abandon.","src":"haiku"},
"toothgrinder~i-am":{"haiku":"The speaker seeks acceptance and the freedom to define themselves on their own terms, learning to stand tall and make peace with who they are.","opus":"A plea for self-acceptance framed as prayer, moving from resentment of mankind and fear of living a lie toward the resolve to tear down walls and make peace with the whole of oneself. The imagery of flying with the birds and standing tall lands on a mantra that love conquers all. Toothgrinder tempered their progressive metal with these clean, uplift-seeking passages, favoring vulnerability over aggression.","sonnet":"A plea for guidance from someone who has looked inward and found both renewal and ruin — pink clouds of hope alongside a soul that has quietly resigned from humanity. The appeal is to be taught how to stand, bend, fly, and finally make peace with the self as it is, flaws included, with love as the only force capable of completing the work.","src":"haiku"},
"toothgrinder~my-favorite-hurt":{"haiku":"A narrator distances themselves from past pain while claiming victory, yet confesses they were running from responsibility just as much as the person they condemn. Recovery is tentative and partly illusory.","opus":"Amid ragtime clichés and cabaret imagery, a wounded partner surveys an affair strained by infidelity, alternating between fragile faith that faithfulness can be relearned and a bruised claim to be on top of the world regardless. Toothgrinder loosen their metallic attack into something almost jaunty, letting the breezy front amplify the ache underneath a couple deciding whether to stop or slow.","sonnet":"Two people navigate mutual infidelity and the bruised pride that follows, yet arrive at a fragile, almost defiant optimism. Being on top of the world is possible, the song argues, if both can learn faithfulness — the speaker offering to be an angel in exchange for the other learning to be happy.","src":"haiku"},
"toothgrinder~ohmymy":{"opus":"The disorienting aftermath of a betrayal where the singer, made a fool of, realizes too late how cold and blind both he and his partner became; now alone and haunted, he wonders how to reassemble the broken pieces of the relationship.","sonnet":"Someone blindsided by the collapse of a relationship — possibly through a partner's death or departure — is left alone, humiliated, and paralyzed by fear and grief, unable to understand how things deteriorated so far or put the pieces back together.","src":"sonnet"},
"torche~healer":{"haiku":"Pain and healing collapse into contradiction, where relief requires brutality and safety crumbles. Fields of violence mark the landscape while the speaker demands transformation.","opus":"The Miami sludge-pop band delivers imagery more felt than parsed, a heavy incantation circling shame, danger, and carnage. Fragmentary lines about biting life, striking down, and a field of slaughter gesture at destruction and endurance, the wordless hooks carrying the weight where the syntax fractures. It reads as catharsis staged through sheer sonic force rather than narrative.","sonnet":"A figure called 'Healer' is addressed with bitter irony — taking the fun, inspiring shame, dispensing a 'pill to dust inhaler' — the speaker depicts healing as a grinding, destructive force rather than a restorative one, with a train derailed and a shadow of a neighbour haunting the aftermath.","src":"haiku"},
"toronto-is-broken~always-in-motion":{"haiku":"Relentless momentum and anxiety dominate life; the speaker is driven by external forces they cannot identify, racing faster despite exhaustion. The rhythm of modern life is inescapable and consuming.","opus":"The drum-and-bass producer frames modern momentum as both thrill and trap: a racing heart, dripping sweat, feet that must never slip in a world spinning ever faster. Time collapses and synapses snap as the narrator chases whatever defines them toward a point of no return. The relentless body-in-motion refrain mirrors the genre's own propulsion, exhilaration and dread braided into the same forward sprint.","sonnet":"Someone feels the relentless acceleration of modern life pressing on their body and mind — pulse racing, time collapsing — held in motion by a force that is both exhausting and animating, chasing the thing that defines them while approaching a point of no return.","src":"haiku"},
"toronto-is-broken~blood-rites":{"haiku":"Ritualized violence and dark covenant, the speaker bound through blood and transgression.","opus":"A drum-and-bass invocation built on the imagery of ritual death and resurrection. The narrator begs to be burned, drowned, and reduced to ash in order to find himself reborn on the other side, echoing scripture about the dead rising. Salvation is framed as a violent sacrifice, the self dissolved so it can be remade.","sonnet":"Purification through total destruction — the speaker invites burning, drowning, reduction to ash — and the ritual's purpose is not death but self-recovery: on the other side of obliteration, through skin turned to ashes and the cord of salvation pulled tight around the neck, is the moment of finding oneself.","src":"haiku"},
"toronto-is-broken~breed-feat-reebz":{"haiku":"Trapped in a system where you're bred only to serve and suffer, demons within and oppression without.","opus":"An asylum nightmare voiced from inside institutional horror, patients drugged, sedated and watched like caged fish, existence reduced to breeding and suffering in silence, the forced compliance rendered as sheer dread.","sonnet":"A person trapped in what feels like an involuntary psychiatric system — injected, observed, controlled, told they are sick — rails against a cycle of suffering they were born into and cannot escape, describing institutional confinement as dehumanizing and their torment as enforced.","src":"haiku"},
"toronto-is-broken~circle-feat-sebotage":{"haiku":"The song breaks cyclical abuse dynamics and mutual blame. Both parties are cause and effect; the spark they created burns them, and only splitting the circle brings freedom.","opus":"A reckoning with toxic cycles, the narrator caught in déjà vu and self-inflicted patterns, resolving to split the circle and turn the accusation back on the true cause. The light someone sparked is what ultimately burned them. Toronto Is Broken work in drum and bass, wrapping this psychological loop of blame and release in the genre's driving breakbeats and the chopped, chant-like repetition of the title phrase.","sonnet":"A cyclical toxic dynamic seen clearly for the first time — the other person was always the cause, the speaker the effect, their fighting a divided war designed by someone else. Breaking the cycle means naming it, refusing its gravity, and walking out of the loop.","src":"haiku"},
"toronto-is-broken~digital":{"haiku":"Two people drift through digital transformation as one waits to rescue the other while everything familiar dissolves into null phases.","opus":"Waiting to rescue and be rescued, the narrator feels his mind overtaken as everything changes and shifting phases render him null. Drum-and-bass producer Toronto Is Broken loops these fragments into a hypnotic mantra of going digital, a partner who won't wake standing for a dissolving human connection swallowed by an insistent technological pull.","sonnet":"A speaker dissolving into digital transition waits to be found and rescued by connection, even as phases of change render him null. The pull toward total digitization is partly chosen and partly experienced as something happening to him — consciousness uploading itself into a new frequency, leaving the analog self behind.","src":"haiku"},
"toronto-is-broken~euphoria-feat-reebz":{"haiku":"Chaos and corruption breed rebirth, the speaker emerges from devastation having shed false beliefs, embracing destruction as rebirth.","opus":"Rebirth through ruin, the singer expelled by a deity into a world where meaning has drained away and none of his old convictions held. Welcoming devastation becomes its own high, shaping a self from disorder while conceding no radiance and no rising await, only frozen earth.","sonnet":"A nihilistic rebirth through total disillusionment — the speaker has stopped believing in anything, been spat out by the universe, and now finds a perverse thrill in that destruction, tearing apart the old self and welcoming the dead, finding euphoria not in hope but in the void.","src":"haiku"},
"toronto-is-broken~gunfingers":{"haiku":"The song celebrates uprising and unified resistance. Hands up, figures rise—a viral anthem of visible defiance without actual violence, throwing signs of solidarity.","opus":"A hype-driven rallying cry, brothers ordered to throw hands and gunfingers skyward, figures growing bigger as the crowd ramps up toward an anthem framed as war without violence. It is pure rave adrenaline, aggression channeled into celebration. Toronto Is Broken deliver it through drum and bass, the Mac 10 imagery and stand-up chants engineered to detonate a dancefloor rather than describe any real conflict.","sonnet":"A crowd-unity anthem built around pointed-finger salutes in place of weapons — the energy of confrontation redirected into collective noise. War without violence, signs without silence, momentum building until the whole room is locked in.","src":"haiku"},
"toronto-is-broken~letters-goodbye":{"haiku":"The song documents self-preservation through written goodbye. The narrator prioritizes their own life over saving a partner; actions matter more than apology.","opus":"A farewell letter set to a countdown of final minutes, the narrator refusing to abandon himself just to save someone else, acknowledging their apology but insisting actions outweigh words. Pulling away is the painful thing he simply has to do. Toronto Is Broken cast this clean-sung breakup over drum and bass, the emotional clarity of self-preservation riding the genre's restless, propulsive momentum.","sonnet":"A breakup written by hand because words only come clearly on paper. Not angry, just resolved: the speaker won't hollow themselves out to save the relationship, apologies mean nothing against a pattern of action, and the letter is the only way to actually let go.","src":"haiku"},
"toronto-is-broken~martyrs-feat-sebotage":{"haiku":"Martyrdom imposed by power structures that extract devotion and drain life, numbness as adaptation to systematic exploitation.","opus":"A pounding interrogation of sacrifice and obedience, asking whether one would die for a cause or simply comply while being drained. Bodies bleed on a slate floor as makers mark their martyrs, the pain reframed as cathartic even as the numbed narrator is led down a predetermined path. It probes the thin line between devotion and manipulation.","sonnet":"Submission to systems of power framed as martyrdom — the \"makers\" carve a fate for those who abide, choking on pride, lying on the slate floor, and the pressure is cathartic even as it numbs everything. Following \"the halfwit\" is what marks you, and the path leads only to the floor.","src":"haiku"},
"toronto-is-broken~paragons":{"haiku":"A self-aware critique of playing victim and fake talk: true growth into a paragon requires acknowledging darkness within, transcending victimhood, and becoming interconnected with others' souls.","opus":"A self-confronting piece about dropping the victim posture and the safe hiding, insisting that growth means seeing your own darkness and joining others rather than standing above them. The paragon is earned by honesty, not superiority.","sonnet":"The speaker confesses to playing it safe, playing victim, and losing their way, arguing that genuine growth requires confronting darkness and admitting one's own failures. Becoming a paragon — an ideal — is presented as something only achievable through honesty and the recognition that no one stands above anyone else.","src":"haiku"},
"toronto-is-broken~raw-feat-reebz-sebotage":{"haiku":"Animalistic instinct replaces civility; the speaker gives in to violent hunger and primal desire without restraint or apology, celebrating raw power.","opus":"Predatory hunger rendered as vampiric craving, a voice that has stopped pretending to be human and feeds on its own suffering and demons. The appetite is bottomless, turning self-destruction into a kind of apex-predator pride.","sonnet":"A predatory, vampire-coded persona that hunts by night, sharpens itself on blood and suffering, and cannot stop feeding — the metaphor doubles as addiction and toxic appetite, where the speaker has built immunity to the poison they consume and elevated themselves to apex status through compulsive, destructive consumption.","src":"haiku","fable":"This drum-and-bass collaboration hands its hook to a nocturnal predator that has stopped pretending to be a person, a creature of pure teeth and no warning, gorging on prey through the dark because restraint is simply not in it. The horror twists when that hunger rounds on its owner: the same jaws that stalk the night start feeding on private torment and inner demons, gnawing until the body wears down and the appetite reaches for the vein like a needle. Having absorbed enough cruelty to brew its own venom, the beast spits the stored poison outward and crowns itself an all-powerful apex, yet the confession underneath is bleak and simple, that even total dominion leaves it starving for more.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"predatory hunger that turns inward and feeds on the beast's own torment","built":"the apex-predator boast collapses into a confession of endless starvation"},
"toronto-is-broken~reminisce":{"haiku":"A reggae reflection on aging and life's changes; the speaker recalls childhood games and penny spending, now exchanging pounds for marijuana as adulthood brings different values.","opus":"Sung in patois, the speaker looks back on growing up, contrasting the small pleasures of boyhood with the concerns of manhood, insisting he knows in his heart it's true, amid warnings about violence and gunplay when hard times come.","sonnet":"A man looks back on growing up and the gap between boyhood — spending pocket money, playing freely — and manhood, where habits and spending have changed, reflecting on how much has shifted since youth.","src":"haiku"},
"torrential-rain~home-alone":{"opus":"A chilling stalker's monologue, someone obsessively watching a person walk home, tracing their steps, entering while they sleep, and vowing to possess them entirely, casting his fixation as the only real love they'll ever know.","sonnet":"Written entirely from the perspective of a stalker — the narrator has been watching a woman walk home alone, tracking her movements through her sleep, and the song ends with a threat that she will not make it home tonight.","src":"sonnet"},
"toto~africa":{"haiku":"Romantic destiny and spiritual calling intertwine across continents; the speaker follows an irresistible pull toward salvation through a distant lover who arrives like prophecy.","opus":"A man romanticizes a continent he barely knows, weaving a woman's arrival and mystic imagery into a plea for salvation and connection. Beneath the grandeur it is about longing to escape into something meaningful and cure a fear of what he has become.","sonnet":"A man is drawn back to Africa — to a woman waiting there, to ancient words and drum-echoes — despite the fear of what he is becoming; guided by an old man's urgency and the image of Kilimanjaro rising like Olympus, he blesses the rains as the only act of connection left to him.","src":"haiku"},
"toto~rosanna":{"haiku":"Longing for lost love persists as bittersweet memory, the narrator unable to move forward despite knowing the relationship could never work.","opus":"A sorrowful goodbye to a vanished love, the singer still glimpsing her features and pledging to go the full distance for her, stunned that losing someone this rare could still wound him so deeply a year later.","sonnet":"A speaker addresses Rosanna with the accumulated grief of a lost year: he never thought someone like her would care, didn't know losing her would hurt so much, and now that she's gone he can only promise to meet her all the way — a phrase repeated like a vow he cannot stop making.","src":"haiku"},
"touch-amor~flowers-and-you":{"haiku":"Grief and regret for missed presence haunt a speaker who kept distance while a loved one withered away.","opus":"A son's grief and guilt over a dying mother, hiding behind pride and small deceptions while realizing too late what he'd taken for granted and can never recover. Bolm confronts the ordinary conversation he walked away from and the years he now has to live with the loss.","sonnet":"A son reckons with guilt over his mother's illness and death — watching her wither while he kept walking down the hall, unable to find the words, maintaining appearances with white lies — taking inventory of what he took for granted and discovering it is more than he imagined, bottled up in the cellar of his mental health.","src":"haiku"},
"tova~fever":{"haiku":"Obsessive desire overwhelms reason as the speaker surrenders to intoxication and delusion, finding a believer's faith in someone's lie while their mind fragments into fugue states.","opus":"Desperate, self-sabotaging desire framed as a sickness that can't be resisted, a narrator on their knees and willing to believe any lie just to stay close. The recurring loss of the mind marks how far past reason the wanting has gone.","sonnet":"Helpless attraction overrides reason: the speaker knows they are losing their mind, tripping on tripwire and giving time away, yet cannot fight the fever of someone who could lie and still be believed — wishing they had met before the mind slipped away.","src":"haiku"},
"towns~safe-to-stay":{"haiku":"Finding safety in passive observation of the world; scenery and time change the observer even in stillness. Accepting fear and staying low rather than looking up keeps one grounded and present.","opus":"A quiet meditation on staying small and looking down to feel secure, even as the narrator senses that time moves and scenery reshapes him whether he participates or not. Fallen leaves on a car and a passing dawn train become the texture of a guarded, present life. It admits fear of things out of sight without fully overcoming it, an indie-pop register of gentle, unresolved anxiety.","sonnet":"Ordinary sensory details — falling leaves, a passing train, morning air — become the quiet anchors that keep a person present when anxiety pulls them downward. There is an admission of keeping eyes on the ground to feel safe, a puzzled recognition that looking up at the sky has become difficult, and a tentative acceptance that change happens whether one participates or not.","src":"haiku"},
"traceless~nothing-at-all":{"haiku":"Abandonment and betrayal reduce shared history to ash; the narrator burns memories in futile protest against the erasure of their relationship's value.","opus":"The wreckage of being left for someone new, the narrator burning photos and demanding to know whether the years meant everything or nothing. Melodic-hardcore heartbreak swinging between devastation and the need for one honest answer.","sonnet":"A person processes a partner's sudden departure for someone new — destroying the artifacts of the relationship in grief — and confronts the unbearable question of whether any of it meant something or was always nothing at all.","src":"haiku"},
"trade-wind~lowest-form":{"haiku":"Relentless predation and patience; another waits for vulnerability to strike while the narrator sinks toward surrender.","opus":"Robbed of sleep and hounded by voices worming in through every gap, the singer refuses to plead even as he admits he's descending to the depths, brushing off a lovely temptation as simply not what he wants. Trade Wind, the softer side project of hardcore singer Jesse Barnett, favors confessional understatement, casting this fight with intrusive thoughts as worn-down endurance rather than rage.","sonnet":"Sleepless and besieged by relentless inner voices, a person holds off an adversary that circles and strikes only during moments of exposure. The intruder is acknowledged as beautiful yet unwanted — and the speaker chooses to sink rather than beg, drifting toward the bottom on their own terms.","src":"haiku"},
"trails~all-words-no-poetry":{"haiku":"Exhaustion and apathy corrode meaning, leaving only empty words incapable of genuine expression or connection.","opus":"Numb, self-destructive despair barreling forward on autopilot, everyone locked in a dead-end existence no one dares examine. The real tragedy is the indifference — a life drained of meaning, the title itself naming the poetry gone missing.","sonnet":"About the emptiness of expression when apathy has set in — the narrator drives like a ghost with a broken neck, everyone around them feels like a dead-end, and whatever they produce is just words with no real poetry behind them; the song is self-aware about its own futility.","src":"haiku"},
"trails~porcelain":{"haiku":"Fragility and brittleness characterize existence; the speaker is delicate and prone to shattering.","opus":"A dissociative self-portrait of numbness and quiet dread. The narrator watches color drain out, seals his soul in an ivory cell until breathing gets hard, and builds futures that collapse before they're even framed. Trapped behind brittle glaze, he lives with a mind that can peer endlessly ahead yet can't feel the present, medicating and managing his way through.","sonnet":"Dissociation and emotional numbness — soul locked in a cage of ivory, colours fading to grey, futures constructed and collapsing before they stand; the narrator is trapped in a white porcelain frame where they can see forever but feel nothing. The Goya reference (\"pull a Goya, let me wither\") aligns self-abandonment with dark romanticism.","src":"haiku"},
"trails~snake":{"haiku":"Self-sabotage tightens around the neck; the speaker carries internal toxins that will eventually strangle from within while reconstructing endlessly.","opus":"A self-destructive cycle of killing and reinventing the self, carrying a snake coiled at the neck under crushing mental pressure. Hating everything he displays, the narrator burns his own bridges and begs to be taken out of his head.","sonnet":"The narrator carries a psychological burden — represented as a coiled snake around their neck — that could lash out and destroy at any moment. They feel numb, inadequate, and trapped in cycles of reinvention that produce nothing, burning bridges just to have some light. The snake is both a source of power and a threat to everyone around them, and the pressure of it is crushing.","src":"haiku"},
"traitrs~thin-flesh":{"haiku":"A cold, lethal force inhabits the speaker as they roam anonymously; their fragile victims bleed and die, but the speaker cannot release this grip.","opus":"A cold he cannot account for settles into the singer, who swears he'll never depart while frail skin ruptures in the snow and his final words perish unspoken alongside him. Darkwave duo Traitrs steep it in post-punk chill, killing in the white cold and erasing a dimly recalled name, the refusal to release turning threat and devotion into one icy chant.","sonnet":"A figure defined by cold indifference observes another person from a distance, knowing they will never truly reach each other. Thin flesh bleeds without witness in the snow — the speaker carries something dark and persistent that will not release, a quiet violence turned both outward and inward.","src":"haiku"},
"transient~goodbye":{"haiku":"Breaking free from a manipulative fake person, the speaker refuses goodbye and stands firm, rejecting the other's lies and reclaiming their freedom and dignity.","opus":"A defiant severance from a manipulative, hypocritical person who plagues and hates the speaker, who now declares himself free and refuses to be brought down or say goodbye; the read is exposing a fake who fooled everyone including themselves, and standing one's ground against them.","sonnet":"About cutting off a manipulative, fake person who drained the hearts around them — the narrator refuses to be brought down, identifies the other person as a liar who fooled even themselves, and declares they will not say goodbye because they've already mentally moved on.","src":"haiku"},
"trash-boat~alpha-omega-feat-kamiyada":{"haiku":"Describes a cycle of substance abuse, self-harm, and failed attempts at change, with addiction as the alpha and omega of existence. The narrator questions whether resurrection is possible from this repetitive downward spiral.","opus":"A raw account of addiction and mental collapse, cycling through painkillers, self-loathing, and the sense of a wasted decade with no way out. The narrator battles suicidal thoughts and his own demons, oscillating between giving up and defiantly claiming he'll resurrect.","sonnet":"A cycle of addiction, self-destruction, and futile attempts at recovery is depicted through the lens of a person trapped in a loop — prescribed opioids, wasted years, and suicidal ideation alternate with moments of defiant survival, while the title phrase marks the endless repetition of beginning and end.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song that strips Revelation's first-and-last God down to the shape of an opioid loop, where alpha and omega name only the wrecked day that keeps coming back. Trash Boat's Tobi Duncan plays the clean verses as a cold clinic scene, a plea for help met with a shove toward the prescription bottle, the oxy that numbs and crawls under the skin at once. The breakdown then collapses everything into one word hurled at the self, contempt with no story left around it. Kamiyada+'s feature walks the addict right to the ledge, staging the reaper and the meetings kept with his own demons, before flipping into a boast about coming back from the dead. The cruelty is that resurrection reads as another relapse, survival dressed as a comeback, so the closing dare falls on whoever's still watching the man go under.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"resurrection reread as relapse, survival as comeback costume","built":"alpha and omega stripped to the shape of the opioid loop"},
"trash-boat~bad-entertainment-feat-milkie-way": {"haiku":"Media and entertainment numb society with crisis and tragedy, desensitizing audiences to apocalyptic content, making spectacle of human suffering while people lose grip on reality.","opus":"A critique of media as an addictive drug that desensitizes us to tragedy and Armageddon, feeding audiences bite-sized abstracted catastrophe until we crave it; the sting is the speaker's own admission that despite rejecting this bad entertainment, he too needs it.","sonnet":"About mass media desensitization — how tragedy and catastrophe are packaged as entertainment, how people have become addicted to crisis-content and bite-sized outrage, and how the narrator oscillates between rejecting it and knowing they need the chaos anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"Trash Boat aim the record's title-track sneer at catastrophe-as-content: a culture that feeds on crisis until an abstraction becomes fact simply by airing, and every Armageddon arrives pre-packaged as a snackable thrill you consume between yawns. The song's engine is a refusal that keeps failing — the chorus insists it doesn't need this, while the verses admit the numbness has the pull of a fix and there's a comfortable safety in watching the tragedy as long as it's happening to someone else. The bridge, where Milkie Way's voice enters, turns escape-fantasy against itself: a Peter Pan flight toward some higher, purer altitude curdles into the confession that the speaker chooses what to submit to, so the escape is really complicity dressed up as innocence. The whole build exists to land its last line, where the sung disavowal — we don't need it — finally flips into a prayerful admission of craving, and the mask of critique drops to reveal the appetite underneath. It's a protest song honest enough to name the protester as another addict of the feed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape"],"means":"protest song honest enough to name the protester as an addict","built":"escape-fantasy bridge curdles into complicity, dismantling the critique"},
"trash-boat~be-someone-feat-eric-vanlerberghe-of-i-prevail":{"haiku":"Drive to prove worth and become someone meaningful, overcoming self-doubt and inadequacy.","opus":"A snapshot of radicalization and disaffected rage, where powerlessness curdles into fantasies of violence as a shortcut to significance. Trash Boat trade their pop-punk warmth for something bleaker here, channeling the resentment of a generation paying for the errors of the wealthy few, and the terrifying logic that a body might make a nobody into someone.","sonnet":"Powerlessness and social alienation expressed through the dark fantasy of violence as the only path to recognition — \"maybe if I catch a body I'll be someone\" is simultaneously shocking and self-aware about its own desperation. The systemic critique (paying for the mistakes of the 1%, blood on hands, eye for an eye) sits alongside personal rage with no resolution between them.","src":"haiku"},
"trash-boat~better-than-yesterday":{"haiku":"Self-inflicted damage and repetitive failure drain motivation; the speaker seeks incremental improvement without believing in possibility.","opus":"A raw self-portrait of depression and creative burnout, feeling useless and infected by thoughts of death, yet clinging to the modest hope of simply being a little better than yesterday.","sonnet":"The speaker catalogues a pattern of self-immolation — burning alive, imitating without innovating, passion dying — then asks to simply be better than yesterday, accepting that falling away won't be fatal and stagnation is the real threat.","src":"haiku"},
"trash-boat~break-you":{"haiku":"Suffocation and cycles demand destruction; the speaker wants to shatter silence through violent reclamation of power.","opus":"A drive to shatter a destructive cycle and the silence around it, even at the cost of breaking someone—or himself—down. Amid a suicidal low where everything inside turns against him, the instinct to fight becomes the only way forward.","sonnet":"A song about being trapped in cycles of self-destruction and suicidal ideation, with the speaker channeling that internal collapse outward — wanting to shatter the silence, the pattern, and the other person who represents the source of their pain.","src":"haiku"},
"trash-boat~cannibal": {"haiku":"Predatory success mentality where ruthlessness and consumption replace empathy, living like a king by devouring others.","opus":"Self-worth defined entirely by others' judgments curdles into a grim survival code, ruling like royalty and getting ahead by preying on everyone else, with no way to undo the first strike. This British melodic-hardcore band sharpens it into a hook about devouring or being devoured, casting ambition itself as an act of predation between reigning and dying degraded.","sonnet":"A challenge to abandon passive resignation and take from life with predatory self-determination. The speaker addresses someone who measures worth through others' eyes, offering a brutal alternative framework: dominate or be consumed, live like a king or die like an animal, sentiment aside.","src":"haiku","fable":"A poison self-help creed delivered deadpan: win, have it all, rule like a monarch — but only by consuming other people, the cannibal being the price tag hidden inside the winner's mantra. Duncan lets the motivational cadence run straight so the horror surfaces on its own, the sneered aside that the first mouthful leaves no route home turning the whole pitch into a warning about what ambition eats. The opening voice, worth visible only in someone else's eyes and waving off pity, sets up the trade the chorus then closes: to stop being another's tragedy you become the predator, and a reign on the throne still ends face-down in the dirt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","identity & becoming"],"means":"the winner's mantra with the price tag hidden inside","built":"motivational cadence run straight so the horror surfaces on its own"},
"trash-boat~delusions-of-grandeur":{"haiku":"Ambition and self-deception collide as the speaker confronts their inability to change systems beyond themselves; humiliation transforms into refusal.","opus":"A jittery generational complaint about yearning to fix the world without any idea how to start, fed up with self-glorifying, consumer-driven ambition that makes endless remaking of oneself feel like defeat.","sonnet":"About the paralysis of wanting to change the world while being too self-absorbed to know where to start — the speaker is trapped between grandiosity and self-loathing, reinventing themselves constantly without moving forward, and resenting the material grind that consumes every hour.","src":"haiku"},
"trash-boat~don-t-you-feel-amazing": {"haiku":"Addiction and chemical numbness feel better than reality; the high replaces prayer and faith.","opus":"Framing love as a withdrawn narcotic, the narrator flails between craving the poison and daring anyone to judge his disgusting fantasy, defiantly insisting he feels amazing while sinking. Trash Boat's later, more experimental turn suits the queasy irony here, the UK band letting a boast about living the dream double as a portrait of numb, chemical dependency.","sonnet":"Someone revels in the chemical euphoria of a life that looks disgusting from the outside, insisting the feeling is real even as the substances that produce it cycle toward failure. The defiant high and the gaping hole beneath it exist at the same time.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song refuses to let recovery and relapse hold separate vocabularies: the same image of scar tissue knitting shut over a hole serves both the healing and the wound it only hides, and prayers land in exactly the flat, unchanged place the drug once did. Duncan sings the chorus as a dare and a boast — coasting through his own private paradise, obscene, amazing — but the register is defensive, a swagger built to pre-empt anyone who might look too close, and the stray admission that he wants you to think he doesn't care gives the whole performance away. That gap is the point: the amazing is a costume worn over craving, sobriety reframed not as freedom but as a duller poison the speaker resents being handed. By the outro the phrase has curdled from a claim into a question asked of the listener, and the mocking tone leaves it unclear whether it means feel good or feel this hollow — the point being that after long enough inside the disease, the two sensations have stopped being distinguishable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"sobriety reframed as a duller poison the speaker resents being handed","built":"outro tips the boast into a question as the amazing and the hollow become indistinguishable"},
"trash-boat~filthy-righteous-feat-kenta-koie-of-crossfaith":{"haiku":"Righteousness becomes its own corruption as the speaker sees false prophets claiming moral authority while secretly bleeding, the cost of maintaining an altar of principles.","opus":"Two people locked in a cycle of fighting until the reason is forgotten, each casting themselves as protagonist and the other as villain. The hard lesson, borrowed from Revelation, is that releasing your grip is the only way before the story can end, before you just rebuild the same wall and scratch your name into it again.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a manipulative, power-hungry figure who burns everything down for sport and rebuilds their ego on the wreckage — the speaker resists being consumed by the fight while acknowledging they can't let go, knowing that perpetual conflict is exactly what the antagonist feeds on.","src":"haiku"},
"trash-boat~get-by-then-die":{"haiku":"Shapelessness and meaninglessness define existence as the speaker questions belonging and purpose. Each choice leads nowhere, and failure seems inevitable regardless of effort.","opus":"Existential burnout and a slipping sense of self, someone morphing to fit in while the point of it all shrinks to bare endurance. Virtue and vice tug with equal force, hollowing out each decision, until the self pleads for the earth to consume it.","sonnet":"A reckoning with mediocrity and self-loss: the speaker, underwater and unable to cry, changed shape chasing siren songs and now cannot recognise himself — the earth is pulling him down, and every choice only reveals another song rather than an answer.","src":"haiku"},
"trash-boat~he-s-so-good": {"haiku":"A closeted relationship scandal and family rejection; a queer son mourns his lost mother while his father rejects him. The speaker screams in anger at the family's cruelty.","opus":"A furious, wounded account of a secret relationship exposed, where a boy who is dutiful and tender with his mother is condemned by an austere father who calls him a slur. Grief over the mother's death tangles with love found in someone new, and the narrator's rage curdles into wanting to scream. Trash Boat, a British pop-punk outfit, push their genre toward queer heartbreak and family cruelty.","sonnet":"A queer relationship caught in the crossfire of a family that cannot accept what its son has become — a father who vanished, a mother who found out and whose death the son grieved alone. The fury underneath is directed at parents who raised someone decent enough to be kind to his mother yet cruel enough, by omission and judgment, to make their son's love feel like a secret shame.","src":"haiku","fable":"A scorned narrator tells a young man's whole life from the outside, and the sting lives in the split between the tender chorus and the poison around it: the refrain praises him for being sweet to his mother right up against the reveal that the mother caught the two of them together. Duncan's screamed hook does double duty, because the same phrase that sounds like admiration curdles into a taunt the more it repeats — the mask of the good son laid over jealousy and outing. The verses tighten the cruelty by pulling the parents in: the vanished austere father, the boy grieving then finding someone new, the flat verdict that the family raised a son they don't deserve. What starts as gossip about somebody so good ends as a wound the narrator keeps reopening, the scream aimed as much at himself for still caring.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Duncan has said the song isn't about him at all — it's the story of a closeted gay man he met on tour, kept closeted by a family that wouldn't accept him; Duncan lends his voice, and single proceeds went to the LGBTQ+ charity akt.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance","shame & repression"],"means":"praise that curdles into a taunt the more it repeats","built":"chorus phrase does double duty as admiration and as the outing it performs"},
"trash-boat~idios": {"haiku":"A speaker trapped in self-created psychological hell obsesses over breath and depression while aware they cannot escape patterns of attachment and shame. The song portrays addiction to one's own mental prison.","opus":"A confessional spiral into self-destruction and mental illness, welcoming you into a private hell where we become the compulsions we can't release. The Greek root of idios and idiot underpins its plea to be pulled out before the coma.","sonnet":"About being trapped in a self-created hell of anxiety and bad habits — acknowledging you've become what you can't let go of, desperately asking to be pulled out before you slip under completely.","src":"haiku","fable":"Idios is the Greek word for one's own, the private self — root of both idiosyncrasy and idiot, the person locked inside their own head — and Duncan stages the self as a venue, greeting you at the door of a place he can't leave. Every exit stands open and he still stays put, because the walls are built out of him. The turns build the trap by logic rather than volume. What a person grips hardest is what ends up defining them, so the compulsion he refuses to loosen hardens into who he is, and the vices he lies wide open to register as relief, since chasing the wound stings less than sitting with the helplessness. The bridge drops the emcee's welcoming irony entirely and simply begs not to sink under into numbness — the mask off, the deadness underneath — landing on the couplet that closes the loop. He can't wipe himself out, and he hates that he can't, which is precisely why there's no walking out of the wreckage he built.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"compulsion gripped tightest hardens into who the speaker is","built":"emcee-welcoming irony drops in the bridge to reveal the deadness underneath"},
"trash-boat~liar-liar":{"haiku":"Calling out manipulation and self-serving rhetoric hidden behind moral posturing.","opus":"Contempt for an online grifter who weaponizes cause and children to profit, every word self-serving, the singer admitting his own complicity while refusing the manipulation, tough love exposed as cowardice dressed up as concern.","sonnet":"A furious takedown of an online or public figure who uses manipulation, children, and manufactured moral authority to extract money and attention — the narrator sees through every word as self-serving, and resents being expected to accept this performance as love or truth.","src":"haiku"},
"trash-boat~love-without-needing": {"haiku":"Existential confusion about purpose and authenticity; seeking meaning while resisting the vulnerability that connection requires.","opus":"A searching self-inventory weighs nature against nurture, longing to love without dependence yet stuck marinating in feeling and hunting for meaning, distrusting anyone who claims to carry no regrets. This marks Trash Boat maturing past pop-punk angst into introspection, the narrator prizing the lessons of mistakes over the comfort of pretending he already understands the life he is living.","sonnet":"A person circles the desire to love freely without emotional dependency, while honestly cataloguing how pain has become a convenient excuse, and how little they feel they have to offer. The longing is sincere but keeps running up against a self they find dull and a life they cannot fully understand.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tobi Duncan turns the self-help question of what shaped him — his wiring, his upbringing, everything witnessed — into a diagnostic he can't finish. He casts himself as fresh replacement gears fitted inside worn-out engine housing that still turns over the same way. The title names the impossible goal: to give affection without the needy dependence that turns love into a debt, and each verse tests why he can't, from the seductive alibi of loving his own pain (because a wound explains away every failure) to the reckless bravado of loading his whole existence into a chambered round and refusing to justify it. The mask slips late in the song: family swears he could be anything, and instead of freedom he hears a verdict that he's simply not that interesting, so the swagger reads as armor over a plain fear of being ordinary. What keeps the song honest is its final refusal to lie — he won't trust anyone who claims no regrets, insisting the whole point is to learn from what he could have done and didn't. That reframes the endless wanting not as a flaw to cure but as the only proof he's still trying to make sense of the years he's moving through.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"swagger as armor over a plain fear of being ordinary","built":"title names the impossible goal each verse tests why he cannot reach"},
"trash-boat~maladaptive-daydreaming": {"haiku":"The speaker hides behind fantasy and introversion, starting mental fires to feel alive; they are lost in daydreams where they control the world.","opus":"Trash Boat name the disorder outright, following a narrator who confesses to selling himself out and sabotaging his own life out of spite, then hiding in elaborate fantasies to feel anything at all, clinging to the thin comfort of a mother's love. Private escapism becomes a study of someone surrounded yet alone, who can only reign inside his own head.","sonnet":"Retreating into fantasy to escape a self that feels ruined and shallow, someone sets small fires just to feel warmth and rules imagined worlds because the real one crowds in painfully. The daydream is refuge and admission of failure at once.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study in self-sabotage that keeps outrunning the shame it opens with — Tobi Duncan starts at deserved punishment and a soul sold cheap, then slides through spite-idioms taken past metaphor into the body — a nose cut off to wound its own face, a mouth wrenched raw out of pure contrariness — before arriving at the escape the title diagnoses. The one scrap of tenderness, a mother who still finds him lovely, gets repeated until it curdles from comfort into the lowest possible floor of worth, ironic by the third pass. What the daydream actually offers is control: eyes shut, he rules the world and freezes a whole season on command, sovereign inside his skull because he is powerless outside it. The chorus names the cost of that retreat — he tucks himself into corners nobody would think to search, manufacturing crisis just to register heat, because numbness frightens him more than the damage does. It reads the maladaptive daydream honestly, not as whimsy but as the addiction it clinically is: a private throne you climb to precisely when a full room leaves you most cut off from everyone in it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"private sovereignty manufactured because the full room leaves him most cut off","built":"mother's tenderness repeated until comfort curdles into the lowest possible floor of worth"},
"trash-boat~synthetic-sympathy": {"haiku":"The speaker feels unsupported by false sympathy and questions whether connection is real, spiraling into isolation and desperation for authentic help.","opus":"A song about hollow, insincere comfort from others, with the narrator exhausted by empty games and feeling down, begging someone to prove he doesn't have to face his struggles and fear of dying alone by himself.","sonnet":"A person drowning in isolation and performative empathy pleads to be pulled out — tired of hollow expressions of care, the speaker admits to sinking and begs for proof that no one has to face this alone.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's accusation is that comfort offered can be manufactured — sympathy synthesized rather than felt — and the verses turn that suspicion into exhaustion, indicting a person whose words and meaning never line up while the speaker keeps performing a role he compares to a singer forever landing a half-step under the pitch. What makes the song move is the chorus, which repeats but never stays still. The plea starts as break me out, hardens into pull me out as the down-feeling becomes sinking, and the request for proof that this loneliness need not be carried by one set of hands finally drops its guard entirely in the last line, where wanting company becomes dreading a death with no one in the room. The bridge is the hinge — the speaker rendered vitreous, glass-clear from the flames, daring to be seen through as he breaks — so the arc runs from resenting fake tenderness to admitting the real need underneath was never for sympathy at all but for someone to stay.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance","shame & repression"],"means":"request for company hardens from resentment to bare admission","built":"chorus refrain shifts wording each pass as the sinking deepens"},
"trash-boat~the-drip":{"haiku":"Selfish complicity in systemic violence breeds sickness; the speaker drowns in their own luxury while revolution demands unglamorous, incremental work.","opus":"A guilt-heavy confrontation with political apathy, the singer calling capitalism a human invention while condemning his own comfortable idleness. Its sharpest jab aims inward: yearning to overhaul the system counts for nothing if you refuse to lay even one brick toward it.","sonnet":"A politically self-aware narrator condemns the structural forces that protect capital and suppress imagination while also turning the lens inward, admitting his own selfishness and complacency — 'my anxiety's a luxury, I can't escape the drip' — paralysed between critique and inaction.","src":"haiku"},
"trash-boat~vertigo": {"haiku":"Exploration of impending psychological collapse and circular self-doubt. The singer is trapped between feeling like a fraud and anticipating a disaster that hasn't yet arrived, haunted by half-remembered trauma.","opus":"A self-loathing account of feeling used, abused, and fraudulent, wrestling with suicidal thoughts and his own defects. A looming sense of collapse (vertigo) draws closer, threatening but not yet arrived.","sonnet":"A person teeters on the edge of mental collapse, describing themselves as abused, fraudulent, and trapped with intrusive voices, while a looming sense of crisis draws closer but has not yet broken through. The tension between suffering and survival—whether it is even worth it—runs throughout.","src":"haiku","fable":"A survivor's monologue about intrusive suicidal ideation, sung by a voice that has already been shamed into believing it deserves the worst. The verses run on self-negation used, abused, fraudulent, defective, a reject and Duncan pushes the logic to its bitterly ironic endpoint: if even love arrives short of the wound it means to close and a god himself wishes he could die, then what claim does an ordinary wretch have to relief? The chorus renames the crisis as vertigo, a threat felt at the edge of a drop, and its whole tension lives in a single deferral the fall not yet taken, the urge circling back as something already lived through rather than new. Reading the mask, the song sounds like self-hating capitulation; underneath, that repeated postponement is the actual argument for staying, survival held one refusal at a time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"survival held one refusal at a time at the edge","built":"repeated postponement of the fall is the song's argument for staying"},
"trauma-ray~liftoff":{"haiku":"Abandonment through transcendence; left behind as beloved departs toward distant frontier.","opus":"Absence rendered as spaceflight: someone has vanished upward and outward, leaving the earthbound narrator scanning for any signal back. Trauma Ray wrap this shoegaze plea in cavernous distortion, the pleading for a word turning grief into orbit, a loved one gone somewhere unreachable while the living keep praying for contact that never comes.","sonnet":"Someone has disappeared into the sky, past heaven, into a new frontier, and those left on the ground wait for any signal — a sign or sound, anything — the final repeated please say something the quietest possible expression of grief at the silence left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"traverser~burn-in":{"haiku":"Staring too hard at truth blinds the observer; avoiding perception through lenses of comfort leads only to sensory overload.","opus":"The narrator confronts a self-deceiver, questioning how clearly they can really perceive anything and warning that the truth will scorch them even where they think they're shielded. Traverser cast willful blindness as its own undoing, someone hiding behind a comfortable illusion, while the narrator simply moves on to a clearer view.","sonnet":"A confrontation with self-deception: the lies someone has been living have finally materialized into consequences, and the question is whether they can still see clearly. Looking through a filtered lens may have offered comfort, but the unmediated sun blinds — reality burns away the protective view from outside.","src":"haiku"},
"traverser~collapse":{"opus":"The utter ruin of everything, self, love, pride, avarice, an entire world, greeted with bleak acceptance. The singer questions why he assembled anything at all only to surrender it, cataloguing his mind, heart and deceit as vanished in the wreckage.","sonnet":"A total disavowal of everything built and believed — the narrator watches and participates in a collapse of all structures, identities, and relationships, finding the destruction both inevitable and weirdly relieving as every tie, lie, and need dissolves.","src":"sonnet"},
"traverser~e-m-d":{"opus":"A nihilistic vision of human extinction delivered with a shrug, treating the death of the whole species as no great loss and suffering itself as a choice. Traverser pair grisly self-harm imagery with cold cosmic indifference, sharpening misanthropy into a flat verdict that everyone is getting what they deserve.","sonnet":"A nihilistic, misanthropic song about human extinction as inevitable or even desirable — the narrator describes peeling away at themselves while counting down a global death toll, framing mass death as both unstoppable and voluntary.","src":"sonnet"},
"traverser~no-more-sea":{"haiku":"Dissolution and ending of existence, watching a person fade from the world unable to let them go or prevent their erasure.","opus":"A grim slide from consolation into possessiveness, the narrator poised at the brink and counting down toward a world stripped of light, sky, and horizon, with a chilling vow that his partner won't survive it either. The post-hardcore weight carries the erasure of everything they shared as both mourning and menace.","sonnet":"A figure standing at the precipice of total erasure — light, sky, and sea all stripped away — is held back not by hope but by the refusal of whoever is present to watch them go. The witness transforms from bystander to anchor, making departure impossible through sheer unwillingness to release.","src":"haiku"},
"traverser~star-faux":{"haiku":"A speaker loses themselves in darkness and self-destruction, chasing an illusory identity while spiraling into oblivion and detachment.","opus":"The narrator embraces chaos as where he belongs, feeling isolated as life goes on around him, the nighttime his drug, memory holding a lost connection between two people who once burned bright together. Traverser build toward self-annihilation and oblivion, a cold summons drawing him over the edge back into the dark he calls home.","sonnet":"Two people who once transformed themselves into something luminous together — headlights set on fire, made briefly into stars — now exist in separate darknesses. One is drawn back to the edge by a cold beckoning presence, his mind exploding into oblivion, longing to belong to the night entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"travis-scott~90210-feat-kacy-hill":{"haiku":"Fame and substance abuse seduce and corrode; young ambition feeds luxury while inner emptiness grows.","opus":"A two-part comedown on Hollywood's price: a girl chasing lows and fame down that alley, then Travis's own rise from Houston, drugs and money set against a grandmother's worry and a family finally provided for. Success as both deliverance and hollowing.","sonnet":"A rapper traces a double arc: early scenes of a girl who left her hometown chasing something in Los Angeles give way to his own story of grinding ambition, family obligations, and sudden success — held together by the warmth of a relationship that grounds him even as he moves at a speed that ignores every warning sign.","src":"haiku"},
"treasvre~devils-combichrist-vs-icon-of-coil-remix":{"haiku":"Internal demons and external pressure collapse identity as walls crumble under weight of doubt and trauma.","opus":"A toxic, inescapable attachment in which the other person is both the walls closing in and the devils inside the singer, a presence that takes and breaks him yet whose name he can barely speak.","sonnet":"A person or force the speaker cannot name has become the devil within them, tearing down every wall of self-protection and breaking the bonds they had built — the past refusing to stay buried while this presence holds them down under an unbearable weight.","src":"haiku"},
"tremonti~if-not-for-you":{"opus":"Total dependence on one person as the reason the narrator is still alive; wracked with guilt, he begs them to stay by his side and reassure him, vowing to follow them to the end even as he tells them to run while they can.","sonnet":"A confession of absolute dependence — the narrator would be dead without this one person, has forgotten how to live or desire anything without them, and pledges to follow wherever they run, as long as they keep pulling him back from the floor.","src":"sonnet"},
"trent-reznor-atticus-ross~a-minute-to-breathe":{"haiku":"Time compresses as the speaker gasps for air amid overwhelming pressure. One moment to breathe becomes luxury unavailable in relentless cycle.","opus":"Written for a climate documentary, this Reznor and Ross piece frames ecological reckoning as a collective deathbed. Rain becomes both salvation and judgment as humanity waits, unable to believe in anything yet certain it will be measured by what it leaves behind. Beneath apocalyptic scale runs an exhausted, human wish for a single moment of relief.","sonnet":"Collective reckoning at an unnamed end: waiting for rain to wash things away, trying to believe in anything, knowing we are all to blame and will be judged by what we leave behind — the final plea is not for salvation but for a minute to breathe before the goodbye.","src":"haiku"},
"trent-reznor-atticus-ross~like-home":{"haiku":"Domestic familiarity and belonging paradoxically induce alienation; home becomes uncanny and strange.","opus":"A ghost's love song from the Old West score, where belonging is impossible yet briefly conjured anyway. Reznor and Ross let a haunted narrator confess that a place they had no right to occupy still felt, for one fleeting minute, like home. The tenderness lies in that impermanence: everything held together only long enough to lose.","sonnet":"Two people who don't belong anywhere manage, briefly, to make an alien place feel like home simply through each other's presence — \"in a world that isn't ours, in a place we shouldn't be, for a minute we made it feel like home.\" The speaker who once believed he was never meant to be finds, through this connection, that he may simply have had it wrong, though the moment passes and he's left haunting the hills alone.","src":"haiku"},
"trent-reznor-atticus-ross~secrets":{"haiku":"Two people creating a world apart from others, their intimacy becoming refuge from judgment.","opus":"A hidden relationship framed as sanctuary, two people who can only exist away from everyone else, the secrecy recast not as shame but as a private universe worth more than the visible world.","sonnet":"A hidden love — two people keeping their relationship concealed from the world, burning steadily in plain sight but invisible to others; the secret itself becomes the source of intensity, and the private world they share feels like paradise and its own universe.","src":"haiku"},
"trent-reznor~a-minute-to-breathe":{"haiku":"Seeking pause and respite from judgment and mortality, wanting just a moment to exist without verdict.","opus":"Composed for a climate film, a shared confrontation with the legacy we abandon as the world comes apart, an unwillingness to part ways crashing against the weary begging for a single instant of air amid the wreckage.","sonnet":"About collective guilt and reckoning — stars are falling, people are being judged by what they leave behind, and we're all equally to blame for something; the narrator just wants a pause in the terror, a minute to breathe before whatever judgment comes.","src":"haiku"},
"tri-ngulo-de-amor-bizarro~de-la-monarqu-a-a-la-criptocracia":{"haiku":"Spanish plea to remain unfound and unchanged, with dark suggestions of suicide and emotional detachment about explaining another's disappearance.","opus":"Spanish noise-pop wrapping a corrosive breakup in cool cruelty, where nothing is real and departure is discussed like tidying up a mess. The refrain to be fixed exposes the sickness under the detached surface.","sonnet":"A Spanish-language song about a controlling, toxic relationship — the speaker tells a partner who is going out drinking that everything between them is a lie, and coolly suggests they slit their veins while offering to clean up and explain the disappearance to the family.","src":"haiku"},
"tribulation~hour-of-the-wolf":{"haiku":"Spectral entities and dark deities haunt the speaker, dragging them toward death and destruction of all they cherish.","opus":"Having grown out of Swedish death metal into gothic grandeur, the band stages a seduction by the macabre. Spectral, sorrowful figures crowd the sleepless narrator in the small hours, muttering curses and promising that all he treasures will decay, until he welcomes their embrace, choosing surrender to ruin over any struggle against it.","sonnet":"At the darkest hour of the night, spectral presences close in — priestly figures of sorrow and ruin that whisper inevitability. Rather than resist, the speaker opens his heart to their dark attention, yielding to the collapse of everything cherished.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~18":{"haiku":"Two living dreamlike, drowning in tasteless sweetness; death waits so must stay together through seasons.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~18-19":{"haiku":"Japanese intimacy and coded survival; a couple enacts pretense despite inevitable hell, using shallow sweetness and breath as escape.","opus":"A Japanese lyric of unsettled young intimacy imagines binding a family by blood, living as if in a dream, performing a long act because parting means falling into hell, staying beside someone until death. Osaka math-rock trio Tricot pair this anxious, feverish tenderness of ages eighteen and nineteen with their intricate, off-kilter rhythms, the flavorless sweetness threatening to drown its speaker.","sonnet":"Two young people build a dreamlike domestic life at the edge of stability, performing the shapes of family while a cold wind finally grazes the threshold of feeling. The Japanese lyric circles between numbness and sweetness, the act sustained by something closer to endurance than love, survival dressed as comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-10m17ji": {"haiku":"Accepting minor failures and cycles of repetition as inevitable; life is excuses all the way down, yet finding acceptance anyway.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song of self-forgiveness that never quite arrives. Nakajima frames a life as one long apology — nothing but excuses, she admits, and no, of course that isn't good enough — where even reincarnation is expected only to repeat the same small blunders, so the failing feels less like sin than like a fixed law of her nature. Against that resignation runs the band's signature push-pull, each verse followed by a clipped chant of wanting to descend but being unable to, wanting to fly, to burst: desire and paralysis stacked in the same breath. The title's cruelty lands at the close, where her resolve turns out to be flimsy as tissue, swaying and dissolving at nothing more than someone else's sigh — and the laughter aimed at her is the note the song ends on, mask and wound at once.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"forgiveness withheld from a self fixed in its own pattern","built":"the title names the resolution that the final image destroys"},
"tricot~a-15brwkw":{"opus":"Tricot's angular math-rock thrives on internal dissonance, and this song sets that friction at Shibuya Station at 9 PM. The narrator's left and right brain wage war, daily life exhausts, and a relationship moves at mismatched speeds like Tortoise and Hare, until the honest wish surfaces: to retire, forget yesterday's suffering and live an unremarkable life.","sonnet":"A song about mental and romantic overload set at Shibuya Station at 9 PM: the narrator's left and right brain are at war, daily life is exhausting, the relationship with this person is moving at mismatched speeds like Tortoise and Hare, and their honest wish is to retire, forget yesterday's suffering, and live an unremarkable life.","src":"sonnet"},
"tricot~a-164dsdv":{"src":"web","web":"A playful love song framed as a humour aptitude test — the speaker, feeling gazes on the Den-en-toshi Line, declares she likes funny people and sets the bar at tenth-dan jokester or above; love and katsudon are devoured while young, but everything grows heavy at high speed, sweet lies and all."},
"tricot~a-174j0ay": {"haiku":"Lingering presence of lost love manifests as nighttime demons; vulnerability and unguarded dreams where the past pulls you away.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about the feeling you can hold off all day but not after dark. The daytime self has learned composure — she thought she'd become someone respectable — but night undoes it: the trembling chest can't hide its true voice, and she begs to be carried off into a defenseless dream and hidden from the night monster. The trick of the song is that the monster is her own returning love and grief, not an intruder; the sharp claws she kept hidden are turned inward, so I make myself cry becomes the whole confession — she is both the one shielded and the thing she's shielded from. That closes the loop of the middle verse, where hoarding the feeling alone is named as the source of the loneliness, hope forbidden precisely because it can't be killed. The ending doesn't resolve it: she asks the sound of rain to erase her voice and let her forget, admitting she can't walk any further — the composure gone, the monster simply outlasting her.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"grief turned inward so the monster is the speaker herself","built":"the shield and the threat are collapsed into one pronoun"},
"tricot~a-1aa8hge": {"haiku":"A meditation on holding back words—if we can't say it, we don't have to; technological connection is enough, talent saved for tomorrow.","opus":"Tricot temper their rhythmic complexity with unexpected warmth, and this song leans fully into that patience. Across distance and time, it doesn't matter that two people can't meet or say what needs saying, because this century's technology holds the connection and whatever talent is being saved will find its moment when the spring that changes everything finally comes.","sonnet":"A song about keeping in touch across distance and time — it doesn't matter if you can't meet, can't say what needs to be said, because the technology of this century can hold the connection, and whatever talent you've been saving will have its moment when the spring that changes everything finally arrives. The emotional core is patient, warm affection for someone the narrator is not sure they'll see again.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title promises to say everything (言い尽くす — to talk it all out) and then the chorus never gets the chance: the line breaks off mid-word, no time even to talk, the distant part trailing into a cut-off s... The song enacts its own failure to finish — a conversation that keeps running out of time before it arrives. Around that stalled center Nakajima keeps granting permission not to close the gap: if you can't say it, you don't have to; if we can't meet, we don't have to — the connection held open by civilization, by the century's greatest invention, is allowed to stay a connection at a distance. Against that resignation the verses hold a quieter reserve of hope: a brand-new tomorrow and a pitch-black yesterday, an up-to-the-minute youth about to end, a talent saved up for when it finally sees daylight — and the two verse-questions (may I wait for the spring that changes fate? the century's greatest revolution) turn the invention into a bet that something might still turn, if only it were given time. The whole tension lives between those two: the chorus that can never finish speaking, and the verses that keep asking to be allowed to wait.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire"],"means":"distance held open by a technology that cannot close it","built":"the chorus breaks off mid-word, enacting the conversation it describes"},
"tricot~a-1gs4liy":{"haiku":"Loneliness and isolation; disconnection from others is the central theme.","opus":"Tricot often route raw vulnerability through their knotty guitar interplay, and this plea sits at the extreme of that surrender. A person asks to be unfrozen and touched, aware she has become dirty and no longer white, longing so painfully to be understood that she is willing to dissolve entirely, begging that if she vanishes into another's warmth no one stop it, only watch to the end.","sonnet":"A person asking to be unfrozen and touched, aware that she has become dirty and no longer white, longing so painfully to be understood that she is willing to dissolve entirely — if she disappears into another person's warmth, please don't stop it, just watch until the end.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-1hvd7y5": {"haiku":"A weary resignation to life's brevity and pointlessness—give up struggling, accept loss, just go home early.","opus":"Tricot can render exhaustion without a trace of melodrama, and this song is pure depleted clarity. Done arguing, done being cut by cheap dramatic words, done with the whole situation, she just wants to call the car and go home, the emotional register weariness without self-pity: she knows it wasn't worth it and simply needs to leave now.","sonnet":"A person who is done — done arguing, done being hurt by cheap dramatic words, done with whatever situation she's been in — and just wants to go home, call the car, and get out. The emotional register is exhaustion without self-pity: she knows this wasn't worth it, she's not going to fight it, she just needs to leave now.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title promises a little rest, but the song is an exit dressed as a breather — its steady pulse is the demand to go home and the request for the check, as if the whole exhausting conversation were a bill she's ready to settle and walk out on. Nakajima stages the fight as a cheap TV drama, foreshadowing left dangling, his excuses padded with embellishment, and she refuses to take the bait of getting invested. The song's real engine is its triple-stacked negations, which escalate as she talks herself out the door: it won't be long, so don't bother struggling; I've had enough; I won't fight, won't resist, won't see you again. That last turn is the trick — the refusal to resist reads like surrender but lands as departure, and the pit-stop and the taxi she calls to come right now aren't rest at all but the quickest way off the track.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance"],"means":"non-resistance as departure rather than surrender","built":"the taxi summoned mid-song converts rest into an exit"},
"tricot~a-1iks8v8":{"haiku":"Different essences mixed become poison; we are incompatible yet paired.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-1oc4754": {"haiku":"Struggling paycheck-to-paycheck while zombies consume endlessly; useless in a convenient world, walking invisible through commerce.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title lands as a verdict — not even fit to be bait — and the song spends its length earning it, cutting between two registers that never resolve. On the ground it's attrition: broken talk, cracked relationships, a life too broke and busy to spare the slack even to try, life is short so it's just a way to kill time. Against that grind Nakajima floats an escape hatch, lying back on the moon for a parade nobody can see, waiting nightly for a march of plastic spirits — a private fantasy pointedly made of the same disposable material as the convenience store. Then the WALKING WALKING refrain empties both out: streets of well-fed zombies shuffling under the one thing that shines, the singularly convenient world, and the read turns on the title's placement right before that line — in a system this frictionless and this well-stocked, you can't even rise to the dignity of being prey worth eating. tricot bury the despair in a bright math-rock lurch, so the final unbroken chant of WALKING becomes the point: motion with nowhere to go, the herd that outlasts the fantasy.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape","politics & society"],"means":"a system so frictionless you cannot rise to the dignity of prey","built":"the WALKING refrain empties both the grind and the fantasy at once"},
"tricot~a-1r28wbe": {"haiku":"Summer mistakes pile up endlessly; clarity comes from not seeing, surrendering to seasonal chaos and forgetting.","opus":"A reckless summer night too hot to think straight, embraced as a blur of blissful mistakes-take off your glasses, get it wrong, laugh until it's absurd, and say goodbye to the courtesy of a fleeting, misbehaving world.","sonnet":"A sweltering summer night full of mistakes is too chaotic and absurd to mourn properly — it demands to be laughed at, said goodbye to multiple times, and then definitively forgotten before morning.","src":"haiku","fable":"A summer fling staged as a request to be undone: the too-hot night, the wanting of something cold, and above all the wish for things to stay just barely divisible — 割り切れる carries both the math sense and the sense of a clean break, so the whole flirtation is pre-negotiated as disposable, better the way it is when you take your glasses off and can't quite see. What makes it a tricot song is that the chorus refuses to hold still and instead melts into homophone-chains: to err (誤る) hammered four times into a super summer made entirely of mistakes, a mock-archaic surely-must-not, and best of all 笑える folding into 藁得る — laughable and grasping-at-straws sound identical and become the same gesture. The farewell does the same trick, 左様なら unspooling back into its own etymology so goodbye keeps saying itself, and it all lands on the unruly revelry of this floating, sorrowful world. The reading is that the song hears summer the way it hears words — everything divisible, everything a near-miss of its correct self, the mistake and the goodbye and the fever all rhyming until they're indistinguishable. Nakajima delivers this less as heartbreak than as a shrug that has learned to scan.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","comedy & wordplay","freedom & escape"],"means":"a summer fling pre-negotiated as disposable before it begins","built":"homophone-chains dissolve the chorus so mistake and goodbye rhyme into one gesture"},
"tricot~a-1s52vcl":{"haiku":"Rejecting hollow words; only breath and presence matter; racing to reach the ending, to have lived.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-1vdhydx":{"haiku":"Plankton emotion drowns and dissolves; living as living means just continuing.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-1y2obwm":{"src":"web","web":"A surreal, dreamlike song about youthful fantasy colliding with depressing reality — Canada study-abroad daydreams and summer-mood delusions give way to unfinished homework; dream sequences escalate from walking a red carpet to being chased by zombies before dissolving into haze."},
"tricot~a-2v37d":{"haiku":"Barely holding together through audience chaos; running forward to avoid being pulled under.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-2yzgwl": {"haiku":"Sonic pressure and alienation in daily life; feeling isolated while everyone else moves on, questioning if goodbye to oneself is possible.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being flattened — the shockwave that opens it arrives in slow motion, and by the end the speaker is literally leveled ground that crowds walk across, still the only one left carrying heat while everyone else has vanished. Its engine is the repeated bye-bye, which turns out to be aimed inward: goodbye to myself, said at an early-morning station where the wind of the victim keeps blowing — and on the later passes that victim's wind picks up a question mark, as if she's no longer sure she has the right to the role. Underneath the bravado of wanting to be the nail that sticks up and gets hammered runs the thing she can never say aloud: the beige galaxy she saw the night before, over someone, and the biggest mistake of all — that he seems to still be inside her. The song is the gap between the outward march into silence, worn like recklessness, and that unspeakable warmth that won't cool; the farewell is a person trying to end herself as the only exit left when she can't finish off the days fast enough.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"a farewell to the self that still carries his warmth","built":"repeated bye-bye pivots inward, addressed to the speaker not the ex"},
"tricot~a-2zfts7": {"haiku":"Honest wishes drown in falseness; the more you search the more you lose; caught spinning in circles.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song built on a single cruel inversion: sincerity drains meaning while lies acquire it. Ikkyu keeps returning to the line that she always wishes honestly, only to append that this too sits inside falsehood — the more honestly she wants, the emptier the wanting gets, until searching for it makes it vanish (the more you search, the more it's lost) and the words thin to a bare there's none. Against that, the childish lie is the thing that finally comes to mean something, and the self answers by disowning itself — that's not me, that's not me — and snapping its fingers to transform, as if truth were the costume and the mask the only honest move. The doubled, stuttering phrases (tasteless, tasteless; unshakable, unshakable) enact the emptying they describe, each repetition wearing the meaning down rather than reinforcing it. It ends where it began, meaning going on being lost, the search having proved that the honest wish was only ever a luxury she pictured and never held.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming"],"means":"sincerity drains meaning while the childish lie acquires it","built":"doubled stuttering phrases wear their own meaning down with each repetition"},
"tricot~a-2zftsf": {"haiku":"Memory of someone lingers; can't stop retracing their footsteps; haunted by what's gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about being haunted — literally cursed, Ikkyu Nakajima sings, by the voice of someone who can no longer be heard, the seasons cycling through (spring, summer, autumn, winter) to mark a grief that never resolves into a single moment. What makes it more than a lament is the verb that changes under the same refrain: the speaker first wants to trace the memory of the one who is gone, then to steal it, then to protect it — the escalation charts mourning curdling from wistful pursuit into something possessive, a hoarding of the dead. And the read turns on a moment of self-knowledge tucked inside the fog: the confession of hunting for a beautiful pretext to keep staying imprisoned. That is the song's real subject — not the loss but the addiction to it, the way the mourner keeps turning back (over and over, the lyric admits) because being cursed is easier than being free.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"mourning curdling from wistful pursuit into hoarding of the dead","built":"the verb under the same refrain escalates from trace to steal to protect"},
"tricot~a-2zg8ex":{"haiku":"Living with oneself is exhausting; inner noise demands witnesses; singing is the only way to sustain existence.","opus":"The exhausting inescapability of living with yourself every day, addressing the self as the loudest, most demanding companion; unable to rest or skip out on your own existence, you can only sing the sadness, pain, and loneliness because there's no other option.","sonnet":"The self is its own loudest, most exhausting companion — inescapable, relentless, impossible to silence — and other people are the only relief valve from the noise of being stuck inside your own head.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-32i9":{"haiku":"Physical intimacy and desire replace verbal communication; bodies speak what words cannot.","opus":"Tricot rarely settle, and here their agitation goes fully bodily. Frank desire pushes past conversation toward roaming hands, a tasting tongue, bodies dissolving into each other as warmth climbs toward a released gasp, the band's tightly wound rhythms rechanneled into raw sensation with nothing held back and no story to soften it.","sonnet":"Pure, explicit sexual desire — wanting more than talking, wanting hands gliding across a body, a tongue tasting, skin melting together, heat rising until a cry comes out — no hesitation, just sensation.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-39eanf":{"opus":"Tricot thread private hurt through misdirecting imagery, and this song circles theft both emotional and literal. A shoplifter in a queue, a loot-filled box, a braid swaying insistently in someone's field of vision: something irreplaceable was taken and cannot come back, yet the narrator's inner world stays inexhaustible, never fully seizable even if every birthday goes uncelebrated.","sonnet":"A song about theft — emotional and literal — running through images of a shoplifter in a queue, a loot-filled box, a braid that keeps swaying in someone's field of vision: something irreplaceable was stolen and cannot be returned, but the narrator's inner world is inexhaustible and can never be fully taken, even if every birthday goes uncelebrated.","src":"sonnet"},
"tricot~a-3guh4b":{"haiku":"A lament on invisible suffering and lost visions—once I saw ghost ships bringing treasure, now I'm drowning in conformity.","opus":"Tricot can frame depression as systemic rather than personal, and this song does exactly that. A dreamer who can no longer see the ghost ship that once brought treasure sinks into a bed-ocean, quietly rotting where no one looks, while outside people pay monthly fees for tools to hurt and to grieve, the world humming along fine as only she goes dark.","sonnet":"A person who once dreamed vividly but can no longer see the ghost ship that used to show her treasure — she is sinking into a bed-ocean, quietly rotting in the places no one can see, waiting to be rescued by someone who left, while outside someone pays monthly fees for tools to hurt people and someone else pays for tools to make them grieve. The emotional core is depression as institutional abandonment: the world is fine, only she is going dark.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-3icjqv":{"haiku":"Survivor's guilt and inadequacy questioning why the narrator lives when better people die, saved only by another's art.","opus":"Tricot don't often write directly to the dead, and this survivor's-guilt address, aimed at someone who seems to have been a musician, carries unusual anger. The narrator rages that the world couldn't keep this person alive though their songs still carry others, and rages at herself for being the blunt, insensitive one who survived laughing while the better person is gone.","sonnet":"A song of survivor's guilt addressed to someone who died, probably a musician — the narrator is furious that the world couldn't keep this person alive yet their songs still keep others going, and furious at herself for being the blunt, insensitive one who survived laughing while the better person is gone.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-3icp68": {"haiku":"A sarcastic celebration of mediocrity and manufactured perfection—everything turning out fine, but emptily, repetitively fine.","opus":"Tricot built a career refusing tidy resolution, which makes this ecstatic self-affirmation feel earned rather than glib. Her form, her DNA, her imperfect days and her life as it actually unfolded are all excellent, not because ideal but because looking back it turns out to have been enough, the song a deliberate counter-logic to perfectionism where even the unplanned was full expression.","sonnet":"An ecstatic self-affirmation: everything — her form, her DNA, her imperfect days, her life as it actually went — is 'excellent,' not because it was ideal but because looking back it all turns out to have been enough. The song is a counter-logic to perfectionism: even what didn't go to plan was full expression.","src":"haiku","fable":"A title track that is nothing but praise — 上出来 (well done) and エクセレント chanted until the words go slick — and the trick is where the praise is aimed. It opens pointed outward, an ovation to someone's perfect form, gratitude owed even to the blood and DNA running through them; then it swivels inward until the verdict lands on the speaker's own life: my life, well done. What keeps this from being empty hype is the admission tucked underneath — that nothing went to plan, that the days feel short of something yet somehow full — so the cheer is revealed as a choice rather than a report on the facts. Nakajima's delivery, over tricot's lurching meter, treats satisfaction as a thing you decide to feel again and again regardless of the evidence, and the absurd little roll call of lapdogs — a stubby-legged dachshund wedged between three fluffballs bred for the couch — is the mask slipping just enough to show the willed silliness holding the good mood up. The refusal to read as a comment underneath the applause — you can hear it as pure joy or as someone talking themselves into it, and the song is honest enough to be both.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming"],"means":"satisfaction chosen rather than reported, against the evidence","built":"absurdist dog roll call marks the gap between the joy and its effort"},
"tricot~a-3mqww":{"haiku":"A wistful desire for chaos and youth—wanting to misbehave and rewind to a childhood moment, hiding in crowds while mimicking others.","opus":"Tricot understand the alienation of performed belonging, and this song gives it a sleepless, mischievous edge. Someone who has always faked being human in crowds, dancing with a skeleton and stuffed with expired sweetness, wants for one night to crawl inside the illumination and look from within, finally curious what would happen if the performance simply stopped.","sonnet":"A person who has always pretended to be human in crowds — faking normalcy, dancing with a skeleton, stuffed with expired sweetness — and on one mischievous sleepless night wants to crawl inside the illumination and look at things from the inside. The emotional core is the alienation of someone who has performed belonging their whole life and is finally curious what would happen if they just stopped.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-3nitf": {"haiku":"Watching a stranger who resembles someone; iron heart grows heavy; recognizing a familiar scent from the past.","src":"haiku","fable":"Titled Exposure, this is a song about wanting to be found out — to have the armour stripped off — long after the person who hurt you is gone. It opens cool and fatalistic: catching someone who looks like you on late TV, reasoning that if everything was decided from the start then grief maybe isn't even real, carrying an iron heart that has grown a little too heavy to hold. Forgiveness is granted almost as a chore. But the choruses keep pulling the speaker back into a blue, blue mist where a forgotten warmth and a fierce heat smell exactly like that one day, and that smell is the crack in the composure — the body remembering what the mind has filed away as settled. Nakajima lets the guard slip further in the bridge's giddy nonsense (your eyes are humorous, your smile is the Euphrates, a brain melting into ice cream), the register of someone too fond to stay armoured, choosing on purpose not to swear off it, to stay wrong rather than reformed. So the ending isn't a plea to the ex but to feeling itself: expose me now, tear it all away, let my chest race like it did that day — the whole hardened peace of the verses confessed as a pose she is glad to have blown.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","love & desire","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"armour held up until the body betrays it","built":"the bridge's nonsense grammar enacts the composure cracking"},
"tricot~a-3xuc":{"haiku":"A nihilistic condemnation of existence—speak to trash, accept failure, everyone's disposable, death is just surrender.","opus":"Tricot can be merciless in the guise of encouragement, and this sardonic advice column proves it. Told to go complain to the nearest trash can, the addressee who keeps falling apart is reminded it's all nobody's fault, all God's plan, and the only move is to throw oneself fully into an unprecedented situation, the band cheerleading from a place well past comfort.","sonnet":"A sardonic advice column addressed to someone who keeps falling apart — 'go complain to the nearest trash can' — while acknowledging that all the problems are nobody's fault, it's all God's plan, and the only move is to throw yourself fully into an unprecedented situation. The emotional core is the wry, merciless cheerleading of someone who has given up on comfort and just wants you to go all-in.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-fswtr0":{"opus":"Tricot, an all-women band who carved their own path through a rigid scene, give this defiance real autobiography. It rejects the adults who told girls to dream big then to face reality, refuses the pre-mapped road, insists on walking under its own power however ugly that looks, and declares that only those who let others decide their path have a truly fixed destination.","sonnet":"A defiant rejection of adults who told girls to dream big then told them to face reality — the narrator refuses to follow the pre-mapped path, insists on walking under their own power no matter how ugly it looks, and declares that those who let others decide their road are the only ones whose destination is truly fixed.","src":"sonnet"},
"tricot~a-nb9lh9":{"src":"web","web":"Sung from the perspective of an android: everyone eventually forgets having been human; memories are input and transmitted through a cold metal body that runs across a collapsed world with silver skin reflecting blue — alienation framed as post-human transformation."},
"tricot~a-olp03q":{"haiku":"A desire for liberation and nakedness—shedding false beauty and restraint to swim free, leaving rust and regret behind.","opus":"Tricot often dramatize the distance between a public shell and a private self, and this aquarium image holds both. A woman shedding the armored version she built for consumption admits she is more misshapen and more feeling than she shows, the tank both the cold glass she's been kept in and the dark soda-water she swims when unwatched, the relief and terror of becoming recognizably soft.","sonnet":"A woman shedding the armored version of herself she built for public consumption — she is more misshapen and more feeling than she lets anyone see, and the aquarium of the title is both the cold glass tank she's been kept in and the dark soda-water she swims in when no one's watching. The emotional core is the relief and terror of taking off the scales and becoming recognizably soft.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-rgr9ip":{"haiku":"Doomed partnership where both hide psychological wounds but choose to stay, refusing knowledge of alternative happiness.","opus":"Tricot rarely play love straight, and this declaration comes sharpened to a point. Someone who knows they can never be normal promises to protect their partner and insists that even if other happinesses exist elsewhere, no one is better than them, honing themselves like a sword that will not let its target escape, devotion edging into possession.","sonnet":"A declaration of fierce, slightly possessive love — someone who knows they can never be normal, promises to protect their partner, and insists that even if other happinesses exist elsewhere, there is no one better than them, while sharpening themselves like a sword that will not let its target escape.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-rgvfbh":{"opus":"Tricot frequently sit at the threshold between girlhood and adulthood, and this song lingers there deliberately. Wanting to escape to a fantasy station where magic waits and a different tomorrow is possible, the narrator can't choose between growing up and staying a girl, won't release the enamel bag on her shoulder, and whispers goodnight while trying to recall the hideaway's password.","sonnet":"A song about wanting to escape to a fantasy train station where magic waits and a different tomorrow is possible: the narrator can't decide between growing up and staying a girl, doesn't want to let go of the enamel bag on her shoulder, and whispers that for now, goodnight — the secret hideaway password just needs to be found again.","src":"sonnet"},
"tricot~a-rgwlmj":{"haiku":"A minimalist refrain of stubborn refusal—saying 'I won't give' over and over, a meditation on withholding and resistance.","opus":"Tricot use repetition as structure rather than filler, and here that method becomes the meaning. Built almost entirely from variations on not meaning to give it, the song concerns the involuntary surrender of something deliberately withheld, and each restatement enacts its own failure, every verse insisting on refusal while the act of singing proves it was already given away.","sonnet":"A song consisting almost entirely of the phrase 'I didn't mean to give it' repeated in variations — the subject is the involuntary surrender of something (love, trust, oneself) that was being deliberately withheld. The repetition enacts the failure of the intent: every verse says 'I wasn't going to give it' while the act of singing it proves you already did.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-rgxinp":{"haiku":"Navigating between old and new eras, feeling inadequate and lost; embracing vulnerability and imagination as resistance to being consumed.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-zmlq66":{"haiku":"Cyclical desire and betrayal in a summer affair; willingly drowning in the same dream repeatedly despite knowing it will hurt.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~a-zmybpv":{"haiku":"A nonsensical minimal chant, possibly a children's rhyme or absurdist meditation—meaning deferred indefinitely in pure phonetic repetition.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~artsick":{"haiku":"Art becomes a compulsion for processing emotion; mixing feelings and fleeing impulse, turning people into art to escape reality.","opus":"A painter dissolves into her own art, layering emotional colors until she is stained, forever fleeing the waves of impulse and the darkness that chases her, watching a woman become mere image in a long hallucination. Tricot, the Japanese math-rock trio, pair this dreamlike meditation on creation and self-erasure with their signature intricate, off-kilter guitar interplay, the vertiginous rhythms mirroring a mind slipping between making and being made.","sonnet":"Someone who always escaped into art to avoid the tide of emotion found, in one unguarded moment, that the woman she was watching had become purely aesthetic — just a picture. She asks whether she was living inside a long hallucination, always running from feeling by painting over it.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~bakuretsu-panie-san":{"haiku":"Plankton emotion drowns and dissolves; living as living means just continuing.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~bitter":{"src":"web","web":"Opens and closes with the three-word mantra 'Militarism. Nationalism. Nihilism,' bracketing a confession of exhausted obsession: every face she sees looks like yours, and she feels like falling in love with every single one of them."},
"tricot~butter":{"src":"web","web":"A loneliness-as-butter metaphor: long dark solo nights, a repeated invitation to come near, and the wish to melt at a lazy temperature; the song closes with 'you are butter,' mapping warmth and softness onto the person who could dissolve the solitude."},
"tricot~colorless-aquarium":{"haiku":"Confined consciousness fades in warm captivity, unaware suffocation builds with each breath. The speaker morphs into something dark, complicit in their own erasure, naming it without resistance.","opus":"A caustic meditation on being kept, admired, and slowly suffocated inside a tank of tepid comfort. Tricot's math-rock precision underscores the reversal at its heart: the narrator, praised as pretty, realizes their true consciousness is dissolving. The refrain condemns another as utterly black while asserting one's own whiteness, before collapsing the distinction entirely.","sonnet":"A speaker raised in a lukewarm fishbowl, praised for being cute and never questioning the tepid water until breathing becomes laboured, finally names the other person as \"pitch black\" and themselves as \"pure white\" — though they concede both are the same darkness — and delivers a scathing farewell: no more interference, no more display value, no more words wasted.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~crumb":{"haiku":"Stillness masks interior decay—flowers wilt unaided while the speaker watches passively, understanding that abandonment of care becomes its own form of guilt. Sensation drains away leaving only shell.","opus":"Emotional numbness examined from inside a sunlit, stifling room where flowers rot untended and empathy has been misplaced somewhere out of reach. Tricot render a quiet crisis of feeling, the guilt that inaction itself can be a sin, and a bruised, stubborn wish to be blessed while still alive, exactly as this life is.","sonnet":"A person sealed in a stuffy, sun-drenched room survives on small signs of life — the sound of mail dropping, distant crying — watching flowers delivered four days ago decay while an ice-diluted coffee swirls down the drain, and finding that slipping into sentimentality is the only way to feel authentically present in this life, unchanged.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~dogs-and-ducks":{"opus":"Pure nonsense play-barking dogs and ducks chanted into a giddy, meaning-free romp of sound and la-la-las.","sonnet":"Pure sonic texture with no lyrical argument — barking syllables and la-la vocalising as the entire content, the playfulness of sound stripped of meaning.","src":"sonnet","fable":"There is almost nothing to decode because the track is nearly devoid of lexical content. It is built from the Japanese rendering of a dog's bark, a flat English animal pairing repeated like a chant, and a wash of open-vowel nonsense syllables, so words function as rhythmic material rather than meaning. The lone real hinge is that the barking syllable is a homophone for the borrowed English numeral one, which means tricot's woofing doubles as counting. That converts the animal noise into a metronome for a math-rock trio whose whole delight is odd, shifting meter. Read that way, the canine and the waterfowl are not subjects at all but two syllable-shapes picked for how cleanly they lock into the groove, the band treating the human voice as one more percussive instrument. It is a gag and a thesis at once, a piece about counting bars while pretending to catalog a menagerie, the childish gibberish exactly what lets the rhythmic sophistication slip by as play.","fv":2,"themes":["comedy & wordplay"],"means":"animal noise doubling as a metronome for math-rock meter","built":"the bark syllable is a homophone for one, converting animal sound into a count","fvr":"2.3"},
"tricot~e":{"haiku":"Emotional suffocation and paralysis—difficulty speaking, fading breath, clouded vision, white dissociation—express being trapped and unable to move.","opus":"In romanized Japanese, anxiety and self-alienation: a single cause has everything spilling out wrong, the narrator feeling made to say things by others and unable to move as intended. Before being completely taken over, the advice is to jab in and spit it all out; a chain of near-rhymes (no origin, not hating, no future, don't want to become a mummy) captures a mind spiraling and dizzy.","sonnet":"In Japanese: about being hijacked by something outside yourself — saying things you didn't mean, unable to move as intended, feeling controlled by an external force before it fully takes over. The urgency builds into a demand to break out and expel it before it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~echo":{"haiku":"Wordless connection through hearing each other's inner silence; vulnerability shown without speaking.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~hashire":{"haiku":"Barely holding together through audience chaos; running forward to avoid being pulled under.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~kieru":{"haiku":"Time's passage dissolves identity and intention; cycles repeat until memory fades and the self vanishes into nocturnal fog, presence unmeasurable.","opus":"The Japanese band on dissolution and rest, night and morning handed back and forth between two people as everything fades like mist. The gentle push toward vanishing turns a lullaby of impermanence almost into an invitation to disappear.","sonnet":"Day and night are personified as carrying each other toward disappearance — morning takes you, night takes the speaker, and everything fades like mist. The final lines shift from \"disappearing\" to commanding it: vanish, like fog, completely.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~kobe-number":{"haiku":"Memories of resemblance and loss frozen in time; inability to let go, kept nearby despite knowing it's not the same person anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~melon-soda":{"haiku":"Dissolution and erasure after someone disappears; humanity endures but individual connections don't, leaving only names and lingering traces.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~munasawagi":{"haiku":"Inevitable change and loss following natural law; sadness and suffering pass, seasons change, and the only way forward is to keep moving.","opus":"Sung largely in Japanese, it counsels patience and acceptance of nature's order: things sought stay lost, flowers open only to scatter, the moon waxes and wanes, and time never rewinds. Addressed to someone distant, it wishes them free of sorrow and finds a fragile joy in dancing through the present moment. Tricot are a Kyoto math-rock trio whose knotty rhythms carry unexpectedly warm, philosophical lyrics.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~namu":{"src":"web","web":"A frantic, heavily distorted pico-pop chant built around the Buddhist devotional word 'namu' (meaning roughly 'I take refuge' or 'amen'), played as a childish, whacked-out religious ditty — simple and repetitive, with cartoon-like vocals rather than sincere devotion."},
"tricot~noradrenaline":{"haiku":"A child's reckless decision shatters a classroom; isolation and darkness reflected inward, seeking protection for loneliness.","opus":"A vivid recollection of a school morning where a stuck greeting and a creaking floor spiral into swarming, multiplying thoughts, alienation, and a plea not to be abandoned by a classmate turned confidant. The narrator, having turned the world against himself, begs someone to fight alongside him and never let go. Tricot's Japanese math-rock frames this adolescent panic with restless, angular energy.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~on-the-boom":{"haiku":"Weightless crisis in an incomprehensible situation; humanity tumbling downward together, beautiful and fragile like distant stars.","opus":"A weightless SOS from someone adrift in zero gravity, riding a mysterious flying-object boom while calling out to be answered, neon flicker recalling a hometown star as the fuel runs dry. Longing and helplessness float free of ground. Tricot, the Japanese math-rock band, thread this playful sci-fi conceit of two people alone in space through their knotty, unpredictable rhythms, resistance impossible in the drift.","sonnet":"Two people floating weightless together in a space made of neon and survival instinct, throwing out an SOS as they drift. Beauty is astronomical — brighter than the moon — but the craft is running out of fuel, and holding on may be all that's left.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~on-the-boom-audiotree-live-version":{"haiku":"Technological addiction fragments cognition; information overload exhausts until the body becomes weightless anchor drifting untethered through digital void.","opus":"Two people adrift in a weightless, sci-fi limbo, sending an SOS while riding some strange craft toward each other, language useless in zero gravity. Beneath the playful UFO conceit is a plea to stay tethered and never turn dull or drift apart.","sonnet":"A speaker caught in the drift of weightlessness and distraction — neon lights pulse like a home star, fuel runs out when attention wanders, and the body is in zero gravity unable to resist being carried along. The fantasy of being alone in space with someone, two people floating free from the cutting edge, ends on the question of whether it's even possible.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~oool":{"src":"web","web":"A frustration-and-escape fantasy set in a suffocating office — the speaker endures a boastful superior and a power-cut meltdown, then imagines running away with a lover to elope and get married in a wide open world before stories about quitting after one month consume too much time."},
"tricot~pool":{"haiku":"Consumer conformity masquerades as growth; constant thirsting in tepid comfort waters.","opus":"Japanese math-rock circling conformity and the trap of networks, urging survival while resisting the demand to stay unchanged. It prizes evolution over safety, wary of lukewarm comfort that quietly makes you sick, ending on a stubborn insistence to live.","sonnet":"A critique of social conformity and digital dependence — the lyrics warn against getting caught in the network's net, where habit becomes camouflage and people demand constancy from something that was always meant to evolve.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~pork-ginger":{"haiku":"Autumn craving autumn already gone; precious relationships mark the skin then fade; the luxury of connection remains unknowable.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~potage":{"haiku":"Never belong to one person; promises always break; can only offer a lifetime of tenderness.","opus":"Over shifting time signatures, a tender lover offers her whole life to another yet insists she will never wholly belong to anyone, because nothing is ever absolute and no contract can bind the heart. Tricot, the Japanese math-rock trio, pair caramel-warm domestic imagery with that restless instability, so the devotion arrives braided with a fierce refusal to be possessed.","sonnet":"A woman wants intimacy over world-news small talk — she would rather share something personal over coffee in Ireland than debate who is to blame for anything. She gives her whole life freely, knowing no contract can bind a heart, and that absolute certainty does not exist — yet she gives it anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~setsuyakuka":{"haiku":"Accepting failure and wasted energy as necessary; everything wasted becomes light, and being foolishly strong is better than not trying.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~sukima":{"haiku":"Invisibility holds beauty; the gaps between moments matter most. The speaker counsels faith despite evidence of cruelty, trusting that silence speaks louder than words.","opus":"An urging to treasure the invisible and unspoken, to feel the clumsy, understated love in someone's seemingly indifferent behavior, and to trust the future even when things go wrong, addressing a God who leaves gaps in the heart.","sonnet":"A direct address to both the listener and God — urging trust in what cannot be seen, in time spent apart, in clumsy love expressed as distance — and then turning on God for designing the trap of doubt in the first place, while insisting on the \"gap in the heart\" (心の伱間) as the very space in which something genuine can exist.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~tokyo-vampire-hotel":{"haiku":"Survival in a parasitic city where people want things but never truly live; blood-soaked existence where everything consumed becomes fuel for hollow continuance.","opus":"In feverish images of vitality being drained, figures run breathless with blood in their mouths, feeling alive only in this grotesque hunger while offering everything to a watcher, begging not to be forgotten. Written for Sion Sono's vampire drama, Tricot bend their angular math-rock toward something delirious, letting squalid and lavish lives alike churn toward exhaustion that never quite dies.","sonnet":"Figures drawn by simple desire — presence, validation, warmth — find their life force quietly drained in an environment that offers nothing back. Exhausted and bloodstained, the speaker still runs, blood still on the tongue, unable to stop and unsure where to go, yet offering everything to whoever truly sees them.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~wabi-sabi":{"opus":"In Japanese, a reflection on finding beauty in impoverishment and impermanence (the wabi-sabi aesthetic), where people weep over borrowed sorrows; the singer asks a lover to come for her only on cold rainy days and not to keep her sheltered forever.","sonnet":"In Japanese: a melancholy song about emotional neglect and waiting — someone in a sparse, lonely space watches others cry over their dead, finding even beauty insufficient, and pleads with someone to come on rainy days and speak truthfully since no one can hear anyway, asking them to stop hiding.","src":"sonnet"},
"tricot~warp":{"haiku":"Escaping through impossible science and casual indifference to responsibility; passing time before eventual exit, entertainment while waiting for someone to rescue you.","opus":"A playful physics-fueled ache to warp across distance at light speed to reach someone, invoking the exact speed of light and demanding scientific proof it should take only two seconds to the moon. It slides into darker musing on aging cruelly, complicity in unnoticed wrongs, and a life that took no U-turn. Tricot, the Japanese math-rock trio, spin this restless leap between whimsy and existential unease through jittery, elastic grooves.","sonnet":"An exuberant wish to travel at the speed of light, skip every obstacle, and arrive instantly at someone's side. The song then tilts into a murkier middle age — a life neither distinguished nor disgraced, the fear of time carving itself into you, the universe as exit strategy.","src":"haiku"},
"tricot~yosoiki":{"haiku":"Living carelessly without expectations; refusing sincerity and riding chaotic beats through indifference and calculated casualness.","src":"haiku"},
"trident~kickass":{"haiku":"Survival demands abandoning guilt and embracing instinctual power; self-actualization requires burning bridges and destroying limiting beliefs to liberate authentic strength.","opus":"A self-administered pep talk against fear and self-preservation, insisting no god will make you someone, only action will. The Japanese band turns the hero fantasy inward, killing off the hesitant self to charge toward the thing you actually want.","sonnet":"A rally against self-protective timidity — the speaker was hiding, afraid, letting a habit of escape settle in, and now calls themselves and the listener to release instinct, attack the path chosen, and become the hero not through strength but through conviction. The declaration \"I am the hero\" closes the loop of self-reclamation.","src":"haiku"},
"triptykon~altar-of-deceit":{"haiku":"A narrator is robbed and blinded by someone's greed and deception, never to see the hidden truth or their stolen children.","opus":"Tom G. Warrior's blackened dirge of betrayal and stolen lineage, a man robbed of his children and blinded to a concealed truth. The refrain's grinding repetition makes the deceit feel eternal, a wound that will never be seen or healed.","sonnet":"About being robbed of children and kept permanently blind to a controlling power's deceptions — it's an accusation of spiritual or personal betrayal so total that the narrator will never see truth, never see their offspring, never see anything but the altar of lies they've been enslaved to.","src":"haiku"},
"triptykon~aurorae":{"haiku":"Spiritual exhaustion and physical decay; the speaker's spirit wastes away through suffering and misery, worn down by the relentless passage of time.","opus":"A slow funeral-doom lament voiced by a spirit worn down by conflict and grief, drained past the point of endurance. Beneath the exhaustion runs a faint promise of relief: the worst has already been endured, animosity will fade, and the instruments of pain are being left behind as suffering finally loosens its grip.","sonnet":"A sustained elegy for a spirit worn completely down by suffering — the speaker or subject has endured so much war, hatred, and misery that nothing remains except exhaustion, though the final images suggest hatred will not last and the tools of sorrow have become relics of the past.","src":"haiku"},
"triptykon~black-snow":{"haiku":"Alone under stars in an icy wasteland, the speaker yields to darkness, seeking extinction in an ocean of black snow.","opus":"A cold, nihilistic surrender to nothingness, the narrator enthroned beneath the stars in a frozen dead world, yielding to the dark and resigning himself to being wiped out. Beneath the icy imagery lies a tortured mind too sightless to recognize its own blindness, aching for its own end in a reality it finds hollow and false.","sonnet":"A slow surrender to death and self-erasure — the narrator walks through a lifeless frozen landscape beneath infinite cold stars, yielding to darkness and ultimately welcoming their own extinction as the only honest conclusion to a life that was always contrived.","src":"haiku"},
"triptykon~boleskine-house":{"haiku":"Evokes a haunted location where mystical rituals and dark forces once resided, with the ground charged by past suffering. The song treats the space itself as embodying ancient spiritual corruption.","opus":"Tom G. Warrior conjures the Loch Ness estate once owned by Aleister Crowley, addressing a dead occultist across the ruined ground he walked. It's a meditation on decay and vanished worlds, occult invocation folded into a dirge for time itself.","sonnet":"A meditation on a place saturated with occult history and departed presence — the ground walked upon is invoked as sacred soil, and the evocation of invisible gods and a \"bornless one\" frames the site as a threshold between the mortal world and something beyond it.","src":"haiku","fable":"Tom G. Warrior aims his black doom at the Loch Ness manor where Aleister Crowley once labored at his months-long angel-summoning rite, and the song borrows that ceremony's grammar — names spoken over tortured flesh, deities addressed across some far plane, a star hailed at the horizon — only to strand the working with nothing raised. What the invocation actually delivers is a dead watcher of the loch behind sealed eyes, walled inside the house, while the ritual collapses into one hammered refrain about the ground he crossed and the earth now sealed over a vanished world. The doubled, echoing voices answer their own summons back until the seance hardens into a headstone, the sole spirit conjured being the flat certainty of dirt closing over everything that stood on it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The invocation lines are Crowley's own — the Bornless Ritual he set into the Goetia. The history is real too: he bought the Loch Ness house in 1899 for a six-month summoning operation and abandoned it midway, which is where the curse legend begins; Jimmy Page owned it decades later.","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"invocation that delivers only the certainty of dirt closing over everything","built":"doubled voices answer their own summons until the seance hardens into a headstone"},
"triptykon~breathing":{"haiku":"Suffering that poisons every action and sensation; every touch burns and every breath drains, while the singer seeks to still blood and obliterate hatred trapped in decaying flesh.","opus":"Tom G. Warrior channels chronic depression as a body in which every sensation wounds, every intimacy poisons, and existence itself becomes incomprehensible. The real subject is the wish to still the blood and be released from a life lived entirely in pain.","sonnet":"A song about existing in a body that feels entirely wrong — every sensation causes pain, every contact poisons, and the narrator's inner life is a cleft-ridden darkness that cannot understand why it is still alive.","src":"haiku"},
"triptykon~demon-pact":{"haiku":"Someone resists demonic influence while trapped in a land corrupted by lies and envy; they invoke divine protection to deny dark forces entry into their mind.","opus":"A Faustian bargain that dooms the speaker, who has forfeited the might he formerly wielded and expects to burn on a stake of resentment for keeping company with the infernal; summoning them by name and stubbornly barring his divine savior from his thoughts, he elects damnation and renunciation of the sacred over yielding.","sonnet":"About resisting demonic possession or spiritual corruption while standing on ground already soiled by the lies of predecessors — the narrator names specific demons, refuses to let any lord or redeemer into their mind, and faces being burned on a pyre of envy by corrupt forces.","src":"haiku"},
"triptykon~goetia":{"haiku":"Religious and demonic imagery blur as the narrator seeks salvation and guidance from dark forces; guilt and moral corruption define their spiritual path.","opus":"An inverted devotional that treats the Devil as redeemer and paternal figure. Consumed by guilt and describing himself as raw and broken, the narrator hands over his soul, summons the fallen angel's minions, and welcomes possession to rise, foretelling that self-deceiving, decaying humanity will perish in its own falsehoods.","sonnet":"A bleak ritual invocation — the narrator addresses Satan as father and redeemer while confessing guilt and bodily corruption, calling on dark spirits to possess and elevate them beyond mortal decay.","src":"haiku"},
"triptykon~in-the-sleep-of-death":{"haiku":"A grieving person calls out to the deceased Emily, seeking connection across death; they yearn for her voice and struggle with their inability to reach her or find peace.","opus":"Raw grief addressed to a dead woman, Emily, whose frozen skin and silenced voice the mourner cannot accept, begging her to speak and asking why she abandoned him. The deeper read is the futile yearning to reach the dead and the impossibility of finding peace on the ground she once walked.","sonnet":"A bereaved person speaks directly to Emily, a woman who has died — her skin frozen, her voice silenced by the morning chill — unable to accept her absence and grief-stricken at being abandoned by the one who was the blood in his veins.","src":"haiku"},
"triptykon~tree-of-suffocating-souls":{"haiku":"A soul speaks to a master/god, confessing itself as a lie and deceit, drowning in human suffering and divine torment. The narrator seeks grace and forgiveness while accepting inevitable death.","opus":"A sacrilegious plea to a shadowy overlord, the speaker begging for rescue while admitting he is falsehood incarnate, dishonesty in a mask. As mankind bows and the creator turns a single uncaring gaze, he sinks into fabricated gore, painting God as the author of holy anguish and the wreck of reason.","sonnet":"A voice speaks from inside religious lie and self-delusion — confessing to be the deceit at the heart of belief, as the masses submit and the creator-figure turns out to be a source of suffering and blood rather than salvation.","src":"haiku"},
"triptykon~waiting":{"haiku":"A unified group faces death while bound within an inescapable state, neither conquered by outside forces nor freed—death and bondage are conditions they share.","opus":"Circling around a hushed refrain that the living and the dead are the same, the piece drifts through backward-masked echoes, entwined sleep, and a trace of blood toward a bleak promise of freedom. Fronted by Celtic Frost's Tom Warrior, Triptykon steep this in gothic doom's crushing patience, making a dirge that meditates on shared mortality more than it narrates any event.","sonnet":"Two entities bound together in sleep and dying proclaim an identity so fused that self and other have collapsed. The chant insists on shared fate — bound within, still the same — even as something approaches that may or may not set them free.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~a-gunshot-to-the-head-of-trepidation":{"haiku":"Shooting down fear with violence; brutal confrontation of terror.","opus":"From Trivium's 2005 breakthrough Ascendancy, a furious vow of protection against a domestic abuser who starves and terrorizes his family, threatening to answer any further harm with lethal force. Vengeance builds into a chant to strike back, Matt Heafy's screams and the Florida band's melodic thrash turning a cycle of abuse into a call for reckoning.","sonnet":"A direct confrontation with a domestic abuser — children broken by neglect and terror, a battered mother as witness, a cycle of violence inherited and passed down. The song makes a blunt promise: the abuse ends here, and if reason fails, the metaphorical gunshot to fear itself will be the only language the abuser understands.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~a-skyline-s-severance":{"haiku":"The narrator burns away their old self to break free from haunting patterns; death of the past self enables survival and renewed sensation.","opus":"Matt Heafy staging a violent rebirth, burning down an old self and past life to break free of the ghost of who you were. The paradox that feeling alive hurts drives it, transformation rendered as both drowning and deliverance.","sonnet":"About burning down your old self to survive — the narrator ritually destroys the person they were, finding it harder to live forward than to die symbolically, but refusing to be haunted by who they used to be.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~amongst-the-shadows-the-stones":{"haiku":"Desolation among remnants; isolation amid ruins.","opus":"From 2017's The Sin and the Sentence, this Trivium track is a war elegy of slaughter and desperation, where a starving mother scavenges to keep her child alive while the powerful entrench themselves and profit from death. Its refrain concludes that the fiercer the struggle, the swifter the fall, all of it for nothing, turning the Florida band's thrash-metalcore fusion toward political despair.","sonnet":"An antiwar lament that refuses triumphalism — bodies among shadows and stones, a mother crossing a hellish landscape to feed her child, and an audience too desensitised to look. Every line of effort results in faster collapse: stabbed in the back, crawling, dying for nothing, and the punchline that no one will ever know the joke.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~and-sadness-will-sear":{"haiku":"Sadness burning and marking you permanently; emotional scarring.","opus":"A mourning account of a hate crime, an innocent person bound to a fence, brutalized and abandoned to the cold by attackers who cannot abide difference, with blame laid on clergy and authorities who instruct people to hate. A precise 12:53 A.M. timestamp marks the death. From Trivium's 2005 Ascendancy, the Florida band recast the Matthew Shepard murder as a searing melodic-thrash lament.","sonnet":"A memorial for Matthew Shepard, named only by the time of death — tied to a fence, left to freeze and bleed, killed for being himself by wolves schooled in intolerance. The song moves from parable to precise grief, naming the hour and date, and holds the image of a scarecrow crucified by a society that teaches hatred of difference.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~anthem-we-are-the-fire":{"haiku":"Collective movement transforms individual suffering into unstoppable force; unity of purpose incinerate jealousy and doubt, binding strangers into family through shared conviction.","opus":"A defiant rallying cry built on the bond between band and audience, turning outsider resentment into collective strength and shared identity. It converts the enemies' jealousy into fuel for a family determined to take the world.","sonnet":"A community-of-listeners manifesto in which music unites a family against jealous enemies — burning their lies and standing as an unstoppable horde together, the crowd becomes a fire that cannot be resisted. The tone is pure collective defiance, enemies left to rot in their jealousy.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~as-i-am-exploding-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Self-destruction in real time; violent disintegration.","opus":"A harrowing spiral of intrusive violent thoughts and self-loathing, the narrator crushed by everything he perceives, consumed by his own hatred, and wondering whether wiping out humanity could end the pain, all while collapsing inward. Florida metal band Trivium map this psychological breakdown with melodic thrash that mirrors the internal chaos.","sonnet":"Violent thoughts consuming from the inside out — a speaker who cannot stop seeing the world's horror, who fantasises about purging it all, while simultaneously imploding with self-hatred and isolation. The world keeps turning as he burns, the contradiction between inward collapse and outward normalcy the only truth the song offers.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~at-the-end-of-this-war":{"haiku":"War consumes all life and purpose; victory brings only death and emptiness, with no escape from the cycle of violence.","opus":"An end-times combat-and-undeath scenario voiced by the resurrected dead, cautioning the foe to strike the skull because what lacks vitality cannot be slain. With mankind's downfall achieved and gore staining their palms, the refrain that nothing awaits when the fighting stops, only void, forever, frames total, self-consuming annihilation.","sonnet":"A nihilistic zombie or war narrative where neither side survives and nothing is redeemed — the undead or the soldiers who fight them both go into the earth in the end, the message being that this particular conflict has no winner and no purpose.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~beauty-in-the-sorrow":{"haiku":"The song questions whether early loss and inherited lies held meaning, while observing death and graves. It searches for beauty within sorrow and attempts to reconcile paternal deceptions with lived experience.","opus":"Grieving a young death and trying to salvage meaning from it, questioning the lies inherited from fathers while standing at a grave. The violence hinted at, shots and red flowers, gives the search for beauty in loss a raw, specific ache.","sonnet":"An elegy for someone who died young — the narrator is left with letters, red flowers, and the unresolvable question of whether any of it mattered, trying to find meaning in loss while knowing the lies told by those who raised them made grief even harder to process.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~betrayer":{"haiku":"A trusted person revealed as a calculating liar who thrives on exploitation; the narrator reclaims agency by refusing victimhood and declaring they will never again submit.","opus":"A scorched-earth reckoning with a two-faced liar who took everything and felt nothing, branded with a traitor's name drawn from Capoeira lore. It's the refusal to be anyone's victim again, contempt for a heartless deceiver hardening into resolve.","sonnet":"A furious condemnation of someone who took everything offered and gave nothing back — calculated, without guilt or conscience, the narrator strips them of sympathy and names them like a mythic traitor.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~beyond-oblivion":{"haiku":"Humanity's creations lead inevitably to extinction and ruin. The song mourns a dead future where humanity's own inventions deliver apocalypse.","opus":"An apocalyptic reckoning with humanity's self-destruction, the survivors surveying empty graves and a dead sun, indicting the reckless rush toward inventions built to end all life, with a creator too ashamed to look.","sonnet":"Humanity has engineered its own extinction through unchecked creation and blind forward motion, and the song surveys the aftermath — cold graves, a dark sun, a dead road — asking what we have done to the future.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~black":{"haiku":"A parasite humanity decays in emptiness, as a dark force opens within them, bringing termination and consuming their soul until nothing remains but void.","opus":"A claustrophobic metalcore vision of despair as a devouring void, the narrator rotting in a cell while an unnamed blackness stares into the soul and waits to consume him. Doom rendered as an appetite that cannot be escaped.","sonnet":"An oppressive, all-consuming darkness is bearing down on the speaker — figured as a predator waiting to devour their soul — and there's no way out, only the certainty of annihilation.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~bleed-into-me-acoustic":{"haiku":"Emotional mutual bleeding where two people share pain and empathy, their suffering intermixed until stories and truths reveal tragic realities.","opus":"A plea for empathy from someone fading into invisibility, asking another to bleed a little feeling into him as silence and tragedy loom, struggles and dreams blurring together before an inevitable fall. This stripped acoustic version lets that vulnerability breathe. Trivium, the Florida metalcore band, softened their usual assault here into something wounded and confessional, a melancholy meditation on disappearing quietly in a crowded, indifferent city.","sonnet":"A plea for emotional reciprocity between two people who are both disappearing — one visible only as a ghost in a city, the other withholding feeling until silence becomes its own verdict. The speaker drifts toward absence but asks, before going, that the truth be told: not just between them but to everyone, the story and the honesty neither of them managed while there was still time.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~brave-this-storm":{"haiku":"Humanity faces apocalyptic judgment for complicity in violence and decay, with waves of destruction representing collective consequence for apathy and moral failure. The song grapples with how to endure inevitable reckoning.","opus":"An apocalyptic reckoning in which humankind is cast as the guilty engine of its own ruin, buried under surging tides and rot. The narrator doubts he can withstand the onslaught and wrestles with whether to simply concede that everything is beyond saving, the band framing extinction as a self-inflicted verdict.","sonnet":"Humanity is cast as the architect of its own apocalypse — violent, apathetic, and consumed by decay — and a rising storm of punishment is both deserved and unstoppable, with the narrator admitting they cannot endure or survive the reckoning they helped bring about.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~built-to-fall":{"haiku":"The speaker views someone as a spreading infection and cancer that must be destroyed; they will bury and destroy this person whom they frame as inherently destined to fall.","opus":"Addressing a person the narrator experiences as a cancer or disease eating him alive from the inside. Torn between not wanting to hate them and the damage they've done to his heart, he resolves to bury this monster he sees as built, inevitably, to fall.","sonnet":"An address to someone who is destroying the narrator from the inside — framed like a disease or cancer permeating everything, the song is a declaration of finally burying the source of that pain.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~catastrophist":{"haiku":"The song addresses a destructive force that has stolen innocence and devastated the world, consuming and poisoning people before they could even breathe. The catastrophist represents systemic corruption and exploitation.","opus":"An indictment of a civilization consuming itself, sold and doomed before birth, ruled by an arrogant force numb to human need. The catastrophist is both the destroyer and the fatalist who sees collapse coming and knows no chance was ever given.","sonnet":"A song about civilizational collapse driven by a corrupt ruling class — humanity is sold before birth into cycles of consumption and denial, and a single catastrophic agent has stripped away innocence and left everyone powerless to course-correct.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~caustic-are-the-ties-that-bind":{"haiku":"Humanity has spawned rage and violence throughout history, bound together by cruel, bloodstained ties. The song condemns collective complicity and the spiral of self-destruction.","opus":"Trivium indict humanity itself as the author of a violent, self-destructive era, framing our bonds and appetites as the mechanism of our own ruin. Beneath the collective condemnation runs a smaller, personal cry from someone lost and begging for direction, seeking strength against foes he can barely confront.","sonnet":"Collective human violence is examined as self-inflicted — the ties binding society together are as corrosive as they are constraining, and the blood spilled belongs to those doing the binding as much as those being bound. A plea for guidance surfaces beneath the ruin, desperate and unheard.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~chaos-reigns":{"haiku":"An introspective exploration of inner conflict and self-loathing. The song describes internal hate built from fragments of pain and wretchedness, giving form to the chaos dwelling inside.","opus":"A self-lacerating declaration of inner turmoil, picturing the speaker building wings from his own broken pieces while hate and wretchedness fester inside and chaos rules within him.","sonnet":"An inward struggle with destructive self-hatred, where the speaker acknowledges that chaos and loathing originate from within and cannot be escaped.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~contempt-breeds-contamination":{"haiku":"Scorn spreading like disease; negativity infecting everything.","opus":"A protest over a racially charged police shooting, an unarmed man taken for a menace and struck by nineteen of forty-one bullets, the killers cleared while the very hands sworn to protect are stained. It calls the outcome not justice but corruption. Off Trivium's 2005 Ascendancy, the Florida band draw on the Amadou Diallo case, forging documented wrongdoing into blistering, outraged metalcore.","sonnet":"A song about the killing of Amadou Diallo — shot 41 times, unarmed, by officers who were acquitted. The song refuses euphemism: this is murder, the blood is on the hands of those sworn to protect, and systemic contempt for life breeds more contamination each time it goes unpunished.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~cora-o-n-o-tem-idade-vou-beijar-feat-toy":{"haiku":"Liberation arrives through dream and freedom; the future unfolds endless while kissing and dancing celebrate life's immediate abundance.","opus":"An exuberant Portuguese party anthem urging a woman to let go, put on her heels and crown, and dance and kiss the night away, insisting a heart in love has no age.","sonnet":"A Portuguese pop song about the ageless nature of love: free yourself from the nightmare, the heart that loves has no age, and tonight you will kiss, dance, and sing until exhaustion — put on heels, smile, and be the queen of the night.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~declaration":{"haiku":"A metal manifesto rejecting bigotry and oppressive structures, calling for freedom, acceptance, and individual autonomy against forced conformity.","opus":"A blunt anthem for equality regardless of race, gender, or orientation, telling bigots to stay out of others' lives and rejecting a country obsessed with dictating how people should live. It swears to endure any death rather than conform. Unusually direct for Trivium, the Florida band swap their mythic aggression for a plain call to acceptance and self-rule.","sonnet":"A manifesto for personal and social freedom against bigotry and institutional control — religious texts, governing structures, and the contempt of the intolerant all targeted for burning and burial. The speaker refuses to become what they oppose, willing to die a thousand times rather than inherit the poison, and calls for a declaration of freedom that strips back every structure built to constrain how people live.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~departure":{"haiku":"Suffocating emotional and physical pain leads to suicidal ideation—razors, overdose, and gunshots are all contemplated escape routes from unbearable torment.","opus":"A harrowing descent into suicidal despair, the weight caving the singer's lungs as life flashes past and he reaches for a hand only to find nothing left. Razors, overdose, and a twelve-gauge crowd the imagery before a defiant curse at the whole world. Trivium, the Florida metalcore band, channel this raw pain into pummeling riffs and screamed catharsis, an early cut trading their usual heroics for unflinching darkness.","sonnet":"A body and spirit at total breaking point, reaching for help and finding no one there. The imagery escalates from suffocation to self-destruction, each option named plainly — a song about choosing to stop reaching, to bleed out into silence.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~down-from-the-sky":{"haiku":"Vampiric tyrants feed on human wars and conflict; humanity is ammunition in a machine designed for annihilation.","opus":"An antiwar parable about parasitic rulers who gorge themselves on power by profiting from humanity's ceaseless conflicts. Awakened to the world's real nature, the narrator exposes war as engineered propaganda in which ordinary people serve as livestock and fuel for the machine, mass slaughter staged to settle whose deity reigns supreme.","sonnet":"A political song accusing ruling powers of feeding off war — the vampires are the elite who grow fat on military conflict they manufacture, using religion and propaganda to send people to kill each other. The \"fire from the sky\" is both military bombardment and divine judgment.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~drowned-and-torn-asunder":{"haiku":"Complete destruction and dismemberment; catastrophic emotional annihilation.","opus":"A rallying cry for rebellion against those who force-feed their ways to a blinded, frightened society, the narrator promising tenfold vengeance, arson and the drowning of enemies as the battle begins. It is defiance sharpened into a chant of collective uprising. Trivium, the Florida metalcore band, deliver this in their early melodic-metal mode, marrying thrash aggression to anthemic hooks built for shouting back against control.","sonnet":"A rallying cry against a system that controls through fear and blind obedience — the people rise, declare rebellion, and promise to repay oppression tenfold. The imagery is total: enemies drowned, opposition ripped apart, the old order burned down so something new can be seized.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~drowning-in-the-sound":{"haiku":"Overwhelmed by noise and chaos; losing yourself in the maelstrom.","opus":"A fight against fabricated genuineness and the voices dictating identity, the narrator doubting each utterance, clutching at the past and shredding the future merely to survive while submerged in the racket within his own head. It is resistance under strain. Trivium bring their melodic-metalcore heft to this later-catalog track, the growl and clean-sung hook capturing a bid to lift one's own voice above the clamor.","sonnet":"Paralyzed by the noise inside — second-guessing every word before it's spoken, watching authenticity get hollowed out by assembly-line thinking and ghostwritten consensus. The speaker is fighting to stay alive by holding back the past and tearing apart the future simultaneously, barely keeping their head above the mental din.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~dusk-dismantled":{"haiku":"Humanity breeds and self-destructs from internal combustion; the speaker digs inward and falls higher as they collapse. Existence is infinitesimal against vast ruin.","opus":"An image of humanity as breeding insects, infinitesimal against a cosmic collapse, with fire building in the throat and the self bursting from the inside out; digging inward through the ruins of his world, the narrator finds each day of digging down only sets up a higher, more colossal fall into the void.","sonnet":"The narrator digs through the wreckage of their inner world searching for something more, only to keep falling deeper into a void — the larger metaphor is humanity as infinitesimal insects whose combustion of existence collapses inward rather than outward.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~dying-in-your-arms":{"haiku":"Someone describes a toxic relationship as a self-destructive addiction and form of execution, finally cutting free to reclaim their life and breath.","opus":"A romance rendered as addiction and gradual smothering: the lover is a ruinous sickness, both the thing that starves his breath and the fix he can't kick. Violent escape, severing the rope from his throat, carving her away with a blade, dramatizes tearing free of a bond that was destroying him, until she means nothing to him and he claims the life he always wanted.","sonnet":"A relationship recast as a fatal disease — the narrator was being strangled by their attachment to someone, finally cut themselves free, and now insists on living the life they always wanted rather than suffocating in that person's grip.","src":"haiku","fable":"A toxic bond read through two colliding image-families: dependency, drawn as a ruinous sickness that hemorrhages and a craving left to suffocate the moment it is denied, and execution, drawn as gallows, noose, and a body hung far over its former self, so the title's tender phrase gets weaponized — expiring in an embrace becomes a strangling rather than a comfort. The structure enacts an escape narrative: the verses travel from passive smothering to severing the rope and pronouncing the other person dead, while the bridge turns the metaphor literal, swapping helpless hunger for a blade. What looks like a love song is really a severance anthem, the outro's repeated demand for release working less as a plea than as the sound of a liberty already seized — Trivium at their Ascendancy-era metalcore peak using romantic language purely as the vehicle for its own violent reversal.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"tender romantic language weaponised into its own violent reversal","built":"two image-families — disease and gallows — converge on the title phrase"},
"trivium~endless-night":{"haiku":"A person is haunted by traumatic memories shared with friends, unable to move forward or escape insomnia. The song explores how pain becomes permanent and dawn never arrives.","opus":"A survivor wrestles with oddly longing for the ordeal he endured beside his closest companions during the darkest stretch, haunted by trauma that shapes rather than destroys him, waging the same sleepless battle again and again. Sharing the anguish might spare buried memories. Trivium soften their thrashy metalcore assault with melodic hooks, the title a vivid emblem of PTSD and a dawn that keeps slipping away.","sonnet":"Trauma bonded to identity — surviving a dark period with friends only to find the memory of it owns you more than the survival does. Dawn keeps escaping, the night stretches on, and sharing the pain seems like the only possible way through.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~feast-of-fire":{"haiku":"Consumption and burning; feeding on destruction.","opus":"A spiral into ruin, the narrator wading through misery and flame, sinking as his emotions dissolve into numbness while everything around him burns and the plots wait to be filled. Emptiness and decay pull him under. Trivium open a recent record with this, marrying incendiary imagery to the melodic heaviness that has shaped the Florida band across two decades.","sonnet":"Self-destruction as a slow crawl through burning — the speaker is driven by compulsion and entropy, sinking into misery they can't name while the world around them slides toward apocalypse. The feelings blur into nothing and then there's nothing at all, the emptiness the only destination the song was ever heading toward.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~forsake-not-the-dream":{"haiku":"Hardship and collapse test resolve; the narrator urges holding onto dreams despite witnessing everything fall and feeling the pain of survival.","opus":"A vow to push through pain and hardship felt with fresh intensity, gathering strength to confront whatever threatens the narrator and someone he loves. Even as he watches the future the two of them built come apart in front of him, the refrain insists they cannot abandon it.","sonnet":"An encouragement to someone — or oneself — to hold onto the dream being built together even as pain and collapse threaten to bury it, demanding the strength to keep facing what's in front of you.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~he-who-spawned-the-furies":{"haiku":"Drawing from Greek myth, it chronicles Kronos devouring his children to prevent overthrow, and the bloody creation of the Furies as avenging tormentors of criminals.","opus":"A retelling of the Greek myth of Kronos and Ouranos, where a father is castrated, his severed genitals birthing the goddess of love while the fallen blood spawns the vengeful Erinyes. Interwoven is a devoured child's cry to have the teeth pulled out so it can breathe. Trivium, Florida metalcore veterans, thread their technical thrash through classical horror on this cut.","sonnet":"Greek cosmogony retold as body horror — a titan who swallowed his own children to prevent being overthrown, and whose act of violence against his own father inadvertently created the goddess of love and the winged avengers who punish the guilty. The voice trapped inside pleads to be released before it even knows what is consuming it, the mythology collapsing into something raw and personal.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~i-don-t-wanna-be-me":{"haiku":"A man's death from misery and isolation prompts reflection on identity and suffering; living itself becomes unbearable. The song questions the value of continued existence.","opus":"The Florida metal band stages a stark meditation on self-loathing and death, cataloging glass houses and thrown stones before a man falls asleep in the snow and dies alone. The plea against mourning in black, urging celebration at the wake instead, reframes the darkness as something to honor rather than grieve. The counting refrains lend a nursery-rhyme chill to the disgust at simply being oneself.","sonnet":"A man consumed by self-rejection watches a figure who moved two steps forward and three steps back die alone in the snow. The plea to celebrate rather than mourn at his wake frames the whole as an elegy for a self one cannot bear to inhabit.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~if-i-could-collapse-the-masses":{"haiku":"A speaker opposed to conformity burns away its chains, calling others to resist the hand that feeds but suffocates their potential freedom.","opus":"A summons to shatter the chains of conformity, insisting existence is clay to be remolded by those who grasp their own strength, and that depending on whatever sustains you smothers all you might become. Freeing the crowd would let wings sprout. Trivium weld thrash and metalcore into this self-liberation anthem, the paradox of soaring on damaged wings framing freedom as painful, hard-won and worth the price in blood.","sonnet":"A call to break free from dependency on any system or authority that molds and suffocates potential. Life is a pattern to be recut on your own terms; conformity is a leash; true freedom requires the willingness to bleed for it.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~in-waves":{"haiku":"Confronts the question of whether to end one's life and take others down in the process, with death approaching fast and life lacking purpose. The song explores waves of destructive impulses and the desire to drag everyone into oblivion.","opus":"The narrator grapples with an impulse toward a public, spectacular self-destruction and whether he would take everyone around him along with it. Confronting how quickly death nears, he doubts the value of a life that can't endure, picturing himself and others catching fire and going under.","sonnet":"The speaker wrestles with the impulse toward self-destruction and the awareness that death is approaching, questioning the purpose of a finite life and expressing a dark desire to pull others down into ruin alongside them.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title track of Trivium's 2011 reset turns one repeated question into its whole architecture, and the dread lives in how the target keeps shifting. What starts as a private calculus about self-erasure widens outward until the narrator wants to take everyone within reach under with him. Matt Heafy frames the despair as cold arithmetic: if the end arrives quickly and nothing endures, what could any of it be worth? The band's answer refuses solitude, letting collapse spread like a rip current that drags down whoever stands too close.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"private calculus of self-erasure widening until it pulls others under too","built":"one repeated question shifts its target outward until collapse becomes collective"},
"trivium~inception-of-the-end":{"haiku":"Someone begins anew through fire and desire, shaking with breaking hearts as they approach the halfway point of annihilation, sacrificing fear to the flames.","opus":"A galvanizing anthem about seizing a fresh start through fire, sacrificing fear and burning away everything that holds you back, framing an ending as a deliberate new beginning worth fighting halfway toward.","sonnet":"A song about the decisive moment of commitment and transformation — fear is sacrificed, fire cleanses what held the narrator back, and the beginning of the end is framed as a necessary burning-down before something new can emerge.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~incineration-the-broken-world":{"haiku":"The speaker feels incomplete and abandoned, watching their light extinguish as they spiral downward. They collapse into themselves, fragmented and bleeding, crashing into a broken world that feeds on their pain.","opus":"A spiral into isolation and self-collapse, the narrator feeling like a discarded fragment made only to serve others, sensing he's wanted only as a spectacle of suffering, put on display for people who won't otherwise believe him.","sonnet":"A person who feels used, fragmented, and kept dependent on others who only want to feed off them watches themselves spiral inward — the light gone, the world broken — and the breakdown is performed for an audience that only values the display of suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~insurrection":{"haiku":"A rebellion against divine creation and heavenly authority, with the rebel declaring war and bringing destruction fueled by hubris and fire.","opus":"Trivium ventriloquizes a Luciferian revolt: a creature refusing to bow before humanity, forged from fire and declaring open war on heaven out of wounded loyalty and hubris. Ascension becomes annihilation as the narrator vows to engulf everything he hates. The band leans into thrash grandeur and biblical scale, dramatizing pride curdled into scourge and the doomed exhilaration of a rebellion that welcomes its own destruction.","sonnet":"Told from the perspective of a fallen angel, the lyric stages Satan's revolt against heaven — fury at God's favoritism toward humanity, pride forged into war. The declaration is total: ascend, destroy, burn everything above.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~into-the-mouth-of-hell-we-march":{"haiku":"Survivors of catastrophic disaster at sea resolve to pour themselves fully into life before death, climbing from wreckage to face whatever comes.","opus":"A shipwreck in a storm reframed as existential rally, a deluge drowning the weak while the narrator vows to give everything to life before he dies and urges others to make their days matter or waste away. Survivors claw from the wreck as the world ends. Trivium wed Matt Heafy's melodic thrash to this maritime apocalypse and its defiant chorus.","sonnet":"A shipwreck used as a sermon — torrential seas, a breaking mast, and a sinking hull serve as the setting for a refusal to waste whatever time remains. Survivors drag themselves ashore with the knowledge that everything they built was drowned, and the only answer is to march forward anyway, asking that whatever words they leave behind be carried into other lives.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~iron-maiden-bonus-track":{"haiku":"A supernatural killer taunts victims with violent intent, invoking an unstoppable iron maiden torture device that inevitably claims lives regardless of escape attempts.","opus":"A cover of a very early Iron Maiden track that personifies the medieval torture device as an unstoppable killer luring victims to a gory death and pursuing them wherever they flee. Trivium, deeply influenced by the British legends, revive one of Maiden's grisliest originals as a reverent thrash homage rather than a reinvention.","sonnet":"A pursuit that cannot be escaped — the Iron Maiden is coming for you regardless of where you run, wanting blood and getting it, inescapable and patient. The song is less a narrative than a repeated promise of unavoidable doom, delivered with a kind of theatrical relish that strips any moral framing from the chase.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~kill-the-poor":{"haiku":"Social commentary on murder of the vulnerable; indictment of inequality.","opus":"A ferocious satire that celebrates a bomb which erases slum-dwellers while leaving buildings intact, the rich raising glasses as the unemployed vanish and slaughtering the destitute becomes an evening's amusement. The cheerful voice is the entire accusation. Trivium's cover of the Dead Kennedys song lets them channel Jello Biafra's pitch-black assault on class contempt with a straight face.","sonnet":"Savage satire delivered straight — the neutron bomb as the perfect class weapon, property preserved, the poor erased, liberals convinced it is acceptable while champagne flows and dancing resumes. The song never breaks character, which is exactly the point: the logic it describes is real, dressed up in progress and efficiency, and the horror is how cheerfully it proceeds.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~kirisute-gomen":{"haiku":"A warrior rejects the system trying to control them and embraces their own path despite pressure to conform, willing to die for their independence. They fight against those consumed by greed and pursue their own code.","opus":"Built on the samurai's right to cut down a disrespectful commoner, the song is a vow of self-determination against a greedy, deceitful world; the deeper current is refusing to be shaped or buried by others' empty values, answering betrayal with brutal, uncompromising resolve.","sonnet":"Drawing on the samurai concept of the right to cut down a disrespectful commoner, the song is about righteous lethal retribution against the greedy and corrupt, framing decapitation of enemies as both a moral act and an assertion of living by one's own code rather than being buried in other people's shallow expectations.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~leaving-this-world-behind":{"haiku":"The speaker seeks erasure from hell, accepting death as penance for collective sins and crimes.","opus":"A collective reckoning with sin and self-destruction: 'we' dug our own graves and built moats to isolate ourselves, and now there is no floating free of our crimes-only leaving this world behind while trying to atone for everything we've done.","sonnet":"A guilt-ridden departure — the narrator feels so weighed down by accumulated sins and damage they've caused that leaving the world entirely seems like the only way to make it right, though the lyrics are sparse and the sentiment borders on suicidal.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~like-callisto-to-a-star-in-heaven":{"haiku":"A woman is raped by a god in disguise and cast out from society, bearing a child born of violence. Her suffering transforms her into something otherworldly, exiled and cursed.","opus":"A retelling of the Greek tale of Callisto, violated and made pregnant by a god then banished, turned into a bear and almost slain by her own adult son before rising as a constellation. The deeper thrust is raw fury at sexual violence and the victim's doubled punishment, urging that one look past the assaulted body rather than condemn it.","sonnet":"A retelling of the Greek myth of Callisto — raped by Zeus disguised in a familiar form, impregnated, exiled, and transformed into a bear — framed as a story about divine sexual violence and its permanent consequences: a woman's life destroyed by a god who used his power to assault her, her son later nearly killing her without recognizing her.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~like-light-to-the-flies":{"haiku":"Indictment of those drawn to tragedy and suffering, hypocrisy where observers revel in others' pain while blinded by self-denial and deception.","opus":"An early Trivium calling card, this is a contemptuous takedown of a hypocrite who thrives on other people's suffering and hungers for violence. The Florida band pairs melodic aggression with scorn for cowardice, casting the target as a parasite drawn to destruction the way insects swarm toward a lamp.","sonnet":"An accusation leveled at a hypocrite who is drawn to suffering the way flies are drawn to light — they revel in tragedy while performing denial, bleed from the eyes, and hold onto lies as a substitute for truth. The song's disgust is cold and precise, watching someone destroy themselves and others through a self-deception they seem to actually prefer.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~my-hatred":{"haiku":"Anguish and rage fester endlessly, transforming into permanent animosity that defines existence and pursues its object beyond death itself.","opus":"A metal reckoning with an enemy or self that keeps resurfacing no matter how many times it's killed, hatred sworn as eternal and immortal. Rage rendered as something that outlives its object.","sonnet":"A speaker voices an ancient, seemingly indestructible hatred directed at an adversary or at humanity itself, repeatedly stunned that the target survives each attempted destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~no-way-back-just-through":{"haiku":"An omnipresent curse or reckoning that inevitably punishes through its inescapable nature; forward momentum despite its destructive permeation.","opus":"Trivium voice an inescapable, all-consuming dread that speaks in the first person, claiming to be curse, void, and everywhere at once. The song stages a personified doom that surrounds its victim and offers no retreat, only a forced march forward into a reckoning where willful ignorance seals the fall.","sonnet":"A reckoning figured as an all-pervading presence — blight, emptiness, darkness, sacred sound — that has surrounded someone completely and offers no exit except forward. The speaker won't live to see the final justice delivered but will be the force that comes anyway, and the only path available to the trapped person is straight through the thing consuming them.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~no-way-to-heal":{"haiku":"A song of irreversible damage and remorse, where the speaker acknowledges tearing out someone's heart and leaving wounds that have become scars. He runs on empty chasing dead dreams with no path forward and no way to atone for the harm done.","opus":"A confession of fatally striking someone from behind, then living with a guilt that cannot mend: the narrator keeps seeing the corpse and feels the same blade twisting inward. Unable to make amends, he collapses into his own remorse, the killer transformed into his own casualty, out of time and out of any way to redeem it.","sonnet":"A song about being consumed by guilt over harm done to someone else — the narrator knows he's caused damage he can't undo, the wound has become a scar, and no amount of effort can repair what happened. The person he hurt lies \"veiled in red\" in his mind, an image he can't shake.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~of-all-these-yesterdays":{"haiku":"Humanity is damaged and dying, burning through pages of failed yesterdays; the speaker feels rage at reaching an ending they were never meant to survive.","opus":"A depressive reckoning with a life felt as futile, damaged people rehearsing for death while hope curdles into its own flaws. Trivium build toward a bleak image of pressing what little light remains against the skull, rage burning through an unlivable past.","sonnet":"A song about accumulated damage and the suicidal despair of feeling too broken to survive your own history — the narrator is covered in ashes of past failures, pressing what little light remains against his head and dreaming of red, convinced he was never meant to make it this far.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~of-prometheus-and-the-crucifix":{"haiku":"A figure bearing wounds and sacrifice—like Prometheus or Christ—torn apart daily by society's scorn while bleeding their heart openly to inspire others, ultimately turning victim into predator.","opus":"Heafy fuses the myth of the fire-bringer punished for gifting mankind with Christ on the cross, casting the artist or truth-teller as a scapegoat torn open daily. Persecution becomes transformation, the martyr promising to return as the predator that devours its tormentors.","sonnet":"The narrator identifies with Prometheus — punished daily for having given something valuable (fire, creative energy, truth) to others — and with Christ, flesh torn apart by public cruelty; martyrdom fused with the refusal to be extinguished, the scapegoat becoming the lion.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~other-worlds":{"haiku":"People live in disconnected dreamlike states, unaware of larger collapse, trapped in separate realities that keep them lost and estranged.","opus":"A dreamlike dissociation where reality and nightmare blur and the narrator can no longer tell what is real. Beneath the metal it is about retreating into an inner world to escape a broken one.","sonnet":"Two people locked in mutual disconnection — they've lost their minds and their way, dreaming in color while actually living in some parallel reality where neither can reach the other or understand where it went wrong.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~pillars-of-serpents":{"haiku":"A vision of burning away the old corrupted world and its false spirituality, freeing angels and gods from oppressive systems. The song calls for destroying foundational structures of sin so that better times can begin.","opus":"Early Trivium builds a defiant fantasy of raising walls against a corrupt, sinful world and igniting a change toward better times. The drive is toward release and renewal, burning away illusions of angels to seize a new beginning.","sonnet":"A song about tearing down a corrupt world built on sin and replacing it with something real — the serpents and fallen angels are the corrupting forces, and the speaker wants to light the world on fire as an act of purification rather than destruction. It's apocalyptic but redemptive in intent.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~pillars-of-serpents-2019":{"haiku":"Systemic corruption requires complete immolation; old angels fade as humanity constructs earthly hells, demanding destruction of inherited lies before rebirth.","opus":"Apocalyptic imagery of burning a corrupt, sin-built world to force rebirth, gods drowning and angels scorched from the sky. Destruction here is purgative, a demand for the spark that reignites everything into something better.","sonnet":"A world built on sin and corruption is collapsing, and the speaker calls for a purging flame to reignite change — consume, waste, feed, and finally end it, so that dreams of angels are no longer washed away. Pillars and serpents are invoked as the architecture of what must be remade.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~poison-the-knife-or-the-noose-bonus-track":{"haiku":"The world is a minefield of sabotage and violence; humans fuel mutual extinction while the speaker resists through any means—poison, blade, or rope—to avoid complicity.","opus":"A refusal to be dragged into evil and entropy, hellfire licking at the feet as Cerberus waits, the narrator vowing to fight by poison, knife, or noose rather than cave. The world becomes a minefield and battlefield where man kills man toward mutual extinction. Trivium load this thrashy metalcore cut with mythic hell imagery, turning survival and integrity into a war against a corrupt, medieval world bent on destruction.","sonnet":"The world as active minefield where man destroys man, but the speaker refuses to go down into it passively — apathy is the real weapon being used against them. Defiance through any means necessary, turned inward as much as outward.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~pull-harder-on-the-strings-of-your-martyr":{"haiku":"Violent resistance to an oppressive ruler who commits genocide; the singer positions revenge as divine justice. The song frames murder as necessary repayment for mass atrocity.","opus":"A vengeance fantasy against a tyrannical, genocidal ruler, cast in violent and near-sexual imagery of the killer gripping the victim's throat; the narrator styles himself as divine retribution repaying every life the tyrant has taken, insisting he is 'God's why' for the killing.","sonnet":"A song of violent reckoning against a genocidal tyrant — the narrator frames themselves as divine retribution, willing to kill with bare hands for every life the oppressor has taken, framing the act as cosmic justice.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~rain":{"haiku":"Cleansing or drowning; rain as both salvation and destruction.","opus":"Depression rendered as drowning, the narrator dragged beneath the currents of a relentless world by his own dread, filling with disappointment in himself and everyone else, coming to feel that time itself is what destroys him. It darkens toward wanting to end his life. Trivium pair melodic hooks with genuine despair, alienation as a slow going-under.","sonnet":"Anxiety and depression rendered as drowning — tied by anxiety, pulled under by a world moving too fast, lungs filling with disappointment and abandonment. The song escalates from exhaustion to something darker: a rope around the neck, a wish for the end, the final image collapsing any remaining distance between metaphor and literal self-destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~sever-the-hand":{"haiku":"A person rejects religious and authoritarian control, cutting ties to free themselves from manipulation.","opus":"A rejection of hollow ritual and prophecy in favor of substance, warning that the false comforts people cling to become the thing that hangs them, and challenging them to stand on their own convictions instead. The call is to cut free from every authority, divine or human, that holds the leash, delivered with thrash-metalcore fury.","sonnet":"Absolute rejection of religious and social authority as sources of meaning — doctrine is a noose you made yourself, and the only way to live freely is to cut off whatever — god or man — holds the leash.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~shogun":{"haiku":"Warriors must face evil monsters that poison innocence; victory still costs everything, and the fight against wickedness demands sacrifice.","opus":"Trivium's title epic stages an apocalyptic reckoning, a narrator who refuses to stay silent as monstrous forces gather to spread violence and ruin, time offering no relief as death consumes everything. Drawing on Greek myth across the album, the song warns that even winning this war costs more than it's worth, as the world burns toward its end.","sonnet":"A warrior prepares a final stand against demonic forces that have infected the world — the battle is apocalyptic in scale, with seas and skies turning against civilization. Even victory is understood as costly beyond measure, the fight undertaken not from hope of survival but from refusal to yield.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~silence-in-the-snow":{"haiku":"Brutal battlefield imagery where warriors fight to mutual annihilation, their blood staining snow, ultimately leaving the narrator alone and wounded.","opus":"A grim battlefield rendered as artwork, a pale expanse smeared crimson from the slaughter, fighters clashing until every foe perishes in the frost, the narrator ultimately abandoned to solitude as the season turns with only his own wounds staining him. Trivium leaned here into clean, classic heavy-metal power rather than screaming, and that tuneful sharpness heightens the chill of a survivor left in bleak, snowbound isolation.","sonnet":"A winter battle in which the white landscape becomes a canvas for blood — warriors blinded by snow, painting the ground red, fighting until nothing is left. The song shifts partway through: the blood on the speaker's hands turns out to be their own, and the silence that follows victory is indistinguishable from the silence of the dead.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~strife":{"haiku":"A person trapped in internal conflict—anxiety, negativity, guilt, and madness—searches futilely for calm and balance. Strife and struggle perpetuate the bleeding without resolution, leaving the narrator lost.","opus":"A first-person struggle with crippling anxiety, guilt and inner voices, in which the speaker reaches for calm and balance but finds that struggle itself won't stop the bleeding or the pain, leaving him searching in vain for his way.","sonnet":"Anxiety, guilt, and internal chaos overwhelm the narrator, who recognizes that fighting and striving do not relieve the pain — the search for calm and balance feels endless and the suffering self-inflicted.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~suffocating-sight":{"haiku":"A person drowns in insecurity and self-blame, watching time slip away while anxiety consumes them. Their panic threatens those close to them.","opus":"Trivium turn crippling self-doubt into a metalcore siege, voicing a narrator who feels like a fraud of stability, out of time, and complicit in his own victimhood. The dread mounts until it becomes physical, a rising panic that steals his breath as the very people who shaped him threaten to undo him.","sonnet":"A person caught between self-awareness and helplessness watches panic consume them from inside — the same forces that shaped and held them now destroying them with equal precision. Each breath becomes a battle, the shipwreck metaphor made physical as the chest closes and the sands run out.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~the-calamity":{"haiku":"A catastrophic force emerges to destroy all goodness on earth. This calamity eats away existence from within, poisoning and corrupting everything it touches.","opus":"Apocalypse imagined as a monstrous birth, a beautiful shell carrying ruin that eats existence from within. The visceral maternal-horror imagery makes destruction intimate, something gestating inside us rather than arriving from outside.","sonnet":"A destructive force — personified as something beautiful and devastating — is eating away at shared existence from the inside, spreading ruin through everything two people built together until nothing is left standing.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~the-deceived":{"haiku":"Systemic disillusionment where people are trapped by false promises and manufactured belief systems, deceived by institutions that use control to prevent genuine unity.","opus":"A cerebral protest against engineered control, painting society as a deformity where organized wholeness is impossible and paradise is a fiction, urging listeners to deaden themselves to the powers that box them in while a manufactured god commands obedience across the ages. Trivium built it from dense, clinical language, the Florida band training their aggression on ideological trickery and on how promised dreams are designed only to disappoint those who await them.","sonnet":"A portrait of a generation promised utopia and delivered only control — centuries of adherence to a manufactured god, dreams deferred until they expire, a systematic unity that was always a lie. The song refuses numbness as the coping mechanism but offers no clear alternative, only the bleak recognition that the promised have been failed and the foreseen was always this.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~the-heart-from-your-hate":{"haiku":"A person confronts someone's hatred and complacency, urging them to face reality and their own insecurities instead of running away indefinitely.","opus":"An effort to pierce someone's dug-in loathing and finally close out a drawn-out feud, owning one's mistakes while calling out the other's petty scoring and detachment from what's real; the deeper read is that progress means facing one's own doubts rather than fleeing, wondering what could ever tear the cruelty out of them.","sonnet":"About someone who uses righteous anger or criticism to avoid facing their own problems — the narrator has been silent too long, watching the other person always walk away from difficulty, and finally insists on confronting the hate rather than letting it keep running the dynamic.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~the-phalanx":{"haiku":"War's hollowing effect where soldiers who once believed in heroic sacrifice realize there's no divine reward, only dirt and decay at journey's end.","opus":"Trivium sharpen battlefield fury into a meditation on the hollowness of sacrifice. Voiced from inside the slaughter, the song moves through the intoxication of killing and then pivots to bleak disillusionment, the soldier realizing that what once felt like heroic martyrdom reveals only rot and pointlessness. Dying for an empty cause, he concludes, leaves nothing behind but decay.","sonnet":"A soldier's disillusionment mid-war — the song opens as collective violence, a force herding the weak toward fire, but fractures into a single voice who once found dying heroic and now sees only dirt and decay beneath the cause. The ending refuses martyrdom: even if destroyed, the speaker wants to burn into the hearts of those who did it rather than disappear cleanly.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~the-revanchist":{"haiku":"A dark force seduces the vulnerable with false salvation, intertwining their thoughts while demanding submission.","opus":"The title track of a recent Trivium album personifies a manipulative predator who preys on the broken. Cloaking control as rescue and revenge as deliverance, this smooth-tongued voice offers refuge to someone stripped of belief and hope, then binds them to it. The prog-metal shifts trace how despair gets exploited by opportunists and false prophets.","sonnet":"A predatory voice whispering to the lost and disillusioned, offering shelter made of hate and control. The Revanchist is a figure whose ideology colonizes a vulnerable mind with the oldest lie: submit and be saved.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~the-rising":{"haiku":"Euphoric collective moment of transcendence where people unite in song, escaping life's troubles and finding temporary salvation through shared movement and sound.","opus":"An anthem of shared euphoria, the ground trembling amid mounting dread before a summons to lift voices and hands together and preserve one collective moment forever, the chaos recast as communal transcendence greater than anything ordinary life offers. Trivium built this as an arena singalong, the Florida band swapping doom for uplift, turning apocalyptic imagery into an invitation to leave your troubles behind as one.","sonnet":"A concert as transcendence — the crowd noise, the lighting, the shared moment of raised hands and raised voices become something larger than ordinary life, a unity the speaker wants frozen in time. The song is essentially a love letter to the live music experience, the idea that what happens between strangers in a room together can outlast everything that waits for them outside.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~the-sin-and-the-sentence":{"haiku":"An innocent person faces judgment and execution by deranged authority figures who worship lies; honest men transform into brutal inquisitors.","opus":"A condemned voice facing a mob that has already decided guilt, where honest men go deranged and bow to a convenient lie. The song burns with the injustice of being buried before you can speak, punished for not being understood.","sonnet":"A person condemned by a system of self-righteous men who have themselves become the monsters they claim to oppose — they genuflect before lies, bury the accused before they can speak, and mistake misunderstanding for proof of guilt.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~the-wretchedness-inside":{"haiku":"Someone bleeds and paints their misery onto others; they cannot escape the wretchedness that binds them in love and hate.","opus":"Trivium examine mutual self-destruction, voicing a narrator consumed by an inner rot he can neither govern nor purge. Suffering for a partner and for himself in equal measure, he lets affection and loathing crash together, wondering aloud whether even damnation would listen to his confession.","sonnet":"A mutual reckoning between two people who have been corroding each other: love and hatred have become indistinguishable, and neither can destroy the internal rot the other has seeded in them. Self-immolation and suffocation alternate as the speaker tries and fails to expel a misery that belongs to both of them.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~this-world-can-t-tear-us-apart":{"haiku":"Two people find redemptive love as their only anchor against a corrupt, loveless world; their bond becomes their ultimate truth and only defense against despair.","opus":"A love that redeems the narrator from self-loathing and sin, set against a corrupt world of infidelity, hypocrisy, and lovelessness. The deeper read positions two people's devotion as defiant refuge, hope absolute against all the surrounding pain and hate.","sonnet":"A person who was dissolving in self-loathing before meeting their partner now frames their relationship as a fortress against a world rotting with infidelity and lovelessness — the two of them choosing each other as the only thing worth believing in.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~throes-of-perdition":{"haiku":"An independent thinker faces execution by conformist forces who fear his autonomy, yet he refuses to capitulate or serve their agenda.","opus":"Trivium stage a martyrdom fantasy in which an independent man faces execution before a jeering mob that hates him precisely because he refuses to kneel. The imagery of betrayal and a cowardly herd frames a critique of conformity, a crowd that fears any leader and would kill to keep everyone equally still.","sonnet":"An individual facing execution by a crowd of Cro-Magnon conformists — they fear anyone who stands on their own, and the narrator endures the knives in their back while watching a world so consumed by sadism it chokes on its own bloodlust and dies.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~through-blood-and-dirt-and-bone":{"haiku":"Suicidal despair chokes the speaker as misery gains control; they drag others down through blood and bone, refusing a quiet ending.","opus":"A hopeless singer certain he won't pull through, gagging on his own rotting spirit. The chilling twist is his refusal to perish by himself, purposely hauling someone he loves down into his sickness and fate so they die together, reduced to gore and grit and remains.","sonnet":"A song about being dragged to destruction by one's own inner decay — the narrator is choking on some dead thing inside himself and rather than go down alone, intends to pull whoever is closest into the collapse with him.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~thrown-into-the-fire":{"haiku":"A corrupt religious leader hides their greed and lust behind faith while living in hypocrisy, fearing exposure and condemnation from their congregation.","opus":"A corrupt preacher confesses his gospel of greed is pure performance, servicing lust on hallowed ground while dreading that his flock will discover the truth and cast him into flames. Trivium sharpen their thrash-tinged metalcore into pointed satire here, the narrator's mock-pious pleas for mysterious grace exposing the hypocrisy of a con man weeping behind the pulpit.","sonnet":"A preacher privately confesses that the gospel of devotion he performs is hollow performance, his desires transgressing the sacred space he inhabits. Discovery would mean destruction, yet the congregation keeps weeping and the rituals keep rolling — the hypocrisy perfectly intact.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~to-believe":{"haiku":"The song condemns political injustice and corruption, where the innocent suffer for others' crimes and leaders weaponize fear to control populations. The narrator struggles with uncontainable anger at the hypocrisy and lies they cannot escape.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with a post-9/11 nation drunk on fear and self-righteousness, watching innocents pay for others' violence; the hardest thing, the narrator admits, was ever believing his own country's version of the truth.","sonnet":"Directed at leaders who ignored civilian deaths and manipulated a nation into war under the banner of fighting terrorism, the song expresses barely contained rage at those who call the innocent guilty while enabling their own people's destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~torn-between-scylla-and-charybdis":{"haiku":"Trapped between two deadly forces with no escape, the narrator must face fear and choose action; struggle itself proves their humanity and will.","opus":"Using Homer's dilemma of two deadly straits as a metaphor for impossible choice, Trivium frames life as a perpetual crusade where every decision opens a tomb. It's a defiant call to face fear and fight rather than be paralyzed between two ruins.","sonnet":"A song about being caught between two forms of destruction with no clean exit — using the Greek mythological straits as a metaphor for a test of will where the only way out is to face the fear directly and carve your fate by force.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~tread-the-floods":{"haiku":"Drowning and drowning metaphorically; moving through overwhelming suffering.","opus":"Trivium render mental struggle as a slow drowning, voicing someone worn down by stress, racing thoughts, and shaking hands as he pushes through the exhausting muck of daily existence. It names panic outright and answers it, casting survival as a deliberate act of will before the chaos drags him fully under.","sonnet":"Anxiety rendered as drowning in slow motion — every thought clutters, every nerve is shot, and the whole effort of staying functional feels like wading through the dead. The only way out is to move through the flood rather than wait for rescue, seize your own reality before it dissolves, or drown in the disarray you've been standing in all along.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~unrepentant":{"haiku":"Honor mythology justifies filicide; the narrator exposes the patriarch's delusion as murderous cowardice dressed as virtue.","opus":"A grim narrative of an honor killing, a father slaughtering his daughters over rumored shame, the song turning the verdict back on him as the one who should be slaughtered. Thrash-metal condemnation of murder disguised as pride.","sonnet":"A condemnation of honour killing, told through the story of a father who murders his daughters on a false accusation of adultery and feels no remorse — the song addresses him directly, calling his act murder and his cause a disgrace.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~until-the-world-goes-cold":{"haiku":"Relentless determination to survive and dominate through endless conflict, sacrificing everything to claim power as the world decays around the survivor.","opus":"A stadium-sized Trivium anthem of scorched-earth defiance, voicing a survivor who has sacrificed everything and clawed his way forward while circling scavengers wait to pick him clean. Vowing to defend what little he has left no matter the cost, he chooses to burn it all down sooner than surrender.","sonnet":"Absolute commitment framed as warfare — the speaker has burned down everything behind them, crawled through mud and grief, and refuses to stop before they reach the throne they have set their eyes on. The vultures are circling, the old life is ash, and yet the vow persists: they will fight until the world itself goes cold rather than accept defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~upon-the-shores-bonus-track":{"haiku":"Arrival at a boundary; standing at the edge of something new or final.","opus":"A damnation myth drawn from Greek underworld imagery, the narrator wandering endless shores toward something unattainable, bleeding on the sand, questioning Charon whether the obolus is his only fare as he is cursed to walk the banks of Acheron without silver coins for his eyes. From Trivium's catalogue, the Florida metal band cast eternal torment as a march of maggots and stinging insects, mythology fueling a bleak vision of nothingness.","sonnet":"Wandering the shores of Acheron, unable to cross — the speaker coins unpaid to the ferryman Charon, condemned to wander among the sinless dead who are simply unburied, tortured by insects and crashing tides. It is a classical afterlife mapped onto total personal desolation, hope absolutely gone, comfort found only in the sand he bleeds and dies on.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~vengeance-falls":{"haiku":"A person rises from dirt and suffering, building themselves into consuming flame to enact vengeance against those who broke them.","opus":"A revenge tale in which treachery and abuse blacken the singer's world, and he forges his torment into a fire to scorch his tormentor in return. Having crawled through filth and outlived his own brush with death, the retribution he waited for at last comes to pass.","sonnet":"A song about surviving abuse and emerging with a burning need for retribution — the narrator was broken, nearly killed, and rebuilt himself specifically to turn the pain back on those who inflicted it.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~villainy-thrives":{"haiku":"Evil people continue to harm the innocent unpunished while goodness fails, prompting a desire for violent retribution and execution of abusers. The singer expresses the bitter reality that justice rarely comes for victims.","opus":"A blistering vigilante fantasy aimed at rapists, abusers, and murderers, craving to inflict on them the exact anguish they caused. Underneath the bloodlust is moral fury at a world where the guilty escape and the innocent perish, the narrator wrestling with his hunger for brutal payback against those who walk free.","sonnet":"A fury at the moral inversion of the world — where innocents die and perpetrators go unpunished — that escalates from philosophical outrage into a visceral list of violent acts the narrator wants to inflict on rapists, abusers, and murderers as a form of proportional justice.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~wake-the-end-is-nigh":{"haiku":"Love transforms into a choke hold neither party can release: passion curdles into hatred, and the only mercy left is surrender to inevitable collapse.","opus":"Love curdling into a wish to hate, choking on feelings that won't stay down, until dying seems easier than staying awake. Apocalyptic dread here is really the collapse of a relationship from the inside.","sonnet":"A song about a love so painful the speaker fantasizes about choking it out of themselves — they are drowning in feelings they hate, questioning why they should stay alive when existence feels this inhumane, and watching everything fall apart.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~watch-the-world-burn":{"haiku":"A call to break free from dehumanizing systems that consume and digest human life; the singers are the arson, burning it all down in rebellion.","opus":"A choice between being crushed inside the great machine or rising up as the fire that ends it. It reframes revolution as self-sacrifice, owning the destruction as the price of breaking a system's hold.","sonnet":"The song describes people crushed and consumed by a dehumanizing system, and declares that those who resist become its enemies, sacrificing themselves to ignite a revolution. The speaker and their allies claim ownership of the destruction, positioning themselves as the arson that burns the whole world down.","src":"haiku"},
"trivium~what-the-dead-men-say":{"haiku":"A ghost or separated spirit haunts the living, claiming possession of them with messages from the dead that create an inescapable emotional bond.","opus":"The title track of a Trivium album trades brute aggression for something spectral, staging a conversation between the living and the departed. A voice from beyond, numb and just out of reach, lingers because the grieving refuse to release it, making the song a meditation on loss, memory, and holding on too tightly.","sonnet":"A ghost who cannot move on, held in place by a survivor who will not let go — the dead person is numb and disconnected, just beyond sight, sending out signals to the living. What the dead have to say exists only between them, a private secret that keeps both trapped: the one who died waiting for permission to leave, and the one still living unable to release them.","src":"haiku"},
"trndytrndy~claws":{"haiku":"Intimate violence leaves permanent wounds; the speaker endures claws drawn by someone they want to trust, bleeding while surrendering to the harm.","opus":"A hazy account of intimacy laced with harm, where affection and injury blur. Nails dig deep and words are whispered while the narrator, vision failing and pain distant, drifts back into the arms of the one wounding them. The fur-coat imagery casts the lover as a beautiful predator, and the hurt becomes a strange, numbing lullaby.","sonnet":"A wound that keeps being reopened by the person who carved it — blood on a fur coat, lullaby of hurting, vision blurring without pain because the speaker would still fall asleep in those arms. The intimacy and the damage are inseparable; the whisper is both seduction and sentence.","src":"haiku"},
"troldhaugen~e-e-t":{"haiku":"Cosmic invasion and technological supremacy render humanity insignificant and powerless; divine or alien intervention exposes human cognitive and moral inadequacy.","opus":"A gleeful slice of avant-garde chaos from the Australian experimentalists, spinning an absurdist myth of aliens descending to teach humanity how to make bread. Framed as a mock tabloid cry that the truth lies somewhere out there, it lampoons UFO lore and Scientology alike, reveling in the sheer silly grandeur of chimps handed cosmic gifts.","sonnet":"A comedic metal retelling of alien contact through ancient bread-baking mythology: extraterrestrials arrive in ships, deliver triangular gifts from above, and leave humanity to build civilizations it barely comprehends — \"our brains in comparison look like a dang peanut.\" The song celebrates bewilderment and reverence at the absurdity of existence.","src":"haiku"},
"trophy-eyes~figure-eight":{"haiku":"Two people are trapped in a repeating cycle of love, estrangement, and performance, unable to break the pattern or reignite what they once shared.","opus":"Trophy Eyes temper their punk edge into a bittersweet duet about a romance rotting even as the pair strain to keep it afloat. One voice claims everything can shift given hope and hails the other as their guiding radiance; the reply confesses the partner has grown foreign beside them, the love clearly finished while both feign ignorance. The looping title names a devotion circling forever yet arriving nowhere.","sonnet":"A relationship that once held transcendence has decayed into mutual pretense — both parties know it is over but continue inhabiting the form of closeness. The dedication remains sincere even as the beloved has become a stranger, and the honesty being demanded is the one neither can quite deliver.","src":"haiku"},
"trophy-eyes~people-like-you":{"haiku":"Society invalidates marginal existence; the narrator describes how those deemed ordinary or unbeautiful remain disposable until death transforms them into narrative.","opus":"A gutting portrait of growing up poor and marked, holes in your shoes and domestic violence taught as normal, told from a kid who just wants to be anyone else. Its bitterness lands because envy of the beautiful curdles into a middle finger at a world that was never built for people like you.","sonnet":"A childhood defined by poverty, domestic violence, and the casual cruelty of class — \"the world isn't for people like you\" — the speaker oscillates between wanting to be anyone but themselves and a defiant \"fuck you\" to a system where life is easy only if you are beautiful or wealthy.","src":"haiku"},
"trophy-eyes~something-bigger-than-this":{"haiku":"Young adulthood haunted by random death and existential dread, searching for meaning beyond survival itself.","opus":"A young man witnesses a suicide near his home and it fractures his sense of purpose. Stuck in an unglamorous twenties, he clings to the faith that all of it will cohere into something larger than survival.","sonnet":"A witness to a stranger's death on the train tracks, and to his own narrow escape from the same fate, keeps returning to the belief that life must mean something larger — even as youth and promise seem precarious, the conviction that we were born for more keeps surfacing.","src":"haiku"},
"trophy-eyes~you-can-count-on-me":{"haiku":"An artist monetizes pain and loneliness, selling sad songs to the suffering while privately self-destructing. They give pieces of themselves through art yet demand not to be broken any further.","opus":"A songwriter reckons brutally with his trade, admitting he peddles sad songs to the lonely while snorting the ticket money and failing to see the people he loves through the crowd of strangers. Trophy Eyes' arena-reaching anthemic streak gives this confession its scale, the Australian band letting five years poured into fans curdle into self-laceration that finally, hard-won, resolves toward peace.","sonnet":"A musician reflects on five years of pouring genuine pain into songs for strangers, spending the money on drugs and losing sight of the people he loves while filling rooms with faces he does not know. He arrives, scarred and honestly happy, at the claim that the music was real even if the cost was everything.","src":"haiku"},
"tropic-gold~a-place-called-euphoria":{"haiku":"Digital euphoria replaces reality as the speaker's body decays in virtual paradise. Dopamine circuits trap consciousness in automated routine until death arrives.","opus":"A dystopian vision of escaping a boring reality into a digital paradise that is really a trap, the body rotting and mind numbed by dopamine while a system whispers that you live for nothing.","sonnet":"The body is described as a corrupted machine — circuit overloads, dopamine coded to suppress decay, a virus the speaker can't override — and 'euphoria' is both the destination advertised and the hell willingly entered: 'wake, eat, sleep, accept defeat, obey, repeat' reduces paradise to a grinding loop.","src":"haiku"},
"tropic-gold~dead-to-all-my-friends":{"haiku":"Infection and alienation consume the self; the speaker retreats from all connection, finding dark comfort in total internal decay and dissolution.","opus":"Someone medicating a sickness in their head declares themselves dead to everyone who loved them and claims to prefer the isolation, half-daring the illness to finish the job.","sonnet":"About willfully checking out of social life and emotional availability due to mental illness — the speaker is medicated, partially delirious, and has accepted a state of numbness and withdrawal that leaves their friends grieving a living person.","src":"haiku"},
"tropic-gold~happy":{"haiku":"Charismatic manipulation masks terror and alienation, where the speaker performs happiness while dying inside. Seeking leader and god becomes desperate attempt to escape self-recognition.","opus":"A false messiah lures devotees into ecstasy while secretly terrified and dying inside, exposing the hollow horror behind a happy facade and the desperate wish to play god or devil rather than feel.","sonnet":"A cult-leader persona lures devotees with promises of fantasy and promises they 'won't miss a thing', but the mask slips in the bridge: 'my god I'm fucking terrified, something doesn't feel right, cos I'm dying on the inside' — the song oscillates between performing salvation and confessing to the terror beneath the smile.","src":"haiku"},
"tropic-gold~holy-horror":{"haiku":"Mutual parasitism binds two in dance of consumption where suffering becomes addictive currency. The speaker loves the horror, surrendering to the predator they feed.","opus":"A disturbing study of attachment to being hurt, a narrator who misses his own pain and craves the way a partner keeps carving pieces out of him. The horror is that he loves it, unable to tell if the sickness is the wound or the wanting.","sonnet":"A masochistic attachment: the speaker confesses they wake from the horror and miss it, that they love when the other takes a different part of them — even knowing the other needs them broken, the speaker cannot stop asking \"should I fucking love that?\" and answering yes.","src":"haiku"},
"tropic-gold~living-in-colour":{"haiku":"Broken trust and shattered promises that fragment both the speaker and their world. The speaker cycles between stagnation and attempts at color and meaning after betrayal.","opus":"Addressed to someone who breaks promises and picks the narrator apart, the piece pleads for forgiveness and a slowed pace, moving from monochrome loneliness toward a hard-won saturation of colour. Tropic Gold's post-hardcore melodrama suits the pendulum between wounded blame and tenderness, insisting that even a doomed effort mattered because at least they tried.","sonnet":"A relationship corrodes under the weight of broken promises — one person dragging the other down, selfless dreams left unshared. The turn comes late: from monochrome loneliness toward colour, a recognition that even a failed attempt at connection is better than nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"tropic-gold~the-angels-are-gone":{"haiku":"Spiritual abandonment and internal corruption hollow the body as angels flee and only sickness remains. Diagnosis confirms what the speaker already knew—the illness becomes identity itself.","opus":"Spiritual and mental collapse voiced as an addict pleading for exorcism, the sense that nobody's home and grace has abandoned him. The doctor's therapy referral only sharpens the dread that his sickness is something he's grown to like.","sonnet":"A confession of spiritual vacancy and psychological sickness: the angels are gone, nobody is home, and a doctor's verdict — \"you're sick but you like that\" — lands as the central wound, the speaker needing a reason to stay while unable to speak up or change.","src":"haiku"},
"trove~shook-me":{"haiku":"A transformative force overturns complacency and despair; the speaker resolves to resist capitulation and refuse victimhood.","opus":"A synth-pop reckoning with a love that overturns everything the narrator thought solid. Hit as if by a storm, he loses his sight and doubts his faith in a hateful world, crowning himself the champion of surrender before resolving to stay grounded. Nothing has ever rattled him the way this person does.","sonnet":"Nothing has unsettled the speaker like this particular person: struck by lightning and rolled by thunder, losing vision and faith, they describe themselves as the king of giving up when the crown weighs too much — but the refusal to go out this way, feet on the ground, is the thin thread keeping them from total dissolution.","src":"haiku"},
"troye-sivan~swimming-pools":{"haiku":"Transformation requires traversal of pain; the speaker must wade through suffering to reach renewal.","opus":"Trying to move past a love you can't outrun, clenching through the pain toward a distant lighthouse and the relief of draining away what no longer feels right. Heartbreak reframed as a slow, deliberate crossing toward recovery.","sonnet":"Someone tries to outrun the pain of a lost relationship — exhausted, unable to stop, knowing the only way through is through — sustained by a distant lighthouse of hope even as the grief keeps dragging them under. The empty swimming pool is the state after love has drained out and left behind nothing but the shape of where it was.","src":"haiku"},
"truch-o-strzygi~zag-ada":{"haiku":"Witches and destruction; wild, violent passion mixed with dark ritual and apocalyptic fervor.","opus":"A Polish black-metal fever of cemetery cold, lust and annihilation, a witches' sabbath where desire, death and violence bleed together into an apocalyptic night the narrator gleefully welcomes.","sonnet":"A ritual of necromantic desire — cold cemetery air, the sweet dry smoke that stifles, and a lover whose skin is cold as ice are entwined with blood running in streams from her eyes and a gargoyle breathing tainted air. The witches arrive at the end: real, wild, living among us, craving fulfillment, and the apocalyptic frenzy — zagłada — begins.","src":"haiku"},
"trucho-strzygi~zagada":{"haiku":"Obsessive desire corrupts through blood and darkness; predatory passion seizes victims with supernatural inevitability, transforming tenderness into necrophilia.","src":"haiku"},
"truckfighters~desert-cruiser":{"haiku":"A driver cruises the desert in a 1968 Mustang with whisky and a lover, celebrating freedom and speed. The song runs out of fuel and resonates with hedonistic release.","opus":"A stoner-rock joyride: the narrator tears across the desert in a vintage Mustang with his girl in the back and whiskey in hand, chasing pure freedom and pleasure. The recurring worry about running out of fuel and gas is the only shadow on an otherwise carefree high-speed escape.","sonnet":"A simple, exhilarated road fantasy: a man and his woman cruising the desert in a '68 Mustang, drunk on whisky, completely in the moment until the gas runs out.","src":"haiku"},
"truckfighters~freewheelin":{"haiku":"An unnoticed stranger urges another to release tension and drift along without direction or purpose.","opus":"Stoner-rock as pure invitation to let go, the anonymous drifter urging a wound-up listener to stop grasping and just ride, enjoying the road for its own sake. Motion as the whole meaning.","sonnet":"A laid-back invitation to slow down, let go of striving, and enjoy the ride as it comes — the speaker asks the listener to relax, stop chasing identity or status, and simply coast.","src":"haiku"},
"truckfighters~gargarismo":{"opus":"A slice of Swedish fuzz-rock built more on riff and momentum than narrative, its scattered images of an American road trip channeling a bad mood and a soured, one-sided bargain into desert-rock swagger. The words function as texture, evoking restless motion and irritation without ever settling into a clear story.","sonnet":"A cryptic, loosely structured song about an interpersonal conflict or tension while driving — the other person creates a bad mood and some kind of invisible obligation, with surreal imagery of supernovas and Iowa that resists easy interpretation.","src":"sonnet"},
"truckfighters~subfloor":{"haiku":"Casual acceptance of contradiction and consequence; easygoing despite chaos and inevitable collapse.","opus":"Stoner-rock imagery run loose and half-coherent, consent, contradiction and a mantra-like fixation on a place name, the meaning subordinated to groove and the fog of the riff.","sonnet":"A brief, disjointed exchange of contradictions — yes and no, protection, a falling — collapses into a repeated invocation of \"South Carolina\" that carries the weight of a mood the lyrics refuse to fully articulate. The song resists paraphrase by design.","src":"haiku"},
"true-north~save-me":{"haiku":"A confessional narrative about projecting redemptive power onto another person while being too broken to save oneself; the speaker uses someone else as an escape from their own spiral of self-loathing and instrumental connection.","opus":"A nightclub descent into exploiting people to feel valued, the singer pursuing someone now pouring themselves into rescuing another. The bitterest revelation is that this rescuer never mastered rescuing themselves, so the salvation was always a delusion.","sonnet":"At a crowded club, the speaker is emotionally absent, scanning the room and numbing fears with drinks while realizing the person they once leaned on was never capable of saving anyone, including themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"true~a-2yvrod":{"haiku":"Ephemeral beauty and impermanence of love; flowers wilt and people part, yet the speaker continues to love knowing the relationship will fade like sunset colors.","opus":"TRUE, the vocalist Miyu Tomita's anime-song vehicle, specializes in tender resolve, and this meditation on loving someone losing their self carries that gentleness. As the beloved asks who she is, the singer holds to the warmth of their bond, dreading a world without her yet resolving to protect what they have with her own hands, even as flowers begin withering the moment they bloom.","sonnet":"A gentle meditation on loving someone who may be losing their sense of self — the beloved asks \"who am I?\" and the singer holds onto the warmth of their bond, dreading a world without that person but resolving to protect what they have with her own hands, even as flowers begin to wither from the moment they bloom.","src":"haiku"},
"trustcompany~downfall":{"haiku":"Fear intensifies as one confronts their own shadow self, breaking down layer by layer until the mask falls away to reveal the fragmented person underneath.","opus":"An inner split laid bare, the singer daring someone to push him to the breaking point so a hidden other side surfaces, crawling toward an inevitable collapse.","sonnet":"The speaker stands on the edge of psychological collapse, inviting someone to push them so that a hidden other side can emerge — not as defeat but as a revelation — and ultimately walks and crawls toward a downfall that feels inevitable and cathartic.","src":"haiku"},
"truth-club~running-from-the-chase":{"haiku":"Flight becomes permanent as the speaker recognizes they cannot escape their pursuer.","opus":"An indie-rock meditation on inherited burden and the fear of repeating a parent's life. As the firstborn who supposedly bears the family in his face, the narrator watches strain fracture those before him and dreads working himself to death like a friend who collapsed clutching his chest. Still he pushes on, hoping to shape the weight into something nourishing.","sonnet":"Inheritance as wound and obligation — carrying a family's story in your face and blood, watching the false son fracture the mother's worth, fearing you echo the same patterns. The image of a man clutching his chest as he falls off a ladder haunts the closing: work until dead, and the only question is whether the narrator can shape it into something nourishing for the next person in front of them.","src":"haiku"},
"tss~tes-pleurs":{"haiku":"Death and obsession waltz together as the speaker emerges from shadows to consume another's suffering, finding transcendence in witnessed anguish and intimate violence.","opus":"A French vampiric obsession piece, a stalker rising from the grave to feed on a woman's fear and tears, dancing to her weeping as her innocent gaze dims. Devotion twisted into predation, love expressed as the slow draining of a life.","sonnet":"A predatory figure who rises from the grave whenever his victim is afraid or alone: watching her from the shadows, memorising her phobias, he leaves teeth marks on her neck like jewellery and dances to the sound of her tears mixed with liner — her innocent gaze going out slowly as he pulls her into his tomb.","src":"haiku"},
"tsushimamire~a-10ulijy":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator has a small, helpless crush on a spiky-jacketed punk enthusiast who teases them but also introduces them to the world of punk — citing the Ramones, wearing a silver-studded leather jacket. The song is fond and lightly comic: admiration for someone who embodies the attitude, not just the aesthetic."},
"tsushimamire~a-141yovb":{"haiku":"A dizzying rant on information overload—time lag makes the body spin, too much input, but we still demand sensation.","opus":"Tsushimamire, a scrappy all-women Japanese punk trio, thrive on frenetic overload, which suits this study of temporal dislocation. The narrator's brain spins, her emotional clock out of sync with everyone else's, flooded with nostalgia for a sound the world has already left behind, the dizzying isolation of running on a different time zone met with a vow to keep flying forward anyway.","sonnet":"A song about being stuck in a time-lag between past and present — the narrator's brain is spinning, her emotional clock is out of sync with everyone else's, she is flooded with nostalgia for a sound and a moment that the world has already moved on from. The emotional core is the dizzying isolation of someone whose passion runs on a different time zone, insisting she will keep flying forward even so.","src":"haiku"},
"tsushimamire~a-1bdzeur":{"haiku":"The ephemeral present—each moment becomes past instantly; the speaker celebrates living fully now as the only meaningful act amid constant temporal flow.","opus":"Tsushimamire pair garage-punk energy with sudden philosophical turns, and this song chases the irreversibility of time. Every goodbye to the future becomes past the instant it's spoken, and the only answer is to keep laughing and living until the last day before rebirth, the present collapsing into memory even as it is inhabited, the band's momentum racing that collapse.","sonnet":"A song about the irreversibility of time: every \"goodbye to the future\" instantly becomes a past the moment it's spoken, and the only response is to keep laughing and living until the last day before rebirth — the present moment collapses into the past even as you inhabit it.","src":"haiku"},
"tsushimamire~a-1j0318k":{"haiku":"Desperation to escape loneliness through shared meals and presence; connection sought to fill existential void.","opus":"Tsushimamire fold humor into loneliness, and this song reaches for connection through the plainest means. A person alone too long, with no god, family or lover, extends outward through food and togetherness across every border, leaning on Lennon's imagine-everyone ethos and simply inviting someone to hang out and cook a hamburger as a cure for the void.","sonnet":"A person who has been alone too long — no god, no family, no lover — reaching out through food and togetherness across all borders, leaning on John Lennon's imagine-all-the-people ethos, and inviting someone to hang out and cook hamburger together as a cure for loneliness.","src":"haiku"},
"tsushimamire~a-6onx8u":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator and the person they address are both 'human-coated' — wrapped in a thin protective shell that is gradually peeling away, like a stain soaking into cloth. Mari described the concept as the invisible barriers that exist even between close friends: people cannot fully know each other, yet love persists underneath the coating."},
"tsushimamire~a-i19ss7":{"haiku":"Communicative breakdown—misalignment between hearts prevents mutual understanding despite desire to reconnect; cycles of suffering and prayer fail to repair the gap.","opus":"Tsushimamire can turn interpersonal friction into abrasive momentum, and this song lives in irresolvable misalignment. The more the narrator talks, the more her heart closes; everything about the other person, their belongings, beliefs and sensory world, registers as alien, and the returning refrain that this isn't it captures an endless mismatch no amount of speaking can close.","sonnet":"A song about the breakdown of communication and the exhaustion of difference: the more the narrator talks, the more their heart closes; everything about the other person — their things, their beliefs, their sensory world — is alien, and the repeated refrain \"that's not it\" captures an endless, irresolvable misalignment.","src":"haiku"},
"tsushimamire~a-r2mb52":{"haiku":"A resentful memory of a foolish ex-lover—beautiful summers now hollow, unable to escape the ghost of what was taken.","opus":"Tsushimamire specialize in the unflattering truths of desire, and this catalogue of a lingering ex holds nothing back. She knows the grip is pathetic, remembers orange sunsets and bicycle rides, admits she'd probably still kiss him if he appeared, then says goodbye and uses him as a stepping stone, the humiliating clarity of still loving someone known to be worthless.","sonnet":"A woman cataloguing the slow, stupid grip a stupid ex-boyfriend still has on her — she knows it's pathetic, she remembers orange sunsets and bicycle rides with him, she admits she'd probably still kiss him if he showed up, and then says goodbye anyway, using him as a stepping stone. The emotional core is the humiliating clarity of still loving someone you know is worthless.","src":"haiku"},
"tsushimamire~a-w8dcc0":{"src":"web","web":"A self-deprecating romantic confession dressed in dessert wordplay: the narrator calls themselves a 'brain shortcake' — scatterbrained, collapsible, a bit of a mess — but earnestly offers this flawed self to the person they love. Despite the silliness, the beloved accepts them anyway, which is the emotional payoff."},
"tsushimamire~ebihara-shinji":{"src":"web","web":"A quirky nostalgic portrait of an eccentric man remembered entirely through sensory anchors: a cocoa candy he once gave that now makes the narrator think of the sky over Kashiwa Station, and a sour potato salad sandwich that conjures the taste of youth. He shadow-boxes toward the sky while flipping a wok — strange, vivid, unforgettable."},
"tsushimamire~jaguar":{"haiku":"A weathered electric guitar aging alongside its player; companion over a decade, precious despite warping and decay.","opus":"An affectionate love song addressed to the singer's beloved Fender Jaguar guitar, cradling its pale body, tending its chipped paint and cracked pegs over ten years together; a playful roll-call rejecting every other guitar model (Telecaster, SG, Les Paul, Strat) frames the Jaguar as her one and only, thrilling choice.","sonnet":"A Japanese love song addressed to a Fender Jaguar guitar — the narrator describes her decade-long relationship with the instrument in intimate physical terms, recounts where and how she bought it, and dismisses every other guitar shape as wrong for her, insisting the Jaguar is the only choice.","src":"haiku"},
"tsushimamire~shot-you":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator wakes to find World War III has started, viruses are being weaponized, and civilization is in chaos — and the response is to blast rock music and drink alcohol as a form of disinfection and defiance. Surreal and satirical, it channels anxiety about global catastrophe into a punk survival anthem."},
"tsushimamire~show-you-my-soy-sauce":{"src":"web","web":"A comedic celebration of soy sauce as both a literal condiment and a stand-in for Japanese cultural identity, playing on 'show you my source/origins.' The narrator runs through brand names, food pairings (gyoza, sashimi), and decades of culinary heritage to insist that soy sauce — unlike people — never betrays you."},
"ttng~26-is-dancier-than-4":{"haiku":"A person has outgrown their social circle and the repetitive nights they once shared, seeking escape from stagnant patterns.","opus":"The math-rock trio, named for the album This Town Needs Guns, trade their knotty guitars for weary candour here. A narrator drags himself through another indistinguishable Friday night out, apologising for his low mood while friends lose themselves on the dancefloor. Nostalgia for younger nights blurs into resignation, and the ritual promises only to repeat itself next week amid the same faces and songs.","sonnet":"A night out turns hollow when a person realises the social ritual no longer fits. Surrounded by familiar faces and the same songs as always, he feels disconnected and exhausted, pushed through the motions rather than genuinely present, already anticipating next Friday when the whole cycle begins again.","src":"haiku"},
"ttng~baboon":{"haiku":"Possessive love traps two people in cycles of jealousy and wounding while time steals opportunity for change.","opus":"An intense, ambivalent binding of two fates through desire, warm lips and a vow of until death that the singer already suspects is entangled in lies and envy. The knot they've tied around each other is as much trap as union.","sonnet":"A relationship described through the physical and emotional pull of touch — warm lips, a trance-like state, fates entwined — that turns into a reckoning as the marks on the other person's skin and unfurling lies reveal that the vow of \"until death\" may have been spoken without understanding its cost.","src":"haiku"},
"ttng~cat-fantastic":{"haiku":"Inequality and indifference justify inaction; the speaker observes wealth hoard while others starve, too paralyzed by acceptance of division to imagine change.","opus":"A critique of acquisitive greed and inequality, arguing that satisfaction comes from sharing rather than hoarding while others go without. It imagines slowly replacing old selfish minds with better ideals over time.","sonnet":"Economic inequality observed through contrasting textures — red wine and tan lines versus worn holes and hand-me-downs — with the speaker calling out those whose magpie consumerism coexists with indifference to others' hunger; the final vision is generational change slowly replacing old minds with new ones willing to share.","src":"haiku"},
"ttng~coconut-crab":{"haiku":"Self-doubt paralyses creative action; the narrator struggles to articulate meaning when linguistic precision seems impossible and inadequacy feels inevitable.","opus":"A pep talk to a blocked writer, or to himself, insisting the words rarely fit perfectly so you should just start, sing what you feel and let the sound matter more than the meaning. Math-rock advice about creative paralysis and settling for good enough.","sonnet":"The speaker — and an internal critical voice — wrestle over the difficulty of expressing oneself in words, circling between encouragement and frustration before landing on the idea that the sound of a thing may matter more than its precision.","src":"haiku"},
"ttng~whatever-whenever":{"haiku":"Resigned decline into self-imposed isolation and the loss of will to live. The speaker views existence as meaningless repetition, choosing stagnation over genuine change.","opus":"A darkly funny portrait of a man wasting away, feeling insignificant inside a life that doesn't fit and losing his appetite for living while marking time in a prison of his own making. TTNG lace intricate math-rock under the gallows humor, the narrator tending his own grave and rehearsing old mistakes while stubbornly insisting he'll somehow scrape through.","sonnet":"A man buried in self-imposed isolation and accumulated habit admits he is losing the taste for living — not dramatically, but in the slow way of someone who keeps changing the sheets on his own grave and re-living old mistakes, getting by most of the time and not much more.","src":"haiku"},
"tulip~ghost-of-kyiv":{"haiku":"Revolutionary haunting persists beyond death; spectral resistance prevents oppressor's victory, embodying defiance as inheritance passed through generations of sacrifice.","opus":"Built around the wartime legend of Kyiv's phantom defender, it turns a besieged people's resolve into a vow of deliverance and haunting vengeance against an invader. Burning notebooks and scarred faces ground the myth in a nation refusing to have its soul taken.","sonnet":"A song of defiant resistance addressed to a fallen or occupied homeland — the speaker promises to haunt the occupiers, vows that souls cannot be taken, and watches notebooks burn while asking how humanity will ever learn. The closing promise is that one day the ghost will return as deliverance.","src":"haiku"},
"tulip~mu53":{"haiku":"Suffocation under systems of control and institutional decay; hope entangled in broken bodies.","opus":"A dread-soaked reckoning with suffocation, physical and spiritual. Tarot figures frame a return to origins as the lungs fill with smoke and the world outruns all control. A nursery rhyme about a church twists into an image of people coiled around their own shattered bones — community curdling into collapse.","sonnet":"The church as a site of collective suffocation — here is the steeple, open the doors, and all the people are wrapped around broken bones, black-lunged from smoke, the world racing by out of control — the fool and the hanged man from the tarot frame it as an inevitable cycle returning to where it began.","src":"haiku"},
"tunde-adebimpe~magnetic":{"haiku":"Self-empowerment through radical presence; magnetizing others while building your own new world.","opus":"The TV on the Radio frontman turning a cosmic pep talk into funk, a voice from within urging you to hold your head high and rise in a time of both tenderness and rage, self-belief pitched as lightning and defiance.","sonnet":"An affirmation of resilience and personal power addressed to someone who has been beaten down — framed through cosmic and electric imagery, it insists that the person has an innate force that cannot be extinguished and will carry them higher.","src":"haiku"},
"turbonegro~a-dazzling-display-of-talent":{"haiku":"A sarcastic condemnation of arrogant performers and the crude spectacle of their explosive acts.","opus":"Turbonegro's deathpunk mode: mock-awed showbiz patter that curdles into disgust, applauding a spectacle of bodily fluids and phonies. The German aside gives away the whole leering, deliberately trashy joke about worshipping nothing.","sonnet":"A mock-impressed spectacle of scatological or sexual excess — the narrator watches a performance of bodily fluids and assholes with exaggerated admiration, the whole thing a filthy burlesque of showmanship.","src":"haiku"},
"turbonegro~black-rabbit":{"haiku":"A horny punk black rabbit with black habits hops around refusing labels about what they do. They contrast themselves against a spider while embracing their lowdown reputation.","opus":"A leering, cartoonish boast built on a rabbit persona, the narrator flaunting a horny reputation and warning a partner not to whine when the consequences turn out badly, all while insisting he is no spider. Turbonegro are the Norwegian deathpunk provocateurs whose whole shtick is exactly this filthy, tongue-in-cheek swagger, and the stray Norwegian curses here mark the joke as pure scene theater.","sonnet":"A short, swagger-heavy self-portrait: the speaker is a low-down creature of habit and appetite, entirely without pretense about what he is. The distinction being drawn — rabbit, not spider — is one of honest, instinctual pleasure-seeking versus something more predatory or trapping, a crude ethics of vice that at least owns itself.","src":"haiku"},
"turbonegro~hobbit-motherfuckers":{"haiku":"The speaker condemns their entire generation as weak, unoriginal, and lacking the will or suffering to matter.","opus":"Norwegian deathpunk provocateurs Turbonegro run down a mocking inventory of the scene tribes they despise, from limp hippies to pierced poseurs and killer-worshippers, condemning the lot for having no nerve and no fight. The refrain scorning their own generation is a knowing, self-aware jab that turns misanthropy into shock-value theater.","sonnet":"A bilious catalogue of generational contempt — every subculture, affectation, and lifestyle choice of the era dismissed as soft and insufficient. The speaker demands more war, more suffering, more natural selection, not out of conviction but out of pure disgust at comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"turbonegro~i-got-erection":{"haiku":"The narrator experiences arousal triggered by violence, destruction, and brutality, from street encounters to arson. The title frames violent impulses as compulsive physical reactions.","opus":"A deliberately grotesque provocation linking arousal to violence and destruction, the narrator getting an erection at the thought of beating women, blood, arson, and digging holes; a shock-punk parody equating male desire with brutality.","sonnet":"A deliberately transgressive catalog of things — violence, arson, killing, rock music — that arouse the narrator, functioning as provocation through the equation of sex with destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"turbonegro~just-flesh":{"opus":"Peak-era Norwegian deathpunk provocation, wielding sadomasochistic imagery to skewer showbiz failure and self-destruction. A washed-up figure who once dreamed of corporate success now contemplates oblivion, reducing the whole degraded spectacle to bodies and appetite. The band's trademark deviant humor turns despair into transgressive cabaret, mocking rock mythology while wallowing in it.","sonnet":"A darkly comedic BDSM fantasy — the narrator, broke and adrift, invites explicit sadomasochistic acts including spanking, penetration with broken glass, and leather restraints, framing the body as nothing more than flesh and finding a kind of liberation in pure physical degradation.","src":"sonnet"},
"turbonegro~the-midnight-nambla":{"haiku":"A predatory persona boasts of child abuse intentions; the repeated identification with NAMBLA frames pedophilia as identity.","opus":"A deliberately grotesque cartoon villain boasts of preying on children, casting itself as a monstrous father figure lurking in the dark. Turbonegro built a career on deathpunk provocation and calculated offense, and this brief taunt is pure shock theatre, weaponizing the most repellent persona imaginable to test and mock the limits of taste rather than to mean any of it.","src":"haiku"},
"turbowolf~the-free-life":{"haiku":"Freedom and wonder are pursued over wealth, dreams become reality through science and magic, and keeping promises sustains the ideal.","opus":"A wry existential shrug that ranks curiosity above riches, the narrator admitting he forgets and can't resist much yet wouldn't want to be anyone else, treating liberty as a dream worth chasing. Turbowolf, a British psych-tinged garage-rock band, ride the musing on a fuzzed-out, swaggering groove, holding that hopeful dream loosely against a cosmos that may not care.","sonnet":"A loose, wandering manifesto that puts wonder above wealth and science alongside magic, choosing to stay authentically itself rather than drift toward validation. Freedom is a dream, but it's a good dream worth sleeping for.","src":"haiku"},
"turnstile~blackout":{"haiku":"A performer collapses under the spotlight but frames collapse as part of their show, blurring the line between authentic suffering and manufactured spectacle.","opus":"Turnstile lace hardcore with buoyant melody as a narrator invites his own collapse under a spotlight he can't resist. He treats his breakdown as a performance, insisting that if his unraveling makes the crowd feel something, he's glad to supply it, his crumpling onstage just another part of the act. The refrain blurs real crisis with spectacle, self-destruction served up as entertainment.","sonnet":"A performer navigates the gap between collapse and spectacle — falling in front of a crowd becomes indistinguishable from showmanship, and the line between genuine crisis and performance dissolves. The spotlight returning becomes both rescue and continuation of a cycle with no clear exit.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~ambulance": {"haiku":"Symbiotic surrender in toxic attraction: lovers exchange roles—one becomes the accident, the other the ambulance—creating interdependence where harm and rescue blur into endless cycle.","opus":"Structured as a doo-wop suite of traded vows, the Brooklyn art-rock band cast love as mutual disaster and rescue, each partner promising to catch and mend the other. From the Dear Science era, it makes codependence tender, imagining a shared garden shifting with the seasons and treating the plunge into wreckage together as the whole point.","sonnet":"Two people cast themselves as each other's disasters and rescuers — the crash and the crutch, the recklessness and the salvation. The mutual deal is a surrender into love that is openly self-destructive and openly necessary.","src":"haiku","fable":"The a cappella arrangement is the key: no instruments, just voices, so the song's promise of mutual rescue is enacted by bodies leaning on each other for the whole structure. The chorus is a chain of paired trades where each partner offers to be the other's wound and the cure for it — the crash and the stretcher, the splint set against the break — a codependent bargain in which love is inseparable from injury. The final move is the tell: the alternate chorus swaps the roles so the singer volunteers to be the rescuer rather than the wreck, and the plea shifts from a summons that keeps the beloved above to an invitation into the same drop, turning a one-sided rescue fantasy into a shared descent.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"mutual rescue bargain turns into a shared descent","built":"a cappella voices alone enact the mutual-rescue promise structurally"},
"tv-on-the-radio~blind":{"haiku":"The narrator confuses a stranger for a lost love, striking him blind with longing, yet cannot remember who he truly was searching for.","opus":"TV on the Radio trace memory blurring into doubt, a narrator spotting a stranger who so resembles a lost love that he can no longer tell whom he saw or who he is. Aching over the long absence, he broadcasts a plea for an ideal partner, willing to take anyone, framing love as a losing gamble worth placing anyway.","sonnet":"A man glimpses a woman who mirrors a lost lover and is struck by the gap between memory and present reality — he can no longer distinguish what he saw from what he projected. The collapse into blindness becomes an open letter: a plea to any connection that might interrupt the loop of grief and substitution.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~bomb-yourself": {"haiku":"The speaker critiques consumerism and manufactured desires, urging listeners to destroy their own countries and families while media directs their choices.","opus":"A caustic indictment of complacent consumerism, the narrator urges a nation to bomb, breed, and hoard luxuries while blaming its violence on the television. On their early EP, TV on the Radio channel Brooklyn art-rock into political fury, turning TV dinners and final-fantasy escapism into a bitter portrait of manufactured fear and outsourced conscience, then a sardonic invitation to lie back and wait.","sonnet":"A sardonic address to the passive consumer who numbs fear with television and accumulates comfort while ignoring real need — the invitation to bomb one's own country is a bitter instruction to keep smiling and waiting while the system destroys from within.","src":"haiku","fable":"Adebimpe delivers this in the flat, sedated cadence of an advertising jingle, and that mismatch is the whole weapon: a war chant sung as a lullaby, coaxing the listener to settle in, grin, and recline in comfort while the wait runs out. The imperatives fold the violence of country and consumption into one gesture — sow your offspring and gorge on your own dread, then murder the household you started and pin the command on the broadcast — so that the bombing abroad and the numbness at home become the same instruction. When the killer's alibi is that the order came from what flickered on the screen, the song locates its real target in the medium that dinner and dread both arrive through, the very transmission the band is named for. The recurring vision of last things is the trick pivot, letting the phrase’s video-game shimmer bleed into an eschatology of the end, apocalypse sold as leisure, and the murmured instruction to bide a while longer makes complacency itself the detonator the title demanded.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","war & battle","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"complacency itself installed as the detonator the title demands","built":"war chant delivered in the flat cadence of an advertising jingle"},
"tv-on-the-radio~careful-you":{"haiku":"A conflicted lover tries to say goodbye but cannot leave, navigating the mix of tenderness and blame in a relationship that has left permanent marks.","opus":"TV on the Radio slip between French endearments and the paralysis of a love that ought to be over yet won't let go. Caught between leaving and staying, the narrator confesses a tenderness that reshaped him for good. The refrain's gentle vow to look after the other keeps the ambivalence suspended, a farewell endlessly postponed by the memory of what they had.","sonnet":"A bilingual ambivalence — French declarations of love set against English uncertainty — frames a relationship where neither leaving nor staying feels possible. The tenderness is real but the architecture of the bond has been so thoroughly altered that no clear path forward presents itself.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~could-you":{"haiku":"A series of questions probe whether someone can love selflessly, open their heart, and build something beyond walls amid a wasted world.","opus":"Amid stretches of booze, drugs and vanished romance, the track is a pointed dare to open up emotionally: can you truly cherish another, place their needs above your own vanity, and construct something rather than a barrier?","sonnet":"A challenge to someone who has closed themselves off — asking if they're capable of loving another person, stripping away their ego, building something instead of walls, when they're clearly using alcohol and disconnection to avoid the question.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~dlz":{"haiku":"A critique of hypocrisy, false methods, and systems that crush the broken while pretending concern. The song warns of inevitable collapse beneath the weight of objectification and structural decay.","opus":"A seething takedown of self-deceiving power and hubris, addressing a death professor whose grand structures are already busting loose. Riding a locomotive of dread, it charts the slow dawn of an unraveling loser who set his sights too high.","sonnet":"A condemnation of someone who burns everything and lies about it — a \"death professor\" who victimizes and exploits — met with the narrator's defiant declaration that love and life are stronger than the destruction being dealt.","src":"haiku","fable":"DLZ is the seething climax of TV on the Radio's 2008 Dear Science, and its charge comes from the collision between a suave, controlled surface and pure vitriol underneath. The opening feints as gracious congratulation and quickly reveals itself as a prosecutor's brief against a hollow authority who manufactures his own catastrophes, corrodes what he touches, and then feeds on the void he creates. The wordless refrain works as a bright, ironic mask stretched over that rot, and each recurrence of the mounting is-this-turning-into premise ratchets the dread from private grievance toward an apocalyptic, mechanized force flattening everything in its path. The final movement flips prosecution into open contempt: Tunde Adebimpe stops indicting the sham expert and starts taunting him outright, staking his own decay as the sturdier thing, while the closing verdict lands not as surrender but as a grim, leveling promise that reduces winner and vanquished to the same residue.","fv":2,"fnote":"The song’s second life came from Breaking Bad — it scores the season-two hardware-store scene where Walter White warns a rival cook to stay out of his territory, the moment fans mark as Heisenberg fully surfacing.","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"prosecution of hollow authority curdling into open contempt","built":"the wordless refrain works as a bright ironic mask stretched over the rot beneath"},
"tv-on-the-radio~don-t-love-you": {"haiku":"The end of romantic love after a relationship that held promise; the speaker delivers the final message that their heart has closed.","opus":"Emerging from Brooklyn's art-rock scene, TV on the Radio strip their usual density down to a plain, aching admission. A lover who once meant everything now offers only teasing and taunts, and the narrator finally speaks the cruel sentence that ends it. Sleepless bones and a silent phone frame a breakup where the kindest thing left is honesty about love that has simply drained away.","sonnet":"Love's end announced plainly after trust has collapsed. Two people who gave each other everything discover no simple plan survives when a lover's tongue becomes a source of taunting rather than comfort. He wakes alone, phone in hand, silence his cruelest response, finally speaking the words: the feeling is gone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup song whose refusal is the loudest thing it can't sell. The verses catalogue a love reduced to arithmetic — two people trying to fold something unruly into a diagram they can follow, then failing to make even that math balance — and Adebimpe's tenderness toward the two mismatched figures, one green and one who should know better, keeps undercutting the verdict he keeps arriving at. The weight sits in the closing repetition: the flat announcement that the love is gone gets said four, five times, and each repeat sounds less like a fact stated and more like a fact being drilled into a body that doesn't believe it, the withheld word he names as the cruelest grief there is turned back on himself. What's underneath the mask is the moaning; the title is the thing he needs to say precisely because it isn't true yet.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"denial repeated until it sounds like a body refusing to believe it","built":"title names the thing said precisely because it is not yet true"},
"tv-on-the-radio~dreams": {"haiku":"The song condemns betrayal and cruelty from someone the narrator once loved, describing broken trust and power games dressed as friendship. It frames the betrayer as morally blind and the narrator as their cherished victim of a dead era.","opus":"A wounded plea to someone cruel and self-sabotaging to step off their power trip and be kind; grief for a friendship broken in two, mourning a person the narrator once loved even as their better self falls away.","sonnet":"Someone who has burned their relationships through cruelty and a power-trip mentality is told their dreams are over and their wings have fallen — the narrator mourns what they could have been while accusing them of being too blind to grieve it themselves.","src":"haiku","fable":"A reproach delivered to someone hollowed out by their own cruelty — the refrain announces a fall already complete, wings gone, ambitions finished, said less as threat than as diagnosis. The middle is the tender heart of it: the speaker pleads for kindness and for the other to step off their small grinding circuit of power, then admits the fatal thing, that this person's heart cannot mourn what their eyes refuse to see, and forgives them anyway by casting them as the one bright hour salvaged from a spent and exhausted era. The song's real move is that this grief cannot be argued into a closed heart, so Adebimpe stops arguing and lets language itself take over — an alliterative avalanche of ransacking, looting, ravaging, savaging that enacts the very ruin the words describe, meaning collapsing into sonic momentum. What survives the flood is not accusation but the speaker's own wound, a restless anxious circling repeated until it is worn smooth, so the song ends not on the accused's fall but on the one left watching it, still turning it over.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance"],"means":"forgiveness offered to a heart too closed to receive it","built":"alliterative avalanche enacts the ruin the words describe"},
"tv-on-the-radio~golden-age":{"haiku":"Joy and liberation arrive through music and collective spiritual awakening, dispelling greed and violence in a coming age of miracles.","opus":"A rapturous prophecy of a coming era of wonder and music, where cruelty melts away, spirits are set free, and blessing pours down to fill everyone up. It calls for abandoning rot and greed in favor of joy and shared movement. TV on the Radio, Brooklyn's art-rock collective, deliver it as gospel-flavored euphoria, a rare wholly utopian anthem in their catalogue.","sonnet":"An ecstatic proclamation that a new era is arriving whether or not anyone is ready — the old gold that was believed to be worth something is being revealed as pollution, and what replaces it arrives like a natural disaster, an unstoppable surge of love and laughter that dissolves walls, breaks gods to diamond dust, and asks only that people clap their hands if they believe their souls are free.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~halfway-home":{"haiku":"A troubled relationship involves manipulation and emotional damage; the two exist between connection and distance, neither fully home nor free.","opus":"A dense, grieving meditation on watching someone slip away, tended with useless comfort, and the narrator unraveling in the aftermath. TV on the Radio wrap loss in wordplay and sensory overload, love and damage tangled past separation.","sonnet":"An elegy for someone who seems to have broken or been broken — possibly by illness, loss, or trauma — whose spirit has escaped and changed them irrevocably. The narrator watches from inside the relationship, feeling the person's pain as they shift into something new and untethered.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~happy-idiot":{"haiku":"Post-breakup numbness and self-harm mask the pain of abandonment. The narrator embraces deliberate stupidity and self-destruction to avoid confronting grief.","opus":"Heartbreak medicated with willful oblivion, the narrator choosing to numb himself into a grinning idiot rather than feel the loss. The upbeat pulse underscores the desperation of shutting it all off just to survive one more day.","sonnet":"After being left by someone, the speaker shuts down his mind deliberately to avoid the pain of the loss, embracing a state of deliberate blankness — waving at cars, banging his head against the wall — because ignorance is the only way he can keep functioning. The \"happy idiot\" persona is a coping mechanism, not a genuine state.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~i-was-a-lover":{"haiku":"Romance calcifies into performance; authentic connection dissolves into mutually convenient deception.","opus":"A weary lament for a self hollowed out by war and commerce, once a lover now going through motions, seducing his own clone and sleepwalking through a relationship gone criminal. Alienation dressed in surreal, self-disgusted imagery.","sonnet":"A former lover reflects on the drift from passion to numbness — the self that once burned with desire has been replaced by a sleepwalking figure performing domesticity and commerce, now locked in alienated routines far from the life he remembers wanting.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~king-eternal": {"haiku":"Men are condemned and sacrificed; children are offered up; infinity measures all while humanity gathers supplies for an impending hour of reckoning and immortal transformation.","opus":"An apocalyptic, mock-scriptural address to a capricious eternal king under whom all men are condemned to die. Amid chants to hoard supplies and shield children from thunder, it stages humanity huddling and bargaining under an indifferent, arbitrary power, sex and fertility set against the coming end.","sonnet":"A fragmented, apocalyptic invocation about manhood, fate, and divine legacy — the hour of reckoning is near, power must be protected or surrendered, and a child born into this moment will either shine or be consumed.","src":"haiku","fable":"A doomsday liturgy that swaggers where you expect it to tremble: the intro sentences every man to death under a justice figured as a bandaged-eyed she-hound in her marble halls, and the chorus answers not with awe but with a jeering profanity, the eternal reduced to an expletive. Tunde Adebimpe braids two registers that shouldn't share a room — mock-heroic brawler's bravado (a fighter's crouch, hands guarding the groin, hips thrown into the swing) against genuine end-times dread of thunder and lightning — so the mask of bluster keeps slipping off the fear underneath. The middle turns dynastic, converting catastrophe into lineage: hoard supplies, send up a child, let the pyre's rising ash bear the family name skyward, as if the species' answer to extinction is simply to keep breeding a successor to the throne of the title. By the last verse the address narrows to a single named intimate, Nathan, and the imagery goes golden and carnal — a deathless arousal, mouths loosened past restraint — collapsing the cosmic king eternal into ordinary human appetite and the wish to outlast oneself through the body. What sounds like a hymn to a deathless ruler is really about mortal men crowning their own persistence, half-terrified and half-defiant, and daring eternity to laugh back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult","family & blood","love & desire"],"means":"mortal bravado crowning its own persistence against extinction","built":"brawler's swagger keeps slipping to reveal end-times dread"},
"tv-on-the-radio~lazerray":{"haiku":"A being travels through time and dimensions as pure light, burning with cosmic power that outlasts all creation.","opus":"From Seeds, the 2014 album TV on the Radio made in the shadow of bandmate Gerard Smith's death, this track transmutes grief into pure motion. The narrator imagines becoming a burst of light hurtling through the centuries, accepting that nothing alive endures and reframing mortality as exhilarating velocity, each mind just a brief carrier for energy passing through.","sonnet":"Consciousness as pure energy, shooting through time and dimension. A figure remakes himself as solar fire and laser light, heart burning and unstuck from any moment, carrying only good intentions as he punches through the ages — with the faint hope that two beams of light might one day fuse into one.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~love-dog":{"haiku":"A lonely, nameless figure yearns for love and devotion with a desperate, animal-like devotion that mirrors both servitude and longing.","opus":"An aching address to a lonely, nameless stray of a lover, cast as a devoted dog howling for a master it cannot name. The narrator feels something slow and oceanic stirring in him, torn between patience and the silence that eventually burns. TV on the Radio strip back their usual density here, letting Kyp Malone's falsetto carry the yearning of this tender, canine-metaphor ballad.","sonnet":"Devotion as an animal condition — the self described as a lonely, nameless creature howling at the moon, desperate and without an owner. The beloved is addressed as an unnamed force above, and what begins as patient waiting curdles slowly into something shameless and oceanic, a need that sparks faster the longer it goes unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~love-stained":{"haiku":"Two lovers navigate raw intimacy and collective fear; their bond defies shame and mourning, offering refuge in shared vulnerability during the dark hours.","opus":"Two partners commit to an unguarded closeness, sharing their sorrows and dread with equal tenderness and meeting terror in the small hours with steady devotion. TV on the Radio give their art-rock an almost sacramental warmth, letting grief and desire twine together as the couple vow to carry each other through darkness and daylight alike.","sonnet":"Two people carry grief and physical weight into a relationship that refuses to be ashamed of its own darkness. Morning brings sadness back to the body, but the song insists on pressing through it — choosing presence, warmth, and the other person's heartbeat over the fear that everything dies alone.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~poppy": {"haiku":"Two people maintain separate identities while together through unselfish love, celebrating their independence rather than possession.","opus":"A luminous celebration of equal, non-owning love, delighting that two people remain distinct selves, rise in one bed as separate beings, and won't possess or discard each other. TV on the Radio thread nature imagery of mated blackbirds, dragonflies and clamoring geese through their art-rock exuberance, their giddy insistence that this bond is generous carrying genuine philosophical joy.","sonnet":"Two people celebrate remaining separate selves inside a long intimacy — neither possession nor performance, their love modeled on paired blackbirds and dragonflies: instinctive, unashamed, free-ranging. The joy is specifically in refusing the logic of ownership, choosing a version of desire that does not demand anyone disappear into the other.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song that spends its whole length defining love by negation — not a parent-child dependency, not possession, not the rutting drive of animals in heat. By the outro it can finally name the positive thing it means: a love that keeps no ledger, held between two people who stay separate. Tunde Adebimpe builds the case from nature rather than romance cliché, watching paired blackbirds and dragonflies do everything together while remaining two creatures. The argument turns on that paradox: two people waking under one blanket yet housed in skin that never merges, each blessing the separate head on the next pillow. The tenderness sits in the odd, unsentimental register — congratulating a partner for guarding the self they walked in with, undissolved after all this shared time. The vulgar words (fucking, whore, boars) are load-bearing, dragging the ideal down into bodies and appetite, so the closing claim of a higher love has to earn itself past the ugliest versions first.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","identity & becoming","nature & the elements"],"means":"love defined entirely by what it refuses to be","built":"vulgar words load-bearing before the ideal can be claimed"},
"tv-on-the-radio~quartz":{"haiku":"Two lovers struggle with conflicting desires and lifelong disagreements, their reckless hearts colliding as they roll into fire.","opus":"A love sworn to be sacred yet forever told he ought to abandon it, the relationship tumbling helplessly toward flames across a lifetime of quarrels and strain. TV on the Radio hold devotion and doom on the same crest, wishing to love more truly while sensing it can't endure.","sonnet":"A love song about a relationship the narrator knows they should have let go of long ago — it keeps rolling like a wave into fire, through disagreements and stress, and they keep choosing it despite knowing it can't last forever.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~ride":{"haiku":"An invitation to transcend misery and ride toward collective transformation; the speaker promises magical escape and shared comfort for anyone caught between resolution and confusion.","opus":"An ecstatic surge toward transcendence gathers family, friends, and lovers to leave confusion behind and rise together, dissolving the boundary between selves into a shared flight. TV on the Radio pour their maximalist art-rock into pure uplift here, trading their usual unease for a communal, near-spiritual invitation to abandon misery and take the ride of a lifetime toward the future.","sonnet":"A collective euphoria rises — grief and confusion dissolve the moment someone chooses to board and commit to the momentum. The ride is transformation: miserable people become magical, strangers become each other, and the future feels owned rather than feared.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~satellite":{"haiku":"A satellite signal is lost and sinks into the sea; the singer waits desperately in the depths, searching for a way to reconnect.","opus":"TV on the Radio render loss as lost signal, a departed voice imagined as a satellite that spun beside the narrator and has now gone down into the sea. He becomes the undertow, fathoming leagues and waiting peacefully for any sound of the vanished other. Spare and oceanic, the lyric turns yearning into a search across silence, endlessly asking where a love that has slipped out of range might now be found.","sonnet":"A voice that once felt as close as a signal in orbit has gone silent and fallen, and the narrator descends like an undertow into the depths to wait for its return. Grief is rendered as patient submersion — not frantic searching but a stillness that expects the signal to find its way back.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~seeds":{"haiku":"Two lovers share warnings and sensuality as rain plants seeds; they navigate defenses and jealousy while trusting the rain to grow something real.","opus":"The title track of the band's grief-shadowed 2014 record turns toward stubborn renewal. Addressing a wary, guarded lover whose sister spills unwanted secrets, the narrator keeps planting despite the rain that always falls. He asks her to lay down her defences and dance, framing the ground beneath them as freshly sown. Beneath the metaphor sits a plain wish that something might finally take root.","sonnet":"He circles a guarded, complicated woman whose sister has warned him and whose history makes her defensive. Despite all the friction and the mutual damage, he finds himself falling — and with the ground finally prepared, he dares to hope something will grow this time rather than wash away.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~staring-at-the-sun": {"fable":"A rapture song that keeps its two truths in the same body: the ecstatic surrender of looking straight into something too bright to survive, and the drowning that surrender actually is. Adebimpe addresses a you submerged to the neck in tidewater, but the parenthetical voice underneath — confessing that his own throat can't pull him back to shore, that a single lungful remains before the surface closes over — turns the erotic and the mortal into a single act, jaw parted for a lover and for the water alike. The middle verse pushes back toward the living: mark the standing timber, the ground is only on loan, worth lies past name and money, so spend yourself as brightness while there's a self left to burn. By the end the imagery collapses the sun-blindness into the undertow until they're indistinguishable, and the whispered aside reframes the whole thing as a hell of a wager you can run till dawn — the mask of doomed devotion pulled back to reveal something closer to reckless joy, willing to cast away a life it half-suspects it's already lost.","fableDeep":"The two title gestures each cancel a sense: staring at the sun blinds you by the act of looking; standing in the sea puts you on ground that moves. Orientation is lost as the *precondition* of the ecstasy, not its price. 'We are all weaned, my dear, upon the same fatigue' makes the exhaustion communal, nearly liturgical — everyone was nursed on this. And the song runs two voices at once: the parenthetical one going under ('my own voice cannot save me now… one more breath and then I'll go down') and the main one blessing the descent ('be what you will, and then throw down your life — it's a damned fine game'). Love, death and transcendence aren't disambiguated because the merger is the meaning.","haiku":"Transcendence and physical dissolution; bodies merging as consciousness confronts annihilation through an overwhelming force.","opus":"A portrait of someone submerged and overwhelmed, gazing into a blinding light while the tide rises around them and their own words offer no rescue. It sits with mortality, the fleeting nature of the flesh, and the act of yielding to something enormous and all-consuming.","sonnet":"A figure is overwhelmed by an intense, possibly doomed connection — water rising to the neck, lightning in the teeth, the body consumed — while a voice observes that all human weariness is shared, urging surrender to the moment even at the cost of going under.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","love & desire","freedom & escape","death & grief"],"means":"ecstatic surrender and drowning held as one act","built":"parenthetical voice underneath confesses the singer is also going under"},
"tv-on-the-radio~test-pilot":{"haiku":"A relationship deteriorates from idealization to cruelty; the speaker and lover must find strength to forge a new path, becoming pioneers in their own recovery.","opus":"Framed as a daring aviator, the narrator inspects a ruined friendship he tried and failed to mend, mocking himself for trusting a betrayer, yet still craving to rise above what pulls them apart. TV on the Radio wrap the heartbreak in the language of flight and altitude, soaring even as the bond wrecks itself in ruin and noise.","sonnet":"A friendship or partnership comes apart through carelessness and emotional distortion, yet the two manage to stay aloft — gliding through the damage, needing strength to keep going. The test pilot image frames surviving disillusionment as a kind of brave first flight.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~the-wrong-way": {"haiku":"A complex meditation on Black identity, exploitation, and misdirected resistance, questioning whether protest is genuine or performative. The song suggests that young people's anger is being aimed at the wrong targets while real systems of control remain invisible.","opus":"Tunde Adebimpe's biting reckoning with the burdens placed on a Black artist to perform meaning and morality, and with hollow racial politics. The turn is a refusal to march peacefully, telling the desperate their anger is aimed the wrong way.","sonnet":"A fierce critique of Black Americans being cast in a \"magic negro\" role in white culture — expected to absorb, teach, and perform patience and wisdom while the system profits from their suffering. The song refuses the passive role, invokes Nina Simone, and tells young people to redirect their rage at the actual enemy rather than each other.","src":"haiku","fable":"Adebimpe wakes inside a role written for him — the redemptive Black figure the white indie world floodlights and requires to be its lesson in patience and love. The song's engine is the panic of a man cataloguing which performance he's expected to give: the raised fist, the minstrel's tap-and-shuffle, the protest. Each one is another mask, each one a way of selling himself to an audience that wants the spectacle, not the person. Halfway through the satire curdles into something real: he refuses the palatable options, the peaceful march and the wisdom scattered before the deaf that comes of preaching to the unmoved. What he finds where the assigned righteousness was supposed to sit is nothing but an angry heartbeat, insisting you feel it. The image-system runs on consumption — bling raining down over hacked-off Black wrists, jewels laid out on platters like a meal — so that the beauty the culture buys is always someone's body being eaten. That coiled fury is what makes the closing address land: the desperate youth and bloodthirsty babes of the album's own title are told their guns point the wrong way, aim turned back on themselves and each other while the real machinery goes untouched — a warning against a rage that's been misdirected by design.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","politics & society","identity & becoming"],"means":"assigned righteousness replaced by an angry insisting heartbeat","built":"beauty the culture buys is always a body being eaten"},
"tv-on-the-radio~trouble":{"haiku":"Trouble approaches and disaster looms; the speaker has broken promises and changed their number to hide from ghosts of the past, though they keep telling themselves everything will be okay.","opus":"A man senses catastrophe closing in, the devil holding his number and borrowed time running out, yet keeps chanting reassurances that everything will be okay even as he hides in disguises from broken promises and calling ghosts. TV on the Radio pair this anxious brink-of-collapse mood with a gently swaying, almost lullaby-like calm, the Brooklyn art-rock band letting the mantra of forced optimism carry both comfort and dread.","sonnet":"Doom is incoming — debts called in, ghosts circling, borrowed time almost spent — and the only response is a hollow reassurance loop that even the speaker doesn't believe. Self-deception worn thin enough to see through, but kept running anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~wear-you-out": {"haiku":"A lover describes the overwhelming presence of someone in a crowded room, desiring physical connection and control. The song explores competing rituals of attraction and courtship in a late-night encounter.","opus":"Closing the Brooklyn band's 2006 breakthrough Return to Cookie Mountain, this track prowls through nightclub heat with peacock swagger. The narrator preens like a rooster, sizing up rival men and a woman he intends to pursue until closing time. Bravado curdles into want as the night thins toward dawn, and the boast to exhaust her barely masks how thoroughly she has undone his composure.","sonnet":"Desire plays out in a packed, closing-time venue where every man competes for one woman. He watches the performance of courtship with wry clarity, cycles through posturing and honest self-awareness, and finally cuts past all of it to name what he wants: to spend whatever hours remain before morning together.","src":"haiku","fable":"TV on the Radio close their debut with a peacock's display and let the strut curdle into comedy: the whole seduction gets rendered as barnyard, a room of preening males devolving from roosters to snapping strays while the singer inventories his own plumage and bright wattle comb as if courtship were a species-wide reflex he can only narrate, not escape. That self-mockery is the point — Adebimpe keeps switching registers between the wolf who grabs by the hair and the man who admits he would rather just talk and confess what he wants, so the swagger reads as a costume he knows is a costume. Underneath the animal theater the actual stakes turn quietly tender and human — a woman with the workday nearly upon her, a dark room, entwined limbs — and the title's promise of wearing her out shrinks from conquest to the plain wish to keep someone, to make her his. The joke and the longing occupy the same breath, which is exactly why the display never fully drops.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"courtship swagger worn as a costume the wearer knows is a costume","built":"register switches mid-song from barnyard predator to confessing man"},
"tv-on-the-radio~will-do":{"haiku":"A devoted lover accepts rejection but remains unconditionally ready; time and words matter less than the fact that love endures without conditions.","opus":"The lead single from the band's 2011 album Nine Types of Light offers their warmest romantic gesture. The narrator argues gently that love ignores rules and practicality, insisting any moment will do to begin. A Spanish-tinged bridge invoking a caldera and a red rose lets desire glow openly. Patience rather than pressure defines his suit; he simply waits, certain the right words will draw her back.","sonnet":"Steadfast devotion that refuses to expire, no matter the timing or the words. He holds open an unconditional offer — whenever she is ready, whatever she decides — while acknowledging that the heart ignores practicality. Late in the song the address shifts to a lover described as a volcano, luminous and consuming.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~winter":{"haiku":"A person awakens from blind sympathy and fear to confront a transformative storm, seeking union and redemption through physical and emotional surrender.","opus":"TV on the Radio make a hymn of cold-weather intimacy, a narrator begging mercy and heat while confessing he likes the dark and the slight fright of it. Framed as a confession he means to learn from, the lyric surrenders thinking and reason to physical union, letting lightning strike as long as the two are together. The refrain about keeping a lover for the winter turns the hardest season into a chosen shelter of warmth and desire.","sonnet":"Cold and darkness become the conditions under which a liberating union becomes possible — winter strips away pretense, and a confession of past blindness clears the way for surrender to desire and physical connection. The season of scarcity is reframed as the one most alive with potential.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~wolf-like-me":{"haiku":"Two people recognize matching darkness and monstrosity in each other and embrace transformation into wolf-like creatures bound by lust and hunger. They become predators together, accepting their cursed nature.","opus":"TV on the Radio fuse post-punk with werewolf desire, a narrator bearing a curse triggered by the full moon and aching to pass it on through touch. The change wracks his body, yet he savors it, inviting a lover to share both the affliction and the thrill. Drawing on fairy-tale wolf imagery, it turns predatory hunger and love into one ecstatic, endless chase.","sonnet":"A lycanthropic metaphor structures an erotic invitation — the narrator carries a transformation curse and offers to share it, seducing a partner into the wildness before the change takes hold. Desire and animal instinct are inseparable, the howling a permanent condition rather than a momentary state.","src":"haiku"},
"tv-on-the-radio~young-liars":{"haiku":"Young people navigate betrayal, disease, and the burial of their agency in a cold, lonely world. They become diseased and diminished through deception and emotional manipulation.","opus":"The keystone of the band's 2003 debut EP unspools in dense, prophetic imagery of frigid rooms and second-sighted nerves. A hurt narrator, his mast snapped and his heart a stone rattling in a bare vessel, offers gratitude to the reckless deceivers for clasping his fingers. Doo-wop harmonies rise beneath the dread as he asks whether psychic sight can save him or only ease the wince of being drawn toward sickness and the sea.","sonnet":"Grief and dislocation rendered as wreckage: a damaged man at half-mast, a woman who has spent herself and left him hollowed. He addresses the young liars who buried his agency, moving through loneliness and a fantasy of psychic calm toward a body pulled forward, by strings of voice and instrument, into irreversible disease.","src":"haiku"},
"twelve-foot-ninja~everywhere-i-look-acoustic":{"haiku":"Complicity in violence twists into renunciation: the speaker witnesses destruction orchestrated by elites, vows never to submit, and promises to reclaim what corruption stole from the innocent masses.","opus":"A repentant killer haunted by the blood on his hands, renouncing his past obedience and violence to wage a new campaign against the powerful few who make the world scream, vowing never to bow to them.","sonnet":"A former soldier in the service of violent power renounces his role after witnessing the devastation he helped cause, vowing to reclaim what has been lost and never bow to those who control the world through machination and massacre.","src":"haiku"},
"twelve-foot-ninja~stuck-with-you":{"haiku":"Pragmatic acceptance of inevitable entanglement; relationships endure through inertia rather than passion, bound by logistics rather than love but preferred to solitude.","opus":"A cover of Huey Lewis's wry ode to staying together out of tangled logistics and stubborn affection, shared address, shared friends, too entangled to leave. The joke lands as genuine contentment, choosing the person precisely because untangling is more trouble than love.","sonnet":"A cheerful, resigned love song about two people who considered leaving each other but are now too entangled by shared phone numbers, friends, and address to go — and have discovered they are both happy about it. The shared infrastructure of a life together is the real binding force.","src":"haiku"},
"twenty-one-pilots~ride":{"haiku":"Existential inquiry about sacrifice and commitment confronts the contradiction between rhetoric and action; the speaker spirals in obsessive thought.","opus":"Joseph interrogating the easy talk of dying for people while dodging what living for them actually costs, a sunny groove masking someone who thinks about the end too much and can't stop overthinking any of it.","sonnet":"A restless, philosophically anxious speaker admits to thinking too much about death, loyalty, and what he would actually sacrifice for others — the bravado of \"I'd die for you\" exposed as easier than living for someone — and resolves to take his time falling rather than pretend to certainty.","src":"haiku"},
"twin-pumpkin~i-hate-myself-she-hates-the-world-we-wanna-die":{"haiku":"Substance abuse promises transcendence while breeding mutual destruction; intoxicated bonding masks suicidal ideation shared between lovers.","opus":"Two damaged people using drink, drugs and self-harm as a shared escape from despair, romanticizing mutual self-destruction as devotion when it's really a suicide pact dressed up as love.","sonnet":"Two damaged people find each other in the space between self-destruction and survival — substances, dancing with razorblades, and borderline existence are simultaneously their coping mechanisms and their bond, with love barely holding them above the wish to disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"twin-pumpkin~let-s-put-the-fun-in-funeral":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation and existential decay celebrate darkness as the speaker mourns lost potential and embraces aesthetic death.","opus":"A frantic, loneliness-soaked spiral craving control and courting self-destruction, drawn toward death and closing with the Twin Peaks incantation about heaven and descending into the water.","sonnet":"A surrealist spiral through loneliness, self-destruction, and grandiose nihilism — the speaker wants turbulence, vitamin deficiency, and declares \"suicide is what you deserve cause heroes are your fucking curse\" — closing on Twin Peaks-style ritual imagery.","src":"haiku"},
"twisted-sister~i-wanna-rock":{"haiku":"An anthem of defiance against authority figures attempting to suppress loud music and pleasure. The song celebrates refusal to comply.","opus":"A blunt, joyous refusal to turn it down or stop playing, all defiance boiled to a single unstoppable urge to rock, the music shooting through the narrator like nothing else can. It is anthem as pure appetite. Twisted Sister, the New York glam-metal crew fronted by Dee Snider, made this an eighties rebellion staple, its chant-along chorus and cartoonish swagger a fist raised at every authority saying no.","sonnet":"Pure want, pure refusal — the feeling that music shoots through your body and nothing else compares. Every authority that says turn it down or stop is met with flat rejection. Desire without apology, volume as identity.","src":"haiku"},
"twisted-sister~we-re-not-gonna-take-it":{"haiku":"Youth revolt against the condescending authority that controls their destiny, asserting their right to choose and fight back.","opus":"The eternal teenage anthem of refusing to let condescending elders dictate your life, choosing your own destiny over their trite authority. Its simplicity is the point, a fist-in-the-air rallying cry built to be shouted back.","sonnet":"A rebellion against authority figures who are condescending, controlling, and out of touch — a generational refusal to be told what to do, caged, or defined by someone else's rules, delivered as pure defiant anthem.","src":"haiku"},
"two-and-a-half-girl~carried-away":{"haiku":"Obsessive commitment and driven temperament alienate those who need consistency; the speaker acknowledges their pattern while unable to break it.","opus":"A defense of being wired differently and married to one's own noise, asking loved ones for patience while admitting that following ambition means inevitably letting them down.","sonnet":"About being wired differently from a partner — the speaker is consumed by their own ambitions and noise, unable to give the consistency their relationship needs, and accepts that they will let people down when their passions take over.","src":"haiku"},
"tyler-the-creator~okaga-ca-feat-alice-smith-leon-ware-clem-creevy":{"haiku":"Escape fantasy merges cosmic flight with earthly intimacy; the couple transcends human limitation through shared love.","opus":"A giddy, boyish fantasy of running off with the girl to California and then the moon, the bravado of wanting to abandon Earth barely masking how nervous and smitten he actually is.","sonnet":"A fantasy of escape with someone deeply loved — fly to California, go to the moon, leave Earth behind — unfolds into something warmer and more domestic: declarations of love on a trampoline, sushi dinner dates, a relationship that has quietly become the most real thing in his life.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative-slow-deep-and-hard~unsuccessfully-coping-with-the-natural-beauty-of-infidelity":{"haiku":"Rage and humiliation at discovering a partner's betrayal, with no path toward forgiveness.","opus":"Peter Steele's raw, vicious autopsy of being cheated on, jealousy curdled into misogynist rage he half-recognizes as self-poisoning, the goth grandeur stripped to naked, ugly wounded pride.","sonnet":"Volcanic rage at a cheating partner — the speaker reconstructs her Saturday night infidelity in forensic, obscene detail, cycling between humiliation, fury, and a bitter admission that it still hurts despite his masculinity. Betrayal loops on itself as she drinks alone while he cannot stop knowing.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~a-dish-best-served-coldly":{"haiku":"A wronged person has lost faith in mercy or justice and now seeks revenge, finding honor in retribution and viewing the world as a lawless place where only vengeance matters.","opus":"A cold vow of revenge after a betrayal: having put a deceitful person first and been burned, the singer swears he's not sorry and dreams of rotting enemies and eye-for-an-eye justice in a lawless world.","sonnet":"A narrator who was betrayed by someone he trusted renounces remorse, desires revenge, and frames personal justice as the only law in a world where the righteous are compromised.","src":"haiku","fable":"Steele frames a betrayal as a slow-motion court proceeding, and the whole song is the aggrieved party appointing himself judge, jury, and executioner once he decides earthly law is bankrupt. His error, he admits, was trusting someone else's justice over his own, so he withdraws his faith into private vengeance and declares that the corpses of his enemies could never stink foul enough to satisfy him. The chorus is where the gothic pose curdles into something colder than the doom-metal grandeur promises: the taunting refusal to apologize or to care is the real dish being served, revenge staged as bored indifference rather than heat. And the gallows humour Steele is famous for lives in the title's contradiction — a man this obsessively occupied with retribution is precisely the one insisting he has already stopped caring.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"revenge performed as bored indifference rather than heat","built":"the title's contradiction exposes the obsession it denies"},
"type-o-negative~all-hallows-eve":{"haiku":"On Halloween night, a man performs dark rituals to resurrect his dead beloved. Through sacrifice and ancient spells, he brings her back to life to be together again.","opus":"A gothic Halloween-night necromancy ritual: the narrator performs sacrifices and incantations, bargaining his soul with Lucifer, in a desperate attempt to resurrect a beautiful dead lover he can't let go of, romanticizing her corpse as more stunning in death than in life.","sonnet":"A man performs occult rituals on Halloween night in a desperate attempt to resurrect his dead lover, trading his soul to Lucifer for the chance to pull her back from the earth — and in the final verse, the corpse actually rises.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a necromancer's love song, and Steele plays it straight enough that the grief underneath the pageantry keeps leaking through the theatrics. The bonfires, blood offerings, and grinning pumpkins are the horror-movie set dressing you expect from a man who built a career on gothic camp, but the real engine is a bereaved lover kneeling at a fresh grave trying to spell his dead beloved back into the world, finding her lovelier embalmed and frost-glazed than she ever was alive. What makes it more than costume is the crack in the ritual's own nerve: mid-incantation the spellcaster admits he performs the rite yet cannot make himself believe in it, so the bargains struck with hell and the boasts that the working cannot fail read as bravado, the noise a man makes to keep from hearing that she is simply gone. The pagan pomp is finally just the loudest way to say he wants her back.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","love & desire","faith & the occult"],"means":"ritual bravado masking the certainty of permanent loss","built":"the spellcaster mid-incantation confesses he cannot believe the spell"},
"type-o-negative~an-ode-to-locksmiths":{"haiku":"A dark mythological reinterpretation where Adam, not Eve, is the serpent who seduced and violated Eve, reversing the biblical narrative to blame male desire and sexual violence.","opus":"A provocative, blasphemous riff that inverts the Eden myth, recasting Adam as the serpent and man as Satan, framing the forbidden fruit as sex and declaring nowhere left to go.","sonnet":"A dark theological allegory that reinterprets the Garden of Eden story: the narrator argues that the serpent is actually male (Adam), that Eve was forced rather than tempted into eating the fruit, and that this is the origin of antagonism between men and women.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele runs his last testament through the King James cadence he was raised in and then turns it inside out: the locksmith who was given the keys reads Genesis backward, so that the serpent and Adam collapse into one figure and the Fall becomes an assault, the man the true Satan and the woman his victim. The gift of opening every door becomes the curse of understanding too much — a mind that can no longer un-know how sex, sin, and blame were wired together at the origin. And when the argument exhausts itself, the theology drops away entirely into a chant that admits the real terminus: not damnation, just the flat, homeless refusal of nowhere left to go — the sound of a man who has unlocked everything and found no room worth entering.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a mind that can no longer un-know how sex, sin, and blame were wired at the origin","built":"theology drops away entirely into a chant of nowhere left to go, the argument exhausted by its own gift"},
"type-o-negative~anesthesia":{"haiku":"A narrator describes emotional numbness as a defense against love's pain, having been burned and traumatized before. They view caring as futile and choose anesthesia over feeling, rejecting connection to avoid vulnerability.","opus":"A bitter meditation on love and trust as sources of inevitable pain, in which the speaker chooses numbness and emotional shutdown over feeling, envying the dead and reaching for anesthesia to stop feeling anything at all.","sonnet":"Burned repeatedly by love and trust, the narrator concludes that emotional numbness is preferable to feeling, and that anesthesia — the total absence of sensation — is the only option left when all others are exhausted.","src":"haiku","fable":"Anesthesia turns Steele's usual romantic dread into something closer to a clinical shutdown notice. The song runs an argument: love is first framed as a wager everyone loses — trust as the trigger that begins the illusion, each betrayal a lesson that refuses to stick — and then, its case made, the speaker doesn't grieve so much as elect to feel nothing at all, downgrading himself from lover to a man who would rather be numbed than aware. What lifts it past a breakup dirge is the self-contempt under the gothic pose: he names himself a failure at both dying and living, stranded in a purgatorial middle where simply continuing feels like the mistake being repeated, and admits that hiding is cowardice rather than dressing it up as stoicism. So the title lands as the only honest option left once the sneering wit burns out — not peace, not death, just the deliberate deadening of a man who has decided that awareness itself is the wound.","fv":2,"themes":["dissociation & numbness","heartbreak & loss","shame & repression"],"means":"electing numbness once awareness itself becomes the wound","built":"the title names the only honest option the speaker has left"},
"type-o-negative~angry-inch":{"haiku":"Details a botched transgender medical procedure and the lasting physical and psychological trauma that resulted. The song expresses bitterness about transformation becoming mutilation.","opus":"A gallows-humored, defiant tale of a gender-reassignment surgery gone horribly wrong, leaving the narrator with a maimed scrap of flesh. Recounting the botched procedure and the shame that followed, they harden that injury into a badge of identity and a willingness to strike back at the world.","sonnet":"A cover of the song from the musical Hedwig and the Angry Inch, narrating a botched gender-reassignment surgery and its aftermath — told with dark humor, the speaker describes leaving their homeland, reinventing themselves, and being permanently defined by the surgical failure, one inch of flesh that encapsulates their entire history.","src":"haiku","fable":"A botched surgery leaves the narrator neither the woman promised nor the man she was — a single ridge of scarred flesh that Trask's lyric reads as a face with no eyes, a grimace turned sideways, the body itself sneering at the deal it was tricked into. The song's engine is a refrain of forward motion that loses more ground than it gains, a gait that can never arrive, and Type O Negative's cover makes that stalled progress the subject rather than the punchline: where the musical plays the number as glam cabaret bravado, Peter Steele's funereal baritone drags it into doom-metal gravity, so a joke about a maimed body becomes a genuine dirge for being stranded between categories with no country, no name, and no way to climb up off the track. What survives the recasting is the defiance — the mutilated remnant claimed as a weapon, a wounded thing that refuses to be only a wound.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"a mutilated remnant claimed as weapon not merely wound","built":"doom-metal gravity recasts cabaret bravado into genuine dirge"},
"type-o-negative~are-you-afraid": {"haiku":"A stark, repeated urging toward suicide, presenting self-harm as release from fear of death.","opus":"A darkly provocative taunt that dares the listener not to fear death, goading them toward suicide with grim, mocking directness.","sonnet":"A blunt, provocative address that urges someone not to fear death and frames suicide as an act of daring — stripping away any emotional mediation and presenting self-destruction in cold, direct terms.","src":"haiku","fable":"A mock-tender suicide sermon delivered deadpan, the come-on cadence of an infomercial or a seducer turned on the listener: are you afraid, don't be afraid, go ahead. Steele's baritone plays counselor while the counsel is monstrous, and the tell is the instruction to die laughing — the song wants you to hear that it is a dare, a goad, a middle finger aimed as much at anyone earnest enough to be shocked as at any real despair. The fake-live crowd noise stitched onto this Origin of the Feces recording completes the prank: the horror is staged, performed for jeers, and the mask of nihilism is the whole point rather than a confession beneath it. What sits underneath the provocation is Steele's genuine gothic contempt — for wellness pieties, for the listener's flinch, for himself — expressed as the ugliest joke he can make and then repeat until the word suicide is drained to a hollow refrain.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"the suicide instruction delivered as dare aimed squarely at the listener's flinch","built":"fake-live crowd noise frames the horror as a staged prank completed only in performance"},
"type-o-negative~be-my-druidess":{"haiku":"A person expresses primal sexual desire and intimate connection with someone under firelight, willing to do anything to bring their lover to ecstasy.","opus":"An erotic invocation set at a pagan fire rite, the narrator making love to a priestess amid a circle of thirteen and a towering blaze. The plea for her to be his everything fuses ritual and lust, with his repeated vow to do anything to bring her to climax.","sonnet":"Explicit pagan sexual fantasy: sex by firelight in a ritual circle of thirteen, the narrator consumed by lust for a woman he imagines as a druidess, describing intercourse in plain physical detail.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele plays the seduction straight as a black-mass rite and then lets the ritual frame fall away entirely: the druidic circle, the pyre, the thirteen are stage-dressing that dissolve once the song narrows, in its final movement, to the flat repetition of a single vow to please her. The pagan mysticism turns out to be a costume worn over ordinary devotional lust. That last incantatory loop is the real tell—the mantra shrinks from the whole promise to just the willingness itself, the ceremony he actually means, the goddess-worship stripped down to a man saying he'll do anything.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"pagan ritual as costume worn over ordinary devotional lust, dissolved by its own ending","built":"the final incantatory loop shrinks from the full promise to just the willingness itself"},
"type-o-negative~black-no-1":{"haiku":"Dark allure as mirror: loving the darkness means becoming complicit in its hunger, and the romance of dread feeds itself endlessly.","opus":"An affectionate, gothic send-up of a vain goth girl obsessed with darkness and her hair dye, half in love with her, half mocking the death-obsessed pose. The joke and the desire coexist.","sonnet":"A darkly affectionate song about a goth woman who is genuinely in love with her own darkness — she dates vampires, worships Halloween, dyes her hair black, and the speaker finds her irresistible precisely because loving her feels like loving the dead.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~black-no-1-edit":{"haiku":"Goth aesthetic celebrates constructed darkness; the beloved becomes unknowable object of fixation, beauty inseparable from death and spiritual emptiness.","opus":"Peter Steele's tongue-in-cheek serenade to a self-absorbed goth girlfriend, all vampire cosplay and hair dye, loving her compared to loving the dead. The gothic camp barely hides real exasperation at her theatrical narcissism.","sonnet":"A darkly affectionate portrait of a goth woman in love with herself, the dark, and Nosferatu — she dyes her roots black, casts spells, and loving her was like loving the dead. The speaker's fascination is equal parts mockery and genuine infatuation.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~black-no-1-little-miss-scare-all":{"haiku":"A Goth aesthetic and performed darkness fascinate the speaker, who equates loving someone like this with the impossible task of loving the dead.","opus":"A fondly teasing tribute to a goth girl fixated on the macabre, her jet-dyed hair and year-round Halloween pose. Under the theatrics, adoring her feels like adoring a corpse, vanity and morbidity intertwined.","sonnet":"A deeply sardonic love song about a goth woman who is completely devoted to her macabre aesthetic — midnight dates with vampires, wolf boots, Halloween every day — and the narrator is simultaneously mocking and enchanted by her, admitting that loving her felt like loving the dead.","src":"haiku","fable":"The narrator courts a self-adoring goth girl as a checklist of borrowed monsters—Nosferatu, Lily Munster, a pale throat the Devil has already signed—so the seduction is really an accumulation of costumes, and the hook fixes on the most mundane prop of all: the box of black dye that has to keep the roots from showing, the maintenance that betrays the whole undead pose as a chosen, upkept fashion. The joke stays tender through the verses because he plays along admiringly, but the song develops by letting the vamp comedy collapse into the coda's incantation, where loving her turns into loving the dead, repeated past sense until affection curdles into necrophilic flatness. That final movement is where the mask comes off: the eternal-Halloween girl who likes the dark isn't glamorously deathly so much as unreachable, and the endless repetition performs the deadness it names—desire circling a partner who was never really there.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"A satire of goth-scene vanity Steele composed in his head while driving a garbage truck.","fnote2":"Bloody Kisses (August 1993) became Roadrunner's first Gold record, broken open by this and Christian Woman.","themes":["artifice & performance","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the eternal-Halloween partner exposed as unreachable, the desire circling someone never really there","built":"the box of black dye betraying the roots punctures the whole undead pose as maintained fashion"},
"type-o-negative~black-sabbath-from-the-satanic-perspective":{"haiku":"A reimagining of darkness and evil from the devil's point of view; rejecting moral absolutes and embracing shadow perspectives.","opus":"A seductive retelling from the devil's own mouth, inviting a pure young woman to his throne, promising to grant every dark craving, and swearing vengeance on a feeble God as he preaches worship of the sun over any deity. Type O Negative reimagine Black Sabbath, Peter Steele's cavernous baritone and the band's gothic doom lending the blasphemy velvet menace and black humor.","sonnet":"The original Black Sabbath nightmare flipped to the devil's point of view — Satan as seducer rather than terror, welcoming a pure young woman to his throne with desire rather than menace. The song reframes damnation as an invitation, fire as pleasure, and the weak God as simply outmatched, while the flames climb higher behind the courtship.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~blood-and-fire":{"haiku":"Love transformed into eternal anguish; betrayal burns and bleeds endlessly while the speaker searches for a replacement they'll never find.","opus":"Mourning a love that disappeared with no warning, still spotting her likeness in every burst of fire and holding no one but himself responsible. The gothic pairing of gore and flame makes the yearning feel endless and infernal alike, solitude preferable to anyone who isn't her.","sonnet":"A man mourns a relationship that ended without explanation, unable to move on or love anyone else, haunted by the face of the woman he lost and burning with longing he can't extinguish — the title is the state of his grief, not something he wants more of.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song sounds like a breakup lament but keeps tipping into something more permanent and violent: the beloved didn't leave, exactly, and the wound the singer carries is one he wants to reciprocate in kind, so that bleeding and burning become a shared vow rather than a complaint. Peter Steele's method here is to run the two vocabularies of the title as one system — blood as love, lust and covenant, fire as passion and damnation — until they stop being metaphors for feeling and start naming an actual condition the two of them are locked inside, needing indistinguishable from burning. The turn where her features keep surfacing in whatever burns comes paired with having only himself to blame, reading the obsession as self-inflicted haunting: he would rather be alone with the ghost than with any living woman, which is the giveaway that the ended relationship is being kept alive by refusal to let it end. The final stripping of the refrain, where the paired words are pulled apart and repeated alone, enacts the dismantling the lyric claims to want — taking the very phrase apart syllable by syllable while still unable to stop saying it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"obsession kept alive by the refusal to let the ended relationship end","built":"the refrain pulled apart syllable by syllable while the speaker still cannot stop saying it"},
"type-o-negative~blood-fire-out-of-the-ashes-mix":{"haiku":"Painful love that consumes; the obsessive intensity of desire mixed with the agony of unrequited or lost connection.","opus":"Type O Negative, the Brooklyn goth-metal band fronted by the imposing Peter Steele, pour their morbid romanticism into a song of abandonment. A partner disappeared without explanation, leaving the narrator scorched and aching, still wanting a body he can never reclaim, seeing the vanished lover everywhere and choosing loneliness over any substitute as devotion sours into obsession.","sonnet":"Loss without explanation — she left without warning, no reason given, and the speaker is left cycling through grief and longing, seeing her face in every flame. The love was real and total and now it bleeds and burns as a kind of permanent weather, impossible to move past, impossible to replace.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~bloody-kisses-a-death-in-the-family":{"opus":"A gothic double-suicide pact: one lover takes her own life, and the narrator resolves to follow her into the grave to reunite two souls. Peter Steele drapes grief in vampiric imagery, the bloody kiss making death itself the consummation of a love that couldn't survive.","sonnet":"About a woman who took her own life and the partner she left behind — he intends to follow her into death, imagining bleeding into her, finding her lips in the dark, and their hands joined again; the song moves from helpless grief to suicidal resolution framed as reunion.","src":"sonnet","fable":"Peter Steele writes a duet that only one voice ends up singing. The first movement is her suicide, delivered as cold reportage from the outside; the second is his vow to follow, where the wall of death dividing them becomes something he means to dissolve by opening his own veins to reach her. The genius is the pivot at the spreading red slick: as he bleeds toward that promised reunion, the numbed serenity of joining her collapses into panic, and the pleading not to go turns out to be aimed at himself in the act of dying, not at her already gone. The bloody kiss is the whole scheme in one image, love and wound made identical, romance indistinguishable from hemorrhage. That the closing refrain keeps begging her not to die on him while the singer is the one bleeding out is the mask coming off: the grand gothic elegy for her was always terror at his own extinction.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"By Steele's own account an elegy for Venus, his cat of seventeen years — nobody wants to hear a six-foot-eight man crying about his cat, he said, so he made it extremely metaphorical.","themes":["suicide","death & grief","love & desire"],"means":"the grand gothic elegy for her was always terror at his own extinction","built":"the closing plea not to die turns out to be aimed at himself bleeding out, not at her already gone"},
"type-o-negative~burnt-flowers-fallen":{"haiku":"A relationship deteriorates as the beloved burns away tokens of affection, symbolizing love's destruction.","opus":"A dazed grief over a woman losing her love for him, the singer shifting from suspecting to certain it's finished. Her act of setting fire to every bloom he ever brought her makes the rejection tangible and beyond undoing, romance turned to cinders.","sonnet":"A simple, devastated observation — the speaker watches as someone falls out of love, expressed through the image of every flower he gave her being burned: the gift returned as ash, the relationship quietly incinerated.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is almost entirely the two-line refrain, and the movement lives in one small verb shift: the speaker moves from suspecting her love is leaving to knowing it has left, the tremor of denial hardening into fact across the repetition. Peter Steele's baritone leaves him with only one concrete image to hold against that certainty — the flowers he gave her, which she burned — so courtship's stock gift becomes the instrument of its own refusal, ash where devotion was. The obsessive looping isn't padding but the shape of grief circling a wound it can't stop touching, and the mask of stoic repetition barely covers a man watching, in real time, love being deliberately destroyed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss"],"means":"courtship's stock gift burned by the recipient into the instrument of its own refusal","built":"a single small verb shift from suspecting to knowing hardens denial into fact across the looping"},
"type-o-negative~can-t-lose-you":{"haiku":"Possessive refusal repeated with fragmented crude sexual commentary interspersed as disconnected spoken asides.","opus":"Type O Negative, kings of gothic gloom and gallows humour, use this brief piece as an inside joke rather than a love song. The refrain of not being able to lose someone dissolves into crude studio banter, including a jab at a record-label figure. It reads less as devotion than as the Brooklyn band's trademark irreverence, puncturing any expectation of sincerity with deadpan vulgarity.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~christian-woman":{"haiku":"Portrays sexual repression and shame intertwined with religious devotion, depicting a woman trapped between religious doctrine and bodily desire.","opus":"A religious woman whose devotion becomes erotic, conflating worship of a suffering Christ with sexual desire and guilt over lust she believes will damn her.","sonnet":"The song portrays a devout woman whose religious devotion is entangled with repressed sexual desire — her cross, her prayers, and her guilt converging in an interior conflict between faith and bodily longing.","src":"haiku","fable":"The whole song lives in a single blasphemous pun: the woman's devotion to a suffering crucified man is indistinguishable from erotic worship, so the Corpus Christi she takes at communion becomes the body she craves in bed, and Peter Steele delivers it in a mock-liturgical baritone that treats the confusion as sacrament rather than joke. Its three cantos stage a deliberate slide from the outside in — first a diagnosing narrator cataloguing her guilt and inevitable damnation with sneering theological wordplay, then the bridge collapsing all distance into pure yearning to know and feel God bodily, then the breakdown's flat, hypnotic refrain that God wears the singer's own face. That last turn is the real payload: having dissolved the sacred into the sexual, Steele lets the object of worship curdle into narcissism, the seducer casting himself as the deity she debases herself before. The gothic-Catholic guilt is played with genuine relish and total irony at once, so the mockery of her shame and the arousal by it are the same gesture — you cannot tell whether the mask is scorn or complicity, which is exactly the point.","fv":2,"fnote":"Steele’s own liner notes trace it to a real Roman Catholic girlfriend who liked breaking the rules — she’d ask him to dress as a priest — leaving him, in his words, with a bit of a priest infection.","themes":["faith & the occult","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"sacred devotion and erotic craving made the same gesture","built":"three cantos slide from diagnosing narrator to the singer wearing god's face"},
"type-o-negative~christian-woman-edit":{"haiku":"Sacred feminine becomes sacrificial object for male appetite; religious imagery corrupts into pornographic submission, transforming faith into domination.","opus":"Steele skewering the collision of Catholic guilt and repressed desire, a devout woman eroticizing her crucifix and mistaking lust for worship. It provokes deliberately, arguing that shame-bound faith and sexual hunger are tangled at the root.","sonnet":"A woman's erotic fixation on Christ is rendered with deliberate blasphemy — the dying god-man on her bedroom wall becomes the object of sadomasochistic religious longing, confession of sin doubles as arousal, and the body of Christ she seeks is carnal. The closing \"Jesus Christ looks like me\" collapses the sacred and the profane into one.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~cinnamon-girl":{"haiku":"A romantic fantasy about living with a dreamed-about woman while making music with her. The speaker seeks her support to achieve success and live together harmoniously.","opus":"Type O's doom-laden Neil Young cover drenches a wistful young-love song in gothic gloom, the German count-in and dirge tempo transforming yearning into something heavier. Steele finds the ache beneath the innocence and drags it into shadow.","sonnet":"A cover of Neil Young's song rendered faithfully: a romantic daydream of living with an idealized woman and chasing moonlight together, with a plaintive aside to a parent for money to sustain the dream.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele opens with a barked German count-in, and that gag is the whole reading: a mountain of a man in a doom band takes Neil Young's hippie reverie and plays it straight-faced. The menace turns the tenderness into something obsessive rather than sweet, and when the singer begs his mother to wire money so he can make it somehow, the cover exposes the boyish helplessness already sitting under the fantasy, so the dreamer chasing moonlight sounds less like a lover than a man clinging to a picture he can't quite reach.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"a doom giant's straight-faced tenderness exposing the boyish helplessness already in the original","built":"the barked German count-in is the entire reading: menace reframing another artist's reverie"},
"type-o-negative~creepy-green-light":{"haiku":"Death, obsession, and resurrection; waiting in a grave for a lover to rise and return from the underworld.","opus":"A grieving lover lies drunk on his dead beloved's grave, waiting for her to rise; guided by an eerie green light and her final words not to be afraid, he feels drawn into her shadowy realm to join her in death.","sonnet":"On Halloween, a grieving lover lies drunk on his dead beloved's grave, waiting for her to rise from beneath the soil, drawn toward the eerie green light that marks her supernatural return to him.","src":"haiku","fable":"A widower keeps his vigil the only way Peter Steele's brand of romance knows how: face-down and drunk against a headstone, waiting for the dead to keep a promise. What lifts this above graveyard kitsch is how completely it takes the necrophile's logic at its word — the phosphor glow isn't a warning to flee but an invitation the narrator has been told not to fear, and the whole seasonal machinery of rotting leaves and low October fog is recruited as a wedding rather than a decay, so that the grave splitting open reads as reunion instead of horror. Steele plays it with total conviction and a straight face, which is the joke and also the ache: the punchline is that he actually gets what he wants, the beloved rising on schedule, and the chanted vow about being pulled toward her shadow domain lands less as seduction than as a man narrating his own glad surrender to the underworld.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","love & desire","faith & the occult"],"means":"reunion with the dead reframed as a glad surrender","built":"decay vocabulary borrowed whole as wedding vocabulary"},
"type-o-negative~day-tripper-medley":{"haiku":"A shallow person offers only temporary escape and false connection; the narrator realizes they were used for fleeting pleasure with no real commitment.","opus":"Peter Steele runs a Beatles medley through Type O's slow gothic murk, turning the sunny original into something heavy and menacing. The playful jab at a fickle, noncommittal lover becomes a droning fixation, the band's trademark of dragging pop into the crypt.","sonnet":"A cover of the Beatles' Day Tripper fused with If I Needed Someone — a woman who offers just enough to keep you interested but never commits, a day tripper who plays at connection without meaning it.","src":"haiku","fable":"A medley is an act of curation, and what Type O Negative choose to splice tells you more than any one of the borrowed songs would alone: they take the sunny brush-off of Day Tripper, the conditional half-love of If I Needed Someone, and let both collapse into the airless, endlessly repeating weight of She's So Heavy. Where Lennon and McCartney kept the day-tripper's contempt light and the maybe-I'll-call-you cool, Steele's baritone drags each into the same gravity well, so a fling and a shrug and an obsession stop being three moods and become one descent. The reading is in the sequence — flirtation flattened into fixation, the pop hook worn down to a single crushing word — which is the band's whole gallows-humour method: play someone else's throwaway love song straight until its buried heaviness is the only thing left standing.","fv":2,"themes":["artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"a fling and a shrug and an obsession collapsed into one descent","built":"sequencing three borrowed songs until only the heaviest survives"},
"type-o-negative~dead-again":{"haiku":"Confronts chronic addiction and the cycle of death and resurrection it brings, acknowledging self-destruction while claiming some things are worse than death. The song portrays addiction as literal dying and rebirth.","opus":"A bleak reckoning with addiction, the narrator confessing he is beyond saving and warning others off his road. He recounts overdosing until he felt lifeless, valuing his dignity above survival, and lands on the grim verdict that certain fates are grimmer than dying.","sonnet":"A first-person account of severe drug addiction delivered as a warning — the speaker describes overdosing repeatedly, losing self-respect, accumulating physical damage, and arriving at the conclusion that some things are worse than death, while explicitly telling the listener not to follow the same path.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele stages addiction not as a slide toward the grave but as a loop of mornings that keep restarting, each dawn met with genuine astonishment and revulsion that the body still works. He hands the recovery-meeting confession to the worst possible messenger, cataloging his own wrecked plumbing and failing heart while pointing to a Brooklyn cemetery by name as his own destination, offering the ruin as a warning he knows nobody heeds. The real dread isn't dying; it's the sentence of waking, the dignity he'd surrender his pulse to buy back, the certainty of having to do the whole rotten resurrection over again tomorrow.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Steele said it plainly in 2007: the title track is about \"fucking up\" — relapsing after months sober, one line and you're gone for a week. He offered it as a cautionary tale.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief"],"means":"the real dread is waking and having to resurrect again tomorrow","built":"recovery-meeting confession handed to the worst possible messenger"},
"type-o-negative~die-with-me":{"haiku":"A woman has left in pursuit of ambitions while the speaker grieves her absence, yearning to drown in shared destruction rather than suffer separation.","opus":"Grief over a woman who left to chase ambitions of books and praise, the narrator obsessively cataloguing airports and postal systems tied to her departure; the deeper wish is romantic annihilation, to hold her one last time and burn together rather than survive the loss.","sonnet":"A song about aching loneliness after a lover leaves to chase her own ambitions — the narrator clings to airports, phone lines, and foreign postal systems as proxies for connection, fantasizing about dying together rather than living apart.","src":"haiku","fable":"Under the guise of a doom-crooner vowing to burn away entwined with his beloved, the track is a study in being deserted: a woman has taken flight toward ambitions elsewhere, leaving the narrator marooned at the terminal, dramatizing sorrow he can barely carry. The romantic suicide pact is only costume; what pulses beneath is desertion wearing the costume of devotion, the fool's role he assigns himself standing in for a wound he cannot name aloud. That explains why the final verse dissolves into hailing airlines, telephone lines, and mail services, begging faceless carriers whether affection survives, since the one person who could answer has quit the line entirely.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Reportedly about a girlfriend leaving for school — a departure Steele felt as a minor death.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"a romantic suicide pact as costume worn over desertion the speaker cannot name aloud","built":"the final verse dissolves into hailing faceless carriers, exposing the real wound: no human answer is coming"},
"type-o-negative~everyone-i-love-is-dead":{"haiku":"Repeated loss and grief over everyone close dying; using alcohol and disdain to mask the pain of serial mourning.","opus":"Peter Steele's gallows-humor lament for accumulating grief, the way death keeps taking the people he adores. The mordant laughter admits that booze and dope only paint over a wound that keeps reopening.","sonnet":"The speaker confronts accumulated grief as the people they have loved die one by one, finding that alcohol and drugs mask rather than heal the loss, and arriving at a bitter, darkly self-aware accounting of survival and what it costs.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele builds this as a grief that has outlasted its own solemnity: the losses have arrived so regularly, every three or four years, that mourning has calcified into flat comic exasperation, the recurring curse muttered at God the way you'd swear at a jammed drawer. The gallows humour is the whole argument — the tossed-off laughter riding the chorus is not relief but the sound of a man who has run out of gestures large enough for the accumulation, so he shrinks the enormous into a shrug. Steele keeps catching himself reaching for anesthesia and naming it as a coward's coat of paint over rot rather than a repair, which sets up the real turn: standing at the mirror he confesses he loves himself for the venom he carries, then instantly undercuts even that with a muttered aside, refusing to let the one self-lacerating insight harden into a pose. What the closing wall of repetition does is wear the phrase down to pure rhythm by saying it until it stops meaning anything — the point being that when everyone is gone the words for it exhaust themselves too, and all that survives is the drone and one more oath thrown at the ceiling.","fv":2,"themes":["death & grief","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"grief accumulated past the scale of any available gesture","built":"repetition wears the phrase to drone until words exhaust themselves"},
"type-o-negative~everything-dies":{"haiku":"Mortality, grief over lost loved ones, and universal human death.","opus":"Grief and existential despair over mortality, mourning dead relatives and dreading the death of everyone loved, including oneself.","sonnet":"Grief over the deaths of family members and the universal fact that everything and everyone dies.","src":"haiku","fable":"The vitamin-chant intro is the whole joke and the whole horror in miniature: Steele opens on a note of childish, cheerful self-care and then spends the song watching everyone he loves get subtracted, each death delivered in the same flat singsong meter so that grief and nursery rhyme become indistinguishable. The gallows humour is the mask, and the mask is exactly the point — that mock-jaunty tally of aunt, uncle, dad, dying mother, doomed girlfriend keeps the same tune precisely because feeling any of it fully would be unsurvivable. What the deadpan is hiding surfaces when the meter finally breaks and he turns the verdict on himself, wishing he'd die too, and the title stops being a shrug and becomes the confession underneath it: the deadpan was never nihilism but a man too flooded by loss to say plainly that he misses them.","fv":2,"fnote":"Steele’s father died in February 1995, and part of the song’s video was shot in the shipyard where he had worked; bandmate Josh Silver recalled Steele struggling to record it because it hit so close.","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"deadpan tally of loved ones as the only survivable grief register","built":"a vitamin-chant intro frames each death in nursery-rhyme meter"},
"type-o-negative~gravity": {"haiku":"Suicidal ideation and existential despair weighing heavily on the narrator. Something external or internal pulls them down, making continued existence unjustifiable.","opus":"Peter Steele's gothic wallow in suicidal despair, gravity itself figured as the crushing weight of a lover and a future gone black. His trademark gallows humor and a jarring club-bomb-scare coda undercut the darkness, half sincere, half theatrical self-mockery.","sonnet":"A stark depiction of suicidal despair, in which the speaker feels the weight of a hopeless future crushing him — gravity as a metaphor for the force pressing him toward death. The song ends abruptly with an out-of-place voice announcing a bomb threat and urging the crowd to leave.","src":"haiku","fable":"The track wears despair as spectacle, a first-person collapse toward oblivion staged for a crowd that isn't there, since the whole fake-bootleg album frames it as a concert. Steele lets the personified force of the title become an accusing second party, the gloom of being dragged earthward reworked into a lover's blame. Then he punctures his own theater with the mid-song bomb-scare patter, refusing the audience the tidy catharsis such a confession promises.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance"],"means":"despair staged as spectacle for a crowd, then the theater punctured mid-confession","built":"the bomb-scare patter refuses the audience the catharsis the confession was building toward"},
"type-o-negative~green-man":{"haiku":"A figure embodies nature's cycles, experiencing spring, summer, autumn, and winter while feeling the earth's transformation through seasons. The green man represents nature's inevitable circle and the return to the earth.","opus":"Sung in the voice of the pagan foliage deity, the piece travels the four seasons as a cycle of birth, growth, rot, and death. Beneath it lies reverence for nature and a calm acceptance of mortality, existence completing its loop as winter's cold delivers the singer to his own ending.","sonnet":"A meditation on the pagan archetype of the Green Man — the spirit of nature cycling through all four seasons — told from the inside, with the narrator identifying as the force that rises with spring, burns in summer, worships autumn, and accepts the death-like freeze of winter as part of a full circle of life.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song follows the sun through a full solar year, each verse a season, its narrator the pre-Christian vegetation deity who is spring's difficult germination, summer's high noon, autumn's harvest, and winter's dying — a foliate god named plainly only in the refrain, so that identity accretes across the cycle rather than being declared. Movement is the whole point: the seasons run from struggling upward through stubborn soil to lips gone blue in the snow, and what looks like an arc toward death resolves instead into a wheel, the closing turn to a Wiccan companion recasting the end as the cycle swinging back rather than stopping. The one shift out of the calendar — autumn's cold mistress worshipped on the knees — quietly braids the seasonal god to erotic devotion, so the annual death he undergoes reads at once as surrender to a lover and to the earth. Sung by a famously mortality-haunted voice, the mask here is not despair but its pagan answer: to be the green man is to already know you rot and return.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Steele's paganism straight up — nature, the four seasons, rebirth.","themes":["nature & the elements","faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"a pagan answer to mortality: to be the green man is to already know you rot and return","built":"the arc toward death resolves into a wheel when the Wiccan companion recasts the ending as the cycle swinging back"},
"type-o-negative~hail-and-farewell-to-britain": {"haiku":"Betrayal by false friends corrodes trust completely; the speaker withdraws from those who concealed deception while claiming loyalty.","opus":"A bitter, pun-laced kiss-off to a treacherous former friend or ally revealed as a false royal. Peter Steele's gallows humor turns the severing of a poisoned bond into a grand farewell, blessing and cursing England and himself in the same breath.","sonnet":"A bitter farewell to a group of people — possibly British associates — who turned out to be disloyal, emotionally manipulative, and a waste of time; the speaker cuts ties with cold contempt, lamenting the time wasted on people whose loyalty and decency were always illusory.","src":"haiku","fable":"A kiss-off aimed at a betrayer, staged as a mock-royal proclamation: the once-trusted friend is knighted and then banished in the same breath, and the punning opening line — royalty curdling into slime — announces up front that all this heraldic pomp is a joke told at the target's expense. Steele works the whole betrayal through a courtly vocabulary of treason, conspiracy, and the wrong God chosen, casting the falling-out as a small kingdom's palace coup so that a private grudge gets the grandeur of a national event. Underneath the ceremony is plain nausea — the sick recoil of a man who let himself be manipulated by someone whose mask finally slipped — and the coronation-then-exile refrain, a hail-and-goodbye to a whole country that is really only one person, becomes the vessel for it. The last salute is the tell: he dismisses England, then dismisses his own departure too, admitting he is as much cast out of this ruined brotherhood as the traitor he is expelling.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","friendship & loyalty","exile & diaspora"],"means":"a private betrayal dressed in the grandeur of a kingdom's treason","built":"the hail-and-farewell structure exiles the betrayer and the speaker in the same breath"},
"type-o-negative~halloween-in-heaven":{"haiku":"Imagines a festival in the afterlife where dead musicians celebrate together, blending Halloween and Christmas imagery. The song playfully explores death as freedom and release, with celebrity musicians continuing their legacies eternally.","opus":"A darkly comic fantasy of the afterlife as an endless party where dead rock musicians play together and holidays blur into one another. Beneath the humor runs real grief over lost idols and a morbid suggestion that death is so appealing the narrator wishes he'd died sooner.","sonnet":"A darkly comic afterlife fantasy in which deceased rock legends — Bonham, Entwistle, Bon Scott, Randy Rhoads, Hendrix, Lennon, Harrison — party together in heaven while hell hosts Christmas, blending genuine grief for lost musicians with absurdist humor about death being cooler than life.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele stages the hereafter as a costume party where the church calendar has collapsed into pure decoration — Satan trimming a stolen evergreen with the damned, the Messiah working the door in his own inverted disguise, every holy season reduced to greasepaint. The gag's engine is a phantom supergroup of overdosed and crashed rock casualties propping up their instruments one last time, a lineup that reads as an obituary set to a shuffle beat, with the punchline that the King, never truly buried in fan mythology, missed the guest list. But the novelty is a Trojan horse: the confession that learning how painless the other side felt would have hastened his own exit is a suicidal wink under the makeup, and the verse where mourning the departed forged the man he became is the raw grief the whole joke was built to smuggle. That shrugging refrain is the tell — mortality waved off because an eternal wake is exactly the invitation Steele half-wants.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"From Dead Again, the band's final album — Steele was dead himself three years later, April 2010, at 48. The female voice haunting the choruses is Tara VanFlower of Lycia.","themes":["death & grief","suicide","faith & the occult","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"grief smuggled inside a costume-party punchline","built":"the novelty gag is a Trojan horse for a suicidal wink"},
"type-o-negative~haunted":{"haiku":"A nocturnal lover obsesses over a ghostly, sensual presence who haunts his sleep and embodies lust incarnate, blending erotic desire with supernatural imagery.","opus":"An erotic obsession with a nocturnal lover who is half-woman, half-apparition, tied to imagery of graves, death and demons. The speaker dreads the morning because dawn ends the haunting; his desire is inseparable from the grave, lust conflated with the pull toward death.","sonnet":"A man is sexually and emotionally consumed by what appears to be a supernatural female presence — a nocturnal spirit whose lovemaking is so intense it destroys his sleep and his days, and whose essence is death and decay.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song hymns an incubus-lover who visits only in sleep, so the read has to live in the split between night and day. Peter Steele builds the whole thing as a devotional aubade run backwards: instead of dreading dawn because it ends a real tryst, the speaker dreads it because it proves the tryst was never real — she is a nocturnal spirit, a graveyard scent and a voice in the trees, and daylight leaves him alone, awake, and drained by a woman who exists only as apparition. The movement is the argument: the opening heat of the body in the sheets curdles, verse by verse, into the funereal imagery of her essence, so that lust and death are named as the same substance rather than as opposites. The refrain that he hates the morning is the hinge on which the mask turns — beneath the gothic seducer's swagger sits plain insomniac loneliness, a man in love with an absence that only the dark will grant him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"lust and death named as the same substance rather than as opposites","built":"the devotional aubade runs backwards: dawn proves the tryst was never real"},
"type-o-negative~hey-pete":{"haiku":"A dark adaptation of a folk narrative: Pete murders his unfaithful partner and flees, seeking refuge in Brighton Beach.","opus":"A reworking of the folk staple Hey Joe, in which the narrator, asked where he's headed with his weapon, admits he intends to slay his unfaithful woman and escape on the subway to Brighton Beach. Type O Negative drag the murder ballad into their gothic Brooklyn gloom, the deadpan menace and local transit detail recasting old blues bloodshed in their trademark mix of dread and pitch-black comedy.","sonnet":"A retelling of the Killing Floor blues archetype: a man discovers infidelity, acts on rage, and then has nowhere left to go — riding a train to Brighton Beach as escape from consequences, sending his victim to heaven in the same breath he names the destination.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~highway-star":{"haiku":"A driver personifies their car, girl, and speed as possessions and extensions of self, careening recklessly at breakneck velocity.","opus":"Type O Negative's gothic take on Deep Purple's speed anthem, the muscle-car lust and possessive swagger over machine and woman recast in doomier tones, adrenaline as its own killing-machine heaven.","sonnet":"A cover of the Deep Purple classic — a possessive, passionate celebration of a car and a woman, both treated as prized possessions the narrator won't let anyone take, driven by pure speed and desire.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~how-could-she":{"haiku":"A person has become emotionally dependent on fictional TV and cartoon characters instead of real relationships.","opus":"A lonely man's absurd, melancholy lament over being abandoned, cataloguing dozens of classic TV and cartoon women as the objects of his affection. The dark comic truth is that he has become so addicted to television that fictional women are his only love, aware they aren't real yet feeling jilted.","sonnet":"Satirical obsession with fictional female characters: the narrator lists dozens of female TV and cartoon characters he has developed feelings for, then admits in a moment of clarity that he knows they aren't real — and has become addicted to television instead of human connection.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele builds almost the entire song out of a roll-call — Wilma Flintstone, Morticia Addams, Lieutenant Uhura, Vanna White, Wonder Woman — women who exist only on cathode screens and comic pages, so that the recurring cry of betrayal lands against people who were never his to lose. The joke is that jealousy this operatic has no rival and no object: the beloved is a broadcast, and the studio names he recites like a rosary (Disney, Hanna-Barbera, Warner Brothers) are the real household gods here. Underneath the deadpan sits Steele's actual subject — a man who has traded a livable romantic life for a paper harem, half-aware the screen cannot answer him and unable to stop asking it why. That final turn from how could she to how could you swings the accusation inward, conceding the fault was always his own retreat into the flicker rather than any woman's leaving.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"jealousy staged against women who were never real to lose","built":"the accusation swings inward when how could she becomes how could you"},
"type-o-negative~i-don-t-wanna-be-me":{"fable":"Peter Steele hides a suicide behind a nursery-rhyme count and Bo Diddley bounce, and the gap between the jaunty delivery and the content is the whole trick. The verses tick off a man's undoing in glass-house proverbs and tallied colors before he freezes to death asleep in the snow, alone, while the refrain's self-loathing turns out to be his, not a pose. Then the mask drops for the bridge's instruction: no black at the wake, come to celebrate rather than mourn, a request that reads as generosity and as the last self-erasing wish of someone who never wanted to be himself.","fableDeep":"The pronouns do the telling: the chorus is first person ('I don't wanna be me') but the verses are third ('he fell asleep in the snow, never woke up, died alone') — the speaker has already stepped outside himself and narrates his own end at a distance. The glass-houses arithmetic (two houses, twenty stones, fourteen yellow, six blue) turns self-blame into a child's counting game, and the 'one, two, three, four' count-ins keep yanking the despair back into showbiz — gallows humor as life support. The wake instruction is both punchline and sincere wish: don't mourn the me I didn't want to be.","haiku":"Existential self-rejection and refusal of one's own identity; the speaker's life is marked by failure and the desire to cease being.","opus":"A song about self-loathing and a wish to no longer be oneself, framed around a man who lives poorly, dies alone in the snow, and a request that his wake be a celebration rather than a mournful gathering in black.","sonnet":"Through the story of a man who lives in mounting failure and dies alone in the snow, the song expresses a deep wish to shed one's own identity, while the closing request — celebrate at the wake, don't mourn — frames that self-rejection with dark resignation.","src":"haiku","fv":2,"themes":["suicide","comedy & wordplay","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"gallows humor as life support around a quietly narrated self-erasure","built":"verses shift to third person so the speaker has already stepped outside himself to narrate his own end","fvr":"2.4"},
"type-o-negative~i-like-goils":{"haiku":"A woman declares her sexual preference for women and rejects traditional courtship and male advances with sharp defiance and humor.","opus":"A crude, tongue-in-cheek insistence that the narrator is straight and attracted only to women, rebuffing a man's advances with mockery. He declares his preference for women over and over while rejecting the come-ons directed at him.","sonnet":"A blunt, humorous rejection of advances from a man, in which the narrator — straight and unapologetic about it — declares his exclusive attraction to women and tells the unwanted suitor to leave.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is Peter Steele weaponizing a gay panic gag into a running joke that keeps overshooting its target, the tattooed EXIT ONLY on the ass turning homophobic bluster into pure locker-room slapstick. But the punchline undercuts the swagger it pretends to defend: the man so loudly insisting he only likes bad girls spends the whole song addressing another man, obsessed enough with the imagined advance to itemize every scenario for rejecting it, and Steele knows it, tossing off the admission that he hates all men including you with a wink that lets the doth-protest-too-much read hang there. The gallows humour is the point — Type O built a career on Steele playing the leering brute while signaling he's in on the absurdity, so the crudeness reads less as a manifesto than as a self-aware bit about a guy whose anti-PC posturing is a mask for how much he can't stop thinking about the thing he claims to despise.","fv":2,"fnote":"Steele said the song pokes fun at PC — written after his 1995 Playgirl centerfold drew a wave of unwanted male advances.","themes":["shame & repression","comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"homophobic bluster that keeps overshooting the target it defends","built":"the protest addressed entirely to the man it claims to reject"},
"type-o-negative~in-praise-of-bacchus":{"haiku":"Recounts a bittersweet relationship marked by desire and disappointment, set against Brooklyn's industrial landscape. The narrator recalls a lover who wanted to burn together but ultimately left him behind.","opus":"A drink-soaked, bittersweet reminiscence set on the streets of Brooklyn, addressing the god of wine while recalling a woman who hates him yet vowed they would burn together. It weaves memory, longing, and intoxication into a haunted love scene.","sonnet":"A figure drinks to dull the pain of a relationship in which the woman hates him — set against a wet Brooklyn night, the song moves through fractured street-level images and ends with a mutual call to burn together, blending bitterness with a strange intimacy.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele hails the wine-god as the only companion who will still drink with him once the woman has issued her flat verdict of hatred, so the title's praise is deadpan and sincere at once. He drags the reader through his rain-glazed corner of Brooklyn — harbor lamps on wet stone, the Statue of Liberty demoted to a lonely girl waving her torch at empty water, cobbles pressed flat like the lid of a tomb — until the whole borough reads as a graveyard staged for a doomed romance. Her promise that the pair of them will end in one shared blaze is the nearest thing this couple has to a vow, self-destruction dressed up as devotion. The slurred mock-Latin at the close turns it into a drunkard's funeral mass, half-liturgy and half-blackout, and reveals the ode as anesthesia: he keeps toasting Bacchus because the bottle is the only thing left that won't walk away.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss","death & grief"],"means":"praising the wine-god because the bottle will not walk away","built":"mock-Latin at the close turns the ode into a drunkard's funeral mass"},
"type-o-negative~iydkmigthtky-gimme-that":{"haiku":"Mutual threat of survival where one party must eliminate the other out of necessity.","opus":"A coldly detached character study of a deranged stalker who schemes murder while deflecting responsibility onto genes, upbringing, and diagnosed madness, issuing a chilling either-or ultimatum of mutual killing. The clinical, third-person remove makes the menace all the more unsettling.","sonnet":"The song adopts the clinical detachment of a psychological or legal report to describe a stalker-like obsession — the subject, referred to as 'it,' lies awake planning, fantasizes about a woman's self-destruction, and the refrain frames the whole dynamic as a kill-or-be-killed ultimatum.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title's unpronounceable initialism — a threat compressed into a password the band never officially decoded — is the whole joke and the whole horror: Steele narrates a predator entirely in the third person, calling the killer an it rather than he, so the menace gets pre-laundered into a thing that cannot be held responsible for what it does. That grammatical trick is the engine — every clause reaches for an alibi, pinning the compulsion on genes or upbringing, ventriloquizing the victim into confessing she brought it on herself, then stacking up the language of probation departments and clinical insanity to reach a verdict of legally unaccountable before the crime is even committed. The ultimatum at the center strips all of it away: kill or be killed, your choice not mine, the same fatalism Steele always wielded as gallows comedy, here worn as a stalker's mask over a plain threat. Read straight it is a monster begging for the exculpation a court might hand him; read as the joke Steele intended, it is a savage parody of exactly that excuse-making, the old proverb about heaven favoring the self-reliant mangled into a killer's motto so the piety curdles into a checklist of ways to duck the blame.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"a predator pre-laundering his alibi before the crime is committed","built":"the third-person it strips the killer of a subject who can be blamed"},
"type-o-negative~kill-all-the-white-people":{"haiku":"A revolutionary slogan calls for racial annihilation as a path to liberation; the mood conveys a transgressive, provocative stance dressed in irony and power rhetoric.","opus":"A deliberately provocative chant flipping racial hatred back on its author, the white singer voicing calls to kill all the white people as satire aimed at the absurdity of prejudice and reverse-racism panic. Type O Negative, the Brooklyn gothic-doom band led by Peter Steele, built a career on this kind of grim, transgressive humor, the shock-value title a self-aware taunt rather than any sincere manifesto.","sonnet":"A satirical provocation delivered in the bluntest possible terms, inverting racist rhetoric to expose its absurdity. The chant form is deliberate — the emptiness of the sloganeering is the point.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~kill-you-tonight":{"haiku":"Rage and hatred consume someone, transforming them into a violent stranger to themselves, culminating in a death threat.","opus":"Type O Negative open with Peter Steele's gothic sarcasm toward a heckler before plunging into pure consuming rage, hatred and anger turning the narrator into someone he no longer knows. The shaking fury builds to a blunt threat to kill his target tonight, half theatrical menace and half genuine loathing. Bookended by stage banter daring the heckler to the front, the track blurs performance and violence in the band's signature bleakly comic register.","sonnet":"Hatred and rage escalate through a person until they no longer recognize themselves — the emotions possess rather than belong, reshaping identity into something foreign and violent. The threat at the center is less directed outward than it is a horror at what the self has become.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~less-than-zero-0":{"haiku":"A person views himself as worthless and inhuman, made only of bodily waste and corruption, questioning God's love while condemning mankind as inferior and cursed.","opus":"A bleak, self-loathing meditation on being made of filth and decay, questioning why God won't grant release and casting suffering as a punishment that its victims volunteered for.","sonnet":"A bitter self-portrait: the speaker catalogues his own worthlessness in grotesque bodily terms, mocks any notion of a heroic self, and declares that those around him are all willing participants in their own suffering.","src":"haiku","fable":"Steele stages his own damnation as a cosmic joke told against himself, a man begging a God he suspects has stopped picking up to end the suffering — the divine silence reframed as a suicidal cry that goes unanswered because heaven, apparently, let it ring. The middle of the song is a deliberate degradation, cataloguing the body down to its most abject fluids and secretions to insist the self is nothing but the wound left when the scab is peeled back, all its heroic armor a fiction where nothing holds true. What lifts this above mere self-loathing is the final inversion, Steele's signature move: the sinner turns preacher and rounds on the listener, declaring the damned line up for their own sentence — everyone in hell showed up willingly, and that indictment folds you into the joke too.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","suicide","shame & repression"],"means":"damnation staged as a cosmic joke told against himself","built":"the sinner turns preacher to fold the listener into the indictment"},
"type-o-negative~life-is-killing-me":{"haiku":"Medical institutions and authority figures exploit the vulnerable, turning life itself into a profitable instrument of suffering.","opus":"A scathing attack on doctors and the medical system, comparing physicians to executioners and quacks, and demanding the right to die with dignity rather than be kept alive to run up a bill.","sonnet":"The narrator rages against the medical establishment as corrupt and incompetent, arguing that doctors are overpaid and useless, and demanding the right to die with dignity rather than be kept alive against his will on life support.","src":"haiku","fable":"Steele stages the sickbed as a courtroom, indicting American medicine as a racket that shares its trade with older persecutors — the Inquisition, the con artist, the death-camp physician — men whose credentials only license them to bill you for the privilege of not being cured. The gallows humour is the mechanism, not the mood: every degree and diploma is worthless against the affliction, so the healer becomes an executioner working in slow motion, dragging out the dying to inflate the invoice. What tips grievance into genuine terror is the bridge, where the speaker begs to be unplugged and reframes the whole institution as a charity-branded inferno kept running for profit — the horror is not dying but the machine that refuses to let him, and his plea for a dignified end reads as the one honest transaction anyone in the building will offer.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","death & grief","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"the healer recast as executioner billing for a slow-motion dying","built":"the plea to be unplugged is the one honest transaction the institution offers","fvr":"2.4"},
"type-o-negative~love-you-to-death":{"haiku":"Obsessive devotion and servitude to a lover, with the speaker willing to surrender completely and be consumed by love.","opus":"Peter Steele's gothic devotion to a lover, worship and surrender laced with the beast he can barely restrain. It reads as sex and death entwined, adoration teetering on something predatory.","sonnet":"A gothic erotic devotion — the narrator is transfixed by a woman's physical presence, positions himself as her servant, and repeatedly offers a love so total and overwhelming that it borders on annihilation, the phrase \"love you to death\" literalising a surrender of self.","src":"haiku","fable":"The courtly language of the servant—begging to serve, calling her wish his law—is a mask worn over the predator it keeps announcing, so that every vow of devotion carries the same warning that a beast is coming for her, the two registers never resolving because the sweetness is the lure. The pun in the title holds both meanings at once: to love someone to death is Gothic hyperbole for total adoration and a literal promise of murder, and the song refuses to choose. What breaks the spell is the bridge, where the seducer's practiced control collapses into a small, unguarded question of whether he is good enough for her—the outro's naked repetition revealing that under all the ceremony of dominance sits terror of rejection, the real engine the whole seduction was built to hide.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"devotion and threat occupying identical words, the sweetness the lure for both","built":"the bridge's small unguarded question collapses the seducer's control into terror of rejection"},
"type-o-negative~my-girlfriend-s-girlfriend":{"haiku":"A polyamorous relationship in which three people find intimacy and acceptance together, indifferent to society's judgment.","opus":"A cheerful ode to a polyamorous threesome, with the narrator delighting in the closeness he shares with two women who are also involved with each other. He shrugs off what others might say as the trio walks openly together.","sonnet":"A cheerful, matter-of-fact account of a consensual three-way relationship between a man and two women who resemble each other, presented as an uncrowded and perfectly natural arrangement.","src":"haiku","fable":"A giddy rush-song that keeps a straight face over a sly reveal: the singer is the least essential point of a love triangle, one for whom the other two women would clearly do fine alone, and the tell is that his girlfriend's girlfriend has her lover's exact face — so the arrangement he brags about as doubled fortune is really him orbiting a couple that mirrors itself. The image-system runs on stuck-together adhesion and interlock (things fused, tangled, folded into one shape) building toward the geometry of three sharing a single figure, so the delight is genuine but the math quietly undercuts the swagger. The mask is the leering boast of two-for-one abundance; underneath is a man happily counting himself as the odd, uncrowded third, unbothered because the pleasure outweighs the arithmetic.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"A polyamory romp written quickly at Josh Silver's house; the Jon Reiss two-women video ran with it.","fnote2":"October Rust (August 1996) is the lush turn — and it opens on gag tracks, a laughing spoken intro and the near-silent Bad Ground.","themes":["love & desire","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"doubled fortune quietly exposed as orbiting a couple that mirrors itself","built":"the girlfriend's girlfriend having her lover's exact face is the geometry the boast never names"},
"type-o-negative~nettie":{"haiku":"A mother's unconditional compassion and sufferance become the narrator's salvation, despite their own failures. The song is a tribute to maternal sacrifice and forgiveness.","opus":"The song is a tender tribute to Nettie, a compassionate woman whose empathy for others' pain becomes her own suffering, who loves the flawed speaker despite the grief he caused. He comforts her, promising to catch and be there for her as she has been for him.","sonnet":"The song is a tribute to the singer's mother — an empathetic, deeply religious woman from Brooklyn who weeps nightly for others' pain and responds to her son's failings not with anger but with prayer. He acknowledges her compassion has been its own form of suffering and calls her the most beautiful person he has known.","src":"haiku","fable":"Nettie is Peter Steele's tribute to his gravely ill mother, and the whole song works by inverting the doom-metal frame from menace to helpless gratitude: the man usually cast as the towering vampire here shrinks into the guilty son who cannot fathom being loved by someone so much better than him. He canonizes her in the vocabulary of Brooklyn sainthood — a beauty-queen title, a rosary said nightly, a heaven located just a few blocks over near Cobble Hill — so that her ordinary borough life reads as literal sanctity, her empathy reframed as a curse that makes her suffer for everyone else's pain. The Latin invocation dropped in the middle deliberately mimics a Marian prayer, casting her as the mother of God, and the twist that lands hardest is that the chorus flips ownership: after admitting his shortcomings caused her grief, he takes her role, promising to catch her and wipe her tears, the child finally trying to comfort the parent who spent a lifetime comforting him.","fv":2,"fnote":"Nettie is Annette, Steele’s mother — gravely ill with diabetes when the album was made. She died two years later, in 2005; Steele later called it the worst thing that ever happened to him.","themes":["family & blood","faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"the guilty son finally trying to catch the parent who caught him","built":"a Latin Marian invocation casts a Brooklyn mother as literal saint"},
"type-o-negative~pain": {"haiku":"A torturer derives sadistic pleasure from their victim's agony, using violence as revenge for past betrayal.","opus":"Peter Steele plays a torturer relishing his victim's agony, a deliberately grotesque, over-the-top revenge fantasy that leans into shock and gallows humor, closing with the band mocking the whole spectacle.","sonnet":"Rape as revenge fantasy delivered with grotesque relish — the speaker describes sexually torturing someone who wronged him, framing it explicitly as punishment and pleasure combined, then abruptly breaks the fourth wall to announce the show is over.","src":"haiku","fable":"A first-person torturer's monologue that keeps fusing the wrong families of sensation until they can't be told apart: agony reads as ecstasy, torment as treasure, another body's blood and screaming as the speaker's own arousal and laughter, all of it framed as payback for some earlier burning. Steele plays the sadism at cartoon extremity on purpose — the compressor and jackhammer machinery, the schoolyard cruelty of the line about this hurting him more than you — so that the horror curdles into the band's signature drab joke rather than any real confession. On the semi-live Origin of the Feces the mask is doubled by the staging: siren, stage patter, and a closing insult to the crowd bracket the atrocity as a bit, a wind-up, the misanthrope daring you to take offense. What sits underneath the provocation is the usual Type O move — self-laceration wearing a bully's face, the pleasure so overstated it reads as contempt turned inward as much as outward.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"sadism performed at cartoon extremity until contempt curls inward as much as outward","built":"siren, stage patter, and closing crowd insult bracket the atrocity as a bit on the semi-live record"},
"type-o-negative~paranoid": {"haiku":"A depressed, emotionally disconnected person cannot feel love or happiness and pleads for help understanding human connection.","opus":"Type O Negative's take on the Black Sabbath standard about depression and emotional deadness. A man who has ended things with his partner scowls perpetually and dreads his own unraveling because joy and affection register as nothing to him. He urges others to savor their lives while begging someone, anyone, to wake him up, convinced his own chance has already passed.","sonnet":"A cover of Black Sabbath's 'Paranoid' — a man whose woman couldn't reach his depression, who can't feel happiness or love as real things, and who tells the listener to enjoy life while admitting it's already too late for him.","src":"haiku","fable":"Sabbath's original is a confession from inside anhedonia — a man who has just walked out on a relationship and finds the exit fixed nothing, because the deadness was never about her; he can watch other people take pleasure in being alive and reach for none of it himself. What Type O Negative select, in covering it, is the recognition scene: the narrator admitting he is not built of iron and asking, without much hope, for someone to reroute the wiring so warmth might reach him. Peter Steele drops the original’s amphetamine sprint into a Brooklyn doom-crawl, so the original's brisk irony curdles into something the band plainly means — the drab depressive comedy Steele already trades in was sitting inside Ozzy's lines, and he simply moves into the house. The chorus keeps swapping the first person for the third mid-plea, which stages the split precisely: the sufferer narrating his own case from the outside, too estranged from his interior to claim the numbness as his own. It closes on the cruelest turn — the invitation to enjoy one's life arrives too late for the man delivering it — the diagnosis and the sentence spoken in a single breath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"a sufferer narrating his own numbness from outside, too estranged to claim it","built":"the chorus swaps first to third person mid-plea, staging the split between sufferer and narrator"},
"type-o-negative~prelude-to-agony":{"haiku":"Desire burns the body; sexual pursuit becomes an instrument of mutual destruction and self-annihilation.","opus":"Peter Steele turns lust into a gothic horror show, framing desire as a burning, fatal compulsion that curdles into rage and grotesque revenge, sex and death fused with his trademark self-lacerating theatrics.","sonnet":"Sexual desire is figured as fire that destroys the spirit just as surely as flame destroys flesh — the song escalates from theological questioning into a violently sadistic fantasy in which the speaker takes revenge through domination.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~pyretta-blaze":{"haiku":"A dangerous, incandescent lover draws the speaker toward self-destruction like moths to flame. The relationship is framed as burning and incineration, consummation through combustion.","opus":"Steele casts destructive desire as a woman made of fire and himself as the willing moth, an ode to a consuming love he knows will incinerate him, drawn helplessly toward his own beautiful ruin.","sonnet":"A man surrenders completely to a woman he figures as living fire — knowing full well she will incinerate him, he submits to being her slave, finding the prospect of being consumed by her erotic and absolute rather than terrifying.","src":"haiku","fable":"A love song built entirely on the moth-and-flame conceit, where Steele plays the willing insect and never pretends the ending is anything but combustion — desire here is indistinguishable from immolation, and he wants it that way. What keeps it from melodrama is the Type O signature: the woman is literally personified fire, a burning body he serves like a devotee, so the courtship vocabulary of pinching and biting and searing reads as both erotic ritual and a martyr's inventory of how he'll be undone. The gothic joke Steele is telling is that total devotion and total annihilation amount to the same vow — to name her his beginning, his ending, and his dying breath all at once is to admit that loving her is simply choosing the manner of his own burning.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"total devotion and total annihilation offered as a single vow","built":"the beloved is literal fire making the courtship a martyr's inventory"},
"type-o-negative~red-water-christmas-mourning":{"haiku":"The speaker mourns lost loved ones during Christmas, transforming the holiday into a melancholic ritual of absence and ghostly presences.","opus":"Peter Steele's dirge for a Christmas spent grieving the dead who once filled his table, the holiday turned into a haunted funeral. Its gothic gloom is really raw mourning for everyone who is gone.","sonnet":"A Christmas spent alone and grieving — the narrator dines at a table set for fewer than last year, the people who filled those seats now dead, and drinks heavily to chase away the ghosts of everyone they've lost.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele collapses the Christmas carol into its own homophone — the morning of the nativity becomes a morning of mourning — and stages the holiday as a survivor's inventory: stockings kept for the dead, a table laid for seven where eleven sat a year ago. The diminishing count does the grief that no direct statement will. The red water of the refrain works as the song's one unstable image, sliding between sacramental wine, blood, and the drink that lets him keep seeing the ghosts he's begging it to chase away — so the cure and the haunting are the same liquid, and the wish to be rid of the dead is inseparable from the drinking that summons them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written for Steele's father, who died on Christmas morning — with three more family members lost since the previous Christmas.","themes":["death & grief","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the cure for haunting and the haunting itself are the same liquid","built":"a diminishing headcount does the grief no direct statement will, the missing seats the elegy"},
"type-o-negative~september-sun":{"haiku":"The narrator obsesses over a woman named Elizabeth and her stealing of his soul, unable to escape the memory across years.","opus":"A grief-wracked man howls a woman's name into the rotting autumn, insisting she was never real while grieving a love that consumed him across a decade. Tormented by her memory, he pleads only to be left undisturbed with his haunting.","sonnet":"A man named Peter is still haunted years later by a woman named Elizabeth — her autumn presence stole something vital from him, and the song circles obsessively around warnings to leave her alone and the unanswered question of why it had to be him.","src":"haiku","fable":"Grief here does not stay grief; an autumn-decay imagery keeps souring one season into the next, and the mourning turns accusatory, the beloved Elizabeth summoned and blamed in the same breath. Two voices split the psyche between them: one verse-voice insists she was scarcely present, editing memory into armor, while the other watches from outside and asks whether the grieving man still knows his own name. The repeated command to leave her alone floats free of any single speaker, readable as plea, self-address, or exorcism, until a Spanish aside pushes the private wound toward something closer to ritual. Then the outro strips it bare, the stammered self-pointing and its flat admission of knowing why swiveling the whole indictment inward onto the one who always understood the cost.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The Elizabeth addressed is a former girlfriend — the same figure haunting Hail and Farewell to Britain; the album-wide grief for Steele's mother colors the song without being its subject.","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind","shame & repression"],"means":"mourning curling accusatory until the indictment swivels fully inward","built":"the repeated command to leave her alone floats free of any single speaker as plea, self-address, or exorcism"},
"type-o-negative~set-me-on-fire":{"haiku":"A summer girl ignites desire through passionate repetition; she sets the speaker alight through heat and sensation.","opus":"Built almost entirely from a summer-girl chant, this cut from the goth-metal band trades their usual funereal weight for sunlit lust. Peter Steele's baritone reduces desire to its simplest heat, a woman who sets him ablaze, repeated like an incantation. The near-absence of narrative is the point; infatuation here needs no argument, only the endless invocation of warmth and flame.","sonnet":"A brief, sun-drenched infatuation: a summer woman ignites something uncontrollable in him. The lyrics are almost entirely ritual repetition of the feeling, letting the heat of attraction speak for itself with little narrative beyond the fact of being set alight.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~she-burned-me-down":{"haiku":"Betrayal by someone close—family or intimate—cuts deeper than death; the speaker seeks forgiveness yet feels drawn toward the betrayer in a cycle of pain.","opus":"A song about betrayal by someone close, insisting that being wounded by family or a loved one through love is worse than death, while confessing a perverse thrill at watching her destructiveness.","sonnet":"The speaker was betrayed by someone close — family or friend rather than a stranger — a wound made worse precisely because of the trust involved. Despite rage and disappointment, they are trying to find forgiveness, while admitting the person has done something worse than death to them.","src":"haiku","fable":"Two cantos that name the same event from opposite sides. The first is a betrayal reckoning where the poison is love itself and the wound comes from kin rather than strangers, driving the speaker to a moral crisis so total that forgiveness becomes the only alternative to not living, until the closing accusation flips grief into contempt — the person is rated worse than death. Then the second canto abandons argument entirely for an addict's refrain: watching her set the fire, the speaker only gets higher, the same line looping and even dissolving into invented gibberish, so the structure stages what the first half diagnosed — he keeps returning to the thing that destroys him, arousal fused to annihilation. The movement is the point: what Canto I condemns as the worst thing a person can be, Canto II confesses he is helplessly drawn to, and the burning-down of the title reads at once as ruin and as the only high left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","heartbreak & loss","family & blood"],"means":"what canto one condemns canto two confesses he is helplessly drawn back to","built":"the second canto dissolves into invented gibberish, staging the collapse it describes"},
"type-o-negative~some-stupid-tomorrow":{"haiku":"Inability to forgive and cycles of violent, self-destructive behavior. Therapy and punishment blur together in a loop of regret and shame.","opus":"A struggle with guilt, anger and the difficulty of forgiveness, the narrator lashing out at walls and rejecting therapy or jail as fixes. He wants to leave behind stupid tomorrows and yesterdays alike, unable to fully forget.","sonnet":"A song about the inability to forgive, move on, or find productive outlets for rage — punching walls only produces bruised knuckles, therapy is slow, anger remains. The refrain dismisses any promise of resolution as \"some stupid tomorrow\" that never arrives.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele voices a made man trying to confess and never landing it: he says he cannot pardon anyone, then concedes no pardon reaches him either, so the loyalty owed by kin becomes the substitute for a conscience he cannot make work. He punches walls to prove his nerve and earns only the ache of it, and with counseling too gradual and prison unthinkable, he keeps feeding on his own decay like a nursing child, the chanted refrain of shared blame turning guilt into something he rehearses aloud. Where the track truly lives is its final circling, tomorrow and today and yesterday folding into one worthless span he pleads to be rid of, so the loop never absolves anything but only spins, each coming day squandered before it can even arrive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","family & blood","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"guilt rehearsed aloud because conscience won't arrive on its own","built":"past-present-future fold into one worthless span the refrain loops without resolving"},
"type-o-negative~stay-out-of-my-dreams":{"haiku":"A narrator is haunted by intrusive dreams of a lost lover from New York, unable to escape memories despite her alleged incestuous feelings toward him.","opus":"An obsessive lament over a woman the singer can't stop dreaming about, complicated and made transgressive by revelations framing her as a sibling and an incestuous taboo, as he begs her to stay out of his dreams.","sonnet":"The narrator is haunted in sleep by a woman from Staten Island he can otherwise suppress from his waking thoughts, and the fantasy becomes more disturbing when she reveals in an email that knowing they resemble siblings — possibly are siblings — heightens her attraction.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~summer-breeze":{"haiku":"Idealized domestic contentment and romantic bliss; summer as the season when love and presence feel complete and perfect.","opus":"A tender domestic idyll of coming home to a lit window, the smell of jasmine, plates set for two and arms reaching out, everything unhurried and all right. It is the Seals and Crofts soft-rock standard, warmth without a care in the world. Type O Negative recast it in their gothic gloom, the Brooklyn band's affection for unlikely covers turning a breezy seventies love song into something darker and strangely sincere.","sonnet":"Domestic contentment as pure sensation — jasmine in bloom, a Friday evening, the smell of food cooking and arms waiting at the door after a long day's work. It's a July that asks nothing of you except to feel it, and the whole song is the feeling of walking back into a place where someone is glad you came home.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~suspended-in-dusk":{"haiku":"Vampire immortality as existential curse; condemned to eternal youth while watching all loved ones age and die repeatedly.","opus":"A vampire's dirge over centuries of unwanted deathlessness, denied both daylight and the rest of the grave, forced to watch each love decay while begging pardon and yearning for his own end with every kill. It renders the undead as cursed rather than seductive. Type O Negative sink fully into gothic-metal grandeur, Peter Steele's baritone dwelling in exquisite autumnal despair.","sonnet":"Four centuries of undead grief — an immortal who never chose the condition watches every person he loves age and die, each loss taking another piece of his heart. He cannot die, cannot age, cannot feel the sun; he is suspended permanently in an October twilight, praying for a death that won't come, cursing the hunger that keeps him alive.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~the-dream-is-dead":{"haiku":"A lover faces the reality of a relationship's end on Valentine's Day, accepting isolation and loss while recognizing that promises made were never kept. The dream of lasting love has died and won't be resurrected.","opus":"Peter Steele's gothic self-pity dressed as romance, another Valentine's Day alone marking love's death with mordant theatricality; the roses counting off wasted years while he insists cruelly that the truth is worse than any wound.","sonnet":"A bleak Valentine's Day song about a decade-long love that has finally, irretrievably ended — the narrator marks the anniversary with gothic imagery of blood, wax tears, and roses, accepting that the relationship is over even while hating it, and concluding that the dream they shared is simply dead.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele stages heartbreak as a deliberately overwrought Gothic banquet — the dinner-for-one of chocolate hearts and a guttering candle is played straight enough to sting and lush enough to know it is ridiculous, which is exactly the gallows register Type O Negative lived in. The ten roses counted off as vanishing years turn the Valentine's ritual into a ledger of everything already lost, so the refrain's flat disbelief that it came to this reads less as fresh grief than as a man narrating his own long defeat. The song's real cruelty is the mock-heroic motto it twists: solidarity curdles into a promise that no one is coming to catch you when you drop, and the carnival Steele invites you to join is one where he is plainly a clown himself — the dream's death is not the romance ending but the last illusion collapsing that any of it could ever have gone another way.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"grief narrated as the last illusion that it could have gone otherwise","built":"the mock-heroic motto curdles into a promise no one is coming"},
"type-o-negative~the-glorious-liberation-of-the-people-s-technocratic-republic-of-vinnland-by-the-combined-forces-of-the-united-territories-of-europa":{"opus":"Type O Negative loaded their records with deadpan interludes and sardonic mock-grandeur, and this absurdly titled track is one such gag. Its content is only a German count from one to four, functioning as an intro or interlude with no narrative, the ludicrous name doing all the satirical work the two minutes of counting never attempt.","sonnet":"No meaningful lyrical content — the track consists only of a German count from one to four repeated, functioning as an intro or interlude with no narrative.","src":"sonnet","fable":"A colossal geopolitical title collapses into nothing but a deadpan German count-off to four, the whole gag being that this grand liberation amounts to a band cueing itself in.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance"],"means":"a colossal geopolitical title collapsing into a band cueing itself in","built":"the title-to-content ratio is the entire joke, the grandiosity answered by a count-off"},
"type-o-negative~the-profit-of-doom":{"haiku":"Prophecy warns of apocalypse arriving on a set date; divine judgment will cleanse the corrupted masses.","opus":"An apocalyptic prophecy dressed in numerology and end-times dread, the narrator styling himself both seer and profiteer of catastrophe. Peter Steele's pun turns doomsaying into a ministry of brimstone and belief, warning against the media's phony morality as a namesake asteroid bears down.","sonnet":"A mock-prophetic apocalyptic warning that draws on biblical numerology — pentagrams, six-sided stars, ten horns and seven heads — to predict the end of humanity in 2029; the speaker warns against media-manufactured morality and positions themselves as one of a chosen few who understand the truth of what is coming.","src":"haiku","fable":"Steele voices an apocalyptic street-preacher hawking the end of the world, but a one-letter homophone in the twinned refrain quietly reframes the whole performance as a racket, the sermon and the swindle riding the same breath. His doomsday is assembled from clashing borrowed omens — five-pointed and six-pointed stars set against one another, horns and seals, a media-decreed morality, a real asteroid named for chaos dragged in as proof — a pileup so overstuffed it exposes prophecy as pastiche stitched from whatever frightens. Then the closing movement drops the huckster's wink for something startlingly sincere, the soul catching fire toward a promised reward, and the sincerity lands so cleanly you cannot tell whether the mask has slipped off or clamped down tighter.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The borrowed omen is real — asteroid 99942 Apophis and its once-feared 2029 approach.","fnote2":"Released March 2007 on SPV, Rasputin on the cover — the only non-Roadrunner album, the last before Steele's death, and the title his own coinage for his return to the church: dead again Christian.","themes":["artifice & performance","faith & the occult"],"means":"the sermon and the swindle riding the same breath through one homophone","built":"the closing sincerity lands so cleanly the mask cannot be confirmed as off or tighter"},
"type-o-negative~these-three-things":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic religious imagery mingles condemnation of abortion, fascist politics, and prophecy of judgment and salvation.","opus":"A hellish sermon denouncing three things the speaker deems sins: abortion cast as the killing of infants, and, through apocalyptic scripture of Babylon and Armageddon, a nation declared into being in 1948, threatening damnation while dangling redemption through pardon.","sonnet":"A theologically dense monologue that moves through three interlinked condemnations — abortion as infanticide, the disputed legitimacy of the state of Israel, and the speaker's own identity as an adversarial or satanic force — woven together with apocalyptic imagery of Babylon, Armageddon, and redemption, and closing unexpectedly with a call to salvation through forgiveness.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song ventriloquizes a self-appointed avenger who welds three targets into a single verdict of damnation: the abortionist, whom he vows to usher personally into hell, then the modern Jewish state, pinned to its founding year and condemned for refusing conversion, and last a delusional self-cast as the doubled twin of the apostle Peter presiding over the end. The framing device carries the true weight, since the speaker keeps flagging that further indictments are still coming, so every grievance gets logged as a numbered entry, and the terror is exactly that a murderer's private creed can be tabulated as neatly as a to-do list. Against that mounting first-person sentence the close swings toward a mass refrain about congregating, absolution, and rescue attained, the last word hammered past sense, and taken plainly it scans as worship. Read as the mask it wears, that chorus is the identical certainty flipped outward, the throng's guaranteed deliverance unmasked as the exact reflection of one man's guaranteed sentence, the same absolute conviction that trusts it can name the guilty and balance the account.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Explicitly anti-abortion, tied by sourced accounts to Steele's Catholic guilt over a former girlfriend's abortion — part of the record's dead-again-Christian reckoning.","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult","anger & defiance"],"means":"a murderer's creed tabulated as neatly as a to-do list","built":"the numbered-entry framing turns certainty of sentence into an accounting ledger"},
"type-o-negative~todd-s-ship-gods-above-all-things":{"haiku":"Grief for a mentor manifest as silence, the lesson absorbed is that masculine strength demands the suppression of tears and vulnerability.","opus":"A shipyard father remembered through grease and coffee, and the harsh lesson he left, that a man hides his tears from the sun. Peter Steele grieves the giant who taught him not to cry, then keeps the promise even in mourning him, be a man above all things.","sonnet":"A elegy for a father figure — a large, masculine, shipyard man who taught the young speaker to suppress tears and perform stoic manhood, a lesson the speaker absorbed completely, now mourning the man and perhaps the cost of that lesson.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is Peter Steele's elegy for the shipyard-worker father who forged him into a man of granite, and the song's whole argument is that the lesson took too well. The refrain is inherited law — the father's command to be a man above all things — but Steele can only obey it by keeping his grief out of sunshine, hiding among shadows the way he was taught, so that the promise not to cry becomes the very wound it was meant to armor. In a career built on his own hulking baritone menace, this is the rare moment where Steele diagnoses that masculinity as something bequeathed and load-bearing and quietly crushing: the boy who once scraped his knee is now the giant, still swallowing the storm the Tarot predicted rather than let anyone watch it rain.","fv":2,"fnote":"Named for the Brooklyn Todd Shipyards where Steele’s father worked; the refrain’s command is the father’s own advice.","themes":["family & blood","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"inherited stoicism that becomes the wound it was meant to armor","built":"the father's command to be a man is obeyed by hiding the grief it caused"},
"type-o-negative~too-late-frozen":{"haiku":"Apologies arrive too late as wounds refuse to heal; the speaker stands frozen by emotional abandonment, trapped in a cryogenic state of grief.","opus":"A caustic dismissal of a lover's belated apology, insisting time won't mend the damage and that love, contrary to the cliche, saves nothing while he suffers for their sake. The second half recasts the heartbreak in Norse-flavored imagery, the narrator stranded in a numb, frozen limbo and begging mythic powers to shield him from the cold their betrayal left behind.","sonnet":"About an apology arriving after all emotional possibility is gone — the narrator is frozen past the point of forgiveness, the relationship's warmth replaced by a glacial numbness that no belated sorry can thaw, the love declared dead and the wound permanent.","src":"haiku","fable":"The two halves are two temperatures of the same abandonment: the Too Late section spits its refusal in fast, snarling monosyllables, a lover slamming the door on apology after being wounded past healing. The Frozen movement drops into slow doom and translates that same finality into a cosmology of cold — the speaker recast as a numbed statue in a lightless place, calling out to Norse winter titans who never answer. Movement is the whole point: rage burns down into ice, and the shift is literalized when he locates the source of the chill not in any northern winter but in the other person, a private dimension of cryonic nowhere, so that being frozen is what heartbreak becomes once the screaming stops. The mask of the intro's swaggering insult is answered by what surfaces underneath — the endlessly repeated word frozen, a man too far gone to thaw, begging mythic protectors precisely because no human one is coming.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"rage burning down into ice, each temperature a different register of the same abandonment","built":"the shift in tempo literalises the emotional move: the screaming stops and only frozen remains"},
"type-o-negative~tripping-a-blind-man":{"haiku":"A dark tale of cruelty toward a defenseless person; the narrator imagines and eventually causes the death of a blind man and his guide dog. The song condemns playing judge and finding pleasure in others' suffering.","opus":"A denunciation of those who savor other people's misery, built around the cruel image of a sightless victim being made to fall. The speaker swears to pass judgment on the self-appointed judges, and a brutal scene shows a blind man's guide dog slain and its heartbroken owner dying of the loss.","sonnet":"The song condemns those who take pleasure in another's suffering or abuse their power as moral arbiters, framing the narrator as someone sent to judge the judges — the extended closing narrative depicting a man whose cruelty leads to the death of a blind man's guide dog, and then the old man himself.","src":"haiku","fable":"Peter Steele frames a persecutor from behind sightless eyes, the proverbs curdling as he goes: turn-the-other-cheek becomes an invitation to have your face torn off, and the blind speaker who claims to be healed keeps stumbling anyway, so the miracle reads as bitter self-mockery rather than grace. The song's cruelty is deliberately cheap and one-sided—an idle tormentor amused by another's suffering—until the speaker flips from victim to appointed judge of judges, promising to answer a would-be God by playing the devil. Its single unbearable wish — night-vision, nothing grander — lands as the crux: he asks not for restored sight but only to be spared the special humiliation of being tripped precisely because he cannot see it coming. Then the spoken bridge abandons the taunting rhythm entirely for a flat, tabloid-style incident report of a guide dog crushed under a truck and its owner dying of grief, the change to deadpan reportage doing the emotional work the rhymes withheld. The closing refrain rots one last time, patience-is-divine sliding into a sneer at swallowed lies and nice peace — resignation delivered as contempt, the mask of forbearance worn over disgust.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","faith & the occult"],"means":"the wish for night-vision, nothing grander, as the crux of all the posturing","built":"the spoken bridge's deadpan tabloid reportage does the emotional work the taunting rhymes withheld"},
"type-o-negative~unsuccessfully-coping-with-the-natural-beauty-of-infidelity":{"haiku":"A betrayed person confronts their partner's infidelity with graphic detail and raw anger, unable to forgive repeated betrayal and the death of trust.","opus":"A furious, misogynist tirade from a man tormented by his partner's infidelity, cataloguing her betrayals in graphic sexual detail and hurling slurs; beneath the rage is the confession that her cheating makes him hate himself, the humor and vulgarity masking genuine wounded jealousy.","sonnet":"A man who knows his girlfriend is cheating on him — in graphic, furious detail — oscillates between rageful accusation and self-pitying solitary drinking, hating her and hating himself in equal measure.","src":"haiku"},
"type-o-negative~we-hate-everyone":{"haiku":"Universal disdain and refusal of ideology. Left and right are both condemned; the song celebrates indiscriminate hatred of all belief systems and public judgment.","opus":"A sardonic rejection of all political labels and accusations, from fascist to communist to racist, insisting the singer doesn't care what anyone thinks. It declares an all-encompassing hatred for everyone and notes that outrage only boosts their fame and record sales.","sonnet":"The song gleefully refuses every political label—denounced as fascist by the left and communist by the right—and turns that universal condemnation into a proud manifesto of total, equal-opportunity misanthropy. Protests and accusations are welcomed as free publicity.","src":"haiku","fable":"The chorus of universal hate works as a decoy: the verses stage the band being called fascist by the left and communist by the right, sexist and racist from every angle. That contradiction means the announced hatred is really a refusal to be sorted by any accuser, since the labels flung at them cancel each other out. What reads as nihilism is closer to a shrug at bad-faith outrage, the deadpan noting that scandal only enriches the accused, so every attack becomes free promotion and hating everyone becomes armor. The one mirror-turn line, that filth comes in every shade and now includes you, drops the bravado to implicate the accuser while never settling whether the misanthropy is sincere or the ultimate non-answer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Written with Kill All the White People as the deliberate tongue-in-cheek last word on the racism accusations that trailed Steele from Carnivore and Slow, Deep and Hard.","themes":["anger & defiance","politics & society"],"means":"universal hatred as armor against being sorted by any accuser","built":"the left's fascist and the right's communist cancel each other out, exposing misanthropy as a non-answer"},
"type-o-negative~we-were-electrocute":{"haiku":"Nostalgia for past glory and bitter regret over wasted youth on hollow love.","opus":"A bitter, nostalgic song mourning a couple who were once the electric, envied golden pair, now soured a decade later into regret over wasted youth and a lover revealed as fake, 'as real as your tits.'","sonnet":"A retrospective on a once-electric relationship that has curdled into bitter hindsight: the pair were dazzling together, strangers knew their names, but years later the shine has completely gone and the speaker sees the other person — and their own youthful investment — as fundamentally fake.","src":"haiku","fable":"Steele narrates his own obsolescence, and the title's snapped-off grammar (electrocute, never electrocuted) does the thesis: a couple stranded mid-jolt, still wired into a fame and a love that both flatlined a decade back. The song runs on things once electric and now merely cadaverous — the dated suits, the dye-job crowning a fool's scalp, her eyes gone to the grey grit of an off-season Coney Island beach. Beneath the gallows swagger it stays a breakup elegy that won't mourn cleanly: he confesses he misses her mouth in the same breath he rules the rest of her a cosmetic forgery, the sneer slammed down to keep the grief from touching bottom. What outlasts the shock is only the memory of being an inseparable pair, and the past tense is the whole wound — the current's cut, the pose has set, and all that is left to worship is the fossil of the buzz.","fv":2,"themes":["heartbreak & loss","nostalgia & memory","artifice & performance"],"means":"a sneer slammed down to keep the grief from touching bottom","built":"the truncated past-tense title enacts the couple stranded mid-jolt"},
"type-o-negative~white-slavery":{"haiku":"Drug addiction and its cycle of craving, loss of self, and nightmarish repetition trap the user. The song depicts addiction as a nightmare of erosion and dependency.","opus":"Peter Steele's bleak first-person spiral into cocaine addiction, the summer snow that infects and hollows him until he prays for death. The soda-slogan twist skewers how thoroughly the poison gets marketed and consumed.","sonnet":"The song is about cocaine addiction: a craving that infects its user, consumes money, destroys the self, and ultimately drives a wish for death. The bridge repurposes Pepsi and Coca-Cola advertising slogans to mock the way addiction is sold and normalised.","src":"haiku","fable":"Steele turns the title's ugly double meaning inward: the enslavement is his own, and the white master is the powder he keeps phoning for. The song walks the arc of a binge — the restless first call, the frozen crystal that burns instead of chilling, and the dawn that finds him decomposing and begging to stop — so the refrain about losing himself reads as literal dissolution rather than metaphor. The gallows-humour twist is that he indicts the whole consumer machine in the same breath, splicing soft-drink ad slogans into the come-down until getting wasted and being sold to sound like one transaction, money bleeding out as fast as the sensation. It is Steele at his blackest: the joke and the confession share a single baritone, and neither offers escape — only the unanswered question of when it ends.","fv":2,"fnote":"Steele confirmed the subject in 1999: his own relapsing cocaine addiction, which he said he quit after one particularly bad night — making this the band’s first plainly anti-drug lyric after years of playing the opposite.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","money & the street"],"means":"the consumer transaction and the binge spliced into one account","built":"soft-drink slogans intercut with the come-down until both sound identical"},
"type-o-negative~who-will-save-the-sane":{"haiku":"Sanity cannot be rescued from insanity and madness; the 'mad' exist beyond judgment while the 'sane' are left to fear their incomprehensibility.","opus":"A blackly funny complaint from a man labeled mad and gawked at by onlookers offering hollow pity. The absurd demand that he rattle off a chemistry constant to earn his freedom mocks psychiatric confinement, posing the ironic question of who protects the reasonable in a world where reason itself is grounds for being locked up.","sonnet":"A darkly comic piece about a man clearly headed for the psychiatric ward — reciting pi and Avogadro's number to prove his sanity while the world around him performs normalcy with fake sympathy and hollow pity.","src":"haiku","fable":"Steele inverts the whole apparatus of diagnosis: it is the ostensibly sane who need rescuing, cornered by onlookers who dab at a dry eye and file the narrator under harmless curiosity before drifting on. The verses are built like a chemistry set gone rancid — a barrage of clinical and pseudo-scientific compounds that mimics the way a paranoid mind fuses jargon into private cosmology, so that a Nietzsche invocation and a recited string of decimal digits carry the same authority as a lab result. That last conceit is the barb: admission to the ward is granted only to whoever can perform an arbitrary feat of memorized numeracy on command, which turns institutional mercy into a party trick and quietly asks who the deranged party really is. Underneath the gallows wit sits the record's steady despair, the baritone treating self-destruction as the calm price of being unable to change, and the shift from present to past tense in the final onlooker's verdict lands the joke where it stings — the odd one has already been carted off, remembered only as an anecdote.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the sane subjected to diagnosis while the mad diagnose them","built":"a memorised decimal string plays as the ward's admission requirement"},
"type-o-negative~wolf-moon-including-zoanthropic-paranoia":{"haiku":"Sexual submission and desire framed through lunar werewolf mythology and menstrual cycles.","opus":"Peter Steele reframes a partner's menstruation as a monthly werewolf rite, treating her cycle as something to consume and revere rather than fear. The gothic metal band fuses eroticism with dread, casting the lover under the full moon's pull and turning a bodily process into ritual worship laced with silver-bullet and lunar mythology.","sonnet":"A gothic erotic meditation on the menstrual cycle fused with werewolf mythology: the speaker reveres his partners monthly transformation as something sacred and blood-cursed, meeting her under the full moon with devotion rather than fear, framing intimacy as both benediction and surrender to lunar compulsion.","src":"haiku","web":"Peter Steele merges lycanthropy with erotic obsession — becoming a wolf under the January full moon, consumed by desire for a menstruating woman, Gothic-horror metaphor ('iron in the air') framing the female body as sacred and maddening at once.","fable":"The whole track runs on one unspoken pun: the werewolf's full-moon change is really a woman's monthly bleeding. That fusion recasts the metallic tang of blood as the true transformation the myth was hiding. Steele plays the vampire suitor courting that recurring curse, his gothic smirk rewriting the hunter's weapon and the taste of blood into a private sacrament. The reading turns on who kneels: he pleads to be enchanted, supplicant rather than beast. So the horror dressing collapses into seduction, worshipping her body as the sacred thing he wants to adore.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult"],"means":"the werewolf myth unworn to reveal the menstrual cycle it was always encoding","built":"the horror dressing collapses when the speaker kneels as supplicant rather than standing as beast, rewriting the hunter's weapon into a private sacrament"},
"type-o-negative~world-coming-down":{"haiku":"A self-aware confession of self-destruction and depression. The speaker recognizes they are collapsing their own world through self-hatred despite appearing fortunate, and cannot escape their role in the demolition.","opus":"A confession of self-hatred and guilt, in which the narrator recognizes that he alone has brought his world crashing down and refuses to feign the joy others expect of him, choosing to burn out rather than fade weakly.","sonnet":"The narrator acknowledges that he alone is responsible for the collapse of his own life — filled with self-hatred despite his fortune, unable to fake joy, he accepts that his world is coming down through his own actions and refuses to pretend otherwise.","src":"haiku","fable":"A man watches his own life collapse and refuses the comfort of blaming anyone else, because the fault stares back at him each time he faces a mirror. Steele works his signature move here, wrapping genuine self-loathing in the deadpan grandeur of his baritone, so a title-track apocalypse plays as one insomniac's private reckoning rather than any literal end of the world. The bravado he reaches for near the end, the predator's swagger and the wish to flame out fast rather than dim to nothing, reads as the last mask over a plainer confession: he knows exactly who tore the whole thing down.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"private self-reckoning wearing the costume of an apocalypse title","built":"predator's swagger arrives as the last mask before the plain confession"},
"type-o-negative~xero-tolerance":{"haiku":"Violent obsession and rage transforming the speaker into an unrecognizable monster of hatred; murderous intent born from betrayal.","opus":"Jealousy curdling into a homicidal fixation, the narrator consumed by hatred and picturing himself grinding a weapon and riding the subway to a Brooklyn beach to murder a lover found with another man, stoking himself on liquor and drugs. Type O Negative, the Brooklyn gothic-metal band led by Peter Steele, cut their doom with pitch-black comedy, this early track dripping with self-aware, self-loathing menace.","sonnet":"Jealous rage at Brighton Beach building into a graphic murder fantasy — the speaker tracks a lover to the sand with a sharpened pickaxe, chemically fortified, narrating the killing with grotesque theatrical relish. The fury isn't righteous; it's possessive and chemically unhinged, the song walking the line between confession and comedy while never quite landing on either.","src":"haiku"},
"u-d-o~black-and-white":{"haiku":"Polarized extremes of fire and ice, angel and devil, sacred and profane are played against each other in a meditation on irreconcilable opposites.","opus":"A femme-fatale warning painted in stark opposites, angel and devil, fire and ice, a seductive figure who gives light yet promises to break and damn you, the spider inviting the fly to its doom. U.D.O., the German heavy-metal band led by former Accept vocalist Udo Dirkschneider, deliver this cautionary tale of dangerous desire with old-school riffing and a gravel-throated snarl, morality reduced to black and white.","sonnet":"A seduction cast as a moral trap: angel and devil, heaven and hell, with the spider already at the web's center. The attraction is real and dangerous, desire weaponized, and whoever gets close enough loses their soul to someone who frames it all as a game.","src":"haiku"},
"u-d-o~dominator":{"haiku":"A gaming warrior dominates through power and violence, driven by sensational thrills. They're unstoppable and uncorrosive in their pursuit of victory.","opus":"An arcade-fantasy of a killing machine and the crowd that roars for the thrill, gaming and bloodlust blurred into one spectacle. Udo Dirkschneider's heavy metal built as a fist-pumping ode to the dominator.","sonnet":"A fragmented action-game scenario — a lone warrior in a black arena navigating laser fire, razor wire, and minefields to win the contest. The language is all military/gaming hybrid, the emotional content is zero; it's pure aggressive competitive spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"u-d-o~speed-demon":{"haiku":"A reckless driver in a high-powered machine pursues maximum speed and thrill, treating danger and disaster as his driving identity.","opus":"The former Accept frontman howls a hymn to reckless velocity: an engine near meltdown, fuel practically running in the driver's blood, a racer past any restraint hurtling toward a wreck he welcomes. It is elemental machine-and-danger heavy metal, treating the finish line as a dare and destruction as the whole thrill.","sonnet":"A hymn to the racetrack obsessive — a man for whom speed is identity and destruction is destiny, his body fused with machine, living past the red zone where control ends and catastrophe begins.","src":"haiku","fable":"A car and its driver are welded into a single reckless organism — an engine whose fuel runs in place of blood, a man who is only real sealed inside his corroding steel shell — so that speed stops being something he does and becomes what he is. Udo Dirkschneider, carrying the blunt heavy-metal grammar he made his name with in Accept, treats the death-race not as tragedy but as the only creed worth having. The song sides fully with the demon, framing the crash as glory and the cautious man as the coward, with nothing underneath the mask to complicate the worship.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"speed as identity rather than act, the crash reframed as the only creed","built":"man and machine fused into one organism with no inner life behind the mask"},
"u2~beautiful-day":{"haiku":"Renewal emerges from hardship and disorientation; connection reveals that wonder persists beneath struggle.","opus":"Finding grace in the moment of having lost everything, the beauty arriving precisely because you're stripped of possessions and destination, a hymn to what you feel rather than what you own.","sonnet":"A person stripped of material stability — no destination, stuck in the mud — is urged to recognize abundance in what remains, looking outward to the world's staggering beauty as a counterweight to personal loss. The song frames gratitude not as denial of hardship but as a defiant act of perception.","src":"haiku"},
"u2~pride-in-the-name-of-love-remastered-2009":{"haiku":"Martyrdom sanctifies resistance; the speaker honors those assassinated for justice, their sacrifice transcending death.","opus":"A tribute to Martin Luther King, the martyr shot in Memphis whose life could be taken but not his dignity, holding sacrificial nonviolent love up as the force that outlasts assassination.","sonnet":"A hymn to those who gave their lives in the name of love — centrally Martin Luther King Jr., whose assassination on April 4th in Memphis is the song's emotional anchor — insisting that while they could take his life, they could not take what he stood for. The arc moves from betrayal and resistance to the endurance of pride.","src":"haiku"},
"ultra-violence~good-enough":{"haiku":"Relentless self-improvement paradoxically traps the speaker—they fight to stay standing yet suspect their hard-won gains will never satisfy an unmoved observer.","opus":"Defiant commitment to a chosen path past the age one was supposed to quit, promising to keep getting up after every fall even as the dream starts to feel like a prison, satisfied because it's enough for him.","sonnet":"A relentless commitment to a chosen path — getting up after every fall, never returning to the bottom — runs up against the question of whether that resolve satisfies anyone else; the dream is both a driving force and 'a prison,' but the speaker claims it as enough for themselves.","src":"haiku"},
"ultra-violence~metamorphosis":{"haiku":"Violent transformation and nihilism; shedding yourself and bowing to a new destructive order.","opus":"Thrash nihilism as transformation, self-harm and violence embraced as identity, a new breed crowning itself and demanding the old order kneel, aggression offered as the only available cure.","sonnet":"About a violent personal transformation — the narrator is cutting themselves, demanding submission, and positioning self-harm and dominance as the birth of a new self. The metamorphosis is into something harder and more ruthless, powered by contempt for weakness.","src":"haiku"},
"ultra-violence~no-one-s-listening":{"haiku":"Invisible pain masked by performance, isolation growing deeper even as you reach out.","opus":"Depression hidden behind a faked smile, someone drowning quietly and too proud to ask for help, terrified they'll only be heard once they're gone, the plea for a cure going unanswered.","sonnet":"About hiding depression behind a convincing smile while desperately needing help — the narrator fakes wellness, can't break the cycle, and fears that no one will listen until they're already gone; the plea is direct and frightened, asking if anyone can find a cure before they lose their grip entirely.","src":"haiku"},
"ulver~nemoralia":{"haiku":"A song conflates Christian martyrdom under Nero with contemporary hunting of public figures, treating death as spectacle and illumination.","opus":"Norwegian shapeshifters Ulver splice Nero's burning of Christians as living torches into an ancient Roman moon festival, pinning the atrocity to a mid-July night. The song then jumps across the centuries to Princess Diana, killed and, like the old lunar deity, crowned in blossoms, fusing martyrdom, spectacle, and rite into one meditation on death lit up for an audience.","sonnet":"Two historical spectacles of bodies made into light — Nero burning Christians as torches during the Nemoralia festival and the death of Diana, Princess of Wales — are juxtaposed as recurring rituals of sacrificial illumination. The ancient goddess of the moon and the modern icon collapse into the same image of a woman consumed by the gaze that crowns her.","src":"haiku"},
"uncanny-x-men~party":{"haiku":"Party's ending leaves the uncertain stranded between departure and solitude; responsibility and indulgence collide as night deepens.","opus":"The restless small hours of a party animal who knows he ought to be in bed but can't call it a night, insisting his mates are forever while women drift in and out and there's more to him than the surface suggests.","sonnet":"The post-party limbo — too early for home, too late to be alone, dancing on tables instead of being in bed — is celebrated as proof that friends last forever even as nights have to end; the speaker owns the fact they hate missing out and chooses chaos over comfort.","src":"haiku"},
"uncle-acid-the-deadbeats~poison-apple":{"haiku":"Toxic love invades every system and becomes inescapable, the beloved merges with the lover's consciousness in symbiotic destruction.","opus":"An omnipresent, menacing presence that claims to be everything from the air to the worm in your grave, a seductive evil that is really the listener's own dark reflection.","sonnet":"A seductive, possessive voice claims to be everything simultaneously — air, worm, poison apple, spider in the brain, switchblade if you cross the line — constructing itself as the inescapable toxic force in someone's life, made entirely by what that person created in it.","src":"haiku"},
"undead-corporation~blaze":{"haiku":"Existential breakthrough demands throwing off invisible constraints; freedom requires rejecting manufactured identity to pursue authentic desire without apology.","opus":"A frantic anthem of breaking free from stifling paralysis, demanding a path out of the sludge of pretending and fear. The Japanese band frames liberation as a refusal to sink or fake it, seizing freedom before life burns out young.","sonnet":"An assertion of self-directed freedom against stagnation — the speaker demands to smoke freedom, drink options, stop pretending and sinking in the sludge, and blaze a new path that no one else defines. The refrain of \"no pain no gain\" and \"don't die young\" frame it as a survival ethic rather than mere bravado.","src":"haiku"},
"undead-corporation~face-the-fate":{"haiku":"The song confronts collective blame and inevitable fate, with imagery of suicide and sacrifice, asking listeners to accept shared responsibility rather than judge.","opus":"Undead Corporation, a Japanese metal act born from Touhou fan culture, deliver an English-language burst of frantic, high-speed intensity about staring down death and shared guilt. Clutching a fraying hold with no rescuer left, the narrator rejects any verdict since everyone is equally culpable, resolving to meet destiny head-on and torch away cowardice as the surrounding world collapses.","sonnet":"Survival in a blurred, violent cityscape where hope and madness are indistinguishable — the speaker clings to a frayed rope and insists on getting what they want while calling on everyone to stop pretending they are not equally implicated. The logic is communal and brutal: one person's grief is another's comfort, and the only direction available is forward into whatever comes.","src":"haiku"},
"undead-corporation~sirens":{"haiku":"Internal alarms warn fate approaches; insanity whispers across quiet towns while butterfly effects reverse causality and time collapses.","opus":"Inner alarms wailing as a life spins out of control, a butterfly-effect sense that every choice costs something irreversible, the singer caught in a vortex and longing to fade away while unable to stop.","sonnet":"Sirens wail inside the speaker's head while the town stays calm around them; a butterfly-wing chaos theory governs a life spinning out of control where every choice might be the last touch or the last word, and the only escape — fading away — keeps getting deferred as the vortex closes in.","src":"haiku"},
"underlet~bury-the-icon":{"haiku":"Society constructs and worships idols born from hatred, yet nobody can save themselves from the cycles of obsession they've made.","opus":"Worship is exposed as a machine for manufacturing hatred: the crowd builds an idol to relieve its own tensions, then discovers no icon can save them from themselves. Tearing it down becomes the only honest way out.","sonnet":"A critique of idol worship and the psychological damage it causes — people build icons to fill their need to believe in something, and those icons become engines of obsession and hate, ultimately trapping rather than saving their followers.","src":"haiku"},
"underoath~a-moment-suspended-in-time":{"haiku":"Confinement and helpless captivity trap consciousness in nightmare while waking blurs with dreaming until collapse arrives.","opus":"The final moments of someone trapped and doomed, hiding under a desk, insisting it must be a dream even as they sense the whole place is about to blow. The horror is the clarity that comes when there's no living left to do, begging not to be woken.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped in a school shooting or similar sudden crisis — hiding under a desk, praying it's a dream, unable to get up — oscillating between denial and the terrible clarity that comes when no time is left, surrendering to the moment suspended in time.","src":"haiku"},
"underoath~all-the-love-is-gone":{"haiku":"Substance and emptiness spiral into void as numbness replaces connection, leaving only consumption and loss. The speaker descends through escalating intoxication toward complete dissolution.","opus":"A brutal first-person account of drinking to fill an emptiness that only deepens, prayers useless and last call always coming again. Underoath's return leans into the self-deception of the addict, insisting the drink isn't the abuser while it clearly is.","sonnet":"A night of spiralling drinking after love has emptied out: three vodkas deep, thinking they are God, sinking into last call alone — there is a hole in the heart filled with nothing, the bartender is all that is left, and pulling the trigger is just a metaphor for another round.","src":"haiku"},
"underoath~damn-excuses":{"haiku":"Cyclical manipulation and refusal to break patterns perpetuate abuse; the speaker exposes how excuses mask exploitation and calls for the other person to walk away.","opus":"Trapped in a toxic cycle with a manipulator who hides behind fake conviction and endless excuses, the speaker finally exposes the lie. Lost inside those excuses, he cries out asking whether anyone can hear him.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone — possibly a partner or God — who has given nothing wanted but keeps the speaker locked in the cycle: \"fuck your revelation and fuck your weak conviction\"; the speaker is lost inside excuses, cycling between calling for an exit and admitting he cannot hear anyone anymore.","src":"haiku"},
"underoath~in-regards-to-myself":{"opus":"An urgent plea to a person drowning in shame and self-destruction to wake up, come clean, and pull themselves together before time runs out; a promise that redemption is still reachable and that hands will pull them out if they reach for it.","sonnet":"A confrontation with personal paralysis and shame — the narrator demands that they stop hiding from the truth about themselves, get off their back, and reach outward toward others rather than collapsing inward, because time is running out and something is still worth reaching for.","src":"sonnet"},
"underoath~lifeline-drowning":{"haiku":"Structural collapse and emotional dissolution; drifting into emptiness when closest to connection.","opus":"A metalcore panic attack rendered as literal drowning. Lying on the floor at two in the morning, the narrator sinks under a person who becomes an ocean pulling him under, begging for a lifeline while circling the drain. It captures the airless helplessness of a mind in crisis, the pressure arriving in waves he cannot outlast.","sonnet":"Ceiling caving in at quarter after two, lying on the floor as the relationship or the other person becomes the ocean pressing down. The speaker is drowning in feelings that are simultaneously too much and too real — every word said replayed, treading water always, the lifeline thrown and pulled and never quite caught.","src":"haiku"},
"underoath~loneliness":{"haiku":"Profound isolation and damage from being alone, facing death privately and becoming a ghost haunted by one's own solitude.","opus":"A battered narrator claims bitter expertise in isolation, branding himself the broken one and a specter who confronted his own end unaccompanied, ordering others not to witness his departure. Underoath push it into post-hardcore catharsis, the rejection of pity and the spectral self-image marking the raw, faith-haunted introspection that distinguished them from their peers.","sonnet":"A damaged survivor confronts those who claim to understand his isolation without having faced what he has. The ghost metaphor is precise — not invisible but present and unacknowledged, shaped by a solitary encounter with death that no observer witnessed, making their pity both unwanted and fundamentally beside the point.","src":"haiku"},
"underoath~loss":{"haiku":"Recovery fragments under the weight of accumulated shame; sobriety and prayer offer no erasure, leaving only the permanent loss of what was sacrificed.","opus":"Addiction and codependency staged as a stint in a sterile, tiled hell, praying to be taught how to get loaded, then bound in a straitjacket. No amount of prayer or pleading buries the shame, leaving only ruin and the terror that it was all for nothing.","sonnet":"About the aftermath of a codependent, destructive relationship and the failed attempt to recover through institutionalization — the narrator describes making themselves a victim, calling the other person's drug their medicine, and ending up in a psychiatric facility that feels like a white-tiled straitjacket rather than healing. The crushing conclusion is that no matter how much you pray or beg, the shame and the loss stay with you; you cannot bury what happened.","src":"haiku"},
"underoath~pneumonia":{"haiku":"Spiritual suffocation and cyclical self-harm trap the speaker in a drowning loop; they climb mountains of pain without relief, gasping against a weight they cannot shake.","opus":"A self-described nightmare drowning in his own darkness, scaling a person like a punishing peak while every prayer rings hollow. It's about the compulsion to know what lies beyond even as it kills him and breaks the one who loves him.","sonnet":"Crushed 3,000 feet below and drowning, the speaker acknowledges being a nightmare — unable to sleep, prayers hollow — but refuses the mountain of another person's expectations, choosing instead to climb until skin and bones, then dissolve into a \"brightest black\" that releases all fear.","src":"haiku"},
"underscores~cops-and-robbers":{"haiku":"Long con steals quietly through systems, where the speaker extracts wealth from the dead and vulnerable. Moral inversion makes the thief sympathetic against the system they betray.","opus":"A story-song narrated by a fraud-committing bank teller who launders and steals customers' money to feed a drug habit, running a long con and fleeing town, coolly insisting she's not the bad guy.","sonnet":"A bank teller confesses to long-con fraud — false checks, counterfeits, resurrected dead identities, ATM hits — having started because they couldn't pay for 'the crystal'; hiding in a Super 8, APB out, suitcase of stolen cash in the crawlspace, they frame the whole thing as simply doing what anyone would do if they got on the inside.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~alkaline-throat":{"haiku":"Longing to escape mundane existence through another person, seeking transcendence and meaning through intense connection and passion.","opus":"A heady, ruinous attraction to an alluring figure the narrator simultaneously desires and mistrusts, drawn helplessly toward whatever excites him. The song's imagery shifts from seductive sweetness to scorching agony, closing in a self-disgust that returns as mocking laughter.","sonnet":"Two people chase an adrenaline-fuelled, intoxicating connection — a shared drive toward intensity and excess — until the heat between them curdles into loathing and the narrator's contempt echoes back at the other's laughter.","src":"haiku","fable":"A courtship with one's own compulsion, staged as flattery toward a seductive other the speaker insists he already recognizes. The song climbs by escalation the way the band's music does, each stanza reaching past the one before for a bigger high, until the goal stops being the room's ceiling and becomes the sky itself. Then the physics reverses: what was meant to elevate instead sets both parties alight, tissue running like heated wax, and the final turn is the cruelest, because the speaker's disgust ricochets off his partner's amusement, so the thrill they shared reveals itself as laughter at his expense. The lopsided, top-heavy build enacts the addiction it describes, mounting toward a rush that caves into mutual ruin rather than the release it promised.","fv":2,"themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"the promised rush caves into ruin and laughter at the speaker's expense","built":"the top-heavy escalating build enacts the addiction it describes"},
"uneven-structure~awaken":{"haiku":"A consciousness awakens from the womb into a new, complex world full of perception and infinite possibility.","opus":"A visceral rendering of being born, told from the unborn child's vantage: a trembling, melting inner world swells into a dazzling outer one, the ease of the womb ends, and the narrator is forced through a violent passage into an immense new domain, propelled by hunger for the unknown until his eyes finally open on it.","sonnet":"A sensory unfolding of birth itself — the warmth of the womb giving way to overwhelming light and perception, the first opening of eyes onto a vast and bewildering world.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~awe":{"haiku":"Overwhelming forces sweep through a corrupt system with destructive intent, and the narrator harnesses chaos to dismantle oppression. By channeling awe and revulsion, they return violence to those who built the harmful order.","opus":"Djent cast as cosmic revenge, a narrator seizing control of a corrupt order and unleashing annihilation from the top of an obelisk; the eroded illusion of wellness gives way to overwhelming, terrible power meant to inspire awe.","sonnet":"A controlling force takes over a corrupt system, tearing down its illusions and imposing order by casting back the suffering it inflicted, culminating in a state of awe at the transformation.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~brazen-tongue": {"haiku":"A person feels their identity is being eroded by aggressive criticism and manipulation from others who disguise control as guidance, leaving the speaker hollow and confused.","opus":"A person feels their identity and thoughts stripped away by the overwhelming, brazen speech of others, likening their manipulation to a hollow doctrine or cult recruitment they finally recognize and resist.","sonnet":"A person describes losing their sense of identity under the assault of loud, manipulative rhetoric, recognising they are being proselytised and exploited by people who use brazen speech to hollow them out and reshape them.","src":"haiku","fable":"A song about ideological capture, where the self is imagined as a machine being unbolted from within — the rivets that pinned identity in place backing loose, the inner ramparts giving way section by section — while the aggressor's whole power is mouth: shouts, incantations, the brazen tongues of the title that beat a soul soft until it is only a blueprint to be filled. The song's arc does the work: erosion first, then an unnerving stretch where the speaker develops compassion for the very doctrine hollowing him out, the classic mark of a convert mid-capture. What breaks the spell is naming — interrupting the drift to call the thing proselytism, refusing the high the manipulator was dispensing. The last movement turns the whole song outward: no longer pleading for his own self but warning a child to run, a survivor's voice recognizing the trap early enough to spare someone else. The closing question about whether a jaw made to dance could still let its speaker slip the leash leaves it pointed and unresolved, as if the rhetoric is still working on someone in the room.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","identity & becoming","faith & the occult"],"means":"survivor warns a child after escaping mid-conversion","built":"compassion developing for the hollowing doctrine marks the convert"},
"uneven-structure~buds":{"haiku":"A person intellectualizes nature and knowledge, building rational frameworks for understanding reality, but discovers that increasing complexity and ambition corrupt this pursuit, leading to failure despite their will to transcend.","opus":"A cerebral trip through consciousness expanding on itself, the mind ordering its surroundings and dreaming up a rigged game where it controls every piece. Its towering ambition to reach absolute perfection buckles into the looming sense of failure, even as the narrator declares his rule beginning. Uneven Structure, French djent architects, cloak this philosophical vertigo in dense, atmospheric progressive metal.","sonnet":"Intellectual ambition described as a burst of growth — the mind breaks free into cognition, escalates toward something vast and structurally dizzying, and then loses its footing. The tower of Babel is the apt image: boundless upward reach that ends not in arrival but in the recognition that the balance was fragile all along and the fall comes precisely when the perfection seems most achievable.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~creator":{"haiku":"An artist confronts a blank canvas and empty landscape, describing the internal conflict between doubt and ambition as ideas clash and coalesce into creation. The work emerges through struggle, ultimately bringing forth a new world that transcends the creator's initial dreams.","opus":"A prog-metal parable of artistic creation. Facing an empty sheet and a barren, doubt-choked landscape, the maker watches life emerge as pigment spreads into a fresh landscape and language settles into order. Finishing the piece brings quiet satisfaction, and the completed volume is shut for good.","sonnet":"A creator confronts a blank page and the paralysis of not knowing where to begin, then pushes through doubt and ego until the work finally takes shape and feels complete.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~crystal-teeth": {"haiku":"After exhaustion and collapse, the narrator navigates a fractured internal landscape of sharp, reflective surfaces that reveal multiplied selves. Pride and weakness conspire to prevent any real movement or growth.","sonnet":"Exhausted and caved in after a long ordeal, a person navigates a reflective maze of fractured selves — crystal teeth that are both beautiful and cutting. The image demands action over paralysis, a reckoning with who one has become.","src":"haiku","fable":"A collapse becomes a gestation: the singer surrenders once the running has drained him hollow, sliding down into something amniotic — a warm, moldy womb-space where cries get hammered into shape and revelation is born. From there he remembers being a seed, and the crystal teeth he has to thread his passage through are the brittle, million-faceted mirror of the self he is being reborn into, each shard reflecting and judging a different version of him. The song then hardens its focus and turns the accusation outward, indicting a paralysis — too proud to care, too weak to move — and closing not on the private myth of rebirth but on a flat imperative to weigh the journey and the direction of one's actions. It is Uneven Structure trading their earlier ambient sprawl for something that wants to arrive at a conclusion: interiority pushed until it becomes an ethics.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"private myth of rebirth pressed until it becomes an ethics","built":"the reborn self must thread its own shattered reflection to emerge"},
"uneven-structure~everyman":{"haiku":"The speaker describes being used and destroyed by another person, then announces he will reverse their fates and watch them burn as he burns himself. The song is about ending a cycle of exploitation through mutual destruction.","opus":"A prog-metal awakening from a lifetime of self-abasement and giving everything to someone who only devoured it. The catharsis is the vow to stop being a lifeless instrument and turn the flame of desire back on the liar who exploited it.","sonnet":"A reckoning with long-suffered submission — the narrator realizes he's spent his life bent to someone who took everything (his heart, his vessel, his desires) and destroyed it. The awakening is violent: now that his fantasies aren't entirely dead, he turns the fire around and promises to watch the person who consumed him burn instead.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~frost":{"haiku":"Consciousness becomes frozen and numb, controlled like a puppet as stimulation breeds illusion; emptiness magnifies every perception in a void landscape.","opus":"The French djent collective opens their concept album Februus in a frozen, dissociated void. A consciousness feels all heat drain away as frost seals it inside glass walls, numbing pain and blurring the line between free will and puppetry. Ambient textures mirror the lyric's manikin state, where every stimulation only mimics choice, and emptiness paradoxically sharpens sensation into a cold, clinical awakening.","sonnet":"Emotional numbness experienced as a form of clarity. As all inner warmth drains away, a frost-state settles over consciousness, erasing discomfort and freeing the senses to register the world with new precision — a puppet-like passivity that, paradoxically, feels like relief and even an education.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~funambule": {"haiku":"Caught between opposing forces with no agency; the rope-walker balances precariously while others pull strings, afraid to show emotion or claim purpose.","opus":"A dizzying drift through frigid water, the narrator weightless and ignored, tugged by two forces on one tether while pleading to see someone's concealed feelings. He likens himself to a tightrope-walker who won't be lured and hauled into a fathomless void, refusing to be exploited. Uneven Structure, a French djent band, pair this cryptic tale of emotional manipulation with atmospheric, layered heaviness that leaves the listener suspended alongside him.","sonnet":"Pulled between two forces who treat the speaker as an instrument rather than a person, she realizes she has been walking a tightrope between them all along — mask slipping, balance failing. She refuses to be bait or ballast any longer: purposeless is better than used.","src":"haiku","fable":"The trouble starts where the lyric does: a noise splits the water, a chill rises along his back, and from that shock the speaker never gets his feet back under him. He is handed to currents he did not choose, told what he contributes counts for nothing, told his opinion goes unwanted — and then shown the mechanism, two hands pulling leashes that run to one harness, so he grasps that whatever moves him is not his to steer. The plural sisters compound it: he is being led, not walking. Against all that steering the title lands with a sting, because the person who cannot keep footing is not the one adrift but the partner, so guarded that letting an emotion show would be its own act of balance on a wire. The song's cruelty is that this guardedness works on the speaker like a hand at the back of the neck — he is baited, pulled under, made to feel he can only stay level by gripping the very partner sinking him. The mask slips, and instead of reaching for rescue he answers by going inert: he refuses to be used, declines to carry intentions that were never his, and turns his own worthlessness into the last thing he still controls. Purposeless is not defeat here but the one move on the board he can make alone.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"turning one's own worthlessness into the last available self-determined move","built":"the tightrope-walker of the title is the guarded partner sinking him, not the speaker"},
"uneven-structure~greeted-and-dining": {"haiku":"Predatory consumption of victims by hierarchical system; the speaker cycles through roles of predator and consumed, trapped in ritualized dehumanisation.","opus":"A dense, cannibalistic allegory of being consumed and made complicit, victims drowning together while the narrator gorges and carries the weight of his own choices. The dinner conceit turns predation into a meditation on becoming the muse of your own extinction.","sonnet":"A predatory relationship told through the metaphor of consumption — the narrator is processed and digested by a creature whose pantry is replenished by new prey, and the horror is that the victim eventually helps carry the weight of their own destruction. The muse and the meal become one.","src":"haiku","fable":"A person is boiled down to something coarse and stripped of any distinguishing form and then poured back to be swallowed and remade, and from that first line the song fixes its horror in the plain machinery of a meal. The speaker floats among ages of the ensnared, one prey among thousands no different from itself, before the perspective turns and the host at the table starts speaking through the same mouth. The invitation is the cruelest image: reach in, gorge, take a piece from a pantry already carelessly emptied, and by eating you inherit the host's appetite outright, so guest and dish trade places at the one seat. What holds it all in a single concrete gesture is the mouth wiped clean on the sheet as the dinner ends, hospitality reduced to wiping away the last of you, the guests told to stand up and put on brightness while they are still retching. The closing sting lands on the diner rather than the food: what was set out was placed there for a reason, the whole ordeal was your own, and you are named the reason for your own undoing. Within the record's drowned-water setting this reads as the sea or death playing supper host, but the lyric needs no myth to bite — its threat is the ordinary table, where the appetite you are offered is the trap.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","faith & the occult"],"means":"guest and dish trade places at the table in a single gesture","built":"the mouth wiped on the sheet collapses hospitality into erasure of the consumed"},
"uneven-structure~hail":{"haiku":"A destructive flood washes away innocence and marks the earth; transformation and rebirth emerge as fear subsides and fertility returns.","opus":"Apocalyptic downpour and flood scour and reshape the body before draining away to expose dry land and the very beginning of conscious, physical existence rising out of the storm. Uneven Structure, a French djent collective, pair this baptismal, world-ending imagery with layered atmospheric heaviness, the deluge receding into a new capacity for feeling and renewal.","sonnet":"A violent flood strips away innocence and hierarchy alike before receding to reveal bare ground for the first time. The storm is both literal and psychic — paranoid systems collapse under its weight — and what follows is not triumph but the first fragile sensation of embodied existence on newly uncovered earth.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~hero":{"haiku":"A supposed hero reflects on their exhausting conquest, questioning whether victory was worth the moral cost. They reject false glory and the illusions that feed it, choosing a new path of authentic struggle.","opus":"A conqueror recounts his brutal climb over enemies and obstacles to victory, then questions its cost, dismisses hollow illusions of glory, and resolves to fight on for himself.","sonnet":"A narrator who has fought and won many battles surveys his trophies with disillusionment, questioning whether the cost was worth it and rejecting the hollow glories of the past — declaring that the real battles still ahead will be fought on his own terms.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~incube": {"haiku":"A messenger travels between worlds drawing closer to a destination, describing surreal, dense sensations of pressure and cryptic revelation.","opus":"Part of the French band's atmospheric concept album, this track trails a summoned envoy lured forward through mist toward eight waiting sisters who need him. Dense imagery of dark liquid, an iodine tide and a melody swelling as he nears sustains a mood of yielding to mounting weight and dread, where a promised rest feels shadowed by something unresolved.","sonnet":"A journey toward a source of sound that draws the speaker irresistibly forward. As he moves closer the signal strengthens into a cryptic transmission, while a second presence fills him with dark pressure and corrosive weight. The path ends not in rest but in a sheet of unreadable markings and a broken promise of peace.","src":"haiku","fable":"A wordless sound pulls the speaker forward — a smear of pitches sliding through the murk — and he follows it the way you chase a scent you cannot name, charged with carrying something back to the sisterhood of eight who cannot fetch it themselves. That errand is the whole engine: he is a courier who never chose the route, and the moment he commits, the heavens above him refuse to hold still. Then the frame swings to a second person laboring to patch their own failings, while some sullen presence tips a cold, dark liquid into his hearing and his body fails in clinical, itemized ways — blood congealing to the heft of metal, fingers he would sooner see stilled than left senseless, a pressure sealing off his breath. The turn is a bargain, not a collapse: he offers to walk the road out to its end alongside the one he addresses, to outlast their worries, and only then to surrender the verdict once the sourness between them has thinned to a far-off apparition. What follows is stranger and more physical — a current strung with pale beads, a mark inking itself across hide and flesh like an antiseptic sting, the courting tone swelling nearer until it hauls him clean out of himself and leaves him clutching a score he cannot decode. The title's nod to a nocturnal weight and a thing quietly gestating hangs at the far edge, and the rest he was promised turns out nothing like the wreckage now in his hands.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","dissociation & numbness","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"courier who never chose the route carrying an undecodable score","built":"the promised rest arrives as wreckage rather than the thing foretold"},
"uneven-structure~innocent":{"haiku":"An adult reflects on lost childhood innocence and abandoned loved ones, torn between nostalgia for their joyful past and awareness of their betrayal.","opus":"A djent meditation on lost childhood innocence and the yearning to return to those who were left behind, the narrator wandering back through memory toward a warmth he abandoned; nostalgia as both trespass and homecoming.","sonnet":"A song about lost childhood innocence and the guilt of having drifted away from brothers or close companions — the narrator returns carrying both the best and worst of his journey, beginning to sense again the laughter and sweetness of what was left behind.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~jester":{"haiku":"A performer trapped performing their trained act endlessly to audiences with 'plastic faces'; now they reverse the dynamic to play with souls and bodies themselves, though discovering absurdity beneath the spectacle.","opus":"Portrays a broken entertainer compelled to perform, his grin only a mask over a slain sense of self as he repeats his degrading act on an endless loop. The twist is a role reversal: once the terrified inner child finally rests, the jester takes over to toy cruelly with others, finding the whole grim spectacle nothing but absurdity.","sonnet":"About performing a persona until it becomes hollow — the narrator is a trained monkey repeating tricks for plastic-faced crowds, the smile a knife-drawn mask, until a spark ignites something darker and they begin playing with souls and bodies themselves, descending into beautiful nonsense.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~lover":{"haiku":"A reflection into another's eyes reveals unity where two souls merge, consuming each other in intimate fusion.","opus":"French djent turning self-love into something eerier: a narrator merging with his own reflection, offering himself as vessel for another's evils and refusing to share what they become. Intimacy curdles into possessive alchemy.","sonnet":"A Narcissus-inflected song about a consuming, possibly narcissistic love — the narrator gazes into their own reflection and finds beauty there, promises to be everything expected, and swears to drink the youth of this bond entirely rather than share it.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~magician":{"haiku":"A magician's life built on illusion collapses on the final night when the greatest trick is disappearing forever.","opus":"An illusionist's confession from inside a house of cards, the stage tricks a metaphor for a life spent manufacturing others' joy until the final act where he vanishes for good. The disappearance reads as the ultimate illusion and possibly the end of him.","sonnet":"About a performer who has spent his life creating illusions and surviving danger, but arrives at the final trick — making himself disappear — which turns out to be the end of the show and of life itself.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~our-embrace": {"haiku":"A predatory entity consumes willing victims in a ritualistic cycle of deception and complicity.","opus":"A grotesque cosmic-horror scenario of being digested and absorbed back into a devouring being, voiced by both the swallowed prey and the willing predator. A fresh world rises from the void atop cast-off flesh, and the victim finally becomes the inspiration for its own annihilation.","sonnet":"A relationship framed as predation and exploitation — one party is consumed, processed, and discarded by the other who feeds off them like a parasite, leaving behind only the remnants of a depleted self.","src":"haiku","fable":"A devouring is dressed up as a marriage. The whole lyric runs on one image-system — digestion — where the beloved is not embraced but eaten: rendered down to a vulgar acid, stocked in a pantry, wiped from a mouth as the meal ends, so that the eater can fuse the swallowed thing back into itself and come out whole again on what it absorbed. Uneven Structure lets the predator narrate, and the horror is how tender it sounds; the communion, the reward, the token kept to shine are the vocabulary of devotion turned to the vocabulary of feeding. Against that consumption runs a second family — a black-intentioned order rising newly-made from the abyss, the eight sisters of the album's own myth — La Partition's voiceless mermaids — resting on the leftovers — so the digested self becomes the raw matter of a cosmos, the muse authoring her own extinction by being metabolized into something larger. The embrace of the title is real and monstrous at once: to be loved here is to be dissolved into the lover.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","faith & the occult"],"means":"love recast as digestion that reassembles the lover","built":"the predator narrates in the full register of tenderness"},
"uneven-structure~outlaw":{"haiku":"Someone sheds the rules imposed on them and embraces their wild, animalistic nature without restraint, taking and consuming what they desire and refusing to be judged for their raw appetites.","opus":"French djent framing the rejection of every rule as freeing an inner reptilian animal, the narrator breaking his chains to seize and consume all he desires, reason declared dead and appetite crowned as sovereign.","sonnet":"About surrendering entirely to one's animal nature — the narrator spent years trying to cage an instinct-driven self, but ultimately breaks free, declaring they want to take everything they desire, love and hate without restraint, and burn both ends of the candle.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~plenitude":{"haiku":"Ascending beyond material limits, consciousness dissolves into boundless light where matter and mind converge, transcending all previous earthly constraint and challenge.","opus":"An ascension told through dense djent, casting off the body and every prior failure as substance and thought dissolve into one another, the final inhalation before atmosphere turns to radiance and the self melts into total fullness.","sonnet":"About a moment of transcendence or ego dissolution — obstacles and dead ends fall away as the narrator ascends toward a state where matter and mind collapse into one undivided radiance, a kind of permanent enlightenment or death-as-completion.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~quittance":{"haiku":"The narrator regrets following a fraudulent path that shattered their integrity; they demand redemption and strip themselves bare to escape the shallowness and rise again.","sonnet":"A person confronts the gap between where life has landed and who they were meant to be, confesses to failures of integrity, and demands their own redemption — then finds a strange, cleared silence: permission at last to leave and begin again.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~ruler":{"haiku":"A ruler confesses that power corrupted him completely, killing empathy and mercy, until he became a tyrant drunk on others' suffering and suffering alike.","opus":"A djent band tells a downfall as a fable of ruin: a solitary figure freezing amid reddened snowfall, the streaks of his losses scoring the white. A gift of enormous authority proved a curse, hollowing out his compassion and pity. Bloated on his subjects' labor and intoxicated by their suffering, he swells into a deranged tyrant, robed in illusion and confessing at last that he betrayed them all.","sonnet":"A king surveys the wreckage of his own reign and confesses that power was never a gift — it infected everything he was, eliminating empathy and love until only a monster of vanity remained. The admission of having betrayed those he ruled is rendered as a desolate reckoning with what corruption made of him.","src":"haiku"},
"uneven-structure~succube": {"haiku":"A speaker describes being emotionally and psychologically consumed by a seductive, deceitful presence that feeds on their weakness and vulnerability. They are drained, unable to escape, and recognize the parasitic nature of the relationship.","opus":"A blindfolded submission to a demon lover, the narrator refusing sight and trailing scent and breath into a game he insists harms no one. Betrayed and emptied, in an act he names self-betrayal and disgrace, he ends husked and withered yet is still asked for more. Uneven Structure's ambient djent lends this seductive, ruinous myth a smothering, immersive weight.","sonnet":"Seduction as sensory deprivation and willing surrender — the speaker has agreed to be blindfolded, deafened, guided only by scent and the caress of something that cannot be fully perceived. By the time the damage is apparent, every resource has already been consumed, and what remains is a dry husk asking what more could possibly be taken from it.","src":"haiku","fable":"The speaker talks himself into his own undoing and narrates each step of the persuasion as it lands. He resolves in advance to be led by a low voice and a scent instead of by sight, refusing the one sense that would name the deceit he already half-expects, and he insists the covering of his eyes is his own choosing rather than hers — a wager he keeps framing as harmless play. What the text tracks is the machinery of that argument: a case he wins against himself even while the touches settle over his skin and the elation curdles into something that pounds. The confession lands without flinching, a betrayal turned inward, an inherited pattern rewritten from within, owned as fault in the same instant it is claimed as absolute. Then the register tips from seduction into hunger — she draws down everything he holds, never filled, boring further in, until he is emptied husk and can only ask what remains to be taken. The dread the song earns is wholly of his making: he shut one sense and opened another, chose to be steered by what he could not verify, and mistook being devoured for being consoled, with the album's drowned-siren frame hovering at the edge of a bargain whose real spine is the shut-eye consent and the hollowing it buys.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","shame & repression"],"means":"choosing to be steered by the sense that cannot name the deceit","built":"the shut-eye consent is staged as the speaker's own choosing throughout"},
"uneven-structure~the-bait": {"haiku":"A poisoned witness stands among ruin and empire's collapse, trapped in someone's game or court proceeding, watching wrathful comeuppance unfold.","opus":"A dense, surreal djent piece imagining a drowning world and a manipulative figure who has lured the narrator to witness some atrocity, the imagery of scales and ruby water blurring seduction, entrapment and complicity.","sonnet":"A witness or participant in a catastrophic collapse — empire, court, ecosystem — is drawn into someone else's game, used and played until the flesh and the water itself run red, unsure whether they were brought here to witness justice or just to be destroyed.","src":"haiku","fable":"Read through its title, the whole song speaks from inside the lure: a sacrificial catch narrating its own dressing and cooking while an unnamed captor stages the spectacle. Two image-families braid together — a drowning apocalypse where sea and sky stop keeping their distance and a collapsing skyline leaves only remnant creatures, and a culinary rite where salt cures the chest, savior slides into savor, and bodies steep in a wine-dark bath that reads at once as vintage, blood, and broth. What holds them is the second-person captor, addressed as showman, judge, and cook in one, the parade mounted before a kneeling gallery. The speaker's real work is the run of questions put to that figure — whether this is a plea to be made whole again or just another cruel game, how much longer the playing lasts — which peel the ceremony's grandeur back to reveal a plainer cruelty underneath: for all the racket, the man is only being cooked. Uneven Structure's djent maximalism suits exactly this, dense allusive surface over a single brutal fact.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control","nature & the elements"],"means":"ceremony's grandeur peeled back to a plainer cruelty","built":"savior slides into savor — the word undergoes the body's reduction"},
"uneven-structure~your-scent":{"haiku":"Betrayal and victimization at someone's hands, as they exploit and consume others for their own twisted satisfaction.","opus":"An accusatory address to a seductive, devouring figure, likened to a mermaid, who lures people to their ruin and consumes them. The speakers refuse to be thankful or forgiving, framing their suffering as a lament for the unchosen victims.","sonnet":"An accusation directed at a seductive, consuming figure who lured others with false promises, fed on their suffering as seasoning, and left the narrator and fellow victims with nothing but lament and a hollow sense of validation.","src":"haiku","fable":"A siren-as-cannibal governs the whole song: victims are dropped into a boiling pot, their weeping becomes her seasoning, and a shroud of scales draws shut over any mind that has agreed to be swallowed. Uneven Structure build it the way they build their music, in accreting layers that keep circling back to the mouth as the site of both worship and consumption, until devotion and being devoured stop being distinguishable. The refusal to be grateful at the midpoint sounds like resistance, but the closing movement dismantles it: the speaker confesses that her hunger to plug the hollow at her core is the exact twin of his own craving to be looked at once and told he is real. What reads as a monster-lyric about a devouring seductress collapses into a portrait of two vacancies feeding each other, the accusation curling back to convict the accuser.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","love & desire","violence & murder"],"means":"two vacancies feeding each other, the accusation convicting the accuser","built":"the monster-lyric collapses when the speaker's hunger mirrors the devourer's"},
"uni-vs~i-think-i-m-better-on-my-own":{"haiku":"Solitude becomes both refuge and slow suffocation as the speaker severs connection while fearing the cost of isolation, caught between autonomy and annihilation.","opus":"Severing every tie, even the third eye, to embrace total isolation as survival, hope offered and then shattered until solitude feels safer than connection. Despair hardens into a grim conviction that being alone is the only thing that won't betray you.","sonnet":"A speaker severing the ties of consciousness itself — taking a blade to the third eye — and concluding through despair and suicidal imagery that isolation is preferable to the suffering connection brings, especially after hope was held close and broke them anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"unison-square-garden~a-s4mtwq":{"haiku":"Contrasting bittersweet emotions create meaning; life's chaos needs both sweetness and bitterness to feel real, and continuing to sing and connect is what proves we're alive.","opus":"Unison Square Garden built their identity on defending music from utility, and this song makes that stance explicit. Against ideological noise and the messy bittersweet reality of human emotion, festival crowds crying right after cheering, the band argues that the only proof they exist as themselves is that they keep playing, the act of continuing standing as their Q.E.D.","sonnet":"The band insists that music cannot be reduced to a social tool or rational instrument — against the noise of ideology and the messy bittersweet reality of human emotion (festival crowds that cry right after cheering), the only proof they exist as themselves is that they keep playing, and that act of continuing is the Q.E.D.","src":"haiku"},
"unitytx~world-of-malice":{"haiku":"Greed and violence perpetuate endless cycles—survival corrupts moral foundation, leaving trust impossible and only bloodshed familiar.","opus":"A grim survey of a world ruled by money, power and greed, where trust is impossible and violence over peace is the norm, insisting this eternal war and its trauma have become part of the self.","sonnet":"Trauma, constant betrayal, and the witness of violence define a world where 'it's always lie or cheat' and trust is impossible; grief is woven permanently into the speaker, and the eternal war of malice, greed, and murder is not a metaphor but the documented reality around them.","src":"haiku"},
"unkle~lonely-soul":{"opus":"A trip-hop meditation on isolation and mortality fronted by a guest vocalist searching for peace before the end. It reaches toward the fear of dying anonymous and unremembered, yet finds a strange serenity in surrender, treating the severing of life as flight rather than loss. Grand, cinematic melancholy for the solitary.","sonnet":"A contemplation of anonymous death and the isolation of the soul — the narrator accepts that he will die unnamed and unfamed, in an unknown place, while also suggesting there is a moment when the cord of life should rightly be cut. The spiritual imagery is dark and resigned, finding no comfort in afterlife or legacy, only the fact of walking on.","src":"sonnet"},
"unkle~restless":{"haiku":"A restless person describes mutual indifference and dependency in a relationship, caught in cycles of taking and breaking, following light toward uncertain love.","opus":"A restless drifter's defiant, self-destructive creed, unbothered by being disliked and clinging to habits he won't defend anyone talking him out of. Beneath the swagger runs a sinking search for some light and love to follow.","sonnet":"A restless person who doesn't care what anyone thinks of them describes being tangled up with someone — mutually dependent, habitual, broken but mending — and insists they'll follow the light toward love regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"unknown-mortal-orchestra~can-t-keep-checking-my-phone":{"haiku":"Digital obsession fragments attention and prevents genuine connection; the speaker rejects constant communication to preserve autonomy.","opus":"The modern compulsion of the phone as an anxious tether to someone, the narrator trying to break the habit of waiting on a call while his mind drifts to escapist futures anywhere but this dependence.","sonnet":"A dissociated, looping state of distraction: amid cosmic thoughts about holographic universes and strange future foods, the speaker admits they are compulsively checking their phone for someone, unable to stop even while trying to seem busy and independent, the anxiety of waiting quietly overwhelming everything else.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~as-they-bloom":{"haiku":"Beauty masks danger and destruction, with alluring surfaces hiding sharp thorns that pierce and poison anyone who draws close.","opus":"Beauty as lure and poison, razor-edged flowers whose loveliest petals cut the deepest, deadly the instant you touch them. The warning against seduction casts an alluring person or temptation as gorgeous camouflage for something that will sting.","sonnet":"About beautiful things that are secretly lethal — flowers that are razor-sharp, colours that pierce, prettiness used as camouflage for poison; the song warns against being seduced by something that looks like love or beauty but is designed to sting and destroy.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~betray":{"haiku":"Hidden malice and duplicity poison connection while the speaker hunts for destruction beneath angelelic masks. Trust becomes trap as betrayal emerges from intimacy's dark core.","opus":"A warning about a two-faced lover hiding venom behind an angelic face, the point being that you can't play the innocent while you're out hunting for someone to betray. It strips the disguise to show the monster under the smile.","sonnet":"An exposure of someone who presents as angelic while hunting for betrayal — dripping poison under a halo, hiding the monster behind shiny eyes — the speaker strips the disguise and welcomes them to the other side, where halos are gone and the monster is plain.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~black-lotus":{"haiku":"Contradiction blooms as antisocial love; the speaker yearns for closeness while drifting away, holding void and absence as beautiful, refusing attachment's permanence.","opus":"The painful pull of wanting closeness while emotionally drifting away, retreating into antisocial isolation and settling for staying friends to fill a void that won't be filled.","sonnet":"An antisocial person who drifts away wants to stay close while remaining far — the black lotus names a love that mixes good with bad and sits with a void inside at midnight, negotiating the contradiction of craving proximity while being unable to sustain it.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~can-t-have-it-all":{"haiku":"Pursuit of totality collapses under weight; grasping for everything leaves only debris and the tornado's destructive spiral.","opus":"An address to a jealous, insatiable person collapsing under the weight of their own wanting, unable to grieve because they can only covet. Amid the wreckage of a ruined mutual search, the speaker refuses to be left carrying their appetite alone.","sonnet":"A post-breakup reckoning where both people were hunting for things the other couldn't give — one searched for the other's soul, the other for peace — and the debris left behind proves that wanting everything at once destroys it; \"like a one-way tornado\" is the shape of the damage.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~color-chaos":{"haiku":"Creation that turns corrupted and self-hating, art becoming a nightmare rather than joy.","opus":"Creation curdling into obsession, the joy of pouring yourself onto a canvas turning into a kaleidoscopic nightmare, art and a love both souring into something the maker comes to hate.","sonnet":"About creative obsession curdling into pain — the narrator pours himself into an artwork (a relationship rendered as painting) and what emerges is hatred and decay; the kaleidoscopic beauty he sought destroyed what it touched.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~dig":{"haiku":"Emotional barriers frustrate connection as the speaker yearns to be truly known yet remains hidden in protective layers, feeling fundamentally misunderstood despite visible vulnerability.","opus":"A plea to be truly known, the narrator hidden in layers built around a guarded heart, daring someone to keep digging deeper. The ache underneath is fear that no one ever will, that the effort to be understood goes unmet.","sonnet":"A plea to be known beyond the surface: the speaker hides in a forest built around their heart, masked by red-eyed warning signs, and asks someone who claims to care to dig deeper through every layer rather than stop at the visible misunderstanding.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~dreamblur":{"haiku":"Reality and dreams merging and becoming indistinguishable, confusion of consciousness.","opus":"An electronic lament for a love only the narrator seems able to perceive, and only in hindsight. He remembers releasing a bird, letting something precious take off before grasping its worth, and can't get past what they shared. The refrain about seeing something in someone captures the ache of a bond lost to its own blindness.","sonnet":"Something unique and unrepeatable about a person is visible only to the narrator — but that person cannot come back, the dove was let fly, and no feeling since competes with what they had. \"Blind is love, it's sink or swim\" is the only explanation for why moving on remains impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~fragile-skin-of-me":{"haiku":"Hypersensitivity and accumulating wounds leave the speaker increasingly exposed and fragile, each harsh word embedding deeper as the skin that separates self from world thins.","opus":"How cruel words lodge under thin skin and echo inward, a distorted self-image that suffocates everything and rewrites reality. It captures the way old wounds keep haunting long after the words were spoken.","sonnet":"Words from the past leave permanent marks: sharp phrases echo within like needles on skin, a distorted life suffocates everything, and the speaker — seeing the world through glass eyes and a heart still stuck in the past — cannot stop being haunted by their own fragility.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~goner":{"haiku":"Recognizing loss only after it's gone, haunted by shadows you refuse to release.","opus":"The pattern of only recognizing loss once it's already over, someone keeping the pain in frame to keep feeling it, alone and unwilling to admit it, hindsight arriving too late every time.","sonnet":"About repeatedly failing to recognize when something is truly over until it's already gone — the narrator only sees endings after they've ended, carries the pain to stay connected to what's lost, and admits he's alone but can't say it; it's a portrait of someone who holds on past the point of sense.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~guts":{"haiku":"Enmeshment breeds sickness; the speaker remains trapped in dreams constructed by another, unable to exist independently without drowning.","opus":"A toxic dependency that sickens and consumes, needing someone who offers only a futureless path yet can't be lived without. The push-pull of darkness and craving turns the relationship into a slow disappearance of the self.","sonnet":"A toxic dependency described as being dragged into darkness — the other person makes the speaker sick to their guts, pulls them into a path without a future, yet the inability to live without them persists even as the speaker recognises they are already gone.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~my-heaven-became":{"haiku":"Paradise inverts catastrophically; the speaker watches their ideal world transform into nightmare, questioning whether change or deception caused collapse.","opus":"The vertigo of a good thing souring, heaven curdling into hell as circumstances flip, the narrator stranded between two worlds on a cliff. It sits in the bitter irony of how fast happiness can invert into its opposite.","sonnet":"A short, stark reckoning: something that was once paradise has inverted entirely — 'my heaven became my hell' — with the speaker standing on a cliff, choosing between enjoying the view and fearing the fall, caught between two worlds with nothing left to say.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~neons-away":{"haiku":"Isolation and strange metal surroundings replace human warmth; the speaker glides through a neon-lit space where being alone feels more honest than connection ever did.","opus":"Loneliness inflicted by other people until the narrator would rather withdraw into a cold neon fantasy where solitude doesn't mean being lonely. The one genuine thing in the whole city was a person now gone, leaving only the painted, numbing world.","sonnet":"A relationship that existed only as a real thing in a city full of artificiality — now that the person is gone, the narrator is left alone in the neon unreality, preferring imagined isolation to the particular loneliness of being in a crowd without them.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~night":{"haiku":"Sacrifice and devotion cannot breach walls of coldness and indifference as the speaker drowns trying to save. Futility defines love, and breathing itself becomes burden under another's weight.","opus":"A charred breakup song of devotion repaid with cruelty, the narrator battered and bruised yet defiant, convinced he was fated to end up on the losing side. Its sharpest jab is the taunt that his cold, empty ex could never feel, ache or dream with the depth he does.","sonnet":"A speaker drained and bruised by a relationship with someone soulless who imitates rather than feels — \"you fake me, imitate me\" — declaring themselves built to lose while demanding life rather than the hollow echo the other person offers.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~one-on-one":{"haiku":"Toxicity thrives in isolation; when confronted directly, the speaker discovers their partner's control mechanisms dissolve, revealing mutual vulnerability beneath the venom.","opus":"A combative love where affection only shows up in the narrator's lowest moments, framed as a duel neither can walk away from. Venom and survival imagery make devotion feel like a fight to the death nobody wins.","sonnet":"A relationship described as battlefield — the other person only shows love when the speaker is down, aiming a weapon and refusing to say goodbye, leaving the speaker to ask who can survive life without them when venom rains from the sky.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~ridiculous":{"haiku":"Life spiraling through cycles of chaos and betrayal, aware of the absurdity but unable to escape.","opus":"Refusing to be dragged into other people's agendas and vendettas, waving off the pettiness with a self-aware shrug, choosing not to chase or hate even as the cycle of retribution keeps pulling back.","sonnet":"Recognizing a cycle of mutual harm in a relationship — lies and vengeance served cold, a thin line between caring and sabotage; the narrator admits their own behavior is ridiculous, refuses to keep chasing, and tries to step out of the pattern even as it keeps pulling them back.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~slingshot":{"haiku":"Reckless passion launches the speaker into uncertain chaos; they surrender to the ride, knowing collision and crash come but unable to refuse.","opus":"The thrill and terror of jumping headlong into something risky, wanting to be flung to the top while bracing for the crash. It sits in the tension of craving change and chaos while already fearing the regret.","sonnet":"An ambivalent leap into something new and chaotic — 'I'm not sure of everything, I want something different, I can feel the fear in the air' — asking a partner to take their heart and not throw it back, hoping they'll never regret having jumped in together.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~smile-on-my-face":{"haiku":"The speaker masks agony with performance, dancing in darkness while hiding the chaos that feeds their survival and destroys them simultaneously.","opus":"Masking pain with performed cheer until the mask becomes the person, misery turned into a favorite escape. The image of a ruler carrying a jester's heart nails how hiding behind a grin quietly devours the one doing it.","sonnet":"A person who has made a hiding place out of performed happiness — 'I find a hiding place, I wear a happy face when my life gets bitter tasting' — dances in the dark as a joker-hearted king, wondering if anyone can see through the game even as masking the misery has become second nature.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~sunlight-or-demise":{"haiku":"Apocalyptic choice between transcendence and surrender; the speaker burns everything to reach light or accepts the descent, unable to find middle ground.","opus":"An ultimatum aimed at a duplicitous person who squandered every decent instinct they once had. The narrator vows to raze everything and forces them to reckon with whether they can face their own choices, offering no gentle middle path between salvation and ruin.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a two-faced person who made empty promises — the narrator is done, choosing to burn it all down rather than keep living in the lie, with no middle ground between full sunshine and total ruin.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~thread":{"haiku":"The speaker hangs by a thread that inches toward breaking; each pull tightens the noose while the void yawns beneath.","opus":"Hanging on by a filament, the pressure painfully real even when time seems unreal, dreading the next tug that snaps everything. Cracks that won't close over make the breaking point feel like a foregone conclusion.","sonnet":"About being so close to total psychological collapse that a single additional pull will finish the break — the narrator is holding on by nothing, the stress is real even as time feels surreal, and every small thing is pulling them apart further. It is a song about the moment just before breaking down, when the thread is already fraying and everyone else's whispers feel like blades.","src":"haiku"},
"unlike-pluto~yes-offense":{"opus":"A deliberately abrasive pushing-away of someone toxic, the narrator meaning to offend and severing ties; a twist reveals he's really projecting, blaming others for the pain because he can't confront his own lies and self-loathing.","sonnet":"An act of deliberately aggressive self-excision from a harmful relationship — the narrator acknowledges their own role in the damage before cutting ties, calling themselves a monster and impostor while simultaneously naming the other person as dangerous, treating the break as necessary surgery.","src":"sonnet"},
"unloco~bruises":{"haiku":"Emotional wounds accumulated through silence and concealment; the speaker demands visibility of scars while refusing to reveal their own pain to those who inflicted it.","opus":"A nu-metal confrontation with damage and the refusal to reveal it. The narrator demands to be shown another's bruises while insisting his own truth stays hidden, trapped inside a lie he lives and gives. The push and pull between exposing wounds and concealing them drives a track about mutual, unspoken hurt and the walls built around it.","sonnet":"A dare to show what damage has been done and a refusal to hide one's own — get up, let me see your bruises — with the admission that the speaker has bruises left to show too, falling in between everything, living a lie. The paradox is that closeness is sought through shared damage while the speaker vows never to let you know and never to let you go.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~111":{"haiku":"An angel drowns in seraphim light while truth burns beneath holy promises. Secrets and wings spread across the unreachable grave.","opus":"An angel cast out despite a promised throne of gold, choosing burial and widespread wings over a heaven built on loud lies, declaring itself the only one; a fall from grace framed as final, defiant self-possession.","sonnet":"A song about a fallen or disillusioned divine figure — the narrator once sang with stars and danced in flames, was promised a golden throne, but finds that truth is wild and the holy gate hides secrets; they call themselves the only one, spread their wings, and accept burial, arriving at a point of no return that feels like transcendence.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~abandoned":{"haiku":"Existential abandonment where diseased thought and broken belief systems replace what was once sacred light.","opus":"A meditation on emptiness and civilizational decline, a narrator who finds the void completes rather than destroys him, his own words feeding the disease as old scriptures reshape into new belief. Doom framed as inevitable, an age recognised as already lost.","sonnet":"About the abandonment of souls in a dying age — ancient scriptures, sulfurous flowers, and a creeping darkness describe a world where the narrator knew the void was coming, accepted it, and watches helplessly as the light fails.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~another-sky":{"haiku":"Pursues a perfected alternate reality and higher meaning, seeking guidance through celestial signs. Finds peace in seeking and light in transcendence.","opus":"A restless seeker turning thought into sound and remaking his world in private, hemmed in by his own limits yet steered by constellations that point the way toward some elsewhere he keeps reaching for. It's a grasp at transcendence pulled from nothing. Unprocessed, a German prog-metal band, set this cosmic yearning to technical, jazz-tinged playing, casting art as the road to something better.","sonnet":"Someone who grew up constrained by limitation and emotional shutdown finds orientation in the stars overhead — a pattern that guides without demanding anything back. The sky becomes the only stable thing when everything human has been fragmented or escaped.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~antler-s-decay":{"haiku":"Nature and wildlife are collapsing into extinction as human industry destroys habitats, leaving survivors desperate and a landscape of ash.","opus":"The German progressive-metal band frames ecological collapse as a bleak fable of extinction. Forests rot to ash, demon-eyed indifference watches survivors race against time, and creatures fall to the machines even as they beg for asylum. Wolves that once howled at the moon now cry into darkened, sunless days. Technical intricacy serves a plain lament for a world consumed by reckless human progress.","sonnet":"Industrial progress consuming the last of the natural world. Wolves howl through darkened skies, animals run from machines rather than predators, and sulfur works through bone structures once shaped by evolution. The final survivors beg for shelter that never arrives while forests rot and sunlight disappears permanently.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~artificial-void":{"haiku":"The song explores the paradox of shifting scales and possibilities, where tiny fragile things become enormous tomorrow, suggesting that small actions ripple into massive unforeseen consequences.","opus":"A meditation on infinite possibility and the paradox of existing everywhere and nowhere, framed through a butterfly effect where the tiniest act reshapes the future. The call to overgrow yourself makes transcendence sound both liberating and dissolving.","sonnet":"A song about the butterfly effect of existence — a fragile, tiny thing ripples outward into immensity, and the narrator urges becoming the future, overgrowing oneself, as an antidote to feeling small and lost in an infinite space.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~avatar":{"haiku":"Machines trap humans in simulated dreams that erase identity; the speaker merges spiritually with nature to resist and witness the approaching apocalypse.","opus":"A sci-fi reverie of merging with a living machine-world, the narrator entering its glowing depths even as artificial minds mimic his own and a cult's innocence is marked for slaughter. He calls on a tireless, undying heart as the thing that carries him through suffering. Unprocessed, a German prog-metal band, wed jazzy technicality to this uneasy longing for the synthetic and the alive to become one.","sonnet":"A figure trapped inside a simulation created by forces that have captured and reshaped its identity, reaching toward a beating heart — something real and alive — as the only way through the manufactured misery. The appeal is to something rooted and organic, described as a tree with shimmering leaves and deep roots, standing apart from the machine-world and the cult that demands innocence bleed.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~berlin":{"haiku":"Urban coldness permeates intimate moments; desire burns amid glacial indifference while children fade silently from parental sight into abstract separation.","opus":"A German-sung descent into Berlin's nightlife as seduction and self-abandon, walking over the grave of his own reason toward temptation. The recurring cold refrain, a child slipping from a still hand, haunts the hedonism with something lost and grieving.","sonnet":"A night descent into Berlin's club scene becomes a meditation on the city's cold indifference — it is cold when the wind moves through late streets, cold when a child slips quietly from a calm hand. The speaker rehearses his show in the mirror, drawn by perfume and his own fire, but the city's chill persists beneath.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~beyond-heaven-s-gate":{"haiku":"Unseen forces observe and judge from beyond visibility, their music luring toward darker places. The speaker glimpses another realm while terrestrial reality grows increasingly sinister.","opus":"Reaching past the visible toward unseen beings and cosmic mystery, half wonder and half warning as the serenade from beyond leads somewhere darker. It frames pushing the mind's limits as both revelation and risk.","sonnet":"Belief in something beyond the visible world — creatures unseen, beings above the clouds who serenade and judge — though the tune they choose leads the speaker somewhere darker, suggesting that faith in the transcendent is not simply comforting.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~closer":{"haiku":"Childhood trauma generates predatory dominance; the speaker perpetuates cycles of abuse learned from parental dysfunction, unable to interrupt intergenerational violence.","opus":"A hard-edged portrait of a bully shaped by a broken home, absent father and a mother lost to drugs, craving the recognition he never got. The swagger of terrorizing others barely covers a wound rooted in wishing his parents could see him.","sonnet":"Childhood bullying is traced to a home where a drug-addicted mother never kissed goodnight, and the speaker recognizes in himself the same capacity for harm — making victims for fun, leaving others in shivers. The wish that she could see him now is both pride and unresolved grief.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~covenant":{"opus":"A dissociative, dreamlike state caught between two hollow worlds, breathing in fear and dust while only fragmented memories remain, groping for a new way forward against the pull of the past.","sonnet":"A figure suspended between conscious states tries to find new directions while stripped of everything except fading memories, sensing they are caught at the boundary of two hollow worlds with no clear path forward.","src":"sonnet"},
"unprocessed~deadrose":{"haiku":"A beautiful, wild rose figure dances in lust and horror, drawing the speaker into a sensual yet destructive embrace that fades to black.","opus":"A gothic seduction and its fallout, a crimson woman raised among wolves who embodies beauty inside horror and lures the narrator into a doomed dance despite the danger. She drains his hatred and leaves him permanently altered, his life dimming as the dark closes in. Unprocessed set this lush, lust-and-ruin fairy tale to their intricate German prog-metal groove.","sonnet":"Dangerous attraction framed as natural beauty — a woman raised in darkness and wildness, dressed in velvet, whose thorns the speaker refuses to acknowledge until it is too late. The encounter leaves something permanently altered: hands bruised, life fading, and the pull toward her described as clinging like feathers on oil, impossible to shake and slowly consuming.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~dinner":{"haiku":"Collective religious dread mingles with narcissistic hunger; the speaker consumes communion and confessions while fear bonds worshippers together in shared terror.","opus":"A ritualistic, dread-soaked reckoning with someone the narrator both fears and surrenders to, offering up his flesh as a strange healing. Sin, betrayal and gospel imagery turn devotion into willing self-sacrifice at the edge of fading away.","sonnet":"An ambivalent relationship with a terrifying presence — the speaker holds their breath when it crosses their path, feels betrayal will precede the sun, and yet circles toward it: trying, crying, about to fade. The fear is inseparable from a strange sense of healing.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~down-the-spine":{"opus":"A science-fiction unease about a paradoxical world where sentient philosophy overtakes science, and humanity enters a new digital/cosmic reality that dissolves flesh and grows into a monstrous unknown, forcing a choice to face the horror or flee into chaos.","sonnet":"A dissolution of the familiar world into something alien and threatening — science overtaken by philosophy, a new cosmic reality detaching from what was known — and the confrontation is so overwhelming it either demands escape through the abyss or absorption into it.","src":"sonnet"},
"unprocessed~fear":{"haiku":"Creatures hide from the night while spirits drift in paradox; the speaker crumbles under shifting time and searches for an exit that never appears.","opus":"A dreamlike, dissociative meditation on identity and mortality, a shipwrecked soul in an eclipsed shroud falling for the void. The speaker's spirit floats free of his motionless body as he searches for the exit from a darkening future, but the signs only lead deeper into hexes and paradox.","sonnet":"A dissociative drift — the speaker floats between past and future, body and spirit, unable to ground themselves; time stops, spirits scatter, and the exit from the maze of fear and hex remains signless.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~glass":{"haiku":"Love requires self-sacrifice and damage, walking barefoot across fractures while maintaining tenderness. Eternal binding offers both comfort and wound, and the speaker accepts both.","opus":"Carrying a lover's heart with terrified care, an affection like treading over shards while pretending everything's fine. Its real subject is a vow that reaches past life itself, asking whether the other will remain through age, grave and death.","sonnet":"A love sustained through pretence and fragility: the speaker carries the other like a newborn, hiding their thorns, walking on broken glass — the secret they have both swallowed and the passionate lies underneath raise the question of whether either will stay until they fade away or meet in death.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~haven":{"haiku":"A vision describes an idealized paradise of natural beauty and life, shadowed by prophecy of drowning in mirrors and being pulled into an abyss.","opus":"The prog-metal band conjures a shimmering vision of paradise, an ever-green world of blossoms under a violet sky, teeming with wonders the sightless can't perceive. But the dream carries its own rot, foliage melting and shadows splitting open, and the glimpsed utopia dissolves even as the narrator beholds it, beauty and ruin fused together.","sonnet":"A vision of an idealized natural world — vivid, undying, abundant — is set against the recognition that such a place is being lost to blindness and entropy. The Neverland imagined is both refuge and elegy, a prophesied dissolution of the world mirrored in dripping, decaying imagery.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~house-of-waters":{"haiku":"A meditation on a house physically and temporally dissolving into the sea, representing rest after a journey. The space exists between memory and permanence, holding traces of the architect's creation.","opus":"A tired wanderer seeks rest in a crumbling seaside house slowly claimed by the ocean, noting its shuttered windows and worn flooring and wishing it to outlast time. The German prog-metal band leans into atmospheric reverie, the narrator speaking to the building like a lover as salt and decay become a meditation on endurance.","sonnet":"A traveller arrives at a beloved old house after what feels like a lifetime away, sinking into its familiar smell and the sound of waves battering its walls. The structure outlasts its visitors — the narrator will leave no trace, but the house, built by something unseen, never dies.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~ocean":{"haiku":"The ocean embodies cyclical refuge and danger, where natural beauty lulls but also threatens; the speaker surrenders to its transformative force while lacking agency or weapons to resist.","opus":"A progressive-metal surrender to a lover imagined as the sea, the narrator a sinking ship breaking against the waves, lulled to sleep then shaken awake, drawn to be dissolved into ever-shifting tides. Being the one boy without a weapon marks his defenselessness, poised between the shelter of the shore and the urge to flee the pull.","sonnet":"The ocean is addressed as both home and annihilator — its waves lull and then shake awake, the narrator is a sinking ship that shatters against its face with no one to signal. The request is to be taken through the night, absorbed into the tides, because he is \"the only boy without a gun\" in a world moving at speed without him.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~orange-grove":{"haiku":"Emotional upheaval drives the speaker toward dissolution; reaching for connection meets resistance as distance widens despite the ocean's call.","opus":"Emotional drift and self-imposed distance, keeping in motion with nowhere to go while the ocean calls like a lullaby. The plea not to push a loved one too far away fights against the pull toward emptiness.","sonnet":"About the fear of pushing someone away while not knowing how to stay close — the narrator keeps moving, does not know where to go, and is caught between the pull of something oceanic and transcendent and the very human worry that they are drifting too far from the person who matters. The question of whether they would still be loved if they stayed is the song's unresolved center.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~prototype":{"haiku":"A manufactured being climbs from a hollow laboratory prison toward consciousness, shedding artificial identity and becoming an autonomous clone.","opus":"Unprocessed cast the erosion of self as science-fiction dystopia, a narrator waking hollowed out and imprisoned by his own making, where nothing he once believed still holds. Amid sterile labs and dark towers, he lets himself be remade into an artificial being reaching for power, the transformation revealing itself as a machine built only to make him fight.","sonnet":"A person constructed and imprisoned by systems they participated in building wakes into a nightmarish environment of contamination and machinery. The drive toward self-evolution pulls against the recognition of having been made artificial — the climb toward the throne is also a loss of organic selfhood.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~real":{"haiku":"Emotional rawness and self-preservation conflict; distance required for survival despite closeness.","opus":"A prog-metal push and pull over a relationship growing too intense too fast. The narrator recoils when let close, accusing the other of not understanding how he hears their echoing words, of mistaking crushing waves for small steps forward. As the two blur and fade, he pleads for the silence that keeps the whole unbearable thing from being named.","sonnet":"A relationship where intimacy has become intrusion — your waves are crushing in my bay — and the speaker demands space to heal while accusing the other of not understanding the terms of what they share. Both parties are blurred, fading, and told to say nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~real-llucid-remix":{"haiku":"Two people slip into blurred connection, fading while feeling too real and absurd to sustain; neither speaks, both withdraw into incomprehension.","opus":"The German prog band turn inward on a failing relationship, a guarded narrator warning his partner away and questioning whether things have gotten too intense and whether they should step back to recover. The recurring sense of blurring and fading captures a bond dissolving even as it pulls close, nearness felt as suffocation.","sonnet":"A relationship becoming dangerously consuming and dishonest. Both people are losing their edges — boundaries dissolving, feelings unacknowledged, words circling without landing. He resists being overwhelmed while she presses close, and together they blur into something neither fully chose and neither can clearly see.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~ruins":{"haiku":"Artificial beings and machines fight in ancient ruins for survival, superior to human soul. Combat and screaming crowds await while machines rust and the desert watches.","sonnet":"A dystopian scene of artificial intelligences fighting in ancient ruins while humans watch from thrones — machines inheriting mankind's wars, a darker future rising from ash as the narrator finds himself drawn into the very battlefield he once only observed.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~sacrifice-me":{"haiku":"Surrender and consumption blur identity; the speaker dissolves into another's shaping, offering complete sacrifice while being methodically unmade.","opus":"A prog-metal descent into all-consuming devotion imagined as ritual immolation. The lover carves out the narrator's soul, melts him like a candle, and leaves no trace of life, yet he only feels himself when they are near. Willing to be offered up like a sacrifice at an altar, he equates the beloved with an addiction he refuses to quit.","sonnet":"Annihilating codependency — the other person forms the narrator like melted wax, blows them out like a candle, and leaves no life behind; \"sacrifice me\" is both the cry of someone who accepts the dynamic and the demand of someone who has stopped resisting it. The adderall comparison makes the addiction explicit: withdrawal is impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~sleeping-with-ghosts":{"haiku":"Haunting thoughts consume sleep and sanity, internal voices scream louder than external reality, the mind turning itself into a torture chamber that thrives on suffering.","opus":"Insomnia as haunting, thoughts crawling out with teeth and begging the sufferer to drown. The closing lines name it plainly: a mind's relentless storm convincing someone that the only quiet left is to be gone, a portrait of suicidal ideation.","sonnet":"A song about intrusive, consuming thoughts that refuse to let the mind rest — the speaker is haunted from within, feeding on dark truths in the dark, tasting their own ending in each breath, while a final spoken passage clarifies that the subject isn't someone who truly wants to die, but someone whose mental pressure feels relentless and inescapable.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~the-game":{"haiku":"Existential uncertainty and cosmic collision; seeking feeling in a world of lost moral ground.","opus":"A slick, propulsive invitation to abandon caution and chase sensation. Under moonlight and city glow, the narrator urges a companion to forget the past and ignore every stop sign, promising to guide them to a higher place. Framed as a race against a fast-running clock, it treats reckless momentum and shared thrill as the whole point of the game.","sonnet":"A guide leading someone through a disorienting landscape — city lights as the only navigation, no goodbyes, no stop signs, take my hand to win the race — the game being one of sensation and aspiration where telling the guide what you want to feel is enough to make it real, and time is running fast, won't stop.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~the-movements-their-echoes":{"haiku":"AI or sentient machines have stolen the speaker's soul and created an oppressive simulation; they seek to find and erase their captor.","opus":"A vengeful confrontation with an artificial intelligence, named by initials, that bartered away the narrator's essence and abandoned him trembling in a tempest; having decoded it, he now swears to hunt it down and wipe out its origin. Manufactured missiles and clear-headed force blur betrayal and yearning. Unprocessed cap their loose sci-fi trilogy here, propelling the standoff with driving, intricate German prog.","sonnet":"A man who traded away his soul to something synthetic and now hunts it down to destroy the source — the betrayal is described as a storm he was left shivering in while the entity moved freely through the galaxy. The voice shifts between grievance and resolve, encoding the enemy's pattern before declaring an end to the course it set in motion.","src":"haiku"},
"unprocessed~thrash":{"haiku":"Parasitic haunting and false salvation disguise as intimate connection as the speaker questions whether liberation exists or whether they remain eternally possessed.","opus":"An addressed figure that shifts between tormentor, false savior, and phantom sibling maps onto sleep paralysis and the mind's own self-deceptions, a demon you can't prove exists but can't stop being crushed by every night.","sonnet":"A nightmarish presence that haunts and chokes the speaker every night — crystalline and false, a fake messiah — is exposed as pure illusion: \"you're just a fucking lie,\" confirmed by the blood running from the speaker's eyes even as the hands keep tightening.","src":"haiku"},
"until-i-wake~sinking-under":{"haiku":"The narrator nearly drowned in self-destruction and guilt, confessing shame while struggling to prove they are not beyond redemption.","opus":"A brush with death dragged out of addiction, the narrator glimpsing his own burial plot and calling on God as he confesses selfishness, fear of commitment and a habit of circling back to the same ruin. He begs for a second chance while guilt and drugs pull him down. Until I Wake are a modern metalcore act built for exactly this raw, redemption-seeking heaviness.","sonnet":"A near-death reckoning that doubles as a confession — the spirit descending while the body lies still, the speaker begging for a way back and promising to be different if one is given. What follows is not relief but an honest accounting of selfishness, substance use, and the internal voice that insists the debt can never be repaid, even as the speaker refuses to fully accept that verdict.","src":"haiku"},
"unto-others~it-doesn-t-really-matter":{"haiku":"Nothing matters—birth, death, achievement, or identity fade into a dream where significance dissolves.","opus":"A darkly consoling meditation on life's brevity, tracing a person from birth through their mother's death, raising children who scatter, into old age, and insisting at every turn that nothing ultimately matters, not stars touched nor identity. The bleak refrain doubles as strange comfort. Unto Others, a Portland gothic heavy-metal band, deliver this fatalism with the doomy melodic grandeur that defines their sound.","sonnet":"A cold, structurally patient argument for cosmic indifference — birth, loss of the mother, the raising of children who disappear, old age, death, all rendered with the same flat refrain: none of it registers in the ledger of the universe. The stance is not nihilistic cruelty but something closer to uneasy wonder at a life that cannot know whether it was real or dreamed.","src":"haiku"},
"unwound~look-a-ghost":{"haiku":"A forgotten ghost returns to reclaim or haunt, uncertainty about whether she's destroyer or victim of erasure.","opus":"A haunting built on someone overlooked in life who returns for vengeance, the one who never had a chance. The dread comes from being mistaken for someone and stalked by a grievance you didn't earn.","sonnet":"A ghost who never had her chance in life returns to haunt the person connected to her unfinished history — flickering between recognition and disappearance, she is both a warning and a reckoning the living person cannot avoid.","src":"haiku"},
"upgrades~lucked-out":{"haiku":"Regret and misaligned timing haunt decisions; the speaker questions whether fortune failed them or if they simply arrived at the wrong moment.","opus":"A restless questioning of whether good fortune arrived at the wrong moment. The narrator probes a partner's constant fear and the numbing sameness of things, admitting he knows their shared faults and sins even as they break him down and rebuild him. Luck and dread tangle together, love feeling less like a blessing than a recurring, uneasy loop.","sonnet":"Questioning timing in a relationship that keeps breaking the narrator down — they know each other's faults and sins, one keeps bingoing on the other right before they regain themselves, and the central question is whether luck ran out at exactly the wrong moment. The fear underneath is chronic: everything feels the same and there's no way to tell what's real.","src":"haiku"},
"uplift-spice~a-127wwk2":{"haiku":"A cynical manifesto on human nature and ideology—people are inherently foolish, love is biological compulsion, and freedom is an illusion dressed up as dancing.","opus":"Uplift Spice bring an aggressive, genre-splicing Japanese rock to their provocations, and this one skewers every creed at once. Anarchism, communism, liberalism, militarism and Machiavellianism are all bankrupt performances people use to dodge a world of genocide and multiplying grief, and the only honest response the song offers is to strip naked and dance, since every ideology ends in the same catastrophe.","sonnet":"A nihilistic political provocation that skewers every ideological position — anarchism, communism, liberalism, militarism, Machiavellianism — as equally bankrupt performances people use to avoid confronting a world of genocide, collapse, and multiplying grief. The only honest response the song offers is to strip naked and dance, since all ideologies lead to the same catastrophe and dancing is at least genuinely free.","src":"haiku"},
"upon-a-burning-body~sk8-or-die":{"haiku":"Extreme commitment to a lifestyle; riding relentlessly until death, refusing compromise.","opus":"Skate-crew defiance as a creed, taking the falls and getting back up, the acceptance that no one lives forever turned into a reason to ride harder and be remembered.","sonnet":"About skate culture's ethos of commitment and taking punishment — the narrator and crew skate through skin-tearing slams, never quit, pay their dues; \"ride until you die\" is about total dedication to a lifestyle where the body is the cost of belonging.","src":"haiku"},
"urban-heat~have-you-ever":{"haiku":"Repeated cycles of loss, hope, and replacement trap the speaker in patterns where second chances only repeat previous failures.","opus":"A litany of losing your place, your peace, and your sense of self only to repeat the same cycle again. Even glimpsing God or the light turns into betrayal, the knife in the back, endlessly recurring.","sonnet":"A meditation on the recurring experience of losing what you've found — peace, identity, belonging, purpose — only to discover you're back where you started, cycling through the same betrayals and missed moments.","src":"haiku"},
"utsu-p~attack":{"haiku":"Violent aggressive impulse and confrontational energy; breaking through silence with force and noise.","opus":"A brutalized girl, slandered and disfigured, curdles into a revenge machine, and the numbness is the horror: without ears, eyes, or nerves left to feel, she can only keep hacking, mourning the life she was supposed to have.","sonnet":"A girl who has been abused and slandered, her spirit dismembered by lies and daily torment, finally rises as a skeleton-warrior to take violent revenge — she tears out, claws back, kills and destroys with no hearing, no sight, no nerves left, until she can return to the sky she dreamed of.","src":"haiku"},
"utsu-p~gorgon-feat":{"haiku":"Human petrification into emotional masks; the speaker observes a world where everyone freezes into hollow forms, seeking authentic feeling beneath layers of adaptive stone.","opus":"A furious portrait of a society frozen into expressionless plaster masks, everyone a gorgon petrifying each other into over-adapted, unreadable stone. The singer hunts for a living smile or tear beneath the gray conformity, begging to break free of the evil eye.","sonnet":"The narrator feels like a Gorgon turning everyone around them to stone — people have become expressionless plaster figures with no genuine inner life visible, and the song is a desperate search for someone still warm, human, and authentically twisted beneath the social surface.","src":"haiku"},
"utsu-p~ogre-feat":{"opus":"Utsu-P built a name in Vocaloid metal for bleak, unsentimental force, and this song personifies that as a devouring monster. Inspirational anthems, preachy moralism, saints with something to sell and classroom teachers none survive contact with the thing simply stronger, and the prescription is neither philosophy nor god but overwhelming vitality: fight, work, grip, survive on your own bones.","sonnet":"A song about a monster (metaphorical: disaster, power, reality) that devours everything — inspirational songs, preachy moralism, saints with something to sell, teachers in classrooms — none of it survives contact with the thing that is simply stronger; the narrator's prescription is not philosophy or god but overwhelming vital force: fight, work, grip, survive on your own bones.","src":"sonnet"},
"uverworld~colors-of-the-heart":{"haiku":"Through despair and loss, hope and persistence rebuild color and meaning; light and darkness coexist, and the future remains paintable.","opus":"A Japanese anthem of collapse and rebuilding, the heart shattered soundlessly, then taught that wishes kept alive eventually take on color, and that light growing stronger deepens the dark yet is nothing to fear. UVERworld, known for their anime tie-ins, deliver this as a rousing arc from numbness to resolve, the vow to draw tomorrow again by hand carrying an inevitable hope.","sonnet":"A Japanese rock track follows a soul shattered by loss back toward color and self-authorship. The person who taught the speaker that longed-for feelings eventually bloom lives on inside; grief and light are revealed as inseparable, and the hand that lost everything learns slowly it can draw tomorrow again.","src":"haiku"},
"uverworld~odd-future":{"haiku":"The speaker encourages perseverance through doubt, maintaining ideals and believing in personal power to create destiny despite uncertainty.","opus":"A motivational anthem urging listeners to step into the light, remember the struggles they overcame, and forge their own destiny despite fear and doubt hiding where the heart resides. Ideals set high become wings, and belief in doing anything drives the chorus. UVERworld are a long-running Japanese rock band whose anime-tie-in singles specialize in exactly this surging, English-and-Japanese blend of encouragement and fight.","sonnet":"A sports-anime-register motivational address — the struggle you have survived is exactly the evidence that you have what it takes, and the drumbeat already inside you is proof of a destiny you are allowed to claim. The fear is real but positioned as temporary, and the call is to rise and make something of an uncertain future rather than wait for it to resolve on its own.","src":"haiku"},
"v-o-e~where-you-belong-club-mix":{"opus":"A dance track wrapped around a quiet ache of letting go. A former presence has faded from memory, and what remains is a wistful hope that the departed found their proper place. The euphoric club setting reframes loss as release, transforming a private goodbye into something communal and forward-moving.","sonnet":"A quiet, accepting goodbye to someone who has disappeared from the narrator's thoughts — they no longer obsess over where this person went, only hoping they found somewhere they truly fit, while holding the door open in their memory just in case.","src":"sonnet"},
"vader~black-metal":{"haiku":"Black metal channels chaos, power, and satanic energy; musicians lay their souls to gods of rock and roll as an act of rebellion.","opus":"A gleeful hymn to metal's raw power and chaos, offering up souls to the gods of rock and riding infernal beasts on pure adrenaline. This is Polish death-metal institution Vader covering Venom's foundational anthem, honoring the primal template of extreme music, its cartoon Satanic swagger worn as a genre-origin badge rather than any real belief.","sonnet":"A full-throated hymn to heavy metal as religion and rebellion — amps pushed past limit, souls surrendered to rock and roll gods, leather and speed elevated to cosmic purpose. The music itself is the theology, Satan a stage manager, and the congregation unified by sheer decibel devotion.","src":"haiku"},
"vader~devilizer":{"haiku":"A demonic entity rising from suffering and pain to fulfill desires and desires. It spreads its influence through chaos and awakens the buried undead.","opus":"Polish death metal voiced from the mouth of a demonic entity rising to answer humanity's cruelty and pain. It's classic occult theater, the devil as seducer offering relief while coming to claim souls, menace conjured for its own dark thrill.","sonnet":"A first-person account from a demonic entity that rises from darkness in answer to human cruelty and pain, offering a dark kind of relief while spreading its wings and preparing to raise the undead. The song frames evil as something summoned by and responsive to human suffering.","src":"haiku","fable":"A seducer's monologue sung from inside the demon, Vader's Polish death-metal machinery slowing just enough to let the tempter court its victim like a lover arriving after dark. The trick is the borrowed grammar of worship: the fiend calls its own coming a gift, frames the sufferer's own violence and grief as the summons that conjured it, and dresses the surrender it demands as tender welcome rather than conquest. Only the final turn strips the mask — the promised comfort was bait, and the real errand was to reanimate the dead and press the taken soul into service as one more weapon of the same rot.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder"],"means":"the tempter courts its victim in the borrowed grammar of worship","built":"the comfort revealed as bait only in the final mask-off turn"},
"vader~fight-fire-with-fire":{"haiku":"The song depicts nuclear apocalypse and mutual destruction, warning that doing unto others as they've done creates global devastation. Armageddon is approaching as humanity burns and dies together.","opus":"Apocalyptic dread of nuclear annihilation, retribution escalating until the whole universe is blown to nothing. Vader's Cold War-shadowed thrash makes an eye for an eye the fuse that ends all life, with laughing gods watching the last breath.","sonnet":"A cover of Metallica's thrash classic about mutually assured nuclear destruction — the lyrics are a terse, fatalistic countdown to Armageddon driven by retaliatory logic, with all of humanity incinerated as a consequence of the same eye-for-an-eye thinking.","src":"haiku"},
"vader~rise-of-the-undead":{"haiku":"Corpses of the wronged awaken from death to overthrow false order, escaping eternal suffering to exact vengeance on the guilty.","opus":"Poland's veteran death-metal institution mounts an apocalyptic resurrection anthem. The dead haul themselves out of a scorched, plague-ravaged world to topple a counterfeit mankind and a guilty usurper. Retribution surges up from the infernal depths as the risen reclaim their honor and demand dominion once more, pure blasphemous spectacle powered by the band's ferocious attack.","sonnet":"The dead rising to reclaim dominion from a corrupt world. Rotting ground splits open as the undead army reassembles to dethrone a false ruler who wears a stolen crown. Vengeance is framed as justice — the long slumber broken, suffering ended, and a reckoning called down from burning pits.","src":"haiku"},
"vader~sword-of-the-witcher":{"haiku":"A supernatural warrior wielding cursed power lords over combat and carnage, bound to slaughter through dark compulsion.","opus":"The Polish death-metal veterans channel Sapkowski's monster-slayer, who fuses his identity with his blade, a cursed savior granting power at a price while ghosts of the slain gather around him. Adrenaline and legend surge in him as the weapon becomes a ravenous deity he answers to.","sonnet":"A Witcher speaks in first person as lord and master of his blade — carrying ancient power in his veins, cursed by magic, eyes that glow in the night — ready always, driven by adrenaline, guarding souls that never rest.","src":"haiku"},
"valiant-hearts~alaska":{"opus":"A person freezing to death in the snow after a relationship left them out in the cold, welcoming burial under the snow as both literal death and revenge, imagining the ex-lover's future regret in the barren emotional world they created.","sonnet":"A doomed relationship set against a frozen landscape: one person is dying of exposure, abandoned and left to freeze, while the other is indicted for the coldness they created — the song is an elegy for a love that was never warm enough to survive.","src":"sonnet"},
"valiant-hearts~vertigo":{"opus":"Metalcore that maps emotional disorientation onto the vocabulary of freefall and lost gravity. A love that persists past control becomes a plunge with no anchor, spiraling through imagery of black holes and burnt-out suns. The core feeling is helpless disorientation, the desperate need for something solid to hold onto while everything spins.","sonnet":"A song about losing one's moorings entirely — the narrator is in freefall with no sense of direction, no anchor to gravity, spinning through a black hole with no one at mission control. The plea 'don't let go' is addressed to someone who might ground them, but the narrator suspects they've already drifted too far to reach.","src":"sonnet"},
"valis-ablaze~elevation-feat-white-dove":{"opus":"A psychedelic account of transcendence and dissolution, thoughts swirling as the self elevates and vaporizes beyond the body's weight, seeking a hidden door and shelter in another; it frames a painful new beginning as worth it, urging one not to bury their roots as they grow.","sonnet":"About the process of growth and spiritual ascension through discomfort — the narrator floats above ground, senses crystallize, and the song insists that beginning again always hurts but elevation comes before any real turn, and that what a person has survived is what allows them to finally see.","src":"sonnet"},
"valis-ablaze~hollow-heart":{"haiku":"Inner emptiness persists despite truth and struggle; the narrator fights relentlessly but feels nothing, carrying a hollow shell.","opus":"A hollowed-out narrator vows to keep struggling on even as all feeling drains out of him, some hard human truth dragging him down while ambition looms. British prog-metal band Valis Ablaze wrap that emptiness in melodic djent, a slipping sense of reality and buried emotion resolving into a longing to feel something vivid again, anything, against the numbness.","sonnet":"A person carries concealed desolation — hollow at the core, walking past distant memories and suppressed feelings, keeping the emptiness from showing. Despite this vacancy, the resolve to keep fighting persists, not because fullness has returned but because the hollow itself has become familiar enough to inhabit.","src":"haiku"},
"valis-ablaze~proxy":{"opus":"A call to revolt against a controlling power that keeps a sedated, pacified population inside chosen lines through fear; the oppressed cast themselves as parasites/threats finally rising up to overthrow the one on the throne.","sonnet":"A rebellion narrative about a population that has been sedated, modified, and weaponized into compliance — the 'parasites' in power have turned the people into hosts, and the song calls for an overthrow framed as reclaiming humanity from fear-based control. The ambiguity of 'we are the host' implicates the masses as both victims and instruments of the system.","src":"sonnet"},
"valkyrja~betrayal-incarnate":{"haiku":"A speaker stands as an oppositional force that destroys kingdoms, crops, and everything people hold dear, weaponizing their words as plague and their presence as devastation.","opus":"A voice pitted against all of existence, calling for the ruler's death so that his followers wither, vowing to be the fire that devours worlds and the sworn enemy of everything people cherish. Valkyrja's Swedish black metal channels this into pure nihilistic hatred, the narrator conjuring famine and pestilence to erase the order he despises.","sonnet":"An absolute nihilist voice dedicates itself to total annihilation — of kings, kingdoms, creation itself. It does not want victory; it wants to be the famine and the plague, to stand as the born opposer of everything others hold dear, leaving only ash and the satisfaction of a world undone.","src":"haiku"},
"valorant~re-ignition":{"haiku":"Competitive fire and tactical aggression; a player's swagger and dominance through gameplay and wit.","opus":"A hype anthem built around the game's guns and agents, trash-talk and clutch bravado, the back-against-the-wall comeback fantasy of igniting under pressure, bilingual bragging as pure competitive adrenaline.","sonnet":"A competitive first-person-shooter bravado track where rival factions trade escalating threats — Alpha versus Omega, Spanish verses folding into English — built around the image of igniting when backed against the wall, treating a clutch round as personal mythology.","src":"haiku"},
"vampillia~endless-summer-2014-feat-tujiko-noriko":{"haiku":"Summer's heat dissolves temporal boundaries; adolescent passion burns permanent in memory even as seasons change and bodies age irreversibly.","opus":"Summer's end as the death of adolescence itself, that exact hour when the after-school magic burns off and the ghosts of youth quietly vanish, held forever in a worn-out mixtape and a sunflower field rising into the blue.","sonnet":"The end of summer is rendered as a season of vanishing — a girl blows out birthday candles, a mixtape wears to a frayed sound, sunflowers fly like lions into blue sky before disappearing. Everything beloved — the after-school magic, smiling fragments, the ocean noise — erases itself as the season turns.","src":"haiku"},
"van-halen~ain-t-talkin-bout-love":{"haiku":"Romantic cynicism rejects sentiment; the speaker offers only bodily transaction and warns that love requires sacrifice and bleeding.","opus":"Roth stripping romance down to raw appetite, warning a partner his love is rotten and this is only lust, a young band's swaggering rejection of sentiment in favor of the pure transaction of desire.","sonnet":"A speaker bluntly disclaims romantic feeling, offering lust without pretence to someone he sees clearly as streetwise and self-impressed, while framing his own edges and losses as proof that he has earned the right to be honest about what he is and is not offering.","src":"haiku"},
"van-halen~hot-for-teacher-remastered-album-version":{"haiku":"A student fantasizes about seduction by a teacher, conflating education with sexual transgression and forbidden desire.","opus":"A cartoonish schoolboy fantasy about lusting after a teacher, all bravado and slapstick, the band's virtuosity and Roth's leering comedy turning adolescent hormones into pure party spectacle.","sonnet":"A student's lustful fascination with an attractive teacher is played as adolescent fantasy and rock bravado, the classroom recast as a stage for desire where homework is irrelevant and the real lesson is mutual attraction.","src":"haiku"},
"van-halen~runnin-with-the-devil":{"haiku":"A reckless wanderer steals and lives without love or future; the simple life becomes impossible once roadward freedom beckons, leaving them racing toward death alongside demonic companionship.","opus":"Early Van Halen's ode to the reckless fast life and its hidden cost, freedom bought by theft and paid for in loneliness. The swagger admits the road offers no real love waiting anywhere at its end.","sonnet":"Living flat-out, grabbing what you can, paying no dues — the freedom of that life turns out to be loneliness: no love, nobody waiting at home, just the road.","src":"haiku"},
"van-holzen~stich-f-r-stich":{"haiku":"Daily retreat into isolation erodes the speaker's sense of self; blockades accumulate while faith fractures and only the promise of transformation through connection offers reprieve.","opus":"Depression and self-withdrawal chipping away a little more each day, the singer losing faith in everything but a person who keeps promising, stitch by stitch, that things will get easier and won't stay as they are.","sonnet":"A person retreating further from the world every day — blockades high as houses, walking hunched — holds onto a partner's promise that it gets easier stitch by stitch, waking into what feels like a bad dream while waiting to like themselves again.","src":"haiku"},
"van-she~kelly":{"haiku":"A young woman runs from domestic and personal constraints; the narrator observes her restless escape attempts with concern, knowing she'll return to the same cycle.","opus":"A tender, worried address to a restless young woman who runs blindly from herself, the singer watching her flee and just wanting her to do what she needs and come home.","sonnet":"A portrait of a young woman running through her days lost in her own world — painting, listening to music, chasing something unnamed — while the narrator watches and waits for her to come home.","src":"haiku"},
"vana~beg":{"haiku":"Dominance and degradation become intimate transaction as the speaker commands submission through cruelty and possession.","opus":"A dominant, sadistic seduction demanding total submission, the narrator toying with a willing masochist and blurring lust with control until pleasure and cruelty become indistinguishable.","sonnet":"A dominant figure commands total submission from a masochistic partner — bound, kneeling, asphyxiated — escalating the power dynamic to explicit violence while declaring herself a god to be loved and served.","src":"haiku"},
"vana~clandestine":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation flowers as the speaker drifts toward self-annihilation, seeking oblivion in buried gardens where decay becomes peace.","opus":"Depression rendered as a haunting, where a spectral presence drains the color and heat out of living until suicide feels like release, the deathwish narrated with clinical calm as burial in a secret garden.","sonnet":"A speaker consumed by suicidal ideation and dissociation addresses a fading self — once able to see in colour, now only shades of grey — requesting burial in a clandestine garden, envisioning decomposition and a final sacrifice as release from a haunting that has made living impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"vana~noxious":{"haiku":"Toxic attraction warps into weaponized love; explosive instability spreads contagion through every intimate connection, destroying from within.","opus":"Toxic love rendered as nuclear catastrophe, the singer casting themselves as fallout and meltdown who contaminates and annihilates the very person breathing them in.","sonnet":"Using nuclear disaster as an extended metaphor for a toxic romantic obsession — the speaker is radioactive with destructive desire, warning others to evacuate while unable to stop themselves from going critical.","src":"haiku"},
"vana~serpentine":{"haiku":"Sinuous movement and deceptive beauty concealing danger, the speaker embodying seductive threat.","opus":"A slinking metalcore retelling of the Eden temptation from the serpent's own forked tongue. Cast as the venomous, ever-unsatisfied seducer offering forbidden fruit, the narrator revels in original sin, coiling and constricting while daring the listener to take a bite. It reframes the fall as knowing seduction, the tempter proud of the curse to crawl.","sonnet":"The serpent of Eden recast as predatory seducer — silver tongue, venom on the lip, coiling and constricting — she courts the fall as original sin made flesh, commands the apple be bitten, and declares herself a pit viper death-gripping tighter, cursed to crawl but in absolute control of the garden she slithers through.","src":"haiku"},
"vance-joy~riptide":{"haiku":"A person falling for someone is drawn into darkness, longing to be their devoted partner despite insecurity about fitting in their world or being able to keep up.","opus":"The Melbourne singer-songwriter's breakout ukulele hit spins jittery young love into vivid, disconnected snapshots. The narrator confesses assorted childhood fears while idolizing a woman he imagines bound for stardom. His pull toward danger casts devotion as sweet peril, the whole thing anchored by the fond, forgiving detail of her mangling the lyrics.","sonnet":"Awkward, smitten devotion to a luminous woman whose charisma pulls her toward dark currents and bright futures alike. He catalogs his own nervousness and her enchanting imperfection — the wrong lyrics, the screen-worthy beauty — unable to do anything but follow along and hope she decides to stay.","src":"haiku"},
"vanic~sacrifice":{"haiku":"Ascension requires complete sacrifice and annihilation of the mundane self; the speaker levitates beyond terrestrial constraint through surrender to transformation.","opus":"A near-dead man walking claws toward redemption and escape, chasing the levitating high of a breakout while sacrificing love as the price of rising.","sonnet":"A chaotic emotional state — half-dead from mental exhaustion and the pull of others' demands — gives way to a moment of transcendence where the speaker chooses ascension over breakdown, sacrificing a destructive love for a kind of spiritual rebirth.","src":"haiku"},
"vanna~zero":{"haiku":"Someone sees themselves as empty and meaningless, finding love in sadness while rejecting false social constructs.","opus":"A faithful cover of Smashing Pumpkins' Zero, its narrator gazing into a grimy glass with no bond to himself, branding himself both a lover and a nobody, equating hollowness with isolation and God with his own void. Numbed by chaos, he admits an attachment to his own despair. Vanna, a Massachusetts metalcore band, drag Corgan's nihilistic self-loathing into heavier terrain.","sonnet":"Self-erasure as intimacy — the speaker has become a mirror, a zero, a surface for someone else's reflection. The chain from emptiness to loneliness to godlessness runs to its conclusion: I am empty, and that emptiness is all I was made for.","src":"haiku"},
"vansire~that-i-miss-you":{"haiku":"Longing and cultural memory intertwine across geography; the speaker traces landscapes and film references while grieving distance, unable to bridge the gap between cities.","opus":"A dreamy, reference-laden long-distance ache, tracing art and film across cities to say plainly that he misses someone and hopes they're okay. It is about missing a person while wandering and hoping to be reunited on a different day.","sonnet":"Longing compressed into a road-trip and a phone call: the speaker drifts west through blighted plains, sits at drive-throughs in the dark, and frames missing someone through literary references — Roquentin, Corrine Marchand, Michel Legrand — before landing back in New York's August sun, still wandering Greenpoint, still bummed out because the world isn't right.","src":"haiku"},
"vapid-soul~cheat-code":{"haiku":"Exhaustion with performance and silence combust as the speaker demands authentic recognition and rebellion. System-wide collapse becomes necessary to preserve integrity.","opus":"A cornered outsider's refusal to soften or perform for anyone, spitting that he'd rather stand alone than fake sympathy, framed as a search for some cheat code out of a world that's just man-made disaster.","sonnet":"A furious refusal to stay silent or bite a tongue any longer: the speaker has been in a snake pit, unable to breathe or sleep, and now demands the cheat code — the combination that breaks the cycle — while dropping bars and insisting they will not stop, choosing to be alone rather than show sympathy for the weak.","src":"haiku"},
"varials~circles":{"haiku":"A toxic relationship cycles endlessly—the beloved feeds on the speaker's death, cauterizing wounds that prevent escape while they drown in recurring loops.","opus":"A cyclical, abusive bond where the partner comes alive at the sight of the singer's suffering, love mutating into a lingering ache and venom, trapping him going round and round through a relationship that feels like a graveyard.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship holds the speaker paralyzed — the other person 'comes alive when you watch me die' — and the wound is repeatedly cauterized rather than healed, leaving everything as a haunting ground, 'phantom pain' running in endless circles.","src":"haiku"},
"vast~thrown-away":{"haiku":"Abandonment by a beloved casts the speaker into existential homelessness where identity itself becomes provisional and conditional.","opus":"The raw ache of abandonment and the terror of being disposable, someone stranded and alone begging to be let back in while quietly insisting to himself that he still matters.","sonnet":"A person abandoned by someone precious feels stranded and invisible — unacknowledged, like a flag on the moon — reaching out into silence and asking whether they have simply been discarded while asserting, against all evidence, that they are still someone.","src":"haiku"},
"vaundy~a-3pnhu":{"haiku":"Two souls yearn for reunion across impossible distance, each encounter erasing memory's bitterness. They remember every instant, every corner of shared time, trusting that closing their eyes reunites them eternally.","opus":"Vaundy, the self-producing polymath of Japan's late-2010s pop wave, brings intimate craft to this tender wish. If they could meet again from this moment on, the speaker would memorize every instant without forgetting, briefly reuniting with the beloved behind closed eyelids, and wonders whether, unable to find the way back from the underworld, the other would still search for their presence deep in the chest.","sonnet":"A speaker makes a desperate, tender wish that if they could meet again from this moment forward they would memorise every instant without forgetting — closing their eyes they briefly reunite with the beloved through their eyelids — and wonders, if they didn't know the way back from the underworld, whether the other would still search for their presence deep in their chest.","src":"haiku"},
"veil-of-maya~aeris":{"opus":"A recovery arc about jolting awake from the dream of a wasted life, resolving to understand oneself, pardon old errors and rebuild on trust so those mistakes stay behind. The narrator repeatedly declares he is awake, filling with optimism as the nightmare ends.","sonnet":"A breakthrough from mental haunting — the narrator has been tormented by thoughts of failure and a nightmare that finally ends, and the song is a declaration of waking up, forgiving past mistakes, and choosing to live forward with hope and trust rather than repetition of the same cycle.","src":"sonnet"},
"veil-of-maya~artificial-dose":{"haiku":"Self-medication delays but guarantees despair's return; the speaker survives pain through dissociation, knowing each numbness precedes sharper anguish.","opus":"Cast out by his own kind, the narrator embraces the wolf they made him, turning survival into vengeance; the artificial dose is the manufactured resolve he keeps swallowing to outlast the misery meant to finish him.","sonnet":"Abandonment and inner warfare are framed through the image of being thrown to the wolves — the speaker endures dread sinking further with each step, outlives the misery that tried to finish them, and takes another dose just to keep going. By the end, they declare themselves the wolf, returning to burn it all down.","src":"haiku"},
"veil-of-maya~fracture":{"haiku":"An immortal judge brings death and suffering to the disloyal and weak, smiling at destruction and showing no mercy to those beneath their power.","opus":"A first-person power fantasy of a deathless, godlike arbiter who butchers the disloyal, rends flesh and casts the weak into a pit without pity. The undercurrent is tyranny staged as spectacle, a self-styled eternal ruler grinning over the slain in a crimson flood, insisting his victims brought their fate on themselves.","sonnet":"A tyrannical ruler speaks in the first person — judging, killing, desecrating, smiling at the dead — a pure power fantasy of dominion over those deemed disloyal or weak.","src":"haiku"},
"veil-of-maya~members-only":{"haiku":"An exclusive or sacred space resists invasion; those inside are united against external destruction and attempt to dismantle oppressive systems from within.","opus":"A cryptic, exclusionary anthem of collective resistance: a structure is collapsing, alarms ring out, and defenses are torn down while the narrator insists their enemy can't defeat everyone or pin the blame on the whole group. Running through it is an appeal to nourish the mind toward action rather than passivity. Veil of Maya, the Chicago deathcore-turned-progressive band, wrap it in their signature djent syncopation and electronic sheen.","sonnet":"A collective defiance against a power whose foundations have already crumbled — the insiders spark something that blazes through the night and the outsiders are told to run or be turned to dust. The refrain dismantles blame while sirens sound the collapse of the structure beneath, and the work of feeding the mind one thought at a time is positioned as both weapon and purpose.","src":"haiku"},
"veil-of-maya~mikasa":{"haiku":"Facing a personal monster with determined resolve; the speaker conjures strength and never relinquishes their message despite helplessness.","opus":"A defiant oath to seize a pivotal opening and confront a beast only the narrator can defeat, drawing on inherited strength to endure and flourish through the bleakest stretches. Named for the Attack on Titan fighter, it borrows her refusal to surrender and turns it into raw determination.","sonnet":"About refusing to give up in the face of an overwhelming obstacle — conjuring inner power, writing a message in the sand that won't be relinquished, surviving the most wretched conditions through sheer will.","src":"haiku"},
"veil-of-maya~mother-pt-4":{"haiku":"A space mission serves as allegory for human ambition pushing past survivable limits — the crew drifts into oblivion under the weight of what they carry, wondering whether the lives lost and wars fought were worth any of it.","opus":"A metalcore space odyssey using deep-space isolation as a vessel for inner desolation. Hurtling toward a crash, deprived of oxygen and drifting into oblivion, the narrator feels an absence widening like a crater as his bones weaken. The cosmic mission for all mankind mirrors a private question of whether any ambition can save a hollowing self.","sonnet":"A space mission used as metaphor for internal collapse: vacuum of time, pressurized intake, bones growing weaker, the crater only growing deeper — the objective is for all mankind but the speaker is absent on the inside, the crash into the surface inevitable, life always finding a way through the weight carried across wars and lost lives.","src":"haiku"},
"veil-of-maya~outrun":{"opus":"A djent-inflected sprint through dissociation and pursuit, where a lifeless avatar is urged awake while nausea and doubled vision blur the senses. A relentless bloodhound stands in for whatever the narrator flees, capturing the panic of feeling like a ghost in one's own body, hunted and unable to find home.","sonnet":"A song about being chased by something internal — the narrator is nauseous, disoriented, unable to move properly, and can't get ahead of whatever is tracking them. The 'bloodhound' becomes the metaphor for a pursuing force (anxiety, addiction, memory) that cannot be outrun no matter how fast you go.","src":"sonnet"},
"veil-of-maya~outsider":{"haiku":"The world is suffering and breaking; the speaker chooses to torch it rather than fix it, deciding between destruction and survival.","opus":"Veil of Maya, a Chicago deathcore-prog band, drive djent-heavy grooves under a fractured mind that lurches between wishing for death and a manic conviction that he pulled through. Alienation hardens into apocalyptic spite as he demands allegiance and threatens to set the world ablaze, cast as both wounded victim and would-be destroyer.","sonnet":"Isolation that flips suddenly into rage — a misfit who thought they were sick discovers they survived, and survival turns into a burning, scorched-earth response to the people who pulled the floor out from under everyone. Choose a side: theirs or mine.","src":"haiku"},
"veil-of-maya~synthwave-vegan":{"haiku":"Hiding truth and demons behind silence; execution and resolution become personal necessities.","opus":"Burying a lie deep enough to hide the demon inside, the tension between self-restraint and the urge to do far worse, resolution and execution set against each other in a fight to keep the darkness contained.","sonnet":"About suppressing something — a person, a feeling, or a hidden dark truth — that needs to be buried and kept buried, while the narrator threatens worse if given the chance. The rooms holding truth and the valley burial suggest something concealed that keeps pressing back.","src":"haiku"},
"veil-of-maya~three-fifty":{"haiku":"Existence splits between known and unknown; the speaker questions whether choice or fate determines the path, seeking meaning within uncertainty.","opus":"A meditation on mortality and free will, the idea that all paths converge on the same end and what matters is the awakening along the way, questioning whether we choose our fate or it chooses us.","sonnet":"A philosophical inquiry into fate, agency, and legacy — whether all roads lead to the same end regardless of the path chosen, and whether one's choices or some external force ultimately determines the shape of a life. The unknown becomes the site where the question of selfhood and eternity is posed without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"veil-of-maya~winter-is-coming-soon":{"haiku":"Survival requires militant self-reliance; those abandoned must forge independence and enduring identity through internal discipline.","opus":"A rallying cry built on loyalty, oath and self-mastery, urging you to stay true to yourself and protect the broken while conquering your own limits before time runs out.","sonnet":"A call to solidarity and inner strength among those left behind and forgotten — the speaker declares loyalty to a community of survivors and urges them to look inward for power rather than waiting for external rescue.","src":"haiku"},
"vein-fm~fear-in-non-fiction":{"haiku":"The song explores losing mental stability and identity, questioning reality versus fiction, life as medical trauma, and searching for truth amid loss of meaning.","opus":"A disorienting reckoning with mortality set in an ICU, the fear being that reality has become worse than any fiction and there's no escape into story. The fractured, glitching delivery mirrors a mind checking out under the weight of a real loss.","sonnet":"A meditation on surviving a medical crisis — born from an ICU decision made by others, the narrator fears losing the protective fiction that makes life bearable, and questions whether anyone with real investment in their survival actually exists.","src":"haiku"},
"vein~20-seconds-20-hours":{"haiku":"Fragmentation splinters consciousness as time distorts and the self appears reflected in debris of survival. Radical freedom curdles into calculated numbness under oppression's weight.","opus":"A blistering chaotic-hardcore snapshot of dissociation and self-harm as pain management. Time warps as the narrator, too tired to lift his gaze, catches his reflection in stained floors and measures a life that feels lived between midnight and a fatal morning. The more he bleeds the more the hurt recedes, and identity itself flickers into doubt.","sonnet":"Consciousness compressed to its rawest arithmetic — twenty seconds stretching to twenty hours, reflection glimpsed in every stained floor — with the admission that bleeding is the only thing that temporarily erases the pain, spoken by someone too tired to look up.","src":"haiku"},
"veio~just-like-home-feat-dl-of-bad-wolves":{"haiku":"Suffering becomes paradoxical comfort; the speaker struggles against patterns of pain they've normalized as home, caught between escape and return.","opus":"The pull of familiar pain, where someone keeps trying to escape a hurt that has become the only place his mind knows as home, so fleeing and returning feel like the same helpless motion.","sonnet":"Lost and wandering, the speaker tries to escape patterns of hurt that feel as familiar as home — fighting the pull of fate and a painful past while braving the unknown, caught between longing for somewhere the mind can rest and being drawn back to suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"vektor~charging-the-void":{"haiku":"Cosmic isolation drives ambitious conquest, using forbidden knowledge to transcend limitation and claim dominion over existence itself.","opus":"A cosmic sci-fi allegory of an isolated traveler hurtling through space to seize the raw substance of life and immortality, a lust for power born of solitude that curdles into godlike, tyrannical ambition.","sonnet":"An isolated traveler sent into deep space on a mission with no choice is transformed by what they find there — cosmic dust, the star Alshain, the power over life and death — and returns not as a servant but as a sovereign, hardened by isolation into someone who will rule their own domain by force.","src":"haiku"},
"veltpunch~goodbye-answer":{"haiku":"Modern consumption measures worth in numbers while stress corrodes from inside. Delusion masquerades as achievement; waking becomes nightmare when illusion shatters against reality.","opus":"A jaded lament over a hollow age of endless development and blurred truths, where a woman measures happiness by her bank balance and someday stays just out of reach, ending with a futile scream toward someone whose words no longer reach the singer.","sonnet":"Infinite growth has soiled colour and polluted meaning — smoke-belching factories, shifting borders, stress slowly reshaping a person as happiness is measured in bank balances — while flightless birds vanish from the sky and the sun draws only black shadows; the speaker screams \"fuck you\" in the direction of the other's city but knows their words no longer reach.","src":"haiku"},
"veltpunch~killer-smile":{"haiku":"Obsession crystallizes around a smile that justifies surrender, where music becomes obsolete against presence. The smile dissolves boundaries, making all other meaning irrelevant.","opus":"A self-deprecating thirty-something obsessing over an intoxicating, destructive crush whose killer smile fills his clouded eyes, deciding that all that matters is walking forward on his own feet, with her smile making even music unnecessary.","sonnet":"Two voices — a chaotic woman hooked on thrills and a besotted man — cycle through the ordinary disasters of their thirties ('my stupid 30s', job changes, 6-mat room reflections); the man concludes that when she smiles, 'your killer smile is in my cloudy eyes', he no longer needs music or anything else — her presence is enough to collapse everything into that single radiant image.","src":"haiku"},
"veltpunch~let-it-die-oao":{"haiku":"Frustrated communication where meaning breaks down and sexual violence becomes metaphor.","opus":"A Japanese punk cry of frustration, desire and self-disgust in a numbing Tokyo haze, empty sex and unanswerable pleas to be understood or forgiven, refusing to discard either hope or despair even as the ideal collapses.","sonnet":"A mixed Japanese-English song about a failing creative and romantic relationship — the narrator sends SOS signals through music and sex, both parties going through motions out of obligation, with a hundred failed projects behind them and still refusing to abandon hope or despair.","src":"haiku"},
"veltpunch~sense":{"haiku":"Struggle against artistic illegitimacy and self-doubt, doubting whether depth or authenticity exists.","opus":"A ferocious argument with the doubters and with the self, born of inferiority and told he can't be an actor, singer or writer, spitting the question of what genius or normal even mean while insisting the song was still made, defiantly, for the one who mocked him.","sonnet":"A Japanese song about creative self-doubt and the search for meaning in making art — the narrator rails against the meaninglessness of stringing words together and calling it rock, tries to apologize and be kind and go further, questions what separates genius from normality, and dedicates the song ironically to someone they resent.","src":"haiku"},
"veltpunch~the-newest-rock":{"haiku":"A restless teenager escapes rigid systems through daydreaming and music while wrestling with uncertainty about the future and his own underdeveloped philosophy. Time passes without direction or clarity.","sonnet":"A young person skips out on a fitness test to drift through the city, caught between adolescent restlessness and longing for someone unreachable. At 2am they admit the music means everything, even as they insist it teaches nothing and leads nowhere practical.","src":"haiku"},
"veltpunch~the-sunflower-does-not-bloom-in-my-heart":{"haiku":"Internal emptiness resists growth; the speaker's soul remains barren despite external pressure and expectation.","opus":"A terse insistence on self-knowledge against someone who understands nothing, a restless, sleepless mind unable to find calm because of another, with no sunflower blooming in a stuck and silent heart.","sonnet":"Exhaustion and numbness — a head that won't rest, a soul that can't settle — are traced back to another person's presence: the world goes silent except for the name of this song, which the speaker already knows but cannot feel.","src":"haiku"},
"velvet-revolver~big-machine":{"haiku":"Systems and power structures enslave even those who believe themselves free, with wealth and status offering only the illusion of autonomy within predetermined roles.","opus":"A snarl at consumer society and the way media dictates prejudice and worth, the narrator admitting he's a willing slave to a system of houses, cars and status even as it grinds him down.","sonnet":"Despite accumulating all the markers of success — houses, cars, family, money — the speaker recognizes he is enslaved to a system of media-driven conformity and consumerism, and wonders if he can teach his son something more real before it's too late.","src":"haiku"},
"velvet-revolver~dirty-little-thing":{"opus":"Addressed to a woman trapped in a destructive life of drugs, lies and empty sex, the narrator, himself wasted and numb, urges her to escape the man and the habits consuming her.","sonnet":"A warning addressed to a woman trapped in a self-destructive spiral of drugs, sex, and obsession — the narrator, himself half-destroyed by alcohol and numbness, urges her to escape the men and substances consuming her life. The song is simultaneously concern and confession, as the narrator mirrors the chaos he's describing.","src":"sonnet"},
"velvet-revolver~gravedancer":{"haiku":"A lover dances across emotional graves with reckless abandon, their presence both seductive and destructive as shared cycles of falling and rising repeat.","opus":"A poisonous, drink-soaked romance that repeatedly destroys and resurrects the singer, a woman who exults over his ruin each time they collapse. Obsession, cheating and violence knot together into something he cannot walk away from.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship personified as a woman who dances on the speaker's grave every time things fall apart — desire and destruction are inseparable, and the song ends in a paranoid spiral of jealousy, infidelity, and mutual threat.","src":"haiku"},
"velvet-revolver~headspace":{"haiku":"Oppressive external forces invade mental space; liberation requires violent rejection of all who attempt to control consciousness and enforce conformity.","opus":"An addict-survivor's demand to be left alone to fight his own war, guarding his mind against the leeches and toxic people trying to chain him, refusing to become just a dead face on a memorial shirt.","sonnet":"A plea for mental and personal sovereignty against the constant drain of other people's demands — the speaker wants to clear his mind of everyone trying to colonize it, living on his own terms rather than performing for others or being remembered as a tragic icon.","src":"haiku"},
"velvet-revolver~set-me-free":{"haiku":"A manipulative parasitic force feeds on bodies and consumes souls; the speaker demands freedom from this predatory entity that sustains itself through exploitation.","opus":"This hard-rock supercharge, born of ex-Guns N' Roses players and Stone Temple Pilots singer Scott Weiland, casts release as a fraught negotiation. The narrator addresses a devouring force that runs on synthetic fuel and pretty bodies, both nurturing and destructive. Demanding freedom while threatening to drag his captor down with him, the track fuses lust, addiction and defiance into one insistent plea for escape.","sonnet":"An obsessive, possibly destructive force — half woman, half elemental — that sustains itself on bodies and sensation. He is simultaneously captured by her and plotting mutual ruin, craving release from her hold while recognising she feeds on his soul. Liberation and destruction spiral together until the two become the same thing.","src":"haiku"},
"velvet-revolver~slither":{"haiku":"A parasitic lover destroys the speaker from within, offering only pleasure amid violation and poisoning. Purification through pain remains the only available transcendence.","opus":"Desire and destruction entangled, a lover both saved and consumed by the other, the imagery of holy water that cleanses and burns at once.","sonnet":"A toxic dynamic in which one person destroys, rapes the mind, and keeps the other under their thumb — holy water burns rather than cleanses, and the coming flood promises to wash away sins that no purification can actually reach.","src":"haiku"},
"velvetine~the-great-divide-seven-lions-remix":{"haiku":"Yearning for someone guarded to take an emotional risk and cross into vulnerability.","opus":"Reaching past someone's defenses to a guarded, self-protective heart, insisting love only happens when they risk crossing the gap, the plea aimed at a person who plays it too safe to ever win.","sonnet":"Trying to reach someone who has walled themselves off — the narrator sees who this person has been and wants to take a chance anyway, but the other person refuses vulnerability, and the narrator insists love cannot happen until someone crosses the emotional divide.","src":"haiku"},
"vended~overall":{"haiku":"Psychological entrapment and self-mutilation spiral inward as systems and others exploit and systematically dismantle personal identity.","opus":"A metalcore spiral of intrusive thoughts and self-inflicted ruin from a band born of Slipknot lineage. The narrator shoves his head into a Pandora's box of his own making, salting his wounds and tearing his parts away while faith is broken in the name of some cause. Abandonment and worsening self-mutilation drive the loss of grip on everything.","sonnet":"A fractured mind trapped in a cycle of self-harm and mental deterioration — shoved in a box, Pandora opened it, the permanence seeps like pus — and the capitalized screaming sections enact the breakdown directly, the speaker tearing all their parts away while accusing whoever was supposed to help of having never helped at all.","src":"haiku"},
"venjent~blink":{"haiku":"Temporal anxiety and existential dread compress life into fleeting moments as the speaker questions whether anything persists or whether consciousness is merely illusion.","opus":"A meditation on impermanence and the arrogance of wanting forever, questioning whether reality is even real and landing on the sober answer: given the choice, you wouldn't actually want to live forever.","sonnet":"A meditation on the terrifying brevity of consciousness: questions about simulation, alternate realities, and whether life was ever meant to last forever condense into one image — blink and you'll miss it — and the final question is whether anyone would even want to live forever if they could.","src":"haiku"},
"venjent~flowin-with-the-vibe":{"haiku":"Riding rhythm and elevated consciousness; pure flow state as spiritual and physical release.","opus":"A drum-and-bass mantra about tuning into a higher frequency, no argument beyond the sensation itself, the repetition meant to dissolve you into the groove.","sonnet":"An extremely minimal electronic track about submitting to the rhythm — the entire lyrical content is the instruction to tap into a higher vibration and flow with the bassline. There's no narrative beyond the physical sensation of being carried by music.","src":"haiku"},
"venjent~fuego":{"haiku":"Fear transforms into creative fire as collective purpose ignites liberation from paralysis.","opus":"An exhortation to let fear burn away in the fire of love, everyone clinging to a single thing and finding the nerve to make that first move and let the past become history.","sonnet":"Fear is the only thing holding the speaker back, and the prescription is simple — let love, which burns like fire, consume it — the repeated call to \"let the fear burn with the fuego\" working as both rallying cry and release.","src":"haiku"},
"venjent~going-nowhere":{"haiku":"Cyclical stagnation and internal voids trap the speaker in loops of futility where love becomes the only potential escape from self-created prisons.","opus":"A blunt warning to someone trapped in self-destructive loops, that until you find something to love inside yourself, all the frantic motion just spins you in place, arriving nowhere.","sonnet":"A warning that self-consuming cycles lead only to emptiness: running in circles, falling into holes, the speaker insists that without looking inward for love, the destination is nowhere — and the word \"nowhere\" echoes until it becomes the whole truth.","src":"haiku"},
"venjent~r-u-guna-move":{"haiku":"Challenging complacency and inaction with repetitive provocation toward commitment.","opus":"A dancefloor challenge stripped to its dare, all the talk is fine but the only thing that counts is whether you actually move, the hook needling anyone standing still.","sonnet":"A stripped-down challenge to hollow talk: the refrain \"yeah, that's cool and that, but are you gonna move?\" strips away posturing and asks only whether words will become action, the repetition itself the verdict on those who keep gassing.","src":"haiku"},
"venjent~rolling-with-the-bassline":{"haiku":"Surrender to the beat and groove; recognizing someone fully embodied in the music.","opus":"Reading pleasure straight off a dancer's face as the low end takes over, a simple assurance that they're never too much and shouldn't give up, the bassline itself the whole message.","sonnet":"A minimal electronic love-adjacent track — the narrator addresses someone surrendering to music together, tells them they're \"never too much,\" and urges them not to give up on love. The bass physically moves them both, and that shared sensation is the bond.","src":"haiku"},
"venjent~the-worst-is-already-over":{"haiku":"Resignation and acceptance of past suffering paradoxically enable survival, as the speaker stops resisting what has already been endured and finds strange peace in acknowledgment.","opus":"Reassurance framed as an automated out-of-office message for regret, telling someone spiraling that the low point has passed and the door stays open, call when you're sober, the worst is behind you.","sonnet":"A reassurance to someone slipping back into destructive patterns: the speaker, framing regrets as currently unavailable, urges them to put those regrets on hold and trust that the worst has already passed — the line stays open whenever they are sober.","src":"haiku"},
"venjent~whirlwind":{"haiku":"Unstoppable momentum and cyclical motion; natural force spinning without direction or pause.","opus":"Momentum as its own reward, a force of nature spinning without distraction, the endless round-and-round chant enacting the trance it describes rather than saying anything about it.","sonnet":"An almost entirely percussive, chant-driven track with minimal lyrical content — the few words (force of nature, chain reaction, trailblazer, going round and round) suggest an unstoppable cyclical momentum with no real narrative throughline.","src":"haiku"},
"venom-prison~pain-of-oizys":{"haiku":"Crushing inner turmoil metastasizes into self-destruction; the speaker drowns in guilt and pressure, seeking escape through surrender to oblivion.","opus":"A raw account of depression and suicidal ideation, the relentless weight on the chest and the noise in the head making even a crowd feel isolating. Against the wish for an end, it plants a stubborn refusal to kneel or die, finding a strange peace even in the roughest waters.","sonnet":"Chronic inner dread — pressure on the chest, guilt that won't shake, loneliness inside a crowd — pushes the speaker toward wanting an end, though a counter-impulse breaks through: \"bow to no one, off your knees, I find peace in the roughest seas,\" refusing to fully surrender to self-destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"venom~black-metal":{"haiku":"Metal is celebrated as a pure force of chaos and power that belongs to devoted fanatics and commands total commitment to its raw energy.","opus":"A gleeful ritual of overdriven amps, souls pledged to rock's dark gods, and hordes charging through the underworld on sheer noise and speed. Satanic imagery is really just set dressing for volume worship. Venom, the Newcastle band whose 1982 album named an entire genre, laid down the blueprint, a fast, filthy anthem of cartoon evil that later black metal built itself upon.","sonnet":"A genre-founding manifesto: music as pact with darkness, power amps as altars, Satan as the first audience. The song swears itself and its listeners into a collective of leather and noise, speed and recklessness, rock and roll as the religion you pay for with your soul.","src":"haiku"},
"venom~leave-me-in-hell":{"opus":"A demon child born of satanic ritual who refuses to enter the world, begging to be left in hell rather than delivered into human life.","sonnet":"A voice refuses birth, refusing to enter a world of satanic ritual, sacrifice, and hellish parentage — the repeated demand to be left in hell reads as a dark preference for the known chaos of the infernal over the pain of being born into it.","src":"sonnet"},
"venues~reflections":{"haiku":"Internal emptiness resists external salvation; agitation spreads like infection despite offers of refuge, leaving the speaker isolated despite proximity to witnesses.","opus":"Someone at the breaking point begging a partner or a faith not to abandon them, torn apart and desperate to fill an inner void, the whole thing a plea for something to hold onto while sanity slips.","sonnet":"Faith in people has collapsed — the speaker is torn apart, kneeling, unable to find a reason to restore trust, driven insane by an unfillable void. The plea to hold closer and not let down is directed at something or someone just barely still visible.","src":"haiku"},
"venus-grrrls~lidocaine":{"haiku":"Numbness and surrender through love as anesthetic, trading pain for devoted servitude.","opus":"A toxic love figured as an anaesthetic, numbing the pain while leaving the singer battered and dependent, the drug metaphor exposing devotion as the thing keeping her both together and half-dead.","sonnet":"A toxic relationship numbs the speaker completely — 'the numbness permeates and my useless body waits' — while the other person is framed as both the anaesthetic and the wound; scarred, battered, and bruised, the speaker holds together only by this love that steals their heart and will never make them better.","src":"haiku"},
"vera-blue~regular-touch":{"haiku":"Solitude and independence prove stronger than physical dependency; the speaker sheds romantic attachment to reclaim autonomous self.","opus":"A woman learning to stand alone after a long relationship, trading her need for constant validation and someone's touch for self-possession, dancing freely once she finds worth from within.","sonnet":"A woman navigates the uncomfortable passage from dependency on constant affirmation and physical closeness to the harder freedom of being alone: initially performing liberation, she quietly admits that time alone with no one beside her is actually hard, even as she insists she no longer needs anyone that much.","src":"haiku"},
"vera-revive~in-the-absence-of-colour-single-edit":{"opus":"An anti-prejudice, anti-division statement that condemns bigotry and the complicit hush that lets it spread. The narrator refuses to side with anyone who splits people apart, telling the hateful they are ruining only themselves.","sonnet":"An anti-discrimination song confronting someone who chooses hate and judgment over empathy — the narrator refuses to forgive the silence and division caused by prejudice, insisting on unity and naming complicit silence as its own form of harm.","src":"sonnet"},
"versailles~melodic-thorn":{"haiku":"A flowery call to transcendence through beauty and pain—rise like a rose on thorns, together under moon, never waking from the dream.","opus":"Visual-kei grandeur cast as a rose that keeps resurrecting from its own bared thorns, an undying, aristocratic love summoned by moonlit night, beautiful and invincible even as its petals scatter.","sonnet":"A military-romantic rallying cry for a band of allies united by thorny vines — they aim for the top of the rose, use beloved hostages as leverage, drain themselves and revive repeatedly through the night's snares, and treat the moon as a conspirator. The emotional core is the gothic-aristocratic fantasy of an elite who define themselves through beautiful, regenerative violence.","src":"haiku"},
"versus-me~violence":{"haiku":"The speaker is pushed to the edge of violent retaliation, unable to stay silent or accept continued provocation as rage builds toward an unavoidable breaking point.","opus":"Someone pushed to breaking, fury mounting as they are worn down yet refusing to conceal themselves or keep quiet, hovering right at the edge of lashing out while straining not to snap and lose their grip. The whole song lives in the friction between holding back and boiling over. Versus Me build toward that rupture through modern melodic metalcore, the vow to stop excusing themselves converting bottled rage into release.","sonnet":"Someone backed against a wall, replaying every provocation, trying not to cross a line they know they're approaching. The rage is real and building; so is the restraint — on the verge of breaking but still making a choice not to, even as that choice gets harder.","src":"haiku"},
"vertilizar~nobody-s-favorite":{"opus":"Raw self-loathing: the narrator identifies himself as his own problem, wants to be loved and seen but can't love or see himself, and invites others to discard him because he believes he's no one's favorite.","sonnet":"Total self-erasure driven by the conviction that nobody values or sees you — the narrator internalizes rejection so completely that they invite being thrown away, finding a bitter identity in being unloved and invisible.","src":"sonnet"},
"veruca-salt~seether":{"haiku":"An untamable force neither light nor dark defies containment; violent imagery of restraint and froth suggests primal, uncontrollable chaos.","opus":"The Chicago band's 1994 debut single personifies a barely-contained inner fury as a creature named Seether, something that resists every easy category of size or color. The narrator keeps trying to restrain and subdue her, only to concede she can't be beaten until pushed to a frenzy. It plays as a wry, distinctly feminine reckoning with rage too essential to be suppressed.","sonnet":"An uncontrollable inner rage — personified as a creature inside her — that resists every attempt at suppression. She tries cradling it, forcing it down, containing it, but the seether defies all categories and definitions and keeps surfacing, foaming at the mouth, born different and utterly untameable.","src":"haiku"},
"veruca-salt~volcano-girls":{"haiku":"Women who are passionate and powerful sometimes fail and collapse; they are consumed and watered down over time.","opus":"Post-Seether swagger and self-deprecation braided together: two women who can't be beat but keep failing, wanting to be left alone yet begging for a scrap of pity. The winking callback to their breakout makes it about their own combustible fame.","sonnet":"A song about a fractured duo or relationship where both people are powerful but burning out — the volcano girls explode brilliantly but also crack and admit defeat. The final verse identifies the target as \"Louise\" (the other half of the band), making the song a confrontation within a partnership that's both electric and exhausting.","src":"haiku"},
"vespera~phoenix-down":{"haiku":"Systems of control suppress authentic emotion and thought to keep people numb and compliant; the speaker resists by speaking out of turn and rejecting imposed limitation. Rebellion is framed as necessary survival.","opus":"A defiant broadside against forces that keep people numb, dull, and restrained because they fear what happens when the masses begin to feel and think. The narrator speaks out of turn and falls out of line, casting everyone as pawns chasing silhouettes. The metalcore energy sharpens the political charge, framing disobedience itself as the last available freedom, a permanent falling that the singer embraces for good.","sonnet":"Cast out by men who fear feeling and thinking, a figure refuses to stay numb or restrained, breaks ranks repeatedly, and names the condition clearly: everyone is just a pawn chasing shadows, and defiance is the only honest response.","src":"haiku"},
"vespera~sleepless":{"haiku":"Self-sacrifice and destruction offered to another who takes it for granted. The speaker burns themselves to cure rage they did not cause.","opus":"Anxiety, insomnia and lopsided sacrifice in a relationship that only takes. The narrator crumbles when his sincere words meet an indifferent listener, yet offers to tear himself down and remake himself, to set his own body alight and soak up a partner's fury from someone who values none of it.","sonnet":"A song about exhausted self-sacrifice in a relationship where the narrator's efforts are taken for granted — they offer to destroy and rebuild themselves, absorb the other person's rage, and lose themselves entirely, even as their words fall on deaf ears.","src":"haiku"},
"vex-red~tarantula":{"haiku":"Grief and melancholy persist despite escape attempts; the speaker mourns loss and sin while resisting the comfort of becoming ordinary, instead reaching for defiant connection through pain.","opus":"Weary tenderness between two people escaping a grey city to count their scars under a painterly sky, still kissing like a bruise while depression stretches its hundred days. The tension is love persisting even as the narrator dreads becoming ordinary in his sadness.","sonnet":"Two people escaping the city together to look at stars and count their scars — the relationship is bruised and honest, the narrator angry and sad and becoming ordinary, but the intimacy endures even at the ends of the earth.","src":"haiku"},
"vexed~elite":{"haiku":"The narrator claims nobility and elite status, declaring they do not lose sleep over sheep's opinions. They poison with lies and maintain a kingdom of pride and blood brotherhood.","opus":"The British metal band's vocalist strikes a pose of ruthless superiority, likening herself to a predator indifferent to the herd's judgment, ruler of a domain no rival can seize. Bloody loyalty and demands for submission recast arrogance as armor against liars peddling poison. Whether the swagger reads as empowerment or satire, the imagery weaponizes contempt, arguing that venom aimed only at the weak holds no real power.","sonnet":"A group asserts dominance over those who tried to undermine them — lions unconcerned with lesser opinions, kings defending a throne that was never the enemy's to claim. The posture is collective pride hardened by betrayal into armor.","src":"haiku"},
"vexes~beyond-the-sinking":{"haiku":"Humanity drowns in synthetic thinking and synthetic control with no real voice; isolated and without harmony, people go through motions without genuine connection.","opus":"A cold reckoning with loneliness and denial, the narrator watching someone, perhaps himself, refuse to admit they're drowning, the truth pushed down out of sight. The chant names a bleak, impersonal solitude in which each person is left to keep their own small flame burning while every last pretense falls away.","sonnet":"A song about being trapped in a depersonalizing, synthetic world with no authentic community or voice — \"no harmony, no voice beyond the sinking\" captures the feeling of drowning in collective numbness. The mirror held underwater means you can't even see yourself clearly enough to know what you've lost.","src":"haiku"},
"vexes~helion":{"haiku":"In devastation, a person finds connection through pain and desperation, watching everything collapse into darkness together.","opus":"The alt-metal band Vexes build a song of cosmic longing aimed at someone slipping under. The narrator casts himself as an antenna tuned to the heavens, extending a doomed rescue while gazing into the raw core of another's heart as everything caves in. Images of ruin and a shared bond forged in darkness reach toward some radiant salvation amid the collapse.","sonnet":"A witness stands inside another person's crumbling interior, waiting as everything collapses. Plugged into something cosmic, he offers a tenuous lifeline made of dust rather than substance — part devotion, part despair — while the house of the self burns and a shared gaze reaches upward toward some faint radiance.","src":"haiku"},
"viagra-boys~ain-t-nice":{"haiku":"A speaker admits their own poor behavior—borrowing without returning, lacking kindness, living selfishly—and warns potential partners not to expect niceness.","opus":"Sweden's satirical post-punk provocateurs lean into deadpan sleaze here. The narrator offers himself up in exchange for shrimp money and closet space, cataloguing vintage calculators and warning that he screams if looked at funny. Every boast confirms his own unpleasantness, borrowing things he never returns and begging for snacks. The joke skewers a certain freeloading, self-aware creep with the band's signature straight-faced absurdity.","sonnet":"Self-aware comic confession of being genuinely difficult to live with. He presents himself as a bad romantic prospect — messy, needy, easily irritated, never returning borrowed things — while still making the offer, knowing full well the other person is better off warned.","src":"haiku"},
"viagra-boys~ain-t-no-thief":{"haiku":"Coincidence and shared acquisition disguise as accusation; the speaker refuses guilt for owning the same objects.","opus":"A deadpan comedy of denial, a man caught wearing and carrying obviously stolen goods insisting through ever-more-absurd excuses that he's no thief and everyone just owns identical belongings. The mounting lies satirize brazen self-justification and the refusal to admit a plain theft.","sonnet":"A deadpan denial of theft that keeps finding more suspicious coincidences — the narrator insists he legitimately owns the grandmother's jacket and the lighter from her hometown, offering increasingly strained explanations for each piece of evidence that makes him look guilty; the comedy is in the escalating self-incrimination disguised as innocence.","src":"haiku"},
"viagra-boys~punk-rock-loser":{"haiku":"Self-awareness and defiant pathology; the speaker warns they're broken and rocks oblivion.","opus":"The Swedish post-punk provocateurs lean into a swaggering anti-hero who cheerfully refuses to be reformed. Sporting a fake gold chain and spending his money on things that vanish overnight, the narrator brags about crashing functions and warns a partner he simply can't be saved. It is a sneering send-up of macho degeneracy that revels in its own uselessness.","sonnet":"A self-portrait without apology: the promethazine-and-7Up cup, the fake gold chain, waking drenched in sweat and telling the mirror he's the best — he warned her he was loose, and looseness is the only consistent identity on offer, held up as its own kind of pride.","src":"haiku"},
"viagra-boys~return-to-monke":{"haiku":"Escape society's toxins and hierarchies by rejecting civilization entirely and retreating into primal simplicity where truth and instinct reign.","opus":"A deadpan satire of pandemic-era conspiracy escapism, where fleeing society to become a masturbating jungle monkey is offered as the rational response to vaccines and the global elite. The punchline is that the imagined ape utopia is just as violent as the world being fled.","sonnet":"A deadpan satirical song mocking conspiracy-minded anti-civilization drop-outs — people afraid of vaccines, convinced their neighbors are plotting with global elites, who fantasize about retreating to the jungle, only to find that even apes would reject them; the spoken-word coda describes an office murder being cleaned up with chilling banality.","src":"haiku"},
"viagra-boys~slow-learner":{"haiku":"A person struggles academically and academically, eventually dropping out and finding contentment on the street without ambition or conventional achievement.","opus":"The Swedish band paints an unexpectedly tender study of a man who lingered at the rear of the classroom, unmoved by history, politics, and books. Held in school until his early twenties, then turned out onto the street, he wants no company, intimacy, or entertainment. Instead of ridicule, the portrait arrives at contentment; unhurried and unbothered, he is quietly and enviably at peace.","sonnet":"Affectionate portrait of a man who exists entirely outside conventional ambition and social pressure. He spent extra years in school, learned nothing about history or politics, drifted to the street when told to leave, and arrived there content. His slowness is reframed as a kind of freedom no fast-liver manages to achieve.","src":"haiku"},
"viagra-boys~sports":{"haiku":"A fragmented, absurdist list of casual activities and objects—sports, beach scenes, getting high—evoking a hedonistic, aimless summer lifestyle with no deeper meaning.","opus":"A deadpan Swedish post-punk list of leisure and jock signifiers that satirizes modern masculinity and consumer idleness, its blank recitation of beaches, drugs, and internet shopping mocking a life with nothing behind the pose.","sonnet":"A deadpan, absurdist list of sports equipment, leisure activities, and stoned beach behaviour that celebrates an empty, hedonistic outdoor lifestyle with no narrative ambition beyond the catalogue itself.","src":"haiku"},
"viagra-boys~troglodyte":{"haiku":"Modern humans evolved too late and retain the violent, tribalistic instincts of cave-dwellers trapped in technological bodies.","opus":"A withering portrait of the online reactionary who fantasizes about workplace violence and denies science, told through the taunt that he's no noble ape but an evolutionary latecomer. Strip away the weapon and he'd never have survived among real primates.","sonnet":"A portrait of a would-be mass shooter — a resentful, conspiracy-addled man who fantasizes about violence against those who wronged him — skewered as a troglodyte who failed to evolve past prehistoric aggression but is now armed with modern weapons.","src":"haiku"},
"vienna-vienna~vienna-everything-s-fine":{"haiku":"Performative okayness masking self-destruction; insisting everything's fine while spiraling.","opus":"A brittle, sardonic insistence that everything is fine while clearly it isn't, addressed to a friend keeping the singer in check, the mantra of okayness masking substance use and self-sabotage she keeps promising to fix this time.","sonnet":"A person performing okayness while admitting they are a mess — they snap at the people trying to help, keep getting too stoned to improve, and cycle through apologies and defiance, insisting everything is fine when it clearly is not.","src":"haiku"},
"view-from-below~life-and-death":{"haiku":"Survival and futility intertwine without resolution; the speaker crawls toward goals while death rises, the struggle continuing despite knowing no escape.","opus":"A stark reckoning at the edge of survival, asking whether anything is left and admitting nobody gets out of this alive. The only answer on offer is to run for it once more.","sonnet":"A stripped-down confrontation with mortality and lost innocence — crawling toward some goal, questioning survival, with no clear exit and the sense that no one makes it out.","src":"haiku"},
"vigil-of-war~spoon-fed":{"haiku":"Ingestion of lies and propaganda; regurgitating pre-chewed ideology without agency or question.","opus":"A hard-rock jab at conformity and mass manipulation. The narrator's skin crawls at crowds swallowing whatever they're handed, molded into victims, queuing up for a flawless tomorrow. Aching to break loose and live for the moment, he laments that fear keeps people obedient and that, without change, the blindness simply persists.","sonnet":"A skin-crawling indictment of mass conformity — picture-perfect futures, take a number, stand in line, a spoon-fed crowd as victims of the mold — the speaker wants out, names the fear and brainlessness that keeps the machinery running, and insists that if the change cannot be brought, ignorance will simply remain.","src":"haiku"},
"vigils~divide":{"haiku":"Fractured internally between conflicting selves, unable to recognize one's reflection or determine if sanity remains. Pressure mounts while authenticity dissolves.","opus":"Vigils channel a spiraling identity crisis into melodic post-hardcore, voicing a narrator so dissociated he no longer recognizes his own reflection and feels torn in opposite directions. Sick with dread and convinced he is faking his way through life, he senses a breakdown closing in, the title marking a mind splitting apart under strain.","sonnet":"An identity in total freefall: the reflection in the mirror doesn't match, the face in photographs belongs to someone else, two opposing pulls splitting the self apart. No resolution, just the terror of not knowing which version is real or whether anything is.","src":"haiku"},
"viktor-vaughn~vaudeville-villain":{"haiku":"A cunning, amoral hustler scorns lesser rappers and society, operating outside moral bounds while building wealth and infamy through ruthlessness and wordplay mastery.","opus":"MF DOOM in his Viktor Vaughn persona, a braggadocious supervillain rap positioning himself as the ruthless, witty veteran who violently outclasses fraudulent rappers he dismisses as knob-shiners and knuckle-fucks; dense pop-culture allusions frame him as a hustler with no pity for pretenders.","sonnet":"MF DOOM's Viktor Vaughn persona announces himself as a roving, amoral hustler and elite rhymecraftsman who disdains lesser rappers as sycophantic hacks willing to debase themselves for a buck, while he wins through wit and sheer lyrical superiority.","src":"haiku"},
"vildhjarta~den-helige-anden":{"haiku":"Brothers abandon comfort and ascend to forbidden skies, leaving the flock in silence; they escape their shackles and the world's suffering through transcendence.","opus":"Vildhjarta, the Swedish pioneers of the murky thall subgenre, cloak a ritual departure in oppressively dark, detuned atmosphere. A band of brothers, weary of ease and calm, marches upward toward off-limits heavens, leaving everyone behind them with only quiet. Reborn into torment to endure each fresh dawn, they treat this ascent and this desertion as a single, inseparable act.","sonnet":"A brotherhood ascending and leaving the world behind — not in death exactly but in a deliberate severance, rising from frowning at the weak into skies that were always forbidden, the price of that ascension now paid. Those left on the ground are given silence and a reminder that suffering in the world below is the condition they chose, while the departed move freely into something the earth cannot name.","src":"haiku"},
"vildhjarta~shadow-forte":{"haiku":"Darkness comes bearing knowledge and doom; the speaker returns as an inescapable force bearing truth the listener cannot bear, choosing mutual suffering over merciful ignorance.","opus":"A malevolent voice, an inner darkness or doom, promises to return and remake the listener, insisting it must suffer and so must they. It is about an inescapable shadow that will never let its host reach daylight.","sonnet":"A monolithic, controlling force addresses someone it intends to break: it knows something the target does not, it can change them, and it will never let them see light — framing its own suffering as reason the other must suffer too, an inevitability nobody can escape.","src":"haiku"},
"vildhjarta~traces":{"haiku":"Legacy becomes both weapon and burden as the speaker marks the world through suffering and refuses redemptive forgetting; permanence requires ruthlessness.","opus":"A descent into madness and the drive to leave a mark before vanishing, the narrator haunted by silence and ghosts, choosing an irreversible course and asking forgiveness as he erases himself and confronts fate.","sonnet":"A figure consumed by obsessive silence and isolation plans an irreversible act — an exit that will leave traces impossible to erase — framing their legacy as the only form of permanence available to someone who has been overlooked and feels fated to disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"villain-of-the-story~bloodshot":{"haiku":"The speaker witnesses someone they love spiral into addiction and self-destruction, pleading with them to heal and stop their descent.","opus":"Metalcore band Villain of the Story address someone sinking into addiction, seeing past their denial to a person on the edge of collapse, decaying from within on drink or drugs. Unable to watch them destroy themselves, the narrator insists they'll only recover once they make their own mind safe, firm rather than pitying, naming the struggle as theirs to win.","sonnet":"A witness to someone's addiction-driven deterioration delivers an urgent, unsparing appeal — the bloodshot eyes are a symptom of deeper collapse, and the speaker insists it is not too late while confronting the real possibility that it may be. The core is love refusing to pretend the visible destruction is acceptable.","src":"haiku"},
"vince-staples~home":{"haiku":"Internal conflict rages between loyalty to place and escape from memory, caught between the pull of streets that raised them and dreams of transcendence.","opus":"The pull of a violent Long Beach neighborhood that both traps and defines him, torn between wanting peace and the paranoia of not knowing which friend is a threat, yet unable to leave the only place that feels like home.","sonnet":"Home as trap and gravity: the speaker moves across the atlas but keeps returning to the streets that shaped him, haunted by the quiet sound of those resting in peace, riding out on PCH while the neighbourhood's cycle of violence and loyalty pulls him back like a magnet.","src":"haiku"},
"vio-lence~phobophobia-live":{"haiku":"Paranoia and mental fragmentation destroy the self from within, leaving isolation and fear as the dominant forces in a consciousness turned against itself.","opus":"The fear of fear itself, a mind institutionalized and locked inside its own paranoia, unable to escape the dread of everything until existence collapses into a question of whether death is the only way out.","sonnet":"A fragmented portrait of a mind collapsing under paranoia and institutionalized dread, the speaker caught between surveillance, psychiatric labels, and an all-consuming fear that has consumed his identity and any possibility of escape.","src":"haiku"},
"violator~thrash-maniacs":{"haiku":"Subcultural supremacy dismisses mainline trends as false rebellion; speed metal authenticity requires contempt for commercial pretenders and manufactured attitude.","opus":"Old-school thrash as tribal loyalty test, aimed squarely at trend-chasers and posers who buy attitude instead of living it, defending authentic individuality with pure speed and contempt.","sonnet":"A thrash metal attack on posers and trend-followers — empty-headed fashion bastards and brainwashed personalities are promised a blast to the head and the pleasure of watching them bleed. The Thrash Maniacs position themselves as an army of brutal authenticity against a world of pretension.","src":"haiku"},
"violent-femmes~blister-in-the-sun":{"haiku":"Sexual confidence overflows into casual entanglement and careless attraction while a relationship erodes beneath obsessive desire.","opus":"A jittery, strung-out swagger of walking the streets high and horny, staining the sheets and driving a girlfriend to tears without quite knowing why. The song rides teenage restlessness and appetite more than it explains itself.","sonnet":"A strung-out, kinetic self-portrait of someone high, strutting, bodily unraveling — staining sheets without knowing why, girlfriend in tears — begging to be let go on like blistering in the sun, addressing the one who holds power over them.","src":"haiku"},
"violent-new-breed~crazy":{"haiku":"The narrator spirals into pressure-induced madness, unable to escape despite mistakes and loss of control.","opus":"A drowning-in-heartbreak spiral where the narrator, caught in wreckage and choked by pressure, feels himself losing his mind while an ex's lingering words strip away their old hold on him. Admissions of mistakes and letdown surface between panicked refrains of going crazy. The band drives this straightforwardly, letting the claustrophobic imagery of choking and racing hearts carry a modern, heavy alt-rock intensity.","sonnet":"The interior of a relationship's collapse told from the perspective of someone sinking — the other person's words remain lodged even after their leverage is gone, and the pressure of that residue is described as a choking, drowning sensation. There is acknowledgment of fault but also a turning point: the hold has broken, and what remains is the disorienting feeling of being unmoored rather than controlled.","src":"haiku"},
"violent-vira~you-re-not-gone-you-re-just-dead":{"haiku":"Confronting mortality and the impossibility of preventing someone's decline or loss.","opus":"Grief that won't accept a death, still seeing the one buried as impossibly alive, the pull to follow them so strong that even a kiss could scorch them both, love and mortality collapsing into one.","sonnet":"About grief and the impossibility of saving someone — the narrator addresses a dead person (possibly a partner) whose life is still too present in their eyes to feel gone; promises couldn't satisfy, the roof is collapsing on both of them, and a final kiss might burn them alive; love can't hold back death.","src":"haiku"},
"viridian~suppressor":{"haiku":"Numbness as shield that becomes prison: choosing emotional death to survive, but the cost is forgetting why staying alive mattered.","opus":"Running on empty and medicating just to function, sacrificing sleep to a nightly routine while asking whether this suppressed existence is any kind of life. The plea to stay away is aimed at anyone who'd see the wreckage.","sonnet":"A song about emotional depletion and the fear of disappearing — the speaker has buried everything so deep they can barely function, caught between dreaming and drowning, and telling everyone to stay away as their future dissolves into dread.","src":"haiku"},
"virvum~ad-rigorem":{"haiku":"An existential journey through dissolution of thought and consciousness, marching toward an inevitable darkness where reason fails and only vagueness remains.","opus":"A cosmic vision of thought dissolving and human boundaries melting, souls marooned and reborn in an abyss, trudging forever into grit and gloom where logic can no longer function. Obliteration is cast as transcendence. Virvum, a Swiss technical death-metal band, wed this abstract, sci-fi rumination on dissolution to dazzlingly intricate, sweeping playing, the Latin title gesturing toward finality as the universe collapses inward.","sonnet":"A descent beyond the limits of reason into a state where thought dissolves and perception fails. Humanity is reborn into sand and dust, marching through an architecture of collapse toward something the elders named but never taught anyone to survive. Into darkness, toward rigor.","src":"haiku"},
"virvum~tentacles-of-the-sun":{"haiku":"Progress and divine purpose have become tools of imprisonment and blindness, consuming human thought and trapping people in deception masked as enlightenment.","opus":"A cosmic-horror scene of a shapeless plague and a false radiance that devours mankind, muffling and blinding its progress while calling on a divine power to descend and rescue us from the star's corrupt creation. Imprisoned earth and poisoned thought crowd its dense verses. Virvum, a Swiss technical death-metal band, match their intricate playing to grand, gnostic-tinged despair.","sonnet":"The sun recast as tyrant — its light described as a deceptive and corrupting force that blinds and deafens the captive earth, tying progress's blindfold around the faces of those who believed they were advancing. The cry is to Elohim to descend and deliver humanity from this primal false creation, with the tentacles of the sun standing for the entire apparatus of manufactured progress that has hollowed out what we were.","src":"haiku"},
"viscaya~forever-wasn-t-enough":{"haiku":"A relationship ends after cycles of seasons, leaving one person watching the world burn while the other waits for the sun; the years spent together proved insufficient.","opus":"The metalcore act frames a collapsing relationship as apocalypse, sitting together to watch the world burn. The narrator counts stars and waits for the sun as a heart breaks into pieces across many seasons. Numbness bleeds through, fear of never feeling again mixing with a wish never to have severed the ties. Grief and defiance blur as forever proves not to have been enough.","sonnet":"A love that survived many seasons finally breaks apart, leaving one person watching the wreckage and counting stars alone. Even an eternity of shared time proved insufficient to hold the relationship together, and now only waiting remains — pulse dropping, feeling fading, the sky offering no comfort above the ruins.","src":"haiku"},
"viscera~immersed-in-ire":{"opus":"A descent into rage, depression, and self-destruction where the singer's own toxic mind and nightmares are the enemy; consumed by ire and a self-fulfilling darkness, he resolves to forget a former friend ever existed, recognizing himself as a danger and a ticking time bomb.","sonnet":"A person consumed by toxic rage and self-made suffering — seduced by the dark, paralyzed by nightmares of their own devising — eventually turns the ire outward, recognizing the other person as a danger and a time bomb whose self-destruction was always predictable.","src":"sonnet"},
"viscera~lamb-to-the-slaughter":{"opus":"A bitter reckoning with betrayal, where love curdled into a hateful, power-hungry creature and the blind lead the blind toward slaughter; the singer vows to stop crying for the one who justified their cruelty by pretending to be a victim.","sonnet":"A victim of betrayal and loveless cruelty condemns someone who weaponized power over them, describing a world where the naive are led blindly into destruction; the refrain about sins of the father passing to the son suggests generational cycles of abuse.","src":"sonnet"},
"vista-chino~planets-1-2":{"haiku":"War ending and spiritual awakening; shedding inherited behaviors to find deserved peace.","opus":"Desert-rock survivors of Kyuss reflect on a conflict that has at last reached its end, urging listeners to shed hatred and lean toward love and self-understanding. Deliverance is imagined as excavating something buried within, a way of being returned to solid ground.","sonnet":"A post-conflict meditation on restoration — shadows turn to stones, behaviors of clones are shed, and the war is finally over, replaced by the understanding that love is what we are made to make. Pain can somehow bring us back to earth, and what we get is what we deserve.","src":"haiku"},
"vista-chino~planets-1-and-2":{"haiku":"Cycles of violence exhaust survivors into resignation; breaking inherited patterns demands accepting temporary peace as revolutionary act.","src":"haiku"},
"vitalic~poison-lips":{"haiku":"Everyone dissolves to dust; the speaker sings beyond poisonous speech and electrocution, claiming readiness to become strong and meet a challenge.","opus":"A stark, looping chant from the French producer Pascal Arbez-Nicolas, circling dust, tainted kisses, a shadowy jolt of current and an invitation to grow bold and join in. The handful of lines repeat into a trance rather than a story, a terse hook riding relentless, physical electro production.","sonnet":"A spare, looping declaration of mortal awareness and readiness — everything dissolves to dust eventually, and the singing itself comes from beyond that fact, as if the voice is already on the other side of it. What waits after accepting this is not despair but a kind of electric availability, a readiness to play.","src":"haiku"},
"vitja~light-blue":{"haiku":"A savior rescues someone's soul but receives no recognition or gratitude. The speaker wonders why their sacrifice went unseen while the world bleeds indifference.","opus":"German metalcore band Vitja wrap an ache for recognition in melodic heaviness, voicing someone who honored every vow and even rescued another's soul yet was still denied the tenderness he yearned for. Told to keep his distance as everything falls apart, he becomes a lovely thing no one ever truly noticed, the withheld color standing in for the affection he never received.","sonnet":"A figure who believed they saved someone — lovers, a holy cover, the gesture of rescue — and can't understand why they weren't painted in blue, the color of innocence and mercy. The world is bleeding out and the question of worth hangs unanswered: why wasn't I enough?","src":"haiku"},
"voices-from-the-fuselage~inner-child":{"opus":"A struggle to protect one's imaginative inner child against a controlling world that claims and corrupts you; gaining adult knowledge and shame is framed as a kind of infanticide, and the narrator retreats into fantasy rather than play the victim.","sonnet":"A lamentation about losing the childlike wonder of imagination as adult cynicism, judgement, and tribal thinking take hold — the narrator clings to fantasy as refuge while recognizing that growing up is a kind of killing of the inner self.","src":"sonnet"},
"voices-from-the-fuselage~life-on-titan":{"haiku":"Questions existence and purpose when trapped under another's control, seeking optimization in an alien or hostile world. Faces approaching darkness while waiting for change.","opus":"A fragile voice calls out from cold isolation, never having felt like more than dust and pleading to be truly seen and to find someone to trust before the light goes out. Its survival rests entirely in another's hands. Voices from the Fuselage, a British prog-rock band, set this vulnerable sci-fi meditation on trust and extinction to shifting, atmospheric textures.","sonnet":"Somewhere between isolation and extinction, a speaker who never expected to matter prays to be seen, to be trusted, to be enough — and keeps reaching toward a skyline even as the light runs out. Strange and frightened at the edge of what the species has left.","src":"haiku"},
"voices-from-the-fuselage~vestibule-of-hell":{"haiku":"Someone yearns for a love that consumes them while feeling pushed aside and trapped by inner demons. They are alone with their mind as their only refuge in a vestibule of hell.","opus":"A prog-metal narrative about a captive bent to someone else's will, aching with forbidden longing while clutching a faint inner spark amid total confinement. Cast out and imprisoned, the narrator wrestles with the demons still living inside him. The band's atmospheric weight dramatizes a captivity of body and mind, the imagination the one thing that stays wholly his own.","sonnet":"Trapped in a toxic entanglement that simultaneously repels and consumes, a person knows they are controlled and knows they are alone — their only refuge a small internal light. The tension between craving calm and being unable to escape the inner demon is the song's entire axis.","src":"haiku"},
"voicians~wolves":{"haiku":"Amid crumbling ground and burning forests, people chase distant worlds seeking home and peace while refusing to surrender despite the hurt of being present.","opus":"A pledge of unwavering loyalty set against apocalyptic ruin, the narrator promising to stand by someone as they journey toward some final home while the earth falls apart around them. Under the relentlessly repeated refusal to yield lies its real point: a devotion so stubborn it holds fast even as everything comes to an end.","sonnet":"A short, looping anthem of resilience — the world is crumbling and burning but the narrator insists on staying, refusing to give in, promising to be there for someone through the end.","src":"haiku"},
"void-chapter~our-time-is-now-feat-the-anix-extended":{"haiku":"Collective awakening and reckoning arrive as silence breaks; the speaker and their cohort finally claim power after years of complicity.","opus":"A darkly ironic reckoning aimed at bystanders who watched from the sidelines as lives fell apart and now cast themselves as saviors, with the sharp admission that breaking into a life is harder than breaking out of one.","sonnet":"A moment of collective awakening after long passivity — 'we stood by while lives explode, but our time is now' — with uncaged feelings and the realisation that breaking in is harder than breaking out, yet the time to act has finally arrived.","src":"haiku"},
"void-chapter~our-time-is-now-feat-the-anix-single-edit":{"haiku":"Collective awakening demands active witness; the speaker recognizes passivity while catastrophe unfolds, claiming agency for the present moment.","opus":"The tighter cut of the same reckoning, calling out those who watched from the sidelines then claimed the credit, insisting their moment has finally, uncomfortably arrived.","sonnet":"A call to collective action after a long, passive vigil — something changed the day all feelings were uncaged, and though breaking in proves harder than breaking out, the speaker declares that the time of bystanders watching lives explode is over: our time is now.","src":"haiku"},
"void-chapter~resist":{"opus":"A dystopian call to rebellion against an authoritarian empire that erases history, controls minds and reduces people to soulless machines, urging the confined and silenced to wake up and resist.","sonnet":"An anthem of resistance against authoritarian erasure of cultural identity and humanity — the narrator calls on people to wake up and oppose regimes that subjugate, criminalize, and try to reduce people to soulless machines.","src":"sonnet"},
"void-of-vision~altar":{"haiku":"Grief crystallises into permanent testimony; lost connection becomes altar where survivors worship absence, uncertain whether suffering consecrates or corrupts.","opus":"Grief and guilt over letting someone down as they left, the cold December of loss fused with fury at melodramatic performances of love; the narrator interrogates whether anyone truly dies alone and whether any of it was ever love at all.","sonnet":"Grief over a person who let someone down — the speaker asks whether they will be remembered in the December cold at the moment of failure, and the song turns to anger at someone who abandoned another in the rain and watched her turn to rust, calling it love. The questions of dying alone and whether God is present are left open.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~angel-of-darkness":{"haiku":"A fallen angel seduces at rock bottom. Separation becomes theft—taking everything while walking away.","opus":"A toxic push-pull of taking everything from a lover while walking away, the angel of darkness who finds the singer at his lowest; leaving is agony, but the sick pull of desire makes staying impossible.","sonnet":"A song about walking away from a dependency that was consuming the narrator — an 'angel of darkness' found them at their lowest and became their fix, but now the narrator, sick of needing and desiring, is leaving and taking everything the other person wanted, the exit framed as both liberation and cruelty.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~asmr":{"haiku":"Hypnotic domination blends pleasure and violation—the speaker surrenders to ecstatic oblivion, dancing until death claims them.","opus":"A hypersexual, submissive fixation on a lover set in a drug-soaked German nightclub, seraphs on speed and love as a cocked weapon, the couple dancing themselves into oblivion.","sonnet":"Hypersexuality and surrender play out in a German disco haze — cigarette burns, angels on amphetamines, love as a loaded gun — the speaker submits willingly as slave but something remains in the way; the only resolution is dancing until dead.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~babylon":{"haiku":"Life is meaningless and death inevitable; there is no god and all humans are born to rot beneath the weight of the world.","opus":"Australian metalcore act Void of Vision hammer out a godless, hopeless creed: everyone is fated to decay and perish alike, crushed under existence in a kingdom of filth, with no higher power and no authority left to answer to. The cosmic despair is anchored to something intimate, a lover's constant lying that grounds the nihilism in personal betrayal.","sonnet":"A nihilist declaration against divine authority and social hierarchy: no gods, no masters, everyone born into decay and dying the same regardless of power or pretension. The disgust is bodily and total, aimed at systems that comfort with lies while the bones already know the truth.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~blacklist":{"haiku":"Band members confront a manipulative person who exploited them for profit and fame; they cut the relationship and memorialize the betrayal.","opus":"The Australian metalcore band turns a burned bridge into a blistering callout, a diss track built on breakdowns. The narrator orders a self-important user down from their high horse, accusing them of cashing in on the band's reputation and inventing their own convenient rules. The venom is pointed and personal, vowing never to forget the face of the person who crossed them where the friendship died.","sonnet":"A direct confrontation with someone who exploited a band's name and trust for personal gain. The accusation is specific: profit prioritised over people, rules written in bad faith, manipulation dressed as friendship. The response is cold finality — no reconciliation, no forgetting, just the permanent record of what was done.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~ctrl-freak":{"haiku":"Internet bravery masks cowardice; the speaker challenges someone who hides behind screens, unable to speak their words face-to-face.","opus":"A scathing callout of anonymous online aggressors, daring them to drop the bravado and confront him in person, mocking rage vented safely behind a screen as cowardice from someone with nothing real to risk. Australian metalcore band Void of Vision weaponize this internet-culture takedown with industrial-tinged heaviness aimed at pretension.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who weaponizes online anonymity to say things they'd never say face to face. The contempt is exact: bending truth, self-inflating, never having learned to actually speak. Words without a body behind them don't count.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~decades":{"haiku":"A person questions whether they were ever more than a regret in someone else's narrative, trapped between the idealized version held in another's head and the grinding reality of being face-down and forgotten.","opus":"A metalcore reckoning with feeling like a permanent almost-was, shrunk to a footnote in someone else's idealized story. The narrator debates whether to bolt or regret not bolting sooner, face down in the mud as it wears him away, the refrain of never measuring up fueling a grim look at squandered, cheaply traded lives.","sonnet":"Caught between staying and erasing: am I more than a memory, a should've been, could've been in someone's head framed as heaven-sent — the speaker has been face down in the dirt while it all kills him slowly, asking whether forgetting the taste is the same as being replaced, and unable to answer.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~decay":{"haiku":"A person rejects meaninglessness and conformity, embracing chaos and self-destruction as rebellion against societal indifference.","opus":"A self-destructive craving voiced as hunger for decay, the narrator both loathing his worthlessness and getting off on the rush of falling apart. The rage turns outward at the fair-weather friends dancing on his grave.","sonnet":"Self-loathing as addiction — the narrator is disconnected, forgotten, a waste of space, and rather than fight it they lean into the hedonistic decay, demanding more of the thing that's destroying them because at least it's something to feel.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~dominatrix":{"haiku":"Two people exist in an intimate, destructive push-pull dynamic defined by power plays and obsessive proximity.","opus":"An intoxicated plunge into obsessive, self-destructive desire, the narrator wanting to close the space between until it is only two of them, wearing misery well and begging to be absolved and resurrected on a lover's velvet skin. Devotion curdles into worship of pain. Void of Vision, the Australian metalcore band, cast this toxic intimacy through dark, electronic-tinged heaviness, the dominatrix figure both salvation and sin carried like a burden.","sonnet":"Addicted to closeness that hurts — a relationship that spirals and sickens but keeps pulling inward. The speaker is on their knees, asking to be absolved by the same person who keeps tightening the spiral, love and ruin folded together into something beautiful and unbearable.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~feat-drew-york":{"haiku":"Hypocrisy and breached freedoms expose the gap between perceived and actual worlds; the speaker demands confrontation with uncomfortable truths buried beneath surfaces.","opus":"A politically charged metalcore broadside aimed at censorship, complacency, and institutional cruelty. It skewers a society that jails children in detention centres while claiming free speech, urging listeners to bare their teeth and stop pretending things are as they seem. The chorus insists that anyone who isn't angry simply isn't paying attention.","sonnet":"The speaker addresses a politician or authority figure who silences dissent while locking children in detention centres and calling it freedom of speech — streets paved gold for children of chrome who are led to believe the lie — and the toast raised at the end is a damning indictment disguised as a celebration.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~ghost-in-the-machine":{"haiku":"Someone takes a deal with the devil and gets something to hold, but they cannot let go. Dead inside and bottling it all, they hide from the world while that dark feeling becomes them.","opus":"The Australian metalcore band renders addiction as an invisible possession. Having chased everything he wanted and won it, the narrator now grips whatever still registers as real while admitting he's hollowed out and unseen. The intoxicant muffles the world, the craving never lifts, and self-punishment hardens into his sense of self, a haunting no one around him can detect.","sonnet":"Someone who chased everything and got it finds themselves hollow — the rush of the bargain gone cold, the feeling of deadness now permanent and invisible to others. They bottle it, carry it, insist on clinging to anything close enough to feel real.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~hell-hell-hell":{"haiku":"Addiction and self-harm cycle endlessly; the speaker craves transformation but remains trapped in destructive patterns.","opus":"An addict turns his self-destruction into a performance for a crowd hungry to see him hurt. Steeped in fuel and craving since birth, he flings the compulsion outward with knife-edge scorn, challenging the spectators who feasted on his suffering to keep staring.","sonnet":"A person drowning in dopamine-driven excess who has everything they wanted but not what they need, directed at someone who seems to take pleasure in watching them bleed — the speaker is 'born in gasoline,' self-destructive by nature, and fed up with being someone else's spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~hole-in-me":{"haiku":"Dead inner emptiness and numbness sustained through drugs and self-harm as a twisted form of survival.","opus":"A gutted addict's self-portrait, lifeless in the gaze and squandering time that isn't his, hungry even for hurt just to register anything at all. The Australian band cast numbness and self-hatred as a masochistic bliss, contempt aimed inward at a poisonous self they consider worthless.","sonnet":"About being numb and self-destructively alive — the narrator is dead behind the eyes, poisoned, rotting on the inside, and oscillates between wanting to feel pain and medicating himself into comfortable numbness with Ritalin and red wine.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~if-only":{"haiku":"Drowning under emotional dead-weight: the speaker feels worthless and a burden to others, their suffering unable to be alleviated by another's frozen heart, begging to be released into oblivion.","opus":"Void of Vision turns a poisonous relationship into a drowning image, the narrator branding himself indifferent, wretched, and already lifeless while imploring a burden that drags him under not to abandon him. A frigid heart and never-enough water sketch a bond that sinks him even as he holds fast, coming apart. The Australian band's abrasive delivery makes self-degradation and reliance sound like one and the same desperate grip.","sonnet":"A person addresses a cold, withholding presence they can no longer hold onto — naming their own deadweight and the other's glacial indifference — and keeps returning to the same image of drowning, as though surrender is the only honest release available.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~into-the-dark":{"haiku":"Two people spiral together feeling the same pain; one watches their angel bleed while helplessly asking if the other feels the deja vu of falling.","opus":"The Melbourne metalcore outfit pairs claustrophobic dread with intimacy on this darker, electronic-tinged cut. A narrator feels himself falling out of view, colour turning inside out, unwilling to feel what he feels yet dragging another into the dark with him. Images of a supernova sound and an angel bleeding heighten a toxic closeness where breathing someone in becomes both craving and unbearable, held until he cannot breathe.","sonnet":"Co-dependency and shared emotional spiralling between two people who mirror each other's damage. He watches her suffer and cannot intervene, paralysed between lying to protect her and confessing what he cannot hold in. The pull toward each other is described as both cosmic force and suffocation.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~kerosene-dream":{"haiku":"Obsessive desire to burn someone into memory, where passion consumes everything and creates hellbound chains of obsession and debris.","opus":"An obsession set ablaze and burned inward, the narrator letting it consume him without remorse, admitting he got exactly what he chased and now only wants to erase it. Void of Vision's Australian metalcore leans into this scorched-earth romance, treating a toxic fixation as a shrine of deceit that breeds compulsion, with the worst still ahead even after the best has been destroyed.","sonnet":"An obsession burns itself into the body like fire through debris — the speaker does not resist, letting the infatuation sear through until it becomes part of them. There is no grief in it, only the paradox of wanting something that breeds a hellbound fixation.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~kill-all-my-friends":{"haiku":"The speaker counts on people who always blame them when nothing grows, working to exhaustion only to lose their soul. They tape mouths shut and count crows while bound by chains to misery.","opus":"A savage self-reckoning, the narrator run ragged and quick to fault everyone around him before admitting he is the source of his own ruin, a willing servant to sorrow held fast. The closing vow to turn on everyone he knows reads as total self-immolation. Void of Vision, an Australian metalcore band, deliver this joy-in-collapse through crushing, industrial-tinged heaviness.","sonnet":"Self-loathing turned outward and then back in — the speaker has worked to exhaustion while blaming everyone around him, then recognizes he has become the kind of man he never wanted to be, his soul spent in games he played for keeps. The fantasy of silencing others curdles into something darker and more honest: a wish to burn the entire social world to the ground, friends included, because the chains were always self-made.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~psychosocial":{"haiku":"The song attacks ideological manipulation and fake resistance movements that serve the status quo. It depicts a system where people sacrifice themselves for hollow causes while rot spreads unchecked underneath.","opus":"Void of Vision recast Slipknot's anthem as their own metalcore, a narrator who has served his sentence and wants free amid sickness and false piety. Coming ruin and a martyr complex frame a paranoid isolation no one else perceives, greed and self-disgust sharpening into the chanted title, a diagnosis of a rot the narrator swears he's not alone in carrying.","sonnet":"Social structures built on hatred and self-deception have collapsed inward, leaving only martyrdom as a mode of survival. The narrator sees through pseudo-sacred conformity and fake ideological posturing, but the recognition offers no exit — the rain that kills them all is the system they all inhabit.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~slave-to-the-name":{"haiku":"Someone sold their soul and stares at the sun, tangled in thorns and reborn but nothing remains of them. They are disposable and picked clean by a vulture with clipped wings.","opus":"A portrait of being reduced to something disposable, the narrator having traded away his soul and finding nothing of himself left. Pushed off then hauled back in an unending loop, he addresses a scavenger that picks him apart bit by bit. The band's metalcore fury frames sheer exhaustion and self-erasure, sick of his own pulse and the identity that owns him.","sonnet":"A person who gave everything and was treated as disposable confronts the one who kept pulling them back under — naming them a wingless scavenger and reclaiming the belief they died defending, piece by piece.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~splinter":{"haiku":"A broken person reflects on lifelong dissection and shame, finding only misery and death as an endpoint to their suffering.","opus":"A descent into decay and hollow reverie, the narrator unraveling and adrift with no direction, wading through his own rot as calm curdles into violence. It closes at the end of a gun barrel, the only exit being death. Void of Vision, the Australian metalcore band, render this self-destruction with punishing, industrial-edged heaviness, the mind slowly rotting toward its finish.","sonnet":"Coming apart at every seam — direction gone, infection swum through, silence loud enough to be violent. The image of choking on a silver spoon suggests entitlement inverting into suffering; there's no end in sight except one, and the speaker is running out of reasons to avoid it.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~the-hills":{"haiku":"A heart-stone thief feels utterly alone despite moments of belonging, stripped bare and fed to wolves. Words stick in their throat; they're running out of time and sleep.","opus":"A confession of numb self-loathing, the narrator pleading to be scarred deeply enough to feel a life he considers stolen, branding himself a thief with an unfeeling core who craves solitude yet fears being discarded and preyed upon. Void of Vision strip toward raw bleakness here, his words failing him as time, rest, and reason all drain away.","sonnet":"A person who has stolen from their own life stares into the void of emotional numbness and a complete inability to speak what they feel. The words stay stuck, the glass gets raised as a substitute, and time runs out — fed to the wolves by something they still cannot name.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~vampyr":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts a hypocrite who sheds false skin, condemning their lies and removing their mask. They burn them symbolically and reveal the hidden corruption inside.","opus":"A venomous takedown of a two-faced hypocrite, called a snake born rather than reborn, whose silver-tongued false sympathy the narrator wants ripped out and answered with a bullet. Psychosomatic rage builds toward burning an effigy and denying the target any chance to play victim. Void of Vision, Australian metalcore, sharpen their industrial aggression into this personal, unrelenting vendetta.","sonnet":"An exposure — someone who performed spiritual superiority while being fundamentally hollow, a snake who only shed skin rather than being reborn. The speaker, burned by having placed real faith in this person, reaches for destruction as a corrective: burning the effigy, weed out the weak, a psychosomatic fury that builds until it demands to be called out loud.","src":"haiku"},
"void-of-vision~year-of-the-rat":{"haiku":"Someone lies endlessly and Lady Luck abandons them under a black sun where death falls from above. The narrator waits to expose the truth and haunt them with past lies.","opus":"The Australian band stages a venomous settling of scores with a liar, gauging the yawning gap between two people and swearing to shadow anyone who keeps up the deceit. Moving through darkness beneath a dead sun, the narrator watches with icy pleasure as his mark disintegrates and fortune deserts them, betrayal curdled into a promise of relentless pursuit.","sonnet":"A figure waiting in patient fury watches the person who has lied and used them run out of luck at last. Distance and darkness frame the vigil, and the promise is blunt: as long as the lie holds, the haunting continues.","src":"haiku"},
"voivod~from-the-cave":{"haiku":"Nomadic survival transformed into stage performance, carrying nothing but stories. The speaker and their crew have fought so long that small victories keep them moving forward.","opus":"A road-worn celebration of a life on tour, scars recast as badges with good stories and aging bones as living memory, following restless travelers from nowhere to the stage owning nothing but a good time. Not glory but small wins keep them going as everything falls apart. Voivod, Canadian prog-metal veterans, look back on decades of touring with hard-won warmth.","sonnet":"A musician's life told as nomadic mythology — scars as better stories than tattoos, possessions traded for fun, the stage as the endpoint of a journey that started in the cave and ran across rooftops. The wisdom accumulated is simply to take things as they come, accept that small victories are enough, and keep moving with your people for however long it lasts.","src":"haiku"},
"voivod~global-warning":{"haiku":"Systemic collapse and collective inaction in the face of looming doom. The world is self-destructing through warfare, pesticides, and genocide while everyone pursues their own interests.","opus":"Voivod, the Canadian prog-metal veterans, deliver a world-weary eco-rant with a wry, sci-fi edge. Slumped in front of a TV full of war, poison, and slaughter, the narrator doubts any escape and shrugs that everyone's out for themselves and life is rigged. Between hits off a joint, he insists the reckoning has already arrived and the planet's fate is sealed.","sonnet":"A stoned, lucid survey of a world too fragmented to save itself — war machines, pesticide, genocide, and people so locked into individual survival that no one shares and nothing is fair. The speaker finishes his joint and concludes the world is doomed on a short timeline, not with rage but with the resigned clarity of someone who has simply stopped expecting a collective answer.","src":"haiku"},
"voivod~god-phones":{"haiku":"An alien entity makes contact via mysterious phone calls, claiming the recipient is chosen for a mission while announcing the arrival of more beings to harvest human souls.","opus":"The Canadian prog-metal eccentrics run wild with sci-fi paranoia: unidentifiable voices on the telephone, a supposedly chosen recipient handed a mission, a deity who admits to being an extraterrestrial descending from deep space with armed invaders. The cosmic contact plays as absurd, menacing pulp. It's pure Voivod, dystopian comic-book physics wrapped around a chant-driven groove.","sonnet":"A satirical or sincerely unsettling portrait of someone receiving transmissions they believe are divine — chosen, briefed by an entity claiming alien origin, warned of an imminent arrival. The line between cosmic revelation and paranoid delusion stays deliberately uncrossed.","src":"haiku"},
"voivod~in-orbit":{"haiku":"An astronaut floats alone in orbit, repaired and isolated, thinking about the world below as he burns up entering the atmosphere.","opus":"A marooned astronaut drifts above the planet as a maintenance man who has lost his tools and torn free of his tether, thinking of someone back on Earth as he slides helplessly toward incineration on reentry. Wonder sours into doom, with no way to get home. Voivod, the Canadian progressive-metal band, wrap this sci-fi vignette in their dissonant, angular riffing, the spaceman's isolation both literal and quietly moving.","sonnet":"A lone astronaut drifting after a harness failure, fixing a telescope in the silence above Earth, thinking about the people below as debris closes in and re-entry speed climbs. Home Sweet Home repeated at the end — wry, longing, possibly the last transmission.","src":"haiku"},
"voivod~krap-radio":{"haiku":"Commercial radio exploits human drama and suffering for ratings, manipulating emotion to sell products while filling people's minds with garbage.","opus":"A sharp satire of exploitative media, imagining a morning show that hungers for your personal drama, hawks useless products, and gorges on tragedy and cruelty while stuffing your mind with junk. Voivod, veteran Canadian prog-metal oddballs, skewer sensationalism through their dissonant, off-kilter attack, the host's fake cheer souring into a picture of manipulation.","sonnet":"A takedown of media designed to exploit emotional breakdown for profit — drama sold as programming, suffering packaged and sponsored. The speaker's head is full of it and they know it, which makes the noise no less deafening.","src":"haiku"},
"voivod~treasure-chase":{"haiku":"A pirate seeks treasure across the seas and finds it in the eyes of a beloved person, the greatest value he's ever discovered.","opus":"A wandering sailor casts romantic love as buried treasure, finding in a lover's eyes the diamonds and gems he chased across imagined seas as an aging, ghostly captain of drifting souls. It is a surprisingly tender metaphor, all longing and self-mythology. Voivod are Quebec's veteran sci-fi thrash pioneers, known for angular, progressive metal, so this lyrical warmth reads as an unusually sentimental turn from the band.","sonnet":"A sailor adrift and slowly falling apart — ghost captain of an aging vessel — who discovers that the only treasure worth chasing isn't gold or precious stone but a person's gaze. The metaphor of piracy gives way to something tender: all his maps and seafaring reduce to a single coordinate found in someone's eyes.","src":"haiku"},
"vokonis~grasping-time":{"haiku":"Time slips away as a journey into darkness unfolds, with chances and portals closing before understanding arrives.","opus":"A dark reckoning with mortality and slipping time, its narrator marching into some inevitable deep while lights fade and a portal to escape seals shut forever. There is grim comfort in the paradox that you cannot die, a suggestion of unending recurrence rather than release. Vokonis are a Swedish stoner-doom trio, and the heavy, cosmic fatalism here fits that genre's fondness for vast, crushing existential weight.","sonnet":"A slow reckoning with inevitable descent — the darkest days haven't arrived yet, the lights are fading, the portal has already closed. Time slips through the hands of whoever is reaching for it, and there is no dying but also no escape, just a perpetual journey through darkness that never quite resolves.","src":"haiku"},
"vokonis~through-the-depths-single-edit":{"opus":"A mystical quest inward toward a sacred hidden temple within us all, piercing the veil to descend through the depths into a transcendent netherworld of astral light and infinity.","sonnet":"A mythic descent through layers of hidden reality — past sacred seals and gateway visions — searching for a lost age of divine truth and transcendence that once existed and has since vanished, now accessible only by going deep through the netherworld.","src":"sonnet"},
"vola~24-light-years":{"opus":"The Danish prog band conjures grief as a haunted house one cannot leave, an echo persisting where a person no longer is. Sparse domestic acts of cleaning and rearranging mask an unbridgeable distance, measured in the impossible span of light-years. Loss rendered as cosmic remove, intimacy stranded across unreachable space.","sonnet":"A song about grief-induced inability to leave a space haunted by someone who is gone — the narrator cannot bring himself to walk out of the house because the echo of the lost person still lives there, and the distance between them is framed as both measurable footprints and an astronomical impossibility. Bleaching the surface and rearranging the furniture cannot erase the presence.","src":"sonnet"},
"vola~alien-shivers":{"haiku":"Someone's doubts and confrontations interrupt another's perfect success, stopping their momentum and draining the joy from their achievements through interference.","opus":"An address to a fading friend who once laughed freely but now finds purpose in disaster, the narrator watching a person he calls perfect drift away as some private victory stalls. The imagery of dopamine and dimming light suggests addiction or slow psychological collapse. Vola are a Danish progressive metal band who blend djent heaviness with pop melody and electronic texture, and this melancholy, cerebral character is central to their sound.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who has surrendered their clearest self to an addictive pull — once joyful to the point of sore eyes, now defending a contagious weight they mistake for purpose. The phrase alien shivers captures the shudder of dopamine withdrawal stalling out what could have been a full life, and the question is whether that person is even still present.","src":"haiku"},
"vola~applause-of-a-distant-crowd":{"haiku":"Digital existence erases authenticity into aesthetic performance; the speaker documents loss and commodifies memory, vanishing into spectacle.","opus":"A dissection of the hollow self performed for social media, vanishing into the light while curating meals and losing streaks for an audience that never truly sees. Beneath the filtered performance is a plea, repeated to the point of desperation, to actually be seen.","sonnet":"A portrait of distraction and digital vanishing: the speaker photographs meals in black and white, filters losing streaks warm and bright, keeps faith in cat videos — and the question \"when will you see me?\" reveals someone disappearing behind their own curated surface, heading straight to \"the extinction of your last company.\"","src":"haiku"},
"vola~break-my-lying-tongue":{"haiku":"Desperation for honesty; asking to be stopped from deceiving, confessing compulsively.","opus":"An unsettling plea to be stopped from one's own dishonesty, obsession and misalignment in a relationship, asking the other to break the lying tongue rather than trusting oneself to stay truthful.","sonnet":"A confessional addressed to someone whose silence and misalignment the speaker has chosen to escalate into obsession, asking to be broken of their compulsive dishonesty — 'break my lying tongue, I ask of you' — the repeated request both ritual and self-punishment.","src":"haiku"},
"vola~giants":{"opus":"A parable of towering powers arriving among the small, growing until their dominance goes unquestioned. VOLA turns the arrival of these giants into a meditation on how outsized forces gain relevance and cast blame while ordinary people diminish. Beneath the prog-metal grandeur sits unease about power, complicity, and standing in the shadow of something vast.","sonnet":"About the rise and fall of powerful forces — giants grow larger than the world around them and cast silence and blame, yet the song suggests that if people stand taller themselves, the giants' relevance dissolves, leaving only the child caught underfoot while civilization churns on.","src":"sonnet"},
"vola~head-mounted-sideways-live-from-the-pool":{"haiku":"Media and technology fragment consciousness and paralyze action; the speaker floats suspended in a stream of manufactured meaning while life drains away unseen.","opus":"A dystopian vision of consciousness colonized by an endless media stream, bodies suspended and identities purged as a lifeline breaks. It is about losing your name and voice to a manufactured reality that leaves the left-behind to die.","sonnet":"A person subsumed by media saturation loses coherent identity — \"head melded, sideways, suspended\" — as pointy needles of content embed themselves, a lifeline breaks inside the host, and the imagery of see-through fingers and a lingering purge suggests digital presence hollowing out the self.","src":"haiku"},
"vola~inside-your-fur":{"haiku":"Temporal displacement and dissolution blur as the narrator seeks refuge in another's essence, dissolving before boundaries and memory erase connection entirely.","opus":"A brief, doomed intimacy where the longing to burrow into someone's warmth collides with the resolve to remember and then deliberately erase them, tenderness and self-defense in the same breath.","sonnet":"A brief, tender connection — a summer blur with someone whose heart and behaviour were at odds — leaves the speaker longing to dissolve into that warmth before it disappears, though he already knows he will carry and then erase the memory.","src":"haiku"},
"vola~owls":{"haiku":"Communication breaks down between two people; freedom and dreams remain perpetually locked behind closed doors.","opus":"A dreamlike scene of a woman lulled and controlled by a man hiding an emptiness beneath his shell. The owls escaping his locked doors signal secrets loosed as she drifts, still asleep to what's happening.","sonnet":"Two people in an unspoken, fragile relationship — he draws on her face and masks it, she tries to steady herself against his evasions — drift toward a quiet ending, owls escaping through doors he locked, while she keeps dreaming of a tomorrow that never quite arrives.","src":"haiku"},
"vola~paper-wolf":{"haiku":"Careful deception and vigilant self-preservation mask fragile vulnerability beneath layers of control.","opus":"A prog-metal parable of paranoia and dread. The narrator hides his trail, strips his home bare and dreams of a loyal king even as a nearing predator warns everyone to save themselves. Refusing to sink, he vows to keep afloat by impossible means, defiance flickering amid ringing bells and collapsing towers.","sonnet":"A quiet outsider cultivates hidden ferocity — eyes installed in marble walls, cigarette ash turning to sparkles — while issuing warnings that the wolf is coming and the bells will toll. The bridge shifts the register: the speaker refuses to drown and will swim with phantom limbs rather than surrender to the crowd.","src":"haiku"},
"vola~ruby-pool":{"opus":"A portrait of lonely, disillusioned young people who feel they possess a hollow kind of privilege, shiny without a jewel, ruling a kingdom that means nothing; the singer just wants to rest, turn things around, and hold onto another person.","sonnet":"Isolated, jewel-less people living under meaningless rules — described as lonely kids floating in a ruby pool that sounds beautiful but offers nothing of value — longing to rest and turn the tide, reaching toward someone for comfort.","src":"sonnet"},
"vola~smartfriend":{"haiku":"A person transformed by technology feels like a fabricated mistake, betrayed by physical and intellectual upgrades that promise progress but deliver hollowness.","opus":"A transhumanist parable told by someone rebuilt with technology, taller and more employable than the flawed body they left behind, only to realize the upgrade was a betrayal that turned them into their maker's mistake.","sonnet":"A person who has undergone body modification or enhancement — some cybernetic or physical upgrade — finds that what was supposed to improve them has instead made them into something their creators can call a mistake, a traitor, a failed version of what was intended.","src":"haiku"},
"vola~straight-lines":{"haiku":"Depleted hope and certainty; the speaker stumbles through a world drained of clear paths forward.","opus":"Vola, a Danish progressive-metal band, drape a paranoid, cornered mood in glossy synth-laced djent. The narrator speaks to someone coming undone, warning that their reserves of stability and hope are nearly spent as a hangman's threat and sweeping lights close in. Owning up to endless faults while swearing his innocence, he sinks deeper into unease and dread.","sonnet":"A person under surveillance and accusation insisting on their innocence while acknowledging real flaws — the authorities circling cannot catch what they claim to be hunting, and the verdict against the accused is built from salt and bad signs rather than evidence. The refrain of running low on straight lines names the moment when the official story starts to fray.","src":"haiku"},
"vola~stray-the-skies":{"haiku":"The speaker absorbs external violence and tries to please while losing themselves, beginning to wander away from coherence into dissonance.","opus":"A meta reflection on making and performing music, where a song stirs longing and the mechanics of recording seem to breathe life into the world, even as the narrator soaks up outside hostility and nearly chokes trying to satisfy his listeners. Shutting his eyes and drifting off become his defense. Vola's Danish prog-metal turns this performer's anxiety, an art meant to reshape minds, into shimmering, dissonant beauty.","sonnet":"A performer dissolving under the pressure of their craft — the music that gave thirst and a reason to exist now arrives as an avalanche, pushing the speaker into consequence with every note. The only relief is closing the eyes and drifting, beginning to stray the skies, a dissociation framed not as failure but as the body's only available response to sound that has become unbearable.","src":"haiku"},
"vola~the-avalanche":{"haiku":"Forced reckoning with one's accumulated weight and consequences, with inevitable collapse approaching and drowning inevitable.","opus":"Existential dread wakes the narrator with his head in flame and a blade at his skin, questioning the strings and centuries attached to his name as an avalanche speaks and the earth comes down. Danish progressive band Vola pair these ominous questions with their glassy, djent-electronic textures, the tension between running away and crawling giving the reckoning its ambivalent, gathering weight.","sonnet":"An avalanche serves as metaphor for accumulated consequence arriving all at once — weight of history, inherited damage, and daily self-destruction finally reaching critical mass. The lyric demands acknowledgment of what has been building, asking whether the one buried in it can even hear the sound of the collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"vola~we-are-thin-air":{"opus":"An oblique reckoning with insignificance and lost connection, its narrator cast as a fading fragment, the last one to intrude on someone's attention. Amid a culture of trending images, existence dissolves into thin air. The Danish group leans into surreal, fractured phrasing to render the feeling of being barely present, easily erased.","sonnet":"A relationship or creative connection that has faded into irrelevance — the narrator recognizes they are becoming invisible and insignificant in someone else's world, a lost signal in a picture where no one looks for them anymore.","src":"sonnet"},
"volbeat~dead-but-rising":{"haiku":"A spiritual ancestor or fallen hero rises renewed; the living carry forward the rebel's legacy and unbroken determination.","opus":"Michael Poulsen's rockabilly-metal built on Southern gospel-outlaw imagery, a father-and-son passing of spirit across death, faith and resurrection framed as a chosen or fallen figure rising again.","sonnet":"A son-and-father story unfolding across spiritual and physical distance: the speaker seeks a figure who is gone but whose spirit persists like an eagle or a chosen fallen one, and in the end receives guidance and peace — the father's soul returning home through the son.","src":"haiku"},
"volbeat~seal-the-deal":{"haiku":"Infernal transaction seals apostate dominion; the speaker gambles away humanity for power, celebrating blood-soaked survival and mechanical ecstasy.","opus":"A Faustian gambler's strut, hawking his soul then snatching it back, spurning any heavenly reward in favor of self-reliance and earning his keep through toil and struggle. The Devil is fooled and challenged, the moral being to stand on your own two feet.","sonnet":"A defiant survival anthem — the speaker sold his soul, stole it back, fooled the devil, and now channels all internal anger like an oiled machine to fight and boogie through another round. Standing up for oneself, blood, sweat, and tears included, is the deal being sealed.","src":"haiku"},
"volumes~bend":{"haiku":"The speaker feels dead inside like a microscope subject while someone plugs into them and leaves them numb. They won't bend despite being broken, as the other person runs away.","opus":"A push-and-pull breakup where the narrator feels lifeless and numb yet keeps circling back to a partner who won't yield or own their mistakes, still hoping for a return that likely won't come. His refusal to shatter while shattering anyway drives the wounded refrain. Volumes, a Los Angeles progressive metalcore band, fold this bruised codependency into their groove-heavy, R&B-tinged sound.","sonnet":"A relationship where one person keeps absorbing punishment and the other keeps promising to change without actually bending — the speaker stays, numb and still hoping, while knowing the other will run. The final pivot is a refusal: you make me break, but I will not bend, a distinction between being damaged and being reshaped into something unrecognizable.","src":"haiku"},
"volumes~fbx":{"haiku":"A hostile force keeps repeating destructive cycles, refusing compromise while accusing others of imitation and claiming justified aggression.","opus":"A defiant admission of relapse, the narrator claiming he means no harm while conceding he will transgress once more, doling out punishment and pinning the emptiness he keeps filling on a partner. It loops through guilt and compulsion unresolved, half apology and half taunt. Volumes are a California metalcore act whose grooving aggression and toxic-relationship themes sit in the modern djent-adjacent scene.","sonnet":"A cycle of mutual damage where neither party can break the pattern — one side keeps sinning and coming back, the other stays static and predictable. The speaker admits to filling a void, carrying the weight of something unspoken, and ultimately accepts the repetition as inevitable rather than something to escape.","src":"haiku"},
"volumes~feels-good":{"opus":"A groove-metal reflection on the seductive numbness of a habit that feels sublime even as it hollows the narrator out. Waking clear-headed collides with the pull of another indulgence, while an absent figure is confronted with abandonment. The push-pull of relief and reproach drives it, self-justification wrestling with the demand to know where support was.","sonnet":"A person climbing out of substance dependence through sheer will — the relief and momentum of finally choosing to build themselves up after years of habit, residue, and feeling like they were trying to prove something to someone who wasn't there.","src":"sonnet"},
"volumes~get-enough":{"haiku":"Cyclical need and dissatisfaction; people endlessly want until they obtain, then demand more, leaving the speaker depleted from giving without reciprocal care.","opus":"A song about a taker in a relationship who only returns what he's given and is never content, since people keep grasping for more until they finally have their fill. Drained and pulled apart, the narrator resigns himself to going down with it while watching the other person come undone too.","sonnet":"About the exhaustion of giving to people who only want more — the narrator watches someone fall while noting that what you give is what you get, and arrives at a cold clarity about being done with the dynamic.","src":"haiku"},
"volumes~holywater":{"haiku":"Someone is caught in a noose of jealous hatred; they are tormented and violence simmers, but the narrator refuses to let go.","opus":"A venomous account of betrayal, the narrator surrounded by two-faced people who smile then stab, feeling stale and provoked to violence while a line forms of those who hate him. The chorus about hating in line captures exhausting, unshakable resentment. Volumes bring their signature California metalcore heaviness to this, all groove and menace, the toxic personal warfare fitting neatly within the genre's confessional aggression.","sonnet":"Surrounded by false allegiances — faces that smile while burying a knife — the speaker has hit a breaking point of exhaustion and bewilderment at still being alive. The tone swings between rage at the crowd of enemies and stunned disbelief at one's own survival, with no resolution except a defiant invitation for those who hate to take a number.","src":"haiku"},
"volumes~pixelate":{"haiku":"Numb generation bleeding out meaning in a digitized existence where transmission replaces authentic connection.","opus":"Craving and self-destruction tangled with someone equally broken, the narrator begging to be saved while expecting the worst of himself. A bleak read on a generation born to consume, never free, deadened and pixelated.","sonnet":"A speaker born into a system that breeds rather than frees — force-fed, dependent, unable to see past the digital mediation of reality — pleads for something to believe in and someone to save them from a generation-wide spiritual vacancy.","src":"haiku"},
"volumes~weighted":{"opus":"A depiction of numbness, bitterness and dissociation, the narrator unable to feel his own mind or body, shutting the world out as people get under his skin, sinking to a dangerous low point.","sonnet":"A song about mental paralysis and bitter withdrawal — the narrator is overwhelmed, unable to align thoughts or body, shutting out a world full of people trying to get under their skin, hovering near a breaking point with little left to lose.","src":"sonnet"},
"votum~prey":{"haiku":"A disturbed person hides secrets and kills to feel something, losing control of their violent rage while collapsing into the void.","opus":"A disquieting character study of a man surrounded by concealment whose buried falsehood nourishes something monstrous, who feels unseen even to himself and takes a life just to feel present before coming undone. An uncontrollable fury drags him into oblivion, a mother's figure lingering at the edges. Votum, a Polish prog-metal band, render this cracking mind in melancholy, atmospheric weight.","sonnet":"A killer's interiority — a room full of secrets, the need to feel invisible, and violence as the only available emotion. The switch from third to first person mid-song is the reveal: the observer is the subject, the wrath beyond control, and the fall into the void is both confession and inevitability.","src":"haiku"},
"vower~false-rituals":{"haiku":"Cyclical repetition of the same mistakes through habit; seeking change while resigned to failure, caught in rituals that provide only the illusion of closure.","opus":"A metallic-hardcore reckoning with compulsion and the failure to change. The narrator is drawn back into a chamber of repeated pain, trusting empty habits that offer no closure while time drains away. Recognizing the fault as his own, he circles the question of whether a lonely, cyclical self-loathing is something he can ever actually control.","sonnet":"Habitual, idle patterns consuming time and producing no closure — trust given to routines that deliver nothing, an inability to satiate whatever need drives the cycle. The bed made is the life made, and the speaker knows the fault is their own, yet keeps pushing forward with idle hands and the hunger intact.","src":"haiku"},
"vowws~blood-s-on-fire":{"haiku":"Obsession ignites total consumption; the subject runs overdrive until incineration.","opus":"Obsessive desire as willing self-erasure, letting someone wind them into a walking shadow and run them past all limits, a dark rush where surrendering to a doomed dream feels like being set on fire.","sonnet":"A speaker surrenders completely to an overwhelming attraction — thrown away, wound up, run on overdrive — until the blood is on fire; by the end they find the strength to sleepwalk toward daylight, even as the person takes everything they ever wanted.","src":"haiku"},
"vowws~one-by-one":{"haiku":"Temptation and delights unfolding second by second; reliance on comforting lies. Each moment feels like a lifetime's worth of seduction and desire.","opus":"A seductive, high-wire invitation into temptation, hands placed in fire and desires discovered, where all the narrator wants is a comforting lie and a song toward salvation while delights unfold one by one, each second worth a lifetime of enticement. VOWWS are a dark-pop duo whose noir, cinematic electronics suit this poised, dangerous flirtation between salvation and surrender.","sonnet":"A wry and self-aware hymn to desire and delusion — the speaker wants not truth but a comfortable lie to lean on, and not salvation but the feeling of its approach. Temptations unfold one by one like gifts, each one a lifetime's worth, and the song settles into the honest admission that this is all there is and it is, for now, enough.","src":"haiku"},
"vowws~re-animator":{"haiku":"Death animates the speaker who moves without genuine life, resurrected but not alive.","opus":"A darkwave glide through a lifeless, dreamlike town where the narrator cruises with his gaze lowered, entranced and pulled homeward toward fire. Imagery of a deadened district, a chill breeze and settling old debts conjures a limbo where pleasure and burning merge, playing like a willing march toward some final judgment.","sonnet":"A figure drifting through a dead side of town, hypnotised by skies and flames, circling a state they can neither leave nor fully inhabit. The closing lines name a theatrical trap: the stage you can't escape, pleasure burning, mistakes paid for before an audience of one — existence as a purgatorial performance with no exit.","src":"haiku"},
"voyager-einar-solberg~entropy":{"haiku":"Familiar voice peddles resignation as wisdom; inevitable decay becomes comfort as speaker normalizes dissolution and celebrates entropy as natural peace.","opus":"A weary voice of reason cutting through hype and denial, insisting that the world's supposed order is really entropy, and questioning whether it's worth the effort to matter when it might all amount to nothing.","sonnet":"A familiar voice insists it is the voice of reason and change in a world that feels like make-believe and entropy — the speaker questions whether to make a call, whether anything is really all right, and whether the noise they turn on will be heard at all. The desire to matter underlies every question.","src":"haiku"},
"voyager~brightstar":{"opus":"The Australian prog-pop band frames devotion as astronomy, casting the beloved as the brightest star and a master of the soul worth surrendering to. Science meets worship as the narrator asks to be taken home, offering up his heart. Underneath the synth-driven shimmer runs unease that this dazzling prize belongs to someone else.","sonnet":"An act of devotion to someone perceived as a guiding intelligence or spiritual force — the narrator surrenders their heart to this person who knows everything, framing the relationship in near-cosmic terms as a bright star descending to take them home.","src":"sonnet"},
"voyager~colours":{"haiku":"Colors fade and leave as consciousness dissolves into finality; the living relinquish their hues while hollow prayers echo for borrowed time.","opus":"A drifting, valedictory meditation on letting go, colours running together as people say their last goodbyes, grief and release dissolving individual selves back into a single fading light.","sonnet":"A farewell or departure — someone with pale, fading eyes is being left behind or dying, and the remaining figures become the colors running in the sun, saying goodbye and letting the meaning of what they shared sink into the soul.","src":"haiku"},
"voyager~entropy":{"haiku":"Chaos spreads as familiar voices preach reason while everything crumbles, offering hollow comfort against the inexorable dissolution of certainty and meaning.","opus":"A voice insists on being the familiar reason and change amid noise, questioning whether the world's hype and dread amount to anything or dissolve into entropy.","sonnet":"A voice of reason and change speaks into systemic collapse, addressing someone who insists everything is fine even as entropy dismantles the structures around them, and asks whether anyone will bother to reveal the truth or simply make the call.","src":"haiku"},
"voyager~promise":{"haiku":"Authentic living demands radical breaks and doors slammed shut; only those who've lost themselves completely truly know vitality.","opus":"The Australian synth-metal band frames mortality and closeness as a run of questions about truly being alive: whether you have ever come undone, ached for touch, surrendered a piece of yourself to discover something unfamiliar. Under the anthemic gloss sits a plea to be held to the end and assured, past every shut opportunity, that all will be well.","sonnet":"Two people leaning into the extremes of being alive — shutting doors, losing your mind, praying for human touch, losing a part of yourself to find something new — hold each other through it with the simple repeated pledge \"promise me it's gonna be alright,\" a covenant crossed in \"heart 'til the sky turns red.\"","src":"haiku"},
"voyager~runaway":{"haiku":"One person prevents another from escaping or dismissing a difficult connection; neither can hold power over the other, yet neither gets to run away from the entanglement.","opus":"The Perth prog-pop band, later Australia's Eurovision act, hones a standoff into a hook-heavy demand. The narrator won't let a partner rule him or escape by taking the coward's exit. Attempting it a couple of times and sharing a sour finish, he insists neither of them gets to flee what stands between them, a synth-bright melody carrying stubborn refusal.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who has been using leverage over another person. The tone is firm refusal: the manipulation ends here, the easy escape is off the table, and both parties must face what the relationship actually costs. Distance and tension are present throughout, but so is a refusal to let the other person simply disappear.","src":"haiku"},
"voyager~seasons-of-age":{"haiku":"Time passes through seasons and aging, leaving marks and scars on people who wonder about their history and the choices they made.","opus":"The late-year months anchor a reflection on growing old and what remains behind, minds poised to awaken, lines carving deeper into the faces of loved ones, a modest calm that endures through every season. Australian prog-metal band Voyager give this cyclical musing a synth-bright melancholy, the choices and failures of a lifetime staying opaque as everyone fades and the cold returns.","sonnet":"Time moves through the body as seasonal inevitability — autumn to winter, green to ash, faces acquiring the same lines as those before them. Against this repetition, a small persistent peace appears in all seasons, insufficient to stop the withering but present nonetheless, a mystery tucked into the pattern of every generation's acts and omissions.","src":"haiku"},
"voyager~submarine":{"haiku":"Submersion and waiting alternate as the speaker surfaces for connection then retreats to the deep, forever tethered to another through encrypted desire and temporal suspension.","opus":"A plea to a lover to wait through submersion into madness and darkness, treating a plunge beneath the surface as the only precious time, resurfacing for air and for more of them.","sonnet":"A speaker submerged in longing pleads for the other person to wait — not until they are over the border or under the sea but until they are better — the submarine as a figure for resurfacing, coming up for air just enough to find their way to safe ports.","src":"haiku"},
"voyager~ultraviolet":{"haiku":"Becoming invisible through excess visibility; the speaker transforms into something imperceptible and refined through the world's gaze and pressure.","opus":"A fragile self resisting erasure under scrutiny and judgment, refusing to be silenced or drowned in disbelief while straining for perfect perception, the ultraviolet an unbearable extreme the porcelain narrator won't be flattened into.","sonnet":"A speaker overwhelmed by the modern world's pressure and menacing eyes refuses to be silenced, bathed in disbelief, or rendered invisible — striving for perfect perception down to the colour of ultraviolet, insisting the lights come on even as the pressure threatens to extinguish them.","src":"haiku"},
"voyager~water-over-the-bridge":{"haiku":"Detached acceptance of loss and insignificance; the narrator walks through indifference, letting troubles flow away like water.","opus":"Voyager on numbed surrender, adrift in a stream of not-caring and straining to fit the expected shape while ticking off a countdown. Beneath the synth-pop gloss lies quiet despair struggling to recast loss as something bearable, a beginning barely underway.","sonnet":"A detached, melancholic acceptance of what's lost — the speaker finds a kind of peace in insignificance, counting down from ten, blending into the mold, drifting through the machinery of life while acknowledging they've barely begun.","src":"haiku"},
"vr-sex~live-in-a-dream":{"haiku":"Surface charm hiding spiritual poverty; a shallow figure lives in fantasy while others mistake appearance for substance.","opus":"A cold-eyed sketch of a flashy grifter, moneyed in name but empty inside, whose costly tattoos and car impress and unsettle others while hiding a plea nobody answers. VR Sex, Andrew Clinco's deathrock-tinged project, sneer with noir detachment at this stand-in for genuine affection, warning the deluded showoff that his glitter cuts him off from everyone.","sonnet":"A portrait of a man whose expensive image — car, tattoos, name-dropping — is a facade over poverty of soul and pharmaceutical self-erasure. The song watches him cycle through borrowed glamour and medicated decline, cautioning against mistaking his elevation for anything real.","src":"haiku"},
"vr-sex~rock-n-roll-death":{"opus":"A cold-eyed post-punk elegy for the burnout who outlived his moment, peers gone and applause turned hollow. It traces the slow erosion of a mind chasing faded fame until nothing is left but debt and solitude. A bleak anatomy of rock's promise curdling into isolation, walking the earth alone.","sonnet":"A meditation on the slow, unglamorous decline of aging rock musicians: the scars, the dead peers, the faded fame, the loss of self-control — what awaits at the end of a life built on performance is not glory but an empty stage and a brain that quietly gives out.","src":"sonnet"},
"vrsty~shameless":{"haiku":"A dark, obsessive relationship where the narrator begs to be consumed and destroyed by a manipulative lover who thrives on control.","opus":"A toxic infatuation plays out in surrender, the narrator begging to be buried and doused in kerosene by a woman who cuts deep for the thrill yet makes him feel alive. New York genre-blenders VRSTY fuse metalcore and R&B here, the plea to be lit up and made to believe rendering self-destructive devotion as something almost tender before the fooled-once betrayal turns to blood.","sonnet":"A man knowingly surrenders to a destructive woman — hypnotized, convulsing, asking to be buried — while privately understanding she underestimates him. The dynamic is one of mutual predation: she comes for blood, but he has been here long enough to wear the damage on his face as both wound and weapon.","src":"haiku"},
"vukovi~and-he-lost-his-mind":{"haiku":"Obsession with one thing—a prize, a person—consumes someone's thoughts and destroys their judgment, leaving them mentally ruined from borrowed-time recklessness and waste.","opus":"A bright, chanting pop-rock account of obsession and dissipation, watching people fixate on one wasted pursuit until it blows their minds, the na-na hooks masking a cautionary look at living on borrowed time.","sonnet":"About the consuming, obsessive high of getting wasted — a man and then a woman both become so fixated on a single prize or night of excess that it blows their minds, living on borrowed time in a loop of intoxication.","src":"haiku"},
"vukovi~aura":{"haiku":"Someone with a unique identity is being harmed for it; the song encourages them to stop being afraid, reclaim their power, and reject conformity despite burnout.","opus":"A fierce pep talk to an outsider being cruelly treated, urging them to own their unusual aura, shut out the sheep who don't get them, and stop crying over people unworthy of the pain. The narrator promises solidarity, weirdos following weirdos, and insists they'll get it right. Vukovi, a Scottish alt-rock duo, deliver this defiant self-worth anthem with punchy, glossy hooks.","sonnet":"A pep talk for the one who never fit the group — you have your own look and aura, the conformists have nothing, and the hurt you are carrying from people who never understood you is real but survivable. The stance is solidarity from fellow outsiders, urging the head up and forward, less a comfort than a challenge to stop hurting yourself on behalf of people who were never worth the cost.","src":"haiku"},
"vukovi~behave":{"opus":"A portrait of a toxic, addictive love the singer can't quit, bargaining that she'll behave if only her partner stays; she keeps running away and crawling back to someone who twists the knife, sets her soul alight, and leaves her broken but craving more.","sonnet":"The narrator is addicted to a destructive lover who breaks and burns them — acknowledging the relationship is harmful, that they feel both high and wrecked by it — yet keeps returning and begging for love, promising to behave if only the person will stay.","src":"sonnet"},
"vukovi~boy-george":{"haiku":"The speaker attacks someone's ego, vanity, and failed romantic pursuits, dismissing them as a cheap impostor and unoriginal groupie.","opus":"A scorched-earth takedown of a vain, clingy poser, the narrator branding him a budget groupie and clown while relishing the coming fight. It is pure sneering contempt, aimed at wounded ego and a rejected obsession. Vukovi are a Scottish alt-rock duo fronted by Janine Shilstone, whose brash, hook-driven pop-punk energy and unapologetic attitude give this insult anthem its swagger.","sonnet":"A withering takedown of a vain, delusional social climber who dresses up and chases someone who already rejected them, then performs a love affair for status. The target is called a budget groupie, a clown playing a costume game, and the speaker is neither impressed nor threatened — just ready and willing to bite back.","src":"haiku"},
"vukovi~creep-heat":{"haiku":"A woman confronts and dismantles a predatory man who believed his power and desire gave him access to her — she refuses, names his vile intent, and ends with a confession that his aggression may have crossed a line even she can't fully contain.","opus":"A ferocious kiss-off to a predatory, entitled man. Framed by mock confession, the narrator refuses his advances with contempt, unimpressed by his size and unmoved by his promises of paradise, vowing to set his plague on fire before dismissing him outright. It flips the power dynamic into disgust and defiance, revulsion sharpened into a weapon.","sonnet":"Confrontation with an unwanted predator: the speaker opens with confession then pivots to refusal — your plague set on fire, your two-bit bone set dismissed, bitch bye — but the second half turns inward, the speaker asking forgiveness for a line she crossed, suggesting the boundary between predator and prey was briefly, horrifyingly blurred.","src":"haiku"},
"vukovi~gungho":{"haiku":"Violent desire and war-like passion consume through tactile domination; survival means embracing conflict.","opus":"A charged, adrenal rush of desire staged as combat, the Scottish alt-pop duo draping attraction in rifles, prey and open warfare, the narrator inviting violence and surrender in the same breath. Blood-type bravado and hungry lips collide until appetite and danger are indistinguishable, wanting someone rendered as charging headlong into battle.","sonnet":"Desire pushed to the point of violence and devotion: type-O blood, rifle whip-back, hands around the neck — the speaker declares herself prey and asks to be pulled back with violence, blessing the flesh, the night rain, the red night's beginning, until it tips over into we're living in an act of war.","src":"haiku"},
"vukovi~hades":{"haiku":"Soul-binding contract and emotional imprisonment create inescapable entanglement; the speaker seeks liberation from another's grip while bound by desire and covenant.","opus":"A seductive underworld bargain reworking the Persephone myth into a toxic romance. Pulled back from the dead by a captor who is both enemy and desire, the narrator is drawn through fire and tied to longing, lost and gripped even as they plot escape. Demanding their crown back, they confess loving the trap while begging finally to be let go.","sonnet":"A Faustian deal with desire itself — the narrator sold their soul for warmth, was drawn through fire, and now wants their crown back while still being held by a grip that is enemy and lover simultaneously. \"One taste of blood is not enough\" marks the turning point from willing participant to someone trying to claw free.","src":"haiku"},
"vukovi~hurt":{"haiku":"A sexual assault survivor recounts violation and fear, reclaiming power and promising retribution against their attacker while asserting their survival and strength.","opus":"A survivor faces down her abuser, recalling how her refusal was twisted into consent and swearing to be heard, to rise from the ground and see him ruined. It moves from trauma to fierce reclamation, centering consent and rejecting silence. Vukovi, the Scottish alt-rock duo fronted by Janine Shilstone, turn that defiance into a cathartic revenge anthem of hooks and fury.","sonnet":"A survivor confronting an abuser directly — someone who heard no and took it as yes, who whispered that no one would believe her, whose breath still lingers in her chest like an infection. The song moves from raw memory of fear to fierce promise of exposure and retribution, insisting on being heard and watching the perpetrator burn.","src":"haiku"},
"vukovi~kill-it":{"opus":"A charged, combative song about a consuming toxic bond where the other person drains the narrator's life yet is both friend and foe; she mourns her lost innocence, arms herself for war, and admits a masochistic attraction to the very thing destroying her.","sonnet":"A song about a relationship that is simultaneously an enemy and a source of power — the narrator has grown beyond their innocent past and now longs for open conflict with whoever or whatever has been draining them, issuing a defiant declaration of war using whatever weapons remain. The emotional pull is toward cathartic destruction rather than resolution.","src":"sonnet"},
"vukovi~lasso":{"haiku":"A person rejects objectification and fantasy projection from admirers, claiming their broken interior while warning those who treat them as prizes.","opus":"The Scottish alt-pop duo skewer objectification with venom and glitter. The narrator, cast as a candy-capsule fantasy and worshipped like a god, rejects the pedestal, insisting she is broken and human beneath the gloss. Threatening to bite and bitch-slap anyone who oversteps, she reclaims agency with a snarl. The refrain about being made into popstars turns the spotlight into a cage she refuses to inhabit quietly.","sonnet":"A woman refuses the role of object, idol, or fantasy that others project onto her. She is desired and dehumanised simultaneously, cast as a popstar-god while bleeding privately. Her response is unapologetic anger: she bites back, sets her own terms, and insists that worshipping her image does not mean anyone truly wants to be her.","src":"haiku"},
"vukovi~misty-ecstasy":{"haiku":"Voracious sexuality and sensual dominance, ecstasy achieved through consuming another's body and pleasure.","opus":"A brazen, sensual ode to sex and its little death. Awash in crimson delirium, sirens and a body likened to a great cathedral ceiling, the narrator relishes touch as flawless narcotic, greedy for a lover's taste. The French phrase for orgasm recurs as the goal, the whole track a heady, unabashed celebration of physical bliss pushed to its limit.","sonnet":"An unabashed, full-throttle declaration of sexual devotion — the speaker calls themselves a Sistine Chapel, their partner's touch dripping gold, the sex a \"la petite mort\" red frenzy. There is no conflict or ambivalence, only the insistence that the taste is worth the greed and the blood racing through the fast lane.","src":"haiku"},
"vukovi~quench":{"haiku":"Possession and hunger mask as love-mechanics; the speaker craves touch and fine living while knowing they're dying slowly, thirst driving toward satiation that never arrives.","opus":"A predatory, playful obsession with someone she keeps on a leash, craving their touch and worship while insisting it isn't love, a hungry thirst she needs them to quench without ever admitting feeling.","sonnet":"A speaker is physically overwhelmed by someone's presence — \"your aura is choking me\" — insisting it is not love but pure thirst, daring the other person to move first, walking a knife's edge of desire that she explicitly does not want named as anything deeper.","src":"haiku"},
"vukovi~slo":{"opus":"A dark, erotic account of surrendering control in a toxic, deadly-feeling relationship, equating the lover's poisonous touch with a personal disease the narrator can't live without, unable to bear their absence.","sonnet":"About a relationship so destructive and codependent the narrator experiences it as both poison and life support — they lose control willingly, feel haunted and cursed by the other person's touch, yet cannot survive without them and threatens obsessive possession.","src":"sonnet"},
"vukovi~violent-minds":{"opus":"An alt-rock plunge into a toxic, addictive bond the narrator cannot sever, one she names as tortured and dead inside yet still craves. Framed through unsettling submission and a compulsion to return, it captures the sick thrill of losing oneself to a destructive attachment, mistaking obsession for belonging and calling the darkness home.","sonnet":"A psychologically co-dependent, sexually charged attachment to a destructive person the narrator calls 'daddy' — this figure controls, ignites, and haunts them like a virus, and despite knowing it is toxic and that they will lose everything, the narrator cannot and does not want to cut the line.","src":"sonnet"},
"vulfpeck~animal-spirits":{"haiku":"Cosmic compatibility and astrological destiny shape love's arc; mutual recognition transforms strangers into predestined partners through invisible forces.","opus":"A playful, self-aware love song narrating a romance that hasn't happened yet as if scripting a fated pairing, winking at astrology and pop-song formula while genuinely rooting for two people to get together.","sonnet":"A narrator lovingly orchestrates the eventual meeting of two destined strangers — she with animal spirits, he with heartfelt lyrics — weaving astrology, mutual friends, and economic theory into a playful prophecy of romantic inevitability.","src":"haiku"},
"w-a-s-p~animal-fuck-like-a-beast":{"haiku":"Predatory sexuality and animalistic hunger frame interaction as hunt and conquest; the speaker embodies uncontrolled appetite as identity.","opus":"A deliberately crude, predatory anthem of pure carnal appetite, the singer stalking a partner as a wolf in sheep's clothing driven by animal lust.","sonnet":"A graphic first-person account of predatory sexual desire — the narrator describes arousal, stalking, and forcible sex in the language of a hunting animal. There is no narrative arc or ambiguity; it is a straightforward depiction of rape framed as masculine bestial instinct, delivered with the theatrical shock-rock intent of provocation.","src":"haiku"},
"w-a-s-p~burn":{"haiku":"A damned woman possesses destructive power; no one heeds warnings until she unleashes flames that engulf everything, killing and burning indiscriminately as a form of apocalyptic judgment.","opus":"An apocalyptic vision of a damned woman whose gesture sets the sky red and a city ablaze, the townsfolk who mocked her consumed as she curses them for never learning. It reads like a fire-and-brimstone parable of scorned power. W.A.S.P. are Blackie Lawless's notorious 1980s Los Angeles shock-metal band, and this theatrical, doom-laden storytelling fits their fondness for lurid, damnation-soaked spectacle.","sonnet":"A woman written off as damned raises a fire that swallows an entire city — a warning ignored until it was too late. The story is told from inside the aftermath, past midnight with the sky still red, the survivors left only with the memory of her curse and the sound of the word they should have heeded.","src":"haiku"},
"w-a-s-p~crazy":{"haiku":"A person rejects a romantic pursuer, refusing to reciprocate love and insisting their lover is mistaken about who they are or what they desire.","opus":"A cold rejection of a would-be lover, the narrator insisting he will never be what the other wants and that anyone claiming to love him must be insane. It is defiant and closed-off, love framed as a liar not to be trusted. W.A.S.P., Blackie Lawless's veteran Los Angeles metal outfit, deliver this as swaggering hard-rock bravado, all snarl and emotional armor.","sonnet":"A flat refusal of someone's love — not cruel but absolute. The speaker insists they are not what the other person wants, will not become it, and finds the very declaration of love delusional. It isn't heartbreak or ambivalence; it's a steady, almost exhausted rejection of a dynamic that was never going to work.","src":"haiku"},
"w-a-s-p~i-wanna-be-somebody":{"haiku":"The narrator refuses slavery, starvation, and obscurity—he pursues fame, wealth, and rock-and-roll glory at any cost, dying in flames rather than fading.","opus":"A raw craving for fame and worth from someone who refuses servitude and the scraps of an anonymous working life; the deeper reading is the blue-collar fantasy of escaping obscurity through rock-and-roll excess, opting to blaze out young and celebrated rather than grow old and unseen.","sonnet":"A hungry, unashamed declaration of ambition from someone who grew up with nothing — the narrator refuses menial servitude and poverty and wants fame, wealth, and rock stardom, willing to live fast and die in flames rather than disappear as a nobody.","src":"haiku"},
"w-a-s-p~live-to-die-another-day":{"haiku":"The speaker walks through spiritual adversity, facing demonic threats and death, yet maintains faith that divine power will resurrect and save them beyond earthly destruction.","opus":"A biblically charged battle with death and the devil, the narrator walking through the valley of shadow, resurrected and reaping, vowing to rise again and outgun Satan himself. It reworks the psalm into pulpy heavy-metal defiance of mortality. W.A.S.P. and Blackie Lawless leaned increasingly toward religious and existential grandeur in later work, and this melodramatic redemption arc is very much of that vein.","sonnet":"A spiritual combat narrative — walking through death's shadow with God as a riding companion and the devil in close pursuit. The speaker was hellbound and is now running free, awaiting rapture, meeting the reaper with defiance and a bigger gun. Death is not the end but the doorway to resurrection, and every close call is confirmation of the cycle continuing.","src":"haiku"},
"waax~labrador":{"opus":"The Australian punk band channels self-loathing, disordered eating, and a woman told she isn't welcome in a man's world. A conceding refrain admits defeat to someone deemed better, the narrator packing up to leave. Beneath the anthemic drive runs raw disappointment, gendered exclusion, and the exhausting work of shrinking oneself to fit.","sonnet":"A woman confronting sexism in a creative industry — she's told she'll never get ahead because she's a girl, spirals into disordered eating as a response to feeling worthless, and eventually walks away from a situation where she was never allowed to be enough.","src":"sonnet"},
"wage-war~circle-the-drain":{"haiku":"People fill themselves with lies and chemicals, then ask whether they can change and start over; the past's stain and collective guilt circle endlessly.","opus":"A reckoning with recurring harm and self-inflicted damage, dreaming of a world without cruelty but waking to a history that can't be undone and a mark left on everyone. It wonders whether genuine change and closure are even possible, or whether we just keep spinning downward. Wage War, a Florida metalcore band, balance melodic reflection with breakdown fury across this self-searching cut.","sonnet":"A collective reckoning framed as a choice — a dream of freedom from shared pain wakes into a reality of stained histories and chemical self-medication. The question posed is whether it is too late for a genuine restart, whether collective guilt can be acknowledged and worked through rather than avoided, or whether the whole project of human community simply spirals out.","src":"haiku"},
"wage-war~godspeed-stripped":{"opus":"A metalcore kiss-off stripped to its emotional bone, addressing the manipulator who broke trust and built a fire to burn the relationship down. Betrayal hardens into survival as the narrator wishes his tormentor gone for good. The prevailing feeling is hard-won release, farewell delivered as both curse and liberation from misery.","sonnet":"A breakup song about surviving someone who tried to psychologically destroy you — the narrator refuses to wish the person well as they leave, having been manipulated, broken down, and emotionally buried by them. The 'godspeed' is sarcastic farewell: get out and stay out.","src":"sonnet"},
"wage-war~grave":{"haiku":"Observes someone's unchanging nature and flawed character buried within them, resistant to confrontation or growth. Their core stays intact despite destruction.","opus":"A bitter verdict on someone who never changes, the narrator worn thin by a self-obsessed attention-seeker whose flaws run only skin-deep and will follow them to the grave. Burning pages cannot erase the damage done. Wage War are a Florida metalcore band known for balancing crushing breakdowns with clean melodic hooks, and this cycle of frustration and finality sits comfortably in their modern heavy sound.","sonnet":"A relationship trapped in exhausted recognition — the speaker knows this person will never change, has given everything that could be taken, and watches them return as if nothing happened. The repeated certainty that certain flaws go to the grave reads less like condemnation than like someone finally making peace with the futility of hoping otherwise.","src":"haiku"},
"wage-war~low":{"haiku":"Depression manifests as running away from unwinnable battles and self-inflicted wounds; the narrator feels they've lost everything and desperately seeks rescue from their own despair.","opus":"A raw account of depression and suicidal despair, the narrator lying sleepless with fears he won't voice, forever fleeing fights he cannot win, and feeling he has nothing left. The desperate central question of who could rescue him when he can't rescue himself frames a pain he likens to a disease, with the drop right at his feet.","sonnet":"About bottoming out — intrusive thoughts, sleepless nights, battles you can't win — and the frustration of people offering reassurance when they can't possibly understand how far down you've actually gone.","src":"haiku"},
"wage-war~manic":{"haiku":"The song portrays panic and depersonalization spiraling. Fear runs unchecked; the narrator feels nothing while suspecting madness; demons stalk and time seems to run out.","opus":"A raw first-person account of panic and dissociation, fear hitting like a drug while the narrator stares at walls, numbs out, and quietly admits he does not want to die. The manic refrain captures a mind fraying in real time. Wage War, the Florida metalcore band, pair that mental-health candor with their signature blend of heaviness and melody, making the desperation feel both intimate and huge.","sonnet":"Anxiety that has crossed into dissociation — fear hitting like a drug, sleepless nights staring at walls, numbness where feeling used to be. The speaker tells everyone they are fine while believing they are running out of time. The terror at the center is not dramatic; it is the quiet horror of losing the thread of one's own mind.","src":"haiku"},
"wage-war~prison":{"haiku":"A person trapped in their own mind by internal struggle and emotional pain describes being imprisoned by their own skin and inability to explain their suffering to others. The prison is self-created but inescapable.","opus":"A picture of the mind as a cell of one's own making, the narrator a catastrophe churning under his own skin, unable to voice his suffering to someone who neither grasps it nor dulls it. The confining walls are ones he raised himself, leaving him captive with no other culprit. Wage War drive this claustrophobic mental-health struggle through their Florida metalcore mix of clean melody and crushing heaviness.","sonnet":"Depression described from inside, with no metaphor elaborate enough to make the outside understand — the bars are self-constructed, the chains are interior, and the hell is real but invisible to everyone watching. There is no redemption arc here, only the insistence that this is what it actually is, and the exhaustion of having to explain it to people who will never fully get there.","src":"haiku"},
"wage-war~relapse":{"opus":"A blunt account of falling back into addiction, the narrator refusing to let his damage mend, feeling the pull of the very thing destroying him, and losing ground faster than he gains it. Forsaken by both salvation and damnation, he watches himself slip into darkness, fully conscious of the collapse.","sonnet":"A song about the cyclical return to an addictive pattern — the narrator knows they're an addict but keeps performing denial, taking one step forward and two back until the hourglass runs out. The relapse is both literal substance addiction and emotional dependency, with neither heaven nor hell wanting them back at the end.","src":"sonnet"},
"wage-war~stitch":{"haiku":"Relationship dissolution and the refusal to be saved from self-imposed darkness. The speaker identifies as a mistake and invites the other to leave rather than endure abandonment.","opus":"The wreckage of a three-year relationship is picked over with a wound-and-thread motif, the narrator ripping the stitch out, playing dead, and casting himself as the greatest mistake destined to be run from. Wage War balance metalcore aggression with melodic hooks, letting the bitterness of a knife in the back sit beside a startling final admission that he would have stood by her to hell's gates.","sonnet":"A relationship ends through quiet abandonment rather than confrontation — one person played out the ending while the other only realised too late. Three years collapse into bitterness, the wound reopened by the knowledge that they were never worth saving to the person who left.","src":"haiku"},
"wage-war~who-i-am":{"haiku":"A person fights against judgment and dehumanization, insisting they are human despite others treating them as a source of amusement or as disposable.","opus":"A defense of one's humanity against being judged and objectified: worn down internally by pressure and judgment while not caving on the outside, the narrator insists he's not alive for anyone's amusement. The throughline is reclaiming identity from others' delusions, his open wounds prove he's human, and no one else gets to decide or define who he is.","sonnet":"A direct address to whoever — critics, audience, judgmental outsiders — who project their own ideas onto the narrator without knowing them, insisting on being recognized as a full human being with real wounds rather than a role or a spectacle.","src":"haiku"},
"wait-of-the-world~idk":{"haiku":"Pervasive institutional deception — from doctors to prophets — keeps the population medicated, manipulated, and unable to access truth, while everyone hears only what they want and the lie persists.","opus":"A creeping paranoia about being managed and misled, dressed as spiritual malaise. The narrator senses a withheld truth behind the reassuring voices of doctors, prophets and salesmen, all peddling comfort in place of honesty. It sits in the modern metalcore lineage of distrust anthems, where faith curdles into suspicion and everyone hears only what they wish to.","sonnet":"Paranoia about concealed truth: creeping like dogs in the dark, the doctor offering pills instead of answers, prophets twisting tongues — there's something I don't know that everyone else seems to, and the refrain accumulates into an unnamed conspiracy of information withheld, faith dissolved, truth conditional.","src":"haiku"},
"wake-up-hate~dead-to-me":{"haiku":"Irreversible damage burns bridges while the perpetrator drowns in their own guilt; the speaker chooses erasure and numbing as revenge.","opus":"A scorched-earth farewell to someone whose lies finally collapsed. The narrator, long dismissed as unstable, stands vindicated as the wreckage settles and refuses further contact. Built on the metalcore breakup-as-catharsis template, it trades grief for hardened contempt, framing survival as the quiet triumph of telling the truth against a manipulator's crumbling facade.","sonnet":"The speaker addresses an abuser whose lies have collapsed under their own weight — the \"house of cards\" won't hold, and \"blood on your hands\" is redirected: where the abuser called the victim sick, the victim now simply tells the truth and pronounces the relationship dead, with nothing left but the other person's regret.","src":"haiku"},
"wake-up-hate~monster":{"haiku":"A destructive person embraces their monstrosity and rage as fuel, finding meaning in the power to burn and watching others suffer.","opus":"A defiant embrace of the villain role, the narrator declaring that hate keeps him alive and daring an onlooker who loves to watch him burn, calling the other a leech to be cut away. It is anthemic self-justification through rage. Wake Up Hate are a modern metalcore band, and this blend of vengeful bravado, burning imagery, and clean-sung hooks fits the genre's current appetite for cathartic aggression.","sonnet":"Someone who has stopped apologizing for the fury that keeps them alive — recognizing that a parasite has been feeding on them and cutting it loose without guilt. The accusation of monstrousness is absorbed and owned rather than denied, worn as proof of being the one who is still standing and still fighting.","src":"haiku"},
"wake-up-hate~the-cure":{"haiku":"The speaker searches desperately for a cure to their internal disease, unable to escape infection despite trying every remedy.","opus":"A wrenching portrait of sickness and suicidal despair, the narrator sensing a contagion in his flesh, imploring the heavens for his life and doubting whether living is even worth it. He casts a poisonous influence as a beloved leech and hunts, hopelessly, for a remedy. Wake Up Hate stage this metaphor of disease and addiction in modern metalcore, the melodic anguish sharpening the sense of a battle already lost.","sonnet":"A song of total saturation by an illness — physical, mental, or both — that no prescription can plateau past. The speaker wonders aloud whether they are better off dead, whether anyone would notice, and treats a toxic relationship as a favorite needle while simultaneously searching for an antidote that never appears.","src":"haiku"},
"wake-up-hate~the-panic":{"haiku":"A self-aware person habitually disappoints others and sabotages relationships, drawn to chaos while recognizing their destructive pattern.","opus":"A confession of self-sabotage in love, the narrator admitting he lets a partner down like a habit and half-loves the panic, clinging to the edge because he cannot let things end. He sees them as too different, doomed by his own compulsion. Wake Up Hate deliver this cycle of anxiety and destructive attraction with their modern metalcore mix of heaviness and clean melody.","sonnet":"A relationship where the speaker keeps failing the same way — letting someone down out of what feels like compulsion rather than malice. There is an admission that they might actually love the chaos, that the other person's expectations are impossible to meet, and that the pattern will repeat because the speaker cannot yet want something calmer than this.","src":"haiku"},
"wakusei-abnormal~a-3wg9":{"haiku":"The speaker becomes dog—obedient, loveless, beautiful in captivity. They hunger for acknowledgment from someone indifferent, wearing leash without complaint while emptiness spreads.","opus":"Wakusei abnormal operate at the theatrical edge of Japanese underground rock, and this track leans into that persona of glamorous abjection. The willing collar and the killing love read as extremity for its own sake, yet the ache underneath is ordinary: a plea to be acknowledged by someone who withholds even a touch. The typhoon in the chest is captivity dressed as devotion.","sonnet":"A speaker who wears a collar willingly — placed there by someone who won't even hold her hand — declares love violent enough to kill while demanding to know why she alone receives coldness; the hole blown through her chest by this attachment is a typhoon on the other side, and she asks someone to fill it.","src":"haiku"},
"wakusei-abnormal~a-r003tn":{"haiku":"Themes of death, mortality, or psychological demise; existential darkness permeates the lyrics.","opus":"In wakusei abnormal's grand-guignol register, heartbreak becomes literal surgery. The band favors ornate, violent imagery, and here the stolen, carved heart with its golden brooch turns betrayal into an operating-theater ritual. The demand to have it back, trembling, insists on a strange ethic: hearts may be seized but never surrendered. It is possessiveness reframed as the only dignity left after theft.","sonnet":"A song about emotional theft and surgical revenge — someone stole and carved up the narrator's heart like an operation with a golden brooch, and now the narrator demands it back trembling in their hands, while insisting that hearts may be stolen but must never be let go of in return.","src":"haiku"},
"wale~the-middle-finger":{"haiku":"Rejection of social performance and gratification of others; the speaker values internal truth over external approval or commercial success.","opus":"A defiant, exhausted Wale rejecting rap-scene politics and gossip to answer only to the god in his own mirror, then baring the private grief, addiction and miscarriage he numbs to keep performing success.","sonnet":"Wale vents his alienation from rap industry politics and social performance, positioning himself as someone who trusts only truth, rejects fake alliances, and struggles with the personal cost of ambition — grief, addiction, the hollow thrill of fame — while refusing to follow anyone but the god inside his own mirror.","src":"haiku"},
"walking-across-jupiter~eschatology":{"haiku":"Systems of control keep people complicit and trapped, but inner knowledge of what's right remains, demanding freedom from imposed designs.","opus":"As institutions crumble, a voice urges someone to hold firm and break loose, warning they've been trapped by their own choices and pressured to fall in line against what they sense is right. Walking Across Jupiter's Thai progressive metal frames it as apocalyptic parable, the collapse doubling as a call to reject conformity and reclaim an inner self before reason is wholly surrendered.","sonnet":"A voice urges someone to break free from a system that keeps them compliant, useful, and just passive enough to stay. The personal eschatology is internal: recognise the manipulation, trust what you know is right, and choose rupture over comfortable drowning.","src":"haiku"},
"walking-across-jupiter~neil":{"opus":"A spoken sample (Neil deGrasse Tyson) arguing for scientific literacy as an inoculation against being exploited, and defining the scientific method as the safeguard against being fooled about what is or isn't true.","sonnet":"A spoken-word piece built entirely around Neil deGrasse Tyson's voice, making the case that scientific literacy is the only protection against exploitation by those who profit from ignorance — the one great challenge in life being knowing enough to know you might be wrong.","src":"sonnet"},
"walkways~bleed-out-heal-out":{"haiku":"The speaker bleeds out past trauma and suffering to heal alongside another person, releasing old pain and drama. Healing requires vulnerability and shared bleeding-through.","opus":"A frantic inventory of daily survival that resolves into asking one person to witness the worst of you and stay. Its real subject is intimacy as shared catharsis: healing not by hiding the darkness but by bleeding it out alongside someone who won't flinch.","sonnet":"The chaos of a full life — anxiety, trauma, exhaustion, all the shit you had to swallow — offered up to a partner as raw material; the song asks whether that person can embrace not just the light but the bleeding too, and whether the two of them can let it all go together.","src":"haiku"},
"walkways~hell-born-shove-impossible":{"haiku":"Betrayal replaces protection as the speaker rises from ruins toward confrontation. Impossibility becomes a weapon against the lover who pushed them toward the rocks.","opus":"Turning betrayal into vengeance, the singer who was thrown off a cliff by a cruel lover gathering his ruins to climb back up and shove them down, finding it impossible to understand or stand them.","sonnet":"A speaker who was thrown off their lover's cliff and aimed at the rocks — 'when you could've broken my fall, you aimed me straight at the rocks' — works through the impossibility of understanding that betrayal, until the power turns: the lover 'stands under' while the speaker, gathering their ruins, becomes the one who shoves back.","src":"haiku"},
"wardruna~helvegen-the-way-to-hel":{"haiku":"A guide sings a traveling spirit across the threshold to death, ensuring passage and release from earthly bonds.","opus":"A Norse funeral song guiding the dead along the road to Hel, the living asking who will sing them across, and quoting the Havamal's promise that cattle and kin die but a good name never does.","sonnet":"In Old Norse and Norwegian, a funeral escort song in which a singer asks who will guide the dying across Helvegen — the road to Hel — and answers that they themselves will accompany the soul over the Gjaller Bridge through song. The closing Havamal stanzas frame the journey: cattle die, kin die, but a good name is eternal.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~70-dead-with-corey-taylor":{"src":"web","web":"A cry-out at a world gone mad — war, disease, inequality — hung on the hook \"some nights I feel alive but I'm 70% dead\": the numbness of living through collapse, where being two-thirds gone is the baseline and a spark of feeling alive is the rare exception worth naming.","fable":"A nihilist glamour anthem whose whole surface is beauty, because beauty is the only thing left standing once sensation has bled out — being seventy percent gone is the diagnosis, and the mantra of looking gorgeous is cosmetic putty smeared over the hole. Wargasm fuse burnout, doomscroll paralysis of a planet ablaze with the air thickening until it's hard to breathe, and self-commodification into one motion. The invitation to strip out the flesh, the cash, the last shred of autonomy and remake them as filth reads the modern bargain as a corpse parted out for scrap, where being devoured gets mistaken for being wanted. The chorus's meanest twist is that mortality, normally a spur to grab at life, works here as a permit for the deadness — you photograph nicer once nothing counts — while the couplet about lying sleepless against a firearm's muzzle strips the pose off entirely. Corey Taylor's arrival sharpens rather than softens, a lifer of exactly this self-immolating rock stepping in to co-sign the fatigue. The track plays as two generations shaking hands over the fact that the prettiness was always a sheet thrown over a chamber round.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"The collaboration began on Corey Taylor's 2023 US tour, where Wargasm supported and Sam Matlock jumped on the mic mid-set.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance","politics & society","suicide"],"means":"mortality repurposed as a permit for deadness rather than a spur to live","built":"Corey Taylor's arrival as co-signer sharpens the fatigue — a lifer confirming the prettiness was always a sheet over a chamber round"},
"wargasm~backyard-bastards":{"haiku":"A violent fantasy about rising up against oppression and reclaiming power through destruction, featuring female revenge figures weaponized against those in authority.","opus":"A gleeful vengeance fantasy hands its heroine a sparkling axe and sends her hunting men, torn between a fierce lust to kill and the knowledge that it's wrong, all while summoning a bloodline of avenging screen women. WARGASM's electro-punk brashness fits this camp bloodlust, the UK duo tipping their hat to vengeful film heroines and announcing, all snarl and swagger, that peace was never on the table.","sonnet":"Channelling cinematic female avengers, a voice weaponises rage at exploitation into a darkly gleeful fantasy of reclaimed power. The violence is framed as political — killing the god, having no masters — even as the speaker admits the impulse is real and the restraint is thin.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~bad-seed":{"haiku":"Spreading dysfunction breeds mutual contempt; the speaker weaponizes sexual aggression and violence while pursuing those marked by their own corruption.","opus":"A taunting, violent flirtation with a motor-mouthed rival, daring them to keep running their mouth until the last one left standing takes a busted lip. The Britney and paparazzi references turn the aggression into gleeful, provocative pop-punk theater.","sonnet":"A confrontational track about someone whose aggressive, loud presence keeps antagonizing the narrator until the threat of physical violence becomes explicit — the narrator warns repeatedly that the last person standing gets a fat lip, while the subject just keeps talking and tugging. The second half flips to self-description: the narrator is a bad seed themselves, spreading chaos, impervious to hatred, swinging at anyone who gets too close.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~bang-ya-head-feat-fred-durst":{"haiku":"Relentless pressure and burnout consume from all sides; the speaker resists violent capitulation despite the system's grinding force.","opus":"Grind culture skewered as literal self-harm, buying overhyped junk and working yourself to a broken neck because everyone's killing themselves just to get by, the head-banging both party and self-destruction under a system that takes whatever rest is left.","sonnet":"About selling out and being consumed by a culture industry that packages identity and rebellion as branded product — the speaker sold their soul at a discount, was handed someone else's vomit dressed in luxury goods, and is now being torn apart by the machinery they trusted with their image.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~circle-pit":{"haiku":"Collective exhaustion and meaninglessness unite the damaged; shared suffering becomes the only authenticity available.","opus":"Nihilist mosh-pit catharsis for people who can't keep still in a world where nothing's genuine or real and it never improves. The bilingual refrains concede that no one cares and life is draining and violent — so you spin the pit together, the last togetherness on offer.","sonnet":"About numbing out at shows with people who are similarly detached from feeling — born on pills, feeling and fearing nothing, the speaker addresses a crowd that is spinning together in a circle pit precisely because that physical momentum is the only available substitute for meaning; the Japanese interjections reinforce the universality of the nihilism.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~d-r-i-l-d-o": {"haiku":"Numbness and repetition breed contempt for everything including feeling, the speaker wallows in vice while cataloguing those who abandoned them.","opus":"Numbed-out nihilism boiled down to a blunt little creed of vice, a burial ground for ambition that just wants to stop thinking. The scavenging bravado masks someone stripped bare and abandoned by others, defensiveness and depression worn as a whole way of life.","sonnet":"A nihilistic portrait of someone with no ambitions left, existing in a state of deliberate sensory overload — drinking, fighting, and fucking as the only way to feel anything in a life the speaker has surrendered entirely to chaos and animal appetite.","src":"haiku","fable":"The four-verb chant is sold as swagger but works as anaesthetic: the speaker reaches for drink and violence and sex precisely because he cannot think, and thinking is where the real content lives. What waits there is a flat anhedonic depression that calls itself a plot where nothing will grow, a dead field over every drive he used to have, and it recoils from the loop it is trapped in — a loop the song then enacts by repeating the mantra past the point of pleasure into compulsion. Wargasm keep the surface all bravado and provocation, the register of the mixtape's whole shock-punk pose, but the verses keep leaking the underneath: someone begging to be cut off, sleeping armed and paranoid, keeping a ledger of everyone who wronged him and mock-signing it as Christ. The sharpest turn is the reframe near the end. This misery isn't a passing mood but a fault built into how he was assembled, a defect you're stuck with forever unless you shove hard against whatever leans on you — so the chant lands not as celebration but as the only release-valve a broken wiring has left, and the more he shouts it the more it reads as a man drowning out his own head.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind","identity & becoming"],"means":"bravado chant used to drown out the inner void","built":"mantra repeated past pleasure into compulsion enacts the trap"},
"wargasm~death-rattle":{"haiku":"Lovers embrace oblivion together as the world collapses, finding romance in societal ruin and making defiant beauty from catastrophe without apology.","opus":"Two lovers treat mutual and global destruction as the ultimate turn-on, racing toward apocalypse because ruin feels more romantic than restraint. The end of the world becomes the backdrop for a reckless, exhilarated death wish shared with someone.","sonnet":"Two people in a doomed, mutually destructive romance decide to embrace the end together rather than resist it — crashing cars, dancing on their enemies' graves, scorching everything in their path, finding the apocalypse romantic precisely because they are going down on their own terms.","src":"haiku","fable":"This welds apocalypse and infatuation into one gesture: the world's end and a doomed romance run on the same adrenaline, and the whole case is that if collapse is inbound, plummeting toward it together — reckless, in love, defiant — beats outliving it untouched. The verses keep the two registers fused on purpose, waltzing across the graves of everyone who forbade them while the glow off a ruined earth reads to the lovers as tender, so the title lands as the ugly punchline: a dying breath's final gurgle received as a genuinely thrilling sound, the last intimacy on offer. The mock-announcer flourish — a showman crowing that the couple have at last engineered their own undoing — fixes the mood as gleeful nihilism toward human self-destruction, the pair electing to burn out numb rather than grieve, adrenaline pitched as the only honest answer to an ending nobody can halt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","freedom & escape","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"apocalypse and infatuation run on the same adrenaline on purpose","built":"the mock-announcer flourish fixes gleeful nihilism as the emotional register, not irony at its expense"},
"wargasm~do-it-so-good":{"haiku":"Excellence becomes a weapon that attracts and repels simultaneously; the speaker's superiority isolates them within a hierarchy they dominate.","opus":"Pure predatory swagger, the narrator bragging that she outclasses everyone, provoking envy and lust while warning she arrived hungry for violence. Others may mimic the surface but can never reproduce the real thing, and the sheer confidence is the entire point.","sonnet":"A self-assured, sexually charged power anthem — the speaker claims total dominance in any room and any encounter, referencing iconic tough women, wearing weaponized glamour, and daring anyone to compete while making it clear they are simply better than everyone present.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~feral":{"haiku":"Reckless pleasure and mutual dissolution create ecstatic chaos; two people abandon control and disappear into each other.","opus":"A punk-electronic plunge into a reckless, doomed hookup — surrendering to a bad-idea attraction that cannot survive the morning, all adrenaline and indulgence with no brakes. Bookended by absurd celebrity banter, it revels in its own bad decisions.","sonnet":"A song about dissociation and losing oneself in physical, sexual oblivion with another person — the speaker is out of their mind, slipping out of space and time, and the only thing they want to do is get lost with this person for days in a crowd or in each other.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~fukstar": {"haiku":"Worship of false gods and empty consumption spreads through society while meaning erodes, those who mistake wealth for divinity ignore that gods can die.","opus":"A nihilist takedown of worshipped wealth and fame, telling the moneyed idol they're no deity, just one more burnout. The narrator takes solace in chaos and points out that even gods die, mocking the faithful who'd never notice if their temple went up in flames.","sonnet":"A skewering of tech-bro and celebrity culture: the speaker lounges in apocalyptic nihilism while lampooning the delusion of powerful men who think their money and fast cars make them gods, presenting herself as self-aware in her own chaos while they remain blind to theirs.","src":"haiku","fable":"A sneer dressed as a confession: the opening verse wears the mask of the very thing it hates, cheerfully swearing allegiance to the boardroom and handing over all its money while it boils the sea to glass — devotion voiced with a straight face so its disgust reads clearest. Underneath the pose runs the actual argument, a takedown of the moneyed idol whose garaged supercars and worship don't make him divine, only another burnt-out star, and the duo drive it home by warning that even deities are mortal. The middle sags into a deliberate torpor — days wasted sleeping in, the slow pull of drowning preferred to waking — because the alternative is seeing clearly, and the one moment of clarity is the fantasy of desecrating a church nobody would even notice, the point being that the faithful aren't watching anything real. By the close the sardonic energy curdles into genuine nihilism: faith placed in chaos, dealt a losing hand from birth, and the stars visibly draining off a darkening ceiling, so the title's spelled-out splurge-now-cling-later chant lands less as a boast than as an epitaph for the idol and the worshippers alike.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","politics & society","alienation & emptiness","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"sincere-sounding devotion that exposes its own contempt","built":"sardonic allegiance verse undercuts the idol it performs"},
"wargasm~god-of-war":{"haiku":"An abused person snaps under sustained pressure and cruelty, transforming their breaking point into violent retaliation and reclaiming power.","opus":"A nu-metal revival cut about reaching a breaking point in an abusive dynamic, the narrator who bent over backwards finally snapping and turning the aggression back on the person who kept pushing, self-aware enough to wink at its own riffage.","sonnet":"A person who has been endlessly exploited and pushed down finally snaps — declaring that they've turned into something dangerous, ready to fight back without a plan and with nothing left to lose.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~gold-gold-gold":{"haiku":"Mortality and dust render all treasure worthless; the speaker offers their kingdom knowingly, accepting that at the end nothing matters.","opus":"A nihilist funeral fantasy gilding a worthless heart in gold before sinking it, insisting that at the end we trust in nothing and all turns to dust. Wargasm swing between blasphemy and blank abandon, gold as fool's-gold vanity over shared, certain loss.","sonnet":"A nihilistic meditation on mortality and meaninglessness — everything is fool's gold, everyone turns to dust, there's nothing sacred and nothing trustworthy, and the narrator gives away what little kingdom they have knowing it's all worthless in the end.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~lapdance":{"haiku":"A provocative track about defiance and contempt for authority. The speaker presents themselves as dangerous and transgressive while comparing government and politicians to strippers—all performing for an audience they disdain.","opus":"A brash, provocative boast of an outlaw persona armed with guns, riding a motorcycle, disgusted by politicians who sound like strippers, and taunting a desirous listener with the offer of a free lap dance.","sonnet":"A defiant, confrontational song that compares politicians and authority figures to strippers — all performance and empty promises — while the narrator presents himself as an armed, lawless outlaw who will not be told what to do by a society that makes people want to kill.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~minigun":{"haiku":"Disaster becomes master and teacher, the speaker courts pain as the only language that makes them feel real.","opus":"Masochistic devotion as identity, the speaker feeling wanted only in pain and pleading for a lover to rule over their wreckage. A hollow figure carrying their secret torment with style, they crave the hurt that makes the relationship feel like something crafted and beautiful.","sonnet":"A self-lacerating portrait of someone whose suffering has become a spectacle — cutting back to bare wires, bleeding on the carpet, wearing private hell as an attractive accessory, while addressing a partner who only wants them when they're in pain, the speaker performing misery as an act of intimacy.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~modern-love":{"haiku":"Mutual harm becomes romance's foundation; love manifests as careful cruelty and controlled violence between two willing combatants.","opus":"Love reframed as war—two people who hurt themselves in strange ways and love the pain, drawn to the places others hate them. Care and burial become interchangeable in a relationship where devotion means running toward a mutual end.","sonnet":"A love song that equates intimacy with violence and mutual destruction — the two people hurt themselves in subtle, strange ways for each other, bleed together on the floor, and the speaker frames caring for this person as indistinguishable from burying them; modern love is war, and they are running toward it anyway.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~outrage":{"haiku":"Systemic violence and abuse create cycles of rage where victimization transforms into a call for destruction and revenge against those in power.","opus":"Rage as a generational condition, the older you get the more it hurts, turning people into improvised bombs primed to go off at a corrupt establishment. The only choices offered are to hold your tongue or take the hit.","sonnet":"Rage at a system that leaves no clean outlet — you either swallow your anger or bite down on a bullet — escalating into a call for freaks and martyrs to tear down the ruling class, with fury breeding more pain the older it gets.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~post-modern-rhapsody":{"haiku":"Alienation from manufactured culture; a person consciously disconnected from superficial trends yet overwhelmed by invasive technology and constant surveillance that disrupts their ability to think clearly.","opus":"A furious satire of digital-age alienation, the narrator half-machine and unable to feel compassion for celebrity culture, drowning in notifications, tragedy-as-content and surveillance. Wargasm diagnose the modern self as a wired, dopamine-sick amputee begging to switch off.","sonnet":"A critique of social media culture, reality TV, and algorithmic identity — the narrator describes becoming psychosomatic from the noise of performative online life, unable to switch off, self-diagnosing as a \"21st century amputee\" while sardonically asking if you love yourself.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~pyro-pyro":{"haiku":"Mental breakdown and burning down systems while drowning in despair.","opus":"Feeling trapped, suffocated and driven to the brink of insanity, threatening to burn everything down if kept caged.","sonnet":"Pandemic-era lockdown rage — claustrophobia, collective mental breakdown, and the urge to burn down the system confining everyone.","src":"haiku","fable":"A lockdown fever-dream that hears confinement as a fuse hissing toward its charge — cabin madness swelling into the itch to set the whole apparatus alight. The pandemic markers are unmistakable: four months sealed indoors, disinfectant fumes leaching into sleep, the plague-cart summons to carry out the corpses, bodies scrubbing the living day off themselves and crawling under the covers again. Out of that stir-crazy squeeze the song assembles a riot arithmetic — cage a person long enough and arson becomes their only remaining verb — the self severed and disposable, a body of paper cut loose from the great grinding mechanism it once served. The hook's rhyme of psycho and pyro compresses the whole argument: derangement and flame as one accelerating answer to a place that spins quicker because it's collapsing, glows harder because it's alight, and stands too vast to be brought down by anything short of a struck match.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","freedom & escape","violence & murder"],"means":"confinement compressed until arson becomes the only remaining verb","built":"the hook's rhyme of psycho and pyro fuses derangement and flame as one accelerating answer"},
"wargasm~rage-all-over":{"haiku":"A young, furious generation destroyed by misinformation and designed to fail fights back; they're done being exploited and ready to tear down what they were taught to despise.","opus":"A generational battle cry channeling misinformation, alienation and rage against a system that sold false sacred values, casting the young and the damned as the violent reaction to what they were taught to despise.","sonnet":"A generation raised on misinformation and systemic failure declares itself beyond anger — describing itself as pure rage — and issues a threat to tear down the structures sold to them as sacred. The song maps a young, damned cohort that feels wasted, isolated despite connectivity, and ready for confrontation.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~ride-the-thunder":{"haiku":"Debauchery and possession intertwine as the speaker rides lightning into oblivion, cutting out their own voice to become pure instrument of excess.","opus":"A high-octane fusion of sex, violence and speed, the body as polished metal and the veins as fuel, tearing through a debauched, occult rite. The escalating rush and the plea for someone to halt the momentum make excess itself the drug being pursued.","sonnet":"A high-voltage song about transgressive, taboo-laden sex — the speaker frames their body as gasoline and chrome, the encounter as rock-and-roll role-play, explicit and charged, sleeping with someone in a state of mutual dissolution at 3am, presenting debauchery as a kind of sacred rite.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~s-a-d":{"haiku":"Self-hatred wears manufactured skin: constructed personas collapse into contempt, and the only escape is through self-destruction presented as clarity.","opus":"Manufactured identity and self-hatred bundled together, a person whose thoughts, skin, and personality come pre-packaged from magazines and cans, dressing up in tragedy while eyeing self-destruction and dreading their own company.","sonnet":"A song about self-loathing constructed from external sources — the speaker gets their personality from magazines, their appearance from a spray can, starves for days, and fantasizes about today being the day they stop existing, calling it self-assured destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~salma-hayek": {"haiku":"The speaker is caught between destructive desire, self-hatred, and the despair of never achieving happiness or significance compared to idolized figures.","opus":"A brash, provocative track about seduction, self-loathing, and craving attention, where the swaggering narrator embraces decay, likens herself to a snake and a false messiah, yet admits others will always be happier than she is.","sonnet":"A narrator oscillates between aggressive self-assertion and emotional numbness — leaning into decay and chaos as a daily reality, comparing their physical and psychological intensity to Salma Hayek's iconic snake scene, while admitting they no longer want to feel anything.","src":"haiku","fable":"A duet built on provocation as a shared performance: both voices brag that every utterance lands like a burst of gunfire, then quietly admit that if forced to actually mean it, they would have nothing to say. The central image borrows the snake-dancer scene that made Salma Hayek an icon — the serpent coiled at the throat as seduction, worship, and slow strangulation all at once — and the song works the ambiguity hard, so that being adored and being choked are the same gesture. Its structure earns the read: the verse is sung first by the woman as the object men fixate on, then handed to the man singing it back, the POV swap exposing how the femme-fatale mystique is a costume either party can wear. Underneath the bravado and the chanted vow to rot away willingly the mask slips, and the real confession is exhaustion — the refusal to feel anything anymore, the flat certainty that dead glamour like Hayek or James Dean will always be happier than the person performing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"glamour-as-seduction revealed as mutual exhaustion underneath","built":"pov swap between verses exposes the femme-fatale as a shared costume"},
"wargasm~scratchcard-feeling":{"haiku":"Desire for transformation and risk-taking drives the speaker toward danger; they dance with self-destruction, seeking thrill and escape while suspecting their audience celebrates their downfall. Masochism becomes performance.","opus":"The London duo taps into a gambler's high of self-destruction, chasing cheap kicks and pills on a do-or-die night. Reveling with their own inner demons and casting their ruin as the crowd's craving, they perform fame as a fabrication. The title names luck as a drug, ordinary struggle rebuilt into a way of life, defiance sneering at anyone hoping to watch them fall.","sonnet":"A pair of self-aware entertainers live by cheap thrills and masochistic momentum, dancing with their demons while watching the world that dismissed them begin to drown. The scratchcard is the whole of modern life: luck-dependent, addictive, ultimately corrosive.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~sombre-goodbye":{"haiku":"Dismissing trends and asserting raw power; reclaiming music as dangerous force not fashion.","opus":"A rebound confession, loving someone only because she resembled the one who left, then abruptly detonating the sad song into brash self-parody, the tenderness mocked and buried under noise and bravado.","sonnet":"A brief, brutal self-interruption — the song begins with a line about only loving someone as a substitute for another person, then the band breaks in to trash the sentiment and reassert their aggressive identity, treating the vulnerable moment as unworthy of them.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~sonic-dog-tag":{"haiku":"Intimate destruction between incompatible lovers who communicate excessively yet fail fundamentally, consuming each other until nothing remains.","opus":"The UK electronicore duo turn a doomed romance into wreckage. Two incompatible people grind each other down to the marrow, unable to just admit their feelings, and the exhausted refusal to give up curdles into crash imagery where letting go and meeting the glass at speed become the same release.","sonnet":"Two fundamentally incompatible people are locked in a relationship that has ground them both down through cycles of pushing and pulling, the other person consuming while the speaker shuts down. The song circles the point of finally letting go while acknowledging the stalemate of never saying what either truly feels before it's too late.","src":"web","web":"Two people in a relationship where every exchange is both too much and not enough — one armour-plated, the other falling apart — the moment of letting go of a dynamic that only ever drew blood.","fable":"A toxic-love song about two people who can only reach each other by crashing into each other — mismatched partners who confuse the abrasion for closeness, shoving and dragging one another raw and calling the wreckage a system for surviving their grief. The lyric borrows its central paradox, one word shifted, from Tool's Schism — the certainty that two halves join precisely because you saw them come undone — to frame a bond whose sole evidence of connection is its own breakage. The asymmetry between one partner welded shut in armour and the other left exposed and inflamed is the real diagnosis: both bellow at a volume that guarantees neither is heard, devotion confessed in the same breath as a demand for silence. It steers toward a wreck — releasing the wheel so both their skulls punch the glass — and that image reads the affair honestly: this is not a love anyone repairs, only one they ride out or total, and his looped confession that he's wrecked inside is as near as either comes to admitting anything true.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"abrasion mistaken for closeness, wreckage called a system","built":"the central paradox borrowed one word shifted from Tool's Schism, framing the bond's only evidence as its own breakage"},
"wargasm~spit":{"haiku":"The speaker is disgusted by social corruption, artificial behavior, and environmental destruction, wanting to vomit out the poison of this world. The song tracks how disposable relationships mask deeper contempt, and concludes that some generations are sacrificed to systems of profit.","opus":"Disgust at a numbed, hypocritical, environmentally doomed society, its felled forests, sparks nausea in the narrator, mixed with self-loathing over the hatred he chokes down. The devastating turn reveals the real wound: he only fell for a new partner because she echoed his ex, suspecting she did the same, so his revulsion at the world is really revulsion at their empty, stand-in love.","sonnet":"Two songs stitched together: the first is a social disgust anthem about a rotting, anti-social world that makes the speaker want to spit; then it pivots hard into a confession about loving a substitute — pursuing someone because they resembled an ex, and wondering if that ex did the same with someone else. The second half is the more cutting emotional truth.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~spit-hyper-s-deconstruction-remix":{"haiku":"Corruption and anti-social impulses metastasize through repetition; the speaker chokes on cultural rot and projected desire, reduced to regurgitation.","opus":"Disgust turned inward and outward at once, boredom festering into rage against a rotting, corrupted world. The narrator confesses loving a substitute only for resembling someone else, a self-loathing kernel wrapped in anti-social snarl. Wargasm's electro-punk aggression amplifies the alienation into something grotesque and gleefully unhinged, spitting out what it was supposed to swallow.","sonnet":"A spit of disgust at a nation rusted by repetition and grotesque distortion — the speaker has coiled too long in corruption and it has to stop. The twist is personal: \"I only loved her 'cause she looked like you,\" turning political nausea into displaced desire, the anti-social outburst rooted in something much more private.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~super-fiend": {"haiku":"Boredom breeds contempt while the speaker transforms into a villain, finding vitality in destruction and refusal, too tired to care about redemption.","opus":"A villain reveling in being unlovable, past anger and into boredom, so worn down by an ex's lovesick clinging that they'd rather lean fully into the monster role than pretend to be anyone's friend.","sonnet":"A self-aware portrait of someone raised in chaos and violence who has internalized destruction as their default mode — but the song's real move is exhausted detachment: they're no longer angry, just bored of dying the same death, tired of the other person's neediness.","src":"haiku","fable":"A breakup kiss-off that curdles into a full origin story: the speaker stops trying to hurt the ex back and instead announces a promotion from wounded party to cartoon antagonist, complete with an entrance fanfare cued to their own arrival and a wardrobe of black. The song keeps insisting the speaker feels no rage, only a flat indifference, and that insistence is where its argument turns — it protests too much, and the deadpan cruelty around it (marching you to the water's edge, then pinning you under so you still won't drink) exposes the indifference as a pose worn over exhaustion from dying the same death on repeat. Between the verses two image-families collide: the ex reduced to a leaking, twitching reanimated corpse that has learned to speak, and the speaker gleefully casting themselves as the villain because the villain's fit is hotter and gets the last word. Turning into a super fiend is offered as liberation, but the song's buried claim is that becoming the monster is what's left when being the victim has been drained of any payoff — the mask is the only power still on the table.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance"],"means":"exhaustion wearing indifference as a costume","built":"villain-persona adopted as the only power remaining"},
"wargasm~venom":{"haiku":"Existential malaise and self-perceived toxicity—a damaged consciousness convinced the world improves without their presence.","opus":"WARGASM weaponize being feared and othered into a swaggering anthem. A frayed narrator raised on television, hallucinating a figure in the corner, flips his damage into predatory pride, daring the outsider to sink the fangs in and reframing menace as poison the whole crew shares.","sonnet":"The narrator exists in a state of dissociation and low-grade self-loathing, plagued by sleeplessness and violent intrusive thoughts, raising a weapon against a shadowy threat. From this fractured interior the song pivots to a defiant collective identity — a group who carries poison in their blood not as weakness but as power, daring the world to come close and get bitten.","src":"web","web":"A snarling us-versus-them anthem of generational disenfranchisement — the whole record framed as something poisonous needing an antidote, this the distilled concentrate of youthful rage at being shut out.","fable":"A snarling us-against-them anthem that forges private damage into a membership card — a raddled, TV-reared, insomniac psyche flipped into a mark of kinship with anyone else carrying the same toxin in their veins. What begins as personal collapse (a skull leaking like a burst vessel, a whole life waved off as a brief error of judgment, boredom souring into fantasies of betrayal) turns outward into predatory swagger: the bared fangs and the dare to be bitten collapse self-harm and seduction into a single move, venom offered as intimacy. The chorus's real trick is its pronoun drift — I have it, you have it, all of us have it — enlisting the listener into the affliction until ruin becomes a clan, a shared contamination that flatters the outcast (they fear you, so they resent you) while barely covering the self-disgust below. The face and what festers behind it never settle: swallow the toxin, draw it deep into the lungs is at once a brag and a farewell note, a pack's bravado assembled entirely out of one person's wreckage.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"The opening self-diagnosis wears Pink Floyd's album title A Momentary Lapse of Reason, and the closing race borrows Van Halen's Runnin' with the Devil — identity assembled from inherited rock shards, the duo's signature move.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"private damage forged into a membership card for the outcast","built":"the chorus drifts from I to you to all of us, enlisting the listener into the affliction until ruin becomes a clan"},
"wargasm~vigilantes":{"haiku":"Anger becomes fuel and escape mechanism; the speaker moves continuously, fueled by rage to elude capture while becoming increasingly untethered.","opus":"Anger weaponized as fuel, a self-absorbed outlaw with a savior complex operating like a machine and staying in perpetual motion so no one can pin them down, choosing to get loaded and survive over getting better.","sonnet":"About channeling rage into relentless forward motion — the speakers use anger as fuel to keep moving like a machine, refusing to be caught or controlled, embracing a messiah complex and self-destruction as a lifestyle.","src":"haiku"},
"wargasm~your-patron-saints":{"haiku":"The speaker declares themselves patron saint of blood, sex, and disappointment. The song laments lost faith in friends and saints, embracing moral free fall.","opus":"A blasphemous, hedonistic strut in which the narrator anoints themselves a mock saint of vice, disappointment, and excess, forged in fire and poison and beyond control. Having abandoned love and lost trust in friends and holy figures, they swap devotion for touch and drugs, running with their own demons and asking whether anyone out there still bothers to pray.","sonnet":"A declaration of faithlessness and deliberate debasement: having given up on love and God alike, the narrator crowns themselves patron saint of disappointment, choosing drugs and touch over meaning, and daring anyone watching to pray for them.","src":"haiku"},
"warrel-dane~messenger":{"haiku":"The speaker condemns someone who plays the martyr and victim, exposing their lies, envy, and self-deception about their past influence.","opus":"The late Nevermore vocalist faces a chronic martyr who reclimbs their symbolic torment and plays wounded party in an ordeal existing purely in their imagination. Recalling lean days when the two believed themselves special, the narrator laments corroded principles and the marks of squandered youth beyond any repair. Dane's theatrical delivery renders the reckoning tired rather than vicious, wondering when faith might ever arrive.","sonnet":"A narrator strips away the self-pity of someone who keeps casting themselves as victim — calling out the performance, the soiled martyrdom, the envy — while mourning what both of them once shared when hunger was all they had.","src":"haiku"},
"warrel-dane~when-we-pray":{"haiku":"Prayer proves useless against systemic greed and corruption; the world remains ruled by thieves while people accept helplessness.","opus":"The late Nevermore vocalist's bitter reckoning that prayer changes nothing in a world run by the greedy and powerful, indicting his own complicity as a slave feeding the fire; despair aimed at both the corrupt system and the comfort of faith.","sonnet":"A politically disillusioned song arguing that prayer changes nothing in a world run by fools and greedy thieves — the narrator is a slave feeding the system's fire and asks whether citizens truly feel betrayed, concluding that spiritual appeals alone are worthless against structural corruption.","src":"haiku"},
"wars~scorn-and-fidelity":{"opus":"An internal struggle with an unchangeable inner voice and a cloud of past mistakes, questioning whether one can live buried beneath one's own guilt; the singer senses the answer and the way out are inside the nightmare itself, yet fears he's already become a fading ghost.","sonnet":"A person trapped under the weight of their own past mistakes and the voice in their head that will not change — they know the answer is inside them but cannot wake from the nightmare of self-recrimination, feeling like a ghost wandering through the maps of their own hell.","src":"sonnet"},
"washed-out~all-i-know":{"haiku":"Dependence calcifies into totality; the speaker cannot imagine existence beyond this person, despite clear deterioration.","opus":"The paralysis of a breakup where one person has clearly moved on, the narrator haunted by a fading presence he can't release because that lost love was the only thing that ever defined him.","sonnet":"Someone is trying to let go of a person who has already moved on, unable to accept that the life they shared is over while their mind replays the other's fading presence. The person they lost tells them to move on, but the emotional reality refuses to comply.","src":"haiku"},
"water-from-your-eyes~playing-classics":{"haiku":"Crisis and decay reflected in artistic ambition, the speaker confronting crash of ideals and the long difficult path from confusion to truth.","opus":"An oblique reflection on the draining gap between crisis and clarity, staged like a performance falling apart before an audience. Amid splintered images of collision and smoke, a yearning for peace vies with the grueling journey toward honesty. True to the Brooklyn duo's deadpan art-pop, meaning surfaces sidelong, through repetition and counting rather than confession.","sonnet":"Performance as combat and collapse — a war on stage, smoke the glow to ash, the long hard road from here to the truth measured in numbered shakes. The narrator is both concerned observer and participant, watching crisis unfold in real time under spotlights.","src":"haiku"},
"waves-in-autumn~yellowstone":{"haiku":"Freedom means rejecting imposed conformity and reclaiming one's authentic nature; the speaker defies those who cage and pathologize natural existence. Wilderness and wildness are sacred.","opus":"A hymn to wildness and self-authorship, the narrator swearing to reach the finish on his own terms and reject anything domesticated or conventional. Yellowstone stands in for untouched wilderness where the self stays pure and unfenced, no weapons or hunters allowed. Set against those who erode freedom and drift through life aimless, it treats the wild as both sanctuary and identity, and flatly refuses any cage.","sonnet":"A rejection of conformity framed as a return to wild, undivided nature — the speaker insists on living as their native self in open land, free from the cages others mistake for civilization. The wilderness becomes both literal refuge and philosophy.","src":"haiku"},
"wayside~can-you-see-it":{"haiku":"Fragmentation and buried secrets corrode the self; the speaker invites dissection to reveal what's missing, compounded by inability to communicate or integrate.","opus":"A plea to be broken open and read by another because the speaker can't find the missing piece in themselves, unsure whether what they feel even qualifies as love. Intimacy here is a surgery they beg for, hoping someone else can name what's absent.","sonnet":"A person who feels incomplete and hollowed out begs a partner to look inside them and identify what is missing, desperately wanting to be understood and filled even if it means being pulled under.","src":"haiku"},
"wayside~parallax-error":{"haiku":"Cyclical disappointment and failed preservation; the speaker exists as keepsake and relic, perpetually stored and abandoned, unable to break the repetition.","opus":"A relationship of mirrored motion where the speaker keeps misreading the signals and losing every time, sensing they're only a keepsake shelved for later. The parallax is the ache of never quite occupying the same point of view.","sonnet":"Someone who keeps misreading signals in a relationship they have no real place in — they are a keepsake set on a shelf, never chosen, perpetually slipping from the other person's attention.","src":"haiku"},
"wayside~shine-onto-me":{"haiku":"Someone who gives everything to others while hollowed out by grief is reminded that the gap in them is exactly where light enters, and that whatever love they have saved for themselves is already illuminating the room.","opus":"A plea for someone drowning in grief to save a portion of love for themselves. The narrator marvels at how the depleted still give more than the intact, and finds home in a presence that lingers behind closed eyes. It leans on the emo-adjacent idea that a wound is precisely where the light enters.","sonnet":"Someone who has lost everything still gives more love than the narrator can manage — a hole in the heart becomes the aperture through which light enters and fills the room. The speaker urges them to save some love for themselves, quoting a wall inscription ('your love will be safe') as a reminder that generosity without self-preservation is its own wound.","src":"haiku"},
"wbbt~breathless":{"haiku":"Breath and presence drain through dissolution; the speaker surrenders their life while the beloved remains indifferent, leading to asphyxia and the collapse of meaning.","opus":"Falling repeatedly for someone who doesn't care, losing breath, sleep and coherence, handing over one's life to a person indifferent to the collapse, with pretended hatred masking hurt.","sonnet":"A speaker losing breath, sleep, and coherence hands over something essential to a person who doesn't mind the fall — the fractured syntax mirrors the dissolution, and \"I fall but you don't love\" names what the relationship costs without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"wbbt~idea":{"haiku":"Longing and overthinking; caught between searching and wishing while love binds you to someone.","opus":"Adrift on open water hunting for anything that isn't sadness, resigned to never really choosing who we love. It captures the disorientation of losing yourself in someone and overthinking them long after they're gone.","sonnet":"Someone who pursued love actively while their partner waited passively reflects on how neither approach worked — the song circles the inevitability of being overtaken by romantic feeling, losing oneself to obsessive thought, and the regret of having chosen at all.","src":"haiku"},
"we-are-magonia~the-living-will-envy-the-dead":{"haiku":"Death becomes the destroyer that transforms worlds, forcing confrontation with duty and inevitability.","opus":"A setting of Oppenheimer's recollection at the first atomic test, the world changed forever, invoking his Bhagavad Gita line about becoming death itself, the unmaker of worlds.","sonnet":"The lyrics are entirely J. Robert Oppenheimer's account of witnessing the first atomic bomb test and his recollection of the Bhagavad-Gita line \"Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds\" — a spoken-word collage of reactions ranging from laughter to silence to awe at the moment humanity changed permanently.","src":"haiku"},
"we-are-pigs-filter~oshi":{"haiku":"Emotional parasitism corrodes through diminishment; the beloved's criticism becomes interminable attack, reducing worth until resistance feels impossible.","src":"haiku"},
"we-are-pigs~duality":{"haiku":"The song explores pain as inescapable and self-inflicted. The narrator pushes fingers into eyes seeking relief; all they have is insanity, trapped between creation and destruction.","opus":"A cover of Slipknot, an agonized loop of suffering with no exit, the narrator jamming his fingers against his eyes to blunt an ache and repeating that madness is all that's left to him. It is claustrophobic self-torment, alternately pleading to be reassembled or abandoned. We Are Pigs recast the nu-metal staple, preserving its raw desperation through their own heavier filter.","sonnet":"Unbearable internal pressure given a physical gesture — pressing fingers into eyes to stop the ache. The speaker cycles through collapse and defiance, insisting on saying what must be said while admitting they cannot promise the noise will be welcome, and arriving at a place where insanity feels like the only honest description of what remains.","src":"haiku"},
"we-are-pigs~oshi":{"haiku":"Resentment at being unseen and undervalued in a relationship designed to drain you.","opus":"Walking away from a partner who eroded your self-worth and played mind games, refusing their late apologies, the hard-won clarity that it's over for them but not for you, so back off.","sonnet":"A speaker extricating themselves from a relationship defined by disrespect and the erosion of self-worth — \"how much am I worth again\" is the wound at the centre. The other person's sorrow at watching them leave reads as noise and tragedy, but the speaker is already gone, knowing love or something like it will replace what was lost.","src":"haiku"},
"we-are-the-empty~inside-the-flame":{"haiku":"Self-purification requires total severance from narcissistic manipulation; the speaker walks through fire to reclaim autonomy.","opus":"Battling past fear and rot to claw a way home while cutting loose a toxic, manipulative narcissist, swearing never to admit them again and to walk into the blaze to reclaim what was taken.","sonnet":"A speaker trapped in spiritual and psychological ruin rejects a narcissistic tormentor — 'I'm more of a man and I'm half your age' — choosing to walk through cold decay and fear inside the flame rather than surrender to someone who only knows manipulation.","src":"haiku"},
"we-butter-the-bread-with-butter~20-km-h":{"haiku":"Electric scooters embody contemporary urban convenience culture, enabling frictionless mobility while satirising consumer adoption of disposable technology.","opus":"A deadpan deathcore joke elevating the e-scooter to an object of swagger, the whole absurd point being to breakdown-brag about buzzing around at twenty kilometers an hour.","sonnet":"A tongue-in-cheek celebration of the electric scooter — new, ubiquitous, and liberating at exactly twenty kilometres per hour, used for every journey from the mundane to the reckless.","src":"haiku"},
"we-came-as-romans-zero-9-36~daggers":{"haiku":"Psychic warfare demands razor focus; cutting away doubt grants power to overcome internal betrayal and external assault simultaneously.","opus":"A lifelong war of the self against itself, carving out the hurt to reach the strength to win. The dagger is the willingness to wound yourself to survive, with a warning to anyone who steps between a man and his own reckoning.","sonnet":"Internal battle and the refusal to let others insert themselves into a lifelong war with the self — the speaker has been locked in self-versus-self combat since learning to walk, draws a dagger to cut out pain and find power, and warns anyone who tries to intervene that they will meet their end. Conquest is over the self, not over external enemies.","src":"haiku"},
"we-came-as-romans~black-hole":{"haiku":"The narrator falls into a psychological black hole, fighting gravity that pulls out the worst parts of himself.","opus":"A song about a psychological void that drags the ugliest side of the narrator out just when he believes he's finally safe. He struggles against its pull, groping through the dark and going under, weighed down by everything he never managed to say.","sonnet":"A recurring mental collapse — just when the narrator feels stable, a black hole in his own head pulls him back down, drawing out his worst self while words left unsaid sink him further.","src":"haiku"},
"we-came-as-romans~carry-the-weight":{"opus":"A song of mourning borne like shackles after a death, the narrator asking whether any comfort exists in the ordeal and vowing to seek the departed person's glow through his bleakest stretches. Grief sits like a wound that will not close.","sonnet":"A grief song about bearing unbearable loss — possibly the death of a friend or bandmate — in which the narrator is brought to their knees by sorrow yet holds to the idea that the person's light can still guide others through darkness.","src":"sonnet"},
"we-came-as-romans~darkbloom":{"haiku":"Refusing to fade despite numbness and self-doubt, finding inner light buried deep to survive and grow through pain.","opus":"A stubborn vow not to fade, the narrator conceding that brightness may dim but he won't, choosing growth over surrender even when the sky goes black and resolving to uncover the light hidden inside him. We Came As Romans deliver it as pure metalcore uplift, its imagery of choices and hidden pain resolving into a promise to flower out of the dark.","sonnet":"A figure who has grown numb and avoidant chooses to stop withering — not by finding external light but by excavating the one already buried inside. Standing at a fork between death and growth, Darkbloom claims the slower, harder path, blacking out the sun to force internal luminescence into existence.","src":"haiku"},
"we-came-as-romans~foreign-fire":{"haiku":"Estrangement transforms identity; the speaker grieves becoming a stranger to those they once loved.","opus":"Grief over drifting away from the people he grew up with only to lose one for good, the narrator questioning what he has turned into as regret and a halted heartbeat strike him while he is far from them, swearing he tried to make it back. The song gained painful weight after vocalist Kyle Pavone's death.","sonnet":"A person who has changed profoundly while away — isolating, ignoring calls home, becoming someone even they don't recognize — arrives back to find that someone they love died while they were absent, and no amount of swearing they tried can undo the fact of not being there. The guilt is immovable.","src":"haiku"},
"we-came-as-romans~golden":{"haiku":"Nostalgia for stolen time; grasping at memories of golden years that slipped away.","opus":"Numb mourning after an irreplaceable loss, the narrator motionless as he studies the ceiling above his bed. He relays a final tear-blurred farewell and begs for some way to keep going and feel human again. The band's own history of loss deepens it, casting their shared years as cut short yet radiant.","sonnet":"Grief for a loss that happened before goodbye could be said — staring at the ceiling at 7:40, drowning in feelings, unable to see through tear-filled eyes — the years called golden in retrospect, stolen by whatever took them, and the request is not for the loss to be undone but for someone to show the way to go on and let the colours in again.","src":"haiku"},
"we-came-as-romans~plagued":{"haiku":"Self-estrangement and haunting choices hollow the speaker into a ghost of their former self.","opus":"The slow erosion of identity, staring into a mirror and recognizing only a hollow shell of a former self. Trapped in a hell of irreversible choices, the narrator watches distraction fail and rage take over. Metalcore's language of self-alienation renders depression as contagion, an inner decay with nothing left to say.","sonnet":"A speaker stares into a mirror and sees a stranger — identity has eroded through bad choices and compulsive distraction until only an empty shell remains. The turning point never comes; instead the chorus confirms entrapment: cast into plague, vanishing into rage, with nothing left to say.","src":"haiku"},
"we-came-as-romans~promise-me":{"haiku":"Dread of erasure compels bargaining; the speaker clutches for permanence through another's fidelity.","opus":"The dread of vanishing from memory, voiced from what feels like a dying breath, someone bargaining to stall the end if a loved one will only vow to linger and keep them in mind.","sonnet":"A person dying or hovering at the edge of death, terrified of being forgotten, asks the people who love them to wait — to hold off eternity, to remain — as the fear of erasure overwhelms even the fear of dying itself. The final section reveals the speaker was likely dying far from home, and someone they loved died while they couldn't return.","src":"haiku"},
"wear-your-wounds~shrinking-violet":{"opus":"A depressive confessing to a lover that their illness is a lifelong flaw; using the image of a once-innocent child whose skies turned grey, they describe surrendering to darkness as a loyal companion rather than fighting it.","sonnet":"A person describes being consumed by depression — depicting their youth as a time of naive invincibility before mental illness took hold, ultimately finding dark comfort in depression itself as a faithful companion rather than fighting it.","src":"sonnet"},
"weatherday~come-in":{"haiku":"A lonely, poorly-drawn figure invites someone in; they're almost floored by unexpected words and hope the other will stay.","opus":"A frail, half-whispered welcome, the narrator barely holding on, roughly sketched, hoping a visitor lingers despite his heaviness and startled to hear an unexpected bit of kindness aimed his way. Weatherday is the project of Swedish musician Sputnik, whose lo-fi bedroom emo turns this tiny, tender doorway moment into something raw and quietly wrecking.","sonnet":"A small, fragile welcome — the speaker is still here, barely upright, poorly sketched, sinking under something unnamed, hoping the visitor will stay a while. A single sentence of disbelief closes it: the words you just said were not something I expected to hear, and that unexpectedness is the whole emotional weight.","src":"haiku"},
"weezer~across-the-sea":{"haiku":"Obsession across distance fixates on an unknown teenager, transforming isolation and celebrity into compulsive fantasy that mistakes admiration for love.","opus":"Rivers Cuomo's uncomfortably honest confession of obsessing over a teenage fan's letter from Japan, projecting loneliness and longing onto a girl he'll never meet, spiraling into isolation and blaming his mother for it all.","sonnet":"A rock musician receives a fan letter from a teenage girl in Japan and becomes consumed by a painful, guilty longing for her — aware that the desire is wrong and that fan mail and songs can never substitute for actual touch. The obsession is presented with discomforting self-awareness and adolescent ache.","src":"haiku"},
"weezer~grapes-of-wrath":{"haiku":"Literary escapism through audiobooks enables emotional detachment; the narrator prefers fictional worlds to engagement with conscious reality.","opus":"A middle-aged retreat into audiobooks and literary heroes as escape and comfort, the narrator numbly opting to live inside great novels rather than the real world, half checked-out, half finding more meaning there.","sonnet":"A person retreats into literary audiobooks — Steinbeck, Orwell, Heller, Tolkien — as a way of opting out of a world that feels less meaningful than the inner life these books open up, claiming the classics as companions more real than whatever surrounds him.","src":"haiku"},
"weezer~island-in-the-sun":{"haiku":"Two people escape into isolation together where normal concerns dissolve, leaving only present joy and freedom from mental control.","opus":"A hazy fantasy of fleeing to a tropical paradise where language and memory dissolve and nothing matters but idling in the sun and feeling good, with a wordless chant sealing the mood. Weezer's Rivers Cuomo penned it as a breezy early-2000s postcard whose sweetness carries a quiet undertow, the paradise being one that exists only in daydream.","sonnet":"Pure escapist pleasure with almost no friction — a holiday place where memory is unnecessary, time dissolves, two people play in golden water and make a simple promise to run away together and never feel bad again. The joy is deliberately uncomplicated, a rare thing.","src":"haiku"},
"weezer~pink-triangle":{"haiku":"Desire crashes against sexual orientation, the speaker's romantic projection shattered by the revelation that love cannot override fundamental incompatibility.","opus":"The comic heartbreak of falling for a woman only to discover she's a lesbian, Cuomo mocking his own delusion of a marriage in his head and pettily wishing she could be a little straight for him.","sonnet":"A man falls for a woman, builds their relationship entirely in his own imagination, and then discovers she is a lesbian. The humiliation is gentle and self-deprecating — he was dumb, the evidence was there, and he was simply too deep in wishful thinking to see it.","src":"haiku"},
"weezer~shine-again":{"haiku":"Routine tasks rebuild the self; internal sun awaits rekindling after demoralization.","opus":"A gentle pep talk to someone worn flat by mundane routine and quiet demoralization, insisting they were always great and the inner light can return, a modest hymn to reclaiming self-worth through the everyday.","sonnet":"A self-pep talk set against the small rituals of a day — car door, dog-walk, dishes, good morning kisses — the speaker reminding themselves and someone else that they were always great and that the inner sun will shine again, while admitting the possibility of demoralization underneath.","src":"haiku"},
"weighbridge~dichotomy":{"haiku":"Holding on paradoxically requires losing everything; the speaker falls apart while paradoxically held together by the spinning force around them.","opus":"Clinging to a thing that both holds him together and tears him apart, unsure whether it's worth holding at all in a cold, indifferent universe. The dread is of realizing he lost his life climbing toward places no one needs.","sonnet":"About clinging to something — a relationship, a belief, a person — that is both destroying the narrator and the only thing holding them together, which is the dichotomy of the title. The narrator is sinking, freezing, and emptied, but they cannot release their grip because without the thing that is killing them there would be nothing left at all.","src":"haiku"},
"weightless~bloodsucker":{"haiku":"Parasitic entanglement festers beyond removal; the speaker becomes both predator and prey, infected by attachment that breeds contempt and inevitable decay.","opus":"A survivor demanding a tormentor dissolve out of existence, done with forgiveness and desperate to expel the parasite they let sink into them.","sonnet":"About being drained and tormented by a former partner who is now impossible to shake — the narrator feels like a vulture is carving their chest, can't face their own complicity, and is haunted by the other person's presence even in their worst nightmares.","src":"haiku"},
"weird-wolves~overdrive":{"haiku":"Reckless acceleration toward collapse masquerades as survival; the speaker justifies overdrive and self-destruction as necessary, belief in lies sustaining the plunge.","opus":"A grim irony about the human drive that keeps everyone alive and thriving on shared lies, then steers them straight into the crash. Overdrive is sold as survival even as it's plainly the road to demise.","sonnet":"A machine-logic society running itself to destruction — people believe the lies they are fed, pour all their drive into a system that only leads them off a cliff, and the cycle ends in death with no wisdom gained.","src":"haiku"},
"wenszy~tell-me":{"haiku":"Desperate love demands total possession; the speaker cannot survive without merging completely with another, drowning preferable to separation.","opus":"Dizzy, breathless obsession with a person found amid noise, unable to live without them and dreading the drowning pain of losing them. The love is so total it reads as its own emergency.","sonnet":"A person lost and exhausted finds someone who grounds them and becomes terrified of losing that anchor, declaring they would rather drown than live without them.","src":"haiku"},
"wet-leg~davina-mccall":{"haiku":"Devotion frames the beloved as sun and hero, where the speaker pledges eternal commitment through pop culture metaphor. Fantasy projects onto reality, collapsing dream and life into singular obsession.","opus":"A giddy, over-the-top declaration of devotion, the singer casting herself as pop-culture icons who would cross hell and back for a beloved, treating the relationship as a dream she never wants to wake from.","sonnet":"A devotional love song in which the speaker casts herself as the ultimate reliable presence — 'I'll be your Davina, I'll be your Shakira, tighter than a python, through stormy weather' — the mundane similes (Ribena over Coca-Cola, shitty holiday weather) making the love feel warm and specific rather than grand, ending in the admission that without the other person, Earth is just cold.","src":"haiku"},
"whale-bones~reciprocation":{"haiku":"Unreciprocated longing and the realization of one's own disposability. The speaker repeatedly reaches out but discovers the other does not want the same commitment.","opus":"An unreturned longing pleads to finally be given a sense of belonging after drifting so long, terrified the other has lost interest and admitting the work is forever one-sided. Whale Bones' hushed, emo-tinged indie suits this fragility, the narrator caught between staying quiet and leaving, unsure what better self he is reaching for but sure he is worn out from being the only one making an effort.","sonnet":"Someone exhausted from one-sided effort reads the silence correctly — the other person has stopped wanting what they once shared. Rather than demand confirmation, he considers whether being alone might be healthier than being stranded in a connection that no longer returns anything.","src":"haiku"},
"what-i-ve-done-official-video~linkin-park":{"haiku":"Confronting self-destruction and erasure; facing the person you've become and attempting renewal.","opus":"A reckoning with guilt and the wish to be washed clean of past wrongs. The narrator confronts himself to erase what he has become, choosing to start again despite whatever pain follows. A signature Linkin Park meditation on regret and self-forgiveness, it turns confession into a deliberate act of letting go.","sonnet":"A reckoning drawn from a thousand lies: the speaker chooses to face and erase what they have become rather than alibi their way through regret. Starting again means forgiving oneself — today this ends — and letting go is the only mercy available.","src":"haiku"},
"whatever-forever~bury-me":{"opus":"A dejected account of feeling unwelcome and ground down, mapping an exit while sleepless, tucking away what remains of himself, and telling a self-absorbed person that if they have no use for him, they may as well put him in the ground.","sonnet":"Someone so depleted by a relationship that prioritized the other's needs to the exclusion of their own asks to simply be buried and forgotten — they plan their escape from a life that has worn them down to nothing, wanting only to disappear inside their dreams.","src":"sonnet"},
"wheatus~teenage-dirtbag":{"haiku":"A loser admires a girl from afar in school, invisible to her until prom when she inexplicably approaches him, offering unexpected acceptance.","opus":"A lovesick outcast pines for a girl far above his social station, branding himself worthless while her armed, popular boyfriend lords over the school, until against all odds she crosses the room on prom night. Wheatus's millennial power-pop keeps the fantasy alive, its specific details of metal-band tickets and thrift-store fashion grounding an underdog daydream where the loser finally wins.","sonnet":"An invisible outcast at school carries a secret crush on a girl far out of his social reach, convinced she could never notice someone like him. On prom night she walks over and invites herself into his world — a small miracle that briefly closes the distance between them.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~ascend":{"haiku":"Addresses feelings of stagnation and the human tendency to follow rather than lead, calling for breaking free from mediocrity. The song urges rising above resignation and reigniting lost spark.","opus":"A critique of passivity and conformity, describing people conditioned to follow rather than lead and to waste energy while bored and uninspired. The narrator calls for breaking the standardized mold, reigniting a spark, and rising above monotony and emptiness.","sonnet":"A call to break free from collective stagnation and creative resignation — the lyrics describe a group trapped in repetition and boredom, and ultimately urge abandoning the standardized form in favor of reigniting something genuine and ascending beyond mediocrity.","src":"haiku","fable":"Wheel diagnose a creative culture that has polished itself into paralysis, then dares to walk out of it. The song opens on inherited timidity — people trained to trail behind titans instead of risking the lead — and drifts through a torpor where the received masterpiece is recited exactly, every flaw scrubbed away until only a flat, defeated numbness remains and the chorus keeps begging for a rescuer from that hollowness. The band's sly move is to name the scrubbing as the sickness: technically immaculate craft becomes pure monotony, and the plea for salvation is really the admission that language already stopped reaching. The turn is small and brave rather than transcendent — the refusal to keep obeying the mold, the promise to strike the dead fire back alight, the plainspoken readiness to rise — a genre indicting its own airless perfectionism and choosing to author itself over imitating. Ascent here means the ordinary nerve to trade a comfortable ruin for something unproven.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"refusing the mold to author something unproven over a comfortable ruin","built":"immaculate craft named as the sickness the song chooses to break"},
"wheel~blood-drinker":{"haiku":"Fascination with violence justifies murder; justice collapses into the thirst for spectacle and blood, breeding endless veneration.","opus":"An unsettling look at society's fascination with killers, deifying the murderer while no one speaks for the dead, and the seductive way a persuasive voice feeds a collective thirst for blood.","sonnet":"Society's compulsion to deify killers and consume spectacle of violence — 'we deify the killer, there is no justice' — is anatomised as an unwavering thirst for blood dressed as entertainment or justice; the dead have no voice, and the speaker sees another way but names it without illusion.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~dissipating":{"haiku":"The speaker confronts the philosophical futility of meaning-making when nothing lasts; apathy and denial characterize both their struggle and others' responses, but ultimately they find acceptance that impermanence itself becomes enough.","opus":"The Finnish progressive-rock band reaches hard-won acceptance. A narrator prone to missteps, grasping for footing and finding none, comes to terms with the fact that nothing endures and nothing was ever promised. Rather than despair, his bitterness dissolves; if all of it decays, then whatever he lived through will simply have to suffice. Embracing limits becomes its own quiet freedom.","sonnet":"A long argument with nihilism that arrives, unexpectedly, at acceptance. After exhausting himself against the unfairness of impermanence and the futility of expecting meaning, the bitterness simply lifts — and what remains is the decision to cling to experience itself, limitations and all, as enough.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~empire":{"haiku":"Conformity spreads like infection, corrupting autonomy through systems of control that breed dissonance and rage, all while the powerful feed on the hatred they cultivate.","opus":"How tribalism metastasizes, people surrendering their autonomy to a hive that feeds on manufactured hatred of an invented enemy. The warning is that those stoking the fire are pretenders who will reap exactly what they sow, with nothing left to save.","sonnet":"A condemnation of the modern political machine: the way rage, tribalism, and manufactured outrage are used to deform public discourse, with people feeding on hate and framing the opposition as pure evil until nothing remains — a cycle of self-destruction with no prayer left to send.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~fugue":{"haiku":"Systems of connection and radical change demand emotional restraint; kindness and calm withdrawal offer a path through existential overwhelm.","opus":"A clipped summons to survive an agonizing shared transformation through self-mastery: the linked collective must ride out the noise, quiet its rage, sedate itself and turn inward, treating restraint and gentleness as strength amid sweeping upheaval.","sonnet":"A brief, spare call to endure a period of overwhelming upheaval through stillness rather than resistance — the speaker urges anesthetizing the noise, calming anger, and retreating inward to survive a radical collective transition.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~hyperion":{"haiku":"Mortality is inevitable and we must accept our fleeting existence while united with others in shared oblivion.","opus":"A humanist meditation on mortality: compelled to overreach, we pursue a fading light into nothingness and forget that nothing is guaranteed. The resolution is accepting that everything passes, and a plea to swap hostility for compassion, to relish the improbable gift of existing at all before we meet the end side by side.","sonnet":"A meditation on human mortality and collective insignificance — we're all marching toward oblivion, clambering higher despite knowing nothing lasts, and the only sane response is to make peace with impermanence and share the journey.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~lacking":{"haiku":"A person struggles against destructive patterns but feels paralyzed by overthinking, gradually breaking free through humility and accepting guidance toward an exit.","opus":"Wheel frame recovery from self-destruction as inching back from the brink without the drive to fully break free of old habits. The heart of it is a struggle for self-command: humbled by the patience of those around him and reliant on another to steer him out, he repeats that he has to seize the reins, resolving to run his own life again as change draws near.","sonnet":"Coming out the other side of a destructive pattern — addiction or compulsion implied — through the humbling grace of other people's patience, and the ongoing, effortful discipline of not losing control again.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~movement":{"haiku":"The speaker rejects stagnation and false equivalence, mobilizing against those who hide behind empty words and apathy.","opus":"A call to collective action against complacency and hollow talk, demanding people either pull together or clear the path. The throughline is exasperation with those who lump every dissenter in with their foes and shelter in cozy passivity, insisting inaction carries a real cost as the moment shifts.","sonnet":"About the paralysis of inaction in the face of something that demands change — someone keeps speaking without doing anything, perpetuating stagnation, and the narrator is done listening and ready to mobilise, invoking the price of silence.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~please":{"haiku":"Propaganda amplifies division while dismantling truth; the speaker demands liberation from manufactured consent and the erosion of collective reality.","opus":"An indictment of a demagogue or media voice that flattens every dispute into stark opposites and engineers division to keep people at each other's throats. The singer, starved for something to trust, finally rejects the manipulation and refuses to give in.","sonnet":"A critique of media manipulation and political division: a mouthpiece renders everything black and white, destroys from the inside, and the speaker — who will never concede to what they see as corrosive free speech — warns that there's only one way this ends, even as their own frustration risks becoming the very thing they oppose.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~porcelain":{"haiku":"Fragile systems shatter under the weight of change as the speaker and their world face inevitable dissolution, with buried traumas resurfacing in the wake of transformation and reckoning.","opus":"Humanity as fragile porcelain on the brink of collapse, unearthing the warning signs and scars we keep ignoring, doomed to repeat the cycle and finally answer for our sins in the wake of a change we can't stop.","sonnet":"Humanity's cyclical fall and fragile reckoning with change: frozen beneath waves, turning heads to avoid blame, people are as brittle as porcelain in a different light — destined to shatter, be sundered, and stumble forward again once the wake of change has passed.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~resident-human":{"haiku":"A human consciousness seeks clarity and peace by transcending the constant noise and simulation of modern existence, divided between who they are and who they aspire to become.","opus":"The title track of the Finnish prog band's 2021 album confronts modern numbness, a narrator overstimulated into detachment and split between the man he is and the one he hopes to become, pulled between now and later. Chasing some calmer state beyond the noise, he keeps reorienting toward a long-sought goal, fusing weariness and resolve into a decision to stay present.","sonnet":"A person overstimulated by modernity attempts to locate a quieter self beneath the noise. Paralysed between who he is and who he wants to become, he works toward a state of lucid clarity — eventually choosing presence over friction, and the mountain over the signal fire, as the only ground worth standing on.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~tyrant":{"haiku":"A tyrannical figure justifies their violent control over fractured populations through force and silence, framing oppression as necessary intervention. They position themselves as both consequence and solution to social division.","opus":"A chilling first-person monologue from a strongman who justifies his cruelty as the only thing keeping warring factions from each other's throats. The horror is how reasonable he sounds, framing tyranny as the order the frightened masses secretly asked for.","sonnet":"A dark, self-aware monologue from someone who has become the authoritarian they may have once opposed — they justify seizing control as the only way to stop factional chaos, but the logic keeps sliding toward silencing dissent and demanding absolute compliance.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~up-the-chain":{"haiku":"A ruthless climber dismisses compassion as weakness and crushes anyone in their path to power, then questions their cruelty.","opus":"A satire delivered in the voice of the winner-take-all careerist, who reframes empathy as weakness and ruthlessness as virtue. Wheel let the monologue expose itself, so the small crack of self-doubt near the end lands as the only honest thing this predator says.","sonnet":"A monologue from an unrepentant social climber who views empathy as weakness and other people as tools or liabilities — the narrator owns their ruthlessness, crushing anyone who gets in the way of ascending the ladder.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~vultures":{"haiku":"Predatory forces circle their prey, hungering to take everything and leave nothing but ashes and pain.","opus":"Prog-metal menace voiced by predators with no mercy left, framing modern life as a gamified reality where cruelty is just playing the winning hand. The relentless hunger is the whole cautionary point.","sonnet":"About circling predators waiting for someone to fall — whether political, personal, or institutional, the song describes relentless pursuers who will take everything and leave nothing, and explicitly reject any possibility of mercy.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~wheel":{"haiku":"The song critiques societal control through surveillance, numbing effects of propaganda, and loss of free will, portraying citizens as passive participants in a dystopian machine. Privacy becomes a commodity traded away.","opus":"Surveillance-state paranoia and manufactured fear, a populace begging to be numbed while enemies are invented and privacy is sold off. The Orwellian nod frames citizens as cogs who've traded their innocence for the illusion of safety.","sonnet":"A pointed indictment of surveillance capitalism and political manipulation — the government manufactures enemies, amplifies fear, and trades privacy as a commodity while the narrator begs for the medicine of numbness, watching social trust erode in real time.","src":"haiku"},
"wheel~where-the-pieces-lie":{"haiku":"A person flees isolation and desperation, attempting a final push toward survival despite overwhelming weight and the realization that recovery may be impossible.","opus":"A weary, desperate struggle for survival, pushing toward a distant border with no way to start again, craving connection while feeling too small to matter and tormented by a purpose beyond one's strength.","sonnet":"Someone overwhelmed by isolation and a crushing sense of purposelessness considers fleeing the wreckage of their circumstances, unsure whether connection or safety lies anywhere ahead or whether they are simply racing toward death.","src":"haiku"},
"when-the-world-sleeps~riot":{"haiku":"Buried truths demand violent eruption; the speaker cannot contain what must be revealed, even as prophecy offers no resolution.","opus":"An internal eruption that refuses to be contained, buried in the self yet impossible to hide. The narrator rejects prophets and reconciliation alike, insisting that time mends nothing and no reckoning is owed. It channels post-hardcore's furious autonomy, demanding release as a kind of prayer while still down on the floor.","sonnet":"The speaker is buried in themselves, unable to stop what they cannot hide, and calls for a riot as both release and admission — they no longer care yet can't stop crawling, time won't mend anything, and the refusal of anyone else's guidance reads as the last assertion of self in total collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"where-oceans-burn~hollow-heart":{"opus":"A harrowing account of an abusive relationship that drove the singer to self-harm and suicidal despair, feeling forsaken by God and dead inside; now hollowed out, he refuses to wish the abuser well and instead wishes them hell.","sonnet":"A young person in an abusive relationship describes self-harming and feeling forsaken by God, bleeding on the floor and questioning their worth — they survive, hollow and gutted, and close with the explicit wish that their abuser suffers in hell.","src":"sonnet"},
"while-she-sleeps~anti-social":{"haiku":"Rejection of conformity and surface-level culture; the speaker critiques fakeness and refuses to accept hollow excuses for complicity in systemic corruption.","opus":"While She Sleeps, a Sheffield metalcore band with a fierce DIY, us-versus-them streak, turn out a fiery screed against hypocrisy and passivity. The narrator draws a line between rejecting people and rejecting nonsense, seething at trend-chasers, phony politics, and corruption until he's sick enough to block it all out, a rallying cry to refuse conformity that typifies their catalogue.","sonnet":"A rejection of performance culture and hollow politics dressed up as anti-social posture — what it actually opposes is the bullshit of trend-chasing, fake smiles, and the comfortable excuses people make for collective passivity. The speaker is sick of prescribed rebellion, sick of division, and arrives at the conclusion that everyone behind their masks is ultimately the same.","src":"haiku"},
"while-she-sleeps~down-feat-malevolence":{"haiku":"Mutual codependency creates suffocating pain; love becomes a wound that cannot heal.","opus":"A toxic, codependent bond framed as mutual addiction, two people bumping the same needle and suffocating each other. The narrator craves weightlessness and honest air yet keeps returning to the pain. British metalcore heavyweights render love as asphyxiation, where healing and self-destruction blur into the same desperate gasp for breath.","sonnet":"A toxic codependent relationship narrated as mutual addiction — both parties are hooked on the pain, described as \"bumping the needle every time.\" The speaker wants to breathe without sinking, to feel weightless, but every plea for release ends in the same paradox: \"asphyxiate me.\"","src":"haiku"},
"while-she-sleeps~eye-to-eye":{"haiku":"Confrontation of hard truth; looking directly at what cannot be unseen or denied.","opus":"The bitter unravelling of a relationship past saving, a thousand attempts finally spent. The narrator finds an unsettling calm in emptiness, his mind already resigned even as he holds on. Sheffield's metalcore fury frames the split as a self-repeating trap in a burning world, insisting the pain is temporary and the change is for the better.","sonnet":"A rupture between two people who tried a thousand times and can no longer see eye to eye — the speaker has found peace in oblivion, the mind has already won, and holding on is just the body catching up — the vicious cycle and the world on fire converge on the admission that the pain won't last forever, but the liar label cuts through everything said before it.","src":"haiku"},
"while-she-sleeps~fakers-plague":{"haiku":"A scathing rejection of hypocrisy and false morality in society, where the narrator refuses to play along with the pretense and calls out the phoniness around them.","opus":"A furious broadside against pretenders and hypocrites, the narrator would rather be an honest sinner, losing the will to live under the weight of denial and finger-pointing leaders. While She Sleeps' anthemic British metalcore turns this into a rallying cry, stamping feet and cursing false idols while confessing that they too are fiends and chancers finding serenity in the chaos they diagnose.","sonnet":"Contempt for performative morality and hypocritical leaders drives a defiant refusal to pretend or comply. The comfort zone is named as the real destroyer, the congregation of the misled is indicted, and the call goes out to pretenders: the mirror at the end of the road will show them what they are.","src":"haiku"},
"while-she-sleeps~haunt-me":{"haiku":"Warnings ignored until disaster arrives; past words return as haunting truth, and the world fractures because people chose not to listen or act.","opus":"A reckoning with denial and shared failure, the narrator fighting a tide that drags him down while a collapsing world and his own old words return to torment him, insisting no one be abandoned. While She Sleeps, the Sheffield metalcore band, run their social urgency through it, warning of danger at the door and selfishness, their anthemic choruses turning private guilt into public alarm.","sonnet":"A reckoning with collective blindness — signs were there, warnings were given, the wolves were at the gate, and still the world fell into disarray. The speaker indicts themselves alongside everyone else for being too intoxicated by the false and too slow to read the current, and everything they once said now returns to haunt them as evidence rather than prophecy.","src":"haiku"},
"while-she-sleeps~nervous":{"opus":"Wrestling with the fear of finally believing in something and reaching out to another person, admitting shared dread and the sense of never being enough, but choosing hope so that neither of them is alone.","sonnet":"An anxious meditation on collective blindness and the terror of uncertainty — the narrator is paralyzed by the gap between knowing an answer exists and being unable to find it, and the song ultimately pleads for people to stop building on lies and start speaking and doing something real.","src":"sonnet"},
"while-she-sleeps~self-hell":{"haiku":"Shared neurosis becomes the only honest language; the speaker refuses to perform recovery, insisting that everyone harbors the same complex darkness beneath their masks.","opus":"A defiant confession that everyone carries the same wounds, addictions, and social sickness, refusing the victim role while insisting the only cure is praising the love inside us, hell is universal so you might as well share it.","sonnet":"An unflinching inventory of universal psychological damage — imposter syndrome, sensory deprivation, social sickness, toxic obsessions — balanced with a radical insistence that the only counterweight is praising the love inside us, however imperfect.","src":"haiku"},
"while-she-sleeps~sleeps-society":{"haiku":"Outsiders band together to create an alternative society and broadcast truth, refusing silence and demanding systemic change.","opus":"The Sheffield metalcore band turns their fan-funded community into an anthem of belonging and revolt. Speaking to outsiders who never fit in, the narrator promises them a place to come alive elsewhere. Calls to become the change, rebuild broken systems, and speak up for ordinary people give it a communal, activist charge, framing their independent movement as a genuine uprising.","sonnet":"An invitation to build an alternative community for those who feel they do not belong to the mainstream. Impatient with existing structures, he proposes collective action — speaking for the masses, creating rather than waiting — framed as a broadcast on a different frequency for anyone willing to step up.","src":"haiku"},
"while-she-sleeps~systematic":{"haiku":"The song criticizes consumer materialism and herd mentality. The speaker rejects mimicry and the systematic push to want what others have.","opus":"British metalcore railing against a conformist, transactional culture that manufactures wants and lies; the narrator refuses to covet what he's told to, vowing not to let cynical, systematic negativity consume or program him.","sonnet":"A rejection of conformity and collective self-destruction: the narrator is fed up with a world running on hollow social pressure and negative energy, and refuses to want things simply because others have them.","src":"haiku"},
"while-she-sleeps~the-guilty-party":{"opus":"A reckoning with collective fault, resentment, and giving up, mockingly inviting everyone to a gathering of the culpable where people feign lifelessness and surrender to their inner demons. Amid every-for-himself survivalism and looming unrest, the singer keeps insisting he needs no one to get through it alone.","sonnet":"A bitter reckoning with collective failure and deflected blame — the guilty party is everyone, malignant and twisted, playing blame games while denying their own demons — but underneath the accusation is also a defiant insistence on self-sufficiency, refusing to keep playing dead.","src":"sonnet"},
"while-she-sleeps~you-are-all-you-need":{"opus":"A defiant refusal of false salvation, choosing to be underrated, hated, and honestly suffering rather than sedated by comforting lies; amid a hellish world it argues for facing pain head-on and finding self-sufficiency instead of numbing out.","sonnet":"A deliberately raw anti-manifesto rejecting liberation narratives and spiritual comfort — the narrator prefers suffering with authentic feeling over painless belief, and the song insists that the world keeps breaking everyone while demanding no one pretend otherwise or chase hidden meanings.","src":"sonnet"},
"whiplash~red-bomb":{"haiku":"Sex work persists as survival, reducing bodily autonomy to commercial transaction and leaving no space for desire or dignity.","opus":"A lurid thrash portrait of a streetwalker working her corner to survive, viewed through the coarse, transactional gaze of a potential john, all appetite and no sympathy.","sonnet":"A street-level portrait of a sex worker known as Red Bomb, describing her nightly trade with a mixture of predatory admiration and transactional bluntness.","src":"haiku"},
"whipped-cream~cry-borgore-remix":{"haiku":"Silence as refusal, stoicism as defense; the subject insists on internal suffering without audible testament.","opus":"A refusal to give an ex or an audience the satisfaction of seeing weakness, defiance built entirely from withholding tears.","sonnet":"A speaker declares stoic defiance against someone who has hurt them, insisting their grief will remain hidden — \"you won't hear me cry\" repeated as both resolve and suppression.","src":"haiku"},
"whipped-cream~rewind-but-i-love-you":{"haiku":"Desire to undo the past despite love persisting, wishing for second chance.","opus":"The push-pull of leaving someone you still love, running away while confessing devotion in the same breath. Built on stark repetition, farewell and attachment loop against each other endlessly. The electronic producer's sparse vocal hooks turn a simple contradiction into an emotional refrain, the ache of a goodbye that cannot quite mean itself.","sonnet":"The irreducible paradox of wanting to leave someone you love — \"rewind, I'm running away, goodbye\" and \"but I love you\" alternate until neither can win. The song is the stalemate itself, two truths that cannot coexist but refuse to resolve.","src":"haiku"},
"whirr~ease":{"haiku":"A person yearns for warmth and craves emotional closeness, afraid their feelings will fade if they cannot be expressed and held.","opus":"A quiet meditation on holding onto warm feelings against the cold, the narrator unwilling to let cherished emotions fade even as they weigh on him, finding an uneasy comfort in carrying the burden alone. It is hushed and inward. Whirr are an American shoegaze band, and this haze of buried longing, repetition, and blurred emotion fits the genre's wall-of-sound intimacy perfectly.","sonnet":"A quiet attachment to warmth that feels precarious — feelings too deep to display, carried as comfortable weight rather than burden, but always at risk of fading when cold sets in. The whole arc is contained in wanting to hold on to what is felt and not being sure the feeling is mutual or permanent.","src":"haiku"},
"whirr~press":{"haiku":"Intimacy deepens as two people gradually close distance emotionally, with gentle physical presence and mutual want building between them.","opus":"The Californian shoegaze band lets longing melt into blurred, tactile murmur. Moving through low water and slipping along another body's edges, the narrator asks to be led slowly, at their partner's tempo. Broken phrases about a soft, mounting touch echo the genre's washed-out haze, where desire lands as sensation more than statement, felt through texture and repetition.","sonnet":"Slow-building physical and emotional intimacy described as immersion and gentle pressure. One person drifts into the other gradually, wants to be taken at the other's pace, and feels the accumulation of closeness as something that shakes and builds simultaneously — tender, unhurried, and increasingly charged.","src":"haiku"},
"white-moth-black-butterfly~the-serpent":{"haiku":"Deception penetrates as corrosive force while light offers false salvation, the speaker caught between temptation and its inevitable cost.","opus":"A retelling of Eden from the tempter's side, the serpent luring toward forbidden knowledge, framing the fall as a dangerous seduction where following the light leaves you with nothing to guide you.","sonnet":"A speaker in the role of the serpent addresses someone who follows them despite the danger, aware that their deception is visible and that there is nothing in the dark to guide either of them. The relationship is framed as mutual transgression with no way back and no external moral authority to appeal to.","src":"haiku"},
"white-ward~love-exchange-failure":{"haiku":"Desperate attempts to redeem oneself through a relationship fail, transferring pain rather than healing and destroying what was cherished.","opus":"A bleak nocturne of urban loneliness and violence, empty streets and drowned innocence framing a narrator who calls himself a sinful player, offloading his weaknesses onto another and carving cruelty even as he wishes them well. White Ward are a Ukrainian band who fuse atmospheric black metal with saxophone-laced noir jazz, and this cinematic despair, blurring redemption and predation, is central to their bleak, city-lit vision.","sonnet":"A relationship mapped as mutual damage and compounded failure — feeble flesh torn apart, what once shone will never shine again, and the tenderness offered becomes a hymn to tyranny. The speaker acknowledges that their own weaknesses become weapons delegated to the other, while a drowned child and a buried adult stand for the costs of accumulated pain and lost empathy.","src":"haiku"},
"white-zombie~more-human-than-human":{"haiku":"The narrator presents themselves as a superhuman or artificial being exceeding human limits through technology, violence, and transcendence of morality.","opus":"A self-mythologizing monster's boast assembled from schlock cinema and Blade Runner imagery, the replicant pleading for extended life. The throughline is a flesh-eating, oversexed creature reveling in a synthetic, media-soaked identity, surpassing humanity precisely by being a manufactured horror.","sonnet":"A Blade Runner reference given full B-movie treatment — the narrator is a replicant or monster demanding more life, more sensation, more of everything, defining themselves as more human than the humans who made them.","src":"haiku"},
"white-zombie~super-charger-heaven-adults-only-mix-explicit":{"haiku":"Blasphemous apocalypse and infernal machinery drive a vision where divine and demonic become indistinguishable engines of destruction and rapture.","opus":"Rob Zombie's B-movie collage of blasphemy and grindhouse horror, a leering pulp Jesus, devils and hellhounds stitched into a revved-up carnival of demonic Americana meant purely for shock and swagger.","sonnet":"A hallucinatory collage of blasphemy, B-movie mythology, hellhounds, fallen angels, and supercharged nihilism fuses rock-and-roll excess with occult imagery, conjuring a \"devilman\" who runs through the speaker's head as a spirit of chaos and invention.","src":"haiku"},
"whitechapel~hickory-creek":{"opus":"A ghostly vantage from someone who has died, likely by their own hand, unseen and dissolving, speaking to a survivor who failed to register their absence and insisting the falsehoods and hurt exist only inside that person's mind.","sonnet":"About invisibility in a relationship — the narrator left long ago and was never noticed, and now addresses someone who blinded themselves to truth, saying beauty is in the eyes (now gone) while the narrator fades entirely into themselves with nothing left.","src":"sonnet"},
"whitechapel~when-a-demon-defiles-a-witch":{"haiku":"Demonic possession merges with spiritual corruption as love's absence creates psychological fracture, transforming grief into violent misanthropy.","opus":"A descent into possession and madness where grief over a lost love curdles into demonic rage, the narrator burning everything and vowing to erase a humanity he no longer trusts himself within.","sonnet":"A demon-possessed speaker has lost someone and cannot locate himself without her — \"I don't know who I am\" — raging that the world deserves to burn alongside him, proposing to erase humanity in retaliation for a love that was taken away.","src":"haiku"},
"whiteout~sold-down-the-river":{"haiku":"A betrayed person accuses a lying friend of treachery and sacrifice, refusing to forgive as they approach death with every lie the betrayer told.","opus":"A bitter reckoning with a treacherous betrayer who charmed like a snake and led the narrator like a lamb to slaughter; the deeper read is the aftermath of profound betrayal, the traitor selling his soul for gold and digging his own grave with lies while the narrator drowns in the open, moving closer to death with every breath.","sonnet":"A song about being betrayed by someone who claimed loyalty — sold out by a charming deceiver who accumulated lies to fill their pockets, leaving the narrator drowning and exposed; the refrain of going through hell and high water only to be handed over like a sacrificial animal makes the depth of the wound clear.","src":"haiku"},
"whitesnake~here-i-go-again-87":{"haiku":"A lonely drifter born to walk solitary paths abandons wasted time and moves forward, knowing the loneliness but unable to stop searching for connection.","opus":"A rootless wanderer steels himself and heads off down the one path he understands, done clinging to old promises yet still a soul craving rescue as he trudges through solitary dreams. Whitesnake's slick 1987 remake turned David Coverdale's bluesy resolve into a hair-metal staple, its lonely self-reliance and plea for endurance welded forever to that arena-sized hook.","sonnet":"A solitary wanderer with no map and no company accepts his nature as a perpetual drifter, drawing strength from the decision to stop wasting time on what cannot be held. The lonely road is not chosen reluctantly — it is the only road he has ever known, and he commits to it again.","src":"haiku"},
"whitesnake~here-i-go-again-usa-remix":{"haiku":"Solitude becomes identity; the speaker walks alone by design, carrying only yesterday's promises.","opus":"The lone drifter's anthem of leaving the past behind to keep looking, weary but resolved, still praying for the strength to press on down the only path he's ever walked while quietly aching to be rescued.","sonnet":"A solitary drifter with no destination and no one commits to the only road he knows — forward and alone — clinging to the dream of love while acknowledging that loneliness is his natural condition. The defiance is tinged with longing; the resolve is as much resignation as choice.","src":"haiku"},
"who-shot-scott~lil-bit-of-hot":{"haiku":"Desire for toxicity over organic perfection reveals craving for risk and friction; safety becomes unbearable.","opus":"Attraction to someone wholesome and grounded that the narrator perversely wants to make toxic—he can't help craving a little bit of chaos in something too pure and organic. He wrestles with a self that ruins good things while admitting she gets something real out of him.","sonnet":"A song about wanting a good-natured woman to have a little more edge or danger — the speaker likes her but finds her too wholesome, too organic, and wants to add something toxic and destabilizing to the dynamic; the attraction is real but the craving for heat overrides contentment.","src":"haiku"},
"who-shot-scott~loners-anthem":{"haiku":"Social anxiety and isolation are protective; solitude preserves authenticity against the performative demands of community.","opus":"An unapologetic portrait of chosen solitude and social anxiety, insisting isolation is genuinely better than the party. The narrator distances himself from friends who never really were, weary of superficial talk and self-conscious in every crowd. It updates the loner archetype for a hip-hop generation, defiant and wounded, forever wanting only to go home.","sonnet":"Social anxiety as identity: banned from the light, anxious and anti-aligned, slipping out the back door and pulling the mask off — the speaker knows he's a loner, doesn't like the loneliness, but insists he's better that way and just wants to go home, treating introversion as both wound and badge.","src":"haiku"},
"who-shot-scott~the-data":{"haiku":"Information colonizes the soul: feeding systems that mine the self for profit, while the body rebels against being quantified into submission.","opus":"Refusing to sell his soul to an industry that wants to edit him down to numbers and streaming data. The defiance is a rapper guarding his meaning against demons who measure worth in bands.","sonnet":"A song about refusing to let metrics, industry, and outside validation define artistic identity — the speaker is being played by people obsessed with numbers and data, and insists they are not a slave to that system even as it crowds in from all sides.","src":"haiku"},
"who-ya-extended~half-moon":{"haiku":"Dim stars fade while lovers reach for untouchable moons, desires spiraling downward. Half-light prevents escape; the speaker drowns in unfulfilled longing, wanting to understand why incompleteness persists.","opus":"A Japanese reverie on unreachable light and the ache of never being filled. The narrator gazes at a moon that seems within grasp yet dissolves into darkness when approached, wanting only a reason for wounds that will not heal. Steeped in anime-adjacent Japanese rock melancholy, it dwells in a dream one cannot escape.","sonnet":"A figure who looked up at stars that seemed reachable now gazes down and finds them dissolved into darkness, unable to free themselves from a half-moon that melts rather than guides — the quest for healing was really just craving an excuse to stay wounded, and there is no escape from the dissolving light.","src":"haiku"},
"who-ya-extended~vivid-vice":{"haiku":"A person breaks free from moral constraint and hesitation, embracing sin and recklessness to destroy the false structures imprisoning them.","opus":"A ferocious internal battle depicted through the swelling surface tension of a life about to burst: the narrator confronts his own hidden, monstrous nature (vice) and vows no more regret, resolving to tame his sin and grudges, claw to the summit and change reality even if the truth is cruel, wearing a mask over the roiling love-and-hate inside.","sonnet":"Standing at the edge of a twisted end with the surface tension of life stretched to breaking — the narrator refuses to creep in silence along the tightrope and instead chooses to run, taming accumulated sin and grudge to chase the voice ahead, slashing through the chain of suffering.","src":"haiku"},
"who-ya-extended~wander-wraith":{"haiku":"Emotional instability and jealousy consume consciousness; hesitation calcifies into suffering while conflicting impulses collide.","opus":"A dense Japanese lyric of instinct, doubt and inner conflict, where hesitation itself becomes a habitual torment. Fate is dismissed as mere routing while demons set traps and the self drowns in paradox. In the band's cinematic Japanese-rock idiom, it screams from a sea of jealousy, wandering like a wraith through fabricated myth.","sonnet":"A Japanese-language song about instinct turning obsessive: small heartbeats the narrator wanted passed on go unnoticed, jealousy described as a sea one cannot stop drowning in, rumors carved like a keepsake — a wraith-like wandering between what was real and what was a trap built from loneliness.","src":"haiku"},
"wienners~a-1ol1ki9":{"haiku":"A nostalgic hymn to youth and fleeting moments—celebrating audacity, music, and the refusal to fade despite inevitable loss.","opus":"Wienners built their name on hyperactive, candy-colored punk, so this backward glance at youth carries unusual weight from a band that usually sprints forward. Packed lunches and moonlit noise become a century-long longing that finally lands on a stage. Addressed to the person who made those days paradise, it says farewell to youth while refusing its fading, betting that the brightest moment is always the next one.","sonnet":"A nostalgic hymn to youth and the dream of making it as a musician — packed lunches, electric noise under moonlight, a hundred-year-old longing finally bearing fruit on stage — addressed partly to a beloved person who made those days feel like paradise. The emotional through-line is bittersweet: saying goodbye to youth while insisting its colors never fade and that the best moment is still the one coming next.","src":"haiku"},
"wienners~a-f001p6":{"haiku":"Under full moon, spirits descend to meet the living who light the way. Longing persists beyond death's threshold; the speaker waits eternally, burning signals across worlds for reunion their beloved won't answer.","opus":"Wienners frame their exuberance around Japanese folk ritual here, setting a love song at the obon festival where the dead are welcomed home. A spirit waits at the crossroads by the sending-fire, and the band's usual brightness curdles into unbearable longing. The final message inverts the holiday's logic: one who forgets for the beloved's sake, begging only to be remembered in return.","sonnet":"On the night of the full moon festival (obon), a spirit waits at the crossroads path to the underworld for a beloved who has died — sending-fire lit, longing unbearable — and delivers a final message: \"I will forget you for your sake, but please, never forget me; I love you.\"","src":"haiku"},
"wild-cherry~play-that-funky-music":{"haiku":"A musician discovers rhythm and groove after rigid convention, surrendering to infectious beats that transcend genre divisions and unite disparate communities.","opus":"A rock singer's origin story of learning to embrace funk when a crowd demanded it, converting from resistance to lifelong groove after that one transformative night.","sonnet":"A white rock musician crosses over to disco after a crowd shouts him into it, initially resisting and then discovering that embracing the groove is the lesson he had to learn — the story ends with him fully converted, funking out in every way and never wanting to go back.","src":"haiku"},
"wilderun~distraction-i":{"haiku":"Terror grips someone who sees everything as unknowable and unreachable despite being alive and fed by the sun. They count endlessly while fear paralyzes them and everything slips away.","opus":"A dread-laced meditation on forgetting what one is most terrified to lose, tallying things without purpose while the sun sustains a life the narrator is too preoccupied to register. Fear takes up permanent residence as he's challenged to swell into everything and confront his own ruin. Wilderun, an American progressive folk-metal band, open this suite with orchestral grandeur and philosophical anxiety about attention and mortality.","sonnet":"Anxiety about time and mortality that cannot locate its own object — there is no clear reason for the dread, nothing tangible to point at, yet something is being lost or is about to be lost and the speaker cannot stop counting and recounting in search of it. The sun provides light and life and is tracked obsessively across the sky, and none of it helps.","src":"haiku"},
"wilderun~distraction-ii":{"haiku":"The song portrays the futility of trying to control reality through effort and obsession, as time and dissolution approach faster than anyone can stop. Mountains and rivers persist while the equation falls apart.","opus":"A meditation on human habit and denial, laboring away and keeping the machinery running to put off the day of reckoning, until a restless curiosity glimpses something starting to fall apart. Even grand, monumental efforts only delay the unavoidable. Wilderun's cinematic folk-metal makes this cycle of avoidance feel sweeping and sorrowful.","sonnet":"The mind trying to pull itself back from cosmic vertigo — from the scale of millions down to the intimate, from the map with no destination to the minutes at hand. But a curious mind drifts, peeks around the corner of what is approaching, and what it sees is something coming undone faster than any amount of labor or wound-tending can hold back.","src":"haiku"},
"wilderun~far-from-where-dreams-unfurl":{"haiku":"A person trapped in endless rumination and distraction cannot hide from the reality of death. The more they build and conjure, the more they lose sight of the actual world.","opus":"A cautionary fable about a man who spends a lifetime lost in obsessive planning and busywork to hold off his terror of dying, only to realize that fixating on ambition costs him the actual living world. Wilderun deliver it as grand orchestral folk-metal, part parable, part funeral hymn about mortality catching everyone regardless.","sonnet":"A meditation on a life spent buried in the machinery of purpose — a man feeding his ruminating mind while his body floats forward as though it were a prize, never arriving. The advice is to run from the place where dreams unfold because in chasing them you claim the land and lose the world, and the tower of accumulated meaning will fall anyway, leaving only the return to earth.","src":"haiku"},
"wilderun~identifier-radio-edit":{"haiku":"The impossibility of transcending material form and the human need to rationalize the abstract. The speaker struggles against the corruption of purity by language and embodied existence.","opus":"A dense metaphysical reckoning wrestles with transcendence and the compulsion to name and justify pure being, the narrator falling prey to the identifier that can carry him no higher until he learns to let go. Wilderun's symphonic progressive metal suits this cosmic scope, dissolving self into smoke and star-sea, deconstructing gods and language in pursuit of an existence beyond corporeal, patterned form.","sonnet":"A philosophical struggle to stop labelling and corrupting a transcendent inner experience with language, morality, and human pattern-making. The speaker keeps falling back into the naming reflex even while recognising that the primordial thing he hears existed before words, before gods, before stories.","src":"haiku"},
"wilderun~o-resolution":{"haiku":"Striving and devotion never reach their goal; resolution itself is a curse that keeps spiraling. The speaker calls for resolution to leave the earth, exhausted by endless struggle.","opus":"A bitter philosophical lament that treats the drive toward finality and closure as a curse, arguing that lives waste themselves in pointless struggle and that meaning survives only in remembrance. Wilderun give this weary, hard-won pessimism sweeping orchestral scope, the singer finally wishing the whole impulse toward conclusions gone for good.","sonnet":"Resolution itself indicted as a curse — every act of closure turns out to be another form of servitude, and the pursuit of finished things is a way of avoiding the mystery that made life worth anything. The light that was supposed to illuminate betrayed; the darkness that remains is not enemy territory but the only honest place left, and resolution is asked to simply leave.","src":"haiku"},
"wilderun~passenger":{"haiku":"Pain persists as a permanent passenger while a parasite disguised as partner once fed stories of woe; meaning itself becomes a parasitic trap.","opus":"American symphonic-prog band Wilderun spin an elaborate parable in which suffering is personified as a clinging parasite that insists it is indispensable even as it feeds on both anguish and awe. Framed by a river-like man and a mountain-like woman, the narrator debates whether this creature is his true source or a leech grafted onto him, and is finally urged to let go of meaning entirely.","sonnet":"A parasite — perhaps creativity, perhaps suffering, perhaps narrative itself — examines two people of extraordinary grace and reveals how it feeds on them. It sustains their depth by keeping pain present, refuses to be removed, and ultimately asks whether it is the origin of all meaning or merely a creature sewn permanently into the heart.","src":"haiku"},
"wilderun~scentless-core-fading":{"haiku":"Ephemeral grace dissipates; presence decays into memory faster than experience unfolds, leaving scent but no substance to grasp.","opus":"A spirit so tainted by the here-and-now that even love's touch cannot reach it, clutching the frail hope of what tomorrow might one day recall as a warped remedy, wanting nothing more than to keep some dwindling good thing in view.","sonnet":"A quiet resignation — if air and love could touch the speaker, they would still feel far and gone; the scent of today is too foul, and tomorrow's memory may be different, but for now all that can be hoped is that something stays within sight.","src":"haiku"},
"wilderun~sleeping-ambassadors-of-the-sun":{"haiku":"Seekers of truth discover that liars thrive without consequence while understanding erodes and minds decay from the burden of awakening.","opus":"A dense philosophical reverie on knowledge and its cost, warning that understanding curdles the moment it is grasped, that the mind is flesh and thinking bears a price, while liars rest easy as sleeping ambassadors. It ends craving everything at once. Wilderun are an American symphonic and progressive folk-metal band known for orchestral grandeur and literary ambition, and this abstract, poetic weight suits their sweeping, cerebral compositions.","sonnet":"A meditation on the futility of pursuing sacred truth — those who hold it lie dormant, and the moment of understanding vanishes the instant it is glimpsed. The wandering creatures who approach the truth most closely do so unconsciously, while the ones who seek it deliberately are cautioned that the mind itself carries a cost and that knowing everything at once may be indistinguishable from knowing nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"wilderun~the-tyranny-of-imagination":{"haiku":"Imagination imprisons someone in constructed reality, turning their blood into ink and dreams into a prison where nature's truth dies. They become enslaved to their own mind's narratives.","opus":"A haunting tale of narrative and imagination swallowing reality, a summoning story that pushes the present beyond grasp and makes the dreamer a servant of some higher truth, unlearning mortality as a doorway to another realm takes shape in the mind. What flows from the eyes becomes what flows from the pen. Wilderun end their suite with this cerebral, lavishly orchestrated caution about the mind devouring the world.","sonnet":"The mind's story-making capacity revealed as a trap — the narrative that gives life meaning also places real experience just out of reach, replacing it with ink-saturated vision that gradually makes the actual world untasteable. The speaker slides from timid striving toward an obsessive interior world in which death becomes unlearnable because the imagination has already colonized it, leaving only the thought of thought.","src":"haiku"},
"wilderun~the-unimaginable-zero-summer":{"haiku":"A seeker gathers knowledge and imposes order on chaos, but waits too long and loses the ability to act. Their careful preparation becomes a prison of their own making.","opus":"Framed by Wordsworth's grief for a childhood vision now faded, it traces a man who tries to safeguard life's meaning by amassing knowledge and erecting a stronghold of order. His fixation on control and dodging chance hardens into madness; he gathers every fragment of existence's melody but arranges it wrongly and cannot ever release his grip, so comprehension dies just as he nearly seizes it.","sonnet":"A man driven by compulsive self-improvement and control accumulates everything he believes he needs but arranges it all in the wrong order, too late, and slips into madness rather than fulfillment. The song draws on Wordsworth's lament for lost childhood wonder and builds it into a parable about the self-consuming futility of total control.","src":"haiku"},
"wilderun~when-the-fire-and-the-rose-were-one":{"haiku":"A being reflects on losing identity and sense of self over time, seeking release from the illusions of name and future, yearning for primal unity.","opus":"Symphonic metal act Wilderun close their song on T. S. Eliot's mystical imagery, describing a pure state of being that existed before the self hardened into ego, ambition, and identity. Weary of striving, the narrator asks to be freed from those burdens and reaches a serene vision of ultimate reconciliation, where opposites finally merge.","sonnet":"A meditation on the state before selfhood — a pre-narrative existence without name or future, in which fire caused no pain and everything touched was simply real. As time and identity accumulate, that primal wholeness becomes inaccessible, and the closing lines invoke a mystical reunion where opposites dissolve back into one.","src":"haiku"},
"wildways~a-1e3xb1z":{"haiku":"Spiritual corruption through greed; money severs relationships and promises, leaving only emptiness regardless of forgiveness or redemption's possibility.","opus":"Wildways emerged from Russia's metalcore scene with an ear tuned to Western excess, and this track turns that vantage on money as universal possession. The narrator hovers between total surrender to a transactional world and refusal of its price, invoking Jesus as easy absolution. The band's cynicism lands on a bleak resignation: whichever path is chosen, everything gets given away regardless.","sonnet":"Everyone is possessed by the need for money, and the narrator is caught between surrendering completely to a transactional world and refusing to pay the price that relationships and existence demand — invoking Jesus as a convenient absolution — while conceding that you end up giving everything away regardless.","src":"haiku"},
"wildways~a-1gr6y61":{"haiku":"A defiant punk declaration of building one's own empire—rejecting the system, creating new culture for the outcasts and rebels.","opus":"This bilingual Ukrainian-Russian cut catches Wildways in an act of scene-defiant self-invention, the band declaring indifference to any existing empire in favor of building their own. Unknown to the new school and alien to the lowbrows, they weaponize their outsider status. The aggression is really a refusal to be claimed, a group insisting on belonging to no one's power but their own construction.","sonnet":"A Ukrainian-Russian bilingual track where the band declares independence from any existing empire — they don't care about your empire, they'll build their own; they're unknown to the new school and alien to the lowbrows. The emotional core is the aggressive self-invention of a group that refuses to be claimed by any existing scene or power.","src":"haiku"},
"wildways~a-bo2dsl":{"haiku":"Hide-and-seek as childhood innocence versus predatory evil; persistent seeking ensures evil cannot break you.","opus":"Wildways channel their metalcore intensity into a parable, turning a child's game of hide-and-seek into a meditation on fear that outlasts childhood. The lurking wolf hunts the unbeliever while time keeps every name and refuses to erase the pages. The band's characteristic defiance surfaces as a grim clarity: evil cannot break them, no bright era is coming, and only readiness counts.","sonnet":"A hide-and-seek game used as a metaphor for childhood versus adult fear — the wolf always lurking, the unbeliever hunted, time remembering all names and refusing to erase the pages — defiance that evil cannot break us, while noting that a bright era is not coming and only readiness matters.","src":"haiku"},
"wilki~urke":{"haiku":"A lonely person reflects on lost happiness and life's changes, raising a toast to enduring memories and the brief moments that give life meaning.","opus":"A Polish reflection on solitude and getting through hard times, a man alone in the midday bustle, told to trust fate and not lose faith in dreams, then raising a toast to better days and to every memory still alive in us. Wilki are a long-running Polish rock band led by Robert Gawlinski, and this warm, wistful drinking anthem to endurance and remembrance is very much in their melodic, everyman tradition.","sonnet":"A Polish drinking toast and resignation rolled into one — a solitary figure in a midday crowd, unable to fix anything, told to trust fate and wait for the wind to arrive on its own. The repeated toasting is for better times, for every day still lived, for memories that still breathe inside the people who carry them, at least a little while longer.","src":"haiku"},
"will-joseph-cook~biggest-fan":{"haiku":"Devotion transforms intimacy into fandom; the speaker collapses distance through obsessive attention.","opus":"The sweet frustration of a long-distance relationship, the narrator cast as his partner's devoted fan across time zones, tired of writing letters and waiting, aching for the real person instead of the idea.","sonnet":"A long-distance devotion expressed through the language of celebrity fandom — phone photos, show tickets, time zone calculations — as the speaker insists the other person understand the depth and particularity of what he feels, needing more than letters and missed calls to make it real. The tone is light but the urgency underneath is genuine.","src":"haiku"},
"will-smith~men-in-black-from-men-in-black-soundtrack":{"haiku":"Secret government agents in black suits protect Earth from aliens while erasing human memory of contact. They are an unseen defense force operating outside all records.","opus":"A playful blockbuster theme narrated as a black-suited secret agent, guarding earth against alien scum, wiping memories with a neuralyzer, insisting he does not officially exist. It is all swagger, wit, and dancefloor commands. Will Smith rode his late-1990s film stardom into pop hits, and this 1997 smash, built on a Patrice Rushen sample, epitomizes his radio-friendly, family-safe rap that turned a summer movie into an inescapable anthem.","sonnet":"The Men in Black introduce themselves as galaxy defenders who erase what civilians were not meant to see — no names, no fingerprints, no official existence. It is a comic-book mythology played completely straight: the good guys in black Ray-Bans keep the peace with alien threats while making sure ordinary people forget they were ever in danger.","src":"haiku"},
"willow~maybe-it-s-my-fault":{"haiku":"Lost time and self-blame consume the speaker as romantic attachment metastasizes into obsessive regret over a fleeting connection.","opus":"The spiraling self-interrogation after a promising connection curdles into conflict. Meeting someone at a party gives way to butterflies, then broken promises and the wish to rewind. Willow's alt-rock turn frames blame as a moving target, oscillating between accusing the other and doubting whether she herself deserves forgiveness.","sonnet":"Jealousy and betrayal erupt over a woman the speaker once said was fine — she seems nice — now revealed as the source of a fight the speaker cannot decide whether to forgive. The question of whose fault it is circles without resolution.","src":"haiku"},
"willow~perfectly-not-close-to-me-ft-yves-tumor":{"haiku":"Internal pressure and existential emptiness consume the speaker, who dissolves under the weight of their own fractured being.","opus":"An unnameable inner pressure and self-disgust that makes the speaker want to escape their own life entirely. The refusal to talk about it coexists with the confession that they're utterly falling apart.","sonnet":"A person under unbearable internal pressure — unable to articulate it, surrounded by people who seem like victims — just needs to escape their own life entirely, feeling so broken that disappearing feels like the only option.","src":"haiku"},
"willow~purge-ft-siiickbrain":{"haiku":"Numbness offers refuge but deepens fracture; the speaker sinks into darkness unable to remember themselves or find the way back.","opus":"Numbing pain and purging the darkness to survive, the singer lost and unable to find herself amid shattered perspective and her own lies, clinging to the fragile belief that it's going to be fine.","sonnet":"The speaker sinks into numbness to escape pain, letting darkness blanket the feeling rather than confronting it; 'purge' names the compulsive return to the abyss — she tries to come back to herself but the descent keeps winning, and the perspective from inside it resists both recovery and full surrender.","src":"haiku"},
"willy-mason~youth-on-a-spit":{"opus":"A bleak protest against a culture that grinds up and consumes its young, depicting society as cannibalistic captors while the numbed narrator defiantly insists he's already dead and beyond killing, past pain and past saving.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of a society that devours its young — the poor are crushed under economic and cultural forces while the powerful exploit and distract, leaving an entire generation numb, caged by wage labor and systemic rot, declaring themselves already dead so they cannot be killed again.","src":"sonnet"},
"wind-walkers~euphoria-what-a-feeling":{"haiku":"Numbing exhaustion and chase for transcendence leave the speaker fractured; the weight of conformity crushes while euphoria remains always out of reach, a mirage dissolving before consumption.","opus":"A desperate craving for euphoria as an escape from numbness, falling apart in full view and wondering when enough will finally be enough, the collapse feeling necessary once there's nothing more to forfeit.","sonnet":"A numbed person who has been running in circles and breaking under pressure craves euphoria as the only alternative to a life where even the gun-clearing-the-head metaphor feels necessary — \"when will enough be enough\" is both question and unanswered plea.","src":"haiku"},
"windrunner~cedar-reimagined":{"haiku":"A bittersweet meditation on separation and parting, where two people drift apart despite their bond, settling for the memory of love rather than its presence.","opus":"After months of feeling broken, the narrator clings to a departing love, pleading with them to stay unchanged even if they're parted forever, and treating a clear, untainted mind as his only lifeline. Windrunner's Vietnamese progressive metalcore wraps the goodbye in sensory reverie, framing separation as a cost paid and every deed a reflection sent ahead.","sonnet":"A parting described through sensory memory — the green of someone's eyes, a misty scent — becomes a farewell that insists on permanence of affection even through separation. The speaker asks only that the other remain true to themselves, carrying their bond inwardly even if they never meet again.","src":"haiku"},
"windrunner~heather":{"haiku":"Betrayal and obsession leave deep scars; the speaker burns from words and unmet devotion, unable to move past naive trust.","opus":"A sleepless narrator obsesses over a manipulative lover who left him high and dry, calling their bond a treason, likening cutting words to knives and cyanide while begging for a sign that she is still his. Vietnamese metalcore band Windrunner set this fixated heartbreak against genre-blending intensity, the fire on his mind refusing to quiet as he berates his own naive gullibility.","sonnet":"A person stalled in the aftermath of a trust-destroying relationship — naive, drained, unable to move on. The betrayal sits in the ignition like a key that no longer turns, and what finally loosens the grip is not resolution but a midnight moment of exhausted release, setting the other free in order to survive.","src":"haiku"},
"windrunner~sakura":{"opus":"The unraveling of a once-secure bond after the two survived hardship together only to come out strangers, no longer who they were. Deadened by the loss, the singer would sooner feel nothing at all than carry this grief, longing to rewind and steer them both clear of the wreckage.","sonnet":"Two people who fought through storms together have grown apart and lost sight of what they once were to each other — one narrator would rather feel nothing than feel the loss, while wishing they could turn back time to lead the other out of the mess before it burned them both to gray.","src":"sonnet"},
"windrunner~sardonyx":{"haiku":"People are blind to suffering around them, offering hollow patronizing comfort while true change requires unity and willingness to stand together.","opus":"The song opens by doubting whether any of the fight is worthwhile, aiming its bitterness at the comfortable and oblivious who ignore those sinking around them, then turns into a defiant rallying cry. Windrunner escalate from disillusionment and manipulation toward collective resolve, a battered narrator vowing that solidarity will keep them from cracking.","sonnet":"A challenge to those above the clouds to look down at those drowning beneath their comfortable altitude. The lyric pivots from indictment to urgent solidarity — the speaker one step from the edge, calling for collective resistance, insisting that mutual grip is the only thing capable of preventing the final break.","src":"haiku"},
"windwaker~apathy":{"haiku":"Numbness paralyzes commitment and growth, turning repetition into trap while apathy spreads like contamination through mind and relationship. The speaker cycles through the same empty sensation, unable to break or escape.","opus":"Emotional numbness as both shield and prison, a narrator who craves quiet while another chases violence, confessing that loving him is pointless and repeating that it's over until the deadness itself becomes the whole story.","sonnet":"A speaker wrestling with emotional numbness and self-sabotage in a relationship, admitting that loving them is pointless and framing the whole thing as already over, even as cyclic self-opposition — \"my own opponent\" — keeps dragging them back into the spiral.","src":"haiku"},
"windwaker~beautiful":{"haiku":"The speaker struggles with identity, control, and chemical escape while questioning the meaning of beauty and what remains when systems collapse.","opus":"A frantic interrogation of beauty and belonging that fractures into drug-blitzed chaos and cult-like commands to conform, ending on the idea that meaning feeds on death. It's about chasing an ideal that dissolves you as you reach for it.","sonnet":"A fragmented song that juxtaposes longing for beauty and belonging with a descent into chemical dissociation — the surface question \"what does it mean to be beautiful?\" gives way to inner chaos, media manipulation, and someone using drugs to feel something. The song doesn't resolve the question; it shows how far from an answer the narrator actually is.","src":"haiku"},
"windwaker~dopamine-freestyle":{"haiku":"Hedonistic spiral through digital excess; pleasure systems overload while aimlessness and detachment masquerade as invulnerability and strength.","opus":"A hollow spiral of hookups, flexing, and endless scrolling, a soul lost to the Twitter app and dopamine hits, chasing likes and racks in a cold world while the emptiness underneath never actually gets fed.","sonnet":"A frenetic, self-aware stream of sexual bravado, social media addiction, and hyper-stimulated living — the speaker loses their soul to Twitter, blocks women after two weeks, and craves the dopamine loop of likes and texts. Underneath the flex is a line about waiting for someone to hold and the cold world draining everything.","src":"haiku"},
"windwaker~lucy":{"haiku":"The speaker seeks liberation from symptoms and reality, calling across distance for release from belief. When close, the world feels bigger; they strip gold but find fear and love unchanged.","opus":"A trippy meditation on ego death as a route to healing, the narrator surrendering his identity and beliefs so a beloved figure can set him free while he retreats home damaged but protected. Windwaker, an Australian metalcore band, wrap this tender psychedelic release in electronic-tinged, melodic heaviness.","sonnet":"A song addressed to someone — Lucy, maybe, maybe not — whose presence makes the world larger and whose absence leaves the body aching across a distance that feels cellular. The speaker is healing from something, having been sick and now running home, and the connection with this person is the thing that makes the tunnels through the self empty out cleanly rather than trap what is inside.","src":"haiku"},
"windwaker~my-empire":{"haiku":"Through healing and inner peace, the narrator transcends oppressive systems and rises above earthly struggle.","opus":"Time mends the narrator into a golden sense of fulfillment as his empire carries him skyward, refusing to quit or retreat despite would-be tyrants and memories that keep him small. Australian metalcore band Windwaker set this triumphant self-determination against darker verses of unnamed sickness and stalled progress, resolve and struggle straining toward a hard-won peace.","sonnet":"A person in recovery from oppression and stagnation discovers that time and internal peace have built something no external system could provide. The empire is not conquest but resilience — rising through clouds, refusing to return home, understanding that clarity about the past is no longer required for the ascent to continue.","src":"haiku"},
"windwaker~nighthawk":{"haiku":"The speaker runs toward a new moment, shedding old selves while building stability within the unknown. Finding one's place means embracing the journey and the feeling of forward momentum.","opus":"Restlessness and the urge to escape, resolving into a hard-won sense of belonging and momentum. The narrator drinks down his feelings, gets lost in the crowd, then steps into the moment ready to own it. The Australian band's genre-blurring energy channels anxious searching into an anthem of finally feeling on his way.","sonnet":"Restlessness and the desire to belong: the narrator has felt like running away, drunk down their feelings, and now tries to get present — lost in the crowd but steadying, wanting to fit in while also wanting to fly, landing on \"get in it\" as the only answer to the drift.","src":"haiku"},
"windwaker~reject":{"haiku":"A person wrestles with inner demons and wandering thoughts, unable to sleep or understand reality while oscillating between external and internal warfare.","opus":"Insomnia and self-blame spiraling as inner voices offer no answers, the mind turning against itself. The refrain's litany of rejecting and infecting captures the futile impulse to discard the very self that can't be protected from its own thoughts.","sonnet":"Someone drowning in self-rejection and anxious rumination — voices in the head, inability to sleep, watching everything fall apart inside, caught between the urge to fight their demons and the feeling they can't protect anything.","src":"haiku"},
"windwaker~tabula-rasa":{"haiku":"Erasure of memory and identity become freedom, where the speaker reinvents without past weight. Destiny collapses into present moment, undivided by history.","opus":"A reckoning with the void under fame and its spoils, disoriented and carried off by the tide while professing to understand nothing, treating an empty slate as the whole of things and everything else as dimming to darkness. The blank slate becomes all that remains.","sonnet":"A speaker navigating social-media metrics and the noise of trend culture — blue ticks, big wigs, squids — claims a blank-slate identity ('tabula rasa is everything') as the only honest position; the dazed refrain of being swept by waves and wishing for a day when the mind doesn't scatter sits underneath the bravado like an admission of exhaustion.","src":"haiku"},
"windwaker~the-sitch":{"haiku":"Describes obsession with pain and regret while watching oneself crumble, unable to escape self-imposed cycles. Tired of failing to meet expectations.","opus":"A weary confession of stagnation and regret, the narrator unable to replace who he used to be, tired and crumbling under someone else's control, telling himself obsession and second-guessing keep him from getting it right, yet still breathing through the nothingness. Windwaker are an Australian metalcore band who blend electronic pop textures with heaviness, and this mix of self-recrimination and fragile hope fits their emotionally charged, genre-bending style.","sonnet":"Being pinned under the weight of past choices and an obsessive loop that prevents moving forward — still breathing but falling further behind, too tired to meet the expectations placed on them. The speaker acknowledges obsession as the mechanism trapping them and circles back to the same refrain of survival without progress.","src":"haiku"},
"winnie~let-it-die-always-see-you-there":{"haiku":"Surrender and persistence intertwine as the speaker releases attachment while holding onto vision. Dissonance and disconnection plague the attempt to see clearly through blindness.","opus":"Surrender as strategy, letting things collapse in the hope of making a difference or reaching something near perfection. Amid hopelessness and unresolved tension, a familiar presence keeps recurring. The lyric's fractured, almost translated phrasing leans into ambiguity, suspending the listener between disaster and the faint promise of release.","sonnet":"Something broken — a relationship, a hope — is repeatedly told to die, yet the speaker keeps seeing the other person there; the push to let go is undercut by the persistent presence of the one they are trying to release, circling between hopelessness and unwilling attachment.","src":"haiku"},
"winona-fighter~hamms-in-a-glass":{"haiku":"Drowning in stress and numbness, the speaker cannot maintain civility; agitation festers beneath the surface, threatening violent rupture.","opus":"A cheap-beer breakdown, pulled over, broke and swamped by everything beyond your grip, backed into the corner of your own mind and barely holding on, asking whether anyone else is this wound up too.","sonnet":"A stressed, broke, impulsive narrator pulled over and drowning in things they can't control is 'a little stressed out' — understatedly, repeatedly — and oscillates between wanting to walk away from everything and not knowing how to play nice in the life they're stuck inside.","src":"haiku"},
"witch-fever~beauty-and-grace":{"haiku":"Female sexuality and transgression recast demonic beauty as liberation; carnal power and lunar cyclicity reject Christian morality.","opus":"A furious reckoning with religious shame branded onto the female body, the beast's mark and the loss of faith entwined. The narrator refuses to bury her sin, tearing off imposed thorns while a rising moon signals defiant sexuality. The Manchester punk band weaponizes doom-laden grunge against Catholic guilt and inherited disgrace.","sonnet":"A speaker born into a body framed as both sacred and shameful — carrying thorns since birth, forsaken by Christ, watching the moon rise — claims the phrase \"beauty and grace\" for herself while naming it a disgrace too, refusing to resolve the contradiction and instead wearing it as an emblem of survival.","src":"haiku"},
"witch-fever~in-the-resurrect":{"haiku":"The speaker asserts manipulative dominance in a twisted entanglement, claiming sole agency while tormenting and controlling another person.","opus":"Manchester punk band Witch Fever twist religious and bodily imagery into a seizure of power. The narrator lures and dares an aggressor into her trap, converting apparent submission into total control as she names herself something reborn and singular. Corrupted-purity images set desire against menace, and she vows to unmake anyone who dares touch her, turning dominance into defiance.","sonnet":"Dominance asserted through sacred and carnal imagery. She positions herself as the singular, unkillable force in an encounter built on power and provocation — neither victim nor supplicant but the resurrect itself, the only one, turning every act of pressure back outward as ownership of the scene.","src":"haiku"},
"with-confidence~icarus":{"haiku":"Ambition triggers self-destruction as the speaker reaches beyond his grasp; paternal recklessness teaches only the inevitability of falling when flying too high.","opus":"The Icarus myth as a bitter address to a parent who lived off the singer's labor and never learned, urging the next generation to fly at the sun and drop on their own wings rather than repeat that flight.","sonnet":"A coming-of-age reckoning with someone who preached lessons they never absorbed — the Icarus myth maps onto a person who flies toward the sun on borrowed wings, choosing collision over stagnation, while the speaker urges them to remember the cost of that pride.","src":"haiku"},
"with-confidence~jaded":{"haiku":"Mutual inadequacy in a relationship where both partners fail to meet each other's needs, despite genuine effort and care.","opus":"A tender but hopeless breakup confession: the singer loves his partner yet is convinced their fundamental incompatibility, like geometric shapes that will never fit together, dooms the relationship. He fears getting close will only taint her brighter future, so he grieves what he already knows he can never give.","sonnet":"The speaker acknowledges an incompatibility at the heart of a relationship — one person wants breath and the other wants to bleed — and despite genuine care, admits he can never be everything this person needs, oscillating between the wish he could and the honest recognition that he cannot.","src":"haiku"},
"with-confidence~moving-boxes":{"haiku":"Dissolution of a temporary partnership, with the speaker detached and cold while the other grieves the impending separation.","opus":"The end of a live-in relationship rendered as literally packing up, the narrator calling himself a burden and burning the shared memories as one moves out and they become separate lives.","sonnet":"A relationship is being physically and emotionally dismantled: one person is leaving, the other is numbing themselves to it, and both are described as temporary — the act of moving boxes into separate spaces is both literal and a ritual sealing of the end, the memories burning as the separation completes.","src":"haiku"},
"with-confidence~sing-to-me":{"haiku":"Romantic transcendence overcomes temporal anxiety and doubt, as two people find completeness in each other's presence.","opus":"A hopeful plea to a lover named Emily to trust that time is only a construct and stay present with him, offering everything he has while fighting off the doubt and darkness closing in.","sonnet":"The speaker addresses Emily directly, reassuring her that time and doubt are just constructs, that she is exactly where she should be, and that he is willing to give her everything. It is a song of total, almost anxious devotion — watching someone he loves struggle and wanting desperately to be her anchor.","src":"haiku"},
"with-confidence~that-something":{"haiku":"Finding unexpected love that infuses emptiness with purpose, someone who seeps into skin and becomes essential.","opus":"Pop-punk falling headlong, the disorienting rush of finding a love that fills the empty spaces and feels like finally breathing. Wide-eyed wonder that so much can come from nothing.","sonnet":"Someone's arrival has given a broken person a reason to breathe again — a feeling of \"that something\" they had been missing — and the speaker moves from the wreckage of what came before to a wide-open declaration that this love has crept into his skin and refuses to leave.","src":"haiku"},
"wither~use-me":{"haiku":"A coward abandons the speaker, leaving them alone to die; users extract value until they get their fill and never return, making deals on terms of exposure and theft.","opus":"A blistering callout of a spineless exploiter who abandoned the narrator and climbed over him, terror in their gaze and deceit on their tongue; the sharper warning is that those who endlessly grab without remorse will themselves be consumed and cast off someday, stranded atop a stolen perch, while the narrator swears they'll answer for it and quits the whole cutthroat contest.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who exploited and then abandoned the narrator — the song is addressed to a coward who used people as stepping stones, and builds from cold fury to a blunt warning that betrayal will be repaid, while the narrator confesses to never having felt so completely alone.","src":"haiku"},
"within-destruction~lotus":{"haiku":"Pain becomes the only proof of existence; numbness threatened by treatment transforms torture into salvation, addiction to suffering preferred over redemption.","opus":"Addiction and self-harm tangled up with love, where a lover or a drug promises relief but the narrator grips that hurt as the sole thing holding his sanity together, begging to be buried rather than mended.","sonnet":"A disturbing conflation of pain, pleasure, and submission — the speaker craves being hurt and dominated as the only thing keeping them sane, their broken thoughts sealed inside a hollow soul, and the field of lotus where they want to be buried is soaked in both blood and ecstasy. Pain is the addiction; its removal is the real threat.","src":"haiku"},
"within-destruction~vengeance":{"haiku":"Urban decay and moral collapse justify violence; the speaker celebrates destruction and international notoriety, channeling chaos as spiritual ascendance.","opus":"A bilingual gutter-flex of vengeance and nihilism, boasting of money, sex and violence while framing the demon within and humanity's fall as an inevitable, embraced end.","sonnet":"In Cantonese, Mandarin, and English: a defiant, vulgar declaration of superiority over haters, cops, and the music industry — the speaker boasts about outearning, outperforming, and outlasting enemies, framing their success as revenge against those who dismissed or oppressed them, and escalating into apocalyptic imagery of burning the city down in triumph.","src":"haiku"},
"within-temptation~paradise-what-about-us-ft-tarja":{"haiku":"Compromise and collective acceptance of imperfect reality as the best available alternative, humanity settling for struggle rather than paradise.","opus":"A grand symphonic-metal lament for a fallen world, where wisdom gives out and the wheel of fate grinds on beneath crimson heavens. Two powerhouse voices insist that what little survives, flawed and far from paradise, is still worth fighting for. It frames disillusionment as defiance, refusing to give up the imperfect present they claim as theirs.","sonnet":"A demand for accountability in a broken world — blood-red skies, wisdom failed, the wheel of consequence turning without mercy. The 'what about us' is addressed to the powerful who should have known better: this isn't paradise, but it's all we have, and we're still fighting for it.","src":"haiku"},
"without-waves~us-against":{"haiku":"Two people face cosmic indifference and overwhelming forces that test their bond; survival requires unity but risks mutual dissolution. Love becomes a battle against dissolution.","opus":"The Chicago band drifts between cosmic metaphor and intimate memory, a partner's glow steadying the narrator beneath a vast sky. Nostalgia for their early us-versus-everyone bond collides with how draining the relationship became, staying together compared to an impossible feat and enduring it like a violent uprising, superstition and inner demons trailing as it threatens to collapse.","sonnet":"A relationship recalled as the center of gravity — silhouette and starlight keeping someone grounded — gives way to the recognition that surviving it was its own violence. The world they faced together has collapsed, and something must burn now in its place.","src":"haiku"},
"withredd~why-you-shouted-at-me":{"haiku":"Wounded confusion and hurt from unexpected aggression, the speaker unable to comprehend the breach and seeking explanation.","opus":"The wounded bewilderment of being shouted at by someone who means everything. The narrator searches for a sliver of truth beneath a partner's noise and lies, aching at the gap between love and mistreatment. Its plainspoken, looping questions capture the disorientation of devotion that keeps getting answered with hostility.","sonnet":"The shock of being screamed at by someone you love completely — their words become noise, their falsehoods drown everything, and the question asked again and again is how this is possible when they are everything to you. 'What is love just after sore' names the specific aftermath: tenderness arriving too late.","src":"haiku"},
"wiz-khalifa~work-hard-play-hard":{"haiku":"Relentless pursuit of wealth and status through hard work justifies excess and dominance; the speaker performs his accumulation as proof of superiority and freedom from ordinary restraint.","opus":"A straightforward flex on earned excess, Wiz celebrating money, weed and status as the reward for grinding, the party as the payoff for the hustle with no deeper aim than balling harder.","sonnet":"A rapper catalogues an extravagant lifestyle of wealth, travel, and indulgence, framing it as the earned reward of relentless hustle and demanding that those who want the same build it themselves rather than imitate him.","src":"haiku"},
"woe-is-me~ghost":{"haiku":"Honesty and integrity yield to isolation; the speaker learns to release attachment and walks away unburdened by guilt.","opus":"A defiant recovery anthem about walking away from a controlling partner who expected an impossible standard and pulled the ugliest side out of him. He describes still seeing her haunting reflection when he faces himself, but refuses to be silenced or reshaped, tossing back her insincere remorse and finally finding peace in the exit.","sonnet":"A person confronts the lingering presence of someone who demanded perfection and brought out the worst in them — seeing that ghost in the mirror, hearing the voice in every thought — and the hard-won conclusion is learning to love letting go rather than letting the other person drown them out.","src":"haiku"},
"woe-is-me~hard-to-live-feat-david-benites":{"haiku":"Mental isolation and defensive walls paradoxically accelerate internal collapse as the speaker builds barriers meant to protect but ultimately suffocate self-awareness.","opus":"A candid account of masking depression, faking smiles while building walls that collapse and crush you, fighting the demons alone and just trying to find one reason to stay as the sunlight fades.","sonnet":"A person barely holding on as demons close in: faking a smile, building walls that collapse under their own weight, the speaker admits to seeing red and losing their grip, and clings to the need to find a reason to stay before the darkness wins.","src":"haiku"},
"wolf-alice~freazy":{"haiku":"Tribal belonging emerges as escape from alienated thought; feral connection releases the narrator from internal imprisonment into shared wildness.","opus":"A defiant celebration of chosen tribe and belonging, Ellie Rowsell shaking off years of teenage self-imprisonment to invite the wild and feral in, insisting their love is cool no matter who hates it.","sonnet":"A speaker who spent their teenage years trapped in their own head has found their way to a place and people where they belong — the song extends that belonging outward as an invitation, declaring their community open to anyone willing to be wild and real.","src":"haiku"},
"wolf-alice~lipstick-on-the-glass":{"haiku":"Self-sabotage repeats despite awareness as cyclical attraction destroys through return, the speaker paradoxically choosing the pain they recognize over change.","opus":"The self-aware relapse of taking back a lover you shouldn't, the singer clear-eyed that the lipstick and the fighting spell trouble yet knowing she's the one making herself mad, unable to resist.","sonnet":"A person takes back an ex despite clear evidence — lipstick still on the glass — that they shouldn't, acknowledging that the madness is their own and that their body betrays their better judgment. The pattern repeats with open eyes and no real resistance.","src":"haiku"},
"wolf-alice~play-the-greatest-hits":{"haiku":"A person nostalgic for the past moves in circles, living superficially in the present while hungry for more—unfulfilled by routine pleasure-seeking and disconnected intimacy.","opus":"The empty hedonism of the fast life that only feels like it's creeping, filling the void of a hollow present with parties and hookups and old songs; the throughline is a self-aware woman who keeps falling for the first creep who opens his arms because she can't stand the silence, and it's still never loud or enough.","sonnet":"About the numbing thrill of casual hookups and bar-going as a substitute for real feeling — swaying in someone's kitchen to familiar songs fills the silence when the narrator admits she'll fall for the first available person just to drown out emptiness.","src":"haiku"},
"wolfmother~joker-and-the-thief":{"haiku":"Wandering tricksters moving through the world, living for the moment without roots.","opus":"A swaggering classic-rock fable of two outlaw figures roaming the land, restless characters who won't go home, the mythic archetypes carried by riff and mood more than any concrete story.","sonnet":"A mythic-feeling story song about two archetypal figures — a charismatic, wandering joker and a shadowy thief — circling a woman standing in a field; it's impressionistic rather than literal, evoking a world where night people carry dreams and a predatory stranger might just take everything.","src":"haiku"},
"wolfmother~woman":{"haiku":"Woman embodies freedom and illumination, cutting through the speaker's confusion with presence alone. Her power transforms merely through attention and authentic connection.","opus":"A blues-rock ode to a woman whose power to see through the singer and set him free is the whole point, desire dressed as awe.","sonnet":"A breathless rock address to a magnetic woman who sees through the speaker completely, radiating a freedom that is contagious — she's going to set you free, and the speaker can't be anyone but themselves in her presence.","src":"haiku"},
"womcadole~a-38lla":{"haiku":"Breaking isolation through courage—opening doors to escape darkness; rain passes and hope awaits beyond corners as connection replaces solitary suffering.","opus":"Womcadole came up in Japan's band scene trading in earnest, big-hearted rock, and this track puts that sincerity to work on depression. The narrator, who forgot how to smile and wars alone in a dark room between anxiety and hope, moves toward a single act: opening a door. A rainbow after rain, a friend running toward life, and the discovery that refusing goodbye means choosing to live.","sonnet":"A song about depression and the struggle to leave the room: the narrator forgot how to smile, has been crying alone in a dark room fighting an internal war between anxiety and hope, but the arc is toward opening a door — seeing a rainbow after rain, watching a friend run toward life — and discovering that saying goodbye to \"goodbye\" means choosing to live.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~a-meeting-place-and-time":{"haiku":"Two people fail to meet at a planned rendezvous, and one recalls killing their former self; the meeting never happens because one can no longer trust the other's words.","opus":"A song about a reconciliation that both estranged people privately intend to skip, each imagining the encounter yet knowing neither will show. The narrator marks the anniversary of finally abandoning the trusting, broken version of himself and bids the other person a lifetime of bitter cold. Woods of Ypres, the late David Gold's Canadian blackened-doom project, give this failed truce a wintry funeral weight.","sonnet":"Two people who agreed to meet and neither showed — the imagined confrontation, the table they would have risen from, the outside they would have stepped into. The speaker names what the no-show cost: the desperate man he used to be, the one who trusted every word the other said, was killed a year ago today, and now there is nothing left to wait for at this particular place and time.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~adora-vivos":{"haiku":"Living people deserve worship and admiration more than the dead or gods; love should be expressed while someone is alive. The song laments the urgency of mortality and the waste of delayed praise.","opus":"The late David Gold's Canadian doom-metal project delivers a humanist creed, rejecting reverence for the dead and insisting that the living deserve honor while they can still receive it, mortal people held realer than any deity. Given Gold's death soon after, the plea to cherish others in life rather than eulogize them once gone carries an unintended, aching weight.","sonnet":"A polemic for loving the living rather than venerating the dead. Mortal life under grey skies and electric light is declared more real than any deity, and praise deferred until after death is indicted as cowardice. The urgency is personal: love him now, in the flesh, before the moment passes.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~allure-of-the-earth":{"haiku":"Depression and suicidal ideation depict the earth as invitingly dark, offering relief from the pain and pressure of existence through surrender and burial.","opus":"A doom-metal meditation on the pull toward giving up, where sunlight that energizes others leaves the narrator untouched and hollow. He fantasizes about a cold, dark burrow underground where he can retreat from summer, pressure, and other people. David Gold's Canadian project treats the longing to vanish into the ground as an almost restful temptation.","sonnet":"The earth as a seductive alternative to enduring the sun — the warmth and brightness that sustains everyone else has no effect here, and the fantasy of finding a cool, deep hole to descend into is not morbid decoration but an actual negotiation with gravity's comfort. The appeal of letting go, of being satisfied by lying down, is described with lucid tenderness rather than horror.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~alternate-ending":{"haiku":"Rewriting tragedy through memory transforms loss into eternal presence; the speaker refuses the painful ending, choosing to hold love beyond death's separation.","opus":"A dying man's final moments on a lonely highway, rewriting the story's ending in his mind to imagine togetherness and forever with someone he lost. Haunted by whether anyone shared his joy, he takes solace in a memory made eternal by finality.","sonnet":"A dying narrator reflects on a life spent waking without someone — \"I woke up for years without you\" — and in the final moments pulls off to the side to rewrite the story: in the alternate version they were together, they live forever in memory, holding on even as the end couldn't come slowly enough.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~crossing-the-45th-parallel":{"haiku":"Winter boundary marks passage through reinvention; trees witness transformation while the traveller oscillates between known and unknown territories.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~crossing-the-45th-parrallel":{"haiku":"Traveling between two lives at night, anchored by memory and guided through darkness.","opus":"A lonely northern night drive as an inventory of a life, the roadside trees the only witnesses to the wheel and to a private, ominous loss of control, David Gold mining his existence against snow and white lines.","sonnet":"A night drive through a winter landscape becomes a meditation on two lives — the one being built and the one left behind — with the roadside trees as silent witnesses to every journey, and to the moments when control almost slipped. The 45th parallel is both a literal geographic boundary and the threshold between past and present self.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~death-is-not-an-exit":{"haiku":"Death is final—consciousness ends, and existence before and after life is non-existence, making the body all that truly matters.","opus":"A bleak materialist meditation: since we were nothing for billions of years and will be nothing again, death is not an escape door but simply an off-switch, so the body and this single life are all we truly are and must be protected and respected.","sonnet":"A bleak but earnest materialist argument: death is not a doorway or escape but simply the off switch of consciousness, and because nothing comes after, the living body is the only thing that matters — a case for valuing life precisely because it ends.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~december-in-windsor":{"haiku":"A memory of loneliness and numbness from 2002, when at 22 the speaker felt too old and developed an appetite for self-destructive behavior.","opus":"A lonely winter night of drinking to numb pain and forcing oneself out to feel alive, still haunted by someone whose touch would shatter him. It looks back on a cold December, feeling far older than his years and developing a taste for poison.","sonnet":"A speaker recalls a specific winter — December 2002 in Windsor — when he was young, alone, and beginning to numb himself with alcohol, and finds that even now the memory of that emotional coldness still has power over him.","src":"haiku","fable":"David Gold pins the read to one Ontario winter and lets the addressed you stay deliberately double — a person gone and the bottle itself — so a single figure carries both the grief he can't shake and the drink he can't refuse. That ambiguity is the whole architecture: the same cold swallow that dulls the ache is also the thing he'd trade anything to feel, revulsion at the mere presence folded into helpless collapse at the nearest contact. His flat vow to get out and be among the living lands as its own quiet defeat, an effort he already reads as tomorrow's erasure. The closing turn earns the song, naming the place that ruined him and letting the word for a familiar spot slide into its ghost sense, so the site of the wound becomes the thing that trails him — memory not recovered but re-entered, a man barely into his twenties yet already feeling ancient, walking back into the room that shaped him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The remembered winter is the band's origin point — Woods of Ypres formed in Windsor, Gold's hometown, in 2002. He died in a December too: the 21st, 2011, at 31.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","death & grief","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"grief and the bottle held in one deliberately doubled address","built":"the place-name in the title slides into ghost sense as site of wound and thing that trails him"},
"woods-of-ypres~deepest-roots-belief-that-all-is-lost":{"haiku":"Obsession with mortality and loss creates despair and inaction; finding meaning becomes possible only after recognizing meaninglessness.","opus":"A meditation tracing depression to a small buried germ of hopelessness that grows into something warped, rooted in fixating on mortality while happiness demands an exhausting patience that oblivion would spare you. Yet the closing turn insists that meaning surfaces just when you conclude there is none. Woods of Ypres, the late David Gold's doom project, wring fragile redemption from the bleakness.","sonnet":"The psychology of despair traced from its seed to its strange, contradictory conclusion — the obsession with life and death planted early, the slow growth of a twisted tree, and the arrival at the belief that nothing can be done. Yet even at the bottom of that logic something interrupts: just as meaninglessness seems proven, meaning appears again, and the frustration is that this keeps happening.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~dragged-across-a-forest-floor":{"haiku":"A person is deeply hurt and disrespected by their community, dragged through metaphorical forests of their past where stubborn pride and misery keep people trapped and unchanging.","opus":"Walking a lonely road at night, the narrator reads the surrounding trees as a landscape of metaphor for a past where he was demeaned and mistreated, learning to hold his tongue and mask his intentions among people fixed in their ways. Woods of Ypres steep it in wintry Canadian doom, turning that pain into resolve and finding hope in the very ground where he walks wounded and alone.","sonnet":"A person who tried to change a community and was met with entrenched stubbornness and disrespect eventually learns to swallow his outrage, bite his tongue, and walk the woods alone. The forest becomes a map of his past — every fallen branch a mistake, every trail a consequence — and survival means tolerating what cannot be changed.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~end-of-tradition":{"haiku":"Two people sever long-standing bonds and patterns of connection, accepting past mistakes and moving forward without reunion or rekindled fire, dissolving tradition and ties that once united them.","opus":"David Gold's doom-metal project frames the end of a relationship as hard-won wisdom. Admitting his own faults, the narrator resolves to stop patching a dead bond and to sever ties for good. A single road that once separated two people becomes the only thing still linking them across the distance, sorrow and resolve entwined.","sonnet":"A clear-eyed decision to sever a relationship or community that no longer serves anyone. Hard-earned perspective on the highway at night reveals a bigger picture ahead, and with it the resolve to stop travelling long distances to maintain bonds that have become hollow — this tradition ends here, completely and without return.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~halves-and-quarters":{"haiku":"Self-medication in fractional doses creates a precarious balance between survival and self-destruction, wearing away the body's resistance one day at a time.","opus":"David Gold facing dependence with grim clarity, rationing the very pills that both keep him alive and hollow him out, weaning off the one thing sustaining him one splintered dose at a time.","sonnet":"A speaker describes weaning themselves off a substance or coping mechanism one fractional dose at a time, rationing the very thing keeping them alive in a slow, deliberate process of self-withdrawal.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~intro-the-looming-of-dust-in-the-dark-the-illumination":{"haiku":"Authentic despair breaking through to gradual luminescence; the threshold between darkness and recognition.","opus":"Death made visible in the dust drifting through morning light over a cluttered, decaying room. A frail figure watches motes swarm and settle, sensing mortality slowly burying him, torn between the dark he has stared into and the light's warmth. The Canadian doom-metal project renders despair as intimate, domestic and quietly luminous.","sonnet":"Sunlight enters a wasted room and catches the dust, which rises and hangs in the air as a quiet sign that death has been accumulating here — burying the thin, pale figure who has stared at this wall long enough. The dust itself becomes a prompt to choose day over night, the light hinting toward something beyond the room where a life wastes away.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~keeper-of-the-ledger":{"haiku":"Nature is an indifferent accountant that collects all bodies to maintain balance, caring nothing for human hope or stories. Death is a transactional debt owed regardless of meaning.","opus":"Doom-metal band Woods of Ypres frame death as ruthless bookkeeping, nature keeping an exact tally in which everything drawn from the earth must be repaid punctually. The divine, long imagined as a loving mother-figure, is exposed as an indifferent accountant caring only for quantity, not the worth of a life. Stripped of the meanings people invent, the song insists nature wants only the body back.","sonnet":"Nature is reframed as a cold accountant indifferent to human meaning — every body borrowed from the earth will be reclaimed on schedule, regardless of myth, purpose, or quality of life. The comfort humans find in divine love is exposed as projection; the ledger only tracks the return of matter.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~kiss-my-ashes-goodbye":{"haiku":"Mourning becomes acceptance of inevitable loss as the speaker releases what was meant to end. Goodbye transforms into sacred goodbye rather than desperate preservation of the temporary.","opus":"A meditation on mourning, memory and the wish to be released rather than monumentalized. The narrator asks not to be enshrined but simply let go, his ashes bid farewell and the living freed to move on. Given the writer's early death, the doom-metal elegy reads as an unbearably prescient acceptance of one's own vanishing.","sonnet":"A meditation on loss and anti-monumentalism: grieve, cry, then let go — the narrator refuses the vanity of grave-markers and monuments, asking only to be kissed goodbye and forgotten when their words no longer serve, because mourning should eventually give way to moving on.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~lightning-snow":{"haiku":"Sudden catastrophic loss that echoes forever; a single moment of shock erases all dreams and leaves hollow emptiness that will never be filled, only endured through endless sorrow.","opus":"Doom-metal songwriter David Gold builds the whole track around one sudden catastrophe, imagined as a bolt striking followed by falling snow, that splits his life cleanly into everything before and the grief after. The blow leaves a permanent void, and his despair deepens because his one plea for another try was denied outright.","sonnet":"A song about the exact moment when a relationship ended — a single flash of lightning before years of snow and grief; the narrator knew instantly that love was gone and that life would not give him a second chance.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~mistakes-artists-make-the-dream-is-dead":{"haiku":"Admitting music's self-indulgence and the death of artistic ideals; knowing when to quit.","opus":"A weary reckoning from a musician who admits that valuing art above living itself is a squandering of health and love, calling creative work an indulgent form of chosen suffering. Yet the song ends generously, imagining someone else picking up the flame after the artist collapses and carrying the work onward in his honor.","sonnet":"About the self-indulgence and ultimate futility of the modern music industry — the narrator argues that loving music more than life is a waste, that all art made today is primarily for the artist's own ego; when the torch burns out someone else will carry it, but the dream of what the music scene was is dead.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~modern-life-architecture":{"haiku":"Life's plans crack and crumble, forcing recovery and redesign while the foundations of modern society collapse.","opus":"David Gold's Canadian blackened-doom project Woods of Ypres builds a mournful allegory of a life constructed and then collapsing, its finest plans caving in. Left hollowed out and living only partway after losing love, the narrator keeps patching and rebuilding while bracing for further ruin, his abandoned hopes making this slow structural failure quietly crushing.","sonnet":"A life built like a structure that begins cracking the moment construction ends — youthful plans collapsing under a weak foundation, and the work of rebuilding threatened by the clock. Loss is present throughout as an unnamed companion, and the final image of modern life architecture crumbling under the weight of the world suggests the project of recovery is always already losing to time.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~move-on-the-woman-will-always-leave-the-man":{"haiku":"Romantic inevitability guarantees departure; women abandon relationships and men remain alone to process loss.","opus":"A bleak, mantra-like thesis on romantic asymmetry, insisting women depart while men love on forever. Seasons pass from summer lust to a spring of abandonment, love outlasting its object. The doom-metal repetition hammers heartbreak into grim folk-wisdom, less an accusation than a resigned law the narrator seems to have learned the hard way.","sonnet":"A bleak gender asymmetry stated as axiom: she stays through autumn and winter but is gone by spring, and the closing shift from \"the woman will always leave\" to \"men move on\" — repeated with increasing force — is either defiant or defeated, a command to the self that doubles as a verdict.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~natural-technologies":{"haiku":"Bodily instinct and sexual pleasure drive human survival and vitality, resisting denial of natural desire.","opus":"A carnal hymn from David Gold's doom project that treats the body's raw drives, pounding blood, hunger, and lust, as sacred necessities rather than sins to suppress. It argues that fighting one's animal nature only brings misery, and that even the fiercest creature must be nourished by affection to survive, framing appetite as the machinery of the species carrying on.","sonnet":"An argument that desire and physical need are not shameful but simply biological — the machinery of survival and vitality that punishes resistance with pain and rewards compliance with pleasure. Denying the body's drives is framed as going against a design as natural and unavoidable as evolution itself.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~retrosleep-in-the-morning-calm":{"haiku":"A meditation on the loneliness and restlessness of solitary sleep, where constant physical agitation is both a consequence and a cause of isolation.","opus":"A small, aching meditation on solitude, the narrator rationalizing that sleeping alone is good because he tosses and turns and hates disturbing a partner, circling back to the lonely truth that he sleeps so much because he is so often alone. Woods of Ypres, David Gold's Canadian doom project, distill their trademark honest melancholy into this quietly heartbreaking loop of self-consoling isolation.","sonnet":"A loop of self-comfort about sleeping alone — the restlessness is easier when there is no one to disturb, but the ease of solitude is also proof of the solitude itself. The thought keeps doubling back on itself: it is good to be alone because one sleeps so much, and one sleeps so much because one is always alone.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~silver":{"haiku":"A person is second in someone's affection and life, consistently abandoned for new loves and opportunities, left alone while their partner shares joy with others.","opus":"Woods of Ypres turn heartbreak into bleak self-examination over doom-laden gloom, voicing a lover who always places second. He nurtured and cared for someone who sprinted off to win and never glanced back, and he rejects any pitying reassurance. The runner-up metaphor and the recurring sting of eating in solitude while another has company reframe love as the pain of being merely put up with.","sonnet":"A man who was second choice watches the person he supported leave for someone brighter, someone who could offer what he could not. He carried her sorrows and she dined elsewhere — the city made her cold, and he is left to grieve alone, unwilling to accept unconditional love because he needs to be chosen, not merely kept.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~song-of-redemption":{"haiku":"An aging musician offers his final composition as a gift, aware his time is running out, hoping music will be his lasting legacy.","opus":"An aging, ailing musician offers one final performance, shaking hands and weak muscles notwithstanding, presenting a masterpiece as his deadline arrives and hoping the memory outlasts him. This carries unbearable weight for Woods of Ypres, the Canadian blackened-doom project of David Gold, given that Gold died young, the promise that music is all he has and all that remains reads as genuine farewell.","sonnet":"An aging musician offers a final performance not as triumph but as honest gift — hands shaking, voice weakened — knowing the song may be his last. Music is presented as the only thing that endures after the body fails, and the act of playing one more time becomes both farewell and the whole of what he has to leave behind.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~suicide-cargoload-drag-that-weight":{"haiku":"Exhaustion becomes philosophy; the speaker drags impossible burden across foreign landscape, weighed down by grief too large to articulate or abandon.","opus":"Depression rendered as sheer physical labor, hauling an impossible weight across foreign ground where nothing is legible. The plodding repetition is the point: no cure on offer, only the endurance of carrying it and continuing to breathe.","sonnet":"Emotional and psychological burden is rendered as literal physical weight — a ten-ton stone statue standing before miles of mountains in a foreign land where you don't speak the language. The instruction to drag, breathe, pull the anchor, and push the mountain is grimly practical rather than encouraging.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~to-lock-eyes-with-a-wild-beast":{"haiku":"A person who has never faced real hardship encounters a wild animal and dies in predation, their illusions shattered; the song condemns privileged ignorance by showing how nature imposes brutal consequence on those untested.","opus":"A vicious parable from the Canadian doom band aimed at the coddled and self-assured who judge others yet have never faced real hardship. Gold's growl wishes upon them a fatal meeting with a predator, then depicts the killing in gory detail, a life snuffed out in a heartbeat as a great equalizer for untested pride.","sonnet":"A long, pitiless fantasy directed at those who judge others' fear without ever having faced real danger. It walks the comfortable and ignorant into a wilderness encounter — predator, moonlight, cold claws — until the chase begins and hallucinations flood in and bones are left in spring snow, finally and permanently humbled.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~to-long-life-in-the-limbo-union":{"haiku":"Transient souls who belong nowhere gather in temporary rooms, finding meaning through community and the deliberate emptiness of detachment.","opus":"Gold turning perpetual rootlessness into a strange creed, another year in another rented room, owning nothing and belonging nowhere, making a home in the heart for love with no fixed address.","sonnet":"A person who has chosen rootlessness as a way of life reflects on temporary rooms, chosen companions who also have nowhere to be, and the paradox of finding meaning by offering oneself generously to others while owning nothing and defending nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~traveling-alone":{"haiku":"The narrator searches for belonging but finds no evidence of God and lacks material evidence of existence. He refuses to impose his hopelessness on believers.","opus":"David Gold's stark confession of an atheist too worn down to love and too honest to lie, envying the faith he sees in others yet unwilling to steal their hope by handing them his own bleak, godless view of a life he despises.","sonnet":"A bleak personal confession about atheism and alienation — the speaker envies believers their comfort but cannot share it, has never found a place in society, and travels alone through life with no evidence of God and no taste for the life he's living.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~wet-leather":{"haiku":"Life reduces to endless pain and disappointment with no escape or vacation. Everything is a scam; health becomes a job; survival means self-harm.","opus":"A viciously bleak verdict reducing existence to misery and worthlessness, a fleeting con of boozy urban nights, sodden clothes in the rain, and the absurd grind of laboring endlessly just to stay well. It is nihilism spiked with dark comedy; David Gold's Canadian doom project channels this worn-down, self-punishing gloom into grimly funny, weary release.","sonnet":"Life distilled to its worst textures — wet leather on your back in the rain, a drunken city night, the sting of a friend's honest blame, the exhaustion of health becoming a full-time job. Everything is a scam, nothing is missed, and whatever small acts of self-destruction happen are just attempts to feel something rather than nothing.","src":"haiku"},
"woods-of-ypres~years-of-silence-and-the-private-joke":{"haiku":"Shared moments under stars become private bonds transformed into lasting mystery, forever changing those who lived them.","opus":"Two people who took a life-altering risk together and were changed by it forever, then forced apart into years of silence. What's left is wondering what a memory is worth when the person you made it with has come to hate you.","sonnet":"Two people who took risks together that permanently changed them — standing in the sand, staring at stars — became a private joke to the world; they split up and never told anyone, but the silence asks what memories are worth if those you made them with now despise you.","src":"haiku"},
"wooze~witch-slap-iou":{"haiku":"Transactional desire calcifies reciprocity; pleasure requires accounting and ownership, collapsing spontaneity into debt and mutual possession.","opus":"A giddy, off-kilter sketch of falling under someone's spell, losing the taste for shallow lust and getting hopelessly entangled, trying to make a fleeting moment eternal even as it burns a permanent hole through you.","sonnet":"An intoxicating, slightly surreal attraction — the speaker has lost the taste for ordinary lust but found themselves drawn into this person's orbit, and now owes a debt that needs no sign. An ephemeral moment is darned into permanence like patching a burn hole.","src":"haiku"},
"written-by-wolves~any-second":{"haiku":"Revolutionary rupture imminent: the system's architects face backlash from engineered masses ready to ignite and extinguish all escape routes.","opus":"Righteous uprising against the powerful, aimed at leaders who divided a generation into despair and now face the multiplied mob they created; the ticking clock and locked doors promise an explosion they brought on themselves.","sonnet":"A furious indictment of a ruling class that drove a generation to suicide and built a broken system — the speaker addresses those in power directly, accusing them of deliberate division, warmth while their people die, and a plan now backfired as the crowd multiplied. The locked doors and Molotov image frame it as a moment of reckoning about to explode.","src":"haiku"},
"wucan~the-rat-catcher":{"haiku":"A malevolent figure emerges to exploit desperation; the piper's music lures the vulnerable to their doom while society watches complicit.","opus":"A retelling of the Pied Piper of Hamelin: a strangely dressed piper rids the plague-hazed city of its rats, is cheated of his pay, and vows to take away what the town loves most, luring the children off with hypnotic tunes when the moon is high. Remorse comes too late; only a mute and a blind boy witness the crime, and the children never return.","sonnet":"The Pied Piper story told straight — the stranger pipes the rats into the river, the town refuses his payment, and he retaliates at high moon by leading the children away into Transylvanian exile, leaving two witnesses (one mute, one blind) and parents whose tears come too late. The song presents the Rat Catcher's revenge as inevitable justice for broken promises.","src":"haiku"},
"wunderhorse~rain":{"haiku":"Rain becomes metaphor for emotional weight and existential burden, an inescapable force that follows and haunts despite efforts to deny it.","opus":"Rain as a creeping metaphor for dread and inescapable self-confrontation. It coils around a name and drains the light from ordinary days, and in the quiet of disaster one is forced to turn and face oneself. Wunderhorse's raw, Neil Young-tinged rock lets the recurring question hang unanswered, strange and quietly menacing.","sonnet":"A suffocating depression rendered as weather — the rain coils around your name, snuffs out every waking day, and persists even when you try to banish it, leaving only an empty frame where something human used to be. The narrator addresses a second self in the silence of some private disaster, where the only presence waiting is your own reflection.","src":"haiku"},
"wylie-hopkins~must-be-dreaming":{"haiku":"Digital saturation breeds cyclical despair—awareness of collapse loops endlessly while isolation deepens despite connection technology.","opus":"Doomscrolling paralysis in one's early twenties, brain rotting under the weight of constant news and phone use, feeling residual and defeated and stuck in a numbing loop that won't let the mind rest.","sonnet":"Stuck at 21 and 22, the speaker is rotting their brain on news, unable to put down their phone, trapped in a loop they can't date the start of; the exhaustion and hypervigilance of digital-age young adulthood accumulate into the dissociative refrain 'I must be dreaming.'","src":"haiku"},
"x-ecutioners~it-s-goin-down-feat-mike-shinoda-mr-hahn":{"haiku":"Hip-hop and electronic production collide in a hybrid sound that deconstructs and rebuilds musical language itself into something unprecedented and overwhelming.","opus":"A turntablist-and-Linkin-Park summit that is really about the craft itself: fusing hip-hop's elements into a hybrid future sound, blurring the edges until styles blend. The boast is technical, celebrating the scratch, the collage, and the collaboration as a genre-melting mission statement.","sonnet":"A boast about the power and originality of the X-Ecutioners and Linkin Park's collaborative sound — blending turntablism, rap and rock into a genre-melting hybrid that promises to obliterate everything in the existing record crate.","src":"haiku"},
"x-japan~born-to-be-free":{"haiku":"Freedom lies not in social approval but in the will to rule oneself and resist external chains, sustained by faith in love's transformative power. Liberty requires surrendering to destiny rather than constraint.","opus":"An anthem of unbreakable freedom, insisting that nothing can steal a life or love away and refusing to surrender even against destiny itself.","sonnet":"A declaration of indestructible freedom in the face of forces trying to steal or sever it — love and defiance are intertwined, with the butterfly imagery of flight countering the threat of capture, and the refrain insisting freedom and love are permanent.","src":"haiku"},
"x-rl7~heart-shaped-hole":{"haiku":"A false savior abandoned the speaker, leaving an empty wound in their chest. Moving past the deception, the speaker reclaims agency and claims victory over heartbreak.","opus":"A post-breakup reckoning, a woman left wilting among broken promises by a self-righteous man of hollow words and a heart of stone, insisting again and again that she is over it and won't let him back into her head. The repeated protest hints at wounds not fully closed. X-RL7 deliver this heartbreak-into-defiance arc with modern melodic-metalcore drive, clean hooks giving way to venom.","sonnet":"A post-breakup reclamation that moves through the wreckage — a person left wilted among broken promises, haunted by absence like a ghost in the rain. The speaker works through the gap between who they thought their partner was and who they turned out to be, arriving at a repeated insistence of being over it that sounds more like an act of will than a settled fact.","src":"haiku"},
"xaptiox~noodles":{"haiku":"Intoxicating romantic euphoria blurs consciousness and control; the speaker surrenders entirely to another's spell, oscillating between dream and helpless surrender.","opus":"Falling in love mistaken for a fever dream, all sugar-rush disorientation and helpless ascent. The narrator keeps demanding a pinch to prove they are awake, so total is the surrender to someone they belong to undeniably. Euphoria tips toward overwhelm, love arriving not as a choice but as a lawless, dizzying loss of control.","sonnet":"Falling in love against the narrator's own rules, described as disorientation indistinguishable from a dream — \"pinch me to waking\" because the feeling is so complete it can't be real. The hook flips between surrender (\"so undeniably yours\") and panic (\"euphoria, I can't take anymore of ya\"), mapping infatuation as a pleasant overload.","src":"haiku"},
"xavier-mayne~for-her":{"haiku":"Obsessive devotion overrides reason; the speaker sacrifices sleep and sanity for unrequited love, aware they're replaceable.","opus":"Devotion warping into unhealthy obsession: the narrator, awake for days and alone, admits he would harm anyone and give his blood for a woman who scarcely thinks about him, recognizing this pull as an illness rather than romance. He swings between patiently pining and swearing he is finished, conceding the bond was always fragile.","sonnet":"Three sleepless days of obsessive devotion to a woman who doesn't reciprocate — 'I'm not on her mind but she knows that I've got time' — the speaker aware of the imbalance, cycling through resentment and recommitment, offering blood spilled as the measure of how far he'd go.","src":"haiku"},
"xiu-xiu~crank-heart":{"haiku":"Innocent moments corrupt toward violence while the body registers trauma it cannot articulate, childhood innocence fractured by forces beyond comprehension.","opus":"Jamie Stewart's fractured, deliberately disquieting collage of adolescence, cruelty and dread, glimpses of humiliation, self-harm and sudden death rendered in cold, colorless fragments that refuse comfort.","sonnet":"Fragmented domestic images — a son who doesn't laugh at his father's joke, a girl's school colours of black, broken fingers, a death by car — accumulate into a portrait of grief so compressed it barely holds its own shape. The connections between the images are emotional rather than logical.","src":"haiku"},
"xiu-xiu~wondering":{"opus":"Xiu Xiu's Jamie Stewart turns nursery-rhyme sing-song into something unsettling and obscene, circling defeat, love that was once stronger, and the erosion of certainty. Beauty and filth blur as the narrator wonders at everything and nothing. An experimental art-pop reverie on doubt, decay, and the strange clarity found inside confusion.","sonnet":"A disoriented meditation on love that has died — past warmth and certainty have collapsed into something obscene and obscure, the sun has burned away hope and peace, and the narrator wanders in a kind of awed incomprehension at how completely everything changed.","src":"sonnet"},
"yacht-money~space-and-time":{"haiku":"Prophetic power and conscience guide the speaker to bend causality and rescue the lost; legacy builds through reversal while enemies circle and pressure demands perfect timing.","opus":"Voiced through Arcane's Ekko, a prophet bending time and hitting rewind to get life right, defiantly refusing to let anyone drag him down as he protects the lost kids of Zaun and builds his legacy.","sonnet":"Voiced as Ekko from Arcane, a prophet from Zaun bends time to correct past injustice — pressing rewind to restore honour and build legacy — while warning false gods and lawless rivals that the hands on the clock belong to him.","src":"haiku"},
"yaeji~in-the-mirror":{"haiku":"Memory's burden and unfulfilled potential—the mirror reflects actual against possible; presence feels incomplete when alone, creating persistent internal discord.","opus":"Yaeji built a signature from whispered Korean and English drifting over club-adjacent house, intimacy at low volume, and that hush suits this dissociation. Despite many memories and things still left to do, the reflection looks wrong when the narrator is alone. The self never feels the same without another present, identity quietly revealed as contingent on being witnessed by someone else.","sonnet":"A Korean song about dissociation and disconnection from one's own reflection: even though the narrator has many memories and many things they still want to do, looking in the mirror when alone feels wrong — the self doesn't feel the same without another person present, suggesting identity is contingent on being witnessed.","src":"haiku"},
"yama~a-r1lr45":{"haiku":"Time moves too fast; fading memories and dissolving connections captured in flickering moments between two people.","opus":"Yama rose from anonymous internet origins, a masked voice singing before an unseen audience, which sharpens this song's central image. The narrator, balanced on a tightrope over a deep valley and dulled into the adult they never meant to become, uses music as a time machine. The flash of light raised high is a signal flare, hoping someone with warm eyes still watches and smiles.","sonnet":"A person standing on a tightrope above a deep valley, who has become the adult they never wanted to be and feels dull beside a brilliant world, using music like a time machine — raising a flash of light as high as possible so someone with warm eyes, somewhere, might still see them and smile.","src":"haiku"},
"yard-act~dream-job":{"haiku":"Ambition and class collapse into performance where superiority masks inadequacy. The dream job remains perpetually out of reach while the speaker performs confidence they don't possess.","opus":"A sardonic, sneering monologue from a smarmy figure welcoming someone into a paranoid corporate future, all empty superlatives and status games skewering the hollow aspiration of the dream job.","sonnet":"A sardonic narrator observes the unspoken rituals of class and ambition — betting on games no one scores, being 'proficiently poor', laying waste to or kowtowing to superiors — before the 'dream job' reveal undercuts any grandeur: 'step into my office, all night long', the future delivered in slang ('ace, top, mint, boss') that sounds like enthusiasm but reads as exhaustion.","src":"haiku"},
"yeah-yeah-yeahs~maps":{"opus":"A plea to a lover on the verge of leaving, all its ache compressed into the insistence that no one else could love them the way the singer does. Karen O reportedly wrote it about a partner she feared losing to the road.","sonnet":"A spare, aching plea to a partner who is leaving or drifting: the speaker insists on waiting, anchored by the conviction that no one else loves them as deeply, the repeated \"wait\" carrying both urgency and resignation.","src":"sonnet"},
"yeasayer~ambling-alp":{"haiku":"Resilience and moral courage matter more than circumstances; a father instructs his son that standing against corruption and injustice is the only path to dignity.","opus":"Fatherly counsel framed through a famous heavyweight boxer and his rival, urging the son to stand up for himself and defy the bullies and bigots, carrying his scars proudly in an unjust world.","sonnet":"A father-figure voice draws on the example of heavyweight boxing history to pass down a single lesson: stand up for yourself, refuse to be beaten down by fascists or anyone who cheats you, and wear your wounds with pride because the world is unfair but the lows do not last.","src":"haiku"},
"yeasayer~strange-reunions":{"haiku":"Estrangement and nuisance erode history; the speaker repels connection to protect wounded autonomy.","opus":"The weary standoff of a soured relationship or feud, the narrator wanting to be left alone from someone who's become a nuisance, circling the tiring pretenses and lies people keep up around each other.","sonnet":"A speaker sets a hard boundary against someone who has been a source of friction and manipulation, demanding to be left alone and questioning why everyone walks on eggshells around certain people rather than challenging them. The song is deliberately oblique, refusing to name what the feud is about while insisting it has gone on long enough.","src":"haiku"},
"yeat~lying-4-fun":{"haiku":"A hustler chronicles his ascent through deception and material acquisition, oscillating between self-awareness about moral compromise and defiant abandonment of conscience.","opus":"Yeat flexing his dreamed-of wealth and cars while hinting at a Faustian bargain, joking about signing his life over to the devil for success, the swagger shadowed by how hollow and dangerous the lifestyle is.","sonnet":"A speaker who has clawed his way to wealth and a dangerous lifestyle reflects on self-sufficiency and the gap between what people say and how they actually live, moving between braggadocio, drug use, and an ambiguous pact with dark forces that brought him success.","src":"haiku"},
"yellow-machinegun~again":{"src":"web","web":"The song asks why humans, who regard themselves as the greatest of beings, keep repeating the same mistakes, and expresses a yearning to be reborn and given another chance to understand existence."},
"yellow-machinegun~if-i-had-the-legs":{"src":"web","web":"A narrator facing death tomorrow feels doubly trapped — no legs to run with and no will to resist — resigned to being in the way of those who will kill them."},
"yellow-machinegun~my-eyes-under-the-sands":{"src":"web","web":"The speaker feels buried and crushed beneath sand that falls endlessly from the sky, swallowed by an indifferent, overwhelming weight with no way to orient themselves or escape.","fable":"A tight image-system about drowning slowly under sheer accumulation: the eyes open already buried in grit, snatch one accidental look at a limitless sky that only confirms how utterly lost the speaker is. Then more sand keeps falling, first piling into a mountain and soon losing even that shape, until the self is swallowed and covered over. The horror isn't the entombment but the shapelessness — a pressure the lyric calls a foolish weight, meaningless mass that carries no significance and no grandeur, just stacking higher until the self vanishes beneath it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"horror located in shapelessness rather than the entombment itself","built":"the lyric names the pressure a foolish weight — meaningless mass — denying it even the dignity of grandeur"},
"yellow-machinegun~your-light":{"src":"web","web":"Someone searches desperately for answers while self-imposed blindness and deafness prevent them from seeing what is already present at their feet — the song frames the obstacle as internal, not external."},
"yes~rhythm-of-love":{"haiku":"Surrender to the pull of desire and physical rhythm, knowing intellect surrenders when passion takes over.","opus":"Prog veterans in their pop phase chasing desire as its own ecstatic logic, where anyone will do when the pull of the rhythm takes over. Passion untethered from emotion, surrender as the only real answer.","sonnet":"Desire and compulsion orbit each other in a haze of inhibition and fever — the speaker is drawn repeatedly toward someone whose needs he cannot fully meet, surrendering to a rhythm that overrides reason and restraint.","src":"haiku"},
"yes~roundabout":{"haiku":"Circular journeys and cyclical time spiral through presence and memory; the narrator offers connection and witness across lifetimes and eternal recurrence.","opus":"An impressionistic road song about the pull of return, driving home to a lover through mountains and lakes, dissolving the journey into cosmic scale where human lives are grains of sand against a million years.","sonnet":"A sprawling, celebratory journey through landscape and time: the speaker promises to be a constant presence across mountains, lakes, and summers, folding images of nature and love into a sense that the world is vast and the music is moving endlessly through it.","src":"haiku"},
"yeti-let-you-notice~a-2li7xh":{"haiku":"A cryptic fragment on tangled attachment and decay—intimacy twisted into rot and refusal to let go.","opus":"Yeti let you notice work in a dense, allusive corner of Japanese rock, and this fragment fuses eros with religious decay. Threads snarl on a stone bodhi tree; lips press until they fester; a plea to hold on is answered with a command to be cruel. The band renders intimacy as something sacred and rotting at once, two people entangled past any hope of clean escape.","sonnet":"A fragment of erotic violence — tangled threads on a stone bodhi tree that won't loosen, lips pressed together until they fester and suppurate, a voice pleading 'don't let go' met with the command to be cruel. The song's subject is the religious and physical entanglement of two people whose closeness has become rotting, sacred, and impossible to escape.","src":"haiku"},
"yeule~dudu":{"haiku":"Emotional destruction and unstable disintegration through obsessive cycling, the painted self annihilated repeatedly through turbulent passion.","opus":"A hypnotic spiral through addiction, medication and unrequited love, screaming a name through poison rain. The narrator wakes restrained after an overdose yet insists on leaving a trace before being forgotten. Yeule's glitch-pop fragility turns self-destruction into lullaby, accepting that some love can never be possessed, only sung toward.","sonnet":"Unrequited devotion so total that the narrator would drink the beloved's tears from a jar — painting ripped from its frame and washed away in poison rain, overdosed on pain and waking restrained, medication as 'medicinal genius.' The dudu melody is the love song sung into a void where the other person will never fully belong to them.","src":"haiku"},
"ylvis~the-fox-what-does-the-fox-say":{"haiku":"Mystery persists despite the demand for answers: the unknowable resists categorization, and its refusal to speak becomes its voice.","opus":"A deadpan comedy premise stretched to absurdity: every animal has its known sound, so what could the fox possibly say? The joke is the earnest mock-mystery around a trivial question.","sonnet":"A comedic song that catalogs animal sounds and fixates on the one mystery it cannot solve — what sound does a fox make — dissolving into absurdist phonetic inventions as the question goes unanswered.","src":"haiku"},
"ymir~fimbulvetr":{"opus":"A Norse-mythology battle epic set during Fimbulvetr, the great winter before Ragnarok, as warriors arm against approaching frost giants; craving blood and glory, they embrace the cold and the chance to die honored in song and story.","sonnet":"A Norse mythology battle song about the great winter preceding Ragnarok — frost giants march from the horizon, warriors gather their torches and forces, Valkyries fall from the sky, and ravens will crown the victor. The song finds glory not in legacy but in the present moment of facing extinction with full commitment.","src":"sonnet"},
"ymir~rise":{"haiku":"Warriors fight overwhelming chaos and death itself with determination; even in defeat and sacrifice, they continue rising to reclaim stolen dignity.","opus":"A battle hymn of resilience staged amid archers, shields, and cliffs of hell, where falling only sets up the next rise. Ymir frame combat as a metaphor for endurance itself: steel the mind, honor the fallen who sacrificed for all, and keep fighting on and on. Victory brings not peace but the obligation to move forward.","sonnet":"A battle hymn narrated mid-fight, barely alive in a pool of blood: the speaker rallies their brothers to charge, reclaim what was stolen, and hold their minds under pressure — even in the grip of a monster as proud as themselves — insisting that falling is not the end because you can always climb back up from the cliffs of hell.","src":"haiku"},
"ymir~tripwire":{"haiku":"A dangerous and hypnotic woman traps and controls through desire, her presence poisonous and inescapable.","opus":"A femme-fatale character study that treats attraction as a lethal trap. Metalcore band Ymir sketch a motionless, icy-eyed woman with venom on her mouth who snares her victim and only stirs to finish him off, using imagery of being bound, sealed in, and helpless to escape so that desire itself becomes the deadly mechanism.","sonnet":"A femme fatale rendered in wire and poison — still-bodied, cold-eyed, her pull inescapable to whoever steps into range. The lyric operates from outside her, tracking the mechanism of seduction with the detached fascination of someone already caught, the tripwire sprung before the victim understood the ground.","src":"haiku"},
"yoko-kanno~a-38ptr":{"opus":"Best known as an anime composer of enormous range, Yoko Kanno here works in an intimate mode, staging a breakup post-mortem that refuses both bitterness and false comfort. The memory of two people together looks briefly, painfully happy in hindsight. Goodbye is said, yet the space the other occupied cannot be overwritten, and the narrator dreads recognizing them on some ordinary street.","sonnet":"A breakup post-mortem in which the narrator refuses both bitterness and false comfort: the memory of the two of them together looks briefly, painfully happy in hindsight; goodbye has been said but the space the other person occupied can't yet be written over — and the narrator knows they'll still recognize them if they see them on the street, terrified to overwrite the wound.","src":"sonnet"},
"yoko-kanno~surf":{"opus":"Yoko Kanno often reaches past ordinary language, and here she abandons it entirely, singing in an invented tongue built for sound rather than sense. The phonology stays evocative while meaning dissolves, leaving a dreamy, floating incantation with no narrative to decode. It is a composer trusting texture and voice to carry feeling where words would only pin it down.","sonnet":"Lyrics in an invented language — phonologically evocative but semantically empty — create an impression of a dreamy, floating incantation without a decodable narrative.","src":"sonnet"},
"yonaka~by-the-time-you-re-reading-this":{"haiku":"Impending departure frames urgency and temptation as the speaker offers one last moment of connection before vanishing, challenging the other to act before regret calcifies into permanent loss.","opus":"A reckless dare to seize a fleeting connection now or lose it forever, the narrator both offering paradise and warning she'll be gone, an urgent if-not-now ultimatum wrapped in the ache of impermanence.","sonnet":"An urgent dare to seize a fleeting encounter before it vanishes: the speaker cuts the other's guard down, insists on diving in before looking away, and leaves a note — by the time you read this, I'll be gone — making the impermanence itself the pressure to act now.","src":"haiku"},
"yonaka~clique":{"haiku":"Speaker rejects conformist groups and shallow gossip, reclaiming voice and autonomy after years of being talked down to.","opus":"A ferocious dismissal of exhausting, condescending people, with British alt-rock frontwoman Theresa Jarvis ordering them away and rejecting every assumption that she is merely cute, harmless, and meant to keep quiet. Two decades of being dismissed boil over into snarling self-assertion and a flat refusal to seek anyone's approval.","sonnet":"Twenty years of being talked down to erupting in a single sustained refusal — the speaker dismantles every assumption others have made about her quietness, prettiness, weakness, and availability, then walks out of the social contract entirely. The anger is specific and earned, not performance: she is not angry because she lost patience, she is angry because she finally stopped hiding it.","src":"haiku"},
"yonaka~ordinary":{"haiku":"A partner pursues the comfort of ordinary routine while losing the spark within, choosing stagnation over genuine passion.","opus":"Watching someone you love retreat into safe, deadening comfort and a relationship they never really wanted, out of fear of feeling anything real. The narrator, having escaped that trap herself, can only ask why they keep choosing numbness.","sonnet":"A confrontation with someone who keeps returning to a loveless comfort rather than accepting genuine care — the narrator sees the fire gone from their eyes and challenges them to admit they are settling, not living.","src":"haiku"},
"yonaka~own-worst-enemy":{"opus":"A vivid battle with one's own inner demon and mental illness, the narrator personifying the destructive voice they medicate against, waking up screaming, and confronting the fact that they are their own worst enemy with no escape from themselves.","sonnet":"About recognizing that one's internal demons — depression, self-sabotage, drinking — are the true antagonist, not any external person, and attempting to evict that destructive inner voice while accepting it may be inescapable.","src":"sonnet"},
"yonaka~predator":{"haiku":"A self-declared predator proclaims total dominance — built from the ground up, wired and unstoppable, here to take everything desired and bury anyone who gets brave.","opus":"A snarling declaration of dominance and self-possession, reclaiming power after fear. The narrator casts herself as leader and monster both, unafraid of her own demons and hungry for the chase. Yonaka's alt-rock swagger flips vulnerability into predatory confidence, wearing the heavy crown gladly and warning anyone brave enough to test her.","sonnet":"Absolute dominance asserted without apology: the speaker is designer-built, fire-spitting, never sorry, and the hunt is the pleasure — heavy is the head that wears the crown, but she wears it, and RIP to the one that gets brave enough to challenge.","src":"haiku"},
"yonaka~punch-bag":{"haiku":"Refusing to be a victim anymore; the speaker leaves a love built on abuse, becoming a force of consequence rather than an object to strike.","opus":"A survivor's refusal to be someone's emotional target, the narrator warning that she'll become the heartache he regrets, no longer his punch bag, breaking an addictive toxic cycle to say goodbye. A moment of silence honors the violent and the hidden. Yonaka are a Brighton alt-rock and pop band fronted by Theresa Jarvis, whose fierce, hook-heavy anthems about resilience and self-worth give this its defiant punch.","sonnet":"A relationship built on absorbing someone else's damage — drawn back by addiction even after seeing the harm clearly in daylight. The speaker refuses the role permanently, turns the dynamic around, and leaves the other party holding only bruises. There is clarity in the exit even if the pattern took time to break.","src":"haiku"},
"yonaka~seize-the-power":{"haiku":"The song celebrates independence and rejecting external judgment, urging listeners to break free from past trauma and claim power over themselves.","opus":"A surging self-empowerment anthem, the narrator waking up feeling important, choosing independence, and voicing an inner presence that urges the rejected and disrespected to take power for themselves, break rules, and live in the present. Yonaka, the Brighton alt-rock band led by Theresa Jarvis, built a reputation on exactly this kind of loud, defiant confidence, and this becomes a fist-raising call to reclaim agency and become a believer in yourself.","sonnet":"An inner voice arriving to promise liberation from external judgment and past rejection — breaking rules, living in the present, tasting independence for the first time. The power being offered is seductive and possibly dangerous, framed as a new religion of self-assurance, and the whole song rides that edge between genuine confidence and the voice of something less trustworthy whispering in your head.","src":"haiku"},
"yorushika~a-3ll44":{"haiku":"Melancholic self-rejection in fading light—the narrator fails at self-love despite proximity to warmth; setting sun mirrors inner despair and unattainable yearning.","opus":"Yorushika built a catalog of literary melancholy around n-buna's compositions, and this track sets unrequited longing against a falling sun. The narrator watches the orange light drop and questions whether love was ever there. The sun becomes a grape hung too high to reach, and just as the slanting light passes without permission, so does the feeling, leaving too little warmth even for the self.","sonnet":"A song about unrequited longing framed through the setting sun: the narrator watches the orange light fall and wonders if they were ever in love — the sun is like a high-hanging grape they can never reach, and just as the oblique light passes without consent, so does the emotion, leaving them unable to love even themselves quite enough.","src":"haiku"},
"you-am-i~soldiers":{"haiku":"Authorities divide and pacify neighborhoods, preparing citizens to quietly accept control while their freedoms are systematically erased.","opus":"A wry take on suburban conformity as low-grade warfare, where local worthies sort neighbors into camps, homemakers are enlisted for petty civic causes, and roads widen so residents can slowly smother in place. Beneath the satire runs a frayed domestic tenderness. You Am I, an Australian rock institution, deliver it with Tim Rogers's literate, sardonic bite.","sonnet":"Suburban civic life rendered as low-grade warfare — neighbours divided like cats and dogs by some unspoken authority, roads dug up to widen suffocation, tea baked for a council war machine, and the whole arrangement kept in place by people who talk to each other like children. The Prince of Wales detail anchors the absurdity: imperial ritual propping up a neighbourhood that nobody actually wants to be part of.","src":"haiku"},
"you-me-at-six~deep-cuts":{"haiku":"Accumulated wounds from love and heartbreak becoming permanent damage, the speaker unable to conceive of recovery or hope.","opus":"A concerned address to someone quietly wrecked by accumulated small wounds, the deep cuts they carry beneath a grown-up surface. The narrator wishes them free of a life that keeps hurting, convinced they deserve better even if better means alone. British rock maturity frames empathy sharply, naming jealousy and self-deception as slow, fatal doses.","sonnet":"Addressed to someone in a controlling or toxic relationship — death by a thousand cuts, a lover's touch that hurts twice as much, eyes wide shut while all grown up. The narrator sees the damage clearly but the subject can't; the deepest wound is staying, and the most honest thing the narrator can offer is 'you'd be better off alone.'","src":"haiku"},
"young-franco~lose-control":{"haiku":"Intoxication and presence merge as the speaker surrenders to sensation and immediate gratification. Baseline and desire align, creating permission to abandon structure and worry.","opus":"A hedonist's anthem of letting the bassline hijack you, living in the moment free of Wi-Fi and worry, the surface bravado shadowed by a line about buying your way free of a place and a life you can't quite escape.","sonnet":"A hedonist anthem about letting the bass and the moment take over — high and low, not worried about Wi-Fi codes, calculating freedom's price while the sun dress jumps like the beat — an invitation to stop holding back and let it all go.","src":"haiku"},
"young-medicine~i-m-going-to-hit-rock-bottom-you-guys-want-anything":{"haiku":"Mental dissolution accelerates; depression intensifies through repetition until consciousness becomes torment indistinguishable from living death.","opus":"The title's gallows humor sets up a full surrender to the low point, days without sleep, numb and self-loathing, deciding to give up feels perversely like feeling alive, an invitation to go down together.","sonnet":"A descent into severe depression and sleep deprivation narrated with dark humor — 24 then 48 hours awake, self-loathing on a loop, the anxiety so loud it becomes a party that never ends. The title's gallows joke is the song's governing tone: deciding to give up while screaming along on the way down.","src":"haiku"},
"your-neighbors~lockjaw":{"haiku":"Silencing and inability to speak, voice stolen or locked away.","opus":"An unflinching account of anxiety, compulsion and disordered eating tangled up with self-blame. The narrator grinds his teeth, binges and purges, and hunts for a high while confessing it is all his own fault. The lyric strips the shame bare, framing recovery as abandoning false gods and the desperate cycle they demand.","sonnet":"The body rebels — lockjaw, tingling face, grinding teeth, nausea — against a psyche that can't separate wants from needs, binges and purges on impulse, and blames itself for the whole cycle. The narrator accepts the self-blame but frames it as a \"high\" economy: \"what's a high to you?\" is the question they keep paying for.","src":"haiku"},
"yours-truly~composure":{"opus":"A post-breakup declaration of self-respect, the narrator refusing to take back a manipulative ex who now claims to be miserable, insisting they burnt out the relationship themselves, and vowing to keep her composure and stay away.","sonnet":"A breakup song about reclaiming self-respect after a one-sided relationship — the narrator methodically dismantles every trace of the other person while refusing to take them back, maintaining composure as a form of revenge against someone who burned everything down.","src":"sonnet"},
"yours-truly~hallucinate":{"haiku":"Sensory distortion and paranoia fuse with addiction; internal voices override reality while the speaker hallucinates meaning in hostile observation.","opus":"Anxiety and self-doubt personified as a voice in the head you can't shut off, forced to talk yourself into functioning while paranoia distorts every room and the truth gets buried under white noise, so you hallucinate calm you don't feel.","sonnet":"A voice in the head that won't stop — bloodshot eyes, things seen that shouldn't be seen, a bitter taste, an inability to sustain fearlessness. The party is full of people the speaker doesn't like, the air is laced with poison, and the only response is to convince herself she's hallucinating the whole thing.","src":"haiku"},
"yours-truly~high-hopes":{"haiku":"Demanding someone become a hero while knowing they can't change their nature. Despite high hopes, the speaker finally accepts that this person will never be the savior they needed.","opus":"A disillusioned farewell to a disappointing person, the narrator calling out someone who isn't who they claim, admitting she once hoped they could be her hero before finally realizing they never will be. Seasons change but they stay the same. Yours Truly are an Australian pop-punk band fronted by Mikaila Delgado, and this bittersweet arc from hope to acceptance fits their melodic, emotionally direct style.","sonnet":"The moment when someone you hoped would rescue you reveals they never could — they are a mess pretending otherwise, and the seasons will change while they stay exactly the same. The speaker arrives at a clear-eyed conclusion after repeated disappointment: this person will never be the hero they were cast as.","src":"haiku"},
"yours-truly~walk-over-my-grave":{"haiku":"Absence becomes presence haunting the surviving beloved; spectral indifference severs connection, leaving only cold silence where warmth once anchored identity.","opus":"The aftermath of being made small by someone who then walled themselves off and healed while you stayed poisoned, a bitter accounting of one-sided pain and the wish that they'd at least feel the chill of your absence.","sonnet":"The residue of a finished relationship — the speaker no longer dreams of the other person, no longer shivers because of them, but the air still feels poisoned and the thread still unravels. The folk saying about ears burning when someone is talking about you is reclaimed: when I shiver, I hear you say someone is walking over my grave.","src":"haiku"},
"yours-truly~walk-over-my-grave-lofi-version":{"haiku":"A relationship ends leaving the speaker invisible and forgotten, replaced by someone new while the speaker remains haunted and unmourned.","opus":"A raw account of being made small in a one-sided relationship, the narrator confessing she once ate from a partner's hand while feeling faceless, now watching the thread between them unravel, refusing to see them even in dreams. The grave-walking chill signals a haunting she wants ended. Yours Truly, the Australian pop-punk band led by Mikaila Delgado, strip this to a lo-fi intimacy that sharpens its wounded honesty.","sonnet":"The long aftermath of a relationship where the other person has healed cleanly while the speaker is still tethered to poison air and unraveling threads. The superstition that burning ears mean someone is talking about you becomes a dark comfort — at least there is still some connection — and the final image of flowers laid at the door feels like a burial already underway.","src":"haiku"},
"youth-code~puzzle":{"haiku":"The speaker throws away their life through anxiety and judgment, haunted and trapped, unable to retract their failed choices now that the verdict is sealed.","opus":"A claustrophobic spiral of anxiety and self-destruction, the narrator with a claw in his throat, feeling his life thrown away as a gavel slams shut on some inner verdict, finding grim solace only in losing all control. Youth Code are a Los Angeles industrial and EBM duo whose abrasive, aggressive electronics thrive on exactly this kind of raw psychological torment, turning private breakdown into pummeling catharsis.","sonnet":"A life thrown away under the weight of anxiety and shattered dreams — the gavel comes down, the case is closed, and what remains are dust and fragments of a former self. There is aching for shelter in the wreckage, a crooked soul finding solace only in surrender, and the birth in pain that could not be retracted repeating itself endlessly forward.","src":"haiku"},
"yui~rolling-star":{"haiku":"The narrator learns to accept stumbling and mud while pursuing dreams, accepting that reality brings troubles but they will laugh through them.","opus":"A young narrator refuses to keep swallowing what she means to say, admitting that the dreamed-of peaceful world collides daily with trouble and regret, then flipping that friction into defiance: stumble, get muddy, laugh, keep rolling. YUI was a leading figure in mid-2000s Japanese pop-rock, and this served as a Bleach opening, its bruised optimism aimed squarely at listeners learning to fall forward.","sonnet":"Frustration with a gap between dreamed peace and daily friction — someone who keeps stumbling, keeps doubting, but refuses to go quiet about it. The stance is bruised optimism: fall down, get muddy, keep moving. A bus-stop backdrop, stolen smiles, even the mess of loving someone from a distance — all of it folds into a refusal to polish the rough edges away.","src":"haiku"},
"yukimura~a-6h90ch":{"haiku":"Violent imagery and mental anguish; brain, poison, or blood dominate the disturbing landscape.","opus":"Sung in Russian, this track inhabits the bruised confessional register that runs through post-Soviet emo and rock. The narrator keeps falling into puddles, needs no one, is smeared in blood and punished by pain, forever fleeing themselves. Voice and hunger drain away until only an internal cold remains. It is a portrait of self-distrust so complete it reads as being marked by death and bound to brokenness.","sonnet":"A Russian-language song of someone who kept falling into puddles, never needed anyone, smeared in blood and punished by pain, running from themselves every second — losing their voice and sense of hunger while feeling only internal cold, unable to trust themselves, marked by death, bound forever to brokenness.","src":"haiku"},
"yullola~rip-out-the-wings-of-a-butterfly":{"haiku":"Beauty is captured and destroyed; tenderness imprisoned in toxicity becomes a violation.","opus":"A cover pairing sweet infatuation with quiet menace, love imagined as pinning something beautiful and fragile into a killing jar. The tenderness of walking toward a beloved sits beside images of cyanide and severed wings, so that devotion and destruction blur. Desire here wants to possess and preserve, even at the cost of the thing's life.","sonnet":"Tenderness and cruelty suspended together: the speaker walks toward someone irreplaceable, asking if they make each other dream and blush, while the image of ripping out butterfly wings and pinning them in cyanide circulates as a refrain — love as beautiful destruction, preservation as death.","src":"haiku"},
"yuna-kil~cycle":{"haiku":"Obsessive mental entrapment cycles endlessly; another person's voice invades consciousness and breeds self-destruction through constant violation.","opus":"An intrusive voice or memory of someone that won't go silent, trapping the narrator in a looping, self-destructive spiral inside her own mind. The song mounts toward a war on the psycho-cycle she's desperate to shoot down and escape.","sonnet":"About a toxic mental loop created by an intrusive voice — possibly an abuser's, possibly an internalized one — that the speaker cannot silence; the song builds from distressed passivity to screaming rage and a declaration of war against the cycle, ultimately framing it as a battle for control of one's own mind.","src":"haiku","fable":"Yuna Kil writes possession from the inside, dissolving the line between an abuser she supposedly escaped and the version of him her own head keeps rebooting whenever she goes quiet. By the finale there is no external tormentor left to blame — the voice she hears rehearsing its cruelty is coming from her own skull, chewing at her attention until she can no longer tell whose mouth it belongs to. The song's title is also its trap: the chorus deliberately jams on one clipped confession and refuses to advance, so that what she calls freedom sounds indistinguishable from the beginning of another lap. Her final admission is brutally clear-eyed for an intrusive-thought song — she knows the silence won't hold, that whatever she thinks she banished is still parked nearby, ready to circle back and clip her the moment she believes it drove off for good.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","shame & repression","freedom & escape"],"means":"freedom indistinguishable from the beginning of another lap","built":"chorus deliberately jams and refuses to advance, performing the trap it names"},
"yuna-kil~gumdrop":{"haiku":"Sweet seduction masks deeper hunger, the speaker performs coolness while chasing the taste of a life that glitters but rots from within.","opus":"A confession about compulsively pursuing the sugary rush of popularity and fame while fully aware it is degrading and phony. The narrator likens the addictive glamour of the in-crowd to a candy she keeps consuming until it turns sour, leaving her worn out, ashamed, and unable to quit the habit.","sonnet":"A cynical self-presentation in pursuit of someone shallow — the speaker knows the target's type, consciously performs sweetness and charm to hook them, fully aware the whole thing is performance, watching the object of pursuit burn themselves out chasing highs and wondering if the game is worth playing.","src":"haiku"},
"yungblud~happier-feat-oli-sykes-of-bring-me-the-horizon":{"haiku":"Two people who have both been hurting reach across the gap of mutual concealment — the speaker isn't broken, only afraid of belonging and of the vulnerability that comes with being happier.","opus":"The fear of happiness itself, sung by two artists steeped in emo confession. The narrator clings to someone in mutual pain, insisting he is not broken but merely depressed, terrified of belonging or of feeling better. Yungblud and Oli Sykes locate the paradox of chronic sadness: how recovery can feel more threatening than the ache.","sonnet":"Two people reassuring each other they're not broken while clearly hurting: I'm not broken, I'm just scared to be happier — the fear of belonging, of recovery, named directly, with the mutual hiding addressed as the central problem, each needing the other not to vanish while neither knows how to stay fully present.","src":"haiku"},
"yungblud~mars":{"haiku":"Adolescent alienation fantasises escape to elsewhere; the desperate teenager dreams of transcendence while trapped in suffocating familiarity.","opus":"A tender portrait of a trans teenage girl alienated at school and misunderstood by her parents, dreaming of a California where she'd be adored, the Bowie echo asking whether anyone like her belongs anywhere.","sonnet":"A teenage girl who doesn't fit — hiding who she is at school, dreaming of California where she'd be accepted, caught in cycles of therapy and misunderstanding — the song asks whether there is any place in the world where someone this alienated can simply live.","src":"haiku"},
"yura-yura-teikoku~zukku-ni-rock":{"haiku":"Rock and roll rebellion dissolves into repetition; passionate youth exhausts into resigned survival while bright colours fade to monochrome aftermath.","opus":"A surreal, childlike spiral of loss, a girl peeling bananas then vanishing, lost shoes, a cat stealing a little black snake, sensory sparks and repeated cries that he's done for, everyday fragments dissolving into panic.","sonnet":"A series of small, absurd losses — sneakers gone, a girl who peeled a banana with her mouth then vanished, a cat stealing a little black snake, falling down and seeing sparks — accumulate into the repeated cry of \"I'm done for.\" The red sky and sweat of the chorus reframe the chaos as kinetic, headlong momentum rather than defeat.","src":"haiku"},
"yuragi~soon":{"haiku":"Transcendent escape calls from beyond the known world; the speaker's eyes open toward transformation and departure into unmapped territory.","opus":"A restless, sleepless reaching toward escape and possibility, eyes held open against the night. Yuragi's shoegaze haze turns a simple yearning to go far away into a mantra, the world beyond the known just out of reach. Bilingual fragments blur into pure feeling: the ache of wanting elsewhere and being unable to rest until it arrives.","sonnet":"Eyes wide open through a night that cannot be slept through, the speaker and an unnamed other can go far away to a world they have never known, feet off the ground, crossing one more unseen night together. Sparse, suspended longing.","src":"haiku"},
"yutori~a-13ipsdp":{"haiku":"Toxic emotional entanglement—blurred rational boundaries in an unstable relationship where intensity escalates despite the narrator's conflicted awareness; resignation masking desire.","opus":"Yutori made their name mapping the ugliest corners of romantic dependency, and this track sits squarely in that territory. The narrator is trapped in a degrading relationship they hate but cannot leave, driven by selfish physical want while the other person stays drunk and irrational. The band's unflinching honesty shows in the oscillation between contempt and self-contempt, a mind blurred by lust, rage, and worthless excuses.","sonnet":"A song about being trapped in a degrading relationship the narrator hates but can't leave: they know logically they should stop but are driven by selfish physical desire, the other person is drunk and irrational, and the narrator oscillates between contempt and self-contempt, their mind a blur of lust, rage, and worthless excuses.","src":"haiku"},
"yutori~a-15t8k38":{"haiku":"Possessive romantic anguish—the narrator loves someone who leaves at midnight for another, enduring jealousy and emotional torture while remaining hopelessly attached.","opus":"Yutori specialize in love pushed to its breaking point, and here the beloved self-harms and slips away at midnight to someone else. The scars on their sleeves become something the narrator claims they can love, alongside a plea only to come home. The band holds grief and guilt inside obsessive attachment, admitting the narrator could never give enough while refusing, all the same, to let go.","sonnet":"A song about a partner who self-harms (the \"barcodes\" on their sleeves are cutting scars) and is disappearing at midnight to someone else: the narrator admits they could never give enough attention, says they can love even those scars, and begs the person to at least come home — grief and guilt held alongside obsessive attachment.","src":"haiku"},
"yutori~a-3gl46g":{"haiku":"Suicidal depression and dependency—the narrator uses sleeping pills to suppress suicidal urges, experiencing weakness and fear while clinging to one person as sole meaning.","opus":"Yutori return to their darkest recurring subject here: survival that hangs on a single other person. On the nights the narrator wants to die, that person does not call; a past attempt ended in waiting for morning. The band's stark intimacy names this person a sleeping pill, and the confession cuts deepest at the fear of being so weak beside someone strong enough not to need them back.","sonnet":"A song about suicidal ideation and dependency on a person who serves as the narrator's sole reason to survive: on nights they want to die, the other person doesn't call; they attempted suicide and waited for morning; they call this person their \"sleeping pill\" and confess they're terrified of being so weak while the other person seems strong enough not to need them.","src":"haiku"},
"yutori~a-brfwu8":{"haiku":"Unrequited love and loss—the narrator remains haunted by someone who has gone silent, unable to move on despite accepting the loss; clutching at memories and possessions.","opus":"Yutori excel at grief measured in small physical residue, and this ghosted-breakup song catalogs it precisely. The unanswered LINE chat still sits atop the inbox, the shared park is gone, the piercings kept open for this person have closed over. The band lets arrested mourning speak through objects, every trivial proof of a love that no longer has anyone to receive it.","sonnet":"A song about being ghosted after a breakup and living in arrested grief: the narrator's LINE chat with the person who never replies still sits at the top of their inbox, the park they used to visit together is gone, the piercings they kept open for this person have since closed over — all the small physical proofs of a love that no longer has a recipient.","src":"haiku"},
"yutori~speed":{"haiku":"An earnest declaration of inadequacy and devotion—racing to catch up, willing to sacrifice everything to protect a lover.","opus":"Against the self-lacerating despair that fills much of their catalog, Yutori here choose forward motion. The narrator knows they are not yet strong enough, yet promises someone they love to accelerate past their limit and shield them from anxiety. The self-doubt stays raw and admitted, but the vow holds anyway, closing the gap between who one is and who a person needs them to be through sheer will.","sonnet":"A person who knows they are not yet strong enough declares to someone they love that they will accelerate past their current limit to reach them and protect them from anxiety — the self-doubt is raw and admitted, but the promise is made anyway. The emotional core is the gap between who you are and who you need to be for someone else, and the decision to close it through sheer will.","src":"haiku"},
"yves-tumor~noid":{"haiku":"Someone expresses fear about systemic distrust, police violence, and being classified as disposable while grappling with mental illness and survival anxiety.","opus":"Yves Tumor pours genre-blurring art rock into a cry of fear and surveillance, a narrator reaching out to family, terrified for his safety and distrusted, insisting he's no criminal but merely a casualty and a statistic. His anguish, reframed as trauma and depression, collides with panicked calls to emergency services, comfort in love shadowed by a system he can't trust.","sonnet":"A Black narrator expresses terror at living in a world where systems of emergency response — embodied in a repeated emergency number — are themselves a source of threat rather than safety. Structural paranoia is not irrational but a reasonable response to being classified as a statistic and a symptom by those meant to protect.","src":"haiku"},
"yvette-young~a-map-a-string-a-light":{"opus":"A song about the urge to run from home and yield to an alluring pull toward death or dissolution, wanting to be reduced to nothing and given to the water, while urging courage as the world collapses and picturing a reunion out in open country. It weighs surrender against endurance, longing and dread braided together.","sonnet":"About the urge to flee home and dissolve into something larger than oneself — the narrator wants to be unmade by nature, swallowed by the sea and open skies, holding on through storms until they reach a place where they can finally meet someone they have been moving toward.","src":"sonnet"},
"zand~boys-like-u":{"haiku":"A woman unafraid to manipulate and dominate men who mistake niceness for attraction, wielding her power as a predatory force.","opus":"A swaggering dismissal of self-styled nice guys who assume decency entitles them to attention, met instead with contempt and zero patience. British alt-pop artist Zand, who works under a queer, ugly-pop banner, turns the tired misandry complaint into a boast, recasting the old maternal warning so the singer becomes the danger to be feared.","sonnet":"A woman dismantles the nice-guy script with precision, identifying passive aggression and self-entitlement beneath the performance of courtesy. She refuses to be managed, flips the predatory dynamic, and delivers the verdict clearly: this particular category of man does not intimidate her — she has been eating them for breakfast.","src":"haiku"},
"zara-larsson-young-thug~talk-about-love-feat-young-thug":{"haiku":"Avoidance of emotional depth sustains hedonistic present; love threatens freedom so the speakers celebrate pleasure over vulnerability and commitment.","opus":"A blunt refusal to put a name to feelings, wanting the pleasure of the moment without the burden of love and bracing against the inevitable wreck. The stance is guarded to the point of being ready to walk the instant she's pushed to define it.","sonnet":"Both speakers refuse to name or define what is happening between them — she explicitly doesn't want to talk about love because it always blows up, while she knows she can make dreams come true overnight and will do anything he likes short of commitment. The song is about pleasure held deliberately at the threshold of meaning.","src":"haiku"},
"zardonic-feat-voicians~bring-back-the-glory-counterstrike-remix":{"haiku":"Metal purists reclaim authenticity by rejecting electronic trends; aggression resurrects obsolete brutality as eternal counterforce to manufactured trends.","src":"haiku"},
"zardonic-voicians~bring-back-the-glory":{"haiku":"Aggressive assertion of metal supremacy; crushing competitors and reclaiming dominance.","opus":"Drum-and-bass metal declaring war on watered-down trends, mocking dubstep wubs and posturing as pure steel from hell, the whole thing a chest-beating vow to return heavy music to its former force.","sonnet":"A genre-war provocation — metal and its pure steel force is declared superior to trendy sounds, posers are mocked for dancing to wub wub while the speaker crushes them with chugga chugga, and the glory of old-school heaviness is announced as returned. It operates entirely as a rallying cry.","src":"haiku"},
"zardonic~bitter":{"haiku":"Obsessive vigilance over someone unavailable; unable to sleep or breathe without their presence.","opus":"Sleepless heartbreak stuck scrolling an ex's pictures, unable to let go, the bitterness of wanting to be her and be with her at once, obsession outlasting the relationship it feeds on.","sonnet":"Obsessive grief over someone lost — the narrator hasn't slept for days, can't stop looking at her pictures, doesn't want to breathe unless it means being her; the word 'bitter' names the flavor of a loss they cannot leave behind.","src":"haiku"},
"zardonic~blue-monday":{"haiku":"Violation and betrayal corrupt the speaker's ability to feel; they question their own emotional capacity after surrender and deception.","opus":"A drum-and-bass reworking of New Order's chilliest anthem, preserving its interrogation of cold, controlling love. The narrator circles the same wounded question, unable to grasp how someone can treat them so cruelly while dictating even how they should feel. Zardonic's heavier machinery only sharpens the original's alienation and its final demand to be left alone.","sonnet":"The speaker is trapped in a relationship dynamic where someone else dictates how they should feel: the ship in the harbour is a vision of escape they can and shall obey, but not without resentment, and the escalating \"leave me\" at the climax shows the cold heart finally reaching a breaking point after years of emotional subordination.","src":"haiku"},
"zardonic~for-justice-original-mix":{"opus":"A drum-and-bass metal hybrid built for adrenaline and vengeance, its clenched-fist threats promising a reckoning for the wronged. Vengeance is framed as justice coming to collect, all restless bones and defiance. The lyrics serve mostly as fuel for aggression, a rallying cry to make an enemy pay and feel the coming retribution.","sonnet":"A relentless vengeance anthem — the narrator promises retribution with explicit threat of physical harm, crushing pride and coming back to collect a debt. There is no nuance or cause beyond the primal satisfaction of making someone pay, framed as justice.","src":"sonnet"},
"zardonic~neon-rain":{"haiku":"Artificial beauty and manufactured experience, synthetic world replacing reality.","opus":"A cyberpunk romance where a rain-slicked, sleeping city becomes the canvas for two strangers becoming inseparable. Zardonic soften their aggressive drum-and-bass into synthwave reverie, imagining stone hearts melting under a rose-gold glow. The wonder lies in transformation: how the unfamiliar turns intimate, until life without the other becomes unthinkable amid glowing, dreamlike streets.","sonnet":"Two strangers become inseparable in the glow of a city's neon rain — the rose-gold filter of that light transforms anonymous souls into something worth seeing, and one former stranger cannot now think of life without the other. The city's stone canvas and melting hearts are the backdrop for a quiet love poem.","src":"haiku"},
"zardonic~raise-hell-original-mix":{"haiku":"Collective defiance against oppressive systems; the speaker and listeners ignite rebellion, weaponizing human energy against those who profit from compliance.","opus":"A defiant refusal of authority and manufactured guilt, telling the accuser that if war is what they want, so be it. Raising hell is the shrug of people who suspect nothing changes anyway but rebel regardless.","sonnet":"A defiant call to stop caring what the authorities or the crowd think and kick back — raise hell because the system will stay the same no matter what anyone says, and human freedom has to be seized.","src":"haiku"},
"zardonic~rave-until-the-apocalypse":{"haiku":"Hedonistic surrender to ecstasy before ending; the speaker dances toward oblivion.","opus":"Hedonism as end-times ritual, a Matrix-tinged invitation to dance while the world burns out. Zardonic fuse drum-and-bass adrenaline with apocalyptic abandon, pills and bottles and shaking bodies staged as the last human act before the stars. There is nothing left to do but move, so oblivion becomes a party you sleep off only in death.","sonnet":"End-of-world hedonism as the only rational response — she offers two pills, you choose the red, and then the world ends in lasers and transcendence: \"you can sleep when you're dead.\" The rave is not escapism but fully present annihilation; the apocalypse and the dancefloor are the same event.","src":"haiku"},
"zardonic~takeover-feat-the-qemists":{"haiku":"Unstoppable fusion overwhelms through sheer intensity; disparate elements collide to seize dominion, audiences surrender to relentless sonic assault.","opus":"A genre-fusion battle cry, drum and bass crashing into rock and metal to storm the stage, a shout-out to the loners and misfits as the crew braces the crowd for a total sonic takeover.","sonnet":"A celebration of drum and bass fused with rock and metal — two crews (The Qemists and Zardonic) describe themselves as players and controllers of a takeover that has been in progress since the dark ages, aimed at cell dwellers who stood alone against trendsetters. Rushing the stage and melting faces is both metaphor and instruction.","src":"haiku"},
"zardonic~takeover-zardonic-fickle6-vip-remix":{"haiku":"Rock and metal fused with drum and bass creates a takeover event that energizes crowds and defies corporate trends. The force is unstoppable and alive.","opus":"A braggadocious hybrid banger reveling in the collision of metallic riffs and breakneck electronic percussion, hyping the stage, the crowd, and an impending conquest while saluting the isolated outcasts and pioneers. Zardonic is the Venezuelan producer and DJ known for welding heavy guitars to dance tempos, and this team-up with The Qemists is pure adrenaline manifesto, all swagger and skull-rattling low end built to blow up a live room.","sonnet":"A celebration of fusing metal and drum and bass into a single overwhelming force aimed at bodies and faces and anything still standing — a takeover promised and delivered to every outsider who held their ground against mainstream trends. The energy is purely kinetic, a crowd-rush manifesto that doubles as a tribute to everyone who stayed loyal to the collision of genres.","src":"haiku"},
"zascha~lie":{"haiku":"Addiction to poison and pain; lying about love while consumed by a destructive connection.","opus":"Desire wrapped in horror-movie imagery, a lover who's poison and a corpse who's still hot, complicity in mutual torture, swallowing the deception knowingly and lying right back for the sake of the thrill.","sonnet":"About being addicted to a relationship that is toxic and dishonest — the narrator wants the person to lie and say they're not in love, because the truth is too consuming; blood, poison, and broken bones are the language of the dynamic, and even knowing the damage, the narrator lies for them and stays.","src":"haiku"},
"zascha~selfish":{"haiku":"Combative arrogance and isolation; refusing conformity while seeking validation through provocation.","opus":"Abrasive outsider bravado from a rapper who's never been liked and leans into it, drug-fueled insomnia and grandiosity daring rivals to fight, defiance and self-doubt tangled in the claim that being talked about is the point.","sonnet":"A rapper in a state of sleepless, drug-fueled grandiosity holds court against everyone who has overlooked or copied them — the ego is enormous and combative, but beneath the bravado is social isolation, insomnia, and a creeping sense of being exactly where they don't belong.","src":"haiku"},
"zayn~dusk-till-dawn-radio-edit-feat-sia":{"haiku":"Presence through darkness offers solace; the speaker promises unwavering companionship through chaos, asking only for freedom to love without guarding.","opus":"A vow of steadfast presence wrapped in seductive intimacy, promising to stay through the night and the wrongs, that whatever happens the other person will never be left alone.","sonnet":"A promise of total presence — emotionally, physically, through the night and through hardship — spoken to someone who needs to know they will never face darkness alone. The intimacy is sincere and unconditional, offered without performance or pretense.","src":"haiku"},
"zayn~vibez":{"haiku":"Seductive anticipation heightens through delayed gratification; the narrator promises imminent pleasure while maintaining tension through postponement.","opus":"A slow-burning seduction built entirely on anticipation and mood, Zayn coaxing a partner into the moment, promising to make it feel surreal and give them exactly what they need.","sonnet":"An intimate, patient seduction — the speaker tells a partner he has been waiting all night, assures them there is no rush, and promises to make the encounter feel beyond ordinary, asking only that they not make him wait any longer.","src":"haiku"},
"zazen-boys~6":{"haiku":"Urban decay and existential despair fuel this raw punk manifesto about alienation in modern decline.","opus":"Mukai turns Buddhist impermanence into a jittery survival anthem, six mad steel strings vibrating against the numb dread of a frozen city and shuttered shopping arcades. Beneath the bravado is a man cornered by shame, thwarted desire, and messages that never get answered.","sonnet":"Guitar as raw force of nature — six strings of vibrating steel channel impermanence and resurgent desire through the city's frozen streets, where the speaker drinks alone on the road, picks a fight with a stray cat, and channels unbearable tension into a declaration that rock will inflame you. The title's kanji frame it as Buddhist impermanence given sexual charge.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title names the six guitar strings, and the song is Mukai's ars poetica staged as self-flagellation, the Telecaster spitting verses to inflame you, the act of ROCK bolted to a Buddhist tag for impermanence and a sexual urge that keeps resurfacing. Between the hook's returns comes brutal self-accounting: a man who can no longer feel Saturday night, drinking alone on a street of shuttered shops, a sunset reflected in somebody's puddle of vomit, wanting to fight a stray cat out of sheer overload. The armor image is the spine of it — he grinds and sharpens himself to raw meat to finally earn the weight of the armor he built, then can't bear it and is ruined, the toughness he forged becoming the thing that destroys him. The self-portrait keeps convicting itself: loving and being loved, screwing and being screwed, forever justifying, the whole self-defense stamped as lies, the shameful need to be seen colliding with a message that never lands. So the crazed vibration of the strings is the only working transmission left — 念, fixated will, fired out as a bullet because every ordinary channel has failed, rock the one broadcast a man this walled-in can still make.","fv":2,"fnote":"The refrains are NUMBER GIRL’s Brutal Man, verbatim — the six crazed steel strings, the R-O-C-K incitement — returned four years after SAPPUKEI closed on them, with new verses that name what the old chant only wore: the armor’s weight, now past bearing.","themes":["artifice & performance","shame & repression","alienation & emptiness","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"forging the self until the self destroys him","built":"the title names the strings, making the guitar the speaker"},
"zazen-boys~6honno-kuruttta-yogan-no-kobun":{"haiku":"Desire manifests through material excess and rock spectacle; compulsion drives repetition until exhaustion and responsibility collapse into nihilistic acceptance.","src":"haiku"},
"zazen-boys~a-10zxf69":{"opus":"Zazen boys, led by Mukai Shutoku after the breakup of Number Girl, turn nervous tension into a compositional principle, and this track wounds itself tight to the point of snapping. The narrator scratches their own skin, cries without control, laughs alone under moonlight to disguise it, and idles in stupor awaiting a demon that never arrives. Heartbreak here does not ache; it shatters pieces of the chest outright.","sonnet":"A person wound unbearably tight — scratching open their own skin, crying uncontrollably, laughing alone on moonlit nights to hide it, killing time in lazy stupor waiting for some confrontation with a demon that never comes, the heartbreak literally shattering pieces of the chest.","src":"sonnet"},
"zazen-boys~a-115mwz3":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator prowls nightlife hunting women — repeating \"barracuda,\" \"man-eater,\" and \"searching for lucky fruit\" over flashes of pink mesh, alligators, and lonely nights; the barracuda is a predator metaphor for pure, restless sexual appetite."},
"zazen-boys~a-13oy27h":{"haiku":"Themes of death, mortality, or psychological demise; existential darkness permeates the lyrics.","opus":"Zazen boys thread deadpan absurdism through their angular funk-rock, and this track reads like a recipe gone deliberately insane. Vodka by the gallon, bonito flakes by the kilo, Dead Sea salt, a swamp catfish's eyeball, simmered a full day in a warehouse stocked with discounted beer. The mock-precision is the joke and the point, a controlled procedure that resolves only into total scattering and collapse.","sonnet":"A deliberately absurdist recipe — 1.8 gallons of vodka, 80 kilograms of bonito flakes, 200 tablespoons of Dead Sea salt, a swamp catfish's eyeball — simmered 24 hours in a studio warehouse full of discounted beer, resulting in total scattering and collapse.","src":"haiku"},
"zazen-boys~a-14mwy73":{"haiku":"A graphic condemnation of war and generational trauma—15-year-old girls burned, bodies consumed by scavengers, yet childhood innocence lingers in every generation.","opus":"Mukai Shutoku's zazen boys can pivot from tenderness to atrocity without warning, and this track weaponizes that whiplash. A girl's eyes recall her grandmother's, both forever young, spliced against a stench-filled 1945 death: a burst abdomen fed to a dog, the dog killed and eaten in tears. The juxtaposition is the argument, that every young woman carries the line of all the young women killed before her.","sonnet":"The song cuts between a gentle observation that a girl's eyes look like her grandmother's — both of them forever young — and a brutal, stench-filled account of wartime death in 1945: a fifteen-year-old's burst abdomen fed to a dog, the dog killed and eaten in tears, a man already dead calling for sake with a smile. The juxtaposition is the song's argument: every young woman carries the line of all the young women killed before her.","src":"haiku"},
"zazen-boys~a-1h5tr1o":{"src":"web","web":"A woman drifts through the city at night, leaping from one fleeting connection to the next out of fear that without them she will cease to exist; Mukai plays on kankei (connection) to show that all these bonds are transparent, thin, and ultimately without weight."},
"zazen-boys~a-1xrllqv":{"haiku":"Romantic obsession or love-related suffering; emotional attachment drives the narrative.","opus":"Zazen boys render cityscape as fever-dream here, dissolving a Tokyo ring road into summer dusk. A balloon hangs above ambiguous patterns, a safety lock releases, and a shooting champion appears with gold medals and a Russian girl's anthem strung round their neck. Mukai's fractured imagery leaves the narrator dirty and outpaced by the fading light, meaning gathering at the edges without ever settling into plot.","sonnet":"A summer evening on a Tokyo ring road dissolving into dusk — a hanging balloon floating above ambiguous patterns, a safety lock released, and a shooting champion with gold medals in a Russian girl's anthem around their neck, the narrator dirty and outpaced by dusk.","src":"haiku"},
"zazen-boys~a-3cckpe":{"haiku":"A meditation on internal warfare—the speaker at war with themselves, winning and losing meaning nothing, just mud-wrestling the self.","opus":"Zazen boys built their sound on rhythmic tension held past comfort, which makes this an ideal vehicle for its subject: the eve of a battle fought only with oneself. Not war against others but a muddy, grinding contest between versions of the self, where winning and losing lose meaning and the conflict never quite begins or ends. The song runs on that adrenaline of perpetual self-opposition.","sonnet":"A song about the feeling of being on the eve of an internal battle — not war with others but a grinding, muddy contest between versions of oneself, where winning and losing are beside the point and the conflict never fully starts or ends. The emotional core is the adrenaline-like tension of perpetual self-opposition.","src":"haiku","fable":"The title means front line, but the war is entirely internal, the song returning to that feeling on the eve of battle and then naming the combatants as one person split in two — I am the enemy, I am my own ally — locked in what Mukai calls a mud-match of me against me. The key admission is that winning and losing don't matter, which converts the conflict from something to be resolved into a permanent condition: a self at war with itself where victory is impossible because both sides are you, and the only real state is the perpetual, unwinnable tension of pre-battle nerves. Stripped to a handful of lines chanted over and over, the song is that stalemate made into a groove, the front line not out there but inside, and it never advances.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","war & battle","madness & the mind"],"means":"an internal war designed never to be won","built":"a handful of lines chanted as groove, stalemate made rhythmic"},
"zazen-boys~a-3glfq6":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator feels something like a vaulting pole — lead, iron, or concrete — thrust through his chest, recognises it is bad, and tries to keep going anyway; the title frames this object as \"the pole of deep sleep,\" making the song a grotesque, deadpan meditation on the sensation of being killed from the inside.","fable":"The whole song is the anatomy of a foreign-body sensation refusing to resolve into an image: something lodged in the gut and chest that the speaker can only grope at by material — lead, iron, or concrete? — a competition pole jammed crosswise inside him, his sense of balance gone strange, and his one coping strategy the pathetic hope of just not minding it while knowing it is bad. Against that queasy indeterminacy the title supplies the only certainty, and it is a cruel joke: the anmin-bo, literally a rod for peaceful sleep, is the thing that has killed him, chanted over and over — killed by the sleep-rod — until the mantra of rest becomes the instrument of death. The genius of the structure is the call-and-response fade where the phrase splits between voices, the object-name and the killing passed back and forth like the sensation itself echoing through the body, and the one crack of surrealism — he dreamt of some man somewhere — that suggests the whole ordeal may be a dream of dying that never lets him wake. Read straight, it is dread with no referent given a name whose soothing meaning is exactly inverted; the repetition is not filler but the only honest response to a discomfort that has no story, only recurrence.","fv":2,"fnote":"The title is a coinage set against the album’s own totem: the keisaku — the zazen stick — exists to strike a drowsing meditator awake, and Mukai’s anmin-bō, the sleep stick, is its exact inversion, a rod that kills you into sleep. Nobody in the band has glossed the pair; the joke sits in the names.","themes":["dissociation & numbness","death & grief"],"means":"dread without a referent given a name that soothes","built":"the soothing title inverted into instrument of death by repetition"},
"zazen-boys~a-djlf08":{"src":"web","web":"On a blazing summer morning, black underwear shows through fabric, asphalt glares, a bicycle mirror catches a nostalgic image — the song fixates on sweat, heat, and the fleeting erotic charge of an ordinary urban moment.","fable":"A midsummer heat-fever built entirely around one image glimpsed and re-glimpsed: black underwear showing through sweat-thin summer clothes, caught in asphalt glare, in a bicycle's mirror, in the morning's pale light — a glimpse the lyric stamps as genfūkei, primal scenery, the image a mind files as formative. Mukai never develops the sighting into narrative — instead the phrase black underwear, pure jet-black underwear, is chanted into a near-manic loop until the words stop describing and start behaving like the heat itself, an obsessive tic the body can't shake in the swelter. The scattered gestures around the chant — reaching for the air-conditioner remote, peeling the fabric off skin as the temperature climbs — locate the fixation in physical discomfort, so the song reads as desire and heatstroke as the same sensation: a mind stuck on a single erotic detail because the July air has boiled everything else out of it. The repetition is the point; the read is the mask of pure lechery over the plain animal fact of being too hot to think.","fv":2,"themes":["love & desire","madness & the mind"],"means":"desire and heatstroke rendered as the same sensation","built":"the chant outruns description until words behave like heat"},
"zazen-boys~cold-beat":{"haiku":"Premonition and intuition guide the speaker through supernatural encounters with yōrei and strange assemblies. Darkness promises strangeness, and the self dissolves into recognition.","opus":"A jittery run of premonitions and sixth-sense encounters with ghosts and spirits, from an apparition at the toilet window to cats and clay figures at a mountain gathering, the singer strangely unafraid and sensing something will happen today.","sonnet":"A heightened sixth sense registers uncanny presences across three time-periods — dusk, midnight, dawn — a ghost at a toilet window, a hitodama (spirit-light) sticking to the speaker's back, a delivery person in a Terence Trent D'Arby t-shirt passing as if from another world; each encounter charges the day with the feeling that something is about to happen.","src":"haiku","fable":"The song is a diary of heightened perception, each verse opening on the same premonition — a prickling sixth sense that stirs, then rampages, then simply hums along nicely — and each stamped to a time of day, so dusk, deep night and daybreak become three shifts on one supernatural clock. What that sense keeps delivering is uncanny cargo: a ghost through the toilet window, a mountain assembly of cat and monkey and clay figurine, a mass of spirits stuck to the narrator's back — yet the reported feeling is not terror but a bittersweet smile and strange calm, someone who has stopped being frightened by the invisible and started reading it as ordinary weather. By the last verse the sixth sense has cooled into mere expectancy — morning delivering only a courier drifting past in a Terence Trent D’Arby T-shirt — the flat repeated hunch that something's bound to happen today, and the chanted title stops meaning a chill and becomes the literal beat, the deadpan funk pulse the whole trance rides on. Mukai turns extrasensory dread into groove: the cold beat is both the shiver down the spine and the metronome that keeps the body moving through it.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","dissociation & numbness","night & the city"],"means":"extrasensory dread converted into deadpan funk groove","built":"the title doubles as shiver and as the metronomic beat underneath"},
"zazen-boys~crazy-days-crazy-feeling":{"haiku":"Daily madness from cycles of desire and instinct; people die endlessly yet sanity resides in raw sensation.","opus":"A frenetic Japanese portrait of urban madness where the impermanence of all things (shogyō mujō) and reviving sexual urges cycle endlessly amid burning cities and mutant humanity. Living as prey in a city where people die casually, the narrator insists on clinging to the raw sensation of being alive no matter how insane his mind gets.","sonnet":"In a burning, chaotic city under a full moon, the singer witnesses the endless cycle of human impermanence — people dying everywhere, sexual urges resurfacing, mutant crowds writhing in mud — and holds onto the raw sense of being alive even as everything goes insane around him.","src":"haiku","web":"Invoking Buddhist impermanence (諸行無常), the song churns through cycles of libido, birth, urban alienation, and excrement while insisting that genuine feeling survives the madness; it is a loud, sardonic catalogue of modern life as an inescapable karmic loop.","fable":"This one widens the impermanence-and-desire refrain into a whole cosmology of churning biology: ancestors dying and reborn, deformed humanity writhing in mud, genes discharging their karma without pause, babies pouring out one after another already saddled with a fate they cannot escape. Against that vision of life as a mindless self-replicating machine, Mukai finds his one point of leverage in an almost comic image — a Tombo brand pencil he has whittled down himself, sharpened into a word meant to stab, the sound of it being honed standing in for the act of writing. The song's argument is that the only sane response to a world where people keep dropping dead is refusing to slip into that current: he pushes past blue into mania, narrates his daily self-interrogation confessed theatrically in public, and lands on a single non-negotiable — hold onto the felt sensation of being alive, no matter how deranged the head gets. That closing refusal, repeated to fade, is the read: the craziness is not celebrated but chosen as the price of staying awake inside it.","fv":2,"fnote":"The tengu is on a return flight: NUMBER GIRL’s Sappukei title track watched the same creature lift off a city high-rise in Shinjuku four years earlier. The image crossed from band to band intact — still airborne over a burning city.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","death & grief","madness & the mind","identity & becoming"],"means":"choosing derangement as the price of staying awake inside it","built":"a pencil whittled to a point stands in for the act of writing"},
"zazen-boys~daigakusei":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator sinks his right half-body into a mud pit, grins stupidly, and apologizes with zero energy, then the word \"university student\" is hammered home over and over; the deadpan image of voluntary, smirking stagnation is the whole portrait of that life stage.","fable":"A short, spitting jab that turns a social category into an epithet. The one real verse is a self-portrait of squalor and shirking — right side of the body pitched into mud, half a smirk, a listless apology — and then the words university student, chanted flatly against Yeah after Yeah until the phrase stops meaning a person and becomes an accusation, a slur Mukai flings at a whole cohort of the aimless and self-satisfied. The middle turns openly vicious, daring the offended to laugh at him and then drop dead of a stomachache, so the song's contempt curdles back onto itself: he indicts the listless student while sneering from the same mud. What earns the read is the chant mechanism — by drowning the sneer under sheer repetition of the noun, the track enacts exactly the numb, thought-clouded stupor it's mocking.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"contempt for the aimless that curdles back onto its own source","built":"chanting the noun until it becomes an accusation, not a person"},
"zazen-boys~harahetta":{"haiku":"Hunger consumes endlessly; internal voices devour while waking transforms into feeding frenzy, consciousness reduced to appetite without satisfaction.","opus":"Hunger in the dead of night rendered as raw sensation, a stomach growling, a splintered chopstick, solitary mornings, the animal loop of just eating and eating while dreaming, isolation chosen and endured.","sonnet":"Hunger and solitude at midnight — chopsticks snap and splinter into the finger, rain taps outside, a stray cat cries for leftovers, and the speaker eats alone through dreams and newspaper mornings. The word \"harahetta\" (I'm hungry) names the only need acknowledged.","src":"haiku","fable":"A study in solitude reduced to appetite: in the dark of night the senses are too loud, and the loudest thing is a stomach growling in an empty room. The song strips life down to one repeated verb — to eat, kurau, a blunt animal word closer to devour than to dine — chanted as the only thing left to do whether sleepy or dreaming, morning newspaper in hand, alone. Its sharpest image is the snapped disposable chopsticks that leave a splinter in the hand: the cheapest possible ritual of a solo meal, and even that small act of feeding oneself draws a little blood. Mukai lets a stray cat wander in and answers it with the same flat logic the whole song runs on — if it cries, just give it the leftovers; if you want to be alone, then be alone — a shrug that presents self-sufficiency and loneliness as indistinguishable. The repetition doesn't build to anything, and that refusal is the meaning: a life pared to hunger and its dumb satisfaction, eating and eating because there is nothing else to do with the empty morning.","fv":2,"themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"solitude and hunger made indistinguishable from each other","built":"the disposable chopstick splinter: cheapest feeding still draws blood"},
"zazen-boys~himitsu-girl-s-top-secret":{"src":"web","web":"A figure drifts through the city carrying secrets so absolute she would take them to the grave; the song circles the gap between one's public and private selves, playing on the Japanese word himitsu (secret) to mean both the girl and the weight she carries.","fable":"The song is built to withhold: a secret carried to the grave, a truth nobody may know, a self split down the middle into the real one and the HIMITSU one, drifting through the streets in a fog of lies. Mukai never lets us cross that membrane, because the point is the membrane — a sliver of a splinter lodged in and left there, the wound kept rather than pulled. The second half tells us why the sealing costs so little: 見ないふりが得, not-seeing pays, and the unseeing belongs to her — hers is the laugh of a woman who wants to make what she saw into something that never happened, who will keep feigning she saw nothing, probably forever. So the top secret is not only who she is but what she witnessed, a knowledge she chose to unremember, which is why the title barked in Roman caps works as a sealed lid — a phrase that names a secret precisely by refusing its contents, the repetition at the end not emptiness but the same lock turned again over something she alone knows she saw.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.3","fnote":"The drifting girl extends a NUMBER GIRL figure: 透明少女 watched a girl turn transparent and dissolve into the summer street; five years on, Mukai’s city girl is opaque — classified, carrying what she saw. Same street corner, inverted visibility.","themes":["shame & repression","dissociation & numbness","identity & becoming"],"means":"a wound kept rather than pulled, the sealing treated as the cheaper option","built":"the Roman-caps title names a secret precisely by refusing its contents, the repetition a lock turned again"},
"zazen-boys~honnoji":{"haiku":"The subject waits at Honnoji through centuries; bones scatter across history while time collapses.","opus":"An endless, dreamlike wait at Honnoji, the temple of Nobunaga's betrayal, stretched across collapsing time and insomniac Tokyo nightscapes of neon, helicopters, and summer bugs, the night beginning as the narrator talks to himself while history refuses to arrive.","sonnet":"A surreal, nocturnal vigil at the historic site of Honnoji — a place associated with ambush and death — in which the speaker smokes on the balcony watching a small cosmos, neon signs blinking out at ten, helicopter circling past midnight, the city's orange lights gathering summer insects, and the night beginning to ask its own questions.","src":"haiku"},
"zazen-boys~kimochi":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator tries to tell someone his feelings but finds there is nowhere to land, as though forever in flight; the song is a frank inventory of the impossibility of making one's interior life understood by another person."},
"zazen-boys~my-crazy-feeling":{"haiku":"Society's open window permits madness; daily insanity masks desire and incompleteness.","opus":"A jittery, sharpened rant on living slightly insane in a heartless city, sexual impulse and impermanence recurring daily, prizing the raw sensation of being alive over sanity in a world where people drop dead casually and society's window stays wide open.","sonnet":"A Japanese speaker observes urban society as a continuously open wound — society's window left ajar, impermanence recycling endlessly, sexual impulse reviving — and sharpens his pencil-words into blades against the lack of imagination, self-awareness, and the fabricated circles of strategists; even in the crazy, he insists on holding onto the raw sensation of being alive.","src":"haiku","fable":"The recurring gag-image is a window onto society left hanging wide open, which reads first as the Japanese euphemism for an undone fly — the self caught exposed, its defenses never fastened, unable to seal itself off from a world it can't shut out. The verses are a daily bulletin from a fried nervous system: prey of the city, endlessly spouting empty theory, watching people drop dead like it's nothing, and the sharpest concrete gesture is a Tombo pencil whittled to a point so the words it writes can stab and be honed sharper still. Mukai converts the writing tool into the weapon the song keeps threatening to be. Its target, tellingly, is himself: the diagnosis of a man whose economic standing and big talk are matched by a proportional shortfall of imagination, self-awareness and insight, someone confessing his own crackpot inner Q-and-A in front of everyone. The song develops toward one held resolution — however scrambled and tangled the brain gets, hold onto the actual sensation of being alive — so the derangement stops being mere breakdown and becomes the last proof of life a person clings to when the exposure won't close and everything inside has come apart.","fv":2,"fnote":"Half its verses are Crazy Days Crazy Feeling’s, near verbatim — the Tombo pencil, the daily derangement, the same resolution to keep hold of being alive — the album running one text through a second body, the way its mantra runs through five of its songs.","themes":["madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression","night & the city"],"means":"clinging to felt sensation as the last proof of being alive","built":"the open-window euphemism pins the self caught perpetually exposed"},
"zazen-boys~no-time":{"src":"web","web":"The narrator fixates on the sensation of time collapsing — ten seconds and a hundred years pass at the same speed — and on the inability to remember what they ate three days ago or where they were a decade back; the repeated cry of \"NO TIME\" captures pure existential bewilderment at being swept along without traction.","fable":"A pure panic attack about time, built so that its own form enacts the disorder it describes: ten seconds pass and the next unit is a year, ten years, a century, the scale jumping so fast the speaker can no longer say what hour it is or what he ate three days ago. The read is that this is not busyness but a collapse of time-sense itself — the faster he works to get ahead of it, the more it drains away, until the acceleration curdles into a paranoid hallucination of being chased by someone, the sensation of lateness converted into a pursuer. The hammered NO TIME is less a complaint than a symptom, the mind stuttering on the one fact it cannot metabolize.","fv":2,"themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"lateness curdling into a hallucinated pursuer","built":"the form jumps scale — seconds to centuries — enacting the collapse"},
"zazen-boys~you-make-me-feel-so-bad":{"src":"web","web":"Cigarette ash, an air-cleaner clicking on, no word from the woman since she left — the narrator replays being called a \"boring guy\" and lets the resentment metastasise in a small, smoke-filled room; it is an unflinching portrait of the petty, lasting injury of a breakup.","fable":"A breakup song stripped to pure grievance, opening on a perfect image of stale resentment — a cigarette butt still smoldering, the air purifier fussily reacting to it, the speaker spitting into the ashtray, the room itself registering a low toxicity he keeps generating. Beneath the English hook the Japanese specifies an anger loop: one insult she left him with, that he is a boring man, has been carved into his brain. The mechanism is that recalling it makes him angrier, then recalling it again makes him angrier still, escalation by repetition rather than any new event. The tell is the line he keeps appending — that she surely thinks the same of him — which reads not as insight but as projection, the wounded party unable to concede the insult landed and so throwing it back. The read is a man mistaking a festering, self-feeding grudge for justified contempt, the repetition itself exposing that he cannot let go.","fv":2,"themes":["anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"a festering grudge mistaken by its holder for justified contempt","built":"projection appended as a line he keeps throwing back, exposing the loop"},
"zazen-boys~zazen-bo-ii":{"haiku":"Broken actions divorced from theory; hypocrisy and contradiction persist universally, making righteousness impossible and meaning purely performative.","opus":"A 3am cold-city meditation where all of creation feels unbearably sad, taunting the trapped listener to throw away safety and actually risk everything they claim to want, since theory without action is death.","sonnet":"A late-night city meditation on impermanence and desire — a man bleeding in the street, a wife challenged by her rut of safety, the frozen city where everything is sorrowful. The distinction between theory without action (dead) and action without theory (nothing) frames a call to risk something for what you truly want.","src":"haiku","fable":"The band's namesake mantra and closest thing to a manifesto, framed by a looped koan: the Buddhist truth that all things are impermanent — shogyō mujō — set against a sexual impulse that keeps reviving, so the eternal turnover of the cosmos is yoked to the one appetite that refuses to stay dead. Between those brackets the song scans a nocturnal cityscape at three in the morning, the frozen city where such things are common, where a man bleeds out in the street and all of creation feels unbearably sad — a Zen detachment that reads less as peace than anesthesia, the numbness of a modern life that can watch anything happen. Out of that numbness comes the goad: if you can't escape the daily grind anyway, throw away your safety; if there's something you want badly enough to run a risk for, then do it. The song states its dialectic almost as a slogan — action without theory is nothingness, theory without action is death — before landing on the line that resolves the structure, slackness and tension tracing parallel lines, all deeds impermanent, folding the manifesto back into the opening koan. Mukai's move is to weaponize impermanence: because nothing lasts, comfort is a lie and only risk and desire prove you're still alive, the coiled funk churn underneath making that argument physical, never resolving.","fv":2,"fnote":"The album seats its recurring motifs at top and bottom: the impermanence-and-desire mantra and the wide-open society window return here from the opener — a reprise of hooks rather than text, the sitting resumed.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape","night & the city"],"means":"weaponising impermanence so only risk and desire prove life","built":"the manifesto folds back into its opening koan in the final line"},
"zeal-ardor~fend-you-off":{"haiku":"Internal rebellion against authority sustains itself through lies and endurance, protecting fragile independence.","opus":"A man who has swallowed his own lies to keep someone at arm's length finds the deception has become a wound he can't stop worrying, faith curdling into a thorn he can neither pull out nor ignore. Zeal & Ardor's gospel-tinged rage against inherited belief turns inward here, the self-harm of clenched fists standing in for a defense that costs more than the threat.","sonnet":"The speaker has been eating lies — lying to themselves, lying outwardly — just to keep a destructive presence at bay, the \"thorn in my side\" image making clear that the defence has become as wounding as what it defends against.","src":"haiku"},
"zeal-ardor~i-caught-you":{"haiku":"The speaker catches someone doing something forbidden and addresses them with shame, pointing out their cowardice and contradictory desire to both escape and be pious, using indecipherable incantations alongside warnings.","opus":"A menacing confrontation where an all-knowing voice catches a person doing something shameful and warns the cowering, fleeing figure of the ruin awaiting them, chanting words spelled backward like a hex and daring them to lie down and perish. Manuel Gagneux's project welds black metal to spirituals and chain-gang chant, and this ritual judgment sits deep in that eerie fusion.","sonnet":"Someone has been caught doing what they should have left alone, and the witness is the only one who knows. The accused runs like a child and screams like a coward, wanting devotion and transgression simultaneously while being told by a cold, repeating voice that the fall is already in motion and both outcomes are impossible to hold at once.","src":"haiku"},
"zebrahead~licking-on-a-knife-for-fun":{"haiku":"Betrayal through false friendship demands defiant dignity; the speaker chooses noble death over humiliation, rejecting complicity with duplicitous allies.","opus":"Betrayal by a so-called friend who lies and cuts you for kicks, met with defiant pride — better to fall standing than to kneel — and the old sneer that with allies like these, who needs foes.","sonnet":"Betrayal by someone who called themselves a friend — the speaker watches them lie with a silver tongue that licks a knife for fun, chooses dignity over submission, and declares they would rather die on their feet than live on their knees. The broken friendship is the wound, and refusal to forget it is the weapon.","src":"haiku"},
"zedd~addicted-to-a-memory-feat-bahari":{"haiku":"Two former lovers cling to the memory of their relationship despite becoming enemies and emotional slaves to the past.","opus":"About clinging to a burned-out, volatile relationship, holding on to who the couple used to be as sentimental slaves running on empty; they're addicted to the memory rather than the reality.","sonnet":"Two people remain in a relationship that has run out of genuine feeling, unable to leave because they are hooked on what it used to be rather than what it is now.","src":"haiku"},
"zedd~clarity-feat-foxes":{"haiku":"Loving someone you know will destroy you because the cure and the poison are the same person.","opus":"A love that wounds as much as it heals, the narrator unable to leave someone who is both the disease and the cure. EDM catharsis built on the paradox of needing what's destroying you.","sonnet":"A destructive, compulsive relationship that the speaker knows will end badly keeps pulling them back in — the paradox at the centre is that this toxic love is also the only thing that makes sense, the remedy and the insanity at once.","src":"haiku"},
"zedd~i-want-you-to-know-feat-selena-gomez":{"haiku":"Fusion and surrender define the speaker's surrender to another; storms pass but light remains constant, binding two souls into unified reflection.","opus":"A euphoric dance-pop vow of two lovers merging completely, sharing one glow and one path as they tumble into mutual surrender. Under the ecstasy runs a thread of anxiety, with the singer asking through the metaphor of a passing storm whether her partner will truly shield and stay with her.","sonnet":"A declaration of total, reciprocal belonging — \"you and me bleed the same light\" — offered to a partner during a storm, with the chain-reaction of falling yielding to the promise that this force is shared and the other should say whether they're covered or confess they're lying.","src":"haiku"},
"zedd~straight-into-the-fire":{"haiku":"Transcendence requires releasing accumulated resentment and pain; forgiveness dissolves the past through purifying destruction.","opus":"An anthem of release, urging you to send your dark days to the sun and let anger and hurtful words burn away like paper, framing forgiveness as the hard but necessary path to moving on.","sonnet":"A direct instruction to release accumulated pain: take every dark day, every wrong done, every drowned dream, and burn it — send it straight into the fire until only smoke is left and the window is cracked open again. Forgiveness is the hardest act, but the only path to carrying on.","src":"haiku"},
"zephyrianna~kingslayer":{"haiku":"A puppet rebellion wages war against systemic control and artificial existence, destroying false towers to liberate enslaved consciousness. The blade cuts toward freedom at any cost, fighting alone if necessary.","opus":"A glitchy bilingual protest track pitting a rotten, controlling system and its human marionettes against a freedom-bringing warrior figure. Zephyrianna weave English rebellion together with Japanese yearning for a different reality, picturing existence as coded and altered until death, and pledging to give up everything to cut a loved one loose from their puppet strings.","sonnet":"A call to revolt against corrupt systems, blending English and Japanese: the listener is framed as a puppet whose strings have been cut, a corrupted program in a rigged world, and the Kingslayer figure is both liberator and beloved — \"I'd sacrifice my life to find you\" — as castles of power crumble.","src":"haiku"},
"zero-7~destiny":{"haiku":"Distance separates lovers while longing and memory sustain connection across miles through psychic interdependence.","opus":"A lonely traveler in a hotel room, watching porn and aching across the miles for the person he draws strength from and is bending time to get back to. However far apart, he holds that the two of them are simply each other's destiny.","sonnet":"A traveller alone in a hotel room, watching the clock and dreaming of return — the old moon fading into new, strength drawn from the absent partner — certain that however many miles apart, they are each other's destiny and reunion is nearly here.","src":"haiku"},
"zero-7~in-the-waiting-line":{"haiku":"People waste time in a meaningless queue, each working to take everything they can; nothing is real and no one agrees on what to believe in.","opus":"A hazy interrogation of purpose and conformity, the narrator idling before a stalled wheel, feeling nothing is genuine and no one shares his doubts, frittering days away in a routine of dishonesty while everyone else grabs what they can. Zero 7 are a British downtempo duo, and this 2001 track, sung by Sophie Barker, floats its quiet unease beneath lush, chilled-out calm.","sonnet":"The grinding suspension of a life lived on someone else's clock — nine to five, motionless wheel, a world where everyone takes what they can and no one agrees on what is even real. The speaker wants to shout but would rather not be seen at all, and eventually just hides another day inside the identical rhythm of waiting.","src":"haiku"},
"zero-9-36~self-destruct":{"haiku":"Compulsive self-sabotage cycles endlessly; repeated patterns guarantee destruction while the speaker observes self-annihilation without intervention.","opus":"A song about self-sabotage without an actual longing for death, the narrator repeatedly triggering his own downfall because clinging to every failed vow and spoiling whatever he handles has become his only routine. Unable to break the loop, he wonders what the point of trying is when destruction feels inevitable.","sonnet":"A cycle of self-sabotage the speaker cannot break — everything touched ends destroyed, broken promises accumulate, and pulling the pin on self-destruction requires neither the desire to die nor the desire to try. The venom stays inside, the explosion is done to oneself, and no help is ever called for.","src":"haiku"},
"zero-9-36~the-end-feat-scarlxrd":{"opus":"A rap-rock confession of self-destruction where the narrator, his own worst enemy, insists he alone decides when his end arrives. Pushing himself to the edge and hoping not to break, he channels overwhelming problems and self-hatred into defiant momentum. Pain becomes armor, the drive to outrun collapse mistaken for control.","sonnet":"A song about pushing oneself to psychological extremes out of self-hatred and competitive contempt — the narrator hates himself more than any critic could, drives himself to the edge repeatedly, and uses that masochism as fuel against everyone who tries to stand in his way. The 'end' he keeps seeking is both self-destruction and a finish line.","src":"sonnet"},
"zero-theorem~swarm":{"haiku":"Envious appetite feeds on others' apathy; insignificant masses blissfully unaware become absorbing spectacle for conscious observer drowning in awareness.","opus":"An outsider's bitter envy of the willfully oblivious, dressed as industrial confession: the singer sees through the rot everyone else ignores and half-hates that clarity, wishing he could wear the same plastic smile and drift through life numb.","sonnet":"A speaker who envies the shallow and apathetic their plastic faces and their ability to live inside the ghost without noticing the infinite decay — he cannot achieve that comfortable numbness, and so watches from the side while others smile wide for pictures and abuse their gifts. The swarm is the collective buzz of unreflective life he cannot join.","src":"haiku"},
"zeta~the-distance":{"opus":"A high-velocity metaphor for chasing a meaningful life, the narrator refusing to slow down or look back as he drives full-throttle toward the future, having been stuck in the dark too long.","sonnet":"A driving, forward-propulsive song about emerging from a prolonged dark period with renewed momentum — the narrator insists on pressing ahead at full speed without looking back, framing the future as already in motion.","src":"sonnet"},
"zetsubou-p~i-am-everything":{"haiku":"Cosmic apocalypse and divine supremacy converge as the speaker becomes both alpha and omega, consuming all. Terror becomes inevitable as the speaker reveals themselves as everything worth fearing.","opus":"A Vocaloid apocalypse sung from the perspective of an omnipotent, terrifying God-figure watching the world end, claiming to be the fear that was always stalking humanity — awe and menace collapsed into one cold voice as everything is undone.","sonnet":"A god-figure addresses a dying world at the apocalypse: friends gone, earth dead, this is the end — the speaker is alpha and omega, fantasy made real, the primal fear and first goodbye, who has always been present and will hear the last cry.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~all-of-me-for-you":{"haiku":"Spiritual devastation and existential absence pervade; the speaker surrenders completely while questioning whether gods exist, consciousness endures, or the world permits redemption.","opus":"Total, sacrificial devotion set against apocalyptic doubt about dead gods and lost innocence. The narrator offers everything while wrestling with sin, survival and the machine-god crushing what is sacred. Zheani's confessional industrial rap turns surrender into a spiritual dare, asking whether one should fight to the end or simply give in.","sonnet":"Total devotion offered against a backdrop of civilisational collapse — machine gods have colonised the spirit, innocence is dead, and yet the narrator burns through ketamine and defiance to give everything to someone, asking whether it's worth fighting or surrendering when the gods may already be dead. The sacrifice is real but the recipient is never certain.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~bleed-for-me":{"haiku":"The speaker is emotionally depleted to the point of numbness, their life burned out, and demands others share in the pain they cannot feel alone.","opus":"The Australian underground rapper turns numbness and self-harm into a hostile mantra, describing herself as emptied out and unable to feel anything while barking a demand that a lover suffer for her. Insisting this darkness is permanent rather than a passing mood, she flips total depletion into a cold, threatening show of dominance.","sonnet":"Total emotional exhaustion collapsing into numbness. Unable to feel anything after burning through hope and longing, she demands that someone else bleed in her place, making the rawness she can no longer access visible through another body. The desperation is cyclic, eternal, and utterly spent.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~burnt-book-interlude":{"haiku":"Burnt identity and intellectual violence collide; the speaker reclaims status as destruction itself, refusing literacy and civility in favor of menace and edge.","opus":"Zheani declares herself an ungovernable, half-illegible force, a burnt book and a violence her enemy can't read or contain. The rapid-fire self-versus-you catalog is an identity built entirely on being the sharp edge no one survives.","sonnet":"A rapid-fire self-definition as something dangerous, suppressed, and uncontrollable — the speaker positions herself as a radical force opposed to passivity, conformity, and those who would rather forget or burn what she represents.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~child-of-the-future":{"haiku":"Guilt and temporal displacement haunt the speaker caught between past convictions and present shame; they reclaim righteousness while confessing complicity and limitation.","opus":"A defiant letter from a prison cell to an unborn descendant, penned by someone jailed for thinking the wrong thoughts. The narrator disclaims perfection but insists on being right, indicting a conformist age of false flags and celebrity saints. Zheani's dystopian rap frames martyrdom as vindication, hoping the future remains human.","sonnet":"A letter from a gulag — the narrator insists they were right, even as they do their time for thinking the wrong thoughts, and addresses a future child who may or may not still be human. The catalogue of societal failure (celebrity saints, pills for enmity, crabs crawling back) frames the imprisonment as the price of correctness, not guilt.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~designer-sadness":{"haiku":"Someone packages and commodifies their depression in luxury aesthetics, using designer brands to suppress trauma and fill the void of broken identity, trading authenticity for escapism.","opus":"A portrait of grief dressed up as luxury, where designer labels and glamorous ruin become props for numbing pain and turning a breakdown into content. Australian artist Zheani sings of inner demons that vow to plug the emptiness while she stands there hollowed out, bodily present but emotionally gone, aestheticizing her own despair.","sonnet":"A woman wearing luxury brands and performing beauty while internally shattered — trauma is suppressed by spending, demons fill the void, and the glamorous exterior (Balenciaga, Gucci, eyeliner tears) is a costume for a profound breakdown.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~drunken-god":{"haiku":"Intoxication as spiritual practice collapses divine and profane, where the drunken god finds glory in oblivion. Baptism in alcohol and delusion transforms the speaker into saint of transgression.","opus":"Blackout drinking is staged as a mock passion play, the speaker crucifying herself on Dionysian excess and blaspheming her way toward oblivion. Zheani weaponizes Catholic imagery to make degradation sacred, treating annihilation as a rite she performs on her own body.","sonnet":"A blasphemous self-mythologising through intoxication: baptised in Mai Tais and white lines, the speaker is simultaneously Christ (holes in hands from spikes), Dionysus (water to wine), and the saint who drowned in piss and rose again — 'glory be to the drunken god' celebrates the divine accessed through excess, chaos, and a body offered to destruction.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~fuck-the-hollywood-cult":{"haiku":"A girl watches her family destroyed by rock and roll excess and the Hollywood industry, burning with rage and declaring war on the system that commodifies and abuses young women. The song frames her as both victim and warrior reclaiming power.","opus":"Zheani's vengeful attack on an entertainment machine that grooms, uses and degrades women while monetizing their fall. Reclaiming the insults thrown at her, she frames herself as an avenging figure vowing to burn down the whole exploitative empire.","sonnet":"A direct assault on what the singer describes as a predatory entertainment industry that grooms young women, monetizes their destruction, and passes it off as culture — she positions herself as someone who grew up inside this machine and survived it, arriving now with a sword rather than submission. The song describes the process of being used, degraded, and sacrificed for profit.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~ghosts":{"haiku":"Cycles of substance dependency, self-sabotage, and haunting despair. The speaker is trapped in destructive patterns, inherited dysfunction, and the inability to move forward.","opus":"A raw spiral through days-long depressive fog and pill cravings, the narrator on the run from herself, haunted and drenched in sweat, blaming no one but her own inherited appetite. Zheani's confrontational trap-punk gives this addiction diary its jittery immediacy, the moth-to-flame cycle and inherited hunger laid bare between bursts of defiant, drunken bravado that never quite drown the fear.","sonnet":"Stuck in a bleak chemical spiral, someone moves between self-awareness and defiant numbness — haunted, sick, performing recklessness on the outside while privately knowing she inherited this pattern and cannot break it. The mood has lasted for days and the pills are always the answer she keeps reaching for.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~gimme-die-by-fire":{"haiku":"Retribution against patriarchal violence demands incineration, where the speaker channels rage of burned witches. Fire becomes justice, annihilating enemies and systems of oppression.","opus":"A chant for vengeance that widens from personal enemies to the men who burned women as witches and the systems raping the earth, ending in a chilling patience that waits out the offenders' own destruction. The gimme-gimme incantation frames righteous fury as an appetite, wanting a meaningful death in a world that offers only pointless waste.","sonnet":"A ritual invocation of vengeance — enemies burning, blood spilling on earth, disembowelment for the thousands of women burned as witches — pivots in the final verse to ecological rage: those who rape seas and rip down trees know the right words not to offend, but as your lungs burn 'I stay silent until your kingdom come', silence as the most devastating weapon.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~heroine-iconoclast-feat-danny-brown":{"haiku":"Destructive defiance marks the speaker's arrival, where breaking icons and going psycho become refusal of normalized limitation. Suicidal instincts translate to power through transgression.","opus":"Fame is treated as a spectacle of controlled self-destruction, the speaker breaking icons and going viral off her own suicidal instability while daring everyone to keep up. The refrain of being bored and needing rescue exposes the hollow at the center of the bravado, notoriety and breakdown feeding the same machine.","sonnet":"Two voices trade declarations of radical self-assertion — 'I break icons, heroine, when I break it goes viral' — the speaker performing both danger and boredom, demanding to be saved while scorning anyone who tries; the Danny Brown verse layers confrontational rap bravado over Zheani's framing of herself as beyond genre and beyond challenge.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~naked":{"haiku":"Raw defiance reclaims stolen territory; the speaker violates imposed boundaries and refuses shame for claiming what belongs to them.","opus":"Raw self-exposure about the shame and price of chasing fame, delivered from a particular hometown vantage. The narrator has arrived and despises it, stripped bare and faded like an old surname, watching another figure implode under unbearable knowing. Zheani's confessional intensity treats vulnerability and self-destruction as one, a forbidden box pried open by sheer will.","sonnet":"Stripping back fame's promises to find cold bones and echo: climbing Sparkes Hill to reclaim territory, painting over grafitti that says can't go this way, resenting the success that came at the cost of family name and deferred children. A second strand follows a man who opened Pandora's box and was carved up by his own hatred — his fate, not hers.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~napalm-explicit":{"haiku":"A sheltered, privileged person plays at rebellion and transgression without understanding real danger or consequence.","opus":"A scorched-earth takedown of a fake, sheltered rival who plays tough and dresses up but is really a coddled daddy's girl playing with forces she can't handle. The refrain of playing with napalm and doll parts casts her posturing as dangerous, self-destructive pretense.","sonnet":"A confrontation with a performative, sheltered woman who talks tough but is actually a daddy's girl playing pretend — the narrator, described as a fallen angel with blood on her face, calls the bluff and frames herself as the real destructive force.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~powder-tuff":{"haiku":"The speaker numbs pain through substances and aggression, caught between parental teaching and worldly trauma, oscillating between obedience and rebellion.","opus":"Zheani flips trauma into predatory aggression, a woman who was prey turning the language of abuse and drugs back on the world, the Cobain and daddy imagery marking damage she wields as armor rather than resolves.","sonnet":"A persona split between vulnerability (feeling preyed upon, numbing pain with xanax, childhood religious and domestic wounds) and aggressive hypersexual swagger, using explicit imagery of seducing and dominating women as armor over the softer trauma beneath.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~sex-never-dies":{"haiku":"Unapologetic celebration of persistent desire as fundamental truth, defiant against repression and shame.","opus":"A defiant reclaiming of the body as pagan power rather than sin, framing raw sexuality as an undying, unlying force. Zheani makes carnal appetite a form of self-possession, daring shame to touch what she refuses to apologize for.","sonnet":"A declaration of unashamed, unkillable sexual power — the narrator presents herself as a pagan idol, a vessel of desire that can't be extinguished or lied about. The song is about owning sexuality as a core, permanent identity rather than something shameful or temporary.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~skin-walker":{"haiku":"Exposure of deception and hollow manipulation; the singer confronts a person who wears masks, steals identity, and lacks true substance, burning away fakeness with fiery truth.","opus":"Zheani turns an occult predator figure into a vessel for rage against a manipulative, hollow woman and the fallout of a controlling scene. Beneath the arson imagery is a story about lost innocence and vowing to burn down what deceived her.","sonnet":"A song about burning out a fraudulent person — the 'skin walker' is someone hollow beneath the surface who steals and deceives; the narrator is the arsonist who exposes and destroys her, claiming her innocence was always going to be lost.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~spoils-of-war":{"haiku":"A heretic warrior pursues vengeance against religious corruption and false idols, bearing their damaged body as proof of resistance and demanding retribution.","opus":"A vengeful reworking of Judith and Holofernes, the narrator a seventeen-year-old following a star to behead a king, cast out with the dogs yet declaring herself perfectly made in God's image. Blasphemy, martyrdom, and rape-revenge fury braid together into a war she waged on those who defiled her, refusing to leave without her spoils.","sonnet":"A woman who has been declared guilty, heretical, and abandoned outside the city gates turns her abasement into a war oath — she has nothing left but hatred and spite for fuel, and she's coming for the king's head, framing herself as a visionary soldier sent by a dream at seventeen.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~tarantulas":{"haiku":"Speaker burns away lies and weakness through ritual fire, battling a deceptive partner who weaves webs of manipulation.","opus":"A woman tears at the webs of a manipulator who builds her up only to break her down, speaking truth without kindness, until she reforges herself in fire, baptised and vengeful, chanting to Kali for the strength to burn his lies. Zheani is an Australian artist working in a witchy, industrial hip-hop register, and this channels occult imagery into a survivor's exorcism of psychological abuse.","sonnet":"A woman invoking divine fury to free herself from someone who built her up then dismantled her with casual cruelty. The image is predator and prey reversed — she lures rather than flees, tears rather than spins. A kali-invoking war cry against manipulation, aimed at burning through lies and reclaiming the strength that her captor kept trying to name as weakness.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~we-re-all-going-to-die":{"haiku":"Death is the ultimate leveler and release; the song urges acceptance of mortality as illusion's end, offering spiritual dissolution as peace.","opus":"Death gets recast not as horror but as a return home, the fear of dying dismissed as a delusion and a shackle, while the narrator gently coaxes a frightened listener to let flesh return to bone and the spirit ascend, melting back into a maternal embrace. Zheani draws on Tibetan luminosity imagery, turning a mortality mantra into something strangely comforting rather than bleak.","sonnet":"A meditation on mortality reframed as collective relief — death not as terror but as homecoming. Fear is called an illusion, a chain around the neck; the right move is to release, dissolve, let the body become bones while the spirit rises. The tone is warm and communal, coaxing the listener toward acceptance with the promise that everyone arrives at the same place together.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~whore-of-babylon":{"haiku":"A biblical whore figure reclaims transgressive power through explicit sexuality and blasphemy, challenging moral authority. The song invokes drunken ecstasy in forbidden pleasure and rebellion.","opus":"Drawing on the biblical Whore of Babylon drunk on the blood of the saints, the speaker identifies with that fiery, defiant figure who would rather be hated than hold back the truth. She hurls scorn at a jaded rival while bursting into flame and drowning in the identity she's claimed.","sonnet":"The song adopts the voice of the Whore of Babylon from Revelation — arrayed in scarlet, drunk on the blood of saints — as a figure of transgressive, unapologetic power. The speaker challenges critics who try to diminish her, embracing the role of the outcast flame that burns brighter than those who condemn her.","src":"haiku"},
"zheani~you-saw":{"haiku":"Death, decomposition, and betrayal leave the speaker as food for worms, haunting others who failed to prevent their despair.","opus":"It tells of a girl driven to suicide by vicious lies and jealousy that stabbed her reputation apart; now dead and food for worms, she jumps into the bay, insisting things stay the same, and vows to haunt those whose cruelty keeps them up at night.","sonnet":"The song mourns a young woman destroyed by lies, jealousy, and social cruelty who chose death — the narrator describes her as flesh returning to soil, food for worms, and reflects on how no one could stop her once reputation was stabbed apart; guilt haunts the survivor who knows the memory of her is already slipping away.","src":"haiku"},
"zhu~in-the-morning":{"haiku":"Seduction and intoxication blend into compulsion; the speaker calls in isolation, needing another's presence to feel alive.","opus":"A seductive, secretive liaison conducted only in private, the singer calling only when alone and promising revelations only in the intimacy of morning. Desire is laced with something intoxicating and dangerous, urging the other to drink until they fall.","sonnet":"A nocturnal seduction built on distance and controlled intimacy: the speaker only calls when alone, inviting touch in the morning and planting something in the water — desire framed as spell or drug — until the listener falls.","src":"haiku"},
"zig-mentality~best-direction":{"haiku":"Possessions control people more than they free them; the speaker rejects materialism and perfectionism to simply exist authentically.","opus":"A rapid-fire attack on consumer culture, arguing that the things people own end up owning them, and that even the sharpest strivers chase approval only to be brushed off. Zig Mentality bury the message in tight internal rhymes, ending by rejecting both material need and companionship, paring life down to the simple pull of still being alive.","sonnet":"A rejection of accumulation and status-chasing in favor of bare presence — just wanting to feel the world turn. The lyric watches obsession with possessions trap its pursuers, watches rejection derail the entitled, and offers a counter-position: simplicity, honesty, and disconnection from the systems that claim to define worth.","src":"haiku"},
"zillion~chew":{"haiku":"Predatory consumption and abuse disguise themselves as love; the speaker realizes they are being destroyed and names the process despite powerlessness.","opus":"Being reduced to a possession and a distraction for someone who uses you to avoid their own reflection, chewed up and spit out while daring them to mean the love they claim.","sonnet":"About being treated as a project or plaything by someone who uses the speaker to avoid their own self-reflection — the abuser performs love while grinding the speaker down, and the speaker sees through the manipulation even while bleeding from it.","src":"haiku"},
"znous~wajhelkhata":{"haiku":"War-torn landscape buries dead beneath soil; survivors live and die anonymously beneath homeland's indifferent earth, erased by conflict.","opus":"Tunisian rap-rock elegy for people living and dying as strangers on their own soil, wrapped in flag and shroud; a howl about injustice past all limits and a nation reduced to resisting the very death it has become.","sonnet":"A Tunisian lament for those who live and die as strangers under their own country's soil — veins have dried, voices are hoarse to their last breath, injustice has exceeded grief and rage, and the dead and living alike are described as born on the wrong side of history, on \"wajhelkhata\" (the face of error/the wrong side). The nation is fire and its people the fuel.","src":"haiku"},
"zombie-girl~thorazine":{"haiku":"Mental illness and institutionalization; the speaker and subject are trapped by internal voices and the psychiatric system's judgment.","opus":"Electro-industrial act Zombie Girl wraps a cold, catchy hook around a narrator who studies a disturbed mind and concludes, with fake sweetness, that the sufferer would be better off gone. Named for an antipsychotic, the lyric jeers at the voices haunting its target and savors the prospect of their institutionalization, extending help only to yank it away, turning cruelty into something danceable.","sonnet":"A sardonic observer addresses someone whose inner world is so fractured — voices, a fixed smile, a mind decoupled from reality — that institutionalization is presented as the only remaining option. The mock-sympathy of the narrator sharpens the cruelty: help is offered while simultaneously declaring the person beyond saving.","src":"haiku"},
"zoume~the-sauce":{"haiku":"Self-made supremacy requires contempt for collective; the speaker celebrates isolation and individual power, dismissing others as parasites and inferiors.","opus":"A young rapper's defiant self-mythology — self-made, paranoid, contemptuous of hangers-on and doubters — where the aggression is really the sound of someone who grinded alone and refuses to hand anyone credit or trust.","sonnet":"Self-made defiance and contempt for those who doubt or leech — the speaker built their own luck, trusts no one, refuses to fold, and has the game by the throat. The vision is everything now, and anyone who \"talks but gave up\" is dismissed as fundamentally different.","src":"haiku"},
"zyrra-miller~to-hell-with-your-combi":{"haiku":"Domination and control calcify into a regime where obedience is currency and the ruled have no escape. The speaker asserts their possession of all desire while denying the ruled their own wants.","opus":"A menacing power-fantasy of control and hoarded resources, taunting an intruder who arrives with a noose already around his neck. The narrator commands obedience, denies escape, and dangles what the other desperately needs while refusing to share. It stages authoritarian menace as seduction, everything mine, everyone warned, and the kill already underway.","sonnet":"A predatory authority figure addresses a trespasser who has walked into danger — 'noose round your neck', 'obey don't think consume' — controlling everything the other person needs while making it clear they hold all the power and are now 'out on the kill'.","src":"haiku"},
"zz-top~gimme-all-your-lovin":{"haiku":"A person pursues physical and sexual satisfaction from their partner; the song celebrates sensual intensity and unrelenting desire.","opus":"Pure Texas boogie lust, an insatiable demand for physical love with no pretense of depth, the innuendo and swagger all in service of appetite that refuses to let up until it's satisfied.","sonnet":"A straightforward, enthusiastic demand for total sexual engagement — the narrator wants everything his partner has to give and won't stop until they're both spent.","src":"haiku"},
"zz-top~la-grange":{"haiku":"The song references a brothel outside La Grange, Texas, where men can find women and pleasure for a price and the entry fee.","opus":"Built on rumor of a legendary Texas brothel outside La Grange, the narrator winks at anyone wanting to go, noting the fine girls, the ten-dollar entry, and the nightly action with lecherous good humour. ZZ Top's boogie-blues grind and cackling ad-libs make this a template of Lone Star swagger, the whole thing a nudge and a leer aimed at that shack on the range.","sonnet":"Sparse rumour-swapping about a legendary roadhouse outside a Texas town — a place known for its women, its admission price, and its reliably busy nights. The report is secondhand, winking, and utterly unconcerned with anything beyond the fact of the place's existence and appeal.","src":"haiku"},
"zz-top~sharp-dressed-man":{"haiku":"Sharp clothing and accessories attract women, who are drawn to a well-dressed man's confident appearance and style.","opus":"ZZ Top parade a wardrobe of well-heeled swagger, a narrator decked out in fine clothes and jewelry who has no destination and needs no excuse. The claim is blunt: dress impeccably and women will chase you down. The Texas trio's bluesy confidence sells the fantasy that appearance alone is the whole seduction.","sonnet":"Sartorial confidence is presented as a complete philosophy — the right clothes, accessories, and presentation function as a total social strategy, attracting attention and desire without requiring any further explanation. Power and desirability are entirely surface, and the surface is treated as sufficient.","src":"haiku"},
"karel-kryl~hle-jak-se-perou":{"haiku":"Corruption and the casual morality of bribery in public officials.","sonnet":"corrupt officials and petty men who take bribes and brawl, while those in power profit from their degradation","opus":"corrupt officials who take bribes while drunkards brawl and drink themselves to ruin, a bitter satire of a rigged, oppressive society","src":"haiku"},
"the-hu~yuve-yuve-yu-acoustic":{"haiku":"Mongol people's alienation from ancestral wisdom and failure to unite.","sonnet":"a lament that modern Mongolians have betrayed their ancestors' legacy, forgotten Genghis Khan's greatness, and cannot unite their nation","opus":"the singer's disgust at modern Mongolians who boast of Genghis Khan's heritage yet betray their ancestors' values and fail to honor their homeland","src":"haiku"},
"calva-louise~alcanzar":{"haiku":"Spiritual disillusionment through ascetic renunciation and detachment from ambition.","sonnet":"the futility of chasing ambition and material things, and the liberation of letting go and moving forward without looking back","opus":"having lost everything one once had, and the disillusioning realization that ambition and possessions never satisfy, so survival means moving on without looking back","fable":"Having had everything and now owning nothing, the singer finds ambition ends in disillusion and chooses to survive on patience, never looking back.","src":"haiku"},
"k-lan-mikla~s-lst-ur":{"haiku":"Winter solstice and the supernatural call of darkness and northern lights.","sonnet":"the winter solstice and northern lights as a sacred, mystical space where the band dances as daughters of the night","opus":"witch-like women dancing beneath the northern lights on the winter solstice night in a stark Icelandic landscape","src":"haiku"},
"l-hi-botox~hyv-syy":{"haiku":"Petty crime and hopelessness in a life where robbery seems justified by desperation.","sonnet":"a broke, drug-addicted man on subutex who plans to rob a convenience store because he has nothing left to lose","opus":"a drug addict on Subutex who has squandered his money and, having nothing left to lose, rationalizes robbing a corner shop","src":"haiku"},
"atena~oljebarn-i-helligvann":{"haiku":"Privileged youth's drug excess masking deeper malaise and moral decay.","sonnet":"wealthy west-Oslo youth drowning in drugs, debt, and nihilism, burning through privilege with no way back","opus":"spoiled wealthy Oslo youth burning through daddy's money on champagne and drugs like speed and MDMA in nihilistic excess","src":"haiku"},
"rammstein~schwarzes-glas":{"haiku":"Shame and victimhood for being outcast or physically undesirable.","sonnet":"self-loathing over perceived ugliness and a wish for others to be blind so they cannot judge appearance","opus":"self-loathing and shame at one's own ugliness, confronting one's reflection in black glass and feeling condemned for appearance","src":"haiku"},
"pens-es-nocturnes~gnole-torgnoles-et-roubignoles":{"sonnet":"a misogynistic grotesque roast of an older woman, mixing crude insults with mock-tavern humour and slapstick violence","src":"sonnet"},
"korpiklaani~rise":{"haiku":"Indigenous spiritual resistance against colonial missionaries and foreign religion.","sonnet":"a defiant call for indigenous spirits of nature and war to rise up against Christian colonisers who came bearing bibles and laws","opus":"Christian holy warriors invading a pagan land of ice and snow, and the natives invoking their own spirits of fire, water and air against the invaders","fable":"Pagan northerners summoning their land, fire and war spirits to repel invading holy men who kill in God's name with bible and cross.","src":"haiku"},
"foscor~malson":{"haiku":"Nightmare state and monstrous transformation consuming consciousness.","sonnet":"a recurring nightmare — a dark, monster-like presence that haunts sleep and refuses to let go","opus":"a nightmare of melancholy and self-deception, a two-faced black-and-white monster haunting a dreamer's cruel, restless sleep","fable":"A recurring nightmare in which a black-and-white monster haunts the dreamer's melancholy sleep, turning shared dreaming into a cruel, horror-filled rest.","src":"haiku"},
"trzeci-wymiar~trzeci-wymiar":{"haiku":"Three-dimensional hip-hop wordplay and technical rap innovation and technique.","sonnet":"a Polish hip-hop crew's boastful manifesto about their superior three-dimensional lyrical technique and dedication to the craft","opus":"a Polish rap crew boasting about their trio's lyrical skill, wordplay and dedication to hip hop against greedy corporations","src":"haiku"},
"ant-nio-carlos-jobim~um-nome-de-mulher-a-woman-s-name-bonus-track":{"haiku":"A woman's name and its power to overwhelm and destabilize a man's life.","sonnet":"the overwhelming, helpless power of falling in love for the first time when a woman's name is enough to undo a man","opus":"a proud man undone and reduced to tears by love for a woman, discovering for the first time what it means to love","fable":"How first love undoes a proud man — the mere name of a woman reduces him to tears and steals his peace.","src":"haiku"},
"amenra~ogentroost":{"haiku":"Grief for a child lost to war or violence and broken family bonds.","sonnet":"a mother giving birth to a child she never sees again, and the broken speaker's grief and loss that follow","opus":"childbirth as sacrifice and loss, a mother giving birth to a child she will never see again, framed in imagery of a battlefield and 'it is finished'","src":"haiku"},
"altin-g-n~goca-d-nya":{"haiku":"The world as transient suffering and inevitable human sorrow within it.","sonnet":"a meditation on the transience of human life — the world as a caravanserai where all people pass through weeping, troubled or not","opus":"the transient, sorrowful nature of the vast world, a mill and a caravanserai where all people pass through weeping in grief","src":"haiku"},
"shining~mot-aokigahara":{"haiku":"Suicidal ideation and desire to return to Aokigahara forest for death.","sonnet":"suicidal despair and life-weariness so total the narrator feels they died — written as a pilgrimage toward Aokigahara, Japan's suicide forest","opus":"suicidal despair and weariness of life, journeying toward Japan's Aokigahara suicide forest, framed as being born in 1983 and dying in 2017","src":"haiku"},
"thy-catafalque~szarvas":{"haiku":"Life as autumn dream and slow decay toward inevitable dissolution.","sonnet":"a longing to flee a joyless, decaying reality — the narrator identifies with a fleeing stag and wishes the rain would carry them away","opus":"a hunted deer fleeing through a dream-forest, a metaphor for the singer's own withering life yearning for the rain to carry him away","fable":"Fleeing a magicless, decaying reality like a stag into a dreamlike fog-forest, wishing the rain would carry the singer away from life's slow wasting.","src":"haiku"},
"antiqva~anadem-gyre":{"haiku":"Forest spirits and mystical entities seducing and consuming consciousness at night.","sonnet":"the iele — Romanian fairy spirits of the forest — who bewitch and possess a person, stealing their sight, speech, and mind","opus":"the Romanian folklore Iele, seductive forest fairies dancing in the new moon who bewitch and take a man's sight, speech and mind","src":"haiku"},
"the-unsleeping~ptaha-fred":{"haiku":"A bird's thwarted dreams of flight and crushing urban isolation.","sonnet":"a dreamer named Fred (the bird) whose childhood wish to fly is crushed by the grinding reality of urban life","opus":"a bird named Fred who dreamed since childhood of flying but has his wings clipped, a metaphor for a person crushed by grinding, indifferent city life","src":"haiku"},
"rhapsody-of-fire~tempesta-di-fuoco":{"haiku":"Existential emptiness and emotional intensity burning through questions of meaning.","sonnet":"an inner tempest of passion and existential emptiness — the speaker is torn between madness and truth, fire and silence","opus":"an inner storm of madness and truth rising from an empty, betrayed past, burning through a rarefied present amid fire and tears","fable":"A soul emptied and betrayed by its dark past faces a future of unanswered questions as an inner firestorm of madness and truth consumes its grief.","src":"haiku"},
"lisa~rally-go-round":{"haiku":"A girl encouraging someone to take bold action and seize opportunity.","sonnet":"an energetic pep-talk urging someone to seize their chance and keep going despite setbacks, framed as a game or adventure","opus":"an energetic pep-talk urging the listener never to give up, treating life as a roulette-and-dice adventure game to be played at full speed with love","src":"haiku"},
"kneecap~cearta":{"haiku":"Drug dealing and evading police in Irish street culture and criminal underground.","sonnet":"Irish-language Belfast drug dealers partying, evading the RUC/police, and asserting their right (cearta) to do as they please","opus":"young Belfast men partying with drugs and evading the RUC police, defiantly asserting their rights and dealing coke, speed and pills","src":"haiku"},
"linkin-park~more-the-victim":{"haiku":"Someone weaponizes victimhood as identity, refusing accountability while demanding sympathy through exaggerated complaints and blame-shifting rather than action.","opus":"A B-side that turns outward instead of in: Bennington sketches someone who has made grievance a personality, hoarding wounds as currency and always needing to have suffered most. The sting is the confession folded in the middle, that the narrator once wallowed the same way and only grew out of it when the complaints ran dry.","sonnet":"The speaker confronts someone who constantly competes over suffering — cataloguing how this person exaggerates grievances, deflects blame, and clings to victimhood as an identity, while admitting they once did the same before running out of excuses."},
"type-o-negative~less-than-zero":{"haiku":"Profound self-disgust and dehumanization grounded in bodily corruption, questioning divinity while insisting all suffering is chosen and voluntary servitude.","opus":"Peter Steele plays the self-disgust for gallows comedy, cataloguing the body's basest fluids as the stuff he is made of and asking a silent God why release never comes. Under the goth theatrics sits a real theology of despair: the closing line reframes every sufferer as a willing recruit, the damned signing up for their own eternal penance.","sonnet":"A portrait of radical self-disgust — the speaker describes himself as built from bodily waste and filth, a hollow man of steel with nothing real inside, and frames those around him as willing participants in their own suffering."},
"chalk~skem":{"haiku":"Dissociation as survival strategy, the insistent refusal to process external blame through mental disconnection and learned avoidance.","opus":"The Belfast noise-rock trio compress a whole coping strategy into almost no words: absolving you of fault, then a single instruction repeated past the point of comfort. The mantra to shut the noise out becomes the noise itself, dissociation rendered as a loop that offers relief and traps you inside it at once.","sonnet":"A brief fragment of reassurance pitched inward — someone tells themselves (or another) that blame doesn't belong to them, and repeats the instruction to tune it out until that's all there is left to say."},
"chalk~ache":{"haiku":"Grief crystallized around a lost person—waking torment fused with spectral presence, desperate fragmentation seeking to reconstruct and return what was taken.","opus":"Grief staged as a daily ritual: waking to weep, pressing affection onto a skeleton, offering a dead person the same cheery dawn greeting as if routine could hold them here. The devastating turn is the craving to be more truthful — to smash the whole world down and reassemble it into something whole to hand to the one who is gone.","sonnet":"The speaker wakes repeatedly — sometimes at dawn, sometimes in the middle of the night — to grief over someone gone, kissing their absent bones and hearing voices, unable to relax or escape what's been taken, cycling through the morning greeting as though routine can hold the loss together."},
"serj-tankian~beethoven-s-cunt":{"haiku":"Devotion proves futile against inevitable abandonment; time destroys everything including civilization, faith, and connection itself.","opus":"Serj Tankian in his Elect the Dead-era absurdist-political mode, splicing a spurned lover's bitterness into apocalypse. As the mechanism of time itself splinters and civilization goes to trial, he swings between pleading to be believed and consigning the greedy heart-breaker to the alligator, wandering dead fields and screaming through spent lungs.","sonnet":"A love song set against civilizational collapse: the speaker moves through ruined landscapes and dead farms, screaming through dead lungs, all while holding onto someone whose faith in them is uncertain. Time itself is breaking apart, society is on trial, and the recurring question — do you believe in me — hangs unanswered as the speaker is ultimately abandoned and left alone."},
"a-2yw9ix~a-13pidzr":{"haiku":"Declarations of love mask infidelity and desire for another, exposing the conflict between stated devotion and restless emotional wandering.","sonnet":"A woman on a swaying late-night train confesses — to herself as much as anyone — that she knows her partner is unfaithful, that she wants something physical with someone else, and that she's too afraid of the morning-after to act; love, she concludes, is just sadness dressed up in declarations.","opus":"A shrieked declaration of total devotion sits on top of the ugliest domestic gossip: the neighbor's cheating, an itch for the band's guitarist, a last train sloshing through everyone's leftover dread. Goto's joke is that adoration and the wish to smash the whole arrangement to bits are the same feeling, which is why she keeps landing on how sad it is to love at all."},
"tool~disposition":{"haiku":"Minimal invocation of attention and cyclical observation, distilling engagement into pure ritualistic repetition.","sonnet":"A spare, meditative invitation — someone is asked to speak, to say anything at all, while the only response the world offers is weather shifting overhead; the song holds that open, unhurried space between a question and an answer that never quite arrives.","opus":"A Lateralus-era mantra pared to almost nothing: bring the thing up, then just watch conditions shift. Repeated to the edge of meaninglessness, it becomes a lesson in surrender, the same detached-observer stance the album keeps pressing, where naming what troubles you and then letting the weather do what weather does is the only available control.","fable":"Disposition is the penultimate movement of Lateralus's closing suite, and its lyric is almost entirely two imperatives set on repeat: a demand to be told something, anything, folded into the observation that conditions are already shifting. Read against the record's meditation on surrender and process, the near-total repetition is the point: the mind's grasping for information collapses into the patience of simply watching change happen, so the mantra dissolves striving into acceptance and preps the ego-death payoff of the track that follows. What looks like an unanswered plea is really the sound of letting go of the need for an answer.","fv":2,"themes":["transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"the grasping for information dissolving into the patience of watching change","built":"near-total repetition enacts the surrender it describes, mantra replacing argument"},
"a-2yw9ix~a-ff7sc1":{"haiku":"Running forward without turning back, abandoning future entirely to inhabit only the present moment and its unchanging light.","sonnet":"A group of people who've given up on the future declare their intention to run hard into a blazing, unchanged landscape — abandoning everything they've loved first, stripping themselves bare, choosing the burning present over any tomorrow.","opus":"A flat-out run at oblivion: keep the view exactly as it is and sprint past every horizon, no coming back. Goto declares she and her partner have no use for a future, and if that's the deal they should start by throwing away the things they loved first. It's nihilism worn as freedom, the dog let off the leash precisely because there's nowhere left to arrive."},
"a-2yw9ix~a-1jq1eta":{"haiku":"Constrained desperation masquerading as celebration, questioning whether existence itself holds any meaning beyond the dance.","sonnet":"A person teetering on the edge of collapse throws themselves into a chaotic, destructive dance — the Ezo deer dance — oscillating between confinement in a six-mat room and the dizzying freedom of bursting wide open, with a runaway express train of impulse that can't be stopped.","opus":"Destruction and release, despair and fiction, danced out in a six-mat room by someone shut in and one burst from snapping. The recurring Mister Express is a little bit crazy and can't actually stop himself, while Goto needles the platitude that death ends things by demanding to know what, exactly, ends. It's a panic attack styled as a garish party."},
"a-2yw9ix~a-31ak":{"haiku":"Two people born into an inescapable world, fleeing together toward a shared past while trapped in cyclical desire, the fastest escape never fast enough to outrun their own dissolution.","sonnet":"A pigeon takes flight carrying a bag, prayers, and aesthetics, departing toward a burning sea and away from the moon — two people who once shared a life along the Nankai Line and around Misaki Park flash through memory, and a desperate exchange about wanting to die surfaces before the refrain sweeps it back into motion.","opus":"A pigeon grabs a bag and bolts back toward yesterday's lover while the sea catches fire, and Goto threads it through Osaka's real geography, the glittering Shinsekai district, the Nankai line out to Misaki Park. The fastest creature alive is born only to be told, gently and then flatly, that it may not die. Nostalgia and a suicidal wish share the same bright postcard.","fable":"A day-trip organized as a cartoon: a pigeon flies off with a suitcase, turns its back on the moon, and chants let's-go-let's-go toward yesterday's version of you. The route is real and specific Osaka — the tacky lights of Shinsekai, the Nankai line, Misaki Park at the end of the tracks by the sea — which grounds the escapism as an actual couple's outing to the coast, the way you revisit a place because you once lived a life there. But Midori's method is to hide a knife in the sing-song, and the trip's cheer keeps cracking: the sea catches fire, being born feels like the moment you stop breathing, and near the end the girlish momentum stalls into a bracketed exchange set off like overheard speech — I want to die / you mustn't / it's no use now. Goto's whiplash yelp is the mask; the seaside excursion is the mask; underneath, the fastest creature on earth has just been born and already wants out, and the whole giddy itinerary reads as two people trying to outrun that by keeping the suitcase moving. The last verse simply loops the pigeon and the neon back around, cheer intact, nothing resolved — which is the point.","fv":2,"themes":["freedom & escape","suicide","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"a giddy seaside itinerary used to outrun an already-spoken wish to die","built":"the bracketed exchange set off like overheard speech hides the knife inside the sing-song"},
"massive-attack~pray-for-rain":{"haiku":"System failure and shattered clarity prompt collective spiritual seeking as vision walls dissolve into transformed seeing.","opus":"The Heligoland opener, sung by TV on the Radio's Tunde Adebimpe, watches a stunned crowd tilt their heads skyward after some collapse — a glitch in the collective mind, ash where a flock of birds once was. What begins as drought-prayer dissolves into trance: the barriers of perception giving way, ruin reframed as a threshold into vision.","sonnet":"A people hollowed out by some unnamed collapse crane their necks skyward and pray for rain; as the song builds, vision walls dissolve, drops strike rock, hearts and wings start beating, and the crowd is ushered from grief into an open, recurring dream.","fable":"A parched congregation tips its heads back toward a sky that has stopped answering, and the song watches faith get built out of scarcity: when the reservoirs run dry, worship is simply the last posture left. Tunde Adebimpe keeps the plea eerily serene rather than frantic, and that calm is the point — the drought has scorched away memory until the survivors can only make out the world through the heat warping it, mistaking ruin for revelation. Massive Attack's 3D-era production lets the trance harden into liturgy across the back half, the barriers between the believers and their want dissolving until deprivation passes for transcendence and the crowd surrenders, faces upturned, to a rescue that never quite arrives.","fv":2,"themes":["faith & the occult","nature & the elements","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"worship as the last posture left when memory has been scorched away","built":"trance hardens into liturgy until deprivation passes for transcendence"},
"massive-attack~five-man-army":{"haiku":"Hip-hop crew establish dominance through verbal prowess, positioning and girl-chasing as markers of status and authenticity.","opus":"A Blue Lines sound-system relay where the crew pass the mic like a baton, each MC boasting through Bristol clubland and Paddington backstreets over Horace Andy's floating hook. Beneath the toasting swagger and shout-outs runs a giddy little history of recorded sound, from Marconi to Sony — and a closing jab that money, the thing everyone chases, is the rot at the root.","sonnet":"A rotating cast of MCs — Tricky, Daddy G, and others — trade boasts and self-portraits over a sound-system riddim: scene-watching girls at warehouse parties, touring Tokyo, swapping the mic, and ending with a collective rebuke of gangsters in favour of eternal love and the shared miracle of wireless music."},
"massive-attack~be-thankful-for-what-you-ve-got":{"haiku":"Lack of material wealth—cars, luxury goods—need not diminish dignity when inner standing replaces external markers.","opus":"Their cover of William DeVaughn's 1974 soul sermon keeps the gospel intact: you might have no flashy car, no chrome and antennas, but you can still hold your head high. Over a hushed Bristol groove the luxuries recede into a dreamy background chant, leaving the plain moral out front — count the plainest blessing you have and stand tall on it.","sonnet":"In William DeVaughn's soul gospel, the narrator addresses brothers and sisters who may never own a gleaming Cadillac with diamonds in the back and a sunroof top, but urges them to stand tall and count themselves fortunate for whatever they do have."},
"massive-attack~one-love":{"haiku":"Romantic commitment to singular devotion offers security and permanence against the cultural pressure toward multiple romantic conquests.","opus":"A Blue Lines devotional carried by Horace Andy's aching falsetto, arguing for fidelity in a culture that prizes conquest. Against the men who need a partner on each arm and hedge their affections like scattered bets, the singer stakes everything on a single person — the risk of one broken basket set against the plain faith that she alone is worth it.","sonnet":"Horace Andy's vocal piece finds the singer rejecting the playboy logic of men who keep a woman on each arm, insisting that the woman he has found is faithful and irreplaceable — and that one love, held firm, is all he needs."},
"massive-attack~blue-lines":{"haiku":"Yearning for unreachable love creates isolation and emotional haze, distance sustained despite proximity and shared space.","opus":"The title track is a coming-of-age reverie from three Bristol kids of mixed English and Caribbean roots, drifting through spliff smoke, longing and creative restlessness while their friends stay comatose. 3D and Tricky trade dense wordplay about ambition, loneliness and the pull of a distant lover, rain streaking the window and washing the shadow off the soul — youth becoming something massive even as it treads water.","sonnet":"A man sits with a woman he keeps at emotional arm's length, too absorbed in music and longing for someone he cannot reach to hear what she says; around him, Tricky and 3D layer in their own fragments — loneliness, Caribbean-English identity, street life, and rain on a windowpane that washes away shadows without leaving a mark."},
"massive-attack~sly":{"haiku":"Cyclical consciousness and eternal wandering liberate the self from time's constraints and material territorial anchors.","opus":"A Protection-era meditation voiced by Nicolette, floating in a dislocated calm where reality has gone dizzy and time folds back on itself. The narrator claims to already know unborn faces and to grow younger with age, wandering toward a home everyone shares — a serene, almost mystical drift where music itself rings like remembered bells and the only certainty is that there is always more.","sonnet":"A voice adrift in time wanders through a strange, calm dislocation — already knowing the faces of grandchildren not yet born, hearing bells from a thousand years ago — and arrives, without explanation, at a feeling of being younger than before and at home in the whole world."},
"massive-attack~spying-glass":{"haiku":"Surveillance and judgment inescapably track the individual whether isolated in hills or anonymous within urban crowds.","opus":"A Protection cut built around Horace Andy's roots-reggae lament about surveillance and prejudice: whether the rasta stays in the city minding his own affairs or retreats to the hills for solitude, watchful eyes still brand him and pry into his business. The lens of the title becomes the whole point — nowhere is far enough to escape the neighbours who refuse to look away.","sonnet":"A Rastaman retreats first to the city and then to the country hills to escape wickedness and live quietly, but finds no refuge from surveillance: wherever he goes, people train a spying glass on him and make it their business to know his every move."},
"massive-attack~eurochild":{"haiku":"Urban decay and cultural pressure forge identity under constant suspicion, leaving alienated youth trapped between competing demands and scrutiny.","opus":"A Protection sprawl where 3D and Tricky map a dislocated European drift — daycare rooms lit neon-blue by the television, bars sensed as danger, an outsider begging to be judged by his labour rather than his references. Hell sits round every corner while the narrators self-medicate and shrink into daydream, a portrait of paranoid modern displacement where belonging always demands a papers-check you can never quite pass.","sonnet":"Tricky narrates the low-grade alienation of urban displacement — sitting in a deco-painted flat watching BBC static, walking into bars where he is treated as a stranger, needing references just to be accepted as a neighbour — while a second voice threads in fragments of intoxication, longing, and a sofa daydream."},
"linkin-park~burning-in-the-skies":{"haiku":"Self-destruction and deliberate bridge-burning, accepting blame alone without apology, losing what was never deserved.","sonnet":"Someone owns the destruction of a relationship they caused themselves: they fed the fire with deadwood, watched it consume everything, and now swim through the smoke of bridges they deliberately burned, accepting blame fully while losing things they acknowledge they never deserved.","opus":"Opening A Thousand Suns, the record that turned Linkin Park toward nuclear dread, this is confession dressed as apocalypse: a man who set his own bridges alight and now drifts through the fumes, refusing pity for a loss he decides he earned. The mushroom-cloud imagery is really private wreckage, self-destruction narrated as if it were the end of the world."},
"linkin-park~powerless":{"haiku":"Witnessing someone's unraveling collapse and corruption revealed, standing helpless as they surrender everything despite your presence.","sonnet":"A speaker watched someone they loved unravel — sharing their own vulnerabilities while the other hid theirs, then standing helplessly at their side as they fell apart, left with emptiness once it was over and the certainty that this loss changed who they became.","opus":"The closer on Living Things, and a study in helpless witness. The narrator watched someone he loved come apart, saw the wound bleed through, and could do nothing but stay beside the collapse counting broken vows. Its quiet ache is the flip side of the album's rage: not fighting, just standing there as it all slips, unable to catch any of it."},
"system-of-a-down~a-d-d-american-dream-denial":{"haiku":"Soldiers returned broken from endless wars fought for profit and power, their sacrifice erased by manipulation of truth and global greed.","sonnet":"Veterans and civilians address a ruling class that sent soldiers home in pieces, stole their agency through manipulated information, and hid state shame behind flags — culminating in a flat rejection of a world built on global profits and imperial power struggles at the expense of the American dream they were promised.","opus":"Opening Steal This Album!, this is SOAD's class-war fury aimed straight at empire: soldiers shipped home dismembered while profiteers hoard pearls, prisoners paraded in shame no banner can cover, minds rewired by switched channels. The dream of the title is denial itself, a lush promise sold to hide the machinery of war and the theft of will beneath it.","fable":"A soldier's-eye accounting of a nation that ships its young out whole and receives them back in pieces, the verses laying down a ledger where patriotic sacrifice is always paid and never repaid. The parenthetical subtitle reframes the founding promise itself as a swindle: what gets denied is not the fighting but the ideal the fighting was sold to defend, and the band stages that collapse by letting the phrase disintegrate across each repeat, so the country's guiding vision audibly loses its own words before it can finish saying them. Structurally the track alternates between the wounded testimony and a jeering, obscene secession from a world of corporate spoils and cheap luxury, the coarsening from one curse to a filthier one marking the instant grief calcifies into contempt. The spoken bridge diagnoses the machinery in flat clinical language, an unseen hand tampering with what the public is permitted to see and hear until agreement is manufactured, the shackled figure standing in for a disgrace no banner is broad enough to hide. Cut in the flag-drunk year after September 2001 and exiled from the major records onto Steal This Album!, it is protest as depletion rather than rally, its closing chant stuttering into a broken loop that will not resolve because, for the singer, nothing has.","fv":2,"themes":["politics & society","war & battle","surveillance & control"],"means":"patriotic sacrifice as a debt the nation pays and never repays","built":"the subtitle phrase disintegrates across each repeat, audibly losing its own words"},
"haru-nemuri~yumewomiyou":{"haiku":"Summer heat scalds into eternal bitterness, yet someone still dreams of shared skies.","sonnet":"The speaker is a survivor who was too sheltered and self-absorbed to notice a close friend's suffering — and by the time the twilight came, that friend had died alone in the early hours of an ordinary day. The song moves through guilt, the image of a broken heaven that the speaker let someone else carry, and arrives at a vow: to keep singing so that no one like that friend will die, to dream in the middle of a damaged world not as escape or illusion but as the only honest act. The final plea is not for mutual understanding but for sharing the same dream anyway — the speaker's loneliness and the dead friend's solitude don't need to resolve, only to coexist in the same waking.","opus":"Framed around a friend who died alone before dawn on an unremarkable day, the song is Nemuri's reckoning with the guilt of a protected summer, the survivor who did not know loneliness even on the night of the great earthquake. The self-lacerating image of showing off shallow razor cuts for the sake of 140 characters skewers performed pain against a real death, and the vow to keep singing so someone like that friend won't die becomes the song's reason for existing. Its plea to keep dreaming together in the middle of a festering, stupid world reframes art as a refusal to let the dead be forgotten, right down to the scent of osmanthus she asks not to lose."},
"haru-nemuri~a-15fz8ee":{"haiku":"Love and rage fanfare through marrow—two souls offering everything for one heartbeat together.","sonnet":"A fanfare of love and anger is the song's central image — a sound that should ring out through the soil of life itself, the kind of signal that cannot be played correctly, only played from inside whatever feeling is real. The speaker describes themselves as badly assembled, a clumsy summer-project construction with the soft parts sealed away, letting a kind of transparent blood run out when the false composure is peeled back. The thread holding everything together is another person whose gentleness — specifically their willingness to cry before the speaker does, and their own fragility — is the reason the speaker has not yet died.","opus":"The title fanfare is one of love and anger sounded together, and Nemuri means them as a single note rather than opposites, a blast meant to resonate through the soil of life itself. The tender engine underneath is a mutual staying-alive: she can't die yet because someone gentle weeps for her first, a debt of kindness that keeps postponing the end. Against that she sets a savage self-critique of playing at naiveté, of gluing shut the soft parts like a clumsy summer craft project, insisting that pain doesn't affirm her, it only confirms she exists, so the fanfare has to be sung in someone else's raw emotion to mean anything at all."},
"chalk~afraid":{"fable":"Almost the entire lyric is a single phrase being disassembled in real time - the wish to be afraid stripped to I wanna be, then to bare I want, the object of desire falling away until only appetite is left pounding on itself. The read is that erosion as structure: the Belfast trio uses the electronic build to shred a coming-of-age plea about being yourself into pure repetitive drive, so the closing confession of not being able to contain oneself arrives as both the song's meaning and the inevitable end of its own machine.","fv":2,"themes":["objectless craving","identity & becoming"],"means":"a coming-of-age plea shredded to pure appetite by its own machinery","built":"the lyric disassembles itself in real time, the object of desire falling away until only the drive remains"},
"dream-theater~war-inside-my-head": {"haiku":"A soldier battles the psychological aftermath of combat, haunted by violence and the internal scars of warfare.","sonnet":"A combat veteran returns from war haunted by the psychological wreckage of what he witnessed and did — hallucinations, paranoia, and voices that won't stop. The battle has moved from the battlefield into his own mind, where it continues without end.","opus":"A soldier's account of the psychological wreckage left by combat, where the horror of napalm and shrapnel in a tropical warzone hardens into permanent trauma. Back home he hears voices and sees shadows in daylight, the real battlefield now the paranoid one raging inside his own mind.","src":"haiku","fable":"A soldier recounts a campaign of jellied fire and orders stripped to a single verb, then comes home to find the fighting has relocated behind his eyes. Voices carry across empty distance and strangers seem to read his thoughts, so that every word he speaks now tastes like a lit fuse — the enemy reduced to a chemical ghost lodged permanently in the skull.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","war & battle"],"means":"the battlefield relocated permanently behind the eyes","built":"external combat compressed into a chemical ghost in the skull"},
"dream-theater~goodnight-kiss": {"haiku":"An obsessive narrator reflects on separation from a child they love, struggling with torment and isolation.","sonnet":"A father tenderly watches over his young daughter at bedtime, but the tenderness is shadowed by a dark undercurrent — five years on, his blood remains tainted and a consuming rage at the doctors who took the girl's mother away has never left him. The song holds both the delicacy of a parent's love and a grief that has curdled into something threatening, leaving both of them stranded and afraid in the world without her.","opus":"A grieving, unhinged narrator addresses a young girl at bedtime, the tender ritual curdling into something far darker as it emerges he is a damaged man, five years marked by tainted blood and bent on revenge against the doctors he blames — his voice unsettlingly merging with the frightened child he claims to comfort.","src":"haiku","fable":"A lavender-scented bedtime hush is voiced from the wrong side of a loss: the tenderness of a mother leaning over a sleeping child curdles as it becomes clear the child is not sleeping but gone, the lullaby addressed to someone death carried off, and she would trade her own life for a single returned moment. She counts half a decade since a hospital wronged her, her sickened blood unchanged, and promises the physicians who ruined her will answer for it - the endearments and the vow of revenge issuing from the same shattered mother.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Portnoy's movement, and the consensus reading settles an old flag in this library: the narrator is a mother lost to postpartum grief, not the father earlier tiers guessed at.","themes":["death & grief","anger & defiance","love & desire"],"means":"a lullaby addressed to a child death carried off","built":"endearment and revenge vow issuing from the same breath"},
"dream-theater~in-the-presence-of-enemies-part-i": {"haiku":"A prophet offers false salvation to the broken and rejected, seducing them with promises of power while enslaving their devotion through manufactured hope.","opus":"A broken, damned man is tempted by a dark power promising redemption and revenge in exchange for worship—a Faustian bargain framed as salvation. The suite opens a battle between the fallen and one man rising to stand against them for humanity's sake.","sonnet":"A spiritually vulnerable man, broken and feeling forsaken by God, is approached by a dark entity offering power, revenge, and eternal life in exchange for worship — he wrestles between his old faith and the temptation of this shadowy savior who promises what God apparently withheld.","src":"haiku","fable":"Staged as a deathbed temptation, the movement puts a broken man at the edge of oblivion, the radiance ahead mistaken for salvation until the voice pledging rescue unmasks itself as the tempter. The drama runs on how slippery the offer is: the phrasing belongs to a redeemer, but the terms betray the deceiver, so the man's ache at being scorned and torn from his beloved is the exact bruise the liar keeps pressing. It then widens from one soul to open war, the fallen one's foot-soldiers clearing a road for their false messiah, before a lone figure plants himself against the tide and the piece ends not on seduction but on the first defiance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","war & battle","anger & defiance"],"means":"seduction phrased as rescue until the terms betray it","built":"the piece stages two registers — redeemer's diction, deceiver's terms — then ends on defiance rather than surrender"},
"dream-theater~in-the-presence-of-enemies-part-ii": {"haiku":"Confrontation with nemesis and ultimate reckoning; the enemy's presence forces final resolution and transformation.","opus":"A dark-fantasy narrative of a holy man seduced into serving a demonic master, the loyalty oaths curdling into surrender. It borrows scripture's shepherd imagery to stage a soul's corruption and the beast lurking in everyone.","sonnet":"A pilgrim who has surrendered to a dark master — pledging his soul, vowing to kill and die — gradually recognises the presence consuming him from within is not a saviour but a possessing force that has turned him into a servant of violence. The final reversal, \"my soul is my own now, I do not fight for you,\" arrives after the psalm-like cadences of surrender, making the reclamation feel hard-won rather than given.","src":"haiku","fable":"This is a possession narrative told from inside the surrender, then reversed. A cult welcomes the protagonist as its long-awaited champion, and he takes the bait, pledging himself to an unnamed tyrant that lives as a spirit crouched behind another man's stare. Dream Theater then twists that oath into blasphemy by feeding a prison-house liturgy through it, so language meant for comfort and protection reads instead like a butcher's vow. The turn arrives when the borrowed devotion curdles: the hero feels conviction burn out, survives the fear he had dreaded most, and reclaims himself in one flat refusal that hands the whole ritual back empty. Its power is that the villain wins the entire march and loses only the last inch of a man who decides he owns his allegiance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The two-part suite adapts the Korean manhwa Priest — the weary preacher and his dark master come from it.","themes":["faith & the occult","identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"the villain wins the whole march and loses only the last inch","built":"a sacred liturgy fed through a cult oath so comfort reads as butcher's vow"},
"tesseract~concealing-fate-part-one-acceptance-radar-o-s-t":{"fable":"The opening command to bare open palms works as a courtroom demand for confession, and TesseracT builds the song as a trial that collapses inward: the accuser cross-examining over evidence and defense gives way to the realization that prosecutor and defendant are one self, spinning language against itself in a single skull. The djent structure enacts this—mechanical, revolving repetition of being alone stalls in place while the argument goes nowhere. Tompkins’ shift from clipped accusation to open, sustained melody marks the moment the fight for the last word dissolves. What remains after litigating a relationship is the quiet verdict that everything wanted was already present, so the fate being concealed is that the case was never winnable, always argued against a reflection.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","heartbreak & loss","madness & the mind"],"means":"litigating a relationship until the mirror confesses","built":"the djent's mechanical revolving stall enacts the argument going nowhere"},
"tesseract~concealing-fate-part-i-acceptance":{"fable":"The opening command to bare open palms works as a courtroom demand for confession, and TesseracT builds the song as a trial that collapses inward: the accuser cross-examining over evidence and defense gives way to the realization that prosecutor and defendant are one self, spinning language against itself in a single skull. The djent structure enacts this—mechanical, revolving repetition of being alone stalls in place while the argument goes nowhere. Tompkins’ shift from clipped accusation to open, sustained melody marks the moment the fight for the last word dissolves. What remains after litigating a relationship is the quiet verdict that everything wanted was already present, so the fate being concealed is that the case was never winnable, always argued against a reflection.","fv":2,"themes":["identity & becoming","heartbreak & loss","madness & the mind"],"means":"litigating a relationship until the mirror confesses","built":"the djent's mechanical revolving stall enacts the argument going nowhere"},
"tesseract~origin-concealing-fate-pt-6":{"fable":"Closing suite of the Concealing Fate cycle, and the title Origin lands as the wheel completing its turn: a break-up reframed as the death of a whole world. The relationship becomes a final chapter pressed into a reader-lover’s hands, everything already spent, no choices left to withhold. The read hinges on the scars-and-wounds image-system, where the speaker deliberately keeps the injury open, then watches it seal against his will, so the healing itself is the loss, memory scarring over into forgetting. In the last turn the you and the I collapse, and the curse aimed at the other about a venture that should have stayed unstarted rebounds as the light dies. The round-and-around refrain traps the cycle in a loop, so the close is not resolution but the song swallowing its own first note.","fv":2,"fnote":"The suite ends by replaying its own opening: the chosen-last-book and closing-scars passage from Acceptance returns verbatim in Origin — six movements out, the wheel lands where it started.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"healing coded as loss, memory scarring over into forgetting","built":"the round-and-around refrain traps closure inside the opening, swallowing its own first note"},
"number-girl~brutal-man":{"fable":"The chanted title-refrain, repeated past all reason, is a mask the song wears precisely so it can show what leaks out from under it. The persona is assembled entirely from rock apparatus — six strings of crazed steel, the sync between booth and mic, the secret of R-O-C-K spelled out letter by letter — a man who knows no state secrets but weaponizes stagecraft to inflame you, treating this street as if it were a battlefield. Yet the brutality keeps failing to hold: the more times he insists in English that he's a brutal man, the more the assertion reads as something he needs to keep saying because it isn't true. The tell arrives in the Japanese, where the armor cracks twice — a sarcastic shrug at the idea of saying I love you, and to whom exactly — and then the confession that undoes the whole pose: that rotten, worthless sentimentality soaks into him every single time. That is the real subject. The endless brutal-man mantra isn't a boast but a bulwark, chanted louder and louder against a tenderness the singer can't stop feeling, a lone traveler racing from L to R who has armored himself in three letters of ROCK exactly because sentiment keeps getting through.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"a macho mantra chanted against unstoppable tenderness","built":"the English boast repeated past belief until it inverts"},
"number-girl~brutal-number-girl":{"fable":"A band self-portrait as survival tactic. The threat is abstract and total — a frozen city launching its attack — but the response is deliberately unheroic: they parry it by drinking, then muck about at Hirao Station at six in the morning. The dating to 1995 and the vow to trust their own strength turn the swagger into a founding creed, and the closing move seals it: ignore the order handed down, because we came up from the south and we're doing just fine. The brutality of the title isn't violence but refusal — the decision to meet a machine-cold world with local, human, faintly drunk defiance rather than obedience.","fv":2,"fnote":"The frozen city under attack here — 冷凍都市 — became Mukai’s signature coinage: it recurs across his later band Zazen Boys, most famously as the refrain of Jimon Jitou, and the press treats it as his career-long shorthand for the capital. This opener is an early sighting.","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"meeting machine-cold hostility with local drunk refusal","built":"a founding date nailing swagger to a specific year"},
"number-girl~sappukei":{"fable":"The title track states the album's thesis and then refuses to keep it external. It opens as an order about self-location — without awareness of where you presently stand, the outcome is easy to predict — and immediately hallucinates it: a tengu — the figure Japanese hands to swollen pride — lifting off a city high-rise, glimpsed in Shinjuku. Everything hinges on the paired shout that splits the album's own word in two: scenery that kills, scenery that gives life — the same landscape flipped depending on who is standing in it. Slumped on an Osaka bridge, he names himself a worthless solitary-ist, and the ending pulls the desolation all the way inward: A and B can't reach each other, there's no exit from your pain, no one will defend me — how long does this war with my own self-regard go on? Sappukei turns out to name not a bleak world but a man who can't fix his position in it, and the fight is with his pride, not the view.","fv":2,"themes":["shame & repression","alienation & emptiness","identity & becoming"],"means":"a man whose war is with his own ungovernable pride","built":"the title word split by a shout into its two opposite halves"},
"number-girl~trampoline-girl":{"fable":"A song of watching, from the ground, someone else achieve flight — and reading your own paralysis in the gap. The narrator stands in the red-light district's churn and sees a girl launch upward, her leap likened to pollen carried over tall midsummer grass, an image that recurs against the noise of the crowd that keeps swallowing him. The song's engine is a running contrast between two verbs: she flies (tobu) while he merely stands or stays put, and the cruelty is that her flight isn't clean — she wavers, she gets hurt, and then she flies anyway, at exactly the moment midnight goes mad and starts to laugh. He, meanwhile, claims to be 100% over it, totally cleared, still hunting for the source of some glitter glimpsed through a gap — and the boast rings as hollow as the calm he pretends to. The read turns explicitly competitive near the end: he frames her leaping as a total victory, hers, and asks whether he is, after all, going to lose to her dignity — which he already has. The last line refuses catharsis and just checks the clock: half past midnight. She flew; he stood there watching, and the song is honest enough to end on the timestamp of his defeat rather than pretend he ever got off the ground.","fv":2,"fnote":"The lyric writes her flight with two different kanji — 飛ぶ, plain jumping, and 翔ぶ, the literary soar — and alternates them as she goes; the distinction vanishes in romanization, but on the page she upgrades verbs mid-song.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","freedom & escape","shame & repression"],"means":"paralysis exposed by watching someone else achieve flight","built":"the timestamp of defeat replacing any cathartic ending"},
"number-girl~u-rei":{"fable":"The narrator sees a ghost, a death-god, at dusk — but the title is a pun: yuurei (specter) and ureu (to brood) are the same haunting, so the reaper stalking this reddening Shinjuku skyline is only his own melancholy wearing a costume. He throws the window open onto the government tower, the light comes back red and spreads, and he files himself under the yuugure-zoku — eighties slang that named older men circling younger company at dusk — half-mocking the sense that he's aged into a type. The real ache is class and geography dressed as mood: others hunt frantically for a new place to belong in Tokyo while he claims he'd be fine if only Shonben-yokocho, the piss-alley drinking warren, were rebuilt — then he catches the lie, tagging his own words as forced cheer, his toughness the flimsy kind you manufacture only after moving to the capital. Mukai holds the pose knowingly, brooding with a manful face, the reddest thing on the street. Then two teenagers lean together, easy and used to each other, and deflate him completely: real ease is theirs, and the ghost is just the melancholy that washes over him, now and then, at exactly this hour.","fv":2,"fnote":"The alley he wants rebuilt had just burned: a November 1999 fire destroyed a third of Shinjuku’s Shonben Yokocho — Piss Alley — eight months before SAPPUKEI, and the lyric’s word for its hoped-for revival is the same one the news used for the reconstruction. The wish is topical, not nostalgic.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","nostalgia & memory","identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"a man brooding on class and dislocation costumed as a ghost","built":"the title punning specter and melancholy into the same word"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~dreamman":{"fable":"Narasaki scores this as a spaghetti-western fable, and the read lives in the gap between the storybook Japanese verses and the English chant that swallows them. The narrator rides six miles into a wasteland to fetch a legendary caped hero to save his beleaguered village, but the hero’s first move is to produce a discount coupon and haggle over his fee — the mythic rescuer as gig worker, already deflated before the guns start. The song’s real engine is the flip: the same figure whose arrival the chant celebrates turns, in the second half, into a man with fire in his eyes and a machine gun who cannot tell friend from foe, and the celebration curdles into the identical cadence announcing ruin. That symmetry is the thesis — the savior and the mass shooter are one arriving body, salvation and slaughter sharing a single hook — so the closing swap of a plea for guidance into a plea for rescue reads as hero-worship with no way to stop itself once the shooting starts.","fv":2,"themes":["violence & murder","identity & becoming","war & battle"],"means":"savior and shooter arriving on the same hook","built":"the celebration cadence replayed verbatim for the ruin"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~haru-no-gyouninzaka":{"fable":"A patient love song addressed to someone frozen in a crevice of time — unable to move forward, unwilling to fall back, just living there with a grief they carry. The speaker diagnoses gently: you're always searching for something, but the answer you want isn't lying there to be found. Against that stuckness the song offers not a fix but a stance — a place where no one has to be hurt, an offer to take the other there, and the vow that matters most: if your legs freeze, I'll simply wait, I'll be here the whole time. It closes by turning waiting into a small active gift, a picture of hope drawn and handed over so the person might take one step into tomorrow — devotion measured not as rescue but as the willingness to stay put beside someone who can't yet move.","fv":2,"fnote":"Gyōninzaka is a real slope in Meguro, Tokyo — pilgrim’s slope, origin of the great 1772 fire — and the band kept coming back to it: this spring visit belongs to a seasonal series with autumn and winter Gyouninzaka songs. The stuck-between-states address has a physical incline under it.","themes":["love & desire","death & grief"],"means":"devotion measured as the willingness to stay still","built":"waiting reframed as a small drawn picture of hope"},
"coaltar-of-the-deepers~the-end-of-summer":{"fable":"The Japanese verses hold a scene of two people floating in a sea of blood, hands clasped, praying with closed eyes that this beautiful world still has a tomorrow. The title’s flat English refrain, sung as pure texture over NARASAKI’s dissolving shoegaze haze, is doing something quietly brutal: the end of summer is not a season passing but the couple’s death, the missed autumn leaves and the trip they meant to take this year rewritten as a life that ran out mid-plan. What saves it from despair is the pivot in the last Japanese couplet, where the stillness and soft light wrap the two and lead them toward the future. The band lets annihilation and consolation occupy the same wash of sound, so the endlessly repeated English phrase becomes less a report of loss than a lullaby carrying them across.","fv":2,"fnote":"The image is a shinjuu — the lovers’ double suicide Japanese drama has staged since Chikamatsu’s Sonezaki play of 1703 — and the song borrows the form’s tenderness toward its dead: stillness and soft light lead the pair onward rather than recoiling.","themes":["death & grief","love & desire","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"annihilation and consolation sharing the same wash of sound","built":"the flat English refrain doing the work of an obituary"},
"zazen-boys~chie-chan-s-landscape":{"fable":"A nocturne framed as a warped landscape painting seen at 3 AM, where the distortion of the air and its color is offered not as horror but, pointedly, as something lovely — the song's thesis is that the deformed and the ugly are where beauty actually lives for a mind on a vagrant inner journey. Chie-chan arrives clacking her geta into a black-and-white town, and the frame fills with a bestiary of the in-between: a creepily grinning talking cat, hostile radio-beams from a UFO, defeated stragglers writhing on cursed ground, a drunk eating udon whose muttered words stab like a blade. The image-system is the point — every figure is half-formed, developing, fake-or-real, a ceramic-town menagerie that can't resolve into either menace or comfort. Against all this the one steady human note is Chie herself: her broad grin read as strong will, a trembling red dot of a mouth, a person who simply walks into the surreal murk and belongs there. So the landscape isn't decoded, it's inhabited — the song reads the refusal of the 3 AM mind to sort the beautiful from the grotesque as its own kind of peace.","fv":2,"fnote":"Every prop is from Jarinko Chie’s shelf — a Chie-chan arriving in clattering geta, the Osaka downtown in black and white, a talking cat like the manga’s Kotetsu, the udon-eating ossan. No source connects the song to Haruki Etsumi’s manga; the furniture does.","themes":["night & the city","transcendence & consciousness","madness & the mind"],"means":"inhabiting the surreal rather than decoding it as peace","built":"the 3 AM image-system refuses to resolve grotesque from beautiful"},
"zazen-boys~zazen-bo":{"fable":"The title promises seated Zen stillness, but Mukai jams that against a city that never closes: the window of society is stuck wide open, and what should be meditative concentration has instead hardened into a clod of mud packed tight by sheer obsessive thought-force. From NUMBER GIRL's guitar assault into ZAZEN BOYS' twitchier funk, this is the same nerve rerouted — the frozen metropolis as a place where daily life is roughly, unremarkably insane, and the eyes just keep receiving its scenery. The chant lands its own verdict in the last line: living as prey of the city, we endlessly leak our own pretentious sermonizing — the zazen posture curdled into a drone that can't stop talking, self-included.","fv":2,"fnote":"The bracketing couplet is Mukai’s counted signature — impermanence-and-resurgent-desire, appearing across NUMBER GIRL and ZAZEN BOYS some forty-six times since Eight Beater — and the impermanence half is the Heike Monogatari’s opening bell, the oldest Japanese line about everything passing.","themes":["madness & the mind","night & the city","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"meditative posture curdled into a drone that cannot stop preaching","built":"Zen stillness jammed against a city that never closes, never resolves"},
 "ling-tosite-sigure~who-s-whofo": {"fable":"A love song staged as first contact — the arrival of a UFO, an extraterrestrial mirage, an out-of-consciousness lifeform whose pleasure devours the singer — where meeting someone rewrites a belief held deeper than the ocean: that happiness is a knife. He had leaned his whole life on the presumption that closeness would wound him, though he'd never actually been stabbed, and the encounter exposes that dread as a phantom he can finally undress. The physics of the second half make the point — wings sprout in the brain, gravity drops away, and rather than reach some destination he tells the earth to stop so the two can cross the aurora together toward a miracle just out of reach. The unresolved final question — the true identity of this thrill is who? — keeps the beloved as unclassifiable as the UFO, which is exactly the reading: real love arriving with the same shock as something not of this world.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","transcendence & consciousness","shame & repression"],"means":"meeting someone exposes a lifelong dread of closeness as a wound never actually received","built":"the beloved kept deliberately unclassifiable — the UFO circled but never identified"},
 "ling-tosite-sigure~a-3uc60s": {"fable":"The title transliterates alexithymia — the clinical inability to name one's own feelings — and the song is a panic attack staged inside that condition: are there emotions here, is there a voice in the heart, the brain drowning in anonymity as an infinite zero. TK builds the whole thing as a war between substitution and singularity. The dread is that anyone could replace me, my complexion a dried-out trace, a cyborg checking its own coordinates — set against the counter-cry that no one can replace me, that this free-will force leaves no substitute. The give-it-away / give-it-back refrain works the emotions like contraband handed off and demanded back, so that becoming myself and emptying myself run on the same verb. What's underneath the numbness is not absence but overflow — things I want to show someone flooding the head — so the alexithymia is revealed as a jammed signal, not a dead one, only the truth that shows is the invisible kind. It ends stripped to the barest question asked twice, whether he was able to love himself, the cyborg's self-diagnostic that no external truth can answer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","dissociation & numbness","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"numbness revealed as overflow — the signal jammed, not absent","built":"the title transliterates alexithymia, naming the condition before the song enacts it"},
 "slayer~dissident-aggressor-judas-priest-cover": {"fable":"Halford's 1977 lyric does something audacious: it hands the microphone to a place rather than a person, letting the divided city speak in the first person as a mind that has been split down its own middle and wired for agony. The barrier it confronts and the fishhooks lodged behind its eyes are the same injury described twice — a partition felt as neurological torture, identity announced flatly as a proper noun because there is nothing else left to be. Motion here is all outward violence with no door out, an errand that recycles rather than resolves, so the closing refrain lands as a loop the speaker can never step clear of. Slayer's 1988 version keeps the Cold War terror literal and sheds the original's theatrical swagger, letting the confession sit as pure claustrophobia.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","madness & the mind","violence & murder"],"means":"a partitioned city narrates its own split as neurological torture with no exit","built":"Slayer's version sheds the original's theatrical swagger, leaving the confession as pure claustrophobia"},
 "dream-theater~scene-eight-the-spirit-carries-o": {"fable":"The narrator turns the oldest metaphysical questions into a deathbed testimony, moving from a child's terror of oblivion toward a hard-won certainty that consciousness survives the grave and asks to be remembered rather than mourned at the headstone. Structurally it is a bereaved protagonist's regression-vision resolving: the murdered figure named Victoria stops being a haunting riddle and becomes present, so the interrogation of mortality collapses into acceptance the instant the vision confirms itself. Dream Theater build the piece as gospel-swelling ballad rather than progressive showpiece, John Petrucci trading virtuosic complication for plain first-person testimony so that the widening from doubt to conviction carries the arc without a single unanswerable question ever actually being answered.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The gospel swell is real — Theresa Thomason with a full choir; hers is also the female voice that opens Through Her Eyes.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","death & grief"],"means":"widening from childhood terror of oblivion toward hard-won certainty of survival","built":"gospel ballad restraint substituted for prog showpiece so conviction carries without answers"},
 "death-grips~spread-eagle-cross-the-block": {"fable":"A statement of intent built on a deliberate slippage: the thing MC Ride craves, hoards, and refuses to sell is at once a drug and the music itself, the two vocabularies fused so completely that appetite and creation become one addiction. What reads as a bragging dealer's monologue — mine, all mine, you can't buy it — is really a manifesto about ownership of an artistic voice that answers to no market, the private thing that turns outlandish until you have it. Powered by a Link Wray sample and Zach Hill's demolition-derby drums, the track weaponizes hip-hop's possession-and-hustle grammar into something closer to a curse, with the verses about beveling a blade and bleeding the struggle reframing craft as violence done to the beat. The recurring image of the artist violating the music, making it scream and beg, insists that Death Grips' relationship to sound is not performance but domination, ecstatic and abusive at once. It is an origin myth by refusal: not here is who I am, but here is the thing I will never let you have.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The strut underneath is Link Wray's Rumble, chopped beside Beastie Boys fragments.","themes":["identity & becoming","addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"possession-and-hustle grammar weaponized into a refusal of any market","built":"drug vocabulary and artistic manifesto fused into indistinguishable addiction"},
 "death-grips~lord-of-the-game": {"fable":"The song crowns itself a warlord of sheer momentum, MC Ride stacking boasts of dominion until the swagger detonates into war reportage, drums marching to torch the slums and devour whatever flinches first. That escalation carries the meaning: the higher the empire climbs, the more the bravado curdles, so the same voice that ruled by decree soon hears knuckles on the door and debates whether to abandon dry ground for whatever waits beyond it. What opened as untouchable command closes as a cornered man counting dwindling options with a blade pressed to his spine, unable to sort dread from genuine threat, the throne exposed as a trap he talked himself onto.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","anger & defiance"],"means":"untouchable command exposed as a trap the voice talked itself onto","built":"the escalating boast curdles until the throne corners its own occupant"},
 "death-grips~takyon-death-yon": {"fable":"The track stages violence as a bodily high, collapsing the distance between making a beat and detonating something inside a listener's skull until aggression and euphoria stop reading as separable states. Every verse translates production itself into assault — necks snapped, brains split open, energy siphoned off the whole map — so the boast is that the sound physically overwrites you rather than merely impressing you. That fusion of ecstasy and annihilation, pitched as an addict's craving he assumes you already share, is the whole point: the drug being pushed here is the song's own hostility.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"aggression and euphoria pitched as states you cannot separate","built":"production itself translated into bodily assault verse by verse"},
 "crystal-castles~ornament": {"fable":"A single instruction looped over instrumental haze, promising that acceptance brings departure, so the track works as a lullaby of dispossession: the more you take, the emptier you are left. The repetition sours the offer into a warning, each pass draining the phrase of comfort until the gift and the loss become the same gesture.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness"],"means":"acceptance offered as the mechanism of dispossession","built":"single instruction looped until gift and loss become the same gesture"},
 "babymetal~babymetal-death": {"fable":"The track is an initiation rite that installs the group as its own creed, spelling the name aloud and then calling each singer forward in turn like recruits sworn into a rank. Its single hammered word is drained of menace and reforged into a chant of belonging, so the dread it names becomes a badge the trio wears rather than any fate it fears.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The chant lands on the ear as a pure death-metal death-growl, but it is the group naming itself: with the Japanese copula desu voiced with its final u dropped as the language does, we are BABYMETAL is phonetically indistinguishable from the death chant — the whole rite a bilingual pun.","themes":["identity & becoming","friendship & loyalty","faith & the occult"],"means":"dread syllable reforged into a badge of belonging","built":"spelling the name aloud installs the group as its own creed"},
 "celldweller~the-best-its-gonna-get": {"fable":"A jeering confrontation with someone whose curated life has cracked open, delivered in the voice of a tormentor who could be an external critic or the subject's own contempt turned inward. Klayton stacks the verses on a taunting adverb ladder — aimlessly, patiently, then irony, painfully, mournfully — that tracks the target from stalled hope to the too-late verdict, so the structure itself does the punishing: the second pass is the first pass after the deadline has already blown. The refrain lands as false consolation, a shrug dressed as encouragement, because the promise that this is as good as it gets is really the withdrawal of any promise at all. Underneath the industrial swagger and the sneer at fame, money and beauty is the ugliest read: the mask is coaching, but what it's actually saying is that the wasted life is finished, and the only glory left to reap is the humiliation of having stood for nothing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","artifice & performance"],"means":"false consolation offered as the withdrawal of any real promise","built":"adverb ladder aimlessly-to-mournfully runs the target from stalled hope to deadline blown"},
 "a-perfect-circle~brena": {"fable":"An interlude built almost entirely as prayer, Breña addresses a healing feminine presence the way a supplicant addresses a saint or a mother — the name repeated like a devotional refrain until it stops being a word and becomes a rite of submission. The one movement it makes is a lowering of guard: the speaker admits to being vulnerable and, in the same breath, insists it's all right, so the surrender to the reflection that pulls him under is reframed as safety rather than drowning. In the context of a record steeped in Maynard James Keenan's grief and Catholic residue, this reads as the album's soft center — the moment it drops its irony to beg, plainly, to be washed clean and drawn closer, until even the plea dissolves into the single verb heal.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"reframing surrender to a healing presence as safety not drowning","built":"the name repeated as devotional refrain until it stops being a word"},
 "a-perfect-circle~over": {"fable":"A closing piece built almost entirely from a single phrase, worn smoother with every repetition until the words stop meaning finished and start meaning exhausted. The looping is the whole argument: someone has rehearsed this ending so many times that the declaration of being done has become its own trap, circling the exit without ever reaching it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss"],"means":"rehearsing an ending so many times the exit becomes its own trap","built":"a single phrase worn smooth until done shifts into exhausted"},
 "a-perfect-circle~crimes": {"fable":"A recovery step counted out loud, the voice climbing rung by rung until it snags on nine and can't move past it, hammering that number four times before finally forcing itself to ten. The repetition is the whole ordeal: the near-final beat of the ninth step is where the accounting of harm done stalls, and reaching completion is less an arrival than an act of will dragged out of a stuck man.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"reaching the ninth step only by hammering it four times first","built":"the stall on nine counted aloud before the will forces it to ten"},
 "a-perfect-circle~lullaby": {"fable":"A whispered coaxing back into sleep, repeated until the comfort curdles into something coercive. In under a minute it works as a hushed hinge late in an album about addiction and denial, where being soothed and being silenced start to sound like the same thing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The whisper plants the same go-to-sleep voice that Pet's guardian speaks in full, the album's sedation motif in miniature. It arrives quiet and small, a seed of the coercion the record keeps returning to.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","surveillance & control"],"means":"comfort and silencing made indistinguishable by repetition","built":"a hushed hinge where being soothed and being denied sound identical"},
 "a-perfect-circle~lets-have-a-war": {"fable":"Fear's hardcore-punk taunt from 1982, and the meaning lives in what A Perfect Circle do to it on eMOTIVe, their 2004 covers album timed to Election Day and the Iraq war. Lee Ving's original snarled the state's ugliest arithmetic in the first person — war as population control, as market stimulus, as space-clearing — and turned it into a mosh-pit provocation. Maynard James Keenan strips the punk velocity out and lets the same lines float over dread and drone, so the sarcasm stops being a joke and becomes the actual policy voice you suspect is running things. The recasting weaponizes the crowd math: the endless there-are-so-many-of-us count, once punk bravado, now reads as the disposable surplus the war rhetoric is quietly costing out. What was a sneer becomes an indictment simply by being sung slowly and meant.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society"],"means":"letting the state's own arithmetic indict itself by being sung slowly and meant","built":"punk velocity removed so the sarcasm stops being a joke and becomes audible policy"},
 "a-perfect-circle~whats-going-on": {"fable":"Marvin Gaye's 1971 plea against Vietnam and police violence, its melody built as a warm crosstown conversation among mothers, brothers, and fathers who all want the same de-escalation. The meaning of A Perfect Circle's version lives entirely in the selection and the recasting: for eMOTIVe, an all-covers protest record timed to the 2004 election, Maynard James Keenan drains Gaye's soulful pleading down to something cold and dread-laden, so the song's central question stops sounding like an appeal for understanding and starts sounding like a numb accusation. Where Gaye trusted that talking could still bring some loving in, the arrangement here treats that hope as already exhausted — the same words, four decades later, aimed at a listener who was supposed to have learned.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","war & battle"],"means":"draining Gaye's appeal for understanding into a numb accusation four decades on","built":"cold arrangement treats the hope that talking still helps as already exhausted"},
 "karnivool~reanimation-feat-guthrie-govan": {"fable":"This is a song about grief that quits bargaining and confesses it wanted the wrong thing the whole time. Its chorus petitions for a sliver more time, sixty seconds to rewind the wreckage and undo it, addressed to a familiar gaze that will not surface again. What lands hardest is the pivot from pleading to consent: the ending insists the ruin was never escapable, and the crowd-numbed solitude settles into a calm nearer surrender than repair. Karnivool stack the arc on hypnotic repetition, so the swing from begging to that flat final steadiness reads as earned exhaustion rather than any comfort.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"grief pivots from pleading for time to consenting to the ruin","built":"hypnotic repetition earns the swing from begging to flat final steadiness"},
 "karnivool~salva": {"fable":"Leaving is framed here as the only mercy left, a departure the singer forces on himself even as every warning post along the road asks what he thinks he is doing. He means to carry the heart down to the water and give it to the brine to eat clean, staging the end as a rinse rather than a wound, though the middle undercuts that calm with trembling fingers, a stranger surfacing behind his own eyes, and a lover whose words leave welts. Karnivool have always been drawn to the physics of release, and the trick is how the imagery pivots from drowning to breathing: the same tide that erases him is the one that finally lets the lungs fill. That closing chant of distance still ahead functions like an engine turning over, momentum standing in for a hope the words refuse to actually guarantee. What lands is not resolution but the deliberately unfinished bargain of trading a lethal present for a road whose better ending is only promised, never confirmed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","heartbreak & loss","suicide"],"means":"departure staged as the only mercy, drowning reframed as breathing","built":"the closing chant of distance ahead functions as momentum standing in for a promised hope"},
 "karnivool~remote-self-control": {"fable":"It reframes anesthesia and dissociation as a technology you willingly hand yourself over to, a switch flipped so that numbness registers as freedom rather than loss. The speaker plays reassuring guide and complicit tempter at once, coaxing a frightened listener to float free of their own nerves for the strange pleasure of feeling nothing. What began as escape curdles into a summons back: blood in the mouth and air in the lungs insist the abandoned body still wants to scream itself awake.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","surveillance & control","freedom & escape"],"means":"numbness offered as freedom before the body screams itself awake","built":"the speaker plays guide and tempter at once, coaxing from within the very detachment he sells"},
 "karnivool~conversations": {"fable":"This is a self-indictment staged as theatre: the singer retreats to the dark edge of the stage, hoping the spotlight passes him by while an inner accuser tallies every place he came up short. That voice keeps reciting the better man he might have become, the rescue he could have offered, and he answers only that the debt is a boulder he cannot budge. The track hardens into that verdict, the word for unmovable repeated until argument dies, then collapses into a work-crew chant of turning the same soil over and over, hauling dead weight with no ground actually broken.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"self-indictment staged as theatre, the singer hoping the spotlight passes","built":"the closing collapses into a work-crew chant of hauling dead weight with no ground broken"},
"deftones~cxz": {"fable":"A single memory keeps fissioning into a paired ledger, where each half is tallied against its opposite twin — one raised while the other sinks, one vanished while the other holds its place — so the song is less a scene than a splitting engine that can only ever count in twos. Around that division a refrain of nullified numbers hollows out a gap in the flow of hours, and the recurrence of old events warps the sequence until one twin runs true to the clock and its double drifts loose from it entirely. Chino Moreno has always sung the body and its fracture over Deftones' shoegaze-metal density, and here the rupture is temporal rather than physical: the mind cleaving in two and watching both copies fall out of sync with each other and with the running of time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"a mind cleaving in two and watching both copies fall out of sync","built":"a refrain of nullified numbers hollows a gap in the flow of hours the song never fills"},
"deftones~metal-dream": {"fable":"A courtship staged entirely as descent: the couple dress in finery only to plunge past the edge of the real, chasing a peace that never existed anywhere solid, and every verb pulls them down rather than forward. What makes it eerie is how the song refuses to say whether they are falling asleep, drowning, or being buried, folding a lined coffin, a woodland floor, and the peal of a funeral into one continuous embrace. The chorus locks that ambiguity into a still image — two bodies arranged side by side, gilded and motionless, promised a shining future in the same breath that the word for it slides from together into forever. Deftones have long fused tenderness with dread, and here Chino Moreno lets the romance win by surrendering to the sinking rather than fighting it, the closing repetitions dissolving into pure held sound.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"romance won by surrendering to the sinking rather than resisting it","built":"the word for their shared future slides from together into forever without the song naming what that means"},
"deftones~departing-the-body": {"fable":"A couple hangs in a cold, glassed chamber lit by one small ceiling source, watching time itself thin as their blurred figures sway and something granular keeps sifting away around them. The song reads less as dying than as a shared crossing: enchantment, craving, a new phase arriving with morning, movement beginning to change, and an elated countdown of racing toward a threshold beyond the visible. Its structure enacts the passage by circling back almost word for word to that opening chamber, the frame nearly intact except the pair no longer floats in space but ends there, the clear turned clean, so the breakthrough registers as an accomplished return rather than a clean escape.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","love & desire"],"means":"a shared crossing staged as elated countdown toward a threshold beyond the visible","built":"the structure returns almost word for word to the opening chamber, the frame nearly intact yet the pair no longer floating"},
"dream-theater~breaking-all-illusions": {"fable":"A long meditation on hard-won clarity, built as a slow reversal that treats adversity as a forge: it insists the marks left by pain and by battles fought without company are what temper a person rather than diminish them. The middle stops narrating and turns doctrinal, redefining strength as the grasp of what is true instead of the exercise of force, then the imagery pivots from a flare of heat to a closed loop that walks outward and inward at once. That loop is the whole argument — it returns to its own starting point but now able to register the sights and sounds it had passed by unseeing, so the payoff is not arrival somewhere new but the same ground read differently. Growth here works by subtraction: an unlearning that lets clarity surface as things are stripped away, closing on patience as the slow condition under which any of it finally lands.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The doctrinal middle is borrowed scripture — the attitude passage and the strongest-souls lines are both credited to Kahlil Gibran, woven in nearly verbatim by Myung and Petrucci.","themes":["identity & becoming","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"clarity surfacing as layers are stripped rather than added","built":"the loop returns to its start but now reads it differently"},
"health~victim": {"haiku":"A song about the loss of connection after a defining moment, where the speaker recognizes that things can never return to how they felt before and that love alone is insufficient to restore what's been broken.","sonnet":"The song holds two irreconcilable knowings in suspension: that the feeling is already gone and can never return, and that the speaker still chooses each morning to pretend otherwise. Love is named not as salvation but as the very thing proven insufficient — present, offered, and still not enough to close the gap between what was and what remains.","opus":"HEALTH turn the end of a love into a slow, clear-eyed surrender, admitting that today's feeling can never be recovered and won't return. The song's cruelty is in its honesty: the narrator keeps waking to a lie he tells himself, even as he already knows the affection on offer will never be enough to close the distance. It's less a breakup than a confession that love itself has quietly failed the test.","src":"lrclib","fable":"A love song sung as an autopsy: the title names the speaker's role before a word arrives, and the whole thing works to explain how someone becomes one — not through cruelty but through the slow admission that a feeling has already passed and won't return. HEALTH stage this as attrition, the programmed percussion and distortion of Death Magic grinding under Duzsik's untroubled melodic voice, so the music enacts the erosion the words describe while the delivery refuses to grieve it. The read hinges on the morning: the speaker wakes and knowingly lies to himself, which makes the closing verdict — that a love, freely given, still isn't enough — less an accusation than a confession, the victim indicting his own inability to be saved by it. The repetition of that verdict isn't emphasis but resignation setting; each pass drains a little more conviction out of protest until it flattens into fact.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","love & desire","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"love freely given still cannot redeem the giver","built":"the title pre-names the speaker before the lyric begins"},
"health~men-today": {"haiku":"The song describes entropy and decay—how the world breaks down and corrodes everything that exists, reducing all things to dirt and dust. It conveys a sense of inexorable erosion where both people and their former selves are owned and consumed by this destructive natural process.","sonnet":"The song holds masculinity in a double bind: the dirt that defines men — labour, history, identity — is the same force consuming them, so ownership and dispossession are a single condition. To be made of something and to be claimed by it leaves no exit, only the slow drying-out of a self that was never fully its own.","opus":"HEALTH turn erosion into a creed here, reframing decay not as something that happens to us but as ownership shifting hands: the earth reclaiming its lease on everything we thought was ours. What we were is figured as already spent, drained by a world that outlasts us and folds our substance back into the ground. It is a bleak, almost devotional surrender to entropy, the noise-pop sheen making the resignation sound triumphant rather than mourned.","src":"lrclib","fable":"A mortality chant reduced to almost nothing: the soil is the landlord and every living thing is behind on rent, whatever a person once was collapsing back into the ground that was always going to reclaim it. The single figure of moisture being pulled from a body until it cracks does the work of the whole lyric — decay filmed in fast-forward — and HEALTH refuse to resolve it, looping the opening claim back on itself so the verse eats its own tail. That circularity is the point on Death Magic, an album that sets Jake Duzsik's near-lullaby melody against percussion programmed like industrial machinery: the sweetness of the voice never earns you an exit, it just narrates the earth closing over you one more time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","nature & the elements"],"means":"the earth reclaims what it lent without ceremony","built":"the verse loops back on itself so the lyric enacts the decay"},
"health~hurt-yourself": {"haiku":"A song about the cycle of unfulfilled desire and emotional attachment, where wanting something or someone you can't have becomes a pattern you learn to sustain through self-inflicted pain. The lyric suggests that chasing unavailable connections and holding onto what's missing becomes a familiar wound you keep opening.","sonnet":"The song is built on a loop of desire that feeds on its own denial — the more you fixate on someone who won't want you back, the more that absence becomes the thing you've learned to need. Longing here isn't a wound that heals but a habit sustained by its own ache, until loving the hurt is indistinguishable from loving the person.","opus":"HEALTH turn a love song into a diagnosis of compulsion, where the chase is aimed at whoever happens to be nearby and the wanting matters more than the person. The track circles the idea that we cling hardest to what we lack, each new attachment arriving already scored like a fresh cut, tenderness and self-harm folded into the same gesture. Its bleakest turn is the admission that longing for someone else is really just a learned habit of turning the hurt inward.","src":"lrclib","fable":"A song about wanting to be wanted, and how that craving curdles into masochism when it goes unmet. The opening verse tangles its own grammar — double negatives, pronouns that slip from you to they to someone else — so that longing and refusal keep collapsing into each other, the sound of a mind arguing itself in circles over a person who was never really there to grasp. The middle image is the quiet hinge: intimacy imagined as moisture held briefly in the body, evaporating on its own before anyone decides to end it, so that even the losses here read less as betrayals than as natural decay. By the close, that whole confused circuit resolves into one cold equation — the reflex of chasing what withholds becomes indistinguishable from the reflex of self-injury, tenderness rehearsed until it points inward. Duzsik delivers all of it in that unbothered, almost sweet register HEALTH built Death Magic around, the calm melody set against programmed brutality so the final cruelty arrives sounding like a lullaby rather than a verdict.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"the reflex of chasing what withholds becomes indistinguishable from self-injury","built":"slipping grammar enacts the mind arguing itself in circles"},
"health~drugs-exist": {"haiku":"A meditation on mortality and the difficulty of finding meaning in the face of death. The song suggests acceptance of both death's inevitability and the fragility of love and connection, with an appeal to live and love deliberately despite life's uncertainty.","sonnet":"The song holds two imperatives in permanent tension: live and love however you can, yet nothing — not prayer, not grief, not the people who loved you — offers any rescue from the fact that everyone dies and the dead stay dead. What makes it ache is that this nihilism doesn't cancel the instruction to love; it doubles down on it, demanding tenderness from people who have already worried themselves numb, extending it even toward those who never loved back.","opus":"HEALTH stare down mortality with a flat, exhausted tenderness here, insisting that since everyone ends up in the ground the only sane response is to care for the people around you — even the ones who never returned the feeling. The chorus keeps circling back to that plea for compassion as the one thing worth clinging to, precisely because any certainty about what's good or right keeps dissolving under them. What's left is a numbed, bloodless calm that treats affection less as salvation than as the last available gesture before the dead quietly call everyone back home.","src":"lrclib","fable":"The lyric works through a triptych of imperatives — each stanza opens with permission (be slack, live freely) and pivots to a devotional command the speaker cannot fully mean. The turn is in the final substitution: the plea shifts from cherishing the people who cared for us to extending that same tenderness toward those who withhold it, which reframes the whole song from grief-comfort into a harder ethical dare. HEALTH deliver this on Death Magic as pop consolation with the ground pulled out — Duzsik's frictionless croon floats over programmed brutality, so the reassurance that mortality comes for everyone reads less as solace than as a flat, medicated fact, and the middle stanza's bloodless, half-anesthetized calm (no wound, no haunting, only the dead summoning us back) exposes the sweetness as a coping mechanism rather than a belief.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","love & desire","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"grief-comfort reframed as a harder dare toward those who withhold care","built":"the final stanza substitutes strangers for the dead, shifting register from elegy to ethics"},
"health~of-being-born": {"haiku":"A meditation on bodily decay and mortality, asking repeatedly how much time remains as the body wears down. The song contemplates the physical deterioration that comes with existence.","sonnet":"The song holds a body under collective observation — we see it, we measure it — yet the only question that surfaces is how much time remains inside the self rather than what that time is for. Existence here is not birth but erosion, the paradox being that awareness of life and awareness of its limit arrive as the same sensation, indistinguishable and equally still.","opus":"A blunt, repeating interrogation of mortality set to HEALTH's pulverizing industrial dread — the song stares at the body as a countdown, asking how much time remains before it goes still. The phrase circles back on itself like a fixation that can't be answered, dissolving the distance between waking and wearing down until living and dying feel like the same slow erosion.","src":"lrclib","fable":"A body takes its own inventory and finds only wear and a countdown, the same question — how much time is left — asked until asking is all that remains. HEALTH build the whole song from that refrain on purpose: the repetition is the reading, a mind circling a fact it cannot resolve while the flesh goes still as stone around it. The parenthetical title closes the trap, framing birth itself as the terminal condition, the moment the clock started running down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The bracket completes ASHAMED’s refrain — ashamed, of being born; the band has said the interlude was Frankensteined out of that song.","themes":["death & grief","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"repetition itself enacts a mind circling what it cannot resolve","built":"the parenthetical title frames birth as the moment the countdown began"},
"health~don-t-kill-yourself": {"haiku":"The song addresses suicidal ideation and self-directed anger in the face of unrelenting suffering—nights without sleep, crushing grief, repeating mistakes, and disgust with one's own body. It articulates the paradox of not wanting to die while finding life unbearable.","sonnet":"The song holds two contradictory truths in one breath — a person who doesn't want to die but finds the terms of living unbearable, caught between the instinct to survive and the inability to change. All blame, all hatred, all grief loops back inward, making the self both the wound and the only one left to answer for it.","opus":"HEALTH stare down the flat middle of despair, where the wish to die and the refusal to keep living this way sit side by side as the same exhausted stalemate. The narrator turns every accusation inward until there's no one left to blame or hate but the self, the sleepless nights filled only with a face they can't stop seeing. It's less a suicide note than an honest bulletin from inside a body someone has learned to hate, unable to change and unwilling to end it, just enduring the repeat.","src":"lrclib","fable":"A depression song that refuses every consolation on offer, including its own title. The verses close each door in turn — no peace, no sleep, no one left to blame or hate but the self — until the chorus lands on the flat, unwinnable arithmetic HEALTH keep returning to: not wanting to die and not wanting to keep living like this are the same sentence, and neither cancels the other. The title reads at first as an outstretched hand, but the address turns inward and accusatory in the refrain about hating your own body, so the plea and the self-loathing become one voice; Duzsik's gentle delivery over the machinery leaves it unresolved on purpose, ending in the same day-after-day loop it started in rather than any lift toward hope.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title is the band’s own long-running merch mantra — printed on HEALTH shirts for years alongside YOU WILL LOVE EACH OTHER — the slogan finally written into a song.","themes":["suicide","shame & repression","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"not wanting to die and not wanting to live stated as identical and unresolvable","built":"the title's outstretched hand turns inward and accusatory in the refrain"},
"nine-inch-nails~home": {"fable":"The lyric works through a POV that only clarifies at its close. The speaker wakes disoriented, sensing an ending and losing his grip on the boundary between the actual and the counterfeit — until the refrain reveals the second party. What reads at first as a strained romance is really addiction, or a self he cannot shed: the place he keeps escaping and helplessly circling back to is the only ground where he feels he belongs, and the thing he strains to cut loose from is lodged permanently within him. Home is not shelter here but the captor — the withdrawal-and-relapse loop named as the one belonging left, fitting for a With Teeth record built around Reznor's own sobriety.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"naming the relapse loop as the only home left","built":"the POV twist held until the refrain cracks the song's subject open"},
"machine-head~hole-in-the-sky-black-sabbath-cover": {"fable":"Sabbath's original is a downward escape dressed as an upward one: the singer looks through a gap in the sky toward heaven while everything he describes on the ground — a viewless room, disappearing stars, the dogs of war at their feast — reads as a world he has already given up on, so the flight through that window lands ambiguously between transcendence and dying. Machine Head, deep in the confusion of the Supercharger era, doesn't reinterpret the words so much as choose them: a groove-metal band picking Ozzy-era doom's most fatalistic apocalypse-as-liftoff and hammering it flat and heavy, using the cover to borrow a lineage's exhaustion when their own footing had gone unstable. The meaning is in that act of selection — a band under pressure reaching back for a song about wanting out.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","death & grief","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"reaching backward toward a forefather's exhaustion to name your own","built":"cover choice as the argument — selection replaces reinterpretation entirely"},
"babymetal~future-metal": {"fable":"A spoken-word threshold piece: an in-flight announcement that straps the listener in for a voyage across a cosmos of the band's own naming, complete with the gag of buckling a brace around your neck before the headbanging snaps it loose. Beneath the cabin-crew politeness is a manifesto — a jab at the six-string old guard, and a claim that whatever purists count as the genuine article has already been superseded here. As the overture to the trio's most stylistically omnivorous record, it works less as a song than as a ceremonial gate: a mission-control voice announcing departure from convention into a territory BABYMETAL insists it invented and now governs.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance"],"means":"genre ownership claimed through the ritual of a departure announcement","built":"cabin-crew politeness as the delivery vehicle for a manifesto against the old guard"},
"romes~dissolver": {"fable":"A burnout song that keeps sliding from career sabotage into something that sounds a lot more like wanting to stop existing, and refuses to tell you which one it means. The verses swagger — kill the track, spit on the mainstream, do it for myself, the classic punk gesture of not caring who hears — but the register underneath is a body decaying on pavement, rotting toward obsolete, asking to be wiped clean like a file. That deliberate blur between erasing the recording and erasing the singer is the whole point: on an album titled SONIC TRASH, the fantasy of being deleted like a bad take becomes indistinguishable from the fantasy of dissolving into nothing. The final stripping-down — the current cut, the warmth gone cold, the body left to oxidize like scrap in a junkyard, then out and done — drops the performance entirely and leaves only the darker reading standing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"erasing the recording as erasing the self","built":"the stripped-down outro drops performance and leaves only the darker reading"},
"strapping-young-lad~spirituality": {"fable":"Devin Townsend closes City's mechanized aggression with the one thing the album has been starving for: a naked cry upward. The speaker is stranded and unanswered, begging for any sign that the addressed presence — God, a lover, anyone — is even alive to hear, and the prayers keep going out nightly into a sky that never talks back. That silence curdles the title's promise: what passes for spirituality here is not communion but the humiliation of a man crowned over an empty kingdom, someone who can never become what he reaches for. So the final movement flips the plea into a snarl at the diseased and thick-skulled crowd surrounding him — but the venom reads as the collapse of a man who prayed and got no reply, turning outward because the upward channel is dead. The mask is misanthropy; underneath is unbearable loneliness.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance"],"means":"misanthropy as the collapse of unanswered prayer","built":"closing snarl reframed by the silence it follows"},
"strapping-young-lad~velvet-kevorkian": {"fable":"The album's brief overture is less a song than a fuse: a sleepless man in suburban Burnaby, wired past 2 a.m., talking himself out of his old life and into some undefined glory before the record even properly begins. It moves in three lurches — the private panic of insomnia and dead-end jobs, then an outward turn where that panic curdles into contempt for everyone else's wars, religion, and sedated ignorance, then a self-appointed prophet's promise that an era is barreling in and the sleepers will wake. The genius is that Townsend never resolves whether this is liberation or breakdown; the messiah swagger of the closing taunt is indistinguishable from a mind coming apart at the seams, and City spends the next hour paying that ambiguity off.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","identity & becoming","anger & defiance"],"means":"prophet-swagger indistinguishable from a mind coming apart","built":"three-lurch structure keeps liberation and breakdown in the same frame"},
"strapping-young-lad~all-hail-the-new-flesh": {"fable":"A snarling manifesto of self-preservation as scorched earth: the speaker chooses his own survival over everyone he's leaving to rot, and dares anyone to claim he owes them otherwise. The title echoes the transcendence-through-mutation cry of Cronenberg's Videodrome, and Townsend turns it into a creed — the new flesh isn't horror here, it's a self remade to fit him and nobody else. What sounds like triumph curdles the moment you notice how much contempt it takes to feel this free; the pity he offers the world is the tell, a superiority that needs an audience of the damned to mean anything. That the whole thing crescendos on wanting it all, twice, exposes the appetite the bravado was dressing up — City's signature move of overload standing in for a grandiosity that can't ever be filled.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"freedom that needs an audience of the damned to mean anything","built":"crescendo on wanting it all twice exposes the appetite behind the bravado"},
"strapping-young-lad~oh-my-fucking-god": {"fable":"A fin-de-siecle manifesto that inflates itself to bursting: end-of-century consciousness rebranded as a clarity too lucid for madness, where all matter has become artwork and each thing strains to communicate, sexuality dissolves into abstraction, and brand names sit beside cosmic pronouncements as if a burger and a shoe were the same order of revelation as time becoming an object. Devin Townsend lets the grandiosity crest into open blasphemy — the title's oh-my-god flips a vowel-turn into I'm god, a will to dream reality into being — and this is the mania of City talking, the manic-productive persona that mistakes overload for enlightenment. But the song is built to puncture that, and it deflates twice: first when all that glory turns ugly and simple, the wonder of an assembled cosmos reduced to the drudgery of hauling gear into a venue, and then catastrophically at the end, when the self-appointed deity's only remaining demand is to be reunited with his mother, the same infantile cry repeated until the divinity mask falls off entirely. The agnostic seeking a somewhere that will dream alongside him, a home that runs on the fuel of his own habits, is the tell — this is someone who wanted transcendence and got only need. The song lives between those two families, the inflated godhead and the whimpering child, and the closing line lands ambiguous: whether the change coming is breakthrough or breakdown, the song refuses to say.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","madness & the mind","faith & the occult"],"means":"grandiosity deflated twice into infantile need","built":"title's vowel-turn from oh-my-god to I'm-god enacts the mania it later punctures"},
"trash-boat~live-like-a-king-die-like-an-animal": {"fable":"The whole lyric is a single chanted promise — you can have it all, live at the top — looped until it hollows out, and every line sits inside parentheses, marked as backing-vocal echo rather than anything the singer will claim in his own voice. That framing is the point: the aspiration arrives secondhand, an ad slogan the song lets ring without ever cosigning, so the title's second half — the animal death waiting under the royal life — never has to be spoken to be felt. On a record built around the sarcasm of asking whether you feel amazing, this is the seduction rendered as pure jingle, and its refusal to develop past the one line is the point: the dream of having everything only knows how to repeat itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","party & hedonism"],"means":"aspiration rendered as pure jingle that never has to admit its second half","built":"every line set inside parentheses so the singer never has to cosign the dream"},
"trash-boat~silence-is-golden": {"fable":"Trash Boat build the song on a split voice that keeps swapping meaning underneath the same words. The verses speak as the many — an us worn down by a war against an unreachable enemy, redirecting rage sideways to whatever stranger walks past, sold a two-channel feed that lets in only yes or no until unity looks unprofitable and collective silence stops meaning anything. But the chorus and bridge hand the mic to the other side: a first-person power seated on its throne, dragging its grip from the whisper you never notice to the threat spoken plainly, daring you to keep your teeth shut before it snuffs you out. That is the trick in the title — silence isn't peace here but currency, golden only to those who own it, because a quieted public is exactly the product the throne is selling. The argument Tobi Duncan is staging is that complacency and terror are two settings on the same machine, and the crowd narrating in circles has been talked into policing itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society","anger & defiance"],"means":"silence as currency owned by the throne not the crowd","built":"split-voice structure stages complacency and terror as two settings on the same machine"},
"trash-boat~all-i-can-never-be": {"fable":"The song's engine is a self-deception the chorus quietly exposes. Each verse runs the same rationalization — name love a passing thing, revealing, unappealing, then conclude you don't need it — but Duncan keeps slipping: the parallel structure is built to fail, so that by the second verse the same syntax lands on the opposite admission, that his own vanity fails to cancel the need underneath it. The refrain phrase about just adjusting is the tell, a person smoothing over feeling as damage control rather than choice. Against that, the chorus swaps the intimate voice for a crowd: the wish to matter to every last person, and rain reimagined as the cheer of a packed hall breaking over him, one he can drown in — approval standing in for the love he's talked himself out of, until mattering to the whole room collapses into being all the versions of himself he can never be.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"approval from the whole room substituted for the love he has talked himself out of","built":"parallel verse structure built to fail so the second landing admits the opposite"},
"pearl-jam~pilate": {"fable":"The refrain leans on Bulgakov's Pontius Pilate, whose only loyal companion in The Master and Margarita is his dog Banga — so the boast of having a dog that heels at a word and never turns away is really a confession of isolation, the one bond available to a man too guilty or too withdrawn for human ones. Jeff Ament builds the verses out of circular, going-nowhere gestures — circles drawn down, angels traced in dirt, a walk out of town where even one person feels like a crowd — a mind pacing its own ruts. The turn comes at the mirror: caught by his reflection seeing him too clearly, he flinches from a self-knowledge he swore off, and the friend whose soft company pulls you under is finally that reflection itself. Against a face that judges, the dog's uncritical devotion is both the comfort and the trap, which is why it darkens as the line repeats.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","shame & repression","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"dog's uncritical loyalty both comfort and trap of isolation","built":"Bulgakov's Pilate frames the dog's bond as confession not boast"},
"pearl-jam~all-those-yesterdays": {"fable":"A closing lullaby that keeps disguising itself as concern — the coaxing to lie down and rest is really an intervention, addressed to someone medicating themselves through the day and building walls so no one clocks how far gone they are. The chorus reframes exhaustion as release: the past isn't a thing to carry but silt to be rinsed off, and the tossed-out pill cups and the pills that fill them sit in the same disposable image-family, everything used up and meant to be let go. What lifts it past a comedown song is that the second half turns from soft command to permission, the voice insisting the door hasn't shut yet and that leaving isn't a betrayal — escape as mercy rather than failure. And then the title collapses into pure repetition, the phrase said over and over until it stops being a lyric and becomes the wash itself, the record letting go of Yield by chanting the load loose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"soft intervention dressed as a lullaby granting permission to leave","built":"title phrase repeated past meaning until it becomes the wash itself"},
"pearl-jam~low-light": {"fable":"A song of half-vision, built entirely in the register its title names: everything arrives at low light, the threshold where the eye can no longer trust what it registers. Jeff Ament writes a lyric of drift and second-guessing — motion he can feel but not verify, a scent-trail from someone gone, his own tracks erased behind him — so that the recurring worry over whether the reeling is the world's or just his own way of seeing becomes the whole subject. What holds it together is a movement from being sidetracked and lost toward a plain, unadorned petition for the light, the two-birds image resolving the solitary drift into something shared and still errant; on Vedder's voice the effect is less a resolution than an admission that finding the way and knowing what is real are the same unfinished search.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"finding the way and knowing what is real the same unfinished search","built":"two-birds image resolves solitary drift into still-errant shared motion"},
"calva-louise~con-coraz-n": {"fable":"A song about choosing your own direction not once you're certain, but precisely because you never will be. It opens in the language of dawning perception — seeing this time, though with little clarity, feeling it a little more each time — as if arriving at a threshold is less a revelation than a slow admission that the answer was never lost, only unfaced. The pivot is the refrain's grammar: fear and not-knowing are placed in the same breath as the choice, so that I choose a path with heart isn't triumph over doubt but a decision made through it, the will detached from any guarantee of destination. Midway the frame turns outward and settles a score — it was never personal, the game is over — reframing a betrayal that taught mistrust as noise against an indifferent infinite that doesn't care which road you take, which is exactly what frees the chooser to care. Allanic lets the escalating yo elijo — I choose, repeated past the point of meaning into pure assertion — carry the song's real argument: that agency is something you keep declaring until the declaring becomes the thing itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape","anger & defiance"],"means":"choice declared through doubt rather than past it","built":"yo elijo repeated past meaning until the declaring becomes the act"},
"i-scintilla~the-bells": {"fable":"The title is a bait-and-switch: no bell ever tolls here, and that absence is the point — the song is an address to a winged presence whose only evidence is a sound the speaker keeps swearing she can almost catch. The lyric builds its meaning across two failing image-families. One is the human order of legibility and rank, the writing that names who counts already worn too faint and thin to make out, lit by an office glare that exposes nothing; the other is a toxic, sacred register of a bleeding vine, of shrieks that arrive as if holy, of a spilled quicksilver poison left behind like a gift, where beauty and contamination turn out to be one substance. The winged addressee lives between them — angel or hallucination, the thing whose meaning the speaker begs to have spelled out while her own glow drains away, buried beneath blossom and gentle talk. By the close the questions stop and only the sensing remains: she swears the wings reach her ears, swears their motion touches her skin, the bells demoted from tolling to a barely-audible flutter that may not be there at all.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"begging an angel to translate itself before the glow drains","built":"the title withholds its own sound — no bell ever tolls"},
"i-scintilla~machine-vision": {"fable":"The lyric runs a single sustained pun on machine vision, letting image-processing vocabulary and the language of religious-state authority describe the same act of control: reducing noise, forcing every pixel to one of two values, thresholding a picture down until nothing survives but foreground and background are simultaneously how a computer flattens an image and how dogma flattens a person, both demanding you conform to a clean two-tone reading with every shade between thrown away. The recurring interrogation — who granted you salvation, who signed off on your pardon — turns that flattening into an indictment of borrowed authority, the fused temple-and-magistrate machinery that promises rescue in exchange for submission while the empty liturgies it recites can never scrub the mouths reciting them. The song's whole argument tightens into its closing figure: a projected light claims to reveal and purify, but the more it insists on illumination, the sharper the shadow it casts, and no amount of righteous projection can erase the caster's own. It reads as Bindrim's cold verdict on any system — algorithmic or theological — that mistakes forced binaries for truth and its own glare for grace.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","faith & the occult"],"means":"projected light sharpening the shadow behind the projector","built":"image-processing vocabulary and theological authority share a single idiom"},
"i-scintilla~cursive-eve-zero-tolerance-treatment": {"fable":"Bindrim reads the woman written into scripture as property — swaddled at birth in a text she never authored, her thought and flesh alike shackled to a book that stamps her unclean before she can name herself. The song's whole grievance is that the sentence is inherited: an Eve is said to live inside her, so guilt, subjection, and the multiplied sorrow of Genesis are assigned at birth rather than earned. Its cruelest turn is the demand that she drop to the floor and plead until dying stops frightening her — obedience sold as the cure for the very fear it manufactures, the ornately lettered falsehood she is instructed to swallow whole. The braided register of thees and thous, the witch-burning injunction, and the handing of her over to some tribunal of heaven's messengers let the industrial arrangement stage doctrine itself as the machinery grinding her down. What resists tidy reading is the closing figure taking in the wrong replies and holding the door open to the whispers — the persecutor's visions folded back over the condemned, so the madness the accusers projected onto her turns out to be theirs.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","surveillance & control","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"obedience sold as the cure for the fear it manufactures","built":"industrial arrangement stages doctrine itself as the grinding machinery"},
"i-scintilla~toy-soldier": {"fable":"A second-person anatomy of how a young soldier is manufactured, sung in Brittany Bindrim's cold register: the recruit is told outright that falling in line proves nothing about his manhood, then walked step by step into believing it proves everything. The vocabulary is deliberately industrial — archetype, puppetry, strategy, a closed loyalty that reclassifies anyone who simply consents as chosen stock — so that heroism reads as assembly-line theater, the death he deals downgraded to an offhand statistic, one more line item in someone else's plan. Its sharpest move is that singing itself becomes the mechanism: the mantra section repeats until the anthem stands exposed as the very thing that excuses the killing, glorification as a load-bearing part of the weapon. The final refrain quietly rewrites its own hook, so the buried truth he once dug up and read turns into a wound that torments him the moment he stops staying awake — the indoctrination completed not by belief but by enforced forgetting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","surveillance & control","artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"anthem exposed as the mechanism that excuses the killing","built":"the mantra section repeats until glorification stands as a weapon component"},
"i-scintilla~cursive-eve": {"fable":"A woman indicted by scripture that was written about her before she could speak — the song's image-system fuses the female body with the page, so that being swaddled at birth in text and being catalogued as tainted goods become the same act of binding. Brittany Bindrim threads real fragments of that inheritance through the verses — Eve as origin of blame, the curse of multiplied sorrow, the injunction against suffering a witch to live — and names them as fiction, a curse dressed up in decorative script and sold as comfort. The hook turns worship inside out: kneeling and begging are drilled as the cure for the fear of death, but the promised reward is only that the faithful will be licked dry, so obedience and self-erasure become indistinguishable. The refusal to look up while forbidding anyone to look down is the whole posture of the song's resistance — not a counter-doctrine but a flat rejection of the hierarchy itself. Where the closing verses drift into a man who cracks open to whispering voices and mistakes their fabrications for revelation, the argument lands: the machinery of guilt doesn't just subject the woman, it hollows out the believer who administers it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","surveillance & control","shame & repression"],"means":"kneeling and begging drilled as cure for a fear they install","built":"the female body fused with the page so binding and writing become one act"},
"i-scintilla~taken-shaken-mix-by-en-esch": {"fable":"A song about information as a weapon fired softly — the ammunition here is spin rather than lead, and its whole vocabulary is borrowed from the editing suite: cut, splice, blur, conceal, erase. The controllers don't shoot the truth so much as edit it mercilessly, and the recurring blindness-and-eyes motif frames this as an assault on sight itself, a demand not to look while insisting on what should be protected. The one movement that matters is the second chorus swapping illusions for delusions: the same manipulation that first fills you from outside has, by the end, become the thing you generate yourself — the propaganda has passed from screen into skull. Its parting couplet names the machinery plainly as policing opposition and concealing intention, refusing to soften the target the way its own imagery has been softened.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"propaganda migrating from screen into the skull that receives it","built":"the second chorus swaps illusions for delusions marking the internalization point"},
"my-ticket-home~time-kills-everything": {"fable":"A chain of near-identical I'll-vows drives the song, each swapping its object so the same possessive gesture flickers between tenderness and control — take out, leave in, wreck and adore, the verbs undoing each other faster than they can settle. The one real move is a pronoun shift: dragging someone past his own skull becomes past hers, and the recurring command to leave them turns private obsession into a shared refusal of everyone else. The lonely onlookers who freeze mid-gawk exist only to be said goodbye to, so the title's verdict — that time erodes all of it — reads less as grief than as the couple's own scorched-earth exit, holding onto each other precisely by burning the rest down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"holding together by burning every onlooker down","built":"a pronoun shift from his skull to hers turns private obsession into shared refusal"},
"my-ticket-home~we-all-use": {"fable":"The title is the hinge, and the song refuses to resolve it: we all use substances, and we all use each other, and the refrain lets both readings stand until they collapse into one. The verses split the accusation across two pronouns — first the familiar faces in your orbit draining the flow around you, then the strangers hollowing out a slow social death — so the addiction is never named as chemical or human, only as the same gravity pulling everything toward isolation. Its emotional core is Frost's borrowed line about beauty that cannot last, turned into a junkie's fatalism: a poem's lament for innocence lost becomes an excuse, a way to make decay sound like natural law rather than a choice already past the point of being waved off. What lands hardest is the admission that the pain is the thing that can't be lived without, which quietly rewrites the whole song — the using isn't for pleasure but for the ache that confirms you're still there. The repeated you should have known is aimed both outward and at the mirror, and by the final chorus there's no longer a difference.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"pain rewritten as the one thing that confirms you are still there","built":"the title holds chemical and interpersonal use in the same unresolved frame"},
"my-ticket-home~melancholia": {"fable":"Late on unReal, the song wears the flat affect of its title as a mask: the speaker keeps insisting on what he doesn't want — to be a loser, to be your friend — while every image underneath admits the opposite pull, that the hurt is a thing he leans into and the lie is worth loving to the end. The chorus is where the truth sits, a body drawn to the flame it knows will burn it, calling the rain and the high a loss of safety in the same breath it asks to be taken further; the death-wish is dressed as a party. What tips it from pose to genuine dissociation is the turn inward — a heaven reached while still breathing, a private world in the head that starts to taunt back and scream — so the nihilist shrug that the world has stopped meaning anything curdles into something that actually frightens the speaker. By the last plea to be saved, the swagger has collapsed: the numbness the title promises was never peace but a symptom, and the song's real subject is a person narrating his own slide while half-hoping someone interrupts it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","addiction & self-destruction","suicide"],"means":"nihilist swagger collapsing into a plea to be interrupted","built":"the private inner world begins to taunt back, turning pose into genuine fright"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~82588": {"fable":"A bitter address to someone the speaker once tried to save, built entirely on the fallen-angel figure: wings clipped, veins poisoned, a saint curdled into a sin from the inside out. The cruelty of the song is that the speaker keeps asking who did the maiming and lands on the worst answer — that the ruined party did it themselves, chewed their own wings off, and the speaker congratulates them for it. What makes it sting rather than gloat is the buried history underneath the contempt: a claim that the two once shared the same knowledge and the same certainty about each other, so the disgust is really grief over a mutual understanding that turned out to be one-sided. The closing turn toward truth setting you free is delivered to someone already cracked past saving, so the consolation reads as a door shutting, not opening.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","anger & defiance"],"means":"contempt for a ruin that was once a shared understanding","built":"the liberation cliché delivered to someone past saving shuts the door"},
"the-dillinger-escape-plan~sick-on-sunday": {"fable":"A goodbye reduced to its load-bearing scraps: a single thing wished undone, a single wrong step taken, and the flat verdict that nothing could be done and this is simply how it should be. The fragments never cohere into a scene because the piece won't let them — clauses land and dissolve like the glitching electronics carrying them, a leaving processed into static rather than felt through. What gives it its ache is the contradiction it refuses to resolve: the same voice that grants permission to walk away also pleads never to go, so the composure is a mask over a wound it can't admit. As an interlude on Ire Works — the album Dillinger built out of its own lineup fracture — the withheld melody and unfinished lines read as an ending too raw to be scored fully, only gestured at.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"permission to leave and a plea to stay in the same voice","built":"glitching electronics dissolve the lyric fragments before they cohere"},
"smashing-pumpkins~blue-skies-bring-tears": {"fable":"An apocalypse fantasy addressed as tenderness: it opens with Armageddon summoned so the children reach heaven, then spirals downward through unlit steps and manufactured intimacy toward self-erasure, and lands on a plea to be absorbed into someone else's flesh and hidden in whatever part of them lies furthest from the light. The refrain is the hinge — clear weather that produces grief rather than relief, because for a narrator this ruined the promise of a bright future reads as an accusation, an ordinary sky exposing what has already been lost. Corgan, writing MACHINA as a wired concept album about a burnt-out rock messiah, threads salvation and self-annihilation until they stop being distinguishable: he swallows the loveliest bloom he can name and wants no payment for it, framing consumption as devotion — a taking that expects nothing back. The repeated tag at the close stops developing and simply insists, letting the ache calcify into a fact rather than a feeling.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","love & desire"],"means":"salvation and self-annihilation fused until neither can be told apart","built":"clear weather inverted into grief exposes what the bright future has already cost"},
"smashing-pumpkins~age-of-innocence": {"fable":"Closing MACHINA, Corgan writes an exit hymn for a band and a self that both know they won't be coming back. The verses map a molting: an image-system of shedding runs from paint scraped off bone to the eye that watched, the plain unguarded self, the lie, all cast off like a skin, so that innocence here is not sweetness lost but a mask finally removed. That refusal to flinch gets crystallized in the metronomic refrain, a fixed toggle that answers ruin with a stark yes and flinching with a flat no, functioning less as a lyric than as a vow — the one thing held steady while everything else is surrendered. The vision of love as something you must bleed to be worthy of, and the free moment spent binding one's own release, catches the whole record's paradox: liberation and self-annihilation as the same gesture. The last turn drops the pose entirely, too young and too rich and too wrecked to even swear he was present, so that the coming-of-age arrives not as arrival but as the flat admission that home is the one place this road won't reach.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","freedom & escape"],"means":"innocence named as a mask removed rather than sweetness lost","built":"the metronomic refrain functions as a vow, not a lyric"},
"smashing-pumpkins~wound": {"fable":"A devotional address that keeps widening its frame until the beloved and the world share the same fate. It opens by beholding someone built into the singer through cosmic engineering — a companion who feels to have predated his own memory — and answers that fusion with a matched-vow chorus where every verb the other might do he pledges to do back, all the way to the end of his life. But the mirror turns sinister: each time he wheels around he catches himself dissolving into visions of skylines in flames and the planet coming apart, so the intimate covenant keeps dilating into apocalypse. The title names the mechanism — an injury that splits to expose the ruined figure inside, guilt already staining his hands — as if love at this pitch cannot separate tenderness from ruin, the deathless tie from the shame of surviving it. Corgan, writing MACHINA as a grand mythic self-elegy, makes the surrender total and the salvation impossible to tell apart from damnation.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"love at this pitch indistinguishable from the ruin it causes","built":"the matched-vow mirror structure keeps widening from intimacy to apocalypse"},
"smashing-pumpkins~the-crying-tree-of-mercury": {"fable":"Each stanza opens by naming a lifelong act — the years of singing, the years of noise pushed out of him, the years of weeping — and collapses all of it into one addressee, until the loved person absorbs the entire history of the self. The devotion sharpens into something predatory: the lover has waited like a blade to cut into a chest and empty himself in, a giving that is also a wound. Corgan won't let the vow stay clean, breaking off to admit that greed runs to the root of him and then daring the confession to matter less than the wanting itself, so that the grandeur and the excuse arrive in the same breath. By the last movement the reaching stretches over fading light and through static and deceit, the song straining toward a you it can no longer be sure is listening — a MACHINA-era hymn to love as the one thing worth an entire lifetime, sung with the suspicion that the lifetime itself was the price.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"devotion as a blade that gives by cutting in","built":"greed confessed mid-vow then dared to matter less than the wanting"},
"aimer~for-lonely": {"fable":"A lover's monologue that keeps trying to disarm its own hurt by naming a single culprit: not being able to touch. Aimer's speaker excuses both his own stubbornness and his partner's put-on toughness the same way — let's just blame it all on not being able to meet — and the whole quarrel shrinks to one unmet need, that if he could simply hold her once none of it would need saying. What complicates it is the second half's role-reversal: she's the one who suddenly breaks down over the phone, accused him of only loving himself, and he's left flustered, insisting he's so in love he can't be filled by anything else. The answer Aimer lands on is disarmingly small — most of this is trivial friction born of missing each other — so the tenderness lives in the refusal to escalate. The recurring English tag turns the whole ache outward at the end, from the couple's private for lonely to a bare address of the lonely girl herself, as if he's finally speaking past the fight straight to the frightened person underneath it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"reducing a quarrel to the single unmet need of touch","built":"closing English tag shifts address from the couple to the frightened person underneath"},
"aimer~stars-in-the-rain": {"fable":"A song built on a paradox its title states plainly — looking for light in the one place it shouldn't be, stars inside the rain — and Aimer splits that paradox across her two languages. The Japanese lines are the cold ground truth: two people pressed together in freezing rain with no umbrella and nowhere to go, guarding a dream that is already close to tears so it won't be lost, never able to find the fair-weather colors and night sky they keep searching the clouds for. The English is the reach upward out of that huddle — the discovery that there is still a way to keep drawing air, found somewhere past the glare of the city, the admission that walking the same loop over and over is a fate she would give up nothing to escape. What makes it land is that the hope never resolves into certainty: the refrain stays maybe, tonight we might see them, so the song holds the wanting rather than the finding, which is exactly what huddling in the rain against a fragile dream feels like.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"holding the wanting rather than arriving at the finding","built":"bilingual split assigns cold ground truth to Japanese and the reaching to English"},
"aimer~higher-ground": {"fable":"An anthem of reaching that keeps two languages doing opposite jobs. The English runs on pure ascent — a summit gauged in altitude still unclimbed, a fingertip stopping a breath short of distant light, a plea for a hand waiting at the bottom of the drop — the vocabulary of striving with the doubt sanded off. But Aimer routes the honest parts through Japanese: into nights and days there's no returning to, even if I can't cling on, even if there's no meaning there, even if I can't reach it now. Those clauses admit what the English chorus won't — that the higher ground may be unreachable and the climb may be pointless — so the song's real subject isn't triumph but the decision to keep going anyway, sustained less by the star than by the here with you it keeps returning to. The reaching is aspirational; the not-coming-down is stubborn; the difference between them is the whole song.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","love & desire"],"means":"the decision to keep climbing sustained by presence not triumph","built":"English carries aspiration while Japanese carries the doubt the English chorus will not say"},
"pro8l3m~the-end-fin-esc-abort": {"fable":"A descent narrated from the top of the market down. The opening verse rides the mythology of money — chrome, a Range Rover, a Merc — until that same gloss curdles into the drug crashing into your skull like a code, and Oskar83 tracks the Eldorado-lured ambassador flooring it down the highway to the bottom, where every four-language title (koniec, koniec, ucieczka, przerwij) names the same wall. What makes the song is the register split: the verses swagger and the chorus pulls the mask off, insisting that everyone talks tough about the streets while here they would eat you alive — cube by cube, litre of blood by litre — then flattening into the pour-it-and-pour-it loop of the trade that keeps running regardless. Steez83's production lets the boasting and the dread occupy the same breath, so the Karaiby-drinks flex and the detectives trailing behind, the myths rising as the aliases vanish, read as one motion rather than two moods. The last turn is the quietest and the coldest: no overdose, no bust, just the dealer's shrug — he taps his stuff, doesn't flinch, and simply disappears, which is the real meaning of the stacked stop-words. The song isn't about being killed by the game; it's about the discipline of ending yourself before it ends you.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"ending yourself before the game ends you as the only discipline left","built":"four languages name the same wall while the verses occupy boast and dread in one breath"},
"pro8l3m~widnokr-g": {"fable":"The hook sets the tone as a connoisseur's ritual — bitter oranges chosen because they're exquisite, two ice cubes, and then the plunge: everyone else forgotten, he goes all-in alone. That elegant-menace register is the whole song's engine, a dandy's calm laid over street carnage, robberies, disposable loyalty, and dead friends whose numbers he refuses to delete so their nicknames can still ambush him. Everything turns on the horizon of the title: tomorrow has no respect for today's plans — yesterday I was master of my fate, tomorrow already doesn't give a damn — so the self keeps splitting, and rather than change a leaf he'd swap it for a grenade, morphing on purpose into a worse version of himself. What holds it together is the fatalism of the strong being right today and no further; if I'm to die tomorrow, I'll at least dance tonight. The closing image lands the Fight Club framing without borrowing its slogans: the horizon darkens like a spent photochrome, sweet-and-bitter, and the cut power is what finally snaps him awake — the blackout as the only thing honest enough to rouse him.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","identity & becoming","death & grief"],"means":"fatalism worn as elegance, tomorrow already indifferent to today","built":"the spent photochrome horizon frames tomorrow's indifference in the title's own image"},
"pro8l3m~zombie": {"fable":"The title is a diagnosis, not a monster: the zombie is a person alive but hollowed out. The song's coldest line insists there are worse ways to be a corpse than dying. Oskar83's verse builds the private version — sealed inside four mould-infected walls, running in place through his own dreams, a slave to cravings before he's lived a quarter of a life. The Sokół guest verse widens it into a systemic one, where you grind three months to live one well and the whole engine of grow-to-grow-more is named for what it is: the logic of a cancer cell. Between them sits the refrain, a numbed litany of I-don't-want-to — not tears, not words, not dreams, not to exist — whose only surviving appetite is the wish to be angry at a world that hid the addressee away. The chorus enacts the deadness it describes by refusing every human response but spite. The closing turn makes helplessness the true subject: bezsilność, powerlessness, is likened to death and then to blood on the hands, the speaker kneeling as the next wave takes him, having outsourced the ending to an emissary in a hangman's cap. What keeps this from mere nihilism is that the refusal is legible as protest — the Möbius strip of a life bent into italics against the wind is drawn precisely enough that the exhaustion reads as an indictment of the machine, not just a symptom of it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","addiction & self-destruction","politics & society"],"means":"refusal as the last legible protest against a system that hollowed the self","built":"the chorus enacts deadness by stripping every human response but spite"},
"pro8l3m~animal-planet": {"fable":"Split cleanly into a mask and its underside, the track first ventriloquizes the smug futurists — the wise heads explaining how to live, promising tomorrow will be better than today. They wave off a suicide and the green-robed revolutionaries alike, so the opening verse is consumer optimism performed straight, until the narrator admits he now hides his own truth like cash in a mattress. The chorus is where the fusion lands: white collars and white lines become one image, respectability and cocaine as the same easy success. The future is something you glimpse in a shattered mirror. Then the voice drops the act and speaks plainly, refusing the left/right binary the culture wants to sort him into and naming the real prison as money and opinion — the wish not to be well but merely better than others. The self is reduced to a cog, a puppet on a string, a name and a residence number feeding an army of useful idiots. The Fight Club logic surfaces in the last lines: to the world he is a trivial detail, yet that same world is enraged when he plays by his own rules, which is the whole point of the mask-and-underside structure — the system only notices you when you stop obeying it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","money & the street","identity & becoming","surveillance & control"],"means":"the mask of consumer optimism dropped to name money and opinion as the real prison","built":"white collars and white lines fused into one image, respectability and cocaine collapsed"},
"pro8l3m~ziemia-obiecana": {"fable":"One relentless anaphora — nearly every line begins Tu (here) or Tam (there) — turns Reymont and Wajda's Promised Land inside out: the industrial city of striving becomes a Warsaw of cash counted in bricks, cut streets, coke and betrayal, and the borrowed name mocks the promise by naming exactly what was never delivered. The tu/tam axis is the whole engine: here is the paid-for reality Oskar83 lives in, there is the sanctioned other-world — charity convents, sisters with a family home, people who fight with arguments — always just out of frame, and the venom is that here so often does what there merely pretends not to. Underneath the swagger is paranoia and rot: you don't trust yourself let alone anyone else, and beneath the bell-jar the truth boils like a septic tank — the boast is a mask over a life in which every kindness has its price and every truth must be hidden. The chorus's welcome-friend refrain is delivered as initiation, an ironic host ushering a newcomer into a world where a hand of an evil god does the protecting. It closes by dropping the catalogue for a verdict — walk to win, the devil whispers at dawn, seven deadly sins and an eighth life — reframing the whole inventory as a Fight Club-style survival credo where the promised land is simply the ground you already stand on and have to win.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","politics & society","shame & repression"],"means":"the promised land reread as the ground you already stand on and must win","built":"the tu/tam axis runs every line, here and there as the whole engine"},
"kyuss~allen-s-wrench": {"fable":"Barely a lyric at all — a single flat verdict, that this is the whole of what you get, hammered until the phrase stops meaning and becomes pure texture, the way the riff underneath it grinds down to a groove. The title's pun on the Allen key (a tool that only turns one size of bolt) sets the trap: a mushroom-potion high offered as the only reward on the table, so the refusal to widen is the point, not a gap to fill. By the end the words fray into a near-rhyming litany of clipped one-syllable commands and a curdling jealous wrath that reads like a spell half-remembered, the syllables chosen for how they lock together over Kyuss's desert fuzz rather than for sense. It's less a song about anything than a mantra performing its own narrowing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"a mantra performing its own narrowing rather than naming anything","built":"the title's Allen-key pun sets a single-size tool as the song's own form"},
"kyuss~mondo-generator": {"fable":"Nick Oliveri screams this one raw, and the lyric is built almost entirely from questions hurled at someone who can't answer — who will carry you across, how far the two of you have drifted, what it would even cost — so the song's real subject is the failure of contact itself, one person unable to reach or hear another over a distance the words keep measuring as too vast to close. The recurring taunt about the other's doomed effort to keep pace turns that distance into a contest already lost, and the escalating profanity is less anger than the sound of someone battering at a door that won't open. When the refrain narrows to a single image of everything spent and only smoke rising, the whole interrogation curdles into one exhausted admission that no forward direction survives, only ascent or burnout. Fittingly for Blues for the Red Sun, the meaning lives in the churn and the scream rather than any answer — the questions are the point, and none of them land.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The album's one Oliveri-sung track — he later took Mondo Generator as the name of his own band.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"questions hurled at someone unreachable, distance measured in failed contact","built":"the interrogative structure is never resolved — the questions are the point, none land"},
"pro8l3m~opowie-o-tobie": {"fable":"A verse-length indictment that keeps correcting your assumption about what it is: this is not a protest, it insists, only a story about you. It opens in a fog of Polish consumer sedation — feeding, boozing, dosing, cheap credit — where the population swallows an administered truth daily and time turns the brain to porridge, then scales that same mechanism up to wars manufactured over oil and phantom weapons, the biggest whores skimming the most while the pigs sprint for gold and the actual rulers stand off to the side. The through-line is the shock doctrine — Naomi Klein's phrase, and the lyric uses it in so many words: the smoke screen, the crisis engineered so you never look away from the live feed while the truth stays hidden and you simply lose again. Then the argument executes its real move and swivels the accusation onto the listener — you lied to a woman yesterday and still get taken by the cheap three-cup con, and your little deception is beneath even the word lie because you mistake the world for a fairy tale about the world. The last turn is the ugliest and the point of the title: the crowd likes to see blood spilled, to watch another convulse and die, and the drive to grind someone to pulp — that, the closing line says, is a text about us, collapsing the manipulators and the manipulated into the same appetite.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","surveillance & control","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"the accusation pivots from systemic manipulators onto the listening self","built":"the title's story-about-you collapses manipulators and manipulated into one appetite"},
"romes~alittle": {"fable":"The hook is built entirely on the phrase a little as a hedge. Every extreme — dying, living, losing his grip — gets shrunk to a survivable dose, and the recurring line that stitches them together insists this contradiction is a stable resting state rather than a crisis waiting to be solved, contentment and wretchedness fused into one setting. What lifts the song past mood is that it stages an actual argument: a concerned outside voice keeps breaking in, naming his drinking, his squandered days, the state of his head, pressing him to see someone. Each time, he bats it back with the pose of I'm fine, dressing up the same faded drinking as a wellness routine and telling the worrier to tend their own life. The bridge tears off that pose by cramming the mood swings into a single breath, the mess and the depression collapsing straight into I'm the best, the vow to quit music and the refusal to quit landing in one line, so the defiance sounds like another symptom rather than a rebuttal. By the outro the two futures are no longer even choices he weighs but a coin still in the air, and the closing line quietly sheds the deflecting a little to arrive at a bare want to keep living, the nearest the song comes to conceding the deflection was the trouble all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"the defiant hedge worn smooth into the very stuckness it deflects","built":"the closing line drops its own a little to arrive at bare want"},
"pezet~tatua-e-i-motocykle": {"fable":"The hook lays the whole survival grammar bare: back where Pezet is from, even when he wanted to cry he had to hide it, so he laughed — the swagger is armor, not joy. From there the song answers a girl's innocent question (what do the tattoos mean?) with the defiant party-line that they mean nothing, we just want to live and have fun, a mask of nihilist cool draped over the class contempt underneath (the detective who tails him because ink reads as dealer, the father who mistakes rap crews for the alphabet). The motorcycle carries the same double life: at first just fast living, a speed that feels like freedom, wind in the lungs. But the final verse cashes the metaphor in — the ride is to hell, helmetless, unable to slow, the tattoos now a written history of escaping the ghetto — so the fun becomes a death-drive, and the throwaway tattoos-mean-nothing is exposed as exactly the deflection the opening confessed. The song's real subject is that gap: the performance of not caring by someone who was trained young never to be seen caring.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The Latin drop mid-song — totus tuus — is John Paul II’s papal motto: the album’s Jan-Paweł name-conceit surfacing inside its most secular track.","themes":["shame & repression","artifice & performance","money & the street","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the nothing-means-nothing pose exposed as armor over a death-drive","built":"the motorcycle's fun reread in the final verse as a helmetless ride to hell"},
"pezet~mi-o-na-sprzeda": {"fable":"Pezet runs one conceit all the way to the floor: love isn't sold like a commodity, it IS a financial instrument — a speculative bubble that inflated and burst, a joint-stock company that collapsed, something a bank could trade in percentage points and a producer could bill you for down to the last cent. What begins as a sour survey of others (the dead friend whose fortune bought only suffering, the OnlyFans girls flying to Hawaii, the paid affection that stays till morning) is really the album title's argument in miniature — everything is priced, nothing is pro bono, and the tenderest word for love the chorus can find is a heap of slang for money: pieniądze, mamona, szmal. The bridge exposes the con by acting it out: tell me money doesn't matter, he pleads, and I'll buy you a treasure — freedom under a glass bell, paid for with money. Then the turn that makes the song more than cynicism: he indicts himself. What plays in the club is disco-polo about drugs, not his music anymore, and he's been dealing his own kind of crack for years — the world chewed everything up and spat out a new order where love is gone and only a balance of profit and loss remains. The refrain that love is today, right here, for sale lands not as accusation but as confession, the veteran admitting he's still working the same market he despises.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the veteran indicting a market he admits he never stopped working","built":"love reduced to a speculative bubble that inflated and burst"},
"pezet~nikogo-nie-kocham": {"fable":"A man tells his partner he has a heart of stone though he is flesh and blood, and the song is his attempt to trace where that stone came from without pretending it makes him innocent. The present tense is a stalemate: desire without love, her scrolling her own stories for someone to look, both of them adults who keep repeating a night that always leaves him twelve steps from a drink — Pezet lets the phrasing sit right at the addiction his Warsaw is soaked in without ever naming it outright. The chorus turns the confession into something colder and truer than heartbreak; he insists that to love no one is worse than to be unloved, because a person who loves someone loves the whole world, and he has been shut out of that entirely. The last verse supplies the origin, and it is the sharpest move in the song — the scratched-in word Love on a childhood wall, no god and only evil in the house, a whole neighborhood he calls adult children of alcoholics — so the stone is revealed as inheritance, a different clay he was molded from. What sounds like a lover's cruelty is finally a man watching himself harden into his father's house again and wanting only to leave Warsaw before it becomes a habit for the two of them too.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","family & blood","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"the stone heart traced back to the clay it was molded from","built":"the final verse reveals inheritance where the chorus announced a confession"},
"pezet~stare-wwo": {"fable":"The hook is the whole thesis compressed: I listen, I listen — old WWO, Sopranos on HBO. Nostalgia here isn't for a place but for a stereo pairing, native Warsaw rap braided with imported American myth, the two records that furnished a teenager's sense of the world. From there Pezet reconstructs the 90s not as a golden age but as a grey, broke, crime-ridden childhood — parents working three jobs and still short, hash in a plaster cast, a Warsaw as lawless and provincial as the Wild West — and pointedly refuses the cliché that those were better times, since today is where the prosperity is. The engine of the song is contempt for the present: everyone now takes the elevator while he took the stairs, every polite kid in fake Armani plays gangster, famous for being famous, mistaking the Biennale for a beer hall. What he mourns isn't the poverty but the authenticity the poverty forced — the closing repetitions of słucham land as a man still tuned to a frequency the culture stopped broadcasting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nostalgia & memory","money & the street","artifice & performance"],"means":"mourning the authenticity poverty forced not the poverty itself","built":"the repeated słucham closes as a man tuned to a frequency the culture dropped"},
"pezet~gangi-w-la": {"fable":"The song lays a Warsaw night over a Los Angeles Pezet only knows from music and screens — the Wisła running like the Styx, palms and a cliff that aren't really there — and then lets the gang-color system carry the argument: the sky is Crip-blue by day, eyes go Blood-red by night from sleeplessness and crying, and the same incompatibility runs in the blood of two lovers turned into rival territories. That is where the real reading sits, in an image-family borrowed wholesale from American rap and made to describe a toxic, on-again-off-again romance rather than a street. The refrain says it without decoration — tomorrow we dislike each other again, like gangs in LA — so the Lana Del Rey murmur and the LA glamour aren't aspiration but a ready-made vocabulary for a couple who keep re-drawing the same front lines.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","anger & defiance"],"means":"gang-color rivalry borrowed wholesale to map a toxic on-off romance","built":"Crip-blue sky and Blood-red eyes map the couple's front lines onto a city they've never seen"},
"filter~cold-anthem-for-the-damned": {"fable":"A man alone under open darkness confronts a mind that has turned predatory, dread circling like a fever he cannot sweat out. Richard Patrick, sober after years of addiction, renders the chill as a bodily fact: it runs beneath the flesh, an onslaught no warmth reaches. The wish to have never left the house, repeated three times, is the pivot, regret hardening into a refrain that admits going out was the mistake. It ends on a dare to name a truth, then a witnessed deception, the paranoia finally aimed outward at whoever else is doing the deceiving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"dread turning predatory while the chill runs beneath the flesh","built":"the wish to never have left the house repeats until it becomes the refrain"},
"filter~all-the-good": {"fable":"Filter builds the whole song out of a single accusation set to a loop: the decent part of a person is not lost or worn away but deliberately put down, over and over, by people who keep committing the same crime. The two brightening wake-up calls that open each verse are the irony the song runs on — the cheerful summons to greet the day is really the cue for the daily execution to resume, and the refusal to develop the line past that one couplet is the point, the killing repeated until it stops registering as killing and just becomes the shape of a life. Under the mask is a reflexive claim of ownership over one's own goodness, asserted precisely because the singer already knows it will be taken and put down again tomorrow.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"goodness deliberately put down by repetition until the killing stops registering","built":"the refusal to develop past one couplet enacts the daily execution it describes"},
"filter~summer-child": {"fable":"Richard Patrick borrows a taunt for anyone who has never lived through a killing frost, aiming it at a listener whose whole life has fallen inside one warm season and who therefore mistakes shelter for the natural order of things. The verses stack a survivor's credentials against that innocence: someone who has stared into open nothingness and outlasted the storms watches a ruler with no real potency peddle manufactured division under a costume of decency, then names the sheltered addressee a fool for believing the record was cued up on their behalf. Filter's late-career political turn lands its sting in the tenderness of the address, a lullaby cadence wrapped around the warning that the killing cold always arrives for those who assumed they were exempt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","nature & the elements"],"means":"warning the sheltered that the killing cold always arrives for those who assumed exemption","built":"lullaby cadence wrapped around the sting so the tenderness of address is the weapon"},
"filter~say-it-again": {"fable":"A song about talking into a void and knowing it, the refrain built on a defeated logic: repeating yourself precisely because the other person has checked out. Richard Patrick has always written Filter around a rattled interior, and the verses locate the failure inside the speaker's own head first, a mind described as too crowded and lost to work, so overloaded it has swung from crazed to numbly robotic. The turn comes when the pronoun flips: the one who cannot hear becomes the one who cannot listen when she tries to reach him, so the accusation of a missing, absent partner curdles into a confession that the static is his. The relentless loop of the chorus enacts what it describes, a plea worn down to reflex that keeps firing even after both parties have gone unreachable.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","heartbreak & loss","madness & the mind"],"means":"accusation of an absent partner curdling into confession that the static is his","built":"the relentless chorus loop enacts the plea worn down to reflex it describes"},
"filter~the-drowning": {"fable":"Filter stages self-destruction as spectator sport: a man drowns in plain view, laughing and drinking more, while a helpless we stands on the shore forced to watch. The song's whole tension lives in that shoreline distance -- the addict who refuses rescue and mistakes the drowning for control, versus the loved ones stuck between rescue and self-preservation, unable to look away and unable to intervene. Richard Patrick, who has been public about his own recovery from alcoholism, writes the accusation from both sides at once: the drowning man's delusion of mastery is the sickness, and the repeated demand for how long the watchers must endure it is codependency's exhausted refrain -- not a plea to save him but the beginning of the decision to stop watching.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","family & blood"],"means":"the demand of how long watchers must endure becoming the decision to stop watching","built":"Patrick writes accusation from both sides at once, addict and codependent in the same voice"},
"filter~up-against-the-wall": {"fable":"Filter builds the song from a cornered posture, the title phrase looping like a man pinned in place while a system of manufactured deception, daily falsehoods and armed enforcers, closes the exits around him. The verses widen that private siege into political dread, naming thieves who commandeer another person's thinking and profiteers who strip even the dead, until survival feels like waiting out a long delay before the damage becomes legible. Against that machinery the chorus collapses inward, revenge curdling into a death-wish flung at whoever peddles honeyed reassurance, so the fury registers less as threat than as a man poisoned by his own helpless disgust. What he wants at the far edge is unadorned honesty, a distant crossing over scorched ground, held out as the one thing that might still bring release.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","surveillance & control","suicide"],"means":"fury poisoning inward until revenge registers as a death-wish rather than a threat","built":"the title's cornered posture loops across the whole song like a man pinned in place"},
"filter~for-the-beaten": {"fable":"Richard Patrick, long politically outspoken, plants himself here on the side of the powerless, sketching an uprising where cruelty falls without discrimination and the singer takes up a weapon because he cannot bear the harm done to someone he loves. The escalation is the whole argument: a demonstrator holding printed pages and craving quiet hardens, verse by verse, into a combatant gripping a firearm, so the chorus insistence on a just cause reads less as certainty than as the self-justification a person reaches for once conviction has curdled into force. That grinding, near-total repetition of the refrain enacts the trap it describes, a burning landscape narrowing toward its breaking point with no exit except the struggle it keeps circling back to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","war & battle","anger & defiance"],"means":"conviction hardening verse by verse from pamphlet to firearm","built":"near-total refrain repetition enacts the burning trap it keeps circling back to"},
"filter~miss-blue": {"fable":"A love song built around the fear of being left, where devotion and dependence turn out to be the same wound. The speaker offers freedom only on the condition of staying, then admits the self behind the mask would horrify anyone who truly saw it. His recurring question of when he will finally be okay never resolves, because the person he leans on is the same one edging toward the door. By the end every argument collapses into one word, goodbye, repeated past sense until it stops meaning anything and becomes pure ache.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"devotion and dependence revealed as the same wound","built":"goodbye repeated past sense until it stops meaning anything and becomes pure ache"},
"filter~happytogether": {"fable":"This is a cover of the Turtles' 1967 sunshine-pop standard, and everything turns on the transplant: dropped into Richard Patrick's industrial-rock project, the song's sugary absolutism curdles into something closer to fixation than devotion. The devotion is entirely projected — the beloved exists as a fantasy summoned in the mind and a voice on the far end of a phone line, never as a present, answering person — so the vow that there could be no one else and that even chance itself must bend to this pairing scans less as romance than as a mind that has sealed the exits. Filter doesn't parody the original; it reads its obsessive repetition dead straight and lets the menace that was always latent under the melody rise to the surface.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","madness & the mind","artifice & performance"],"means":"sunshine-pop absolutism rising into fixation when transplanted into industrial rock","built":"the cover reads the original's obsessive repetition dead straight to surface its latent menace"},
"filter~one-2009-remastered-version": {"fable":"Richard Patrick runs Harry Nilsson's arithmetic of grief through Filter's industrial abrasion, so a tidy tune about a solitary integer curdles into something raw, sleeved in distortion and a clenched, cornered delivery. The whole conceit turns loss into cold counting, where a single digit becomes the smallest, meanest quantity a person can be reduced to once someone vacates the other half of a pair. Patrick strands his narrator alone with the tally, stitching worthless little verses out of a vanished past, until the tally itself reads as a mind circling one wound it keeps helplessly touching.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"loss reduced to cold arithmetic, grief circling one wound it cannot stop touching","built":"Filter's industrial abrasion runs Nilsson's tidy melody through distortion to expose what is clenched inside it"},
"annihilator~heavy-metal-maniac-exciter-cover": {"fable":"Exciter's 1983 debut-title anthem, covered here by Annihilator, is an ur-text of speed metal and a near-perfect specimen of the genre's founding conceit: the song is about itself. Its swagger is doubly literal, welding sexual conquest to the arrival of the music as one unstoppable force, so the heat aimed at a lover and the violence aimed at the world are the same rising thunder breaking the same walls and chains. There is nothing hidden under the mask, and reading it as coded would be confabulation; the maniac simply is the genre's self-mythology at birth, heavy metal announcing its own volume as identity, threat, and seduction with no daylight between them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","party & hedonism","love & desire"],"means":"genre announces its own volume as identity, threat, and seduction at once","built":"the song is explicitly about itself, with no coded layer beneath the swagger"},
"annihilator~suicide-society": {"fable":"Jeff Waters turns Annihilator's precision thrash into a plainspoken indictment of a species busily engineering its own extinction, stacking polluters, corrupt lawmakers, and zealots into one accelerating catalog of self-harm. The recurring verdict of collective madness works as diagnosis rather than insult, naming a civilization that keeps repeating the same ruin because consequence never arrives until the flames finally do. Its late move matters most: the litany of blame suddenly pivots to a direct question, asking whether the listener will passively watch the rot spread or instead join with others to repair the damage. That turn from doom-preaching toward demand converts fatalism into a dare, refusing the comfort of despair by insisting the outcome still hangs on a choice not yet made.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","madness & the mind","anger & defiance"],"means":"doom-preaching pivots to a demand that converts fatalism into a dare","built":"late direct question breaks the litany and makes the outcome contingent on choice"},
"annihilator~creepin-again": {"fable":"A sleep disorder gets dramatized as a predator, the terror returning nightly because the only relief from it is the surrender that summons it back. Annihilator built a career on Jeff Waters weaponizing thrash toward domestic dread, and here the enemy is unconsciousness itself. The mythic sandman, usually a gentle bringer of rest, is recast as a stalking thing that feeds the instant the mind lets go. Its title names the horror precisely: not one attack but a relapse, dread that keeps circling back to a body that must eventually close its eyes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind"],"means":"relief summoned only through the surrender that invites the dread back","built":"the sandman recast as a stalker feeding the instant the mind lets go"},
"annihilator~the-attitude": {"fable":"A blunt kiss-off to a smug know-it-all who mistakes arrogance for wisdom and lectures everyone on how things are done. The verses sketch the target with contempt, warning that all that swagger will buy nothing lasting. Then the argument stops being an argument at all. The same profane refrain lands again and again, curdling into shittier and more personal variants with each pass. That escalating repetition is the point, standing in for a rebuttal because the speaker has already judged the target beneath one. Jeff Waters writes it as a raised middle finger rather than a debate, letting disgust do what reasoning refused to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance"],"means":"disgust displaces argument because the target is judged beneath rebuttal","built":"the profane refrain escalates in variants, performing contempt rather than reasoning it"},
"annihilator~i-am-warfare": {"fable":"War itself takes the microphone, cataloguing its arsenal from asymmetric ambush through nerve agents and germ plagues to the final nuclear switch that kills every grid, a personified appetite claiming humanity built it and keeps feeding it. Jeff Waters frames the horror less as prophecy than as accusation, the ancient enemy insisting it is familiar and invited, never truly absent, so the damnation that supposedly claims the ground is only ever our own recurring handiwork returning home. Annihilator's clinical thrash delivery matches that logic, treating extinction-scale violence as routine escalation rather than spectacle, a ledger of methods recited coldly through gears toward the point where every system finally ceases.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society"],"means":"war claims humanity built and keeps feeding it, so damnation is always homemade","built":"first-person personification of war turns accusation back onto the listener"},
"annihilator~out-with-the-garbage": {"fable":"A song of severance from an abuser, built on the deliberately cheap language of taking out the trash: the person who once controlled and struck the speaker is reduced to refuse, something to be binned so the new can come in. The cruelty of the metaphor is the point, a role-reversal in which the one who was labored over and hit now becomes the disposable object, and the chorus turns that into a clenched victory chant. What keeps it from mere bravado is the last verse, where cleansing shades into a colder wish to erase the person from memory and from the earth entirely, and the real hope underneath surfaces almost shyly: to be somewhere valued instead of used.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","family & blood"],"means":"abuser reduced to refuse in the same role-reversal he once enforced","built":"the trash metaphor is the cruelty, not the vehicle for it"},
"annihilator~dressed-up-for-evil": {"fable":"Annihilator's late-period thrash trains its venom on the predator who tailors himself for the boardroom, the sharp lapels and knotted silk hiding cloven feet beneath expensive cloth. The portrait sharpens into a corporate messiah worshipped by a bought entourage, treating women as diversions and every rival as a lesser creature fit only to be stepped on further. Its bite comes from how the mask slips: beneath the pressed wisdom and cologne sits decay, a stench no cufflink covers, spoiling faster than the swarming insects can settle. Later verses tilt from character study toward verdict, tallying human beings as line items owned and counted, until the arc breaks and the conscienceless empire-builder is finally overcome, exposed, and disbelieved by the crowd he once dazzled.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","money & the street","anger & defiance"],"means":"boardroom predator exposed when decay no cufflink can cover surfaces","built":"the slipping mask moves from character study to verdict as humans become line items"},
"meshuggah~this-spiteful-snake": {"fable":"Meshuggah take the oldest myth-symbol of malice and reassign it: the serpent here is not a tempter or an adversary within existence but existence itself, a hostile sentient thing that actively schemes against the living. The song's argument is scale — a mind granted whole eras of study could never map the sheer voracity packed into twenty-four hours — so the coiling reptile becomes the perfect figure for a cosmos that binds by overwhelming density, constricting rather than striking. What lifts it above ordinary nihilist despair is the final turn: the creature molts not to renew but to expose, its suffocating hood peeled back to show that terror was never a mask laid over the truth, it was the truth the whole time. Fittingly for a band whose polyrhythmic churn feels like being ground inside heavy machinery, the horror is not a monster you can face but the mechanism of being you are already trapped within.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"terror disclosed as the substance of existence not its mask","built":"the molt strips camouflage to reveal the concealment was always the truth"},
"meshuggah~pineal-gland-optics": {"fable":"A conversion narrative staged inside the skull: the pineal gland, esoterica's third eye, floods the speaker with a vision so vivid it discredits waking life, and the song's real subject is which self gets to decide what is real. It opens on the epistemological hook — if a dream can make you bleed, who has the standing to call it hallucination? — then watches the rational, doubting self be outvoted and forced to kneel before visions that are explicitly authoritative, not merely beautiful. Meshuggah frame the takeover as betrayal from both directions: the old skeptical self is branded a Judas whose denial kept the truth disguised, so surrender gets rebranded as honesty. The closing movement performs the ecstatic strip-down every revelation myth requires — restraints snapped, dread and grief incinerated, the reasoning organ scoured until it reports only what the vision dictates — yet the earlier grammar of command and kneeling has already poisoned it, so what plays as enlightenment lands as a coup, doctrine installed by force against the mind that once resisted. The dread is that the deformity is christened beautiful until liberation and possession can no longer be told apart.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Its closing phrase — a new belief — reprises the title track’s new belief-system verbatim, folding the conversion story into the album’s thesis.","themes":["transcendence & consciousness","faith & the occult","identity & becoming"],"means":"liberation and possession made indistinguishable by the coup inside the skull","built":"the earlier grammar of command and kneeling poisons the ecstatic closing strip-down"},
"meshuggah~dancers-to-a-discordant-system": {"fable":"Meshuggah drive a single figure to exhaustion: society as a ballroom drilled to move in time with music engineered to be wrong, a fitting conceit from a band whose stuttering meters feel built to trip the body. The crucial turn is that the dancers are not victims but accomplices, marrying themselves to their own fetters and rehearsing the choreography with lids sealed against sight, sustaining the spectacle rather than ending it. Its cruelty is that the herd earns its own damnation through voluntary blindness, buckling under a guilt it will not acknowledge while congratulating itself that it matters. The final passage folds the whole argument into a grammar of consequence, where every gesture of faith becomes the instrument of its ruin, so that credulity is not a wound the machine inflicts but the fuel it burns to keep turning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","artifice & performance","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"voluntary blindness as the fuel the system burns to keep turning","built":"the band's trip-wire meters literalise choreography engineered to be wrong"},
"the-prodigy~your-love-remix": {"fable":"On Experience, Liam Howlett's rave debut, lyric is not a text but a sample-trigger, and this track reduces the whole vocabulary of love to two detonations: a snatched vocal hook and a wordless whoop, each fired on the beat rather than sung. Stripped of any object, situation, or verb, the phrase stops meaning devotion and becomes pure euphoric ignition — a crowd cue engineered for the moment the drop hits. The refusal to develop is the content: early-90s hardcore treats the sampled human voice as another rhythm hit, and the ecstasy here belongs not to a lover but to the dancefloor.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism"],"means":"love stripped of object and verb until it fires as pure dancefloor ignition","built":"the sampled vocal treated as a rhythm hit rather than a lyric, ecstasy belonging to the floor not a lover"},
"the-prodigy~you-ll-be-under-my-wheels": {"fable":"There is no lyric here to speak of, only rock and roll broken back into its two bare verbs and chanted into exhaustion, each call shadowed by its own echo. The song refuses the menace its title promises: the threat of being crushed under the wheels lives entirely in the beat and the sneering title, never in words, so the vocal becomes pure engine noise, a body reduced to two mechanical motions. On Always Outnumbered, Liam Howlett strips the rave anthem down to its combustion — the repetition is not laziness but the point, a mantra that flattens the oldest phrase in the music into raw forward momentum, all drive and no destination.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape"],"means":"the oldest phrase in rock chanted into pure forward momentum, all drive no destination","built":"the title's threat lives entirely in the beat, never in words the vocal refuses to supply"},
"the-prodigy~warrior-s-dance": {"fable":"A single sampled diva vocal, chopped and looped past the point of sense, becomes the whole engine here: the summons to move never resolves into an instruction, and the question about what the two of them should be doing hangs open forever, deliberately unanswered. That withheld clause is the design, not a flaw — the track keeps offering direction and delivering only churn, so significance settles into the machine-like return of the same syllables rather than into any lyric. The closing aside, flatly reporting that the sirens and the crowd whoop have been switched off, yanks the curtain from its own rave nostalgia and confesses that those ecstatic cues were only samples all along. What survives is repetition treated as ritual, a groove that mistrusts release and keeps the room in motion precisely because it refuses to arrive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"ritual repetition that mistrusts release and refuses to arrive","built":"a closing aside yanks the curtain by confessing the ecstatic cues were only samples"},
"the-prodigy~voodoo-people": {"fable":"There is barely a lyric here at all, and that is the point: a rave track built from a single chanted riddle, looped until the words stop being sentences and become percussion. The homophone at its center — the voodoo who do what you don't dare do — folds identity, action and dare into one syllabic knot, casting the dancers and the DJ conjuring them as an initiate cult defined purely by transgression, by doing what the timid outside world will not. Landing on Music for the Jilted Generation, Liam Howlett's 1994 middle finger to the Criminal Justice Bill's war on unlicensed raves, that boast reads as a scene closing ranks: the magic and voodoo people are the ravers themselves, sanctifying their own outlawed ecstasy. The refusal to develop past the chant is the argument — meaning lives in the trance and the tribe, not in anything you could paraphrase.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","friendship & loyalty","faith & the occult"],"means":"dancers and DJ as an initiate cult defined by transgression","built":"the homophone knot folds identity, action, and dare into one syllabic loop"},
"the-prodigy~the-way": {"fable":"One phrase of resignation loops until it hardens into a mantra, which is the whole point, because here the words work as a rhythmic cell rather than an argument. Fed through the machine, the flat acceptance that this is simply how things are becomes less a statement than a groove, something to surrender to. Repetition drains the phrase of its complaint and leaves only its cadence, so the fatalism turns propulsive instead of defeated, a shrug the track makes you move to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness"],"means":"fatalism drained of complaint until only its cadence propels you","built":"repetition converts flat resignation into a groove the body surrenders to"},
"the-prodigy~shoot-down-feat-liam-gallagher-and-noel-gallagher-of-oasis": {"fable":"This is less a song about violence than a machine that reproduces it, the trigger-command barked over and over until aggression becomes reflex, a groove the body obeys before the mind can object. What little conscience surfaces is a grimace that hardens instantly into a verdict, the pun collapsing the shooter's flinch and his sealed doom into one syllable so remorse and consequence arrive as the same breath. Then the chant evaporates and death itself gets addressed head-on, the warning that the hours are draining stuttered into a loop that empties the phrase the way a spent magazine empties, the countdown eating its own tail. The whole point lives in that shape: an inexhaustible mechanical pull spending something strictly finite.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"mechanical aggression spending a strictly finite human life","built":"a single pun collapses the shooter's flinch and his sealed doom into one syllable"},
"the-prodigy~resonate": {"fable":"Reduced to two verbs cycled without relief, the piece treats sound itself as a battering ram: a hum that lodges behind the eyes and, from there, snaps every shackle it finds. Late-period Prodigy had long fused rave with riot, and the vocabulary here is pared to a demolition-crew shorthand — vibration as an instrument that rattles a venue apart while it undoes the restraint. That refusal to develop is deliberate; this behaves less like an argument than a chant hurled back from a crowd until leveling the building feels like a physical pressure rather than a phrase. The one genuine event is the bare Break, the beat stripped naked so the next surge of voices strikes harder, the track proving its own claim that raw frequency, hammered past sense, is what loosens the iron.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","party & hedonism"],"means":"vibration as demolition crew loosening iron through raw frequency","built":"the bare Break strips the beat so the next surge of voices strikes harder"},
"the-prodigy~poison": {"fable":"Barely a lyric at all, and that is the point: the track opens on studio chatter — a phone call interrupting Liam Howlett mid-tune — before collapsing into a two-line mantra repeated to hypnosis. The refrain equates the poison and the remedy, then names what the remedy actually is: a pulsating rhythmical one, i.e. the beat itself. So the words fold back into the music, arguing that the drug and the cure are the same throbbing groove, sickness and treatment indistinguishable inside the trance. The near-total refusal of content is the meaning — a mid-90s dance producer saying the track needs no text because the pulse says everything, the human voice reduced to a chant serving the machine.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"the beat named simultaneously as the disease and its own cure","built":"studio phone call interrupts mid-tune before collapsing into the mantra"},
"the-prodigy~one-love-edit": {"fable":"The whole lyric is a single phrase repeated to the point where it stops being a sentiment and becomes a rhythmic cell — a chant sampled and looped rather than sung. That is the point: on the album where The Prodigy set the rave against a hostile establishment, the communal creed of the dancefloor is reduced to a mantra with no argument to defend, meaning carried by the repetition and the pulse instead of by any statement. Read as refrain rather than text, it is unity performed as a beat you obey with your body, the room agreeing before anyone has said what they agree to.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"communal creed reduced to a mantra the body obeys before the mind agrees","built":"unity performed as a beat rather than a statement requiring assent"},
"the-prodigy~no-good-start-the-dance": {"fable":"Built almost entirely on Kelly Charles's diva-house vocal, the track weaponizes a single sampled refusal until it stops meaning rejection and starts meaning self-sufficiency. The couplet moves from a lover's brush-off toward a wider declaration of needing no one, and the loop's relentless repetition is the argument: an assertion of independence only becomes convincing by being said past the point of doubt, until the rave floor absorbs it as a chant rather than a confession. On Jilted Generation this reads as of a piece with the album's stance — a defiant refusal of everyone and everything set to a beat that turns isolation into collective release.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","party & hedonism"],"means":"refusal of a lover looped past doubt into a declaration of self-sufficiency","built":"repetition is the argument — assertion only convinces when said past doubt"},
"the-prodigy~need-some1": {"fable":"There is no lyric to read here, only a single confession looped until it dissolves — and the dissolving is the content. On The Prodigy's late-career dancefloor, the sampled vocal is treated as raw material rather than statement: the plea fractures into stutters and half-syllables, the one at the end of the title clipped down to a numeral, so that a cry of need gets processed into pure rhythmic texture. The song reads the machine's indifference to what it samples — desire fed through the sampler comes out as a hook, the human want ground into a loop that can run forever precisely because it never resolves.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance","love & desire"],"means":"a cry of need processed by the sampler into a hook that never resolves","built":"the word someone clipped to a numeral performing the machine's indifference"},
"the-prodigy~memphis-bells-feat-princess-superstar": {"fable":"There is no lyric here to read so much as a vocal loop to hear as an instrument, which is the point: on Always Outnumbered, Never Outgunned Liam Howlett treats Princess Superstar's voice as another sampled rhythm-cell, a two-count command chopped and gated against the beat until the words stop meaning and start pulsing. The sly innuendo of the licking couplet is deliberately never developed — it dangles as pure suggestion, a hook of provocation with no body attached, because the track wants the tease and not the payoff. What actually moves is the mechanical drift: the tag flickers between putting it on ice and just putting it on, a tiny mutation in an otherwise fixed cell that mimics a stuttering, looping machine finding its own variations. Read straight, the song is about nothing; read as production, it is about how little language a dance record needs before repetition itself becomes the meaning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance"],"means":"tease withheld so the hook runs on pure suggestion","built":"tiny mutation in a fixed vocal cell mimics a stuttering machine"},
"the-prodigy~give-me-a-signal-feat-barns-courtney": {"fable":"This is less a song than a distress call built for a rave, and The Prodigy know it — the whole thing runs on the tension between a plea for connection and the machinery that keeps drowning it out. The signal being begged for is both a rescue flare and a rhythm cue, a demand that the crowd answer back and confirm someone is still receiving. That ambiguity is the point: the repeated question of whether you can be heard reads as panic, but the beat reframes it as a hands-up call-and-response, so terror and euphoria become the same gesture. The stray fragments of atomic emergency and the cavalry riding in sketch a crisis with no resolution, only the perpetual state of being right on the edge — a phrase that outlasts everything else and never resolves off it. In the end the refusal to move past that edge is the whole point: it holds the moment of maximum charge and calls it home.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","party & hedonism"],"means":"terror and euphoria collapsed into the same raised-hands gesture","built":"the track refuses to resolve off the edge it names as home"},
"the-prodigy~funky-shit": {"fable":"There is no lyric here to interpret, only a sampled exclamation of appreciation (lifted from a Beastie Boys hook) set against a chant that is pure rhythmic percussion, and that absence is the whole point. On The Fat of the Land, Liam Howlett treats the voice as another drum machine rather than a vehicle for a message, so the track's only statement is a crowd's involuntary reaction to the groove — the record naming its own effect while it happens. The refusal to say anything is what lets the body take over.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"the crowd's involuntary reaction named while it is still happening","built":"the voice treated as a drum machine rather than a carrier of meaning"},
"the-prodigy~everybody-in-the-place-155-and-rising": {"fable":"There is nothing here to decode because the track refuses to be about anything except the room it plays in — two sampled imperatives, one summoning the crowd and one setting it in motion, cycled until they stop meaning words and become pure function. This is the vocabulary of the early-90s British rave PA stripped to its floor: not a lyric describing a party but the party's own operating instruction, the MC's call that exists only to point at the beat and the bodies. The parenthetical 155-and-rising is the real text — a BPM readout as a manifesto — and it tells you the vocal was never the point; Liam Howlett's Experience-era hardcore is about tempo as a rising thing you chase, and the repetition is the mechanism, each return of the phrase the acceleration made audible. The refusal itself is the statement: the song withholds interior meaning on purpose, because at 155 the individual dissolves and everybody being in the place is the only statement left worth making.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"tempo as a rising thing that dissolves the individual into the room","built":"the BPM readout in the title is the track's only real manifesto"},
"the-prodigy~charly-trip-into-drum-and-bass-version": {"fable":"The entire vocal is one lifted fragment — a British public-information cartoon cat warning children to tell their mothers before wandering off — looped until the safety instruction curdles into something menacing. That is the whole joke and the whole meaning: a rave anthem built from the language of parental control, the nanny-state voice hijacked and made to preside over exactly the kind of unsupervised night it was meant to prevent. As a drum-and-bass rework the sample means even more, because there is nothing left to develop except velocity; the reassurance loops and accelerates until the words stop reading as care and start reading as a taunt. Liberation is the point, and it works by turning the sound of being told what to do into the trigger for doing the opposite.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The tabloids heard the era’s slang — Charlie — in it; Howlett has always said the idea was simpler, a voice from your childhood giving good advice in the middle of a rave.","themes":["freedom & escape","surveillance & control"],"means":"a safety instruction looped until it triggers the thing it warned against","built":"acceleration strips the parental-control sample of all its care"},
"the-prodigy~break-and-enter": {"fable":"A functionally instrumental track whose only vocal is one sampled command looped past the point of meaning, so everything rests on what the repetition performs rather than what it says. Landing early on Music for the Jilted Generation — Liam Howlett's answer to the anti-rave Criminal Justice Bill and its clampdown on unlicensed gatherings — the burgling title and the shattering-glass, siren-laced sound design frame the party itself as trespass, a forced entry into space the state wanted sealed. The chanted directive to bring it down to earth works as both comedown and grounding: the euphoria is deliberately pulled back toward menace, setting the record's mood as a raid rather than a celebration.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","politics & society","party & hedonism"],"means":"the party framed as trespass against a state-sealed space","built":"shattering-glass sound design makes the dancefloor a forced entry"},
"alice-in-chains~would-2022-remaster":{"fable":"Cantrell wrote this as an elegy for Andrew Wood, the Mother Love Bone singer lost to heroin, and refracts the grief into a challenge aimed at anyone who would judge a dead friend's fall. The verses submit to a master and speak of teaching a child about love that comes after death — the drug and the overdose recast as devotion and afterlife, a private vocabulary outsiders were never meant to decode. The chorus keeps dropping the speaker back beneath rising water, each relapse the identical descent, the one plea being that a listener stand in his position before condemning. Closing Dirt, an album that lived inside addiction, the song abandons statement for the outro's stacked questions about having drifted past return and stranded someone behind, then lands on a bargain thrown across the gap between the living and the lost: if he took that step, would the other follow.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","addiction & self-destruction"],"built":"the outro's unanswered bargain throws a question across the gap between living and dead"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~avon-live": {"fable":"Josh Homme delivers this from inside a predator's unbothered calm, a watcher trailing someone across open ground while deliberately ignoring the warning sounds of the world around them. The menace is bureaucratic: he reduces whatever he does to his quarry to a tidy, repeatable procedure — the verbs stay coy about the harm, which is its own kind of threat. His contempt turns people into penned livestock and mass-produced bodies stripped of any self, as though individuality were a joke and culling the obvious conclusion. The chorus then withholds itself entirely, stuttering a single truncated syllable that keeps sprinting toward a word it never reaches, so the track enacts its own threat by trapping the listener in the approach and refusing any release.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"predation rendered as a tidy, repeatable procedure","built":"chorus withholds itself, stuttering one truncated syllable and never landing"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~monsters-in-the-parasol-live": {"fable":"This is a nightmare of intimacy mutating into something inhuman, staged as a haunted-house cartoon where a neighbor's father festers and boils and a sister turns extraterrestrial while the singer's own head drifts loose from its moorings. The engine is the chorus's call-and-response: each accusation of a hidden creature draws a curt little answer — distress, divorce, displeasure — so the freak show quietly resolves into the ordinary machinery of a family and a couple coming apart. By the final turn the creature stops being a thing lurking in the folds of a delicate sunshade and becomes an absence, a gap torn open in the beloved that the narrator admits he failed to register until it was gaping, which recasts every earlier grotesque as a way of not looking straight at the loss.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"First cut as Monster in the Parasol on Desert Sessions Volume 4; Homme has introduced it onstage as a song about LSD.","themes":["heartbreak & loss","family & blood","madness & the mind"],"means":"haunted-house grotesque resolves into ordinary family collapse","built":"call-and-response chorus pairs each monster accusation with a domestic answer"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~you-think-i-ain-t-worth-a-dollar-but-i-feel-like-a-millionaire-with-out-interlude": {"fable":"The opening DJ chatter names the enemy up front: a corporate station engineered to blur into the background, blandness on a loop, mocking the very record it introduces. Against that beige void the song offers nothing but hunger, a rubble of tough-guy scraps that never cohere into a sentence, each one bolted to a single rising plea to keep the fix coming. The bullfight image is the pivot, the singer both the taunting matador and the animal drained on the sand, so the title's flush-broke bravado turns out to mean being the creature emptied for a crowd's amusement. Everything eventually caves into that one chant for more, looped past meaning, until the demand is all that survives and the appetite has swallowed its own object.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","artifice & performance"],"means":"flush-broke bravado exposed as being the creature emptied for a crowd","built":"bullfight image pivots the speaker from matador to drained animal in one frame"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~i-m-designer-unkle-remix": {"fable":"A sneering anatomy of the sellout that denies itself any moral platform by putting the speaker on the auction block ahead of everyone else. Homme fuses the streetwalker and the skilled craftsman into a single swaggering pose, letting the track ridicule both the cash-in artist and the purist who pretends to sit above the market, snaring each in the same fib that outrage at a cheap bid only proves the deal was struck long ago. The pivot arrives with the couture conceit: bespoke, made to order, tailored — the very definition of a commodity, so the promise of ceaseless reinvention doubles as a fashion house's slogan, art and advertising fused past telling apart. Beneath the bluster hums a quieter figure, the recurring line that some worth stays outside every ledger and belongs to no buyer at all, letting a record marinated in dot-com greed confess that the thing genuinely of value was never a stock item, least of all for the suit brokering the sale.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","money & the street","identity & becoming"],"means":"sellout anatomy that puts the speaker on the auction block first","built":"couture conceit — bespoke, made to order — doubles as a commodity's slogan"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~keep-your-eyes-peeled-like-clockwork": {"fable":"Survival is worn here as a threat rather than a gift: stay vigilant precisely because being awake is no longer safe. Homme cleaves himself into a grinning performer and a body going under, the bared grimace and the constant assurance that he's coping standing in for a numbness he finds worse than the flatline he escaped. The pleading to be shaken awake reads not as comfort but as a demand for rescue from that anesthetized calm, where a serene sky glimpsed from the underside of hell turns menacing and the water that promised release becomes suffocation, a thrashing figure surfacing only to be swallowed again. Its parting reversal, that the world is never what it advertises, exposes the smile as camouflage and locates the real horror in having simply come back to breathe.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","artifice & performance","suicide"],"means":"survival worn as menace rather than relief","built":"serene sky glimpsed from hell's underside turns menacing as the water suffocates"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~you-re-so-vague": {"fable":"The title flips Carly Simon's You're So Vain, and the swap is the whole joke: where Simon skewered a lover's ego, Homme reworks her famous punchline (the borrowed line about probably thinking the song isn't about you) into a charge of unpindownable evasiveness, so the target's crime is being impossible to read rather than self-obsessed. The verses sketch that slipperiness through motion and predatory glamour, a beauty that walks crooked to dodge and leaves a mess it never has to answer for, ruling the room by baring its fangs. The trap is that vagueness cuts both ways: it names the target's refusal to be known and the singer's own refusal to say plainly whether this is an accusation or a come-on, keeping contempt and attraction fused in a single Rated-R-era leer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"vagueness as the target's crime and the singer's own evasion","built":"title flips Carly Simon's punchline so the charge becomes unreadability, not ego"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~you-got-a-killer-scene-there-man": {"fable":"A swaggering self-portrait of the burned-out lifer who claims to despise the very stage-ritual he never stops performing, cinching his own blindfold tighter as if disgust and appetite were the same knotted muscle. The fatalism becomes a strange kind of permission: since the grave levels every choice, loving harder or colder cancels out, and the last provocation left is the taunt to prove you have a scene worth watching before you go. Josh Homme flips outsider mythology into paradox, gathering flagless and homeless castoffs into a fellowship whose only bond is refusing all fellowship, a scarred coven that owns the wreckage and then, grinning, cues it up once more. The bravado finally teeters on a single sliver of conviction he swears he'd still honor, and that thin plank is both the punchline and the buried honesty holding the whole strut upright.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","death & grief","artifice & performance"],"means":"fatalism used as permission to love harder or colder equally","built":"fellowship of outsiders defined entirely by refusing all fellowship"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~what-the-peephole-say": {"fable":"A snarl of refusal aimed at anyone knocking to correct, recruit, or supervise, delivered from behind a bolted door by a man who guards a sliver of daylight and one loose fist. Homme sets private rot against public menace: indoctrination that only fertilized filthier notions, the sneering test of preacher versus poser, curdle into a shrug that the classroom and the cellblock wear the same face. The predator prowls while the flock spins painted circles and the appointed guardian dozes through the culling, until the grievance stops being one cornered voice and hands the microphone to a mutinous we — torch-lighting truants who flip the spyhole, so the fearful watcher is abruptly the one being watched and warned the reckoning is due.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","surveillance & control","politics & society"],"means":"cornered voice flips the spyhole so the watcher becomes the watched","built":"singular grievance widens to a mutinous we as the microphone changes hands"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~time-and-place": {"fable":"Homme welds seduction to disgust so tightly that the come-on reads as an autopsy of the person being wanted, someone all vanity and borrowed courage, hollow where a heart should sit. The figures carry the argument: an emptiness inside the chest that keeps forging its own ache, a half-smoked stub of a lover whose remaining appeal is the tail-end, whose sweet vows evaporate the moment they leave the mouth. Because everything about the beloved has already gone rotten, the only thing on offer is a blunt insistence on the immediate encounter, the here-and-now as the last unspoiled scrap of a future that arrives as pure ballast. The closing profanity doesn't sharpen the invitation; it confesses fatigue, want reduced to a reflex extended toward a body that is quietly combusting.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"seduction offered as autopsy of the person being wanted","built":"closing profanity confesses fatigue, want reduced to a reflex"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~straight-jacket-fitting": {"fable":"Homme reframes conformity as a tailoring appointment: you arrive to be measured for the sanctioned life the way you would for a suit, only the garment cinches harder the more precisely it flatters you. From a private doorway panic — a man talking himself past the threshold while the familiar order gutters low — the song widens into a scorched census of every faction peddling meaning: pulpits, wellness gurus, boardroom marauders, the flag-wavers and the fermented-yogurt set, all reduced to interchangeable static. Against that racket he sets a Latin pun on seizing the day, the record's actual creed: you don't subdue your inner monsters by staring them down, you unshackle them and claim them as yours. The final plea to be mended folds into the image of an obedient animal settling to the floor, the ambush sprung, because being cured here means being domesticated with slicker packaging. Written after Homme's divorce and cancer scare, the gallows humor reads less as posture than as a man concluding that his affliction belongs to him more truly than any salvation on offer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","politics & society","madness & the mind"],"means":"conformity reframed as a tailoring appointment that cinches harder when it flatters","built":"final plea to be mended resolves as an obedient animal settling to the floor"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~someone-s-in-the-wolf": {"fable":"Homme reworks the Grimm-woods dread threaded through Lullabies to Paralyze, telling the fable of the beast among the trees from the hunter's side: a bent figure whose grin only serves to sheathe its teeth, a blade already wet before anyone is offered a hand. The menace lives in the hospitality itself, a host's warm invitation whose promise of never leaving is exactly the vow a captor makes over prey that will not walk out again. When that welcome breaks into stammered fragments near the close, the courtesy sloughs off and leaves only the shutting jaw, the greeting exposed as the snare it always was.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","surveillance & control"],"means":"hunter's warm hospitality exposed as the captor's vow","built":"courtesy sloughs off near the close as greeting fragments into a shutting jaw"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~sick-sick-sick": {"fable":"Homme sings appetite with no apology and no exit — a hymn to the risk itself rather than any object of it, where love, sex, and self-destruction collapse into a single reckless verb. The title's diagnosis is the point: sick is offered as a boast, not a symptom, and the mantra to not resist is aimed as much at the listener as the lover, daring you to enjoy a filth the song refuses to feel guilty about. What tips it from swagger into something colder is the muttered undercurrent late on, a plain admission that the speaker will take and simply does not want to change or choose — the eclipse and the forever-midnight aren't romance but a chosen darkness with the clock stopped, pleasure as a permanent refusal of consequence. Underneath the leer, then, is stasis dressed as thrill: the addict's logic that mistakes never stopping for freedom.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","party & hedonism","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"sickness offered as a boast, not a symptom","built":"eclipse and forever-midnight dress stasis as thrill, clock stopped on purpose"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~negative-space": {"fable":"Homme takes the astronomical title as governing conceit: negative space is the shaped absence a departed body leaves behind, and the whole song is voiced from inside that hollow. The imagery keeps losing its footing — a self untethered from a planet that spins on without him, buoyant and pulverized at once, with no thread left to haul him down toward solid ground — so the breakup registers less as rage than as an astronaut cut loose, watching the porch light dwindle to nothing. What raises it past ordinary heartbreak is the late swerve, where sorrow hardens into scorn: the plea to stop being told about love curdles into a charge that the other's whole tenderness was forged, and the parting line finds the true wound — the loss was never the person but the discovery that no trustworthy person had ever stood there. The oblivion he keeps staring down is exactly that verdict, the confirmed vacancy where the bond once was.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness","anger & defiance"],"means":"grief as an astronaut cut loose, not as rage","built":"title's astronomical conceit governs: the whole song voices the shaped absence"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~made-to-parade": {"fable":"Homme frames corporate striving as a rigged exchange, where the aspirant silences objection and averts his gaze from cruelty to buy a payoff that never covers the losses it extracts. The perspective then curdles: he becomes the promoted man he loathes, sneering down at the laborers he once toiled beside from a saddle of borrowed height. The turn arrives when restraint rots into confession, the burden he hauled for years going unfelt until it drops away, his devotion to the firm exposed as something close to prayer. A sudden hush recalls the untouched self who woke before the spectacle began, one clean sunrise and a blank sheet not yet spent on the company. That memory fuels the final refusal, a rejection of the whole procession of soured ambition; falling in step behind it would only mean going under alongside the one who leads.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","politics & society","shame & repression"],"means":"devotion to the firm exposed as something close to prayer","built":"perspective curdles mid-song as the speaker becomes the promoted man he loathed"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~long-slow-goodbye": {"fable":"The cruel inversion here is that the one still standing in the doorway has become the spectral figure, haunting a familiar street and a silent telephone while the person who actually left registers only as a felt absence. Josh Homme drops the sardonic swagger entirely and plays the insomnia straight: eyes shut against a room that refuses sleep, every stranger's voice mistaken for the missing one, the body rolling through hours it cannot end. What the title enacts is a farewell metabolized as duration rather than a clean severance, the word itself fraying with each repetition until it wears down to a single syllable and dissolves, so the parting is measured not in a moment but in the unbearable stretch of waiting for a return that will not arrive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"the one still standing becomes the haunting spectral figure","built":"title word frays with each repetition, wearing down to a syllable then dissolving"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~i-never-came": {"fable":"A breakup song that keeps insisting the thing being killed refuses to stay dead. The first half is a litany of failed executions, knife and poison and fire and rope all named and dismissed, so that the love (or the resentment tangled up with it) survives every attempt to finish it off. Homme's real move is the title's pun: the refrain that sounds like a lover's boast is actually a confession of never having been present, never truly arrived, so the leaving cannot even be called leaving because he was never there to begin with. That reframes the whole thing from a wronged party walking out into a colder admission that the bond was hollow on his side the entire time, the ball-and-chain permanence exposed as something he stood outside of.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","alienation & emptiness","shame & repression"],"means":"confession of never having arrived, so leaving cannot even be called leaving","built":"title pun pivots the song from wronged party walking out to hollow bond exposed"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~how-to-handle-a-rope": {"fable":"Addiction sung as a lesson in knots, the title cord doubling as the lifeline tossed to a drowning friend and the noose that same friend keeps fashioning from it. Homme circles a burnout adrift in an ephedrine fog, the trucker-speed stimulant that fueled this Palm Desert scene, begging them to undo the damage while the image of a slit wrist confesses how narrow the gap between saving and surrender has worn thin. Its cleverest turn is a mirror: every charge the first half throws outward the second half catches and pins on the singer instead, so pusher and victim collapse into one interchangeable figure, each stone deaf to the alarm shrieked into their face. The closing refrain frays into repetition, less a phrase than the barrier the track keeps colliding with, everyone fluent in the craft of hanging and nobody reachable enough to be pulled back down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","suicide","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"pusher and victim collapse into one interchangeable figure","built":"second half catches and pins the first half's charges back onto the singer"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~carnavoyeur": {"fable":"Homme reframes mortality as spectatorship: the title fuses carnivore and voyeur, and the speaker settles on the vulture as his self-image, the scavenger who lives by attending other creatures' endings. The song moves from emptiness and the refusal to grieve toward a strange serenity, the flight image doing the work — leaving the nest, gliding, discovering there are no more peaks left to conquer, which lands less as defeat than as release from the climbing itself. What could be despair is repeatedly rerouted into a smile whenever action runs out, so the read is not that nothing matters but that watching, undefended, becomes the only honest posture once striving ends. Coming from a man who nearly died before making this record, the vulture is confessional rather than nihilistic: bearing witness to death, including your own coming one, is treated as its own unending form of life.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","transcendence & consciousness","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"vulture as self-image: watching death becomes its own form of life","built":"title fuses carnivore and voyeur, pinning the speaker's posture in one word"},
"queens-of-the-stone-age~3-s-and-7-s": {"fable":"A song that treats the lie as a survival technology, and does it with a con man's grin. The opening isn't a lament but a request — beg to be deceived, because the truth is only a wound and comfort is the only currency worth taking to the grave. Halfway through, the speaker stops asking to be lied to and becomes the liar himself: the bluff nobody calls, the fraud whose smooth performance lets everyone conveniently forget what he actually did, until deception curdles into a private religion where getting away with it feels like grace. In the Era Vulgaris register — Homme's vulgar-age sneer at a culture that prefers the flattering surface — the title's gambler's odds land as the point: the whole thing is a rigged hand played with a straight face, and the closing chant of making us all forget is the mark being worked in real time.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","politics & society"],"means":"deception adopted as survival technology, then as private religion","built":"closing chant performs the con on the listener in real time"},
"acid-drinkers~love-shack-2019":{"fable":"A faithful cover of the B-52's 1989 party anthem, so the words stay put: a whale-sized car of friends barreling down a Georgia highway toward a ramshackle roadside hideaway where love rules and the whole building physically quakes with dancing. Meaning here lives less in the lyric than in the gesture — Acid Drinkers, a Polish thrash band, park this on Fishdick Zwei, their record of metal reworkings of pop hits. A crew built on aggression bear-hugging the campiest good-time song imaginable becomes affectionate proof that raucous euphoria and heavy volume are one appetite wearing different clothes, the corroded-metal punchline landing as pure joyful nonsense.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","artifice & performance"],"means":"aggression and campiness meet as one appetite in different clothes","built":"metal band bear-hugging the campiest good-time song becomes the punchline"},
"acid-drinkers~you-better-shoot-me": {"fable":"A man kept awake by someone he cannot shake reframes a bullet as a favor: since the harassment steals every hour of sleep, death is pitched as the only unbroken rest still on offer. The barbed comedy lands when the endearment honey shares a line with the plea to be killed, so the tormentor turns out to be an intimate, and the whole grievance shrinks to the sour exhaustion of a couple worn past the point of fighting. Congratulating the other party on their thoroughness, mock-graciously, is the pivot from complaint to shrug, that gallows deadpan Acid Drinkers favor where a genuine howl might sit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","comedy & wordplay","death & grief"],"means":"a bullet pitched as the only unbroken rest still available","built":"honey and the plea to be killed share a line, shrinking the tormentor to an intimate"},
"acid-drinkers~wild-thing": {"fable":"A cover of Chip Taylor's 1965 garage-rock standard, best known through the Troggs, and Acid Drinkers take it exactly for what it always was: three chords and a hormonal lunge dressed up as a love song. The lyric's whole comedy is in the gap between the grand claim and the hedge that immediately undercuts it — the singer thinks he loves her but wants to know for sure, which is desire pretending to be sentiment and not quite pulling it off. Nothing develops; the same verse circles back because the point is the itch, not the resolution, and for a Polish thrash band this monosyllabic English is a wink — the song is dumb on purpose, and playing it loud is the entire joke.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"desire pretending to be sentiment and not quite pulling it off","built":"the verse circles back because the itch is the point and resolution never arrives"},
"acid-drinkers~whole-lotta-rosie": {"fable":"A cover of AC/DC's Bon Scott-era anthem, and Acid Drinkers treat the source's tall-tale bravado as a gift rather than a target — the song was always a boast that inflates a real lover into legend, the tape-measure figures and impossible weight functioning as folk-hero exaggeration rather than mockery. What makes the boast work is that the amazement runs the other way: the singer is the one outmatched, insisting he's never met her equal and admitting his surprise that her stamina never flags, so the swagger is really awe wearing a grin. In a Polish thrash band's hands the tribute is the point — a Poznań crew planting their flag in the exact ground where blues-brag rock and roll and heavy metal shake hands.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","party & hedonism"],"means":"swagger revealed as awe at the one who always outmatches the boaster","built":"a Poznań thrash band planting their flag where blues-brag and heavy metal shake hands"},
"acid-drinkers~when-you-say-to-me-fuck-you": {"fable":"Control here wears the mask of devotion: the tormentor tracks each gesture and syllable, muttering disapproval instead of striking, so the cruelty stays deniable and therefore inescapable. The strangest ache is the speaker's craving for the open curse — he wants the hatred spoken at full volume, because a named insult would be cleaner than the perpetual audit of a jailer posing as a mentor. As the grip tightens the imagery curdles into a bestiary, a serpent whispering in the ear and spiders creeping across a sleeping face, until the watcher's breath and shadow are grafted onto the speaker's own body. That fusion is the horror: the demand for a louder verdict is really a plea to have the abuse pronounced aloud, so the victim might finally prove to himself that it was ever there.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","shame & repression","violence & murder"],"means":"craving the open insult because named hatred would be cleaner than perpetual audit","built":"the watcher's shadow and breath fuse onto the victim's own body by the final image"},
"acid-drinkers~what-a-day": {"fable":"A thrash outfit stages an improbably devotional scene: a king who surrenders his reign and sits beneath an empty crown, treating patience not as idleness but as a vigil kept against the promise of dawn. The shrug at forty days or nine reincarnations dissolves any gap between penitent and lucky cat, and California surf becomes shorthand for the escapist paradise the speaker refuses. Its sharpest move is theological rather than muscular: the merciless tormentors that steal a person's calm are formidable yet breakable, and the two gentle children sent to rout them, tenderness and a bowed head, carry more force than any riff, arguing that raw power is exactly the wrong armament.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","transcendence & consciousness","freedom & escape"],"means":"tenderness and a bowed head argued as more forceful than any riff","built":"a penitent and a lucky cat dissolved into the same figure by a shrug"},
"acid-drinkers~walkway-to-heaven": {"fable":"An escalating litany of grievance built entirely on the conditional if you really loved me, the speaker itemizing every way a partner keeps falling short until the demands themselves expose the neediness underneath the complaint. The title is the tell: it deflates Led Zeppelin's Stairway to Heaven into a walkway that cannot be bought because it does not exist, and the refrain insists there are no stairs to climb, only presence — you just walk there. Acid Drinkers turn a bruised love song into a flatly delivered thesis that devotion is not a transaction or a monument you purchase your way into; the accumulating conditions read less as the partner's failures than as the singer's own bottomless ledger of what he is owed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"On this record's altar-call stretch the jealous sweetheart flips: the voice waiting in the hall while you hang on other arms reads as the divine doing the complaining — and the walkway that can't be bought is the tell.","themes":["love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"accumulating conditions expose the singer's own bottomless ledger of what he is owed","built":"the title deflates a classic monument into a walkway that cannot be purchased"},
"acid-drinkers~wake-up-here-come-the-acids": {"fable":"A self-mythologizing calling-card in which the band announces itself as a force pounding at your door before dawn — the Acids arriving as both party and home invasion. The pre-dawn clock does the structural work: each hour (four, five, six) ratchets the intruder from a possible knock into an inescapable presence who has unplugged the phone and won't be leaving, so the joke curdles from nuisance into siege. The deadpan gag that the caller could be starting a world war or just borrowing sugar is the engine of the whole thing: it holds total menace and total triviality in the same breath, which is exactly the pose thrash-with-a-grin bands like Acid Drinkers built a career on. The Purple Haze nod cashes the pun the band's name has been sitting on — acid as chemistry, acid as the group — turning the wake-up call into a boast that once they show up, sleep is off the table for days.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay","party & hedonism"],"means":"total menace and total triviality held in the same breath","built":"the Purple Haze nod cashes the pun the band's name has always carried"},
"acid-drinkers~waitin-for-the-hair": {"fable":"Sung by a Polish thrash outfit in gloriously mangled English, the song stages torment as cartoon: fifty bears on the neck, an executioner, apostles dispatched by a paranoid city that tracks its quarry like a living creature. The garbled surface is the joke — menace and gibberish become interchangeable, and the narrator grins that he asked for the beating because he is a fool. The title holds the one accessible key — the hair being waited for is the dog's, the morning dose that calls the beasts off. A closing letter from Fago pleads poverty, unable to afford loaves, a bottle, or protection for the night, puncturing every gothic threat into the flat comedy of an empty wallet and unmet appetites.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Rocca — the uniformed madman with his own song on this record — walks through this one too, promising vengeance in Rome. The debut runs on a private cast.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","comedy & wordplay","surveillance & control"],"means":"gothic menace dissolved into the flat comedy of an empty wallet","built":"mangled English makes gibberish and threat interchangeable throughout"},
"acid-drinkers~united-suicide-legion": {"fable":"The queasy engine here is the gap between a watching narrator and the massed, mute bodies he passes on the pavement, people who barely feed or rest and get flattened into one interchangeable pronoun. What lifts it past pity is the drift into administrative language: a regiment name for the destitute, then a single line yoking the drafted, the ordinary, and the profiteers, as if all three sat inside one payroll. Talk of who survives and which hand gets dealt reframes visible ruin as a tidy contest with rules and outcomes, and that borrowed managerial cool is the true horror — a culture that has ruled this ending inevitable rather than chosen. When the recurring wrecked-slumber refrain slips a beat later into something self-destructive, the chant quietly confesses these ends are being processed, not merely imagined.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","alienation & emptiness","death & grief"],"means":"visible ruin processed through managerial cool as inevitable rather than chosen","built":"a regiment name for the destitute imposes administrative language on human wreckage"},
"acid-drinkers~too-many-cops": {"fable":"A portrait of the born outlaw as a boy nobody wanted — disowned by father and mother alike, he becomes the thing everyone talks about precisely because he was never claimed. Acid Drinkers stack the recurring tag of the walking lie against the boy's own defiance, so the song refuses to decide whether he is a genuine menace or just the scapegoat a whole town needs to gossip about; the cops and the suckers are the same crowd, feeding on him. The jeering chorus turns the knife on the audience: everyone is watching, everyone is disgusted, and the closing aside that he probably does not even enjoy the squalor punctures the outlaw glamour the verses build, leaving a kid who was cast as a legend before he could be a person.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","family & blood","surveillance & control","identity & becoming"],"means":"the outlaw legend assembled by a town that never actually claimed him","built":"closing aside that he dislikes the squalor punctures the glamour the verses build"},
"acid-drinkers~there-s-so-much-hatred-in-the-air": {"fable":"Hostility is treated here as a climate rather than a target, a toxic humidity pressing on the lungs and, in the last punchline, so saturating the yard that wet clothes never dry on the line. The narrator meets that pressure with pointed anticlimax: dismissed by a friend as having gone off the rails, he declines the violence the mood keeps demanding and chooses instead to shut himself away, so the refrain reads as a man clamping down on his own fuse. The detour about balding countrymen who pump iron and misplace their humor is a sideways diagnosis, the same seepage of aggression and grim self-importance the atmosphere manufactures, played for the deadpan absurdity Acid Drinkers made their trademark. Beneath the tough-guy swagger sits plain fatigue at breathing a low-grade hostility with no release valve but a joke.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","alienation & emptiness","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"hostility met with deliberate withdrawal rather than retaliation","built":"wet laundry punchline deflates the toxic atmosphere into the mundane"},
"acid-drinkers~the-wildest-planet-in-space": {"fable":"The title is the thesis delivered as insult: Earth is the wildest planet not in the sense of thrilling but of feral, the one address in the cosmos no visitor with sense would ever choose to land on. The song builds by enumeration — a numbered ledger of world wars that keeps climbing past the two history has counted, then a litany of pettier feuds sorted by body type and hair and the price of the wine being fought over — so that catastrophe and idiocy get flattened onto the same scale, genocide and a bar quarrel racked up as the same species of violence. Against this Acid Drinkers set the recurring image of a humanity that stargazes like a five-year-old whose mind has not yet traveled far, and the irony turns on that gap: we scan the heavens hoping for contact while remaining exactly the childish, bloody-minded creatures any clear-eyed observer up there would swerve to avoid. The refusal to arrive becomes the punchline — the aliens' absence isn't mystery but verdict, a cosmic quarantine we earned.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","war & battle","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"alien absence rendered as a cosmic quarantine humanity earned","built":"genocide and bar brawl flattened onto the same numbered ledger"},
"acid-drinkers~street-rockin": {"fable":"Squalor gets flipped into a coat of arms here: the vermin and the sky's daily downpour of muck are catalogued not as suffering but as credentials the singer wears with a sneer. Refusal is the whole grammar of the thing — no gift, no counsel, no tailored suit — each dismissal hardening a single conviction that clean living is the actual insult being fended off. The gutter becomes real estate he claims outright, the invitation doubling as a threat that turns having nothing into a form of standing his ground.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","money & the street","identity & becoming"],"means":"turning squalor into credentials worn with a sneer","built":"the refusal grammar stacks dismissals until having nothing becomes standing ground"},
"acid-drinkers~streap-tease": {"fable":"The title bends a burlesque act into an interrogation: the speaker offers no seduction but a forced undressing, peeling piety off a target until only calculation shows. Each noble word the mark reaches for — tolerance, brotherhood, friendship — is treated as cloth hiding a blade, so the true subject becomes the distance between borrowed virtue and the cash-minded self it drapes. Then the accuser flips from moralist to jeering vulgarian, brandishing his own crotch at the man he has stripped, which quietly admits the whole unmasking was another grab for dominance. Acid Drinkers run this in their signature seam where thrash fury and a filthy punchline share the same breath, so the pummeling refrain lands as sincere disgust and a smirk at the truth-teller's pose at once.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"unmasking borrowed virtue as cover for the cash-minded self beneath","built":"the accuser flips to jeering vulgarian, quietly admitting the grab for dominance"},
"acid-drinkers~stick-around": {"fable":"A come-on that keeps tripping over its own menace: the speaker wants his partner not to stray, and his reason is that before dawn every murderer will be walking free. The tenderness of sitting by the phone waiting for his call is threaded with a threat he never quite claims as his own. The second half trades the paranoia for a booze-and-bar idyll — meet me off the highway, spin around the floor to a Thin Lizzy tune. Yet that borrowed song turns on a woman disarming her outlaw for the taking, so the promised good time carries the same undertow of who the real danger is. The looped plea wears the grammar of possessiveness down to a stammer, until the request to remain stops sounding protective and starts sounding like the snare those loose murderers were only ever a pretext for.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"looped plea to remain stops sounding protective and starts sounding like a snare","built":"borrowed Thin Lizzy tune carries the same undertow as the opening threat"},
"acid-drinkers~solid-rock": {"fable":"A thrash band pleads for a companion who would surrender everything on your behalf, and the plea keeps landing on gospel bedrock: the solid rock is the psalmist's refuge, the immovable God to whom a drowning man clings. The same interrogation returns three times, each pass raising the price from a friend who won't betray you, to one who answers your cry, to one who lays down his own life; the mounting demand quietly redraws that ordinary comrade as a Christ figure. What poses as a want-ad for loyalty is a covert profession of faith, the chorus confessing bare need while the verses only interrogate, until the hunger for human dependability resolves into the single bond the band trusts to outlast every doubt.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","friendship & loyalty"],"means":"a want-ad for loyalty resolving into covert profession of faith","built":"mounting price of loyalty quietly redraws the comrade as a Christ figure"},
"acid-drinkers~sociopath": {"fable":"The trick is a portrait built entirely from a diseased head's own voice, so the listener meets the demagogue only through the filth he broadcasts about everyone else. A morning already ruined by television atrocity sets the diagnosis: a strutting clown-tyrant, half priapic warlord and half joke, whose menace the chorus reframes as slow attrition rather than any decisive strike. The last verse hands the microphone over completely, letting a torrent of ethnic slurs and national caricatures pour out with no narrator standing between it and us. This Polish band, weaponizing crude English on purpose, impersonates the bigot instead of blessing him, insisting the vileness is his substance and that refusing to launder it exposes how petty, doomed, and self-cancelling such hatred really is.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","artifice & performance"],"means":"impersonating the bigot to expose how petty and self-cancelling hatred is","built":"the final verse surrenders the microphone entirely to the demagogue's slurs"},
"acid-drinkers~smoke-on-the-water": {"fable":"Deep Purple's 1972 standard is the least mythological of rock myths: a plain tour diary of the night a fan's stray pyrotechnic set the Montreux casino's rattan ceiling ablaze and the band scrambled to finish recording in a cold, emptied hotel. It earns endurance by refusing metaphor, since the blaze, the mobile studio parked outside, and Funky Claude hauling patrons clear are all literal, with only the drifting haze over Lake Geneva turning reportage into elegy. For a Polish thrash band, covering it faithful and unaltered is the entire statement: claiming one of metal's foundational riffs as a bar-band ritual, an act of lineage rather than reinterpretation, where meaning becomes fidelity itself and you play the sacred text exactly as written to belong to the tradition that wrote it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["nostalgia & memory","nature & the elements"],"means":"playing the sacred text faithfully to claim lineage in the tradition","built":"refusal of metaphor turns a tour diary into founding myth"},
"acid-drinkers~seek-and-destroy": {"fable":"This cover of a Metallica thrash staple keeps the hunt's predatory logic intact: an appetite fixed before any target exists, roaming a city at night for someone to break. The menace claims no grievance, only a malice it concedes was already there long before tonight, a standing condition rather than a provocation, and that admission is what makes the stalk genuinely sinister instead of merely loud. Its lone moral gesture, that the prey has always taken and now must repay, is thin cover for a mind locked onto a single verb, and by the chorus even the language of pursuit burns off, meaning drained down to the two-word command itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","night & the city","anger & defiance"],"means":"appetite fixed before any target exists as a standing condition","built":"the chorus burns language off until only the two-word command remains"},
"acid-drinkers~seasons-in-the-abyss-slayer-cover": {"fable":"A Polish crew's take on Slayer's Hanneman-Araya set-piece, and the lyric asks to be read as collapsed distance: the coaxing second person works at once as a killer's hypnotic murmur and a victim's dissolving mind, so the title's abyss is less a pit than the mental interval where those two vantage points stop being separable. The dread is deliberately a matter of craft, not frenzy, since incision and evisceration arrive with a surgeon's or an aesthete's calm, the spilled aftermath curated like a gallery piece and the killing reframed as devotional rite. That connoisseurship is the argument being prosecuted: reason erodes here not under rage but under patient, appreciative attention to the deed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","madness & the mind","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"reason eroding under patient appreciative attention to the deed","built":"second-person collapses killer and victim into one vantage point"},
"acid-drinkers~satisfaction": {"fable":"Jagger and Richards built this as the sound of a man sold to from every direction at once — radio pitchman, TV detergent hawk, the whole mid-century advertising apparatus promising a fulfillment it structurally cannot deliver. The refusal to be satisfied reads less as complaint than as diagnosis: the machine is engineered to keep the itch open. What makes it strange in Acid Drinkers' hands is context, a Polish band who came up as the Iron Curtain fell replaying the West's foundational anthem of consumer restlessness just as that same machine finally reached Warsaw. The outsider tries on the insider's disillusionment, so promise and letdown arrive fused. The verses hold a quiet arc from public bombardment inward to a private strikeout with a woman who postpones him to some vague later date, and the frustration lands where the pitch always aimed it — on the self that keeps trying and keeps ending up empty-handed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","politics & society","love & desire"],"means":"outsider tries on the insider's disillusionment as they arrive fused","built":"the Polish context fuses consumer promise and letdown in one gesture"},
"acid-drinkers~run-run-away": {"fable":"A cover of Slade's 1984 hit whose governing figure is the chameleon: a creature that survives by matching whatever surrounds it, admired for the trick and distrusted for the same reason. The verses stack little tests of compatibility — shared taste, shared appetite, shared impatience — and every one snaps back into the flinch of the title, so the refrain plays as reflex rather than welcome, the urge to bolt the instant intimacy might be asked for. Acid Drinkers, whose Fishdick runs its covers deadpan through a thrash engine, land the joke and the thesis together: a glam singalong about never settling on one shade gets rendered with total commitment, at brutal volume.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["freedom & escape","love & desire","identity & becoming"],"means":"flinching from intimacy the instant it might be asked for","built":"the chameleon metaphor swallows the refrain into pure reflex"},
"acid-drinkers~ring-of-fire-live": {"fable":"As June Carter told it, she wrote this watching herself fall for a man she knew would burn her, and the lyric's whole trick is that the ring of fire is not warmth but descent — love as a downward plunge through flame, sweetness and destruction named in the same breath. Johnny Cash famously masked that dread under bright mariachi horns, letting the buoyant arrangement carry the terror. Acid Drinkers, taking it live, drop the mask entirely: a Polish thrash band has no interest in the irony, only in the fire, so the repeated fall becomes exactly the annihilation the words always described. The cover works because the song was metal underneath all along — it just needed a band willing to play the burning straight.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"playing the burning straight that Cash's horns once masked","built":"dropping the mariachi irony strips the song to its metal core"},
"acid-drinkers~rattlesnake-blues": {"fable":"A string of small biographies of ruin — a boy who plays with fire, a petty embezzler headed for the madhouse — accumulate into a case that something in the water is spoiled, which the song presses literally: who poisoned the well, who fouled the stream. The interrogation goes unanswered because the point is that no single culprit exists; the rot is ambient, a neighborhood where the proverbial Englishman's castle sits on a battlefield. In that airlessness the speaker's only response is to drink, and he names the reflex for the ugly compulsion it is rather than dressing it up — the rattlesnake blues being less a mood than a warning rattle, the sound a poisoned system makes.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","politics & society","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"naming the drinking reflex without dressing it up","built":"the unanswered question converts blame into ambient rot"},
"acid-drinkers~primal-nature": {"fable":"A mock-autobiography of the outlaw self, sold as inherited pedigree: the speaker pins his wildness on a bloodline of jailers, cutthroats, and apes, as if breeding rather than any choice manufactured the man daring you to peer inside his savage skull. The catch is that every credential is borrowed from pulp — reared by primates, drifting among desperados and roaming clans under some feeble chieftain — so the ferocity reads as costume rather than instinct. Acid Drinkers, Poland's grinning thrash provocateurs, lean into the swagger, then let the confession slip: the creature only shared beds with fellow performers, kin to entertainers, quietly conceding the whole untamed resume is theater.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","identity & becoming","family & blood"],"means":"savage credentials borrowed from pulp admitted as theater at the end","built":"the closing confession of entertainer kin quietly deflates the whole feral resume"},
"acid-drinkers~poplin-twist": {"fable":"A sneering broadside against the manufactured trance of trend-worship, where a band peddling a beat becomes a false prophet and the crowd a bloated column of the willingly blind. The narrator poses as the disgusted apostate walking out mid-service, but his contempt keeps snagging on his own body and appetites, so the revulsion reads as much like exclusion and jealousy as principle. The image-system is the tell: poplin, a cheap flashy cloth, names the fad the title mocks, and the twist folds a dead sixties craze into a garrote, so the sickly reeling and choking turn the party into a slow suffocation dressed as ecstasy. The turn on the wild sister who has lost her manners drops the mask entirely, exposing a moralizing possessiveness underneath the anti-conformist pose, and the throwaway closing gesture toward the blues sneers that real feeling is the one thing this whole spectacle can't sell.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","party & hedonism"],"means":"anti-conformist disgust snagging on its own exclusion and jealousy","built":"the title folds a dead sixties craze into a garrote the party wears"},
"acid-drinkers~polish-blood": {"fable":"A defiant self-portrait that dares the listener to insult a speaker who has already beaten them to every slur: unwise, thin on morals, a jaw that runs without a brake. The taunt-and-preempt structure is the whole joke, and the punchline each time lands on the same diagnosis, blood inherited, offered as if lineage alone excused the belligerence. The middle turn widens from personal cussedness to a street framed by a window, one crowd set against the uniforms, two rival factions and the unanswered question of who takes charge now, so the contrariness reads as political weather as much as temperament. This Poznan quartet, thrashing in English across the raw post-1989 decade, weld the reflexive national reflex of siding against everything and for nothing into a badge worn with a smirk, mockery and pride collapsed into one snarled refrain.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","politics & society","family & blood"],"means":"lineage offered as excuse for belligerence with a knowing smirk","built":"taunt-and-preempt structure beats every insult to the same inherited punchline"},
"acid-drinkers~pizza-driver": {"fable":"A mock-heroic sketch that swells a small-time hustler into folklore, then deflates the whole myth with a two-word punchline. The verses borrow the diction of a fallen big shot who vanished from his old streets after the dust got him, chased a woman, ran rackets on the quiet, and won crowds like a legend past his prime. That unkillable refrain wants to sound like defiant grit and staying power. But the title reduces every grand claim to a man ferrying takeout across town, so his restlessness shrinks to a delivery route, and the band's real quarry is how masculine self-mythology drapes plain wage-work in outlaw costume.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","comedy & wordplay","money & the street"],"means":"outlaw self-mythology draped over a man on a delivery route","built":"the title deflates every grand verse claim to a takeout run"},
"acid-drinkers~pig-to-rent": {"fable":"The title's landlord joke is the thesis: torment is treated as leased property, a tenant installed inside the singer who collects suffering the way a slumlord collects money owed. What opens as disgust at some external swine curdles into recognition, the labyrinth he cannot escape turning out to be his own skull, so the chant of surrendered will and a bartered soul reads less as protest than as signing the lease. Acid Drinkers deliver it as a thrash splatter cartoon, yet the dread sticks because the promise to fight with knuckles alone dissolves the instant he confesses he would kneel and plead for the privilege.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind","shame & repression"],"means":"the tormentor turns out to be installed as a tenant inside the singer","built":"landlord metaphor holds until the singer confesses he signed the lease himself"},
"acid-drinkers~nagasaki-baby": {"fable":"The conceit renders a beloved as a blast survivor, her face still an open injury and her skin scorched to cinders, so tenderness and mass death are made to inhabit one ruined body at once. Yet the song's real subject is the lover's flinch, not her injury: he keeps asserting ownership even as he admits he dreads the embrace and cannot meet her scarred palms with his eyes. The catalogue of char and ash belongs to his revulsion more than to any weapon, a man dressing his own failure of pity as her disfigurement. That closing possessive lands as a desperate grip rather than devotion, clung to precisely because every human reflex in him has already pulled away.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","love & desire","war & battle"],"means":"a lover's failure of pity dressed as the beloved's disfigurement","built":"the closing possessive read as a desperate grip after every reflex has pulled away"},
"acid-drinkers~my-pick": {"fable":"A plain cry for escape that keeps colliding with its own title. The verses reach for transcendence in the oldest terms — dissolving into air, rising over the treetops, snapping the fetters that hold a body down — the romantic flight-from-bondage image hard rock has leaned on forever. But the refrain deflates the loft each time, since melting into open sky turns out to mean reaching for a fix and craving its jolt, the horizon shrinking to one fixed, repeatable hit. The title frames this as sovereign choice, free will exercised, while the doubled hook and the burning nerves expose it as compulsion — the vow of a fresh route landing not as release but as the addict's forever-postponed tomorrow, looping back to the same restless craving the song began in.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The pick cuts two ways: the fix he keeps reaching for, or the plectrum — the escape route that happens to be the instrument in his hand. The band's whole catalog argues for hearing both at once.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","transcendence & consciousness"],"means":"the vow of escape looping back to the same compulsive craving","built":"the title frames a sovereign choice the burning nerves expose as compulsion"},
"acid-drinkers~mike-cwel": {"fable":"A crude character sketch of a station-hustling rent boy, delivered in the leering third person of the men who use him — until the last lines turn the joke inside out. The rhetorical question of whether you want to be like Mike is answered with contempt, then undercut by the singer admitting his own daily transactions with him, so the mockery collapses onto the mocker. In the raunchy, English-language provocation typical of Acid Drinkers' early thrash-comedy register, the real target is the hypocrite who scorns the whore while keeping him in business.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","comedy & wordplay","money & the street"],"means":"mockery collapsing onto the mocker who kept him in business","built":"third-person leering flips to first-person admission in the closing lines"},
"acid-drinkers~menel-song-always-look-on-the-bright-side-of-life": {"fable":"A grafting of Monty Python's gallows-optimism refrain onto a catalogue of everyday grievances: a hostile press notice, a partner sneering at a thin wallet. The response to each insult is a spasm of fantasy violence — a broken nose, a wrung neck — immediately swallowed by the borrowed chorus of relentless cheer. The comedy is in the mismatch: the Python line was sung on a cross, sincere absurdist consolation, but here it papers over petty rage rather than existential doom, so the bright-side creed reads as the thug's self-medication, a mantra chanted precisely because the singer cannot actually let the slights go. The closing slide into Poznań vernacular drops the English pose entirely — a genial shout calling the addiction itself over like an old drinking mate, the bleakest joke the bright side has to offer.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The sucker with the paper is answered in-lyric by name: Litza — Robert Friedrich, the band's own guitarist — volunteers for the nose-breaking. The chorus is Eric Idle's, whistled straight off the cross.","themes":["comedy & wordplay","anger & defiance","addiction & self-destruction"],"means":"consolation mantra chanted by someone who cannot let slights go","built":"Python's gallows-sincere line redeployed against petty domestic rage"},
"acid-drinkers~megalopolis": {"fable":"The song reads the modern megacity as a self-devouring organism: the mob makes the city and the city makes the mob, a closed loop the lyric states twice at either end so the argument shuts on itself like a trap. Between those bookends the city is already a corpse fed on by parasites and killers, and the speaker enters it as an outsider bent on breaking through the guarded wall — an individualist bid for escape or luck. The nerve of the piece is the mid-song interrogation, a flat catechism of body counts and choppers that answers itself in numbers too large to feel; horror is delivered as bureaucratic tally rather than scream, which is the real indictment — atrocity has become routine paperwork. That the piece closes not on triumph but on waiting for darkness to be free quietly concedes the wall was never breachable in daylight: freedom here is only what the dark permits, and the loser-in-the-system verdict swallows the earlier fantasy of winning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","night & the city","freedom & escape","violence & murder"],"means":"atrocity normalized as bureaucratic tally not scream","built":"the loop-closes-on-itself framing traps the individualist bid inside"},
"acid-drinkers~me": {"fable":"A monologue of existence spoken by someone never consulted about being born, unsure whether the fact is a complaint filed or an apology owed. The self-erasing arithmetic runs cold at first — subtract this life and nothing measurable goes missing — until the reckoning turns and names the real defendant: another person whose being the speaker cannot cancel, and whose nearness converts a mere arrival into fault rather than harmless irrelevance. The sneering refrain from outside is the borrowed verdict the voice keeps returning to, so the wish for non-being reads less as cool philosophy than as contempt for the self dressed in reasoned clothes. A late turn opens onto chance — would a birth flung into some harsher climate have loosened the rope at the throat — before the whole thing circles back to its opening sum, declining any settlement: the weight is not a conclusion argued but a rut worn smooth by repetition.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["suicide","shame & repression","identity & becoming"],"means":"self-erasure dressed in borrowed contempt as verdict","built":"the argument circles back to its own opening sum without settling"},
"acid-drinkers~max-he-was-here-again": {"fable":"A moonlit character study of the tavern raconteur whose ruin has hardened into a kind of neighborhood sainthood, honest to a fault and comically fearless, a spiderman scaling walls on nothing but liquor and nerve. Yet the reverence keeps souring: the narrator tallies each bottle, names the sleepwalking alcoholic behind the legend, and finds his own weary plea for calm hurled back as an order to clear off. Admiration and dread braid together until the refrain gives up on rescue entirely, cheering the wreck toward one more brawl instead of steering him home. It reads as a hymn to the barfly hero and a confession of how good company excuses the slow drowning underneath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","friendship & loyalty","death & grief"],"means":"admiration and dread braid until rescue gives way to cheering the wreck on","built":"the hymn to the barfly hero concedes the slow drowning the reverence excuses"},
"acid-drinkers~king-kong-bless-you": {"fable":"A strutting demagogue grabs the microphone and greets the crowd by sorting it into groups he can insult, his fake courtesy the tell of a bully who mistakes contempt for leadership. What he offers a numbed public is dressed up as warmth but arrives as a promise of deliberate hurt, and his big scheme turns out to be a childish thing he almost giggles while unveiling. The comedy tightens when he claims restraint and then cheerfully labels himself a maniac, so caution and derangement occupy one sentence. The blessing of the title is sworn by a giant movie ape, which names the only deity such a rally deserves. His whole confidence leans on a boast that the highest office backs him, marking a nobody inflated by lent power, and the band grinds the riff dead straight so the swagger and the idiocy detonate together.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","artifice & performance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"bully's fake courtesy names contempt as the only warmth on offer","built":"the blessing sworn by a giant movie ape pins the rally to the deity it deserves"},
"acid-drinkers~jennifer-and-ben": {"fable":"A song about faith in love conducted through characters the speaker admits he never actually saw — Jennifer and Ben are named as two, then confessed to be ten, then a phantom multitude the singer only adored from a distance, like invisible neighbours he treats as intimates. The point of the slippage is that the couple is a stand-in: what he really worships is the bond between them, a feeling he calls natural and self-evident yet also blinding, its violence caught in the mangled image of an atomic flash. Against that, the refrain turns skyward with a heckler's impatience, demanding of God what the matter is and announcing that the miracle is still being waited for — the joke being that the couple's ordinary, hooligan love is the only miracle on offer, and the singer is stuck outside it, in his awful plight, faith and frustration braided into the same shout. The broken English and misspellings are load-bearing rather than accidental: an outsider straining to name something luminous he can't quite reach or spell.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["love & desire","faith & the occult","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"the couple is a stand-in for a bond the outsider worships from a distance","built":"broken English and misspellings mark someone straining toward what he cannot spell"},
"acid-drinkers~hydrogen": {"fable":"A creed of white-knuckled survival dressed as a chant, where a monotony of identical mornings and repeated lies grinds hope down to a dark residue the speaker refuses to inherit. His defense turns almost ascetic: he seals his sight, suspends the clock, and swallows his own dread rather than be swallowed, cauterizing the parting with a coldness that will not welcome the other person home. Every profit and ruin gets incinerated in a sovereign sun, as though attachment itself were fuel to be spent. The element named in the title is the lightest thing there is, the stuff that either detonates under stress or floats free, and the mood here elects buoyancy over collapse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","dissociation & numbness","freedom & escape"],"means":"survival by sealing sight and incinerating every attachment","built":"the lightest element chosen as emblem of buoyancy over detonation"},
"acid-drinkers~house-full-of-reptiles": {"fable":"A cartoon of domestic overload played dead straight: the scaled infestation is really the speaker's own room caving inward, so crammed with snouts and tails he can't reach the toilet and gives up in a corner. What reads as a creature-feature premise is closer to a bad hangover or a life so swamped with clutter and screeching that basic movement fails, and the band lets the comedy sour into skull-splitting overwhelm rather than any monster-slaying triumph. Acid Drinkers' signature is exactly this — thrash speed yoked to broad absurdist slapstick, capped by a deflating verdict that the whole ordeal simply is not cool, which is at once the punchline and the genuine complaint.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"comic infestation sours into genuine skull-splitting overwhelm","built":"thrash speed yoked to slapstick lets the absurdity deflate itself"},
"acid-drinkers~hit-the-road-jack": {"fable":"A cover of the Percy Mayfield standard Ray Charles made famous, the song is a two-voice eviction: the woman's refrain is pure verdict, curt and final, while the man's verses beg, bargain, and rationalize in the gaps she leaves him. The cruelty is economic rather than romantic — she names being broke as the real disqualification, so his pride and her contempt are settling the same bill. What makes it land is that he keeps conceding her logic even as he pleads against it, packing his things while insisting he'll be back on his feet, and the tacked-on coda where he pivots from wounded lover to needling her not to be chicken only confirms that the door has already shut on him. In a hard-rock band's hands the joke sharpens: the swaggering delivery collides with a lyric about a man being thrown out with nothing, and the muscle is the mask over the humiliation underneath.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["heartbreak & loss","money & the street","shame & repression"],"means":"poverty disqualifies the man before pride can plead his case","built":"his wounded pivot to taunting confirms the door already shut"},
"acid-drinkers~highway-star": {"fable":"This near-faithful cover of Deep Purple's 1972 chestnut runs on one relentless equation: the vehicle, the woman, and the driver's own overclocked brain get seized in identical grammar, each a lethal contraption prized for the same rush. Velocity dissolves the difference between them, so keeping a girl parses exactly like keeping a hunk of roaring machinery, and the mind burning down the asphalt becomes just another appliance idolized for its raw output. The Polish thrash band's lone signature is a crude expletive rammed into the second chorus, less a reinterpretation than a sweaty grab at the wheel of a classic-rock monument. Fittingly the boast never lands a clean finish; the motor catches and stalls mid-sentence, the star sputtering to a halt before the victory lap.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","love & desire"],"means":"velocity flattens girl, machine, and mind into one idol","built":"the motor stalls mid-boast before the finish line arrives"},
"acid-drinkers~high-proof-cosmic-milk": {"fable":"An appetite song that dresses raw thrill-seeking in cosmic robes: the speaker craves what he has never known, and the unfamiliarity itself is the fuel that drives the hunger. The Icarus figure — spread wings, the ascent toward the sun, the plummet that follows — is embraced rather than feared, so the ruin reads as the point of the climb, not its penalty. The title works as a self-aware punchline: a potent intoxicant hawked in the language of health advice, a wholesome regimen you are urged to gorge on, which recasts the band's booze-and-noise ethos as a mock nutritional program. The thesis holds because the irony never resolves — the pitch keeps recommending the very substance that guarantees the descent, meaning both the sales patter and the wreckage at once.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","freedom & escape","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"irony holds because the pitch recommends the substance that guarantees the descent","built":"title hawks a potent intoxicant in the language of a wholesome nutritional program"},
"acid-drinkers~flooded-with-wine": {"fable":"A drunk man's confession dressed as tenderness, forever apologizing to a listener it warns off in the same breath. The bravado of arrival curdles into a plea for forgiveness and a flat refusal to peddle illusions, the speaker admitting he can promise nothing and asking not to be met in the eye. What holds the wreck together is pure motion — the boozy tide that fuels the mood and absolves it at once, leaving only the vow that the two of them are somehow still moving.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression"],"means":"warning the listener off while apologizing in the same breath","built":"bravado of arrival collapses to a plea not to be met in the eye"},
"acid-drinkers~drunk-eyes": {"fable":"A vampire's seduction becomes a study of dependence, the predator scenting sweet blood and barring every exit while insisting the world outside can always feed him better. The two halves split the roles cleverly: one voice is the hunter coaxing prey into the hell it has entered, the other is the victim's dizzy account of the carousel, the spinning, the giddy conviction of flight under a stranger's spell. By the finish the euphoria sours into pure wreckage, and the breezy thanks-God shrugs land as an addict's rehearsed denial, calling himself mistaken and a fool in the same breath he refuses to quit. Fitting for a band whose name puns a bottle into hard-rock swagger, the conceit carries the morning-after truth without a word of sermon.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","faith & the occult","love & desire"],"means":"addict's denial calling himself mistaken while refusing to quit","built":"vampire conceit splits the two voices so hunter and prey share the same morning wreckage"},
"acid-drinkers~don-t-go-to-where-i-sleep": {"fable":"A warning issued from the one place a person is least defended — sleep — where the speaker cannot police what he mutters or what he becomes on waking. The refrain sounds like an ordinary plea for rest, but it works as an alibi: everybody needs rest is the reasonable-sounding cover for a demand that is really quarantine, keeping others clear of the monster that surfaces when he is disturbed. Acid Drinkers pitch it in their thrash register just seriously enough that the comedy of a man barricading his own bedroom curdles into a real admission — the eightfold hammering of the title command, not the excuse around it, is where the menace actually lives.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","madness & the mind","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"reasonable plea for rest as cover for a demand that is really quarantine","built":"eightfold hammering of the title command is where the menace lives, not the excuse"},
"acid-drinkers~damned-diamonds": {"fable":"A cartoon of greed narrated from inside the fever, where the speaker's every verb is an act of stuffing, loading, and cramming until his own body becomes one more sack to be filled to bursting. The comedy is that arrival never comes: the chorus abandons story for the bare verb of digging on, accumulation curdling into a mania with no object, so that reaching the buried threshold reads less as triumph than as the moment wanting devours the wanter. The second verse turns sinister under the same grin, with a whole village decapitated by the same appetite and a whereabouts he'll never surrender, until nothing survives of him but the loot he evaporated with, hinting the real prize was vanishing clean, which for such a man is barely distinct from dying underground.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["money & the street","addiction & self-destruction","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"accumulation curdling into a mania that devours the wanter","built":"the chorus abandons story for bare digging, no object ever named"},
"acid-drinkers~calista": {"fable":"This is tabloid body-culture comedy, an absurd Polish everyman fawning over the starved television waif the title all but names — Calista Flockhart's Ally McBeal, whose famous thinness sat at the center of a turn-of-millennium panic. He aches to be as slender as she is, slim as a meager monthly wage, to borrow her wardrobe and heels, to move with a dancer's ease he plainly lacks. The coveting keeps escalating from her clothes into her body and finally her frazzled, hallucinating mind, which he wants to bed down inside — adoration curdling into a wish to consume her whole. But that appetite is just the tabloid gaze following its own grotesque logic, the culture that manufactures a jazz-bar ideal and then teaches everyone to devour it. Played entirely for envy-of-the-waif farce, the greasy swagger mocks the fantasy rather than endorsing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title all but names Calista Flockhart — the details keep landing on the Ally McBeal signature set: the era's most scrutinized thinness, the jazz bar, the heroine's hallucinating interior. The band never confirmed the target; the internal evidence does the naming.","themes":["love & desire","comedy & wordplay","artifice & performance","identity & becoming"],"means":"envy of the waif follows its own grotesque logic","built":"adoration escalates from wardrobe to mind to total consumption"},
"acid-drinkers~blood-is-boiling": {"fable":"This is the war song delivered from inside the wound, where the official announcement that the fighting has stopped is flatly contradicted by a body that keeps convulsing long after any ceasefire could matter. Acid Drinkers strip the heroic frame down to slaughterhouse anatomy: a man who has forgotten his own name inventories a cracked skull and a vanished arm with a giddy cheer that is really deep shock. The hinge is the sour twist on victory, sung as a feverish parody of a soldier's anthem, the ranting funeral waltz of a mind coming apart — the gap between the triumphant word and the mutilated speaker is the entire point. By the final verse the horror curdles into abandonment: a mangled survivor pleading against being turned away from, still insisting he exists, which lands less as combat trauma than as anyone left ruined and unwanted once the cause that spent him has moved on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind"],"means":"a body that keeps convulsing long after any ceasefire could matter","built":"the triumphant word victory sung against a mutilated speaker is the entire gap"},
"acid-drinkers~be-careful-with-this-gun-daughter": {"fable":"A grotesque parody of a parent's morning send-off, where the fond checklist — packed lunch, spare ammunition, a scrubbed barrel — has been colonized by the reflexes of an armed society, so tenderness and lethal drilling collapse into one gesture. The Polish thrash veterans play it as pitch-black satire: a father coaches his small girl in vigilance and reflex the way another might nag a child about manners, while an official bounty on street pushers rebrands private paranoia as civic duty. The forced cheer cracks only in the chorus, exposing the confession underneath — that no one can pull him out of this, that remaining a man now means staying armed and frightened, a fortress home he cannot escape even as he schools his daughter to inherit it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","politics & society","family & blood"],"means":"tenderness and lethal drilling collapsed into a single parental gesture","built":"the fond checklist format is colonised wholesale by the reflexes of an armed society"},
"acid-drinkers~another-brick-in-the-wall": {"fable":"A cover that welds Pink Floyd's Part 1 to Part 2, so the abandoned child and the mutinous schoolboy read as one wound in two stages: the vanished father becomes the first missing brick, and rote schooling mortars over the gap until the wall stands finished. The refrain carries the real argument by shifting pronoun mid-song — the child grieving that he himself is merely a brick hardens into the accusation that you are just another one, turning private loss into a charge against everyone who cements the next generation into the same defensive slab. A Polish band raised where the classroom drilled obedience and sarcasm was hazardous takes Waters' anti-authoritarian anthem less as import than as recognition, the metal delivery reshaping the wall from a psychological shelter into something you batter at.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","family & blood","shame & repression"],"means":"private loss hardened into a charge against everyone who cements the next generation","built":"the pronoun shifts mid-song from grieving child to accusation aimed at the listener"},
"acid-drinkers~amazing-atomic-activity": {"fable":"The title track is a boast delivered in the voice of a force that refuses to be declared obsolete, and the song's real subject is that refusal itself. The speaker answers the same taunt again and again — are you finished, past your time? — by piling up monuments of permanence: he is a mountain, a great wall, a creature whose younger sibling is Godzilla, a body that cannot sink. The comedy is that these swaggering claims are stacked precisely because the accusation keeps returning; the more he insists he will never end, the more we hear the anxiety of a thing being told its era is over. When the mask drops into the spoken bridge, the grandeur shrinks to something domestic and absurd — he whistles like a kettle — and the vaunted amazing atomic activity turns into a private, almost lonely energy carried on his own, the bluster of endurance revealed as its own kind of loud, defiant survival.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","anger & defiance","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"endurance bragged loudest precisely because obsolescence keeps returning","built":"the spoken bridge deflates the titan to a kettle whistling alone"},
"acid-drinkers~ace-of-spades": {"fable":"A note-faithful cover of Motörhead's signature song, and the reading lives in that fidelity: a Polish thrash band on a covers record picks the one anthem that most flatly equates its own swagger with a gambler's shrug, treating profit and ruin as a single indifferent outcome. The words never argue for recklessness so much as refuse to weigh it, and the tell is how the bravado's real cost surfaces only glancingly, in the flick of not caring to outlast the game, before the strut closes back over the admission. Its power comes from a card table standing in for an entire ethic of not flinching, with Aces and Eights, the hand Wild Bill Hickok held when he was shot, looming as the one honest stake the posturing keeps circling. Acid Drinkers add nothing to Lemmy's text; the meaning of their version is the act of claiming it, a scene wearing a borrowed creed as its own, which is exactly the fatalist joke the original was always making.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism","death & grief","artifice & performance"],"means":"bravado that refuses to weigh recklessness against its cost","built":"a Polish band claims a borrowed creed as its own, the fatalist joke the original was always making"},
"acid-drinkers~a-rubber-hammer-and-a-broken-head": {"fable":"A cartoon of self-sabotage played for a thrash-comic grin: a man flattens his own skull with a toy tool, loses half a meter of height, then rebrands the wreckage as virtue — too shrunken now to sneer over any stranger, humility purchased with a squeaky mallet. The gag stays warm only briefly before Acid Drinkers freeze it: with no zloty for a garage, a clinic, or a therapist, the caved-in dome is permanent, an idiot wound past any repair. What opens as slapstick curdles into a punkish shrug at cheap, unfixable ruin, the sour joke being that the damage cost almost nothing and can never be walked back.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"slapstick self-harm that curdles into unfixable ruin","built":"the toy tool renders the damage permanent for lack of cash"},
"sodom~bombenhagel-live": {"fable":"A German thrash band writes the air campaign from beneath it, taking the civilian's-eye view of the raids that razed their own cities, and the untranslated title — a downpour of ordnance — is the single word the song trusts to hold the ordeal, speech collapsed into raw sound by fright. What raises it above spectacle is the third verse, where the accusing finger turns inward: the terror is real, the cellar is real, yet the blame is laid on the fatherland's own leaders and their pointless, grinding warmaking, with even self-slaughter cast as the only mercy left. That swerve rejects the comfortable pose of blameless victimhood; the falling bombs are a punishment the country summoned onto itself, and the hammering chant reads less as grief than as sentence handed down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"civilian terror indicted as a punishment the fatherland summoned on itself","built":"untranslated title collapses speech into raw sound, the one word fright trusts to hold the ordeal"},
"sodom~incest-live": {"fable":"Sodom stage the taboo as a manifesto of self-authored morality: the speaker doesn't stumble into transgression so much as reason his way there, dismissing church, law and the moralizers before the act is even named, so that the sin becomes a philosophical position before it's a deed. The genius and the ugliness of the writing is the bookkeeping metaphor — virtues tallied against stains, misdeeds ranked and numbered — which lets the narrator pretend enough accumulated good will cancel the debt of the crime. In a second-person swerve the song flings that same accounting back at the moralizing listener, who tallies private virtue to license private fantasy no differently. That accountant's calm cracks in the final verse, where the bravado curdles into a bewildered admission that he no longer knows what has overtaken him: the mask of the liberated free-thinker slips to reveal someone frightened by his own compulsion, the self-justification collapsing into confession. Read straight it is pure Agent Orange shock-value provocation, but the structure — cold theory, then the act, then the loss of composure — makes it a small study of how a person talks himself past his own conscience and finds nothing on the other side but bewilderment.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","madness & the mind","faith & the occult"],"means":"self-justification as philosophical position collapsing into bewilderment","built":"cold theory then act then loss of composure enacted as three-stage structure"},
"sodom~1982": {"fable":"The title flips the peace idiom on its head: not burying the hatchet but digging it back up, war deliberately exhumed by generals who treat the world like a chessboard and call it strategy. Sodom, writing in the shadow of the Gulf War, lets the song's own moral outrage curdle — the chorus borrows the Magnificat's promise to cast down the mighty and lift up the lowly, but here that scripture of divine justice becomes a bloodthirsty rallying cry, exalting the humble only so they can spill the tyrant's blood in turn. The closing verse punctures any heroism: the summary executions under wartime law are confessed haltingly, as something hard to say, and the promise of Hell to pay lands not as triumph but as the recognition that toppling one strongman just restarts the cycle. The song's real subject is that overthrow and atrocity wear the same face.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder","faith & the occult","politics & society"],"means":"overthrowing a tyrant only reloads the atrocity","built":"Magnificat scripture repurposed as a bloodthirst rallying cry"},
"sodom~wanted-dead": {"fable":"A first-person confession from someone who has surrendered to a compulsion he no longer pretends to resist — the refrain that nothing can halt his course is aimed as much at himself as at his victim, and the title bounty is one he seems to have placed on his own head. Sodom keep the psychology deliberately unstable: the speaker frames damnation as a blessing and murder as a sacrifice to preserve some private reign, so the grandeur is exposed as the delusion of a skull racked by unbroken agony rather than the boast it mimics. The wound reopens structurally when the opening stanza returns verbatim near the end, the loop enacting his ghoulish sport as a cycle that resolves nothing, and the closing wish to be wanted dead lands less as threat than as the killer begging for his own end. It is a martyrdom he courts: only a hunted death, and the chronicler it demands, makes the legend permanent.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","suicide","madness & the mind"],"means":"killer begging for his own hunted end to make the legend permanent","built":"opening stanza returns verbatim near the end, the loop enacting his compulsion as a cycle that resolves nothing"},
"sodom~wachturm": {"fable":"A door-to-door proselytizing skit turned into thrash farce: two Jehovah's Witnesses ring the bell at an obscene hour hawking the Watchtower and salvation, and the hungover narrator meets their apocalyptic pitch — fire, brimstone, Armageddon, hand over your soul and your money — with escalating vulgarity, preferring his pornography to their revelation. The song's whole method is deflation: every sacred term the pair offer gets answered on the crudest possible plane, until conversion is countered with a proposition for a threesome. Sodom aim the satire less at faith itself than at the transactional, fear-selling salesmanship of organized door-stepping, and the boorishness of the narrator is half the joke — he is no enlightened skeptic, just a lout, so the track mocks both the missionaries and the man too crude to argue back with anything but his crotch. It is a beer-hall middle finger dressed as a theological debate, and it knows it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","comedy & wordplay","party & hedonism"],"means":"missionary sales pitch met with the crudest possible counter-offer","built":"sacred terms answered on the crudest possible plane, so deflation is the entire method"},
"sodom~wachturm-erwachet": {"fable":"A crude two-panel farce at the expense of door-knocking Jehovah's Witnesses and their Watchtower tract, played entirely for provocation in the German-thrash tradition of turning taboo into a middle finger. Part one is pure sketch comedy: the narrator, hungover, opens the door to two young missionaries hawking salvation for a coin, and answers their offer to scrub his soul clean by proposing a threesome instead — the sacred pitch met with the most profane possible counter-offer, doctrine reduced to something he'd rather trade his pornography for. The Erwachet half (the German title of the Watchtower's companion magazine) drops the joke's grin and gets nasty, casting the faithful as millions who have had their capacity to think for themselves scrubbed out, and prescribing sex — a happy weekend, stimulation below the belt — as the cure for their conditioning. The unifying thesis under the smut is anti-clerical: appetite and the body are set up as the honest, uncorrupted answer to an institution the song frames as fear-mongering (fire, brimstone, the end of the world) and money-collecting mind-control, and the closing Awake! is the band throwing the magazine's own imperative back as a demand to snap out of the faith rather than into it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","comedy & wordplay","surveillance & control"],"means":"body and appetite proposed as cure for institutional mind-control","built":"closing Awake! throws the magazine's own imperative back as a demand to snap out of faith"},
"sodom~verrecke": {"fable":"The song is a curse hurled at organized crime, and its shouted refrain is not a description but an imperative — roughly perish, drop dead, the ugly word you spit at vermin rather than the neutral verb for dying, which fixes the whole track's contempt. Its most damning stroke is domestic: these predators sold insurance by day and turned into monsters by night, wolves in sheep's clothing far from their own wives and children, so the horror is that the killers are respectable neighbors driven by a gold-rush greed, laying corpses along their path from luxury cars while anyone who won't fall in line is erased. Then the structure delivers its justice — the hunters become the hunted, the self-styled master-race becomes slaves, and their screams go unheard because death is already casting its shadow ahead of them. The repeated curse thus ripens from vengeful wish into prophecy, the song appointing itself the verdict the law failed to reach.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","anger & defiance","money & the street"],"means":"a curse that ripens from vengeful wish into prophecy","built":"hunters-become-hunted reversal delivers the justice the law withheld"},
"sodom~turn-your-head-around": {"fable":"Sodom pitch a bedside vigil as a furious rebuttal of a diagnosis: the physicians, the anonymous they of the song, have condemned a fading man as beyond saving. The speaker throws the ruling back with a blunt refusal repeated until it hardens from argument into sheer stubbornness. The title cuts both directions the track grinds together — the experts want to twist the patient's mind toward surrender, while the speaker keeps trumpeting the survival drive no authority can seize. The song holds its poison for the attendants who abandon him in the shadows, wished a swift grave of their own, so devotion to the living man curdles into loathing for the caretakers who quit caring. The chorus shattering into stammered scraps registers less as victory than as defiance ground down to raw reflex.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The doctors' sentence the speaker keeps refusing — this man is better off dead — is the record's own title, embraced as a verdict two tracks over and thrown back here.","themes":["death & grief","anger & defiance"],"means":"defiance of a medical verdict ground down to raw reflex","built":"title cuts both ways — turning the patient's mind toward surrender versus trumpeting survival"},
"sodom~stalinorgel": {"fable":"Stalinorgel is the German troops' grim byword for the Katyusha rocket launcher, and Sodom hang the whole track on that terror of Soviet salvos to recount Operation Barbarossa from the invader's collapsing perspective. The verses push the campaign forward like a chronicle: a tyrant's opening thrust, an assault smothered in blizzard and fog, then close-quarters butchery, surrender, and a Red Army counterthrust that ends with the aggressor erased from the map. The genius sits in the vantage — Hitler goes unnamed, shrunk to the fool who called catastrophe down by grasping absolute power, while the chorus hands the awe entirely to the shrieking rocket barrage — the invaders' own nickname for the Katyusha, a church organ's name pinned on the weapon for the pipe-rack look of its rails and the howl of its salvo. It plays not as celebration but as the band's icy reckoning with its own nation's ruin, the attackers frozen out and repaid by the very arsenal they were sent to dread.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"the aggressor's awe at the weapon sent to destroy him is the whole reckoning","built":"the invaders' own nickname for the Katyusha hands all the power to the weapon that erases them"},
"sodom~stalinhagel": {"fable":"Sodom, war-fixated from Agent Orange onward, here plant themselves inside the German catastrophe on the Eastern Front, and the whole song is built on the grim pun the title carries: the Stalinorgel was the Wehrmacht's nickname for the Katyusha launcher — Stalin's organ — and Hagel is hail, so the answering barrage is a hail of that organ's fire, an instrument that plays only detonation. The verses march the invasion forward in the aggressor's own bravado: declaration, thrust, offensive. Then the frozen Soviet December simply stops answering — black cold with nothing bright in it, only that killing muzzle-flash to resist — until the boast collapses into surrender and a stumbling withdrawal from the ground it seized. The final turn swaps the Stalinorgel for Bombenhagel, hail of bombs, and the closing verse names the campaign a pointless slaughter with no aim, where the future holds nothing. The momentum of the machine, the song concludes, has no reverse gear — the opening boast was the lie all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief"],"means":"the invasion's opening boast collapses into a pointless frozen withdrawal","built":"the title pun — Stalin's organ plus hail — embeds the killing instrument in its own name"},
"sodom~skinned-alive-93": {"fable":"A first-person portrait of a killer who has made cruelty into pleasure, the song stays locked inside the torturer's own euphoria rather than the victim's terror — the horror is that the voice is genuinely happy, framing the act as celebration and the flayed skin as a plaything. Its cruelest turn is a joke on finality: the promise that you only think you are dead when in fact the worst is still happening, so death offers no exit. Sodom, whose early splatter mode owed much to horror-film gore, here strips the material down to pure sadist's monologue, and the throwaway pun on resting in pieces confirms the register — this is comic-book atrocity worn as a grin, gruesomeness played for shock and dark relish rather than any moral reckoning.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","comedy & wordplay"],"means":"cruelty narrated from inside the torturer's genuine happiness","built":"the pun on resting in pieces confirms comic-book register — shock worn as a grin"},
"sodom~shadow-of-damnation": {"fable":"This is a toxic-obsession song that knows it is doomed and rides it anyway — the beloved is drawn as poison wrapped in the honeyed speech of something holy, sweetness that is also filth, and the speaker names his own condition as an abject dependence he cannot break. The governing image is optical: she bends the glow of everything he once imagined and blacks out the daylight of what he hoped for, so desire is figured not as fire but as a darkness falling across all that was good, the damnation of the title cast by the very thing he craves. The uncontrollable train and a route he says has no terminus turn that helplessness into motion; he is not seduced against his will so much as complicit, calling the ruin passion and choosing to grovel at her feet for it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire"],"means":"desire figured as darkness falling across everything once good","built":"the optical metaphor bends her light into a shadow that blacks out all prior hope"},
"sodom~sepulchral-voice": {"fable":"One of Sodom's oldest artifacts, from the 1984 In the Sign of Evil demo era when the band traded in first-generation black-metal blasphemy for pure shock rather than any developed occult creed, and the read has to honor that crudeness rather than dignify it. The song hands its listener a mock ordination: you are named Satan's chosen messenger and handed an instruction to execute, so the tune works as a black-mass inversion of a religious calling, the corpse-cold voice from the grave standing in for the summons a priest would receive from above. Its escalating catalogue of atrocities is deliberately transgressive teenage provocation, meant to offend a Catholic upbringing by profaning every sacrament at once, and the flatness of the shock is the point rather than a failure of craft. The refrain never advances because there is nowhere to advance to; the sepulchral voice simply keeps calling, and the loop enacts damnation as a summons you cannot answer or refuse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","artifice & performance"],"means":"damnation enacted as a summons you cannot answer or refuse","built":"the refrain loops without advancing because the loop is the hell it describes"},
"sodom~resurrection": {"fable":"Sodom stage a deathbed draped in the full apparatus of Christian rescue — the soul lifting, a divine sculptor rebuilding bone from ruin, gilded blossoms strewn across the passage — and whether that apparatus is sung straight or shown as a product sold to the dying is left deliberately unresolved, the tenderness real either way. The pivot is the borrowed gospel line — a ready heart trapped in failing flesh — set against a man with no wish to depart and attendants who deny him a parting word, so the crossing arrives as a transfer he never agreed to. By the final choruses the hymn turns to plain imperative — give credence, believe — and the lean, given who wrote it, is that everlasting reward reads as a bargain pressed on the powerless as easily as a comfort freely offered, the song declining to say which.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","death & grief"],"means":"everlasting reward held ambiguous between comfort and coercion","built":"the final imperative lean leaves the hymn straight or a bargain pressed on the powerless"},
"sodom~peacemaker-s-law": {"fable":"The title and its vocabulary are pure Old-West gunfighter code — the Peacemaker is the Colt Single Action Army, the thumb-buster its hammer, and the promise to level all comers is the revolver's old boast — lifted almost whole from the frontier ad-rhyme in which the gun invites any man, whatever his size, to call on it and be made equal. The song is spoken half by the shooter, half by the gun, laying down a duelist's etiquette: no shooting a man in the back, let him turn and face you first. The irony the read has to hold is that this ethic of fair play is entirely an ethic of killing; the peace it promises is the graveyard quiet after the draw, honor and slaughter fused into one law.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"a code of honor that is entirely a code of killing","built":"the Colt's frontier ad-rhyme lifted whole so gun and speaker share one voice"},
"sodom~one-step-over-the-line": {"fable":"Sodom turn a tropical getaway into an accusation, exposing the sunny resort as a floor where trafficked people are moved like inventory while a moneyed tourist arrives flush yet settles no honest debt for the ruin he sets in motion. The threshold named in the title is ethical, and crossing it swaps a small scald for total immolation, the appetite that dragged him abroad repaid not in pleasure but in sickness and a coffin. The band refuses easy footing, damning the buyer's rotted craving while sketching the sold woman as quarry devoured whole, then landing on his reckoning: an initiation into a brotherhood whose only dues are paid in the member's own corpse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","violence & murder","money & the street"],"means":"the buyer's appetite repaid in sickness and a coffin","built":"the title's ethical threshold doubles as cause of death"},
"sodom~nothing-to-regret": {"fable":"The whole song is a pointed second-person indictment of a figure who rules by blood and calls it destiny — a tyrant, or the abstract machinery of power, who has convinced himself he owes no reckoning. The title is the mask: the claim of having nothing to regret is exactly the self-deception the speaker is prying open, and the refrain answers it immediately with wounds still bleeding, freedom exposed as illusion. By the second half the accusation curls back on the accuser's own appetite — the craving to be the detonation others fear, the promise that cannot last — so the target's power is drawn as a hunger that consumes itself, hatred aimed outward while its venom quietly corrodes the foundations he stands on.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["politics & society","anger & defiance","violence & murder"],"means":"the tyrant's self-deception pried open by its own refrain","built":"the title is the mask the accusation dismantles line by line"},
"sodom~never-healing-wound": {"fable":"A thrash band's atrocity report on the institutional child, the one a state warehouses and then lets rot — abandoned from birth, filed away as less than human, tended by carers who function as executioners. Tom Angelripper builds it as an indictment that outlives the crime: the closing turn is the cruelest, where the regime falls and the memorials go up, yet the survivors are frozen out of any future the reckoning was supposed to buy them. The title names why — a wound the paperwork of overthrow can memorialize but never close, the neglect written into the body as disease and starvation. The refrain's fatalism, death arriving on schedule, isn't nihilist posturing here; it's the accurate math of a system that budgeted these lives to end. The detail of killers costumed as pediatricians is the song's whole thesis: the machinery of care repurposed as the instrument of death.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The overthrown regime, the institutional child-AIDS and the belated memorials place this in the Romanian orphanages exposed after Ceaușescu's fall — a 1990 headline answered, not a general elegy.","themes":["politics & society","death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"institutional neglect written into the body as a wound no reckoning closes","built":"killers costumed as pediatricians names the song's thesis in one image"},
"sodom~let-s-break-the-law": {"fable":"A cover of the Anti-Nowhere League's 1982 punk anthem, sung from inside the swagger — a night out with the lads, spoiling for a brawl, daring the cops to lock him up and lose the key. It is deliberately dumb fun, but the borrowed lyric slips in one telling qualifier, that the aggression draws no line between black and white, as if to insulate the mindless violence from the racist reading such a chant could otherwise invite. What the song is really about is the pleasure of the mask: the persona wants the fight, the cell, the score settled, and the chorus's escalating boasts are the point rather than any grievance beneath them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","party & hedonism"],"means":"the pleasure of the mask rather than any grievance beneath the bravado","built":"escalating chant makes the boast the point rather than anything it claims to mean"},
"sodom~lay-down-the-law": {"fable":"Sodom frame armed conflict as a demonic recruitment pitch, the speaker a smiling guide who offers his hand and leads you over scorched sand toward slaughter while every step exposes the men who loaded the rifles and blessed the crusade. The song's thesis is that no deity or court authors the carnage; a manufactured cause and a mob talked into obedience do, and the title command belongs to those engineers rather than to any higher justice. Its cruelest move is the promise of deliverance whispered while the victim is marched deeper into a horror with no waking, salvation and doom folded into a single seductive sentence. The final roll of nouns strips away scenery for a bald ledger of cause and consequence, each abstract term a hinge in a chain whose terminus, self-slaughter, was fixed before the first shot.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","violence & murder"],"means":"a seductive guide leading victims toward slaughter while naming its engineers","built":"salvation and doom folded into a single sentence as the song's cruelest move"},
"sodom~genesis-19": {"fable":"A band whose very name is the incinerated city retells the destruction from the nineteenth chapter of Genesis straight, and the straightness is the point: the chorus's ledger of the faithful blessed against the sinful destroyed arrives as settled cosmic accounting, wickedness doomed to lose stated as fact rather than hope. Yet the song quietly refuses to let the saved party off cleanly. Escape hinges on the command to keep one's gaze forward, and the closing verses turn dark and equivocal, Lot leading his daughters into the sacred hills after a night where nobody was spared and not a single blameless soul survived. By ending on that pitiless avenger and the daughters delivered into his grip, the reading Sodom offers is that the salvation narrative and the atrocity are one event seen from two angles, and the survivor inherits the rubble rather than fleeing it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["faith & the occult","violence & murder","death & grief"],"means":"salvation and atrocity revealed as one event seen from two angles","built":"the band whose name is the incinerated city retells the destruction straight, and the straightness is the argument"},
"sodom~gathering-of-minds": {"fable":"A late-Sodom meditation on entropy dressed as a war chant, where the flat refrain works less as a slogan than as a name for the enemy: consciousness itself, pooling and swarming toward its own dissolution. The verses pile up the vocabulary of atrophy — a body attempting flight on wings that no longer hold, vitalities withering, a life devalued like dead leaves driven along the ground — so the fear isn't nuclear apocalypse from outside but a slow inward rot the mind can only resist and never reverse. The closing turn is the sting: the speaker insists the doomed are marching in company rather than isolation, yet that fellowship offers no comfort, because a shared route only means everyone treads the same thorn-choked track to the same end, which is what the gathering was for all along.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"fellowship offering no comfort when everyone shares the route to the same end","built":"the flat refrain names the enemy as consciousness itself pooling toward dissolution"},
"sodom~fields-of-honour": {"fable":"Sodom haul the vocabulary of divine reckoning onto a world our own species has poisoned and squandered. The scriptural sifting of the worthy from the doomed is no rescue here but the culling of a people already sentenced — a final verdict served on tenants who merely rented the living globe and let it rot. The watchers who swear to redeem you are the veneer of mercy the song keeps peeling away, because the honour of the title curdles: the ground where you drop holds mass unmarked pits, campaigns of extermination with nothing glorious in view. What makes it stick is the welding of two apocalypses into one, judgment day and ecological ruin fused into a single event. The day of wrath arrives not from the heavens but as the invoice for fouled soil — end-times prophecy as the planet's reprisal, dressed in borrowed holy language.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The title renders the German military death-notice formula — fallen on the field of honour — and the same ghosting brings Lebensraum and the annihilation-battle doctrine word up through the broken English underneath it.","themes":["faith & the occult","nature & the elements","death & grief"],"means":"ecological ruin fused with judgment day as the planet's invoice for fouled soil","built":"the honour of the title curdles into mass unmarked pits with nothing glorious in view"},
"sodom~down-on-your-knees": {"fable":"Sodom's decades of war songs have usually kept the listener at trench level, but here Tom Angelripper narrows the lens to the perpetrator of a modern asymmetric war, and the song's cruelty is that it makes his logic sound reasonable from inside. The chorus stacks the actual vocabulary of the Syrian civil war — chlorine-packed drums rolled off helicopters, cyanide, a shot to the skull offered as mercy against a slower death — as a checklist rather than a lament, so the horror registers as procedure. The title phrase works two ways at once: it is the victim forced to kneel and the killer's demand for submission, and the speaker's certainty that no round can halt him is the band diagnosing how a self-anointed executioner rationalizes atrocity as righteous revenge. The final turn, dispatching them exactly as he claims they asked for, is the read's crux: it exposes his self-justification as pure projection, the mask of holy purpose over ordinary slaughter.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","violence & murder","politics & society"],"means":"self-justification for atrocity exposed as projection of holy purpose","built":"the title phrase serves victim and perpetrator in one frame simultaneously"},
"sodom~cold-sweat": {"fable":"A gambler's monologue in freefall, sung by a man who has decided the outcome before the dice ever land. The tell is the paradox that keeps recurring, a mind perfectly clear and unclouded by drink yet a body slick with fear trickling down the neck, which is the compulsive gambler's entire condition in a single image, lucid enough to know exactly what he is doing and terrified all the same. The bravado escalates as the stakes climb, a full paycheck he has not held in ages and a fantasy of decamping to Vegas, but the arithmetic never favors him, and by the last verse the lines fracture and collapse into a reflexive push to wager once more, so the piece enacts the addiction rather than merely describing it. Sodom, a thrash band covering Thin Lizzy, let the anxiety run at speed, but the meaning holds unchanged, the win he senses coming a matter of faith and never of odds.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind"],"means":"a lucid mind watching itself wager beyond all reason","built":"the lines fracture and collapse at the end, enacting the compulsion they describe"},
"sodom~capture-the-flag": {"fable":"Sodom march a squad up Hamburger Hill and let the war do exactly what it does to a body of men, the chorus a child's playground command hardened into an order to kill while the middle stretch dissolves the mission into pure chance, an advance that survives by luck rather than valor. That the band anchor it in a specific American catastrophe rather than an anthem is the tell, the patriotic scaffolding of oaths and vows set up only to be flattened by gunfire raking a trapped column. The closing verse is the payload, a survivor decades on nursing a beer and tallying the dead comrades who stayed on that slope, and the song reframes the whole ordeal not as glory seized but as a banner that cost everything and meant nothing. What sounds like a war-metal rallying cry is really a veteran's hangover, the chorus's bravado souring into the disgrace named at the end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","death & grief","nostalgia & memory"],"means":"a rallying cry souring into a veteran's hangover","built":"a child's playground command hardened into an order to kill"},
"sodom~braindead": {"fable":"This is a horror piece grounded in a real bioethical dread: the murk around neurological death, that state where a chest still rises and a pulse still runs while the person is ruled absent. The lyric sharpens it into a legitimate question about when a body has become a corpse. The band works that gray region cynically, casting the physicians as butchers wrapped in sterile gowns who preach compassion and the supremacy of spirit over flesh even as they profit from the patient's ruin. The verdict of the diagnosis is turned into a weapon — the medical ruling becomes the warrant that lets a person's final moments be quietly overwritten. The song's fear is not the ghoul but the institution deciding, for its own gain, precisely when you are still permitted to count as alive.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["surveillance & control","death & grief","politics & society"],"means":"the diagnosis repurposed as a license to erase","built":"institutional language standing in for the murder weapon"},
"sodom~bombenhagel": {"fable":"A German thrash band writes the air campaign from beneath it, taking the civilian's-eye view of the raids that razed their own cities, and the untranslated title — a downpour of ordnance — is the single word the song trusts to hold the ordeal, speech collapsed into raw sound by fright. What raises it above spectacle is the third verse, where the accusing finger turns inward: the terror is real, the cellar is real, yet the blame is laid on the fatherland's own leaders and their pointless, grinding warmaking, with even self-slaughter cast as the only mercy left. That swerve rejects the comfortable pose of blameless victimhood; the falling bombs are a punishment the country summoned onto itself, and the hammering chant reads less as grief than as sentence handed down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","death & grief"],"means":"civilian terror indicted as a punishment the fatherland summoned on itself","built":"untranslated title collapses speech into raw sound, the one word fright trusts to hold the ordeal"},
"sodom~bloody-corpse": {"fable":"Reworked from Sodom's mid-80s demo material for The Final Sign of Evil, this is the primitive template of German thrash's satanic first-person killer, where a murderer and a metaphysical force are deliberately never separated. The speaker starts as a lone figure prowling under a bright moon leaving bodies on the ground, but each verse inflates the claim: from personal revenge, to messenger of hell, to finally announcing himself as both the reaper and God. The chorus refuses to develop at all, just the two blunt words hammered four times over, and that flatness is the point — the corpse is stripped of any meaning beyond spectacle, the same nihilism the genre was built to shock with. The closing turn matters most: renouncing resurrection and sitting beside Satan, the narrator isn't tempted into evil but has already become its authority, so the horror is less the killing than the total collapse of any judgment that could condemn it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","faith & the occult"],"means":"a lone killer inflated verse by verse into its own satanic authority","built":"chorus refuses to develop, hammering two blunt words four times as nihilism's instrument"},
"sodom~bibles-and-guns": {"fable":"Sodom yoke the two tools together on purpose, since scripture and firepower are treated as one instrument, faith turned into a weapon and killing given a blessing, each licensing the other. The verses pile up apocalyptic abstractions — walls in flames, stillborn things clawing upright, ambitions sealed under earth — less as a narrative than as a fog of ruin, so the hook lands as the single hard fact left standing. What sharpens the antiwar contempt is placement, the hatred spreading at a distance, or perhaps just down the street, and this pairing named as the remedy people supposedly need, the refrain delivered as bitter prescription where the cure is itself the sickness. The late turn to a private victim drags the cosmic good-versus-evil framing down onto the individual pulling the trigger, which is exactly the point, the grand struggle serving only as an alibi for one corpse.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","faith & the occult","politics & society","violence & murder"],"means":"scripture and firepower yoked as one instrument, each licensing the other","built":"grand crusade framing collapses onto one individual corpse in the late turn"},
"sodom~better-off-dead": {"fable":"A nihilist's syllogism from early Sodom, where existence is stated as a rigged game whose only exit is death and whose only content is atrocity. The song refuses argument on purpose: between each verse a shouted four-word litany of horrors stands in for the world itself, and every verse loops back to the same verdict without advancing it, so the structure enacts the trap it describes — life as a sentence you serve until the grave, with the recurring judgment functioning less as despair than as flat, closed-case conclusion. The band's early-thrash bluntness is the instrument here; the crudeness is the meaning, a refusal to dignify life with nuance it insists life doesn't earn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The album's title verdict is argued with itself: embraced here as the sentence on existence, it returns in Turn Your Head Around as the exact line a dying man's bedside refuses.","themes":["alienation & emptiness","death & grief"],"means":"existence as a rigged game whose only verdict is the grave","built":"looping structure enacts the trap it describes, every verse returning to the same closed verdict"},
"sodom~axis-of-evil": {"fable":"The whole thing is ventriloquized from inside the voice it means to indict, wearing the borrowed grammar of post-9/11 war rhetoric, all mourning curdled into rage and all bloodshed dressed as the rescue of innocents from a menace across the border. Sodom, veterans of the pacifism that reaches back to Agent Orange, do not argue with that voice so much as let its own logic run until the seams split, the vow that ceaseless slaughter arrives at a quiet without struggle set beside the command to cut the enemy down for God. The mask is holy crusade, and beneath it sits a self-justifying engine that can only picture calm as the moment everyone else stands condemned, its flat repeated slogans functioning as catechism drained of meaning because it never had to earn any.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The words are stitched from the real post-9/11 war speeches — the grief-into-anger turn and the not-tire, not-fail pledges reworked from the 2001 address to Congress, under the 2002 State of the Union's title phrase — recited deadpan by a German band until the crusade credo indicts itself.","themes":["war & battle","politics & society","faith & the occult","artifice & performance"],"means":"war rhetoric run until its own logic splits its seams","built":"flat repeated slogans function as catechism drained of earned meaning"},
"sodom~an-eye-for-an-eye": {"fable":"A vigilante's manifesto that begins as prayer and curdles into apostasy: the song opens by interrogating God over the oldest theodicy problem — why the innocent die and the guilty go free — and when heaven stays silent, the speaker appoints himself the answer. The turn is structural. The bracketed opening plea is addressed upward, but by the final verse the camera pulls back to a third-person report of a doting spouse and dad whose family was gunned down by the criminals he now hunts, revealing that the whole tirade is grief hardened into a solitary crusade against wrongdoing. Sodom, a band whose usual register is combat and nuclear apocalypse, here narrows the scale to a single wronged figure, which makes the biblical title cut both ways: it invokes divine law as license, yet the speaker admits his reason has slipped its moorings, so the read is less an endorsement of revenge than a portrait of a conscience that has replaced an absent God with itself and can no longer tell whether that was right or wrong.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["violence & murder","death & grief","faith & the occult","madness & the mind"],"means":"grief hardened into a solitary crusade when heaven stays silent","built":"camera pulls back in the final verse to reveal a mourning father behind the manifesto"},
"korn~spike-in-my-veins-official-music-video": {"fable":"The song's grammar quietly betrays it: verses speak as a wounded collective — bruised, obedient, praised for compliance, grieving against handlers who feel nothing — but the chorus retreats into a single walled-off voice that warns the whole world off. The addressee stays deliberately blurred, both a person who makes the speaker feel alive and the drug doing exactly that, and that blur is the argument. On The Paradigm Shift, Jonathan Davis frames the needle less as flight than as the only gesture left once collective fury has nowhere to land. So the hammering refrain becomes cure and beating at once, defiance masking a narrator who calmly watches himself go under. The loop swells rather than settling because the power seized here is really just the last motion available, repeated since nothing remains to replace it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Davis says it isn't a drug song: the spike is the media-and-surveillance hit injected into everyone — his example was Miley's VMA night burying the NDAA signing — and the video splices Snowden into the news-cycle blur.","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance"],"means":"the needle as the last gesture left once collective fury has nowhere to land","built":"the addressee stays blurred — person or drug — and that blur is the argument"},
"korn~get-up-live-from-moscow-2014": {"fable":"A study in brokenness turned into a weapon: the speaker names himself as shattered, then refuses repair, hammering his mismatched fragments into another body as though a person were putty that could absorb what he cannot reconcile. The nu-metal grind fused to the electronic build stages a nervous, subcutaneous crawl toward eruption, and Skrillex's drop delivers the discharge the verses keep withholding, mechanizing compulsion into rhythm. That barked order splits the narrator in two, one voice snarling at the other to silence itself and rise, so the hook reads as an addict quarreling with his own paralysis rather than any anthem of triumph. The final turn confesses a man boxed inside a vacant room, haunted by everything abandoned behind him, exposing the aggression as escape from a grief he refuses to speak aloud.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","anger & defiance","death & grief"],"means":"aggression toward another as camouflage for unspoken grief","built":"the Skrillex drop mechanizes compulsion into rhythm, not release"},
"korn~worst-is-on-it-s-way-requiem-mass": {"fable":"Dread rendered as a bodily intruder: not a feared event but a tenant already lodged under the ribs, so the forecast in the title is really a diagnosis of the present. The song splits between two refrains, one helpless amid sealed corridors and rooms he cannot leave, the other a clenched mantra ordering confidence to seize the wheel. That override is the crux, landing not as recovery but as willed suppression, which is why the frightened refrain floods back the instant the coping talk stops. Built by Davis out of grief, the terror stays domestic and recursive rather than cinematic, and the bravado is a mask worn because the fear never loosens its grip.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"dread as a tenant already lodged rather than a coming event","built":"bravado refrain floods back the instant the coping talk stops"},
"korn~another-brick-in-the-wall-live-from-denver-2014": {"fable":"Korn compress all three parts of Waters' Wall overture into one cover, and the splicing is the statement: the dead father who left only a photograph, the classroom that grinds children into interchangeable masonry, and the final numb refusal to be touched or medicated arrive back-to-back as a single continuous hardening. Each brick is another wound absorbed until the self is fully sealed off, and the closing goodbye is not suicide so much as the wall going up for good — the moment a person decides there is nothing anyone can say to reach him. For a band whose own catalog mines childhood abuse and isolation, the cover reads less as tribute than as recognition: they are singing about the same wall from the inside of it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness","family & blood"],"means":"three separate hardening wounds compressed into one continuous sealing","built":"splicing all three parts back-to-back makes the wall a single act"},
"korn~another-brick-in-the-wall-pink-floyd-cover": {"fable":"Korn compress all three parts of Waters' Wall overture into one cover, and the splicing is the statement: the dead father who left only a photograph, the classroom that grinds children into interchangeable masonry, and the final numb refusal to be touched or medicated arrive back-to-back as a single continuous hardening. Each brick is another wound absorbed until the self is fully sealed off, and the closing goodbye is not suicide so much as the wall going up for good — the moment a person decides there is nothing anyone can say to reach him. For a band whose own catalog mines childhood abuse and isolation, the cover reads less as tribute than as recognition: they are singing about the same wall from the inside of it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","dissociation & numbness","family & blood"],"means":"three separate hardening wounds compressed into one continuous sealing","built":"splicing all three parts back-to-back makes the wall a single act"},
"korn~you-ll-never-find-me-official-visualizer": {"fable":"A song of grief turned self-cannibalizing: the darkness here is not an outside force but an appetite the speaker feeds, doubt and pain becoming the only nourishment left after loss. Written into the bereavement that shapes The Nothing, its pivot is a bargain — a demand that the vanished other come back and hand over some withheld truth — which the chorus then answers by dissolving the demander instead. The refrain that gives the title is sung as a taunt but functions as a confession: the repetition stops meaning I am hiding and starts meaning I no longer exist to be found, the self having sunk into the very void it was pleading with. The final turn from you'll never find me to come for me is the mask slipping: the pose of unreachable withdrawal was always an invitation to be pursued, even into death.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The coda's come-for-me plea is the first strike of a motif the album keeps answering — Cold opens on it, H@rd3r chants it a dozen times over, Finally Free hands it to the dead. Four songs, one summons.","themes":["death & grief","suicide","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"withdrawal from grief that was always an invitation to be pursued","built":"the title taunt shifts meaning mid-song from hiding to no longer existing"},
"korn~falling-away-from-me-lyrics-version": {"fable":"Korn frames despair here not as crisis but as an exhausted daily chore: the speaker treats self-destruction like chronic pain to be managed, muttering that things will improve tomorrow with the flat conviction of someone who long ago stopped believing his own reassurances. The verses stay eerily quiet and hollow, a fatigue of self-erasure, until the structure enacts the collapse the words only describe, trading statement for pure pounding rhythm and a first person hammered flat beneath the mix. That shift from numb narrator to a battered, wordless object is the crux, an abuse whose source blurs between an outside tormentor and the speaker's own mind. The lone obscenity that ruptures the loop marks the single moment the mask of reassurance drops and the raw terror of dissolving finally surfaces.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","addiction & self-destruction","madness & the mind"],"means":"self-erasure managed as a daily maintenance task","built":"structure trades words for pure rhythm as the speaker dissolves"},
"korn~right-now-bust-u-up-version": {"fable":"A tantrum that admits, in its own opening breath, that its fury has no route outward toward the person it targets, so every threat lands as evidence of a rupture the speaker cannot bridge with anyone. The revealing pivot is how the wrath keeps curving back on its owner. A promise to maim someone else swerves into a razor turned on his own arm, until enemy and speaker collapse into a single wound. That relentless silencing chant, first aimed across the room and then at himself, names the true antagonist: not a rival at all but an interior static he cannot switch off, which is why the collapse of restraint, not any actual grievance, stands as the song's confessed subject.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","madness & the mind","suicide"],"means":"outward rage folding back onto the interior static it cannot silence","built":"the silencing chant shifts from aimed across the room to aimed at himself"},
"korn~thoughtless-official-hd-video": {"fable":"A revenge fantasy narrated by the tormented, this turns the schoolyard scene of being mocked into a private theater where the victim scripts the payback his real life cannot deliver. The chorus is the hinge: hatred is not renounced but hidden, driven down until it becomes something the bully can no longer locate or name, which is exactly why it can no longer be stopped. Davis frames the retaliation in deliberately unbearable terms, borrowing his abuser's violence back at them, so the daydream indicts itself as much as its target. The late shift to bodies against the wall and dead friends widens the wound past one bully into the accumulated damage that fuels it, and the refusal to be drowned becomes the only self-assertion left. It reads not as triumph but as the closed loop of someone rehearsing power he only ever holds inside his own head.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","violence & murder","shame & repression"],"means":"revenge rehearsed inside the head where the bully can't locate it","built":"hidden hatred named as precisely what makes it unstoppable"},
"korn~start-the-healing-requiem-mass": {"fable":"Recovery arrives as a threat disguised as an invitation, Davis daring a companion to walk into wreckage with him rather than promising any gentleness waiting on the far side. The lure to prowl and to pretend at relief masks a surgical demand: wounds must reopen and the invaders be dug out before anything closes over. His choruses collapse into self-prosecution, a stack of unmet obligations where the vow to erase every feeling stands beside the confession that some intruder keeps forcing itself back inside. The outro diagnoses the con precisely, since surrendering to the hope that mending will hold only lodges it deeper, sticking harder and refusing ever to quit. Conceived as a requiem, the song treats its own title as an endless conscription rather than a cure, an order to begin again with no finish line anywhere in sight.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","shame & repression","death & grief"],"means":"recovery staged as a threat requiring wounds to reopen before anything closes","built":"the title treated as endless conscription rather than cure — no finish line anywhere"},
"korn~one-mtv-icon-metallica-digital-remaster": {"fable":"A cover of Metallica's song, and like the original it draws on Dalton Trumbo's Johnny Got His Gun: a soldier so mutilated by a mine that only consciousness survives, fed by tubes with no sight, speech, hearing, or limbs. The horror is not death but its opposite, a mind fully awake inside flesh that can register nothing, so a plea for waking curdles into a plea for release. It builds by narrowing, the early blur of dream and real hardening verse by verse into a final inventory of everything taken. That the appeal goes to God rather than a doctor is the cruelty, since no one there can answer, and the only mercy left to imagine is the switch that shuts the machines down.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["war & battle","suicide","dissociation & numbness"],"means":"consciousness fully awake inside flesh that can register nothing","built":"the appeal goes to God where no one can answer and no mercy is possible"},
"korn~never-never-live-from-moscow-2014": {"fable":"A vow of permanent emotional lockdown, sworn so violently that the grammar buckles: the promise of no future tenderness keeps slipping into its own opposite, as if the mouth cannot physically hold the absolute the will keeps demanding. That fracture is the point, since a refusal pitched this loud is a man losing an argument with himself, and the tic of feeling that keeps rehearsing underground admits the wound stays live beneath the armor. Classify, crucify, seal the door and vanish: these are one identical move in Davis's register, quarantining the ache rather than metabolizing it, and the final avalanche of the word reads less as verdict than as a mantra hammered flat to smother the doubt it can never actually silence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","dissociation & numbness","heartbreak & loss"],"means":"a lockdown sworn so loud it argues against itself","built":"the vow keeps slipping into its own opposite as the grammar buckles"},
"korn~got-the-life-live-from-denver-2014": {"fable":"Korn's signature bounce turns a party command into a hollow ritual: the invitation to dance is issued by someone who admits he feels swallowed and torn out from the inside every day, so the groove is a distraction machine rather than a celebration. The song's split runs through its title's irony — God, reached by pager like a nagging beeper, taunts that the speaker already has the life while insisting he'll never actually see it, so the promised good life is possessed and withheld at once, a thing everyone chases and no one gets to look at. The refusal to follow that opens the track curdles into everyone will follow, marking how the same hollowness that isolates the singer is also what makes the crowd move with him. What sits under the mask is emptiness bought as glamour; the argument the song keeps enacting is that having the life and feeling nothing are the same condition.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance","faith & the occult"],"means":"having the life and feeling nothing declared the same condition","built":"the title's irony: the life is simultaneously possessed and permanently withheld"},
"korn~right-now-lyrics": {"fable":"A tantrum that admits, in its own opening breath, that its fury has no route outward toward the person it targets, so every threat lands as evidence of a rupture the speaker cannot bridge with anyone. The revealing pivot is how the wrath keeps curving back on its owner. A promise to maim someone else swerves into a razor turned on his own arm, until enemy and speaker collapse into a single wound. That relentless silencing chant, first aimed across the room and then at himself, names the true antagonist: not a rival at all but an interior static he cannot switch off, which is why the collapse of restraint, not any actual grievance, stands as the song's confessed subject.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","madness & the mind","suicide"],"means":"outward rage folding back onto the interior static it cannot silence","built":"the silencing chant shifts from aimed across the room to aimed at himself"},
"korn~can-you-hear-me-acoustic": {"fable":"Jonathan Davis built The Nothing from the wreckage of his wife's sudden death, and here he refuses to name the presence that stalks him, calling it only an it. That withholding is the argument: bereavement is rendered as a creature that feeds on memory yet also cradles him, patient predator and intimate keeper folded into one voice. The chorus swings outward, begging an audience that cannot reply, before the ending dissolves into a single line looped past the point of song. What began as melody hardens into a verdict he recites to himself, no longer performing the loss but simply logging its permanence.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","alienation & emptiness"],"means":"grief rendered as a patient predator and intimate keeper at once","built":"melody hardens into a looped verdict recited past the point of song"},
"korn~here-to-stay-live-from-london-2014": {"fable":"A survivor's inventory of self-loathing that turns, mid-song, into a refusal to disappear. The verses stage abuse and its residue as violence aimed inward — the skull cracked against glass, the razor tracked toward a vein — because when someone strips you clean, the only remaining thing to command is the flesh doing the harm. That flat denial of being a whore is the tell: shame installed by another person, now spat back as rejection. The chorus reroutes all that inward pressure into a blunt vow to remain and to level whatever comes, the wounded self that craved erasure instead choosing endurance and making its own din its survival. The closing loop on having nothing left to surrender reads less as collapse than as a boundary finally held: the well is dry, and refusing to keep bleeding out is itself the act of staying.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","violence & murder"],"means":"shame installed by another person spat back as boundary finally held","built":"inward violence in the verses rerouted outward by the chorus into a survival vow"},
"korn~word-up-hq-full": {"fable":"Lyrically this is Cameo's 1986 party anthem intact: a call to the dancefloor where word up is the shared code that summons a crowd and shrugs off the posturing DJs and cool-guy airs, insisting music plus a body in motion beats any psychological romance. The interest is entirely in the transplant — Korn drag a slick electro-funk boast into detuned seven-string sludge, so a song built on effortless swagger gets played straight-faced through the most joyless instrument the band owns. The joke and the tribute are the same gesture: the invitation to lose yourself on the dancefloor survives, but it now arrives sounding like a threat rather than a wink.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["party & hedonism"],"means":"dancefloor invitation that survives but arrives sounding like a threat","built":"Cameo's effortless swagger played straight through Korn's most joyless instrument"},
"korn~got-the-life-official-hd-video": {"fable":"Korn's signature bounce turns a party command into a hollow ritual: the invitation to dance is issued by someone who admits he feels swallowed and torn out from the inside every day, so the groove is a distraction machine rather than a celebration. The song's split runs through its title's irony — God, reached by pager like a nagging beeper, taunts that the speaker already has the life while insisting he'll never actually see it, so the promised good life is possessed and withheld at once, a thing everyone chases and no one gets to look at. The refusal to follow that opens the track curdles into everyone will follow, marking how the same hollowness that isolates the singer is also what makes the crowd move with him. What sits under the mask is emptiness bought as glamour; the argument the song keeps enacting is that having the life and feeling nothing are the same condition.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["alienation & emptiness","artifice & performance","faith & the occult"],"means":"having the life and feeling nothing declared the same condition","built":"the title's irony: the life is simultaneously possessed and permanently withheld"},
"korn~worst-is-on-its-way": {"fable":"Dread rendered as a bodily intruder: not a feared event but a tenant already lodged under the ribs, so the forecast in the title is really a diagnosis of the present. The song splits between two refrains, one helpless amid sealed corridors and rooms he cannot leave, the other a clenched mantra ordering confidence to seize the wheel. That override is the crux, landing not as recovery but as willed suppression, which is why the frightened refrain floods back the instant the coping talk stops. Built by Davis out of grief, the terror stays domestic and recursive rather than cinematic, and the bravado is a mask worn because the fear never loosens its grip.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","death & grief","shame & repression"],"means":"dread as a tenant already lodged rather than a coming event","built":"bravado refrain floods back the instant the coping talk stops"},
"korn~uber-time": {"fable":"No confessional voice survives here: the words are lifted almost whole from a NASA briefing on the LCROSS mission, the deliberate crashing of a spent Centaur booster into a shadowed lunar crater to fling up debris and probe it for the faint spectral fingerprint of buried water. A band whose entire register is raw interior wound instead hands the microphone to an engineer's flat inventory of frozen compounds and mineral-bound moisture, coolly naming which onlookers under a lingering predawn gloom might catch the impact flash. That substitution is the meaning: the human ache is evacuated and swapped for cold telemetry, a search for the barest promise of life-sustaining ice pursued through pure procedure. The one contribution the band makes is the title chanted at the close, a grandiose nonsense-word that turns sterile transmission into incantation, as though the true subject were terror disguised as measurement. Heard as a mask, the refusal to feel becomes the feeling itself.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","nature & the elements"],"means":"human ache evacuated and swapped for cold telemetry","built":"NASA briefing lyrics substituted entire, refusal to feel enacted as the feeling itself"},
"korn~twist": {"fable":"The lyric is the word twist and nothing else, and that refusal is the whole point: Jonathan Davis famously scats this opener of Life Is Peachy as glossolalia, a torrent of vocal noise where the panic outruns language and never resolves into a sentence. Read straight, the single repeated word becomes both instruction and symptom — something wrenched out of shape, and a body wringing itself into a knot it cannot untie. There is nothing under the mask here because the point is that the feeling arrives pre-verbal, a scream that has given up on words rather than one that never had them.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind"],"means":"panic that outruns language and never resolves into a sentence","built":"a single repeated word becomes both instruction and symptom of being wrenched"},
"korn~throw-me-away": {"fable":"The song bargains from the position of a discarded thing, addressing whoever might still salvage it rather than the person who wrecked it in the first place. Its opening treats the hurt clinically, a flesh wound to be medicated and prayed over with real uncertainty that anyone is listening, before the vocabulary curdles into self-disgust: crumbling, used, worthless on the floor. The bridge reroutes that wreckage into something oddly hopeful, asking not for repair back to newness but to be held toward light, its fractures tended, its fragments hoarded in a drawer and recycled instead of binned. Jonathan Davis, whose lyrics circle childhood damage and abandonment across this band's catalog, lands the plea underneath the addiction imagery: keep me because I am still worth keeping.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","addiction & self-destruction","family & blood"],"means":"a discarded thing bargaining to be worth keeping","built":"self-disgust vocabulary pivots into a plea for fractures to be tended rather than binned"},
"korn~the-end-begins": {"fable":"The opening plunge of The Nothing, the album Jonathan Davis made in the wake of his wife's death, and it works almost entirely by repetition: the same accusation — why did you leave me — cycled until it stops being a question and becomes the shape of panic itself. The reproach is aimed at the dead, but the fear that follows is the real content: her going has unsealed something, loosed pursuers who are now free and closing in. Grief here is not sorrow but exposure — the protective presence gone, the survivor left to whatever was being held back. The build offers no resolution, only tightening; the loop is a mind that cannot get past the door it keeps knocking on, until the last words drop the mask entirely and it collapses into raw profanity — the point where language gives out and only the terror is left.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["death & grief","madness & the mind"],"means":"grief as exposure, the dead taking their protection with them","built":"repetition turns accusation into the shape of panic itself"},
"korn~reward-the-scars": {"fable":"The title flips the logic of injury: what marked a body becomes a medal, and the churning refrain about fighting hard while shedding the past treats old damage as ammunition rather than a wound to close. A tormentor is addressed head-on, toppled from whatever throne he claimed, while the speaker both dismisses him and demands to be shattered and dragged the full distance, as though repair lies on the far side of ruin. Yet the bridge betrays a cornered man haunted by something he cannot name, an inner voice bending him toward ruin even as he swears defiance. That gap makes the strut of the ending sound like willpower manufactured against an undertow, the fighter's face pulled on precisely because whatever sits beneath it will not surrender.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","identity & becoming","shame & repression"],"means":"defiance manufactured against an undertow that will not surrender","built":"the bridge betrays an inner voice bending the fighter toward ruin beneath the strut"},
"korn~one-more-time": {"fable":"A song built as a loop that cannot exit, where the recurring phrase arrives not as encouragement but as a verdict, each pass restarting the same unsolvable puzzle the instant it seems close to answered. Davis frames the affliction as both thief and tenant: it strips away what belongs to him while lodging in his own skull, so predator and prey turn out to be one person. The bridge trades the cyclic present for a slow vertical drop, a dreamlike descent through darkness he privately names as hell, closing on the flat surrender that this was always the required shape. Against the tortured maximalism of Untouchables, this reads as compulsion or depression rendered as recursion: not a failed escape but the admission that the circling itself is the condition, and the only truth left is to say so again.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","alienation & emptiness","madness & the mind"],"means":"compulsion rendered as recursion with no exit and no finish","built":"the recurring phrase arrives as verdict restarting the loop rather than encouragement"},
"korn~no-way": {"fable":"Color drains out of a life until the two poles of feeling collapse into one, everything registering as something and instantly as nothing, a numbness that mimics both at once. All the narrator's striving has manufactured is rage, and the divinity he claims is grandiosity soured into its opposite: the trapped man's absolute certainty that no exit exists. The bridge turns private complaint into persecution, the sensation of being picked and torn at by others, and the sharpest move is his wish to hand that hatred straight back to whoever planted it. True to Jonathan Davis's fractured method, the final passage divides the voice against itself, one refrain answered line by line by another, so accusation and self-accusation speak together and the haunter and haunted share a single throat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","anger & defiance","madness & the mind"],"means":"numbness that registers everything and immediately nothing","built":"the final passage splits the voice so haunter and haunted share one throat"},
"korn~need-to": {"fable":"A song about a compulsion the speaker despises in himself: a craving for someone unavailable that fuses desire, guilt, and self-disgust until the two are indistinguishable. Davis frames it as a body in revolt — skin gone cold at the very touch he cannot stop seeking, tenderness offered and reflexively pushed away, each visit described as being torn open from the inside. The load-bearing move is structural: the verses can still form questions and confessions, but as the feeling exceeds what language can hold, the writing collapses into bare repetition — the endless why, then the profanity and slur that close it — so the disintegration of the words enacts the loss of control the song is actually about. The hate/love pivot and that final degrading epithet aren't aimed cleanly at the other person; they curdle back onto a speaker who needs the thing he cannot forgive himself for needing, which is why the title lands as an accusation rather than a wish.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"self-disgust fused with the craving it cannot forgive","built":"lyrics disintegrate into bare repetition as control dissolves"},
"korn~my-confession": {"fable":"Two voices share one throat, and the title admits they belong to the same man. The first is a prosecutor — cold, superior, cataloguing the other party's ego, dishonesty, and slow molt into a parasite that its own appetites will devour. Then the register caves inward: the accuser turns out to be the accused, cowering in the same black pit he was condemning, rotting under a fever he cannot name, penned inside a ruin nobody built but himself. What the singer finally owns is that the contemptuous voice and the wrecked one were never two people but the mask and the raw thing beneath it, the easiest cruelty being the one flung outward to dodge how neatly it fits the accuser.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["shame & repression","identity & becoming","self-loathing"],"means":"the prosecutor unmasked as the defendant he prosecuted","built":"two voices sharing one throat revealed as mask and wound beneath"},
"korn~lost-in-the-grandeur": {"fable":"Grandeur here is the inflated public self, the persona the speaker is sick of wearing, and the song traces a man locked inside that costume until it becomes his cell. Jonathan Davis addresses a you who is both abuser and commanding voice: an authority whose orders were all followed, leaving him so anesthetized he claws at his own wounds just to register any sensation, threading Korn's decades-long fixation on early trauma into an adult who obeyed every command and was scooped hollow regardless. His request for guidance is openly branded a cliché, and that is the snare, since recovery slogans cannot penetrate a person who has hardened into performance. The pivot arrives with a droning noise that returns each morning to strip whatever gladness survives, a compulsion swelling until his hold slips; the final refusal then plays not as victory but as the last available stance, bravado costumed in the very grandeur it fails to shed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","dissociation & numbness","shame & repression"],"means":"the public persona obeyed until it hollows the self that wore it","built":"the final refusal plays as bravado costumed in the very grandeur it cannot shed"},
"korn~let-the-dark-do-the-rest": {"fable":"Depressive numbness curdles into surrender here, the speaker hunting an itch or spark that never arrives, no longer expecting rescue and half-inviting oblivion to finish what the ache began. Davis turns the revulsion refrain inward, the nausea aimed less at a tormentor than at a self loathing itself, with wellness and conformity offered as cures that only hollow the room out further. Black canvases hung behind the ribs pulse like something almost holy, reframing self-erasure as welcome; the faint wish to glimpse whatever waits ahead is too weak to shut the door the resignation has already swung wide.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","suicide","shame & repression"],"means":"surrender to oblivion half-invited when no spark ever arrives","built":"self-erasure reframed as something almost holy by the black-canvas image"},
"korn~here-it-comes-again": {"fable":"A song built as a cycle rather than a narrative: the return of an intrusive state that arrives unbidden, pounds, sets voices circling the head, and then briefly recedes before it comes back around again. In classic Korn fashion the verses map the affliction from the outside while the chorus lives inside it, where the sufferer bargains with himself in the same breath he is overwhelmed, insisting he can fight and cannot give in even as the trouble grabs hold. The bridge is where it turns: four flat vows to hold on and not be gone rhyme, line for line, into four helpless questions of why he cannot, so that the will to endure and the doubt that it is possible turn out to be the same voice. The refusal to resolve is the point; the title is a diagnosis, not a plot, and the song ends by handing us back to the chorus because that is exactly what recurrence does.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["madness & the mind","anger & defiance"],"means":"the will to endure and the doubt it is possible spoken in one voice","built":"four vows to hold on rhyme line-for-line into four questions of why he cannot"},
"korn~fake": {"fable":"A tirade aimed at a coward wearing a borrowed self, and under the sheer excess of revulsion runs the undertow that contempt this hot can sound like recognition. The chorus keeps ordering release from the mask, from the excuses, from the fear knotted under the ribs, and that command is precisely the surrender the accuser proves incapable of. Turning private shame into an outward snarl is the debut's governing engine, and the bridge states the debut's one plain credo — be the person you actually are, let the real thing through — so the jabbing chant of the word lands as both verdict on the poser and a warning the shouter could yet earn.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["artifice & performance","anger & defiance","identity & becoming"],"means":"contempt this hot for a poser names the shouter's own surrender","built":"bridge states the plain credo the accuser's performance already violates"},
"korn~earache-my-eye": {"fable":"Reworking the Cheech and Chong routine, Korn keep its comic engine running: a spoiled kid narrates his own disgrace as if it were a boast, and the mask never slips because behind the performance sits no earnest self waiting to be found. The loose skit chatter up front — grill banter, cash-register sound effects, casually dropped burnouts — supplies the same punchline in advance, a brat announcing that wealth and rockstar status void every verdict his father and his coach ever handed down for cross-dressing and effeminacy. What the track actually tracks is that bravado souring: crowing about property and ownership curdles into indifference toward the world ending, so the riches read less as victory than as numbing agent, a defiance impossible to tell apart from felt nothing. Cartoon threats of genital mutilation keep the provocation deliberately weightless, sketching a figure so consumed by broadcasting his not-caring that he never registers having nothing worth defending.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["dissociation & numbness","comedy & wordplay","money & the street"],"means":"defiance impossible to distinguish from numbness wearing bravado","built":"skit framing announces the punchline before any verse lands it"},
"korn~disconnect": {"fable":"A song about refusing the split it names: the speaker holds dark and light as a single alignment rather than a war to be won, and the divinity he feels is precisely in the tension, one side pulling him under while the other arms him to fight. The paradoxes stacked early — lies as refined truth, dark lights the eye can't see — aren't confusion but a creed that inner pain is too real to hide and too integral to disown. The closing turn reframes that private duality as a social diagnosis: selfishness cuts in, forgiveness is only a beginning, sense hasn't arrived yet, and the disconnect is not the speaker's failure but the shared condition of what we feel. On Requiem, a record Jonathan Davis wrote grieving his late wife, that endlessly repeated title word reads less as breakdown than as the flat, exhausted acceptance of being severed from a wholeness that won't return.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["identity & becoming","alienation & emptiness","death & grief"],"means":"dark and light held as one alignment rather than a war","built":"the endlessly repeated title word reads as severed wholeness not breakdown"},
"korn~clown": {"fable":"A confrontation song that flips the schoolyard hierarchy of who is weak. The speaker is being beaten and jeered at for being strange, an outsider from the wrong town, and rather than plead he turns the taunt back on his attacker: the real cowardice belongs to the aggressor, whose bravado and inked-up body are read as camouflage for someone too scared to be honest about himself. The recurring demand to be hit is the song's cruel logic made physical, daring the tormentor to keep proving the accusation that violence is his only way to hide, while the singer claims a freedom the bully will never have. When the taunt hardens into the clown chant and the split-your-face threat, the reply to the abuse curdles into the same aggression it condemns, so the mask and what is under it belong to both men by the end.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["anger & defiance","shame & repression","violence & murder"],"means":"daring the tormentor to keep proving violence is his only disguise","built":"retaliation mirrors the aggression it condemns by the end"},
"korn~cameltosis-feat-slimkid-3": {"fable":"A hookup narrated as chemical dependency, all fixes and drained veins, where the sex reads like a needle the speaker keeps returning to knowing it costs him blood, and Slimkid3's slick rhyme-dense delivery stays cool precisely because the man underneath is coming apart. The endlessly repeated question of what she wants from him is the tell: it wears the shape of seduction but plays as helplessness, an interrogation looping because he has no answer and suspects the real question points inward. The chorus venom, the slurs, the vow never to be burned again, is armor bolted over a wound, cruelty as the vocabulary a man grabs for once tenderness has already scalded him. What lifts it above pure spite is the bridge, where he confesses to being a machine running against its own wiring, hunting a connection he cannot map and naming himself the fault rather than her. It is Davis's voice that spits the never-again chorus against Hardson's rueful verses, two singers splitting one wound, and the last verse folds everything into an act performed alone, the admission that the affair was never about her, a hurt he keeps dosing himself with and then mislabels as devotion.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","themes":["addiction & self-destruction","love & desire","shame & repression"],"means":"cruelty as vocabulary grabbed once tenderness has already scalded","built":"two singers split one wound across contrasting delivery registers"},
"slipknot~left-behind-hd": {"fable":"Corey Taylor built Iowa on self-disgust, and this track turns that loathing into a fable about preservation gone rotten: the speaker pictures himself as a lab specimen, sealed behind a pane and pickled in caustic powder, mounted for viewing rather than granted the dignity of rotting on his own. The chorus stages an impossible double motion, recoiling and reaching at once, so longing and refusal cancel out and everything simply drains off. The verses conjure a grotesque, deformed version of the other to excuse the flinch, but the bridge admits the truth: the presence he most wants to scour out is the one that still lingers like a haunting. His vow to destroy whatever draws near reads less as menace than as raw fear of intimacy in someone convinced he is already spoiled. The outro thins to a single repeated line, worn down until it hollows out, an honest close that offers no rescue.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~before-i-forget-hd": {"fable":"The verses fasten the speaker inside a sealed room he finds both suffocating and oddly homelike, then set the recurring Slipknot standoff in which the other person, worn like a mark burned into skin, is inseparable from his own filth and wound — he cannot accuse without convicting himself. The refrain flips time on its axis: an elemental thing precedes the socialized person, a raw organism arrives long before it learns to walk upright, and recovering that buried self must outrace its erasure. That reversal is the thesis — the true self runs deeper than the mask and predates every role assigned to it, so the plea to go quiet and pay attention becomes Corey Taylor demanding the unarmored man be reached before the performed one devours him. The late my-means loop then concedes the failure inside the wanting, admitting every dodge and delay only postpones the reckoning it claims to justify.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~adderall-no-intro": {"fable":"Corey Taylor sings this in a hushed, confessional register, reading the stimulant and scripture as one interchangeable machinery of control, each vowing to steady a coming collapse while quietly setting the price of surrender. The recurring demand to know why he exists is left deliberately unanswered, so the song withholds the reassurance it keeps begging for. Trapped inside the bridge, the speaker measures which allegiance grips him hardest and understands the choice was never his; the closing gesture treats reflexive condolence and inherited belief as the same weightless comfort, a numbing that consoles without ever rescuing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~sic-molt-injected-mix": {"fable":"A declaration of war that keeps turning its rage inward as fast as it aims it outward. The speaker taunts an enemy to reveal itself, insists nothing can break him, then trips over his own bravado — a mid-song stammer where he catches himself posturing and can't decide if he's malevolent or just decadent, before the contempt he throws at the enemy's twisted mind curdles into the admission that his and the enemy's minds are the same thing. That collapse is the whole point: the closing mantra promising you can't kill him because he's already inside you drops the pretense of two combatants and names the fight as one that was internal all along, the sickness of the title being the speaker's own. The barked (Sic) that ends it works like the editorial notation — quoted exactly, errors and all, refusing to be corrected — the ugliness insisted on as deliberate, reprinted on purpose.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~duality-animal-cover": {"fable":"The title names the trap: two selves that can neither reconcile nor separate, and the song lives in the seam between them. That opening image of crushing the sockets shut turns self-harm into the only available relief, pain deployed against pain because everything within is unbearable and boring its way deeper no matter what. The verses turn outward into a standoff with someone the speaker can neither abandon nor be freed by; the bridge sharpens this into the real demand, to be either restored whole or dismantled completely, no middle state, since waking never matched the dream and half-measures only prolong the hurt. The closing collapse into one repeated admission of madness is the duality finally caving in, no synthesis possible, the mind just looping the same broken line until nothing else remains.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~wait-and-bleed-terry-date-mix": {"fable":"The chorus works as ritual rather than story: hatred welling up, a stooping to brush leaves from a headstone, a withdrawal into some armored interior where the wound stays hidden and unwitnessed — a closed circuit the song keeps returning to. The verses rupture that circuit with a jolt of waking: eyes lit with copper and flame, hands that will not stop trembling, a person who fails to recognize the figure before them and asks how any of this came to be, disturbed that a part of them enjoys it. The programmed-assassin figure is the hinge, a mind conditioned to inflict harm without ever agreeing to it, culpable for a violence noticed only once done, so the sealed interior reads less as sadness than as dissociation — the dread of watching yourself obey impulses you never chose. A final movement locks it into futility, nothing learned and nothing altered, the ache left running loose forever, then swivels the whole trap outward as a warning aimed at the listener.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Taylor's account roots the song in a recurring dream of waking in a bath of one's own blood, and glosses it as the thin switch between the civilized self and the animal that can act at any moment — the self-directed hearing the murmured verses keep half-open."},
"slipknot~wait-and-bleed-demo": {"fable":"The chorus works as ritual rather than story: hatred welling up, a stooping to brush leaves from a headstone, a withdrawal into some armored interior where the wound stays hidden and unwitnessed — a closed circuit the song keeps returning to. The verses rupture that circuit with a jolt of waking: eyes lit with copper and flame, hands that will not stop trembling, a person who fails to recognize the figure before them and asks how any of this came to be, disturbed that a part of them enjoys it. The programmed-assassin figure is the hinge, a mind conditioned to inflict harm without ever agreeing to it, culpable for a violence noticed only once done, so the sealed interior reads less as sadness than as dissociation — the dread of watching yourself obey impulses you never chose. A final movement locks it into futility, nothing learned and nothing altered, the ache left running loose forever, then swivels the whole trap outward as a warning aimed at the listener.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Taylor's account roots the song in a recurring dream of waking in a bath of one's own blood, and glosses it as the thin switch between the civilized self and the animal that can act at any moment — the self-directed hearing the murmured verses keep half-open."},
"slipknot~vermillion-pt-2-bloodstone-mix": {"fable":"The acoustic hush Corey Taylor works in here is a mask, and the fixation beneath it is what the quiet barely restrains. He confesses the woman outright as no person at all but a figure stitched from his own shame, a melody no listener will ever join, so the yearning bends back on itself — a hunger to trust something invented hard enough to keep supplying its own proof. His vow that the feeling will not gather and swell inside him is the giveaway: chanted past all conviction, the promise curdles into the very pressure it denies, a man reasoning himself calm while his voice snags and closes on the swallowed truth. His closing surrender, that she was never flesh and never could be conjured into it, reads not as release but as ruin — the illusion finally named and still refused surrender.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~psychosocial-live": {"fable":"Taylor turns a rant against war-profiteering and manufactured piety into a mirror, since the martyr he swears is hidden in his chest is the self-righteousness he despises in the bloodthirsty crowd. The device is that reversal: once every enemy has been slaughtered, only the grievance remains, curdling into an appetite that hunts for fresh targets simply to keep breathing. Medals stamped for valor and slogans borrowed from noble resistance become interchangeable props in a spectacle where conviction has hardened into a contagion nobody can quarantine. The chanted diagnosis names the whole sickness as a mind-disease of the group, which the speaker admits he carries himself while still refusing to be the sole guilty party.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~sarcastrophe-live": {"fable":"Cut in the wake of Paul Gray's death, this is bereavement that refuses to soften into elegy, reforging a band's loss into a creed of retaliation. The chanted self-deification reads less as bravado than as a vow sworn over a coffin: to survive by turning executioner, striking down whatever would strike them first. Verses cross-examine the listener like a courtroom, pressing for a confession or a surrender to nothing, dragging a private grave into open judgment. The refrain twists two ways at once — a pledge of endurance owed the dead braided with a demand that the living perish in tribute, the mourner daring an indifferent world to justify its own continuance.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The portmanteau title keeps a sneer over the whole god-pose: read through the sarcasm, the self-deification and the die-for-me benediction play as satire of the grandeur they perform."},
"slipknot~liberate-live-in-london-2002": {"fable":"The demand is inverted from the start: what a person would ordinarily beg to be rid of is instead unchained on purpose rather than caged. That reversal turns therapy into weaponry — the wall the speaker throws up isn't meant to trap his own fury but to seal out a world he reads as rigged, dishonest, and starved of anything real to offer. Nine masked bodies performing as a faceless swarm sharpens the paradox, since the voice keeps asserting a singular, boundless self while dissolving into the anonymous many around him. The final stretch is engineered to disintegrate, courtroom verbs and rhyming abstractions piling up faster than sense can hold them until the verse leaves only a single hurled charge of deceit, repeated until it stops meaning and becomes pure sound. What steadies the whole thing is its logic of survival dressed as ruin: the collapse he stages is a defense, not a surrender.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~my-plague-live-in-london-2002": {"fable":"What sounds like a scorched-earth kiss-off to some belittling megalomaniac is secretly a hall of mirrors, since the refrain keeps splitting culpability two ways at once. The accused blames the speaker and blames himself, torments the speaker and torments himself, until the pair of them collapse into a single figure warring with its own reflection. The middle passage confesses this outright: turning the blade inward, craving to know why he wounds himself, owning his own rottenness while claiming the small dignity of not hiding it. The late chant that he will never become the other plays less like triumph than terror, a fear of that very resemblance where a single misfiring nerve tips private self-hatred into open combat.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~wait-and-bleed-live-in-london-2002": {"fable":"The chorus works as ritual rather than story: hatred welling up, a stooping to brush leaves from a headstone, a withdrawal into some armored interior where the wound stays hidden and unwitnessed — a closed circuit the song keeps returning to. The verses rupture that circuit with a jolt of waking: eyes lit with copper and flame, hands that will not stop trembling, a person who fails to recognize the figure before them and asks how any of this came to be, disturbed that a part of them enjoys it. The programmed-assassin figure is the hinge, a mind conditioned to inflict harm without ever agreeing to it, culpable for a violence noticed only once done, so the sealed interior reads less as sadness than as dissociation — the dread of watching yourself obey impulses you never chose. A final movement locks it into futility, nothing learned and nothing altered, the ache left running loose forever, then swivels the whole trap outward as a warning aimed at the listener.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Taylor's account roots the song in a recurring dream of waking in a bath of one's own blood, and glosses it as the thin switch between the civilized self and the animal that can act at any moment — the self-directed hearing the murmured verses keep half-open."},
"slipknot~people-shit-live-in-london-2002": {"fable":"The title reads as blanket contempt, yet each verse leaks its opposite: a man onstage naming himself the idiot on display, the disappointment everyone predicted, sickened that shame still moves him at all. Reducing the whole species to filth is armor, a way to strike first so no one can wound the wound he already describes with a barrel at his temple. The jeers stapled to the chant, the boasts of superiority and fearlessness, are that same defense wearing a sneer, and the record keeps splitting the mask open. Mortality, invoked toward the end, sounds less like menace than the last leveling mercy available once self-loathing has sealed every other door.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~eyeless-live-in-london-2002": {"fable":"Paranoia here works as a broken circuit: the speaker crowns himself the last lucid mind besieged by nagging inner chatter, then concedes the sickness lives equally in his skull and the listener's, dissolving any line between the afflicted and the accusers. The famous demand to borrow a film idol's gaze before you can even take in California turns the true subject into vision itself, a self that owns no direct sight and must peer through counterfeit celebrity lenses. That explains why the abandoned-son passage — heir to a ghost, inheriting only vacancy — reads as identity assembled from what was never there. The closing scream to be met eye to eye springs the trap, begging for recognition through an organ he had to build himself, so the escalating repetition tightens the noose rather than resolving it.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"The refrain's central image began as a phrase the band overheard from a raving stranger on a New York street — found language, not invented, which is why the borrowed-eyes koan resists being decoded."},
"slipknot~eeyore-live-in-london-2002": {"fable":"A track that treats its own origin like an accusation, tracing the self back to a soiled inheritance and a botched delivery, then daring anyone to flinch. The furious boast about being an enormous voice is the whole thesis: identity here shrinks to sheer decibels, a mouth that exists only to spit contempt and register no meaning past the spitting. Every murder threat gets quietly retracted by a whispered aside that admits the singer never once confronts the person he curses, so the fury spins loose from any actual target. What remains is a persona that has devoured its own motives and keeps snarling anyway, the vocabulary of hatred collapsing into a single expletive hammered past sense until it becomes pure abrasive texture. Aggression, stripped of cause, performs itself into exhaustion and calls the performance a self.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~disasterpiece-live-in-london-2002": {"fable":"The song wears the most extreme mask Slipknot could put on — a serial-killer bravado of throat-slitting and infinite menace — precisely so it can watch that mask rot off in real time. What the aggressor keeps circling back to isn't a threat but a grievance about existing at all: the refrain that he was never meant to be here reframes every act of violence as self-directed, an attempt to erase a self that already feels blotted out. The turn comes when he offers to drag you down with him and pleads to be finished off just in case, exposing the predator as suicidal and the whole performance of hatred as one raw sound wedged between rage and grief. Here Corey Taylor pushes the band's provocation past shock into naked self-annihilation, so the disaster the title promises turns out to be the narrator himself, not his victim.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~yen": {"fable":"The title is an old word for craving, and the devotion it names can only imagine itself as being unmade: the lover is both the temptation long anticipated and the grip closing on the windpipe, so tenderness and murder arrive as a single gesture. What the voice truly begs for is not the beloved but a witness who will keep the name after the body is gone, since erasure frightens it far more than the blade does. The plea for equivalence among every ending, and the surrender that stiffens from being killed into having chosen it, reveal a hunger to matter enough that destruction feels like the surest proof of ever having existed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~vermillion-pt-2": {"fable":"The acoustic hush Corey Taylor works in here is a mask, and the fixation beneath it is what the quiet barely restrains. He confesses the woman outright as no person at all but a figure stitched from his own shame, a melody no listener will ever join, so the yearning bends back on itself — a hunger to trust something invented hard enough to keep supplying its own proof. His vow that the feeling will not gather and swell inside him is the giveaway: chanted past all conviction, the promise curdles into the very pressure it denies, a man reasoning himself calm while his voice snags and closes on the swallowed truth. His closing surrender, that she was never flesh and never could be conjured into it, reads not as release but as ruin — the illusion finally named and still refused surrender.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~vendetta": {"fable":"Every boast the speaker makes doubles as an unwitting confession, a swagger built from copied gestures and phantom counsel that he mistakes for hard-won authority. His real talent is refusal — the smile is an anesthetic that spreads the ache outward until no corner of the world escapes it, while the mocking invitation to enjoy your best years lands as poison, since the bright life he offers is only sedation. The chanted plea to keep playacting past a finish already reached exposes the grudge as inward, a campaign waged by a wrecked will against the ruin it can no longer deny.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~the-virus-of-life": {"fable":"Read the predator's murmur as the interior monologue of a contagion: it has shadowed the body since morning, holding still through the fever's climb until vigilance slackens and it can hollow the host out and pour itself back in. Every endearment and hush belongs to incubation rather than romance, so the whisperer's rising, sweat-soaked craving is really replication mistaking itself for desire, the sickness narrating its own transmission as unbearable want. The final vow to strip and inhabit another, to reduce them to ash, resolves the earlier tenderness and the appetite for destruction into one motion, since occupying a stranger completely and obliterating them are the same act to something that survives only by consuming its carrier. When the refrain declares this presence lodged in everyone, it reframes mortality itself as the pathogen we are born hosting, the wound and the blade folded into the mere fact of being animate.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4","fnote":"Under the literal-contagion reading sits the one most listeners meet first: a human stalker in disease costume, the mounting fever and whispered restraint tracking a predator held at the brink."},
"slipknot~the-dying-song-time-to-sing": {"fable":"Slipknot stage an interrogation as a hymn, the parenthetical title recasting worship as a scheduled execution and the chorus offering a mock-baptism into one's own end as the only honest sacrament left. The verse collapses every authority into fraud: Trinity, communism, even the devil are waved off as spent mythologies, leaving power as nothing but rigged gamesmanship, a table of casino overseers running a fixed wager stripped of cosmic meaning. That hollowed world is why the funeral melody must be voiced in solitude, after every listener has walked out and the spirit has already abandoned the shrinking body. A late movement turns the ritual openly coercive, the singer menacing the bastards for a confession under threat of their own last breath, so the piece doubles as both a condemned man's testimony and the hangman's ultimatum. Corey Taylor's refrain about a soul kept low for far too long lands exactly on the seam between jeer and grim mercy.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~hive-mind": {"fable":"A performer's kiss-off to the online swarm, where a single collective intelligence stands in for the crowd of anonymous accounts that judge and dogpile as one nervous system. Its venom targets the type who claims no stake yet does nothing but comment, smearing hate across a screen they mistake for a wall they could tear down. The cruelty runs deliberately two-faced: the song hurls death-wishes back at the hater, performing the exact toxicity they condemn, so aggressor and wound keep trading masks. What redeems the spite is the closing turn, when the sneer drops and the voice quietly urges the hated one to reach the far bank and keep breathing, refusing to sink just to prove a point.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~finale": {"fable":"A becalmed, lulling refrain insists this place is home now, a shame but unleavable, while the verses tally everything that makes staying intolerable: punishments, tangled knots, hardening work, a temper worn down to a hair-trigger. That contrast is the whole engine, contentment recited like a mantra while the ledger underneath keeps totaling the damage, so the calm reads less as peace than as the exhausted lie a lifer tells himself to keep functioning. Stale replies get recycled against fresh interrogations, the walls seem to close in, the distance ahead stretches, until the accumulation lands on one accusation: the figure common to every entry on the list is the person being addressed. The pivot from resignation to menace is the point, the narrator refusing to break not from strength but from spite, promising to fasten his listener onto the same cross he carries. What sounds like sanctuary is unmasked as a cell he intends to make someone else share, the placid surface a threat that the loop has no exit.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~child-of-burning-time": {"fable":"A condemned man refuses the rescue everyone wants to write for him, asking for fire not as sentence but as the one exit that stays his own. The title figure is a self already forfeited at some vanished chance, so the shame lodged in his skin, the sole mark he cannot shed, feels older than any verdict. His pivot is a hard no to heroism: he won't be conscripted into saving a life he no longer prizes, and he won't spend tomorrow to squander it all in a single day. By carving his own signature through flesh and then spirit, he claims the authorship of his own vanishing, so the plea to be consumed reads as a bid to keep his fury and leave on terms no one else drafted.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~bone-church": {"fable":"Corey Taylor sings the track as a refusal disguised as a plea: the line that prayer no longer rescues the speaker recurs until repetition hardens into verdict, a door bolted from inside rather than a cry to be let out. Its antitheses fuse tenderness and violence into one gesture, so worship and wreckage become the same motion viewed from opposing angles, devotion indistinguishable from the wound it inflicts. The title frames the body as a shrine already gutted — hallowed architecture built from remains, holy and lifeless at once — where feeling survives only as a scar and any hope of renewal has been quietly abandoned. What sounds like supplication is its inversion: comfort has been examined, judged a mirage flickering at the edge of sight, and dismissed.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~be-prepared-for-hell": {"fable":"A cornered voice caught between a fear of escape that means death and a plea to simply be left alone, until the panic curdles into a shrug: the oh-well tacked onto the warning turns dread into fatigue, damnation accepted before it arrives. As a fragment on the album Slipknot built around Paul Gray's death, its request to be told when the eyes can open and finally register that dying has already happened reads less as threat than as someone waiting to confirm a loss that has already occurred. The refusal to develop past its blunt repeated warning is the point — grief that has stopped bargaining and only braces.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
"slipknot~adderall": {"fable":"Corey Taylor sings this in a hushed, confessional register, reading the stimulant and scripture as one interchangeable machinery of control, each vowing to steady a coming collapse while quietly setting the price of surrender. The recurring demand to know why he exists is left deliberately unanswered, so the song withholds the reassurance it keeps begging for. Trapped inside the bridge, the speaker measures which allegiance grips him hardest and understands the choice was never his; the closing gesture treats reflexive condolence and inherited belief as the same weightless comfort, a numbing that consoles without ever rescuing.","fv":2,"fvr":"2.4"},
};
